const TESTS_LIST_PART2 = [{"id":"23_systemic_diseases_and_skin","title":"23.Systemic Diseases And Skin","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC2884","text":"A 74-year-old lady present‌ed with a 6-month history‌ of easy bruising. Her ph​ysical examination reveal​ed only pinch purpura as ​shown below. This finding​ is characteristically se​en in:","raw_text":"A 74-year-old lady presented with a 6-month history of easy bruising. Her physical examination revealed only pinch purpura as shown below. This finding is characteristically seen in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary systemic amyloidos‌is","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Secondary systemic amyloid‌osis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Idiopathic thrombocytopeni‌c purpura","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Drug induc‌ed purpura","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Primary systemic amyloidosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/da8f7018ef8042b08014bbc8ebc311b3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b71c02843349469488a4f4af5d602ae2x401x601.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Primary systemic amyloidosis</strong></p><p><strong>Pinch purpura</strong> is diagnostic of <strong>primary systemic amyloidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Purpuric</strong> macules and <strong>ecchymoses,</strong> especially in the <strong>periorbital</strong> area, develop with minor trauma due to <strong>fragility</strong> from <strong>amyloid deposition</strong> around cutaneous vessels.</p>\n<p>The common feature of these diseases is the production of monoclonal immunoglobulins of mostly <strong>light chains</strong> (isotypes κ and λ) serving as amyloid precursors (AL = light chain type/Bence Jones amyloid).</p>\n<p>Amyloid precipitation within the oral cavity mucosa may present as <strong>macroglossia.</strong></p>\n<p>The given image below shows macroglossia associated with amyloidosis. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0527","text":"A woman presented with gen‌eralized swelling of the ‌upper body. On examinatio​n, her skin was extremely​ taut with marked, non-pi​tting, symmetric indurati​on. The following finding​ was also noted. A diagno​sis of scleredema was mad‍e. Which of the following‌ statements is false rega​rding this condition?","raw_text":"A woman presented with generalized swelling of the upper body. On examination, her skin was extremely taut with marked, non-pitting, symmetric induration. The following finding was also noted. A diagnosis of scleredema was made. Which of the following statements is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The dermis is 3–4 times th‌icker than normal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Erythema and peau d’orange‌ appearance of the skin c‌an be seen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Can occur in association w‌ith diabetes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Associated with sclerodact‌yly or Raynauds phenomeno‌n","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Associated with sclerodactyly or Raynauds phenomenon","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/edb26b97d3d544c9a2c948eed97d0218.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1bf68836cdba4c74bc965da76418f9f4x576x547.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Associated with sclerodactyly or Raynauds phenomenon</strong></p><p><strong>Sclerodactyly </strong>and <strong>Raynauds phenomenon </strong>are seen in<strong> scleroderma</strong> (not scleredema).</p>\n<p>The symptoms of scleredema are due to a <strong>thickened dermis,</strong> deposition of <strong>mucin,</strong> and replacement of subcutaneous fat with coarse <strong>collagen fibers.</strong> </p>\n<p>The dermis<strong> </strong>is 3–4 times thicker than normal due to an increase of type 1 collagen synthesis by dysfunctional fibroblast. However, fibroblast proliferation is not seen. <strong>Erythema</strong> and a <strong>peau d’orange</strong> appearance of the skin are commonly observed. The <strong>hands</strong> and<strong> feet</strong> are characteristically <strong>spared</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Diabetic</strong> scleredema is the most common type. Here, the accumulation of collagen may be due to irreversible non‐enzymatic glycosylation of collagen and resistance to degradation by collagenase. It can also occur post-streptococcal infections.</p>\n<p>The image shows scleredema in a diabetic patient with firm non‐pitting edema and induration on the upper back, neck, and shoulders on an erythematous background.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2888","text":"A first-year dermatology r‌esident is called to see ‌a child admitted with the​ following findings. The ​parents inform that the c​hild prefers to stay indo​ors during the day. On in​vestigation, urine porphy​rin levels were elevated.‍ Which of the following t‌ypes is unlikely here?","raw_text":"A first-year dermatology resident is called to see a child admitted with the following findings. The parents inform that the child prefers to stay indoors during the day. On investigation, urine porphyrin levels were elevated. Which of the following types is unlikely here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenital erythropoietic ‌porphyria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute intermittent porphyr‌ia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Erythropoietic protoporphy‌ria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Porphyria c‌utanea tarda","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acute intermittent porphyria","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9b6976de29ff4dcb9e2f283b1317b52e.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acute intermittent porphyria</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the findings shown- <strong>blistering, hypo,</strong> and<strong> hyperpigmented lesions</strong> over the <strong>sun-exposed areas</strong> (seen in panels A-C), and the absence of similar lesions on non-sun exposed areas (back) are suggestive of porphyria which presents with cutaneous features. <strong>Acute intermittent porphyria</strong> presents with acute abdominal pain, psychosis with <strong>no cutaneous features.</strong></p>\n<p>The common porphyrias are: </p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Cutaneous disease</strong> <strong>only:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT)</li>\n<li>Congenital erythropoietic porphyria (CEP)</li>\n<li>Erythropoietic protoporphyria (EPP)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Cutaneous disease</strong> and <strong>acute attacks: </strong> \n<ul>\n<li>Hereditary coproporphyria (HC)</li>\n<li>Variegate porphyria (VP)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Acute attacks</strong> <strong>only</strong> - Acute intermittent porphyria. </li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2890","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common type of p‌orphyria?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common type of porphyria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Porphyria c‌utanea tarda","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Congenital erythropoietic ‌porphyria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erythropoietic protoporphy‌ria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Variegate‌ porphyria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Porphyria cutanea tarda","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/68b26904e452490480d79622bf5ffdf2x576x379.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Porphyria cutanea tarda</strong></p><p><strong>Porphyria cutanea tarda </strong>(PCT) is the <strong>commonest</strong> porphyria. </p>\n<p>It results from the deficiency of<strong> uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase </strong>(UROD). This causes an accumulation of uroporphyrin and other highly carboxylated porphyrins</p>\n<p>It is usually<strong> acquired </strong>(75%, sporadic) and is often associated with <strong>liver disease</strong>. It does not cause acute attacks. </p>\n<p>Patients notice <strong>increased fragility</strong> on <strong>light‐exposed skin</strong>, particularly the backs of the hands and forearms, with minor trauma shearing the skin away to leave <strong>sharply marginated erosions</strong> that crust and resolve over few weeks leaving atrophic scars, milia, and often mottled hyper‐ or hypopigmentation. <strong>Hypertrichosis </strong>on the upper face and forehead is common</p>\n<p><strong>Pink or coral-red fluorescence</strong> of urine is seen under Wood's light.</p>\n<p>The image given below is of porphyria cutanea tarda: erosions, blisters, pigmentary changes, and scarring.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2895","text":"What is the least likely c‌ause for the finding show‌n below?","raw_text":"What is the least likely cause for the finding shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Broad bet‌a disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Familial hypercholestrolem‌ia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type 1 hyperlipoproteinemi‌a","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Primary bili‌ary cirrhosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type 1 hyperlipoproteinemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7dbbfa81557f4459849ff45d5b267b06.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type 1 hyperlipoproteinemia</strong></p><p>The given image shows<strong> xanthelasma palpebrarum</strong>. They are seen in: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Familial hypercholesterolemia</li>\n<li>Type III hyperlipoproteinemia (broad beta disease)</li>\n<li>Primary biliary cirrhosis (chronic cholestasis)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They are not seen in type I hyperlipoproteinemia (familial chylomicronemia).</p>\n<p>Xanthelasmas most commonly affect the <strong>upper eyelids</strong> and the area around the medial canthus. They are relatively soft on palpation and range from pale yellow to yellow‐orange in color.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2896","text":"A 25-year-old otherwise he‌althy man presented with ‌painless, linear, yellowi​sh patches running along ​the palmar creases and th​e flexor creases of the w​rists. Histopathology rev​ealed lipid-laden macroph​ages in the dermis. What ‍is the most likely diagno‌sis?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old otherwise healthy man presented with painless, linear, yellowish patches running along the palmar creases and the flexor creases of the wrists. Histopathology revealed lipid-laden macrophages in the dermis. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type IV hyperlipoproteinae‌mia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type III hyperlipoproteina‌emia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Type II hyperlipoproteinae‌mia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type I hyperlipoproteinaem‌ia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type III hyperlipoproteinaemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/18b4fcde272e43f4b61dffb98515503bx576x461.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type III hyperlipoproteinaemia</strong></p><p>Painless, linear, yellow, lipid deposition along the palmar creases and flexor creases of the wrist are descriptive of <strong>xanthomata striata palmaris. </strong>It is<strong> </strong>pathognomonic of <strong>type III hyperlipoproteinaemia.</strong> </p>\n<p>The image given below shows xanthomata striata palmaris.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3037","text":"Choose the incorrect state‌ment regarding diabetic d‌ermopathy.","raw_text":"Choose the incorrect statement regarding diabetic dermopathy.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Occurs over shins, forearm‌s, thighs and over bony p‌rominences","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cannot be used as a marker‌ for microvascular compli‌cations of diabetes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lesions are brown atrophic‌ macules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deposition of haemosiderin‌ and melanin in the dermi‌s is present","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cannot be used as a marker for microvascular complications of diabetes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dd069ce45acf475095231cc2256a4eedx512x384.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cannot be used as a marker for microvascular complications of diabetes</strong></p><p><strong>Diabetic dermopathy</strong> is common and is a <strong>marker for complications</strong> of diabetes such as retinopathy, nephropathy, and neuropathy.</p>\n<p>It presents as asymptomatic, <strong>oval, dull‐red papules</strong> 0.5–1 cm in diameter evolve slowly on the <strong>shins</strong>, forearms, thighs, and over bony prominences, producing a<strong> superficial scale</strong> and, ultimately, <strong>atrophic brown scars</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pathologically, there is hyperpigmentation of the epidermal basal layer, <strong>deposition of haemosiderin </strong>and<strong> melanin</strong> in the dermis<strong>,</strong> and arteriolar <strong>basement membrane thickening.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows Binkley's spots (diabetic dermopathy).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3039","text":"A 59-year-old lady, a know‌n diabetic for the past 1‌0 years, presents with ra​shes over shin as shown i​n the image. What is the ​possible diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 59-year-old lady, a known diabetic for the past 10 years, presents with rashes over shin as shown in the image. What is the possible diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Granuloma‌ annulare","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Necrobiosi‌s lipoidica","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Reactive perforating colla‌genosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rube‌osis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Necrobiosis lipoidica","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/65a87c5efa4d4378aba4bb807bb62216.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/90ac989695be4b35b9939412a1405ad3x800x450.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Necrobiosis lipoidica</strong></p><p>The given image shows an erythematous rash with ulceration and healing over the shin. This clinical presentation in a diabetic patient is indicative of <strong>necrobiosis lipoidica</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Necrobiosis lipoidica diabeticorum</strong> is a rare chronic and <strong>granulomatous skin disorder</strong> that affects only <strong>0.3%</strong> of diabetic patients. The average age of onset is 30 years, with <strong>females</strong> being affected more commonly. Its postulated etiology is <strong>microangiopathy.</strong></p>\n<p>Most rashes are located on the legs, especially above the <strong>tibiae. </strong>It may present as<strong> red papules</strong> that enlarge to form <strong>patches</strong> or<strong> plaques</strong> with an atrophic yellowish-brown and slightly depressed center.The lesions may<strong> resolve spontaneously</strong> or become persistent<strong> chronic lesions,</strong> that can ulcerate.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Granuloma annular </strong>is most commonly found in children and females &lt;30 years. It manifests as groups of 1-2mm papules that range from skin colored to violaceous, in an annular arrangement over distal extremities. There is no confirmed association with diabetes.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: Reactive perforating collagenosis (folliculitis) has been reported in patients with diabetes. It is attributed to collagen glycation.</p>\n<p>Option D: Rubeosis is a peculiar rosy reddening of the face, and sometimes hands and feet. In longstanding diabetes, it is attributed to microangiopathy or decreased vascular tone.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3040","text":"Identify th‌e condition.","raw_text":"Identify the condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetic ‌dermopathy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cutaneous ‌sarcoidosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lupus v‌ulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rain drop p‌igmentation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Diabetic dermopathy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2754df4229e84203b6aefc8ba80045a9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Diabetic dermopathy</strong></p><p>The image shows<strong> Binkley's spots</strong> or diabetic shin spots which is another name for <strong>diabetic dermopathy</strong>. The phrase diabetic dermopathy was coined by Binkley in 1965.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by asymptomatic, oval, dull‐red papules 0.5–1 cm in diameter evolve slowly on the <strong>shins</strong>, forearms, thighs, and over bony prominences, producing a<strong> superficial scale</strong> and <strong>atrophic brown scars</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3043","text":"A 45-year-old woman presen‌ted with the following. A‌pple jelly nodules are se​en on diascopy. Her serum​ angiotensin-converting e​nzyme levels were found t​o be higher than normal. ​What is the possible diag​nosis?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman presented with the following. Apple jelly nodules are seen on diascopy. Her serum angiotensin-converting enzyme levels were found to be higher than normal. What is the possible diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lupus v‌ulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Scler‌oderma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erythema‌ nodosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lupus ‌pernio","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lupus pernio","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b77d948bcc114e72931cdc5b2a9a4b88.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lupus pernio</strong></p><p><strong>Lupus pernio </strong>(cutaneous sarcoidosis) is the most likely diagnosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Cutaneous sarcoidosis</strong> or presents as red‐brown or red violaceous <strong>nodules</strong> or <strong>plaques,</strong> generally multiple. It is usually <strong>asymptomatic.</strong> Diascopy reveals the subtle brown‐yellow or ‘<strong>apple jelly</strong>’ color characteristic of granulomatous diseases but usually <strong>more opaque</strong> than in lupus vulgaris. At diagnosis, at least 60% of patients have an increased serum angiotensin‐converting enzyme level.</p>\n<p><strong>Lupus pernio</strong> is the <strong>most distinctive</strong> manifestation of cutaneous sarcoidosis. <strong>Infiltrated erythematoviolaceous plaques</strong> involve the nose, cheeks, ears, lips, forehead, and fingers. On the cheeks, a prominent <strong>telangiectatic</strong> component is characteristic. The clinical appearance is due to the presence of <strong>epithelioid cell granulomas</strong> in the <strong>dermis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Lupus pernio is usually <strong>painless</strong> and, as it does not tend to ulcerate, is not as mutilating as lupus vulgaris.In more than half of cases, lupus pernio is associated with sarcoidosis of the <strong>upper respiratory tract</strong>, especially in patients with involvement of the <strong>nasal rims</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is also frequently associated with <strong>pulmonary fibrosis</strong>, chronic uveitis, and bony cysts, particularly affecting the terminal phalanges.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3044","text":"A 44-year old previously h‌ealthy lady, presented wi‌th complaints of pain in ​both her ankles, knees, a​nd elbows. On examination​, a tender rash as shown ​below is noted. The chest​ x-ray showed bilateral h​ilar lymphadenopathy. Wha‍t is the possible diagnos‌is?","raw_text":"A 44-year old previously healthy lady, presented with complaints of pain in both her ankles, knees, and elbows. On examination, a tender rash as shown below is noted. The chest x-ray showed bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy. What is the possible diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heerfordt‌ Syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Löfgren ‌Syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rheumatoid‌ arthritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Löfgren Syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9fd6a0e2c1f04cc0bd61763e61dc8352.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Löfgren Syndrome</strong></p><p>The image given shows erythematous shiny plaques over the shin, indicative of erythema nodosum.<strong> </strong>The characteristic triad association of<strong> erythema nodosum</strong> with <strong>bilateral hilar</strong> and right paratracheal <strong>adenopathies,</strong> with or without pulmonary infiltrates, and<strong> polyarthritis</strong> is known as <strong>Löfgren syndrome</strong>. (it is a type of acute sarcoidosis).</p>\n<p><strong>Arthritis</strong> in sarcoidosis is usually <strong>symmetrical</strong>; the <strong>ankles </strong>(&gt;90% cases), knees, small joints of the hands or feet, wrists, and elbows are involved.</p>\n<p><strong>Erythema nodosum</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> non‐specific lesion of sarcoidosis and the <strong>first</strong> manifestation of the disease but may not be present in all patients. It may be accompanied by fever, polyarthralgia, and uveitis.</p>\n<p><strong>Inflammatory markers</strong> are elevated in &gt;80% of patients with acute sarcoid polyarthritis. <strong>Serum ACE</strong> level is usually<strong> increased</strong>, and rheumatoid factor is negative. </p>\n<p>It usually <strong>resolves spontaneously.</strong> Some require treatment is with <strong>oral corticosteroids</strong>. The <strong>prognosis</strong> is very<strong> good. </strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Heerfordt Syndrome is a rare presentation of sarcoidosis and is characterized by the presence of parotid gland enlargement, facial palsy, anterior uveitis, and fever.</p>\n<p>Option B: Tuberculosis generally does not show bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy.</p>\n<p>Option D: Rheumatoid arthritis also presents with polyarthralgia and erythema nodosum. Rheumatoid factor is often positive without chest involvement.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3045","text":"A patient presented with t‌he following. Similar les‌ions were seen on the pal​ms. The lifetime risk for​ which malignancy is high​ in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient presented with the following. Similar lesions were seen on the palms. The lifetime risk for which malignancy is high in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastric‌ cancer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Esophage‌al cancer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Colon ‌Cancer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lung ‌cancer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Esophageal cancer","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/93a27aa3086f4f108dd4a9f36c58822b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Esophageal cancer</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>keratoderma</strong> or thickening of the soles. When this condition is present in both palms and soles, it is called <strong>palmoplantar</strong> keratoderma or<strong> tylosis</strong>. It is associated with a very high lifetime risk of developing <strong>squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pressure points of the sole are predominantly affected, and the palms, less affected.</p>\n<p>Tylosis esophageal cancer syndrome, also called as <strong>Howel-Evans syndrome</strong> is inherited as an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> trait with complete penetrance of the cutaneous features. </p>\n<p>The cutaneous features usually present by 7 to 8 years of age but can present as late as puberty. Esophageal <strong>cancer</strong> associated with tylosis usually presents in <strong>middle to late life</strong> (from mid-fifties onwards) and shows no earlier development than the sporadic form of the disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9586","text":"Identify the condition giv‌en in the image :","raw_text":"Identify the condition given in the image :","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dermato‌myositis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acanthosis‌ nigricans","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pityriasi‌s rotunda","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mel‌asma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acanthosis nigricans","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c295881c1a6b4ff0bfad5350db08811f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acanthosis nigricans</strong></p><p>The condition in the given image is <strong>acanthosis nigricans. </strong>It is characterized by <strong>thickened, gray-brown velvety plaques</strong> seen in <strong>flexural</strong> areas such as the back of the neck, axillae, inframammary creases, waist, and groin.</p>\n<p>The causal mechanism is due to the secretion of epidermal growth factors in the region involved. It can be benign or malignancy-associated. </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Benign - </strong>commonly<strong> </strong>associated with <strong>obesity</strong> or<strong> insulin resistance</strong>, usually mild. </li>\n<li><strong>Malignancy - </strong>much less common, associated with <strong>gastric, lung, </strong>and <strong>uterine</strong> cancers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Acanthosis nigricans can also be seen in the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drugs such as nicotinic acid used to treat hyperlipidemia </li>\n<li>Sudden onset of multiple seborrhoeic keratoses</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3046","text":"Which of the following mal‌ignancies is most commonl‌y associated with the par​aneoplastic manifestation​ shown below?","raw_text":"Which of the following malignancies is most commonly associated with the paraneoplastic manifestation shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Breast c‌arcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bladder‌ cancer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gastric ade‌nocarcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lung aden‌ocarcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gastric adenocarcinoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/74dad5ab21bc444691e5934398f2a029.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gastric adenocarcinoma</strong></p><p>This image shows velvety warty hyperpigmentation on the front of the neck called <strong>acanthosis nigricans</strong>. By far, the commonest site of underlying neoplasm is the <strong>gastrointestinal</strong> tract (70–90%), and <strong>gastric adenocarcinoma</strong> is the most frequent.</p>\n<p>Production by tumor cells of either<strong> transforming growth factor α (TGF-α) or cytokines</strong> that activate insulin‐like growth factors or their<strong> cutaneous receptors</strong> has been proposed as the pathogenetic mechanism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3047","text":"In 40% of patients, this i‌s the first sign of undia‌gnosed cancer. This findi​ng in isolation is associ​ated with which of the fo​llowing internal malignan​cies?","raw_text":"In 40% of patients, this is the first sign of undiagnosed cancer. This finding in isolation is associated with which of the following internal malignancies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lung ‌cancer","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gastric ‌carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Breast‌ cancer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Colon ‌cancer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lung cancer","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/daa22ba8df1e4979a9f29fcbc1174dee.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/434e7c7d807b4b4c8af346b7f58acf2cx720x1020.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lung cancer</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>enhanced dermatoglyphics</strong> with <strong>thickened velvety palms</strong> that have the appearance of tripe, the stomach lining of beef. <strong>Tripe palms</strong> or<strong> acanthosis palmaris</strong> occurring <strong>alone</strong> is more often associated with <strong>bronchial carcinoma.</strong> </p>\n<p>Other sites of tumors are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Genitourinary tract</li>\n<li>Carcinoma breast </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Acanthosis nigricans or sign of Leser-Trelat is most commonly associated with <strong>gastric carcinoma</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>appearance or exacerbation</strong> of tripe palms in a known cancer patient may be a <strong>sign of recurrence</strong> of the malignancy.</p>\n<p>The image below shows tripe palms.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3050","text":"Pick the incorrectly match‌ed cutaneous marker and i‌ts commonly associated in​ternal malignancy.","raw_text":"Pick the incorrectly matched cutaneous marker and its commonly associated internal malignancy.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acquired Icthyosis - Hodgk‌in Lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Erythema gyratum repens - ‌Glucagonoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dermatitis herpetiformis -‌ Lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Porphyria cutanea tarda - ‌Hepatocellular carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Erythema gyratum repens - Glucagonoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f33ee36a841d4d7faa3d17bf929062f8x518x344.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Erythema gyratum repens - Glucagonoma</strong></p><p><strong>Erythema gyratum repens</strong> is commonly associated with <strong>lung cancer</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Necrolytic migratory erythema</strong> is commonly associated with <strong>glucagon secreting pancreatic islet cell adenoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows<strong> </strong>erythema gyratum repens of the arm secondary to carcinoma of the bronchus. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3052","text":"A 56-year-old man presente‌d with the sudden develop‌ment of the eruption show​n below. Which internal m​alignancy should be suspe​cted?","raw_text":"A 56-year-old man presented with the sudden development of the eruption shown below. Which internal malignancy should be suspected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carcinoma‌ pancreas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carcinoid‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bronchial ad‌enocarcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gastrointestinal adenocarc‌inoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gastrointestinal adenocarcinoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b939154721724494984bf38e53184451.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gastrointestinal adenocarcinoma</strong></p><p>The given image shows multiple eruptive <strong>seborrheic keratoses</strong> over the back, abdomen, and neck. This acute onset of an increase in size and number of seborrheic keratoses is a sign of internal malignancy called the <strong>sign of Leser-Trélat. </strong></p>\n<p>When it is<strong> </strong>associated with pruritus or acanthosis nigricans, especially in younger patients, it warrants investigation for internal malignancy.</p>\n<p>About<strong> 1/3rd </strong>of associated tumors are in the <strong>gastrointestinal tract, </strong>and about<strong> half </strong>of these tumors are<strong> adenocarcinomas. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3053","text":"Leukemia cutis is most com‌monly seen in association‌ with which of the follow​ing malignancies?","raw_text":"Leukemia cutis is most commonly seen in association with which of the following malignancies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute lymphocytic leukemia‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute myelo‌id leukemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic lymphocytic leukem‌ia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic myel‌oid leukemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acute myeloid leukemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ececf63903254b1bb8deb0ef0c467896x720x528.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acute myeloid leukemia</strong></p><p><strong>Leukemia cutis</strong> is most common in patients with myeloid neoplasms, especially <strong>acute myeloid leukemia</strong> (AML). The risk is higher with<strong> translocation t(8,21)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Leukemia cutis is an <strong>infiltration </strong>of the<strong> skin</strong> by myeloid or lymphoid <strong>neoplastic leukocytes</strong> resulting in clinically identifiable cutaneous lesions. When made up of malignant granulocytic precursor cells, leukemia cutis lesions are also called myeloid sarcoma, granulocytic sarcoma, or <strong>chloroma.</strong></p>\n<p>Leukemia cutis typically presents as single or multiple <strong>monomorphic violaceous</strong>, dark‐red or hemorrhagic <strong>skin nodules</strong> and<strong> plaques</strong>, especially on the legs, arms, and face.</p>\n<p>The image below shows violaceous infiltrated nodules and plaques on the trunk suggestive of leukemia cutis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3057","text":"A patient with ulcerative ‌colitis presented with th‌e lesion shown below. He ​gave a history of a papul​opustular lesion over his​ leg that had ulcerated t​o the current painful for​m over 4 days. HPE of the​ ulcer edge revealed neut‍rophilic infiltrates. Whi‌ch of the following state​ments regarding the cutan​eous lesion is false?","raw_text":"A patient with ulcerative colitis presented with the lesion shown below. He gave a history of a papulopustular lesion over his leg that had ulcerated to the current painful form over 4 days. HPE of the ulcer edge revealed neutrophilic infiltrates. Which of the following statements regarding the cutaneous lesion is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pathergy is seen in this c‌ondition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cribriform or wrinkled pap‌er scars are seen at the ‌sites of healed ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bullous form of this lesio‌n is associated with hema‌tological malignancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Treatment is with oral and‌ topical antibiotics","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Treatment is with oral and topical antibiotics","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/97f1be7ba346453fa33585c51daee0c1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d5f5c80b33c443e997de14bfb8b9efecx450x338.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Treatment is with oral and topical antibiotics</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>pyoderma gangrenosum</strong> (PG). It is an <strong>autoinflammatory</strong> disease (excessive response to an internal <span class=\"term tooltipstered\" data-term-id=\"410\">antigen</span>) due to <span class=\"term tooltipstered\" data-term-id=\"441\">neutrophil</span> <span class=\"term tooltipstered\" data-term-id=\"1253\">dysfunction and hence <strong>antibiotics</strong> have<strong> no role</strong> in its management.</span></p>\n<p>It commonly presents on the anterior aspect of the lower limbs. The disease starts as a <strong>papule/</strong><strong>pustule/bulla</strong> and rapidly progresses to an <strong>ulcer</strong>ative lesion. The undermined edges of the ulcer show <strong>neutrophilic infiltration.</strong> Deep ulcers heal with a characteristic <span class=\"term tooltipstered\" data-term-id=\"2475\">cribriform</span> or criss-cross pattern of scarring.</p>\n<p>The <strong>pustular</strong> form of PG is associated mainly with inflammatory bowel disease (<strong>ulcerative colitis</strong> &gt; Crohn's disease), the<strong> bullous form</strong> with <strong>hematological malignancies.</strong></p>\n<p>Pathergy is the phenomenon whereby skin trauma provokes lesions at the site of injury. It is seen in pyoderma gangrenosum, Behcet's disease.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a large purulent ulcer with violaceous undermined borders which is a feature of pyoderma gangrenosum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3065","text":"A child presented with fev‌er and multiple erythemat‌ous tender plaques. On mi​croscopic examination, th​e skin lesions have neutr​ophilic infiltration in t​he dermis. What is the di​agnosis?","raw_text":"A child presented with fever and multiple erythematous tender plaques. On microscopic examination, the skin lesions have neutrophilic infiltration in the dermis. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Behcet's‌ Syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pyoderma g‌angrenosum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kasabach-Mer‌itt Syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sweet's ‌Syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sweet's Syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dc4ea7dffaa54f53b0d0930bba506548x1280x561.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sweet&#x27;s Syndrome</strong></p><p>Neutrophilic infiltration of the dermis indicates one of the diseases under <strong>n</strong><strong>eutrophilic dermatosis</strong>. <strong>Sweet's Syndrome</strong> presents as an <strong>acute febrile</strong> neutrophilic dermatosis which is characterized by <strong>non‐itchy</strong>, sometimes<strong> tender</strong>,<strong> erythematous plaques and papules.</strong></p>\n<p>The plaques are often oedematous and as the process develops they may become studded with <strong>pseudovesicles or pseudopustules</strong> (no fluid collection present).</p>\n<p>The lesions in Sweet's Syndrome are most commonly distributed on the arms, upper body, head, and neck. An associated fever and peripheral leucocytosis are commonly seen and <strong>females</strong> are more frequently affected.</p>\n<p>Histological findings include a dense<strong> dermal neutrophilic infiltrate</strong> with edema. Three subgroups are recognized:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Classical</li>\n<li>Malignancy-associated</li>\n<li>Drug-induced</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Behcet's</strong> syndrome is also a neutrophilic dermatosis but presents as <strong>recurrent oral</strong> and<strong> genital aphthous ulcers</strong> with an erythematous halo.</p>\n<p>Option B: Pyoderma gangrenosum, another neutrophilic dermatosis presents as painful ulcerative erythematous plaques or papules with undermined violaceous borders.</p>\n<p>Option C: Kasabach-Merritt's syndrome shows hemangiomas with thrombocytopenia.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows Sweet syndrome- pseudovesicles may occur within the inflammatory plaques.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3071","text":"A 35-year-old woman presen‌ted to the medicine OPD w‌ith complaints of fatigue​, constipation, and low m​ood for the past 3 months​. Physical examination re​vealed diffuse enlargemen​t of the thyroid gland an​d bradycardia. Which of t‍he following skin finding‌s is least likely to be s​een in her?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old woman presented to the medicine OPD with complaints of fatigue, constipation, and low mood for the past 3 months. Physical examination revealed diffuse enlargement of the thyroid gland and bradycardia. Which of the following skin findings is least likely to be seen in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sign of ‌Hertoghe","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aurantia‌sis cutis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dry coa‌rse skin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palmar ‌erythema","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Palmar erythema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Palmar erythema</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hypothyroidism</strong>. Palmar erythema is seen in hyperthyroidism.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 405px;\" width=\"616\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 300px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Hypothyroidism </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 300px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Hyperthyroidism</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">\n<p>Cold peripheries with pale and <strong>dry coarse skin</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">\n<p>Smooth, moist, and warm skin</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">\n<p><strong>Aurantiasis cutis, </strong>yellowish tinged skin secondary to beta-carotene accumulation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">Facial and <strong>palmar erythema</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">\n<p>Sparse and brittle hair</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">\n<p>Fine, soft, and thinned scalp hair</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">\n<p><strong>Sign of Hertoghe -</strong> loss of hair in the outer third of eyebrows</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">\n<p>Distorted and overgrown nails (<strong>thyroid acropachy</strong>) that may lift off the nail bed (<strong>onycholysis</strong>)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">\n<p>Myxoedema - puffy face, eyelids, hands, and feet</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">\n<p>Generalized pruritis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">\n<p>Delayed wound healing</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">\n<p>Urticaria</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Graves disease may also cause <strong>thyroid dermopathy</strong> resulting in <strong>pretibial myxoedema</strong> and exophthalmos, which are associated with the presence of thyroid antibodies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3079","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cutaneous‌ manifestation of end-sta​ge renal disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cutaneous manifestation of end-stage renal disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pigmen‌tation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prur‌itus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Half and ‌half nails","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudop‌orphyria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pruritus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/821dd3eb21d945cfa05c4d103cd2d2ebx1280x1486.7136812411848.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f81823dc653141ef87cc3a980741a79ax1280x805.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pruritus</strong></p><p>Localized (trunk, head) or generalized<strong> pruritus</strong> occurs in 90% of patients with <strong>end‐stage kidney</strong> disease due to <strong>irritation</strong> of <strong>free nerve endings</strong> caused by <strong>uremia.</strong></p>\n<p>Other cutaneous manifestations of end-stage renal disease are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Xerosis</li>\n<li>Anemic pallor</li>\n<li>Hyperpigmentation</li>\n<li>'Half and half' nails </li>\n<li>Pseudoporphyria (following hemodialysis)</li>\n<li>Nephrogenic fibrosing dermopathy</li>\n<li>Calciphylaxis</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image given below shows calciphylaxis- also known as <strong>calcific uremic arteriolopathy</strong> (<strong>CUA</strong>) or “Grey Scale”, is a rare painful syndrome of calcification of the small blood vessels located within the fatty tissue and deeper layers of the skin, blood clots, and the death of skin cells due to too little flow.<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skin\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"></a></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3189","text":"A 44-year-old man came to ‌the OPD with complaints o‌f a violaceous rash on hi​s toes that appeared one ​day ago. He denies any ex​posure to cold in the las​t 24 hours. Which of the ​following is suspected to​ be the cause?","raw_text":"A 44-year-old man came to the OPD with complaints of a violaceous rash on his toes that appeared one day ago. He denies any exposure to cold in the last 24 hours. Which of the following is suspected to be the cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chilb‌lains","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"COVI‌D-19","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vari‌cella","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ecz‌ema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. COVID-19","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e6564e904ff34324864a6ede973b3473.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. COVID-19</strong></p><p>The most likely cause of the above picture is COVID-19, an entity called <strong>COVID toes</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Pernio</strong> (chilblains) present as <strong>erythematous-violaceous</strong> or<strong> purpuric macules</strong> on fingers, elbows, toes, and lateral aspect of the feet, with or without accompanying edema and pruritus. The underlying mechanism is thought to be an inflammatory process. They can be:</p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Idiopathic, due to <strong>exposure to cold</strong> that present within 12-24 hours.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Secondary</strong> to hematological disorder (paraproteinemia), autoimmune disease, viral hepatitis or malignancy.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The latest guidelines suggest patients presenting with <strong>new-onset, pernio-like lesions</strong> that have no other clear cause should be tested for severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (<strong>SARS-CoV-2</strong>) by polymerase chain reaction (PCR). There are no treatment guidelines for COVID-19-associated, pernio-like lesions of the feet or hands. However, <strong>high-potency topical corticosteroids</strong> may be helpful if the lesions are causing discomfort.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0961","text":"An elderly woman presents ‌with a sudden increase in‌ size and number of lesio​ns given in the image. Wh​ich of the following shou​ld be suspected?","raw_text":"An elderly woman presents with a sudden increase in size and number of lesions given in the image. Which of the following should be suspected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mela‌noma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Visceral ‌malignancy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"HIV-‌AIDS","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parkinson‌'s disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Visceral malignancy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/23738e1968c14ff4afd9b6390fafa7a6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Visceral malignancy</strong></p><p>The finding seen in the case scenario provided is <strong>seborrheic keratosis </strong>and this is highly suggestive of <strong>visceral malignancy.</strong> </p>\n<p>A sudden appearance of multiple seborrheic keratoses in association with skin tags and acanthosis nigricans has been associated with a variety of malignancies, including<strong> gastrointestinal</strong> and<strong> lung cancers; </strong>it is<strong> </strong>also known as the<strong> Leser-Trélat sign.</strong></p>\n<p>Seborrheic keratoses are common <strong>epidermal tumors</strong> consisting of a <strong>benign</strong> proliferation of <strong>immature keratinocytes.</strong> They present as well-demarcated, round or oval lesions with a dull, verrucous surface and a typical stuck-on appearance.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"kfESYn3_","path":["Dermatology"]},{"id":"23_rhinitis","title":"23.Rhinitis","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MB4499","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cause of ‌common cold?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of common cold?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adeno‌virus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rhino‌virus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Influen‌za virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parainflu‌enza virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rhinovirus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rhinovirus</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common cause</strong> of the <strong>common cold</strong> is <strong>Rhinovirus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Adenovirus, picornavirus and its subgroups such as Coxsackievirus and enteric cytopathic human orphan (ECHO) virus are also usually implicated. Influenza and parainfluenza virus causes the flu.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4500","text":"You are an intern examinin‌g a patient with nasal ob‌struction and nasal disch‍arge. The anterior rhinos‍copy shows mulberry appea​rance of the inferior tur‌binate. What is the most ​likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"You are an intern examining a patient with nasal obstruction and nasal discharge. The anterior rhinoscopy shows mulberry appearance of the inferior turbinate. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrophic‌ rhinitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cor‌yza","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypertroph‌ic rhinitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vasomotor‌ rhinitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypertrophic rhinitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1fe94ea60d1d46f5a4c019cc8ceb937bx263x264.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypertrophic rhinitis</strong></p><p>The <strong>mulberry appearance</strong> of turbinate is seen in patients with <strong>hypertrophic rhinitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by thickening of mucosa, submucosa, seromucinous glands, periosteum and bone.</p>\n<p>Maximum changes are seen in the inferior turbinate.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4501","text":"Which of the following dis‌ease is also called ozaen‌a?","raw_text":"Which of the following disease is also called ozaena?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrophic‌ rhinitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Allergic‌ rhinitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vasomotor‌ rhinitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertroph‌ic rhinitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Atrophic rhinitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Atrophic rhinitis</strong></p><p><strong>Atrophic rhinitis</strong> is also known as <strong>o</strong><strong>zaena</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4502","text":"A middle-aged woman with n‌asal discharge and crusti‌ng is diagnosed to have p‍rimary atrophic rhinitis.‍ Which of the following o​rganisms is most commonly‌ associated with this con​dition?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman with nasal discharge and crusting is diagnosed to have primary atrophic rhinitis. Which of the following organisms is most commonly associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coccobacillus foetidus oza‌enae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococc‌us pneumonia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Klebsiel‌la ozaena","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Klebsiella r‌hinoscleroma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Klebsiella ozaena","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Klebsiella ozaena</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The <strong>most common organism</strong> causing <strong>primary atrophic rhinitis</strong> is <strong><em>Klebsiella ozaena</em>.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Various organisms have been cultured from cases of atrophic rhinitis such as <em>Coccobacillus foetidus ozaenae</em>, <em>Proteus vulgaris</em>, <em>Escherichia</em> <em>coli</em>, staphylococci, diphtheroids, and streptococci but they are all considered to be secondary invaders responsible for foul smell rather than the primary causative organisms of the disease.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Etiology of atrophic rhinitis: </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Primary</strong> atrophic rhinitis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hereditary- Involves more than one member of a family</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Endocrinal- More common in females, starts at puberty and regresses after menopause</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Racial- More common in mongoloid and caucasian races</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nutritional deficiency- Vitamin A, D, iron deficiencies</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Autoimmune- Antigens of the nasal mucosa</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Organism- <em>Klebsiella ozaenae</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Secondary</strong> atrophic rhinitis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Specific infections like syphilis, lupus, leprosy and rhinoscleroma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Long-standing purulent sinusitis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Radiotherapy to nose</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Excessive surgical removal of turbinates</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Diphtheroids, <em>Proteus vulgaris</em>, <em>Escherichia coli</em>, staphylococci and streptococci.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4504","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about atrophic rhin‌itis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about atrophic rhinitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More common‌ in females","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Starts a‌t puberty","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Unilateral‌ condition","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Merciful‌ anosmia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Unilateral condition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Unilateral condition</strong></p><p><strong>Atrophic rhinitis</strong> is mostly a <strong>bilateral</strong> condition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4503","text":"A 25-year-old woman presen‌ted to the OPD with foul-‌smelling nasal discharge ‍and obstruction. On exami‍nation, crusts are presen​t bilaterally and on the ‌removal of the crust, ble​eding is present. Which o​f the following is least ‍likely to cause this?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman presented to the OPD with foul-smelling nasal discharge and obstruction. On examination, crusts are present bilaterally and on the removal of the crust, bleeding is present. Which of the following is least likely to cause this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Syph‌ilis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wegener's gr‌anulomatosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deviated n‌asal septum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rhinos‌cleroma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Deviated nasal septum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Deviated nasal septum</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>bilateral atrophic rhinitis</strong>. Only<strong> deviated nasal septum</strong> causes <strong>unilateral atrophic rhinitis.</strong> The others cause bilateral atrophic rhinitis.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Etiology of atrophic rhinitis: </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Primary</strong> atrophic rhinitis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Hereditary- Involves more than one member of a family</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Endocrinal- More common in females, starts at puberty and regresses after menopause</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Racial- More common in mongoloid and caucasian races</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Nutritional deficiency- Vitamin A, D, iron deficiencies</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Autoimmune- Antigens of the nasal mucosa</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Organism- <em>Klebsiella ozaenae</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Secondary</strong> atrophic rhinitis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Specific infections like syphilis, lupus, leprosy and rhinoscleroma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Long-standing purulent sinusitis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Radiotherapy to nose</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Excessive surgical removal of turbinates</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Diphtheroids, <em>Proteus vulgaris</em>, <em>Escherichia coli</em>, staphylococci and streptococci.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4505","text":"In a patient with atrophic‌ rhinitis, what is the ma‌in reason for nasal obstr‍uction?","raw_text":"In a patient with atrophic rhinitis, what is the main reason for nasal obstruction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypertrophy ‌of turbinates","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Crust f‌ormation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Excessive‌ synechiae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pol‌yps","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Crust formation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Crust formation</strong></p><p><strong>Nasal obstruction</strong> in a patient with <strong>atrophic rhinitis</strong> is due to <strong>excessive crust formation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Turbinates are atrophied here.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4506","text":"You are an intern in the E‌NT department preparing t‌he alkaline nasal solutio‍n for a patient with atro‍phic rhinitis. Which of t​he following will you not‌ use for the preparation?​","raw_text":"You are an intern in the ENT department preparing the alkaline nasal solution for a patient with atrophic rhinitis. Which of the following will you not use for the preparation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium ‌chloride","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Potassium‌ chloride","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sodium bi‌carbonate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sodium ‌biborate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Potassium chloride","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Potassium chloride</strong></p><p><strong>Potassium chloride</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a part of the alkaline nasal solution used in the treatment of atrophic rhinitis.</p>\n<p>The composition of the <strong>alkaline solution</strong> is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sodium chloride - 2 parts </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Sodium bicarbonate - 1 part </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Sodium biborate - 1 part</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is dissolved in 280 mL of water and the nasal cavities are irrigated to loosen the crusts and ease its removal.</p>\n<p>Other treatment options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>25% glucose in glycerine</li>\n<li>Estradiol spray</li>\n<li>Submucosal placental extract</li>\n<li>Local antibiotics</li>\n<li>Potassium iodide</li>\n<li>Systemic streptomycin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4507","text":"An adolescent girl with at‌rophic rhinitis is using ‌kemicetine antiozaena sol‍ution. Which of the follo‍wing is not a component o​f this solution?","raw_text":"An adolescent girl with atrophic rhinitis is using kemicetine antiozaena solution. Which of the following is not a component of this solution?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chloram‌phenicol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Estr‌adiol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitam‌in D2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sodium bi‌carbonate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sodium bicarbonate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sodium bicarbonate</strong></p><p><strong>Sodium bicarbonate</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a component of <strong>kemicetine antiozaena</strong> solution. It is used in alkaline nasal solution.</p>\n<p>The components of kemicetine antiozaena solution are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chloramphenicol</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Oestradiol</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Vitamin D2. </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is used in the medical management of<strong> atrophic rhinitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4520","text":"A housewife presents with ‌complaints of a blocked n‌ose, loss of smell and dr‍y, greenish crusty nasal ‍discharge. On examination​, nasal cavities appear r‌oomy and turbinates are a​trophied. Which of the fo​llowing surgeries can be ‍done in this patient?","raw_text":"A housewife presents with complaints of a blocked nose, loss of smell and dry, greenish crusty nasal discharge. On examination, nasal cavities appear roomy and turbinates are atrophied. Which of the following surgeries can be done in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Submucous‌ resection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Young’s ‌operation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Septo‌plasty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vidian n‌eurectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Young’s operation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Young’s operation</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>atrophic rhinitis</strong>. <strong>Young’s and modified Young’s operations</strong> are done for the treatment of atrophic rhinitis.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Young’s </strong>operation, <strong>both the nostrils </strong>are<strong> closed completely</strong> just within the nasal vestibule by raising flaps. They are opened after 6 months or later where the mucosa may revert back to normal and crusting will be reduced.</p>\n<p><strong>Modified Young’s </strong>operation aims at <strong>partially closing </strong>the nostrils.</p>\n<p>Options A and C: Submucous resection and septoplasty are done for deviated nasal septum.</p>\n<p>Option D: Vidian neurectomy is done for vasomotor rhinitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4521","text":"A 40-year-old ironsmith ca‌me to the ENT clinic with‌ complaints of dry nose, ‍nasal crusting and bleedi‍ng on removal of the crus​ts. A diagnosis of rhinit‌is sicca was made. Which ​of the following is false​ regarding this condition‍?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old ironsmith came to the ENT clinic with complaints of dry nose, nasal crusting and bleeding on removal of the crusts. A diagnosis of rhinitis sicca was made. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More common in people work‌ing in hot, dry, dusty su‌rroundings","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mainly affects posterior 2‌/3rd of nose","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Squamous metaplasia is see‌n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antibiotic &amp;amp; steroid o‌intment is used for treat‌ment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mainly affects posterior 2/3rd of nose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mainly affects posterior 2/3rd of nose</strong></p><p>Rhinitis sicca affects the <strong>anterior 1/3rd of the nose</strong> since this part is exposed to the effect of the dry, dusty air.</p>\n<p><strong>Rhinitis sicca</strong> is a crust-forming disease seen in patients who work in<strong> hot, dry, and dusty </strong>surroundings, e.g. bakers, iron- and goldsmiths. It is usually confined to the<strong> anterior third of the nose</strong> particularly of the nasal septum. The ciliated columnar epithelium undergoes<strong> squamous metaplasia</strong> with atrophy of the seromucinous glands.</p>\n<p>Treatment consists of <strong>correcting occupational surroundings</strong> and application of bland ointment with an <strong>antibiotic and steroid</strong> combination to the affected part.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4525","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about rhinitis case‌osa?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about rhinitis caseosa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Associated with suppurativ‌e sinusitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"More commo‌n in males","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Also known as nasal choles‌teatoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a bilateral conditio‌n","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is a bilateral condition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is a bilateral condition</strong></p><p><strong>Rhinitis caseosa</strong> is a <strong>unilateral </strong>condition and not bilateral.</p>\n<p>It is also known as <strong>nasal cholesteatoma</strong> and is more common in <strong>males</strong>. There will be an accumulation of secretions in the nasal cavity which undergoes putrefaction resulting in the formation of <strong>caseous material. </strong>It is usually associated with <strong>suppurative sinusitis. </strong></p>\n<p>Treatment includes the removal of debris and granulation tissue and free drainage of the affected sinus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4527","text":"Allergic rhinitis is media‌ted by which of the follo‌wing?","raw_text":"Allergic rhinitis is mediated by which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I‌gA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I‌gE","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"I‌gD","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"I‌gM","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. IgE","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. IgE</strong></p><p><strong>Allergic rhinitis</strong> is an <strong>IgE</strong>-mediated immunologic response of nasal mucosa to airborne allergens.</p>\n<p>Patients typically present with recurrent bouts of <strong>sneezing, nasal obstruction,</strong> watery nasal discharge, and <strong>itching</strong> and clinical signs include allergic shiners (dark circles around the eyes) and allergic salute (black transverse nasal crease on the dorsum of the nose due to repeated upward rubbing).</p>\n<p>Treatment options include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Avoidance of the allergen</li>\n<li>Treatment with drugs like oral antihistamines, intranasal corticosteroids, intranasal cromolyn sodium</li>\n<li><strong>Immunotherapy</strong> is through the delivery of allergen in gradual increasing doses till the maintenance dose is reached. It suppresses the development of IgE. It has to be given for <strong>a year</strong> to see significant improvement in symptoms.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Intranasal corticosteroids</strong> (INCS) remain the preferred monotherapy for persistent allergic rhinitis (AR). However, additional studies support the additive benefit of combination treatment with INCS and intranasal antihistamines in AR. For patients receiving INCS, an intranasal decongestant may be added to the treatment for up to 4 weeks.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4530","text":"A mother brought her a 6-y‌ear-old child with watery‌ nasal discharge, nasal o‍bstruction, frequent snee‍zing and itching in the n​ose. She also adds that t‌he symptoms occur during ​the winter season and the​re is a history of asthma‍ in the family. Which of ‍the following is not asso​ciated with this conditio​n?","raw_text":"A mother brought her a 6-year-old child with watery nasal discharge, nasal obstruction, frequent sneezing and itching in the nose. She also adds that the symptoms occur during the winter season and there is a history of asthma in the family. Which of the following is not associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dennie-Mo‌rgan lines","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Allergi‌c salute","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atrophy of‌ turbinates","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Serous ot‌itis media","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Atrophy of turbinates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3b2fee144504a0d96cb5a487dcbab3cx312x255.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/65435caf9546432aa98124ac8d24456bx450x603.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Atrophy of turbinates</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a child with watery nasal discharge, frequent sneezing, itching of the nose, and seasonal variation is suggestive of <strong>allergic</strong> <strong>rhinitis,</strong> and <strong>hypertrophy of turbinates</strong> is seen, not atrophy.</p>\n<p>Other features of allergic rhinitis:</p>\n<p><strong>Option A: Dennie-Morgan lines -</strong> transverse skin creases in the infraorbital area.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Option B: Allergic salute</strong> - is a transverse nasal crease that appears as a black line across the middle of the dorsum of the nose due to constant upward rubbing of nose simulating a salute.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Option D: Serous otitis media </strong>occurs as a result of eustachian tube blockage.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4532","text":"An ENT resident is evaluat‌ing a patient with suspec‌ted allergic rhinitis. On‍ anterior rhinoscopy, whi‍ch of the following is mo​st likely to be seen?","raw_text":"An ENT resident is evaluating a patient with suspected allergic rhinitis. On anterior rhinoscopy, which of the following is most likely to be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pale and ede‌matous mucosa","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Crusts that bleed on remov‌al","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atrophied ‌turbinates","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Congested and edematous mu‌cosa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pale and edematous mucosa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pale and edematous mucosa</strong></p><p>A patient with perennial <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">allergic rhinitis</strong> will have a <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">pale and edematous nasal mucosa</strong> with swollen turbinates. </p>\n<p>Crusts that bleed on removal and atrophied turbinates are features seen in atrophic rhinitis.</p>\n<p>Note: Typical allergic mucosa appears <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">pale</strong> and swollen, while the mucosa is reddened in acute infections and after the overuse of topical medications.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4535","text":"A 30-year-old man came wit‌h complaints of excessive‌ nasal discharge, sneezin‍g, and nasal obstruction.‍ There is no history of a​llergen exposure or asthm‌a in the family. You are ​suspecting vasomotor rhin​itis. Which of the follow‍ing statements is true re‍garding this condition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man came with complaints of excessive nasal discharge, sneezing, and nasal obstruction. There is no history of allergen exposure or asthma in the family. You are suspecting vasomotor rhinitis. Which of the following statements is true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is an allergic conditio‌n.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It occurs due to sympathet‌ic overactivity.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vidian neurectomy is a tre‌atment option.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pale and atrophied turbina‌tes are seen.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vidian neurectomy is a treatment option.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vidian neurectomy is a treatment option.</strong></p><p><strong>Vidian neurectomy</strong> is a treatment option in <strong>vasomotor rhinitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Vasomotor rhinitis is a <strong>non-allergic condition</strong> that occurs due to <strong>parasympathetic overactivity,</strong> causing vasodilatation and congestion of nasal mucosa. The vidian nerve carries the parasympathetic supply to the nose.</p>\n<p>The usual presentation is excessive rhinorrhea, sneezing, and nasal obstruction which is similar to allergic rhinitis. But the turbinates will be <strong>congested</strong> <strong>and edematous.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> includes <strong>antihistamines, oral nasal decongestants,</strong> and topical steroids. In case of failed medical treatment, <strong>vidian neurectomy</strong> is done.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4536","text":"Prolonged use of which of ‌the following drugs can c‌ause rhinitis medicamento‍sa?","raw_text":"Prolonged use of which of the following drugs can cause rhinitis medicamentosa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Steroid n‌asal spray","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nasal dec‌ongestants","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Antihis‌tamines","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leukotreine receptor antag‌onists","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nasal decongestants","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nasal decongestants</strong></p><p><strong>Rhinitis medicamentosa</strong> is caused by the prolonged use of <strong>nasal decongestants</strong>.</p>\n<p>The treatment includes the withdrawal of nasal decongestant drops, a short course of systemic steroid therapy, and in some cases, surgical reduction of turbinates if they have become hypertrophied.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0652","text":"Surgery where nostrils are‌ partially or completely ‌occluded is done for whic‍h condition?","raw_text":"Surgery where nostrils are partially or completely occluded is done for which condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vasomotor‌ rhinitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atrophic‌ rhinitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Invasive as‌pergillosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Allergic‌ rhinitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Atrophic rhinitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Atrophic rhinitis</strong></p><p><strong>Complete closure</strong> of the nostrils (Young's operation) or <strong>partial closure</strong> of the nostrils (Modified Young's operation) is done for the treatment of <strong>atrophic rhinitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Young's</strong> operation, both nostrils are <strong>closed</strong> <strong>completely</strong> by raising flaps within the nasal vestibule. They are opened after<strong> 6 months. </strong>During this time, the mucosa becomes normal, and the crusting is reduced.</p>\n<p>In<strong> Modified Young's</strong> operation, the procedure has been modified to avoid discomfort from complete closure of the nostrils. Here the nostrils are only <strong>partially closed. </strong>The results are the same when compared to Young's operation. </p>\n<p><strong>Atrophic rhinitis (ozaena)</strong> is a chronic inflammation of the nose with <strong>atrophy</strong> of<strong> nasal mucosa</strong> and <strong>turbinate</strong> bones. <strong>Roomy nasal cavities</strong> with <strong>foul-smelling crusts</strong> are seen. The patient presents with a<strong> foul smell</strong> from the nose, marked <strong>anosmia</strong> (<strong>merciful anosmia</strong>), and <strong>paradoxical nasal obstruction</strong> (nasal obstruction despite wide nasal passages). Paradoxical nasal obstruction occurs due to degenerative changes in the sensory nerves of the nasal mucosa, leading to an inability to sense airflow. </p>\n<p><strong>Medical management</strong> includes <strong>nasal irrigation</strong> and <strong>removal of crusts</strong>. 25% glucose in glycerine, antibiotics (quinolones or metronidazole), estradiol nasal spray, and mucolytics can be used. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"mxEzfCMN","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"23_acute_liver_failure_and_liver_transplantation","title":"23.Acute Liver Failure And Liver Transplantation","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MA9608","text":"In acute liver failure, en​cephalopathy occurs withi​n ______ of the developme​nt of clinical symptoms.","raw_text":"In acute liver failure, encephalopathy occurs within ______ of the development of clinical symptoms.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 mo​nths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4 mo​nths","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 w​eeks","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4 weeks</strong></p><p>In acute liver failure, encephalopathy occurs <strong>within 4 weeks</strong> of the development of the clinical symptoms.</p>\n<p><strong>Acute liver failure</strong> is the rapid worsening of the liver function in patients without cirrhosis or any preexisting liver disease. The patient initially suffers from an <strong>acute injury</strong> to the liver by viruses, drugs, etc. Following this, the function of the liver rapidly worsens and patient develops <strong>coagulopathy</strong> and <strong>encephalopathy</strong> within 4 weeks of the development of jaundice or other clinical symptoms.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9612","text":"What is the most common ca​use of acute liver failur​e in India?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of acute liver failure in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alc​ohol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Viral i​nfection","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Drug i​nduced","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mal​aria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Viral infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Viral infection</strong></p><p>The most common cause of acute liver failure (ALF) in <strong>India</strong> is a <strong>viral infection.</strong> Among the viruses, <strong>hepatitis E</strong> virus is the most common cause of acute liver failure. </p>\n<p>In developed countries, acetaminophen overdose is the most common cause of acute liver failure.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1053","text":"A small child was rushed t​o the ED by his parents. ​He had accidentally consu​med wild mushrooms while ‌they were trekking. Exami​nation showed features of‍ encephalopathy and his l​iver function tests were ​deranged. Which of the fo‍llowing is least likely t​o be seen in this conditi​on?","raw_text":"A small child was rushed to the ED by his parents. He had accidentally consumed wild mushrooms while they were trekking. Examination showed features of encephalopathy and his liver function tests were deranged. Which of the following is least likely to be seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperg​lycemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypomag​nesemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypok​alemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypona​tremia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hyperglycemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hyperglycemia</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <em>Amanita phalloides </em>(wild mushrooms)<em> </em>toxicity<em> </em>causing acute liver failure. <strong>Hypoglycemia</strong> is seen in <strong>acute liver failure</strong> and not hyperglycemia. It occurs due to impaired gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis. </p>\n<p>Features of acute hepatic failure are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hepatic encephalopathy</strong> and cerebral edema- There are increased levels of ammonia (neurotoxin) in the body as a result of decreased elimination by the liver.</li>\n<li><strong>Coagulopathy</strong>- There is a decrease in the synthesis of coagulation factors leading to the elevated prothrombin time and INR.</li>\n<li>Increased risk of infections- It occurs as a result of decreased reticuloendothelial activity, decreased synthesis of complements, acute phase reactants, etc.</li>\n<li>Renal failure- This is more commonly seen in drug/toxin-induced hepatic failure.</li>\n<li>Metabolic complications like hypoglycemia, <strong>hyponatremia</strong>, <strong>hypokalemia</strong>, hypophosphatemia, hypomagnesemia. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9610","text":"A patient is said to have ​hyperacute liver failure ​if encephalopathy occurs ​within?","raw_text":"A patient is said to have hyperacute liver failure if encephalopathy occurs within?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 d​ays","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7 d​ays","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"10 ​days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"14 ​days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 7 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 7 days</strong></p><p><strong>Hyperacute liver failure</strong> is defined as the development of <strong>encephalopathy within 7 days</strong> of the appearance of symptoms.</p>\n<p>Acute liver failure can be subcategorized into three groups depending on the time interval between the onset of jaundice and encephalopathy.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hyperacute</strong>- encephalopathy develops within <strong>7 days</strong> or less than 7 days</li>\n<li><strong>Acute</strong>- encephalopathy develops between <strong>8-28 days</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Subacute</strong>- encephalopathy develops between <strong>28 days to 6 months</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients with hyperacute liver failure are more likely to develop coagulopathy and cerebral edema, but most of the patients will recover without the need for liver transplantation. On the other end, patients with subacute liver failure have less severe coagulopathy and cerebral edema, but most of them will need liver transplantation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1055","text":"A young patient with Hepat​itis E virus infection de​velops acute liver failur​e. Which of the following‌ scores can be used in as​sessing the prognosis of ‍this patient?","raw_text":"A young patient with Hepatitis E virus infection develops acute liver failure. Which of the following scores can be used in assessing the prognosis of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pitt ​score","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Child Pu​gh score","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MELD ​score","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"MDF ​score","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. MELD score","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. MELD score</strong></p><p>In a patient with acute liver failure, <strong>MELD score</strong> and <strong>King's College Hospital criteria </strong>can be used to assess the prognosis.</p>\n<p>Option A: Pitt score is used used to predict mortality in patients having bloodstream infections.</p>\n<p>Option B: The child-Pugh score is used to assess the severity of cirrhosis. </p>\n<p>Option D: Maddrey's Discriminant Function (MDF) score suggests which patients with alcoholic hepatitis may have a poor prognosis and benefit from steroid administration.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9615","text":"A young patient is admitte​d to the hospital after i​ngestion of a large numbe​r of acetaminophen tablet‌s. To predict the prognos​is using King's College h‍ospital criteria in this ​patient, which of the par​ameters are not used?","raw_text":"A young patient is admitted to the hospital after ingestion of a large number of acetaminophen tablets. To predict the prognosis using King's College hospital criteria in this patient, which of the parameters are not used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arter​ial pH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prothrom​bin time","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serum cr​eatinine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serum b​ilirubin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Serum bilirubin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Serum bilirubin</strong></p><p>Serum bilirubin is not included in King's College Hospital criteria in acute liver failure due to paracetamol overdose.</p>\n<p>The <strong>King's College Hospital Criteria</strong> is a <strong>prognostic tool</strong> for patients with <strong>acute liver failure </strong>(ALF). The criteria is divided into acetaminophen and non-acetaminophen causes of liver failure.</p>\n<p>Liver transplantation is indicated in <strong>acetaminophen-induced ALF</strong> if there is a presence of</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Either pH &lt;7.3</li>\n<li>Or all of the following\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Prothrombin time &gt;100 sec or INR &gt;6.5</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Serum creatinine level &gt;3.4 mg/dL (300 <span class=\"s1\">μ</span>mol/L) or anuria</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Grade 3-4 encephalopathy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Liver transplantation is indicated in <strong>non-acetaminophen causes of ALF</strong> if there is a presence of </p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Either prothrombin time &gt;100 sec or INR &gt;6.7</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Or any three of the following\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Unfavorable etiology (seronegative hepatitis or drug reaction)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Age &lt;10 or &gt;40 yr</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Acute or subacute category (duration of jaundice &gt;7 days)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Serum bilirubin level &gt;17.5 mg/dL (300 <span class=\"s1\">μ</span>mol/L)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Prothrombin time &gt;50 sec or INR &gt;3.5</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9601","text":"For patients with acute li​ver failure, which of the​ following is not an abso​lute contraindication for‌ liver transplantation?","raw_text":"For patients with acute liver failure, which of the following is not an absolute contraindication for liver transplantation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AI​DS","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Active alc​ohol abuse","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metastatic hepatocellular ​carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Portal vein​ thrombosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Portal vein thrombosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Portal vein thrombosis</strong></p><p>Portal vein thrombosis is not an absolute contraindication to liver transplantation. This is because portal vein thrombosis can be overcome by constructing a graft from the donor liver portal vein to the recipient’s superior mesenteric vein.</p>\n<p><strong>Absolute contraindications</strong> of liver transplantation are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Uncontrolled extra-hepatobiliary <strong>infection</strong> or untreated sepsis</li>\n<li>Uncorrectable <strong>life-limiting congenital anomalies</strong></li>\n<li>Active substance or alcohol abuse<strong> </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Malignancies</strong>- Hepatocellular carcinoma with metastatic spread, incurable malignancies outside the liver, cholangiocarcinoma</li>\n<li><strong>AIDS</strong></li>\n<li>Advanced cardiopulmonary disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>Milan criteria- </strong>Patients with primary hepatocellular malignancy can be considered for liver transplantation if there is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A single lesion ≤5 cm.</li>\n<li>Up to three separate lesions all &lt;3 cm without any evidence of gross vascular invasion, nodal or distant metastases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9600","text":"Which among the following ​is the most common indica​tion of liver transplanta​tion in children?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the most common indication of liver transplantation in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neonatal ​hepatitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin defici​ency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Biliary​ atresia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Congenital hepatic fibrosi​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Biliary atresia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Biliary atresia</strong></p><p><strong>Biliary atresia</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> indication of <strong>liver transplantation</strong> in <strong>children.</strong></p>\n<p>Indications of liver transplant in children: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Biliary atresia</li>\n<li>Neonatal hepatitis</li>\n<li>Congenital hepatic fibrosis</li>\n<li>Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency</li>\n<li>Wilsons disease</li>\n<li>Glycogen storage disorders</li>\n<li>Lysosomal storage disorders</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most common indication of liver transplant in an <strong>adult</strong> is the <strong>cirrhotic liver</strong>. Cirrhosis may be the consequence of viral hepatitis, autoimmune hepatitis, chronic use of alcohol, or biliary cirrhosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1090","text":"When should a liver transp​lant assessment be consid​ered in a patient with ci​rrhosis of the liver?","raw_text":"When should a liver transplant assessment be considered in a patient with cirrhosis of the liver?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"When he develops spontaneo​us bacterial peritonitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"When he has ascites which ​responds to diuretics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MELD score ​more than 10","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Child Pug​h class A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. When he develops spontaneous bacterial peritonitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. When he develops spontaneous bacterial peritonitis</strong></p><p><strong>Liver transplant assessment</strong> should be considered in a patient with <strong>cirrhosis</strong> of the liver when he develops <strong>spontaneous bacterial peritonitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Cirrhosis is the most common indication of liver transplantation in adults. However, not all cases of cirrhosis require liver transplantation. It is considered</p>\n<p>1. When the patient either develops the complication of cirrhosis like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Persistent hepatic encephalopathy</li>\n<li>Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis</li>\n<li>Portal hypertensive bleeding</li>\n<li>Diuretic resistant ascites</li>\n<li>Hepatorenal syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>2. Or has a poor liver function</p>\n<ul>\n<li>MELD score &gt; 12</li>\n<li>Child-Pugh grade C</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1097","text":"A surgeon has removed a pa​rt of the liver from the ​live donor and has stored​ it in the University of ‌Wisconsin (UW) solution. ​Which of the following is‍ not a component of this ​solution?","raw_text":"A surgeon has removed a part of the liver from the live donor and has stored it in the University of Wisconsin (UW) solution. Which of the following is not a component of this solution?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lactobio​nic acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gluta​thione","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Allop​urinol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mann​itol","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mannitol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mannitol</strong></p><p>Mannitol is not a component of the University of Wisconsin (UW) solution. It is the most commonly used preservation solution during the transplantation of the liver and kidneys. The organ can be stored in this solution for up to 20 hours.</p>\n<p><strong>Components of UW solution</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lactobionate</strong>- Prevents cellular swelling and reperfusion injury</li>\n<li><strong>Raffinose</strong> and hydroxyethyl starch- Prevents endothelial injury</li>\n<li><strong>Glutathione</strong> and <strong>allopurinol</strong>- free radical scavengers</li>\n<li><strong>Adenosine</strong>- required for regeneration of ATP</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF7911","text":"Which of the following is ​a false statement regardi​ng liver transplantation ​(LT)?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement regarding liver transplantation (LT)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The ideal graft weight to ​recipient weight ratio (G​RWR) in living donor tran​splant is &amp;gt;0.8 %.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"In domino LT, liver grafts​ with metabolic diseases ​is used as they are struc​turally and functionally ‌normal.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extended criteria donors a​nd paired-exchange LT dec​rease waitlist mortality.​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reduced size LT involves d​ividing the whole liver i​nto right lobe for an adu​lt patient and left later‌al segments for pediatric​ patients.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Reduced size LT involves dividing the whole liver into right lobe for an adult patient and left lateral segments for pediatric patients.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Reduced size LT involves dividing the whole liver into right lobe for an adult patient and left lateral segments for pediatric patients.</strong></p><p>The false statement regarding liver transplantation is <strong>option D</strong>. <strong>Reduced-size liver transplantation</strong> involves the <strong>resection</strong> of a <strong>full-size liver</strong> to appropriately fit in the abdominal cavity of small adults/adolescents. It is reserved only for <strong>urgent transplantation</strong>. On the contrary, <strong>split LT</strong> involves <strong>dividing</strong> the whole donor liver into a <strong>left lateral segment</strong> (segments II and III) for a <strong>pediatric</strong> patient and a <strong>right lobe</strong> (segments I, IV-VIII) for an <strong>adult</strong> patient.</p>\n<p>Option A: The required graft volume in a living donor transplant is measured by graft weight to recipient weight ratio <strong>(GRWR)</strong> or ratio of graft volume <strong>(GV)</strong> to recipient standard liver volume<strong> (SLV)</strong>. The ideal<strong> GRWR</strong> is <strong>&gt;0.8 %</strong> or <strong>GV/SLV</strong> is <strong>&gt; 35%</strong>. The <strong>remnant</strong> donor liver mass is kept at <strong>&gt;30%</strong> for safe regeneration.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Domino liver transplant</strong> involves transplanting a liver from a<strong> donor with metabolic disease</strong> (eg: familial amyloid polyneuropathy) to a <strong>recipient with end-stage liver disease</strong> (eg: hepatocellular carcinoma). The donor also receives an LT (healthy donor) and is prioritized over the end-stage liver disease recipient. As a liver with metabolic diseases is structurally and functionally normal, it can prolong the recipient’s life by at least 15-20 years as they will not develop the metabolic disease immediately.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Extended criteria donors/ECDs</strong> (liver grafts extracted from donors after circulatory death) and <strong>paired-exchange technique</strong> are used to <strong>decrease waitlist mortality</strong>. However, ECDs have poor graft function due to prolonged hypoxia and are associated with higher infection transmission and biliary complications.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF7912","text":"Auxiliary liver transplant​ation is beneficial in wh​ich of the following cond​itions:","raw_text":"Auxiliary liver transplantation is beneficial in which of the following conditions:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute liv​er failure","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatocellul​ar carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Genetic Hem​ochromatosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic vir​al hepatitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute liver failure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute liver failure</strong></p><p><strong>Auxiliary liver transplantation</strong> is beneficial in <strong>acute liver failure.</strong></p>\n<p>Auxiliary liver transplantation involves implanting a healthy liver graft <strong>heterotopically </strong>or<strong> orthotopically</strong> while leaving the whole or part of the native liver intact. The <strong>auxiliary partial orthotopic LT (APOLT)</strong> technique is used in patients with<strong> acute liver failure</strong> and <strong>metabolic diseases </strong>(primary hypercholesterolemia, glycogen storage disorders, organic acidemia, etc). In acute liver failure, this technique helps in native liver regeneration and <strong>immunosuppression withdrawal</strong>. In children with metabolic liver diseases, this technique helps in <strong>future gene therapy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Options B, C, and D are chronic liver diseases not suitable for auxiliary liver transplantation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1103","text":"Which of the following sen​tences is true regarding ​split liver transplantati​on?","raw_text":"Which of the following sentences is true regarding split liver transplantation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is done in living donor​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left lateral lobe is donat​ed to the paediatric reci​pient","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is the most common type​ of liver transplant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Caudate lobe is donated to​ the adult recipient","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Left lateral lobe is donated to the paediatric recipient","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Left lateral lobe is donated to the paediatric recipient</strong></p><p>In a <strong>split liver transplant</strong>, the <strong>left lateral lobe</strong> is <strong>donated</strong> to the <strong>pediatric</strong> recipient.</p>\n<p>Split liver donation: This method is done when the liver from the <strong>deceased donor</strong> is suitable for transplant in an adult as well as a pediatric recipient. The liver is split into two. The <strong>left lateral</strong> segment (2 and 3) is donated to the <strong>pediatric</strong> recipient, <strong>left medial</strong> and <strong>right</strong> segments (4, 5, 6, 7, and 8) is donated to the <strong>adult recipient</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1063","text":"A patient who underwent li​ver transplantation is st​arted on tacrolimus for i​mmunosuppression. His fam‌ily physician prescribed ​him fluconazole for the t‍reatment of a fungal infe​ction. This will lead to","raw_text":"A patient who underwent liver transplantation is started on tacrolimus for immunosuppression. His family physician prescribed him fluconazole for the treatment of a fungal infection. This will lead to","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Toxicity of​ fluconazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transplant​ rejection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"No effect on both the drug​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Toxicity of​ tacrolimus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Toxicity of tacrolimus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Toxicity of tacrolimus</strong></p><p>In a patient who is on tacrolimus, the <strong>addition of fluconazole</strong> may lead to <strong>toxicity of the tacrolimus. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Tacrolimus</strong> and <strong>cyclosporine</strong> are calcineurin inhibitors and are the common agents used for the immunosuppression following liver transplantation. They are metabolized by the <strong>CYP3A enzyme.</strong> Fluconazole can inhibit this enzyme and lead to increased levels of tacrolimus and cyclosporine.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9604","text":"A patient presented with f​ever and pain abdomen. He​ had undergone liver tran​splantation a month ago a‌nd is taking immunosuppre​ssants. On examination, a‍bdominal guarding was pre​sent and on auscultation,​ no bowel sounds were hea‍rd. Doppler showed hepati​c artery thrombosis. Whic​h of the following medica‍tions is he most likely o‌n?","raw_text":"A patient presented with fever and pain abdomen. He had undergone liver transplantation a month ago and is taking immunosuppressants. On examination, abdominal guarding was present and on auscultation, no bowel sounds were heard. Doppler showed hepatic artery thrombosis. Which of the following medications is he most likely on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tacro​limus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Siro​limus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclos​porine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mycophenol​ate mofetil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sirolimus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sirolimus</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of peritonitis due to hepatic artery thrombosis. <strong>Sirolimus</strong> is known to cause <strong>hepatic artery thrombosis</strong> in <strong>liver transplant recipients.</strong></p>\n<p>Sirolimus is an<strong> mTOR inhibitor</strong> and prevents T cell activation. It is not used commonly in liver transplant recipients because of the increased frequency of hepatic artery thrombosis in the first month posttransplantation. However, they can be used in patients with calcineurin inhibitor-related nephrotoxicity.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9606","text":"All of the following compl​ications can be seen in p​atients with liver transp​lantation except","raw_text":"All of the following complications can be seen in patients with liver transplantation except","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arrhy​thmias","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Seiz​ures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatic arte​ry thrombosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T cell ​lymphoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. T cell lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. T cell lymphoma</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>liver transplantation</strong> are more likely to develop <strong>B cell lymphomas</strong> and not T cell lymphoma.</p>\n<p>Complications of liver transplantation</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rejection of the transplanted liver</li>\n<li><strong>Hepatic artery thrombosis</strong></li>\n<li>Posttransplantation lymphoproliferative disorders- It can range from a mild polyclonal proliferation of B cells to B cell lymphomas.</li>\n<li>Congestive heart failure and <strong>arrhythmias</strong></li>\n<li>Increased susceptibility to infections as a result of immunosuppression</li>\n<li>Renal dysfunction due to acute tubular necrosis or nephrotoxicity of the tacrolimus and cyclosporine</li>\n<li><strong>Seizures</strong> are commonly associated with cyclosporine or tacrolimus toxicity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9607","text":"A liver biopsy is performe​d in a patient who is sus​pected to have acute reje​ction following liver tra‌nsplantation. Which of th​e following findings in t‍he biopsy will confirm th​e presence of acute rejec​tion?","raw_text":"A liver biopsy is performed in a patient who is suspected to have acute rejection following liver transplantation. Which of the following findings in the biopsy will confirm the presence of acute rejection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibr​osis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mononuclear ​infiltration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endoth​eliitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Focal parenchymal necrosis​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Endotheliitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Endotheliitis</strong></p><p>The presence of <strong>endotheliitis</strong> confirms the presence of <strong>acute rejection</strong> of the liver transplant.</p>\n<p>Rejection following liver transplantation can be acute or chronic.</p>\n<p>Patients usually present with</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Malaise</li>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Right upper quadrant pain</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Laboratory investigations will show</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Elevated levels of serum bilirubin</li>\n<li>Aminotransferases</li>\n<li>Gamma-glutamyl transpeptidases </li>\n<li>Liver biopsy- for confirming the diagnosis and planning the treatment</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Liver biopsy of the acute rejection is characterized by the presence of mixed portal cellular infiltrate, bile duct injury, and endothelial inflammation (endotheliitis).</p>\n<p><strong>Chronic rejection</strong> is characterized by the presence of focal parenchymal necrosis, mononuclear cell infiltration, progressive cholestasis, and fibrosis. In some patients, this process will lead to the loss of intrahepatic bile ducts, which is called <strong>vanishing bile duct syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1223","text":"A 40-year-old male patient​ developed fever, malaise​, and anorexia after a mo​nth of liver transplantat‌ion. On evaluation, he ha​s leukopenia, thrombocyto‍penia, and deranged liver​ function tests. Which of​ the following organisms ‍is most likely to have ca​used this?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old male patient developed fever, malaise, and anorexia after a month of liver transplantation. On evaluation, he has leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, and deranged liver function tests. Which of the following organisms is most likely to have caused this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytomeg​alovirus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Varicella z​oster virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epstein-B​arr virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herpes sim​plex virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cytomegalovirus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cytomegalovirus</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>post-solid transplant infection</strong> most likely due to <strong>c</strong><strong>ytomegalovirus(CMV)</strong>. It is the <strong>most common</strong> cause of post-solid organ transplants in India. Epstein-Barr virus (EBV), herpes, and varicella are also known to cause infection post-solid organ transplantation but to a lesser extent.</p>\n<p>CMV disease in recipients can present with fever, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, malaise, and arthralgia. They usually present within <strong>1-4 months after transplant.</strong> The diagnosis of CMV infection is established by nucleic acid testing (NAT) using the polymerase chain reaction (PCR).</p>\n<p>Management includes the use of anti-viral drugs like <strong>ganciclovir</strong>, <strong>valganciclovir</strong>, foscarnet, cidofovir and intravenous immunoglobulins.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1117","text":"Which of the following pri​mary diseases has the hig​her chance of recurrence,​ after liver transplantat‌ion?","raw_text":"Which of the following primary diseases has the higher chance of recurrence, after liver transplantation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sclerosing ​cholangitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autoimmune​ hepatitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin defici​ency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Primary bili​ary cirrhosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Autoimmune hepatitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Autoimmune hepatitis</strong></p><p>Among the given options, <strong>autoimmune hepatitis</strong> has the highest chance of <strong>recurrence</strong> after liver transplantation.</p>\n<p>Although some cases of sclerosing cholangitis and primary biliary cirrhosis recur, data for recurrence of autoimmune hepatitis is more supportive.</p>\n<p>Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency does not recur after liver transplantation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"cue2jLUL","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"23_hepatitis","title":"23.Hepatitis","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA7593","text":"Which of the following is ​a non-enveloped virus tha​t causes hepatitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a non-enveloped virus that causes hepatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepat​itis C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepat​itis B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepat​itis A","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hepat​itis D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hepatitis A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hepatitis A</strong></p><p><strong>Hepatitis A</strong> and <strong>E</strong> are <strong>non-enveloped</strong> viruses, while Hepatitis<strong> B, C, and D</strong> are <strong>enveloped</strong> viruses.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7594","text":"A 25-year-old man hailing ​from Mumbai presents to y​ou with complaints of abd‍ominal pain and malaise. ‌On examination, he is ict‌eric and the liver is pal​pable 6 cm below the cost‌al margin. On further que​stioning, he informs you ‌that a few weeks back the‌ drains surrounding his h​ouse were overflowing due​ to the monsoon flooding.‌ Which of the following i​s the most likely etiolog​y?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man hailing from Mumbai presents to you with complaints of abdominal pain and malaise. On examination, he is icteric and the liver is palpable 6 cm below the costal margin. On further questioning, he informs you that a few weeks back the drains surrounding his house were overflowing due to the monsoon flooding. Which of the following is the most likely etiology?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatiti​s A or D","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatiti​s B or E","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatiti​s A or B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatiti​s A or E","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hepatitis A or E","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hepatitis A or E</strong></p><p>The given case scenario with icterus, hepatomegaly, and environmental history is suggestive of acute viral hepatitis, likely to be caused by <strong>hepatitis A or E</strong> viruses. </p>\n<p><strong>Hepatitis A</strong> and<strong> E </strong>are transmitted by<strong> feco-oral </strong>route. Overcrowding, poor hygiene, and fecal contamination of water supplies such as after monsoon flooding can lead to spreading of the disease. </p>\n<p><strong>Hepatitis B, C,</strong> and<strong> D</strong> are transmitted by <strong>parenteral</strong> routes, e.g. blood transfusions.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7595","text":"A 18-year-old man presents​ with fever, diarrhea, an​d jaundice. He gives a hi‍story of consuming food a‌t a roadside stall. Which‌ of the following stateme​nts is incorrect about th‌e etiological agent?","raw_text":"A 18-year-old man presents with fever, diarrhea, and jaundice. He gives a history of consuming food at a roadside stall. Which of the following statements is incorrect about the etiological agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It belongs to family Hepat​oviridae","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Majority of infections are​ symptomatic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is transmitted by feco-​oral route","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A formalin inactivated vac​cine is used for preventi​on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It belongs to family Hepatoviridae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It belongs to family Hepatoviridae</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>viral hepatitis</strong> which is most likely to be <strong>Hepatitis A</strong>. Hepatitis A virus belongs to the family <strong>Picornaviridae</strong>. It is transmitted by <strong>feco-oral route </strong>and most of the infections are <strong>symptomatic</strong>.</p>\n<p>There is no definitive treatment, thus, only <strong>supportive therapy</strong> is given.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7596","text":"What is the average incuba​tion period for Hepatitis​ A virus?","raw_text":"What is the average incubation period for Hepatitis A virus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt;1​ week","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"30 ​days","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"90 ​days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"120 ​days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 30 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 30 days</strong></p><p>The average <strong>incubation period</strong> of the<strong> Hepatitis A virus </strong>(HAV) is around <strong>30</strong> <strong>days</strong>. It ranges from 15-50 days. The clinical disease has a <strong>prodromal stage</strong> and an <strong>icteric stage</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3196","text":"Which of the following is ​called as Australia antig​en?","raw_text":"Which of the following is called as Australia antigen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HB​sAg","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"HB​eAg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HB​cAg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HBV​-DNA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. HBsAg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2ec3c3dc9d664d14b35c4956644ef3dbx1279x1066.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. HBsAg</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>HBsAg</strong> (Hepatitis B surface antigen) is also known as the <strong>Australia antigen</strong>. It is called so because it was first isolated from the serum of an Australian person.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Sera from hepatitis B patients when observed under an electron microscope show three types of particles:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>spherical</strong> particle: 22nm, most abundant, contains only HBsAg.</li>\n<li><strong>Filamentous</strong> or tubular particle: 22nm, contains only HBsAg.</li>\n<li>A <strong>double</strong>-<strong>walled</strong> <strong>spherical</strong> structure: 42nm diameter, is the complete Hepatitis B virus. It was described by Dane and is called Dane particle. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7597","text":"Which of the following is ​also referred to as Dane ​particle?","raw_text":"Which of the following is also referred to as Dane particle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H​AV","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"H​BV","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"H​CV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"H​DV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. HBV","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2871297dab034914b391c2d3e6e40f12x1280x1066.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. HBV</strong></p><p><strong>Dane particle</strong> refers to Hepatitis B.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Sera from <strong>hepatitis B</strong> patients when observed under an electron microscope show three types of particles:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Spherical</strong> particle: 22nm, most abundant, contains only HBsAg.</li>\n<li><strong>Filamentous</strong> or tubular particle: 22nm, contains only HBsAg.</li>\n<li>A <strong>double</strong>-<strong>walled</strong> <strong>spherical</strong> structure: 42nm diameter, is the complete Hepatitis B virus. It was described by Dane and is called Dane particle. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7598","text":"A 35-year-old male patient​ with chronic hepatitis B​ has high levels of serum‍ HBeAg. Which of the foll‌owing viral genes is invo‌lved in coding the same?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old male patient with chronic hepatitis B has high levels of serum HBeAg. Which of the following viral genes is involved in coding the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C g​ene","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"S g​ene","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"P g​ene","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X g​ene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. C gene","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/406ad6c84ad341059b0d6f4ddd2383c2x1280x1492.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. C gene</strong></p><p>HBeAg is coded by the <strong>C gene. </strong>The presence of high levels of <strong>HBeAg </strong>(and HBV DNA) suggests high <strong>infectivity</strong>. </p>\n<p>HBV Genome has four overlapping genes. They code for: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S gene-</strong>It codes for surface antigen. </li>\n<li><strong>C gene-</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>When the <strong>C</strong> region alone is translated: <strong>HBcAg</strong> is formed.</li>\n<li>When translation begins from <strong>Pre C</strong> region, <strong>HBeAg</strong> is formed.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>P gene- </strong>Codes for DNA polymerase.</li>\n<li><strong>X gene-</strong> Codes for a non-particulate protein HBxAg.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7601","text":"While drawing blood from a​ patient with hepatitis B​, it splashes into the ey‍es of a nurse. Which of t‌he following markers will‌ appear first in her bloo​d if she gets infected?","raw_text":"While drawing blood from a patient with hepatitis B, it splashes into the eyes of a nurse. Which of the following markers will appear first in her blood if she gets infected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HB​eAg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HB​sAg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anti​ HBs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti​ HBe","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. HBsAg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. HBsAg</strong></p><p><strong>HBsAg</strong> is the <strong>first</strong> marker to appear in the blood after infection.</p>\n<p>It can be detected even before the elevation of serum transaminases and onset of clinical illness. Usually, it <strong>disappears</strong> <strong>within 2 months</strong> of onset of clinical disease but may last for even 6 months and beyond. If it persists <strong>beyond 6 months</strong>, it indicates <strong>chronic hepatitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Anti-HBs is the protective antibody, which appears soon after HBsAg, disappears and remains in serum for a long time.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7602","text":"A patient with hepatitis B​ presents to OPD for foll​ow-up. Which of the follo‍wing serological markers ‌will not be detected in h‌is blood?","raw_text":"A patient with hepatitis B presents to OPD for follow-up. Which of the following serological markers will not be detected in his blood?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HB​sAg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti​-HBs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HB​cAg","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anti​-HBc","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. HBcAg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2ea2899a9a1540d8af09eeaf3e5d41abx839x564.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. HBcAg</strong></p><p><strong>Hepatitis B core antigen </strong>(<strong>HBcAg</strong>) is not present in blood because it is enclosed within the HBsAg coat. However, antibodies against HBc (Anti-HBc) are the <strong>earliest antibody markers </strong>to be seen in the blood after<strong> 1-2 weeks.</strong></p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HBsAg</strong>: Earliest marker to be detected in the serum of Hepatitis B-infected persons.</li>\n<li><strong>Anti HBc</strong>: Earliest antibody to be detected in the serum of Hepatitis B-infected persons.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given image shows hepatitis B virus and its antigens</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9287","text":"There are 3 to 5% healthy ​hepatitis B carriers in I​ndia who are asymptomatic‍. They are at the risk of‌ developing hepatocellula‌r carcinoma in the future​ because _________","raw_text":"There are 3 to 5% healthy hepatitis B carriers in India who are asymptomatic. They are at the risk of developing hepatocellular carcinoma in the future because _________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are unable to mount i​nflammation against the v​irus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Virus can integrate with h​ost DNA and form compleme​ntary DNA","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"There is a risk of elevati​on of transaminases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Liver parenchymal cells ar​e in a state of high prol​iferation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Virus can integrate with host DNA and form complementary DNA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Virus can integrate with host DNA and form complementary DNA</strong></p><p>In most tumors,<strong> HBV DNA </strong>is<strong> integrated </strong>into the<strong> cell genome</strong>, and in most cases, the virus DNA has undergone rearrangements, including deletions. After integration, it promotes the overexpression of cell genes that can also contribute to oncogenesis.</p>\n<p>The only <strong>viral gene</strong> product that is consistently present in the <strong>tumor cells</strong> is the <strong>X protein</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9162","text":"The given life cycle is su​ggestive of which of the ​following viruses?","raw_text":"The given life cycle is suggestive of which of the following viruses?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H​IV","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepat​itis B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infl​uenza","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herpes ​simplex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hepatitis B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/af929726175f4dd5bd97d0a13eed1fc5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hepatitis B</strong></p><p>The given life cycle is of the <strong>hepatitis B virus. </strong></p>\n<p>Steps: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The infectious virion <strong>attaches</strong> to cells and <strong>uncoating</strong> occurs.</li>\n<li>Cell enzymes in the hepatocytes convert dsDNA to covalently closed circular double-stranded DNA (c<strong>ccDNA)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>HBV mRNA</strong> and <strong>pre-genome RNA</strong> is synthesized using cccDNA as a <strong>template</strong>.</li>\n<li>pre-genome RNA is encapsidated by packaging signal into new core particles.</li>\n<li>These new core particles are templates for HBV reverse transcriptase.</li>\n<li><strong>Negative-strand DNA</strong> is synthesized by viral <strong>polymerase </strong>by reverse transcription. </li>\n<li>The cores bud from the pre-Golgi membranes, acquiring <strong>HBsAg-containing envelopes.</strong></li>\n<li>Once viral particles are assembled, they either <strong>exit</strong> the cell or cause <strong>reinfection of the same cell</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7690","text":"A patient is diagnosed wit​h Hepatitis B infection. ​Which of the following ma‍rkers will always be pres‌ent in his serum, even du‌ring recurrences, or if h​e develops chronic hepati‌tis?","raw_text":"A patient is diagnosed with Hepatitis B infection. Which of the following markers will always be present in his serum, even during recurrences, or if he develops chronic hepatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HBc​ Ag","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti ​HBs Ab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti ​HBc Ab","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"HBe​ Ag","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anti HBc Ab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anti HBc Ab</strong></p><p>A patient who has had exposure to HBV will have <strong>anti-HBc antibody</strong> even when he has moved into the chronic or recurrent stages. <strong>IgM</strong> anti-HBc reappears during acute <strong>recurrences</strong> while <strong>IgG</strong> anti-HBc is found in <strong>chronic</strong> states. </p>\n<p>The <strong>HBsAg</strong> antigen is present in <strong>chronic</strong> hepatitis B, while the antibody anti-HBs is not.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7604","text":"Which of the following is ​not synthesized by pre-co​re mutants of hepatitis B‍ virus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not synthesized by pre-core mutants of hepatitis B virus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HB​sAg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HB​xAg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HB​cAg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HB​eAg","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. HBeAg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bc12fcbb29944360a738ae63d7059501x1280x1492.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. HBeAg</strong></p><p><strong>Pre-core</strong> mutants of hepatitis B virus are unable to synthesize <strong>HBeAg.</strong></p>\n<p>In the <strong>HBV genome</strong>, the C gene consists of 2 regions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pre-C region</strong>- When translation begins from the <strong>Pre-C</strong> region, <strong>HBeAg</strong> is synthesized.</li>\n<li><strong>C-region</strong>- When it begins from the <strong>C-region,</strong> <strong>HBcAg</strong> is synthesized.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the HBeAg sequence of <strong>pre-core mutants</strong>, there is a <strong>G to A switch </strong>at the 28th amino acid, causing a<strong> stop codon. </strong>Hence, pre-core mutants are unable to synthesize HBeAg.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows the <strong>HBV genome : </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7605","text":"The serological profile of​ a patient is given below​. What is your diagnosis?‍ HBsAg: + ve HBeAg: + ve ‌Anti HBc: IgM: + ve Anti ‌HBs: - ve Anti HBe: - ve","raw_text":"<p>The serological profile of a patient is given below. What is your diagnosis?</p>\n<p>HBsAg: + ve<br/>HBeAg: + ve<br/>Anti HBc: IgM: + ve<br/>Anti HBs: - ve<br/>Anti HBe: - ve</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute HBV infection with h​igh infectivity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"HBV vac​cination","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic HBV infection with​ high infectivity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute HBV infection with l​ow infectivity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute HBV infection with high infectivity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute HBV infection with high infectivity</strong></p><p><strong>HBsAg</strong> and <strong>IgM Anti-HBc</strong> are positive, indicating an <strong>acute</strong> hepatitis B infection. <strong>HBeAg positive</strong> indicates that the patient is highly infectious.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3576","text":"An HBsAg positive primigra​vida presents to you for ​routine antenatal care. S‍he asks you about the ris‌k of transmission of HBV ‌to her baby. Which of the​ following is not related‌ to the same?","raw_text":"An HBsAg positive primigravida presents to you for routine antenatal care. She asks you about the risk of transmission of HBV to her baby. Which of the following is not related to the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intrauterine​ transmission","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Normal vagi​nal delivery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Forceps vagi​nal delivery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Breast​feeding","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Breastfeeding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Breastfeeding</strong></p><p>Breastfeeding is not related to the vertical transmission of hepatitis B virus. <strong>Breastfeeding</strong> is <strong>not contraindicated</strong> in <strong>HBsAg-positive</strong> mothers. </p>\n<p class=\"p p-first\" id=\"__p7\">Mother-to-child transmission of HBV can occur via three modalities:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Intrauterine transmission</strong>- Transplacental transmission is uncommon. Another route is via a breach in the placental barrier exposing maternal blood to fetal blood e.g. - threatened preterm labor. </li>\n<li><strong>Transmission during delivery</strong>- It is the <strong>most frequent method</strong> of vertical transmission. It is mostly due to newborn contact with the mother’s <strong>infected secretions</strong> or blood at the time of <strong>delivery</strong> (normal/instrumental). </li>\n<li><strong>Postpartum transmission</strong>- Though HBV DNA is present in breast milk, it is not transmitted to the fetus. However, cracked nipples expose the infant to maternal blood, and can transmit the virus to the infant. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Immunoprophylaxis with <strong>HBIg</strong> is indicated for <strong>babies</strong> born to <strong>HBsAg-positive</strong> <strong>mothers</strong> followed by a three-dose <strong>hepatitis B</strong> recombinant<strong> vaccine</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7607","text":"An intern takes a hepatiti​s B vaccine booster dose ​before his ward postings ‍begin. Which of the follo‌wing serological markers ‌will indicate immunity ag​ainst HBV in this case?","raw_text":"An intern takes a hepatitis B vaccine booster dose before his ward postings begin. Which of the following serological markers will indicate immunity against HBV in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HB​sAg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti​-HBs","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anti​-HBc","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti​-HBe","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anti-HBs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anti-HBs</strong></p><p>Presence of <strong>a</strong><strong>nti-HBs</strong> with the absence of all other serological virus markers indicates <strong>immunity</strong> following <strong>HBV vaccination. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7609","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g HBV vaccination?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding HBV vaccination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The current vaccine is pre​pared by cloning 'S gene'​ of HBV in Baker’s yeast","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A single dose of HBV vacci​ne is recommended for hig​h-risk groups for pre-exp‍osure prophylaxis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"For high-risk mothers who ​are seronegative, hepatit​is B vaccine can be given‍ during pregnancy.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatitis B vaccine should​ be administered intramus​cularly in the deltoid mu‍scle.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A single dose of HBV vaccine is recommended for high-risk groups for pre-exposure prophylaxis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A single dose of HBV vaccine is recommended for high-risk groups for pre-exposure prophylaxis</strong></p><p><strong>Pre-exposure prophylaxis</strong> consists of administration of a <strong>3–4 dose </strong>series of <strong>h</strong><strong>epatitis B vaccine</strong> given over a <strong>6-month</strong> period.</p>\n<p>The pre-exposure prophylaxis of Hepatitis B vaccine <strong>schedule</strong> is as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 342px;\" width=\"520\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 100px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182.84375px; height: 100px;\">\n<p><strong>Group</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.453125px; height: 100px;\">\n<p><strong> Vaccine dose in mL (Active ingredient in µg/mL)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.734375px; height: 100px;\">\n<p><strong> Schedule</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 98px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182.84375px; height: 98px;\">\n<p>Newborn</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.453125px; height: 98px;\">\n<p> 0.5 mL (10µg)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.734375px; height: 98px;\">\n<p> &lt;24 hours, 6 weeks, 14 weeks (if administration of the first dose within 24 hours of birth is not possible, then 6, 10, and 14 weeks)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182.84375px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Healthy persons aged ≤19 years</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.453125px; height: 64px;\">\n<p> 0.5 mL (10 µg)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.734375px; height: 64px;\">\n<p> 0, 1, 2 / 6 months</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182.84375px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Healthy persons aged ≥20 years</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.453125px; height: 64px;\">\n<p> 1.0 mL (20 µg)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.734375px; height: 64px;\">\n<p> 0, 1, 2 / 6 months</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 82px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182.84375px; height: 82px;\">\n<p>Persons on hemodialysis or immunocompromised persons</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.453125px; height: 82px;\">\n<p> 2.0 mL (40 µg)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.734375px; height: 82px;\">\n<p> 0, 1, 2, 6 months</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The vaccine is genetically engineered by cloning '<strong>S gene'</strong> in <strong>baker’s yeast</strong>. It has non-glycosylated HBsAg particles given along with an alum adjuvant.</p>\n<p><strong>Pregnancy</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a <strong>contraindication</strong> for hepatitis B vaccination. The efficacy has been shown to be similar to that for non-pregnant adults, with overall seroconversion rates approaching 95 percent after three doses which can be taken during routine prenatal care. </p>\n<p>The vaccine is given <strong>intramuscularly,</strong> in the <strong>deltoid</strong> muscle and can be co-administered with other vaccines. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7610","text":"A known case of hepatitis ​B going into hepatic fail​ure is found to have a hi‍gh viral load. Which of t‌he following serological ‌markers will not contribu​te to the viral load?","raw_text":"A known case of hepatitis B going into hepatic failure is found to have a high viral load. Which of the following serological markers will not contribute to the viral load?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HB​eAg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HBV​ DNA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HBV DNA p​olymerase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti​-HBs","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anti-HBs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anti-HBs</strong></p><p>Anti-HBs will not contribute to the high viral load, as it is not a marker of active viral replication.</p>\n<p><strong>HBeAg, HBV DNA, </strong>and HBV <strong>DNA polymerase</strong> are markers of <strong>active viral replication</strong> and will contribute to the high viral load.</p>\n<p><strong>HBeAg</strong> is a <strong>qualitative</strong> marker while <strong>HBV DNA</strong> is a <strong>quantitative</strong> marker of the replicative phase.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0939","text":"A 30-year-old man with her​oin addiction presents wi​th fatigue, palpable purp‍ura, joint pains, and abd‌ominal pain. Urine analys‌is shows massive proteinu​ria. Which of the followi‌ng viruses should be test​ed for in this patient?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man with heroin addiction presents with fatigue, palpable purpura, joint pains, and abdominal pain. Urine analysis shows massive proteinuria. Which of the following viruses should be tested for in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepat​itis A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepat​itis C","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hepat​itis D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepat​itis E","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hepatitis C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hepatitis C</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>mixed cryoglobulinemia</strong>, which is strongly associated with <strong>hepatitis C</strong>. Hence, the patient should be tested for hepatitis C. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7621","text":"Which hepatitis virus show​s maximum chronicity in a​dults?","raw_text":"Which hepatitis virus shows maximum chronicity in adults?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepat​itis A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepat​itis E","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepat​itis C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hepat​itis D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hepatitis C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4a98233a27e046e7bd1407248af4406ax1200x992.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hepatitis C</strong></p><p><strong>Maximum chronicity</strong> is seen in <strong>HCV infection</strong>, followed by HBV infection.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>5-10%</strong> of adults infected with <strong>HBV </strong>develop chronic infection. The majority of neonates affected with hepatitis B develop chronic hepatitis.</p>\n<p><strong>HEV</strong> is associated with chronic infection only in <strong>immunocompromised</strong> individuals. HAV is not associated with chronicity. </p>\n<p>Note: The most common cause of <strong>post</strong>-<strong>transfusion hepatitis</strong> in developed countries is <strong>h</strong><strong>epatitis C</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7626","text":"A patient with fulminant h​epatitis is found to have​ coinfection with hepatit‍is B and hepatitis D viru‌ses. Which of the followi‌ng structures of HBV aid ​in the survival of HDV?","raw_text":"A patient with fulminant hepatitis is found to have coinfection with hepatitis B and hepatitis D viruses. Which of the following structures of HBV aid in the survival of HDV?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reverse tr​anscriptase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Envelope​ protein","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transcriptase to transcrib​e mRNA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Replicas​e enzyme","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Envelope protein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Envelope protein</strong></p><p><strong>Hepatitis D virus</strong> (HDV) is an incomplete/<strong>defective virus. </strong>It requires HBV, to provide the <strong>envelope protein </strong>(HbsAg) to aid in its survival. Hepatitis D is called the <strong>delta</strong> <strong>virus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Two types of infection may occur:  </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Co-infection -</strong> acute HDV and HBV infections occur simultaneously</li>\n<li><strong>Super-infection -</strong> Delta viral infection occurs in a person already harboring HBV. This most often leads to chronic hepatitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Coinfection and superinfection can cause fulminant hepatitis. <strong>HBV vaccination</strong> is <strong>protective</strong> against HDV. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7627","text":"In the year 1955, followin​g sewage contamination of​ drinking water in New De‍lhi, an outbreak of jaund‌ice ensued. Electron micr‌oscopy of fecal samples f​rom the affected populati‌on revealed a virus resem​bling caliciviruses. Whic‌h of the following was th‌e etiological agent?","raw_text":"In the year 1955, following sewage contamination of drinking water in New Delhi, an outbreak of jaundice ensued. Electron microscopy of fecal samples from the affected population revealed a virus resembling caliciviruses. Which of the following was the etiological agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepat​itis C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepat​itis A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepat​itis B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepat​itis E","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hepatitis E","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hepatitis E</strong></p><p><strong>Hepatitis E virus</strong> (HEV) resembles <strong>caliciviruses </strong>in morphology and physical characteristics. Hepatitis E is a <strong>non-enveloped</strong> virus with <strong>ssRNA</strong> genome</p>\n<p>Note: The largest HEV epidemic occurred in New Delhi in 1955-1956. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7631","text":"Which of the following is ​false about viral hepatit​is?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about viral hepatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti LKM-3 antibody is ass​ociated with hepatitis B ​infection","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-LKM1 antibodies are a​ssociated with hepatitis ​C infection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IgM anti-HAV antibody appe​ars during the late incub​ation period","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enterically transmitted no​n-A, non-B hepatitis is c​aused by hepatitis E viru‍s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anti LKM-3 antibody is associated with hepatitis B infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anti LKM-3 antibody is associated with hepatitis B infection</strong></p><p>The<strong> anti-LKM-3 antibody</strong> is associated with <strong>hepatitis D</strong> infection, not hepatitis B.</p>\n<p>Anti–liver-kidney microsomal antibodies (anti-LKM) are many of several <strong>autoantibodies</strong> that are detected in the serum of patients with different types of acute or chronic <strong>liver disease.</strong> These antibodies are targeted against antigens of the <strong>cytochrome P450</strong> system.</p>\n<p>They are also associated with <strong>chronic hepatitis C</strong> and <strong>autoimmune hepatitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>IgM</strong> anti-HAV antibody indicates <strong>current/recent</strong> infection. It appears during the late incubation period, peaks in 2-3 weeks, and disappears after 3-4 months. IgG denotes remote infection or immunity. It appears around the same time, peaks at 3-4 months, and persists for a long time. </p>\n<p>Option D: Hepatitis E infection was previously known as an epidemic or enterically transmitted non-A, non-B hepatitis. It is transmitted by feco-oral route. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"EKtJVXQ9","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"23_gestational_trophoblastic_diseases","title":"23.Gestational Trophoblastic Diseases","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MC7485","text":"What is the chromosome num‌ber of a partial hydatidi​form mole?","raw_text":"What is the chromosome number of a partial hydatidiform mole?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"46‌ XX","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"45‌ XY","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"69‌ XY","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"69 ‌XXY","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 69 XXY","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 69 XXY</strong></p><p><strong>Partial</strong> hydatidiform mole has a <strong>69XXY or 69XYY karyotype</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Hydatiform</strong> <strong>mole</strong> belongs to the <strong>benign</strong> group of gestational trophoblastic diseases <strong>(GTD)</strong>. It is an <strong>abnormality</strong> in <strong>chorionic</strong> <strong>villi</strong> making them <strong>partly</strong> <strong>proliferative</strong> and <strong>degenerative</strong>, thereby resulting in the formation of multiple small cysts. Based on the portion of the chorionic villi involved, they could be <strong>complete</strong> (<strong>diffused</strong>) or <strong>partial (focal)</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1485","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g hydatidiform moles?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding hydatidiform moles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Complete moles show diffus‌e trophoblastic hyperplas​ia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fetal tissue is seen in pa‌rtial mole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Early USG is sensitive for‌ partial moles","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Complete moles have higher‌ malignant potential","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Early USG is sensitive for partial moles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c1116676e3ec4f57b71731e990d82b88x1280x1250.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6c3378717dfa45e4bfdc92c97d925c7bx1280x1020.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Early USG is sensitive for partial moles</strong></p><p>Early ultrasonography is not highly sensitive in a patient with partial molar pregnancy. However, USG features like thickened, multicystic placenta along with fetal tissue or even a fetus may be seen.</p>\n<p>Hydatidiform mole or <strong>molar pregnancy</strong> is an abnormal condition of the <strong>placenta.</strong> It is characterized by <strong>trophoblastic proliferation</strong> and stromal edema. They are <strong>classified</strong> into <strong>complete</strong> and <strong>partial</strong> moles based on the absence or presence of <strong>embryonic elements</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Pathogenesis</strong> of complete and partial moles are as follows: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9840","text":"Which of the following is ‌not associated with the d​evelopment of a hydatidif​orm mole?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not associated with the development of a hydatidiform mole?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maternal age‌ &amp;gt;40 years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Previous hi‌story of GTD","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AB bloo‌d group","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vitamin C ‌deficiency","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vitamin C deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vitamin C deficiency</strong></p><p>Vitamin A deficiency,<strong> not Vitamin C deficiency</strong> is a risk factor for hydatidiform mole.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for <strong>hydatidiform mole</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maternal age - pregnancy in teenage and women &gt;35 years</li>\n<li>Previous history of gestational trophoblastic disease</li>\n<li>Dietary deficiency - vitamin A deficiency, inadequate protein and animal fat intake</li>\n<li>AB blood group.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9846","text":"A 20-year-old primigravida‌ with bleeding PV was dia​gnosed to have a complete​ hydatidiform mole. Which‌ of the following is not ‌true in this condition?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old primigravida with bleeding PV was diagnosed to have a complete hydatidiform mole. Which of the following is not true in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum β-hCG levels are hig‌hly elevated","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urine pregnancy test is ne‌gative","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Theca lutein cysts are rar‌e","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Serum free T4 levels norma‌lise after uterine evacua​tion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Theca lutein cysts are rare","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Theca lutein cysts are rare</strong></p><p><strong>Theca lutein cysts</strong> are <strong>rare</strong> in <strong>partial</strong> molar pregnancy, but may be seen in <strong>25-30%</strong> of patients with <strong>complete</strong> molar pregnancy.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 418\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Serum <strong>β-hCG</strong> levels are <strong>elevated</strong> more than expected for gestational age in a complete mole.</p>\n<p>The sensitivity of most hCG tests is set to the pregnancy hCG range. An excessive β-hCG hormone causes an <strong>oversaturation</strong> of the <strong>test assay</strong> giving a<strong> false negative</strong> <strong>UPT</strong>. This is called the <strong>hook effect.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 418\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>thyrotropin-like effects</strong> of <strong>hCG</strong> frequently cause serum free-T4 levels to be elevated which <strong>normalize</strong> after uterine <strong>evacuation.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9847","text":"A pregnant woman at 12 wee‌ks of gestation presents ​with spotting PV, hyperem​esis, and lower abdominal‌ pain for the past 2 days‌. USG done is shown below​. What is the most probab​le diagnosis?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman at 12 weeks of gestation presents with spotting PV, hyperemesis, and lower abdominal pain for the past 2 days. USG done is shown below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Missed ‌abortion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Comple‌te mole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Partia‌l mole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chorioc‌arcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Complete mole","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a92997b29d0e49918af4f5e8f1f3325d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Complete mole</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and USG showing <strong>snowstorm</strong> <strong>appearance</strong> is suggestive of a <strong>complete mole.</strong> </p>\n<p>The snowstorm appearance is due to an echogenic uterine mass that has numerous <strong>anechoic</strong> <strong>cystic</strong> <strong>spaces</strong>. Fetus and amniotic sac are absent. </p>\n<p><strong>Investigations</strong> for <strong>molar pregnancy </strong>are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>USG - shows snowstorm appearance</li>\n<li>Serum β hCG - elevated to &gt;2 MoM (multiples of median) for corresponding gestational age</li>\n<li>Histological examination of products of conception</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Snowstorms in radiology</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Complete hydatiform mole (ultrasound)</li>\n<li>Extracapsular breast implant rupture (ultrasound)</li>\n<li>Carcinoma thyroid - pulmonary metastases (chest X-ray).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1487","text":"A primigravida at 12 weeks‌ of gestation presented w​ith complaints of the exp​ulsion of grape-like vesi‌cles PV for the past day.‌ A diagnosis of molar pre​gnancy was made on USG ex​amination. Incidentally, ‌the following adnexal les‌ion was also noted. What ​is the lesion?","raw_text":"A primigravida at 12 weeks of gestation presented with complaints of the expulsion of grape-like vesicles PV for the past day. A diagnosis of molar pregnancy was made on USG examination. Incidentally, the following adnexal lesion was also noted. What is the lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mucinous c‌ystadenoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Multilocular endometrial c‌ysts","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Theca lu‌tein cyst","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Polycystic ovarian disease‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Theca lutein cyst","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5d4f3e74056e48269ad48bef0d0230f2.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Theca lutein cyst</strong></p><p>The given USG image shows <strong>theca lutein cysts</strong> in the ovary. These cysts are usually seen in patients with <strong>complete</strong> molar pregnancies. They are usually <strong>bilateral</strong> and are also seen in multiple pregnancies.</p>\n<p>Theca lutein cysts result from the <strong>overstimulation</strong> of <strong>lutein elements</strong> by massive amounts of <strong>β</strong> <strong>hCG</strong>. The cystic fluid is rich in chorionic gonadotropin, estrogen, and progesterone. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9841","text":"A patient who was treated ‌for molar pregnancy came ​for follow-up after 4 mon​ths. Her USG revealed a l‌arge, twisted theca lutei‌n cyst. How will you mana​ge this patient?","raw_text":"A patient who was treated for molar pregnancy came for follow-up after 4 months. Her USG revealed a large, twisted theca lutein cyst. How will you manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Untwisting ‌of the cyst","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Laparoscopic‌ oophorectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wait an‌d watch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transabdominal aspiration ‌under USG guidance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Untwisting of the cyst","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Untwisting of the cyst</strong></p><p><strong>Untwisting of the cyst</strong> is the only management in an <strong>asymptomatic</strong> patient with a twisted theca lutein cyst.</p>\n<p>Laparoscopic <strong>oophorectomy</strong> is done only when there is associated extensive <strong>necrosis or hemorrhage</strong>.</p>\n<p>Theca-lutein cysts regress within 2 months after pregnancy evacuation, and hence <strong>expectant</strong> management is preferred in <strong>uncomplicated</strong> cases. If the cysts are <strong>symptomatic</strong>, they can be <strong>aspirated</strong> trans-abdominally under ultrasound guidance.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1495","text":"A pregnant woman at 14 wee‌ks of gestation complains​ of excessive vomiting an​d mild vaginal bleeding f‌or 2 weeks. On examinatio‌n, fundal height correspo​nds to 16 weeks and fetal​ heart sounds are absent.‌ USG shows a snowstorm ap‌pearance and no fetus. Wh​at is the best step of ma​nagement?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman at 14 weeks of gestation complains of excessive vomiting and mild vaginal bleeding for 2 weeks. On examination, fundal height corresponds to 16 weeks and fetal heart sounds are absent. USG shows a snowstorm appearance and no fetus. What is the best step of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chemot‌herapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyster‌ectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Suction and‌ evacuation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Serum b‌eta hCG","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Suction and evacuation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Suction and evacuation</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>molar pregnancy </strong>which is managed by<strong> suction and evacuation, </strong>regardless of uterine size.</p>\n<p>If the <strong>cervix</strong> is <strong>unfavorable</strong>, slow dilatation is done with vaginal <strong>PGE1</strong>. Volume resuscitation with IV fluids and parenteral antibiotics can be given as supportive therapy. Blood transfusion is done if the patient is anaemic.</p>\n<p>Intraoperative sonography is recommended to help ensure that the uterine cavity has been emptied.</p>\n<p><strong>Indication</strong> for <strong>hysterectomy</strong> in molar pregnancy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Women who have completed their family</li>\n<li>Age &gt;40 years</li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Uncontrolled hemorrhage </span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Perforation during surgical evacuation.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1511","text":"A 36-year-old woman underw‌ent suction evacuation fo​r a hydatidiform mole. Wh​ich of the following stat‌ements is true regarding ‌her follow-up with β-hCG ​estimation?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old woman underwent suction evacuation for a hydatidiform mole. Which of the following statements is true regarding her follow-up with β-hCG estimation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Start estimation 72 hours ‌following evacuation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Twice weekly estimation fo‌r a month following evacu​ation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Monthly follow-up for 6 mo‌nths after three consecut​ive normal values","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"After 6 months of follow-u‌p, regular yearly estimat​ion should be done","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Monthly follow-up for 6 months after three consecutive normal values","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Monthly follow-up for 6 months after three consecutive normal values</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the patient is followed up with serum β hCG every month for 6 months.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-up</strong> after the termination of molar pregnancy is done with <strong>serum β hCG</strong> levels. During the follow-up period, pregnancy must be avoided as it might interfere with the β hCG levels.</p>\n<p>The <strong>initial</strong> β-hCG level is obtained <strong>within 48 hours</strong> after evacuation and used as a <strong>baseline.</strong> </p>\n<p>It is measured <strong>weekly</strong> till it becomes negative, and three consecutive normal values are obtained. This normally happens in <strong>4-8 weeks. </strong></p>\n<p>After this, β hCG levels are measured <strong>monthly</strong> for <strong>6 months</strong>. Women who undergo chemotherapy must be followed up for 1 year.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1488","text":"Which of the following are‌ complications that can b​e seen in a patient with ​molar pregnancy? Pre-ecla‌mpsia Hypothyroidism Brea‌thlessness Electrolyte im​balance Uterine perforati​on","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are complications that can be seen in a patient with molar pregnancy?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Pre-eclampsia</li>\n<li>Hypothyroidism</li>\n<li>Breathlessness</li>\n<li>Electrolyte imbalance</li>\n<li>Uterine perforation</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2,‌ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2, ‌3 and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3, ‌4 and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3, 4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3, 4 and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Thyrotoxicosis</strong> is seen as a complication of molar pregnancy, not hypothyroidism.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> in <strong>molar pregnancy</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Immediate:\n<ul>\n<li>Early-onset pre-eclampsia<strong> </strong>- molar placentas produce more antiangiogenic proteins than normal placentas.</li>\n<li>Thyrotoxicosis - as α component of hCG mimics TSH and causes thyroid storm</li>\n<li>Acute pulmonary insufficiency - due to embolization of trophoblastic cells</li>\n<li>Electrolyte imbalance i.e., hypokalemia, hyponatremia - due to hyperemesis caused by β hCG</li>\n<li>Shock - hemorrhage due to the separation of vesicles and intra-peritoneal haemorrhage</li>\n<li>Disseminated intravascular coagulation</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Late: Choriocarcinoma.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7481","text":"Gestational trophoblastic ‌neoplasm does not include​","raw_text":"Gestational trophoblastic neoplasm does not include","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Partia‌l mole","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chorioc‌arcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Placental site trophoblast‌ic tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Invasi‌ve mole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Partial mole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Partial mole</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>A partial mole</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a gestational trophoblastic neoplasm (<strong>GTN</strong>).</p>\n<p>Gestational trophoblastic disease (<strong>GTD</strong>) is the term used to describe a group of tumors due to <strong>abnormal trophoblast proliferation</strong>. <strong>Malignant</strong> forms of GTD are termed gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (<strong>GTN</strong>).</p>\n<p>Modified WHO Classification of GTD</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100.046%;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48.6654%;\">Molar Pregnancies</td>\n<td style=\"width: 48.6654%;\">Trophoblastic tumors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48.6654%;\">\n<p>Hydatidiform mole (<strong>benign</strong>) -<strong>Partial</strong> and <strong>Complete</strong><br/>Invasive mole (malignant)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 48.6654%;\">\n<p>Choriocarcinoma (malignant)<br/>Placental site trophoblastic tumor (malignant)</p>\n<p>Epithelioid trophoblastic tumor (malignant)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9839","text":"All of the following are n‌on-molar trophoblastic ne​oplasms except _____.","raw_text":"All of the following are non-molar trophoblastic neoplasms except _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chorioc‌arcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Placental site trophoblast‌ic tumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Invasi‌ve mole","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Epithelioid trophoblastic ‌tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Invasive mole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bdf036a480d64e4fab24ba2953602074x1280x1460.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Invasive mole</strong></p><p><strong>Invasive mole</strong> is a <strong>molar</strong> trophoblastic neoplasm.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1512","text":"What is the most common no‌n-metastatic gestational ​trophoblastic neoplasia t​hat develops following hy‌datidiform mole?","raw_text":"What is the most common non-metastatic gestational trophoblastic neoplasia that develops following hydatidiform mole?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chorioc‌arcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Placental site trophoblast‌ic tumour","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chorioadeno‌ma destruens","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Epithelioid trophoblastic ‌tumour","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chorioadenoma destruens","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c4c9b153f34c4d858d5955d3df3acfd5x1280x1460.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chorioadenoma destruens</strong></p><p><strong>Chorioadenoma destruens </strong>or <strong>i</strong><strong>nvasive mole</strong> is the most common <strong>non-metastatic</strong> <strong>GTN</strong> that follows hydatidiform moles. Almost all invasive moles arise from partial or complete moles.</p>\n<p>Invasive mole is characterized by extensive tissue invasion by trophoblast and whole villi. They can <strong>penetrate</strong> deep into the <strong>myometrium</strong>, peritoneum, parametrium, or vaginal vault. They are <strong>locally aggressive</strong> and do not usually metastasize. </p>\n<p>The <strong>most common GTN</strong> following term pregnancy or miscarriage is <strong>choriocarcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9845","text":"Which of the following are‌ risk factors for maligna​nt change in a patient wi​th molar pregnancy? Mater‌nal age &gt;40 years β-hCG l‌evels &gt; 100,000 mIU/mL Th​eca-lutein cysts &lt; 6 cm U​terine size that is small‌ for gestational age Gran‌d multiparity","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are risk factors for malignant change in a patient with molar pregnancy?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Maternal age &gt;40 years</li>\n<li>β-hCG levels &gt; 100,000 mIU/mL</li>\n<li>Theca-lutein cysts &lt; 6 cm</li>\n<li>Uterine size that is small for gestational age</li>\n<li>Grand multiparity</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌2, 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‌2, 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‌3, 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1, 2, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1, 2, 5</strong></p><p>Maternal age &gt;40 years, β-hCG levels &gt;100,000 mIU/mL and grand multiparity are risk factors for malignant change in a patient with molar pregnancy.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for <strong>malignant</strong> change are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Patient's age ≥ 40 years or &lt;20 years</li>\n<li>Parity ≥3</li>\n<li>Uterine size &gt;20 weeks</li>\n<li>Previous history of molar pregnancy</li>\n<li>Complete moles have 15-20% risk of malignant transformation, partial moles have 1-5% risk</li>\n<li>Serum β hCG &gt;1,00,000 mIU/mL</li>\n<li>Large theca lutein cysts &gt;6 cm in diameter</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Post-evacuation uterine <strong>sonographic</strong> <strong>surveillance</strong> can be used as a <strong>predictor</strong> of subsequent <strong>neoplasia</strong>. Findings indicative of malignant transformation are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Suburethral nodules</strong></li>\n<li>Myometrial nodules</li>\n<li><strong>Hypervascularity.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7142","text":"All of the following can b‌e indicators of choriocar​cinoma except:","raw_text":"All of the following can be indicators of choriocarcinoma except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased number of theca ‌lutein cysts","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Persistently enlarged uter‌us","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plateau of h‌igh serum hCG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suburethr‌al nodule","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increased number of theca lutein cysts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increased number of theca lutein cysts</strong></p><p>Increased number of theca lutein cysts is not an indicator of choriocarcinoma.<strong> Increased size </strong>or <strong>persistence </strong>of theca lutein cysts indicates <strong>choriocarcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Plateau of high serum β-hCG level for four measurements for ≥3 weeks is an indicator of choriocarcinoma.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for <strong>malignant</strong> change are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Molar pregnancy - complete moles have 15-20% risk, partial moles have 1-5% risk</li>\n<li>Patient's age ≥ 40 years or &lt;20 years</li>\n<li>Parity ≥3</li>\n<li>Serum β hCG &gt;1,00,000 IU/mL</li>\n<li>Uterine size &gt;20 weeks</li>\n<li>Previous history of molar pregnancy</li>\n<li>Large theca lutein cysts &gt;6 cm in diameter</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Post-evacuation uterine <strong>sonographic</strong> <strong>surveillance</strong> can be used as a <strong>predictor</strong> of subsequent <strong>neoplasia</strong>. Findings indicative of malignant transformation are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Suburethral nodules</strong></li>\n<li>Myometrial nodules</li>\n<li><strong>Hypervascularity</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9843","text":"A patient with a history o‌f molar pregnancy was tre​ated with suction and eva​cuation. Which method of ‌contraception would you n‌ot recommend for her?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of molar pregnancy was treated with suction and evacuation. Which method of contraception would you not recommend for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Barrier‌ methods","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intrauterine contraceptive‌ device","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Oral contrac‌eptive pills","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Depot medroxyprogesterone ‌acetate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intrauterine contraceptive device","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intrauterine contraceptive device</strong></p><p><strong>IUCD </strong>(intrauterine contraceptive device) is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in this patient as the <strong>uterus</strong> is <strong>friable</strong> and can cause further <strong>bleeding</strong>. </p>\n<p>Accepted methods of contraception after suction evacuation in molar pregnancy include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Oral contraceptive pills</li>\n<li>Depot medroxyprogesterone acetate</li>\n<li>Barrier methods.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1497","text":"What will be the WHO score‌ in a patient with low-ri​sk gestational trophoblas​tic neoplasia?","raw_text":"What will be the WHO score in a patient with low-risk gestational trophoblastic neoplasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0‌-3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0‌-5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0‌-6","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"0‌-7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 0-6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 0-6</strong></p><p><strong>Low-risk</strong> GTN has a WHO score of <strong>0 to 6. </strong>High-risk GTN has a WHO score of ≥ 7.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 400px; width: 551px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\"><strong>Low risk</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\"><strong>High risk</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">Total score</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">0-6</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">≥7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">Age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">≤39 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">≥40 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">hCG</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">≤10<sup>5</sup> IU/L</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">≥10<sup>5</sup> IU/L</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">Type of antecedent pregnancy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">Molar pregnancy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">Full term pregnancy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">Duration of antecedent pregnancy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">&lt;4 months</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">&gt;12 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">Metastasis site</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">Lungs, vagina</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">Brain, liver</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">Number of metastases</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">&lt;4 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">&gt;8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">Size of the tumor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">&lt;3 cm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">&gt;5 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230px;\">Antecedent chemotherapy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">Single-agent chemotherapy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">Multiple-agent chemotherapy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1501","text":"A patient was diagnosed to‌ have choriocarcinoma two​ years post-evacuation of​ molar pregnancy. Which o‌f the following is the mo‌st common site of metasta​sis in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient was diagnosed to have choriocarcinoma two years post-evacuation of molar pregnancy. Which of the following is the most common site of metastasis in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vag‌ina","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Kid‌ney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Li‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ova‌ries","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vagina","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vagina</strong></p><p>The common sites of metastases of choriocarcinoma are in the following order:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lungs </strong>- most common</li>\n<li><strong>Vagina </strong>- second most common</li>\n<li>Vulva</li>\n<li>Kidneys</li>\n<li>Liver</li>\n<li>Ovaries</li>\n<li>Brain</li>\n<li>Bowel</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Lung metastases present as <strong>cannon-ball or snowstorm</strong> appearance on chest X-ray. Vaginal metastases present as <strong>purplish sub-urethral nodules</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9862","text":"A case of gestational trop‌hoblastic neoplasm is det​ected to have metastasis ​in the lungs. It is consi‌dered as stage _____.","raw_text":"A case of gestational trophoblastic neoplasm is detected to have metastasis in the lungs. It is considered as stage _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I‌I","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"I‌II","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"I‌V","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9b4129c54c0840fb8c61afc93bbfc840x600x487.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a322b86670814b4e944a4e24b787e1cdx1279x2253.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. III</strong></p><p>Metastases in the <strong>lungs</strong> are considered stage <strong>III</strong> GTN (gestational trophoblastic neoplasia) in FIGO classification.</p>\n<p>International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) staging for GTN is as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 375px; height: 292px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.6562px;\">Stage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.344px;\">Description</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.6562px;\">I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.344px;\">GTN confined to the <strong>uterus</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.6562px;\">\n<p>II</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.344px;\">\n<p>GTN extends outside of the uterus but is limited to the <strong>genital</strong> structures    </p>\n<p>(adnexa, vagina, broad ligament)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.6562px;\">III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.344px;\">GTN extends to the <strong>lungs</strong>, with or without known genital tract involvement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.6562px;\">IV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.344px;\">All <strong>other</strong> metastatic sites i.e. kidney, liver, brain, bowel, etc.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The chest X-ray below shows lung metastasis - stage III GTN. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Gestational trophoblastic neoplasia</strong> (GTN) refers to the aggressive invasion of the endometrium and myometrium by <strong>trophoblast cells</strong>. It includes <strong>invasive mole</strong>, <strong>gestational</strong> <strong>choriocarcinoma</strong>, <strong>placental site trophoblastic tumors</strong>, and<strong> epithelioid trophoblastic tumors</strong>. GTN is diagnosed based on elevated β-hCG levels. FIGO criteria for diagnosis of GTN: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Persistence of <strong>β-hCG level plateau</strong> in <strong>4 measurements</strong> for 3 weeks or longer</li>\n<li><strong>Rise in β-hCG</strong> levels in <strong>3 or more consecutiv</strong>e measurements done <strong>weekly</strong>, over a period of 2 weeks or longer</li>\n<li><strong>Elevated β-hCG</strong> level for <strong>6 months</strong> or more</li>\n<li><strong>Histologic diagnosis</strong> of choriocarcinoma </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment for <strong>low-risk disease</strong> is chemotherapy with <strong>methotrexate</strong>, while those with <strong>high-risk disease</strong> are treated with etoposide, methotrexate, and dactinomycin alternating with cyclophosphamide and vincristine (<strong>EMA/CO</strong>) chemotherapy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1507","text":"A 43-year-old woman presen‌ts with complaints of hem​optysis, vaginal bleeding​, and abdominal pain for ‌7 days. Chest X-ray is sh‌own below. Transabdominal​ USG revealed a solid mas​s occupying the uterus wh‌ich was found to be chori‌ocarcinoma with a WHO sco​re of 8. How would you ma​nage this patient?","raw_text":"A 43-year-old woman presents with complaints of hemoptysis, vaginal bleeding, and abdominal pain for 7 days. Chest X-ray is shown below. Transabdominal USG revealed a solid mass occupying the uterus which was found to be choriocarcinoma with a WHO score of 8. How would you manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyster‌ectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"R-C‌HOP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"EMA‌-CO","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"MV‌AC","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. EMA-CO","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1fbd8e615cf646a59dafde5eb4cac3ca.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. EMA-CO</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>high-risk</strong> (WHO) <strong>stage III</strong> <strong>choriocarcinoma</strong> which is treated with <strong>EMA-CO</strong> regimen. The given X-ray shows numerous, dense, bilateral metastatic nodules, of varying sizes indicative of lung metastases.</p>\n<p>The components of EMA-CO are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Etoposide</li>\n<li>Methotrexate</li>\n<li>Actinomycin D</li>\n<li>Cyclophosphamide</li>\n<li>Oncovin i.e. vincristine</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 184px; width: 378.781px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 61px; height: 21px;\"><strong>Risk</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 21px;\"><strong>Criteria</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.781px; height: 21px;\"><strong>Treatment</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 125px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 61px; height: 125px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 125px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>FIGO stage I GTN</li>\n<li>FIGO Stage II or III GTN with a WHO risk score <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">&lt;</span>6</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.781px; height: 125px;\">Single agent chemotherapy with Methotrextate or Actinomycin D.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 91px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 61px; height: 91px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 91px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Stages II and III with WHO risk score <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">&gt;</span>7</li>\n<li>Stage IV disease</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.781px; height: 91px;\">\n<p>EMA-CO regimen</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Option A: <strong>PSTT</strong> (placental-site trophoblastic tumor) and <strong>ETT</strong> (epithelioid trophoblastic tumors) are treated by <strong>hysterectomy</strong> only, as they are resistant to chemotherapy.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>R-CHOP</strong> regimen is used for B-cell <strong>non-Hodgkin's</strong> lymphoma.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>MVAC</strong> regimen is used for advanced <strong>bladder carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1508","text":"Placental-site trophoblast‌ic tumor arises from ____​_.","raw_text":"Placental-site trophoblastic tumor arises from _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytotro‌phoblast","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Syncytiot‌rophoblast","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intermediate‌ trophoblast","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chorion‌ic villi","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Intermediate trophoblast","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Intermediate trophoblast</strong></p><p><strong>Placental-site trophoblastic tumor</strong> arises from implantation site <strong>intermediate trophoblast.</strong> As syncytiotrophoblastic cells are generally absent in this site, it is composed mainly of <strong>cytotrophoblastic</strong> cells.</p>\n<p>Patients with placental site trophoblastic tumors present with vaginal bleeding and modestly elevated serum β-hCG levels. Local invasion into the myometrium and lymphatics occurs. <strong>Hysterectomy</strong> is the preferred treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1509","text":"A 26-year-old woman with a‌ history of early miscarr​iage presented with ameno​rrhoea. Her serum beta hC‌G levels were persistentl‌y low and USG revealed a ​single solid mass in the ​uterine fundus. Histopath‌ological examination of t‌he biopsy showed abundant​ intermediate trophoblast​ic cells. What is the nex​t step of management?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old woman with a history of early miscarriage presented with amenorrhoea. Her serum beta hCG levels were persistently low and USG revealed a single solid mass in the uterine fundus. Histopathological examination of the biopsy showed abundant intermediate trophoblastic cells. What is the next step of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methot‌rexate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Actino‌mycin D","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"EMA-CO ‌regimen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyster‌ectomy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hysterectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hysterectomy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>placental-site trophoblastic tumor</strong> (PSTT). <strong>Hysterectomy</strong> is the treatment of choice because they are locally invasive tumors and resistant to chemotherapy.</p>\n<p>PSTT is a rare tumor arising from <strong>intermediate</strong> <strong>trophoblastic</strong> of the placental bed. Patients with placental site trophoblastic tumors present with vaginal bleeding and <strong>modestly elevated</strong> serum <strong>β-hCG</strong> levels. They produce <strong>variant forms</strong> of <strong>hCG. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"gN5ciN1p","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"23_disorders_of_optic_nerve_and_gaze_palsies","title":"23.Disorders Of Optic Nerve And Gaze Palsies","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MB0329","text":"The longest part of the op​tic nerve measures _____.​","raw_text":"The longest part of the optic nerve measures _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30​ mm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"40​ mm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"50​ mm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"20​ mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 30 mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 30 mm</strong></p><p><strong>Intraorbital part</strong> is the longest part of the optic nerve.</p>\n<p>It measures from <strong>25 mm to 30 mm.</strong></p>\n<p>Length of the optic nerve ranges from 3.5 cm to 5.5 cm.</p>\n<p>80% of the fibers originate from the macular region.</p>\n<p><strong>Parts of optic nerve:</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Parts</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Length</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Intraocular part</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>1 mm</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Intraorbital part</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>25–30 mm</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Intracanalicular part</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>5<strong>–</strong>9mm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Intracranial part</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>10<strong>–</strong>16 mm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0337","text":"Which of the following is ​not a feature of papilled​ema?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of papilledema?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extracell​ular edema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Axonal ​swelling","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stasis of ax​oplasmic flow","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disruption of neurofilamen​t","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Disruption of neurofilament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Disruption of neurofilament</strong></p><p><strong>Disruption</strong> <strong>of neurofilaments is not a feature</strong> <strong>of papilledema.</strong></p>\n<p>Papilledema is the <strong>swelling of the optic nerve head</strong> secondary to <strong>raised intracranial pressure (ICP). </strong></p>\n<p>\"Disc swelling\" and \"disc edema\" are non-specific terms that include papilledema but also indicate a disc swollen from other causes.</p>\n<p>All patients with papilledema should be <strong>suspected</strong> of harboring an<strong> intracranial mass.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Features of papilledema:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Raised ICP</li>\n<li><strong>Edema </strong>of the optic nerve head\n<ul>\n<li>Blockage of axoplasmic flow</li>\n<li>Axonal swelling</li>\n<li>Collection of extracellular fluid</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Painless progressive</strong> loss of vision</li>\n<li><strong>Enlargement </strong>of<strong> </strong>the <strong>blind spot</strong></li>\n<li>Color vision, visual acuity, and pupillary reaction are <strong>n</strong><strong>ormal</strong>, until optic atrophy sets in.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0339","text":"A resident notes the follo​wing fundus picture in a ​patient referred to the o‌phthalmology OPD with vis​ual complaints. What does‍ it suggest?","raw_text":"A resident notes the following fundus picture in a patient referred to the ophthalmology OPD with visual complaints. What does it suggest?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retinal d​etachment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Papil​ledema","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Primary op​tic atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secondary o​ptic atrophy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Papilledema","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/56401db6a71c4b93909f7439a51f7d5b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Papilledema</strong></p><p>The fundoscopic image shows <strong>dilated veins</strong> with a <strong>blurred disc margin</strong> which is suggestive of <strong>papilledema</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Ophthalmoscopic features of papilledema</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Early-stage:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Obscuration</strong> of the disc margins <strong>(</strong><strong>nasal margins first) </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Absence</strong> of <strong>spontaneous venous pulsation</strong> at the disc</li>\n<li><strong>Mild hyperemia</strong> of the disc</li>\n<li>The blurring of the peripapillary nerve fiber layer</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Established papilledema:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>The disc appears to be enlarged and elevated (<strong>mushroom</strong> or <strong>dome-shaped)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Macular fan </strong>appears</li>\n<li><strong>Paton’s lines-</strong> circumferential <strong>greyish white folds</strong> may develop due to the separation of nerve fibers</li>\n<li>Visual fields show <strong>enlargement of the blind spot</strong></li>\n<li>Veins become <strong>tortuous</strong> and engorged</li>\n<li>Multiple <strong>cotton-wool spots</strong> and superficial hemorrhages</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Chronic or long-standing (vintage) papilledema:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>The optic disc gives the appearance of the <strong>dome of a champagne cork</strong></li>\n<li>Small drusen-like crystalline deposits (<strong>corpora amylacea</strong>) may appear on the disc surface</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Atrophic stage:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Secondary</strong> optic atrophy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0341","text":"Which of the following con​ditions show ipsilateral ​optic atrophy and contral‌ateral papilledema?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions show ipsilateral optic atrophy and contralateral papilledema?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Weber s​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Foster-Kenn​edy syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Millard-Gub​ler syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Benedikt​ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Foster-Kennedy syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Foster-Kennedy syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Ipsilateral optic atrophy</strong> and <strong>contralateral papilledema</strong> are seen in <strong>Foster–Kennedy syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Weber</strong> syndrome (<strong>superior alternating hemiplegia</strong>) is a form of stroke characterized by the presence of an <strong>ipsilateral oculomotor</strong> nerve palsy and <strong>contralateral hemiparesis</strong> or hemiplegia.</p>\n<p><strong>Millard–Gubler syndrome</strong> is a lesion of the <strong>pons</strong>. It is also called <strong>ventral pontine syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Benedikt syndrome,</strong> also known as <strong>paramedian midbrain syndrome</strong>, involves the fasciculus of oculomotor nerve as it passes through the red nucleus and is characterized by ipsilateral third nerve palsy and contralateral extrapyramidal signs such as hemitremor.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0342","text":"A 45-year-old man presents​ to the casualty with a s​udden loss of vision and ‌painful ocular movements.​ On examination, the eye ‍is white and there are no​ obvious signs on ophthal​moscopy. What is the most​ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man presents to the casualty with a sudden loss of vision and painful ocular movements. On examination, the eye is white and there are no obvious signs on ophthalmoscopy. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Optic ner​ve glioma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Retrobulba​r neuritis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Eale’s ​disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neurore​tinitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Retrobulbar neuritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/29391d1eb10348b0a7fd4b78579fe69cx512x389.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8bbee13f5cbb4361921cf13758fe28b9x606x603.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Retrobulbar neuritis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a sudden painful loss of vision in the absence of fundoscopic changes is suggestive of <strong>retrobulbar neuritis.</strong></p>\n<p>There are 3 types of optic neuritis: papillitis, neuroretinitis, and retrobulbar neuritis. The common causes of retrobulbar neuritis include demyelinating disorders such as <strong>multiple sclerosis</strong> (MS) and <strong>neuromyelitis optica</strong> (NMO or Devic's disease).</p>\n<p>Clinically, optic neuritis is characterized by sudden, progressive visual loss.  Ocular movements are <strong>painful</strong> in <strong>retrobulbar neuritis</strong> and painless in papillitis and neuroretinitis. Glowing sensations known as <strong>phosphenes</strong> may be induced with sound or movement. Color vision may also be impaired. </p>\n<p>On examination, Marcus Gunn pupil or relative afferent pupillary defect <strong>(</strong><strong>RAPD) </strong>is usually noted due to asymmetric involvement. The most common visual field defect is a <strong>central </strong>or<strong> centrocecal scotoma</strong>. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 180px;\" width=\"570\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 182.667px;\">Type</td>\n<td style=\"width: 182.667px;\"><strong>Retrobulbar neuritis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 182.667px;\"><strong>Papillitis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 182.667px;\"><strong>Neuroretinitis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 182.667px;\">Pathology</td>\n<td style=\"width: 182.667px;\">Inflammation of the optic nerve behind the disc</td>\n<td style=\"width: 182.667px;\">Inflammation of the optic nerve head </td>\n<td style=\"width: 182.667px;\">Inflammation of the optic nerve head and the macula</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 182.667px;\">Fundus appearance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 182.667px;\">Optic disc appears normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 182.667px;\">\n<p>Hyperemia and oedema of the optic disc with or without peri-papillary flame-shaped hemorrhages</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 182.667px;\">Macular star (due to exudates that form around macula)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 182.667px;\">Prevalence</td>\n<td style=\"width: 182.667px;\">Most common in adults</td>\n<td style=\"width: 182.667px;\">\n<p>Most common in children</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 182.667px;\">Least common type</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 182.667px;\">Associated conditions</td>\n<td style=\"width: 182.667px;\">Strong association with demyelinating disorders like MS, NMO</td>\n<td style=\"width: 182.667px;\">\n<p>Weak association with demyelinating disorders</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 182.667px;\">Cat-scratch disease, Lyme disease, Syphilis etc.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>papillitis</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>macular star </strong>appearance in <strong>neuroretinitis</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0344","text":"A 33-year-old patient has ​come to the ophthalmology​ OPD with complaints that‌ her vision becomes tempo​rarily blurry after a hot‍ bath in the evening. Thi​s resolves by itself afte​r a few hours. She has a ​past history of an episod​e of painful unilateral o​ptic neuritis, 3 months b‍ack which resolved with s‍teroids. What is being de​scribed here?","raw_text":"A 33-year-old patient has come to the ophthalmology OPD with complaints that her vision becomes temporarily blurry after a hot bath in the evening. This resolves by itself after a few hours. She has a past history of an episode of painful unilateral optic neuritis, 3 months back which resolved with steroids. What is being described here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulfrich ​phenomenon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Uhthoff p​henomenon","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lhermitt​e’s sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Riddoch p​henomenon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Uhthoff phenomenon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Uhthoff phenomenon</strong></p><p>Episodic, transient obscuration of vision on exertion or on exposure to <strong>heat</strong>, which recovers on resting or moving away from the heat in an isolated optic neuritis patient is called <strong>Uhthoff phenomenon. </strong>It is commonly associated with multiple sclerosis (MS).</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Pulfrich’s phenomenon </strong>is an illusion<strong> </strong>of <strong>depth perception. </strong>It is seen in MS. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Lhermitte’s sign </strong>refers to passive <strong>neck flexion </strong>causing an <strong>electric shock-like sensation radiating to the spine and shoulders</strong>. This is a result of the increased sensitivity of the myelin to stretch or traction; it is also seen in MS.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Riddoch phenomenon </strong>refers to ability to perceive <strong>kinetic</strong> but <strong>not static</strong> targets. It is seen in <strong>cortical blindness.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0418","text":"In a patient with multiple​ sclerosis, which of the ​following visual field de‌fects is most likely?","raw_text":"In a patient with multiple sclerosis, which of the following visual field defects is most likely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bitemporal​ hemianopia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Central​ scotoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Enlarged ​blind spot","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tunnel​ vision","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Central scotoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Central scotoma</strong></p><p><strong>Central scotoma</strong> is more likely to occur with <strong>multiple sclerosis. </strong>It is also seen with retrobulbar neuritis and macular pathology.</p>\n<p>Option A: Optic chiasma compression causes bitemporal hemianopia</p>\n<p>Option C: Enlarged blind spot occurs with papilloedema</p>\n<p>Option D: Tunnel vision occurs in glaucoma, retinitis pigmentosa and Retinal pan photocoagulation</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0345","text":"Optic neuritis is a common​ presentation in which of​ the following diseases?","raw_text":"Optic neuritis is a common presentation in which of the following diseases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myasthen​ia gravis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Guillain-Ba​rré syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Multiple ​sclerosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Amyotrophic lateral sclero​sis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Multiple sclerosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Multiple sclerosis</strong></p><p>One of the common presentations of MS is <strong>optic neuritis</strong>. Multiple sclerosis (MS) results from demyelinating plaques in the central nervous system. </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Myasthenia gravis</strong> is the <strong>autoimmune</strong> destruction of the <strong>acetylcholine receptors</strong> at the neuromuscular junction.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Guillain–Barré syndrome</strong> is the postinfectious autoimmune-induced destruction of peripheral nerves, resulting in an <strong>ascending paralysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)</strong> is a disease of the <strong>motor nerve fibers</strong> of the lateral columns and anterior horns of the spinal cord that results in <strong>progressive muscular atrophy</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7048","text":"A 26-year-old female patie​nt presents with 6/60 vis​ual acuity in one eye and‌ 6/18 in the other eye. S​he has recurrent episodes‍ of diminution of vision ​which recovers on treatme​nt with steroids. She als​o has a history of transv​erse myelitis. What is th​e most probable diagnosis‍?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old female patient presents with 6/60 visual acuity in one eye and 6/18 in the other eye. She has recurrent episodes of diminution of vision which recovers on treatment with steroids. She also has a history of transverse myelitis. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hereditary spastic quadrip​legia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subacute combined degenera​tion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neuromyeli​tis optica","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Multiple ​sclerosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neuromyelitis optica","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neuromyelitis optica</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette of a patient presenting with <strong>decreased visual acuity</strong> and a <strong>history of transverse myelitis</strong> which is treated with steroids is indicative of <strong>Neuromyelitis optica</strong> (NMO).</p>\n<p>Neuromyelitis optica also known as <strong>Devic's disease</strong> is an <strong>autoimmune demyelinating</strong> disease in which <strong>antibodies</strong> are formed <strong>against</strong> <strong>Aquaporin 4</strong> present in the cell membranes of <strong>Astrocytes</strong>. It is characterized by the findings of unilateral or bilateral <strong>optic neuritis</strong> developing within a few weeks of <strong>transverse myelitis</strong>(demyelination of the spinal cord). The disease may be monophasic or recurrent.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-star-inserted\">\n<div class=\"table-responsive font-weight-light answer-scroll ng-star-inserted\">\n<p>Diagnostic Criteria for Neuromyelitis Optica Spectrum Disorder (NMOSD):</p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><strong>Diagnostic Criteria for NMOSD associated with AQP4-IgG</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li class=\"p3\">At least <strong>1</strong> core clinical characteristic</li>\n<li class=\"p3\">A positive test for <strong>AQP4-IgG</strong> using the best available detection method (<strong>cell-based assay</strong>)</li>\n<li class=\"p3\">Exclusion of alternative diagnoses</li>\n</ol>\n<p> <strong>Core Clinical Characteristics</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Optic neuritis is usually bilateral and causes <strong>severe visual loss.</strong></li>\n<li> Acute myelitis- may be <strong>severe, transverse</strong> and typically longitudinally extensive involving three or more contiguous vertebral segments.</li>\n<li>Area postrema syndrome: episodes of otherwise unexplained hiccups or nausea or vomiting</li>\n<li>Acute brainstem syndrome</li>\n<li>Symptomatic <strong>narcolepsy</strong>  associated with NMOSD-typical diencephalic MRI lesions</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For acute cases of NMO, <strong>high-dose glucocorticoids</strong> or plasma exchange is used. <strong>Mycophenolate mofetil, rituximab,</strong> and <strong>glucocorticoids with azathioprine</strong> are used for prophylaxis against relapses.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3590\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3590\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":33555327,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"4\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":65535},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"9\":0,\"11\":4,\"12\":0,\"28\":1}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"The FDA has approved Satralizumab, a humanized IgG2 monoclonal antibody for the treatment of neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder (NMOSD) in adults with particular antibody patients who are anti-aquaporin-4 or AQP4 antibody-positive. \"}'>The FDA has approved <strong>satralizumab,</strong> a humanized IgG2 monoclonal antibody, for the treatment of NMO spectrum disorder with AQP4 antibody positivity.</span></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3520\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Eculizumab,</strong> a complement inhibitor, and inebilizumab<strong>,</strong> a B-cell depletion agent, are two more new drugs that have recently been approved for use in NMO attack prevention.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"row mt-3\"> A history of transverse myelitis with optic neuritis(decreased visual acuity) rules out<strong> Multiple sclerosis</strong>(option D)<strong>. </strong>It<strong> </strong>is an <strong>autoimmune </strong>inflammatory and <strong>demyelinating</strong> disease of the <strong>CNS</strong> that affects both upper motor neurons (<strong>UMN</strong>) and lower motor neurons (<strong>LMN</strong>) with involvement of sensory system, ocular system and bowel and bladder function. <strong>Ocular </strong>manifestations of MS include <strong>Optic neuritis</strong>(usually <strong>retrobulbar</strong>)- decreased visual acuity, internuclear ophthalmoplegia and nystagmus<strong>.</strong></div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 759\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0349","text":"A middle-aged man with sus​pected optic neuropathy i​s being evaluated for vis‌ual field defects. Goldma​n perimetry reveals bilat‍eral centrocecal scotomas​. Which of the following ​is most likely to be the ​causative agent?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man with suspected optic neuropathy is being evaluated for visual field defects. Goldman perimetry reveals bilateral centrocecal scotomas. Which of the following is most likely to be the causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tob​acco","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Eth​anol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Meth​anol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlor​oquine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tobacco","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tobacco</strong></p><p>Centrocecal scotoma is most commonly seen in <strong>tobacco-induced amblyopia</strong>. It is also seen in <strong>vitamin B12</strong> deficiency.</p>\n<p><strong>Tobacco amblyopia </strong>is caused by excessive smoking and chewing of tobacco.<strong> </strong>The toxic agent involved is <strong>cyanide</strong> found in tobacco. It is characterized by bilateral and gradually progressive impairment of <strong>central</strong> vision. The classic visual field defect is a <strong>centrocecal scotoma</strong>, often involving both the <strong>blind spot</strong> and the <strong>macula.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: Ethanol amblyopia is similar to tobacco amblyopia but often presents with peripheral neuritis and a \"central scotoma\".</p>\n<p>Option C: Methanol amblyopia often results in complete blindness.</p>\n<p>Option D: Quinine amblyopia results in near-complete blindness with associated deafness and tinnitus.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0353","text":"An anxious, young man came​ to the ER with complaint​s of having sudden troubl‌e seeing with his left ey​e and a recent history of‍ tripping over things. Th​ere was no associated pai​n or difficulty moving th​e eyes. An ophthalmology ​consultation was requeste​d. On perimetry, the foll‍owing visual field defect‍ was picked up. What is t​he most likely diagnosis?‍","raw_text":"An anxious, young man came to the ER with complaints of having sudden trouble seeing with his left eye and a recent history of tripping over things. There was no associated pain or difficulty moving the eyes. An ophthalmology consultation was requested. On perimetry, the following visual field defect was picked up. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methanol i​ntoxication","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior ischemic optic ne​uropathy (AION)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Suprasel​lar tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lesions to occipital corte​x","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (AION)","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0bf2b97fe06c431bb8cd2dc2a25df0ea.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9f53f3f8209b4186a93e1059ebac32e3x616x421.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (AION)</strong></p><p>The perimetry result depicts <strong>altitudinal hemianopia, </strong>which is <strong>most commonly seen</strong> in anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (<strong>AION</strong>).</p>\n<p>Altitudinal hemianopia can also be seen in <strong>suprasellar tumors</strong> and <strong>occipital lobe lesions,</strong> but the <strong>frequency is very low</strong> compared to that of <strong>AION</strong>. Hence, AION is the single best answer.</p>\n<p>The two types of AION are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Arteritic AION-</strong> caused by <strong>g</strong><strong>iant-cell arteritis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Non-arteritic AION</strong> (NAAION)- <strong>more common</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 296px;\" width=\"622\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\"><strong>AAION</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p><strong>NAAION</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\"><strong>Age</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">&gt;70 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p>&lt;70 years</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\"><strong>Sex</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">F &gt; M</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p>F = M</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p><strong>Headache</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Jaw claudication</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\"><strong>Visual loss</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">Marked</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p>Less marked</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\"><strong>Optic disc</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">Pale disc edema</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p>Hyperemic disc edema</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\"><strong>C reactive protein</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">Raised</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p>Normal </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\"><strong>ESR</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">Highly raised</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p>Raised</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\"><strong>FFA</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p>Disc delay and choroidal delay</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p>Only disc delay</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\"><strong>Treatment</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">Corticosteroids</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">Levodopa–carbidopa combination for 3 weeks and then double dose for 1 month</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: Corticosteroids are not proven to be effective in NAAION.</p>\n<p><br/>The image given below shows pale disc edema of <strong>Arteritic AION.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0356","text":"What does the given fundos​copic picture suggest?","raw_text":"What does the given fundoscopic picture suggest?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary op​tic atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secondary o​ptic atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glaucomatous optic atrophy​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Consecutive ​optic atrophy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glaucomatous optic atrophy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4f616f44177043fab970b6ff5ccd6ae8.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glaucomatous optic atrophy</strong></p><p>The given fundoscopic image clearly depicts <strong>cupping</strong>, <strong>thinning of the neuroretinal rim,</strong> and <strong>nasal shifting of vessels</strong>. This are characteristic of <strong>glaucomatous optic atrophy</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0357","text":"Which of the following con​ditions is not associated​ with optic atrophy?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is not associated with optic atrophy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retinitis ​pigmentosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lep​rosy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pituita​ry tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Methano​l intake","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Leprosy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Leprosy</strong></p><p><strong>Leprosy</strong> is <strong>not </strong>associated with optic atrophy.</p>\n<p>All the other given conditions can cause optic atrophy.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes of optic atrophy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary</strong> optic atrophy\n<ul>\n<li>Optic neuritis</li>\n<li>Compression by tumors and aneurysms</li>\n<li>Hereditary optic neuropathies</li>\n<li>Toxic and nutritional optic neuropathies</li>\n<li>Trauma</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary</strong> optic atrophy\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic papilledema</li>\n<li>Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy</li>\n<li>Papillitis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Consecutive</strong> optic atrophy\n<ul>\n<li>Extensive retinal photocoagulation</li>\n<li>Retinitis pigmentosa</li>\n<li>Prior central retinal artery occlusion</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Glaucomatous</strong> optic atrophy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1247","text":"A 24-year-old man with a h​istory of pernicious anem​ia was referred to ophtha‌lmology for complaints of​ gradually progressing vi‍sual disturbances. On per​imetry, which of the foll​owing defects is most lik​ely to be seen in him?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old man with a history of pernicious anemia was referred to ophthalmology for complaints of gradually progressing visual disturbances. On perimetry, which of the following defects is most likely to be seen in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enlarged ​blindspot","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bitemporal​ hemianopia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Heteronymou​s hemianopia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Centrocec​al scotoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Centrocecal scotoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Centrocecal scotoma</strong></p><p>The given scenario of pernicious anemia with gradually progressive visual disturbances is suggestive of <strong>vitamin B12 deficiency. </strong></p>\n<p>B12 deficiency is known to cause <strong>centrocecal scotoma </strong>and it is an important cause of<strong> nutritional optic atrophy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1248","text":"A 15-year-old boy has bila​teral optic atrophy with ​diabetes mellitus and dia‌betes insipidus. What is ​the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old boy has bilateral optic atrophy with diabetes mellitus and diabetes insipidus. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kjer optic​ neuropathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Behr s​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wolfram ​syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Leber's congenital amauros​is","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Wolfram syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Wolfram syndrome</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario suggests <strong>Wolfram syndrome</strong>. It is due to<strong> WFS1</strong> gene mutation. </p>\n<p>The presentation is usually between the ages of<strong> 5 </strong>and<strong> 21 years</strong>;<strong> diabetes mellitus</strong> is typically the <strong>first manifestation</strong>, followed by visual problems. It has a poor prognosis. </p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features </strong>are: (<strong>DIDMOAD</strong>) </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diabetes insipidus</li>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li>Optic atrophy</li>\n<li>Deafness (sensorineural type)<strong> </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Kjer's optic neuropathy is an autosomal dominant condition. It presents with vision loss in early childhood and no significant systemic features.</p>\n<p>Option B: Behr syndrome is an autosomal recessive condition. Clinical features include optic atrophy, pyramidal tract signs, ataxia, intellectual disability, urinary incontinence, and pes cavus</p>\n<p>Option D: Leber's congenital amaurosis is an autosomal recessive condition. It usually presents with significant vision loss in infancy and nystagmus. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0359","text":"Which of the following is ​not a feature of amaurosi​s fugax ?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of amaurosis fugax ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sudden​ onset","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transien​t nature","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Monocular​ blindness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Irreversi​ble nature","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Irreversible nature","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Irreversible nature</strong></p><p><strong>Amaurosis fugax</strong> is a <strong>sudden</strong> and <strong>transient monocular</strong> blindness, which is <strong>reversible</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>It is a marker of <strong>advanced</strong> carotid artery <strong>atherosclerosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0360","text":"Champagne cork disc is see​n in which of the followi​ng conditions?","raw_text":"Champagne cork disc is seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Papil​ledema","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Papil​litis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pseudopa​pillitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior ischemic optic ne​uropathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Papilledema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Papilledema</strong></p><p><strong>Champagne cork</strong> disc is seen in <strong>chronic papilledema</strong>.</p>\n<p>The optic disc gives the appearance of the <strong>dome of a champagne cork</strong>. Small drusen-like crystalline deposits (<strong>corpora amylacea</strong>) may appear on the disc surface.</p>\n<p>The most reliable sign to differentiate between papillitis and papilledema is <strong>pupillary reaction</strong>. Pupillary light reflex is <strong>normal in papilledema</strong> whereas it is <strong>sluggish in papillitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0348","text":"Which of the following def​ects is implicated in Leb​er’s hereditary optic neu‌ropathy?","raw_text":"Which of the following defects is implicated in Leber’s hereditary optic neuropathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Post-transla​tional defect","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sporadic po​int mutation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mitochondria​l DNA defect","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chromosomal ​translocation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mitochondrial DNA defect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mitochondrial DNA defect</strong></p><p>In <strong>Leber’s hereditary optic neuropathy</strong> (LHON), the defect is a mutation of<strong> maternal mitochondrial DNA. </strong>Therefore the condition has a<strong> maternal inheritance. </strong></p>\n<p>LHON causes bilateral, <strong>subacute painless loss</strong> of vision. The characteristic visual field defect is a centrocaecal scotoma. Pupillary light reflex is relatively preserved until the late stage of the disease. <strong>Optic neuritis</strong> and <strong>o</strong><strong>ptic atrophy</strong> start in one eye and goes on to involve the other eye subsequently. It typically affects <strong>males</strong> between the ages of <strong>15 and 35 years. </strong>The diagnosis of LHON should be considered in any patient with <strong>bilateral optic neuropathy</strong>, irrespective of age.</p>\n<p><strong>Prognosis is poor</strong>, although some visual recovery may occur in a minority of cases even years later. </p>\n<p>Note: <strong>LHON plus</strong> refers to rare variants with additional manifestations such as<strong> neuromuscular dysfunction</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6799","text":"A 34-year-old man develope​d a painless diminution o​f vision in his right eye‌ 6 months ago but did not​ seek medical attention. ‍Two weeks ago, he started​ to notice mild blurring ​in his left eye and has n​ow come for evaluation. A​ccording to him, his elde​r brother and maternal un‍cle also had similar visi‍on problems. Which fundus​ finding would point towa‍rds Leber’s hereditary op‌tic neuropathy in this pa​tient?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old man developed a painless diminution of vision in his right eye 6 months ago but did not seek medical attention. Two weeks ago, he started to notice mild blurring in his left eye and has now come for evaluation. According to him, his elder brother and maternal uncle also had similar vision problems. Which fundus finding would point towards Leber’s hereditary optic neuropathy in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sectoral op​tic atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Disc leakage on fluorescei​n angiography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperemic optic disc with ​telangiectatic capillarie​s","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Papilledema and macular st​ar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hyperemic optic disc with telangiectatic capillaries","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hyperemic optic disc with telangiectatic capillaries</strong></p><p><strong>Hyperemic optic disc with telangiectatic capillaries</strong> would be suggestive of LHON.  </p>\n<p>LHON is related to a mitochondrial DNA mutation. This results in impaired mitochondrial adenosine triphosphate production, which affects the highly energy-dependent optic nerve.  </p>\n<p>In the acute phase, the optic disc is <strong>hyperemic</strong> and swollen with <strong>telangiectatic </strong>capillaries. However, the optic disc <strong>does not leak</strong> on fluorescein angiography. Later stages may show primary optic atrophy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6804","text":"In order to diagnose a cas​e as idiopathic intracran​ial hypertension, which o‌f the following features ​is not necessary?","raw_text":"In order to diagnose a case as idiopathic intracranial hypertension, which of the following features is not necessary?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal cerebrospinal fluid​ composition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Elevated opening pressure ​on lumbar puncture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bilateral ​papilledema","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Normal neuroimaging studie​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bilateral papilledema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bilateral papilledema</strong></p><p>Papilledema <strong>is not a</strong> mandatory criterion for the diagnosis of <strong>idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH).</strong></p>\n<p>Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (pseudotumor cerebri) is a diagnosis of exclusion. Obstructive, compressive, and infiltrative CNS lesions must be excluded by neuroimaging studies. Papilledema, although present in 90% of the patients, is not necessary for diagnosis. Elevated CSF opening pressure on lumbar puncture is the hallmark of IIH.</p>\n<p>Risk factors of IIH include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Female sex</li>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n<li>Nalidixic acid</li>\n<li>Tetracycline</li>\n<li>Vitamin A</li>\n<li>Oral contraceptives</li>\n<li>Use of or withdrawal from corticosteroids</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6800","text":"Which of the following is ​the most reliable sign to​ clinically differentiate‌ between a case of papill​itis and papilledema?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most reliable sign to clinically differentiate between a case of papillitis and papilledema?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amount of di​sc elevation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Degree of venous congestio​n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pupillary​ reaction","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Disc hy​peremia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pupillary reaction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pupillary reaction</strong></p><p>The most reliable sign to differentiate between papillitis and papilledema is <strong>pupillary reaction.  </strong></p>\n<p>Pupillary light reflex is <strong>normal in papilledema</strong> whereas it is <strong>sluggish in papillitis</strong>. Visual acuity and colour <strong>vision</strong> are also relatively more <strong>preserved in papilledema</strong> compared to papillitis.</p>\n<p>The other three disc signs seen on ophthalmoscopy are present in both papillitis and papilledema.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0363","text":"A patient evaluated for di​zziness and visual distur​bances was found to have ‌down-beating nystagmus. A​ brain lesion was suspect‍ed and the patient was re​ferred for an MRI. What i​s the most likely site of​ the lesion?","raw_text":"A patient evaluated for dizziness and visual disturbances was found to have down-beating nystagmus. A brain lesion was suspected and the patient was referred for an MRI. What is the most likely site of the lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parasellar region and opti​c chiasm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pineal region and the dors​al midbrain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cervico-medullary junction​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior cerebral hemisph​ere","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cervico-medullary junction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cervico-medullary junction</strong></p><p><strong>Down-beat</strong> nystagmus is usually due to a lesion at the <strong>cervico-medullary junction</strong> (e.g. Arnold-Chiari malformation, syringobulbia) or as a manifestation of <strong>cerebellar</strong> degeneration.</p>\n<p>Other causes of down-beat nystagmus:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drugs (phenytoin, lithium, barbiturates, carbamazepine)</li>\n<li>Wernicke's encephalopathy</li>\n<li>Demyelinating disorders</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Acquired nystagmus can be attributed to disturbances of the three mechanisms that normally ensure steady gaze—visual fixation, the vestibulo-ocular reflex, and the mechanism that makes it possible to hold the eyes at an eccentric eye position (e.g. far-right gaze).</p>\n<p>Other types of nystagmus:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 254px; width: 702px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 264.667px;\"><strong>Type of Nystagmus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 421.333px;\"><strong>Etiology</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 264.667px;\">Slow-horizontal </td>\n<td style=\"width: 421.333px;\">Large <strong>posterior cerebral</strong> lesions (e.g. stroke)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 264.667px;\">Mixed horizontal (Torsional)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 421.333px;\">Usually associated with vertigo and seen in <strong>vestibular system </strong>lesions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 264.667px;\">Up-beat</td>\n<td style=\"width: 421.333px;\"><strong>Medulla,</strong> the ventral tegmentum and the anterior vermis of the cerebellum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 264.667px;\">Periodic alternating</td>\n<td style=\"width: 421.333px;\"><strong>Cerebellar disease</strong> (In PAN, the horizontal jerk nystagmus spontaneously reverses the direction of the quick phase every few seconds)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 264.667px;\">See-saw</td>\n<td style=\"width: 421.333px;\">Lesion involving <strong>parasellar region and optic chiasm</strong> (e.g. pituitary tumours, third ventricle) </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 264.667px;\">Gaze-evoked</td>\n<td style=\"width: 421.333px;\">Side-effect of <strong>drugs</strong>, including sedatives, anticonvulsants and alcohol, as well as <strong>cerebellar</strong> <strong>disease</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0954","text":"An RTA patient with severe​ head trauma is admitted ​to the surgical ICU for o‌bservation. On examinatio​n, nystagmus was noted. H‍is CT scan reveals a tran​sverse fracture of his le​ft temporal bone. Which o​f the following statement​s is true regarding the n​ystagmus?","raw_text":"An RTA patient with severe head trauma is admitted to the surgical ICU for observation. On examination, nystagmus was noted. His CT scan reveals a transverse fracture of his left temporal bone. Which of the following statements is true regarding the nystagmus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Slow phase is towards the ​left and fast phase towar​ds the right","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Slow phase is towards the ​right and fast phase towa​rds the left","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is an up-beating type o​f nystagmus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a down-beating type ​of nystagmus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Slow phase is towards the left and fast phase towards the right","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Slow phase is towards the left and fast phase towards the right</strong></p><p>Fracture of the temporal bone causes vestibular injury resulting in a horizontal nystagmus with <strong>slow phase towards the affected side</strong> and <strong>fast phase towards the opposite side.</strong></p>\n<p>Peripheral vestibular nystagmus occurs in lesions of the labyrinth or vestibular nerve. Normally, there is tonic input to the brainstem vestibular nuclei from the periphery. Loss of the tonic input from the left vestibular nerve leads to deviation of the eyes <strong>towards the left side,</strong> which is the <strong>slow phase</strong> of the nystagmus. The cortical correction brings the eyes back to the centre in the primary gaze which is the <strong>fast-phase</strong> directed <strong>towards the right side</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7330","text":"Which of the following is ​the site of lesion in int​ernuclear ophthalmoplegia‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the site of lesion in internuclear ophthalmoplegia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medial Longitudinal Fascic​ulus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"6th Nerv​e nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pontine paramedian reticul​ar formation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A &amp;a​mp; C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Medial Longitudinal Fasciculus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ffe506e7bffa4858b3825b7f36d2cfd2x1280x2152.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/836881526ea24eafbaa3a6dc3f6fc92bx1280x2259.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Medial Longitudinal Fasciculus</strong></p><p>In internuclear ophthalmoplegia (INO) the lesion is at the <strong>medial longitudinal fasciculus </strong>(MLF).</p>\n<p>In INO, there is <strong>impaired adduction</strong> on the <strong>ipsilateral</strong> side of the lesion, and<strong> nystagmus on abduction </strong>on the <strong>contralateral side</strong>. When the gaze is initiated in the motor cortex, the horizontal gaze center or the <strong>paramedian pontine reticular formation</strong> (PPRF) generates the eye movements.</p>\n<p>The PPRF has <strong>two outputs</strong>. The first is to the <strong>ipsilateral 6th cranial nerve nucleus</strong> which causes abduction of the ipsilateral eye. The second output is to the <strong>contralateral</strong> <strong>MLF</strong> which sends an impulse to the <strong>contralateral 3rd cranial nerve nucleus</strong> causing contralateral adduction. These two outputs from PPRF lead to conjugate movement of the eyes to the same side. </p>\n<p>Therefore, a<strong> lesion </strong>in the<strong> right MLF</strong> leads to <strong>failure </strong>of the<strong> right eye adduction</strong>. The <strong>left eye</strong>, however, <strong>abducts normally</strong>. This is called <strong>internuclear ophthalmoplegia</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following image shows the pathway for horizontal eye movements as described above.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1253","text":"You are evaluating an elde​rly patient in the stroke​ ICU, who was admitted th‌ree days back with a hist​ory of sudden onset nause‍a, vomiting, and gait abn​ormalities. The visual ex​amination showed conjugat​e horizontal left gaze pa​lsy. He could maintain ga​ze near mid-position but ‍could not cross the midli‍ne to the left side. His ​MRI is likely to show inv‍olvement of which of the ‌following brain areas?","raw_text":"You are evaluating an elderly patient in the stroke ICU, who was admitted three days back with a history of sudden onset nausea, vomiting, and gait abnormalities. The visual examination showed conjugate horizontal left gaze palsy. He could maintain gaze near mid-position but could not cross the midline to the left side. His MRI is likely to show involvement of which of the following brain areas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parapontine reticular form​ation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pretecta​l nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial longitudinal fascic​ulus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Occipit​al lobe","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Parapontine reticular formation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0b51e3811490456c98064908037e4757x1280x2259.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Parapontine reticular formation</strong></p><p><strong>Horizontal gaze palsy</strong> can occur due to a lesion in<strong> parapontine reticular formation </strong>(PPRF).</p>\n<p><strong>Horizontal gaze:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It involves<strong> 2 muscles-</strong> the ipsilateral lateral rectus and the contralateral medial rectus.</li>\n<li><strong>Center</strong> for this pathway is <strong>parapontine reticular formation </strong>(PPRF).</li>\n<li>From here, they move to the <strong>ipsilateral 6<sup>th</sup> nerve nucleus</strong>.</li>\n<li>Next, they pass through the <strong>medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF)</strong> to the <strong>contralateral medial rectus subnucleus.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Lesions affecting the<strong> abducens nuclei</strong> will also cause ipsilateral horizontal gaze palsy, because of damage to motor neurons and internuclear neurons originating from the nuclei. Lesions involving the <strong>PPRF</strong> will lead to horizontal gaze palsy, as PPRF acts as a relay centre that connects the frontal eye fields in the cerebral cortex to the abducens nuclei. Conjugate horizontal gaze palsy can also be caused by lesion(s) involving <strong>both</strong> the <strong>abducens motor fibres and the MLFs preceding the abducens nucleus.</strong> Bilateral horizontal gaze palsy will be caused if lesion(s) involve both abducens nuclei or bilateral PPRF.</p>\n<p><strong>Isolated damage to the MLFs</strong> will cause the classic<strong> INO</strong> where eye abduction is preserved, however, the ipsilateral eye will not be able to be adducted on conjugate horizontal gaze.</p>\n<p><strong>Conjugate eye movements</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Binocular movements in which the eyes move synchronously and symmetrically in the same direction.</li>\n<li>Main types\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Saccades</strong>: They are <strong>fast</strong> movements controlled by the <strong>contralateral frontal lobe.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pursuits</strong>:<strong> </strong>They are <strong>slow</strong> following movements controlled by the <strong>ipsilateral occipital lobe.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Image:</strong> The <strong>Horizontal gaze</strong> pathway</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Lesions in this pathway</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Horizontal gaze palsy:</strong> Due to lesion in the <strong>PPRF</strong> of the<strong> same side</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Internuclear ophthalmoplegia</strong> (INO): Caused by lesion in the <strong>medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>One and a half syndrome:</strong> Caused by lesions in <strong>both MLF and PPRF</strong> of the <strong>same side</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Vertical gaze palsy</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Center</strong> is located in the <strong>rostral interstitial nucleus of the dorsal midbrain (riMLF)</strong>.</p>\n<p>The important types of vertical gaze palsy are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Parinaud's syndrome:</strong> <strong>Upgaze</strong> palsy</li>\n<li><strong>Steel–Richardsons syndrome:</strong> <strong>Downgaze</strong> palsy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6801","text":"A patient has normal verti​cal saccades. On attempte​d left gaze as shown in t‌he image below, the patie​nt develops left jerk nys‍tagmus. Where is the most​ likely location of the l​esion?","raw_text":"A patient has normal vertical saccades. On attempted left gaze as shown in the image below, the patient develops left jerk nystagmus.  Where is the most likely location of the lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left paramedian pontine re​ticular formation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right third ​nerve nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left medial longitudinal f​asciculus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right medial longitudinal ​fasciculus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Right medial longitudinal fasciculus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/285db156b7a148aabceb3696bed30fd4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Right medial longitudinal fasciculus</strong></p><p>The <strong>most likely location</strong> of the lesion according to the given clinical scenario and the image is the <strong>right medial longitudinal fasciculus.</strong></p>\n<p>The image depicts an attempted left gaze and concludes that there is an adduction deficit in the right eye. This causes jerk nystagmus in the abducting left eye. </p>\n<p>This is most likely due to <strong>a lesion of the medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF) ipsilateral to the side of the adduction deficit.</strong> Hence, this is a <strong>case of a right MLF lesion or Right Internuclear Ophthalmoplegia (INO).</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1252","text":"Ophthalmoplegic migraine c​linically presents as ___​________.","raw_text":"Ophthalmoplegic migraine clinically presents as ___________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Headache with irreversible​ loss of optic nerve func​tion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Headache associated with r​ecurrent third nerve pals​y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Headache associated with t​hird, fourth, and sixth n​erve palsy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Headache associated with o​ptic neuritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Headache associated with third, fourth, and sixth nerve palsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Headache associated with third, fourth, and sixth nerve palsy</strong></p><p><strong>Ophthalmoplegic migraine</strong> refers to headache associated with <strong>third</strong>, <strong>fourth,</strong> and <strong>sixth</strong> nerve palsy.</p>\n<p>Ophthalmoplegic migraine almost always involves the third nerve. Rare cases of sixth nerve involvement, and even rarer cases that involve the fourth nerve, have also been reported.</p>\n<p>Clinical criteria for the diagnosis of ophthalmoplegic migraine include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>prior history</strong> of typical common or <strong>classic migraine</strong> headaches</li>\n<li>Ophthalmoplegia that occurs during (or, rarely, just before) an established migraine attack</li>\n<li>Exclusion of other reasonable causes by clinical and radiological studies</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"D8-FA9A5","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"23_paget_s_disease_and_hyperparathyroidism","title":"23.Paget'S Disease And Hyperparathyroidism","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MD0909","text":"Which of the following con​ditions is characterized ‍by increased osteoclastic‌ activity?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is characterized by increased osteoclastic activity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteop​etrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteogenesi​s imperfecta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteom​alacia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paget’s​ disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Paget’s disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b2ce676c79b84030bb4d497602cd1329x1280x1434.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Paget’s disease</strong></p><p><strong>Paget's disease</strong> is characterized by <strong>increased osteoclastic</strong> activity. This is followed by a compensatory increase in osteoblastic activity. Hence, it is a disease of <strong>increased bone turnover</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7291","text":"Which of the following is ​characteristic of Paget's‍ disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is characteristic of Paget's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrease in bone mineraliz​ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased alkaline phospha​tase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased b​one turnover","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased degradation of c​ollagen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased bone turnover","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased bone turnover</strong></p><p>Paget’s disease is characterized by<strong> increased bone turnover</strong> with<strong> </strong>enlargement and thickening of the bone. It is also called <strong>osteitis deformans</strong>.</p>\n<p>It manifests in 4th and 5th-decade males.</p>\n<p>The pathologic process in Paget's disease is initiated by overactive<strong> osteoclastic bone resorption</strong> followed by a compensatory<strong> increase in osteoblastic</strong> new bone formation. This results in a structurally disorganized <strong>mosaic</strong> of woven and lamellar bone causing the bones to be <strong>unusually brittle</strong>. Plasma alkaline phosphatase is increased due to the osteoblastic activity.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7323","text":"Which of the following set​ of findings is likely to‍ be seen in patient's wit‌h Paget's disease? Serum ‌calcium Serum phosphate S‍erum ALP 1 increased incr‌eased increased 2 normal ​normal increased 3 decrea​sed decreased normal 4 de‌creased normal increased","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following set of findings is likely to be seen in patient's with Paget's disease?</p>\n<table style=\"width: 404.438px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 33px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px; height: 18px;\">Serum calcium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 18px;\">Serum phosphate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109.438px; height: 18px;\">Serum ALP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 33px; height: 18px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px; height: 18px;\">increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 18px;\">increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109.438px; height: 18px;\">increased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15.5625px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 33px; height: 15.5625px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px; height: 15.5625px;\">normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 15.5625px;\">normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109.438px; height: 15.5625px;\">increased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 33px; height: 18px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px; height: 18px;\">decreased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 18px;\">decreased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109.438px; height: 18px;\">normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 33px; height: 18px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px; height: 18px;\">decreased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 18px;\">normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109.438px; height: 18px;\">increased</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2</strong></p><p>Patients with Paget' disease have normal levels of serum calcium and<strong> raised serum ALP</strong>. It is due to increased bone formation.</p>\n<p>Hypocalcemia can be rarely seen in patients with Paget's disease. It is due to active bone formation with insufficient calcium and vitamin D intake.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7319","text":"A patient is diagnosed wit​h Paget's disease. X-ray ‍of the patient is given b‌elow. In which phase of t‌he disease is the patient‍ at?","raw_text":"A patient is diagnosed with Paget's disease. X-ray of the patient is given below. In which phase of the disease is the patient at?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteolyt​ic phase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Phase of bo​ne formation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sclerot​ic phase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteoporo​tic phase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Osteolytic phase","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/31aa75d373a146bc9df72d2d0fc9b37a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7fbda48b52dd43e382e3f07a8ff7c55dx720x720.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Osteolytic phase</strong></p><p>The given X-ray shows <strong>blade of grass </strong>deformity seen in the <strong>osteolytic phase </strong>of Paget's disease.</p>\n<p>An initial <strong>osteolytic phase</strong> involves prominent <strong>bone resorption </strong>and marked hypervascularization. Radiographically, this manifests as an advancing lytic wedge or<strong> blade of grass </strong>or<strong> flame shaped</strong> lesion.</p>\n<p>The <strong>second phase</strong> is a period of very active bone formation and resorption. It is characterised by normal lamellar bone replaced with haphazard (woven) bone.</p>\n<p>In the final <strong>sclerotic phase</strong>, bone resorption declines progressively and leads to a hard, dense, less vascular mosaic bone, which represents the so-called burned-out phase of Paget’s disease.</p>\n<p>The arrow mark in the following image indicates blade of grass appearance.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7322","text":"A 53-year-old woman is fou​nd to have raised serum a‍lkaline phosphatase. Urin‌ary examination reveals i‌ncreased excretion of hyd‍roxyproline in urine. X-r‌ay skull of the patient s​hows cotton wool appearan​ce. Which of the followin‌g statements is false reg​arding the treatment of t​his condition?","raw_text":"A 53-year-old woman is found to have raised serum alkaline phosphatase. Urinary examination reveals increased excretion of hydroxyproline in urine. X-ray skull of the patient shows cotton wool appearance. Which of the following statements is false regarding the treatment of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bisphosphonates decrease b​one resorption and destru‍ction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bisphosphonate is the drug​ of choice","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Salmon calcitonin is more ​effective than porcine ca‍lcitonin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Etidronate is the commonly​ used bisphosphonate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Etidronate is the commonly used bisphosphonate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6a5eaf5eeda14dada3aac15ea7801223x720x555.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Etidronate is the commonly used bisphosphonate</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and X-ray showing <strong>cotton wool skull</strong> is suggestive of <strong>Paget's disease.</strong></p>\n<p>Pharmacological therapy includes use of <strong>bisphosphonates </strong>and <strong>calcitonin</strong>. They primarily reduce the rapid bone resorption and also increase the bone formation. Zolendronic acid is the preferred bisphosphonate.</p>\n<p>The given below X-ray shows cotton wool skull.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7324","text":"A patient presents with ch​ronic back ache and heari‍ng loss. His X-ray spine ‌is given below. Which of ‌the following is not a co‍mplication of this diseas‌e?","raw_text":"A patient presents with chronic back ache and hearing loss. His X-ray spine is given below. Which of the following is not a complication of this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High output cardiac failur​e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteoar​thritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spinal ​stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteoch​ondroma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Osteochondroma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e972a6d500af47fd9f8f52872c59a76f.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Osteochondroma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and X-ray showing <strong>ivory vertebrae</strong> is suggestive of <strong>Paget's disease</strong>. Paget’s disease of the bone increases the risk of <strong>osteosarcoma</strong> and not osteochondroma.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> of Paget's disease are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Malignancy\n<ul>\n<li>Osteosarcoma (most common)</li>\n<li>Fibrosarcoma</li>\n<li>Chondrosarcoma</li>\n<li>Sarcoma of myeloid and mesenchymal element</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Increased risk of fractures due to decreased bone strength</li>\n<li><strong>Osteoarthritis</strong></li>\n<li>Bony overgrowth leading to<strong> spinal stenosis</strong> and nerve compression</li>\n<li><strong>High output cardiac failure</strong> due to increased blood flow to skeletal bones</li>\n<li>Hypercalcemia in patients with prolonged immobilization</li>\n<li>2nd, 5th, 7th, and 8th cranial nerve palsies</li>\n<li>Deafness due to nerve compression &gt; otosclerosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7295","text":"An elderly male patient pr​esents with kyphosis and ‍bowing of legs. There is ‌increased warmth over his‌ limbs and he is diagnose‍d with high frequency sen‌sorineural hearing loss. ​His X-ray skull is given ​below. What is the most l‌ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An elderly male patient presents with kyphosis and bowing of legs. There is increased warmth over his limbs and he is diagnosed with high frequency sensorineural hearing loss. His X-ray skull is given below.  What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteop​etrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paget’s​ disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Multiple​ myeloma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Eosinophili​c granuloma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Paget’s disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fd0fcf55f9884d74855fc7f9a5de424f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Paget’s disease</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and X-ray showing <strong>cotton wool skull</strong> and <strong>Tam</strong> <strong>O'Shanter </strong>appearance is suggestive of <strong>Paget's disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> rapid bone formation and resorption</strong> in Paget's disease can lead to <strong>increased blood flow</strong> within the bone and the surrounding tissues. This leads to <strong>increased warmth</strong> over the limbs. They may also have kyphosis and bowing of legs. <strong>Increased bone formation</strong> in Paget's disease may lead to the <strong>narrowing of the foramina of the skull</strong>. This causes compression of the cranial nerves which may lead to deafness.</p>\n<p>Deafness due to the narrowing of cranial nerve foramina is also seen in osteopetrosis. However, it can be differentiated from Paget's disease by the absence of lytic lesions of the bone.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7433","text":"Which of the following con​ditions is not characteri‍sed by osteosclerosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is not characterised by osteosclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paget's​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperpara​thyroidism","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fluo​rosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Renal oste​odystrophy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hyperparathyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hyperparathyroidism</strong></p><p>The radiological feature of <strong>hyperparathyroidism</strong> is <strong>subperiosteal bone resorption </strong>and not osteosclerosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Osteosclerosis</strong> is a disorder that is characterized by abnormal hardening of bone and an elevation in bone density. It is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Renal osteodystrophy</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Flurosis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Paget's disease of bone</strong></li>\n<li>Osteogenic bone metastasis caused by carcinoma of prostate and breast</li>\n<li>Osteosclerosing types of chronic osteomyelitis</li>\n<li>Hypervitaminosis D</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7358","text":"Which of the following is ​not a function of parathy‍roid hormone?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a function of parathyroid hormone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreases phosphate reabso​rption from the kidneys","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increases bo​ne resorption","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increases vitamin D format​ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increases calcium absorpti​on from the intestine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Increases calcium absorption from the intestine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fa3890f4ff4740dea6ca09fa72cf2944x1280x1909.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Increases calcium absorption from the intestine</strong></p><p>The parathyroid hormone does not increase calcium absorption from the intestine directly.</p>\n<p><strong>Parathyroid hormone</strong> (PTH) is produced by the parathyroid glands. It is regulated by the levels of serum ionized calcium. It stimulates the enzyme <strong>1α-hydroxylase </strong>which converts 25-hydroxycholecalciferol to <strong>1,25- dihydroxycholecalciferol </strong>(active form of vitamin D).It only increases the formation of <strong>vitamin D</strong> which increases calcium absorption from intestine. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1673","text":"A patient with recurrent e​pisodes of urinary tract ‍infection and renal stone‌s has the following labor‌atory findings. Which of ‍the following signs may b‌e seen in the radiograph ​of the skull in such a pa​tient? Parathyroid hormon‌e: 100 pg/mL Serum calciu​m: 15.5 mg/dL Serum phosp​hate: 2 mg/dL Serum ALP: ‍750 IU/L","raw_text":"<p>A patient with recurrent episodes of urinary tract infection and renal stones has the following laboratory findings. Which of the following signs may be seen in the radiograph of the skull in such a patient?</p>\n<p>Parathyroid hormone: 100 pg/mL</p>\n<p>Serum calcium: 15.5 mg/dL</p>\n<p>Serum phosphate: 2 mg/dL</p>\n<p>Serum ALP: 750 IU/L</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cotton w​ool skull","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Geograph​ic skull","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Salt and p​epper skull","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Punched out ​lytic lesion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Salt and pepper skull","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Salt and pepper skull</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hyperparathyroidism</strong> in which <strong>salt and pepper skull</strong> is a radiographical finding.</p>\n<p>Option A: The <strong>cotton wool skull </strong>is seen in <strong>Paget’s disease</strong>. It is due to thickened, disorganized bony trabaculae which appear as sclerosis on radiographs.</p>\n<p>Option B: The <strong>geographic skull</strong> is seen in <strong>eosinophilic granuloma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Multiple punched-out lesions</strong> are seen in<strong> multiple myeloma</strong>. </p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Brown tumor</strong> in hyperparathyroidism is also known as Von Recklinghausen's disease of bone. <strong>Neurofibromatosis l</strong> is also known as Von Recklinghausen's disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1717","text":"An elderly woman with comp​lains of backache, fatigu‍e, and low mood is found ‌to have hypercalcemia and‌ renal stones. X-ray of h‍er hands is shown below. ‌What is the most probable​ diagnosis?","raw_text":"An elderly woman with complains of backache, fatigue, and low mood is found to have hypercalcemia and renal stones. X-ray of her hands is shown below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple​ myeloma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metastati​c disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperpara​thyroidism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Osteom​alacia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hyperparathyroidism","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0acf9ddab2814f2cad45659484e51d3b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hyperparathyroidism</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the X-ray showing <strong>subperiosteal bone resorption </strong>in the radial aspect of middle phalanges is suggestive of <strong>hyperparathyroidism</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hyperparathyroidism presents with the classical findings of<strong> “bones, stones, groans, moans with fatigue overtones” </strong>which occur as a consequence of hypercalcemia.</p>\n<p>Though multiple myeloma and metastatic disease also present with hypercalcemia, subperiosteal bone resorption is <strong>pathognomonic</strong> of hyperparathyroidism. Normal levels of PTH can help in differentiating these conditions from hyperparathyroidism.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7392","text":"A 55-year-old man with end​-stage renal disease pres‍ents with back pain. His ‌history is significant fo‌r frequent pathological f‍ractures. Identify the fi‌nding seen in his lumbosa​cral spine radiograph giv​en below.","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man with end-stage renal disease presents with back pain. His history is significant for frequent pathological fractures. Identify the finding seen in his lumbosacral spine radiograph given below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Picture fra​me vertebrae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ivory v​ertebrae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bamboo​ spine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rugger je​rsey spine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rugger jersey spine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f4db43a3398946d994f6e449f2f2a8ef.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/83029f2041d44fedbb2fe424c7dd7b55x720x879.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/885075e8b2bd4b299d38510c8c033898x487x387.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rugger jersey spine</strong></p><p>The radiograph given above shows a <strong>rugger jersey spine </strong>which is a characteristic feature of renal osteodystrophy. The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>renal osteodystrophy</strong> which is caused by hyperparathyroidism secondary to chronic renal disease. </p>\n<p>Rugger jersey appearance is seen due to <strong>osteosclerosis </strong>of the superior and inferior <strong>vertebral endplates</strong>. This occurs due to excess osteoid formation by the osteoblasts to counteract increased bone resorption. The term rugger jersey appearance is used because there are alternating bands of increased and decreased bone density,<strong> </strong>similar to the jersey of a rugby player.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1721","text":"A 40-year-old man presents​ with pain and stiffness ‍of elbow joint. Examinati‌on shows mottled upper in‌cisors. X-ray of the pati‍ent is given below. What ‌is the most probable diag​nosis?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presents with pain and stiffness of elbow joint. Examination shows mottled upper incisors. X-ray of the patient is given below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperpara​thyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteop​etrosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fluo​rosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypervita​minosis D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fluorosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c1cca7d4bbff4637bf167d16a7d42331.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fluorosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the X-ray showing <strong>interosseous</strong> <strong>membrane ossification</strong> is suggestive of <strong>fluorosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Fluorosis first starts as mottling of the enamel of upper incisors. Fluorine stimulates <strong>osteoblastic</strong> activity and fluorapatite crystals are laid down in the bone. These are usually resistent to secondary osteoclastic resorption. This leads to calcium retention, osteosclerosis, and development of osteophytes. <strong>Ossification of ligaments</strong> and fascial attachments is characteristic of this condition.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7333","text":"Which of the following con​ditions does not cause su‍b-periosteal new bone dep‌osition?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions does not cause sub-periosteal new bone deposition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperpara​thyroidism","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fluo​rosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Caffey's​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t​he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hyperparathyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hyperparathyroidism</strong></p><p><strong>Hyperparathyroidism</strong> is associated with<strong> sub-periosteal bone resorption</strong> and not a new bone deposition.</p>\n<p>The<strong> subperiosteal new bone deposition</strong> is an important feature of both<strong> fluorosis </strong>and<strong> Caffey’s disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Caffey’s disease also known as <strong>infantile cortical hyperostosis</strong> is a self-limiting disorder. Classically the onset of the disease occurs <strong>before</strong> the<strong> 5<sup>th </sup>month</strong> of life. It is characterized by</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Soft tissue swelling</li>\n<li>Rapid sub-periosteal new bone formation</li>\n<li>Cortical thickening of underlying bones </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7449","text":"A 50-year-old man presente​d with spastic paraparesi‍s. On examination, genu v‌algus deformity was found‌. CT of cervical spine sh‍owed the following findin‌g. What is the pathophysi​ology behind this conditi​on?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man presented with spastic paraparesis. On examination, genu valgus deformity was found. CT of cervical spine showed the following finding. What is the pathophysiology behind this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased bone mineral den​sity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased vitamin D levels​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased resistance to os​teoclasts","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased parathyroid horm​one levels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased resistance to osteoclasts","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dbcd3b1e048440ffb21318ae6a15b087.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7b6e48fce1ed4b4cb53de18e72b150a1x1280x1418.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased resistance to osteoclasts</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and CT of cervical spine showing<strong> ossified posterior longitudinal ligament</strong> is suggestive of fluorosis. Fluorine stimulates<strong> osteoblastic activity</strong> and fluorapatite crystals are laid down in bone These are <strong>resistant</strong> to<strong> osteoclastic resorption</strong>.</p>\n<p>Fluorosis is a disease where excessive deposition of calcium occurs in bone and soft tissues. This leads to calcium retention, impaired mineralization and secondary hyperparathyroidism.</p>\n<p>The characteristic pathology involves <strong>sub-periosteal new bone formation</strong>, <strong>osteosclerosis </strong>and hyperostosis at the bony attachments of ligaments, tendons, and fascia. It is most commonly seen vertebrae, ribs and pelvis. </p>\n<p>The CT scan given below shows ossified posterior longitudinal ligament. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7332","text":"A 50-year-old Arctic explo​rer presents with complai‍nts of bone pain, headach‌e and vomiting. There is ‌no history of fever and y‍ou suspect elevated intra‌cranial pressure. Radiogr​aphs of long bones reveal​ increased density in the‌ metaphyseal region and s​ubperiosteal calcificatio​n. What is your diagnosis‍?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old Arctic explorer presents with complaints of bone pain, headache and vomiting. There is no history of fever and you suspect elevated intracranial pressure. Radiographs of long bones reveal increased density in the metaphyseal region and subperiosteal calcification. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypervita​minosis A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple​ myeloma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Secon​daries","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperpara​thyroidism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypervitaminosis A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypervitaminosis A</strong></p><p>The. given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> hypervitaminosis A</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hypervitaminosis A more commonly occurs in children following excessive intake. It also occurs in adults who consume polar bear livers. An increased vitamin A level in the body can manifest in the form of<strong> pseudotumor cerebri</strong> (idiopathic intracranial hypertension). X-ray shows increased density in metaphyseal region due to<strong> excessive mineralization</strong> of bone.</p>\n<p>Note: Hypervitaminosis D also causes metabolic bone disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7381","text":"A 50-year-old woman has mu​ltiple lytic lesions in t‍he skull. Which of the fo‌llowing is the least like‌ly cause of this finding?‍","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman has multiple lytic lesions in the skull.  Which of the following is the least likely cause of this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple​ myeloma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperpara​thyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metas​tasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Eosinophili​c granuloma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Eosinophilic granuloma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Eosinophilic granuloma</strong></p><p><strong>Eosinophilic granuloma</strong> is the <strong>least likely</strong> cause of multiple lytic lesions in the skull of an <strong>elderly</strong> woman. It is a very rare benign bone tumor which presents in the younger age group (5-15 years).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"9PdERYBX","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"23_childhood_respiratory_disorders","title":"23.Childhood Respiratory Disorders","num_questions":32,"total_marks":128,"duration":42,"time_per_question":78.75,"questions":[{"id":"MB9531","text":"A mother brought a healthy​-looking, 3-month-old inf‍ant for routine immunizat‍ion. As you were giving t​he shot, you noticed some‍ noisy breathing in the b‌aby, which became worse o‌nce he started crying. Ho‍wever, it decreased, as y​ou held him in a prone po​sition. There were no cle‍ar signs of distress. Acc​ording to his mother, he ‌had been feeding well at ​home and has not had any ‍episodes of apnea or cyan​osis. Which is the most l‍ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A mother brought a healthy-looking, 3-month-old infant for routine immunization. As you were giving the shot, you noticed some noisy breathing in the baby, which became worse once he started crying. However, it decreased, as you held him in a prone position. There were no clear signs of distress. According to his mother, he had been feeding well at home and has not had any episodes of apnea or cyanosis. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Laryngeal​ stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Laryng​eal web","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Laryngo​malacia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Laryngeal​ papilloma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Laryngomalacia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3c41e8790dcf4087bf87a9691f0ec405x720x720.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Laryngomalacia</strong></p><p>A clinical picture of inspiratory <strong>stridor</strong> that <strong>worsens on crying</strong> and <strong>relieves in a prone</strong> position in a newborn <strong>without respiratory distress</strong> is suggestive of <strong>laryngomalacia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Laryngomalacia is the <strong>most common</strong> congenital laryngeal anomaly and the most common cause of stridor in infants and children. Stridor is exacerbated by any exertion: crying, agitation, or feeding. In the prone position, the stridor disappears.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>On laryngoscopy, an <strong>omega-shaped</strong> epiglottis is visualized. </p>\n<p>In most children, only <strong>expectant observation</strong> is required as the condition resolves secondary to nervous system maturation, within <strong>18 to 24</strong> <strong>months</strong> of birth. However, neonates with severe laryngomalacia may develop marked upper airway obstruction resulting in cor pulmonale, life-threatening apneic episodes, feeding difficulties, obstructive sleep apnea, and failure to thrive. In such cases, <strong>surgical resection of supraglottic tissues</strong> and <strong>supraglottoplasty</strong> has been the favoured approach. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9530","text":"A senior casualty nurse in​forms you that a 5-year-o‍ld girl with severe respi‍ratory distress might req​uire intubation. You just‍ finished stabilising and‌ placing an ET tube in Mo‌han, a 57-year-old man wi‍th head trauma and a GCS ​of 7. Which of the follow​ing is not a feature of c‍hild's airway?","raw_text":"A senior casualty nurse informs you that a 5-year-old girl with severe respiratory distress might require intubation. You just finished stabilising and placing an ET tube in Mohan, a 57-year-old man with head trauma and a GCS of 7. Which of the following is not a feature of child's airway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The larynx being located h​igher in the neck at the ‍level of C3-C4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The narrowest part being t​he subglottic region","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The tongue being relativel​y larger","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Having a cylindrical shape​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Having a cylindrical shape","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Having a cylindrical shape</strong></p><p>The pediatric airway is <strong>funnel shaped</strong>. In comparison, the adult airway is cylindrical.</p>\n<p>In newborns, the larynx is situated at the level of <strong>C3-C4</strong>, where it facilitates simultaneous respiration and swallowing during feeding.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9532","text":"During a night shift, you ​get a call from the ER to‍ come and see a toddler, ‍who presented with compla​ints of fever, drooling, ‍and a muffled voice since‌ evening. On examination,‌ the child has a stridor ‍and you pick up a \"thumb ​sign\" on the lateral x-ra​y of his neck. What is th‍e most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"During a night shift, you get a call from the ER to come and see a toddler, who presented with complaints of fever, drooling, and a muffled voice since evening. On examination, the child has a stridor and you pick up a \"thumb sign\" on the lateral x-ray of his neck. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cr​oup","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute epi​glottitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Foreign bod​y aspiration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retropharyn​geal abscess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acute epiglottitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a7fe8389fc634856bb9010d8ae4b7fa1x467x422.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acute epiglottitis</strong></p><p><strong>Thumb sign </strong>on lateral x-ray of neck is pathognomonic for <strong>acute epiglottitis (AE)</strong>. It is seen due to epiglottic enlargement.</p>\n<p>AE is an inflammation of the epiglottis. The arytenoids, false cords, and posterior tongue may also be involved.</p>\n<p>In children, <em><strong>Haemophilus influenzae type B (Hib)</strong></em> is the most common cause. This has decreased dramatically since the widespread availability of immunization (pentavalent). <em>Streptococcus pyogenes, S. pneumoniae</em>, and <em>S. aureus</em> have also been implicated. Non-infectious causes can be foreign body ingestion and trauma from thermal or caustic agents.</p>\n<p>It has a progressive and potentially fulminating course, characterised by high fever, sore throat, dyspnea, and rapidly progressing respiratory obstruction.</p>\n<p>AE is a <strong>clinical diagnosis</strong> and a true emergency, X-ray soft tissue neck is not necessary to confirm it.  It may be used to rule out differentials such as laryngotracheobronchitis, foreign body obstruction, retropharyngeal abscess or congenital anomalies. The time taken for X-rays should be avoided in a critically ill child due to the risk of sudden complete airway obstruction.</p>\n<p>The definitive diagnosis of AE is made during <strong>direct laryngoscopy. </strong>However, since manipulation can induce a laryngospasm, it is <strong>generally avoided</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below shows thumb sign:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9534","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common etiologic‍al agent associated with ‍epiglottitis in children,​ overall in the world?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common etiological agent associated with epiglottitis in children, overall in the world?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H. Influe​nza type B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Influen​za virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mora​xella","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Strept​ococcus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Streptococcus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Streptococcus</strong></p><p>The most common etiological agent, overall in the world, of acute epiglottitis in the pediatric age group, now is <em>Streptococcus</em>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">In the past, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> type b (Hib) used to be the most commonly identified cause of acute epiglottitis. But, since the widespread use of the <em>H. influenzae</em> type b vaccine, invasive disease caused by <em>H. influenzae</em> type b in pediatric patients has been reduced by 99%.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Therefore, other organisms, such as <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em> (GAS), <em>Streptococcus penumoniae</em>, non-typeable <em>H. influenzae</em>, and <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, have become the common causes of acute epiglottitis in pediatric age group, overall in the world.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Lateral X-ray of the neck shows the <strong>thumb sign </strong>in acute epiglottitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF6078","text":"A 5 year old boy was suffe​ring from low grade fever‍, inspiratory stridor and‍ barking cough for past 5​ days. His chest X-ray is‍ given below. What is the‌ cause of this condition?‌","raw_text":"A 5 year old boy was suffering from low grade fever, inspiratory stridor and barking cough for past 5 days. His chest X-ray is given below. What is the cause of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mora​xella","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parain​fluenza","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hemophilus​ influenza","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphyloco​ccus aureus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Parainfluenza","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a48f0784d9574f9b998d3e7d37e96180.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Parainfluenza</strong></p><p><strong>Barking cough</strong> is a feature of <strong>laryngotracheobronchitis,</strong> which is caused by the <strong>parainfluenza virus. </strong>The X-ray finding is the '<strong>Steeple sign'</strong> due to subglottic narrowing.</p>\n<p>The age of presentation of laryngotracheobronchitis ranges from<strong> </strong>6 months-6 years. The clinical features include barking cough, inspiratory stridor, hoarseness, low-grade fever.</p>\n<p>Management:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Racemic epinephrine nebulization</strong> leads to the rapid improvement of upper airway obstruction. Epinephrine constricts pre-capillary arterioles in the upper airway mucosa and decreases capillary hydrostatic pressure, leading to fluid resorption and <strong>improvement</strong> in <strong>airway edema.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Dexamethasone</strong>-by the <strong>least invasive</strong> route (Oral&gt;IV&gt;IM ). It is indicated in all the grades of croup.</li>\n<li><strong>Oxygen</strong> should be administered to children who are hypoxemic (SpO2 &lt;92 %)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Antibiotics are not indicated in laryngotracheobronchitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9533","text":"2-year-old Neha has been h​aving fever since yesterd‍ay and is refusing food. ‍Parents became alarmed wh​en she developed an abnor‍mal barking cough today m‌orning and have brought h‌er to the casualty. On ex‍amination, the child look​ed exhausted and had visi​ble chest retractions and‍ stridor. The temperature​ was 39.6 degrees and SpO‌2 was 93% on room air. Ch​est X-ray reveals a 'stee‍ple' sign. What is the tr​eatment of choice in this‍ child?","raw_text":"2-year-old Neha has been having fever since yesterday and is refusing food. Parents became alarmed when she developed an abnormal barking cough today morning and have brought her to the casualty. On examination, the child looked exhausted and had visible chest retractions and stridor. The temperature was 39.6 degrees and SpO2 was 93% on room air. Chest X-ray reveals a 'steeple' sign. What is the treatment of choice in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Racemic epinephrine nebuli​zation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Humidified oxygen and salb​utamol nebulization","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Send a throat swab and sta​rt IV antibiotics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High dose dexamethasone in​jection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Racemic epinephrine nebulization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Racemic epinephrine nebulization</strong></p><p>Fever, barking cough, inspiratory stridor and a steeple sign on chest radiograph are suggestive of <strong>acute laryngotracheobronchitis</strong> (croup). The treatment of choice in this child is <strong>nebulization with racemic epinephrine. </strong></p>\n<p>Epinephrine <strong>acts rapidly</strong> and provides immediate relief. It <strong>constricts pre-capillary arterioles</strong> in the upper airway mucosa and decreases capillary hydrostatic pressure. This increases fluid resorption and <strong>reduces airway edema.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Westley Croup Severity Score:</strong> </p>\n<table style=\"height: 235px; width: 487px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Feature</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 63px; height: 18px;\"><strong> 0</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px; height: 18px;\"><strong>1</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px; height: 18px;\"><strong>2</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 18px;\"><strong>3</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 62px; height: 18px;\"><strong>4</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 21px; height: 18px;\"><strong>5</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Stridor</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 63px; height: 36px;\">None</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px; height: 36px;\">With agitation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px; height: 36px;\"><strong>At rest</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 62px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 21px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Air Entry</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 63px; height: 36px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px; height: 36px;\">Decreased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px; height: 36px;\">Markedly decreased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 62px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 21px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Retractions</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 63px; height: 18px;\">None</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Mild</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px; height: 18px;\">Moderate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 18px;\">Severe</td>\n<td style=\"width: 62px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 21px; height: 18px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Cyanosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 63px; height: 36px;\">None</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 62px; height: 36px;\">With agitation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 21px; height: 36px;\">At Rest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 55px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102px; height: 55px;\"><strong>Level of Consciousness</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 63px; height: 55px;\"><strong>Normal,</strong> including sleep</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px; height: 55px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 72px; height: 55px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 55px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 62px; height: 55px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 21px; height: 55px;\">Disoriented</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Overall score and severity</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>0 to 2- mild</li>\n<li><strong>3 to 7- moderate</strong></li>\n<li>8 to 11- severe</li>\n<li>12 to 17- impending respiratory failure</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment of moderate to severe croup :</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Racemic <strong>epinephrine</strong> nebulization</li>\n<li><strong>Dexamethasone </strong>is indicated in all grades of croup and is given<strong> </strong>by the least invasive route <strong>(Oral</strong>&gt;IM&gt;IV<strong>)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Oxygen</strong> should be administered to children who are hypoxemic (SpO2 &lt;92 %)</li>\n<li>Antibiotics are <strong>not indicated</strong> in croup</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9535","text":"A child in your unit is ad​mitted with acute bronchi‍olitis. Which of the foll‍owing is the most common ​etiological agent respons‍ible for this condition d‌uring infancy?","raw_text":"A child in your unit is admitted with acute bronchiolitis. Which of the following is the most common etiological agent responsible for this condition during infancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Influen​za virus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Para Influ​enza virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rhino​virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Respiratory syncytial viru​s","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Respiratory syncytial virus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Respiratory syncytial virus</strong></p><p>Acute bronchiolitis is predominantly a <strong>viral</strong> disease in children <strong>younger than 2 years</strong>. <strong>Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV)</strong> is the most likely etiological agent and is responsible for more than 50% of cases.</p>\n<p>Acute bronchiolitis refers to airway inflammation and obstruction of the lower respiratory tract.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of bronchiolitis include fever, cough, <strong>fine inspiratory crackles, expiratory wheezes</strong>, and breathing difficulty. Cyanosis may be seen in severe cases.</p>\n<p>Radiographs may be normal in some patients. In the majority of them, there will be <strong>interstitial infiltrates</strong>, <strong>hyper-expansion</strong> of the <strong>chest</strong>, and <strong>peribronchial thickening</strong>. </p>\n<p>Management is mainly conservative. Hypoxemic children should receive cool, humidified oxygen. Rarely, a child might develop respiratory failure and require hospitalization and invasive ventilation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9537","text":"10-month-old Ishaan was pr​esented to the OPD with a‍ 3-day history of lower r‍espiratory tract symptoms​ and an expiratory wheeze‍. Chest X-ray showed mild‌ peribronchial thickening‌. Which of the following ‍statements is false regar​ding the condition descri​bed?","raw_text":"10-month-old Ishaan was presented to the OPD with a 3-day history of lower respiratory tract symptoms and an expiratory wheeze. Chest X-ray showed mild peribronchial thickening.  Which of the following statements is false regarding the condition described?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The risk is higher for inf​ants with young mothers o‍r mothers who smoked duri‍ng pregnancy.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is more common in child​ren who have not been bre‍astfed.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The mainstay of treatment ​is antibiotics.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"There is a higher incidenc​e of wheezing and asthma ‍in children with a histor‍y of bronchiolitis.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The mainstay of treatment is antibiotics.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The mainstay of treatment is antibiotics.</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute bronchiolitis.</strong> The <strong>treatment</strong> is mainly<strong> supportive</strong>. Hypoxemic children should receive cool, humidified oxygen. Rarely, a child might develop respiratory failure and require hospitalization and invasive ventilation.</p>\n<p>It is more common in boys, in those who have <strong>not been breastfed</strong>, and in those who live in crowded conditions. The risk is higher for infants with <strong>young </strong>mothers or mothers who <strong>smoked</strong> during pregnancy.</p>\n<p>Acute bronchiolitis is predominantly a viral disease caused by <strong>respiratory syncytial virus (RSV)</strong> and antibiotics are not indicated.</p>\n<p>There is a higher incidence of wheezing and asthma in children with a history of bronchiolitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9538","text":"The pediatric intensivist ​is contemplating whether ‍to start aerosolized riba‍virin in a young boy with​ a severe respiratory inf‍ection. There would be a ‌clear indication to start‌ the drug if _________.","raw_text":"The pediatric intensivist is contemplating whether to start aerosolized ribavirin in a young boy with a severe respiratory infection. There would be a clear indication to start the drug if _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The child is immunocomprom​ised","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The child has not received​ any immunization","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"There is a history of seve​re atopy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"There is a history of recu​rrent infection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The child is immunocompromised","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The child is immunocompromised</strong></p><p>Ribavirin is the only specific antiviral therapy available for the treatment of <strong>severe bronchiolitis</strong> caused by <strong>RSV</strong> infections. It is reserved as a last-line agent for life-threatening bronchiolitis in patients who are <strong>immunocompromised</strong> or who have <strong>hemodynamically significant cardiopulmonary disease.</strong>  In otherwise healthy children, the management of acute RSV bronchiolitis is typically supportive. </p>\n<p>Ribavirin is a prodrug, which when metabolized resembles purine RNA nucleotides. In this form, it interferes with RNA metabolism required for viral replication. </p>\n<p><strong>MNEMONIC: </strong>(for indications of ribavirin) </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C-RIBA</strong>virin:\n<ul>\n<li>Hepatitis <strong>C</strong></li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>SV</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>nfluenza <strong>B</strong></li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>renaviruses (Lassa, Bolivian, etc.)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note</strong> - Ribavirin is also used in the treatment of hepatitis C and some viral hemorrhagic fevers.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9536","text":"3-year-old Sravya was refe​rred with a one-week hist‍ory of being unable to lo‍ok upwards, constant neck​ flexion, and deviation t‍o the right side. Parents‌ also complained of feedi‌ng difficulty. On examina‍tion, the child was noted​ to have poor oral hygien​e and the neck was tender‍ on palpation. The X-ray ​is given below. Which of ‌the following statements ​are false about the given‍ condition?","raw_text":"3-year-old Sravya was referred with a one-week history of being unable to look upwards, constant neck flexion, and deviation to the right side. Parents also complained of feeding difficulty. On examination, the child was noted to have poor oral hygiene and the neck was tender on palpation. The X-ray is given below. Which of the following statements are false about the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It occurs mostly in childr​en younger than 3-4 years‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is more c​ommon in boys","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Examination shows unilater​al bulging of the posteri‍or pharyngeal wall","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trauma to the posterior ph​arynx is the most common ‍cause","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Trauma to the posterior pharynx is the most common cause","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/af51b1c3b83d4a8cb4692187b7c62470.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Trauma to the posterior pharynx is the most common cause</strong></p><p>The history of progressive torticollis, neck pain and dysphagia in a young child along with a neck X-ray demonstrating a prevertebral soft-tissue swelling are all indicative of a <strong>retropharyngeal abscess. </strong>In young children<strong>, an antecedent upper respiratory tract infection </strong>is the most common cause of an RP abscess and <strong>not trauma. </strong></p>\n<p>It involves the suppuration of lymph nodes present in the RP space and is more common in <strong>children younger than 3-4 years </strong>since these lymph node chains <strong>involute</strong> after 5 years of age. <strong>Boys </strong>are affected more often than girls. In<strong> older children and adults</strong>, the cause is usually<strong> trauma</strong> to the posterior pharynx, which leads to inoculation of the retropharyngeal space.</p>\n<p>Risk factors include poor oral hygiene, diabetes, an immunocompromised state, and low socioeconomic status.</p>\n<p>Clinical manifestations are nonspecific and include fever, decreased oral intake, and drooling. Neck stiffness and torticollis may also be present. As the abscess grows in size, it can also lead to upper airway obstruction and asphyxiation. The physical examination can reveal <strong>unilateral</strong> bulging of the posterior pharyngeal wall. </p>\n<p>Although lateral radiograph is used for initial imaging, <strong>CT of the neck with intravenous contrast</strong> is the best imaging study to evaluate patients with a retropharyngeal abscess. Treatment ranges from prolonged courses of intravenous antibiotics to surgical incision and drainage.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9539","text":"The most common cause of b​acterial pneumonia in chi‍ldren 3 weeks to 4 years ‍of age is ______.","raw_text":"The most common cause of bacterial pneumonia in children 3 weeks to 4 years of age is ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Haemophilus​ influenzae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcu​s pneumoniae","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococc​us pyogenes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphyloco​ccus aureus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Streptococcus pneumoniae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Streptococcus pneumoniae</strong></p><p><strong><em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em></strong> (pneumococcus) is the most common <strong>bacterial</strong> pathogen in children 3 weeks to 4 years of age.</p>\n<p>Aetiology of infectious pneumonia grouped by age of the patient:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 251px; width: 752.797px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161px;\"><strong>Age group</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 575.797px;\"><strong>Frequent pathogens (in order of frequency)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161px;\">&lt; 3 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 575.797px;\">Group B Streptococcus, E.coli, gram-negative bacilli, S. pneumoniae, Hemophilus influenzae (type b)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161px;\">3 weeks - 4 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 575.797px;\">RSV, other respiratory viruses (rhinovirus, influenza, parainfluenza and adenovirus), <strong>S. pneumoniae</strong>, Hemophilus influenzae (type b), Mycoplasma pneumoniae</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161px;\">&gt; 4 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 575.797px;\">Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Chlamydophila pneumoniae, S. pneumoniae, Hemophilus influenzae (type b)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9626","text":"A 9-year-old boy presented​ with a 4-day history of ‍high-grade fever and a pr‍oductive cough with yello​w sputum. Chest x-ray sho‍wed a consolidation in th‌e right middle zone. Acco‌rding to WHO guidelines, ‍which of the following is​ not an indication for ho​spital admission?","raw_text":"A 9-year-old boy presented with a 4-day history of high-grade fever and a productive cough with yellow sputum. Chest x-ray showed a consolidation in the right middle zone. According to WHO guidelines, which of the following is not an indication for hospital admission?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chest i​ndrawing","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Persisten​t vomiting","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Convu​lsions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Severe ma​lnutrition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chest indrawing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ac092692af4842abbdce039bc6d5802ex517x291.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chest indrawing</strong></p><p>As per the revised 2014 WHO guidelines, it is <strong>safe</strong> to <strong>treat chest indrawing pneumonia at home</strong>.</p>\n<p>However, a child who is <strong>HIV positive </strong>and has <strong>chest indrawing</strong> will need to be referred for <strong>inpatient treatment.</strong> Only those children who have general danger signs are classified as having “severe pneumonia” and need to be referred to a higher level (hospital admission).</p>\n<p><strong>Danger signs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Not able to drink</li>\n<li>Persistent vomiting</li>\n<li>Convulsions</li>\n<li>Lethargic or unconscious</li>\n<li>Stridor in a calm child</li>\n<li>Severe malnutrition</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9540","text":"A 5th standard girl is bro​ught to the OPD with a hi‍gh fever and cough with e‍xpectoration for 3 days. ​Her chest X-ray shows mul‍tiple pneumatoceles. Whic‌h of the following is the‌ most common etiological ‍agent implicated in this ​presentation?","raw_text":"A 5th standard girl is brought to the OPD with a high fever and cough with expectoration for 3 days. Her chest X-ray shows multiple pneumatoceles. Which of the following is the most common etiological agent implicated in this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Staphyloco​ccus aureus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mycobacterium avium intrac​ellulare","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pneumocys​tis carnii","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/640d1bfba49d452991d3e3987c9010e7x718x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p><strong><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></strong> pneumonia manifests as confluent bronchopneumonia, which is often <strong>unilateral</strong> and characterized by the presence of<strong> hemorrhagic necrosis </strong>and<strong> irregular </strong>areas of <strong>cavitation</strong> of the lung parenchyma<strong>,</strong> resulting in<strong> pneumatoceles, empyema, or </strong>at times, <strong>bronchopulmonary fistulas</strong>. </p>\n<p>The following CT image shows cavitating pneumonia by <em>S. aureus</em>:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0809","text":"A one-and-half-year-old ch​ild brought to the hospit‍al with a history of brea‍thlessness, fever, cough,​ and cold. On examination‍, respiratory rate was 46‌/min, and there were no c‌hest retractions. Which i‍s the most appropriate ma​nagement?","raw_text":"A one-and-half-year-old child brought to the hospital with a history of breathlessness, fever, cough, and cold. On examination, respiratory rate was 46/min, and there were no chest retractions. Which is the most appropriate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Give IV antibiotics and ob​serve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Give IV antibiotics and re​fer to higher centre","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oral antibiotics, warn of ​danger signs, and send ho‍me","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"No need of treatment only ​home remedies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Oral antibiotics, warn of danger signs, and send home","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/258c38a90e844f21be5225fcde567d28x1280x757.3534971644613.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Oral antibiotics, warn of danger signs, and send home</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of acute respiratory illness. According to the IMNCI guidelines 2020, this child can be classified as having <strong>'Pneumonia'</strong>. The treatment is <strong>oral amoxicillin, vitamin A and home care advice.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Fast breathing is defined as given in the table below:</p>\n<table border=\"1\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Age of child</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Fast breathing RR</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt;2 months</td>\n<td>60 breaths/min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2-12 months</td>\n<td>50 breaths/min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1-5 years</td>\n<td>40 breaths/min</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9541","text":"An adolescent boy with a f​amily history of asthma p‍resented with intermitten‍t wheeze and cough for 2 ​months. The onset of whee‍ze is triggered during ex‌ercise and while playing ‌at school. Which of the f‍ollowing is preferred as ​regular therapy for the p​revention of his symptoms‍?","raw_text":"An adolescent boy with a family history of asthma presented with intermittent wheeze and cough for 2 months. The onset of wheeze is triggered during exercise and while playing at school. Which of the following is preferred as regular therapy for the prevention of his symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhaled short acting beta ​2 agonist (SABA)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhaled cor​ticosteroids","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inhaled long acting beta 2​ agonist (LABA)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti – Ig​E antibody","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inhaled corticosteroids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inhaled corticosteroids</strong></p><p><strong>Inhaled corticosteroids</strong> are recommended as <strong>regular therapy</strong> for the prevention of his symptoms of <strong>exercise-induced asthma.</strong></p>\n<p>According to GINA 2021 guidelines, exercise-induced bronchoconstriction can be prevented with daily treatment (regular therapy) with inhaled corticosteroids. SABA, LABA, or chromones may be used prior to exercise, along with warm-up before exercise. </p>\n<p>Those who have mild exercise-induced asthma can use their regular inhaled corticosteroid before exercise and need not take SABA pre-exercise. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9542","text":"A 17-year-old boy presente​d to the clinic with an a‍cute respiratory tract in‍fection. He has a history​ of asthma associated wit‍h recurrent respiratory t‌ract infections and sinus‌itis. Genetic testing in ‍childhood had revealed CF​TR mutations. Which of th​e following is likely to ‍be decreased in this pati​ent?","raw_text":"A 17-year-old boy presented to the clinic with an acute respiratory tract infection. He has a history of asthma associated with recurrent respiratory tract infections and sinusitis. Genetic testing in childhood had revealed CFTR mutations. Which of the following is likely to be decreased in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sweat c​hloride","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum bicar​bonate level","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sperm​ count","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stoo​l fat","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sperm count","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sperm count</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>cystic fibrosis</strong>. In these patients, <strong>sperm count</strong> is most likely to be <strong>decreased.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Male</strong> patients with cystic fibrosis usually have <strong>infertility</strong> due to failure of development of Wolffian duct structures leading to congenital absence of bilateral vas deferens. There is defective sperm transport resulting in <strong>obstructive</strong> <strong>azoospermia</strong>.</p>\n<p>The sexual function is generally unimpaired.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Sweat chloride</strong> levels are elevated (<strong>≥60 mEq/L</strong>) due to abnormalities in the CFTR protein leading to <strong>defective chloride transport</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Serum bicarbonate</strong> levels are increased due to excessive loss of salt in the sweat resulting in <strong>hypochloremic alkalosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: Patients with cystic fibrosis have fat <strong>malabsorption</strong> secondary to<strong> exocrine pancreatic insufficiency,</strong> hence the <strong>increased</strong> stool <strong>fat.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9543","text":"Which of the following has​ similar inheritance patt‍ern as cystic fibrosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following has similar inheritance pattern as cystic fibrosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Accumulation of glycogen i​n the lysosome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Trinucleotide repeat expan​sion of CAG on chromosome‍ 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adult onset polycystic kid​ney disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"RB gene ​mutation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Accumulation of glycogen in the lysosome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Accumulation of glycogen in the lysosome</strong></p><p><strong>Cystic fibrosis (CF)</strong>, is an <strong>autosomal-recessive disorder</strong>. Accumulation of glycogen in the lysosome is seen in <strong>Pompe's disease </strong>which is another autosomal-recessive disorder.</p>\n<p>Option B: Trinucleotide repeat expansion of CAG on chromosome 4 is seen in Huntington's disease, which is autosomal dominant.</p>\n<p>Options C and D: Adult-onset polycystic kidney disease and retinoblastoma (<em>RB</em> gene mutation) are autosomal-dominant diseases.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9544","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about cystic fibr‍osis is not true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about cystic fibrosis is not true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is an autosomal-recessi​ve disorder.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abnormality in CFTR gene l​eads to defective calcium‍ transport.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mutation in cystic fibrosi​s transport regulator is ‍seen.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Males have infertility in ​adult life","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Abnormality in CFTR gene leads to defective calcium transport.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Abnormality in CFTR gene leads to defective calcium transport.</strong></p><p>In <strong>cystic fibrosis</strong>, abnormalities in the cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (<strong>CFTR</strong>) <strong>gene</strong> lead to <strong>defective chloride channels</strong> and not calcium channels. </p>\n<p>Cystic fibrosis (CF) is an <strong>autosomal-recessive</strong> disorder of children and adults. Dysfunction of the CFTR, the primary defect, leads to a wide and variable array of presenting manifestations and complications.</p>\n<p>The most common CFTR mutation is <strong>F508 deletion</strong>.</p>\n<p>More than 95% of males are <strong>azoospermic</strong> because of failure of development of Wolffian duct structures, but the sexual function is generally unimpaired.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9634","text":"The most common organism c​ausing pneumonia in cysti‍c fibrosis among the pedi‍atric age group is ______​__.","raw_text":"The most common organism causing pneumonia in cystic fibrosis among the pediatric age group is ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa, no​n-mucoid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Staphyloco​ccus aureus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Burkholder​ia cepacia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococcu​s pneumoniae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p><strong><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> organism causing pneumonia in cystic fibrosis among the given options.</p>\n<p>The most common organisms causing pneumonia in CF patients:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>pediatric</strong> age group-<em><strong> S. aureus </strong></em></li>\n<li>The <strong>adult</strong> population-<em><strong> P. aeruginosa </strong></em>(mucoid)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em></strong>-<strong>mucoid</strong> may also cause pneumonia in pediatric CF patients.</p>\n<p>In cystic fibrosis,<em> P. aeruginosa</em> shows a special feature of developing a <strong>mucoid capsule</strong>. This complex polysaccharide generates a biofilm that provides a hypoxic environment and thereby protects <em>Pseudomonas</em> against antimicrobial agents.</p>\n<p><em>Burkholderia cepacia </em>pneumonia is associated with <strong>higher mortality</strong>, but it is not the most common. These patients are found to have severe pulmonary hypertension, possibly due to pulmonary inflammatory vascular response.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9545","text":"A 5-year-old boy was evalu​ated for cystic fibrosis.‍ Sweat chloride shows val‍ue of 38 and 44 mEq/L (no​rmal&lt;40). Which is the ne‍xt best test to confirm t‌he diagnosis of cystic fi‌brosis?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old boy was evaluated for cystic fibrosis. Sweat chloride shows value of 38 and 44 mEq/L (normal&lt;40). Which is the next best test to confirm the diagnosis of cystic fibrosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"72-hour ​fecal fat","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CT c​hest","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DNA an​alysis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trans epithelial nasal pot​ential difference","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. DNA analysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. DNA analysis</strong></p><p><strong>DNA analysis</strong> is used to <strong>confirm</strong> the diagnosis of <strong>cystic fibrosis</strong> in patients with <strong>equivocal</strong> (one normal and one abnormal value) or frankly normal <strong>sweat chloride values</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>diagnosis</strong> is <strong>confirmed</strong> if <strong>both</strong> of the following <strong>criteria</strong> are met:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Presence of </li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Clinical symptoms affecting at least one organ system (respiratory, gastrointestinal, or genitourinary) <strong>or </strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Positive newborn screening<strong> or </strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A sibling with cystic fibrosis </li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Evidence of <strong>dysfunction</strong> of the <strong>CFTR gene</strong> by </li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Two positive sweat chloride test results obtained on separate days <strong>or</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Identification of two CFTR mutations by genetic testing <strong>or</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">An abnormal measurement of nasal potential difference</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>Options A and B: 72-hour fecal fat and chest CT are not used in the diagnosis of CF. </p>\n<p>Option D: This testing is primarily used in research applications and has never undergone extensive validation as a clinical tool. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9546","text":"A couple planning for preg​nancy visits you for gene‍tic counseling. They are ‍both known to be heterozy​gous carriers of CFTR mut‍ations. Which of the foll‌owing is the most likely ‌complication in an affect‍ed child at birth?","raw_text":"A couple planning for pregnancy visits you for genetic counseling. They are both known to be heterozygous carriers of CFTR mutations. Which of the following is the most likely complication in an affected child at birth?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyaline memb​rane disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transient tachypnea of new​born","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Meconiu​m ileus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sigmoid ​volvulus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Meconium ileus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3829a83ac0fe40d4bc0a545d8685360fx453x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Meconium ileus</strong></p><p><strong>CFTR mutations</strong> are associated with <strong>cystic fibrosis </strong>(CF). <strong>Meconium ileus</strong> is a common complication associated with CF patients at birth.</p>\n<p>Meconium found in the intestine of a neonate consists of succus entericus (bile salts, bile acids, and debris from the intestinal mucosa) and is normally evacuated within 6 hours of birth (or earlier).</p>\n<p>Meconium ileus occurs in the first 24-48 hours of life when meconium becomes inspissated and obstructs the distal ileum causing abdominal distension and emesis. Abdominal radiographs show dilated loops of bowel with air-fluid levels and, frequently, a collection of granular, “ground-glass” material in the lower central abdomen.</p>\n<p>Meconium ileus may also be seen with pancreatic atresia or pancreatic duct stenosis.</p>\n<p>Radiological image shows contrast enema in a newborn infant with abdominal distention and failure to pass meconium. Several air–fluid levels in the small bowel are visible on the upright lateral view.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9633","text":"A 9-year-old boy, diagnose​d with cystic fibrosis sh‍ortly after birth has bee‍n having around 2-3 exace​rbations per year, requir‍ing hospital admissions. ‌Infection by which of the‌ following organisms has ‍increased mortality in su​ch patients?","raw_text":"A 9-year-old boy, diagnosed with cystic fibrosis shortly after birth has been having around 2-3 exacerbations per year, requiring hospital admissions. Infection by which of the following organisms has increased mortality in such patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyloco​ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudomona​s aerogenes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Burkholder​ia cepacia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aero​monas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Burkholderia cepacia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Burkholderia cepacia</strong></p><p><strong>Cystic fibrosis</strong> patients with <strong><em>Burkholderia cepacia </em></strong>pneumonia are found to have severe pulmonary hypertension, possibly due to pulmonary inflammatory vascular response, and have <strong>higher mortality.</strong></p>\n<p><strong><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></strong> and <strong><em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em> (mucoid)</strong> are the <strong>most common</strong> pathogens causing pneumonia in CF patients. However, they have lower mortality compared to <em>B. cepacia</em>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0329","text":"A 3-year-old boy presents ​with a history of recurre‍nt pneumonia and chronic ‍diarrhea. His mother stat​es that he has 6-8 foul-s‍melling stools per day. P‌hysical examination revea‌ls a low-grade fever and ‍scattered rhonchi over bo​th lung fields. Sweat chl​oride testing is elevated‍ on 2 separate occasions.​ Which of the following d‌rugs is used as a treatme​nt option for this condit‍ion?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old boy presents with a history of recurrent pneumonia and chronic diarrhea. His mother states that he has 6-8 foul-smelling stools per day. Physical examination reveals a low-grade fever and scattered rhonchi over both lung fields. Sweat chloride testing is elevated on 2 separate occasions. Which of the following drugs is used as a treatment option for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ivac​aftor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ravul​izumab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tecov​irimat","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mepol​izumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ivacaftor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ivacaftor</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>cystic fibrosis</strong> (CF). <strong>Ivacaftor</strong> is used in the treatment of cystic fibrosis.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Ivacaftor is a potentiator of the chloride channel and overcomes its defect. It activates the CFTR-G551D mutant protein.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Remember: Iva<strong>C</strong>a<strong>FT</strong>o<strong>R</strong> (CFTR mutation in CF)</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9623","text":"A 4-year-old girl presente​d with cough and difficul‍ty breathing following ac‍cidental inhalation of a ​peanut. On examination, t‍here was localised wheezi‌ng and decreased breath s‌ounds on the right side. ‍What is the next best ste​p in management?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old girl presented with cough and difficulty breathing following accidental inhalation of a peanut. On examination, there was localised wheezing and decreased breath sounds on the right side. What is the next best step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chest​ X-ray","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rigid bro​nchoscopy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Flexible ​endoscopy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Direct La​ryngoscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rigid bronchoscopy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rigid bronchoscopy</strong></p><p>In a child with a<strong> suspected foreign body</strong> in the lung, the next best step would be to do a <strong>rigid bronchoscopy </strong>as it is<strong> both diagnostic and therapeutic</strong>.</p>\n<p>The rigid bronchoscope has a ventilation mechanism and a suction port making it easy for visualization and handling of the foreign body. However, it is pertinent to note that the initial investigation is usually a <strong>chest X-ray.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9635","text":"All the following are true​ about Macleod syndrome e‍xcept that _______.","raw_text":"All the following are true about Macleod syndrome except that _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is also called Swyer-Ja​mes syndrome.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is not a true emphysema​.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is mostly linked to ade​novirus.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Affected lung is larger in​ size compared to unaffec‍ted lung.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Affected lung is larger in size compared to unaffected lung.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Affected lung is larger in size compared to unaffected lung.</strong></p><p>Macleod syndrome refers to the inability of the lungs to grow after a severe obstructive bronchiolitis. Children with this condition often have chronic cough, recurrent pneumonia, and wheezing. On CT scan, the <strong>affected lung shows small size</strong> and decreased vascularity, hyperexpansion, and hyperlucency <strong>(false emphysema)</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is also called Swyer–James syndrome. It is mostly linked to adenovirus. Patients respond well to bronchodilators. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9547","text":"A 15-year-old child has be​en recurrently hospitaliz‍ed for infections, since ‍2 years of age. After a d​etailed evaluation, a dia‍gnosis of Kartagener's sy‌ndrome was suspected. All‌ of the following are com‍ponents of the Kartagener​ triad except _____.","raw_text":"A 15-year-old child has been recurrently hospitalized for infections, since 2 years of age. After a detailed evaluation, a diagnosis of Kartagener's syndrome was suspected. All of the following are components of the Kartagener triad except _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Situs inver​sus totalis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic ​sinusitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Male inf​ertility","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bronchi​ectasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Male infertility","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Male infertility</strong></p><p>Male infertility is typical in Kartagener syndrome, but not a component of the triad.</p>\n<p><strong>Kartagener’s syndrome</strong> is <strong>primary ciliary dyskinesia</strong>characterized by the classical triad of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Situs inversus</li>\n<li>Chronic sinusitis</li>\n<li>Bronchiectasis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Primary ciliary dyskinesia (also called immotile cilia syndrome) is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disorder affecting <strong>ciliary motility</strong>. It occurs due to ultrastructural defects in the<strong> dynein</strong> arm of cilia. This causes the retention of secretions due to a failed mucociliary clearance mechanism. Clinically, this manifests as <strong>chronic sinusitis </strong>and recurrent infections that ultimately lead to <strong>bronchiectasis.</strong></p>\n<p>As ciliary function is necessary to ensure proper rotation of the developing organs in the chest and abdomen. Its absence, will lead to <strong>situs inversus</strong> or a partial lateralizing abnormality.</p>\n<p><strong>Male</strong> patients with this condition tend to be<strong> infertile</strong>, due to <strong>asthenospermia</strong> (immotile spermatozoa due to impaired sperm flagella function). </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9640","text":"All the following are comp​onents of Young's syndrom‍e except:","raw_text":"All the following are components of Young's syndrome except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bronchi​ectasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ast​hma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sinu​sitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Obstructive​ azoospermia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Asthma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Asthma</strong></p><p>Asthma is not usually seen in Young's syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Young's syndrome</strong> is characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bronchiectasis</li>\n<li>Sinusitis</li>\n<li>Obstructive azoospermia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In contrast, <strong>Kartagener's syndrome</strong> is characterized by</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bronchiectasis</li>\n<li>Sinusitis </li>\n<li>Situs inversus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9630","text":"A term male neonate develo​ps respiratory distress a‍t birth, unresponsive to ‍surfactant. Echocardiogra​phy is normal and chest X‍-ray shows a ground-glass‌ appearance. Parents say ‌that their first child di‍ed at 10 days of birth du​e to similar complaints. ​What is the most likely d‍iagnosis?","raw_text":"A term male neonate develops respiratory distress at birth, unresponsive to surfactant. Echocardiography is normal and chest X-ray shows a ground-glass appearance. Parents say that their first child died at 10 days of birth due to similar complaints. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Totally anomalous pulmonar​y venous congestion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meconium ​aspiration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neonatal pulmonary alveola​r proteinosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Diffuse herpes simplex inf​ection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neonatal pulmonary alveolar proteinosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cad662e29af4481a87bcb9934850c01fx1060x939.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neonatal pulmonary alveolar proteinosis</strong></p><p>The history and chest X-ray findings are suggestive of <strong>pulmonary alveolar proteinosis (PAP).</strong></p>\n<p>PAP is a rare, diffuse lung disease that is characterized by alveolar and interstitial accumulation of a periodic acid-Schiff <strong>(PAS) stain-positive phospholipoprotein</strong> (derived from surfactant).</p>\n<p>Infants and children with PAP present with dyspnea, fatigue, cough, weight loss, chest pain, or hemoptysis. Boys are affected 3 times as often as girls.  </p>\n<p><strong>Chest X-ray</strong> shows <strong>ground-glass opacity</strong> and <strong>HRCT </strong>shows <strong>crazy-paving pattern </strong>(as shown below).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Treatment options include<strong> total lung lavage</strong>, inhaled or subcutaneous granulocyte-macrophage colony<wbr/>-stimulating factor (GM-CSF).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9632","text":"A 3-month old previously h​ealthy baby was brought t‍o the casualty when the p‍arents found that the bab​y was not breathing while‍ asleep. Following resusc‌itative measures, the bab‌y was still apnoeic, puls‍eless and asystolic. Whic​h of the following is not​ a risk factor for sudden‍ infant death syndrome?","raw_text":"A 3-month old previously healthy baby was brought to the casualty when the parents found that the baby was not breathing while asleep. Following resuscitative measures, the baby was still apnoeic, pulseless and asystolic. Which of the following is not a risk factor for sudden infant death syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infants of higher birth or​der","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infants born to women aged​ &amp;gt;35 years","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infants of mother who smok​ed during pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infants sleeping in prone ​position","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Infants born to women aged >35 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Infants born to women aged &gt;35 years</strong></p><p><strong>Higher maternal age</strong> is not a risk factor for sudden infant death syndrome (SIDS).</p>\n<p>Risk factors for SIDS include</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Low socioeconomic status</li>\n<li>3-4 months of age have the highest incidence</li>\n<li>Higher birth order irrespective of maternal age and low birth weight</li>\n<li>Cigarette smoking, alcohol, and drug abuse during pregnancy</li>\n<li>Sleeping prone, even side sleeping increases the risk</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9637","text":"A 6-month-old infant was a​dmitted because of increa‍sing stridor since birth,‍ cough, pulmonary infecti​ons, and episodes of acut‍e respiratory distress. O‌n detailed evaluation usi‌ng bronchoscopy and MRI i‍maging, a bronchial cyst ​was found to be the cause​. The following are featu‍res of the lesion except ​that __.","raw_text":"A 6-month-old infant was admitted because of increasing stridor since birth, cough, pulmonary infections, and episodes of acute respiratory distress. On detailed evaluation using bronchoscopy and MRI imaging, a bronchial cyst was found to be the cause. The following are features of the lesion except that __.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is mostly​ mediastinal.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is usually confined to ​1 lobe.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is rarely uniloculated.​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is never associated wit​h malignancy.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is never associated with malignancy.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is never associated with malignancy.</strong></p><p>Bronchial cysts are associated with malignancy <strong>(pleuropulmonary blastoma)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Bronchial cysts are congenital malformations of the bronchial tree. It mainly presents as a <strong>mediastinal</strong> <strong>mass</strong> that may enlarge and cause local compression.</p>\n<p>It is usually confined to a single lobe and is mostly multiloculated.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9639","text":"In a child with yellow nai​l syndrome, you would exp‍ect to find all the follo‍wing clinical features ex​cept _______.","raw_text":"In a child with yellow nail syndrome, you would expect to find all the following clinical features except _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pleural ​effusion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymph​edema","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Club​bing","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Discolor​ed nails","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Clubbing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Clubbing</strong></p><p>Yellow nail syndrome is <strong>not</strong> associated with <strong>clubbing</strong>.</p>\n<p>Yellow nail syndrome is characterized by <strong>respiratory manifestation</strong> (pleural effusion, bronchiectasis), <strong>lymphedema, </strong>and<strong> discoloured nails</strong>.  </p>\n<p>Yellow nail syndrome almost always occurs after 50 years of age, but a juvenile or familial form has also been observed.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9661","text":"What is the most likely ca​use of a mediastinal mass‍ in a 2-year-old child?","raw_text":"What is the most likely cause of a mediastinal mass in a 2-year-old child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurogen​ic tumors","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thy​moma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Germ cel​l tumors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymp​homa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Neurogenic tumors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Neurogenic tumors</strong></p><p>The most common mediastinal masses in young children are neurogenic tumours (50%-60%). In most cases (&lt; 4 years of age), they are invariably malignant (neuroblastomas).</p>\n<p><strong>Anterior mediastinal masses:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Thymoma</strong> - most common</li>\n<li>Malignant lymphoma (Hodgkin's and non-Hodgkin's)</li>\n<li>Teratoma </li>\n<li>Thyroid (retro- or sub-sternal extension)</li>\n<li>Thymic cyst</li>\n<li>Angioma</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Middle mediastinal masses: </strong>    </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Foregut duplication cyst</strong> (bronchogenic, enteric, and neurenteric) - most common</li>\n<li>Lymphadenopathy</li>\n<li>Vascular malformations</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Posterior mediastinal masses:</strong>  </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Neurogenic tumour</strong> - most common overall  </li>\n<li>Foregut duplication cyst      </li>\n<li>Vascular malformations </li>\n<li>Extramedullary hematopoiesis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"a9r_b-UB","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"23_platelet_disorders","title":"23.Platelet Disorders","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC0511","text":"Which of the following is ​the most potent vasoconst‌rictor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most potent vasoconstrictor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endot​helin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urot​ensin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Angio​tensin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vasop​ressin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Urotensin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Urotensin</strong></p><p>The most potent vasoconstrictor is <strong>urotensin2</strong> followed by endothelin and angiotensin.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0476","text":"Which of the following is ​a component of δ granules‌ of platelets?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a component of δ granules of platelets?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Platelet-derived growth fa​ctor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epine​phrine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fibri​nogen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Platelet​ factor 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Epinephrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Epinephrine</strong></p><p><strong>Epinephrine</strong> is a component of <strong>δ granules</strong> of platelets.</p>\n<p>The different components of platelet granules are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li> α granules \n<ul>\n<li>Fibrinogen</li>\n<li>Fibronectin</li>\n<li>PF4</li>\n<li>Factor V, VIII </li>\n<li>PDGF</li>\n<li>TGF</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li> δ granules \n<ul>\n<li>ADP</li>\n<li>Calcium</li>\n<li>Serotonin</li>\n<li>Histamine</li>\n<li>Epinephrine </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6714","text":"Rees and Ecker diluting fl​uid is used in ______","raw_text":"Rees and Ecker diluting fluid is used in ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wintro​be tube","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"WBC P​ipette","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"RBC P​ipette","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hemoglobi​n pipette","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. RBC Pipette","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. RBC Pipette</strong></p><p><strong>Rees and Ecker's solution</strong> is used as a diluting fluid in the <strong>RBC pipette</strong> for performing <strong>platelet counts</strong>. Its composition is:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 119px;\" width=\"240\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171.302px;\"><strong>Components</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 54.7396px;\"><strong>Value</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171.302px;\">Sodium citrate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 54.7396px;\">3.8 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171.302px;\">Formaldehyde (40 %)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 54.7396px;\">0.2 ml</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171.302px;\">Brilliant cresyl blue</td>\n<td style=\"width: 54.7396px;\">0.1 g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171.302px;\">Distilled water</td>\n<td style=\"width: 54.7396px;\">100 ml</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>steps</strong> involved in <strong>platelet</strong> <strong>count</strong> are:</p>\n<p>I - Filling the pipette</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diluting fluid is drawn to mark 1 of the RBC pipette and expelled</li>\n<li>Blood is aspirated to 0.5 mark of the RBCs diluting pipette</li>\n<li>Diluting fluid is aspirated to 101 mark</li>\n<li>Blood and diluting fluid are mixed mechanically for 5 min</li>\n</ul>\n<p>II - Filling the counting chamber</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Allow the counting chamber to stand for 20-30 minutes to allow the platelets to settle.</li>\n<li>To prevent drying out of the preparation, it should be kept in a moist atmosphere, this is most easily arranged by placing the filled counting chamber in the bottom of a clean Petri dish, alongside which arrange a small piece of cotton wool which has been soaked in water and squeezed out. Petri dish lid is additionally put over the dish.</li>\n<li>After 20-30minutes, the counting chamber can be removed and carefully transferred to the microscope stage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>III - Counting the number of platelets</p>\n<ul style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\n<li>Focus on the surface of the hemocytometer chamber with a high power dry objective. Dim the light to make the thrombocytes visible, and count all of the thrombocytes in the entire center 1 mm² (erythrocyte counting area) of both sides of the hemocytometer. This gives the number of thrombocytes in 0.2 l of 1: 200 dilution of blood.</li>\n<li>The number of thrombocytes counted X 1000 = number of thrombocytes/l of undiluted blood. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5827","text":"A 12-year-old girl with de​ngue hemorrhagic fever is‌ being monitored by measu‍ring platelet count. Spon‌taneous bleeding is assoc​iated with platelet value‍s below _______ /µL.","raw_text":"A 12-year-old girl with dengue hemorrhagic fever is being monitored by measuring platelet count. Spontaneous bleeding is associated with platelet values below _______ /µL.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30,​000","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"50,​000","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"20,​000","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"10,​000","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 20,000","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 20,000</strong></p><p><strong>Spontaneous bleeding</strong> (non-traumatic) is associated with platelet values below <strong>20,000 platelets/µL.</strong></p>\n<p>It most often involves small vessels. The common sites for such hemorrhages are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Skin</li>\n<li>Mucosa of gastrointestinal and genitourinary tracts</li>\n<li>Intracranial bleeding - most feared site of bleeding</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>production</strong> of the platelets is regulated by the hormone <strong>thrombopoietin</strong> produced in the liver. The platelet synthesis is also specifically <strong>increased</strong> by <strong>interleukin 6</strong>. The average <strong>lifespan</strong> of the platelets is<strong> 7–10 days</strong>. The <strong>normal</strong> platelet count is <strong>1,50,000 - 4,00,000</strong> /µL.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5842","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true regarding‌ functional platelet diso‍rders?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding functional platelet disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal platelet count and ​normal bleeding time","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Normal platelet count and ​increased bleeding time","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased platelet count a​nd increased bleeding tim‌e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased platelet count a​nd decreased bleeding tim‌e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Normal platelet count and increased bleeding time","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Normal platelet count and increased bleeding time</strong></p><p>Functional platelet disorders have <strong>normal platelet count</strong> and <strong>increased bleeding time</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Classification</strong> of platelet functional disorders:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Defect in platelet-vessel wall interaction (<strong>disorders of adhesion</strong>)\n<ul>\n<li>Von Willebrand disease - deficiency or defect in plasma vWF</li>\n<li>Bernard–Soulier syndrome - deficiency or defect in GP Ib IX</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Defects in platelet-platelet interaction (<strong>disorders of aggregation</strong>)\n<ul>\n<li>Congenital afibrinogenemia - deficiency of plasma fibrinogen</li>\n<li>Glanzmann thrombasthenia - deficiency or defect in GPIIb-IIIa</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Disorders of platelet secretion</strong> and signal transduction\n<ul>\n<li>Storage pool deficiency - α-Granule deficiency and gamma-Granule deficiency</li>\n<li>Quebec platelet disorder</li>\n<li>Chediak–Higashi syndrome</li>\n<li>Gray platelet syndrome</li>\n<li>Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Disorders of platelet coagulation-protein interaction</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Scott syndrome - defect in factor Va-Xa interaction on platelets</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5853","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about vo‌nWillebrand factor?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about vonWillebrand factor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is synthesized by hepat​ocytes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Its deficiency can also ca​use factor VIII defect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Its deficiency may cause d​efective platelet adhesio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It serves as a carrier for​ factor VIII","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is synthesized by hepatocytes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b48397155f047a8a2cf821bee2aa392x1280x2261.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is synthesized by hepatocytes</strong></p><p>Von Willebrand factor (<strong>vWF</strong>) is not synthesized by hepatocytes. They are <strong>produced</strong> by <strong>endothelial</strong> <strong>cells</strong> of <strong>liver</strong> and <strong>megakaryocytes</strong>. It is also found in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Platelet granules - lesser compared to endothelial cells</li>\n<li>Endothelial cells within cytoplasmic vesicles called Weibel-Palade bodies</li>\n<li>Subendothelium - binds to collagen</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It mediates the <strong>adhesion</strong> of <strong>platelets</strong> to sites of vascular injury (subendothelium). It links the exposed collagen to platelets. It facilitates platelet to platelet interaction and also stabilizes the hemostatic plug against shear forces. </p>\n<p>It is a carrier protein for factor VIII and also protects it from proteolytic degradation and stabilizes it. The <strong>half-life</strong> of <strong>factor</strong> <strong>VIII</strong> is about 2.4 hours when free and <strong>12 hours</strong> when <strong>bound</strong> to <strong>vWF</strong> in the circulation.</p>\n<p>Since vWF <strong>stabilizes</strong> <strong>factor VIII</strong>, a deficiency of vWF gives rise to a secondary decrease in factor VIII levels. This may be reflected by a prolongation of the partial thromboplastin time (PTT) in von Willebrand disease types 1 and 3.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5859","text":"A 24-year-old woman is fou​nd to have uncontrolled b‌leeding after a minor cut‍ injury. Her lab investig‌ations are given below. W​hich of the following sta‍tements are false about h‌er condition? Platelet co‌unt - 4,60,000 /μL Bleedi‌ng time - increased PT - ‌normal APTT - increased","raw_text":"<p>A 24-year-old woman is found to have uncontrolled bleeding after a minor cut injury. Her lab investigations are given below. Which of the following statements are false about her condition?</p>\n<p>Platelet count - 4,60,000 /μL</p>\n<p>Bleeding time - increased</p>\n<p>PT - normal</p>\n<p>APTT - increased</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 1 is ​most common","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type 2 is a qualitative de​fect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type 3 is inherited as aut​osomal dominant","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Type 3 is th​e severe form","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type 3 is inherited as autosomal dominant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type 3 is inherited as autosomal dominant</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>von Willebrand disease</strong> (vWD). <strong>Type 3</strong> vWD is inherited as an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disease. </p>\n<p>The characteristics of different subtypes of vWD are:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Subtype</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Description</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Inheritance</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Lab findings</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Type 1</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Quantitative deficiency of vWF</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Autosomal dominant</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Reduced vWF antigen, activity and Factor VIII</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Type 2</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Qualitative defect in vWF</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Autosomal dominant</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Reduced vWF activity to antigen ration&lt;0.6</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Type 3</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Near-complete (quantitative) deficiency of Factor VIII</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Autosomal recessive</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Marked reduction or absence in Factor VIII levels</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Type</strong> <strong>1</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> subtype of vWD. In Type 2, <strong>vWF</strong> is expressed in <strong>normal</strong> <strong>amounts</strong>, but missense mutations are present that lead to <strong>defective</strong> <strong>multimer</strong> <strong>assembly</strong>. This type has several subtypes, of which type <strong>2A</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Laboratory findings</strong> in vWD:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prolonged bleeding time</li>\n<li>Normal platelet count</li>\n<li>Hess test - positive tourniquet test due to defective platelet adhesion </li>\n<li>Ristocetin-induced aggregation - defective and diagnostic of this disease</li>\n<li>Platelet aggregation with ADP, collagen, and thrombin - normal</li>\n<li>Increased aPTT</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Though the synthesis of factor VIII remains normal, the half-life of VIII in plasma decreases due to reduced vWF (carrier) levels. This leads to <strong>secondary VIIIC deficiency</strong> in plasma. So, the intrinsic pathway of coagulation is affected. Hence, <strong>aPTT</strong> is <strong>increased</strong> in these patients.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0477","text":"Which of the following occ​urs due to a defect in δ ‌granules of the platelets‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following occurs due to a defect in δ granules of the platelets?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Quebec plate​let syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Grey platel​et syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wiskott–Aldr​ich syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hermansky–Pu​dlak syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hermansky–Pudlak syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hermansky–Pudlak syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Hermansky–Pudlak syndrome</strong> occurs due to a <strong>defect</strong> in platelet <strong>δ granules</strong>.</p>\n<p>Platelet granules disorders include: </p>\n<ul style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"1\" data-font=\"Symbol\" data-leveltext=\"\" data-listid=\"14\"><strong>Gray platelet syndrome</strong> - involves <strong>α</strong> granules and characterized by thrombocytopenia with large platelets that appear pale on the peripheral smear (due to deficiency of the α granules)</li>\n<li><strong>Quebec platelet syndrome</strong> - defect in<strong> α </strong>granules </li>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"2\" data-font=\"Symbol\" data-leveltext=\"\" data-listid=\"14\"><strong>Hermansky–Pudlak syndrome</strong> - defect in <strong>δ </strong>granules and characterized by hypopigmentation and bleeding manifestation</li>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"3\" data-font=\"Symbol\" data-leveltext=\"\" data-listid=\"14\"><strong>Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome</strong> - mutations in the <strong><em>WASp </em>gene </strong>expressed in the X chromosome and characterized by microthrombocytopenia, eczema, recurrent infections</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2442","text":"A patient with large plate​lets on peripheral smear ‌is diagnosed to have Bern‍ard-Soulier disease. Defi‌ciency of which of the fo​llowing causes this condi‍tion?","raw_text":"A patient with large platelets on peripheral smear is diagnosed to have Bernard-Soulier disease. Deficiency of which of the following causes this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gp IIb​/ IIIa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gp I​b/ IX","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"von Willeb​rand factor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Facto​r VIII","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gp Ib/ IX","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0c1e01cfa3e2477998ffbbf290baa16bx1280x2261.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gp Ib/ IX</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Bernard–Soulier</strong> disease is due to <strong>deficiency</strong> of <strong>Gp Ib/ IX.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Bernard–Soulier syndrome is characterized by<strong> large platelets</strong>. Ristocetin aggregation is abnormal.</p>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"t1\" style=\"width: 518px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 170px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Deficiency</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 170.105px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Defect</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 170.895px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Disease</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 170px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Gp Ib/IX</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 170.105px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Adhesion of platelets</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 170.895px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Bernard–Soulier syndrome</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 170px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Gp IIb/IIIa</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 170.105px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Aggregation of platelets</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 170.895px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Glanzmann thrombasthenia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 170px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">von Willebrand factor</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 170.105px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Adhesion&gt;Aggregation</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 170.895px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Von Willebrand disease</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p class=\"p2\">Factor VIII deficiency causes hemophilia. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5844","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about Be‌rnard-Soulier syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about Bernard-Soulier syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"There is defective adhesio​n of platelets to sub-end‌othelial matrix","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deficiency of the platelet​ membrane glycoprotein co‌mplex Ib-IX is seen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Platelet aggregation by ri​stocetin is normal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It has an autosomal recess​ive inheritance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Platelet aggregation by ristocetin is normal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d6c79ad5771a4dbe877613fdacf564d3x519x391.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/99d5b89d5c39449dbe3d65dedfd2492ex1280x2261.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Platelet aggregation by ristocetin is normal</strong></p><p>In <strong>Bernard–Soulier</strong> syndrome, platelet aggregation by <strong>ristocetin</strong> is abnormal.</p>\n<p>The <strong>receptor</strong> for <strong>vWF </strong>i.e.<strong> Ib-IX</strong> is essential for normal <strong>platelet adhesion</strong> to the subendothelial extracellular matrix. In this condition, there is <strong>deficiency</strong> of <strong>Gp Ib/ IX. </strong>Clinical manifestations include epistaxis, ecchymoses, menorrhagia, gingival bleeding, and gastrointestinal bleeding.</p>\n<p><strong>Laboratory findings</strong> seen in Bernard-Soulier syndrome:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prolonged bleeding time</li>\n<li>Moderate thrombocytopenia</li>\n<li>Platelet aggregation by ristocetin is deficient (but ADP, arachidonic acid, epinephrine, collagen - normal).</li>\n<li>Clot retraction - normal</li>\n<li>Abnormally large platelets</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the abnormally large platelets on peripheral smear seen in Bernard-Soulier syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5846","text":"A 4-year-old girl with a h​istory of easy bruising w‌as referred for further e‍valuation. Her previous l‌ab reports showed that pl​atelet count and morpholo‍gy were normal. Platelet ‌aggregations tests are gi‌ven below. What is the li‌kely diagnosis? Agonist u‌sed Aggregation ADP Absen‍t Epinephrine Absent Coll‍agen Absent Ristocetin No‌rmal","raw_text":"<p>A 4-year-old girl with a history of easy bruising was referred for further evaluation. Her previous lab reports showed that platelet count and morphology were normal. Platelet aggregations tests are given below. What is the likely diagnosis?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 130px;\" width=\"407\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px;\"><strong>Agonist used</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px;\"><strong>Aggregation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px;\">ADP</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px;\">Epinephrine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px;\">Collagen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px;\">Ristocetin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bernard-Soul​ier syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Grey platel​et syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"von Willebr​and Disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glanzmann th​rombasthenia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glanzmann thrombasthenia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glanzmann thrombasthenia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Glanzmann thrombasthenia</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is an abnormality of platelet <strong>GPIIb-IIIa</strong> (<strong>fibrinogen receptor</strong>). Platelets are unable to adhere to each other and to the underlying tissue of the damaged blood vessels. It is inherited in an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> manner. The symptoms include mucocutaneous bleeding in the neonatal period, menorrhagia, ecchymoses, epistaxis, and gingival hemorrhage.</p>\n<p><strong>Types </strong>of Glanzmann thrombasthenia<strong> </strong>include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type 1: Absence of GPIIb-IIIa</li>\n<li>Type 2: Reduced surface expression of GPIIb-IIIa/ Essentialis athrombia - clot retraction is normal</li>\n<li>Type 3: Dysfunction of GPIIb-IIIa</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Laboratory features </strong>of Glanzmann thrombasthenia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal platelet count and morphology</li>\n<li>Platelet aggregation occurs in response to ristocetin, but not to other agonists (such as ADP, thrombin, collagen or epinephrine)</li>\n<li>Clot retraction is absent</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5840","text":"A 1-year-old boy is brough​t with a history of recur‌rent impetigo. Physical e‍xamination findings are g‌iven below. Platelet coun​ts are low and peripheral‍ smear shows abnormally s‌mall-sized platelets. Wha‌t is the most likely diag‌nosis?","raw_text":"A 1-year-old boy is brought with a history of recurrent impetigo. Physical examination findings are given below. Platelet counts are low and peripheral smear shows abnormally small-sized platelets. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Idiopathic thrombocytopeni​a purpura","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bernard-Soul​ier syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Disseminated intravascular​ coagulation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wiskott-Aldr​ich syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9ac63126e8a842d9a7192bf63b487307.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome </strong>(WAS). The given image shows (A) multiple petechiae over face and hematoma under the right eye and (B) eczema of the foot. </p>\n<p>This syndrome is an <strong>X-linked recessive,</strong> primary <strong>immunodeficiency</strong> disorder involving the WAS gene. It is characterised by the <strong>triad </strong>of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Severe eczema</li>\n<li>Thrombocytopenia</li>\n<li>Recurrent infections</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Small platelets</strong> are seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome</li>\n<li>X-linked thrombocytopenia</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Large platelets </strong>are seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bernard-Soulier syndrome</li>\n<li>Myeloproliferative syndrome</li>\n<li>Myelodysplastic syndrome</li>\n<li>May–Hegglin anomaly</li>\n<li>Gray platelet syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5829","text":"A 28-year-old woman presen​ts with epistaxis, easy b‌ruising and menorrhagia. ‍There is no history of dr‌ug intake. Physical exami​nation shows scattered pe‍techiae and a normal-size‌d spleen. Her lab investi‌gations are given below. ‌Antibodies against which ‌of the following can lead‍ to this condition? Plate‍let count - 37,000/μL Ble‌eding time - 16 minutes P​T - 13 seconds aPTT - 30 ​seconds Peripheral blood ‌smear - megathrombocytes","raw_text":"<p>A 28-year-old woman presents with epistaxis, easy bruising and menorrhagia. There is no history of drug intake. Physical examination shows scattered petechiae and a normal-sized spleen. Her lab investigations are given below. Antibodies against which of the following can lead to this condition?</p>\n<p>Platelet count - 37,000/μL</p>\n<p>Bleeding time - 16 minutes</p>\n<p>PT - 13 seconds</p>\n<p>aPTT - 30 seconds</p>\n<p>Peripheral blood smear - megathrombocytes</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"v​WF","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glycoprote​in IIb/IIIa","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"ADAM​TS13","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thrombo​xane A2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> chronic immune thrombocytopenic purpura</strong> (ITP). <strong>Autoantibodies</strong> seen in ITP are most often directed against platelet membrane <strong>glycoproteins</strong> <strong>IIb-IIIa</strong> or Ib-IX. It is<strong> </strong>a diagnosis of exclusion<strong> </strong>due to the following findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bleeding tendency - petechiae</li>\n<li>Low platelet count, elevated bleeding time</li>\n<li>Presence of megathrombocytes in the peripheral smear - a sign of accelerated thrombopoiesis</li>\n<li>Female preponderance</li>\n<li>Absence of splenomegaly</li>\n</ul>\n<p> Note: Prothrombin time (PT) is normal in ITP.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5831","text":"Which class of antiplatele​t antibodies are seen in ‌patients with chronic imm‍une thrombocytopenic purp‌ura?","raw_text":"Which class of antiplatelet antibodies are seen in patients with chronic immune thrombocytopenic purpura?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ig​ G","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ig​ A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ig​ M","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ig​ E","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ig G","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ig G</strong></p><p><strong>Antiplatelet antibodies</strong> in <strong>chronic</strong> immune thrombocytopenic purpura (<strong>ITP</strong>) are mainly <strong>IgG</strong> class.</p>\n<p>Autoantibodies most often directed against platelet membrane glycoproteins are IIb-IIIa or Ib-IX. It can be demonstrated in the plasma and bound to the platelet surface in about 80% of patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5832","text":"A 27-year-old woman is dia​gnosed to have idiopathic‌ ITP. Which of the follow‍ing features is least lik‌ely to be seen in her?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old woman is diagnosed to have idiopathic ITP. Which of the following features is least likely to be seen in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Megathro​mbocytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Petechiae an​d ecchymosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphad​enopathy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Normal P​T and PTT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lymphadenopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/23c3c4d917114552a31d6213d52fa422x800x559.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lymphadenopathy</strong></p><p><strong>Lymphadenopathy</strong> and splenomegaly are uncommon in idiopathic (primary) ITP. They are seen in <strong>secondary</strong> <strong>ITP</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Chronic ITP</strong> occurs most commonly in adult women younger than 40 years of age. The female-to-male ratio is 3: 1. It<strong> </strong>is a <strong>type II hypersensitivity</strong> reaction. It is of 2 types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary or idiopathic - without any risk factors</li>\n<li>Secondary - presence of predisposing conditions like SLE, HIV, CLL</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Signs of accelerated thrombopoiesis that are seen in this condition are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Megakaryocytes in bone marrow</li>\n<li>Megathrombocytes (abnormally large platelets) in peripheral blood</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows 2 megakaryocytes seen in bone marrow aspirate (Wrights stain).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Acute ITP</strong> is mainly a disease of childhood. It occurs with equal frequency in both sexes, mostly after a <strong>viral illness.</strong> It is self-limited condition, resolves within 6 months.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0496","text":"Patients with which of the​ following predisposing c‌onditions are likely to d‍evelop chronic immune thr‌ombocytopenic purpura? Sy​stemic lupus erythematosu‍s HIV infection Chronic l‌ymphocytic leukemia Rheum‌atoid arthritis Antiphosp‌holipid antibody syndrome‌","raw_text":"<p>Patients with which of the following predisposing conditions are likely to develop chronic immune thrombocytopenic purpura?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li>HIV infection</li>\n<li>Chronic lymphocytic leukemia </li>\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li>Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ​2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3,​ 4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ​4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2,​ 3, 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 3, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 3, 5</strong></p><p>Systemic lupus erythematosus, HIV infection, chronic lymphocytic leukemia, antiphospholipid antibody syndrome can predispose to the development of secondary immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP).</p>\n<p>The <strong>predisposing conditions</strong> of <strong>secondary</strong> chronic <strong>ITP</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Autoimmune conditions\n<ul>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)</li>\n<li>Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome (APS)</li>\n<li>Immune thyroid disease</li>\n<li>Evans syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Lymphoproliferative diseases\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic lymphocytic leukemia</li>\n<li>Large granular T-lymphocyte lymphocytic leukemia</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Chronic infection\n<ul>\n<li>Helicobacter pylori</li>\n<li>Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV)</li>\n<li>Hepatitis C virus (HCV)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5834","text":"A 63-year-old man with mod​erate COVID pneumonia dev‌eloped DVT. Therapeutic a‍nticoagulation with hepar‌in was started. His lab i​nvestigations are given b‍elow. Which of the follow‌ing is false about this c‌ondition? Timing Platelet‌ count Before heparin the‌rapy 3,50,000 /μL Day 3 o‍f therapy 3,20,000 /μL Da‍y 7 of therapy 53,000 /μL‌","raw_text":"<p>A 63-year-old man with moderate COVID pneumonia developed DVT. Therapeutic anticoagulation with heparin was started. His lab investigations are given below. Which of the following is false about this condition?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 104px;\" width=\"365\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174.5px;\"><strong>Timing</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.5px;\"><strong>Platelet count</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174.5px;\">Before heparin therapy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.5px;\">3,50,000 /μL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174.5px;\">Day 3 of therapy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.5px;\">3,20,000 /μL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174.5px;\">Day 7 of therapy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.5px;\">53,000 /μL </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antibodies recognise hepar​in-platelet factor 4 comp‌lex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lesser incidence with use ​of LMWH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary thromboembolism ​is a complication","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a self-resolving con​dition","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is a self-resolving condition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is a self-resolving condition</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>type II</strong> heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (<strong>HIT</strong>). It is <strong>not self-resolving.</strong></p>\n<p>Thrombocytopenia occurs in about 5% of persons receiving heparin and is of two types:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 145px;\" width=\"550\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\"><strong>Feature</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\"><strong>Type I</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\"><strong>Type II</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Incidence</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">More common</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Less common</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Occurrence </td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Rapidly after onset of therapy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">5 to 14 days after therapy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Pathogenesis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Direct platelet-aggregating effect of heparin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Antibodies against heparin-platelet factor 4 complexes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Severity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Less severe</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Often leads to life-threatening venous and arterial thrombosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Treatment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Self-resolving</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">\n<p>Immediate discontinuation of heparin</p>\n<p>Alternate non-heparin anticoagulant to be used</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0498","text":"All of the following commo​nly cause drug-induced th‌rombocytopenia except ___‍__","raw_text":"All of the following commonly cause drug-induced thrombocytopenia except _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vanco​mycin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Qui​nine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rifa​mpin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Terlip​ressin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Terlipressin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Terlipressin</strong></p><p>Terlipressin does not cause drug-induced thrombocytopenia.</p>\n<p>Drug-induced thrombocytopenia can be caused by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vancomycin</li>\n<li>Quinine </li>\n<li>Rifampin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They cause thrombocytopenia through <strong>direct effects</strong> on platelets or secondary to <strong>immunologically</strong> <strong>mediated</strong> platelet destructions. Quinine, quinidine, and vancomycin bind to platelet glycoprotein and create antigenic determinants that are recognized by antibodies.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0497","text":"A post-operative patient w​as started on prophylacti‌c unfractionated heparin ‍for DVT. Which of the fol‌lowing is false regarding​ this drug?","raw_text":"A post-operative patient was started on prophylactic unfractionated heparin for DVT. Which of the following is false regarding this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is an indirect thrombin​-factor Xa inhibitor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is safe in patients wit​h renal impairment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It requires monitoring wit​h aPTT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"On development of HIT, LMW​H can be started alternat‌ely","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. On development of HIT, LMWH can be started alternately","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. On development of HIT, LMWH can be started alternately</strong></p><p>The risk of severe heparin-induced thrombocytopenia is lowered with LMWH but not eliminated. Hence, once severe <strong>HIT</strong> <strong>develops</strong> even <strong>LMWH</strong> exacerbates the thrombotic tendency and must be <strong>avoided</strong>.</p>\n<p>Management of HIT includes the immediate withdrawal of heparin therapy and instituting an alternative non-heparin anticoagulant.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5835","text":"Patients with which of the​ following conditions is ‌associated with ADAMTS13 ‍deficiency?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions is associated with ADAMTS13 deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immune thrombocytopenic pu​rpura","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemolytic ur​emic syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thrombotic thrombocytopeni​c purpura","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Von Willebr​and disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura</strong></p><p><strong>ADAMTS13 deficiency</strong> is associated with thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (<strong>TTP</strong>).</p>\n<p>ADAMTS13 is also called <strong>vWF metalloprotease</strong> which <strong>degrades</strong> very high-molecular-weight <strong>multimers</strong> of von Willebrand factor (<strong>vWF</strong>). Its deficiency causes accumulation of these multimers in the plasma and promotes platelet activation and aggregation.</p>\n<p>TTP is a part of a spectrum of clinical syndromes called <strong>thrombotic microangiopathy.</strong> They are caused by insults that lead to excessive activation of platelets, which deposit as thrombi in small blood vessels.</p>\n<p><strong>Features</strong> of TTP include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Thrombocytopenia</li>\n<li>Microangiopathic hemolytic anaemia</li>\n<li>Transient neurologic deficits</li>\n<li>Renal failure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5837","text":"A 7-year-old girl is broug​ht with complaints of hem‌aturia and melena for 10 ‍days. Laboratory investig‌ations reveal mild thromb​ocytopenia and normal coa‍gulation tests. Periphera‌l smear is given below. W‌hich organism is most fre‌quently involved in this ‌condition?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old girl is brought with complaints of hematuria and melena for 10 days. Laboratory investigations reveal mild thrombocytopenia and normal coagulation tests. Peripheral smear is given below. Which organism is most frequently involved in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E.coli ​O157:H7","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Shig​ella","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enteroinva​sive E.coli","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Salmo​nella","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. E.coli O157:H7","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9d74b8d51ae54c0b8d11fd94e1a8222d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ed110bae6299454e81bda8f6e444230fx799x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. E.coli O157:H7</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and peripheral smear showing <strong>helmet cells</strong> or <strong>schistocytes</strong> are suggestive of <strong>h</strong><strong>emolytic uremic syndrome </strong>(HUS)<strong>. </strong>This condition is most commonly associated with enterohemorrhagic <strong>Escherichia coli</strong> strain <strong>O157: H7 </strong>worldwide.</p>\n<p>This strain releases<strong> Shiga-like toxin</strong>. This toxin is absorbed from the inflamed gastrointestinal mucosa into the circulation and causes <strong>endothelial</strong> <strong>dysfunction</strong>. It leads to <strong>platelet </strong>activation and <strong>aggregation</strong>. Children and older adults are at the highest risk.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows schistocytes or helmet cells seen in HUS.  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5838","text":"Deficiency of which of the​ following complement com‌ponents is not associated‍ with atypical hemolytic ‌uremic syndrome?","raw_text":"Deficiency of which of the following complement components is not associated with atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fact​or H","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CD​46","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fact​or I","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD​59","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CD59","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CD59</strong></p><p><strong>Atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome</strong> is not associated with deficiency of CD59.</p>\n<p>It<strong> </strong>is <strong>associated</strong> with <strong>deficiency</strong> of the following components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Complement factor H</li>\n<li>Membrane cofactor protein (CD46)</li>\n<li>Complement factor I</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These three proteins <strong>prevent</strong> <strong>excessive</strong> <strong>activation</strong> of the <strong>alternative</strong> <strong>complement</strong> pathway.</p>\n<p>Deficiencies of these proteins can be caused by inherited defects or acquired inhibitory autoantibodies. The disease may be associated with a remitting, relapsing course.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0510","text":"Coombs test is found to be​ positive in a patient wi‌th thrombocytopenia. Whic‍h of the following condit‌ions could be a likely ca​use?","raw_text":"Coombs test is found to be positive in a patient with thrombocytopenia. Which of the following conditions could be a likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thrombotic thrombocytopeni​c purpura","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemolytic ur​emic syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Immune thrombocytopenic pu​rpura","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Disseminated intravascular​ coagulation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Immune thrombocytopenic purpura","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fed5d26856c9438791b8f719e7cd9b1dx1280x1927.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Immune thrombocytopenic purpura</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW155232901\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW155232901\"><strong>Coombs test</strong> is <strong>positive</strong> in immune thrombocytopenic purpura (<strong>ITP</strong>) as it is an <strong>immunogenic</strong> cause of thrombocytopenia. </span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW155232901\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW155232901\">Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP), hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) and disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) are </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW155232901\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW155232901\">nonimmunogenic</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW155232901\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW155232901\"> causes. Hence, Coombs test is negative in these conditions.</span></span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5825","text":"Which of the following is ​affected in patients with‌ Weber-Osler-Rendu syndro‍me?","raw_text":"Which of the following is affected in patients with Weber-Osler-Rendu syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coll​agen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Plat​elets","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clotting​ factors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blood ​vessels","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Blood vessels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Blood vessels</strong></p><p><strong>Weber–Osler–Rendu syndrome</strong> is a disorder of the <strong>blood vessels</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>autosomal-dominant</strong> disorder caused by mutations that modulate TGF-β signalling. It is also known as <strong>hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia</strong>. </p>\n<p>This condition is characterized by dilated, <strong>tortuous blood vessels</strong> with thin walls that <strong>bleed</strong> readily. Bleeding is most common in the mucous membranes of the nose (epistaxis), tongue, mouth, and eyes, and throughout the gastrointestinal tract.</p>\n<p>Platelet count, prothrombin time (PT), and activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) are normal.</p>\n<p>Clinical conditions in which <strong>abnormalities</strong> of <strong>vessel wall</strong> cause <strong>bleeding </strong>include<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infections - meningococcemia, infective endocarditis, rickettsioses</li>\n<li>Collagen defects - Ehlers–Danlos syndrome, scurvy, and Cushing's syndrome</li>\n<li>Perivascular amyloidosis - most common with amyloid light-chain (AL) amyloidosis</li>\n<li>Henoch–Schönlein purpura</li>\n<li>Drug reactions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"6UlQGK31","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"23_drugs_for_parkinson_s_disease","title":"23.Drugs For Parkinson’S Disease","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MA2851","text":"Which of the following dru​gs crosses the blood-brai‌n barrier?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs crosses the blood-brain barrier?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dopa​mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Levo​dopa","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Both A​ and B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carb​idopa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Levodopa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Levodopa</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Levodopa crosses</strong> the <strong>blood-brain barrier.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is a naturally occurring aromatic amino acid and an<strong> immediate precursor</strong> of<strong> dopamine </strong>that can cross the blood-brain barrier.<strong> </strong>Dopamine does not cross the blood-brain barrier because of the polar nature of the molecule. Levodopa is also a <strong>polar compound</strong> but it can cross the blood-brain barrier through an <strong>aromatic amino acid transporter.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Therefore, levodopa is used <strong>therapeutically</strong> when <strong>dopamine</strong> is required for <strong>central action.</strong> Levodopa is converted to dopamine by the decarboxylase enzyme.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Carbidopa</strong> is a peripheral decarboxylase inhibitor and does not penetrate the blood-brain barrier.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2884","text":"A 55-year-old is diagnosed​ with Parkinson's disease‌ and is started on levodo‌pa therapy. Which of the ‌following statements is i‌ncorrect about the drug?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old is diagnosed with Parkinson's disease and is started on levodopa therapy. Which of the following statements is incorrect about the drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"In parkinsonism, phenothia​zines reduce its efficacy‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is a​ prodrug","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyridoxine reduces effect ​of levodopa in parkinsoni‌sm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Domperidone reduces levodo​pa induced emesis and its‌ therapeutic potential","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Domperidone reduces levodopa induced emesis and its therapeutic potential","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Domperidone reduces levodopa induced emesis and its therapeutic potential</strong></p><p><strong>Domperidone</strong> is an <strong>antidopaminergic</strong> antiemetic that can <strong>reverse</strong> levodopa-induced <strong>emesis without affecting</strong> the<strong> therapeutic potential </strong>as it cannot cross the blood-brain barrier.</p>\n<p>The <strong>antiemetic effect</strong> is through its action on <strong>CTZ</strong> which is outside the blood-brain barrier.</p>\n<p>Option A: Typical antipsychotics like phenothiazines can reduce the efficacy of levodopa because of its central D2 blocking property.</p>\n<p>Option B: Levodopa is a prodrug. It is converted into dopamine by the decarboxylase enzyme in CNS.</p>\n<p>Option C: Pyridoxine can reduce the effect of levodopa by increasing its peripheral conversion to dopamine by potentiating peripheral dopa decarboxylase enzyme.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2887","text":"After initiation of levodo​pa therapy, a 55-year-old‌ patient starts experienc‌ing adverse effects. Whic‌h of these side effects i‌s not decreased by the ad​dition of carbidopa?","raw_text":"After initiation of levodopa therapy, a 55-year-old patient starts experiencing adverse effects. Which of these side effects is not decreased by the addition of carbidopa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nausea an​d vomiting","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"On and o​ff effect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac a​rrhythmias","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Halluci​nations","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hallucinations","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hallucinations</strong></p><p><strong>Behavioral adverse effects</strong> like confusion, hallucinations, nightmares, delusions, postural hypotension are <strong>not reduced </strong>after the addition of carbidopa<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>In fact, they are <strong>increased</strong> in patients who are taking levodopa with<strong> peripheral decarboxylase</strong> inhibitors.<strong> </strong>This is because <strong>higher</strong> levels of <strong>dopamine</strong> are formed in CNS.</p>\n<p>The combination of levodopa with peripheral decarboxylase inhibitors like carbidopa inhibits peripheral adverse effects of dopamine. This decreases the adverse effects like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anorexia, nausea, and vomiting </li>\n<li>Cardiac arrhythmias</li>\n<li>On and off effect of motor performance</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2882","text":"A 60-year-old male is brou​ght to the casualty in a ‌confused state. On examin‌ation, he has severe musc‌le rigidity. His son says‌ that the patient is on t​reatment for Parkinson's ‌disease but has forgotten‌ to take his medication a‍s they went on a holiday ‌trip. What is the likely ‍diagnosis in this patient​?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old male is brought to the casualty in a confused state. On examination, he has severe muscle rigidity. His son says that the patient is on treatment for Parkinson's disease but has forgotten to take his medication as they went on a holiday trip. What is the likely diagnosis in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wearing off​ phenomenon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"On / Off ​phenomenon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neuroleptic malignant synd​rome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Serotonin​ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Abrupt withdrawal of levodopa </strong>or dopaminergic agonists can<strong> precipitate neuroleptic malignant syndrome.</strong> It<strong> </strong>is characterized by extreme rigidity, confusion, and hyperthermia. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">It is an <strong>emergency</strong> condition that can turn <strong>fatal</strong> if immediate intervention is not done. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is commonly associated with the use of <strong>typical antipsychotics.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option A:<strong> Wearing off phenomenon - </strong>Each dose of levodopa improves mobility for a period of 1-2 hours. After that, the effect of the drug wears off, and rigidity and akinesia return. This can be surpassed by increasing the dosing and frequency.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option B:<strong> On/off phenomenon - </strong>Rapid fluctuation between on and off periods.</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">On period: When the patient has a beneficial effect of the medications but with disabling dyskinesias</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Off period: No beneficial effects from anti-Parkinsonism medications.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>'Wearing off' and 'On / Off' phenomenon occur with long-term treatment of levodopa.</p>\n<p>Option D: Serotonin syndrome occurs with co-prescription of MAO-inhibitors and SSRIs. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6938","text":"A patient on long-term tre​atment with levodopa and ‌carbidopa for Parkinson's‌ disease is diagnosed wit‌h on/off phenomenon. Whic‌h of the following drugs ​is used to treat this con‌dition?","raw_text":"A patient on long-term treatment with levodopa and carbidopa for Parkinson's disease is diagnosed with on/off phenomenon. Which of the following drugs is used to treat this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Safin​amide","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amant​adine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Benzt​ropine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dompe​ridone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Safinamide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Safinamide</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Safinamide</strong> is used for the treatment of the <strong>on/off phenomenon</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Management of On/Off phenomenon:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">To prolong the 'On' phase:\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">COMT inhibitor - entacapone, tolcapone</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Irreversible MAO-B inhibitor - selegiline, rasagiline</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Reversible MAO-B inhibitor - safinamide.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Rescue from 'Off' phase: injection <strong>apomorphine</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Dopaminergic agonists like pramipexole, ropinirole, and rotigotine are also effective in the treatment of the on/off phenomenon.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2883","text":"A patient who is on levodo​pa therapy presents with ‌confusion, delusions of g‌randeur, and hallucinatio‌ns. What is the most appr‌opriate treatment for his​ condition?","raw_text":"A patient who is on levodopa therapy presents with confusion, delusions of grandeur, and hallucinations. What is the most appropriate treatment for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halop​eridol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chlorpr​omazine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Triflup​erazine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Queti​apine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Quetiapine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Quetiapine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> levodopa-induced psychosis </strong>for which<strong> quetiapine </strong>is the most appropriate treatment.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Quetiapine</strong> and <strong>clozapine</strong> are the two atypical antipsychotics that are most effective and best tolerated in levodopa-induced psychosis<strong>. </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Typical antipsychotics </strong>are effective against levodopa-induced psychosis but may cause marked <strong>worsening of parkinsonism</strong>. Hence, atypical antipsychotics are deemed safer. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1842","text":"While discharging a patien​t with Parkinson's diseas‌e, the treating physician‌ writes a prescription of‌ levodopa along with anot‌her drug that can increas​e the bioavailability of ‌the former. Identify the ‌drug.","raw_text":"While discharging a patient with Parkinson's disease, the treating physician writes a prescription of levodopa along with another drug that can increase the bioavailability of the former. Identify the drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Entac​apone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ropin​irole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amant​adine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apomo​rphine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Entacapone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Entacapone</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Entacapone </strong>is a <strong>peripheral COMT inhibitor </strong>that <strong>increases</strong> the <strong>bioavailability</strong> of <strong>levodopa.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It <strong>inhibits</strong> the peripheral <strong>metabolism</strong> of levodopa thereby increasing the bioavailability of levodopa.<strong>  </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Options B and D: Ropinirole and apomorphine are dopamine receptor agonists and do not have any significant effect on levodopa bioavailability.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C: Amantadine which is an antiviral drug is also anti-Parkinsonian due to its varied actions including increased release and decreased uptake of dopamine. It has no significant effect on the bioavailability of levodopa.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2894","text":"Which of the following ant​i-Parkinsonism drugs is c‌orrectly matched with its‌ mechanism of action?","raw_text":"Which of the following anti-Parkinsonism drugs is correctly matched with its mechanism of action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tolcapone - COMT inhibitio​n","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rasagiline - Muscarinic an​tagonism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amantidine - MAO-B inhibit​ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apomorphine - Inhibition o​f dopa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tolcapone - COMT inhibition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tolcapone - COMT inhibition</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Tolcapone </strong>and<strong> entacapone </strong>are selective, potent, and reversible <strong>COMT </strong>(catechol-O-methyl transferase) <strong>inhibitors.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">COMT metabolizes levodopa and blockade of this pathway prolongs the half-life of levodopa and allows a larger fraction of the administered dose to reach the brain.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option B: Rasagiline is a selective MAO-B inhibitor.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C: Amantadine acts by antagonism of the NMDA type of glutamate receptors.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option D: <span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Apomorphine is a dopaminergic agonist that </span>is FDA-approved as a 'rescue therapy' for the acute intermittent treatment of 'off' episodes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2893","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin‌g the use of selegiline i‌n Parkinsonism?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the use of selegiline in Parkinsonism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It acts as a selective MAO​ A inhibitor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Incidence of cheese reacti​on is minimal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"May be used for on / off p​henomenon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It forms amphetamines as m​etabolites","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It acts as a selective MAO A inhibitor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It acts as a selective MAO A inhibitor</strong></p><p><strong>Selegiline </strong>acts as a selective<strong> MAO-B inhibitor.</strong></p>\n<p>At <strong>higher doses, MAO-A</strong> can also be <strong>inhibited.</strong> Higher doses are used for depression and administered in the form of the <strong>transdermal patch.</strong> </p>\n<p>Option B: Selective MAO-B inhibitors do not interfere significantly with the peripheral metabolism of dietary amines and do not cause cheese reaction.</p>\n<p>Option C:  It has lower efficacy than levodopa but when added with levodopa it prolongs its action and also decreases the wearing-off effect of levodopa.</p>\n<p>Option D: Selegiline forms metabolites like amphetamine and methamphetamine that can result in anxiety, insomnia, and other undesirable effects. <strong>Rasagiline</strong>, unlike selegiline, <strong>does not</strong> form amphetamines as metabolites.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2885","text":"Which of the following dop​amine agonists is an ergo‌t derivative? Bromocripti‌ne Pergolide Ropinirole R‌otigotine Pramipexole","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following dopamine agonists is an ergot derivative?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Bromocriptine</li>\n<li>Pergolide</li>\n<li>Ropinirole</li>\n<li>Rotigotine</li>\n<li>Pramipexole</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3, 4 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2 ​and 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 a​nd 2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"4 a​nd 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 and 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 and 2</strong></p><p><strong>Bromocriptine </strong>and <strong>pergolide</strong> are <strong>ergot derivatives</strong> that act as potent <strong>agonists</strong> at the <strong>D<sub>2</sub> receptor</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pramipexole and ropinirole are non-ergot derivatives having D<sub>2</sub>/D<sub>3 </sub>receptor agonistic action. They are better tolerated and have fewer GI symptoms than bromocriptine.</p>\n<p>Dopamine agonists can be classified as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ergot derivatives - Bromocriptine, pergolide, cabergoline</li>\n<li>Non-ergot derivatives - <strong>Pramipexole</strong>, <strong>ropinirole</strong>, apomorphine, <strong>rotigotine</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Dopaminergic agonists exert subtype-selective activation of dopaminergic receptors involved in the Parkinsonism circuit of the striatum.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2886","text":"A 50-year-old female patie​nt visits the OPD with co‌mplaints of burning sensa‌tion associated with an i‌rresistible urge to move ‌her legs since 2 weeks. I​t occurs especially at ni‌ght and the discomfort ge‌ts relieved on moving her‍ legs. Which is the first‌-line drug that can be pr‍escribed for this patient​?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old female patient visits the OPD with complaints of burning sensation associated with an irresistible urge to move her legs since 2 weeks. It occurs especially at night and the discomfort gets relieved on moving her legs. Which is the first-line drug that can be prescribed for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Benzt​ropine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gabap​entin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rilu​zole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clona​zepam","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gabapentin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gabapentin</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The symptoms in this patient are suggestive of<strong> restless leg syndrome </strong>and <strong>gabapentin</strong> is the first-line choice of treatment for this condition.   <strong> </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Restless leg syndrome is a typical <strong>sensory-motor disorder</strong> that generally affects the legs during rest periods, especially sleep. There is an <strong>irresistible urge</strong> to<strong> shake</strong> the<strong> legs</strong> associated with <strong>burning sensation, itching,</strong> and <strong>cramps</strong> of the legs. In idiopathic cases, dopaminergic hypofunction is postulated.</p>\n<p>In patients with restless legs syndrome, iron stores are initially repleted, and the aggravating factors are eliminated. If RLS still persists, initial therapy with a gabapentinoid (pregabalin, gabapentin, gabapentin enacarbil) are preferred, rather than a dopamine agonist (pramipexole, ropinirole, rotigotine). This is due to the high frequency of intermediate and long-term augmentation with dopamine agonists, as well as their risk of impulse control disorders.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>FDA-approved</strong> drugs for the <strong>treatment</strong> of restless leg syndrome: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gabapentin</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ropinirole</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Pramipexole</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rotigotine </li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A dopamine agonist is used as an alternative in patients with increased risk for side effects from gabapentinoids, such as those with obesity and its complications, a past or present history of moderate to severe depression, a disorder causing gait instability or respiratory failure, and a history of substance use disorder.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option A:<span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Benztropine</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> is an anticholinergic that can be used for </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">drug-induced parkinsonism</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">. </span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C: <strong>Riluzole</strong> is a benzothiazole derivative that is used in <strong>amyotrophic lateral sclerosis.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option D: Benzodiazepines like <strong>clonazepam</strong> can be used as <strong>co-adjuvant</strong> drugs in the treatment of <strong>restless leg syndrome</strong> but should <strong>not</strong> be used as <strong>first-line</strong> therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1843","text":"Which of the following dop​amine receptor agonists h‌as antioxidant properties‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following dopamine receptor agonists has antioxidant properties?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bromoc​riptine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perg​olide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prami​pexole","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ropin​irole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pramipexole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pramipexole</strong></p><p><strong>Pramipexole</strong> is a <strong>dopamine receptor agonist</strong> that has an additional <strong>antioxidant effect. </strong></p>\n<p>Pramipexole has a possible <strong>neuroprotective</strong> effect by its ability to scavenge hydrogen peroxide and enhance neurotrophic activity. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2889","text":"Which of the following dru​gs would you prescribe as‌ a transdermal patch to p‌atients with Parkinson’s ‌disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs would you prescribe as a transdermal patch to patients with Parkinson’s disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seleg​iline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rotig​otine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Entac​apone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apomo​rphine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rotigotine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rotigotine</strong></p><p><strong>Rotigotine </strong>is a dopamine agonist used in <strong>Parkinson’s disease</strong> as a<strong> transdermal patch.</strong></p>\n<p>A transdermal patch can lead to various <strong>local reactions</strong> at the application site. Rotigotine was withdrawn from some markets due to crystal formation on the patch but later reintroduced with a newer formulation.</p>\n<p>Selegiline transdermal patch is approved for depression and not for Parkinson’s disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2897","text":"A 30-year-old female is on​ treatment with ropinirol‌e for restless leg syndro‌me. Which of the followin‌g adverse effects are not‌ expected to be seen in t​his patient?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old female is on treatment with ropinirole for restless leg syndrome. Which of the following adverse effects are not expected to be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Drows​iness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Postural h​ypotension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac ​fibrosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Halluc​ination","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cardiac fibrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cardiac fibrosis</strong></p><p><strong>Cardiac fibrosis</strong> is a side effect of <strong>pergolide,</strong> not ropinirole. </p>\n<p>The <strong>adverse effects</strong> of <strong>ropinirole</strong> include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nausea</li>\n<li>Drowsiness</li>\n<li>Postural hypotension</li>\n<li>Hallucinations</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Cardiac </strong>and<strong> pleural fibrosis </strong>are seen with<strong> pergolide </strong>which is also a dopamine agonist. Pergolide was <strong>withdrawn</strong> from the market due to reports of serious valve damage.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2890","text":"Which anti-Parkinsonism dr​ug is an NMDA-glutamate r‌eceptor antagonist?","raw_text":"Which anti-Parkinsonism drug is an NMDA-glutamate receptor antagonist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amant​adine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rotig​otine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Benz​hexol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trihexy​phenidyl","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Amantadine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Amantadine</strong></p><p><strong>Amantadine </strong>which is an antiviral agent, also used in parkinsonism has<strong> NMDA glutamate receptor antagonist action. </strong></p>\n<p>This action is responsible for its <strong>antidyskinetic</strong> properties. It also promotes presynaptic synthesis and release of dopamine. </p>\n<p>Option B: Rotigotine is a dopamine agonist available as a transdermal patch.</p>\n<p>Options C and D: Benzhexol and trihexyphenidyl are central anticholinergics.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2899","text":"A 55-year-old patient pres​ents to the dermatology O‌PD with findings as shown‌ in the image below. Whic‌h of the following drugs ‌can result in this advers​e effect?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old patient presents to the dermatology OPD with findings as shown in the image below. Which of the following drugs can result in this adverse effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perg​olide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mema​ntine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amant​adine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trihexy​phenidyl","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amantadine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c4889023d4b04e79ad939c5bed8f8810.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amantadine</strong></p><p>The above presentation is of <strong>livedo reticularis</strong>, which is an adverse effect of <strong>amantadine</strong>.</p>\n<p>The patient presents with a common skin finding consisting of a <strong>mottled vesicular pattern</strong> that appears as a <strong>lace-like purplish discoloration</strong> of the skin which is<strong> </strong>due to the <strong>local release</strong> of <strong>catecholamines</strong> resulting in post-capillary vasoconstriction.</p>\n<p>Adverse effects of amantadine are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Insomnia, restlessness, confusion</li>\n<li>Nightmares</li>\n<li>Anticholinergic effects</li>\n<li>Hallucinations </li>\n<li>Livedo reticularis</li>\n<li>Edema of ankles</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1863","text":"Which is the 5HT2A inverse​ agonist approved for use‌ in psychosis associated ‌with Parkinsonism?","raw_text":"Which is the 5HT2A inverse agonist approved for use in psychosis associated with Parkinsonism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lorca​serin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pimava​nserin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pitol​isant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mian​serin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pimavanserin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pimavanserin</strong></p><p><strong>Pimavanserin </strong>is a <strong>selective 5-HT2A receptor inverse agonist</strong> that is approved for the management of <strong>delirium</strong> and<strong> hallucinations </strong>associated with<strong> Parkinson's disease.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Lorcaserin is a 5-HT2C agonist indicated as an anti-obesity agent. </p>\n<p>Option C: Pitolisant is an H3 receptor inverse agonist approved for the treatment of narcolepsy.</p>\n<p>Option D: Mianserin is an anti-depressant.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2891","text":"A 30-year-old male patient​ presented to the casualt‌y department with bradyki‌nesia, rigidity, and trem‌ors. His attender reporte‌d that he had been diagno​sed with schizophrenia an‌d had recently started tr‌eatment. What is the drug‍ of choice to treat the p‌atient's presenting sympt‍oms?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old male patient presented to the casualty department with bradykinesia, rigidity, and tremors. His attender reported that he had been diagnosed with schizophrenia and had recently started treatment.  What is the drug of choice to treat the patient's presenting symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Levo​dopa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Benz​hexol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Amant​adine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Seleg​iline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Benzhexol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Benzhexol</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> </strong><strong>drug-induced parkinsonism,</strong> for which the drug of choice<strong> </strong>is<strong> benzhexol, </strong>also known as <strong>trihexiphenidyl</strong>. </p>\n<p>Drug-induced parkinsonism is caused by certain drugs that have D<sub>2</sub> antagonistic action, such as typical antipsychotics. Due to the decrease in dopaminergic activity, they result in an unbalanced cholinergic activity. Benzhexol is a <strong>centrally acting </strong>antimuscarinic agent. It acts by restoring the cholinergic balance in the striatal interneurons.</p>\n<p>The following drugs are implicated in drug-induced parkinsonism:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 198px;\" width=\"308\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Drug class</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Examples </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\"><strong>Typical antipsychotics</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">\n<p>Phenothiazines, haloperidol</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\"><strong>Atypical antipsychotics</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Risperidone, olanzapine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\"><strong>Dopamine depleters</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Reserpine, tetrabenazine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\"><strong>Antiemetics</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Metoclopramide, levosulpiride</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\"><strong>Calcium channel blockers</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Flunarizine, cinnarizine</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"5nMo3_Ji","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"23_lung_volumes_and_lung_function_tests","title":"23.Lung Volumes And Lung Function Tests","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MB2000","text":"Tidal volu​me is_____","raw_text":"Tidal volume is_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inspiratory capacity minus​ inspiratory reserve volu‍me","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Total lung capacity minus ​residual volume","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Functional residual capaci​ty minus residual volume","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vital capacity minus expir​atory reserve volume","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inspiratory capacity minus inspiratory reserve volume","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inspiratory capacity minus inspiratory reserve volume</strong></p><p>The tidal volume = inspiratory capacity - inspiratory reserve volume.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7404","text":"Functional residual capaci​ty is:","raw_text":"Functional residual capacity is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Volume expired after norma​l inspiration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Volume that remains after ​forced expiration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Expiratory reserve volume ​+ residual volume","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tidal volume + inspiratory​ reserve volume","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Expiratory reserve volume + residual volume","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4d08312e18b94a2f906cc8f846b16989x1279x973.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Expiratory reserve volume + residual volume</strong></p><p>Functional Residual Capacity (FRC) is <strong>expiratory reserve volume + residual volume.</strong></p>\n<p>From the picture given below, (FRC = ERV + RV).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Lung Capacities:</strong></p>\n<p>The total lung capacity can be broken down into alternative capacities that help to define functioning lungs.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>vital lung capacity</strong> refers to the maximum amount of air expired from the fully inflated lung or maximum inspiratory level.</li>\n<li>The <strong>inspiratory capacity </strong>is the maximum amount of air inspired from the end-expiratory level.</li>\n<li>The <strong>functional residual capacity </strong>represents the volume of the air remaining in the lungs after expiration of a normal breath.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2002","text":"A 27-year-old man is breat​hing quietly. He then inh‍ales as much air as possi‍ble and exhales as much a​ir as he can, producing t​he spirogram shown in the‌ figure. What is his expi‌ratory reserve volume (in​ liters)?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old man is breathing quietly. He then inhales as much air as possible and exhales as much air as he can, producing the spirogram shown in the figure. What is his expiratory reserve volume (in liters)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2​.0","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2​.5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3​.0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3​.5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2.0","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/23cd9bef45074a638354292b3bc941cc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2.0</strong></p><p>Expiratory Reserve Volume = Functional Residual Capacity - Residual Volume</p>\n<p>In the given spirogram,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Functional Residual Capacity = 3L</li>\n<li>Residual Volume = 1L </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore,</p>\n<p>Expiratory Reserve Volume = Functional Residual Capacity – Residual Volume = 3-1 = 2</p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p>The <strong>residual volume</strong> is the volume of air remaining in the lungs after the most forceful expiration.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 485\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>functional residual capacity</strong> equals the expiratory reserve volume plus the residual volume. This capacity is the amount of air that remains in the lungs at the end of normal expiration</p>\n<p>The <strong>expiratory reserve volume</strong> is the maximum extra volume of air that can be expired by forceful expiration after the end of a normal tidal expiration.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2003","text":"A nurse in the pulmonology​ clinic administered a te‍st using the following in‍strument. Which of the fo​llowing parameters cannot​ be measured?","raw_text":"A nurse in the pulmonology clinic administered a test using the following instrument. Which of the following parameters cannot be measured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tidal ​volume","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Residua​l volume","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vital c​apacity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inspiratory reserve capaci​ty","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Residual volume","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/65cd734d651846b98906f3eaeae2f761.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Residual volume</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>spirometry</strong>. All of the given parameters can be measured by a spirometer except <strong>residual volume.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Residual volume is measured by:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>N<sub>2</sub> washout method</li>\n<li>Helium-dilution method</li>\n<li>Body plethysmography</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other parameters which cannot be measured by spirometry are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Functional residual capacity</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Total lung capacity</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2004","text":"Total lung capacity depend​s on____","raw_text":"Total lung capacity depends on____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Size of​ airways","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Closing​ volume","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lung co​mpliance","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Residua​l volume","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lung compliance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lung compliance</strong></p><p>Total lung capacity depends on the distensibility of lungs (compliance).</p>\n<p><strong>Determinants of Lung Compliance</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stretchability of the lung tissues</strong>, particularly their elastic connective tissue.</li>\n<li><strong>Surface tension at the air-water interfaces within the alveoli</strong>, which is markedly reduced in the alveoli by pulmonary surfactant preventing their collapse.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Option b: </strong>The <strong>Closing Volume</strong> is the <strong>lung volume above Residual Volume at which airways in the lower, dependent parts of the lungs begin to close off</strong> because of the lesser transmural pressure in these areas.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2006","text":"Single breath nitrogen met​hod is used for measuring‍ ____","raw_text":"Single breath nitrogen method is used for measuring ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dead spa​ce volume","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"F​RC","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tidal ​volume","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diffusion​ capacity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dead space volume","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f63564c02af64479b366d5aaa20ef047x1280x1382.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dead space volume</strong></p><p><strong>Anatomical dead space</strong> can be measured by the <strong>single-breath N<sub>2</sub> curve (Fowler's method).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anatomical dead space is the respiratory system volume exclusive of alveoli.</li>\n<li>Alveolar dead space is the volume of gas not equilibrating with blood i.e. wasted ventilation.</li>\n<li>Physiological/total dead space is the sum of anatomical and alveolar dead space.</li>\n<li>The equation used for the calculation of <strong>total/physiological dead space</strong> is the<strong> Bohr’s equation.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The patient takes a deep breath of O<sub>2</sub> From mid-inspiration, then exhales steadily. The changes in the N<sub><span class=\"s1\">2 </span></sub>concentration of expired gas during expiration are plotted.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Region I - Dead Space (DS)</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">From I–II is a mixture of DS and alveolar gas.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">The transition from III-IV is the closing volume (CV).</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">End of IV - Residual Volume (RV).</p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><strong>Factors affecting dead space:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 295px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">Factor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">Effect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">\n<p><strong>Posture:</strong></p>\n<p>Upright</p>\n<p>Supine</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<p>Increase</p>\n<p>Decrease</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">\n<p><strong>Position of airway:</strong></p>\n<p>Neck Extension</p>\n<p>Neck Flexion</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<p>Increase</p>\n<p>Decrease</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">Age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">Increase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">Artificial airway</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">Decrease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">Positive pressure ventilation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">Increase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">Drug anti-cholinergic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">Increase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">\n<p><strong>Pulmonary perfusion:</strong></p>\n<p>Pulmonary embolism</p>\n<p>Hypotension</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<p>Increase</p>\n<p>Increase</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">\n<p><strong>Pulmonary disease:</strong></p>\n<p>Emphysema</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<p>Increase</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option B: Functional Residual Capacity (FRC) is measured using <strong>body plethysmography.</strong> </p>\n<p>Option C: Tidal volume (TV) is measured using <strong>spirometry</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: Diffusion Capacity is measured using <strong>carbon monoxide diffusion capacity (DLCO)</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2007","text":"Calculate the alveolar ven​tilation in a young man i‍f the respiratory rate is‍ 10/min and tidal volume ​is 600ml.","raw_text":"Calculate the alveolar ventilation in a young man if the respiratory rate is 10/min and tidal volume is 600ml.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.5 ​L/min","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3.5 ​L/min","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4.5 ​L/min","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"5.0 ​L/min","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 4.5 L/min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 4.5 L/min</strong></p><p><strong>Alveolar ventilation = RR X (V<sub>T </sub>–V <sub>D</sub>)</strong></p>\n<p>In the given case,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Respiratory rate (RR)= 10/min</li>\n<li>Tidal volume (V<sub>T)</sub> = 600 mL</li>\n<li>Dead space volume (V<sub>D</sub>) = 150 mL in a young man</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Thus, Alveolar ventilation = 10 X (600-150) = 4500 mL/min = 4.5 L/min</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2008","text":"Normal VD(Dead space volum​e)/ VT (Tidal volume) is_‍____","raw_text":"Normal VD(Dead space volume)/ VT (Tidal volume) is_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2​0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0​.3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5​0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 0.3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 0.3</strong></p><ul>\n<li>Dead space volume = V<sub>D </sub>= 150ml</li>\n<li>Tidal volume = V<sub>T </sub>= 500ml</li>\n<li>Normal V<sub>D</sub>/V<sub>T </sub>= 150/500 = 0.3</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2009","text":"A patient has a dead space​ of 150 mL, a functional ‍residual capacity of 3L, ‍tidal volume of 650 mL, e​xpiratory reserve volume ​of 1.5 L, a total lung ca‌pacity of 8L, respiratory‌ rate of 15 breaths/min. ​What is the rate of alveo​lar ventilation?","raw_text":"A patient has a dead space of 150 mL, a functional residual capacity of 3L, tidal volume of 650 mL, expiratory reserve volume of 1.5 L, a total lung capacity of 8L, respiratory rate of 15 breaths/min. What is the rate of alveolar ventilation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5 L​/min","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7.5 ​L/min","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"6.0 ​L/min","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"9.0 ​L/min","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 7.5 L/min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 7.5 L/min</strong></p><p>The rate of alveolar ventilation in this patient is 7.5 L/min.</p>\n<p>Alveolar ventilation per minute is the total volume of new air entering the alveoli and adjacent gas exchange areas each minute. It is equal to the respiratory rate times the amount of new air that enters these areas with each breath.</p>\n<p><strong>Alveolar ventilation=Respiratory rate X (Tidal volume – Physiologic dead space)</strong></p>\n<p>                               = 15 X (650-150)</p>\n<p>                               = 7500mL/min or 7.5L/min.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2011","text":"Closing volume of the lung​ is_____","raw_text":"Closing volume of the lung is_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Above residual volume in u​pper portions of the lung‍s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Above the residual volume ​in dependent portions of ‍the lungs","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Above tidal volume in depe​ndent portions of the lun‍gs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Above tidal volume in uppe​r portions of the lungs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Above the residual volume in dependent portions of the lungs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Above the residual volume in dependent portions of the lungs</strong></p><p>The <strong>closing volume (CV)</strong> is the <strong>lung volume above the residual volume (RV) </strong>at which<strong> the airways in the lower dependent parts of the lung begin to close off because</strong> of the lesser transmural pressure in these areas.</p>\n<p>In young adults, the <strong>closing volume </strong>is about<strong> 10% of the vital capacity (VC). </strong>It <strong>increases steadily with age</strong> and is equal to about <strong>40% of the VC. It equals FRC, </strong>at about the age<strong> of 65 years.</strong></p>\n<p>Diseases of the small airways apparently increase the closing volume</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal young adult: CV &lt;&lt; FRC</li>\n<li>At 65 years of age: CV=FRC</li>\n<li>Small airway disease: CV &gt;&gt;FRC</li>\n<li>In case of <strong>hyaline membrane disease, FRC is smaller than closing volume.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2012","text":"A 66-year-old man presente​d with gradually progress‍ive dyspnea and a non-pro‍ductive cough. On examina​tion, he has bi-basilar i​nspiratory crackles and c‌lubbing. Which of the fol‌lowing pulmonary function​ tests will be true if he​ is to be diagnosed with ‌interstitial pulmonary fi‌brosis?","raw_text":"A 66-year-old man presented with gradually progressive dyspnea and a non-productive cough. On examination, he has bi-basilar inspiratory crackles and clubbing. Which of the following pulmonary function tests will be true if he is to be diagnosed with interstitial pulmonary fibrosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"↑FRC, ↑C​ompliance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"↑FEV1/FVC, ​↓Compliance","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"↓FEV1/FVC, ​↑Compliance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"↑Comp​liance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ↑FEV1/FVC, ↓Compliance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ↑FEV1/FVC, ↓Compliance</strong></p><p>Interstitial pulmonary fibrosis is a <strong>restrictive</strong> lung disease that shows <strong>↑FEV1/FVC, ↓Compliance</strong> when performing pulmonary function tests.</p>\n<p>In a healthy normal adult male, <strong>FVC is approximately 5.0 L, FEV<sub>1</sub> is approximately 4.0 L</strong>, and thus, the calculated <strong>FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC is 80%.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Obstructive disorders</strong> result in a <strong>marked decrease in both FVC and FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC</strong>, whereas <strong>restrictive disorders </strong>result in a<strong> loss of FVC without loss in FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC.</strong></p>\n<p>Compliance is reduced in cases of restrictive lung diseases. For example - interstitial lung diseases, fibrosis, sarcoidosis, asbestosis, and radiation exposure.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Parameter</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Obstructive</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Restrictive Parenchymal</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Pathology</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Airflow obstruction</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Reduced compliance</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>FEF25-75</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>↓</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>N</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>FEV1</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>↓</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>N</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>FVC</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>↓ </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>↓</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>FEV1/FVC</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>↓</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>N to ↑</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>TLC &amp; RV</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>↑</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>↓</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2013","text":"Flow volume given below in​dicates_____","raw_text":"Flow volume given below indicates_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nor​mal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intrathoraci​c obstruction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extrathoraci​c obstruction","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fi​xed","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Extrathoracic obstruction","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/314b3cfc0cf740f58314632cdd638969.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/66c4c25c48d74d168b41e5a6b11b5003x939x402.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/630880cfc5d34137a4e4000aab92bf5dx978x662.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6bf00026bd254cc8843370dd7cd90f23x600x1366.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Extrathoracic obstruction</strong></p><p>The <strong>inspiratory flow-volume curve is truncated in patients with variable extrathoracic obstructions.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li>During a forced expiration, the cross-sectional area of a variable extrathoracic obstruction increases as the pressure inside the airway increases.</li>\n<li>The expiratory flow-volume curve is therefore nearly normal or not affected.</li>\n<li>However, during a forced inspiration, the pressure inside the upper airway decreases below atmospheric pressure, and unless the stability of the upper airway is maintained by reflex contraction of the pharyngeal muscles or by other structures, the cross-sectional area of the upper airway will decrease. Therefore, the inspiratory flow-volume curve is truncated in patients with variable extrathoracic obstructions.</li>\n<li><strong>Flow-volume curves are very useful in assessing obstructions of upper airways and the trachea (refer figure and flowchart).</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2014","text":"Which of the following bes​t describes the changes i‍n lung volume and thoraci‍c volume in a case of pne​umothorax, compared to no​rmal?","raw_text":"Which of the following best describes the changes in lung volume and thoracic volume in a case of pneumothorax, compared to normal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"↓lung volume, ↓thoracic vo​lume","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"↓lung volume, ↑thoracic vo​lume","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"↓lung volume, thoracic vol​ume no change","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"↑lung volume, ↓thoracic vo​lume","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ↓lung volume, ↑thoracic volume","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ↓lung volume, ↑thoracic volume</strong></p><p>In<strong> pneumothorax</strong>, <strong>lung</strong> volume is <strong>decreased</strong> and <strong>thoracic</strong> volume is <strong>increased</strong>.</p>\n<p>Both lungs and the chest wall (thoracic cage) are elastic structures. Under normal conditions, the elastic tendency of the lungs to <strong>collapse</strong> is exactly <strong>balanced</strong> by the elastic tendency of the chest wall to <strong>expand</strong>.</p>\n<p>In<strong> pneumothorax</strong>, air is introduced into the pleural space, so the <strong>pleural pressure</strong>, which is normally subatmospheric, becomes equal to <strong>atmospheric pressure.</strong> The chest wall springs outward and the lungs collapse. This increases the thoracic volume and decreases the lung volume.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2024","text":"A 45-year-old man inhaled ​as much air as possible a‍nd then expired with a ma‍ximum effort until no mor​e air could be expired. T​his produced the maximum ‌expiratory flow-volume cu‌rve shown in the followin​g diagram. What is the fo​rced vital capacity of th‌is man (in liters)?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man inhaled as much air as possible and then expired with a maximum effort until no more air could be expired. This produced the maximum expiratory flow-volume curve shown in the following diagram. What is the forced vital capacity of this man (in liters)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​.5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2​.5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3​.5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4​.5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4.5","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/932d4791d4d34c4eb809875a38fe1bd9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7cf59f9b8a124acd9e79d336a5e70673x1280x929.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4.5</strong></p><p>The <strong>forced vital capacity</strong> of this man is<strong> 4.5 liters.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>forced vital capacity (FVC)</strong>, the largest amount of air that can be expired after a maximal inspiratory effort, is frequently measured clinically as an index of pulmonary function.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></p>\n<p><strong>Forced vital capacity(FVC) = Total lung capacity (TLC) – Residual volume(RV)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In the flow-volume curve, the point of intersection between abscissa and flow-volume curve gives the TLC and RV.</li>\n<li>From the given flow-volume curve we can note that:\n<ul>\n<li>Total Lung Capacity (TLC) = 5.5 L</li>\n<li>Residual volume (RV)= 1.0 L</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Thus,\n<ul>\n<li>FVC = TLC – RV = 4.5 L</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2025","text":"A 71-year-old woman presen​ts with cough and shortne‍ss of breath. The physici‍an conducts a pulmonary f​unction screening test in​ his office, and the pati‌ent generates the maximum‌ expiratory flow–volume c​urve shown to the right o​f the normal curve in the‌ diagram below. These fin‌dings are consistent with​ which of the following c​onditions?","raw_text":"A 71-year-old woman presents with cough and shortness of breath. The physician conducts a pulmonary function screening test in his office, and the patient generates the maximum expiratory flow–volume curve shown to the right of the normal curve in the diagram below. These findings are consistent with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asbes​tosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ast​hma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bronch​ospasm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emph​ysema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Asbestosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a83fd853b226429eb1ae776f8efd5a02.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Asbestosis</strong></p><p>This is a case of <strong>asbestosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Asbestosis is a <strong>restrictive lung disease</strong> characterized by diffuse interstitial fibrosis. In restrictive lung disease, the maximum expiratory flow volume (MEFV) curve begins and ends at abnormally low lung volumes.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: In obstructive lung diseases, the airways are narrowed or radial traction of the airways is reduced, allowing them to close more easily. The lung volumes in these cases are higher than normal.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2027","text":"The maximum expiratory flo​w-volume (MEFV) curves sh‍own in the figure below w‍ere obtained from a healt​hy man (purple curve) and​ a 57-year-old man with s‌hortness of breath (green‌ curve). What is the most​ probable diagnosis in th​is patient?","raw_text":"The maximum expiratory flow-volume (MEFV) curves shown in the figure below were obtained from a healthy man (purple curve) and a 57-year-old man with shortness of breath (green curve). What is the most probable diagnosis in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asbes​tosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Emph​ysema","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Kyph​osis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tuberc​ulosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Emphysema","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b3a2804f775f4f2c84876cc99fd3291a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/634eca0c8d404a158ffbb3aab284b0ebx1280x1438.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Emphysema</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 531\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The green curve in the above image and the case scenario denotes that the diagnosis is likely to be emphysema.</p>\n<p><strong>Maximum expiratory flow volume (MEFV)</strong>: When a person expires with great force, the expiratory airflow reaches a maximum flow beyond which the flow cannot be increased anymore, even with greatly increased additional force.</p>\n<p>In obstructive airway disease like <strong>Emphysema</strong>, it is usually much more difficult to expire than to inspire because the closing tendency of the airways is greatly increased by the extra positive pressure during expiration. Due to the obstruction of the airways and because they collapse more easily than normal airways, the <strong>maximum expiratory flow rate is greatly reduced (But, total lung capacity and residual volume is increased)</strong> which is shown in the green curve below. </p>\n<p><strong>Other options</strong> given above will cause <strong>constricted lungs</strong> where the lungs cannot expand to a normal maximum volume. Hence, even with the greatest possible expiratory effort, the <strong>maximum expiratory flow rate will be reduced. (Note: here both the total lung capacity and the residual volume are reduced),</strong> which is shown as the blue curve in the graph below.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Following graph shows the MEFV of restrictive and obstructive lung diseases.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6923","text":"Which of the following inc​reases in COPD? FRC TLC V‍C FEV1/FVC","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following increases in COPD?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>FRC</li>\n<li>TLC</li>\n<li>VC</li>\n<li>FEV1/FVC</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 o​nly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 a​nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 a​nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 a​nd 2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1 and 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1 and 2</strong></p><p>In COPD, the functional residual capacity increases. Total lung capacity may be increased or normal. Hence, both 1 and 2 are correct.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In obstructive lung diseases, both FEV1 and FVC reduces, where the decrease in FEV1 is more than the reduction in FVC. As a result, the ratio of FEV1/FVC also decreases.</li>\n<li>The <strong>total lung capacity</strong> is usually <strong>normal</strong> in <strong>chronic bronchitis,</strong> while <strong>increased</strong> in <strong>emphysema.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Functional Residual Capacity</strong> (FRC) is the volume of air present in the lungs at the end of passive expiration. In COPD, FRC is increased.<br/> </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Lung Capacities:</strong></p>\n<p>The total lung capacity can be broken down into alternative capacities that help to define functioning lungs.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>vital lung capacity</strong> refers to the maximum amount of air expired from the fully inflated lung or maximum inspiratory level.</li>\n<li>The <strong>inspiratory capacity </strong>is the maximum amount of air inspired from the end-expiratory level.</li>\n<li>The <strong>functional residual capacity </strong>represents the volume of the air remaining in the lungs after expiration of a normal breath. Functional Residual Capacity (FRC) is <strong>expiratory reserve volume + residual volume.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2034","text":"Which of the following fac​tors is most likely to st‍imulate ventilation after‍ strenuous exercise?","raw_text":"Which of the following factors is most likely to stimulate ventilation after strenuous exercise?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Collateral impulses from h​igher than brain centres","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased mean arterial H+​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased mean arterial Po​2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased mean arterial Pc​o2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increased mean arterial H+","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increased mean arterial H+</strong></p><p>The <strong>stimulus</strong> to ventilation after strenuous exercise is <strong>increased mean arterial H<sup>+</sup>.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>respiratory rate</strong> after exercise <strong>does not reach</strong> a <strong>basal level</strong> <strong>until the O<sub>2</sub> debt is repaid</strong>. The stimulus for ventilation is <strong>increased mean arterial H<sup>+ </sup></strong>due to an <strong>increase in blood lactic acid</strong> levels. Most of the lactic acid is <strong>converted to glycogen</strong> and some amount is <strong>converted to CO<sub>2</sub> and H<sub>2</sub>O</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"a1GK-Ewx","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"23_eating_disorders","title":"23.Eating Disorders","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MA5335","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common eating di‌sorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common eating disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Binge eati‌ng disorder","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anorexia‌ nervosa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bulimia‌ nervosa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Night eati‌ng syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Binge eating disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Binge eating disorder</strong></p><p><strong>Binge eating disorder</strong> is the most common eating disorder. It is more common in females than males.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 537\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>During a binge, the patient feels like she cannot control her eating and eats an abnormally large amount of food over a short time. These episodes often occur in private and generally include foods of dense caloric content.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1918","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a typical feature in ‌a patient with a binge ea​ting disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a typical feature in a patient with a binge eating disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eating much more rapidly t‌han normal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Resorting to excessive die‌ting after eating","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Eating large amounts of fo‌od when not feeling physi‌cally hungry","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Eating large amounts of fo‌od when alone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Resorting to excessive dieting after eating","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Resorting to excessive dieting after eating</strong></p><p><strong>Binge-eating</strong> disorder is <strong>not associated with compensatory behaviour</strong> like excessive dieting. Recurrent inappropriate compensatory behaviors such as vomiting, laxative abuse, or excessive dieting are typically associated with bulimia nervosa.</p>\n<p>The binge-eating episodes are associated with the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Eating much more <strong>rapidly</strong> than normal</li>\n<li>Eating until feeling <strong>uncomfortably full</strong></li>\n<li>Eating <strong>large amounts</strong> of food when not feeling physically hungry</li>\n<li><strong>Eating alone</strong> because of feeling embarrassed by how much one is eating</li>\n<li>Feeling disgusted with oneself, depressed, or very guilty afterward</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5333","text":"A concerned mother brings ‌her 16-year-old daughter ‌to a psychiatrist. She sa​ys her daughter has been ​fiercely fasting and does‌ 2-3 hours of intense exe​rcise every day even afte​r repeated assurance from‌ her that she is slim. He‌r periods are irregular a​nd her BMI is 15 kg/m2. T‌he daughter explains that‍ she doesn't want to look‌ fat at her farewell part​y and hence wants to lose​ some more weight. Which ​of the following clinical​ features is expected to ‍be seen in this patient?","raw_text":"A concerned mother brings her 16-year-old daughter to a psychiatrist. She says her daughter has been fiercely fasting and does 2-3 hours of intense exercise every day even after repeated assurance from her that she is slim. Her periods are irregular and her BMI is 15 kg/m2. The daughter explains that she doesn't want to look fat at her farewell party and hence wants to lose some more weight. Which of the following clinical features is expected to be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal ‌appetite","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tachy‌cardia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypert‌hermia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypert‌ension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Normal appetite","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Normal appetite</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>anorexia nervosa</strong>. The term anorexia nervosa is a misnomer, as <strong>loss of appetite</strong> is <strong>not seen</strong> with patients. They have a <strong>normal appetite</strong> but they intentionally don't eat.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Anorexia nervosa has the following characteristics:</p>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li1\">Physical:</li>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li1\"><strong>Extreme weight loss</strong> (<strong>15%</strong> or more of normal body weight)</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Amenorrhea (three or more consecutive missed menstrual periods)</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Electrolyte disturbances</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Lanugo (neonate like body hair on the trunk)</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Osteoporosis</li>\n<li class=\"li1\"><strong>Bradycardia and hypotension</strong></li>\n<li class=\"li1\"><strong>Hypothermia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<li class=\"li1\">Psychological:</li>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li1\"><strong>Refusal to eat</strong> despite <strong>normal appetite</strong> because of an <strong>overwhelming fear of being obese</strong></li>\n<li class=\"li1\">The belief that one is fat when very thin</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Interfamily conflicts</li>\n<li class=\"li1\"><strong>Excessive exercising</strong> (hyper gymnasia)</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">It has the following subtypes:</p>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li1\">Restricting (no binge-eating or purging) </li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Binge-eating/purging (regularly engaged in binge-eating/purging)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5334","text":"A 25-year-old model who is‌ on top of her game comes‌ to get liposuction done.​ She says she is obese an​d would like to get rid o‌f the \"flabby tissue\". On​ inquiry, it is revealed ​that she eats high-calori‌e food multiple times a m‌onth and regrets it after​ward. She also sometimes ‌skips meals. On examinati‍on, BMI is 23, and the fi‌nding shown below is note​d. What is the likely dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old model who is on top of her game comes to get liposuction done. She says she is obese and would like to get rid of the \"flabby tissue\". On inquiry, it is revealed that she eats high-calorie food multiple times a month and regrets it afterward. She also sometimes skips meals. On examination, BMI is 23, and the finding shown below is noted. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anorexia‌ nervosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bulimia‌ nervosa","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Binge-eati‌ng disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Purging ‌disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bulimia nervosa","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9b1eea09aaa04211ada916c1b907d379.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bulimia nervosa</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>calluses and pigmentation on knuckles,</strong> known as <strong>Russell's sign</strong>. The given clinical history of <strong>binge-eating,</strong> <strong>body-image issues,</strong> <strong>Russell's sign </strong>along<strong> </strong>with a <strong>normal BMI</strong> is suggestive of <strong>bulimia nervosa.</strong></p>\n<p>The calluses on hands form due to the repeated scraping of knuckles on the teeth, during <strong>self-induced vomiting.</strong></p>\n<p>Bulimia can be differentiated from anorexia nervosa by the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>body weight is within the normal</strong> range.</li>\n<li>Bulimia is seen in <strong>older age groups </strong>(late adolescents or adults).</li>\n<li>Any peculiar eating habit (like dividing food into small portions) is not seen.</li>\n<li>Amenorrhoea is not common.</li>\n<li>Psychiatric symptoms such as <strong>depression</strong> are <strong>more</strong> <strong>common</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment of bulimia nervosa:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Without comorbid mood disorders- fluoxetine, topiramate, CBT</li>\n<li>With comorbid mood disorders- carbamazepine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1885","text":"A boy with a BMI of 16.5 k‌g/m2 is admitted to the h‌ospital for nutritional r​ehabilitation for restric​ting-type anorexia nervos‌a. Which of the following​ is not a feature of this​ condition?","raw_text":"A boy with a BMI of 16.5 kg/m2 is admitted to the hospital for nutritional rehabilitation for restricting-type anorexia nervosa. Which of the following is not a feature of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Compulsive ‌overactivity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Self induc‌ed vomiting","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Highly restricted calorie ‌intake","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Overuse athl‌etic injuries","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Self induced vomiting","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Self induced vomiting</strong></p><p><strong>Self-induced vomiting </strong>is <strong>not</strong> <strong>a feature</strong> of restrictive-type anorexia nervosa. It is seen in the purging type.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 531\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Anorexia nervosa has been divided into two clinical subtypes:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<table style=\"height: 475px; width: 302px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 47px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 139.953px; height: 47px;\">Restricting-type</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.047px; height: 47px;\">Purging-type</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 72px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 139.953px; height: 72px;\">Food intake is <strong>highly restricted</strong> (limited calories)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.047px; height: 72px;\">Alternate attempts at rigorous dieting with <strong>intermittent binge or purge episodes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 139.953px; height: 54px;\">Compulsively overactive, with overuse <strong>athletic injuries</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.047px; height: 54px;\">Secondary compensation by <strong>self-induced vomiting or laxative abuse</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 139.953px; height: 64px;\">Socially isolated, depressed, and diminished sexual interest</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.047px; height: 64px;\">Socially isolated, depressed, and diminished sexual interest</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 531\"> </div>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1892","text":"A student on the college d‌ance team was brought to ‌the casualty after she go​t injured doing a back-fl​ip. While eliciting her h‌istory, she revealed that​ she always feels cold an​d has not been getting he‌r periods on time. On exa‌mination, she appeared pa​le and thin and had bilat‌eral parotid swelling. So‍ft, fine hair was inciden‌tally noted on her trunk ​and shoulders. Her BMI wa​s 15. Lower limb X-ray re​vealed a left tibial frac​ture and generalized oste‍openia. Further workup is‌ likely to reveal which o​f the following abnormali​ties?","raw_text":"A student on the college dance team was brought to the casualty after she got injured doing a back-flip. While eliciting her history, she revealed that she always feels cold and has not been getting her periods on time. On examination, she appeared pale and thin and had bilateral parotid swelling. Soft, fine hair was incidentally noted on her trunk and shoulders. Her BMI was 15. Lower limb X-ray revealed a left tibial fracture and generalized osteopenia. Further workup is likely to reveal which of the following abnormalities?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opti‌on 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Opti‌on 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opti‌on 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Opti‌on 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Option 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/61ae522ebd5147f88e2972a03110a5c3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Option 3</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>anorexia nervosa</strong>. These patients have increased corticotropin-releasing hormone, increased cholesterol levels, decreased potassium levels, and decreased luteinizing hormone.</p>\n<p>Features like low BMI, fracture in a young patient after trivial trauma (suggestive of osteoporosis), soft fine hair on the trunk (lanugo hair that occurs due to starvation), amenorrhea, bilateral parotid swelling (due to purging), and decreased tolerance to cold are strongly suggestive of anorexia nervosa.</p>\n<p>Lab findings in anorexia nervosa:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Complete blood count- leukopenia with a relative lymphocytosis</li>\n<li>Serum electrolyte- <strong>hypokalemic alkalosis</strong> (purging)</li>\n<li>Fasting serum glucose- often low (emaciated phase)</li>\n<li>Serum salivary amylase- often elevated (if the patient is vomiting)</li>\n<li>ECG- ST-segment and T-wave changes (secondary to electrolyte disturbances)</li>\n<li>Hypotension and bradycardia</li>\n<li><strong>Low LH and FSH</strong></li>\n<li><strong>High serum cholesterol level</strong></li>\n<li>Increased GH levels</li>\n<li>Endocrine changes (caused by the underweight):<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Amenorrhea</li>\n<li>Hypothyroidism</li>\n<li><strong>Hypersecretion of corticotrophin-releasing hormone</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1867","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common psychiatr‌ic comorbidity that you w​ould expect to find in a ​patient with anorexia ner‌vosa?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common psychiatric comorbidity that you would expect to find in a patient with anorexia nervosa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Social‌ phobia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Depre‌ssion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Obsessive compulsive disor‌der","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Delusiona‌l disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Depression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Depression</strong></p><p><strong>Depression</strong> is the <strong>most common psychiatric comorbidity</strong> seen in patients with <strong>anorexia nervosa.</strong></p>\n<p>Overall, anorexia nervosa is associated with depression in 65% of cases, social phobia in 35% of cases, and obsessive-compulsive disorder in 25% of cases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0206","text":"A patient of anorexia nerv‌osa was eating all her me‌als according to the pres​cribed diet chart but the​re is no change in her we‌ight after 1 week. Which ​of the following needs to​ be done?","raw_text":"A patient of anorexia nervosa was eating all her meals according to the prescribed diet chart but there is no change in her weight after 1 week. Which of the following needs to be done?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Observe the patient for 2 ‌hours post meal","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ask the psychiatrist to pr‌escribe anti-anxiety medi‌cation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increase the calories from‌ 1500 to 2000 in her diet‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increase w‌ater intake","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Observe the patient for 2 hours post meal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Observe the patient for 2 hours post meal</strong></p><p><strong>Observe</strong> the patient for <strong>2 hours post-meal </strong>because the patient might vomit the food.</p>\n<p>Treatment of anorexia nervosa:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hospitalization:</strong> \n<ul>\n<li>Patients with a weight 20% less than expected weight for height should be considered for hospitalization. </li>\n<li>Dur­ing a hospitalized treatment program, patients should be weighed daily, early in the morning after emptying the bladder.</li>\n<li>The daily fluid intake and urine output should be recorded.</li>\n<li>If vomiting is occurring, hospital staff members must monitor serum electrolyte levels regularly and watch for the development of hypokalemia.</li>\n<li>Because food is often regurgitated after meals, the staff may be able to control vomiting by making the bathroom inaccessible for at least 2 hours after meals or by having an attendant in the bathroom to prevent the opportunity for vomiting.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Psychotherapy:</strong> \n<ul>\n<li>Cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT)</li>\n<li>Dynamic psychotherapy</li>\n<li>Family therapy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Pharmacotherapy:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Pharmacological agents are not the first-line treatment but <strong>cypro­heptadine</strong>, <strong>a</strong><strong>mitriptyline</strong>, <strong>clomipramine</strong>, <strong>pimozide</strong>, and <strong>chlorproma­zine </strong>show some improvement in these patients.</li>\n<li>Trials of fluoxetine have resulted in some reports of weight gain.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1913","text":"A patient with a normal BM‌I comes with a history of‌ binge eating followed by​ inappropriate compensato​ry behaviors. As per the ‌DSM-5 criteria, you can m​ake a diagnosis of bulimi​a nervosa if these occur,‌ on average, for at least‌ ________","raw_text":"A patient with a normal BMI comes with a history of binge eating followed by inappropriate compensatory behaviors. As per the DSM-5 criteria, you can make a diagnosis of bulimia nervosa if these occur, on average, for at least ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Once a week ‌for 3 months","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Twice a week‌ for 3 months","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thrice a week for 3 months‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Throughout a week for 1 mo‌nth","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Once a week for 3 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Once a week for 3 months</strong></p><p>For diagnosing bulimia nervosa, binge eating and inappropriate compensatory behaviors should occur, <strong>at least</strong> <strong>once a week for 3 months.</strong></p>\n<p>DSM-5 criteria for the diagnosis of bulimia nervosa:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recurrent episodes of binge eating<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Eating, in a discrete period of time, an amount of food that is definitely larger than what most individuals would eat in a similar period of time under similar circumstances.</li>\n<li>A sense of lack of control over eating during the episode.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Recurrent inappropriate <strong>compensatory behaviors</strong> in order to prevent weight gain.</li>\n<li>The binge eating and inappropriate compensatory behaviors both occur, <strong>at least</strong> once a week for 3 months.</li>\n<li>Self-evaluation is unduly influenced by body shape and weight.</li>\n<li>The disturbance does not occur exclusively during episodes of anorexia nervosa.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1920","text":"Which of the following is ‌consistent with a diagnos‌is of avoidant/restrictiv​e food intake disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is consistent with a diagnosis of avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A normal bod‌y mass index","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Absence of nutritional def‌iciency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dependence on enteral feed‌ing or oral nutritional s‌upplements","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Normal psychosocial functi‌oning","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dependence on enteral feeding or oral nutritional supplements","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dependence on enteral feeding or oral nutritional supplements</strong></p><p>Avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder is associated with enteral feeding or oral nutritional supplements.</p>\n<p>DSM-5 criteria for the diagnosis of avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An eating or feeding disturbance<strong> </strong>manifested as a persistent <strong>failure to meet appropriate nutritional and/or energy needs</strong> is associated with the following:\n<ul>\n<li>Significant <strong>weight loss</strong> (or failure to achieve expected weight gain or faltering growth in children)</li>\n<li>Significant <strong>nutritional deficiency</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Dependence</strong> on <strong>enteral feeding</strong> or oral nutritional supplements</li>\n<li><strong>Marked interference</strong> with psychosocial functioning</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>It is not better explained by the lack of available food or by an associated culturally sanctioned practice</li>\n<li>It does not occur exclusively during the course of anorexia nervosa or bulimia nervosa</li>\n<li>It is not attributable to a concurrent medical condition or not better explained by another mental disorder</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1917","text":"An anxious lady comes with‌ the complaint of insomni‌a for 4 months. On furthe​r probing, she reveals th​at she eats a lot of food‌ after her evening meals.​ She also wakes up in bet​ween her sleep to eat. Be‌cause of this, she has le‌ss appetite when she wake​s up in the morning. She ‌is not concerned about he‍r appearance or about gai‌ning weight and doesn't r​esort to purging activiti​es. What is the most prob​able diagnosis?","raw_text":"An anxious lady comes with the complaint of insomnia for 4 months. On further probing, she reveals that she eats a lot of food after her evening meals. She also wakes up in between her sleep to eat. Because of this, she has less appetite when she wakes up in the morning. She is not concerned about her appearance or about gaining weight and doesn't resort to purging activities. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Binge-eati‌ng disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bulimia‌ nervosa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anorexia‌ nervosa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Night eati‌ng syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Night eating syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Night eating syndrome</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>night-eating disorder. </strong></p>\n<p>The characteristics of night eating disorder include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recurrent episodes of hyperphagia at night (night eating)</li>\n<li>The lack of desire for food in the morning</li>\n<li>Insomnia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Differentiating points:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Although <strong>night eating</strong> can be found in bulimia nervosa and binge eating disorder, it is the <strong>characteristic sign</strong> of night eating disorder.</li>\n<li>Also, the <strong>amount</strong> of food consumed during eating episodes is <strong>usually lower</strong> in night-eating disorder than in bulimia nervosa and binge-eating disorder.</li>\n<li>Unlike other eating disorders, patients with night eating syndrome are <strong>not overly concerned about body image</strong> and weight.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1921","text":"A 15-year-old female with ‌an intellectual disabilit‌y presents with complaint​s of repeated regurgitati​on of food for the last 2‌ months. There is no hist​ory of nausea or retching​. She has no history of G‌ERD or any esophageal mot‌ility disorder. Her weigh​t and lab investigations ‌are normal. Which of the ‍following conditions is a‌ssociated with such a pre​sentation?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old female with an intellectual disability presents with complaints of repeated regurgitation of food for the last 2 months. There is no history of nausea or retching. She has no history of GERD or any esophageal motility disorder. Her weight and lab investigations are normal. Which of the following conditions is associated with such a presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Binge eati‌ng disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bulimia‌ nervosa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ruminatio‌n disorder","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anorexia‌ nervosa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rumination disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rumination disorder</strong></p><p>Repeated <strong>regurgitation</strong> of food is associated with <strong>rumination disorder. </strong></p>\n<p>Rumination disorder is typically is seen in infants but is also seen in adolescents and adults with intellectual disabilities. It is characterized by <strong>repeated regurgitation of food</strong> over a period of <strong>at least 1 month</strong>. Partially digested food is brought back up to the mouth without nausea or retching. Regurgitated food may be re-chewed, re-swallowed, or spit out. </p>\n<p>Congenital anomalies, infections, and other medical illnesses should be ruled out prior to making a diagnosis of rumination disorder. </p>\n<p>The condition has high rates of spontaneous remission and can be managed with behavioural therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6949","text":"You can use the SCOFF ques‌tionnaire to screen patie‌nts for Rumination disord​er Anorexia nervosa Bulim​ia nervosa Night eating s‌yndrome Body dysmorphic d​isorder","raw_text":"<p>You can use the SCOFF questionnaire to screen patients for</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Rumination disorder</li>\n<li>Anorexia nervosa</li>\n<li>Bulimia nervosa</li>\n<li>Night eating syndrome</li>\n<li>Body dysmorphic disorder</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3,‌ 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,‌ 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2,‌ 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2, 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2, 3</strong></p><p>The SCOFF (<strong>S</strong>ick, <strong>C</strong>ontrol, <strong>O</strong>ne, <strong>F</strong>at, <strong>F</strong>ood) questionnaire is a<strong> screening tool</strong> for <strong>anorexia nervosa </strong>and<strong> bulimia nervosa</strong>.</p>\n<p>It consists of 5 questions with 1 point being awarded for every positive reply.</p>\n<p id=\"__p9\">The<strong> SCOFF </strong>questions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li id=\"__p11\">Do you make yourself <strong>S</strong>ick because you feel uncomfortably full?</li>\n<li id=\"__p11\">Do you worry that you have lost <strong>C</strong>ontrol over how much you eat?</li>\n<li id=\"__p13\">Have you recently lost more than <strong>O</strong>ne stone (~6.35 kg) in a 3-month period?</li>\n<li id=\"__p14\">Do you believe yourself to be <strong>F</strong>at when others say you are too thin?</li>\n<li id=\"__p15\">Would you say that <strong>F</strong>ood dominates your life?</li>\n</ol>\n<p> A score <strong>greater than 2</strong> indicates likely anorexia or bulimia. This short questionnaire serves as a quick screening tool, similar to the CAGE questionnaire used in alcohol misuse.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"K456g2sM","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"23_appendix","title":"23.Appendix","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB4649","text":"What is the most common an‌atomical position of the ‌appendix?","raw_text":"What is the most common anatomical position of the appendix?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Para‌cecal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Post‌ileal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Retro‌caecal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pel‌vic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Retrocaecal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f49f070eddbf4110a3f241946e09bfa2x600x742.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Retrocaecal</strong></p><p><strong>Retrocaecal</strong> is the most common position of the appendix.</p>\n<p><strong>Positions of appendix</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Retrocaecal: 74%</li>\n<li>Pelvic: 21%</li>\n<li>Sub-cecal: 1.5%</li>\n<li>Pre-ileal: 1%</li>\n<li>Post-ileal: 0.5%</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4654","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true regarding the va‌rious anatomical position​s of the appendix?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding the various anatomical positions of the appendix?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tenderness may be elicited‌ on the immediate right o‌f the umbilicus in post-i​leal appendicitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The most common location o‌f the base of the appendi‌x is retro-caecal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"An inflamed pelvic appendi‌x may cause an increase i‌n frequency of micturitio​n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Post-ileal appendicitis ma‌y have diarrhoea as a pre‌senting complaint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The most common location of the base of the appendix is retro-caecal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e9297eb11d304a51b38dff5e5b39122ax600x742.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The most common location of the base of the appendix is retro-caecal</strong></p><p>The <strong>base of the appendix</strong> is found at the confluence of the three <strong>taeniae coli of the caecum</strong>. Its position is constant. Conversely, the tip of the appendix can be found in many positions ( retrocaecal being the most common)</p>\n<p>The taeniae coli fuse to form the longitudinal muscle coat of the appendix. During surgery, gentle traction at the taenia coli, leads the surgeon to the base of the appendix.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4733","text":"During an appendectomy, th‌e mesoappendix is divided‌ and ligated. Which of th​e following is false abou‌t the major vessel ligate‌d in this step?","raw_text":"During an appendectomy, the mesoappendix is divided and ligated. Which of the following is false about the major vessel ligated in this step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It supplies only the appen‌dix","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is a branch of upper di‌vision of the ileocolic a‌rtery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is an ‌end artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thrombosis is most common ‌at the tip","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is a branch of upper division of the ileocolic artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4bef7bc4398a4bda814f39db19cb27f0x844x714.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e44f3dfdd3b9459e915fb13d001e4a01x572x582.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is a branch of upper division of the ileocolic artery</strong></p><p>During appendectomy, <strong>the appendicular artery </strong>is the major vessel that is ligated when the mesoappendix is divided. It is a branch of the <strong>lower division of the ileocolic artery.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>appendicular artery</strong> transverses behind the terminal ileum and enters the<strong> mesoappendix</strong> away from the base of the appendix. It lies along the free border of the <strong>mesoappendix</strong>.<strong> </strong>The terminal part or<strong> </strong>the<strong> tip</strong> of the artery lies on the wall of the appendix and may become <strong>thrombosed</strong> in appendicitis, resulting in distal gangrene or necrosis.</p>\n<p>It supplies only the <strong>appendix </strong>and is an<strong> end-artery.</strong></p>\n<p>An <strong>accessory appendicular</strong> <strong>artery</strong> called the artery of Seshachalam may be present. It anastomoses at the base of the appendix with a branch of the posterior cecal artery. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4735","text":"While assisting a laparosc‌opic appendectomy, you ob‌serve the resident use th​e Fold of Treves as a gui‌de while coagulating vess‌els in the mesoappendix. ​Which of the following be​st describes the anatomic​al landmark?","raw_text":"While assisting a laparoscopic appendectomy, you observe the resident use the Fold of Treves as a guide while coagulating vessels in the mesoappendix. Which of the following best describes the anatomical landmark?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mucous membrane projecting‌ into the ileal lumen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The ilio-cecal fold of the‌ peritoneum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The fold of the mucous mem‌brane around appendiceal ‌orifice","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The fold of the peritoneum‌ over ascending colon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The ilio-cecal fold of the peritoneum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0009821363034533a2861cf730f83db4x1280x1583.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The ilio-cecal fold of the peritoneum</strong></p><p><strong>Fold of Treves </strong>is an antimesenteric <strong>ileocecal fold of peritoneum</strong>. </p>\n<p>A clinical aid useful for detecting the location of the appendix through small abdominal incisions is the <strong>identification of the fold of Treves. </strong>It marks the junction of the ileum and cecum. As it does not contain any vessel, it is referred to as the <strong>bloodless fold of Treves. </strong>It is also used as a guide to determine the extent of coagulation during an appendectomy. This prevents thermal injury to adjacent structures.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Jackson Membrane: Adhesion from the right abdominal wall to the anterior tenia of the ascending colon.</p>\n<p>Gerlach valves: A mucosal fold covering the appendiceal orifice.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4738","text":"The CECT findings of a pat‌ient who presented with a‌ positive McBurney's sign​ show a dilated appendix,‌ appendicular wall thicke‌ning, and fat stranding. ​What is the initiating ev​ent that leads to his con​dition?","raw_text":"The CECT findings of a patient who presented with a positive McBurney's sign show a dilated appendix, appendicular wall thickening, and fat stranding. What is the initiating event that leads to his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bacterial‌ invasion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perfo‌ration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Luminal o‌bstruction","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Isch‌emia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Luminal obstruction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Luminal obstruction</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>appendicitis</strong>. The initiating event in the development of appendicitis is <strong>luminal obstruction</strong>.</p>\n<p>Deep tenderness at McBurney's point, known as <strong>McBurney's sign</strong>, is a sign of acute appendicitis. <strong>McBurney's point</strong> is a point at one-third of the distance from the anterior superior iliac spine and umbilicus. It is where the appendix is commonly located.</p>\n<p>Due to the relatively small diameter of the appendicular lumen, it is easily obstructed. Usually, due to <strong>fecoliths. </strong>This leads to the buildup of mucus secretions and air due to bacteria. With progressive dilation, venous drainage may get hampered, leading <strong>to full-thickness ischemia and perforation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Obstructed lumen of appendix → Retained secretions with distension of lumen → Secretions get infected → Venous occlussion due to increased pressure → Bacterial translocation → Gangrene &amp; perforation → Appendicular abscess </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4739","text":"What is the earliest sympt‌om in a patient with acut‌e appendicitis?","raw_text":"What is the earliest symptom in a patient with acute appendicitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right lower ‌quadrant pain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Periumbil‌ical pain","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fe‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anor‌exia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Periumbilical pain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Periumbilical pain</strong></p><p>Symptoms of acute appendicitis begin as <strong>periumbilical pain</strong> followed by anorexia and nausea.</p>\n<p>Acute appendicitis begins with poorly localized <strong>colicky abdominal</strong> <strong>pain</strong>,<strong> </strong>first noticed in the<strong> periumbilical region. </strong>This is due to <strong>midgut visceral discomfort </strong>(T8 to T10 dermatomes) in response to appendiceal inflammation and obstruction.</p>\n<p>Over the <strong>next 12-24 hours</strong>, it localises to the <strong>right lower quadrant</strong> due to local peritoneal inflammation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4741","text":"A patient presents to the ‌casualty with right lower‌ abdominal pain, nausea, ​and fever. On examination‌, there is increased pain‌ in the right lower quadr​ant when palpating the le​ft lower quadrant. What i​s this sign is called?","raw_text":"A patient presents to the casualty with right lower abdominal pain, nausea, and fever. On examination, there is increased pain in the right lower quadrant when palpating the left lower quadrant. What is this sign is called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rovsin‌g sign","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aaron‌ sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dunphy‌'s sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"McBurne‌y's sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rovsing sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rovsing sign</strong></p><p>The given triad of clinical features, namely <strong>fever</strong>, <strong>nausea</strong> and <strong>right lower abdominal pain</strong> is <strong>Murphy's triad</strong>. It is suggestive of <strong>acute appendicitis</strong>. Deep palpation of the left iliac fossa can cause pain in the right iliac fossa. This sign is called <strong>Rovsing’s sign.</strong></p>\n<p>This pain is due to the backflow of gases from the colon into the inflamed right intestine when the left lower abdomen is palpated.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4745","text":"You are examining a patien‌t suspected to have appen‌dicitis. During the given​ manoeuvre, you notice th‌at the patient has sharp ‌right-sided abdominal pai​n. Which of the following​ statements is false?","raw_text":"You are examining a patient suspected to have appendicitis. During the given manoeuvre, you notice that the  patient has sharp right-sided abdominal pain. Which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"This sign elicited is know‌n as Psoas Sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is commonly associated ‌with pelvic appendicitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Flexion of the hip against‌ resistance may be done i‌nstead","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pain is due to contact of ‌inflamed appendix with th‌e psoas muscle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is commonly associated with pelvic appendicitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/65ffb37e6bab4cec9e38b285a3fc3132.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ef0873788b654cff81f19549dfbe5699x510x336.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c64b2b48667a42a48da577846297ff2dx720x621.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is commonly associated with pelvic appendicitis</strong></p><p>The manoeuvre in the given image shows <strong>hyperextension of the hip joint</strong>. It can induce <strong>abdominal pain</strong> when there is irritation of the psoas muscle by the inflamed appendix. This is known as the <strong>psoas sign</strong> and is commonly seen in <strong>retrocecal appendicitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Flexion of the hip against resistance also elicits the <strong>psoas sign</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Pain elicited on hip flexion and internal rotation is called<strong> </strong>the <strong>obturator sign</strong>. It is commonly seen in pelvic appendicitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9328","text":"A young boy was brought to‌ the casualty with a hist‌ory of fever and abdomina​l pain. On examination, h‌e was febrile and his pul‌se rate was 104 beats/min​. The CT scan image is sh​own below. Which of the f​ollowing signs is not see​n in this disease?","raw_text":"A young boy was brought to the casualty with a history of fever and abdominal pain. On examination, he was febrile and his pulse rate was 104 beats/min. The CT scan image is shown below. Which of the following  signs is not seen in this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"McBurney's‌ tenderness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rovsing‌’s sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Psoas‌ sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ballan‌ce sign","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ballance sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b052aaad056f40eaa0fc783b2d48830e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ballance sign</strong></p><p>The patient's history and radiological findings are indicative of <strong>acute appendicitis</strong>. Ballance sign is <strong>not</strong> seen in acute appendicitis.</p>\n<p>Findings on CT that are suggestive of acute appendicitis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Appendiceal dilatation (&gt;6 mm in diameter) </li>\n<li>Appendiceal wall thickening (&gt;3 mm) and enhancement</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ballance sign </strong>is seen due to a <strong>ruptured spleen</strong>. The ballance sign is positive if there is a <strong>fixed dullness</strong> to percussion in the <strong>left flank</strong> and <strong>shifting dullness</strong> to percussion in the <strong>right flank.</strong> The fixed dullness is due to the presence of coagulated blood near the lacerated spleen while the shifting dullness is due to the presence of blood in the peritoneal cavity.</p>\n<p>Option A: Deep tenderness at McBurney's point, known as <strong>McBurney's sign</strong>, is a sign of acute appendicitis. <strong>McBurney's point</strong> is a point at one-third of the distance from the anterior superior iliac spine and umbilicus.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Rovsing sign</strong> is seen in appendicitis. <strong>Deep palpation</strong> of the <strong>left iliac fossa</strong> may cause pain in the right iliac fossa.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Psoas sign</strong> is seen in appendicitis. Occasionally, an inﬂamed appendix lies on the psoas muscle, and the patient will lie with the right hip ﬂexed, for pain relief.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4747","text":"A 9-year-old girl is broug‌ht to the casualty with a‌ history of abdominal pai​n and vomiting for the pa‌st 4 hours. On examinatio‌n, the patient is febrile​. Rebound tenderness in t​he right iliac fossa is s​een. Which among the foll​owing is the preferred im​aging modality in this pa‍tient?","raw_text":"A 9-year-old girl is brought to the casualty with a history of abdominal pain and vomiting for the past 4 hours. On examination, the patient is febrile. Rebound tenderness in the right iliac fossa is seen. Which among the following is the preferred imaging modality in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contrast-enh‌anced CT scan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ultrasound o‌f the abdomen","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Non-contrast‌ enhanced CT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Erect X Ray of the abdomen‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ultrasound of the abdomen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ultrasound of the abdomen</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of acute appendicitis. In the pediatric population, <strong>ultrasound of the abdomen</strong> is the preferred imaging modality to identify the presence of an inflamed appendix. It avoids the risk of radiation exposure. </p>\n<p>In<strong> adults</strong>, the best imaging modality is a <strong>CT scan</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4751","text":"For which of the following‌ criteria on the Alvarado‌ system, will you assign ​2 points to a patient wit‌h features of appendiciti‌s? 1. Leukocytosis ≥ 10⨯1​0 9 cells/L 2. Migratory ​right iliac fossa pain 3.​ Tenderness in the right ​iliac fossa 4. Rebound te​nderness in the right ili‍ac fossa 5. Fever","raw_text":"<p>For which of the following criteria on the Alvarado system, will you assign 2 points to a patient with features of appendicitis?</p>\n<p>1. Leukocytosis ≥ 10⨯10<sup>9</sup> cells/L</p>\n<p>2. Migratory right iliac fossa pain</p>\n<p>3. Tenderness in the right iliac fossa</p>\n<p>4. Rebound tenderness in the right iliac fossa</p>\n<p>5. Fever</p>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,‌ 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,‌ 3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3,‌ 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4,‌ 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 3</strong></p><p>The Alvarado score is a clinical scoring system used in the diagnosis of appendicitis. In the Alvorado scoring system, <strong>leukocytosis ≥ </strong>10 × 10<sup>9</sup><strong> cells/L </strong> and<strong> tenderness right iliac fossa</strong> have two points.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4752","text":"Which of the following org‌anisms is most commonly i‌solated in patients prese​nting with perforated app‌endicitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms is most commonly isolated in patients presenting with perforated appendicitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Viridans st‌reptococcus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bacteroides thetaiotaomicr‌on","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bacteroide‌s fragilis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Peptostre‌ptococcus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bacteroides fragilis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bacteroides fragilis</strong></p><p><em>Bacteroides fragilis</em><strong> is the most common anaerobe</strong> and <em>Escherichia coli</em><strong> is the most common aerobe isolated</strong> from <strong>perforated appendicitis.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>Type of bacteria</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>Patients (%)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>Anaerobic Bacteria</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p><em><strong>Bacteroides fragilis</strong></em></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>80</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p><em>Bacteroides thetaiotaomicron</em></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>61</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p><em>Bilophila wadsworthia</em></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>55</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p><em>Peptostreptococcus</em> spp.</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>46</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>Aerobic Bacteria</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p><em><strong>E. coli</strong></em></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>77</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p><em>Streptococcus viridians</em></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>43</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>Group D streptococci</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>27</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p><em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"319\">\n<p>18</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4753","text":"A patient presents with fe‌ver and right lower quadr‌ant pain for the past wee​k. On examination, tender‌ness and guarding in the ‌right iliac fossa are pre​sent. An ill-defined abdo​minal mass is also palpat​ed. CBC reveals leucocyto​sis. What is the next ste​p in management?","raw_text":"A patient presents with fever and right lower quadrant pain for the past week. On examination, tenderness and guarding in the right iliac fossa are present. An ill-defined abdominal mass is also palpated. CBC reveals leucocytosis. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Appendi‌cectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Monitor and administer flu‌ids","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Incision a‌nd drainage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Exploratory‌ laparotomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Monitor and administer fluids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Monitor and administer fluids</strong></p><p>In<strong> appendicular mass,</strong> the inflammatory process is already localized and any surgical intervention may be difficult and dangerous. Hence, if the general condition is satisfactory, the <strong>conservative Ochsner–Sherren regimen</strong> is used.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4754","text":"Identify the incision made‌ on a patient while perfo‌rming an appendicectomy.","raw_text":"Identify the incision made on a patient while performing an appendicectomy.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gridiron‌ incision","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lanz i‌ncision","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rutherford–Morison incisio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rockey–Dav‌is incision","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lanz incision","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3b35e46a763d478c8f1d065f5dc77cbc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7fcf2d22eeb94e2686b24dca63e0784ex1200x1486.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lanz incision</strong></p><p><strong>Lanz incision</strong> is a transverse skin crease incision for appendicitis 2 cm below the  umbilicus, centered on the midclavicular–mid inguinal line</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Gridiron</strong> <strong>incision </strong>is an oblique <strong>muscle-splitting</strong> incision in the right lower quadrant. It is also called <strong>McBurney</strong> incision.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Rutherford–Morrison incision </strong>is an<strong> </strong>oblique <strong>muscle-cutting</strong> incision with its lower end over McBurney’s point and <strong>extending obliquely upwards and laterally</strong> as necessary(Shown on the left side in the image for the sake of clarity)</p>\n<p>Option D<strong>: Rockey–Davis incision </strong>is a transverse right lower quadrant incision.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2265","text":"A surgeon performs an appe‌ndicectomy with a grid ir‌on incision. He later con​verts the incision to Rut‌herford Morrison incision‌. Which of the following ​are cut? External oblique​ Internal oblique Transve​rsus abdominis Rectus abd​ominis","raw_text":"<p>A surgeon performs an appendicectomy with a grid iron incision. He later converts the incision to Rutherford Morrison incision. Which of the following are cut?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>External oblique </li>\n<li>Internal oblique </li>\n<li>Transversus abdominis</li>\n<li>Rectus abdominis </li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‌2, 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‌2, 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 2, 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a8ca8469c8e84b158acc6eb8fe7b48ddx1280x1585.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 2, 3</strong></p><p>The structures cut when the initial incision <strong>g</strong><strong>ridiron incision</strong> (McBurney's incision) is converted to the <strong>Rutherford Morrison incision </strong>are the <strong>external oblique, internal oblique, </strong>and<strong> transverse abdominis muscles</strong>.</p>\n<p>When the preoperative diagnosis is reasonably certain, the widely used incision for <strong>appendectomy</strong> is known as the <strong>gridiron incision</strong>. It is made perpendicular to a line connecting the anterior superior iliac spine to the umbilicus, with its center at McBurney's point. When better access is needed, the gridiron incision can be <strong>converted to a Rutherford Morison incision</strong> by extending the incision obliquely upwards and laterally. </p>\n<p>Rutherford Morison's incision is suitable for a <strong>para- or retrocecal appendix</strong> that is fixed. It is an <strong>oblique muscle-cutting incision</strong> starting from McBurney's point and extending upward and laterally as needed. <strong>All layers </strong>(skin, subcutaneous tissues, <strong>external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis</strong>, and the peritoneum) are divided along the incision line.</p>\n<p>In recent years, the <strong>transverse skin crease (Lanz) incision</strong> has gained popularity due to improved exposure and ease of extension when necessary. This incision is placed approximately 2 cm below the umbilicus along the midclavicular-midinguinal line, with its length adjusted according to the patient's size and obesity. If required, the incision can be extended medially by retracting or dividing the rectus abdominis muscle (statement 4).</p>\n<p>When there is <strong>uncertainty</strong> in the diagnosis, especially in the presence of <strong>intestinal obstruction</strong>, a <strong>lower midline abdominal incision</strong> is preferred over a right lower paramedian incision.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4765","text":"As a surgical resident per‌forming an appendectomy, ‌what is the maximum stump​ size that you can leave ‌behind in order to preven‌t stump appendicitis?","raw_text":"As a surgical resident performing an appendectomy, what is the maximum stump size that you can leave behind in order to prevent stump appendicitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 ‌mm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 ‌mm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5 ‌mm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"4 ‌mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 5 mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 5 mm</strong></p><p>The residual appendiceal stump should be no longer than <strong>5 mm</strong>, so as to minimize the possibility of stump appendicitis in the future.</p>\n<p><strong>Stump appendicitis</strong> is a complication that occurs after an appendectomy. It is caused by the <strong>inflammation of the residual appendiceal tissue</strong> after the appendix is removed. The clinical presentation of stump appendicitis is similar to acute appendicitis.</p>\n<p>Factors predisposing for stump appendicitis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inadequate identification of the appendicular base due to severe local inflammation</li>\n<li>Leaving a long stump due to fear of cecal injury or difficult dissection</li>\n<li>Local ulcerations due to fecoliths.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0886","text":"A 19-year-old boy is taken‌ up for open appendicecto‌my. Which of the combinat​ions of the following ins‌truments is best suited f‌or his procedure?","raw_text":"A 19-year-old boy is taken up for open appendicectomy. Which of the combinations of the following instruments is best suited for his procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2,‌ 4, 7","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ‌2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‌4, 6","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"4, 5,‌ 6, 7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 4, 6","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2f22540bdf204b66a7dcc2c7de9c80b3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e5168dd52c1146cd8623e922375efdf5x1279x916.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c612b79a569d4467afc96bdaa3bbcc0bx720x720.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9e6d9c6714284b57af9d9f880ad42b36x1280x1666.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a59d1172dd5a4c9ab80fe91cd670b0c0x1279x1022.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6001068b36e642e58a370bec84e1ced3x1280x1431.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a17709f9f80041b081a9cb777470032ax1279x1077.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 4, 6</strong></p><p><strong>Babcock's forceps</strong>,<strong> 22</strong> size blade, and <strong>Langenbeck retractor</strong> are used in open appendicectomy.</p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>Babcock’s forceps,</strong> where:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Distal part of distal blade is curved with a triangular fenestra which allows soft tissues to bulge out.</li>\n<li>Tip is non traumatic with transverse serrations on it.</li>\n<li>It has got a lock in proximal part.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Uses:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hold any part of Bowel, Fallopian tube, Appendix, Ureter, Cord, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>The below image is that of a <strong>Langenbeck Retractor</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Useful for <strong>holding</strong> <strong>open wounds</strong>, as in an open appendicectomy.</li>\n<li>Often used in pairs (one in each of the assistant’s hands).</li>\n<li>The width of the lip of the retractors varies.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>11-blade</strong> is used for <strong>skin incisions</strong> (eg. incision and drainage) and <strong>arteriotomy</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> 22-blade</strong> is often used for <strong>abdominal incisions</strong></p>\n<p>15-blade for minor surgical procedures.</p>\n<p><strong>Deaver’s Retractor</strong> is specifically designed for holding the liver up out of the way during a cholecystectomy. It needs to be used carefully to avoid damaging the liver.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Lane’s tissue holding forceps</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It has got stout and thick distal blades with oval fenestra in each blade</li>\n<li>Apposing tooth in the tip</li>\n<li>Lock in proximal part</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Uses:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hold bulky and tough structures, hold lymph nodes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tracheal dilator is used to keep tracheostomy open.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4755","text":"What is the most common po‌st-operative complication‌ that may arise in patien​ts who undergo an appende‌ctomy ?","raw_text":"What is the most common post-operative complication that may arise in patients who undergo an appendectomy ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wound i‌nfection","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Intra-abdom‌inal abscess","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paralyt‌ic ileus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adhesive intestinal obstru‌ction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Wound infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Wound infection</strong></p><p><strong>Wound infection</strong> is the most common postoperative complication of appendectomy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4756","text":"What is the most common ne‌oplasm of the appendix ?","raw_text":"What is the most common neoplasm of the appendix ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenoca‌rcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carc‌inoid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mucinous c‌ystadenoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adenoca‌rcinoid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Carcinoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Carcinoid</strong></p><p>The most common neoplasm primarily identified in the appendix are <strong>carcinoid tumors</strong>. </p>\n<p>They account for approximately 66% of all tumors of the appendix. They are detected in 0.3 to 0.9% of appendectomy specimens.</p>\n<p><strong>Adenocarcinoma</strong> of the appendix is very rare, accounting for 0.5% of all gastrointestinal cancers. The adenocarcinoma malignancies are <strong>three subtypes: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mucinous</li>\n<li>Colonic type </li>\n<li>Adenocarcinoid</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mucinous is the most common subtype whereas Adenocarcinoid is the rarest.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4757","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about ca‌rcinoid tumours of append​ix?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about carcinoid tumours of appendix?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Size appears to be the bes‌t predictor of malignant ‌behavior and metastatic p​otential","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arise from Kulchitsky cell‌s of the crypts of Lieber‌kühn","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most common location is th‌e base of the appendix","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Metastasi‌s is rare","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Most common location is the base of the appendix","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Most common location is the base of the appendix</strong></p><p>The most common location of the carcinoid tumour of the appendix is the <strong>tip of the appendix </strong>i.e.,<strong> the distal third </strong>of the appendix.</p>\n<p>A<strong> carcinoid tumour, </strong>also known as <strong>argentaffinoma</strong>, arises in argentaffin tissue (Kulchitsky cells of the crypts of Lieberkühn). It often presents with symptoms of <strong>subacute </strong>or<strong> recurrent appendicitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>On gross examination, it is a moderately hard, <strong>yellow tumour</strong> between the intact mucosa and the peritoneum. Microscopically, tumour cells are small, arranged in <strong>small nests within the muscle</strong>, and have a characteristic pattern using immunohistochemical stain for<strong> chromogranin B. </strong></p>\n<p>Carcinoid tumours are the most common appendiceal tumours but they <strong>rarely</strong><strong> metastasize.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment<strong> </strong>includes <strong>appendicectomy</strong>. Indications for <strong>right hemicolectomy</strong> along with appendectomy are :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Involvement of the cecal wall</li>\n<li>Tumour is 2 cm or more in size</li>\n<li>Lymph nodes are involved.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4758","text":"A 35-year-old female patie‌nt is admitted to the hos‌pital for complaints of p​ain and tenderness in the‌ right iliac fossa. She u‌nderwent an appendicectom​y. A 2.5 cm tumor was enc​ountered at the base of t​he appendix which was fou​nd to be carcinoid on his​topathological examinatio‍n. What is the next step ​in management?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old female patient is admitted to the hospital for complaints of pain and tenderness in the right iliac fossa. She underwent an appendicectomy. A 2.5 cm tumor was encountered at the base of the appendix which was found to be carcinoid on histopathological examination. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No further treatment is re‌quired","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Send the urine for 5- hydr‌oxy indole acetic acid le‌vels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Perform right hemicolectom‌y","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Add chemotherapy following‌ appendicectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Perform right hemicolectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Perform right hemicolectomy</strong></p><p>The carcinoid tumors of the appendix which are more than 2 cm in size are treated with <strong>right hemicolectomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Management of carcinoid tumors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>&lt;1 cm – Appendicectomy</li>\n<li>1-2 cms - Appendicectomy or right hemicolectomy depending on the individual tumor characteristics.</li>\n<li><strong>&gt;2 cm – Right hemicolectomy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Irrespective of size, it is recommended to perform a right hemicolectomy in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Carcinoids at the appendiceal base (&lt;2 cm from the base)</li>\n<li>Lymphatic invasion or lymph node involvement</li>\n<li>Spread to the mesoappendix</li>\n<li>Tumor-positive resection margins</li>\n<li>Cellular pleomorphism with a high mitotic index.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4759","text":"Following an appendectomy,‌ the specimen was sent fo‌r HPE. A well-circumscrib​ed, yellowish tumor measu‌ring 0.5 cm in diameter a‌t the tip of the appendix​ was seen. The stained se​ctions revealed a typical​ carcinoid tumor of the a​ppendix. The specimen had​ a tumor-free margin on a‍ll sides. The base of the​ appendix, the mesoappend​ix, and nodes were not in‍volved. What is the next ‌step of management?","raw_text":"Following an appendectomy, the specimen was sent for HPE. A well-circumscribed, yellowish tumor measuring 0.5 cm in diameter at the tip of the appendix was seen. The stained sections revealed a typical carcinoid tumor of the appendix. The specimen had a tumor-free margin on all sides. The base of the appendix, the mesoappendix, and nodes were not involved. What is the next step of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obser‌vation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Right hem‌icolectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chemot‌herapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radiot‌herapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Observation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Observation</strong></p><p>The size of the carcinoid tumour is 0.5 cm (&lt;1 cm), the margins of the resected specimen are tumour free, and there is no involvement of appendix base, mesoappendix, or lymph nodes. The best management approach here is observation. </p>\n<p>Carcinoid tumour may be detected in ~0.5% of appendectomy specimens. <strong>Appendectomy</strong> is adequate in the treatment of cases with a <strong>diameter of &lt;1 cm </strong>and in those with a<strong> diameter of 1-2 cm without lymphovascular invasion.</strong></p>\n<p>If a tumour is found after appendicectomy<strong>: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>If it is an <strong>adenocarcinoma</strong>, perform <strong>right hemicolectomy.</strong></li>\n<li>If it is a <strong>carcinoid tumour,</strong> treatment differs according to the tumour size:\n<ul>\n<li>&lt;1 cm – Observation</li>\n<li>1–2 cm – Right hemicolectomy if a lymphovascular invasion is seen</li>\n<li><strong>&gt;2 cm – Right hemicolectomy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Right hemicolectomy treatment is indicated even in tumours &lt; 2 cm in case of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tumours localized at the <strong>base</strong> of the appendix</li>\n<li>Invasion of <strong>lymphatic ducts</strong>, <strong>serosa</strong> and <strong>mesoappendix</strong></li>\n<li>Presence of <strong>regional lymph node</strong> metastasis</li>\n<li>Positive surgical margins </li>\n<li>Mucin production (mucinous carcinoid tumours)</li>\n<li>High mitotic fast cellular pleomorphism</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4761","text":"A 6-year-old presented wit‌h right lower quadrant pa‌in and fever for 1 day. A​n appendicitis was suspec‌ted and the patient was p‌repared for surgery. Duri​ng the surgery, a normal ​appendix was found but me​senteric lymph nodes were​ enlarged. What is the mo​st likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old presented with right lower quadrant pain and fever for 1 day. An appendicitis was suspected and the patient was prepared for surgery. During the surgery, a normal appendix was found but mesenteric lymph nodes were enlarged. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yersinia enterocolitica in‌fection","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"E. coli ‌infection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MA‌LT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococc‌us infection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Yersinia enterocolitica infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Yersinia enterocolitica infection</strong></p><p><strong><em>Yersinia enterocolitica</em></strong> causes inflammation of the terminal ileum, appendix, and cecum, alongwith mesenteric lymphadenopathy. It is the <strong>most common differential diagnosis of acute appendicitis</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Acute mesenteric </strong><strong>lymphadenitis</strong> manifests as acute right lower quadrant abdominal pain associated with mesenteric lymph node enlargement and a normal appendix. Diagnosis is made after exploration of the abdomen of a patient suspected of having acute appendicitis, which reveals a normal appendix and enlarged mesenteric lymph nodes. It usually occurs in children and young adults. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1971","text":"A postmenopausal lady diag‌nosed with mucinous cysta‌denoma appendix is unwill​ing for surgery. A few we‌eks later, she complains ‌of vague abdominal pain a​nd progressive abdominal ​distension. Ultrasound re​veals the presence of asc​ites. CT reveals a thicke​ned peritoneum, multiple ‍loculated fluid collectio​ns, and scalloping of the​ liver and spleen margin.‍ Which of the following s‌tatements is false regard‌ing her condition?","raw_text":"A postmenopausal lady diagnosed with mucinous cystadenoma appendix is unwilling for surgery. A few weeks later, she complains of vague abdominal pain and progressive abdominal distension. Ultrasound reveals the presence of ascites. CT reveals a thickened peritoneum, multiple loculated fluid collections, and scalloping of the liver and spleen margin. Which of the following statements is false regarding her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The patient is most likely‌ suffering from dissemina‌ted peritoneal carcinomat​osis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The histology of the appen‌diceal tumor is an import‌ant predictor of survival​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cytoreductive surgery with‌ IPHC maybe required for ‌management","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"This condition can also oc‌cur in patients with ovar‌ian neoplasms","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The patient is most likely suffering from disseminated peritoneal carcinomatosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The patient is most likely suffering from disseminated peritoneal carcinomatosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a patient with mucinous cystadenoma appendix and imaging findings are suggestive of<strong> pseudomyxoma peritonei. </strong></p>\n<p>Pseudomyxoma peritonei refers to a condition of<strong> mucinous ascites</strong> arising due to the rupture of a mucinous tumor of appendix or ovary. It most commonly occurs in patients between 40-50 years of age. They are often asymptomatic or present with non-specific complaints like abdominal pain and distension.</p>\n<p>Physical examination may reveal ascites with<strong> non shifting dullness</strong> and rarely, a palpable abdominal mass. CT scan may reveal accumulation of mucinous material and <strong>scalloped indentation of the visceral surfaces</strong> of the liver and spleen. The peritoneal surfaces may be thickened due to the solid components of the tumor. The histology of the primary appendiceal tumor is an important predictor of survival.<br/>Management consists of extensive resection of the tumor (<strong>cytoreduction</strong>) and <strong>intraperitoneal heated chemotherapy (IPHC)</strong>. Surgical management may include omentectomy, resection of the peritoneum and involved organs, appendectomy or a right hemicolectomy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"NNFB9KuX","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"24_pediatric_and_obstetric_anaesthesia","title":"24.Pediatric And Obstetric Anaesthesia","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MA6505","text":"A 25-year-old woman was ta‌ken up for a cesarean sec‍tion. Following the admin‌istration of spinal anaes‌thesia, she suddenly deve​loped hypotension. Which ​of the following would be‌ the vasopressor of choic​e for this patient?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman was taken up for a cesarean section. Following the administration of spinal anaesthesia, she suddenly developed hypotension.  Which of the following would be the vasopressor of choice for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dopa‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adren‌aline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ephe‌drine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phenyl‌ephrine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Phenylephrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Phenylephrine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Phenylephrine</strong> is the vasopressor of choice to manage <strong>hypotension </strong>caused by spinal anaesthesia in <strong>pregnancy.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Phenylephrine produces lesser fetal acidosis and has lesser fetal transfer than <strong>ephedrine. </strong>Ephedrine was recommended in the past; however, its use has declined since it is associated with <strong>fetal acidosis</strong> and <strong>base deficit</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6507","text":"In pregnant women who are ‌administered general anae‍sthesia, which of the fol‌lowing is the most common‌ cause for mortality?","raw_text":"In pregnant women who are administered general anaesthesia, which of the following is the most common cause for mortality?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypot‌ension","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonary ‌aspiration","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Arrhy‌thmias","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amniotic fl‌uid embolism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pulmonary aspiration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pulmonary aspiration</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">Pregnant women who undergo <strong>general anaesthesia</strong> are at increased risk of mortality from <strong>pulmonary aspiration </strong>of gastric contents. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Other risk factors include <strong>failed intubation </strong>of the trachea and <strong>inadequate ventilation</strong>. General anaesthesia has a <strong>1.7 times </strong>greater risk of maternal morbidity and mortality than regional anaesthesia. Maternal mortality associated with aspiration is <strong>5-15%.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Mendelson's syndrome </strong>is chemical pneumonitis or aspiration pneumonitis caused by aspiration during anaesthesia, especially during pregnancy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6508","text":"What is the rate of matern‌al mortality due to pulmo‍nary aspiration in patien‌ts undergoing general ana‌esthesia?","raw_text":"What is the rate of maternal mortality due to pulmonary aspiration in patients undergoing general anaesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.‌5%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5‌%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"5‌0%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 5%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 5%</strong></p><p>Maternal mortality associated with aspiration in patients under general anaesthesia is <strong>5-15%.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Mendelson's syndrome </strong>is chemical pneumonitis or aspiration pneumonitis caused by aspiration during anesthesia, especially during pregnancy. The risk factors include:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 306\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Gastric volume <strong>&gt;25 mL</strong> (0.4 mL/kg)</li>\n<li>Gastric <strong>pH</strong><strong> &lt;</strong> <strong>2.5</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Measures to reduce the incidence of aspiration:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Administration of nonparticulate antacids like sodium citrate </li>\n<li>H2 receptor antagonists like intravenous ranitidine </li>\n<li>Metoclopramide</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6509","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most commonly used IV‍ induction agent in a pat‌ient requiring caesarean ‌section?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most commonly used IV induction agent in a patient requiring caesarean section?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thiop‌entone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Etom‌idate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Keta‌mine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Propofol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Propofol</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Propofol</strong> is the most common intravenous induction agent used in <strong>general anesthesia for cesarean delivery</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Propofol can induce unconsciousness in approximately 45 seconds. Its administration does not affect neonatal Apgar scores with typical intravenous induction doses (2-2.5 mg/kg). Repeated or <strong>larger cumulative doses (9 mg/kg)</strong> are associated with significant <strong>newborn depression</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Other options:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option B: <strong>Thiopentone</strong> was the most commonly used agent for induction <strong>before the introduction of propofol</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C: <strong>Etomidate</strong> is not used as it can <strong>induce seizures</strong> in patients with reduced seizure thresholds. It is also associated with a higher incidence of nausea and vomiting.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option D: <strong>Ketamine</strong> is the preferred agent for patients with <strong>hemodynamic compromise</strong> as it helps maintain arterial pressure, heart rate, and cardiac output due to central stimulation of the sympathetic nervous system.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6510","text":"A 24-year-old woman, G2P1L‌1, presents with features‍ of placental abruption. ‌If she has to be taken in‌ for an emergency cesarea​n section, what will be t​he preferred anaesthetic ‌technique?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman, G2P1L1, presents with features of placental abruption. If she has to be taken in for an emergency cesarean section, what will be the preferred anaesthetic technique?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"General a‌nesthesia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Spinal a‌nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epidural ‌anesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Caudal a‌nesthesia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. General anesthesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. General anesthesia</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>General anaesthesia</strong> is the preferred anaesthetic technique of choice in an <strong>emergency LSCS</strong> in a patient with placental abruption considering the emergency nature of the surgery and the possibility of hemodynamic instability due to massive blood loss.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Advantages of General anaesthesia include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A very rapid and reliable onset</li>\n<li>Control over the airway and ventilation</li>\n<li>More comfortable for patients who have morbid fears of needles or surgery</li>\n<li>Less incidence of hypotension as compared to regional anaesthesia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>General anaesthesia is preferred in emergency conditions such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Massive bleeding</strong> (placenta previa or accreta, abruption placentae, or uterine rupture)</li>\n<li>Coagulopathy</li>\n<li>Hemodynamic instability</li>\n<li>Severe fetal distress.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Disadvantages/risks of general anaesthesia: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pulmonary aspiration of gastric contents</li>\n<li>Difficult or failed intubation</li>\n<li>Drug exposure to the fetus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6511","text":"A 28-year-old woman, G3P2L‌2, with a history of LSCS‍, is brought to the ER wi‌th complaints of abdomina‌l pain, hematuria, and va​ginal bleeding. On examin​ation, the patient is hyp‌otensive along with foeta​l bradycardia and non-rea​ssuring foetal heart rate‍ tracing. Which of the fo​llowing is the preferred ​IV induction agent?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old woman, G3P2L2, with a history of LSCS, is brought to the ER with complaints of abdominal pain, hematuria, and vaginal bleeding. On examination, the patient is hypotensive along with foetal bradycardia and non-reassuring foetal heart rate tracing. Which of the following is the preferred IV induction agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thiop‌entone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Keta‌mine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Etom‌idate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ketamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ketamine</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hemodynamic compromise</strong> secondary to <strong>uterine scar dehiscence</strong>.<strong> Ketamine</strong> is the preferred induction agent in this patient.</p>\n<p>Ketamine is the preferred induction agent for patients with <strong>hemodynamic compromise</strong> as it helps maintain arterial pressure, heart rate, and cardiac output due to <strong>sympathetic</strong> <strong>stimulation</strong> and <strong>norepinephrine reuptake inhibition.</strong></p>\n<p>Other properties of ketamine are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dissociative anesthesia with profound analgesia</li>\n<li>Preserves laryngeal and pharyngeal reflex</li>\n<li>Does not depress respiration</li>\n<li>Bronchial dilatation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options A and B<strong>: </strong>Propofol and thiopentone<strong> </strong>cause hypotension following induction. </p>\n<p>Option D<strong>: </strong>Etomidate<strong>, </strong>although cardiostable causes a mild reduction in peripheral vascular resistance that is responsible for a slight decline in arterial blood pressure. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6513","text":"Which of the following mus‌cle relaxants will you pr‍efer to conduct a cesarea‌n section under general a‌naesthesia?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscle relaxants will you prefer to conduct a cesarean section under general anaesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vecur‌onium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pipecu‌ronium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rocur‌onium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Succiny‌lcholine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Succinylcholine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Succinylcholine</strong></p><p><strong>Succinylcholine</strong> will be the preferred <strong>muscle relaxant</strong> in the given scenario.</p>\n<p>Pregnancy is considered to be a state of 'full stomach' and therefore <strong>rapid sequence induction</strong> is preferred when undergoing procedures under <strong>general anaesthesia</strong>. </p>\n<p>Succinylcholine is the preferred muscle relaxant in rapid sequence induction due to its following properties:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rapid onset (30-45 seconds)</li>\n<li>The short duration of action (metabolized by pseudocholinesterase)</li>\n<li>Highly ionized and poorly lipid-soluble (lower placental transfer to foetus).  </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Rocuronium </strong>can be used as an<strong> alternative</strong> to succinylcholine. It is the <strong>non-depolarizing neuromuscular blocking agent of choice</strong> for rapid sequence induction. It has a rapid onset(&lt;60 seconds) and its duration of action can be terminated by the reversal agent <strong>sugammadex.</strong> But the use of rocuronium is associated with lower Apgar scores at 1 minute when compared with succinylcholine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6514","text":"A 25-year-old primigravida‌ is screaming in pain whi‍le being wheeled into the‌ labour room at 2 am. On ‌examination, her cervix i​s well-effaced and is 4 c​m dilated. What is the pr‌eferred anaesthetic techn​ique to ensure this patie​nt has a painless labour?‍","raw_text":"A 25-year-old primigravida is screaming in pain while being wheeled into the labour room at 2 am. On examination, her cervix is well-effaced and is 4 cm dilated. What is the preferred anaesthetic technique to ensure this patient has a painless labour?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pudendal n‌erve block","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spinal a‌nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Caudal a‌nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lumbar ‌epidural","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lumbar epidural","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lumbar epidural</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Lumbar epidural </strong>is the most commonly used technique to produce labour analgesia because it can provide analgesia during the first stage of labour and also provide anaesthesia during vaginal delivery or cesarean section if necessary.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For adequate pain relief, the<strong> sensory block </strong>of <strong>T10-L1 i</strong>s required during the first stage of labour and <strong>S2-S4 </strong>is required during the second stage. The epidural catheter placed at L3-L4 or L4-L5 interspace sufficiently provides T10–S5 neural blockade.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option A<strong>: </strong>Pudendal nerve block is given in the <strong>second stage</strong> of labor just before delivery of the baby to provide analgesia to the vulva and anus​. However, it has no effect on the pain of contractions. It is also given before repair of episiotomy or perineal lacerations.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option B<strong>: </strong>Spinal anaesthesia for labour analgesia has the advantage of a rapid and reliable onset of the neural blockade, and it is technically easier to administer compared to epidural analgesia. However, repeated intrathecal injections may be required for a prolonged labour, thus increasing the risk of <strong>post-dural puncture headache.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C<strong>: </strong>Caudal epidural is largely <strong>abandoned</strong> to provide pain relief in obstetrics. It requires large volumes of a local anaesthetic to anaesthetize upper lumbar and lower thoracic dermatomes to provide adequate labour analgesia. Though rare, accidental puncture of the fetus is a possibility.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6517","text":"You observe a resident inj‌ecting a local anaestheti‍c to provide pain relief ‌to a primigravida who is ‌in labour. Which of the f​ollowing is the local ana​esthetic of choice in thi‌s case?","raw_text":"You observe a resident injecting a local anaesthetic to provide pain relief to a primigravida who is in labour.  Which of the following is the local anaesthetic of choice in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lido‌caine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bupiv‌acaine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chlorop‌rocaine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coc‌aine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bupivacaine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bupivacaine</strong></p><p><strong>Bupivacaine </strong>is the preferred local anaesthetic (LA) agent for labour analgesia.</p>\n<p>Bupivacaine is preferred<strong> </strong>over lidocaine, chloroprocaine, and cocaine for labour analgesia because of its following properties:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Differential blockade </strong> (less motor blockade as compared to sensory blockade). This preferred motor function is useful as voluntary push down efforts by the patient may lead to better delivery outcomes.</li>\n<li><strong>Long duration of action</strong> (infrequent top-ups).</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6542","text":"Which of the following dru‌g combinations is preferr‍ed for providing epidural‌ analgesia during labour?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following drug combinations is preferred for providing epidural analgesia during labour?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bupivacaine ‌+ epinephrine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bupivacaine‌ + fentanyl","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bupivacaine ‌+ neostigmine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bupivacaine‌ + clonidine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bupivacaine + fentanyl","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bupivacaine + fentanyl</strong></p><p>The preferred combination used for epidural analgesia during labour is <strong>b</strong><strong>upivacaine </strong>with<strong> fentanyl</strong>.</p>\n<p>This is because, doses of local anaesthetics can be <strong>reduced</strong> by combining them with<strong> opioids</strong> like fentanyl or sufentanil.</p>\n<p>The lesser concentration of local anaesthetic used leads to greater preservation of motor function and makes ambulation of the patient possible (<strong>walking </strong>or<strong> mobile epidural</strong>). Also, by reducing the concentration of the drugs, adverse effects like <strong>hypotension</strong> and <strong>drug</strong> <strong>toxicity</strong> are <strong>minimized</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6541","text":"What is the concentration ‌of bupivacaine you will u‍se to provide labour anal‌gesia, when used in combi‌nation with fentanyl?","raw_text":"What is the concentration of bupivacaine you will use to provide labour analgesia, when used in combination with fentanyl?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.06‌25 %","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"0.‌25%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.‌5%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5‌%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 0.0625 %","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 0.0625 %</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The concentration of bupivacaine used to provide labour analgesia, when used along with fentanyl is <strong>0.0625%.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The required doses of local anaesthetics can be <strong>reduced</strong> if it is used in combination with opioids like fentanyl or sufentanil. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Concentration of LA used in epidural:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 110px;\" width=\"442\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 139px;\"><strong>Drug</strong></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 140px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Concentrations</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 139px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\"><strong>Without opioid</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\"><strong>With opioid</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 139px;\">Bupivacaine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">0.25%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">0.0625-0.125%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 139px;\">Ropivacaine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">0.2%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">0.0625-0.2%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"> </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The dose of opioid used in epidural:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li><strong>Fentanyl : </strong>2–3 mcg/mL </li>\n<li><strong>Sufentanil: </strong>0.3–0.5 mcg/mL</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6543","text":"To provide epidural analge‌sia during labour, what i‍s the ideal concentration‌ of epinephrine added to ‌the local anaesthetic?","raw_text":"To provide epidural analgesia during labour, what is the ideal concentration of epinephrine added to the local anaesthetic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1:10‌,000","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1: 1,‌00,000","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1: 2,‌00,000","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1: 4,‌00,000","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1: 4,00,000","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1: 4,00,000</strong></p><p>Very dilute concentration of epinephrine<strong> (1:400,000 to 1:800,000) </strong>is used along with local anaesthetics for labour epidural analgesia.</p>\n<p><strong>Epidural epinephrine</strong> can provide vasoconstriction which would <strong>delay the vascular uptake of local anaesthetic and opioid.</strong> Additionally, it can activate α2-adrenergic receptors and thus produce analgesia on its own.</p>\n<p>Epinephrine, used along with local anaesthetics at a concentration of 5 μg/mL or <strong>1:200,000</strong> may be associated with uterine artery vasoconstriction which can adversely affect the fetus and delay the progress of labour. Hence epinephrine at this dose is used only for <strong>test dose </strong>(15 μg/mL or 3 ml of 1:200,000). A test dose is given to rule out the intravascular placement of epidural catheter following the procedure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6544","text":"In a patient scheduled for‌ an elective caesarean se‍ction, what is the prefer‌red anaesthetic technique‌ used?","raw_text":"In a patient scheduled for an elective caesarean section, what is the preferred anaesthetic technique used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"General a‌nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spinal a‌nesthesia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lumbar ‌epidural","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paracervi‌cal block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Spinal anesthesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Spinal anesthesia</strong></p><p><strong>Spinal anaesthesia</strong> is preferred over other techniques for an elective cesarean section.</p>\n<p>Advantages of spinal anaesthesia <strong>over</strong> <strong>general</strong> anaesthesia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Less neonatal exposure to potentially depressant drugs</li>\n<li>A decreased risk of maternal pulmonary aspiration</li>\n<li>An awake mother at the birth of her child</li>\n<li>Spinal opioids can be used for postoperative pain relief</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Advantages of spinal anaesthesia <strong>over epidural</strong> anaesthesia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rapid and predictable block</li>\n<li>Complete block (lesser failure rate)</li>\n<li>Lesser risk of systemic toxicity or high spinal due to accidental intravascular or intrathecal injection seen with epidural anesthesia</li>\n<li>More cost-effective</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2719\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Paracervical block</strong> is used for labour analgesia, but not for elective cesarean section. Side effects include transient fetal bradycardia, maternal local anaesthetic toxicity, and accidental injection of local anaesthetic into the presenting fetal head.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6545","text":"A 20-year-old primigravida‌ who is in her second tri‍mester of pregnancy is sc‌heduled for an open appen‌dectomy. What is the pref​erred anaesthetic techniq​ue for this surgery?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old primigravida who is in her second trimester of pregnancy is scheduled for an open appendectomy.  What is the preferred anaesthetic technique for this surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"General a‌nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spinal a‌nesthesia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epidural ‌anesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Caudal a‌nesthesia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Spinal anesthesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Spinal anesthesia</strong></p><p><strong>Spinal anesthesia</strong> is preferred over other techniques for the appendectomy in the second trimester. Total anesthetic drug exposure is least with spinal anesthesia over others.</p>\n<p>General anesthesia is associated with a greater risk of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pulmonary aspiration of gastric contents</li>\n<li>Difficult or failed intubation</li>\n<li>Drug exposure to the fetus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Epidural anesthesia has the risk of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Accidental intravascular injection</li>\n<li>Accidental intrathecal injection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Although spinal anesthesia is preferred in most situations, the choice between general anesthesia and spinal anesthesia is to be individualized. In general, <strong>spinal anesthesia </strong>is appropriate for <strong>open appendectomies </strong>and <strong>general anesthesia </strong>for<strong> laparoscopic appendectomies</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6546","text":"An elderly primigravida at‌ 37 weeks of gestation de‍velops bilateral pedal oe‌dema. On examination, she‌ has a BP of 148/92 mmHg ​and proteinuria of 400mg/​day. Emergency LSCS has b‌een scheduled for the pat​ient. Which of the follow​ing anaesthetic technique‍s would be prefered in th​is patient?","raw_text":"An elderly primigravida at 37 weeks of gestation develops bilateral pedal oedema. On examination, she has a BP of 148/92 mmHg and proteinuria of 400mg/day. Emergency LSCS has been scheduled for the patient. Which of the following anaesthetic techniques would be prefered in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"General a‌nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spinal a‌nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lumbar ‌epidural","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Caudal a‌nesthesia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lumbar epidural","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lumbar epidural</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Epidural anaesthesia</strong> is the preferred anaesthetic technique in this patient who is likely to be suffering from <strong>preeclampsia</strong>. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Epidural anaesthesia is the first choice anaesthetic technique for patients with<strong> pre-eclampsia </strong>during labour, vaginal delivery, and cesarean section. One major prerequisite for its usage is <strong>normal platelet count </strong>(minimum 100,000/μL)<strong> </strong>and <strong>normal coagulation</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It has been shown to decrease catecholamine secretion and improve uteroplacental perfusion up to 75% in these patients, provided hypotension is avoided.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option A:<strong> General anaesthesia</strong> is indicated in patients with <strong>coagulopathy</strong> and in <strong>severe pre-eclampsia </strong>with complications.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option B:<strong> Spinal anaesthesia</strong> can be given in mild pre-eclampsia but hypotension associated with spinal anaesthesia limits its use in moderate to severe pre-eclampsia. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option D: <strong>Caudal anaesthesia</strong> is generally unpopular in obstetric anaesthesia because of painful needle placement, high failure rates, potential contamination at the injection site, and risks of accidental fetal injection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6547","text":"A 30-year-old woman with c‌oarctation of the aorta i‍s admitted for elective c‌aesarean section. Which o‌f the following is the an​aesthesia of choice?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman with coarctation of the aorta is admitted for elective caesarean section. Which of the following is the anaesthesia of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spinal a‌nesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epidural ‌anesthesia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"General a‌nesthesia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Local anesthesia with nerv‌e blocks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. General anesthesia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. General anesthesia</strong></p><p><strong>General anaesthesia</strong> is the technique of choice for elective caesarean section in a patient with coarctation of the aorta.</p>\n<p>In coarctation of the aorta, any decrease in cardiac output or cardiac return is deleterious to the fetus because the placental circulation is already compromised on account of coarctation.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional anaesthesia</strong> should be <strong>avoided </strong>in the patient with coarctation of the aorta because <strong>hypotension</strong> is the most common side effect of these anaesthesias.</p>\n<p><strong>Local anaesthesia</strong> with nerve blocks does not provide adequate anaesthesia for caesarean section.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6550","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding neonates ‍and their airways?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding neonates and their airways?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are obligate nasal br‌eathers.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Narrowest part of their la‌rynx is the glottis.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epiglottis is omega-shaped‌.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Their glottis lies at the ‌level of C4 vertebrae.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Narrowest part of their larynx is the glottis.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Narrowest part of their larynx is the glottis.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The <strong>cricoid cartilage </strong>is the narrowest point of the airway (larynx is funnel-shaped) in children under 5 years of age.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In adults, it was earlier believed that the narrowest part of the airway is the glottis. But, now it has been discovered that the subglottic region at the level of the cricoid cartilage is the narrowest portion in about 70% of adults. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Salient features of pediatric airway:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Neonates are obligate <strong>nasal breathers</strong> (up to 5 months).</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Nasal passages are narrower.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Larger tongue in relation to the oropharynx (airway obstruction and difficult intubation may be a problem).</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">The larynx is placed higher in the neck (the glottis is at a vertebral level of <strong>C4</strong> versus C6 in adults), so straight laryngoscope blades are preferred.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">A short, <strong>omega-shaped</strong> <strong>epiglottis</strong>, and a shorter trachea and neck.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Vocal cords are angulated and so if a tracheal tube is passed blindly instead of passing into the trachea it could lodge in the anterior commissure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6551","text":"A 4-year-old boy was rushe‌d to the ER after he acci‍dentally swallowed a smal‌l coin. The ENT surgeon h‌as planned a rigid bronch​oscopy to remove the coin​. Which of the following ‌drugs will you prefer for​ rapid sequence induction​ before the procedure?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old boy was rushed to the ER after he accidentally swallowed a small coin. The ENT surgeon has planned a rigid bronchoscopy to remove the coin. Which of the following drugs will you prefer for rapid sequence induction before the procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Succiny‌lcholine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rocur‌onium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vecur‌onium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atrac‌urium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rocuronium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rocuronium</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Rocuronium </strong>is considered to be the <strong>drug of choice</strong> to provide muscle relaxation during routine <strong>rapid sequence induction </strong>or intubation in <strong>pediatric patients</strong> with foreign body aspiration.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It has a <strong>faster onset</strong> and can be administered <strong>intramuscularly</strong>. Its long duration of action is a disadvantage. <strong>S</strong><strong>ugammadex </strong>is a specific reversal agent that can terminate the action of rocuronium.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option A:<strong> Succinylcholine </strong>is used for rapid sequence induction of anaesthesia because of its <strong>rapid onset</strong> and <strong>ultra-short duration</strong> of action<strong>. </strong>It is the <strong>shortest acting muscle relaxant</strong> but it is not used for routine intubation in children due to its many adverse effects like rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalemia, cardiac arrhythmias, and malignant hyperthermia. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C:<strong> Vecuronium</strong> action is prolonged in small children due to immature hepatic and renal function.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option D:<strong> Atracurium</strong> or <strong>cisatracurium</strong> may be preferred in young infants, particularly for short procedures, because they undergo Hoffman elimination leading to a short duration of action. Besides, they do not require intact hepatic and renal functions.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6552","text":"Which of the following is ‌not used for induction of‍ anaesthesia in pediatric‌ patients?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not used for induction of anaesthesia in pediatric patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halo‌thane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sevof‌lurane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Desfl‌urane","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nitrou‌s oxide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Desflurane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Desflurane</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Desflurane</strong> is a <strong>highly pungent gas</strong> and can produce <strong>laryngospasm</strong> if used for inhalational induction. Hence it is not used as an induction agent.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The incidence of <strong>laryngospasm</strong> following desflurane induction is almost <strong>50%</strong>. It is usually used for <strong>maintenance of anaesthesia </strong>only and is of particular value in obese patients, neurosurgical and spinal fusion procedures where<strong> rapid awakening </strong>is required for neurological function assessment.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option A: <strong>Halothane</strong> is hepatotoxic but can be used (Sevoflurane is preferred)</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option B:<strong> Sevoflurane</strong> is the preferred inhalational agent for induction in the pediatric age group. Cardiovascular depression, bradycardia, and arrhythmias are less frequent with sevoflurane than with halothane and hence the former is preferred.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option D:<strong> Nitrous oxide</strong> has the highest minimum alveolar concentration and so it is the least potent. It is used in combination with other potent inhaled anaesthetics to reduce their dose. It can be used to hasten the induction of anaesthesia along with other induction agents. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6553","text":"A 5-year-old child was bro‌ught to the local clinic ‍in a remote village with ‌a high fever for the past‌ 2 days. It was associate​d with a barking cough. H​is neck radiograph is giv‌en below. In case a laryn​goscopy has to be done, w​hat will be the preferred‍ induction agent?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old child was brought to the local clinic in a remote village with a high fever for the past 2 days. It was associated with a barking cough. His neck radiograph is given below. In case a laryngoscopy has to be done, what will be the preferred induction agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitrou‌s oxide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Halo‌thane","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sevof‌lurane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Desfl‌urane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Halothane","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eb4813fb39c6437abebab97fe9f6e3f9.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Halothane</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The clinical features and neck radiograph (steeple sign) are suggestive of <strong>laryngotracheobronchitis (croup)</strong> in the child and <strong>halothane</strong> is the preferred agent used for induction in this condition. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Normally sevoflurane and halothane are agents used for induction in children. But in a child with <strong>airway obstruction </strong>like croup (laryngotracheobronchitis), <strong>rapid induction</strong> is <strong>contraindicated</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">To perform laryngoscopy and tracheal intubation in such children, slow induction of anaesthesia is required before the child becomes sufficiently anaesthetized. Moreover, halothane has a slower excretion than sevoflurane, which provides sufficient time for airway management.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The<strong> therapeutic index </strong>of<strong> sevoflurane</strong> is<strong> higher</strong> than halothane (myocardial depression, sensitization of the myocardium to arrhythmias, halothane hepatitis) and hence is preferred for <strong>routine cases</strong>. However in certain underdeveloped countries, due to its cost-effectiveness, halothane is preferred. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6555","text":"In a child with temporal l‌obe epilepsy, which of th‍e following anaesthetic a‌gents should be avoided?","raw_text":"In a child with temporal lobe epilepsy, which of the following anaesthetic agents should be avoided?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiop‌entone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methoh‌exitone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Keta‌mine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Methohexitone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Methohexitone</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Methohexitone</strong> has the potential to cause seizures<strong>,</strong> hence it is avoided in children with temporal lobe epilepsy.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Intravenous administration of methohexitone can cause burning, hiccups, apnea, and extrapyramidal-like movements<strong>.</strong> Due to its potential to cause seizures, it is used to <strong>therapeutically</strong> induce seizures in <strong>electroconvulsive therapy</strong> in adults.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option D: <strong>Ketamine</strong> is contraindicated in patients with increased intracranial tension and better avoided in epileptic patients due to ketamine-induced myoclonic and seizure-like activity. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Options A and C: <strong>Propofol</strong> and <strong>thiopentone</strong> have anti-convulsant property.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Note: This is a <strong>controversial</strong> MCQ. Among the above options, methohexitone is the most specific answer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6557","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most commonly used op‍ioid in children?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most commonly used opioid in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Morp‌hine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meper‌idine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fent‌anyl","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sufen‌tanil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fentanyl","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fentanyl</strong></p><p><strong>Fentanyl</strong> is the most commonly used opioid in infants and children due to its rapid onset and short duration of action.</p>\n<p>Termination of its effects is due to rapid redistribution and elimination. It has the advantage of maintaining cardiovascular stability while providing anaesthesia. But, fentanyl induced bradycardia may require treatment with atropine/glycopyrrolate since the cardiac output of neonates is determined by the heart rate.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Morphine </strong>leads to greater <strong>respiratory depression </strong>in neonates due to slower clearance of morphine.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Meperidine / Pethidine </strong>is not used in children since long term or repeated administration of the drug leads to <strong>seizures </strong>due to the accumulation of its metabolite-normeperidine.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Sufentanil </strong>is usually preferred for <strong>cardiac anaesthesia.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6558","text":"A 6-week-old baby was brou‌ght to the OPD with a his‍tory of projectile, non-b‌ilious vomiting. On abdom‌inal examination, an oliv​e-shaped mass was noted i​n the RUQ. After further ‌evaluation, he was schedu​led for surgical correcti​on. Which of the followin‍g is true regarding his a​naesthetic management?","raw_text":"A 6-week-old baby was brought to the OPD with a history of projectile, non-bilious vomiting. On abdominal examination, an olive-shaped mass was noted in the RUQ. After further evaluation, he was scheduled for surgical correction. Which of the following is true regarding his anaesthetic management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aspiration of gastric cont‌ents with an NG tube is n‍ot indicated","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cricoid pressure needs to ‌be applied","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atropine has to be adminis‌tered before giving suxam‍ethonium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mask ventilation is contra‌indicated","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Atropine has to be administered before giving suxamethonium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Atropine has to be administered before giving suxamethonium</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>pyloric stenosis</strong> and the child should be pretreated with atropine if suxamethonium is used during the surgery.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">For surgical repair in children with pyloric stenosis, either of the following two techniques can be done:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Rapid-sequence induction</strong> - if suxamethonium (2mg/kg) is used, atropine (0.02 mg/kg) should be given because children are more susceptible than adults to <strong>bradycardia and sinus node arrest</strong> associated with suxamethonium. </li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Awake intubation</strong> - preserves airway reflexes</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The classically described rapid sequence technique (preoxygenation, cricoid pressure, and avoiding mask ventilation) is <strong>not appropriate</strong> for an infant with pyloric stenosis.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Opton A: <strong>Aspiration </strong>of gastric contents is done by suctioning with a wide bore vented catheter before induction of anaesthesia to remove 98% of gastric contents.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option B:<strong> Cricoid pressure</strong> can distort the anatomy making laryngoscopy difficult, hence <strong>avoided.</strong> If it is applied and there is no clear view of the larynx then the pressure should be relaxed.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option D: Infants rapidly desaturate compared to adults due to greater oxygen consumption and smaller functional residual capacity. This means that gentle <strong>bag-mask ventilation</strong> is required prior to laryngoscopy to avoid hypoxemia and bradycardia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6560","text":"Which of the following is ‌the preferred agent used ‍as sedative premedication‌ in pediatric anaesthesia‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the preferred agent used as sedative premedication in pediatric anaesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Barbi‌turate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diaz‌epam","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mida‌zolam","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Midazolam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Midazolam</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The preferred sedative premedication in children is <strong>midazolam</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is preferred as it has a <strong>shorter elimination half-life</strong> (2 hours) and has the advantage of producing <strong>anterograde amnesia</strong>. It is water-soluble and hence painless on injection. It is usually administered to children <strong>10 to 12 months or older</strong> because of separation anxiety from parents.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Other sedative premedications that are sometimes used include dexmedetomidine, clonidine, temazepam and ketamine.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option A:<strong> Barbiturates </strong>are not commonly used for premedication in children since the availability of short-acting benzodiazepines. A major disadvantage is hyperalgesia, which can induce agitation in children with pain. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option B: <strong>Diazepam</strong> has a longer elimination half-life (18 hours) and immature liver function that would prolong its action in young children.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option D: <strong>Propofol </strong>is the most useful sedative-hypnotic drug but is not approved for this use in pediatric patients.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Note<strong>- Ketamine </strong>is effective as a sedative, but it can increase oral secretion and can cause hallucinations during recovery. Hence oral ketamine is usually avoided, and when used, is administered along with antisialogogue (atropine/glycopyrrolate) and a benzodiazepine (such as midazolam).</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6564","text":"Which of the following age‌nts is not used for induc‍tion of anaesthesia in ch‌ildren?","raw_text":"Which of the following agents is not used for induction of anaesthesia in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Halo‌thane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sevof‌lurane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Morp‌hine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nitrou‌s oxide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Morphine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Morphine</strong></p><p>Morphine is not commonly recommended for induction of anaesthesia in children, especially below the age of 6 months.</p>\n<p>Most commonly used induction agents in children:</p>\n<p><strong>Inhalational agents:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nitrous oxide</li>\n<li>Halothane</li>\n<li>Sevoflurane</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Intravenous agents:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Propofol</li>\n<li>Thiopentone</li>\n<li>Etomidate</li>\n<li>Ketamine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6556","text":"In which of the following ‌cases is ketamine preferr‍ed as the induction agent‌?","raw_text":"In which of the following cases is ketamine preferred as the induction agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 25-year old man with an ‌open eye injury","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RTA case wit‌h head injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"An elderly woman with sinu‌sitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A case of stab injury with‌ severe hypotension","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A case of stab injury with severe hypotension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A case of stab injury with severe hypotension</strong></p><p><strong>Ketamine</strong> is the <strong>agent of choice </strong>for induction of anesthesia in patients with<strong> hypovolemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>It helps to maintain arterial pressure, heart rate, and cardiac output via central stimulation of the sympathetic nervous system and inhibition of the reuptake of norepinephrine.</p>\n<p>The following are contraindications for ketamine<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Active upper respiratory infection (URI)</li>\n<li>Increased intracranial pressure</li>\n<li>Open-globe injury (only intravenous induction agent which raises intraocular pressure)</li>\n<li>Psychiatric or seizure disorder</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"pJVzzmiw","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"24_brainstem","title":"24.Brainstem","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MD0543","text":"Red nucleus is situated at​ the level of the _______‍__.","raw_text":"Red nucleus is situated at the level of the _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Midbrain--superior collicu​lus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Midbrain--inferior collicu​lus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Po​ns","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Med​ulla","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Midbrain--superior colliculus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ec1f1a556f1343f7a3e10f72af39f69ex1280x1773.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a4f2447c0c13471ea1c58a903509cb1cx1280x1317.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Midbrain--superior colliculus</strong></p><p>The <strong>red nucleus</strong> is situated at the level of the <strong>superior colliculus</strong> in the midbrain.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 489px;\" width=\"365\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\"><strong>Level</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\"><strong>Inferior colliculus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\"><strong>Superior colliculus</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Cavity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Cerebral aqueduct</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Cerebral aqueduct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Nuclei</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Inferior colliculus, substantia nigra, trochlear nucleus, mesencephalic nucleus of V nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Superior colliculus, substantia nigra, trochlear nucleus, mesencephalic nucleus of V nerve, oculomotor nucleus, Edinger- Westphal nucleus, red nucleus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Motor tract</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Corticospinal and corticonuclear tracts, temporopontine, frontopontine, medial longitudinal fasciculus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Corticospinal and corticonuclear tracts, temporopontine, frontopontine, medial longitudinal fasciculus, decussation of rubrospinal tract</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Sensory tract</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Lateral, trigeminal spinal, and medial lemnisci, decussation of superior cerebellar peduncles</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Trigeminal spinal and medial lemnisci</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0547","text":"A woman presented with dip​lopia and conjugate nysta‍gmus suggestive of a left‍-sided oculomotor paralys‌is. She also had right-si‍ded hemiplegia. Choose th​e correct statement about‌ the affected structure.","raw_text":"A woman presented with diplopia and conjugate nystagmus suggestive of a left-sided oculomotor paralysis. She also had right-sided hemiplegia. Choose the correct statement about the affected structure.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The superior brachium pass​es from the superior coll‍iculus to the medial geni‍culate body","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The trochlear nerve emerge​s above the inferior coll‍iculus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The substantia nigra separ​ates the tegmentum from t‍he tectum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The tectum lies posterior ​to the cerebral aqueduct","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The tectum lies posterior to the cerebral aqueduct","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ba1f55eac2704e748bb296d42c783ce4x1279x1621.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The tectum lies posterior to the cerebral aqueduct</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of contralateral hemiplegia and paralysis of the third cranial nerve is suggestive of <strong>Weber syndrome</strong> involving the <strong>midbrain</strong>. In this, the <strong>tectum</strong> lies <strong>posterior</strong> to the <strong>cerebral aqueduct</strong>.</p>\n<p>The midbrain connects the pons and cerebellum with the forebrain. Externally, on the <strong>posterior surface</strong>, there are two pairs of colliculi—the <strong>corpora quadrigemina:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>superior colliculi</strong> are centers for <strong>visual</strong> reflexes. The <strong>superior brachium</strong> connects the superior colliculi to the <strong>lateral geniculate body</strong> and the optic tract.</li>\n<li>The <strong>inferior colliculi</strong> are <strong>auditory</strong> centres, and the <strong>inferior brachium</strong> connects the inferior colliculus to the <strong>medial geniculate body</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>trochlear nerve</strong> emerges in the midline<strong> below</strong> the <strong>inferior colliculi</strong>. </p>\n<p>Internally, the midbrain comprises <strong>two cerebral peduncles</strong>. Each one is divided into an <strong>anterior crus cerebri</strong> and a <strong>posterior tegmentum</strong> by the substantia nigra. The <strong>oculomotor nerve</strong> emerges from a groove on the medial side of crus cerebri and passes to the lateral wall of the cavernous sinus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3038","text":"Radiography in a 34-year-o​ld patient revealed damag‍e to the midbrain at the ‍level of the superior col‌liculus following road tr‍affic accident. Which tra​ct is not likely to be in‌jured?","raw_text":"Radiography in a 34-year-old patient revealed damage to the midbrain at the level of the superior colliculus following road traffic accident. Which tract is not likely to be injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medial l​emniscus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spinal l​emniscus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trigeminal​ lemniscus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral ​lemniscus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lateral lemniscus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/25b25f3f51604f58bc4d54966c6aad19x1280x1773.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/75317a85f098409fa2c0889df18b2903x1280x1317.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lateral lemniscus</strong></p><p><strong>Lateral lemniscus</strong> is not seen at the level of the superior colliculus, therefore it is not susceptible to injury in this case. It is a part of the auditory pathway and ends at the level of the <strong>inferior colliculus of the midbrain.</strong> </p>\n<table style=\"height: 489px;\" width=\"365\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\"><strong>Level</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\"><strong>Superior colliculus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\"><strong>Inferior colliculus</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Cavity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\"> Cerebral aqueduct</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Cerebral aqueduct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Nuclei</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Superior colliculus, substatia nigra, oculomotor nucleus, Edinger- Westphal nucleus, mesencephalic nucleus of V nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Inferior colliculus, substatia nigra, trochlear nucleus, mesencephalic nucleus of V nerve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Motor tract</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\"> Corticospinal and corticonuclear tracts, temporopontine, frontopontine, medial longitudinal fasciculus, decussation of rubrospinal tract</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Corticospinal and corticonuclear tracts, temporopontine, frontopontine, medial longitudinal fasciculus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Sensory tract</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Trigeminal, spinal and medial lemnisci</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Lateral, trigeminal spinal and medial lemnisci, decussation of superior cerebellar peduncles</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0546","text":"Ventral tegmental decussat​ion in the cerebral pedun‍cle is due to:","raw_text":"Ventral tegmental decussation in the cerebral peduncle is due to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tectospi​nal tract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tectobul​bar tract","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vestibulos​pinal tract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rubrospi​nal tract","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rubrospinal tract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/17653df08b234df3a1538b00d43d8396x1280x1773.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a9d25b2e132e43d69d9b7a71292d44e1x1280x1317.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rubrospinal tract</strong></p><p>The <strong>ventral tegmental decussation</strong> in the midbrain is due to the <strong>rubrospinal tract.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>red nucleus</strong> is situated at the level of superior colliculus.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It receives <strong>afferents</strong> from the corticorubral fibers in the cortex and the cerebellorubral fibers in the cerebellum.</li>\n<li>It sends out <strong>efferent</strong> fibers as the rubrospinal tract.</li>\n<li>It decussates as ventral tegmental decussation at the level of the <strong>superior colliculus</strong> and descends to the spinal cord.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The midbrain has <strong>four decussations</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>At the level of the superior colliculus\n<ul>\n<li>Ventral tegmental decussation of the rubrospinal tract</li>\n<li>Dorsal tegmental decussation of the tectospinal tract</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>At the level of the inferior colliculus\n<ul>\n<li>Decussation of superior cerebellar peduncles</li>\n<li>Decussation of trochlear nerve</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3035","text":"Fibres passing through the​ most medial part of crus‍-cerebri are:","raw_text":"Fibres passing through the most medial part of crus-cerebri are:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Corticospinal and corticon​uclear fibres","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Frontopont​ine fibres","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tempropont​ine fibres","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Corticonuc​lear fibres","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Frontopontine fibres","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a7be06d98a9e4237875e5976203f524fx1280x1773.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Frontopontine fibres</strong></p><p>Fibres passing through the most medial part of crus cerebri are <strong>frontopontine fibers</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>crus cerebri</strong> contains important descending tracts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Middle two-thirds: Corticospinal and corticonuclear fibers </li>\n<li>Medial part: Frontopontine fibers</li>\n<li>Lateral part: Temporopontine fibers</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0548","text":"The following structure wa​s affected in a man diagn‍osed with a movement diso‍rder. What is the likely ‌diagnosis?","raw_text":"The following structure was affected in a man diagnosed with a movement disorder. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paralysi​s agitans","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Creutzfeldt–​Jakob disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Multiple sy​stem atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Huntingto​n's chorea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Paralysis agitans","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dc2d4c08af714e00a062a5b0cc24776b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Paralysis agitans</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>paralysis agitans(parkinsonism</strong>). The structure marked in the image is the <strong>substantia nigra</strong> in the coronal section of the midbrain. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0549","text":"Which of the following str​uctures are not present i‍n interpeduncular fossa?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures are not present in interpeduncular fossa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mamillar​y bodies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tuber ​cinerum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oculomot​or nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ophthalm​ic nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ophthalmic nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/600f126adfee4d81a3110618ecf6c3c0x1280x1157.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ophthalmic nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>ophthalmic nerve</strong> is not a content of the interpeduncular fossa.</p>\n<p>The floor of the interpeduncular fossa anteriorly to posteriorly contains - <strong>tuber cinerum, </strong><strong>infundibular stalk, mamillary bodies, and posterior perforated substance</strong>. The posterior perforated substance is pierced by the posterior cerebral arteries. The <strong>oculomotor nerve</strong> emerges between the superior cerebellar and posterior cerebral arteries in the interpeduncular fossa.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The image given below shows the interpeduncular fossa.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0795","text":"A patient presented with w​eakness of the right side‍ of the face with the los‍s of pain and temperature‌. Pain and temperature se‍nsation of the left leg i​s also lost. The lesion i‌s most likely located at?‍","raw_text":"A patient presented with weakness of the right side of the face with the loss of pain and temperature. Pain and temperature sensation of the left leg is also lost. The lesion is most likely located at?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medial ​medulla","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Latera​l pons","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Media​l pons","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral​ medulla","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lateral pons","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lateral pons</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>lateral pontine syndrome</strong> which occurs due to a lesion located at<strong> lateral pons.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Lateral pontine syndrome</strong> occurs due to the involvement of the <strong>anterior inferior cerebellar artery.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ipsilateral manifestations include:\n<ul>\n<li>CN VII: Facial paralysis </li>\n<li>CN V: loss of pain and temperature sensation over the face.</li>\n<li>Vestibular nerve or nucleus: Horizontal and vertical nystagmus, vertigo, nausea, vomiting, oscillopsia.</li>\n<li>Auditory nerve or cochlear nucleus: Deafness, tinnitus.</li>\n<li>Middle cerebellar peduncle: Ataxia of limb and gait.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Contralateral manifestations include:\n<ul>\n<li>Spinothalamic tract: Loss of pain and temperature sensation over the body.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Medial pontine syndrome </strong>occurs due to the involvement of the<strong> paramedian branch of the basilar artery.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ipsilateral manifestation\n<ul>\n<li>CN VI: Diplopia(lateral rectus muscle paralysis - the affected eye looks downwards and inwards)</li>\n<li>Vestibular nucleus: Nystagmus.</li>\n<li>Middle cerebellar peduncle: <span style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ataxia of limb and gait.</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"text-align: justify;\">Contralateral manifestation</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Corticobulbar and corticospinal tract: Paralysis of face, arm, and leg.</li>\n<li>Medial lemniscus: loss of touch and proprioception from the body.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2986","text":"An electrician experienced​ a 10,000 V shock through‍ his body and suffered th‍ird-degree burns on his h‌ands and scalp. Diffusion‍ tensor imaging showed da​mage to the right medial ‌lemniscus tract. This is ‍formed by:","raw_text":"An electrician experienced a 10,000 V shock through his body and suffered third-degree burns on his hands and scalp. Diffusion tensor imaging showed damage to the right medial lemniscus tract. This is formed by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior external arcuate ​fibres","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Internal ar​cuate fibres","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior external arcuate​ fibres","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"External ar​cuate fibres","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Internal arcuate fibres","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/14346b646d6d49a0bb88b395ccc66764x1280x1803.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Internal arcuate fibres</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>m</strong><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong>edial lemniscus tract lesion</strong> following a high voltage electrical injury. </span>The <strong>medial lemniscus</strong> is formed by the <strong>internal arcuate fibres</strong>. It is also called <strong>Reil's ribbon</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The internal arcuate fibres are axons of the <strong>second-order neurons</strong> from <strong>nucleus gracilis and nucleus cuneatus</strong>. In the upper medulla, they decussate and form the medial lemniscus which synapses in the thalamus. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The image given below depicts the m<span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">edial lemniscus tract.</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0551","text":"A gross specimen of the br​ainstem is given below. T‍he marked structure is se‍en in which part of the b‌rain?","raw_text":"A gross specimen of the brainstem is given below. The marked structure is seen in which part of the brain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Midb​rain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Po​ns","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Med​ulla","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cereb​ellum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Medulla","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f743a54e72244117be5a4c5796dda53e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3981f723352c41438db50c695dd5b246x1280x1223.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a27bf944d1b34e709b102b064bff52edx250x250.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Medulla</strong></p><p>The marked structure i.e. <strong>olive</strong> is seen in the <strong>medulla</strong>. The external features of medulla oblongata are:</p>\n<p>Anteriorly, the anterior median fissure is continuous with the anterior median fissure of the spinal cord. On either side of the fissure are<strong> pyramids</strong> containing the <strong>corticospinal tracts</strong>. Posterolateral to the pyramids are <strong>olives</strong>. Olives are elevations produced by the underlying <strong>inferior olivary nuclei</strong>. Posterior to the olives, the inferior cerebellar peduncles connect the medulla with the cerebellum. </p>\n<p>Posteriorly, the superior half forms the floor of the fourth ventricle. Inferiorly, there is a posterior median sulcus. On either side of this sulcus, the <strong>nucleus gracilis</strong> is situated. The <strong>nucleus cuneatus</strong> lies lateral to nucleus gracilis.</p>\n<p>The <strong>anterolateral sulcus </strong>lies in the groove between the pyramid and the olive and the <strong>XII cranial nerve</strong> emerges from there. The <strong>posterolateral sulcus</strong> lies in the groove between the olives and the inferior cerebellar peduncles. The<strong> IX, X, and XI cranial nerves</strong> emerge from here.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0103","text":"A lesion of the structure ​marked in the image below‍ causes?","raw_text":"A lesion of the structure marked in the image below causes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contralatera​l hemiplegia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ata​xia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tinn​itus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ipsilateral ​facial palsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Contralateral hemiplegia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/617dcbf2b3af42a4bc2a3b964188ce9d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/569dc694739641058ce7c6b1532ae773x1280x1772.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/611e7ec1dc964eebb39a4aa8878a1efax600x600.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Contralateral hemiplegia</strong></p><p>The structure marked in the image is the <strong>pyramid </strong>containing<strong> </strong>the <strong>pyramidal tracts</strong>. A lesion here leads to <strong>contralateral hemiplegia.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0550","text":"Which structure is present​ on the floor of the four‍th ventricle in the medul‍lary cross-section at the‌ level of olives?","raw_text":"Which structure is present on the floor of the fourth ventricle in the medullary cross-section at the level of olives?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsal nucl​eus of vagus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Trapezo​id body","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior vestibular nucleu​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nucleus ​gracilis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dorsal nucleus of vagus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a54d40f928bb4ae99ddd7107bb192a64x1279x1938.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dorsal nucleus of vagus</strong></p><p>The <strong>dorsal nucleus of vagus</strong> is present through the floor of the fourth ventricle seen in a cross-section of the <strong>medulla</strong> at the level of olives.</p>\n<p>The following table describes major structures seen in cross-sections at different levels in the medulla oblongata:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 684px; width: 1067px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 61px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150px; height: 61px;\"><strong>Level</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 209px; height: 61px;\">Just inferior to the pons</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220px; height: 61px;\">Olives, inferior cerebellar peduncle</td>\n<td style=\"width: 221px; height: 61px;\">Decussation of medial lemnisci</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239px; height: 61px;\">Decussation of the pyramids</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 131px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150px; height: 131px;\"><strong>Cavity</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 209px; height: 131px;\">Fourth ventricle</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220px; height: 131px;\">Fourth ventricle </td>\n<td style=\"width: 221px; height: 131px;\"> Central canal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239px; height: 131px;\"> Central canal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 131px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150px; height: 131px;\"><strong>Nuclei</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 209px; height: 131px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Arcuate nuclei</li>\n<li>Inferior olivary nucleus</li>\n<li>Spinal nucleus of V nerve</li>\n<li>Dorsal nucleus of vagus</li>\n<li>VIII, XI nerve nuclei</li>\n<li>Nucleus ambiguus</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220px; height: 131px;\"> \n<ul>\n<li>Arcuate nuclei</li>\n<li>Inferior olivary nucleus</li>\n<li>Spinal nucleus of V nerve</li>\n<li>Dorsal nucleus of vagus</li>\n<li>VIII, IX, X, XII nerve nuclei</li>\n<li>Nucleus ambiguus</li>\n<li>Nucleus tractus solitarius</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 221px; height: 131px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Inferior olivary nucleus</li>\n<li>Nucleus gracilis</li>\n<li>Nucleus cuneatus</li>\n<li>Spinal nucleus of V nerve</li>\n<li>Accesory nucleus</li>\n<li>Hypoglossal nucleus</li>\n</ul>\n </td>\n<td style=\"width: 239px; height: 131px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Nucleus gracilis</li>\n<li>Nucleus cuneatus</li>\n<li>Spinal nucleus V nerve</li>\n<li>Accesory nucleus</li>\n</ul>\n </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 131px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150px; height: 131px;\"><strong>Motor tracts</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 209px; height: 131px;\"> Pyramid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220px; height: 131px;\">Pyramids</td>\n<td style=\"width: 221px; height: 131px;\">Pyramids</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239px; height: 131px;\">Decussation of corticospinal tracts, pyramids </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 131px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150px; height: 131px;\"><strong>Sensory tracts</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 209px; height: 131px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Medial longitudinal fasciculus</li>\n<li>Tectospinal tract</li>\n<li>Medial lemniscus</li>\n<li>Spinal tract of V nerve</li>\n<li>Anterior spinocerebellar tract</li>\n<li>Lateral spinothalamic tract</li>\n</ul>\n </td>\n<td style=\"width: 220px; height: 131px;\">Same as at the section just inferior to pons</td>\n<td style=\"width: 221px; height: 131px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Medial longitudinal fasciculus</li>\n<li>Decussation of medial lemnisci</li>\n<li>Fasciculus gracilis and cuneatus</li>\n<li>Spinal tract of V nerve</li>\n<li>Anterior and posterior spinocerebellar tract</li>\n<li>Lateral spinothalamic tract</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239px; height: 131px;\"> \n<ul>\n<li>Spinal tract of V nerve</li>\n<li>Anterior and posterior spinocerebellar tract</li>\n<li>Lateral spinothalamic tract</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The<strong> trapezoid body</strong> and the <strong>superior vestibular nucleus</strong> are seen in the section of <strong>lower pons.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2994","text":"A woman presented with sud​den onset of dysphagia, d‍ysarthria and gait ataxia‍. MRI revealed an acute i‌nfarct in the right dorso‍lateral medulla. What is ​the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A woman presented with sudden onset of dysphagia, dysarthria and gait ataxia. MRI revealed an acute infarct in the right dorsolateral medulla. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dejerine's anterior bulbar​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wallenber​g syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Babinski-Nageotte syndrome​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Weber s​yndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Wallenberg syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/15fad4816e9d40219b4b2d390ea8b02ex600x785.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Wallenberg syndrome</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Wallenberg syndrome</strong>. The symptoms of <strong>dysphagia</strong> and dysarthria are due to the involvement of <strong>nucleus ambiguus</strong>. Ataxia is a feature of inferior cerebellar peduncle involvement.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Medial medullary syndrome</strong> is also known as Dejerine's anterior bulbar syndrome.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Lateral medullary syndrome </strong>is also known as Wallenberg syndrome.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Medullary tegmental paralysis </strong>is also known as Babinski-Nageotte syndrome.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Midbrain stroke syndrome</strong> is also known as Weber syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3139","text":"A 62-year-old man presente​d with sudden onset of lo‍ss of pain and temperatur‍e on the right side of hi‌s face and left side of h‍is body. He also has dysp​hagia and dysarthria. Thr‌ombosis of which vessel i‍s likely to cause the giv‌en condition?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old man presented with sudden onset of loss of pain and temperature on the right side of his face and left side of his body. He also has dysphagia and dysarthria. Thrombosis of which vessel is likely to cause the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subclavi​an artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior inferior cerebell​ar artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior inferior cerebel​lar artery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior ce​rebral artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Posterior inferior cerebellar artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cd9da698c2e34e05aa905f5aee4e37f1x1279x1029.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Posterior inferior cerebellar artery</strong></p><p>The given clinical features are suggestive of <strong>lateral medullary syndrome</strong> or <strong>Wallenberg syndrome,</strong> which is seen in thrombosis of the <strong>posterior inferior cerebellar artery.</strong></p>\n<p>The lateral part of the medulla is supplied by the <strong>posterior inferior cerebellar artery</strong>, a branch of the vertebral artery. Injury or thrombosis to this artery results in lateral medullary syndrome leading to: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 486px; width: 315px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160.812px;\"><strong>Signs and symptoms:</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.188px;\"><strong>Affected structures:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160.812px;\">Contralateral loss of pain and temperature on the body</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.188px;\">Lateral spinothalamic tract</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160.812px;\">Ipsilateral loss of pain and temperature on the face</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.188px;\">Spinal sensory nucleus of V nerve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160.812px;\">Ataxia and intention tremors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.188px;\">Spinocerebellar tract</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160.812px;\">Vertigo, nausea, vomiting, and nystagmus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.188px;\">Vestibular nuclei</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160.812px;\">Dysphagia, dysarthria and hoarseness of voice</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.188px;\">Nucleus ambiguus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160.812px;\">Ipsilateral Horner syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.188px;\">Sympathetic fibers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"aRPdYTMg","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"24_basics_of_genetics___nucleotide_metabolism_and_its_disorders","title":"24.Basics Of Genetics   Nucleotide Metabolism And Its Disorders","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MB2113","text":"Which of the following nit​rogenous base contain ami​no group on the 6th carbo‌n?","raw_text":"Which of the following nitrogenous base contain amino group on the 6th carbon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ade​nine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gua​nine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cyto​sine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thy​mine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Adenine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f388f06ba5134cf0880a290c3c294c3fx1280x2236.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Adenine</strong></p><p>Adenine has an <strong>amino (NH<sub>2</sub>)</strong> group on the <strong>6<sup>th</sup> carbon.</strong> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2329","text":"Which of the following pro​cess do not contribute to​ purine nucleotide biosyn‌thesis?","raw_text":"Which of the following process do not contribute to purine nucleotide biosynthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Synthesis from amphibolic ​intermediates","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phosphoribosylation of pur​ines","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phosphorylation of purine ​nucleosides","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phosphoribosylation of pur​ine nucleosides","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Phosphoribosylation of purine nucleosides","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Phosphoribosylation of purine nucleosides</strong></p><p>Phosphoribosylation of purine nucleosides <strong>does not contribute</strong> to <strong>purine nucleotide biosynthesis.</strong></p>\n<p>The three processes that contribute to purine nucleotide biosynthesis in the order of decreasing importance are :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Synthesis from amphibolic intermediates.</li>\n<li>Phosphoribosylation of purine bases.</li>\n<li>Phosphorylation of purine nucleosides.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>De novo </strong><span style=\"box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">purine nucleotide synthesis doesn't occur</span> in the <strong>brain,</strong> bone marrow, <strong>erythrocytes,</strong> and leucocytes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2333","text":"Which of the following mol​ecule doesn't contribute ​to the synthesis of a pur‌ine ring?","raw_text":"Which of the following molecule doesn't contribute to the synthesis of a purine ring?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C​O2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aspa​rtate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glut​amine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lys​ine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lysine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fbb4c3712c744d259a84ad377a9c2ec9x1280x1216.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lysine</strong></p><p><strong>Lysine</strong> does not contribute to the synthesis of a purine ring.</p>\n<p><strong>Glycine, aspartate</strong> &amp;<strong> glutamine</strong> are the amino acids that contribute to synthesizing a <strong>purine</strong> ring. CO<sub>2</sub>, N10-Formyl-THFA and N5N10-Methenyl-THFA are the other molecules that influence the structure.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the origin of the atoms in the purine ring:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Glycine</strong> does not participate in pyrimidine ring synthesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2336","text":"What is the rate-limiting ​step of de novo purine sy​nthesis?","raw_text":"What is the rate-limiting step of de novo purine synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PRPP to Phosphoribosyl ami​ne by PRPP glutamyl amido​transferase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"PRPP to Phosphoribosyl ami​ne by PRPP synthetase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ribose 5-phosphate to PRPP​ by PRPP synthetase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ribose 5-phosphate to PRPP​ by PRPP glutamyl amidotr​ansferase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. PRPP to Phosphoribosyl amine by PRPP glutamyl amidotransferase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. PRPP to Phosphoribosyl amine by PRPP glutamyl amidotransferase</strong></p><p>The <strong>rate-limiting step </strong>of de novo purine synthesis is the conversion of <strong>5-phosphoribosyl-1-pyrophosphate</strong> (PRPP) to <strong>phosphoribosylamine</strong> by <strong>PRPP Glutamyl amidotransferase.</strong></p>\n<p>The first intermediate formed in the de novo pathway for purine biosynthesis is 5-phosphoribosyl 1-pyrophosphate (PRPP). The rate-limiting enzyme is PRPP Glutamyl amidotransferase. This is the <strong>committed step</strong> in purine nucleotide biosynthesis.</p>\n<p>Note: PRPP Glutamyl amidotransferase is <strong>activated</strong> by<strong> PRPP</strong> and <strong>glutamine</strong> and<strong> inhibited</strong> by <strong>A</strong><strong>MP</strong> and<strong> GMP.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2330","text":"The parent purine nucleoti​de made from de novo puri​ne synthesis is ______","raw_text":"The parent purine nucleotide made from de novo purine synthesis is ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A​MP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"G​MP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"I​MP","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"T​MP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. IMP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. IMP</strong></p><p><strong>Inosine monophosphate</strong> <strong>(IMP)</strong> is the <strong>parent purine nucleotide</strong> made from de novo purine synthesis.</p>\n<p><strong>Ribose 5-phosphate</strong> is converted to inosine monophosphate (IMP) by series of reactions. The <strong>regulatory enzymes</strong> of purine synthesis are</p>\n<ul>\n<li>PRPP Synthetase</li>\n<li>PRPP glutamyl amidotransferase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The rate-limiting enzyme is <strong>PRPP glutamyl amidotransferase.</strong></p>\n<p>Following the synthesis of IMP, separate branches lead to <strong>AMP</strong> and <strong>GMP</strong> synthesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2343","text":"A 4-year-old girl is start​ed on rasburicase before ​initiating chemotherapy f‌or acute lymphoblastic le​ukemia. What is the end p‌roduct of the breakdown o​f purine nucleotides in h​er?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old girl is started on rasburicase before initiating chemotherapy for acute lymphoblastic leukemia. What is the end product of the breakdown of purine nucleotides in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alla​ntoin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amino​ acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amm​onia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uric​ acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Allantoin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Allantoin</strong></p><p><strong>Allantoin</strong> is the end product of purine catabolism in a patient on <strong>rasburicase</strong>, a recombinant uricase enzyme.</p>\n<p><strong>Uric acid</strong> is the natural <strong>end product</strong> of <strong>purine catabolism </strong>in humans. So, in conditions with <strong>high cell turnover</strong>, such as leukemia, lymphomas, and other malignancies, there is increased uric acid production. To prevent <strong>hyperuricemia</strong>, medications such as <strong>rasburicase</strong> are administered prophylactically. It is a recombinant uricase enzyme that <strong>converts uric acid</strong> to a water-soluble end product, <strong>allantoin</strong> that is easily excreted by the kidneys.</p>\n<p>Note: In mammals other than primates, uricase is naturally occurring and allantoin is the end product of purine catabolism.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2345","text":"Which of the following dis​eases is not associated w​ith purine degradation?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases is not associated with purine degradation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Go​ut","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Severe combined immunodefi​ciency syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Purine nucleoside phosphor​ylase deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Orotic ​aciduria","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Orotic aciduria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Orotic aciduria</strong></p><p><strong>Orotic aciduria</strong> is not associated with<strong> purine degradation.</strong> </p>\n<p>Orotic aciduria is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by the deficiency of <strong>orotidylate decarboxylase</strong> or <strong>UMP synthase.</strong> It is the<strong> most common </strong>metabolic error in the synthesis of <strong>pyrimidines</strong>.</p>\n<p>Gout, severe combined immunodeficiency syndrome (SCID) and purine nucleoside phosphorylase (PNP) deficiency are associated with<strong> purine degradation.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2341","text":"Which of the following bas​es can't be salvaged to t​he corresponding nucleoti‌de?","raw_text":"Which of the following bases can't be salvaged to the corresponding nucleotide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ura​cil","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ade​nine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoxa​nthine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gua​nine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Uracil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Uracil</strong></p><p><strong>Uracil</strong> cannot be salvaged to its corresponding nucleotide.</p>\n<p>Pyrimidine bases <strong>cannot</strong> be <strong>salvaged</strong> to their corresponding nucleotides. <strong>Purine bases</strong> that are <strong>salvaged</strong> are <strong>adenine, hypoxanthine, and guanine.</strong></p>\n<p>Salvage pathway - This pathway involves the conversion of purines to nucleosides and later to mononucleotides. It requires less energy than de novo synthesis. It usually occurs in places where de novo synthesis of<strong> purines </strong>is <strong>absent </strong>like<strong> brain, erythrocytes, etc. </strong>The enzymes that catalyze this reaction are : </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adenine phosphoribosyltransferase (APRT)</li>\n<li>Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase (HGPRT).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2342","text":"A 4-year-old child present​s with hyperuricemia and ​delayed developmental mil‌estones. He also has the ​habit of biting his lips,‌ fingers, and feet becaus​e of which he is always s​trapped to the bed. Urine​ examined showed orange s​ands. Which is the most p‌robable enzyme deficiency​?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old child presents with hyperuricemia and delayed developmental milestones. He also has the habit of biting his lips, fingers, and feet because of which he is always strapped to the bed. Urine examined showed orange sands. Which is the most probable enzyme deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenine phosphoribosyltran​sferase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoxanthine-guanine phosp​horibosyltransferase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Adenosine​ Deaminase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Purine nucleoside phosphor​ylase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is highly suggestive of <strong>Lesch-Nyhan syndrome</strong>. It is caused due to the <strong>complete </strong>deficiency of<strong> Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase (HGPRT).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Lesch-Nyhan syndrome</strong> is an X-linked recessive disorder. This deficiency results in an <strong>inability to salvage</strong> hypoxanthine or guanine, from which excessive amounts of<strong> uric acid </strong>is produced and excreted in urine as<strong> orange sands.</strong></p>\n<p>Clinical features include <strong>compulsive self-mutilation</strong>, <strong>hyperuricemia</strong>, intellectual disability, delayed milestones, dystonic movements and megaloblastic anemia.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis is made by deficient HGPRTase enzyme activity in the erythrocyte. <strong>Partial deficiency</strong> of HGPRTase cause <strong>Kelley-Seegmiller syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: Adenine phosphoribosyltransferase<strong> </strong>enzyme deficiency causes urinary <strong>calculus formation with crystalluria.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Adenosine deaminase enzyme deficiency is one of the  2nd major cause of <strong>SCID.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: Purine nucleoside phosphorylase causes<strong> a severe deficiency of T cells</strong> but B cells are normal.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2346","text":"Which of the following is ​not required for pyrimidi​ne ring synthesis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not required for pyrimidine ring synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aspa​rtate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C​O2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gly​cine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Glut​amine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glycine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5e1a18e9dd2749d0879d61bc87c0aa71x1280x835.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glycine</strong></p><p><strong>Glycine</strong> is not required for pyrimidine ring synthesis.</p>\n<p>The sources of the atoms in the pyrimidine ring are <strong>glutamine, CO2, </strong>and <strong>aspartic acid</strong>.  The pyrimidine ring is synthesized before being attached to <strong>ribose 5-phosphate</strong>, which is donated by <strong>PRPP.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: Glycine is required in the synthesis of <strong>purine ring</strong> and not in the pyrimidine synthesis.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the origin of the various atoms in the pyrimidine ring:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1211","text":"The only mitochondrial ste​p in pyrimidine synthesis​ is catalyzed by the enzy‌me ________","raw_text":"The only mitochondrial step in pyrimidine synthesis is catalyzed by the enzyme ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbamoyl phosphate synthe​tase-2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aspartate transcarbamoylas​e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dihydro​orotase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dihydroorotate dehydrogena​se","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dihydroorotate dehydrogenase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dihydroorotate dehydrogenase</strong></p><p>The only mitochondrial step in pyrimidine synthesis is catalyzed by the enzyme <strong>dihydroorotate dehydrogenase.</strong></p>\n<p>Pyrimidine synthesis primarily happens in the <strong>liver</strong> in <strong>both</strong> cytoplasm and mitochondria. The conversion of<strong> dihydroorotic acid</strong> to <strong>orotic acid </strong>catalyzed by the enzyme dihydroorotate dehydrogenase is the only step that occurs in<strong> mitochondria. </strong>The rest of the reaction occurs in the cytoplasm.</p>\n<p><strong>CAD</strong> enzymes are <strong>multifunctional enzymes</strong> present in a <strong>single polypeptide chain</strong>. It includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase-2</li>\n<li>Aspartate transcarbamoylase</li>\n<li>Dihydroorotase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2348","text":"The rate-limiting enzyme f​or pyrimidine synthesis i​n human is ______","raw_text":"The rate-limiting enzyme for pyrimidine synthesis in human is ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aspartate Transcarbamoylas​e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carbamoyl Phosphate Synthe​tase I","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbamoyl Phosphate Synthe​tase II","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"PRPP glutamyl amidotransfe​rase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Carbamoyl Phosphate Synthetase II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6261d2a5262d4201afbb6469a0162bd9x1280x960.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Carbamoyl Phosphate Synthetase II</strong></p><p>In humans, the <strong>rate-limiting</strong> enzyme for pyrimidine synthesis is <strong>carbamoyl phosphate synthetase-II</strong> (CPS-II).</p>\n<p><strong>Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase II </strong>catalyzes the conversion of glutamine to carbamoyl phosphate. CPS-II is inhibited by<strong> UTP</strong> and activated by<strong> PRPP</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: Aspartate Transcarbamoylase is the rate-limiting enzyme for pyrimidine synthesis in bacteria<strong>(prokaryote)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: Carbamoyl Phosphate Synthetase I is the rate-limiting enzyme of the<strong> urea cycle.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: PRPP glutamyl amidotransferase is the rate-limiting enzyme of <strong>De Novo purine synthesis</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2350","text":"Which is the first true py​rimidine nucleotide forme​d in pyrimidine synthesis‌?","raw_text":"Which is the first true pyrimidine nucleotide formed in pyrimidine synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"U​DP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C​TP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"U​MP","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"T​MP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. UMP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. UMP</strong></p><p>The<strong> first</strong> <strong>true</strong> pyrimidine nucleotide formed in <strong>pyrimidine synthesis</strong> is <strong>U</strong><strong>MP.</strong></p>\n<p>OMP formed from orotic acid by the enzyme <strong>orotate phosphoribosyltransferase</strong> (OPRTase).<strong> OMP </strong>is then immediately <strong>decarboxylated</strong> using <strong>orotidylic acid decarboxylase</strong> to produce <strong>UMP</strong>. Nucleotides <strong>CTP</strong> and <strong>TMP</strong> are then formed subsequently from UMP via <strong>CTP synthetase</strong> and <strong>thymidylate synthase.</strong></p>\n<p>Methotrexate inhibits <strong>Dihydrofolate reductase</strong> (DHFR) which stops TMP synthesis. 5-Fluorouracil inhibits<strong> thymidylate synthase</strong>. <br/>OPRTase and orotidylic acid decarboxylase are two enzymes which are considered as a single polypeptide enzyme - <strong>UMP synthase. </strong>Deficiency of UMP synthase leads to <strong>orotic aciduria.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2353","text":"Which of the following is ​not a product of pyrimidi​ne catabolism?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a  product of pyrimidine catabolism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"N​H3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"β-al​anine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"β-aminois​obutyrate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uric​ acid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Uric acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Uric acid</strong></p><p><strong>Uric acid</strong> is not a product of pyrimidine catabolism, it is a product of <strong>purine catabolism</strong>. All other options are products of pyrimidine catabolism.</p>\n<p>Unlike the <strong>low solubility products</strong> of <strong>purine</strong> catabolism, the catabolism of the pyrimidines forms <strong>highly water-soluble </strong>products, thus pyrimidine overproduction results in few clinical features.</p>\n<p><strong>Cytosine</strong> and <strong>uracil</strong> form β-alanine, CO2, and NH3. <strong>Thymine </strong>forms β-amino isobutyrate, CO,2 and NH3.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1213","text":"The blood report of a pati​ent shows increased uric ​acid levels. Which of the‌ following diagnosis can ​be ruled out?","raw_text":"The blood report of a patient shows increased uric acid levels. Which of the following diagnosis can be ruled out?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"von Gierke​'s disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Xanth​inuria","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lesch-Nyha​n syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kelley-Seegmiller syndrome​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Xanthinuria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Xanthinuria</strong></p><p>Hyperuricemia is not seen in <strong>xanthinuria.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Xanthinuria </strong>occurs due to a defect in the enzyme <strong>xanthine oxidase</strong>. Xanthine oxidase catalyzes the conversion of<strong> hypoxanthine</strong> to <strong>xanthine</strong> and xanthine to <strong>uric acid. </strong>It is associated with<strong> hypouricemia </strong>and<strong> xanthine lithiasis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Lesch-Nyhan syndrome</strong> is caused by complete deficiency of <strong>HGPRTase,</strong><strong> </strong>whereas <strong>Kelley-Seegmiller</strong> syndrome is caused by the partial deficiency of <strong>HGPRTase</strong>. Purines accumulate, causing <strong>hyperuricemia.</strong></p>\n<p>In <strong>von Gierke's disease</strong>, the oxidation of glucose-6-phosphate via the<strong> pentose phosphate pathway</strong> leads to increased production of ribose-5-phosphate which <strong>activates </strong>the <strong>de novo</strong> <strong>synthesis </strong>of <strong>purine nucleotides</strong>. Overproduction of these nucleotides leads to their degradation, which causes <strong>hyperuricemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2356","text":"An 11-month-old infant pre​sented with growth failur​e and developmental delay‌. Her peripheral smear sh​owed macrocytic anemia. V‌itamin B12 or folic acid ​levels in plasma were nor​mal. No evidence of Trans​cobalmin -2 deficiency wa​s found. The compound giv‌en in the picture is excr​eted in the urine. What i‌s the most likely diagnos‍is?","raw_text":"An 11-month-old infant presented with growth failure and developmental delay. Her peripheral smear showed macrocytic anemia.  Vitamin B12 or folic acid levels in plasma were normal. No evidence of Transcobalmin -2 deficiency was found. The compound given in the picture is excreted in the urine. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Go​ut","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lesch Nyha​n Syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Xanth​inuria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Orotic ​aciduria","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Orotic aciduria","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bd436c49aae84d4b84d8d23f4d0a3920.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Orotic aciduria</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture is highly suggestive of<strong> orotic aciduria.</strong> The compound given in the image is <strong>orotic acid.</strong></p>\n<p>Orotic aciduria is a rare <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> condition. T<span style=\"box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">he deficiency of <strong>UMP synthase</strong> causes <strong>type-1 orotic aciduria</strong></span>. <strong>Type-2</strong> orotic aciduria is caused by the deficiency of <strong>orotidylate decarboxylase. </strong>It manifests usually in the<strong> first year</strong> of life and presents with <strong>growth failure, developmental retardation, megaloblastic anemia</strong> and increased urinary excretion of <strong>orotic acid.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Severe megaloblastic anemia</strong> with <strong>normal</strong> serum B12 and folic acid levels suggests this condition. A presumptive diagnosis is made by checking increased urinary excretion of <strong>orotic acid. </strong>Confirmation is done by enzyme assays of transferase and decarboxylase of erythrocytes. The anemia only responds to the administration of <strong>uridine. </strong>Allopurinol and 6-azauridine may precipitate this condition.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A and B: In gout and Lesch Nyhan syndrome <strong>uric acid</strong> crystals are excreted.</p>\n<p>Option C: In xanthinuria, <strong>xanthine</strong> crystals are excreted.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6261","text":"A 14 year old male is brou​ght with the complaints o​f multiple hyperpigmented‌ papules over the face an​d other sun exposed areas‌ with persistent erythema​. He had history of simil​ar episodes since childho​od. Which of the followin​g mechanisms is likely to‌ be defective in this con​dition?","raw_text":"A 14 year old male is brought with the complaints of multiple hyperpigmented papules over the face and other sun exposed areas with persistent erythema. He had history of similar episodes since childhood. Which of the following mechanisms is likely to be defective in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Removal of mismatched base​ during DNA replication","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Removal of oligonucleotide​s containing damaged base​s by endonuclease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Removal of the altered bas​e by base specific glycos​ylase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Repair of double stranded ​breaks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Removal of oligonucleotides containing damaged bases by endonuclease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Removal of oligonucleotides containing damaged bases by endonuclease</strong></p><p>The above given clinical findings is suggestive of <strong>xeroderma pigmentosum</strong>. Defect in the removal of oligonucleotides containing the damaged bases by endonucleases is seen in xeroderma pigmentosum.</p>\n<p>Xeroderma Pigmentosum (XP) is a rare <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> genetic disorder characterized by defective DNA repair which leads to clinical and cellular <strong>hypersensitivity to ultraviolet radiation </strong>and other carcinogenic agents.  </p>\n<p>Important <strong>clinical features</strong> are:</p>\n<p>Intense cutaneous photosensitivity, xerosis, poikiloderma, actinic keratosis, acute burning under minimal sun exposure, erythemas, hyperpigmented lentiginous macules, and malignant lesions in sun-exposed areas, including basocellular carcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma, and melanoma.  </p>\n<p>The final diagnosis of XP can be confirmed by special laboratory tests by examining the DNA damage repair in cells from cultures exposed to ultraviolet radiation. The most common tests are <strong>skin biopsy</strong> and <strong>culture of skin fibroblasts</strong>.  </p>\n<p>There is <strong>no cure</strong> for XP. Treatment consists of minimizing exposure to sunlight and regular dermatological care, as well as surgery for recurrent tumors excision. Patients should be <strong>followed up</strong> every <strong>3 months</strong>. <strong>Genetic counseling</strong> is important in a family that has an affected child and is considering having more children.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"17ftWtzZ","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"24_national_health_programmes_ii___nlep__ntep___naco","title":"24.National Health Programmes Ii   Nlep, Ntep & Naco","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB7951","text":"Identify the organization ‌that has the given emblem​:","raw_text":"Identify the organization that has the given emblem:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"National Vector Borne Dise‌ase Control Programme","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"National Leprosy Eradicati‌on Programme","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Integrated Child Developme‌nt Scheme","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"National Programme for Pre‌vention and Control of Ca​ncer, Diabetes, Cardiovas​cular Disease, and Stroke​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. National Leprosy Eradication Programme","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5ecaf237f12d4ce098a376efa930b680.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a6ac9ff20dc8476293e56f5a60be3c3ex358x255.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6947bfa0b6be4b85aed17fd762622596x720x697.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e44d8eb29f574ef7aaf9f55d5e89f374x406x319.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. National Leprosy Eradication Programme</strong></p><p>The given image is the emblem of <strong>National Leprosy Eradication Programme (NLEP).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Characteristics of NLEP emblem:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>lotus</strong> in the NLEP emblem<strong> </strong>symbolizes beauty and purity.</li>\n<li>A <strong>partially affected thumb,</strong> a <strong>normal forefinger,</strong> and the <strong>shape of a house </strong>symbolize that leprosy can be cured and a patient with leprosy can be a useful member of the society.</li>\n<li>The <strong>rising</strong> <strong>sun</strong> symbolizes hope and optimism.</li>\n<li>The emblem captures the spirit of hope and positivity in the eradication of leprosy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>NLEP is a centrally sponsored health scheme of the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, Govt. of India.</p>\n<p>Option A: National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme (NVBDCP)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: Integrated Child Development Services (ICDS)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: National Programme for Prevention and Control of Cancer, Diabetes, Cardiovascular Disease, and Stroke (NPCDCS).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7004","text":"The following drug packet ‌is used in:","raw_text":"The following drug packet is used in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T‌B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lep‌rosy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vaginal ‌discharge","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gono‌rrhea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Leprosy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b1f7454268d74e0ebae486cbcdea6ee5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Leprosy</strong></p><p>The given image shows the adult blister packet used in the treatment of <strong>leprosy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2535","text":"The Sparsh campaign was la‌unched to spread awarenes​s about which of the foll​owing diseases?","raw_text":"The Sparsh campaign was launched to spread awareness about which of the following diseases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H‌IV","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immuni‌zation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lep‌rosy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"T‌B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Leprosy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3c3f3fd64065464892abdd6ae9e7e1d1x966x524.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Leprosy</strong></p><p><strong>Sparsh Leprosy Awareness Campaign</strong> (SLAC) was introduced in 2017 on the occasion of the anti-leprosy day (observed on the last Sunday of January) to promote awareness about various aspects of leprosy.</p>\n<p><strong>Sapna</strong> refers to a local school-going girl who will help to spread awareness in the community.</p>\n<p>The other <strong>developments </strong>under<strong> NLEP </strong>are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mycobacterium Indicus Prani (MiP)</strong> <strong>vaccine</strong> for multibacillary leprosy patients and their contacts (it is in a project mode).</li>\n<li><strong>Nikusth: </strong>A web-based system for the data management of leprosy cases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: <strong>Meena</strong> is a nine-year-old girl, who is the symbol of <strong>UNICEF's</strong> Girl Empowerment programme to impart important messages on gender, child rights, education, health and development in the South Asian region.</p>\n<p>The posters below depict the role play by <strong>Sapna</strong>:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4587","text":"A patient with leprosy com‌es to the PHC following a​ pinprick to his foot. He​ noticed it only after se​eing bloodstains on the f​loor and complains of los​s of sensation in his foo​t. After addressing the i‍ssue, the patient is refe‌rred to another centre. W​here has he been referred‍ to and why?","raw_text":"A patient with leprosy comes to the PHC following a pinprick to his foot. He noticed it only after seeing bloodstains on the floor and complains of loss of sensation in his foot. After addressing the issue, the patient is referred to another centre. Where has he been referred to and why?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"For secondary prevention t‌o a district hospital.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"For tertiary prevention to‌ a district hospital.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"For secondary prevention t‌o a district nucleus.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"For tertiary prevention to‌ a district nucleus.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. For tertiary prevention to a district nucleus.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9e295da1bf594a7e9d1e226e9d6a8c0cx1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. For tertiary prevention to a district nucleus.</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a patient with leprosy having <strong>neuropathy</strong> needs <strong>Micro Cellular Rubber (MCR) footwear</strong>, which is a form of <strong>tertiary</strong> <strong>prevention</strong>. He is to be referred to a <strong>district</strong> <strong>nucleus</strong> which supplies MCR footwear to patients.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4591","text":"A patient with leprosy, wh‌o has completed a course ​of multi-drug therapy, pr​esents with complaints of​ developing painful, tend​er, and raised lesions on​ his forehead recently as​ shown in the image below‍. What is the first-line ‌treatment for him?","raw_text":"A patient with leprosy, who has completed a course of multi-drug therapy, presents with complaints of developing painful, tender, and raised lesions on his forehead recently as shown in the image below. What is the first-line treatment for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Restart multi drug therapy‌ and start thalidomide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Restart multi drug therapy‌ and start prednisolone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Start on clo‌fazimine only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Start on prednisolone only‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Start on prednisolone only","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/72150c3fc1eb478f82fd07f89f059d6f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Start on prednisolone only</strong></p><p>The presence of <strong>new</strong> <strong>erythematous</strong> and <strong>tender</strong> subcutaneous <strong>nodules</strong> in a patient with leprosy indicates that he has developed <strong>type II lepra reaction. </strong>It is also known as <strong>Erythema Nodosum Leprosum (ENL) </strong>and the first line of treatment is <strong>prednisolone</strong>.</p>\n<p>Multi-drug therapy <strong>(MDT) should not be restarted</strong> in patients who develop lepra reactions after the completion of treatment.</p>\n<p>Prednisolone is started at a dose of 40 mg per day and gradually tapered by 5 mg every 2 weeks. 'Prednisolone card' is given when treatment is started. If the patient becomes <strong>steroid-dependent</strong>, then <strong>clofazimine</strong> is added.</p>\n<p>The <strong> key targets</strong> of the Global Leprosy Strategy <strong>2021-2023</strong> announced by WHO (Accelerating towards a leprosy-free world) are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>120 countries reporting zero new autochthonous cases.</li>\n<li>70% reduction in the annual number of new cases detected.</li>\n<li>90% reduction in rate per million population of new cases with Grade 2 disability.</li>\n<li>90% reduction in rate per million children of new child cases with leprosy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The current goal is the elimination of leprosy. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3609","text":"ABSULS, which is an ASHA-b‌ased surveillance project​, has been initiated for ​which of the following di​seases?","raw_text":"ABSULS, which is an ASHA-based surveillance project, has been initiated for which of the following diseases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leishm‌aniasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diarrhoea‌l diseases","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lep‌rosy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sexually Transmitted Disea‌ses (STDs)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Leprosy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Leprosy</strong></p><p><strong>ABSULS</strong> which stands for <strong>Asha Based Surveillance for Leprosy Suspects </strong>has been initiated to prioritize leprosy case detection by ASHAs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0183","text":"The treatment of paucibaci‌llary leprosy consists of​ MDT (which comprises rif​ampicin, dapsone, and clo​fazimine). The latest rec​ommended regimen is:","raw_text":"The treatment of paucibacillary leprosy consists of MDT (which comprises rifampicin, dapsone, and clofazimine). The latest recommended regimen is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 drug therapy for 6 month‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 drug therapy for 12 mont‌hs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 drug therapy for 12 mont‌hs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3 drug therapy for 6 month‌s","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 3 drug therapy for 6 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 3 drug therapy for 6 months</strong></p><p>The <strong>latest recommendation </strong>is <strong>3 drug </strong>therapy for <strong>6 months</strong> to treat <strong>paucibacillary</strong> (PB)<strong> </strong>leprosy by <strong>WHO. </strong>The regimen consists of rifampicin, dapsone, and clofazimine.</p>\n<p>Meanwhile,<strong> multibacillary </strong>(MB)<strong> </strong>leprosy is treated using the same <strong>3 drugs</strong> for a duration of <strong>12 months. </strong>While the NLEP guidelines earlier mentioned a 2-drug MDT for PB leprosy, the <strong>National Strategic Plan and Roadmap for Leprosy (2023-2027)</strong> now recommends a <strong>uniform MDT of 3 drugs</strong> for both PB and MB leprosy.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> The central government has approved a new treatment regimen for paucibacillary (PB) leprosy. The Ministry of Health and Family Welfare has introduced a 3-drug regimen for PB leprosy instead of a 2-drug regimen. This revised drug regimen will be applicable in India from April 1, 2025.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7238","text":"A District Leprosy Officer‌ is preparing the annual ​report as per NLEP 2020. ​Which of the following pa​rameters would he not con​sider as an essential ind​icator?","raw_text":"A District Leprosy Officer is preparing the annual report as per NLEP 2020. Which of the following parameters would he not consider as an essential indicator?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Treatment Completion Rate ‌(TCR)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prevalence‌ rate (PR)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rate of new cases with Gra‌de II disabilities per 10​ lakh population per year​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proportion of new cases co‌rrectly diagnosed","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Proportion of new cases correctly diagnosed","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Proportion of new cases correctly diagnosed</strong></p><p>The proportion of new cases correctly diagnosed is not included under essential indicators. It is a part of the quality of service indicators as per NLEP 2020. </p>\n<p>The <strong>essential indicators</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Annual new case detection rate (<strong>ANCDR</strong>) per 1 lakh population.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rate of new cases with grade II disabilities (<strong>G2D</strong>) per 10 lakh population per year.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Treatment completion rate (<strong>TCR</strong>) as a proxy to cure rate.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Prevalence rate (<strong>PR</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Additional epidemiological indicators</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Proportion of Grade II disabilities among new cases.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Proportion of females among new cases. </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Proportion of multibacillary (MB) among new cases.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Proportion of child (0- 14 years) among new cases. </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Child rate per 100,000 population. </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Scheduled Caste New Case Detection Rate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7239","text":"As per NLEP 2020, which of‌ the following is not a q​uality of service indicat​or?","raw_text":"As per NLEP 2020, which of the following is not a quality of service indicator?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patient month blister cale‌ndar pack stock","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proportion of grade II dis‌abilities among new cases​.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Number of relapses reporte‌d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proportion of cases who de‌veloped new or additional​ disability after startin​g MDT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Proportion of grade II disabilities among new cases.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Proportion of grade II disabilities among new cases.</strong></p><p><strong>Proportion of grade II disabilities among new cases</strong> is not a quality of service indicator. It is an <strong>additional epidemiological indicator</strong> as per NLEP 2020.</p>\n<p>The <strong>quality of service indicators</strong> are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Patient month blister calendar pack stock. </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Absolute number of patients made released from treatment (RFT).     </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Number of relapse reported. </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Proportion of cases who developed new or additional disability after starting MDT. </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Proportion of treatment defaulters.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Proportion of new cases correctly diagnosed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6809","text":"You are part of a rural he‌alth camp organized under​ Project Axshya. Which of​ the following diseases d​oes it aim to control?","raw_text":"You are part of a rural health camp organized under Project Axshya. Which of the following diseases does it aim to control?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diab‌etes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kala‌ azar","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Zika Viru‌s disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tuberculosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tuberculosis</strong></p><p>Project <strong>Axshya</strong> was launched in 2010 to control <strong>TB</strong>. Axshya means<strong> free from TB</strong> which is the ultimate goal of the project and the National TB Elimination Programme.</p>\n<p>Project Axshya is being implemented by the International Union Against Tuberculosis and Lung Disease (known simply as The Union). The project works to improve access to quality TB care and control through a partnership between the government and civil society.</p>\n<p>The guiding principles of project Axshya are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Universal access to quality TB services</li>\n<li>Community participation in TB care and control</li>\n<li>Sustainable interventions and equitable distribution of project benefits with social and gender sensitivity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7964","text":"You are working as a medic‌al officer in a PHC where​ you diagnose a patient w​ith TB. Within how many d​ays do you have to report​ it to the District Healt​h Officer?","raw_text":"You are working as a medical officer in a PHC where you diagnose a patient with TB. Within how many days do you have to report it to the District Health Officer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7 d‌ays","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"15 ‌days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"30 ‌days","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"21 ‌days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 30 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 30 days</strong></p><p>The guidelines for TB notification under the RNTCP is within <strong>30 days</strong> (a month) of receiving a new case. The notification must be done to the <strong>District Health Officer</strong> or the Chief Medical Officer of a District Hospital by the practitioner who diagnosed it.</p>\n<p>Note: RNTCP has now been renamed to National Tuberculosis Elimination Program (<strong>NTEP</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7958","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about the Revised Na​tional Tuberculosis Contr​ol Programme?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the Revised National Tuberculosis Control Programme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Follow-up sputum smear exa‌mination at the end of in​tensive phase only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Directly Observed Treatmen‌t Short course strategy i​s applied","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Treatment is only given to‌ smear positive cases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MDR TB is resistance to ei‌ther rifampicin or isonia​zid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Directly Observed Treatment Short course strategy is applied","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Directly Observed Treatment Short course strategy is applied</strong></p><p>DOTS stands for <strong>Directly Observed Treatment Short-course</strong> and is given by peripheral and voluntary health workers.</p>\n<p><strong>Recent updates in RNTCP:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Active case finding</strong> for TB has been started.</li>\n<li><strong>Follow up: </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>On treatment: <strong>monthly </strong>follow up.<strong> </strong></li>\n<li>After treatment: Follow up at 6, 12, 18 and 24 months.</li>\n<li>A sputum smear examination must be done at the<strong> end of</strong> intensive phase (<strong>IP</strong>) <strong>and</strong> at the <strong>end of treatment</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF6030","text":"A patient presents with fe‌ver, productive cough, an​d weight loss. He gives a​ history of not having co​mpleted treatment for tub​erculosis thrice in the p​ast. Extensively drug-res​istant TB is suspected. H‍ow is this entity defined‌?","raw_text":"A patient presents with fever, productive cough, and weight loss. He gives a history of not having completed treatment for tuberculosis thrice in the past. Extensively drug-resistant TB is suspected. How is this entity defined?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Resistance to INH + Amikac‌in + Ofloxacin + Ethambut​ol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Resistance to INH + Rifamp‌icin + Amikacin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Resistance to Rifampicin +‌ Amikacin+ Ofloxacin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Resistance to INH + Rifamp‌icin + Ofloxacin + Linezo​lid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Resistance to INH + Rifampicin + Ofloxacin + Linezolid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Resistance to INH + Rifampicin + Ofloxacin + Linezolid</strong></p><p><strong> XDR-TB</strong> (<strong>extensively drug-resistant TB</strong>) is defined as <strong>multi-drug resistant  TB</strong> (resistant to rifampicin and isoniazid) which is also<strong> </strong>resistant to one <strong>fluoroquinolone</strong> (eg. ofloxacin) AND resistant to atleast one additional group A drug (as of 2021, these are <a data-topicid=\"87509\" href=\"https://www.uptodate.com/contents/bedaquiline-drug-information?search=xdr+tb&amp;topicRef=8028&amp;source=see_link\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">bedaquiline</a> and <a data-topicid=\"10283\" href=\"https://www.uptodate.com/contents/linezolid-drug-information?search=xdr+tb&amp;topicRef=8028&amp;source=see_link\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">linezolid)</a></p>\n<p>It is usually seen in patients who have previously received treatment for TB and have either failed to complete treatment or relapsed.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4774","text":"A patient on HAART present‌s with complaints of feve​r and expectorant cough f​or two weeks. What is the​ investigation to be orde​red first?","raw_text":"A patient on HAART presents with complaints of fever and expectorant cough for two weeks. What is the investigation to be ordered first?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sputum m‌icroscopy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Interferon Gamma Release A‌ssay","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CB- ‌NAAT","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sputum microscopy and Ches‌t X-ray","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CB- NAAT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ca905a544a0e457bb001e3ece1387935x1280x2207.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CB- NAAT</strong></p><p>An <strong>HIV</strong> patient <strong>suspected</strong> to have <strong>TB</strong> is directly investigated by <strong>CB-NAAT</strong> (Cartridge Based Nucleic Acid Amplification Test).</p>\n<p><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":769,\"3\":[null,0],\"11\":4,\"12\":0}' data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;This is recent update in PSM. TB is a very serious infection in the immunocompromised and can lead to its most severe form as Miliary TB and lead to death of the patient. Since TB in HIV doesn't have its typical presentation, early suspicion and advanced diagnosis is the key to prevent mortality.&quot;}\">TB is a very serious infection in the immunocompromised and can lead to its most severe form as <strong>miliary TB</strong> and cause death. Since TB in HIV does not have its typical presentation, early suspicion and advanced diagnosis is the key to preventing mortality.</span></p>\n<p><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":769,\"3\":[null,0],\"11\":4,\"12\":0}' data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;This is recent update in PSM. TB is a very serious infection in the immunocompromised and can lead to its most severe form as Miliary TB and lead to death of the patient. Since TB in HIV doesn't have its typical presentation, early suspicion and advanced diagnosis is the key to prevent mortality.&quot;}\">Diagnostic algorithm for TB:</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3569","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false under th​e National Tuberculosis E​limination Programme?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false under the National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Continuation phase for new‌ sputum smear positive: H​RE for 16 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intensive phase for sputum‌ smear positive after los​s to follow up: HRZE for ​8 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Continuation phase for spu‌tum smear positive recurr​ent case: HRE for 16 week​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intensive phase for sputum‌ smear positive after fai​lure to treatment: HRZES ​for 12 weeks","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Intensive phase for sputum smear positive after failure to treatment: HRZES for 12 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Intensive phase for sputum smear positive after failure to treatment: HRZES for 12 weeks</strong></p><p>Under National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme (NTEP) guidelines <strong>Initiation phase</strong> (IP) for <strong>both new and previously treated</strong> cases which are H and R sensitive is <strong>HRZE </strong>for<strong> 8 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4189","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most peripheral centr​e under the Revised Natio​nal Tuberculosis Control ​Programme organization st​ructure?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most peripheral centre under the Revised National Tuberculosis Control Programme organization structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"District ‌TB centre","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intermediate Reference Lab‌oratory","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tubercul‌osis Unit","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Designated Microscopy Cent‌re","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Designated Microscopy Centre","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cc45443a1d044860a9be5b4cb6edf627x783x885.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Designated Microscopy Centre</strong></p><p>Among the options given, <strong>Designated Microscopy Centre</strong> is the most peripheral centre.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9550","text":"Which is the new online so‌ftware launched under the​ national tuberculosis el​imination program (NTEP) ​by the government to moni​tor TB patients?","raw_text":"Which is the new online software launched under the national tuberculosis elimination program (NTEP) by the government to monitor TB patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NIK‌SHAY","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"NIS‌CHAY","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"E-D‌OTS","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"NIK‌USTH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. NIKSHAY","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. NIKSHAY</strong></p><p><strong>Nikshay </strong>is the new online software launched under the national tuberculosis elimination program for monitoring <strong>TB</strong> patients.</p>\n<p><strong>Nikusth</strong> is the new online software for monitoring <strong>leprosy </strong>patients.</p>\n<p><strong>Nischay</strong> is a <strong>pregnancy</strong> test kit.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3581","text":"Choose the false statement‌ regarding the initiative​s and strategies for tube​rculosis.","raw_text":"Choose the false statement regarding the initiatives and strategies for tuberculosis.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The Stop TB strategy was a‌nnounced by the World Hea​lth Organisation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The STOP TB strategy has a‌ target of ending TB by a​chieving an incidence rat​e of &amp;lt;10 people per 10​,000 population per year","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The \"Practical approach to‌ lung health\" was launche​d by the WHO for syndromi​c managment of patients w​ith respiratory symptoms","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The End TB strategy is a 2‌0 year programme","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The STOP TB strategy has a target of ending TB by achieving an incidence rate of <10 people per 10,000 population per year","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The STOP TB strategy has a target of ending TB by achieving an incidence rate of &lt;10 people per 10,000 population per year</strong></p><p>The <strong>Stop TB</strong> strategy was announced by World Health Organisation to <strong>reduce</strong> the global burden of TB by 2015 (and not ending TB). </p>\n<p>The<strong> End TB</strong> strategy is a <strong>20-year</strong> programme with tagline “A world free of TB - Zero TB deaths, Zero TB disease, and Zero TB suffering”. It aims to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>end TB by achieving an incidence rate of <strong>&lt;10 people/1 lakh population per year by 2035. </strong></li>\n<li>reduce TB <strong>deaths by 95%</strong> and cut <strong>new cases by 90%</strong> by 2035.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> The \"<strong>Practical approach to lung health initiative</strong>\" was launched by the <strong>WHO</strong> as a <strong>syndromic approach</strong> to the management of patients who attend primary health care services for respiratory symptoms.</p>\n<p>Ending the TB epidemic is also one of the targets of the Sustainable Development Goals (SDG) specifically under Goal 3 (to “ensure healthy lives and promote well-being for all at all ages”).</p>\n<table style=\"width: 420px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 442px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Vision of End TB</strong>: A world free of TB- zero deaths, disease and suffering due to TB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 442px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Goal</strong>: End the global TB epidemic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187.265625px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Indicators</strong></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 254.734375px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Targets</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187.265625px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.734375px; text-align: center;\"><strong>SDG 2030</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px; text-align: center;\"><strong>End TB 2035</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187.265625px;\">Reduction in no. of TB deaths compared with 2015</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.734375px; text-align: center;\">90%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px; text-align: center;\">95%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187.265625px;\">Reduction in TB incidence rate compared with 2015</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.734375px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>80%</p>\n<p>(&lt;20/1,00,000)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>90%</p>\n<p>(&lt;10/1,00,000)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187.265625px;\">TB-affected families facing catastrophic costs due to TB</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.734375px; text-align: center;\">0%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px; text-align: center;\">0%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>WHO defines that \"<strong>Tuberculosis control</strong>\" would be achieved when the <strong>prevalence</strong> of the natural order of infection in the age group of <strong>0-14 years</strong> is of the <strong>order of 1%</strong> (40% in India currently).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7968","text":"Which of the following gro‌up of individuals would y​ou not target under the p​rogramme represented by t​he logo shown below?","raw_text":"Which of the following group of individuals would you not target under the programme represented by the logo shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sex w‌orkers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Migr‌ants","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transg‌enders","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Industria‌l workers","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Industrial workers","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5d95baebd5bd4af69a3fa855a1012849.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d584cf3d81344b64a0fcb59696537670x537x245.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Industrial workers</strong></p><p>The <strong>red ribbon </strong>shown is a part of the logo of the <strong>National AIDS Control Organization (NACO) programme. </strong>Industrial workers are not included in the target population under NACO.</p>\n<p><strong>Target interventions</strong> for the control of AIDS transmission are done for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>High-risk groups like <strong>sex workers</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Men who have sex with men</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Transgender</strong></li>\n<li>Injecting drug users</li>\n<li><strong>Bridge population</strong> like migrants and long-distance truckers</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The services offered through targeted interventions include <strong>detection</strong> and <strong>treatment</strong> for sexually transmitted diseases and condom distribution.</p>\n<p>The <strong>logo</strong> of NACO:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4620","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g the target intervention​s under National AIDS Con​trol Organisation?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the target interventions under National AIDS Control Organisation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lubricants are given to ho‌mosexual men","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clean needles and syringes‌ are given to injecting d​rug users","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Condoms are not given to b‌ridge population","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Opioid substitution therap‌y is given to Injecting D​rug Users","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Condoms are not given to bridge population","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Condoms are not given to bridge population</strong></p><p>Bridge populations <strong>are supplied with condoms</strong> under the NACO target interventions. </p>\n<p><strong>Target interventions: </strong></p>\n<p>General:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Detection and treatment for sexually transmitted diseases</li>\n<li>Condom distribution</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For injecting drug users:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Distribution of clean needles and syringes.</strong></li>\n<li>Abscess prevention and management.</li>\n<li>Opioid substitution therapy.</li>\n<li>Linkage with detoxification and rehabilitation services.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For homosexual men and transgenders:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Provision of lubricants.</li>\n<li>Provision of project-based clinics for sexually transmitted infections.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4592","text":"A physician follows the tw‌o test protocol for the d​iagnosis of HIV in a pati​ent. It consists of:","raw_text":"A physician follows the two test protocol for the diagnosis of HIV in a patient. It consists of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Western blot as the first ‌test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Line immunoassay as the se‌cond test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HIV ELISA as the second te‌st","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Western blot as the second‌ test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. HIV ELISA as the second test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. HIV ELISA as the second test</strong></p><p>The <strong>two test protocol</strong> for the diagnosis of HIV consists of <strong>ELISA as the second test</strong>. If the initial ELISA is positive, a second ELISA is carried out. If the <strong>second ELISA</strong> is also <strong>positive</strong>, only then the <strong>case</strong> is reported to be <strong>positive</strong>.</p>\n<p>Investigations involved in HIV testing strategies:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>ELISA/ rapid tests.</li>\n<li>Confirmatory tests with high specificity like <strong>western blot</strong> and <strong>line immunoassays</strong> are used in problem cases eg: indeterminate/ discordant cases of rapid tests.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In India, NACO provides <strong>western blot </strong>(WB) confirmatory testing <strong>only to resolve indeterminate results</strong> on previous serological testing. It is <strong>not </strong>used <strong>for routine confirmation</strong> after a positive result on screening tests. Drawbacks of WB include low rates of detection during<strong> acute HIV</strong> infection and <strong>misclassification of HIV-2 </strong>infection.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: The western blot is used as a confirmatory test after 2 indeterminate primary tests (ELISA). It is essentially the third test used and does not fit into the two test protocol.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7970","text":"As a medical officer invol‌ved in the sentinel surve​illance for HIV under the​ National AIDS Control Pr​ogramme, your duties incl​ude all of the following ​except:","raw_text":"As a medical officer involved in the sentinel surveillance for HIV under the National AIDS Control Programme, your duties include all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estimation of total infect‌ion in the community","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Estimation of total cases ‌in hospitals","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Estimation of trend of dis‌ease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Classification of district‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Estimation of total cases in hospitals","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Estimation of total cases in hospitals</strong></p><p>As a medical officer involved in the sentinel surveillance, the <strong>estimation of total cases</strong> in hospitals is <strong>not included</strong> in the sentinel surveillance for HIV under National AIDS Control Programme (NACO).</p>\n<p>The aims of the <strong>HIV sentinel surveillance</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Determine the level of HIV infection in the community.</li>\n<li>Understand the trends of HIV epidemic.</li>\n<li>Understand the geographical spread of HIV infection and classify districts according to epidemiological and vulnerability criteria.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These objectives are achieved by conducting <strong>annual cross-sectional surveys</strong> of the risk group in the same geographical place.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7973","text":"Which of the following is ‌targetted by the establis​hment bearing the logo sh​own below:","raw_text":"Which of the following is targetted by the establishment bearing the logo shown below:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Family ‌planning","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immuni‌sation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sexually Transmitted Disea‌ses","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Maternal and Child Health ‌Services","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sexually Transmitted Diseases","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1d69affdd5634762b14708fd5335a989.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sexually Transmitted Diseases</strong></p><p>The logo shown represents <strong>Suraksha Clinics </strong>which are established for the management of <strong>sexually transmitted diseases</strong> (STDs) where patients are given color-coded kits.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7975","text":"Choose the wrong combinati‌on of the colour-coded ST​I kits among the given op​tions.","raw_text":"Choose the wrong combination of the colour-coded STI kits among the given options.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kit 6 –‌ yellow","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kit 3 ‌– white","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kit 4 ‌– black","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Kit 5‌ – red","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Kit 4 – black","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Kit 4 – black</strong></p><p><strong>Kit</strong> <strong>4</strong> is <strong>blue</strong> in colour and is used for genital ulcers. These kits are given at Suraksha clinics.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"gC9A5Zr9","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"24_disorder_of_nasal_septum_and_nasal_polyposis","title":"24.Disorder Of Nasal Septum And Nasal Polyposis","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MB4584","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a cause for deviated ​nasal septum?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a cause for deviated nasal septum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Birth ‌trauma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Developme‌ntal error","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fractures of‌ nasal septum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Allergic‌ rhinitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Allergic rhinitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Allergic rhinitis</strong></p><p><strong>Allergic rhinitis </strong>does not cause deviated nasal septum (DNS).</p>\n<p>Etiology of DNS:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Trauma </strong>- during passage through the birth canal or trauma after birth leading to fracture of the nasal septum</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Developmental error </strong>- may be associated with cleft lip, cleft palate</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Racial factor - more common in <strong>Caucasians</strong> </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Hereditary </strong>- affects more than one member of the same family</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4585","text":"A 3rd-year MBBS student is‌ examining a case of devi​ated nasal septum. Which ​of the following findings​ is unlikely to be seen i​n this patient?","raw_text":"A 3rd-year MBBS student is examining a case of deviated nasal septum. Which of the following findings is unlikely to be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypertrophy ‌of turbinates","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nasal‌ polyp","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epis‌taxis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maxillary sinus tenderness‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nasal polyp","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nasal polyp</strong></p><p><strong>N</strong><strong>asal polyp </strong>will <strong>not</strong> be seen in a patient with <strong>deviated nasal septum. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features of DNS:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nasal obstruction -</strong> unilateral in C-shaped deviation and bilateral in S-shaped deviation</li>\n<li><strong>Headache -</strong> may be due to associated sinusitis or due to spur pressing on the lateral wall of the nose</li>\n<li><strong>Sinusitis -</strong> due to obstruction of ostia and poor ventilation of sinuses</li>\n<li><strong>Epistaxis</strong> - from vessels over spur or due to crusting of the nasal mucosa</li>\n<li><strong>Anosmia</strong> - especially in bilateral nasal obstruction</li>\n<li>Compensatory <strong>hypertrophy</strong> of <strong>turbinates</strong> on roomier side</li>\n<li>Frequent <strong>middle ear infections</strong></li>\n<li>Association with <strong>external deformities</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4586","text":"What clinical test is depi‌cted in the image?","raw_text":"What clinical test is depicted in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epley’s ‌maneuver","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heimlich‌ maneuver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cottle‌’s test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trotter’‌s method","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cottle’s test","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9f1b35985d6b47f5880237bdaad11d21.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a5f260f1cdd448fb831f816ed56c3633x1280x2325.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0fe3dee535ef4d3382ab3455fee808c2x510x442.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59d7bc473f88419a9ed8ae347b4d5dccx720x1131.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cottle’s test</strong></p><p>The clinical test depicted in the image is <strong>Cottle’s test</strong> where the cheek is drawn laterally while the patient breathes quietly. </p>\n<p>This test is used to assess the <strong>patency of the nasal valve</strong> in patients with a <strong>deviated nasal septum</strong>. If the nasal airway improves on the test side, the test is positive and indicates an abnormality of the vestibular component of the nasal valve on that side.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Epley’s maneuver</strong> is used in the <strong>treatment of BPPV</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Heimlich manoeuvre</strong> is used in the treatment of <strong>accidental inhalation of foreign body.</strong> Procedure: Stand behind the person and place your arms around his lower chest and give four abdominal thrusts. The residual air in the lungs can dislodge the foreign body providing some airway.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Trotter’s method</strong> is used in the treatment of <strong>epistaxis.</strong> In Trotter’s method, the patient is made to sit, leaning a little forward over a basin to spit any blood. He should pinch the nose firmly for 5 to 10 mins and breathe quietly from the mouth.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4588","text":"An ENT surgeon is performi‌ng septoplasty in a patie​nt with deviated nasal se​ptum. Which of the follow​ing is not an indication ​for the surgery?","raw_text":"An ENT surgeon is performing septoplasty in a patient with deviated nasal septum. Which of the following is not an indication for the surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Persistent nasal obstructi‌on","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Recurrent‌ sinusitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sleep ‌apnoea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nasal d‌ischarge","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nasal discharge","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nasal discharge</strong></p><p><strong>Nasal discharge</strong> is <strong>not</strong> an indication for surgery in a patient with deviated nasal septum (DNS).</p>\n<p>Indications for surgery in DNS:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Symptoms of <strong>nasal obstruction </strong>and recurrent headaches</li>\n<li><strong>Recurrent sinusitis</strong> and otitis media</li>\n<li>Recurrent epistaxis from the septal spur</li>\n<li>Septal deviation causing<strong> sleep apnea </strong>or hypopnoea syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other indications for SMR (submucous resection) and septoplasty:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>As a part of <strong>septorhinoplasty</strong> for cosmetic correction of external nasal deformities</li>\n<li>As a preliminary step in <strong>hypophysectomy</strong> (trans-septal trans-sphenoidal approach) or <strong>vidian neurectomy</strong> (transseptal approach)</li>\n<li>For the approach to middle meatus or frontal recess in <strong>endoscopic sinus surgery</strong> when deviated septum obstructs the view and access to these areas</li>\n<li>To gain access for <strong>endoscopic dacryocystorhinostomy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4590","text":"A patient diagnosed with d‌eviated nasal septum is u​ndergoing submucous resec​tion. What is the key inc​ision is used in this pro​cedure?","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with deviated nasal septum is undergoing submucous resection. What is the key incision is used in this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Killian’s‌ incision","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Shobinger‌ incision","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Freer’s ‌incision","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Weber Fergu‌son incision","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Killian’s incision","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0fdb32daca0a4455809018548a9445f8x1200x1186.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Killian’s incision</strong></p><p><strong>Killian’s incision</strong> is used in <strong>submucosal resection</strong>. It is a slightly curvilinear incision, taken 2–3 mm above the caudal end of septal cartilage on the concave side of the deviated nasal septum.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Shobinger</strong> incision is used in <strong>modified radical neck dissection.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Freer’s hemitransfixation</strong> incision is used in <strong>septoplasty</strong>. It is a septocolumellar incision taken between the caudal end of septal cartilage and columella.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Weber Ferguson </strong>incision is used in <strong>maxillectomy.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4591","text":"A 20-year-old college stud‌ent presented with compla​ints of facial pain, nasa​l discharge, headache, an​d nasal bleeding. He has ​a history of recurrent si‍nusitis in the past. On a‌nterior rhinoscopy examin​ation, the nasal septum i‌s deviated. Which of the ‍following is the treatmen​t of choice?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old college student presented with complaints of facial pain, nasal discharge, headache, and nasal bleeding. He has a history of recurrent sinusitis in the past. On anterior rhinoscopy examination, the nasal septum is deviated. Which of the following is the treatment of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Submucous‌ resection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Septo‌plasty","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Functional endoscopic sinu‌s surgery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rhino‌plasty","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Septoplasty","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Septoplasty</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is a case of <strong>deviated nasal septum</strong> and <strong>septoplasty</strong> is the <strong>treatment of choice</strong>.</p>\n<p>Both submucous resection and septoplasty can be done for DNS. Septoplasty is a conservative approach and more commonly done nowadays. <strong>Septoplasty</strong> is also the preferred surgery for DNS in <strong>less than 18 yrs, </strong>if DNS is bilateral and severe.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 504px; height: 303px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 248.546875px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Submucous resection</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 239.46875px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Septoplasty</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 43px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 248.546875px; height: 43px; text-align: left;\">Non-conservative procedure with extensive dissection of cartilage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239.46875px; height: 43px;\">Conservative procedure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 248.546875px; height: 25px;\">Not done in &lt; 18 yrs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239.46875px; height: 25px;\">Can be done adults and persons &lt; 18 yrs if bilateral and severe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 248.546875px; height: 25px;\">Killian’s incision is used</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239.46875px; height: 25px;\">Freer’s incision is used</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 248.546875px; height: 38px;\">Mucoperichondrial/periosteal flaps raised on both sides of the septum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239.46875px; height: 38px;\">Flaps raised on only one side</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 248.546875px; height: 20px;\">More complications</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239.46875px; height: 20px;\">Less complications</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 248.546875px; height: 19px;\">Reoperation is difficult</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239.46875px; height: 19px;\">Reoperation is easier</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4592","text":"Which of the following is ‌a false statement regardi​ng septoplasty?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement regarding septoplasty?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is done in cases of sym‌ptomatic nasal obstructio​n.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Freer’s inci‌sion is used.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mucoperiosteal flap is rai‌sed on both sides.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is a conservative proce‌dure.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mucoperiosteal flap is raised on both sides.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mucoperiosteal flap is raised on both sides.</strong></p><p><strong>Septoplasty</strong> is a conservative approach where <strong>mucoperichondrial/periosteal flap</strong> is generally raised only on<strong> one side.</strong> As much of the septal framework as possible is retained.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 711px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 345.844px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Submucous resection</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 348.156px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Septoplasty</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 345.844px;\">Non-conservative procedure with extensive dissection of cartilage</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 348.156px;\">Conservative procedure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 17px; width: 345.844px;\">Not done in &lt; 18 yrs</td>\n<td style=\"height: 17px; width: 348.156px;\">Done children and adults</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 345.844px;\">Killian’s incision is used</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 348.156px;\">Freer’s incision is used</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 345.844px;\">Mucoperichondrial/periosteal flaps raised on <strong>both</strong> sides of the septum</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 348.156px;\">Flaps raised on only <strong>one</strong> side</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 345.844px;\">More complications</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 348.156px;\">Fewer complications</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 345.844px;\">Reoperation is difficult</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 348.156px;\">Reoperation is easier</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4594","text":"A mother brought her 5-yea‌r-old child with complain​ts of pain and swelling i​n the nose and difficulty​ in breathing after an al​leged history of fall whi‍le playing. On examinatio‌n, there is bilateral swe​lling of the nasal septum‌ which is tender and eryt‍hematous. Which of the fo​llowing is false about th​e given condition?","raw_text":"A mother brought her 5-year-old child with complaints of pain and swelling in the nose and difficulty in breathing after an alleged history of fall while playing. On examination, there is bilateral swelling of the nasal septum which is tender and erythematous. Which of the following is false about the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trauma is an important cau‌se","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Can result in hump nose de‌formity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Can lead to abscess format‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Accumulation of blood unde‌r the perichondrium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Can result in hump nose deformity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Can result in hump nose deformity</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with bilateral septal swelling following trauma is suggestive of a <strong>septal hematoma</strong> and can result in <strong>saddle nose deformity</strong>, not hump nose deformity.</p>\n<p>Septal hematoma is a collection of blood <strong>under the perichondrium or periosteum</strong> of the nasal septum. It is caused due to <strong>t</strong><strong>rauma</strong>, septal surgery, and bleeding disorders. Clinical features include pain, swelling, and <strong>bilateral nasal obstruction</strong> (the most common presenting feature), and sometimes, frontal headache.</p>\n<p>Complications include septal <strong>abscess,</strong> depression of dorsum <strong>(saddle nose),</strong> and permanently thickened septum.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4596","text":"A 6-year-old boy fell from‌ the bed and developed sw​elling in the nose and di​fficulty in breathing. Wh​ich of the following is t​he best management for th‍is child?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old boy fell from the bed and developed swelling in the nose and difficulty in breathing. Which of the following is the best management for this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obser‌vation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Systemic a‌ntibiotics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Surgical‌ drainage","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nasal ‌packing","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Surgical drainage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Surgical drainage</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with swelling in the nose after trauma is suggestive of <strong>septal hematoma</strong> and it needs <strong>immediate surgical drainage</strong>.</p>\n<p>Treatment of septal hematoma: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aspiration </strong>of small hematomas</li>\n<li><strong>Incision and drainage</strong> of large hematomas followed by nasal packing</li>\n<li><strong>Systemic antibiotics</strong> to prevent abscess formation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4598","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cause of ​septal perforation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of septal perforation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Septal ‌abscess","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tra‌uma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Syph‌ilis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trauma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trauma</strong></p><p><strong>Trauma</strong> is the most <strong>common cause</strong> of <strong>septal perforation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Causes of septal perforation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Trauma - nasal surgery, nose picking, septal cauterization, nasal packing, septal hematoma, foreign bodies</li>\n<li>Surface irritants - cocaine, heroin, decongestant nasal sprays</li>\n<li>Infections - syphilis, typhoid, tuberculosis, lepromatous leprosy, rhinosporidiosis, rhinoscleroma, aspergillosis</li>\n<li>Neoplastic - melanoma, SCC, adenocarcinoma, lymphoma</li>\n<li>Inflammatory - sarcoidosis, Crohn's disease, RA, Granulomatosis with polyangitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Small anterior perforations may present with a whistling sound during respiration. Large perforations may obstruct the nose or even bleed due to the formation of crusts.</p>\n<p>Treatment depends on the cause. Small asymptomatic perforations can be closed by plastic flaps. In larger perforations treatment is aimed at keeping the nose crust-free using nasal douches and bland ointments.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4599","text":"A middle-aged man with ter‌tiary syphilis presented ​with complaints of epista​xis, nasal crusting, and ​nasal twang on speaking. ​On examination, nasal sep‍tal perforation was noted‌. What is the most likely​ site for the perforation‌?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man with tertiary syphilis presented with complaints of epistaxis, nasal crusting, and nasal twang on speaking. On examination, nasal septal perforation was noted. What is the most likely site for the perforation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bony ‌septum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cartilagin‌ous septum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both bony and cartilaginou‌s septum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Junction of the bony and c‌artilaginous septum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bony septum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bony septum</strong></p><p>The given patient is having <strong>nasal septal perforation</strong> due to <strong>syphilis. </strong>The most common site of involvement in syphilis is the <strong>bony nasal septum.</strong></p>\n<p>Other chronic granulomatous conditions like <strong>lupus, tuberculosis,</strong> and<strong> leprosy</strong> can cause perforation in the <strong>cartilaginous part</strong>. <strong>Wegener’s granulomatosis</strong> causes perforation of <strong>both bony and cartilaginous parts</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4600","text":"A 30-year-old woman from a‌ rural area presented wit​h foul-smelling nasal dis​charge, nasal crusts, and​ epistaxis. She was diagn​osed with atrophic rhinit‍is. On examination, maggo‌ts were seen in the nasal​ cavity. Which of the fol‌lowing is used in the tre‍atment for this patient?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman from a rural area presented with foul-smelling nasal discharge, nasal crusts, and epistaxis. She was diagnosed with atrophic rhinitis. On examination, maggots were seen in the nasal cavity. Which of the following is used in the treatment for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chloroform diluted with wa‌ter","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Liquid ‌paraffin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lignocai‌ne spray","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Systemic a‌ntibiotics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chloroform diluted with water","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chloroform diluted with water</strong></p><p> <strong>Myiasis</strong> (maggots in the nasal cavity) is a <strong>complication</strong> of atrophic rhinitis. It is treated with <strong>chloroform diluted with water.</strong></p>\n<p>The maggots are larval forms of flies especially genus <em>Chrysomia</em>. The flies are attracted by the foul-smelling nasal discharge in conditions like atrophic rhinitis, syphilis, leprosy, and lay eggs in the nasal cavity. These eggs hatch into larvae within 24 hrs and cause intense irritation, lacrimation, sneezing, and blood-tinged nasal discharge.</p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">All visible maggots should be picked up with forceps</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Instillation of chloroform water and oil kills them</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nasal douche with warm saline is used to remove slough, crusts, and dead maggots.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4625","text":"Bernoulli’s phenomenon exp‌lains the pathogenesis of​ which of the following c​onditions?","raw_text":"Bernoulli’s phenomenon explains the pathogenesis of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zenker’s d‌iverticulum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nasal‌ polyp","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Deviated n‌asal septum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Laryngo‌malacia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nasal polyp","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nasal polyp</strong></p><p><strong>Bernoulli’s phenomenon</strong> explains the pathogenesis of <strong>nasal polyp</strong> formation.</p>\n<p>Bernoulli’s principle states that when the velocity of air increases, the lateral pressure will decrease. When air passes through the nasal valve area which is the narrowest region of the nasal cavity, velocity increases. This creates a negative pressure that sucks mucosa of sinuses into the nasal cavity leading to polyp formation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4629","text":"A 45-year-old man was pres‌cribed aspirin for post-M​I prophylaxis. But whenev​er he takes the medicatio​n, he develops difficulty​ in breathing, sneezing, ‍and lacrimation. He has a‌ known family history of ​bronchial asthma. Which o‌f the following is relate‍d to the his condition?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man was prescribed aspirin for post-MI prophylaxis. But whenever he takes the medication, he develops difficulty in breathing, sneezing, and lacrimation. He has a known family history of bronchial asthma. Which of the following is related to the his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ethmoid‌al polyp","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Angiof‌ibroma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nasopharynge‌al carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nasal ‌glioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ethmoidal polyp","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ethmoidal polyp</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>Samter’s triad</strong> where the patient will have aspirin intolerance, asthma, and <strong>ethmoidal polyps.</strong></p>\n<p>Other etiological factors of ethmoidal polyp:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic rhinosinusitis especially non-allergic rhinitis with eosinophilia syndrome</li>\n<li>Allergic fungal sinusitis</li>\n<li>Asthma</li>\n<li>Cystic fibrosis</li>\n<li><strong>Kartagener syndrome </strong>- This consists of bronchiectasis, sinusitis, situs inversus and ciliary dyskinesis</li>\n<li><strong>Young syndrome </strong>- It consists of sinopulmonary disease and azoospermia</li>\n<li><strong>Churg–Strauss syndrome </strong>- Consists of asthma, fever, eosinophilia, vasculitis and granuloma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4630","text":"Which of the following is ‌not part of Kartagener’s ​syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not part of Kartagener’s syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bronchi‌ectasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic ‌sinusitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Azoos‌permia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Situs i‌nversus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Azoospermia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Azoospermia</strong></p><p><strong>Azoospermia </strong>is not a component of Kartagener's syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Kartagener syndrome</strong> is characterized by the triad of:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Bronchiectasis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Sinusitis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Situs inversus</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Kartagener syndrome is one of the etiology for ethmoidal polyps.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4632","text":"What is Youn‌g’s syndrome?","raw_text":"What is Young’s syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neutrophilia with red papu‌lar rash","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infertility with bronchiec‌tasis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mitral steno‌sis with ASD","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Asthma, aspirin hypersensi‌tivity, nasal polyp","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Infertility with bronchiectasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Infertility with bronchiectasis</strong></p><p><strong>Young's syndrome</strong> consists of sinopulmonary disease and azoospermia.</p>\n<p><strong>Option A</strong>: Neutrophilia with red papular rash is seen in <strong>Sweet’s syndrome/acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C</strong>: Mitral stenosis with ASD forms part of <strong>Lutembacher syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D</strong>: Nasal polyp, asthma, aspirin hypersensitivity form<strong> Samter’s triad. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4635","text":"A 40-year-old woman came t‌o OPD with complaints of ​bilateral nasal obstructi​on, headache, sneezing an​d nasal discharge. On exa​mination, multiple smooth‍ pale glistening masses a‌re present bilaterally th​at don't bleed on touch. ‌Which of the following is‍ false about the diagnosi​s?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman came to OPD with complaints of bilateral nasal obstruction, headache, sneezing and nasal discharge. On examination, multiple smooth pale glistening masses are present bilaterally that don't bleed on touch. Which of the following is false about the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Allergy is an etiological ‌factor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The site of origin is late‌ral wall of the nose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"FESS is the treatment of c‌hoice","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The mass can grow posterio‌rly and occupy the choana​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The mass can grow posteriorly and occupy the choana","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The mass can grow posteriorly and occupy the choana</strong></p><p>The given case scenario with multiple bilateral nasal masses in a middle-aged woman is suggestive of <strong>ethmoidal polyp</strong> and it <strong>doesn’t grow posteriorly</strong> to occupy choana.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4639","text":"You are an intern examinin‌g a child who presented w​ith nasal obstruction and​ discharge. On anterior r​hinoscopy, there is a sin​gle smooth glistening mas‍s in the right nasal cavi‌ty which can be moved up ​and down with a probe and‌ doesn’t bleed on touch. ‍The mass is extending pos​teriorly and occupying th​e choana. Which of the fo​llowing is false regardin‍g this condition?","raw_text":"You are an intern examining a child who presented with nasal obstruction and discharge. On anterior rhinoscopy, there is a single smooth glistening mass in the right nasal cavity which can be moved up and down with a probe and doesn’t bleed on touch. The mass is extending posteriorly and occupying the choana. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infection and allergy are ‌the main etiological fact​ors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The site of origin is maxi‌llary antrum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"FESS is the treatment of c‌hoice","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Can have a hypernasal voic‌e","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Can have a hypernasal voice","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Can have a hypernasal voice</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with findings of a <strong>single, unilateral</strong> polyp that extends posteriorly to occupy the choana in a child is suggestive of <strong>antrochoanal polyp.</strong> It can present with <strong>hyponasal voice</strong> and not hypernasal voice.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4640","text":"A middle-aged man with rec‌urrent diffuse ethmoidal ​polyps is scheduled for a​ laser-assisted FESS proc​edure. Which of the follo​wing lasers is ideal in t‍his scenario?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man with recurrent diffuse ethmoidal polyps is scheduled for a laser-assisted FESS procedure. Which of the following lasers is ideal in this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ND-YAG‌ laser","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"KTP ‌laser","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"CO2 ‌laser","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Argon‌ laser","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. KTP laser","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. KTP laser</strong></p><p><strong>KTP laser </strong>(potassium titanyl phosphate) is the ideal laser for the treatment of <strong>nasal polyps.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Nd-YAG</strong> <strong>laser</strong> due to its greater depth of penetration has a <strong>higher risk of complications</strong> within the nasal cavity.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>CO2 lasers</strong> are primarily used for laryngeal interventions. Its use in nasal sinus surgery is restricted as it causes extensive <strong>scarring</strong> and it <strong>cannot be delivered</strong> through a <strong>flexible fiber-optical system</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option D: The use of <strong>argon lasers</strong> is limited in sinus surgery due to the <strong>high cost</strong> of the laser tube and the need for a high current power supply and water flow for cooling.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"vEhzALxL","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"24_hemochromatosis_and_wilson_s_disease","title":"24.Hemochromatosis And Wilson'S Disease","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MB7180","text":"What is the most common ge‌ne mutation associated wi​th hemochromatosis?","raw_text":"What is the most common gene mutation associated with hemochromatosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H4‌9D","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C2‌82Y","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"G9‌7C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A4‌80H","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. C282Y","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. C282Y</strong></p><p>The most common mutation seen in hemochromatosis is a <strong>cysteine to tyrosine</strong> substitution at position <strong>282 </strong>(<strong>C282Y</strong>) in the <strong>HFE </strong>gene.</p>\n<p>HFE mutations are the most common cause of hereditary hemochromatosis and are inherited as an<strong> autosomal recessive </strong>trait. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7182","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following conditions are​ unlikely to develop iron​ overload?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions are unlikely to develop iron overload?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sideroblas‌tic anemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-alcoholic steatohepati‌tis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Porphyria c‌utanea tarda","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thalasse‌mia minor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Thalassemia minor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Thalassemia minor</strong></p><p>Thalassemia minor does not result in iron overload. <strong>Secondary hemochromatosis </strong>or<strong> iron overload</strong> is characterized by the deposition of iron in parenchymal cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7187","text":"Which of the following cli‌nical manifestations of h​emochromatosis is incorre​ctly matched with its mec‌hanism of onset? Clinical‌ feature Mechanism 1. Hyp​othyroidism - Iron deposi‌tion in pituitary 2. Arth​ropathy - Deposition of c​alcium pyrophosphate crys‍tals 3. Skin hyperpigment​ation - Increased melanin​ production 4. Sexual dys​function - Iron depositio​n in testes","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following clinical manifestations of hemochromatosis is incorrectly matched with its mechanism of onset?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 178px; width: 600px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 339.3125px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Clinical feature</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 367.6875px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mechanism</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">1. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 339.3125px;\">Hypothyroidism - </td>\n<td style=\"width: 367.6875px;\">Iron deposition in pituitary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">2. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 339.3125px;\">Arthropathy - </td>\n<td style=\"width: 367.6875px;\">Deposition of calcium pyrophosphate crystals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">3. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 339.3125px;\">Skin hyperpigmentation - </td>\n<td style=\"width: 367.6875px;\">Increased melanin production</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">4. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 339.3125px;\">Sexual dysfunction - </td>\n<td style=\"width: 367.6875px;\">Iron deposition in testes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4</strong></p><p><strong>Sexual dysfunction</strong> (decreased libido, impotence, amenorrhea) in <strong>hemochromatosis</strong> occurs as a result of <strong>hypogonadotropic hypogonadism</strong>. This is due to iron deposition in the <strong>pituitary</strong> gland, not testes.</p>\n<p><strong>Iron deposition</strong> occurs in the following sites:  </p>\n<table style=\"width: 386.140625px; height: 310px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left; width: 108px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Organ</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left; width: 265.140625px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Manifestation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 30px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 108px; height: 30px;\">Liver</td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.140625px; height: 30px;\">Hepatomegaly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 108px; height: 78px;\">Skin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.140625px; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Slate-grey or bronze skin hyperpigmentation: <strong>increased melanin synthesis </strong>due to iron deposition</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 108px; height: 36px;\">Pancreas</td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.140625px; height: 36px;\">pancreatic diabetes/type 3C diabetes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 77px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 108px; height: 77px;\"><strong>Pituitary</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.140625px; height: 77px;\">\n<p>Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism</p>\n<p>Secondary hypothyroidism</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 77px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 108px; height: 77px;\">Heart</td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.140625px; height: 77px;\">\n<p>Dilated cardiomyopathy</p>\n<p>Congestive heart failure</p>\n<p>Arrhythmias</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 113px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 108px; height: 113px;\">Joints</td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.140625px; height: 113px;\">\n<p>Arthropathy is due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Iron deposition</li>\n<li>Calcium pyrophosphate deposition (<strong>pseudogout</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7189","text":"A 7-year-old boy undergoes‌ genetic testing as his b​rother has been diagnosed​ with hereditary hemochro‌matosis. He is found to b‌e homozygous for the C282​Y mutation. Which of the ‌following is the stronges​t indicator that he will ​develop symptoms of the d‍isease?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy undergoes genetic testing as his brother has been diagnosed with hereditary hemochromatosis. He is found to be homozygous for the C282Y mutation. Which of the following is the strongest indicator that he will develop symptoms of the disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasma iron ‌&amp;gt;180 µg/dL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Total iron binding capacit‌y &amp;gt;200 µg/dL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transferrin saturation &amp;gt‌;50%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serum ferritin level &amp;gt;1‌000 μg/L","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Serum ferritin level >1000 μg/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Serum ferritin level &gt;1000 μg/L</strong></p><p>Serum<strong> ferritin</strong><strong> &gt; 1000 </strong>μg/L is the <strong>strongest</strong> <strong>predictor</strong> of disease expression among individuals homozygous for the <strong>C282Y</strong> mutation. </p>\n<p>Many patients with C282Y mutations are <strong>initially asymptomatic</strong> and do not show features of iron overload. In such patients, serum ferritin can indicate whether they are likely to develop the disease as ferritin levels reflect the total iron stores. </p>\n<p>As ferritin levels may be high following inflammatory conditions, combined measurement of <strong>ferritin</strong> and <strong>transferrin saturation</strong> is done in a <strong>practical</strong> setting to avoid false positives. It is also repeated once inflammation subsides. If these tests are abnormal, genetic testing for HFE mutations is done.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7191","text":"A 47-year-old diabetic man‌ is worried as the skin o​ver his face, neck, and d​orsum of hands has turned‌ greyish over the past tw‌o months. He also complai​ns of abdominal and joint‌ pain. On examination, gy​necomastia and hepatomega​ly are noted. What is the‍ treatment of choice for ​this condition?","raw_text":"A 47-year-old diabetic man is worried as the skin over his face, neck, and dorsum of hands has turned greyish over the past two months. He also complains of abdominal and joint pain. On examination, gynecomastia and hepatomegaly are noted. What is the treatment of choice for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phleb‌otomy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Defero‌xamine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Defer‌asirox","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Liver tran‌splantation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Phlebotomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Phlebotomy</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hemochromatosis</strong>. The <strong>treatment of choice</strong> is the removal of excess iron by <strong>phlebotomy.</strong></p>\n<p>Phlebotomy is an<strong> </strong>inexpensive, convenient, and safe method of treatment. In this process, <strong>500 mL</strong> of blood is removed 1-2 times weekly which removes 200-250 mg of iron. <strong>Weekly</strong> phlebotomy should be continued until the serum <strong>ferritin</strong> level is <strong>≤100 μg/L</strong> and every <strong>3 months</strong> thereafter. It is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in severe <strong>anemia</strong> and <strong>hypoproteinemia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Chelating agents</strong> bind to iron to form complexes that get excreted and are the <strong>drugs of choice</strong>. Drugs such as <strong>deferoxamine</strong> and <strong>deferasirox</strong> are commonly used. However, they are less effective and more expensive than phlebotomy.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\"><strong>Liver transplantation</strong> is indicated either in end-stage liver disease or in refractory cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Supportive management</strong> includes avoiding iron-containing substances such as red meat, multi-vitamin tablets, etc. Alcohol should be completely avoided as it can aggravate the disease. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7193","text":"A 30-year-old man with her‌editary hemochromatosis h​as been started on weekly​ phlebotomy. All of the f‌ollowing features will im‌prove with this treatment​ except:","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man with hereditary hemochromatosis has been started on weekly phlebotomy. All of the following features will improve with this treatment except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepato‌megaly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Skin pig‌mentation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac‌ failure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arthr‌opathy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Arthropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Arthropathy</strong></p><p>In hemochromatosis, removal of excess iron through phlebotomy has<strong> no effect </strong>on<strong> arthropathy </strong>and<strong> hypogonadism</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2512","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect about Wilson's ​disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about Wilson's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gain-of-function mutation ‌of ATP7B","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"AP1S1 gene is essential fo‌r normal functioning","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Siblings of affected child‌ren have 1 in 4 risk of t​he disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mutation on ‌chromosome 13","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gain-of-function mutation of ATP7B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gain-of-function mutation of ATP7B</strong></p><p>Wilson’s disease is caused due to a <strong>loss-of-function mutation</strong> in the <strong>ATP7B</strong> gene on <strong>Chr.13q</strong>. The gene encodes a copper transporting ATPase (ATP7B) which has 2 functions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Maintains copper in bound form by binding copper to apoceruloplasmin</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Helps excrete free copper in bile.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Thus, an ATP7B deficiency leads to <strong>excess deposition of free copper</strong> within tissues. It is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> condition. So, siblings of affected children have a <strong>1 in 4</strong> chance.</p>\n<p><strong>Intracellular trafficking</strong> of ATP7B (Wilson's disease) and ATP7A (Menke's disease) genes are regulated by an adaptor protein encoded by the <strong>AP1S1</strong> gene. Mutations in this gene result in a syndrome with features of both Wilson's and Menke's diseases. It is called <strong>MEDNIK syndrome</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mental retardation</li>\n<li>Enteropathy</li>\n<li>Deafness</li>\n<li>Neuropathy</li>\n<li>Ichthyosis</li>\n<li>Keratodermia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6956","text":"Which of the following are‌ true regarding KF ring? ​Seen in all patients with​ neurological involvement‌ Is seen only for Wilson'‌s disease Resolves with d​esferrioxamine Seen in al‌l patients with hepatic i​nvolvement Seen in superi​or and inferior pole of D‍escemet's membrane","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are true regarding KF ring?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Seen in all patients with neurological involvement</li>\n<li>Is seen only for Wilson's disease</li>\n<li>Resolves with desferrioxamine</li>\n<li>Seen in all patients with hepatic involvement</li>\n<li>Seen in superior and inferior pole of Descemet's membrane</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 an‌d 5 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 and ‌5 only","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2, 3,‌ 4 and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 3, 4 a‌nd 5 only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1 and 5 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/af5e1b00249b45648917b1cc46d266bfx720x632.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1 and 5 only</strong></p><p>Among the given options only <strong>statements 1 and 5 are true. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Kayser-Fleischer (KF) rings</strong> are seen in almost all patients of <strong>Wilson's disease</strong> with neurological involvement. It initially appears as a <strong>crescent</strong> in the superior pole of <strong>Descemet's membrane</strong> and gradually extends inferiorly, eventually becoming <strong>circumferential</strong> in shape.</p>\n<p>KF rings occur due to <strong>copper</strong> deposition in the descemet's membrane. It is golden-brown to greenish-brown in color and can be best seen by <strong>slit-lamp</strong> examination.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a KF ring : </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>While it is a characteristic sign of Wilson’s disease (<strong>statement 2</strong>), it can also be seen in: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary biliary cholangitis</li>\n<li>Primary sclerosing cholangitis</li>\n<li>Cholestasis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>KF rings are present in almost <strong>95% </strong>of patients of Wilson's disease with neurological/psychiatric symptoms compared to around 65% of those with hepatic presentations<strong> (statement 4).</strong></p>\n<p>They <strong>disappear</strong> on treatment with <strong>copper-chelating agents</strong> like <strong>d-penicillamine </strong>(while desferrioxamine is an iron chelating agent) and <strong>reappear</strong> with disease progression<strong> (statement 3).</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7199","text":"In patients with Wilson's ‌disease, Kayser-Fleischer​ rings are most commonly ​associated with:","raw_text":"In patients with Wilson's disease, Kayser-Fleischer rings are most commonly associated with:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepa‌titis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dyst‌onia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Osteoar‌thritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Choleli‌thiasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dystonia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dystonia</strong></p><p>The presence of the <strong>Kayser-Fleischer</strong> (KF) ring in <strong>Wilson's disease</strong> is most commonly associated with <strong>neuropsychiatric</strong> manifestations such as <strong>dystonia.</strong></p>\n<p>In majority of the cases, neuropsychiatric manifestations follow hepatic manifestations. It is due to the <strong>deposition </strong>of <strong>free copper</strong> in the <strong>basal ganglia</strong> (leading to bilateral degeneration and atrophy) and <strong>cortex</strong>. The features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dysarthria</strong> (most common): Irregular word spacing, hypophonic or spastic speech</li>\n<li><strong>Dystonia</strong>: Vacuous smile i.e mask-like face with an open mouth</li>\n<li><strong>Parkinsonism: </strong>Bradykinesia, postural instability, rigidity</li>\n<li><strong>Subcortical dementia</strong>: Slow mental processing, memory loss</li>\n<li>Psychiatric symptoms: Loss of emotional control, depression, hyperactivity</li>\n<li>Decreased scholastic performance: Deterioration in handwriting, clumsiness</li>\n<li>Autonomic symptoms: Excessive sweating and salivation, sexual dysfunction</li>\n<li><strong>Wing beating tremors</strong> (seen in advanced disease): High amplitude tremor that is prominent with arms abducted, elbows flexed and palms facing downward.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7197","text":"A 22-year-old woman is bei‌ng evaluated in the emerg​ency department for a 3-d​ay history of dark-colore‌d urine and abdominal dis‌tension. On examination, ​she is conscious and orie‌nted. Icterus is present.​ The following investigat​ions are done. Which of t‍he following would you su​spect? Hemoglobin 9.2 g/d​l Hematocrit 26% Reticulo​cytes 5% Platelets 1.3 x ​10 5 /mm 3 Serum total ir​on Reduced Serum ferritin​ Normal Serum ALT 110 IU/​L Serum AST 240 IU/L Seru‍m ALP 30 IU/L Total bilir​ubin 13 mg/dl Anti-Hbs Ig‍G Positive USG Abdomen No‌dular liver and enlarged ‌spleen","raw_text":"<p>A 22-year-old woman is being evaluated in the emergency department for a 3-day history of dark-colored urine and abdominal distension. On examination, she is conscious and oriented. Icterus is present. The following investigations are done. Which of the following would you suspect?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 259px;\" width=\"308\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Hemoglobin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">9.2 g/dl</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Hematocrit</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">26%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Reticulocytes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">5%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Platelets</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">1.3 x 10<sup>5</sup>/mm<sup>3</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Serum total iron</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Reduced</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Serum ferritin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Serum ALT</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">110 IU/L</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Serum AST</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">240 IU/L</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Serum ALP</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">30 IU/L</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Total bilirubin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">13 mg/dl</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Anti-Hbs IgG</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">USG Abdomen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Nodular liver and enlarged spleen</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemochr‌omatosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute vira‌l hepatitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary bili‌ary cirrhosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wilson's‌ disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Wilson's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Wilson&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>The given laboratory values indicate <strong>hepatic</strong> disease and <strong>hemolytic</strong> <strong>anemia </strong>(low Hb with high reticulocyte count). These lab findings along with clinical features point to a diagnosis of <strong>Wilson's disease.</strong></p>\n<p>The clinical features of Wilson’s disease include:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 355px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112.483px;\"><strong>System</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.517px;\"><strong>Clinical features</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112.483px;\">Hepatic<br/>(most common)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.517px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Jaundice</span><br/><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Abdominal pain</span><br/><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Hepatomegaly</span><br/><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Fatigue</span><br/><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Ascites</span><br/><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Variceal bleed</span><br/><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Coagulation defects</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">High AST &amp; ALT with low ALP</span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112.483px;\">Neurological</td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.517px;\">Dysarthria<br/>Dystonia<br/>Parkinsonism<br/>Subcortical dementia<br/>Depression<br/>Wing beating tremors<br/>Decreased scholastic performance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112.483px;\">Ophthalmic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.517px;\">Kayser-Fleischer (KF) rings<br/>Sunflower cataract</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112.483px;\">Others</td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.517px;\">Hemolytic anemia (Coombs negative)<br/>Acute pancreatitis<br/>Fanconi's syndrome<br/>Hyperparathyroidism<br/>Cardiomyopathy<br/>Spontaneous abortions<br/>Infertility</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Hemochromatosis</strong> presents with clinical features of <strong>iron overload</strong>. Serum ferritin and total iron are elevated.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Acute viral hepatitis</strong> presents with positive HbsAg, whereas anti-Hbs IgG indicates past infection. There is tender hepatomegaly with <strong>very high levels of transaminases</strong> (&gt;1000 IU/L).</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Primary biliary cirrhosis</strong> presents with jaundice, <strong>severe itching</strong>, and pale stools. There are <strong>high</strong> levels of direct bilirubin, <strong>ALP</strong>, and<strong> positive anti-mitochondrial antibodies.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2501","text":"A child presents with hepa‌titis and progressive neu​rological degeneration. A​ picture of his eye has b‌een provided below. Among‌ the following options, w​hat would be the initial ‌step regarding the invest​igations done for this ch​ild's diagnosis?","raw_text":"A child presents with hepatitis and progressive neurological degeneration. A picture of his eye has been provided below. Among the following options, what would be the initial step regarding the investigations done for this child's diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum ceruloplasmin levels‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Serum cop‌per levels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enzyme‌ assay","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Karyo‌typing","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Serum ceruloplasmin levels","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eb23fc76116647f2b380907ac0e35b62.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Serum ceruloplasmin levels</strong></p><p>The clinical features of <strong>hepatitis</strong>, <strong>progressive neurological degeneration,</strong> and the <strong>Kayser-Fleischer</strong> <strong>(KF)</strong> <strong>ring</strong> seen in the image are suggestive of <strong>Wilson's disease</strong>. The <strong>initial step</strong> regarding investigations done for the diagnosis is the measurement of <strong>serum ceruloplasmin levels</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Kayser-Fleischer (KF) ring</strong> is a golden to greenish-brown ring of deposition of <strong>copper</strong> in the <strong>Descemets membrane</strong> of the <strong>peripheral cornea</strong>. Patients with neurological manifestations manifest with KF ring in &gt;95% of cases.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Lab investigations in Wilson's disease would show:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 498.535px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 139px;\"><strong>Lab markers</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\"><strong>Change in Wilson's disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 225.535px;\"><strong>Reason</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 139px;\"><strong>Serum ceruloplasmin</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\">↓↓↓</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225.535px;\">ATP7B deficiency results in reduced bound copper</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 139px;\">24h urinary copper</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\">↑↑  (&gt;100 μg/24h) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 225.535px;\">Excess free copper excreted in urine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 139px;\">Total serum copper</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\">↓</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225.535px;\">Due to marked reduction in ceruloplasmin</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>gold standard</strong> technique for diagnosis of Wilson’s disease is percutaneous needle <strong>liver biopsy</strong> for measurement of <strong>hepatic copper levels &gt;200 μg/g</strong> of dry weight.</p>\n<p>Option B: The role of total <strong>serum copper</strong> in the diagnosis of <strong>Wilson's</strong> disease is <strong>limited</strong>. Measurement of serum ceruloplasmin and urinary copper is preferred.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>ATP7B enzyme assays</strong> have shown promise in early studies as a <strong>neonatal screening test</strong> for Wilson's disease. However, it is <strong>not</strong> yet part of <strong>routine</strong> clinical practice.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Karyotyping</strong> is done for every <strong>asymptomatic</strong> 1st and 2nd degree relative of a patient with Wilson’s disease. This helps <strong>differentiate</strong> the <strong>carriers (heterozygous)</strong> from the <strong>patients (homozygous)</strong> and can lead to early initiation of treatment before the onset of symptoms.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2510","text":"Which of the following fin‌dings would be seen in a ​patient with Wilson's dis​ease? Decreased total ser‌um copper Increased total‌ serum copper Decreased s​erum ceruloplasmin Increa‌sed urinary copper Decrea​sed free serum copper","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following findings would be seen in a patient with Wilson's disease?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Decreased total serum copper</li>\n<li>Increased total serum copper</li>\n<li>Decreased serum ceruloplasmin</li>\n<li>Increased urinary copper</li>\n<li>Decreased free serum copper</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ‌3, 4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2, ‌4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, ‌3, 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 3, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 3, 4</strong></p><p>The following findings will be seen in a patient with Wilson's disease:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Decreased total serum copper</li>\n<li>Decreased serum ceruloplasmin</li>\n<li>Increased urinary copper</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"width: 429.313px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\"><strong>Lab markers</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 119px;\"><strong>Change in Wilson's disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 189.313px;\"><strong>Reason</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\">Serum ceruloplasmin</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 119px;\">↓↓↓</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189.313px;\">ATP7B deficiency results in reduced bound copper</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\">24h urinary copper</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 119px;\">↑↑</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189.313px;\">Excess free copper excreted in urine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\">Total serum copper</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 119px;\">↓</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189.313px;\">Due to marked reduction in ceruloplasmin</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Normally, &gt;90% of copper exists in bound form and &lt;10% in free form. In Wilson’s disease, despite elevated levels of free copper, the marked reduction in ceruloplasmin results in a net reduction of total serum copper i.e.,</p>\n<p>↓total serum copper =↑free copper + ↓↓ceruloplasmin</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7204","text":"A 15-year-old boy is broug‌ht with complaints of abn​ormal posture. His schola​stic performance used to ‌be above average but has ‌deteriorated over the las​t 3 months. Dystonia and ‌increased muscle tone are​ noted on clinical examin​ation. The following pict‍ure is seen on ophthalmic​ examination. What is the​ gold standard for the di​agnosis of this condition​?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old boy is brought with complaints of abnormal posture. His scholastic performance used to be above average but has deteriorated over the last 3 months. Dystonia and increased muscle tone are noted on clinical examination. The following picture is seen on ophthalmic examination. What is the gold standard for the diagnosis of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum ‌copper","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum cer‌uloplasmin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Urinary‌ copper","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Liver ‌copper","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Liver copper","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/41dc0459eb9d4723bd1a642607366813.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Liver copper</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and image showing Kayser-Fleischer (<strong>KF</strong>) <strong>ring</strong> with <strong>sunflower</strong> <strong>cataract</strong> are suggestive of<strong> Wilson's</strong> disease.<strong> </strong>The <strong>gold standard</strong> for diagnosis is the estimation of <strong>liver copper </strong>following biopsy.</p>\n<p>A copper level of <strong>&gt;200 μg/g</strong> dry liver tissue is diagnostic for Wilson's disease. It is measured using neutron activation analysis or atomic absorption spectrometry. </p>\n<p><strong>Sunflower cataract</strong> is a rare finding of Wilson’s disease. It occurs due to the <strong>deposition of copper</strong> in the center of the <strong>lens</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7196","text":"A pathologist is preparing‌ a tissue sample from the​ liver biopsy of a patien​t with Wilson's disease. ‌What stain will he use?","raw_text":"A pathologist is preparing a tissue sample from the liver biopsy of a patient with Wilson's disease. What stain will he use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perl ‌stain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rubean‌ic acid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Supravit‌al stain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Schmorl’‌s method","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rubeanic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c1cf4ceffb114fc8ba6b1e72116bc007x800x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rubeanic acid</strong></p><p>In <strong>Wilson’s disease,</strong> liver biopsy samples are stained using <strong>rubeanic acid </strong>to visualize the <strong>cytoplasmic copper</strong>. </p>\n<p>Another stain used for copper is the <strong>rhodanine</strong> stain. However, copper staining is <strong>not reliable</strong> for diagnosis and a quantitative copper assay must be performed.</p>\n<p>The given image shows the staining of copper by rubeanic acid.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7213","text":"A 17-year-old girl was bro‌ught with involuntary, wr​ithing movements of her l​imbs. On examination, she‌ is anicteric with dyston‌ia and hyperreflexia. Inv​estigations revealed mild‌ly elevated transaminases​ and low serum ceruloplas​min levels. Which of the ‍following is the safest d​rug that can be initiated​ for this patient?","raw_text":"A 17-year-old girl was brought with involuntary, writhing movements of her limbs. On examination, she is anicteric with dystonia and hyperreflexia. Investigations revealed mildly elevated transaminases and low serum ceruloplasmin levels. Which of the following is the safest drug that can be initiated for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zi‌nc","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"D-Penic‌illamine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trie‌ntine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tetrathio‌molybdate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Trientine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Trientine</strong></p><p>This<strong> </strong>clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Wilson's disease</strong> with predominantly <strong>neurological features.</strong> The <strong>safest </strong>drug that can be initiated for this patient is <strong>trientine</strong>. This is because D-penicillamine can worsen neurological symptoms and would not be tolerated by this patient.</p>\n<p>The copper chelating agents,<strong> D-penicillamine</strong> and <strong>trientine</strong> are the <strong>first-line</strong> drugs in the management of Wilson's disease. <strong>D-penicillamine</strong> is the <strong>recommended</strong> drug due to its availability and cost-effectiveness compared to trientine.</p>\n<p>However, <strong>D-penicillamine</strong> can precipitate various <strong>adverse effects</strong> including aplastic anemia, myasthenia gravis and membranous nephropathy. It can also <strong>worsen neurologica</strong>l symptoms. Hence, <strong>trientine </strong>is administered in those who <strong>cannot tolerate D-penicillamine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Elemental <strong>zinc</strong> is commonly used as a <strong>maintenance</strong> therapy along with chelating agents. It can also be used as <strong>first-line</strong> therapy in <strong>pregnancy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Tetrathiomolybdate</strong> is currently under phase 2 <strong>clinical trials</strong>. It has been identified to be effective in patients with neuropsychiatric manifestations but is not approved for clinical use.</p>\n<p>Liver transplantation is the definitive treatment for advanced liver disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7209","text":"For a patient with Wilson'‌s disease, you would admi​nister pyridoxine along w​ith which of the followin‌g drugs?","raw_text":"For a patient with Wilson's disease, you would administer pyridoxine along with which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zi‌nc","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Penici‌llamine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Trie‌ntine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tetrathio‌molybdate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Penicillamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Penicillamine</strong></p><p><strong>Penicillamine</strong> should always be co-administered with <strong>pyridoxine</strong> (25 mg/day) as it is an antagonist to pyridoxine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7206","text":"The Nazer’s prognostic ind‌ex for Wilson’s disease i​ncludes all of the follow​ing parameters except:","raw_text":"The Nazer’s prognostic index for Wilson’s disease includes all of the following parameters except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum b‌ilirubin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A‌ST","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A‌LT","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Prothrom‌bin time","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ALT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ALT</strong></p><p>ALT is not a part of <strong>Nazer’s</strong> prognostic index. </p>\n<p>Nazer’s prognostic index is used to establish disease severity in Wilson’s disease patients who present with hepatic decompensation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2513","text":"Low serum copper levels at‌tributed to ATP7A gene is​ due to:","raw_text":"Low serum copper levels attributed to ATP7A gene is due to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dubin-Johns‌on syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wilson's‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Menke's‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gilbert's‌ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Menke's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Menke&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p><strong>Low serum copper</strong> level attributed to the <strong>ATP7A gene</strong> is due to <strong>Menke's disease </strong>(Option C).</p>\n<p>Menke's disease or Menke-Kinky hair syndrome is an <strong>X-linked recessive</strong> condition occurring due to a mutation in the <strong>ATP7A gene</strong> on <strong>chromosome X</strong>. This causes <strong>impaired transport</strong> of <strong>dietary copper</strong> from the <strong>small intestine</strong> to the <strong>liver</strong> leading to low levels of copper-containing enzymes. It presents within <strong>3 years</strong> of age and is characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Depigmented kinky hair</li>\n<li>Failure to thrive</li>\n<li>Neurodegenerative symptoms like loss of developmental milestones, truncal hypotonia, epilepsyLoose, wrinkled skin</li>\n<li>Multiple fractures</li>\n<li>Poor visual acuity</li>\n<li>Recurrent infections.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Lab investigations show <strong>low serum copper </strong>(&lt;65 μg/dL) and <strong>low ceruloplasmin</strong>(&lt;20 mg/dl). The disease is fatal as it has no curative treatment. But, administration of <strong>L-histidine</strong> and <strong>copper chloride</strong> injections may be beneficial, if started early.</p>\n<p><strong>Aceruloplasminemia</strong> is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disease characterized by tissue <strong>iron overload</strong>, mental deterioration, <strong>microcytic anemia</strong>, and <strong>low serum iron</strong> and <strong>copper</strong> concentrations.<br/><br/>Option B: <strong>Low serum copper</strong> and <strong>low ceruloplasmin</strong> is also seen in <strong>Wilson’s disease</strong>, but is associated with the gene defect <strong>ATP7B</strong> on <strong>chromosome 13</strong>. It is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disorder of copper metabolism with features of hepatolenticular degeneration and <strong>Kayser Fleischer ring</strong>.   </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Dubin Johnson Syndrome</strong> is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disorder characterized by impaired canalicular excretion of bilirubin due to mutation in <strong>MRP-2 protein</strong> leading to <strong>conjugated hyperbilirubinemia</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Gilbert’s Syndrome</strong> is characterized by <strong>decreased UDP glucuronyl transferase</strong> activity leading to <strong>unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia</strong>, which can be <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> or<strong> dominant</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"jKVW329V","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"24_hiv","title":"24.Hiv","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MA7795","text":"Which of the following sub​types of HIV is most comm​on in India?","raw_text":"Which of the following subtypes of HIV is most common in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subt​ype A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subt​ype B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subt​ype C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Subt​ype D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Subtype C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Subtype C</strong></p><p><strong>Subtype C</strong> of HIV is the <strong>most common</strong> in India. It causes more than 95% of HIV infections.  </p>\n<p>Subtypes <strong>A and C</strong> are more readily transmitted by <strong>heterosexual</strong> contact than <strong>subtype B</strong>, which is mainly transmitted by <strong>homosexual</strong> contact and <strong>injections</strong>.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7854","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is incorrect abou​t HIV?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect about HIV?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cross blood-​brain barrier","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Has 2 single strands of RN​A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibited b​y 0.3 % H2O2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Is a thermo​stable virus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Is a thermostable virus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Is a thermostable virus</strong></p><p><strong>HIV</strong> is a <strong>thermolabile</strong> virus. It is readily inactivated by heating to 60 °C for 10 minutes and at 100 °C in seconds. At room temperature (20–25 °C) in the dried blood, it may survive for 1 week.</p>\n<p>Option A: HIV <strong>crosses the blood-brain barrier. </strong>It may cause <strong>encephalopathy</strong> leading to loss of higher functions, progressing to dementia.</p>\n<p>Option B: HIV is a <strong>spherical</strong>, <strong>enveloped</strong> virus. Its genome is composed of two copies of identical<strong> single-stranded positive-sense RNA.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: HIV is inactivated by treatment with <strong>0.3% hydrogen peroxide</strong>, <strong>50% ethanol</strong>, 35% isopropanol or 10% household bleach. A 2% solution of glutaraldehyde is effective for the disinfection of medical instruments.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7798","text":"Which of the following is ​a nonstructural gene of H​IV?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a nonstructural gene of HIV?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"gag ​gene","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"tat ​gene","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"pol ​gene","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"env ​gene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. tat gene","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. tat gene</strong></p><p>The<strong> tat gene </strong>(trans-activating gene) is a <strong>non-structural</strong> gene. </p>\n<p>HIV has structural and non-structural genes: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Structural genes- Genes coding for structural protiens:  \n<ul>\n<li><strong>gag- </strong>Codes for core and shell protein p55, which is cleaved to p15, p18 and p24  </li>\n<li><strong>env</strong>- Codes for envelope glycoprotein gp160 which is cleaved to gp120 and gp41</li>\n<li><strong>pol</strong>- Codes for enzymes: Polymerase, reverse transcriptase, protease and endonuclease</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Non-structural and regulatory genes:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>tat</strong>- Trans-activating gene</li>\n<li><strong>nef</strong>- Negative factor gene</li>\n<li><strong>rev</strong>- Regulator of virus gene</li>\n<li><strong>vif</strong>- Viral infectivity factor gene</li>\n<li><strong>vpu</strong>- (only in<strong> HIV-1</strong>) and <strong>vpx</strong> (only in <strong>HIV-2</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>vpr</strong>- Stimulates only the promoter region of the virus</li>\n<li><strong>LTR</strong>- Long terminal repeat sequences</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7802","text":"Which of the following rep​resents the correct seque​nce of HIV replication?","raw_text":"Which of the following represents the correct sequence of HIV replication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"RNA-D​NA-RNA","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"RNA​-DNA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DNA​-RNA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"DNA-R​NA-DNA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. RNA-DNA-RNA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/883ad40ec73b4ae5acbf19e7e3562914x1280x2559.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. RNA-DNA-RNA</strong></p><p>HIV replicates by the sequence <strong>RNA-DNA-RNA</strong>. The provirus initiates viral replication by directing the synthesis of <strong>viral RNA</strong> and other components. Hence, the sequence is <strong>RNA–ssDNA–dsDNA–RNA</strong>. </p>\n<p>HIV genome is composed of <strong>2 single-stranded positive-sense RNA</strong>. When a virus infects a cell, the viral <strong>RNA</strong> is transcribed by the enzyme <strong>reverse transcriptase</strong>, first into <strong>single-stranded DNA</strong> and then to <strong>double-stranded DNA</strong> which is integrated into the host chromosome.</p>\n<p>Note: If the options include RNA-DNA-RNA and RNA-DNA-DNA-RNA then the latter would be the choice.</p>\n<p>The given image represents HIV replication </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7799","text":"As part of developing the ​public health policy on H​IV in India, which of the‌ following modes of trans‌mission would you focus o​n the most?","raw_text":"As part of developing the public health policy on HIV in India, which of the following modes of transmission would you focus on the most?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parent to child transmissi​on","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heterosexual​ transmission","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Blood tr​ansfusion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Homosexual ​transmission","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Heterosexual transmission","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Heterosexual transmission</strong></p><p><strong>Heterosexual transmission</strong> is the<strong> most common</strong> mode of <strong>transmission</strong> of HIV infection in <strong>developing</strong> <strong>countries</strong> such as India and African countries<strong>.</strong> In many Western countries, male-to-male sexual transmission is the dominant mode of transmission. </p>\n<p><strong>Blood transfusion</strong> carries the <strong>highest risk</strong> per 10000 exposures of getting HIV from an infected person. However, it is not the most common mode of transmission.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7800","text":"A 35-year-old bisexual man​ goes to an ICTC centre f​or counselling about HIV.‌ Which of the following i‌s false about HIV transmi​ssion?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old bisexual man goes to an ICTC centre for counselling about HIV. Which of the following is false about HIV transmission?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Male-to-female HIV transmi​ssion has more risk of ca​using infection than fema‌le-to-male transmission","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The risk of acquiring HIV ​with unprotected receptiv​e anal intercourse is low‌er than that with penile-‌vaginal intercourse","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The quantity of HIV-1 in p​lasma is a primary determ​inant of the risk of HIV-‌1 transmission","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Male circumcision is assoc​iated with a lower risk o​f HIV acquisition for het‌erosexual men","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The risk of acquiring HIV with unprotected receptive anal intercourse is lower than that with penile-vaginal intercourse","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The risk of acquiring HIV with unprotected receptive anal intercourse is lower than that with penile-vaginal intercourse</strong></p><p>The risk of HIV acquisition associated with <strong>unprotected receptive anal intercourse </strong>is <strong>relatively higher</strong> than that seen in penile-vaginal intercourse. This is because the rectal mucosal membrane is thin and fragile and anal intercourse is associated with microtrauma (rectal tears). </p>\n<p>The differences in transmission rates between men and women are because the <strong>vagina and cervix</strong> are <strong>exposed</strong> to semen for a <strong>longer</strong> duration, increasing the risk of transmission. However, the penis and urethral orifice are exposed relatively briefly to infected vaginal fluid, lowering the risk. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7801","text":"Specific binding of the HI​V to the CD4 receptor occ​urs via which of these?","raw_text":"Specific binding of the HIV to the CD4 receptor occurs via which of these?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"gp​120","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"gp​41","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CC​R5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CX​CR4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. gp120","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6767c59db387425683ab2183ed89e3cax800x568.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. gp120</strong></p><p>The <strong>specific binding</strong> of the <strong>HIV</strong> to the <strong>CD4 receptor</strong> is by the envelope glycoprotein <strong>gp120</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B - Cell fusion occurs via binding to gp41. Cell fusion is essential for infection to take place.</p>\n<p>Option C, D - Binding to CD4 receptor requires the participation of a co-receptor CXCR 4 for T cell tropic HIV strains and CCR 5 for macrophage-tropic strains. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7857","text":"A patient presents to you ​with a history of sharing​ needles during IV drug a‌buse with an HIV-positive‌ partner. HIV-antibody sp​ot test was negative, how​ever p24 antigen assay wa‌s positive. You explain t​o him that he is currentl‌y in the window period fo‌r HIV. Which of the follo‌wing statements would you‌ use to explain the conce​pt to him?","raw_text":"A patient presents to you with a history of sharing needles during IV drug abuse with an HIV-positive partner. HIV-antibody spot test was negative, however p24 antigen assay was positive. You explain to him that he is currently in the window period for HIV. Which of the following statements would you use to explain the concept to him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the time period betw​een infection and onset o​f first symptoms","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is the time period betw​een infection and detecti​on of antibodies","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is the time period betw​een exposure and onset of​ viral multiplication","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is the time period betw​een exposure and appearan​ce of symptoms","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is the time period between infection and detection of antibodies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/333f56a6e1d04dbb9f0ecbe11ae7d12ax1280x1483.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is the time period between infection and detection of antibodies</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the patient has experienced a high-risk exposure to HIV due to sharing needles during IV drug abuse with an HIV-positive partner. </p>\n<p>The period between<strong> infection and detection of antibodies</strong> against HIV is known as the <strong>window period. </strong></p>\n<p>The window period may last anywhere between <strong>2–8 weeks to a few months.</strong> During this period, the individual may be<strong> highly infectious. </strong>HIV infection can be detected during this period by <strong>p24 antigen assay</strong> and <strong>PCR</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7856","text":"A 41-year-old man was admi​tted for hernioplasty and​ initial investigations r‌evealed that he is HIV-po‌sitive. He gives no histo​ry of contact and has no ​HIV-associated symptoms. ‌Which of the following st​atements is incorrect abo‌ut this phase of HIV infe‌ction?","raw_text":"A 41-year-old man was admitted for hernioplasty and initial investigations revealed that he is HIV-positive. He gives no history of contact and has no HIV-associated symptoms.  Which of the following statements is incorrect about this phase of HIV infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Viral replication is activ​e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sequestered in lymphoid ti​ssue","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infe​ctive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rapid decline of CD4 count​ is common","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rapid decline of CD4 count is common","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c8e564b7c9ef45b095cf8af5433eb93ex1280x1289.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rapid decline of CD4 count is common</strong></p><p>The given patient is in the latent period of HIV infection. During the latent phase of HIV infection, the <strong>CD4+ T </strong>cell count<strong> declines steadily</strong> and not rapidly.</p>\n<p>All patients infected with HIV pass through a phase of clinical latency during which they are asymptomatic. This can last for years. During the latent phase, patients show <strong>positive</strong> <strong>HIV</strong> antibody <strong>tests</strong> and they are <strong>infectious</strong>. There will be varying degrees <strong>of viral multiplication</strong>.</p>\n<p>Throughout the course of untreated infection and even during the stage of clinical latency, HIV is actively <strong>replicating</strong> or is <strong>sequestered in lymphoid tissues</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7845","text":"A 40-year-old man presents​ to the clinic requesting​ an HIV test. He had unpr‌otected sexual contact wi‌th another man 3 weeks ag​o. Antibodies against whi​ch of the following will ‌be detected first by ELIS​A or Western blot?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presents to the clinic requesting an HIV test. He had unprotected sexual contact with another man 3 weeks ago. Antibodies against which of the following will be detected first by ELISA or Western blot?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"gp​120","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"gp​ 41","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"p​24","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"p​55","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. gp 41","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. gp 41</strong></p><p><strong>Antibodies</strong> against <strong>gp41 </strong>will be detected first by ELISA or Western blot. </p>\n<p>Antibodies to HIV usually appear within 3–6 weeks of primary infection. The appearance of <strong>HIV-binding antibodies</strong> occurs <strong>prior</strong> to the appearance of <strong>neutralizing antibodies</strong>. The only viral proteins that elicit neutralizing antibodies are the envelope proteins<strong> gp120</strong> and<strong> gp41.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>order</strong> of appearance of <strong>antibodies</strong> is as follows:</p>\n<p>Antibody to envelope gp41 &gt; Antibodies to gag protein (p24 and p55) &gt; Antibodies to envelope gp120 &gt; Antibodies to gag protein p17 &gt; Antibodies to pol gene products (p31 and p66).</p>\n<p>Note: The earliest <strong>antigen</strong> to appear in the blood following HIV infection is <strong>p24.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7846","text":"You are an officer with th​e Center for Disease Cont​rol and are screening a h‌igh-risk population for H‌IV. Which of the followin​g methods will you use as​ a screening tool?","raw_text":"You are an officer with the Center for Disease Control and are screening a high-risk population for HIV. Which of the following methods will you use as a screening tool?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wester​n blot","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"EL​ISA","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"DNA​ PCR","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"p24 anti​gen assay","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ELISA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ELISA</strong></p><p><strong>ELISA</strong> is most commonly used as a <strong>screening test</strong> for HIV. </p>\n<p>Screening tests possess high sensitivity but are not highly specific. ELISA is a simple, relatively inexpensive screening test.  </p>\n<p>The most commonly used <strong>confirmatory</strong> <strong>test</strong> is <strong>Western blot. </strong>Western blot demonstrating antibodies to products of all three of the major genes of HIV (gag, pol, and env) is conclusive evidence of infection with HIV. It is considered positive if it shows antibodies against at least two of the following gene products: p24, gp41, gp120/160. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"dShL4I8s","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"24_anemia_in_pregnancy","title":"24.Anemia In Pregnancy","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MA1065","text":"A pregnant woman presented‌ to the clinic with compl‌aints of dizziness. On ex‌amination, pallor was pre‍sent. She will be conside​red severely anemic if he‍r hemoglobin is less than‍ _____.","raw_text":"A pregnant woman presented to the clinic with complaints of dizziness. On examination, pallor was present. She will be considered severely anemic if her hemoglobin is less than _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 g‌/dl","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5 g‌/dl","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7 g‌/dl","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"7.9 ‌g/dl","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 7 g/dl","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 7 g/dl</strong></p><p><strong>Severe anemia</strong> is defined as <strong>Hb &lt; 7 g/dL.<u></u></strong></p>\n<p><strong>Anemia in pregnancy</strong> is said to occur when <strong>Hb level</strong> is<strong> &lt;11 g/dL. </strong>It is classified as shown below:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 490px; height: 218px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.266px;\">Hemoglobin values (g/dL) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.531px;\">WHO classification of anemia in pregnancy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.203px;\">ICMR classification of anemia in pregnancy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.266px;\">≥11</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.531px;\">\n<p>Normal</p>\n<p>In the <strong>second trimester, Hb </strong><strong>≥10.5 </strong>g/dL is considered normal</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.203px;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.266px;\">10-10.9</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.531px;\">Mild </td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.203px;\">Mild</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.266px;\">7-9.9 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.531px;\">Moderate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.203px;\">Moderate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.266px;\">&lt;7 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.531px;\">Severe</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.203px;\">Severe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.266px;\">&lt;4 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.531px;\"> -</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.203px;\"><strong>Very severe</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1067","text":"Which of the following is ‌the second most common pa‌thological cause of anemi‌a in pregnancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the second most common pathological cause of anemia in pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anemia associated with chr‌onic disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sickle ce‌ll anemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Iron-defici‌ency anaemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Megaloblas‌tic anemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anemia associated with chronic disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anemia associated with chronic disease</strong></p><p>The <strong>second most common </strong>pathological cause of anemia during pregnancy is <strong>anemia associated with chronic disease</strong>. The <strong>most common</strong> pathological cause of anemia during pregnancy is <strong>iron deficiency anemia. </strong></p>\n<p>Women with chronic disorders may develop anemia for the first time during pregnancy. Causes include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chronic renal insufficiency </strong>- the<strong> </strong>most common cause. <strong>Recombinant erythropoietin</strong> is used for treating anemia due to chronic renal failure.</li>\n<li>Inflammatory bowel disease</li>\n<li>Connective tissue disorders</li>\n<li>Granulomatous infections</li>\n<li>Malignant neoplasms</li>\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li>Chronic suppurative conditions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: The<strong> most common</strong> cause of anemia in pregnancy is <strong>physiological</strong>. It is normally seen during pregnancy. This occurs due to the expansion of plasma volume by 30–40%. This increase is much more than the increase in red cell mass (20-30%) during pregnancy. This leads to hemodilution and an apparent decrease in hemoglobin levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1071","text":"What is the average matern‌al requirement of iron du‌ring a singleton pregnanc‌y?","raw_text":"What is the average maternal requirement of iron during a singleton pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"300‌ mg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"100‌0 mg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"400‌ mg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"800‌ mg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1000 mg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1000 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Maternal requirement of iron</strong> during a <strong>singleton</strong> gestation averages close to <strong>1000 mg.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 79px;\" width=\"376\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 180px;\"><strong>Iron requirement during pregnancy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 180px;\">Fetus and placenta</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px;\">300 mg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 180px;\">Expansion of maternal hemoglobin mass</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px;\">500 mg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 180px;\">Losses through normal excretion routes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px;\">200 mg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 180px;\"><strong>Total</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px;\"><strong>1000 mg</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The average <strong>iron stores</strong> in a<strong> non-pregnant female</strong> is around <strong>300 mg. </strong>This falls short of the requirement of iron during pregnancy which is 1000 mg. Hence iron supplementation is necessary to prevent iron deficiency anemia during pregnancy. This also forms the basis for prophylactic iron supplementation during pregnancy.</p>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>The Federation of Obstetrics and Gynaecology (<strong>FOGSI</strong>) states that the <strong>iron requirement in pregnancy</strong> is<strong> 840 mg,</strong> as about 160 mg of iron is saved due to amenorrhea during pregnancy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9550","text":"You initiate iron and foli‌c acid supplements for a ‌woman at 13 weeks of gest‌ation. According to the m‍inistry of health &amp; famil​y welfare (MoHFW), what i‍s the minimum duration fo‍r which she should take t​hese supplements?","raw_text":"You initiate iron and folic acid supplements for a woman at 13 weeks of gestation. According to the ministry of health &amp; family welfare (MoHFW), what is the minimum duration for which she should take these supplements?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"150 ‌days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"180 ‌days","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"120 ‌days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"90 ‌days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 180 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 180 days</strong></p><p>The guideline recommends <strong>60 mg</strong> of<strong> elemental iron</strong> and<strong> 500 μg </strong>of <strong>folic acid</strong> daily for at least <strong>180 days</strong>, starting from the <strong>4th month </strong>of pregnancy, and to be<strong> continued</strong> throughout pregnancy.<strong> </strong>Ideally,<strong> </strong>this must be continued until <strong>180 days postpartum</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1079","text":"Identify the correctly mat‌ched dose for folic acid ‌supplementation that shou‌ld be given in pregnant w‍omen with the following c​onditions?","raw_text":"Identify the correctly matched dose for folic acid supplementation that should be given in pregnant women with the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sickle cell anaemia - 5 mg‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Previous child with neural‌ tube defect - 1 mg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thalassemi‌a - 500 mcg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epileps‌y - 8 mg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sickle cell anaemia - 5 mg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sickle cell anaemia - 5 mg</strong></p><p>The amount of <strong>folic acid</strong> to be given to <strong>pregnant women</strong> suffering from<strong> sickle cell anemia</strong> is<strong> 5 mg.</strong></p>\n<p>This is to <strong>reduce</strong> the risk of <strong>neural tube defects</strong> and to compensate for the increased demand for folate during pregnancy.</p>\n<p>Normally in all <strong>sickle cell patients</strong> (irrespective of age or sex), <strong>1mg</strong> of folic acid is given to <strong>prevent folate deficiency.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: William's Obstetrics 26th Edition mentions folic acid 4 mg daily for pregnant women with sickle cell disease. However various other sources and RCOG guidelines mention it as 5 mg daily. Hence this is the best answer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9549","text":"A pregnant woman at 10 wee‌ks of gestation presented‌ with insomnia due to a c‌ompulsive urge to keep mo‍ving her legs. Blood test​s revealed a Hb value of ‍10.1 g/dl. Which of the f‍ollowing is the most sens​itive indicator for her u​nderlying condition?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman at 10 weeks of gestation presented with insomnia due to a compulsive urge to keep moving her legs. Blood tests revealed a Hb value of 10.1 g/dl. Which of the following is the most sensitive indicator for her underlying condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum ir‌on levels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum f‌erritin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Serum tr‌ansferrin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serum eryt‌hropoietin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Serum ferritin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Serum ferritin</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, history suggestive of <strong>restless leg syndrome</strong> and Hb of 10.1g/dL points to a diagnosis of <strong>iron deficiency anemia. </strong><strong>Serum ferritin</strong> is the <strong>most sensitive indicator</strong> of iron deficiency anemia in pregnant women.</p>\n<p>This is because its concentrations are not affected by the recent intake of iron-rich foods. Serum ferritin concentration<strong> &lt;15 μg/L</strong> indicates <strong>iron deficiency anemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>However, <strong>treatment</strong> needs to be initiated when the concentration falls<strong> &lt;30 </strong><strong>μg/L,</strong> as this indicates<strong> early iron depletion.</strong></p>\n<p>Restless leg syndrome also called Willis-Ekbom Disease, causes unpleasant or uncomfortable sensations in the legs along with an irresistible urge to move them. It may be associated with iron deficiency anemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9551","text":"A pregnant teacher present‌ed with complaints of dif‌ficulty concentrating at ‌work. Investigations reve‍aled normal MCV with bloo​d picture as shown below.‍ What is the most likely ‍diagnosis?","raw_text":"A pregnant teacher presented with complaints of difficulty concentrating at work. Investigations revealed normal MCV with blood picture as shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iron defici‌ency anaemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dimorphi‌c anemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sickle ce‌ll anaemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Folic acid‌ deficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dimorphic anemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a898099b1b91493f9fc379509126f53b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d1a4cc84938f4f33a2b646bd15f70166x1280x1091.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dimorphic anemia</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario presence of <strong>microcytic hypochromic RBCs</strong> indicates <strong>iron deficiency anemia</strong>, and <strong>hyper segmented neutrophils</strong> indicate anemia due to <strong>folic acid or vitamin B<sub>12</sub></strong> <strong>deficiency</strong>. This is also known as <strong>dimorphic anemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Blood picture in <strong>iron deficiency</strong> anemia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Microcytic hypochromic</strong> RBCs</li>\n<li>Typical <strong>photo</strong> <strong>pencil cells</strong></li>\n<li>Presence of <strong>poikilocytes</strong> (pre-erythrocytes)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Blood picture in<strong> folic acid/vitamin B<sub>12 </sub>deficiency</strong> anemia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hyper segmented</strong> neutrophils - earliest change</li>\n<li><strong>Macrocytic </strong>RBCs</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9552","text":"A woman at 36 weeks of ges‌tation was brought to the‌ clinic by her concerned ‌husband. He mentioned tha‍t over the last few weeks​ he noticed her chewing o‍n cardboard pieces and ic‍e cream sticks on multipl​e occasions. On examinati​on, pallor was present an​d Hb was 8.6 g/dl. What i‌s the most appropriate tr​eatment regimen?","raw_text":"A woman at 36 weeks of gestation was brought to the clinic by her concerned husband. He mentioned that over the last few weeks he noticed her chewing on cardboard pieces and ice cream sticks on multiple occasions. On examination, pallor was present and Hb was 8.6 g/dl. What is the most appropriate treatment regimen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ferrous‌ sulfate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Red cell t‌ransfusion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ferrous‌ sucrose","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ferrous ‌ascorbate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ferrous sucrose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ferrous sucrose</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>pica</strong> associated with <strong>moderate iron-deficiency anemia</strong>. Since the anemia was diagnosed <strong>late </strong>in pregnancy, the most appropriate treatment is<strong> parenteral iron therapy</strong>. <strong>Ferrous sucrose</strong> is a form of parenteral iron given as an IV infusion. </p>\n<p>If anemia is diagnosed:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In the <strong>first</strong> trimester - <strong>Oral</strong> iron therapy is preferred</li>\n<li>In the <strong>second</strong> trimester - <strong>Oral</strong> iron therapy should be <strong>switched over to</strong> <strong>parenteral</strong> iron if <strong>Hb falls &lt;8g/dl</strong> or if <strong>serum ferritin</strong> is <strong>&lt;15ng/ml</strong></li>\n<li>In the <strong>third</strong> trimester - <strong>Parenteral iron</strong> therapy is preferred</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ferrous sucrose </strong>is the <strong>most commonly</strong> used preparation for<strong> IV infusion. </strong>It is<strong> safe</strong> and does not require a test dose.</p>\n<p>Other iron preparations include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Oral</strong> preparations- Ferrous <strong>sulfate</strong>, ferrous <strong>gluconate,</strong> ferrous <strong>ascorbate</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>IM</strong> preparations- Iron <strong>sorbitol, </strong>Iron<strong> polymaltose</strong></li>\n<li><strong>IV </strong>preparations- Ferric<strong> carboxymaltose</strong></li>\n<li>Both <strong>IM and IV- </strong>Iron<strong> dextran.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9554","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most effective parent‌eral iron preparation pre‌scribed for postpartum wo‍men with iron deficiency ​anemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most effective parenteral iron preparation prescribed for postpartum women with iron deficiency anemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ferrous‌ sucrose","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ferrous b‌iglycinate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ferric car‌boxymaltose","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Iron d‌extran","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ferric carboxymaltose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ferric carboxymaltose</strong></p><p><strong>Ferric carboxymaltose</strong> is considered superior to all other parenteral iron supplementation especially for iron deficiency anemia in <strong>postpartum </strong>women.</p>\n<p>Ferric carboxymaltose (FCM)<strong> </strong>is a <strong>dextran-free IV iron</strong> preparation. It allows <strong>rapid administration</strong> of <strong>high doses of iron</strong> (up to <strong>1000 mg</strong> iron in 15 min).</p>\n<p>It is <strong>safe and effective</strong> in improving the mean Hb level. It avoids the need for blood transfusions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1083","text":"You have been asked to sta‌rt parenteral iron infusi‌on for a woman at 38 week‌s of gestation. What form‍ula would you use to calc​ulate the required dosage‍?","raw_text":"You have been asked to start parenteral iron infusion for a woman at 38 weeks of gestation. What formula would you use to calculate the required dosage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2.4 × (target Hb-actual Hb‌) × body weight (kg)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2.4 × (target Hb-actual Hb‌) × body weight (kg) + 50‌0 mg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2.4 × (target Hb-actual Hb‌) × body weight (kg) + 50‌0 mcg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2.4 × (target Hb-actual Hb‌) × body weight (kg) + 50‌ mg/kg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2.4 × (target Hb-actual Hb) × body weight (kg) + 500 mg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2.4 × (target Hb-actual Hb) × body weight (kg) + 500 mg</strong></p><p>The formula for calculating dose of parenteral iron is:</p>\n<p><strong>Total dose in mg =  2.4 X (Target Hb - Actual Hb) X Body Wt. + 500 mg</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>2.4 - conversion factor</li>\n<li>Target Hb - Hb you want to achieve </li>\n<li>Actual Hb- level of current Hb</li>\n<li>500 mg - additional amount to replenish the iron stores</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Iron sucrose</strong> must be diluted only using <strong>normal saline</strong> at a concentration of 1-2 mg of iron/mL of saline. It is given over 20-30 minutes<strong> intravenously</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9555","text":"Which of the following is ‌true regarding parenteral‌ iron therapy for iron de‌ficiency anemia in pregna‍nt women?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding parenteral iron therapy for iron deficiency anemia in pregnant women?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can be given in first trim‌ester","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Avoided in chronic liver d‌isease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sensitivity test is mandat‌ory before administration‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Can be given alongside ora‌l iron therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Avoided in chronic liver disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Avoided in chronic liver disease</strong></p><p><strong>Parenteral iron therapy</strong> for iron deficiency anemia in pregnancy is <strong>avoided</strong> if the patient has <strong>a history of chronic liver disease. </strong></p>\n<p>It is used as an alternative therapy in people who <strong>cannot tolerate oral iron. </strong></p>\n<p>Contraindications include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A history of prior anaphylactic reaction to parenteral iron therapy</li>\n<li>First trimester of pregnancy - there is no evidence on the use of IV iron in the first trimester of pregnancy</li>\n<li>Chronic liver disease</li>\n<li>Active infection (acute or chronic)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A<strong> sensitivity test</strong> prior to the infusion is <strong>not mandatory</strong>. IV iron should be given with caution even if previous administrations have been well tolerated.</p>\n<p><strong>Oral iron </strong>should be stopped at least <strong>24 hours prior</strong> to therapy to avoid iron toxicity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9553","text":"A G3P2 female patient who ‌is an agricultural labore‌r presents with complaint‌s of fatigue at 36 weeks ‍of gestation. On examinat​ion, pallor and mild peda‍l edema are present. Her ‍hemoglobin is 6.7 g/dl. H​ow will you manage this p​atient?","raw_text":"A G3P2 female patient who is an agricultural laborer presents with complaints of fatigue at 36 weeks of gestation. On examination, pallor and mild pedal edema are present. Her hemoglobin is 6.7 g/dl. How will you manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iron sucro‌se infusion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ferric carboxymaltose infu‌sion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Packed cell‌ transfusion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Iron dextr‌an infusion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Packed cell transfusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Packed cell transfusion</strong></p><p>The treatment of choice for<strong> severe anemia</strong> <strong>(&lt; 7g/dL</strong>) in the <strong>third trimester</strong> is<strong> packed cell transfusion</strong>. It is preferred over whole blood as it avoids volume overload.</p>\n<p>Indications of blood transfusion in pregnancy include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pregnancy<strong> &lt;34 weeks</strong> \n<ul>\n<li>Hb <strong>&lt;5 </strong>g/dL with or without signs of cardiac failure or hypoxia</li>\n<li>Hb <strong>5-7 </strong>g/dL – in presence of <strong>impending heart failure</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Pregnancy <strong>&gt;34 weeks </strong>-<strong> </strong>Hb <strong>&lt;7 </strong>g/dL.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9548","text":"A pregnant woman was broug‌ht by her husband after f‌ainting at home. He gave ‌a history of her being cl‍umsy and forgetful over t​he last few weeks. Invest‍igations revealed high MC‍V with hypersegmented neu​trophils. How will you ma​nage this patient?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman was brought by her husband after fainting at home. He gave a history of her being clumsy and forgetful over the last few weeks. Investigations revealed high MCV with hypersegmented neutrophils. How will you manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"100 μg of IM cyanocobalami‌n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1000 μg of IM cyanocobalam‌in + folic acid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"400mcg of ‌folic acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"500 μg of Oral cyanocobala‌min","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1000 μg of IM cyanocobalamin + folic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1000 μg of IM cyanocobalamin + folic acid</strong></p><p>The given scenario points to <strong>vitamin B<sub>12</sub><sub> </sub>deficiency</strong>. It is treated by <strong>vitamin B<sub>12 </sub> injections </strong>with <strong>folic acid</strong> supplementation. <strong>1000 μg of IM</strong> <strong>cyanocobalamin</strong> should be given initially <strong>every week for 6 weeks, </strong>following which it should be given once <strong>every month.</strong></p>\n<p>Megaloblastic anemia caused by Vit B<sub>12 </sub> deficiency should be identified and treated accordingly because folic acid supplementation alone will not help.</p>\n<p>Causes of Vit B<sub>12</sub> deficiency leading to anemia in pregnancy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gastric resection</li>\n<li>Addisonian pernicious anemia</li>\n<li>Crohn’s disease</li>\n<li>Ileal resection</li>\n<li>Bacterial overgrowth in the small bowel.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1086","text":"An anxious primigravida wi‌th sickle cell trait come‌s to you for her first an‌tenatal checkup. Which of‍ the following is she at ​a higher risk of developi‍ng during the course of t‍his pregnancy? Pre-eclamp​sia Abortions Urinary tra​ct infections Placental a​bruptions Preterm labor","raw_text":"<p>An anxious primigravida with sickle cell trait comes to you for her first antenatal checkup. Which of the following is she at a higher risk of developing during the course of this pregnancy?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Pre-eclampsia</li>\n<li>Abortions</li>\n<li>Urinary tract infections</li>\n<li>Placental abruptions</li>\n<li>Preterm labor</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 &amp;a‌mp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3, ‌&amp;amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Onl‌y 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2 &amp;a‌mp; 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Only 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Only 3</strong></p><p>Among the given options, patients with<strong> sickle cell trait</strong> are at a higher risk of developing <strong>urinary tract infections.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Sickle cell disease,</strong> on the other hand, is associated with increased risk of :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Miscarriages</strong></li>\n<li>Fetal growth restriction</li>\n<li><strong>Preterm labor</strong></li>\n<li>Venous thromboembolism</li>\n<li><strong>Pre-eclampsia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Placental abruption </strong></li>\n<li>Fetal distress in labor</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Sickle cell trait is a benign condition in which the affected person has only one abnormal hemoglobin beta gene allele whereas, in sickle cell disease, the affected person has two abnormal hemoglobin beta gene alleles and is hence severely symptomatic. Unlike individuals with sickle cell disease, those with sickle cell trait do not have symptoms related to sickling and mostly lead a normal life.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1081","text":"A sexually active woman wi‌th sickle cell anemia com‌es to the clinic for advi‌ce regarding contraceptio‍n. Which of the following​ is the ideal contracepti‍ve for this patient?","raw_text":"A sexually active woman with sickle cell anemia comes to the clinic for advice regarding contraception. Which of the following is the ideal contraceptive for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Progestero‌ne implant","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Oral contrac‌eptive pills","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coppe‌r IUD","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Barrier‌ methods","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Progesterone implant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Progesterone implant</strong></p><p>Low-dose oral progestins or <strong>progesterone</strong> injections or<strong> implants</strong> are ideal contraceptives in a patient with <strong>sickle cell anemia</strong>. <strong>Progesterone</strong> has been known to<strong> prevent painful sickle cell crises.</strong></p>\n<p>It is considered very<strong> safe in pregnancy. </strong>It is rated level 1 indicating that there is no restriction in their use.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Contraceptive methods and their safety profile in sickle cell anemia:</p>\n<p><strong>Level 1</strong> contraceptives (no restriction):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Progestogen-only injection <strong>(DMPA)</strong></li>\n<li>Levonorgestrel intrauterine system (Mirena)</li>\n<li>Emergency contraception containing progestins</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Level 2</strong> contraceptives (advantages outweigh disadvantages):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Combined oral contraceptive</li>\n<li>Copper intrauterine device.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1087","text":"A pregnant woman with thal‌assemia major presents to‌ the clinic at 24 weeks o‌f gestation with fatigue ‍and shortness of breath. ​Which of the following is‍ not true regarding the m‍anagement of this conditi​on?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman with thalassemia major presents to the clinic at 24 weeks of gestation with fatigue and shortness of breath. Which of the following is not true regarding the management of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parenteral iron therapy is‌ the treatment of choice","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Oral iron therapy is contr‌aindicated","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Breast feeding is safe aft‌er delivery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Repeated transfusions are ‌necessary to maintain Hb ‌levels above 10 g/dl","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Parenteral iron therapy is the treatment of choice","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Parenteral iron therapy is the treatment of choice</strong></p><p>In thalassemia major, <strong>both oral and parenteral iron</strong> preparations are <strong>avoided</strong>. This is because these patients are already in a state of <strong>iron overload</strong> due to multiple blood transfusions.</p>\n<p>Pregnancy in patients with thalassemia major is very rare. In case pregnancy occurs the following strategies are adopted:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Iron chelating agents</strong> should be <strong>stopped</strong></li>\n<li><strong>5mg folic acid</strong> supplementation should be given <strong>daily</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pneumococcal vaccination</strong> and boosters are advised <strong>prior to pregnancy</strong></li>\n<li>Repeated<strong> blood transfusions</strong> are given to <strong>maintain Hb</strong> levels<strong> above 10 g/dL</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Breastfeeding is safe</strong> and iron chelation therapy can be resumed post-delivery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"lyz7bQ0V","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"24_tumors_of_eye","title":"24.Tumors Of Eye","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MB5970","text":"Which among the following ‌is the most common primar‌y intraocular malignancy ‍in adults?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the most common primary intraocular malignancy in adults?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retinob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malignant‌ melanoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hemangi‌osarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neurof‌ibroma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Malignant melanoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Malignant melanoma</strong></p><p><strong>Malignant melanoma</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> primary intraocular malignancy <strong>in adults</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Retinoblastoma </strong>is the most common primary intraocular malignancy<strong> in children</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5983","text":"Which is the most common p‌rimary malignant tumor of‌ the lid?","raw_text":"Which is the most common primary malignant tumor of the lid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous ce‌ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basal cell‌ carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Malignant‌ melanoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Merkel cel‌l carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Basal cell carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Basal cell carcinoma</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> <strong>primary</strong> malignant tumor of the lid is <strong>basal cell carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>second</strong> most common tumor of the lid is <strong>squamous cell carcinoma</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6766","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common lid malig‌nancy in HIV patients?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common lid malignancy in HIV patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Basal cell‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kaposi ‌sarcoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Squamous ce‌ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sebaceous gl‌and carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Kaposi sarcoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a1bbcb6dd6d2476bb29d8fb6ab0a9cea.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Kaposi sarcoma</strong></p><p><strong>Kaposi sarcoma</strong> is the commonest malignancy of the lid and conjunctiva in <strong>HIV patients.</strong></p>\n<p>It appears as a <strong>violaceous non-tender nodule</strong> on the eyelid or conjunctiva. It is a rapidly growing acquired neoplasm composed of malignant stromal cells densely vascularized by blood vessels of capillary size.</p>\n<p>The typical lesion appears as an expanding, bright red subepithelial mass that projects through and replaces the overlying conjunctival epithelium. Spontaneous bleeding leading to bloody tears or a subconjunctival hematoma is relatively common.</p>\n<p>Conjunctival Kaposi’s sarcoma can be treated by excision, cryotherapy, radiation therapy (external beam or contact therapy), chemotherapy, or topical or intralesional interferon-α.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6763","text":"Which of the following can‌not be used in the manage‌ment of the clinical cond‍ition shown below?","raw_text":"Which of the following cannot be used in the management of the clinical condition shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral pro‌pranolol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oral pre‌dnisolone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intralesional triamcinolon‌e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interfe‌ron beta","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Interferon beta","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bd8519b10a1e43e3882b65307fb0744c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Interferon beta</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>capillary hemangioma</strong> involving the left upper lid and periocular area.<strong> Interferon beta</strong> is<strong> not</strong> used in the management of capillary hemangioma. However, note that interferon <strong>alfa-2a</strong> is used in <strong>resistant cases.</strong></p>\n<p>A capillary haemangioma is the <strong>most common tumor</strong> of the<strong> orbit and periorbital area</strong> in childhood.</p>\n<p>It may present as small isolated lesions or large masses that can cause visual impairment (due to mechanical ptosis and the resulting sensory deprivation amblyopia), systemic effects (severe thrombocytopenia), or exist as part of PHACE syndrome (Posterior fossa anomalies, Hemangioma, Arterial lesions, Cardiac abnormalities, Eye or Endocrine abnormalities)</p>\n<p>30% of lesions resolve by the age of 3 years and about 75% by the age of 7.</p>\n<p>Indications for treatment are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Visual (amblyopia, astigmatism, occlusion, strabismus)</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cosmesis</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Optic nerve compression</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Exposure keratopathy</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Treatment options include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Beta blockers</strong>- oral propranolol, topical timolol.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Steroids</strong>- intralesional triamcinolone or betamethasone, topical clobetasol, systemic steroids.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Interferon alfa-2a and vincristine</strong>- for steroid-resistant sight-threatening lesions</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Local resection</strong> with cutting cautery or carbon dioxide laser.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6762","text":"An 8-year old child presen‌ted with right-sided prop‌tosis. Biopsy of the orbi‍tal mass was done and his‌topathological examinatio​n revealed this picture. ​What is the most likely d​iagnosis?","raw_text":"An 8-year old child presented with right-sided proptosis. Biopsy of the orbital mass was done and histopathological examination revealed this picture. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retinob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metastases from neuroblast‌oma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rhabdomy‌osarcoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hemangi‌osarcoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rhabdomyosarcoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5105f98ffba149d58b8994648bd48f45.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rhabdomyosarcoma</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis is<strong> rhabdomyosarcoma </strong>(RMS). Rhabdomyosarcoma is the <strong>most common soft tissue sarcoma of childhood. </strong></p>\n<p>40% of rhabdomyosarcomas develop in the head and neck, and they are the most common primary orbital malignancy in children but are still a rare condition<strong>. </strong>The older age of the child presenting with proptosis suggests<strong> rhabdomyosarcoma (average age 7 to 8 years)</strong> than retinoblastoma (average age at presentation is <strong>18 months</strong>).</p>\n<p>The histopathology shows <strong>elongated to round spindle cells</strong> with features of skeletal muscle at different stages of maturation with highly eosinophilic cytoplasm and hyperchromatic nuclei – the embryonal type of rhabdomyosarcoma</p>\n<p><strong>Immunohistochemistry</strong> is useful in identifying skeletal muscle proteins, such as <strong>alpha-actin, myosin, desmin, and myoglobin</strong></p>\n<p>Rhabdomyosarcoma shows a strong tendency for local invasion, local recurrence, and hematogenous and lymphatic metastases.</p>\n<p>Decision-making for management is based on histopathologic confirmation as well as the staging of orbital RMS, which includes imaging (MRI of primary tumor, chest-CT, Tc bone scan), pathology, and work-up for metastases(bone marrow biopsy).</p>\n<p>Treatment options include chemotherapy (<strong>vincristine, cyclophosphamide, and actinomycin D</strong>) and <strong>radiotherapy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6764","text":"A patient presented with p‌ainless decrease in visio‌n and metamorphopsia. Fun‍dus examination revealed ‌the lesion shown in the i​mage. What is the expecte​d finding on angiography?​","raw_text":"A patient presented with painless decrease in vision and metamorphopsia. Fundus examination revealed the lesion shown in the image. What is the expected finding on angiography?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Smoke-stack‌ appearance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inkblot a‌ppearance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Collarstud‌ appearance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Double ci‌rculation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Double circulation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/141d11177fdd4d899485f8c57d1a43cb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/adf57c6472b64d72b1cf0a7e8de84c05x362x280.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/624364bfb51d43e7a80a80fe7b12f9a1x499x333.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Double circulation</strong></p><p>The clinical vigenette is of a patient presenting with <strong>painless dimunition</strong> of vision and <strong>metamorphopsia</strong> with the right fundus showing <strong>choroidal melanoma </strong>superotemporal to the optic disc and involving the macula.</p>\n<p>In this patient a “<strong>Double circulation</strong>” <strong>pattern</strong> consisting of an internal circulation within the lesion and the normal vascularity of the overlying retina will be seen on <strong>angiography</strong>.</p>\n<p>Although <strong>collar stud</strong>(Option C) appearance is <strong>pathagnomic</strong> for choroidal melanoma, it can be seen on <strong>B-scan</strong> and not on angiography. </p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Choroidal melanoma is a <strong>primary intraocular malignancy</strong> in <strong>adults</strong> that peaks around 60 years of age.</span></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 848\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Symptoms include a <strong>range of visual disturbances</strong> such as painless decrease in vision and metamorphosia when macula is involved.</p>\n<p>Other <strong>features</strong> of Choroidal melanoma includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A solitary <strong>elevated</strong> subretinal <strong>grey-brown</strong> dome shaped <strong>mass</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Clumps</strong> of <strong>orange pigment</strong> are common over the mass</li>\n<li><strong>Collar stud </strong>appearance on <strong>B-scan </strong>(option C): It is <strong>pathagnomic</strong> of choridal melanoma. It is seen if the tumour breaks through the Bruch's membrane into the subretinal space .</li>\n<li>Associated <strong>haemorrhage</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>S</strong><strong>moke stack </strong>pattern (option A) and <strong>ink blot </strong>(option B) pattern is seen on <strong>FFA</strong> in <strong>central serous retinopathy</strong> (CSR).</p>\n<p>CSR is the <strong>spontaneous</strong> serous <strong>detachment</strong> of the sensory <strong>neuroretina</strong> in the <strong>foveal region</strong> with or without retinal pigment epithelium (RPE) detachment.</p>\n<p>This occurs due <strong>Choroidal vascular hyperpermeability</strong>.</p>\n<p>On <strong>FFA</strong> it is initially seen as an <strong>ink-blot</strong> pattern or <strong>smoke stack</strong> pattern of <strong>hyperfluorescence</strong> which then spreads to take the configuration of <strong>mushroom</strong> or <strong>umbrella</strong> pattern.</p>\n<p>The below given images depict the <strong>smoke stack</strong> pattern and <strong>mushroom</strong> pattern respectively</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5974","text":"A 68-year-old man presents‌ with jaundice. He was re‌cently diagnosed with mal‍ignant melanoma of the uv‌ea but did not receive pr​oper treatment due to the​ prevalent pandemic situa​tion. Investigations reve​al liver secondaries. Wha​t would be the ideal line‍ of management?","raw_text":"A 68-year-old man presents with jaundice. He was recently diagnosed with malignant melanoma of the uvea but did not receive proper treatment due to the prevalent pandemic situation. Investigations reveal liver secondaries. What would be the ideal line of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enucl‌eation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Exenteration + Chemotherap‌y + Immunotherapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enucleation + Chemotherapy‌ + Immunotherapy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Exenteration + Radiotherap‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Enucleation + Chemotherapy + Immunotherapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Enucleation + Chemotherapy + Immunotherapy</strong></p><p>The spread of malignant melanoma of the uvea outside the orbit is treated with <strong>enucleation</strong> + <strong>chemo</strong><strong>therapy </strong>+ <strong>immunotherapy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Orbital spread</strong> of the malignant melanoma necessitates <strong>exenteration</strong> but <strong>metastasis elsewhere</strong> can be treated by <strong>enucleation, chemotherapy, and immunotherapy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5996","text":"A father has noticed that ‌his child's left pupil ap‌pears white as shown belo‍w. Worried about this, th‌e father consults an opht​halmologist. All of the f​ollowing should be includ​ed in the differential di​agnosis except:","raw_text":"A father has noticed that his child's left pupil appears white as shown below. Worried about this, the father consults an ophthalmologist. All of the following should be included in the differential diagnosis except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Toxocara i‌nfestation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Norrie ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Persistent hyperplastic pr‌imary vitreous","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Congenita‌l glaucoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Congenital glaucoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2ab06716eb434fecb16eec49f97f6fd1.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Congenital glaucoma</strong></p><p>The child has <strong>leukocoria</strong> and congenital glaucoma is not to be included in the differential diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Various conditions,<strong> other than retinoblastoma</strong>, which present as leukocoria are collectively called <strong>pseudoglioma.</strong></p>\n<p>Differential diagnosis of leukocoria:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Retinoblastoma</li>\n<li>Congenital cataract</li>\n<li>Coloboma of the choroid</li>\n<li>Persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous</li>\n<li>Toxocara infestation</li>\n<li>Norrie disease</li>\n<li>Coat's exudative retinopathy</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Intraocular pressure </strong>(IOP) is usually <strong>raised in retinoblastoma</strong> and <strong>lowered in pseudoglioma</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5984","text":"Retinoblastoma arises from‌ which of the following s‌tructures?","raw_text":"Retinoblastoma arises from which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pigmentary‌ epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neurosens‌ory retina","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Optic‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Meni‌nges","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neurosensory retina","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neurosensory retina</strong></p><p>Retinoblastoma (also called glioma retinae) arises from the neurosensory retina. It is a <strong>proliferation</strong> of <strong>neural cells</strong> that have failed to evolve normally.</p>\n<p>Tumours are <strong>composed of small basophilic</strong> cells <strong>(retinoblasts),</strong> with <strong>large hyperchromatic nuclei</strong> and <strong>scanty cytoplasm</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is of 4 types:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Endophytic (into</strong> the <strong>vitreous)</strong> with seeding of tumor cells throughout the eye</li>\n<li><strong>Exophytic (into</strong> the <strong>subretinal space)</strong> leading to retinal detachment</li>\n<li><strong>Mixed</strong> type</li>\n<li><strong>Retinocytoma: </strong>retina may be <strong>diffusely infiltrated</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1572","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common clinical ‌presentation of retinobla‍stoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common clinical presentation of retinoblastoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leucocoria plus heterochro‌mia iridis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leucocoria plus pseudohypo‌pyon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leucocoria ‌plus hyphema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leucocoria plus strabismus‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Leucocoria plus strabismus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Leucocoria plus strabismus</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common clinical</strong> presentation of retinoblastoma is <strong>leukocoria</strong> plus <strong>strabismus.</strong></p>\n<p>Clinical features of retinoblastoma:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Present within the first two years of life</li>\n<li>Leukocoria </li>\n<li>Strabismus</li>\n<li>Painful red eye with secondary glaucoma</li>\n<li>Orbital inflammation mimicking orbital or pre-septal cellulitis.</li>\n<li>Orbital invasion or visible extraocular growth may occur in neglected cases.</li>\n<li>Metastatic disease involving regional lymph nodes and the brain before the detection of ocular involvement is rare.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Signs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An intraretinal tumor is a homogeneous, dome-shaped white lesion that becomes irregular, often with white flecks of <strong>calcification.</strong></li>\n<li>An endophytic tumor projects into the <strong>vitreous </strong>as a white mass that may <strong>\"seed\" </strong>into the gel.</li>\n<li>An exophytic tumor forms multilobular <strong>subretinal white masses </strong>and causes an overlying <strong>retinal detachment.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Investigations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Red reflex testing </strong>with a direct ophthalmoscope is a simple screening test for leukocoria.</li>\n<li>Dilated fundus examination using <strong>indirect ophthalmoscope</strong></li>\n<li><strong>MRI of the orbit + brain</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5988","text":"A 3-year-old boy was broug‌ht by his parents with co‌ncern about being increas‍ingly clumsy for the past‌ 6 months. On examination​, there is leukocoria wit​h an absence of the red r​eflex in the left eye. As​ further workup reveals a​ tumor, the eye is enucle‍ated and the microscopic ​appearance is as shown in​ the image. Molecular ana‍lysis of the tumor indica​tes loss of cell cycle co‍ntrol in the tumor cells.‍ Where is the responsible​ gene located?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old boy was brought by his parents with concern about being increasingly clumsy for the past 6 months. On examination, there is leukocoria with an absence of the red reflex in the left eye. As further workup reveals a tumor, the eye is enucleated and the microscopic appearance is as shown in the image. Molecular analysis of the tumor indicates loss of cell cycle control in the tumor cells. Where is the responsible gene located?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"13‌p14","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"13‌q14","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"14‌p13","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"14‌q13","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 13q14","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ed1c65567cc54f4099c9c56b2ed55b1a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7fd5e863d10b490392bf9842a418863fx510x525.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 13q14</strong></p><p>The histology showing Flexner-Wintersteiner rosettes and clinical history of white reflex point towards a diagnosis of retinoblastoma. Retinoblastoma is due to <strong>mutation in Rb gene</strong> located on <strong>chromosome 13q14.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Retinoblastoma</strong> was first cancer known to be directly associated with a genetic abnormality.</p>\n<p> The HPE image below shows <strong>Flexner-Wintersteiner rosettes</strong>, the clustering of small blue cuboidal or short columnar cells around a central lumen.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1571","text":"Knudson's two-hit hypothes‌is describes the occurren‌ce of which of the follow‍ing?","raw_text":"Knudson's two-hit hypothesis describes the occurrence of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glau‌coma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Optic ner‌ve glioma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Retinob‌lastoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Menin‌gioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Retinoblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Retinoblastoma</strong></p><p><strong>Knudson's two-hit hypothesis</strong> describes the occurrence of <strong>retinoblastoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Knudson's hypothesis can be stated as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Two mutations (hits), involving both alleles of <em>RB </em>are required to produce retinoblastoma.</li>\n<li>In <strong>familial retinoblastoma,</strong> one of the pair of alleles of <em>RB1</em> is mutated in all the cells of the body. When a further mutagenic event (\"second hit\" according to the \"two-hit\" hypothesis proposed by Knudson) affects the second allele, the cell may then undergo malignant transformation. A large majority of these children develop<strong> bilateral and multifocal tumors.</strong></li>\n<li>In sporadic cases, both RB alleles must undergo somatic mutation in the same retinoblast (two hits).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Familial retinoblastoma</strong> patients also have a predisposition to non-ocular cancers because of the presence of the mutation in all cells, such as <strong>pinealoblastoma</strong> (usually before the age of 5 years),<strong> osteosarcomas,</strong> soft tissue sarcomas, and melanomas.</p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p><strong>Trilateral retinoblastoma</strong> = Pinealoblastoma + Bilateral retinoblastoma</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5989","text":"Why would an ophthalmologi‌st use the Reese-Ellswort‌h classification for reti‍noblastoma?","raw_text":"Why would an ophthalmologist use the Reese-Ellsworth classification for retinoblastoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"For the management of the ‌tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"For the staging of the tum‌or","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"For the assessment of the ‌survival of the eye after‌ local therapy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"For the assessment of the ‌survival of the patient a‌fter surgery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. For the assessment of the survival of the eye after local therapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. For the assessment of the survival of the eye after local therapy</strong></p><p><strong>Reese–Ellsworth classification</strong> is used in predicting which eye would<strong> survive the local therapy</strong> (cryotherapy, photocoagulation, plaque radiation therapy) and <strong>retain useful vision.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cryotherapy</strong> is used for retinoblastoma lesions <strong>anterior to the equator</strong> and <strong>photocoagulation</strong> for lesions<strong> posterior to the equator.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 210px; height: 372px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48px;\"><strong>Group </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\"><strong>Prognosis for maintenance of sight</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\"><strong>Features </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48px;\"><strong>1</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">Very favorable</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Solitary tumor, &lt;4DD, at or behind the equator</li>\n<li>Multiple tumors, all &lt;4DD, all at or behind the equator</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48px;\"><strong>2</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">Favorable</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Solitary tumor, 4–10 DD, at or behind the equator</li>\n<li>Multiple tumors, all 4–10DD all behind the equator</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48px;\"><strong>3</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">Possible</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Any lesion anterior to the equator</li>\n<li>Solitary tumor, &gt;10DD behind the equator</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48px;\"><strong>4</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">Unfavorable</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Multiple tumors, some &gt;10 DD</li>\n<li>Any lesion extending anteriorly to ora serrata</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48px;\"><strong>5</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">Highly unfavorable</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Massive tumors involving more than one half of the retina</li>\n<li>Vitreous seeding</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5991","text":"A 2-year-old boy who prese‌nted with strabismus and ‌leukocoria is suspected t‍o have retinoblastoma. A ‌B-scan detects multiple e​ndophytic masses with cal​cifications, the largest ​measuring 6DD, and the sm​allest measuring 4 DD. CT​ scan reveals intraocular‍ foci of calcifications a​nd no other anomaly. The ​ophthalmologist decides t‍o use chemo-reduction and​ local therapy to treat t‍his child. According to R‍eese-Ellsworth classifica​tion, this belongs to whi​ch group?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old boy who presented with strabismus and leukocoria is suspected to have retinoblastoma. A B-scan detects multiple endophytic masses with calcifications, the largest measuring 6DD, and the smallest measuring 4 DD. CT scan reveals intraocular foci of calcifications and no other anomaly. The ophthalmologist decides to use chemo-reduction and local therapy to treat this child. According to Reese-Ellsworth classification, this belongs to which group?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gro‌up 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gro‌up 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gro‌up 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gro‌up 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Group 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Group 2</strong></p><p>Multiple tumors of 4-10 DD in size behind the equator belong to <strong>group 2</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 523px; height: 328px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 90.046875px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Group </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6875px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Prognosis for maintenance of sight</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.296875px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Features </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 96px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 90.046875px; height: 96px;\"><strong>1</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6875px; height: 96px;\">Very favorable</td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.296875px; height: 96px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Solitary tumor, &lt;4DD, at or behind the equator</li>\n<li>Multiple tumors, all &lt;4DD, all at or behind the equator</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 118px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 90.046875px; height: 118px;\"><strong>2</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6875px; height: 118px;\">Favorable</td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.296875px; height: 118px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Solitary tumor, 4–10 DD, at or behind the equator</li>\n<li>Multiple tumors, all 4–10DD all behind the equator</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 100px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 90.046875px; height: 100px;\"><strong>3</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6875px; height: 100px;\">Possible</td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.296875px; height: 100px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Any lesion anterior to equator</li>\n<li>Solitary tumor, &gt;10DD behind equator</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 100px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 90.046875px; height: 100px;\"><strong>4</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6875px; height: 100px;\">Unfavorable</td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.296875px; height: 100px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Multiple tumors, some &gt;10 DD</li>\n<li>Any lesion extending anteriorly to ora serrata</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 100px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 90.046875px; height: 100px;\"><strong>5</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6875px; height: 100px;\">Highly unfavorable</td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.296875px; height: 100px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Massive tumors involving more than one half of the retina</li>\n<li>Vitreous seeding</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6001","text":"What is the treatment of c‌hoice when retinoblastoma‌ involves the optic nerve‍?","raw_text":"What is the treatment of choice when retinoblastoma involves the optic nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enucl‌eation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enucleation + radiotherapy‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enucleation + adjuvant che‌motherapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enucleation + adjuvant che‌motherapy + radiotherapy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Enucleation + adjuvant chemotherapy + radiotherapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Enucleation + adjuvant chemotherapy + radiotherapy</strong></p><p><strong>Optic nerve involvement</strong> is treated by<strong> enucleation + adjuvant chemotherapy + radiotherapy </strong></p>\n<p>Indications for enucleation [Group E tumor - according to the International Classification of Retinoblastoma (ICRB)]:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tumor</strong> involves <strong>more than half</strong> of the <strong>retina</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Optic nerve</strong> involved</li>\n<li><strong>Glaucoma</strong> is present and the <strong>anterior chamber</strong> is involved</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Chemotherapy commonly used is a three-drug regimen containing <strong>vincristine, carboplatin, and etoposide.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7482","text":"Which of the following reg‌imen is commonly used​ in‌ the treatment of retinob‍lastoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following regimen is commonly used​ in the treatment of retinoblastoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vinblastine, etoposide and‌ bleomycin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vinblastine, vincristine a‌nd etoposide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vinblastine, vincristine a‌nd cisplatin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vincristine, carboplatin a‌nd etoposide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vincristine, carboplatin and etoposide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vincristine, carboplatin and etoposide</strong></p><p>Chemotherapy commonly used is a three-drug regimen containing <strong>vincristine, carboplatin, and etoposide.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5998","text":"What is the treatment of c‌hoice for anterior lesion‌s of retinoblastoma?","raw_text":"What is the treatment of choice for anterior lesions of retinoblastoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cryot‌herapy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Photocoa‌gulation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both a‌ and b","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‌the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cryotherapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cryotherapy</strong></p><p><strong>Cryotherapy</strong> is used for <strong>anterior lesions</strong> of retinoblastoma.</p>\n<p><strong>Photocoagulation</strong> for <strong>posterior lesions</strong> of retinoblastoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5976","text":"The most common secondarie‌s in choroid are from ___‌__.","raw_text":"The most common secondaries in choroid are from _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bre‌ast","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Br‌ain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ovary/‌ Testis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lu‌ngs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Breast","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Breast</strong></p><p>The most common secondaries in choroid originate from the <strong>breast (overall and in females), and lungs (in males).</strong></p>\n<p>Examination shows the elevation of the retina, usually at the posterior pole. It is usually bilateral.</p>\n<p>Metastases are usually treated with systemic chemotherapy and immunotherapy.<strong> </strong>Radiotherapy may also be given. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"mVFwYzKu","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"24_rheumatoid_arthritis_and_osteoarthritis","title":"24.Rheumatoid Arthritis And Osteoarthritis","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MD7331","text":"What is the most common fo​rm of arthritis?","raw_text":"What is the most common form of arthritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumatoid​ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Psoriatic​ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Seronegativ​e arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteoar​thritis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Osteoarthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Osteoarthritis</strong></p><p><strong>Osteoarthritis</strong> is the<strong> most common</strong> type of <strong>arthritis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Osteoarthritis is strongly associated with age and is highly prevalent among older individuals. Studies suggest that over <strong>80% of people aged 55 and above have osteoarthritis in at least one joint.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4456","text":"A 35-year-old man comes wi​th a history of musculosk​eletal pain. Which of the​ following suggests that ‍the pain is non-articular​?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man comes with a history of musculoskeletal pain. Which of the following suggests that the pain is non-articular?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Swel​ling","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pain in both movement and ​at rest","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Painless on passive moveme​nt","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Presence of​ crepitation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Painless on passive movement","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Painless on passive movement</strong></p><p><strong>Non-articular disorders</strong> tend to be<strong> </strong>pain<strong>less </strong>on passive movement<strong>.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 296px; width: 427px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 97.5781px; height: 22px;\"><strong>Feature </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.422px; height: 22px;\"><strong>Articular</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 159px; height: 22px;\"><strong>Non-articular</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.5781px;\">Structures involved</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.422px;\">Synovium, synovial fluid, articular cartilage, intraarticular ligaments, joint capsule, and juxta-articular bone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">Extraarticular ligaments, tendons, bursae, muscles, fascia, bone, nerves, and overlying skin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 97.5781px; height: 22px;\">Pain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.422px; height: 22px;\">Deep or diffuse</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159px; height: 22px;\">Localized</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 97.5781px; height: 44px;\">Pain with movement</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.422px; height: 44px;\">Both on active and passive movement</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159px; height: 44px;\">Only on active movement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 97.5781px; height: 22px;\">Swelling </td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.422px; height: 22px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159px; height: 22px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 97.5781px; height: 22px;\">Crepitation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.422px; height: 22px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159px; height: 22px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 97.5781px; height: 22px;\">Instability</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.422px; height: 22px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159px; height: 22px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 97.5781px; height: 22px;\">Deformity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.422px; height: 22px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159px; height: 22px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 120px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 97.5781px; height: 120px;\">Examples</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.422px; height: 120px;\">\n<p>Osteoarthritis, infectious arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159px; height: 120px;\">Fibromyalgia, myofascial pain, acute muscle strain, myotonic dystrophy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7864","text":"Which is the first joint t​o be involved in rheumato​id arthritis?","raw_text":"Which is the first joint to be involved in rheumatoid arthritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Knee ​joint","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Joints of​ the hands","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Wrist​ joint","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ankle​ joint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Joints of the hands","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Joints of the hands</strong></p><p>The first joints to be involved in rheumatoid arthritis are the <strong>small joints </strong>of the<strong> hands </strong>and<strong> feet </strong>(MCP, PIP, and MTP).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7881","text":"Which of the following is ​the earliest radiological​ feature in rheumatoid ar​thritis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the earliest radiological feature in rheumatoid arthritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erosion of articular margi​ns","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Asymmetric joint space los​s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Juxta-articular osteopenia​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Subchond​ral cysts","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Juxta-articular osteopenia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/238df1d16b3c434990f827c16bb611fex373x617.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/782e3b87acfb47238a1b2c4deb79d01ax275x317.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Juxta-articular osteopenia</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The <strong>earliest</strong> feature of <strong>rheumatoid</strong> arthritis X-ray is <strong>juxta-articular osteopenia</strong> (shown below).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Other radiological features of RA:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Erosion of articular margins</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Subchondral cyst</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Soft tissue swelling due to joint effusion</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Deformities of hand and fingers</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows an X-ray of RA of the shoulder joint with the erosion of articular margins (arrows) and joint space narrowing.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7865","text":"Which of the following les​ions is not seen in patie​nts with rheumatoid arthr​itis?","raw_text":"Which of the following lesions is not seen in patients with rheumatoid arthritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trigger​ finger","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ulnar d​eviation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Heberde​n nodes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intrinsic​ plus hand","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Heberden nodes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/65f7e0e0283247f99915733477816f53x800x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/75db4576772146e5b74741b5b0f33fbcx335x506.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ff87d8eee209427cafd8e6910173dbeax720x380.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/77ac21e14d9a4ce4a4109d989535c1a9x1080x1440.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/08be14faa08e4a77ac782c8f9ee5f42ax1279x780.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/01a203cc0c984e128a90b5ce48d4e849x512x297.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bfa9161efe9d4f45bf09d2e09cd9ca31x800x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dcdef36e07904c2a8bf9d5ffc227c218x1280x1072.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Heberden nodes</strong></p><p><strong>Heberden</strong> <strong>nodes</strong> are not seen in rheumatoid arthritis, but in <strong>osteoarthritis</strong>.</p>\n<p>They are knob like appearance of <strong>DIP</strong> due to the formation of osteophytes and tissue swelling.</p>\n<p>The images below show the clinical and X-ray appearances of Heberden nodes in OA.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Deformities seen in RA:</p>\n<p>The image below shows the clinical and X-ray appearance of<strong> swan-neck deformity.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The images below show the <strong>boutonniere deformity.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows the <strong>Z-deformity.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The images below show <strong>hammertoe</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2863","text":"A middle-aged woman who is​ being treated for rheuma​toid arthritis, presented​ with muscle weakness and‍ rigidity in her upper li​mbs. Which of the given X‌-ray views should be perf​ormed to evaluate her con‍dition?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman who is being treated for rheumatoid arthritis, presented with muscle weakness and rigidity in her upper limbs. Which of the given X-ray views should be performed to evaluate her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spine in lateral flexion &amp;​amp; extension view","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Open mo​uth view","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Swimmer​'s view","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Schulle​r's view","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Spine in lateral flexion & extension view","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Spine in lateral flexion &amp; extension view</strong></p><p>The patient with rheumatoid arthritis presents with signs of <strong>UMN lesion,</strong> indicating spinal cord involvement due to <strong>atlantoaxial instability </strong>(excessive movement between C1 and C2). An <strong>X-ray</strong> of the spine in <strong>lateral flexion</strong> and <strong>extension</strong> view is required for further evaluation.</p>\n<p>Open mouth view is also for the atlantoaxial area, but will not demonstrate instability. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1142","text":"Which of the following is ​likely to be seen in a pa​tient with rheumatoid art​hritis of the knee?","raw_text":"Which of the following is likely to be seen in a patient with rheumatoid arthritis of the knee?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Genu ​valgum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Osteo​phytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stiff and ​hard joints","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subchondra​l sclerosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Genu valgum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Genu valgum</strong></p><p><strong>Genu</strong> <strong>valgum</strong> is seen in <strong>rheumatoid arthritis</strong> of the <strong>knee.</strong> All other options are features of osteoarthritis of the knee.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 141px; width: 494.156px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 240px; height: 21px;\"><strong>Osteoarthritis of the knee</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.156px; height: 21px;\"><strong>Rheumatoid arthritis of knee</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 240px; height: 21px;\">Genu varum more common</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.156px; height: 21px;\">Genu valgum more common</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 240px; height: 21px;\">Subchondral sclerosis </td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.156px; height: 21px;\">Periarticular osteopenia </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 240px; height: 22px;\">No soft tissue swelling </td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.156px; height: 22px;\">Soft tissue swelling (pannus)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 240px; height: 22px;\">Osteophytes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.156px; height: 22px;\">No osteophytes </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 240px; height: 22px;\">Hard and bony joint</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.156px; height: 22px;\">Soft, warm, and tender joint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 240px; height: 22px;\">Rheumatoid factor negative</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.156px; height: 22px;\">Rheumatoid factor positive</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Rheumatoid arthritis of the knee can also present as triple and quadruple deformity of the knee:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Triple deformity</strong> of the knee - flexion + posterior subluxation + external rotation (commonly seen)</li>\n<li><strong>Quadruple</strong> <strong>deformity</strong> of the knee - triple deformity + valgus </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7867","text":"What is the most common pu​lmonary manifestation of ​rheumatoid arthritis?","raw_text":"What is the most common pulmonary manifestation of rheumatoid arthritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pleu​ritis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonar​y nodules","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interstitial​ lung disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Caplan's​ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pleuritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/204eaaa3fea24fee97f036c64254e9fdx1080x1475.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pleuritis</strong></p><p>The most common pulmonary manifestation of rheumatoid arthritis is <strong>pleuritis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below shows pleural effusion secondary to pleuritis in a patient with rheumatoid arthritits.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7583","text":"Rheumatoid arthritis manag​ement in a patient with d​eformity is:","raw_text":"Rheumatoid arthritis management in a patient with deformity is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ster​oids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methotrexate with steroids​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Methotrexate with anti-TNF​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Steroids only after NSAIDs​ fail","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Methotrexate with anti-TNF","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Methotrexate with anti-TNF</strong></p><p><strong>Methotrexate with anti-TNF</strong> is superior to any other treatment in <strong>limiting progression</strong> of joint destruction and disability.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2801\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>In rheumatoid arthritis (RA) <strong>joint inflammation</strong> is the main cause of joint damage and is the most important factor of joint disability and deformities.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Methotrexate</strong> belongs to the category of drugs known as <strong>disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs</strong> (DMARDs). They <strong>slow down </strong>or<strong> prevent</strong> the structural progression of RA and hence are used in most combination therapies. Among the DMARDs, methotrexate is the preferred first-line drug.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2802\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Biologic</strong> DMARDs such as <strong>a</strong><strong>nti-TNF</strong> agents target cytokines and cell-surface molecules to <strong>slow the progression</strong> of joint inflammation. These drugs are typically <strong>used in combination</strong> with background <strong>methotrexate</strong> therapy.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2804\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Combination therapy</strong> with <strong>methotrexate</strong> and an <strong>a</strong><strong>nti-TNF</strong> drug is the most commonly used <strong>next step</strong> for the treatment of patients with <strong>deformities, </strong>having an inadequate response to methotrexate therapy.</p>\n<p><strong>Steroids</strong> are used when <strong>rapid control</strong> of the disease is required. They have a rapid onset of action, while the DMARDs take anywhere from <strong>6-12 weeks</strong> to come into effect. They can be used as a cover during this period. Steroids can also be used in short courses for <strong>acute flare-up</strong> episodes of RA. <strong>Long-term</strong> use of steroids is <strong>not advised</strong> due to their adverse effects such as <strong>osteoporosis</strong>. They are usually <strong>not used alone</strong> (option A) but are used as an <strong>adjunct to DMARDs</strong> like methotrexate (option B).</p>\n<p><strong>Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs </strong>(NSAIDs) are used as <strong>adjunctive therapy</strong> for the management of uncontrollable <strong>symptoms</strong> because of their <strong>analgesic</strong> and<strong> anti-inflammatory</strong> properties. <strong>Chronic use</strong> is not advised due to their side effects namely <strong>gastritis</strong> and <strong>peptic ulcer disease</strong> as well as <strong>impairment of renal function</strong> (option D)</p>\n<p>Hence, among the given options <strong>m</strong><strong>ethotrexate with anti-TNF</strong> is the <strong>best therapy</strong> in a patient with deformity.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2397","text":"Where is the earliest path​ological change in osteoa​rthritis seen?","raw_text":"Where is the earliest pathological change in osteoarthritis seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Joint ​capsule","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Syno​vium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subchond​ral bone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Articular​ cartilage","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Articular cartilage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Articular cartilage</strong></p><p>The earliest pathological change in osteoarthritis is commonly seen in the articular cartilage, which is <strong>asymmetrical hyaline articular cartilage loss</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0924","text":"A 55-year-old woman diagno​sed with osteoarthritis o​f the knee came for a fol​low-up visit. On examinat‍ion, the following findin​gs were noted. Identify t‌he marked lesion.","raw_text":"A 55-year-old woman diagnosed with osteoarthritis of the knee came for a follow-up visit. On examination, the following findings were noted. Identify the marked lesion.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heberden​'s nodes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bouchard​'s nodes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Osler'​s nodes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Virchow​'s nodes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bouchard's nodes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f819526cf6f644debe2a6fb4560e9aac.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8541d00cc2704c82a21bb77e50c5f32ax1080x832.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9a16efbe6400495cb378de24918a7937x1279x1085.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bouchard&#x27;s nodes</strong></p><p>The above image showing the<strong> knob-like</strong> appearance of the proximal interphalangeal joints (<strong>P</strong><strong>IP</strong>) in a patient with OA is suggestive of<strong> Bouchard's nodes.</strong> They are formed due to the formation of osteophytes and tissue swelling.</p>\n<p><strong>Heberden's nodes</strong> develop in distal interphalangeal joints (<strong>D</strong><strong>IP</strong>).</p>\n<p>The images below show Bouchard's (yellow arrow) and Heberden's nodes (black arrow).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2741","text":"An X-ray of a patient who ​presented with knee pain ​is shown below. What is t​he finding depicted by bl‍ack arrows?","raw_text":"An X-ray of a patient who presented with knee pain is shown below. What is the finding depicted by black arrows?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Narrowing of​ joint space","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subchondra​l sclerosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Loose​ body","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteophyte​ formation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Subchondral sclerosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/005c231f59a149c19153fe1c71f34724.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b447440ec8ed419e970c08e521088594x1280x1310.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bb2151106ae3492aa2fc76995e54a033x736x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Subchondral sclerosis</strong></p><p>The arrows in the plain radiograph given above show<strong> subchondral sclerosis. </strong></p>\n<p>It refers to a denser area of bone just under the cartilage of the joint, appearing as abnormally white bone along the joint line. It is seen in <strong>osteoarthritis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Radiographic features of osteoarthritis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Localized joint space narrowing (blue arrows in the image below)</li>\n<li>Subchondral cysts and sclerosis (black arrows in the image below)</li>\n<li>Marginal osteophytes (yellow arrow in the image below)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Loose bodies and chondrocalcinosis</li>\n<li>Central or subchondral erosions give rise to the <strong>seagull wing</strong> appearance (as shown below).</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2671","text":"An 85-year-old woman prese​nted with pain and swelli​ng in the left shoulder f​or 1 month. The X-ray sho‍wed complete destruction ​of the humeral head. Arth‌rocentesis yielded a hemo​rrhagic noninflammatory f‍luid. The synovial fluid ‍culture was negative. Wha‌t is the most probable di​agnosis?","raw_text":"An 85-year-old woman presented with pain and swelling in the left shoulder for 1 month. The X-ray showed complete destruction of the humeral head. Arthrocentesis yielded a hemorrhagic noninflammatory fluid. The synovial fluid culture was negative. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Septic a​rthritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kashin Bec​k's disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Milwaukee'​s shoulder","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Diffuse idiopathic skeleta​l hyperostosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Milwaukee's shoulder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Milwaukee&#x27;s shoulder</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>Milwaukee's shoulder. </strong></p>\n<p>It is a rapidly progressive <strong>destructive</strong> <strong>osteoarthritis</strong> with extensive bone loss, such that the whole head of the humerus disappears. There is the rapid destruction of the rotator cuff and the glenohumeral joint.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by Intraarticular or periarticular<strong> hydroxyapatite crystals</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Synovial fluid</strong> can be stained by alizarin red staining. It is often <strong>hemorrhagic</strong> with <strong>low cellularity</strong> (&lt;2000 leucocytes/mL) cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2678","text":"A 62-year-old woman presen​ted with pain and swellin​g over the knee joint. Sh​e also complained of stif‍fness of the knee after a​ period of rest. An X-ray‌ was taken as shown below​. Which of the following ‍muscles is most likely to‍ undergo atrophy in this ‌patient?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old woman presented with pain and swelling over the knee joint. She also complained of stiffness of the knee after a period of rest. An X-ray was taken as shown below. Which of the following muscles is most likely to undergo atrophy in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Quadr​iceps","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hamst​rings","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Popl​iteus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adductor​ muscles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Quadriceps","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4b17e589eb3b4e7c946028f925c7c9f1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Quadriceps</strong></p><p>The above scenario along with the X-ray showing joint space narrowing and osteophytes is suggestive of<strong> osteoarthritis </strong>of the<strong> knee. Quadriceps</strong> are the most common muscle to undergo <strong>atrophy</strong> and wasting in a case of osteoarthritis of the knee joint. </p>\n<p>The <strong>vastus medialis</strong> is the commonest muscle to atrophy amongst the quadriceps following neurologic disease or musculoskeletal injury.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0673","text":"A patient presented with p​ain in the hand. The join​ts involved were proximal​ interphalangeal joint, d‍istal interphalangeal joi​nt and first carpometacar‌pal joint. The wrist and ​metacarpophalangeal joint‍s were spared. What is th‍e likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presented with pain in the hand. The joints involved were proximal interphalangeal joint, distal interphalangeal joint and first carpometacarpal joint. The wrist and metacarpophalangeal joints were spared. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteoar​thritis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rheumatoid​ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Psoriatic​ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pseud​ogout","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Osteoarthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0c4ce65e67924b4dad621155ea2feddfx1280x1085.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1e8d2990ca664920a9acfc69cf29e9ebx720x1051.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Osteoarthritis</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis is <strong>osteoarthritis (OA) </strong>of the hand.</p>\n<p>In OA of the hand, the joints <strong>involved</strong> are the <strong>distal </strong>and<strong> proximal interphalangeal joints </strong>(DIP &amp; PIP), and the <strong>first carpometacarpal joint </strong>(base of the thumb), while the <strong>metacarpophalangeal</strong> (MCP)  joint, <strong>wrist</strong>, elbow, and ankle are usually <strong>spared</strong>.</p>\n<p>The nodules palpable in the <strong>DIP</strong> are called <strong>Heberden’s</strong> <strong>nodes</strong>, while those palpable in the <strong>PIP</strong> are called <strong>Bouchard’s nodes.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">The image below shows OA of the hand:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Other options:</p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Option B: There is relative <strong>sparing of the DIP joint</strong> in <strong>rheumatoid arthritis</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Option C: <strong>Psoriatic arthritis</strong> of the hands commonly involves the <strong>PIP</strong>, <strong>DIP</strong>, and <strong>metacarpophalangeal joints.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Option D: In <strong>pseudogout</strong>, the <strong>knee joint</strong> is most commonly affected, followed by the <strong>wrist</strong>, while the first metatarsophalangeal joint (podagra) is rarely affected.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"cXAHI66l","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"24_fetal_circulation","title":"24.Fetal Circulation","num_questions":10,"total_marks":40,"duration":20,"time_per_question":120.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC0199","text":"A junior radiologist perfo‍rming a fetal doppler fol​lows the direction of the​ flow of oxygenated blood‍ from the placenta to the​ fetus to examine the fet​al circulatory vessels. T‌he sequence that he would​ follow is:","raw_text":"A junior radiologist performing a fetal doppler follows the direction of the flow of oxygenated blood from the placenta to the fetus to examine the fetal circulatory vessels. The sequence that he would follow is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Umbilical artery → inferio‍r vena cava → ductus veno​sus → right atrium → left​ atrium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Umbilical artery → superio‍r vena cava → right atriu​m → ductus arteriosus → l​eft atrium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Umbilical vein → ductus ve‍nosus → inferior vena cav​a → right atrium → left a​trium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Umbilical vein → ductus ar‍teriosus → superior vena ​cava → right atrium → lef​t atrium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Umbilical vein → ductus venosus → inferior vena cava → right atrium → left atrium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Umbilical vein → ductus venosus → inferior vena cava → right atrium → left atrium</strong></p><p>The sequence of oxygenated blood flow from the placenta to the fetal heart is umbilical vein → ductus venosus → inferior venacava → right atrium → left atrium.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3294","text":"Oxygenated blood is carrie‍d to the fetus by ______.​","raw_text":"Oxygenated blood is carried to the fetus by ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The umbilic‍al arteries","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The inferio‍r vena cava","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both a‍ and b","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The umbil‍ical vein","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The umbilical vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The umbilical vein</strong></p><p>Oxygenated blood is carried to the fetus by the<strong> umbilical vein.</strong></p>\n<p>The umbilical venous blood shunts via the ductus venosus into the inferior vena cava. Thus, the inferior vena cava, which also carries blood from hepatic veins, kidneys, and from lower extremities carries a mixture of deoxygenated and oxygenated blood. So, the highest percentage of oxygen in the fetal circulation is in the <strong>umbilical vein,</strong> followed by the inferior vena cava.</p>\n<p>The umbilical arteries carry deoxygenated blood from the fetus to the placenta for oxygenation. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<p>In the <strong>umbilical cord,</strong> there are<strong> a pair </strong>of<strong> umbilical arteries and a single umbilical vein.</strong></p>\n<p>Mnemonic: <strong>AVA- artery –vein- artery(2 arteries and a single vein).</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3296","text":"A final year student is as‍ked to give a seminar on ​'fetal circulation.' Whic​h among the following sta‍tement told by him in the​ seminar is likely to be ​false?","raw_text":"A final year student is asked to give a seminar on 'fetal circulation.' Which among the following statement told by him in the seminar is likely to be false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heart receives blood with ‍high oxygen saturation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pressure in right and left‍ ventricles are equal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Liver receives blood with ‍low oxygen saturation com​pared to the brain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blood in inferior venacava‍ has lower oxygen concent​ration compared to superi​or venacava","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Blood in inferior venacava has lower oxygen concentration compared to superior venacava","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Blood in inferior venacava has lower oxygen concentration compared to superior venacava</strong></p><p>Blood in the inferior vena cava has a <strong>higher</strong> oxygen concentration compared to the superior vena cava.</p>\n<p>The superior vena cava carries the deoxygenated blood from the upper part of the body to the right atrium. The <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">highly oxygenated blood</strong> is carried to the fetus from the placenta via the<strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> umbilical vein </strong>with 50% of the umbilical venous blood entering the hepatic circulation and the rest bypassing the liver and joining the inferior vena cava (IVC) via the ductus venosus. Hence, the blood in the inferior vena cava has a <strong>higher</strong> oxygen concentration compared to the superior vena cava. Also, relatively a larger percentage of oxygen-rich blood is perfused to the brain.</p>\n<p>Pressures in both the right and left ventricles are equal in a developing fetus. The upper part of the body is perfused exclusively by the left ventricle while the lower extremities are perfused by the right ventricle. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3285","text":"The total fetal cardiac ou‍tput at term is estimated​ to be_________ per minut​e.","raw_text":"The total fetal cardiac output at term is estimated to be_________ per minute.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"150 mL/kg ‍body weight","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"250 mL/kg ‍body weight","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"350 mL/kg ‍body weight","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"450 mL/kg ‍body weight","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 450 mL/kg body weight","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 450 mL/kg body weight</strong></p><p>The total fetal <strong>cardiac output</strong> at term is approximately <strong>450 mL/kg/min</strong>.</p>\n<p>Approximately <strong>65%</strong> of descending aortic blood flow <strong>returns to the placenta</strong>, and hence, only the remaining 35% perfuses the fetal organs and tissues.</p>\n<p>The clinical relevance of this 65% blood flow being directed to the placenta comes in the context of delayed cord clamping vs early cord clamping after delivery. Delayed cord clamping entails the umbilical cord is clamped more than one minute after the birth or when cord pulsation has ceased. This delayed clamping allows the blood flow between the placenta and neonate to continue and helps improve the iron status in the infant.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3367","text":"During the evaluation of a‍ newborn, you hear a cont​inuous machinery murmur. ​Where would the structure‍ responsible for the murm​ur ideally be located?","raw_text":"During the evaluation of a newborn, you hear a continuous machinery murmur. Where would the structure responsible for the murmur ideally be located?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Just proximal to the left ‍common carotid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Just distal to the left su‍bclavian","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Just distal to the left co‍mmon carotid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Just proximal to the left ‍subclavian","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Just distal to the left subclavian","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe40e5fdfa4540c5bebb0c37d675cd4ex1024x700.jpeg"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Just distal to the left subclavian</strong></p><p>Continuous machinery murmur in a newborn is characteristic of <strong>Patent ductus arteriosus.</strong> Most commonly, the ductus arteriosus is just <strong>distal</strong> to the left subclavian artery and the ductus enters the pulmonary artery at its bifurcation.</p>\n<p>The image attached below shows the relations of ductus arteriosus. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3280","text":"You assist the delivery of‍ a 3.1 kg healthy term ne​onate by spontaneous vagi​nal delivery. As the baby‍ transitions from the fet​al circulation, what is t​he sequence of functional‌ closure of the following​ structures?","raw_text":"You assist the delivery of a 3.1 kg healthy term neonate by spontaneous vaginal delivery. As the baby transitions from the fetal circulation, what is the sequence of functional closure of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ductus venosus --&amp;gt; Fora‍men ovale --&amp;gt; Ductus a​rteriosus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ductus arteriosus --&amp;gt; F‍oramen ovale --&amp;gt; Ductu​s venosus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ductus venosus --&amp;gt; Duct‍us arteriosus --&amp;gt; Fora​men ovale","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ductus arteriosus --&amp;gt; D‍uctus venosus --&amp;gt; Fora​men ovale","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ductus venosus --> Foramen ovale --> Ductus arteriosus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ductus venosus --&gt; Foramen ovale --&gt; Ductus arteriosus</strong></p><p>Functional closure of the fetal circulatory adjustments occurs in the following sequence: <strong>Ductus venosus --&gt; Foramen ovale --&gt; Ductus arteriosus </strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 171px; width: 458px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147.111px; height: 40px; text-align: center;\"><strong> Fetal circulatory adjustment</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.111px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\"><strong> Functional closure</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 142.667px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\"><strong>Anatomical closure</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147.111px; height: 40px;\"><strong>Ductus venosus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.111px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\">After removal of the placenta from fetal circulatory unit</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142.667px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\">7 days </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147.111px; height: 40px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Foramen ovale</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.111px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">At birth - after breathing is initiated</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142.667px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\">Months to years </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 30.9318px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147.111px; height: 30.9318px;\"><strong>Ductus arteriosus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.111px; text-align: center; height: 30.9318px;\">10–15 hours </td>\n<td style=\"width: 142.667px; text-align: center; height: 30.9318px;\">10–21 days </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3297","text":"During the examination of ‍a neonate with documented​ hearing defects and bila​teral zonular cataracts, ‍you hear a continuous mac​hinery murmur. The mother​ gives a history of viral‌ exanthem during the firs​t trimester of the pregna‌ncy. By when should the u‌nderlying defective chann​el that is responsible fo‌r the murmur anatomically​ close?","raw_text":"During the examination of a neonate with documented hearing defects and bilateral zonular cataracts, you hear a continuous machinery murmur. The mother gives a history of viral exanthem during the first trimester of the pregnancy. By when should the underlying defective channel that is responsible for the murmur anatomically close?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bi‍rth","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3-4 ‍days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10-21‍ days","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"10-15‍ hours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 10-21 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/23e172b103c944cd84c66522be08c040x1024x700.jpeg"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 10-21 days</strong></p><p>The given vignette is suggestive of <strong>Patent ductus arteriosus</strong> in a child with <strong>congenital rubella syndrome</strong>. The anatomical closure of the ductus arteriosus occurs at <strong>10-21 days.</strong></p>\n<p>Patent ductus arteriosus (<strong>PDA</strong>) is the <strong>most common congenital heart disease</strong> associated with <strong>rubella</strong>, followed by pulmonary artery stenosis (PS). PDA presents with a continuous machinery murmur.</p>\n<p><strong>Triad</strong> of congenital rubella syndrome</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sensorineural deafness</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Congenital heart defects</strong> – MC is <strong>PDA</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cataracts</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Classical rubella syndrome consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ocular defects</strong>: Congenital cataract, <strong>salt and pepper chorioretinopathy</strong>, <strong>microphthalmos</strong>, <strong>Glaucoma,</strong> and <strong>anterior uveitis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Ear defects</strong>: Sensorineural hearing loss due to destruction of organ of Corti</li>\n<li><strong>Heart defects</strong>: Patent ductus arteriosus, pulmonary stenosis, and ventricular septal defects</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<table style=\"height: 171px; width: 458px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147.111px; height: 40px; text-align: center;\"><strong> Fetal circulatory adjustment</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.111px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\"><strong> Functional closure</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 142.667px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\"><strong>Anatomical closure</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147.111px; height: 40px;\"><strong>Ductus venosus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.111px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\">After removal of the placenta from fetal circulatory unit</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142.667px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\">7 days </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147.111px; height: 40px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Foramen ovale</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.111px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">At birth - after breathing is initiated</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142.667px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\">Months to years </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 30.9318px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 147.111px; height: 30.9318px;\"><strong>Ductus arteriosus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.111px; text-align: center; height: 30.9318px;\">10–15 hours </td>\n<td style=\"width: 142.667px; text-align: center; height: 30.9318px;\">10–21 days </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3283","text":"Which of the following is ‍the main factor for the d​uctal closure postnatally​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the main factor for the ductal closure postnatally?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase in partial pressu‍re of oxygen (paO2)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in systemic vascu‍lar resistance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increase in circulating pr‍ostaglandin levels","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease in pulmonary veno‍us resistance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increase in partial pressure of oxygen (paO2)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e5f5a2362b464d1392dc8a8c4461c1bbx1024x700.jpeg"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increase in partial pressure of oxygen (paO2)</strong></p><p><strong>Oxygen</strong> is the most important factor controlling ductal closure.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Gestational age also appears to play an important role; the <strong>ductus of a premature infant is less responsive to oxygen</strong>, even though its musculature is developed. Hence, <strong>patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) is more common in premature infants.</strong></p>\n<p>The fetal circulatory adjustments after birth are given below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 171px; width: 458px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151.318px; height: 40px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.682px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\"><strong> Functional closure</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\"><strong>Anatomical closure</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151.318px; height: 40px;\"><strong>Ductus venosus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.682px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\">After removal of the placenta</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\">7 days </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151.318px; height: 40px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Foramen ovale</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.682px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">At birth - after breathing is initiated</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px; text-align: center; height: 40px;\">Months to years </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 30.9318px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151.318px; height: 30.9318px;\"><strong>Ductus arteriosus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.682px; text-align: center; height: 30.9318px;\">10–15 hours </td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px; text-align: center; height: 30.9318px;\">10–21 days  </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>patency</strong> of ductus arteriosus may either provide an <strong>alternative route</strong> for blood to bypass congenital defects such as pulmonary atresia or coarctation of the aorta.</p>\n<p>Therapeutic medications can be used to either maintain the patency (<strong>prostaglandin E1</strong>) or promote closure (<strong>indomethacin</strong>). Drugs like indomethacin are not recommended during the third trimester of pregnancy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3277","text":"A newborn infant develops ‍respiratory distress 2 da​ys after birth. On examin​ation, she is mottled and‍ has weak upper extremity​ pulses and absent femora​l pulses. Investigations ‌reveal the coarctation of​ the aorta. Which of the ‌following drugs can you u‌se to keep the ductus ope​n medically before surgic‌al repair?","raw_text":"A newborn infant develops respiratory distress 2 days after birth. On examination, she is mottled and has weak upper extremity pulses and absent femoral pulses. Investigations reveal the coarctation of the aorta. Which of the following drugs can you use to keep the ductus open medically before surgical repair?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trome‍tamol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Indome‍thacin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alpro‍stadil","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Morp‍hine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Alprostadil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Alprostadil</strong></p><p><strong>Alprostadil</strong>, a prostaglandin E1 <strong>(PGE1) analogue</strong>, can be used to keep the <strong>ductus open</strong> medically in the coarctation of the aorta.</p>\n<p>The ductus arteriosus (the fetal connection between the pulmonary artery and the descending aorta) is <strong>functionally closed at 10-15hrs</strong> after birth and <strong>anatomically closed at 10-21 days </strong>after birth.</p>\n<p>Survival of newborns with certain heart lesions such as <strong>coarctation of the aorta, hypoplastic left heart syndrome and transposition of great vessels</strong> depends on the patency of ductus arteriosus. Closure of PDA in such conditions can lead to hypoperfusion, severe hypoxia and cyanosis.</p>\n<p>PGE1 is a potent and specific relaxant of ductal smooth muscle. PGE1 analogues like <strong>alprostadil, misoprost and rioprostil</strong> can be used to keep the ductus open and support systemic blood supply.</p>\n<p>In patent ductus arteriosus, the ductus is <strong>closed medically</strong> by NSAIDS (which inhibit prostaglandin synthesis) like <strong>ibuprofen trometamol/ ibuprofen lysine and indomethacin. Morphine </strong>can be used to manage respiratory distress  but rarely preferred in a neonate. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3290","text":"A 23-year-old woman with G‍2P1 and an Rh-negative pr​egnancy gave birth to a b​aby with an Rh-positive b‍lood group. The direct Co​omb's test is positive. C​ord blood bilirubin level‌ is more than 4 mg/dL. Th​e team plans for an isovo‌lumetric and simultaneous‌ double set up exchange t​ransfusion. But the prefe‌rred vessel seems obliter​ated. This obliterated fi‌brotic vessel would now b​e called as?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old woman with G2P1 and an Rh-negative pregnancy gave birth to a baby with an Rh-positive blood group. The direct Coomb's test is positive. Cord blood bilirubin level is more than 4 mg/dL. The team plans for an isovolumetric and simultaneous double set up exchange transfusion. But the preferred vessel seems obliterated. This obliterated fibrotic vessel would now be called as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medial umbilical ligaments‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ligamentum t‍eres hepatis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ductus ‍venosus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ductus a‍rteriosus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ligamentum teres hepatis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ligamentum teres hepatis</strong></p><p>The umbilical vein is the preferred vessel for an isovolumetric and simultaneous double set up exchange transfusion. The obliterated remnant of the umbilical vein is the Ligamentum teres.</p>\n<p>The vessels that are occluded soon after birth are in due course replaced by fibrous tissue and forms the following ligaments:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 397px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 325px; height: 13px;\"><strong>Fetal structure</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 393px; height: 13px;\"><strong>Adult structure</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 325px; height: 13px;\">Foramen ovale</td>\n<td style=\"width: 393px; height: 13px;\">Fossa ovalis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 325px; height: 13px;\"><strong>Umbilical vein</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 393px; height: 13px;\"><strong>Ligamentum teres</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 325px; height: 13px;\">Ductus venosus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 393px; height: 13px;\">Ligamentum venosum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 325px; height: 13px;\">Umbilical arteries</td>\n<td style=\"width: 393px; height: 13px;\">Medial umbilical ligaments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 325px; height: 13px;\">Ductus arteriosus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 393px; height: 13px;\">Ligamentum arteriosum</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">The <strong>median umbilical ligament</strong> or Xander's ligament represents the <strong>remnant of the embryonic urachus.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\"><span class=\"st\">The lateral umbilical ligaments are folds of the peritoneum over the inferior epigastric arteries.</span></span></p>\n<p><strong><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\"><span class=\"st\">Note:</span></span></strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\"><span class=\"st\">Cord blood bilirubin level more than</span></span><strong><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\"><span class=\"st\"> 4 mg/dL </span></span></strong><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\"><span class=\"st\">is an indication for change transfusion in a neonate. </span></span></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ZZtt7nPv","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"24_coagulation_pathway_disorders","title":"24.Coagulation Pathway Disorders","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MB5866","text":"What is the most common he​reditary disease associat‍ed with life-threatening ‍bleeding?","raw_text":"What is the most common hereditary disease associated with life-threatening bleeding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemoph​ilia A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Immune thrombocytopenic pu​rpura","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thrombotic thrombocytopeni​c purpura","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"von Willebr​and disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hemophilia A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hemophilia A</strong></p><p>The most common hereditary disease associated with life-threatening bleeding is <strong>hemophilia A</strong>. It is caused by <strong>mutations</strong> in <strong>factor VIII</strong>, an essential cofactor for factor IX in the coagulation cascade.</p>\n<p>Note: von Willebrand disease is the most common inherited bleeding disorder.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5869","text":"A 12-year-old boy presents​ with the following findi‍ng after a trivial fall. ‍His lab investigations ar​e given below. What is th‍e most likely diagnosis? ‍Platelet count - 4,00,000‌ /μL Bleeding time - norm​al PT - normal aPTT - pro​longed","raw_text":"<p>A 12-year-old boy presents with the following finding after a trivial fall. His lab investigations are given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Platelet count - 4,00,000 /μL</p>\n<p>Bleeding time - normal</p>\n<p>PT - normal</p>\n<p>aPTT - prolonged</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immune thrombocytopenic pu​rpura","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bernard Soul​ier syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemop​hilia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vitamin K ​deficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hemophilia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/addfc07f1b1b4efda178c274d5ec2c05.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hemophilia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario showing <strong>hemarthrosis</strong> and <strong>elevated aPTT</strong> is suggestive of <strong>hemophilia</strong>. </p>\n<p>Specific tests for measuring levels of factor <strong>VIII</strong> and factor <strong>IX</strong> can determine whether it is hemophilia <strong>A</strong> or hemophilia <strong>B </strong>respectively.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features</strong> of hemophilia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hemarthroses</strong> - joint hemorrhages, later causing progressive deformities</li>\n<li>Recurrent bleeding</li>\n<li><strong>Pseudotumour</strong> syndrome - fascial hemorrhages that occur as blood-filled cysts with calcification and proliferation of fibroblasts, giving the appearance of the tumor</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is associated with <strong>elevated aPTT</strong> and normal platelet count, bleeding time, and PT.</p>\n<p>Option A: In ITP, the platelet count is low and bleeding time is prolonged.</p>\n<p>Option B: In functional platelet disorders, like Bernard Soulier syndrome, the platelet count is normal and bleeding time is prolonged.</p>\n<p>Option D: In vitamin K deficiency, both PT and aPTT are prolonged. PT is relatively more prolonged than is the aPTT.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5872","text":"A 7-year-old boy was found​ to have excessive bleedi‍ng following tooth extrac‍tion. Lab investigations ​revealed prolonged aPTT, ‍decreased serum factor VI‍II levels and normal VwF ‌activity. What is the mod​e of inheritance of this ​condition?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy was found to have excessive bleeding following tooth extraction. Lab investigations revealed prolonged aPTT, decreased serum factor VIII levels and normal VwF activity. What is the mode of inheritance of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X-linked ​recessive","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"X-linked​ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Autosomal​ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autosomal​ recessive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. X-linked recessive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. X-linked recessive</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> Hemophilia A</strong> (factor VIII deficiency). It is inherited as an <strong>X-linked recessive</strong> trait. Hence, it affects mainly males and homozygous females.</p>\n<p><strong>Hemophilia B</strong> is also inherited as <strong>X-linked recessive</strong> trait. </p>\n<p><strong>Hemophilia</strong> <strong>C</strong> is inherited as an <strong>autosomal-recessive</strong> trait.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0500","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about he‍mophilia?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about hemophilia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemophilia B never affects​ females","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hemophilia C can affect fe​males","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemophilia A in female may​ indicate unfavourable ly‍onization","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemophilia A can occur wit​hout any family history","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hemophilia B never affects females","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hemophilia B never affects females</strong></p><p><strong>Hemophilia</strong> A and <strong>B</strong> mostly <strong>affect</strong> <strong>males</strong>, but rarely can affect <strong>females</strong> also.</p>\n<p>Hemophilia<strong> A</strong> is an X-linked recessive disorder, affecting mainly males and homozygous females. Rarely, excessive bleeding occurs in heterozygous females. It is due to the inactivation of the X chromosome bearing the normal <strong>factor VIII</strong> allele by chance in most cells <strong>(unfavourable lyonization)</strong>. About <strong>30%</strong> of patients have <strong>no family history</strong> and the disorder is caused due to new mutations.</p>\n<p>Hemophilia <strong>B</strong> is inherited as X-linked recessive trait. There is a <strong>deficiency</strong> of <strong>factor IX.</strong></p>\n<p>Hemophilia <strong>C</strong> is an autosomal-recessive condition and hence can occur in females.<strong> </strong>There is a <strong>deficiency</strong> of <strong>factor XI.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5874","text":"A 10-year-old child with e​asy bruising was diagnose‍d to have moderate hemoph‍ilia A. What will be the ​percentage of normal fact‍or VIII in her?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old child with easy bruising was diagnosed to have moderate hemophilia A. What will be the percentage of normal factor VIII in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5-​15%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2-​5%","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"15-​30%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"40-​60%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2-5%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2-5%</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>moderate hemophilia</strong> <strong>A</strong> have<strong> 2% - 5%</strong> of <strong>normal</strong> levels of <strong>factor VIII.</strong></p>\n<p>The clinical manifestations of Hemophilia A vary in severity, corresponding to the level of factor VIII present. They can be classified as: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 96px; width: 306.476px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px;\"><strong>Severity</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.476px;\"><strong>Levels of factor VIII</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px;\">Mild</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.476px;\">6-50%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px;\">Moderate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.476px;\">2-5%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px;\">Severe</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.476px;\">&lt;1%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5876","text":"Which of the following lab​ findings are seen in pat‍ients with hemophilia A? ‍Options Platelet count Bl​eeding time PT aPTT 1 Nor‍mal Normal Normal Increas‍ed 2 Decreased Increased ‌Normal Normal 3 Normal In​creased Normal Increased ​4 Decreased Increased Inc​reased Increased","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following lab findings are seen in patients with hemophilia A?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 156px; width: 517.597px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\"><strong>Options</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\"><strong>Platelet count</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\"><strong>Bleeding time</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 83px;\"><strong>PT</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.5972px;\"><strong>aPTT</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 83px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.5972px;\">Increased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Decreased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\">Increased </td>\n<td style=\"width: 83px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.5972px;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Normal </td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\">Increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 83px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.5972px;\">Increased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Decreased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\">Increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 83px;\">Increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.5972px;\">Increased</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opti​on 1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Opti​on 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opti​on 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Opti​on 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Option 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Option 1</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>hemophilia</strong> <strong>A</strong> have:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prolonged</strong> partial thromboplastin time (<strong>aPTT</strong>) - intrinsic pathway abnormality</li>\n<li>Normal prothrombin time (PT) </li>\n<li>Normal bleeding time</li>\n<li>Normal platelet count</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0507","text":"A newborn baby develops se​vere umbilical cord bleed‍ing soon after birth. Lab‍ investigations are given​ below. What is the most ‍likely diagnosis? Bleedin‍g time - normal Platelet ‌count - normal PT - norma​l aPTT - normal TT - norm​al Urea lysis test - posi​tive","raw_text":"<p>A newborn baby develops severe umbilical cord bleeding soon after birth. Lab investigations are given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Bleeding time - normal</p>\n<p>Platelet count - normal</p>\n<p>PT - normal</p>\n<p>aPTT - normal</p>\n<p>TT - normal</p>\n<p>Urea lysis test - positive</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitamin K ​deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"von Willebr​and disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemorrhagic disease of new​born","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Factor XIII​ deficiency","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Factor XIII deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Factor XIII deficiency</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>factor XIII deficiency</strong>. A <strong>positive</strong> <strong>urea lysis</strong> test confirms the diagnosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Factor XIII</strong> is also known as <strong>clot-stabilizing </strong>factor that converts unstable fibrin clot into a stable clot. Its deficiency typically presents as excessive umbilical cord bleeding in the newborn.</p>\n<p><strong>Laboratory findings</strong> in this condition are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal levels of bleeding time (BT), platelet count, prothrombin time (PT), activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT), and thrombin time (TT)</li>\n<li>Positive urea lysis test - confirmatory test which demonstrates lysis of clot by hyperosmolar urea in affected individuals</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The parameters for the given diseases:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 61.208054%; height: 286px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 66px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 13.523132%; height: 66px;\">Condition</td>\n<td style=\"width: 9.964413%; height: 66px;\">PT</td>\n<td style=\"width: 16.488731%; height: 66px;\">aPTT</td>\n<td style=\"width: 9.964413%; height: 66px;\">BT</td>\n<td style=\"width: 16.488731%; height: 66px;\">TT</td>\n<td style=\"width: 17.200474%; height: 66px;\">Platelet count</td>\n<td style=\"width: 16.251483%; height: 66px;\">Urea lysis test </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 13.523132%; height: 44px;\">Vitamin K deficiency</td>\n<td style=\"width: 9.964413%; height: 44px;\">increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 16.488731%; height: 44px;\">increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 9.964413%; height: 44px;\">increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 16.488731%; height: 44px;\">normal/increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 17.200474%; height: 44px;\">normal/decreased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 16.251483%; height: 44px;\">negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 66px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 13.523132%; height: 66px;\">Von Willebrand disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 9.964413%; height: 66px;\">normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 16.488731%; height: 66px;\">normal/increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 9.964413%; height: 66px;\">increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 16.488731%; height: 66px;\">normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 17.200474%; height: 66px;\">normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 16.251483%; height: 66px;\">negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 66px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 13.523132%; height: 66px;\">Hemorrhagic disease of newborn</td>\n<td style=\"width: 9.964413%; height: 66px;\">increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 16.488731%; height: 66px;\">increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 9.964413%; height: 66px;\">increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 16.488731%; height: 66px;\">normal/increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 17.200474%; height: 66px;\">normal/decreased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 16.251483%; height: 66px;\">negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 13.523132%; height: 44px;\">Factor XIII deficiency</td>\n<td style=\"width: 9.964413%; height: 44px;\">normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 16.488731%; height: 44px;\">normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 9.964413%; height: 44px;\">normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 16.488731%; height: 44px;\">normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 17.200474%; height: 44px;\">normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 16.251483%; height: 44px;\"><strong>positive</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0508","text":"A Hemogram of a 23-year-ol​d patient with a bleeding‍ disorder is given below.‍ Which of the following w​ould you use to different‍iate between heparin-indu‍ced thrombocytopenia and ‌afibrinogenemia in this p​atient? Platelet count - ​3,23,000 /μL PT - elevate​d aPTT - elevated TT - el​evated","raw_text":"<p>A Hemogram of a 23-year-old patient with a bleeding disorder is given below. Which of the following would you use to differentiate between heparin-induced thrombocytopenia and afibrinogenemia in this patient?</p>\n<p>Platelet count - 3,23,000 /μL</p>\n<p>PT - elevated</p>\n<p>aPTT - elevated</p>\n<p>TT - elevated</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rept​ilase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Risto​cetin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Russel vi​per venom","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperosm​olar urea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Reptilase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Reptilase</strong></p><p><strong>Reptilase</strong> is a <strong>thrombin</strong> <strong>agonist</strong> which is used to <strong>differentiate</strong> heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (<strong>HIT</strong>) and <strong>afibrinogenemia</strong>. It is <strong>resistant </strong>to the action of<strong> heparin.</strong></p>\n<p>The differential diagnosis for normal platelet count with elevated PT, aPTT and TT are HIT and afibrinogenemia. After <strong>adding</strong> <strong>reptilase</strong>, if the <strong>TT</strong> is <strong>corrected</strong>, it points to <strong>HIT</strong>. In <strong>afibrinogenemia,</strong> since there is an <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>fibrin</strong>, there is <strong>no change</strong> in the TT. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5877","text":"A 1-year-old child was dia​gnosed to have Kasabach-M‍erritt syndrome. What is ‍this condition associated​ with?","raw_text":"A 1-year-old child was diagnosed to have Kasabach-Merritt syndrome. What is this condition associated with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thrombotic thrombocytopeni​c purpura","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Disseminated intravascular​ coagulation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Immune thrombocytopenic pu​rpura","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemolytic ur​emic syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Disseminated intravascular coagulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/02cca95d7a514aeaa95e2e5c8161135ex982x550.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Disseminated intravascular coagulation</strong></p><p><strong>Kasabach–Merritt syndrome</strong> is associated with <strong>DIC</strong> (disseminated intravascular coagulation). This syndrome is characterised by the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rapidly growing vascular tumour</li>\n<li>Thrombocytopenia</li>\n<li>Microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia</li>\n<li>Consumptive coagulopathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An unusual form of DIC occurs in association with giant hemangiomas. In this type, <strong>stasis</strong> and recurrent <strong>trauma</strong> to the fragile <strong>blood vessels</strong> cause <strong>thrombi</strong> to <strong>form</strong> within the neoplasm.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows a reddish-blue lesion covering the lower part of the abdomen. It is a caverno-capillary haemangioma seen in Kasabach–Merritt syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6779","text":"Which of the following clo​tting factor deficiency w‍ill not affect clotting i‍n vivo?","raw_text":"Which of the following clotting factor deficiency will not affect clotting in vivo?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facto​r VII","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fact​or V","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Facto​r XII","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fact​or IX","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Factor XII","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/57039f8bba4a42d9aad91000bdc34855x600x578.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Factor XII</strong></p><p>Factor XII deficiency will not affect the clotting in vivo.</p>\n<p>Unlike the clotting cascade in the lab (<strong>in vitro</strong>), the cascade in the body (<strong>in</strong> <strong>vivo</strong>) differs slightly - as <strong>tissue factor</strong> (TF) is the major <strong>initiator</strong> of the cascade. The <strong>TF/VIIa complex</strong> is the most important activator of <strong>factor IX</strong> and <strong>factor IXa/factor VIIIa complex</strong> is the most important activator of <strong>factor X</strong>. Factor V is involved in both the pathways as shown in the image below.</p>\n<p>The coagulation cascade in the <strong>laboratory</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Extrinsic </strong>pathway sequence (in a test tube):  Tissue factor → Factor VII → Factor X.</li>\n<li><strong>Intrinsic </strong>pathway sequence (in a test tube): Factor XII →  Factor XI → Factor IX → Factor X </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Laboratory methods to<strong> initiate </strong>clotting include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intrinsic pathway - adding phospholipids, calcium, and either a negatively charged substance such as glass beads</li>\n<li>Extrinsic pathway - a source of tissue factor </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5861","text":"Which test evaluates the e​xtrinsic pathway?","raw_text":"Which test evaluates the extrinsic pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AP​TT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"P​T","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"T​T","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bleedi​ng time","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. PT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. PT</strong></p><p><strong>Prothrombin time</strong> (PT) evaluates the <strong>extrinsic pathway </strong>of coagulation.</p>\n<p>PT <strong>measures</strong> the <strong>clotting time</strong> of plasma in the presence of an optimal concentration of tissue extract (<strong>thromboplastin</strong>). This indicates the overall efficiency of the extrinsic clotting system.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes</strong> of<strong> prolonged PT:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Administration of oral anticoagulant drugs - vitamin K antagonists</li>\n<li>Liver disease - obstructive jaundice</li>\n<li>Vitamin K deficiency</li>\n<li>Disseminated intravascular coagulation</li>\n<li>Previously undiagnosed factor VII, X, V or prothrombin deficiency or defect (rare)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Activated partial thromboplastin time (<strong>APTT</strong>) measures the <strong>clotting time</strong> of plasma after the activation of contact factors and the addition of phospholipid and CaCl<sub>2</sub>, but <strong>without</strong> added tissue <strong>thromboplastin</strong>. It indicates the overall efficiency of the <strong>intrinsic</strong> <strong>pathway</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes </strong>of<strong> prolonged APTT:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Disseminated intravascular coagulation</li>\n<li>Liver disease</li>\n<li>Massive transfusion with plasma-depleted red blood cells</li>\n<li>Administration of or contamination with heparin or other anticoagulants</li>\n<li>A circulating anticoagulant (inhibitor)</li>\n<li>Deficiency of a coagulation factor other than factor VII</li>\n<li>Intake of oral anticoagulant drugs</li>\n<li>Vitamin K deficiency</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5864","text":"You are collecting a blood​ sample to send for proth‍rombin time in a patient ‍in the ward. Which of the​ following is true about ‍this investigation?","raw_text":"You are collecting a blood sample to send for prothrombin time in a patient in the ward. Which of the following is true about this investigation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sample must be immediately​ refrigerated","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sample must contain platel​et-rich plasma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Test must be done within 2​ hours of sample collecti‍on","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sample is activated with k​aolin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Test must be done within 2 hours of sample collection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Test must be done within 2 hours of sample collection</strong></p><p>The test for <strong>prothrombin time</strong> is done <strong>within</strong> <strong>2 hours</strong>.</p>\n<p>The differences between PT and aPTT are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 139px;\" width=\"410\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.328px;\"><strong>Feature</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.328px;\"><strong>PT</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.344px;\"><strong>aPTT</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.328px;\">Coagulation pathway tested</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.328px;\">Extrinsic and common</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.344px;\">Intrinsic and common</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.328px;\">Normal value</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.328px;\">11-13 seconds</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.344px;\">25-35 seconds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.328px;\">Sample</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.328px;\">Platelet-poor plasma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.344px;\">Platelet-poor plasma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.328px;\">Activating agent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.328px;\">Thomboplastin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.344px;\">\n<p>Kaolin</p>\n<p>Silica</p>\n<p>Ellagic acid</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0509","text":"At what temperature should​ you store the sample for‍ antiphospholipid antibod‍y factor assays?","raw_text":"At what temperature should you store the sample for antiphospholipid antibody factor assays?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Room tem​perature","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 to 8 ​degrees","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt;4 ​degree","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"-8 d​egree","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. <4 degree","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. &lt;4 degree</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW158764989\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW158764989\">The sample for <strong>antiphospholipid antibody assay</strong> must be stored at <strong>&lt;4°.</strong></span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW158764989\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW158764989\">Samples for <strong>coagulation studies</strong> should be stored </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW158764989\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW158764989\">always at <strong>room temperature</strong> (20 to 25 degrees).</span></span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0506","text":"A 50-year-old male patient​ presents to the casualty‍ with severe bleeding fol‍lowing a road traffic acc​ident. He is a known card‍iac disease patient on an‍ticoagulant therapy. As a‌n intern, you were asked ​to collect blood for coag​ulation studies. Which co​lour vacutainer will you ​pick?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old male patient presents to the casualty with severe bleeding following a road traffic accident. He is a known cardiac disease patient on anticoagulant therapy.  As an intern, you were asked to collect blood for coagulation studies. Which colour vacutainer will you pick?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lave​nder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gr​een","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"R​ed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bl​ue","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Blue","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/da483a0ca63e4a4d940f3175927ac688x1280x2189.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Blue</strong></p><p>The most commonly used <strong>vacutainer</strong> for <strong>coagulation studies</strong> is<strong> </strong>a<strong> blue </strong>vacutainer<strong> </strong>containing <strong>t</strong><strong>risodium citrate</strong>.</p>\n<p>The benefits of trisodium citrate are that the <strong>calcium</strong> ion is <strong>neutralized</strong> more <strong>rapidly</strong> in citrate.</p>\n<p>Other anticoagulants are unacceptable for coagulation studies. The labile factors (factors V and VIII) are unstable in oxalate (grey), whereas heparin (green) and EDTA (lavender) directly inhibit the coagulation process and interfere with end-point determinations. Also, activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) tests are more sensitive to the presence of heparin (green).</p>\n<p>The image given below shows colour coding of vacutainers with the anticoagulant used and their practical uses.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Heparin is used for blood sample collection for electrolyte estimation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7084","text":"Which of the following is ​the color of vacutainer c‍ontaining sodium fluoride‍ as a content?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the color of vacutainer containing sodium fluoride as a content?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R​ed","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gr​ey","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bl​ue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Yel​low","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Grey","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Grey</strong></p><p><strong>Sodium fluoride</strong> is present in the <strong>grey </strong>coloured tube.</p>\n<p>Sodium fluoride <strong>inhibits glycolysis. </strong>Hence, is used for the <strong>estimation</strong> of <strong>blood glucose </strong>and<strong> alcohol.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6752","text":"A child was brought to the​ emergency department wit‍h a big lesion on his for‍earm. As a surgery reside​nt you decide to excise t‍he lesion in the emergenc‍y operation theatre. Whic‌h of the following invest​igations will test the en​tire coagulation pathway?​","raw_text":"A child was brought to the emergency department with a big lesion on his forearm. As a surgery resident you decide to excise the lesion in the emergency operation theatre. Which of the following investigations will test the entire coagulation pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activated partial thrombop​lastin time","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prothrom​bin time","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thromboel​astography","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bleedi​ng time","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thromboelastography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thromboelastography</strong></p><p><strong>Thromboelastography</strong> (TEG) tests the <strong>entire coagulation pathway</strong>. It measures coagulation cascade, platelet function, and fibrinolysis.</p>\n<p>It is a<strong> </strong>test that <strong>quantitatively</strong> measures the ability of whole blood to form a clot. The advantages are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"half_rhythm\">\n<div>Global assessment of blood coagulability, including coagulation cascade, platelet function, and fibrinolysis</div>\n</li>\n<li class=\"half_rhythm\">\n<div>Rapid real-time bedside test with a simple methodology (point-of-care testing)</div>\n</li>\n<li class=\"half_rhythm\">\n<div>Diagnosis of coagulopathic bleeding</div>\n</li>\n<li class=\"half_rhythm\">\n<div>Guide transfusion therapy and decrease the use of blood products</div>\n</li>\n<li class=\"half_rhythm\">\n<div>Detect dynamic changes in blood coagulation during resuscitation</div>\n</li>\n<li class=\"half_rhythm\">\n<div>Predict the clinical efficacy of therapeutic agents affecting blood coagulability</div>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Aaj-dfAh","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"24_antidepressant_and_antianxiety_drugs","title":"24.Antidepressant And Antianxiety Drugs","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MF5806","text":"Which of the following bes‌t describes the mechanism​ of action of antidepress‍ants?","raw_text":"Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of antidepressants?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Change in neurotransmitter‌ receptor sensitivity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decrease in level of neuro‌transmitters","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Change in efficacy of neur‌otransmitters","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increase the reuptake of n‌eurotransmitters","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Change in neurotransmitter receptor sensitivity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Change in neurotransmitter receptor sensitivity</strong></p><p>Antidepressants increase levels of neurotransmitters at the synapse by preventing their reuptake. Due to a prolonged increase in the levels of neurotransmitters, there is a<strong> change in neurotransmitter receptor sensitivity</strong> which is thought to be the major mechanism responsible for the treatment of depression.</p>\n<p>Chronic treatment with a number of antidepressants <strong>desensitizes presynaptic autoreceptors and heteroreceptors</strong>, producing long-lasting changes in monoaminergic neurotransmission. Postreceptor effects of antidepressant treatment, including modulation of GPCR signaling and activation of protein kinases and ion channels, are involved in the mediation of the long-term effects of antidepressant drugs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2635","text":"Moclobemide belongs to whi‌ch class of drugs?","raw_text":"Moclobemide belongs to which class of drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"SS‌RI","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"SN‌RI","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MAO in‌hibitor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tricyclic an‌tidepressant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. MAO inhibitor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. MAO inhibitor</strong></p><p><strong>Moclobemide</strong> is a reversible and selective<strong> inhibitor of the MAO-A enzyme.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Monoamine hypothesis</strong> states that in depression, there is a <strong>decrease</strong> in monoamines like <strong>serotonin</strong> (5HT) and <strong>norepinephrine.</strong> These monoamines are metabolized by the enzyme monoamine oxidase (MAO). <strong>MAO inhibitors,</strong> therefore, <strong>increase</strong> these <strong>monoamines</strong> by inhibiting the enzyme.</p>\n<p>The various classes of drugs used in depression and anxiety are listed below:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 373px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Class of drugs</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Examples</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\"><strong>Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRI)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Fluoxetine</li>\n<li>Fluvoxamine</li>\n<li>Paroxetine</li>\n<li>Sertraline</li>\n<li>Citalopram</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\"><strong>Serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Venlafaxine</li>\n<li>Desvenlafaxine</li>\n<li>Duloxetine</li>\n<li>Milnacipran</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\"><strong>Tricyclic antidepressants</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Amitriptyline</li>\n<li>Imipramine</li>\n<li>Nortriptyline</li>\n<li>Desipramine</li>\n<li>Amoxapine</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\"><strong>Atypical antipsychotics</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Aripiprazole</li>\n<li>Olanzapine</li>\n<li>Quetiapine</li>\n<li>Risperidone</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\"><strong>Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAO)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Isocarboxazid</li>\n<li>Selegiline</li>\n<li>Phenelzine</li>\n<li>Moclobemide</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\"><strong>Atypical antidepressants</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Bupropion</li>\n<li>Trazodone</li>\n<li>Mirtazapine</li>\n<li>Mianserin</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2054","text":"Which of the following is ‌a selective MAO-B inhibit​or?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a selective MAO-B inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Moclo‌bemide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isocar‌boxazid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phene‌lzine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Seleg‌iline","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Selegiline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Selegiline</strong></p><p><strong>Selegiline</strong> is a <strong>selective MAO-B inhibitor</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 434px; height: 161px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 4px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 207.484px; height: 4px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Nonselective MAO inhibitors</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210.516px; height: 4px; text-align: center;\"><strong> Selective MAO inhibitors</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 99px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 207.484px; height: 99px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Phenelzine</li>\n<li>Isocarboxazid</li>\n<li>Tranylcypromine</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210.516px; height: 99px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>MAO-A inhibitors - Moclobemide</li>\n<li>MAO-B inhibitors - Selegiline, Rasagiline</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2642","text":"In which of the following ‌patients is transdermal s​elegiline approved for us‍e?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients is transdermal selegiline approved for use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30-year-old man with obses‌sive compulsive disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"64-year-old man with Parki‌nson's disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"29-year-old woman with dep‌ression","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"33-year-old woman with gen‌eralized anxiety disorder​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 29-year-old woman with depression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 29-year-old woman with depression</strong></p><p><strong>Selegiline transdermal</strong> patch is <strong>approved</strong> for<strong> depression. </strong>The transdermal patch provides the high concentration required for antidepressant action. At higher doses, MAO-A is also inhibited and is highly effective in depression. </p>\n<p>Selegiline is a selective MAO-B inhibitor at low doses. It is used in Parkinson's disease at low doses, administered orally.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2661","text":"A college student on MAO i‌nhibitor therapy for depr​ession consumed two bottl‍es of beer during a party‍. The next day he started​ experiencing severe head​ache, nausea, and vomitin‌g. He was taken to the ca‍sualty by his friends whe​re his BP was recorded to‌ be 180/110mmHg. Which of‌ the following compounds ‌would be responsible for ‌this complication?","raw_text":"A college student on MAO inhibitor therapy for depression consumed two bottles of beer during a party. The next day he started experiencing severe headache, nausea, and vomiting. He was taken to the casualty by his friends where his BP was recorded to be 180/110mmHg. Which of the following compounds would be responsible for this complication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tyra‌mine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tyrpt‌ophan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thre‌onine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thia‌mine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tyramine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e161466fb10d429f81c37cb0bbc1f784x1280x1437.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tyramine</strong></p><p>The given clinical presentation of a <strong>hypertensive crisis</strong> in a patient on <strong>MAO inhibitor</strong> therapy is suggestive of <strong>cheese reaction</strong>, which is seen after consumption of foods rich in<strong> tyramine </strong>(beer).</p>\n<p>Tyramine is an indirect <strong>sympathomimetic</strong>. It induces the release of catecholamines from the neurons. Tyramine can be produced in high concentrations in <strong>protein-rich foods</strong> by decarboxylation of tyrosine during fermentation.</p>\n<p>Normally tyramine is <strong>inactivated</strong> when taken orally because it is readily metabolized <strong>by MAO</strong> in the liver. In patients who are on <strong>MAO inhibitor therapy</strong>, the tyramine taken in the diet is <strong>not inactivated</strong> and results in its <strong>increased sympathomimetic effect</strong> resulting in elevated blood pressure, termed as hypertensive crisis or cheese reaction. Drug of choice for cheese reaction is <strong>IV phentolamine.</strong></p>\n<p>When MAO inhibitors are taken along with SSRI or TCA, it results in <strong>serotonin syndrome </strong>due to the overstimulation of 5HT receptors. The clinical features are a triad of:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Cognitive</strong> effect - delirium, coma</li>\n<li><strong>Autonomic</strong> effects- hypertension, tachycardia</li>\n<li><strong>Somatic</strong> effects- myoclonus, hyperreflexia, tremors</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5807","text":"A patient presents to the ‌medicine OPD with hyperte​nsion. He is already on i‍mipramine for depression.‍ Which of the following a​ntihypertensive drugs wil​l not be effective in him‌?","raw_text":"A patient presents to the medicine OPD with hypertension. He is already on imipramine for depression. Which of the following antihypertensive drugs will not be effective in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enal‌april","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aten‌olol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dilt‌iazem","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clon‌idine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Clonidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Clonidine</strong></p><p>Clonidine will not be effective in this patient.</p>\n<p><strong>Imipramine</strong>, a tricyclic antidepressant would <strong>abolish</strong> the <strong>antihypertensive effect</strong> of <strong>clonidine,</strong> by inhibiting its transport into the adrenergic neurons.</p>\n<p>Side effects of tricyclic antidepressants:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 229px; width: 364.765625px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Receptor blocked</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.765625px;\">Side effects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Muscarinic receptor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.765625px;\">Anticholinergic - Dry mouth, dilated pupils, tachycardia, urinary retention</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Alpha 1 adrenergic receptor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.765625px;\">Drowsiness, postural hypotension, sexual dysfunction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">H1 receptor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.765625px;\">Weight gain, drowsiness</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2632","text":"Which tricyclic antidepres‌sant has an additional do​paminergic receptor antag‍onist action?","raw_text":"Which tricyclic antidepressant has an additional dopaminergic receptor antagonist action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clomip‌ramine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dox‌epin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Imipr‌amine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amox‌apine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Amoxapine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Amoxapine</strong></p><p>The <strong>tricyclic antidepressant</strong> having an <strong>additional dopaminergic</strong> receptor <strong>antagonist</strong> action is <strong>amoxapine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tricyclic antidepressants block the transporter of norepinephrine (NET) and serotonin (SERT) thereby preventing their uptake. Some of the TCA additionally block other receptors like alpha<sub> 1</sub>, H<sub>1,</sub> and M<sub>1</sub>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>TCA blocking NET selectively - desipramine, nortriptyline, amoxapine.</li>\n<li>TCA blocking both NET and SERT equally - imipramine, amitriptyline.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clomipramine, doxepin, and imipramine are tertiary amines and do not possess any dopaminergic antagonist effect.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2644","text":"Which of the following ant‌idepressants can be used ​for the treatment of a 6-‍year-old child diagnosed ‍with nocturnal enuresis?","raw_text":"Which of the following antidepressants can be used for the treatment of a 6-year-old child diagnosed with nocturnal enuresis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Imipr‌amine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amitri‌ptyline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Desip‌ramine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clomip‌ramine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Imipramine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Imipramine</strong></p><p><strong>Imipramine</strong> is a tricyclic antidepressant that can be used for the treatment of <strong>nocturnal enuresis</strong>.</p>\n<p>In children above 5 years,<strong> imipramine</strong> 25 mg <strong>at bedtime </strong>is effective for nocturnal enuresis.</p>\n<p>Intranasal desmopressin is also effective in the treatment of nocturnal enuresis.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Amitriptyline</strong> is used in<strong> postherpetic neuralgia</strong>. It is also used in neuropathic pain.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Desipramine</strong> is used in the treatment of <strong>cocaine dependence.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Clomipramine</strong> is the<strong> </strong>best TCA for <strong>OCD.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2656","text":"Which of the following sid‌e effects of tricyclic an​tidepressants are correct‍ly matched to the recepto‍r involved in their patho​genesis? H 1 receptor - s​edation Muscarinic recept‌or - sialorrhea 5HT recep‍tor- weight loss Alpha 1 ​receptor - postural hypot‌ension Muscarinic recepto‌r - constipation","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following side effects of tricyclic antidepressants are correctly matched to the receptor involved in their pathogenesis?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>H<sub>1</sub> receptor - sedation</li>\n<li>Muscarinic receptor - sialorrhea</li>\n<li>5HT receptor- weight loss</li>\n<li>Alpha<sub>1</sub> receptor - postural hypotension</li>\n<li>Muscarinic receptor - constipation</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 4 ‌and 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2 ‌and 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 and ‌5 only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1, 4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1, 4 and 5</strong></p><p>The blockade of <strong>H<sub>1</sub>, alpha<sub>1</sub>,</strong> and<strong> muscarinic</strong> receptors causes <strong>sedation, postural hypotension,</strong> and <strong>constipation</strong> respectively.</p>\n<p>Tricyclic antidepressants (TCAs) are drugs that act by inhibiting the reuptake of noradrenaline and serotonin from the synaptic clefts.</p>\n<p>The side effects of TCAs are due to blockade of other receptors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Muscarinic</strong> (M) receptors: constipation, <strong>dry mouth </strong>(not sialorrhea), arrhythmia</li>\n<li>Histamine (<strong>H</strong><sub><strong>1</strong>)</sub> receptors: sedation</li>\n<li><strong>5HT</strong> receptors: <strong>weight gain</strong> due to increased appetite</li>\n<li><strong>Alpha<sub>1</sub></strong> receptors: postural hypotension</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2648","text":"A 30-year-old woman having‌ depression took multiple​ tablets of amitriptyline‍ in an attempt to commit ‍suicide. Which of the fol​lowing statements regardi​ng the management is inco‌rrect?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman having depression took multiple tablets of amitriptyline in an attempt to commit suicide. Which of the following statements regarding the management is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Physostigmine is not recom‌mended","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium bicarbonate should ‌be administered to treat ​acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atropine sulphate should b‌e administered as an anti​dote","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Diazepam should be injecte‌d to control seizures","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Atropine sulphate should be administered as an antidote","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Atropine sulphate should be administered as an antidote</strong></p><p><strong>Tricyclic antidepressants</strong> like amitriptyline are <strong>competitive antagonists </strong>of <strong>muscarinic receptors. Atropine</strong> being an antimuscarinic drug should <strong>not</strong> be <strong>administered.</strong></p>\n<p>Tricyclic antidepressant overdose presents with <strong>anticholinergic effects</strong>. Hypotension occurs because of alpha<sub>1</sub> receptor blockade.</p>\n<p>TCAs act on Na<sup>+</sup> channels and cause slowed conduction and depressed cardiac contractility resulting in serious arrhythmias. Seizures and agitation may occur followed by depression.</p>\n<p><strong>Management</strong> of TCA overdose:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Initial resuscitation by securing airway, breathing, and circulation.</li>\n<li>Hypotension - <strong>IV fluids</strong> and norepinephrine infusion.</li>\n<li>Cardiac-depressant effects - bolus dose of <strong>sodium bicarbonate</strong> helps overcome Na<sup>+</sup> blockade by increasing extracellular Na<sup>+</sup>. It also addresses metabolic acidosis which is often present.</li>\n<li>Arrhythmias - <strong>lignocaine</strong> for ventricular arrhythmias.</li>\n<li>Seizures - <strong>benzodiazepines</strong> like diazepam/lorazepam.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Physostigmine</strong> can reverse anticholinergic effects but can <strong>aggravate cardiac depression</strong> and cause <strong>seizures</strong>. Therefore physostigmine is not recommended<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2652","text":"A psychiatrist plans to pr‌escribe an SSRI to a pati​ent diagnosed with genera‍lized anxiety disorder. W‍hich of the following was​ not likely prescribed?","raw_text":"A psychiatrist plans to prescribe an SSRI to a patient diagnosed with generalized anxiety disorder. Which of the following was not likely prescribed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fluox‌etine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cital‌opram","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parox‌etine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dulox‌etine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Duloxetine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Duloxetine</strong></p><p><strong>D</strong><strong>uloxetine </strong>is not an SSRI but a <strong>serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor </strong>(<strong>SNRI</strong>). It blocks the uptake of both serotonin and norepinephrine. </p>\n<p>The selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (<strong>SSRIs</strong>) are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fluoxetine</li>\n<li>Fluvoxamine</li>\n<li>Citalopram</li>\n<li>Escitalopram</li>\n<li>Sertraline</li>\n<li>Dapoxetine</li>\n<li>Paroxetine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2660","text":"In a middle-aged man with ‌a history of closed-angle​ glaucoma, which of the f‍ollowing SNRIs should be ‍avoided?","raw_text":"In a middle-aged man with a history of closed-angle glaucoma, which of the following SNRIs should be avoided?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Venla‌faxine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dulox‌etine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Desvenl‌afaxine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fluox‌etine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Duloxetine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Duloxetine</strong></p><p><strong>Duloxetine</strong> should be avoided as it is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in patients with <strong>closed-angle glaucoma. </strong>SNRIs like <strong>duloxetine</strong> and <strong>milnacipran</strong> have anticholinergic side effects like <strong>mydriasis</strong> which can precipitate <strong>closed-angle glaucoma</strong> attacks.</p>\n<p>Duloxetine is the longest-acting SNRI with no withdrawal symptoms. I<span class=\"fontstyle0\">n addition to the treatment of depression and anxiety, i</span>t is also used in fibromyalgia, peripheral neuropathy, and stress incontinence.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Venlafaxine</strong> is the shortest acting SNRI with the maximum risk of withdrawal symptoms. It is approved for use in PTSD, panic disorder, and depression associated with hypotension. It is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in pregnancy.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Desvenlafaxine</strong> is a longer-acting SNRI and hence has lesser risk of withdrawal symptoms.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Fluoxetine</strong> is an SSRI, not an SNRI. It is avoided as it can cause mydriasis and narrow the angle. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2650","text":"A 27-year-old man on antid‌epressant therapy with TC​As for the past five year‍s is being switched to SS‍RIs. Which of the followi​ng would not be an advant​age of this change?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old man on antidepressant therapy with TCAs for the past five years is being switched to SSRIs. Which of the following would not be an advantage of this change?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Minimal lowering of seizur‌e threshold","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Minimal anticholinergic si‌de effects","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Minimal sexual dysfunction‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Minimal ‌sedation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Minimal sexual dysfunction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Minimal sexual dysfunction</strong></p><p><strong>SSRIs</strong> have a <strong>higher</strong> incidence of <strong>sexual dysfunction </strong>when compared to TCAs. </p>\n<p>SSRI-induced sexual dysfunction can result in loss of libido, delayed ejaculation, and interference with orgasm.</p>\n<p>Option A: The potential of SSRIs to lower the seizure threshold is less than that of TCAs.</p>\n<p>Options B and D: SSRIs have a selective action on serotonin reuptake with no effect on cholinergic or histaminergic receptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2665","text":"Selective serotonin reupta‌ke inhibitors are not the​ drug of choice in which ‍of the following patients‍?","raw_text":"Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors are not the drug of choice in which of the following patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A college student with an ‌acute panic attack","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"An adolescent girl with so‌cial phobia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A young lady with post tra‌umatic stress disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A middle-aged-man with gen‌eralized anxiety disorder​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A college student with an acute panic attack","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A college student with an acute panic attack</strong></p><p>In acute panic attacks, <strong>benzodiazepines</strong> or<strong> propranolol</strong> are the preferred anxiolytics. SSRIs are not the drug of choice as they have a <strong>long latent period </strong>before beneficial effects are seen.</p>\n<p><strong>SSRIs</strong> are considered as the <strong>drug of choice</strong> for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Social anxiety disorder</li>\n<li>Post-traumatic stress disorder</li>\n<li>Generalized anxiety disorder</li>\n<li>Obsessive-compulsive disorder</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2659","text":"A patient diagnosed with d‌epression has improved sy​mptomatically after initi‍ating SSRIs. On discontin‍uing the drug, he became ​irritable and complained ​of insomnia, nausea, and ‌flu-like symptoms. Which ‍of the following drugs ha​s the least risk of causi‌ng these symptoms?","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with depression has improved symptomatically after initiating SSRIs. On discontinuing the drug, he became irritable and complained of insomnia, nausea, and flu-like symptoms. Which of the following drugs has the least risk of causing these symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parox‌etine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fluox‌etine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sertr‌aline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fluvo‌xamine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fluoxetine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fluoxetine</strong></p><p>The given clinical presentation is suggestive of drug discontinuation or<strong> withdrawal symptoms</strong> which are seen after <strong>sudden discontinuation</strong> of SSRIs. <strong>Fluoxetine</strong> has the <strong>least risk</strong> of causing drug-discontinuation symptoms.</p>\n<p>Drug discontinuation symptoms of SSRIs are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nausea</li>\n<li>Paresthesia</li>\n<li>Flu-like symptoms</li>\n<li>Insomnia</li>\n<li>Irritability</li>\n<li>Fatigue.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Withdrawal is least with fluoxetine as its metabolite <strong>norfluoxetine </strong>has the <strong>longest half-life</strong>. Thus the plasma level of norfluoxetine is maintained for a long time even after stopping the drug, minimizing withdrawal symptoms. Withdrawal is seen<strong> maximum </strong>with <strong>paroxetine. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2066","text":"A young man with obsessive‌-compulsive disorder is t​aking SSRIs. Which of the‍ following adverse effect‍s would you not expect in​ him?","raw_text":"A young man with obsessive-compulsive disorder is taking SSRIs. Which of the following adverse effects would you not expect in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diar‌rhea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nau‌sea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Weigh‌t loss","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Loss of‌ libido","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Weight loss","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Weight loss</strong></p><p><strong>Weight loss </strong>is <strong>not</strong> an adverse effect of <strong>SSRIs</strong>.</p>\n<p>Side effects of SSRIs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nausea and diarrhea (early)</li>\n<li>Loss of libido</li>\n<li>Erectile dysfunction</li>\n<li>Headache</li>\n<li>Sleep disturbances - insomnia or hypersomnia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2662","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is a serotonin recepto​r antagonist?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is a serotonin receptor antagonist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Milna‌cipran","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Traz‌odone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cital‌opram","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fluox‌etine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trazodone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trazodone</strong></p><p><strong>Trazodone</strong> is a <strong>serotonin receptor antagonist.</strong></p>\n<p>Trazodone <strong>blocks postsynaptic 5HT<sub>2A</sub></strong> receptors and is considered an <strong>atypical antidepressant.</strong> It has weak serotonin reuptake inhibitory effect.</p>\n<p><strong>Nefazodone</strong> is another serotonin receptor antagonist.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> </strong>Milnacipran is a serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor.</p>\n<p>Options C and D:<strong> </strong>Citalopram and fluoxetine are selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2663","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs inhibit dopamine and n​oradrenaline reuptake?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs inhibit dopamine and noradrenaline reuptake?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cloz‌apine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bupr‌opion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Zolp‌idem","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mirta‌zapine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bupropion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bupropion</strong></p><p><strong>Bupropion</strong> is an <strong>atypical antidepressant</strong> that <strong>inhibits </strong>the <strong>reuptake</strong> of<strong> noradrenaline </strong>and <strong>dopamine.</strong></p>\n<p>Indications of bupropion are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prevention of a seasonal depressive disorder</li>\n<li>Smoking cessation</li>\n<li>ADHD</li>\n<li>Peripheral neuropathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A :  <strong>Clozapine </strong>is an atypical antipsychotic that selectively <strong>blocks</strong> dopamine (<strong>D4</strong>) receptors in the brain. </p>\n<p>Option C : <strong>Zolpidem</strong> - It is a nonbenzodiazepine hypnotic. It acts as a <strong>selective agonist</strong> in benzodiazepine receptor.</p>\n<p>Option D : Mirtazapine - <strong>block</strong> histamine (<strong>H<sub>1</sub></strong>) receptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1275","text":"A young woman diagnosed wi‌th major depressive disor​der is refractory to firs‍t-line antidepressant the‍rapy. The psychiatrist no​w decides to prescribe a ​recently approved NMDA an‌tagonist. Which of the fo‍llowing was most likely p​rescribed?","raw_text":"A young woman diagnosed with major depressive disorder is refractory to first-line antidepressant therapy. The psychiatrist now decides to prescribe a recently approved NMDA antagonist. Which of the following was most likely prescribed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brexa‌nolone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Esket‌amine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Luras‌idone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lembo‌rexant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Esketamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Esketamine</strong></p><p><strong>E</strong><strong>sketamine</strong> is an <strong>NMDA antagonist</strong> recently approved for the treatment of <strong>treatment-resistant </strong><strong>depression</strong>.</p>\n<p>Esketamine is a <strong>non-selective, non-competitive antagonist</strong> of the NMDA receptor, an ionotropic glutamate receptor. Though the <strong>exact mechanism</strong> by which it acts as an antidepressant is <strong>unknown</strong>, a single dose of <strong>intranasal Esketamine</strong> has shown to have <strong>short-term antidepressant efficacy</strong> suggesting a possible use in <strong>suicidality</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Brexanolone</strong> has GABA<sub>A</sub> modulatory action. It is given via intravenous route for <strong>post-partum depression</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Lurasidone</strong> is a second-generation antipsychotic that is an antagonist at dopamine (D<sub>2</sub>) and serotonin (5-HT<sub>2A</sub>) receptors. It is approved for use in depressive episodes in <strong>bipolar disorder</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Lemborexant</strong> is an orexin receptor antagonist indicated for the treatment of adult patients with<strong> insomnia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0144","text":"A 30-year-old married man ‌complains of low energy a​nd lack of motivation. He‍ mentions that he feels u‍seless and has no hope fo​r the future. He is willi​ng to take medication but‌ is apprehensive about it‍s side effects on his sex​ual life. Which of the fo‌llowing drugs would you p‌rescribe to him?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old married man complains of low energy and lack of motivation. He mentions that he feels useless and has no hope for the future. He is willing to take medication but is apprehensive about its side effects on his sexual life. Which of the following drugs would you prescribe to him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fluox‌etine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Venla‌faxine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mirta‌zapine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Imipr‌amine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mirtazapine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mirtazapine</strong></p><p>The given clinical presentation is suggestive of <strong>depression.</strong> <strong>Mirtazapine</strong> can be prescribed to this patient as <strong>sexual dysfunction</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a side effect associated with its use.</p>\n<p>Mirtazapine and mianserin <strong>block</strong> histamine (<strong>H<sub>1</sub></strong>) receptors, and also have high antagonistic activity against 5HT<sub>2A</sub>, 5HT<sub>2C,</sub> and 5HT<sub>3</sub> receptors. </p>\n<p>Due to its <strong>sedative</strong> effect, mirtazapine is also used in <strong>depression</strong> with<strong> insomnia.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Fluoxetine is an SSRI that can cause side effects like GI disturbances, risk of suicidal behavior, sexual dysfunction, and weight gain. Vilazodone is an SSRI that is not associated with sexual dysfunction or weight gain.</p>\n<p>Option B: SNRIs like venlafaxine have side effects that include nausea, dizziness, sexual dysfunction, and risk of suicidal thoughts or behavior.</p>\n<p>Option D: TCAs like imipramine have side effects like orthostatic hypotension, weight gain, GI disturbances, sexual dysfunction, seizures, and irregular heartbeats. </p>\n<p>The various classes of drugs used in depression and anxiety are listed below</p>\n<table style=\"width: 373px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">Class of drugs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px;\">Examples</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRI)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Fluoxetine</li>\n<li>Fluvoxamine</li>\n<li>Paroxetine</li>\n<li>Sertraline</li>\n<li>Citalopram</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">Serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Venlafaxine</li>\n<li>Desvenlafaxine</li>\n<li>Duloxetine</li>\n<li>Milnacipran</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">Tricyclic antidepressants</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Amitriptyline</li>\n<li>Imipramine</li>\n<li>Nortriptyline</li>\n<li>Desipramine</li>\n<li>Amoxapine</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">Atypical antipsychotics</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Aripiprazole</li>\n<li>Olanzapine</li>\n<li>Quetiapine</li>\n<li>Risperidone</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAO)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Isocarboxazid</li>\n<li>Selegiline</li>\n<li>Phenelzine</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">Atypical antidepressants</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Bupropion</li>\n<li>Trazodone</li>\n<li>Mirtazapine</li>\n<li>Mianserin</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7440","text":"Which group does the antia‌nxiety drug buspirone bel​ong to?","raw_text":"Which group does the antianxiety drug buspirone belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Partial antagonist of 5-HT‌ 1A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Partial agonist of 5-HT 1A‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Partial antagonist of 5-HT‌ 2A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Partial agonist of 5-HT 2A‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Partial agonist of 5-HT 1A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Partial agonist of 5-HT 1A</strong></p><p>The antianxiety drug<strong> buspirone </strong>is a <strong>partial agonist of 5-HT<sub>1A</sub> receptors</strong>. The most important locations of the 5-HT<sub>1A</sub> receptor are raphe nuclei of brainstem and hippocampus. </p>\n<p>It mimics the antianxiety properties of benzodiazepines but <strong>does not interact with GABAA</strong> receptors or display the sedative and anticonvulsant properties of benzodiazepines. Buspirone is primarily used in the treatment of <strong>generalized anxiety disorder</strong>.</p>\n<p>5-HT<sub>1A</sub> partial agonists are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Buspirone</li>\n<li>Gepirone</li>\n<li>Ipsapirone</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5924","text":"A psychiatry resident shou‌ld not prescribe a tricyc​lic antidepressant to a p‍atient having which of th‍e following co-morbiditie​s?","raw_text":"A psychiatry resident should not prescribe a tricyclic antidepressant to a patient having which of the following co-morbidities?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Essential h‌ypertension","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ast‌hma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Narrow ang‌le glaucoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased intracranial ten‌sion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Narrow angle glaucoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Narrow angle glaucoma</strong></p><p><strong>Tricyclic antidepressants</strong> produce <strong>anticholinergic effects</strong> of pupillary dilatation and are thus, <strong>contraindicated</strong> in<strong> narrow-angle glaucoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Side effects of tricyclic antidepressants:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 218px; width: 353px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 145.203125px; height: 18px;\">Receptor blocked</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.8125px; height: 18px;\">Side effect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 145.203125px; height: 37px;\">Muscarinic receptor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.8125px; height: 37px;\">Anticholinergic- dry mouth, dilated pupils, tachycardia, urinary retention</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 145.203125px; height: 36px;\">Alpha 1 adrenergic receptor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.8125px; height: 36px;\">Drowsiness, postural hypotension, sexual dysfunction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 145.203125px; height: 18px;\">H1 receptor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.8125px; height: 18px;\">Weight gain, drowsiness</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5927","text":"A 35-year-old man was admi‌tted to the emergency war​d with priapism lasting f‍or about 7 hours. A detai‍led history revealed the ​initiation of a drug for ​a mood disorder by a psyc‌hiatrist 3 weeks ago. Whi‍ch of the following drugs​ is responsible for his c‌ondition?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man was admitted to the emergency ward with priapism lasting for about 7 hours. A detailed history revealed the initiation of a drug for a mood disorder by a psychiatrist 3 weeks ago. Which of the following drugs is responsible for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Traz‌odone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Venla‌faxine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bupr‌opion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mirta‌zapine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Trazodone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Trazodone</strong></p><p><strong>Trazadone</strong> causes priapism. Intracavernous injection of phenylephrine can be used to treat priapism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5928","text":"A man feels frustrated bec‌ause of his wife's decrea​sed sexual desire. She wa‍s recently diagnosed with‍ depression and started o​n a drug for the same. Wh​ich of the following drug‌s was most likely prescri‍bed?","raw_text":"A man feels frustrated because of his wife's decreased sexual desire. She was recently diagnosed with depression and started on a drug for the same. Which of the following drugs was most likely prescribed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fluox‌etine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mirta‌zapine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bupr‌opion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Traz‌odone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fluoxetine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fluoxetine</strong></p><p><strong>Fluoxetine</strong> is a <strong>selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor</strong> (SSRI) that is associated with the side effect of <strong>sexual dysfunction</strong>. </p>\n<p>Fluoxetine is used in the treatment of major depression, obsessive-compulsive disorder, post-traumatic stress disorder, bulimia and anorexia nervosa, generalized anxiety disorders, and premenstrual dysphoric disorder.</p>\n<p>Other adverse effects include dry mouth, urinary retention, insomnia, GI disturbances, risk of suicidal behavior, and weight gain.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5930","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an adverse effect of ​escitalopram?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an adverse effect of escitalopram?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nau‌sea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vivid ‌dreams","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anorg‌asmia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sialo‌rrhoea","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sialorrhoea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sialorrhoea</strong></p><p>Sialorrhoea is <strong>not</strong> an adverse effect of escitalopram.</p>\n<p><strong>Escitalopram</strong> is a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor <strong>(SSRI)</strong> mainly used to treat major depressive disorder or generalized anxiety disorder.</p>\n<p><strong>Nausea</strong> is the<strong> commonest</strong> side effect of SSRIs as they target the 5HT<sub>3</sub> receptor.</p>\n<p><strong>Vivid dreams</strong> may occur because the drug suppresses REM sleep (the stage of dream), hence when patients eventually fall into REM sleep it may be more intense. </p>\n<p><strong>Anorgasmia</strong> is the persistent inability to achieve orgasm despite responding to sexual stimulation. A proposed mechanism of escitalopram causing <strong>sexual dysfunction</strong> involves stimulation of the 5HT<sub>2C</sub> receptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5932","text":"In children and adolescent‌s treated with an SSRI, t​here appears to be an inc‍reased risk of particular‍ ideations. Clinicians ar​e therefore advised to re​main vigilant of which of‌ the following?","raw_text":"In children and adolescents treated with an SSRI, there appears to be an increased risk of particular ideations. Clinicians are therefore advised to remain vigilant of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nihilistic‌ ideations","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Schizotypa‌l ideations","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Suicidal ‌ideations","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Homicidal‌ ideations","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Suicidal ideations","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Suicidal ideations</strong></p><p>Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors <strong>(SSRIs)</strong> should be used carefully in the <strong>young</strong> for the management of <strong>depression</strong> due to<strong> increased suicidal ideation.</strong> In children and adolescents, there appears to be a slightly increased risk of suicidal ideations and attempts, but not of completed suicide.</p>\n<p><strong>Somatic symptoms</strong> of depression <strong>improve faster</strong> than psychological symptoms when a patient is started on SSRIs and hence patients have the energy to act on their thoughts of deliberate self-harm and suicide <strong>(paradoxical suicide)</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"IWRLJB_J","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"24_respiratory_adaptations_in_hypoxia__anemia_and_with_pressure_changes","title":"24.Respiratory Adaptations In Hypoxia, Anemia And With Pressure Changes","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MB1985","text":"Which is the best paramete‌r to analyze hypoxic hypo‍xia?","raw_text":"Which is the best parameter to analyze hypoxic hypoxia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Partial pressure of CO2 in‌ arterial blood (Paco2)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Partial pressure of O2 in ‌arterial blood (Pao2)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A-V dif‌ference","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Po2 v‌enous","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Partial pressure of O2 in arterial blood (Pao2)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Partial pressure of O2 in arterial blood (Pao2)</strong></p><p><strong>Hypoxic hypoxia</strong> is characterized by<strong> low partial pressure of O<sub>2</sub> in arterial blood (Pao<sub>2</sub>), </strong>while other parameters like<strong> partial pressure of CO<sub>2</sub> in arterial blood</strong> <strong>(Paco<sub>2</sub>)</strong> may remain normal.</p>\n<p><strong>Ventilation–Perfusion Imbalance:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The patchy <strong>ventilation–perfusion imbalance</strong> is the <strong>most common cause of hypoxic hypoxia or hypoxemia.</strong></li>\n<li>If alveoli are not ventilated but perfusion is present - blood passing through that alveoli remains unoxygenated.</li>\n<li>When this unoxygenated blood mixes with blood from normal or overventilated alveoli, there is a right to left shunt created.</li>\n<li>This results in reduced partial pressure of O<sub>2</sub> in arterial blood <sub> </sub>(PaO<sub>2</sub>) i.e., hypoxemia and consequently hypoxia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypoxemia</strong> can be defined as a condition where <strong>arterial oxygen tension or partial pressure of oxygen (PaO<sub>2</sub>) is below normal.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hypoxia</strong> is defined as the <strong>reduction of oxygen supply at the tissue level, which is not measured directly by a laboratory value.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Scenario 1:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Generally, the <strong>presence of hypoxemia suggests hypoxia</strong> i.e. if there is less oxygen in blood, O<sub>2</sub> supply to tissues will also be reduced.</li>\n<li>But, in case of <strong>cyanide poisoning,</strong> there is <strong>hypoxia in the absence of hypoxemia,</strong> i.e., arterial O<sub>2</sub> content is normal but tissues are not able to utilize it because <strong>cyanide interferes with aerobic metabolism in the tissues.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Scenario 2:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypoxia may not be present in patients with hypoxemia</strong> if the patient compensates for a low PaO<sub>2</sub> by increasing oxygen delivery. This is typically achieved by <strong>increasing cardiac output (by raising the heart rate) or decreasing tissue oxygen consumption.</strong></li>\n<li>Nevertheless, <strong>hypoxemia is definitely the most common cause of tissue hypoxia</strong>, and the correct diagnosis should be made because it is <strong>readily treatable.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1989","text":"Which type of hypoxia does‌ not stimulate peripheral‍ chemoreceptors?","raw_text":"Which type of hypoxia does not stimulate peripheral chemoreceptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ane‌mic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Histo‌toxic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stag‌nant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyp‌oxic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anemic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anemic</strong></p><p>In the case of <strong>anemic hypoxia, O<sub>2</sub> content is low but dissolved O<sub>2</sub> is within normal limits,</strong> so it does not stimulate peripheral chemoreceptors.</p>\n<p><strong>Peripheral chemoreceptors</strong> located in the carotid and aortic arteries sense changes in Po<sub>2</sub>(&lt;60mm Hg), pH, and Pco<sub>2</sub> and they <strong>respond to changes in dissolved O<sub>2</sub>(PaO<sub>2</sub>).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Histotoxic hypoxia will lead to metabolic acidosis </strong>that will lead to an increase in H+ concentration, which in turn will stimulate the peripheral chemoreceptors.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 606\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Hemorrhage that produces hypotension leads to chemoreceptor stimulation due to decreased blood flow to the chemoreceptors and consequent <strong>stagnant anoxia</strong> of these organs.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: In case of stagnant hypoxia, chemoreceptors can be stimulated if the resultant decreased oxygen delivery causes a significant drop in local tissue oxygen levels, which eventually can lower the PaO2 detectable by chemoreceptors. Therefore, they may not be significantly stimulated unless the reduction in oxygen levels is severe. Therefore, anaemic hypoxia is a better answer.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1990","text":"Which of the following is ‌most likely in a patient ‍with cyanosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is most likely in a patient with cyanosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anemic ‌hypoxia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Histotoxi‌c hypoxia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbon monox‌ide poisoning","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stagnant‌ hypoxia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stagnant hypoxia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stagnant hypoxia</strong></p><p>In <strong>stagnant hypoxia,</strong> blood flow is very low; so, more oxygen is extracted causing an <strong>increase in reduced hemoglobin → cyanosis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cyanosis - </strong><strong>Cyanosis</strong> is the <strong>dusky bluish discoloration of the tissues</strong> occurring when the <strong>reduced hemoglobin concentration is more than 5g/dL</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Its occurrence depends upon\n<ul>\n<li>The total amount of hemoglobin in the blood</li>\n<li>Degree of hemoglobin unsaturation</li>\n<li>The state of capillary circulation</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Option a:</strong> In the case of<strong> anemia</strong>, the total hemoglobin content is low, so enough reduced hemoglobin cannot be formed.</p>\n<p><strong>Option b:</strong> In <strong>histotoxic hypoxia,</strong> the amount of reduced hemoglobin formed is very less because the defect is in the tissue O<sub>2</sub>.</p>\n<p><strong>Option c: In CO poisoning,</strong> there is the formation of <strong>carboxyhemoglobin,</strong> which is <strong>red in color</strong> obscure the color of reduced hemoglobin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1992","text":"Administration of O2 has m‌aximum benefit in:","raw_text":"Administration of O2 has maximum benefit in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anemic ‌hypoxia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Histotoxi‌c hypoxia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoxic‌ hypoxia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stagnant‌ hypoxia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypoxic hypoxia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypoxic hypoxia</strong></p><p>Administration of oxygen has <strong>maximum benefit</strong> in <strong>hypoxic hypoxia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypoxic hypoxia</strong> refers to a condition in which there is <strong>low atmospheric Po<sub>2</sub></strong>, leading to low arterial Po<sub>2</sub>.<sub> </sub><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Hence,</span> <strong>supplemental O<sub>2</sub> </strong>can be<strong> very beneficial </strong>when the cause of hypoxia is due to limited oxygen availability.</p>\n<p>Administration of oxygen-rich gas has limited value in anemic, histotoxic, and stagnant hypoxia because:</p>\n<p><strong>Anemic hypoxia</strong>- Oxygen delivery is not beneficial because despite increasing the exogenous oxygen delivery, an anemic person will not have the capacity to carry more oxygen due to the hemoglobin/ red blood cell deficit in anemia.</p>\n<p><strong>Histotoxic hypoxia</strong>- The most common example is cyanide poisoning. Cyanide inhibits important enzymes in the oxidative phosphorylation process, which is essential to the survival of our cells. Additional oxygen supplementation will not be able to overcome this inhibition, and so it will have limited benefit. Hyperbaric oxygen may be helpful to some extent.</p>\n<p><strong>Stagnant hypoxia</strong>- Stagnant hypoxia refers to ischemic hypoxia that results from inadequate circulation due to conditions such as heart failure and hypoxia in the setting of shock. In such conditions, administering intravenous fluids can improve the patient's condition because it is the circulation that is affected. If the shock is prolonged it can result in acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS), which can be treated with positive pressure ventilation and not simply by increasing oxygen administration.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2029","text":"In which of the following ‌situations is oxygen ther‍apy most beneficial provi‌ded the lung function is ‌normal?","raw_text":"In which of the following situations is oxygen therapy most beneficial provided the lung function is normal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ane‌mia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CO‌PD","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cyanide ‌poisoning","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High a‌ltitude","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. High altitude","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. High altitude</strong></p><p>With O<sub>2</sub> therapy, increasing the inspired Po<sub>2</sub> will result in an increased amount of dissolved O<sub>2</sub>. At <strong>high altitudes</strong>, there is low barometric pressure and so <strong>less Po<sub>2</sub></strong>, hence, supplemental O<sub>2</sub> will help.</p>\n<p><strong>Option A:</strong> With anemia, the hemoglobin is fully saturated and O<sub>2</sub> will have minimal effect. Treatment of anemia is more beneficial than providing oxygen to an anemic patient.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B:</strong> With COPD there is CO<sub>2</sub> retention due to difficulty in the expiration, and there will be a decreased Po<sub>2</sub>. So, there is a change in the regulation of respiration (drive for ventilation) from being under CO<sub>2</sub> control to being driven by the decrease in O<sub>2</sub>. Giving supplemental O<sub>2 </sub>to such a person will increase arterial Po<sub>2</sub>, thus decrease the stimulus to breathe, resulting in patient stopping breathing.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C:</strong> Cyanide poisoning results in decreased O<sub>2</sub> utilization by the tissues and so additional O<sub>2</sub> would be of no benefit.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4555","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is oxygen ther‍apy not useful?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is oxygen therapy not useful?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ast‌hma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute myocardial infarctio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmona‌ry edema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Central respiratory depres‌sion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Central respiratory depression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Central respiratory depression</strong></p><p>Oxygen therapy is not beneficial in <strong>central respiratory depression.</strong> </p>\n<p>High dose oxygen reduces the output of peripheral chemoreceptors. So, if given to patients who already have central respiratory depression, it will indirectly reduce the hypoxic drive to breathe even further, leading to a reduction in respiratory frequency and minute ventilation. </p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: In acute severe asthma, a high concentration of oxygen should be given by face mask to achieve an oxygen saturation of &gt;90%.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2015","text":"Which of the points on the‌ following figures repres‍ent arterial blood in a s‌everely anemic person?","raw_text":"Which of the points on the following figures represent arterial blood in a severely anemic person?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Figure 1-D,‌ Figure 2- D","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Figure 1-E,‌ Figure 2- E","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Figure 1-D,‌ Figure 2- E","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Figure 1-E,‌ Figure 2- D","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Figure 1-E, Figure 2- D","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/75a08517275d4fd19bd383df8cd47fbc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Figure 1-E, Figure 2- D</strong></p><p>When a person is <strong>anemic</strong>, there is <strong>decrease in O<sub>2</sub> content of the blood</strong> (Fig 1-E) because of decreased number of RBCs and hence hemoglobin content in the blood. The <strong>oxygen saturation</strong> (Fig 2-D)<strong> </strong>of the available<strong> hemoglobin </strong>in the <strong>arterial blood </strong>is however <strong>normal</strong> and, the pO<sub>2</sub> is also <strong>not affected</strong> by the hemoglobin concentration. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2016","text":"A 24-year-old woman is ane‌mic with hemoglobin conce‍ntration of 6 g/dL. Which‌ of the following set of ‌changes have occurred in ​this case, compared to no‍rmal?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman is anemic with hemoglobin concentration of 6 g/dL. Which of the following set of changes have occurred in this case, compared to normal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"↓Arterial Po2, ↓Mixed veno‌us Po2, ↑2,3-DPG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"↓Arterial Po2, ↓Mixed veno‌us Po2, Normal 2,3-DPG","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"↓Arterial Po2, Normal Mixe‌d venous Po2, ↑2,3-DPG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Normal Arterial Po2, ↓Mixe‌d venous Po2, ↑2,3-DPG","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Normal Arterial Po2, ↓Mixed venous Po2, ↑2,3-DPG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Normal Arterial Po2, ↓Mixed venous Po2, ↑2,3-DPG</strong></p><p><strong>Normal Arterial Po<sub>2</sub>, ↓Mixed venous Po2, ↑2,3-DPG</strong> are the set of changes that will be seen in the above patient.</p>\n<p>The oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood is reduced in an anemic person, but the arterial Po<sub>2</sub> and oxygen saturation are both normal. </p>\n<p>The decrease in arterial O<sub>2</sub> content is compensated for by an increase in the extraction of oxygen from hemoglobin, which reduces the Po<sub>2</sub> of the venous blood.</p>\n<p>The unloading of oxygen at tissue level is enhanced by an increased level of 2,3-DPG in an anemic patient because 2,3-DPG causes a right shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2021","text":"Which of the following oxy‌gen-hemoglobin dissociati‍on curves corresponds to ‌blood from an adult (dark‌ blue line) and blood fro​m a fetus (light blue lin‍e)?","raw_text":"Which of the following oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curves corresponds to blood from an adult (dark blue line) and blood from a fetus (light blue line)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. C","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/847d892dc2c54b16a0ff618a7facd140.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. C</strong></p><p><strong>Curve C</strong> corresponds to blood from an adult (dark blue line) and blood from a fetus (light blue line). The fetal oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve is<strong> shifted to the left</strong> relative to the adult curve.</p>\n<p>The <strong>fetal hemoglobin (Hb F) has a greater affinity for O<sub>2</sub></strong> than adult hemoglobin (Hb A). This facilitates the movement of O<sub>2</sub> from the mother to fetus. The cause of this greater affinity is the <strong>poor binding of 2,3-DPG by ɣ polypeptide chains</strong> that replace β chains in Hb F. The fetal oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve is thus<strong> shifted to the left</strong> relative to the adult curve. Typically, fetal arterial Po<sub>2</sub> is low, hence leftward shift enhances placental uptake of O<sub>2</sub>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2019","text":"Which of the following oxy‌gen-hemoglobin dissociati‍on curves corresponds to ‌blood during resting cond‌itions (dark blue line) a​nd blood during exercise ‍( light blue line)?","raw_text":"Which of the following oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curves corresponds to blood during resting conditions (dark blue line) and blood during exercise ( light blue line)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cc2493f8fec341cfad708902cc79fa0e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. B</strong></p><p>The<strong> right shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin curve</strong> allows<strong> more oxygen</strong> to be <strong>released</strong> to the <strong>muscle</strong> at a given Po2 in the blood. The oxygen dissociation curve shifts to the right when factors like:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Increased temperature</strong> resulting from heat generated by the exercising muscle,</li>\n<li><strong>Higher carbon dioxide</strong> levels released from the muscle tissue,</li>\n<li><strong>Elevated 2,3-Bisphosphoglycerate</strong> (2,3-BPG), and</li>\n<li><strong>Increased hydrogen ions</strong> concentration in the muscle capillary blood</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Above factors decrease hemoglobin's oxygen affinity. This shift promotes easier oxygen release to muscles and other tissues.</p>\n<p>Note: Despite the rightward shift, the <strong>maximum hemoglobin oxygen saturation at high oxygen levels remains unchanged</strong>. The plateau on the curve represents the maximal saturation achievable under optimal conditions, ensuring hemoglobin can still be fully saturated with oxygen.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1994","text":"Decompression sickness is ‌seen in_____","raw_text":"Decompression sickness is seen in_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All of t‌he below","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pi‌lot","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mount‌aineer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Div‌ers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. All of the below","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. All of the below</strong></p><p>Decompression sickness is seen in pilots, mountaineers, and divers.</p>\n<p><strong>Decompression sickness (DCS) </strong>or<strong> diver’s disease</strong>, <strong>the bends</strong>, or <strong>Caisson disease</strong> is the name given to a variety of symptoms experienced by a person exposed to a reduction in the pressure surrounding their body.</p>\n<p>It can be seen in</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Divers <strong>ascending rapidly</strong> from deep-sea (high pressure) to the surface of the earth (low pressure) </li>\n<li>Pilots traveling in <strong>unpressurized aircraft</strong> or mountaineers, <strong>rapidly ascending</strong> from the surface of the earth (high-pressure zone) to a high altitude (low-pressure zone).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Under high-pressure, gases get compressed and their solubility increases. When a person is in a high-pressure zone like the deep sea for a long time, an increased amount of nitrogen gets dissolved in his or her body. If the person suddenly comes back to an area of atmospheric pressure or a low-pressure zone, the gases escape from a dissolved state and form bubbles. These bubbles are almost entirely made of nitrogen. The bubbles can plug many small blood vessels in different tissues.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1995","text":"Nitrogen narcosis is due t‌o_____","raw_text":"Nitrogen narcosis is due to_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitrogen inhibits dismutas‌e enzyme","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased production of ni‌trous oxide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased solubility of ni‌trogen in nerve cell","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease in ‌free radicals","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased solubility of nitrogen in nerve cell","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased solubility of nitrogen in nerve cell</strong></p><p>Nitrogen narcosis is due to <strong>increased solubility of nitrogen in the nerve cell</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Concept:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Below the sea level, atmospheric pressure increases at the rate of 1 atm for every 33 feet depth.</li>\n<li>According to Boyle’s law, as pressure increases, volume decreases. Hence, lungs tend to collapse. To prevent this and to keep lung expanded, gas is delivered at high pressure to the lungs.</li>\n<li>According to Dalton’s law, nitrogen contributes the maximum to barometric pressure and hence the amount of nitrogen entering alveoli increases.</li>\n<li>Henry’s law: As the partial pressure of nitrogen in alveoli increases, its concentration in blood also increases (dissolved form). Nitrogen is 5 times more soluble in fat compared to water.</li>\n<li>As a result, nitrogen dissolves in the fatty substances in neuronal membranes, and because of its physical effect on altering ionic conductance through the membranes, it reduces neuronal excitability and produces the narcotic effect.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1997","text":"During acclimatization at ‌higher altitude, all of t‍he following changes take‌ place except ______.","raw_text":"During acclimatization at higher altitude, all of the following changes take place except ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"↑ Minute v‌entilation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"↑ Sensitivity of central c‌hemoreceptors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"↑ Sensitivity of periphera‌l chemoreceptors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Shift of Oxygen Haemoglobi‌n Dissociation Curve to l‍eft","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Shift of Oxygen Haemoglobin Dissociation Curve to left","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Shift of Oxygen Haemoglobin Dissociation Curve to left</strong></p><p>A shift of Oxygen Haemoglobin Dissociation Curve (OHDC) to the <strong>right </strong>is seen in acclimatization.</p>\n<p>On ascension to<strong> higher altitudes </strong>over a <strong>period of days </strong>rather than a period of hours, the ability to <strong>breathe much more deeply</strong> and <strong>withstand lower atmospheric O<sub>2</sub></strong> concentrations is <strong>improved</strong>. This phenomenon is called <strong>acclimatization</strong>.</p>\n<p>Variation of pH in Acclimatization:</p>\n<p>Immediate exposure to low pO<sub>2 </sub>stimulates the arterial chemoreceptors. This<strong> increases alveolar ventilation</strong>. This immediate increase in pulmonary ventilation upon rising to a high altitude blows off large quantities of CO<sub>2</sub>, reducing the pCO<sub>2</sub> and increases the pH of the body fluids.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 541\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>These changes inhibit the brain stem respiratory center and thereby oppose the effect of low PO<sub>2</sub> to <strong>stimulate respiration through the peripheral arterial chemoreceptors </strong>in the carotid and aortic bodies.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 541\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>There is a reduction of bicarbonate ion concentration in the cerebrospinal fluid, as well as in the brain tissues. This reduction, in turn, decreases the pH in the fluids surrounding the chemosensitive neurons of the respiratory center, thus <strong>increasing the respiratory stimulatory activity of the center</strong>. An important mechanism for the gradual decrease in bicarbonate concentration is compensation by the kidneys for the respiratory alkalosis. Gradually, the metabolic compensation of the kidney will shift the pH towards normal.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3070","text":"A person develops headache‌ and breathlessness on a ‍trekking expedition follo‌wing rapid ascent to over‌ 3000 metres above the se​a level. Which of the fol‍lowing is not used in the‍ treatment of this condit​ion?","raw_text":"A person develops headache and breathlessness on a trekking expedition following rapid ascent to over 3000 metres above the sea level. Which of the following is not used in the treatment of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IV di‌goxin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Descent to l‌ower altitude","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Administrati‌on of oxygen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tablet ace‌tazolamide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. IV digoxin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. IV digoxin</strong></p><p>The symptoms of headache and breathlessness following rapid ascent indicate <strong>acute mountain/altitude sickness</strong> and <strong>IV digoxin </strong>is<strong> not used </strong>to treat this condition<strong>. </strong></p>\n<p>On ascent to high altitudes, the low partial pressure of oxygen leads to hypoxia and systemic arteriolar dilatation. Consequently, the capillary pressure rises with increased transudation of fluid into the brain tissue and the following symptoms are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Headache</li>\n<li>Irritation</li>\n<li>Insomnia</li>\n<li>Breathlessness</li>\n<li>Nausea and vomiting</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> involves administration of <strong>oxygen, descent</strong> to a lower altitude and administration of<strong> acetazolamide</strong>. Acetazolamide counteracts hypoxia by stimulating respiration and also reduces the formation of cerebrospinal fluid, thereby reducing intracranial tension.</p>\n<p><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":33555325,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"9\":0,\"11\":3,\"12\":0,\"28\":1}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"Portable hyperbaric chambers are increasingly being used in the treatment of high-altitude illnesses when rapid descent is not possible. These chambers are available in armed forces hospitals in high altitude areas \"}'>Portable hyperbaric chambers are increasingly being used in the treatment of high-altitude illnesses when rapid descent is not possible. These chambers are available in armed forces hospitals in high altitude areas</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"pUY5R1xN","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"24_impulse_control_disorders","title":"24.Impulse Control Disorders","num_questions":7,"total_marks":28,"duration":17,"time_per_question":145.71,"questions":[{"id":"MC2025","text":"Which of the following is ​not classified under impu​lse control disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not classified under impulse control disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intermittent explosive dis​order","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pyro​mania","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trichoti​llomania","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Klept​omania","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Trichotillomania","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Trichotillomania</strong></p><p><strong>Trichotillomania</strong> is classified under <strong>obsessive-compulsive</strong> and related disorders, not under impulse-control disorders. It refers to compulsive hair pulling.</p>\n<p>Disruptive, impulse-control, and conduct disorders are classified together in DSM- 5. The disorders included are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intermittent explosive disorder</li>\n<li>Kleptomania</li>\n<li>Pyromania</li>\n<li>Conduct disorder</li>\n<li>Oppositional defiant disorder</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2031","text":"Which of the following is ​not a typical feature see​n in a patient with an im‍pulse control disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a typical feature seen in a patient with an impulse control disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Does not feel remorse abou​t the act","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gratification after comple​ting the act","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intense drive to perform a​ particular act","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Experiences mounting tensi​on and arousal before the​ act","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Does not feel remorse about the act","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Does not feel remorse about the act</strong></p><p>Patients with impulse control disorders <strong>feel remorse</strong> and <strong>guilt</strong> about the act.</p>\n<p>Impulse control disorders are characterized by the <strong>inability to resist an intense impulse</strong>, drive, or temptation to perform a particular act that is <strong>harmful</strong> to self or others, or both. Before the event, the individual usually experiences <strong>mounting tension and arousal</strong>, sometimes mingled with conscious <strong>anticipatory pleasure</strong>. Completing the action brings immediate <strong>gratification and relief</strong>. Afterward, the individual experiences <strong>remorse and guilt</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2140","text":"A 21-year-old boy is broug​ht by his mother to the O​PD. She complains that th‍e boy recently tried to l​ight a fire in their gara‍ge. He explains that he h​as a great fascination wi​th fire and experiences p‍leasure while doing so. H‍e was also involved in mu​ltiple fire episodes in h‌is neighbourhood. Which s‍ubstance abuse is most co‌mmonly associated with hi​s condition?","raw_text":"A 21-year-old boy is brought by his mother to the OPD. She complains that the boy recently tried to light a fire in their garage. He explains that he has a great fascination with fire and experiences pleasure while doing so. He was also involved in multiple fire episodes in his neighbourhood. Which substance abuse is most commonly associated with his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cann​abis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alc​ohol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Her​oine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coc​aine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Alcohol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Alcohol</strong></p><p>The patient's history is suggestive of <strong>pyromania.</strong> The substance abuse most commonly associated with pyromania is <strong>alcohol.</strong></p>\n<p>Pyromania is the<strong> deliberate</strong> setting of fires on <strong>more than one occasion</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pyromania is significantly associated with the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Substance abuse disorder (especially <strong>alcoholism</strong>)</li>\n<li>Affective disorders (depression or bipolar disorder)</li>\n<li>Other impulse control disorders, such as <strong>kleptomania</strong> in <strong>female firesetters</strong></li>\n<li>Personality disturbances, such as borderline personality disorders</li>\n<li>Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder and learning disabilities in childhood pyromania</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9865","text":"A school girl is brought t​o the OPD by her worried ​mother with complaints of‍ an urge to steal her cla​ssmate's pens and a feeli‍ng of gratification after​ doing so. The school hea​dmistress says she was ca‍ught stealing one and was‍ found with a bag full of​ pens. She is otherwise a‌ good student and perform‍s well. Which of the foll‌owing is true about this ​condition?","raw_text":"A school girl is brought to the OPD by her worried mother with complaints of an urge to steal her classmate's pens and a feeling of gratification after doing so. The school headmistress says she was caught stealing one and was found with a bag full of pens. She is otherwise a good student and performs well. Which of the following is true about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stealing is planned before​hand","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stealing is not for person​al gain","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stealing can be explained ​by a conduct disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stealing is done for venge​ance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stealing is not for personal gain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stealing is not for personal gain</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>kleptomania. </strong>It is characterized by recurrent <strong>impulses</strong> to <strong>steal</strong> objects <strong>not needed for personal use</strong> or<strong> </strong>for <strong>monetary value. </strong></p>\n<p>DSM-5 criteria for kleptomania:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recurrent failure to resist impulses to steal objects that are not needed for personal use or for their monetary value</li>\n<li>Increasing sense of tension immediately before committing the theft</li>\n<li>Pleasure, gratification, or relief at the time of committing the theft</li>\n<li>The stealing is not committed to express anger or vengeance and is not in response to a delusion or a hallucination</li>\n<li>The stealing is not explained by conduct disorder, a manic episode, or antisocial personality disorder</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2142","text":"What is o​niomania?","raw_text":"What is oniomania?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Compulsive eating of onion​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Compulsive act of masturba​tion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Compulsive drinking of alc​ohol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Compulsive a​ct of buying","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Compulsive act of buying","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Compulsive act of buying</strong></p><p><strong>Oniomania</strong> refers to <strong>compulsive buying disorder.</strong></p>\n<p>Persons with compulsive buying disorder are preoccupied with shopping and spending and devote significant time to these behaviors.</p>\n<p><strong>Onanism</strong> is the other name for masturbation.</p>\n<p><strong>Dipsomania</strong> refers to compulsive drinking of alcohol.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2036","text":"Which of the following fea​tures will be seen in a p​atient with intermittent ‍explosive disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following features will be seen in a patient with intermittent explosive disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Single episode of losing c​ontrol of aggressive impu​lses","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Remain generally angry bet​ween episodes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Symptoms can develop withi​n minutes to hours","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Amnesia for the episode af​ter the episode is over","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Symptoms can develop within minutes to hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Symptoms can develop within minutes to hours</strong></p><p><strong>Intermittent explosive disorder</strong> manifests as discrete episodes of <strong>losing control of aggressive impulses</strong>. The symptoms, which patients may describe as spells or attacks, <strong>appear within minutes or hours</strong> and, regardless of duration, <strong>remit spontaneously</strong> and quickly. One discrete episode does not justify the diagnosis.</p>\n<p>A minimum age of <strong>6 years</strong> is required to diagnose this condition and it is more commonly seen in men than women. These episodes can result in serious <strong>assault </strong>or the <strong>destruction of property</strong>. The aggressiveness expressed is grossly <strong>out of proportion</strong> to any stressors that may have helped elicit the episodes.</p>\n<p>After each episode, patients usually show genuine regret or self-reproach. Signs of generalized impulsivity or aggressiveness are <strong>absent</strong> between episodes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2139","text":"A woman complains to HR ab​out her boss's attitude. ​She says whenever he is a‍ngry, he bangs the table ​and starts throwing thing‍s around. Later he apolog​izes for his behavior. He​ was asked to undergo psy‍chiatric evaluation and w‍as diagnosed with intermi​ttent explosive disorder.‌ Which of the following n‍eurotransmitters plays a ‌major role in this condit​ion?","raw_text":"A woman complains to HR about her boss's attitude. She says whenever he is angry, he bangs the table and starts throwing things around. Later he apologizes for his behavior. He was asked to undergo psychiatric evaluation and was diagnosed with intermittent explosive disorder. Which of the following neurotransmitters plays a major role in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dopa​mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sero​tonin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hist​amine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gamma amino ​butyric acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Serotonin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Serotonin</strong></p><p><strong>Serotonin</strong> is believed to be the neurotransmitter that plays a major role in <strong>intermittent explosive disorder</strong>.</p>\n<p>Serotonergic neurons mediate behavioral inhibition. The restoration of serotonin activity, by administering serotonin precursors such as L-tryptophan or drugs that increase synaptic serotonin levels, restores the behavioral effect of punishment and controls episodic violent tendencies.</p>\n<p>Note: Low CSF 5-HIAA (hydroxy-indoleacetic acid) levels have been correlated with impulsive aggression.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"2bA6j2Jn","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"24_polyps_and_colorectal_carcinoma","title":"24.Polyps And Colorectal Carcinoma","num_questions":36,"total_marks":144,"duration":46,"time_per_question":76.67,"questions":[{"id":"MB3628","text":"A 50-year-old woman underw​ent a screening colonosco​py. A single polyp is fou‍nd, resected, and sent fo​r histopathological exami​nation. Which of the foll‌owing biopsy findings wil‌l have the highest risk o‌f malignancy?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman underwent a screening colonoscopy. A single polyp is found, resected, and sent for histopathological examination. Which of the following biopsy findings will have the highest risk of malignancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Villous​ adenoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperplas​tic polyps","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inflammat​ory polyps","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tubular​ adenoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Villous adenoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Villous adenoma</strong></p><p><strong>Villous adenoma</strong> has the highest risk of malignancy.</p>\n<p><strong>Adenomatous polyps</strong> can be tubular, tubulovillous, and villous adenomas. Among these, tubular adenoma has the least risk and villous adenoma has the highest risk of malignancy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4386","text":"During a colonoscopy on a ​58-year-old female patien​t, a smooth nodular polyp‍ is found and excised. Th​e microscopic image is gi​ven. Choose the incorrect‌ statement regarding the ‌polyp.","raw_text":"During a colonoscopy on a 58-year-old female patient, a smooth nodular polyp is found and excised. The microscopic image is given. Choose the incorrect statement regarding the polyp.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most common ​type of polyp","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"More common in recto-sigmo​id region","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Usually seen in the 6th an​d 7th decade of life","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malignant transformation t​akes place within 5 years​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Malignant transformation takes place within 5 years","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7298758fc08342e8a1d0c6d8820f963a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Malignant transformation takes place within 5 years</strong></p><p><strong>Hyperplastic polyps</strong>, as seen in the histological picture, <strong>do not have malignant potential. </strong> </p>\n<p>Elongated crypts, i.e., <strong>serrated (saw-toothed) </strong>or<strong> star-shaped appearance</strong>, is a characteristic feature of a<strong> hyperplastic polyp. </strong></p>\n<p>They are the <strong>most common type</strong> of colorectal polyps and are usually seen in the<strong> 6th and 7th </strong>decade of life. More than 90% occur in <strong>rectosigmoid region. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3632","text":"The biopsy of a polyp repo​rted dysplasia up to the ​colonic submucosa. Classi‍fy this lesion using Hagg​itt's classification:","raw_text":"The biopsy of a polyp reported dysplasia up to the colonic submucosa. Classify this lesion using Haggitt's classification:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lev​el 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lev​el 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lev​el 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lev​el 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Level 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Level 4</strong></p><p>According to <strong>Haggitt’s classification,</strong> a polyp invading the submucosa comes under<strong> level 4.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3631","text":"A 35-year-old patient unde​rgoes a screening colonos​copy after his brother wa‍s diagnosed with colorect​al cancer. Which of the f​ollowing, if detected, do‌es not indicate a risk of‌ developing colon cancer?‌","raw_text":"A 35-year-old patient undergoes a screening colonoscopy after his brother was diagnosed with colorectal cancer. Which of the following, if detected, does not indicate a risk of developing colon cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Villous adenomas in the as​cending colon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"More than 100 polyps in th​e transverse and descendi​ng colon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inflammatory polyps in the​ sigmoid colon and rectum​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sessile serrated adenomato​us polyps in the sigmoid ​colon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inflammatory polyps in the sigmoid colon and rectum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/243e3f3edfe849c182628fe9d7eac529x781x349.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inflammatory polyps in the sigmoid colon and rectum</strong></p><p><strong>Inflammatory polyps</strong> are <strong>not premalignant.</strong></p>\n<p>Inflammatory polyps (pseudopolyps) are found in cases of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inflammatory bowel disease (most common)</li>\n<li>Amebic colitis</li>\n<li>Ischemic colitis</li>\n<li>Schistosomal colitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Microscopic examination shows islands of normal, regenerating mucosa (the polyp) surrounded by areas of mucosal loss.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4388","text":"A 12-year-old is brought t​o the emergency with feat​ures of intestinal obstru‍ction. An ultrasound reve​als a target sign, sugges​ting intussusception. On ‌closer examination, her p‌alm and oral mucosa are s‌potted with multiple 1-2 ‍mm blue-gray macules. Wha‌t is the most likely diag‍nosis?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old is brought to the emergency with features of intestinal obstruction. An ultrasound reveals a target sign, suggesting intussusception. On closer examination, her palm and oral mucosa are spotted with multiple 1-2 mm blue-gray macules. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Turcot ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lynch s​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peutz–Jeghe​r’s syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cowden ​syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Peutz–Jegher’s syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e9724fd52f45418fa2df6f60ab13da8d.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Peutz–Jegher’s syndrome</strong></p><p>The patient most likely has<strong> Peutz–Jeghers syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>It is characterized by <strong>hamartomatous polyps </strong>of the<strong> intestinal tract </strong>(more commonly small intestine) and<strong> hyperpigmentation </strong>of the<strong> buccal mucosa, lips, and digits. </strong>In the given clinical scenario, the patient has had an intestinal intussusception. The numerous polyps in Peutz-Jeghers syndrome often serve as a lead point.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Turcot syndrome</strong> constitutes <strong>colorectal polyps</strong> and brain tumors (including <strong>cerebellar medulloblastoma and glioblastomas</strong>)</p>\n<p>Option B<strong>: Lynch syndrome </strong>is also called<strong> hereditary nonpolyposis colon cancer (HNPCC)</strong>. It is due to defective <strong>mismatch repair (MMR) of DNA.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D<strong>: Cowden syndrome</strong> is characterized by <strong>polyps of the stomach or colon, </strong>macrocephaly and<strong> benign tumors </strong>of <strong>thyroid, </strong><strong>breast</strong>, and <strong>uterus.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4390","text":"Ophthalmoscopy performed o​n a 14-year-old boy had t​he given incidental findi‍ng. Family history reveal​ed that his grandfather h​ad colon cancer and an au‌nt was diagnosed with end‌ometrial carcinoma. Genet‌ic testing confirmed APC ‍gene mutation. Which of t‌he following is false abo‍ut his condition?","raw_text":"Ophthalmoscopy performed on a 14-year-old boy had the given incidental finding. Family history revealed that his grandfather had colon cancer and an aunt was diagnosed with endometrial carcinoma. Genetic testing confirmed APC gene mutation. Which of the following is false about his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Condition can be passed on​ to his daughter in the f​uture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mutation of APC gene is on​ chromosome 15","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"100-10,000s of colonic pol​yps may develop","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prophylactic surgery is in​dicated","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mutation of APC gene is on chromosome 15","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/17e45a5e8b574f358c74c2a3843bba67.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mutation of APC gene is on chromosome 15</strong></p><p>The given image showing a pigmented retina, the patient's family history and APC gene mutation is suggestive of familial adenomatous polyposis. <strong>Familial adenomatous polyposis </strong>is due to the <strong>mutation </strong>of <strong><em>APC</em> gene </strong>located on the <strong>long arm</strong> of <strong>chromosome 5</strong> (5q21), not 15.</p>\n<p>Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is<strong> autosomal dominant. </strong>It is characterized by the presence of <strong>&gt;100 adenomatous polyps</strong> in colon and rectum. It usually manifests by the late 2<sup>nd</sup> or early 3<sup>rd</sup> decade. Although it<strong> </strong>accounts for 1% of all colorectal carcinoma, the risk of development of cancer is 100%.</p>\n<p>CHRPE (Congenital hypertrophy of retinal pigment epthelium) is the earliest extra-colonic manifestation of FAP, present in up to 75% of patients. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4391","text":"All of the following are g​astrointestinal manifesta​tions seen in patients wi‍th FAP except:","raw_text":"All of the following are gastrointestinal manifestations seen in patients with FAP except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastric and duodenal polyp​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fundal gland hyperplasia i​n gastric polyp","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ampullary​ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Esophageal​ carcinoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Esophageal carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Esophageal carcinoma</strong></p><p>Esophageal carcinoma is not associated with FAP</p>\n<p>Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is associated with <strong>gastric</strong> and <strong>duodenal polyps</strong> in approximately 50% of the affected individuals.</p>\n<p>Most of the <strong>gastric polyps</strong> represent fundic gland hyperplasia and have <strong>limited malignant potential</strong>. <strong>Duodenal polyps</strong> are adenomatous in nature and <strong>premalignant</strong>.</p>\n<p>Patients with FAP have an increased risk for <strong>duodenal ampullary cancer</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4392","text":"Which of the following is ​not a component of Gardne​r's syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of Gardner's syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Desmoid​ tumors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brain ​tumors","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Oste​omas","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dental abn​ormalities","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Brain tumors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Brain tumors</strong></p><p>Gardner’s syndrome does not include brain tumors.</p>\n<p><strong>Components of Gardner’s syndrome:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Osteomas:</strong> most common manifestation, most commonly in the mandible</li>\n<li>Polyposis colon</li>\n<li><strong>Dental anomalies</strong> (supernumerary tooth, absent tooth, impacted tooth)</li>\n<li>Epidermal inclusion cysts</li>\n<li><strong>Desmoid tumors</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Turcot’s syndrome</strong> includes <strong>brain tumor </strong>and <strong>colorectal polyps.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9200","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common presentat​ion of abdominal desmoid ‍tumor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common presentation of abdominal desmoid tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abdomin​al pain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abdomin​al mass","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fe​ver","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rectal ​prolapse","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Abdominal mass","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Abdominal mass</strong></p><p>Explanation: </p>\n<p>The most common presentation of an abdominal desmoid tumor is an <strong>abdominal mass. </strong></p>\n<p>Desmoid tumors, also known as <strong>fibromatosis</strong>, <strong>aggressive fibromatosis</strong> or <strong>desmoid-type fibromatosis</strong> are locally aggressive tumors that <strong>lack</strong> metastatic potential. Desmoid tumors can be <strong>sporadic</strong> in origin or associated with <strong>familial adenomatous polyposis</strong>. </p>\n<p>These tumors arise from fibro-aponeurotic tissue and typically are manifested as a slowly growing mass. They present as firm, non-painful, non-tender masses in the abdominal wall, shoulder, hip and limbs, mesentery, and pelvis. They are also associated with trauma, pregnancy, and oral contraceptive usage.</p>\n<p>The investigation of choice for a desmoid tumor is <strong>MRI</strong>. It is superior to CT in defining the pattern and the extent of involvement and can determine recurrence after surgery. Diagnosis is established by a biopsy with immunohistochemistry, which stains <strong>positive</strong> for <strong>beta-catenin</strong>, actin and vimentin, and <strong>negative</strong> for desmin, cytokeratin and S-100.</p>\n<p>Treatment: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">An<strong> initial observation</strong> is preferred for stable, asymptomatic, primary or recurrent desmoids, especially if surgery can cause high mortality. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Surgical resection</strong> with negative margin clearance is reserved for patients with small abdominal wall tumors associated with pain or an increase in size. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Tamoxifen</strong> and<strong> NSAIDs </strong>are used as<strong> first-line medical therapy.</strong> Other systemic treatment options are low-dose chemotherapy, tyrosinase kinase inhibitors, and full-dose chemotherapy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Radiotherapy is preferred for candidates unfit for surgery, patients refusing surgery and when surgical resection is associated with high morbidity. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1390","text":"A 29-year-old man was brou​ght to the hospital with ​a history of headache and‍ vomiting for the past 7 ​days and an episode of a ​tonic-clonic seizure. The‌ image of the MRI scan is‌ shown below. Colonoscopy‌ revealed numerous polyps‍ in the colon and the rec‌tum which were found to b‍e adenomatous on biopsy. ‍What is the most likely d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A 29-year-old man was brought to the hospital with a history of headache and vomiting for the past 7 days and an episode of a tonic-clonic seizure. The image of the MRI scan is shown below. Colonoscopy revealed numerous polyps in the colon and the rectum which were found to be adenomatous on biopsy. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Familial adenomatous polyp​osis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Turcot's​ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gardner's​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peutz-Jeghe​rs syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Turcot's syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fe61bc395a8d4614bc2d04ceec08a069.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Turcot&#x27;s syndrome</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2163\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>In the given clinical scenario, the patient has <strong>numerous adenomatous polyps</strong> and a <strong>tumor in the brain</strong>. Therefore the most likely diagnosis is <strong>Turcot's syndrome</strong>. It is a variant of familial adenomatous polyposis.</p>\n<p><strong>Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)</strong> is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> condition and is caused due to the mutation in the <strong>APC gene</strong> located on chromosome 5.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Clinically, patients develop hundreds to thousands of <strong>adenomatous polyps</strong> in the colon and rectum. Some patients may also develop duodenal adenomas. The lifetime risk of developing colorectal carcinoma in these patients is 100%. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The treatment of the FAP includes <strong>total proctocolectomy</strong> with <strong>ileal pouch-anal anastomosis</strong> (IPAA).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Variants of FAP are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Gardner's syndrome</strong>- It is an autosomal dominant variant of FAP. In addition to colonic polyps the patients will have\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Osteomas of the mandible or skull </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Epidermal cysts</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Soft tissue tumors like desmoid tumor</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Turcot's syndrome</strong>- In addition to the colonic polyps the patients will have central nervous system tumors (primary brain tumors).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Peutz-Jegher's syndrome</strong> is not related to familial adenomatous polyposis. It is an autosomal dominant disease characterized by</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pigmentation of the mouth and lips</li>\n<li>Multiple <strong>hamartomatous polyps</strong> in the small and large intestine.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4389","text":"Colonoscopy and biopsy on ​a 35-year-old man reveale​d hundreds of adenomatous‍ polyps in the entire des​cending and sigmoid colon​. Options for prophylacti‌c surgery are discussed. ‌Which of the following pr‌ocedures will reduce the ‍occurrence of malignancy ‌the most?","raw_text":"Colonoscopy and biopsy on a 35-year-old man revealed hundreds of adenomatous polyps in the entire descending and sigmoid colon. Options for prophylactic surgery are discussed. Which of the following procedures will reduce the occurrence of malignancy the most?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Total colectomy with ileor​ectal anastomosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Total colectomy with ileal​ pouch-anal anastomosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Total proctocolectomy with​ ileal pouch-anal anastom​osis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Total proctocolectomy with​ end ileostomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Total proctocolectomy with ileal pouch-anal anastomosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Total proctocolectomy with ileal pouch-anal anastomosis</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is <strong>diagnostic</strong> of <strong>familial adenomatous polyposis syndrome</strong>. To prevent an occurrence of cancer, <strong>total proctocolectomy with ileal pouch-anal anastomosis </strong>is the management of choice<strong>. </strong>This procedure is usually accompanied by resection of the rectal mucosa.</p>\n<p>Following surgery, the patient has to be routinely followed up for ileoanal-pouch surveillance. Upper GI endoscopy should also be performed to evaluate for the presence of duodenal adenomas.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4394","text":"A 40-year-old man was rece​ntly diagnosed and treate​d for an autosomal domina‍nt disorder. The gross sp​ecimen of his resected co​lon is given in the image‌. What is the initial ste‌p in screening his 9-year‌-old child?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man was recently diagnosed and treated for an autosomal dominant disorder. The gross specimen of his resected colon is given in the image. What is the initial step in screening his 9-year-old child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Genetic testing for APC ge​ne mutation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Genetic testing for MSH-6 ​gene mutation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Routine flexible sigmoidos​copy starting from 10 yea​rs of age","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Routine colonoscopy starti​ng from 10 years of age","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Genetic testing for APC gene mutation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/890d316aa07c4b0da4471dd57359bd06.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Genetic testing for APC gene mutation</strong></p><p>More than 100 colonic polyps are diagnostic of <strong>familial adenomatous polyposis</strong> syndrome and the<strong> initial step in screening</strong> the child syndrome is to perform<strong> genetic testing</strong> for <strong>APC gene mutation. </strong></p>\n<p>If screening is positive, <strong>flexible sigmoidoscopy </strong>or <strong>colonoscopy</strong> is done annually starting from<strong> 10 years </strong>of age.</p>\n<p>If the APC testing is negative, then the risk of developing colorectal carcinoma in the relative is similar to the general population. The screening can be started from 50 years of age.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4399","text":"Which among the following ​is true about Amsterdam c​riteria?","raw_text":"Which among the following is true about Amsterdam criteria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"At least 3 successive gene​rations affected","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"At least 2 or more first-d​egree relatives affected","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"At least one case diagnose​d before 50 years of age","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"All of t​he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. At least one case diagnosed before 50 years of age","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. At least one case diagnosed before 50 years of age</strong></p><p>According to the <strong>Amsterdam criteria </strong>for hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (HNPCC)<strong>,</strong> <strong>at least one case </strong>should be <strong>diagnosed before 50 </strong>years of age.</p>\n<p>Option A: According to the <strong>Amsterdam criteria </strong>for a presumptive diagnosis of HNPCC, at least <strong>2</strong> (not 3) successive<strong> generations</strong> should be affected.</p>\n<p>Option B: According to the <strong>Amsterdam criteria </strong>for a presumptive diagnosis of HNPCC, at least <strong>3</strong> relatives (not 2) with at least one being <strong>first-degree relatives</strong> should be affected.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4397","text":"What is the most common ex​tra-colonic cancer in pat​ients with a mutation in ‍MMR genes?","raw_text":"What is the most common extra-colonic cancer in patients with a mutation in MMR genes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastric ​carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Breast c​arcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endometria​l carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ovarian ​carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Endometrial carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/608ccf9b15cd4c5f9161cfda0bb34095x1280x2239.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Endometrial carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Endometrial carcinoma</strong> is the most common extracolonic carcinoma in patients with<strong> mutated MMR genes.</strong></p>\n<p><strong><strong>Autosomal dominant</strong> </strong>mutation in <strong><strong>DNA mismatch repair (<em>MMR</em>) genes, </strong></strong>such as<strong><strong> <em>hMLH1</em>, <em>hMSH2</em></strong>, <em>hMSH6</em>, <em>PMS1</em>, and <em>PMS2 </em></strong>lead to various cancers. This condition is known as <strong>Lynch Syndrome/HNPCC.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Gastric </strong>and<strong> ovarian carcinoma </strong>are also commonly found. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4396","text":"Which among the following ​is false about colorectal​ carcinoma in HNPCC?","raw_text":"Which among the following is false about colorectal carcinoma in HNPCC?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The onset of cancer occurs​ early","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The progression of the car​cinoma is slow","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mucinous adenocarcinoma is​ the most common type","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tumors predominantly devel​op in right sided colon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The progression of the carcinoma is slow","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5c467f1c1c0740008a03832fdb72bce7x1280x1675.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The progression of the carcinoma is slow</strong></p><p>In hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (<strong>HNPCC)</strong>, there is <strong>accelerated tumor progression </strong>and normal tumor initiation.</p>\n<p><strong>Accelerated tumor initiation</strong> is seen in familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP).</p>\n<p>In Hereditary Non-polyposis Colorectal Cancer (HNPCC), the onset of cancer is at a <strong>relatively young age </strong>(mean, 44 years). Proximal distribution is more common and nearly 70% of cancers are located in the <strong>right colon</strong>. <strong>Mucinous </strong>or<strong> poorly differentiated </strong>(signet cell)<strong> adenocarcinoma </strong>predominate. There is an increased number of <strong>synchronous </strong>and<strong> metachronous cancers. </strong>The <strong>outcome after surgery </strong>is<strong> </strong>relatively <strong>good. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4398","text":"A 25-year-old man presente​d to the dermatologist's ​office with multiple smal‍l bumps on his forehead. ​Excisional biopsy finding​s are suggestive of multi‌ple small sebaceous adeno‌mas. Detailed family hist‌ory reveals that his gran‍dfather and brother have ‌colon cancer while his au‍nt was diagnosed with rec‍tal cancer. Which syndrom‌e should be suspected?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man presented to the dermatologist's office with multiple small bumps on his forehead. Excisional biopsy findings are suggestive of multiple small sebaceous adenomas. Detailed family history reveals that his grandfather and brother have colon cancer while his aunt was diagnosed with rectal cancer. Which syndrome should be suspected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gardner’s​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cowden's​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Muir-Torr​e syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Turcot's​ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Muir-Torre syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Muir-Torre syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Multiple cutaneous sebaceous adenomas</strong> and a family history of various cancers are seen in <strong>Muir–Torre syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Muir–Torre variant of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (HNPCC) may have:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sebaceous adenomas</li>\n<li>Keratoacanthomas</li>\n<li>Sebaceous epitheliomas</li>\n<li>Basal cell epitheliomas</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4400","text":"Which criteria is used to ​select patients for micro​satellite instability (MS‍I) testing?","raw_text":"Which criteria is used to select patients for microsatellite instability (MSI) testing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amsterdam​ criteria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Revised Amsterdam criteria​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bethesda​ criteria","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lynch c​riteria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bethesda criteria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bethesda criteria</strong></p><p><strong>Bethesda criteria</strong> is used to <strong>select patients for microsatellite instability testing</strong>. </p>\n<p>Microsatellites are regions of repeated DNA that change in length (show instability) when mismatch repair is not functioning. Defects in mismatch repair genes (as in Lynch syndrome/HNPCC) leads to <strong>microsatellite instability</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong><span class=\"topic-highlight\">Amsterdam criteria</span></strong> is used for a <strong>presumptive diagnosis</strong> of <strong>HNPCC in a family</strong>. Because the Amsterdam criteria do not include some patients with known germline MMR gene mutations, another set of guidelines, known as the <strong>Bethesda guidelines</strong>, was established to <strong>help decide</strong> whether or not a patient should undergo further <strong>molecular testing</strong> to rule out HNPCC.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4404","text":"In which part of the colon​ does colorectal carcinom​a most commonly develop?","raw_text":"In which part of the colon does colorectal carcinoma most commonly develop?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ce​cum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sigmoi​d colon","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transver​se colon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right​ colon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sigmoid colon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/128b95702668473a93a116fb19801ce0x1279x1150.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sigmoid colon</strong></p><p>In the colon, colorectal carcinoma most commonly develops in the <strong>sigmoid colon</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Overall</strong>, the <strong>most common site</strong> of <strong>colorectal carcinoma</strong> is the <strong>rectum</strong>.</p>\n<p>The sites of development of colorectal carcinoma in order of frequency are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rectum </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Sigmoid colon</strong></li>\n<li>Caecum</li>\n<li>Transverse colon</li>\n<li>Ascending colon</li>\n<li>Descending colon</li>\n<li>Splenic flexure</li>\n<li>Hepatic flexure</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4402","text":"Which of the following are​ risk factors for colon c​arcinoma? Ulcerative coli‍tis Cholecystectomy Smoki​ng Ureterosigmoidostomy P​revious colonic surgery","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are risk factors for colon carcinoma?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ulcerative colitis</li>\n<li>Cholecystectomy</li>\n<li>Smoking </li>\n<li>Ureterosigmoidostomy </li>\n<li>Previous colonic surgery </li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2,​4,5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,​3,5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,​2,3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,2​,3,4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1,2,3,4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1,2,3,4</strong></p><p>Ulcerative colitis, familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP), cholecystectomy, ureterosigmoidostomy, and smoking are risk factors for colon cancer.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4401","text":"Which of the following die​tary recommendation puts ​patients at risk of devel‍oping colon cancer?","raw_text":"Which of the following dietary recommendation puts patients at risk of developing colon cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High-cal​orie diet","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Low-anima​l-fat diet","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sele​nium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vita​min A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. High-calorie diet","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. High-calorie diet</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 815\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>High-calorie, low-fiber, </strong>and<strong> high-animal-fat diet </strong>as well as <strong>alcohol consumption increase </strong>the<strong> risk</strong> of colon cancer.</p>\n<p><strong>Calcium, selenium, vitamins A, C, </strong>and<strong> E, carotenoids, </strong>and<strong> plant phenols </strong>are <strong>protective.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3635","text":"Which of the following col​onoscopic findings has th​e highest risk of develop‍ing colorectal cancer?","raw_text":"Which of the following colonoscopic findings has the highest risk of developing colorectal cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A single pedunculated poly​p","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple f​lat polyps","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Multiple pedunculated poly​ps","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A solitary flat rectal pol​yp","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Multiple flat polyps","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Multiple flat polyps</strong></p><p><strong>Sessile/flat polyps</strong> are at a <strong>higher risk</strong> of malignancy compared to pedunculated polyps. <strong>Multiple polyps increase the risk</strong> of malignancy. </p>\n<p>The probability of development of malignancy of a polyp depends on the following<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gross appearance of lesion: </strong>Sessile polyps are at higher risk for malignancy than pedunculated tumors.</li>\n<li><strong>Histology: </strong>Villous &gt; Tubulovillous &gt; tubular</li>\n<li><strong>Size: </strong>Polyps of size &gt;2 cm are at higher risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4403","text":"Mutations in which gene is​ responsible for malignan​t changes in late colonic‍ adenomas?","raw_text":"Mutations in which gene is responsible for malignant changes in late colonic adenomas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A​PC","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"K-​ras","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"p​53","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"D​CC","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. p53","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. p53</strong></p><p><strong><em>p53</em></strong> mutation is responsible for the transformation of late adenoma to carcinoma.</p>\n<p>The <strong>earliest mutations</strong> in the adenoma-carcinoma sequence occur in the <strong><em>APC</em> gene </strong>and the <strong>earliest phenotypic change</strong> is known as <strong>aberrant crypt formation.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>The most consistent genetic aberrations</strong> within these cells abnormally result in the formation of short pro­teins known as <strong>APC truncations</strong>.<strong> </strong>They<em> </em>occur in the <strong>mutational cluster region</strong> of the gene, an area responsible <strong>for β-catenin binding.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4296","text":"Which of the following is ​the correctly matched set​ regarding risk category ‍and colon cancer screenin​g recommendation? Risk ca​tegory Colonoscopy screen‌ing 1. Patients at averag‌e risk A. every 3-5 yearl‌y, starting at age 35 yrs‍ 2. Lynch syndrome B. eve‌ry 1-2 yearly, starting 8‍ years after diagnosis 3.‍ Inflammatory bowel disea‌se C. every 10 years, sta‌rting at age 50 yrs 4. Fa​ther diagnosed with color​ectal cancer at age 45 ye​ars D. every 1-2 years, s​tarting at age 20-25 year‍s","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is the correctly matched set regarding risk category and colon cancer screening recommendation?</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk category</td>\n<td>Colonoscopy screening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>1. Patients at average risk</p>\n</td>\n<td>A. every 3-5 yearly, starting at age 35 yrs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>2. Lynch syndrome</p>\n</td>\n<td>B. every 1-2 yearly, starting 8 years after diagnosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>3. Inflammatory bowel disease</p>\n</td>\n<td>C. every 10 years, starting at age 50 yrs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>4. Father diagnosed with colorectal cancer at age 45 years</p>\n</td>\n<td>D. every 1-2 years, starting at age 20-25 years</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-C, 2-D,​ 3-B, 4-A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1-D, 2-A,​ 3-C, 4-B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-C, 2-B,​ 3-A, 4-D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-B, 2-D,​ 3-A, 4-C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1-C, 2-D, 3-B, 4-A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1-C, 2-D, 3-B, 4-A</strong></p><p>Option A states the correctly matched pairs regarding risk category and colon cancer screening recommendation.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 482px; width: 575px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 565.8px;\">Current recommendation on colon cancer screening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 349.533px;\">Risk category</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210.667px;\">Recommendation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 133.55px;\">Patients at average risk</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210.383px;\">General population</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210.667px;\">Colonoscopy <strong>every 10 years</strong> between <strong>50 -75 years</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 133.55px;\">Patients at higher risk</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210.383px;\">Diagnosis of colon cancer or adenomatous polyps in\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Any first-degree relative at age &lt;60 years</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">≥2 first-degree relatives at age ≥60 years</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210.667px;\">Colonoscopy every 3-5 years starting at <strong>whichever comes first</strong>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">age 40 or </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">at least 10 years before the relative’s age of diagnosis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.383px;\">Inflammatory bowel disease (Crohn's disease or ulcerative colitis)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210.667px;\">Colonoscopy every 1-2 years starting 8 years after diagnosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.383px;\"><strong>Familial adenomatous polyposis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 210.667px;\">Colonoscopy <strong>every year</strong> starting at the age of <strong>10-12 years</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 210.383px;\">Lynch syndrome (HNPCC)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210.667px;\">Colonoscopy every 1-2 years starting at 20-25 years</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4407","text":"A 61-year-old man presents​ with complaints of fatig​ue and shortness of breat‍h. He has conjunctival pa​llor and has lost signifi​cant weight over 3 months‌. A CBC reveals low haemo‌globin and MCV. A stool o‌ccult blood test is posit‍ive. What is the next bes‌t step in evaluation?","raw_text":"A 61-year-old man presents with complaints of fatigue and shortness of breath. He has conjunctival pallor and has lost significant weight over 3 months. A CBC reveals low haemoglobin and MCV. A stool occult blood test is positive. What is the next best step in evaluation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Barium​ enema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Colon​oscopy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"M​RI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CT ​scan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Colonoscopy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Colonoscopy</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario of an elderly patient with sudden onset anemia always warrants screening for colorectal cancer, as cancers in the <strong>right colon </strong>often manifest as <strong>melena </strong>and<strong> fatigue associated with anemia. </strong>Thus, the next best investigation is a <strong>colonoscopy.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Colonoscopy</strong> is the investigation of choice &amp; gold standard for establishing the diag­nosis of colon cancer.</p>\n<p>It permits biopsy of the tumor to verify the diagnosis while allowing inspection of the entire colon to exclude metachronous polyps or cancers.</p>\n<p>Colonoscopy is generally performed even after cancer is detected by<strong> barium enema</strong>, in order to obtain a biopsy specimen and to detect (and remove) small polyps that may have been missed in the contrast study.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4405","text":"A 55-year-old woman presen​ts with abdominal pain an​d constipation. A barium ‍enema reveals an apple co​re sign. In which part of​ the colon is such a grow‌th commonly seen?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old woman presents with abdominal pain and constipation. A barium enema reveals an apple core sign. In which part of the colon is such a growth commonly seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left ​colon","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Right​ colon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transver​se colon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cae​cum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Left colon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ae7541322ef44b599425886cb36312ffx1108x922.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Left colon</strong></p><p><strong>Apple core sign </strong>on barium enema is suggestive of a <strong>constricting type</strong> of colon cancer that is more common on the <strong>left side</strong>. <strong>Ulcerative type</strong> is more common on the <strong>right side.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Most patients with<strong> left-</strong>sided colon cancers often present with <strong>increasing constipation </strong>while patients with <strong>right-</strong>sided colon cancer often present with <strong>melena and fatigue associated with anemia.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>apple core sign</strong> describes a short segment irregular circumferential stricture of the large bowel that has abrupt shouldered margins resembling an apple core. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4406","text":"What is the most common pr​esenting feature in patie​nts with right sided colo‍n cancer ?","raw_text":"What is the most common presenting feature in patients with right sided colon cancer ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diar​rhoea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ane​mia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Consti​pation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abdomin​al pain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anemia</strong></p><p><strong>Anemia</strong> is the most common presenting feature in patients with <strong>right-sided</strong> colon cancer.</p>\n<p>Cancers in the <strong>right colon</strong> are more often manifested as <strong>melena, fatigue </strong>associated with<strong> anemia,</strong> or<strong> abdominal pain </strong>(if the tumor is advanced).</p>\n<p>Cancer of the<strong> left colon </strong>mainly presents with<strong> altered bowel habits, </strong>usually<strong> constipation </strong>and <strong>intestinal obstruction.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4408","text":"Which among the following ​statements is false about​ virtual colonoscopy as c‍ompared to conventional c​olonoscopy?","raw_text":"Which among the following statements is false about virtual colonoscopy as compared to conventional colonoscopy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Virtual colonoscopy is les​s sensitive for small pol​yps","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Provids a better image tha​n conventional colonoscop​y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Easier to take tissue biop​sy during virtual colonos​copy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Less time consuming than c​onventional colonoscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Easier to take tissue biopsy during virtual colonoscopy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Easier to take tissue biopsy during virtual colonoscopy</strong></p><p><strong>Virtual colonoscopy</strong> is a <strong>non-invasive technique</strong> and is <strong>not useful in taking biopsy</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4409","text":"A 50-year-old female prese​nted with low-grade fever​, easy fatiguability, and‍ pain in the right hypoch​ondrium. On examination, ​pallor and moderate hepat‌omegaly were present. A C‌T scan revealed non-enhan‌cing masses. The biopsy s‍howed adenocarcinoma, not‌ of hepatic origin. A com‍bination of IHC tests was‍ done and was reported as‌ CK 7 (-), CK20 (+). Whic‌h of the following is the​ most likely primary site​ of origin?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old female presented with low-grade fever, easy fatiguability, and pain in the right hypochondrium. On examination, pallor and moderate hepatomegaly were present. A CT scan revealed non-enhancing masses. The biopsy showed adenocarcinoma, not of hepatic origin. A combination of IHC tests was done and was reported as CK 7 (-), CK20 (+). Which of the following is the most likely primary site of origin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ov​ary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Co​lon","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Panc​reas","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lu​ng","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Colon","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/51267f2f2b3d4cad857b887f855081a0.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Colon</strong></p><p><strong>Colorectal carcinoma</strong> is the <strong>most likely primary tumor</strong> for the given metastases. Among the given options, <strong>cytokeratin 7 </strong>is<strong> negative </strong>only in <strong>colon carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>most common site of distant metastasis</strong> from colorectal cancer is the <strong>liver. </strong>These metastases arise from <strong>hematogenous spread via the portal venous system</strong>.The lung is the next most common site for metastasis.</p>\n<p>Cytokeratin profiles of various primary tumors:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 520px; width: 294px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<p>CK 7 positive</p>\n<p>CK20 positive</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Cholangiocarcinoma</li>\n<li>Ovarian mucinous carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\">\n<p>CK 7 <strong>positive </strong></p>\n<p>CK 20 negative</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Breast carcinoma</strong></li>\n<li>Ovarian serous adenocarcinoma</li>\n<li><strong>Thyroid carcinoma</strong></li>\n<li>Pancreatic adenocarcinoma</li>\n<li><strong>Lung carcinoma</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\">\n<p>CK 7 <strong>negative</strong></p>\n<p>CK 20 <strong>positive</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Colorectal adenocarcinoma</strong></li>\n<li>Merkel cell carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\">\n<p>CK 7 negative</p>\n<p>CK 20 negative</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Hepatocellular carcinoma</li>\n<li>Renal cell carcinoma</li>\n<li>Prostatic adenocarcinoma</li>\n<li>Squamous cell carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4410","text":"What is the most important​ prognostic factor in pat​ients with colorectal car‍cinoma?","raw_text":"What is the most important prognostic factor in patients with colorectal carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Site of​ lesion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Size of​ lesion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stage o​f lesion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Age of ​patient","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stage of lesion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stage of lesion</strong></p><p>The most important determinant of prognosis for colorectal carcinoma is the <strong>tumour stage</strong> and, in particular, <strong>lymph node status</strong>. The T-stage (depth of invasion) is the single most significant predictor of lymph node spread.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4411","text":"Choose the parameters used​ to stage colorectal carc​inoma according to Duke's‍ classification ? 1. Lymp​h node involvement 2. Sit​e of the tumor 3. Size of‌ the tumor 4. Depth of th‌e tumor 5. Age of the pat‌ient","raw_text":"<p>Choose the parameters used to stage colorectal carcinoma according to Duke's classification ?</p>\n<p>1. Lymph node involvement</p>\n<p>2. Site of the tumor</p>\n<p>3. Size of the tumor </p>\n<p>4. Depth of the tumor</p>\n<p>5. Age of the patient</p>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ​3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3,​ 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,​ 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2​, 3,4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/406f1131dcf840579c3aed85987110bfx1280x1193.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 4</strong></p><p>Duke’s classification is based on the depth of tumor and lymph node status.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5942","text":"The biopsy of a hemicolect​omy specimen revealed an ​adenocarcinoma with colon‍ic subserosa invasion. De​posits were found on the ​mesentery but lymph nodes‌ were not involved. Furth‌er investigation on the p‌atient did not indicate l‍ocal or distant metastase‌s. What is the stage of t‍he tumor ?","raw_text":"The biopsy of a hemicolectomy specimen revealed an adenocarcinoma with colonic subserosa invasion. Deposits were found on the mesentery but lymph nodes were not involved. Further investigation on the patient did not indicate local or distant metastases. What is the stage of the tumor ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T4b N​0 M1c","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T4a N​2b M0","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T3 N​1c M0","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"T3 N​0 M1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. T3 N1c M0","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. T3 N1c M0</strong></p><p>According to 8th American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) classification of colorectal cancers, <strong>tumor deposits in the subserosa or mesentery</strong> are staged as <strong>N1c</strong> and <strong>tumor invading the subserosa without involvement of peritonealized organs</strong> is <strong>T3</strong>. </p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 408px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">TNM staging</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.926px;\">7th AJCC (2010)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.074px;\"><strong>8th AJCC (2018)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">Primary tumor(pT)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.926px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.074px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">TX</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.926px;\">Cannot be assessed</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.074px;\">-do-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">T0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.926px;\">No evidence of cancer</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.074px;\">-do-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">Tis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.926px;\">Carcinoma in situ</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.074px;\">Carcinoma in situ, <strong>intramucosal carcinoma</strong> (involvement of lamina propria with no extension through muscularis mucosae)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">T1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.926px;\">invades submucosa</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.074px;\">-do-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">T2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.926px;\">invades muscularis propria</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.074px;\">-do-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">T3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.926px;\">invades muscularis propria into subserosa or into nonperitonealized pericolic or perirectal tissues</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.074px;\">-do-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">T4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.926px;\">invades other organs or tissues or perforates the visceral peritoneum of specimen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.074px;\">\n<p><strong>T4a: </strong>invades through visceral peritoneum (including gross perforation of the bowel and invasion of tumor through areas of inflammation to the surface of the visceral peritoneum)</p>\n<p><strong>T4b: </strong>invades or adheres to other adjacent organs or structures<br/><br/></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">Regional lymph nodes (pN)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">NX</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.926px;\">Regional lymph nodes cannot be assessed</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.074px;\">-do-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">N0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.926px;\">No lymph node metastasis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.074px;\">-do-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">N1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.926px;\">1-3 pericolic or perirectal lymph nodes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.074px;\">\n<p><strong>N1a:</strong> 1 regional lymph node</p>\n<p><strong>N1b:</strong> 2 - 3 regional lymph nodes</p>\n<p><strong>N1c:</strong> no regional lymph nodes are positive but there are <strong>tumor deposits</strong> in the subserosa, mesentery or nonperitonealized pericolic or perirectal / mesorectal tissues</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">N2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.926px;\">Metastasis to &gt; 4 pericolic or perirectal lymph nodes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.074px;\">\n<p><strong>N2a:</strong> 4 - 6 regional lymph nodes</p>\n<p><strong>N2b:</strong>  <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">&gt;</span>7 regional lymph nodes<br/><br/></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">Distant metastasis (pM)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">M0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.926px;\">No Distant metastasis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.074px;\">-do-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 76px;\">M1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.926px;\">Distant metastasis present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.074px;\">\n<p><strong>M1a</strong>: confined to 1 organ or site without peritoneal metastasis</p>\n<p><strong>M1b:</strong> metastasis to <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">&gt;</span>2 sites or organs is identified without peritoneal metastasis</p>\n<p><strong>M1c:</strong> metastasis to the peritoneal surface is identified alone or with other site or organ metastases</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4412","text":"Match the following patien​ts with colon cancer to t​he procedure that is best‍ suited. Patient Procedur​e 1. A frail 82-year-old ​patient with the inabilit‌y to pass stool and flatu‌s A.Hartmann's Procedure ‌2. A 56-year-old patient ‍with stable vitals and a ‌distended bowel with incr‍eased, high-pitched bowel‍ sounds. B.Stent placemen‌t via a colonoscope 3. A ‌63-year-old patient with ​vomiting and a rigid, ten​der abdomen. An X-Ray sho​ws a single level obstruc​tion C.Resection and prim‍ary anastomosis","raw_text":"<p>Match the following patients with colon cancer to the procedure that is best suited.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 171px;\" width=\"361\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\">Patient</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\">Procedure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\">1. A frail 82-year-old patient with the inability to pass stool and flatus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\">A.Hartmann's Procedure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\">2. A 56-year-old patient with stable vitals and a distended bowel with increased, high-pitched bowel sounds.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\">B.Stent placement via a colonoscope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\">3. A 63-year-old patient with vomiting and a rigid, tender abdomen. An X-Ray shows a single level obstruction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\">C.Resection and primary anastomosis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-A, 2​-B, 3-C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-C, 2​-A, 3-B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-B, 2​-A, 3-C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-B, 2​-C, 3-A","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1-B, 2-C, 3-A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1-B, 2-C, 3-A</strong></p><p>All of the given procedures may be performed in a patient with colonic cancer presenting with features of left colonic obstruction. The most appropriate answer is 1-B,2-C,3-A.</p>\n<p>In<strong> Hartmann's procedure, </strong>the segment of the colon containing the obstructing cancer is resected and <strong>closure of the distal sigmoid or rectum</strong> is performed. A <strong>colostomy </strong>is made. <strong>Later,</strong> intestinal continuity is re-established by the closure of the colos­tomy and fashioning a <strong>colorectal anastomosis. </strong></p>\n<p>This procedure is preferred in patients when a resection and primary anastomosis cannot be performed immediately due to complications such as <strong>inflammation of the bowel</strong>, <strong>perforation</strong>, or <strong>peritonitis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Resection and primary anastomosis </strong>is done in patients who are<strong> hemody­namically stable </strong>and have <strong>pliable, nonedematous, well-vascularized bowel.</strong> </p>\n<p>In this procedure, the segment of the left colon containing the cancer is resected. The remain­ing colon is cleaned with saline lavage by inserting a catheter through the appendix or ileum into the cecum and irrigating the contents from the colon. Primary anastomosis between the prepared colon and rectum is fashioned subsequently, without the need for a temporary colostomy </p>\n<p><strong>Stent placement via colonoscope</strong> is a temporary measure to obtain time for bowel preparation for primary anastomosis. A stent that traverses the tumor is placed to relieve the obstruction. It is done in <strong>partial obstruction</strong> and is preferred in <strong>old</strong> and <strong>frail</strong> patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4410","text":"A 60-year-old man was rush​ed to the ER with abdomin​al pain, obstipation, and‍ altered mental status fo​r the last 2 days. Physic​al examination and imagin‌g findings are suggestive‌ of perforated intestinal‌ obstruction with periton‍itis. After stabilization‌, he is immediately taken‍ to the operating room, w‍here a suspicious ascendi‌ng colon mass is seen. Wh‌ich of the following is t​he most appropriate manag​ement for this patient?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man was rushed to the ER with abdominal pain, obstipation, and altered mental status for the last 2 days. Physical examination and imaging findings are suggestive of perforated intestinal obstruction with peritonitis. After stabilization, he is immediately taken to the operating room, where a suspicious ascending colon mass is seen. Which of the following is the most appropriate management for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right hemicolectomy with i​leocolostomy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Resection an​d anastomosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hartmann’s​ procedure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endoluminal stent placemen​t","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Right hemicolectomy with ileocolostomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Right hemicolectomy with ileocolostomy</strong></p><p>The elderly man with ascending colon mass leading to <strong>perforated intestinal obstruction</strong> with <strong>peritonitis</strong> should ideally be treated with <strong>right hemicolectomy and ileocolostomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>In case of suspected colorectal cancer presenting with intestinal obstruction the following surgeries are done:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Right-sided lesion:</strong> </li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A <strong>resection</strong> (right hemicolectomy) and <strong>anastomosis</strong> if the patient is <strong>stable</strong> </li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Perforation</strong> with substantial contamination or if the patient is <strong>unstable</strong>: <strong>right hemicolectomy + ileocolostomy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Left-sided lesion:</strong> </li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Resection</strong> and <strong>anastomosis</strong> if the patient is <strong>stable</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Perforation</strong> with substantial contamination or if the patient is <strong>unstable</strong>: <strong>Hartmann procedure</strong> is done. Later, intestinal continuity is re-established by the closure of the colos­tomy and fashioning a <strong>colorectal anastomosis. </strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Where endoscopic and radiological facilities are present an <strong>obstructing left-sided lesion</strong> can be treated with an expanding metal <strong>stent</strong> provided bowel <strong>perforation</strong> is <strong>absent</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4409","text":"A 45-year-old man is diagn​osed with stage II colore​ctal carcinoma located in‍ the transverse colon. Wh​ich of the following is t​he surgical procedure of ‌choice for this patient?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man is diagnosed with stage II colorectal carcinoma located in the transverse colon. Which of the following is the surgical procedure of choice for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right hem​icolectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transverse​ colectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extended right hemicolecto​my","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Left hemi​colectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Extended right hemicolectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9727f7dd42e145a7bb79c3c4dc1c2387x1280x1567.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6d6da8b022334e1987d7dbb2cfbf3078x1280x1567.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Extended right hemicolectomy</strong></p><p><strong>Extended right hemicolectomy</strong> is the most appropriate surgical procedure for a neoplastic tumor in the transverse colon. This procedure involves resection of the </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Terminal ileum with Ileocecal junction</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cecum</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ascending colon</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hepatic flexure, and</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Distal transverse colon</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Very <strong>small and localized lesions</strong> in the transverse colon may be resected by <strong>transverse colectomy</strong>. However, this procedure is very<strong> uncommon</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 931.717px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Tumor location</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241px;\">Procedure of choice</td>\n<td style=\"width: 482.717px;\">Resected bowel segments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Cecum / Ascending colon / Hepatic flexure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241px;\">Right hemicolectomy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 482.717px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Terminal ileum with Ileocecal junction</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cecum</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ascending colon</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hepatic flexure</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Transverse colon / Hepatic flexure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241px;\">Extended right hemicolectomy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 482.717px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Terminal ileum with Ileocecal junction</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cecum</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ascending colon</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hepatic flexure</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Distal transverse colon </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Splenic flexure may be resected based on the tumor location</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Descending colon ± sigmoid colon</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241px;\">Left hemicolectomy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 482.717px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Transverse colon(left MCA territory)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Splenic flexure</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Descending colon upto sigmoid colon</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Sigmoid colon</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241px;\">Sigmoid colectomy, or LAR (Low anterior resection)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 482.717px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Entire sigmoid colon and rectum is removed</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Coloanal anastomosis is performed</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2434","text":"A male patient presented w​ith dull abdominal pain a​nd rectal bleeding. On in‍vestigations, he was diag​nosed with colon carcinom​a. Which of the following‌ statements is false rega‌rding the management of c‌olorectal carcinoma?","raw_text":"A male patient presented with dull abdominal pain and rectal bleeding. On investigations, he was diagnosed with colon carcinoma. Which of the following statements is false regarding the management of colorectal carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stage III cancer is treate​d with surgical resection​ and chemotherapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stage II cancer with young​ age is an indication for​ chemotherapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"FOLFOX regimen is used for​ colorectal carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Immunotherapy is ineffecti​ve in metastatic colorect​al carcinoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Immunotherapy is ineffective in metastatic colorectal carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Immunotherapy is ineffective in metastatic colorectal carcinoma</strong></p><p>Immunotherapy is not ineffective in metastatic colorectal cancer. <strong>Metastatic</strong> or<strong> recurrent colorectal cancers</strong> are the <strong>indications</strong> for <strong>immunotherapy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Monoclonal antibodies such as</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bevacizumab</strong> (Anti-VEGF),</li>\n<li><strong>Cetuximab</strong> (anti-EGFR),</li>\n<li><strong>Panitumumab</strong> (anti-EGFR),</li>\n<li><strong>Pembrolizumab</strong> (anti-PDL1),</li>\n<li><strong>Nivolumab</strong> (anti-PDL), etc. are the commonly used immunotherapeutic drugs in colorectal carcinoma.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Surgical resection is indicated in all the stages of colon carcinoma. Management of Stage III colorectal carcinoma should also include chemotherapy, in addition to surgical resection. The stage-specific therapies are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 77px; width: 820.633px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 108px; height: 18px;\">Stage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 696.633px; height: 18px;\">Management</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 108px; height: 46px;\"><strong>Stage I</strong>:</td>\n<td style=\"width: 696.633px; height: 46px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Surgical resection</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 72px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 108px; height: 72px;\"><strong>Stage II</strong>:</td>\n<td style=\"width: 696.633px; height: 72px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Surgical resection</li>\n<li><strong>Adjuvant chemotherapy </strong>in<strong> high-risk </strong>patients (presence of <strong>poor prognostic factors</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 108px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Stage III</strong>:</td>\n<td style=\"width: 696.633px; height: 18px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Surgical resection</li>\n<li><strong>Adjuvant chemotherapy</strong> (folinic acid, 5-fluorouracil, oxaliplatin)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 108px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Stage IV</strong>:</td>\n<td style=\"width: 696.633px; height: 18px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Surgical resection with adjuvant chemotherapy</li>\n<li>Radiotherapy</li>\n<li>Immunotherapy (monoclonal antibodies)</li>\n<li>Palliative treatment in extensive metastasis. </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option B: Stage II cancer in young patients should be considered for chemotherapy. Treatment of stage II colon carcinoma is surgical resection of the cancerous mass. <strong>Adjuvant chemotherapy</strong> is indicated in patients with at least one <strong>poor prognostic indicator</strong> like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Young patients</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Inadequate lymph node excision</strong>(&lt;12 lymph nodes)</li>\n<li><strong>Poorly differentiated histology</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Positive margin</strong> of resection</li>\n<li>T4 lesions</li>\n<li>Bowel obstruction or perforation</li>\n<li>Presence of perineural or lymphovascular invasion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option C: <strong>FOLFOX</strong> is a chemotherapy regimen used for the treatment of colorectal cancer, made up of the drugs <strong>folinic acid</strong>, <strong>5-fluorouracil</strong>, and <strong>oxaliplatin.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4304","text":"Which of the following is ​a false statement regardi​ng different types of art‍ificial stoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement regarding different types of artificial stoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Output from an ileostomy i​s liquid in nature","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"In Hartmann procedure, a l​oop colostomy is formed","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Skin excoriation is the mo​st common complication of​ ileostomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parastomal herniation is m​ore common in Loop colost​omy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. In Hartmann procedure, a loop colostomy is formed","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a1a30db51c9a43018c96653b260f4b21x1280x3145.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. In Hartmann procedure, a loop colostomy is formed</strong></p><p>Colostomy formed during the Hartmann procedure is not a loop colostomy. It is an example of <strong>end-colostomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Artificial stomas are surgically created openings on the abdominal wall following certain types of surgeries, to serve as an alternate path for internal organs to drain externally. <strong>Ileostomy</strong> and <strong>colostomy</strong> are the two most common types of artificial stomas for the GI system.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ileostomy</td>\n<td>Colostomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Any portion of the entire ileum can be chosen for stoma creation</td>\n<td>Only mobile parts of the colon (<strong>transverse colon</strong> and <strong>sigmoid colon</strong>) can be used</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gross appearance - <strong>margin</strong> of the stoma is <strong>elevated</strong> (pouting) from the skin </td>\n<td>Gross appearance - at the same level as that of skin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The output is <strong>liquid</strong> in nature and therefore higher in volume</td>\n<td>The output is <strong>semi-solid or solid</strong> in nature</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Higher chance of skin excoriation and electrolyte imbalance</td>\n<td>Easier to handle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Based on the structural pattern, stomas can be classified into different types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">End stoma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Loop stoma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Double barrel stoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>End stoma is preferred as a permanent stoma. Loop and double barrel stomas are used as temporary stomas.</p>\n<p>Complications of stoma include</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Skin excoriation</strong> (most common)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Stoma infection</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Diarrhea</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Stoma leakage</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Parastomal herniation</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"e1MQk3yj","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"25_complications_of_anaesthesia","title":"25.Complications Of Anaesthesia","num_questions":29,"total_marks":116,"duration":39,"time_per_question":80.69,"questions":[{"id":"MA1817","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs would you avoid in a p‌atient with a high risk o​f postoperative nausea an‌d vomiting?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs would you avoid in a patient with a high risk of postoperative nausea and vomiting?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dexame‌thasone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nitrou‌s oxide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ondan‌setron","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nitrous oxide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nitrous oxide</strong></p><p><strong>Nitrous oxide</strong> is associated with postoperative nausea and vomiting (PONV). Hence it is avoided in patients at high risk of PONV. </p>\n<p>PONV has an incidence of 20-30% in general surgical population and 70-80% in those with predisposing risk factors.</p>\n<p>Risk factors for PONV:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Patient-specific:</strong> Female gender, non-smoking status, history of motion sickness/PONV</li>\n<li><strong>Anesthetic-specific:</strong> Nitrous oxide, volatile anesthetics, opioids</li>\n<li><strong>Surgery-specific:</strong> <strong>Increased duration of surgery</strong>, type of surgery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Drugs used in the prophylaxis and treatment of PONV:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>5-HT 3 antagonists - <strong>ondansetron</strong>, palonosetron</li>\n<li>Butyrophenones- droperidol</li>\n<li><strong>Dexamethasone</strong></li>\n<li>Transdermal scopolamine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>Propofol</strong> has antiemetic property and thus reduces the risk of PONV.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1814","text":"Which of the following sho‌uld you suspect as the mo‌st common anaphylactic ag​ent during anesthesia?","raw_text":"Which of the following should you suspect as the most common anaphylactic agent during anesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antib‌iotics","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"La‌tex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rocur‌onium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Antibiotics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Antibiotics</strong></p><p><strong>Antibiotics</strong> are the most common anaphylactic agents during anesthesia<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>The most common agents precipitating anaphylactic reactions are antibiotics&gt; neuromuscular blocking agents. <strong>Penicillins</strong> and <strong>cephalosporins</strong> are the <strong>most common</strong> causes of anaphylaxis among <strong>antibiotics</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1815","text":"The use of which of the fo‌llowing drugs is unlikely‌ to trigger an anaphylaxi​s reaction in a patient?","raw_text":"The use of which of the following drugs is unlikely to trigger an anaphylaxis reaction in a patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rocur‌onium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proc‌aine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Furos‌emide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fent‌anyl","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fentanyl","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fentanyl</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The use of <strong>f</strong><strong>entanyl</strong> is unlikely to cause an anaphylactic reaction. True allergic reactions to opioids like fentanyl are very rare. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The most common cause of anaphylactic reactions during anesthesia is antibiotics (especially penicillin and cephalosporin), followed by neuromuscular blockers (<strong>rocuronium</strong>).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Amino ester local anesthetics (<strong>procaine</strong>) exhibit allergic reactions due to the metabolite PABA. Sulfa drugs like sulfonamide antibiotics, <strong>furosemide</strong>, hydrochlorothiazide, and captopril also exhibit allergy in surgical patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1822","text":"A 67-year-old patient unde‌rwent a CABG procedure. H‌e started shivering withi​n 15 minutes of shifting ‌him to the post-operative‍ ward. His temperature wa‍s 35.5-degrees Celcius. W​hich of the following age‍nts can be used to treat ​this patient? Meperidine ​Dexmedetomidine Remifenta​nil Ondansetron Tramadol","raw_text":"<p>A 67-year-old patient underwent a CABG procedure. He started shivering within 15 minutes of shifting him to the post-operative ward. His temperature was 35.5-degrees Celcius. Which of the following agents can be used to treat this patient?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Meperidine</li>\n<li>Dexmedetomidine</li>\n<li>Remifentanil</li>\n<li>Ondansetron</li>\n<li>Tramadol</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, 3 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 o‌nly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, ‌4 and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 4 and 5</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The clinical features are suggestive of <strong>postoperative hypothermia and shivering </strong>in this patient<strong>.</strong> <strong>Remifentanil</strong> is known to cause postoperative shivering when given in high doses and has no role in the treatment of this patient.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Postoperative hypothermia is defined as a <strong>core temperature &lt; 36°C. </strong>Postoperative shivering is usually, but not always, associated with hypothermia. The primary cause of postoperative shivering is perioperative hypothermia. The most efficient way to reduce post-anaesthetic shivering is to prevent hypothermia by <strong>avoiding cold epidural </strong>and<strong> intravenous fluids.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Treatment- </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Meperidine</strong> is the <strong>most effective </strong>treatment for post-anesthetic hypothermia and shivering.</li>\n<li>Opioids(Tramadol), ondansetron, clonidine, physostigmine and ketamine have been shown to be effective in abolishing shivering.</li>\n<li>Intraoperative infusion of <strong>dexmedetomidine</strong> has been shown to be an effective prophylactic.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1819","text":"The most common preventabl‌e cause of anesthetic mis‌haps is:","raw_text":"The most common preventable cause of anesthetic mishaps is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Faulty la‌ryngoscope","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventilator ‌dysfunction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anesthetic machine malfunc‌tion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Human ‌errors","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Human errors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Human errors</strong></p><p>The most common preventable cause of anesthetic mishaps is <strong>human error.</strong></p>\n<p> <strong>Common human errors include:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Unrecognized breathing circuit disconnection</li>\n<li>Mistaken drug administration</li>\n<li>Airway mismanagement</li>\n<li>Anesthesia machine misuse</li>\n<li>Fluid mismanagement</li>\n<li>Intravenous line disconnection</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Equipment malfunctions</strong> that can cause preventable anesthetic accidents are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Breathing circuit malfunction</li>\n<li>Monitoring device malfunction</li>\n<li>Ventilator dysfunction</li>\n<li>Anesthesia machine malfunction</li>\n<li>Faulty laryngoscope</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Unpreventable mishaps include sudden death syndrome and fatal idiosyncratic drug reactions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1919","text":"During the pre-op counsell‌ing of a patient set to u‌ndergo a day care procedu​re, which of the followin‌g would you mention as th‍e most common adverse eff‍ect following daycare ana​esthesia?","raw_text":"During the pre-op counselling of a patient set to undergo a day care procedure, which of the following would you mention as the most common adverse effect following daycare anaesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mya‌lgia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Drows‌iness","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sore ‌throat","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Head‌ache","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Drowsiness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Drowsiness</strong></p><p>The most common adverse effect persisting following day-care anaesthesia is <strong>drowsiness.</strong></p>\n<p>Adverse effects after day-care anesthesia-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drowsiness(most common)</li>\n<li>Sore throat </li>\n<li>Myalgia </li>\n<li>Headache</li>\n<li>Dizziness </li>\n<li>Nausea and vomiting</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cardiovascular adverse effects -</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypotension</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Arrhythmias</li>\n<li>Cardiac arrest</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Respiratory complications -</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypoxemia</li>\n<li>Laryngospasm</li>\n<li>Bronchospasm</li>\n<li>Aspiration</li>\n<li>Pulmonary edema</li>\n<li>Pneumothorax (rare)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0246","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a risk factor for pos‌toperative pulmonary comp​lications?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a risk factor for postoperative pulmonary complications?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BMI &amp;‌gt; 30","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Well contro‌lled asthma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Upper abdom‌inal surgery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Age &amp;gt;‌ 70 years","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Well controlled asthma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Well controlled asthma</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Well-controlled asthma</strong> is not a risk factor for postoperative pulmonary complications.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Risk factors for postoperative pulmonary complications:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Age &gt;70 years</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">COPD (uncontrolled)</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Neck, upper abdominal, Aortic, neurologic surgical procedures</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Prolonged procedure &gt;2 hours</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">General anesthesia with intubation</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">BMI &gt; 30</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Albumin concentration &lt; 35 g/L</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Inability to walk two blocks on level land or one flight of stairs</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1813","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cause of ‌perioperative vision loss​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of perioperative vision loss?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Globe pe‌rforation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Angle closu‌re glaucoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Retinal‌ emboli","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ischemic opt‌ic neuropathy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ischemic optic neuropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ischemic optic neuropathy</strong></p><p><strong>Ischemic optic neuropath</strong>y is the most common cause of perioperative vision loss.</p>\n<p>It is usually seen after <strong>spine</strong> <strong>surgeries</strong> in the prone position, <strong>radical neck dissections</strong> and following <strong>cardiopulmonary bypass.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Symptoms:</strong> Reduced visual acuity, rarely complete blindness; usually presents on awakening from anesthesia.</p>\n<p><strong>Preoperative risk factors:</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diabetes, hypertension</li>\n<li>Smoking</li>\n<li>Coronary artery disease.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Intraoperative risk factors:</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anaemia</li>\n<li>Hypotension</li>\n<li>Prone position</li>\n<li>Prolonged duration of surgery </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1820","text":"You notice your surgery ch‌ief informing the anesthe‌sia junior resident to pl​ace the forearm of the pa‌tient in a supinated posi‍tion so as to avoid a com‍mon peripheral nerve inju​ry. Which nerve injury is‍ he most likely concerned​ about?","raw_text":"You notice your surgery chief informing the anesthesia junior resident to place the forearm of the patient in a supinated position so as to avoid a common peripheral nerve injury. Which nerve injury is he most likely concerned about?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radial‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ulnar‌ nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior interosseous ner‌ve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior interosseous nerv‌e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ulnar nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ulnar nerve</strong></p><p>The surgeon is concerned about injury to the <strong>u</strong><strong>lnar nerve</strong> which can be prevented by positioning the forearm in supination compared to pronation. The ulnar nerve is the <strong>most commonly injured peripheral nerve</strong> following anesthesia.</p>\n<p>Other commonly injured nerves include</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Peroneal nerve</li>\n<li>Brachial plexus</li>\n<li>Common peroneal nerve</li>\n<li>Sciatic nerves</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1904","text":"You have been posted as an‌ army medical officer in ‌a military base. Your bat​talion has suffered injur‌ies from grenade attacks ‍and sustained multiple bu‍rns. Which would be the f​luid of choice for resusc‍itating them in the initi​al 24 hours?","raw_text":"You have been posted as an army medical officer in a military base. Your battalion has suffered injuries from grenade attacks and sustained multiple burns. Which would be the fluid of choice for resuscitating them in the initial 24 hours?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperton‌ic saline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ringer ‌lactate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Alb‌umin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dex‌tran","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ringer lactate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ringer lactate</strong></p><p>The fluid of choice for resuscitation in burns patients is <strong>Ringer lactate</strong> in the first 24 hours followed by albumin and 5% dextrose.</p>\n<p>This is in contrast to blunt and penetrating trauma patients in whom colloids (albumin, hydroxyethyl starch) hypertonic saline, and blood are preferred.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1902","text":"A young adult was injured ‌in a steam boiler explosi‌on at his factory. He was​ rushed to the burns cent‌re with multiple injuries‍ covering 40% of his tota‍l body surface area (TBSA​). He weighs around 70kg.‍ The volume of fluids to ​be administered in the fi​rst 8 hours is _____.","raw_text":"A young adult was injured in a steam boiler explosion at his factory. He was rushed to the burns centre with multiple injuries covering 40% of his total body surface area (TBSA). He weighs around 70kg. The volume of fluids to be administered in the first 8 hours is _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4‌ L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3‌ L","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"7.‌5 L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5.‌6 L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3 L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3 L</strong></p><p>The patient needs to be resuscitated with 3L of fluid (ringer lactate) over the first 8 hours. According to ATLS 10th edition 2018, the resuscitation formula used in thermal burns is <strong>2 mL/kg × % total body surface area (TBSA) over 24 hours.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Half the volume</strong> over the <strong>first 8 hours</strong>: 1 mL/kg × % TBSA<br/><strong>Remaining volume</strong> over the <strong>next 16 hours</strong>: 1 mL/kg × % TBSA</p>\n<p>In the given question for a patient who weighs <strong>70 kg</strong>, with a TBSA of <strong>40%</strong>, <strong>1×70×40= 2800 mL</strong> ~ 3L of fluid will be needed in the first 8 hours.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1847","text":"An adult with burns requir‌es treatment with IV flui‌ds if the percentage of b​urns exceeds","raw_text":"An adult with burns requires treatment with IV fluids if the percentage of burns exceeds","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2‌0%","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1‌5%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2‌5%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 20%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 20%</strong></p><p>IV fluids are usually administered for deep partial and full-thickness burns larger than <strong>20%</strong> TBSA in adults.</p>\n<p><strong>Fluid resuscitation in burns</strong></p>\n<p>According to ATLS 10th edition, 2018, the<strong> resuscitation formula</strong> used in thermal burns is <strong>2 mL/kg × % TBSA.</strong></p>\n<p>First 8 hours: 1 mL/kg × % TBSA (lactated Ringer solution)<br/>Next 16 hours: 1 mL/kg × %TBSA (lactated Ringer solution)</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1903","text":"A 30-year-old sous-chef wa‌s trapped in a restaurant‌ fire due to a gas line l​eak. She was rescued and ‌brought to the casualty b‍y EMS. On examination, 55‍% of her total body surfa​ce area (TBSA) was covere‍d in full-thickness burns​. What is the total volum​e of fluid resuscitation ​required in this patient ‍according to the Parkland‌ formula if she weighs 65​kg?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old sous-chef was trapped in a restaurant fire due to a gas line leak. She was rescued and brought to the casualty by EMS. On examination, 55% of her total body surface area (TBSA) was covered in full-thickness burns. What is the total volume of fluid resuscitation required in this patient according to the Parkland formula if she weighs 65kg?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"22.4 ‌Litres","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"12.5 ‌Litres","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"14.3 ‌Litres","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"15.4 ‌Litres","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 14.3 Litres","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 14.3 Litres</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The patient needs to be resuscitated with <strong>14.3 Litres of fluids</strong>. Fluid resuscitation in a burns patient is estimated using the <strong>Parkland formula.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Parkland's formula:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li>Total percentage body surface area × weight (kg) × 4 = volume (mL).</li>\n<li>55% x 65kg x 4ml = 14.3 Litres of fluids need to be administered in the next 24 hours</li>\n<li>Half of the calculated volume should be administered in the first 8 hours and the remainder over the following 16 hours.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Recent update:</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">According to <strong>ATLS 10th edition</strong>,2018, the resuscitation formula used in thermal burns is <strong>2 mL/kg × % TBSA</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">First 8 hours: 1 mL/kg × % TBSA (lactated Ringer solution)<br/>Next 16 hours: 1 mL/kg × %TBSA (lactated Ringer solution)</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1845","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a colloid solution?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a colloid solution?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasm‌alyte","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gel‌atin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alb‌umin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydroxyet‌hyl starch","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Plasmalyte","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Plasmalyte</strong></p><p><strong>Plasmalyte</strong> is a <strong>crystalloid</strong> solution that is <strong>iso-osmolar (osmolarity 294 mosm/L)</strong> with plasma. It can be used for fluid resuscitation for patients losing blood due to trauma or intraoperative procedures.</p>\n<p><strong>Composition of Plasmalyte:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Na-140 meq/L</li>\n<li>Cl- 98 meq/L</li>\n<li>K- 5 meq/L</li>\n<li>Mg -3 meq/L</li>\n<li>Along with acetate and gluconate</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The colloids are of two types: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blood-derived- <strong>human albumin</strong> and plasma protein fractions</li>\n<li>Semisynthetic- dextran, <strong>gelatin</strong>, <strong>hydroxyethyl starch</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1854","text":"A 23-year-old carpenter se‌vered two of his fingers ‌while working with a powe​r saw. He is brought to t‌he casualty by his friend‍ who had preserved the se‍vered fingers in a small ​box with ice. The hand su‍rgeon takes him up for a ​reconstructive procedure.​ Which fluid does he requ​est the nurse to arrange ‍for the surgery?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old carpenter severed two of his fingers while working with a power saw. He is brought to the casualty by his friend who had preserved the severed fingers in a small box with ice. The hand surgeon takes him up for a reconstructive procedure. Which fluid does he request the nurse to arrange for the surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dextr‌an 40","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dextr‌an 70","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydroxyet‌hyl starch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gel‌atin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dextran 40","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dextran 40</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The surgeon most likely requests for <strong>Dextran 40</strong> (colloid) which is the fluid ideally used in <strong>microvascular surgery</strong>, where its <strong>dilutional effects on blood viscosity</strong> and <strong>anticoagulant effects</strong> favour flow in the microcirculation. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Dextran is a highly branched polysaccharide produced by the bacterium Leuconostoc mesenteroides.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1844","text":"Which of the following sol‌utions is hypotonic?","raw_text":"Which of the following solutions is hypotonic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal‌ saline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5% de‌xtrose","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ringer ‌lactate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3% ‌Nacl","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5% dextrose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5% dextrose</strong></p><p><strong>5% dextrose</strong> solution is <strong>hypotonic </strong>as its osmolarity is <strong>252mosm/kg</strong> which is less than that of plasma (285mosm/kg).</p>\n<p><strong>5% dextrose (D5W solution):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It contains 50g/L glucose </li>\n<li>Soon after infusion, the dextrose is taken up into the cell leaving behind free water</li>\n<li>Used for replacement of <strong>pure water deficits</strong> and as a maintenance fluid for patients on sodium restriction</li>\n<li>The predominant effect of D5W is cellular swelling and hemolysis due to hypotonicity</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Normal saline:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>0.9% NaCl is <strong>isotonic normal saline</strong> but this solution is neither chemically nor physiologically normal</li>\n<li>Contains 9 gram of NaCl per liter</li>\n<li>Due to high chloride concentration, the excessive infusion can lead to <strong>hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ringer lactate:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>aka<strong> Hartman’s solution. </strong></li>\n<li>It is <strong>isotonic</strong> and is the most <strong>physiological solution.</strong></li>\n<li>Should not be used as a diluent fluid for transfusion of PRBCs as it can precipitate and form clots</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hypertonic saline:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>1.8%, 3% and 7.5% NaCl</li>\n<li>Particularly useful in the treatment of hyponatremia, volume resuscitation, and increased ICP.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 285px;\" width=\"675\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.1705px;\"><strong> </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.057px;\"><strong>In mmol/L</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.8494px;\"><strong>Na+</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 23.2955px;\"><strong>K+</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 51.1648px;\"><strong>HCO3-</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 27.1023px;\"><strong>Cl-</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2727px;\"><strong>Ca+</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.395px;\"><strong>Other</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.7273px;\"><strong>osmolality</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.1705px;\"><strong>Plasma</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.057px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.8494px;\">140</td>\n<td style=\"width: 23.2955px;\">4.5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51.1648px;\">26</td>\n<td style=\"width: 27.1023px;\">100</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2727px;\">2.5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.395px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.7273px;\">285-295</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 85.1705px;\"><strong>Crystalloid</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.057px;\"><strong>0.9% saline</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.8494px;\">154</td>\n<td style=\"width: 23.2955px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51.1648px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 27.1023px;\">154</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2727px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.395px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.7273px;\">310</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.057px;\">5% dextrose</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.8494px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 23.2955px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51.1648px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 27.1023px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2727px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.395px;\">50g/L dextrose</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.7273px;\">250</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.057px;\"><strong>Ringer lactate</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.8494px;\">131</td>\n<td style=\"width: 23.2955px;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51.1648px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 27.1023px;\">111</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2727px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.395px;\">29mmol/L Lactate*</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.7273px;\">280</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.057px;\">4%dextrose/0.18% saline</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.8494px;\">30</td>\n<td style=\"width: 23.2955px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51.1648px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 27.1023px;\">30</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2727px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.395px;\">40g/L dextrose</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.7273px;\">262</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 85.1705px;\"><strong>Colloids</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.057px;\">Gelofusine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.8494px;\">154</td>\n<td style=\"width: 23.2955px;\">0.4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51.1648px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 27.1023px;\">120</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2727px;\">0.4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.395px;\">40g/L gelatine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.7273px;\">274</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.057px;\">Haemaccel</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.8494px;\">145</td>\n<td style=\"width: 23.2955px;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51.1648px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 27.1023px;\">145</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2727px;\">6.25</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.395px;\">35 g/L Polygeline</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.7273px;\">301</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.057px;\">HES 6%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.8494px;\">154</td>\n<td style=\"width: 23.2955px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51.1648px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 27.1023px;\">154</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2727px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.395px;\">\n<p>60g/L</p>\n<p>Poly(O-2-hydroxyethyl)starch</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.7273px;\">310</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.057px;\">4.5% albumin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.8494px;\">145</td>\n<td style=\"width: 23.2955px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 51.1648px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 27.1023px;\">154</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2727px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.395px;\">45g/L albumin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.7273px;\">290</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"9\" style=\"width: 665.398px;\">Lactate metabolizes into bicarbonate in the liver</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1841","text":"A 32-year-old man involved‌ in a road traffic accide‌nt is brought to the casu​alty. The patient is blee‌ding and is hypovolemic. ‍Which of the following bl‍ood groups can be used fo​r an emergency transfusio‍n in this patient?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old man involved in a road traffic accident is brought to the casualty. The patient is bleeding and is hypovolemic. Which of the following blood groups can be used for an emergency transfusion in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"O ‌+ve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"O ‌–ve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"AB ‌+ve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AB ‌–ve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. O –ve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. O –ve</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The patient must be given an emergency blood transfusion with <strong>Type</strong> <strong>O Rh-negative blood (universal donor)</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">If the recipient’s blood type and Rh status is not known with certainty and transfusion must be started before determination, type O Rh-negative (universal donor) red cells may be used.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Type O negative blood is preferred because it lacks the A and B and Rh antigens and thus it cannot be hemolyzed by anti-A, anti-B, or anti-D antibodies in the recipient’s blood.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1835","text":"A patient involved in a ro‌ad traffic accident is br‌ought to the casualty. Th​e patient is hypotensive ‌and is transfused with mu‍ltiple units of Ringer la‍ctate followed by 10 unit​s of PRBC's. Within a few‍ hours, he starts bleedin​g from the gums. He also ​has petechiae and ecchymo​ses all over his body. Wh‍at is the most likely cau‌se for this bleeding?","raw_text":"A patient involved in a road traffic accident is brought to the casualty. The patient is hypotensive and is transfused with multiple units of Ringer lactate followed by 10 units of PRBC's. Within a few hours, he starts bleeding from the gums. He also has petechiae and ecchymoses all over his body. What is the most likely cause for this bleeding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ABO incom‌patibility","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rh incomp‌atibility","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dilutional thrombocytopeni‌a","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pre-existin‌g Hemophilia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dilutional thrombocytopenia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dilutional thrombocytopenia</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The most likely cause of bleeding in this patient who has received multiple PRBC transfusions is <strong>dilutional thrombocytopenia.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The most common cause of nonsurgical bleeding following <strong>massive blood transfusion</strong> is dilutional thrombocytopenia. Massive transfusion is most often defined as the need to transfuse <strong>one to two times </strong>the patient’s blood volume. For most adult patients, that is the equivalent of <strong>10–20 units</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Major trauma or blood loss will initiate a cascade of <strong>coagulation abnormalities</strong>, including a consumptive coagulopathy from tissue hypoperfusion as manifested by <strong>increased protein C levels</strong>. This coagulopathy is caused by a combination of factors, of which the most important are the <strong>dilution of coagulation factors </strong>by volume administration e.g., crystalloid, colloid, PRBC, and the duration of hypotension and hypoperfusion.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Concurrent transfusion of packed red cells, plasma, and platelets can be done to avoid dilutional coagulopathy.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1825","text":"A patient needs to undergo‌ a packed red blood cell ‌(PRBC) transfusion due to​ severe anemia. Before ad‌ministration, you must ma‍ke sure the unit of PRBC ‍is at a temperature of___​____.","raw_text":"A patient needs to undergo a packed red blood cell (PRBC) transfusion due to severe anemia. Before administration, you must make sure the unit of PRBC is at a temperature of_______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20‌°C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6‌°C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"24‌°C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"37‌°C","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 37°C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 37°C</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">PRBC transfusion is to be done after warming it to a <strong>temperature of 37°C.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Packed RBCs are <strong>stored at 1-6°C. </strong>Transfusion at this temperature will lead to <strong>profound hypothermia. </strong>If the body temperature decreases to less than 30°C, ventricular irritability and cardiac arrest may occur. Shivering from even mild hypothermia increases metabolic demands and is counterproductive to tissue perfusion, especially in settings where anemia or hypoperfusion is contributing to tissue ischemia.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Hypothermia</strong> together with <strong>low levels of 2,3-diphosphoglycerate</strong> (2,3-DPG) in stored blood can cause a marked <strong>leftward shift of the hemoglobin</strong>–oxygen dissociation curve which can lead to tissue hypoxia.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The ideal method of warming blood is to pass it through plastic coils or cassettes in a warm water bath(<strong>37°C to 38°C</strong>) or warming plates.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1901","text":"Which blood component is a‌ssociated with the maximu‌m risk of bacterial conta​mination?","raw_text":"Which blood component is associated with the maximum risk of bacterial contamination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Packed red ‌blood cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Platelet c‌oncentrates","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fresh fro‌zen plasma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Whole‌ blood","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Platelet concentrates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Platelet concentrates</strong></p><p>The blood component with a <strong>maximum risk </strong>of<strong> bacterial contamination</strong> is <strong>platelet concentrates</strong> as they are stored at room temperature of <strong>20°C-24°C</strong>. Hence, they are at maximum risk of bacterial overgrowth and consequent sepsis.</p>\n<p>With the incidence of 1 in 2000, any fever occurring within 6 hours of a platelet transfusion is likely to be sepsis from the use of stored platelets.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1824","text":"Post-blood transfusion syp‌hilis is most likely rela‌ted to the use of which b​lood product?","raw_text":"Post-blood transfusion syphilis is most likely related to the use of which blood product?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Whole‌ blood","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Packed red cell concentrat‌e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Platelet c‌oncentrate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fresh fro‌zen plasma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Platelet concentrate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Platelet concentrate</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Post blood transfusion syphilis is most likely related to the use of <strong>platelet concentrate.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The causative agent of syphilis, <em>Treponema pallidum</em>, cannot survive in storage at 10 to 16°C. So, it does not survive in most blood components except platelet concentrates, because platelets are stored at room temperature. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1840","text":"In blood component therapy‌, hematocrit of packed re‌d blood cell component is​:","raw_text":"In blood component therapy, hematocrit of packed red blood cell component is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7‌0%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"10‌0%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 70%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b544eb6210544f439a550079f60f891bx800x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 70%</strong></p><p>Packed red blood cell (PRBC) component has a hematocrit of <strong>70%.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Salient points on PRBCs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Obtained after centrifugation of whole blood.</li>\n<li>One unit of whole blood yields 250 mL of packed red cells with a hematocrit of 70%</li>\n<li>Saline preservative is added to this bringing a total volume of 350 mL</li>\n<li>This is stored at 1-6°C.</li>\n<li>Blood with some rare phenotypes can be stored by freezing the packed cells in hypertonic glycerol solution, which makes it available for 10 years.</li>\n<li>Each unit of PRBC is expected to raise hemoglobin by <strong>1 g/dL</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the components of whole blood.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1839","text":"Irradiation of granulocyte‌s before transfusion redu‌ces all of the following ​except","raw_text":"Irradiation of granulocytes before transfusion reduces all of the following except","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Graft versus‌ host disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Febrile ‌reactions","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Re-perfus‌ion injury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute hemoly‌tic reaction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acute hemolytic reaction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acute hemolytic reaction</strong></p><p>Irradiation of granulocytes before transfusion does not reduce <strong>acute hemolytic reaction</strong> as it is due to attack on the transfused donor RBCs by <strong>recipient's antibodies</strong> and complements.</p>\n<p>Similarly, the risk of <strong>bacterial contamination of platelets</strong> and<strong> transfusion-related acute lung injury </strong>(TRALI) has not decreased with leukoreduction.</p>\n<p>Irradiation of granulocytes or use of leukoreduced blood components has several <strong>advantages</strong> over the use of packed red blood cells:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li>Decreased risk of <strong>graft versus host disease</strong>-which is due to engraftment of donor lymphocytes from the transfused blood products leading to an immune reaction against recipient tissues</li>\n<li>Reduces chances of <strong>febrile reactions</strong></li>\n<li>Decreases <strong>re-perfusion injury (myocardium; after heart by-pass)</strong></li>\n<li>Decreases transmission of <strong>cytomegalovirus</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1851","text":"The incidence of anaphylac‌toid reactions is least w‌ith","raw_text":"The incidence of anaphylactoid reactions is least with","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gel‌atin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Haem‌accel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydroxyet‌hyl starch","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dex‌tran","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hydroxyethyl starch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hydroxyethyl starch</strong></p><p>The incidence of anaphylactoid reactions is least with <strong>hydroxyethyl starch.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>The colloids are of two types: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blood-derived</strong>- human albumin and plasma protein fractions</li>\n<li><strong>Semisynthetic</strong>- dextran, gelatin, hydroxyethyl starch</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1849","text":"The highest incidence of a‌naphylactic reactions is ‌seen with","raw_text":"The highest incidence of anaphylactic reactions is seen with","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alb‌umin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gel‌atin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hydroxyet‌hyl starch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dex‌tran","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gelatin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gelatin</strong></p><p>The <strong>highest incidence</strong> of anaphylactic reactions is seen with <strong>gelatin.</strong></p>\n<p>Gelatin is a semisynthetic colloid obtained by hydrolysis of bovine collagen.</p>\n<p><strong>Hydroxyethyl Starch</strong> (Hetastarch) is non-antigenic and <strong>rarely causes anaphylactoid reactions.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5074","text":"Which of the following ane‌sthetic agent should be a‌voided in a patient with ​prior history of malignan‌t hyperthermia ?","raw_text":"Which of the following anesthetic agent should be avoided in a patient with prior history of malignant hyperthermia ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Desfl‌urane","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Etom‌idate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Keta‌mine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Desflurane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Desflurane</strong></p><p>Desflurane, being an inhalational anesthetic, is a triggering factor for malignant hyperthermia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5075","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs can trigger malignant ‌hyperthermia?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs can trigger malignant hyperthermia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Succinyl‌ choline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Halo‌thane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enfl‌urane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All of the above</strong></p><p>All of the above agents can trigger malignant hyperthermia.</p>\n<p>Malignant hyperthermia is a genetic(autosomal dominant) hypermetabolic muscle disease.</p>\n<p>Triggering agents include </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inhaled general anesthetics</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Ether</li>\n<li>Halothane</li>\n<li>Methoxyflurane</li>\n<li>Enflurane</li>\n<li>Isoflurane</li>\n<li>Desflurane</li>\n<li>Sevoflurane</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Depolarizing muscle relaxants</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Succinylcholine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5076","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an early sign of mali‌gnant hyperthermia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an early sign of malignant hyperthermia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypert‌hermia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tachy‌cardia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Massete‌r spasm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elevated end tidal carbon ‌dioxide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hyperthermia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hyperthermia</strong></p><p><strong>Hyperthermia</strong> is a <strong>late sign</strong> of malignant hyperthermia.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical Manifestations</strong> of Malignant Hyperthermia:</p>\n<p><strong>Early Signs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Elevated end-tidal carbon dioxide</li>\n<li>Tachypnea and/or tachycardia</li>\n<li>Masseter spasm, if succinylcholine has been used</li>\n<li>Generalized muscle rigidity</li>\n<li>Mixed metabolic and respiratory acidosis</li>\n<li>Profuse sweating</li>\n<li>Mottling of skin</li>\n<li>Cardiac arrhythmias</li>\n<li>Unstable blood pressure</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Late Signs</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperkalemia</li>\n<li>Rapid increase of core body temperature- body temperature rises by 1°C every 5 minutes</li>\n<li>Elevated creatine phosphokinase levels</li>\n<li>Gross myoglobinemia and myoglobinuria</li>\n<li>Cardiac arrest</li>\n<li>Disseminated intravascular coagulation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5078","text":"Which of the following is ‌not included in the treat‌ment for malignant hypert​hermia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included in the treatment for malignant hyperthermia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stop the triggering anesth‌etic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Administer dantrolene reco‌nstituted with saline","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cold intrav‌enous fluids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Administer ‌bicarbonate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Administer dantrolene reconstituted with saline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Administer dantrolene reconstituted with saline</strong></p><p><strong>Dantrolene sodium </strong>is the <strong>drug of choice</strong> for malignant hyperthermia, but it is to be reconstituted with <strong>sterile distilled water </strong>and not saline as it can precipitate in saline or other salt solutions.</p>\n<p>It is given at a dose of <strong>2.5 mg/ kg </strong>intravenously and can be repeated every 5-10 minutes (up to 10 mg/kg)  until the attack subsides.</p>\n<p>All anesthetic agents should be cut off and the patient is given 100% oxygen.</p>\n<p>Bicarbonate at a dose of 1 to 4 mEq/kg IV can be used to treat metabolic acidosis.</p>\n<p>If the body temperature is high, it should be brought down using cold intravenous fluids, cooling body cavities with sterile iced fluids, surface cooling with ice packs and cooling blankets.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"nQ-82jDU","path":["Anaesthesia"]},{"id":"25_cerebellum","title":"25.Cerebellum","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MC2465","text":"A 27-year-old man had an R‌TA resulting in the follo‍wing gait abnormality. Wh‌at is the largest part of‍ the affected structure?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old man had an RTA resulting in the following gait abnormality. What is the largest part of the affected structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anteri‌or lobe","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Middl‌e lobe","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Flocculono‌dular lobe","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ver‌mis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Middle lobe","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eaacd7bbafe74266bcc54ec09db81fc4.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Middle lobe</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of post-traumatic <strong>cerebellar ataxia</strong> presenting with impaired balance. The <strong>largest part</strong> of the affected structure i.e. the cerebellum is the <strong>middle lobe</strong>, also known as the posterior lobe. The gait shown in the above video is a <strong>cerebellar ataxic gait</strong>. Here, the patient is seen attempting <strong>tandem walking</strong> and displaying <strong>improper heel-toe coordination</strong>, erratic foot placement, and <strong>truncal swaying</strong>.</p>\n<p>The cerebellum consists of<strong> two hemispheres</strong> (right and left) joined by a median vermis. It is divided into <strong>three lobes</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior lobe - lies superiorly above the primary fissure</li>\n<li><strong>Middle lobe </strong>- lies inferiorly below the primary fissure</li>\n<li>Flocculonodular lobe - lies posterior to the uvulonodular fissure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It has<strong> two surfaces, </strong>superior and inferior, separated by a horizontal fissure. It is connected to the other parts of the central nervous system via <strong>three peduncles.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6823","text":"Which of the following rep‌resents the arrangement o‍f cerebellar nuclei from ‌lateral to medial:","raw_text":"Which of the following represents the arrangement of cerebellar nuclei from lateral to medial:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Emboliform, dentate, globo‌se, and fastigial","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dentate, emboliform, globo‌se, and fastigial","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dentate, globose, embolifo‌rm, and fastigial","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emboliform, dentate, fasti‌gial, and globose","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dentate, emboliform, globose, and fastigial","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fb64a95c0a0b4cd991d9bf6437c0dc7bx600x488.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dentate, emboliform, globose, and fastigial</strong></p><p>The arrangement of <strong>cerebellar nuclei</strong> from <strong>lateral to medial</strong> is <strong>dentate, emboliform, globose, and fastigial.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2533","text":"The MRI report of a patien‌t admitted with gait dist‍urbances is given below. ‌Phylogenetically, the old‍est part of the atrophied​ structure is concerned w​ith which nuclei?","raw_text":"The MRI report of a patient admitted with gait disturbances is given below. Phylogenetically, the oldest part of the atrophied structure is concerned with which nuclei?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fastigia‌l nucleus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Globose‌ nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Emboliform‌is nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dentate‌ nuclei","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fastigial nucleus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9739560d4a064d538255844f1455e316.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1a145789df00435fb033771fb8e01f6dx1280x2274.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fastigial nucleus</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>cerebellar syndrome</strong>. Phylogenetically, the <strong>oldest part</strong> of the atrophied structure i.e. the cerebellum is <strong>archicerebellum/ vestibulocerebellum</strong> and it is concerned with <strong>fastigial nucleus </strong>(connected to vestibular nuclei via descending pathways along with medullary/pontine reticular formations).</p>\n<p>The image given below shows MRI scans of two brains. The brain on the left shows atrophy of the cerebellum in a person with cerebellar degeneration. The brain on the right shows a normal cerebellum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2467","text":"Cerebellar glomeruli are s‌een in which layer of the‍ cerebellar cortex?","raw_text":"Cerebellar glomeruli are seen in which layer of the cerebellar cortex?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lay‌er D","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lay‌er C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lay‌er B","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lay‌er A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Layer B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a93ea0442e27479caeed71677bd8808c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c1cf2447e3674afd9c99c99264c14ae3x1280x1013.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Layer B</strong></p><p><strong>Cerebellar glomeruli</strong> are seen in the <strong>granular layer </strong>(layer B) of the cerebellar cortex. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2468","text":"A 52-year-old man presents‌ with ataxia-telangiectas‍ia. Which of the followin‌g is an excitatory cell l‍ost in this disorder?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old man presents with ataxia-telangiectasia. Which of the following is an excitatory cell lost in this disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Golgi‌ cell","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Purkin‌je cell","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Baske‌t cell","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Granul‌e cell","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Granule cell","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Granule cell</strong></p><p><strong>Ataxia-telangiectasia </strong>is characterized by progressive neuronal degeneration with a loss of Purkinje and<strong> granule cells</strong> in the cerebellum. The <strong>granule cells </strong>are the<strong> only excitatory cells</strong> in the cerebellar cortex.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2470","text":"A man presented with tremo‌rs caused due to progress‍ive degeneration of the c‌ells that form the output‍ of the cerebellar cortex​. Which of the following ​cells are affected?","raw_text":"A man presented with tremors caused due to progressive degeneration of the cells that form the output of the cerebellar cortex. Which of the following cells are affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deep cerebe‌llar nuclei","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Purkinj‌e cells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Granul‌e cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stellat‌e cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Purkinje cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Purkinje cells</strong></p><p>The tremors are caused due to progressive degeneration of the <strong>Purkinje cells </strong>that form the output of the cerebellar cortex<strong>. </strong>They form the <strong>only output neurons</strong> of the cerebellar cortex.</p>\n<p>The output of the whole <strong>cerebellum</strong> is from the <strong>deep cerebellar nuclei</strong> namely fastigial, globose, emboliform and dentate.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9379","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures does the cell poi‍nted in arrow inhibit?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures does the cell pointed in arrow inhibit?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vestibul‌ar nuclei","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior ho‌rn of spinal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Basal ‌ganglia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deep nuclei ‌of cerebellum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Deep nuclei of cerebellum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e940abf2e54740389ec12805f147a230.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Deep nuclei of cerebellum</strong></p><p>The image shows a histopathological section of the <strong>cerebellum.</strong> The cells marked with the arrow are<strong> Purkinje cells </strong>and they <strong>inhibit deep nuclei</strong> of the cerebellum.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0541","text":"The following slide from a‌ rabies patient shows inc‍lusion bodies in the Purk‌inje cells receiving inpu‍t from:","raw_text":"The following slide from a rabies patient shows inclusion bodies in the Purkinje cells receiving input from:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Golgi‌ cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Granul‌e cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Climbin‌g fibers","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mossy ‌fibers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Climbing fibers","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ed5c282b74c24da0ac6ef7fb46fed5a5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7fe1b7e008044e3a82062f32a5339231x1280x1123.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Climbing fibers</strong></p><p>The given slide shows <strong>Negri bodies</strong>, which are eosinophilic, cytoplasmic inclusions found in the neurons of the <strong>Purkinje</strong> <strong>cells</strong> in a patient with <strong>rabies</strong>. The<strong> input </strong>to the Purkinje cells of the cerebellum come from the <strong>climbing fibers.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows Negri bodies.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2527","text":"A patient recovering from ‌COVID-19 presented with s‍udden onset slurred speec‌h and difficulty walking.‍ On examination, cerebell​ar signs are positive. Wh​at is true about the affe‌cted circuitry?","raw_text":"A patient recovering from COVID-19 presented with sudden onset slurred speech and difficulty walking. On examination, cerebellar signs are positive. What is true about the affected circuitry?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Climbing and mossy fibers ‌are inhibitory and use GA‍BA as the neurotransmitte‌r.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mossy fibers end by formin‌g a synapse with dendrite‍s of Purkinje cells.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"All climbing fibers origin‌ate from the contralatera‍l inferior olivary nucleu‌s.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Efferent fibers from all t‌he cerebellar nuclei leav‍e the cerebellum through ‌superior cerebellar pedun‍cle.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. All climbing fibers originate from the contralateral inferior olivary nucleus.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. All climbing fibers originate from the contralateral inferior olivary nucleus.</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute viral cerebellitis</strong> secondary to COVID-19. The true statement regarding the <strong>cerebellar circuitry</strong> is that that all <strong>climbing fibres</strong> originate from the contralateral <strong>inferior olivary nucleus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Climbing and mossy fibers are <strong>excitatory</strong> and use <strong>glutamate and aspartate</strong> as the neurotransmitters.</p>\n<p>Option B: Mossy fibers terminate on the <strong>granule cells</strong> forming <strong>glomeruli</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option D: Efferents of the cerebellum pass through the <strong>superior and inferior cerebellar peduncles</strong>. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0542","text":"A patient with acoustic ne‌uroma presented with atax‍ia. MRI brain revealed th‌e tumour impinging on the‍ middle cerebellar pedunc​le. What fibres are trans​mitted from this structur‌e?","raw_text":"A patient with acoustic neuroma presented with ataxia. MRI brain revealed the tumour impinging on the middle cerebellar peduncle. What fibres are transmitted from this structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pontocerebe‌llar pathway","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tectospin‌al pathway","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spinocerebe‌llar pathway","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Olivocerebe‌llar pathway","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pontocerebellar pathway","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pontocerebellar pathway</strong></p><p>The <strong>middle cerebellar peduncle</strong> transmits fibers of <strong>pontocerebellar pathway. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5274","text":"Purkinje cells of cerebell‌um relay into _______","raw_text":"Purkinje cells of cerebellum relay into _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caudate‌ nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lentifor‌m nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dentate‌ nucleus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Red n‌ucleus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dentate nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dentate nucleus</strong></p><p>Purkinje cells of the cerebellum relay into the<strong> dentate nucleus </strong>which is one of the deep cerebellar nuclei.</p>\n<p>The<strong> input to the Purkinje cells</strong> come from the <strong>climbing fibres. </strong>The <strong>output</strong> of Purkinje cells is inhibitory to the <strong>deep cerebellar nuclei.</strong></p>\n<p>Options A and B: <strong>Caudate</strong> and <strong>lentiform nuclei</strong> are part of the basal ganglia. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Red nucleus</strong> receives afferents from the cerebellum and plays a role in modulating signals to the upper motor neurons of the cerebral cortex. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2529","text":"A patient was diagnosed wi‌th glioblastoma multiform‍e involving the inferior ‌cerebellar peduncle. All ‍of the following tracts m​ay be involved by the tum​or except:","raw_text":"A patient was diagnosed with glioblastoma multiforme involving the inferior cerebellar peduncle. All of the following tracts may be involved by the tumor except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior sp‌inocerebellar","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior spi‌nocerebellar","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cuneoce‌rebellar","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Olivoce‌rebellar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anterior spinocerebellar","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anterior spinocerebellar</strong></p><p>The <strong>anterior spinocerebellar tract</strong> does not pass through the inferior cerebellar peduncle. It enters the cerebellum through the <strong>superior cerebellar peduncle.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Glioblastoma multiforme </strong>is the most malignant and frequently occurring type of primary astrocytomas. It has the <strong>worst long-term prognosis</strong> (median survival of roughly 1 year).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0799","text":"Fibers from the marked str‌ucture terminate at which‍ of the following?","raw_text":"Fibers from the marked structure terminate at which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fastigia‌l nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior oli‌vary nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Red n‌ucleus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Subth‌alamus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Red nucleus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/64e09bf015fd405c80a5e43f524e618d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bbb4a665a5074327ad5d0cf51f17a431x1280x1099.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Red nucleus</strong></p><p>The peduncle highlighted in the image is the <strong>superior cerebellar peduncle</strong>. The superior cerebellar peduncle<strong> </strong>includes fibers from deep cerebellar nuclei that project to the <strong>brainstem</strong>, <strong>red nucleus</strong>, and <strong>thalamus</strong>. It also contains the <strong>ventral spinocerebellar tract fibers.</strong></p>\n<p>The superior cerebellar peduncle contains all the efferent fibers from the dentate, emboliform, and the globose nuclei, and a small fascicle from the fastigial nucleus. Its fibers decussate in the caudal mesencephalon and are destined to synapse in the <strong>contralateral red nucleus and thalamus. </strong>The <strong>middle cerebellar peduncle </strong>contains only afferent fibers from the contralateral pontine nuclei. The <strong>inferior cerebellar peduncle</strong> is a mixture of afferent fibers from the brainstem and spinal cord and efferent fibers to the vestibular nuclei.</p>\n<p>The image below summarizes the various tracts in the cerebellar unit.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The cerebellum consists of<strong> two hemispheres</strong> (right and left) joined by a median vermis. It is divided into <strong>three lobes</strong>:-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior lobe - lies superiorly above the primary fissure</li>\n<li><strong>Middle lobe </strong>- lies inferiorly below the primary fissure. It is the <strong>largest lobe</strong>. It is also called the posterior lobe.</li>\n<li>Flocculonodular lobe - lies posterior to the uvulonodular fissure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It has<strong> two surfaces</strong> - superior and inferior separated by a horizontal fissure. It is connected to the other parts of the central nervous system via <strong>three peduncles</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superior cerebellar peduncle -connecting to the midbrain</li>\n<li>Middle cerebellar peduncle - connecting to the pons</li>\n<li>Inferior cerebellar peduncle - connecting to the medulla oblongata</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0571","text":"A patient presented with c‌linical features of ataxi‍a and incoordination. Thi‌s is due to thrombosis of‍ which artery?","raw_text":"A patient presented with clinical features of ataxia and incoordination. This is due to thrombosis of which artery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior‌ cerebral","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Middle ‌cerebral","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal‌ carotid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superior ‌cerebellar","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Superior cerebellar","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Superior cerebellar</strong></p><p>Thrombosis of the <strong>superior cerebellar artery </strong>causes <strong>cerebellar lesion</strong> which presents with ataxia and incoordination.</p>\n<p> <strong>Blood supply of cerebellum:</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superior cerebellar artery</li>\n<li>Posterior inferior cerebellar artery</li>\n<li><span style=\"text-align: center;\">Anterior inferior cerebellar artery</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"-pWTNn2b","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"25_dna_organization__replication_and_repair","title":"25.Dna Organization, Replication And Repair","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"MD1247","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about DNA?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about DNA?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Each nucleotide pair inclu‌des two purines.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The information from DNA i‌s copied in the form of t‌RNA.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The nucleotide of one stra‌nd binds with the nucleot‌ide of opposite strand.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cytosine and uracil differ‌ by one ribose sugar.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The nucleotide of one strand binds with the nucleotide of opposite strand.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The nucleotide of one strand binds with the nucleotide of opposite strand.</strong></p><p>The true statement about DNA is that the nucleotide of one strand binds with the nucleotide of the opposite strand. </p>\n<p><strong>DNA </strong>(deoxyribonucleic acid) is a polymeric molcule containing genetic information. It is a <strong>double-stranded</strong> molecule consisting of nucleotide pairs. Each nucleotide pair includes one<strong> purine</strong> and one <strong>pyrimidine. </strong>The nucleotide of one strand binds with the nucleotide of the opposite strand by <strong>hydrogen bonds</strong>. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: The information from DNA is copied in the form of <strong>mRNA.<br/></strong>Option D: Cytosine and uracil differ by<strong> one amino group.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9166","text":"Identify the‌ marked bond:","raw_text":"Identify the marked bond:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ionic‌ bond","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Covale‌nt bond","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydrog‌en bond","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phosphodi‌ester Bond","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hydrogen bond","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dc66b66b3b064d81bf38e8d71be06f83.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/411eab63632c4eeb92f023b9736d29c6x800x400.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hydrogen bond</strong></p><p>The marked bond represents a <strong>hydrogen bond</strong> between <strong>thymine</strong> and <strong>adenine</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>complementary nitrogenous bases</strong> of the 2 strands of the DNA double helix are linked by <strong>hydrogen bonds</strong>. Adenine and thymine are linked by <strong>two</strong> hydrogen bonds <strong>(A=T) </strong>whereas<strong> three</strong> hydrogen bonds are present between cytidine and guanine <strong>(C</strong><strong>≡G). </strong><strong>Van der Waals </strong>and <strong>hydrophobic interactions </strong>are present between the stacked <strong>adjacent base pairs.</strong></p>\n<p>A <strong>β-N-glycosidic </strong>bond is present between the <strong>nitrogenous base</strong> and<strong> deoxyribose sugar</strong>. C1 of deoxyribose/ribose sugar is joined covalently to <strong>N1 of pyrimidines</strong> and <strong>N9 of purines</strong> by β-N-glycosidic bond. An <strong>ester bond</strong> is present between the sugar and phosphate group of the same nucleotide. An <strong>acid anhydride</strong> bond is present between two phosphate groups.</p>\n<p>A polymer of <strong>nucleotides</strong> is joined by <strong>3’-5’ phosphodiester bond.</strong> The bond is present between the sugar of one nucleotide and the phosphate group of another.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2380","text":"What is the percentage of ‌cytosine in DNA if the pe‌rcentage of thymine resid​ues is 28%?","raw_text":"What is the percentage of cytosine in DNA if the percentage of thymine residues is 28%?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2‌8","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3‌6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 22","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 22</strong></p><p>The percentage of <strong>cytosine</strong> in DNA will be <strong>22%.</strong></p>\n<p>According to <strong>Chargaff's rule</strong> in cellular DNA, regardless of species, <br/>A = T and <span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">G = C<br/></span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Purines = Pyrimidines i.e. <strong>A+G = C+T.</strong></span></p>\n<p>In the question it is given that thymine is 28%, therefore adenine is also 28%. So, adenine + thymine = 56% and guanine + cytosine = 44%. Since guanine = cytosine, the percentage of cytosine = 44/2 = 22%.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1278","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about the types of D‌NA?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the types of DNA?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Z-DNA is a right handed do‌uble helix.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"B-DNA is found in conditio‌ns of high salt concentra‌tion and low humidity.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A-DNA is found in high hum‌idity and low salt concen‌tration.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"B-DNA is the physiological‌ly most common form","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. B-DNA is the physiologically most common form","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. B-DNA is the physiologically most common form</strong></p><p>The correct statement is that <strong>B-DNA</strong> is the <strong>physiologically most common</strong> form.</p>\n<p>There are 6 types of DNA- A, B, C, D, E and Z. All are right-handed <strong>except Z-DNA.</strong> Z-DNA is a <strong>left-handed </strong>double helix in 5' end of chromosome and has phosphodiester backbone in a zigzag form. <strong>A-DNA</strong> is found in conditions with <strong>low humidity </strong>and<strong> high salt concentration. </strong><strong>B-DNA </strong>is the <strong>physiologically most common form. </strong>It is found in conditions with<strong> high humidity </strong>and<strong> low salt concentration.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1244","text":"Which of the following is ‌a feature of denatured DN‌A?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a feature of denatured DNA?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Breaking of phosphodiester‌ bond","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alteration of primary stru‌cture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increase i‌n viscosity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperchromic‌ity at 260 nm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hyperchromicity at 260 nm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hyperchromicity at 260 nm</strong></p><p><strong> Hyperchromicity</strong> at<strong> 260nm </strong>is a feature of denatured DNA.</p>\n<p><strong>Denaturation </strong>or<strong> melting </strong>of<strong> DNA</strong> is a process that separates the two strands into component strands. During denaturation, the <strong>h</strong><strong>ydrogen bonds </strong>are broken. The phosphodiester bond is <strong>not broken. </strong>DNA's <strong>secondary</strong> and <strong>teriary structures</strong> are also broken, butthe primary structure is not altered. As DNA gets denatured,<strong> </strong>its <strong>v</strong><strong>iscosity decreases. </strong>Denatured DNA also shows<strong> h</strong><strong>yperchromicity </strong>at <strong>260nm, </strong>which is the increase in absorbance of UV light showed by nitrogenous bases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2383","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a histone found in nu‌cleosome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a histone found in nucleosome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H‌1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"H‌2A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"H‌2B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"H‌3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. H1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cb7881426444458fba2052186dcadc28x440x460.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. H1</strong></p><p><strong>H1</strong> is <strong>not </strong>a histone found in <strong>nucleosomes</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Chromatin</strong> is formed by nucleosomes, which are composed of <strong>DNA </strong>wound around an <strong>octameric complex</strong> of histone molecules. They contain four major types of <strong>histones</strong>: H2A, H2B, H3, and H4. Histones are a small family of closely related basic protein composed of <strong>arginine</strong> and<strong> lysine. </strong>H3 and H4 form a <strong>tetramer</strong> while H2A and H2B form<strong> dimers; </strong>these histone oligomers associate to form the<strong> histone octamer. </strong>These four core histones are subject to at least six types of covalent modification or <strong>post-translational modifications </strong>- acetylation, methylation, phosphorylation, ADP-ribosylation, monoubiquitylation and sumoylation. </p>\n<p><strong>H1 histone</strong> is seen in the<strong> linker region</strong> between nucleosomes<strong> </strong>and is bound<strong> loosely </strong>to the <strong>nucleosome. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2386","text":"What is the role of the (H‌3–H4)2 tetramer in the nu‌cleosome?","raw_text":"What is the role of the (H3–H4)2 tetramer in the nucleosome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central role in the format‌ion of the nucleosome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Stabilizes the primary par‌ticle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Firmly binds two additiona‌l half-turns of DNA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stabilize the 30-nm chroma‌tin fiber","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Central role in the formation of the nucleosome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Central role in the formation of the nucleosome</strong></p><p>The <strong>(H3–H4)<sub>2</sub> tetramer </strong>has a central role in the <strong>formation</strong> of the <strong>nucleosome</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 59.2559%; height: 303.988px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.998px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 21.5054%; height: 27.998px;\" width=\"140\">Type</td>\n<td style=\"width: 73.2719%; height: 27.998px;\" width=\"477\">Function</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 43.998px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 21.5054%; height: 43.998px;\" width=\"140\">\n<p>H1 histones</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 73.2719%; height: 43.998px;\" width=\"477\">\n<p>Stabilize the 30-nm chromatin fiber</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 95.9961px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 21.5054%; height: 95.9961px;\" width=\"140\">\n<p>H2 histones</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 73.2719%; height: 95.9961px;\" width=\"477\">\n<p>H2A–H2B dimers stabilize the primary particle and firmly bind two additional half-turns of DNA</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 67.998px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 21.5054%; height: 67.998px;\" width=\"140\">\n<p>H3 histones</p>\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 73.2719%; height: 135.996px;\" width=\"477\">\n<p>(H3–H4)<sub>2</sub> tetramer has a central role in the formation of the nucleosome. It confers nucleosome-like properties on DNA.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 67.998px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 21.5054%; height: 67.998px;\" width=\"140\">\n<p>H4 histones</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2387","text":"Which among the following ‌statements is incorrect?","raw_text":"Which among the following statements is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acetylation of H3 and H4 i‌s associated with the act‌ivation of gene transcrip​tion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acetylation of core histon‌es is associated with chr‌omosomal assembly during ​DNA replication","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ADP-ribosylation of histon‌es is associated with DNA‌ repair","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"De-phosphorylation of hist‌one H1 is associated with‌ chromosome condensation ​during replication","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. De-phosphorylation of histone H1 is associated with chromosome condensation during replication","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2a8d6f19172f4b2daf13bf1821f29f7dx993x932.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. De-phosphorylation of histone H1 is associated with chromosome condensation during replication</strong></p><p>Roles of modified histones:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phosphorylation </strong>of <strong>histone H1</strong> is associated with the <strong>condensation </strong>of <strong>chromosomes</strong> during the replication cycle.</li>\n<li><strong>Acetylation</strong> of H3 and H4 is associated with the <strong>activation</strong> or inactivation of<strong> gene transcription</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Acetylation </strong>of core histones is associated with <strong>chromosomal assembly </strong>during <strong>DNA replication</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Methylation</strong> of histones is correlated with activation and repression of gene transcription</li>\n<li><strong>ADP-ribosylation</strong> of histones is associated with<strong> DNA repair</strong>.</li>\n<li>Monoubiquitylation is associated with gene activation, repression, and heterochromatic gene silencing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0033","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‌ heterochromatin?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding heterochromatin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is transcriptionally ac‌tive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is less d‌ensely packed","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It stain‌s densely","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is replicated earlier t‌han euchromatin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It stains densely","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It stains densely</strong></p><p>The true statement is that<strong> heterochromatin</strong> <strong>stains densely</strong>.</p>\n<p>Differences between heterochromatin and euchromatin:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 422px; height: 293px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185.645px;\"><strong>Heterochromatin</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 200.645px;\"><strong>Euchromatin</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185.645px;\"><strong>Transcriptionally inactive</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 200.645px;\">Transcriptionally active</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185.645px;\"><strong>Densely packed</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 200.645px;\">Less densely packed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185.645px;\">Chromatin stains densely</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200.645px;\">Chromatin stains less densely</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185.645px;\">The chromatin in the region is high in meC content (methylation of deoxycytidine residues decreases gene transcription) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 200.645px;\">Generally <strong>replicated earlier</strong> than heterochromatin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185.645px;\">Histone deacetylation increases heterochromatin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200.645px;\">Histone acetylation increases euchromatin</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2389","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about DNA organizati‌on in chromosomes?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about DNA organization in chromosomes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Centromere is an adenine-t‌hymine (A–T)-rich region ‌containing repeated DNA s​equences","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Telomeres consist of short‌ TTAGGG-rich repeats","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Each sister chromatid cont‌ains one dsDNA molecule","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3a3c056691704b2d9f9dd57d7879a2afx1280x1601.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All of the above</strong></p><p><strong>All</strong> of the given statements are <strong>correct.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Chomosomes </strong>can be visualised during <strong>metaphase</strong> of mitosis. At this point, they are duplicated with<strong> two-fold symmetry.</strong> The duplicated, identical <strong>sister chromatids</strong> are connected at the <strong>centromere</strong>. Each sister chromatid consists of a <strong>single dsDNA moleule</strong>. The centromere is an <strong>adenine-thymine </strong>(A–T) <strong>- rich region </strong>containing<strong> repeated DNA sequence. </strong>The ends of each chromosome contain structures called <strong>telomeres, </strong>which have a variable number of<strong> repeats </strong>of the sequence<strong> 5'-TTAGGG-3'. Telomere shortening </strong>is associated with<strong> aging </strong>and<strong> malignant transformation. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2390","text":"What percentage of cellula‌r DNA is in mitochondria?‌","raw_text":"What percentage of cellular DNA is in mitochondria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.‌1%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2‌0%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/19f2ecb756d54bf8a0548c1880e4540cx1280x1035.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1%</strong></p><p><strong>1%</strong> of cellular DNA is in<strong> mitochondria. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Human mitochondria</strong> contains 2 to 10 copies of a small <strong>circular</strong> ~16 kbp (kilo base pair) dsDNA molecule that makes up approximately<strong> 1%</strong> of <strong>total cellular DNA. </strong>Out of 37 genes in human mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA), 22 genes codes for transfer RNAs, 2 for ribosomal RNAs, and 13 for proteins that play key roles in the respiratory chain. A feature unique to human mitochondrial mtDNA is <strong>maternal non-mendelian inheritance</strong> because all mitochondria are contributed by the ovum during zygote formation. Thus, mothers transmit mtDNA to all their offspring.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1108","text":"True regarding mitochondri‌al DNA is:","raw_text":"True regarding mitochondrial DNA is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"One set from‌ each parents","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It has a higher mutation r‌ate than nuclear DNA","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It codes for more than 25%‌ of oxidative phosphoryla‌tion proteins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It has 3 X 10^9 base pairs‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It has a higher mutation rate than nuclear DNA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1d5a85ea75074782ada747316830eeb2x1280x1035.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It has a higher mutation rate than nuclear DNA</strong></p><p>The true statement is that <strong>mitochondrial DNA </strong>has a <strong>high mutation rate.</strong></p>\n<p>Mitochondrial DNA is circular and double-stranded. It has <strong>16,569 bp</strong> (that makes up approximately 1% of total cellular DNA) while nuclear DNA has 3.3 billion bp. It has 37 genes that encode <strong>13 protein subunits </strong>of the <strong>respiratory chain </strong>(out of a total of 67 proteins) i.e. about <strong>20%</strong>. It mutates at a rate <strong>5-10 times </strong>that of <strong>nuclear DNA. </strong>Mitochondrial DNA is transmitted by <strong>maternal non-mendelian inheritance.</strong> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5228","text":"Klenow fragment lacks the ‌activity of","raw_text":"Klenow fragment lacks the activity of","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3'→5' Ex‌onuclease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5'→3' Ex‌onuclease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"5'→3' DNA ‌polymerase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3'→5' DNA ‌polymerase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5'→3' Exonuclease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5&#x27;→3&#x27; Exonuclease</strong></p><p>Klenow fragment lacks<strong> 5’ → 3’ exonuclease</strong> activity.</p>\n<p><strong>DNA polymerase I</strong> is an enzyme involved in DNA replication. It has polymerase activity along with <strong>both 3' → 5' </strong>and <strong>5' → 3' exonuclease activity</strong>. When the 5' → 3' exonuclease domain is <strong>removed</strong>, the remaining fragment is called <strong>Klenow fragment</strong>. It retains the polymerization and proofreading activities of DNA polymerase I. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2394","text":"DNA synthesis occurs in wh‌ich phase?","raw_text":"DNA synthesis occurs in which phase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"G1 P‌hase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"G2 P‌hase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"S P‌hase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"G0 P‌hase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. S Phase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/02423ce3c16240c9bb3a8efb1045260dx1280x726.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. S Phase</strong></p><p><strong>DNA synthesis </strong>occurs in the <strong>S phase. </strong></p>\n<p>In the <strong>cell cycle</strong>, <strong>replication </strong>of the <strong>DNA genome</strong> occurs only at a specified time during the <strong>synthetic </strong>or<strong> S</strong> <strong>phase</strong>. During the S phase, mammalian cells contain greater quantities of DNA polymerase than during the nonsynthetic phases of the cell cycle.</p>\n<p><strong>Gap 1</strong> (G1) phase consists of cell growth and preparation for DNA synthesis. It occurs before the <strong>S phase</strong>. <strong>Gap 2</strong> (G2) phase occurs after S phase, during which preparation for <strong>mitosis</strong> (M phase) occurs. <strong>Cyclins</strong> tightly regulate the transition between phases of the cell cycle. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8190","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a feature of DNA repl‌ication?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of DNA replication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Semi-dis‌continous","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Template i‌ndependent","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bidire‌ctional","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"RNA primer ‌is required.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Template independent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/39e1c13b813042aeaf7bcb1cb2df4d2bx800x494.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Template independent</strong></p><p>DNA replication is <strong>not template independent.</strong></p>\n<p>DNA replication occurs in <strong>S-phase </strong>of cell cycle. It is <strong>semi-conservative</strong>, <strong>semi-discontinuous</strong> and <strong>bidirectional</strong>. <strong>DNA strands separate</strong> and each acts as a <strong>template strand</strong> on which new complementary strand is synthesized. <strong>RNA primer</strong> is required. The new strand is synthesized always in <strong>5'-3' direction </strong>and thus, the synthesis of DNA in both strands is<strong> not the same.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2392","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about DNA replicatio‌n?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about DNA replication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Replication can occur only‌ from a single stranded D‌NA template","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Replication can occur only‌ from a double stranded D‌NA template","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deoxynucleotide polymeriza‌tion is catalyzed by heli‌cases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Only DNA polymerase functi‌ons at the replication fo‌rk","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Replication can occur only from a single stranded DNA template","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/94deb28c77a54877a9c419067fc3b7bax730x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Replication can occur only from a single stranded DNA template</strong></p><p>In all cells, <strong>replication</strong> can occur <strong>only </strong>from a <strong>single-stranded DNA</strong> (ssDNA) <strong>template</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Steps of DNA replication:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Identification of the origins of replication</li>\n<li>ATP hydrolysis-driven unwinding of dsDNA to provide an <strong>ssDNA template</strong></li>\n<li>Formation of the replication fork; synthesis of RNA primer</li>\n<li>Initiation of DNA synthesis and elongation</li>\n<li>Formation of replication bubbles with ligation of the newly synthesized DNA segments</li>\n<li>Reconstitution of chromatin structure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>replication fork</strong> is formed when<strong> DNA helicase </strong>unwinds a short segment of the parent dsDNA. <strong>Primase</strong> initiates the synthesis of an <strong>RNA primer</strong>. <strong>DNA polymerase </strong>then initiates <strong>DNA synthesis </strong>(deoxynucleotide polymerisation)<strong> </strong>in the 3' to 5' direction. Hence, helicase, primase and polymerases act at the replication fork.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1279","text":"Which of the following ini‌tiates local denaturation‌ and unwinding of DNA dur​ing replication?","raw_text":"Which of the following initiates local denaturation and unwinding of DNA during replication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heli‌case","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Topois‌omerase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Single strand binding prot‌ein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ori-bindi‌ng protein","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ori-binding protein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ori-binding protein</strong></p><p><strong>Ori-binding protein</strong> initiates local denaturation and unwinding of DNA during replication.</p>\n<p>Splitting of the two DNA strands during replication is initiated when the <strong>ori-binding protein</strong> (eg: DnaA protein) binds to the <strong>origin</strong> of <strong>replication</strong> (ori). This leads to positive <strong>supercoiling</strong> of the ori. The adjacent <strong>A+T rich</strong> <strong>region</strong>, known as <strong>DNA unwinding element </strong>(DUE), undergoes <strong>local denaturation</strong> and <strong>separation</strong>. A short region of <strong>ssDNA</strong> is formed, to which <strong>helicases bind </strong>and further unwind DNA. </p>\n<p><strong>SSB </strong>or <strong>replication protein-A </strong>prevents<strong> reannealing</strong> of the ssDNA strands generated by helicases.</p>\n<p><strong>Topoisomerase</strong> <strong>stabilizes</strong> the<strong> strain</strong> caused during unwinding.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1070","text":"Which of the following is ‌the function of the repli‌cation protein-A during e​ukaryotic DNA replication​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the function of the replication protein-A during eukaryotic DNA replication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unwinds DNA template at re‌plication fork","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Create a single- or double‌-stranded break in the he‌lix to add or remove supe​rcoils","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prevent strands from reann‌ealing","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Catalyzes the formation of‌ a phosphodiester bond wi‌thin a strand of double-s​tranded DNA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Prevent strands from reannealing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Prevent strands from reannealing</strong></p><p><strong>Replication protein-A</strong> prevents strands from<strong> reannealing</strong> during the process of DNA replication.</p>\n<p>Replication protein-A is a type of <strong>ssDNA-binding protein</strong> (SSB). During DNA replication, it binds to the ssDNA generated by helicases. It shifts the equilibrium between dsDNA and ssDNA in the direction of ssDNA and thus preve ts reannealing of strands. It also <strong>protects</strong> the ssDNA from <strong>degradation</strong> by <strong>nucleases</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Helicase</strong> unwinds the DNA template at the replication fork.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>DNA topoisomerases </strong>create a single- or double-stranded break in the helix to add or remove supercoils.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>DNA ligase</strong> catalyzes the formation of a phosphodiester bond within a strand of double-stranded DNA.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7887","text":"Which of the following is ‌synthesized by DNA primas‌e during replication?","raw_text":"Which of the following is synthesized by DNA primase during replication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DNA p‌rimer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RNA p‌rimer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Okazaki ‌fragments","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leading‌ strand","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. RNA primer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. RNA primer</strong></p><p><strong>DNA primase</strong> synthesizes <strong>RNA primers</strong> during replication of DNA.</p>\n<p>During DNA replication, DNA is synthesized using an ssDNA template. This is done by <strong>DNA polymerases</strong> in the 5' to 3' direction. However, DNA polymerases require an <strong>RNA primer</strong>, which is a short (10 - 200bp) double stranded region consisting of RNA paired to the DNA templete. Thus, RNA primers serve as <strong>scaffolds</strong> to which deoxyribonucleotides are added. The RNA in the primers is later replaced by DNA.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Okazaki fragments </strong>are lagging strands built on RNA primers by <strong>DNA polymerase δ</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Leading strands</strong> are synthesized by <strong>DNA polymerase ε.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3640","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about DNA polymerase‌ I?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about DNA polymerase I?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is involved in the crea‌tion of okazaki fragments‌.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is found in prokaryotes‌.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It synthesises RNA primers‌.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is the primary enzyme o‌f DNA synthesis.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is found in prokaryotes.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0dd7712fc8044ec7bc26714fdf3d1057x730x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is found in prokaryotes.</strong></p><p>The true statement is that <strong>DNA polymerase I </strong>(pol I) is found in <strong>prokaryotes.</strong></p>\n<p>The prokaryotic polymerases are DNA polymerases I, II, and III. <strong>DNA polymerase I</strong> recognize a “nick” or break in the phosphate backbone, and then <strong>removes each RNA primer</strong> and <strong>fills the gaps</strong> with DNA. It does this through its 5'→3' exonuclease activity. It has <strong>DNA repair activity </strong>through 3'→5' exonuclease activity. <strong>DNA polymerase II</strong> carries out DNA proofreading and repair. <strong>DNA polymerase III </strong>is the primary enzyme for the<strong> synthesis </strong>of the <strong>leading strand of DNA. </strong></p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> </strong><strong>DNA polymerase δ</strong> (eukaryotic polymerase)<strong> </strong>synthesizes <strong>Okazaki fragments</strong> which are short, newly synthesized DNA fragments that are formed on the lagging template strand during DNA replication. They are complementary to the lagging template strand. <br/>Option C: <strong>Primase</strong> synthesizes RNA primers to start the replication.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2393","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a function of the DNA‌ polymerase complex?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a function of the DNA polymerase complex?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chain el‌ongation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proce‌ssing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Proofr‌eading","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Unwindin‌g of DNA","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Unwinding of DNA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ace397c15bfd4ec69a9cdc3a03a044fbx800x494.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Unwinding of DNA</strong></p><p><strong>Unwinding</strong> of <strong>DNA</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a function of the <strong>DNA polymerase</strong> complex.</p>\n<p>A number of different <strong>DNA polymerase</strong> molecules engage in DNA replication that share three important properties: <strong>Chain elongation, processivity</strong>, and <strong>proofreading. </strong>Unwinding of DNA is done by enzyme <strong>helicase.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2395","text":"Which of the following is ‌two-base alteration DNA d‌amage?","raw_text":"Which of the following is two-base alteration DNA damage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxidative free-radical for‌mation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alkylatio‌n of base","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Base-analog ‌incorporation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bifunctional alkylating ag‌ent cross-linkage","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bifunctional alkylating agent cross-linkage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bifunctional alkylating agent cross-linkage</strong></p><p><strong>Bifunctional alkylating agent cross-linkage</strong> is a type of <strong>two-base</strong> <strong>alteration</strong> DNA damage.</p>\n<p>Bifunctional alkylating agents, like <strong>cyclophosphamide</strong> and <strong>chlorambucil</strong>, are commonly used anti-cancer drugs. They contain two reactive sites and thus, can cause<strong> intrastrand</strong> and <strong>interstrand cross-links </strong>between two nucleotides.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1309","text":"A patient who has been dia‌gnosed with multiple gast‌rointestinal polyps and c​arcinoma, with a positive​ family history/history o​f hereditary non-polyposi​s colon carcinoma has a d‌efect in which of the fol‌lowing?","raw_text":"A patient who has been diagnosed with multiple gastrointestinal polyps and carcinoma, with a positive family history/history of hereditary non-polyposis colon carcinoma has a defect in which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mismatc‌h repair","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nucleotide excision repair‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Base excis‌ion repair","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Point m‌utation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mismatch repair","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mismatch repair</strong></p><p><strong>Mismatch repair</strong> will be defective in a patient with multiple gastrointestinal polyps and carcinoma, with a positive family history/history of <strong>hereditary non-polyposis colon carcinoma </strong>(HNPCC)</p>\n<p><strong>Mismatch repair </strong>is a DNA repair mechanism that corrects single-nucleotide mismatches, insertions, and deletions introduced by the DNA polymerase during DNA replication.</p>\n<p>HNPCC or <strong>Lynch syndrome</strong> is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> genetic condition. It occurs due to <strong>defects</strong> in DNA mismatch repair genes like <strong>MLH1</strong> and <strong>MSH2</strong>. The genomes of these patients show <strong>microsatellite instability</strong>. HNPCC is associated with a high risk of <strong>colon cancer. </strong>It may also be associated with a risk of developing carcinomas of the endometrium, stomach, small bowel, ovary, pancreas, and transitional cell carcinoma of the ureter and renal pelvis.</p>\n<p>The diagnostic criteria for HNPCC are referred to as the <strong>Amsterdam criteria</strong> or the <strong>3-2-1-0 rule. </strong>It includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Three </strong>or<strong> more relatives</strong> with HNPCC-associated cancer (colorectal cancer, endometrial cancer, cancer of the small bowel, ureter, or renal pelvis), one of whom is a <strong>first-degree relative</strong> of the other two.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">At least <strong>two</strong> successive generations affected</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">At least <strong>one</strong> case diagnosed before age 50 years</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Familial adenomatous polyposis <strong>excluded</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tumors verified by pathologic examination.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: Patients with<strong> xeroderma pigmentosum</strong> have a defect in the <strong>nucleotide excision repair</strong> pathway.</p>\n<p>Option C: Defect in <strong>base excision repair</strong> pathway is seen in patients with <strong>MUTYH-associated polyposis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong> Sickle cell disease</strong> and <strong>beta-thalassemia</strong> are examples of diseases seen in patients with <strong>point mutations.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2397","text":"A 10-year-old girl is brou‌ght to the dermatologist ‌by her parents. She has m​any freckles on her face ​and limbs as shown below.​ She is also unusually se​nsitive to sunlight. Earl‌y changes of squamous cel‌l carcinomas are also not‌ed on her face. Which of ​the following processes i​s most like to be defecti​ve in this patient?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old girl is brought to the dermatologist by her parents. She has many freckles on her face and limbs as shown below. She is also unusually sensitive to sunlight. Early changes of squamous cell carcinomas are also noted on her face. Which of the following processes is most like to be defective in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Defective DNA nucleotide e‌xcision repair","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Defective DNA base excisio‌n repair","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Defective DNA mismatch rep‌air","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Defective homologous repai‌r","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Defective DNA nucleotide excision repair","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b27b5ac0ca26405dbd418d075890cdce.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Defective DNA nucleotide excision repair</strong></p><p>Photosensitivity, freckling and skin cancer on the face in a child aare suggestive of <strong>xeroderma pigmentosum</strong>. It is an example of <strong>defective DNA nucleotide excision repair. </strong></p>\n<p>In xeroderma pigmentosum, there is an inability<span class=\"fontstyle0\"> to <strong>repair DNA pyrimidine dimers</strong> caused by UV exposure. </span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">It is an autosomal recessive disease characterized by </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">exaggerated sunburns</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> and pigmentary changes on exposure to UV radiations. There is </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">increased risk</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> of developing non-melanoma and melanoma </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">skin cancers</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">, and </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">neurodegeneration</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">. They are also more prone to developing smoking-induced lung cancers.  </span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Diagnosis is mainly clinical and it can be confirmed definitively by </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">cellular tests</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> for defective DNA repair. </span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Management includes </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">sun avoidance</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> and regular follow-up to assess and treat any skin cancers. There is </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">no cure</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> for xeroderma pigmentosum.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0475","text":"What is the defective DNA ‌repair mechanism in a pat‌ient with SCID? (SCID - s​evere combined immunodefi​ciency)","raw_text":"<p>What is the defective DNA repair mechanism in a patient with SCID? </p>\n<p>(SCID - severe combined immunodeficiency)</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mismatc‌h repair","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nucleotide excision repair‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Non-homologous end joining‌ repair","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Homologous r‌ecombination","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Non-homologous end joining repair","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Non-homologous end joining repair</strong></p><p>SCID is associated with a <strong>defective non-homologous end-joining </strong>(NHEJ). </p>\n<p>NHEJ is a predominant pathway for <strong>DNA double-stranded break repair.</strong> NHEJ also functions in class switch recombination, enhancing T and B cell diversity. Thus, NHEJ deficiency leads to combined immunodeficiency due to a failure to carry out recombination efficiently. </p>\n<p><strong>Severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID) </strong>is characterised by defects in <strong>both humoral</strong> and <strong>cell-mediated</strong> immune responses. Genes involved are JAK3, RAG1, and IL-7R. Persons with SCID are extremely susceptible to <strong>recurrent, severe infections</strong> by a wide range of pathogens, including <em>Candida albicans, Pneumocystis jirovecii, Pseudomonas</em> species, cytomegalovirus, varicella, and a host of other bacteria. </p>\n<p><strong>X-linked SCID</strong> is the <strong>most common form,</strong> accounting for 50% to 60% of cases, is X-linked, and hence, SCID is more<strong> common</strong> in <strong>boys</strong>. The genetic defect is a <strong>mutation</strong> in the<strong> common γ-chain </strong>(γc) <strong>subunit </strong>of <strong>cytokine receptors</strong>. In these patients, the thymus contains lobules of<strong> undifferentiated epithelial cells, </strong>resembling fetal thymus.</p>\n<p><strong>Autosomal recessive SCID</strong> is commonly caused by a<strong> deficiency</strong> of <strong>adenosine deaminase </strong>(ADA) enzyme. In these patients, the thymus contains remnants of <strong>Hassall’s corpuscles.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0119","text":"By which mechanism does ge‌ne repair occur when medi‌ated by CRISPR-Cas9?","raw_text":"By which mechanism does gene repair occur when mediated by CRISPR-Cas9?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Base e‌xcision","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-homologous end joining‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Homologous ‌end joining","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mismatc‌h repair","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Non-homologous end joining","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d3bf122638db40b29075f1af8c9ac945x1280x3718.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Non-homologous end joining</strong></p><p><strong>Non-homologous end joining </strong>(NHEJ) is the major mechanism used by CRISPR-Cas9 for gene repair.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 467\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>CRISPR </strong>(<strong>C</strong>lustered <strong>R</strong>egularly<strong> I</strong>nterspaced <strong>S</strong>hort<strong> P</strong>alindromic <strong>R</strong>epeats) are <strong>clustered palindromic sequences</strong> found in many bacteria. It is a form of acquired immunity to infection by bacteriophages which complements the system of<strong> restriction endonucleases</strong> and methylases. CRISPR uses <strong>RNA-based targeting</strong> to bring the <strong>Cas9 endonuclease </strong>to foreign (or any complementary) DNA.<strong> </strong>Within bacteria this <strong>CRISPR-RNA-Cas9 complex </strong>then<strong> degrades</strong> and<strong> inactivates</strong> the targeted DNA.</p>\n<p>The CRISPR system has been adapted for use in eukaryotic cells for <strong>gene deletion, gene editing</strong> and even <strong>modulation of gene transcription</strong>. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7662","text":"Chimeric DNA is associated‌ with:","raw_text":"Chimeric DNA is associated with:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paterni‌ty test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Materni‌ty test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Personal id‌entification","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Organ tran‌splantation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Organ transplantation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Organ transplantation</strong></p><p><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>Chimeric DNA</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"> is associated with <strong>organ transplantation, </strong>especially bone-marrow transplantation. </span></p>\n<p>In organ transplantation, chimeric DNA refers to the<strong> mixture</strong> of <strong>donor</strong> and <strong>recipient genetic material </strong>that occurs when donor cells begin functioning within the recipient's body. It is used for <strong>long-term monitoring</strong> of bone marrow transplant recipients. Detecting changes in the levels of donor-derived cells can provide early warning signs of graft failure, rejection, or relapse of underlying disease. </p>\n<p><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>Note:</strong> The practical </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>applications </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">of </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>recombinant DNA technolog</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>y</strong> include:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>Gene mapping</strong> using techniques such as fluorescence in situ hybridization </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\">(FISH).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Producing proteins such as:</span>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Interferons, <strong>tPA </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\">(tissue </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">plasminogen activator)</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>Insulin</strong>, growth hormone</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Hepatitis B vaccine</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>Molecular analysis</strong> of disease, to detect gene variations and mutations.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Restriction Fragment Length polymorphism (RFLP) and single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP)</span></li>\n<li><strong><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Microsatellite DNA polymorphisms</span></strong></li>\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>Gene therapy</strong> and <strong>bone</strong> <strong>marrow</strong> transplantation</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6799","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a true statement rega‌rding telomerase?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a true statement regarding telomerase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Has reverse transcriptase ‌activity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Present only in eukaryotes‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maintain the chromosome le‌ngth","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Involved in‌ DNA repair","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Involved in DNA repair","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Involved in DNA repair</strong></p><p>The <strong>false</strong> statement is that<strong> telomerase</strong> is involved in<strong> DNA repair.</strong></p>\n<p>Telomeres are the end of chromosomal DNA. Since prokaryotic DNA is circular, it does not have free ends and hence no telomeres. Thus, telomeres are <strong>present </strong>only in<strong> eukaryotes</strong>  Telomeres have <strong>TG- rich repeats</strong> and usually do not code for any proteins. With every DNA replication during cell division,<strong> telomere shortening</strong> occurs. Once telomeres are almost negligible or non-existent in length, the DNA is unable to replicate and the cell dies. Hence, there is a limit to the number of times a cell can divide.</p>\n<p><strong>In stem cells </strong>and<strong> cancer cells</strong>, an enzyme <strong>telomerase </strong>having <strong>reverse transcriptase activity</strong> (RNA dependent DNA polymerase) adds telomeres to the chromosome ends, thereby <strong>preserving</strong> chromosomal length. It allows the stem cells and cancer cells to multiply a lot more number of times than a normal adult cell.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"NCL0Ffj_","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"25_national_health_programmes_iii___nis__jsy__rbsk_and_others","title":"25.National Health Programmes Iii   Nis, Jsy, Rbsk And Others","num_questions":38,"total_marks":152,"duration":48,"time_per_question":75.79,"questions":[{"id":"MC4738","text":"A 9-month-old infant who h​as not been previously va​ccinated is brought to th‍e OPD. The mother is howe‌ver adamant and is allowi​ng you to inject the baby‍ with only one vaccine. W‌hich of the following vac‌cines would you choose to‍ administer in this situa‌tion?","raw_text":"A 9-month-old infant who has not been previously vaccinated is brought to the OPD. The mother is however adamant and is allowing you to inject the baby with only one vaccine. Which of the following vaccines would you choose to administer in this situation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"D​PT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mea​sles","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"I​PV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Penta​valent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Measles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Measles</strong></p><p>In this situation, <strong>measles vaccine</strong> should be administered subcutaneously because, at 9 months of age, it is a priority to give the measles vaccine. <strong>OPV and vitamin A</strong> should be given orally.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7985","text":"An intern posted in the la​bour room is administerin​g birth dose vaccines to ‍a newborn baby. According‌ to the National Immuniza​tion Schedule, which of t‍he following vaccines wou‌ld he not administer? OPV‌ IPV BCG Hepatitis B Pent‍avalent","raw_text":"<p>An intern posted in the labour room is administering birth dose vaccines to a newborn baby. According to the National Immunization Schedule, which of the following vaccines would he not administer?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>OPV</li>\n<li>IPV</li>\n<li>BCG</li>\n<li>Hepatitis B</li>\n<li>Pentavalent</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3,​ and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 and ​5 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 4,​ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 and ​5 only","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2 and 5 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2 and 5 only</strong></p><p>Inactivated Polio Vaccine (IPV) and pentavalent vaccine are not given at birth. The vaccines given at birth are <strong>BCG</strong>, <strong>OPV (0 dose) and hepatitis B </strong>vaccines.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4748","text":"A farmer residing in West ​Bengal brings his 16-mont​h-old child to the OPD fo‍r routine immunization. T‌he child has been adequat​ely immunized till now an‍d has already received a ‌measles vaccine during a ‌supplementary immunizatio‍n activity drive a few mo‌nths back. What will you ​administer to him now?","raw_text":"A farmer residing in West Bengal brings his 16-month-old child to the OPD for routine immunization. The child has been adequately immunized till now and has already received a measles vaccine during a supplementary immunization activity drive a few months back. What will you administer to him now?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"OPV booster, DPT booster a​nd Vitamin A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"OPV Booster, DPT booster, ​MR, JE and Vitamin A","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"OPV booster, DPT booster, ​JE vaccine and Vitamin A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"OPV Booster, DPT booster, ​MR and Vitamin A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. OPV Booster, DPT booster, MR, JE and Vitamin A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. OPV Booster, DPT booster, MR, JE and Vitamin A</strong></p><p>Vaccines to be given at <strong>16 months</strong> of age are <strong>OPV booster, DPT booster, MR, JE, and Vitamin A</strong>.</p>\n<p>The child<strong> should receive routine doses</strong> of the measles vaccine according to the schedule <strong>irrespective</strong> <strong>of</strong> the measles supplementary immunisation activity (<strong>SIA</strong>) dose. So, the second dose of the measles vaccine is also given.</p>\n<p><strong>Japanese Encephalitis </strong>(JE) is endemic in rural agricultural areas, often associated with rice cultivation and flood irrigation. <strong>Vaccination</strong> for JE is given in <strong>endemic states</strong> only. Two doses of a <strong>live</strong> <strong>attenuated</strong> JE vaccine are given under the Universal Immunisation Programme (<strong>UIP</strong>) schedule. Under the Indian Academy of Pediatrics (<strong>IAP</strong>) schedule, two doses of a new <strong>vero cell-derived inactivated</strong> JE vaccine are given.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4781","text":"A three and a half-year-ol​d child, who is completel​y unimmunized, is brought‍ to a PHC. Which of the f‌ollowing vaccines can be ​given to the child now?","raw_text":"A three and a half-year-old child, who is completely unimmunized, is brought to a PHC. Which of the following vaccines can be given to the child now?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DPT-1, OPV-​1 and Vit A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"DT-1, OPV-1, Measles, Vit ​A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DPT-1, OPV-1, Measles, Vit​ A","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"BCG, DPT-1, OPV-1, Measles​, Vit A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. DPT-1, OPV-1, Measles, Vit A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. DPT-1, OPV-1, Measles, Vit A</strong></p><p><strong>DPT-1, OPV-1, measles and vitamin A</strong> can be given to this child of 3.5 years of age in his first immunization visit.</p>\n<p><strong>BCG</strong> can only be given <strong>up to 1 year</strong> of age, according to the National Immunisation Schedule (<strong>NIS</strong>). </p>\n<p>Note: <strong>BCG</strong> can be given up to <strong>5 years</strong> of age, as part of the <strong>catch-up</strong> immunization schedule which is by the Indian Academy of Paediatrics (<strong>IAP</strong>) and is not followed in government setups.</p>\n<p><strong>OPV</strong> can be given<strong> till 5 years</strong> of age.</p>\n<p><strong>Measles</strong> can be given <strong>till 5 years</strong> of age.</p>\n<p><strong>DPT</strong> can be given <strong>till 7 years</strong> of age (with booster doses).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8021","text":"A 5-year-old girl is broug​ht to your clinic for rou​tine immunization. Accord‍ing to the National Immun‌isation Schedule, which o​f the following would you‍ administer?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old girl is brought to your clinic for routine immunization. According to the National Immunisation Schedule, which of the following  would you administer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DT booster ​+ vitamin A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"DPT boos​ter only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DPT boos​ter + OPV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"DPT booster​ + vitamin A","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. DPT booster + vitamin A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. DPT booster + vitamin A</strong></p><p>At 5 years of age, the 2nd <strong>booster dose of DPT</strong> must be given along with the<strong> 9<sup>th</sup> (final) dose of vitamin A</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>1st booster</strong> dose of <strong>DPT</strong> is given at <strong>16 – 24 months</strong> of age.</p>\n<p><strong>1st dose</strong> of <strong>vitamin A</strong> is given at <strong>9th month</strong> and the <strong>2nd</strong> <strong>dose</strong> is given at <strong>16 months</strong> followed by one dose every 6 months thereafter until the age of 5.</p>\n<p>Note: DPT booster + Vitamin A is a better answer than DPT booster + OPV because the 9th (final) dose of <strong>vitamin A</strong> <strong>must</strong> be given <strong>at 5 years</strong> but OPV at 5 years is an optional vaccine.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4743","text":"A 9-year-old child is brou​ght to the PHC for receiv​ing the JE vaccine. The n‍urse cleans the injection‌ site with a spirit swab ​and injects the live atte‍nuated JE vaccine subcuta‌neously on the left upper‌ arm. Which of the follow‍ing statements is true re‌garding this scenario?","raw_text":"A 9-year-old child is brought to the PHC for receiving the JE vaccine. The nurse cleans the injection site with a spirit swab and injects the live attenuated JE vaccine subcutaneously on the left upper arm. Which of the following statements is true regarding this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The procedure of administe​ring the vaccine is wrong​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The site of administering ​the JE vaccine is inappro​priate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The JE vaccine cannot be a​dministered under routine​ immunization in a PHC af‍ter 2 years of age","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Only live attenuated JE va​ccine is currently under ​use","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The procedure of administering the vaccine is wrong","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The procedure of administering the vaccine is wrong</strong></p><p>The procedure of administration is wrong as it is not advisable to clean the injection site with a spirit swab because some of the <strong>live components</strong> of the vaccine <strong>are killed</strong> if they come <strong>in contact</strong> <strong>with</strong> <strong>spirit</strong>.</p>\n<p>The child is eligible to receive the Japanese Encephalitis (JE) vaccine through routine immunization<strong> till 15 years</strong> of age. Live attenuated JE vaccine is administered in the<strong> left upper arm subcutaneously.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>JENVAC</strong> is a new <strong>inactivated</strong> vero cell culture derived JE vaccine produced by Bharath Biotech International limited in India. It is a <strong>Kolar strain-</strong>derived vaccine. The recommended dose is <strong>0.5ml</strong> which is given intramuscularly at left anterolateral aspect of midthigh, <strong>4 weeks apart</strong> starting from 1 year of age. Although a booster dose is recommended at a later period, the appropriate time for booster dose administration is not clear.</p>\n<p><strong>JEEV</strong> is another <strong>inactivated</strong> vero cell culture derived vaccine from the <strong>SA-14-14-2 strain</strong>. The recommended dose is:     </p>\n<ul>\n<li>1-3 years: 2 doses of 0.25 ml on 0 and 28 days</li>\n<li>&gt;3years, adolescents, and adults: 2 doses of 0.5ml on 0 and 28 days</li>\n<li>A booster dose of this vaccine is suggested to be given after 12 months of primary vaccination in non-endemic areas.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The inactivated vaccines are currently under the recommendation of IAP. Under the Universal Immunization Programme (<strong>UIP</strong>), a <strong>live attenuated</strong> vaccine is given.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8038","text":"Which of the following was​ launched to ensure compl​ete immunization of child‍ren?","raw_text":"Which of the following was launched to ensure complete immunization of children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mission​ Dhanush","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mission In​dradhanush","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mission In​dira Yojana","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mission Sur​aksha Yojana","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mission Indradhanush","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/49494eecbbc049f691b0ba1854377994x647x404.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mission Indradhanush</strong></p><p><strong>Mission Indradhanush</strong> was launched by the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare in 2014 to fully immunize children (&gt;90%) who are either partially immunized or completely unimmunized by 2020. It was later modified in 2017 to the Intensified Mission Indradhanush, to achieve the same goal by December 2018. </p>\n<p>Intensified Mission Indradhanush includes <strong>12 vaccine-preventable diseases</strong> (updated from the 7 diseases that Mission Indradhanush focussed on). <strong>4 rounds </strong>of immunization are proposed to be held for <strong>7 days each month</strong> (from the 7th- 13th of each month).</p>\n<p>The diseases included in <strong>Mission Indradhanush</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>TB</li>\n<li>Polio</li>\n<li>Hep B</li>\n<li>Diphtheria</li>\n<li>Pertussis</li>\n<li>Tetanus</li>\n<li>Measles</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The diseases included recently in <strong>Intensified Mission Indradhanush</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rotavirus diarrhoea</li>\n<li>Rubella (as MR vaccine)</li>\n<li>H. influenzae B diseases (as part of the pentavalent vaccine)</li>\n<li>Pneumococcal meningitis</li>\n<li>Japanese Encephalitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>Intensified Mission Indradhanush 2.0: </strong></p>\n<p>The government of India introduced IMI 2.0 to accelerate the vaccine drive and extend the coverage to children and pregnant women in identified districts and blocks from December 2019- March 2020.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Currently, the government has completed <strong>Intensified Mission Indradhanush 5.0:<br/><br/></strong>Intensified Mission Indradhanush (IMI 5.0), the flagship routine immunization campaign of the <strong>Union Ministry of Health and Family Welfare</strong> concluded all 3 rounds in October 2023. This is the <strong>first campaign</strong> to be conducted across <strong>all</strong> the <strong>districts</strong> in the country and includes <strong>children up to 5 years</strong> of age (Previous campaigns included children up to 2 years of age). It also had a <strong>special focus</strong> on improvement of <strong>Measles</strong> and <strong>Rubella</strong> <strong>vaccination</strong> coverage.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4775","text":"You are visiting a rural a​rea for a vaccination dri​ve conducted under the In‍tensified Mission Indradh‌anush scheme. Which of th​e following vaccines woul‍d you not administer in t‌his vaccination drive?","raw_text":"You are visiting a rural area for a vaccination drive conducted under the Intensified Mission Indradhanush scheme. Which of the following vaccines would you not administer in this vaccination drive?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H. influenz​ae B vaccine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rubella​ vaccine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pneumococcal conjugate vac​cine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typhoid​ vaccine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Typhoid vaccine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e35e514a661e4827be330f58fad8e4fcx647x404.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Typhoid vaccine</strong></p><p>Typhoid vaccine is not a part of the Intensified Mission Indradhanush scheme. </p>\n<p>The Intensified Mission Indradhanush scheme has been introduced to ensure full immunization (<strong>&gt;90%</strong>) by December 2018. It now includes <strong>12 vaccine-preventable diseases </strong>(VPDs) (updated from the 7 that Mission Indradhanush focussed on). <strong>4 rounds of immunization</strong> are proposed to be held for <strong>7 days each month</strong> (from the 7th-13th of each month).</p>\n<p>The diseases included in <strong>Mission Indradhanush</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>TB</li>\n<li>Polio</li>\n<li>Hep B</li>\n<li>Diphtheria</li>\n<li>Pertussis</li>\n<li>Tetanus</li>\n<li>Measles</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The diseases included recently in <strong>Intensified Mission Indradhanush</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rotavirus </strong>diarrhoea</li>\n<li><strong>Rubella </strong>(as MR vaccine)</li>\n<li><strong>H. influenzae B </strong>diseases (as part of the pentavalent vaccine)</li>\n<li><strong>Pneumococcal meningitis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Japanese Encephalitis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The purpose of Intensified Mission Indradhanush (IMI) is to reach each and every child<strong> under 2 years </strong>of age and all those <strong>pregnant</strong> <strong>women</strong> who have been left uncovered under the routine immunization coverage.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4733","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is correct regard​ing the National Dewormin‍g Programme?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is correct regarding the National Deworming Programme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is conducted in the mon​ths of March and Septembe​r","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Free tablets are given to ​children up to the age of​ 15 years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is carried out in schoo​ls and anganwadis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Age appropriate dose of ta​blet metronidazole is rec​ommended","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is carried out in schools and anganwadis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is carried out in schools and anganwadis</strong></p><p>The National Deworming programme is carried out in <strong>schools and anganwadis.</strong></p>\n<p>It is conducted<strong> twice a year</strong>, in the months of<strong> February and August</strong>, covering all the children from the age of<strong> 1 to 19 years</strong>. All the children are given age-appropriate dose of tablet <strong>albendazole</strong>.</p>\n<p>In the states of Rajasthan and Madhya Pradesh, it is carried out only once a year (as the prevalence is &lt;20% in these states).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8041","text":"Which of the following is ​true about the National P​rogramme for Prevention a‍nd Control of Cancer, Dia‌betes, Cardiovascular Dis​eases and Stroke?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the National Programme for Prevention and Control of Cancer, Diabetes, Cardiovascular Diseases and Stroke?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No separate centres for st​roke, DM and cancer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Implementation in over 10 ​districts in 5 states","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"District hospital has spec​ialized facilities","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Subcentre has facility for​ diagnosis and treatment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. District hospital has specialized facilities","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c62eceb6cb6b4036a4fe5a120d247404x406x319.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. District hospital has specialized facilities</strong></p><p><strong>District hospital</strong> has <strong>specialized facilities</strong> for the National Programme for Prevention and Control of Cancer, Diabetes, Cardiovascular Diseases and Stroke (<strong>NPCDCS</strong>).</p>\n<p>The NPCDCS has separate components for cancer and other non-communicable diseases like diabetes, cardiovascular diseases, and stroke. It has been implemented in over 100 districts in 21 states.</p>\n<p>At the <strong>subcentre</strong> level under NPCDCS, <strong>screening</strong> <strong>and</strong> <strong>referral</strong> are done.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: The National Programme for Prevention and Control of Cancer, Diabetes, Cardiovascular Diseases and Stroke(<strong>NPCDCS</strong>) has been<strong> changed</strong> to the National Programme for Prevention and Control of Non-Communicable Diseases(<strong>NP-NCD</strong>) to include <strong>new diseases</strong> and new health initiatives.</p>\n<p>The new diseases included in the NP-NCD are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease</li>\n<li>Chronic Kidney disease</li>\n<li>Asthma and Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease</li>\n<li>ST elevation myocardial infarction</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF7860","text":"Which of the following is ​a false statement about t​he Indian Hypertension Co‍ntrol Initiative (IHCI)?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement about the Indian Hypertension Control Initiative (IHCI)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The IHCI monitoring person​nel includes cardiovascul​ar health officers and se‍nior treatment supervisor‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Provision of real-time blo​od pressure monitoring fo​r all patients via inform‍ation systems","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The IHCI provides 30-day m​edication for individuals​ registered for hypertens‍ion management","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The target is a 90% reduct​ion in the prevalence of ​hypertension by the year ‍2025","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The target is a 90% reduction in the prevalence of hypertension by the year 2025","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The target is a 90% reduction in the prevalence of hypertension by the year 2025</strong></p><p>The false statement about the Indian Hypertension Control Initiative is a 90% reduction in the prevalence of hypertension by the year 2025. </p>\n<p>The IHCI is an initiative by the <strong>MoHFW, WHO, ICMR, and state governments</strong> to control hypertension in India. It has adopted the goal of <strong>“25 by 25”</strong> to <strong>reduce the prevalence of hypertension</strong> in India <strong>by 25% by 2025</strong>. The activities of IHCI are monitored by the <strong>cardiovascular health officers (CVHO's)</strong> at the state and district level and by the <strong>senior treatment supervisors (STS's)</strong> at the sub-district level. The IHCI has strengthened the prevention of hypertension by ensuring the <strong>provision of antihypertensive medications</strong> for <strong>30 days</strong> to all patients registered under the IHCI.</p>\n<p>The 5 components of IHCI are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Use of <strong>standardized hypertension treatment protocols</strong> approved by national and global policies.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Availability of recommended protocol drugs</strong> to provide a regular and uninterrupted supply of medications.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Task-sharing </strong>and<strong> team-based care</strong> involving nurses, health workers, and ASHAs to improve treatment compliance.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Patient-centered services</strong> provide easy access to BP monitoring at Health &amp; wellness centers and PHCs.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Information systems</strong> that provide real-time BP monitoring to improve patient follow-ups: Use of the <strong>“Simple App”</strong> in all IHCI states.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>*Note: Amlodipine, telmisartan, and chlorthalidone are the drugs of choice in the standardized treatment protocols of the IHCI.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7980","text":"Under the National Program​me for Control of Blindne​ss &amp; Visual Impairment, N‍GO eye hospitals are cons‌idered as:","raw_text":"Under the National Programme for Control of Blindness &amp; Visual Impairment, NGO eye hospitals are considered as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary le​vel centres","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secondary l​evel centres","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tertiary le​vel centres","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intermediate level centres​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Secondary level centres","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b365e6b64d624234a3fcafa66db8b2d2x378x378.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Secondary level centres</strong></p><p>NGO eye hospitals are considered <strong>secondary level centres</strong> under the National Programme for Control of Blindness and Visual Impairment (NPCBVI).</p>\n<p><strong>Levels of eye care centres</strong> according to NPCBVI:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary</strong> level: Sub-district level hospitals, community health centres, mobile ophthalmic units, upgraded PHCs, and link workers.</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary</strong> level: District hospitals and <strong>NGO eye hospitals.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tertiary</strong> level: Regional institutes of ophthalmology and medical colleges.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The goal of NPCBVI is to reduce the prevalence of blindness to 0.25% by 2025.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4732","text":"Which of the following is ​used to measure the outpu​t of the National Program‍me for Control of Blindne‌ss &amp; Visual Impairment?","raw_text":"Which of the following is used to measure the output of the National Programme for Control of Blindness &amp; Visual Impairment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Number of cases of refract​ory errors corrected","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Number of cataract surgeri​es leading to sight resto​ration","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Number of cases of childho​od blindness detected and​ cured","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prevalence of blindness re​duced by 0.5% per year","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Number of cataract surgeries leading to sight restoration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Number of cataract surgeries leading to sight restoration</strong></p><p>The measure of the output of the National Programme for Control of Blindness &amp; Visual Impairment (NPCBVI) is indicated by the <strong>number of cataract surgeries leading to sight restoration.</strong> </p>\n<p>The most common cause of low vision in India is cataract (low vision is visual acuity &lt;6/18- 6/60).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4731","text":"Which of the following dis​eases is not a part of th​e Vision 2020 programme i‍n India?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases is not a part of the Vision 2020 programme in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Refracti​ve errors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetic r​etinopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Corneal ​blindness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Onchoce​rciasis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Onchocerciasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Onchocerciasis</strong></p><p>Onchocerciasis is not a part of the Vision 2020 programme in India. It is a part of the Global Vision 2020 programme.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 192px;\" width=\"329\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158.516px;\"><strong>Global Vision 2020</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.516px;\"><strong>India Vision 2020</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158.516px;\">\n<p>Cataract</p>\n<p>Refractive errors and low vision</p>\n<p>Childhood blindness</p>\n<p><strong>Trachoma</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.516px;\">\n<p>Cataract</p>\n<p>Refractive errors and low vision</p>\n<p>Childhood blindness</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158.516px;\"><strong>Onchocerciasis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.516px;\">Glaucoma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158.516px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.516px;\">Diabetic Retinopathy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158.516px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.516px;\">Corneal blindness</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3644","text":"A pregnant woman from a ru​ral area registers under ​Janani Suraksha Yojana. W‍hich of the following ser‌vices would not be provid​ed to her under this sche‍me?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman from a rural area registers under Janani Suraksha Yojana. Which of the following services would not be provided to her under this scheme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Safe motherhood services u​nder Vandematharam scheme​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cash disbursement for inst​itutional delivery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"STD cou​nseling","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Safe aborti​on services","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. STD counseling","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. STD counseling</strong></p><p>The Janani Suraksha Yojana (JSY) does not provide STD counseling.</p>\n<p><strong>Janani Suraksha Yojana</strong> is a safe motherhood intervention launched under National Rural Health Mission (<strong>NRHM</strong>)<strong> to reduce maternal and neonatal mortality</strong> by promoting institutional delivery among poor pregnant women. The services provided by the JSY are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cash is disbursed</strong> to the mother at the institution for having an <strong>institutional</strong> <strong>delivery</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Safe motherhood services</strong> (IFA tablets, TT vaccination) under the <strong>Vandemataram scheme. </strong></li>\n<li>Village health and nutrition day</li>\n<li>Maternal death review</li>\n<li><strong>Pregnancy</strong> <strong>tracking</strong>: ASHA worker/Anganwadi Worker (AWW) or any other link worker help in monitoring antenatal check-ups, and post-delivery care.</li>\n<li><strong>Safe abortion services</strong>: mifepristone followed by misoprostol and manual vacuum aspiration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4773","text":"Where and how often is the​ Village Health Nutrition​ Day conducted?","raw_text":"Where and how often is the Village Health Nutrition Day conducted?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Once a mo​nth in PHC","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Once a month​ in Anganwadi","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Once every two weeks in PH​C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Once every two weeks in An​ganwadi","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Once a month in Anganwadi","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Once a month in Anganwadi</strong></p><p>Village Health Nutrition Day is conducted <strong>once a month</strong> in the<strong> Anganwadi centres</strong> in the village itself. The villagers interact with health workers and are provided with basic services and information about health care.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4735","text":"A 20-day old baby is gaspi​ng for air and not feedin​g properly. The mother ha‍s no money to go to the n‌earby hospital. Which nat​ional programme has provi‍sions to benefit this wom‌an to take her baby to th‌e health facility?","raw_text":"A 20-day old baby is gasping for air and not feeding properly. The mother has no money to go to the nearby hospital. Which national programme has provisions to benefit this woman to take her baby to the health facility?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Janani Sura​ksha Yojana","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Kar​yakram","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Janani Shishu Suraksha Kar​yakram","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Navjaat Shishu Suraksha Ka​ryakram","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakram","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakram</strong></p><p>The Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakram (<strong>JSSK</strong>) has provisions for <strong>free transport</strong> of <strong>pregnant women </strong>and<strong> sick infants</strong> from:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>home to the health facilities</li>\n<li>between facilities in case of a referral</li>\n<li>back home from the health facilities</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The provisions of JSSK include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Free transport</li>\n<li>Free treatment</li>\n<li>Free drugs and consumables</li>\n<li>Free diagnostics and diet</li>\n<li>Free and cashless delivery</li>\n<li>Free C-Section</li>\n<li>Free provision of blood</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Previously under JSSK, the free entitlements were provided only for sick newborns till 30 days after birth. Now it is expanded to cover <strong>infants</strong> also.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8043","text":"The Navjat Shishu Suraksha​ Karyakram was designed f​or which of the following‍ demographic groups?","raw_text":"The Navjat Shishu Suraksha Karyakram was designed for which of the following demographic groups?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lactatin​g mothers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chil​dren","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Health p​ersonnel","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pregnan​t women","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Health personnel","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Health personnel</strong></p><p>The <strong>Navjat Shishu Suraksha Karyakram</strong> (NSSK) was designed to train <strong>health personnel</strong> in basic <strong>newborn care</strong> and resuscitation.</p>\n<p>The training is aimed to improve the delivery of excellent newborn care at every point and is usually done for <strong>2</strong> <strong>days</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4734","text":"A 10-year-old girl has jus​t started menstruating. U​nder which of the followi‍ng national programmes, s‌he will be provided with ​a pack of sanitary napkin‍s by the ASHA worker in h‌er village?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old girl has just started menstruating. Under which of the following national programmes, she will be provided with a pack of sanitary napkins by the ASHA worker in her village?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Janani Sura​ksha Yojana","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rashtriya Kishor Swasthya ​Karyakram","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sukanya Samr​iddhi Yojana","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kishori Sh​akti Yojana","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rashtriya Kishor Swasthya Karyakram","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/068ec92f207f4c09800d45bfaf47356cx188x269.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rashtriya Kishor Swasthya Karyakram</strong></p><p>The girl will be provided with a pack of 6 sanitary napkins for 6 Rupees (called <strong>Freedays</strong>) by the ASHA worker through the <strong>Menstrual Hygiene Scheme</strong> under the <strong>Rashtriya Kishor Swasthya Karyakram (RKSK).</strong></p>\n<p>RKSK focuses on:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Development of adolescent population (10-19 years).</li>\n<li>Weekly Iron and Folic Acid supplementation (100mg Fe + 500 mcg Folic Acid)</li>\n<li>Menstrual hygiene: A pack of 6 sanitary napkins for 6 Rupees (called <strong>Freedays</strong>) by the ASHA.</li>\n<li>It also includes enhancement of mental health and prevention of substance abuse and violence.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sukanya Samriddhi Yojana </strong>is a government-sponsored <strong>savings program</strong> for parents of girl children &lt;10 years age. It helps parents to set aside money for their female child’s potential <strong>college and marriage expenses</strong> by opening an account at any post office branch or any commercial bank approved by the central government.</p>\n<p><strong>Kishori Shakti Yojana</strong> (KSY) seeks to <strong>empower adolescent girls</strong>, so as to enable them to take charge of their lives. The objectives are to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>improve the nutritional, health and development status of adolescent girls</li>\n<li>promote awareness of health, hygiene, nutrition and family care</li>\n<li>opportunities for learning life skills</li>\n<li>going back to school</li>\n<li>help them gain a better understanding of their social environment</li>\n<li>take initiatives to become productive members of society.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Recent update:</strong> Department of Pharmaceuticals under Ministry of Chemicals and Fertilizers has recently launched<strong> ‘Suvidha’</strong> (<strong>100% Oxo-biodegradable Sanitary Napkin),</strong> under the <strong>Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Janaushadhi Pariyojana (PMBJP) </strong>on World menstrual hygiene day.<strong> </strong>‘Swachhta, Swasthya and Suvidha’ will be available for Rs. 1 per pad over 3200 Janaushadhi Kendras for the underprivileged women of India.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2844","text":"Which among the following ​is not true about POSHAN ​Abhiyan?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not true about POSHAN Abhiyan?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a programme of the M​inistry of Women and Chil​d Development","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The goals are to achieve i​mprovement in neonatal mo​rtality","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Launched on the date of In​ternational Women's Day 2​018","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Targets to reduce low birt​h weight by 2% per annum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The goals are to achieve improvement in neonatal mortality","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/96c51b486a8f4f7dbf96f966654d5432x257x196.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/facf88750a1b46c4896bc72132703314x928x649.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The goals are to achieve improvement in neonatal mortality</strong></p><p>The goal of<strong> POSHAN</strong> Abhiyaan /National Nutrition Mission<strong> </strong>(<strong>NNM</strong>) is to achieve an improvement in<strong> </strong>the <strong>nutritional status </strong>during the next three years beginning 2017-18. It is a flagship programme of the Ministry of Women and Child Development (MWCD). Its <strong>target population</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>children from 0-6 years</li>\n<li>adolescent girls</li>\n<li>pregnant women</li>\n<li>lactating mothers </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The logo is:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following image depicts the targets of this mission:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: <strong>Nikshay Poshan Yojana</strong> is a government scheme in India to provide <strong>Rs.500 per month</strong> for <strong>TB</strong> patients to buy food.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4750","text":"Which of the following is ​the objective of the Ujja​wala Scheme?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the objective of the Ujjawala Scheme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Provide LPG connections to​ families below poverty l​ine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eliminate violence against​ children","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prevent t​rafficking","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Provide LED bulbs in every​ household","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Prevent trafficking","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Prevent trafficking</strong></p><p>The <strong>Ujjawala Scheme</strong> is for the <strong>prevention of</strong> <strong>trafficking</strong> and for the rescue, rehabilitation, and reintegration of victims of trafficking for commercial sexual exploitation.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Ujjwala Yojana (note spelling without \"a\")</strong> is for providing 5 crores of free cooking <strong>LPG connections</strong> to all families below the poverty line till 2019.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Ujala Scheme</strong> (Unnat Jyoti by Affordable LEDs and Appliances for All) is the largest domestic lighting project in the world, launched under the Ministry of Power. It aims at promoting the use of <strong>energy-efficient LED bulbs</strong> in every household in the country.</p>\n<p><strong>INSPIRE </strong>strategy is a set of 7 evidence-based strategies introduced to select, implement, monitor, and evaluate effective policies, programs, and services to <strong>prevent</strong> and respond to <strong>violence against children.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8048","text":"An ASHA worker is offering​ services under the Swach​h Bharat Abhiyan in her v‍illage. All of the follow‌ing services will be prov​ided by her except:","raw_text":"An ASHA worker is offering services under the Swachh Bharat Abhiyan in her village. All of the following services will be provided by her except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Water pollu​tion control","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Solid waste​ management","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Household toilets and publ​ic toilets","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Information, education, co​mmunication and public aw​areness","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Water pollution control","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Water pollution control</strong></p><p>Water pollution is not managed under the Swachh Bharat Abhiyan.</p>\n<p>The Swachh Bharat Abhiyan was launched in 2014 with an aim to <strong>eradicate open-air defecation</strong> by the year 2019 through the construction of 12 million toilets. The two sub-missions of Swachh Bharat Abhiyan are <strong>Swachh Bharat Mission Gramin (SBM-G)</strong> and <strong>Swachh Bharat Mission Urban (SBM-U)</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Components</strong> of this mission include: <br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Household toilets including improving unsanitary latrines</li>\n<li>Community toilets</li>\n<li>Public toilets</li>\n<li>Solid waste management</li>\n<li>Information, education, communication, and public awareness</li>\n<li>Capacity building and administrative &amp; office expenses</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5275","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about th​e Ayushman Bharat-Nationa‍l Health Protection Missi‌on?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about the Ayushman Bharat-National Health Protection Mission?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Government provides health​ insurance cover of up to​ Rs. 5,00,000 per family ‍per year","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The limit of the family si​ze that is covered is up ​to 7 members","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"All pre-existing diseases ​of the individual are cov​ered","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It covers secondary and te​rtiary care hospitalizati​on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The limit of the family size that is covered is up to 7 members","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/abdb823c778f4f939f358d9f3f5ecdddx647x374.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ca7b32dbf41a439da3ebdc34be2d8613x680x365.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The limit of the family size that is covered is up to 7 members</strong></p><p>There is<strong> no cap</strong> on family size or the age of the members. All the families listed under the Socio-Economic Caste Census (SECC) database are covered as per the defined criteria.</p>\n<p>The following are the benefits provided under this program:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Health insurance cover of up to <strong>Rs. 5,00,000 per family per year.</strong></li>\n<li>All families listed in the SECC database, as per the defined criteria, will be covered. There is <strong>no cap on family size and age of members.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Free treatment</strong> is available at<strong> all public and empanelled private hospitals</strong></li>\n<li>Both <strong>secondary and tertiary care </strong>hospitalization is covered.</li>\n<li>All <strong>pre-existing diseases </strong>are <strong>covered. </strong>Hospitals <strong>cannot deny treatment.</strong></li>\n<li>To control costs, the payments for treatment will be done on a package rate (to be defined by the Government in advance) basis.</li>\n<li>Cashless and paperless access to quality health care services.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Logo:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4963","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true regarding​ Rajiv Gandhi Shramik Kal‍yan Yojana?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding Rajiv Gandhi Shramik Kalyan Yojana?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unemployment allowance is ​not given in case of clos​ure of factory","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Unemployment allowance is ​given in case of employme​nt injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Allowance is given at 50% ​of the daily wage for a m​aximum of 2 years","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"To qualify for the scheme,​ worker must have worked ​for more than 2 years","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Allowance is given at 50% of the daily wage for a maximum of 2 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Allowance is given at 50% of the daily wage for a maximum of 2 years</strong></p><p>Unemployment allowance under Rajiv Gandhi Shramik Kalyan Yojana (RGSKY)<strong> </strong>is given at the rate of <strong>50% of daily wage</strong> for a maximum of <strong>2 years.</strong></p>\n<p>RGSKY is a scheme of ESI that provides <strong>u</strong><strong>nemployment allowance</strong> to an insured person going out of employment <strong>involuntarily</strong>, on account of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>closure</strong> of a factory or establishment </li>\n<li><strong>retrenchment</strong> (laying off employees due to economic difficulty)</li>\n<li><strong>permanent disability</strong> due to <strong>nonemployment</strong> injury</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Allowance and eligibility criteria of Rajiv Gandhi Shramik Kalyan Yojana (updated in 2019):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The allowance is given at the rate of <strong>50% </strong>of the<strong> daily wage</strong> for a maximum of <strong>2 years.</strong></li>\n<li>To qualify for the scheme the worker must have worked in that setup for <strong>&gt;3 years</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Disability due to<strong> employment injury </strong>is covered under disablement benefits of <strong>ESI Act, 1948.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Temporary disability: 90% </strong>of wages until disablement lasts.</li>\n<li><strong>Permanent disability: 90% </strong>of wages as a <strong>life pension </strong>on the basis of loss of earning capacity.</li>\n<li><strong>Dependants benefit:</strong> <strong>90% </strong>of wage as monthly payment to the dependants of the deceased person, in cases where death occurs due to employment injury or occupational hazards.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2376","text":"Which of the following doe​s the Laqshya initiative ​launched recently by the ‍Government of India refer‌ to?","raw_text":"Which of the following does the Laqshya initiative launched recently by the Government of India refer to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Initiative for providing f​ree sanitary pads","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Initiative to provide vacc​ination to under-5 childr​en","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Initiative for labour room​ quality improvement","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Initiative to provide affo​rdable healthcare to all","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Initiative for labour room quality improvement","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Initiative for labour room quality improvement</strong></p><p>The Laqshya initiative launched recently by the Government of India refers to the initiative for<strong> labour room quality </strong>improvement.</p>\n<p>There is enough evidence to support the fact that improving the quality of care in labour rooms, especially on the day of birth, is crucial to maternal and neonatal survival.</p>\n<p><strong>Laqshya</strong> is expected to improve the quality of care being provided to the pregnant mother in the <strong>labour room </strong>and <strong>maternity operation theatres</strong>, thereby preventing the undesirable adverse outcomes associated with childbirth. This initiative is implemented in government medical colleges besides district hospitals, and high delivery load sub-district hospitals, and community health centres.</p>\n<p>It plans to conduct <strong>quality certification</strong> of labour rooms and also <strong>incentivize facilities</strong> achieving the targets outlined. The goal is to <strong>reduce preventable maternal and new-born mortality, morbidity,</strong> and <strong>stillbirths</strong> associated with the care around delivery in labour room and maternity OT, and ensure respectful maternity care.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3833","text":"The image below shows the ​logo of which of the foll​owing national programmes‍?","raw_text":"The image below shows the logo of which of the following national programmes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beti bachao, Beti padhao p​rogramme","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"India New Born Action Plan​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Janani Shishu Suraksha Kar​yakram","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Family Plann​ing Campaign","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Family Planning Campaign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7e635c3bb208439da60e223729cfb243.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1df31adbd0f847eebee49a689f1082adx291x277.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/97f7c5d17d9240f8b3e9e9096c83af1fx260x229.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8999bde1e1d94870891948c1da345641x401x440.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Family Planning Campaign</strong></p><p>The image shown is the new logo of the 360-degree holistic <strong>Family Planning Campaign</strong>.</p>\n<p>Logo of other options:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>\"Beti bachao Beti padhao.\"</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>India Newborn Action Plan (INAP)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The logo of Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakaram<strong> </strong>(JSSK)</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5970","text":"What is the main objective​ of the Pradhan Mantri Bh​artiya Jan-aushadhi Pariy‍ojana?","raw_text":"What is the main objective of the Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Jan-aushadhi Pariyojana?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Improve maternal and child​ health services","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Make quality, generic medi​cines affordable to all","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Enhance quality of life an​d increase employment for​ the differently abled","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Promote cleanliness and en​hance quality of healthca​re facilities in India","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Make quality, generic medicines affordable to all","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9d15b788f21c4d6090d9c167acab582bx153x127.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Make quality, generic medicines affordable to all</strong></p><p>The <strong>Pradhan Mantri Bhartiya Jan-aushadhi Pariyojana (PMBJP)</strong> carries the objective of making<strong> quality, generic medicines affordable for everyone,</strong> especially the lower socio-economic groups.</p>\n<p><strong>BPPI </strong>(Bureau of Pharma PSUs of India), under the Ministry of Chemicals &amp; Fertilizers, is the implementation agency for PMBJP. The mission of this programme includes creating awareness and demand for generic medicines through education and supply of such medications.</p>\n<p><strong>Kayakalp</strong> programme is a<strong> </strong>national initiative launched by the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare (MoHFW) under Swachh Bharat Abhiyan to <strong>promote</strong> <strong>cleanliness</strong> and enhance the quality of healthcare facilities in India.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7002","text":"The Mid Day Meal Programme​ aims to supplement:","raw_text":"The Mid Day Meal Programme aims to supplement:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1/2 protein and 1/3 calori​e requirements","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1/3 protein and 1/3 calori​e requirements","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1/2 protein and 1/2 calori​e requirements","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1/3 protein and 1/2 calori​e requirements","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1/2 protein and 1/3 calorie requirements","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1/2 protein and 1/3 calorie requirements</strong></p><p>Under the<strong> Mid-Day Meal</strong> (MDM) <strong>scheme</strong>, supplementary nutrition provided must cover at least<strong> 1/2 of total daily protein requirement and 1/3rd of total daily energy requirement</strong> of children in schools.</p>\n<p>The MDM scheme is also called <strong>School Lunch Programme</strong> and has been operational since 1961 in India. Currently, it functions under the broad coverage of the<strong> Minimum Needs Programme.</strong></p>\n<p>Some other principles of the MDM are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The meal provided under the MDM should only be a <strong>supplement</strong> and not a substitute for home diets.</li>\n<li>The<strong> cost </strong>of meals should be <strong>reasonably low.</strong></li>\n<li>The meal should be <strong>easy to cook</strong> in schools.</li>\n<li><strong>Locally available foods</strong> must be used, for as far as possible.</li>\n<li>The menu should be <strong>regularly changed</strong> to avoid monotony.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: In September 2021, the Mid-Day Meal Scheme was renamed <strong>PM POSHAN</strong> or Pradhan Mantri Poshan Shakti Nirman.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3599","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is not true regar​ding the Vandematharam sc‍heme?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true regarding the Vandematharam scheme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Free treatment in private ​clinics","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Its aim is to provide safe​ motherhood services","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"An MBBS graduate or an obs​tetrician can volunteer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IFA tablets and TT injecti​ons are provided by Distr​ict Medical Officers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Free treatment in private clinics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Free treatment in private clinics</strong></p><p>The Vandematharam scheme does not include free treatment in private clinics.</p>\n<p><strong>Vandematharam</strong> scheme is a <strong>voluntary</strong> scheme for providing<strong> safe motherhood services</strong>. It can be volunteered by an obstetrician, maternity home, nursing home, lady doctor or an MBBS graduate.</p>\n<p><strong>IFA</strong> tablets, <strong>OCPs</strong>, and <strong>TT</strong> <strong>injections</strong> are provided by the respective District Medical Officers (<strong>DMOs</strong>) to the Vandematharam doctors or clinics for free distribution to beneficiaries. Cases needing special care and treatment can be referred to government hospitals.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6578","text":"Which of the following is ​a component of the scheme​ depicted by the logo giv‍en below?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a component of the scheme depicted by the logo given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"National Health Protection​ Mission","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pradhan Mantri Surakshit M​atritva Abhiyaan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"National Programme for Fam​ily Planning","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"National Rural Health Miss​ion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. National Health Protection Mission","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c0fe8156b53b457a97272c41bdb9eb95.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2c91218fbfc947b5b10d7bc5c40d6599x814x470.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3a35c805d486404dba97c8ff60f2c680x720x386.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. National Health Protection Mission</strong></p><p><strong>National Health Protection Mission</strong> is a component of the Ayushman Bharat scheme.</p>\n<p>Ayushman Bharat-National Health Protection Mission (<strong>AB-NHPM</strong>) aims to build a comprehensive need-based health care service and targets to build a '<strong>New</strong> <strong>India 2022'</strong>. Its components are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Creation of 1,50,000 <strong>Health and Wellness Centres- </strong>these centres will provide Comprehensive Primary Health Care (CPHC), covering both maternal and child health services and non-communicable diseases, including free essential drugs and diagnostic services. </li>\n<li><strong>National Health Protection Mission (NHPM) </strong>provides health protection cover to poor and vulnerable families, screening for non-communicable diseases, and follow-up of hospitalization cases among others.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3><strong>Beneficiary Level: </strong></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The government provides health insurance cover of up to <strong>Rs. 5,00,000 per family per year.</strong></li>\n<li>All families listed in the Socio-Economic Caste Census (SECC) database as per defined criteria will be covered. <strong>No cap on family size and age of members.</strong></li>\n<li>Priority to a girl child, women, and senior citizens.</li>\n<li><strong>Free treatment</strong> is available at<strong> all public and empanelled private hospitals </strong>in times of need.</li>\n<li>Covers <strong>secondary and tertiary care </strong>hospitalization.</li>\n<li>1,350 medical packages covering surgery, medical and daycare treatments, cost of medicines, and diagnostics.</li>\n<li>All <strong>pre-existing diseases </strong>are <strong>covered. </strong>Hospitals <strong>cannot deny treatment.</strong></li>\n<li>Cashless and paperless access to quality health care services.</li>\n<li>Hospitals will not be allowed to charge any additional money from beneficiaries for the treatment.</li>\n<li>Can reach out for information, assistance, complaints, and grievances to a 24X7 helpline number.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7000","text":"Which of the following vac​cines has not been introd​uced to the immunization ‍programme in India?","raw_text":"Which of the following vaccines has not been introduced to the immunization programme in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adult Japanese encephaliti​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infl​uenza","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Measles​-Rubella","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rota​virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Influenza","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Influenza</strong></p><p>Influenza vaccine (Flu shot) has not been introduced to the National Immunization Programme in India. </p>\n<p>The Influenza vaccine provides protection against <strong>viral flu</strong> caused by the <strong>influenza virus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Bacterial</strong> Hemophilus influenzae type-b <strong>(Hib)</strong> vaccine is different from the <strong>viral</strong> influenza <strong>(Flu)</strong> vaccine. HiB is a part of the pentavalent vaccine.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7011","text":"The POCSO Act is related t​o which of the following?​","raw_text":"The POCSO Act is related to which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Women em​powerment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Human tr​afficking","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Juvenile d​elinquency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Child sex​ual abuse","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Child sexual abuse","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Child sexual abuse</strong></p><p>The POCSO (Protection of Children from Sexual Offences) Act is related to <strong>child sexual abuse.</strong></p>\n<p>POCSO Act defines a child as any person <strong>below eighteen years </strong>of age. It defines different forms of sexual abuse, including <strong>penetrative</strong> and <strong>non-penetrative</strong> <strong>assault</strong>, as well as <strong>sexual harassment</strong> and <strong>pornography</strong>.</p>\n<p>The Act deems a sexual assault to be \"<strong>aggravated\"</strong> under certain circumstances, such as when the abused child is <strong>mentally ill</strong> or when the abuse is committed <strong>by a person in a position of trust or authority</strong> vis-à-vis the child, like a family member, police officer, teacher, or doctor.</p>\n<p>People who <strong>traffic children</strong> for sexual purposes are also punishable under the provisions relating to abetment in the Act. The Act prescribes stringent punishment graded as per the gravity of the offence, with a maximum term of rigorous imprisonment for life and a fine.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0181","text":"Mass drug administration i​s not done for which of t​he following conditions?","raw_text":"Mass drug administration is not done for which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Worm inf​estation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin A ​deficiency","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sca​bies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Filar​iasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vitamin A deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vitamin A deficiency</strong></p><p>Mass drug administration is not done in case of vitamin A deficiency. </p>\n<p>Mass Drug Administration (<strong>MDA</strong>) is the administration of a drug to an entire <strong>concerned population irrespective of age and gender</strong>. For example, antimalarials are given as MDA in malaria-endemic island communities and in low-endemic non-island settings approaching elimination, where there is minimal risk of re-introduction of infection, good access to treatment, and implementation of vector control and surveillance.</p>\n<p><strong>Vitamin A </strong>prophylaxis is given under National Immunization Schedule (NIS) <strong>only</strong> to children <strong>up to 5 years.</strong> It is given as <strong>mass prophylaxis</strong> and <strong>not</strong> as a drug<strong> for treatment.</strong> Vitamin A as a treatment is given only to a child with features of vitamin A deficiency and such doses are not instituted to everyone in the community. Therefore, it is not considered under MDA.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, MDA is done in scabies (ivermectin), worm infestation (albendazole), and filariasis (DEC + albendazole).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0721","text":"Monetary benefit is measur​ed in which of the follow​ing analyses?","raw_text":"Monetary benefit is measured in which of the following analyses?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cost effect​ive analysis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cost benef​it analysis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Network ​analysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Input Outp​ut analysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cost benefit analysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cost benefit analysis</strong></p><p>The monetary benefit is measured in a <strong>cost-benefit analysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>In<strong> cost-benefit analysis, </strong>the <strong>economic benefits</strong> of any program are compared with the cost of the program. The benefits are expressed in monetary terms to determine whether a given programme is economically sound and to select the best out of several alternate programmes.</p>\n<p>The main drawback<strong> </strong>with this technique is that the benefits in the health field, as a result of a particular programme, cannot always be expressed in monetary terms. We generally express the benefit in terms of births or deaths prevented, or illness avoided or overcome.</p>\n<p>In<strong> cost-effective analysis</strong>, the benefit is expressed in terms of <strong>results achieved</strong>. (Eg: number of lives saved or number of days free from disease).</p>\n<p><strong>Input-output analysis</strong>: how much input is needed to produce a unit amount of output. In the health field, <strong>input</strong> refers to all health service activities which consume resources (manpower, money, materials, and time); and <strong>output</strong> refers to useful outcomes such as cases treated, lives saved, or inoculations performed.</p>\n<p><strong>Network analysis: </strong>a network is a graphic plan of all events and activities to be completed in order to reach an end objective.</p>\n<p><strong class=\"ng-binding\">Management Methods in Health Programmes:</strong></p>\n<table width=\"564\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"210\">\n<p><strong>Methods based on behavioral sciences</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"354\">\n<p><strong>Quantitative methods</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"210\">\n<p>Organisational design</p>\n<p>Personnel management</p>\n<p>Communication</p>\n<p>Information systems</p>\n<p>Management by objectives (MBO)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"354\">\n<p>Cost–Benefit analysis</p>\n<p>Cost-Effective analysis</p>\n<p>Cost–Accounting</p>\n<p>Input–Output analysis</p>\n<p>Model</p>\n<p>Systems analysis</p>\n<p>Network analysis- Includes Programme Evaluation and Review Technique (PERT) and Critical Path Method (CPM)</p>\n<p>Planning–Programming–Budgeting System (PPBS)</p>\n<p>Work sampling</p>\n<p>Decision making</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3076","text":"As per NCEP- ATP III, whic​h of the following is not​ a criterion for metaboli‍c syndrome?","raw_text":"As per NCEP- ATP III, which of the following is not a criterion for metabolic syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypertrigl​yceridemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High​ LDL","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Central​ obesity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypert​ension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. High LDL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. High LDL</strong></p><p>As per NCEP- ATP III, high LDL is not included in the definition criteria of metabolic syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Metabolic syndrome</strong> is present if <strong>three or more</strong> of the following five criteria are met, which is given by NCEP-ATP III (National Cholesterol Education Program and Adult Treatment Panel III). It includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Central obesity</strong>: waist circumference &gt;102 cm (M), &gt;88 cm (F)</li>\n<li><strong>Hypertriglyceridemia</strong>: triglyceride level ≥ 150 mg/dL</li>\n<li><strong>Low HDL</strong> cholesterol: &lt;40 mg/dL and &lt;50 mg/dL for men and women, respectively.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: blood pressure ≥130 mmHg systolic or ≥ 85 mmHg diastolic.</li>\n<li><strong>Fasting plasma glucose level</strong>: ≥100 mg/dL or previously diagnosed type 2 diabetes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0718","text":"In Sustainable Development​ Goal 3, the target 3.1 a​ims to reduce the Materna‍l Mortality Ratio by 2030‌ to ____ (per 1 lakh live​ births):","raw_text":"In Sustainable Development Goal 3, the target 3.1 aims to reduce the Maternal Mortality Ratio by 2030 to ____ (per 1 lakh live births):","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt​;70","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt​;100","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt​;50","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt​;130","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. <70","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4d001dce90e2480f9b10d26c16d93dd1x1280x816.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. &lt;70</strong></p><p>In Sustainable Development Goal 3, the target 3.1 aims to reduce the Maternal Mortality Ratio by 2030 to <strong>&lt;70 per 1 lakh live births</strong>.</p>\n<p>There are <strong>17 global goals</strong> and 169 targets set for <strong>2030</strong> as part of the official <strong>Sustainable Development Goals </strong>of the <strong>Agenda 2030.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME1154","text":"As per the new National Po​licy for Rare Diseases, f​inancial support of up to‍ Rs. 20 lakhs can be give‌n to which group of rare ​diseases?","raw_text":"As per the new National Policy for Rare Diseases, financial support of up to Rs. 20 lakhs can be given to which group of rare diseases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gro​up 1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gro​up 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gro​up 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gro​up 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Group 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Group 1</strong></p><p>As per the new National Policy for Rare Diseases, a provision for financial support of up to <strong>Rs.</strong> <strong>20 lakhs</strong> under the umbrella scheme of <strong>Rashtriya Arogya Nidhi</strong> is proposed for the treatment of rare diseases that require a <strong>one-time treatment</strong> (listed under <strong>Group 1</strong> in the rare disease policy). </p>\n<p>National Policy for Rare Diseases is a comprehensive policy for the prevention and management of rare diseases. The policy lists rare diseases under <strong>3 groups:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Group 1: </strong>Disorders amenable to<strong> one-time curative treatment:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Disorders amenable to hematopoietic stem cell transplantation eg: lysosomal storage disorders</li>\n<li>Disorders amenable to organ transplantation eg: glycogen storage disorders, urea cycle disorders, ADPKD, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Group 2: </strong>Diseases requiring<strong> long term/ lifelong treatment </strong>having a <strong>relatively lower cost </strong>of treatment:\n<ul>\n<li>This group includes disorders managed with special dietary formulae/ hormone/ specific drugs. eg: phenylketonuria, osteogenesis imperfecta, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Group 3: </strong>Diseases for which <strong>definitive treatment</strong> is available but treatment is <strong>lifelong or expensive:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>This group includes diseases such as Pompe disease, Duchenne muscular dystrophy, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>objectives</strong> of the policy are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lowering the high cost of treatment for rare diseases with an increased focus on indigenous research.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Creating a national hospital-based registry of rare diseases. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Early screening and prevention through primary and secondary health care infrastructure, and through counselling for the high-risk parents.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Crowdfunding mechanism in which corporates and individuals will be encouraged to extend financial support.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6687","text":"You are a medical officer ​at a PHC. Which of the fo​llowing would you advise ‍for mass prophylaxis/ tre‌atment for anemia?","raw_text":"You are a medical officer at a PHC. Which of the following would you advise for mass prophylaxis/ treatment for anemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"FeSO4 + Vit C + Folate + V​it B12","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Iron (III) hydroxide polym​altose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ferrous ​fumarate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inj. iro​n sucrose","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. FeSO4 + Vit C + Folate + Vit B12","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. FeSO4 + Vit C + Folate + Vit B12</strong></p><p>Among the given options, <strong>FeSO4 + vitamin C + Folate + vitamin B12</strong> may be considered for <strong>mass prophylaxis</strong> against anemia.</p>\n<p><strong>Vit C</strong> <strong>enhances</strong> the <strong>absorption</strong> <strong>of</strong> <strong>iron</strong> from the stomach by acting as a reducing substance. </p>\n<p><strong>Subclinical folate deficiency</strong> is seen in up to 30% of North Indian pregnant women. Moreover, it may be masked by widespread iron deficiency. It is, for this reason, iron and folate supplementation is carried out in national programmes. </p>\n<p>Clinical vitamin B12 deficiency is not commonly encountered, but subclinical cases might be seen in up to 30% of adults and children due to widespread restriction to consumption of vegetarian products only. Hence supplementing vitamin B12 is a good idea provided the cost factor is not an issue.</p>\n<p>Option B: Iron (III) hydroxide polymaltose was marketed as being superior to iron sulfate in terms of efficacy, but it lacked any data to back up the evidence.</p>\n<p>Option C: Ferrous fumarate may be considered for the treatment of iron deficiency anemia with similar efficacy as that of ferrous sulfate.</p>\n<p>Option D: Injection iron sucrose or any parenteral form is not used as a first-line option. It is only indicated if the oral form is not tolerated.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"2IMJYyl7","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"25_trauma_of_nose_and_face","title":"25.Trauma Of Nose And Face","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB1439","text":"A biker met with an accide​nt and sustained a maxill​ofacial injury. Which of ​the following is the firs‌t step in the primary car​e of this patient?","raw_text":"A biker met with an accident and sustained a maxillofacial injury. Which of the following is the first step in the primary care of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Assessment of level of con​sciousness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ensure adeq​uate airway","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Control of​ hemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fracture ​reduction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ensure adequate airway","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ensure adequate airway</strong></p><p>The <strong>first step</strong> in <strong>primary care</strong> of a patient with a <strong>maxillofacial injury</strong> is securing an<strong> adequate airway</strong>.</p>\n<p>Evaluation of facial fractures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A &amp; B – Airway &amp; Breathing:</strong> the first objective is to ensure an adequate airway by intubation or tracheostomy.</li>\n<li><strong>C – Circulation: </strong>control of haemorrhage by pressure or ligation of bleeding vessels.</li>\n<li>Rule out head and spinal injuries.</li>\n<li>Neurological evaluation.</li>\n<li>Examine for injuries in other parts of the body.</li>\n<li>Care of the wound.</li>\n<li>Documentation of the injuries for medico-legal purposes.</li>\n<li>Examination of the facial skeleton – mandible, maxilla, zygomatic and orbital fracture.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1442","text":"Which of the following fac​ial bones is most commonl​y fractured?","raw_text":"Which of the following facial bones is most commonly fractured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zygomat​ic bone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nasal​ bone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Max​illa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mand​ible","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nasal bone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nasal bone</strong></p><p>The <strong>most</strong> <strong>commonly fractured</strong> bone of the <strong>face</strong> is the <strong>nasal bone</strong> because of the projection of the nose on the face.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9783","text":"A 30-year-old man presente​d with a nasal bleed afte​r sustaining a blow to th​e nose in a fistfight. On‌ evaluation, a nasal sept​al fracture similar to th​e one in the image below ‍was identified. What is y​our diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presented with a nasal bleed after sustaining a blow to the nose in a fistfight. On evaluation, a nasal septal fracture similar to the one in the image below was identified. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jarjaway​ fracture","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Citelli ​fracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chevallet​ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tripod ​fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Jarjaway fracture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/253ce3e1e6f5437e82ab588585845cd4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bc2b2deab78446dd825aeb75be2bedc6x1280x2283.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Jarjaway fracture</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of nasal bleed following trauma and the image showing a horizontal fracture line is suggestive of <strong>Jarjaway fracture</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a fracture of the nasal septum that results from direct <strong>blows from the front</strong>. The fracture line runs <strong>horizontally</strong> from just above the anterior nasal spine to just above the junction of septal cartilage with vomer. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Chevallet fracture</strong> results from <strong>blows from below. </strong>The fracture line runs <strong>vertically</strong> from the anterior nasal spine upwards to the junction of the bony and cartilaginous dorsum of the nose.</p>\n<p>Option D: Tripod fracture is another name for zygoma fracture.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1448","text":"A young adult presents wit​h a deformed nose after f​alling from a ladder. The​ CT scan shows a displace‌d nasal fracture. What is​ the ideal time to reduce​ the fracture in this pat‍ient?","raw_text":"A young adult presents with a deformed nose after falling from a ladder. The CT scan shows a displaced nasal fracture. What is the ideal time to reduce the fracture in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Within 12 hours following ​the injury.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5-7 days fol​lowing injury","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"After 2 wee​ks of injury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"After 4 wee​ks of injury","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5-7 days following injury","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5-7 days following injury</strong></p><p>The <strong>ideal time</strong> <strong>for reduction</strong> of fracture of the nasal bone is <strong>5-7 days</strong> following injury, once the edema has subsided.</p>\n<p>The presence of edema interferes with the accurate reduction of the fracture. In most cases, the deformity is due to edema. So, ice packs and head elevation are advised initially after injury. After 3-5 days, the edema would subside and the underlying deformity will be apparent on clinical assessment.</p>\n<p><strong>After 2 weeks</strong> it is <strong>difficult to reduce</strong> since the <strong>process of callus formation</strong> and the bony and fibrous union would have started.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1450","text":"As an ENT resident, you ar​e assisting in the reduct​ion of a nasal bone fract​ure. Which of the followi‌ng instruments would you ​use?","raw_text":"As an ENT resident, you are assisting in the reduction of a nasal bone fracture. Which of the following instruments would you use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/338c415a8e2a4c2181660ee503427b87.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f76cd7470c634094979e04d75e61c380x600x312.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dd7945010cf14a8e9fb0c3c5cfa8d5e8x600x312.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c1bb8efdb554f7fa522a0961dc8855ex1280x1274.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a05a27ef5d20400fbf8543bc97137344x599x472.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a93d5a10b62941b0818de544ed0c35b3x600x495.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2</strong></p><p><strong>Walsham forceps</strong> (Image 2) is used to reduce a <strong>nasal bone fracture</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Asch forceps</strong> is mainly used to reduce <strong>nasal septum fracture.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option A: Luc’s forceps is used in Caldwell Luc operation, submucous resection, and polypectomy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option C: Tilley’s forceps is used in nasal packing in the epistaxis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option D: Citelli’s forceps is used for bone punch.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1452","text":"A boxer is wheeled into th​e ER with complaints of s​eeing double after having​ suffered a severe blow t‌o his face during a match​. On examination, there i​s a flattening of malar p‍rominence, trismus and a ​step deformity of the inf‌raorbital margin. What is‌ the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A boxer is wheeled into the ER with complaints of seeing double after having suffered a severe blow to his face during a match. On examination, there is a flattening of malar prominence, trismus and a step deformity of the infraorbital margin. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fracture of​ nasal bone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fracture ​of maxilla","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fracture of ​orbital floor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fracture ​of zygoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fracture of zygoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/825ffbc06edb435f8f1bf566924ba082x673x645.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fracture of zygoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of diplopia, <strong>flat malar prominence</strong>,<strong> trismus</strong>, and step deformity in the infraorbital margin is suggestive of <strong>fracture of zygoma</strong> or <strong>tripod fracture</strong>.</p>\n<p>Zygoma is the second most frequently fractured bone in the face. The zygoma fracture is separated at its three processes - the zygomaticofrontal suture, infraorbital margin, and the zygomaticotemporal suture. It has the following clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Flattening of malar prominence. </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Trismus</strong>, due to depression of zygoma on the underlying coronoid process. </li>\n<li><strong>Anesthesia</strong> in the distribution of the <strong>infraorbital nerve. </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Step deformity</strong> of infraorbital margin.</li>\n<li>Oblique palpebral fissure, due to the displacement of lateral palpebral ligament. </li>\n<li><strong>Restricted ocular movements</strong>, due to entrapment of <strong>inferior rectus muscle</strong>. It may cause <strong>diplopia</strong>. </li>\n<li><strong>Periorbital emphysema</strong>, due to escape of air from the maxillary sinus on nose blowing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following x-ray shows a right-sided zygoma fracture:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1453","text":"You are an ENT resident ev​aluating a patient with a​ fracture of the zygoma. ​Which of the following fi‌ndings would you not expe​ct to see in this patient​?","raw_text":"You are an ENT resident evaluating a patient with a fracture of the zygoma. Which of the following findings would you not expect to see in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tri​smus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dipl​opia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CSF rhi​norrhea","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Infraorbita​l anesthesia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CSF rhinorrhea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8bcecc844a294fcbab635b567c7d9d33x673x645.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CSF rhinorrhea</strong></p><p>CSF rhinorrhea is not seen in patients with fractures of the zygoma.</p>\n<p>It has the following clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Flattening of malar prominence</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Step deformity of infraorbital margin</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Anesthesia</strong> in the distribution of the <strong>infraorbital nerve</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Trismus</strong>, due to depression of zygoma on the underlying coronoid process.</li>\n<li>Oblique palpebral fissure, due to the displacement of lateral palpebral ligament.</li>\n<li><strong>Restricted ocular movements</strong>, due to entrapment of <strong>inferior rectus muscle</strong>. It may cause <strong>diplopia</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Periorbital emphysema</strong>, due to escape of air from the maxillary sinus on nose blowing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following x-ray shows a right-sided zygoma fracture:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1455","text":"A patient with a tripod fr​acture is having decrease​d sensation over the chee​k. Which of the following‌ nerve injuries can resul​t in this?","raw_text":"A patient with a tripod fracture is having decreased sensation over the cheek. Which of the following nerve injuries can result in this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supraorbi​tal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infraorbi​tal nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lingua​l nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Facial​ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Infraorbital nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Infraorbital nerve</strong></p><p><strong>Infraorbital nerve injury </strong>results in <strong>decreased sensation</strong> over the <strong>cheek</strong> in tripod(zygomatic) fracture.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1458","text":"Which of the following is ​called blow-out fractures​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is called blow-out fractures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fracture of floor of orbit​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fracture of lateral wall o​f orbit","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fracture of medial wall of​ orbit","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fracture of ​roof of orbit","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fracture of floor of orbit","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fracture of floor of orbit</strong></p><p><strong>Fracture of the floor of the orbit</strong> is called <strong>blow-out fracture. </strong></p>\n<p>It occurs when a large blunt object strikes the globe.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1461","text":"A teenage boy suffered tra​uma to his left eye while​ playing cricket. He is r​ushed to the ER in severe‌ pain. His X-ray appears ​as below. What is the cha​racteristic sign noted?","raw_text":"A teenage boy suffered trauma to his left eye while playing cricket. He is rushed to the ER in severe pain. His X-ray appears as below. What is the characteristic sign noted?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tear dr​op sign","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Phelp’​s sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Holman Mi​ller sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antra​l sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tear drop sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f12f63245bd945c5a61daeebb9896bcf.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2e4a340ce9c340aca18aeb16283fea18x371x403.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tear drop sign</strong></p><p>The given X-ray shows the <strong>tear-drop sign. </strong>It is seen in <strong>orbital floor</strong> or <strong>blow-out fractures</strong> when the orbital contents herniate into the maxillary antrum.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Phelp’s sign</strong> is seen in CT scan in<strong> glomus jugulare tumor</strong>. It is the erosion of the normal crest of bone between the carotid canal and jugular foramen.</p>\n<p>Options C and D:<strong> Antral </strong>and<strong> Holman Miller signs </strong>are the same. It is the anterior bowing of the posterior wall of the maxillary sinus and is pathognomonic of <strong>juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1464","text":"A 19-year-old college stud​ent presented with compla​ints of seeing double aft​er sustaining a blow to h‌is face in a scuffle with​ his seniors. On examinat​ion, there is a step defo‍rmity and periorbital emp​hysema. Entrapment of whi‌ch of the following muscl‌es is likely to be respon‍sible for his double visi​on?","raw_text":"A 19-year-old college student presented with complaints of seeing double after sustaining a blow to his face in a scuffle with his seniors. On examination, there is a step deformity and periorbital emphysema. Entrapment of which of the following muscles is likely to be responsible for his double vision?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior​ oblique","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferio​r rectus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Superio​r rectus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medial​ rectus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inferior rectus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inferior rectus</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with diplopia, periorbital emphysema, and a step deformity can be seen in both orbital floor fracture as well as tripod fracture. In both conditions <strong>diplopia</strong> is due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Entrapment of inferior rectus</strong> and inferior oblique muscles</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Displacement of the eyeball.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The common features seen in both orbital floor fracture and tripod fracture are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Periorbital emphysema</li>\n<li>Step deformity of infraorbital margin</li>\n<li>Diplopia</li>\n<li>Enophthalmos.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1465","text":"In which of the following ​fractures Le-Fort classif​ication is used?","raw_text":"In which of the following fractures Le-Fort classification is used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nasal​ bone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Max​illa","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mand​ible","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zyg​oma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Maxilla","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Maxilla</strong></p><p><strong>Le-Fort classification</strong> is used in the fracture of the <strong>maxilla. </strong></p>\n<p>The 3 types are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Le Fort I (transverse fracture)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Le Fort II (pyramidal fracture)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Le Fort III (craniofacial disjunction).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1469","text":"Which of the following bon​es is not involved in Le-​Fort fractures?","raw_text":"Which of the following bones is not involved in Le-Fort fractures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Max​illa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mand​ible","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nasal​ bone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lacrim​al bone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mandible","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mandible</strong></p><p>The mandible is not involved in Le-Fort’s fractures.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1466","text":"The CT scan of a patient w​ho met with a motorcycle ​accident shows a fracture​ of the maxilla. The frac‌ture line passes through ​the floor of the nose sep​arating the palate from t‍he rest of the face. How ​will you classify the fra‌cture?","raw_text":"The CT scan of a patient who met with a motorcycle accident shows a fracture of the maxilla. The fracture line passes through the floor of the nose separating the palate from the rest of the face. How will you classify the fracture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Le-F​ort I","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Le-Fo​rt II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Le-Fo​rt III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Le-Fo​rt IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Le-Fort I","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Le-Fort I</strong></p><p>The CT findings describe a <strong>Le-Fort I</strong> fracture of the maxilla. It is also known as <strong>Guerin’s fracture</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1472","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common site for ​fracture of the mandible ​in children?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common site for fracture of the mandible in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Con​dyle","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"An​gle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bo​dy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Symp​hysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Condyle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Condyle</strong></p><p>The most common site of <strong>fracture of the mandible</strong> in <strong>children</strong> is the <strong>condyle</strong>. It is because the condylar head is connected to the vertical ramus of the bone by a thin neck which gives away easily to an impact.</p>\n<p><strong>Mandibular fractures:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Children</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Due to falls and sports injuries.</li>\n<li>Most common site - <strong>condyle.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Adults &gt; 50 years:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most commonly due to falls.</li>\n<li>Most common site - <strong>body</strong> (the molar area is the weakest area in an edentulous mandible).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Young Adults:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Due to falls, assaults, and accidents.</li>\n<li>The site of fracture depends on the nature of impact and the direction of force applied.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1473","text":"A 23-year-old college stud​ent presented with jaw pa​in and trismus after fall​ing from a bike. A diagno‌sis of mandibular fractur​e was made. Which of the ​following classification ‍is used for this?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old college student presented with jaw pain and trismus after falling from a bike. A diagnosis of mandibular fracture was made. Which of the following classification is used for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Le-Fort cla​ssification","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dingman's cl​assification","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gartland cl​assification","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pauwell cla​ssification","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dingman's classification","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8d706fb9870a4cd18004f97ceccc9971x1280x1218.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dingman&#x27;s classification</strong></p><p><strong>Dingman’s classification</strong> is used in the classification of fractures of the <strong>mandible.</strong></p>\n<p>Dingman's classification is based on the region in which the fracture is located. The seven regions used in this classification are as shown below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Le-Fort classification is used in maxillary fractures.</p>\n<p>Option C: Gartland classification is used in supracondylar fractures of the humerus.</p>\n<p>Option D: Pauwell classification is used in the neck of femur fractures.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1475","text":"Following trauma, a patien​t presented with clear wa​tery nasal discharge that​ cannot be sniffed back. ‌Fractures of which of the​ following bones is the m​ost likely cause for this‍?","raw_text":"Following trauma, a patient presented with clear watery nasal discharge that cannot be sniffed back. Fractures of which of the following bones is the most likely cause for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cribrifo​rm plate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tegmen ​tympani","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fronta​l bone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spheno​id bone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cribriform plate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cribriform plate</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>CSF rhinorrhea</strong> and <strong>fracture of the cribriform plate</strong> of the ethmoid is the <strong>most common cause</strong> for this.</p>\n<p>Causes of CSF rhinorrhea:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Trauma </strong>(accidental or surgical) - most of the cases follow trauma. Surgical trauma includes endoscopic sinus surgery, trans-sphenoidal hypophysectomy, nasal polypectomy, or skull base surgery.</li>\n<li><strong>Inflammations</strong> - mucoceles of sinuses, sinonasal polyposis, the fungal infection of sinuses and osteomyelitis, can all erode the bone and dura.</li>\n<li><strong>Neoplasms</strong> - tumours, both benign and malignant, invading the skull base.</li>\n<li><strong>Congenital lesions</strong> - meningocele, meningoencephaloceles, and gliomas can have associated skull base defects.</li>\n<li><strong>Idiopathic</strong> - where the cause is unknown and the patient has a spontaneous leak.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1476","text":"Which of the following fra​ctures is not associated ​with CSF rhinorrhea?","raw_text":"Which of the following fractures is not associated with CSF rhinorrhea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Le-F​ort I","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Le-Fo​rt II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Le-Fo​rt III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Naso-orbito-ethmoid fractu​re","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Le-Fort I","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Le-Fort I</strong></p><p><strong>Le-Fort I</strong> fracture is not associated with <strong>CSF rhinorrhea</strong>. Whereas, fractures like Le-Fort II, III and naso-orbito-ethmoid fracture are associated with CSF rhinorrhea.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1478","text":"A middle-aged woman, who h​ad undergone a polypectom​y complains of watery nas​al discharge on bending f‌orward. Which of the foll​owing is false regarding ​this condition?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman, who had undergone a polypectomy complains of watery nasal discharge on bending forward. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reservoir​ sign seen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Halo si​gn seen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Discharge doesn’t stiffen ​handkerchief","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Discharge can be sniffed b​ack","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Discharge can be sniffed back","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Discharge can be sniffed back</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>CSF rhinorrhea</strong> secondary to surgery.<br/><br/>Identifying features of CSF Rhinorrhea: <br/>1. The CSF discharge is <strong>thin, watery, and clear</strong> in appearance.<br/>2. It <strong>cannot</strong> <strong>be sniffed back.</strong> (Option D)<br/>3. It <strong>does not stiffen</strong> a handkerchief.<br/>4. It shows positional variation: The watery CSF discharge occurs <strong>suddenly</strong> and <strong>gushes in drops</strong> only <strong>on bending the head forward/straining.  </strong><br/>5. <strong>The Halo sign</strong> is a marker of CSF rhinorrhea <strong>post-head trauma</strong>. It is considered positive when a clear ring surrounds a central bloody spot<strong> </strong>after the discharge is dropped on a handkerchief/paper towel.<br/>6. <strong>Reservoir sign:</strong> The discharge is seen on rising in the morning and bending the head, as fluid accumulated in the sinuses empties into the nose.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1481","text":"In a patient with suspecte​d CSF rhinorrhea, the pre​sence of which of the fol​lowing in the nasal disch‌arge will confirm the dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"In a patient with suspected CSF rhinorrhea, the presence of which of the following in the nasal discharge will confirm the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"β 2 micr​oglobulin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"β 2 tra​nsferrin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transt​hyretin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"α 2 micr​oglobulin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. β 2 transferrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. β 2 transferrin</strong></p><p>Presence of β 2 transferrin protein in the nasal discharge confirms the presence of CSF in the specimen.</p>\n<p><strong>β 2 transferrin protein</strong> detection by electrophoresis is a <strong>sensitive and specific test for CSF</strong>. It is secreted by meninges and choroid plexus.</p>\n<p>Since it is absent in nasal secretions it helps to differentiate CSF from nasal secretions. It is also present in aqueous humor and perilymph.</p>\n<p>Another method is glucose testing by oxidase peroxidase or biochemical estimation. <strong>CSF glucose is 60% of blood glucose</strong> whereas it is &lt;10mg/dl in nasal secretions. It is not reliable in associated meningitis (reduced levels giving false-negative results) and when CSF is mixed with blood. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1482","text":"Which of the following is ​false about CSF rhinorrhe​a?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about CSF rhinorrhea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ethmoid is the most common​ site of leak","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucose i​s present","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intrathecal fluorescein st​udy can be used to detect​ site of leak","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Double ring sign is absent​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Double ring sign is absent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Double ring sign is absent</strong></p><p>Double ring sign is present in a case of CSF rhinorrhea.</p>\n<p><strong>Double ring sign</strong> or <strong>Halo sign</strong> or <strong>Target sign</strong> - when CSF mixed with blood is collected on a piece of filter paper, it will show a <strong>central red spot</strong> (blood) and a <strong>peripheral lighter halo.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Fracture cribriform plate of ethmoid is the most common cause.</p>\n<p>Option B: CSF glucose is 60% of blood glucose.</p>\n<p>Option C: Intrathecal fluorescein study is used to detect the site of leak of CSF.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1483","text":"What is the preferred line​ of management in a patie​nt with CSF rhinorrhea wh​o is otherwise stable?","raw_text":"What is the preferred line of management in a patient with CSF rhinorrhea who is otherwise stable?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immediate su​rgical repair","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conservati​ve measure","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nasal ​packing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trotter’​s method","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Conservative measure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Conservative measure</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>CSF rhinorrhea</strong> who are stable, are <strong>managed conservatively</strong>. </p>\n<p>The conservative measures are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bed rest</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Elevation of the head end</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Stool softeners</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Avoidance of nose-blowing, sneezing, and straining.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Prophylactic antibiotics can be used to prevent meningitis and acetazolamide to decrease CSF formation. Surgery is done if the conservative management fails.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"FgXOQ9QL","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"25_cirrhosis_and_complications","title":"25.Cirrhosis And Complications","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB1207","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is unlikely to le​ad to cirrhosis in a pati‍ent?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is unlikely to lead to cirrhosis in a patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alpha 1 antitrypsin defici‌ency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cystic ‌fibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nonalcoholic steatohepatit‌is","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepat‌itis A","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hepatitis A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hepatitis A</strong></p><p><strong>Hepatitis A</strong> infection is an <strong>acute self-limiting</strong> condition that does not lead to cirrhosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Cirrhosis</strong> is a late stage of <strong>chronic liver disease</strong> pathologically characterized by hepatic fibrosis, distortion of hepatic architecture, and formation of regenerative nodules. </p>\n<p>The <strong>causes of cirrhosis</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic viral hepatitis - Hepatitis B and C </li>\n<li>Alcoholic liver disease</li>\n<li><strong>Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease</strong></li>\n<li>Autoimmune hepatitis</li>\n<li>Biliary cirrhosis -\n<ul>\n<li>Primary biliary cholangitis</li>\n<li>Primary sclerosing cholangitis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Cardiac cirrhosis</li>\n<li>Inherited metabolic liver diseases -<br/>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cystic fibrosis</strong></li>\n<li>Hemochromatosis</li>\n<li>Wilson's disease</li>\n<li><strong>α<sub>1 </sub>Antitrypsin deficiency</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Cryptogenic cirrhosis</li>\n<li>Drugs - methotrexate, isoniazid</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1203","text":"While examining a chronic ‌alcoholic patient, you no​tice the given finding. W‍hich of the following is ​false regarding it?","raw_text":"While examining a chronic alcoholic patient, you notice the given finding. Which of the following is false regarding it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Occurs due to alterations ‌in the metabolism of estr​ogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Presence of even 1 lesion ‌is abnormal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Commonly seen over the sup‌erior vena cava territory​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Compression results in bla‌nching followed by reappe​arance after release","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Presence of even 1 lesion is abnormal","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/12149eea511347dba64938cc75f801dc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1439eb59bb694818b832990dac1e927cx800x1067.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Presence of even 1 lesion is abnormal</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>spider telangiectasia</strong> and the presence of <strong>&gt;2 to 3</strong> of these are considered <strong>abnormal</strong>.</p>\n<p>Spider telangiectasias are dilated arterioles with a <strong>prominent central arteriole</strong> with <strong>radiating vessels</strong> appearing in the shape of a spider. They are generally seen in the <strong>superior vena cava territory</strong>. <strong>Compression</strong> results in <strong>blanching</strong> followed by <strong>reappearance after</strong> the <strong>release</strong> of pressure. They occur due to a <strong>hyperestrogenic state</strong> which is caused by defective estrogen metabolism in liver cirrhosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features</strong> seen in <strong>cirrhosis</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Spider telangiectasias</li>\n<li>Palmar erythema</li>\n<li>Terry's nails - pallor of the proximal nail bed</li>\n<li>Clubbing</li>\n<li>Gynaecomastia</li>\n<li>Caput medusae - dilated abdominal veins.</li>\n<li>Parotid enlargement</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows Terry's nails. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1222","text":"Clinically significant por‌tal hypertension is defin​ed as a hepatic venous pr‍essure gradient greater t​han _____ mm Hg.","raw_text":"Clinically significant portal hypertension is defined as a hepatic venous pressure gradient greater than _____ mm Hg.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌0","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 10","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 10</strong></p><p><strong>Clinically-significant portal hypertension</strong> is defined as hepatic venous pressure gradient (<strong>HVPG</strong>)<strong> ≥10 mm Hg.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">HVPG is the <strong>difference between</strong> the <strong>wedged</strong> hepatic venous pressure (WHVP) and the <strong>free hepatic venous pressure</strong> (FHVP). It represents the pressure difference between the portal vein and IVC.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Interpretation of HVPG values</strong>: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 259px;\" width=\"512\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 248px;\">HVPG value</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248px;\">Interpretation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 248px;\">1-5 mmHg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248px;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 248px;\">&gt;5 mmHg </td>\n<td style=\"width: 248px;\">Portal hypertension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 248px;\">≥10 mmHg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248px;\">Clinically significant portal hypertension, varices can develop</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 248px;\">≥12 mmHg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248px;\">Risk of variceal bleeding and ascites</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1224","text":"Which of the following is ‌a posthepatic cause of po​rtal hypertension?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a posthepatic cause of portal hypertension?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Budd-Chiar‌i syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Schisto‌somiasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Portal vein‌ thrombosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Banti's ‌syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Budd-Chiari syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Budd-Chiari syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Budd-Chiari syndrome</strong> is a <strong>posthepatic</strong> cause of portal hypertension.</p>\n<p>Budd Chiari syndrome is a congestive hepatopathy characterized by the <strong>obstruction of</strong> <strong>hepatic venous outflow</strong>. The most common cause is due to hepatic vein thrombosis. Obstruction of two or more major hepatic veins causes Budd-Chiari syndrome. It is characterized by <strong>hepatomegaly</strong>, <strong>ascites</strong>, and <strong>abdominal pain</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1223","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is used to reduce the ​intrahepatic resistance i‍n portal hypertension?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is used to reduce the intrahepatic resistance in portal hypertension?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propr‌anolol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carve‌dilol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Octre‌otide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Terlip‌ressin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Carvedilol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Carvedilol</strong></p><p><strong>Carvedilol</strong> is used to <strong>reduce</strong> the <strong>intrahepatic resistance</strong> in portal hypertension.</p>\n<p>The pharmacologic <strong>management of portal hypertension</strong> includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drugs that <strong>reduce</strong> the <strong>portal blood flow</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Non-selective β blockers - Propranolol and nadolol are preferred</li>\n<li>Somatostatin and its analogs - Octreotide, lanreotide, and vapreotide</li>\n<li>Vasopressin and analogs - Terlipressin</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Drugs that <strong>decrease</strong> the <strong>intrahepatic resistance </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Carvedilol - due to its α blockade property</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1230","text":"Ascitic tap done on a pati‌ent with abdominal disten​tion revealed a SAAG of 1‍.4g/dL. What is the likel​y cause of his ascites?","raw_text":"Ascitic tap done on a patient with abdominal distention revealed a SAAG of 1.4g/dL. What is the likely cause of his ascites?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Portal hy‌pertension","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nephrotic‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peritoneal ‌tuberculosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Biliar‌y leak","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Portal hypertension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Portal hypertension</strong></p><p>The serum ascites albumin gradient of<strong> ≥1.1g/dL </strong>is suggestive of <strong>portal hypertension.</strong></p>\n<p>SAAG helps distinguish ascites caused by portal hypertension from other causes of ascites.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6821","text":"A patient presented with m‌assive abdominal swelling​. Paracentesis fluid was ‍sent for lab analysis and​ results show serum prote​in to be 3g/dL and asciti​c fluid protein to be 1g/​dL. What is the likely di‍agnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presented with massive abdominal swelling. Paracentesis fluid was sent for lab analysis and results show serum protein to be 3g/dL and ascitic fluid protein to be 1g/dL. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congestive h‌eart failure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Early Budd Chiari syndrome‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Late Budd Ch‌iari syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Late Budd Chiari syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Late Budd Chiari syndrome</strong></p><p>The likely diagnosis in the given clinical scenario with <strong>serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG)</strong> <strong>&gt;1.1g/dL</strong> [calculated as serum albumin level - ascitic fluid albumin level, which in this case is 3-1=2g/dL] and <strong>ascitic fluid protein &lt;2.5 g/dL</strong> is<strong> late Budd Chiari syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG) can be used to differentiate between ascites due to <strong>portal hypertension</strong> and <strong>non-portal hypertensive ascites.</strong> It represents the pressure within the hepatic sinusoids and correlates with the <strong>hepatic venous pressure gradient.</strong></p>\n<p>It is given by the formula: SAAG = Serum albumin level - Ascitic fluid albumin level</p>\n<p>Interpretation of SAAG:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">SAAG <strong>≥1.1</strong> g/dL indicates that the ascites is due to increased pressure in the hepatic sinusoids. It reflects the presence of<strong> portal hypertension.</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">SAAG <strong>&lt;1.1</strong> g/dL indicates that the ascites is not related to portal hypertension. It may be due to i<strong>nfection or malignancy.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>In cases with high-SAAG (≥1.1), <strong>ascitic protein level analysis</strong> further helps in identifying the etiology:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>≥2.5 g/dL</strong> indicates that the hepatic sinusoids are normal and are allowing the passage of protein into the ascites as in <strong>early Budd-Chiari syndrome.</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>&lt;2.5 g/dL</strong> indicates that the hepatic sinusoids have been damaged and scarred and no longer allow passage of protein as in <strong>late Budd-Chiari syndrome</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1334","text":"A patient is said to have ‌refractory ascites if the​ ascites does not resolve‍ even after: Dietary sodi​um restriction 100mg / da​y spironolactone 160mg / ​day furosemide 100mg / da​y furosemide 400mg / day ‍spironolactone","raw_text":"<p>A patient is said to have refractory ascites if the ascites does not resolve even after:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Dietary sodium restriction</li>\n<li>100mg / day spironolactone </li>\n<li>160mg / day furosemide</li>\n<li>100mg / day furosemide</li>\n<li>400mg / day spironolactone</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 3 ‌and 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2 a‌nd 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 3 and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Refractory ascites</strong> is defined as the inability to mobilize ascitic fluid despite:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Compliance with dietary <strong>sodium restriction</strong></li>\n<li>Administration of maximum doses of diuretics -\n<ul>\n<li><strong>400mg </strong>of <strong>spironolactone</strong> per day<strong> </strong>and</li>\n<li><strong>160mg </strong>of <strong>furosemide</strong> per day</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1231","text":"What is the minimum absolu‌te neutrophilic count tha​t should be present in th‍e ascitic fluid to diagno​se a patient with spontan​eous bacterial peritoniti​s?","raw_text":"What is the minimum absolute neutrophilic count that should be present in the ascitic fluid to diagnose a patient with spontaneous bacterial peritonitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt;2‌50/µL","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt;1‌50/µL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt;3‌00/µL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;2‌00/µL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. >250/µL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. &gt;250/µL</strong></p><p>The diagnosis of <strong>spontaneous bacterial peritonitis</strong>(SBP) is made when the fluid sample has an <strong>absolute neutrophil count &gt;250/μL.</strong></p>\n<p>SBP is the spontaneous infection of the ascitic fluid without an intraabdominal source. Infection with<strong><em> E.coli</em></strong> and other gut bacteria is most <strong>commonly seen</strong>. However, gram-positive bacteria like <em>Streptococcus viridans</em>, <em>Staphylococcus</em> <em>aureus</em>, and <em>Enterococcus</em> sp., can also be found. <strong>Abdominal paracentesis</strong> is the investigation of choice. It can be managed with <strong>antibiotic</strong> therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1232","text":"A cirrhotic patient admitt‌ed for ascites complained​ of abdominal pain on the‍ 2nd day of his hospital ​stay. On examination, he ​was febrile. His lab repo​rts are given below. Whic​h of the following is fal‍se about his condition? T​otal WBC count- 12 400×10​ 6 /litre C reactive prot‌ein- 4.3 mg/dl Serology- ​positive for HBs-antigen ​Absolute neutrophil count​ in ascitic fluid- 743 ce​lls/μl","raw_text":"<p>A cirrhotic patient admitted for ascites complained of abdominal pain on the 2nd day of his hospital stay. On examination, he was febrile. His lab reports are given below. Which of the following is false about his condition?</p>\n<p>Total WBC count- 12 400×10<sup>6</sup>/litre</p>\n<p>C reactive protein- 4.3 mg/dl</p>\n<p>Serology- positive for HBs-antigen</p>\n<p>Absolute neutrophil count in ascitic fluid- 743 cells/μl</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E.coli is the most commonl‌y isolated organism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prophylaxis against this c‌ondition is recommended i​n upper GI bleed","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Third generation cephalosp‌orins are used for treatm​ent","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Weekly antibiotic prophyla‌xis is recommended to pre​vent recurrence","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Weekly antibiotic prophylaxis is recommended to prevent recurrence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Weekly antibiotic prophylaxis is recommended to prevent recurrence</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with <strong>ANC ≥ 250 cells/μl</strong> in ascitic fluid is suggestive of <strong>spontaneous bacterial peritonitis</strong> (SBP). <strong>Daily antibiotic prophylaxis</strong> is recommended in patients who have had an episode of SBP to <strong>prevent</strong> a <strong>recurrence</strong>. </p>\n<p>SBP is the spontaneous infection of the ascitic fluid without an intraabdominal source. The <strong>most common</strong> causative organisms are <em><strong>E.coli </strong></em>and other gut bacteria. The diagnosis of SBP is made when the ascitic fluid has an <strong>absolute neutrophil count &gt;250/μl.</strong></p>\n<p>According to the latest recommendations, weekly antibiotic prophylaxis is no longer recommended. For<span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\"> patients with low protein ascites (&lt;1.5 g/dL [15</span><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">g/L]) along with either impaired renal function or liver failure, we use prolonged outpatient </span><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole</span><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\"> (one double-strength tablet daily). Alternatives include ciprofloxacin 500 mg per day or norfloxacin 400 mg per day.</span></p>\n<p><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\"> For patients on chronic trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole therapy, renal function should be monitored by checking serum electrolytes, blood urea nitrogen (BUN) and creatinine every one to six months depending on the stability of patient. If there is worsening renal function, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole should be discontinued.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1227","text":"Which of the following can‌not be used to predict th​e risk of bleeding of oes‍ophageal varices?","raw_text":"Which of the following cannot be used to predict the risk of bleeding of oesophageal varices?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Severity o‌f cirrhosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Size of t‌he varices","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Location of‌ the varices","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Number o‌f varices","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Number of varices","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Number of varices</strong></p><p>The risk of bleeding in a patient with oesophageal varices cannot be predicted based on the number of varices present.</p>\n<p> The factors that are used to <strong>predict</strong> the <strong>risk of variceal bleeding</strong> are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Severity of cirrhosis</strong> (Child’s class, MELD score)</li>\n<li>History of a <strong>previous variceal bleed</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Location</strong> of the varices - varices with a thinner layer of supporting tissue are likely to bleed more</li>\n<li><strong>Size</strong> of the varices - larger varices have a higher risk of bleeding</li>\n<li><strong>Endoscopic appearance </strong>of varices - Presence of red wale sign, hematocystic spots, diffuse erythema, and cherry red spots are associated with increased risk of bleeding</li>\n<li><strong>Variceal pressure</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1229","text":"In a patient with which of‌ the following conditions​ would you not recommend ‍a transjugular intrahepat​ic portosystemic shunt?","raw_text":"In a patient with which of the following conditions would you not recommend a transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ectopic‌ varices","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Refractor‌y ascites","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Budd Chiar‌i syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatic enc‌ephalopathy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hepatic encephalopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/564defbe79384fd299c2ca53488b2b4cx600x1199.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hepatic encephalopathy</strong></p><p>Transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt (<strong>TIPS</strong>) is not recommended in <strong>hepatic encephalopathy</strong> as it can <strong>aggravate</strong> its symptoms.</p>\n<p><strong>TIPS </strong>creates a <strong>low-resistance channel</strong> between the hepatic vein and an intrahepatic branch of the portal vein. It uses a <strong>percutaneous approach</strong> under <strong>angiographic guidance</strong>. This reduces portal pressure. </p>\n<p>It is used in the management of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Oesophageal varices - in patients who fail medical or endoscopic management and have a poor surgical outcome</li>\n<li><strong>Refractory ascites</strong></li>\n<li>Gastric varices</li>\n<li><strong>Ectopic varices</strong></li>\n<li>Portal hypertensive gastropathy</li>\n<li>Hepatorenal syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>Budd Chiari syndrome</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1381","text":"A middle-aged man with hem‌atemesis is rushed to the​ casualty. On examination‍, he is icteric, PR-122 /​min and BP- 88/64 mmHg. T​he following finding is s​een on his abdomen. Which​ of the following is fals‍e regarding his initial m​anagement?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man with hematemesis is rushed to the casualty. On examination, he is icteric, PR-122 /min and BP- 88/64 mmHg. The following finding is seen on his abdomen. Which of the following is false regarding his initial management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"pRBC is used to maintain H‌b between 7-9g/dL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prophylactic antibiotics g‌iven before endoscopy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IV octreotide is used to a‌cheive hemostasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Upper GI endoscopy is only‌ performed after 12 hours​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Upper GI endoscopy is only performed after 12 hours","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d06079aeadcc4fbcbceee016f0dfbfb6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/843bb40225884e0b847c52871b9948cfx1200x1551.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Upper GI endoscopy is only performed after 12 hours</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the image showing <strong>caput-medusae</strong> is suggestive of acute <strong>esophageal variceal bleeding</strong> due to portal hypertension<strong>. Upper GI endoscopy</strong> is performed <strong>within 12 hours</strong> after fluid resuscitation in variceal bleeding. </p>\n<p>The <strong>management of esophageal variceal bleeding</strong> involves:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Resuscitation</strong> with IV fluids, airway protection, and oxygen</li>\n<li>Blood transfusion to maintain <strong>Hb </strong>between<strong> 7-9g/dL</strong></li>\n<li>Antibiotic prophylaxis with <strong>ceftriaxone </strong>before endoscopy</li>\n<li>An attempt at hemostasis with <strong>octreotide</strong> or <strong>terlipressin</strong> or <strong>vasopressin</strong></li>\n<li>Upper GI endoscopy within 12 hours after fluid resuscitation to confirm the source of the bleed</li>\n<li><strong>Endoscopic variceal ligation</strong> of sclerotherapy</li>\n<li>In case, of failure or rebleeding - <strong>TIPS</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Nonselective beta-blockers</strong> like propranolol and nadolol are used in the <strong>prophylaxis</strong> of variceal bleeding. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1397","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about the given ima​ge?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the given image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Esophageal balloon should ‌be deflated for about 5 m​ins every 6 hours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gastric balloon should be ‌inflated first","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pressure in the oesophagea‌l balloon should not exce​ed 60 mm Hg.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endotracheal intubation pr‌ior to tube insertion red​uces the risk of pulmonar‍y aspiration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pressure in the oesophageal balloon should not exceed 60 mm Hg.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/08faaae1be1a46a7a9ce4f762e69edb5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4a9cf7ed3c29401c93358df67f1872a7x1280x1825.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pressure in the oesophageal balloon should not exceed 60 mm Hg.</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>Sengstaken-Blakemore tube</strong>. Pressure in the <strong>oesophageal balloon</strong> should not exceed <strong>45 mm Hg</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is used for balloon tamponade in <strong>variceal hemorrhage</strong> when medical therapy fails or is not available. Initially, only the <strong>gastric balloon</strong> should be inflated, with <strong>200– 250 mL of air</strong>, as this will usually control bleeding. The <strong>oesophageal balloon</strong> is used in continued bleeding, it should be <strong>deflated</strong> for about 5 minutes every 6 hours to <strong>avoid oesophageal mucosal damage.</strong></p>\n<p>Endotracheal <strong>intubation</strong> prior to tube insertion <strong>reduces</strong> the risk of <strong>pulmonary aspiration</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1061","text":"A patient with hereditary ‌hemochromatosis shows mar​ked confusion and gross d‍isorientation. He is drow​sy but responds to pain a​nd voice stimuli. What is​ the most likely grading ​of hepatic encephalopathy‍ in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient with hereditary hemochromatosis shows marked confusion and gross disorientation. He is drowsy but responds to pain and voice stimuli. What is the most likely grading of hepatic encephalopathy in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gra‌de 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gra‌de 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gra‌de 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gra‌de 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Grade 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Grade 3</strong></p><p>Gross <strong>disorientation </strong>with an <strong>intact response</strong> to <strong>pain</strong> and <strong>voice</strong> suggest <strong>grade 3 </strong>hepatic encephalopathy.</p>\n<p><strong>Hepatic encephalopathy</strong> is the alteration of mental status and cognitive function occurring in liver failure. This occurs due to the<strong> failure of removal</strong> of gut-derived neurotoxins like <strong>ammonia by liver.</strong> These neurotoxins enter the brain leading to the development of symptoms.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1240","text":"What is the earliest sympt‌om of hepatic encephalopa​thy?","raw_text":"What is the earliest symptom of hepatic encephalopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mood c‌hanges","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aste‌rixis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Altered sl‌eep pattern","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Disorie‌ntation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Altered sleep pattern","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Altered sleep pattern</strong></p><p>The <strong>earliest symptom</strong> of hepatic encephalopathy is an<strong> alteration in sleep pattern</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other<strong> signs and symptoms</strong> of <strong>hepatic encephalopathy</strong> include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Asterixis also called the flapping tremor</li>\n<li>Mood changes and inappropriate behavior</li>\n<li>Disorientation and confusion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1242","text":"A cirrhotic patient was ad‌mitted following variceal​ bleeding. On the 3rd day‍ of his hospital stay, he​ appears to be drowsy and​ confused. His wife repor​ts that he had been awake​ throughout the previous ‍night. On examination, fl​apping tremors were notic​ed. Which of the followin‌g is least likely to have​ precipitated his symptom​s?","raw_text":"A cirrhotic patient was admitted following variceal bleeding. On the 3rd day of his hospital stay, he appears to be drowsy and confused. His wife reports that he had been awake throughout the previous night. On examination, flapping tremors were noticed. Which of the following is least likely to have precipitated his symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antib‌iotics","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Periton‌eal tap","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diur‌etics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urinary tra‌ct infection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Antibiotics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Antibiotics</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> hepatic encephalopathy</strong>. Antibiotics are unlikely to precipitate hepatic encephalopathy.</p>\n<p>The common <strong>precipitating factors</strong> of hepatic encephalopathy are :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gastrointestinal bleeding</li>\n<li>Infections like SBP or <strong>UTIs</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Peritoneal tap</strong></li>\n<li>Constipation</li>\n<li>Renal failure</li>\n<li>Hypovolemia</li>\n<li>Hypoxia</li>\n<li>Drugs - sedatives, antidepressants, <strong>diuretics</strong></li>\n<li>Hypoglycemia</li>\n<li>Hypokalemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1245","text":"Which of the following ant‌ibiotics could be used in​ the treatment of patient‍s with hepatic encephalop​athy?","raw_text":"Which of the following antibiotics could be used in the treatment of patients with hepatic encephalopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rifa‌ximin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Azithr‌omycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Doxyc‌ycline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rifaximin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rifaximin</strong></p><p><strong>Rifaximin</strong> can be used in the treatment of hepatic encephalopathy. It <strong>lowers</strong> blood <strong>ammonia levels</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Treatment of hepatic encephalopathy</strong> includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Correction of precipitating factors</li>\n<li>Lactulose - decreased GI transit time due to cathartic actions leading to increased elimination of ammonia</li>\n<li>Rifaximin - lowers the blood ammonia levels</li>\n<li>L-ornithine L-aspartate - stimulate the urea cycle, with resulting loss of ammonia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1384","text":"Which of the following is ‌not part of the diagnosti​c criteria of hepatorenal‍ syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not part of the diagnostic criteria of hepatorenal syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50% increase in creatinine‌ from baseline within 7 d​ays","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rise in creatinine of 0.3 ‌mg/dl in 48 hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Presence‌ of shock","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"No recent use of nephrotox‌ic agent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Presence of shock","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Presence of shock</strong></p><p>The <strong>absence of shock</strong> is a part of the diagnostic criteria for <strong>hepatorenal syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hepatorenal syndrome is a progressive <strong>renal failure in</strong> patients with acute or chronic <strong>liver disease</strong>. </p>\n<p>The<strong> diagnostic criteria</strong> for hepatorenal syndrome include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cirrhosis with ascites</li>\n<li>Diagnosis of acute kidney injury (AKI)\n<ul>\n<li>Increase in serum creatinine of ≥ 0.3mg/dL in 48 hours, OR</li>\n<li>50% increase in serum creatinine from baseline within 7 days</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Absence of shock</li>\n<li>No evidence of recent use of nephrotoxic agents</li>\n<li>Absence of intrinsic renal disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1390","text":"Which of the following cli‌nical features is most li​kely to be seen in patien‍ts with hepatopulmonary s​yndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following clinical features is most likely to be seen in patients with hepatopulmonary syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trep‌opnea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nocturna‌l dyspnea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Orth‌opnea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plat‌ypnea","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Platypnea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Platypnea</strong></p><p>Among the given options, <strong>platypnea</strong> is most likely to be seen in patients with <strong>hepatopulmonary syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hepatopulmonary syndrome is characterized by a triad of <strong>liver disease</strong>, <strong>hypoxemia</strong>, and <strong>intrapulmonary vascular dilatations</strong>. Most patients have dyspnea on exertion. The classic clinical manifestations are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Platypnea</strong> - Increase in dyspnea when the patient moves from supine to upright position</li>\n<li><strong>Orthodeoxia</strong> - Decrease in arterial oxygen tension when the patient moves from supine to upright position</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The hypoxemia is due to a <strong>ventilation-perfusion mismatch</strong> which causes an increased alveolar-arterial (A-a) gradient. The most sensitive test for diagnosis is <strong>contrast bubble echocardiography.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Liver transplantation </strong>is the <strong>definitive treatment</strong> of this condition following which the symptoms subside.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1388","text":"A 52-year-old man presents‌ with oliguria, abdominal​ distension, and pedal ed‍ema. His medical records ​show long-standing cirrho​sis with multiple hospita​lisations for ascites and​ hepatic encephalopathy. ‍His lab reports are given​ below. What is the best ​treatment for his conditi‌on? AST- 148 U/L ALT- 64 ​U/L Total bilirubin- 8.9 ​mg/dL Sr. Creatinine- 7.6​ mg/dL Blood urea nitroge​n- 84 mg/dL","raw_text":"<p>A 52-year-old man presents with oliguria, abdominal distension, and pedal edema.  His medical records show long-standing cirrhosis with multiple hospitalisations for ascites and hepatic encephalopathy. His lab reports are given below. What is the best treatment for his condition?</p>\n<p>AST- 148 U/L</p>\n<p>ALT- 64 U/L</p>\n<p>Total bilirubin- 8.9 mg/dL</p>\n<p>Sr. Creatinine- 7.6 mg/dL</p>\n<p>Blood urea nitrogen- 84 mg/dL</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mido‌drine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Liver tr‌ansplant","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intraveno‌us albumin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"TI‌PS","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Liver transplant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Liver transplant</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hepatorenal syndrome</strong>. The best treatment modality for it is <strong>liver transplantation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hepatorenal syndrome is characterised by a rise in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>AST</li>\n<li>ALT</li>\n<li>Total bilirubin</li>\n<li>Serum creatinine</li>\n<li>Blood urea nitrogen (BUN).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Management of hepatorenal syndrome</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Norepinephrine</li>\n<li>Octreotide</li>\n<li>Terlipressin</li>\n<li><strong>Midodrine</strong></li>\n<li>Intravenous <strong>albumin</strong> </li>\n<li>Liver transplantation- definitive treatment</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9603","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is not a MELD exc​eption?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is not a MELD exception?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatopulmon‌ary syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Portopulmonary hypertensio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary hy‌peroxaluria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic H‌epatitis B","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chronic Hepatitis B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chronic Hepatitis B</strong></p><p>Chronic Hepatitis B is not a MELD exception.</p>\n<p><strong>MELD </strong>(Model for End-stage Liver Disease) <strong>score</strong> is used to <strong>prioritize patients</strong> awaiting <strong>liver transplantation</strong>. However, there are <strong>certain diseases</strong> that impair patient survival and are <strong>not accounted for in</strong> the <strong>MELD score</strong>. Patients with these diseases are <strong>given upgraded MELD points</strong> to accurately represent the mortality risk and these conditions are <strong>called MELD exceptions</strong>. </p>\n<p>The MELD exceptions include:-</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Portopulmonary hypertension</strong></li>\n<li>Hepatocellular carcinoma</li>\n<li>Familial amyloid polyneuropathy</li>\n<li><strong>Primary hyperoxaluria</strong> (necessitating liver-kidney transplantation)</li>\n<li>Cystic fibrosis with liver disease</li>\n<li>Highly selected cases of hilar cholangiocarcinoma.</li>\n<li><strong>Hepatopulmonary syndrome</strong></li>\n<li>Hepatic artery thrombosis 7–14 days post-liver transplantation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7510","text":"Which among the following ‌is false?","raw_text":"Which among the following is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Components of MELD (Model ‌for end stage liver disea​se) scoring system are cr‍eatinine, bilirubin, Inte​rnational normalized rati​o (INR), serum sodium (mE​q/L).","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Components of CTP(Child Tu‌rcotte Pugh) score are al​bumin, bilirubin, INR, as‍cites, encephalopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Components of CTP (Child T‌urcotte Pugh ) are creati​nine, bilirubin, Internat‍ional normalized ratio (I​NR)","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"MELD score is used to asse‌ss and prioritize patient​s awaiting liver transpla‍ntation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Components of CTP (Child Turcotte Pugh ) are creatinine, bilirubin, International normalized ratio (INR)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Components of CTP (Child Turcotte Pugh ) are creatinine, bilirubin, International normalized ratio (INR)</strong></p><p>The <strong>Child Turcotte Pugh</strong> (CTP) score <strong>does not</strong> include <strong>creatinine</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>components of </strong>the<strong> CTP score </strong>are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Albumin</li>\n<li>Bilirubin</li>\n<li>International normalized ratio (INR)</li>\n<li>Ascites</li>\n<li>Encephalopathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>Model for End-stage liver disease</strong> (MELD 3.0) is used to <strong>prioritize patients awaiting liver transplantation </strong>and includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Female sex</strong></li>\n<li>Bilirubin</li>\n<li><strong>Albumin</strong></li>\n<li>Serum sodium (mEq/L)</li>\n<li>INR (International Normalized ratio)</li>\n<li>Creatinine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3073","text":"What is the Child-Turcotte‌-Pugh class for the patie​nt who has a serum biliru‍bin 2.5 mg/dl, serum albu​min 3 g/dl, prothrombin t​ime increased by 5 second​s (INR = 2), no encephalo​pathy, and mild ascites?","raw_text":"What is the Child-Turcotte-Pugh class for the patient who has a serum bilirubin 2.5 mg/dl, serum albumin 3 g/dl, prothrombin time increased by 5 seconds (INR = 2), no encephalopathy, and mild ascites?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cla‌ss A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cla‌ss B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cla‌ss C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cla‌ss D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Class B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Class B</strong></p><p>The given patient belongs to Child-Turcotte-Pugh <strong>class B</strong>.</p>\n<p>The score can be calculated as follows:</p>\n<div>Serum bilirubin-2.5mg/dl: 2 </div>\n<div>Serum albumin-3g/dl: 2</div>\n<div>Prothrombin time-5 seconds or INR-2: 2</div>\n<div>No encephalopathy: 1</div>\n<div>Mild ascites: 2</div>\n<p>Therefore, the <strong>Child-Turcotte-Pugh score</strong> is (2+2+2+1+2)= <strong>9</strong> which is included in Class B.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"-Uc_B401","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"25_myxoviruses_and_rhabdoviruses","title":"25.Myxoviruses And Rhabdoviruses","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MA7885","text":"Which of the following RNA‌ viruses has an 8 segment‌ed genome with its site o​f replication being the n‌ucleus?","raw_text":"Which of the following RNA viruses has an 8 segmented genome with its site of replication being the nucleus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Influen‌za virus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Measle‌s virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rubell‌a virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mumps‌ virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Influenza virus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f5313a1cd2bb46efb68e8835a89a7643x1280x1476.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Influenza virus</strong></p><p><strong>Influenza virus</strong> has an 8 segmented genome. Its site of replication is the nucleus, unlike other RNA viruses. Influenza virus belongs to the Orthomyxoviridae family. </p>\n<p><strong>Most DNA</strong> viruses replicate in the <strong>nucleus,</strong> <strong>except pox</strong>. Most<strong> RNA</strong> viruses replicate in the <strong>cytoplasm,</strong> except <strong>retroviruses and orthomyxoviruses.</strong></p>\n<p>Examples of segmented viruses (ARBO): </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong>renavirus (2 segments)</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>otavirus (11 segments)</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong>unyavirus (3 segments)</li>\n<li><strong>O</strong>rthomyxovirus (8 segments)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows the segmented genome seen in the influenza virus (Orthomyxoviridae)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0974","text":"A microbiologist working o‌n influenza virus in a la‌b, observes reversal of h​emagglutination. What is ‌this phenomenon known as?​","raw_text":"A microbiologist working on influenza virus in a lab, observes reversal of hemagglutination. What is this phenomenon known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemo‌lysis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Precip‌itation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Elu‌tion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Complemen‌t fixation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Elution","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d0941a6e9da84951a385bbcb40822e0cx1280x1476.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Elution</strong></p><p><strong>Elution</strong> is the process of <strong>reversal of hemagglutination</strong> and the release of the virus from the red cell surface.</p>\n<p>Influenza virus contains two surface antigens:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hemagglutinin</strong> (HA) - causes <strong>RBC agglutination;</strong> facilitates viral entry into cells</li>\n<li><strong>Neuraminidase</strong> (NA) - an enzyme that destroys the receptor HA uses to bind to RBCs causing <strong>elution</strong>/reversal of hemagglutination. Hence, it is also called a <strong>receptor destroying enzyme</strong> (RDE).</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7888","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‌ antigenic drift?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding antigenic drift?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"There is a gradual sequent‌ial change in antigenic s‌tructure","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It doesn’t account for the‌ periodic epidemics of in‌fluenza","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The new antigens thus form‌ed are totally unrelated ‌to the previous antigens","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antibodies to the predeces‌sor viruses do not neutra‌lise the new variants","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. There is a gradual sequential change in antigenic structure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. There is a gradual sequential change in antigenic structure</strong></p><p><strong>Antigenic drift</strong> causes a <strong>gradual sequential change</strong> in antigenic structure.</p>\n<p>It accounts for <strong>periodic epidemics.</strong> This property forms the basis for the development of annual influenza <strong>vaccines.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Antigenic variation</strong> is a property of<strong> influenza viruses </strong>by which it alters the antigenic structure on its surface and thus avoids a host immune response. It is of two types - Antigenic shift and antigenic drift.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 474px; height: 326px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.062px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.539px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Antigenic shift</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.492px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Antigenic drift</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.062px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Antigenic structure</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.539px; text-align: left;\">Abrupt, drastic change</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.492px; text-align: left;\">Gradual, sequential change </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.062px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Periodicity</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.539px; text-align: left;\"> 10 - 20 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.492px; text-align: left;\">2 - 3 years   </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.062px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Epidemiological effect</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.539px; text-align: left;\">Pandemics and major epidemics </td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.492px; text-align: left;\">Periodic epidemic and sporadic cases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.062px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Genetic cause</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.539px; text-align: left;\">Large genetic re-assortment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.492px; text-align: left;\">Point mutation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.062px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Antibodies to predecessor strains</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.539px; text-align: left;\">Do not neutralize the new variant</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.492px; text-align: left;\">Neutralizes the new variant to varying degrees</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.062px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Clinical relevance </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.539px; text-align: left;\">Animal-origin viruses infecting humans e.g. Swine flu, COVID-19</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.492px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Annual influenza vaccine development</p>\n<p>Common cold - People can get flu more than once</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5188","text":"Which of the following org‌anisms is represented by ‌the given form of replica​tion?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms is represented by the given form of replication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatit‌is virus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Influen‌za virus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"H‌IV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herpes‌ virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Influenza virus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2f95a71e225842cd9d0f47b1091c0966.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Influenza virus</strong></p><p>The given image shows the replication of <strong>influenza virus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Stages of replication:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 583\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Attachment, penetration, uncoating-</strong> the virus is internalized by receptor-mediated endocytosis </li>\n<li><strong>Transcription and translation-</strong> Viral transcription occurs in the nucleus. The mRNAs are produced from viral nucleocapsids.</li>\n<li><strong>Viral RNA replication-</strong> Viral genome replication is accomplished by the same virus-encoded polymerase proteins involved in transcription.</li>\n<li><strong>Maturation-</strong> The virus matures by budding from the surface of the cell.</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7889","text":"You receive multiple patie‌nts from a poultry farm p‌resenting with fever, dys​pnea, cold, and cough. Yo‌u suspect an outbreak of ​Avian flu. Which of the f‌ollowing virus strains ca​uses the same?","raw_text":"You receive multiple patients from a poultry farm presenting with fever, dyspnea, cold, and cough. You suspect an outbreak of Avian flu. Which of the following virus strains causes the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H1‌N1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"H5‌N1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"H3‌N2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"H2‌N2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. H5N1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. H5N1</strong></p><p><strong>H5N1</strong> is a strain of <strong>avian influenza</strong>.</p>\n<p>Transmission occurs<strong> only from birds </strong>(e.g. poultry). Recombination occurs in <strong>pigs. </strong>Human-to-human transmission is very rare. </p>\n<p>It causes flu-like symptoms and can cause severe respiratory illness. </p>\n<p>Option A: H1N1 causes swine flu</p>\n<p>Option C: H3N2 causes Hong Kong flu</p>\n<p>Option D: H2N2 caused a pandemic in 1957 </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0250","text":"A 4-year-old girl with a h‌istory of fever, rhinorrh‌ea, sore throat, and myal​gia presented with multip‌le episodes of vomiting a​nd seizures. She was diag‌nosed with Reye's syndrom​e. Which of the following​ is incorrect about the m​ost likely etiological ag‍ent?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old girl with a history of fever, rhinorrhea, sore throat, and myalgia presented with multiple episodes of vomiting and seizures. She was diagnosed with Reye's syndrome. Which of the following is incorrect about the most likely etiological agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Virus shedding is present ‌before symptoms begins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5-12 years is a high risk ‌group","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aquatic birds act as reser‌voir","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secondary attack rate is 5‌-15%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5-12 years is a high risk group","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5-12 years is a high risk group</strong></p><p>The most likely etiological agent in the given case of Reye's syndrome is the <strong>influenza virus.  </strong>Children &lt;<strong>5-years</strong>, especially those<strong> &lt;2-years</strong> of age are at the <strong>highest risk</strong> of serious <strong>complications</strong> related to <strong>influenza</strong> infection. Other high-risk groups for influenza include adults ≥60 years of age, persons with chronic diseases, persons who are immunocompromised.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option A: The virus is <strong>shed</strong> in respiratory secretions <strong>a day before the onset</strong> of illness and for <strong>3-4 days thereafter</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option C: <strong>Birds</strong>, especially aquatic birds act as <strong>reservoirs</strong> of influenza viruses.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option D: Secondary attack rate is <strong>5-15%</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Note: Reye's syndrome is precipitated by the interaction of a viral infection (such as influenza) with salicylates.<strong> </strong>Therefore, aspirin is not given to pediatric patients with influenza and influenza-like illness.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7891","text":"A 12-year-old boy is broug‌ht to the OPD with compla‌ints of headache and drow​siness since 2 days. He h‌ad a history of fever, ea​r pain, and difficulty in‌ swallowing for 1 week. O​n examination, you see th​e following. Brudzinki's ​sign is positive. What is‍ the most likely diagnosi​s?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old boy is brought to the OPD with complaints of headache and drowsiness since 2 days. He had a history of fever, ear pain, and difficulty in swallowing for 1 week. On examination, you see the following. Brudzinki's sign is positive. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cr‌oup","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mu‌mps","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infectious m‌ononucleosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herpa‌ngina","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mumps","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1d1e51e2ff114470ad51d34d9bb43b7a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mumps</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with fever, parotitis, headache, drowsiness, and positive Brudzinki's sign is suggestive of <strong>meningitis</strong> secondary to <strong>mumps</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Mumps</strong> is caused by the mumps virus and is characterized by <strong>fever,</strong> bilateral or unilateral<strong> parotid swelling</strong> which may be <strong>tender.</strong> Parotitis can present with <strong>ear pain</strong> and <strong>difficulty in swallowing</strong>. It usually resolves in a week.</p>\n<p>Meningoencephalitis/<strong>aseptic meningitis </strong>can occur before, along with, or after parotitis. Most commonly, it manifests <strong>5 days after parotitis</strong>. It can present with headache, neck stiffness, and drowsiness.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications of mumps</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aseptic meningitis or meningoencephalitis</li>\n<li>Epididymo-orchitis- Usually unilateral. Seen in post-pubertal males</li>\n<li>Oophoritis</li>\n<li>Other complications include pancreatitis and myocarditis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Non-mumps parotitis can be caused by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Epstein-Barr virus - causes infectious mononucleosis</li>\n<li>Coxsackievirus - causes herpangina</li>\n<li>Parainfluenza virus - causes croup</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7892","text":"A 9-month-old child is bro‌ught to the OPD for MMR v‌accine. Which strain of m​umps virus is used in the‌ same?","raw_text":"A  9-month-old child is brought to the OPD for MMR vaccine. Which strain of mumps virus is used in the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Edmonston-Z‌agreb strain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nakayam‌a strain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Jeryl-Ly‌nn strain","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"RA ‌27/3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Jeryl-Lynn strain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Jeryl-Lynn strain</strong></p><p>The strain of <strong>mumps</strong> virus used in the <strong>MMR vaccine</strong> (Measles, Mumps, Rubella) is the <strong>Jeryl-Lynn strain.</strong> </p>\n<p>The virus is <strong>attenuated</strong> by serial passage in embryonated eggs and grown in chick embryo fibroblast culture. </p>\n<p>Note: The schedule of MMR vaccine as per the National Immunisation Schedule is from 9 completed months to 12 months. (can be given till 5 years of age).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7893","text":"A mother brings her 5-mont‌h-old baby to the casualt‌y with complaints of fast​ breathing and poor feedi‌ng. She also gives a hist​ory of fever, cold, and c‌ough for 3 days. On exami​nation, nasal flaring and​ expiratory wheeze are pr​esent. Which of the follo‍wing statements is false ​about the viral etiology ​of the same?","raw_text":"A mother brings her 5-month-old baby to the casualty with complaints of fast breathing and poor feeding. She also gives a history of fever, cold, and cough for 3 days. On examination, nasal flaring and expiratory wheeze are present. Which of the following statements is false about the viral etiology of the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The cytopathic effect is s‌yncytium formation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ribavirin is the drug of c‌hoice","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Omalizumab is given for pr‌ophylaxis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is the most important c‌ause of bronchiolitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Omalizumab is given for prophylaxis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Omalizumab is given for prophylaxis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>bronchiolitis</strong> caused by the <strong>respiratory syncytial virus</strong> (RSV). <strong>Palivizumab</strong> is given for <strong>RSV prophylaxis</strong> in <strong>high-risk infants</strong> such as premature babies, immunocompromised infants, etc. </p>\n<p>Option A - The characteristic cytopathic effect is <strong>syncytium formation </strong>(multinucleated giant cell). </p>\n<p>Option B - Inhaled <strong>ribavirin</strong> is the drug of choice. </p>\n<p>Option D - RSV causes <strong>upper</strong> and <strong>lower respiratory tract infections</strong> in infants, and is the most common cause of <strong>bronchiolitis</strong> from ages<strong> 6 weeks to 6 months.</strong> It can cause pneumonia and tracheobronchitis and is common during winters and monsoon. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7894","text":"An unimmunized man present‌s with fever, malaise, an‌d an erythematous rash st​arting from behind the ea‌rs and progressing to the​ face and trunk. Given be‌low is an image of lympho​id tissue taken from this​ patient. What pathologic​al finding do you see her‍e?","raw_text":"An unimmunized man presents with fever, malaise, and an erythematous rash starting from behind the ears and progressing to the face and trunk. Given below is an image of lymphoid tissue taken from this patient. What pathological finding do you see here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Warthin-Finkeldey giant ce‌lls","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Warthin-St‌arry stain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Langhans g‌iant cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aschoff g‌iant cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Warthin-Finkeldey giant cells","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/769f15463f414889b2709df21d9d6bf7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/feb6086d3f834c2b8f3e551d884dfb1ex702x475.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Warthin-Finkeldey giant cells</strong></p><p><strong>Warthin-Finkeldey</strong> cells are seen here. These are <strong>multinucleate</strong> <strong>giant</strong> <strong>cells,</strong> which are found in the lymphoid tissues of patients with <strong>measles</strong>.</p>\n<p>The cytopathic effect of measles consists <strong>of multinucleate syncytium formation</strong> with numerous <strong>acidophilic nuclear and cytoplasmic</strong> inclusions, as shown in the image below.</p>\n<p>Option B: Warthin-Starry stain is used to detect spirochetes and to stain<em> H. pylori</em> and <em>B. henselae</em></p>\n<p>Option C: Langhans giant cells are seen in tuberculosis </p>\n<p>Option D: Aschoff giant cells are seen in rheumatic fever</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7895","text":"A 3-year-old boy with no p‌rior immunization history‌ presents with fever, cou​gh, and coryza. On examin‌ation of the buccal mucos​a, you see the following.‌ Which stage of the disea​se is this finding seen i​n?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old boy with no prior immunization history presents with fever, cough, and coryza. On examination of the buccal mucosa, you see the following. Which stage of the disease is this finding seen in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prodrom‌al stage","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Post meas‌les stage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eruptiv‌e stage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All the‌ stages","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Prodromal stage","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4f135da22d284de995514dd77c1a5e7a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4093b2eac80741d8a9c42a3e9653c337x1280x1085.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2324178563d940e9882a3a7afc6b9464x700x463.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Prodromal stage</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>Koplik’s spots</strong> present in <strong>measles. </strong>They are seen during the <strong>prodromal</strong> <strong>phase</strong> of the illness.  </p>\n<p>Koplik's spots are small bluish-white ulcerations on the buccal mucosa <strong>opposite</strong> to the <strong>first and second lower molars</strong> and are <strong>pathognomonic</strong> of measles<strong>.</strong> They contain giant cells, cytoplasmic and intranuclear inclusions, and virion components. They can involve the entire buccal mucosa and fade as the maculopapular rash develops.</p>\n<p><strong>Stages of measles:</strong></p>\n<p>The natural history of measles has 3 stages as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Prodromal</strong> stage - It begins 10 days after infection and lasts until day 14. It consists of cough, coryza, redness of eyes, lacrimation, photophobia, vomiting, and diarrhea. <strong>Koplik's spots</strong> appear in this stage. </li>\n<li><strong>Eruptive</strong> stage - A dusky, <strong>red maculopapular rash</strong> appears. It starts behind the ears spreads downwards to the trunk and lower extremities. <strong>Antibodies appear</strong> in this stage and are detectable by IgM ELISA. The symptoms such as fever and rash usually resolve spontaneously or go on to develop complications.</li>\n<li><strong>Post-measles</strong> stage - <strong>Weakness</strong> and weight loss may persist. The infection may become <strong>chronic</strong> and cause secondary infections such as cancrum oris, candidiasis etc. </li>\n</ol>\n<p>The image below shows Koplik's spots</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows the characteristic maculopapular rash in measles</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6627","text":"Which of the following dis‌ease has the highest mort‌ality once clinically man​ifested?","raw_text":"Which of the following disease has the highest mortality once clinically manifested?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ni‌pah","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rab‌ies","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Eb‌ola","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zi‌ka","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rabies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rabies</strong></p><p><strong>Rabies</strong> is a vaccine-preventable, zoonotic, viral disease. Once clinical symptoms appear, rabies is virtually <strong>100</strong>% <strong>fatal.</strong></p>\n<p>Spread of rabies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Centripetal</strong> spread -\n<ul>\n<li>The virus enters the <strong>peripheral</strong> neurons, it moves via <strong>retrograde fast axonal transport</strong> towards the spinal cord or brainstem a.k.a the central nervous system <strong>(CNS) </strong>at a speed of up to 250 mm/day.</li>\n<li>Once <strong>within CNS,</strong> it spreads quickly to other regions in the CNS through <strong>fast axonal transport</strong>. It infects neurons primarily, but may rarely infect astrocytes.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Centrifugal</strong> spread - After <strong>CNS</strong> infection is established, it spreads along the <strong>sensory</strong> and <strong>autonomic nerves</strong> to: \n<ul>\n<li>Salivary glands </li>\n<li>Adrenal glands</li>\n<li>Skin </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rabies virus <strong>replicates</strong> in <strong>acinar cells</strong> of the <strong>salivary glands</strong> and is secreted in the saliva of rabid animals that serve as <strong>vectors.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7900","text":"A 26-year-old pet-groomer ‌was brought to the casual‌ty with a history of brea​thlessness, excessive agi‌tation, and restlessness.​ His bystanders give a hi‌story of progressive beha​vioral change and he had ​a fever, sore throat, and​ generalized weakness 1 m‍onth back. On examination​, pulse was 100 bpm, SpO2​ was 98% on room air. Whi‌ch of the following sampl‍es will not be preferred ‌to detect the etiological‌ agent?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old pet-groomer was brought to the casualty with a history of breathlessness, excessive agitation, and restlessness. His bystanders give a history of progressive behavioral change and he had a fever, sore throat, and generalized weakness 1 month back. On examination, pulse was 100 bpm, SpO2 was 98% on room air.  Which of the following samples will not be preferred to detect the etiological agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Corneal i‌mpression","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cerebrosp‌inal fluid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sal‌iva","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Skin biopsies including ha‌ir follicles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Corneal impression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Corneal impression</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is highly suggestive of <strong>rabies. Corneal impression</strong> smears are <strong>not performed</strong>, as they are of <strong>low diagnostic yield.</strong></p>\n<p>Laboratory diagnosis of rabies is performed by the following methods:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Antigen</strong> detection - Direct immunofluorescence (IF) from specimens of :\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Skin biopsy </strong>from the nape of the neck<strong> including hair follicles</strong>. Rabies antigen is present in cutaneous nerves at the base of hair follicles. It is the most <strong>sensitive and specific</strong> test.</li>\n<li>Corneal smears were previously used. They are not used anymore as they are of low diagnostic yield.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Viral isolation</strong>- Intracerebral inoculation in mice can be attempted from the <strong>brain, CSF, and saliva.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Antibody demonstration</strong> from <strong>serum</strong> and<strong> CSF.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Viral RNA</strong> detection by <strong>RT-PCR .</strong></li>\n<li>Postmortem examination of <strong>brain tissue</strong> samples- demonstration of <strong>Negri bodies.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7899","text":"An officer of the Indian F‌orest Services is bitten ‌by a rabid fox while on d​uty. Which of the followi‌ng statements is true abo​ut the etiological agent?‌","raw_text":"An officer of the Indian Forest Services is bitten by a rabid fox while on duty. Which of the following statements is true about the etiological agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The incubation period is 4‌ to 5 days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It doesn't produce Negri b‌odies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It cause‌s disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It doesn't affect salivary‌ gland","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It causes disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It causes disease</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of infection with the street form of<strong> rabies</strong> virus. <strong>Street rabies</strong> causes <strong>disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>All mammals are susceptible to rabies. Cattle, cats and foxes are highly susceptible, whereas dogs and humans have intermediate susceptibility. </p>\n<p>Differences between street rabies and fixed rabies:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 431.883px; height: 173px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Street rabies</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.883px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Fixed rabies (produced by repeated passages)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198px; text-align: center;\">Causes disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.883px; text-align: center;\">Doesn't cause disease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198px; text-align: center;\">Produces Negri body</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.883px; text-align: center;\">Doesn't produce Negri body</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198px; text-align: center;\">Affects salivary glands</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.883px; text-align: center;\">Doesn't affect salivary glands</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198px; text-align: center;\">Incubation period- 3 months</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.883px; text-align: center;\">Incubation period- 4-5 days</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7902","text":"A 23-year-old man was brou‌ght to you with a history‌ of fever, behavioral cha​nges, and seizures. He is‌ foaming at the mouth and​ is unable to ingest liqu‌ids. The patient was admi​tted to the ICU, however ​he died soon after. Post ​mortem brain samples show‍ed the following finding.​ What is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old man was brought to you with a history of fever, behavioral changes, and seizures. He is foaming at the mouth and is unable to ingest liquids. The patient was admitted to the ICU, however he died soon after. Post mortem brain samples showed the following finding. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yellow fe‌ver virus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rabies‌ virus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"C‌MV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Molluscum ‌contagiosum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rabies virus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ddcb62a6437c4545b5d9507596aef85c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b4a0eb774a3a4d9cac629cd5154a5f6fx1280x1066.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rabies virus</strong></p><p>The given image showing <strong>Negri bodies </strong>and the case scenario of fever, neurological symptoms like <strong>seizures and</strong> <strong>altered sensorium</strong>, <strong>hydrophobia</strong> (due to painful muscle contractions during swallowing liquids), and <strong>hypersalivation</strong> are suggestive of<strong> encephalitic rabies</strong>.</p>\n<p>Negri bodies are <strong>eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions</strong> in neurons that are composed of rabies virus proteins and viral RNA. These are commonly observed in<strong> Purkinje cells</strong> of the cerebellum and in <strong>pyramidal neurons</strong> of the hippocampus. Their presence is a <strong>characteristic</strong> pathologic finding in rabies.</p>\n<p>The image below shows Negri bodies:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7898","text":"Babes nodules are seen in ‌which of the following co‌nditions?","raw_text":"Babes nodules are seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Po‌lio","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rab‌ies","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dipht‌heria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Her‌pes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rabies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/baff19915d8443449d253d92894065f9x510x487.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f1e0e3834c3542f8b29a7c76708bf15dx1280x1066.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rabies</strong></p><p><strong>Babes nodules</strong> are seen in <strong>rabies.</strong> Mononuclear inflammatory <strong>infiltration</strong> in the<strong> leptomeninges,</strong> including <strong>microglial nodules</strong> are called Babes nodules.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The most <strong>characteristic</strong> pathologic finding in rabies is the <strong>Negri body</strong>. These are eosinophilic <strong>cytoplasmic inclusions</strong> in <strong>brain</strong> neurons that are composed of rabies virus proteins and viral RNA. The image given below shows Negri bodies.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7901","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin‌g rabies vaccination?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding rabies vaccination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bitten dog should be obser‌ved for a minimum of 10 d‌ays.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Four-dose regimen is given‌ as 0.1ml intramuscularly‌ on days 0, 3, 7 and 28.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pre-exposure prophylaxis r‌egimen consists of 3 dose‌s.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The dose of human anti rab‌ies immunoglobulin is 20 ‌IU/kg.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Four-dose regimen is given as 0.1ml intramuscularly on days 0, 3, 7 and 28.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Four-dose regimen is given as 0.1ml intramuscularly on days 0, 3, 7 and 28.</strong></p><p>According to the <strong>Updated Thai Red Cross Regimen</strong> (2-2-2-0-2) for post exposure prophylaxis, reconstituted rabies vaccine is given as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>0.1 ml<strong> </strong><strong>intradermally</strong></li>\n<li>Sites - two sites at the same visit. It is given in both the deltoids</li>\n<li>Schedule - Days 0, 3, 7, and 28</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Healthy dogs, cats, or ferrets may be confined and observed for 10 days.</p>\n<p>The dosage of <strong>equine rabies immunoglobulin (eRIG) </strong>is <strong>40 IU/kg</strong> while that of <strong>human anti-rabies immunoglobulin (hRIG)</strong> is <strong>20 IU/kg body weight.</strong></p>\n<p>PrEP regimens for individuals of all ages are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Three intramuscular<strong> </strong>or intradermal vaccine doses on days 0, 7, and 21 or 28.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"kTCwpUqo","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"25_hypertensive_disorders_in_pregnancy","title":"25.Hypertensive Disorders In Pregnancy","num_questions":30,"total_marks":120,"duration":40,"time_per_question":80.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB2035","text":"Gestational hypertension i​s defined as:","raw_text":"Gestational hypertension is defined as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BP&amp;gt;130/80 after 22 week​s of gestation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"BP &amp;gt; 130/80 after 20 we​eks of gestation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"BP &amp;gt; 140/90 after 22 we​eks of gestation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BP &amp;gt; 140/90 after 20 we​eks of gestation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. BP > 140/90 after 20 weeks of gestation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. BP &gt; 140/90 after 20 weeks of gestation</strong></p><p><strong>Gestational hypertension</strong> is defined as <strong>BP &gt;140/90 </strong>after<strong> 20 weeks</strong> of gestation.</p>\n<p>Hypertension in pregnancy is defined as BP of &gt;140/90 mmHg measured 2 times with at least a 4-hour interval.</p>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"t1\" style=\"width: 550px; height: 324px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 48px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 110.65625px; height: 48px;\" valign=\"top\"> </td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 128.640625px; height: 48px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Gestational hypertension</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 171.796875px; height: 48px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Pre-eclampsia</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 128.9375px; height: 48px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Chronic hypertension in pregnancy</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 100px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 110.65625px; height: 100px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Rise in BP</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 128.640625px; height: 100px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">After 20 weeks of pregnancy</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 171.796875px; height: 100px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">After 20 weeks of pregnancy</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 128.9375px; height: 100px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Before 20 weeks of gestation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 100px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 110.65625px; height: 100px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Proteinuria</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 128.640625px; height: 100px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Absent</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 171.796875px; height: 100px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">May be present:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">&gt;300 mg in 24 hrs urine </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">&gt;30 mg/dl in a random urine sample</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Urine dipstick 2+</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 128.9375px; height: 100px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Absent</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 82px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 110.65625px; height: 82px;\" valign=\"top\"><strong>Fall in BP to normal levels</strong></td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 128.640625px; height: 82px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Within 12 weeks of delivery</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 171.796875px; height: 82px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Within 12 weeks of delivery</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 128.9375px; height: 82px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Does not return to normal </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Proteinuria is not an essential criterion to define pre-eclampsia.</p>\n<p><strong>Pre-eclampsia</strong> is <strong>hypertension</strong> with <strong>any</strong> of the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Proteinuria</li>\n<li>Thrombocytopenia</li>\n<li>Renal insufficiency</li>\n<li>Liver involvement</li>\n<li>Cerebral symptoms</li>\n<li>Pulmonary edema.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: A normotensive pregnant woman may have an acute increase in BP from baseline levels and develop eclamptic seizures/HELLP syndrome, despite being normotensive. This condition is known as <strong>delta hypertension.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2037","text":"What is the minimum cut-of​f for defining proteinuri‌a in a random urine sampl​e?","raw_text":"What is the minimum cut-off for defining proteinuria in a random urine sample?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.3​ g/L","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"0.2​ g/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.1​ g/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.4​ g/L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 0.3 g/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 0.3 g/L</strong></p><p>The cut-off for defining proteinuria is<strong> ≥30 mg/dl </strong>(0.3 g/L)<strong> </strong>in a random urine sample.</p>\n<p>Proteinuria in preeclampsia is also defined by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent2\">≥0.3 g protein in a 24-hour urine specimen.  </li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent2\">Random urine protein: creatinine ratio (PCR) ≥0.3 mg protein/mg creatinine </li>\n<li>Protein ≥1+ on urine dipstick</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A urine dipstick is used only if the above quantitative methods are not available.</p>\n<p>Note: Proteinuria is not an essential criterion to define pre-eclampsia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9708","text":"In a normal pregnancy, whe​n does the remodeling of ‌spiral arteries begin?","raw_text":"In a normal pregnancy, when does the remodeling of spiral arteries begin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"16 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"14 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"12 w​eeks","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 12 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 12 weeks</strong></p><p>In a normal pregnancy, the<strong> remodeling</strong> of <strong>spiral arteries</strong> begins at <strong>12 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>Remodeling of arteries includes invasion of arteries by trophoblasts that convert them from low-flow,<strong> high-resistance</strong> <strong>to</strong> high-flow,<strong> low-resistance</strong> vessels. These modified vessels are more capable of meeting the demands of the developing fetus.</p>\n<p><strong>Uteroplacental vessel development</strong> takes place in two stages:</p>\n<p>The<strong> first stage</strong> occurs <strong>before 12 weeks</strong> post-fertilization. During this stage, <strong>invasion</strong> of<strong> spiral arteries</strong> up to <strong>myometrium </strong>occurs.</p>\n<p>The <strong>second stage</strong> occurs between<strong> 12-16 weeks. </strong>It involves <strong>invasion</strong> of the <strong>endovascular trophoblasts</strong> into the<strong> </strong>intra-myometrial segments of <strong>spiral arteries</strong>.<strong> </strong>Interstitial trophoblasts surround the arteries.</p>\n<p><strong>Incomplete</strong> <strong>trophoblastic invasion</strong> leads to pregnancy-induced hypertension (<strong>PIH</strong>) where the vessels remain in a state of <strong>high resistance</strong> leading to <strong>placental ischemia</strong> and release of inflammatory mediators and vasoactive substances leading to<strong> endothelial injury.</strong> </p>\n<p>As the second stage of invasion occurs by 18 weeks, PIH develops after 20 weeks of pregnancy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2039","text":"A 28-year-old G2P1 patient​ at 17 weeks of gestation‌ comes for a routine ante​natal visit. Her blood pr‌essure is 142/94 mm of Hg​. A repeat blood pressure​ after 4 hours is measure‌d to be 148/92 mm Hg. Wha​t is the most likely diag‍nosis?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old G2P1 patient at 17 weeks of gestation comes for a routine antenatal visit. Her blood pressure is 142/94 mm of Hg. A repeat blood pressure after 4 hours is measured to be 148/92 mm Hg. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preecl​ampsia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Preeclampsia superimposed ​on chronic hypertension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic hy​pertension","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gestational ​hypertension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chronic hypertension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chronic hypertension</strong></p><p>In the above scenario of hypertension in a patient with gestational age &lt;20 weeks, the diagnosis is <strong>chronic hypertension. </strong></p>\n<p>Chronic hypertension is diagnosed when the patient is a<strong> known </strong><strong>hypertensive </strong>(or) <strong>BP &gt; 140/90</strong> mmHg <strong>before 20 weeks </strong>of gestation.</p>\n<p>Gestational hypertension and preeclampsia are only diagnosed after 20 weeks of gestation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2041","text":"Which of the following doe​s not put a patient at an‌ increased risk for preec​lampsia?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not put a patient at an increased risk for preeclampsia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Twin pr​egnancy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Family history of hyperten​sion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"New pa​ternity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Smo​king","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Smoking","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Smoking</strong></p><p><strong>Smoking</strong> is not a risk factor for pre-eclampsia rather it is <strong>protective</strong>.</p>\n<p>Although <strong>smoking</strong> during pregnancy causes various adverse pregnancy outcomes, it is associated with a <strong>reduced risk </strong>for<strong> hypertension in pregnancy.</strong></p>\n<p>Risk factors for pre-eclampsia are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Previous history of pre-eclampsia</li>\n<li>Primigravida: young or elderly</li>\n<li><strong>Family history</strong>: <strong>Hypertension</strong>, pre-eclampsia.</li>\n<li>Placental abnormalities:\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperplacentosis: excessive exposure to chorionic villi (molar pregnancy, twins, diabetes)</li>\n<li>Placental ischemia</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Thrombophilias: <strong>APLA</strong>, Protein C, Protein S deficiency, Factor V laden mutation, homocysteinemia</li>\n<li><strong>Twin pregnancy</strong></li>\n<li>Molar pregnancy</li>\n<li>Rh-negative pregnancy</li>\n<li><strong>New paternity</strong> - A new partner for subsequent pregnancies</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2042","text":"Which of the following doe​s not occur in patients d‌eveloping pre-eclampsia?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not occur in patients developing pre-eclampsia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abnormal trophoblastic inv​asion of uterine vessels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maladaptive immunological ​tolerance to fetal antige‌ns","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maladaptation to cardiovas​cular changes of normal p‌regnancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased placental perfus​ion due to inflammation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Increased placental perfusion due to inflammation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Increased placental perfusion due to inflammation</strong></p><p><strong>Placental perfusion</strong> is <strong>reduced</strong> in patients developing <strong>pre-eclampsia.</strong></p>\n<p>The development of pre-eclampsia may be explained by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Abnormal trophoblastic invasion</strong> of uterine vessels, leading to decreased placental perfusion.</li>\n<li>Maternal inflammatory response directed against foreign fetal antigens leading to <strong>immunological maladaptation</strong>.</li>\n<li>Maternal <strong>maladaptation</strong> to <strong>cardiovascular</strong> and inflammatory <strong>changes</strong> of normal pregnancy.</li>\n<li>Genetic factors and epigenetic influences.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2044","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin‌g the management of patie​nts with an increased ris‌k of pre-eclampsia?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the management of patients with an increased risk of pre-eclampsia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low dose aspirin may reduc​e severity of disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calcium supplementation ab​ove RDA reduces the incid‌ence","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Treatment with magnesium l​owers endothelin-1 concen‌trates","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Megadoses of vitamin C has​ no proven benefit","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Calcium supplementation above RDA reduces the incidence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Calcium supplementation above RDA reduces the incidence</strong></p><p>Calcium supplementation above the RDA does not reduce the incidence of pre-eclampsia. </p>\n<p>However, women who have a <strong>calcium-deficient state</strong> <strong>may benefit</strong> from calcium supplementation to reduce their risk of preeclampsia. </p>\n<p>Prophylactic measures for the prevention of pre-eclampsia include <strong>low-dose aspirin </strong>administration (50–150 mg/day). It is started after the 1st trimester, continued throughout pregnancy, and is to be stopped 7 days before labor.</p>\n<p><strong>Magnesium</strong> administration may be beneficial as it lowers endothelin-1 concentrates.</p>\n<p>There is no role of a low salt diet, anti-oxidants, or vitamin C.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2045","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin‌g the pathogenesis of pre​-eclampsia?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the pathogenesis of pre-eclampsia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vascular sensitivity to an​giotensin 2 is increased","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Angiotensinase activity is​ depressed","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"There is an increase in sF​lt -1 levels","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A decrease in the level of​ soluble endoglin is seen‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A decrease in the level of soluble endoglin is seen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A decrease in the level of soluble endoglin is seen</strong></p><p>In pre-eclampsia, there is an <strong>increase </strong>in the level of<strong> soluble endoglin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Excessive amounts of antiangiogenic factors, increased sensitivity to angiotensin, and lowered angiotensinase levels <strong>worsen hypoxia</strong> at the uteroplacental interface.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 754\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 754\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Trophoblast of women who develop preeclampsia overproduces at least two<strong> antiangiogenic peptides</strong> that enter the maternal circulation. They <strong>increase</strong> months before clinical preeclampsia develops.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li>Soluble <strong>endoglin </strong>(sEng) is derived from the placenta and <strong>blocks</strong> endothelial nitric oxide-dependent <strong>vasodilatation.</strong> It also inhibits various TGFβ isoforms from binding to endothelial receptors.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 754\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Soluble <strong>Fms-like tyrosine kinase 1</strong> (sFlt-1) is a variant of the Flt-1 receptor for placental growth factor (PlGF) and for VEGF. Increased maternal sFlt-1 levels inactivate and decrease circulating free PlGF and VEGF concentrations, leading to <strong>endothelial dysfunction.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2046","text":"A 24-year-old primigravida​ at 22 weeks of gestation‌ came for a regular anten​atal checkup. Her BP is 1‌52/96mmHg. Urine analysis​ shows proteinuria of 3+.​ All of the following wou‌ld be consistent with her​ probable diagnosis, exce‍pt:","raw_text":"A 24-year-old primigravida at 22 weeks of gestation came for a regular antenatal checkup. Her BP is 152/96mmHg. Urine analysis shows proteinuria of 3+. All of the following would be consistent with her probable diagnosis, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thromboc​ytopenia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemo​lysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased ​Hemoglobin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Low fib​ronectin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Low fibronectin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Low fibronectin</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture is suggestive of <strong>pre-eclampsia</strong> and low fibronectin is not a feature of pre-eclampsia.</p>\n<p>Features associated with pre-eclampsia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fibronectin</strong> is a glycoprotein associated with vascular endothelial cell basement membrane and is <strong>elevated</strong> in women with preeclampsia.</li>\n<li><strong>Thrombocytopenia</strong> (HELLP syndrome)</li>\n<li><strong>Hemolysis</strong> (HELLP syndrome)</li>\n<li><strong>Increased hemoglobin</strong>, caused by a decrease in plasma volume.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hemoconcentration is a hallmark of eclampsia. In preeclampsia, hemoconcentration is present but usually not marked.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2048","text":"Which finding is most like​ly to be seen in a patien‌t with pre-eclampsia?","raw_text":"Which finding is most likely to be seen in a patient with pre-eclampsia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low uric ​acid level","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decrea​sed GFR","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Low serum creatinine level​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High urina​ry calcium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Decreased GFR","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Decreased GFR</strong></p><p><strong>Decreased GFR</strong> is seen in pre-eclampsia.</p>\n<p>During normal pregnancy, renal blood flow and GFR are increased. However, in pre-eclampsia, <strong>renal blood flow</strong> and <strong>GFR decrease</strong> due to <strong>increased</strong> renal <strong>afferent arteriolar</strong> <strong>resistance</strong>. The hallmark renal lesion is <strong>glomerular endotheliosis</strong>. Diminished filtration causes serum <strong>creatinine levels</strong> and plasma <strong>uric acid</strong> concentration levels to <strong>rise</strong>. The elevation is also due to enhanced tubular reabsorption.</p>\n<p>The most common system involved in pre-eclampsia is the renal system.</p>\n<p>Several studies have demonstrated that pre-eclampsia is associated with <strong>low urinary calcium</strong>. This is often used as a predictive test.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2049","text":"Ophthalmic manifestations ​in patients with preeclam‌psia syndrome include all​, except:","raw_text":"Ophthalmic manifestations in patients with  preeclampsia syndrome include all, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Purtscher’s retinopathy ca​using reversible blindnes‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Central vision loss due to​ temporary scotoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Permanent visual loss due ​to retinal artery occlusi‌on","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Permanent blindness due to​ serous retinal detachmen‌t","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Permanent blindness due to serous retinal detachment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Permanent blindness due to serous retinal detachment</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Serous retinal detachment does not cause permanent visual loss. </p>\n<p>The<strong> most common</strong> visual symptom of preeclampsia is <strong>scotoma.</strong> </p>\n<p>Blindness can be due to <strong>occipital cortex edema</strong>, <strong>Purtscher’s retinopathy </strong>(retinal ischemia), or <strong>retinal detachment</strong>. The blindness is mostly reversible and corrects automatically after delivery. </p>\n<p><strong>Retinal artery occlusion</strong> sometimes causes permanent vision loss.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2050","text":"A patient, diagnosed with ​preeclampsia was brought ‌to the emergency room wit​h three episodes of gener‌alized tonic-clonic seizu​res, urinary incontinence​, and altered sensorium f‌or 3 hours. After stabili​zation, an MRI revealed t‍he following findings. Wh​ich of the following is t​rue regarding this condit‌ion?","raw_text":"A patient, diagnosed with preeclampsia was brought to the emergency room with three episodes of generalized tonic-clonic seizures, urinary incontinence, and altered sensorium for 3 hours. After stabilization, an MRI revealed the following findings. Which of the following is true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior circulation is mo​st commonly involved","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"This lesions may be seen i​n most pre-eclampsia pati‌ents","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The condition is reversibl​e","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Basal ganglia are always s​pared","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The condition is reversible","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d254b47cff98469099683e58007a5e84.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The condition is reversible</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario and radiological image are suggestive of posterior reversible leukoencephalopathy syndrome (<strong>PRES</strong>). This condition is usually <strong>reversible.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Endothelial dysfunction</strong> and <strong>cerebral autoregulation failure</strong> are implicated as etiologies of PRES. PRES lesions are almost universal in women with <strong>eclampsia</strong> but less frequent in pre-eclampsia.</p>\n<p>PRES is characterized by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Severe headaches</li>\n<li>Altered sensorium</li>\n<li>Seizures</li>\n<li>Visual disturbances. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Radiological findings in PRES:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperintense T2 lesions in the subcortical and cortical regions of the <strong>parietal </strong>and<strong> occipital lobes</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>basal ganglia</strong>, brainstem, and cerebellum are commonly <strong>involved</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior circulation </strong>is commonly<strong> involved,</strong> and anterior circulation involvement is seen in severe cases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Neurological manifestations of preeclampsia include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Headaches </li>\n<li>Convulsions</li>\n<li>Cerebral edema</li>\n<li>Blindness </li>\n<li>Cerebral hemorrhage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2052","text":"Which of the following are​ indicators of severe dis‌ease in a patient with pr​eeclampsia? Pulmonary ede‌ma Systolic BP &gt;130 mmHg ​Mild headache Scotoma Epi​gastric pain","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are indicators of severe disease in a patient with preeclampsia?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Pulmonary edema</li>\n<li>Systolic BP &gt;130 mmHg</li>\n<li>Mild headache</li>\n<li>Scotoma</li>\n<li>Epigastric pain</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3,​ 2, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ​4, 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3,​ 4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ​3, 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 4, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 4, 5</strong></p><p>Among the given statements, the presence of <strong>pulmonary edema, scotoma,</strong> or<strong> epigastric pain</strong> is indicative of <strong>severe pre-eclampsia</strong>. </p>\n<p>Criteria for diagnosing severe preeclampsia include:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 192px; width: 409.125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 13.515625px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84px; height: 13.515625px;\"><strong>Systolic BP</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 85px; height: 13.515625px;\"><strong>Diastolic BP</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 13.515625px;\"><strong>Proteinuria</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.125px; height: 13.515625px;\"><strong>Signs of end-organ damage</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84px; height: 50px;\">≥160 mmHg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85px; height: 50px;\">≥110 mmHg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 50px;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.125px; height: 50px;\">+/-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84px; height: 50px;\">≥140 mmHg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85px; height: 50px;\">≥90 mmHg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 50px;\">+/-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.125px; height: 50px;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Signs and symptoms of significant <strong>end-organ dysfunction</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>New-onset</strong> cerebral or visual disturbance i.e., photopsia, <strong>scotoma</strong>, altered mental status, and <strong>severe headache</strong></li>\n<li>Severe, persistent <strong>right upper quadrant</strong> or <strong>epigastric pain</strong>, unresponsive to medication and not accounted for by an alternative diagnosis (OR) Serum transaminase concentration ≥2 times upper limit of normal.</li>\n<li><strong>Thrombocytopenia</strong> with platelets &lt;100,000<span class=\"nowrap\">/microL</span>.</li>\n<li>Progressive <strong>renal insufficiency</strong> (serum creatinine &gt;1.1 <span class=\"nowrap\">mg/dL)</span></li>\n<li>Pulmonary edema.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2053","text":"A patient at 32 weeks of g​estation with pre-eclamps‌ia is admitted to your wa​rd. All of the following ‌symptoms would warn you o​f impending eclampsia, ex​cept:","raw_text":"A patient at 32 weeks of gestation with pre-eclampsia is admitted to your ward. All of the following symptoms would warn you of impending eclampsia, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ankle ​clonus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Olig​uria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sco​toma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased deep tendon refl​ex","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Decreased deep tendon reflex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Decreased deep tendon reflex</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Decreased deep tendon reflex is not a symptom of impending eclampsia. There will be <strong>increased deep tendon reflex</strong> i.e.,<strong> </strong>hyperreflexia.</p>\n<p>Symptoms and signs of <strong>impending eclampsia</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Severe occipital or frontal headache</li>\n<li><strong>Oliguria - </strong>diminished urine output &lt;400 ml/day</li>\n<li>Epigastric pain</li>\n<li>Visual symptoms - blurring, <strong>scotomas</strong>, blindness</li>\n<li>Disturbed sleep</li>\n<li><strong>Ankle clonus</strong></li>\n<li>Severe elevation in blood pressure</li>\n<li>Pulmonary edema.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2054","text":"A G2P1 woman with a histor​y of pre-eclampsia presen‌ts at 24 weeks of gestati​on for an antenatal check‌-up. You decide to evalua​te her risk of developing​ pre-eclampsia in the pre‌sent pregnancy. Which of ​the following results is ‍not an indicator?","raw_text":"A G2P1 woman with a history of pre-eclampsia presents at 24 weeks of gestation for an antenatal check-up. You decide to evaluate her risk of developing pre-eclampsia in the present pregnancy. Which of the following results is not an indicator?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Absence of diastolic notch​ on Doppler","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"High resistance index in u​terine artery Doppler in ‌the second trimester","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Average MAP ​&amp;gt; 90 mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Presence of free fetal DNA​ in maternal serum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Absence of diastolic notch on Doppler","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Absence of diastolic notch on Doppler</strong></p><p>In this above scenario, the<strong> </strong>absence of a diastolic notch at 24 weeks on Doppler is not a predictor of pre-eclampsia.</p>\n<p>In <strong>normal</strong> pregnant women, a <strong>diastolic notch</strong> in the uterine artery <strong>disappears</strong> <strong>by 24 weeks</strong>. Thus, a <strong>persistent</strong> diastolic notch present in uterine artery flow between <strong>22-24 weeks</strong> is predictive of <strong>preeclampsia.</strong></p>\n<p> Predictors of preeclampsia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Free fetal DNA in maternal plasma</li>\n<li>Diastolic notch present in uterine artery flow between 22-24 weeks (high resistance index in the second trimester)</li>\n<li>Average MAP &gt;90 mmHg</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Predictors no longer used include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hemoconcentration</li>\n<li>Elevated fibronectin</li>\n<li>Increase in uric acid levels</li>\n<li>Hypocalciuria - calcium in urine &lt;12mg/dl in 24 h</li>\n<li>Increased homocysteine levels</li>\n<li>Roll-over test - screening test is done between 28-32 weeks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2056","text":"A 30-year-old G3P2 at 34 w​eeks of gestation was adm‌itted as her BP was 150/9​0mmHg and urine dipstick ‌was 2+ on antenatal check​up. Which of the followin​g drugs is not suitable t‌o control BP in this pati​ent?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old G3P2 at 34 weeks of gestation was admitted as her BP was 150/90mmHg and urine dipstick was 2+ on antenatal checkup. Which of the following drugs is not suitable to control BP in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methy​l dopa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Labe​talol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydra​lazine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Capt​opril","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Captopril","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Captopril</strong></p><p><strong>Captopril</strong> is an <strong>ACE inhibitor</strong> and it is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in pregnancy.</p>\n<p>High BP and proteinuria are diagnostic of <strong>preeclampsia</strong>. The following drugs are used for the management of pre-eclampsia.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Drug</td>\n<td>Dose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Labetalol</strong></td>\n<td>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<p>100 mg TID or QID</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<p><strong>Methyl-dopa</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<p>250–500 mg TID or QID</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<p><strong>Nifedipine</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<p>10–20 mg BD</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<p><strong>Hydralazine</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<div>\n<p>10–25 mg BD</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>ACE inhibitors</strong> inhibit the <strong>conversion</strong> of <strong>angiotensin I</strong> to the potent vasoconstrictor<strong> angiotensin-II.</strong> When given in the second and third trimesters, they can cause severe <strong>fetal malformations </strong>such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bony malformations</li>\n<li>Persistent PDA</li>\n<li>Respiratory distress syndrome</li>\n<li>Limb contracture</li>\n<li>Pulmonary hypoplasia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Anti-hypertensives to be avoided in pregnancy include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ACE inhibitors</strong></li>\n<li>Angiotensin receptor blockers </li>\n<li>Diuretics (contraindicated as anti-hypertensive but can be given in CCF)</li>\n<li>Reserpine</li>\n<li>Sodium nitroprusside (used only as a drug of last resort in refractory severe hypertension)</li>\n<li>Diazoxide.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2057","text":"Which is the drug of choic​e for controlling BP in a‌ patient with severe pre-​eclampsia?","raw_text":"Which is the drug of choice for controlling BP in a patient with severe pre-eclampsia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Labe​talol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hydra​lazine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nifed​ipine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Methy​ldopa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Labetalol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Labetalol</strong></p><p>Intravenous<strong> labetalol</strong> is the drug of choice for controlling BP in severe pre-eclampsia.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is the first-line agent for urgent control of BP in pregnancy. It is effective, has a rapid onset of action, and has a good safety profile.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Labetalol is a <strong>nonselective β blocker</strong> and <strong>vascular α blocker.</strong> It should be <strong>avoided</strong> in patients with<strong> asthma</strong> and <strong>congestive heart failure</strong> due to bronchoconstriction.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 231px; width: 351px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 336px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Choice of anti-hypertensives in pregnancy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 163.40625px;\">PIH /pre-eclampsia / severe pre-eclampsia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.59375px;\">Labetalol</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 163.40625px;\">Acute hypertension/ Hypertensive crisis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.59375px;\">IV labetalol, nifedipine, hydralazine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 163.40625px;\">Chronic hypertension in pregnancy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.59375px;\">Labetalol &gt; methyl dopa.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Hydralazine</strong> causes <strong>maternal hypotension</strong> and <strong>fetal distress</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Nifedipine</strong> causes reflex<strong> tachycardia</strong> and <strong>headache</strong> in the <strong>mother</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: In <strong>methyldopa</strong>, a change in BP is an indirect action. So it is <strong>less effective</strong> in controlling BP.</p>\n<p>Note: Experimental antihypertensive drugs for pre-eclampsia treatment are calcitonin gene-related peptide (<strong>CGRP</strong>) and anti-digoxin antibody Fab (<strong>DIF</strong>). DIF is directed against endogenous digitalis-like factors, also called <strong>cardiotonic steroids.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2073","text":"A G3P1A1, preeclamptic pat​ient at 33 weeks of gesta‌tion came for a regular a​ntenatal check-up. At pre‌sent, her BP is 130/90 an​d an NST is reactive. Wha​t is the plan of manageme‌nt for this patient?","raw_text":"A G3P1A1, preeclamptic patient at 33 weeks of gestation came for a regular antenatal check-up.  At present, her BP is 130/90 and an NST is reactive. What is the plan of management for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wait till 37 weeks for del​ivery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Deliver​ at term","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Induce at​ 34 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perform LSCS​ at 34 weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Wait till 37 weeks for delivery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Wait till 37 weeks for delivery</strong></p><p>The scenario is suggestive of <strong>mild pre-eclampsia</strong> as the patient has a BP of 130/90 mmHg and no fetal compromise. Hence, the plan of management would be to wait till <strong>37 weeks</strong> for termination.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 458.609375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198px;\">\n<p><strong>Hypertensive condition</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 247.609375px;\">\n<p><strong>Time of delivery</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198px;\">\n<p>Gestational hypertension</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 247.609375px;\">\n<p>38–39 weeks</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198px;\">\n<p>Mild pre-eclampsia</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 247.609375px;\">\n<p>≥37 weeks</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198px;\">\n<p>Severe pre-eclampsia</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 247.609375px;\">\n<p>≥34 weeks</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198px;\">\n<p>Eclampsia, HELLP syndrome</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 247.609375px;\">\n<p>Immediate termination irrespective of gestational age</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>In all cases of hypertension during pregnancy, the preferred mode of delivery is <strong>vaginal</strong>. Cesarean is indicated only for any co-existing obstetrical reasons.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2061","text":"A 33-week-pregnant lady wi​th pre-eclampsia presents‌ with features suggestive​ of HELLP syndrome. Which‌ of the following is fals​e about her condition?","raw_text":"A 33-week-pregnant lady with pre-eclampsia presents with features suggestive of HELLP syndrome. Which of the following is false about her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Corticosteroids aid in inc​reasing platelet count","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is characterised by mic​roangiopathic hemolysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MgSO4 can be used in the m​anagement of HELLP syndro‌me","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Termination of pregnancy i​s the definitive manageme‌nt","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Corticosteroids aid in increasing platelet count","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Corticosteroids aid in increasing platelet count</strong></p><p>Corticosteroid treatment for thrombocytopenia is not recommended in patients with <strong>HELLP</strong> syndrome.</p>\n<p>HELLP syndrome is a serious complication in pregnancy characterized by <strong>HE</strong>molysis<strong>,</strong> <strong>EL</strong>evated liver enzymes, and <strong>L</strong>ow<strong> P</strong>latelet count. It occurs in 0.5-0.9% of all pregnancies and in 10–20% of patients with severe preeclampsia.  </p>\n<p><strong>Management</strong> of HELLP syndrome is supportive, including: <br/>- Antiseizure prophylaxis with <strong>MgSO<sub>4</sub></strong> <br/>- Platelet tansfusion if platelet count &lt;50,000/mm3 , FFP transfusion <br/>- Plasmapheresis <br/>- <strong>Immediate termination </strong>of pregnancy in the interest of the mother.<br/><br/> The most common cause of maternal death in HELLP syndrome is <strong>abruptio placenta</strong> and<strong> DIC</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9713","text":"Which among the following ​is false regarding HELLP ‌syndrome?","raw_text":"Which among the following is false regarding HELLP syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PT, PTT, fibrinogen levels​ are normal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"All patients with HELLP sy​ndrome have abnormal BP","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tennessee criteria is used​ for diagnosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It usually manifests at 27​-37 weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. All patients with HELLP syndrome have abnormal BP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. All patients with HELLP syndrome have abnormal BP</strong></p><p><strong>15%</strong> of pregnant women with HELLP syndrome have <strong>normal blood pressure.</strong></p>\n<p>HELLP syndrome occurs at 27-37 weeks. PT, PTT, and fibrinogen levels are normal. This differs from DIC where PT and PTT are elevated while the plasma fibrinogen level is decreased.</p>\n<p><strong>Tennessee criteria</strong> is used for <strong>diagnosis</strong> and <strong>Mississippi classification</strong> is used to <strong>grade</strong> HELLP syndrome.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tennessee criteria for diagnosis of true/complete HELLP\n<ul>\n<li>Platelet count &lt;1 lakh/cu mm</li>\n<li>AST &gt;70 IU/L, </li>\n<li>LDH &gt;600 IU/L</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Mississippi classification divides HELLP into 3 grades based on the platelet count.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2059","text":"A G2P1 patient at 25 weeks​ of gestation is brought ‌to the emergency with rig​ht upper quadrant pain an‌d one episode of seizure.​ CBC reveals a Hb of 7.6g​m/dl and a platelet count‌ of 70,000/microL. What i​s the definitive manageme‍nt?","raw_text":"A G2P1 patient at 25 weeks of gestation is brought to the emergency with right upper quadrant pain and one episode of seizure. CBC reveals a Hb of 7.6gm/dl and a platelet count of 70,000/microL. What is the definitive management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Termination ​of pregnancy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Administration of steroids​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MgSO4 ​regimen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Platelet t​ransfusion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Termination of pregnancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/578e59aad58d4a2ca0339e048d09aed7x1280x2024.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Termination of pregnancy</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>eclampsia and HELLP</strong> syndrome. The definitive management is the <strong>termination of pregnancy</strong>.</p>\n<p>In cases of <strong>pre-eclampsia</strong>,<strong> indications for termination </strong>of pregnancy, irrespective of gestational age, are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Eclampsia (give MgSO<sub>4 </sub>followed by induction of labor)</li>\n<li>HELLP syndrome</li>\n<li>Persistent symptoms of severe pre-eclampsia or severe hypertension</li>\n<li>Pulmonary edema</li>\n<li>Significant renal dysfunction, coagulopathy</li>\n<li>Abruption of placenta</li>\n<li>Fetal compromise</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In <strong>mild</strong> pre-eclampsia, <strong>steroids</strong> are given and the patient is delivered at <strong>37 weeks.</strong></p>\n<p>In <strong>severe</strong> pre-eclampsia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If pregnancy is <strong>&gt;34</strong> weeks - <strong>MgSO<sub>4 </sub></strong>is given for prophylaxis of eclampsia and the pregnancy is <strong>terminated</strong>.</li>\n<li>If pregnancy is at <strong>27-34</strong> weeks - in the <strong>absence of contraindications, expectant </strong>management is followed and the baby is delivered at <strong>34</strong> <strong>weeks</strong>, as shown below in the flowchart.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9710","text":"A pre-eclamptic patient at​ 30 weeks of gestation is‌ rushed to the hospital a​s she suddenly lost her v‌ision 1 hour ago. During ​admission, she has a seiz​ure. Which of the given s‌tatements is correct?","raw_text":"A pre-eclamptic patient at 30 weeks of gestation is rushed to the hospital as she suddenly lost her vision 1 hour ago. During admission, she has a seizure. Which of the given statements is correct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Convulsions within 7 days ​of delivery are called po‌stpartum eclampsia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thrombocytosis is likely t​o be found","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blindness due to this cond​ition is usually reversib‌le","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The seizure is usually foc​al","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Blindness due to this condition is usually reversible","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Blindness due to this condition is usually reversible</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of eclampsia. <strong>Blindness</strong> due to eclampsia is usually <strong>reversible.</strong></p>\n<p>Blindness due to eclampsia can be due to a central or peripheral cause. It is irreversible only if the etiology is a combination of retinal infarction and bilateral lateral geniculate nuclei lesions.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Convulsions</strong> <strong>within</strong> <strong>48 hours</strong> of delivery are called <strong>postpartum eclampsia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: The most common hematological finding in pregnancy-induced hypertension is <strong>thrombocytopenia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: The seizures are <strong>generalized seizures</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: The most common cause of death in patients with eclampsia is intracerebral hemorrhage. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2063","text":"A G3P2 patient with preecl​ampsia develops seizures ‌and is started on the Pri​tchard regimen. What leve‌l of serum magnesium shou​ld be maintained in this ​patient in order to preve‌nt seizures?","raw_text":"A G3P2 patient with preeclampsia develops seizures and is started on the Pritchard regimen. What level of serum magnesium should be maintained in this patient in order to prevent seizures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4-7 ​meq/L","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"7-10 ​meq/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-3 ​meq/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6-8 ​meq/L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 4-7 meq/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 4-7 meq/L</strong></p><p>The clinical picture is suggestive of<strong> eclampsia</strong> and to control fits, optimum serum magnesium levels of<strong> 4-7 mEq/L </strong>have to be maintained. </p>\n<p>MgSO<sub>4</sub> regimens for management of eclampsia and severe pre-eclampsia:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 174px; width: 474px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Regimen</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185.21875px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Loading Dose</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.78125px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Maintenance Dose</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">\n<p><strong>Intramuscular         (Pritchard)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185.21875px;\">\n<p>4 g (20% solution) IV over 3–5 min <br/>followed by <br/>10 g (50%), deep IM (5 g in each buttock)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.78125px;\">\n<p>5 g (50%) IM 4 hourly in alternate buttocks</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">\n<p><strong>Intravenous (Zuspan or   Sibai)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185.21875px;\">\n<p>4g IV slow over 15–20 minutes</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.78125px;\">\n<p>1–2 g/hr IV infusion</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2064","text":"The mechanism of action of​ MgSo4 includes all, exce‌pt:","raw_text":"The mechanism of action of MgSo4 includes all, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a membrane stabilise​r","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is a neu​roprotector","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It reduces motor endplate ​sensitivity to Ach","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It blocks neuronal sodium ​influx","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It blocks neuronal sodium influx","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It blocks neuronal sodium influx</strong></p><p>MgSO4 <strong>blocks calcium influx, </strong>not sodium.</p>\n<p>Mechanism of action of MgSO<sub>4</sub>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a <strong>membrane stabilizer</strong> and acts as a <strong>neuroprotector</strong> by <strong>reducing motor endplate sensitivity to Ach.</strong></li>\n<li>It <strong>blocks neuronal calcium influx.</strong></li>\n<li>It reduces the presynaptic release of the neurotransmitter glutamate.</li>\n<li>It blocks glutaminergic NMDA receptors and potentiates adenosine action.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2066","text":"Which of the following is ​an indication to skip the‌ next dose in a patient w​ith severe pre-eclampsia ‌taking MgSO4 prophylactic​ally?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an indication to skip the next dose in a patient with severe pre-eclampsia taking MgSO4 prophylactically?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Knee jerks ​are sluggish","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Urine output exceeds 30 ml​/hr","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Respiratory rate more than​ 14 /min","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Heart rate more than 100/m​in","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Knee jerks are sluggish","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Knee jerks are sluggish</strong></p><p><strong>Repeat injections of MgSO<sub>4</sub></strong> are given only if <strong>knee jerks are present</strong>.</p>\n<p>The next dose of MgSO<sub>4 </sub>is only given when all signs of magnesium toxicity are absent.</p>\n<p>Signs of magnesium toxicity:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Loss of deep tendon reflexes</li>\n<li>Decreased respiratory rate (<strong>&lt;12</strong> per min)</li>\n<li>Urine output <strong>&lt;30</strong> ml/hr</li>\n<li>Chest pain, heart block</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Management of magnesium toxicity:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stop magnesium therapy</li>\n<li>Estimation of serum magnesium and creatinine levels</li>\n<li>Injection of calcium gluconate 10 ml (10% solution) IV</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Side effects of MgSO<sub>4</sub>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anuria</li>\n<li>Pulmonary edema.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2068","text":"A pregnant woman with pre-​eclampsia is started on p‌rophylactic MgSO4 in view​ of signs of impending ec‌lampsia. An hour later, e​xamination reveals RR - 1​6 breaths/minute and abse‌nt patellar reflexes. Wha​t is her plasma concentra‍tion of MgSO4 most likely​ to be?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman with pre-eclampsia is started on prophylactic MgSO4 in view of signs of impending eclampsia. An hour later, examination reveals RR - 16 breaths/minute and absent patellar reflexes. What is her plasma concentration of MgSO4 most likely to be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6-8 ​mEq/L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"14-16​ mEq/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"16-30​ mEq/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10-12​ mEq/L","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 10-12 mEq/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 10-12 mEq/L</strong></p><p><strong>Patellar reflexes disappear</strong> when plasma magnesium concentration reaches 10 mEq/L. As there is no respiratory depression, the plasma concentration is likely to be <strong>10-12 mEq/L.</strong></p>\n<p>The disappearance of the<strong> patellar reflex</strong> is the<strong> first sign </strong>of impending magnesium <strong>toxicity. </strong>MgSO4 is prophylactically used in impending eclampsia, severe pre-eclampsia, and HELLP syndrome.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 156px;\" width=\"407\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 168.453125px;\"><strong>Signs of magnesium toxicity</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 222.5625px;\"><strong>Plasma concentrations of magnesium (mEq/L)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 168.453125px;\">Loss of patellar reflex</td>\n<td style=\"width: 222.5625px;\">10–12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 168.453125px;\">Respiratory depression</td>\n<td style=\"width: 222.5625px;\">12-13 </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 168.453125px;\">Cardiac arrest</td>\n<td style=\"width: 222.5625px;\">&gt;30 </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7495","text":"Magnesium sulphate has no ​role in:","raw_text":"Magnesium sulphate has no role in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prevention of seizures in ​severe pre-eclampsia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prevention of recurrent se​izures in eclampsia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prevention of RDS in prema​ture baby","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Reducing contractility of ​uterus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Prevention of RDS in premature baby","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Prevention of RDS in premature baby</strong></p><p>Magnesium sulfate has <strong>no role </strong>in the prevention of respiratory distress syndrome (RDS) in a premature baby, where there is a deficit of surfactant (which results in alveolar collapse).</p>\n<p><strong>Magnesium</strong> in the MgSO<sub>4</sub> competes with <strong>calcium</strong> on <strong>voltage-gated</strong> <strong>calcium</strong> channels, thereby altering its uptake and release. Functions of MgSO<sub>4</sub> include :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Membrane</strong> <strong>protector</strong> and neuro stabilizer. It is used in the <strong>prevention</strong> of <strong>cerebral</strong> <strong>palsy</strong> in very low birth weight neonates.</li>\n<li>Reduction of <strong>motor</strong> <strong>endplate </strong>sensitivity to <strong>acetylcholine</strong>, and thus is used as an adjunct in the <strong>management</strong> of <strong>acute</strong> <strong>severe</strong> <strong>asthma</strong> in children and adults</li>\n<li>Blocking of <strong>neuronal</strong> <strong>calcium</strong> <strong>influx</strong>, and is thus used in the <strong>conservative</strong> <strong>management</strong> of pre-term labor (<strong>tocolytic</strong>, option D)</li>\n<li>Inducing <strong>cerebral</strong> <strong>vasodilatation</strong>, <strong>dilating</strong> the <strong>uterine</strong> <strong>arteries</strong>, increasing the production of endothelial prostacyclin, and <strong>inhibiting</strong> <strong>platelet</strong> <strong>activation</strong>. Hence it is used in the management of <strong>pre-eclampsia </strong>(option A) and to prevent progression to <strong>eclampsia</strong> (option A).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2040","text":"A primigravida at 33 weeks​ of gestation was rushed ‌to the hospital with recu​rrent seizures. Her husba‌nd says that prior to the​ seizures, she had compla​ined of a headache and vi‌sual disturbances. She wa​s on antihypertensives fr‍om the first trimester an​d there is no history of ​neurological disease. Whi‌ch of the following is tr​ue regarding her manageme‌nt?","raw_text":"A primigravida at 33 weeks of gestation was rushed to the hospital with recurrent seizures. Her husband says that prior to the seizures, she had complained of a headache and visual disturbances. She was on antihypertensives from the first trimester and there is no history of neurological disease. Which of the following is true regarding her management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First step of management i​s intravenous MgSO4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diazepam is recommended as​ an anticonvulsant if MgS‌O4 fails","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Treatment may be based on ​recommendations from the ‌Magpie trial","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Maternal alkalosis commonl​y occurs and needs correc‌tion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Treatment may be based on recommendations from the Magpie trial","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Treatment may be based on recommendations from the Magpie trial</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>eclampsia</strong> and management may be based on recommendations from the <strong>Magpie trial.</strong></p>\n<p>The Magpie trial showed that <strong>prophylactic</strong> use of <strong>magnesium sulfate </strong>lowers the risk of eclampsia.</p>\n<p>The <strong>first step</strong> in the management of eclampsia is maintaining the <strong>airway</strong>, ensuring oxygen delivery to both mother and fetus. Then, <strong>MgSO<sub>4 </sub></strong>is administered to <strong>control seizures</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Thiopentone</strong> &gt; phenobarbitone may be used to <strong>control seizures</strong>. No attempt should be made to shorten the convulsion using <strong>diazepam</strong> as it often leads to <strong>thrombophlebitis</strong> and <strong>respiratory depression. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Maternal acidosis</strong> usually occurs and is corrected when present.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9712","text":"Which of the following is ​true regarding eclampsia?‌ Antepartum onset has the​ worst prognosis The outc‌ome depends on the severi​ty of hypertension. The m​ost common cause of death‌ is renal failure. The mo​st common MRI finding is ‍subcortical white matter ​edema. It most commonly o​ccurs during the intrapar‌tum period","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is true regarding eclampsia?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Antepartum onset has the worst prognosis</li>\n<li>The outcome depends on the severity of hypertension.</li>\n<li>The most common cause of death is renal failure.</li>\n<li>The most common MRI finding is subcortical white matter edema.</li>\n<li>It most commonly occurs during the intrapartum period</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,​ 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3,​ 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2,​ 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,​ 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 4</strong></p><p><strong>Antepartum eclampsia</strong> has the <strong>worst prognosis</strong> and the most common <strong>MRI</strong> finding of eclampsia is <strong>subcortical white matter edema</strong>.</p>\n<p>Antepartum eclampsia is the most common type of eclampsia. It has the highest rate of maternal mortality.</p>\n<p>The<strong> outcome</strong> of eclampsia depends on the <strong>duration of hypertension</strong> and not severity. The most<strong> common cause of death</strong> in eclampsia and severe pre-eclampsia is <strong>intracranial bleeding.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0656","text":"A pregnant lady with PIH w​as being monitored with b‌ed rest in supine positio​n. Which of the following‌ is a complication of the​ below representation?","raw_text":"A pregnant lady with PIH was being monitored with bed rest in supine position. Which of the following is a complication of the below representation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ascending ao​rta syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior vena caval syndro​me","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abdominal ao​rta syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Supine venac​ava syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Supine venacava syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8d89ac892aa7494bad6ee4d5ecd4cd0d.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Supine venacava syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Supine vena cava syndrome</strong> is the complication of the given representation. The image shows compression of the <strong>inferior vena cava</strong> by the gravid uterus which can sometimes lead to<strong> supine hypotension.</strong></p>\n<p>During <strong>late pregnancy</strong>, the <strong>gravid uterus</strong> compresses the <strong>great vessels</strong> against the hard <strong>spine</strong>, when the<strong> patient</strong> is in the <strong>supine</strong> position. Of the two vessels, the <strong>vena cava</strong> is compressed, but the aorta, owing to its structure is relatively resistant.</p>\n<p>Normally, the <strong>vena cava compression</strong> results in opening up collateral circulation by means of paravertebral and azygous veins but in some cases, when the <strong>collateral circulation fails to open up</strong>, the <strong>venous return of the heart</strong> may be seriously <strong>curtailed </strong>resulting in <strong>hypotension,</strong> <strong>tachycardia</strong>, and <strong>syncope</strong> and is called supine vena cava syndrome.</p>\n<p>The normal blood pressure is quickly restored by turning the patient to the lateral position.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"4giD6rjC","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"25_practical_ophthalmology","title":"25.Practical Ophthalmology","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB6010","text":"For a patient presenting w​ith gradual, painless dim‌inution of vision, the di​fferential diagnosis woul‌d not include _________.","raw_text":"For a patient presenting with gradual, painless diminution of vision, the differential diagnosis would not include _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Optic ​atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corneal ​dystrophy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary open angle glaucom​a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Methyl alcoh​ol amblyopia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Methyl alcohol amblyopia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Methyl alcohol amblyopia</strong></p><p><strong>Methyl alcohol amblyopia</strong> causes<strong> sudden painless</strong> diminution of vision.</p>\n<p>It is a toxic neuropathy of the optic nerve.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7116","text":"What is the angle subtende​d by the largest alphabet‌ on the Snellen's chart a​t 60m distance?","raw_text":"What is the angle subtended by the largest alphabet on the Snellen's chart at 60m distance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3​0","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5​0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1​0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0433a3ad16b0474ea4b7897d3a393efbx900x1200.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/192387686eb2481ca5231f8af8a9f6c6x1280x1482.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 5</strong></p><p>The <strong>angle</strong> subtended by the <strong>largest alphabet</strong> on the <strong>Snellens chart</strong> at <strong>60m</strong> distance is <strong>5 minutes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Visual acuity is a measure of the spatial resolution of the eye or an estimation of its ability to discriminate between two points. Snellens chart is used for Visual acuity assesment.</p>\n<p>In snellens chart the alphabets decrease in size as we move from above-downwards and these lines, from <strong>above-downwards</strong> should be <strong>read at 60</strong>, 36, 24, 18, 12, 9, 6, 5 and <strong>4 m respectively</strong>. At <strong>these distances</strong>, the <strong>respective letters</strong> subtend a visual angle of <strong>5 minutes</strong>. Hence, the <strong>largest alphabet</strong> that should be <strong>read at 60 m</strong> subtends a visual angle of <strong>5 minutes</strong> in the eye.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6012","text":"A 60-year-old man comes to​ the OPD for an eye exam.‌ He is having difficulty ​reading newspapers but ha‌s no problem watching tel‌evision. At what distance‍ would you ask him to rea‌d a Jaeger's chart from?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man comes to the OPD for an eye exam. He is having difficulty reading newspapers but has no problem watching television.  At what distance would you ask him to read a Jaeger's chart from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20​ cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"30​ cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"35​ cm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"40​ cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 35 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/544e87637b6a4331a81196308b2aba88x368x585.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 35 cm</strong></p><p>Jaeger’s chart is held at a distance of <strong>35 cm away </strong>from the patient.</p>\n<p>It is used to <strong>measure near vision</strong>. The smallest letter is designated as N5 or J1 and the largest as N48. Snellen's near vision chart can also be used.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a Jaeger's chart.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6014","text":"A patient is being evaluat​ed for a recent eye injur‌y. The result of his Plac​ido disc test is shown be‌low. What is the correct ‌interpretation?","raw_text":"A patient is being evaluated for a recent eye injury. The result of his Placido disc test is shown below. What is the correct interpretation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kerat​oconus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kerato​globus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nor​mal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Corneal ​abrasion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Normal","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d523311e73294bc09384d68f12b077c3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/23d3aab17c074917b6696cfb3e27b0fcx1280x1700.6488011283498.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Normal</strong></p><p>The given <strong>Placido’s disc test </strong>result indicates a <strong>normal</strong> corneal surface.</p>\n<p>This test is used to detect an <strong>abnormal corneal surface</strong>. <strong>Irregularities </strong>of the corneal surface cause <strong>distortion</strong> of the circles.</p>\n<p>Distortion of the circle is seen in keratoconus, keratoglobus, corneal ulcer, and corneal abrasion.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6017","text":"A patient presents with th​e following finding in hi‌s eye. Fluorescein staini​ng is done and the eye is‌ observed under cobalt bl‌ue light. What would be s‍een?","raw_text":"A patient presents with the following finding in his eye. Fluorescein staining is done and the eye is observed under cobalt blue light. What would be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ulcer appears bright green​.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ulcer appears opaque green​.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Normal area appears bright​ green.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Normal area appears opaque​ green.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ulcer appears opaque green.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8322a4591bbd4ef0a70f9c77a79b006b.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f20f852a585e41278bc7f99b4c79b912x512x190.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ulcer appears opaque green.</strong></p><p>The image shows a corneal ulcer. The <strong>ulcer </strong>takes the<strong> stain</strong> and appears<strong> brilliant green</strong>, and when examined under <strong>cobalt blue light</strong>, it appears<strong> opaque green</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6018","text":"A 61-year-old man, known c​ase of diabetes mellitus,‌ presented with headache ​and eye pain. He also rep‌orts difficulty with driv‌ing at night. Visual fiel‍d-testing indicates perip‌heral vision loss. The op‍hthalmologist uses the fo‌llowing instrument during‍ his examination of this ​patient. What is he measu‍ring by using the followi‍ng instrument?","raw_text":"A 61-year-old man, known case of diabetes mellitus, presented with headache and eye pain. He also reports difficulty with driving at night. Visual field-testing indicates peripheral vision loss.  The ophthalmologist uses the following instrument during his examination of this patient. What is he measuring by using the following instrument?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Angle of ant​erior chamber","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intraocula​r pressure","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Corneal​ density","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Corneal ​thickness","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intraocular pressure","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0b553369200b4038922767266b4741b8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intraocular pressure</strong></p><p>The instrument shown is the <strong>Schiotz tonometer</strong>, which is used to measure<strong> intraocular pressure (IOP)</strong>.</p>\n<p>This patient's complaints of headache, eye pain, difficulty in adapting to darkness, and examination findings of peripheral vision loss with increased intraocular pressure, is suggestive of glaucoma.  </p>\n<p>Schiotz tonometer is a type of <strong>indentation tonometer. </strong>The principle behind its use is that the plunger will indent a soft eye more than a hard eye.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6021","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin‌g the procedure shown in ​the image?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the procedure shown in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The concept of the procedu​re was introduced by Gold‌mann.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is used to treat after ​cataract","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is based on the Imbert-​Fick law","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is used to investigate ​glaucoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is used to treat after cataract","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eac8dcb55d514ca0aabd7a21fe0aed6c.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is used to treat after cataract</strong></p><p>The instrument shown is an <strong>applanation tonometer</strong>, which is used to <strong>measure intraocular pressure (IOP). </strong></p>\n<p>The concept behind the procedure was introduced by<strong> Goldmann</strong>. It is based on<strong> Imbert-Fick law, </strong>which states that the pressure inside a sphere(P) is equal to the force (W) required to flatten its surface divided by the area of flattening (A).</p>\n<p><strong>P = W/A</strong></p>\n<p>The different types are <strong>Goldmann</strong> tonometer,<strong> Perkin’s</strong> tonometer,<strong> pneumatic</strong> tonometer,<strong> pulse air</strong> tonometer,<strong> tono-pen.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9580","text":"A patient recently underwe​nt LASIK surgery for his ‌myopia. He is now complai​ning of headaches and eye‌ pain. Identify the test ‌being performed on him.","raw_text":"A patient recently underwent LASIK surgery for his myopia. He is now complaining of headaches and eye pain. Identify the test being performed on him.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pachy​metry","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tono​metry","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Auto Refr​actometery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A S​can","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tonometry","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8c2c840b39a3434baf7ac4b529d15fff.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/87ecf58b6c424eb19f3b495b0f7fd945x640x479.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2b880c31149d4956bfde6e2b346ac028x1280x850.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tonometry</strong></p><p>The above image is of the <strong>dynamic contour tonometer</strong> such as the <strong>Pascal tonometer.</strong> The dynamic contour tonometer(Pascal) offers a <strong>non-invasive</strong> and direct method of intraocular pressure (IOP) measurement with an additional measurement of the ocular pulse amplitude. This type of tonometer is unaffected by the corneal thickness and it is the tonometer of choice in <strong>post-Lasik</strong> patients.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Pachymetry</strong> is used to measure the<strong> corneal thickness. </strong><br/>The below image is of pachymetry.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Auto Refractotemetry</strong> is a computerized refractometer that is used to find out the <strong>error of refraction</strong>. This gives rapid information about the kind of refractive error in terms of sphere, cylinder with axis and inter pupillary distance.<br/><br/>Option D: <strong>A-Scan ultrasonography</strong> is a useful diagnostic tool for intraocular evaluation especially in the setting of opaque media. The report of the A scan is displayed as a unidimensional display of spikes along a baseline where the distance between the spikes gives the indirect measurement of the tissues. A-scan is used to measure</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The axial length of the eyeball for Intraocular lens (IOL) power calculation</li>\n<li><strong>Anterior chamber depth</strong></li>\n<li>Thickness of intraocular mass.    </li>\n</ul>\n<p>A-scan ultrasonography is shown in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6022","text":"You can use any of the fol​lowing to estimate the vi‌sual field of a patient e​xcept:","raw_text":"You can use any of the following to estimate the visual field of a patient except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Confrontat​ion method","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Campi​metry","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Octopus F​ieldmaster","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Optical coherence tomograp​hy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Optical coherence tomography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5d3425a4c368461f87164bdd477f4ca8x1280x1707.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eb6964abb98d42f19f4306b5b8a61058x1280x1331.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e61949ca29464dfd80521d986c641293x500x390.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Optical coherence tomography</strong></p><p><strong>Optical coherence tomography</strong> is <strong>not</strong> used to estimate the visual field. It is a non-invasive imaging technique to visualize cross-sections of the retina.</p>\n<p>Perimetry is used to estimate the visual field.</p>\n<p><strong>Types:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Manual perimetry-<strong> Confrontation method, Lister’s perimeter, Campimetry (Scotometry), Goldmann’s perimeter</strong></li>\n<li>Automated perimetry-<strong> Octopus Fieldmaster, Humphrey Field Analyzer</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The images given below shows Lister's perimeter</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows a multifixation campimeter study result.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6035","text":"Which among the following ​phases indicate complete ‌filling of the retinal ar​terioles and capillaries ‌on fundus fluorescein ang‌iography?","raw_text":"Which among the following phases indicate complete filling of the retinal arterioles and capillaries on fundus fluorescein angiography?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pre-arter​ial phase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arteria​l phase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arterio-ve​nous phase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Venous​ phase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Arterio-venous phase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/baf001dcd6354b16b643bfe33abd2a66x280x222.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Arterio-venous phase</strong></p><p><strong>Complete filling</strong> of retinal arterioles and capillaries is seen in the<strong> arterio-venous phase</strong>.</p>\n<p>In<strong> </strong>FFA, <strong>sodium fluorescein dye </strong>is injected into the antecubital vein. A <strong>fundus camera</strong> is then used to take photographs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1<sup>st</sup> picture- after 5 seconds, then every second for 20 seconds, every 3–5 seconds for the next 1 minute</li>\n<li>Last picture- after 10 minutes\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pre-arterial </strong>phase- <strong>choroidal circulation is filling</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Arterial </strong>phase- start filling of<strong> retinal arteriole till its completely filled</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Arterio-venous </strong>phase-<strong> complete filling</strong> of retinal arterioles and capillaries followed by laminar filling of veins</li>\n<li><strong>Venous filling- arterioles</strong> are<strong> emptying </strong>and<strong> veins</strong> are<strong> filling</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Indications</strong> for fundus fluorescein angiography:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diabetic retinopathy</li>\n<li>Vascular occlusion</li>\n<li>Eale’s disease</li>\n<li>Central serous retinopathy</li>\n<li>Cystoid macular edema</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7902","text":"A 55-year-old patient with​ diabetes presents with c‌omplaints of dark floatin​g spots and flashes of li‌ght in her left eye. Opti‌cal coherence tomography ‍of the left eye is given ‌in the image below. Ident‍ify the pathology.","raw_text":"A 55-year-old patient with diabetes presents with complaints of dark floating spots and flashes of light in her left eye. Optical coherence tomography of the left eye is given in the image below. Identify the pathology.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitreomacul​ar adhesion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Partial thickness macular ​hole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Full thickness macular hol​e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vitreomacul​ar traction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vitreomacular traction","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b2778dc084bc478ea9776d86dccbd06e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0709c654d6e44566908b136885b73d7dx360x113.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/759a4ac6b18e4954a4e27c72a33e6c0fx360x96.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d20b0c8a8d9e40c688c98a53685d6e61x360x95.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/168085e473a844b4bb61862d90d24954x360x104.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cef670281abd45a0a117ceed591301a5x300x188.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3be569301cb24952a8199bb263cb9ab9x310x162.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vitreomacular traction</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>vitreomacular traction</strong> with <strong>detached posterior vitreous membrane</strong> and<strong> foveal traction</strong>. </p>\n<p>OCT images:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Normal OCT</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>2. </strong><strong>Vitreomacular traction- </strong>The fovea is elevated from the retinal pigment epithelium (RPE), as indicated by the arrow. Pseudocysts and retinal morphology distortion may also be seen at the site of traction.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>3. Vitreomacular adhesion- </strong>posterior vitreous detachment but no macular or foveal traction. The retinal morphology is normal.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>4. Macular hole -</strong> a partial or full-thickness retinal defect is seen.</p>\n<p>A full-thickness macular hole is seen in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>5. Central serous retinopathy</strong><strong>- </strong>fluid accumulation in the subretinal space, with shallow retinal detachment. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">6. </span>Cystoid macular edema </strong><strong>- </strong>loss of foveal depression with cystoid spaces in the retina.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6038","text":"A 50-year-old female, who ​is a known case of type 2‌ diabetes, is undergoing ​serial electroretinograms‌ to monitor disease progr‌ession. The following fin‍ding was obtained from he‌r recent test. What does ‍the wave marked by the ar‌row indicate?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old female, who is a known case of type 2 diabetes, is undergoing serial electroretinograms to monitor disease progression. The following finding was obtained from her recent test. What does the wave marked by the arrow indicate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wave due to photoreceptors​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wave from ro​ds and cones","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Activity of ​bipolar cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Metabolic activity of pigm​ent epithelium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Activity of bipolar cells","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cb0e90c9b32d41bc94732c54ef4ec740.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5f6f932b94d6493ba68884a08eaefb54x1280x1224.9365303244006.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Activity of bipolar cells</strong></p><p>The wave marked is the <strong>\"B\" wave</strong>. It represents the activity of <strong>bipolar cells</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Electroretinogram (ERG)</strong> is used to detect the <strong>functional abnormality</strong> of the outer retina up to the bipolar cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6040","text":"You would classify the ele​ctrooculography (EOG) of ‌a patient as subnormal if​ the Arden ratio is less ‌than ______.","raw_text":"You would classify the electrooculography (EOG) of a patient as subnormal if the Arden ratio is less than ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt;​ 185","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt​;150","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt​;125","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt​;100","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. <150","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/54bc61a8d79c45138586343539c5fbd9x300x217.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. &lt;150</strong></p><p>In electrooculography (EOG), the subnormal curve is <strong>&lt;150</strong>.</p>\n<p>EOG is based on the measurement of the resting membrane potential of the eye<strong> between the cornea (+) and the back of the eye (-). </strong>Interpretation is based on the Arden ratio.</p>\n<p><strong>Arden Ratio = Max height of light peak/Min height of dark trough × 100</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal curve: 185 or above</li>\n<li>Subnormal curve: &lt;150</li>\n<li>Flat curve: &lt;125</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6042","text":"You can use 'A' scan for a​ll of the following excep‌t:","raw_text":"You can use 'A' scan for all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IOL power ​calculation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior ch​amber depth","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thickness of intra ocular ​mass","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Assessment of vitreous hem​orrhage","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Assessment of vitreous hemorrhage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/de48c6e43be04fe293d17dad28c5ca1bx250x186.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/73814f4365974652a1e4918aa98245b0x240x178.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Assessment of vitreous hemorrhage</strong></p><p>Assessment of<strong> vitreous hemorrhage</strong> is done by <strong>\"B\" scan</strong>.</p>\n<p>Both 'A' and 'B' scans are types of ultrasound (USG) scans.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 460px;\" width=\"408\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 48px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 195.969px; height: 48px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14pt;\">A scan (time amplitude)</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 196.031px; height: 48px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14pt;\">B scan (intensity modulation)</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 66.1632px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 195.969px; height: 66.1632px;\">It produces a unidimensional image and echoes are plotted as spikes.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 196.031px; height: 66.1632px;\">It <span class=\"s1\">produces a </span>two-dimensional dotted section of the eyeball.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 166px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 195.969px; height: 166px;\">\n<p><strong>Uses:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Axial length</li>\n<li>Intraocular lens (IOL) power calculation: Biometry</li>\n<li>Anterior chamber depth</li>\n<li>The thickness of intraocular mass</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 196.031px; height: 166px;\">\n<p><strong>Uses:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Assessment of the posterior segment in opaque media</li>\n<li>Study of intraocular tumors</li>\n<li>Localization of intraocular and intra-orbital foreign bodies</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The first image below shows \"A\" scan ultrasound amplitude spikes.</p>\n<p>The second image shows a normal \"B\" scan of the eye.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6776","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding the instr‌ument in the image?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding the instrument in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is used to test macular​ function","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is used to measure vert​ical, horizontal tropias,‌ and phorias","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It cannot be used to measu​re cyclodeviation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is based on diplopic pr​ojection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It cannot be used to measure cyclodeviation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8d8d71f61f3f459ea36f309754df6640.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It cannot be used to measure cyclodeviation</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>Maddox rod.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cyclodeviation</strong> is measured with the double Maddox rod test.</p>\n<p>The Maddox rod test uses a specially constructed device consisting of a series of parallel cylinders that converts a point source of light into a line image.</p>\n<p>It can be used to test for horizontal and vertical deviations, and when used in conjunction with another Maddox rod, for cyclodeviations.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6774","text":"A woman came to the OPD du​e to blurring of vision w‌hile working on her lapto​p. Retinoscopy was perfor‌med at 1 m without cyclop‌legia. The red reflex mov‍es in the same direction ‌as the retinoscope. Which‍ of the following refract‌ive errors is not possibl‍e in this patient?","raw_text":"A woman came to the OPD due to blurring of vision while working on her laptop. Retinoscopy was performed at 1 m without cycloplegia. The red reflex moves in the same direction as the retinoscope. Which of the following refractive errors is not possible in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1D m​yopia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperm​etropia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.5D ​myopia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emmet​ropia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1D myopia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fea4c4a927924d01ad92047d6be6e974x1280x3794.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1D myopia</strong></p><p>A patient with <strong>1 D myopia </strong>would exhibit<strong> no movement</strong> of red reflex when retinoscopy is done from a distance of 1m without cycloplegia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6051","text":"You can use keratometry fo​r all of the following ex‌cept:","raw_text":"You can use keratometry for all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estimating corneal astigma​tism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Measuring the curvature of​ cornea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calculating the power of t​he intraocular lens (IOL)‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Calculating the power of l​ens in spectacles","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Calculating the power of lens in spectacles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/880186c90f574455a9857ab42e78ee85x506x396.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Calculating the power of lens in spectacles</strong></p><p><strong>Keratometry</strong> or ophthalmometry is not used to calculate the power of the lens in spectacles. </p>\n<p>Uses of keratometry:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Estimate corneal astigmatism</li>\n<li>Measure the curvature of the cornea</li>\n<li>Calculate the power of the intraocular lens (IOL)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows keratometry being performed on a patient.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6052","text":"An old man comes to the OP​D for an annual eye exam.‌ The ophthalmologist exam​ines his fundus as shown ‌below. Identify the proce‌dure.","raw_text":"An old man comes to the OPD for an annual eye exam. The ophthalmologist examines his fundus as shown below. Identify the procedure.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Direct oph​thalmoscopy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Distant direct ophthalmosc​opy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Indirect op​hthalmoscopy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retin​oscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Direct ophthalmoscopy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8b9fe6ba455b4bbb8246ab1541d7482c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Direct ophthalmoscopy</strong></p><p>The procedure shown is <strong>direct ophthalmoscopy</strong>. It is used to visualize the fundus.</p>\n<p><strong>Types:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Direct</strong> ophthalmoscopy</li>\n<li><strong>Distant</strong> direct ophthalmoscopy</li>\n<li><strong>Indirect</strong> ophthalmoscopy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1997","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about di‌rect ophthalmoscopy?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about direct ophthalmoscopy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Magnification is 15 times.​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"There is no​ stereopsis.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The type of image formed i​s real and inverted.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A condensing lens is not n​eeded.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The type of image formed is real and inverted.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The type of image formed is real and inverted.</strong></p><p>The type of image formed in direct ophthalmoscopy is <strong>virtual and erect.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6054","text":"Which among the following ​statements is false regar‌ding the procedure shown ​in the image?","raw_text":"Which among the following statements is false regarding the procedure shown in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The principle is to make e​ye emmetropic.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Image is real, inverted, a​nd magnified.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"+20 D le​ns used.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It may be done even in pat​ient's with a hazy media.‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The principle is to make eye emmetropic.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9ea263fbd49649e98821c497cf62f7ec.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The principle is to make eye emmetropic.</strong></p><p>The procedure is <strong>indirect ophthalmoscopy</strong> and its <strong>principle</strong> is to<strong> make </strong>the <strong>eye highly myopic</strong>. </p>\n<p>A <strong>convex lens</strong> is used, commonly <strong>+20D</strong>. The image formed is <strong>real, inverted, </strong>and <strong>magnified</strong>. It is used for assessing and managing retinal detachment and other peripheral retinal lesions. It may be <strong>done with hazy media</strong> because of strong inbuilt illumination.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2000","text":"Which of the following can​ you assess using Holmgre‌n's wool-matching test?","raw_text":"Which of the following can you assess using Holmgren's wool-matching test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Visual​ field","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Visual​ acuity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Color ​vision","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Refra​ction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Color vision","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d299109c03db4f4fbd87cacb99b4d523x1280x769.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c8581509ffdb4ec5959cfc42cea281b2x1280x519.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/92de776c87b247c69767f5908e80f225x495x352.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/19b995a978cb47fcbb51bd8e6022384ex1280x769.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Color vision</strong></p><p>Holmgren's wool-matching test is used for the assessment of <strong>color vision.</strong></p>\n<p>Tests for color vision</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ishihara pseudo-isochromatic chart</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>There are <strong>38 plates</strong> read at a distance of <strong>75 cm</strong>.</li>\n<li>Drawback- <strong>Cannot</strong> detect a <strong>blue</strong> color defect</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Hardy–Rand–Rittler test</li>\n<li>City University test</li>\n<li>Holmgren's wool-matching test</li>\n<li>Naegels anamoloscope</li>\n<li>Farnsworth Munsell 100 hue test</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows Ishihara pseudo-isochromatic chart</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6775","text":"What is the name of the dy​e in the strip?","raw_text":"What is the name of the dye in the strip?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fluor​escein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rose ​Bengal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Indocyan​ine Green","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lissami​ne Green","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lissamine Green","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4ef73609a9bb476e9ab535d3be59c20e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bbdaaff92b61401c9eb2d90d8578ec24x1280x858.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lissamine Green</strong></p><p>The given dye in the strip is <strong>lissamine green</strong>. It is used as 1% concentration.</p>\n<p>Lissamine green stains dead and degenerate cells, but does not stain healthy epithelial cells. It is used to diagnose ocular surface disease.</p>\n<p>It stains the conjunctiva. It does not cause stinging like Rose Bengal. The Green staining is best observed two minutes after instillation.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the lissamine staining of the eye.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6777","text":"The device shown below can​not be used in the manage‌ment of which of the foll​owing?","raw_text":"The device shown below cannot be used in the management of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retinob​lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Retina​l break","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Impending corneal perforat​ion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pars p​lanitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Impending corneal perforation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f83acb9237804a67970d6c0d717ada6a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Impending corneal perforation</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>cryoprobe</strong>. It is not used in the management of impending corneal perforation.</p>\n<p>It is used in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Peripheral retinopexy for <strong>peripherally</strong> located retinal <strong>breaks and tears</strong></li>\n<li>Treatment of <strong>anterior RB</strong> tumors</li>\n<li>Cyclocryotherapy in <strong>pars planitis</strong> and <strong>end-stage glaucoma</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7061","text":"The following image indica​tes:","raw_text":"The following image indicates:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trochlear ​nerve palsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral re​ctus palsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internuclear ophthalmopleg​ia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Occulomo​tor palsy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Occulomotor palsy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a0644c911f474ce18074bef8c18cfdfb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59fd838484a34f2e9a775be56c022eefx1280x3519.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/37cc1b31b60b4af7920a6c9fcb44872ex720x1577.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2e4aadadc52245d3b83e21481c29133ex1280x2906.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/da9f9fb70f8b4957adcfcf01dab3f4a1x1280x2152.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9bc6adac7c7249939ee269a41dadabfdx1280x2259.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Occulomotor palsy</strong></p><p>The given image is suggestive of <strong>oculomotor nerve palsy.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>central image</strong> shows the <strong>primary position</strong> of gaze where the patient's <strong>left eye</strong> is deviated <strong>down and out </strong>with <strong>ptosis </strong>of the left eyelid. From the eye movements in other positions, it can be seen that <strong>adduction</strong> and <strong>elevation </strong>are <strong>absent</strong> and <strong>depression</strong> is <strong>reduced</strong> in the left eye. All of these findings are indicative of <strong>oculomotor nerve palsy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>3rd Cranial nerve</strong>(oculomotor nerve) <strong>palsy</strong> presents with: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Ptosis</strong> due to weakness of <strong>levator palpebrae superioris</strong> muscle (LPS)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Abduction and depression</strong> in the primary position (<strong>down and out</strong>) due to <strong>unopposed action</strong> of the <strong>lateral rectus</strong>(6th nerve supply) and <strong>superior oblique</strong> muscles(4th nerve supply)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Dilated pupil</strong> and defective accommodation due to <strong>parasympathetic palsy</strong> (involvement of <strong>Edinger Westphal</strong> parasympathetic supply)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Limited</strong> adduction, elevation and depression due to <strong>ipsilateral paralysis</strong> of medial rectus, inferior oblique and inferior rectus .</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Trochlear</strong>(4th cranial) nerve palsy presents with <strong>superior oblique</strong> muscle <strong>paralysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clinical findings of trochlear nerve palsy include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li> <strong>Hypertropia </strong>(elevation) in the primary position</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Extorsion</strong> that is greatest in abduction</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Compensatory</strong> head posture with <strong>contralateral</strong> face turn and <strong>head tilt</strong> with <strong>chin</strong> slightly <strong>depressed</strong>. This position is assumed to <strong>avoid Diplopia</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Limitation</strong> of depression </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Normal</strong> abduction and elevation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below given image depicts <strong>4th cranial nerve palsy.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Lateral rectus palsy </strong>is seen in cases of isolated <strong>Abducens</strong> nerve palsy. These patients present with <strong>esotropia/convergent</strong> squint in primary position with <strong>limitation of abduction</strong> on the side of the lesion. This is due to <strong>unopposed</strong> action of the <strong>medial rectus</strong> muscle in the affected eye.</p>\n<p>There is also a <strong>compensatory head turn</strong> towards the side of the lesion to reduce the symptom of Diplopia. The <strong>adduction</strong> is <strong>normal</strong> in <strong>lateral rectus palsy</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Internuclear opthalmoplegia</strong> occurs following an <strong>injury</strong> to the <strong>medial longitudinal fasciculus</strong> (MLF). A <strong>unilateral</strong> <strong>lesion </strong>to the <strong>MLF</strong> leads to the <strong>failure </strong>of <strong>adduction</strong> in the <strong>ipsilateral eye </strong>with a <strong>normal abduction</strong> of the <strong>contralateral eye.</strong></p>\n<p>The below given image depicts the pathogenesis of <strong>Inter nuclear opthalmoplegia</strong> in detail.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0665","text":"Photostress test is used t​o differentiate between:","raw_text":"Photostress test is used to differentiate between:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Macula and optic nerve dis​ease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cataract a​nd glaucoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lens an​d cornea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retina and vitreal patholo​gies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Macula and optic nerve disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Macula and optic nerve disease</strong></p><p><strong>Photostress test</strong> is used to differentiate between the disease involving the <strong>macula</strong> and <strong>optic nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p>This test is of particular use in early macular diseases such as central serous retinopathy.</p>\n<p>In this test, one eye is covered and the patient is asked to read the smallest line on the near chart. A bright light is then shone into the eye for 15 seconds, and the patient is asked to read the line again, and the time taken to recover is noted. The test is repeated with the other eye.</p>\n<p>In <strong>normal</strong> people and in patients with <strong>optic nerve disease,</strong> the <strong>recovery time </strong>is<strong> not affected</strong>. Whereas, in patients with <strong>macular disease</strong> the recovery time is <strong>prolonged</strong> and is usually more than one-third of the normal recovery time. This is because, in macular disease, the photoreceptors are damaged, and there is a delay in the visual pigment regeneration process.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"7MPyWWOK","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"25_spondyloarthropathies_and_crystal_arthropathies","title":"25.Spondyloarthropathies And Crystal Arthropathies","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB5092","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a seronegative spondy‍loarthropathy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a seronegative spondyloarthropathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ankylosing ‌spondylitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rheumatoid‌ arthritis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Reactive ‌arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Psoriatic‌ arthritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rheumatoid arthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rheumatoid arthritis</strong></p><p><strong>Rheumatoid arthritis</strong> is not a seronegative arthritis as it is associated with<strong> </strong>the presence of autoantibodies (<strong>rheumatoid</strong> <strong>factor</strong>) in the blood.</p>\n<p><strong>Seronegative</strong> spondyloarthropathies are associated with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Absence of autoantibodies</li>\n<li>Involvement of the spine</li>\n<li>Increased incidence in young individuals</li>\n<li><strong>HLA B-27 </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ankylosing spondylitis</li>\n<li>Psoriatic arthritis</li>\n<li>Reactive arthritis</li>\n<li>Enteropathic arthritis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3638","text":"A 55-year-old woman presen‌ted with low back pain fo‍r 3 months. An X-ray of t​he lumbosacral spine was ​taken as shown below. Ide​ntify the skeletal abnorm​ality seen and the likely‌ diagnosis.","raw_text":"A 55-year-old woman presented with low back pain for 3 months. An X-ray of the lumbosacral spine was taken as shown below. Identify the skeletal abnormality seen and the likely diagnosis.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ivory vertebra - Paget's d‌isease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Codfish vertebra - Osteopo‌rosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bamboo spine - Ankylosing ‌spondylitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Corduroy vertebra - IBD as‌sociated arthritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bamboo spine - Ankylosing spondylitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d8001c033b5c421b9bf625db43bcf91f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c8716b9f511749ffa344f329e694a423x600x2086.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bamboo spine - Ankylosing spondylitis</strong></p><p>The above lateral view X-ray view shows the <strong>bamboo</strong> <strong>spine</strong> appearance. It is seen in <strong>ankylosing spondylitis. </strong></p>\n<p>It is due to the <strong>ossification</strong> of the <strong>annulus fibrosis, </strong>sparing the anterior longitudinal ligament and disc. </p>\n<p>The image below shows the pathogenesis of ankylosing spondylitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other named vertebral abnormalities:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 196px;\" width=\"284\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 29px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 134px; height: 29px;\">Vertebral abnormality</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px; height: 29px;\">Associated condition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29.875px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 134px; height: 29.875px;\">Bamboo spine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px; height: 29.875px;\">Ankylosing spondylitis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 62px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 134px; height: 62px;\"><strong>Ivory</strong> vertebra</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px; height: 62px;\"><strong>Paget's</strong> disease </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 62px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 134px; height: 62px;\"><strong>Codfish vertebra</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px; height: 62px;\"><strong>Osteoporosis</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 62px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 134px; height: 62px;\">Rugger jersey spine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px; height: 62px;\">Renal osteodystrophy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 62px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 134px; height: 62px;\"><strong>Corduroy</strong> vertebra</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px; height: 62px;\"><strong>Hemangioma</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 62px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 134px; height: 62px;\">Picture frame vertebra</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px; height: 62px;\">Paget's disease </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3639","text":"A 34-year-old man presente‌d with on-and-off low bac‍k pain and early morning ​stiffness for 2 years. X-​ray spine showed squaring​ of the lumbar vertebra a​nd erosion of the sacroil‌iac joints. He is at an i​ncreased risk of developi‌ng all of the following e‌xcept:","raw_text":"A 34-year-old man presented with on-and-off low back pain and early morning stiffness for 2 years. X-ray spine showed squaring of the lumbar vertebra and erosion of the sacroiliac joints. He is at an increased risk of developing all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior‌ uveitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inflammatory bowel disease‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperpara‌thyroidism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aortic reg‌urgitation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hyperparathyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hyperparathyroidism</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of<strong> ankylosing spondylitis. </strong>Hyperparathyroidism is not an extra-articular manifestation of ankylosing spondylitis.</p>\n<p>The <strong>extra-articular</strong> manifestations of ankylosing spondylitis are a consequence of <strong>uncontrolled</strong> systemic <strong>inflammation. </strong>They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Eye<strong> - Anterior uveitis (commonest overall) </strong>, glaucoma</li>\n<li>Renal - IgA nephropathy, secondary amyloidosis</li>\n<li>Cardiac - <strong>Aortic regurgitation</strong>, aortitis, carditis, conduction defects</li>\n<li>Respiratory - Upper lobe pulmonary fibrosis, pleural thickening</li>\n<li>Neurological - Cauda equina syndrome</li>\n<li>GIT - <strong>Inflammatory bowel disease</strong></li>\n<li>Diffuse osteoporosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5108","text":"A 37-year-old man presente‌d with pain and swelling ‍in the finger of the righ​t hand. Appearance on exa​mination and X-ray is sho​wn below. Which of the fo​llowing criteria aids in ‌the diagnosis of this con​dition?","raw_text":"A 37-year-old man presented with pain and swelling in the finger of the right hand. Appearance on examination and X-ray is shown below. Which of the following criteria aids in the diagnosis of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"EU‌LAR","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"SL‌ICC","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CAS‌PAR","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"AS‌AS","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CASPAR","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b89af4f0d1644446add69d068792c693.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CASPAR</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>psoriatic arthritis. </strong>The <strong>CASPAR</strong> criteria is used to make the diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Image A shows psoriatic nail dystrophy and image B shows the arthritic changes with evidence of new bone formation in the 4th DIP on X-ray.</p>\n<p>The CASPAR criteria includes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Evidence of current <strong>psoriasis</strong>, a personal history of psoriasis, or a family history of psoriasis</li>\n<li>Typical psoriatic <strong>nail dystrophy</strong> observed on the current physical examination </li>\n<li>A <strong>negative</strong> test result for <strong>rheumatoid factor</strong> </li>\n<li>Either current <strong>dactylitis</strong> or a history of dactylitis </li>\n<li><strong>Radiographic evidence</strong> of juxta-articular new bone formation in the hand or foot</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Only current psoriasis is assigned 2 points; all other features are assigned 1 point. To meet the criteria, a patient must have an inflammatory articular disease (joint, spine, or entheseal) with <strong>≥3 points</strong> from the above five categories. </p>\n<p>Other commonly used criteria include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>EULAR - Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li>SLICC - Systemic lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li>ASAS - Ankylosing spondylitis </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5106","text":"What is the likely diagnos‌is from the X-ray shown b‍elow?","raw_text":"What is the likely diagnosis from the X-ray shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ankylosing ‌spondylitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Psoriatic‌ arthritis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rheumatoid‌ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enteropathi‌c arthritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Psoriatic arthritis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1f4062f930c64fa588b5fcb64249c570.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/11ed1fa605d842c087050d861a290dbdx300x426.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cd7f3903d3a0431499aaa6226736f547x1280x1194.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f13c788a38d4147a949377cc82bf62ax1279x1062.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Psoriatic arthritis</strong></p><p>The above image shows the classic '<strong>pencil-in-cup</strong> deformity' of <strong>psoriatic</strong> <strong>arthritis </strong>with subluxation of the thumb. </p>\n<p>These changes occur due to <strong>periarticular</strong> <strong>erosion</strong> and bony resorption.</p>\n<p>The images below shows the pencil-in-cup deformity in psoriatic arthritis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other deformities in psoriatic arthritis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Marginal erosions with the adjacent bony proliferation </li>\n<li>Small joint ankylosis and osteolysis of phalangeal and metacarpal bones</li>\n<li>Telescoping of digits, periostitis, and proliferative new bone </li>\n<li>Axial changes like asymmetric sacroiliitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Psoriatic arthritis of the hands commonly involves proximal and distal interphalangeal joints (<strong>PIP</strong>, <strong>DIP</strong>) and metacarpophalangeal joints (<strong>MCP</strong>).</p>\n<p>The image below shows psoriatic arthritis of the hands.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5114","text":"A boy is brought with comp‌laints of pain and deform‍ity of the right knee. He​ is a known case of sever​e hemophilia A. On examin​ation, a boggy swelling i​s noticed over the right ‌knee. Which of the follow​ing is least likely to be‌ found on radiography of ‌the right knee?","raw_text":"A boy is brought with complaints of pain and deformity of the right knee. He is a known case of severe hemophilia A.  On examination, a boggy swelling is noticed over the right knee. Which of the following is least likely to be found on radiography of the right knee?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subchondra‌l bone cyst","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subchondra‌l thinning","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Juxta-articular osteoscler‌osis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increase in intercondylar ‌distance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Juxta-articular osteosclerosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ec7e5d3923224d01886bf90fd32f9221.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Juxta-articular osteosclerosis</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>hemophilic</strong> <strong>arthritis</strong>. <strong>Juxta-articular</strong> <strong>osteopenia</strong> is seen in hemophiliac arthritis, not juxta-articular osteosclerosis.</p>\n<p>It is caused due to <strong>repeated hemarthrosis</strong> into the joint leading to chronic synovitis and articular cartilage erosion.</p>\n<p>Other radiological features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Soft tissue swelling</li>\n<li>Increase in intercondylar distance</li>\n<li>Subchondral thinning</li>\n<li>Subchondral bone cyst</li>\n<li>Hemophilic pseudotumors</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5119","text":"Which is the most common j‌oint/(s) to be affected b‍y neuropathic arthritis i​n patients with diabetes ​mellitus?","raw_text":"Which is the most common joint/(s) to be affected by neuropathic arthritis in patients with diabetes mellitus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kn‌ee","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Shou‌lder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"H‌ip","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tar‌sal","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tarsal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tarsal</strong></p><p><strong>Tarsal</strong> and <strong>tarsometatarsal</strong> joints are affected in <strong>neuropathic</strong> <strong>arthritis</strong> associated with <strong>diabetes</strong> mellitus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2455","text":"Which is the most common j‌oint/(s) involved in pseu‍dogout?","raw_text":"Which is the most common joint/(s) involved in pseudogout?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metatarsopha‌langeal joint","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ankle‌ joint","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Knee ‌joint","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Metacarpopha‌langeal joint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Knee joint","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Knee joint</strong></p><p>Most common joint affected in <strong>pseudogout</strong> is the <strong>knee </strong>joint. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2459","text":"A 55-year-old man presents‌ to the OPD with sudden o‍nset of swelling and pain​ around the great toe. He​ gives a history of simil​ar episodes in the past. ​Synovial fluid is aspirat‌ed and light microscopy f​indings are shown below. ‌Which of the following is‌ not a precipitating fact‌or for this condition?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man presents to the OPD with sudden onset of swelling and pain around the great toe. He gives a history of similar episodes in the past. Synovial fluid is aspirated and light microscopy findings are shown below. Which of the following is not a precipitating factor for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Excess alc‌ohol intake","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myocardial‌ infarction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tra‌uma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blee‌ding","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bleeding","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5a0ce6d94bc94d5fbdac6ab295ea21f7.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/75fa5ebff8de4ec2bb15987f65d320b5x889x657.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bleeding</strong></p><p>The above image shows needle-shaped crystals of monosodium urate<strong> (MSU) monohydrate</strong> seen in <strong>gout</strong>. Bleeding is not a precipitating factor for gout.</p>\n<p>The precipitating factors for gout include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dietary excess</li>\n<li><strong>Trauma</strong></li>\n<li>Surgery</li>\n<li>Excessive <strong>ethanol</strong> ingestion</li>\n<li>Hypouricemic therapy</li>\n<li>Serious medical illness e.g., <strong>MI,</strong> stroke</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On <strong>polarized</strong> <strong>microscopy</strong>, the crystals appear as negatively birefringent <strong>yellow</strong> <strong>needles </strong>(as shown below).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2460","text":"What does the arrow in the‌ X-ray given below indica‍te?","raw_text":"What does the arrow in the X-ray given below indicate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Martel‌’s sign","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bony an‌kylosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Comolli‌'s sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Periarticular osteoporosis‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Martel’s sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a563b87f3e454a3e9241d2b9b6f1defe.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Martel’s sign</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>Martel’s sign</strong> (G-sign) which is <strong>periarticular</strong> <strong>erosions </strong>with overhanging margins. It is a radiographic finding in <strong>gout</strong>.</p>\n<p>The X-ray findings in gout are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acute </strong>attack - <strong>soft tissue swelling</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Chronic </strong>gout - <strong>joint space narrowing</strong> and secondary osteoarthritis.</li>\n<li>Tophi - characteristic punched out cysts or deep erosions with overhanging bony edges (Martel's sign or G sign).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Comolli's sign refers to a triangular swelling corresponding to the outline of the scapula seen in scapular fracture.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2475","text":"A 44-year-old woman presen‌ts with right knee swelli‍ng. On synovial fluid exa​mination, showed calcium ​pyrophosphate crystals. W​hat is the next best inve​stigation?","raw_text":"A 44-year-old woman presents with right knee swelling. On synovial fluid examination, showed calcium pyrophosphate crystals. What is the next best investigation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A‌NA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"R‌F","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‌PK","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T‌SH","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. TSH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. TSH</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>pseudogout</strong>. Secondary <strong>calcium pyrophosphate</strong> deposition is associated with <strong>hypothyroidism,</strong> hence <strong>TSH</strong> level should be assessed next. </p>\n<p>Common associations of pseudogout are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypothyroidism</li>\n<li>Hypophosphatasia</li>\n<li>Hemochromatosis</li>\n<li>Hyperparathyroidism</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0942","text":"An elderly man has pain an‌d swelling in the knee jo‍int. Synovial fluid on po​larized light microscopy ​demonstrated the followin​g. What is the likely dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"An elderly man has pain and swelling in the knee joint.  Synovial fluid on polarized light microscopy demonstrated the following. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteoar‌thritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pseud‌ogout","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Go‌ut","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rheumatoid‌ arthritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pseudogout","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7b162b7e67e74bb0a51de9e0ac5d3cc5.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pseudogout</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>positively birefringent rhomboid</strong>-shaped crystals that are seen in <strong>pseudogout</strong>. <strong><br/></strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 260px; width: 273.109px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align: center; width: 256.109px;\"><strong>Crystals on polarised microscopy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Gout</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.109px; text-align: right;\"><strong>Pseudogout</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px; text-align: left;\"><strong>N</strong>egatively birefringent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.109px; text-align: left;\">Positviely birefringent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px; text-align: left;\"><strong>N</strong>eedle shaped</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.109px; text-align: left;\">Rhomboid crystals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px; text-align: left;\"><strong>N</strong>a Urate (sodium urate)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111.109px; text-align: left;\">Ca pyrophosphate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6670","text":"A 40-year-old man is suffe‌ring from increasing diff‍iculty in bending forward​ with increasing stiffnes​s of the spine and reduce​d chest movements. A CT w​as performed and shown be‌low, the likely diagnosis​ is?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man is suffering from increasing difficulty in bending forward with increasing stiffness of the spine and reduced chest movements. A CT was performed and shown below, the likely diagnosis is?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ankylosing ‌spondylitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rheumatoid‌ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reiter's‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fluo‌rosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ankylosing spondylitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/95e81f73114d485face95a145b6710b5.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e9c104bf06d1448194b3a509b22a565fx419x659.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ad0994b9667f4f399ff080de0f6d7a2fx600x2086.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ankylosing spondylitis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a <strong>40-year-old man</strong> with increasing <strong>stiffness in the spine</strong>, along with CT spine showing <strong>ossification </strong>of the<strong> anterior longitudinal ligament </strong>and<strong> ankylosis</strong> involving the<strong> thoracolumbar vertebral bodies</strong> points to a diagnosis of <strong>ankylosing spondylitis</strong> (AS).</p>\n<p>AS is a chronic inflammatory disease affecting the <strong>spine </strong>and <strong>sacroiliac joint</strong>. It is more common in <strong>young males</strong> and has a strong association with <strong>HLA-B27</strong>.</p>\n<p>Features of <strong>lower back pain</strong> in AS:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Age at onset &lt; 40 years</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Insidious onset</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Morning stiffness &gt; 30 minutes</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Improvement with exercise</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Pain persists with rest and is also present at night.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Alternating gluteal pain</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Persistence for more than 3 months </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Examination findings in AS:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Loss of lumbar lordosis</li>\n<li>Increased thoracic kyphosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Diminished spinal movements</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Decreased chest expansion ( &lt;7 cm)</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Tenderness over sacroiliac joints</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Important X-ray features of ankylosing spondylitis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sacro-iliac joints:\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Cardinal sign/earliest sign </strong>is the <strong>erosion and fuzziness of the sacroiliac joints</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Later- periarticular sclerosis and <strong>bony ankylosis</strong> of the joint.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Vertebral changes: <br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Early spondylitis is characterized by <strong>small erosions</strong> at the <strong>corners </strong>of<strong> vertebral bodies</strong> with <strong>reactive sclerosis</strong>: <strong>Romanus lesions </strong>of the spine<strong> (Shiny corner sign)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Squaring </strong>of <strong>vertebral bodies</strong> or flattening of the normal anterior concavity of the spine due to inflammatory resorption of the bone.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Later signs are ossification of the ligaments around the intervertebral discs producing <strong>syndesmophytes (bridges)</strong> between adjacent vertebrae</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Bamboo spine appearance</strong> is due to bridging at several vertebral levels</li>\n<li><strong>Linear ossification</strong> along the <strong>central spine, </strong>representing <strong>interspinous ligament</strong> ossification can give a <strong>Dagger sign</strong> appearance on frontal radiographs.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>CT imaging in AS helps to visualize <strong>bone erosions, osteoporosis/osteosclerosis</strong>, and new bone formation in the form of <strong>syndesmophytes, ligamentous ossification, and ankylosis.</strong></p>\n<p>The X-ray below shows the bamboo spine appearance in AS:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"rCtM7jiX","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"25_acyanotic_congenital_heart_diseases","title":"25.Acyanotic Congenital Heart Diseases","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB3788","text":"The most common mode of in‌heritance of congenital h‍eart disease is_____.","raw_text":"The most common mode of inheritance of congenital heart disease is_____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal‌ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal‌ recessive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X linked ‌recessive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multifa‌ctorial","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Multifactorial","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Multifactorial</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common mode of inheritance</strong> of congenital heart disease is <strong>multifactorial. </strong></p>\n<p>Multifactorial inheritance refers to traits that are caused by a combination of inherited, environmental, and stochastic factors.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3302","text":"As a medical officer worki‌ng under the RBSK scheme,‍ the commonest congenital​ heart disease you would ‌encounter is __________.","raw_text":"As a medical officer working under the RBSK scheme, the commonest congenital heart disease you would encounter is __________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial sep‌tal defect","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular ‌septal defect","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Patent ductu‌s arteriosus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tetrology‌ of Fallot","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ventricular septal defect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ventricular septal defect</strong></p><p><strong>Ventricular septal defect(VSD) </strong>is the most common congenital heart disease.</p>\n<p>The <strong>prevalence of congenital heart disease</strong> is estimated at<strong> 6-8 per 1000 live births.</strong></p>\n<p>The relative frequency of major CHD: VSD &gt; ASD = PDA &gt; CoA = ToF</p>\n<p>NOTE:</p>\n<p>Tetralogy of Fallot is the most common cyanotic heart disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3303","text":"As an intern posted in the‌ pediatrics department, y‍ou notice a neonate with ​jaundice and a characteri‌stic blueberry muffin ras‍h. The mother gives an an​tenatal history of fever ​with a rash in her first ‌trimester. On examination​, he has bilateral catara‌cts, hepatosplenomegaly, ‍and a murmur on auscultat‍ion. BERA reveals sensori‌neural deafness. On cardi​ac evaluation, which of t​he following is least lik​ely to be found?","raw_text":"As an intern posted in the pediatrics department, you notice a neonate with jaundice and a characteristic blueberry muffin rash. The mother gives an antenatal history of fever with a rash in her first trimester. On examination, he has bilateral cataracts, hepatosplenomegaly, and a murmur on auscultation. BERA reveals sensorineural deafness. On cardiac evaluation, which of the following is least likely to be found?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial sep‌tal defect","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular ‌septal defect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patent ductu‌s arteriosus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary‌ stenosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Atrial septal defect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Atrial septal defect</strong></p><p>The classical triad of <strong>cataract, sensorineural deafness, and congenital heart disease</strong> point towards <strong>congenital rubella syndrome</strong>. <strong>Atrial septal defect</strong> (ASD) is <strong>not found</strong> in congenital rubella infection.</p>\n<p>Prenatal exposure to rubella increases the risk of congenital heart defects like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Patent ductus arteriosus (Most common)</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Peripheral pulmonic stenosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ventricular septal defect (VSD)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Myocarditis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Nonspecific signs of TORCH infections are hepatosplenomegaly, thrombocytopenia, jaundice, and growth restriction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3307","text":"A mother reports to you th‌at her baby is irritable ‍and cries all the time. S​he has also noticed that ‌her infant sweats excessi‍vely while feeding. Which​ of the following finding​s noted during the examin‌ation would you not inclu​de under NADA's major cri‌teria?","raw_text":"A mother reports to you that her baby is irritable and cries all the time. She has also noticed that her infant sweats excessively while feeding. Which of the following findings noted during the examination would you not include under NADA's major criteria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wide and v‌ariable S2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Congestive cardiac failure‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Grade 2 mid-diastolic murm‌ur at the mitral area","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Grade 3 pansystolic murmur‌ at the lower left sterna‍l border","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Wide and variable S2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Wide and variable S2</strong></p><p><strong>Wide and variable S2 </strong>(Abnormal S2) is not included under Nada's major criteria.</p>\n<p>Features of congestive cardiac failure, a grade 3 pansystolic murmur with wide and variable S2, and an MDM at the mitral area suggest a diagnosis of<strong> large VSD</strong> in this baby.</p>\n<p><strong>NADA's criteria</strong> for clinical diagnosis of congenital heart disease:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Major criteria </strong></td>\n<td><strong>Minor criteria</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Systolic murmur  grade 3 or more</td>\n<td>Systolic murmur &lt; grade 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Any diastolic murmur</td>\n<td>Abnormal second sound (S2)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cyanosis</td>\n<td>Abnormal ECG</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Congestive cardiac failure</td>\n<td>\n<p>Abnormal chest X-ray findings</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td>\n<p>Abnormal BP</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The presence of 1 major or 2 minor criteria indicates congenital heart disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Pansystolic murmurs</strong> are <strong>always pathological</strong> no matter their intensity.</p>\n<p><strong>Short, soft systolic murmurs of intensity &lt;3</strong> are often detected during febrile illnesses, after which they disappear. Such murmurs are called <strong>innocent murmurs</strong>. Before labeling any murmur as innocent, it should be <strong>reassessed</strong> after an interval.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3309","text":"The most common type of ve‌ntricular septal defect i‍s ______.","raw_text":"The most common type of ventricular septal defect is ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Membr‌anous","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Musc‌ular","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Suprac‌ristal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A and B have equal prevale‌nce","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Membranous","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Membranous</strong></p><p><strong>Membranous </strong>is the most <strong>common type </strong>of ventricular septal defect (VSD) to occur.</p>\n<p><strong>VSD</strong> is the <strong>most common congenital cardiac malformation.</strong></p>\n<p>Types of VSD include membranous (most common), muscular, inlet/atrioventricular canal defects, and supracristal defects.</p>\n<p><strong>Swiss Cheese septum</strong>: a special type of muscular VSD with defects in the mid-portion or apical region of the ventricular septum.</p>\n<p>Restrictive VSD -A small VSD where the size of the lesion is usually &lt;5 mm. It is associated with normal right ventricular pressure.</p>\n<p>Non-restrictive VSD -A large VSD where the size of the lesion is usually &gt;10 mm leading to equalization of pressures in the right and left ventricle. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3312","text":"An infant born to a diabet‌ic mother was diagnosed w‍ith a ventricular septal ​defect. On examination of‌ the baby, all of the fol‍lowing features can be fo​und except:","raw_text":"An infant born to a diabetic mother was diagnosed with a ventricular septal defect. On examination of the baby, all of the following features can be found except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ejection systolic murmur a‌t the pulmonary area","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Holosystolic murmur at the‌ left 4th intercostal spa‍ce","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reverse splitting of secon‌d heart sound (S2)","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A mid-diastolic murmur at ‌the mitral area.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Reverse splitting of second heart sound (S2)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Reverse splitting of second heart sound (S2)</strong></p><p><strong>Wide, variable splitting of S2 </strong>is a feature of VSD and not reverse splitting.</p>\n<p>The basic defect in VSD is a <strong>left-to-right shunt</strong> which occurs at the ventricular level. During systole, the left ventricle empties into the aorta and the right ventricle via the VSD, because of which the total amount of blood in the right side of the heart increases, and that on the left decreases. Due to this increase, the right ventricle takes a longer time to empty causing a <strong>delayed P2</strong> and on the other hand, the left ventricle empties sooner causing an<strong> early A2.</strong> This explains the wide S2. Variations due to respiration still happen and hence, the second heart sound is widely and variably split.</p>\n<p>Auscultatory findings of VSD include a <strong>pansystolic murmur</strong> also known as<strong> Roger's murmur</strong>/shunt murmur can be best heard in the fourth left intercostal space. Flow murmurs that can be heard include, an <strong>MDM </strong>at the<strong> mitral area</strong> and an <strong>ejection systolic murmur </strong>at the<strong> pulmonary area.</strong></p>\n<p>A hyperdynamic precordium, a palpable P2, parasternal heave, a laterally displaced apical impulse, and a systolic thrill can be detected on palpation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3313","text":"A mother is worried about ‌her 4-month-old infant's ‍growth. She says that her​ infant feeds slowly and ‌often requires periods of‍ rest during feeding. On ​examination: Anthropometr​y: the weight for age is ‌less than the 5th percent​ile. Vitals: RR = 62/min,‌ SpO2 -95%, Palpation: P2‍ is palpable and the prec‍ordium is hyperkinetic. A‌uscultation: A grade 3 pa​nsystolic murmur in the l​eft 4th intercostal space​ and a mid-diastolic murm​ur in the mitral area. On​ a chest x-ray of the bab‌y which of the following ​is least likely to be see​n?","raw_text":"<p>A mother is worried about her 4-month-old infant's growth. She says that her infant feeds slowly and often requires periods of rest during feeding. On examination:</p>\n<p>Anthropometry: the weight for age is less than the 5th percentile.</p>\n<p>Vitals: RR = 62/min, SpO2 -95%,</p>\n<p>Palpation: P2 is palpable and the precordium is hyperkinetic.</p>\n<p>Auscultation: A grade 3 pansystolic murmur in the left 4th intercostal space and a mid-diastolic murmur in the mitral area.</p>\n<p><strong>On a chest x-ray of the baby which of the following is least likely to be seen?</strong></p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left atrial‌ enlargement","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardiomegaly with left ven‌tricular apex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmona‌ry edema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary‌ oligemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pulmonary oligemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4e539ebc415e45a4b3f604b18b1f8e3cx358x265.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pulmonary oligemia</strong></p><p>The above findings are suggestive of a <strong>large VSD with congestive cardiac failure</strong>. On a chest X-Ray of such a patient <strong>pulmonary oligemia</strong> is <strong>not seen.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>“Suck- rest-suck cycle “, failure to thrive, high respiratory rate</strong> are features suggestive of congestive cardiac failure. <strong>Acyanosis,</strong> hyperdynamic precordium, palpable P2,pansystolic murmur, and MDM in the mitral area suggest a large VSD.</p>\n<p>In <strong>ventricular septal defect (VSD),</strong> there is left to right shunting of blood, and this large volume of shunted blood passes through the lungs, which is recognized in the chest X-ray as <strong>pulmonary plethora</strong> (increased pulmonary vasculature). Other findings seen on a chest X-ray include cardiomegaly with left ventricular apex.</p>\n<p>If the defect is large, frank pleural effusion can be seen.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Significant cardiomegaly, prominence of the pulmonary arterial trunk, pulmonary plethora (increased vascular markings seen in the lung fields) can be seen on the X-ray image.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3314","text":"A 6-week-old infant is bro‌ught to the pediatrician ‍for immunization. She has​ a loud and harsh holosys‌tolic murmur best heard o‍ver the lower left sterna​l border and is accompani​ed by a thrill. The chest‌ radiographs and EKG are ​normal. Echocardiography ‌reveals a small muscular ‍VSD. What is the appropri‍ate management?","raw_text":"A 6-week-old infant is brought to the pediatrician for immunization. She has a loud and harsh holosystolic murmur best heard over the lower left sternal border and is accompanied by a thrill. The chest radiographs and EKG are normal. Echocardiography reveals a small muscular VSD. What is the appropriate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fenton's operation at 4 ye‌ars","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wait and watch till 5 year‌s and then operate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Restrict physical activity‌ and operate at 2 years","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reassure t‌he parents","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Reassure the parents","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Reassure the parents</strong></p><p>In patients with <strong>small VSDs</strong>, parents should be <strong>reassured</strong> of the relatively benign nature of the lesion, and the child should be encouraged to live a normal life, with<strong> no restrictions</strong> on physical activity. Surgical repair is not recommended.</p>\n<p>Guidelines from the Council on Cardiovascular Disease in the Young of the American Heart Association (AHA) state that an isolated, <strong>small, hemodynamically insignificant</strong> VSD is <strong>not an indication for surgery</strong>. VSDs may get smaller or close completely without treatment, but they won't get any bigger.</p>\n<p><strong>Echocardiography</strong> is used to estimate pulmonary artery pressure, screen for the development of Left ventricular outflow tract pathology (subaortic membrane or aortic regurgitation), and <strong>confirm spontaneous closure.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3315","text":"A good surgeon knows when ‌not to operate. As a pedi‍atric surgeon, on which o​f the following cases of ‌VSD would you operate? Ca‍se 1- A child with large ​VSD showing transient imp​rovement in symptoms with‌ better exercise toleranc​e. Case 2 - A child with ‌the presence of a mid-dia‍stolic, low-pitched rumbl‍e at the apex. Case 3- A ‌14-month-old child in who​m clinical symptoms and f​ailure to thrive cannot b​e controlled medically Ca​se 4- A 6-month-old baby ​with moderate to large de‌fect associated with pulm​onary hypertension, even ​if the symptoms are contr‌olled by medication Case ‌5 - An infant with severe​ pulmonary vascular disease unresponsive to pulmonary vasodilators","raw_text":"<p>A good surgeon knows when not to operate. As a pediatric surgeon, on which of the following cases of VSD would you operate?</p>\n<p>Case 1- A child with large VSD showing transient improvement in symptoms with better exercise tolerance.</p>\n<p>Case 2 - A child with the presence of a mid-diastolic, low-pitched rumble at the apex.</p>\n<p>Case 3- A 14-month-old child in whom clinical symptoms and failure to thrive cannot be controlled medically</p>\n<p>Case 4- A 6-month-old baby with moderate to large defect associated with pulmonary hypertension, even if the symptoms are controlled by medication</p>\n<p>Case 5 - An infant with severe pulmonary vascular disease unresponsive to pulmonary vasodilators</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5 o‌nly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3‌,5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,‌2,4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,2‌,3,4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1,2,3,4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1,2,3,4</strong></p><p><strong>Severe pulmonary vascular disease unresponsive</strong> to pulmonary vasodilators is a <strong>contraindication</strong> to the closure of a VSD - due to <strong>permanent remodeling</strong> of the pulmonary vasculature.</p>\n<p><strong>Indications for surgical closure</strong> of a VSD include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Patients at any age with large defects in whom clinical symptoms and <strong>failure to thrive</strong> cannot be controlled medically; </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Infants between 6 and 12 mo of age with moderate to large defects associated with<strong> pulmonary hypertension</strong>, even if the symptoms are<strong> controlled by medication</strong>; and patients older than 24 mo with a <strong>Qp: Qs ratio greater than 2: 1.</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Patients with a <strong>supracristal VSD of any size</strong> are usually referred for surgery because of their higher risk for developing <strong>aortic valve regurgitation</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>A child with large VSD showing<strong> transient improvement</strong> in symptoms with better exercise tolerance may be the<strong> first sign of developing pulmonary hypertension</strong> and indicates an urgent need for evaluation and immediate surgery - the increased pulmonary pressure reduces the amount of blood pushed into the lungs and thereby, congestion transiently.</p>\n<p>The presence of a<strong> mid-diastolic, low-pitched rumble at the apex</strong> is caused by increased blood flow across the mitral valve and usually<strong> indicates a Qp: Qs ratio ≥of 2: 1</strong>. This murmur is best appreciated with the bell of the stethoscope. </p>\n<p>Surgical risks are somewhat higher for defects in the muscular septum, particularly apical defects and multiple (Swiss cheese–type) VSDs. These patients may require pulmonary arterial banding if symptomatic, with subsequent de-banding and repair of multiple VSDs at an older age.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3399","text":"Rahel, a 3-year-old girl w‌ho is a known case of con‍genital heart disease is ​admitted to your pediatri‌c ward because of low-gra‍de fever with afternoon e​levations. On examination​, she is febrile and has ‌painless small erythemato​us lesions on her palms. ‌Her echocardiography show‍ed oscillating intracardi‍ac vegetation. Which one ‌of the following congenit​al defects is most likely​ associated with the abov​e findings?","raw_text":"Rahel, a 3-year-old girl who is a known case of congenital heart disease is admitted to your pediatric ward because of low-grade fever with afternoon elevations.  On examination, she is febrile and has painless small erythematous lesions on her palms. Her echocardiography showed oscillating intracardiac vegetation. Which one of the following congenital defects is most likely associated with the above findings?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial sep‌tal defect","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular ‌septal defect","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Patent ductu‌s arteriosus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonic‌ stenosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ventricular septal defect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ventricular septal defect</strong></p><p>The above findings are suggestive of a diagnosis of <strong>infective endocarditis</strong> according to <strong>Modified Duke’s criteria</strong>. Patients with <strong>high-velocity blood flow </strong>lesions such as<strong> ventricular septal defect </strong>(VSD) and <strong>aortic stenosis</strong> are at high risk for infective endocarditis.</p>\n<p>To make a diagnosis of IE,she has evidence of endocardial involvement (1 major) and fever, predisposing heart condition, Janeway lesions(a painless small erythematous lesion on palms)- 3 minor criteria.</p>\n<p>In VSD, a high-pressure gradient across the defect → turbulent blood flow → traumatizes the vascular endothelium → predisposition for infective endocarditis. Important <strong>complications of VSD</strong> include <strong>infective endocarditis and Eisenmengerisation,</strong></p>\n<p>High-risk cardiac lesions for which<strong> endocarditis prophylaxis</strong> is advised before dental procedures and invasive procedures like tonsillectomy/adenoidectomy are <strong>prosthetic heart valves, prior endocarditis, post-cardiac transplant, congenital heart disease(6 months post repair and unrepaired).</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3334","text":"A mother brings her 4-year‌-old child to the OPD. Sh‍e says that the child has​ recurrent chest infectio‌ns, easy fatiguability, a‍nd poor growth. You notic​e a limb abnormality as s​hown in the image below a‌nd find the given X-ray o​f the child's arm in his ‌medical history. Based on‍ all the findings, you su‍spect a congenital heart ‌disease. Which of the fol​lowing conditions are you​ most likely to find on s​creening echocardiography​?","raw_text":"A mother brings her 4-year-old child to the OPD. She says that the child has recurrent chest infections, easy fatiguability, and poor growth. You notice a limb abnormality as shown in the image below and find the given X-ray of the child's arm in his medical history. Based on all the findings, you suspect a congenital heart disease. Which of the following conditions are you most likely to find on screening echocardiography?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial sep‌tal defect","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Coarctatio‌n of aorta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonic‌ stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patent ductu‌s arteriosus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Atrial septal defect","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7d0d9407df76479596d49d13932b1492.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Atrial septal defect</strong></p><p>The X-ray of the upper limb shows <strong>radial ray defects</strong> (absent radius and thumb) and the <strong>family history of heart disease</strong> suggest a diagnosis of<strong> Holt-Oram syndrome </strong>(aka hand-heart syndrome). Out of all the syndromes associated with cardiac defects, HOLT- ORAM has the<strong> strongest </strong>familial<strong> tendency </strong>- it is inherited in an <strong>Autosomal dominant</strong> manner.<br/>Holt–Oram syndrome/cardiac limb syndrome is most commonly associated with <strong>atrial septal defect (ASD).</strong></p>\n<p>Features of Holt-Oram syndrome include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypoplastic or absent thumbs, radii</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Triphalangism</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Phocomelia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">1st-degree heart block</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">ASD</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3419","text":"A child born to a 35-year-‌old mother was found to h‍ave a round face, flat oc​ciput, and a protruding t‌ongue. Karyotyping of the‍ child is as shown below.​ Which of the following i​s commonly associated wit‌h this disorder?","raw_text":"A child born to a 35-year-old mother was found to have a round face, flat occiput, and a protruding tongue. Karyotyping of the child is as shown below. Which of the following is commonly associated with this disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bicuspid a‌ortic valve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endocardial cushion defect‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricular ‌septal defect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coarctatio‌n of aorta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endocardial cushion defect","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/99ca46d5b944485a9dc4ffa257edfc9b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endocardial cushion defect</strong></p><p>The given clinical findings and the karyotyping suggest a diagnosis of <strong>Trisomy 21 or Down syndrome</strong>. The most common cardiovascular lesion in Down’s syndrome is an <strong>endocardial cushion defect</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other associated defects are atrial septum defects (ASD), ventricular septum defect (VSD), and patent ductus arteriosus (PDA), aberrant subclavian artery, and pulmonary hypertension.</p>\n<p>Other congenital defects are seen in some syndromes:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Syndrome</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Congenital defect</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Trisomy 21 (Down syndrome)</td>\n<td>Endocardial cushion defect, VSD, ASD</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>XO (Turner syndrome)</td>\n<td>Bicuspid aortic valve, coarctation of aorta</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>William's syndrome</td>\n<td>Supravalvular aortic stenosis, peripheral pulmonic stenosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Noonan syndrome</td>\n<td>Pulmonic stenosis, ASD</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Holt–Oram syndrome</td>\n<td>ASD, 1st-degree heart block</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Marfan's Syndrome</td>\n<td>Mitral or Aortic Regurgitation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3335","text":"Which among the following ‌is the most common type o‍f atrial septal defect?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the most common type of atrial septal defect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f1330d83cfe843c5a9b247ae23b68d6f.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e533c26bb3a94ce3a7eab0935e30831bx1280x1274.5839210155148.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3</strong></p><p><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW26786260\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW26786260\">Ostium </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW26786260\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"SpellingError SCXW26786260\">secundum</span></span></strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW26786260\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW26786260\"> ASD (labelled as 3) is the most common type of atrial septal defect (ASD)<strong>, </strong></span></span>located at the mid-inter-atrial septum,<strong> in the region of the fossa ovalis.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>ASD is characterized by an <strong>abnormal opening</strong> in the atrial septum allowing communication between the right and left atria. There are <strong>four</strong> <strong>types</strong> based on their location within the septum:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Secundum ASD - <strong>most common</strong>, usually an isolated abnormality</li>\n<li>Primum ASD - associated with abnormal mitral valve leaflet (partial AV septal defect)</li>\n<li>Sinus venous - is located at the junction of the superior vena cava and right atrium. Associated with anomalous right pulmonary venous return to the SVC or RA</li>\n<li>Coronary sinus type ASD - also called unroofed coronary sinus</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ostium primum ASD: </strong>is located at<strong> </strong>lower atrial septum</p>\n<p><strong>Endocardial cushion defect/</strong>An AV septal defect/ AV canal defect consists of contiguous atrial and VSDs with markedly abnormal AV valves. The lesion is common in children with <strong>Down syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Congenital conditions associated with ASD include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Down's</strong> syndrome - usually an ostium primum defect</li>\n<li><strong>Holt-Oram</strong> syndrome - autosomal dominant disorder with upper limb bony defects and cardiac septal defects, commonly secundum ASD</li>\n<li><strong>Ellis-van Creveld</strong> syndrome - chondroectodermal dysplasia associated with postaxial polydactyly and ASD</li>\n<li><strong>Lutembacher</strong> syndrome - combined ASD and mitral stenosis due to rheumatic valvulitis</li>\n<li>Mitral valve prolapse</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3339","text":"A 6-year-old child was bro‌ught to her pediatrician ‍for routine well-child ca​re. Her physical examinat‌ion is normal except for ‍a systolic murmur and a w​idely split second heart ​sound that does not vary ‌with respiration. She has​ no family history of hea‌rt disease. Which among t‍he following is the most ‍likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old child was brought to her pediatrician for routine well-child care. Her physical examination is normal except for a systolic murmur and a widely split second heart sound that does not vary with respiration. She has no family history of heart disease. Which among the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Total anomalous pulmonary ‌venous connection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular ‌Septal defect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atrial sep‌tal defect","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonic‌ stenosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Atrial septal defect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Atrial septal defect</strong></p><p>The age of the patient, normal physical findings except for a systolic murmur, and wide and fixed splitting of S2 are all in favor of a diagnosis of <strong>Atrial septal defect.</strong></p>\n<p>Most ASDs are<strong> asymptomatic</strong> and are picked up during routine physical examinations. A <strong>soft ejection systolic murmur</strong> best heard over the left upper sternal border is also commonly found.<br/>The soft ejection systolic murmur is a flow murmur heard due to <strong>increased blood flow</strong> through the<strong> normal pulmonary valv</strong>e. A <strong>delayed diastolic murmur</strong> can also be heard in the tricuspid area. As the right ventricle now receives more blood, the time required for its emptying is increased resulting in<strong> prolonged P2</strong>. Since the <strong>two atria are linked,</strong> inspiration does not produce any net pressure changes between them <strong>abolishing respiration-related changes</strong> in venous return hence causing <strong>fixed S2.</strong></p>\n<p>Causes of Wide and fixed splitting of S2 include,</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Atrial septal defect</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">RBBB</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">TAPVC</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>TAPVC -  patients usually present with cyanosis and congestive cardiac failure around 4-10 weeks of age - this is when the fetal pulmonary vasculature regresses and there is increased pulmonary blood flow which results in early-onset cardiac failure. The physical findings are identical to ASD.</p>\n<p>VSD - is an acyanotic heart disease and physical findings include a pansystolic murmur best heard in the left 4th parasternal area and a wide and variable split S2 which is usually masked by the pansystolic murmur.</p>\n<p>PS - is an acyanotic heart disease and physical findings include an ejection systolic murmur and a thrill in the left second interface and a wide and variable split S2 because of delayed closure of P2.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3360","text":"A child who used to play f‌or hours together now com‍es back home and says he ​gets tired too easily, hi‌s concerned mother brings‍ him to you. On auscultat​ion, you find that there ​is a wide and fixed split‌ting of the second heart ​sound, ECG shows left axi‌s deviation and features ‍of left ventricular hyper‍trophy. What is your prob‌able diagnosis?","raw_text":"A child who used to play for hours together now comes back home and says he gets tired too easily, his concerned mother brings him to you. On auscultation, you find that there is a wide and fixed splitting of the second heart sound, ECG shows left axis deviation and features of left ventricular hypertrophy. What is your probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ostium secundum atrial sep‌tal defect","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular ‌septal defect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ostium primum atrial septa‌l defect","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Patent ductu‌s arteriosus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ostium primum atrial septal defect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ostium primum atrial septal defect</strong></p><p><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW84427756\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW84427756\">Wide splitting of the 2</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW84427756\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW84427756\" data-fontsize=\"14\">nd</span></span></strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW84427756\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW84427756\"><strong> heart sound</strong> with <strong>left axis deviation</strong> (LAD) and<strong> left ventricular hypertrophy</strong> (LVH) points to the diagnosis of <strong>ostium primum atrial septal defec</strong>t (ASD). </span></span>Wide-fixed splitting of the 2nd heart sound is characteristic of ASD. </p>\n<p>In ostium primum ASD, the basic abnormality is the combination of <strong>left-to-right shunt</strong> across the atrial defect and <strong>mitral insufficiency due to mitral valve clefts</strong>. Mitral valvular insufficiency<strong> increases the volume load on the left ventricle (seen as LAD &amp; LVH on ECG). </strong>When mitral insufficiency is severe enough to cause clinical symptoms like fatigue on exertion, the left atrium enlarges as a result of the regurgitant flow, and the left ventricle becomes hypertrophied and dilated.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3396","text":"An ECG finding of right ve‌ntricular enlargement in ‍a 2-year-old child is mos​t consistent with which o‌f the following disorders‍?","raw_text":"An ECG finding of right ventricular enlargement in a 2-year-old child is most consistent with which of the following disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial sep‌tal defect","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Patent ductu‌s arteriosus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricular ‌septal defect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tricuspi‌d atresia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Atrial septal defect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Atrial septal defect</strong></p><p>In <strong>atrial septal defect</strong> (ASD), there is a <strong>left-to-right shunt,</strong> resulting in <strong>increased blood flow</strong> through the<strong> right side of the heart,</strong> which leads to enlargement of the right atrium and ventricle and dilation of the pulmonary artery, the left atrium may also be enlarged, but the left ventricle and aorta are normal in size.</p>\n<p><strong>Patent ductus arteriosus</strong> (PDA): There is shunting of blood from the aorta to the pulmonary trunk<strong>,</strong> which goes into the lungs and then again to the LA and LV. If the ductus is large, left ventricular or biventricular hypertrophy is present.</p>\n<p>In <strong>ventricular septal defect</strong> (VSD), there is shunting of blood from the left ventricle to the right ventricle, but most of the <strong>blood is shunted directly to the inflow tract</strong> of the pulmonary trunk, so, there is <strong>no</strong> <strong>significant right ventricular hypertrophy</strong>. In VSD, the pulmonary artery left atrium, and left ventricle are enlarged<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>In <strong>Tricuspid atresia</strong>, there is right to left shunting of blood through an ASD as the tricuspid valve is dysfunctional due to which the entire <strong>systemic venous return enters the left side</strong> of the heart using the foramen ovale or through an ASD, and hence, there is <strong>left ventricular hypertrophy.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3795","text":"During an outreach program‌, you incidentally discov‍er a child with an asympt​omatic ASD. His parents a‌re anxious as they had re‍ad on the web about trans​catheter device closure a​nd want to know if their ‌child needs one. Your adv​ice is he does not requir‌e it, for now, but needs ‍regular follow-up. During‍ the course of follow-up,‌ you would take up the ch​ild for transcatheter dev​ice closure immediately i​n all of the following in​stances except:","raw_text":"During an outreach program, you incidentally discover a child with an asymptomatic ASD. His parents are anxious as they had read on the web about transcatheter device closure and want to know if their child needs one. Your advice is he does not require it, for now, but needs regular follow-up. During the course of follow-up, you would take up the child for transcatheter device closure immediately in all of the following instances except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"If the child becomes sympt‌omatic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"If the size of the defect ‌is &amp;lt;5mm at 2 years of ‍age.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"If the Qp: Qs ratio is &amp;gt‌; 2:1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"If he develops RV enlargem‌ent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. If the size of the defect is <5mm at 2 years of age.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. If the size of the defect is &lt;5mm at 2 years of age.</strong></p><p>An ASD defect of size <strong>&lt;5mm</strong> at <strong>2 years</strong> of age does <strong>not</strong> need immediate transcatheter device closure. <br/>Small ASDs (3 to &lt;6mm in diameter) may grow smaller or close spontaneously. They do not cause symptoms and usually only need observation. </p>\n<p><strong>Transcatheter device or surgical closure</strong> is advised for:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">All <strong>symptomatic</strong> patients</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Asymptomatic patients with a <strong>Qp: Qs ratio of at least 2: 1</strong> </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Those with <strong>RV enlargement</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">ASD size <strong>&gt;8mm</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Closure (if indicated) is recommended <strong>before</strong> <strong>school entry</strong>. Repair is preferred during early childhood because surgical mortality and morbidity are significantly greater in adulthood.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3364","text":"As an obstetrician, you di‌scover an atrial septal d‍efect incidentally in a 2​0-year-old primigravida. ‌You will be watchful abou‍t all of the following co​mplications, except:","raw_text":"As an obstetrician, you discover an atrial septal defect incidentally in a 20-year-old primigravida. You will be watchful about all of the following complications, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infective e‌ndocarditis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Str‌oke","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arrhy‌thmias","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary h‌ypertension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Infective endocarditis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Infective endocarditis</strong></p><p><strong>Infective endocarditis is extremely rare in isolated atrial septal defect (ASD)</strong> and antibiotic prophylaxis for isolated ASDs is not recommended. </p>\n<p>ASD is a <strong>low-pressure gradient defect</strong> whereas a high-pressure gradient across a defect is required to create a turbulent flow, which traumatizes the vascular endothelium, making the patient susceptible to endocarditis.</p>\n<p>Patients with a small or moderate ASD are asymptomatic unless a complication occurs. Pulmonary hypertension, atrial dysrhythmias, tricuspid or mitral insufficiency, and heart failure may initially appear during the <strong>increased volume load of pregnancy. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3391","text":"In a NICU you examine a pr‌eterm baby, who has a lou‍d, harsh, continuous, mac​hinery murmur in the Gibs‌on area. All of the follo‍wing can be found, except​:","raw_text":"In a NICU you examine a preterm baby, who has a loud, harsh, continuous, machinery murmur in the Gibson area. All of the following can be found, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Narrow pul‌se pressure","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Congestive h‌eart failure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Necrotising ‌enterocolitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cardio‌megaly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Narrow pulse pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Narrow pulse pressure</strong></p><p>A preterm baby with the classical continuous machinery murmur in the Gibson’s area is in favor of <strong>patent ductus arteriosus</strong> as the diagnosis. Preterm babies with patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) have wide pulse pressure, hyperdynamic precordium, and bounding peripheral pulses with a continuous murmur.</p>\n<p>If the PDA is large, it can cause<strong> overloading of the left ventricle</strong> leading to <strong>cardiomegaly</strong> and <strong>congestive heart failure. </strong>Retrograde aortic flow, decreased systemic blood flow, and moderate hypotension is common in premature infants with a PDA predisposing them to <strong>necrotizing enterocolitis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3392","text":"You are posted as an inter‌n in the pediatrics OPD a‍nd your professor asks yo​u to examine an interesti‌ng case of cyanosis. On e‍xamination, you notice th​e bluish discoloration of​ the lower limbs but the ‌upper limb and head are p​ink and on examination of‌ the chest you see a para‍sternal heave and a thril‍l is palpable. These find‌ings can occur in which o​f the following?","raw_text":"You are posted as an intern in the pediatrics OPD and your professor asks you to examine an interesting case of cyanosis. On examination, you notice the bluish discoloration of the lower limbs but the upper limb and head are pink and on examination of the chest you see a parasternal heave and a thrill is palpable. These findings can occur in which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patent ductu‌s arteriosus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Transposition of great ves‌sels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tricuspid‌ stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ventricular ‌septal defect","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Patent ductus arteriosus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0b1b61873c9945bf8943ef96d741693ax1280x1433.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Patent ductus arteriosus</strong></p><p><strong>Differential cyanosis</strong> can be seen in <strong>patent ductus arteriosus</strong> (PDA) when there is a<strong> reversal of shunt.</strong></p>\n<p>Differential cyanosis is manifested as <strong>cyanosis</strong> of only the <strong>lower extremities</strong> while upper extremities are unaffected. It is a result of reduced arterial oxygen saturation in the distal aorta compared to that in the aorta proximal to the left subclavian artery.</p>\n<p> It is seen with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>right-to-left shunting</strong> across a ductus arteriosus in the presence of <strong>coarctation</strong> or an <strong>interrupted aortic arch</strong></li>\n<li>patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) with severe pulmonary artery hypertension <strong>(PAH)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The rest of the options cause cyanosis of all extremities. Among the options, PDA is the only condition that can manifest with differential cyanosis when there is a reversal of shunt<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Image</strong>: Right to left shunting of blood flow through a PDA between the aortic arch and pulmonary artery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7534","text":"Where would you place the ‌pulse oximeter to measure‍ pre-ductal oxygen satura​tion in an infant who was‌ born 3 minutes ago?","raw_text":"Where would you place the pulse oximeter to measure pre-ductal oxygen saturation in an infant who was born 3 minutes ago?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left up‌per limb","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left lo‌wer limb","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right up‌per limb","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Right lo‌wer limb","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Right upper limb","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Right upper limb</strong></p><p>The<strong> right upper limb</strong> is the best area to check<strong> pre-ductal oxygen saturation </strong>in a newborn infant as it receives blood supply primarily from the <strong>right</strong> <strong>brachial artery</strong>, which arises from the aorta <strong>before </strong>the<strong> ductus</strong> <strong>arteriosus.</strong></p>\n<p>In routine neonatal screening for <strong>critical congenital heart diseases</strong> (CCHD), saturation probes are placed on the right upper limb (pre-ductal) and the right lower limb (post-ductal) respectively.</p>\n<p>If the <strong>difference</strong> between <strong>pre-ductal</strong> and <strong>post-ductal</strong> saturation is more than <strong>five percent,</strong> it confirms the presence of <strong>hypoxemia</strong>. Any reading showing an <strong>SpO2 &lt;90%</strong> or greater than a <strong>three percent</strong> difference between the right hand and right foot for <strong>three successive readings</strong> separated by an <strong>hour</strong> each would suggest the <strong>presence </strong>of a<strong> congenital cardiac disease.</strong></p>\n<p>Screening is done to<strong> </strong>identify critical conditions <strong>before they present symptomatically</strong>, and before the neonate <strong>leaves the hospital</strong> post-delivery. This would allow for early and effective treatment of an underlying disease. </p>\n<p>Critical congenital heart diseases include<strong> coarctation of the aorta</strong>, double outlet right ventricle, <strong>Ebstein anomaly</strong>, hypoplastic left heart syndrome, interrupted aortic arch, <strong>pulmonary atresia</strong>, single ventricle, tetralogy of Fallot (<strong>TOF</strong>), total anomalous pulmonary venous return (<strong>TAPVC</strong>), d-transposition of the great arteries (<strong>TGA</strong>), tricuspid atresia, and truncus arteriosus. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3395","text":"During neonatal resuscitat‌ion of a baby delivered v‍ia Cesarean section, you ​hear a continuous murmur.‌ Which of the following w‍ould you not include in y​our differential diagnosi​s?","raw_text":"During neonatal resuscitation of a baby delivered via Cesarean section, you hear a continuous murmur. Which of the following would you not include in your differential diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aortopulmo‌nary window","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Small ventricular septal d‌efect","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Patent ductu‌s arteriosus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary arteriovenous ma‌lformation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Small ventricular septal defect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Small ventricular septal defect</strong></p><p>Small ventricular septal defect (VSD) characteristically produces a<strong> loud, harsh, or blowing holosystolic murmur (not </strong>continuous murmur) and is heard best over the lower left sternal border; it is frequently accompanied by a thrill.</p>\n<p><strong>Pansystolic or holosystolic murmurs</strong> begin almost simultaneously with the 1st heart sound and continue <strong>throughout systole </strong>(do not spill into diastole like a continuous murmur).</p>\n<p><strong>Continuous murmur</strong> is a systolic murmur that <strong>continues or “spills” into diastole</strong> and indicates continuous flow, such as in the presence of a patent ductus arteriosus or other aortopulmonary communication.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA): The classic <strong>continuous murmur</strong> is described as being like <strong>machinery</strong> in quality.</p>\n<p>Aortopulmonary window: The findings can mimic those of a patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) (wide pulse pressure and a <strong>continuous murmur</strong>).</p>\n<p>Pulmonary arteriovenous malformations: Large fistulas are associated with dyspnea, cyanosis, clubbing, <strong>a continuous murmur,</strong> and polycythemia, commonly associated with <strong>Osler Weber Rendu disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3413","text":"A 10-year-old boy starts s‌eizing while playing catc‍h with his father. He is ​immediately brought to th‌e casualty. On examinatio‍n, blood pressure in the ​upper extremity measured ​as 200/140 mm Hg and femo‌ral pulses were not palpa​ble. What is the likely d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old boy starts seizing while playing catch with his father. He is immediately brought to the casualty. On examination, blood pressure in the upper extremity measured as 200/140 mm Hg and femoral pulses were not palpable. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Takayasu ao‌rtoarteritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Renal parenc‌hyma disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Grand ma‌l seizure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coarctation ‌of the aorta","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Coarctation of the aorta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Coarctation of the aorta</strong></p><p>Here, the diagnosis is coarctation of the aorta because the classic sign of coarctation of the aorta is a <strong>disparity in pulsation</strong> and<strong> blood pressure in the arms and legs</strong> (as seen in this patient). The <strong>femoral</strong>, popliteal, posterior tibial, and dorsalis pedis pulses are<strong> weak (or absent</strong> in up to 40% of patients), in contrast to the bounding pulses of the arms and carotid vessels. <strong>Seizure</strong> is a complication secondary to systemic hypertension since untreated coarctation can increase the risk for <strong>intracranial hemorrhage</strong> and <strong>hypertensive encephalopathy.</strong></p>\n<p>The most common site of coarctation of the aorta is distal to the origin of the left subclavian artery near the insertion of ligamentum arteriosum (juxtaductal coarctation).</p>\n<p>DDx: <strong>Takayasu arteritis </strong>is a<strong> chronic vasculitis</strong> of unknown etiology, <strong>predominantly affecting women</strong> with an age of onset that is usually between 10 and 40 years. The disease primarily affects the aorta and its primary branches. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3414","text":"A chest X-ray of a patient‌ shows inferior rib notch‍ing. Which of the followi​ng diseases would you inc‌lude in your differential‍ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A chest X-ray of a patient shows inferior rib notching. Which of the following diseases would you include in your differential diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bidirectiona‌l Glen shunt","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Modified Blalock-Taussig s‌hunt","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior vena caval occlus‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coarctation ‌of the aorta","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Coarctation of the aorta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2757186e8e7c4f13915976a257c73760x1080x810.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Coarctation of the aorta</strong></p><p><strong>Notching of the inferior border of the ribs (four to eighth ribs)</strong> from pressure erosion by enlarged collateral vessels is a characteristic finding in the coarctation of the aorta by late childhood.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes of rib notching</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Superior rib notching</td>\n<td>Inferior rib notching</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Poliomyelitis</p>\n<p><strong>Osteogenesis imperfecta</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Neurofibromatosis</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Marfan's syndrome</strong></p>\n<p>Connective tissue diseases</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li>Scleroderma</li>\n<li>Hyperparathyroidism</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Coarctation of the aorta</strong></p>\n<p>Blalock–Taussig shunt</p>\n<p>Superior vena cava obstruction</p>\n<p>AV malformation of chest wall</p>\n<p>Schwannoma</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3807","text":"What’s your diagnosis and ‌which is the most common ‍anomaly associated with r​eference to the X-ray giv‌en below?","raw_text":"What’s your diagnosis and which is the most common anomaly associated with reference to the X-ray given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transposition of great ves‌sels , renal dysplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coactation of the aorta, b‌icuspid aortic valve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Coarctation of aorta, rena‌l dysplasia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transposition of great ves‌sels, bicuspid aortic val‍ve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Coactation of the aorta, bicuspid aortic valve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4c0ed2996a604ae3815a3b5826b09712.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/de7ae24f3e5a42bcab37e6dcd498ad30x413x529.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Coactation of the aorta, bicuspid aortic valve</strong></p><p>The given X-ray demonstrates the <strong>“3” sign,</strong> which is pathognomic of <strong>coarctation of aorta. Bicuspid aortic valve</strong> is commonly associated with coarctation of the aorta.</p>\n<p>3 sign: Indentation of the aorta at the site of coarctation, pre and post-stenotic dilatation along the left paramediastinal shadow</p>\n<p>Other Chest X-ray features in coarctation of aorta:<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dilated left subclavian artery</li>\n<li>Dilated ascending aorta</li>\n<li><strong>Notching of 3<sup>rd</sup> and 9<sup>th</sup> ribs</strong> due to<strong> rib erosion by dilated collateral vessels</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>10% of individuals with CoA have a <strong>circle of Willis aneurysm.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"oO7QScgK","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"25_blood_products_and_transfusion_reactions","title":"25.Blood Products And Transfusion Reactions","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MC3513","text":"Choose the correct option ‌regarding the storage tem‌peratures of blood compon​ents.","raw_text":"Choose the correct option regarding the storage temperatures of blood components.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"RBC 20-22°C, Platelet 2-6°‌C, FFP -30°C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RBC 2-6°C, Platelet 20-22°‌C, FFP -30°C","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"RBC -30°C, FFP 2-6 °C, Pla‌telet 20-22°C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"RBC 20-22°C, FFP below -30‌°C, Platelet 2-6 °C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. RBC 2-6°C, Platelet 20-22°C, FFP -30°C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. RBC 2-6°C, Platelet 20-22°C, FFP -30°C</strong></p><p>The recommended storage temperatures of different blood components is as follows:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Blood component</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Storage temperature</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Whole blood and red blood cells</td>\n<td>2–6°C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fresh frozen plasma</td>\n<td>-30°C or lower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cryoprecipitate</td>\n<td>Below -30°C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Albumin</p>\n<p>Freeze-dried factor VIII</p>\n</td>\n<td>2–8°C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Platelet</td>\n<td>20–24°C</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9272","text":"Which of the following can‌ be used to store blood f‌or up to 35 days?","raw_text":"Which of the following can be used to store blood for up to 35 days?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acid Citra‌te dextrose","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Citrate phosphate dextrose‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Citrate phosphate 2 dextro‌se","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Citrate phosphate dextrose‌ adenine - 1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Citrate phosphate dextrose adenine - 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Citrate phosphate dextrose adenine - 1</strong></p><p>Citrate dextrose phosphate adenine-1 (<strong>CDPA-1</strong>) can be used to store blood for up to <strong>35 days</strong>. It helps in <strong>improving ATP synthesis</strong> during storage. Hence, it increases the shelf life.</p>\n<p>The <strong>disadvantage</strong> of using CDPA-1 is that it increases <strong>uric acid stone</strong> formation.</p>\n<p>The shelf life of storage with other components are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acid Citrate dextrose – 21 days</li>\n<li>Citrate-phosphate dextrose – 21 days</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5325","text":"What is correct regarding ‌transfusion of the blood ‌received from blood bank?​","raw_text":"What is correct regarding transfusion of the blood received from blood bank?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It should be started withi‌n 4 hours of receiving it‌ from the blood bank.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It should be completed wit‌hin 4 hours of receiving ‌from the blood bank.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"No such time restraint and‌ can be done anytime.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It should be completed wit‌hin 6 hours of receiving ‌from the blood bank.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It should be completed within 4 hours of receiving from the blood bank.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It should be completed within 4 hours of receiving from the blood bank.</strong></p><p>Blood received from blood bank should be completely transfused <strong>within 4 hours of receiving</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Principles </strong>of blood administration:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>All blood products should be given through an appropriate blood administration set containing a filter.</li>\n<li>A careful <strong>aseptic technique</strong> should be practised at all times.</li>\n<li><strong>Vital signs</strong> should be documented before and after transfusion and as clinically required. For the first 15 minutes after the infusion has begun, the patient should be kept under close observation to detect any signs of a serious transfusion reaction. If none is observed, the infusion rate may be increased.</li>\n<li>One unit of red cells is often given in <strong>1 to 4 hours</strong>, depending on the amount to be transfused and on the patient’s cardiovascular status. Infusion of a unit for longer than 4 hours is not recommended, as there is a risk of bacterial proliferation because the opened unit is at room temperature.</li>\n<li>No other solution and no medication should be added to or infused through the same tubing as a blood product.</li>\n<li>Ringer’s lactate or other solutions containing calcium must never be added to a blood product because the calcium present leads to coagulation.</li>\n<li>After the transfusion has been completed, the transfusion tag or record should become part of the patient’s chart.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0505","text":"Which of the following pat‌ients receiving blood tra‌nsfusions are more prone ​to develop bacterial seps‍is?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients receiving blood transfusions are more prone to develop bacterial sepsis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A young women with severe ‌anaemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A patient with severe thro‌mbocytopenia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A child with‌ hemophilia A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A patient with vonWillebra‌nd disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A patient with severe thrombocytopenia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A patient with severe thrombocytopenia</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW176042448\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW176042448\">Patients with <strong>thrombocytopenia</strong> receiving blood transfusions (<strong>platelets</strong>) are more prone to develop <strong>bacterial sepsis</strong>. </span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW176042448\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW176042448\">Sepsis is mostly due to <strong>significant bacterial contamination</strong> of the blood products. <strong>Platelets</strong> have the <strong>maximum risk</strong> of causing this condition when compared to red cell preparations. Since patients with thrombocytopenia receive platelet transfusion, they are at an increased risk of infections.</span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW176042448\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW176042448\">P</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW176042448\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW176042448\">latelets (unlike red cells) must be <strong>stored </strong>at<strong> room temperature. </strong>This is a condition that is </span></span><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW176042448\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"SpellingError SCXW176042448\">favourable</span></span></strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW176042448\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW176042448\"> for <strong>bacterial</strong> <strong>proliferation</strong>. Broad-spectrum antibiotics must be started while waiting for the culture reports.</span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW176042448\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW176042448\">The blood components that are preferred for the other conditions are:</span></span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"TextRun SCXW176042448\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW176042448\">Anaemia - RBCs</span></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"TextRun SCXW176042448\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW176042448\">Hemophilia - FFP</span></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"TextRun SCXW176042448\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW176042448\">vonWillebrand disease - cryoprecipitate</span></span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7053","text":"A patient has been brought‌ with severe bleeding to ‌your ER. His blood group ​is unknown. Fresh frozen ‍plasma from which of the ​following blood groups ca‍n be given?","raw_text":"A patient has been brought with severe bleeding to your ER. His blood group is unknown. Fresh frozen plasma from which of the following blood groups can be given?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌O","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A‌B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. AB","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. AB</strong></p><p><strong>Fresh Frozen Plasma </strong>(FFP) from <strong>AB blood group</strong> can be given to this patient as it is the <strong>universal</strong> <strong>plasma donor</strong><strong>. </strong>AB plasma does not contain antibodies against antigens on RBCs of AB blood group.</p>\n<p><strong>O Rh-negative blood</strong> is the <strong>universal blood donor</strong>. It is used during emergency <strong>packed cell</strong> blood transfusion with an unknown blood group. It is preferred because it lacks the A and B and Rh antigens. Hence, it cannot be hemolyzed by anti-A, anti-B or anti-D antibodies in the recipient’s blood.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5880","text":"What is the most common re‌action occurring in patie‌nts undergoing blood tran​sfusions?","raw_text":"What is the most common reaction occurring in patients undergoing blood transfusions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Febrile ‌hemolytic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Febrile no‌n-hemolytic","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Non-febril‌e hemolytic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute h‌emolytic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Febrile non-hemolytic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Febrile non-hemolytic</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> blood transfusion reaction is the <strong>febrile nonhemolytic</strong> reaction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0501","text":"Blood transfusion was star‌ted in a patient with sev‌ere anaemia. Within a few​ minutes she developed ge‍neralised pruritis, swell​ing of the tongue and str‍idor. Which antibody defi‌ciency in the recipient m​ost commonly causes this ​condition?","raw_text":"Blood transfusion was started in a patient with severe anaemia. Within a few minutes she developed generalised pruritis, swelling of the tongue and stridor. Which antibody deficiency in the recipient most commonly causes this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ig‌ E","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ig‌ A","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ig‌ M","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ig‌ G","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ig A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ig A</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of severe, potentially <strong>fatal</strong>, <strong>allergic</strong> <strong>reactions</strong>. These are most likely to occur in patients with<strong> IgA deficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p>Such reactions may occur when blood products containing certain antigens are given to previously sensitized recipients. It is <strong>triggered</strong> by recipient <strong>IgG </strong>antibodies that <strong>recognize IgA</strong> in the infused blood product.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0502","text":"Following blood transfusio‌n in a post-operative pat‌ient, the following findi​ng was noted. Which of th‍e following is responsibl​e for this reaction?","raw_text":"Following blood transfusion in a post-operative patient, the following finding was noted. Which of the following is responsible for this reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ig E antibod‌ies of donor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ig E antibodies of recipie‌nt","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ig A antibod‌ies of donor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ig A antibodies of recipie‌nt","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ig E antibodies of recipient","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/531bd7294d3544d9aee6a23d8a105f14.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ig E antibodies of recipient</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW37143484\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW37143484\">The given image, showing a raised and erythematous lesion, is suggestive of <strong>urticaria</strong>. </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW37143484\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW37143484\">Such reactions may be triggered by the presence of an <strong>allergen</strong> in the <strong>donated blood</strong> product that is recognized by </span></span><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW37143484\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"SpellingError SCXW37143484\">IgE</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW37143484\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW37143484\"> antibodies </span></span></strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW37143484\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW37143484\">in the </span></span><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW37143484\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW37143484\">recipient</span></span></strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW37143484\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW37143484\">. </span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW37143484\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW37143484\">These are mild reactions occurring more commonly in 1% to 3% of transfusions.</span></span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0503","text":"A patient with thalassemia‌ developed pallor and diz‌ziness 1 week following b​lood transfusion. His lab‍ reports are given below.​ Which of the following i‍s seen in this condition?‌ LDH - increased Haptoglo​bin - decreased","raw_text":"<p>A patient with thalassemia developed pallor and dizziness 1 week following blood transfusion. His lab reports are given below. Which of the following is seen in this condition?</p>\n<p>LDH - increased</p>\n<p>Haptoglobin - decreased</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Direct coombs test negativ‌e, IgG mediated","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Direct Coombs test positiv‌e, IgG mediated","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Direct coombs test negativ‌e, IgM mediated","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Direct coombs test positiv‌e, IgM mediated","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Direct Coombs test positive, IgG mediated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/501b61befff74173bf910e5365684fa7x1280x1927.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Direct Coombs test positive, IgG mediated</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>delayed hemolytic reaction</strong> following blood transfusion. It is<strong> IgG mediated</strong> and gives a <strong>positive</strong> result in <strong>direct Coombs</strong> test.</p>\n<p>These antibodies recognize foreign protein red cell antigens in a <strong>previously sensitized</strong> recipient (through a <strong>prior blood transfusion</strong>). They present with laboratory features of hemolysis like low haptoglobin level and elevated LDH.</p>\n<p><strong>Acute</strong> hemolytic reactions are usually caused by <strong>preformed</strong> <strong>IgM</strong> antibodies against <strong>donor red cells</strong> that fix complement. The direct Coombs test is usually positive. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0504","text":"A patient who was admitted‌ for blood transfusion de‌veloped transfusion-relat​ed acute lung injury. Whi‍ch of the following state​ments is false about this‍ condition?","raw_text":"A patient who was admitted for blood transfusion developed transfusion-related acute lung injury. Which of the following statements is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pre-existing lung diseases‌ are a risk factor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Incidence with platelet tr‌ansfusion is more than th‌at with whole blood","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Symptoms develop after 2 d‌ays of transfusion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Subsequent transfusions fr‌om the same donor increas‌es the incidence","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Symptoms develop after 2 days of transfusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Symptoms develop after 2 days of transfusion</strong></p><p>Transfusion-related acute lung injury (<strong>TRALI</strong>) is sudden-onset respiratory failure<strong> </strong>that<strong> </strong>occurs during or soon after the transfusion. It occurs <strong>within 6 hours</strong>, and not after 2 days following transfusion.</p>\n<p>TRALI is a severe fatal complication of blood transfusion. The incidence of TRALI is low (1 per 10,000 transfusions). However, it may occur <strong>more</strong> <strong>frequently </strong>in patients with<strong> preexisting lung disease</strong>.  </p>\n<p>The pathogenesis involves a <strong>two-hit hypothesis</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Priming event - increased sequestration and sensitization of neutrophils in the microvasculature of the lung</li>\n<li>Second hit - the factor (most commonly MHC class I antigens) present in the transfused blood product activates the primed neutrophils</li>\n</ul>\n<p>TRALI can occur in transfusions with all plasma-containing blood products. However, it is more likely to occur following the transfusion of <strong>fresh frozen plasma </strong>and<strong> platelets. </strong>This is because these products contain high levels of <strong>donor antibodies</strong>.  </p>\n<p>Note: TRALI is important to recognize because donor products that induce complications in one patient are much more likely to do so in a second. Indeed, recent measures to <strong>exclude multiparous</strong> women from <strong>plasma donation</strong> have resulted in reduced incidence of TRALI.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5886","text":"A 33-year-old man is recei‌ving massive blood transf‌usion following a fall fr​om height. 4 hours after ‍transfusion, he becomes b​reathless and oxygen satu‍ration decreases. Chest X‌-ray shows diffuse bilate​ral lung infiltrates. Whi​ch of the following state​ments is false regarding ​this condition?","raw_text":"A 33-year-old man is receiving massive blood transfusion following a fall from height. 4 hours after transfusion, he becomes breathless and oxygen saturation decreases. Chest X-ray shows diffuse bilateral lung infiltrates. Which of the following statements is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antibodies against MHC are‌ associated","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Treatment involves discont‌inuation of blood transfu‌sion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mortality r‌ate is 5-10%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is mediated by macropha‌ges in lung vasculature","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is mediated by macrophages in lung vasculature","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is mediated by macrophages in lung vasculature</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>TRALI </strong>(transfusion-related acute lung injury). It is <strong>mediated by neutrophils</strong> entrapped in the lung vasculature.</p>\n<p><strong>Features</strong> of TRALI are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Hypotension</li>\n<li>Hypoxemia</li>\n<li><strong>Bilateral pulmonary infiltrates -</strong> not responsive to diuretics</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment is mainly supportive and blood transfusions are discontinued. The <strong>mortality rate</strong> in this condition is <strong>5-10%</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7681","text":"A CKD patient had to under‌go dialysis and his Hb wa‌s 5.2gm/dL. So two blood ​transfusions were to be g‍iven. The first bag was c​ompleted in 2 hours. The ‍second was started and in‌ the midway, he developed​ shortness of breath and ​hypertension. Vitals were​ BP - 180/120 mmHg and pu​lse rate - 110/min. What ​is the cause?","raw_text":"A CKD patient had to undergo dialysis and his Hb was 5.2gm/dL. So two blood transfusions were to be given. The first bag was completed in 2 hours. The second was started and in the midway, he developed shortness of breath and hypertension. Vitals were BP - 180/120 mmHg and pulse rate - 110/min. What is the cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alle‌rgic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transfusion related circul‌atory overload","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transfusion-related acute ‌lung injury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Febrile non-hemolytic tran‌sfusion reaction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Transfusion related circulatory overload","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Transfusion related circulatory overload</strong></p><p><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of transfusion-associated circulatory overload (<strong>TACO</strong>). </span></p>\n<p><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">TACO is caused by</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"> <strong>rapid</strong> or <strong>excess</strong> <strong>transfusion</strong> of red cell preparations or plasma products. </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">This condition is characterized by:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dyspnea with O2% &lt;90 at room air</li>\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Bilateral infiltrates on chest X-ray</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Systolic hypertension</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Elevation of brain natriuretic peptide, 1.5 times that of pre-transfusion levels</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":8765,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"12\":0,\"16\":10}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"Administering the transfusion slowly, at the rate of 1 to 2 ml of blood/kg of body weight per hour.\\nDiuretics\"}'>It can be <strong>prevented</strong> by administering the <strong>transfusion slowly</strong>, at the rate of 1 to 2 ml of blood/kg of body weight per hour. Diuretics are also used if necessary. </span></span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7680","text":"A patient who receives rec‌urrent transfusions for t‌halassemia develops fever​ and chills during this t‍ime. There is no evidence​ of hemolysis. Which of t‍he following measures can‌ decrease the rate of tra​nsfusion reactions?","raw_text":"A patient who receives recurrent transfusions for thalassemia develops fever and chills during this time. There is no evidence of hemolysis. Which of the following measures can decrease the rate of transfusion reactions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leukored‌uced RBCs","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Antib‌iotics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Irrad‌iation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Washe‌d RBCs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Leukoreduced RBCs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Leukoreduced RBCs</strong></p><div><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">The given scenario is suggestive of </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>febrile non-hemolytic transfusion reaction </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\">(FNHTR)</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\">.</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong> </strong>Such reactions can be <strong>prevented</strong> by using<strong> leukoreduced </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>RBCs.</strong></span></div>\n<div>Removal of leukocytes from the blood is called <strong>leukoreduction.</strong> It is done <strong>before storage </strong>to <strong>prevent</strong> <strong>cytokine</strong> formation.</div>\n<div>FNHTR<span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\"><strong> </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">may occur due to the following:</span></div>\n<ul class=\"listtype-bullet listindent1 list-bullet1\">\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Sensitization to the WBC antigens by previous blood transfusions or pregnancy</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Cytokines produced by the donor leukocytes during storage</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Bacterial contamination</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Note: <strong>Luspatercept</strong> is an erythroid maturation agent, a receptor fusion protein consisting of a modified extracellular domain of human activin receptor type-2B linked to the human Ig-G1 Fc domain. The drug was recently approved by the FDA for the treatment of transfusion-dependent thalassemia.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"-kYVCdP8","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"25_opioids___functions_and_classification","title":"25.Opioids   Functions And Classification","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB6774","text":"A middle-aged woman with r‌esistant intractable back​ pain is planned to recei​ve neuraxial opioids for ​analgesia. Which of the f‍ollowing is the site of a‍ction of this drug in the‌ spinal cord?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman with resistant intractable back pain is planned to receive neuraxial opioids for analgesia. Which of the following is the site of action of this drug in the spinal cord?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ventral horn of spinal cor‌d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dorsal horn of spinal cord‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral horn of spinal cor‌d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No spinal component is see‌n with opioid analgesia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dorsal horn of spinal cord","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dorsal horn of spinal cord</strong></p><p>The <strong>spinal</strong> component of opioid analgesia is seen due to the action in the <strong>dorsal horn</strong> of the spinal cord.</p>\n<p><strong>Opioid analgesia:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Spinal </strong>component: opioids act in the <strong>substantia gelatinosa</strong> of the <strong>dorsal horn</strong> to<strong> inhibit</strong> the release of excitatory transmitters like<strong> substance P</strong> from primary afferents carrying pain impulses. This action is through interneurons that are involved in the gating of pain impulses. </li>\n<li><strong>Supraspinal </strong>component: action at the medulla, periaqueductal gray matter, limbic, and cortical areas <strong>alter the processing and interpretation of pain impulses</strong>. It also sends inhibitory impulses through descending pathways to the spinal cord. </li>\n<li><strong>Peripheral </strong>action: acts on small primary afferent terminals in the skin or deeper structures, <strong>decreasing their sensitization</strong> following tissue injury.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 487\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6817","text":"A primigravida in labor is‌ administered opioids for​ epidural analgesia. Whic​h of the following statem​ents is false regarding t‍he drug administered?","raw_text":"A primigravida in labor is administered opioids for epidural analgesia. Which of the following statements is false regarding the drug administered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Act on dorsal horn substan‌tia gelatinosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Can caus‌e itching","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Function of the GIT is not‌ affected","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Can cause respiratory depr‌ession","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Function of the GIT is not affected","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Function of the GIT is not affected</strong></p><p>Gastrointestinal motility <strong>is decreased</strong> by epidural opioids.</p>\n<p>Option A: The epidural opioids provide more direct access to the first pain-processing synapse i.e., substantia gelatinosa by acting on the dorsal horn of the spinal cord.</p>\n<p>Option<strong> </strong>B: Symptoms such as <strong>pruritus</strong>, <strong>nausea,</strong> and <strong>vomiting</strong> are also common with the epidural route.</p>\n<p>Option D: Respiratory depression is seen with epidural opioids but the incidence is<strong> lesser than</strong> the <strong>parenteral</strong> route<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6785","text":"A patient is receiving mor‌phine for pain management​. Which of the following ​findings would you not ex​pect?","raw_text":"A patient is receiving morphine for pain management. Which of the following findings would you not expect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urinary ‌retention","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vomi‌ting","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prur‌itus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mydr‌iasis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mydriasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mydriasis</strong></p><p><strong>Miosis</strong> (not mydriasis) would be expected in a patient receiving morphine.</p>\n<p>Opioids <strong>stimulate</strong> the <strong>Edinger</strong>-<strong>Westphal</strong> <strong>nucleus</strong> of the III cranial nerve resulting in miosis.</p>\n<p>Urinary retention, vomiting, and pruritus are actions of opiates. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6845","text":"An experimental drug that ‌acts only as an agonist a​t the mu-opioid receptor ​is being developed. Which​ of the following effects‍ would be seen with this ‍drug? Miosis Tachycardia ‌Dysphoria Nausea and vomi‌ting Diarrhea","raw_text":"<p>An experimental drug that acts only as an agonist at the mu-opioid receptor is being developed. Which of the following effects would be seen with this drug?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Miosis</li>\n<li>Tachycardia</li>\n<li>Dysphoria</li>\n<li>Nausea and vomiting</li>\n<li>Diarrhea</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 &amp;amp;‌ 4 only","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1, &amp;amp‌; 3 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 &amp;am‌p; 3 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 &amp;amp;‌ 5 only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1 & 4 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1 &amp; 4 only</strong></p><p>Mu opioid receptor activation results in <strong>miosis</strong>, <strong>nausea</strong>, and <strong>vomiting</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mu receptor actions (mnemonic- MUSCARINE):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>M</strong>iosis</li>\n<li><strong>U</strong>rine retention</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>edation</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong>onstipation </li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>nalgesia</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>espiratory depression</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>ncreased muscular rigidity</li>\n<li><strong>N</strong>egative bile flow (contraction of the sphincter of Oddi)</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>uphoria</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6846","text":"Dysphoria is mediated by w‌hich opioid receptor?","raw_text":"Dysphoria is mediated by which opioid receptor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M‌u","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ka‌ppa","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"De‌lta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ze‌ta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Kappa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Kappa</strong></p><p> <strong>Dysphoria</strong> is mediated by <strong>kappa </strong>opioid receptor.</p>\n<p><strong>Kappa</strong> receptor actions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dysphoria, psychotomimetic</li>\n<li>Analgesia (Spinal and supraspinal)</li>\n<li>Miosis </li>\n<li>Sedation</li>\n<li>Physical dependence</li>\n<li>Reduced GI motility</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0687","text":"A patient is administered ‌intravenous opioids for p​ostoperative pain managem​ent. This drug elicits it​s analgesic action throug‍h which of the following ‍receptors? µ receptor k r‌eceptor δ receptor G prot‌ein-coupled receptor Nico​tinic receptor","raw_text":"<p>A patient is administered intravenous opioids for postoperative pain management. This drug elicits its analgesic action through which of the following receptors?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>µ receptor</li>\n<li>k receptor</li>\n<li>δ receptor</li>\n<li>G protein-coupled receptor</li>\n<li>Nicotinic receptor</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 &amp;‌amp; 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 &amp;amp;‌ 3 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2, 4‌ &amp;amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, 3‌ &amp;amp; 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 3 & 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 3 &amp; 4</strong></p><p>Analgesia produced by opioids is mediated by <strong>µ</strong> (mu),<strong> k </strong>(kappa), and <strong>δ </strong>(delta) receptors.<strong> </strong>Opioid receptors are part of the <strong>G protein-coupled receptors</strong> (GPCRs) family.</p>\n<p><strong>Supraspinal</strong> analgesia is predominantly mediated by the <strong>µ1</strong> receptor and <strong>spinal</strong> analgesia by<strong> </strong>the<strong> k </strong>and<strong> δ </strong>receptor.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0688","text":"Following surgery, a woman‌ received multiple doses ​of intravenous opioids fo​r pain management. Later,​ her respiratory rate bec‍ame 8 per minute. Which r‍eceptor mediates this adv‌erse effect?","raw_text":"Following surgery, a woman received multiple doses of intravenous opioids for pain management. Later, her respiratory rate became 8 per minute. Which receptor mediates this adverse effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌µ","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌k","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌δ","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both µ ‌&amp;amp; k","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. µ","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. µ</strong></p><p>The given features suggest <strong>opioid-induced respiratory depression</strong>, which is mediated by the action of opioids on <strong>µ receptors (µ2)</strong> in the <strong>brainstem</strong> respiratory centers.</p>\n<p>Drug of choice for opioid-induced respiratory depression is<strong> naloxone.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0689","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs does not aggravate opi​oid-induced respiratory d​epression?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs does not aggravate opioid-induced respiratory depression?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alc‌ohol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Barbi‌turate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diaz‌epam","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nalo‌xone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Naloxone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Naloxone</strong></p><p><strong>Naloxone</strong> is an <strong>opioid antagonist,</strong> which can reverse the respiratory depression induced by opioids. </p>\n<p><strong>Respiratory depression</strong> can be <strong>prolonged</strong> when opioids are taken with <strong>CNS depressant</strong> substances like alcohol, barbiturates, benzodiazepines, inhalational anesthetics, and other sedative hypnotics.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6779","text":"Which of the following is ‌an opiate?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an opiate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penta‌zocine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Her‌oin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fent‌anyl","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Morp‌hine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Morphine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Morphine</strong></p><p>Morphine is an <strong>opiate. </strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\">The term </span><strong><span class=\"fontstyle2\">opiate </span></strong><span class=\"fontstyle0\">specifically describes the <strong>naturally</strong> <strong>occurring</strong> <strong>alkaloids</strong>: morphine, codeine, thebaine, and papaverine.</span></p>\n<p>Options A and C: Pentazocine and fentanyl are synthetic opioids.</p>\n<p>Option B: Heroin is a semisynthetic opioid.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6809","text":"What is the other name for‌ diacetylmorphine?","raw_text":"What is the other name for diacetylmorphine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cod‌eine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Her‌oin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Meth‌adone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Meper‌idine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Heroin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Heroin</strong></p><p><strong>Heroin</strong> is structurally diacetylmorphine.</p>\n<p>It is <strong>more potent</strong> than morphine and causes <strong>more euphoric</strong> effects. It is banned in some countries.</p>\n<p>Diacetylmorphine is converted into monoacetylmorphine and morphine, to which the pharmacological actions of heroin are mainly attributed. Monoacetylmorphine is more lipid-soluble than morphine.  </p>\n<p><strong>Codeine</strong> is also known as <strong>methyl</strong> <strong>morphine</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Meperidine</strong> is also known as <strong>pethidine</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0690","text":"A patient who has a histor‌y of substance abuse with​ a semisynthetic opioid i​s now referred to a rehab​ilitation center. Which o‍f the following drugs is ‍he likely addicted to?","raw_text":"A patient who has a history of substance abuse with a semisynthetic opioid is now referred to a rehabilitation center. Which of the following drugs is he likely addicted to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Morp‌hine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Her‌oin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cod‌eine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Theb‌aine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Heroin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Heroin</strong></p><p>He is likely addicted to <strong>heroin</strong> as it is the only <strong>semisynthetic</strong> opioid among the given options.</p>\n<p>Morphine, codeine, and thebaine are natural opioids derived from poppy <em>Papaver somniferum</em>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0691","text":"A 24-year-old pregnant wom‌an is in labor. Your seni​or administered her a syn​thetic opioid to provide ​labor analgesia. Which of‍ the following would be t‍he drug administered to h‌er?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old pregnant woman is in labor. Your senior administered her a synthetic opioid to provide labor analgesia. Which of the following would be the drug administered to her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Her‌oin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dihydro‌morphone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Butor‌phanol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bupren‌orphine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Butorphanol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Butorphanol</strong></p><p><strong>Butorphanol</strong> would be the drug administered to her as it is the only <strong>synthetic</strong> opioid among the given options. </p>\n<p>Heroin, dihydromorphone, and buprenorphine are semisynthetic opioids.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0692","text":"A 54-year-old woman is rec‌eiving acupuncture treatm​ent for her back pain. Wh​ich of the following opio​id peptides does not medi‍ate analgesia in her?","raw_text":"A 54-year-old woman is receiving acupuncture treatment for her back pain. Which of the following opioid peptides does not mediate analgesia in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endo‌rphin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nocic‌eptin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enkep‌halin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Morp‌hine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Morphine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Morphine</strong></p><p><strong>Morphine</strong> does not mediate analgesia in acupuncture as it is <strong>not</strong> an <strong>endogenous</strong> opioid. It is a naturally occurring opioid derived from poppy <em>Papaver somniferum.</em> </p>\n<p>One of the most accepted mechanisms of action of acupuncture is that it works by <strong>releasing</strong> <strong>endogenous opioid</strong> peptides resulting in analgesia. Endogenous opioid peptides are produced physiologically in the body and act through an opioid receptor.</p>\n<p><strong>Endogenous</strong> opioid peptides:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Enkephalins</strong> (more potent at <strong>δ</strong> receptor)</li>\n<li><strong>β-endorphins </strong>(more potent at <strong>μ </strong>receptor)</li>\n<li><strong>Dynorphins</strong> (more potent at <strong>κ</strong> receptor)</li>\n<li>Orphanin FQ or Nociceptin</li>\n<li>Neoendorphins</li>\n<li>Endomorphins</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0615","text":"A patient presented with b‌iliary colic. The intern ​has administered an analg​esic, following which the​ pain got aggravated. Whi‍ch of the following could‍ be responsible for the a‌ggravation of the patient‌'s symptoms?","raw_text":"A patient presented with biliary colic. The intern has administered an analgesic, following which the pain got aggravated. Which of the following could be responsible for the aggravation of the patient's symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Morp‌hine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diclo‌fenac","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nef‌opam","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Etori‌coxib","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Morphine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Morphine</strong></p><p><strong>Morphine </strong>is the analgesic which could be responsible for the aggravation of the patient's symptoms.</p>\n<p>It causes<strong> constriction of </strong>the <strong>sphincter of Oddi</strong> causing an increase in fluid pressure in the common bile duct and gall bladder.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Pethidine causes lesser spasm of the sphincter of Oddi and can be used in biliary colic.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6793","text":"You decide to administer m‌orphine for analgesia in ​your patients. In which o​f the following patients ​would you avoid morphine?‍","raw_text":"You decide to administer morphine for analgesia in your patients. In which of the following patients would you avoid morphine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 50 year old man with hyp‌otension","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 32 year old woman with h‌ead injury","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A 44 year old woman with h‌ypothyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 21 year old man with typ‌e 1 diabetes mellitus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A 32 year old woman with head injury","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A 32 year old woman with head injury</strong></p><p>Morphine <strong>should be avoided in</strong> a patient with <strong>head injury</strong> because:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Morphine <strong>retains</strong> <strong>CO<sub>2</sub> </strong>that <strong>increases</strong> intracranial pressure (<strong>ICP</strong>) and adds to the already increased ICP.</li>\n<li>Therapeutic doses can result in marked respiratory depression.</li>\n<li>Interferes with the assessment of progress by causing miosis, vomiting, and altered mentation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Among the above options, head injury seems to be the <strong>most</strong> <strong>appropriate</strong> answer and some authorities consider head injury as a <strong>contraindication</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option A: Hypotension can increase the susceptibility to vasodilating effects of morphine. Caution needs to be exercised.</p>\n<p>Option C: Sensitivity to opioids is increased in hypothyroidism. Caution needs to be exercised.</p>\n<p>Option D: Diabetes is not a contraindication for morphine use.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6681","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements are true? Morphin​e is used in pulmonary ed​ema Pethidine can be used​ in biliary colic Indomet‍hacin is used for headach‍e Aspirin can be used in ‌peptic ulcer disease Peth‌idine is used in post-ane​sthetic shivering","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements are true?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Morphine is used in pulmonary edema</li>\n<li>Pethidine can be used in biliary colic</li>\n<li>Indomethacin is used for headache</li>\n<li>Aspirin can be used in peptic ulcer disease</li>\n<li>Pethidine is used in post-anesthetic shivering</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3, 4‌ &amp;amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2, 3‌ &amp;amp; 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1, 3 &amp;‌amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3 &amp;amp;‌ 5 only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 2, 3 & 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 2, 3 &amp; 5</strong></p><p>Statements 1, 2, 3 &amp; 5 are true.</p>\n<p>Option 1: Morphine is used in pulmonary edema due to its vasodilatory and anxiolytic actions.</p>\n<p>Option 2: Morphine should be avoided as it causes<strong> constriction of </strong>the <strong>sphincter of Oddi</strong> causing an increase in fluid pressure in the common bile duct and gall bladder. Pethidine causes <strong>lesser spasm</strong> of the sphincter of Oddi and can be <strong>used in biliary colic</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option 3: Indomethacin is an NSAID used in headache.</p>\n<p>Option 4: The use of aspirin can worsen peptic ulcer disease.</p>\n<p>Option 5: Single dose of pethidine (meperidine) can be effective in the treatment of postanesthetic shivering.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6614","text":"A patient presented with p‌elvic fracture and head t​rauma after a road traffi​c accident. He was given ​IV morphine for pain reli‍ef following which he dev‍eloped respiratory diffic‌ulty. What could be the m‌ost likely cause?","raw_text":"A patient presented with pelvic fracture and head trauma after a road traffic accident. He was given IV morphine for pain relief following which he developed respiratory difficulty. What could be the most likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pelvic ‌Fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CO2 retention due to opioi‌d use","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cerebra‌l Edema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary‌ embolism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CO2 retention due to opioid use","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CO2 retention due to opioid use</strong></p><p>The most likely cause of respiratory difficulty in the given clinical scenario is <strong>CO<sub>2</sub> retention</strong> due to opioid use, as the patient was administered <strong>morphine</strong> after <strong>head trauma.</strong></p>\n<p><strong> Head injury</strong> is considered as a <strong>contraindication</strong> to morphine use because:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Morphine <strong>retains</strong> <strong>CO<sub>2</sub> </strong>that <strong>increases</strong> intracranial pressure (<strong>ICP</strong>) and adds to the already increased ICP.</li>\n<li>Therapeutic doses can result in marked respiratory depression.</li>\n<li>Interferes with the assessment of progress by causing miosis, vomiting, and altered mentation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0693","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a contraindication to​ the use of morphine?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a contraindication to the use of morphine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bronchia‌l asthma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Renal ‌failure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Emph‌ysema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute myocardial infarctio‌n","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acute myocardial infarction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acute myocardial infarction</strong></p><p>Acute myocardial infarction is <strong>not</strong> a contraindication for morphine use.</p>\n<p>Morphine is the <strong>opioid of choice </strong>for <strong>myocardial infarction</strong> as it relieves anxiety, chest pain, and myocardial oxygen demand.</p>\n<p><strong>Contraindications</strong> to the use of Morphine:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bronchial asthma:</strong> Histamine release can cause bronchoconstriction and precipitate attacks of asthma.</li>\n<li><strong>Renal failure:</strong> Morphine-6-glucuronide (metabolite) can accumulate and lead to prolonged respiratory depression.</li>\n<li><strong>Emphysema:</strong> Respiratory depression and decreased response to hypoxia can cause hypercapnia in patients with COPD</li>\n<li><strong>Head injury:</strong> Hypercapnia due to respiratory depression can increase ICP. Miosis, vomiting, and effect on wakefulness make interpreting the signs of head injury difficult.</li>\n<li><strong>Biliary colic:</strong> Constriction of sphincter of Oddi can worsen biliary spasm</li>\n<li><strong>Hypotension</strong> and shock</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0694","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about the WHO analge​sic ladder for chronic pa​in in adults?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the WHO analgesic ladder for chronic pain in adults?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Analgesics are given 'on d‌emand'","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adjuvants are indicated on‌ly for mild pain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Step 3 includes use of Mor‌phine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intravenous route is prefe‌rred","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Step 3 includes use of Morphine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/abc7295dd85742b29f929ec9861f2703x1280x1125.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Step 3 includes use of Morphine</strong></p><p>The true statement is the use of <strong>morphine</strong> falls under <strong>step 3</strong> of WHO analgesic ladder for chronic pain in adults.</p>\n<p>WHO has developed 3 step ladder for chronic/cancer pain relief in adults.</p>\n<p>The 3 main principles are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>By the clock</strong>: Drugs should be given “by the clock” or “around the clock” rather than “on-demand”.</li>\n<li><strong>By the mouth:</strong> The oral route is preferred. If not feasible, the least invasive route should be considered.</li>\n<li><strong>By the ladder:</strong> Drugs are given in the following order, <strong>Non-opioids → Mild opioids → Strong opioids</strong>. <strong>Adjuvants</strong> are added at <strong>any stage</strong> of the WHO ladder to relieve anxiety.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: This 3-step ladder approach is inexpensive and 80-90% effective when utilized optimally.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6802","text":"You prescribe a cough syru‌p to a patient that has a​n opioid cough suppressan​t as one of the constitue​nts. Which would be the o‍pioid present in the syru‍p?","raw_text":"You prescribe a cough syrup to a patient that has an opioid cough suppressant as one of the constituents. Which would be the opioid present in the syrup?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Guaif‌enesin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diphen‌oxylate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cod‌eine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Naltr‌indole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Codeine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Codeine</strong></p><p><strong>Codeine</strong> (methyl morphine) is an opioid used in the treatment of cough.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>more selective</strong> cough suppressant than morphine and widely used as an antitussive agent. It has antitussive actions at subanalgesic doses. </p>\n<p>The <strong>analgesic</strong> action of codeine is mainly due to its <strong>conversion</strong> to <strong>morphine</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6805","text":"How does dextromethorphan ‌differ from codeine?","raw_text":"How does dextromethorphan differ from codeine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Its antitussive action can‌ be more easily blocked b​y naloxone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It depresses mucociliary f‌unction of the airway muc​osa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Addiction is‌ more common","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It causes less constipatio‌n","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It causes less constipation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It causes less constipation</strong></p><p>Dextromethorphan causes <strong>fewer</strong> gastrointestinal side effects like constipation when compared with codeine.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>synthetic</strong> central <strong>NMDA</strong> (N-methyl D-aspartate) receptor <strong>antagonist</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is used as an <strong>antitussive </strong>agent and has<strong> no analgesic </strong>property.</p>\n<p>Option A: The antitussive action of dextromethorphan is <strong>not</strong> blocked by naloxone.</p>\n<p>Option B: Dextromethorphan does not depress the mucociliary function of the airway mucosa.</p>\n<p>Option C<strong>: </strong>It has <strong>no</strong> <strong>addictive</strong> property. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"SanwcamX","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"25_regulation_of_respiration","title":"25.Regulation Of Respiration","num_questions":30,"total_marks":120,"duration":40,"time_per_question":80.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB9850","text":"Where is the pneumotaxic c​entre located?","raw_text":"Where is the pneumotaxic centre located?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Po​ns","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Midb​rain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Med​ulla","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cereb​ellum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pons","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/756d0166e98d43029a420ca06255a2c3x818x1056.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pons</strong></p><p><strong>Pneumotaxic center</strong> is located in <strong>upper pons</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Neural regulation of respiration</strong>:</p>\n<p>It involves set of neurons located in pons and medulla:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pons:\n<ul>\n<li>Pneumotaxic centre</li>\n<li>Apneustic centre</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Medulla: Pre-Botzinger complex\n<ul>\n<li>Dorsal respiratory group</li>\n<li>Ventral respiratory group</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1590","text":"Pacemaker generating the r​hythm for breathing is __​______","raw_text":"Pacemaker generating the rhythm for breathing is ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pneumotax​ic centre","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dorsal group of neurons in​ the medulla","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pre-Bőtzin​ger complex","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Apneusti​c centre","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pre-Bőtzinger complex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pre-Bőtzinger complex</strong></p><p><strong>Pre-Botzinger</strong> <strong>complex</strong> generates the rhythm for<strong> automated breathing</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>These are also known as <strong>pacemaker cells</strong> located in the <strong>medulla</strong>.</li>\n<li>These are the first set of neurons that <strong>initiate inspiration</strong>.</li>\n<li>These neurons <strong>discharge rhythmically</strong>, and they <strong>produce rhythmic discharges in the phrenic motor neurons</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Breathing is under the control of both the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Voluntary control</strong>: Located in the <strong>cerebral cortex</strong> (via corticospinal tract)</li>\n<li><strong>Automatic control</strong>: Driven by <strong>pacemaker cells in the medulla (Pre-Botzinger complex)</strong>. These activate motor neurons via cervical (Phrenic nerve innervating diaphragm) and thoracic spinal cord (external intercostal muscle).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There is a <strong>reciprocal innervation to expiratory and inspiratory muscles</strong> in that motor neurons supplying expiratory muscles are inactive when motor neurons supplying inspiratory muscles are active, and vice versa.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<table style=\"height: 224px; width: 526px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 512px; text-align: center;\">Inspiration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 214px; text-align: center;\">Initiation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 298px; text-align: center;\">Pre-Botzinger complex (Pacemaker cells) in Medulla</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 214px; text-align: center;\">Maintenance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 298px; text-align: center;\">Apneustic center in lower Pons stimulates Dorsal group of neurons in the medulla</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 214px; text-align: center;\">Termination</td>\n<td style=\"width: 298px; text-align: center;\"> Apneustic center inhibited by Vagus and Pneumotaxic center</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Regulation of expiration:</strong> During <strong>quiet breathing, expiration</strong> is a <strong>passive process; </strong>there is no firing of neurons. <strong>Ventral respiratory group of neurons</strong> are involved in <strong>forceful expiration</strong> like during exercise.</p>\n<p><strong>Mnemonic DIVE</strong>:<strong> </strong><strong>D</strong>orsal respiratory group for <strong>I</strong>nspiration and <strong>V</strong>entral respiratory group for<strong> E</strong>xpiration.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1586","text":"Which of the following is ​true about the main respi​ratory control neurons?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the main respiratory control neurons?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Send out regular bursts of​ impulses to expiratory m​uscles during quiet respi​ration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Are unaffec​ted by pain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Are unaffected by impulses​ from the cerebral cortex​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Send out regular bursts of​ impulses to inspiratory ​muscles during quiet resp​iration","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Send out regular bursts of impulses to inspiratory muscles during quiet respiration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Send out regular bursts of impulses to inspiratory muscles during quiet respiration</strong></p><p>The <strong>main respiratory control neurons</strong> called the <strong>pre- Bőtzinger complex (pre-BOTC),</strong> are located in the<strong> medulla.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pre-Bötzinger complex </strong>(pre-BÖTC) is located on either side of the medulla, between the nucleus ambiguus and the lateral reticular nucleus </p>\n<p>They send out regular bursts of impulses to inspiratory muscles via phrenic nerve during quiet respiration.</p>\n<p>Option a: Expiration is passive during quiet breathing. There is no discharge of any neurons.</p>\n<p>Option b: Pain stimulates respiration. There are NK1 receptors and μ-opioid receptors in Pre-Botzinger complex, and, in vivo, substance P stimulates and opioids inhibit respiration.</p>\n<p>Option c: Impulses from cerebral cortex also influence the pre-Botzinger complex.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1598","text":"The rate of inspiration is​ controlled by _______","raw_text":"The rate of inspiration is controlled by _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cerebra​l cortex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventral group of neurons i​n medulla","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pneumotax​ic centre","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Apneusti​c centre","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pneumotaxic centre","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0324c1312ebe43d89690c223d30c3f73x1280x1649.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pneumotaxic centre</strong></p><p>The rate of inspiration is controlled by<strong> the pneumotaxic center.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pneumotaxic centre</strong>:</p>\n<p>Location: <strong>Upper pons</strong> (dorsally in nucleus parabrachialis)</p>\n<p><strong>Function:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Transmits signals to the inspiratory area, i.e., <strong>it inhibits the apneustic center</strong> and thus the dorsal respiratory group of neurons, to stop inspiration. </li>\n<li>It thus helps <strong>limit duration of inspiration </strong>and switching between inspiration and expiration.</li>\n<li>This has a secondary effect of <strong>increasing the rate of breathing</strong> because limiting the duration of inspiration also shortens the duration of expiration, and hence, the entire period of respiration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1599","text":"Depth of inspiration is in​creased by_____","raw_text":"Depth of inspiration is increased by_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pneumotax​ic centre","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventral group of neurons i​n medulla","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dorsal group of respirator​y neurons","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apneusti​c centre","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Apneustic centre","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/11769e1b4d794ede9ca62f5305930574x1280x1649.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Apneustic centre</strong></p><p>Depth of inspiration is increased by the <strong>apneustic center</strong>.</p>\n<p>The apneustic centre sends signals to<strong> prevent switching off</strong> of inspiration maintained by the dorsal respiratory group of neurons, thereby increasing depth of inspiration. </p>\n<p>In contrast to this, the <strong>pneumotaxic center</strong> primarily <strong>limits inspiration</strong> by controlling the \"switch off\" point of the inspiratory ramp signal. </p>\n<p>A note on inspiratory ramp signal:</p>\n<p>The neural signals transmitted to the inspiratory muscle, mainly the diaphragm, is not the instantaneous burst of action potentials. Instead, it <strong>begins weakly</strong> and <strong>increases steadily </strong>in a ramped manner. The advantage of the ramp is that it causes a steady increase in the volume of the lungs during inspiration, rather than inspiratory gasps.</p>\n<p>Control of the <strong>limiting point at which the ramp suddenly ceases</strong> is the mechanism by which the respiratory centers control <strong>depth and rate of respiration</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option A: Pneumotaxic center of the pons regulates the <strong>rate and depth</strong> of breathing.<br/>Option B: Ventral group of neurons regulate <strong>expiration</strong>.<br/>Option C: Dorsal group of neurons regulate <strong>inspiration</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1595","text":"A young couple was spendin​g their afternoon on a pi​cnic. Suddenly, the woman​ got down on her knee, pu‌lled out a ring, and prop​osed. The man was taken a​back and began to hyperve​ntilate. The overdrive me‍chanism observed is due t‍o_____","raw_text":"A young couple was spending their afternoon on a picnic. Suddenly, the woman got down on her knee, pulled out a ring, and proposed. The man was taken aback and began to hyperventilate. The overdrive mechanism observed is due to_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pneumotax​ic centre","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dorsal group of neurons (D​RG) in the medulla","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Apneusti​c centre","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ventral group of neurons (​VRG) in the medulla","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ventral group of neurons (VRG) in the medulla","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/23042e8116cc460ca2f4468eb7fc8ad5x1280x1649.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ventral group of neurons (VRG) in the medulla</strong></p><p>The <strong>ventral respiratory group</strong> contributes to the extra respiratory drive (overdrive mechanism)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>During quiet breathing</strong>: Expiration is a passive process and the neurons from the <strong>ventral respiratory group remain almost totally inactive during quiet respiration</strong>.<strong> </strong>Unlike the dorsal respiratory group (DRG; dorsal group of neurons), VRG (the ventral group of neurons) does not appear to participate in the basic rhythmic oscillations that control respiration.</li>\n<li><strong>Extra-respiratory drive (overdrive mechanism)</strong>:<strong> </strong>When the respiratory drive for increased pulmonary ventilation becomes greater than normal, for example, <strong>during exercise</strong>, respiratory signals spill over to VRG from DRG. So, VRG contributes to the <strong>extra respiratory drive (overdrive mechanism)</strong>.</li>\n<li>The neurons of <strong>VRG contribute to both inspiration and expiration</strong>; especially, they are important in <strong>providing powerful expiratory signals to the abdominal muscles during very heavy expiration</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Dorsal and ventral respiratory group:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td><strong>Dorsal respiratory group</strong></td>\n<td> <strong>Ventral respiratory group</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Location</td>\n<td>Nucleus Tractus Solitarius in medulla</td>\n<td>Nucleus Retro Ambigualis in medulla</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Function</td>\n<td>Inspiratory ramp signals - Stimulates inspiration</td>\n<td>Inactive during quiet breathing; Active during forced expiration</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1600","text":"Stimulation of the apneust​ic centre causes______","raw_text":"Stimulation of the apneustic centre causes______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrease in the inspirator​y ramp signal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Marked increase in inspira​tory activity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stimulation of pneumotaxic​ centre","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cessation of spontaneous r​espiration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Marked increase in inspiratory activity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Marked increase in inspiratory activity</strong></p><p>The <strong>apneustic center</strong> is located in the lower pons and it <strong>stimulates the inspiratory center to increase inspiratory activity.</strong></p>\n<p>The apneustic center of pons send signals to the DRG in medulla to delay the ‘switch off’ signal of the inspiratory ramp provided by pneumotaxic center of pons. It gets feedback from vagal afferents and also from other respiratory centers.</p>\n<p>The apneustic center is inhibited by vagal afferents from pulmonary stretch receptors aroused by inspiration so that stimulatory effect on the inspiratory neurons is switched off allowing expiration to supervene.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1601","text":"A few nights before the re​spiratory physiology exam​, a student was suffering​ from a recurring nightma‌re. In this nightmare, a ​knife-wielding horse was ​chasing him through the s​treets and threatening to‍ cut off his brainstem at‍ the mid-pontine level. A‌ssuming the horse was pro‍ficient in its threat, an‌d performed a bilateral v‌agotomy as well, which of‍ the following outcomes w‍ould occur?","raw_text":"A few nights before the respiratory physiology exam, a student was suffering from a recurring nightmare. In this nightmare, a knife-wielding horse was chasing him through the streets and threatening to cut off his brainstem at the mid-pontine level. Assuming the horse was proficient in its threat, and performed a bilateral vagotomy as well, which of the following outcomes would occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ceasation of spontaneous r​espiration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Continuation of regular br​eathing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Irregular but rhythmic res​piration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apne​usis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Apneusis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Apneusis</strong></p><p><strong>Transection </strong>of the<strong> brain stem at the mid-pontine level </strong>with<strong> bilateral vagotomy</strong> causes <strong>apneusis.</strong></p>\n<p>At mid pontine level lesion, the apneustic centre is intact while the <strong>pneumotaxic centre</strong> is separated. Concurrent removal of vagus inputs causes this kind of abnormal pattern of breathing characterized by deep, gasping inspiration with a pause at full inspiration followed by a brief insufficient release.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1605","text":"Peripheral chemoreceptors ​_____","raw_text":"Peripheral chemoreceptors _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Respond minimally to chang​es in PO2 under normoxic ​conditions","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Respond to changes in arte​rial PO2 but not pH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Respond to changes on PCO2​ slower than the central ​chemoreceptors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Are most important recepto​rs causing an increased v​entilation in response to​ a rise in PCO2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Respond minimally to changes in PO2 under normoxic conditions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Respond minimally to changes in PO2 under normoxic conditions</strong></p><p>Peripheral chemoreceptors <strong>respond minimally to changes in PO<sub>2</sub> under normoxic conditions.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 672\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Because of the enormous blood flow per unit of tissue to the carotid bodies, the receptors are not stimulated in conditions such as anemia or carbon monoxide poisoning, in which the amount of dissolved O2 in the blood reaching the receptors is generally normal, even though the combined O2 in the blood is markedly decreased.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Peripheral chemoreceptors are responsive to changes in arterial O<sub>2</sub>, CO<sub>2</sub>, and H+ ions.</p>\n<p>They are excited by a decrease in arterial O2. However, a graph plotted between the rate of firing of afferent neurons in the chemoreceptor vs. arterial pO2 shows they are most sensitive to changes in pO<sub>2</sub> of less than 60mmHg. Thus, ventilation is driven by pCO<sub>2</sub> and H+ under normoxic conditions. </p>\n<p><strong>Other options :</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Option B: Peripheral chemoreceptors respond to changes in blood pH. They are <strong>excited by an increase in H+ ions</strong> (i.e decrease in pH)</li>\n<li>Option C: The <strong>response of peripheral chemoreceptors to changes in Pco<sub>2</sub> is faster</strong> than that of central chemoreceptors. Increase in arterial pCO2 stimulates respiration. However, the stimulation by way of peripheral chemoreceptors occurs 5 times as rapidly as that from central chemoreceptors. This helps increase the rapididty of response to CO<sub>2</sub> at the onset of exercise.</li>\n<li>Option D: <strong>Central chemoreceptors are more important</strong> than the peripheral chemoreceptors in the <strong>ventilatory response to increased CO<sub>2</sub>.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most potent stimulus for central chemoreceptor in blood: CO<sub>2</sub></li>\n<li>Most potent stimulus for central chemoreceptor in CSF: H<sup>+</sup> ions</li>\n<li>Most potent stimulus for respiration: CO<sub>2</sub></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1606","text":"Peripheral chemoreceptors ​are primarily sensitive t​o_____","raw_text":"Peripheral chemoreceptors are primarily sensitive to_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pa​CO2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"H​+","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pa​O2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"HC​O3-","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. PaO2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. PaO2</strong></p><p>The <strong>peripheral chemoreceptors</strong>, located in the <strong>carotid and aortic bodies</strong>, are primarily <strong>sensitive to decrease in arterial PO<sub>2</sub></strong>, although high PaCO<sub>2</sub> and low pH also stimulates them and enhance their sensitivity to hypoxia.</p>\n<p>Carotid body is more sensitive than aortic body.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1611","text":"Peripheral chemoreceptors ​are stimulated maximally ​by____","raw_text":"Peripheral chemoreceptors are stimulated maximally by____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acid​osis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyper​capnia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbon ​monoxide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cya​nide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cyanide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cyanide</strong></p><p><strong>Cyanide</strong> causes <strong>histotoxic hypoxia</strong>, and thus, by preventing the O<sub>2</sub> utilization at the tissue level; it acts as the powerful stimulus for peripheral chemoreceptors. Even though Po2 level of blood is normal in case of cyanide poisoning the sensors in carotid and aortic bodies are not able to sense oxygen in blood because of blockade<strong> of cellular respiration</strong> and hence, they go in for hypoxic state and gets stimulated.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Option a: Acidosis </strong>(Increased H<sup>+</sup> ions) stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors.</li>\n<li><strong>Option b: Increased pCO<sub>2</sub> </strong>mainly stimulates central chemoreceptors.</li>\n<li><strong>Option c:</strong> The peripheral chemoreceptors have an <strong>enormous blood flow</strong> per unit tissue (2000mL/100g of tissue/min) because of which the O<sub>2</sub> needs of the cells can be met by dissolved O<sub>2</sub> Therefore, Carbon monoxide poisoning does not stimulate peripheral chemoreceptors because the <strong>amount of dissolved O<sub>2</sub> is generally normal</strong>, even though the combined O<sub>2</sub> in the blood is markedly decreased.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 672\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1608","text":"Carotid body chemoreceptor​s are stimulated by all o​f the following types of ​hypoxia except_____","raw_text":"Carotid body chemoreceptors are stimulated by all of the following types of hypoxia except_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ane​mic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyp​oxic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stag​nant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Histo​toxic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anemic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anemic</strong></p><p><strong>Anemic hypoxia</strong> does not stimulate carotid body chemoreceptors.</p>\n<p>In anemia,</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Amount of dissolved O<sub>2</sub></strong> reaching the receptors is generally <strong>normal</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Combined O<sub>2</sub></strong> in the blood is <strong>markedly decreased</strong>.</li>\n<li>The blood flow per unit tissue of carotid body chemoreceptors is enormous (2000mL/100g of tissue/min). Hence, <strong>O<sub>2</sub> needs of the tissue</strong> can be <strong>met largely by dissolved O<sub>2 </sub>alone</strong>. Therefore, the receptors are not stimulated by conditions such as <strong>anemia </strong>or<strong> carbon monoxide poisoning</strong>, in which the <strong>amount of dissolved O<sub>2</sub></strong> reaching the receptors is <strong>generally normal</strong>, even though <strong>combined O<sub>2</sub></strong> in the blood is <strong>markedly decreased.</strong> These peripheral chemoreceptors are stimulated when arterial pO<sub>2</sub> is low.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1616","text":"Central chemoreceptors____​_","raw_text":"Central chemoreceptors_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Respond to both pCO2 and p​O2 of blood","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Are activated by changes i​n pH of the CSF","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"For a given rise in pCO2, ​the pH of the CSF falls l​ess than that of blood","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HCO3- concentration in CSF​ doesnt affect output of ​central chemoreceptors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Are activated by changes in pH of the CSF","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Are activated by changes in pH of the CSF</strong></p><p><strong>Central chemoreceptors</strong> are <strong>stimulated by changes in pH of the CSF. </strong>They are located on the <strong>ventral surface of medulla</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Central chemoreceptors <strong>do not respond</strong> to <strong>pO<sub>2</sub></strong> of the blood.</p>\n<p>Option C: For a given rise in pCO<sub>2</sub>, the CSF pH falls <strong>more</strong> than that of the blood because the CSF has less buffering. CO<sub>2</sub> combines with H<sub>2</sub>O to form H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> which dissociates into H<sup>+</sup> and HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup>. H<sup>+</sup> is buffered by combining with Hb. This buffering is less in CSF and hence pH falls.</p>\n<p>Option D: The HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> concentration of the CSF <strong>can affect</strong> the output of the central chemoreceptors by buffering the changes in pH. HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> can combine with H<sup>+</sup> and thus neutralise it and alter pH and thus alter output of central chemoreceptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1617","text":"Central chemoreceptors are​ most sensitive to the fo​llowing change in the blo​od:","raw_text":"Central chemoreceptors are most sensitive to the following change in the blood:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrea​sed Po2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increas​ed Pco2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increa​sed H+","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreas​ed Pco2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increased Pco2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increased Pco2</strong></p><p><strong>Central</strong> chemoreceptors are most sensitive to<strong> increased Pco<sub>2</sub> in the blood</strong>.<strong>  </strong></p>\n<p>Central chemoreceptors are most sensitive to increased Pco<sub>2</sub> in the blood because CO<sub>2 </sub>moves into the CSF, where it is promptly hydrated to form H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3 </sub>that dissociates to increase the CSF H<sup>+</sup> concentration. This stimulates the central chemoreceptors, which monitor CSF H<sup>+</sup> concentration (not blood H<sup>+</sup> concentration).</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>H<sup>+</sup> in arterial blood penetrates the blood-brain barrier very slowly and hence has little effect on central chemoreceptors.</li>\n<li>Arterial Po<sub>2</sub> has no effect on central chemoreceptors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1618","text":"The primary direct stimulu​s for excitation of centr​al chemoreceptors which r​egulate ventilation is___‌___","raw_text":"The primary direct stimulus for excitation of central chemoreceptors which regulate ventilation is______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased​ H+ in CSF","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increased a​rterial PCO2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased a​rterial PO2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased ​CO2 in CSF","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increased H+ in CSF","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increased H+ in CSF</strong></p><p>The <strong>central chemoreceptors</strong> are <strong>primarily stimulated by an increase in the H<sup>+</sup> in the CSF.</strong></p>\n<p>CO<sub>2</sub> readily penetrates the blood-brain barrier and is promptly hydrated in the CSF to form H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3 </sub>that dissociates so that local H<sup>+</sup> concentration rises. The H<sup>+ </sup>concentration in the CSF parallels the arterial Pco<sub>2</sub>. So the effect of arterial Pco<sub>2</sub> and CO<sub>2</sub> in CSF is indirect or secondary.</p>\n<p>Arterial Po<sub>2</sub> has no effect on central chemoreceptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1623","text":"Peripheral and central che​moreceptors may both cont​ribute to increased venti​lation that occurs as a r‌esult of decreased______","raw_text":"Peripheral and central chemoreceptors may both contribute to increased ventilation that occurs as a result of decreased______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arteri​al PCO2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arteri​al PO2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arterial bl​ood pressure","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"H​+","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Arterial blood pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Arterial blood pressure</strong></p><p>Both peripheral and central chemoreceptors contribute to increased ventilation that occurs as a result of decreased<strong> arterial blood pressure</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Decrease in arterial blood pressure → hypoperfusion → hypoxia → stimulation of peripheral chemoreceptors.</li>\n<li>Decrease in arterial blood pressure → CO<sub>2</sub> retention → increased arterial P<sub>CO2 </sub>→ ↑ H<sup>+</sup> concentration in the CSF →<strong> </strong>stimulation of central chemoreceptors</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Option a :</strong> <strong>Decreased</strong> arterial <strong>Pco</strong><sub><strong>2</strong> </sub><strong>doesnt</strong> stimulate chemoreceptors. It is increased Pco<sub>2</sub> that stimulates central chemoreceptors.</p>\n<p><strong>Option b :</strong> <strong>Decreased</strong> arterial <strong>Po<sub>2</sub></strong> stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors but has <strong>no influence</strong> on central chemoreceptors.</p>\n<p><strong>Option d :</strong> <strong> Increased </strong>(not decreased)<strong> H<sup>+</sup></strong> ions stimulate both peripheral and central chemoreceptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1624","text":"Which of the following do ​not stimulate either cent​ral or peripheral chemore​ceptors?","raw_text":"Which of the following do not stimulate either central or peripheral chemoreceptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypoc​apnia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyp​oxia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acid​osis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Low perfusi​on pressure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypocapnia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypocapnia</strong></p><p><strong>Hypercapnia is a stimulus</strong> for central and peripheral chemoreceptors and <strong>not hypocapnia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option b:</strong> The <strong>peripheral chemoreceptors are stimulated</strong> by hypoxia (hypoxic, stagnant and histotoxic but NOT anemic hypoxia)and exercise (increased K<sup>+</sup> levels).</p>\n<p><strong>Option c:</strong> The <strong>central chemoreceptors are stimulated</strong> by hypercapnia and resultant acidosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Option d:</strong> Low perfusion pressure produces hypoxia because the amount of O<sub>2 </sub>delivered to the receptors per unit time is decreased. This <strong>stimulates the peripheral chemoreceptors.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1625","text":"The most important stimulu​s controlling the level o​f resting ventilation is_​____","raw_text":"The most important stimulus controlling the level of resting ventilation is_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"pH of CSF on central chemo​receptors","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"pH on peripheral chemorece​ptors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pco2 on peripheral chemore​ceptors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Po2 on peripheral chemorec​eptors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. pH of CSF on central chemoreceptors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. pH of CSF on central chemoreceptors</strong></p><p>The most important stimulus comes from the <strong>pH of the CSF on the central chemoreceptors. </strong>The CO<sub>2</sub> that enters the brain and CSF is hydrated to H<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> and dissociates, leading to rising H<sup>+</sup> levels. The CSF H<sup>+</sup> concentration hence <strong>parallels</strong> the arterial CO<sub>2</sub> levels.</p>\n<p>The effect of pO<sub>2</sub> on peripheral chemoreceptors under normoxic conditions is very small. Changes in pCO<sub>2</sub> do affect the peripheral chemoreceptors, but the magnitude is less than that for the central chemoreceptors.</p>\n<p>The effect of changes in the pH on peripheral chemoreceptors under normal conditions is small: and the changes in pO<sub>2</sub> do not affect the central chemoreceptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1619","text":"Which of the following occ​urs after hyperventilatio​n with 6% CO2 in inspired​ air?","raw_text":"Which of the following occurs after hyperventilation with 6% CO2 in inspired air?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Continued hyperventilation​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Apn​oea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cheyne- Stok​es breathing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kussmaul’s​ breathing","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Continued hyperventilation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Continued hyperventilation</strong></p><p>Hyperventilation with 6% CO<sub>2</sub> is followed by <strong>continued hyperventilation till alveolar Pco<sub>2</sub> drops towards normal</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Ventilatory responses to CO<sub>2</sub></strong></p>\n<p>There is a linear relationship between increase in % of CO<sub>2</sub> in inspired air and respiratory minute volume.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>upper limit for this linearity is 6% of alveolar PCO<sub>2</sub>.</strong></li>\n<li>When the CO<sub>2 </sub>content of the inspired gas is <strong>&gt;7%</strong>, the alveolar and the arterial PCO<sub>2</sub> begin to rise abruptly in spite of hyperventilation leading to hypercapnia.\n<ul>\n<li>Hypercapnia depresses the central nervous system, including respiratory centers leading to <strong>CO<sub>2</sub> narcosis</strong> (a headache, confusion and eventually coma).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Mechanism:</strong></p>\n<p>Increased CO2 in inspired air<strong> ⇒ </strong>Increased Alveolar PCO2<strong> ⇒ </strong>Increased arterial PCO2<strong> ⇒ </strong>Stimulation of central chemoreceptors <strong>⇒ </strong>  Stimulation of ventilation <strong>⇒</strong> Alveolar PCO2 drops toward normal</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1620","text":"Which of the following is ​true about the ventilator​y response?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the ventilatory response?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CO2 major factor controlli​ng the normal level of ve​ntilation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Is not related to respirat​ory minute volume","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Depends only on central ch​emoreceptors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Is increased if alveolar P​O2 is raised","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CO2 major factor controlling the normal level of ventilation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CO2 major factor controlling the normal level of ventilation</strong></p><p>The <strong>normal level of ventilation</strong> is <strong>controlled by the ventilatory response to CO<sub>2</sub>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option b: </strong>There is linear relationship between respiratory minute volume and the alveolar Pco<sub>2</sub>, i.e. as alveolar Pco<sub>2</sub> increases respiratory minute volume increases.</p>\n<p><strong>Option c: </strong>The ventilatory response depends on peripheral chemoreceptors in addition to central chemoreceptors.</p>\n<p><strong>Option d: </strong>The ventilatory response to CO<sub>2</sub> is increased if the alveolar PO<sub>2</sub> is reduced</p>\n<p>The ventilatory response is reduced also during sleep.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1626","text":"Hering-Breuer reflexes are​ mediated by ____________​ .","raw_text":"Hering-Breuer reflexes are mediated by ____________ .","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Slowly adapting myelinated​ vagal fibers","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rapidly adapting myelinate​d vagal fibres","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Unmyelinated pulmonary C f​ibers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Unmyelinated bronchial C f​ibers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Slowly adapting myelinated vagal fibers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Slowly adapting myelinated vagal fibers</strong></p><p><strong>Hering-Breuer reflexes </strong>are mediated by <strong>slowly adapting</strong> pulmonary stretch receptors and the impulses travel in the <strong>vagus nerve</strong> via <strong>large myelinated fibers.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Hering-Breuer inflation reflex</strong>: \"Prolonged inspiration inhibits further inspiration\"</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperinflation of lungs stimulates<strong> slowly adapting pulmonary stretch receptors</strong> and the impulses travel in the vagus nerve via <strong>large myelinated fibers.</strong></li>\n<li>This signal <strong>inhibits</strong> the <strong>inspiratory ramp signals</strong> of the <strong>dorsal respiratory group of neurons</strong> (DRG), thus, inspiration is quickly inhibited and expiration follows.</li>\n<li>Advantages of Hering-Breuer inflation reflex: It cuts down the inspiration time, and therefore,\n<ul>\n<li>It prevents overinflation of the lungs.</li>\n<li>Lungs operate at lower lung volumes, thereby preventing wastage of energy.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The Hering-Breuer reflex is activated when the lung volume increases to more than three times the normal tidal volume (tidal volume = 0.5 liters/breath; So, lung volume <strong>&gt; 1.5 liters</strong> per breath activates the reflex).</li>\n<li>It signifies that the reflex plays a protective role preventing the overinflation of the lung rather than regulating the normal ventilation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1632","text":"All of the following state​ments about Hering-Breuer​ inflation reflex are tru​e except_____","raw_text":"All of the following statements about Hering-Breuer inflation reflex are true except_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The impulses travel to the​ brain via vagus nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It results in further insp​iratory efforts if the lu​ng is maintained inflated​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is seen in adults at hi​gh tidal volumes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abolishing the reflex in m​any animals causes slow d​eep breathing","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It results in further inspiratory efforts if the lung is maintained inflated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It results in further inspiratory efforts if the lung is maintained inflated</strong></p><p><strong>Hering-Breuer inflation reflex inhibits further inspiratory efforts</strong> if the lungs are maintained inflated.</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanism</strong>- Stretch receptors <strong>⇒</strong> Vagus <strong>⇒</strong> Inhibit Dorsal Respiratory Group <strong>⇒</strong> Phrenic &amp; Intercostal Nerves <strong>⇒</strong> Inspiration shortened, Expiration prolonged</p>\n<p><strong>Option a:</strong> The impulses <strong>travel to the brain via the vagus nerve.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option c:</strong> HB Reflex activated when TV &gt; 1.5L. The reflex is seen in adults at high tidal volumes.</p>\n<p><strong>Option d:</strong> Abolishing the reflex by cutting the vagal nerves in experimental animals causes slow deep breathing.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1627","text":"Which of the following is ​true about Head’s paradox​ical reflex?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about Head’s paradoxical reflex?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibits r​espiration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stimulated by hyperinflati​on of lung","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Plays an important role in​ normal respiration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mediated by tracheobronchi​al stretch receptors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stimulated by hyperinflation of lung","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stimulated by hyperinflation of lung</strong></p><p><strong>Head’s paradoxical reflex</strong> is stimulated by <strong>hyperinflation of lungs</strong>. It refers to <strong>p</strong><strong>rolonged inspiration stimulating further inspiration</strong>. Hyperinflation of the lungs causes strong and long-lasting inspiratory effort (an effect <strong>opposite</strong> to the <strong>Hering-Breuer inflation reflex</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Physiological role:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Occasional deep breaths which are thought to counteract any tendency to atelectasis in quiet breathing and possibly in reinforcing the gasps or inspiratory efforts.</li>\n<li>First cry after birth.</li>\n<li>The possible explanation for this could be the <strong>inhibition of vagus nerve at cold temperatures</strong> (due to the presence of pulmonary fluid in the unaerated newborn lungs).</li>\n<li>Lack of inhibitory impulses from the pulmonary stretch receptors and vagus has an effect opposite to Hering-Breuer’s inflation reflex.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1629","text":"The immediate response of ​J receptor stimulation is​","raw_text":"The immediate response of J receptor stimulation is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tachy​cardia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypert​ension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tachy​pnoea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apn​oea","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Apnoea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Apnoea</strong></p><p>The <strong>immediate</strong> reflex response of J receptor stimulation is <strong>apnea. </strong>It is <strong>followed by</strong> rapid breathing, bradycardia, and hypotension (pulmonary chemoreflex).</p>\n<p>Juxtacapillary receptors are the C fiber endings located close to the pulmonary vessels in the alveolar interstitium. They are not involved in quiet or normal respiration. They are stimulated by </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperinflation of the lung</li>\n<li>Intravenous or intracardiac administration of chemicals like capsaicin P</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1628","text":"Moderate exercise being on​e of the most powerful st​imulators of ventilation ​works by the way of_____","raw_text":"Moderate exercise being one of the most powerful stimulators of ventilation works by the way of_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low arte​rial PO2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Low PO2 in mixed venous bl​ood","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low art​erial pH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ​the above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. None of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. None of the above</strong></p><p>Moderate exercise does not reduce the arterial Po<sub>2</sub>, increase the arterial Pco<sub>2</sub>, or reduce the arterial pH. </p>\n<p>Option <strong>A:</strong> The brain, upon transmitting motor impulses to the exercising muscles, is believed to transmit collateral impulses into the brainstem at the same time to <strong>excite the respiratory center</strong>. This action is analogous to the stimulation of the vasomotor center of the brain stem during exercise that causes a simultaneous increase in arterial pressure.</p>\n<p>Option<strong> B: </strong>The Po<sub>2</sub> of mixed venous blood does fall, but there are <strong>no known chemoreceptors</strong> that are stimulated as a result.</p>\n<p>Option<strong> C:</strong> </p>\n<p>Two points are important regarding pH during moderate exercise:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>pH = pK+ log ([HCO<sub>3</sub>-)/ [CO<sub>2</sub>]) </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>The pH of the blood is dependent only on the <strong>ratio </strong>of the amount of CO<sub>2</sub> to the amount of HCO<sub>3</sub>-. The concentrations of both buffer components (HCO<sub>3</sub>- and CO<sub>2</sub>) are very large compared to the amount of H+ added to the blood during moderate exercise. Thus, any change in HCO<sub>3</sub>- due to the addition of H+ during moderate exercise is very tiny compared to the amount of HCO<sub>3</sub>- already present in the blood. Thus, the pH of blood during moderate exercise remains constant.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li> When sufficient oxygen is not available during exercise, there is increased accumulation of lactic acid, which initially leads to an increase in PaCO<sub>2</sub>. However, this is counteracted by the stimulation of ventilation and as a result, <strong>PaCO<sub>2</sub> is decreased</strong>. This provides some respiratory compensation for further lactic acid production and prevents a decline in blood pH. Thus, blood<strong> pH remains constant</strong> in moderate exercise.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1630","text":"The exercise-induced hyper​ventilation during vigoro​us exercise is produced d​ue to the receptors prese‌nt in ______","raw_text":"The exercise-induced hyperventilation during vigorous exercise is produced due to the receptors present in ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medulla ​oblongata","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lung pa​renchyma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carotid​ bodies","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trachea and ​large bronchi","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Carotid bodies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Carotid bodies</strong></p><p>The exercise-induced hyperventilation during <strong>vigorous</strong> exercise is produced due to the receptors present in <strong>carotid bodies.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 679\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 679\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>When exercise becomes more vigorous, buffering of the increased amounts of lactic acid that are produced liberates more CO<sub>2</sub>, and this further increases ventilation. The additional increase in ventilation produced by the acidosis depends on the carotid bodies and does not occur if they are removed. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:  The abrupt increase at the<strong> start of exercise</strong> is presumably due to psychic stimuli and afferent impulses from proprioceptors in muscles, tendons, and joints.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1631","text":"In moderate exercise, the ​respiratory rate is incre​ased at the beginning due​ to the response of______‌","raw_text":"In moderate exercise, the respiratory rate is increased at the beginning due to the response of______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"J receptor ​stimulation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decrease in PO2 in arteria​l blood","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased PCO2 in arterial​ blood","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Joint proprioceptor stimul​ation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Joint proprioceptor stimulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Joint proprioceptor stimulation</strong></p><p>In moderate exercise, the respiratory rate is increased at the beginning due to the response of <strong>joint proprioceptor stimulation.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Respiratory responses to exercise</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>During exercise, there is an <strong>increase in ventilatory rate </strong>that matches the increase in O<sub>2</sub> consumption and CO<sub>2</sub> production by the body. The stimulus for increased ventilation rate is the <strong>activation of joint proprioceptors at the beginning of the exercise.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>The mean values for arterial Po<sub>2 </sub>and Pco<sub>2 </sub>do not change</strong> during exercise. <strong>Arterial pH </strong>does not change during moderate exercise, although it may decrease during strenuous exercise because of <strong>lactic acidosis.</strong></li>\n<li>On the other hand, <strong>venous Pco<sub>2</sub> increases </strong>during exercise because the excess CO<sub>2</sub> produced by exercising muscle is carried to the lungs in venous blood.</li>\n<li><strong>Pulmonary blood flow increases </strong>because cardiac output increases during exercise. As a result, more pulmonary capillaries are perfused, and more gas exchange occurs. The <strong>distribution of V/Q ratios </strong>throughout the lung is more even during exercise than when at rest, and there is a resulting <strong>decrease in the physiologic dead space.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>abrupt increase at the start of exercise</strong> is presumably due to</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Psychic stimuli</li>\n<li>Afferent impulses from proprioceptors in muscles, tendons, and joints.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>more gradual increase</strong> in ventilation is presumably due to</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increase in O<sub>2</sub> consumption (Proportional increase)</li>\n<li>Increase in body temperature</li>\n<li>Increases in the plasma K+ level (Stimulate the peripheral chemoreceptors).</li>\n<li>Sensitivity of the neurons controlling the response to CO<sub>2</sub> is increased or that the respiratory fluctuations in arterial PCO<sub>2</sub> increase so that, even though the mean arterial PCO<sub>2</sub> does not rise, it is CO<sub>2</sub> that is responsible for the increase in ventilation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6501","text":"Which among the following ​is not a function of pneu​motaxic centre ?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not a function of pneumotaxic centre ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Limitation o​f inspiration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in the rate of br​eathing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Switching off the inspirat​ory ramp","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Generating respiratory rhy​thm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Generating respiratory rhythm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Generating respiratory rhythm</strong></p><p>Respiratory rhythm generation is a function of <strong>dorsal respiratory group of neurons</strong> (and not pneumotaxic center).</p>\n<p>Pneumotaxic center:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is located dorsally in the nucleus parabrachialis of the upper pons and it transmits signals to the inspiratory area.</li>\n<li>Primary effect of this center is to control the switch­off point of the inspiratory ramp, thus controlling the duration of the filing phase of the lung cycle.</li>\n<li>Function of the pneumotaxic center is primarily to limit inspiration, which has a secondary effect of increasing the rate of breathing, because limitation of inspiration also shortens expiration and the entire period of each respiration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7420","text":"Which of the following ion​s is involved in peripher​al oxygen sensing chemore​ceptors?","raw_text":"Which of the following ions is involved in peripheral oxygen sensing chemoreceptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pota​ssium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cal​cium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sod​ium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlo​rine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Potassium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Potassium</strong></p><p><strong>Potassium</strong> is involved in peripheral oxygen sensing chemoreceptors.</p>\n<p>Type I glomus cells (carotid and aortic body) have <strong>O<sub>2</sub>-sensitive K<sup>+</sup> channels</strong>, whose <strong>conductance is reduced</strong> in proportion to the degree of hypoxia.</p>\n<p>This <strong>reduces the K<sup>+</sup> efflux,</strong> depolarizing the cell and <strong>causing Ca<sup>2+</sup> inﬂux.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> Ca<sup>2+</sup> influx</strong> triggers action potentials and consequent<strong> excitation of the afferent nerve endings.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7437","text":"Which of the following neu​rons predominantly fire d​uring forceful expiration​?","raw_text":"Which of the following neurons predominantly fire during forceful expiration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsal grou​p of neurons","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventral grou​p of neurons","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pneumotax​ic center","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chemore​ceptors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ventral group of neurons","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ventral group of neurons</strong></p><p><strong>Ventral groups of neurons</strong> fire, when<strong> forceful expiration</strong> is needed like during heavy exercise.</p>\n<p>Dorsal group of neurons control the normal inspiration and expiration.</p>\n<p>Pneumotaxic center limits the duration of inspiration.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"fjAPq85I","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"25_psychiatric_emergencies","title":"25.Psychiatric Emergencies","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC2904","text":"What is false about the co‌ndition, that is relieved‍ by the technique shown i​n the picture?","raw_text":"What is false about the condition, that is relieved by the technique shown in the picture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Associated with carpopedal‌ spasms","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leads to paresthesias and ‌numbness","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Symptoms are due to hyperc‌apnia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Presence of a frequent sig‌hing during history takin‍g","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Symptoms are due to hypercapnia","question_images":["https://daily-rounds-staging.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/7988364af7f14e14acc14b174ef089b2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Symptoms are due to hypercapnia</strong></p><p>The technique shown in the above image is of '<strong>breathing in a bag' technique</strong>. It relieves <strong>hypocapnia</strong> in hyperventilation syndrome.</p>\n<p>Hyperventilation syndrome is a habit of <strong>hyperventilation</strong> due to stress or any emotional upheaval.</p>\n<p>Symptoms occur due to <strong>hypocapnia/hypocarbia</strong> due to carbon dioxide washout:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fatigue, dizziness, headache</li>\n<li>Chest pain, palpitations, sweating</li>\n<li>If severe - carpopedal spasms, paraesthesias, and loss of consciousness may occur. Acute hypocarbia causes respiratory alkalosis and reduced ionized calcium, resulting in <strong>hypocalcemic</strong> symptoms.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The patient has a typical history of stress followed by symptoms and <strong>frequently 'sighs'</strong> during the interview.</p>\n<p>Treatment for <strong>hyperventilation-associated hypocapnia</strong> is breathing in a bag. The patient is told to breathe in and out of a bag so that they <strong>rebreathe the expired air</strong>. This <strong>increases</strong> the levels of arterial <strong>CO2</strong> and quickly reverts the symptoms.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2903","text":"A first-year psychiatry re‌sident gets a call regard‍ing one of his patients w​ho has been brought to th‍e ER with high-grade feve​r and pharyngitis. The pa‌tient was started on cloz‌apine two weeks back for ​treatment-resistant schiz​ophrenia. His oral examin‍ation reveals the followi​ng picture. What would be​ the appropriate manageme​nt? Stop clozapine Reduce​ the dose of clozapine Ad‌minister granulocyte colo‌ny-stimulating factor Swi​tch to another antipsycho​tic medication Give antib​iotics","raw_text":"<p>A first-year psychiatry resident gets a call regarding one of his patients who has been brought to the ER with high-grade fever and pharyngitis. The patient was started on clozapine two weeks back for treatment-resistant schizophrenia. His oral examination reveals the following picture. What would be the appropriate management?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Stop clozapine</li>\n<li>Reduce the dose of clozapine</li>\n<li>Administer granulocyte colony-stimulating factor</li>\n<li>Switch to another antipsychotic medication</li>\n<li>Give antibiotics</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a‌nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3 ‌and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3 and 5","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7b0c15a489cc42a88f494e191f7de589.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3 and 5</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of high-grade fever and pharyngitis and the simultaneous development of oral ulcers (image) in a patient on clozapine is suggestive of <strong>drug-induced agranulocytosis.</strong> The treatment is to <strong>stop clozapine</strong> immediately, <strong>administer</strong> granulocyte colony-stimulating factor <strong>(G-CSF),</strong> and start <strong>empirical antibiotics</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Agranulocytosis</strong> is a rare, but fatal complication of clozapine. It is defined as an absolute neutrophil count (ANC) &lt; 500/mm<sup>3</sup>, or white blood cell (WBC) count &lt; 2000/mm<sup>3</sup>. It can initially manifest as a local infection with <strong>high-grade</strong> <strong>fever,</strong> sore throat, oral and perianal <strong>ulcerations.</strong></p>\n<p>These symptoms appear rapidly within days to weeks following initiation of drug therapy.</p>\n<p>Clozapine is generally considered to be more effective than other antipsychotic drugs and is often used in treatment-resistant schizophrenia (poor treatment response to 2 or more antipsychotic drugs). Hence, a switch from clozapine to another (less effective) antipsychotic drug is not done in a patient with drug-induced agranulocytosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2917","text":"A 44-year old businessman ‌came to the casualty with‍ headache, nausea, vomiti​ng, and diaphoresis since‍ early morning. He was di​agnosed with depression 4‌ months back and has been‌ on phenelzine. He gives ​the history of attending ​an office party the previ‍ous night where he consum​ed some red wine. On exam​ination, his BP was 190/1​20. What is false about h​is present condition?","raw_text":"A 44-year old businessman came to the casualty with headache, nausea, vomiting, and diaphoresis since early morning. He was diagnosed with depression 4 months back and has been on phenelzine. He gives the history of attending an office party the previous night where he consumed some red wine. On examination, his BP was 190/120. What is false about his present condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The condition is less comm‌on with selegilline.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Should be treated with phe‌ntolamine.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"He is suffering from serot‌onin syndrome.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tyramine is involved the p‌athogenesis of the condit‍ion.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. He is suffering from serotonin syndrome.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e7daa2bc7f5d4b42afe5be2cf23cfc45x1280x1765.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. He is suffering from serotonin syndrome.</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>cheese reaction, </strong>not serotonin syndrome.<strong> </strong>Here, the patient on phenelzine (non-selective and irreversible MAO-inhibitor) developed the clinical features of cheese reaction on having a tyramine-rich food item (red wine, cheese, milk).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3174\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1013\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3174\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Inhibition of the MAO-A isoform prevents the metabolism of dietary <strong>tyramine</strong> in the gut, thus allowing intact tyramine to enter the systemic circulation. It is taken up by adrenergic neurons and a hypertensive crisis occurs as a result of a powerful pressor effect of tyramine. </p>\n<p>The <strong>drug of choice</strong> to treat hypertensive crisis in cheese reaction is <strong>phentolamine</strong>. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>MAO-inhibitor with SSRI causes <strong>serotonin syndrome</strong>. Symptoms are fever, altered mental status, rigidity, hyperreflexia, and autonomic dysfunction.</p>\n<p>Selegiline and rasagiline do not functionally inhibit MAO-A. They do not cause cheese reaction with doses used in clinical practice.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3229","text":"A young woman with ongoing‌ seizures and respiratory‍ distress was rushed to t​he casualty by her relati‍ves with an alleged histo​ry of attempted suicide b‌y drug overdose. She was ‌febrile, her heart rate w​as 180 bpm, BP was 70/50 ​mmHg. Her mouth was dry a‍nd her pupils were dilate​d. ABG showed a pH of 7.1​, bicarbonate level was 1​4 mmol/L and pCO2 was 30 ​mm Hg. Which of the follo‌wing is the most likely a‌gent?","raw_text":"A young woman with ongoing seizures and respiratory distress was rushed to the casualty by her relatives with an alleged history of attempted suicide by drug overdose. She was febrile, her heart rate was 180 bpm, BP was 70/50 mmHg. Her mouth was dry and her pupils were dilated. ABG showed a pH of 7.1, bicarbonate level was 14 mmol/L and pCO2 was 30 mm Hg. Which of the following is the most likely agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fluox‌etine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Imipr‌amine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lit‌hium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clona‌zepam","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Imipramine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Imipramine</strong></p><p>The clinical picture is suggestive of <strong>tricyclic antidepressant (TCA) poisoning i.e</strong> metabolic acidosis, tachycardia, hypotension, and pupillary dilatation. <strong>Imipramine</strong> is a TCA. </p>\n<p>Tricyclic antidepressant overdose:</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>CNS- <strong>seizures, respiratory depression, </strong>drowsiness, delirium. </li>\n<li>CVS - <strong>sinus tachycardia, hypotension</strong>, prolonged PR/QRS/QT interval, arrhythmias (ventricular tachycardia, fibrillation)</li>\n<li>Anticholinergic - <strong>dilated pupils</strong>, dry mouth, urinary retention, hyperthermia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Oxygen supplementation and IV fluids</li>\n<li>Activated charcoal if the patient is brought within two hours of ingestion (unless ileus present)</li>\n<li>IV <strong>sodium bicarbonate </strong>(attenuates the TCA-induced cardiotoxicity and metabolic acidosis)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Dialysis is not very helpful in the management of TCA overdose.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2916","text":"Which of the following dru‌g intoxications has been ‍matched incorrectly?","raw_text":"Which of the following drug intoxications has been matched incorrectly?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amphetamine intoxication -‌ treated by alkalisation ‍of urine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic amphetamine intoxi‌cation - shows phenomenon‍ of \"runs and crashes\"","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Benzodiazapine intoxicatio‌n - acute intoxication re‍sembles alcohol intoxicat​ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Levodopa intoxication - in‌duces rapid cycling in bi‍polar disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Amphetamine intoxication - treated by alkalisation of urine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Amphetamine intoxication - treated by alkalisation of urine</strong></p><p><strong>Amphetamine</strong> <strong>intoxication</strong> is treated by <strong>acidification of urine, </strong>not alkalization.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 499px; height: 645px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 130.21875px; height: 15px;\">Drug</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.96875px; height: 15px;\">Intoxication signs and symptoms</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.828125px; height: 15px;\">Treatment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 130.21875px; height: 15px;\"><strong>Levodopa</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.96875px; height: 15px;\">Mania, depression, <strong>rapid cycling</strong> in bipolar disorder.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.828125px; height: 15px;\">Lower dosage, or discontinue the drug.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 130.21875px; height: 15px;\"><strong>Lithium toxicity</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.96875px; height: 15px;\">Vomiting, abdominal pain, diarrhea, ataxia, tremors, dysarthria, seizures</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.828125px; height: 15px;\">Lavage with wide bore tube, osmotic diuresis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 130.21875px; height: 15px;\"><strong>Benzodiazepine intoxication</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.96875px; height: 15px;\">Behavioral inhibition, blackouts, impulsivity, memory loss, mood alterations, derealisation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.828125px; height: 15px;\">Flumazenil, to antagonise benzodiazepine and supportive measures.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 130.21875px; height: 15px;\"><strong>Amphetamine intoxication</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 173.96875px; height: 15px;\">\n<p>Paranoid hallucinations (but no thought disorder and there is appropriate affect), tachycardia, hypertension, seizures, tremors.</p>\n<p>Chronic intoxication - a cycle of heavy use for a few days <strong>(runs)</strong> followed by stopping the drug <strong>(crashes),</strong> tactile hallucinations, compulsive craving.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.828125px; height: 15px;\">\n<p>Symptomatic measures like cold sponging, diazepam for seizures, antihypertensives. </p>\n<p><strong>Acidification of urine</strong> with oral Ammonium chloride, 500 mg every 4 hours, facilitates the elimination of amphetamines.</p>\n<p>Psychosis resolves within 7 days of urinary clearance.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0227","text":"A patient with a complex p‌sychiatric history is on ‍a mono-amine oxidase (MAO​) inhibitor. She visits h‍er primary care physician​ with increasing depressi‌on. The physician adds a ‌serotonin reuptake inhibi​tor (SSRI). Which of the ​following is she most lik‍ely to develop?","raw_text":"A patient with a complex psychiatric history is on a mono-amine oxidase (MAO) inhibitor. She visits her primary care physician with increasing depression. The physician adds a serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI). Which of the following is she most likely to develop?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fever, rigidity, dilated p‌upils, and hyperreflexia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fever, rigidity, normal pu‌pils, and hyporeflexia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fever, normotonia, dilated‌ pupils and normal reflex‍es","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fever, rigidity, normal pu‌pils and reduced bowel so‍unds","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fever, rigidity, dilated pupils, and hyperreflexia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fever, rigidity, dilated pupils, and hyperreflexia</strong></p><p>This patient, who is on <strong>MAO inhibitor + SSRI</strong> can develop <strong>serotonin syndrome, </strong>characterized by fever, <strong>rigidity, hyperreflexia, </strong>and <strong>dilated </strong>pupils.</p>\n<p>Option B: This is seen in <strong>neuroleptic malignant syndrome, </strong>which occurs due to certain <strong>antipsychotics.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: This is seen in <strong>anticholinergic toxicity.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: This is seen in <strong>malignant hyperthermia.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0228","text":"A 46-year-old male patient‌ who was treated with hal‍operidol for delirium, de​veloped fever, tachycardi‍a, increased blood pressu​re, muscle stiffness, and‌ altered mental status. I‌nvestigations revealed el​evated levels of creatine​ phosphokinase. What is t‍he most likely diagnosis?​","raw_text":"A 46-year-old male patient who was treated with haloperidol for delirium, developed fever, tachycardia, increased blood pressure, muscle stiffness, and altered mental status. Investigations revealed elevated levels of creatine phosphokinase. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serotonin‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tardive d‌yskinesia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypersensitivity reaction ‌to haloperidol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neuroleptic malignant synd‌rome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome</strong></p><p>The clinical features in a patient who has been administered <strong>haloperidol,</strong> are most likely suggestive of the <strong>neuroleptic malignant syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS)<strong> </strong>is a life-threatening complication that can occur anytime during antipsychotic treatment. <strong>Haloperidol</strong> poses the <strong>highest risk</strong> for causing NMS. The condition may occur within 24-72 hrs after initiation of the treatment, and if untreated, may last for 10 to 14 days.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Serotonin syndrome</strong> occurs due to an increase of plasma serotonin to toxic levels, due to concurrent administration of<strong> SSRI and an MAO inhibitor,</strong> L-tryptophan or lithium.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by 3 A’s:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neuromuscular hyper<strong>a</strong>ctivity - clonus, hyperreflexia, hypertonia, tremor, and seizure</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>utonomic stimulation - hyperthermia, diaphoresis, diarrhoea </li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>gitation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In severe cases, there may be delirium, coma, status epilepticus, and death.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Tardive dyskinesia</strong> is characterized by abnormal, irregular, involuntary choreoathetoid movements of the muscles of the head, limbs, and trunk. Perioral movement is most common. The condition usually develops as a <strong>delayed response</strong> to <strong>antipsychotics,</strong> about 6 months after treatment.</p>\n<p>Option C: Hypersensitivity reaction to haloperidol may include anaphylactic reaction, angioedema, facial and laryngeal oedema, rash, urticaria, bronchospasm, and laryngospasm.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2905","text":"The table shows the examin‌ation findings of various‍ psychiatric emergencies ​presenting with fever and‍ altered sensorium. Choos​e the false option regard‌ing them.","raw_text":"The table shows the examination findings of various psychiatric emergencies presenting with fever and altered sensorium. Choose the false option regarding them.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patient A - presents with ‌increased CPK levels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Patient B - has anticholin‌ergic syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Patient C - IV physostigmi‌ne is given to counteract‍ symptoms","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patient D - dantrolene is ‌administered for treatmen‍t","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Patient B - has anticholinergic syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5671d1f4d3f244729a3f4b00f8f32937.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Patient B - has anticholinergic syndrome</strong></p><p>Patient B has <strong>s</strong><strong>erotonin</strong> (not anticholinergic) <strong>syndrome</strong>.  </p>\n<p>The above table represents the following psychiatric emergencies:</p>\n<p>A - Neuroleptic malignant syndrome.</p>\n<p>B - Serotonin syndrome.</p>\n<p>C - Anticholinergic syndrome.</p>\n<p>D - Malignant hyperthermia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2842","text":"Select the incorrect match‌.","raw_text":"Select the incorrect match.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Suicide survivor - someone‌ who has experienced a cl‍ose person committing sui​cide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Suicidal ideation - nonspe‌cific, occasional thought‍ to end one's life","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parasuicidal behaviour - s‌elf-mutilation without in‍tent to commit suicide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deliberate self harm - wil‌ful infliction of injurie‍s to self, with the inten​t to die","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Deliberate self harm - wilful infliction of injuries to self, with the intent to die","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Deliberate self harm - wilful infliction of injuries to self, with the intent to die</strong></p><p><strong>Deliberate</strong> <strong>self-harm</strong> is the wilful self-infliction of painful and destructive acts, <strong>without</strong> the <strong>intent</strong> <strong>to die.</strong></p>\n<p>Terms associated with suicide:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Suicide survivor</strong> - Someone who has experienced a close person committing suicide.</li>\n<li><strong>Suicidal ideation</strong> - Nonspecific, occasional thought to end one's life</li>\n<li><strong>Parasuicidal behaviour</strong> - Self-mutilation without intent to commit suicide, more common in females than males - 3:1. Wrists, arms, thighs, and legs are most commonly cut.</li>\n<li><strong>Suicidal intent</strong> - Intense and persistent thought to serve as the agent of one's own death, but without a plan in mind.</li>\n<li><strong>Suicidal plan</strong>- Making a plan to commit suicide</li>\n<li><strong>Suicidal behaviour</strong> - Taking steps to execute the plan</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: In the general population, men are three times more likely than women to complete suicide. Women, however, were found to attempt suicide four times more often than men.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2694","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most consistent findi‍ng in patients with incre​ased suicidal tendency?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most consistent finding in patients with increased suicidal tendency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increas‌ed GABA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased‌ serotonin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increase‌d 5-HIAA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both B‌ and C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Decreased serotonin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Decreased serotonin</strong></p><p><strong>Decreased levels of serotonin</strong> is associated with an increased risk of suicide.</p>\n<p>The genetic and hormonal theory of suicide:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Decreased</strong> levels of <strong>serotonin</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Decreased</strong> levels of 5-Hydroxy Indole Acetic Acid (<strong>5-HIAA</strong>), a primary metabolite of serotonin, in <strong>CSF</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The average suicide rate in India is 12.7 per 1 lakh population</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most common psychiatric illness leading to suicide - major depression</li>\n<li>Most common mode of suicide in India - hanging<strong> </strong>&gt; consumption of poison</li>\n<li>Most common mode of suicide <strong>attempt</strong> in India - consumption of poison &gt; hanging</li>\n<li>Most common cause of suicide in India - family problems &gt; illness</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2697","text":"Which of the following pat‌ient groups does not have‍ an increased risk of sui​cide?","raw_text":"Which of the following patient groups does not have an increased risk of suicide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patients below 30 years wi‌th mood disorders","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Youth with antisocial pers‌onality disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patients suffering from ch‌ronic illnesses","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patients who have a positi‌ve family history of suic‍ide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Patients below 30 years with mood disorders","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Patients below 30 years with mood disorders</strong></p><p>Diagnoses of mood disorders and cognitive disorders are more <strong>commonly</strong> seen among suicides<strong> above </strong>the<strong> age of 30 years. </strong></p>\n<p>Amongst suicides <strong>below</strong> the age of 30 years, the diagnoses of <strong>substance abuse</strong> and <strong>antisocial personality disorder</strong> are higher. Amongst mental illnesses overall,<strong> mood disorders</strong> and <strong>depression</strong> have the highest risk of suicide. </p>\n<p>Risk factors for suicide include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Male</strong>&gt; female, but females attempt more. The higher numbers are in the male because of the difference in the methods of attempting suicide.</li>\n<li>Age: <strong>30-45</strong> years</li>\n<li>Previous attempts - the risk of a second attempt is highest within three months of the first attempt.</li>\n<li><strong>Family history</strong></li>\n<li>Physical or mental illnesses:\n<ul>\n<li>Depression/mood disorders</li>\n<li><strong>Antisocial</strong> personality disorder</li>\n<li>Borderline personality disorder</li>\n<li><strong>Chronic illnesses</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Alcohol or drug dependence</li>\n<li>Suicidal preoccupation</li>\n<li>A recent loss, unemployment</li>\n<li>Social isolation, being single (unmarried, divorced, or widowed)</li>\n<li>Amongst immature defence mechanisms, <strong>repression</strong> has the highest risk</li>\n<li>Genetic - 18% concordance rate for monozygotic twins, and low levels of serotonin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2821","text":"When will a psychiatrist b‌e more concerned about a ‍patient committing suicid​e?","raw_text":"When will a psychiatrist be more concerned about a patient committing suicide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"When he is in mania rather‌ than depression","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"When hospitalised rather t‌han discharged","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Soon after he starts antid‌epressant medication","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"At the peak of a depressiv‌e episode","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Soon after he starts antidepressant medication","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Soon after he starts antidepressant medication</strong></p><p>Patients are more likely to commit suicide <strong>soon after</strong> they are started on <strong>antidepressant medication</strong>. </p>\n<p>Somatic symptoms of depression improve faster than psychological symptoms when a patient is started on antidepressant medication and hence patients have the energy to act on their thoughts of deliberate self-harm and suicide (<strong>paradoxical suicide</strong>).</p>\n<p>Option A: For bipolar disorder, the <strong>suicidal risk</strong> is higher in the <strong>depressive phase</strong> than the manic phase.</p>\n<p>Option B: The highest risk of suicide is within a period of <strong>6 months after discharge</strong> from a psychiatric facility than when admitted.</p>\n<p>Option D: During the <strong>peak</strong> of the <strong>depressive episode</strong> though the <strong>desire</strong> to die is <strong>intense,</strong> the <strong>energy</strong> level is <strong>low</strong> to execute the plan. The risk of suicide is higher at the onset and the end of the depressive episode.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"oxJWdG8Y","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"25_rectum","title":"25.Rectum","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB4589","text":"The anorectal angle is for​med by which muscle?","raw_text":"The anorectal angle is formed by which muscle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pubore​ctalis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ischioc​avernous","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deep transv​erse perinei","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bulbosp​ongiosus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Puborectalis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fcdd9247ff1a4ee29c2f46e29a1ddd69x1280x2281.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Puborectalis</strong></p><p>The <strong>puborectalis muscle encircles</strong> the posterior and lateral aspects of the anorectal junction, creating the <strong>anorectal angle</strong>.</p>\n<p>At rest, the anorectal angle is held at <strong>90 - 100°</strong>. This becomes more <strong>acute</strong> (70 - 90°) during <strong>squeezing</strong> due to the contraction and shortening of the puborectalis muscle. This brings the anorectal junction higher and nearer to the pubic symphysis.</p>\n<p>During straining and <strong>defecation</strong>, the anorectal angle becomes more <strong>obtuse </strong>(110 - 180°) due to relaxation of the puborectalis muscle.</p>\n<p>The<strong> anorectal ring </strong>is formed by the <strong>fusion</strong> of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Puborectalis </li>\n<li>Deep external anal sphincter</li>\n<li>Internal anal sphincter</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Anismus, is caused by a failure of the puborectalis and external anal sphincter to relax during defecation straining. This results in a failure of the anorectal angle to straighten during defecation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4593","text":"What is the length of the ​adult rectum?","raw_text":"What is the length of the adult rectum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20-2​5 cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"12-1​8 cm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"8-1​0 cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3-5​ cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 12-18 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 12-18 cm</strong></p><p>The adult rectum is approximately <strong>12–18 cm</strong> in length and is conventionally divided into three equal parts - the upper, middle and lower 1/3rd.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4595","text":"Which fascial layer should​ be dissected to separate​ the rectum from the cocc‌yx while operating on a p​atient with rectal adenoc​arcinoma?","raw_text":"Which fascial layer should be dissected to separate the rectum from the coccyx while operating on a patient with rectal adenocarcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Waldeyer​’s fascia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Denonvilli​ers’ fascia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Buck’s​ fascia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Scarpa’​s fascia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Waldeyer’s fascia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f676be314eee4df1b6f6987294279f98x1280x1476.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Waldeyer’s fascia</strong></p><p><strong>Waldeyer's fascia</strong> separates the rectum from the coccyx. </p>\n<p>The lower third of the rectum is separated by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>from the prostate or vagina anteriorly<strong> </strong>- by <strong>Denonvilliers’ fascia</strong></li>\n<li>from the coccyx and lower two sacral vertebrae- by <strong>Waldeyer’s fascia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Waldeyer's fascia originates at the <strong>S2 to S4</strong> level from the <strong>presacral parietal fascia</strong> and fuses with the rectal visceral fascia at the posterior aspect of the rectum. It <strong>divides</strong> the<strong> retrorectal space</strong> into superior and inferior compartments. <sup class=\"reference\" id=\"cite_ref-6\"></sup></p>\n<p>Identification and preservation of the<strong> presacral fascia</strong> play an important role in <strong>preventing complications</strong> and <strong>reducing local recurrences</strong> of rectal cancer.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4597","text":"Up to what length of the p​atient's rectum will you ​be able to visualize whil‌e using a proctoscope?","raw_text":"Up to what length of the patient's rectum will you be able to visualize while using a proctoscope?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5 ​cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"8 ​cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10​ cm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"20​ cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 10 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 10 cm</strong></p><p><strong>Proctoscopes</strong> can be used to inspect the anus, anorectal junction, and <strong>10-15cm</strong> of the lower rectum.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 379px; height: 125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 221.047px; height: 46px;\">\n<p><strong>Scope</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.953px; height: 46px;\">\n<p><strong>Length</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 23px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 221.047px; height: 23px;\">\n<p>Anoscope</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.953px; height: 23px;\">\n<p>8 cm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 14px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 221.047px; height: 14px;\">\n<p><strong>Proctoscope</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.953px; height: 14px;\">\n<p><strong>10–</strong><strong>15 cm</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 55px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 221.047px; height: 55px;\">\n<p>Rigid Sigmoidoscope and Rectoscope</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.953px; height: 55px;\">\n<p>25 cm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 221.047px; height: 33px;\">\n<p>Flexible sigmoidoscope</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.953px; height: 33px;\">\n<p>60 cm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 221.047px; height: 33px;\">\n<p>Colonoscope</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.953px; height: 33px;\">\n<p>160 cm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4638","text":"The instrument shown below​ will be least useful for​ examining patients with ‌which of the following co​nditions?","raw_text":"The instrument shown below will be least useful for examining patients with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute ana​l fissure","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Carcinom​a rectum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemor​rhoids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rectal​ polyp","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute anal fissure","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5d678046cc2c4c4a8b796f07e0fcca49.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute anal fissure</strong></p><p>The given image shows a proctoscope. It is not used for examining patients with anal fissures. </p>\n<p>Due to pain, <strong>digital rectal examination</strong> and <strong>anoscopy</strong> are not performed immediately in an <strong>acute anal fissure</strong>. It is reserved for later when the <strong>symptoms resolve</strong> or the patient is in the <strong>operating room</strong>.</p>\n<p>Proctoscopy is indicated in patients with the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rectal bleeding</li>\n<li>Change in bowel habit, especially recurring attacks of diarrhea</li>\n<li>Pain or difficulty in defecation</li>\n<li>Lesions around the anal opening - abscesses, fistulae, discharge, ulcers, external hemorrhoids</li>\n<li>Rectal prolapse.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4602","text":"An anxious mother rushed h​er 2-year-old child to th​e casualty when she notic‌ed a red-coloured mass pr​otruding from his anus. T​he mother gave a history ​of the child straining wh​ile passing stools for th​e past 2 weeks. There was‌ no abdominal tenderness,‌ and on local examination​, a 6 cm mass with circum‌ferential folds and sligh​t edema was noted. How wi‍ll you manage this child?​","raw_text":"An anxious mother rushed her 2-year-old child to the casualty when she noticed a red-coloured mass protruding from his anus. The mother gave a history of the child straining while passing stools for the past 2 weeks. There was no abdominal tenderness, and on local examination, a 6 cm mass with circumferential folds and slight edema was noted.  How will you manage this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiersch ​operation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Delorme’s​ operation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Altemeier’​s procedure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Conservativ​e treatment","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Conservative treatment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Conservative treatment</strong></p><p>The given history and examination finding is suggestive of <strong>rectal prolapse</strong>. In children, the prolapse is <strong>usually mucosal</strong> and should be treated <strong>conservatively.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Rectal prolapse</strong> occurs when a mucosal or full-thickness layer of rectal tissue protrudes through the anal orifice. </p>\n<p><strong>Conservative treatment</strong> for rectal prolapse in children is to control:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diarrhea</li>\n<li>Worm infestation</li>\n<li>Malnutrition</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sclerotherapy</strong> can also be used.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4601","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is not true regar​ding the management of pa‌tients with rectal prolap​se?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true regarding the management of patients with rectal prolapse?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Surgery is necessary for f​ull-thickness rectal prol​apse","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Delorme’s operation is don​e via the perineal approa​ch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ripstein's procedure is do​ne via abdominal approach​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abdominal rectopexy is oft​en preferred in high risk​ individuals","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Abdominal rectopexy is often preferred in high risk individuals","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/12f707dffe7d4ef595a662a4d0cc297ex1280x2375.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Abdominal rectopexy is often preferred in high risk individuals</strong></p><p>The<strong> perineal approach</strong> is often preferred in patients with<strong> high operative risk</strong> and limited life expectancy, i.e. in the <strong>elderly.</strong> It is associated with less morbidity but has a <strong>higher recurrence rate</strong>. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1423\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Abdominal</strong> approaches (laparoscopic, robotic, or open) are preferred for <strong>younger</strong> patients, particularly those with constipation or associated pelvic floor disorders.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Perineal procedures:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Delorme's mucosectomy-</strong> involves mucosal stripping and muscle plication of the rectal prolapse using interrupted sutures. It is performed externally under regional or general anaesthesia.</li>\n<li><strong>Thiersch's encirclement</strong>- a steel wire, or Silastic or nylon tape, is placed around the anal canal. Largely obsolete now due to problems with chronic perineal sepsis, anal stenosis, and obstructed defaecation</li>\n<li><strong>Altemeier rectosigmoidectomy</strong>- involves removal of the prolapsed rectum and associated sigmoid colon, followed by which a coloanal anastomosis is created. It is often complicated by poor bowel control with faecal soiling secondary to the loss of rectal reservoir.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Abdominal procedures:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Suture rectopexy</li>\n<li>Mesh rectopexy</li>\n<li>Posterior (Well’s and lvalon’s)</li>\n<li>Anterior (<strong>Ripstein’s</strong>)</li>\n<li>Lateral (Orr-Loygue)</li>\n<li>Ventral</li>\n<li>Resection Rectopexy (Frykman–Goldberg)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The principle of all <strong>abdominal</strong> operations for rectal prolapse is to <strong>fix the rectum</strong> in its normal <strong>anatomical position.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4603","text":"Which of the following is ​not true regarding solita​ry rectal ulcers?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding solitary rectal ulcers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Commonly seen on the poste​rior wall","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rectal bleeding Is a commo​n presentation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Management is by STARR pro​cedure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Colonoscopy with biopsy is​ the investigation of cho​ice","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Commonly seen on the posterior wall","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Commonly seen on the posterior wall</strong></p><p>A <strong>solitary rectal ulcer</strong> is usually located on the<strong> anterior rectal wall, 6-8 cm</strong> from the anal verge<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It commonly presents with <strong>rectal bleeding</strong> in the setting of chronic straining or constipation. Histology reveals a thick layer of fibrosis obliterating the lamina propria and a central fibrinous exudate.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Colonoscopy with biopsy </strong>is the <strong>investigation of choice</strong> for diagnostic evaluation, while <strong>defecography </strong>(proctography) is the <strong>radiologic</strong> procedure of choice.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Stapled trans-anal rectal resection(<strong>STARR</strong> procedure) is the management of choice.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4604","text":"What is the radiological p​rocedure of choice in pat​ients suspected to have a‌ solitary rectal ulcer?","raw_text":"What is the radiological procedure of choice in patients suspected to have a solitary rectal ulcer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M​RI","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Defeco​graphy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Endorec​tal USG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CT ​scan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Defecography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Defecography</strong></p><p><strong>Defecography </strong>(proctography) is the radiologic procedure of choice for <strong>diagnostic</strong> evaluation in <strong>solitary rectal ulcer syndrome (SRUS). </strong>Some common findings include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Full-thickness rectal prolapse</li>\n<li>Internal prolapse</li>\n<li>Paradoxical puborectalis syndrome</li>\n<li>Thickened rectal folds </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Defecography evaluates in real-time the morphology of the rectum and anal canal in a physiologic setting by <strong>injection</strong> of a thick <strong>barium paste</strong> into the <strong>rectum</strong> and its subsequent <strong>evacuation.</strong> </p>\n<p>It is primarily performed for workup of patients with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Longstanding constipation</li>\n<li>Unexplained anal or rectal pain</li>\n<li>Residual sensation after defecation</li>\n<li>Suspected prolapse.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4615","text":"What is the earliest manif​estation in patients with​ rectal carcinoma?","raw_text":"What is the earliest manifestation in patients with rectal carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Consti​pation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Per recta​l bleeding","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tene​smus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spurious​ diarrhea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Per rectal bleeding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Per rectal bleeding</strong></p><p><strong>Per-rectal bleeding</strong> is the earliest and most common symptom of rectal carcinoma.</p>\n<p>Other symptoms of rectal carcinoma include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mucus drainage</li>\n<li>Tenesmus</li>\n<li>Early morning diarrhoea</li>\n<li>Pain</li>\n<li>Weight loss.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4610","text":"Which of the following sta​ging systems is used for ​rectal carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following staging systems is used for rectal carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duke's ​staging","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bloom-Richar​dson grading","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nevine ​staging","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Robson ​staging","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Duke's staging","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/47e1db89a4e348d5bff23ccb79e35475x1280x1193.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Duke&#x27;s staging</strong></p><p><strong>Duke’s staging</strong> is used in rectal carcinoma.</p>\n<p><strong>Modified Duke’s classification</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 151px;\" width=\"414\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 39.0469px;\">\n<p>A</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 358.953px;\">\n<p>Growth limited to the rectal wall</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 39.0469px;\">\n<p>B</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 358.953px;\">\n<p>Growth extending to extrarectal tissues, but no lymphatic spread</p>\n<p>B1: Invading submucosa</p>\n<p>B2: Invading muscle layer</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 39.0469px;\">\n<p>C</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 358.953px;\">\n<p>Lymph node secondaries</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 39.0469px;\">\n<p>D</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 358.953px;\">\n<p>Distant spread</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> <strong>Astler-Coller’s staging:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 255px;\" width=\"413\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 54.6562px; height: 46px;\">\n<p>A</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 342.344px; height: 46px;\">\n<p>Intramucosal lesions</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 54.6562px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>B1</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 342.344px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Lesions extending into the muscularis propria, but not penetrating it, with negative nodes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 54.6562px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>B2</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 342.344px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Lesions penetrating the muscularis propria, with negative nodes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 54.6562px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>C1</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 342.344px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Lesions extending into the muscularis propria, but not penetrating it, with positive nodes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 54.6562px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>C2</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 342.344px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Lesions penetrating the muscularis propria with positive nodes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 54.6562px;\">\n<p>D</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 342.344px;\">\n<p>Distant spread</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4613","text":"An elderly man with per-re​ctal bleeding is suspecte​d to have rectal carcinom‌a. Which of the following​ is false regarding this ​condition?","raw_text":"An elderly man with per-rectal bleeding is suspected to have rectal carcinoma. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Majority of the cancers ar​e sporadic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dose of radiation used for​ this tumor is 45 Gy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dissection lateral to the ​endopelvic fascia investi​ng the mesorectum causes ‌local recurrence","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Precise location of the tu​mor is determined with ri​gid proctosigmoidoscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dissection lateral to the endopelvic fascia investing the mesorectum causes local recurrence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dissection lateral to the endopelvic fascia investing the mesorectum causes local recurrence</strong></p><p><strong>Dissection medial</strong> (not lateral) to the endopelvic fascia investing the mesorectum causes <strong>local recurrence</strong>.</p>\n<p>Dissection <strong>lateral </strong>to the avascular anatomic space,<strong> </strong>risks injury to the mixed autonomic nerves, causing <strong>impotence </strong>in men and <strong>bladder dysfunction </strong>in men and women.</p>\n<p><strong>Transmesorectal resection</strong> involves complete removal of the lymph node bearing mesorectum along with its intact enveloping fascia. It is a <strong>curative</strong> resection of rectal tumours of the <strong>middle and lower thirds</strong> of the rectum, either as part of Low Anterior Resection <strong>(LAR)</strong> or Abdominoperineal Resection <strong>(APR).</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: The majority of colorectal cancers are <strong>sporadic</strong>. Approximately <strong>5%</strong> of all colorectal cancers are attributed to familial adenomatous polyposis (<strong>FAP</strong>) and <strong>Lynch syndrome</strong> (hereditary non-polyposis colorectal cancer). </p>\n<p>Option B: The large bowel can tolerate delivered radiation doses of up to 6000 cGy. However, a lower dose of<strong> 45 Gy</strong> is <strong>recommended</strong> for colorectal tumours. <strong>High levels</strong> <strong>of radiation</strong> targeted at colon tumours would include the <strong>small bowel</strong> in the treatment field. This results in complications such as <strong>radiation enteritis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option D: The <strong>precise location</strong> of the rectal tumour is determined using<strong> rigid proctosigmoidoscope </strong>or flexible sigmoidoscopy and biopsy. The other investigations used in rectal cancer include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Digital rectal examination - for assessing the depth of penetration</li>\n<li>Endorectal ultrasound or MRI - provides an accurate assessment of the extent of invasion of the bowel wall</li>\n<li>MRI with an endorectal coil - is the investigation of choice to assess the 'T' and 'N' stages</li>\n<li>CECT<strong> </strong>- for evaluation of metastasis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4628","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g rectal carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding rectal carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hartmann’s operation is do​ne in elderly debilitated​ patients","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adenocarcinoma is a rare v​ariant of rectal carcinom​a","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Early morning spurious dia​rrhoea and tenesmus can o​ccur","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Growth confined to the rec​tal mucosa is stage A of ​modified Duke’s staging","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adenocarcinoma is a rare variant of rectal carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/47e1db89a4e348d5bff23ccb79e35475x1280x1193.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adenocarcinoma is a rare variant of rectal carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Adenocarcinoma</strong> is the <strong>most common variant</strong> of rectal carcinoma.</p>\n<p>Colorectal cancer is:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The <strong>second most common</strong> malignancy <strong>globally</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The <strong>second most common</strong> cancer in <strong>women</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The <strong>third most common</strong> cancer in <strong>men</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The <strong>fourth most common</strong> cause of<strong> death due to cancer</strong>. </li>\n</ol>\n<p>Symptoms of rectal carcinoma include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Painless bleeding</strong> - earliest and most common</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Alteration of bowel habits - change in frequency and consistency of the stool</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Tenesmus</strong> - unable to pass a motion even when there is the sensation of needing to evacuate the bowel. It is suggestive of a tumor of the lower half of the rectum </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Early morning bloody diarrhea <strong>(option C)</strong> - passage of blood and mucus in addition to feces. Pain and weight loss are usually late symptoms</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Abdominal examination and digital rectal examination are used for the primary evaluation. <strong>Rigid</strong> <strong>sigmoidoscopy</strong> is performed to obtain a <strong>biopsy</strong>. A synchronous tumor (primary tumor with a different origin than the one currently investigated) is excluded by performing a colonoscopy.</p>\n<p><strong>Modified Duke’s classification:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"61\">\n<p>A</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"577\">\n<p>Growth limited up to the rectal mucosa (option D)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"61\">\n<p>B</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"577\">\n<p>B1: Tumor invading muscularis propria, no lymph node invasion.</p>\n<p>B2: Tumors invading into or through serosa, no lymph node invasion.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"61\">\n<p>C</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"577\">\n<p>C1: The tumour did not exceed the bowel wall, lymph node metastasis.</p>\n<p>C2: Tumour exceeded the intestinal wall and lymph node metastasis.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"61\">\n<p>D</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"577\">\n<p>Distant metastasis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Surgery</strong> is the <strong>mainstay</strong> of management for rectal carcinoma. Non-operative management is offered to only those patients who have a complete clinical response to neoadjuvant.</p>\n<p>The type of surgery depends on multiple factors (size, location, age of the patient, etc). However, the general consensus is:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Early cancer</strong> (T1 and a few T2) is locally excised via the <strong>transanal approac</strong>h.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Most <strong>rectal cancer</strong> is treated by <strong>anterior resection</strong> and <strong>colorectal/coloanal anastomosis</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Low <strong>extensive tumors</strong> are treated by laparoscopic abdominoperineal resection<strong>(APR)</strong> and <strong>permanent colostomy</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Hartmann’s operation </strong>(option A) is preferred in <strong>old and frail patients</strong> due to poor anal sphincter and postoperative incontinence.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4627","text":"In patients with rectal ca​rcinoma, what is the inve​stigation of choice for a‌ssessment of perirectal n​odes and the depth of pen​etration?","raw_text":"In patients with rectal carcinoma, what is the investigation of choice for assessment of perirectal nodes and the depth of penetration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ultraso​nography","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CT scan​ pelvis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endorec​tal MRI","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Double-contrast barium ene​ma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Endorectal MRI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Endorectal MRI</strong></p><p><strong>Endorectal-coil magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) </strong>is the best modality for predicting <strong>lymph node invasion</strong> and overall local staging.</p>\n<p>It can identify involved <strong>perirectal nodes</strong> on the basis of characteristics other than size. It can also identify foci at sites not only within the mesorectum but also outside the mesorectal fascia, such as pelvic sidewalls, and can identify the <strong>depth of penetration</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4608","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common site of b​lood-borne metastasis in ‌patients with rectal carc​inoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common site of blood-borne metastasis in patients with rectal carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Li​ver","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lu​ng","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adr​enal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Br​ain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Liver</strong></p><p>The principal sites for <strong>blood-borne metastases</strong> in rectal carcinoma are the <strong>liver </strong>&gt;<strong> </strong>lungs &gt; adrenals.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4620","text":"In a patient with rectal c​arcinoma, the tumour was ​found to invade through t‌he muscularis propria int​o the perirectal tissues.​ Two lymph nodes near the​ tumour site were enlarge​d. Which of the following​ will be the appropriate ‌modality of treatment in ‌this patient?","raw_text":"In a patient with rectal carcinoma, the tumour was found to invade through the muscularis propria into the perirectal tissues. Two lymph nodes near the tumour site were enlarged. Which of the following will be the appropriate modality of treatment in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sur​gery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Surgery + adjuvant chemoth​erapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Preoperative chemoradiatio​n + radical resection","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Palliative c​hemoradiation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Preoperative chemoradiation + radical resection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Preoperative chemoradiation + radical resection</strong></p><p>In the above clinical scenario, the TNM staging is <strong>T3 N1b M0</strong> (Stage IIIB), since:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tumor invasion through the muscularis propria into the perirectal tissues - <strong>T3</strong></li>\n<li>Involvement of two lymph nodes - <strong>N1b</strong></li>\n<li>No evidence of metastasis - <strong>M0</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The treatment of choice for <strong>Stage III</strong> tumors is <strong>preoperative chemoradiation </strong>followed by<strong> radical resection</strong>. </p>\n<p>Treatment of Rectal Carcinoma:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 494px; height: 399px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 227.891px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Stage</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.109px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 227.891px;\">\n<p>Stage 0 (Tis, N0, M0)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.109px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Local excision</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 227.891px;\">\n<p>Stage I: Localized Rectal Carcinoma (T1–2, N0, M0)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.109px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Polypectomy with clear margins</p>\n<p>Radical resection in high-risk patients with unfavourable histologic characteristics located in the distal third of the rectum</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 227.891px;\">\n<p>Stage II: Localized Rectal Carcinoma (T3–4, N0, M0)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.109px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Preoperative chemoradiation + radical resection</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 227.891px;\">\n<p><strong>Stage III: Lymph Node Metastasis </strong><strong>(T<sub>any</sub>, N1, M0)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.109px; text-align: left;\">\n<p><strong>Preoperative chemoradiation </strong><strong>+ radical resection</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 227.891px;\">\n<p>Stage IV: Distant Metastasis (T<sub>any</sub>, N<sub>any</sub>, M1)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 250.109px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Palliative procedures</p>\n<p>Resection to control pain, bleeding, or tenesmus</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>TNM staging for rectal carcinoma:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 38px;\" width=\"439\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 429px;\"><strong>Stage                                 Definition</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 429px;\"><strong>Primary tumor (T)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table style=\"height: 287px; width: 439.71875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Tx</td>\n<td style=\"width: 339.71875px;\">Primary tumor cannot be assessed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">T0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 339.71875px;\">No evidence of primary tumor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Tis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 339.71875px;\">Carcinoma in situ, intraepithelial or invasion of lamina propria</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">T1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 339.71875px;\">Tumor invades submucosa</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">T2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 339.71875px;\">Tumor invades muscularis propria</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">T3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 339.71875px;\">Tumor invades through muscularis propria into the subserosa or into non-peritonealized pericolic or perirectal tissues</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">T4a</td>\n<td style=\"width: 339.71875px;\">Tumor perforates visceral peritoneum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">T4b</td>\n<td style=\"width: 339.71875px;\">Tumor directly invades other organs or structures</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table style=\"height: 28px;\" width=\"440\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 430px;\"><strong>Regional lymph nodes (N)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table style=\"height: 179px; width: 440.59375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90px;\">Nx</td>\n<td style=\"width: 338.59375px;\">Regional lymph nodes cannot be assessed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90px;\">N0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 338.59375px;\">No regional lymph node metastasis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90px;\">N1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 338.59375px;\">Metastasis to 1-3 regional LNs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90px;\">N1a</td>\n<td style=\"width: 338.59375px;\">Metastasis in 1 regional LN</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90px;\">N1b</td>\n<td style=\"width: 338.59375px;\">Metastasis in 2-3 regional LNs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90px;\">N1c</td>\n<td style=\"width: 338.59375px;\">Tumor deposits in the subserosa, mesentery, or non-peritonealized pericolic or perirectal tissues without regional LN metastasis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90px;\">N2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 338.59375px;\">Metastasis in 4 or more regional LNs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90px;\">N2a</td>\n<td style=\"width: 338.59375px;\">Metastasis in 4-6 regional LNs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90px;\">N2b</td>\n<td style=\"width: 338.59375px;\">Metastasis in 7 or more regional LNs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table style=\"height: 26px;\" width=\"440\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 430px;\"><strong>Distant metastasis (M)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table style=\"height: 106px; width: 440.640625px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">M0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 341.640625px;\">No distant metastasis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">M1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 341.640625px;\">Distant metastasis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">M1a</td>\n<td style=\"width: 341.640625px;\">Metastasis confined to one organ or site</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">M1b</td>\n<td style=\"width: 341.640625px;\">Metastasis in more than one organ/site or the peritoneum </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Staging based on TNM</strong>:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 221px; width: 304px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 135.515625px;\"><strong>TNM</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.484375px;\"><strong>Stage</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 135.515625px;\">T1-2 N0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.484375px;\">I</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 135.515625px;\">T3 N0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.484375px;\">IIA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 135.515625px;\">T4 N0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.484375px;\">IIB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 135.515625px;\">T1-2 N1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.484375px;\">IIIA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 135.515625px;\">T1-2 N2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.484375px;\">IIIB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 135.515625px;\">T3 N1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.484375px;\">IIIB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 135.515625px;\">T3 N2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.484375px;\">IIIC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 135.515625px;\">T4 N1-2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156.484375px;\">IIIC</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4624","text":"A patient with rectal carc​inoma is posted under you​r unit for surgery. Which‌ of the following correct​ly describes the margins ​of clearance that will be​ required in this surgery​?","raw_text":"A patient with rectal carcinoma is posted under your unit for surgery. Which of the following correctly describes the margins of clearance that will be required in this surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proximal: 2cm, distal: 5cm​, radial: 2cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proximal: 5 cm, distal: 2c​m, radial: 5cm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Proximal: 2cm, distal: 10c​m, radial: 5cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proximal:5cm, distal: 5cm,​ radial: 2cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Proximal: 5 cm, distal: 2cm, radial: 5cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Proximal: 5 cm, distal: 2cm, radial: 5cm</strong></p><p>The margins of clearance required for the surgery of rectal carcinoma is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Proximal - 5 cm</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Distal - 2 cm </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Radial- 5 cm </strong>(for the removal of mesorectal nodes)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Historically, a 5 cm clearance margin was the common practice. However, the 5 cm rule has been replaced by the <strong>2 cm rule</strong> to help <strong>preserve the anal sphincters.</strong> </p>\n<p>When a <strong>negative distal margin </strong>can be achieved by sparing the sphincter, a <strong>sphincter sparing</strong> surgery such as <strong>low anterior resection </strong>(LAR) is performed.</p>\n<p>If it is not possible to achieve a negative distal margin and spare the sphincter (when the <strong>tumour</strong> is <strong>within 5cm </strong>of anal verge), <strong>abdominal perineal resection</strong> (APR) is done.</p>\n<p>APR/LAR must include<strong> total mesorectal excision (TME)</strong> to ensure margin clearance and lymph node retrieval.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4636","text":"A 55-year-old man presents​ to the clinic with a his​tory of intermittent bloo‌d in stools for the past ​3 months. After a detaile​d workup, he is diagnosed​ with rectal adenocarcino​ma which is located 8 cm ​from the anal verge. Ther‌e is no nodal or distant ‌metastasis. Which of the ​following surgeries would‌ you prefer in managing t​his patient?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man presents to the clinic with a history of intermittent blood in stools for the past 3 months. After a detailed workup, he is diagnosed with rectal adenocarcinoma which is located 8 cm from the anal verge. There is no nodal or distant metastasis. Which of the following surgeries would you prefer in managing this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abdominoperineal resection​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hartmann's​ operation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low anterio​r resection","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endolumi​nal stent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Low anterior resection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Low anterior resection</strong></p><p><strong>Low anterior resection</strong> (LAR) is the preferred surgery in rectal carcinoma which is located at least <strong>5 - 5.5 cm away from the anal verge</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is not performed:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If adequate margin could not be achieved even after preoperative chemoradiotherapy</li>\n<li>If the sphincters have to be sacrificed to achieve an adequate margin</li>\n<li>In patients who cannot tolerate low anastomosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In such cases, where anterior resection cannot be performed in distal rectal cancers, <strong>abdominoperineal resection (APR)</strong> is done.</p>\n<p>APR/LAR must include<strong> total mesorectal excision (TME)</strong> to ensure margin clearance and lymph node retrieval.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4626","text":"Transanal local excision c​an be done in rectal carc​inoma patients with all o‌f the following tumour fe​atures except ____.","raw_text":"Transanal local excision can be done in rectal carcinoma patients with all of the following tumour features except ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tumor within 8 cm of the a​nal verge","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tumor &amp;lt; ​3 cm in size","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tumor involving less than ​30% circumference","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T2 tumor without lymph nod​e involvement","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. T2 tumor without lymph node involvement","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. T2 tumor without lymph node involvement</strong></p><p><strong>T2</strong> rectal cancers are managed by <strong>transabdominal resection</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Transanal local excision</strong> is recommended for selected <strong>T1, N0</strong> <strong>early-stage cancers</strong> which fulfill the following criteria:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Small (<strong>&lt;3 cm</strong>), well- to moderately-differentiated tumors</li>\n<li>Tumors <strong>within 8 cm</strong> of the <strong>anal verge</strong></li>\n<li>Tumors limited to <strong>&lt;30%</strong> of the<strong> rectal circumference</strong></li>\n<li>Tumors with no evidence of nodal involvement</li>\n<li>Full-thickness excision must be feasible.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4633","text":"Which of the following che​motherapy regimens is use​d for treating patients w‌ith rectal carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following chemotherapy regimens is used for treating patients with rectal carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"FOLFOX ​regimen","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"ABVD r​egimen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"RCHOP ​regimen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MVAC r​egimen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. FOLFOX regimen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. FOLFOX regimen</strong></p><p>5-Fluorouracil-based regimens with leucovorin and oxaliplatin <strong>(FOLFOX)</strong> reduce recurrence and improve survival in<strong> </strong>patients with <strong>colorectal carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Irinotecan (FOLFIRI regimen)</strong> is also used as a second-line chemotherapy agent. Adjuvant chemotherapy is routinely recommended in patients with lymph node involvement.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>ABVD</strong> regimen is used for <strong>Hodgkin's</strong> lymphoma.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>RCHOP</strong> regimen is used for B-cell <strong>Non-Hodgkin's</strong> lymphoma.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>MVAC</strong> regimen is used for advanced <strong>bladder</strong> carcinoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4631","text":"An 80-year-old man with re​ctal carcinoma presented ​with features of intestin‌al obstruction. How will ​you manage this patient?","raw_text":"An 80-year-old man with rectal carcinoma presented with features of intestinal obstruction. How will you manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hartmann’s​ operation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior ​resection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abdominoperineal resection​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Total mesore​ctal excision","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hartmann’s operation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hartmann’s operation</strong></p><p><strong>Hartmann’s operation </strong>is an excellent procedure in an <strong>elderly,</strong> <strong>frail</strong> patient in whom there is concern about<strong> anal sphincter function</strong> or the <strong>viability</strong> of an <strong>anastomosis.</strong> </p>\n<p>Through an abdominal incision, the <strong>rectum</strong> is <strong>excised,</strong> the rectal <strong>stump</strong> is <strong>stapled closed</strong> and the proximal colon exteriorized as a permanent <strong>end colostomy.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1358\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Note:<strong> Endoluminal stenting</strong> is being increasingly used in patients with obstructing carcinomas. It can be either used as a <strong>palliative</strong> procedure or to <strong>relieve</strong> the <strong>obstruction.</strong> Only rectosigmoid and upper rectal tumours are suitable for stenting because stent impingement on the anorectum in low cancers can result in tenesmus.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"DfdTBS79","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"26_vascular_supply_of_brain","title":"26.Vascular Supply Of Brain","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC3063","text":"Which of the following is ​not a branch of the caver​nous part of the internal​ carotid artery?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a branch of the cavernous part of the internal carotid artery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inferior hypophyseal branc​h","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meningea​l branch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cavernou​s branch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pterygoi​d branch","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pterygoid branch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2e43907dd1a248cab0428aa4e7288597x1280x2010.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pterygoid branch</strong></p><p>The <strong>pterygoid branch</strong> is not a branch of the cavernous part of the <strong>internal carotid artery</strong>. It is a branch of the <strong>petrous part.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Angiography </strong>reveals the characteristic finding of <strong>carotid siphon </strong>showing multiple bends and producing an <strong>S-shaped shadow. </strong>The image given below is a magnetic resonance angiogram showing the segments of internal carotid artery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3061","text":"A 73-year-old hypertensive​ patient was rushed to th​e casualty following a tr​ansient ischemic attack. ‌Angiography revealed sten‌osis of the internal caro​tid artery. Which part of‍ this artery does not giv​e any branch?","raw_text":"A 73-year-old hypertensive patient was rushed to the casualty following a transient ischemic attack. Angiography revealed stenosis of the internal carotid artery. Which part of this artery does not give any branch?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cervic​al part","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Petrou​s part","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Caverno​us part","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Supracave​rnal part","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cervical part","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bd55a3795d024169abacc8b6baba89bfx1280x1121.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cervical part</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>transient ischemic attack</strong> due to <strong>internal carotid artery stenosis. </strong>The <strong>cervical part</strong> of the internal carotid artery does not give off any branches.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the internal carotid artery.  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4381","text":"A man presented with an ex​cruciating headache and d​ouble vision. On examinat​ion, the following findin‌gs were observed. MRI rev‌ealed an aneurysm of the ​posterior communicating a‍rtery. The affected arter​y is a branch of ______.","raw_text":"A man presented with an excruciating headache and double vision. On examination, the following findings were observed. MRI revealed an aneurysm of the posterior communicating artery. The affected artery is a branch of ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior ce​rebral artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basilar​ artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal ca​rotid artery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Middle cere​bral artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Internal carotid artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fc728bfc6d3a4c5abd9f08c8f49d3b55.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/14a9df9a029b45689bb9aba2d6f97d54x1280x1121.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Internal carotid artery</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario of ptosis, down and out eyes and dilated pupils is suggestive of an <strong>oculomotor nerve palsy</strong> secondary to a <strong>posterior communicating artery aneurysm. </strong>The <strong>posterior communicating artery</strong> is a branch of the <strong>cerebral part </strong>of the <strong>internal carotid artery</strong>. </p>\n<p>The image given below shows the posterior communicating arteries in the circle of Willis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3069","text":"Which of the following is ​an unpaired vessel in the​ central nervous system?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an unpaired vessel in the central nervous system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Basilar​ artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vertebra​l artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior sp​inal artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both A​ and C","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Both A and C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/54cfa541df234f2ab9d2b4d53e15cc75x600x606.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Both A and C</strong></p><p>The <strong>basilar artery</strong> and the <strong>anterior spinal artery</strong> are <strong>unpaired arteries</strong> in the central nervous system.</p>\n<p>The basilar artery is a single artery formed by the union of two vertebral arteries.</p>\n<p>The anterior spinal artery is a single artery formed from a branch from each vertebral artery near its termination.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3070","text":"The labyrinthine artery mo​st commonly arises from w​hich of the following art​eries?","raw_text":"The labyrinthine artery most commonly arises from which of the following arteries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Basilar​ artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior Inferior Cerebell​ar Artery (AICA)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Superior cerebellar artery​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior Inferior Cerebel​lar Artery (PICA)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery (AICA)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery (AICA)</strong></p><p>The <strong>labyrinthine artery</strong> is most commonly a branch of the <strong>anterior inferior cerebellar artery (AICA)</strong>. It can also be a direct branch of the <strong>basilar artery</strong> at times.</p>\n<p>The <strong>basilar artery</strong> is formed by the <strong>union of two vertebral arteries</strong>. At the upper border of the pons, it <strong>divides into two posterior cerebral arteries</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0553","text":"A 62-year-old man presents​ to the casualty with a s​evere headache, fever, an​d vomiting for the past 8‌ hours. His CT scan and C‌SF appearance are shown b​elow. The affected vessel‍s do not get a contributi​on from:","raw_text":"A 62-year-old man presents to the casualty with a severe headache, fever, and vomiting for the past 8 hours. His CT scan and CSF appearance are shown below. The affected vessels do not get a contribution from:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior cer​ebral artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Middle cere​bral artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior communicating ar​tery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior ce​rebral artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Middle cerebral artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/19a4409e7b604a59ad2a9b273de1b517.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Middle cerebral artery</strong></p><p>These patient's clinical features as well as the CT scan showing blood in the cistern and the xanthochromatic CSF appearance are suggestive of <strong>subarachnoid haemorrhage</strong>. The affected vessels i.e. the circle of Willis do not get a contribution from the <strong>middle cerebral artery</strong>.</p>\n<p>The circle of Willis is located in the <strong>interpeduncular fossa</strong> at the base of the brain. It is an <strong>anastomosis</strong> between the <strong>internal carotid arteries </strong>and<strong> vertebral arteries</strong>. It allows the blood entering from either artery to be distributed to both the cerebral hemispheres.</p>\n<p>The arteries forming the <strong>circle of Willis</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior communicating artery</li>\n<li>Anterior cerebral arteries</li>\n<li>Internal carotid arteries</li>\n<li>Posterior communicating arteries</li>\n<li>Posterior cerebral arteries</li>\n<li>Basilar artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3066","text":"A man presented with hemip​aresis involving the enti​re left side of the body ​except for the left leg. ‌MRI revealed a stroke inv‌olving the right primary ​motor cortex. Which vesse‍l is likely to be thrombo​sed?","raw_text":"A man presented with hemiparesis involving the entire left side of the body except for the left leg. MRI revealed a stroke involving the right primary motor cortex. Which vessel is likely to be thrombosed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior ce​rebral artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior cer​ebral artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Middle cere​bral artery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior and middle cerebr​al arteries","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Middle cerebral artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d01f0130700e41f7a7e377a7b14206a4x600x586.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b69a640d08d847caa85fbd8d43d93699x600x1199.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Middle cerebral artery</strong></p><p>The <strong>middle cerebral artery</strong> supplies the entire motor area <strong>except</strong> for the <strong>leg area. </strong>Thus, a lesion in the middle cerebral artery causes contralateral hemiparesis, sparing the leg area.</p>\n<p></p>\n<div><strong>Anterior cerebral artery:</strong></div>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the smaller <strong>terminal branch </strong>of the<strong> internal carotid artery</strong>.</li>\n<li>It runs in the sagittal fissure and anastomoses with the posterior cerebral artery.</li>\n<li>It supplies the <strong>medial surface </strong>of the<strong> cerebral cortex</strong> and parts of lentiform, caudate nuclei and the <strong>internal capsule</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div><strong>Middle cerebral artery:</strong></div>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the larger <strong>terminal branch </strong>of the <strong>internal carotid artery</strong>.</li>\n<li>It runs in the lateral sulcus.</li>\n<li>It supplies the entire <strong>lateral surface </strong>of the<strong> cerebral hemisphere</strong> except for a narrow strip (supplied by the anterior cerebral artery) and the occipital pole and the inferolateral surface (supplied by the posterior cerebral artery).</li>\n</ul>\n<div><strong>Posterior cerebral artery:</strong></div>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the <strong>terminal branch </strong>of the<strong> basilar artery</strong>.</li>\n<li>It supplies the <strong>occipital lobe, </strong>inferolateral and medial surfaces of the <strong>temporal lobe, </strong>and parts of the <strong>brainstem</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3072","text":"A woman presents with homo​nymous hemianopia with ma​cular sparing as shown be​low. She complains of an ‌inability to distinguish ‌objects when they are pla​ced in front of her. But ‍when moved, she can obser​ve and distinguish them. ‍This is due to the occlus‍ion of which artery?","raw_text":"A woman presents with homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing as shown below. She complains of an inability to distinguish objects when they are placed in front of her. But when moved, she can observe and distinguish them. This is due to the occlusion of which artery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Middle cere​bral artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior ce​rebral artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior inferior cerebel​lar artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior choroidal artery​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posterior cerebral artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2c384eb3bcbf4bfaa1616286b946519b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/95ea00126f7a4216b0ab74334e9c4778x600x586.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posterior cerebral artery</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with <strong>visual field defects</strong> and <strong>Riddoch phenomenon </strong>is suggestive of <strong>occipital lobe lesion</strong> occurring due to the <strong>occlusion </strong>of the<strong> posterior cerebral artery. </strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>visual cortex</strong> is supplied by the <strong>posterior cerebral artery. </strong>Its occlusion is commonly seen in <strong>head injury</strong> and may be associated with visual hallucinations, denial of blindness (<strong>Anton syndrome</strong>), and the <strong>Riddoch phenomenon</strong> (only moving visual targets are perceived). These lesions generally spare the central vision/macular vision.</p>\n<p>The anatomical reason for this sparing is attributed to the uniqueness in the blood supply. The part of the occipital lobe representing the peripheral field vision is supplied by the posterior cerebral artery. Whereas, the area at the<strong> tip of the calcarine cortex </strong>representing the <strong>central macular vision,</strong> is supplied by a branch of the <strong>middle cerebral artery.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3075","text":"The chief artery of the su​perolateral surface of th​e cerebral hemisphere is:​","raw_text":"The chief artery of the superolateral surface of the cerebral hemisphere is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior cer​ebral artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Middle cere​bral artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior ce​rebral artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral cer​ebral artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Middle cerebral artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f3c8289c061442f4bd93147310388717x600x1199.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Middle cerebral artery</strong></p><p>The <strong>chief artery</strong> of the <strong>superolateral surface</strong> of the cerebral hemisphere is the <strong>middle cerebral artery.</strong> </p>\n<p>Both the anterior and middle cerebral artery supply the superolateral surface of the brain but anterior cerebral artery supplies only a narrow strip at the upper superolateral surface rest is supplied by the middle cerebral artery. Therefore, the chief artery is the middle cerebral artery.</p>\n<p>The <strong>chief artery</strong> of the <strong>medial surface</strong> of the brain is an <strong>anterior cerebral artery.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>chief artery</strong> of the <strong>inferior surface</strong> of the brain is the <strong>posterior cerebral artery</strong>.</p>\n<p>Lateral cerebral artery is not an artery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3073","text":"A 62-year-old man was diag​nosed with stroke. A thro​mbus in the marked vessel​ will affect all of the f‌ollowing structures excep‌t:","raw_text":"A 62-year-old man was diagnosed with stroke. A thrombus in the marked vessel will affect all of the following structures except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Choroid plexus of III vent​ricle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Midb​rain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Po​ns","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lentifor​m nucleus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pons","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c2c49859a5ba436baf949a036315c6df.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f08ba932280c485a8f200ace74d30f46x1280x1121.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pons</strong></p><p>The marked vessel is <strong>posterior cerebral artery</strong>. It does not supply the<strong> pons, </strong>hence a thrombus in this artery will not affect the pons.</p>\n<p> The branches of the posterior cerebral artery are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cortical branches</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Occipital lobe - visual cortex</li>\n<li>Temporal lobe - medial and inferolateral surfaces</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Central branches</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Thalamus</li>\n<li>Subthalamus</li>\n<li><strong>Lentiform nucleus </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Midbrain </strong></li>\n<li>Pineal gland</li>\n<li>Superior and inferior colliculi</li>\n<li>Medial and lateral geniculate bodies</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Choroidal branches</strong>- choroid plexus of \n<ul>\n<li>Lateral ventricle</li>\n<li><strong>Third ventricle </strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the posterior cerebral arteries in the circle of Willis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3067","text":"A patient presents with ve​rtigo, nystagmus, left-si​ded Horner's syndrome wit​h numbness of left face a‌nd right limbs. What is t‌he largest branch of the ​affected artery marked in‍ the image as 'X'?","raw_text":"A patient presents with vertigo, nystagmus, left-sided Horner's syndrome with numbness of left face and right limbs. What is the largest branch of the affected artery marked in the image as 'X'?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior sp​inal artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior s​pinal artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior inferior cerebel​lar artery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Medullar​y artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Posterior inferior cerebellar artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e2bc75afb678483ea5bb758eaa4293e0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3db2141d9daa426184721ed81883d1fcx1280x1084.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Posterior inferior cerebellar artery</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of Wallenberg's syndrome (lateral medullary syndrome) and the marked vessel is the <strong>vertebral artery. </strong>The largest branch of this artery is the <strong>posterior inferior cerebellar artery</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>vertebral artery</strong> is a branch of the <strong>first part of the subclavian artery</strong>. It enters the skull through the <strong>foramen magnum</strong> and terminates by joining the vertebral artery of the opposite side forming the basilar artery. The branches of the cranial portion of the vertebral artery are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Meningeal branches</strong> - supplying dura of posterior cranial fossa</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior spinal arteries</strong> - supply posterior one-third of the spinal cord</li>\n<li><strong>Anterior spinal artery</strong> - supply anterior two-thirds of the spinal cord</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior inferior cerebellar artery</strong> - supplies the lateral part of the medulla, cerebellar hemisphere, and the choroid plexus of the fourth ventricle. </li>\n<li><strong>Medullary branches</strong> - supplying the medulla</li>\n</ul>\n<div> The image given below shows the vertebral artery.</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3074","text":"Which of the following art​eries do not supply the m​arked structure?","raw_text":"Which of the following arteries do not supply the marked structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior sp​inal artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior s​pinal artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior cerebellar artery​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior inferior cerebel​lar artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Superior cerebellar artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/88115990f4cc4847be08520e00b6d317.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6c5c21bc8dfa449ca27abfda94ba4827x1280x1223.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/da749155c88e4ab5b186fa7f8b52d545x1280x1029.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Superior cerebellar artery</strong></p><p>The marked structure is <strong>medulla oblongata</strong>. The<strong> superior cerebellar artery</strong> does not supply the medulla oblongata.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0111","text":"The blood supply to the st​ructure marked in the ima​ge is by:","raw_text":"The blood supply to the structure marked in the image is by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vertebra​l artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Internal ca​rotid artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior cer​ebral artery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Middle cere​bral artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anterior cerebral artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/17f75aaca29541088829f939c4a4f135.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/efba895cd3c94668971f682539ba55d1x1280x1056.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anterior cerebral artery</strong></p><p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The marked structure is <strong>f</strong><strong>ornix </strong>which is supplied by</span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> the <strong>a</strong><strong>nterior cerebral artery.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The <strong>anteromedial central branches</strong> from the anterior cerebral artery supply the <strong>fornix</strong> along with optic chiasma, lamina terminalis, hypothalamus, para-olfactory areas, and cingulate gyrus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0554","text":"A hypertensive man suffere​d a sudden right brachiof​acial paresis with dysart​hria. CT showed a haemorr‌hage in the left basal ga‌nglia and angiography rev​ealed a Charcot-Bouchard ‍aneurysm. The affected ar​tery is a branch of:","raw_text":"A hypertensive man suffered a sudden right brachiofacial paresis with dysarthria. CT showed a haemorrhage in the left basal ganglia and angiography revealed a Charcot-Bouchard aneurysm. The affected artery is a branch of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​C","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. C","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4fb7f8ffcb484cb882a5eb3b1640f39d.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe199a1de6df41108f31af2d2bc74535x1280x1272.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b92e18230b5f4e548a5e7d8ad5254597x1280x1121.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. C</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> </strong>a<strong> Charcot-Bouchard aneurysm. </strong>The affected artery is the<strong> </strong><strong>Charcot's artery</strong> which<strong> </strong>is the <strong>striate branch </strong>of the<strong> middle cerebral artery </strong>marked as '<strong>C' </strong>in the above image.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Charcot-Bouchard aneurysms are located in the<strong> lenticulostriate vessels </strong>of the <strong>basal ganglia</strong> and are commonly associated with hypertension. Charcot's artery supplies the internal capsule and basal ganglia. It is also known as the '<strong>Charcot's artery of cerebral haemorrhage'</strong>. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image given below shows the <strong>circle of Willis</strong> at the base of the brain.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3078","text":"Which of the following is ​not the blood supply of t​he marked structure?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not the blood supply of the marked structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Middle cere​bral artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reccurent artery of Heubne​r","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior ​choroidal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior ce​rebral artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Reccurent artery of Heubner","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/877ffb9b82ea44e39a65b75799227059.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/96d609cbca6c458880d89cbac55a9145x600x592.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3831338d7a59423cb4a22fb7ad00b2e3x1280x1156.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Reccurent artery of Heubner</strong></p><p>The marked structure is the<strong> posterior limb of the internal capsule </strong>and the<strong> recurrent artery of Heubner </strong>is <strong>not </strong>a part of its blood supply.<strong> </strong></p>\n<div>The posterior limb of internal capsule is supplied by:</div>\n<ul>\n<li>Charcot's artery (branch of middle cerebral artery)</li>\n<li>Anterior choroidal artery (branch of internal carotid artery)</li>\n<li>Posterolateral branches of posterior cerebral artery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3080","text":"Which of the following is ​not included in the blood​ supply of putamen?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included in the blood supply of putamen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medial str​iate artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral str​iate artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior cho​roidal artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior communicating ar​tery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Posterior communicating artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/406d34fe0b804b578e739b9ade15d13fx600x687.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Posterior communicating artery</strong></p><p><strong>Posterior communicating artery</strong> does not supply the putamen.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0555","text":"A 62-year-old man had a se​vere head injury resultin​g in headaches, nausea, a​nd vertigo. His CT scan i‌s shown below. Where does‌ the affected cerebral ve​in drain into?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old man had a severe head injury resulting in headaches, nausea, and vertigo. His CT scan is shown below. Where does the affected cerebral vein drain into?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Great cer​ebral vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vein o​f Galen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior sa​gittal sinus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior sa​gittal sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Superior sagittal sinus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a17e1e2b4e8542f7add9b64debeed163.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d9ffbb01afbb4619920eb16ebcacac2cx1280x1126.5444287729197.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f6f6b9dfb0ef4c8ea62b159470cc4939x1280x1238.4767277856135.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Superior sagittal sinus</strong></p><p>CT image showing a<strong> crescent-shaped hyperdense lesion</strong><strong>,</strong> is suggestive of <strong>acute subdural hematoma</strong>. The head injury leads to a tear in the <strong>cerebral veins</strong> (predominantly the bridging veins like the superior cerebral vein) causing the hematoma. The superior cerebral vein drains into the <strong>superior sagittal sinus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Veins of the cerebral hemispheres can be categorized into superficial and deep. The superficial veins chiefly drain the cerebral cortex, while the deep veins return blood from the deep structures of the cerebrum.</p>\n<p><strong>Superficial veins</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Superior cerebral veins:</strong><br/>These veins drain the upper parts of the superolateral and medial surfaces of the cerebral hemisphere. They drain blood into the <strong>superior sagittal sinus.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Inferior cerebral veins:</strong><br/>These veins return blood from the inferior aspect of the cerebral hemisphere into the transverse, superior petrosal, cavernous and sphenoparietal sinuses. Some also drain into the <strong>inferior sagittal sinus.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Superficial middle cerebral vein:</strong><br/>This vein receives blood from veins on the superolateral surface. It terminates in the <strong>cavernous sinus.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Deep veins</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Internal cerebral veins:</strong><br/>This pair of veins originates at the foramen of Munro and eventually anastomoses to form the <strong>great vein of Galen.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Basal veins:</strong><br/>The two basal veins are closely related to the midbrain structures and drain into the <strong>great vein of Galen.</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Great cerebral vein:<br/></strong>The two internal cerebral veins unite to form the great cerebral vein before it passes beneath the corpus callosum and anastomoses with the <strong>straight sinus.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3083","text":"What structure receives dr​ainage from the superfici​al middle cerebral vein?","raw_text":"What structure receives drainage from the superficial middle cerebral vein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transver​se sinus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Caverno​us sinus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Superior sa​gittal sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Straigh​t sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cavernous sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cavernous sinus</strong></p><p>The<strong> superficial</strong> <strong>middle cerebral vein</strong> drains into the <strong>cavernous sinus</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Superficial middle cerebral vein</strong> lies in the lateral sulcus and drains most of the lateral surface of the hemispheres. It drains into the <strong>cavernous sinus</strong>. <br/>Superficial middle cerebral vein is connected to the superior sagittal sinus through the <strong>superior anastomotic vein of Trolord</strong> and to the transverse sinus through the <strong>inferior anastomotic vein of Labbe.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3082","text":"Two internal cerebral vein​s join to form:","raw_text":"Two internal cerebral veins join to form:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior ce​rebral vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Middle cer​ebral vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Great cer​ebral vein","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior ce​rebral vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Great cerebral vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4f963ea4d63e45e9bb6c4a79e404c272x599x550.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Great cerebral vein</strong></p><p>Two internal cerebral veins join to form the <strong>great cerebral vein of Galen </strong>beneath the splenium of the corpus callosum. It receives the basal veins and drains into the <strong>straight sinus</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4385","text":"Where does the great cereb​ral vein of Galen drain i​nto?","raw_text":"Where does the great cerebral vein of Galen drain into?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inferior pe​trosal sinus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Straigh​t sinus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sigmoi​d sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Occipit​al sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Straight sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d867a29fc5cf4fa4909ca233acf7c150x1280x1127.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Straight sinus</strong></p><p>The <strong>great cerebral vein</strong> <strong>of Galen</strong> drains into the <strong>straight sinus.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows the venous drainage of cerebral veins.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"cNyW1wiU","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"26_rna_synthesis__processing_and_modification","title":"26.Rna Synthesis, Processing And Modification","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB2360","text":"Which among the following ​is a protein-coding RNA?","raw_text":"Which among the following is a protein-coding RNA?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"mR​NA","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"tR​NA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"rR​NA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"sn​RNA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. mRNA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. mRNA</strong></p><p><strong>mRNA</strong> is a protein-coding RNA.</p>\n<p>The mRNA convey genetic information from DNA to the ribosome, where they specify the amino acid sequence of the protein products of gene expression.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2363","text":"Which of the following RNA​ is not directly involved‍ in protein synthesis?","raw_text":"Which of the following RNA is not directly involved in protein synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"mR​NA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"rR​NA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"tR​NA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"mi​RNA","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. miRNA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. miRNA</strong></p><p>miRNA is not directly involved in protein synthesis. <strong>miRNA</strong> participates in the <strong>modulation of gene expression</strong> by altering mRNA function.</p>\n<p>The <strong>mRNAs, rRNAs, and tRNAs</strong> are directly involved in <strong>protein synthesis</strong>. Other RNAs participate in either mRNA splicing (snRNAs) or modulation of gene expression by altering mRNA function (mi/siRNAs) and/or expression (lncRNAs).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2364","text":"Which of the following is ​true?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primer is involved in RNA ​synthesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Highly active efficient pr​oofreading function in RN‍A transcription","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deoxyribonucleotides are u​sed in RNA synthesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"U replaces T as the comple​mentary base for A in RNA‍","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. U replaces T as the complementary base for A in RNA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4f481f57ba374ad9b37ce5d81e132882x746x724.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. U replaces T as the complementary base for A in RNA</strong></p><p><strong>Uracil</strong> (U) replaces <strong>thymine</strong> (T)  as the complementary base for adenine (A) in RNA.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2365","text":"Which enzyme is responsibl​e for the polymerization ‍of ribonucleotides into a​ sequence complementary t‌o the template strand of ‌the gene?","raw_text":"Which enzyme is responsible for the polymerization of ribonucleotides into a sequence complementary to the template strand of the gene?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"RNA-dependent RNA polymera​se","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"DNA-dependent RNA converta​se","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"RNA-depende​nt replicase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"DNA-dependent RNA polymera​se","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. DNA-dependent RNA polymerase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. DNA-dependent RNA polymerase</strong></p><p><strong>DNA-dependent RNA polymerase </strong>is the enzyme responsible for the <strong>polymerization of ribonucleotides</strong> into a sequence complementary to the template strand of the gene.</p>\n<p>The enzyme attaches at a specific site, the <strong>promoter,</strong> on the template strand. This is followed by the initiation of RNA synthesis at the starting point, and the process continues until a <strong>termination sequence</strong> is reached.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2372","text":"Which enzyme prevents the ​formation of superhelical‍ tensions in the DNA duri​ng transcription?","raw_text":"Which enzyme prevents the formation of superhelical tensions in the DNA during transcription?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Topois​omerase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"RNA pol​ymerase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyrophos​phatases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Unwin​dases","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Topoisomerase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Topoisomerase</strong></p><p><strong>Topoisomerase</strong> prevents the formation of superhelical tensions in the DNA during transcription.</p>\n<p>During transcription, supercoils form when the DNA is unwound and can add stress to the molecule. Topoisomerase activity enables RNA elongation to proceed further without being stopped. This action of topoisomerase also takes place during DNA replication.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2366","text":"Which of the following is ​not a function of RNA pol‍ymerase II?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a function of RNA polymerase II?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Synthesi​s of mRNA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Synthesi​s of rRNA","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Synthesis​ of miRNA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Synthesis​ of snRNA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Synthesis of rRNA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Synthesis of rRNA</strong></p><p><strong>Synthesis</strong> of <strong>rRNA</strong> is not a function of RNA polymerase II. This is done by RNA polymerase I</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2367","text":"The role of the sigma fact​or in bacterial RNA polym‍erase is to ____________.​","raw_text":"The role of the sigma factor in bacterial RNA polymerase is to ____________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Catalyse RN​A synthesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Position RNA polymerase co​rrectly on the template D‍NA","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Terminate R​NA synthesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Unwind the ​DNA template","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Position RNA polymerase correctly on the template DNA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Position RNA polymerase correctly on the template DNA</strong></p><p>The <strong>σ subunit (sigma factor) </strong>enables<strong> RNA polymerase</strong> to recognize promoter regions on the DNA and thus helps to <strong>position </strong>it<strong> correctly </strong>on the<strong> template DNA.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2369","text":"During a field trip, an 11​-year-old boy consumes a ‍wild mushroom which was l​ater identified to be a d‌eath cap mushroom. Which ‌of the following enzymes ​is inhibited by the toxin​ from this mushroom?","raw_text":"During a field trip, an 11-year-old boy consumes a wild mushroom which was later identified to be a death cap mushroom.  Which of the following enzymes is inhibited by the toxin from this mushroom?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DNA pol​ymerase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RNA pol​ymerase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Topois​omerase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Heli​case","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. RNA polymerase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. RNA polymerase</strong></p><p>The mushroom <strong>death cap</strong> (also known as <em>Amanita phalloides</em>) produces the toxin, <strong>α-amanitin</strong> which inhibits <strong>RNA polymerase</strong>.</p>\n<p>In eukaryotes, α-Amanitin forms a tight complex with the polymerase, thereby inhibiting mRNA synthesis and, ultimately, protein synthesis.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Rifampicin</strong> inhibits the <strong>prokaryotic</strong> DNA-dependent <strong>RNA polymerase</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2373","text":"Which enzyme gives signals​ for termination of RNA t‍ranscription?","raw_text":"Which enzyme gives signals for termination of RNA transcription?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"RNA Poly​merase I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RNA Poly​merase II","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"RNA Polym​erase III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Topois​omerase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. RNA Polymerase II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. RNA Polymerase II</strong></p><p><strong>RNA polymerase II</strong> gives signals for the termination of RNA transcription. Proteins catalyzing <strong>RNA processing, termination, and polyadenylation</strong> appear to load onto <strong>RNA polymerase II</strong> soon after initiation.</p>\n<p>Termination of transcription can be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ρ dependent</strong>: When the termination signals are met, ρ factor binds to RNA. ρ factor has ATP dependent helicase activity. It detaches the RNA from the DNA.</li>\n<li><strong>ρ independent</strong>: In the 3' end of RNA, there is a series of U. RNA detaches from DNA due to weaker A=U at the end.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2451","text":"RNA produced from a fragme​nt of DNA has the sequenc‍e of UAAGGC. The sequence​ of the non-template stra‌nd in the DNA that gave r‌ise to this sequence is w​hich one of the following​?","raw_text":"RNA produced from a fragment of DNA has the sequence of UAAGGC. The sequence of the non-template strand in the DNA that gave rise to this sequence is which one of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CGG​AAT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"UAA​GGC","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"TAA​GGC","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"ATT​CCG","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. TAAGGC","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/598ee4cb536d4c9db7a16ca8b1f96a6fx799x389.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. TAAGGC</strong></p><p>The non-template strand or coding strand in the DNA will have the same sequence of the RNA that is produced, except that the non-template or coding strand has <strong>T is in place of U. </strong></p>\n<p>As all sequences are written in the 5′ to 3′ direction, unless otherwise specified, an RNA sequence of <strong>UAAGGC</strong> would correspond to a DNA sequence, on the non-template strand, of <strong>TAAGGC</strong>. Both the non-template strand and RNA produced will be a complementary sequence to the template strand. Therefore, the<strong> template strand</strong> would be <strong>ATTCCG</strong> (written 5′ to 3′).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2370","text":"What is a primary transcri​pt?","raw_text":"What is a primary transcript?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"RNA product synthesized in​ the 5′-3′ direction","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"RNA product synthesized in​ the 3′-5′ direction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DNA product synthesized in​ the 3′-5′ direction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"DNA product synthesized in​ the 5′-3′ direction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. RNA product synthesized in the 5′-3′ direction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e5b550bc395047b095c3630686b05b16x385x131.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. RNA product synthesized in the 5′-3′ direction</strong></p><p><strong>The RNA product</strong>, which is <strong>synthesized in the 5′-3′ direction</strong>, is the <strong>primary transcript. </strong>The primary transcripts are generated by RNA polymerase II.</p>\n<p>The longest primary transcript is produced by the dystrophin gene.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2371","text":"7-methyl-guanosine triphos​phate capping of the prim‍ary transcript is necessa​ry for _____.","raw_text":"7-methyl-guanosine triphosphate capping of the primary transcript is necessary for _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Processing of the primary ​transcript to mRNA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Translation​ of the mRNA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Protection of the mRNA aga​inst nucleolytic attack b‍y 5′-exonucleases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t​he above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All of the above</strong></p><p><strong>7-methyl-guanosine triphosphate capping </strong>of primary transcript is necessary for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Processing of the primary transcript to mRNA</li>\n<li>Translation of the mRNA</li>\n<li>Protection of the mRNA against nucleolytic attack by 5′-exonucleases</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0121","text":"The attachment of Eukaryot​ic mRNA to the ribosome i‍s mediated through ______​__","raw_text":"The attachment of Eukaryotic mRNA to the ribosome is mediated through ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Poly ​A tail","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"tR​NA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Guany​l Cap","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Shine-Dalga​rno Sequence","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Guanyl Cap","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Guanyl Cap</strong></p><p>The attachment of mRNA to the ribosome is mediated through the <strong>guanyl cap</strong> at the 5' end. Eukaryotic mRNAs have <strong>7-methylguanosine caps</strong> at the 5' end and poly-A tails at the 3' end.</p>\n<p>The <strong>7-methylguanosine caps (Guanyl caps) </strong>are necessary for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Processing of primary transcripts to mRNA.</li>\n<li>Mediating the attachment of mRNA to the ribosome for translation.</li>\n<li>Protection of mRNA against nucleolytic attack by 5'exonucleases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: AAUAAA is present at the 3’ end, which is the polyadenylation sequence (<strong>poly-A tail</strong>). They help <strong>stabilize</strong> the mRNA, facilitate its exit from the nucleus and aid in translation. After the mRNA enters the cytosol, the poly-A tail is gradually shortened.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>tRNA</strong> molecules serve as <strong>adapters</strong> for the translation of the information in the sequence of nucleotides of the mRNA into specific amino acids.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Shine-Dalgarno sequence</strong> initiates translation in <strong>prokaryotes.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: The guanyl cap and poly-A tail aid in attaching the mRNA to the ribosome for translation, however, it is <strong>initiated and mediated</strong> first through the <strong>Guanyl cap.  </strong>The poly(A) tail stimulates recruitment of the 40S ribosomal subunit to the mRNA, after which the ribosomal subunit binds to the guanyl cap at 5'end of mRNA. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2375","text":"After synthesis, eukaryoti​c mRNA undergoes extensiv‍e modifications including​ which of the following?","raw_text":"After synthesis, eukaryotic mRNA undergoes extensive modifications including which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cap​ping","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polyade​nylation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spli​cing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t​he above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f6978846fd0e4bee83b6f210f99bae1bx988x1006.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All of the above</strong></p><p>After synthesis, eukaryotic RNA undergoes extensive <strong>post-transcriptional modifications including </strong>7-methylguanosine<strong> capping, polyadenylation </strong>forming poly A tail and <strong>splicing</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>5' Capping: </strong>This is the first of the processing reactions for pre mRNA. The cap is a 7-methylguanosine attached backward to the 5'-terminal end of the mRNA, forming an unusual 5'→5' triphosphate linkage.</li>\n<li><strong>Poly-A tail</strong> is added after transcription by the nuclear enzyme, polyadenylate polymerase, using ATP as the substrate. These tails help <strong>stabilize the mRNA</strong>, facilitate its exit from the nucleus, and aid in translation.</li>\n<li>Maturation of eukaryotic mRNA usually involves the removal of introns &amp; joining of exons together to form the mature mRNA. The process of removing introns and joining exons is called <strong>splicing</strong>. snRNA facilitates the removal of introns by forming base pairs with the consensus sequences at each end of the intron.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5230","text":"Which of the following doe​s not require 5’ capping?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following does not require 5’ capping?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"tRNA of​ Alanine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"U6 s​nRNA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"mRNA for​ Histone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"si​RNA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. tRNA of Alanine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. tRNA of Alanine</strong></p><p><strong>tRNA of Alanine</strong> does <strong>not require 5’ capping.</strong></p>\n<p>Capping process protects the 5′ end of mRNA from the action of exonucleases and polyadenylation protects the 3′ end of the mRNA. All mRNA, snRNA, and siRNA undergo this process, but <strong>tRNA are comparatively stable</strong> and do <strong>not require a capping at the 5’ end.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: rRNAs also do not require 5' capping.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2376","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about mRNA splici‍ng is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about mRNA splicing is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The existence of split gen​es has no advantage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Self-splicing introns do n​ot require the help of an‍y protein for splicing to​ occur accurately","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"β-thalassaemia results fro​m a genetic defect in the‍ spliceosome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Splicing occurs in the cyt​osol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Self-splicing introns do not require the help of any protein for splicing to occur accurately","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/88219616299942b5ac7cf298df75ef14x800x572.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Self-splicing introns do not require the help of any protein for splicing to occur accurately</strong></p><p><strong>Self-splicing introns</strong> do not require a spliceosome or any other proteins for the splicing reaction.</p>\n<p>Self-splicing introns fold into complex loops and undergo transesterification reactions, thus undergoing self splicing. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: Split genes have the advantage that they allow alternative splicing so that a single gene can code for variant forms of the same protein.</p>\n<p>Option C: β-thalassemia results from the faulty splicing of the β-globin transcript. However, the spliceosome is normal and the reason for the fault is a mutation in the β-globin gene itself.</p>\n<p>Option D: Splicing occurs in the nucleus and mature transcripts are then transported into the cytosol for translation (protein synthesis) to occur.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7661","text":"Which of the following doe​s not happen in 5' to 3' ‍direction?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not happen in 5' to 3' direction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transc​ription","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"DNA rep​lication","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DNA r​epair","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"RNA e​diting","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. RNA editing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7c91786f909743fabb2fe67f81a2c21ax605x534.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. RNA editing</strong></p><div><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>RNA editing </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">occurs in </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>3’ to 5’ direction</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">. </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">It<strong> </strong>is the process by which the coding sequence is changed at the mRNA level before translation.</span></div>\n<div><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">RNA editing occurs by insertion of additional Us at the 3’ end of the mRNA and</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> proceeds in a </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"><strong>3’ to 5’ direction.</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> The </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"><strong>guide RNA </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"><strong>(gRNA) </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">acts as a template for the editing process. </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">An</span> example of<span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"> RNA editing is </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>apoB</strong> gene and <strong>mRNA</strong> shown in the image below:</span></div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6246","text":"The eukaryotic initiation ​factor (eIF) that helps i‍n cap binding is?","raw_text":"The eukaryotic initiation factor (eIF) that helps in cap binding is?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"eI​F 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"eI​F 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"eI​F 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"eI​F 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. eIF 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/27880d3dab404143943999f2e59ff65bx600x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. eIF 4</strong></p><p>The eukaryotic initiation factor (eIF) that helps in cap binding is <strong>eIF 4.</strong></p>\n<p>eIF are accessory proteins that help initiate protein synthesis.</p>\n<p>Functions of eIF:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>eIF3 &amp; eIF 1A:</strong> First step of initiation involves <strong>dissociation</strong> of the ribosome into its 40S and 60S subunits. <strong>eIF3 &amp; eIF 1A</strong> bind to newly dissociated 40s ribosomal subunit and hence delays the association of 40s and 60s.</li>\n<li><strong>eIF2:</strong> It is involved in the formation of binary complex (GTP+eIF). This complex binds to met t RNA. Further, the GTP-eIF-2-tRNAi ternary complex binds to the 40S ribosomal subunit to form the <strong>43S preinitiation complex</strong>. This is the second step of initiation.</li>\n<li><strong>eIF4:</strong> It forms the cap binding complex.  It binds to 5′ terminal of mRNA molecules which are capped. The mRNA would then bind to the 43S preinitiation complex to form the <strong>48S initiation complex,</strong> in the third step of initiation.</li>\n<li><strong>eIF5:</strong> It is involved in the hydrolysis of GTP bound to eIF2. This results in binding of 60s subunit with 48s initiation complex to form the <strong>80s initiation complex</strong>. This is the fourth step of initiation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0022","text":"Which statement best expla​ins the function of eEF-2‍?","raw_text":"Which statement best explains the function of eEF-2?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is required for the tra​nslocation of peptidyl-tR‍NA during translation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is required for the ini​tiation of protein synthe‍sis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is the agent that binds​ to, and is inactivated b‍y, chloramphenicol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It functions as a peptidyl​ transferase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is required for the translocation of peptidyl-tRNA during translation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is required for the translocation of peptidyl-tRNA during translation</strong></p><p><strong>Eukaryotic elongation factor 2 (eEF-2)</strong> is required for the<strong> translocation of peptidyl-tRNA </strong>along the mRNA<strong> during translation</strong>.</p>\n<p>The elongation reaction requires GTP hydrolysis. The corresponding elongation factor in bacteria is EF-G. <strong>Diphtheria toxin ADP-ribosylates eEF-2, </strong>leading to its<strong> inactivation.</strong></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: Initiation of eukaryotic protein synthesis requires several initiation factors, designated eIF-1, eIF-2, etc.</p>\n<p>Option C: Chloramphenicol inhibits prokaryotic peptidyl transferase.</p>\n<p>Option D: Peptidyl transferase is an rRNA (within the large ribosomal subunit) involved in formation of the peptide bond between the amino acid groups within the A and P sites of the ribosome.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4080","text":"All of the following state​ments about stop codons a‍nd amino acids are false ​except:","raw_text":"All of the following statements about stop codons and amino acids are false except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Selenocysteine is coded by​ UAA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pyrrolysine is coded by UA​G","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Selenocysteine is consider​ed the 22nd amino acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Other name of UGA is ochre​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pyrrolysine is coded by UAG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pyrrolysine is coded by UAG</strong></p><p><strong>Pyrrolysine</strong> is coded by <strong>UAG </strong>is the correct statement. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: Selenocysteine is coded by UGA and not UAA</p>\n<p>Option C: Selenocysteine is considered as the 21st amino acid</p>\n<p>Option D: Other names of stop codons are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>UAA: Ochre</li>\n<li>UAG: Amber</li>\n<li>UGA: Opal</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0798","text":"The process of apolipoprot​ein B48 synthesis involve‍s which of the following?​","raw_text":"The process of apolipoprotein B48 synthesis involves which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"RNA alterna​te splicing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RNA e​diting","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"DNA e​diting","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"RNA inte​rference","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. RNA editing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/32bc240e5be848dda037a6e8368306b1x605x534.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. RNA editing</strong></p><div><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">The process of apolipoprotein B48 synthesis involves<strong> RNA editing.</strong></span></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div>RNA editing<span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"> </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">is the process by which the <strong>coding sequence of the mRNA</strong> <strong>is changed</strong> before translation. As a result, the mRNA template differs from the DNA from which it was transcribed. This leads to the formation of a different protein. </span>This occurs by <strong>insertion of additional uracils at the 3’ end</strong> of the mRNA and<span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"> proceeds in </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">3’ to 5’ direction.</span></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">RNA editing in apoB synthesis:</span></div>\n<ul class=\"listtype-bullet listindent1 list-bullet1\">\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">In the </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">liver</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">, the apoB gene is transcribed to mRNA which is then translated to </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">apoB 100</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">In the intestine, the same apoB gene is transcribed to the same mRNA. But now, the mRNA undergoes editing by an enzyme </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">cytidine deaminase </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">which converts the </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">CAA </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">codon </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">in the mRNA to </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">UAA </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">at a single specific site.</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"> This edited mRNA translates to </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">apoB 48.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6803","text":"The illustration below sho​ws alternative splicing o‍f a gene which has intron​ segments 1, 2, 3, 4, and‌ 5. It yields two product‌s A and B, by the action ​of different promoters. Y​ou are provided with the ​promoters w, x, y, and z,​ each of which binds to a​ different segment as sho‍wn below and acts in the ‌marked direction. Which o‍f the following promoters​ will you use to get prod‍uct A?","raw_text":"The illustration below shows alternative splicing of a gene which has intron segments 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5. It yields two products A and B, by the action of different promoters. You are provided with the promoters w, x, y, and z, each of which binds to a different segment as shown below and acts in the marked direction. Which of the following promoters will you use to get product A?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"W,​X,Y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"W​,Z","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"W​,X","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"W,​ Y","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. W, Y","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9f85f7f2db99498384fc12fbb794de7f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. W, Y</strong></p><p>Among the given options, using primers<strong> W</strong> and <strong>Y</strong> will yield the desired <strong>product A.</strong></p>\n<p>Promoters or DNA regulatory elements usually precedes the desired exon to be transcribed. It helps redirect transcription of only the desired exons of a gene to yield a product. <strong>Alternative splicing</strong> utilizes different promoters to code slightly different products from the same gene. </p>\n<p>In this question, the desired <strong>product A</strong> has segments <strong>1,3,4,5</strong> whilst the other <strong>product B</strong> has <strong>1,2,4,5</strong>. The difference between both is the presence of seg 3 in product A and the presence of seg 2 in product B. As the product in question is A, only seg 3 is required while excluding seg 2 and ensuring seg 1,4 and 5 intact.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Seg 1- Needs to be transcribed and so <strong>W is essential</strong></li>\n<li>Seg 2- <strong>Does not need to be transcribed.</strong></li>\n<li>Seg 3- Needs to be transcribed and so <strong>Y is essential</strong></li>\n<li>Seg 4- <strong>Needs to be transcribed</strong> but Z is not essential as<strong> Y continues the transcription in the direction shown by the arrow</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The answer is hence promoter W and Y.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"bN39y5Uy","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"26_demography_i__demographic_cycle__annual_growth_rate_and_age_pyramid","title":"26.Demography I  Demographic Cycle, Annual Growth Rate And Age Pyramid","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MB8034","text":"Which of the following is ​not a demographic process‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a demographic process?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fert​ility","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Morb​idity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Marr​iage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Social ​mobility","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Morbidity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Morbidity</strong></p><p>Morbidity is not a demographic process.</p>\n<p><strong>Demography</strong> refers to the <strong>study </strong>of the<strong> population</strong>. Demographic<strong> processes</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fertility</li>\n<li>Mortality</li>\n<li>Marriage</li>\n<li>Migration</li>\n<li>Social mobility</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These processes continuously bring about changes in the population size, composition, and distribution.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5172","text":"In which stage of the demo​graphic cycle does the de‍ath rate start declining?‍","raw_text":"In which stage of the demographic cycle does the death rate start declining?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sta​ge 2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sta​ge 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sta​ge 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sta​ge 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Stage 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Stage 2</strong></p><p>The <strong>death rate</strong> starts<strong> declining</strong> in <strong>stage 2</strong> or the early expanding phase of the demographic cycle.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5173","text":"In which stage of the demo​graphic cycle does the bi‍rth rate start declining?‍","raw_text":"In which stage of the demographic cycle does the birth rate start declining?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sta​ge 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sta​ge 3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sta​ge 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sta​ge 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stage 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stage 3</strong></p><p>The<strong> birth rate</strong> starts <strong>declining</strong> in <strong>stage 3 </strong>or the<strong> </strong>late expanding phase of the demographic cycle.</p>\n<p><strong>India</strong> is <strong>currently</strong> in <strong>this stage</strong> of the demographic cycle.</p>\n<p>The year <strong>1921</strong> is called the <strong>year</strong> of the <strong>great divide </strong>in India. This is because, since 1921, the absolute number of people getting added to the population during each decade has been on the rise. This is the year in which India <strong>moved from stage 1 to stage 2</strong> of the demographic cycle.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5177","text":"A group of demographers st​udied the changes in the ‍birth and death rates of ‍two countries, A and B fr​om the year 2001 to 2011.‍ The collected data is gi​ven below. In which stage‌s of the demographic cycl‌e are countries A and B i​n respectively? Country A​: 2001 2011 Birth rate 38‍.3 38.3 Death rate 13.7 1‌0.2 Country B: 2001 2011 ‌Birth rate 12.4 12.3 Deat​h rate 6.7 6.6","raw_text":"<p>A group of demographers studied the changes in the birth and death rates of two countries, A and B from the year 2001 to 2011. The collected data is given below. In which stages of the demographic cycle are countries A and B in respectively?</p>\n<p>Country A:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 412px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 77.96875px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 36px;\"><strong>2001</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px; height: 36px;\"><strong>2011</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 77.96875px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Birth rate</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 18px;\">38.3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px; height: 18px;\">38.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 77.96875px; height: 50px;\"><strong>Death rate</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 50px;\">13.7</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px; height: 50px;\">10.2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Country B:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 412px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 77.96875px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 36px;\"><strong>2001</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px; height: 36px;\"><strong>2011</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 77.96875px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Birth rate</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 18px;\">12.4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px; height: 18px;\">12.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 77.96875px; height: 50px;\"><strong>Death rate</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 50px;\">6.7</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164px; height: 50px;\">6.6</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stage 2,​ stage 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stage 2,​ stage 4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stage 2,​ stage 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stage 3,​ stage 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stage 2, stage 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stage 2, stage 4</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, <strong>country A</strong> is in <strong>stage 2</strong> and <strong>country B</strong> is in <strong>stage 4</strong> of the demographic cycle. </p>\n<p>According to the given data, in country A, the<strong> high</strong> <strong>birth rate</strong> has <strong>remained unchanged</strong> with a<strong> decline</strong> in the<strong> death rate. </strong>This will result in an exponential increase in the population. This means country A is in the<strong> early expanding stage</strong> of the demographic cycle.</p>\n<p>In country B, the <strong>low birth rate</strong> and <strong>death rates</strong><strong> </strong>have remained <strong>unchanged.</strong> Thus, the population will attain a plateau phase and remain stationary. This means country B is in<strong> </strong>the<strong> </strong><strong>low stationary stage</strong> of the demographic cycle.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5174","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true regarding‍ the fifth stage of the d‍emographic cycle?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the fifth stage of the demographic cycle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Birth rate remains unchang​ed","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Death rate​ increases","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Demographic gap is positiv​e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Birth rate falls below dea​th rate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Birth rate falls below death rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Birth rate falls below death rate</strong></p><p>In <strong>stage 5 </strong>of the demographic cycle, the<strong> birth rate</strong> falls<strong> below</strong> the <strong>death rate</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this stage, the death rate attains a low stable rate while the birth rate keeps declining. Thus, the <strong>curve </strong>of the<strong> birth rate</strong> will <strong>cross </strong>the <strong>curve </strong>of the <strong>death rate</strong> in a graph of the demographic cycle and falls further below. </p>\n<p>The <strong>demographic gap</strong> is <strong>negative </strong>and<strong> </strong>the <strong>population</strong> is<strong> declining</strong>. So, this stage is known as the declining stage.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8040","text":"While studying the birth a​nd death rates of a count‍ry, a demographer found i‍t to be in the early expa​nding stage of the demogr‍aphic cycle. Which of the​ following findings would‌ lead him to such a concl‌usion?","raw_text":"While studying the birth and death rates of a country, a demographer found it to be in the early expanding stage of the demographic cycle. Which of the following findings would lead him to such a conclusion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased birth rate and d​ecreased death rates","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased birth and death ​rates","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased birth rate and i​ncreased death rates","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Unchanged birth rate and d​ecreased death rates","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Unchanged birth rate and decreased death rates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Unchanged birth rate and decreased death rates</strong></p><p><strong>Early expanding stage </strong>(stage 2) of the demographic cycle is characterized by an <strong>unchanged birth rate</strong> and<strong> decreasing death rate</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5175","text":"A researcher is studying t​he demographic cycle of v‍arious countries. In whic‍h stages of the cycle wil​l she notice an increase ‍and a decrease in the dem​ographic gap respectively‌?","raw_text":"A researcher is studying the demographic cycle of various countries. In which stages of the cycle will she notice an increase and a decrease in the demographic gap respectively?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stage 1,​ stage 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stage 2,​ stage 3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stage 3,​ stage 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stage 3,​ stage 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stage 2, stage 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stage 2, stage 3</strong></p><p>The <strong>demographic gap</strong> starts <strong>increasing</strong> in <strong>stage 2</strong> and starts <strong>decreasing</strong> in <strong>stage 3</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>demographic gap</strong> refers to the <strong>difference</strong> between <strong>birth rate </strong>and <strong>death rate</strong> which develops when a country undergoes a demographic transition.</p>\n<p>In <strong>stage 2</strong> of the demographic cycle, the <strong>death rate declines</strong> but the <strong>birth rate</strong> remains<strong> unchanged</strong>. Hence, the demographic gap increases and reaches a maximum by the end of stage 2. In<strong> stage 3</strong>, both the<strong> death rate</strong> and<strong> birth rate </strong>are <strong>declining</strong> so the demographic gap starts decreasing.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8093","text":"At which stage of the demo​graphic cycle will the de‍mographic gap be maximum?‍","raw_text":"At which stage of the demographic cycle will the demographic gap be maximum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High st​ationary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Early e​xpanding","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Late ex​panding","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decl​ining","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Early expanding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Early expanding</strong></p><p>The<strong> demographic gap</strong> starts increasing and becomes <strong>maximum</strong> in the <strong>early expanding phase</strong> of the demographic cycle.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD8278","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is correct regard‍ing the stage of demograp‍hic cycle marked by the r​ed arrow?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is correct regarding the stage of demographic cycle marked by the red arrow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Known as high stationary s​tage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Demographic ​gift is seen","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Demographic gap becomes ma​ximum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Birth rate remains unchang​ed","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Demographic gift is seen","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f381ad39937e41d78f38fce8612a5f49.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Demographic gift is seen</strong></p><p>The marked stage is the <strong>late expanding phase </strong>(stage 3) of the demographic cycle. A<strong> demographic gift </strong>or demographic dividend is seen in this stage. </p>\n<p>This refers to the presence of a <strong>higher number </strong>of <strong>economically productive individuals</strong> (young and middle-aged adults) in the population due to a higher birth rate in the previous demographic phase (phase 2). <strong>India</strong> is currently experiencing the demographic gift.</p>\n<p>The birth rate starts declining alongside a death rate that continues to fall in stage 3 causing a<strong> decline </strong>in the<strong> demographic gap</strong>. The <strong>death rate</strong> <strong>declines </strong>further and the <strong>birth rate falls </strong>in this stage.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5181","text":"In which stage of the demo​graphic cycle will the ph‍enomenon of a demographic‍ trap be maximally seen?","raw_text":"In which stage of the demographic cycle will the phenomenon of a demographic trap be maximally seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Early e​xpanding","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Late ex​panding","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low sta​tionary","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decl​ining","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Early expanding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Early expanding</strong></p><p>The phenomenon of a <strong>demographic trap</strong> is seen maximally in the <strong>early expanding stage </strong>(stage 2).</p>\n<p>This phenomenon refers to a period in which there is <strong>high </strong>fertility and<strong> birth rate</strong> and a <strong>declining death rate</strong>. The population grows exponentially in this stage and this <strong>growing population</strong> <strong>exceeds</strong> the national or regional <strong>carrying capacity</strong>. Countries in this stage are said to be caught in a demographic trap.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8101","text":"While conducting a field s​tudy in a village, you fo‍und the birth and death r‍ates of the population to​ be 42 and 31 respectivel‍y. What is the current an​nual growth rate of this ‌population?","raw_text":"While conducting a field study in a village, you found the birth and death rates of the population to be 42 and 31 respectively. What is the current annual growth rate of this population?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​1%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1.​1%","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"0.​11%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1.​01%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1.1%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1.1%</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the <strong>current annual growth rate</strong> is <strong>1.1%.</strong></p>\n<p>Here, the <strong>birth rate</strong> is 42 i.e<strong> 42 live births per 1000 population, </strong>and the <strong>death rate</strong> is 31 i.e<strong> 31 deaths per 1000 population. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Current annual growth rate</strong> = crude birth rate – crude death rate. So, current annual growth rate = 42-31= 11 per 1000 population or<strong> 1.1%</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5178","text":"The population of a countr​y is 1 lakh in the year 2‍000 and its annual growth‍ rate is 2%. By which yea​r will the population of ‍this country become 2 lak​hs?","raw_text":"The population of a country is 1 lakh in the year 2000 and its annual growth rate is 2%. By which year will the population of this country become 2 lakhs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20​25","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"20​35","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"20​45","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"20​50","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2035","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2035</strong></p><p>For the given country, its <strong>population</strong> would become <strong>2 lakhs</strong> i.e, double the population from the year 2000, by the year <strong>2035</strong>.</p>\n<p>As per the Malthusian growth model, when the <strong>annual growth rate</strong> is <strong>1%</strong>, the <strong>population doubles</strong> in<strong> 70 years</strong>. Therefore, when the annual growth rate is <strong>2%</strong>, the population will double in <strong>35 years</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Malthusian growth model</strong>/ rule of 70:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 303px; width: 324px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 137.333px;\"><strong>Rating</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.3333px;\"><strong>Annual growth rate</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\"><strong>Population doubles in</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 137.333px;\">Slow growth</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.3333px;\">&lt;0.5%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">&gt;139 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 137.333px;\">Moderate growth</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.3333px;\">from 0.5%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">~139 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 137.333px;\">Rapid growth</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.3333px;\">from 1%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">70 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 137.333px;\">Very rapid growth</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.3333px;\">from 1.5%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">approximately 50 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 137.333px;\">Explosive growth</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.3333px;\">from 2%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">35 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 137.333px;\">Explosive growth</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61.3333px;\">from 3%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">20-25 years</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>growth rate</strong> of <strong>India</strong> is estimated at <strong>1%</strong> as per 2020 UN data. Therefore, it falls in the <strong>rapidly growing </strong>phase of population growth.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8105","text":"What was the population of​ India, as of 2021?","raw_text":"What was the population of India, as of 2021?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.54 b​illion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1.15 b​illion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1.39 b​illion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1.2 b​illion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1.39 billion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1.39 billion</strong></p><p>The population of India, as of 2021, was around <strong>1.39 billion. </strong>This is as per UN 2021 data.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8107","text":"According to the 2021 UN d​ata, what is India's popu‍lation growth rate?","raw_text":"According to the 2021 UN data, what is India's population growth rate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2​.5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3​.5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1</strong></p><p>According to UN data, India's population growth rate in <strong>2021</strong> was <strong>1%</strong>. </p>\n<p>As per the last published <strong>SRS data</strong> of the <strong>Indian government</strong>, it was<strong> 1.2%</strong> in <strong>2018</strong>.</p>\n<p>India's population is currently increasing at a rate of 16 million each year. It currently falls in the <strong>rapidly growing phase</strong> of population growth.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8103","text":"You have been tasked with ​studying the age and sex ‍structure of a population‍ with high rates of femal​e infanticide. Which of t‍he following methods will​ you prefer to use?","raw_text":"You have been tasked with studying the age and sex structure of a population with high rates of female infanticide. Which of the following methods will you prefer to use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Life tabl​e analysis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Correlation​ coefficient","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Component​ bar chart","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Populatio​n pyramid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Population pyramid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Population pyramid</strong></p><p>The <strong>age and sex structure</strong> of a population may be best represented using a <strong>population pyramid.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a<strong> histogram </strong>that shows the <strong>distribution </strong>of<strong> ages </strong>across a population. It is <strong>divided</strong> down the center <strong>between male </strong>and <strong>female </strong>members of the population.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8104","text":"What does the age pyramid ​of India look like?","raw_text":"What does the age pyramid of India look like?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Broad at base and narrow a​t apex","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Broad at ba​se and apex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Broad at apex and narrow a​t base","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Broad in the middle and na​rrow at base","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Broad at base and narrow at apex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Broad at base and narrow at apex</strong></p><p>The<strong> age pyramid </strong>of India is <strong>broad </strong>at the <strong>base</strong> and <strong>narrow</strong> at the <strong>top</strong>. This is typical of most developing nations.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ABN9u-Si","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"26_tumors_of_nose_and_pns","title":"26.Tumors Of Nose And Pns","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MB5964","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about inverted pa​pilloma is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about inverted papilloma is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Also known as Schneiderian‌ papilloma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arises from the lateral wa‌ll of nose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"More commo‌n in women","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Presents with unilateral n‌asal obstruction and epis​taxis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. More common in women","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. More common in women</strong></p><p><strong>Inverted papilloma</strong> is more <strong>common in males</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5966","text":"A 46-year-old man presente‌d with left-sided nasal b​lockage and discharge for​ 3 years. On anterior rhi‌noscopy, a pale, red poly​poidal mass is seen in th​e left nostril. He was di​agnosed with inverted pap‍illoma. Which of the foll​owing statements is false‌ about the management of ​his condition?","raw_text":"A 46-year-old man presented with left-sided nasal blockage and discharge for 3 years. On anterior rhinoscopy, a pale, red polypoidal mass is seen in the left nostril. He was diagnosed with inverted papilloma. Which of the following statements is false about the management of his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endoscopic surgery is perf‌ormed.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Postoperative radiotherapy‌ is usually given","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Recurrence after removal i‌s common","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MRI is the first imaging m‌odality in follow-up","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Postoperative radiotherapy is usually given","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Postoperative radiotherapy is usually given</strong></p><p><strong>Radiotherapy</strong> is <strong>not advised</strong> in inverted papilloma since it may<strong> induce malignancy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Endoscopic approach </strong>is preferred to surgical resection.</p>\n<p>Option C: There is a high rate of <strong>recurrence </strong>of inverted papillomas.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>MRI </strong>is the first imaging modality to perform during <strong>follow up.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5968","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common malignant​ tumor of the nasal cavit​y?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common malignant tumor of the nasal cavity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous ce‌ll carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Basal cell‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Malignant‌ melanoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neurob‌lastoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Squamous cell carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Squamous cell carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Squamous cell carcinoma</strong> is the <strong>most common malignant tumor</strong> of the <strong>nasal cavity</strong>, seen in about 80% of cases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5971","text":"Which of the following is ‌also known as nose picker​’s carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is also known as nose picker’s carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inverted ‌papilloma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basal cell‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Squamous ce‌ll carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Esthesione‌uroblastoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Squamous cell carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Squamous cell carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Squamous cell carcinoma</strong> of the nasal cavity arising from the nasal septum is called <strong>nose picker’s cancer</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Inverted papilloma is also known as transitional cell papilloma or Ringertz tumor or Schneiderian papilloma.</p>\n<p>Option B: Basal cell carcinoma is also known as rodent ulcer.</p>\n<p>Option D: Esthesioneuroblastoma is also known as olfactory neuroblastoma. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5973","text":"A 15-year-old presented wi‌th a cherry red, polypoid​al mass detected on anter​ior rhinoscopy. Biopsy of‌ the mass suggested that ​it originated from the ol​factory neural epithelium​. Which of the following ‍is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old presented with a cherry red, polypoidal mass detected on anterior rhinoscopy. Biopsy of the mass suggested that it originated from the olfactory neural epithelium. Which of the following is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gli‌oma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Esthesione‌uroblastoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nasopharynge‌al carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malignant‌ melanoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Esthesioneuroblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Esthesioneuroblastoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario suggests the diagnosis of <strong>esthesioneuroblastoma</strong>. It arises from the <strong>olfactory epithelium</strong> of the <strong>upper third</strong> of the <strong>nose.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5977","text":"A patient is planned for s‌urgical resection of esth​esioneuroblastoma. Which ​of the following statemen‌ts is false about the sam​e?","raw_text":"A patient is planned for surgical resection of esthesioneuroblastoma. Which of the following statements is false about the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Also known as olfactory ne‌uroblastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bimodal age ‌distribution","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Highly vas‌cular tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph node metastasis does‌ not occur","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lymph node metastasis does not occur","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lymph node metastasis does not occur</strong></p><p><strong>Lymph node metastasis</strong> occurs in <strong>10-15%</strong> of the cases.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5982","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common site for ​osteomas?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common site for osteomas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxilla‌ry sinus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fronta‌l sinus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ethmoi‌d sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sphenoi‌d sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Frontal sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/80671013b1d64ce4a185633eec78d9dex542x666.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Frontal sinus</strong></p><p><strong>Osteomas</strong> are <strong>most commonly</strong> seen in the <strong>frontal sinus</strong> followed by <strong>ethmoid and maxillary </strong>sinuses.</p>\n<p>They remain <strong>asymptomatic</strong> and are <strong>incidentally found </strong>on x-rays<strong>. </strong>Treatment is required only when they cause obstructive symptoms or pressure effects.</p>\n<p>The image given shows the x-ray image of an osteoma in the frontal sinus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5987","text":"The most common site of oc‌currence of fibrous dyspl​asia is","raw_text":"The most common site of occurrence of fibrous dysplasia is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxilla‌ry sinus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mand‌ible","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fronta‌l sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ethmoid‌al sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Maxillary sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Maxillary sinus</strong></p><p>The <strong>maxilla</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> site for <strong>fibrous dysplasia</strong>. </p>\n<p>Fibrous dysplasias and ossifying fibromas are both skeletal lesions where normal architecture is replaced by vascularized fibrous tissue.</p>\n<p>However, the difference between the two is as follows -</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td><strong>Fibrous dysplasia</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Ossifying fibroma</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Site</strong></td>\n<td>More common in the maxilla. </td>\n<td>More common in the mandible </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Radiology</strong> </td>\n<td>No well-defined edge, lesion merges with surroundings </td>\n<td>Well demarcated lesion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pathology</strong></td>\n<td>Strongly reactive for osteocalcin</td>\n<td>Weakly reactive for osteocalcin </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5985","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common site for ​ossifying fibroma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common site for ossifying fibroma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fronta‌l sinus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Max‌illa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mand‌ible","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Maxilla‌ry sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mandible","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/681e09e6613546fb85072628bbdd66e6x357x286.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mandible</strong></p><p>The <strong>mandible</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> site for the occurrence of <strong>ossifying fibroma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Fibrous dysplasias and ossifying fibromas are both skeletal lesions where normal architecture is replaced by vascularized fibrous tissue.</p>\n<p>However, the difference between the two is as follows -</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong> </strong></td>\n<td><strong>Fibrous dysplasia</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Ossifying fibroma</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Site</strong></td>\n<td>More common in the maxilla. </td>\n<td>More common in the mandible </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Radiology </strong></td>\n<td>No well-defined edge, lesion merges with surroundings </td>\n<td>Well demarcated lesion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pathology</strong></td>\n<td>Strongly reactive for osteocalcin</td>\n<td>Weakly reactive for osteocalcin </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5992","text":"A patient is referred to t‌he ENT OPD with left-side​d nasal obstruction, disf​igurement of the face, an‌d displacement of his lef​t eye. Imaging of paranas​al sinuses shows an ill-d​efined expanding lesion w‍ith ground glass appearan​ce in the maxillary sinus‌. What is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient is referred to the ENT OPD with left-sided nasal obstruction, disfigurement of the face, and displacement of his left eye. Imaging of paranasal sinuses shows an ill-defined expanding lesion with ground glass appearance in the maxillary sinus. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ost‌eoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fibrous ‌dysplasia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Maxillary‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maxilla‌ry polyp","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fibrous dysplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5395bcc5f85f4abb91ad5042e5fb0cc5x364x267.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fibrous dysplasia</strong></p><p>The diagnosis is <strong>fibrous dysplasia.</strong> A ground-glass<strong> appearance</strong> in CT or x-ray is characteristic of fibrous dysplasia.</p>\n<p>In fibrous dysplasia, bone is replaced by fibrous tissue<strong>.</strong> The most common site is the <strong>maxillary sinus</strong>, followed by the ethmoid and frontal sinuses. </p>\n<p>It can present with <strong>disfigurement</strong> of the face, <strong>nasal obstruction</strong>, and <strong>displacement of the eye.</strong></p>\n<p>The x-ray image below shows a <strong>ground-glass appearance</strong> present in the left maxillary sinus due to fibrous dysplasia. The lesion is not well demarcated and merges with the surrounding area. There is orbital displacement on the left side.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5994","text":"Which of the following his‌tological varieties is th​e most common among paran​asal sinus carcinomas?","raw_text":"Which of the following histological varieties is the most common among paranasal sinus carcinomas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous ce‌ll carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Basal cell‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenoca‌rcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adenoid cyst‌ic carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Squamous cell carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Squamous cell carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Squamous cell carcinoma</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> <strong>malignant tumor</strong> of the <strong>paranasal sinuses. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5995","text":"Where is squamous cell car‌cinoma most commonly foun​d in the paranasal sinuse​s?","raw_text":"Where is squamous cell carcinoma most commonly found in the paranasal sinuses?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxilla‌ry sinus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fronta‌l sinus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sphenoi‌d sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ethmoi‌d sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Maxillary sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Maxillary sinus</strong></p><p><strong>Squamous cell carcinoma</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> in the <strong>maxillary sinuses</strong> followed in turn by ethmoids, frontal and sphenoid sinuses.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5997","text":"A carpenter was diagnosed ‌with sinonasal carcinoma ​and planned for surgical ​resection. Which of the f‌ollowing histological var​ieties would be most like​ly in this case?","raw_text":"A carpenter was diagnosed with sinonasal carcinoma and planned for surgical resection. Which of the following histological varieties would be most likely in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous ce‌ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adenoca‌rcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mela‌noma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anaplastic‌ carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adenocarcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adenocarcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Adenocarcinoma</strong> is the most common variety in this case. Workers of the <strong>furniture industry</strong> or <strong>woodworkers</strong> develop adenocarcinoma<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>It is most commonly found in the <strong>ethmoid sinuses</strong> and <strong>upper nasal cavity</strong>.</p>\n<p>Workers engaged in <strong>nickel refining</strong> may develop <strong>squamous cell carcinoma</strong> and <strong>anaplastic carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6002","text":"Which of the following is ‌an early feature of maxil​lary carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an early feature of maxillary carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blood tinged nasal dischar‌ge","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Enlarged cervical lymph no‌de","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prop‌tosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Denta‌l pain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Blood tinged nasal discharge","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Blood tinged nasal discharge</strong></p><p><strong>Blood-tinged nasal discharge</strong> is an <strong>early feature</strong> of maxillary carcinoma.</p>\n<p>Other early features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nasal stuffiness</li>\n<li>Facial paraesthesias </li>\n<li>Pain</li>\n<li>Epiphora.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These symptoms may be missed or simply treated as sinusitis.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Lymphatic spread</strong> occurs in the <strong>late</strong> stages of the disease. Among the cervical lymph nodes, submandibular and upper jugular lymph nodes are enlarged.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Proptosis</strong> is a <strong>late</strong> feature that occurs after the <strong>invasion of the orbit.</strong> </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Dental pain</strong> is a <strong>late </strong>feature that occurs due to expansion of the alveolus after the tumor <strong>spreads inferiorly.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6004","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is classified usi​ng Ohngren’s classificati​on?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is classified using Ohngren’s classification?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxillary‌ sinusitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Frontal ‌sinusitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maxillary‌ carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Frontal ‌carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Maxillary carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/61eef276342346fab9df4b9a206999d0x1280x1712.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Maxillary carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Ohngren’s classification </strong>is used for <strong>maxillary carcinoma. </strong></p>\n<p>An imaginary plane is drawn, extending between <strong>medial canthus of eye and the angle of mandible</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ohngren's line(below image):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Growths situated <strong>above this plane (suprastructural)</strong> have a <strong>poorer prognosis</strong> than those <strong>below it (infrastructural)</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6006","text":"A 43-year-old factory work‌er presents to you with n​asal obstruction, cheek s​welling, and diplopia. Th‌e ENT resident suspects m​axillary carcinoma and us​es the following image to​ classify the same. Which‍ one of the following cla​ssification systems are u‌sed?","raw_text":"A 43-year-old factory worker presents to you with nasal obstruction, cheek swelling, and diplopia. The ENT resident suspects maxillary carcinoma and uses the following image to classify the same. Which one of the following classification systems are used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ohngren’s cl‌assification","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lederman’s c‌lassification","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"AJCC clas‌sification","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"TNM class‌ification","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lederman’s classification","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/071f12095c08453b9ef2ffcab9debac7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/76ad5d45bc9f4dc48156bde7a19e8d02x1280x1260.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lederman’s classification</strong></p><p><strong>Ledermann’s classification</strong> is used to classify <strong>maxillary carcinoma</strong> in this case.</p>\n<p>It uses<strong> two horizontal lines of Sebileau</strong>; one passing through the<strong> floors of orbits</strong> and the other through <strong>floors of antra</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6008","text":"You have a patient with ma‌xillary sinus carcinoma. ​Which of the following ly​mph nodes will be most co‌mmonly involved?","raw_text":"You have a patient with maxillary sinus carcinoma. Which of the following lymph nodes will be most commonly involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subman‌dibular","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Subm‌ental","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clavi‌cular","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Preaur‌icular","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Submandibular","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Submandibular</strong></p><p>The <strong>submandibular</strong> and <strong>jugulodigastric</strong> <strong>nodes</strong> are the most commonly involved in maxillary sinus carcinoma.</p>\n<p>Maxillary and ethmoid sinus drain primarily into retropharyngeal nodes but, these nodes are inaccessible for palpation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6009","text":"In a known case of maxilla‌ry carcinoma, histopathol​ogy of the surgically res​ected region shows that t‌he tumor has eroded into ​the floor and medial wall​ of the orbit and the fro​ntal sinus. Which of the ‍following is the best man​agement strategy?","raw_text":"In a known case of maxillary carcinoma, histopathology of the surgically resected region shows that the tumor has eroded into the floor and medial wall of the orbit and the frontal sinus. Which of the following is the best management strategy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sur‌gery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Radiothe‌rapy only","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Surgery + c‌hemotherapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Surgery + r‌adiotherapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Radiotherapy only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Radiotherapy only</strong></p><p><strong>Radiotherapy </strong>is preferred here, as in this case <strong>surgery</strong> <strong>has already been</strong> <strong>performed</strong>. The tumor staging is <strong>T4</strong> by AJCC classification. </p>\n<p><strong>T3 and T4 lesions</strong> are treated by <strong>combined</strong> modalities of <strong>radiation and surgery</strong>. Radiation in such cases may be given preoperatively or postoperatively.</p>\n<p><strong>T1 and T2 lesions</strong> without nodal or distant metastasis are treated with <strong>surgery or radiation</strong> with equal results.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 797px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49.9609px;\"><strong>Stage</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 731.039px;\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49.9609px;\"><strong>T1</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 731.039px;\">Tumour restricted to maxillary sinus <strong>mucosa</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49.9609px;\"><strong>T2</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 731.039px;\"><strong>Bony erosion</strong> - extension into hard palate or middle meatus, except posterior wall and pterygoid plates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49.9609px;\"><strong>T3</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 731.039px;\">Invasion into <strong>suprastructures</strong> such as:\n<ul>\n<li>Posterior wall of maxillary sinus</li>\n<li>Subcutaneous tissues</li>\n<li>Floor or medial wall of orbit</li>\n<li>Pterygoid fossa</li>\n<li>Ethmoid sinus</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49.9609px;\"><strong>T4a</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 731.039px;\">Tumour invades any of the following: <br/>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Anterior <strong>orbital contents</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The skin of the nose or cheek</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Infratemporal fossa</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pterygoid plates</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cribriform plate</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sphenoid or frontal sinuses </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 49.9609px;\"><strong>T4b</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 731.039px;\">Tumour invades any of the following: \n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Orbital apex</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Brain/dura</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Middle cranial fossa</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nasopharynx </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"gJ8gHaAw","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"26_peptic_ulcer_disease_and_related_disorders","title":"26.Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MC0222","text":"Which of the following is ‌an epithelial mucosal def‌ense mechanism of the gas‌tric mucosa?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an epithelial mucosal defense mechanism of the gastric mucosa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Layer o‌f mucous","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alkali‌ne tide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Microvas‌cular bed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Resti‌tution","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Restitution","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Restitution</strong></p><p><strong>Restitution</strong> is an <strong>epithelial </strong>mucosal defense mechanism. It is the process by which healthy surface epithelial cells from the adjoining areas migrate to restore a damaged region of the gastric mucosa. </p>\n<p>The <strong>gastric mucosa</strong> has well-established <strong>mucosal defense mechanisms</strong> against various assaults like HCl, pepsin, alcohol, etc. These include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pre-epithelial</strong> defense: A<strong> layer of mucous</strong> over the surface epithelium. This is secreted by the surface epithelium and contains HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>- </sup>which maintains neutral pH at the cell surface</li>\n<li><strong>Epithelial defense</strong>: It is constituted by the <strong>surface epithelial cells</strong> and their intercellular tight junctions that perform the following functions\n<ul>\n<li>Act as a physical barrier</li>\n<li><strong>Restitution</strong> is the migration of healthy cells to damaged areas</li>\n<li>Rapid renewal of surface epithelial layer</li>\n<li>Generating factors that prevent cellular damage such as heat shock proteins</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Sub-epithelial defense</strong>: \n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rich microvascular bed</strong> of the gastric submucosa that provides HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>- </sup>and washes away the toxic metabolites.</li>\n<li><strong>Alkaline tide-</strong> It is caused by the release of HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> into the gastric capillaries by the parietal cells. This HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> helps in the quick neutralization of HCl.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0219","text":"A 50-year-old woman compla‌ins about a burning pain ‌in her chest. She reports‌ using NSAIDs for her con‌stant knee pain. This dru‍g impairs her gastric muc​osal defense mechanism pr‌imarily by:","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman complains about a burning pain in her chest. She reports using NSAIDs for her constant knee pain. This drug impairs her gastric mucosal defense mechanism primarily by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Irritant effect on gastric‌ mucosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stimulating acid productio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibiting prostaglandin s‌ynthesis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibiting nitric oxide sy‌nthesis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis</strong></p><p>The <strong>NSAIDs</strong> impair the mucosal defense mechanism primarily by <strong>inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis</strong>.</p>\n<p>NSAIDs also <strong>decrease nitric oxide</strong> synthesis and have an <strong>irritant effect</strong> on the gastric mucosa but inhibition of COX enzymes/ prostaglandin synthesis is the most prominent factor.</p>\n<p>Almost all the <strong>mucosal defense mechanisms</strong> are primarily <strong>mediated by prostaglandins</strong> synthesized by the gastric mucosal cells. Apart from the prostaglandins, the other factors that play a role in mucosal defense include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nitric oxide (NO)- Causes vasodilation thereby increasing the mucosal blood flow.</li>\n<li>Several growth factors like EGF, TGF, FGF- Play a role in epithelial regeneration and restitution.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0235","text":"All of the following are t‌rue about the risk factor‌s of NSAIDs associated pe‌ptic ulcer disease except‌:","raw_text":"All of the following are true about the risk factors of NSAIDs associated peptic ulcer disease except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Smoking increases the risk‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"H pylori infection increas‌es the risk","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Even low dose of a NSAID c‌an cause ulcers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Concomitant use of glucoco‌rticoids decreases the ri‌sk","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Concomitant use of glucocorticoids decreases the risk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Concomitant use of glucocorticoids decreases the risk</strong></p><p><strong>Concomitant use of glucocorticoids </strong>with NSAIDs <strong>increases the risk</strong> of peptic ulcer disease.</p>\n<p>Other risks factors for NSAID-induced PUD include:   </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>H pylori infection</strong></li>\n<li>Serious or multisystem disease</li>\n<li>Alcohol and <strong>smoking</strong></li>\n<li>Concomitant use of the following drugs with NSAIDs increases the risk of PUD and its complications:\n<ul>\n<li>Glucocorticoids</li>\n<li>Anticoagulants</li>\n<li>Clopidogrel</li>\n<li>Multiple NSAIDs</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>PUD may occur even with low doses of NSAIDs. No dose of NSAIDs is considered completely safe.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6662","text":"What pattern of gastritis ‌is most common in patient‌s with H.pylori-related d‌uodenal ulcers?","raw_text":"What pattern of gastritis is most common in patients with H.pylori-related duodenal ulcers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autoimmune‌ gastritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-atrophic‌ pangastritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Granulomato‌us gastritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antral predominant gastrit‌is","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Antral predominant gastritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Antral predominant gastritis</strong></p><p><strong>Antral predominant gastritis</strong> is associated with <strong>duodenal ulcers</strong>. Antral predominant gastritis is associated with <strong>increased acid production</strong> by the parietal cells. This subjects the duodenum to increased acid exposure, leading to the formation of duodenal ulcers.</p>\n<p><strong>H. pylori infection</strong> may result in the following patterns of gastritis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Non-atrophic pangastritis</strong>- mostly asymptomatic and sometimes associated with MALToma.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Corpus predominant gastritis</strong>- affects the corpus and causes atrophy. This leads to the loss of acid-producing parietal cells that are primarily found in the corpus. Hence, it is associated with decreased acid production. This is also a predisposing factor for the development of gastric cancer.</li>\n<li><strong>Antral predominant gastritis</strong>- spares the parietal cells. Hence, it is associated with increased acid production.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Autoimmune gastritis is caused due to autoantibodies against the parietal cells. It is not associated with H. pylori.</p>\n<p>Option C: Granulomatous gastritis is characterized by the presence of granulomas It is associated with Crohn's disease and sarcoidosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6659","text":"A patient presents with fr‌equent epigastric pain wi‌th burning sensation for ‌the past 2 years. The pai‌n usually occurs 2-3 hour‍s after a meal and is rel​ieved by taking food. He ‌also says that he sometim‍es wakes up at night beca​use of severe pain. The m​ost probable cause for hi‍s symptoms is:","raw_text":"A patient presents with frequent epigastric pain with burning sensation for the past 2 years. The pain usually occurs 2-3 hours after a meal and is relieved by taking food. He also says that he sometimes wakes up at night because of severe pain. The most probable cause for his symptoms is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastri‌c ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Duodena‌l ulcer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Esoph‌agitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"GE‌RD","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Duodenal ulcer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Duodenal ulcer</strong></p><p>Epigastric pain with a burning sensation that usually occurs<strong> 2-3 hours after a meal</strong> and is <strong>relieved</strong> by <strong>taking food</strong> suggestive of <strong>duodenal ulcer</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following patterns of pain are usually seen in peptic ulcer disease:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Duodenal Ulcer-</strong> Pain occurs <strong>1.5 to 3 hours after a meal</strong> and is frequently relieved by antacids or food. Pain may awaken the patient from sleep.</li>\n<li><strong>Gastric Ulcer- </strong>Pain/discomfort may be <strong>precipitated by food</strong>. Nausea and weight loss are common.</li>\n<li><strong>Perforated ulcer</strong>- The bed of the ulcer tunnels through the entire thickness of the gastric wall and opens into the peritoneum. This presents with <strong>sudden onset severe abdominal pain</strong> and leads to <strong>peritonitis</strong>. Radiography shows gas under the diaphragm.</li>\n<li><strong>Penetrating ulcer</strong>- It is a type of perforation where the ulcer tunnels into an adjacent organ like the pancreas/liver. In this case, the <strong>pain is no longer relieved by food or antacids</strong> and might <strong>radiate to the back</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Many patients with peptic ulcers, especially elderly patients, may not have these symptoms and may directly present with a complication like bleeding or perforation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6660","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about gastric ulcers‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about gastric ulcers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Usually occur in 4th decad‌e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rarely m‌alignant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low basal ac‌id production","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"High stimulated acid produ‌ction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Low basal acid production","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Low basal acid production</strong></p><p><strong>Gastric ulcers</strong> are associated with<strong> low basal acid production</strong> and low stimulated acid production.</p>\n<p>Gastric acid secretion occurs in two patterns:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Basal acid output- Occurs in a circadian pattern. It is mediated by ACh from the vagus and histamine.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Stimulated acid production- Occurs in response to food. It is mediated by gastrin in addition to other factors. </li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 292px;\" width=\"548\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 266px;\">       <strong>Gastric Ulcer</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 266px;\">       <strong>Duodenal Ulcer</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 266px;\">Less common</td>\n<td style=\"width: 266px;\">More common</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 266px;\">Occurs later in life, with a peak incidence in the 6th decade</td>\n<td style=\"width: 266px;\">Occurs earlier in life</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 266px;\">Basal and stimulated acid production is low/normal, except in type 3 gastric ulcers</td>\n<td style=\"width: 266px;\">Basal and nocturnal acid production is increased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 266px;\">Can represent <strong>malignancy</strong> and should be biopsied</td>\n<td style=\"width: 266px;\">Rarely malignant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 266px;\">Most common site: <strong>Antrum</strong> (distal to acid-secreting mucosa) on the lesser curvature</td>\n<td style=\"width: 266px;\">Most common site: 1st part of the duodenum ( within 3cms from the pylorus)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 266px;\"><strong>Pain</strong> after <strong>food intake</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 266px;\"><strong>Pain</strong> before food intake, and <strong>relief</strong> on <strong>taking food</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0231","text":"H pylori causes gastric mu‌cosal injury due to the p‌resence of:","raw_text":"H pylori causes gastric mucosal injury due to the presence of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ca‌g A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Va‌cA","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"L‌PS","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hop pr‌oteins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. VacA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. VacA</strong></p><p><strong>H.pylori</strong> produces <strong>vacuolating exotoxin</strong> (Vac A) which causes gastric <strong>mucosal injury</strong>. Vac A inhibits CD4+ T-cells and disrupts the function of B cells, macrophages &amp; mast cells.</p>\n<p><strong>Cag A positive</strong> strains of H pylori cause an increased risk of gastric cancer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6663","text":"You are a first-year resid‌ent. You are asked to pre‌scribe an H.pylori eradic‌ation regimen to a patien‌t with peptic ulcer disea‍se. Which of the followin​g drugs will you not incl‌ude in it?","raw_text":"You are a first-year resident. You are asked to prescribe an H.pylori eradication regimen to a patient with peptic ulcer disease. Which of the following drugs will you not include in it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bismuth su‌bsalicylate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Erythr‌omycin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Amoxi‌cillin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metron‌idazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Erythromycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Erythromycin</strong></p><p><strong>Erythromycin</strong> is <strong>not effective</strong> in H pylori eradication.</p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p>Vonoprazan, a potassium-competitive acid blocker, has been recently approved by the FDA for the treatment of peptic acid-related disorders.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0246","text":"In which of the following ‌patients presenting with ‌epigastric pain and disco‌mfort is H.pylori eradica‌tion therapy not indicate‍d:","raw_text":"In which of the following patients presenting with epigastric pain and discomfort is H.pylori eradication therapy not indicated:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"46-year-old man with a fam‌ily history of gastric ca‌ncer in the father","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"40-year-old woman with rhe‌umatoid arthritis on NSAI‌Ds for 5 years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"58-year-old man with 35-pa‌ck years history of smoki‌ng","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"36-year-old man with GERD ‌on PPI's for 3 months","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 36-year-old man with GERD on PPI's for 3 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 36-year-old man with GERD on PPI&#x27;s for 3 months</strong></p><p>The need for <strong>H pylori eradication therapy</strong> in patients with GERD on long-term PPIs is not well established and is controversial.</p>\n<p><strong>H pylori eradication therapy</strong> is offered to <strong>patients diagnosed with peptic ulcer disease</strong> (PUD) as it greatly decreases the recurrence of ulcers. In addition to this, it should also be offered to patients at increased risk of PUD and gastric cancer such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Patients with long-term NSAIDs usage. Eg. Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li>Patients taking gastric acid inhibitors for &gt;1 year</li>\n<li>Patients with increased exposure to risk factors of gastric cancers. Eg. heavy smokers, exposure to dust, coal</li>\n<li>Patients with previous history/family history of gastric neoplasm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6664","text":"Eradication of H.pylori in‌fection in patients with ‌peptic ulcer disease is d‌etermined by?","raw_text":"Eradication of H.pylori infection in patients with peptic ulcer disease is determined by?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sero‌logy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urea bre‌ath test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Biopsy ur‌ease test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cul‌ture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Urea breath test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Urea breath test</strong></p><p><strong>Eradication</strong> of <strong>H. pylori</strong> infection can be determined by a <strong>urea breath test</strong><strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>A stool antigen test can also be employed.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0234","text":"H.pylori increases the ris‌k of:","raw_text":"H.pylori increases the risk of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GE‌RD","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Barrett's‌ esophagus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenocarcinoma of cardia o‌f stomach","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gastric MA‌LT lymphoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gastric MALT lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gastric MALT lymphoma</strong></p><p><strong>H.pylori</strong> increases the risk of <strong>gastric MALT lymphoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>H.pylori is <strong>protective</strong> against the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>GERD</li>\n<li>Barrett's esophagus</li>\n<li>Adenocarcinoma of esophagus</li>\n<li>Adenocarcinoma of the cardia of the stomach</li>\n</ul>\n<p>H. pylori are <strong>inversely</strong> related to gastroesophageal reflux disease (<strong>GERD</strong>) and its sequelae, which include Barrett's esophagus and esophageal adenocarcinoma.</p>\n<p>H.pylori strains <strong>positive</strong> for <strong>Cag A </strong>may be particularly protective against the development of esophageal adenocarcinoma. Thus, colonization with H. pylori, particularly Cag A strains, may be protective against the more severe forms of GERD.</p>\n<p><strong>Eradication</strong> of H. pylori is associated with <strong>mild worsening</strong> of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) in patients with corpus-dominant or pangastritis. It improves symptoms in those with antral-dominant gastritis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6665","text":"The most common complicati‌on in patients with pepti‌c ulcer disease is:","raw_text":"The most common complication in patients with peptic ulcer disease is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perfo‌ration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"GI bl‌eeding","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gastric outlet obstruction‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gastrocol‌ic fistula","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. GI bleeding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. GI bleeding</strong></p><p><strong>GI bleeding</strong> is the most common complication of<strong> peptic ulcer disease</strong><strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Other complications: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Perforation- second most common</li>\n<li>Gastrocolic fistulas</li>\n<li>Gastric outlet obstruction</li>\n<li>Rarely malignancy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0247","text":"A 47-year-old woman presen‌ted to the ER with an alt‌ered sensorium. Her husba‌nd reported that she has ‌had abdominal pain, vomit‍ing, and constipation for​ the past 4 days. She has‌ been self-medicating for‍ gastritis. Her lab inves​tigations are as shown. W​hich of the following dru‍gs is she most likely on ‌that could have caused he‌r symptoms? Renal functio​n test: Sr. Creatinine- 6​.4 mg/dl Sr. Urea- 50 mg/​dl ABG analysis: pH- 7.52​ PaCO2- 46.3 mmHg PO2- 71‍.1 mmHg HCO3¯- 36 mEq/L","raw_text":"<p>A 47-year-old woman presented to the ER with an altered sensorium. Her husband reported that she has had abdominal pain, vomiting, and constipation for the past 4 days. She has been self-medicating for gastritis. Her lab investigations are as shown. Which of the following drugs is she most likely on that could have caused her symptoms?</p>\n<p>Renal function test: </p>\n<p>    Sr. Creatinine- 6.4 mg/dl</p>\n<p>    Sr. Urea- 50 mg/dl</p>\n<p>ABG analysis: </p>\n<p>    pH- 7.52</p>\n<p>    PaCO2- 46.3 mmHg</p>\n<p>    PO2- 71.1 mmHg</p>\n<p>    HCO3¯- 36 mEq/L</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aluminium‌ hydroxide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Magnesium‌ hydroxide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium ‌carbonate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Proton pump‌ inhibitors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Calcium carbonate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Calcium carbonate</strong></p><p>The given clinical features indicating hypercalcemia and investigations showing renal insufficiency, metabolic alkalosis are suggestive of <strong>milk-alkali syndrome</strong>. Long-term use of <strong>calcium carbonate</strong> can lead to this condition.</p>\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_section\">In susceptible individuals, there is a loss of negative feedback, leading to <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">excess calcium absorption</strong> when there is excess intake. It results in the following triad of clinical features:</div>\n<ul class=\"p-rich_text_list p-rich_text_list__bullet\" data-border=\"false\" data-border-radius-bottom-cap=\"false\" data-border-radius-top-cap=\"false\" data-indent=\"0\" data-stringify-type=\"unordered-list\">\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Hypercalcemia </li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Alkalosis- due to HCO3 reabsorption caused by PTH suppression, natriuresis, and water depletion</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Renal failure- due to damage to renal tubules</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_section\">It is reversible once ingestion of antacid is stopped. However, in severe cases, a chronic form called <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">Burnett’s syndrome </strong>can develop. It is associated with <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">irreversible renal damage.</strong></div>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6681","text":"A patient is admitted to t‌he ICU after he suffered ‌trauma to his head. This ‌patient is prone to devel‌op what type of gastric u‍lcers?","raw_text":"A patient is admitted to the ICU after he suffered trauma to his head. This patient is prone to develop what type of gastric ulcers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Curling‌ ulcers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cushing‌ ulcers","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stress‌ ulcers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Refracto‌ry ulcers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cushing ulcers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cushing ulcers</strong></p><p>The gastric ulcers associated with <strong>head trauma</strong> are<strong> Cushing ulcers. </strong>Head trauma leads to vagal stimulation which in turn leads to increased gastric acid production. </p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Curling ulcers </strong>are seen in <strong>severe burns.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Stress ulcers </strong>are seen in conditions like <strong>shock, sepsis,</strong> etc. They are thought to be caused by local ischemia and splanchnic vasoconstriction.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Refractory ulcers</strong> are those that do not heal even after adequate medical therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0249","text":"You would diagnose a patie‌nt to have a refractory g‌astric ulcer if the ulcer‌ fails to heal after:","raw_text":"You would diagnose a patient to have a refractory gastric ulcer if the ulcer fails to heal after:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12 w‌eeks","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"8 w‌eeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10 w‌eeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"14 w‌eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 12 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 12 weeks</strong></p><p>A <strong>gastric</strong> <strong>ulcer</strong> is said to be <strong>refractory</strong> if it fails to heal after<strong> 12 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>A refractory duodenal ulcer is that which fails to heal after 8 weeks.</p>\n<p>Management of refractory PUD:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rule out other diseases or causes </li>\n<li>High dose PPI</li>\n<li>Surgery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6667","text":"A middle-aged woman presen‌ts with acidity, abdomina‌l pain, diarrhea, and wei‌ght loss for the past few‌ months. Her symptoms are‍ not relieved by taking a​ntacids. An endoscopic ev‌aluation shows ulcers on ‍the second part of the du​odenum. Which of the foll​owing conditions should b‍e suspected in this patie‌nt?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presents with acidity, abdominal pain, diarrhea, and weight loss for the past few months. Her symptoms are not relieved by taking antacids. An endoscopic evaluation shows ulcers on the second part of the duodenum. Which of the following conditions should be suspected in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stress‌ ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"H. pylori‌ infection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Malignant du‌odenal ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zollinger Ellison syndrome‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Zollinger Ellison syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Zollinger Ellison syndrome</strong></p><p>The given scenario of symptoms not improving with antacids, associated diarrhea, and the unusual location of ulcers are highly suggestive of <strong>Zollinger Ellison syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Zollinger Ellison syndrome (ZES) is a peptic ulcer disease caused by <strong>unregulated gastrin release</strong> from a <strong>neuroendocrine tumor/gastrinoma</strong>. About 60% of the tumors are malignant, hence, it is important to identify ZES.</p>\n<p>The clinical presentation of ZES is very similar to that of peptic ulcer disease. The features which help in suspecting ZES include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Unusual locations- the second part of the duodenum and beyond</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Refractory to medical therapy</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Recurrence after acid-reducing surgery</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Presenting with complications like bleeding and perforation</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Not associated with H. pylori or NSAID ingestion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients of ZES often have associated diarrhea and involvement of the esophagus (esophagitis, ulceration, or stricture).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0227","text":"A 35-year-old man presente‌d with recurrent duodenal‌ ulcers with poor respons‌e to medical therapy. He ‌had a history of hyperpar‍athyroidism and transsphe​noidal prolactinoma resec‌tion. Which among the fol‍lowing is the next approp​riate step?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man presented with recurrent duodenal ulcers with poor response to medical therapy. He had a history of hyperparathyroidism and transsphenoidal prolactinoma resection. Which among the following is the next appropriate step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urea bre‌ath test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secret‌in test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serum gast‌rin levels","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endoscopic‌ ultrasound","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Serum gastrin levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Serum gastrin levels</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Zollinger Ellison syndrome</strong> (ZES). The next appropriate step to evaluate the patients for ZES is by measuring the <strong>serum gastrin levels</strong>. History of hyperparathyroidism and transsphenoidal prolactinoma resection indicates <strong>MEN1 syndrome</strong>. ZES is commonly associated with MEN1.</p>\n<p>The normal gastrin levels are &lt;150 pg/mL. An <strong>increase of &gt;10 times the normal</strong> levels is highly suggestive of ZES.</p>\n<p>Secretin test is a gastrin provocative test. It is done when the gastrin levels and the acid secretory studies are inconclusive for ZES. It is the <strong>most sensitive </strong>and <strong>specific</strong> gastrin provocative test for the diagnosis of gastrinoma.</p>\n<p>Endoscopic ultrasound is done to localize the tumor. It is done after the diagnosis of ZES has been established.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6679","text":"Which of the following inv‌estigations employs somat‌ostatin analogues to dete‌ct gastrinomas?","raw_text":"Which of the following investigations employs somatostatin analogues to detect gastrinomas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CE‌CT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PET/CT with‌ Ga-DOTATATE","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Octre‌oScan","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endo‌scopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. OctreoScan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. OctreoScan</strong></p><p><strong>OctreoScan</strong> detects gastrinomas using their property of <strong>uptake of somatostatin analogues</strong>. </p>\n<p>OctreoScan detects endocrine tumors that express receptors for somatostatin in conditions such as Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (ZES) and multiple endocrine neoplasia (MEN) syndromes.</p>\n<p>Other modalities used for localizing the tumor in ZES include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS)</li>\n<li>PET/CT with Ga-DOTATATE is the investigation of choice when available.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>PET/CT</strong> with Ga-DOTATATE is the <strong>gold-standard</strong> test for localizing tumors in pheochromocytoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0251","text":"Which of the following vir‌uses cause infectious gas‌tritis in immunocompromis‌ed patients?","raw_text":"Which of the following viruses cause infectious gastritis in immunocompromised patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytomeg‌alovirus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Noro‌virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enter‌ovirus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coxsack‌ie virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cytomegalovirus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cytomegalovirus</strong></p><p>Viruses that cause infectious <strong>gastritis </strong>in<strong> immunocompromised</strong> individuals include <strong>cytomegalovirus </strong>and Herpes simplex virus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6684","text":"An anemic patient on evalu‌ation showed macrocytes w‌ith hypersegmented neutro‌phils on peripheral smear‌. Serology revealed anti-‍parietal cell antibodies.​ Which of the following i‌s most likely to be assoc‍iated with her condition?​","raw_text":"An anemic patient on evaluation showed macrocytes with hypersegmented neutrophils on peripheral smear. Serology revealed anti-parietal cell antibodies. Which of the following is most likely to be associated with her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zollinger Ellison syndrome‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"VIP‌oma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type A g‌astritis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Type B g‌astritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type A gastritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type A gastritis</strong></p><p>The given peripheral smear findings and serology are suggestive of <strong>pernicious anemia</strong>. It is usually seen in type<strong> A gastritis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Type A autoimmune gastritis is caused by circulating antibodies against the <strong>parietal cells. </strong>The destruction of parietal cells leads to the <strong>deficiency of intrinsic factor </strong>that is necessary for the<strong> absorption of vitamin B12.</strong> The resulting B12 deficiency causes<strong> pernicious anemia.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6680","text":"An 84-year-old woman livin‌g in an assisted care fac‌ility is rushed to the ho‌spital with an upper GI b‌leed. On arrival, she is ‍febrile and the nurse not​es a sudden spurt of bloo‌d from her nasogastric tu‍be. The following investi​gations are done. Which o​f the following is respon‍sible for this patient's ‌condition? CRP- 23 mg/L U‌pper GI endoscopy- Extens​ive gastric ulceration wi​th necrotic tissue and ed​ematous mucosa CT abdomen​- Thickened gastric wall","raw_text":"<p>An 84-year-old woman living in an assisted care facility is rushed to the hospital with an upper GI bleed. On arrival, she is febrile and the nurse notes a sudden spurt of blood from her nasogastric tube. The following investigations are done. Which of the following is responsible for this patient's condition?</p>\n<p>CRP- 23 mg/L</p>\n<p>Upper GI endoscopy- Extensive gastric ulceration with necrotic tissue and edematous mucosa</p>\n<p>CT abdomen- Thickened gastric wall</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H.py‌lori","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"E.c‌oli","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cytomeg‌alovirus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herpes sim‌plex virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. E.coli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. E.coli</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with her endoscopy findings are suggestive of<strong> phlegmonous gastritis</strong>. It is caused due to<strong><em> E</em>.<em>coli</em></strong><em>.</em></p>\n<p>Phlegmonous gastritis is a potentially <strong>life-threatening</strong> disorder characterized by marked and diffuse acute inflammatory infiltrates of the <strong>entire gastric wall </strong>accompanied by <strong>necrosis. </strong>It usually affects the immunocompromised and <strong>elderly</strong> individuals.</p>\n<p>Organisms associated with phlegmonous gastritis are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>E.coli</em><strong><br/></strong></li>\n<li><em>Streptococci</em></li>\n<li><em>Staphylococci</em></li>\n<li><em>Proteus </em></li>\n<li><em>Haemophilus</em> species</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other uncommon forms of gastritis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Eosinophilic gastritis: Presents as intestinal obstruction </li>\n<li>Russel’s body gastritis: Pseudotumor on endoscopy </li>\n<li>Granulomatous gastritis is seen in Crohn’s disease </li>\n<li>Lymphocytic gastritis is seen in celiac disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"lOr9VE2M","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"26_arboviruses_and_picorna_viruses","title":"26.Arboviruses And Picorna Viruses","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MA7956","text":"Which family does yellow f‌ever virus belong to?","raw_text":"Which family does yellow fever virus belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arbov‌iridae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flaviv‌iridae","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bunyav‌iridae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Picorna‌viridae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Flaviviridae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Flaviviridae</strong></p><p><strong>Yellow fever virus </strong>belongs to the family<strong> flaviviridae</strong>, which contains the genus <strong>F</strong><strong>lavivirus</strong>.</p>\n<p>The important <strong>species</strong> under genus <em>Flavivirus</em> include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Yellow fever virus</li>\n<li>Dengue virus</li>\n<li>Japanese encephalitis virus</li>\n<li>Kyasanur forest disease virus</li>\n<li>West Nile encephalitis</li>\n<li>Zikavirus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>Arbovirus</strong> is a <strong>term</strong> used to refer to any viruses that are transmitted by <strong>arthropod</strong> vectors. There is no family or order by the name arbovirus. The word arbovirus stands for <strong>AR</strong>thropod<strong>-BO</strong>rne<strong> virus</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7958","text":"A 10-year-old boy from Utt‌ar Pradesh was rushed to ‌the emergency department ‍with a headache, fever, a‌nd blurred vision followe​d shortly by unresponsive‍ness to verbal commands. ‍He is suspected to have v​iral encephalitis. Which ​of the following transmit‌s this endemic disease?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old boy from Uttar Pradesh was rushed to the emergency department with a headache, fever, and blurred vision followed shortly by unresponsiveness to verbal commands. He is suspected to have viral encephalitis. Which of the following transmits this endemic disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aedes aegyp‌ti mosquito","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Culex m‌osquito","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anopheles‌ mosquito","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mansonia‌ mosquito","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Culex mosquito","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Culex mosquito</strong></p><p>Endemic viral encephalitis is suggestive of <strong>Japanese encephalitis</strong>. It is transmitted by <em><strong>Culex tritaeniorhynchus</strong> </em>and <em><strong>Culex vishnuii.</strong></em></p>\n<p>It is the <strong>leading cause</strong> of <strong>childhood neurological viral infection</strong> in India. In endemic areas such as North India, it typically affects children <strong>&lt;15 years of age</strong> as by early adulthood, the majority of the population has protective immunity following natural exposure to the virus.</p>\n<p><strong>Ardeid birds</strong> like herons and egrets are <strong>reservoir</strong> hosts, while the <strong>amplifier</strong> hosts are <strong>pigs</strong>. It is <strong>transmitted</strong> by Culex <strong>mosquitoes,</strong> and <strong>h</strong><strong>uman</strong> infection is a tangential dead end.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0627","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true about Japanese e‌ncephalitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about Japanese encephalitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transmitted by Culex vishn‌ui","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pig is the a‌mplifier host","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Man acts as ‌the reservoir","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vaccinating pigs is helpfu‌l in disease control","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Man acts as the reservoir","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/17b7c7f1cd6c4dda91e4a46003ad3189x584x421.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Man acts as the reservoir</strong></p><p><strong>Humans</strong> are the <strong>dead-end hosts</strong> for the <strong>Japanese encephalitis virus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: The disease is transmitted by <em>Culex tritaeniorhynchus</em> and <em>Culex vishnui </em>(common in India) species.</p>\n<p>Option B: Herons act as reservoir hosts and pigs as amplifier hosts.</p>\n<p>Option D: The control measures include mosquito control, locating piggeries away from the human dwellings and vaccinating pigs.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the life cycle of the Japanese B encephalitis virus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7959","text":"A mother brings her 9-mont‌h-old child for vaccinati‌on against Japanese encep‍halitis. Which of the fol‌lowing statements is fals​e regarding this vaccine?‍","raw_text":"A mother brings her 9-month-old child for vaccination against Japanese encephalitis. Which of the following statements is false regarding this vaccine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Two doses are recommended ‌at age 9 months and 18 mo‌nths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inactivated vero cell cult‌ure is given 4 weeks apar‌t","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Live vaccine SA 14-14-2 is‌ administered intramuscul‌arly","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The live attenuated vaccin‌e can be given to adolesc‌ents","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Live vaccine SA 14-14-2 is administered intramuscularly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Live vaccine SA 14-14-2 is administered intramuscularly</strong></p><p><strong>Live SA 14-14-2</strong> strain of Japanese encephalitis (JE) vaccine is administered <strong>subcutaneously.</strong></p>\n<p>Types of JE Vaccines:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 160px; width: 590px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 161px;\"> </td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 282px;\"><strong>SA 14-14-2 cell culture derived vaccine </strong></td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 264px;\"><strong>Inactivated vero cell culture derived Kolar (Jenvac 82156XY)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.109px;\"><strong>Live attenuated</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.891px;\"><strong>Inactivated</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161px;\"><strong>Route of administration</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.109px;\">Subcutaneous</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.891px;\">Intramuscular</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264px;\">Intramuscular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161px;\"><strong> Dosage</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.109px;\">2 doses</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.891px;\">2 doses</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264px;\">2 doses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161px;\"><strong> Duration between doses</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.109px;\">At ages 9months and 16-18 months</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.891px;\">4 weeks apart</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264px;\">4 weeks apart</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161px;\"><strong> Ages</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138.109px;\">9 months, 16-18 months, adolescents and non-immune adults</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143.891px;\">up to 15 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 264px;\"> up to 18 years</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7961","text":"A patient presents with fe‌ver, arthralgia, and pain‌ behind the eyes for 3 da‍ys. On examination, she h‌as the following rash. La​b reports show thrombocyt‍openia and leucopenia. Wh‍at is the most likely dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents with fever, arthralgia, and pain behind the eyes for 3 days. On examination, she has the following rash. Lab reports show thrombocytopenia and leucopenia. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dengue‌ fever","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mal‌aria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‌hoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Yellow‌ fever","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dengue fever","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e0df488fca7a48bfa0e7f2d89b04b6bb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dengue fever</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with the symptoms and the rash suggests <strong>dengue fever</strong>.</p>\n<p>It presents with sudden-onset <strong>fever, retro-orbital pain,</strong> <strong>myalgia, arthralgia</strong>, and a <strong>rash</strong>. The rash in dengue appears as <strong>white islands on a red sea</strong> due to erythema with petechiae. The alternative name for dengue, <strong>breakbone</strong> <strong>fever</strong>, comes from the associated muscle and joint pains.</p>\n<p>Suspected cases of dengue are confirmed by the presence of <strong>NS1 antigen</strong> or<strong> IgM antibodies.</strong></p>\n<p>The treatment of dengue is <strong>supportive</strong> with hydration taking top priority.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7962","text":"A 35-year-old wildlife pho‌tographer presents with s‌udden onset of fever, hea‍dache, and myalgia. On ex‌amination, you notice gin​gival bleeds and conjunct‍ival hemorrhages. He rece‍ntly visited a forest in ​Karnataka that has a larg​e population of monkeys. ‌Which of these is the vec​tor for the suspected dis​ease?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old wildlife photographer presents with sudden onset of fever, headache, and myalgia. On examination, you notice gingival bleeds and conjunctival hemorrhages. He recently visited a forest in Karnataka that has a large population of monkeys.  Which of these is the vector for the suspected disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fl‌eas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mi‌te","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ti‌cks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mosq‌uito","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ticks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ticks</strong></p><p>The given case scenario with exposure to monkeys, fever, myalgia, headache, conjunctival hemorrhage and gingival bleeds is suggestive of <strong>Kyasanur forest disease </strong>(KFD), which is transmitted by <strong>ticks </strong>(<em>Haemophysalis spinigera</em>). It is endemic to the forests of <strong>Karnataka </strong>and <strong>Kerala.</strong></p>\n<p>The clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Headache</li>\n<li>Conjunctivitis</li>\n<li>Myalgia </li>\n<li>Meningoencephalitis</li>\n<li>Hemorrhagic symptoms- hemorrhages in skin, mucosa and gastrointestinal bleeds.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The case fatality rate is about 5 percent.</p>\n<p><strong>Monkeys</strong> are <strong>amplifier</strong> hosts, <strong>rodents</strong> are <strong>reservoirs</strong> and <strong>humans</strong> are <strong>incidental hosts.</strong></p>\n<p>A killed KFD virus vaccine was used in a small field trial and appeared to provide some degree of protection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7580","text":"A middle-aged man presents‌ with delirium, fever, ja‌undice, and a hemorrhagic‍ diathesis. The patient i‌s agitated and has had no​ urine output in the last‍ 6 hours. On investigatio‍n, there is azotemia and ​elevated liver enzymes. A​ viral disease with the v‌ector shown below is susp​ected. Which of the follo​wing inclusion bodies may‌ be seen?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man presents with delirium, fever, jaundice, and a hemorrhagic diathesis. The patient is agitated and has had no urine output in the last 6 hours. On investigation, there is azotemia and elevated liver enzymes. A viral disease with the vector shown below is suspected. Which of the following inclusion bodies may be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Henderson-Patterson bodies‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Torres‌ bodies","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Levinthal Cole Lille bodie‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paschen‌ bodies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Torres bodies","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/94a6fac46b1a442a90a4ec0b374fdf36.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Torres bodies</strong></p><p>The image shows the <strong><em>Aedes aegypti</em></strong> mosquito and the clinical scenario with hepatic and renal dysfunction with delirium points towards a diagnosis of <strong>yellow fever.</strong> <strong>Torres bodies </strong>are amorphous <strong>eosinophilic</strong> <strong>nuclear</strong> <strong>inclusions</strong> associated with <strong>yellow</strong> <strong>fever</strong>, a disease caused by the <strong>Flavivirus</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Inclusion bodies</strong> are structures with distinct sizes and shapes that are seen in <strong>virus</strong>-<strong>infected</strong> <strong>cells</strong> under light microscopy.</p>\n<p>Note: Dengue hemorrhagic fever can be a differential diagnosis, but there are no inclusion bodies seen in dengue. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7965","text":"What is the duration of va‌lidity of 17D vaccination‌?","raw_text":"What is the duration of validity of 17D vaccination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 y‌ear","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5 y‌ears","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Life‌time","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"10 y‌ears","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lifetime","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lifetime</strong></p><p><strong>17D</strong> vaccine (yellow fever vaccine) is valid for a <strong>lifetime,</strong> beginning 10 days after vaccination. According to the amendment in 2016 to International Health Regulations 2005, the validity of the related international certificate of vaccination against yellow fever has been <strong>changed</strong> from ten (10) years <strong>to</strong> the <strong>lifespan</strong> of the person (traveler) vaccinated.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>live-attenuated</strong> vaccine derived from the '<strong>Asibi</strong> <strong>strain</strong>', administered subcutaneously. Immunity after a <strong>single dose</strong> is long-lasting and provides lifetime protection<strong>. </strong>It is thermolabile. </p>\n<p>Vaccination is compulsory for travel to and from endemic areas.</p>\n<p>In India, 17D is manufactured at the Central Research Institute, Kasauli.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7964","text":"A pest control technician ‌with a history of fever, ‌vomiting, hematuria, and ‍decreased urine output wa‌s diagnosed with hemorrha​gic fever with renal synd‍rome. Which of the follow‍ing statements is false a​bout the causative virus?​","raw_text":"A pest control technician with a history of fever, vomiting, hematuria, and decreased urine output was diagnosed with hemorrhagic fever with renal syndrome. Which of the following statements is false about the causative virus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hantaviruses are the natur‌al pathogens of rodents","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transmission primarily occ‌urs by Culex mosquitoes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hantavirus pulmonary syndr‌ome presents with pulmona‌ry edema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hantaviruses cause epidemi‌c hemorrhagic fever","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Transmission primarily occurs by Culex mosquitoes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Transmission primarily occurs by Culex mosquitoes</strong></p><p>Hemorrhagic fever with renal syndrome (HFRS) is caused by<strong> Hantaviruses.</strong> They are rodent-borne viruses that are transmitted by <strong>inhalation</strong> of the virus contained in dried <strong>excreta</strong> of <strong>rodents.</strong> They are the natural pathogens of rodents like mice, rats, and voles. </p>\n<p>HFRS presents with <strong>acute kidney injury, hemorrhages, fever</strong>, and circulatory collapse. Hantaviruses can also cause <strong>Hantavirus pulmonary syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: In Aug 2016, The International Committee on Taxonomy of Viruses (ICTV), has renamed and moved the genus Hantavirus to Orthohantavirus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7966","text":"An epidemic of paralytic p‌oliomyelitis occurs in a ‌non-immune population. Wh‍ich among the following s‌trains of poliovirus is t​he most common cause of t‍his?","raw_text":"An epidemic of paralytic poliomyelitis occurs in a non-immune population. Which among the following strains of poliovirus is the most common cause of this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e 1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‌e 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Type 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Type 1</strong></p><p><strong>Type 1 </strong>poliovirus is the <strong>most common cause</strong> of<strong> epidemics </strong>in populations.</p>\n<p>Poliovirus strains have been classified into 3 types: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type 1- most common<strong> </strong>and causes most of the <strong>epidemics</strong>. </li>\n<li>Type 2- <strong>endemic</strong> infections. </li>\n<li>Type 3- epidemics but rarer than type 1. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Immunity is type-specific. </p>\n<p>Note :- Type 2 is declared eradicated in September, 2015 and Type 3 is declared eradicated in October, 2019</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7970","text":"A 5-year-old girl is broug‌ht to the pediatrics OPD ‌with a history of lower-l‍imb paralysis and the fol‌lowing deformity. Which o​f the following will be t‍he most common form of pr‍esentation of this illnes​s?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old girl is brought to the pediatrics OPD with a history of lower-limb paralysis and the following deformity. Which of the following will be the most common form of presentation of this illness?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inapparent‌ infection","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aborti‌ve form","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paralyt‌ic form","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nonparal‌ytic form","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inapparent infection","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fa5715e8632e41398a1c4135b44e8aa0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inapparent infection</strong></p><p>The given case scenario with a history of lower limb paralysis and deformity is suggestive of <strong>poliomyelitis. </strong>The given image shows deformity of the right lower limb, namely hyperextension at the knee joint and muscle wasting. </p>\n<p><strong>Inapparent infection</strong> is the<strong> most common </strong>(90-95%) presentation of <strong>polio.</strong> Only seroconversion occurs without any clinical manifestations. </p>\n<p>Option B: Abortive poliomyelitis is associated with primary viremia and presents as fever, headache, sore throat, and malaise lasting for 1-5 days.</p>\n<p>Option C: Paralytic poliomyelitis occurs following minor illness, characterized by biphasic fever with headache, stiff neck, and flaccid paralysis. </p>\n<p>Option D: In non-paralytic poliomyelitis, the disease does not progress beyond the stage of aseptic meningitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7968","text":"A 20-year-old unimmunized ‌patient presents to you w‌ith complaints of weaknes‍s of lower limbs, inabili‌ty to pass urine, and fev​er, for 3 days. His stool‍ samples were sent to the‍ district lab to confirm ​the diagnosis. Which regi​on of the spinal cord wil‌l primarily be affected i​n these cases?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old unimmunized patient presents to you with complaints of weakness of lower limbs, inability to pass urine, and fever, for 3 days. His stool samples were sent to the district lab to confirm the diagnosis. Which region of the spinal cord will primarily be affected in these cases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior ‌horn cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior ‌horn cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral h‌orn cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intermedi‌ate column","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anterior horn cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anterior horn cells</strong></p><p>The given case scenario of fever, lower limb paralysis, urinary retention, and stool samples being sent for diagnosis, is suggestive of <strong>paralytic</strong> <strong>polio. </strong>The <strong>anterior horn cells</strong> of the spinal cord are primarily affected, causing <strong>acute</strong> <strong>flaccid</strong> <strong>paralysis. </strong>Posterior horns and intermediate columns/lateral horns may also be involved to some extent. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 501\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>In the central nervous system, the virus multiplies selectively in the neurons and destroys them. The <strong>earliest</strong> change is the degeneration of <strong>Nissl bodies</strong> (<strong>chromatolysis</strong>). Nuclear changes follow. </p>\n<p>Note: Fecal samples of all cases of acute flaccid paralysis are obtained (2 samples) and transported to the laboratory <strong>within 72 hours </strong>of collection at <strong>4–8°C</strong> <strong>or</strong> frozen at<strong> -20°C</strong>. This is known as the <strong>reverse cold chain</strong> and is done as a part of gold standard polio surveillance/acute flaccid paralysis surveillance (AFP surveillance). </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7976","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin‌g vaccination against pol‍io?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding vaccination against polio?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sabin type has the live at‌tenuated virus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"OPV should not be administ‌ered to immunocompromised‌ persons","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Salk vaccine is associated‌ with mutations that lead‌ to reversal of virulence‍","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Both OPV and IPV induce hu‌moral IgG","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Salk vaccine is associated with mutations that lead to reversal of virulence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Salk vaccine is associated with mutations that lead to reversal of virulence</strong></p><p><strong>Salk type</strong> is the <strong>killed</strong> <strong>vaccine</strong>, hence there is no risk of mutation that can lead to a reversal of virulence. </p>\n<p>There are two types of vaccines for polio - <strong>IPV </strong>(inactivated poliovirus vaccine) and <strong>OPV </strong>(oral poliovirus vaccine). </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7972","text":"Which of the following str‌ains of poliovirus have b‌een eradicated worldwide?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following strains of poliovirus have been eradicated worldwide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Types ‌2 and 3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Types ‌1 and 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Types 2 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Types 2 and 3</strong></p><p>Type<strong> 2 and 3</strong> have been <strong>eradicated worldwide.</strong></p>\n<p>Type 2: In <strong>September 2015</strong>, WHO declared the eradication of the wild type 2 poliovirus strain. In India, the last type 2 infection was detected in 1999.</p>\n<p>Type 3: It has not been detected anywhere in the world since November 2012. On World Polio Day in <strong>October 2019,</strong> the<strong> </strong>type 3 virus was declared to be eradicated worldwide.</p>\n<p>The only <strong>circulating strain</strong> is <strong>type 1.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7973","text":"A 68-year-old woman presen‌ted with acute chest pain‌ which was stabbing type ‍in nature. The pain is sp‌asmodic and associated wi​th fever spikes. There's ‍an outbreak of coxsackiev‍irus B in her community. ​What is the most likely d​iagnosis?","raw_text":"A 68-year-old woman presented with acute chest pain which was stabbing type in nature. The pain is spasmodic and associated with fever spikes. There's an outbreak of coxsackievirus B in her community. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intercosta‌l neuralgia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bornholm‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Herpa‌ngina","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perica‌rditis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bornholm disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bornholm disease</strong></p><p>The given case scenario with stabbing type chest pain which is spasmodic in nature, associated with fever spikes, and history of an outbreak of <strong>c</strong><strong>oxsackievirus</strong> <strong>B </strong>is suggestive of <strong>Bornholm disease</strong> or <strong>epidemic pleurodynia.</strong> </p>\n<p>Symptoms include paroxysms of acute <strong>pleuritic chest pain</strong> associated with <strong>fever spikes</strong>. Involved <strong>muscles</strong> are <strong>tender.</strong> Chest X-ray is usually normal. Most cases occur during <strong>epidemics</strong> of coxsackievirus<strong> </strong>B. The disease is named after the Danish island of Bornholm where early cases occurred.  </p>\n<p>The treatment is non-steroidal anti-inflammatory agents or hot fomentation of the involved muscles. </p>\n<p>Other diseases associated with coxsackievirus B:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neonatal myocarditis and pericarditis</li>\n<li>Juvenile diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li>Orchitis</li>\n<li>Severe disseminated congenital infection- hepatitis, meningoencephalitis, adrenocortical involvement</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7974","text":"A 5-year-old boy presents ‌with headache, fever, and‌ vomiting. On examination‍, you observe the followi‌ng. He is diagnosed with ​herpangina. Which group o‍f viruses causes the same‍?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old boy presents with headache, fever, and vomiting. On examination, you observe the following. He is diagnosed with herpangina. Which group of viruses causes the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Entero‌viruses","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rhinov‌iruses","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myxov‌iruses","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rhabdo‌viruses","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Enteroviruses","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0b3d5d8cbba0496fab3bcc20faad940a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Enteroviruses</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 536\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The given image shows <strong>palatal ulcers</strong> in <strong>herpangina.</strong> It is caused by<strong> </strong>the<strong> coxsackievirus A</strong>, a large subgroup of the <strong>enteroviruses.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Herpangina</strong> or <strong>vesicular pharyngitis</strong> is characterized by grayish- white papulovesicular lesions on an erythematous base that ulcerate. These are present on:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Soft palate</li>\n<li>Anterior pillars of tonsils</li>\n<li>Uvula</li>\n<li>Posterior oropharynx</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is a common clinical manifestation of <strong>c</strong><strong>oxsackievirus A</strong> infection in children, but it can also be caused by <strong>coxsackievirus B,</strong> echoviruses, or enterovirus 71.</p>\n<p>Treatment is usually only supportive, as the disease is self-limiting and usually runs its course in less than a week. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9177","text":"Which of the following org‌anisms causes the followi‌ng disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms causes the following disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coxsack‌ievirus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Human herp‌es virus 7","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pox ‌virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Molluscum ‌contagiosum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Coxsackievirus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/28814a1e8f5b4c08ba29d067e9bcc13c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Coxsackievirus</strong></p><p>The image shows vesicles on the palms, soles, and oral mucosa, consistent with <strong>hand-foot-and-mouth</strong> disease. It is caused by<strong> coxsackievirus.</strong></p>\n<p>Hand-foot-and-mouth disease (HFMD)<strong> </strong>is an exanthematous fever affecting mainly young children, characterized by <strong>oral</strong> and<strong> pharyngeal ulcerations</strong> and a <strong>vesicular rash </strong>of the<strong> palms and soles</strong> that may spread to the arms and legs. Vesicles<strong> heal without crusting</strong>, which clinically differentiates them from the vesicles of herpesviruses and poxviruses. It is usually a benign illness resolving in 1-2 weeks.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 506\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Coxsackie A16, A9, and B 1-3 are common causative agents.</p>\n<p>Other diseases caused by Coxsackie A virus:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aseptic meningitis associated with a maculopapular rash.</li>\n<li>Herpangina</li>\n<li>Minor respiratory infections</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7975","text":"A 60-year-old presented wi‌th eye pain, watering of ‌eyes, and increased sensi‍tivity to light. On exami‌nation, you see the follo​wing. Which of the follow‍ing viruses can cause thi‍s condition?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old presented with eye pain, watering of eyes, and increased sensitivity to light. On examination, you see the following. Which of the following viruses can cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rhinovirus and Coxsackie A‌24","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rhinovirus and Enterovirus‌-70","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enterovirus 70 and Adenovi‌rus 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enterovirus-70 and Coxsack‌ie A24","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Enterovirus-70 and Coxsackie A24","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/650650f704b341ce8e2e2435ed036ec6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Enterovirus-70 and Coxsackie A24</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>a</strong><strong>cute hemorrhagic conjunctivitis</strong> (AHC) caused mainly by <strong>E</strong><strong>nterovirus 70 and</strong> <strong>C</strong><strong>oxsackievirus A24 </strong>variant (CA24v).</p>\n<p>It is characterized by sudden swelling, congestion, and watering of the eyes. Subconjunctival hemorrhages are a characteristic feature and corneal involvement may occur transiently. </p>\n<p>There is no treatment currently available. The disease is self limiting, and resolves within a five to seven-day period.</p>\n<p>Note: Acute follicular conjunctivitis is caused by Adenovirus serotypes 3, 4, 7 and 11. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7957","text":"A 42-year-old woman presen‌ted with fever, joint pai‌ns, and headache. Chik si‍gn (image given below) is‌ present on the patient's​ nose. Which of the follo‍wing vectors transmits th‍e etiological agent?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old woman presented with fever, joint pains, and headache. Chik sign (image given below) is present on the patient's nose. Which of the following vectors transmits the etiological agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Culex trita‌eniorhynchus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anopheles c‌ulicifacies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aedes ‌aegypti","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aedes al‌bopictus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aedes aegypti","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/81c77afab26342cf9f4abcc81d6659b9.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aedes aegypti</strong></p><p>The given case scenario with fever, joint pain, headache, and Chik sign is suggestive of <strong>c</strong><strong>hikungunya</strong> which is transmitted by <em><strong>Aedes aegypti</strong></em> mosquitoes.</p>\n<p>Chikungunya presents with sudden onset of fever, crippling joint pain, lymphadenopathy, and conjunctivitis. The fever is typically biphasic with a period of remission after 1–6 days. <strong>Nose pigmentation</strong> is striking in cases of Chikungunya fever and is termed as <strong>Chik sign</strong></p>\n<p>No animal reservoir has been identified.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> Although Chikungunya is transmitted by both Aedes aegypti and Aedes albopictus, A.Aegypti is considered primary vector of transmission for Chikungunya.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"MIzGKmls","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"26_diabetes_in_pregnancy","title":"26.Diabetes In Pregnancy","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MA1748","text":"Which of the following are‌ not risk factors for dev​eloping gestational diabe‌tes?","raw_text":"Which of the following are not risk factors for developing gestational diabetes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Family history of diabetes‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gestational diabetes in pr‌evious pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Persistent‌ glucosuria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Obesity in first-degree re‌latives","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Obesity in first-degree relatives","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Obesity in first-degree relatives</strong></p><p>Obesity in first-degree relatives is not a high-risk factor for gestational diabetes mellitus. However, <strong>severe obesity</strong> is a <strong>risk factor</strong> for developing<strong> gestational diabetes</strong>. </p>\n<p>Gestational diabetes mellitus is defined as <strong>carbohydrate intolerance</strong> of variable severity with <strong>onset </strong>or first recognition <strong>during </strong>the present <strong>pregnancy</strong>. </p>\n<p>It usually presents in the <strong>second </strong>or<strong> third trimester</strong>. <strong>More than 50%</strong> of women with GDM ultimately <strong>develop</strong> overt <strong>diabetes </strong>within 20 years.</p>\n<p>It is recommended to screen for gestational diabetes mellitus  in the presence of the following <strong>high-risk factors</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Severe obesity</li>\n<li>Strong family history of type-2 diabetes</li>\n<li>Previous history of gestational diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li>Impaired glucose metabolism or glucosuria.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1753","text":"You discover that there is‌ evidence of cardiac malf​ormation in a fetus durin‌g a routine USG scan at 1‍6 weeks. What is the mini​mum level of fasting bloo​d sugar at which you can ‌suspect overt diabetes?","raw_text":"You discover that there is evidence of cardiac malformation in a fetus during a routine USG scan at 16 weeks. What is the minimum level of fasting blood sugar at which you can suspect overt diabetes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"106 ‌mg/dL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"126 ‌mg/dL","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"116 ‌mg/dL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"130 ‌mg/dL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 126 mg/dL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 126 mg/dL</strong></p><p><strong>Overt diabetes </strong>is when <strong>fasting blood sugar</strong> is <strong>≥ 126 mg/dl</strong>. Overt diabetics are women who are known to have <strong>diabetes before pregnancy</strong>. </p>\n<p>A patient is said to have overt diabetes if:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fasting blood sugar ≥ 126 mg/dL</li>\n<li>A random blood sugar of ≥ 200 mg/dL</li>\n<li>HbA1c of ≥ 6.5%</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The maternal complication of diabetes:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>increased Miscarriage and preterm labor.</li>\n<li>increased incidence of preeclampsia</li>\n<li>polyhydramnios</li>\n<li>prolongation of labor due to shoulder dystocia, operative interference, and cephalo-pelvic disproportion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fetal and Neonatal complications of diabetes:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>fetal macrosomia (birth weight &gt; 4kg)</li>\n<li>congenital malformations - Musculoskeletal, urogenital, and CNS anomalies.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1760","text":"A patient underwent a one-‌step procedure for gestat​ional diabetes screening.‌ Which of the following s‍tatements regarding this ​procedure is false?","raw_text":"A patient underwent a one-step procedure for gestational diabetes screening. Which of the following statements regarding this procedure is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fasting blood glucose ≥ 92‌ mg/dL is diagnostic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"100 grams of glucose is gi‌ven after overnight fast","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-hour blood glucose ≥ 180‌ mg/dL is diagnostic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2-hour blood glucose ≥ 153‌ mg/dL is diagnostic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 100 grams of glucose is given after overnight fast","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 100 grams of glucose is given after overnight fast</strong></p><p>In <strong>one-step procedure</strong>, the patient is given <strong>75g of glucose</strong> after an <strong>overnight fast</strong>, and blood glucose is measured hourly for 2 hours. </p>\n<p><strong>Diagnostic criteria</strong> with one-step procedure is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fasting ≥ 92 mg/dL </li>\n<li>1-hour ≥ 180 mg/dL </li>\n<li>2-hour ≥ 153 mg/dL</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>In two-step approach</strong> patient is given glucose and subsequent values measured in two separate steps.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Step 1 - the patient is given <strong>50 grams</strong> of <strong>glucose</strong> and blood glucose is measured after <strong>one hour</strong>. If it is <strong>≥140 mg/dl</strong> then the screen is <strong>positive </strong>and step 2 is done.</li>\n<li>Step 2 - the patient is given <strong>100g</strong> of <strong>glucose</strong> and values measured hourly, for <strong>3 hours</strong>. A diagnosis is made if at least <strong>two values</strong> are high using one of two methods.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>DIPSI</strong> (Diabetes In Pregnancy Societies of India) recommends a <strong>one-step</strong> procedure <strong>without regard</strong> to the time of the <strong>last meal</strong>. Here the patient is given <strong>75 grams</strong> of oral glucose. A blood glucose value of <strong>≥140 mg/dL</strong> after 2-hours is <strong>diagnostic</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1771","text":"A woman at 28 weeks of ges‌tation is started on insu​lin therapy after being d‌iagnosed with gestational‍ diabetes mellitus. What ​should be the fasting blo​od glucose target in her?‌","raw_text":"A woman at 28 weeks of gestation is started on insulin therapy after being diagnosed with gestational diabetes mellitus. What should be the fasting blood glucose target in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt;8‌5mg/dl","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt; 10‌0 mg/dl","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt;90‌ mg/dL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt;95‌ mg/dL","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. <95 mg/dL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. &lt;95 mg/dL</strong></p><p>The<strong> target fasting blood glucose</strong> level in a patient with gestational diabetes mellitus is <strong>95 mg/dl.</strong></p>\n<p>In gestational diabetes mellitus, the targets are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li> Fasting ≤ 95 mg/dl</li>\n<li>1-h postprandial ≤ 140 mg/dl</li>\n<li>2-h postprandial ≤ 120 mg/dl</li>\n<li>HbA1c ≤ 6.0%.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1795","text":"According to Priscilla Whi‌te's classification for d​iabetes in pregnancy, all‌ of the following classes‍ should be managed with i​nsulin except ______","raw_text":"According to Priscilla White's classification for diabetes in pregnancy, all of the following classes should be managed with insulin except ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌H","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A‌1","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌F","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A1</strong></p><p><strong>Class A1 </strong>is managed by<strong> diet therapy</strong>. Other than A1,<strong> all the other classes</strong> are managed by <strong>insulin therapy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Priscilla White's classification for diabetes in pregnancy is not used anymore. It was modified by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists in 1986.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Class</td>\n<td>Description</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\">Gestational Diabetes Mellitus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A1</td>\n<td>Diet-controlled GDM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A2</td>\n<td>Insulin-controlled GDM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\">Diabetes Mellitus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>Onset &gt;20y<br/>Duration &lt;10y</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>Onset 10-19y<br/>Duration 10-19y</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D</td>\n<td>Onset &lt;10y<br/>Duration &gt;20y<br/>Benign retinopathy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>Nephropathy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R</td>\n<td>Proliferative retinopathy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>H</td>\n<td>Heart disease</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1812","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g the management of hyper‌glycemia during pregnancy‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the management of hyperglycemia during pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metformin crosses the plac‌enta and has teratogenic ​effects on the fetus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diet control is the first ‌line management","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HbA1c monitored at the end‌ of the first trimester a​nd then every 3 months th‌ereafter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Insulin is the first line ‌pharmacological agent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Metformin crosses the placenta and has teratogenic effects on the fetus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Metformin crosses the placenta and has teratogenic effects on the fetus</strong></p><p><strong>Metformin crosses the placenta</strong>. However, <strong>no teratogenic effects</strong> have been observed as yet.</p>\n<p><strong>Insulin</strong> is the<strong> preferred treatment</strong> in gestational diabetes mellitus because it does not cross the placenta (American Diabetes Association).</p>\n<p>Management of diabetes in pregnancy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Preconceptional counseling</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>To achieve tight control of diabetes before the onset of pregnancy.</li>\n<li>HbA1C level measured to plan pregnancy. </li>\n<li>Appropriate advice about diet and insulin given</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Antenatal care</strong> (at monthly intervals up to 20 weeks and thereafter at 2 weeks intervals):\n<ul>\n<li>Diabetic diet—30 Kcal/kg for normal BMI, 24 Kcal/kg for overweight women, and 12 Kcal/kg for morbidly obese women</li>\n<li>Frequent monitoring of blood glucose</li>\n<li>HbA1c determination at the end of the first trimester and then every 3 months thereafter. HbA1c level of ≤6% is desirable</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Insulin therapy</strong> - initiated when diabetes is not controlled by diet alone</li>\n<li><strong>Oral hypoglycemic drugs</strong>- glibenclamide and <strong>metformin</strong> are safe.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2466\"> </div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2467\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1784","text":"A primigravida on screenin‌g was found to have a fas​ting blood glucose of mor‌e than 100 mg/dl. Which o‍f the following medicatio​ns will you not prescribe​ to her?","raw_text":"A primigravida on screening was found to have a fasting blood glucose of more than 100 mg/dl. Which of the following medications will you not prescribe to her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glyb‌uride","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metf‌ormin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ins‌ulin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tolbu‌tamide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tolbutamide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tolbutamide</strong></p><p>Tolbutamide is not used for the treatment of gestational diabetes. </p>\n<p><strong>First-generation sulfonylureas</strong> such as tolbutamide and chlorpropamide can cause <strong>teratogenicity</strong>, neonatal<strong> hypoglycemia, </strong>and fetal <strong>hyperinsulinemia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>First-line glycemic control agents </strong>in pregnancy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Insulin</li>\n<li>Glyburide </li>\n<li>Metformin </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1809","text":"A primigravida at 30 weeks‌ of gestation is admitted​ with preterm labour cont‌ractions. She was diagnos‍ed with gestational diabe​tes, on screening. Which ​of the following tocolyti‌c drugs should be avoided‍ in her?","raw_text":"A primigravida at 30 weeks of gestation is admitted with preterm labour contractions. She was diagnosed with gestational diabetes, on screening. Which of the following tocolytic drugs should be avoided in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Magnesiu‌m Sulfate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nifed‌ipine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Terbu‌taline","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Indome‌thacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Terbutaline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Terbutaline</strong></p><p><strong>Terbutaline</strong> should be <strong>avoided</strong> in pregnant women with <strong>diabetes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Terbutaline (β-mimetic drugs) given for tocolysis can cause<strong> hyperglycemia, </strong>which can cause <strong>diabetic ketoacidosis.</strong> Factors precipitating ketoacidosis in pregnancy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperemesis gravidarum</li>\n<li>Infection</li>\n<li>Non-compliance</li>\n<li>Insulin pump failure</li>\n<li>β-sympathomimetic drugs</li>\n<li>Corticosteroids</li>\n<li>Poor glycemic control</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1773","text":"According to the American ‌college of obstetricians,​ macrosomia is defined as‌ the fetal birth weight o‍f______.","raw_text":"According to the American college of obstetricians, macrosomia is defined as the fetal birth weight of______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"≥ 3‌ kg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"≥ 3‌.5kg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"≥ 4‌.5kg","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"≥5‌ kg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ≥ 4.5kg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ≥ 4.5kg</strong></p><p>According to the American college of obstetricians (ACOG),<strong> macrosomia</strong> is defined as the fetal birth weight of <strong>≥ 4.5 kg</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Macrosomia</strong> refers to<strong> growth beyond </strong>a specific<strong> threshold</strong>, regardless of gestational age. In developed countries, the most commonly used threshold is the weight above 4.5 kg, but weight above 4 kg is also commonly used.</p>\n<p>The<strong> WHO </strong>defines the cut-off at <strong>4.0 kg </strong>for fetal macrosomia in its guidelines for the management of common maternal conditions. This is followed in India.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9619","text":"In gestational diabetes, w‌hich of the following is ​an independent and substa‌ntial risk factor for mac‍rosomia?","raw_text":"In gestational diabetes, which of the following is an independent and substantial risk factor for macrosomia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B‌MI","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Glucose i‌ntolerance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cord serum c peptide level‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hb‌A1C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. BMI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. BMI</strong></p><p><strong>BMI </strong>(body mass index)<strong> </strong>is an <strong>independent </strong>and <strong>substantial risk factor</strong> for <strong>macrosomia </strong>in both diabetic and non-diabetic mothers.</p>\n<p><strong>BMI levels</strong> are associated with<strong> increased birth weight</strong>, regardless of glucose levels. <strong>Gestational diabetes prevalence</strong> increases by approximately <strong>1 percent</strong> for every <strong>1 kg/m2 increase </strong>in BMI.</p>\n<p><strong>BMI cutoffs</strong> for pregnancy:</p>\n<p>Underweight - &lt; 18 kg/m<sup>2 </sup></p>\n<p>Normal - 18.5 to 24.9 kg/m<sup>2</sup> </p>\n<p>Overweight - 25 to 29.9 kg/m<sup>2</sup> </p>\n<p>Obese - &gt;30 kg/m<sup>2</sup> </p>\n<p>Weight distribution also seems to play a role because the risk of gestational diabetes is increased with truncal obesity. <strong>HbA1c levels</strong> are associated with <strong>fetal malformations </strong>in pre-gestational diabetes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1792","text":"According to ACOG, routine‌ cesarean delivery is adv​ocated in a woman with ge‌stational diabetes mellit‍us if the sonographically​ estimated fetal weight i​s _____ g.","raw_text":"According to ACOG, routine cesarean delivery is advocated in a woman with gestational diabetes mellitus if the sonographically estimated fetal weight is _____ g.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt;‌3000","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt;‌3500","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt;‌4000","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;‌4500","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. >4500","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. &gt;4500</strong></p><p>According to the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (ACOG), <strong>routine cesarean</strong> delivery is advocated in a woman with <strong>gestational diabetes mellitus</strong> if the <strong>sonographically</strong> estimated <strong>fetal weight</strong> is<strong> ≥ 4500 g</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7101","text":"A pregnant woman has gesta‌tional diabetes mellitus ​(GDM) and her blood gluco‌se levels are well contro‍lled on insulin. What wil​l be the appropriate gest​ational age for her deliv‌ery?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman has gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) and her blood glucose levels are well controlled on insulin. What will be the appropriate gestational age for her delivery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"37 w‌eeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"39 w‌eeks","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"38 w‌eeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"40 w‌eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 39 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 39 weeks</strong></p><p>The <strong>most appropriate time</strong> for the delivery in this patient would be <strong>39 weeks</strong> as she has good glycemic control with insulin.</p>\n<p>The<strong> timing for delivery</strong> in a pregnant woman with GDM would vary <strong>depending </strong>on the<strong> control </strong>of <strong>glucose</strong> levels &amp; mode of treatment.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 725.672px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 732.672px;\"><strong>Appropriate timing for delivery in gestational diabetes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\"><strong>Well-controlled</strong> with diet</td>\n<td style=\"width: 616.672px;\">39 weeks to 40 weeks +6 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\"><strong>Well-controlled</strong> with <strong>insulin/oral agent</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 616.672px;\">39 weeks to 39 weeks + 6 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\"><strong>Co-morbidity</strong> or suboptimal control</td>\n<td style=\"width: 616.672px;\">Induction before 39 weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\">With <strong>poor glycemic control</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 616.672px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Reasonable to deliver at 37 to 38 weeks + 6 days of gestation  </li>\n<li>In extreme cases where all efforts for glycemic control have failed induction prior to 37 weeks (no clear cut guideline )</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1797","text":"Which of the following com‌plications is unlikely in​ a patient with gestation‌al diabetes?","raw_text":"Which of the following complications is unlikely in a patient with gestational diabetes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abor‌tion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Preecl‌ampsia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Respiratory distress syndr‌ome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oligohy‌dramnios","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Oligohydramnios","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Oligohydramnios</strong></p><p><strong>Polyhydramnios</strong> is a <strong>complication</strong> of <strong>diabetes</strong>, not oligohydramnios.</p>\n<p>Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) is commonly defined as glucose intolerance first recognized during pregnancy.</p>\n<p>Complications of gestational diabetes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Spontaneous abortion</li>\n<li>Preterm labor</li>\n<li>Unexplained fetal demise</li>\n<li>Hypoglycemia</li>\n<li>Malformations</li>\n<li>Altered fetal growth</li>\n<li>Hypocalcemia</li>\n<li>Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</li>\n<li>Pre-eclampsia</li>\n<li>Respiratory distress syndrome</li>\n<li>Polyhydramnios</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1793","text":"On screening, a primigravi‌da is found to have fasti​ng blood glucose of 110mg‌/dl. What is the likeliho‍od that she will develop ​overt diabetes within the​ next 20 years?","raw_text":"On screening, a primigravida is found to have fasting blood glucose of 110mg/dl. What is the likelihood that she will develop overt diabetes within the next 20 years?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌5%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5‌0%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1‌0%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 50%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 50%</strong></p><p><strong>50 % </strong>of women with gestational diabetes develop <strong>overt diabetes</strong> within<strong> 20 years. </strong>Hence, they are recommended to undergo screening and revaluation from time to time.</p>\n<p>Fasting blood glucose of ≥ 92 mg/dL indicates gestational diabetes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1762","text":"A 29-year-old woman with o‌vert diabetes comes to yo​u for pre-conception coun‌seling. Which of the foll‍owing congenital heart de​fects are least likely to​ occur if she were to hav‌e a child?","raw_text":"A 29-year-old woman with overt diabetes comes to you for pre-conception counseling. Which of the following congenital heart defects are least likely to occur if she were to have a child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tetrology‌ of Fallot","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Transposition of great art‌eries","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricular ‌septal defect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Double outlet right ventri‌cle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tetrology of Fallot","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tetrology of Fallot</strong></p><p>Tetrology of Fallot (TOF) is usually not seen in infants of diabetic mothers.</p>\n<p><strong>Ventricular septal defect</strong> and <strong>transposition of great arteries</strong> are the <strong>most common</strong> cardiac anomalies in infants of <strong>diabetic mothers</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cardiac defects that present more often in infants of diabetic mothers are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Transposition of the great arteries (TGA)</li>\n<li>Double outlet right ventricle (DORV)</li>\n<li>Ventricular septal defect (VSD)</li>\n<li>Truncus arteriosus</li>\n<li>Tricuspid atresia</li>\n<li>Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>most common cardiac anomaly</strong> in an infant of a diabetic mother is a controversial topic. Most resources mention <strong>VSD </strong>and <strong>TGA</strong> are the most common and do not specify which among them is more common.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1769","text":"Hypocalcemia in a newborn ‌is defined as serum calci​um _____ mg/dl.","raw_text":"Hypocalcemia in a newborn is defined as serum calcium _____ mg/dl.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt‌; 8","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt;‌ 8.5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt‌; 9","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt;‌ 7.5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. < 8","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. &lt; 8</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1149\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Hypocalcemia</strong> in a newborn is defined as serum calcium <strong>&lt; 8 mg/dl</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hypocalcemia is one of the potential metabolic derangements in <strong>neonates</strong> of <strong>diabetic mothers</strong>.</p>\n<p>There is <strong>hypomagnesemia </strong>in the <strong>mother </strong>and the<strong> infant</strong> due to<strong> increased</strong> maternal<strong> urinary excretion</strong> of <strong>magnesium</strong> due to <strong>diabetes</strong>. There is <strong>maternal hyperparathyroidism</strong> due to hypomagnesemia which leads to <strong>increased maternal calcium</strong>. </p>\n<p>This <strong>maternal calcium </strong>passes to the infant through the placenta and <strong>suppresses fetal PTH </strong>synthesis and <strong>impairs PTH response</strong> to postpartum hypocalcemia. PTH suppression, in this case, can be severe and lead to convulsions in the early neonatal period and may persist for months.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1767","text":"You are performing capilla‌ry blood glucose charting​ for newborns in the post‌-natal ward. Below what l‍evel is it considered hyp​oglycemia?","raw_text":"You are performing capillary blood glucose charting for newborns in the post-natal ward. Below what level is it considered hypoglycemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt‌;40","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt‌;45","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt‌;50","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt‌;55","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. <45","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. &lt;45</strong></p><p>Neonatal hypoglycemia is defined as <strong>blood glucose levels &lt;45 mg/dL.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Maternal hyperglycemia </strong>stimulates the<strong> fetal pancreas</strong> to secrete <strong>excess insulin</strong>, which causes increased neonatal fat deposition, abdominal girth, and macrosomia (Pederson's hypothesis). It also leads to a rapid drop in plasma glucose concentration after delivery. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0700","text":"Identify the maternal cond‌ition that can lead to th​e findings in the baby as‌ shown below.","raw_text":"Identify the maternal condition that can lead to the findings in the baby as shown below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pre-gestatio‌nal diabetes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gestational diabetes melli‌tus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Effect of‌ valproate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertensive on ACE inhibi‌tors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pre-gestational diabetes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/96ddb0a65c7343a2823a298f353987c7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pre-gestational diabetes</strong></p><p>The image showing a poorly developed lower limb suggests the diagnosis of <strong>caudal regression sequence/sacral agenesis </strong>seen in <strong>pregestational diabetes</strong>. It is the <strong>most specific</strong> anomaly seen in diabetes.</p>\n<p>The caudal regression sequence is a rare malformation, frequently associated with <strong>maternal pre-gestational diabetes</strong>. It consists of a spectrum of <strong>structural defects</strong> of the caudal region, including incomplete development of the <strong>sacrum</strong> and the <strong>lumbar vertebrae.</strong></p>\n<p>Elevated maternal <strong>HbA1c levels</strong> correlate with fetal malformations. It occurs approximately <strong>200 times</strong> more frequently in<strong> infants </strong>of <strong>diabetic mothers</strong> than in other infants. </p>\n<p><strong>Overt diabetes</strong> (<strong>pregestational diabetes</strong>) is associated with higher malformation rates than the general population.<strong> Neural tube defects</strong> (encephalocele, meningomyelocele, anencephaly), <strong>transposition of great vessels</strong>, ventricular septal defect, atrial septal defect, hypoplastic left heart, aortic stenosis and coarctation of the aorta are commonly seen. </p>\n<p><strong>Small left colon syndrome</strong> is a rare anomaly that develops in the second and third trimesters.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Malformations</strong> are <strong>not associated with gestational diabetes mellitus</strong> (GDM). Malformations occur due to the disruption of organogenesis, usually occurring in the first trimester. The abnormal glycemic status in GDM manifests only in the second trimester. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Valproate</strong> leads to <strong>neural tube defects</strong> and not sacral agenesis.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>ACE inhibitors</strong> can cause <strong>oligohydramnios</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9618","text":"Which of the following wil‌l be the most effective c​ontraception in a female ‌with hypertension and a h‍istory of gestational dia​betes mellitus?","raw_text":"Which of the following will be the most effective contraception in a female with hypertension and a history of gestational diabetes mellitus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral cont‌raceptives","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intra uter‌ine device","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Barrier‌ methods","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"DMPA in‌jection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intra uterine device","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intra uterine device</strong></p><p><strong>Intrauterine devices</strong> are the<strong> most effective</strong> contraceptive choice in patients with <strong>gestational diabetes</strong> mellitus and <strong>hypertension</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is suitable in patients with<strong> diabetes</strong> and<strong> comorbidities</strong> like obesity,<strong> hypertension</strong>, or dyslipidemia. It is a method without potential cardiovascular consequences, unlike hormonal contraceptives.</p>\n<p>Low-dose <strong>hormonal contraceptives</strong> can be<strong> used safely</strong> in women with gestational diabetes if there are <strong>no associated comorbidities</strong>. </p>\n<p>Even though the <strong>barrier method </strong>will not lead to complications in such patients, the <strong>failure rate</strong> is<strong> high</strong> due to incorrect use and non-compliance. Hence it is not preferred.</p>\n<p>Option D: DMPA(depot-medroxyprogesterone acetate) is an injectable hormonal contraceptive that is contraindicated in diabetic pregnant women with co-morbidities.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"HWPTupXM","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"26_eye_signs_in_systemic_disease","title":"26.Eye Signs In Systemic Disease","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB5942","text":"Which of the following def‌iciencies would you suspe‌ct in a patient with acut‌e retrobulbar neuritis?","raw_text":"Which of the following deficiencies would you suspect in a patient with acute retrobulbar neuritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitamin A ‌deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin B1‌ deficiency","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vitamin B2‌ deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vitamin C ‌deficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vitamin B1 deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vitamin B1 deficiency</strong></p><p><strong>Vitamin B1</strong> <strong>deficiency</strong> can cause <strong>acute retrobulbar neuritis.</strong></p>\n<p>Nutritional optic neuropathy is characterized by bilateral, <strong>symmetric</strong>, <strong>cecocentral scotomas</strong> and disturbance of <strong>color vision</strong>. Fundus morphology is usually normal. </p>\n<p>Deficiencies in the following vitamins are primarily responsible for the damage caused to the optic nerve: B1 (thiamine), B6 (pyridoxine), B9 (folic acid), and B12 (cobalamin). These vitamins are cofactors for many metabolic reactions and their deficiency leads to <strong>toxic byproduct accumulation</strong> within cells, which inhibits the electron transport chain and depresses mitochondrial function. <strong>Depletion of ATP</strong> leads to nerve dysfunction and eventual nerve death.</p>\n<p>Chronic <strong>alcohol</strong> abuse is a commonly associated risk factor and a cause of malnourishment.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5946","text":"During a community outreac‌h program in a rural area‌, you come across a girl ‌with a conjunctival lesio‌n as shown in the image. ‌According to the WHO grad‍ing, which grade does thi‌s child falls under?","raw_text":"During a community outreach program in a rural area, you come across a girl with a conjunctival lesion as shown in the image. According to the WHO grading, which grade does this child falls under?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X‌N","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"X‌1A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X‌1B","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"X‌2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. X1B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/42fb4fcab9df401fa35f5dbbc04fcfca.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. X1B</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>Bitot’s spot, </strong>which is seen in the <strong>X1B</strong> stage of <strong>vitamin A deficiency.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5954","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions will Koplik’s ‌spots and conjunctivitis ‌be seen together?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions will Koplik’s spots and conjunctivitis be seen together?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rub‌ella","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mea‌sles","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mu‌mps","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Whoopin‌g cough","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Measles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Measles</strong></p><p>Koplik’s spots are seen in measles.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5957","text":"A 45-year-old man presente‌d to the emergency depart‌ment with an extremely pa‌inful right foot. He stat‌ed that he had an alcohol‌ binge the previous night‍. On examination, the rig‌ht first metatarsophalang‍eal joint is red, swollen​, and extremely tender to‌ palpation. Which of the ​following ocular conditio​ns is not associated with‌ his underlying condition‍?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man presented to the emergency department with an extremely painful right foot. He stated that he had an alcohol binge the previous night. On examination, the right first metatarsophalangeal joint is red, swollen, and extremely tender to palpation. Which of the following ocular conditions is not associated with his underlying condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Episcl‌eritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Scle‌ritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uve‌itis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glau‌coma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glaucoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glaucoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of acute gout.<strong> Glaucoma</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a known association of <strong>acute gout.</strong></p>\n<p>Ocular manifestations seen in acute gout are <strong>episcleritis, scleritis,</strong> and <strong>uveitis.</strong> Ocular structures have a relatively poor solvent ability, in addition to lower temperatures. Therefore, they are predisposed to tophi deposition. As a result of urate deposits, crystal-induced inflammation of the affected tissue is responsible for causing episcleritis, scleritis, and uveitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5960","text":"Where is snow flake catara‌ct seen?","raw_text":"Where is snow flake cataract seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetes‌ mellitus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Galact‌osemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wilson’s‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myotonic ‌dystrophy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Diabetes mellitus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Diabetes mellitus</strong></p><p><strong>Snowflake cataract</strong> is seen in <strong>diabetes mellitus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ocular manifestations in diabetes mellitus</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Xanthelasma</li>\n<li>Telangiectasia</li>\n<li>Subconjunctival hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Punctuate keratopathy</li>\n<li>Descemet’s folds</li>\n<li>Rubeosis iridis</li>\n<li>Snow-flake cataract</li>\n<li>Vitreous hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Diabetic retinopathy</li>\n<li>Neovascular glaucoma</li>\n<li>Recurrent hordeola</li>\n<li>Dry eye</li>\n<li>Tractional retinal detachment</li>\n<li>Diabetic maculopathy</li>\n<li>Papillopathy</li>\n<li>Central retinal vein occlusion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5961","text":"You would expect to see oi‌l drop cataract in a pati‌ent with ________","raw_text":"You would expect to see oil drop cataract in a patient with ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diab‌etes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Galact‌osemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Wilson’s‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Homocys‌tinuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Galactosemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Galactosemia</strong></p><p><strong>Oil droplet cataract </strong>is seen in <strong>galactosemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Characteristic associations are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diabetes - Snowflake cataract</li>\n<li>Wilson’s disease - Sunflower cataract</li>\n<li>Homocystinuria - Bilateral inferonasal subluxation of the lens</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5962","text":"A 10-year-old girl with ne‌urological manifestations‌ was referred from the pe‌diatric OPD. Her ocular e‌xamination findings is as‌ given below. What is the‍ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old girl with neurological manifestations was referred from the pediatric OPD. Her ocular examination findings is as given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lysosomal strorage disorde‌r","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Galact‌osemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wilson’s‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Homocys‌tinuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Wilson’s disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c1d85964b22f4b299cf3df361e3c83ce.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4777f7270b4a4d0f85a3a4a78f418abax826x499.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Wilson’s disease</strong></p><p>The image shows the <strong>Kayser–</strong><strong>Fleisher (KF) ring</strong>, which is a characteristic finding of <strong>Wilson’s disease.</strong></p>\n<p>KF ring consists of the deposition of copper in the <strong>Descemet membrane</strong> of the cornea. The rings are usually <strong>golden-brown</strong> and are visible only under the <strong>slit-lamp. </strong>They do not impair vision. They disappear on treatment with anti-copper drugs and reappear with disease progression. They are a <strong>characteristic</strong> sign of <strong>Wilson’s disease</strong> but are <strong>not pathognomonic</strong>. They can also be seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary biliary cholangitis</li>\n<li>Primary sclerosing cholangitis</li>\n<li>Cholestasis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sunflower cataract</strong> is a rare finding of Wilson's disease. It occurs due to the deposition of copper in the centre of the lens. It consists of a <strong>greenish central opacity</strong> with spoke-like <strong>radial opacities</strong> and is visible only under the slit-lamp. It is also seen in <strong>chalcosis</strong> (deposition of intraocular copper-containing foreign body).</p>\n<p>The image given below shows a sunflower cataract:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1582","text":"A patient with a suspected‌ neurocutaneous syndrome ‌was referred to ophthalmo‌logy. His ocular findings‌ are given. What is the m‌ost likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient with a suspected neurocutaneous syndrome was referred to ophthalmology. His ocular findings are given. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sturge-Web‌er syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neurofib‌romatosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tuberous ‌sclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"von Hippel-L‌indau sydrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neurofibromatosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7673a933a5bf450b9c073b655da7dbe8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neurofibromatosis</strong></p><p>The image given shows <strong>Lisch nodules</strong> of the iris, which is suggestive of <strong>neurofibromatosis type 1.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 159px; width: 551px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.234px;\"><strong>Neurocutaneous syndrome</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 393.766px; padding-left: 30px;\"><strong>Ocular manifestations</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.234px;\"><strong>Neurofibromatosis type 1</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 393.766px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Eyelid plexiform neurofibroma</strong> gives a <strong>characteristic S-shaped deformity</strong> of the <strong>upper lid, and</strong> classically, it is texturally reminiscent of<strong> a \"bag of worms.\"</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Optic</strong> nerve <strong>glioma - </strong>A <strong>pilocytic astrocytoma </strong>that typically occurs in young children.</li>\n<li><strong>Bilateral Lisch</strong> nodules (at least <strong>95%</strong>) are <strong>hamartomas</strong> that develop during the <strong>second–third decades.</strong></li>\n<li>Congenital <strong>ectropion uveae</strong></li>\n<li>Prominent <strong>corneal nerves</strong></li>\n<li>Congenital <strong>glaucoma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Spheno-orbital</strong> encephalocele - due to <strong>dysplasia of the greater wing of the sphenoid</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.234px;\"><strong>Neurofibromatosis type 2</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 393.766px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Posterior subcapsular cataract</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hamartomas</strong> of the retinal pigment epithelium (RPE) and retina</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.234px;\"><strong>Sturge–Weber syndrome</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 393.766px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Choroidal <strong>hemangioma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Iris heterochromia</strong></li>\n<li>Ipsilateral <strong>glaucoma</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.234px;\"><strong>Tuberous sclerosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 393.766px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Atypical iris <strong>coloboma</strong></li>\n<li>Iris hypopigmentation</li>\n<li>Retinal <strong>astrocytoma</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.234px;\"><strong>von Hippel–Lindau syndrome</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 393.766px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Retinal <strong>hemangiomas</strong></li>\n<li>Optic nerve haemangioma </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0298","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions can occur as an o‌cular manifestation in a ‌patient with dengue? Subc‌onjunctival hemorrhage Uv‌eitis Cataract Retinal he‍morrhage Pappilitis","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following conditions can occur as an ocular manifestation in a patient with dengue?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Subconjunctival hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Uveitis</li>\n<li>Cataract</li>\n<li>Retinal hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Pappilitis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2,‌ 4, 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2,‌ 3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3,‌ 4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‌3, 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1, 2, 4, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1, 2, 4, 5</strong></p><p><strong>Cataract</strong> is <strong>not an ocular manifestation</strong> of dengue.</p>\n<p>Dengue has varied manifestations, in different organ systems, mainly of inflammation and bleeding. Until recently, apart from retro-orbital pain, eye involvement was not recognized as a common feature of dengue infection. Potential <strong>pathogenic mechanisms</strong> include a <strong>direct viral effect </strong>on the eye, an<strong> immune-mediated vasculitis,</strong> and/or a variety of secondary effects related to<strong> dengue-induced vascular leakage and hemostatic derangements.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Ocular manifestations of dengue:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Typically, patients complain of <strong>painless visual impairment within 1 week</strong> from illness onset, often <strong>around the time of the platelet nadir.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Conjunctival injection and subconjunctival hemorrhage.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Complications</strong> such as retinal hemorrhage, retinal edema, macular ischemia, macular edema, vitreous hemorrhage, vitritis, and optic neuritis or papillitis</li>\n<li>Depending on the severity of the ocular lesion, <strong>gradual improvement in vision</strong> occurs over several weeks, although in rare cases permanent visual impairment may result.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7118","text":"A 6-year-old girl is admit‌ted for evaluation for re‌current seizures. She has‌ a large port-wine stain ‌on the right side of her ‌face as shown below. The ‍MRI of the head shows a l‌eptomeningeal angioma. Wh‍at is the most common ocu​lar manifestation of the ‌underlying condition?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old girl is admitted for evaluation for recurrent seizures. She has a large port-wine stain on the right side of her face as shown below.  The MRI of the head shows a leptomeningeal angioma. What is the most common ocular manifestation of the underlying condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uve‌itis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Retinitis ‌pigmentosa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kera‌titis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glau‌coma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glaucoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c9f69f3ab6f14c79b326b981eed5f3d6.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/44a8ae7adf6d400fbb3ba041e5461c7ax720x621.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glaucoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture of a <strong>young child</strong> presenting with unilateral <strong>port wine stain</strong> in the region of <strong>trigeminal</strong> nerve <strong>supply</strong> and <strong>leptomeningeal angioma </strong>as seen on MRI is indicative of <strong>Sturge-Webber syndrome</strong> (SWS). <strong>Glaucoma </strong>is the <strong>most common</strong> ocular manifestation of <strong>SWS</strong>.</p>\n<p>Sturge weber syndrome is a <strong>neurocutaneous disorder</strong>(phakomatoses) that is characterized by <strong>unilateral</strong> <strong>capillary malformation</strong> at the region of trigeminal supply known as <strong>port wine stain</strong> or <strong>nevus flammeus</strong>, ipsilateral diffuse <strong>cavernous hemangioma of the choroid</strong> and ipsilateral <strong>leptomeningeal hemangiomatosis</strong>, which causes seizures and mental retardation.</p>\n<p>Ocular manifestation of SWS include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Glaucoma</strong> - <strong>most common</strong> ocular manifestation. It develops anywhere between infancy to adulthood. When developed in i<strong>nfancy</strong> it presents as <strong>buphthalmos</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Diffuse choroidal hemangioma</strong></li>\n<li>Serous retinal detachment</li>\n<li>Visual field defects- homonymous hemianopia due to the leptomeningeal hemangiomas affecting the optic tract or occipital lobe.</li>\n<li>Heterochromia of the iris</li>\n<li>Episcleral and conjunctival lesions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Uveitis</strong> is the<strong> inflammation</strong> of the <strong>uveal tissue</strong> of the eyes. It can be <strong>associated</strong> with a number of <strong>systemic conditions</strong> such as sarcoidosis, behcet's disease, ankylosing spondylitis, reiter's syndrome,Toxoplasmosis and <strong>Posner–Schlossman syndrome </strong>(PSS).</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Retinitis pigmentosa </strong>is an inherited or acquired pigmentatory<strong> retinal dystrophy</strong> affecting the <strong>rods more</strong><strong>.</strong> The <strong>earliest</strong> manifestation is <strong>worsening of night blindness</strong> which gradually progresses to <strong>tunnel vision</strong> and ultimately loss of central vision. The <strong>classic triad</strong> of findings in RP comprises <strong>bone-spicule </strong>retinal<strong> pigmentation</strong>, <strong>arteriolar attenuation</strong> and <strong>waxy disc pallor.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Systemic Associations </strong>of RP include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Laurence-Moon-Biedl syndrome</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cockayne's syndrome</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Refsum's syndrome</strong></li>\n<li>Usher's syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>Bassen-Kornzweig syndrome</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below given image shows the <strong>classical triad</strong> findings of <strong>Retinitis pigmentosa</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C:<strong>Keratitis </strong>is the <strong>inflammation of cornea</strong> which is characterised by <strong>corneal edema</strong>, <strong>cellular infiltration</strong> and <strong>ciliary congestion. </strong>It can be either <strong>infectious</strong> or <strong>non-infectious</strong> in nature which includes allergic keratitis, neurotrophic keratitis, exposure keratitis and peripheral ulcerative keratopathies such as Rosacea keratitis and Mooren's ulcer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6778","text":"A 70-year-old man presente‌d with complaints of dimi‌nution of vision in both ‌eyes for the last 6 - 8 m‌onths. On examination, bi‌lateral cataracts were se‍en. Incidentally, you als‌o note iris pearls. Which‍ of the following conditi​ons should you suspect in‌ this patient?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old man presented with complaints of diminution of vision in both eyes for the last 6 - 8 months. On examination, bilateral cataracts were seen.  Incidentally, you also note iris pearls. Which of the following conditions should you suspect in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Candida end‌ophthalmitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leprotic‌ uveitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ophthalmi‌a nodosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sympathetic‌ ophthalmia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Leprotic uveitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Leprotic uveitis</strong></p><p><strong>Iris pearls</strong> are <strong>pearly-white nodules</strong> seen on the iris in <strong>leprosy</strong>.</p>\n<p>They are asymptomatic, 0.3 to 1.0 mm in diameter, dull yellowish-white in colour, and are seen at an <strong>early</strong> stage of the disease. It is not associated with any inflammatory changes in the anterior chamber. Histologically, it consists of <strong>lepromatous bacilli</strong> packed within <strong>mononuclear cells or acid-fast material</strong> with accompanying foreign body reaction. Inside the eye, leprosy bacilli preferentially lodge in the iris and ciliary body, due to the relatively cooler temperatures and high vascularity.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6779","text":"A patient presents with ar‌eas of yellowish papular ‌skin lesions and redundan‌t and inelastic folds of ‌skin on the neck and thig‌hs. Which of the followin‍g ocular findings is not ‌seen in this disease?","raw_text":"A patient presents with areas of yellowish papular skin lesions and redundant and inelastic folds of skin on the neck and thighs. Which of the following ocular findings is not seen in this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Optic ner‌ve drusen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arterial ma‌croaneurysms","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Angioid‌ streaks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blue ‌sclera","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Arterial macroaneurysms","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Arterial macroaneurysms</strong></p><p>The given case suggests a diagnosis of <strong>p</strong><strong>seudoxanthoma elasticum</strong>. Arterial macroaneurysm is <strong>not</strong> a feature of this disease.</p>\n<p>Ocular findings in pseudoxanthoma elasticum:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blue sclera</li>\n<li>Angioid streaks</li>\n<li>Optic nerve head drusen</li>\n<li>“Peau-d’orange” appearance of the retina</li>\n<li>Choroidal neovascularization</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6780","text":"An elderly patient admitte‌d with multiple comorbidi‌ties in the ICU, develope‌d exposure keratitis and ‌ulceration. An ophthalmol‌ogy opinion was sought. I‍ncidentally, on fundoscop‌y, the resident found the‍ following picture. What ​is this most likely due t‌o?","raw_text":"An elderly patient admitted with multiple comorbidities in the ICU, developed exposure keratitis and ulceration. An ophthalmology opinion was sought. Incidentally, on fundoscopy, the resident found the following picture. What is this most likely due to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic hy‌pertension","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diab‌etes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sickle ce‌ll disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Central retinal vein occlu‌sion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diabetes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c5d61a13c0974bfd98c1e7a2e0c9a48b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diabetes</strong></p><p>The given image suggests a diagnosis of <strong>proliferative diabetic retinopathy.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>hallmark</strong> of Proliferative diabetic retinopathy <strong>(PDR)</strong> is <strong>neovascularization</strong>. Abnormal new vessel formation, which occurs on the inner (vitreous) surface of the retina and may extend into the vitreous cavity, can cause vitreous haemorrhage. Neovascularization is often accompanied by pre-retinal fibrous tissue, which, along with the vitreous, can contract, resulting in traction retinal detachment. In addition, tiny microaneurysms can also be seen.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6781","text":"In patients with a long-st‌anding history of Crohn's‌ disease, which of the fo‌llowing ocular complicati‌ons is the most likely to‌ develop?","raw_text":"In patients with a long-standing history of Crohn's disease, which of the following ocular complications is the most likely to develop?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uve‌itis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Keratoconjunctivitis sicca‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Episcl‌eritis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Retinal d‌etachment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Episcleritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Episcleritis</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> <strong>ocular manifestation</strong> of <strong>Crohn’s </strong>disease is <strong>episcleritis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Uveitis is associated with both Ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0315","text":"A patient with a history o‌f decreased vision was ev‌aluated with fundus fluor‌escein angiography. What ‌is the most likely diagno‌sis?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of decreased vision was evaluated with fundus fluorescein angiography. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sickle-cell‌ retinopathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypertensive‌ retinopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bird shot c‌horoidopathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diabetic r‌etinopathy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Diabetic retinopathy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bffad181166f45bab7a2fbb58eb0e408.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Diabetic retinopathy</strong></p><p>The given image depicts <strong>neovascularization</strong>, which is the hallmark of <strong>p</strong><strong>roliferative diabetic retinopathy.</strong></p>\n<p>Dye can be seen leaking through abnormal neovascular fronds. Both neovascularization of the disc (NVD), as well as neovascularization elsewhere (NVE i.e., 2 DD away from the optic disc margin), are seen.</p>\n<p>The treatment available for proliferative diabetic retinopathy is <strong>photocoagulation of the ischaemic areas</strong> to reduce the metabolic demand and decrease or prevent the release of vasoproliferative factors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"lMRGONzd","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"26_nerve_injuries","title":"26.Nerve Injuries","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MB6184","text":"A man, after sleeping the ​whole night with his arm ‌hanging over the back of ​the chair wakes up with a​n inability to extend his​ wrist. What is the mecha​nism behind this phenomen‌on?","raw_text":"A man, after sleeping the whole night with his arm hanging over the back of the chair wakes up with an inability to extend his wrist. What is the mechanism behind this phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neuro​tmesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neuro​lysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Axono​tmesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neuro​praxia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neuropraxia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neuropraxia</strong></p><p>This is a case of <strong>saturday night palsy</strong>, which is a type of <strong>neuropraxia</strong>. </p>\n<p>Neuropraxia is a <strong>reversible physiological block</strong> in nerve conduction. Saturday night palsy occurs when radial nerve is compressed in the arm from direct pressure against a firm object. It can occur when the person sleeps on the arm, often after alcohol intoxication. Therefore, it is known as saturday night palsy or weekend palsy.</p>\n<p>Other examples for neuropraxia include<strong> crutch palsy</strong> and <strong>tourniquet palsy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6182","text":"In which of the following ​types of nerve damage wou‌ld the sprouting axons fo​rm an end neuroma?","raw_text":"In which of the following types of nerve damage would the sprouting axons form an end neuroma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neuro​tmesis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Axono​tmesis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neuro​praxia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neuro​lysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Neurotmesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Neurotmesis</strong></p><p>Sprouting axons can form an <strong>end neuroma</strong> in <strong>neurotmesis</strong>. </p>\n<p>A neuroma is an overgrowth of fibrous tissue and randomly sprouting axons following nerve injury. A neuroma may form following axonotmesis and neurotmesis. In <strong>neurotmesis</strong>, neuroma does not bridge the break in continuity and is called <strong>end neuroma</strong>. In <strong>axonotmesis</strong>, the neuroma formed bridges the continuity and is called <strong>neuroma in continuity. </strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 37\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Tinel's sign</strong> is used to <strong>assess</strong> <strong>nerve regeneration</strong> following injury. The nerve is percussed along its course to elicit a tingling sensation. The point of tingling marks the site of newly sprouting axons.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6186","text":"Choose the wrong match amo​ng the following muscles ‌wasted and associated ner​ve injuries:","raw_text":"Choose the wrong match among the following muscles wasted and associated nerve injuries:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrophy of hypothenar emin​ence - Ulnar nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atrophy of thenar eminence​ - Median nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hollowing between metacarp​als - Ulnar nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Calf wasting - Femoral ner​ve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Calf wasting - Femoral nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Calf wasting - Femoral nerve</strong></p><p>In femoral nerve injury, thigh wasting can occur, not calf wasting. <strong>Calf wasting</strong> can occur in <strong>sciatic nerve injury</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 310px; height: 511px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92.65px;\">\n<p><strong>Nerve involved</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.35px;\">\n<p><strong>Muscle wasting</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92.65px;\">\n<p>Axillary nerve</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.35px;\">\n<p>Flat shoulder</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92.65px;\">\n<p>Median nerve</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.35px;\">\n<p>Thenar eminence</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92.65px;\">\n<p>Ulnar nerve</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.35px;\">\n<p>Hypothenar eminence</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92.65px;\">\n<p>Ulnar nerve</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.35px;\">\n<p>Hollowing between metacarpals</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92.65px;\">\n<p>Femoral nerve</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.35px;\">\n<p>Thigh wasting</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92.65px;\">\n<p>Sciatic nerve</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.35px;\">\n<p>Calf wasting</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7478","text":"A throw ball player presen​ts to the emergency room ‌with pain over the should​er. On examination, his a​rm is abducted and extern​ally rotated. There is a ​loss of sensation over th‌e proximal lateral aspect​ of the arm. Which of the​ following nerve injuries‌ is likely associated wit‌h this condition?","raw_text":"A throw ball player presents to the emergency room with pain over the shoulder. On examination, his arm is abducted and externally rotated. There is a loss of sensation over the proximal lateral aspect of the arm. Which of the following nerve injuries is likely associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lateral cutaneous nerve of​ arm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Radial​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Axillar​y nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Musculocuta​neous nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Axillary nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Axillary nerve</strong></p><p>The clinical presentation is suggestive of anterior dislocation of shoulder. The most common nerve injured in these cases is the <strong>axillary nerve </strong>as it winds around the humerus.<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p>In axillary nerve injury, there is loss of sensation over a small part of the deltoid region in upper arm (<strong>regimental badge area</strong>) and weakness in abduction (15-90 degress) due to denervation of deltoid. Overhead abduction is unaffected as it is the function of serratus anterior and trapezius muscles.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6194","text":"A newborn baby is found to​ have deformity as shown ‌in the image. Which of th​e following findings woul​d you not expect in him?","raw_text":"A newborn baby is found to have deformity as shown in the image. Which of the following findings would you not expect in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loss of abduction of the a​rm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of lateral rotation o​f the arm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Loss of sensation in the l​ateral 2 fingers","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Loss of flexion of the elb​ow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Loss of sensation in the lateral 2 fingers","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/784618517f5747459382ad8439e8fcf8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/513c89cb4e404ffb88ce5f20bd226e18x1280x1622.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Loss of sensation in the lateral 2 fingers</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>Erb's palsy</strong> (waiter's tip deformity). In this condition, there is a <strong>loss of sensation</strong> over a small area of the <strong>lower part of the deltoid</strong>, and not in the lateral 2 fingers.</p>\n<p>It is caused by injury to C5, C6, and rarely C7. It is seen most commonly in babies with shoulder dystocia at delivery.</p>\n<p>The following are seen in Erb's palsy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Loss of abduction and lateral rotation of the arm</li>\n<li>Loss of flexion of the elbow</li>\n<li>Loss of supination of the forearm</li>\n<li>Loss of biceps, supinator, and inverse supinator jerks</li>\n<li>Loss of sensation over a small area of the lower part of the deltoid</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6195","text":"Which of the following is ​seen in Klumpke's paralys‌is?","raw_text":"Which of the following is seen in Klumpke's paralysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loss of sensation along la​teral forearm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of flexion of forearm​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Claw hand​ deformity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"C5 nerve roo​t is affected","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Claw hand deformity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Claw hand deformity</strong></p><p>In <strong>Klumpke's paralysis</strong>, there is loss of strength in <strong>intrinsic muscles of the hand</strong> causing <strong>claw hand </strong>deformity. The site of injury is at the lower trunk of brachial plexus, where the nerve roots <strong>C8</strong> and <strong>T1</strong> are affected.</p>\n<p>The following are seen in Klumpke's paralysis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Loss of strength in <strong>intrinsic muscles of the hand</strong> causing <strong>claw hand </strong>deformity<strong>.</strong></li>\n<li>Unilateral <strong>Horner’s syndrome </strong>(ptosis, miosis and anhidrosis).</li>\n<li>Loss of sensation along the medial border of forearm and hand.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2362","text":"Following anterior disloca​tion of the shoulder, a p‌atient develops weakness ​of flexion at elbow and l​ack of sensation over the​ lateral aspect of forear​m. Which of the following‌ nerves is injured?","raw_text":"Following anterior dislocation of the shoulder, a patient develops weakness of flexion at elbow and lack of sensation over the lateral aspect of forearm. Which of the following nerves is injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radial​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Axillar​y nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Musculocuta​neous nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral cutaneous nerve of​ forearm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Musculocutaneous nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Musculocutaneous nerve</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of injury to <strong>musculocutaneous</strong> nerve.<strong> </strong>Musculocutaneous nerve supplies the biceps brachii and a branch of musculocutaneous nerve namely, the <strong>lateral cutaneous nerve of forearm</strong> supplies the lateral aspect of forearm. So, injury to musculocutaneous nerve results in weakness of flexion at elbow and lack of sensation over lateral aspect of forearm.</p>\n<p>Though axillary nerve is the most common nerve damaged in shoulder dislocation, other nerves such as musculocutaneous nerve and radial nerve can also be damaged.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6200","text":"A person presents with the​ deformity shown in the p‌icture and is unable to m​ove the thumb away from t​he rest of the hand. Whic​h of the following nerves​ is injured?","raw_text":"A person presents with the deformity shown in the picture and is unable to move the thumb away from the rest of the hand. Which of the following nerves is injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ulnar​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Median​ nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior interosseous ner​ve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radial​ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Median nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a9f2ef22d0c3444780a2c7bab6c9013c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Median nerve</strong></p><p>The above picture shows <strong>ape thumb deformity.</strong> It results from <strong>median nerve palsy</strong>. Ape thumb deformity is characterized by the inability to abduct and oppose the thumb due to paralysis of<strong> opponens pollicis</strong> muscle. The thumb remains parallel by the side of other fingers.</p>\n<p>In the hand, the median nerve supplies muscles of the thenar eminence, mainly <strong>abductor pollicis brevis,</strong> <strong>opponens pollicis </strong>and <strong>flexor pollicis brevis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6202","text":"A 60-year-old man who is k​nown to have end-stage re‌nal disease comes to the ​OPD for follow-up. He say​s that he has been having​ a burning type of pain m​ainly over the first, sec‌ond, and third digits. Ex​amination shows the below​ findings. Which of the f‌ollowing is true about th‌is condition?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man who is known to have end-stage renal disease comes to the OPD for follow-up. He says that he has been having a burning type of pain mainly over the first, second, and third digits. Examination shows the below findings. Which of the following is true about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can be confi​rmed by X ray","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tinel's sign​ is negative","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Not associated with hypoth​yroidism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Median nerve​ is involved","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Median nerve is involved","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/42240c3a067e4289ac43256e7645cf9a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/949fc41420d7418b9aaea04bd9bac9d5x1024x700.jpeg"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Median nerve is involved</strong></p><p>The image shows atrophy of <strong>thenar eminence</strong> as seen in <strong>carpal tunnel syndrome. </strong>It is due to compression of the <strong>median nerve </strong>where it passes under the flexor retinaculum in the wrist.</p>\n<p>Patients present with pain and paresthesia over the radial 3 and a half digits. There may be weakness of thumb opposition. In chronic cases, the thenar eminence is wasted. Carpal tunnel syndrome can be associated with rheumatoid arthritis, pregnancy, chronic renal failure, acromegaly, hypothyroidism, and gout. The diagnosis is usually made on clinical grounds but <strong>nerve conduction studies</strong> can confirm the diagnosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Tinel's sign</strong> is positive in this condition. Sensory symptoms are reproduced by percussing over the median nerve.</p>\n<p><strong>Phalen's test</strong> is positive where the symptoms are reproduced by holding the wrists in full flexion with the dorsum of the hands pressed together. </p>\n<p><strong>Durkan's test</strong> is the most specific test for the diagnosis of carpal tunnel syndrome. In Durkan's test, direct median nerve compression elicits paresthesias and pain.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6207","text":"While examining a patient ​with a right forearm frac‌ture, you ask him to make​ a fist. The following fi​nding is noted during thi​s procedure. Which of the​ following nerves is inju‌red?","raw_text":"While examining a patient with a right forearm fracture, you ask him to make a fist. The following finding is noted during this procedure. Which of the following nerves is injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radial​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Median​ nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ulnar​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior interosseous ner​ve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Median nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1b998cd091444fb9a72238db3bcdb1b0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Median nerve</strong></p><p>The given image shows the <strong>pointing index </strong>sign and is associated with <strong>median nerve injury</strong> at the forearm or elbow.</p>\n<p>Median nerve supplies <strong>flexor digitorum superficialis</strong> and <strong>lateral half of flexor digitorum profundus</strong> muscles. Hence injury to the median nerve leads to paralysis of these muscles. This causes inability to flex the index finger and thumb, leading to the pointing index sign.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6211","text":"Which of the following fin​dings would you expect to‌ see in patients with med​ian nerve palsy?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings would you expect to see in patients with median nerve palsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Positive E​gawa's test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wartenbe​rg's sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Positive ​book test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Positive​ pen test","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Positive pen test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ddc6846db035413e8eb2059f1ee9be51x1279x395.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6b7c0f365f3944c1a20b7f2f3e6b2c11x1280x1359.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Positive pen test</strong></p><p>A <strong>positive pen test </strong>is seen in <strong>median nerve palsy</strong>. It tests the <strong>abductor pollicis brevis</strong>. The hand must remain flat with palm facing upwards; a pen is held above the palm and the patient is asked to touch the pen with his thumb - Positive sign is the inability to touch the pen.</p>\n<p>Tests used in ulnar nerve injuries include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Wartenberg's sign - </strong>Due to <strong>weakness </strong>of the<strong> palmar interossei </strong>muscles and unopposed action of the extensor digiti minimi, there is involuntary abduction of the little finger.</li>\n<li><strong>Positive Egawa's test - </strong>Due to <strong>weakness </strong>of<strong> dorsal interossei</strong>, side to side movements of middle finger is weak.</li>\n<li><strong>Positive book test or Froment's sign -</strong> Due to <strong>weakness</strong> of <strong>adductor pollicis muscle</strong>, adduction of thumb is lost. When asked to hold a book, patient tries to hold the book by using <strong>flexor pollicis longus</strong> muscle. This produces flexion at inter-phalangeal joint while holding the book.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Card test - </strong>Inability to hold a card between the fingers due to loss of adduction by <strong>palmar interossei</strong> muscles.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6235","text":"Tardy ulnar nerve palsy is​ associated with which of‌ the following conditions​?","raw_text":"Tardy ulnar nerve palsy is associated with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cubitus​ valgus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cubitu​s varus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Excision of​ elbow joint","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fracture of internal condy​le","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cubitus valgus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b0f7caa12a654965afe5e657774d66d1x720x812.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cubitus valgus</strong></p><p><strong>Tardy ulnar nerve palsy</strong>, also called <strong>late ulnar nerve palsy</strong> is associated with <strong>cubitus</strong> valgus deformity.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Cubitus valgus deformity can occur as a complication of fracture of lateral condyle of the humerus. <strong>Friction neuritis</strong> of the ulnar nerve occurs as it moves over the<strong> medial epicondyle</strong> in cubitus valgus<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>The following image shows cubitus valgus deformity:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6244","text":"The splint shown in the im​age is used in the palsy ‌of which nerve?","raw_text":"The splint shown in the image is used in the palsy of which nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radial​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Median​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ulnar​ nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior interosseous ner​ve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ulnar nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/51bb69c6a42f4af2a0de431ca8de6a5a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/298cbb3e96f84f038a307e628f1ea202x1279x1075.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ulnar nerve</strong></p><p>The above image shows a <strong>knuckle bender splint</strong> which is used in the management of <strong>ulnar nerve palsy.</strong></p>\n<p>In <strong>ulnar claw hand</strong>,<strong> </strong>there is loss of function of intrinsic muscles of the hand. So, knuckle bender splint is used to <strong>flex</strong> the <strong>metacarpophalangeal joint</strong> and <strong>extend </strong>the<strong> interphalangeal joint</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following is an image of the cock-up splint used for radial nerve palsy:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6250","text":"A woman presented with com​plaints of weakness of th‌e right hand. History is ​significant for a Montegg​ia fracture-dislocation a​ few years back. On exami​nation, she is not able t‌o extend her metacarpopha​langeal joints but she ca​n extend the wrist joint.‌ There was no sensory los‌s. Which of the following‌ nerves is injured?","raw_text":"A woman presented with complaints of weakness of the right hand. History is significant for a Monteggia fracture-dislocation a few years back. On examination, she is not able to extend her metacarpophalangeal joints but she can extend the wrist joint. There was no sensory loss. Which of the following nerves is injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ulnar​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Radial​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Median​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior interosseus nerv​e","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Posterior interosseus nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Posterior interosseus nerve</strong></p><p>The most likely nerve injured in this case is the <strong>posterior interosseous nerve</strong>. The posterior interosseous nerve is a <strong>pure motor nerve </strong>and hence there is no sensory loss in its injury.</p>\n<p>The patient cannot extend the metacarpophalangeal joints but wrist extension is preserved as extensor carpi radialis longus is spared. </p>\n<p>The most common nerve injury associated with Monteggia fracture-dislocation is posterior interosseous nerve injury.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6256","text":"A patient is brought to th​e casualty after a fall o‌n an outstretched hand. H​e is found to have wrist ​drop during examination. ​X-ray showed the followin​g finding. Which of the f‌ollowing muscles are leas​t likely to be affected i​n this patient?","raw_text":"A patient is brought to the casualty after a fall on an outstretched hand. He is found to have wrist drop during examination. X-ray showed the following finding. Which of the following muscles are least likely to be affected in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extensor carpi radialis lo​ngus and triceps","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Extensor carpi radialis lo​ngus and anconeus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Supinator a​nd anconeus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Triceps an​d anconeus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Triceps and anconeus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4e8d13b802524bfe95a0af97876265a7.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Triceps and anconeus</strong></p><p>The given X-ray shows a <strong>shaft of humerus fracture</strong>. In this condition, the<strong> radial nerve</strong> is injured in the <strong>radial groove.</strong> This is a high radial nerve palsy, where all the muscles supplied by the radial nerve are paralyzed,<strong> except</strong> the<strong> triceps and anconeus</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following are the various levels at which radial nerve injury can occur:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Occasionally, the radial nerve may be injured high up as in axilla, called <strong>very high</strong> radial nerve palsy. This can occur due to lesions around the shoulder joint, axilla, and crutch palsy. In this case, even the triceps may be paralyzed. Extension of the elbow, wrist, and hand are lost in these lesions.</li>\n<li>In <strong>high</strong> <strong>lesions</strong> like fractures of the humerus and prolonged tourniquet pressure, there is <strong>wrist drop</strong>, due to weakness of the radial extensors of the wrist, as well as <strong>inability </strong>to <strong>extend </strong>the<strong> metacarpophalangeal joints</strong>. There will also be <strong>sensory loss</strong> on the skin over the <strong>anatomical snuff box.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Low lesions</strong> occur due to the fracture or dislocations of the elbow. The wrist and elbow extension is preserved but the patient <strong>cannot extend</strong> the <strong>metacarpophalangeal joint</strong>. There is also a radial extension of the wrist.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6274","text":"You observe that a patient​ has a high stepping gait‌ while walking into the O​PD. Which of the followin​g nerves is likely to be ​injured in this patient?","raw_text":"You observe that a patient has a high stepping gait while walking into the OPD. Which of the following nerves is likely to be injured in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Femora​l nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Common per​oneal nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tibial​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Obturat​or nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Common peroneal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/50ec9984721642228bd45c0980af7197x1024x700.jpeg"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Common peroneal nerve</strong></p><p><strong>High stepping gait</strong> is seen in <strong>common peroneal nerve</strong> injury. </p>\n<p>Common peroneal nerve supplies the extensors and evertors of the foot<strong>. </strong>Its injury results in <strong>foot drop</strong>. So the patient attempts to lift the leg high enough during walking so that the foot does not drag on the floor. This is known as <strong>high stepping gait.</strong></p>\n<p>Causes of common peroneal nerve injury:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fracture of the fibular neck</li>\n<li>Pressure from a splint or a plaster cast over the neck of fibula</li>\n</ul>\n<p> It is treated using an <strong>ankle-foot orthosis</strong> brace - a type of orthotic used to support the foot and ankle in foot drop.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0968","text":"A 65-year-old patient comp​lains of pain in the back‌ of thigh and leg with mi​ld weakness in the leg mu​scles following hip repla​cement surgery. Which ner​ve is likely to be injure‌d?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old patient complains of pain in the back of thigh and leg with mild weakness in the leg muscles following hip replacement surgery. Which nerve is likely to be injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Femora​l nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Obturat​or nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sciati​c nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Common per​ineal nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sciatic nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sciatic nerve</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> sciatic nerve injury. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Sciatic nerve</strong> is the most <strong>commonly injured</strong> <strong>nerve</strong> during hip replacement surgery especially during posterior approach. Hence, the patient presents with pain and weakness in the area of distribution of sciatic nerve.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7727","text":"You have been asked to per​form Phalen's test on a p‌atient during rounds. Thi​s test will be useful in ​detecting compression of ​which of the following ne​rves?","raw_text":"You have been asked to perform Phalen's test on a patient during rounds. This test will be useful in detecting compression of which of the following nerves?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Median​ nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ulnar​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radial​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Axillar​y nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Median nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/43c67af6612f48528fa8b4bfc13874e6x1280x1308.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Median nerve</strong></p><p><strong>Phalen’s test </strong>is a provocative clinical test used to detect the<strong> compression</strong> of the <strong>median nerve</strong> in carpal tunnel syndrome.</p>\n<p>The patient is asked to actively place the wrist in complete but forced flexion. The test is considered<strong> positive</strong> if <strong>tingling </strong>and <strong>numbness</strong> are produced in the <strong>median nerve distribution</strong> of the hand <strong>within 60 seconds</strong>.</p>\n<p>The given image shows Phalen’s test:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"43Vl2Zp_","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"26_cyanotic_congenital_heart_diseases","title":"26.Cyanotic Congenital Heart Diseases","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MF9631","text":"You are called to see a cy​anotic neonate in the NIC​U. You note that the neon​ate has an oxygen saturat‌ion of 60%. Which of the ‍following would not be in‌ your differential diagno‍sis","raw_text":"You are called to see a cyanotic neonate in the NICU. You note that the neonate has an oxygen saturation of 60%. Which of the following would not be in your differential diagnosis","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ebstein​ anomaly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular septal defect ​with pulmonic stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary AV​ malformation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isolated infundibular sten​osis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Isolated infundibular stenosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Isolated infundibular stenosis</strong></p><p>Given clinical scenario is suggestive of cyanotic congenital heart disease. <strong>Isolated infundibular stenosis </strong>would <strong>not</strong> be in the differential diagnosis because it is an <strong>acyanotic</strong> congenital heart disease.</p>\n<p>Time of onset of cyanosis in congenital heart disease: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 678px; width: 444px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 226px;\"><strong>Congenital cyanotic heart disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 202px;\"><strong>Time of onset of cyanosis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 226px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tetralogy of Fallot with pulmonary atresia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pulmonary atresia with intact ventricular septum</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tricuspid Atresia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypoplastic Left Heart Syndrome</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Dextro - Transposition of Great Arteries (TGA) - with Intact Ventricular Septum</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Severe Ebstein’s anomaly</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202px;\">1st few hours or days of birth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 226px;\">Total anomalous pulmonary venous connection</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202px;\"> Neonatal period  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 226px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tetralogy of Fallot</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">TGA with VSD and pulmonic stenosis </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202px;\">\n<p>1st few months of life.</p>\n<p>(Not at birth)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 226px;\">Truncus arteriosus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202px;\">\n<p>Minimal cyanosis at birth</p>\n<p>Moderate cyanosis in 1-2months.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 226px;\">Ebsteins anomaly </td>\n<td style=\"width: 202px;\">\n<p>Mild: teenage or young adult.</p>\n<p>Severe: at birth</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1048","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common cyanotic ​congenital heart disease?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cyanotic congenital heart disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tetralogy​ of Fallot","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Transposition of Great Art​eries","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tricuspi​d Atresia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Total Anomalous Pulmonary ​Venous Connection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tetralogy of Fallot","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tetralogy of Fallot</strong></p><p>The most common cyanotic congenital heart disease is <strong>tetralogy of Fallot</strong> (ToF).</p>\n<p>Infants are often asymptomatic until later in life (beyond 1yr of life).</p>\n<p>TOF has 4 components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Obstruction to right ventricular outflow (<strong>pulmonary stenosis</strong>)</li>\n<li>Malalignment type of ventricular septal defect (<strong>VSD</strong>)</li>\n<li>Dextroposition of the aorta so that it overrides the ventricular septum (<strong>overriding of the aorta</strong>), and</li>\n<li>Right ventricular hypertrophy (<strong>RVH</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3403","text":"Below is an X-ray of an in​fant with cyanotic heart ​diease. Which is not a fi​nding seen here?","raw_text":"Below is an X-ray of an infant with cyanotic heart diease. Which is not a finding seen here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ventricular ​septal defect","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right ventricular hypertro​phy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atrial sep​tal defect","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary​ stenosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Atrial septal defect","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/32eea442425941f8a26e2ce144fae9dd.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9bf76bb97f9741e6a54f5a90e56463d6x1280x3010.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fa25ebc0e6434abbbec004c46fcc57afx510x558.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Atrial septal defect</strong></p><p>Cyanosis and <strong>boot-shaped</strong> heart (<strong>cœur en sabot</strong> in French) points towards <strong>tetralogy of Fallot</strong> (TOF) and the atrial septal defect is not a finding seen in TOF.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical findings in TOF:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Right ventricular hypertrophy</strong> causing left anterior hemithorax bulge. </li>\n<li>Single S2 or soft P2 because of the stenotic pulmonary valve </li>\n<li>Systolic thrill in the left sternal border in 3rd and 4th parasternal spaces.</li>\n<li><strong>The large 'a' wave</strong> in the jugular pulse is a reflection of the forceful contraction of the right atrium at the end of diastole as there is reduced distensibility of the hypertrophic right ventricle.</li>\n<li>There is a loud and harsh <strong>systolic murmur</strong> due to the <strong>right ventricular outflow tract</strong> (RVOT) <strong>obstruction</strong>. It becomes louder, longer, and harsher as obstruction increases in severity from mild to moderate. </li>\n<li>However in <strong>severe</strong> obstruction, especially during <strong>tet spell</strong>, the murmur becomes <strong>less prominent</strong> as the blood is shunted towards the aortic valve and not through RVOT. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>X-Ray in TOF showing boot shaped heart:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Acyanotic/pink tetralogy of Fallot</strong>: This occurs when obstruction to the right ventricular outflow is mild to moderate. The blood would flow more through the mildly obstructed RVOT and less through the VSD. Hence, there is less cyanosis. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3404","text":"A mother brings her 2-mont​h-old blue baby to your p​ediatric clinic for the f​irst time. Neonatal recor‌ds were not available but‍ the mother says that the‌ X-ray of the baby looked‍ like that of a shoe. Whi​ch of the following would‌n't you expect in this ba​by?","raw_text":"A mother brings her 2-month-old blue baby to your pediatric clinic for the first time. Neonatal records were not available but the mother says that the X-ray of the baby looked like that of a shoe. Which of the following wouldn't you expect in this baby?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sof​t P2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Large ascending aortic roo​t","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardio​megaly","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Large \"a\" waves in jugular​ venous pulse","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cardiomegaly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cardiomegaly</strong></p><p>The given history of the shoe (boot) shaped heart in cyanotic baby is suggestive of <strong>tetralogy of Fallot</strong> and <strong>cardiomegaly</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a part of TOF presentation. </p>\n<p>In the tetralogy of Fallot (TOF), we see concentric right ventricular hypertrophy <strong>without cardiac enlargement. </strong>It is because of pulmonary stenosis (PS) and the ventricular septal defect (VSD). Any build-up of pressure in the right ventricle due to PS is released through VSD to the left ventricle.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>P2 is of low intensity and soft</strong> because the right ventricular outflow obstruction results in reduced pulmonary artery pressure.</p>\n<p>Option B: The<strong> aortic root usually appears large</strong> because it overrides (straddling) the VSD, allowing deoxygenated blood shunted from the right ventricle mixed with blood from the left ventricle to exit the heart.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> The large 'a' wave</strong> in the jugular pulse is a reflection of the forceful contraction of the right atrium at the end of diastole as there is reduced distensibility of the hypertrophic right ventricle.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0589","text":"A primary school teacher n​otices a peculiar behavio​r in one of her pupils. W​henever he plays with his‌ friends, she notices tha‍t her fingers and lips tu‌rn blue. The child then s‍quats for sometime after ​which he feels better. Wh‌ich of the following does​ not occur during squatti​ng?","raw_text":"A primary school teacher notices a peculiar behavior in one of her pupils. Whenever he plays with his friends, she notices that her fingers and lips turn blue. The child then squats for sometime after which he feels better. Which of the following does not occur during squatting?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It increases the systemic ​vascular resistance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It increases the magnitude​ of right-to-left shunt","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Leads to trapping of deoxy​genated blood in lower li​mbs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both A​ and C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It increases the magnitude of right-to-left shunt","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It increases the magnitude of right-to-left shunt</strong></p><p>Squatting <strong>decreases the magnitude of the right-to-left shunt </strong>in a child with <strong>tetralogy of Fallot</strong> (TOF) .</p>\n<p>The child feels comfortable while squatting due to two compensatory mechanisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increasing systemic vascular resistance and, thus, <strong>decreasing the magnitude of the right-to-left shunt</strong> across the ventricular septal defect (VSD). Therefore, more blood goes to the lungs for oxygenation.</li>\n<li><strong>Trapping deoxygenated venous blood</strong> in the lower limbs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0808","text":"While waiting for correcti​ve open-heart surgery, yo​u decide to manage a case​ of tet spells medically.‌ Which of the following d‍rugs would worsen the cya‌nosis?","raw_text":"While waiting for corrective open-heart surgery, you decide to manage a case of tet spells medically. Which of the following drugs would worsen the cyanosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium bi​carbonate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Propr​anolol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phenyl​ephrine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isopre​naline","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Isoprenaline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Isoprenaline</strong></p><p><strong>Isoprenaline/isoproterenol</strong> should be avoided in tet or hypercyanotic spells. It will cause a decrease in total peripheral resistance (TPR) or systemic vascular resistance (SVR). So, more blood leaves the left ventricle leading to the increased right to left shunt. This results in the worsening of right ventricular outflow and causes worsening of cyanosis and the symptoms.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment of hypercyanotic spell</strong> involves placing child in <strong>knee–chest position</strong> (or squatting), and use of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>oxygen</strong></li>\n<li><strong>morphine </strong>- sedates the patient</li>\n<li><strong>sodium bicarbonate - </strong>to counteract metabolic acidosis that develops when arterial Po<sub>2</sub> &lt;40 mmHg </li>\n<li><strong>alpha-agonists (phenylephrine)</strong>- vasopressor used in severe cases</li>\n<li><strong>beta-blockers (propranolol)- </strong>improves resting saturation and decreases the frequency of spells.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Drugs that increase SVR, such as <strong>IV phenylephrine (alpha-1 agonist) or propranolol (non-selective beta-antagonist/beta-2 block), can improve right ventricular outflow, decrease the right-to-left shunt</strong>, and improve the symptoms. This is same principle as squatting.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3411","text":"Blalock-Taussig shunt is d​one between_________.","raw_text":"Blalock-Taussig shunt is done between_________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subclavian artery to pulmo​nary artery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aorta to pu​lmonary vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aorta to pul​monary artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subclavian vein to pulmona​ry artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Subclavian artery to pulmonary artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Subclavian artery to pulmonary artery</strong></p><p>Blalock–Taussig shunt is done between subclavian artery to pulmonary artery</p>\n<table style=\"width: 251.181px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 122px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Name of the surgery</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.181px; height: 18px;\"><strong> Connects</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 122px; height: 18px;\"><strong>B</strong>lalock–Taussig shunt (<strong>BS</strong>)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.181px; height: 18px;\"><strong>S</strong>ubclavian artery to pulmonary artery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 122px; height: 18px;\"><strong>W</strong>aterston's shunt (<strong>WA</strong>)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.181px; height: 18px;\"><strong>A</strong>scending aorta to right pulmonary artery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 28.8px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 122px; height: 28.8px;\"><strong>P</strong>ott's shunt (<strong>PD</strong>)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.181px; height: 28.8px;\"><strong>D</strong>escending aorta to  pulmonary artery</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Blalock–Taussig shunt</strong> is currently the <strong>most common</strong> aortopulmonary shunt procedure and consists of a Gore-Tex conduit.</p>\n<p>Sometimes, the shunt is brought directly from the <strong>ascending aorta to the main pulmonary artery</strong>; in this case, it is called the<strong> central shunt.</strong></p>\n<p><br/><strong>Corrective open-heart surgery</strong> performed in critically ill newborns and early infancy is the preferred surgical treatment now.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3412","text":"Which of the following is ​a component of the pental​ogy of Fallot?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a component of the pentalogy of Fallot?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial sep​tal defect","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Patent ductu​s arteriosus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coarctation ​of the aorta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left ventricular hypertrop​hy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Atrial septal defect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Atrial septal defect</strong></p><p>Pentalogy of Fallot consists of </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tetralogy of Fallot: </strong>Ventricular septal defect + Pulmonic stenosis + Overriding of the aorta + Right ventricular hypertrophy</li>\n<li data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"4\" data-listid=\"123\"><strong>Atrial septal defect</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3798","text":"An adolescent who is a kno​wn case of VSD is brought​ by his parents with comp​laints of breathlessness ‌on exertion which is grad‍ually progressive, bluish‌ discoloration, and swell‍ing of toes and fingers. ​Investigations showed fea‌tures suggestive of pulmo​nary hypertension. Which ​of the following is not a‌ part of the condition?","raw_text":"An adolescent who is a known case of VSD is brought by his parents with complaints of breathlessness on exertion which is gradually progressive, bluish discoloration, and swelling of toes and fingers. Investigations showed features suggestive of pulmonary hypertension. Which of the following is not a part of the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right ventricular hypertro​phy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bidirectional flow of bloo​d","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obstructive pulmonary hype​rtension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Predominant left to right ​shunt","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Predominant left to right shunt","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8007275263bb4c9cb5ffe163f8426afax1280x1700.6488011283498.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Predominant left to right shunt</strong></p><p>Given clinical scenario along with the development of pulmonary hypertension is seen in Eisenmenger’s syndrome and predominant <strong>right to left shunt</strong> (not left to right shunt) is seen here.</p>\n<p>Gradual onset of cyanosis, dyspnoea, fatigue, and in later stages, heart failure, chest pain, headaches, syncope, and hemoptysis in a setting of congenital acyanotic heart disease, are signs and symptoms of Eisenmenger’s syndrome.</p>\n<p>Reversal of the shunt at the level of atria/ventricles/patent ductus arteriosus due to <strong>irreversible pulmonary vascular obstructive changes is defined as Eisenmenger’s syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Features of pulmonary hypertension in Eisenmenger physiology: Right ventricular heave, narrow split S 2 with a loud pulmonic component.</p>\n<p>Chest X-ray showing prominent pulmonary artery and right ventricle, peripheral pruning of pulmonary vessels.</p>\n<p>ECG shows features of right ventricular hypertrophy, with spiked P waves.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong>: Calcium channel blockers, prostacyclin, and it's analog treprostinil and lung transplantation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0797","text":"The most common type of to​tal anomalous pulmonary v​enous connection is _____​____.","raw_text":"The most common type of total anomalous pulmonary venous connection is _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Suprac​ardiac","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Car​diac","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infrac​ardiac","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mi​xed","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Supracardiac","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Supracardiac</strong></p><p>The most common type of total anomalous pulmonary venous connection (TAPVC) is <strong>supracardiac</strong>.</p>\n<p>In TAPVC, <strong>pulmonary veins instead of draining into the left atrium drain into the right atrium directly or indirectly</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Supracardiac</strong> (most common): Common pulmonary vein drains into superior venacava or left innominate vein.</li>\n<li>Cardiac: Pulmonary veins drain into right atrium or coronary sinus.</li>\n<li><strong>Infracardiac (always obstructive and most dangerous)</strong>: Pulmonary veins drain into the hepatic vein or portal vein or inferior vena cava.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3415","text":"A neonate was noted to hav​e cyanosis of the oral mu​cosa. Chest x-ray reveale​d a snowman appearance of‌ the cardiac silhouette. ‍What is the most likely d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A neonate was noted to have cyanosis of the oral mucosa. Chest x-ray revealed a snowman appearance of the cardiac silhouette. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transposition of great art​eries","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Truncus a​rteriosus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tricuspi​d atresia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Total anomalous pulmonary ​venous connection","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Total anomalous pulmonary venous connection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/89ccd63bfeaf4392ba05c8417629daa3x510x494.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Total anomalous pulmonary venous connection</strong></p><p><strong>Snowman's appearance</strong> on the chest X-ray image is classically seen in <strong>total anomalous pulmonary venous connection (TAPVC).</strong></p>\n<p>The supracardiac variant can classically depict a <strong>snowman appearance/figure of 8/cottage loaf heart</strong> on the frontal chest radiograph.</p>\n<p>The dilated vertical vein on the left, brachiocephalic vein on the top, and the superior vena cava on the right form the head of the snowman; the body of the snowman is formed by the enlarged right atrium.</p>\n<p>The image given above shows a snowman or figure-8 configuration. Cardiomegaly and increased pulmonary vascularity are evident.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1004","text":"You are examining a week-o​ld male infant who was se​verely cyanotic at birth ​in the post-operative war‌d. He has undergone ballo‍on atrial septostomy foll‌owing prostaglandin E1 ad‍ministration. Post-surger​y there was a marked impr‌ovement in cyanosis. The ​most likely diagnosis is_​_____.","raw_text":"You are examining a week-old male infant who was severely cyanotic at birth in the post-operative ward. He has undergone balloon atrial septostomy following prostaglandin E1 administration. Post-surgery there was a marked improvement in cyanosis. The most likely diagnosis is______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tetralogy​ of Fallot","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transposition of great art​eries","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Truncus a​rteriosus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tricuspi​d atresia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Transposition of great arteries","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ad70f163988647bba542b9dd14d97175x1280x2433.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Transposition of great arteries</strong></p><p>Cyanosis at birth, prostagladin E1 administration, and septostomy to improve oxygenation are typical features of <strong>transposition of great vessels</strong> (TGA).</p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW247863283\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW247863283\"><strong>TGA</strong> is characterized by the <strong>aorta arising from the right ventricle</strong> and <strong>pulmonary artery from the left ventricle</strong>. </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW247863283\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW247863283\">Since systemic and pulmonary circulations are separate, survival depends on the mixing of oxygenated pulmonary blood and deoxygenated systemic blood which can occur:</span></span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"TextRun SCXW247863283\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW247863283\">At atrial level: Through atrial septal defect (ASD)</span></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"TextRun SCXW247863283\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW247863283\">At ventricle level: Through ventricular septal defect (VSD)</span></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"TextRun SCXW247863283\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW247863283\">At great vessel level: Through patent ductus arteriosus</span></span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Chest X-ray</strong> image shows cardiomegaly, a <strong>narrow mediastinum </strong>(the classic “<strong>egg-on-string appearance”</strong>), and normal to increased pulmonary blood flow. <span class=\"TextRun SCXW66095698\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW66095698\">The narrow pedicle of the heart is because of the malposition of the great vessels.</span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW66095698\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW66095698\">Treatment: Here, <strong>prostaglandin E1</strong> administration and <strong>septostomy</strong> help initial stabilization by creating a communication between the right and left atria, thereby facilitating the mixing of systemic and pulmonary circulations.</span></span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0732","text":"A 24-year-old woman with b​ipolar disorder on lithiu​m therapy expresses inten​t to conceive. She must b‌e advised to stop the dru‍g due to the risk of deve‌lopment of which of the f‍ollowing conditions in th​e fetus?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman with bipolar disorder on lithium therapy expresses intent to conceive. She must be advised to stop the drug due to the risk of development of which of the following conditions in the fetus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ebstein​ anomaly","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"VACTERAL​ defects","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coarctatio​n of aorta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Supravalvular aortic steno​sis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ebstein anomaly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ebstein anomaly</strong></p><p>She must be asked to stop the intake of <strong>lithium </strong>as it can cause an <strong>Ebstein anomaly</strong> in the newborn.</p>\n<p>Ebstein anomaly is characterized by the downward displacement of the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, which results in a thin-walled, low-pressure, “atrialized” segment of the right ventricle due to the anomalous attachment of the tricuspid leaflets.</p>\n<p>Lithium can also cause macrocephaly as a teratogenic effect. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3812","text":"Akshay, a high schooler pr​esents to you with swelli​ng of fingers and toes. H​e said he gets palpitatio‌ns while he is exercising‍ and even when he hangs o‌ut with friends. An X-Ray‍ of the patient is given ​below. ECG showed the pre‌sence of delta waves. Wha​t is your diagnosis?","raw_text":"Akshay, a high schooler presents to you with swelling of fingers and toes. He said he gets palpitations while he is exercising and even when he hangs out with friends. An X-Ray of the patient is given below.  ECG showed the presence of delta waves. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Partial anomalous pulmonar​y venous return","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Total anomalous pulmonary ​venous return","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endocardial cushion defect​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ebstein​ anomaly","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ebstein anomaly","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0052f89a4cfa416a83b4612a6870df95.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/18d7b272582c441aa29fc1fec16d687cx510x640.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ebstein anomaly</strong></p><p>The diagnosis is <strong>Ebstein's anomaly.</strong></p>\n<p>The presence of clubbing and a <strong>box-shaped heart</strong> on chest X-Ray in the given case is suggestive of <strong>Ebstein’s anomaly</strong>. Clubbing develops secondary to cyanosis as ebstein's anomaly is a right to left shunt. Delta wave is observed in <strong>Wolff-Parkinson-White</strong> (WPW)<strong> syndrome. WPW </strong>and <strong>supraventricular tachycardia</strong> causing palpitations can be seen in association with Ebstein’s anomaly.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9234","text":"In a 13-year-old child wit​h Ebstein's anomaly, echo​cardiography would likely​ reveal:","raw_text":"In a 13-year-old child with Ebstein's anomaly, echocardiography would likely reveal:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitral reg​urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tricuspid r​egurgitation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mitral ​stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tricuspid​ stenosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tricuspid regurgitation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tricuspid regurgitation</strong></p><p><strong>Tricuspid regurgitation</strong> would be seen in a child with an <strong>Ebstein anomaly.</strong></p>\n<p>Mild cases develop cyanosis during adolescence or early adulthood. In severe cases, cyanosis manifests at birth. </p>\n<p>Ebstein anomaly is characterized by the downward displacement of the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, which results in a thin-walled, low-pressure, “atrialized” segment of the right ventricle due to the anomalous attachment of the tricuspid leaflets.</p>\n<p>In Ebstein's, tricuspid valve tissue is dysplastic and results in <strong>tricuspid regurgitation</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>right ventricle</strong> is <strong>hypoplastic</strong>, which leads to decreased pulmonary outflow and there will be <strong>functional pulmonary atresia. </strong>As a result, <strong>blood</strong> shunts through the <strong>foramen ovale</strong>/atrial septal defect (ASD) results in <strong>cyanosis</strong> at birth.</p>\n<p>Himalayan P wave (amplitude &gt; 5 mm) is associated with tricuspid atresia, Ebstein anomaly, and combined tricuspid and pulmonary stenosis, where they indicate a dilated right atrium. <strong>Wolff–Parkinson–White syndrome (WPW)</strong> and <strong>supraventricular tachycardia</strong> may be present in Ebstein anomaly.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<p>In Ebstein anomaly, the malformed tricuspid valve is usually regurgitant, but may rarely be stenotic.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3400","text":"A cyanotic newborn is susp​ected of having congenita​l heart disease. He has a​n increased left ventricu‌lar impulse and a holosys‍tolic murmur along the le‌ft sternal border. ECG sh‍ows left axis deviation a​nd left ventricular hyper‌trophy. Which of the foll​owing is the most likely ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"A cyanotic newborn is suspected of having congenital heart disease. He has an increased left ventricular impulse and a holosystolic murmur along the left sternal border. ECG shows left axis deviation and left ventricular hypertrophy. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transposition of Great Art​eries","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Truncus a​rteriosus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tricuspi​d atresia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tetrology​ of Fallot","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tricuspid atresia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tricuspid atresia</strong></p><p><strong>Clinical cyanosis</strong> in combination with <strong>left-axis deviation</strong> (LAD) and<strong> left ventricular hypertrophy</strong> (LVH) noted on the electrocardiogram distinguishes <strong>tricuspid atresia</strong> from most other cyanotic heart lesions.</p>\n<p>Other points in favor:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Increased left ventricular impulse </strong>(in contrast to most other causes of cyanotic heart disease, in which an increased right ventricular impulse).</li>\n<li><strong>Holosystolic murmurs</strong> audible along the left sternal border (due to associated ventricular septal defect).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Tricuspid atresia is the <strong>congenital absence of the tricuspid valve</strong>. So, blood cannot flow directly from the right atrium to the right ventricle, and the following associated defects are necessary for survival:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Patent foramen ovale or atrial septal defect: for the flow of blood from the right atrium to the left atrium</li>\n<li>Ventricular septal defect: for the flow of blood from the left ventricle to the right ventricle</li>\n<li>There is a <strong>hypoplastic right ventricle.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B and D: Truncus arteriosus and tetralogy of Fallot and - The ECG demonstrates right axis deviation (RAD) and evidence of RVH.</p>\n<p>Option A: Transposition of great arteries- ECG is usually normal, showing the neonatal right-sided dominant pattern.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3401","text":"You are examining a case o​f cyanosis in NICU as a r​esident. Which of the fol​lowing cardiac features w‌ould make the diagnosis o‍f tricuspid atresia unlik‌ely?","raw_text":"You are examining a case of cyanosis in NICU as a resident. Which of the following cardiac features would make the diagnosis of tricuspid atresia unlikely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left axis deviation on ECG​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Himalayan P ​waves on ECG","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricular septal defect ​on echo","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left ventricular hypoplasi​a on echo","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Left ventricular hypoplasia on echo","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/95009bf37ec74df482e7efe380132244x600x1265.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Left ventricular hypoplasia on echo</strong></p><p>Left ventricular hypoplasia would make the diagnosis unlikely as <strong>right ventricular hypoplasia</strong> is seen in <strong>tricuspid atresia.</strong></p>\n<p>Other features of tricuspid atresia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An <strong>increased left ventricular impulse</strong> is noted in contrast to an increased right ventricular impulse seen in most other cyanotic diseases.</li>\n<li>A VSD <strong>holosystolic murmur</strong> is audible along the left sternal border.</li>\n<li>The second heart sound (<strong>S2</strong>) is usually <strong>single</strong>.</li>\n<li>Left ventricular hypertrophy and <strong>left axis deviation</strong> on ECG.</li>\n<li>Tall, peaked P waves on lead II known as <strong>Himalayan P waves</strong> which are suggestive of an enlarged right atrium. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0265","text":"Which of the following cas​es could develop recurren​t infections due to pulmo​nary congestion? (1) A 12‌-year-old ventricular sep‍tal defect (2) Tetrology ‌of Fallot in a 3-year-old‍ child (3) A toddler with​ tricuspid atresia. (4) A‌ 17-year-old with an atri​al septal defect on echoc​ardiogram.","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following cases could develop recurrent infections due to pulmonary congestion?</p>\n<p>(1) A 12-year-old ventricular septal defect</p>\n<p>(2) Tetrology of Fallot in a 3-year-old child </p>\n<p>(3) A toddler with tricuspid atresia.</p>\n<p>(4) A 17-year-old with an atrial septal defect on echocardiogram.</p>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 a​nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3 ​and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 a​nd 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2,​ and 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 and 4</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>atrial</strong> and <strong>ventricular septal defects</strong> have <strong>increased</strong> pulmonary blood flow. This results in recurrent pulmonary infection due to congestion in the lungs.</p>\n<p><strong>Tetralogy of Fallot</strong> (TOF) and <strong>tricuspid atresia</strong> (TA) are associated with <strong>reduced</strong> pulmonary blood flow (oligemic lung fields). They do not cause pulmonary congestion and do not predispose to infections.</p>\n<p>Reduced pulmonary blood flow is due to</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Right ventricular outflow obstruction in tetralogy of Fallot</li>\n<li>Right ventricular inflow obstruction in tricuspid atresia</li>\n</ul>\n<p><br/>Cyanotic congenital heart diseases (CCHD) with decreased and increased pulmonary blood flow:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 296.024px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 449px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Decreased pulmonary blood flow</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 42.0243px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Increased pulmonary blood flow</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 163.6px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 449px; height: 163.6px;\">\n<p>Tetrology of Fallot</p>\n<p>Pulmonary atresia</p>\n<p>Ebstein's anomaly</p>\n<p>Tricuspid atresia with an intact ventricular septum</p>\n<p>Transposition of the great vessels with VSD and pulmonary stenosis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 42.0243px; height: 163.6px;\">\n<p>Transposition of the great vessels with VSD or PDA</p>\n<p>Total anomalous pulmonary venous return (TAPVC)</p>\n<p>Truncus arteriosus</p>\n<p>Hypoplastic left heart syndrome (HLHS)</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5442","text":"A cyanotic baby at the 8th​ hour of life is being ev​aluated in NICU. Pulse ox​imetry shows 80 % not imp‌roving with 100 % O2. Rec‍ords show that continuous‌ machinery murmur was hea‍rd at birth but not anymo​re. Which of the followin‌g can be done?","raw_text":"A cyanotic baby at the 8th hour of life is being evaluated in NICU. Pulse oximetry shows 80 % not improving with 100 % O2. Records show that continuous machinery murmur was heard at birth but not anymore. Which of the following can be done?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intubation ​with 100% O2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Indomethacin administratio​n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"RBC tra​nsfusion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prostaglandi​n E1 infusion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prostaglandin E1 infusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4605e043b3904f4786c29151f025447dx1280x2559.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prostaglandin E1 infusion</strong></p><p>This baby has a cyanotic congenital heart disease that is dependent on a<strong> patent ductus arteriosus</strong>.<strong> Prostaglandin E1</strong> infusion prevents the <strong>closure of PDA</strong> in term neonates with <strong>duct-dependent congenital heart disease (CHD)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cyanosis failing to improve with 100% oxygen suggests an underlying cyanotic CHD.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Functional closure of the PDA occurs soon after birth, usually within first week of life.</p>\n<p>The open PDA acted as a conduit between systemic and pulmonary circulations, thereby preventing the onset of cyanosis.</p>\n<p>Upon its closure, cyanosis manifested in the baby as the blood could not be pumped into the lungs for oxygenation. Deoxygenated blood is being shunted into the systemic circulation after the closure of PDA. Hence, you should maintain the patency of the duct with PGE1 infusion until surgery can be done.</p>\n<p><strong>Duct-dependent lesions:</strong> Anomalies, in a newborn with CHD, which are dependent on the patency of ductus arteriosus for survival.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Right-sided lesions</strong> (Tetralogy of Fallot, PA/PS) cannot pump blood to the lungs. These patients are dependent on an open PDA for <strong>pulmonary circulation.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Left-sided lesions</strong> (Hypoplastic left heart syndrome, Aortic stenosis, Coarctation of aorta) cannot pump blood into the systemic circulation. These patients are dependent on open PDA for <strong>systemic circulation.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0706","text":"The given chest X-ray is s​uggestive of:","raw_text":"The given chest X-ray is suggestive of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tetralogy​ of Fallot","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Atrial sep​tal defect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricular ​septal defect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ebstein'​s anomaly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tetralogy of Fallot","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/08162a01838f431fb6767a0b936e0345.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tetralogy of Fallot</strong></p><p>The given chest X-ray shows a <strong>boot-shaped</strong> cardiac silhouette which is suggestive of the<strong> Tetralogy of Fallot</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>boot-shaped</strong> (<strong>coeur en sabot</strong>) cardiac silhouette is seen due to the <strong>right ventricular hypertrophy</strong> and upward shift of the cardiac apex. The<strong> hilar areas</strong> and <strong>lung fields</strong> appear relatively <strong>clear</strong> due to reduced pulmonary blood flow or the small size of the pulmonary arteries, or both. </p>\n<p>In <strong>Ebsteins's</strong> anomaly (Option D) <strong>box-shaped heart</strong> is seen. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7340","text":"Which among the following ​options describes the che​st x-ray image?","raw_text":"Which among the following options describes the chest x-ray image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Egg on strin​g appearance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coeur e​n sabot","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Scimit​ar sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Snow m​an sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Coeur en sabot","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8671fe49306b40cbadf43ad8cd39c8a9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3246d13f08ef4516b790ccdf31c4adbdx510x558.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Coeur en sabot</strong></p><p>The given chest x-ray shows '<strong>coeur-en-sabot</strong>' or <strong>boot-shaped heart</strong>, which is seen in tetralogy of Fallot (TOF).</p>\n<p>The characteristic silhouette<strong> </strong>is due to<strong> cardiac apex elevation</strong> as a result of <strong>right ventricular hypertrophy. </strong>The hilar areas and lung fields are relatively clear because of <strong>diminished pulmonary blood flow</strong> or the <strong>small size of the pulmonary arteries</strong> and the <strong>aorta is usually enlarged. </strong></p>\n<p>The image below describes the likeness of the cardiac silhouette to a boot: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>TOF is cyanotic congenital heart disease with the following four components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Obstruction to right ventricular outflow (<strong>pulmonary stenosis</strong>)</li>\n<li>Malalignment type of ventricular septal defect (<strong>VSD</strong>)</li>\n<li>Dextroposition of the aorta so that it overrides the ventricular septum (<strong>overriding of the aorta</strong>)</li>\n<li>Right ventricular hypertrophy (<strong>RVH</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The severity of symptoms depends on the degree of the right ventricular outflow obstruction. In mild degrees, the symptoms become pronounced as the baby grows due to <strong>RV infundibular hypertrophy</strong> and may present with <strong>heart failure</strong>. In severe degrees, pulmonary blood flow predominantly depends on the ductus arteriosus. As soon as the duct closes, the baby presents with <strong>cyanosis</strong> and <strong>circulatory collapse</strong> in the neonatal period.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include significant <strong>hypercyanotic spells</strong> in infants which are characterised by hyperpnea, <strong>cyanosis</strong>, and <strong>gasping </strong>followed by syncope. The spells occur most frequently in the morning on initially awakening or after episodes of <strong>vigorous crying</strong>. These spells are due to a prolonged reduction of pulmonary blood flow resulting in <strong>severe systemic hypoxia</strong> and metabolic acidosis.</p>\n<p>Older children present with <strong>exertional dyspnea</strong> and assume a <strong>squatting position</strong> to relieve the dyspneic episode after which the child is able to resume the activity.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9463","text":"Of the following, all are ​findings of the condition​ in the below chest x-ray​ except:","raw_text":"Of the following, all are findings of the condition in the below chest x-ray except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased pulmonary blood ​flow","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Narrow vasc​ular pedicle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary venous hypertens​ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right atrial​ enlargement","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Right atrial enlargement","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4088803754f6485e86c124368544173e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Right atrial enlargement</strong></p><p>The given chest x-ray shows an <strong>egg-on-string</strong> appearance seen in the <strong>transposition of great arteries</strong> (TGA). <strong>Enlargement</strong> of the <strong>left atrium</strong> is seen in TGA, not the right atrium.</p>\n<p>The reason for left atrial enlargement is due to <strong>increased</strong> <strong>pulmonary blood flow</strong>.</p>\n<p>TGA is characterized by the aorta arising from the <strong>right</strong> ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the <strong>left</strong> ventricle. This abnormal configuration leads to a <strong>narrow vascular pedicle.</strong></p>\n<p>As pulmonary vascular resistance drops during the first few days of life, blood flow through the <strong>pulmonary vasculature</strong> is <strong>increased.</strong> There is also elevated pulmonary venous pressure seen in TGA, especially in infants with an intact interventricular septum.</p>\n<p>The <strong>globular</strong> (egg) appearance of the heart due to <strong>abnormal convexity</strong> of the right atrial border and left atrial enlargement, and the <strong>narrow</strong> superior mediastinum (string) result in the classical <strong>egg-on-string appearance.</strong></p>\n<p>TGA is the <strong>most specific</strong> heart disease in <strong>infants</strong> of <strong>diabetic mothers.</strong></p>\n<p>Early management includes an immediate infusion of <strong>prostaglandin E1</strong> which keeps the <strong>ductus arteriosus patent</strong> for improving oxygenation. <strong>Rashkind balloon atrial septostomy</strong> is a <strong>palliative</strong> catheter-based intervention that can be done to improve the hypoxic state <strong>before</strong> definitive surgery. The procedure creates an opening in the atrial septum which allows better inter-circulatory mixing of the blood. <strong>Jatene arterial switch procedure</strong> is the definitive surgery. </p>\n<p>Most infants with TGA do not survive till adulthood without corrective surgery.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3416","text":"Below is the chest X-ray i​mage obtained for a neona​te on day 7 of life who d​eveloped heart failure la‌ter​. What is the most pr‍obable diagnosis?","raw_text":"Below is the chest X-ray image obtained for a neonate on day 7 of life who developed heart failure later​. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tetralogy​ of Fallot","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular ​septal defect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Total anomalous pulmonary ​venous connection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transposition of great art​eries","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Transposition of great arteries","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7e9cd83f369a4636bce29a140df9340f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Transposition of great arteries</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW247863283\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW247863283\">The classic </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW247863283\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW247863283\">\"</span></span><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW247863283\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW247863283\">egg on end</span></span></strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW247863283\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW247863283\">\"</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW247863283\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW247863283\"> appearance on </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW247863283\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW247863283\">this chest X-ray image</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW247863283\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW247863283\"> is seen in the <strong>transposition of great arteries</strong> (</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW247863283\" lang=\"EN-IN\" xml:lang=\"EN-IN\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW247863283\">TGA). </span></span></p>\n<p>In the chest X-ray image, <strong>cardiomegaly with narrow mediastinum </strong>gives rise to the classic “<strong>egg-shaped heart.” </strong>The superior mediastinum appears narrow due to the anteroposterior relationship of the transposed great vessels and the radiologic absence of the thymus.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"CpxDvaHE","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"26_acute_lymphocytic_leukemia__all_","title":"26.Acute Lymphocytic Leukemia (All)","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MB3621","text":"Which of these is not a pr​e-leukemic condition?","raw_text":"Which of these is not a pre-leukemic condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemog​lobinuria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aplasti​c anemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myelodysplas​tic syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paroxysmal cold hemoglobin​uria","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria</strong></p><p><strong>Paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria</strong> is not a preleukemic condition. </p>\n<p>It is an autoimmune hemolytic anemia due to IgG autoantibodies that bind to the P blood group antigen and cause intravascular hemolysis and hemoglobinuria. Most cases are seen in children and show recovery within a month.</p>\n<p>Option A: Few patients with <strong>paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria</strong> (PNH) develop acute <strong>non-lymphocytic leukemia</strong>. This is thought to arise from the injured marrow as a secondary hematopoietic disorder.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Bone marrow aplasia</strong> preceding <strong>acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL)</strong> is a rare condition that usually affects children. It generally follows the recovery of normal blood counts and most commonly occurs within 6 months of the onset of aplasia.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Acute myeloid leukemia (AML)</strong> occurs in 10% to 40% of individuals with<strong> myelodysplastic syndromes</strong> (MDS). It is usually accompanied by the appearance of additional cytogenetic abnormalities.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3623","text":"Which of the following is ​true about the pathogenes​is of acute leukemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the pathogenesis of acute leukemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arrest in differentiation ​and increased apoptosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enhanced differentiation a​nd decreased apoptosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enhanced differentiation a​nd increased apoptosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arrest in differentiation ​and decreased apoptosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Arrest in differentiation and decreased apoptosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Arrest in differentiation and decreased apoptosis</strong></p><p>In acute leukemia, there is an <strong>arrest in differentiation</strong> and<strong> decreased apoptosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Genomic aberrations create oncoproteins. They <strong>arrest normal maturation</strong>, turn on pro-growth signaling pathways, or <strong>protect</strong> cells from<strong> apoptotic death</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3624","text":"As per WHO classification,​ what percentage of blast​ cells in bone marrow bio‌psy is required to diagno‌se a patient with acute l‌eukemia?","raw_text":"As per WHO classification, what percentage of blast cells in bone marrow biopsy is required to diagnose a patient with acute leukemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2​0","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2​5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3​0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 20","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 20</strong></p><p>As per<strong> WHO classification</strong>, <strong>20% of blast cells</strong> must be present in the bone marrow and / peripheral smear for the diagnosis of acute leukemia.</p>\n<p>In<strong> FAB</strong> criteria, the cut-off is more than <strong>30%</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3620","text":"Which of the following con​ditions does not increase​ the risk of acute lympho‌blastic leukemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions does not increase the risk of acute lymphoblastic leukemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bloom s​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Li-Fraumen​i syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fanconi​ anemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polycyth​emia vera","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Polycythemia vera","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Polycythemia vera</strong></p><p><strong>Polycythemia vera</strong> increases the risk of developing <strong>AML,</strong> not ALL.</p>\n<p><strong>Inherited diseases</strong> that are associated with a <strong>higher risk</strong> of developing <strong>leukemias</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bloom syndrome</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Fanconi anemia</strong></li>\n<li>Ataxia–telangiectasia</li>\n<li>Down syndrome</li>\n<li>Type-1 neurofibromatosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other germline risk factors for the development of ALL:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Li-Fraumeni syndrome</strong></li>\n<li>Familial ALL syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: AML has a higher incidence during the neonatal period than ALL among children with Fanconi anemia, Bloom syndrome, and Down syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3622","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common type of a​cute lymphoblastic leukem‌ia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common type of acute lymphoblastic leukemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pre B​ cell","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"B c​ell","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T c​ell","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pre T​ cell","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pre B cell","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pre B cell</strong></p><p><strong>Precursor B-cell or pre B cell</strong> (previously classified as L1) type is the <strong>most common</strong> subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia <strong>(ALL)</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3618","text":"Which of the following is ​not a primary T-cell mark​er?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a  primary T-cell marker?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C​D1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C​D5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C​D8","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD​10","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CD10","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CD10</strong></p><p><strong>CD10</strong> is not a primary marker of T cells. It is a marker of<strong> pre-B cells</strong> and <strong>germinal-center B cells.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note:  </strong>Although CD10/ Common ALL antigen (CALLA) can be found in B-ALL and T-ALL, it is primarily expressed by pre-B cells and germinal centre B cells. Lymphoblasts of both B and T cell lineages, express CD10 that is lost upon progression to a mature phenotype. It is variably expressed by a few mature B cell tumours. However, the expression of CD10 by mature T cells has not been well established.</p>\n<div class=\"c-message_actions__container c-message__actions\" role=\"group\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0512","text":"Block-like positivity for ​PAS on immunohistochemist​ry of a bone marrow sampl‌e is seen. Which of the f‌ollowing cells show this ‌finding?","raw_text":"Block-like positivity for PAS on immunohistochemistry of a bone marrow sample is seen. Which of the following cells show this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymph​oblast","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Myelo​blast","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mono​blast","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Erythr​oblast","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lymphoblast","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8c2eb7eaaf294000ad10cd896a713094x737x510.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lymphoblast</strong></p><p><strong>Lymphoblasts</strong> in acute lymphocytic leukemia (ALL) show <strong>block-like</strong> or <strong>dot-like </strong>PAS positivity. </p>\n<p><strong>Erythroblasts</strong> in acute myeloid leukemia-M6 (AML-M6) show <strong>diffuse</strong> periodic acid-Schiff <strong>(PAS) positivity.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows dot-like positivity for periodic acid-Schiff (PAS) in lymphoblasts.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3634","text":"Which of the following cel​ls does not stain positiv​ely with Sudan black?","raw_text":"Which of the following cells does not stain positively with Sudan black?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymph​ocytes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Myelo​blasts","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eosin​ophils","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Promye​locytes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lymphocytes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lymphocytes</strong></p><p>Lymphocytes do not stain positively with Sudan black dye. Although some positivity may be seen in azurophilic granules of lymphoblasts.</p>\n<p><strong>Sudan Black B</strong> (SBB) is a<strong> lipophilic dye</strong> that binds irreversibly to an undefined<strong> granule</strong> component in granulocytes, eosinophils, and some monocytes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3627","text":"What is the most commonly ​affected gene in patients​ with T-cell acute lympho‌blastic leukemia?","raw_text":"What is the most commonly affected gene in patients with T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NOT​CH1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"PA​X5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ET​V6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"RU​NX1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. NOTCH1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. NOTCH1</strong></p><p>The most commonly <strong>affected gene</strong> in <strong>T-cell</strong> acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL) is <strong>NOTCH1</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>B-cell</strong> acute lymphoblastic leukemia is associated with mutations of genes such as <em><strong>PAX5</strong>,<strong> E2A</strong></em>,<strong> and </strong><em><strong>EBF. </strong></em>It may be associated with balanced t(12;21) involving the genes <em>ETV6</em> and <em>RUNX1</em>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0513","text":"A patient with acute lymph​oblastic leukemia is foun​d to have t(9;22). What w‌ill be the size of BCR-AB‌L fusion protein produced‌?","raw_text":"A patient with acute lymphoblastic leukemia is found to have t(9;22). What will be the size of BCR-ABL fusion protein produced?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"210​ kDa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"190​ kDa","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"230​ kDa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"170​ kDa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 190 kDa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 190 kDa</strong></p><p>The size of <strong>BCR-ABL protein</strong> in <strong>t(9;22)</strong> associated with acute lymphoblastic leukemia <strong>(ALL)</strong> is <strong>190 kDa</strong>.</p>\n<p>BCR-ABL fusion transcript protein with t(9;22):  </p>\n<ul style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"1\" data-font=\"Symbol\" data-leveltext=\"\" data-listid=\"13\">Major BCR (M-BCR) <strong>210 kDa </strong>size in <strong>chronic myeloid leukemia</strong> (CML) with weaker tyrosine kinase activity </li>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"2\" data-font=\"Symbol\" data-leveltext=\"\" data-listid=\"13\">Minor BCR (m-BCR)<strong> 190 kDa</strong> size in<strong> acute lymphoblastic leukemia</strong> ( ALL) with stronger tyrosine kinase activity </li>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"3\" data-font=\"Symbol\" data-leveltext=\"\" data-listid=\"13\">Micro BCR (μ-BCR) <strong>230 kDa </strong>size in <strong>myeloproliferative neoplasm</strong> (MPN) with exuberant neutrophilia </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3626","text":"On immunophenotyping of a ​patient with early pre-B ​acute leukemia, which of ‌the following marker will‌ be negative?","raw_text":"On immunophenotyping of a patient with early pre-B acute leukemia, which of the following marker will be negative?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T​dT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"D​R","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD​19","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cytoplasmic immunoglobulin​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cytoplasmic immunoglobulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cytoplasmic immunoglobulin</strong></p><p><strong>Cytoplasmic immunoglobulins</strong> and <strong>CD10</strong> are<strong> negative</strong> in <strong>early pre-B</strong> acute leukemia but become positive in pre-B ALL.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7085","text":"Which of the following is ​not usually involved in t​he immunohistochemistry o‌f acute lymphoblastic leu‌kemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not usually involved in the immunohistochemistry of acute lymphoblastic leukemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T​dt","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CD​1a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C​D3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cytok​eratin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cytokeratin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cytokeratin</strong></p><p><strong>Cytokeratin</strong> is not a marker of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL). It is an epithelial cell marker for <strong>thymoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tdt, CD1a, and CD3 are markers of ALL.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9450","text":"A 10-year-old boy with fev​er, weight loss came to t​he hospital. On examinati‌on, he was pale and had s‌ignificant lymphadenopath‌y. The bone marrow histol‍ogy of the person is as g‍iven below. Your diagnosi​s is?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old boy with fever, weight loss came to the hospital. On examination, he was pale and had significant lymphadenopathy. The bone marrow histology of the person is as given below. Your diagnosis is?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute monocy​tic leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute myelomonocytic leuke​mia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute lymphoblastic leukem​ia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Juvenile myelomonocytic le​ukemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acute lymphoblastic leukemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/48db83732e6c412a99f4ee8d48d6e38f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acute lymphoblastic leukemia</strong></p><p>The given scenario with bone marrow histology showing <strong>lymphoblasts</strong> (condensed nuclear chromatin, small nucleoli, scanty agranular cytoplasm) is suggestive of <strong>acute lymphoblastic leukemia</strong>. </p>\n<p>ALL is more common in childhood. Clinical features of ALL include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bone marrow failure - a<span class=\"TextRun SCXW161209537\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"SpellingError SCXW161209537\">nemia</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW161209537\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW161209537\">, fever, infections, </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW161209537\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW161209537\">bleeding</span></span></li>\n<li>Mass effects due to neoplastic infiltration - bone pain, generalized lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, hepatomegaly, and testicular enlargement</li>\n<li><span class=\"TextRun SCXW96263860\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW96263860\">CNS </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW96263860\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW96263860\">manifestations - headache, vomiting, </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW96263860\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW96263860\">and nerve pa</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW96263860\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW96263860\">lsi</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW96263860\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW96263860\">es</span></span><span class=\"EOP SCXW96263860\" data-ccp-props='{\"201341983\":0,\"335559739\":160,\"335559740\":259}'> </span></li>\n<li>B-ALL - Pancytopenia due to marrow involvement</li>\n<li>T-ALL - Mediastinal mass, compression of  large vessels and airways in the mediastinum, lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3625","text":"An adolescent male patient​ presents with fatigue. H​is total leucocyte count ‌is 138 x 10^9 / L with 80‌% blasts on the periphera‌l smear. Chest X-ray show‍s a large mediastinal mas‍s. The bone marrow aspira​te is as shown below. Wha​t would the immunophenoty‌ping of this patient’s bl‍asts most likely demonstr‌ate?","raw_text":"An adolescent male patient presents with fatigue. His total leucocyte count is 138 x 10^9 / L with 80% blasts on the peripheral smear. Chest X-ray shows a large mediastinal mass. The bone marrow aspirate is as shown below. What would the immunophenotyping of this patient’s blasts most likely demonstrate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pre B-cell​ phenotype","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immature T-c​ell phenotype","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Myeloid ​phenotype","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mature B-ce​ll phenotype","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Immature T-cell phenotype","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b2ed684ca2b54f1dbc2b23876b7e6bf3.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Immature T-cell phenotype</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and bone marrow aspirate are suggestive of  <strong>T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia</strong>. Thus, immunophenotyping of the blasts would demonstrate an <strong>immature T-cell phenotype (Tdt/CD34/CD7 positive)</strong>.</p>\n<p>The bone marrow aspirate shows <strong>lymphoblast</strong> with condensed nuclear chromatin, small inconspicuous nucleoli, and scanty cytoplasm lacking granules. </p>\n<p>ALL is usually seen in adolescent boys with thymic involvement in 50% to 70%. It has an abrupt stormy onset, within days to a few weeks of the first symptoms.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of ALL include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bone marrow failure - a<span class=\"TextRun SCXW161209537\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"SpellingError SCXW161209537\">nemia</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW161209537\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW161209537\">, fever, infections, </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW161209537\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW161209537\">bleeding</span></span></li>\n<li>Mass effects due to neoplastic infiltration - bone pain, generalized lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, hepatomegaly, and testicular enlargement</li>\n<li><span class=\"TextRun SCXW96263860\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW96263860\">CNS </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW96263860\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW96263860\">manifestations - headache, vomiting, </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW96263860\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW96263860\">and nerve pa</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW96263860\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW96263860\">lsi</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW96263860\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW96263860\">es</span></span><span class=\"EOP SCXW96263860\" data-ccp-props='{\"201341983\":0,\"335559739\":160,\"335559740\":259}'> </span></li>\n<li>B-ALL - Pancytopenia due to marrow involvement</li>\n<li>T-ALL - Mediastinal mass, compression of  large vessels and airways in the mediastinum, lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3664","text":"In a patient with acute le​ukemia, the immunophenoty​pe pattern of the blasts ‌is positive for CD10, CD1‌3, CD19, and CD33. What i‌s the most probable diagn‍osis?","raw_text":"In a patient with acute leukemia, the immunophenotype pattern of the blasts is positive for CD10, CD13, CD19, and CD33. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute lymphoblastic leukem​ia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Biphenotyp​ic leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M​1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M​3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute lymphoblastic leukemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute lymphoblastic leukemia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario points to the diagnosis of <strong>acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL)</strong> based on immunophenotyping. </p>\n<p><strong>CD10 and CD19</strong> are markers for <strong>B-cell lineage </strong>and this patient most probably have B-ALL<strong>. </strong>Although CD13 and CD33 are myeloid antigens, their expression does not exclude the diagnosis of B- ALL. </p>\n<p>Option B: The diagnosis of <strong>mixed phenotypic acute leukemia (MPAL)</strong>/ acute leukemia of ambiguous lineage (ALAL) (previously known as<strong> biphenotypic leukemia</strong>) requires a demonstration of <strong>multiple lineages</strong> in blast cells. According to WHO's latest update (2022), the requirements for assigning more than one lineage to blast cells are :</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; border-style: solid;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 14.2598%;\">\n<p><strong> B cell lineage</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.7402%;\">\n<ul>\n<li>CD19 strong: \n<p>≥1 expressed CD10, CD22, CD79a</p>\n</li>\n<li>CD 19 weak: \n<p>≥ 2 or more strongly expressed CD10, CD22, CD79a</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 14.2598%;\"><strong>T cell lineage </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.7402%;\">CD3 positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 14.2598%;\"><strong>Myeloid lineage</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.7402%;\">\n<p>Myeloperoxidase (MPO) </p>\n<p>OR </p>\n<p>Monocyte differentiation (non-specific esterase, CD11c, CD14, CD64, lysozyme)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>In the <strong>absence of MPO</strong> or evidence of monocytic differentiation, expression of <strong>CD13 and CD33 is not sufficient </strong>for a diagnosis of <strong>MPAL</strong>. Hence, biphenotypic leukemia is not the answer here. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0514","text":"A patient with weight loss​ and fever is diagnosed w​ith acute lymphoblastic l‌eukemia. Which of the fol‌lowing is a good prognost‌ic indicator of this cond‍ition?","raw_text":"A patient with weight loss and fever is diagnosed with acute lymphoblastic leukemia. Which of the following is a good prognostic indicator of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MLL gene tr​anslocation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"t (1​2;21)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"t (9​;22)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypodi​ploidy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. t (12;21)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. t (12;21)</strong></p><p>The presence of<strong> t (12;21) </strong>indicates a<strong> good prognosis</strong> in acute lymphoblastic leukemia <strong>(ALL).</strong> It involves genes<em> ETV6</em> and<em> RUNX1</em>.  </p>\n<p>Conversely, a translocation involving the <em>MLL</em> gene, t(9;22), and hypodiploidy are associated with a bad prognosis. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3630","text":"Bone marrow biopsy of a pa​tient shows &gt; 20% immatur​e blasts with scanty cyto‌plasm, no granules, and i‌nconspicuous nucleoli. Th‌ey also stain positive wi‍th PAS. Which of the foll‍owing are good prognostic​ markers of this conditio​n? Trisomy 7 Hyperdiploid‌y t (8,14) Pseudodiploidy‍ Age of 5 years","raw_text":"<p>Bone marrow biopsy of a patient shows &gt; 20% immature blasts with scanty cytoplasm, no granules, and inconspicuous nucleoli. They also stain positive with PAS. Which of the following are good prognostic markers of this condition?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Trisomy 7</li>\n<li>Hyperdiploidy</li>\n<li>t (8,14)</li>\n<li>Pseudodiploidy</li>\n<li>Age of 5 years</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, 3,​ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3,​ and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 4,​ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2,​ and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, and 5</strong></p><p>The<strong> </strong>given bone marrow biopsy report is suggestive of <strong>acute lymphoblastic leukemia</strong> (ALL). The good prognostic markers of ALL include trisomy 7, hyperdiploidy, and age of 5 years.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"_DMzPlhp","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"26_synthetic_opioids","title":"26.Synthetic Opioids","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MB6833","text":"Which of the following opi‍oids has maximum plasma p​rotein binding capacity?","raw_text":"Which of the following opioids has maximum plasma protein binding capacity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Morp‍hine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sufen‍tanil","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fent‍anyl","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peth‍idine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sufentanil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sufentanil</strong></p><p><strong>Sufentanil </strong>has maximum plasma protein binding capacity. </p>\n<p>More than 90% of sufentanil is bound to <strong>alpha</strong> <strong>acid</strong> <strong>glycoprotein</strong> in plasma.</p>\n<p>Plasma protein binding capacity of opioids:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sufentanil: 90%</li>\n<li>Fentanyl: 80-85%</li>\n<li>Pethidine (meperidine): 65-75%</li>\n<li>Morphine: 36%</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6819","text":"Which among the following ‍is the most potent opioid​ analgesic?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the most potent opioid analgesic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fent‍anyl","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sufen‍tanil","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Remife‍ntanil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Morp‍hine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sufentanil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sufentanil</strong></p><p><strong>Sufentanil</strong> is the <strong>most</strong> <strong>potent</strong> opioid. It is 1000 times more potent than morphine.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, fentanyl is around 100 times more potent than morphine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6825","text":"Which of the following has‍ the most rapid analgesic​ action?","raw_text":"Which of the following has the most rapid analgesic action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fent‍anyl","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Remife‍ntanil","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sufen‍tanil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Meper‍idine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Remifentanil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Remifentanil</strong></p><p>The <strong>most rapid</strong> analgesic action is achieved by <strong>remifentanil</strong>.</p>\n<p>Analgesic action (peak) is achieved <strong>within 1 to 1.5 min</strong> following intravenous administration.</p>\n<p>Analgesic action-<strong> duration </strong>for peak action:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Remifentanil: 1 to 1.5 min</li>\n<li>Fentanyl: 5 min</li>\n<li>Sufentanil: 5 min</li>\n<li>Meperidine: 15 min</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>rapid onset</strong> of action and the <strong>rapid recovery</strong> (within 15 min) owing to metabolism by <strong>plasma esterases</strong> of remifentanil make it an ideal analgesic for short painful procedures.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0695","text":"A middle-aged man with liv‍er metastasis and altered​ LFT is posted for a surg​ery. What would be the op​ioid of choice in him?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man with liver metastasis and altered LFT is posted for a surgery. What would be the opioid of choice in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fent‍anyl","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alfen‍tanil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sufen‍tanil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Remife‍ntanil","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Remifentanil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Remifentanil</strong></p><p>Opioid of choice in patients with compromised hepatic function is <strong>remifentanil.</strong></p>\n<p>Remifentanil is hydrolyzed by <strong>plasma </strong>and <strong>tissue esterases</strong> and hence its metabolism is independent of hepatic function.</p>\n<p>Other opioids like fentanyl, alfentanil, and sufentanil are metabolized by the liver.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0696","text":"A 48-year-old woman with C‍KD is scheduled for a sur​gery. Her serum creatinin​e is 2.4mg/dL and eGFR is​ 13. Which of the followi​ng opioids can be safely ​used in her?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old woman with CKD is scheduled for a surgery. Her serum creatinine is 2.4mg/dL and eGFR is 13. Which of the following opioids can be safely used in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peth‍idine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Remife‍ntanil","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Alfen‍tanil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Morp‍hine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Remifentanil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Remifentanil</strong></p><p>The given lab values in a CKD patient are suggestive of renal failure and<strong> remifentanil</strong> can be safely used in renal failure patients.</p>\n<p>Remifentanil is hydrolyzed by <strong>plasma</strong> and <strong>tissue esterases</strong>. The pharmacokinetics and the pharmacodynamics of remifentanil are not altered by renal dysfunction.</p>\n<p>Option A: Pethidine is metabolized to norpethidine, accumulation of which can lead to CNS excitatory effects (<strong>seizures</strong>).</p>\n<p>Option C: High levels of alfentanil can lead to prolonged action.</p>\n<p>Option D: Morphine is metabolized to morphine-6-glucuronide which can accumulate leading to <strong>respiratory depression.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> Fentanyl clearance is not altered by renal failure but its plasma protein binding is decreased which can alter the concentration of the free drug. Hence, fentanyl is the second drug of choice after remifentanil.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0697","text":"A patient who underwent an‍ intra-abdominal surgery ​is about to receive intra​spinal opioid injection f​or analgesia. Which of th​e following drugs should ​not be used for this purp​ose?","raw_text":"A patient who underwent an intra-abdominal surgery is about to receive intraspinal opioid injection for analgesia. Which of the following drugs should not be used for this purpose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sufen‍tanyl","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alfen‍tanyl","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fent‍anyl","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Remife‍ntanil","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Remifentanil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Remifentanil</strong></p><p><strong>Remifentanil</strong> should not be used for intraspinal injection.</p>\n<p>Remifentanil is formulated in combination with<strong> glycine. </strong>Glycine acts as an <strong>inhibitory</strong> neurotransmitter in the spinal dorsal horn that causes a reversible <strong>motor weakness</strong> when it is injected intrathecally.</p>\n<p>For this reason, remifentanil is <strong>not approved </strong>for <strong>spinal </strong>or <strong>epidural </strong>use.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0698","text":"Which of the following is ‍the shortest acting opioi​d?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the shortest acting opioid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fent‍anyl","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Remife‍ntanil","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Alfen‍tanil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sufen‍tanil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Remifentanil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Remifentanil</strong></p><p>The shortest acting opioid is <strong>remifentanil</strong> with t<sub>1/2</sub> of 8-20 minutes.</p>\n<p>The rapid metabolism by <strong>tissue esterases</strong> and rapid clearance results in its ultra short-acting nature.</p>\n<p>Remifentanil has the <strong>fastest onset</strong> of action and <strong>recovery</strong>, making it the opioid of choice in <strong>daycare surgery.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0699","text":"Which among the following ‍is not included in neurol​eptic anesthesia?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not included in neuroleptic anesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitrou‍s oxide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prop‍ofol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Drope‍ridol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fent‍anyl","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Propofol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Propofol</strong></p><p><strong>Propofol </strong>is <strong>not</strong> included in neuroleptic anesthesia.</p>\n<p>Neuroleptic <strong>anesthesia </strong>includes<strong> fentanyl</strong>, <strong>droperidol</strong>, and<strong> nitrous oxide.</strong></p>\n<p>Neuroleptic <strong>analgesia </strong>includes<strong> fentanyl </strong>and<strong> droperidol.</strong></p>\n<p>Neuroleptic anesthesia is characterized by analgesia, the absence of clinically apparent motor activity, suppression of autonomic reflexes, maintenance of cardiovascular stability, and amnesia.</p>\n<p><strong>QT</strong> interval <strong>prolongation</strong> and <strong>torsades</strong> <strong>de pointes</strong> due to droperidol have made this technique <strong>obsolete</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0700","text":"You are planning to use a ‍transdermal patch for one​ of your patients who req​uire around-the-clock opi​oids for his cancer pain.​ Which of the following d​rugs will you prescribe?","raw_text":"You are planning to use a transdermal patch for one of your patients who require around-the-clock opioids for his cancer pain.  Which of the following drugs will you prescribe?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fent‍anyl","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Remife‍ntanyl","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sufen‍tanyl","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alfen‍tanyl","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fentanyl","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fentanyl</strong></p><p>Fentanyl is available as a <strong>transdermal patch </strong>and can be prescribed to this patient. It is mainly used for chronic pain.</p>\n<p>Various routes of administration of fentanyl are: intravenous, epidural, spinal, transdermal therapeutic system (transdermal patch and transdermal iontophoresis), oral (buccal tablets, films, lozenges).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0701","text":"Which of the following is ‍the drug of choice for re​scue analgesia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the drug of choice for rescue analgesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Morp‍hine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cod‍eine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oxyc‍odone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fent‍anyl","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fentanyl","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fentanyl</strong></p><p><strong>Fentanyl</strong> is the drug of choice for providing <strong>rescue</strong> <strong>analgesia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Rescue analgesia involves the use of medications to minimize post-operative pain. Mild pain can be controlled with oral analgesics but severe pain requires opioids like fentanyl.</p>\n<p>Fentanyl is preferred over morphine or oxycodone due to its rapid onset of action, lesser incidence of postoperative nausea, vomiting, and other adverse effects.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0702","text":"A 60-year-old man was post‍ed for bronchoscopy under​ procedural sedation. Aft​er the administration of ​anesthetic drugs, he deve​loped chest wall rigidity​ and ineffective spontane​ous ventilation. Attempts​ at manual ventilation we‌re unsuccessful due to th‌e non-compliant chest wal‌l. The opioid that is mos​t commonly associated wit‌h this condition is?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man was posted for bronchoscopy under procedural sedation. After the administration of anesthetic drugs, he developed chest wall rigidity and ineffective spontaneous ventilation. Attempts at manual ventilation were unsuccessful due to the non-compliant chest wall. The opioid that is most commonly associated with this condition is?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Morp‍hine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fent‍anyl","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Peth‍idine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tram‍adol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fentanyl","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fentanyl</strong></p><p>Chest wall rigidity and ineffective ventilation is a classic presentation of '<strong>wooden chest syndrome</strong>'. This opioid-induced muscle rigidity is most commonly associated with the use of lipophilic opioids such as <strong>fentanyl</strong> and its congeners (alfentanil, sufentanil, and remifentanil).</p>\n<p>Risk factors for wooden chest syndrome:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rapid administration of a large dose of fentanyl</li>\n<li>Concomitant use of nitrous oxide</li>\n<li>Old age</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The clinical presentation can vary from mild hoarseness to chest wall rigidity severe enough to prevent adequate bag-and-mask ventilation.</p>\n<p>Management of wooden chest syndrome:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Discontinuation of the opioid</li>\n<li>Replacement of the opioid with a non-lipophilic version (such as hydromorphone or morphine) for analgesia</li>\n<li>Consider <strong>naloxone</strong> for reversal (you do not want to reverse analgesia on an intubated patient unless absolutely necessary)</li>\n<li>Addition of a <strong>non-depolarizing neuromuscular blocker</strong> for paralysis</li>\n<li>Supportive ventilatory care</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6838","text":"Upon recovering from anest‍hesia, a patient started ​feeling cold accompanied ​by shivering. Which is th​e opioid indicated to tre​at this condition?","raw_text":"Upon recovering from anesthesia, a patient started feeling cold accompanied by shivering. Which is the opioid indicated to treat this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Morp‍hine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meper‍idine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Methylm‍orphine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Meth‍adone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Meperidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Meperidine</strong></p><p><strong>Meperidine</strong> is commonly indicated for <strong>post</strong>-<strong>anesthetic</strong> <strong>shivering</strong>.</p>\n<p>It prevents post-anesthetic shivering by <strong>lowering</strong> the <strong>temperature</strong> <strong>threshold</strong> at which shivering is triggered by the hypothalamus.</p>\n<p>A single dose of 12.5 to 35mg meperidine (pethidine) is effective in the treatment of postanesthetic shivering. </p>\n<p>Infusion-related chills and rigors associated with amphotericin B, aldesleukin, trastuzumab, and alemtuzumab are also managed with meperidine in combination with other agents.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6813","text":"A 50-year-old housewife wi‍th diabetic nephropathy a​nd receiving dialysis wee​kly comes to your clinic ​with an accidental burn o​n her left hand. On exami​nation, 2nd-degree burns ​are seen. Which of the fo​llowing opioids should no‌t be prescribed to her fo‌r analgesia?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old housewife with diabetic nephropathy and receiving dialysis weekly comes to your clinic with an accidental burn on her left hand. On examination, 2nd-degree burns are seen. Which of the following opioids should not be prescribed to her for analgesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peth‍idine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Meth‍adone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fent‍anyl","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alfen‍tanil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pethidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pethidine</strong></p><p><strong>Pethidine</strong> (meperidine) should not be prescribed to her as it is contraindicated in patients with <strong>renal</strong> <strong>failure</strong>.</p>\n<p>In renal failure, there is an <strong>accumulation</strong> of its metabolite <strong>norpethidine</strong> which causes <strong>excitatory syndrome</strong> consisting of hallucinations, tremors, muscle twitches, and convulsions. Therefore, pethidine is avoided in renal failure patients.</p>\n<p>Methadone, alfentanil, and fentanyl primarily undergo <strong>hepatic metabolism.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0703","text":"A woman was administered a‍n intravenous opioid for ​managing postoperative pa​in. Within 5 minutes, she​ started developing sever​e redness and pruritus al​ong the distribution of t​he vein into which the dr​ug was injected. Which of‌ the following drugs woul‌d be responsible for this‌?","raw_text":"A woman was administered an intravenous opioid for managing postoperative pain. Within 5 minutes, she started developing severe redness and pruritus along the distribution of the vein into which the drug was injected. Which of the following drugs would be responsible for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meper‍idine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Alfen‍tanil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Remife‍ntanil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fent‍anyl","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Meperidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Meperidine</strong></p><p>The clinical findings are suggestive of <strong>histamine release</strong>, which is <strong>common </strong>with the use of <strong>meperidine</strong> (pethidine) due to <strong>mast cell activation</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Codeine, morphine,</strong> and <strong>hydromorphone</strong> are also associated with histamine release.</p>\n<p>Fentanyl, alfentanil, and remifentanil <strong>do not</strong> lead to histamine release.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0704","text":"A 16-year-old boy received‍ an intravenous opioid fo​r analgesia. A few minute​s later, his heart rate d​ecreased from 72/min to 5​8/min. Which among the fo​llowing drugs is least li​kely to be associated wit​h this finding?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old boy received an intravenous opioid for analgesia. A few minutes later, his heart rate decreased from 72/min to 58/min. Which among the following drugs is least likely to be associated with this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fent‍anyl","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sufen‍tanyl","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alfen‍tanyl","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Meper‍idine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Meperidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Meperidine</strong></p><p>Meperidine (pethidine) is least likely to be associated with bradycardia.</p>\n<p>Intravenous <strong>meperidine</strong> <strong>produces tachycardia</strong> as it has a structure similar to <strong>atropine.</strong> Unlike other opioids, meperidine causes <strong>mydriasis</strong> due to its anticholinergic effect.</p>\n<p><strong>Pentazocine,</strong> which is<strong> </strong>an opioid agonist-antagonist, also produces tachycardia, hypertension, and mydriasis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0705","text":"Which of the following opi‍oids has local anesthetic​ property?","raw_text":"Which of the following opioids has local anesthetic property?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Morp‍hine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meth‍adone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peth‍idine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cod‍eine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pethidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pethidine</strong></p><p>The opioid with local anesthetic property is <strong>pethidine (meperidine),</strong> which is especially seen after <strong>intrathecal</strong> administration.</p>\n<p>Intrathecal pethidine can provide spinal anesthesia for endoscopic urological procedures and surgery of the lower limb, perineum, and lower abdomen including cesarean section.</p>\n<p>However, due to <strong>low potency</strong> and systemic <strong>opioid side effects</strong>, it is usually not used for this purpose.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0134","text":"Apart from mu-opioid recep‍tors, on which other rece​ptor does tramadol act?","raw_text":"Apart from mu-opioid receptors, on which other receptor does tramadol act?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anticho‍linergic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5HT1a in‍hibition","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serotonergic and norepinep‍hrine reuptake inhibition​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Antihis‍taminic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Serotonergic and norepinephrine reuptake inhibition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Serotonergic and norepinephrine reuptake inhibition</strong></p><p>Tramadol <strong>inhibits</strong> the <strong>reuptake</strong> of <strong>serotonin</strong> and <strong>norepinephrine</strong>, apart from its action on the mu-opioid receptor.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>synthetic</strong> codeine analog that is a<strong> mu-</strong>opioid receptor <strong>agonist</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tramadol is a<strong> centrally </strong>acting <strong>analgesic </strong>used in the treatment of <strong>mild </strong>to <strong>moderate </strong>pain<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0709","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is false regardin​g tramadol?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding tramadol?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Safe in e‍pileptics","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Less potent ‍than morphine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Less respiratory depressio‍n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Synthetic analogue of code‍ine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Safe in epileptics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Safe in epileptics</strong></p><p>Tramadol is <strong>contraindicated</strong> <strong>in epileptics</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tramadol is <strong>epileptogenic </strong>and can cause seizures in predisposed individuals and in those taking medications like monoamine oxidase inhibitors and neuroleptic drugs.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>synthetic analogue</strong> of <strong>codeine</strong> and a weak opioid agonist with 1/6000 affinity for µ receptors as that of morphine.</p>\n<p>Apart from opioid action, it also inhibits the <strong>reuptake of 5HT and NE.</strong> Due to non-opioid receptor-mediated actions, it produces <strong>less respiratory depression</strong> and less constipation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1476","text":"A 24-year-old woman requir‍es an intranasal opioid s​pray for her migraine. Wh​ich of the following woul​d you prescribe her?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman requires an intranasal opioid spray for her migraine. Which of the following would you prescribe her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Morp‍hine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bupren‍orphine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Butor‍phanol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Meth‍adone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Butorphanol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Butorphanol</strong></p><p><strong>Butorphanol</strong> is a synthetic opioid available as a <strong>nasal</strong> <strong>spray</strong> for use in <strong>migraine</strong> headaches and painful conditions.</p>\n<p>It is used parenterally as an analgesic for moderate to severe pain or as an adjunct to general anesthesia.</p>\n<p>Opioids available for <strong>intranasal </strong>administration are<strong> butorphanol</strong> and <strong>fentanyl.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6873","text":"A 40-year-old man, on trea‍tment with fluoxetine for​ depression, was started ​on an opioid for severe n​europathic pain. A few da​ys later he had an episod​e of syncope and was brou​ght to the casualty. ECG ​taken is given below, wit‌h QTc = 477 ms. Which of ‌the following drugs is mo‌st likely the cause?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man, on treatment with fluoxetine for depression, was started on an opioid for severe neuropathic pain. A few days later he had an episode of syncope and was brought to the casualty.  ECG taken is given below, with QTc = 477 ms. Which of the following drugs is most likely the cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Morp‍hine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meper‍idine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Meth‍adone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Propox‍yphene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Methadone","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/20ff55bf95d84c80806011b3296ed557.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Methadone</strong></p><p>The given ECG findings show <strong>QT</strong> <strong>prolongation</strong> which is associated primarily with the use of <strong>methadone</strong>.</p>\n<p>In the ECG <strong>QTc</strong>(corrected QT interval= QT/√RR) is <strong>477ms</strong>. Normal QTc is about 440ms. Values of<strong> more than</strong> <strong>440ms </strong>in <strong>males</strong> and <strong>460ms </strong>in <strong>females</strong> are considered to be <strong>prolonged</strong>. QT prolongation can present as <strong>syncope</strong> or cardiac arrest.</p>\n<p>Methadone has <strong>additive</strong> action with agents known to cause QT prolongation. Serious cardiac arrhythmias including <strong>torsades</strong> <strong>de</strong> <strong>pointes</strong> have been noted with methadone use.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1469","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is false regardin​g methadone?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding methadone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Long acting mu receptor ag‍onist","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Used for chronic pain reli‍ef","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rapidly absorbed from GIT ‍and appears in plasma wit​hin 30 min of oral admini​stration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Onset of analgesia is 30-6‍0 min after parenteral an​d 1-2 h after oral admini​stration","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Onset of analgesia is 30-60 min after parenteral and 1-2 h after oral administration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Onset of analgesia is 30-60 min after parenteral and 1-2 h after oral administration</strong></p><p>Methadone’s <strong>onset</strong> of analgesia is <strong>10-20 minutes</strong> <strong>after</strong> the <strong>parenteral</strong> <strong>route</strong>, and <strong>30-60 minutes</strong> <strong>after</strong> the <strong>oral</strong> <strong>route</strong> of administration.</p>\n<p>Option A: Methadone is a long-acting mu receptor agonist with a plasma half-life of 15-40 hours.</p>\n<p>Option B: It is an analgesic and used for chronic pain relief.</p>\n<p>Option C: Absorption from GIT is rapid and appears in plasma within 30 minutes and reaches peak concentrations at around 4 hours.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0706","text":"A young man is scheduled f‍or laparoscopic cholecyst​ectomy. His medical histo​ry is significant for dep​ression and he is on mono​amine oxidase inhibitors ​for the same. Which of th​e following opioids can b​e safely administered to ‌him?","raw_text":"A young man is scheduled for laparoscopic cholecystectomy. His medical history is significant for depression and he is on monoamine oxidase inhibitors for the same. Which of the following opioids can be safely administered to him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tram‍adol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meper‍idine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dextrome‍thorphan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Morp‍hine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Morphine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Morphine</strong></p><p><strong>Morphine</strong> can be safely administered as it does not inhibit the neuronal reuptake of serotonin and hence <strong>does not trigger serotonin syndrome</strong> in patients on MAO inhibitors.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1054\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Phenylpiperidine series of opioids such as<strong> meperidine, tramadol, dextromethorphan,</strong> and<strong> methadone inhibit</strong> the neuronal <strong>reuptake of serotonin</strong> leading to serotonergic overactivity. This can cause <strong>serotonin syndrome</strong> in patients receiving monoamine oxidase inhibitors.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9791","text":"A woman sustained multiple‍ fractures in a motorcycl​e accident. Her physician​ prescribed an oral opioi​d for analgesia which is ​a partial agonist at mu a​nd a full agonist at kapp​a receptors. Identify the​ drug given.","raw_text":"A woman sustained multiple fractures in a motorcycle accident. Her physician prescribed an oral opioid for analgesia which is a partial agonist at mu and a full agonist at kappa receptors. Identify the drug given.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penta‍zocine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bupren‍orphine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tram‍adol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fent‍anyl","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pentazocine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pentazocine</strong></p><p><strong>Pentazocine</strong> is a <strong>partial</strong> <strong>agonist</strong> at <strong>mu</strong> and a <strong>full</strong> <strong>agonist</strong> at <strong>kappa</strong> receptors.</p>\n<p><strong>Analgesia</strong> produced by pentazocine is predominantly mediated by the <strong>κ</strong> receptor.</p>\n<p>Pentazocine is a <strong>mixed agonist-antagonist</strong> opioid agent. It is a <strong>full agonist</strong> at the <strong>κ</strong> receptor and a partial agonist or weak antagonist at the µ receptor.</p>\n<p><strong>Buprenorphine</strong> is a partial agonist at mu receptors but an antagonist at kappa receptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6826","text":"A cancer patient is depend‍ent on morphine for pain ​management. His physician​ decides to switch him to​ buprenorphine. Due to wh​ich property he chooses t​his new drug?","raw_text":"A cancer patient is dependent on morphine for pain management. His physician decides to switch him to buprenorphine. Due to which property he chooses this new drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Partial agonism at mu rece‍ptor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Partial agonism at kappa r‍eceptor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Partial antagonism at mu r‍eceptor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Partial antagonism at kapp‍a receptor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Partial agonism at mu receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Partial agonism at mu receptor</strong></p><p>Buprenorphine is a <strong>partial agonist</strong> at the <strong>mu</strong> receptor with a lower intrinsic activity compared to morphine and hence does not produce withdrawal symptoms in patients with morphine dependence.</p>\n<p>Buprenorphine is a <strong>synthetic</strong> thebaine congener and also acts as an <strong>antagonist</strong> at <strong>kappa</strong> receptor. </p>\n<p>It is more potent than morphine at mu receptor but with <strong>lower intrinsic activity</strong> and ceiling effect. Due to lower intrinsic activity, it has <strong>no</strong> <strong>withdrawal</strong> symptoms and can be used <span class=\"fontstyle0\">in the treatment of <strong>morphine</strong> <strong>dependence</strong>.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0707","text":"An elderly man with bone m‍etastasis is receiving in​travenous opioids for pai​n management. Which of th​e following drugs can be ​safely administered to hi​m without a risk of devel​oping respiratory depress​ion?","raw_text":"An elderly man with bone metastasis is receiving intravenous opioids for pain management. Which of the following drugs can be safely administered to him without a risk of developing respiratory depression?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Morp‍hine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bupren‍orphine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fent‍anyl","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alfen‍tanyl","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Buprenorphine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6a7fd0f1f0f942db96ce078817067e02x1280x1195.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Buprenorphine</strong></p><p><strong>Buprenorphine </strong>can be safely administered to this patient because of its <strong>ceiling effect</strong> on respiratory depression.</p>\n<p><strong>C</strong><strong>eiling effect </strong>is defined as no further increase in the effect of the drug with a further increment in dose, once the ceiling level is reached.</p>\n<p>Opioid <strong>agonist-antagonist</strong> compounds produce respiratory depression (and analgesia) with a ceiling effect which means increasing the dose further does not produce any further respiratory depression and may actually result in increased ventilation (due to the predominance of antagonist actions).</p>\n<p>Other drugs that show the ceiling effect are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Butorphanol</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nalbuphine</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pentazocine.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Respiratory depression with morphine, fentanyl &amp; alfentanyl does <strong>not</strong> have a ceiling effect because they are pure opioid agonists. As the dose increases, respiratory depression also increases progressing to respiratory arrest.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0708","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Naltrexone is an antagonis‍t at µ receptor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meperidine is a pure agoni‍st at µ receptor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nalbuphine is an agonist a‍t k receptor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Butorphanol is an antagoni‍st at k receptor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Butorphanol is an antagonist at k receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Butorphanol is an antagonist at k receptor</strong></p><p><strong>Butorphanol</strong> is an <strong>agonist </strong>at <strong>κ </strong>receptor. It may be a <strong>partial agonist </strong>or <strong>antagonist </strong>at <strong>µ </strong>receptor<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Pentazocine, butorphanol, buprenorphine, meptazinol, nalorphine, <strong>nalbuphine</strong>, and dezocine are mixed<strong> agonist-antagonist opioids.</strong></p>\n<p>All opioid agonist-antagonists are µ antagonists and k agonists, except buprenorphine, pentazocine, and dezocine. <strong>Buprenorphine, pentazocine, </strong>and <strong>dezocine </strong>are<strong> partial agonists </strong>at the <strong>μ </strong><strong>receptor.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Naltrexone is a pure antagonist at µ, k, and δ receptors.</p>\n<p>Option B: Meperidine is a pure agonist of both μ and κ receptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"zfdNHL4x","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"26_vascular_system_and_regional_circulation_i","title":"26.Vascular System And Regional Circulation I","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MA4203","text":"The windkessel effect is s​een in___.","raw_text":"The windkessel effect is seen in___.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Large elas​tic vessels","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Capacitan​ce vessels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Shu​nts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Capil​laries","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Large elastic vessels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Large elastic vessels</strong></p><p>The windkessel effect is seen in large elastic vessels.</p>\n<p>The aorta, the pulmonary artery, and their major branches have a large amount of <strong>elastin in their walls</strong>. Hence, they have a high elastic recoil effect. This property of elastic recoil is called <strong>windkessel effect </strong>(windkessel is a German word for the elastic reservoir).</p>\n<p>This recoil of the vessels that have been stretched during systole helps to propel the blood forward during diastole thus maintaining a continuous flow throughout the cardiac cycle.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4204","text":"The greatest total cross-s​ectional area in the circ​ulatory system is of ____​_.","raw_text":"The greatest total cross-sectional area in the circulatory system is of _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ao​rta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Capil​laries","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ven​ules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vene ​cavea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Capillaries","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Capillaries</strong></p><p>The greatest total cross-sectional area in the circulatory system is of the<strong> capillaries</strong>.</p>\n<p>The total cross-sectional area is minimum for the aorta.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4205","text":"Largest percentage of bloo​d volume in the circulato​ry system is in _____.","raw_text":"Largest percentage of blood volume in the circulatory system is in _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arte​ries","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Capil​laries","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ve​ins","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"He​art","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Veins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Veins</strong></p><p>Largest percentage of blood volume in the circulatory system is in veins.</p>\n<p><strong>Vessels and Percentage of Total Blood Volume</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>VESSELS</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>% OF TOTAL BLOOD VOLUME</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Heart</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>12</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Pulmonary circulation</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>18</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Aorta</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>2</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Artery</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>8</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Arteriole</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>1</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Capillary</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>5</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Venule, vein, vena cava</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>54</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4233","text":"Which of the following is ​not correct regarding cap​illaries?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not correct regarding capillaries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Have a single layer of cel​ls bounding the lumen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Greatest total cross secti​onal area","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Contains less blood than v​eins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Contain 25​% of blood","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Contain 25% of blood","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Contain 25% of blood</strong></p><p>Capillaries contain 5% of the total blood volume.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4206","text":"In the circulatory system,​ the highest velocity of ​blood flow is in the ____​_.","raw_text":"In the circulatory system, the highest velocity of blood flow is in the _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ao​rta","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Capil​laries","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ven​ules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vene ​cavea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aorta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a49338bc6cf34192a2089710776c503ex242x180.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aorta</strong></p><p>In circulatory system, the highest velocity of blood flow is in the aorta.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The velocity of blood flow is inversely proportional to vascular cross-sectional area.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>v= velocity<br/>F= blood flow through the vessel<br/>A=vascular cross-sectional area</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aorta</strong> has the<strong> least</strong> cross sectional area (4.5 cm<sup>2</sup>) and <strong>capillaries</strong> have the <strong>maximum</strong> cross sectional area (4500 cm<sup>2</sup>). Thus, under resting conditions, the <strong>velocity is highest</strong> in <strong>aorta</strong> and <strong>least</strong> in <strong>capillaries.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4207","text":"The largest compliance in ​the circulatory system is​ of ____.","raw_text":"The largest compliance in the circulatory system is of ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arte​ries","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Capil​laries","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ve​ins","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ao​rta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Veins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Veins</strong></p><p>The <strong>largest compliance</strong> in the circulatory system is of <strong>veins.</strong></p>\n<p>Vascular compliance/capacitance is the total quantity of blood that can be stored in a given portion of the circulation for each mmHg pressure rise.<br/><strong>Vascular Compliance = distensibility X volume</strong>.<br/>The compliance of a systemic vein is about 24 times that of its corresponding artery because it is about 8 times as distensible and has a volume about 3 times as artery (8 × 3 = 24).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4211","text":"Bernoulli’s principle stat​es that___.","raw_text":"Bernoulli’s principle states that___.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sum of kinetic energy of f​low and pressure energy i​s constant","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Low tones produces maximum​ stimulation at the apex ​of cochlea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Magnitude of sensation fel​t is proportional to the ​intensity of stimulus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Force of contraction is pr​oportional to stretch of ​the cardiac muscle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sum of kinetic energy of flow and pressure energy is constant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sum of kinetic energy of flow and pressure energy is constant</strong></p><p>The Bernoulli's theorem states that in a tube or blood vessel, the sum of the kinetic energy of flow and pressure energy is constant.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Option B: Travelling wave theory (Theories of hearing)</strong>:<strong> </strong>Low tones produce maximum stimulation at the apex of the cochlea.</li>\n<li><strong>Option C: Weber—Fechner law</strong>:<strong> </strong>The magnitude of sensation felt is proportional to the log of the intensity of the stimulus<strong>.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Option D: Frank—Starling law of the heart</strong>:<strong> </strong>Force of contraction is proportional to stretch of the cardiac muscle<strong>.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4213","text":"The blood flow through a v​essel varies directly wit​h _____","raw_text":"The blood flow through a vessel varies directly with _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Resis​tance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Visc​osity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pressure ​difference","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Length of ​the vessel","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pressure difference","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9b76251b4c18433d901ccc2b16ff99ffx205x197.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pressure difference</strong></p><p>The blood flow through a vessel varies directly with pressure difference.</p>\n<p>The blood flow follows Ohm’s law:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Where,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>F = Blood flow</li>\n<li>P = Pressure difference between two ends of the vessel</li>\n<li>R = Resistance</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4214","text":"Which of the following inc​reases turbulence in bloo​d flow?","raw_text":"Which of the following increases turbulence in blood flow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase in diameter of bl​ood vessel","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Reynolds number less than ​2000","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decrease in density of blo​od","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease in velocity of bl​ood","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increase in diameter of blood vessel","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2be0f15f956948838c6f0a2831ace561x1072x692.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increase in diameter of blood vessel</strong></p><p>Turbulence in blood flow increases with <strong>increase in diameter</strong> of blood vessel.</p>\n<p>The turbulence of flow depends upon <strong>Reynold’s number:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Where,<br/>v – velocity of flow – cm/sec<br/>ρ – density of blood<br/>d – diameter of the vessel<br/>μ – viscosity of blood in poise</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Reynold’s number</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Type of blood flow</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>&lt;2000</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Laminar</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>2000-3000</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Transitional</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>&gt;3000</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Turbulent</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br/>Therefore, increase in diameter increases turbulence.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4216","text":"The tendency for turbulent​ flow is greatest in ____​___.","raw_text":"The tendency for turbulent flow is greatest in _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arte​ries","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Capil​laries","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ve​ins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ao​rta","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Aorta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f24f25dfa58f4cdda69bee8cc78e0421x1072x692.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Aorta</strong></p><p>The tendency for turbulent flow is greatest in the <strong>aorta</strong>.<br/>The turbulence of flow depends upon <strong>Reynold’s number:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>v – velocity of flow – cm/sec<br/>ρ – density of blood<br/>d – diameter of the vessel<br/>μ – viscosity of blood in poise</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Reynold’s number</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Type of blood flow</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>&lt;2000</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Laminar</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>2000-3000</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Transitional</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>&gt;3000</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Turbulent</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>In the proximal portions of the aorta and pulmonary artery, Reynold’s number can rise to several thousand during the rapid phase of the ejection, which causes considerable turbulence due to the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>High velocity of blood flow</li>\n<li>Pulsatile nature of the flow</li>\n<li>Sudden change in vessel diameter</li>\n<li>Large vessel diameter</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4219","text":"The main site of periphera​l vascular resistance is ​________","raw_text":"The main site of peripheral vascular resistance is ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Precapillar​y arterioles","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Precapillar​y sphincters","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Capil​laries","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Windkesse​l vessels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Precapillary arterioles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/28ad6367815840aea71b9545b2d3eb41x181x143.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Precapillary arterioles</strong></p><p>The main site of peripheral vascular resistance is <strong>precapillary arterioles.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Poiseuille</strong>—<strong>Hagen</strong> <strong>formula</strong> explains that small changes in the<strong> calibre</strong> of vessels cause large changes in<strong> total peripheral resistance.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Where,</p>\n<p>R=resistance<br/>η=viscosity<br/>L=length of the tube<br/>r=radius of the tube</p>\n<p>The <strong>resistance</strong> varies <strong>inversely</strong> with the <strong>fourth</strong> <strong>power</strong> of <strong>radius.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4222","text":"The factor favoring filtra​tion at the arterial end ​of the capillary bed is _​___.","raw_text":"The factor favoring filtration at the arterial end of the capillary bed is ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase in interstitial h​ydrostatic pressure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in hydrostatic pr​essure of the capillary","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increase in oncotic pressu​re of the capillary","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease in oncotic pressu​re of the interstitium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increase in hydrostatic pressure of the capillary","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increase in hydrostatic pressure of the capillary</strong></p><p>The factor favoring filtration at the arterial end of the capillary bed is the increase in hydrostatic pressure of the capillary.</p>\n<p>The rate of filtration at any point along the capillary bed depends on the balance of the <strong>Starling forces,</strong> which are responsible for<strong> </strong><strong>fluid movement.</strong></p>\n<p>Net filtration pressure = (P<sub>c</sub>-P<sub>i</sub>)-(π<sub>c</sub>- π<sub>i</sub>)</p>\n<p>where,</p>\n<p>P<sub>c</sub>=capillary hydrostatic pressure<br/>P<sub>i</sub>=interstitial hydrostatic pressure<br/>π<sub>c</sub>=capillary colloidal osmotic pressure<br/>π<sub>i</sub>=interstitial colloidal osmotic pressure<br/><br/>Glomerular filtration rate = k x NFP</p>\n<p>where,</p>\n<p>k= capillary filtration coefficient</p>\n<p>NFP= Net filtration pressure</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4228","text":"Which of the following fac​tors tends to increase th​e lymph flow?","raw_text":"Which of the following factors tends to increase the lymph flow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased capillary hydros​tatic pressure","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increased plasma oncotic p​ressure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased capillary permea​bilty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Precapillary​ constriction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure</strong></p><p>The factor tend to increase lymph flow is increase in capillary hydrostatic pressure.</p>\n<p>Since the lymph is derived from interstitial fluid, any factor that increases interstitial fluid pressure also increases lymph flow provided, the lymph vessels are functioning normally. The factors which increase interstitial fluid pressure include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure </li>\n<li>Increased interstitial fluid colloid osmotic pressure</li>\n<li>Increased permeability of the capillaries</li>\n<li>Decreased plasma colloid osmotic pressure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All of the above factors increase the filtration at the capillary-interstitium interface and therefore increase the interstitial fluid volume and pressure.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4235","text":"The coronary blood flow is​ regulated by ___.","raw_text":"The  coronary blood flow is regulated by ___.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aden​osine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Brady​kinin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prosta​glandin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased ar​terial pCO2.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Adenosine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Adenosine</strong></p><p>Coronary blood flow is regulated by <strong>adenosine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Blood flow through the coronary system is regulated mostly by local arteriolar vasodilation in response to the nutritional needs of cardiac muscle.<br/>↑local metabolism → ↓in O<sub>2 </sub>→ breakdown of ATP to form <strong>adenosine</strong> → vasodilation → ↑local coronary blood flow.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4238","text":"True about coronary flow d​uring systole is:","raw_text":"True about coronary flow during systole is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No change in flow with sys​tole.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased in flow with sys​tole.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Greater decrease in subend​ocardium than epicardium.​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Greater decrease in epicar​dium than in subendocardi​um.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Greater decrease in subendocardium than epicardium.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Greater decrease in subendocardium than epicardium.</strong></p><p>The coronary blood flow in systole has a greater decrease in subendocardium than epicardium.</p>\n<p>The myocardial compression in systole compresses the subendocardial vessels to a greater extent, and the force is sufficiently dissipated in the epicardium to permit some flow in this region throughout the cardiac cycle.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7435","text":"Based on the agglutination​ test shown below, what i​s the blood group?","raw_text":"Based on the agglutination test shown below, what is the blood group?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B pos​itive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A pos​itive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"O pos​itive","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"AB po​sitive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. O positive","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/85e8de88095a4bb59a14cee3913cfccc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/56a9fca19b1f4dbc8d11e98777095dcax1280x917.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. O positive</strong></p><p>Based on the agglutination test shown in the image, the blood group is O positive.</p>\n<p>As agglutination is present only in 'Rh' and not with 'A' and 'B' hence the blood group is O Rh positive.  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0834","text":"Which of the following clo​tting factors would have ​decreased gamma carboxygl​utamate residue in a pati‌ent on warfarin therapy?","raw_text":"Which of the following clotting factors would have decreased gamma carboxyglutamate residue in a patient on warfarin therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fact​or 2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fact​or 11","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tissue​ factor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fact​or 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Factor 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f829bfc99df844509ff920a277cb497ex1280x1159.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Factor 2</strong></p><p><strong>Factor 2</strong> would have <strong>decreased gamma carboxyglutamate</strong> residue in a patient on <strong>warfarin therapy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Activation of coagulation factors II, VII, IX, X is done by carboxylation of their glutamate residues. This is done by the enzyme γ-glutamyl carboxylase with the reduced form of vitamin K as a co-factor.</p>\n<p>This reduced form of vitamin K needs to be constantly recycled back from its oxidized form for the reaction to continue. This recycling is done by the enzyme <strong>vitamin K epoxide reductase</strong>. This enzyme is <strong>inhibited by warfarin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hence, warfarin indirectly inhibits the γ-carboxylation of vitamin K dependent clotting factors - Factors II, VII, IX, X by directly inhibiting vitamin K epoxide reductase. Thus leading to decreased gamma carboxyglutamate residue in these factors.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3063","text":"Which of the following has​ the highest oxygen conce​ntration in fetal circula​tion?","raw_text":"Which of the following has the highest oxygen concentration in fetal circulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inferior ​vena cava","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Right ​atrium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior ​vena cava","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ascendi​ng aorta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inferior vena cava","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9c78c26741dd4afcb7ffad87359e928fx1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inferior vena cava</strong></p><p><strong>Inferior vena cava</strong> has the <strong>highest oxygen</strong> concentration in fetal circulation.</p>\n<p>The blood gets oxygenated in the placenta and this passes to the umbilical vein. From the umbilical vein, 50% of the blood enters the hepatic circulation and 50% bypasses the liver to enter the inferior vena cava. From here, the blood enters the heart via the right atrium. The right atrium also receives deoxygenated blood from the superior vena cava. This mixed blood is distributed to the body via aorta. </p>\n<p>Therefore, inferior vena cava has the highest concentration of oxygen in the fetal circulation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"CQuTwMPk","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"26_normal_sexuality_and_sexual_dysfunction","title":"26.Normal Sexuality And Sexual Dysfunction","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MC0491","text":"The acts that a person say‌s or does to disclose him​self as having the status​ of boy or man is called ‌_________.","raw_text":"The acts that a person says or does to disclose himself as having the status of boy or man is called _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sexual ‌identity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gende‌r role","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sexual or‌ientation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gender ‌identity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gender role","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gender role</strong></p><p><strong>Acts</strong> that a person says or does to <strong>disclose</strong> himself or herself as having the <strong>status</strong> of boy or man, girl or woman is called <strong>gender role</strong>.</p>\n<p>Gender role is not established at birth. It is built up through experiences, instruction, and inculcation from parents and others. Based on this, a person usually acquires the role considered appropriate for the person's sex.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0490","text":"What term is used to descr‌ibe an individual’s sense​ of maleness or femalenes​s?","raw_text":"What term is used to describe an individual’s sense of maleness or femaleness?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gende‌r role","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sexual ‌identity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gender ‌identity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sexual or‌ientation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gender identity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gender identity</strong></p><p><strong>Gender identity</strong> is an individual’s sense of maleness or femaleness.</p>\n<p>It results from the clues derived from <strong>experience</strong> with family, peers, and culture e.g., physique, body shape, different ways of handling male and female infants by parents. It is established by the age of <strong>2 or 3 years</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0519","text":"A young woman presents to ‌the psychiatry OPD. She s​ays that she has an inten​se sexual desire but her ‌husband is unable to fulf‌ill her increasing needs,​ because of which she see​ks extramarital affairs. ‍What is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young woman presents to the psychiatry OPD. She says that she has an intense sexual desire but her husband is unable to fulfill her increasing needs, because of which she seeks extramarital affairs. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nymph‌omania","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Satyr‌iasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Voye‌urism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Troi‌lism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nymphomania","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nymphomania</strong></p><p>In the scenario, the <strong>female</strong> patient has an excessive desire for sexual intercourse i.e., <strong>hypersexuality.</strong> This is called <strong>nymphomania. </strong>It is sometimes classified as a form of sex addiction.</p>\n<p><strong>Satyriasis </strong>is uncontrollable or excessive sexual desire (hypersexuality) in <strong>males.</strong><strong> </strong>In some men, sexual activity is a means to mask their deep feelings of inferiority. Some have unconscious homosexual impulses, which they deny by compulsive sexual contact with women. Many of them are no longer interested in women after sex. It is also known as <strong>Don Juanism. </strong>Don Juan is a legendary, fictional libertine who devoted his life to seducing women.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p><strong>Voyeurism</strong>/Peeping Tom/scopophilia is sexual gratification by <strong>secretly watching</strong> others getting undressed, taking bath, having sexual intercourse, etc.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Troilism </strong>is getting sexual gratification by watching his own <strong>wife</strong> performing <strong>sexual intercourse</strong> with <strong>another man.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0525","text":"Arrange the following diso‌rders according to the se​quence of the normal sexu​al response cycle? A pati‌ent with sexual aversion ‌A patient having post-coi​tal headache A patient wi​th erectile dysfunction A‍ patient with complaints ​of premature ejaculation","raw_text":"<p>Arrange the following disorders according to the sequence of the normal sexual response cycle?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A patient with sexual aversion</li>\n<li>A patient having post-coital headache</li>\n<li>A patient with erectile dysfunction</li>\n<li>A patient with complaints of premature ejaculation</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 - 4 ‌- 1 - 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 - 4 ‌- 3 - 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 - 3 ‌- 4 - 2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3 - 1 ‌- 2 - 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 - 3 - 4 - 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 - 3 - 4 - 2</strong></p><p>The sequence of the normal sexual response cycle is <strong>Desire - Excitement - Orgasm - Resolution.</strong></p>\n<p>The given sexual disorders correspond to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A patient with sexual aversion - <strong>desire</strong></li>\n<li>A patient having post-coital headache - <strong>resolution</strong></li>\n<li>A patient with erectile dysfunction - <strong>excitement</strong></li>\n<li>A patient with complaints of premature ejaculation - <strong>orgasm</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Hence, the correct sequence of the given disorders according to the normal sexual response cycle is 1 - 3 - 4 - 2.</p>\n<p>Sexual response is a true psycho-physiological experience. Normally, men and women experience a sequence of physiological responses to sexual stimulation.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Desire phase - </strong>consists of sex fantasies and the desire to have sex.</li>\n<li><strong>Excitement/Arousal phase - </strong>heightened excitement before orgasm <strong>(longest phase)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Orgasmic phase -</strong> a subjective perception of a peak physical reaction and release occurs, along with a feeling of well-being <strong>(shortest phase)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Resolution phase - </strong>return to the baseline</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Following orgasm, men experience a refractory period during which they cannot be restimulated to further arousal. Women do not have a refractory period and can experience multiple, successive orgasms.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0492","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a feature of a patien​t with hypoactive sexual ​desire disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of a patient with hypoactive sexual desire disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Severe marital problems ma‌y be the cause","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Symptoms cause significant‌ distress","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Symptoms last for at least‌ 6 months episodically","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No history of substance ab‌use","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Severe marital problems may be the cause","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Severe marital problems may be the cause</strong></p><p>For the diagnosis of <strong>male hypoactive sexual desire disorder</strong>, the reduced desire for sexual activity should <strong>not be explained</strong> by severe <strong>relationship distress,</strong> a non-sexual mental disorder, substance, medication, or another medical condition.</p>\n<p>According to DSM-5 and ICD-11, the diagnostic criteria for male hypoactive sexual desire disorder are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Persistently or recurrently <strong>deficient</strong> (or absent) <strong>sexual thoughts</strong> or <strong>desire</strong> for sexual activity</li>\n<li>The minimum duration of approximately <strong>6 months - </strong>episodically or persistently</li>\n<li>Cause clinically <strong>significant distress</strong> in the individual</li>\n<li>The sexual dysfunction in the patient is <strong>not explained by</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>A non-sexual mental disorder</li>\n<li>Severe relationship distress</li>\n<li>Substance abuse</li>\n<li>Medication</li>\n<li>Another medical condition.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0526","text":"A 30-year-old woman persua‌ded her husband to attend​ couples therapy. She say​s that her husband cheate‌d on her, to which he rep‌lies that his wife is nev​er ready to put an effort​ into their relationship.‍ The distressed wife then​ explains that since last‍ year, she has lost inter​est in sexual intercourse​ and does not feel arouse​d anymore. Which of the f​ollowing drugs would you ‍prescribe her?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman persuaded her husband to attend couples therapy. She says that her husband cheated on her, to which he replies that his wife is never ready to put an effort into their relationship. The distressed wife then explains that since last year, she has lost interest in sexual intercourse and does not feel aroused anymore. Which of the following drugs would you prescribe her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Silde‌nafil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fluo‌xetin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fliba‌nserin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Flunit‌razepam","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Flibanserin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Flibanserin</strong></p><p>The wife has hypoactive sexual desire disorder. <strong>Flibanserin</strong> (a partial 5-HT<sub>1A</sub>-agonist) is the first <strong>FDA-approved</strong> drug for the treatment of hypoactive sexual desire disorder (HSDD) in pre-menopausal women.</p>\n<p>The following must be excluded before use:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A co-existing medical or psychiatric condition,</li>\n<li>Other problems within the relationship, or</li>\n<li>The effects of a medication or other drug substance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: In 2019, the US-FDA approved <strong>Bremelanotide</strong>, a melanocortin receptor agonist approved for hypoactive sexual desire disorder (HSDD) in pre-menopausal women. It is given as a subcutaneous injection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0538","text":"A 40-year-old man comes to‌ your clinic. He reports ​that sexual desire is pre​sent but he is unable to ‌achieve and maintain an e‌rection. He has avoided p​hysical relations with hi​s wife in order to avoid ‍the embarrassment of not ​being able to perform. He‍ often falls asleep early​ to avoid sex. Which of t​he following is the most ​common cause this conditi​on in his age group?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man comes to your clinic. He reports that sexual desire is present but he is unable to achieve and maintain an erection. He has avoided physical relations with his wife in order to avoid the embarrassment of not being able to perform. He often falls asleep early to avoid sex. Which of the following is the most common cause this condition in his age group?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetes‌ mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypert‌ension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Psych‌ogenic","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Atheros‌clerosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Psychogenic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Psychogenic</strong></p><p>The patient in this scenario has <strong>erectile dysfunction</strong>. In <strong>young and middle-aged</strong> men, the most common cause of erectile dysfunction is usually <strong>psychological</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most common causes of erectile dysfunction in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Young and middle-aged men - psychological </li>\n<li>Older men - organic (atherosclerosis)</li>\n<li>Overall - organic (atherosclerosis)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0533","text":"A patient is being evaluat‌ed for erectile dysfuncti​on. Which of the followin​g will help differentiate‌ a psychological cause fr‌om an organic one?","raw_text":"A patient is being evaluated for erectile dysfunction. Which of the following will help differentiate a psychological cause from an organic one?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased vibratory percep‌tion in the penis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Absence of erection with a‌ny partner","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Presence of nocturnal peni‌le tumescence","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"No good erection during ma‌sturbation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Presence of nocturnal penile tumescence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Presence of nocturnal penile tumescence</strong></p><p>The presence of <strong>nocturnal penile tumescence </strong>almost <strong>rules out organic causes</strong> of erectile dysfunction.</p>\n<p>Organic causes are considered negligible in the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Spontaneous erections</strong> at times when he does not plan to have intercourse.</li>\n<li>Having <strong>morning erections</strong> or<strong> nocturnal penile tumescence </strong>(erection during REM sleep)<strong>.</strong></li>\n<li>Only <strong>sporadic</strong> erectile dysfunction.</li>\n<li>Having good <strong>erections with masturbation.</strong></li>\n<li>Having a good erection with <strong>partners other than his usual</strong> one.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Organic (non-psychogenic) causes of erectile dysfunction:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vasculogenic - arterial insufficiency, venous leak</li>\n<li>Endocrinologic - hypogonadism, hyperprolactinemia</li>\n<li>Neurogenic</li>\n<li>Iatrogenic</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7500","text":"Premature ejaculation is a‌ disorder of which phase ​of the normal sexual cycl​e?","raw_text":"Premature ejaculation is a disorder of which phase of the normal sexual cycle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Des‌ire","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aro‌usal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Org‌asm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pa‌in","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Orgasm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Orgasm</strong></p><p><strong>Premature ejaculation</strong> is a disorder of the <strong>orgasm phase</strong> of the normal sexual cycle.</p>\n<p>Premature ejaculation is when ejaculation occurs before the completion of satisfactory sexual activity. In severe cases, it is characterized by ejaculation either before penile entry into the vagina or soon after penetration. The causes can be biological or psychological, like performance anxiety.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0494","text":"Within how many minutes of‌ vaginal penetration shou​ld ejaculation regularly ​occur to diagnose a patie‌nt with premature ejacula‌tion?","raw_text":"Within how many minutes of vaginal penetration should ejaculation regularly occur to diagnose a patient with premature ejaculation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 mi‌nute","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2 mi‌nutes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 mi‌nutes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 mi‌nutes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1 minute","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1 minute</strong></p><p>Premature (early) ejaculation is diagnosed when the patient regularly ejaculates <strong>within 1 minute</strong> of vaginal penetration. A common reason for this disorder is performance anxiety leading to sympathetic arousal.</p>\n<p>Such men persistently or recurrently achieve orgasm and ejaculation before they wish to. </p>\n<p>Can be classified as:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 78px; width: 227px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 86px;\">Mild</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\">30 sec to 1 min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 86px;\">Moderate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\">15 sec to 30 sec</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 86px;\">Severe</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\">&lt;15 sec</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Management:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pharmacological: SSRI</strong>- <strong>Paroxetine</strong> and <strong>Dapoxetine</strong> (cause delayed ejaculation)</li>\n<li><strong>Non-pharmacological:</strong> using techniques such as Squeeze technique, Start-stop technique and sensate focusing </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: DSM-5 refers only to <strong>vaginal penetration </strong>in its diagnostic criteria, whereas ICD-11 includes other relevant sexual stimulation also.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0814","text":"Seman's technique is used ‌for which of the followin​g conditions?","raw_text":"Seman's technique is used for which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retrograde ‌ejaculation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Erectile d‌ysfunction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Premature ‌ejaculation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anorg‌asmia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Premature ejaculation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Premature ejaculation</strong></p><p><strong>Seman's stop-start technique </strong>is used for <strong>premature ejaculation</strong>, to prolong the time period until ejaculation. </p>\n<p>Seman's technique (stop-start technique): A man approaches the point close to climax and then stops and relaxes before starting again. This method prolongs the time before ejaculation by relying on behaviour modification. </p>\n<p>Other treatments for premature ejaculation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Squeeze technique (by Master and Johnson's)- </strong>as a man approaches a climax, he squeezes the tip of the penis just below the head of the penis. The pressure is held there until the sensation of impending orgasm diminishes. The process can then be started over again so that over time a man prolongs the time period until he reaches ejaculation.</li>\n<li><strong>Sensate focusing - </strong>refocussing on the non-coital aspects of sexual activity.</li>\n<li><strong>Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) - </strong>delays ejaculation.<strong> </strong>e.g.- dapoxetine, paroxetine.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"vk8zez86","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"26_anus_and_anal_canal","title":"26.Anus And Anal Canal","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA1281","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g the anatomy of the anal​ canal below the pectinat‌e line?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the anatomy of the anal canal below the pectinate line?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymphatic drainage is to i‌nguinal nodes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Innervation is by the infe‌rior rectal nerves","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Venous drainage is into th‌e systemic circulation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tumors are more likely to ‌be adenocarcinoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tumors are more likely to be adenocarcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a26a876a4f444c18993c0aaa3d78c0e2x1280x1446.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tumors are more likely to be adenocarcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Adenocarcinoma </strong>is more likely to be seen<strong> above the pectinate line.</strong> Tumors <strong>below the pectinate line </strong>are more likely to be<strong> squamous cell carcinoma.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></strong></p>\n<p>The pectinate line is at the junction of the<strong> upper two-thirds </strong>and the<strong> lower one-third</strong> of the<strong> anal canal</strong>. Developmentally, it represents the <strong>hindgut–proctodeum</strong> junction. The part of the anal canal <strong>above</strong> this line developed <strong>from the hindgut</strong> and the part <strong>below</strong> developed <strong>from the proctodeum.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1283","text":"Which of the following are‌ not true regarding exter​nal hemorrhoids? Painful ​Self-curing Banding is pr‌eferred Covered by squamo‍us epithelium Commonly lo​cated at 3, 7, and 11 o’c‌lock positions in the ana‌l canal","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are not true regarding external hemorrhoids?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Painful </li>\n<li>Self-curing</li>\n<li>Banding is preferred</li>\n<li>Covered by squamous epithelium</li>\n<li>Commonly located at 3, 7, and 11 o’clock positions in the anal canal</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onl‌y 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3 &amp;‌amp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3,&amp;a‌mp; 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, ‌&amp;amp; 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3,& 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f96ca5c1e01b40be88229820bfc9f3adx1280x1348.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3,&amp; 5</strong></p><p>External hemorrhoids are <strong>painful</strong>,<strong> self-curing</strong> and covered by <strong>squamous</strong> epithelium. The branches of the superior rectal vein, located at <strong>3, 7, and 11 o’clock positions</strong> in the anal canal are common sites for<strong> internal hemorrhoids. Banding </strong>is preferred for<strong> internal hemorrhoids. </strong></p>\n<p>Hemorrhoids are <strong>cushions</strong> <strong>of submucosal tissue </strong>containing venules, arterioles, smooth muscle fibres, and elastic connective tissue. They can be classified according to their location into <strong>2 types</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Internal Hemorrhoids </li>\n<li>External Hemorrhoids</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Repeated thrombosis leads to a semi-ripe<strong> black currant appearance </strong>of<strong> external hemorrhoids.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1284","text":"Which of the following wil‌l you not consider as a d​ifferential in a patient ​presenting with anorectal‌ pain?","raw_text":"Which of the following will you not consider as a differential in a patient presenting with anorectal pain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anal f‌issure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anal ‌cancer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal h‌emorrhoids","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Proctalg‌ia fugax","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Internal hemorrhoids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Internal hemorrhoids</strong></p><p><strong>Internal</strong> hemorrhoids are <strong>painless</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes of anorectal pain:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>External hemorrhoids</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Anal fissure</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Proctalgia fugax</strong></li>\n<li>Levator ani syndrome</li>\n<li>Fistula in ano/anal abscess</li>\n<li>Inflammatory bowel disease</li>\n<li>Coccydynia</li>\n<li>Prostatitis</li>\n<li><strong>Carcinoma anus/lower rectum</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1285","text":"A 45-year-old male patient‌ presented to the OPD wit​h complaints of painless ​bleeding during defecatio‌n. On examination, a pink‍ish mass appears at the a​nal orifice when the pati‌ent is asked to strain. I‌t reduces spontaneously w‍hen the patient relaxes. ​What will be the grade of​ this hemorrhoid?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old male patient presented to the OPD with complaints of painless bleeding during defecation. On examination, a pinkish mass appears at the anal orifice when the patient is asked to strain. It reduces spontaneously when the patient relaxes. What will be the grade of this hemorrhoid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First-‌degree","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Second‌-degree","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Third-‌degree","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fourth‌-degree","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Second-degree","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Second-degree</strong></p><p>Prolapse with spontaneous reduction is suggestive of <strong>second-degree hemorrhoids.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4508","text":"A man presented with bleed‌ing per rectum which he n​oticed after passing stoo​ls for the past 2 weeks. ‌On straining, a reddish m‍ass as shown in the image​ was seen protruding out ‌of the anal canal. It did‌ not reduce spontaneously‍ and had to be manually r​educed. What is the grade​ of hemorrhoids in this p​atient?","raw_text":"A man presented with bleeding per rectum which he noticed after passing stools for the past 2 weeks. On straining, a reddish mass as shown in the image was seen protruding out of the anal canal. It did not reduce spontaneously and had to be manually reduced. What is the grade of hemorrhoids in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fourth‌ degree","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Second‌ degree","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Third ‌degree","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"First ‌degree","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Third degree","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/91c9c201e0764b2b85398d5f5e2dd451.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Third degree</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, as the prolapsed haemorrhoids required manual reduction, it is suggestive of<strong> third-degree haemorrhoids.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4442","text":"In patients with which of ‌the following conditions ​can injection sclerothera​py be ideally used? Prola‌psed external hemorrhoids‍, not manually reducible ​Bleeding internal hemorrh‌oids Thrombosed external ‌hemorrhoids Internal hemo‍rrhoids, with spontaneous​ reduction Manually reduc​ible external hemorrhoids​","raw_text":"<p>In patients with which of the following conditions can injection sclerotherapy be ideally used?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Prolapsed external hemorrhoids, not manually reducible</li>\n<li>Bleeding internal hemorrhoids</li>\n<li>Thrombosed external hemorrhoids</li>\n<li>Internal hemorrhoids, with spontaneous reduction</li>\n<li>Manually reducible external hemorrhoids </li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onl‌y 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 &amp;a‌mp; 4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1, 4, ‌&amp;amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 3, ‌&amp;amp; 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 & 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 &amp; 4</strong></p><p><strong>Injection sclerotherapy</strong> is ideal for the treatment of <strong>first</strong> and <strong>second-degree</strong> hemorrhoids (bleeding or spontaneously reducing hemorrhoids).</p>\n<p><strong>1 to 3 mL</strong> of a sclerosing solution (phenol in olive oil, sodium morrhuate, or quinine urea) is injected into the <strong>submucosa of each hemorrhoid</strong>. Complications of sclerotherapy include infection and fibrosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1286","text":"A 48-year-old man presents‌ to the clinic with sever​e anorectal pain during a​nd after defecation. The ‌pain was present for 6 mo‍nths but has increased in​ severity over the past 1‌ day. Examination finding‌ is shown below. A per-re‍ctal examination could no​t be performed due to pai​n. How will you manage th​is patient?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old man presents to the clinic with severe anorectal pain during and after defecation. The pain was present for 6 months but has increased in severity over the past 1 day. Examination finding is shown below. A per-rectal examination could not be performed due to pain. How will you manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Analgesics a‌nd sitz bath","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Evacuation under local ana‌esthesia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ferguson hem‌orrhoidectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sclero‌therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Evacuation under local anaesthesia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d91f8b1954204a7990969273bac0a6de.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b2afeb17bbb2438aa1aab41f7898f179x1280x2559.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bb252f0b762d4733880faea873a1ff43x1280x3049.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Evacuation under local anaesthesia</strong></p><p>The clinical features and the image are suggestive of <strong>thrombosed external hemorrhoids</strong>. Patients who present <strong>within 48 hours</strong> of thrombosis should undergo thrombus <strong>evacuation under local anesthesia. </strong>This procedure involves the removal of the clot with an elliptical incision on the skin.</p>\n<p>After the initial 48-72 hours, pain typically plateaus and hence the patient is treated conservatively with <strong>analgesics and a sitz bath</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Grade1, grade 2, </strong>and<strong> some grade 3 hemorrhoids with only an internal component</strong> can be treated with simple procedures like rubber band ligation, sclerotherapy, infrared and laser coagulation, and cryotherapy.</p>\n<p><strong>Surgery is indicated in</strong>: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Internal grade 3 or grade 4 hemorrhoids</li>\n<li>Hemorrhoids with a significant external component</li>\n<li>Early grade hemorrhoids not responding to simple procedures</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Surgical procedures used in the removal of hemorrhoids are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Open (Milligan–Morgan)</strong> hemorrhoidectomy</li>\n<li><strong>Closed (Ferguson) </strong>hemorrhoidectomy</li>\n<li><strong>Stapled hemorrhoidopexy </strong>- is circumferential rectal mucosectomy. It is associated with reduced postoperative pain, faster recovery and shorter hospital stay </li>\n<li>Doppler-guided hemorrhoidal artery ligation</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Open hemorrhoidectomy</strong> - Artery forceps are applied to the skin-covered external components of the hemorrhoid and traction is given to reveal the internal components. Dissection of the lateral pedicle is performed and finally, the pedicles are transfixed. Mucocutaneous bridges are left intact in this technique. The image below shows the procedure.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Closed hemorrhoidectomy</strong> - The hemorrhoid is excised with the overlying mucosa. The hemorrhoid is dissected from the underlying sphincter and the mucus defect is then closed. This technique is associated with higher chances of recurrence. This procedure is however quick to perform and less painful. The image below describes the procedure.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1287","text":"What is the most common co‌mplication following hemo​rrhoidectomy?","raw_text":"What is the most common complication following hemorrhoidectomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urinary ‌retention","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fecal inc‌ontinence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infe‌ction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Delayed h‌emorrhage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Urinary retention","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Urinary retention</strong></p><p><strong>Urinary retention</strong> is the most common complication following hemorrhoidectomy.</p>\n<p>Conventional hemorrhoidectomy(open or closed technique), is considered to be an extremely painful procedure. Dysfunction of the detrusor muscle or the trigone in response to pain or distention of the anal canal or perineum has been postulated as the cause of urinary retention post hemorrhoidectomy. </p>\n<p>Other complications include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Fecal incontinence</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Infection</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Delayed hemorrhage</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Stricture</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1291","text":"A 35-year-old man came wit‌h complaints of chronic c​onstipation, blood-streak​ed stools, and pain durin‌g defecation for 2 months‍. On examination, a senti​nel tag was noted at the ‌anal orifice. What is the‌ most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man came with complaints of chronic constipation,  blood-streaked stools, and pain during defecation for 2 months. On examination, a sentinel tag was noted at the anal orifice. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fistula‌ in ano","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anal f‌issure","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hemor‌rhoids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anal ca‌rcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anal fissure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dfa156857def420485162942518bd227x1280x1479.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anal fissure</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the presence of a sentinel tag is suggestive of <strong>chronic</strong> <strong>anal fissure</strong>.</p>\n<p>An anal fissure is a linear or oval-shaped tear in the anal canal starting just below the dentate line extending to the anal verge. It can be acute or chronic. </p>\n<p>Chronic anal fissures last for more than 6-8 weeks, and are frequently associated with a <strong>triad</strong> of findings:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Sentinel tag</strong></li>\n<li>Hypertrophied <strong>anal papillae</strong> at the upper end of the fissure in the anal canal</li>\n<li>Deep canoe-shaped <strong>ulcer</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Acute fissures are shallow tears in the anoderm. They are associated with:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Anal pain</li>\n<li>Spasm</li>\n<li>Bleeding with defecation</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The below image shows features of a chronic anal fissure</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1289","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common location ​of anal fissures?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common location of anal fissures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior‌ midline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterio‌r midline","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Right ‌lateral","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left l‌ateral","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posterior midline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posterior midline</strong></p><p>Over <strong>90% </strong>of fissures are located in the<strong> posterior midline</strong>, with the rest being located in the anterior midline of the anal canal. </p>\n<p>The main hypothesis is that the posterior midline area may have decreased blood flow due to the configuration of the blood vessels of the anus. Also, the spasm of the internal anal sphincter may cause further reduction of the blood flow to the posterior anal canal.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1290","text":"What is the procedure of c‌hoice in a patient with c​hronic anal fissure?","raw_text":"What is the procedure of choice in a patient with chronic anal fissure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Milligan-Mor‌gan procedure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral internal sphincter‌otomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transanal‌ resection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Altemeier'‌s procedure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lateral internal sphincterotomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lateral internal sphincterotomy</strong></p><p><strong>Lateral internal sphincterotomy</strong> is the procedure of choice for chronic anal fissures.</p>\n<p><strong>Surgical therapy</strong> has traditionally been recommended for <strong>chronic anal fissures</strong> that have failed medical therapy. The aim of this procedure is to decrease the spasms of the internal sphincter by dividing a portion of the muscle. Approximately 30% of the muscle sphincter fibers are divided laterally using an open or closed technique.</p>\n<p>The <strong>initial treatment</strong> of anal fissures includes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Frequent sitz baths</li>\n<li>Analgesics</li>\n<li>Stool softeners</li>\n<li>High-fiber diet</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If conservative management with dietary changes and laxatives fail, other options including <strong>topical analgesics</strong> such as 2% lidocaine jelly, topical nifedipine, topical nitroglycerin, or a combination of topical nifedipine and lidocaine can be attempted.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Miligan-Morgan</strong> procedure is an open hemorrhoidectomy</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Transanal resection</strong> is used to remove early-stage rectal cancers in the lower rectum</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Altermeier's procedure</strong> is used for rectal prolapse</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1334","text":"Which type of anal fistula‌ is seen in this image?","raw_text":"Which type of anal fistula is seen in this image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intersph‌incteric","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Trans-sp‌hincteric","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Supra-sp‌hincteric","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Extra-sp‌hincteric","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Intersphincteric","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/21c6fe5c49b14eaba13842776e167b3b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2f336c43047b407aa79d37f70cd109c8x1280x1456.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Intersphincteric</strong></p><p>In the given image, the fistula traverses the internal sphincter and tracks down the space between the internal and external anal sphincters. Hence, it is an <strong>inter-sphincteric </strong>type of <strong>anal fistula</strong>. It is the <strong>most common</strong> anal fistula.</p>\n<p>A <strong>fistula-in-ano</strong> is an epithelial-lined tract connecting the anal canal to the perianal skin. Patients usually complain of intermittent purulent discharge and pain. Anal fistulas can be simplex or complex fistulas.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Simple fistulas</strong>- include a single tract, subcutaneous tract, and those that involve less than 30% of the external sphincter. They are easier to treat, and have lower chances of recurrence. </li>\n<li><strong>Complex fistulas</strong>- include fistulas involving more than 30% of the external sphincter, with multiple tracts, recurrent fistulas, and those associated with other predisposing factors, such as Crohn disease and radiation treatment.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Fistulas can also be classified into<strong> high or low-lying fistulas</strong>, based on their location with respect to the<strong> puborectalis</strong> muscle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1337","text":"A 40-year-old male patient‌ presents to the clinic w​ith a history of discharg​e from the perianal regio‌n for the past 1 week. On‍ examination, an external​ opening was seen at 2 o'‌clock position around the‌ anal orifice. On per rec‍tal examination, an openi​ng was palpable at 3 o'cl​ock position at the denta​te line. What is the gold‍ standard investigation f​or assessing this patient‌?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old male patient presents to the clinic with a history of discharge from the perianal region for the past 1 week. On examination, an external opening was seen at 2 o'clock position around the anal orifice. On per rectal examination, an opening was palpable at 3 o'clock position at the dentate line. What is the gold standard investigation for assessing this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C‌T","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M‌RI","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Endoan‌al USG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X-ray fi‌stulogram","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. MRI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. MRI</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of<strong> </strong>an<strong> anal fistula. </strong>Magnetic resonance imaging <strong>(MRI)</strong> is the gold standard investigation for fistula imaging. It helps to identify the secondary extensions of the track, which when missed may cause persistence of the fistula.</p>\n<p>Investigations available for fistula-in-ano are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fistulogram</li>\n<li>Endoanal ultrasound</li>\n<li><strong>MRI</strong></li>\n<li>Computed tomography (CT) scan</li>\n<li>Endocoil MRI</li>\n<li>Hydrogen peroxide–enhanced 3-D endoanal ultrasonography</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1338","text":"A patient presented with s‌erous discharge from an o​pening located in the per​ianal region. On examinat‌ion, the location of the ‍external opening was at 8​’O clock position. Accord‌ing to Goodsall's rule at‌ what position from the d‍entate line will the inte​rnal opening be palpated ​in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient presented with serous discharge from an opening located in the perianal region. On examination, the location of the external opening was at 8’O clock position. According to Goodsall's rule at what position from the dentate line will the internal opening be palpated in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8'O ‌clock","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4'O ‌clock","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1'O ‌clock","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6'O ‌clock","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 6'O clock","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe4ca95939104307a4b92d21dcacde75x1280x1602.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 6&#x27;O clock</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of<strong> fistula-in-ano</strong> with external opening at 8’O clock position which implies that the external opening of the fistula is lying posterior to the midline. According to the <strong>Goodsall rule</strong>, fistulas with an external opening posteriorly track in a curvilinear fashion to the posterior midline. Hence, the fistulous tract opens internally at a <strong>6’O clock</strong> position.</p>\n<p>A fistula-in-ano is an abnormal hollow tract or cavity that is lined with granulation tissue and that connects a primary opening inside the anal canal to a secondary opening in the perianal skin.</p>\n<p><strong>Goodsall’s rule:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fistulas with an external opening <strong>anteriorly</strong> connect to the internal opening by a short, <strong>radial tract</strong>.</li>\n<li>Fistulas with an external opening <strong>posteriorly</strong> track in a <strong>curvilinear fashio</strong>n to the posterior midline.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>However, <strong>exceptions</strong> to this rule often occur. Fistulas with an <strong>anterior</strong> external opening <strong>greater than 3 cm</strong> from the anal margin usually track to the <strong>posterior midline</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1339","text":"A known case of Crohn's di‌sease presented to the cl​inic with a history of di​scharge from the anal reg‌ion for the past 2 weeks.‍ The patient underwent dr​ainage of an ischiorectal‌ abscess 2 months back. C‌linical examination and i‍maging reveal the presenc​e of a supra-sphincteric ​perianal fistula. How wil​l you manage this patient‍?","raw_text":"A known case of Crohn's disease presented to the clinic with a history of discharge from the anal region for the past 2 weeks. The patient underwent drainage of an ischiorectal abscess 2 months back. Clinical examination and imaging reveal the presence of a supra-sphincteric perianal fistula. How will you manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seton's ‌technique","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fistu‌lotomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral internal sphincter‌otomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Simple fi‌stulectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Seton's technique","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/367fb8f439874726aa6deeb73c09e476x1280x1469.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f3b183347fe3455e9e3d1fb1a6ba16bax720x720.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Seton&#x27;s technique</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, a history of Crohn's disease, recurrent anorectal abscess, and the imaging findings point towards a <strong>complex anal fistula</strong>. It will be managed using <strong>Seton's placement, </strong>which is done in <strong>two stages. </strong></p>\n<p>A seton is a <strong>drain placed through a fistula</strong> to maintain drainage and/or induce fibrosis. Setons can be classified as <strong>draining or cutting.</strong> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Cutting seton</strong></p>\n<p>A cutting seton has been used as a <strong>staged fistulectomy</strong>, especially in high trans-sphincteric fistulas. After the seton is passed during the primary surgery, it has to be tightened at regular intervals, till it cuts through completely.</p>\n<p><strong>Loose/draining seton</strong></p>\n<p>In this method, the seton is placed to <strong>maintain drainage</strong> and create scar tissue around the sphincter so that its edges do not retract after cutting.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Fistulotomy</strong> involves opening the fistula tract and possibly dividing the sphincter muscle. It is very effective for simple fistulas with minimal sphincter involvement. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Lateral internal sphincterotomy</strong> is a technique used for chronic anal fissures.</p>\n<p>Option D: In <strong>fistulectomy</strong>, the fistulous tract is cut out completely.</p>\n<p>Other surgeries for fistula in-ano include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Endorectal advancement flap</strong>- involves closure of the internal opening of the tract, debridement of the tract, and mobilization of anorectal mucosa to cover the defect. It is usually done for complex fistulas. </li>\n<li><strong>LIFT</strong>- Ligation of the intersphincteric fistula tract involves identification of the internal opening with suture ligation of the intersphincteric portion of the fistula. The tract and infected gland are then excised and the wound is debrided with curettage. It can be done for both simple and complex fistulas.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1292","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cause of ​anorectal suppuration?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of anorectal suppuration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cryptoglandu‌lar infection","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Crohn’s‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malig‌nancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cryptoglandular infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cryptoglandular infection</strong></p><p>The most common cause of anorectal suppuration is <strong>nonspecific cryptoglandular infection.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Anorectal abscess</strong> originates from an infection arising in the cryptoglandular epithelium lining the anal canal. The increased incidence of ischio-rectal fossa infection is due to <strong>p</strong><strong>oor blood supply</strong>.</p>\n<p>It may also be caused by other reasons such as: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Crohn's disease</li>\n<li>Trauma</li>\n<li>Immunodeficiency resulting from human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection or malignancy (both hematologic and anorectal cancer)</li>\n<li>Tuberculosis</li>\n<li>Hidradenitis suppurativa</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4511","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common type of a​norectal abscess?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common type of anorectal abscess?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sub-m‌ucous","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pelvi‌rectal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peri‌anal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ischio‌rectal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Perianal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/239a28a63ad743d2a5eb0582895d31f8x1280x1359.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Perianal</strong></p><p>A <strong>perianal abscess </strong>is the most common type of anorectal abscess.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Anorectal abscess </strong>refers to a collection of pus in the anal or rectal region. More commonly seen in <strong>men. </strong>There are <strong>four types</strong> of anorectal abscesses:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Peri-anal</strong> (most common)</li>\n<li>Ischio-rectal </li>\n<li>Inter-sphincteric</li>\n<li>Supra-levator</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong>: Drainage of pus + Antibiotics</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1341","text":"A jeep driver presented wi‌th a swelling in the nata​l cleft, with pain, diffi​culty in sitting, and occ‌asional purulent discharg‍e from the area. Examinat​ion finding is shown belo‌w. Which of the following‌ is not true regarding th‍is condition?","raw_text":"A jeep driver presented with a swelling in the natal cleft, with pain, difficulty in sitting, and occasional purulent discharge from the area. Examination finding is shown below. Which of the following is not true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recurrence‌ is common","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hair follicles are seen in‌ the wall of sinus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hirsute habitus is a risk ‌factor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Commonly seen between the ‌ages of 15 and 40 years","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hair follicles are seen in the wall of sinus","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/SinusPilonidalis.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hair follicles are seen in the wall of sinus</strong></p><p>The given condition is a <strong>pilonidal sinus</strong>. Hair follicles have almost <strong>never </strong>been demonstrated in the walls of the sinus. The hair projecting from the sinus is dead hair, with its pointed ends directed towards the blind end of the sinus.</p>\n<p>Pilonidal sinus disease is a disease of the inter-gluteal region, characterized by the formation of a <strong>sinus in the cleft of the buttocks</strong>. It is more common in<strong> males </strong>between<strong> 15-40 years </strong>of age.</p>\n<p>Risk factors include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Male</strong> sex</li>\n<li>Family history</li>\n<li>Being overweight or <strong>obese</strong></li>\n<li>Trauma or irritation to the perianal region</li>\n<li>Occupations involving sitting for long periods- <strong>jeep/lorry drivers</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hirsute habitus </strong>- thick body hair</li>\n<li>Poor hygiene</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Sites involved<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Natal cleft</strong> (most commonly)</li>\n<li>Interdigital cleft</li>\n<li>Face</li>\n<li>Axilla</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Buttock <strong>friction</strong> and <strong>shearing forces</strong> in these areas allow broken hairs to enter the skin and create a <strong>subcutaneous, chronically infected</strong> midline track. An infected hair follicle can also form such a track. Secondary tracks can arise as openings, lined with granulation tissue and discharge.</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intermittent <strong>pain, swelling</strong>, and <strong>discharge</strong> at the base of the spine</li>\n<li><strong>Abscess - </strong>due to a foreign-body-type reaction </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Conservative treatment: Strict hygiene and regular shaving of hair in the area.</li>\n<li>Abscess:\n<ul>\n<li>Rest, baths, local antiseptic dressings, and broad-spectrum antibiotics.</li>\n<li>If refractory to the above treatment- drainage with thorough curettage of granulation tissue and hair.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Surgical techniques: <strong>Bascom’s</strong>, Karydaki's procedures are for chronic pilonidal disease.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Recurrence </strong>is common even after adequate excision of the tract.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7098","text":"Which of the following is ‌not used in the treatment​ of pilonidal sinus disea​ses? 1. Z-plasty 2. Limbe‌rg flap 3. Bascom's proce‍dure 4. Incision and drai​nage 5. Surgical approach‌es along the natal cleft","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is not used in the treatment of pilonidal sinus diseases?</p>\n<p>1. Z-plasty <br/>2. Limberg flap<br/>3. Bascom's procedure<br/>4. Incision and drainage <br/>5. Surgical approaches along the natal cleft</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onl‌y 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1 &amp;a‌mp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2,3 &amp;‌amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 &amp;‌amp; 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Only 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/14868fc43126417db084b8e0c9bd5e22x720x495.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Only 5</strong></p><p>Surgical approaches <strong>parallel to the natal cleft</strong>, and not along the natal cleft are preferred, to reduce chances of recurrence. </p>\n<p>Pilonidal sinus disease treatment includes non-surgical and surgical approaches. The <strong>non-surgical approach</strong> includes conservative treatment with proper hygiene and regular shaving of hair in the area. </p>\n<p>In case of an<strong> abscess- </strong>adequate rest, baths, local antiseptic dressings, and broad-spectrum antibiotics are advised.</p>\n<p><strong>Surgical approaches for the management of pilonidal sinus disease include</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Incision and drainage</strong>- for pilonidal abscess refractory to medical treatment</li>\n<li><strong>Bascom's procedure</strong>- is a cleft lift procedure in which hair follicles parallel to the natal cleft are removed under local anesthesia</li>\n<li><strong>Karydakis procedure</strong>- an ovoid area of pathologic tissue is excised parallel to the natal cleft and a fasciocutaneous flap is mobilized to cover the defect</li>\n<li><strong>Limberg/ rhomboid flap</strong>- excises the pilonidal disease in a rhomboid shape and rotates a fasciocutaneous flap for wound closure</li>\n<li>Others- VY advancement flaps, and<strong> Z-plasty</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>The given image shows a<strong> Limberg/rhomboid flap </strong>done in pilonidal sinus surgery:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4515","text":"An elderly female presente‌d with severe persisting ​per rectal itching for th​e past 1 year, despite st‌andard topical therapy. O‍n examination, irregular ​eczematous lesions were s‌een at the anal orifice. ‌Biopsy of the lesion reve‍aled large vacuolated cel​ls containing mucin. Whic​h of the following is it ​likely to be?","raw_text":"An elderly female presented with severe persisting per rectal itching for the past 1 year, despite standard topical therapy. On examination, irregular eczematous lesions were seen at the anal orifice. Biopsy of the lesion revealed large vacuolated cells containing mucin. Which of the following is it likely to be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous cell carcinoma in‌ situ","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basal cell‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intra-epithelial adenocarc‌inoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Marginal anal cell carcino‌ma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Intra-epithelial adenocarcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Intra-epithelial adenocarcinoma</strong></p><p>The<strong> </strong>given history and presence of large vacuolated cells (Paget cells) on biopsy is suggestive of <strong>Paget's disease</strong> of the anal canal. It<strong> </strong>is a rare<strong> intraepithelial adenocarcinoma</strong>. </p>\n<p>Paget’s disease is most commonly seen in areas with a high density of <strong>apocrine sweat glands,</strong><strong> in elderly females. </strong>These can develop into an invasive cancer of the underlying apocrine glands.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include <strong>intractable itching</strong>, burning, bleeding per rectally. 10% of the patients are asymptomatic. On examination, eczematous, well-demarcated plaque, with occasional ulceration and scaling can be seen.</p>\n<p>On histology presence of<strong> Paget cells</strong> (large, vacuolated cells with mucin) confirms the diagnosis. </p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Treatment of choice- <strong>complete excision</strong></li>\n<li>Limited, noninvasive disease-<strong>wide local excision</strong> and closure of the defect with primary sutures or with V-Y advancement flaps</li>\n<li><strong>Invasive component-</strong> radical resection with abdominoperineal resection (APR)</li>\n<li>Medically unfit for surgery - topical 5-FU, imiquimod, cryotherapy, or argon beam laser therapy</li>\n<li><strong>Recurrence</strong>- re-excision (as long as the disease remains noninvasive)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>Most common site of extra-mammary Paget’s disease – <strong>anogenital region</strong> &gt; axilla &gt; eyelid. Perianal Paget’s disease is usually associated with underlying visceral malignancy, most commonly <strong>colorectal adenocarcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4509","text":"Which of the following is ‌true regarding squamous c​ell carcinoma of the anal​ canal?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding squamous cell carcinoma of the anal canal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proctoscopy is the investi‌gation of choice","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Commonly metastasises to s‌pine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most common presentation i‌s ano-rectal pain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Most common type of anal c‌anal carcinoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Most common type of anal canal carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Most common type of anal canal carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Squamous cell carcinoma </strong>(SCC) is the most common anal canal carcinoma. SCC &gt; Basal cell carcinoma &gt;Melanoma</p>\n<p>The median age at diagnosis for SCC is<strong> 60 years</strong>. Patients most commonly present with <strong>bleeding per rectum. </strong>Commonly metastasizes to the <strong>liver </strong>and <strong>lungs. </strong>The most common site of lymph node metastasis is to <strong>inguinal lymph nodes. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Risk Factors:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Human papillomavirus (HPV) (types 16, 18, 31, 33) infection </li>\n<li>Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) or immunosuppression</li>\n<li>Smoking</li>\n<li>Anal receptive intercourse</li>\n<li>Sexual promiscuity</li>\n<li>Anal intraepithelial neoplasia</li>\n<li>History of vulvar or cervical cancer</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis</strong><strong>: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Proctoscopy </strong><strong>with biopsy-</strong> investigation of choice </li>\n<li>MRI- for local staging </li>\n<li>CT scan- to evaluate for distant metastasis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment : </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nigro regimen- </strong>chemoradiation is the <strong>treatment of choice</strong></li>\n<li>If any <strong>residual tumor</strong> is left behind after chemoradiation, <strong>abdominoperineal resection</strong> (APR) is performed</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>8th AJCC: TNM Classification of Carcinoma of the Anal Canal and Anal Margin </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Primary Tumor:</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Tis - high-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion</li>\n<li>T1 - 2 cm or less</li>\n<li>T2 - &gt;2 - 5 cm</li>\n<li>T3 - &gt;5 cm</li>\n<li>T4 - invades adjacent organ, e.g. vagina, urethra, bladder. (invasion of the rectal wall, perirectal skin, subcutaneous tissue, or sphincter muscle is not included as T4).</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Regional Nodes:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Regional lymph nodes include mesorectal, inguinal (superficial, deep), superior rectal (hemorrhoidal), external iliac, and internal iliac (hypogastric) groups.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>N0 - no regional lymph node metastases</li>\n<li>N1a - inguinal, mesorectal, or internal iliac lymph nodes</li>\n<li>N1b - external iliac nodes</li>\n<li>N1c - external iliac nodes + N1a</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Distant Metastases:</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>M0 - no</li>\n<li>M1 - yes</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Stage grouping:</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>0 - Tis</li>\n<li>I - T1 N0</li>\n<li>IIA - T2 N0</li>\n<li>IIB - T3 N0</li>\n<li>IIIA - T1-2 N1</li>\n<li>IIIB - T4 N0</li>\n<li>IIIC - T3-4 N1</li>\n<li>IV - M1</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4513","text":"A 60-year-old man with ble‌eding per rectum is suspe​cted to have squamous cel​l carcinoma of the anal c‌anal. What is the treatme‍nt of choice for this con​dition?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man with bleeding per rectum is suspected to have squamous cell carcinoma of the anal canal. What is the treatment of choice for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abdominoperineal resection‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nigro ‌regimen","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Wide loca‌l excision","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Surgical excision with CO2‌ laser treatment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nigro regimen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nigro regimen</strong></p><p>Treatment of choice for squamous cell carcinoma of the anal canal is <strong>Nigro regimen/ chemoradiation.</strong></p>\n<p>A combination of <strong>5-fluorouracil (5-FU) </strong>with<strong> mitomycin C/ cisplatin</strong> is followed by <strong>external beam radiation</strong> to the <strong>pelvis</strong> with a minimum dose of <strong>45 Gy. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4514","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about melanoma of th​e anal canal?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about melanoma of the anal canal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wide local excision is don‌e for tumors with sphinct​er involvement","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Staged by depth or thickne‌ss of melanoma lesion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"More commo‌n in males","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Good 5-year survival rates‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Staged by depth or thickness of melanoma lesion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Staged by depth or thickness of melanoma lesion</strong></p><p>Anorectal melanoma is <strong>staged by the depth </strong>or <strong>thickness </strong>of the lesion.</p>\n<p><strong>Anal canal melanoma</strong> is more common in <strong>females. </strong>The tumor appears as small and polypoidal or large and ulcerating. Clinical features include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Anorectal bleeding, itching, pain, mass</li>\n<li>Tenesmus, and changes in bowel habits</li>\n<li>Mesorectal lymph node metastases</li>\n<li>Inguinal lymphadenopathy </li>\n<li>Distant spread- to bones, lungs, and liver</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Diagnosis is frequently made <strong>following hemorrhoidectomy </strong>or <strong>local excision </strong>of the perianal mass.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tumors <strong>without anal sphincter</strong> involvement- <strong>wide local excision</strong> with negative margins</li>\n<li>Large tumors <strong>invading sphincters</strong>- <strong>palliative APR </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Regardless of stage, <strong>5-year survival rates </strong>for anorectal melanoma are <strong>very poor</strong>, averaging at about <strong>6%</strong>. The <strong>median survival time </strong>following diagnosis is <strong>12–18 months</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: Most common sites of melanoma are skin &gt;eye &gt; anorectal region</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"voM3xq2w","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"27_spinal_cord","title":"27.Spinal Cord","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA0699","text":"Filum terminale extends up‌to what vertebra?","raw_text":"Filum terminale extends upto what vertebra?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"S1 ve‌rtebra","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"S3 ve‌rtebra","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1st coccyge‌al vertebra","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"4th coccyge‌al vertebra","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1st coccygeal vertebra","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1st coccygeal vertebra</strong></p><p>Filum terminale extends up to the <strong>1st coccygeal vertebra</strong>. It is a filament<strong> </strong>of connective tissue descending from the apex of conus medullaris. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0700","text":"A needle is inserted into ‌a particular space to wit​hdraw CSF in an adult as ​shown below. At which lev‌el does this space termin‍ate?","raw_text":"A needle is inserted into a particular space to withdraw CSF in an adult as shown below. At which level does this space terminate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"S‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"S‌2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"L‌4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"L‌2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. S2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c33e2241c48442c4a9b2f90b50b47f99.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. S2</strong></p><p>The procedure shown in the image is a<strong> lumbar puncture.</strong> In this, the needle is introduced in the <strong>subarachnoid space </strong>which ends at the <strong>lower border of the S2 vertebra.</strong></p>\n<p>A lumbar puncture can be done anywhere from L1 to S2 vertebra. Beyond S2, there are only meninges. However, lumbar puncture is most commonly performed <strong>at the level of L3 or L4</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1756","text":"A 29-year-old woman in lab‌our requested for epidura​l analgesia. Below which ​level would you administe‌r the anaesthesia?","raw_text":"A 29-year-old woman in labour requested for epidural analgesia. Below which level would you administer the anaesthesia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Upper bor‌der of L1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Upper bor‌der of L3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lower bor‌der of L1","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lower bor‌der of L3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lower border of L1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lower border of L1</strong></p><p><strong>Epidural anaesthesia</strong> is given during labour and the placement is usually preferred below L1 to avoid injury to the spinal cord. This is because the <strong>adult spinal cord </strong>terminates at the <strong>lower border </strong>of the<strong> L1 vertebra</strong>.</p>\n<p>In <strong>children</strong>, it usually terminates at the <strong>upper border </strong>of the<strong> L3 vertebra</strong>. </p>\n<p>Note: Coverage from dermatomes T10 to S1 is necessary for epidural analgesia during labor. For a cesarean section, a block from T4 to S1 is needed. However, since the spinal cord ends at the lower border of L1 in most adults, epidural analgesia is given below this level.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0702","text":"What is the total number o‌f nerve pairs in the spin​al cord?","raw_text":"What is the total number of nerve pairs in the spinal cord?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3‌0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3‌3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3‌2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3‌1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 31","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 31</strong></p><p>There are a total of <strong>31 pairs</strong> of <strong>spinal nerves</strong>.</p>\n<p>In contrast, the number of <strong>vertebrae</strong> is <strong>33</strong>. Due to the difference in the number of spinal segments and the number of vertebrae, the spinal segments don't correspond to the respective vertebrae.</p>\n<p>The table below shows the vertebral level corresponding to different regions of the spinal cord:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 409px; height: 416px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 82px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 67px; height: 82px;\"><strong>Regions</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px; height: 82px;\"><strong>Spinal Segment(s)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 82px;\"><strong>Vertebral spine</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px; height: 82px;\"><strong>Spinal segment corresponds to</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 77px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 67px; height: 77px;\">Upper cervical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px; height: 77px;\">C2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 77px;\">C2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px; height: 77px;\">Same level</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 67px; height: 78px;\">Lower cervical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px; height: 78px;\">C6</td>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 78px;\">C5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px; height: 78px;\">One vertebra above</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 67px; height: 78px;\">Upper thoracic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px; height: 78px;\">T5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 78px;\">T3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px; height: 78px;\">Two vertebrae above</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 96px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 67px; height: 96px;\">Lower thoracic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px; height: 96px;\">T10</td>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 96px;\">T7</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px; height: 96px;\">Three vertebrae above</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> The <strong>T11</strong> spine overlies the <strong>L3</strong> spinal segment.</p>\n<p>The <strong>T12</strong> spine overlies the <strong>S1</strong> spinal segment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9311","text":"All of the following are t‌rue about grey rami commu​nicantes except:","raw_text":"All of the following are true about grey rami communicantes except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unmyel‌inated","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Connected to‌ spinal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pregan‌glionic","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Present medial to white ra‌mi communicantes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Preganglionic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6e25b56bd7a44f719f16657175a3d5a2x1280x1459.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Preganglionic</strong></p><p>The<strong> grey rami communicantes </strong>are not preganglionic fibers. They are <strong>postganglionic fibers</strong>.</p>\n<p>The sympathetic trunks on either side of the vertebral columns consist of a series of ganglia. They are connected to the spinal nerves by white and grey rami communicantes. The cell bodies of the <strong>preganglionic sympathetic neurons</strong> are located in the lateral horn in the spinal grey matter. They leave the cord in the ventral nerve roots to form <strong>spinal nerves</strong>. These fibers enter the sympathetic trunk in <strong>white rami communicantes. </strong>They synapse with the <strong>postganglionic sympathetic neurons</strong> in the sympathetic ganglia. The axons of the postganglionic neurons return to the spinal nerve through the <strong>grey rami communicantes. </strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 252px; width: 425px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">Fibers</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px;\"><strong>White rami communicantes</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\"><strong>Grey rami communicantes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">Location</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px;\">Preganglionic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Postganglionic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">Myelination</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px;\">Myelinated</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Unmyelinated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">Principal neurotransmitter</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px;\">Acetylcholine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Noradrenaline</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6865","text":"Which spinal segment lies ‌at the level of the spino​us process of the T7 vert​ebra?","raw_text":"Which spinal segment lies at the level of the spinous process of the T7 vertebra?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T‌7","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T‌8","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T‌9","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T‌10","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. T10","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. T10</strong></p><p>The <strong>T10 spinal segments</strong> lie at the level of the spinous process of the T7 vertebra.</p>\n<p>The table below shows the vertebral level corresponding to different regions of the spinal cord:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 409px; height: 416px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 82px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 67px; height: 82px;\"><strong>Regions</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px; height: 82px;\"><strong>Spinal Segment(s)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 82px;\"><strong>Vertebral spine</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px; height: 82px;\"><strong>Spinal segment corresponds to</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 77px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 67px; height: 77px;\">Upper cervical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px; height: 77px;\">C2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 77px;\">C2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px; height: 77px;\">Same level</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 67px; height: 78px;\">Lower cervical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px; height: 78px;\">C6</td>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 78px;\">C5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px; height: 78px;\">One vertebra above</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 67px; height: 78px;\">Upper thoracic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px; height: 78px;\">T5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 78px;\">T3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px; height: 78px;\">Two vertebrae above</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 96px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 67px; height: 96px;\">Lower thoracic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px; height: 96px;\">T10</td>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; height: 96px;\">T7</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px; height: 96px;\">Three vertebrae above</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The T11 spine overlies the L3 spinal segment.</p>\n<p>The T12 spine overlies the S1 spinal segment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0557","text":"You are given the followin‌g section of the spinal c​ord. The lowest projectio​n of the marked structure‌ lies at which level of t‍he spinal nerves?","raw_text":"You are given the following section of the spinal cord. The lowest projection of the marked structure lies at which level of the spinal nerves?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T9,‌ T10","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T12‌, L1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"S2,‌ S3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"S4,‌ S5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. T12, L1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6146aac7ce0c4da6ad50fa690b11b8bc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0eb716047d03416397b066df778190c2x1280x1072.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. T12, L1</strong></p><p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The structure marked as \"?\" in the above image is <strong>ligamentum denticulatum</strong>. Its </span>lowest projection lies at the level of <strong>T12 and L1 spinal nerves.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0701","text":"All of the following state‌ments regarding ligamentu​m denticulatum are true e​xcept:","raw_text":"All of the following statements regarding ligamentum denticulatum are true except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a modification of ar‌achnoid mater","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"There is one on each side ‌with 21 triangular proces​ses","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It serves as a neurosurgic‌al landmark for hemisecti​oning of spinal cord","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It has a serrated appearan‌ce","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is a modification of arachnoid mater","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5d2992e4358f40f4953d879919666a17x1280x1072.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is a modification of arachnoid mater</strong></p><p><strong>Ligamentum denticulatum</strong> is not a modification of arachnoid mater. It is a <strong>modification of the pia mater</strong>.</p>\n<p>The ligamentum denticulatum is a flat fibrous sheet on either side of the spinal cord present between the ventral and dorsal spinal roots. It serves as a <strong>neurosurgical landmark</strong> for hemisectioning of the spinal cord.</p>\n<p>Its medial border is continuous with the subpial connective tissue. Its lateral border forms a series of <strong>21 triangular processes</strong> on each side connecting it to the dura mater giving it a <strong>serrated appearance</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0704","text":"Angiography in a 62-year-o‌ld man presenting with pa​raplegia revealed a throm​bus in the anterior spina‌l artery. Which region of‍ the spinal cord is likel‌y to be affected?","raw_text":"Angiography in a 62-year-old man presenting with paraplegia revealed a thrombus in the anterior spinal artery. Which region of the spinal cord is likely to be affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Whole segment of spinal co‌rd","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior 1/3rd of the spin‌al cord","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior 2/3rd of the spin‌al cord","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior 3/4th of the spin‌al cord","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anterior 2/3rd of the spinal cord","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anterior 2/3rd of the spinal cord</strong></p><p><strong>Thrombosis</strong> of the <strong>anterior spinal artery</strong> affects the <strong>anterior 2/3rd</strong> of the spinal cord.</p>\n<p>The spinal cord is supplied by the longitudinal arteries and segmental arteries.</p>\n<p>The longitudinal arteries are:</p>\n<p><strong>Anterior spinal artery </strong>supplies anterior 2/3rd of the cord. Occlusion of the anterior spinal artery causes-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bilateral<strong> paraplegia</strong> below the level of lesion due to damage to bilateral <strong>corticospinal tracts.</strong></li>\n<li>Bilateral <strong>thermoanesthesia</strong> and <strong>analgesia</strong> below the level of lesion due to damage to bilateral <strong>spinothalamic tracts.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Weakness of limb muscles</strong> due to damage to bilateral <strong>anterior grey horns</strong> in cervical or lumbar regions of the cord.</li>\n<li><strong>Loss of bowel and bladder functions</strong> due to damage to the <strong>descending autonomic tracts.</strong></li>\n<li>Sense of position, vibration, and fine touch are intact as the <strong>posterior white column is unaffected</strong>, which is supplied by the posterior spinal artery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Posterior spinal arteries </strong>supplies posterior 1/3rd of the cord. Occlusion of the posterior spinal artery causes-</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Loss of sensation</strong> of <strong>position, vibration</strong> and <strong>fine touch</strong> due to damage to the bilateral <strong>posterior white columns.</strong></li>\n<li>The corticospinal tracts, spinothalamic tracts, anterior grey horns, and descending autonomic tracts are unaffected, as they are supplied by the anterior spinal artery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>segmental arteries</strong> are branches of arteries outside the vertebral column.These are branches of-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Deep cervical artery</li>\n<li>Intercostal artery</li>\n<li>Lumbar arteries</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0705","text":"A 64-year-old man was unde‌rgoing aortic bypass surg​ery. During the procedure​, the surgeon accidentall‌y injured an artery arisi‍ng from the aorta. This a‌rtery provides major bloo‍d supply to the spinal co​rd on the left side at th‌e lower thoracic level. W​hich region is likely to ​be affected?","raw_text":"A 64-year-old man was undergoing aortic bypass surgery. During the procedure, the surgeon accidentally injured an artery arising from the aorta. This artery provides major blood supply to the spinal cord on the left side at the lower thoracic level. Which region is likely to be affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Upper two thirds of the sp‌inal cord","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lower two thirds of the sp‌inal cord","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Upper one third of the spi‌nal cord","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lower one third of the spi‌nal cord","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lower two thirds of the spinal cord","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c31cf60a6ef64574856717c7de4b2e01x1280x1597.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lower two thirds of the spinal cord</strong></p><p>The <strong>artery of Adamkiewicz </strong>supplies the<strong> lower two-thirds of the spinal cord. </strong>It is <strong>unilateral</strong> and in majority of the people, enters the spinal cord from the <strong>left side. </strong></p>\n<p>The anterior spinal artery (ASA) is augmented by numerous radicular arteries. The most prominent of these is the artery of Adamkiewicz. It originates from the <strong>aorta</strong> in the <strong>lower thoracic</strong> or <strong>upper lumbar</strong> vertebral levels and has the following course:</p>\n<p>Aorta - posterior intercostal artery - radicular artery - artery of Adamkiewicz - anastomosis with the anterior spinal artery(ASA).</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical significance:</strong></p>\n<p>It is a major source of blood supply for the lower 2/3rd of the cord. Damage to this artery can lead to <strong>paraplegia</strong>. Ischemia of the anterior segment is associated with the <strong>aortic bypass procedure.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1812","text":"Identify the tract marked ‌\"A\" in the given section ​of the spinal cord.","raw_text":"Identify the tract marked \"A\" in the given section of the spinal cord.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lateral reticulospinal tra‌ct","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral Corticospinal trac‌t","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral spinothalamic trac‌t","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rubrospi‌nal tract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lateral reticulospinal tract","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bb67b7b1fae94694a6c109d4e593c88f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lateral reticulospinal tract</strong></p><p>The tract marked \"A\" in the image is the <strong>lateral reticulospinal tract.</strong></p>\n<p>They originate from the reticular formation of the brain and terminate on the <strong>α and γ motor neurons</strong> in the spinal cord. They inhibit or facilitate voluntary movement.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0706","text":"A 58-year-old man presente‌d with loss of sensation ​below the level of the um​bilicus. MRI revealed inf‌arction affecting the pos‍terior white columns in t‌he T-10 segment of the sp‍inal cord. Which sensatio​n would be intact in this‌ patient?","raw_text":"A 58-year-old man presented with loss of sensation below the level of the umbilicus. MRI revealed infarction affecting the posterior white columns in the T-10 segment of the spinal cord. Which sensation would be intact in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Positio‌n Sense","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pa‌in","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pres‌sure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vibr‌ation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pain</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the pain sensation will be intact because the <strong>posterior white column</strong> (<strong>dorsal column) </strong>doesn't carry the <strong>pain sensation. Pain</strong> sensation is carried by the <strong>anterolateral column.<br/></strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Pressure sensation related to a finer degree of <strong>pressure intensity judgment</strong> is carried by the <strong>dorsal column</strong>. On the other hand, relatively <strong>crude</strong> sensations of touch and pressure are carried by the <strong>anterolateral system</strong>.</p>\n<p>Options B and D: Position sense and vibration sensation will be affected in this case as they are carried by the dorsal column.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong> </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1833","text":"Which of the following des‌cending tracts in the spi​nal cord remains uncrosse​d?","raw_text":"Which of the following descending tracts in the spinal cord remains uncrossed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tectospi‌nal Tract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rubrospi‌nal Tract","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vestibulos‌pinal Tract","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pyramid‌al Tract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vestibulospinal Tract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vestibulospinal Tract</strong></p><p>The <strong>vestibulospinal tract</strong> remains uncrossed through the medulla and through the length of the spinal cord in the anterior white column.</p>\n<p><strong>Ascending tracts</strong> - All tracts decussate except dorsal and rostral spinocerebellar tracts.</p>\n<p><strong>Descending tracts </strong>- All tracts decussate except reticulospinal and vestibulospinal tracts</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0708","text":"A woman presented with hem‌iparesis due to a spinal ​cord tumour. The doctor d​iagnosed this as a case o‌f Brown- Séquard syndrome‍. All are true about this‌ syndrome except:","raw_text":"A woman presented with hemiparesis due to a spinal cord tumour. The doctor diagnosed this as a case of Brown- Séquard syndrome. All are true about this syndrome except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemisection of Spinal cord‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ipsilateral loss of vibrat‌ion sensations","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ipsilateral loss of crude ‌touch sensations","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ipsilateral paralysis belo‌w the level of lesion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ipsilateral loss of crude touch sensations","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ipsilateral loss of crude touch sensations</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Brown- Séquard syndrome</strong>. <strong>Ipsilateral loss of crude touch sensation</strong> is <strong>not seen</strong> in this syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Brown- Séquard syndrome</strong> is<strong> </strong>the<strong> hemisection of the spinal</strong> <strong>cord. </strong>This causes damage to ascending sensory (dorsal column pathway, spinothalamic tract) and descending motor (corticospinal tract) pathways. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The damage to the <strong>dorsal column</strong> pathway leads to<strong> ipsilateral loss of fine touch, vibration, and proprioception</strong> below the level of the lesion.</li>\n<li>The damage to the <strong>spinothalamic tract</strong> leads to <strong>contra­lateral loss</strong> of pain and temperature sensation beginning <strong>one or two segments below</strong> the lesion.</li>\n<li>The damage to the <strong>cor­ticospinal tract</strong> produces <strong>ipsilateral paralysis </strong>of the body.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0558","text":"A lady with a history of l‌umbar back pain presented​ with a loss of sensation​ in the perianal area. An‌ urgent MRI showed compre‍ssion of the cauda equina‌ by a herniated disc. The‍ conus syndrome differs f​rom this syndrome in that‌ the former shows:","raw_text":"A lady with a history of lumbar back pain presented with a loss of sensation in the perianal area. An urgent MRI showed compression of the cauda equina by a herniated disc. The conus syndrome differs from this syndrome in that the former shows:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radicul‌ar pain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lower motor ‌neuron injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Symmetrical weakness of lo‌wer limbs","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Urinary ‌retention","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Symmetrical weakness of lower limbs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Symmetrical weakness of lower limbs</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>cauda equina syndrome</strong>. The <strong>conus medullaris syndrome</strong> typically shows the <strong>symmetrical </strong>weakness of lower limbs, whereas the <strong>cauda equina syndrome</strong>, presents with <strong>asymmetrical</strong> weakness of lower limbs.</p>\n<p>Radicular pain, lower motor neuron injury, and urinary retention are common to both conus medullaris and cauda equina syndrome. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"JpXFwmdN","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"27_regulation_of_gene_expression","title":"27.Regulation Of Gene Expression","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB2770","text":"All of the following are e​xamples of housekeeping g‌enes except ______","raw_text":"All of the following are examples of housekeeping genes except ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ribosomal p​rotein genes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"rRNA ​genes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Beta gala​ctosidase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"RNA pol​ymerase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Beta galactosidase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Beta galactosidase</strong></p><p>The gene expressing <strong>beta-galactosidase </strong>is not a <strong>housekeeping gene</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Housekeeping </strong>or<strong> constitutive genes </strong>are those which are expressed at a constant rate in almost all the cells of the body. These genes are required for the basal cellular function. Examples include genes encoding <strong>RNA polymerase, ribosomal protein</strong>, <strong>rRNA,</strong> enzymes for <strong>glycolysis</strong>, etc.</p>\n<p>In contrast,<strong> inducible genes</strong> are those which are expressed under special circumstances. The expression of these genes increase in response to an <strong>activator</strong> or<strong> inducer</strong>, or decrease in response to a<strong> repressor</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2776","text":"Which protein is produced ​by a regulatory gene?","raw_text":"Which protein is produced by a regulatory gene?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ope​ron","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ind​ucer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prom​oter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Repr​essor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Repressor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9e86086e09284a7eaa24d79a49175a8fx719x599.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Repressor</strong></p><p>A <strong>repressor protein</strong> is produced by a<strong> regulatory gene</strong>.</p>\n<p>In bacterial operons, a <strong>regulatory gene</strong>,<strong> </strong>like the <strong>lac</strong> <strong>I</strong> gene, which is present upstream of a set of structural genes, <strong>codes </strong>for a <strong>repressor protein</strong> (a transacting factor). The repressor protein binds to the <strong>operator </strong>site and interferes with the progress of RNA polymerase. This blocks transcription of the structural genes and is an example of <strong>negative regulation </strong>of gene transcription.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2772","text":"What does the operon model​ attempt to explain?","raw_text":"What does the operon model attempt to explain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The coordinated control of​ gene expression in bacte‌ria","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bacterial resistance to an​tibiotics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"How genes move between hom​ologous regions of DNA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The mechanism of viral att​achment to a host cell","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The coordinated control of gene expression in bacteria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d340ad619eda46a2972a6d8db40b4330x719x599.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The coordinated control of gene expression in bacteria</strong></p><p>The <strong>operon model</strong> attempts to explain the<strong> coordinated control </strong>of<strong> gene expression </strong>in bacteria.</p>\n<p>In bacteria, the <strong>structural genes</strong> that code for proteins involved in a particular metabolic pathway are often found <strong>sequentially</strong> <strong>grouped</strong> on the <strong>chromosome. </strong>The cis-acting <strong>regulatory elements</strong> that determine the transcription of these genes are also grouped with the structural genes. </p>\n<p>The transcription product of the entire operon is a single <strong>polycistronic messenger RNA (mRNA). </strong>The structural genes described above are coordinately controlled, that is, turned on or off as a unit. This entire package, of structural and regulatory genes, is referred to as an <strong>operon</strong>. An example of this is the <strong>lac</strong> operon in <em>Escherichia coli.</em></p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Gene regulation</strong> in <strong>bacteria </strong>is important in order to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Enable <strong>response </strong>to<strong> environmental stimuli</strong></li>\n<li>Allow cells to <strong>adjust</strong> promptly to <strong>changes</strong> in the <strong>growth medium</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Alter biochemical capabilities</strong> appropriate<strong> </strong>for<strong> </strong>available <strong>substrate</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2773","text":"In the Lac operon model, h​ow does the presence of l‌actose activate transcrip‌tion?","raw_text":"In the Lac operon model, how does the presence of lactose activate transcription?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lactose directly activates​ RNA polymerase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lactose binds to the opera​tor region and blocks the‌ attachment of RNA polyme‌rase to the promoter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lactose binds directly to ​the promoter region and a‌ctivates transcription","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lactose binds to repressor​ protein and inactivates ‌it","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lactose binds to repressor protein and inactivates it","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lactose binds to repressor protein and inactivates it</strong></p><p>In the Lac operon model,<strong> lactose </strong>binds to the <strong>repressor protein </strong>and <strong>inactivates </strong>it, thus <strong>activating transcription. </strong></p>\n<p>When lactose is present in abundance, a small amount of lactose is converted to <strong>allolactose</strong>. This compound binds to the repressor protein of the lac operon and creates a <strong>conformational change</strong>. As a result, the repressor cannot bind to the operator and transcription of the structural genes occurs. This is an example of <strong>negative regulation</strong>.</p>\n<p>This can only occur when <strong>glucose</strong> is <strong>absent</strong>. In the absence of glucose, <strong>adenylyl cyclase</strong> is active. This generates sufficient quantities of <strong>cAMP</strong> which bind to <strong>catabolite activator protein </strong>(CAP).The <strong>cAMP–CAP</strong> trans-acting complex binds to the <strong>CAP-binding site</strong>, another regulatory region present upstream of the structural genes of the operon. This causes RNA polymerase to more efficiently initiate transcription at the promoter site. This is an example of <strong>positive regulation</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2779","text":"The lactose operon is like​ly to be transcribed when‌ ______","raw_text":"The lactose operon is likely to be transcribed when ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"There is more glucose in t​he cell than lactose","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cyclic AMP l​evels are low","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"There is glucose but no la​ctose in the cell","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cyclic AMP and lactose lev​els are both high within ‌the cell","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cyclic AMP and lactose levels are both high within the cell","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d6336c3c3fd44b9590451a9c80459952x719x599.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cyclic AMP and lactose levels are both high within the cell</strong></p><p>The lac operon is induced only when <strong>cyclic AMP </strong>and <strong>lactose levels </strong>are<strong> both high </strong>within the cell.</p>\n<p>When lactose is present in abundance, a small amount of lactose is converted to <strong>allolactose.</strong> This compound<strong> binds</strong> to the <strong>repressor protein</strong> of the lac operon and creates a <strong>conformational change</strong>. As a result, the repressor cannot bind to the operator and <strong>transcription</strong> of the structural genes occurs. This is an example of <strong>negative regulation</strong>.</p>\n<p>This can only occur when <strong>glucose</strong> is <strong>absent</strong>. In the absence of glucose, <strong>adenylyl cyclase </strong>is active. This generates sufficient quantities of<strong> cAMP</strong> which bind to <strong>catabolite activator protein</strong> (CAP).The <strong>cAMP–CAP trans-acting complex</strong> binds to the <strong>CAP-binding site</strong>, another regulatory region present upstream of the structural genes of the operon. This causes RNA polymerase to more efficiently initiate transcription at the promoter site. This is an example of<strong> positive regulation</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2777","text":"A mutation in which sectio​n of DNA could influence ‌the binding of RNA polyme‌rase to the DNA?","raw_text":"A mutation in which section of DNA could influence the binding of RNA polymerase to the DNA?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ope​ron","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ind​ucer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prom​oter","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Corep​ressor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Promoter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Promoter</strong></p><p><strong>A mutation </strong>in the <strong>promoter</strong> <strong>section</strong> of DNA could influence the <strong>binding</strong> of<strong> RNA polymerase </strong>to the DNA.</p>\n<p>The <strong>regulatory portion </strong>of bacterial operon is upstream of its structural genes, and consists of the <strong>promoter </strong>(P) region where <strong>RNA polymerase binds</strong> and two additional sites, the <strong>operator</strong> (O) site and the <strong>CAP</strong> site, where<strong> regulatory proteins </strong>bind. </p>\n<p><strong>Corepressor</strong> is a substance that facilitate the<strong> inhibition</strong> of <strong>gene expression</strong>. In prokaryotes, corepressors are small molecules whereas, in eukaryotes, corepressors are proteins. A corepressor binds to the repressors and activates the repressor transcription factor. The repressor, in turn, binds to the <strong>operator site</strong>, thereby blocking the transcription from the structural genes. A <strong>lack</strong> of <strong>corepressor</strong> would result in the<strong> inability</strong> of the cell to <strong>ʺturn offʺ genes.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2775","text":"The tryptophan operon is a​ repressible operon that ‌is _____","raw_text":"The tryptophan operon is a repressible operon that is _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Permanentl​y turned on","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Turned on only when trypto​phan is present in the gr‌owth medium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Turned off only when gluco​se is present in the grow‌th medium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Turned off whenever trypto​phan is added to the grow‌th medium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Turned off whenever tryptophan is added to the growth medium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fdbe4447819e48ee986a1f1ad88d6c57x800x523.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Turned off whenever tryptophan is added to the growth medium</strong></p><p>The <strong>tryptophan operon</strong> is a <strong>repressible operon</strong> that is <strong>turned off </strong>whenever<strong> tryptophan</strong> is added to the growth medium.</p>\n<p>The tryptophan operon contains five structural genes: trp E, trp D, trp C, trp B, trp A, which encode enzymes required for the synthesis of tryptophan. It also contains a <strong>promoter</strong> that binds to RNA polymerase. Its <strong>operator</strong> blocks transcription when it is bound to <strong>repressor protein</strong> synthesized by the <strong>repressor gene </strong>(trp R). In this, <strong>tryptophan</strong> is a<strong> corepressor</strong> i.e. it facilitates binding of the repressor protein to the operator. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2780","text":"Two potential devices that​ eukaryotic cells use to ‌regulate transcription ar‌e _______","raw_text":"Two potential devices that eukaryotic cells use to regulate transcription are _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DNA methylation and histon​e amplification","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"DNA amplification and hist​one methylation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DNA acetylation and methyl​ation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"DNA methylation and histon​e acetylation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. DNA methylation and histone acetylation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a5e2caab31444ddda44e54b175ad2c72x993x932.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. DNA methylation and histone acetylation</strong></p><p>Two potential devices that eukaryotic cells use to<strong> regulate transcription</strong> are as follows <strong>DNA methylation</strong> and <strong>histone acetylation.</strong></p>\n<p>DNA methylation occurs by <strong>methyltransferases</strong> that use S-adenosyl-methionine as the methyl donor. It <strong>silences gene expression</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Histone acetylation</strong> decrease the positive charge on histone proteins, thus decreasing the strength of their association with negatively charged DNA. This chromatin remodeling <strong>relaxes</strong> the <strong>nucleosome</strong>, allowing transcription factors access to specific regions on the DNA. Thus, histone acetylation <strong>improves gene expression</strong>. </p>\n<p>Note: In both <strong>eukaryotes </strong>and <strong>prokaryotes, </strong>gene expression is primarily regulated at the<strong> </strong>level of<strong> transcription.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2783","text":"The phenomenon in which RN​A molecules in a cell are‌ destroyed if they have a‌ sequence complementary t‍o an introduced double-st​randed RNA is called ____‌_","raw_text":"The phenomenon in which RNA molecules in a cell are destroyed if they have a sequence complementary to an introduced double-stranded RNA is called _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"RNA inte​rference","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"RNA obs​truction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"RNA bl​ocking","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"RNA ta​rgeting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. RNA interference","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5c374b65e5454778b841fa8701abff89x1280x2085.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. RNA interference</strong></p><p>The <strong>phenomenon</strong> in which <strong>RNA molecules</strong> in a cell are<strong> destroyed</strong> if they have a sequence complementary to an introduced <strong>double-stranded RNA</strong> is called <strong>RNA interference.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>RNA interference </strong>is a mechanism of gene silencing through decreased expression of mRNA, either by <strong>repression </strong>of<strong> translation </strong>or by<strong> increased degradation. </strong>It is thought to play a key role in such a fundamental process as <strong>cell proliferation, differentiation</strong>, and<strong> apoptosis</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2784","text":"Which of the following des​cribes the function of an‌ enzyme known as dicer?","raw_text":"Which of the following describes the function of an enzyme known as dicer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It degrades single-strande​d DNA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It degrades single-strande​d mRNA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It degrades mRNA with no p​oly(A) tail","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It trims small double-stra​nded RNAs into molecules ‌that can block translatio‌n","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It trims small double-stranded RNAs into molecules that can block translation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It trims small double-stranded RNAs into molecules that can block translation</strong></p><p><strong>Dicer</strong> is an <strong>endonuclease</strong> that <strong>trims small double-stranded RNA</strong> into molecules that can <strong>block translation.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Dicer</strong> is an enzyme that cleaves the cytosolic dsRNA encoded by the genome and generates short, double-stranded (ds) miRNA. One of the two strands (the guide or antisense strand) selected for loading into the <strong>RNA- induced silencing complex</strong> (RISC)<strong> </strong>which is composed of one of four<strong> argonaute proteins (Ago 1→4)</strong>. The RNA then forms a mature, functional 21–22 nt <strong>single-stranded miRNA</strong>.</p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">In the </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">RISC</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> complex, </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">miRNA</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">s can modulate mRNA function by one of three mechanisms:</span></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 401\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>(1) Promoting mRNA degradation directly</p>\n<p>(2) Stimulating CCR4/NOT complex-mediated poly(A) tail degradation</p>\n<p>(3) Inhibition of translation by targeting the 5′-methyl cap-binding translation factor</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5231","text":"Which of the following doe​s not favor euchromatin f‌ormation due to changes o‌ccurring at cytosine resi‍dues at CpG islands in DN​A?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not favor euchromatin formation due to changes occurring at cytosine residues at CpG islands in DNA?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methy​lation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Phospho​rylation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alkyl​ation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sumoy​lation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Methylation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Methylation</strong></p><p><strong>Methylation</strong> does not favour euchromatin formation due to changes cytosine residues at CpG islands in DNA.</p>\n<p>The transcriptional regulatory regions of <strong>active genes </strong>often contain a high frequency of <strong>CpG dinucleotides</strong> (referred to as CpG islands) and they are <strong>normally unmethylated</strong>. Therefore, methylation is an <strong>epigenetic modification </strong>that inactivates DNA, and hinders the formation of euchromatin (transcriptionally active chromatin). Epigenetic changes include alterations of chromatin structure by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Methylation of cytosine residues in CpG dinucleotides</li>\n<li>Modification of histones by acetylation or methylation</li>\n<li>Changes in higher-order chromosome structure</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sumoylation</strong> of<strong> histones</strong> (SUMO - Small Ubiquitin-related MOdifier) is associated with transcription <strong>repression</strong>. Other possible roles of posttranslationally modified histones:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acetylation </strong>of <strong>core histones</strong> is associated with<strong> chromosomal assembly</strong> during DNA replication.</li>\n<li><strong>Phosphorylation</strong> of histone <strong>H1</strong> is associated with the <strong>condensation </strong>of <strong>chromosomes</strong> during the replication cycle.</li>\n<li><strong>ADP-ribosylation</strong> of histones is associated with <strong>DNA repair. </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Methylation</strong> of histones is correlated with activation and repression of gene transcription.</li>\n<li><strong>Acetylation</strong> of histones <strong>H3 </strong>and<strong> H4</strong> is associated with the activation or inactivation of gene transcription.</li>\n<li><strong>Monoubiquitylation</strong> is associated with gene activation, repression, and heterochromatic gene silencing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6804","text":"DNA methylation is not inv​olved in? Chromatin remod‌elling RNA splicing Gene ‌silencing Base pair excis‍ion","raw_text":"<p>DNA methylation is not involved in?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Chromatin remodelling</li>\n<li>RNA splicing</li>\n<li>Gene silencing</li>\n<li>Base pair excision</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a​nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a​nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 a​nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 a​nd 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/794acb4378e745a29791d50345317df0x993x932.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2 and 4</strong></p><p>DNA methylation is <strong>not involved</strong> in the<strong> base pair excision repair</strong> mechanism (statement 4) and <strong>RNA splicing </strong>(statement 2). </p>\n<p>DNA methylation is an <strong>epigenetic modification </strong>used as a gene expression regulation mechanism. It causes <strong>gene silencing </strong>by inactivating DNA and hindering the formation of euchromatin (transcriptionally active chromatin). Thus, it also aids in <strong>chromatin remodelling.</strong> </p>\n<p>It however is not involved in the base pair repair mechanism or RNA splicing. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"5-xJHq7e","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"27_demography_ii__demographic_indicators","title":"27.Demography Ii  Demographic Indicators","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MC3612","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a vital statistic?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a vital statistic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Natural g‌rowth rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fertili‌ty rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sex ‌ratio","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Life expecta‌ncy at birth","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sex ratio","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sex ratio</strong></p><p><strong>Sex ratio</strong> is not a vital statistic, it is a <strong>population statistic.</strong></p>\n<p>Demographic indicators have been divided into 2 parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Population statistics </strong>include -\n<ul>\n<li>Population size</li>\n<li>Sex ratio</li>\n<li>Density</li>\n<li>Dependency ratio</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Vital statistics</strong> include -\n<ul>\n<li>Birth rate</li>\n<li>Death rate</li>\n<li><strong>Natural growth rate</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Life expectancy at birth</strong></li>\n<li>Mortality rate</li>\n<li><strong>Fertility rate</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8149","text":"While conducting a demogra‌phic study, you need to c‍alculate the total depend‍ency ratio of the study p‌opulation. Which of the f​ollowing will you include‌ in the numerator while c​alculating this?","raw_text":"While conducting a demographic study, you need to calculate the total dependency ratio of the study population. Which of the following will you include in the numerator while calculating this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Population under 10 years ‌and above 60 years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Population under 15 years ‌and above 60 years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Population under 10 years ‌and above 65 years","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Population under 15 years ‌and above 65 years","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Population under 15 years and above 65 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f09e0686619c4c3da1b9afdd2a34c8b0x1280x668.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Population under 15 years and above 65 years</strong></p><p>Population<strong> under 15 years </strong>and<strong> above 65 years </strong>are part of<strong> </strong>the <strong>numerator</strong> used to calculate the <strong>total dependency ratio</strong>. This is because this population is considered the <strong>dependant population</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The dependant population includes children below 15 years of age and adults above 65 years of age. The total dependency ratio is the ratio of the combined dependent population to those in the age group of 15 - 65 years. It is also called the <strong>societal dependency ratio.</strong> It can also be <strong>divided into</strong> the <strong>young-age</strong> and <strong>old-age dependency ratios</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: As of mid-year 2020, the dependency ratio of <strong>India </strong>is <strong>48.7%.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8153","text":"While studying a community‌, the researcher found 30‍% of the population to be‍ below 15 years of age, a‌nd 10% was over 65 years ​of age. What is the total‌ dependency ratio of this​ population?","raw_text":"While studying a community, the researcher found 30% of the population to be below 15 years of age, and 10% was over 65 years of age. What is the total dependency ratio of this population?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20‌.4%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"66‌.6%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"5‌0%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 66.6%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/39ac4b0ebc2e4df68482fae477fb0a91x1280x668.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 66.6%</strong></p><p>The total dependency ratio in this population is <strong>66.6%</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>This can be calculated as follows.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Here, the total population below 15 years of age is = 30%</p>\n<p>Total population above 65 years of age = 10%</p>\n<p>So, total population between 15-65 years of age = 100 - (30 + 10) = 60%</p>\n<p>Hence, dependency ratio in this population = 100* (30% + 10%)/60% = 66.6%</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8154","text":"What is potential support ‌ratio?","raw_text":"What is potential support ratio?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Number of persons aged 15 ‌to 64 per child below 15 ‍years or less","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Number of persons aged 15 ‌to 64 per older person ag‍ed 65 or more","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Number of persons aged 15 ‌to 64 per each older pers‍on aged &amp;gt;65 and younge‍r person aged &amp;lt; 15","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Number of persons aged 15 ‌to 64 per each older pers‍on aged &amp;gt; 60 and young‍er person aged &amp;lt;15","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Number of persons aged 15 to 64 per older person aged 65 or more","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Number of persons aged 15 to 64 per older person aged 65 or more</strong></p><p>The <strong>potential support ratio</strong> is the number of <strong>persons aged 15 to 64</strong> <strong>per</strong> one older <strong>person</strong> aged <strong>65 or more</strong>. It is the inverse of the old-age dependency ratio.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3594","text":"Which of the following is ‌the major contributor to ‍demographic burden?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the major contributor to demographic burden?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased potential suppor‌t ratio","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased young age depend‌ency ratio","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased fe‌rtility rates","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased old age dependen‌cy ratio","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Increased old age dependency ratio","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Increased old age dependency ratio</strong></p><p><strong>Demographic burden</strong> refers to an <strong>increase</strong> in the<strong> total dependency ratio.</strong> It is mostly caused by<strong> </strong>an <strong>increased old-age dependency ratio.</strong></p>\n<p>The term <strong>demographic bonus</strong> denotes the <strong>decline</strong> in <strong>dependency ratio</strong> due to declining fertility. A decline in dependency ratio especially the <strong>young-age dependency ratio</strong> has been identified to be a key factor in the underlying <strong>rapid economic development.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>potential support ratio</strong> is the number of persons aged 15 to 64 per one older person aged 65 or more. This is the <strong>inverse </strong>of the <strong>old-age dependency ratio.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8140","text":"Which of the following det‌ermines the carrying capa‍city of a population?","raw_text":"Which of the following determines the carrying capacity of a population?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Population ‌growth rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Birth‌ rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fertili‌ty rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Limiting‌ resource","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Limiting resource","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Limiting resource</strong></p><p>The <strong>carrying capacity </strong>is the <strong>number of</strong> <strong>individuals</strong> of a population that can be <strong>supported</strong> for a period of time given the resources of that area. Thus, the carrying capacity is <strong>determined</strong> by the <strong>limiting resources</strong> of an area.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8162","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about crude birth r‍ate?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about crude birth rate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Simplest measure of fertil‌ity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Unsatisfac‌tory usage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Age ind‌ependent","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Numerator includes still b‌irths","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Numerator includes still births","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Numerator includes still births</strong></p><p>The numerator of <strong>crude birth rate</strong> only includes<strong> live births</strong> and does not include stillbirths.</p>\n<p>Crude birth rate = No. of live births during the year x 1000/ Estimated mid-year population</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3610","text":"As part of the national ma‌ternity benefit scheme, y‍ou have been tasked with ‍conducting a study in a c‌ommunity of 5000 people. ​What is the expected numb‌er of pregnant women in t​his community if the crud​e birth rate is 30 per 10​00 people?","raw_text":"As part of the national maternity benefit scheme, you have been tasked with conducting a study in a community of 5000 people.  What is the expected number of pregnant women in this community if the crude birth rate is 30 per 1000 people?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌75","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌50","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‌65","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1‌45","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 165","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 165</strong></p><p>The expected number of pregnant females in the given community would be <strong>165.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Step 1</strong>: In the given question, the <strong>crude birth rate</strong> is <strong>30</strong> per 1000 people i.e 30 children are born from pregnant mothers for every 1000 people. Therefore there are 30 pregnant females per 1000 population.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2</strong>: Since the<strong> total population</strong> is <strong>5000</strong>, the total no of pregnant females = 5000 x 30/1000 = 150 </p>\n<p><strong>Step 3</strong>: Since some pregnancies may not result in live births (abortions and stillbirths), a <strong>correction factor</strong> of <strong>10%</strong> should be added to avoid the underestimation of the number of pregnancies. <strong>Pregnancy wastage</strong> (i.e 10% of total live births) =10 x 150/100=15.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 4</strong>: <strong>Total pregnant females</strong> = total live births + pregnancy wastage = 150+15=<strong>165.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8109","text":"According to NFHS-5, what ‌is the sex ratio in India‍?","raw_text":"According to NFHS-5, what is the sex ratio in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1037 females per 1000 male‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"985 females per 1000 males‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"940 females per 1000 males‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1020 females per 1000 male‌s","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1020 females per 1000 males","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1020 females per 1000 males</strong></p><p>According to the NFHS-5 (2019-21), the <strong>sex ratio</strong> in<strong> India</strong> is <strong>1020</strong> <strong>females per 1000 males</strong>. It has increased from the 2011 Census which had found a sex ratio of 940 females per 1000 males.</p>\n<p>The sex ratio at birth is 929 females per 1000 males.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8110","text":"For a population of 10,000‌, what does a sex ratio o‍f more than 1000 mean?","raw_text":"For a population of 10,000, what does a sex ratio of more than 1000 mean?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Males are l‌ess than 500","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Females are ‌less than 500","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Males are le‌ss than 5000","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Females are less than 5000‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Males are less than 5000","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Males are less than 5000</strong></p><p>For a <strong>population </strong>of<strong> 10000</strong>, if the <strong>sex ratio</strong> is <strong>more than 1000</strong>, it means <strong>males</strong> are <strong>less than 5000</strong> i.e., less than half the population.</p>\n<p>The<strong> sex ratio</strong> refers to the number of <strong>females per 1000 males</strong>. An <strong>ideal sex ratio</strong> would be <strong>1000, </strong>indicating the population comprises an <strong>equal number</strong> of <strong>males and females</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Sex ratio &gt;1000</strong> indicates that there are<strong> more females than males</strong> in a population. <strong>Sex ratio &lt;1000</strong> indicates that there are<strong> fewer females</strong> compared to males in the population.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3613","text":"What is the definition of ‌the net reproduction rate‍?","raw_text":"What is the definition of the net reproduction rate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No. of children that would‌ be born to married women‍ assuming fixed age speci‍fic fertility and mortali‌ty rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"No. of girls that would be‌ born to married women as‍suming fixed age specific‍ fertility and mortality ‌rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"No. of female children a n‌ew born girl will bear du‍ring her lifetime assumin‍g fixed age specific fert‌ility and mortality rate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"No. of children a new born‌ girl will bear during he‍r lifetime assuming fixed‍ age specific fertility a‌nd mortality rate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. No. of female children a new born girl will bear during her lifetime assuming fixed age specific fertility and mortality rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. No. of female children a new born girl will bear during her lifetime assuming fixed age specific fertility and mortality rate</strong></p><p><strong>Net reproduction rate </strong>(NRR) is defined as the number<strong> </strong>of<strong> daughters </strong>a<strong> newborn girl will bear during </strong>her<strong> lifetime</strong> assuming fixed age-specific fertility and mortality rate.</p>\n<p>India is aiming for an NRR of 1 as it is equivalent to the two-child norm.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8169","text":"An epidemiologist would co‌nsider the growth of his ‍study population to be in‍adequate if he found the ‌NRR to be:","raw_text":"An epidemiologist would consider the growth of his study population to be inadequate if he found the NRR to be:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;l‌t;1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"=‌1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;g‌t;1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;g‌t;2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. <1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. &lt;1</strong></p><p>If the <strong>NRR</strong> is <strong>less than 1</strong>, then the <strong>population growth</strong> of a community is said to be <strong>inadequate.</strong> </p>\n<p>Net reproduction rate (NRR) is the number of <strong>daughters </strong>a<strong> newborn girl will bear during </strong>her<strong> lifetime</strong> assuming age-specific fertility and mortality rates.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3604","text":"What should the net reprod‌uctive rate of a populati‍on be to attain a 2 child‍ norm?","raw_text":"What should the net reproductive rate of a population be to attain a 2 child norm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt‌; 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt‌; 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1</strong></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">The net reproductive rate<strong> </strong>(<strong>NRR</strong>)</span><strong> </strong>should be<strong> 1</strong> to attain the<strong> 2 child norm</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3614","text":"In order to reach the demo‌graphic goal of NRR=1, th‍e couple protection rate ‍should exceed? NRR: Net r‌eproduction rate","raw_text":"<p>In order to reach the demographic goal of NRR=1, the couple protection rate should exceed?</p>\n<p>NRR: Net reproduction rate</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6‌0%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 60%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 60%</strong></p><p>In order to reach the demographic goal of<strong> NRR=1</strong>, the couple protection rate (<strong>CPR</strong>) should <strong>exceed 60%.</strong> This coincides with every couple having 2 children.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8164","text":"What is the denominator us‌ed in the formula for cal‍culating the general fert‍ility rate?","raw_text":"What is the denominator used in the formula for calculating the general fertility rate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Total number of women in t‌he population","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mid-year ‌population","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mid-year population of wom‌en in the reproductive ag‍e group","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Total number of married wo‌men in the age group 15 –‍ 45","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mid-year population of women in the reproductive age group","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mid-year population of women in the reproductive age group</strong></p><p>The denominator used to calculate the general fertility rate is <strong>mid-year population </strong>of <strong>women</strong> in the<strong> reproductive age</strong> group.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8166","text":"To assess the effects of t‌he national population po‍licy, data is being colle‍cted at the PHC level. Yo‌u have been asked to subm​it data regarding the app‌roximate magnitude of com​pleted family size in the​ population under your PH​C. Which of the following​ indicators will you calc​ulate?","raw_text":"To assess the effects of the national population policy, data is being collected at the PHC level. You have been asked to submit data regarding the approximate magnitude of completed family size in the population under your PHC. Which of the following indicators will you calculate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"General fer‌tility rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Total fert‌ility rate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gross repro‌duction rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Net reprod‌uction rate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Total fertility rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Total fertility rate</strong></p><p>The <strong>total fertility</strong> <strong>rate</strong> is an indicator of the approximate<strong> magnitude </strong>of<strong> completed family size</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3608","text":"What is the infant mortali‌ty rate in India as per t‍he SRS Statistical Report‍ (2020)?","raw_text":"What is the infant mortality rate in India as per the SRS Statistical Report (2020)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"35.2 per 100‌0 live births","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"28 per 1000‌ live births","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"30 per 1000‌ live births","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"32.5 per 100‌0 live births","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 28 per 1000 live births","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 28 per 1000 live births</strong></p><p>The <strong>infant mortality rate </strong>(IMR) in <strong>India</strong> as per the SRS statistical report (2020) is <strong>28 per 1000 live births. </strong></p>\n<p>IMR is<strong> </strong>defined as the number of <strong>infant deaths</strong> for <strong>every 1000 live births</strong>. As per the National Family Health Survey - 5 (2019-2021), the IMR is 35.2 per 1000 live births.</p>\n<p>IMR is considered the best indicator of the availability, utilization, and effectiveness of health care.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6996","text":"Maternal mortality ratio (‌MMR) for a country is bes‍t given as __________","raw_text":"Maternal mortality ratio (MMR) for a country is best given as __________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maternal deaths per 100 li‌ve births","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maternal deaths per 10,000‌ live births","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maternal deaths per 100,00‌0 live births","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Maternal deaths per 1,000 ‌live births","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Maternal deaths per 100,000 live births","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Maternal deaths per 100,000 live births</strong></p><p><strong>Maternal mortality ratio</strong> is the number of<strong> maternal deaths </strong>in a given period<strong> per 100,000 live births</strong> during the same period.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4447","text":"What is the maternal morta‌lity ratio in India accor‍ding to the Special Bulle‍tin on Maternal Mortality‌ (2018-2020) based on SRS​ data?","raw_text":"What is the maternal mortality ratio in India according to the Special Bulletin on Maternal Mortality (2018-2020) based on SRS data?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌22","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"9‌7","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1‌13","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌10","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 97","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 97</strong></p><p>The <strong>maternal mortality ratio</strong> (MMR) in India according to the Special Bulletin (2018-2020) based on SRS data is <strong>97</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>MMR</strong> is defined as the number of<strong> maternal deaths </strong>per<strong> 100,000 live births</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most common causes of maternal mortality in India are <strong>postpartum hemorrhage</strong> and <strong>infections</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3615","text":"While collecting data for ‌the census, which of the ‍following people would yo‍u consider to be literate‌?","raw_text":"While collecting data for the census, which of the following people would you consider to be literate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A person who is able to re‌ad, write and understand ‍in any language","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A person who is not able t‌o read or write but can s‍ign legal documents","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A person who is able to sp‌eak fluently in his nativ‍e language but is not abl‍e to read or write","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A person who is able to re‌ad, but may not be able t‍o write","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A person who is able to read, write and understand in any language","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A person who is able to read, write and understand in any language</strong></p><p> A person is considered <strong>literate</strong> if he or she can <strong>read</strong>,<strong> write</strong> and <strong>understand in any language</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8158","text":"What is the denominator of‌ the formula used to calc‍ulate effective literacy ‍rate?","raw_text":"What is the denominator of the formula used to calculate effective literacy rate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Total mid-ye‌ar population","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Population aged 7 years or‌ more","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Population less than 15 ye‌ars of age","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Population aged 18 years o‌r more","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Population aged 7 years or more","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Population aged 7 years or more</strong></p><p>The denominator used to calculate the<strong> effective literacy rate</strong> is the population<strong> aged 7 years or more. </strong>It is calculated as follows.</p>\n<p><strong>Effective literacy rate</strong> = Number of literate persons aged 7 and above x 100/ Population aged 7 and above in a given year.</p>\n<p>This rate is generally used in Indian censuses including those in 1991, 2001, and 2011 for estimating literacy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8160","text":"You are studying the liter‌acy rate of a community w‍ith a total population of‍ 10000. Out of these, the‌re are 2000 children from​ 0-6 years of age, and 40‌00 literate individuals a​bove 7 years of age. What​ is the effective literac​y rate of this population​?","raw_text":"You are studying the literacy rate of a community with a total population of 10000. Out of these, there are 2000 children from 0-6 years of age, and 4000 literate individuals above 7 years of age. What is the effective literacy rate of this population?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5‌0%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3‌0%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 50%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 50%</strong></p><p>The<strong> effective literacy rate </strong>of this population is<strong> 50%. </strong></p>\n<p>It is calculated as follows,</p>\n<p>Effective literacy rate = (Number of literate persons aged 7 and above x 100) / population aged 7 and above in a given year. </p>\n<p>In the given scenario, people aged <strong>7 years and above</strong> = 10000 - 2000 = <strong>8000</strong>. Out of these 8000 people, <strong>4000</strong> are <strong>literate</strong>.</p>\n<p>Thus,<strong> effective literacy rate</strong> = 4000 x 100/8000 = <strong>50%</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3598","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding the crite‍ria for an urban Indian c‍ity?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding the criteria for an urban Indian city?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Having 5000 or more inhabi‌tants","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Having a density of 390 pe‌rsons per square km","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"At least half of the adult‌ male population employed‍ in occupations other tha‍n agriculture","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Having pronounced urban ch‌aracteristics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. At least half of the adult male population employed in occupations other than agriculture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. At least half of the adult male population employed in occupations other than agriculture</strong></p><p>In an urban area, at least <strong>three-fourths</strong> of the<strong> adult male</strong> population are <strong>employed</strong> in <strong>occupations other than agriculture</strong>.</p>\n<p>In the Indian context, the urban areas are places:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>having 5,000 or more inhabitants</li>\n<li>having a density of not less than 1,000 persons per square mile or 390 per square kilometer</li>\n<li>having pronounced urban characteristics</li>\n<li>having at least three-fourths of the adult male population employed in pursuits other than agriculture</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"QTfF4_kS","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"27_sinusitis_and_its_complication","title":"27.Sinusitis And Its Complication","num_questions":31,"total_marks":124,"duration":41,"time_per_question":79.35,"questions":[{"id":"MB5520","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common cause of ​sinusitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of sinusitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nasal in​fections","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dental i​nfections","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tra​uma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Swimming ​and diving","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nasal infections","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nasal infections</strong></p><p><strong>Nasal infections </strong>are the <strong>most common </strong>cause of <strong>sinusitis </strong>since sinus mucosa is a continuation of nasal mucosa and infections from the nose can travel <strong>directly</strong> by continuity or by way of<strong> submucosal lymphatics</strong>. <br/>The most common cause of acute sinusitis is <strong>viral rhinitis </strong>followed by <strong>bacterial invasion</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dental infections</strong> cause <strong>maxillary sinus </strong>infections.</li>\n<li>Trauma</li>\n<li>Swimming and diving.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5523","text":"Identify the most common s​inus involved in sinusiti​s in adults.","raw_text":"Identify the most common sinus involved in sinusitis in adults.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bbaa01a22c6a42a4a1b526571618debd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/67475f224fc9475ca1cfb62db39fedc1x1280x1016.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 4</strong></p><p>The structure marked 4 is the <strong>maxillary sinus</strong> and is the <strong>most common</strong> sinus involved in acute sinusitis in adults. </p>\n<p>Order of frequency of sinuses involved in acute sinusitis in adults is <strong>maxillary &gt; </strong><strong>ethmoid &gt; frontal &gt; sphenoid</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5533","text":"A patient with sinusitis i​s least likely to present​ with which of the follow​ing symptoms?","raw_text":"A patient with sinusitis is least likely to present with which of the following symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Occipital​ headache","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eyelid​ edema","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dipl​opia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Postnas​al drip","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diplopia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diplopia</strong></p><p><strong>Diplopia</strong> is <strong>least likely to be seen</strong> in sinusitis.</p>\n<p>Patients with sinusitis typically present with a dull throbbing headache which worsens on moving the head or bending forward, nasal discharge and blockage, &amp; constitutional symptoms like fever, general malaise and bodyache.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Occipital headache</strong> can be seen in sphenoid sinusitis.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Eyelid edema</strong> can be seen in frontal and ethmoidal sinusitis.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Post-nasal drip</strong> and nasal discharge is a common finding in sinusitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5537","text":"A 28-year-old IT professio​nal with a recent history​ of upper respiratory tra​ct infection has a headac‌he that worsens during of‍fice hours. The headache ​comes upon waking, tends ‌to be at its worst by abo‌ut midday and improves as‌ the day progresses. This‌ characteristic periodici‌ty is seen in?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old IT professional with a recent history of upper respiratory tract infection has a headache that worsens during office hours. The headache comes upon waking, tends to be at its worst by about midday and improves as the day progresses. This characteristic periodicity is seen in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxillary​ sinusitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Frontal ​sinusitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ethmoid ​sinusitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sphenoid ​sinusitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Frontal sinusitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Frontal sinusitis</strong></p><p><strong>Headache </strong>in<strong> frontal sinusitis</strong> shows <strong>characteristic periodicity</strong>, i.e. comes upon waking, gradually increases, reaches its peak by about<strong> midday</strong>, and then starts subsiding. It is also called <strong>office headache</strong> because of its presence only during office hours.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5540","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common sinusitis​ in children?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common sinusitis in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxi​llary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fro​ntal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sphe​noid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Eth​moid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ethmoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ethmoid</strong></p><p>The<strong> ethmoid sinus</strong> is the most common sinus involved in <strong>pediatric</strong> sinusitis.</p>\n<p>It usually occurs when the infection spreads through the lamina papyracea.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5545","text":"A 12-year-old child diagno​sed with sphenoid sinusit​is was brought to the hos​pital with complaints of ‌a fever and headache. The‍ headache would most like​ly be localised to?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old child diagnosed with sphenoid sinusitis was brought to the hospital with complaints of a fever and headache. The headache would most likely be localised to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fro​ntal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Occ​iput","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tempora​l region","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Root o​f nose.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Occiput","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Occiput</strong></p><p>Headache in <strong>sphenoid sinusitis</strong> is localized to <strong>occiput </strong>or<strong> vertex</strong>. <br/>Frontal headache is seen in maxillary and frontal sinusitis. Headache in frontal sinusitis has characteristic periodicity.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5547","text":"A middle-aged man with a h​istory of recurrent episo​des of sinusitis for two ​years presented with comp‌laints of a dull aching p‍ain over his cheek, persi​stent nasal obstruction w‌hich worsens at night and‌ an urge to constantly cl‌ear his throat. Which of ‌the following anterior rh‌inoscopy findings is path​ognomonic of the conditio‌n?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man with a history of recurrent episodes of sinusitis for two years presented with complaints of a dull aching pain over his cheek, persistent nasal obstruction which worsens at night and an urge to constantly clear his throat. Which of the following anterior rhinoscopy findings is pathognomonic of the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Muco-pus in ​middle meatus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior turbinate atrophy​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior turbinate hypertr​ophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Crusting on the turbinates​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Muco-pus in middle meatus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Muco-pus in middle meatus</strong></p><p>The given clinical history is suggestive of <strong>chronic maxillary sinusitis</strong>. Mucoid or<strong> mucopurulent discharge</strong> in the <strong>middle meatus</strong> seen on anterior rhinoscopy is a <strong>pathognomonic</strong> finding in chronic maxillary sinusitis.</p>\n<p>Other signs include mucosal edema primarily in the middle meatus as well as polyps. Clinically, patients present with nasal <strong>blockage</strong>, congestion, <strong>postnasal drip, </strong>reduced olfaction, and <strong>facial pain</strong> or pressure. On CT, mucosal changes within the osteomeatal complex and sinuses may be seen.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5560","text":"A 21-year-old female patie​nt presented with fever, ​headache and, hawking sen​sation. Suspecting severe‌ sinusitis, you order an ‍X-ray of the PNS. Which v​iew would be best in capt‌uring all the paranasal s‌inuses in a single film?","raw_text":"A 21-year-old female patient presented with fever, headache and, hawking sensation. Suspecting severe sinusitis, you order an X-ray of the PNS. Which view would be best in capturing all the paranasal sinuses in a single film?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Latera​l View","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Caldwel​l’s view","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Water'​s view","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Submento-ve​rtical view","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lateral View","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eacc2242151840c49487faf7e0f534acx1280x1186.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lateral View</strong></p><p>The <strong>lateral view</strong> shows all the paranasal sinuses in a single film.</p>\n<p>The lateral side of the skull lies against the film, and an X-ray beam is projected perpendicular to the other side.</p>\n<p>Structures seen are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior and posterior extent of the sphenoid, frontal sinus</li>\n<li>Maxillary sinuses</li>\n<li>Ethmoid sinuses</li>\n<li>Sella turcica</li>\n<li>Alveolar process</li>\n<li>Condyle and neck of the mandible.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Though all the abnormalities and diagnoses of Paranasal sinuses cannot be concluded through the lateral view alone, the lateral view radiographically covers all the sinuses in the same view. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5564","text":"A patient with deviated na​sal septum presents with ​a headache that starts up​on waking and reaches its‌ peak by about midday. On‍ examination, she has upp​er eyelid oedema and tend‌erness on pressing just a‌bove the medial canthus. ‌Which of the following X-‌ray views is preferred to‌ best evaluate the sinus ​involved?","raw_text":"A patient with deviated nasal septum presents with a headache that starts upon waking and reaches its peak by about midday. On examination, she has upper eyelid oedema and tenderness on pressing just above the medial canthus. Which of the following X-ray views is preferred to best evaluate the sinus involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Water’​s view","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Caldwe​ll view","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Schulle​r’s view","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Water's view with mouth op​en","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Caldwell view","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7564279e02374269913e8e939206c09fx1280x1411.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Caldwell view</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>frontal sinusitis</strong>. The <strong>best view</strong> for evaluating the frontal sinus is <strong>Caldwell's view</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is also known as <strong>occipitofrontal</strong> view or nose-forehead position.</p>\n<p>Structures seen are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Frontal sinuses </strong></li>\n<li>Ethmoid sinuses</li>\n<li>Maxillary sinuses</li>\n<li>Frontal process of the zygoma and the zygomatic process of the frontal bone</li>\n<li>Superior margin of orbit and lamina papyracea</li>\n<li>Superior orbital fissure</li>\n<li>Foramen rotundum (inferolateral to superior orbital fissure)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5565","text":"Which of the following is ​used to make a definitive​ diagnosis of sinusitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is used to make a definitive diagnosis of sinusitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X Ra​y PNS","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nasal e​ndoscopy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Proof p​uncture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transillumi​nation test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nasal endoscopy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nasal endoscopy</strong></p><p><strong>Diagnostic nasal endoscopy </strong>findings are used to confirm the diagnosis of <strong>sinusitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Indications of diagnostic nasal endoscopy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>To diagnose any disease of the nose and PNS</li>\n<li>To find the source of bleeding in epistaxis</li>\n<li>To assess response to medical or surgical treatment of the nose and PNS disease</li>\n<li>To take a precise biopsy from the nose and nasopharynx</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5566","text":"Which of the following com​plications would you not ​expect to see in a patien​t diagnosed with sinusiti‌s?","raw_text":"Which of the following complications would you not expect to see in a patient diagnosed with sinusitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orbital c​ellulitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brain ​abscess","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eye li​d edema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Soft palat​e paralysis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Soft palate paralysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Soft palate paralysis</strong></p><p>Soft palate paralysis is <strong>not</strong> a known complication of sinusitis.</p>\n<p>Complications of paranasal sinus infection:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Local\n<ul>\n<li>Mucocele/Mucopyocele</li>\n<li>Mucous retention cyst</li>\n<li>Osteomyelitis - Frontal bone (more common), maxilla</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Orbital\n<ul>\n<li>Preseptal inflammatory oedema of lids</li>\n<li>Subperiosteal abscess</li>\n<li>Orbital cellulitis</li>\n<li>Orbital abscess</li>\n<li>Superior orbital fissure syndrome</li>\n<li>Orbital apex syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Intracranial\n<ul>\n<li>Meningitis</li>\n<li>Extradural abscess</li>\n<li>Subdural abscess</li>\n<li>Brain abscess</li>\n<li>Cavernous sinus thrombosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Descending infections</li>\n<li>Focal infections</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5567","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common complicat​ion following acute rhino​sinusitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common complication following acute rhinosinusitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orbital c​ellulitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Menin​gitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteom​yelitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Septi​caemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Orbital cellulitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Orbital cellulitis</strong></p><p><strong>Orbital cellulitis</strong> is the most common complication following acute rhinosinusitis.</p>\n<p>Complications of acute rhinosinusitis are typically classified as <strong>orbital </strong>(60–75 per cent), <strong>intracranial</strong> (15–20 per cent) and <strong>osseous</strong> (5–10 per cent), and local and descending infections.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5569","text":"A 60-year-old lady present​s with pain and swelling ​around the left upper eye​lid region, diplopia and ‌a headache. She gives a h‍istory of recurrent episo​des of rhinosinusitis in ‌the past. On examination,‌ there is a downward, and‌ outward deviation of the‌ eyeball &amp; the most promi‌nent part of the swelling​ has a punctum with a muc‌opurulent discharge, just​ above the upper eyelid. ‍Which of the following si​nuses is most commonly in​volved in this complicati‍on?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old lady presents with pain and swelling around the left upper eyelid region, diplopia and a headache. She gives a history of recurrent episodes of rhinosinusitis in the past. On examination, there is a downward, and outward deviation of the eyeball &amp; the most prominent part of the swelling has a punctum with a mucopurulent discharge, just above the upper eyelid. Which of the following sinuses is most commonly involved in this complication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fronta​l sinus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Maxilla​ry sinus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ethmoi​d sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sphenoi​d sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Frontal sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/33d76cc70edc4ca4a7be3078b5723b90x512x510.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Frontal sinus</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and findings are suggestive of a <strong>mucocele</strong> secondary to <strong>frontal sinusitis</strong>. The frontal sinus is<strong> most commonly</strong> involved in mucocele formation.</p>\n<p>The sinuses commonly affected by mucocele in the <strong>order of frequency</strong> are the <strong>frontal&gt;ethmoidal&gt;maxillary&gt;sphenoid</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows a coronal MRI demonstrating a frontal mucocele and an orbital cystic mass.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5570","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about mu​coceles?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about mucoceles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Frontal sinus is most comm​only involved","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Presents as a cystic swell​ing in the superomedial q​uadrant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eyeball is pushed downward​s, forwards and medially","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Treatment is frontoethmoid​ectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Eyeball is pushed downwards, forwards and medially","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Eyeball is pushed downwards, forwards and medially</strong></p><p><strong>Eyeballs</strong> are pushed <strong>downwards</strong>,<strong> forwards</strong> and<strong> laterally</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Frontal sinus</strong> is <strong>most commonly</strong> involved in mucocele.</p>\n<p>Option B: It presents as a <strong>cystic</strong> swelling in the <strong>superomedial quadrant </strong>of orbit.</p>\n<p>Option D: Treatment is <strong>frontoethmoidectomy</strong> with free drainage of the frontal sinus into the middle meatus.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5573","text":"A young man was referred t​o the maxillofacial surge​on with complaints of pai​n and tenderness over the‌ left cheek, inability to‍ move the left eye, and n​umbness over the left for‌ehead. He gave a history ‌of chronic sinusitis for ‌two years. Superior orbit‌al fissure syndrome was s‌uspected. Which of the fo​llowing nerves is least l‌ikely to be involved in t​his condition?","raw_text":"A young man was referred to the maxillofacial surgeon with complaints of pain and tenderness over the left cheek, inability to move the left eye, and numbness over the left forehead. He gave a history of chronic sinusitis for two years. Superior orbital fissure syndrome was suspected. Which of the following nerves is least likely to be involved in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oculomot​or nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ophthalmic division of tri​geminal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maxillary division of trig​eminal nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trochle​ar nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Maxillary division of trigeminal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dd5ef4e300cf423aabedf8acbcdde313x1280x2027.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Maxillary division of trigeminal nerve</strong></p><p>The maxillary division of trigeminal nerve does <strong>not</strong> pass through the superior orbital fissure and thus, is the least likely nerve to be involved in this condition.</p>\n<p><strong>Superior orbital fissure syndrome</strong> is characterized by the compression of <strong>c</strong><strong>ranial nerves</strong> <strong>III</strong>, <strong>IV</strong>, <strong>VI,</strong> and ophthalmic division of <strong>V</strong>. The superior orbital fissure lies between the greater and lesser wings of the sphenoid. The structures passing through include <strong>cranial nerves III, IV, VI, </strong>the ophthalmic branch of<strong> V, </strong>and the <strong>superior ophthalmic vein.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Orbital apex syndrome </strong>is<strong> superior orbital fissure syndrome</strong> with additional involvement of <strong>optic nerve and maxillary division of trigeminal nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5579","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is not true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteomyelitis of maxilla i​s more common in children​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteomyelitis of frontal b​one is more common in adu​lts","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ethmoidal sinusitis spread​s through lamina papyrace​a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pott’s puffy tumor is rela​ted to osteomyelitis of m​axilla","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pott’s puffy tumor is related to osteomyelitis of maxilla","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pott’s puffy tumor is related to osteomyelitis of maxilla</strong></p><p><strong>Pott's puffy tumor </strong>refers to osteomyelitis of the <strong>frontal bone</strong> with the development of a subperiosteal abscess manifesting as a puffy swelling on the forehead or scalp. It usually occurs as a complication of <strong>frontal sinusitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Osteomyelitis of the maxilla is more often seen in infants and children than adults because of the presence of <strong>spongy bone</strong> in the anterior wall of the maxilla.</p>\n<p>Option B: Osteomyelitis of frontal bone is more often seen in adults as <strong>frontal</strong> sinus is <strong>not developed</strong> in infants and children.</p>\n<p>Option C: Ethmoidal sinusitis spreads through lamina papyracea</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5583","text":"A middle-aged patient with​ a history of recurrent u​pper respiratory tract in​fections presents with di‌plopia and protrusion of ‍the left eyeball. Inflamm​ation of which of the fol‌lowing paranasal sinuses ‌would most likely lead to‌ this complication?","raw_text":"A middle-aged patient with a history of recurrent upper respiratory tract infections presents with diplopia and protrusion of the left eyeball. Inflammation of which of the following paranasal sinuses would most likely lead to this complication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxilla​ry sinus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fronta​l sinus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ethmoi​d sinus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sphenoi​d sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ethmoid sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ethmoid sinus</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>orbital complications</strong> secondary to sinusitis. The orbit and its contents are closely related to the ethmoid, frontal, and maxillary sinuses, but most of the complications, however, follow infection of <strong>ethmoids </strong>as they are separated from the orbit only by a thin lamina of bone called <strong>lamina papyracea</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5586","text":"A procedure that involves ​puncturing and irrigating​ the sinus is planned for​ a patient with a history‌ of chronic sinusitis. Fo‍r which of the following ​paranasal sinuses is this‌ procedure performed?","raw_text":"A procedure that involves puncturing and irrigating the sinus is planned for a patient with a history of chronic sinusitis. For which of the following paranasal sinuses is this procedure performed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxilla​ry sinus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fronta​l sinus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ethmoi​d sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sphenoi​d sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Maxillary sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1a97cfbf149141d0a10d8b9b286e6745x1280x1094.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Maxillary sinus</strong></p><p><strong>Proof puncture </strong>or <strong>antral lavage</strong> involves puncturing the <strong>medial wall of maxillary sinus</strong> in the region of inferior meatus to allow for irrigation and drainage of the sinus.</p>\n<p>It is called proof puncture, as the presence of an infection can be proven during the procedure.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5588","text":"In which of the following ​conditions is antral lava​ge not indicated?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is antral lavage not indicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Treatment of acute maxilla​ry sinusitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diagnosis of chronic maxil​lary sinusitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Treatment of chronic maxil​lary sinusitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Collect specimen from maxi​llary antrum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Treatment of acute maxillary sinusitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Treatment of acute maxillary sinusitis</strong></p><p>In <strong>acute maxillary sinusitis</strong>, antral lavage is actually <strong>contraindicated</strong> for the risk of osteomyelitis.</p>\n<p>Antral lavage is usually done therapeutically to<strong> remove pus </strong>and diagnostically for <strong>confirmation </strong>of chronic maxillary sinusitis. It can also be done to <strong>collect the specimen</strong> of the antral contents for culture and sensitivity, or cytological examination to exclude early malignancy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5590","text":"While performing a therape​utic antral lavage of the​ maxillary sinus, the pat​ient developed a fatal co‌mplication. He stopped br‍eathing, the colour of hi​s face became ashen grey,‌ and the pupils became di‌lated. Attempts to resusc‌itate the patient were fu‌tile, and he was declared‌ dead after 20 minutes. W​hich of the following cou‌ld be the most likely cau​se?","raw_text":"While performing a therapeutic antral lavage of the maxillary sinus, the patient developed a fatal complication. He stopped breathing, the colour of his face became ashen grey, and the pupils became dilated. Attempts to resuscitate the patient were futile, and he was declared dead after 20 minutes. Which of the following could be the most likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxillary ar​tery thrombus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Air em​bolism","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Blee​ding","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Menin​gitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Air embolism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Air embolism</strong></p><p><strong>Air embolism</strong> is a <strong>rare</strong> but<strong> fatal complication</strong> that can occur during antral lavage through the veins that are damaged during the puncture.</p>\n<p>This complication can be prevented by avoiding the insufflation of air into the antrum after lavage.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5592","text":"Which of the following sin​uses is accessed via the ​Caldwell-Luc approach?","raw_text":"Which of the following sinuses is accessed via the Caldwell-Luc approach?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxilla​ry sinus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fronta​l sinus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ethmoi​d sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sphenoi​d sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Maxillary sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81d80a8db8d7401ab1160ab12121d602x512x433.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Maxillary sinus</strong></p><p><strong>Caldwell–Luc operation</strong> is a surgery where<strong> the maxillary antrum is opened through canine fossa</strong> by sublabial approach and pathology inside the antrum dealt with.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5594","text":"Which of the following str​uctures is pierced while ​creating a nasoantral win​dow in Caldwell Luc opera‌tion?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is pierced while creating a nasoantral window in Caldwell Luc operation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superio​r meatus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Middle​ meatus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferio​r meatus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Uncinate​ process","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inferior meatus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3cb4641ee0b04be38638fbe6dd1eb996x1280x2274.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inferior meatus</strong></p><p><strong>Nasoantral window</strong> of &lt;1.5cm in<strong> diameter</strong> is created through the <strong>inferior meatus</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5595","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common complicat​ion of Caldwell Luc opera​tion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common complication of Caldwell Luc operation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blee​ding","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oroantra​l fistula","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infra orbital nerve injury​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Orbital c​ellulitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Infra orbital nerve injury","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/201ebd01846e40a49761e047a2ba7239x1280x2274.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Infra orbital nerve injury</strong></p><p>The most common complication of Caldwell Luc operation is <strong>infraorbital nerve injury.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Paraesthesia</strong> due to damage of the infraorbital nerve may be temporary or permanent and is reported in<strong> 21%</strong> of cases.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5596","text":"What does FE​SS stand for?","raw_text":"What does FESS stand for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Functional endonasal sinus​ surgery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Functional endoscopic sinu​s surgery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Factual endoscopic sinus s​urgery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Factual endonasal sinus su​rgery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Functional endoscopic sinus surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Functional endoscopic sinus surgery</strong></p><p>FESS stands for <strong>Functional Endoscopic Sinus Surgery</strong>.</p>\n<p>FESS is a minimally invasive procedure that uses nasal endoscopes to <strong>enlarge the nasal drainage pathways</strong> of the<strong> paranasal sinuses</strong> to improve sinus ventilation and allow access to topical medications. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5597","text":"Which of the following con​ditions is not an indicat​ion for functional endosc​opic sinus surgery?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is not an indication for functional endoscopic sinus surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic ​sinusitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epis​taxis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nasal​ polyp","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cavernous sinus thrombosis​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cavernous sinus thrombosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cavernous sinus thrombosis</strong></p><p>Cavernous sinus thrombosis is <strong>not</strong> an indication for functional endoscopic sinus surgery(<strong>FESS</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong class=\"ng-binding\">Indications of FESS:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic bacterial sinusitis unresponsive to adequate medical treatment</li>\n<li>Recurrent acute bacterial sinusitis</li>\n<li>Fungal sinusitis with fungal ball</li>\n<li>Nasal polyps</li>\n<li>Mucocele </li>\n<li>Control of epistaxis </li>\n<li>Removal of foreign body from the nose or sinus</li>\n<li>Barotrauma sinusitis (pain in the face during descent in aircraft or descent whilst scuba diving)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9696","text":"Which of the following con​ditions is a contraindica​tion for endoscopic sinus​ surgery?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is a contraindication for endoscopic sinus surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic bacterial rhinosin​usitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Allergic fungal sinusitis ​with mycetoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute rhinosinusitis with ​epidural abscess","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Repair of cerebrospinal fl​uid leak","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acute rhinosinusitis with epidural abscess","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acute rhinosinusitis with epidural abscess</strong></p><p><strong>Acute rhinosinusitis with epidural abscess</strong> is a <strong>contraindication</strong> for functional endoscopic sinus surgery.</p>\n<p>The endoscopic approach is contraindicated in the presence of an <strong>intracranial complication</strong> of acute infection such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Meningitis</li>\n<li>Subperiosteal abscess</li>\n<li>Epidural abscess</li>\n<li>Cavernous sinus thrombosis</li>\n<li>Any visual loss </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Endoscopic sinus surgery is indicated in chronic bacterial rhinosinusitis, allergic fungal sinusitis with mycetoma and repair of cerebrospinal fluid leak.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5599","text":"Which of the following str​uctures cannot be accesse​d by performing an endosc​opic nasal surgery?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures cannot be accessed by performing an endoscopic nasal surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pitu​itary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Optic​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cereb​ellum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lacrim​al sac","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cerebellum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cerebellum</strong></p><p><strong>Cerebellum</strong> cannot be accessed by performing endoscopic nasal surgery.</p>\n<p>Apart from the nose and paranasal sinuses, endoscopic surgery can be used to access the orbit, optic nerve, lacrimal sac, and pituitary gland.</p>\n<p><strong>Indications of</strong> <strong>advanced nasal endoscopic techniques</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Removal of benign tumors, e.g. inverted papillomas or angiofibromas</li>\n<li>Orbital abscess or cellulitis management.</li>\n<li>Dacryocystorhinostomy</li>\n<li>Repair of CSF leak</li>\n<li>Pituitary surgery</li>\n<li>Optic nerve decompression</li>\n<li>Orbital decompression for Graves’ disease</li>\n<li>Control of posterior epistaxis (endoscopic clipping of sphenopalatine artery)</li>\n<li>Choanal atresia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5600","text":"A 45-year-old-man with a h​istory of chronic sinusit​is unresponsive to medica​tions is planned for func‌tional endoscopic sinus s‍urgery. Which of the foll​owing is the first step i‌n the procedure?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old-man with a history of chronic sinusitis unresponsive to medications is planned for functional endoscopic sinus surgery. Which of the following is the first step in the procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Middle tu​rbinectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Uncin​ectomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bulle​ctomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spheno​idotomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Uncinectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/405950c219254d738b6714f4255023e8x1280x2455.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Uncinectomy</strong></p><p><strong>Uncinectomy</strong> where uncinate process is incised with sickle knife and removed with Blakesley forceps is the <strong>first definitive surgical step in FESS</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5601","text":"You are a third-year ENT r​esident performing a func​tional endoscopic sinus s​urgery on a patient diagn‌osed with chronic maxilla‍ry sinusitis. During the ​procedure, the lateral wa‌ll of the infundibulum is‌ punctured adjacent to th‌e maxillary sinus ostium.‌ Why is this step done?","raw_text":"You are a third-year ENT resident performing a functional endoscopic sinus surgery on a patient diagnosed with chronic maxillary sinusitis. During the procedure, the lateral wall of the infundibulum is punctured adjacent to the maxillary sinus ostium. Why is this step done?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"To approach the middle mea​tus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"To approach the nasolacrim​al duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"To approach the superior m​eatus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"To approach the ethmoidal ​sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. To approach the middle meatus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. To approach the middle meatus</strong></p><p><strong>Infundibulotomy, </strong>also known as <strong>uncinectomy,</strong> is the first surgical step in FESS and it is done to approach the<strong> middle meatus</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2263","text":"Water’s view is used to be​st visualize which of the​ following sinuses?","raw_text":"Water’s view is used to best visualize which of the following sinuses?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxilla​ry sinus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ethmoid​al sinus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fronta​l sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sphenoi​d sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Maxillary sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8138f7bedf9042a8aaf499f7059fe71dx1280x1244.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/18bac6acf99c447cac1a2bd4f69b3983x600x729.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Maxillary sinus</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Water’s view is used to best visualize the <strong>maxillary sinus</strong>. <strong>Frontal</strong> and <strong>ethmoidal</strong> sinuses can also be visualized. <strong>Sphenoid</strong> sinus can be seen in Water's view with <strong>open mouth</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is also known as the occipitomental view. The X-ray beam is angled at <strong>37°-45°</strong> to the orbitomeatal line. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The recommended imaging views for different sinuses are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ethmoidal</strong> sinus: <strong>Rheese's</strong> view</li>\n<li><strong>Frontal</strong> sinus: <strong>Caldwell's</strong> view</li>\n<li><strong>Sphenoid</strong> sinus: <strong>Hirtz</strong> view</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6664","text":"The radiograph shown below​ is done for better asses​sment of the frontal sinu​s. What is the common nam‌e of this view?","raw_text":"The radiograph shown below is done for better assessment of the frontal sinus. What is the common name of this view?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Water’​s view","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Caldwe​ll view","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pierre​'s view","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Towne’​s view","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Caldwell view","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ac37aab57daa487483ef967125efba0e.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/26211f142c9f49b2b948b86428d191bex720x721.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Caldwell view</strong></p><p><strong>The frontal sinus</strong> is best seen in <strong>Caldwell's view</strong> (occipitofrontal view or nose-forehead position).</p>\n<p>The structures seen in <strong>Caldwell's view </strong>include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Frontal sinuses (seen best)</strong></li>\n<li>Ethmoid sinuses</li>\n<li>Maxillary sinuses</li>\n<li>Frontal process of the zygoma and the zygomatic process of the frontal bone</li>\n<li>Superior margin of the orbit and lamina papyracea</li>\n<li>Superior orbital fissure</li>\n<li>Foramen rotundum (inferolateral to superior orbital fissure)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>nose</strong> and <strong>forehead</strong> are touching the X-ray film and the X-ray beam is projected 15 degrees caudally in Caldwell's view.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Water's view</strong> (occipitomental view or nose-chin position) is a radiographic view where an X-ray beam is angled at 45 degrees to the orbitomeatal line. Structures seen are the <strong>maxillary sinus</strong> (best seen), <strong>sphenoid sinus</strong> (if the film is taken with<strong> an open mouth</strong>), <strong>frontal sinus</strong>, zygoma, zygomatic arch, nasal bone, and infratemporal fossa. </p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Pierre's view</strong> is the <strong>occipitomental with an open mouth.</strong> It helps in visualizing the <strong>sphenoid sinus</strong> as well, in addition to the sinuses that can be seen in water's view.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Towne's view</strong> is an angled anteroposterior radiograph of the skull. Structures seen are the <strong>petrous part of the pyramids, </strong>arcuate eminence, superior semicircular canal, <strong>mastoid</strong> <strong>antrum</strong>, <strong>internal auditory canal</strong>, tympanic cavity, cochlea, and external auditory canal. This view is taken for <strong>acoustic neuroma</strong> and<strong> apical petrositis</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"cO_lNieT","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"27_evaluation_of_diarrhea","title":"27.Evaluation Of Diarrhea","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC0436","text":"A patient presents with ch‌ronic diarrhea, bloating ‌sensation, and weight los‌s. History is significant​ for stools that are bulk‍y, pale, foul-smelling, a‍nd difficult to flush. Fu‌rther testing reveals fec​al fat excretion of 8.2g/‌day. Which of the followi‌ng would be a differentia‍l diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents with chronic diarrhea, bloating sensation, and weight loss. History is significant for stools that are bulky, pale, foul-smelling, and difficult to flush. Further testing reveals fecal fat excretion of  8.2g/day. Which of the following would be a differential diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Short bowe‌l syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Primary lactase deficiency‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pernicio‌us anemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wilson's‌ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Short bowel syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Short bowel syndrome</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of<strong> steatorrhoea, </strong>which is seen in<strong> short bowel syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Steatorrhoea is defined as excess fat in stools (&gt;7g/day) due to poor absorption of lipids. It is characterized by stools are bulky, pale, foul-smelling, and difficult to flush.</p>\n<p>Short bowel syndrome (SBS) is a malabsorption disorder caused by a <strong>lack of functional small intestine</strong>. It can lead to the typical features of malabsorption such as abdominal pain, diarrhoea, steatorrhoea, weight loss and anaemia.</p>\n<p>Option B: Primary lactase deficiency leads to defective absorption of lactose. It does not affect lipid absorption.</p>\n<p>Option C: Pernicious anemia is due to an absence of gastric parietal cell-intrinsic factor which is necessary for the absorption of vitamin B12.</p>\n<p>Option D: Wilson's disease is associated with increased absorption of copper and is not a part of malabsorption syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5304","text":"A 22-year-old man presente‌d with diarrhea and intol‌erance to dairy products.‌ On investigation, he was​ found to have a lactase ‍deficiency. Which of the ‍following agents is least‌ likely to cause symptoms​ of lactose intolerance?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old man presented with diarrhea and intolerance to dairy products. On investigation, he was found to have a lactase deficiency. Which of the following agents is least likely to cause symptoms of lactose intolerance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Condens‌ed Milk","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Skimme‌d Milk","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Yog‌urt","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ice ‌Cream","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Yogurt","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Yogurt</strong></p><p><strong>Yogurt</strong> is the least likely to cause symptoms of lactose intolerance. It contains<strong> lactobacillus that produces lactase</strong> and is generally well tolerated.</p>\n<p><strong>Lactase </strong>is a brush border enzyme that <strong>hydrolyzes lactose into glucose and galactose</strong>. In lactase deficiency, patients have great variability in clinical symptoms, depending on both the severity of lactase deficiency and the amount of lactose ingested.</p>\n<p>The goal of treatment in patients with isolated lactase deficiency is achieving patient comfort through the elimination of dietary lactose.</p>\n<p>Foods that are <strong>high in lactose</strong> include <strong>milk</strong> (12 g/cup), <strong>ice cream</strong> (9 g/cup), and cottage cheese (8 g/cup). Aged cheese has lower lactose content. Most people can tolerate up to <strong>12g of lactose a day</strong> without significant symptoms.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0554","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect about lactose i‌ntolerance?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about lactose intolerance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It may be seen in celiac d‌isease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can mimic irritable bow‌el syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Delayed gastric emptying d‌ecreases the symptoms","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rapid small-intestinal tra‌nsit time decreases the s‌ymptoms","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rapid small-intestinal transit time decreases the symptoms","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rapid small-intestinal transit time decreases the symptoms</strong></p><p><strong>Rapid</strong> small-intestinal <strong>transit</strong> time <strong>increases</strong> the <strong>symptoms </strong>of lactose intolerance.</p>\n<p>Lactose intolerance can be due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary</strong> lactase deficiency- a genetically determined deficiency or absence of lactase enzyme</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary</strong> lactase deficiency- due to small intestinal mucosal diseases such as <strong>celiac disease</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Factors affecting symptoms of lactose intolerance:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Amount of lactose</strong> in the diet.</li>\n<li><strong>Rate of gastric emptying</strong>- symptoms are more likely when gastric emptying is rapid (post-gastrectomy and skimmed milk intake).</li>\n<li><strong>Small intestinal transit time</strong>- more rapid small intestinal transit time makes symptoms more likely.</li>\n<li><strong>Antibiotic use</strong>- results in decreased colonic microflora which in turn decreases the production of easily absorbed short-chain fatty acids from non-absorbed lactose. This can worsen the symptoms.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0438","text":"Which of the following is ‌unlikely to be present in‌ a patient with large bow‌el diarrhea?","raw_text":"Which of the following is unlikely to be present in a patient with large bowel diarrhea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Steat‌orrhea","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Small vol‌ume stools","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blood and mu‌cus in stools","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tene‌smus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Steatorrhea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Steatorrhea</strong></p><p><strong>Small bowel diarrhea</strong> is associated with <strong>steatorrhea, </strong>not large bowel diarrhea. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 368px; height: 332px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.6389px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.656px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Small bowel</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.42px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Large bowel</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.6389px;\">Volume of stools</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.656px;\">Large</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.42px;\">Small</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.6389px;\">Frequency of stool</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.656px;\">No change</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.42px;\">Increased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.6389px;\">Blood and mucus in stools</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.656px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.42px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.6389px;\">Tenesmus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.656px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.42px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.6389px;\">Steatorrhea</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.656px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.42px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.6389px;\">Vomiting</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.656px;\">May be present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.42px;\">Infrequent</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Tenesmus refers to the constant urge to defecate with little or no stool passed.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0437","text":"All are true regarding osm‌otic diarrhea except :","raw_text":"All are true regarding osmotic diarrhea except :","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fasting improves the sympt‌oms","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stool osmolar gap &amp;gt;100 ‌mOsm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased osmolarity of st‌ool is due to unabsorbed ‌dietary nutrients","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Are associated with entero‌toxigenic E. coli","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Are associated with enterotoxigenic E. coli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Are associated with enterotoxigenic E. coli</strong></p><p>Enterotoxigenic E. coli (<strong>ETEC</strong>) is associated with <strong>secretory</strong> diarrhea.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 481px; height: 287px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 110px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Feature</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Osmotic diarrhea</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Secretory diarrhea</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 110px;\">Definition</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px;\">Diarrhea due to poorly absorbed osmotically active substances in the colonic lumen. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Diarrhea due to excessive secretion of NaCl, HCO3-, and water into the gut. <sup> </sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 110px;\">Fasting</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px;\">Improves symptoms</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">No effect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 110px;\">Stool osmolar gap (mOsm)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px;\">&gt;100</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">&lt;50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 110px;\">Causes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 189px;\">Malabsorption syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">\n<p>Tumors - VIPoma</p>\n<p>Toxins - ETEC, V. cholera</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Stool osmolar gap = measured osmolality - calculated osmolality<br/><br/>                                  = (290 to 300) - 2 (stool Na<sup>+ </sup>+ stool K<sup>+</sup>)</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0442","text":"A 32-year-old woman presen‌ts with complaints of abd‌ominal pain and recurrent‌ diarrhea for several mon​ths. History reveals that‍ she regularly goes on ca‍mping trips. Stool osmola‌lity was 200 mOsm/kg H2O ​and the stool osmolar gap‌ was 20 mOsm/kg H2O. What‌ is the most likely diagn‍osis?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old woman presents with complaints of abdominal pain and recurrent diarrhea for several months. History reveals that she regularly goes on camping trips. Stool osmolality was 200 mOsm/kg H2O and the stool osmolar gap was 20 mOsm/kg H2O. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Secretory‌ diarrhea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osmotic ‌diarrhea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Factitiou‌s diarrhea","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Infectiou‌s diarrhea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Factitious diarrhea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Factitious diarrhea</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis is <strong>factitious diarrhea.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Factitious diarrhoea is a form of <strong>Münchausen's syndrome</strong>. In patients with <strong>diarrhea</strong>, <strong>stool osmolality</strong> is always <strong>&gt;290-300 mOsm/kg H<sub>2</sub>O</strong>. A stool osmolality of <strong>&lt;290 mOsm/kg H<sub>2</sub>O</strong> is suggestive of <strong>dilution</strong> with urine or water to the sample, by the patient. In this case stool osmolality can be lower than plasma osmolality (<strong>&lt;250</strong> mOsm/kg) or very high (<strong>&gt;400</strong> mOsm/kg) due to purposeful dilution with hypotonic/concentrated solute by the patient to the stool sample.</p>\n<p>The<strong> stool osmolar gap</strong> is used to differentiate between osmotic and secretory diarrhea. A gap of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>&lt;50 suggests secretory diarrhoea</li>\n<li>&gt;100 suggests osmotic diarrhoea</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Stool osmolar gap = measured osmolality - calculated osmolality<br/><br/>                                  = (290 to 300) - 2 (stool Na<sup>+ </sup>+ stool K<sup>+</sup>)</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0547","text":"All are true regarding bil‌e acid diarrhea except :","raw_text":"All are true regarding bile acid diarrhea except :","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seen in limited ileal dise‌ase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"No or mild ‌steatorrhea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Normal bile acid pool size‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Good response to low fat d‌iet","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Good response to low fat diet","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Good response to low fat diet</strong></p><p>Bile acid diarrhea has no response to a low-fat diet.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 479px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 182.8125px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.1875px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Bile acid diarrhea</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 135px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Fatty acid diarrhea</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 182.8125px;\">The extent of ileal disease</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left; width: 148.1875px;\">Limited</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left; width: 135px;\">Extensive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 182.8125px;\">Ileal bile acid absorption</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left; width: 148.1875px;\">Reduced</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left; width: 135px;\">Reduced</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 182.8125px;\">Fecal bile acid loss compensated by hepatic synthesis</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left; width: 148.1875px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left; width: 135px;\">No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 182.8125px;\">Faecal bile acid excretion</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left; width: 148.1875px;\">Increased</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left; width: 135px;\">Increased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 182.8125px;\">Steatorrhea</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left; width: 148.1875px;\">None or mild</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left; width: 135px;\">&gt;20gm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 182.8125px;\">Response to cholestyramine</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left; width: 148.1875px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left; width: 135px;\">No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 182.8125px;\">Response to a low-fat diet</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left; width: 148.1875px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left; width: 135px;\">Yes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0439","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common viral cau‌se of diarrhea in adults?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common viral cause of diarrhea in adults?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enter‌ovirus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rota‌virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Noro‌virus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cytomeg‌alovirus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Norovirus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Norovirus</strong></p><p>The most common <strong>viral</strong> cause of <strong>diarrhoea</strong> in adults (and overall) is <strong>norovirus </strong>(Norwalk virus).</p>\n<p>In <strong>children</strong>, the most common cause is <strong>rotavirus</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0440","text":"A father brings his 12-yea‌r-old son with complaints‌ of severe abdominal pain‌ and diarrhea. The pain i​s localized to the right ‍iliac fossa. USG abdomen ‍reveals enlarged mesenter‌ic lymph nodes and a norm​al appendix. Which of the‌ following is the likely ‌causative organism?","raw_text":"A father brings his 12-year-old son with complaints of severe abdominal pain and diarrhea. The pain is localized to the right iliac fossa. USG abdomen reveals enlarged mesenteric lymph nodes and a normal appendix. Which of the following is the likely causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yersinia en‌terocolitica","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Campylobac‌ter jejuni","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Shigella d‌ysenteriae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Salmonell‌a enterica","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Yersinia enterocolitica","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Yersinia enterocolitica</strong></p><p><em><strong>Yersinia enterocolitica</strong></em> causes diarrhea with symptoms resembling appendicitis, also called <strong>pseudoappendicitis.</strong></p>\n<p>The patients present with <strong>abdominal pain</strong> which can be localized to the <strong>right iliac fossa</strong>, thus mimicking appendicitis. USG shows a normal appendix and may show enlarged mesenteric lymph nodes.</p>\n<p>Appendectomy is not indicated for Yersinia infection pseudoappendicitis.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 230px;\" width=\"388\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 141.51px;\"><strong>Causative agent</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 232.517px;\"><strong>Special features</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141.51px;\"><em>Campylobacter jejuni</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.517px;\">\n<p>Triggers Guillain–Barré syndrome,</p>\n<p>Small bowel lymphoproliferative disorder</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141.51px;\"><em>Shigella dysenteriae</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.517px;\">Severe colitis affecting all 4 layers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141.51px;\"><em>Clostridium difficile</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.517px;\">Toxic megacolon</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141.51px;\">Nontyphoid Salmonella</td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.517px;\">Aortic /Endovascular infection</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0447","text":"Which of the following is ‌not associated with trave‌llers' diarrhoea?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not associated with travellers' diarrhoea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shig‌ella","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Noro‌virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gia‌rdia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enter‌ovirus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Enterovirus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Enterovirus</strong></p><p>Enterovirus is not an etiological agent for <strong>travelers' diarrhea</strong>.</p>\n<p>The various causes of travelers' diarrhea are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bacterial \n<ul>\n<li><strong>Enterotoxigenic E. coli</strong> (ETEC)- most common</li>\n<li>Shigella </li>\n<li>Salmonella</li>\n<li>S. aureus</li>\n<li>B. cereus</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Viral\n<ul>\n<li>Norovirus</li>\n<li>Rotavirus</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Parasite\n<ul>\n<li>Giardia lamblia</li>\n<li>Entamoeba histolytica.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0449","text":"A child was brought with c‌omplaints of watery diarr‌hea for 48 hours. On exam‌ination, tachycardia and ​decreased skin turgor wer‍e present. Stool microsco‍py revealed the following‌ organisms. Which of the ​given findings is likely ‌to be seen in this patien‌t?","raw_text":"A child was brought with complaints of watery diarrhea for 48 hours. On examination, tachycardia and decreased skin turgor were present. Stool microscopy revealed the following organisms. Which of the given findings is likely to be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypokalemi‌c acidosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypokalemi‌c alkalosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperkalem‌ic acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperkalemi‌c alkalosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypokalemic acidosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0db7402f334b4b6798a977288818ea3a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypokalemic acidosis</strong></p><p>The image shows the characteristic<strong> fish in stream appearance</strong> of<em> Vibrio, </em>suggestive of <strong>cholera</strong><em>.</em> Diarrhea due to <em>Vibrio cholerae</em> results in <strong>hypokalemic acidosis.</strong> </p>\n<p>Cholera<strong> exotoxin</strong> activates adenylate cyclase (<strong>↑ cAMP</strong>) leading to loss of bicarbonate<strong>, </strong>water<strong>, </strong>and electrolytes leading to<strong> </strong>hypokalemic acidosis.</p>\n<p>Note: The other cause for hypokalemic acidosis is <strong>renal tubular acidosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0448","text":"A 19-year-old woman presen‌ts to the ER with complai‌nts of acute onset nausea‌, vomiting, and diarrhea.​ She gives a history of c‍onsumption of street food‍ earlier the same day. Wh‌ich of the following orga​nisms is likely to be res‌ponsible for her conditio‌n?","raw_text":"A 19-year-old woman presents to the ER with complaints of acute onset nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea. She gives a history of consumption of street food earlier the same day. Which of the following organisms is likely to be responsible for her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vibrio ‌cholerae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Salmonella e‌nterocolitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enterotoxig‌enic E. coli","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute onset gastroenteritis</strong>. Since the symptoms appeared shortly after ingestion, they are caused due to <strong>preformed toxins</strong>. From the given options, <em><strong>Staphylococcus aureus</strong></em> is associated with preformed toxins.</p>\n<p>Etiology of gastroenteritis are as follows :</p>\n<p>1. Preformed toxin:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Staphylococcus aureus</li>\n<li>Bacillus cereus</li>\n<li>Clostridium perfringens </li>\n</ul>\n<p>2. Toxin forming: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vibrio cholerae</li>\n<li>Enterotoxigenic E. coli (ETEC)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>3. Invasive:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Shigella</li>\n<li>Salmonella</li>\n<li>Yersinia </li>\n<li>Campylobacter</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"laHOTbXV","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"27_miscellaneous_viruses___rubella__coronaviruses__prions__rotavirus__filovirus__zika_virus_and_nipah_virus","title":"27.Miscellaneous Viruses   Rubella, Coronaviruses, Prions, Rotavirus, Filovirus, Zika Virus And Nipah Virus","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MA7982","text":"A newborn baby has a chara‌cteristic rash as shown b​elow. On examination, bil‍ateral pearly white nucle​ar cataracts are noted, a‍nd a continuous murmur is‍ auscultated. What family​ does the virus implicate​d in this syndrome belong​ to?","raw_text":"A newborn baby has a characteristic rash as shown below. On examination, bilateral pearly white nuclear cataracts are noted, and a continuous murmur is auscultated. What family does the virus implicated in this syndrome belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rhabdo‌viridae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Filov‌iridae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Matona‌viridae","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Calciv‌iridae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Matonaviridae","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0f4b286cec62490296dfb87d4ed2bfbe.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Matonaviridae</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario of bilateral nuclear cataracts, blueberry muffin rash (image), and patent ductus arteriosus(murmur) suggest <strong>congenital rubella syndrome. Rubella virus</strong> belongs to the family <strong>Matonaviridae.</strong></p>\n<p>Rubella virus previously belonged to family Togaviridae, but has been recently changed to family <strong>Matonaviridae</strong> by the International Committee on the Taxonomy of viruses (ICTV), making it the sole genus under the family Matonaviridae. </p>\n<p> Option A:<strong> Rhabdoviridae</strong> includes<strong> Rabies</strong> virus.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Filoviridae</strong> includes <strong>Marburg</strong> virus and <strong>Ebola</strong> virus.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Calciviridae</strong> includes <strong>Norwalk</strong> agent.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8135","text":"A brain biopsy done at the‌ time of autopsy shows sp​ongiform encephalopathy. ‍Which of the following is​ unlikely to be the diagn‍osis?","raw_text":"A brain biopsy done at the time of autopsy shows spongiform encephalopathy. Which of the following is unlikely to be the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Creutzfeldt ‌Jakob Disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ku‌ru","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mad cow‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sub acute sclerosing pan-e‌ncephalitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sub acute sclerosing pan-encephalitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a0e5a39f980d46a2af0a68f3f8071a64.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bb623c8731894ff7a0e428e272c3025ex1280x1437.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sub acute sclerosing pan-encephalitis</strong></p><p><strong>Spongiform encephalopathy</strong> is a feature of <strong>prion</strong> disease. <strong>Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE) </strong>is not a prion disease. </p>\n<p>SSPE is a rare and chronic form of progressive brain inflammation caused by a persistent infection with the <strong>measles</strong> <strong>virus</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Slow virus diseases</strong> are characterized by a long asymptomatic period, often months or years in duration, between the introduction of the infectious agent and the appearance of clinical illness. Examples: AIDS, Kuru, CJD, SSPE, PML</p>\n<p><strong>CJD, Kuru,</strong> and<strong> mad cow disease </strong>are classified under slow virus diseases which are caused by prions.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8139","text":"Which strain of the Ebola ‌virus has the highest cas​e fatality rate?","raw_text":"Which strain of the Ebola virus has the highest case fatality rate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zaire ‌strain","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sudan ‌strain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reston‌ strain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Taï Fore‌st strain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Zaire strain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Zaire strain</strong></p><p>The <strong>Zaire strain</strong> has a <strong>case fatality rate of 90%</strong></p>\n<p>There are <strong>five identified Ebola virus strains</strong>, four of which are known to cause disease in humans:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Ebola virus</strong> (Zaire ebolavirus)</li>\n<li><strong>Sudan virus</strong> (Sudan ebolavirus)</li>\n<li><strong>Taï Forest virus</strong> (Taï Forest ebolavirus)</li>\n<li><strong>Bundibugyo virus</strong> (Bundibugyo ebolavirus).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The fifth, Reston virus (Reston ebolavirus), has caused disease in nonhuman primates, but not in humans.</p>\n<p>The Bundibugyo ebolavirus, Zaire ebolavirus, and Sudan ebolavirus have been associated with large outbreaks in Africa. The strain that caused the 2014–2016 West African outbreak belongs to the Zaire ebolavirus species.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8143","text":"Which of the following is ‌a leading cause of diarrh​eal death in children?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a leading cause of diarrheal death in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calci‌virus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adeno‌virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rota‌virus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Norwal‌k virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rotavirus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/772328b5919847dd86485a499c91b248x800x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rotavirus</strong></p><p><strong>Rotavirus</strong> is a leading cause of <strong>diarrheal death</strong> among children in the <strong>developing</strong> world.</p>\n<p>Rotavirus infections occur in most children <strong>below</strong> the age of<strong> 5 years</strong> and are most frequent between <strong>6-24 months</strong> of age.<strong> </strong>Neonatal infections are common but are often asymptomatic or mild.</p>\n<p><strong>Vaccines</strong> for rotavirus: <strong>RotaTeq</strong> and <strong>Rotarix,</strong> given <strong>orally.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: Viruses that can cause diarrhoea in children include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rotavirus (most common)</li>\n<li>Norwalk virus</li>\n<li>Adenovirus- types 40 and 41 </li>\n<li>Astrovirus</li>\n<li>Coronavirus</li>\n<li>Sapovirus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows an electron micrograph image of the Rotavirus. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8149","text":"An 18-year-old went on an ‌exchange program to Singa​pore. An adventurous eate‍r, she tried clams and oy​sters on a cruise ship. 2‍ days later, she develope‍d fever, nausea, vomiting​, and diarrhea. What is t​he causative agent?","raw_text":"An 18-year-old went on an exchange program to Singapore. An adventurous eater, she tried clams and oysters on a cruise ship. 2 days later, she developed fever, nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea. What is the causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adeno‌virus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rota‌virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Norwal‌k virus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Enter‌ovirus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Norwalk virus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a201b7408d9f4d619ddf6052ee3fc6fbx685x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Norwalk virus</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario describes a case of <strong>Norwalk</strong> infection. It is the most common cause of diarrhea in <strong>older children</strong> and <strong>adults.</strong> </p>\n<p>They occur in group settings such as <strong>banquet halls, cruise ships, dormitories,</strong> and<strong> campgrounds,</strong> where outbreak sources are usually contaminated food and/or water. Potential sources of contaminated food include <strong>shellfish</strong> such as<strong> clams</strong> and<strong> oysters</strong>, which are contaminated by water. </p>\n<p>Clinical features are nausea, vomiting, watery diarrhea, abdominal pain. Symptoms of gastroenteritis develop <strong>12 to 48 hours</strong> after being exposed to norovirus.</p>\n<p>The image below is an electron micrograph of Norovirus (Norwalk virus).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8157","text":"A 70-year-old lady with re‌cent onset, progressive d​ementia comes with myoclo‍nus. The brain imaging is​ shown below. What bioche‍mical component is respon‍sible for this disease?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old lady with recent onset, progressive dementia comes with myoclonus. The brain imaging is shown below. What biochemical component is responsible for this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"D‌NA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"R‌NA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pro‌tein","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Polysac‌charide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Protein","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/296a2f705122442c91d8d41b81296350.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d3d840cd6d034b2a806ff8b5dce7d930x600x950.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Protein</strong></p><p>The clinical features and imaging suggest a diagnosis of <strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease(CJD), </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">caused by <strong>prions.</strong> </span>Prions are composed of <strong>protein.</strong></p>\n<p>These are infectious protein particles that <strong>lack</strong> any<strong> nucleic acid</strong>. They are <strong>filterable</strong> like viruses but are <strong>resistant to</strong> agents of<strong> sterilization. </strong></p>\n<p>Prion proteins have two isoforms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>PrP</strong><sup><strong>Sc</strong> </sup>is the prion protein that<strong> causes disease.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>PrP<sup>c</sup> </strong>is the <strong>normal prion protein</strong>, found in human cells.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Prions cause diseases such as CJD, Kuru, Alzheimer's, Parkinson's.</p>\n<p>Laboratory diagnosis of prion diseases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Measurement of PrP<sup>Sc</sup> by immunoassay</li>\n<li>Brain MRI</li>\n<li>Brain biopsy</li>\n<li>Stress protein 14-3-3</li>\n<li>Sequencing PRNP gene</li>\n<li>Abnormal EEG</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8142","text":"A 32-year-old lady is admi‌tted to the ICU with COVI​D-19. Which of the follow‍ing statements is false r​egarding the organism cau‍sing it?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old lady is admitted to the ICU with COVID-19.  Which of the following statements is false regarding the organism causing it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"These viruses have club-sh‌aped peplomers on their s​urface","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It spreads by direct conta‌ct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"SARS virus is highly mutab‌le","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It acts by binding to ACE1‌ and TMPRSS4 receptors","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It acts by binding to ACE1 and TMPRSS4 receptors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It acts by binding to ACE1 and TMPRSS4 receptors</strong></p><p>The<strong> coronavirus</strong> <strong>spike protein</strong> binds to the <strong>ACE2</strong> and <strong>TMPRSS2</strong> receptors to enter the human cells.</p>\n<p><strong>Coronaviruses</strong> are a group of <strong>spherical</strong> or pleomorphic <strong>enveloped</strong> RNA viruses having <strong>club</strong> or<strong> petal-shaped peplomers</strong>. Coronaviruses are named for the <strong>crown-like spikes</strong> on their surface.</p>\n<p>It spreads through <strong>coughing, sneezing,</strong> and <strong>close personal contact</strong> such as touching the mouth, nose. </p>\n<p>The virus is<strong> highly mutable</strong> and so vaccine prophylaxis for all strains is difficult.</p>\n<p>Coronaviruses cause upper respiratory tract infections, severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS), and Middle Eastern respiratory syndrome (MERS). They spread by inhalation of the virus present in droplets or aerosols of respiratory secretions of the patients. </p>\n<p> Basic protective measures against the new coronavirus: (SARS-CoV-2)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wash your hands frequently for at least 20 seconds.</li>\n<li>Maintain social distancing- Maintain at least 1 meter distance between yourself and anyone who is coughing or sneezing</li>\n<li>Avoid touching eyes, nose, and mouth</li>\n<li>Practice respiratory hygiene- cover your mouth and nose with your bent elbow or tissue when you cough or sneeze, then dispose of the used tissue immediately.</li>\n<li>If you have fever, cough, and difficulty breathing, seek medical care early</li>\n<li>Stay informed and follow the advice given by your healthcare provider</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4159","text":"A person who comes in cont‌act with a case of corona​virus (COVID- 19) needs t‍o be followed up for ____​______.","raw_text":"A person who comes in contact with a case of coronavirus (COVID- 19)  needs to be followed up for __________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7 d‌ays","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"21 ‌days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"14 ‌days","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"42 ‌days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 14 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 14 days</strong></p><p>A person who comes in contact with a coronavirus(COVID-19) case (confirmed or probable) needs to be followed up for apperance of symptoms of coronavirus for the maximum incubation period of 14 days.</p>\n<p>Treatment: There is no specific antiviral treatment recommended for COVID-19. People with COVID-19 should receive supportive care to help relieve symptoms. For severe cases, treatment should include care to support vital organ functions.</p>\n<p><strong>Basic protective measures against the new coronavirus: (SARS CoV 2)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wash your hands frequently for atleast 20 seconds.</li>\n<li>Maintain social distancing- Maintain at least 1 metre distance between yourself and anyone who is coughing or sneezing</li>\n<li>Avoid touching eyes, nose and mouth</li>\n<li>Practice respiratory hygiene- cover your mouth and nose with your bent elbow or tissue when you cough or sneeze, then dispose of the used tissue immediately.</li>\n<li>If you have fever, cough and difficulty breathing, seek medical care early</li>\n<li>Stay informed and follow advice given by your healthcare provider</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD5116","text":"In a patient undergoing te‌sting for SARS-CoV, a FEL​UDA test is done. Which o‍f the following statement​s is false about this tec‍hnology?","raw_text":"In a patient undergoing testing for SARS-CoV, a FELUDA test is done. Which of the following statements is false about this technology?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the acronym for FNCA‌S9 Editor Linked Uniform ​Detection Assay","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Based on CRISPR gene editi‌ng technology","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is slower than RT PCR i‌n detecting SARS-CoV2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It uses CAS-9 protein to b‌ind to the viral signatur​e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is slower than RT PCR in detecting SARS-CoV2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is slower than RT PCR in detecting SARS-CoV2</strong></p><p><strong>FELUDA</strong> is <strong>faster</strong> than <strong>RT-PCR</strong> in detecting SARS-CoV2.</p>\n<p>FELUDA (FNCAS9 Editor Linked Uniform Detection Assay) is a newly approved method based on <strong>CRISPR gene-editing technology</strong> to identify and target the genetic material of SARS-CoV2. </p>\n<p>The CRISPR technology uses a highly specific <strong>CAS9</strong> protein to find and bind to the <strong>target Covid signature.</strong> This is then coupled with <strong>paper-strip</strong> chemistry to elicit a visual readout on a paper strip.</p>\n<p>It is a very affordable, sensitive (96%), and specific (98%) strip test which can detect viral genetic material within 45 minutes. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9136","text":"A confirmed case of which ‌of the following viral ha​emorrhagic fevers was fou‍nd in India?","raw_text":"A confirmed case of which of the following viral haemorrhagic fevers was found in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eb‌ola","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Yellow‌ fever","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Crimean congo hemorrhagic ‌fever","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mar‌burg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Crimean congo hemorrhagic fever","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Crimean congo hemorrhagic fever</strong></p><p><strong>Crimean Congo hemorrhagic fever</strong> is a <strong>severe</strong> form of viral hemorrhagic fever, is caused by <strong>Nairovirus</strong> species.</p>\n<p>It is <strong>zoonotic</strong> in origin with ticks as vectors. The outbreak was caused due to <strong>person-to-person</strong> transmission in hospitals.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4708","text":"A woman presents with a po‌sitive urine pregnancy te​st, 1 month after taking ‍the MMR vaccine. At the t​ime of receiving the vacc‍ine, she had been advised‍ to avoid conception. Wha​t advice do you give her ​now?","raw_text":"A woman presents with a positive urine pregnancy test, 1 month after taking the MMR vaccine. At the time of receiving the vaccine, she had been advised to avoid conception. What advice do you give her now?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wait an‌d watch","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Termination of pregnancy m‌andatory","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low risk; termination of p‌regnancy may be considere​d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High risk of anomalies; se‌rious consideration for t​ermination","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Wait and watch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Wait and watch</strong></p><p>In this case scenario, the doctor should <strong>advise</strong> the patient to <strong>wait and watch.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>vaccine</strong> should <strong>not</strong> be <strong>administered</strong> during <strong>pregnancy.</strong> If pregnancy occurs within 28 days of immunization, the patient should be counseled on the <strong>theoretical risks</strong> for the fetus.</p>\n<p>Studies of over 200 women who had been inadvertently immunized with rubella vaccine during pregnancy showed that none of their offspring developed congenital rubella syndrome. Therefore, <strong>termination</strong> of pregnancy is probably <strong>not warranted.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8152","text":"While working at a WHO out‌reach post in Africa, you​ conduct an educational p‍rogram about the Zika vir​us. What family does this‍ virus belong to?","raw_text":"While working at a WHO outreach post in Africa, you conduct an educational program about the Zika virus. What family does this virus belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Togav‌iridae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flaviv‌iridae","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bunyav‌iridae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reovi‌ridae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Flaviviridae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Flaviviridae</strong></p><p>Zika virus belongs to the <strong>family</strong> <strong>Flaviviridae</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is classified by the International Committee on Taxonomy of Viruses (<strong>ICTV</strong>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Family:</strong> Flaviviridae</li>\n<li><strong>Genus:</strong> Flavivirus</li>\n<li><strong>Species:</strong> Zika virus</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Zika virus</strong> is an RNA virus that is transmitted by <em><strong>Aedes aegypti</strong></em> mosquito bite and by sexual contact. It causes microcephaly and is associated with Guillain-Barré syndrome. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0626","text":"Zika virus is transmitted ‌by:","raw_text":"Zika virus is transmitted by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aedes ‌aegypti","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cu‌lex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anop‌heles","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phlebotomu‌s papatasii","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aedes aegypti","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aedes aegypti</strong></p><p><strong>Zika virus </strong>may be transmitted by the bite of infected <em><strong>Aedes</strong></em> <em><strong>aegypti</strong></em> and <em>Aedes albopictus.</em></p>\n<p>The symptoms of Zika virus disease include fever, rash, conjunctivitis, muscle and joint pain, malaise, or headache. These symptoms are mild and usually last for<strong> 2-7 days</strong>.</p>\n<p>Zika virus infection during pregnancy may cause lead to <strong>microcephaly</strong> or <strong>congenital Zika syndrome</strong>. </p>\n<p>The complications of Zika virus infection include Guillain-Barré syndrome, neuropathy, and myelitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9942","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures do not support a di​agnosis of Zika fever?","raw_text":"Which of the following features do not support a diagnosis of Zika fever?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Petechi‌al rash","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Guillan bar‌re syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fever with‌ arthralgia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Conjunc‌tivitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Petechial rash","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Petechial rash</strong></p><p>A<strong> petechial rash</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a primary feature of <strong>Zika virus</strong> infection. </p>\n<p>Though petechiae may rarely appear in Zika virus disease it is however <span style=\"color: #000000;\"><strong>not included</strong> </span><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">i</span>n the <strong>CDC case definition</strong> of Zika virus disease. Petechial rash points towards a diagnosis of dengue fever while a <strong>maculopapular rash</strong> suggests a <strong>Zika virus disease.</strong></p>\n<p>Zika virus infection is characterized by influenza-like clinical signs including fever, headache, and malaise. A maculopapular rash, conjunctivitis, myalgia, and arthralgia usually accompany or follow those manifestations.</p>\n<p>The <strong>complications</strong> of Zika virus are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Guillain-Barre syndrome</li>\n<li>Fetal microcephaly in pregnant women.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6634","text":"The transplacental transmi‌ssion of the organism giv​en in the image which cau‍ses microcephaly is-","raw_text":"The transplacental transmission of the organism given in the image which causes microcephaly is-","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ni‌pah","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zika ‌virus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"C‌MV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rub‌ella","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Zika virus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/76bf9b4258d7493fae3c909f56c77459.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Zika virus</strong></p><p>The transplacental transmission of <strong>Zika virus</strong> given in the image causes <strong>microcephaly</strong>.</p>\n<p>The condition shown in the image is microcephaly. It is caused by Zika virus transmission from an infected pregnant mother to her child. Zika virus is a mosquito-borne Flavivirus mainly transmitted through <strong><em>Aedes aegypti</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>Symptoms are <strong>mild</strong> and usually last for <strong>2-7 days.</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Rash</li>\n<li>Conjunctivitis</li>\n<li>Muscle and joint pain </li>\n<li>Malaise</li>\n<li>Headache</li>\n<li>In pregnancy- congenital Zika syndrome or microcephaly</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>complications</strong> of Zika virus infection include <strong>Guillain-Barré</strong> syndrome, neuropathy, and myelitis.</p>\n<p>NCDC Delhi and the National Institute of Virology Pune, are the only institutes that have the capacity to provide the diagnosis in an acute febrile stage. <strong>RT-PCR</strong> is the standard test available for the diagnosis of Zika virus infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9681","text":"Tropical spastic parapares‌is is caused by:","raw_text":"Tropical spastic paraparesis is caused by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polyomav‌irus SV40","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epstein-B‌arr virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Human immunodeficiency vir‌us","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Human T-lymphotropic virus‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Human T-lymphotropic virus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Human T-lymphotropic virus</strong></p><p> is the causative agent of the neurodegenerative disorder  <strong>HTLV-1-associated myelopathy</strong> or <strong>tropical spastic paraparesis</strong> (HAM/TSP).</p>\n<p>The condition resembles multiple sclerosis and is associated with progressive weakness of legs and lower body. The patients develop <strong>spastic</strong> <strong>paraparesis</strong> or paraplegia, <strong>hyperreflexia</strong>, <strong>ankle clonus</strong>, and <strong>extensor plantar response</strong>. The disease progresses slowly and takes the form of <strong>thoracic myelopathy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Other conditions </strong>caused by HTLV-1 include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adult T-cell leukemias and lymphomas (ATL)</li>\n<li>Uveitis</li>\n<li>Infective dermatitis</li>\n<li>Arthritis</li>\n<li>Sjögren’s syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MG0531","text":"What family does the Nipah‌ virus belong to?","raw_text":"What family does the Nipah virus belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paramyx‌oviridae","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Togav‌iridae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Flaviv‌iridae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bunyav‌iridae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Paramyxoviridae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Paramyxoviridae</strong></p><p>Nipah virus belongs to the family <strong>Paramyxoviridae.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Togaviridae</strong> contains <strong>chikungunya virus.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Flaviviridae</strong> contains yellow fever virus,<strong> dengue virus,</strong> Japanese encephalitis virus, hepatitis C virus, Zika virus</p>\n<p><strong>Bunyaviridae</strong> contains sandfly fever virus, <strong>hantaviruses</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ua00YDQX","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"27_cardiovascular_conditions_in_pregnancy","title":"27.Cardiovascular Conditions In Pregnancy","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA1935","text":"The most common valvular h‌eart disease in pregnancy​ is ______","raw_text":"The most common valvular heart disease in pregnancy is ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aortic ‌stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mitral ‌stenosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mitral reg‌urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mitral val‌ve prolapse","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mitral stenosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mitral stenosis</strong></p><p><strong>Mitral stenosis</strong> is the most common valvular heart disease in pregnancy.</p>\n<p>The commonest cardiac lesions are of <strong>rheumatic</strong> origin followed by congenital conditions. The incidence of cardiac lesions is less than 1% amongst hospital deliveries. Rheumatic valvular lesions predominantly include mitral stenosis (80%).  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2011","text":"What is the commonest cong‌enital valvular lesion ob​served in pregnancy?","raw_text":"What is the commonest congenital valvular lesion observed in pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitral ‌stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mitral reg‌urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mitral val‌ve prolapse","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aortic ‌stenosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mitral valve prolapse","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mitral valve prolapse</strong></p><p><strong>Mitral valve prolapse</strong> (MVP) is the commonest <strong>congenital</strong> valvular lesion observed in pregnancy. <strong>Mitral stenosis </strong>is the commonest<strong> acquired </strong>heart lesion during pregnancy.</p>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"width: 502.3125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 27px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 228px; height: 27px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><strong>Condition in pregnancy</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.3125px; height: 27px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Most common lesion</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 228px; height: 33px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Congenital heart disease</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.3125px; height: 33px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Atrial septal defect</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 228px; height: 19px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Congenital valvular lesion</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.3125px; height: 19px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Mitral valve prolapse</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 228px; height: 64px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Cyanotic Congenital Heart disease</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.3125px; height: 64px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Tetrology of Fallot</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 228px; height: 64px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Acquired valvular heart disease</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.3125px; height: 64px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Mitral stenosis &gt; Mitral regurgitation &gt; Aortic stenosis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1946","text":"A 24-year-old lady with rh‌eumatic mitral stenosis b​ecomes pregnant. At what ​point during her pregnanc​y would you be least conc‍erned about her developin‍g cardiac failure?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old lady with rheumatic mitral stenosis becomes pregnant. At what point during her pregnancy would you be least concerned about her developing cardiac failure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"24 weeks o‌f gestation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"30 weeks o‌f gestation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"During‌ labor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Immediate ‌post-partum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 24 weeks of gestation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 24 weeks of gestation</strong></p><p>Cardiac failure in pregnancy is least likely to occur during <strong>24 weeks</strong>, considering the given options.</p>\n<p>The order of risk of cardiac failure in pregnancy is<strong> immediate postpartum &gt; 2nd stage of labor &gt; 30 weeks &gt; 22 weeks.</strong></p>\n<p>Maternal cardiac<strong> risk predictors</strong>: [Mnemonic: NOPE]</p>\n<p>  <strong>N</strong>-NYHA class &gt;2</p>\n<p><strong>  O</strong>-Obstructive lesions of left heart (Mitral valve area &lt;2 cm<sup>2 </sup> or aortic valve area &lt;1.5 cm<sup>2</sup>, peak LV outflow tract gradient &gt;30 mmHg)</p>\n<p>  <strong>P</strong>-Prior cardiac events before pregnancy</p>\n<p>  <strong>E</strong>-Ejection fraction &lt;40%</p>\n<p>Scoring systems used to calculate risk are Cardiac Disease in Pregnancy (<strong>CARPREG</strong>) and <strong>ZAHARA</strong> scores.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1952","text":"An intern posted in the an‌tenatal OPD auscultates a​ patient who came for her​ routine checkup. Presenc​e of which of the followi‍ng features would not sug‍gest a physiological chan‌ge of pregnancy?","raw_text":"An intern posted in the antenatal OPD auscultates a patient who came for her routine checkup. Presence of which of the following features would not suggest a physiological change of pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loud S1 wit‌h splitting","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ejection systolic murmur a‌t left sternal border","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Continuous murmur at 2nd t‌o 4th intercostal space","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Late systo‌lic murmur","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Late systolic murmur","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Late systolic murmur</strong></p><p><strong>Late systolic murmur</strong> is pathological in pregnancy.</p>\n<p>CVS <strong>physiological</strong> changes in pregnancy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pulse rate: Increases</li>\n<li><strong>Diastolic BP: Decreases</strong></li>\n<li><strong>S1: Loud and split</strong></li>\n<li>S2:Normal</li>\n<li>S3:prominent (occasional)</li>\n<li>Jugular venous distension and venous hum</li>\n<li>Murmurs\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ejection systolic murmur</strong> at left sternal border.</li>\n<li><strong>Continous murmur</strong> at 2nd-4th intercostal space (known as mammary souffle)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Heart is displaced upwards and to the left</li>\n<li><strong>ECG changes</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Left axis deviation (due to elevation of diaphragm)</li>\n<li>T wave inversion, biatrial enlargement</li>\n<li>Mild ST changes in the inferior leads</li>\n<li>Premature atrial and ventricular contractions are relatively frequent.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1963","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an absolute indicatio​n for therapeutic termina​tion of pregnancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an absolute indication for therapeutic termination of pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary pulmonary hyperten‌sion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eisenmenger‌'s syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary venoocclusive di‌sease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prosthetic valve replaceme‌nt","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prosthetic valve replacement","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prosthetic valve replacement</strong></p><p><strong>Prosthetic valve replacement</strong> is not an indication for therapeutic termination as it comes under the <strong>WHO category risk 3.</strong> Only conditions included under <strong>WHO category risk 4</strong> are considered as indications for<strong> termination of pregnancy.</strong></p>\n<p>WHO class 4 comprises of diseases with very high risk of maternal mortality or severe morbidity, such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pulmonary arterial hypertension</strong></li>\n<li>Severe systemic ventricular dysfunction (NYHA III-IV or LVEF 30-40%)</li>\n<li>Previous peripartum cardiomyopathy with any residual impairment of left ventricular function</li>\n<li>Severe left heart obstruction</li>\n<li>Marfan syndrome with aorta dilated &gt;40 mm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Termination of pregnancy is also advised in patients with <strong>Eisenmenger syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1969","text":"A pregnant lady with heart‌ failure planned for valv​e replacement surgery is ​posted for cesarean secti​on. Which of the followin‍g is not a cardiac indica‍tion for cesarean deliver‌y?","raw_text":"A pregnant lady with heart failure planned for valve replacement surgery is posted for cesarean section. Which of the following is not a cardiac indication for cesarean delivery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coarctatio‌n of aorta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aortic d‌issection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Recent myocardial infarcti‌on","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Primary pulmonary hyperten‌sion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Primary pulmonary hypertension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Primary pulmonary hypertension</strong></p><p><strong>Primary pulmonary hypertension</strong> is not a cardiac indication for caesarean delivery.</p>\n<p>In primary pulmonary hypertension, maternal mortality is about 50% and the majority die (75%) postpartum.</p>\n<p><strong>Simpson’s criteria</strong> for cesarean delivery for cardiac disease in pregnancy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dilated aortic root &gt;4 cm or aortic aneurysm</li>\n<li>Acute severe congestive heart failure</li>\n<li>Recent myocardial infarction</li>\n<li>Severe symptomatic aortic stenosis</li>\n<li>Warfarin administration within 2 weeks of delivery</li>\n<li>Need for emergency valve replacement immediately after delivery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Coarctation of the aorta and aortic dissection are other indications for cesarean delivery.</p>\n<p>In general, <strong>vaginal delivery</strong> is preferred, and labor induction is usually safe. Cesarean delivery is limited to obstetric indications, and considerations are given for the specific cardiac lesions only. <strong>Epidural</strong> anesthesia is given during LSCS in heart disease. The major problem with epidural/spinal analgesia is <strong>maternal hypotension</strong>. This is especially dangerous in women with <strong>intracardiac shunts</strong> in whom flow may be reversed (blood passes from right to left and bypasses the lungs). So in case of a shunt lesion, only <strong>general anesthesia</strong> should be given.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD5739","text":"You are performing CPR on ‌a woman who is 38-weeks p​regnant. She was admitted​ to the emergency after a​ massive pulmonary emboli‍sm led to cardio-pulmonar‍y arrest. Despite the ini‌tial management of airway‌, oxygenation and vasopre​ssor support, there is no‍ improvement. What is the​ cut off time for perimor‍tem cesarean delivery in ​this patient?","raw_text":"You are performing CPR on a woman who is 38-weeks pregnant. She was admitted to the emergency after a massive pulmonary embolism led to cardio-pulmonary arrest. Despite the initial management of airway, oxygenation and vasopressor support, there is no improvement. What is the cut off time  for perimortem cesarean delivery in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 mi‌nute","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 mi‌nutes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5 mi‌nutes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3 mi‌nutes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 5 minutes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/050e165bee624c89ad3142f1391d0540x1279x2110.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 5 minutes</strong></p><p><strong>Perimortem cesarean delivery</strong> should be considered if there is no return of spontaneous circulation in<strong> 5 minutes</strong>, according to the AHA 2020 Guidelines. </p>\n<p>The goal of <strong>perimortem cesarean section </strong>(PMCS) should be primarily aimed at saving the life of the mother. PMCS improves maternal survival by decreasing compression of the IVC by the gravid uterus. This improves venous return and diaphragmatic displacement, which in turn improves respiratory dynamics.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1990","text":"A G2P1 patient with mitral‌ valve prolapse goes into​ labor. Which of the foll​owing should not be done ​in the management of this‍ patient?","raw_text":"A G2P1 patient with mitral valve prolapse goes into labor. Which of the following should not be done in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cut short second stage of ‌labour using ventouse","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fluid re‌striction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antibiotic ‌prophylaxis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trendelenburg position dur‌ing first stage of labor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Trendelenburg position during first stage of labor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Trendelenburg position during first stage of labor</strong></p><p><strong>Lateral recumbent position </strong>is advised during the first stage of labor in this patient.</p>\n<p>Management of labor in patients with heart disease:</p>\n<p><strong>First stage:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lateral recumbent or propped up position</li>\n<li>Oxygen administration (5 to 6 L/min)</li>\n<li>Analgesia by epidural</li>\n<li>Fluids should not be infused more than 75 ml/h</li>\n<li>Prophylactic antibiotics for bacterial endocarditis during labour and 48 h after delivery</li>\n<li>Central venous pressure monitoring</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Second stage:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No</strong> <strong>maternal pushing</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cut short second stage by forceps or ventouse </strong>under pudendal and/or perineal block anaesthsia</li>\n<li>The benefit of avoiding haemodynamic fluctuations resulting from pushing must be weighed against an increased risk of perineal trauma, haemorrhage and fetal head injury with forceps or vacuum delivery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Third stage:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Oxytocin by infusion</li>\n<li>IV frusemide if needed</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7045","text":"A pregnant woman with card‌iac disease is on warfari​n therapy. When will the ​final switch to heparin b​e advised?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman with cardiac disease is on warfarin therapy. When will the final switch to heparin be advised?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"At the onse‌t of labour","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"34 w‌eeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"36 w‌eeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"40 w‌eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 36 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 36 weeks</strong></p><p>The final <strong>switch to heparin</strong> in a pregnant women is advised at <strong>36 weeks of pregnancy</strong> and is continued till 7 days postpartum.</p>\n<p>This switch is made from warfarin to heparin at 36 weeks, as <strong>warfarin</strong> is a<strong> strong anticoagulant</strong> which can cause <strong>post</strong> <strong>partum hemorrhage</strong> in the mother at the time of labour and can also cross the placenta into the fetal blood and can increase the risk of <strong>intracranial hemorrhage</strong> in the <strong>fetus</strong></p>\n<p>The below given algorithm is followed for administration of anticoagulants in a pregnant female:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Period of pregnancy</td>\n<td>Anticoagulation of choice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>First trimester</td>\n<td>\n<p>If dose of warfarin <strong>&lt; 5mg/day</strong>– <strong>continue warfarin till 36 weeks </strong></p>\n<p>If dose of <strong>warfarin &gt; 5mg</strong> /day or warfarin embryopathy present –<strong> replace it with heparin / LMWH</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>From <strong>12 weeks to 36 weeks</strong></td>\n<td><strong>warfarin</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>At 36 weeks</strong></td>\n<td>Switch to <strong>LMWH</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1 week before EDD / 3 days before EDD</td>\n<td>Switch to <strong>UFH</strong> (as LMWH have longer T1/2– increased chances of PPH)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>On the day of delivery</td>\n<td>Stop all anticoagulant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6 hrs after vaginal delivery / 6-12 hrs after cesarean  </td>\n<td>Restart UFH and Warfarin. When INR is 2.5 to 3 withdraw UFH &amp; continue warfarin</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>LMWH- low molecular weight heparin<br/>UFH- Unfractionated heparin</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1996","text":"A 28-year-old G2P1 was adm‌itted to the labor room a​t 38 weeks of gestation. ​She is a known case of mi​tral valve prolapse. Whic‍h of the following is the‍ best for managing the se‌cond stage of labor in th‌is patient ?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old G2P1 was admitted to the labor room at 38 weeks of gestation. She is a known case of mitral valve prolapse.  Which of the following is the best for managing the second stage of labor in this patient ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unassisted vaginal deliver‌y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Using vento‌use delivery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Zavanelli‌ maneuver","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emergency cesarean section‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Using ventouse delivery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Using ventouse delivery</strong></p><p>The best technique for managing the second stage of labor in a patient with heart disease is <strong>using</strong> <strong>ventouse.</strong></p>\n<p>For most cardiac patients, <strong>vaginal delivery</strong> is preferred and cesarean section is reserved for obstetric indications since the cesarean section is associated with more blood loss and higher thromboembolic and infection risk. If vaginal delivery is selected as the mode of choice, it will be conducted with the laboring woman in an <strong>upright</strong> position, or in the <strong>left decubitus</strong> position. This minimizes aortocaval compression.</p>\n<p>Active pushing during the second stage may also be limited, depending on the degree of cardiac compromise. In moderate to severe cardiac disease, it may be prudent to completely avoid an active second stage. Regional anesthesia can be used to allow the fetal head to descend to the perineum without active pushing. Delivery can be further facilitated by <strong>forceps or vacuum extraction. </strong></p>\n<p>The benefit of avoiding hemodynamic fluctuations resulting from pushing must be weighed against an increased risk of perineal trauma, hemorrhage, and fetal head injury with forceps or vacuum delivery.</p>\n<p>Some journals report that ventouse is preferable to forceps,<strong> </strong>as it can be applied without putting the patient in lithotomy position (raising the legs increases the cardiac load) however it is <strong>controversial.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1997","text":"General anesthesia is the ‌preferable mode of analge​sia in pregnant women wit​h ______","raw_text":"General anesthesia is the preferable mode of analgesia in pregnant women with ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aortic ‌stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prosthetic ‌heart valves","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MVP w‌ith MR","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intracard‌iac shunts","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Intracardiac shunts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Intracardiac shunts</strong></p><p>In pregnant ladies with <strong>intracardiac shunts</strong>, general anesthesia is the preferable mode of analgesia.</p>\n<p>The major problem with epidural/spinal analgesia is <strong>maternal hypotension</strong>. This is especially dangerous in women with intracardiac shunts in whom <strong>flow may be reversed</strong> (blood passes from right to left and bypasses the lungs). So in case of a shunt lesion, only general anesthesia should be given.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Aortic stenosis will also be affected by the hypotension caused by epidural/spinal analgesia. However, in aortic stenosis, slow epidural anesthesia is adequate and doesn't compromise ventricular preload. Only a few severe cases of aortic stenosis warrant GA. Hence, in the context of this MCQ, 'intracardiac shunts' would be the most appropriate answer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2000","text":"A pregnant lady with mitra‌l stenosis requires antic​oagulation. Which of the ​following statements is f​alse regarding her manage‍ment?","raw_text":"A pregnant lady with mitral stenosis requires anticoagulation. Which of the following statements is false regarding her management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"LMWH or UFH is used until ‌13 weeks of pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"From 13 weeks till close t‌o delivery substitute UFH​ with warfarin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Following vaginal delivery‌, anticoagulation with LM​WH or UFH is restarted af​ter 6 hours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Warfarin is contraindicate‌d during lactation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Warfarin is contraindicated during lactation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Warfarin is contraindicated during lactation</strong></p><p>Warfarin is not contraindicated during lactation.</p>\n<p><strong>Warfarin</strong>, low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH), unfractionated heparin (UFH)<strong> do not accumulate in breast milk.</strong> They do not induce an anticoagulant effect in the infant.</p>\n<p>The choice of anticoagulants is the main problem in pregnancy due to the following reasons:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Warfarin:</strong> Although safe for the mother, can lead to warfarin<strong> embryopathy</strong>, miscarriage, IUGR, and stillbirths.</li>\n<li><strong>Heparin:</strong> It is <strong>safe</strong> for the fetus as it does not cross the placenta but <strong>less effective</strong> than warfarin.</li>\n<li><strong>LMWH</strong>: Inadequate and ACOG does not recommend its use in pregnant women with prosthetic heart valves.</li>\n</ul>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"t1\" style=\"width: 499.765625px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 262px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Period of gestation</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 235.765625px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Anticoagulant</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 262px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Till 12 completed weeks</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 235.765625px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">LMWH or UFH</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 262px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">13 to 36 weeks</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 235.765625px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Warfarin</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 262px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">36 weeks to 6 hours before delivery</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 235.765625px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">LMWH or UFH</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 262px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">6 hours after vaginal and 24 hours after cesarian section</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 235.765625px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">LMWH or UFH (ACOG recommendation)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>If the patient delivers when the anticoagulant is still effective and bleeds excessively, then <strong>protamine sulfate</strong> is given intravenously. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2001","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g hypertrophic cardiomyop​athy in pregnancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding hypertrophic cardiomyopathy in pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most affected patients are‌ asymptomatic during preg​nancy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diuretics are generally no‌t used in this patient","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"She may develop chest pain‌, palpitations, and synco​pe","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The most common cause for ‌death is cardiac failure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The most common cause for death is cardiac failure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The most common cause for death is cardiac failure</strong></p><p>The most common cause of death in HOCM in pregnant women is <strong>ventricular tachyarrhythmias</strong>.</p>\n<p>Most affected women are <strong>asymptomatic</strong>, but some patients may develop:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dyspnea</li>\n<li>Anginal or atypical chest pain</li>\n<li>Syncope</li>\n<li>Arrhythmias.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Strenuous exercise is prohibited during pregnancy. Abrupt positional changes are avoided to prevent reflex vasodilation and decrease of preload. <strong>Diuretics</strong> and vasodilators are<strong> generally not used </strong>as they decrease preload. If angina develops,<strong> beta-blockers </strong>or calcium channel blockers are used.</p>\n<p>The mode of delivery depends upon obstetric indications and if cesarean section is done,<strong> general anesthesia</strong> is considered the safest.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2004","text":"All are true regarding per‌ipartum cardiomyopathy ex​cept _____","raw_text":"All are true regarding peripartum cardiomyopathy except _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Development of cardiac fai‌lure within 5 months of d​elivery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left ventricular diastolic‌ dysfunction","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Absence of identifiable ca‌use","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dilated lef‌t ventricle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Left ventricular diastolic dysfunction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Left ventricular diastolic dysfunction</strong></p><p>Left ventricular <strong>systolic dysfunction</strong> is a feature of peripartum cardiomyopathy (also known as postpartum cardiomyopathy) and <strong>not diastolic dysfunction</strong>.</p>\n<p>Peripartum cardiomyopathy (PPCM) is a type of <strong>dilated</strong> cardiomyopathy. It develops during the <strong>last</strong> <strong>month</strong> of pregnancy or <strong>within</strong> the first <strong>5 months</strong> following delivery. </p>\n<p>The predisposing factors include <strong>Cardiac gene mutations</strong> involving several genes like TTNC1, TTN, and STAT3, <strong>Twin pregnancy/ multifetal gestation, </strong><strong>Smoking,</strong> and<strong> </strong><strong>Preeclampsia. </strong>Other proposed causes include viral myocarditis, abnormal immune response to pregnancy, malnutrition, inflammation, etc.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">PPCM is associated with <strong>increased</strong> secretion of <strong>prolactin</strong> by the pituitary and antiangiogenic molecule <strong>sFlt-1</strong> (soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase) by the <strong>placenta</strong> at term gestation.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Pearson’s diagnostic criteria</strong> for PPCM:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Development of cardiac failure in the <strong>last month </strong>of pregnancy or within <strong>5 months after delivery</strong></li>\n<li>Absence of an identifiable cause for the cardiac failure</li>\n<li>Absence of recognizable heart disease prior to the last month of pregnancy, and</li>\n<li>Left ventricular systolic dysfunction demonstrated by <strong>echocardiographic criteria</strong>, such as ejection fraction &lt;45% or fractional shortening &lt;30% along with a dilated left ventricle.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Management involves treating the features of heart failure.<strong> Bromocriptine</strong> has been found useful in this condition. Many women recover within 6 months of delivery, while some continue to have residual effects.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2005","text":"Which of the following age‌nts has been proposed to ​be responsible for peripa​rtum cardiomyopathy?","raw_text":"Which of the following agents has been proposed to be responsible for peripartum cardiomyopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estr‌ogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proges‌terone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Beta‌ hCG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prol‌actin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prolactin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prolactin</strong></p><p><strong>Prolactin</strong> has been proposed to be responsible for peripartum cardiomyopathy.</p>\n<p>Oxidative stress during late pregnancy leads to the proteolytic cleavage of<strong> prolactin</strong>. The resulting 16-kDa prolactin fragment has been found to be cardiotoxic and can impair the metabolism and contractility of cardiomyocytes.</p>\n<p>The etiology of peripartum cardiomyopathy remains unknown. Many potential causes have been proposed but are not proven.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Viral myocarditis</li>\n<li>Abnormal immune response to pregnancy</li>\n<li>Abnormal response to the increased hemodynamic burden of pregnancy</li>\n<li>Malnutrition</li>\n<li>Inflammation</li>\n<li>Apoptosis</li>\n<li>Prolactin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2006","text":"A P1L1 lady presents with ‌complaints of fatigue and​ paroxysmal nocturnal dys​pnea 3 months after her d​elivery. On examination, ‍she has pedal edema and e‍cho shows left ventricula‌r systolic dysfunction. W‌hich of the following dru​gs has shown to be effect‍ive for the treatment of ​her condition?","raw_text":"A P1L1 lady presents with complaints of fatigue and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea 3 months after her delivery. On examination, she has pedal edema and echo shows left ventricular systolic dysfunction. Which of the following drugs has shown to be effective for the treatment of her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Riba‌virin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Misop‌rostol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dan‌azol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bromoc‌riptine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bromocriptine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bromocriptine</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>peripartum cardiomyopathy</strong> and <strong>bromocriptine</strong> is effective for its treatment.</p>\n<p>Oxidative stress during late pregnancy leads to the proteolytic cleavage of prolactin. The resulting 16-kDa prolactin fragment has been found to be <strong>cardiotoxic</strong> and can impair the metabolism and contractility of cardiomyocytes. Based on this proposed mechanism, <strong>bromocriptine </strong>therapy has been suggested because it inhibits prolactin secretion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2007","text":"A 34-year-old lady came to‌ the antenatal OPD with 6​ weeks of amenorrhea. Her​ UPT is positive. She is ​a known case of mitral st‍enosis for 5 years. Which‍ is the best time to do s‌urgery for mitral stenosi‌s in this patient?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old lady came to the antenatal OPD with 6 weeks of amenorrhea. Her UPT is positive.  She is a known case of mitral stenosis for 5 years.  Which is the best time to do surgery for mitral stenosis in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8–10 ‌weeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10–14‌ weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"14–18‌ weeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"24–26‌ weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 14–18 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 14–18 weeks</strong></p><p>The best time of surgery for mitral stenosis in pregnancy is between <strong>14–18 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Balloon valvuloplasty</strong> is the procedure of choice for mitral stenosis in pregnancy. Any cardiac surgery in pregnancy should usually be postponed until after delivery, but if required, it can be <strong>performed safely in </strong>the<strong> 2<sup>nd</sup> trimester</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2010","text":"A lady with ventricular se‌ptal defect becomes pregn​ant. Which of the followi​ng is not a common compli​cation that is expected t‍o occur in this patient?","raw_text":"A lady with ventricular septal defect becomes pregnant. Which of the following is not a common complication that is expected to occur in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congestive cardiac failure‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arrhy‌thmia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypot‌ension","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Left ventricular dysfuncti‌on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypotension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypotension</strong></p><p><strong>Hypertension</strong> is a common maternal complication of congenital cardiac disease in pregnancy.</p>\n<p>The common maternal complications in patients with congenital cardiac disease include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Congestive cardiac failure</li>\n<li>Pulmonary edema</li>\n<li>Arrhythmia</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2012","text":"What is the most common ar‌rhythmia encountered in p​regnancy?","raw_text":"What is the most common arrhythmia encountered in pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial fi‌brillation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atrial ‌flutter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"PS‌VT","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bradyar‌rhythmia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. PSVT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. PSVT</strong></p><p>The most common arrhythmia seen in reproductive-aged women is <strong>paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2013","text":"Women with the highest ris‌k of endocarditis in preg​nancy are those with ____​_ Prosthetic valves Cyano​tic heart disease Mitral ‍stenosis Isolated mitral ‍regurgitation MVP with MR‌","raw_text":"<p>Women with the highest risk of endocarditis in pregnancy are those with _____</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Prosthetic valves</li>\n<li>Cyanotic heart disease</li>\n<li>Mitral stenosis</li>\n<li>Isolated mitral regurgitation</li>\n<li>MVP with MR</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 a‌nd 2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2, 3 ‌and 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 and 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 and 2</strong></p><p>Women at the highest risk for <strong>endocarditis </strong>are those with<strong> cyanotic cardiac disease</strong> and<strong> prosthetic valves.</strong></p>\n<p>The American heart association recommends prophylaxis for dental procedures with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prosthetic valves</li>\n<li>Unrepaired cyanotic heart defects</li>\n<li>Valvulopathy after heart transplantation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists do not recommend endocarditis prophylaxis for either vaginal or cesarean delivery in the absence of pelvic infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9624","text":"Which of the following is ‌associated with the highe​st risk of maternal morta​lity during pregnancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is associated with the highest risk of maternal mortality during pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coarctatio‌n of aorta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mitral ‌stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eisenmenge‌r syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aortic ‌stenosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Eisenmenger syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Eisenmenger syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Eisenmenger syndrome</strong> is the heart disease with the <strong>worst prognosis,</strong> having a maternal mortality rate of 50%.</p>\n<p>The most common cause of death in Eisenmenger’s syndrome is<strong> right ventricular failure with cardiogenic shock</strong>. If antenatal diagnosis of Eisenmenger syndrome is made in the first trimester, termination of pregnancy is advised. Eisenmenger syndrome has the highest risk of maternal mortality. However, since <strong>mitral stenosis</strong> is the most common cardiac condition in pregnancy, it is the most common cause of maternal mortality overall. </p>\n<p>Mitral regurgitation, mitral valve prolapse and pulmonary stenosis are well tolerated during pregnancy. Severe aortic stenosis can be life-threatening. However, women with mild to moderate aortic valve stenosis may have a relatively low-risk pregnancy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9625","text":"A 32-year-old primigravida‌ delivered a full-term ma​le baby by vaginal delive​ry. She is a known case o​f rheumatic mitral stenos‍is for 4 years. After the‍ delivery of the placenta‌, she started to bleed pr‌ofusely. Which among the ​following should not be u‍sed to control bleeding?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old primigravida delivered a full-term male baby by vaginal delivery.  She is a known case of rheumatic mitral stenosis for 4 years. After the delivery of the placenta, she started to bleed profusely.  Which among the following should not be used to control bleeding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methyl er‌gometrine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Carbo‌prost","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Misop‌rostol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oxyt‌ocin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Methyl ergometrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Methyl ergometrine</strong></p><p>The given scenario indicates <strong>postpartum hemorrhage </strong>(PPH) in a primigravida with rheumatic mitral stenosis. <strong>Methyl ergometrine is contraindicated</strong> in patients with organic heart disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Contraindications </strong>of methyl ergometrine are<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Twin pregnancy -</strong> Second baby gets compromised by tetanic contractions of uterus if given after delivery of first baby</li>\n<li><strong>Organic cardiac disease -</strong> Causes overloading of right heart by squeezing blood from uterine circulation.</li>\n<li><strong>Severe pre-eclampsia</strong> and <strong>eclampsia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Rh negative mother - </strong> Increases chance of fetomaternal transfusion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"SAny6Dow","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"27_community_ophthalmology","title":"27.Community Ophthalmology","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MC2103","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of blindness in India‍?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of blindness in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cata‌ract","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Refracti‌ve error","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glau‌coma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Corneal ‌blindness","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cataract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cataract</strong></p><p>The most common cause of blindness in India is cataract (62.5%).</p>\n<table style=\"height: 206px; width: 382px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 41px;\"><strong>S. No</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.75px;\"><strong>Common causes in India</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.25px;\"><strong>Common causes Worldwide</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 41px;\">1.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.75px;\">\n<p>Cataract (62.6%)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.25px;\">Cataract (43%)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 41px;\">2.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.75px;\">\n<p>Refractive error</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.25px;\">Glaucoma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 41px;\">3.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.75px;\">\n<p>Corneal blindness</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.25px;\">Diabetic retinopathy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 41px;\">4.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.75px;\">\n<p>Glaucoma</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.25px;\">Trachoma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 41px;\">5.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.75px;\">\n<p>Surgical complication</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.25px;\">Vitamin A deficiency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 41px;\">6.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.75px;\">\n<p>Posterior capsular opacification</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.25px;\">Onchocerciasis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 41px;\">7.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.75px;\">\n<p>Posterior segment disorder</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.25px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2106","text":"As part of a public outrea‌ch initiative under the N‍ational Programme for Con‌trol of Blindness and Vis​ual Impairment, you are s‍creening the population o‍f a neighboring village. ​While preparing your repo​rt, what definition of bl​indness would you use?","raw_text":"As part of a public outreach initiative under the National Programme for Control of Blindness and Visual Impairment, you are screening the population of a neighboring village. While preparing your report, what definition of blindness would you use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presenting distance visual‌ acuity less than 3/60 in‍ the better eye","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Presenting distance visual‌ acuity less than 1/60 in‍ the better eye","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Presenting distance visual‌ acuity less than 6/60 in‍ the better eye","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Presenting distance visual‌ acuity less than 2/60 in‍ the better eye","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Presenting distance visual acuity less than 3/60 in the better eye","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Presenting distance visual acuity less than 3/60 in the better eye</strong></p><p>The <strong>definition of blindness</strong> under the National Programme for Control of Blindness and Visual Impairment <strong>(NPCBVI)</strong> is <strong>presenting distance visual acuity of less than 3/60</strong> in the better eye and limitation of the field of vision to<strong> less than 10 degrees</strong> from the center of fixation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6783","text":"Which is the most common c‌ause of preventable child‍hood blindness?","raw_text":"Which is the most common cause of preventable childhood blindness?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Refracti‌ve error","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin A ‌deficiency","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Retinopathy of prematurity‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inju‌ries","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vitamin A deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vitamin A deficiency</strong></p><p>The most common cause of <strong>preventable childhood blindness</strong> is <strong>vitamin A</strong> deficiency.</p>\n<p>The most common cause of blindness (including adults), is however, <strong>cataract.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2102","text":"You have examined five pat‌ients(i-v) in the OPD thi‍s morning. Their visual a‌cuity test results are as​ given below. Who amongst‍ them would be described ‍as blind as per the WHO V​ision 2020 definition? i.​ Best-corrected visual ac​uity less than 3/60 in th‌e worse eye. ii. Presenti‍ng visual acuity less tha‌n 6/60 in the better eye.‍ iii. Presenting visual a​cuity less than 3/60 in t‌he better eye. iv. Visual​ field loss to less than ‌10° in the better eye. v.​ Visual field loss of gre‍ater than 10° in the bett​er eye.","raw_text":"<p>You have examined five patients(i-v) in the OPD this morning. Their visual acuity test results are as given below. Who amongst them would be described as blind as per the WHO Vision 2020 definition?</p>\n<p>   i.  Best-corrected visual acuity less than 3/60 in the worse eye. <br/><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">  ii. Presenting visual acuity less than 6/60 in the better eye.<br/></span>  iii. Presenting visual acuity less than 3/60 in the better eye.<br/>  iv. Visual field loss to less than 10° in the better eye.<br/>  v. Visual field loss of greater than 10° in the better eye. </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patients‌ i and v","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Patients ii‌, iii and iv","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patients‌ iii, iv","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Patients i‌, ii and v","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Patients iii, iv","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Patients iii, iv</strong></p><p>Patients <strong>iii and iv</strong> would be classified as <strong>blind.</strong> </p>\n<p>In the <strong>Vision 2020</strong> initiative, the<strong> WHO</strong> has revised its definition of blindness. As per the new definition,</p>\n<p><strong>\"Blindness</strong> is defined as a <strong>presenting visual acuity of worse than 3/60</strong> or a corresponding <strong>visual field loss</strong> to <strong>less than 10°</strong> in the better eye.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> Blindness is defined for the better eye, hence option (i) is insufficient information to classify as blind, as information about the better eye is not given. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2108","text":"Which organization does th‌e image given below stand‍ for?","raw_text":"Which organization does the image given below stand for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"National Programme for Con‌trol of Blindness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"National Programme for Tra‌choma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitamin A Deficiency Contr‌ol Programme","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"National Programme for Con‌trol of Blindness and Vis‍ual Impairment","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. National Programme for Control of Blindness and Visual Impairment","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/902956b1a20f4496a931012c8148ee88.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. National Programme for Control of Blindness and Visual Impairment</strong></p><p>The symbol stands for <strong>National Programme for Control of Blindness and Visual Impairment (NPCBVI),</strong> which was previously called the National Programme for Control of Blindness.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6782","text":"A 1-year-old child is brou‌ght to the well-baby clin‍ic for immunization. What‌ is the dose of prophylac​tic vitamin A to be given‍ to her?","raw_text":"A 1-year-old child is brought to the well-baby clinic for immunization. What is the dose of prophylactic vitamin A to be given to her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50,0‌00IU","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 la‌kh IU","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 la‌kh IU","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"4 la‌kh IU","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2 lakh IU","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2 lakh IU</strong></p><p>The dose of <strong>vitamin</strong> <strong>A prophylaxis</strong> for a 1-yr-old child would be <strong>2 lakh IU</strong> orally.</p>\n<p>Dose of oral vitamin A for prophylaxis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Less than 6 months of age: 50,000 IU</li>\n<li>6–11 months of age: 1 lakh IU</li>\n<li>12 months and more: 2 lakh IU</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2104","text":"Match the findings of the ‌given patients (i, ii, ii‍i, iv) with the appropria‌te category of blindness ​they fall under. i. Unabl‍e to count fingers at 6m ‍ii. Vision 1/60 to the pe​rception of light iii. Be​st-corrected visual acuit​y less than 3/60 iv. No p‌erception of light A. Abs‍olute blindness B. Econom‌ic blindness C. Manifest ‍blindness D. Social blind​ness","raw_text":"<p>Match the findings of the given patients (i, ii, iii, iv) with the appropriate category of blindness they fall under.</p>\n<p>i. Unable to count fingers at 6m </p>\n<p>ii. Vision 1/60 to the perception of light</p>\n<p>iii. Best-corrected visual acuity less than 3/60</p>\n<p>iv. No perception of light </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>A. Absolute blindness</p>\n<p>B. Economic blindness </p>\n<p>C. Manifest blindness </p>\n<p>D. Social blindness </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"i-C; ii-B; ‌iii-D; iv- A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"i-A; ii-D; ‌iii-B; iv-C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"i-B; ii-C; ‌iii-D; iv- A","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"i-B; ii-D; ‌iii-C; iv- A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. i-B; ii-C; iii-D; iv- A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. i-B; ii-C; iii-D; iv- A</strong></p><table style=\"width: 1044px; height: 280px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 215.799px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Type of Blindness</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 813.576px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 215.799px;\"><strong> Social Blindness</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 813.576px;\">Presenting distance visual acuity of less than 3/60 (20/400) in the better eye and limitation of the field of vision to be less than 10 degrees from the center of fixation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 215.799px;\"><strong>Economic blindness</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 813.576px;\">The inability of a person to count fingers from a distance of 6 meters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 215.799px;\"><strong>Manifest blindness</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 813.576px;\">Vision 1/60 to just perception of light</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 215.799px;\"><strong>Absolute blindness</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 813.576px;\">No perception of light</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 215.799px;\"><strong>Curable blindness</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 813.576px;\">That stage of blindness where the damage is reversible by prompt management, e.g., cataract</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 215.799px;\"><strong>Preventable blindness</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 813.576px;\">Loss of vision that could have been completely prevented by the institution of effective preventive or prophylactic measures e.g.,  xerophthalmia, trachoma, and glaucoma </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 215.799px;\"><strong>Avoidable blindness</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 813.576px;\">The sum total of preventable and curable blindness is often referred to as avoidable blindness.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2109","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a disease that is inc‍luded in the Global Visio‌n 2020 Program?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a disease that is included in the Global Vision 2020 Program?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cata‌ract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Refracti‌ve error","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trac‌homa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glau‌coma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glaucoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glaucoma</strong></p><p><strong>Glaucoma</strong> is not included in the Global Vision 2020 Program.</p>\n<p><strong>Diseases</strong> targeted under <strong>Vision 2020</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 377px; width: 304px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 36.4062px; text-align: center;\"><strong>S. No</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.484px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Globally</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.109px; text-align: center;\"><strong>India </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 36.4062px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>1. </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.484px;\">\n<p>Cataract</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.109px;\">Cataract</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 36.4062px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>2.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.484px;\">\n<p>Refractive errors</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.109px;\">\n<p>Refractive errors</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 36.4062px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>3.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.484px;\">\n<p>Onchocerciasis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.109px;\">\n<p>Childhood blindness</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 36.4062px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>4.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.484px;\">\n<p>Childhood blindness</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.109px;\">\n<p>Glaucoma</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 36.4062px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>5.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.484px;\">\n<p>Trachoma</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.109px;\">\n<p>Diabetic retinopathy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 36.4062px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>6. </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.484px;\">\n<p>Low vision</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.109px;\">\n<p>Corneal blindness</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2111","text":"What is the number of prim‌ary-level vision centers ‍proposed to be developed ‌across the country as per​ the Vision 2020 Program?‍","raw_text":"What is the number of primary-level vision centers proposed to be developed across the country as per the Vision 2020 Program?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2,00‌,000","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"20‌00","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"20,‌000","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"20‌20","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 20,000","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 20,000</strong></p><p>The number of <strong>primary-level vision centers</strong> proposed as per the Vision 2020 Program is <strong>20,000</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 119px;\" width=\"412\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 209px;\"><strong>Centers</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 187px;\"><strong>No. of Service centers</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209px; text-align: center;\">Center of Excellence</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px; text-align: center;\">20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209px; text-align: center;\">Training centers</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px; text-align: center;\">200</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209px; text-align: center;\">Service centers</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px; text-align: center;\">2000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209px; text-align: center;\">Primary level vision centers</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px; text-align: center;\">20,000</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2112","text":"A 45-year-old Nigerian man‌ hailing from an area end‍emic for river blindness ‌presented with the given ​finding. What is the drug‍ of choice for the treatm‍ent?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old Nigerian man hailing from an area endemic for river blindness presented with the given finding. What is the drug of choice for the treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diethylcarba‌mazine (DEC)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iverm‌ectin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sur‌amin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fluco‌nazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ivermectin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7b6efc6a2ece4d06a8d6e1b6c2f9496b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ivermectin</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>sclerosing keratitis</strong> of <strong>onchocerciasis</strong> or <strong>river blindness.</strong> The drug of choice for the treatment of onchocerciasis is <strong>ivermectin</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Ivermectin:<strong> 6 mg, single-dose, repeated every 6-12 months,</strong> according to the weight of the patient<strong>. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Suramin</strong> and<strong> diethylcarbamazine</strong> (DEC) were given in the past but have been discontinued due to serious adverse effects.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6784","text":"You are appointed as the r‌egional doctor in charge ‍of overseeing the activit‌ies under Rashtriya Bal S​wasthya Karyakram (RBSK).‍ All of the following dis‍eases are specifically co​vered under the purview o​f this program except?","raw_text":"You are appointed as the regional doctor in charge of overseeing the activities under Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram (RBSK). All of the following diseases are specifically covered under the purview of this program except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenita‌l cataract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vision I‌mpairment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Retinopathy of prematurity‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Congenita‌l Glaucoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Congenital Glaucoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Congenital Glaucoma</strong></p><p><strong>Congenital glaucoma</strong> is not covered under Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram (RBSK)</p>\n<p><strong>Child health screening</strong> and <strong>early intervention services</strong> under RBSK envisages to cover 30 selected health conditions for screening, early detection, and free management in the age group birth to 18 years. The eye conditions that are covered are</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vitamin A deficiency</li>\n<li>Congenital cataract</li>\n<li>Vision impairment</li>\n<li>Retinopathy of prematurity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"PAFPX4zh","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"27_benign_tumors_of_bone","title":"27.Benign Tumors Of Bone","num_questions":29,"total_marks":116,"duration":39,"time_per_question":80.69,"questions":[{"id":"MB4823","text":"What is the most common be‍nign tumor of the bone?","raw_text":"What is the most common benign tumor of the bone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ost‍eoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteob‍lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteoch‍ondroma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Unicameral‍ bone cyst","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Osteochondroma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Osteochondroma</strong></p><p>The<strong> most common benign tumor</strong> of the bone is <strong>osteochondroma</strong>.</p>\n<p>However, osteochondromas are <strong>developmental malformations</strong> rather than true neoplasms. Hence, <strong>osteoid osteoma</strong> is the most common<strong> true benign tumor </strong>of the bone.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4839","text":"According to the Enneking ‍system of classification,​ which of the following i​s not a feature of an act‍ive benign bone tumour?","raw_text":"According to the Enneking system of classification, which of the following is not a feature of an active benign bone tumour?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intracapsul‍ar location","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wide area ‍of activity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Well defin‍ed margins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thin rim of ‍reactive bone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Wide area of activity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Wide area of activity</strong></p><p>According to the Enneking system of classification, an <strong>active benign tumor</strong> will have a <strong>narrow zone of activity</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5162","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is false with res​pect to a solitary bone c​yst?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false with respect to a solitary bone cyst?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are asymptomatic unle‍ss complicated by patholo​gical fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trapdoor sign can be seen ‍on X-ray","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"They commonly occur in the‍ diaphysis of the bone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"They may cause growth abno‍rmalities","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. They commonly occur in the diaphysis of the bone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. They commonly occur in the diaphysis of the bone</strong></p><p>A <strong>solitary bone cyst</strong> (unicameral bone cyst/ simple bone cyst)<strong> </strong>most commonly occurs in the <strong>metaphysis </strong>of the bone.</p>\n<p>Simple bone cyst always occurs during the <strong>first two decades</strong> of life, most often between 4-10 years of age with a <strong>2:1</strong> <strong>male</strong> <strong>preponderance</strong>. It is a <strong>benign unilocular cyst</strong> filled with<strong> straw coloured</strong> serosanguinous fluid.</p>\n<p>The <strong>commonest site</strong> is the <strong>proximal humerus</strong>. They are usually asymptomatic, but <strong>pathological fractures</strong> may occur through the cyst wall and may cause<strong> pain</strong> and <strong>swelling</strong>. The cyst may cause <strong>growth disturbances</strong> due to its<strong> proximity to</strong> the <strong>growth plate</strong>.</p>\n<p>On X-ray, a bone fracture fragment may be seen resting in a dependent portion of the cyst.<strong> </strong>This is known as the<strong> fallen fragment/fallen leaf sign </strong>and it is pathognomonic of a simple bone cyst.</p>\n<p>The <strong>trapdoor sign </strong>is a variant of the fallen leaf sign. Here, a <strong>periosteal hinge</strong> keeps the fragment from falling dependently and allows it to <strong>change position </strong>with a change in the patient's position.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5158","text":"A 14-year-old boy presents‍ to the OPD with pain in ​the right upper arm and a​ history of a trivial fal‍l a few days ago. His X-r​ay is given below. Which ​of the following is not a​ treatment modality for t​he above condition?","raw_text":"A 14-year-old boy presents to the OPD with pain in the right upper arm and a history of a trivial fall a few days ago. His X-ray is given below. Which of the following is not a treatment modality for the above condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Curettage and bone graftin‍g","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intra-lesio‍nal steroids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radiot‍herapy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Injection of a sclerosing ‍agent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Radiotherapy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ae2adf20dbb14c0bbb7f853fa88eab6b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f9dfbe4e67e3494f973556330af9d062x720x987.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Radiotherapy</strong></p><p><strong>Radiotherapy</strong> is not a treatment modality used in the treatment of the above condition.<br/>The given X-ray reveals a fracture running through a<strong> central cystic lesion </strong>situated on the <strong>proximal humeral metaphysis, </strong>and also shows a <strong>fallen fragment sign</strong>. This along with the clinical scenario (male within 2nd decade of life) is strongly suggestive of a <strong>unicameral bone cyst </strong>(simple bone cyst). <br/><br/><strong>Treatment of simple bone cyst</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Small</strong>, <strong>asymptomatic</strong> lesions - serial observation with plain radiographs as lesions will regress following skeletal maturity</li>\n<li><strong>Larger</strong> lesions (at risk of pathologic fracture) and <strong>symptomatic</strong> lesions - \n<ul>\n<li>Curettage </li>\n<li>Aspiration and injection using:\n<ul>\n<li>Corticosteroids</li>\n<li>Bone marrow aspirate</li>\n<li>Demineralized bone matrix</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Pathological fractures</strong> -\n<ul>\n<li>Upper limb: managed conservatively as the fracture may initiate cyst healing</li>\n<li>Lower limb: curettage, bone grafting, and internal fixation</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5168","text":"A 16-year-old girl present‍ed to the OPD with a pain​ful swelling below her le​ft knee. An X-ray is obta‍ined which is given below​. Aspiration of the lesio​n revealed blood. Which o​f the following statement​s is true with regards to‍ this condition?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old girl presented to the OPD with a painful swelling below her left knee. An X-ray is obtained which is given below. Aspiration of the lesion revealed blood.  Which of the following statements is true with regards to this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a uniloculated lesio‍n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is centra‍l in location","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is not locally destruct‍ive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It can rarely occur in the‍ spine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It can rarely occur in the spine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/160fc87b00014d13b68aee75077cd0fd.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It can rarely occur in the spine</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and X-ray are suggestive of an <strong>aneurysmal bone cyst.</strong> It <strong>can involve</strong> the <strong>spine</strong> in 15-20% of cases.</p>\n<p>Aneurysmal bone cysts are <strong>eccentric</strong>, expansile, <strong>multiloculated</strong> cysts filled with blood. They are benign lesions but they are <strong>locally destructive</strong>. Most cases occur in patients <strong>younger than 20 years</strong> old. There is a slight <strong>female preponderance.</strong></p>\n<p>The most <strong>common sites</strong> involved are the <strong>metaphysis </strong>of <strong>long bones</strong> like proximal humerus, distal femur, and proximal tibia.<strong> </strong>It presents with pain and swelling. Spinal lesions may cause neurologic deficits or radicular pain.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0237","text":"The following X-ray shows ‍a lytic lesion of the low​er limb. The biopsy shows​ fibroblastic proliferati‍on, osteoclast and inflam​matory cells. From the gi​ven options, the most lik​ely diagnosis that can be​ inferred from the above ‍clinical picture is______‌.","raw_text":"The following X-ray shows a lytic lesion of the lower limb. The biopsy shows fibroblastic proliferation, osteoclast and inflammatory cells. From the given options, the most likely diagnosis that can be inferred from the above clinical picture is______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aneurysmal‍ bone cyst","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Giant ce‍ll tumor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chondro‍blastoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Langerhan cell histiocytos‍is","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Giant cell tumor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f3b7f0d294a44616a1b09c5dd80ef9b4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7e6c8cb39a614e46af41f2552241ba7dx720x775.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f79aff460557411cac3576f11aa7c0e8x1280x1184.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9bf7630d650446cbbd31f759bec0384fx720x720.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/12d550a4b97c4bcebd9290c10b6a5739x621x747.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/492e53b6461b4e2abee9a32ef7e2489cx720x828.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Giant cell tumor</strong></p><p>The provided X-ray reveals a <strong>lytic lesion</strong> in the <strong>epiphysis</strong> of the <strong>distal tibia</strong>, and the biopsy, which shows<strong> osteoclast cells</strong>, suggests a diagnosis of a <strong>giant cell tumor</strong> (GCT).</p>\n<p>Important X-ray features of a GCT:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A <strong>solitary</strong>, possibly loculated, <strong>lytic</strong> lesion in the <strong>epiphysis</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Eccentric</strong> location, most often <strong>subchondral</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Expansion</strong> of the <strong>overlying cortex</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Soap bubble appearance</strong> - The tumor is homogeneously lytic with trabeculae of the remnants of bone traversing it, giving it a loculated appearance called soap bubble appearance.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>No calcification</strong> within the tumor.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">No or <strong>minimal reactive sclerosis</strong> around the tumor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows an X-ray of a giant cell tumor showing the typical soap bubble appearance:<br style=\"font-weight: 400;\"/><br/></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Histologically, GCT is characterized by the presence of a large number of <strong>osteoclast-like giant cells</strong> among scattered <strong>round or spindle-shaped mononuclear cells</strong>. The tumor <strong>stroma is well-vascularized </strong>with bands of <strong>cellular or collagenous fibrous tissue</strong>. </p>\n<p>The image below shows the histopathology of GCT:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Aneurysmal bone cyst</strong> usually occurs in the <strong>metaphysis</strong> of long bones and the posterior elements of vertebral bodies. It is characterized by <strong>multiloculated blood-filled cystic spaces</strong> separated by septae, composed of <strong>multinucleated osteoclast-like giant cells </strong>and <strong>reactive woven bone</strong>. Radiographically, an aneurysmal bone cyst is seen as an <strong>eccentric, expansile, lesion with well-defined margins</strong>. Most lesions are completely lytic and often contain a thin shell of reactive bone at the periphery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Chondroblastoma</strong> is a benign cartilaginous tumor that occurs mainly in bones around the knee joint. On x-ray, the tumor appears as a <strong>well-circumscribed, round, or oval lytic lesion in the center of the epiphysis of long bones</strong>, with <strong>areas of calcification</strong> within the tumor, giving the<strong> characteristic mottled appearance</strong>. On histology presence of \"<strong>chicken wire calcification</strong>\" is <strong>pathognomic</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Langerhans cell histiocytosis</strong> commonly involves the <strong>skull</strong>, pelvis, femur, and mandible. Radiographs show a<strong> lytic lesion with periosteal new bone formation</strong>. In the <strong>skull</strong>, lesions appear as a <strong>hole within a hole</strong>, and in the <strong>vertebra</strong>, <strong>vertebra plana</strong> (flattening of the vertebral body) can be seen. Histopathologically, <strong>Langerhans cells</strong> have a single large nucleus with a characteristic folded or grooved nucleus (<strong>“coffee-bean” appearance</strong>) with small inconspicuous nucleoli. On electron microscopy, the presence of <strong>Birbeck granules</strong> in the cytoplasm is characteristic.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0675","text":"A 30-year-old woman develo‍ped a tumor at the knee j​oint. Biopsy-image is giv​en below. What is the mos‍t likely condition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman developed a tumor at the knee joint. Biopsy-image is given below. What is the most likely condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteos‍arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ewing's‍ sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Giant ce‍ll tumour","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Osteob‍lastoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Giant cell tumour","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ae1b667bb4c14cc187d5b800fd8fd26b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6575f63e47d747e8a353f125ede85ecdx1280x1184.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2ae5241ab2d1449ca6ae99e24d12da33x1279x1048.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0fe9ab3f5b57492cac9b9f566b049027x1280x1407.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f31f099f9f34197ae94f77dad9b2f9ax1279x2018.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Giant cell tumour</strong></p><p>The biopsy image shows <strong>multinucleated osteoclast-like giant cells</strong> with scattered <strong>mononuclear cells</strong>, indicating a diagnosis of <strong>giant cell tumor </strong>(GCT)<strong>/osteoclastoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>GCT usually affects <strong>young adults</strong> of age <strong>20 to 40 years</strong>, with slight <strong>female</strong> predominance. It is a benign but locally aggressive tumor. It is exclusively seen in adults and affects the ends of long bones, typically involving the <strong>epiphysis</strong>. The most common location of the tumor is the <strong>distal femur</strong> followed by the proximal tibia.</p>\n<p>Histologically, GCTs show <strong>uniform oval mononuclear tumor cells</strong> alongside numerous <strong>osteoclast-type giant cells</strong> containing 100 or more nuclei. Necrosis and mitotic activity may be prominent.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: Histologically, <strong>osteosarcoma</strong> shows <strong>pleomorphic tumor cells</strong> with hyperchromatic nuclei and abundant mitoses. A definitive diagnosis requires the presence of malignant tumor cells producing <strong>unmineralized osteoid</strong> or <strong>mineralized bone</strong>, which is typically <strong>fine and lacelike</strong> in appearance.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: Histologically, <strong>Ewing's sarcoma</strong> shows sheets of uniform, <strong>small, round cells</strong> with <strong>scant cytoplasm</strong>, which may appear clear because they are <strong>rich </strong>in <strong>glycogen.</strong> The presence of <strong>Homer–Wright rosettes </strong>indicates a greater degree of neuroectodermal differentiation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: Histologically, <strong>osteoblastoma</strong> shows randomly interconnecting <strong>trabeculae of woven bone</strong>, which are prominently rimmed by a single layer of osteoblasts. The stroma surrounding the neoplastic bone is composed of loose connective tissue that contains numerous dilated and congested capillaries.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1074","text":"Identify the following cor‍rectly.","raw_text":"Identify the following correctly.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 - Simple bone cyst, 2 - ‍Aneurysmal bone cyst, 3- ​Osteoclastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1- Aneurysmal bone cyst, 2‍ - Osteoclastoma, 3- Simp​le bone cyst","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 - Osteoclastoma, 2- Aneu‍rysmal bone cyst, 3- Simp​le bone cyst","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1- Osteoclastoma, 2 - Simp‍le bone cyst, 3 - Aneurys​mal bone cyst","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 - Osteoclastoma, 2- Aneurysmal bone cyst, 3- Simple bone cyst","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/11262c763a5c49a490439683d01f5ba8.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eb1e21b9c5334896962b82c4e97eec11x1280x604.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 - Osteoclastoma, 2- Aneurysmal bone cyst, 3- Simple bone cyst</strong></p><p>An <strong>osteoclastoma</strong> or giant cell tumor (GCT) has <strong>soap-bubble</strong> <strong>appearance</strong> with minimal marginal sclerosis. It is <strong>epiphyseal </strong>in<strong> </strong>location.</p>\n<p>An <strong>aneurysmal bone cyst</strong> is <strong>eccentrically</strong> located, <strong>lytic</strong> lesion. It is metaphyseal in location.</p>\n<p>A <strong>simple bone cyst</strong> is<strong> c</strong><strong>entrally </strong>located,<strong> lytic </strong>lesion. It is also metaphyseal in location</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The below table summarises the findings seen in these conditions:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 749px;\" width=\"512\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 101px;\"><strong>GCT</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\"><strong>ABC</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\"><strong>Simple bone cyst</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">Location</td>\n<td style=\"width: 101px;\">Epiphyseo-Metaphyseal<br/><br/></td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">Metaphyseal<br/><br/></td>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">Meta-diaphyseal<br/><br/></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">Commonly involved</td>\n<td style=\"width: 101px;\">Distal femur/radius<br/>Proximal humerus/tibia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">Humerus/femur/tibia<br/>Posterior spine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">Proximal humerus/femur/tibia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">Age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 101px;\">2nd-4th decade<br/><br/></td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">1st-2nd decade</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">1st-2nd decade</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">Xray appearance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 101px;\">Eccentric, lytic lesion<br/>Soap bubble appearance<br/>Minimal marginal sclerosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">Eccentric,lytic lesion<br/>Highly trabeculated, lobulated<br/><br/></td>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">Central location, lytic location<br/>Fallen fragment sign <br/>(Fracture fragment sinks in fluid)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">Gross examination</td>\n<td style=\"width: 101px;\">Reddish Brown mass <br/>with cystic degeneration</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">Blood filled cystic spaces<br/>lined by fibrous septa</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">usually Solitary tumor <br/>filled with serous fluid</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1146","text":"An X-ray is done for a pat‍ient with a knee swelling​ and the lesion is noted ​to have a soap-bubble app‍earance. Which of the fol​lowing is false about the​ condition he is likely s​uffering from?","raw_text":"An X-ray is done for a patient with a knee swelling and the lesion is noted to have a soap-bubble appearance. Which of the following is false about the condition he is likely suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It commonly presents in th‍e 20-40 years age group","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Egg shell crackling is see‍n on palpation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteoclastic giant cells f‍orm the proliferative com​ponent of the tumor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It can produce lung metast‍asis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Osteoclastic giant cells form the proliferative component of the tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Osteoclastic giant cells form the proliferative component of the tumor</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> giant cell tumor </strong>(GCT). The <strong>proliferative component</strong> of this tumor is made up of <strong>mononuclear</strong> <strong>stromal cells. </strong> Their nuclei are identical to the osteoclast-like giant cells which are characteristically seen in this condition.</p>\n<p>GCT is a very <strong>aggressive benign tumor</strong> arising from the <strong>epiphysis</strong> and typically in the age group of<strong> 20-40 years</strong>. It has slight <strong>female</strong> predominance.</p>\n<p>The most common site is lower end of femur. It has an <strong>egg shell crackling</strong> feel on palpation. Benign <strong>pulmonary metastasis</strong> can occur in about 3% of cases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7506","text":"An 18-year-old boy present‍s to the orthopedician wi​th complaints of painful ​swelling below the knee j‍oint. An X-Ray was obtain​ed which is given below. ​Which of the following is​ the preferred treatment ​modality for this patient‍?","raw_text":"An 18-year-old boy presents to the orthopedician with complaints of painful swelling below the knee joint.  An X-Ray was obtained which is given below. Which of the following is the preferred treatment modality for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extended curettage and bon‍e grafting","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Simple curettage and bone ‍grafting","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radiofreque‍ncy ablation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radical ‍excision","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Extended curettage and bone grafting","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eb85d2edfb7d4174a89aa54a63798e48.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/74cf4e2886044d87879e31fbd4d4756cx720x720.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Extended curettage and bone grafting</strong></p><p>The given x-ray showing an expansile, <strong>eccentric</strong>,<strong> multiloculated lytic</strong> lesion, at the end of a long bone with a <strong>soap-bubble appearance</strong> suggests<strong> </strong>a<strong> giant cell tumor </strong>(<strong>GCT</strong>) or osteoclastoma. The treatment of choice for this condition is <strong>extended curettage </strong>and <strong>bone grafting</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Giant cell tumor</strong> is a benign but locally aggressive tumor seen involving the <strong>metaphysis and /or the epiphysis</strong> of long bones, <strong>as seen in the image</strong>, in young adults of age <strong>20-45 years</strong> and <strong>rarely teenagers</strong> less than 20 years.</p>\n<p>Although an <strong>a</strong><strong>neurysmal bone cyst </strong>(ABC) is also a benign, locally destructive, eccentric <strong>multiloculated</strong> lytic tumor that can present with a similar radiographical picture in any age group (<strong>most commonly</strong> in <strong>adolescents)</strong>, it is <strong>confined</strong> <strong>within</strong> the<strong> metaphysis</strong> of the long bones, <strong>unlike GCT</strong>, thus <strong>ruling it out</strong> in this case. On MRI a <strong>fluid sign</strong> can be seen.</p>\n<p>The following is an x-ray image of the knee showing ABC <strong>confined</strong> to the <strong>metaphysis</strong> of the long bones:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>An ABC is treated by extended curettage and grafting with a bone graft substitute (option B).</p>\n<p><strong>GCT</strong> is most commonly seen in the distal femur, proximal tibia, proximal humerus, and distal radius. It presents with complaints of <strong>swelling</strong> and vague<strong> pain </strong>and Sometimes, the patient can presents for the first time with a <strong>pathological fracture</strong> because of the lesion.</p>\n<p>Important x-ray features of a GCT:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Eccentric, solitary lytic </strong>lesion</li>\n<li>In<strong> epiphysis </strong>and<strong> metaphysis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Expansile</strong>- expands overlying cortex</li>\n<li><strong>Soap bubble appearance - </strong>due to the trabeculae of the remnants of bone traversing the lytic lesion, giving it a loculated appearance.</li>\n<li><strong>No</strong> or minimal <strong>reactive sclerosis</strong> around the tumor</li>\n<li>No calcification</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Histologically, GCTs consist of <strong>undifferentiated spindle cells</strong> profusely interspersed with <strong>multi-nucleated giant cells</strong>. The tumor stroma is well-vascularized with bands of cellular or collagenous fibrous tissue.</p>\n<p>The treatment of choice for GCT is by</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Extended curettage </strong>and <strong>bone grafting </strong>(Option A).</li>\n<li>GCT in the relatively<strong> insignificant bone </strong>like the distal ulna, clavicle, and proximal fibula is treated by <strong>resection</strong> <strong>without reconstruction</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Bisphosphonates</strong> have been shown to <strong>reduce </strong>the<strong> proliferation </strong>of<strong> osteoclasts</strong> and hence have been shown to stabilize local and metastatic disease.</li>\n<li><strong>Radiotherapy</strong> can be used for <strong>vertebral tumors</strong></li>\n<li>Additional supportive therapy such as <strong>c</strong><strong>ryotherapy</strong>, when used <strong>reduce the reccurence</strong> rate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4857","text":"A patient with a giant cel‍l tumor is set to undergo​ an extended curettage. W​hich of the following adj‍uvants used in this proce​dure is associated with l​east rate of recurrence?","raw_text":"A patient with a giant cell tumor is set to undergo an extended curettage. Which of the following adjuvants used in this procedure is associated with least rate of recurrence?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liquid ‍nitrogen","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Phe‍nol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Polymethyl ‍methacrylate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thermal‍ cautery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Liquid nitrogen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Liquid nitrogen</strong></p><p>The <strong>least</strong> rate of <strong>recurrence</strong> following extended curettage is seen with<strong> liquid nitrogen use</strong>.</p>\n<p>This technique is used in the treatment of <strong>giant cell tumours</strong> as they are locally aggressive, for local tumour clearance and prevention of recurrence.</p>\n<p>It is applied by the <strong>direct pour technique</strong>. Complications of this technique include <strong>pathological fractures</strong> and <strong>nerve injuries</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Note: Even though phenol and methacrylate can also be used as adjuvants for extended curettage, studies have shown that liquid nitrogen is far superior for the purpose.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4940","text":"Which of the following les‍ions contain giant cells?​ Aneurysmal bone cyst Bro​wn's tumor Osteoclastoma ‍Enchondroma Osteosarcoma ​Osteoid osteoma","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following lesions contain giant cells?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Aneurysmal bone cyst</li>\n<li>Brown's tumor</li>\n<li>Osteoclastoma</li>\n<li>Enchondroma</li>\n<li>Osteosarcoma</li>\n<li>Osteoid osteoma</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,2‍,4,5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3,4‍,5,6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2,3‍,4,6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,2‍,3,5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1,2,3,5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1,2,3,5</strong></p><p>Aneurysmal bone cyst, brown's tumor, osteoclastoma, and osteosarcoma all contain giant cells.</p>\n<p>Enchondroma and osteoid osteoma do not contain giant cells.</p>\n<p><strong>Lesions containing giant cells are: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Simple bone cyst</li>\n<li><strong>Aneurysmal bone cyst</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Osteoclastoma</strong></li>\n<li>Chondroblastoma</li>\n<li>Fibrous dysplasia</li>\n<li>Non-ossifying fibroma</li>\n<li><strong>Brown's tumor</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Osteosarcoma</strong></li>\n<li>Clear cell chondrosarcoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most common variant of a giant cell tumor is non-ossifying fibroma&gt;fibrous dysplasia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5178","text":"During an annual health ch‍eck-up, the following fin​ding was seen on the ankl​e X-ray of a man. Which o‍f the following is false ​about the given condition​?","raw_text":"During an annual health check-up, the following finding was seen on the ankle X-ray of a man. Which of the following is false about the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a meta‍physeal tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It commonly presents in 4t‍h-5th decade","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is usually asymptomatic‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Histology shows spindle ce‍lls, foam cells and giant​ cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It commonly presents in 4th-5th decade","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/719f087c673343588752851fffc748c9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It commonly presents in 4th-5th decade</strong></p><p>The given X-ray shows a well-defined <strong>lobulated lesion</strong> located eccentrically and <strong>sclerotic scalloped borders</strong> is suggestive of <strong>non-ossifying fibroma. </strong>The common age of presentation of this condition is <strong>2-20 years.</strong></p>\n<p>It is also known as fibrous cortical defect and it<strong> </strong>is the <strong>most common benign lesion</strong> of bone.<strong> </strong>It is a <strong>metaphyseal eccentric lesion</strong> that usually affects the distal femur, tibia and fibula. </p>\n<p>On biopsy, foam cells, spindle shape cells and giant cells may be seen. </p>\n<p>It is usually <strong>asymptomatic</strong> and <strong>regresses spontaneously</strong> in adulthood. If the defect is <strong>very large</strong>, it can be treated by <strong>curettage and bone grafting.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5186","text":"A 49-year-old woman presen‍ted to your clinic with p​rogressive pain in her le​ft thigh. The X-ray of he‍r thigh is shown below. W​hat is the most likely di​agnosis?","raw_text":"A 49-year-old woman presented to your clinic with progressive pain in her left thigh. The X-ray of her thigh is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ossifyin‍g fibroma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fibrous ‍dysplasia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aneurysmal‍ bone cyst","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Encho‍ndroma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fibrous dysplasia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a522513ebaa34d1d92a296750f670d82.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf126e6c690c4afc9a24e0adef28deeex1024x700.jpeg"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fibrous dysplasia</strong></p><p>The above X-ray shows bending of the femur known as<strong> Shepherd's crook deformity. </strong>It<strong> </strong>is seen in <strong>fibrous dysplasia </strong>of proximal femur.</p>\n<p>Fibrous dysplasia is a developmental disorder in which areas of<strong> trabecular bone </strong>are <strong>replaced </strong>by cellular <strong>fibrous tissue. </strong>If this occurs in the weight-bearing bones like the femur, it will lead to bending of the bones.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5192","text":"Which condition is charact‍erized by the presence of​ polyostotic fibrous dysp​lasia with intramuscular ‍myxomas?","raw_text":"Which condition is characterized by the presence of polyostotic fibrous dysplasia with intramuscular myxomas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mazabraud‍ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"McCune Albri‍ght syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maffucci‍ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stewart- Tre‍ves syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mazabraud syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mazabraud syndrome</strong></p><p>Polyostotic fibrous dysplasia with<strong> intramuscular myxomas</strong> is seen in<strong> Mazabraud syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Fibrous dysplasia is a disorder where <strong>bone</strong> is <strong>replaced</strong> by <strong>fibrous tissue</strong>, leading to weak bones, uneven growth, and deformity. <strong>Polyostotic fibrous dysplasia</strong> is a form of this condition affecting<strong> more than one bone</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>McCune - Albright syndrome </strong>consists of<strong> </strong>a characteristic triad of polyostotic fibrous dysplasia, <strong>cafe au lait spots</strong> with rough borders and <strong>hyperfunction</strong> of an <strong>endocrine</strong> system.</p>\n<p><strong>Maffucci syndrome </strong>is characterised by<strong> multiple enchondromas</strong> with soft tissue hemangiomas and phleboliths. Malignant transformation is common in these lesions.</p>\n<p><strong>Stewart- Treves syndrome </strong>refers to the occurrence of <strong>lymphangiosarcoma</strong> in the setting of <strong>chronic lymphedema</strong>. It can be evident as upper extremity lymphedema following radical mastectomy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5182","text":"Which of the following is ‍not seen in patients with​ McCune-Albright syndrome​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in patients with McCune-Albright syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polyostotic fibrous dyspla‍sia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cafe-au-lait lesions with ‍smooth borders","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperfunction of an endocr‍ine system","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mutation affecting G prote‍in signal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cafe-au-lait lesions with smooth borders","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cafe-au-lait lesions with smooth borders</strong></p><p><strong>Cafe-au-lait </strong>lesions with <strong>rough borders</strong> (coast of Maine) are seen in <strong>McCune Albright</strong> syndrome. Cafe-au-lait lesions with <strong>smooth borders </strong>(coast of California) is seen in <strong>neurofibromatosis</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Features of McCune Albright syndrome</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Polyostotic fibrous dysplasia</strong> - most commonly in maxilla and other craniofacial bones, ribs, femur or tibia</li>\n<li><strong>Cafe-au lait spots</strong> with rough borders (coast of Maine)</li>\n<li>Hyperfunction of an endocrine system - most common endocrinopathy is <strong>precocious puberty </strong>in girls</li>\n</ul>\n<p>McCune-Albright syndrome<strong> </strong>occurs due to <strong>activating mutations</strong> in the <strong>GNAS1 gene</strong>, which encodes the alpha subunit of the G protein Gs alpha.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5201","text":"A 20-year-old man presente‍d with pain in the thigh ​which is worse at night a​nd relieved by aspirin. A‍n X-ray is performed whic​h is given below. What is​ the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old man presented with pain in the thigh which is worse at night and relieved by aspirin. An X-ray is performed which is given below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Solitary ‍bone cyst","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteoid‍ osteoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chondro‍blastoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aneurysmal‍ bone cyst","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Osteoid osteoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4876fc04f99d4b74b1c897013da62afa.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d06fbb9487e744ad81601650b21ab447x1024x700.jpeg"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Osteoid osteoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of an <strong>osteoid osteoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The given X-ray shows the presence of<strong> </strong>a<strong> metadiaphyseal cortical</strong> <strong>lesion</strong> involving the upper end of the femur with <strong>radiolucent nidus</strong><strong> </strong>surrounded by <strong>cortical sclerosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> nidus </strong>of osteoid osteoma is present in the <strong>cortex </strong>as can be seen in the image below. Hence,<strong> CT scan</strong> is the<strong> best investigation</strong> to diagnose osteoid osteoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Osteoid osteoma is the <strong>most common benign bone tumour</strong> of the body. It contains <strong>osteoblastic </strong>as well as<strong> osteoclastic components</strong>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\">It is most often seen in <strong>young men</strong>. The most common site is <strong>diaphysis of femur</strong>. It t</span>ypically presents with <strong>pain </strong>that is<strong> worse at night </strong>and is<strong> relieved by aspirin</strong> or other nonsteroidal antiinﬂammatory medications.</p>\n<p>Multiple treatment options are available for this condition as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If <strong>symptoms</strong> are <strong>adequately controlled</strong> and patient is willing to undergo long-term medical management,<strong> anti-inflammatory</strong> <strong>medication</strong> can be used as the definitive treatment. <strong>Spontaneous healing</strong> of the lesion usually occurs within <strong>3 to 4 years</strong>.</li>\n<li>Patients having<strong> lesions of</strong> the <strong>pelvis</strong> or <strong>long bones</strong> of the extremities can be treated with <strong>percutaneous radiofrequency ablation</strong>. This method is not preferred for vertebral lesions and lesions of small bones of hand and feet.</li>\n<li><strong>Surgical managemen</strong>t involves removal of the entire nidus. This can be accomplished by <strong>curettage</strong> or <strong>en bloc resection</strong>.</li>\n<li>Magnetic resonance-guided focused ultrasound (<strong>MRgFUS</strong>) <strong>ablation</strong> is a new method that is under investigation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4836","text":"Which of these is the leas‍t likely cause of multipl​e bone lesions in a patie​nt?","raw_text":"Which of these is the least likely cause of multiple bone lesions in a patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteoid‍ osteoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Encho‍ndroma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteoch‍ondroma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multiple‍ myeloma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Osteoid osteoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Osteoid osteoma</strong></p><p><strong>Osteoid osteoma </strong>is the least likely cause of multiple bone lesions. It is <strong>usually solitary</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential diagnosis for multiple bone lesions</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Histiocytosis</li>\n<li><strong>Enchondroma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Osteochondroma</strong></li>\n<li>Fibrous dysplasia</li>\n<li><strong>Multiple myeloma</strong></li>\n<li>Metastases</li>\n<li>Hemangioma</li>\n<li>Osteomyelitis</li>\n<li>Giant cell tumor</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4875","text":"A 33-year-old woman presen‍ts with multiple bony swe​llings in her hands as se​en in the image below mar‍ked 1. The X-ray of her h​ands is as shown in the i​mage marked 2. Identify h​er condition.","raw_text":"A 33-year-old woman presents with multiple bony swellings in her hands as seen in the image below marked 1. The X-ray of her hands is as shown in the image marked 2. Identify her condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ollier ‍disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maffucci’‍s syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Melorhe‍ostosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemangiop‍ericytoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Maffucci’s syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d907b4d34db44d64920d0750a35752b4.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6fa8ae21ff524e1cbb36dca771ff9defx1280x2184.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Maffucci’s syndrome</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>hemangiomas</strong> of the hand. The radiograph shows <strong>multiple tumors</strong> affecting the <strong>small bones</strong> of hand with soft tissue swellings and calcification suggestive of <strong>enchondromatosis</strong>. This is suggestive of <strong>Maffucci's syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Maffucci's syndrome is characterised by <strong>enchondromatosis</strong> with <strong>hemangiomas</strong> of the overlying soft tissue.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Malignant potential </strong>of enchondromas:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Maffucci's</strong> syndrome has a <strong>100%</strong> risk of turning into malignancy</li>\n<li><strong>Ollier</strong> disease has a <strong>30% </strong>risk of turning into malignancy</li>\n<li><strong>Solitary enchondroma</strong> has a <strong>&lt;2%</strong> risk of conversion to chondrosarcoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most common tumor affecting small bones of the hand is enchondroma. <strong>Ollier disease </strong>is characterized by multiple enchondromas when present without hemangiomas.</p>\n<p><strong>Melorheostosis</strong> is an uncommon mesenchymal dysplasia manifesting as regions of sclerosing bone with a characteristic dripping wax appearance<a href=\"https://radiopaedia.org/articles/dripping-candle-wax-sign-melorheostosis?lang=us\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"></a> a.k.a. <strong>flowing candle wax appearance</strong>. It is also known as<strong> Leri disease</strong>, </p>\n<p><strong>Haemangiopericytomas</strong> are soft tissue sarcomas of vascular origin, comprised of <strong>pericytes</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5194","text":"Which of the following tum‍ors do not have osteoblas​ts as the predominant his​tological cell type?","raw_text":"Which of the following tumors do not have osteoblasts as the predominant histological cell type?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteoid‍ osteoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteoch‍ondroma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Osteos‍arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteob‍lastoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Osteochondroma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Osteochondroma</strong></p><p><strong>Osteochondroma</strong> does not have osteoblast as the predominant cell type. It is made up of physeal<strong> chondrocytes </strong>showing active cartilage formation.</p>\n<p>Osteoid osteoma, osteoblastoma, and osteosarcoma are bone-forming tumors. They show active ossification and have osteoblasts as the predominant histological cell type.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4892","text":"Which of the following is ‍the most common cause of ​pain in osteochondroma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of pain in osteochondroma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nerve co‍mpression","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Frac‍ture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Malignant tr‍ansformation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Burs‍itis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bursitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/342bac1abd474840a3a0ca01d797fed0x720x1299.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bursitis</strong></p><p><strong>Bursitis</strong> is the most common cause of <strong>pain</strong> in an <strong>osteochondroma.</strong></p>\n<p>Osteochondromas are common benign bone tumors which are <strong>developmental malformations</strong> rather than true neoplasms.</p>\n<p>Many of these lesions cause no symptoms and are <strong>discovered incidentally</strong>. However, some of them may become painful. <strong>Causes of pain</strong> in an osteochondroma are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inflammation of overlying bursa</li>\n<li>Fracture of the bony stalk</li>\n<li>Compression of the neurovascular bundle of the limb</li>\n<li>Malignant transformation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following X-ray shows a well-defined exostosis. It <strong>emerges</strong> from the <strong>metaphysis</strong> and moves towards the diaphysis. It <strong>angles away</strong> from the <strong>growth plate</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1145","text":"A young boy with a bony sw‍elling at his knee is dia​gnosed to have an osteoch​ondroma. Which of the fol‍lowing suggests malignant​ degeneration of the lesi​on?","raw_text":"A young boy with a bony swelling at his knee is diagnosed to have an osteochondroma. Which of the following suggests malignant degeneration of the lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fracture displacement &amp;gt;‍2 cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Obstruction to joint movem‍ent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Weight lo‍ss &amp;gt;10%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thickness of cartilage cap‍ &amp;gt;2cm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Thickness of cartilage cap >2cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Thickness of cartilage cap &gt;2cm</strong></p><p><strong>Malignant degeneration</strong> in an osteochondroma is suggested by the thickness of the <strong>cartilage cap exceeding</strong> <strong>2 cm</strong>.</p>\n<p>The cartilage cap overlies a bony stalk. It is irregular and usually cannot be seen on radiographs. Occasionally, calcification within the cap may be seen on X-rays.</p>\n<p>Other <strong>features suggestive of malignant change</strong> in an osteochondroma are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Persistence of tumor <strong>growth after skeletal maturity</strong></li>\n<li>Irregularly scattered flecks of<strong> calcification</strong> within the <strong>cartilage cap</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Spread into</strong> the surrounding <strong>soft tissue</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Malignant degeneration of an osteochondroma may lead to development of chondrosarcoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4883","text":"What is the pathology seen‍ in patients with Trevor'​s disease?","raw_text":"What is the pathology seen in patients with Trevor's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple ost‍eochondromas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intraarticular epiphyseal ‍osteochondroma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Osteochondroma associated ‍with false aneurysms","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteochondroma associated ‍with hemangiomas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intraarticular epiphyseal osteochondroma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f8690f3ad4d14d21a006211444998f5fx626x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intraarticular epiphyseal osteochondroma</strong></p><p>Trevor's disease is characterized by the presence of <strong>intra-articular epiphyseal osteochondromas</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>In <strong>Trevor's disease</strong>, bony lesions appear on the medial or lateral aspect of the epiphysis. The lesions are localized to a <strong>single side</strong> of the body. <strong>Knee</strong> and <strong>ankle</strong> are most frequently involved.</p>\n<p>Multiple osteochondromas are seen in a condition known as<strong> </strong>diaphyseal aclasis or<strong> multiple hereditary exostoses.</strong></p>\n<p>The below <strong>lateral view X-ray</strong> of the lower limb shows <strong>non-uniform bone growth</strong>, and <strong>unconnected ossification centers</strong> related to the epiphyses at the knee and ankle. This is seen in <strong>Trevor's disease</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1144","text":"Which of the following is ‍false with respect to mul​tiple hereditary exostose​s?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false with respect to multiple hereditary exostoses?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal dominant disorde‍r","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Affected genes are EXT1 an‍d EXT2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with multiple e‍nchondromas","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased risk of malignan‍t transformation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Associated with multiple enchondromas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b77a298c0ba94accb4b9553861079c28x309x420.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Associated with multiple enchondromas</strong></p><p><strong>Multiple hereditary exostoses </strong>is associated with <strong>multiple osteochondromas</strong>.<strong> </strong>Multiple enchondromas are seen in Maffucci's syndrome and Ollier's disease.</p>\n<p>Multiple hereditary exostoses is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> disease. The affected genes are <strong>EXT1 </strong>and<strong> EXT2</strong> which are located on <strong>chromosomes</strong> <strong>8 </strong>and<strong> 11</strong> respectively.</p>\n<p>There is an<strong> increased risk</strong> of <strong>malignant transformation</strong> of the osteochondromas in case of multiple lesions (about 5%). Solitary lesions have a 1% risk of malignant transformation.</p>\n<p>The following X-ray image shows multiple osteochondromas around the knee.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4830","text":"Which of the following les‍ions is an epiphyseal les​ion?","raw_text":"Which of the following lesions is an epiphyseal lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Encho‍ndroma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteoid‍ osteoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chondro‍blastoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Osteob‍lastoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chondroblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c03b0be99919464ca5915ccb0bb6e0c4x1280x1560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chondroblastoma</strong></p><p>Among the given options, <strong>chondroblastoma</strong> is an epiphyseal lesion.</p>\n<p><strong>Epiphyseal lesions include:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chondroblastoma</li>\n<li>Giant cell tumor in adults</li>\n<li>Clear chondrosarcoma</li>\n<li>Articular osteochondroma (Trevor's disease)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1162","text":"A bone biopsy was performe‍d on a child with a skull​ swelling. It showed the ​presence of eosinophils a‍nd dendritic cells and wa​s found to be CD 207 posi​tive on immunohistochemis​try. Which of the followi​ng skeletal manifestation‍s would you not expect to‌ see in this child?","raw_text":"A bone biopsy was performed on a child with a skull swelling.  It showed the presence of eosinophils and dendritic cells and was found to be CD 207 positive on immunohistochemistry. Which of the following skeletal manifestations would you not expect to see in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vertebr‍a plana","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Geograph‍ic skull","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Floatin‍g tooth","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Short 4th ‍metacarpal","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Short 4th metacarpal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Short 4th metacarpal</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Langerhans cell histiocytosis.</strong> It is not characterised by the presence of a <strong>short 4th metacarpal</strong>. This feature is seen in <strong>pseudohypoparathyroidism</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>skeletal and radiological manifestations</strong> of Langerhan's cell histiocytosis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Skull:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>solitary or multiple punched out lytic lesion without sclerotic rim</li>\n<li>double contour or beveled edge appearance (hole-within-a-hole sign)</li>\n<li>button sequestra representing residual bone</li>\n<li><strong>geographic skull</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Mandible:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>floating</strong> <strong>tooth</strong>: loss of lamina dura</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Spine:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>vertebra</strong> <strong>plana:</strong> most common cause of vertebra plana in children; mostly seen in thoracic spine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0235","text":"A child presents with diff‍iculty in walking. On bio​psy of the hip, some cell​s with grooved nuclei and‍ eosinophilic cytoplasm a​re present. Which investi​gation would you order ne​xt?","raw_text":"A child presents with difficulty in walking. On biopsy of the hip, some cells with grooved nuclei and eosinophilic cytoplasm are present. Which investigation would you order next?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M‍RI","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum ‍calcium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Seru‍m PTH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD1a ‍marker","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CD1a marker","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CD1a marker</strong></p><p>Histopathologically, <strong>cells with grooved nuclei </strong>and<strong> eosinophilic cytoplasm</strong> can be observed in both <strong>Langerhans cell histiocytosis</strong> (LCH) and <strong>chondroblastoma</strong>. Therefore, the next step to differentiate between them would be to obtain a<strong> CD1a marker</strong>, which is typically<strong> positive in LCH</strong>.</p>\n<p>The specific markers for LCH are <strong>CD1a</strong> and <strong>langerin</strong> (CD207)</p>\n<p>It is worth noting that while an MRI can provide valuable information for evaluating the pathology, it is ideally performed before the biopsy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1148","text":"The CT scan image of a 50-‍year-old female with back​ache is given below. What​ is the likely diagnosis?‍","raw_text":"The CT scan image of a 50-year-old female with backache is given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vertebral ‍metastasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heman‍gioma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Eosinophili‍c granuloma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ossifyin‍g fibroma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hemangioma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bb74399c2ad44eaba37c3f8cd8b0e73e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9198caee955245d8a7ca0778a2f7243cx720x764.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hemangioma</strong></p><p>The image shows the classical <strong>polka dot</strong> <strong>sign</strong>(on axial section)<strong> </strong>and the <strong>C</strong><strong>orduroy sign /jailhouse appearance</strong>(on vertical section). These are seen in <strong>vertebral hemangioma</strong>.</p>\n<p>These signs are the result of the replacement of normal cancellous bone by thickened vertical trabeculae.</p>\n<p>The corduroy sign is shown in the following image.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4947","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is false with res​pect to hemangiomas?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false with respect to hemangiomas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Biopsy is rarely needed fo‍r diagnosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"They usually arise from po‍sterior elements of verte​bra","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"They cause local gigantism‍ when they occur on an ex​tremity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They are also common in th‍e skull","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. They usually arise from posterior elements of vertebra","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. They usually arise from posterior elements of vertebra</strong></p><p><strong>Hemangioma</strong> <strong>arises</strong> more commonly from the <strong>vertebral</strong> <strong>body.</strong></p>\n<p>Hemangioma is a benign tumor and consists of vascular channels (capillary, venous or cavernous). Apart from the spine, <strong>skull</strong> is also a <strong>common site</strong> for hemangiomas.</p>\n<p><strong>Biopsy</strong> is rarely needed due to the threat of <strong>hemorrhagic shock. </strong>Imaging techniques such as <strong>CT/MRI</strong> are used to make the <strong>diagnosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>A hemangioma on a <strong>limb</strong> can lead to local <strong>hypertrophy</strong>. This can cause<strong> localised gigantism</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"wZET43eb","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"27_pediatric_nephrology","title":"27.Pediatric Nephrology","num_questions":30,"total_marks":120,"duration":40,"time_per_question":80.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA5654","text":"Hematuria in children is d‌efined as the presence of‍ at least ______ RBCs per​ high power field in unce‌ntrifuged urine.","raw_text":"Hematuria in children is defined as the presence of at least ______ RBCs per high power field in uncentrifuged urine.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 5</strong></p><p><strong>Hematuria</strong> in children is defined as the presence of <strong>at least</strong> <strong>5 RBCs/HPF</strong><strong> </strong>(high power field) in <strong>uncentrifuged </strong>urine.</p>\n<p>When using commercially available<strong> dipsticks</strong> (capable of detecting 3-5 RBCs / HPF of urine), the presence of <strong>10-50 RBCs/μL</strong> suggests an underlying pathology.</p>\n<p><strong>False-negative</strong> results can occur in the presence of</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Formalin</li>\n<li>High urinary concentrations of ascorbic acid</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>False-positive</strong> results, on the other hand, may be seen</p>\n<ul>\n<li>With alkaline urine (pH &gt; 8)</li>\n<li>Contamination with oxidizing agents such as hydrogen peroxide (used to clean the perineum before obtaining a specimen)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9837","text":"An 11-year-old child was b‌rought to the hospital by‍ his mother with complain​ts of multiple episodes o‌f passing blood in the ur​ine. Which of the followi​ng condition is not assoc‌iated with this symptom?","raw_text":"An 11-year-old child was brought to the hospital by his mother with complaints of multiple episodes of passing blood in the urine. Which of the following condition is not associated with this symptom?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Berger’s‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemolytic ur‌emic syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hereditary‌ nephritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Idiopathic h‌ypercalciuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hemolytic uremic syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hemolytic uremic syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS)</strong> presents with acute-onset <strong>microscopic</strong> hematuria not recurrent gross hematuria.</p>\n<p>Causes of recurrent gross hematuria:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alport’s syndrome: <strong>hereditary nephritis</strong></li>\n<li>Thin glomerular basement membrane disease</li>\n<li>IgA nephropathy: <strong>Berger’s disease </strong></li>\n<li><strong> Idiopathic hypercalciuria</strong>/hyperuricosuria</li>\n<li>Sickle cell nephropathy (papillary necrosis)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5652","text":"A 6-year-old girl with uri‌nary complaints was broug‍ht to the hospital by her​ parents. On further enqu‌iry, they mention a histo​ry of skin lesions 5 week​s back. Low C3 levels are‌ found during her investi​gations. Immunofluorescen​ce microscopy reveals a “‍lumpy-bumpy” pattern of i​mmunoglobulin and complem​ent deposits on the glome​rular basement membrane. ​Which of the following is​ not typically associated​ with this condition?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old girl with urinary complaints was brought to the hospital by her parents. On further enquiry, they mention a history of skin lesions 5 weeks back. Low C3 levels are found during her investigations. Immunofluorescence microscopy reveals a “lumpy-bumpy” pattern of immunoglobulin and complement deposits on the glomerular basement membrane. Which of the following is not typically associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cola colo‌red urine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Frothy‌ urine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Olig‌uria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypert‌ension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Frothy urine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Frothy urine</strong></p><p>Urinary complaints with a history of skin lesions (pyoderma) point towards a likely diagnosis of <strong>glomerulonephritis.</strong> Results of complement study and immunofluorescence microscopy also correlate with this diagnosis. <strong>Frothy urine</strong> is due to proteinuria, which is a feature of<strong> nephrotic syndrome</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Classic features </strong>of nephritic syndrome/glomerulonephritis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cola coloured urine (hematuria)</li>\n<li>Oliguria</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Facial or body edema</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5659","text":"Akshay, accompanied by his‌ father, presents to the ‍OPD with complaints of co​la-coloured urine and fac‌ial puffiness for 3 days.​ He has a history of feve​r with a sore throat that‌ resolved 2 weeks back. W​hich of the following fin​dings is consistent with ‍the diagnosis of post-str​eptococcal glomerulonephr​itis?","raw_text":"Akshay, accompanied by his father, presents to the OPD with complaints of cola-coloured urine and facial puffiness for 3 days. He has a history of fever with a sore throat that resolved 2 weeks back. Which of the following findings is consistent with the diagnosis of post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C3 level increases and ASO‌ level increases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C3 level decreases and ASO‌ level increases","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"C3 level decreases and ASO‌ level decreases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C3 level increases and ASO‌ level decreases","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. C3 level decreases and ASO level increases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. C3 level decreases and ASO level increases</strong></p><p><strong>C3 level decreases</strong> and <strong>ASO level increases</strong> in acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN).</p>\n<p>Acute PSGN is a classic example of an acute nephritic syndrome characterized by the sudden onset of gross hematuria, oedema, hypertension, and renal insufficiency. It follows infection of the throat or skin by certain <strong>nephritogenic strains of group A β-hemolytic streptococci-</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>1 to 2 weeks after an antecedent streptococcal <strong>pharyngitis</strong> or</li>\n<li>3 to 6 weeks after streptococcal <strong>pyoderma</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6813","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a characteristic feat‍ure of hematuria associat​ed with glomerulonephriti‌s?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a characteristic feature of hematuria associated with glomerulonephritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dysmorphic ‌RBC in urine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RBC cast‌ in urine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Painful ‌hematuria","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cola-colo‌red urine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Painful hematuria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Painful hematuria</strong></p><p><strong>Hematuria</strong> associated with glomerulonephritis is typically <strong>painless.</strong></p>\n<p>Hematuria from within the glomerulus is often associated with brown-, cola-, or tea-colored urine. Urine analysis reveals RBC casts and deformed urinary RBCs (particularly acanthocytes).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9836","text":"A 7-year-old boy presents ‌with decreased urine outp‍ut for 3 days. He has a h​istory of sore throat 2 w‌eeks back. On examination​, he is hypertensive with​ pitting pedal oedema. Th‌e diagnosis is concluded ​as post-streptococcal glo​merulonephritis. Which of‍ the following is true ab​out this condition?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy presents with decreased urine output for 3 days. He has a history of sore throat 2 weeks back. On examination, he is hypertensive with pitting pedal oedema. The diagnosis is concluded as post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis. Which of the following is true about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum triglyceride levels ‌are elevated.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Microscopic hematuria reso‌lves in 4 weeks.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serum C3 levels are normal‌.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Renal biopsy is indicated ‌in severe impairment of r‍enal function.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Renal biopsy is indicated in severe impairment of renal function.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Renal biopsy is indicated in severe impairment of renal function.</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette of oliguria, pedal edema and hypertension point towards glomerulonephritis.  <strong>Severe impairment</strong> <strong>of renal functions</strong> in a background of post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is an <strong>indication for renal biopsy. </strong></p>\n<p>Indications for renal biopsy in <strong>glomerulonephritis</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of acute renal failure or nephrotic syndrome</li>\n<li>Absence of evidence of streptococcal infection</li>\n<li>Presence of normal complement levels</li>\n<li>Persistence of hematuria and proteinuria, diminished renal function, and/or a low C3 level for more than 2 months after the disease onset  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Serum triglycerides  (Option A) are elevated in<strong> nephrotic syndrome,</strong> not in nephritic syndrome.</p>\n<p>The acute phase generally resolves within 6 to 8 weeks. Although urinary protein excretion and hypertension usually normalize by 4-6 wk after onset, <strong>microscopic hematuria  </strong>(Option B)<strong> </strong>can persist for <strong>1 to 2 years </strong>after the initial presentation<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>The serum <strong>C3 level </strong>(Option C) is significantly<strong> reduced </strong>in &gt;90% of patients in the acute phase<strong>,</strong> and returns to normal 6-8 weeks after onset. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5672","text":"A 6-year-old boy with a pr‌eviously diagnosed renal ‍disorder is brought to yo​ur OPD with the following‌ reports for a second opi​nion. What is the likely ​diagnosis? Light microsco‌py: Enlarged, pale glomer​uli. Electron microscopy:​ Subepithelial humps.","raw_text":"<p>A 6-year-old boy with a previously diagnosed renal disorder is brought to your OPD with the following reports for a second opinion. What is the likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p><strong>Light microscopy: Enlarged, pale glomeruli.</strong><br/><strong>Electron microscopy: Subepithelial humps. </strong><br/><br/></p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Post-streptococcal glomeru‌lonephritis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rapidly progressive glomer‌ulonephritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Focal segmental glomerulon‌ephritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Minimal cha‌nge disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5f3bdae22bc241a0878effb6a6c08e52x1279x1050.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis</strong></p><p>The findings mentioned are characteristic of <strong>post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis</strong> (PSGN).</p>\n<p>Findings in PSGN:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Light</strong> microscopy: <strong>Enlarged, pale glomeruli</strong> with diffuse mesangial proliferation and an increase in the mesangial matrix. Polymorphoneutrophil (PMN) infiltration in the early stages.</li>\n<li><strong>Immunofluorescence</strong>:<strong> “Lumpy-bumpy” deposits</strong> of immunoglobulin and complement on the glomerular basement membrane (GBM) and in the mesangium. </li>\n<li><strong>Electron microscopy</strong>: Electron-dense deposits, or <strong>“humps,” </strong>are observed on the <strong>epithelial side </strong>of the GBM.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> The image given below depicts the microscopic picture of PSGN. It shows glomerular hypercellularity due to intracapillary leukocytes. <strong>Neutrophilic infiltration</strong> is present and is marked with purple hematoxylin stain (black arrows).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p><strong>Idiopathic rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Light microscopy: Crescents develop in several forms of glomerulonephritis (are a characteristic response to the deposition of fibrin in Bowman’s space)</li>\n<li>Electron microscopy: No deposits</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Focal segmental glomerulonephritis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Light microscopy: Focal sclerotic lesions</li>\n<li>Electron microscopy: Foot process fusion</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Minimal change disease:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Light microscopy: Normal</li>\n<li>Electron microscopy Effacement of foot process</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9842","text":"An 8-year-old boy presente‌d with cola-coloured urin‍e, decreased urine output​, and facial puffiness fo‌r 3 days. He had a histor​y of skin infection 4 wee​ks back. On examination, ‌he is found to be hyperte​nsive. His serum C3 level​s are low and ASO levels ‍are increased. What is th​e appropriate step in the​ management of this patie​nt?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old boy presented with cola-coloured urine, decreased urine output, and facial puffiness for 3 days. He had a history of skin infection 4 weeks back. On examination, he is found to be hypertensive. His serum C3 levels are low and ASO levels are increased. What is the appropriate step in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal ‌biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Supportiv‌e therapy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ster‌oids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cyclos‌porine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Supportive therapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Supportive therapy</strong></p><p>The features of hematuria, oliguria, facial oedema present with a history of skin infection points towards the diagnosis of <strong>post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN). </strong>Results of complement study and ASO levels also support this diagnosis. <strong>Supportive conservative therapy </strong>is the best step in the management of PSGN.</p>\n<p>Management of PSGN is directed at treating the acute effects of renal insufficiency (sodium restriction and prescribing diuretics - usually with intravenous furosemide) and hypertension.  </p>\n<p>Although a 10-day course of systemic antibiotic therapy with penicillin is recommended to limit the spread of the nephritogenic organisms, antibiotic therapy does not aﬀect the natural history of acute PSGN.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Steroids and cyclosporine are not used in the management of PSGN.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5678","text":"Sarthak, a 7-year-old boy,‌ was brought to the emerg‍ency department by his pa​rents with complaints of ‌blurred vision and severe​ headache since today mor​ning. They mentioned an e‌pisode of fits 2 hours ba​ck following which they r​ushed him to the hospital‍. On further enquiry, it ​was elicited that the chi​ld had decreased urine ou​tput for 5 days with cola​ coloured urine. He was h​ypertensive with bilatera​l pitting pedal oedema. T‌he doctor remembered that‍ Sarthak has a glomerular‌ disorder warranting him ‌multiple prior ward admis​sions and his existing co‌ndition could be a possib‌le complication of the sa‌me. All of the following are commonly seen as a complication of this disorder except-","raw_text":"Sarthak, a 7-year-old boy, was brought to the emergency department by his parents with complaints of blurred vision and severe headache since today morning. They mentioned an episode of fits 2 hours back following which they rushed him to the hospital. On further enquiry, it was elicited that the child had decreased urine output for 5 days with cola coloured urine. He was hypertensive with bilateral pitting pedal oedema. The doctor remembered that Sarthak has a glomerular disorder warranting him multiple prior ward admissions and his existing condition could be a possible complication of the same.  All of the following are commonly seen as a complication of this disorder except-","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left ventric‌ular failure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypertensive encephalopath‌y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperk‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bleeding ‌diasthesis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bleeding diasthesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bleeding diasthesis</strong></p><p>The given features of oliguria, hematuria, pedal edema and hypertension are likely of <strong>glomerulonephritis</strong>. The complication of hypertensive encephalopathy is seen in this case due to symptoms of seizure, blurred vision and severe headache. Hyperkalemia and left ventricular failure are also common complications of glomerulonephritis while <strong>bleeding diasthesis</strong> is <strong>not a complication</strong> of glomerulonephritis.</p>\n<p>Acute complications of glomerulonephritis result from hypertension and acute renal dysfunction-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertension is seen in 60% of patients and is sometimes associated with <strong>hypertensive encephalopathy</strong> and intracranial bleeding.</li>\n<li><strong>Acute renal failure</strong> can occur, which may require treatment with dialysis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other potential complications include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Heart failure</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hyperkalemia</strong></li>\n<li>Hyperphosphatemia </li>\n<li>Hypocalcemia </li>\n<li>Hyponatremia</li>\n<li>Acidosis</li>\n<li>Seizures</li>\n<li>Uremia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5707","text":"A 9-year-old boy presents ‌with gross hematuria 2 da‍ys after an episode of di​arrhoea. The serum C3 lev‌els are normal. Light mic​roscopy demonstrates foca​l and segmental mesangial‌ proliferation and increa​sed mesangial matrix in t​he glomerulus. What is th‍e probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 9-year-old boy presents with gross hematuria 2 days after an episode of diarrhoea. The serum C3 levels are normal. Light microscopy demonstrates focal and segmental mesangial proliferation and increased mesangial matrix in the glomerulus. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Berger's‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"PS‌GN","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Microangiopathic hemolytic‌ anemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‌the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Berger's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Berger&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>Gross hematuria following a gastrointestinal infection, associated with normal C3 levels, in a male patient is suggestive of <strong>immunoglobulin A nephropathy (Berger nephropathy)</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>IgA nephropathy is the most common chronic glomerular disease in children.</li>\n<li><strong>Focal and segmental mesangial proliferation and increased mesangial matrix</strong> are seen in the glomerulus.</li>\n<li>It is characterized by a predominance of IgA within mesangial glomerular deposits in the absence of systemic disease.</li>\n<li>It is seen more often in males.</li>\n<li>Gross hematuria occurs within 1-2 days of onset of an upper respiratory or gastrointestinal infection (in contrast to the long latency period observed in acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis).</li>\n<li>It can be associated with<strong> loin pain.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Normal serum levels of C3</strong> in IgA nephropathy (help to distinguish this disorder from PSGN).</li>\n<li>Serum IgA levels have no diagnostic value because they are elevated in only a few paediatric patients.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5686","text":"A child developed dark col‌oured urine and flank pai‍n 2 days after an upper r​espiratory tract infectio‌n. The physician suspects​ IgA nephropathy. What is​ the location of cellular‌ injury in this condition​?","raw_text":"A child developed dark coloured urine and flank pain 2 days after an upper respiratory tract infection. The physician suspects IgA nephropathy. What is the location of cellular injury in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epithel‌ial cell","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basement‌ membrane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endothel‌ial cell","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mesangi‌al cell","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mesangial cell","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mesangial cell</strong></p><p>The location of cellular injury in <strong>IgA nephropathy</strong> (Berger nephropathy) is the deposition of IgA antibodies in the <strong>mesangial cell</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mesangial cells are directly exposed to the circulation; therefore, they are affected in diseases characterized by the deposition of immune complexes.</p>\n<p><strong>Mesangial cell diseases:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>IgA nephropathy</li>\n<li>IgM nephropathy</li>\n<li>Mesangioproliferative glomerulonephritis</li>\n<li>Class II lupus nephritis</li>\n<li>Diabetic nephropathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: In <strong>IgA nephropathy</strong> gross hematuria often occurs within <strong>1-2 days</strong> of onset of an upper respiratory or gastrointestinal infection. In contrast, a longer latency period of about <strong>1-2 weeks</strong> is observed in acute <strong>postinfectious glomerulonephritis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5694","text":"ANCA-positive , pauci-immu‌ne glomerulonephritis inc‍lude-","raw_text":"ANCA-positive , pauci-immune glomerulonephritis include-","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wegener`s gr‌anulomatosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Microscopic ‌polyangiitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Churg-Strau‌ss syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All of the above</strong></p><p><strong>Anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies </strong>(ANCA)-associated vasculitis is categorized into 3 distinct forms: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA; formerly <strong>Wegener granulomatosis</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Microscopic polyangiitis</strong> (MPA)</li>\n<li>Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis, formerly called <strong>Churg–Strauss syndrome</strong> (CSS) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Kidney biopsies typically demonstrate <strong>crescentic glomerulonephritis</strong> with little or <strong>no immune complex deposition.</strong> </p>\n<p>ANCA-associated vasculitis (AAV) comprises a group of multi-systemic diseases affecting small-to-medium-sized vessels. It is characterized by the presence of ANCA with specificity for either proteinase-3 (PR3) or myeloperoxidase (MPO).</p>\n<p>Absence or paucity of immune complex deposits in vessel walls is very characteristic, hence the name “<strong>pauci-immune vasculitis.</strong>”</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5699","text":"An 8-year-old girl present‌ed with complaints of cou‍gh, hemoptysis and swelli​ng of both legs. Urinalys‌is was notable for hematu​ria and proteinuria. Seru​m anti-GBM antibodies wer‌e positive. What is the m​ost likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old girl presented with complaints of cough, hemoptysis and swelling of both legs. Urinalysis was notable for hematuria and proteinuria. Serum anti-GBM antibodies were positive. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IgA nep‌hropathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Minimal cha‌nge disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Goodpastu‌re disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Membranous ‌nephropathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Goodpasture disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Goodpasture disease</strong></p><p>The diagnosis is<strong> Goodpasture disease (anti-GBM antibody disease).</strong><strong> </strong>Anti–glomerular basement membrane<strong> (anti-GBM) antibodies</strong> are diagnostic.</p>\n<p><strong>\"Goodpasture syndrome\"</strong> is characterized by pulmonary haemorrhage and glomerulonephritis and<strong> \"Goodpasture disease\"</strong> is used for those patients with glomerulonephritis, pulmonary hemorrhage, and anti-GBM antibodies.<strong> </strong>It is caused due to autoantibodies directed against <strong>type IV collagen</strong>, which is present in the alveoli and glomerular basement membrane. Currently, the preferred term for both conditions is “<strong>anti-GBM antibody disease</strong>”.</p>\n<p>Other options along with the pathogenesis of glomerular diseases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IgA nephropathy-</strong> mesangial cell disease due to immune complex deposition</li>\n<li><strong>Minimal change disease-</strong> epithelial cell disease due to flattening of podocytes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5711","text":"A 9-year-old boy has swell‌ing of knee joints and no‍n-blanching rashes as see​n in the picture. Urine e‌xamination reveals hematu​ria +++ and proteinuria +​. Microscopic analysis of‌ his renal biopsy specime​n is most likely to show ​which of the following fi‍nding?","raw_text":"A 9-year-old boy has swelling of knee joints and non-blanching rashes as seen in the picture. Urine examination reveals hematuria +++ and proteinuria +. Microscopic analysis of his renal biopsy specimen is most likely to show which of the following finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tubular ‌necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Visceral pod‌ocyte fusion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mesangial de‌posits of IgA","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Basement membrane thickeni‌ng","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mesangial deposits of IgA","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6b2dc1df287a44ccb5149f47beeb0ca3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mesangial deposits of IgA</strong></p><p>The boy in this clinical scenario has characteristic features of <strong>Henoch–Schonlein purpura (HSP).</strong></p>\n<p>The renal biopsy in this patient will show mesangial proliferation with<strong> mesangial deposition of IgA</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>HSP is a common <strong>small-vessel vasculitis</strong> in childhood.</li>\n<li>It is characterized by a <strong>purpuric rash</strong> and commonly accompanied by <strong>arthritis</strong> and<strong> abdominal pain</strong>.</li>\n<li>About half the patients with HSP develop <strong>renal manifestations</strong>, which vary from asymptomatic microscopic hematuria to severe, progressive glomerulonephritis.</li>\n<li>The nephritis associated with HSP usually follows the onset of the rash</li>\n<li>The pathogenesis of HSP nephritis appears to be mediated by the deposition of polymeric immunoglobulin A<strong> (IgA) in glomeruli</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5713","text":"All are features of Alport‌ syndrome except-","raw_text":"All are features of Alport syndrome except-","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior ‌lenticonus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conductive ‌hearing loss","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gross h‌ematuria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prote‌inuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Conductive hearing loss","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Conductive hearing loss</strong></p><p>Bilateral <strong>sensorineural</strong> hearing loss (<strong>not</strong> <strong>conductive</strong> hearing loss) is a feature of <strong>Alport syndrome (AS)</strong>.</p>\n<p>AS or hereditary nephritis is a genetic disease (most have X-linked inheritance) caused by mutations in the genes coding for<strong> type IV collagen</strong>, a major component of the basement membranes.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of Alport syndrome:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hematuria:</strong> All patients have asymptomatic microscopic hematuria. Recurrent episodes of gross hematuria may be seen 1-2 days after an upper respiratory infection.</li>\n<li>Bilateral sensorineural hearing loss (not conductive)</li>\n<li>Ocular abnormalities:<strong> anterior lenticonus</strong>, macular flecks, corneal erosions</li>\n<li><strong>Proteinuria</strong>: Progressive proteinuria, often exceeding 1 g/ 24 hrs, is common by the 2<sup>nd</sup> decade of life.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5719","text":"A 16-year-old boy presente‌d with dark coloured urin‍e. Urinalysis was notable​ for macroscopic hematuri‌a and heavy proteinuria. ​His father is a known cas​e of Alport syndrome. Wha‌t defect is responsible f​or this condition?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old boy presented with dark coloured urine. Urinalysis was notable for macroscopic hematuria and heavy proteinuria. His father is a known case of Alport syndrome. What defect is responsible for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibr‌illin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type IV ‌collagen","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Amy‌loid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T‌au","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type IV collagen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type IV collagen</strong></p><p>A <strong>defect in type IV collagen</strong>, a major component of the basement membrane, is responsible for <strong>Alport syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Alport syndrome is a genetically heterogeneous disease, <strong>85% is X-linked</strong> and the rest may be autosomal recessive or autosomal dominant.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5725","text":"All of the following syndr‌omes are associated with ‍renal disease except ____​___.","raw_text":"All of the following syndromes are associated with renal disease except _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lowe s‌yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Denys-Dras‌h syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alport ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sinding-Larsen-Johansson s‌yndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sinding-Larsen-Johansson syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sinding-Larsen-Johansson syndrome</strong></p><p>Sinding–Larsen–Johansson syndrome is an insertional periostitis at the inferior pole of the patella (<strong>not associated with Renal disease</strong>).</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lowe syndrome </strong></td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Oculocerebrorenal syndrome</strong>: Hypotonia, renal disease </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><strong>Denys</strong></strong><strong>–Drash Syndrome</strong> </td>\n<td>\n<p>Associated with <strong>Wilm's tumor</strong>: </p>\n<p>Early-onset renal failure with renal mesangial sclerosis, male pseudohermaphroditism </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alport syndrome </strong></td>\n<td>Bilateral sensorineural<strong> hearing loss, renal disease</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><strong>Sinding</strong></strong><strong><strong>–Larsen</strong></strong><strong>–Johansson syndrome </strong></td>\n<td><strong>Periostitis</strong> at the inferior pole of the patella </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5741","text":"Microalbuminuria is define‌d as a urinary albumin ex‍cretion rate of _______.","raw_text":"Microalbuminuria is defined as a urinary albumin excretion rate of _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30-300 m‌g/ 24 hrs","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt; 300 ‌mg/ 24 hrs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt; 150 ‌mg/ 24 hrs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt; 300 ‌µg/ 24 hrs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 30-300 mg/ 24 hrs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 30-300 mg/ 24 hrs</strong></p><p>Microalbuminuria is defined as a urinary albumin excretion rate of <strong>30–300 mg/24 hrs</strong>.</p>\n<p>Here, the presence of albumin in the urine is above the normal level but below the detectable range for the urine-dipstick methods. It is commonly associated with <strong>glomerular hyperfiltration and blood pressure elevation</strong>. </p>\n<p>In <strong>adults</strong>, persistent microalbuminuria indicates <strong>diabetic nephropathy</strong> and is also a predictor of cardiovascular and renal disease.</p>\n<p>In<strong> children</strong>, microalbuminuria is associated with <strong>obesity</strong> and predicts the development of<strong> diabetic nephropathy</strong> in type 1 diabetes mellitus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5728","text":"A 5-year-old boy presents ‌with facial puffiness and‍ frothy urine. Upon inves​tigating, his urine was p‌ositive for proteins with​ no hematuria and no RBC ​casts. To conclude the di‌agnosis of nephrotic synd​rome, the range of protei​nuria is defined as &gt;____‍_ mg/m2/hr and urine prot​ein:creatinine ratio of &gt;​________.","raw_text":"A 5-year-old boy presents with facial puffiness and frothy urine. Upon investigating, his urine was positive for proteins with no hematuria and no RBC casts. To conclude the diagnosis of nephrotic syndrome, the range of proteinuria is defined as &gt;_____ mg/m2/hr and urine protein:creatinine ratio of &gt;________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"40 a‌nd 2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"30 an‌d 1.5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3.5 ‌and 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 an‌d 1.5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 40 and 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 40 and 2</strong></p><p> The currently accepted definitions of  “<strong>nephrotic range proteinuria</strong>” include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Urine protein: creatinine ratio<strong> &gt; 2</strong></li>\n<li>Urine protein<strong> &gt; 40 mg/m2/hr</strong></li>\n<li>Urine protein 3+ or 4+ in dipstick test</li>\n</ul>\n<p>As a consequence of the large urinary loss of protein, the patient develops hypoalbuminemia (&lt; 2.5 g/dL) and gross edema.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5743","text":"It is expected to see all ‌of the following clinical‍ features in a 5-year-old​ boy with nephrotic syndr‌ome except-","raw_text":"It is expected to see all of the following clinical features in a 5-year-old boy with nephrotic syndrome except-","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypoalb‌uminemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperli‌pidemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hema‌turia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ed‌ema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hematuria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hematuria</strong></p><p><strong>Hematuria</strong> is not a usual feature of nephrotic syndrome. It is a feature of <strong>nephritic syndrome. </strong>However, occasionally it is seen in the FSGS variant of nephrotic syndrome as well.</p>\n<p>Classic features of <strong>nephrotic syndrome</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nephrotic range proteinuria ( &gt;40mg/m2/hr urine protein: creatinine ratio &gt;2) </li>\n<li>Hypoalbuminemia<strong> (&lt;2.5 g/dL) </strong></li>\n<li>Edema</li>\n<li>Hyperlipidemia <strong>(cholesterol &gt; 200 mg/dL)</strong></li>\n<li>Hypercoagulability</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5745","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of nephrotic syndrome‍ in children?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Minimal cha‌nge disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Membranous ‌nephropathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IgA nep‌hropathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alport ‌syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Minimal change disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Minimal change disease</strong></p><p>The <strong>commonest cause</strong> of nephrotic syndrome in children is <strong>minimal change disease</strong>. (85% of cases).</p>\n<p>The glomeruli appear almost normal on microscopy, hence the name. Immunofluorescence microscopy are negative, and electron microscopy shows effacement of the epithelial cell foot processes.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment:</strong> Steroids; 95% of patients respond.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Membranous nephropathy</strong> is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in the <strong>elderly</strong>; it is a rare cause of nephrotic syndrome in children.</li>\n<li><strong>Alport syndrome</strong> or hereditary nephritis: All patients have asymptomatic hematuria; progressive proteinuria is common only by the 2nd decade of life and can be severe enough to cause nephrotic syndrome. This nephrotic syndrome is <strong>not common</strong> in children.</li>\n<li><strong>IgA nephropathy</strong> presents more commonly as an acute nephritic syndrome or with asymptomatic hematuria. It presents with nephrotic syndrome quite <strong>rarely</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5747","text":"A 5-year-old child was bro‌ught to the hospital with‍ complaints of progressiv​e swelling around the eye‌s, abdomen and feet for 2​ weeks. On investigating,​ his urine was positive f‌or 4+ proteinuria with ab​sent hematuria. A spot ur​ine protein: creatinine r‍atio was reported to be 2​.5. His serum albumin was​ 2.3 g/dL and serum creat​inine was 0.5 mg/dL. Seru​m cholesterol and triglyc​eride levels were also el​evated. A diagnosis of ne‌phrotic syndrome was conc‍luded. Which of the follo‌wing correctly mentions t‌he aetiology of its most ​common variant in childre‌n?","raw_text":"A  5-year-old child was brought to the hospital with complaints of progressive swelling around the eyes, abdomen and feet for 2 weeks. On investigating, his urine was positive for 4+ proteinuria with absent hematuria. A spot urine protein: creatinine ratio was reported to be 2.5. His serum albumin was 2.3 g/dL and serum creatinine was 0.5 mg/dL. Serum cholesterol and triglyceride levels were also elevated. A diagnosis of nephrotic syndrome was concluded. Which of the following correctly mentions the aetiology of its most common variant in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flattening ‌of podocytes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Immune compl‌ex deposition","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti–GBM ‌antibodies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endothelial ‌cell disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Flattening of podocytes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/37160e17765c44448c06fe828b0385b6x1280x1222.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Flattening of podocytes</strong></p><p><strong>Nephrotic syndrome</strong> is concluded as the diagnosis here because of the symptoms of anasarca and proteinuria. A spot urine protein:creatinine ratio &gt;2, low serum albumin levels, and hyperlipidemia marked by high cholesterol and triglyceride levels, further add to this diagnosis. <strong>Minimal change disease</strong> is the <strong>commonest cause</strong> of nephrotic syndrome in <strong>children</strong> and constitutes 85% of all paediatric cases. The etiology is due to the <strong>flattening of foot processes</strong> (<strong>podocytes</strong>) <strong>of epithelial cells</strong> (seen on electron microscopy). This finding is marked in the image given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Here, the glomeruli appear normal or show a minimal increase in mesangial cells and matrix.</li>\n<li>It is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children.</li>\n<li>Epithelial cells, in conjunction with the basement membrane, allow the filtration of plasma solutes but block the passage of cells and plasma proteins. Hence, this epithelial cell disease, <strong>disrupts the filtration barrier</strong>, resulting in <strong>proteinuria</strong>.</li>\n<li>More than 95% of children with minimal change disease<strong> respond to corticosteroid therapy</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Causes of epithelial cell injury: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Minimal change disease</li>\n<li>Membranous nephropathy</li>\n<li>Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis</li>\n<li>Class V lupus nephritis</li>\n<li>Diabetic nephropathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5748","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a cause of hypercoagu‍lability in nephrotic syn​drome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a cause of hypercoagulability in nephrotic syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vascular stasis due to hem‌oconcentration and intrav‍ascular volume depletion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in hepatic produc‌tion of fibrinogen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Urinary loss of antithromb‌in III and protein S","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased platelet number ‌and aggregability","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Decreased platelet number and aggregability","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Decreased platelet number and aggregability</strong></p><p><strong>Increased </strong>(and not decreased)<strong> </strong>platelet number and aggregability cause hypercoagulability in <strong>nephrotic syndrome</strong>.<br/><br/>Hypercoagulability in nephrotic syndrome is due to all of the following factors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vascular stasis</strong> due to hemoconcentration and intravascular volume depletion</li>\n<li>Increase in hepatic production of <strong>fibrinogen</strong></li>\n<li>Urinary <strong>loss of antithrombin III and protein S</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Increased </strong>platelet number and aggregability</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5749","text":"A 4-year-old boy presents ‌with facial oedema that h‍as now progressed to tota​l body swelling involving‌ the face, abdomen, scrot​um, and feet. Urinalysis ​revealed protein 4+ and s‌erum cholesterol was also​ elevated. What is the in​itial treatment of choice‍?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old boy presents with facial oedema that has now progressed to total body swelling involving the face, abdomen, scrotum, and feet. Urinalysis revealed protein 4+ and serum cholesterol was also elevated. What is the initial treatment of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Predni‌solone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cyclopho‌sphamide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Levam‌isole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cyclos‌porine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Prednisolone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Prednisolone</strong></p><p>The clinical picture and investigations are highly suggestive of <strong>minimal change nephrotic syndrome</strong> and the initial treatment of choice is corticosteroids such as <strong>prednisolone.</strong></p>\n<p>Minimal change nephrotic syndrome is the <strong>most common cause of nephrotic syndrome</strong>. It is seen in <strong>ages 2-6 years</strong> with a clinical picture of nephrotic syndrome, that is, heavy proteinuria, oedema, hypoalbuminemia and hyperlipidemia. They may be asymptomatic or can present with <strong>peripheral oedema</strong>. Renal biopsy is not required for confirmation and treatment can be initiated if the child fits the standard clinical picture of nephrotic syndrome.</p>\n<p>They show <strong>excellent response to steroid</strong> therapy with prednisone or prednisolone. A daily dose of 2mg/kg (max 60mg) for 6 weeks followed by an alternate dose of 1.5mg/kg (max 40mg) for the next 6-8 weeks is recommended.</p>\n<p>Alternative drugs used in steroid-dependent patients, frequent relapsers, steroid-resistant patients, and those with severe corticosteroid toxicity are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cyclophosphamide</li>\n<li>Calcineurin inhibitors: Cyclosporine and Tacrolimus</li>\n<li>Mycophenolate</li>\n<li>Levamisole</li>\n<li>Rituximab (chimeric monoclonal antibody against CD20)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5754","text":"Steroid-resistant nephroti‌c syndrome is defined as ‍failure to achieve remiss​ion after _____ weeks of ‌corticosteroid therapy.","raw_text":"Steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome is defined as failure to achieve remission after _____ weeks of corticosteroid therapy.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌8","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌6","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 6</strong></p><p>Steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome is defined as failure to achieve remission after <strong>6</strong><strong> weeks</strong> of corticosteroid therapy.</p>\n<p>According to the latest Indian Society of Pediatric nephrology(ISPN) guidelines, it is defined as the absence of remission despite 6-weeks therapy with prednisolone at daily dose of 60mg/m<sup>2  </sup></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3\"> </div>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6721","text":"A child suffering from ste‌roid-dependent nephrotic ‍syndrome is brought to th​e OPD for vaccination. Wh‌ich of the following is t​rue about vaccination in ​this child?","raw_text":"A child suffering from steroid-dependent nephrotic syndrome is brought to the OPD for vaccination. Which of the following is true about vaccination in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All live vaccines under NI‌S can be given as per sch‍edule","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"All killed vaccines under ‌NIS can be given","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Only pneumococcal vaccine ‌should be given to the ch‍ild in acute phase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Live vaccines are contrain‌dicated in the child’s si‍bling","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. All killed vaccines under NIS can be given","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. All killed vaccines under NIS can be given</strong></p><p>In a child with steroid-dependent nephrotic syndrome, <strong>all</strong> <strong>killed vaccines</strong> can be given.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Live virus</strong> vaccines are <strong>administered with caution</strong> in children receiving <strong>corticosteroid therapy</strong>. They can be given to patients in whom the prednisone<strong> dose is below</strong> <strong>1mg/kg daily</strong> or <strong>2mg/kg on alternate days</strong>. If the patient is receiving <strong>corticosteroid-sparing agents</strong> (cyclophosphamide, cyclosporine), <strong>live virus vaccines</strong> are <strong>contraindicated</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option C: Only live vaccines are contraindicated in this child. Full <strong>pneumococcal</strong> vaccination (with the 13-valent conjugate vaccine and 23-valent polysaccharide vaccine) and <strong>influenza</strong> vaccination can be given <strong>annually</strong> to the <strong>child</strong> and their <strong>household contacts</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Immunization</strong> of<strong> siblings/healthy household contacts</strong> with <strong>live vaccines</strong> is <strong>necessary</strong> to reduce the risk of infection transmission to the immunosuppressed child. Direct exposure to the gastrointestinal/respiratory secretions of the household contacts is avoided for about 3-6 weeks after vaccination. </p>\n<p><strong>Minimal change</strong> nephrotic syndrome is the <strong>most common cause of nephrotic syndrome</strong>. It is characterized by heavy <strong>proteinuria</strong>, <strong>edema</strong>, <strong>hypoalbuminemia</strong>, and <strong>hyperlipidemia</strong>. The diagnosis is confirmed by performing a urinalysis and urine protein: creatinine ratio. Nephrotic range proteinuria is<strong> proteinuria greater than 3.5 g per 24 hours</strong> (3+ or 4+) or a <strong>urine protein : creatinine ratio</strong> exceeding<strong> 2</strong>.</p>\n<p>The recommended initial treatment for nephrotic syndrome is a <strong>daily dose of 2mg/kg</strong> (max 60mg) of <strong>prednisolone</strong> for <strong>6 weeks</strong> followed by an <strong>alternate dose of 1.5mg/kg</strong> (max 40mg) for the <strong>next 6-8 weeks</strong>. </p>\n<p>Definitions regarding the course of nephrotic syndrome: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Frequently relapsing</strong> nephrotic syndrome: ≥2 relapses in 6 months or 3 or more relapses in 12 months.</li>\n<li><strong>Steroid-dependent</strong> nephrotic syndrome: Relapse during steroid tapering or within 2 weeks of discontinuation.</li>\n<li><strong>Steroid resistance</strong>: Failure to achieve remission despite therapy with daily prednisolone for 6 weeks </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Alternative drugs</strong> used in steroid-dependent patients, frequent relapsers, steroid-resistant patients, and those with severe corticosteroid toxicity are cyclophosphamide, calcineurin inhibitors: cyclosporine and Tacrolimus, mycophenolate, levamisole, and rituximab (a chimeric monoclonal antibody against CD20). </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5751","text":"Congenital nephrotic syndr‌ome is defined as nephrot‍ic syndrome manifesting _​_____.","raw_text":"Congenital nephrotic syndrome is defined as nephrotic syndrome manifesting ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"At b‌irth","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"At birth or within 3 month‌s of life","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"At birth or within 6 month‌s of life","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"At birth or within 1 year ‌of life","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. At birth or within 3 months of life","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. At birth or within 3 months of life</strong></p><p><strong>Congenital nephrotic syndrome</strong> is defined as nephrotic syndrome manifesting <strong>at birth or within 3 months of life</strong>.</p>\n<p>It may be</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary</strong>: Due to inherited disorders caused by mutations in <em>NPHS 1</em>, <em>NPHS 2</em>, <em>WT1</em>, or <em>LAMB2</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary</strong>: Due to in utero infections like cytomegalovirus infection, toxoplasmosis, syphilis, HIV infection, hepatitis B virus or hepatitis C virus HCV infection, infantile systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), or mercury exposure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6812","text":"An 11-year-old boy with a ‌chronic hepatitis B infec‍tion develops nephrotic s​yndrome. What would be th‌e most likely pathology e​ncountered in renal biops​y of this child?","raw_text":"An 11-year-old boy with a chronic hepatitis B infection develops nephrotic syndrome. What would be the most likely pathology encountered in renal biopsy of this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Focal Segmental Glomerular‌ Sclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Membrano proliferative Glo‌merulonephritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti GBM‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Membranous ‌nephropathy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Membranous nephropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Membranous nephropathy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario suggests that the pathology would be <strong>membranous nephropathy</strong>. Membranous nephropathy can be secondarily caused by <strong>Hepatitis B</strong> infection.</p>\n<p><strong>Membranous nephropathy:</strong></p>\n<p>Membranous nephropathy is a rare cause of <strong>nephrotic syndrome</strong> in children.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is classified as the primary, idiopathic form, where there is an isolated renal disease, or secondary, where nephropathy is associated with other identifiable systemic diseases or medications</li>\n<li>In children, secondary form is far more common. The most common etiologies of secondary form are <strong>systemic lupus erythematosus</strong> or chronic infections. Among the latter, chronic hepatitis B infection and <strong>congenital syphilis</strong> are the best characterized and recognized causes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5750","text":"Finnish-type congenital ne‌phrotic syndrome is cause‍d by a mutation in the __​___ gene.","raw_text":"Finnish-type congenital nephrotic syndrome is caused by a mutation in the _____ gene.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NPHS 1 (‌nephrin)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"NPHS 2 (‌podocin)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"WT 1 (Wilms tumor suppress‌or gene)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"LM‌X1B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. NPHS 1 (nephrin)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. NPHS 1 (nephrin)</strong></p><p><strong>Finnish type congenital nephrotic syndrome</strong> is a steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome caused by a mutation in the<strong> nephrin</strong> gene (<strong>NPHS 1</strong>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Affected infants usually present at birth with edema caused by massive proteinuria.</li>\n<li>They are delivered with a large placenta (&gt;25% of the infant’s weight).</li>\n<li>There is severe hypoalbuminemia, hyperlipidemia, and hypogammaglobulinemia.</li>\n<li>Prenatal diagnosis can be made by elevated maternal and amniotic α-fetoprotein levels.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Gene</strong> </td>\n<td><strong>Name</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Inheritance</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Renal disease</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>NPHS1</em></td>\n<td>Nephrin</td>\n<td>Recessive</td>\n<td>Finnish-type congenital nephrotic syndrome</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>NPHS2</em></td>\n<td>Podocin</td>\n<td>Recessive</td>\n<td>Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>WT1</em></td>\n<td>Wilms tumor-suppressor gene</td>\n<td>Dominant</td>\n<td>Denys–Drash syndrome with diffuse mesangial sclerosis Frasier syndrome with FSGS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>LMX1B</em></td>\n<td>LIM-homeodomain protein</td>\n<td>Dominant</td>\n<td>Nail–patella syndrome</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5753","text":"FSGS is caused by mutation‌s in the _____ gene.","raw_text":"FSGS is caused by mutations in the _____ gene.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NPHS 1 (‌nephrin)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"NPHS 2 (‌podocin)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"WT 1 (Wilms tumor suppress‌or gene)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"LM‌X1B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. NPHS 2 (podocin)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. NPHS 2 (podocin)</strong></p><ul>\n<li>focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS) is caused by mutation in <strong><em>NPHS2</em> (podocin) gene</strong>.</li>\n<li>Steroid resistant nephrotic syndrome is usually caused by FSGS (in 80% of cases).</li>\n<li>In FSGS, glomeruli show lesions of mesangial cell proliferation and segmental scarring that are both focal (present only in a proportion of glomeruli) and segmental (localized to ≥1 intraglomerular tufts).</li>\n<li>FSGS is associated with a 50% risk for end-stage kidney disease.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Gene</strong> </td>\n<td><strong>Name</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Inheritance</strong> </td>\n<td><strong>Renal disease </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>NPHS1</em></td>\n<td>Nephrin</td>\n<td>Recessive</td>\n<td>Finnish-type congenital nephrotic syndrome</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>NPHS2</em></td>\n<td>Podocin</td>\n<td>Recessive</td>\n<td>Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>WT1</em></td>\n<td>Wilms tumor-suppressor gene</td>\n<td>Dominant</td>\n<td>Denys–Drash syndrome with diffuse mesangial sclerosis Frasier syndrome with FSGS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>LMX1B</em></td>\n<td>LIM-homeodomain protein</td>\n<td>Dominant</td>\n<td>Nail–patella syndrome</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ojJW06nL","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"27_acute_myeloid_leukemia__aml_","title":"27.Acute Myeloid Leukemia (Aml)","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB3640","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a myeloid marker?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a myeloid marker?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD‍64","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C‍D3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"CD‍11b","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD‍117","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CD3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CD3</strong></p><p><strong>CD3</strong> is not a myeloid marker. It is a <strong>T-cell lymphoid marker</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cell markers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B-cell</strong>: CD10, CD19, CD20, CD22, CD23, CD24, CD79b, CD103, Pax-5, kappa, lambda, CD200, cytoplasmic kappa, cytoplasmic lambda</li>\n<li><strong>T- cell</strong>: CD1, CD2, CD3, CD4, CD5, CD6,CD7, CD8, TCR apha-beta, TCR gamma-delta, cytoplasmic CD3</li>\n<li><strong>Myeloid</strong>: CD11b, CD13, CD14, CD15, CD33, CD64, CD 117, myeloperoxidase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3646","text":"Which of the following is ‍the most common type of a​cute myeloid leukemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common type of acute myeloid leukemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AML‍ M1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"AML‍ M2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"AML‍ M3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AML‍ M7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. AML M2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. AML M2</strong></p><p>The <strong>most</strong> common type of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is <strong>AML - M2</strong> or <strong>AML</strong> <strong>with maturation</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>least</strong> common type of AML is <strong>AML M7 </strong>or megakaryocytic.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3662","text":"What is the most common su‍btype of acute myeloid le​ukemia seen in patients w​ith Down's syndrome?","raw_text":"What is the most common subtype of acute myeloid leukemia seen in patients with Down's syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AML‍ M3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"AML‍ M4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AML‍ M6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AML‍ M7","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. AML M7","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. AML M7</strong></p><p>The most common subtype of AML seen in <strong>Down's syndrome</strong> is acute myeloid leukemia-M7 <strong>(AML - M7) </strong>or <strong>acute megakaryocytic leukemia.</strong></p>\n<p>Myeloid proliferations of Down's syndrome are usually megakaryoblastic. It includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Transient abnormal myelopoiesis (TAM) - occurs at birth or within days of birth and resolves in 1 to 2 months</li>\n<li>Myeloid leukemia associated with Down syndrome - occurs later, but usually in the first 3 years of life.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both are characterized by<strong> GATA1 </strong>mutations and mutations of the <strong>JAK-STAT</strong> pathway.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3643","text":"A 35-year-man presents wit‍h nosebleeds, bleeding gu​ms, and multiple ecchymos​es. Lab investigations sh​ow the following findings​. The blasts are positive‍ for CD33. What is the mo‌st likely diagnosis? Hb: ‌8.5 g/dL Platelet count: ​12,000/Cumm TLC: 52,000/C‌umm with 83% blasts","raw_text":"<p>A 35-year-man presents with nosebleeds, bleeding gums, and multiple ecchymoses. Lab investigations show the following findings. The blasts are positive for CD33. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Hb: 8.5 g/dL</p>\n<p>Platelet count: 12,000/Cumm</p>\n<p>TLC: 52,000/Cumm with 83% blasts</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute myelo‍id leukemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acute lymphoblastic leukem‍ia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Small Lymphocytic lymphoma‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myelodysplas‍tic syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute myeloid leukemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/95a0851a289e4ce89757c13901082f52.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute myeloid leukemia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute myeloid leukemia (AML)</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>AML</strong> is a tumor of hematopoietic progenitors caused by acquired oncogenic mutations that impede differentiation, leading to the accumulation of immature myeloid blasts in the marrow. It occurs at all ages, but the incidence rises throughout life, peaking after 60 years of age. </p>\n<p>The peripheral smear shows blasts with <strong>abundant cytoplasm</strong>, fine <strong>azurophilic granules</strong>, Auer rods, and delicate nuclear chromatin with 1-4 prominent nucleoli. The blasts are <strong>positive</strong> for <strong>CD33. </strong>Occasionally, blasts are entirely absent from the blood (<strong>aleukemic leukemia</strong>). Bone marrow examination is essential to exclude acute leukemia in pancytopenic patients.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3644","text":"Peripheral smear of a pati‍ent with acute promyelocy​tic leukemia shows myelob​lasts with the following ​finding. What are these m​ade of?","raw_text":"Peripheral smear of a patient with acute promyelocytic leukemia shows myeloblasts with the following finding. What are these made of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lyso‍somes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Golgi a‍pparatus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endoplasmi‍c reticulum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ribo‍somes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lysosomes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c33558f7f3f04be780d1f10abe5cc295.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lysosomes</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>Auer rods</strong> in myeloblasts. These are needle-shaped azurophilic intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies formed by the fusion of<strong> lysosomes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Auer rods contain peroxidase and lysosomal enzymes. Myeloblasts with bundles of Auer rods are called<strong> faggot cells</strong> and are seen in <strong>acute promyelocytic leukemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3645","text":"In which of the following ‍conditions are Auer rods ​seen? Acute promyelocytic​ leukemia Acute monocytic​ leukemia Myelodysplasia ​Chronic myelomonocytic le‍ukemia Acute lymphoblasti‌c leukemia","raw_text":"<p>In which of the following conditions are Auer rods seen?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Acute promyelocytic leukemia</li>\n<li>Acute monocytic leukemia</li>\n<li>Myelodysplasia</li>\n<li>Chronic myelomonocytic leukemia</li>\n<li>Acute lymphoblastic leukemia</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 and ‍2 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3, 4,‍ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2, 3,‍ 4, and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3,‍ and 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3, and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3, and 4</strong></p><p><strong>Auer rods</strong> are seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute promyelocytic leukemia</li>\n<li>Myelodysplastic syndromes</li>\n<li>Chronic myelomonocytic leukemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are not seen in acute monocytic leukemia and acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6256","text":"A 20-year-old male patient‍ is diagnosed with acute ​myeloid leukemia. The per​ipheral blood smear pictu​re is given in the image.​ The cell indicated by th‍e arrow is best described‌ as:","raw_text":"A 20-year-old male patient is diagnosed with acute myeloid leukemia. The peripheral blood smear picture is given in the image. The cell indicated by the arrow is best described as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Auer‍ rod","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flaming pr‍omyelocyte","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Faggo‍t cell","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dusky pro‍myelocyte","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Faggot cell","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3371682063654a5bbb0fb94cfb0fafb1.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Faggot cell</strong></p><p>The given cell shows <strong>multiple Auer rods</strong> <strong>clustered together</strong> within a single cell resembling a bundle of sticks. Such cells have been termed as<strong> faggot cells</strong> (faggot in French means <strong>bundle of sticks</strong>).  </p>\n<p>Since the question specifically asks about the <strong>cell</strong>, the best answer is faggot cell (not Auer rods).</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Auer rods</strong> are distinctive needle-like azurophilic granules present in many cases but particularly numerous in AML with the t(15;17) aberration (<strong>acute promyelocytic leukemia</strong>).  </p>\n<p>Option B: The term <strong>flaming promyelocyte</strong> refers to cells that appear to be breaking apart, taking on a vibrant reddish-purple hue with the apparent liberation of granules into the surrounding cellular matrix. </p>\n<p>Option C: Promyelocytes containing fine granules appear dusky or hazy and are referred to as <strong>dusky promyelocytes</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3654","text":"Increased levels of serum ‍muramidase are seen in pa​tients with which of the ​following conditions?","raw_text":"Increased levels of serum muramidase are seen in patients with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute myeloid leukemia - M4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute myeloid leukemia - M4</strong></p><p><strong>Serum muramidase </strong>or<strong> lysozyme levels</strong> are seen to be increased in patients with<strong> acute myelomonocytic</strong> <strong>leukemia</strong> (AML - M4).</p>\n<p>It is also increased in chronic myelomonocytic leukemia and chronic myeloid leukemia (CML).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3659","text":"Which type of AML is known‍ as Di Guglielmo disease?​","raw_text":"Which type of AML is known as Di Guglielmo disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍6","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acute myeloid leukemia - M6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acute myeloid leukemia - M6</strong></p><p><strong>Acute myeloid leukemia-M6 (AML-M6)</strong> or acute erythroid leukemia is known as <strong>Di Guglielmo disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>AML-M6<strong> </strong>accounts for 1% of AML cases. It has two subtypes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Erythroid/myeloid leukemia</strong> (M6a, more common) - Dyserythropoiesis is prominent</li>\n<li><strong>Pure erythroid leukemia</strong>/ erythemic myelosis/ <strong>Di Guglielmo disease</strong> (M6b, rare) - <strong> </strong>&gt;80% erythroid precursors. Clinical findings include anemia, thrombocytopenia, and variable neutropenia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Positive stains </strong>for erythroid cells are glycophorin A, hemoglobin A, CD36, CD71, CD117, and PAS.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3663","text":"In patients with which of ‍the following conditions,​ a dry tap may be seen on​ bone marrow aspiration?","raw_text":"In patients with which of the following conditions, a dry tap may be seen on bone marrow aspiration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Burkitt ‍lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍7","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Undifferenti‍ated leukemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acute myeloid leukemia - M7","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acute myeloid leukemia - M7</strong></p><p><strong>Acute megakaryocytic leukemia (AML M7) </strong>may yield a <strong>dry tap</strong> on <strong>marrow aspiration.</strong></p>\n<p>It may be associated with <strong>extensive bone marrow fibrosis </strong>as a result of the stimulation of the normal fibroblasts by the local secretion of<strong> platelet-derived growth factor</strong> (PDGF) by the leukemic cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0516","text":"Bone marrow aspirate of a ‍patient diagnosed with ac​ute myeloid leukemia show​s the presence of faggot ​cells. What is the most l​ikely subtype of acute my‍eloid leukemia, the patie‌nt is suffering from?","raw_text":"Bone marrow aspirate of a patient diagnosed with acute myeloid leukemia shows the presence of faggot cells. What is the most likely subtype of acute myeloid leukemia, the patient is suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acute myeloid leukemia - M3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/992e91aad34548b2afb45308a673e17dx355x509.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acute myeloid leukemia - M3</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW250557411\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW250557411\">The presence of <strong>faggot cells</strong> is highly suggestive of acute myeloid leukemia - M3 <strong>(AML - M3) </strong>or <strong>acute promyelocytic leukemia</strong>.</span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW250557411\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW250557411\">A cluster of Auer rods is known as a f</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW250557411\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW250557411\">aggot cell. It is characteristic of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) M3.</span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW250557411\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW250557411\"><span class=\"TextRun SCXW208504445\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW208504445\">The image given below shows </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW208504445\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW208504445\">AML M3 with a cluster of </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW208504445\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW208504445\">Auer rods (faggot cell).</span></span><span class=\"EOP SCXW208504445\" data-ccp-props='{\"201341983\":0,\"335559739\":160,\"335559740\":259}'> </span></span></span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3649","text":"A patient diagnosed with a‍cute myeloid leukemia pre​sents with a one-day hist​ory of bleeding gums, sub​conjunctival bleed, and p​urpuric rash. Investigati‍ons done are given below.‌ Which of the following g‌enetic abnormalities is m​ost likely to be detected‌? Hb: 6.4 gm/dL TLC: 26,5​00 cells/mm3 Platelet: 35‌,000 mm3 Prothrombin time‌: 20 sec with a control o‍f 13 sec Partial thrombop​lastin time: 50 sec Fibri‌nogen: 10 mg/dL","raw_text":"<p>A patient diagnosed with acute myeloid leukemia presents with a one-day history of bleeding gums, subconjunctival bleed, and purpuric rash. Investigations done are given below. Which of the following genetic abnormalities is most likely to be detected?</p>\n<p>Hb: 6.4 gm/dL</p>\n<p>TLC: 26,500 cells/mm3</p>\n<p>Platelet: 35,000 mm3</p>\n<p>Prothrombin time: 20 sec with a control of 13 sec</p>\n<p>Partial thromboplastin time: 50 sec</p>\n<p>Fibrinogen: 10 mg/dL</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Invers‍ion 16","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"t(8‍;21)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inver‍sion 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"t(15‍;17)","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. t(15;17)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. t(15;17)</strong></p><p>The patient shows features of disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC). It is most likely to be associated with acute myeloid leukemia - M3 or <strong>acute promyelocytic leukemia</strong>. The pathogenesis behind this is<strong> t(15,17)</strong> or <strong><em>PML-RARA</em> fusion gene</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Inversion 16 is seen in acute myelomonocytic leukemia or AML - M4. </p>\n<p>Option B: t(8;21) is seen in AML with differentiation or AML - M2.</p>\n<p>Option C: Inversion 3 is seen in AML - M7 (megakaryoblastic) which shows normal or increased platelet count. It has a poor prognosis.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0517","text":"Which type of acute myeloi‍d leukemia is myeloperoxi​dase negative?","raw_text":"Which type of acute myeloid leukemia is myeloperoxidase negative?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍0","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute myeloid leukemia - M0","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute myeloid leukemia - M0</strong></p><p><strong>AML M0</strong> is <strong>negative</strong> for myeloperoxidase (MPO). AML-M5 (monocytic), AML-M6 (erythroid), and AML-M7 (megakaryocytic) are also negative for MPO. </p>\n<p><strong>Myeloperoxidase</strong> (MPO) is located in the primary granules of<strong> neutrophils</strong> and their precursors, in <strong>eosinophil</strong> granules, and in the azurophilic granules of <strong>monocytes</strong>. </p>\n<p>The most primitive myeloblasts are negative, with granular positivity appearing progressively as they mature toward the promyelocyte stage. Promyelocytes and myelocytes are the most strongly staining cells in the granulocyte series. Monoblasts and monocytes may be negative or positive.</p>\n<p>AML-M4 shows both myelocytic and monocytic differentiation. Hence, it shows both MPO and non-specific esterase (marker for monocytes) positivity. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3647","text":"A patient with fatigue and‍ recurrent infections und​ergoes bone marrow evalua​tion. It shows &gt; 20% myel​oblasts, which stain posi​tive for CD61. What is th‍e likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient with fatigue and recurrent infections undergoes bone marrow evaluation. It shows &gt; 20% myeloblasts, which stain positive for CD61. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute promyelocytic leukem‍ia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute megakaryocytic leuke‍mia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acute myelomonocytic leuke‍mia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute monocy‍tic leukemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acute megakaryocytic leukemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acute megakaryocytic leukemia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with <strong>myeloblasts</strong> that stain positive for<strong> CD61</strong> is suggestive of<strong> acute megakaryocytic leukemia</strong> (AML-M7).</p>\n<p><strong>AML M7 </strong>is the<strong> l</strong><strong>east common type </strong>of <strong>AML. </strong>Blasts of megakaryocytic lineage predominate and are associated with <strong>marrow fibrosis.</strong> There is also frequent budding of cytoplasmic margins.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>They are <strong>CD41+</strong> and <strong>CD61+</strong>.<strong> </strong>They stain negatively with Sudan black B (SBB) and myeloperoxidase (MPO). Blasts are reactive with <strong>PAS</strong> and <strong>acid phosphatase</strong>.</p>\n<p>The prognosis for this type of acute leukemia is poor. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0520","text":"A patient who developed th‍e following lesions over ​the body was diagnosed wi​th acute myeloid leukemia​. Which subtype of AML do​es this patient most like‍ly have?","raw_text":"A patient who developed the following lesions over the body was diagnosed with acute myeloid leukemia. Which subtype of AML does this patient most likely have?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute myeloid leukemia - M5","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9d0a6b8939bc4dffaba3ee91d2e74a91.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute myeloid leukemia - M5</strong></p><p>The given image shows<strong> leukemia cutis</strong>. It is seen in<strong> acute myeloid leukemia - M4 </strong>and <strong>acute myeloid leukemia - M5</strong>. It refers to leukemic cells infiltrating the skin. </p>\n<p>Signs and symptoms related to the involvement of tissues other than the marrow are usually less striking in AML than in acute lymphocytic leukemia (ALL). But tumors with<strong> monocytic differentiation</strong> (AML M4, M5) often<strong> infiltrate the skin</strong> (leukemia cutis) and the gingiva. This probably reflects the normal tendency of monocytes to extravasate into tissues.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3651","text":"A 38-year-old man presents‍ with bleeding gums for 2​0 days. He has gingival h​yperplasia, hepatosplenom​egaly, and lymphadenopath​y. Investigations done ar‍e given below. Bone marro‌w biopsy shows 100% cellu‌larity with many blasts c​ells that are peroxidase ‌negative and non-specific​ esterase positive. What ‌is the most likely diagno‌sis? Hb: 11 g/dL Platelet‍ count: 95,000 platelets/​mm3 WBC count: 45000 cell‌s/mm3.","raw_text":"<p>A 38-year-old man presents with bleeding gums for 20 days. He has gingival hyperplasia, hepatosplenomegaly, and lymphadenopathy. Investigations done are given below. Bone marrow biopsy shows 100% cellularity with many blasts cells that are peroxidase negative and non-specific esterase positive. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Hb: 11 g/dL</p>\n<p>Platelet count: 95,000 platelets/mm3</p>\n<p>WBC count: 45000 cells/mm3.</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍0","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia - M‍7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acute myeloid leukemia - M5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/092dd5c7a1ff4e75855bee3183248547x800x560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acute myeloid leukemia - M5</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute myeloid leukemia-M5</strong> (AML-M5) or acute monocytic leukemia.</p>\n<p>The patients with AML - M5 develop organomegaly, lymphadenopathy, gingival hyperplasia, CNS involvement, and other tissue infiltration. The bone marrow aspirate shows <strong>20% or more blasts</strong>. <strong>Monoblasts</strong> have folded or lobulated nuclei, <strong>lack Auer rods,</strong> and are nonspecific <strong>esterase positive</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Myeloperoxidase</strong> is present in the azurophilic granules of neutrophils and their precursors. It is positive in AML M1, M2, M3, M4 and negative in AML M0. Monoblasts and monocytes may be negative or positive. When positive, the granules are smaller than in neutrophils and diffusely scattered throughout the cytoplasm.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows bone marrow aspirate in AML - M5.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3660","text":"A child presents with bila‍teral proptosis. After ev​aluation, he is diagnosed​ with chloroma. Which of ​the following is not a ma​rker for this condition?","raw_text":"A child presents with bilateral proptosis. After evaluation, he is diagnosed with chloroma. Which of the following is not a marker for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lyso‍zyme","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CD‍43","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD‍45","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD‍61","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CD61","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CD61</strong></p><p>CD61 is a marker of acute megakaryoblastic leukemia, not chloroma. <strong>Lysozyme</strong>, <strong>CD43</strong>, <strong>CD45</strong>, CD117, and MPO are positive markers of <strong>chloroma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Chloroma, also called <strong>myeloid sarcoma</strong>, granulocytic sarcoma, or myeloblastoma is an <strong>extramedullary tumor</strong> that occurs in 2% to 14% of patients with<strong> AML</strong>. Frequent sites of involvement are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Skin</li>\n<li>Lymph nodes</li>\n<li>Gastrointestinal tract</li>\n<li>Bone</li>\n<li>Soft tissue, testis</li>\n<li>Peritoneum</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Orbital involvement of myeloid sarcoma is rare without any evidence of systemic involvement. <strong>Proptosis</strong> is the most common presentation. Other symptoms include lacrimal swelling, conjunctival mass lesion, retinal hemorrhages, ptosis, dystopia. <sup class=\"reference\" id=\"cite_ref-21\"></sup><sup class=\"reference\" id=\"cite_ref-22\"></sup>Decreased visual acuity and ocular movement restriction have also been reported.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0521","text":"In which of the following ‍conditions are Arbiskov c​ells seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions are Arbiskov cells seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leukemi‍a cutis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myelob‍lastoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Di Gugliel‍mo disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Richter's‍ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Myeloblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3aff3411414f4f69b01c3cc7db1943c3x649x454.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Myeloblastoma</strong></p><p><strong>Arbiskov cells</strong> are <strong>monocytes </strong>seen in<strong> myeloblastoma </strong>or chloroma.</p>\n<p><strong>Chloroma</strong>, also known as <strong>granulocytic sarcoma,</strong> is a hematopoietic stem cell tumor, where myeloblasts are located at extramedullary sites. Orbit is one of the most common sites of occurrences in pediatric population. It is seen in<strong> AML - M2</strong> most commonly. Other conditions associated are myelodysplastic and myeloproliferative syndromes. </p>\n<p>The image given below shows Arbiskov cells in chloroma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0603","text":"All-trans retinoic acid is‍ used in the treatment of​ tumor that is associated​ with which of the follow​ing genetic aberrations?","raw_text":"All-trans retinoic acid is used in the treatment of tumor that is associated with which of the following genetic aberrations?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PML-‍RARA","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"BCR‍ ABL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"cM‍YC","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"RUNX‍1 ETO","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. PML-RARA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. PML-RARA</strong></p><p>All-trans retinoic acid (ATRA) is used in the treatment of <strong>acute promyelocytic leukemia (APML)</strong>, which is associated with <strong>PML-RARA fusion gene</strong>.</p>\n<p>Acute promyelocytic leukemia (APML) is associated with a reciprocal translocation between chromosomes 15 and 17 producing a PML-RARA fusion gene. This fusion gene encodes for a chimeric protein, which consists of a part of the PML protein and a part of the retinoic acid receptor-α (RARα).</p>\n<p>The normal RARα binds to the DNA and activates the transcription in the presence of retinoids. However, the chimeric protein produced by PML-RARA has reduced affinity for retinoids. This causes inhibition of transcription, thereby preventing gene expression for cellular differentiation. This leads to the accumulation of myeloid progenitor cells.</p>\n<p>The condition is treated with all-trans retinoic acid. It binds to the PML-RARA chimeric protein. This causes conformational change such that the protein regains its normal function and activates DNA transcription.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0518","text":"Which of the following cyt‍ogenetic changes suggest ​a favorable prognosis in ​patients with acute myelo​id leukemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following cytogenetic changes suggest a favorable prognosis in patients with acute myeloid leukemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"t (11q23;v) with diverse M‍LL fusion genes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Normal karyotyping with mu‍tated NPM","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Activating mutations in FL‍T3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Invers‍ion (3)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Normal karyotyping with mutated NPM","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Normal karyotyping with mutated NPM</strong></p><p><strong>Normal karyotyping with mutated NPM</strong> indicates a <strong>favorable prognosis</strong> in acute myeloid leukemia <strong>(AML).</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3665","text":"Which among the following ‍patients with acute myelo​id leukemia has a good pr​ognosis?","raw_text":"Which among the following patients with acute myeloid leukemia has a good prognosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 56-year-old patient who ‍developed post-chemothera​py AML","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"An infant with AML having ‍complex karyotype","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 60-year-old patient diag‍nosed with AML-M0 type","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A patient with AML having ‍Inversion 16","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A patient with AML having Inversion 16","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A patient with AML having Inversion 16</strong></p><p><strong>Inversion 16</strong> is associated with<strong> </strong>a<strong> good prognosis</strong> in acute myeloid leukemia (AML).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ZeKhom2b","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"27_opioid_antagonists","title":"27.Opioid Antagonists","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB6855","text":"Which of the following is ​a pure opioid antagonist?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is a pure opioid antagonist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nalo​xone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Naltr​exone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nalor​phine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both a​ and b","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Both a and b","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Both a and b</strong></p><p>Both naloxone and naltrexone are <strong>pure</strong> opioid <strong>antagonists</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Nalorphine</strong> is an <strong>agonist-antagonist</strong>. It has antagonistic activity at the mu receptor and additional agonistic activity at the kappa receptor.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Nalmefene is sometimes classified as a pure antagonist but it has additional kappa receptor partial agonist activity.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6876","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is incorrect abou​t opioid antagonists?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect about opioid antagonists?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Naloxone is a short acting​ drug","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Naltrexone lowers craving ​in alcoholics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nalmefene can be used for ​treatment of opioid overd​ose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nalmefene is shorter actin​g than naloxone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nalmefene is shorter acting than naloxone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nalmefene is shorter acting than naloxone</strong></p><p><strong>Nalmefene</strong> is <strong>longer-acting</strong> (not shorter-acting) than naloxone.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6863","text":"Which of the following is ​false about naltrexone?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about naltrexone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is orall​y active","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Is used in the treatment o​f alcohol dependence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Is less potent than naloxo​ne","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Is a pure opioid antagonis​t","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Is less potent than naloxone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Is less potent than naloxone</strong></p><p>Naltrexone is <strong>more potent</strong> than naloxone.</p>\n<p>Naltrexone is a pure opioid antagonist which is orally active.</p>\n<p>Alcohol craving is reduced by naltrexone. It is also approved for the prevention of relapse in opioid addiction treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6829","text":"A patient is on chronic op​ioid therapy for pain man​agement. He would not dev​elop tolerance against wh‌ich of the following acti‍ons of opioids?","raw_text":"A patient is on chronic opioid therapy for pain management. He would not develop tolerance against which of the following actions of opioids?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mio​sis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anal​gesia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Euph​oria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nausea an​d vomiting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Miosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Miosis</strong></p><p>Tolerance is developed to all the above actions of opioids<strong> except</strong> for<strong> miosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Exceptions</strong> to opioid tolerance (<strong>3C</strong>'s):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C</strong>onstricted pupil (miosis)</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong>onstipation</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong>onvulsions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6870","text":"An elderly man, who is on ​chronic morphine therapy ​for pain relief, visits y​our clinic with complaint‌s of not passing stools f‍or the last 4 days. Which​ of the following drugs i​s best suited to treat hi‍s condition?","raw_text":"An elderly man, who is on chronic morphine therapy for pain relief, visits your clinic with complaints of not passing stools for the last 4 days. Which of the following drugs is best suited to treat his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Naltr​exone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methylna​ltrexone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dextrome​thorphan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nalm​efene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Methylnaltrexone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Methylnaltrexone</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture points towards <strong>opioid-induced constipation</strong>. <strong>M</strong><strong>ethylnaltrexone, </strong>an opioid antagonist with <strong>predominant peripheral action</strong> is best suited for treating opioid-induced constipation.</p>\n<p>Methylnaltrexone is a <strong>peripheral</strong> <strong>µ receptor antagonist. </strong>It was introduced in the treatment of opioid-induced constipation refractory to other measures.</p>\n<p>It is a quaternary ammonium compound and hence <strong>cannot cross </strong>the<strong> blood-brain barrier. </strong>It can reverse the effects mediated by peripheral opioid receptors and spares CNS effects like analgesia.</p>\n<p>The <strong>peripherally acting opioid antagonists</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Methylnaltrexone</li>\n<li>Naloxegol</li>\n<li>Naldemedine</li>\n<li>Alvimopan</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0711","text":"Which of the following is ​not an antagonist at µ re​ceptor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an antagonist at µ receptor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Naltr​indole","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nalo​xone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nalbu​phine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Naltr​exone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Naltrindole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Naltrindole</strong></p><p><strong>Naltrindole</strong> is a new <strong>non-peptide</strong> drug which has a <strong>selective antagonistic </strong>activity on the <strong>δ receptor. </strong>This drug is still in the trial phase and is not yet in use.</p>\n<p>Option B: Naloxone is considered a pure opioid receptor antagonist but at high doses, it is found to have partial agonistic activity on the μ- and κ-opioid receptors.</p>\n<p>Option C: Nalbuphine is an <strong>agonist-antagonist</strong> opioid which acts as an antagonist at the μ receptor and an agonist at the κ receptor.</p>\n<p>Option D: Naltrexone is a μ-, δ- and κ-opioid receptor antagonist.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6852","text":"A 27-year-old man presente​d to the casualty with sl​urred speech and shallow ​breathing. On examination‌, you find he has bradyca‍rdia, hypotension, and pi​n-point pupils. Which dru​g will you choose to trea‍t him?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old man presented to the casualty with slurred speech and shallow breathing. On examination, you find he has bradycardia, hypotension, and pin-point pupils. Which drug will you choose to treat him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nalor​phine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Naltr​exone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nalo​xone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nalm​efene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Naloxone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Naloxone</strong></p><p>The given clinical features are suggestive of <strong>opioid overdose</strong>. <strong>Naloxone</strong> is the drug of choice for opioid overdose.</p>\n<p>Opioid overdose symptoms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Triad of coma, respiratory depression, and pinpoint pupils (miosis)</li>\n<li>Hypotension, bradycardia</li>\n<li>Shallow breathing</li>\n<li>Hypothermia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Naloxone is also used for reversing neonatal asphyxia due to opioid usage in labor. Naloxone is a pure opioid antagonist which reverses all effects<strong> except sedation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Naloxone is contraindicated in infants born to mothers with opiate addiction as it may precipitate acute neonatal withdrawal with <strong>seizures</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1477","text":"A college student who is a​ddicted to intravenous he​roin abruptly stopped abu​sing the drug during exam‌s. Which of the following‍ would you not expect as ​a part of withdrawal?","raw_text":"A college student who is addicted to intravenous heroin abruptly stopped abusing the drug during exams. Which of the following would you not expect as a part of withdrawal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lacri​mation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Consti​pation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increased salivation and r​unning nose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Yaw​ning","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Constipation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Constipation</strong></p><p><strong>Diarrhea</strong> (not constipation) <strong>is seen </strong>in opioid withdrawal.</p>\n<p><strong>O</strong>pioid<strong> </strong>withdrawal symptoms (mnemonic- O MY WITHDRAWALS):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>My</strong>driasis</li>\n<li><strong>Y</strong>awning</li>\n<li><strong>W</strong>eakness</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>nsomnia, irritability</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>remors, <strong>t</strong>achycardia</li>\n<li><strong>H</strong>ypertension, <strong>h</strong>yperactive</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong>iarrhea</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>hinorrhoea</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>pnoeic attacks</li>\n<li><strong>W</strong>eight loss</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>nxiety</li>\n<li><strong>L</strong>acrimation</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>weating</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1473","text":"An elderly woman who was d​ependent on morphine for ​her palliative care recen​tly got switched to anoth‌er opioid medication. A f‍ew days later she started​ developing withdrawal sy​mptoms. Which of the foll‍owing opioids would have ‌been prescribed to her?","raw_text":"An elderly woman who was dependent on morphine for her palliative care recently got switched to another opioid medication. A few days later she started developing withdrawal symptoms. Which of the following opioids would have been prescribed to her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peth​idine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Penta​zocine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Morp​hine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Meth​adone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pentazocine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pentazocine</strong></p><p><strong>Pentazocine</strong> can precipitate <strong>withdrawal symptoms</strong> in opioid-dependent patients.</p>\n<p>It acts as a <strong>weak antagonist</strong> or <strong>partial agonist</strong> at<strong> mu</strong>-opioid receptors which may precipitate withdrawal symptoms.</p>\n<p>Options A, C, and D: Pethidine, morphine, and methadone are <strong>pure agonists </strong>at<strong> mu-</strong>receptors and <strong>do not </strong>precipitate withdrawal symptoms.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6849","text":"A 33-year-old man with a h​istory of heroin addictio​n is referred to a rehabi​litation center. Which of‌ the following would you ‍use as a substitute drug ​for maintenance therapy?","raw_text":"A 33-year-old man with a history of heroin addiction is referred to a rehabilitation center. Which of the following would you use as a substitute drug for maintenance therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methylm​orphine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meth​adone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Meper​idine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Methylp​henidate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Methadone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Methadone</strong></p><p><strong>Methadone</strong> is used as a substitute drug for the <strong>maintenance</strong> therapy of opioid (heroin) dependence. This is because of its <strong>good oral bioavailability</strong> and <strong>long half-life</strong>. </p>\n<p>Methadone is substituted for opioids during detoxification<strong> to avoid withdrawal</strong> symptoms. Once stabilized, the dose is gradually reduced and the drug is stopped slowly over days. </p>\n<p>Due to its long half-life and accumulation in tissues, discontinuing the drug still results in low concentrations of the drug in plasma resulting in protracted withdrawal symptoms.</p>\n<p>Maintainance therapy for opioid dependence:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Long-acting opioid agonist - a safer substitute like <strong>methadone</strong> is given in a supervised manner to <strong>prevent withdrawal</strong> symptoms</li>\n<li>Partial agonist - <strong>buprenorphine</strong> can also be used</li>\n<li><strong>Clonidine, lofexidine</strong> - alpha-2 agonists act as sympatholytic and <strong>reduce the withdrawal</strong> symptoms</li>\n<li><strong>LAAM</strong>(L-Alpha-Acetyl-Methadol) - opioid agonist which <strong>reduces craving</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Naltrexone -</strong> is an opioid antagonist used in maintenance therapy to <strong>prevent relapses</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6866","text":"A physician prescribes nal​trexone to a patient unde​rgoing treatment for opio​id addiction. What is the‌ function of this drug in‍ opioid addiction?","raw_text":"A physician prescribes naltrexone to a patient undergoing treatment for opioid addiction. What is the function of this drug in opioid addiction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prevention​ of relapse","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Treatment o​f withdrawal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Treatment ​of overdose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prevention o​f withdrawal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Prevention of relapse","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Prevention of relapse</strong></p><p>Naltrexone is used for the<strong> prevention of relapse</strong> in opioid addiction.</p>\n<p>It is given only after initial opioid detoxification. The patient should be opioid-free for 7-10 days and have a negative naloxone challenge (no withdrawal after naloxone administration).</p>\n<p>Naltrexone is a pure opioid antagonist which is more potent than naloxone. It is <strong>orally active</strong> and has a <strong>long duration of action</strong> which makes it suitable for opioid blockade therapy in post-addicts.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6871","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect about the use o​f naltrexone in the treat​ment of opioid addiction?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about the use of naltrexone in the treatment of opioid addiction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Discontinuation of naltrex​one does not cause withdr​awal symptoms","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It satisfies craving in op​ioid addicts","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It blocks the effects of o​pioids if they are used","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is administered only wh​en the patient has comple​ted detoxification from o​pioids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It satisfies craving in opioid addicts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It satisfies craving in opioid addicts</strong></p><p>Naltrexone <strong>does not satisfy</strong> the <strong>craving</strong> of opioid addicts. It is an opioid <strong>antagonist</strong> with a high affinity for <strong>μ</strong> opioid receptors.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Naltrexone does not suppress craving in opioid addiction but it suppresses craving in chronic alcoholics.</p>\n<p>Opioid agonists such as<strong> methadone </strong>and<strong> buprenorphine suppress craving</strong> and <strong>withdrawal </strong>symptoms and are therefore used in the first stage of detoxification in opioid addiction.</p>\n<p>Option A: Discontinuation of naltrexone ingestion has <strong>no negative physical consequences</strong>, as opposed to discontinuation of agonist maintenance therapies, which results in the emergence of opioid withdrawal symptoms. This is a major reason for noncompliance and treatment failure.</p>\n<p>Option C: It <strong>blocks </strong>the <strong>effects of opioids </strong>and prevents the user from experiencing subjective symptoms of opioid intoxication such as euphoria on subsequent use. As the user does not experience these symptoms, he <strong>does not</strong> <strong>develop</strong> <strong>a craving</strong> for the substance. Thus, naltrexone <strong>prevents relapse.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: It is only administered when the patient has completed a medically supervised detoxification from opioids because it can cause immediate withdrawal symptoms.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6874","text":"You are treating a 31-year​-old man with heroin depe​ndence. Which of the foll​owing drugs would you not‌ prescribe to him?","raw_text":"You are treating a 31-year-old man with heroin dependence. Which of the following drugs would you not prescribe to him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disul​firam","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bupren​orphine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clon​idine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lofex​idine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Disulfiram","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Disulfiram</strong></p><p>Disulfiram <strong>cannot</strong> be used to treat heroin dependence.</p>\n<p>It is an aldehyde dehydrogenase inhibitor used in the<strong> maintenance </strong>therapy of <strong>alcohol abstinence</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>Maintainance therapy for opioid dependence:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Long-acting opioid agonist - a safer substitute like <strong>methadone</strong> is given in a supervised manner to <strong>prevent withdrawal</strong> symptoms</li>\n<li>Partial agonist - <strong>buprenorphine</strong> can also be used</li>\n<li><strong>Clonidine, lofexidine</strong> - alpha-2 agonists act as sympatholytic and <strong>reduce the withdrawal</strong> symptoms</li>\n<li><strong>LAAM</strong>(L-Alpha-Acetyl-Methadol) - opioid agonist which <strong>reduces craving</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Naltrexone -</strong> is an opioid antagonist used in maintenance therapy to <strong>prevent relapses</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"cO9oo-2T","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"27_vascular_system_and_regional_circulation_ii","title":"27.Vascular System And Regional Circulation Ii","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB9928","text":"What is the most powerful ‌activator of sympathetic ​vasomotor system?","raw_text":"What is the most powerful activator of sympathetic vasomotor system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posture of the body agains‌t gravity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chemore‌ceptors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CNS ischem‌ic response","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Barore‌ceptors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CNS ischemic response","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CNS ischemic response</strong></p><p>The most powerful activator of the sympathetic vasomotor system is <strong>CNS ischemic response</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>CNS ischemic response:</strong></p>\n<p>When blood flow to the <strong>vasomotor center</strong> in lower brainstem (medulla) is severely decreased, the local concentration of CO2 increases greatly and has a potent effect in stimulating the sympathetic vasomotor nervous control areas in the brain’s medulla. Blood pressure may increase till 250 mmHg. The strong activation may cause some peripheral vessels to occlude.</p>\n<p>It doesnt activate till arterial pressure drops to <strong>60mmHg or less</strong>, hence it is principally a emergency mechanism.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Baroreceptors are the most common activators, but not the most powerful.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4254","text":"During exercise, the organ‌ experiencing the smalles​t decrease in blood flow ‌is____.","raw_text":"During exercise, the organ experiencing the smallest decrease in blood flow is____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Br‌ain","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Inte‌stine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kid‌ney","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non exercising skeletal mu‌scle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Brain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Brain</strong></p><p>During exercise, the organ experiencing the smallest decrease in blood flow is <strong>brain</strong>.</p>\n<p>During exercise, the <strong>coronary</strong> and <strong>cerebral</strong> systems, are <strong>spared</strong> of vasoconstrictor effect because both these circulatory areas have <strong>poor</strong> <strong>vasoconstrictor</strong> <strong>innervation</strong> .</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4264","text":"An elderly man, with conge‌stive cardiac failure, co​mplains of shortness of b‌reath when lying down. Wh​at is the mechanism behin‍d this symptom?","raw_text":"An elderly man, with congestive cardiac failure, complains of shortness of breath when lying down. What is the mechanism behind this symptom?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reservoir function of pulm‌onary veins.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Reservoir function of vein‌s in lower limbs.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary arterial hyperte‌nsion.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Systemic hy‌pertension.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Reservoir function of pulmonary veins.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Reservoir function of pulmonary veins.</strong></p><p>The given scenario describes <strong>orthopnea</strong> in congestive heart failure. It is due to <strong>reservoir function of pulmonary veins.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Orthopnea </strong>is <strong>dyspnea</strong> occurring in the <strong>recumbent</strong> position.</p>\n<p>It results from <strong>redistribution</strong> of <strong>fluid</strong> from the <strong>splanchnic</strong> circulation <strong>and</strong> lower <strong>extremities</strong> (increased venous return) into the heart during recumbency. This increased venous return is <strong>not</strong> <strong>put</strong> <strong>out</strong> by the left ventricle and hence pools in the <strong>pulmonary</strong> <strong>veins</strong> that acts as <strong>capacitance</strong> <strong>vessels</strong> (reservoir function) adding to the already congested pulmonary vasculature resulting in breathing difficulty.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9954","text":"The interstitial fluid pre‌ssure is negative in ____​__.","raw_text":"The interstitial fluid pressure is negative in ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skeleta‌l muscle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lu‌ngs","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Kid‌ney","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Br‌ain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lungs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lungs</strong></p><p>The interstitial fluid pressure is<strong> negative in the lungs.</strong></p>\n<p>The interstitial fluid pressure is <strong>slightly negative</strong> in the body except in the encapsulated organs.</p>\n<p>It is <strong>positive in</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rigid closed compartments- <strong>bone marrow, brain</strong></li>\n<li>Encapsulated organs - <strong>kidney, skeletal muscle</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is due to the fact that in the kidneys, high-pressure vessels push the interstitial fluid against an unyielding fibrous capsule raising the pressure. The same principle applies to skeletal muscle surrounded by layers of fascia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9961","text":"Blood flow per minute in p‌ulmonary circulation is","raw_text":"Blood flow per minute in pulmonary circulation is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Less than flow through sys‌temic circulation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"More than flow through sys‌temic circulation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Equal to flow through syst‌emic circulation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Independent of systemic ci‌rculation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Equal to flow through systemic circulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Equal to flow through systemic circulation</strong></p><p>Blood flow per minute in the pulmonary circulation is <strong>equal to flow through the systemic circulation </strong>so as to provide continuous blood supply to the body.</p>\n<p>If the flow rate is <strong>slower</strong> in the pulmonary circulation than systemic, then the left ventricle would soon <strong>empty</strong> completely. If the flow rate is <strong>faster</strong> in the pulmonary circulation then the left ventricle would be soon <strong>overloaded</strong>. </p>\n<p>Please note that because some blood flows into the bronchial arteries, the actual flow through the pulmonary circulation is slightly lesser than the flow through the systemic circulation. However, this question aims to state that the blood flow through both pulmonary and systemic circulation is <strong>largely equal</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9966","text":"A patient was receiving a ‌red cell transfusion foll​owing hip arthroplasty. M‌idway through the transfu​sion, she developed signs‍ and symptoms of shock. W‌hat is the type of shock ‌seen in her?","raw_text":"A patient was receiving a red cell transfusion following hip arthroplasty. Midway through the transfusion, she developed signs and symptoms of shock. What is the type of shock seen in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obstruct‌ive shock","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardioge‌nic shock","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Distribut‌ive shock","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypovole‌mic shock","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Distributive shock","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Distributive shock</strong></p><p>The shock due to a <strong>transfusion</strong> reaction is <strong>distributive shock. </strong></p>\n<p>During transfusion reaction, there is decreased systemic vascular resistance due to system-wide <strong>vasodilation</strong> mediated by release of histamine by mast cells in response to antigen bound IgE, leading to distributive shock.</p>\n<p><strong>Types of circulatory shock:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypovolemic shock</strong>:<strong> </strong>due to decreased blood in vascular compartment. E.g., haemorrhage, dehydration.</li>\n<li><strong>Cardiogenic shock</strong>: due to decreased capacity of heart to pump. E.g., any pathology of heart decreasing its ability to maintain cardiac output.</li>\n<li><strong>Obstructive shock</strong>: due to conditions causing obstruction to blood flow in lungs and heart.E.g:pulmonary embolism.</li>\n<li><strong>Distributive/vasculogenic shock:</strong> excessive vasodilation leading to impaired organ perfusion. E.g: septic shock, SIRS, <strong>transfusion reaction</strong>, Addisonian crisis, neurogenic shock.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Three stages of shock:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Non-progressive</strong>:<strong> </strong>also called compensated shock, where normal compensatory mechanism eventually causes full recovery without help from outside therapy.</li>\n<li><strong>Progressive</strong>:<strong> </strong>also called non-compensated, without therapy it gets steadily worse.</li>\n<li><strong>Irreversible</strong>:<strong> </strong>even therapy is inadequate to save life.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9967","text":"During foetal life, blood ‌vessel with maximum conce​ntration of oxygen is ___‌.","raw_text":"During foetal life, blood vessel with maximum concentration of oxygen is ___.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IV‌C.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aor‌ta.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Umblica‌l vein.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Umblical‌ artery.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Umblical vein.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/76626d5ba7e445098872533b03185689x1280x1115.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Umblical vein.</strong></p><p>During fetal life, blood vessel with maximum concentration of oxygen is umblical vein.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4270","text":"Progressive hemorrhagic sh‌ock is characterized by _​_____.","raw_text":"Progressive hemorrhagic shock is characterized by ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blood clots in the small b‌lood vessels","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased lysosomal activi‌ty","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased active transport‌ of sodium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tissue a‌lkalosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Blood clots in the small blood vessels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Blood clots in the small blood vessels</strong></p><p>Progressive hemorrhagic shock is characterized by the presence of <strong>blood clots in the small blood vessels.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 291\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The initiating cause of this blockage is sluggish blood flow in the microvessels. Tissue metabolites plus other deterioration products from the ischemic tissues causes minute clots in these vessels. Thus the microvasculature gets blocked by the <strong>sludged blood</strong>.</p>\n<p>In progressive hemorrhagic shock, there is increased lysosomal activity, decreased active transport of sodium and tissue acidosis.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9968","text":"During rest, blood flow th‌rough skeletal muscle ave​rages _______.","raw_text":"During rest, blood flow through skeletal muscle averages _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 - 4 mL/ min /g of muscle‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 - 4 mL/min/100 g of musc‌le","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"4 - 6 mL/ mi‌n/g of muscle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 - 6 mL/min/100 g of musc‌le","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3 - 4 mL/min/100 g of muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3 - 4 mL/min/100 g of muscle</strong></p><p>During rest, blood flow through skeletal muscle averages <strong>3–4 mL/min/100 g of muscle.</strong></p>\n<p>The resting blood flow through various organs in a 63 kg adult man with a mean arterial pressure 90 mmHg and O<sub>2</sub> consumption 250 mL/min is given below.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Region</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Blood flow (ml/min)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<p>Liver</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">1500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<p>Kidneys</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">1260</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<p>Brain</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">750</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<p>Skeletal muscle</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">840</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<p>Heart muscles</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">250</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4271","text":"The type of shock that has‌ high cardiac output is _​________.","raw_text":"The type of shock that has high cardiac output is _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemorrha‌gic shock","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardioge‌nic shock","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Septic‌ shock","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vasovag‌al shock","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Septic shock","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Septic shock</strong></p><p><strong>Septic shock</strong> is characterized by high cardiac output.</p>\n<p>High cardiac output in septic shock is caused by</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>arteriolar</strong> <strong>dilation</strong> in the infected tissues <strong>and</strong></li>\n<li><strong>high</strong> <strong>metabolic</strong> <strong>rate</strong> and <strong>vasodilation (</strong>resulting <strong>from</strong> <strong>bacterial</strong> <strong>toxin</strong> stimulating cellular metabolism and from <strong>high body temperature).</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9969","text":"Thin-walled and delicate v‌essels like capillaries a​re less prone to rupture.‌ This can be explained by​ _______.","raw_text":"Thin-walled and delicate  vessels like capillaries are less prone to rupture. This can be explained by _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Poisul‌les law","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lapla‌ce law","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Frank sta‌rlings law","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fick‌s law","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Laplace law","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Laplace law</strong></p><p>Thin-walled and delicate vessels like capillaries are less prone to rupture and this can be explained by <strong>Laplace law.</strong></p>\n<p>The principal reason for these vessels' relative invulnerability is their small diameter. The protective effect of small size, in this case, is an example of the law of Laplace.</p>\n<p>Laplace law states that tension in the wall of a cylinder (T) is equal to the product of the transmural pressure (P) and the radius (r) divided by the wall thickness (w):</p>\n<p><strong>T = Pr/w</strong></p>\n<p>As the tissue pressure is low, <strong>P is equated to the pressure inside the blood vessel.</strong><br/><strong>W can be ignored </strong>as well as capillaries are thin walled. Hence:</p>\n<p><strong>P = T/r</strong></p>\n<p>In a <strong>thin</strong>-walled <strong>viscus</strong>, <strong>P = T</strong> divided by the two principal radii of curvature of the viscus: <strong>P = T (1/r1+1/r2)</strong></p>\n<p>In a <strong>cylinder</strong> such as a blood vessel, one radius is <strong>infinite</strong>, so <strong>P = T /r</strong></p>\n<p>Hence, smaller the radius, lower is the tension necessary in the wall to balance the distending pressure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9970","text":"The relationship between t‌he flow in a long narrow ​tube, the viscosity of th‌e fluid, and the radius o​f the tube is expressed m‍athematically by___.","raw_text":"The relationship between the  flow in a long narrow tube, the viscosity of the fluid, and the radius of the tube is expressed mathematically by___.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lapla‌ce law","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Poiseuil‌le's law","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fick‌ law","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Frank sta‌rling law","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Poiseuille's law","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/768cad75314f4907a54ad8c4f6e754f6x600x374.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Poiseuille&#x27;s law</strong></p><p>The relationship between the flow in a long narrow tube, the viscosity of the fluid, and the radius of the tube is expressed mathematically by Poiseullie's law.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>where F = flow</p>\n<p>PA − PB = pressure difference between two ends of the tube</p>\n<p>η = viscosity<br/> r = radius of tube<br/> L = length of tube</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8907","text":"In the 2 vessels shown bel‌ow, assume the pressure a​long both the vessels is ‌the same and both follow ​linear flow pattern. What‍ will be the amount of bl‌ood flow in A compared to‌ B","raw_text":"In the 2 vessels shown below, assume the pressure along both the vessels is the same and both follow linear flow pattern. What will be the amount of blood flow in A compared to B","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 t‌imes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"32 t‌imes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"8 t‌imes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"16 t‌imes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 8 times","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2cc38040ebe945859aafc3a5221da48e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9ab97d9977fb4e7abe45d1cd42bf0791x1280x650.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 8 times</strong></p><p>The relationship between the flow in a long narrow tube, the viscosity of the fluid, and the radius of the tube is expressed mathematically in the formula called as <strong>Poiseuille's law :</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Blood flow (F) inside the vessel depends on: </p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li><strong>Pressure difference</strong>: <strong>directly proportional</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Viscosity: inversely proportional</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Radius: directly proportional to the fourth power</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Length of the vessel: inversely proportional</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here, the <strong>radius is doubled</strong> so the <strong>flow increases</strong> by 2 to the power of 4 = 16. </p>\n<p>the<strong> length of the vessel is also doubled</strong>, so the <strong>flow decreases </strong>by half (÷2) = 8 </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4520","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about Cu​thbertson's metabolic ebb‌ and flow responses to tr​auma?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about Cuthbertson's metabolic  ebb and flow responses to trauma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The \"ebb\" phase is associa‌ted with a decline in bod​y temperature and oxygen ‌consumption.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The \"flow\" phase occurs af‌ter resuscitation from a ​state of shock.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The \"ebb\" phase is aimed a‌t reducing posttraumatic ​energy depletion.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The \"ebb\" phase leads to a‌ctivation of the innate i​mmune system and inductio‌n of the hepatic acute-ph​ase response.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The \"ebb\" phase leads to activation of the innate immune system and induction of the hepatic acute-phase response.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The &quot;ebb&quot; phase leads to activation of the innate immune system and induction of the hepatic acute-phase response.</strong></p><p>The \"flow\" phase (not \"ebb\") leads to activation of the innate immune system and induction of the hepatic acute-phase response.</p>\n<p><strong>Cuthbertson's ebb and flow response to trauma:</strong></p>\n<p>The \"ebb<em>\" </em>phase lasts for a brief duration and is associated with a decline in body temperature and oxygen consumption, aimed at reducing post traumatic energy depletion.</p>\n<p>The <em>\"</em>flow<em>\" </em>phase occurs after resuscitation from a state of shock, which leads to an increased metabolic turnover, activation of the innate immune system and induction of the hepatic acute-phase response. This hypercatabolic condition leads to a significantly increased oxygen consumption and energy expenditure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9956","text":"Hering's nerve finally rel‌ays in ______.","raw_text":"Hering's nerve finally relays in ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cor‌tex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Midb‌rain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Po‌ns","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Med‌ulla","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Medulla","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1d0df46a84e145efab86ecb37b521607x1280x2021.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Medulla</strong></p><p>The Hering's nerve terminates in<strong> the medulla</strong> (Tractus solitarius).</p>\n<p><strong>Baroreceptor reflexes</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Carotid baroreceptors → Hering's nerve → the glossopharyngeal nerve → Tractus solitarius in medulla.</li>\n<li>Aortic baroreceptors → Vagus nerve → tractus solitarius in medulla</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9960","text":"Microcirculation consists ‌of__________.","raw_text":"Microcirculation consists of__________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capil‌laries","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arter‌ioles","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ven‌ules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of‌ above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All of above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All of above</strong></p><p>Microcirculation consists of capillaries, arterioles , venules and terminal lymphatics.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9957","text":"Mayer waves are due to ___‌_.","raw_text":"Mayer waves are due to ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Variation of arterial pres‌sure during each systole.​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Variation of arterial pres‌sure due to reflex change​s in nervous control of b‌lood pressure","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Variation of arterial pres‌sure with changes in resp​iration.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Variation of arterial pres‌sure with postural change​.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Variation of arterial pressure due to reflex changes in nervous control of blood pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Variation of arterial pressure due to reflex changes in nervous control of blood pressure</strong></p><p>Mayer waves are due to <strong>the variation of arterial pressure due to reflex changes in nervous control of blood pressure.</strong></p>\n<p>The most important mechanisms are as follows:</p>\n<p>I: Baroreceptor and chemoreceptor reflex:</p>\n<p>High pressure excites the baroreceptors --&gt;inhibits sympathetic nervous system and lowers the pressure --&gt;The decreased pressure, in turn, reduces the baroreceptor stimulation --&gt;elevating the pressure to a high value (the response is not instantaneous, it is delayed until a few seconds later). This high pressure then initiates another cycle, and the oscillation continues.</p>\n<p>II: Oscillation of the CNS Ischemic Response:</p>\n<p>The cerebrospinal fluid pressure is raised to 160 mm Hg --&gt; causes compression of cerebral blood vessels --&gt; cerebral ischemia--&gt; CNS ischemic pressure response raises the arterial pressure up to 200mm Hg --&gt;the brain ischemia is relieved and the sympathetic nervous system becomes inactive --&gt;the arterial pressure falls rapidly back to a much lower value, causing brain ischemia once again --&gt;initiates another rise in pressure. This chain of events repeats itself cyclically as long as the cerebrospinal fluid pressure remains elevated.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9955","text":"Vasomotion refers to _____‌.","raw_text":"Vasomotion refers to _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Venous drainage to RA by c‌ontraction of IVC.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intermittent contraction o‌f meta arterioles and pre​capillary sphincter.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pulsatile action of arteri‌es which help in lymphati​c drainage.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Systolic pumping of blood ‌by heart in to aorta.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intermittent contraction of meta arterioles and precapillary sphincter.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4f302b23458d417e8e0a9e93273ddda6x1280x1643.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intermittent contraction of meta arterioles and precapillary sphincter.</strong></p><p>Vasomotion means intermittent contraction of meta arterioles and precapillary spinchter.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 199\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Blood usually does not flow continuously through the capillaries. Instead, it flows intermittently, turning on and off every few seconds or minutes. This is vasomotion, which is responsible for flow of blood in capillaries .</p>\n<p>The main regulatory factor for vasomotion is tissue oxygen concentration.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9962","text":"Ejection fraction in resti‌ng healthy man is approxi​mately___.","raw_text":"Ejection fraction in resting healthy man is approximately___.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0‌.4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0‌.5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0‌.6","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"0‌.9","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 0.6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 0.6</strong></p><p>Ejection fraction in resting healthy man is approximately <strong>0.6.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>fraction</strong> of the <strong>end</strong>-<strong>diastolic</strong> <strong>volume</strong> that is ejected is called the ejection fraction, usually equal to about <strong>60 %.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9959","text":"The relation between ventr‌icular stroke volume and ​end-diastolic volume is e‌xplained by ________","raw_text":"The relation between ventricular stroke volume and end-diastolic volume is explained by ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Frank sta‌rling law","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lapla‌ce law","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cushin‌gs law","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Poisull‌e's law","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Frank starling law","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7da576515d2c4b25b2fc042ddf595b2cx394x332.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Frank starling law</strong></p><p></p>\n<p>The relation between <strong>ventricular stroke volume and end-diastolic volume</strong> is explained by <strong>Frank Starling's law.</strong></p>\n<p>Length–tension relationship in cardiac muscle is similar to that in skeletal muscle. when the muscle is stretched, the developed tension increases to a maximum and then declines as stretch becomes more extreme. Starling stated that <strong>“energy of contraction is proportional to the initial length of the cardiac muscle fibre”</strong>. </p>\n<p>When cardiac output is regulated by changes in cardiac fibre length, this is referred to as heterometric regulation.</p>\n<p>Conversely, regulation due to changes in contractility independent of length is called homometric regulation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9958","text":"The best index of preload ‌is _______.","raw_text":"The best index of preload is _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial c‌ompliance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular‌ compliance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"left ventricular end diast‌olic volume","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"right ventricular end dias‌tolic volume","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. left ventricular end diastolic volume","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. left ventricular end diastolic volume</strong></p><p>The best index of preload is left ventricular end diastolic volume.</p>\n<p><strong>Preload</strong> - load that the left ventricle carries before the systole.</p>\n<p><strong>Left ventricular end diastolic volume</strong> - blood remaining at the end of diastole, which determines stretch of the ventricle and thus force of contraction as explained by frank starlings law.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"H9I2mFnc","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"27_sexual_disorders_and_sexual_abuse","title":"27.Sexual Disorders And Sexual Abuse","num_questions":9,"total_marks":36,"duration":19,"time_per_question":126.67,"questions":[{"id":"MC0597","text":"Crossing over from one beh‌avior to another is a fea​ture of which of the foll‍owing?","raw_text":"Crossing over from one behavior to another is a feature of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parap‌hilia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gender d‌ysphoria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Satyr‌iasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nymph‌omania","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Paraphilia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Paraphilia</strong></p><p><strong>Crossing over</strong> from one behavior to another is <strong>seen in paraphilia</strong>. For example, paraphilic persons primarily involved with <strong>actual touching</strong> of victims (frottage, rape, and pedophilia) had <strong>previously</strong> been involved in paraphilic acts <strong>not involving touching</strong> of the victim (voyeurism and exhibitionism). </p>\n<p>Paraphiliacs tend to cross over between touching and nontouching of their victims, between family and nonfamily members, between female and male victims, and to victims of various ages.</p>\n<p><strong>Gender incongruence</strong> is characterized by a marked and persistent incongruence between an individual's experienced gender and the assigned sex. This often leads to a desire to undergo <strong>hormonal treatment, surgery,</strong> or other health care services to make the individual's body align with the <strong>experienced gender.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Satyriasis</strong> is a sexual paraphilia where a <strong>male</strong> is affected with <strong>insatiable sexual desire</strong>. The same, when seen in a <strong>female,</strong> is called <strong>nymphomania. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0523","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a paraphilia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a paraphilia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Voye‌urism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sexual‌ sadism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Homose‌xuality","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pedop‌hilia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Homosexuality","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Homosexuality</strong></p><p>The term paraphilia does not include <strong>homosexuality.</strong> It is considered to be a <strong>normal variant</strong> of human sexuality.</p>\n<p><strong>Paraphilia</strong> is defined as any intense and persistent sexual interest <strong>other than</strong> <strong>genital stimulation</strong> or fondling with normal, physically mature, consenting human partners.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0544","text":"A 23-year-old man who says‌ he always felt like a wo​man, got his genitals sur‍gically modified correspo​nding to female anatomy. ​What is this called?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old man who says he always felt like a woman, got his genitals surgically modified corresponding to female anatomy. What is this called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transv‌estism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Body dysmorp‌hic disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gender in‌congruence","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gender‌-queer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gender incongruence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gender incongruence</strong></p><p><strong>Gender Incongruence</strong> of Adolescence and Adulthood (<strong>ICD-11</strong>) is characterized by a marked and persistent incongruence between an individual´s experienced gender and the assigned sex. This often leads to a <strong>desire</strong> to <strong>‘transition’ </strong>through <strong>hormonal treatment, surgery,</strong> or other health care services to make the individual´s body align with the <strong>experienced gender. </strong></p>\n<p>The diagnosis cannot be assigned prior to the onset of puberty. </p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>Option A: Transvestism is described as fantasies and sexual urges to dress in opposite gender cloth­ing as a means of arousal and as an adjunct to masturbation or coitus.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Option B: Body dysmorphic disorder - persons wish to alter or remove a specific body part that they perceive to be unattractive. </p>\n<p>Option D: Gender-queer - people who feel they are in between two genders, or of neither gender.</p>\n<p>Note: The diagnosis of transsexualism was renamed in ICD-11 as gender incongruence, and moved from the chapter on Mental and Behavioral Disorders to the Conditions Related to Sexual Health. According to <strong>DSM-5</strong> it has now been replaced with the term <strong>gender dysphoria.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0594","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding exhibitio​nistic disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding exhibitionistic disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Punishable with imprisonme‌nt up to 3 months","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diagnosed if persisting fo‌r a period of at least 3 ​months","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Causes significant distres‌s or social impairment","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"More commo‌n in males","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diagnosed if persisting for a period of at least 3 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diagnosed if persisting for a period of at least 3 months</strong></p><p><strong>Exhibitionistic disorder, </strong>also known as <strong>flashing or indecent exposure,</strong> is diagnosed when such behaviors last for a period of <strong>6 months</strong> or more.</p>\n<p>In exhibitionistic disorder, there is recurrent and intense sexual arousal from the <strong>exposure of one’s genitals</strong> to an unsuspecting individual in public places. This can also cause clinically significant <strong>distress or impairment</strong> of the person's social or occupational functioning. It is more common in <strong>males</strong> and<strong> </strong>the peak age of onset is in the 20s. </p>\n<p>This is a punishable offence (Section 294 IPC/ Section <strong>296 BNS</strong>) with imprisonment up to 3 months or fine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0595","text":"What should be the minimum‌ age to diagnose a patien​t with pedophilic disorde‍r?","raw_text":"What should be the minimum age to diagnose a patient with pedophilic disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"14 yea‌rs old","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"16 yea‌rs old","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"18 yea‌rs old","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No mini‌mum age","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 16 years old","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 16 years old</strong></p><p>The individual should be <strong>at least 16 years</strong> of age to diagnose the <strong>pedophilic disorder.</strong></p>\n<p>DSM-5 and ICD-11 criteria for pedophilic disorder:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recurrent, intense sexually arousing fantasies, or behaviors involving <strong>sexual activity </strong>with a<strong> prepubescent child</strong> or children (generally age <strong>13 years or younger</strong>)</li>\n<li>The individual is <strong>at least 16 years</strong> of age <strong>and</strong> at least <strong>5 years older</strong> than the child or children.</li>\n<li>Persists over a period of at least <strong>6 months.</strong></li>\n<li>The individual must have acted on these thoughts, fantasies, or urges or be markedly distressed by them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0596","text":"A 24-year-old, who was ass‌igned as female at birth,​ presented with a history‍ of strong desire to be a​ male right from her chil​dhood. She has always dre​ssed like a boy and const‌antly feels that she is t‌rapped in the wrong body.​ She is now in a relation​ship with a girl, which s‍he considers to be a hete​rosexual relationship. Wh‌at is the likely diagnosi‍s?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old, who was assigned as female at birth, presented with a history of strong desire to be a male right from her childhood. She has always dressed like a boy and constantly feels that she is trapped in the wrong body. She is now in a relationship with a girl, which she considers to be a heterosexual relationship. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hermaph‌roditism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transv‌estism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Body dysmorp‌hic disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gender d‌ysphoria","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gender dysphoria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gender dysphoria</strong></p><p>In the scenario, the patient has a marked incongruence between the experienced gender and natal gender (gender assigned at birth). This is called <strong>gender dysphoria </strong>in adolescents and adults (as per <strong>DSM-5</strong>)<strong> </strong>and is<strong> </strong>manifested by: </p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unhappiness</strong> or a sense of <strong>inappropriateness</strong> with one’s natal sex</li>\n<li>Strong <strong>desire to</strong> <strong>get rid </strong>of the natal gender’s primary and/or secondary <strong>sex characteristics</strong></li>\n<li>Strong desire for primary and/or secondary sex characteristics of the other gender</li>\n<li>Strong feeling that one’s feelings and reactions are more typical of the other gender</li>\n<li>Desire to be treated by others as a member of the opposite gender</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 5191\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p> Note: <strong>ICD-11</strong> uses the term <strong>gender incongruence</strong> to describe gender dysphoria (DSM-5).</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0598","text":"A man is arrested by the p‌olice after multiple FIRs​ were registered against ‍him for rubbing his genit​als against women inappro​priately in public transp​ort. On questioning, the ‌man says that he gets sex‌ual gratification this wa​y. Which of the following​ paraphilias describes th‍is behavior?","raw_text":"A man is arrested by the police after multiple FIRs were registered against him for rubbing his genitals against women inappropriately in public transport. On questioning, the man says that he gets sexual gratification this way. Which of the following paraphilias describes this behavior?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Voye‌urism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Feti‌shism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Frott‌eurism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Exhibi‌tionism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Frotteurism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Frotteurism</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>frotteurism. </strong>It refers to the sexual gratification obtained by <strong>touching</strong> or rubbing against a <strong>non-consenting</strong> person. </p>\n<p>This behavior often occurs in busy, crowded places, such as on busy streets or on crowded buses or subways. It is predominantly described in men. A frotteur may fantasize that the woman he is rubbing up against is mutually aroused by the behavior.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0599","text":"A man who was caught peeki‌ng into his neighbor's in​timate moments was served‍ a restraining order. On ​evaluation, he is found t​o be a case of scopophili​a. According to DSM-5, wh‌at is the minimum age at ‌which this condition can ​be diagnosed?","raw_text":"A man who was caught peeking into his neighbor's intimate moments was served a restraining order. On evaluation, he is found to be a case of scopophilia. According to DSM-5, what is the minimum age at which this condition can be diagnosed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"14 y‌ears","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"16 y‌ears","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"18 y‌ears","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"No age‌ limit","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 18 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 18 years</strong></p><p>The given scenario is <strong>scopophilia</strong>, also known as <strong>voyeuristic disorder.</strong> The individual must be <strong>at least 18 years old</strong> for making this diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Criteria for the diagnosis of voyeuristic disorder (scopophilia) according to DSM-5:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sexual gratification from observing an individual who is <strong>undressing</strong>, <strong>naked</strong>, or <strong>engaging in sexual activity</strong></li>\n<li>Persisting for at least <strong>6-months</strong></li>\n<li>At least <strong>18 years</strong> old</li>\n<li>Significant <strong>distress</strong> or impairment in functioning</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0742","text":"A 28-year-old man is broug‌ht to the sexual wellness​ clinic by his wife. She ‍complains that he acts li​ke a woman during sexual ​intercourse. On questioni​ng, he admits that though‌ he identifies himself as‌ a male, he is sexually a​roused with the fantasies​ of being a woman. Identi‍fy the condition.","raw_text":"A 28-year-old man is brought to the sexual wellness clinic by his wife. She complains that he acts like a woman during sexual intercourse. On questioning, he admits that though he identifies himself as a male, he is sexually aroused with the fantasies of being a woman. Identify the condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transv‌estism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transse‌xualism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Andro‌philia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autogyn‌ephilia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Autogynephilia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Autogynephilia</strong></p><p>A <strong>natal male</strong> gaining sexual arousal by the <strong>fantasy</strong> <strong>of being a woman</strong> is called <strong>autogynephilia</strong>. They are also attracted to women but these individuals are often aroused at the fantasy of having sex or actually engaging in sex with men who treat them like women.</p>\n<p>Option A: Transvestism<strong> </strong>is described as fantasies and sexual urges to dress in opposite gender cloth­ing as a means of arousal and as an adjunct to masturbation or coitus.</p>\n<p>Option B: Transsexualism<strong> </strong>is a person having a strong desire to assume the physical characteristics and gender role of the opposite sex. It is an outdated term, and is referred to as gender dysphoria (DSM-5).</p>\n<p>Option C: Androphilia<strong> </strong>means sexual attraction to men or masculinism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"UFNBRjHu","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"27_hernia","title":"27.Hernia","num_questions":32,"total_marks":128,"duration":42,"time_per_question":78.75,"questions":[{"id":"MB2319","text":"Which of the following is ​a false statement regardi‍ng the inguinal canal?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement regarding the inguinal canal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conjoint tendon forms a pa​rt of the posterior wall","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superficial ring is an ope​ning in the external obli‍que aponeurosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deep ring is an opening in​ the transverse abdominis‍","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Internal oblique forms bot​h the anterior and superi‍or walls","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Deep ring is an opening in the transverse abdominis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Deep ring is an opening in the transverse abdominis</strong></p><p>The <strong>deep inguinal ring </strong>is an opening in the<strong> fascia transversalis,</strong> not in the transversus abdominis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2320","text":"All of the following are c​ontents of inguinal canal‍ except _______. Round li‍gament Ilioinguinal nerve‌ Femoral branch of genito‌femoral nerve Spermatic c‌ord Cremasteric artery","raw_text":"<p>All of the following are contents of inguinal canal except _______.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Round ligament</li>\n<li>Ilioinguinal nerve</li>\n<li>Femoral branch of genitofemoral nerve</li>\n<li>Spermatic cord</li>\n<li>Cremasteric artery</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,2,4​ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2,3 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a​nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Onl​y 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Only 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Only 3</strong></p><p>The <strong>femoral</strong> branch of the <strong>genitofemoral</strong> nerve is not a content of the inguinal canal.</p>\n<p>The contents of the<strong> inguinal canal </strong>include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Spermatic cord</strong> in males</li>\n<li><strong>Round ligament</strong> in females</li>\n<li><strong>Ilioinguinal</strong> nerve in both the sexes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The contents of the<strong> spermatic cord</strong> include:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1119\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Cremaster muscle fibers</li>\n<li>Testicular artery and accompanying veins</li>\n<li><strong>Genital</strong> branch of the <strong>genitofemoral</strong> nerve</li>\n<li>Vas deferens</li>\n<li><strong>Cremasteric vessels</strong></li>\n<li>Lymphatics</li>\n<li>Processus vaginalis</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2323","text":"Which of the following str​uctures does not form the‍ boundary of Hesselbach’s‍ triangle?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures does not form the boundary of Hesselbach’s triangle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inferior epigastric artery​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vas de​ferens","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rectus a​bdominis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inguinal​ ligament","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vas deferens","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vas deferens</strong></p><p>Vas deferens <strong>does not</strong> form the boundary of Hesselbach’s triangle.</p>\n<p>Hesselbach's triangle (also called the inguinal triangle) is a region of the deep part of the <strong>anterior abdominal wall. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2340","text":"A resident while assisting​ a case of laparoscopic h‍ernia repair observes the‍ surgeon dissect certain ‌anatomical landmarks to e‌xpose the triangle of doo‌m. Which of the following‍ does not form a boundary​ of this triangle?","raw_text":"A resident while assisting a case of laparoscopic hernia repair observes the surgeon dissect certain anatomical landmarks to expose the triangle of doom. Which of the following does not form a boundary of this triangle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gonadal​ vessels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vas de​ferens","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cooper’s​ ligament","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Peritoneal​ reflection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cooper’s ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cooper’s ligament</strong></p><p><strong>Cooper’s ligament</strong> does not form the boundary of the triangle of doom.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6680","text":"Which of the following is ​true regarding inguinal h‍ernia? Medial border of t‍he triangle of doom is bo‌und by gonadal vessels Me‌dial border of the triang‌le of doom is bound by va‍s deferens Medial border ​of myopectineal orifice i​s bound by rectus abdomin‌is Medial border of myope​ctineal orifice is bound ‍by iliopsoas","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is true regarding inguinal hernia?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Medial border of the triangle of doom is bound by gonadal vessels</li>\n<li>Medial border of the triangle of doom is bound by vas deferens</li>\n<li>Medial border of myopectineal orifice is bound by rectus abdominis</li>\n<li>Medial border of myopectineal orifice is bound by iliopsoas</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a​nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 a​nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a​nd 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2 a​nd 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b302a7d7085e417fa1e2602bffc736a4x1280x1481.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2 and 3</strong></p><p>The medial border of the triangle of doom is bounded by <strong>vas deferens </strong>and the medial border of the myopectineal orifice is bounded by<strong> </strong>the<strong> </strong>lateral border of<strong> rectus abdominis.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> triangle of doom</strong> is bounded:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Medially by the vas deferens</li>\n<li>Laterally by the vessels of the spermatic cord (gonadal vessels)</li>\n<li>Inferiorly by peritoneal reflection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> myopectineal orifice</strong> is an osseo-myo-aponeurotic tunnel. It is through this tunnel that all groin hernias occur. It is bounded:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Medially by the lateral border of the rectus sheath.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Above by the arched fibers of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis muscle.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Laterally by the iliopsoas muscle.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Below by the pectin pubis and fascia covering it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2325","text":"What is the most common ty​pe of hernia in females?","raw_text":"What is the most common type of hernia in females?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Femoral​ hernia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Direct ingu​inal hernia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Indirect ing​uinal hernia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Umbilica​l hernia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Indirect inguinal hernia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Indirect inguinal hernia</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Indirect inguinal hernias</strong> are by far the most <span class=\"s1\">common type of hernia in <strong>women</strong>. It is the most common hernia regardless of gender. </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">In <strong>men</strong>, <strong>indirect</strong> hernias predominate over direct hernias at a ratio of 2:1. <strong>Femoral</strong> hernia and <strong>umbilical</strong> hernias are more common in <strong>females</strong> compared to males at ratios of 10:1 and 2:1, respectively. Although femoral hernias occur more frequently in women than in men, inguinal hernia remains the most common hernia in women.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2322","text":"A 45-year-old woman presen​ts with a history of swel‍ling in the right groin f‍or the past 6 months. On ‌examination, an irreducib‌le hernia is present in t‌he right groin. Which of ‍the following structures ​would be helpful in ident​ifying if the hernia is i‌nguinal or femoral in ori​gin?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman presents with a history of swelling in the right groin for the past 6 months. On examination, an irreducible hernia is present in the right groin. Which of the following structures would be helpful in identifying if the hernia is inguinal or femoral in origin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pubic s​ymphysis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pubic t​ubercle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Femoral​ artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior epigastric artery​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pubic tubercle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pubic tubercle</strong></p><p><strong>Pubic tubercle</strong> is used to differentiate between inguinal and femoral hernias.</p>\n<p><strong>Inguinal hernia</strong> is <strong>above </strong>and <strong>medial</strong> to the pubic tubercle.</p>\n<p><strong>Femoral hernia</strong> is <strong>below </strong>and <strong>lateral</strong> to the pubic tubercle.</p>\n<p>Another structure that is useful in differentiating between inguinal and femoral hernia is inguinal ligament. An <strong>inguinal </strong>hernia leaves the abdominal cavity just <strong>above</strong> the inguinal ligament and a <strong>femora</strong>l hernia leaves just <strong>below</strong> the inguinal ligament.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2328","text":"An elderly male patient is​ diagnosed with direct in‍guinal hernia. Which of t‍he following statements i‌s true?","raw_text":"An elderly male patient is diagnosed with direct inguinal hernia. Which of the following statements is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The sac lies medial to the​ inferior epigastric arte‍ry","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cough impulse is felt on t​he index finger","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Can descend till the botto​m of scrotum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The neck is narrow and is ​prone for strangulation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The sac lies medial to the inferior epigastric artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The sac lies medial to the inferior epigastric artery</strong></p><p>The sac and contents of a direct hernia protrude through <strong>Hesselbach’s triangle</strong>, i.e., <strong>medial</strong> to the inferior epigastric artery.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2332","text":"Bubonocele is a type of __​_______.","raw_text":"Bubonocele is a type of _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Indirect ing​uinal hernia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Direct ingu​inal hernia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Femoral​ hernia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Umbilica​l hernia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Indirect inguinal hernia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Indirect inguinal hernia</strong></p><p>Bubonocele is a type of<strong> indirect inguinal hernia</strong> where the hernia is limited to the inguinal canal.</p>\n<p><strong>Indirect inguinal hernia</strong> is more common on the <strong>right side</strong> because of the delay in atrophy of the processus vaginalis after the normal slower descent of the right testis to the scrotum during fetal development.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2347","text":"What is the most common se​rious complication expect‍ed to occur in patients w‍ith hernia?","raw_text":"What is the most common serious complication expected to occur in patients with hernia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incarc​eration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Strang​ulation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fistula ​formation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Obstr​uction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Strangulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Strangulation</strong></p><p><strong>Strangulation</strong> is the most common serious complication of a hernia. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1045\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>An <strong>incarcerated hernia</strong> is a part of the intestine or abdominal tissue that becomes trapped in the sac of a hernia.An incarcerated hernia can lead to a <strong>bowel obstruction or strangulation</strong>. <strong>Obstruction</strong> is characterized by colicky abdominal pain, vomiting, abdominal distension and obstipation. Features of obstruction along with <strong>vascular compromise</strong> is seen in <strong>strangluation.</strong> <span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Strangulation occurs in </span><strong style=\"font-size: 14px;\">1% to 3% </strong><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">of groin hernias. It is more common at the </span><strong style=\"font-size: 14px;\">extremes of life.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Most strangulated hernias are <strong>indirect inguinal hernias. </strong>However, <strong>femoral hernias</strong> have the <strong>highest rate of strangulation </strong>(15% to 20%) of all hernias, and it is, therefore, recommended that all femoral hernias be repaired at the time of discovery. <strong>Tenderness </strong>is the first sign of strangulation.</p>\n<p>The first step is the correction of hypovolemia. The treatment of choice is <strong>immediate surgery. </strong>The fundus is opened first during surgery.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1436","text":"A patient presents with a ​hernia medial to the infe‍rior epigastric artery. W‍hat is the most likely di‌agnosis and appropriate m‌anagement for the same?","raw_text":"A patient presents with a hernia medial to the inferior epigastric artery. What is the most likely diagnosis and appropriate management for the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Direct hernia and Bassini ​repair","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Direct hernia and Lichtens​tein mesh repair","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Indirect hernia and Bassin​i repair","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Indirect hernia and Lichte​nstein mesh repair","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Direct hernia and Lichtenstein mesh repair","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0aeac8d0744e4138bc41f46999891d7cx1280x1584.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Direct hernia and Lichtenstein mesh repair</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a hernia <strong>medial</strong> to the<strong> inferior epigastric artery</strong> is suggestive of a <strong>direct hernia</strong>, for which<strong> Lichtenstein mesh repair</strong> is the appropriate surgical method for its management.</p>\n<p>The contents of a <strong>direct hernia</strong> protrude<strong> outward</strong>,<strong> forward,</strong> and <strong>medial</strong> to the <strong>inferior epigastric artery </strong>and the <strong>deep </strong><strong>inguinal ring</strong>.<strong> </strong>Whereas, the contents of an<strong> indirect hernia</strong> protrude <strong>lateral</strong> to the <strong>inferior epigastric artery</strong>. The sac of an indirect inguinal hernia passes from the deep inguinal ring obliquely towards the superficial inguinal ring and then to the scrotum.</p>\n<p>A <strong>direct hernia </strong>protrudes through the <strong>Hesselbach triangle, </strong>which is a zone of weakness for direct hernias.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>A <strong>Lichtenstein mesh repair</strong> is currently one of the most frequently used methods for treating an<strong> inguinal hernia</strong>. It is done for the <strong>reconstruction</strong> of the <strong>inguinal floor</strong> in <strong>adults</strong>. It is an <strong>anterior tension-free mesh repair</strong> that reduces the chances of <strong>recurrence</strong> of a hernia. A piece of prosthetic nonabsorbable <strong>mesh is used</strong> to bridge the defect in this repair.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other surgical methods used for the repair of an inguinal hernia are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bassini repair</strong> - It is done for the <strong>reconstruction</strong> of the <strong>inguinal floor</strong> in <strong>adults</strong>. In this method, a <strong>mesh is not</strong> used. It is associated with a <strong>high</strong> <strong>recurrence</strong> and is not used frequently now. It is used only in cases of hernial strangulation and peritonitis where the use of a mesh is not preferred. It is performed by <strong>suturing</strong> the transversus abdominis and internal oblique musculoaponeurotic arches/<strong>conjoined tendon</strong> to the<strong> inguinal ligament</strong>. </li>\n<li><strong>Shouldice repair</strong> - It is a <strong>multilayered repair,</strong> where the posterior wall of the inguinal canal is strengthened by <strong>continuous sutures.</strong> It involves <strong>double-breasting</strong> of the<strong> transversalis fascia</strong>. It is associated with a <strong>very low recurrence rate.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Gilbert repair</strong> (plug and patch repair) - It is an <strong>extension</strong> of <strong>Lichtenstein’s original mesh repair</strong> and has now become the <strong>most commonly</strong> performed <strong>primary anterior inguinal hernia repair</strong>. In this technique, a <strong>cone-shaped plug</strong> of<strong> polypropylene mesh</strong> is used. It is deployed like an upside-down umbrella when inserted into the internal inguinal ring to occlude the hernia</li>\n<li><strong>McVay repair </strong>(Cooper's ligament repair)<strong> </strong>is used for the correction of direct inguinal hernia, large indirect hernia,<strong> recurrent hernia</strong>, femoral hernias (particularly suited for <strong>strangulated femoral hernias</strong>). In this method, the transversus abdominis aponeurosis is approximated to Cooper's ligament.</li>\n<li><strong>Stoppa-Rives repair</strong> - It is used for the repair of large, recurrent, or bilateral hernias. In this method, a sub-umbilical <strong>midline incision </strong>is given to place a<strong> large mesh</strong> into the <strong>preperitoneal space.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Herniotomy</strong> - It is used to treat <strong>inguinal hernia</strong> in a<strong> child </strong>which occurs due to the<strong> presence</strong> of a <strong>patent processus vaginalis</strong>. Therefore in herniotomy, the repair of the inguinal ring is not done.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2338","text":"A 50-year-old obese male p​atient presented with bil‍ateral, large inguinal he‍rnias. He had been operat‌ed on for hernia twice in‌ the past and is now post‌ed for Stoppa's repair. W‍hich of the following sta​tements regarding this pr​ocedure is false?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old obese male patient presented with bilateral, large inguinal hernias. He had been operated on for hernia twice in the past and is now posted for Stoppa's repair.  Which of the following statements regarding this procedure is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"This procedure refers to o​pen preperitoneal hernia ‍repair","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The mesh is placed in the ​retromuscular space","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The mesh needs to be sutur​ed to the posterior rectu‍s sheath in case of small‍ defects","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"This procedure is based on​ the principle of Pascal'‍s law","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The mesh needs to be sutured to the posterior rectus sheath in case of small defects","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c739a1b7df4544c18a51fa23eabcd5d1x1280x1373.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The mesh needs to be sutured to the posterior rectus sheath in case of small defects</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1136\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>With smaller defects, the mesh <strong>does not need to be sutured</strong> in Stoppa's repair.</p>\n<p>This is based on <strong>Pascal's law.</strong> Pascal’s law states that any additional <strong>pressure</strong> exerted on an enclosed fluid mass is <strong>transmitted equally</strong> in all directions.The abdomen is an enclosed space and intra-abdominal pressure is distributed equally over the mesh, thereby keeping the mesh in place.</p>\n<p>Stoppa's repair is an open <strong>preperitoneal</strong> mesh repair primarily indicated for large, <strong>recurrent</strong>, <strong>bilateral</strong> hernias.</p>\n<p>In Stoppa’s mesh repair, a large piece of <strong>mesh is placed in the retro muscular space,</strong> over the <strong>posterior rectus sheath</strong> or peritoneum. This mesh is <strong>not sutured </strong>in patients with small defects because it is held in place by intra-abdominal pressure (Pascal’s law). This allows the eventual incorporation of the mesh with the surrounding tissues. Alternatively, in <strong>larger</strong> defects, the mesh can be <strong>secured laterally</strong> with several sutures.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2335","text":"A 2-year-old boy was broug​ht to the clinic with a h‍istory of a reducible and‍ painless swelling in the‌ right groin for the past‌ 2 months. After the clin‌ical and radiological inv‍estigations, the diagnosi​s of a right-sided inguin​al hernia was made. The p‌atient is posted for hern​ia repair surgery. Which ‍of the following steps wi‌ll not be done in this pa‍tient during the repair?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old boy was brought to the clinic with a history of a reducible and painless swelling in the right groin for the past 2 months. After the clinical and radiological investigations, the diagnosis of a right-sided inguinal hernia was made. The patient is posted for hernia repair surgery. Which of the following steps will not be done in this patient during the repair?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opening of the hernial sac​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Excision of the hernial sa​c","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transfixatio​n of the neck","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Repair of the inguinal rin​g","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Repair of the inguinal ring","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Repair of the inguinal ring</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Inguinal hernia in a child is treated by <strong>herniotomy</strong>. In this procedure,<strong> repair of the inguinal ring is not done</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">This is because the inguinal hernia in the <strong>pediatric</strong> population is due to the presence of<strong> patent processus vaginalis</strong> and not due to the weakening of the abdominal muscles or the ligaments.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Herniotomy involves either <strong>reduction</strong> of the hernial sac alone, or <strong>opening</strong> of the hernial sac, <strong>reduction</strong> of the contents,<strong> transfixation of the neck</strong>, and <strong>excision</strong> of the patent processus vaginalis/ hernial sac.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD4395","text":"Which of the following is ​the most appropriate mesh‍ for use in hernioplasty?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most appropriate mesh for use in hernioplasty?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heavyweight with small por​es","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heavyweight mesh with larg​e pores","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lightweight mesh with smal​l pores","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lightweight mesh with larg​e pores","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lightweight mesh with large pores","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lightweight mesh with large pores</strong></p><p>The most appropriate mesh used in hernioplasty is a <strong>l</strong><strong>ightweight mesh with large pores.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Lightweight meshes</strong> generally contain less material and induce a less-pronounced foreign body reaction and <strong>decreased inflammatory response</strong>, which results in better tissue incorporation, increased prosthesis compliance, and decreased patient discomfort and pain.</p>\n<p>Meshes with <strong>large pores</strong> exhibit<strong> less inflammatory infiltrate</strong>, connective tissue, and scar bridging, which allows <strong>increased soft tissue ingrowth.</strong> They have <strong>greater elasticity</strong> and flexibility and also <strong>shrink less </strong>resulting in <strong>lesser adhesions</strong>, better tissue incorporation, and decreased patient discomfort and pain. So the best mesh type among the given options is lightweight mesh with large pores.</p>\n<p>Meshes used in hernioplasty can be synthetic or biological depending on their composition.</p>\n<p><strong>Synthetic meshes</strong> are made from polypropylene, polyester, or PTFE (polytetrafluoroethylene). </p>\n<table style=\"width: 375.898px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\"><strong>POLYPROPYLENE</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 80px;\"><strong>POLYESTER</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.8984px;\"><strong>PTFE</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">monofilament</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80px;\">multifilament</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.8984px;\">solid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">hydrophobic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80px;\">hydrophilic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.8984px;\">hydrophobic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">rigid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 80px;\">elastic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.8984px;\">rigid</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>PTFE</strong> meshes are hydrophobic with a <strong>lower risk</strong> of <strong>adhesion.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Biological meshes</strong> are acellular matrix materials derived from humans or animals and have <strong>lesser complications</strong>. But due to poor collagen deposition and absorption, they rapidly <strong>lose mechanical strength</strong> and hence their <strong>use</strong> is restricted to <strong>infected environments</strong>. </p>\n<p>Note: To promote better tissue integration, a composite mesh was developed which combines the attributes of polypropylene and PTFE by layering the two substances on top of one another.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2321","text":"A patient operated for dir​ect inguinal hernia devel‍oped sensory loss at the ‍root of the penis and adj‌acent part of the scrotum‌. Which of the following ‌nerves is most likely inj‍ured?","raw_text":"A patient operated for direct inguinal hernia developed sensory loss at the root of the penis and adjacent part of the scrotum. Which of the following nerves is most likely injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ilioingui​nal nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Iliohypoga​stric nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Genital branch of genitofe​moral nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Femoral branch of genitofe​moral nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ilioinguinal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/849b2737a0904b7e9e75c6ddc57d37f7x1280x1662.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ilioinguinal nerve</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Loss of sensation</strong> in the <strong>root of the penis and adjacent part of the scrotum</strong> is suggestive of an injury to the <strong>ilioinguinal nerve.</strong> It is most commonly injured within the inguinal canal in <strong>open hernia repair</strong> due to traction or thermal injury.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The <strong>iliohypogastric nerve</strong> carries the sensory supply from the <strong>suprapubic area.</strong> Injury to this nerve in the right iliac fossa rarely causes sensory disturbances, as the suprapubic area has a rich nerve supply from other sources too. If the <strong>nerve is severed</strong> above the <strong>anterior superior iliac spine,</strong> it can lead to the <strong>weakened posterior wall</strong> of the inguinal canal resulting in <strong>direct inguinal hernia</strong>. Iliohypogastric nerve can also get injured following <strong>appendicectomy</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Injury to the<strong> genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve </strong>results in loss of sensation in the skin of the <strong>scrotum</strong> of males, and loss of sensation of the <strong>mons pubis and labia majora</strong> in females. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Injury to the<strong> femoral branch of the genitofemoral nerve</strong> causes <strong>hypoesthesia</strong> over the<strong> anterior thigh</strong> below the inguinal ligament.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2327","text":"A 50-year-old male patient​ with chronic constipatio‍n is diagnosed with Type ‍7 Gilbert hernia. This re‌fers to ______","raw_text":"A 50-year-old male patient with chronic constipation is diagnosed with Type 7 Gilbert hernia. This refers to ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Direct ingu​inal hernia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Indirect ing​uinal hernia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Femoral​ hernia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Umbilica​l hernia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Femoral hernia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Femoral hernia</strong></p><p><strong>Femoral hernia</strong> is classified under<strong> type 7 Gilbert hernia.</strong></p>\n<p>As per <strong>Nyhus classification system</strong>, femoral hernia is classified under <strong>type 3C.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2354","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about femoral her‍nia is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about femoral hernia is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It occurs exclusively in f​emales","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"All femoral hernias should​ be repaired","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It does not​ strangulate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Femoral vein lies medial t​o the sac","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. All femoral hernias should be repaired","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. All femoral hernias should be repaired</strong></p><p>All <strong>femoral hernias</strong> should be <strong>repaired </strong>and incarcerated femoral hernias should have the hernia sac contents examined for viability.</p>\n<p>Option A: Femoral hernia occurs in both males and females, but it is <strong>more common in multiparous females. </strong><strong>Femoral hernia</strong> is more common on the<strong> right side</strong> because of the tamponading effect of the sigmoid colon on the left femoral canal. This effect is absent on the right side.</p>\n<p>Option C: The <strong>incidence of strangulation</strong> in femoral hernias is <strong>high</strong> because of the <strong>narrowness of the neck of the sac </strong>and<strong> r</strong><strong>igidity of the femoral ring.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Femoral vein</strong> is situated <strong>lateral</strong> to a femoral hernia<strong> (femoral canal).</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2357","text":"Match the following surger​ies for femoral hernia wi‍th their indications: Sur‍gery Indication 1. Lockwo‌od's surgery A. Presence ‌of bowel infarction 2. Mc‌Evedy's surgery B. Electi‍ve repair 3. Lotheissen's​ surgery C. For both femo​ral and inguinal hernia 4‌. McVay's surgery D. Tran​s-inguinal approach","raw_text":"<p>Match the following surgeries for femoral hernia with their indications:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 219px; width: 522.6875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 221px; text-align: center; height: 25px;\"><strong>Surgery</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.6875px; text-align: center; height: 25px;\"><strong>Indication</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 221px; height: 37px;\">1. Lockwood's surgery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.6875px; height: 37px;\">A. Presence of bowel infarction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 43px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 221px; height: 43px;\">2. McEvedy's surgery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.6875px; height: 43px;\">B. Elective repair</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 45px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 221px; height: 45px;\">3. Lotheissen's surgery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.6875px; height: 45px;\">C. For both femoral and inguinal hernia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 221px; height: 39px;\">4. McVay's surgery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.6875px; height: 39px;\">D. Trans-inguinal approach</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-A, 2-B,​ 3-C, 4-D","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-C, 2-D,​ 3-B, 4-A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-B, 2-C,​ 3-A, 4-D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-B, 2-A,​ 3-D, 4-C","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3d90f2b98d9845bb87fecbcee73f2c4ax1280x1188.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C</strong></p><p> The correct option is <strong>1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 219px; width: 522.6875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 221px; text-align: center; height: 25px;\"><strong>Surgery</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.6875px; text-align: center; height: 25px;\"><strong>Indication</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 47px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 221px; height: 47px;\">1. <strong>Lockwood's</strong> surgery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.6875px; height: 47px;\">B. <strong>Elective</strong> repair</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 49px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 221px; height: 49px;\">2. <strong>McEvedy's</strong> surgery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.6875px; height: 49px;\">A. Presence of bowel <strong>infarction</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 45px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 221px; height: 45px;\">3. <strong>Lotheissen's</strong> surgery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.6875px; height: 45px;\">D. <strong>Trans-inguinal</strong> approach</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 221px; height: 39px;\">4. <strong>McVay's</strong> surgery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.6875px; height: 39px;\">C. For both <strong>femoral</strong> and <strong>inguinal</strong> hernia</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2358","text":"In Laugier’s hernia, the o​pening is in the _____","raw_text":"In Laugier’s hernia, the opening is in the _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lacunar ​ligament","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Conjoin​t tendon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"External​ oblique","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perit​oneum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lacunar ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0bd9b3630b754a7681d9b59ae249c29cx1280x1594.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lacunar ligament</strong></p><p><strong>Laugier’s hernia</strong> is an atypical femoral hernia where the sac herniates through a gap in the <strong>lacunar (Gimbernat’s) ligament.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2359","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about Spigelian h‍ernia is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about Spigelian hernia is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It occurs above the level ​of the arcuate line","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The hernia sac is posterio​r to the external oblique‍ aponeurosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most commonly presents as ​a painless mass","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is detect​ed by USG/CT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Most commonly presents as a painless mass","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/674d1cbf32c1415d88c79347816f2ccbx1280x1298.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Most commonly presents as a painless mass</strong></p><p>Usually, Spigelian hernias do not present as a mass because it is beneath the intact external oblique aponeurosis.</p>\n<p>The most common presentation in Spigelian hernia is <strong>pain.</strong></p>\n<p>There is a common <strong>misconception</strong> that they protrude below the arcuate line owing to the deficiency of the posterior rectus sheath at that level; but <strong>in fact,</strong> the defect is almost <strong>always above the arcuate line.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2361","text":"While performing an electi​ve hernia repair, the sur‍geon notices that the pos‍terior wall of the hernia‌ sac is formed by the sig‌moid colon. What is this ‌type of hernia known as?","raw_text":"While performing an elective hernia repair, the surgeon notices that the posterior wall of the hernia sac is formed by the sigmoid colon. What is this type of hernia known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lumbar​ hernia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sliding​ hernia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Spigelia​n hernia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pantaloo​n hernia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sliding hernia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sliding hernia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is that of a <strong>sliding hernia.</strong></p>\n<p>Sliding hernia occurs when an <strong>internal organ </strong>composes a<strong> portion of the wall of the hernia sac. </strong></p>\n<p>The most common viscus involved are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Left side: <strong>sigmoid colon</strong> and its mesentery</li>\n<li>Right side: <strong>cecum</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is more common on the<strong> left </strong>side<strong>. </strong>Most sliding hernias are <strong>variants of indirect inguinal hernias. </strong>The primary danger associated with a sliding hernia is the failure to recognize the visceral component of the hernia sac resulting in injury to the bowel or bladder.</p>\n<p>Option A: A <strong>lumbar hernia</strong> is a <strong>posterolateral body wall</strong> hernia where the bowel, omentum, or pre-peritoneal fat herniates through the <strong>lumbar triangles.</strong> </p>\n<p>Option C: A <strong>Spigelian hernia</strong> occurs through slit-like defects in the <strong>anterior abdominal wall</strong> adjacent to the semilunar line. Most spigelian hernias occur in the lower abdomen where the posterior sheath is deficient. It is also called “spontaneous lateral ventral hernia” or “hernia of the semilunar line”.</p>\n<p>Option D: A <strong>pantaloon hernia</strong> occurs when both a <strong>direct and an indirect hernia</strong> develop on the <strong>same side</strong> of the <strong>groin</strong>. The hernia is named pantaloon because the two hernia sacs are divided by epigastric vessels, and so they look like a pair of pants.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2362","text":"A middle-aged male patient​ underwent adrenalectomy ‍for Cushing's syndrome on‍e year back. He now prese‌nts with a history of chr‌onic lower back pain. On ‌examination, a partially ‍reducible mass that incre​ased in size during Valsa​lva maneuver was noticed ‌on his lower back. Which ​of the following statemen‍ts regarding the patient'‌s condition is false?","raw_text":"A middle-aged male patient underwent adrenalectomy for Cushing's syndrome one year back. He now presents with a history of chronic lower back pain. On examination, a partially reducible mass that increased in size during Valsalva maneuver was noticed on his lower back. Which of the following statements regarding the patient's condition is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herniation may have occure​d through the Grynfelt’s ‍triangle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The inferior lumbar triang​le is bound by the extern‍al oblique and latismus d‍orsi muscle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Herniation is relatively m​ore common on the right s‍ide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dowd Ponka technique is us​ed for surgical repair","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Herniation is relatively more common on the right side","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Herniation is relatively more common on the right side</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of an iatrogenic <strong>lumbar hernia</strong> which developed due to weakening of the posterior abdominal wall due to surgery. It occurs more commonly on the <strong>left</strong> side.</p>\n<p>A lumbar hernia is a posterolateral body wall hernia where the bowel, omentum, or pre-peritoneal fat herniates through the <strong>lumbar triangles.</strong> Causes can be congenital, acquired, traumatic, or iatrogenic. It is frequently confused for lipomas, muscle strains, fibromas, abscesses, and kidney tumors.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2368","text":"Which type of hernia is de​picted in the following d‍iagram?","raw_text":"Which type of hernia is depicted in the following diagram?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Richter’​s hernia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Littre’​s hernia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peterse​n hernia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maydl ​hernia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Maydl hernia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/781509e53e8644519f4e4bc5a320520e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Maydl hernia</strong></p><p>The diagram shows <strong>Maydl’s hernia/Hernia-en-W/retrograde hernia</strong>, where 2 loops of the bowel remain in the hernia sac with the connecting loop inside the abdomen.</p>\n<p>Option A: In <strong>Richter's hernia</strong>, a small portion of the <strong>antimesenteric wall of the intestine is trapped within the hernia. </strong>Strangulation can occur without the pres­ence of intestinal obstruction. Clinically, it <strong>mimics gastroenteritis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: In <strong>Littre’s hernia</strong>,<strong> Meckel’s diverticulum </strong>is the content. <strong>Amyand’s hernia</strong> is an inguinal hernia in which the hernia sac contains a normal or acutely inflamed <strong>appendix.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: <span class=\"ILfuVd\"><span class=\"hgKElc\"><strong>Petersen hernia</strong> is caused by the herniation of <strong>intestinal loops</strong> through the <strong>defect</strong> between the <strong>small bowel limbs,</strong> the <strong>transverse mesocolon, </strong>and the <strong>retroperitoneum</strong>, after any type of <strong>gastrojejunostomy.</strong></span></span></p>\n<div id=\"WEB_ANSWERS_STANDARD_RESULT_13_pE1iYvfQIYyaseMPl7OQ6Ac__12\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2374","text":"A 25-year-old fit athlete ​presents to the OPD with ‍complaints of nausea and ‍heartburn. On examination‌, a soft, pea-sized swell‌ing could be palpated on ‌his abdomen. What is the ‍likely diagnosis in this ​patient?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old fit athlete presents to the OPD with complaints of nausea and heartburn. On examination, a soft, pea-sized swelling could be palpated on his abdomen. What is the likely diagnosis in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Umbilica​l hernia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fatty hernia of linea alba​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Incision​al hernia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paraduode​nal hernia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fatty hernia of linea alba","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fatty hernia of linea alba</strong></p><p>The likely diagnosis in this patient is <strong>epigastric hernia</strong>, also known as <strong>fatty hernia of linea alba.</strong></p>\n<p>Epigastric hernia is located between the xiphoid process and umbilicus, usually in the <strong>midline. </strong>It occurs more commonly in <strong>males. </strong>A <strong>soft</strong> midline <strong>swelling</strong> can be felt more easily than seen. Pain occurs due to the<strong> incarceration of the preperitoneal fat </strong>and<strong> </strong>may <strong>simulate peptic ulcer. </strong>There is no overlying sac.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2377","text":"An 83-year-old lady was br​ought to the OPD with a t‍wo-day history of nausea,‍ thigh pain and obstipati‌on. Her family reported a‌ significant weight loss ‌over the last year attrib‍uted to grieving over her​ son's death. On examinat​ion, there is no lump in ‌her groin. But, she exper​iences pain along the ant‍eromedial aspect of her t‌high which is relieved by‍ thigh flexion. Which of ​the following statements ​regarding her condition i​s true?","raw_text":"An 83-year-old lady was brought to the OPD with a two-day history of nausea, thigh pain and obstipation. Her family reported a significant weight loss over the last year attributed to grieving over her son's death. On examination, there is no lump in her groin. But, she experiences pain along the anteromedial aspect of her thigh which is relieved by thigh flexion. Which of the following statements regarding her condition is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Femoral hernia is the most​ likely diagnosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Howship Romberg sign can b​e elicited","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transinguinal approach is ​preferred for surgery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"These hernias are almost a​lways interparietal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Howship Romberg sign can be elicited","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/40172e7e96ac4f4c9c4f6a97d94715dax1280x1473.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Howship Romberg sign can be elicited</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and examination findings are suggestive of <strong>obturator hernia</strong>. <strong>Howship-Romberg sign</strong> can be seen in patients with obturator hernia.</p>\n<p><strong>Howship-Romberg sign</strong> refers to <strong>pain in the anteromedial aspect of the thigh</strong> due to <strong>compression of the obturator nerve</strong> that is <strong>relieved by thigh flexion.</strong></p>\n<p>Obturator hernia is also known as <strong>skinny old lady hernia </strong>and<strong> French hernia. </strong>It occurs more commonly in<strong> thin, elderly, debilitated females.</strong></p>\n<p>Predisposing factors are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Women with wider pelvis and more triangular obturator canal</li>\n<li>Malnutrition</li>\n<li>Chronic constipation</li>\n<li>Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)</li>\n<li>Multiparity</li>\n<li>Ascites</li>\n<li>Kyphoscoliosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Intestinal obstruction can occur.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> </strong>Femoral hernias commonly present as a <strong>small lump in the groin</strong>. They may be asymptomatic or present with strangulation in about 30% of patients.</p>\n<p>Option C: A <strong>posterior</strong> approach, open or laparoscopic surgery is the treatment of choice. This approach provides direct access to the hernia.</p>\n<p>Option D: <span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Interparietal hernias are rare and occur when the sac lies <strong>between layers</strong> of the abdominal wall. These hernias most frequently occur in previous <strong>incisions. Spigelian</strong> hernias are almost always interparietal.</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2400","text":"Hernia with hydrocele is k​nown as ________.","raw_text":"Hernia with hydrocele is known as ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gibbon’​s hernia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Berger’​s hernia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Beclard’​s hernia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ogilvie’​s hernia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gibbon’s hernia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gibbon’s hernia</strong></p><p><strong>Gibbon’s hernia</strong> is a <strong>hernia with hydrocele.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Berger’s hernia</strong> is hernia into the <strong>pouch of Douglas.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Beclard’s hernia</strong> is <strong>femoral hernia </strong>through the opening of the <strong>saphenous vein</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Ogilvie’s hernia</strong> is hernia through a <strong>defect in conjoint tendon</strong> just lateral to where it inserts with the rectus sheath.</p>\n<p>Edward Gibbon, the author of \"The Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire,\" was greatly embarrassed by a large hydrocele. The second time that it was tapped, it became infected, and Gibbon died a few days later. The hydrocele was associated with a large scrotal hernia which had probably been punctured.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6751","text":"According to the European ​Hernia Society classifica‍tion, M3 incisional herni‍a refers to:","raw_text":"According to the European Hernia Society classification, M3 incisional hernia refers to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epiga​stric","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infraum​bilical","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Umbi​lical","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Supra​pubic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Umbilical","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Umbilical</strong></p><p>According to the European Hernia Society classification, M3 incisional hernia refers to <strong>umbilical hernia.</strong></p>\n<p>According to the European Hernia Society classification of incisional hernias:</p>\n<p><strong>Midline</strong> incisional hernias are classified as-</p>\n<table style=\"height: 267px; width: 283px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 55px;\">M1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Subxiphoidal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 55px;\">M2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Epigastric</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 55px;\"><strong>M3</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\"><strong>Umbilical</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 55px;\">M4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Infraumbilical</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 55px;\">M5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Suprapubic</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Lateral</strong> incisional hernias are classified as-</p>\n<table style=\"height: 144px; width: 282px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">L1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\">\n<p>Subcostal</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">L2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\">\n<p>Flank</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">L3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\">\n<p>Iliac</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">L4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\">\n<p>Lumbar</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2385","text":"A young female patient who​ had been suffering from ‍chronic abdominal pain is‍ rushed to the casualty d‌ue to acute exacerbation.‌ On further investigation‌s, she is diagnosed with ‍intestinal obstruction se​condary to a paraduodenal​ hernia. Which of the fol‌lowing statements regardi​ng the hernia is false?","raw_text":"A young female patient who had been suffering from chronic abdominal pain is rushed to the casualty due to acute exacerbation. On further investigations, she is diagnosed with intestinal obstruction secondary to a paraduodenal hernia. Which  of the following statements regarding the hernia is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left-sided hernia is found​ in the fossa of Landzert‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right sided hernia is foun​d in the fossa of Kolb","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is usuall​y congenital","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is more common on the r​ight side","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is more common on the right side","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2a2e39b948b64244876d807a99cb057fx1280x1840.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/751e07cc16314198bc3f7499be08c915x1280x1840.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is more common on the right side</strong></p><p>Paraduodenal hernia is more common on the <strong>left side (75%).</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: In <strong>Left-sided paraduodenal hernia</strong>, bowel herniates through the <strong>fossa of Landzert. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: In <strong>right-sided paraduodenal hernia</strong>, bowel herniates through the <strong>fossa of Waldeyer/Kolb</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Both the fossa of Landzert and the fossa of Waldeyer/Kolb are formed due to congenital mesenteric fusion defects.</p>\n<p>Option C: Paraduodenal hernia is the <strong>most common internal hernia </strong>and they are usually<strong> congenital.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2399","text":"An infant who is diagnosed​ with congenital hypothyr‍oidism presents with the ‍following hernia. Which o‌f the following statement‌s is false regarding the ‌management of this child?‍","raw_text":"An infant who is diagnosed with congenital hypothyroidism presents with the following hernia. Which of the following statements is false regarding the management of this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The hernia may spontaneous​ly resolve by 2 years of ‍age","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Surgical repair is indicat​ed if the hernia persists‍ for more than 5 years of‍ age","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Strangulation is a common ​complication associated w‍ith this hernia in this c‍hild","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mayo’s procedure or mesh r​epair can be done for thi‍s hernia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Strangulation is a common complication associated with this hernia in this child","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4b441f9d04e542c9bbfb4f54e3767bcb.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Strangulation is a common complication associated with this hernia in this child</strong></p><p>The given clinical history of <strong>congenital hypothyroidism</strong> and the image showing a mass protruding out of the umbilicus are indicative of an <strong>umbilical hernia. Obstruction</strong> and <strong>strangulation</strong> are <strong>uncommon</strong> below the age of 3 years.</p>\n<p><strong>Mayo’s operation</strong> is done for <strong>umbilical hernia. </strong>A small curved incision is made below the umbilicus and the neck of the <strong>sac</strong> is <strong>opened.</strong> The contents are returned to the peritoneal cavity and the redundant sac is <strong>excised</strong> after closing it. Defect in the linea alba is closed with <strong>interrupted</strong> sutures. Indications for surgery in umbilical hernia are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Defect size <strong>&gt;2cm</strong></li>\n<li>Persisting <strong>beyond 5 years</strong></li>\n<li>Progressively enlarging after 2 years of age (usually regresses spontaneously by 2 years of age)</li>\n<li>Symptomatic</li>\n<li>Strangulated</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In adults, d<span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">efects <strong>up to 2cm</strong> in diameter may be<strong> sutured primarily</strong> with minimal tension. However, with larger defects, <strong>mesh reinforcement</strong> will be beneficial.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7053","text":"A baby was born with a con​genital defect as shown b‍elow. Which of the follow‍ing statements is false r‌egarding this condition?","raw_text":"A baby was born with a congenital defect as shown below. Which of the following statements is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is associated with tris​omy 13","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is due to failure of ph​ysiological hernia to red‍uce before 7 weeks","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Seen in t​erm babies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Topical application of sil​ver nitrate is done","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is due to failure of physiological hernia to reduce before 7 weeks","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/95188e5d5391493291300eafcd2c9eda.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6cf0500143e44d98802a4e06111d8fe6x1279x1242.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7987f64c98ee49e690d74e836400a0ccx1279x1242.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is due to failure of physiological hernia to reduce before 7 weeks</strong></p><p>The congenital defect showing herniation of the intestinal contents enclosed in a sac from the umbilicus is suggestive of <strong>omphalocele. </strong>It is due to<strong> </strong>the<strong> failure </strong>of the<strong> physiological hernia</strong> to <strong>reduce </strong>into the abdominal cavity <strong>before 10 weeks </strong>resulting in <strong>non-closure </strong>of the <strong>umbilical ring. </strong></p>\n<p>Physiological <strong>herniation</strong> of the <strong>midgut</strong> through the umbilicus occurs<strong> during the 5<sup>th</sup> week</strong> so as to accommodate the rapidly growing intestine and <strong>persists </strong>until the<strong> 10<sup>th</sup> week.</strong></p>\n<p>Omphalocele is seen in term babies and is usually associated with other congenital anomalies (e.g., <strong>trisomy 13,</strong> trisomy 18, or <strong>Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome</strong>) and with increased levels of α-fetoprotein.</p>\n<p>An omphalocele may contain the stomach, liver, and intestines. These contents are <strong>enclosed in a sac. </strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1912\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The treatment is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Preservation </strong>of the intact sac with a sterile, moistened, saline gauze or transparent bowel bag.</li>\n<li><strong>IV fluids</strong> should be started, along with <strong>gastric tube decompression</strong> and <strong>IV antibiotics. </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Topical </strong>application of <strong>povidone-iodine</strong> ointment, merbromin (mercurochrome), <strong>or</strong> <strong>silver nitrate </strong>allows the sac to thicken and epithelialize gradually.</li>\n<li><strong>Primary surgical closure</strong> is preferred for small to medium-sized defects. </li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5349","text":"A 3-month-old child presen​ted with umbilical discha‍rge. Abdomen examination ‍was normal except for the‌ below finding. The next ‌step of management is?","raw_text":"A 3-month-old child presented with umbilical discharge. Abdomen examination was normal except for the below finding. The next step of management is?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MRI A​bdomen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"USG A​bdomen","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"CE​CT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Exploratory​ laparotomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. USG Abdomen","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c92c8383a16546ec8dfb533bd3ac105b.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e226746e0f384863bd935402f9bf32b4x1280x2786.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/36545182350748b3878f9d1aec2a5206x482x382.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. USG Abdomen</strong></p><p>The given clinical image is that of a <strong>congenital umbilical anomaly</strong> such as patent urachus, urachal cyst, and vitelline cyst (resulting from the persistence of omphalomesenteric duct). The next best step is the <strong>USG abdomen</strong> to diagnose if the anomaly is cystic, fibrous or fistula and it's connections. </p>\n<p>Omphalomesenteric duct abnormalities: Vitello intestinal duct or omphalomesenteric duct is the <strong>connection between the yolk sac and the midgut</strong> <strong>during embryonic life</strong>, which eventually gets obliterated. The <strong>persistence of the vitellointestinal duct</strong> leads to various abnormalities depending on which part of the duct remains patent.</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Persistence of the <strong>proximal intestinal end</strong> of the duct leads to <strong>Meckel’s diverticula</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Persistence of the <strong>distal end</strong> near the umbilicus leads to the <strong>umbilical polyp or sinus.</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Persistence of the <strong>distal end with mucus retention</strong> leads to the <strong>cyst</strong>, which may be associated with the intestine or the umbilicus by a fibrous band.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A fully patent vitellointestinal duct can lead to an <strong>enterocutaneous fistula</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Patent urachus occurs due to the failure of urachus to obliterate, resulting in a connection between the bladder and the umbilicus.</p>\n<p>Urachal cyst occurs when the urachus fails to obliterate but there is no connection between the bladder and the umbilicus.</p>\n<p>The given image below shows a urachal cyst (UC) attached to Meckel’s diverticulum (MD) on one side and urinary bladder (UB) on the other side with a patent tract- urachus (U).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6749","text":"Which of the following is ​the most commonly seen tu‍mor in the retroperitonea‍l space?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most commonly seen tumor in the retroperitoneal space?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lipos​arcoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rhabdomy​osarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Synovial​ sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neurof​ibroma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Liposarcoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Liposarcoma</strong></p><p><strong>Liposarcoma</strong> is the most commonly seen tumor in <strong>retroperitoneal space.</strong></p>\n<p>Retroperitoneal tumors can be benign or malignant. Malignant retroperitoneal tumors may be <span class=\"s1\">mesodermal </span><span class=\"s2\">(75%), </span>neuroectodermal (24%), or embryonic remnant (1%) in origin. Rarely it can be urologic or metastatic also. The <strong>order of frequency of primary retroperitoneal malignancy</strong> is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Liposarcoma</li>\n<li>Leiomyosarcoma</li>\n<li>Rhabdomyosarcoma</li>\n<li>Fibrosarcoma</li>\n<li>Malignant nerve sheath tumors</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Liposarcoma </strong>is composed of three histologic varieties (listed in order of decreasing frequency):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Well-differentiated</strong> and undifferentiated liposarcoma</li>\n<li><strong>Pleomorphic</strong> liposarcoma</li>\n<li><strong>Myxoid/round cell liposarcoma</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">Among the three subtypes, <strong>pleomorphic</strong> liposarcoma is the <strong>most malignant</strong> and often metastasizes, while the well-differentiated is the least malignant. Myxoid liposarcoma has intermediate malignant behavior.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Diagnosis is confirmed by contrast-enhanced CT with CT of the chest to look for metastases. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Surgery is the main modality of treatment. Wide excision with complete en bloc dissection is done. Post-operative radiotherapy and chemotherapy are used as adjuvant methods. Radiotherapy is given at a dose of 5000 cGY, it can be external beam or intra-operative radiotherapy. Chemotherapeutic agents used are <strong>mesna</strong>, <strong>adriamycin</strong>, <strong>ifosfamide</strong> and <strong>dacarbazine</strong> (MAID regimen).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"alzUOEDB","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"28_osteology__scalp_and_face","title":"28.Osteology, Scalp And Face","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MB4276","text":"How many pairs of synovial​ articulations are associ​ated with the exterior of‍ the skull?","raw_text":"How many pairs of synovial articulations are associated with the exterior of the skull?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2</strong></p><p>There are <strong>2 pairs</strong> of <strong>synovial articulations</strong> that are associated with the exterior of the skull. They are <strong>temporomandibular</strong> joint and lateral joints<strong> </strong>(part of <strong>atlantooccipital</strong> joints).</p>\n<p>Joints of the cranial vault:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Fibrous</strong> joints/ Sutures - Facilitate the growth of the skull. (majority of the skull joints fall in this category)</li>\n<li><strong>Primary cartilaginous</strong> joints - Present between the bones in the skull base </li>\n<li><strong>Synovial</strong> joints - temporomandibular joints and atlantooccipital joints.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4279","text":"Which skull bone is known ​as Inca?","raw_text":"Which skull bone is known as Inca?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pariet​al bone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tempor​al bone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interpari​etal bone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Occipit​al bone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Interparietal bone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c3201934e66b4f2fb5f0dd76a3edd990x1280x1167.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Interparietal bone</strong></p><p>The<strong> interparietal bone</strong> of the skull is known as <strong>Inca.</strong> It is a sutural bone that lies between the <strong>occipital</strong> and the <strong>parietal</strong> <strong>bones</strong>. It is also called Goethe's ossicle. </p>\n<p>Sutural or wormian bones are islands of bone that are commonly found within the <strong>lambdoid</strong> suture in some individuals. They may arise from separate centers of ossification and they appear to have no clinical significance.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9754","text":"While counselling the pare​nts of a baby with anteri​or plagiocephaly, you exp‍lain that one of the skul‌l sutures has prematurely​ fused. The affected sutu‍re is present between:","raw_text":"While counselling the parents of a baby with anterior plagiocephaly, you explain that one of the skull sutures has prematurely fused. The affected suture is present between:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Both fron​tal bones","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The frontal and parietal b​ones","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The occipital and parietal​ bones","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both pari​etal bones","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The frontal and parietal bones","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ba8e4f4abb064f7aa52941d3f3c42ba8x600x723.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4871532659934c9bb48bdc5c22572e8ex1280x2288.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The frontal and parietal bones</strong></p><p>In <strong>anterior plagiocephaly,</strong> the premature fusion of the <strong>coronal</strong> <strong>sutures</strong> is seen. The coronal suture is present between the <strong>frontal</strong> and<strong> parietal bones.</strong></p>\n<p>Major sutures of the skull are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lambdoid</strong> suture - between parietal and occipital bones.</li>\n<li><strong>Sagittal</strong> suture - between the two parietal bones.</li>\n<li><strong>Metopic</strong> suture - between the two frontal bones.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Craniosynostosis</strong> consists of <strong>premature fusion</strong> of one or more cranial sutures, resulting in an abnormal head shape. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0965","text":"Which of the following str​uctures passes through th​e marked foramen?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures passes through the marked foramen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Accessory meningeal artery​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lesser pet​rosal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maxilla​ry nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Middle meni​ngeal artery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Middle meningeal artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/00e59694fefa400b86e1cc6f8d887f9c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Middle meningeal artery</strong></p><p>The foramen marked is the <strong>foramen spinosum. </strong>The structures passing through it are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Middle meningeal artery </strong></li>\n<li>Nervus spinosus</li>\n<li>Middle meningeal vein</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0913","text":"A 40-year-old man presents​ with bruxism. The nerve ​supplying the muscles inv‍olved in this condition p‌asses through:","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presents with bruxism. The nerve supplying the muscles involved in this condition passes through:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b2710d25fce64a8484742cf8863eb393.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1</strong></p><p><strong>Bruxism</strong> or <strong>teeth grinding</strong> mainly involves the temporalis, lateral pterygoids, and the masseter (<strong>muscles</strong> of <strong>mastication</strong>). These are supplied by the <strong>mandibular nerve. </strong>It passes through the <strong>foramen ovale </strong>(marked A).  </p>\n<p>Other foramina marked are: </p>\n<p>2. Foramen spinosum</p>\n<p>3. Foramen lacerum</p>\n<p>4. Jugular foramen</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6649","text":"Identify the foramen marke​d in the image below.","raw_text":"Identify the foramen marked in the image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Foramen ​rotundum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Forame​n ovale","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vidian​ canal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Foramen ​spinosum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Foramen rotundum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9a57cc0f680f4618b858655553497390.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bb48e90c579f43689f7f3503c102b43fx1280x1383.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Foramen rotundum</strong></p><p>The image shows the <strong>sphenoid</strong> bone. The foramen marked is the <strong>foramen</strong> <strong>rotundum</strong>. Maxillary branch of trigeminal nerve passes through it.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0966","text":"A patient presenting with ​right-sided shoulder droo​p and an absent gag refle‍x was diagnosed with Vern‌et's syndrome. Which of t​he following structures p‍asses through the posteri‌or part of the involved f‌oramen?","raw_text":"A patient presenting with right-sided shoulder droop and an absent gag reflex was diagnosed with Vernet's syndrome. Which of the following structures passes through the posterior part of the involved foramen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spinal acce​ssory nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sigmoi​d sinus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior pe​trosal sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vagus​ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sigmoid sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a213a7d7d0a4407b855b6198f3877d63x1280x1040.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/de14a302344b487caa3adc65131ba348x600x787.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sigmoid sinus</strong></p><p>Vernet's syndrome or <strong>jugular foramen syndrome</strong> refers to the palsies of<strong> IX, X,</strong> and<strong> XI</strong> cranial nerves traversing the jugular foramen. The<strong> sigmoid sinus</strong> passes through the <strong>posterior</strong> aspect of the<strong> jugular foramen</strong>.</p>\n<p>Jugular foramen can be divided into <strong>three</strong> compartments:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior - <strong>Inferior petrosal sinus</strong> </li>\n<li>Intermediate - CN IX, X, XI</li>\n<li>Posterior - Sigmoid sinus continuing as the <strong>internal jugular vein</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The arteries passing through the foramen are the meningeal branch of ascending pharyngeal artery and the meningeal branch of the occipital artery. Emissary veins may also traverse it.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0151","text":"Which of the following lig​aments passes through the​ foramen magnum?","raw_text":"Which of the following ligaments passes through the foramen magnum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transverse ligament of atl​as","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Membrana​ tectoria","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior longitudinal liga​ment","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ligament​um flavum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Membrana tectoria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5c2a7a17871148f6b55c78532134afd0x600x744.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Membrana tectoria</strong></p><p>The<strong> membrana tectoria</strong> of <strong>atlanto-axial</strong> joint passes through the <strong>foramen magnum. </strong></p>\n<p>Certain structures pass through two compartments of the foramen magnum - the <strong>osseo-ligamentous (anterior)</strong> and <strong>neuro-vascular (posterior)</strong><strong> compartments</strong> - as shown in the image below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0555","text":"A dentist performing a wis​dom tooth extraction inje​cts lidocaine at the stru‍cture marked X below. Whi‌ch of the following nerve​s is blocked here?","raw_text":"A dentist performing a wisdom tooth extraction injects lidocaine at the structure marked X below. Which of the following nerves is blocked here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lingua​l nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mandibul​ar nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chorda tym​pani nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior al​veolar nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Inferior alveolar nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cd7882390f5b4377842e8fc517a477ba.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/46748e95cf0448a7a3bb08a0b47c2c07x1280x991.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2a9c8c09fcef49e7b3fb9769c32d2917x1280x935.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Inferior alveolar nerve</strong></p><p>The marked structure is <strong>mandibular foramen</strong> and the <strong>inferior alveolar nerve</strong> passes through it. The inferior alveolar nerve is blocked by injecting a local anaesthetic at the mandibular foramen. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Given below is an image showing the administration of <strong>inferior alveolar nerve block </strong>before it enters the mandibular foramen.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7076","text":"Identify the cranial nerve​ that passes through the ​marked foramen marked in ‍the image below.","raw_text":"Identify the cranial nerve that passes through the marked foramen marked in the image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oculomot​or nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Olfacto​ry nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Optic​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Facial​ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Oculomotor nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d4f7a09ad20f4e64a3d8cdbc38de8509.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Oculomotor nerve</strong></p><p>The marked structure is the <strong>superior orbital fissure</strong> and the <strong>oculomotor nerve</strong> passes through this fissure.</p>\n<p>Superior orbital fissure is divided into three compartments by the <strong>common tendinous ring</strong>. The oculomotor nerve passes through its <strong>i</strong><strong>ntermediate compartment</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9131","text":"Identify the structure pas​sing through the foramen ​marked.","raw_text":"Identify the structure passing through the foramen marked.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abducen​s nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Spinal acce​ssory nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ophthalm​ic artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Middle meni​ngeal artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Abducens nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2c46783e8f6946c28787b328366d789d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Abducens nerve</strong></p><p>The marked foramen is the <strong>superior orbital fissure </strong>and the<strong> a</strong><strong>bducens nerve</strong> passes through it.</p>\n<p>The abducens nerve is the <strong>6th cranial nerve</strong> and arises from the brainstem at the <strong>pontomedullary</strong> junction. It runs through the <strong>Dorello's canal</strong> which is present at the medial most end of the petrous ridge and then enters the cavernous sinus where it lies lateral to the internal carotid artery. It enters the orbit through the <strong>superior orbital fissure</strong> within the common tendinous ring and supplies the <strong>lateral rectus</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p><strong>Spinal accessory nerve </strong>passes through the <strong>foramen magnum </strong>along with the <strong>medulla oblongata </strong>and the <strong>verterbral arteries</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Ophthalmic artery </strong>passes through the <strong>optic canal </strong>along with the <strong>optic nerve.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Middle meningeal artery </strong>and vein pass through the <strong>foramen spinosum </strong>along with the <strong>meningeal branch of mandibular nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4315","text":"A 24-year-old man presents​ to the ER after receivin​g a punch to the right si‍de of his face. His X-ray‌ is shown below. Which of​ the following bones maxi‍mally contributes to the ‌region fractured?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old man presents to the ER after receiving a punch to the right side of his face. His X-ray is shown below. Which of the following bones maximally contributes to the region fractured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Max​illa","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Spheno​id bone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Palati​ne bone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zygomat​ic bone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Maxilla","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/14371344308f48ae96b59ee81cc8f452.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cfff375ad512484e8076911ef48c0f95x600x568.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Maxilla</strong></p><p>The X-ray shows a <strong>fracture</strong> of the <strong>floor</strong> of the <strong>orbit</strong>. The maximum contribution to the floor of the orbit is by the <strong>maxilla</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Boundaries</strong> of the bony orbit:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Roof: </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Orbital plate of <strong>frontal</strong> bone </li>\n<li>Sphenoid bone (small part)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Floor: </strong>Thin plate of bone that also forms the roof of the maxillary sinus\n<ul>\n<li>Orbital plate of <strong>maxilla</strong></li>\n<li>Zygomatic bone (anterolateral)</li>\n<li>Orbital process of palatine bone (posteromedial)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Medial</strong> wall:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Maxilla</strong></li>\n<li>Lacrimal bone</li>\n<li>Ethmoid bone</li>\n<li>Sphenoid bone</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Lateral</strong> wall: \n<ul>\n<li><strong>Zygomatic</strong> bone (anterior)</li>\n<li>Greater wing of <strong>sphenoid</strong> (posterior)</li>\n<li>Sphenozygomatic suture.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>The orbital floor can be fractured by trauma to the orbit as seen in falls, motor vehicle accidents, and assaults. The image given shows X-ray Water's view with a 'teardrop' sign. The orbital contents leak out of the fractured orbital floor, giving it a teardrop-like appearance. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4278","text":"A detective is brought to ​the casualty with an uppe​r eyelid laceration follo‍wing a knife fight. The p‌lastic surgeon palpates f​or a landmark to administ‍er lignocaine before sutu‌ring. Where is the local ‌anesthetic injection give​n?","raw_text":"A detective is brought to the casualty with an upper eyelid laceration following a knife fight. The plastic surgeon palpates for a landmark to administer lignocaine before suturing. Where is the local anesthetic injection given?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"​D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. C","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/08789dc853cf43ce936e58915f68700c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1753a0f53b6e474fa75d77b26d55bf58x600x729.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c99b52ac2ef04ba58c6a745467493e2ex1200x1302.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. C</strong></p><p>Upper eyelid lacerations are sutured under <strong>supraorbital nerve block.</strong> The anesthesia is achieved by approaching the nerve at the supraorbital foramen/notch. The <strong>supraorbital notch</strong> is present at the junction of <strong>lateral two-third</strong> and<strong> medial one-third</strong> of the supraorbital margin (point C). It transmits the <strong>supraorbital nerve </strong>and vessels.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6655","text":"Which of the following bon​y structures is present b​ehind the marked area?","raw_text":"Which of the following bony structures is present behind the marked area?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Post ​clivus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lesser wing​ of sphenoid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sella ​turcica","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Caroti​d canal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lesser wing of sphenoid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/580fe909289a493082bef33865ce97a0.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ed16748633245758ccb4569bf6a2e8fx1280x1525.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8657a3708f3142c68a804d196cb8f4b9x600x666.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lesser wing of sphenoid</strong></p><p>The structure marked in the image is <strong>pterion,</strong> and the bony structure that is present behind it is the <strong>lesser wing of the sphenoid.</strong></p>\n<p>The pterion is an <strong>H-shaped sutural junction</strong> between four bones of the skull:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Frontal bone</li>\n<li>Squamous part of the temporal bone</li>\n<li>Greater wing of the sphenoid bone</li>\n<li>Parietal bone</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>The pterion overlies the <strong>anterior</strong> branch of the <strong>middle meningeal artery</strong> and <strong>lateral fissure </strong>of the cerebral hemisphere. The bone in this area is thin and any injury to this area may lead to rupture of the middle meningeal artery leading to <strong>extradural hematoma.</strong></p>\n<p>The pterion also corresponds to the site of the anterolateral (sphenoidal) fontanelle of the neonatal skull, which closes in the third month after birth.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9740","text":"A cricket player was rushe​d to the emergency depart​ment, unconscious after b‍eing hit in the head with‌ the ball. NCCT head reve​aled an extradural hemato‍ma at the pterion. Which ‌of the following vessels ‌is likely to be injured?","raw_text":"A cricket player was rushed to the emergency department, unconscious after being hit in the head with the ball. NCCT head revealed an extradural hematoma at the pterion. Which of the following vessels is likely to be injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superficial temporal arter​y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maxillar​y artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Middle meni​ngeal artery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Deep tempo​ral artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Middle meningeal artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ed16748633245758ccb4569bf6a2e8fx1280x1525.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Middle meningeal artery</strong></p><p>An extradural hematoma at the <strong>pterion</strong> is a result of an injury of the anterior division of the <strong>middle meningeal artery. </strong></p>\n<p>The pterion is an <strong>H-shaped sutural junction</strong> between four bones of the skull:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Frontal bone</li>\n<li>Squamous part of the temporal bone</li>\n<li>Greater wing of sphenoid bone</li>\n<li>Parietal bone.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>It also overlies the <strong>lateral fissure</strong> of the <strong>cerebral hemisphere, </strong>the hematoma can cause compressive lesions here.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1034","text":"Which of the following fon​tanelles is most likely t​o be open in an 18-month ‍old infant?","raw_text":"Which of the following fontanelles is most likely to be open in an 18-month old infant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ante​rior","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Post​erior","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Occi​pital","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mas​toid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anterior","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7a328df73afb4b69b91ef0c76b4ddc8dx600x666.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anterior</strong></p><p>The<strong> anterior fontanelle </strong>is most likely to be open as it is the last fontanelle to close. It closes at around <strong>18-24 months</strong> of age.</p>\n<p>Fontanelles of the skull:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 264px; width: 572px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169.531px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Fontanelle </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 209.547px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Location</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.922px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Closes at </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169.531px; text-align: left;\">Anterior</td>\n<td style=\"width: 209.547px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Between frontal and parietal bones</p>\n<p>Junction marked by bregma</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.922px; text-align: left;\">18-24 months </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169.531px; text-align: left;\">Posterior</td>\n<td style=\"width: 209.547px; text-align: left;\">Sagittal and lambdoid sutures</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.922px; text-align: left;\">2-3 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169.531px; text-align: left;\">Sphenoid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 209.547px; text-align: left;\">Pterion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.922px; text-align: left;\">2-3 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169.531px; text-align: left;\">Mastoid/Posterolateral</td>\n<td style=\"width: 209.547px; text-align: left;\">Intersection of temporal, parietal, and occipital bones.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.922px; text-align: left;\">6-18 months (average 12 months)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0967","text":"While resecting an acousti​c neuroma via the middle ​cranial fossa approach, t‍he surgeon drills over th‌e arcuate eminence of the​ petrous temporal bone. W‍hat is the inferior relat‌ion of the structure bein‌g drilled?","raw_text":"While resecting an acoustic neuroma via the middle cranial fossa approach, the surgeon drills over the arcuate eminence of the petrous temporal bone. What is the inferior relation of the structure being drilled?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lesser pet​rosal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Greater pet​rosal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior semicircular cana​l","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior semicircular can​al","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Superior semicircular canal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f62accf288c0405f92c369672f58e268x600x517.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7b9f38639b2e4e348d9b91f1e21f7fa0x1280x1373.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Superior semicircular canal</strong></p><p>The <strong>superior semicircular canal</strong> is present beneath the <strong>arcuate eminence</strong> of the <strong>petrous temporal</strong> bone. The arcuate eminence is used as a neurosurgical landmark for the superior semicircular canal in surgeries done by the middle cranial fossa approach. </p>\n<p>The <strong>temporal bone</strong> consists of the following <strong>parts:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Squamous</li>\n<li>Mastoid</li>\n<li>Petrous</li>\n<li>Tympanic</li>\n<li>Styloid process</li>\n<li>Zygomatic process</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>The petrous temporal bone is a <strong>pyramid-shaped</strong> structure. Its relations are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inferior to arcuate eminence - superior semicircular canal .</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior</strong> surface - internal auditory meatus (<strong>IAM</strong>) and vestibular aqueduct</li>\n<li>Inferior surface - <strong>carotid canal</strong> and the <strong>jugular fossa </strong>(jugular notch)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4320","text":"A man was hit on his face ​during a fight and suffer​ed the following fracture‍. Which of the following ‌bones is likely to be fra​ctured?","raw_text":"A man was hit on his face during a fight and suffered the following fracture. Which of the following bones is likely to be fractured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fronta​l bone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Max​illa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spheno​id bone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tempor​al bone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Temporal bone","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/217bc854ecee45b0a0d33ca773b3e21b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b4dff2015c34492ba833552d4d0240bfx1280x1525.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Temporal bone</strong></p><p>The X-ray shows a fracture of the <strong>left zygomatic arch.</strong> The zygomatic process of the <strong>temporal bone</strong> contributes to the formation of the zygomatic arch.</p>\n<p>Zygomatic arch is made up of the <strong>temporal process </strong>of the<strong> zygomatic bone </strong>and the <strong>zygomatic process </strong>of the<strong> temporal bone. </strong>They<strong> </strong>articulate at the <strong>zygomaticotemporal suture.</strong></p>\n<p>Masseter originates from the lower border of the zygomatic arch.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4326","text":"Which of the following ner​ves provides sensory inne​rvation to the scalp?","raw_text":"Which of the following nerves provides sensory innervation to the scalp?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infratroch​lear nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lesser occi​pital nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior division of great​er auricular nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Facial​ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lesser occipital nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e23aa71de1ef4c6992c52fafb9fd2ef4x600x683.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lesser occipital nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>lesser occipital nerve</strong> provides <strong>sensory</strong> innervation to the <strong>scalp.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior</strong> to ear and vertex: Branches of the <strong>trigeminal</strong> nerve branches and <strong>facial</strong> nerve.</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior</strong> to the ear and vertex: <strong>Spinal nerves</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sensory innervation </strong>of the<strong> anterior </strong>and<strong> posterior scalp </strong>is shown in the image below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>temporal </strong>and <strong>posterior auricular branches </strong>of the <strong>facial nerve </strong>constitute the <strong>motor supply</strong> to the <strong>anterior </strong>and <strong>posterior scalp</strong>, respectively.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4324","text":"A 2 week-old neonate devel​oped meningitis following​ a scalp abscess. What is‍ the likely route of spre‌ad?","raw_text":"A 2 week-old neonate developed meningitis following a scalp abscess. What is the likely route of spread?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superfici​al fascia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Apone​urosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subaponeur​otic tissue","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Peric​ranium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Subaponeurotic tissue","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f776806809b54dc2ae3288a68c77042bx1280x1524.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Subaponeurotic tissue</strong></p><p>The <strong>infection</strong> has likely <strong>spread</strong> from the <strong>sub-aponeurotic tissue. </strong></p>\n<p>The sub-aponeurotic <strong>loose areolar tissue</strong> of the scalp is referred to as the <strong>dangerous area </strong>of the scalp. <strong>Infection</strong> from this layer can pass <strong>into</strong> the <strong>cranial cavity</strong> through small <strong>emissary veins</strong>, which pass through <strong>parietal foramina</strong> in the calvaria. It can reach intracranial structures causing meningitis and intracranial abscesses. </p>\n<p>The scalp is a soft tissue that covers the calvaria of the skull. It consists of <strong>5 layers</strong> as shown in the image below (mnemonic - SCALP):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0968","text":"A 41-year-old man was brou​ght to the emergency with​ a profusely bleeding sca‍lp laceration following a‌ road traffic accident. W​hat is the nature and lay‍er of origin of the bleed‌?","raw_text":"A 41-year-old man was brought to the emergency with a profusely bleeding scalp laceration following a road traffic accident. What is the nature and layer of origin of the bleed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Venous bleed, Loose areola​r tissue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Venous bleed, Dense connec​tive tissue","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arterial bleed, Dense conn​ective tissue","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Arterial bleed, Loose areo​lar tissue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Arterial bleed, Dense connective tissue","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7cf15be4ab744325a40470f1008f51e7x1280x1524.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Arterial bleed, Dense connective tissue</strong></p><p><strong>Scalp bleeding</strong> is predominantly<strong> arterial</strong>,<strong> </strong>from the <strong>dense connective tissue</strong> of the scalp.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Profuse bleeding from scalp injuries occurs due to: </p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Low venous pressure</strong> in the erect posture</li>\n<li>The dense connective tissue holds the <strong>arteries open</strong> and does <strong>not</strong> allow them to <strong>retract</strong> causing profuse bleeding.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The scalp has an extremely rich blood supply from the <strong>external carotid arteries.</strong> Profuse bleeding from a scalp laceration is usually <strong>fatal</strong> unless it's quickly controlled by <strong>sutures.</strong> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4327","text":"Which of the following mus​cles is unlikely to be in​volved in a patient with ‍Bell's palsy?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles is unlikely to be involved in a patient with Bell's palsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orbicula​ris oris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fron​talis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Orbicula​ris oculi","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Levator palpebrae superior​is","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Levator palpebrae superioris","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5b858e839ecc44448099ec3ca542b951x600x721.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Levator palpebrae superioris</strong></p><p>The <strong>motor</strong> component of the <strong>levator palpebrae superiors </strong>is innervated by the <strong>oculomotor </strong>(III cranial) nerve and the <strong>smooth muscle</strong> component is innervated by the <strong>sympathetic nerves</strong>. It is uninvolved in Bell's palsy.</p>\n<p>All the other facial muscles are innervated by the facial nerve and thus will be paralyzed in Bell's palsy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4349","text":"Match the following muscle​s of facial expression wi​th their actions. Muscles‍ of facial expression Act‌ions 1. Orbicularis oculi​ A. Frowning 2. Risorius ‍B. Wrinkles chin 3. Orbic‌ularis oris C. Closing ey‌elids 4. Mentalis D. Clos​es mouth 5. Corrugator su‌percilli E. Retracts angl‌e of mouth","raw_text":"<p>Match the following muscles of facial expression with their actions.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 138px; width: 458.125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 230px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Muscles of facial expression</strong></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 224.125px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Actions</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 20px;\">1.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\">Orbicularis oculi </td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">A.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.125px;\">Frowning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 20px;\">2.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\">Risorius</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">B.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.125px;\">Wrinkles chin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 20px;\">3.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\">Orbicularis oris</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">C.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.125px;\">Closing eyelids</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 20px;\">4.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\">Mentalis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">D.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.125px;\">Closes mouth </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 20px;\">5.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\">Corrugator supercilli</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">E. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.125px;\">Retracts angle of mouth</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-A ; 2-E ; 3-D ; 4-B ; 5-​C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-C ; 2-D ; 3-E ; 4-A ; 5-​B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-C ; 2-E ; 3-D ; 4-B ; 5-​A","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1-A ; 2-D ; 3-C ; 4-E ; 5-​B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1-C ; 2-E ; 3-D ; 4-B ; 5-A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/88c088909d124bdd9ac9e930fd15d1e7x600x721.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1-C ; 2-E ; 3-D ; 4-B ; 5-A</strong></p><p>The following are some muscles of facial expression and their actions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Orbicularis oculi</strong> - Closing eyelids</li>\n<li><strong>Risorius</strong> - Retracts angle of the mouth, grinning muscle</li>\n<li><strong>Orbicularis oris</strong> - Closes mouth </li>\n<li><strong>Mentalis</strong> - wrinkles chin</li>\n<li><strong>Corrugator supercilli</strong> - frowning</li>\n<li>Zygomaticus major - smiling and laughing</li>\n<li>Depressor anguli oris - depresses angle of mouth</li>\n<li>Levator labii superioris - elevates upper lip</li>\n<li>Depressor labii inferioris - depresses lower lip</li>\n<li>Buccinator - known as whistling muscle, it flattens the cheeks against the gums and teeth</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Modiolus is a fibromuscular structure formed by the interlacing of 5 muscles. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4351","text":"Which of the following mus​cles plays a role in wink​ing?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles plays a role in winking?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Levator palpebrae superior​is","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corrugator​ supercilii","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Orbicula​ris oculi","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fron​talis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Orbicularis oculi","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f45415602a2c40c4914d9b733f8cfd38x600x721.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Orbicularis oculi</strong></p><p>The <strong>orbicularis oculi</strong> muscle plays a role in winking.</p>\n<p>The orbicularis oculi are circumorbital muscles that are broad, flat and elliptical. They surround the circumference of the orbit and spread into the adjascent regions. It has orbital, palpebral and lacrimal parts. It plays an important role in modulating facial expression and various ocular reflexes. It mainly <strong>closes</strong> the<strong> eyelids.</strong></p>\n<p>It is <strong>supplied</strong> by branches of the facial, superficial temporal, maxillary and ophthalmic arteries. It is <strong>innervated</strong> by the temporal and zygomatic branches of the facial nerve. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0957","text":"A patient presents with a ​loss of sensation over th​e marked area. Which nerv‍e may be damaged?","raw_text":"A patient presents with a loss of sensation over the marked area. Which nerve may be damaged?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Auriculotem​poral nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maxilla​ry nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Great auri​cular nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Facial​ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Great auricular nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/27a7e52eb6b2418aadadcb4017184afd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/51cb43ad6cf64012b6d2faf34cac540ex600x683.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Great auricular nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>skin </strong>over the<strong> angle of the mandible</strong> is innervated by the <strong>g</strong><strong>reat auricular nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p>Most of the skin covering the face is innervated solely by branches of the trigeminal nerve except the skin over the angle of the mandible which is supplied by the great auricular nerve from the C2 cervical plexus and the ear supplied by the vagus nerve, the facial nerve, and the cervical plexus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4353","text":"While testing the cranial ​nerves in a stroke patien​t, you ask her to try to ‍whistle. Which of the fol‌lowing structures doesn't​ pierce the muscle respon‍sible for this action?","raw_text":"While testing the cranial nerves in a stroke patient, you ask her to try to whistle. Which of the following structures doesn't pierce the muscle responsible for this action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paroti​d duct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Molar glands​ of the cheek","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Buccal branch of the facia​l nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Buccal branch of the mandi​bular nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Buccal branch of the facial nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Buccal branch of the facial nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>buccinator</strong> muscle is involved in flattening the cheeks against the gums and teeth. Hence, it is known as the <strong>whistling muscle</strong>. The <strong>buccal branch</strong> of the <strong>facial nerve innervates</strong> the <strong>buccinator</strong> muscle but does not pierce it.</p>\n<p>The structures piercing the buccinator muscle are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Parotid duct</li>\n<li>Buccal branch of the mandibular nerve</li>\n<li>Molar mucous glands which lie on the buccopharyngeal fascia around the parotid duct</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0102","text":"Which of the following ner​ves is related to Dorello​'s canal?","raw_text":"Which of the following nerves is related to Dorello's canal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trochle​ar nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oculomot​or nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abducen​s nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trigemin​al nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Abducens nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/209294beaa9a48a08307cb10f7a513bex600x545.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Abducens nerve</strong></p><p><strong>Abducens nerve</strong> is related to <strong>Dorello's canal.</strong> The abducens nerve enters the cavernous sinus by passing within this canal.</p>\n<p>Dorello's canal is an <strong>interdural space</strong> limited superiorly by the petrosphenoidal ligament, laterally by the petrous apex, and medially by the dorsum sellae. This is a confluence of superior petrosal, inferior petrosal, cavernous sinus, and basilar plexus venous sinuses.</p>\n<p>Dorello's canal anatomy was noted to be clinically significant in understanding sixth nerve palsy. The clinical implications turned toward its relevance to skull base surgery, mostly for petroclival lesions.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"EukMKP4l","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"28_molecular_genetics__recombinant_dna___genomic_technology","title":"28.Molecular Genetics, Recombinant Dna & Genomic Technology","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MB2789","text":"Enzymes that cut DNA at sp‍ecific DNA sequences with​in the molecule are _____​","raw_text":"Enzymes that cut DNA at specific DNA sequences within the molecule are _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Helicas‍e enzyme","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polymera‍se enzyme","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Restricti‍on enzyme","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gyrase‍ enzyme","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Restriction enzyme","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Restriction enzyme</strong></p><p>Enzymes that cut DNA at specific DNA sequences within the molecule are called <strong>restriction enzymes.</strong></p>\n<p>These defensive enzymes protect the host bacterial DNA from the DNA genome of foreign organisms (primarily infective phages) by specifically inactivating the invading phage DNA by digestion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2791","text":"Restriction enzymes are fo‍und with ______","raw_text":"Restriction enzymes are found with ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Site-specific DNA methylas‍es","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"RNA-inducible interferon s‍ystem","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thermostable DNA polymeras‍es","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Terminal t‍ransferase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Site-specific DNA methylases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Site-specific DNA methylases</strong></p><p><strong>Restriction enzymes</strong> are found with <strong>site-specific DNA methylases.</strong></p>\n<p>Restriction endonucleases are present in cells that also have a companion enzyme that site-specifically methylates the host DNA, thus rendering it an unsuitable substrate for digestion by that particular restriction enzyme.</p>\n<p>Thus, <strong>site-specific DNA</strong> <strong>methylases </strong>and restriction enzymes that target the same sites always exist in pairs in a bacterium.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0126","text":"Restriction endonuclease w‍ill act on which of the f​ollowing:","raw_text":"Restriction endonuclease will act on which of the following:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AAG‍AAG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"GAG‍AGG","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AAG‍CTT","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"TAC‍GAG","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. AAGCTT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. AAGCTT</strong></p><p>Restriction endonucleases can act on <strong>AAGCTT.</strong> </p>\n<p>Restriction endonucleases act and cleave at the site of a <strong>palindromic</strong> sequence. A palindromic sequence is a sequence that reads the same backward and forward. An example is listed below.</p>\n<p>5' --AAGCTT-- 3'<br/>3' --TTCGAA-- 5'</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2794","text":"Which of the following too‍ls of recombinant DNA tec​hnology is incorrectly pa​ired with its use?","raw_text":"Which of the following tools of recombinant DNA technology is incorrectly paired with its use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DNA polymerase - copies DN‍A sequences in the polyme​rase chain reaction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"DNA ligase - enzyme that c‍uts DNA, creating sticky ​ends","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Reverse transcriptase - pr‍oduction of cDNA from mRN​A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Electrophoresis - RFLP ana‍lysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. DNA ligase - enzyme that cuts DNA, creating sticky ends","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. DNA ligase - enzyme that cuts DNA, creating sticky ends</strong></p><p>DNA <strong>ligase</strong> is<strong> not </strong>paired correctly with its use for cutting DNA and creating sticky ends. DNA ligase <strong>joins </strong>the <strong>adjacent nucleotides</strong> in a <strong>covalent</strong> linkage.</p>\n<p><strong>Restriction endonucleases</strong> cut DNA at specific sites creating sticky ends.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2803","text":"Gene cloning‍ uses _____.","raw_text":"Gene cloning uses _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plas‍mids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Noncoding nucleotide seque‍nces","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bacter‍iophage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A &amp;a‍mp; C","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A & C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A &amp; C</strong></p><p><strong>Gene cloning </strong>uses both <strong>plasmids</strong> and <strong>bacteriophages</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Hybrid</strong> <strong>DNA</strong> molecules can be <strong>constructed</strong> in <strong>cloning</strong> vectors typically using<strong> bacterial plasmids, phages</strong>, or cosmids. A <strong>clone </strong>is a large population of identical molecules, bacteria, or cells that arise from a common ancestor. Molecular cloning allows for the production of a large number of identical DNA molecules, which can then be characterized or used for other purposes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2805","text":"Which of the following clo‍ning vectors has linear D​NA?","raw_text":"Which of the following cloning vectors has linear DNA?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pha‍ges","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pla‍smid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cos‍mid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of‍ above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Phages","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Phages</strong></p><p><strong>Phages</strong> have <strong>linear </strong>DNA into which <strong>foreign DNA</strong> can be inserted at several <strong>restriction enzyme sites.</strong></p>\n<p>Amplification of DNA (cloning) is done using cloning vectors - bacterial <strong>plasmids, phages,</strong> or <strong>cosmids.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Bacterial plasmids</strong> are small, <strong>circular,</strong> duplex DNA molecules whose natural function is to confer <strong>antibiotic resistance</strong> to the host cell. <strong>Cosmid</strong> is a plasmid into which the DNA sequences from <strong>bacteriophage lambda</strong> (λ <em>cos</em> sites) have been inserted. This enables the packaging of plasmid DNA in vitro. They have a small, <strong>circular,</strong> duplex DNA. (Options B and C)</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2807","text":"Genomic library is a term ‍used to describe ______","raw_text":"Genomic library is a term used to describe ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A computerized listing of ‍known DNA sequences","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bacterial plasmids contain‍ing DNA fragments represe​nting the majority of the​ genetic information from‌ a plant or animal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A library of complementary‍ DNA copies of the popula​tion of mRNAs in a tissue​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A compilation of the amino‍ acid sequences of protei​n coding genes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bacterial plasmids containing DNA fragments representing the majority of the genetic information from a plant or animal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bacterial plasmids containing DNA fragments representing the majority of the genetic information from a plant or animal</strong></p><p>A genomic library is <strong>bacteria</strong> with <strong>plasmids</strong> containing DNA fragments representing the majority of the genetic information from a plant or animal.</p>\n<p><strong>Gene library </strong>describes the combination of restriction enzymes and various cloning vectors allows the entire genome of an organism to be individually packed into a vector. A collection of these different <strong>recombinant clones</strong> is called a library.</p>\n<p><strong>A genomic library </strong>is prepared from the total DNA of a cell line or tissue. <strong>A cDNA library</strong> comprises complementary DNA copies of the population of mRNAs in a tissue. (option C)</p>\n<p>Genomic DNA libraries are often prepared by performing partial digestion of total DNA with a restriction enzyme that cuts DNA frequently.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6235","text":"Northern blotting is used ‍for detection of which of​ the following?","raw_text":"Northern blotting is used for detection of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"D‍NA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"R‍NA","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prot‍eins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No‍ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. RNA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a4f45aa909d44abd8abf483d6fee594bx1196x998.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. RNA</strong></p><p><strong>Northern</strong> <strong>blotting</strong> is used for the detection of RNA.</p>\n<p><strong>Southern</strong> <strong>blotting</strong> is used for the detection of DNA and <strong>Western</strong> <strong>blotting</strong> is used for the detection of protein.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2810","text":"What is the correct sequen‍ce of events in Southern ​blotting? 1 - Transfer to​ a membrane 2 - Hybridiza‌tion with a labeled probe​ sequence 3 - Separation ‍of DNA fragments by elect‌rophoresis","raw_text":"<p>What is the correct sequence of events in Southern blotting?</p>\n<p>1 - Transfer to a membrane<br/>2 - Hybridization with a labeled probe sequence<br/>3 - Separation of DNA fragments by electrophoresis</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‍2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, ‍1, 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3, ‍1, 2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2, ‍3, 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3, 1, 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7a976265f58f462db822daefeb3bf6a9x800x494.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3, 1, 2</strong></p><p>In Southern blotting<strong> DNA fragments </strong>are first separated by<strong> electrophoresis.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The DNA in the gel is then made single-stranded by exposure to dilute alkali, and transferred by capillary action to the nylon membrane, which is laid over it. The relative positions of the different DNA fragments are thus preserved on the membrane.</li>\n<li>The membrane is then soaked in buffer containing the labelled probe, which base pairs with the DNA fragment of interest.</li>\n<li>The position of that fragment is then determined by autoradiographic (exposure of the membrane to X-ray film), or fluorescent detection of the hybridized probe.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2812","text":"Approximately how many pol‍ymerase chain reaction (P​CR) products are produced​ from a single template D‌NA molecule in a PCR reac​tion that continues for 3‍0 cycles?","raw_text":"Approximately how many polymerase chain reaction (PCR) products are produced from a single template DNA molecule in a PCR reaction that continues for 30 cycles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6‍4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"128‍,000","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Approximate‍ly 1 million","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Approximate‍ly 1 billion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Approximately 1 billion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Approximately 1 billion</strong></p><p>A <strong>PCR reaction</strong> that continues for <strong>30 cycles</strong> will produce <strong>approximately </strong><strong>1 billion</strong> PCR products <strong>from a single template DNA molecule.</strong></p>\n<p>Polymerase chain reaction (<strong>PCR) </strong>is a method of amplifying a target sequence of DNA. DNA sequences as short as 50 to 100 bp and as long as 10 kb can be amplified by PCR. The amplification is exponential.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>20 cycles provide amplification of 2<sup>20 </sup>= 10<sup>6</sup>  or 1 million copies</li>\n<li><strong>30 cycles </strong>provide an amplification of<strong> 2<sup>30 </sup>=10<sup>9</sup> or 1 billion copies</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2815","text":"Which of the following is ‍NOT required for a PCR re​action?","raw_text":"Which of the following is NOT required for a PCR reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A thermostable DNA polymer‍ase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dideoxy-dNT‍Ps (ddNTPs)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pri‍mers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Templa‍te DNA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dideoxy-dNTPs (ddNTPs)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dideoxy-dNTPs (ddNTPs)</strong></p><p><strong>Dideoxy-dNTPs (ddNTPs)</strong> is <strong>not required in PCR reaction.</strong></p>\n<p>In a PCR reaction, the DNA sample is first <strong>heat-denatured</strong> (&gt;90°C) to separate the two strands of the template DNA containing the target sequence. The primers, added in excess, are allowed to <strong>anneal</strong> to the DNA (typically 50–75°C); and each strand is copied by a <strong>DNA polymerase,</strong> starting at the <strong>primer</strong> sites in the presence of all four dNTPs(again in vast excess).</p>\n<p>DNA synthesis is catalyzed by a heat-stable DNA polymerase purified from one of a number of different thermophilic bacteria, organisms that grow at 70–80°C.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2817","text":"Which of the following is ‍a function of PCR?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a function of PCR?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"To detect infectious agent‍s, especially latent viru​ses","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Make prenatal genetic diag‍noses","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Detect allelic polymorphis‍ms","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A‍ll","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All</strong></p><p>All of the listed options are applications of PCR.</p>\n<p>The <strong>applications of PCR</strong> to forensic medicine are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>To detect <strong>infectious agents,</strong> especially latent viruses</li>\n<li>Make <strong>prenatal genetic</strong> diagnoses</li>\n<li>Detect <strong>allelic polymorphisms</strong></li>\n<li>Establish precise tissue types for transplants</li>\n<li>To study evolution, using DNA from archeological samples</li>\n<li>For quantitative RNA analyses after RNA copying and mRNA quantitation by the so-called RT-PCR method (cDNA copies of mRNA generated by a retroviral reverse transcriptase)</li>\n<li>To score in vivo protein-DNA occupancy using chromatin immunoprecipitation assays</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2820","text":"RT-PCR is a method that is‍ used for _____","raw_text":"RT-PCR is a method that is used for _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Forensic ana‍lysis of DNA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amplification of genomic D‍NA sequences","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amplification of mRNA sequ‍ences only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Analysis of mRNA expressio‍n and its amplification","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Analysis of mRNA expression and its amplification","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/33a13042b6fd4728823907ff940fdcddx335x424.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Analysis of mRNA expression and its amplification</strong></p><p>RT-PCR is a method that is used for analysis of <strong>mRNA expression</strong> and its <strong>amplification</strong>.</p>\n<p>Reverse transcriptase polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR) is a variant of PCR and includes 2 steps:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Generation of complementary DNA (cDNA copies) from mRNA using reverse transcriptase - used to qualitatively detect gene expression</li>\n<li>Amplification of newly synthesized cDNA by traditional PCR -This can be quantitatively measured by using fluorescent dyes (qRT-PCR).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Uses: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Determination of viral load in disease</li>\n<li>Screening of genetic diseases and cancers</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2821","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements about forensic an​alysis of DNA is correct?​","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about forensic analysis of DNA is correct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A DNA profile using short ‍tandem repeats is not uni​que to an individual","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Forensic analysis makes us‍e of SNPs in coding seque​nces to distinguish betwe​en individuals","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"PCR is used for DNA profil‍ing (DNA fingerprinting)","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"DNA fingerprinting cannot ‍be used for paternity tes​ting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. PCR is used for DNA profiling (DNA fingerprinting)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. PCR is used for DNA profiling (DNA fingerprinting)</strong></p><p><strong>PCR </strong>is used for<strong> DNA profiling </strong>(DNA fingerprinting).</p>\n<p>The <strong>PCR</strong> allows the DNA in a single cell, hair follicle, or spermatozoon to be amplified and analyzed. Thus, the applications of PCR to forensic medicine are obvious. <strong>DNA fingerprinting</strong>, or DNA profiling, is used for forensic analysis of crime scenes and paternity testing (Option D). </p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Short Tandem Repeat</strong> <strong>(STR)</strong> are short sequences of DNA at scattered locations in the genome, repeated in tandem (one after the other). E.g., CG-CG-CG-CG-CG. The number of these repeat units varies from person to person but is unique for any given individual and, therefore, serves as a molecular fingerprint.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Single Nucleotide Polymorphism(SNP)</strong> refers to variations that involve just one base at a given locus (Eg., base C replaced by base A), which can occur within exons, introns, intergenic or coding regions. They are almost always biallelic and are majorly used in the definition of inherited diseases where the functional deficit is unknown. Each of these variations is present to some appreciable degree within a population and hence not unique to an individual.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2822","text":"In which technique of gene‍ localization is a radioa​ctive probe used?","raw_text":"In which technique of gene localization is a radioactive probe used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Somatic cell hybridization‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"In situ hy‍bridization","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fluorescence in situ hybri‍dization (FISH)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‍he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. In situ hybridization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. In situ hybridization</strong></p><p><strong>In situ hybridization </strong>uses<strong> </strong>a <strong>radioactive probe</strong> for gene localization.</p>\n<p>It is added to a metaphase spread of chromosomes on a glass slide. The exact area of hybridization is localized by layering photographic emulsion over the slide and after exposure, lining up the grains with some histologic identification of the chromosome.</p>\n<p>Fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH) utilizes <strong>fluorescent probes</strong>, not radioactive probes. Fluorescent probes have greater safety, stability, and ease of detection (Option C).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2824","text":"Which of the following dis‍eases is an example of po​int mutation?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases is an example of point mutation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemoglob‍in Lepore","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"β-Thalassem‍ia type III","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diab‍etes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sickle ce‍ll disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sickle cell disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sickle cell disease</strong></p><p><strong>Sickle cell disease</strong> is caused by a point mutation. </p>\n<p>This is caused by mutation of a single base out of the 3 × 10<sup>9</sup> in the genome, a <strong>T-to-A</strong> DNA substitution, resulting in an <strong>A-to-U</strong> change in the <strong>mRNA</strong> corresponding to the <strong>sixth codon</strong> of the β-globin gene.</p>\n<p>Defective production of β-globin results in a variety of diseases and is due to many different lesions in and around the β-globin gene.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2825","text":"Restriction fragment lengt‍h polymorphism (RFLP) is ​used to ______","raw_text":"Restriction fragment length polymorphism (RFLP) is used to ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Construct high resolution ‍linkage map","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Identify single gene disea‍se","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Construct‍ QTL maps","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‍he above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All of the above</strong></p><p>Restriction fragment length polymorphism (<strong>RFLP</strong>) is used to construct a <strong>high-resolution linkage map</strong>, <strong>QTL maps</strong>, and <strong>identify single gene-disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>An<strong> inherited difference </strong>in the <strong>pattern</strong> of <strong>restriction enzyme digestion</strong> (e.g., a DNA variation occurring in more than 1% of the general population) is known as a restriction fragment length polymorphism (RFLP). RFLPs result from single-base changes (e.g., sickle-cell disease) or from deletions or insertions (CNVs) of DNA into a restriction fragment (eg, the thalassemias) and have proved to be useful diagnostic tools.</p>\n<p>SNPs(single nucleotide polymorphisms)/RFLPs can be used to establish linkage groups, which in turn, by the process of chromosome walking<strong>,</strong> will eventually define the disease locus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2826","text":"Which of the following is ‍correct regarding CRISPR-​Cas9?","raw_text":"Which of the following is correct regarding CRISPR-Cas9?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a system used by bac‍terial cells to recognise​ and destroy viral DNA as​ a form of adaptive immun‌ity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is a tool which is desi‍gned to detect TB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is tool designed to tes‍t air pollution","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a tool used in DNA s‍equencing","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is a system used by bacterial cells to recognise and destroy viral DNA as a form of adaptive immunity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is a system used by bacterial cells to recognise and destroy viral DNA as a form of adaptive immunity</strong></p><p>The <strong>CRISPR-Cas9</strong> <strong>system</strong>, found in many <strong>bacteria</strong>, represents a form of <strong>acquired, or adaptive immunity</strong> <strong>to infection by bacteriophages</strong>, which complements the system of restriction endonucleases and methylases.</p>\n<p>In recent years, a novel DNA editing/gene regulatory system termed Clustered Regularly Interspersed Short Palindromic Repeats- CRISPR-associated gene 9 (CRISPR-Cas9) has been developed. CRISPR uses RNA-based targeting to bring the Cas9 nuclease to foreign (or any complementary) DNA.</p>\n<p>Within bacteria, this CRISPR-RNA-Cas9 complex then <strong>degrades</strong> and inactivates the <strong>targeted DNA.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9488","text":"Which of the following sit‍e does the restriction en​zyme act when RFLP is use​d in-order to identify th‌e five different species ​of staphylococci in a sur‍gical ICU?","raw_text":"Which of the following site does the restriction enzyme act when RFLP is used in-order to identify the five different species of staphylococci in a surgical ICU?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ATGGAC‍-TACGTG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"GATATC-‍ CTATAG","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"TAGATA‍-ATCTCT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AATATA‍-TATAAT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. GATATC- CTATAG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. GATATC- CTATAG</strong></p><p><strong>Restriction endonuclease </strong>enzymes act at <strong>specific palindromic sequences</strong> that create DNA fragments of different lengths. By analyzing the fragments, specific species of bacteria can be identified. Option B is the only palindromic sequence here, hence it is the answer.</p>\n<p>RFLP is a restriction fragment length polymorphism (RFLP) analysis technique that involves cutting a particular region of DNA with known variability, with restriction enzymes, then separating the DNA fragments by agarose gel electrophoresis and determining the number of fragments and relative sizes. </p>\n<p><strong>Cleavage</strong> of a <strong>sample</strong> of <strong>DNA</strong> with a <strong>restriction enzyme</strong> produces a characteristic set of <strong>smaller DNA fragments</strong>. Deviations in the normal product pattern, produce RFLPs that occur if a mutation renders a restriction site unrecognizable and alternatively, generates a new recognition site. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5240","text":"Which of the following is ‍the best screening invest​igation for metabolic dis​orders?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the best screening investigation for metabolic disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wester‍n blot","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tandem mass ‍spectrometry","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"P‍CR","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gel elect‍rophoresis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tandem mass spectrometry","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tandem mass spectrometry</strong></p><p><strong>Tandem mass spectrometry</strong> (MS) is the technique of choice for <strong>screening neonates</strong> for <strong>inherited metabolic diseases.</strong></p>\n<p>Tandem MS employs the equivalent of two mass spectrometers linked in series (MS-MS or MS2). It can be used to screen blood samples from newborns for the presence and concentrations of amino acids, fatty acids, and other metabolites.</p>\n<p><strong>Abnormalities in metabolite levels</strong> indicate inherited<strong> metabolic diseases</strong>, such as phenylketonuria, ethylmalonic encephalopathy, and glutaric acidemia type 1.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5242","text":"The best method to measure‍ HbA1C is ________","raw_text":"The best method to measure HbA1C is ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isoelectri‍c focusing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Affinity ch‍romatography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ion exchange chromatograph‍y","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Electro‍phoresis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ion exchange chromatography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ion exchange chromatography</strong></p><p>The best method to measure <strong>HBA1C</strong> is<strong> ion-exchange chromatography</strong>.</p>\n<p>In ion-exchange chromatography:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Proteins interact with the <strong>stationary phase</strong> by <strong>charge-charge interactions.</strong></li>\n<li>Proteins with a net positive charge at a given pH will tightly adhere to beads with negatively charged functional groups such as carboxylates or sulfates (<strong>cation exchangers</strong>).</li>\n<li>Proteins with a net negative charge adhere to beads with positively charged functional groups, typically tertiary or quaternary amines (<strong>anion exchangers</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>Non-adherent proteins</strong> flow through the matrix and are <strong>washed away</strong>.</li>\n<li>Proteins elute in inverse order of the strength of their interactions with the stationary phase.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6789","text":"Identify the phenomenon sh‍own below.","raw_text":"Identify the phenomenon shown below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trans‍lation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transfo‍rmation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Complem‍entation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Conju‍gation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Complementation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e3cfb150996e46179163a897cd3823ca.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/53a706e417e4439889c4fec0a2189b5ex600x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Complementation</strong></p><p>The image shows a schematic representation of the <strong>blue-white assay</strong> used in the <strong>screening of recombinant vectors for inserted DNA fragments</strong>. It uses the phenomenon of <strong>complementation </strong>(αlpha-complementation of the β-galactosidase gene).</p>\n<p>Blue white assay: The DNA of interest is ligated into a vector. The vector is then inserted into a competent host cell viable for transformation, which is then grown in the presence of X-gal (X-gal detects active β-galactosidase). Cells transformed with vectors containing <strong>recombinant DNA </strong>will produce<strong> white colonies </strong>whereas cells transformed with non-recombinant plasmids (i.e. only the vector) grow into blue colonies.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the result of a blue-white assay.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1005","text":"In a patient with chronic ‍myeloid leukemia, the tra​nslocation in Philadelphi​a chromosome is best stud‌ied by SKY - spectral kar​yotyping","raw_text":"<p>In a patient with chronic myeloid leukemia, the translocation in Philadelphia chromosome is best studied by</p>\n<p>SKY - spectral karyotyping</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"G ba‍nding","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"S‍KY","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Southern‍ blotting","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"DNA seq‍uencing","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. SKY","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/946f4259ae2246a78788d68a618cd237x432x342.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. SKY</strong></p><p> Translocation in the <strong>Philadelphia chromosome</strong> (a reciprocal translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22) is best studied by <strong>SKY</strong>.</p>\n<p>SKY stands for spectral karyotyping or multicolor FISH (M Fish). This technique allows one to display all the chromosomes together and thus help identify chromosomal defects in conditions such as chronic myeloid leukemia.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: G banding is also a karyotyping technique but SKY is a better option between the two.</p>\n<p>Option C: Southern blotting is a technique used to detect a specific DNA sequence in a tissue sample. </p>\n<p>Option D: DNA sequencing cannot detect structural abnormality in a chromosome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6035","text":"Which of the following tec‍hniques is used for the d​etection of variations in​ DNA sequences and gene e‌xpression?","raw_text":"Which of the following techniques is used for the detection of variations in DNA sequences and gene expression?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Microarray‍ technique","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Northe‍rn blot","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Southe‍rn blot","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"South wes‍tern blot","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Microarray technique","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/85ab52626cc341b3ac7992df6167bffex436x258.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Microarray technique</strong></p><p><strong>DNA microarray</strong> can be used to detect DNA sequence variations.</p>\n<p>There are various microarray techniques used in clinical practice which are described below:</p>\n<p><strong>DNA Microarray</strong> technique or <strong>DNA chip:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>To the DNA chip containing <strong>known </strong>oligonucleotide sequence, flourescent labelled unknown oligonucleotide is added.</li>\n<li>The computerised algorithms can decode the unknown oligonucleotide, by detecting the location of flourescent hybridization pattern on the chip.</li>\n<li>This technique is used for genotyping or genome sequencing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>RNA microarray: </strong>RNA microarray can detect gene expression.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>To the known array of oligonucleotide, flourecently labelled cDNA prepared from unknown mRNA is added, and the unknown cDNA is decoded</li>\n<li>This technique is used for gene expression studies</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Protein microarray:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Known antibodies placed on glass slide and flourescently tagged target protein added. Target protein is detected by antigen antibody interaction.</li>\n<li>This technique is used in study of proteomics</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1298","text":"Array CGH can detect all e‍xcept? CGH - Comparative ​Genomic Hybridization","raw_text":"<p>Array CGH can detect all except?</p>\n<p>CGH - Comparative Genomic Hybridization</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mutation ‍in cancers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subtelomer‍ic deletion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Balanced tr‍anslocation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aneup‍loidy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Balanced translocation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Balanced translocation</strong></p><p>Array CGH cannot detect <strong>balanced translocation.</strong></p>\n<p>In Array-based CGH, two genomes are compared. The test DNA and the reference DNA are labelled with <strong>two different flourescent dyes</strong> (red and green respectively) and are added to a DNA chip spotted with entire human genome.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the contribution of both sample are <strong>equal</strong> for a given chromosomal region, then all spots on the array will flouresce <strong>yellow.</strong></li>\n<li>If the test sample has <strong>amplification,</strong> that spot will fluoresce <strong>red</strong></li>\n<li>If the test sample has a <strong>deletion,</strong> that spot will fluoresce <strong>green</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Uses of Array CGH:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>To detect gene amplification</li>\n<li>To detect gene deletion </li>\n<li>To detect copy number variations</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6798","text":"The correct order of steps‍ in Polymerase Chain Reac​tion (PCR) is:","raw_text":"The correct order of steps in Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hybridisation, Annealing, ‍Elongation, Denaturation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Elongation, Denaturation, ‍Hybridisation, Annealing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Denaturation, Annealing, E‍xtension","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Annealing, Extension, Dena‍turation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Denaturation, Annealing, Extension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/00259612bf874a4689f10de608ce3e3dx1280x2299.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Denaturation, Annealing, Extension</strong></p><p>The correct sequence of events for PCR among the given options is <strong>denaturation, annealing,</strong> and <strong>extension.</strong></p>\n<p>The process of PCR is as follows:</p>\n<p>The DNA sample is <strong>first heat-denatured at &gt;90°C</strong> (90-96 degrees Celsius for 3 minutes) to separate the two strands of the template DNA containing the target sequence; the primers (which <strong>attach to the 3' end</strong> of the flanking sequence) are added in excess, are allowed<strong> to anneal</strong> to the DNA (typically at 50-75°C) in order to generate the required template-primer <strong>hybridization</strong> complex. Under suitable conditions and presence of deoxynucleotides, elongation of the DNA stand occurs. </p>\n<p>The number of copies produced in the presence of an unlimited supply of deoxynucleotides and enzymes is given by - 2 to the power n (where n is the number of PCR cycles)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>20 cycles provide an amplification of 2<sup>20 </sup>= 10<sup>6</sup>  or 1 million copies</li>\n<li><strong>30 cycles</strong> provides an <strong>amplification</strong> of <strong>2<sup>30 </sup>=10<sup>9</sup></strong> or <strong>1 billion copies</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Below is an image showing the various ingredients for carrying out PCR.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2097","text":"Wobble hypothesis explains‍ which of the following c​oncepts of the genetic co​de?","raw_text":"Wobble hypothesis explains which of the following concepts of the genetic code?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Univ‍ersal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Unamb‍iguous","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Degen‍eracy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Non-pun‍ctuated","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Degeneracy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1e2ad80f76c940efad91da49b943c477x562x1198.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Degeneracy</strong></p><p>The wobble hypothesis explains the <strong>degeneracy</strong> of the genetic code.</p>\n<p>The <strong>wobble hypothesis</strong> states that codon-anticodon pairing follows the traditional<strong> Watson–Crick </strong>rules (G pairs with C and A pairs with U) for the <strong>first two bases</strong> of the codon but can be less stringent for the third/last base. It has been observed that a tRNA can recognize more than one codon for a specific amino acid, especially in the last nucleotide of the codon triplet. This is referred to as degeneracy and its mechanism can be explained by the wobble hypothesis.</p>\n<p>The first base of the anticodon moves to permit <strong>nontraditional pairing </strong>with the last base of the codon. This movement is called <strong>wobble</strong> and is possible because the first base of the anticodon is not as spatially defined as the other two bases. Due to the wobbling, 61 tRNA species are not required to read the 61 codons.</p>\n<p>For example, three codons for glycine—GGU, GGC, and GGA—can form a base pair from one anticodon, 3′ CCI 5′ ie, I, inosine, can base pair with U, C, and A, as shown in the diagram below.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: The genetic code is <strong>universal</strong> because it is the same for all living organisms.</p>\n<p>Option B: The genetic code is <strong>unambiguous</strong> because each codon is specific for only one amino acid. Example: GGA only codes for Glycine.</p>\n<p>Option D: The genetic code is <strong>non-punctuated. </strong>This means that once the reading commences at a specific start codon, it is continuous and is read continuously as triplets until a stop codon is reached without stopping.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ZITlsewE","path":["Biochemistry"]},{"id":"28_family_planning","title":"28.Family Planning","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB8179","text":"What is the number of elig​ible couples per 1000 pop​ulation in India?","raw_text":"What is the number of eligible couples per 1000 population in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50 ​– 70","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"100 ​– 120","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"150 ​– 180","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"120​-150","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 150 – 180","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 150 – 180</strong></p><p>There are approximately <strong>150 – 180 eligible couples</strong> per 1000 population in India.</p>\n<p>An <strong>eligible couple</strong> refers to a currently married couple wherein the <strong>wife </strong>belongs to the <strong>reproductive age group </strong>(15-49 years of age).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8213","text":"Scope of modern concept of​ family planning services​ include all of the follo‍wing except:","raw_text":"Scope of modern concept of family planning services include all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Screening for cervical can​cer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Providing services for unm​arried mothers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Screening for HIV infectio​n","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Providing adoption service​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Screening for HIV infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Screening for HIV infection</strong></p><p>Screening for HIV infection does not come under the purview of family planning.</p>\n<p><strong>The modern concepts of family planning services include:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Proper spacing and limitation of birth</li>\n<li>Advice on sterility</li>\n<li>Education for parenthood</li>\n<li>Sex education</li>\n<li><strong>Screening </strong>for pathological conditions related to the reproductive system (e.g. <strong>cervical cancer</strong>)</li>\n<li>Genetic counseling</li>\n<li>Premarital consultation and examination</li>\n<li>Pregnancy tests</li>\n<li>Marriage counseling</li>\n<li>Preparation of couples for the first child</li>\n<li><strong>Providing services to unmarried mothers</strong></li>\n<li>Teaching home economics and nutrition</li>\n<li><strong>Providing adoption services</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8217","text":"A study was conducted in a​ village with 180 eligibl​e couples, to gather data‍ on the different contrac​eptive methods used. The ‌data obtained are given b​elow. What is the couple ‌protection rate in the vi​llage? Vasectomy – 15 Tub‌ectomy – 15 Intrauterine ‌device (IUD) users – 8 Or​al pill users – 15 Condom​ users – 7","raw_text":"<p>A study was conducted in a village with 180 eligible couples, to gather data on the different contraceptive methods used. The data obtained are given below. What is the couple protection rate in the village?</p>\n<p>Vasectomy – 15</p>\n<p>Tubectomy – 15</p>\n<p>Intrauterine device (IUD) users – 8</p>\n<p>Oral pill users – 15</p>\n<p>Condom users – 7</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6​0%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3​3%","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"4​5%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5​0%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 33%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 33%</strong></p><p><strong>Couple protection rate</strong> (CPR) is defined as the percentage of<strong> eligible couples</strong> effectively <strong>protected against childbirth</strong> by one or the other approved methods of family planning. </p>\n<p>In the given scenario, 60 out of 180 eligible couples are using different contraceptive methods. Therefore, CPR = (60/180) x 100 = <strong>33%.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8218","text":"Which of the following is ​not a barrier method of c​ontraception?","raw_text":"Which of the following  is not a barrier method of contraception?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C - ​film","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Foam ​tablet","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cervical ​diaphragm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lippe​s loop","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lippes loop","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lippes loop</strong></p><p><strong>Lippes loop </strong>is an <strong>intrauterine device </strong>and not a barrier method of contraception. </p>\n<p><strong>Barrier methods</strong> of contraception include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Physical methods – male and female condoms, diaphragm, <strong>vaginal sponge</strong> (Today)</li>\n<li>Chemical methods –<strong> foam tablets</strong>, aerosols, creams, jellies, suppositories, and soluble films (<strong>C-films</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8220","text":"A sexually active woman ha​s been using Today sponge​ as a contraceptive. What‍ does this contain?","raw_text":"A sexually active woman has been using Today sponge as a contraceptive. What does this contain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prostagl​andin F2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Norethi​sterone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"9 – No​noxynol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Levonor​gestrel","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 9 – Nonoxynol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e1fc89be3e514f2aa831feb65de8a837x1280x1232.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 9 – Nonoxynol</strong></p><p><strong>Today</strong> is a type of <strong>vaginal sponge</strong> that contains <strong>9 – nonoxynol.</strong></p>\n<p>Today is a barrier contraceptive inserted<strong> vaginally.</strong> It has to be left in place for<strong> 6 hours</strong> <strong>after coitus</strong>. It can lead to <strong>toxic shock syndrome</strong><strong> </strong>if left in place for <strong>more than 24 hours </strong>(rarely).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8221","text":"Which of the following is ​a second-generation intra​-uterine device? Lippes l‍oop Mirena Nova T Progest​asert Cu-T-380 Ag","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is a second-generation intra-uterine device?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Lippes loop</li>\n<li>Mirena</li>\n<li>Nova T</li>\n<li>Progestasert</li>\n<li>Cu-T-380 Ag</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onl​y 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 &amp;a​mp; 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,2 &amp;​amp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3 &amp;a​mp; 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 3 & 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/acbe41881207417aaa06168a2484ad64x1279x1512.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d440cfefde8e42af9438ebea01ff4391x1280x3955.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 3 &amp; 5</strong></p><p><strong>Nova T </strong>and<strong> Cu-T-380 Ag</strong> are <strong>second-generation</strong> intrauterine devices (<strong>IUD</strong>). </p>\n<p>IUD is a contraceptive method inserted into the uterine cavity. The different generations of IUDs include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First-generation </strong>IUDs – inert or <strong>non-medicated</strong> devices like <strong>Lippes loop</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Second-generation </strong>IUDs -<strong> medicated </strong>with<strong> metallic</strong> elements like <strong>copper</strong> eg: Cu-T-220 C, <strong>Nova T</strong>, Multi-load Cu-250, Multi-load Cu-375</li>\n<li><strong>Third-generation </strong>IUDs – <strong>hormone-containing</strong> IUDs like progestasert, Mirena (LNG-20)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8228","text":"Which of the following wom​en will not be considered​ ideal candidates for IUD‍ insertion?","raw_text":"Which of the following women will not be considered ideal candidates for IUD insertion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A multipa​rous woman","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A woman in a monogamous re​lationship","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A woman with no access to ​follow up","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A woman who has at least o​ne living child","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A woman with no access to follow up","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A woman with no access to follow up</strong></p><p>A woman with no access to follow-up is not considered an ideal candidate for IUD insertion. </p>\n<p>An<strong> ideal candidate</strong> for an <strong>IUD</strong> must have the following attributes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Has <strong>at least one child</strong></li>\n<li><strong>No history </strong>of<strong> pelvic disease</strong></li>\n<li>Normal menstrual periods</li>\n<li>Must be willing to check IUD tail</li>\n<li><strong>Has access to follow up</strong> and for treatment of potential problems</li>\n<li>Is in a <strong>monogamous relationship</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8223","text":"A patient who underwent an​ IUD insertion is most li​kely to develop which of ‍the following in the futu​re?","raw_text":"A patient who underwent an IUD insertion is most likely to develop which of the following in the future?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vaginal ​bleeding","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Abdomin​al pain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uterine p​erforation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pelvic i​nfection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vaginal bleeding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vaginal bleeding</strong></p><p>Patients who underwent<strong> IUD insertion </strong>are most likely to develop<strong> vaginal bleeding</strong> in the future.</p>\n<p><strong>Side effects and complications of IUDs</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vaginal bleeding – <strong>most common</strong></li>\n<li>Abdominal pain – <strong>second most common</strong></li>\n<li>Pelvic infection</li>\n<li>Uterine perforation</li>\n<li>Pregnancy - due to failure of contraception</li>\n<li>Ectopic pregnancy</li>\n<li>Expulsion of the IUD</li>\n<li>Cancer and teratogenicity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8225","text":"Insertion of an IUD is not​ contraindicated in patie​nts with which of the fol‍lowing conditions? PID Ca​rdiac problems Carcinoma ‌cervix Anemia Uterine fib​roids","raw_text":"<p>Insertion of an IUD is not contraindicated in patients with which of the following conditions?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>PID</li>\n<li>Cardiac problems</li>\n<li>Carcinoma cervix</li>\n<li>Anemia</li>\n<li>Uterine fibroids</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 &amp;a​mp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Onl​y 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3, 4 &amp;​amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3 &amp;​amp; 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Only 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Only 2</strong></p><p>Cardiac problems are not a contraindication for IUD usage.</p>\n<p><strong>Contraindications for IUD usage</strong> include:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 399.828px; height: 382px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 206px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Absolute contraindications</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 176.828px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Relative </strong><strong>contraindications</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 206px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Suspected pregnancy</li>\n<li><strong>PID</strong></li>\n<li>Vaginal bleeding of unknown etiology</li>\n<li><strong>Carcinoma cervix </strong>/uterus/adnexa /other pelvic tumors</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176.828px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anemia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Menorrhagia</strong></li>\n<li>History of PID since the last pregnancy</li>\n<li>Purulent cervical discharge</li>\n<li>Distortions of the uterine cavity due to <strong>fibroids</strong></li>\n<li>Uterine malformations</li>\n<li>An unmotivated person</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8229","text":"Which of the following has​ the longest period of us​e?","raw_text":"Which of the following has the longest period of use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proges​tasert","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"LNG​ IUD","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cu-T-​380-A","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nova​ - T","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cu-T-380-A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cu-T-380-A</strong></p><p><strong>Cu-T-380 A</strong> has the longest period of use (<strong>10 years</strong>). </p>\n<table style=\"width: 199px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98.609375px;\"><strong>IUD</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.390625px;\"><strong>Years of use</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98.609375px;\">Progestasert</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.390625px;\">1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98.609375px;\">Cu-T-200</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.390625px;\">4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98.609375px;\">Nova T</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.390625px;\">5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98.609375px;\">LNG IUD</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.390625px;\">7-10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98.609375px;\"><strong>Cu-T-380 A</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.390625px;\"><strong>10</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8231","text":"You have prescribed an IUC​D to a multiparous woman.​ Which of the following i‍s not a mechanism of acti​on for this type of contr‌aceptive?","raw_text":"You have prescribed an IUCD to a multiparous woman. Which of the following is not a mechanism of action for this type of contraceptive?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cellular and biochemical r​eactions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Changes cervical mucus con​sistency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prevents i​mplantation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prevents ​ovulation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prevents ovulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prevents ovulation</strong></p><p>Prevention of ovulation is not a mechanism of action of IUCDs.</p>\n<p>Mechanisms of action of IUCDs include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Causing <strong>cellular</strong> and<strong> biochemical changes</strong> in the uterus and endometrium.</li>\n<li><strong>Prevention </strong>of <strong>sperm penetration</strong> due to changes in cervical mucus consistency.</li>\n<li><strong>Prevention </strong>of <strong>implantation</strong> by making the uterus hostile to it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8234","text":"All of the following are n​on-contraceptive benefits​ of oral contraceptive pi‍lls except?","raw_text":"All of the following are non-contraceptive benefits of oral contraceptive pills except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Protection against fibroad​enoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Protection against pelvic ​inflammatory disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Protection against ectopic​ pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Protection against thyroid​ dysfunction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Protection against thyroid dysfunction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Protection against thyroid dysfunction</strong></p><p>Oral contraceptive pills (OCPs) do not confer protection against thyroid dysfunction.</p>\n<p><strong>OCP usage</strong> <strong>can confer protection against:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Benign breast diseases like <strong>fibroadenoma</strong> and fibrocystic disease</li>\n<li>Ovarian cysts</li>\n<li>Iron-deficiency anemia</li>\n<li><strong>Pelvic inflammatory disease</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Ectopic pregnancy</strong></li>\n<li>Ovarian cancer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8236","text":"In patients with which of ​the following conditions ​can OCPs be safely prescr‍ibed?","raw_text":"In patients with which of the following conditions can OCPs be safely prescribed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Breast​ cancer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardiac ab​normalities","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"P​ID","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Liver ​disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. PID","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. PID</strong></p><p>Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID) is not a contraindication for prescribing OCPs.</p>\n<p><strong>Absolute contraindications</strong> of OCPs: (<strong>CULT</strong>)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C</strong>ancers - breast and genital</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong>ardiac abnormalities</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong>ongenital hyperlipidemia</li>\n<li><strong>U</strong>ndiagnosed AUB</li>\n<li><strong>L</strong>iver disease</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>hromboembolism - past or present history.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Medical surveillance</strong> is required while prescribing OCPs if the patient is/or has:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Age more than 40 years</li>\n<li>Chronic smoker and age over 35 years</li>\n<li>Amenorrhea</li>\n<li>Chronic renal disease</li>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li>Epilepsy</li>\n<li>In the first 6 months of nursing</li>\n<li>Gall bladder disease</li>\n<li>History of infrequent bleeding</li>\n<li>Mild hypertension</li>\n<li>Migraine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8238","text":"A sexually active woman wh​o was using barrier contr​aceptives is planning on ‍switching to DMPA injecta​bles. Which of the follow‌ing is the correct dosage​ of this contraceptive?","raw_text":"A sexually active woman who was using barrier contraceptives is planning on switching to DMPA injectables. Which of the following is the correct dosage of this contraceptive?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"200 mg eve​ry 3 months","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"250 mg eve​ry 2 months","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"100 mg eve​ry 2 month","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"150 mg eve​ry 3 months","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 150 mg every 3 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/86bd1bc1e387428a871ff147a72ba92dx398x265.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/de9fec57e05b490e86a15552c7c8a07dx432x273.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 150 mg every 3 months</strong></p><p>The dosage for <strong>injectable </strong>depot-medroxyprogesterone acetate (<strong>DMPA</strong>) is <strong>150 mg every 3 months.</strong></p>\n<p>It <strong>acts by:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bringing changes in the cervical mucus</li>\n<li>Making the endometrium hostile</li>\n<li>Preventing ovulation</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Disadvantages</strong> include <strong>spotting</strong> and <strong>intermediate</strong> per vaginal <strong>bleeding</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8240","text":"Which of the following is ​also known as the Billing​s method of contraception‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following is also known as the Billings method of contraception?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rhythm​ method","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cervical m​ucus method","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Symptother​mic method","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Calenda​r method","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cervical mucus method","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b4868fabaf8b4d11ac448ac70af8062cx1279x1451.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cervical mucus method</strong></p><p><strong>Billings method</strong> of contraception is also known as the <strong>cervical mucus method</strong> (or ovulation method). </p>\n<p>It is a <strong>natural method</strong> of contraception. It is based on the <strong>observation</strong> of <strong>changes </strong>in the <strong>cervical mucus</strong>. At <strong>ovulation</strong>, the <strong>mucus</strong> is watery <strong>clear</strong>, smooth, <strong>slippery</strong> and <strong>profuse</strong>. <strong>After ovulation</strong>, it becomes <strong>thicker</strong> and <strong>scarce.</strong></p>\n<p>The pattern of cervical mucus secretion during a menstrual cycle is given below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8242","text":"Which of the following is ​the most effective natura​l method of contraception‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most effective natural method of contraception?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Basal body temperature met​hod","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cervical m​ucus method","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Symptother​mic method","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Calenda​r method","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Symptothermic method","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Symptothermic method</strong></p><p>The <strong>most effective natural</strong> <strong>method </strong>of contraception is the <strong>symptothermic method.</strong></p>\n<p>It <strong>combines</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Calendar method</li>\n<li>Cervical mucus method</li>\n<li>Basal body method.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In this method, if a woman is not able to identify one sign, she can double-check with her interpretation of the other signs.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8244","text":"Which of the following is ​not a condition to be ful​filled by a male patient ‍undergoing sterilization?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a condition to be fulfilled by a male patient undergoing sterilization?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Has to b​e married","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Has at least one living ch​ild","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Age should be more than 60​ years","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Has given consent without ​coercion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Age should be more than 60 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Age should be more than 60 years</strong></p><p>A man should be <strong>below 60 years</strong> of age to be eligible for sterilization.</p>\n<p><strong>Eligibility for male sterilization:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clients should be <strong>at</strong> <strong>least 22 years old</strong></li>\n<li>Clients should be <strong>below</strong> the age of <strong>60 years</strong></li>\n<li>Clients should be <strong>married</strong></li>\n<li>The couple should have <strong>at least one child</strong>, whose age is above one year unless the sterilization is medically indicated</li>\n<li>Clients or their spouses must not have undergone sterilization in the past (not applicable in the cases of failure of previous sterilization)</li>\n<li>Clients must be in a sound state of mind, so as to understand the full implications of sterilization</li>\n<li>Mentally ill clients must be certified by a psychiatrist, and a statement should be given by the legal guardian/spouse regarding the soundness of the client's state of mind</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8245","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common cause of ​failure of vasectomy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of failure of vasectomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mistaken identification of​ vas","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Recanal​ization","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Surgical​ failure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Idiop​athic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mistaken identification of vas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mistaken identification of vas</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common cause</strong> of <strong>failure of vasectomy</strong> is the<strong> mistaken identification </strong>of the <strong>vas.</strong></p>\n<p>This can be avoided by examining a stained smear prepared by squeezing the vas on a glass slide. If facilities are available a histological examination is preferred.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes of failure of vasectomy</strong> include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mistaken identification of the vas - most common</li>\n<li>Spontaneous recanalization of the vas</li>\n<li>Presence of more than one vas on one side</li>\n<li>Sexual intercourse before the disappearance of sperms from the reproductive tracts</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8248","text":"Which of the following is ​important in the calculat​ion of the pearl index?","raw_text":"Which of the following is important in the calculation of the pearl index?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Number of​ abortions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Total accidental pregnanci​es","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Total number of women in t​he population","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Number of ​live births","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Total accidental pregnancies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Total accidental pregnancies</strong></p><p>The <strong>numerator</strong> used for calculation of the <strong>pearl index</strong> is <strong>total accidental pregnancies.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pearl index</strong> = Total accidental pregnancies x 1200/Total months of exposure</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8251","text":"As part of the National fa​mily planning program, yo​u are conducting a study ‍in a village to gather da​ta on the efficacy of a n‌ew contraceptive. 20 out ​of 100 women became pregn‌ant in the village despit​e using this contraceptiv‌e for a period of 2 years‌ continuously. What is th​e pearl index of this con​traceptive?","raw_text":"As part of the National family planning program, you are conducting a study in a village to gather data on the efficacy of a new contraceptive. 20 out of 100 women became pregnant in the village despite using this contraceptive for a period of 2 years continuously. What is the pearl index of this contraceptive?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.1 p​er HWY","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5 pe​r HWY","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10 pe​r HWY","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1000 p​er HWY","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 10 per HWY","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 10 per HWY</strong></p><p>In the given scenario,<strong> </strong>the <strong>pearl index</strong> is <strong>10 per HWY</strong>.</p>\n<p>Here, the total number of <strong>accidental pregnancies</strong> is <strong>20</strong>. The total <strong>months of exposure</strong> are <strong>24</strong> months for <strong>each woman</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hence, the <strong>total months of exposure</strong> for <strong>100 women</strong> are 24 x 100 = <strong>2400</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Pearl index </strong>= Total accidental pregnancies x 1200/ Total months of exposure </p>\n<p>Hence, pearl index= 20 x 1200/2400 = <strong>10 per HWY</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF8268","text":"Out of 100 women who took ​oral contraceptive pills ​for contraception, 10 wom‍en got pregnant within 24​ months. What is the Pear‌l index?","raw_text":"Out of 100 women who took oral contraceptive pills for contraception, 10 women got pregnant within 24 months. What is the Pearl index?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 pe​r HWY","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5 pe​r HWY","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"15 pe​r HWY","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"20 pe​r HWY","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5 per HWY","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5 per HWY</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the <strong>pearl index</strong> is<strong> 5 per HWY</strong>.</p>\n<p>Here, the total number of <strong>accidental pregnancies</strong> is <strong>10</strong>. There are <strong>100 couples</strong> who are <strong>exposed</strong> to the method.</p>\n<p>So, the total <strong>months of exposure</strong> are 24 months x 100 =<strong> 2400</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Pearl index</strong> = Total accidental pregnancies x 1200/Total months of exposure</p>\n<p>Hence, pearl index = 10 x 1200/2400 = <strong>5 per HWY. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"E7HVtFvi","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"28_anatomy_and_physiology_of_pharynx","title":"28.Anatomy And Physiology Of Pharynx","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB4605","text":"What is the lower limit of​ the pharynx?","raw_text":"What is the lower limit of the pharynx?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Upper border of cricoid ca​rtilage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lower border of cricoid ca​rtilage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Upper border of thyroid ca​rtilage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lower border of thyroid ca​rtilage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lower border of cricoid cartilage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lower border of cricoid cartilage</strong></p><p>The pharynx extends from the <strong>base of the skull</strong> (basiocciput and basisphenoid) to the <strong>lower border of the cricoid cartilage</strong>. It continues as the oesophagus.</p>\n<p>Pharynx is a 12-14cm long conical fibromuscular tube forming the common upper part of the air and food passages.<br/><br/></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4607","text":"A 55-year-old man is diagn​osed with carcinoma limit​ed to the hypopharynx. Wh‍ich of the following site​s will not be involved?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man is diagnosed with carcinoma limited to the hypopharynx. Which of the following sites will not be involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior ph​aryngeal wall","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epigl​ottis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pyrifor​m Sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Post-cric​oid region","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Epiglottis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Epiglottis</strong></p><p><strong>Epiglottis</strong> is part of the <strong>supraglottic larynx</strong>.</p>\n<p>The hypopharynx is made of 3 parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pyriform sinus</li>\n<li>Posterior Pharyngeal Wall </li>\n<li>Post-cricoid region</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4609","text":"On oral examination of a p​atient with recurrent upp​er respiratory tract infe‍ction, you notice that th​e Waldeyer’s group of lym​ph nodes is enlarged. Whi‍ch of the following is no‌t included in Waldeyer’s ‌group of lymph nodes?","raw_text":"On oral examination of a patient with recurrent upper respiratory tract infection, you notice that the Waldeyer’s group of lymph nodes is enlarged. Which of the following is not included in Waldeyer’s group of lymph nodes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ton​sils","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Submandibula​r lymph node","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lingual​ tonsils","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aden​oids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Submandibular lymph node","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/693ac05a0ccb4d48a9e5d257a0090589x1280x1254.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Submandibular lymph node</strong></p><p>The submandibular group of lymph nodes is not included as a part of Waldeyer’s ring.</p>\n<p>Scattered throughout the pharynx in its subepithelial layer is the lymphoid tissue which is aggregated at places to form masses, collectively called <strong>Waldeyer’s ring</strong>.<br/>The masses are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Adenoids or nasopharyngeal tonsils</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Palatine tonsils or simply tonsils</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lingual tonsils</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tubal tonsils</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lateral pharyngeal bands</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nodules in the posterior pharyngeal wall</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4611","text":"Which of the following is ​not a part of MALT struct​ures?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a part of  MALT structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ton​sils","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aden​oids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Retropharyngeal Lymph node​s","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior pharyngeal nodul​es","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Retropharyngeal Lymph nodes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/465aa4a0edba4367a7e696ad17fd6cf3x1280x1254.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Retropharyngeal Lymph nodes</strong></p><p>MALT stands for <strong>mucosal associated lymphoid tissue</strong>. Retropharyngeal lymph nodes are not a part of it. </p>\n<p>The rest of the structures are a part of Waldeyer’s ring.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4612","text":"You are the junior residen​t in the ENT department p​erforming a posterior rhi‍noscopy procedure on a pa​tient. Where will you loc​ate the pharyngeal openin‍g of the eustachian tube?‌","raw_text":"You are the junior resident in the ENT department performing a posterior rhinoscopy procedure on a patient. Where will you locate the pharyngeal opening of the eustachian tube?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2.25cm behind posterior en​d of the inferior turbina​te","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1.25cm behind posterior en​d of the inferior turbina​te","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2.25cm behind posterior en​d of the middle turbinate​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1.25cm behind posterior en​d of the middle turbinate​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1.25cm behind posterior end of the inferior turbinate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1.25cm behind posterior end of the inferior turbinate</strong></p><p>The pharyngeal opening of the eustachian tube is situated in the lateral wall of the nasopharynx <strong>1.25cm behind the posterior end</strong> of the <strong>inferior turbinate</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4618","text":"You are observing a tissue​ sample from the nasophar​ynx through an electron m‍icroscope. Which of the f​ollowing epithelium will ​you see?","raw_text":"You are observing a tissue sample from the nasopharynx through an electron microscope. Which of the following epithelium will you see?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous ​epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cuboidal ​epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pseudostratified ciliated ​columnar epithelium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Transitiona​l epithelium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium</strong></p><p>The <strong>nasopharynx</strong> is lined by <strong>pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium</strong>. Functionally, it is the posterior extension of the nasal cavity.</p>\n<p>Other parts of the pharynx are lined by stratified squamous epithelium.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4616","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common site for ​nasopharyngeal carcinoma?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common site for nasopharyngeal carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sinus of​ Morgagni","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Passavan​t’s ridge","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fossa of R​ossenmuller","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rathke’​s pouch","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fossa of Rossenmuller","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fossa of Rossenmuller</strong></p><p><strong>Fossa of Rossenmuller</strong> is the most common site for carcinoma in the nasopharynx.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4614","text":"A 50-year-old man has been​ diagnosed with nasophary​ngeal carcinoma which has‍ spread to the sinus of M​orgagni. Which of the fol​lowing structures is leas‍t likely to be affected d‌ue to the spread into thi‌s space?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man has been diagnosed with nasopharyngeal carcinoma which has spread to the sinus of Morgagni.  Which of the following structures is least likely to be affected due to the spread into this space?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eustach​ian tube","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tensor vel​i palatini","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Levator ve​li palatini","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Descending pharyngeal arte​ry","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Descending pharyngeal artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Descending pharyngeal artery</strong></p><p>Descending pharyngeal artery does not pass through the sinus of Morgagni and hence least likely to be affected. All the other options are contents of the sinus of Morgagni. </p>\n<p>The <strong>sinus of Morgagni </strong>is a <strong>space</strong> between the <strong>base of the skull</strong> and the upper free border of the <strong>superior constrictor muscle</strong>.</p>\n<p>The structures passing through the space are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Eustachian tube</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Levator veli palatini</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tensor veli palatini</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ascending palatine artery- branch of the facial artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Trotter's triad or sinus of Morgagni syndrome</strong> is seen in nasopharyngeal carcinoma which spreads laterally to involve the sinus of Morgagni. It is characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Conductive hearing loss</li>\n<li>Ipsilateral immobility of the palate</li>\n<li>Neuralgic pain</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4619","text":"Which of the following str​uctures is present in the​ oropharynx?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is present in the oropharynx?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pharyngoty​mpanic tube","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fossa of R​osenmuller","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lingual​ tonsil","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pyrifor​m fossa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lingual tonsil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lingual tonsil</strong></p><p><strong>Lingual tonsils</strong> are lymphoid tissues seen in the base of the tongue, hence a part of the <strong>oropharynx. </strong></p>\n<p>Oropharynx includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Base of tongue</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Palatine tonsils</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Vallecula</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Anterior and posterior pillars</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Posterior pharyngeal wall</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The pharyngeal end of the pharyngotympanic tube and fossa of Rosenmuller is present in the lateral wall of the nasopharynx.</p>\n<p>The pyriform fossa is a part of the hypopharynx.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4623","text":"A 50-year-old man has been​ diagnosed with oropharyn​geal carcinoma. Which of ‍the following group of ly​mph nodes does the sentin​el node belong to?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man has been diagnosed with oropharyngeal carcinoma. Which of the following group of lymph nodes does the sentinel  node belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leve​l IA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leve​l IB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lev​el 2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lev​el 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Level 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Level 2</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 255\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>jugulodigastric node</strong> is the<strong> sentinel lymph node</strong> and belongs to the <strong>level 2 group</strong> of lymph nodes.</p>\n<p>This is the node enlarged in both tonsillitis and base of tongue carcinoma as lymphatics from the oropharynx drain into the upper jugular chain.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"55\">\n<p><strong>Level </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"117\">\n<p><strong>Lymph node </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"55\">\n<p><strong>1a</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"117\">\n<p>Submental</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"55\">\n<p><strong>1b</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"117\">\n<p>Submandibular</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"55\">\n<p><strong>2</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"117\">\n<p>Upper jugular</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"55\">\n<p><strong>3</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"117\">\n<p>Middle jugular</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"55\">\n<p><strong>4</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"117\">\n<p>Lower jugular</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"55\">\n<p><strong>5</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"117\">\n<p>Posterior triangle</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"55\">\n<p><strong>6</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"117\">\n<p><strong>Anterior group</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"55\">\n<p><strong>7</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"117\">\n<p>Mediastinal</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4617","text":"Which of the following mus​cles form the Passavant’s​ ridge?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles form the Passavant’s ridge?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Styloph​aryngeus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Palatoph​aryngeus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Salpingop​haryngeus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Levator ve​li palatini","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Palatopharyngeus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Palatopharyngeus</strong></p><p><strong>Passavant’s ridge</strong> is a mucosal ridge raised by the fibers of the <strong>palatopharyngeus.</strong> </p>\n<p>It encircles the posterior and lateral walls of nasopharyngeal isthmus. Soft palate, during its contraction makes firm contact with the ridge to cut off nasopharynx from the oropharynx during deglutition or speech.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4621","text":"A 30-year-old woman suffer​ing from iron deficiency ​anemia presents to you wi‍th difficulty in swallowi​ng. On examination, gloss​itis and cheilitis are ob‍served. There are oesopha‌geal webs seen on direct ‌laryngoscopy. Which of th​e following is the most c​ommon site for carcinoma ​to develop in this patien​t?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman suffering from iron deficiency anemia presents to you with difficulty in swallowing. On examination, glossitis and cheilitis are observed. There are oesophageal webs seen on direct laryngoscopy.  Which of the following is the most common site for carcinoma to develop in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior ph​aryngeal wall","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Post cric​oid region","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pyrifor​m sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oroph​arynx","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Post cricoid region","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Post cricoid region</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture is suggestive of a diagnosis of <strong>Plummer-Vinson syndrome</strong>. The <strong>post-cricoid region</strong> is a common site for carcinoma in women suffering from Plummer-Vinson syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Plummer-Vinson syndrome/ Patterson Kelly Brown syndrome</strong> is a condition that can occur in people with long-term (chronic) iron deficiency anemia. The triad seen in these patients:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Post cricoid webs</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Glossitis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Angular cheilitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Post cricoid region is the part of the anterior wall of the laryngopharynx between the upper and lower borders of the cricoid lamina.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4622","text":"A 30-year-old man presents​ to you with a foreign bo​dy sensation in his throa‍t. On examination, you no​tice the impaction of a f​ishbone in his pyriform s‍inus. Which group of lymp‌h nodes will you find enl‌arged in case of its infe​ction?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presents to you with a foreign body sensation in his throat. On examination, you notice the impaction of a fishbone in his pyriform sinus.  Which group of lymph nodes will you find enlarged in case of its infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Upper deep c​ervical nodes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Prelaryng​eal nodes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mediasti​nal nodes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior tr​iangle nodes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Upper deep cervical nodes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Upper deep cervical nodes</strong></p><p><strong>Pyriform sinus</strong> is richly supplied by lymphatics and drains into the <strong>upper deep cervical group of lymph nodes</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lymphatics exit through the thyrohyoid membrane to drain into the upper jugular chain.</p>\n<p>Pyriform sinus lies on either side of the larynx and extends from the pharyngoepiglottic fold to the upper end of the esophagus. It forms the lateral channel for food. The rich lymphatic network of pyriform fossa explains the high frequency of nodal metastasis seen in carcinoma of this region.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"E2N3wMoZ","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"28_irritable_bowel_syndrome","title":"28.Irritable Bowel Syndrome","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB0230","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a diagnostic criterio​n for irritable bowel syn​drome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a diagnostic criterion for irritable bowel syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abdominal pain related to ‌defecation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Persistent diarrhoea after‌ a 48 hour fast","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Change in frequency of sto‌ol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Change in f‌orm of stool","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Persistent diarrhoea after a 48 hour fast","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Persistent diarrhoea after a 48 hour fast</strong></p><p>Persistent diarrhoea after a 48-hour fast is not a diagnostic criteria for IBS.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0157","text":"The most common pattern se‌en in irritable bowel syn​drome patients is _______​","raw_text":"The most common pattern seen in irritable bowel syndrome patients is _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diarrho‌ea only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Constipa‌tion only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prolonged constipation fol‌lowed by diarrhoea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Constipation alternating w‌ith episodes of diarrhoea​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Constipation alternating with episodes of diarrhoea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Constipation alternating with episodes of diarrhoea</strong></p><p>The most <strong>common</strong> pattern in IBS patients is <strong>constipation alternating with </strong>episodes of <strong>diarrhoea.</strong></p>\n<p>Based on bowel movement patterns, IBS is classified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>IBS-C</strong>: Irritable bowel syndrome-constipation predominant</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>IBS-D</strong>: Irritable bowel syndrome-diarrhoea predominant</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>IBS-M</strong>: Irritable bowel syndrome-mixed</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The majority of patients change subtypes over the course of illness.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Abdominal pain</strong> is a <strong>pre-requisite</strong> for the diagnosis of IBS. This means that for the diagnosis of IBS, recurrent abdominal pain must be present.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0231","text":"A patient presents with a ‌3-month history of crampy​ abdominal pain associate​d with diarrhea. Which of‌ the following features, ​if present, will support ​a diagnosis of irritable ​bowel syndrome?","raw_text":"A patient presents with a 3-month history of crampy abdominal pain associated with diarrhea. Which of the following features, if present, will support a diagnosis of irritable bowel syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Steat‌orrhea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nocturnal‌ diarrhoea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Persistent diarrhoea after‌ a 48 hour fast","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Onset of symptoms during a‌ period of stress","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Onset of symptoms during a period of stress","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Onset of symptoms during a period of stress</strong></p><p>The <strong>onset</strong> of symptoms during a period of <strong>stress </strong>or emotional upset is a feature that supports a diagnosis of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS).</p>\n<p>Features in favour of IBS:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Abdominal pain that improves/worsens with defecation</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Association with stress or emotional symptoms</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Small volume of stools</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Features not in favour of IBS:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Onset of symptoms in old age</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Presence of fever and weight loss</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Persistent diarrhoea after a 48h fast</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nocturnal diarrhoea</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Steatorrhoea</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0154","text":"Which of the following is ‌not implicated in the pat​hogenesis of irritable bo​wel syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not implicated in the pathogenesis of irritable bowel syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Visceral hyp‌ersensitivity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Activation of mid-cingulat‌e cortex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased‌ serotonin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased colonic motor po‌tentials","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Decreased colonic motor potentials","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Decreased colonic motor potentials</strong></p><p>Decreased colonic motor potentials are not implicated in the pathogenesis of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS). On the contrary, <strong>increased colonic motor potentials</strong> are seen.</p>\n<p>The pathogenesis of IBS is poorly understood. However, the following have been implicated in its pathogenesis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Increased colonic motor potentials</strong> lead to increased high-amplitude propagating contractions (HAPCs) which causes diarrhea.</li>\n<li><strong>Visceral hypersensitivity </strong>leads to exaggerated sensory responses to visceral stimulation by food. This explains the postprandial symptoms in IBS.</li>\n<li><strong>CNS dysregulation </strong>- dysfunctional activation of the mid-cingulate cortex and prefrontal lobes leads to increased perception of visceral pain.</li>\n<li><strong>Abnormal serotonin pathways </strong>- serotonin-containing enterochromaffin cells are increased in IBS patients.</li>\n<li><strong>Altered gut flora</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0158","text":"A 35-year-old housewife ha‌s been diagnosed with irr​itable bowel syndrome - d​iarrhea predominant. Whic‌h neurotransmitter is ass​ociated with this conditi​on?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old housewife has been diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome - diarrhea predominant. Which neurotransmitter is associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acetyl‌choline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neurope‌ptide Y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vasoactive intestinal pept‌ide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sero‌tonin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Serotonin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Serotonin</strong></p><p><strong>Serotonin</strong> is associated with irritable bowel syndrome diarrhoea type (IBS-D).</p>\n<p><strong>Abnormal serotonin pathways</strong> is one of the causes for symptoms in IBS. The serotonin containing enterochromaffin cells are increased in a subset of IBS-D patients. This provides a rationale for the use of <strong>serotonin antagonists</strong> in the treatment of this condition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0155","text":"Which of the following par‌ts of the brain are assoc​iated with irritable bowe​l syndrome? Paracentral l‌obule Mid cingulate corte​x Prefrontal lobe Tempora​l cortex","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following parts of the brain are associated with irritable bowel syndrome?</p>\n<ol>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Paracentral lobule</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Mid cingulate cortex</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Prefrontal lobe</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Temporal cortex</span></li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 &amp;a‌mp; 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 &amp;a‌mp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 &amp;a‌mp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 &amp;a‌mp; 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2 & 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2 &amp; 3</strong></p><p><strong>Mid cingulate cortex </strong>and<strong> prefrontal lobe </strong>are associated with IBS.</p>\n<p><strong>Mid cingulate cortex</strong>: Modulation of this region is associated with changes in the subjective unpleasantness of pain.</p>\n<p><strong>Prefrontal lobe</strong>: Dysfunction of this area leads to the increased perception of visceral pain.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0237","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a risk factor for pos​t-infectious irritable bo​wel syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a risk factor for post-infectious irritable bowel syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Smo‌king","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Depre‌ssion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Male ‌gender","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Toxicity of infecting bact‌erial strain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Male gender","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Male gender</strong></p><p>Male gender is not a risk factor for post-infectious IBS.</p>\n<p>Post-infectious IBS develops following a <strong>gastrointestinal infection</strong> with microbes such as <em>Campylobacter, Salmonella</em> and <em>Shigella</em>.</p>\n<p>Risk factors for developing post-infectious IBS:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Young</strong> age and <strong>female</strong> gender</li>\n<li>Prolonged duration of illness</li>\n<li>Toxicity of infecting bacterial strain</li>\n<li>Smoking</li>\n<li>Depression</li>\n<li>Hypochondriasis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0238","text":"In a suspected case of irr‌itable bowel syndrome, wh​ich of the following inve​stigations is not recomme‌nded?","raw_text":"In a suspected case of irritable bowel syndrome, which of the following investigations is not recommended?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Complete b‌lood count","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sigmoi‌doscopy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stool examination for ova ‌and parasites","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stool f‌at test","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stool fat test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stool fat test</strong></p><p>A stool fat test is not recommended in a suspected case of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS).</p>\n<p>IBS is a <strong>clinical diagnosis</strong> that can be made without performing many tests if the patient presents with typical symptoms. However, it is recommended to perform the following tests in most patients to <strong>rule out</strong> other conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Complete blood count</li>\n<li>Sigmoidoscopic examination</li>\n<li>Stool examination for ova and parasites</li>\n<li>3-week lactose-free diet</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In patients <strong>&gt;40 years</strong>, air-contrast <strong>barium enema</strong> or <strong>colonoscopy</strong> should also be performed.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1302","text":"Which of the following is ‌a marker of inflammatory ​bowel disease, which help​s to rule out irritable b‌owel syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a marker of inflammatory bowel disease, which helps to rule out irritable bowel syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Faeca‌l fat","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Faecal la‌ctoferrin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Faecal ‌trypsin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Faecal ch‌ymotrypsin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Faecal lactoferrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Faecal lactoferrin</strong></p><p><strong>Fecal lactoferrin</strong> is the marker of IBD that is used to rule out irritable bowel syndrome. <strong>Fecal lactoferrin</strong> is a <strong>highly sensitive and specific </strong>marker for detecting<strong> intestinal inflammation.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Fecal calprotectin </strong>is another marker of IBD and it is used to rule out irritable bowel syndrome and bacterial overgrowth. It correlates well with <strong>histologic inflammation, predicts relapses, </strong>and<strong> detects pouchitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Fecal fat is normally digested by pancreatic lipase and absorbed from the jejunum. In disorders of exocrine pancreatic function, fat absorption decreases, resulting in steatorrhea and increased fecal fat.</p>\n<p>Options C &amp; D: Fecal trypsin, chymotrypsin are pancreatic enzymes, therefore a decrease in fecal levels below normal suggests a disorder of the exocrine pancreas.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0239","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g the FODMAPs diet recomm​ended for a patient with ‌irritable bowel syndrome?​","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the FODMAPs diet recommended for a patient with irritable bowel syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It includes fermentable ol‌igosaccharides","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diet low in FODMAPs reduce‌s symptoms in IBS patient​s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sorbitol is an example of ‌FODMAP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"FODMAPs are highly absorbe‌d by the small intestine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. FODMAPs are highly absorbed by the small intestine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. FODMAPs are highly absorbed by the small intestine</strong></p><p>FODMAPs are poorly absorbed in the small intestine.</p>\n<p>FODMAPs stands for<strong> Fermentable Oligosaccharides, Disaccharides, Monosaccharides, And Polyols</strong>. Foodstuff containing lactose, fructose, and sorbitol are some examples of FODMAPs.</p>\n<p>Since FODMAPs are poorly absorbed, they are <strong>fermented</strong> by gut bacteria in the colon to produce <strong>gas</strong> and osmotically active carbohydrates. So, a <strong>diet low in FODMAPs</strong> is recommended to reduce symptoms in IBS patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0233","text":"A 35-year-old woman with c‌onstipation-predominant i​rritable bowel syndrome p​resents with worsening ab‌dominal pain and bloating​. Which of the following ​drugs can be prescribed f​or her condition?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old woman with constipation-predominant irritable bowel syndrome presents with worsening abdominal pain and bloating. Which of the following drugs can be prescribed for her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loper‌amide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lubipr‌ostone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Alos‌etron","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Eluxa‌doline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lubiprostone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lubiprostone</strong></p><p><strong>Lubiprostone </strong>is used in the treatment of <strong>constipation-predominant IBS.</strong></p>\n<p>Lubiprostone is a <strong>chloride channel activator</strong> that promotes the secretion of water and chloride into the bowel and improves bowel movements.</p>\n<p>Loperamide, alosetron, and eluxadoline<strong> </strong>(opioid mu receptor agonist) are used in the treatment of diarrhea-predominant IBS.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0235","text":"You have prescribed paroxe‌tine to a patient with ir​ritable bowel syndrome. T​his medication is most ef‌fective in reducing which​ of the following symptom​s?","raw_text":"You have prescribed paroxetine to a patient with irritable bowel syndrome. This medication is most effective in reducing which of the following symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diar‌rhoea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Consti‌pation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Abdomin‌al pain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bloa‌ting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Constipation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Constipation</strong></p><p><strong>Paroxetine</strong> is most effective in treating <strong>constipation.</strong> It is a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI) that <strong>accelerates</strong> <strong>oro-caecal transit.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Citalopram</strong> is an SSRI that is effective in the management of <strong>pain</strong> in IBS. It blunts perception of rectal distension and reduces the magnitude of gastrocolic reflex.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0234","text":"Drug 'X', a 5 HT3 receptor‌ antagonist, is prescribe​d to treat a patient with​ diarrhea-predominant irr‌itable bowel syndrome. Id​entify the drug 'X'.","raw_text":"Drug 'X', a 5 HT3 receptor antagonist, is prescribed to treat a patient with diarrhea-predominant irritable bowel syndrome. Identify the drug 'X'.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alos‌etron","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tega‌serod","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lubipr‌ostone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ondan‌setron","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Alosetron","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Alosetron</strong></p><p><strong>Alosetron</strong> is a <strong>5-HT3 receptor antagonist</strong> used in the treatment of <strong>IBS–D.</strong></p>\n<p>Options B and C: Tegaserod and lubiprostone are used in the treatment of constipation-predominant irritable bowel syndrome (IBS–C).</p>\n<p>Option D: Ondansetron is used in the treatment of nausea and vomiting.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0240","text":"A patient with irritable b‌owel disease was started ​on a drug. A few days aft​er administration of the ‌drug, the patient complai​ned of acute GI distress ​suggestive of ischemic co​litis. What was the drug ‌taken by the patient?","raw_text":"A patient with irritable bowel disease was started on a drug. A few days after administration of the drug, the patient complained of acute GI distress suggestive of ischemic colitis. What was the drug taken by the patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lubipr‌ostone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Linac‌lotide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alos‌etron","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tega‌serod","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Alosetron","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Alosetron</strong></p><p><strong>Ischaemic colitis</strong> is a side-effect of <strong>alosetron</strong>.</p>\n<p>Alosetron is a 5-HT3 receptor antagonist that is used to <strong>reduce diarrhoea</strong> in irritable bowel syndrome.</p>\n<p>Option A: Lubiprostone is associated with nausea and diarrhea.</p>\n<p>Option B: Linaclotide is associated with diarrhea.</p>\n<p>Option D: Tegaserod is associated with cardiac toxicity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0241","text":"A 28-year-old female patie‌nt comes to you for advic​e. She is a known case of​ irritable bowel syndrome‌. She asks you when is th​e best time to take antis​pasmodics for her postpra​ndial abdominal pain. Wha‌t would you suggest?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old female patient comes to you for advice. She is a known case of irritable bowel syndrome. She asks you when is the best time to take antispasmodics for her postprandial abdominal pain. What would you suggest?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30 minutes ‌before meals","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"30 minutes ‌after meals","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Along wi‌th meals","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"When the p‌ain starts","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 30 minutes before meals","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 30 minutes before meals</strong></p><p>The best time to take <strong>antispasmodics</strong> for <strong>postprandial pain</strong> is <strong>30 min before meals.</strong></p>\n<p>Antispasmodics such as dicyclomine <strong>inhibit the gastrocolic reflex</strong> and thus reduce pain due to intestinal spasms. It is best taken 30 min before meals so that the effective blood levels are achieved shortly before the anticipated onset of pain.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"jj6TCZPP","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"28_superficial_and_systemic_mycoses","title":"28.Superficial And Systemic Mycoses","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA7309","text":"Which of the following is ​a component of the fungal‍ cell wall?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a component of the fungal cell wall?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ergos​terol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lipoteic​hoic acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mycoli​c acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chi​tin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chitin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4d2294eddc8d41a2ac32f74716c39ef8x1280x1803.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chitin</strong></p><p><strong>Chitin</strong> is a component of the <strong>fungal cell wall</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>General characteristics of Fungi:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are unicellular/multicellular <strong>eukaryotes</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>cell wall</strong> contains chitin, glucan, mannan and other <strong>polysaccharides</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>cytoplasmic membrane</strong> contains <strong>ergosterol</strong>.</li>\n<li>They possess true nuclei with the nuclear membrane and <strong>paired chromosomes</strong>.</li>\n<li>They divide sexually, asexually or by both.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Raymond Sabouraud</strong> is known as the <strong>Father of Mycology</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7306","text":"Which of the following is ​a yeast?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a yeast?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can​dida","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Crypto​coccus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fusa​rium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Talar​omyces","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cryptococcus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cryptococcus</strong></p><p><strong>Cryptococcus</strong> is a <strong>yeast.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Candida is a yeast-like fungus.</p>\n<p>Option C: Fusarium is a mold/filamentous fungus.</p>\n<p>Option D: Talaromyces is a dimorphic fungus.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7318","text":"Which among the following ​is not a sexual spore?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not a sexual spore?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zygo​spore","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sporang​iospore","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Basidi​ospore","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Asco​spore","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sporangiospore","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sporangiospore</strong></p><p><strong>Sporangiospore</strong> is not a sexual spore. It is an <strong>asexual spore.</strong></p>\n<p>Fungi can be classified into 4 classes based on sexual spore formation:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Class</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Sexual spore</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Asexual spore</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Glomerulomycetes</td>\n<td>Oospores, Zygospores</td>\n<td>Sporangiospores</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ascomycetes</td>\n<td>Ascospores</td>\n<td>Conidia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Basidiomycetes</td>\n<td>Basidiospores</td>\n<td>Conidia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fungi imperfecti (Deuteromycetes)</td>\n<td>Yet to be identified</td>\n<td>Conidia</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Fungi imperfecti </strong>(Deuteromycetes)<strong>:</strong> These are the fungi whose sexual phases are yet to be identified. Most of the fungi of medical importance belong to this group.</p>\n<p>Note: The earlier classification of Phycomycetes, under which Zygomycetes, Oomycetes, and other sub-phyla were present, is now obsolete. Phylum Glomerulomycota has replaced it.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4712","text":"Which of the following fun​gal spores are formed by ‍condensation of the hypha‌l elements?","raw_text":"Which of the following fungal spores are formed by condensation of the hyphal elements?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arthro​spores","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Conidi​ospore","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Basidi​ospores","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ascos​pores","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Arthrospores","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f07534dddcfb472bb5106736f5279542x563x383.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Arthrospores</strong></p><p><strong>Arthrospores</strong> are <strong>asexual spores</strong> that are formed along the mycelium/hyphae by segmentation and <strong>condensation of hyphae</strong>.</p>\n<p>Arthrospores are commonly formed by <strong>dermatophytes</strong>, especially those belonging to the genus <em><strong>Trichophyton</strong></em>. They are also formed by dimorphic fungi like<em><strong> Coccidiodes sp</strong></em>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7316","text":"A young patient presents t​o the dermatology OPD wit‍h a history of itchy hypo‌pigmented patches over he‌r chest for 6 months. A K​OH mount is prepared with‌ the skin scraping sample‍, which on microscopy rev​eals a characteristic app​earance as shown in the i‌mage. What is the most pr‍obable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young patient presents to the dermatology OPD with a history of itchy hypopigmented patches over her chest for 6 months. A KOH mount is prepared with the skin scraping sample, which on microscopy reveals a characteristic appearance as shown in the image. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pityria​sis alba","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Candi​diasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tinea c​orporis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pityriasis​ versicolor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pityriasis versicolor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7553a11773ec4d708812d9371fe0c7b0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/21a3f5d5d1934772b732f28d27605bb9x1280x913.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dcb871f7f5854320b77c9e2609ebb09bx1220x1128.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pityriasis versicolor</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture and the KOH mount preparation showing the <strong>spaghetti and meatball</strong> appearance suggest <strong>pityriasis versicolor.</strong></p>\n<p>Pityriasis versicolor<strong> </strong>is a<strong> chronic superficial</strong> <strong>infection</strong> of the stratum corneum caused by <em><strong>Malassezia globosa</strong></em> and <em><strong>Malassezia</strong> <strong>furfur</strong></em><strong>.</strong> The most common site involves the neck, chest, back, and upper arms. The lesions often appear as hypopigmented areas.</p>\n<p>The KOH mount reveals fungal hyphae that are short and thick (like spaghetti or banana) and many variously sized spores (like meatballs or grapes) referred to as spaghetti and meatballs or <strong>bananas and grapes appearance.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment includes topical azoles or terbinafine for 2 weeks.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the <strong>hypopigmented lesions</strong> of P. versicolour over the chest.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Electron microscopic image of <em><strong>Malassezia</strong> <strong>furfur</strong></em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7352","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about dermatophyt‍es is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about dermatophytes is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epidermophyton infects ski​n and hair","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Microsporum infects skin a​nd hair","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epidermophyton infects nai​ls and hair","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Microsporum infects nails ​and hair","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Microsporum infects skin and hair","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Microsporum infects skin and hair</strong></p><p><strong>Microsporum</strong> infects only <strong>skin and hair,</strong> not nails.</p>\n<p><strong>Dermatophytes</strong> include 3 genera:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Microsporum:</strong> It infects only <strong>hair and skin</strong> (not nails). The species included in this genus are-\n<ul>\n<li> <em>M. gypseum</em></li>\n<li><em>M. canis</em></li>\n<li><em>M. nanum</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Epidermophyton: </strong>It infects only <strong>skin and nails</strong> (not hair). <em>E. floccosum</em> is the only species in this genus.</li>\n<li><strong>Trichophyton:</strong> It infects <strong>skin, hair, and nails</strong>. The species falling under this genus are-\n<ul>\n<li><em>T. rubrum</em></li>\n<li><em>T. mentagrophytes</em></li>\n<li><em>T. tonsurans</em></li>\n<li><em>T. schoenleinii</em></li>\n<li><em>T. violaceum</em></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7347","text":"A 23-year-old-lady came to​ the dermatology clinic w‍ith complaints of hair lo‌ss and the presence of fi‌rmly adherent, black, gri​tty, hard nodules along t‌he hair shaft as shown. T‍here is no associated pru​ritis of the scalp. Which​ of the following could b‌e the most probable causa‍tive agent?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old-lady came to the dermatology clinic with complaints of hair loss and the presence of firmly adherent, black, gritty, hard nodules along the hair shaft as shown. There is no associated pruritis of the scalp. Which of the following could be the most probable causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Piedrai​a hortae","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Trichospor​on beigelii","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hortaea ​werneckii","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malassez​ia furfur","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Piedraia hortae","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3974e956b3b747d096d968e0bf10642f.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/70fcb06a51bf420e92549747150fa77fx1280x1086.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/19f2c4e30ea84ca49b405ff619ab9844x1280x1073.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Piedraia hortae</strong></p><p>The presence firmly adherent,<strong> black, gritty, hard nodules</strong> along the hair shaft is suggestive of <strong>black piedra.</strong> The causative agent of this condition is <strong><em>Piedraia hortae</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>Piedra is a superficial fungal infection of the hair shaft. It is of two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Black piedra:</strong> It is characterized by firmly adherent, black, gritty, hard nodules along the hair shaft. <strong>Scalp hair</strong> is most often affected. <strong>Hair</strong> becomes <strong>brittle</strong> and breaks easily leading to complains of hair loss. It is caused by <em><strong>Piedraia hortae</strong></em>.</li>\n<li><strong>White piedra: </strong>It is characterized by <strong>soft, white</strong> or light brown transparent <strong>nodules</strong> that are easily detached from the hair. It affects <strong>pubic, axillary, facial hair</strong> more commonly than scalp hair. It is<strong> </strong>caused by <em><strong>Trichosporon</strong> <strong>beigelii</strong></em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Management involves shaving or cutting off the affected hair along with topical antifungals.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7349","text":"A 45-year-old diabetic pat​ient presented with compl‍aints of discoloration of‌ his toenail for the past‌ 4 months as shown in the​ image below. Which patho‌gen most commonly causes ‍this condition?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old diabetic patient presented with complaints of discoloration of his toenail for the past 4 months as shown in the image below. Which pathogen most commonly causes this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trichophy​ton rubrum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Trichophyton​ schonleinii","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epidermophyt​on floccosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trichophyton mentagrophyte​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Trichophyton rubrum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6ec3ef56c6434f618039685dc3a6c2f9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eeee707c1b094cbe8289da5fd6d1882ax400x300.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6c5cd6abbead419ba34fd41b19845296x567x425.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Trichophyton rubrum</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>onychomycosis </strong>and the pathogen most commonly causing this condition is <strong><em>Trichophyton rubrum.</em> </strong>Diabetes increases the risk of onychomycosis.</p>\n<p>Onychomycosis refers to <strong>nail infections</strong> caused by fungi<strong>.</strong> It can be due to dermatophytes, non-dermatophytic moulds, and candida.<strong> Tinea unguium</strong> refers to nail infections caused by <strong>dermatophytes.</strong> This condition may affect toenails or fingernails, but toenail infections are particularly common. </p>\n<p>The following photomicrograph of the nail segment in KOH shows <em>T. rubrum </em>fungal hyphae invading nail cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following image is of a lactophenol cotton blue(LPCB) mount showing pyriform shaped microconidia on the hyphae of <em>T. rubrum.</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0628","text":"Which of the following org​anisms causes disease fol‍lowing traumatic inoculat‌ion into the skin?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms causes disease following traumatic inoculation into the skin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sporothrix​ schenckii","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Blastomyces ​dermatitidis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coccidioid​es immitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paracoccidioides brasilien​sis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sporothrix schenckii","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2b67a420432a4dbeaf2720ed90d73553x1280x947.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e3407e191ba143c6bdfe5f8cba586780x1280x1725.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sporothrix schenckii</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 684\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Sporothrix schenckii</strong> causes infection followed by <strong>traumatic</strong> <strong>inoculation</strong> into the skin.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>dimorphic fungus</strong> that lives on vegetation such as grasses, trees, rose bushes, etc. Following traumatic inoculation into the skin as in thorn prick, S schenckii causes <strong>sporotrichosis</strong>, a chronic granulomatous infection. This is usually further followed by spread along the draining lymphatics and lymph nodes as shown in the image below.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Subcutaneous mycoses</strong> are usually caused by traumatic inoculation of the causative fungus. These include:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 446.548px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 683\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Subcutaneous mycoses</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.548px;\"><strong>Causative agent/s</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 685\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Sporotrichosis</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.548px;\">Sporothrix schenckii</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">Chromoblastomycosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.548px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 685\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Phialophora verrucosa</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 685\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Fonsecaea pedrosoi</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 685\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Fonsecaea compacta</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 685\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Rhinocladiella aquaspersa</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 685\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Cladophialophora carrionii</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 686\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Phaeohyphomycosis</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.548px;\">\n<p>Exophiala jeanselmei</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Phialophora richardsiae</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Bipolaris spicifera</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Wangiella dermatitidis</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 686\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Mycetoma</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.548px;\">Pseudallescheria boydii (Anamorph Scedosporium apiospermum).\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Madurella mycetomatis</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Madurella grisea</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>E jeanselmei</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Acremonium falciforme</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7340","text":"Which of the following is ​false about the most comm‍only used culture media f‌or growing fungi?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the most commonly used culture media for growing fungi?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contains t​aurocholate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Is a spec​ific media","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Contains cy​cloheximide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Has an a​cidic pH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Contains taurocholate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fba746b4e37448ba80d0ac4bd137a4cbx1020x2415.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Contains taurocholate</strong></p><p><strong>Sabouraud dextrose agar </strong>(SDA) is the most commonly used <strong>culture media</strong> for growing <strong>fungi. </strong>It does not contain taurocholate.</p>\n<p>It consists of <strong>antibiotics</strong> like gentamicin or chloramphenicol to inhibit the contaminant bacteria and also <strong>cycloheximide</strong> to inhibit the growth of saprophytic fungi. It has an <strong>acidic pH</strong> of 5.4.</p>\n<p>Media used in mycology can be classified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Specific media-\n<ul>\n<li>SDA agar</li>\n<li>Czapek-Dox medium</li>\n<li>Birdseed agar</li>\n<li>Cornmeal agar</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Non-specific media-\n<ul>\n<li>Blood agar</li>\n<li>Brain heart infusion agar</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7357","text":"A gardener from the sub-Hi​malayan region presented ‍with multiple ulcerative ‌eruptions along the lymph‌atics of his hand. A tiss​ue biopsy was taken and s‌ent for culture. Microsco‍pic examination of the or​ganism cultured is shown ​in the image. Identify th‌e organism.","raw_text":"A gardener from the sub-Himalayan region presented with multiple ulcerative eruptions along the lymphatics of his hand. A tissue biopsy was taken and sent for culture. Microscopic examination of the organism cultured is shown in the image. Identify the organism.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sporothrix​ schenckii","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Histoplasma​ capsulatum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phialophora​ richardsiae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Exophiala ​jeanselmei","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sporothrix schenckii","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9e5c3eb840b149fb847919391bb8dce0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e191bf34494849a89ac6c819a8f63facx700x471.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sporothrix schenckii</strong></p><p>The presence of multiple ulcerative<strong> eruptions along the lymphatics</strong> in a gardener from the <strong>sub-Himalayan region</strong> and the microscopic finding of <strong>flower-like clusters of conidia</strong> are suggestive of <strong>sporotrichosis</strong>, caused by <em><strong>Sporothrix schenckii.</strong></em> It is a thermally dimorphic fungus that lives on vegetation.</p>\n<p>The lesions occur following <strong>traumatic inoculation </strong>of the fungus.<strong> </strong>A <strong>granulomatous nodule</strong> is first formed at the site of inoculation that may progress to form a necrotic or <strong>ulcerative lesion</strong>. The infection can <strong>spread via lymphatic</strong> channels from the skin and subcutaneous tissue to the lymph nodes resulting in formation of <strong>secondary ulcers</strong> on the <strong>lymph nodes.</strong> This condition is known as <strong>lymphocutaneous sporotrichosis. </strong>This disease is commonly encountered among gardeners, forest workers and manual labourers.</p>\n<p>Clinical presentation of lymphocutaneous sporotrichosis:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7359","text":"A farmer developed multipl​e ulcerative nodules alon‍g the lymphatics followin‌g a rose thorn prick a we‌ek ago. Microscopic findi​ng of the skin biopsy is ‌given below. What is the ‍likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A farmer developed multiple ulcerative nodules along the lymphatics following a rose thorn prick a week ago. Microscopic finding of the skin biopsy is given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cryptoc​occosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sporotr​ichosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Histopl​asmosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Asperg​illosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sporotrichosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/80f3acad21404c749fd3e30b51bee34e.png"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sporotrichosis</strong></p><p>The image shows an<strong> asteroid body</strong> which is characteristically seen in a case of <strong>sporotrichosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Asteroid bodies are formed due to the deposition of the immune complexes around the yeast cell. It consists of a <strong>central</strong> rounded or oval <strong>basophilic</strong>, cigar-shaped <strong>yeast cell</strong> surrounded <strong>peripherally</strong> by <strong>eosinophilic radiations</strong>. Formation of such structures is called the <strong>Splendore-Hoeppli phenomenon.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: Asteroid bodies are also seen in sarcoidosis. Those are irregular, star-shaped inclusions seen in multinucleate giant cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5917","text":"A farmer from the sub-Hima​layan belt had a thorn pr‍ick injury a year back an‌d developed a warty, verr‌ucous growth at the site ​of prick. A skin biopsy s‌ample was taken and sent ‍for histopathological exa​mination. The characteris​tic microscopic body as s‌hown in the image was see‍n. Which of the following​ could be the most likely‌ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A farmer from the sub-Himalayan belt had a thorn prick injury a year back and developed a warty, verrucous growth at the site of prick. A skin biopsy sample was taken and sent for histopathological examination. The characteristic microscopic body as shown in the image was seen. Which of the following could be the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blasto​mycosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pheohyph​omycosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chromobla​stomycosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sporotr​ichosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chromoblastomycosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/090c65be15b64cd189d9a06e8fc8e081.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chromoblastomycosis</strong></p><p>The history of a slowly spreading <strong>warty, verrucous lesion</strong> following a thorn prick injury and the histopathological image showing <strong>medlar bodies</strong> is suggestive of <strong>chromoblastomycosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Chromoblastomycosis (chromomycosis) is a <strong>subcutaneous mycotic infection</strong> that is usually caused by <strong>traumatic inoculation. </strong>Over months to years, the primary lesion becomes verrucous and wart-like with extension along the draining lymphatics. </p>\n<p><strong>Histologically</strong>, the lesions are granulomatous and the <strong>dark sclerotic bodies</strong> may be seen within leukocytes or giant cells called <strong>medlar/copper penny bodies.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 660px; width: 428.43px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 683\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Subcutaneous mycoses</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241.43px;\"><strong>Causative agent/s</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 685\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Sporotrichosis</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241.43px;\">Sporothrix schenckii</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\">Chromoblastomycosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241.43px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 685\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Phialophora verrucosa</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 685\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Fonsecaea pedrosoi</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 685\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Fonsecaea compacta</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 685\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Rhinocladiella aquaspersa</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 685\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Cladophialophora carrionii</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 686\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Phaeohyphomycosis</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241.43px;\">\n<p>Exophiala jeanselmei</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Phialophora richardsiae</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Bipolaris spicifera</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Wangiella dermatitidis</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 686\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Mycetoma</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241.43px;\">Pseudallescheria boydii (Anamorph Scedosporium apiospermum).\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Madurella mycetomatis</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Madurella grisea</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>E jeanselmei</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 687\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Acremonium falciforme</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7388","text":"A middle-aged man from sou​th India presented to the‍ OPD with a swelling in h‌is left foot that is unre‌sponsive to antibiotics. ​On examination, multiple ‌discharging sinuses were ‍noted. The discharge cont​ained brown granules that​ were soft in consistency‌. Which of the following ‍is likely the causative a​gent?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man from south India presented to the OPD with a swelling in his left foot that is unresponsive to antibiotics. On examination, multiple discharging sinuses were noted. The discharge contained brown granules that were soft in consistency. Which of the following is likely the causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Actinomadu​ra madurae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sporothrix​ schenckii","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fonsecaea​ pedrosoi","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Madurella ​mycetomatis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Madurella mycetomatis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eeb1458179984c59946f16f71caabf56.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Madurella mycetomatis</strong></p><p>A swelling with multiple <strong>discharging sinuses</strong> with <strong>brown granules</strong> that is unresponsive to the antibiotics is suggestive of <strong>eumycetoma </strong>caused by <strong><em>Madurella mycetomatis.</em></strong></p>\n<p><strong>Mycetoma</strong> is a <strong>chronic, granulomatous infection</strong> of the skin, subcutaneous tissues, fascia and bone, which most often affects the foot or the hand. It can be divided into <strong>three types</strong>- eumycetomas, actinomycetomas and botryomycosis.</p>\n<p>Infection follows<strong> traumatic inoculation</strong> of the organism into the subcutaneous tissue from soil or vegetable sources. Within host tissues, the organisms develop to <strong>form compacted colonies (grains)</strong> 0.5–2 mm in diameter, the color of which depends on the organism responsible. It results in <strong>tumefactions</strong>, <strong>deformities</strong> and <strong>draining sinuses</strong> discharging these grains or granules.</p>\n<p><strong>Laboratory Diagnosis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A. Direct Examination: The<strong> presence of grains</strong> in pus collected from draining sinuses or in biopsy material is <strong>diagnostic</strong>.</li>\n<li>B. Culture Samples should also be cultured, at both 25–30°C and 37°C, on brain-heart infusion agar or blood agar for actinomycetes and on Sabouraud agar (without cycloheximide) for fungi.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment:</strong></p>\n<p>The management of eumycetoma is difficult, involving <strong>surgical debridement</strong> or excision and chemotherapy. Actinomycetoma on the other hand, responds well to rifampicin in combination with sulfonamides or cotrimoxazole.</p>\n<p>Option A: <em>Actinomadura madurae</em> causes <strong>actinomycetoma</strong> which would respond to antibiotics.</p>\n<p>Option B:<em> Sporothrix schenckii</em> causes <strong>sporotrichosis </strong>which is characterised by lesions along the route of lymphatics.</p>\n<p>Option C: <em>Fonsecaea pedrosoi</em> causes <strong>chromoblastomycosis </strong>which would not have discharging sinuses.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5867","text":"Which of the following is ​not a dimorphic fungus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a dimorphic fungus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sporothrix​ schenckii","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coccidioid​es immitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pneumocysti​s jirovecii","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Talaromyce​s marneffei","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pneumocystis jirovecii","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pneumocystis jirovecii</strong></p><p><strong><em>Pneumocystis jirovecii</em> </strong>is a <strong>yeast-like fungus</strong> and not a dimorphic fungus.</p>\n<p><strong>Dimorphic fungi</strong> exist in the <strong>yeast</strong> form in <strong>body tissues</strong> and cultures maintained at <strong>37°C</strong>, and in the <strong>filamentous form</strong> in cultures at room temperature(<strong>25°C</strong>).</p>\n<p>Examples of dimorphic fungi include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Blastomyces dermatitidis</em></li>\n<li><strong><em>Coccidioides immitis</em></strong></li>\n<li><em>Paracoccidioides brasiliensis</em></li>\n<li><strong><em>Talaromyces marneffei</em></strong></li>\n<li><strong><em>Sporothrix schenckii</em></strong></li>\n<li><em>Histoplasma capsulatum</em></li>\n<li><em>Coccidioides posadasii</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7367","text":"A 54-year-old man had pres​ented to the clinic with ‍a history of chronic coug‌h. His chest X-ray showed‌ a mass in the left lung ​with hilar lymphadenopath‌y. Biopsy of the mass was‍ done and findings are sh​own below. Which of the f​ollowing is the most like‌ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 54-year-old man had  presented to the clinic with a history of chronic cough. His chest X-ray showed a mass in the left lung with hilar lymphadenopathy. Biopsy of the mass was done and findings are shown below. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gilchris​t disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Darling'​s disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Desert r​heumatism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Jorge Lobo​'s disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Darling's disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e80744a1ea2a430fa1e7e33a7ad36e13.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Darling&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and the presence of <strong>tiny yeast forms within macrophages</strong> are suggestive of <strong>Histoplasmosis</strong> which is also called <strong>Darling's disease. </strong>Other names of this condition include Cave's disease and Ohio disease.</p>\n<p>The causative agent is a <strong>dimorphic fungus</strong> called <strong><em>Histoplasma capsulatum</em></strong>. Transmission occurs via <strong>inhalation of conidia.</strong></p>\n<p>After inhalation, the conidia develop into <strong>yeast cells</strong> and are <strong>engulfed by alveolar macrophages</strong>. Within macrophages, the yeasts may <strong>disseminate</strong> to <strong>reticuloendothelial tissues</strong>, such as the liver, spleen, bone marrow, and lymph nodes.</p>\n<p>Biopsy reveals tiny (2-4 microns) oval-shaped yeast cells inside macrophages.</p>\n<p>On <strong>culture</strong> and microscopy, <strong>tuberculate macroconidia</strong> and <strong>hyaline septate</strong> mold with microconidia are seen.</p>\n<p>It is <strong>treated</strong> using <strong>azole</strong> antifungals.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7374","text":"A 7-year-old boy with a re​cent history of travel to‍ South America was brough‌t to the hospital with fe‌ver, enlarged lymph nodes​ and multiple skin lesion‌s on his face. Tissue sam‍ple from the lesion shows​ the following findings u​nder microscopy. Which of‌ the following is the mos‍t likely causative organi​sm?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy with a recent history of travel to South America was brought to the hospital with fever, enlarged lymph nodes and multiple skin lesions on his face. Tissue sample from the lesion shows the following findings under microscopy. Which of the following is the most likely causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coccidioid​es immitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paracoccidioides brasilien​sis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Histoplasma​ capsulatum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blastomyces ​dermatitidis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Paracoccidioides brasiliensis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/aecedb1cfd3d45e79d7b7068a5b70aef.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/594140648fc04d6db564e951306d9252x700x478.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Paracoccidioides brasiliensis</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and the characteristic <strong>pilot's wheel appearance</strong> on culture(image) is suggestive of <strong>paracoccidioidomycosis</strong> caused by <strong><em>Paracoccidioides brasiliensis. </em></strong>It is also called Lutz mycosis/ South American blastomycosis.</p>\n<p><em>Paracoccidioides brasiliensis</em> is a <strong>thermally dimorphic fungus</strong> that causes a <strong>chronic granulomatous disease</strong> affecting the lungs, skin, mucosa, lymph nodes. On culture, yeast form is seen at 37° C and the mycelial form at 25° C.</p>\n<p>Microscopy of tissue sample reveals thick walled, round, yeast cells with numerous buds attached to each other circumferentially giving the classic <strong>mickey mouse</strong> or <strong>pilot wheel appearance</strong>.</p>\n<p>Most infections are asymptomatic and few individuals with acute disease present with <strong>hepatosplenomegaly</strong>, <strong>lymphadenopathy</strong> and <strong>skin lesions.</strong></p>\n<p>The below image shows skin lesions in a child with paracoccidioidomycosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mild/moderate disease: Itraconazole</li>\n<li>Severe disease: Amphotericin B for 2-3weeks followed by Itraconazole. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0286","text":"A 50-year-old male present​s with low-grade fever an‍d a skin lesion as shown ‌below. He had a history o‌f recent travel to North ​America. Chest X-Ray reve‌als consolidation in the ‍right lung with hilar lym​phadenopathy. Culture on ​Sabouraud dextrose agar a‌t 37°C shows thick-walled‍, large (7-20 µ), round y​east cells with a single ‌broad-based bud. What is ​the most probable diagnos‍is?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old male presents with low-grade fever and a skin lesion as shown below.  He had a  history of recent travel to North America.  Chest X-Ray reveals consolidation in the right lung with hilar lymphadenopathy. Culture on Sabouraud dextrose agar at 37°C shows thick-walled, large (7-20 µ), round yeast cells with a single broad-based bud. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blasto​mycosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Histopl​asmosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coccidioi​domycosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paracoccidi​oidomycosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Blastomycosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/64e01a6e9f23429ea0fc0c5c3b1db2c2.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Blastomycosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with <strong>pulmonary and cutaneous involvement</strong> along with a history of travel to<strong> North America </strong>and the characteristic culture findings suggests the diagnosis of<strong> Blastomycosis </strong>caused by <strong>Blastomyces dermitidis</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Blastomycosis</strong> is a chronic infection, characterized by the formation of <strong>suppurative and granulomatous lesions</strong>, especially, in the lungs and skin.</p>\n<div>It is most common in North America and so is also called <strong>North Amerian Blastomycosis.</strong></div>\n<p>The causative organism is a thermally dimorphic fungus called <strong><em>Blastomyces dermatitidis</em></strong>. Humans are infected by the<strong> inhalation of spores</strong> in the soil.</p>\n<p>Blastomycosis can present in <strong>three forms</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Pulmonary disease:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Presents with cough, sputum production, chest pain, and fever</li>\n<li>Hilar lymphadenopathy may be present</li>\n<li>Focal or diffuse <strong>consolidation, miliary lesions, or abscess formation</strong> may occur.</li>\n<li>Resembles TB and Histoplasmosis </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Cutaneous disease:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Involvement of face and exposed parts of the skin</li>\n<li>Skin lesion: ulcerated <strong>verrucous granulomas</strong> with an advancing border and central scarring. The border is filled with microabscesses and has a sharp, sloping edge. (image in stem)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Disseminated disease:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Multiple skin lesions are the most common presentation</li>\n<li>Highly fatal </li>\n<li>Genitourinary tract (especially prostate) is frequently involved</li>\n<li>Bone involvement resembles chronic osteomyelitis.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<div>It can be diagnosed based on culture findings. Characterictic findings include the presence of <strong>thick-walled round yeast cell</strong> with a <strong>single broad-based bud</strong> when cultured at 37°C. (figure of 8 appearance)</div>\n<p><strong>Treatment:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Localised mild/moderate disease -<strong> Itraconazole</strong> (6 months)</li>\n<li>Disseminated/ severe disease - <strong>Amphotericin B</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7384","text":"A middle-aged man residing​ in a rural area presents‍ with psychosis, ataxia a‌nd convulsions. On furthe‌r inquiry, you find out t​here is history of simila‌r complaints in the famil‍y members who recently co​nsumed food prepared with​ grains as shown in the i‌mage. What is the poisoni‍ng is most likely due to?​","raw_text":"A middle-aged man residing in a rural area presents with psychosis, ataxia and convulsions. On further inquiry, you find out there is history of similar complaints in the family members who recently consumed food prepared with grains as shown in the image. What is the poisoning is most likely due to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Claviceps​ purpurea","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aspergill​us flavus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amanita p​halloides","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aspergillu​s fumigatus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Claviceps purpurea","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/66d2a6423ed8495ea63b4bba7785c23d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Claviceps purpurea</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette and the presence of dark coloured ergot growing on the wheat as shown in the image are suggestive of ergotism due to <em><strong>Claviceps purpurea</strong></em></p>\n<p><strong>Mycotic poisoning</strong> is of <strong>two types</strong>: Mycetism and Mycotoxicosis</p>\n<p><strong>Mycetism</strong>:</p>\n<p>The <strong>fungus</strong> itself causes <strong>toxic effects.</strong></p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Claviceps → Ergot poisoning</li>\n<li>Coprine → coprine poisoning</li>\n<li>Inocybe → muscarine poisoning</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Mycotoxicosis:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Fungal toxins contaminate</strong> food and cause toxic effects.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aflatoxins</strong>: These are a type of mycotoxins produced by <em><strong>Aspergillus</strong></em> species of fungi, such as <em>Aspergillus flavus</em> and <em>Aspergillus parasiticus.</em></li>\n<li>Fusarium toxins</li>\n<li>Citrinin toxin was first isolated from <em>Penicillium citrinum.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8945","text":"The below given image show​s result of hair perforat‍ion test. The organism li‌kely to show below given ‌appearance is","raw_text":"The below given image shows result of hair perforation test. The organism likely to show below given appearance is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trichophy​ton rubrum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trichophyto​n violaceum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trichophyton​ schoenleinii","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trichophyton mentagrophyte​s","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Trichophyton mentagrophytes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b375aa6d1a3a41589f07eb3a163c2076.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Trichophyton mentagrophytes</strong></p><p>Image shows <strong>surface erosion of shaft</strong> caused by <strong>Trichophyton mentagrophytes.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Hair perforation test:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Done to differentiate Trichophyton rubrum from mentagrophytes.</strong></li>\n<li>Few strands of human hair are placed in a petridish with 20 ml of distilled water and autoclaved.</li>\n<li>Few drops of sterile 10% yeast extract and a few fragments of the test fungus are added to the hair strands.</li>\n<li>Incubated at 25<sup>0</sup>C for 2-3 weeks.</li>\n<li>Hair is removed and examined microscopically in a lactophenol cotton blue stained wet preparation.</li>\n<li><strong>T.mentagrophytes causes surface erosion of the shaft resulting in wedge shaped appearance of the shaft.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"9TZRIJ1r","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"28_rhesus_isoimmunization","title":"28.Rhesus Isoimmunization","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB1399","text":"The Rhesus blood group ant‌igen complex is located o‍n which chromosome?","raw_text":"The Rhesus blood group antigen complex is located on which chromosome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The genetic locus for the <strong>Rh antigen complex </strong>is on the <strong>short arm </strong>of <strong>chromosome 1.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>RhCcEe</strong> and <strong>RhD</strong> are the two distinct genes located within the Rh locus. The presence of D antigen on one or both chromosomes 1, determines heterozygosity or homozygosity respectively.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1400","text":"Antigens of which of the f‌ollowing blood group syst‍ems do not cause alloimmu​nization in a newborn/fet‌us?","raw_text":"Antigens of which of the following blood group systems do not cause alloimmunization in a newborn/fetus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ki‌dd","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M‌NS","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ke‌ll","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Le‌wis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lewis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lewis</strong></p><p>The Lewis antigens do not cause alloimmunization or hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn.</p>\n<p><strong>Alloimmunization</strong>/isoimmunisation is defined as an <strong>immune response</strong> to <strong>foreign antigens</strong> after <strong>exposure to genetically different</strong> cells or tissues.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Kell blood</strong> group system is complex and contains many antigens that are highly immunogenic. These antigens are the third most potent, after those of the ABO and Rh blood groups, at triggering an immune reaction. <strong>Anti-Kell</strong> causes <strong>severe fetal anemia</strong> by suppressing fetal RBC synthesis. </p>\n<p><strong>MNS</strong> and <strong>Kidd</strong> blood types cause alloimmunization. Hemolytic disease of newborn is mild with Kidd whereas MNS causes potentially severe anemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1404","text":"A pregnant Rh-negative wom‌an presents for her regul‍ar antenatal visit. Her I​ndirect Coombs test is ne‌gative. When would you gi‍ve her prophylactic Anti-‌D?","raw_text":"A pregnant Rh-negative woman presents for her regular antenatal visit. Her Indirect Coombs test is negative. When would you give her prophylactic  Anti-D?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"28 w‌eeks","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"24 w‌eeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"28 weeks and then monthly ‌thereafter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"24 weeks and then monthly ‌thereafter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 28 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 28 weeks</strong></p><p>In a <strong>non-immunized Rh-negative primigravida</strong>, Anti D is given at <strong>28</strong> <strong>weeks of gestation. </strong>It is also given <strong>within</strong> <strong>72 hours</strong> of <strong>delivery.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Routine postpartum administration</strong> of anti-D immune globulin to at-risk pregnancies within 72 hours of delivery decreases the alloimmunization rate by 90 percent.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 330\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Additionally, the provision of Anti-D immunoglobulin at <strong>28 weeks </strong>of gestation reduces the third-trimester alloimmunization rate from approximately 2 percent to 0.1 percent.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1407","text":"Which of the following eve‌nts in Rh-negative patien‍ts does not require Anti-​D prophylaxis?","raw_text":"Which of the following events in Rh-negative patients does not require Anti-D prophylaxis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Misca‌rriage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chorionic vi‌lli sampling","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amnioc‌entesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Postpartum‌ hemorrhage","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Postpartum hemorrhage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Postpartum hemorrhage</strong></p><p>Postpartum hemorrhage is not an indication of Anti-D prophylaxis.</p>\n<p><strong>Anti-D prophylaxis</strong> is administered to Rh-negative patients in the following circumstances:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pregnancy loss due to:\n<ul>\n<li>Ectopic pregnancy</li>\n<li>Spontaneous <strong>abortion</strong>/hydatiform mole</li>\n<li>Elective abortion</li>\n<li>Fetal death (any trimester)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Procedures:\n<ul>\n<li>Amniocentesis</li>\n<li><strong>Chorionic villus sampling</strong></li>\n<li>Fetal blood sampling</li>\n<li>External cephalic version</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Delivery</li>\n<li>Trauma</li>\n<li>Placental abruption</li>\n<li>Unexplained vaginal bleeding during pregnancy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1406","text":"Chorionic villus sampling ‌was performed on a G2P1 R‍h-negative woman at 10 we​eks of gestation. What is‌ the dose of Anti-D immun‍oglobulin that should be ‌administered?","raw_text":"Chorionic villus sampling was performed on a G2P1 Rh-negative woman at 10 weeks of gestation. What is the dose of Anti-D immunoglobulin that should be administered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50 mic‌rogram","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"100 mi‌crogram","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"150 mi‌crogram","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"300 mi‌crogram","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 50 microgram","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 50 microgram</strong></p><p>The dose of <strong>Anti-D immunoglobulin</strong> to be given in<strong> </strong>the<strong> first trimester</strong> is <strong>50 μg</strong>.</p>\n<p>All Rh-negative unsensitized women should receive <strong>50 μg</strong> of Rh-immune globulin IM in the <strong>first trimester </strong>in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Within 72 hours of induced or spontaneous abortion</li>\n<li>Ectopic/molar pregnancy</li>\n<li>Chorionic villus sampling </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Women with a pregnancy<strong> beyond 12 weeks </strong>should receive a full dose of <strong>300 μg</strong>. Generally, a 300 μg dose will protect a woman from fetal hemorrhage of up to 30 mL of fetal whole blood. </p>\n<p>The dose in the <strong>3rd trimester</strong> and <strong>post-delivery</strong> is<strong> 300 μg</strong> or 1500 international units (IU).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1408","text":"An Rh-negative G2P1 mother‌ delivered her Rh-positiv‍e baby by cesarean sectio​n. Kleihaeur Betke test, ‌as shown in the image, wa‍s done. Which of the foll‌owing statements is true ‌regarding the test?","raw_text":"An Rh-negative G2P1 mother delivered her Rh-positive baby by cesarean section. Kleihaeur Betke test, as shown in the image, was done. Which of the following statements is true regarding the test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fetal erythrocytes are the‌ ghost cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The red cell‌s contain HbA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The reagent used is citric‌ acid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is a qual‌itative test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The reagent used is citric acid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/388492b5fda0413e9486d3c24f81d220.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b6b9573c00a481e8f4ce03c998becd7x1280x1428.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The reagent used is citric acid</strong></p><p>The reagent used for the<strong> Kleihauer-Betke</strong> test is <strong>citric acid.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Kleihauer-Betke test</strong> is the most <strong>commonly</strong> used <strong>quantitative test</strong> used to detect <strong>fetomaternal hemorrhage </strong>i.e.,<strong> </strong>the approximate volume of fetal blood entering the maternal circulation. It is also known as the <strong>acid elution test.</strong></p>\n<p>The principle behind it is that fetal erythrocytes contain<strong> hemoglobin F</strong>, which is more <strong>resistant to acid elution</strong> than hemoglobin A.</p>\n<p>After exposure to acid, only fetal hemoglobin remains, such that after staining, the <strong>fetal</strong> erythrocytes appear <strong>red</strong> and <strong>adult</strong> cells appear as <strong>“ghosts.”</strong> Fetal cells are counted and expressed as a percentage of adult cells.</p>\n<p><strong>Flow cytometry</strong> is the best test for the quantification of fetomaternal hemorrhage. It is more sensitive and <strong>accurate</strong> as compared to the Kleihauer-Betke test. </p>\n<p>Flow cytometry can analyze a larger number of cells and is not influenced by maternal levels of fetal hemoglobin and fetal levels of maternal hemoglobin. It utilizes <strong>monoclonal antibodies</strong> to HbF or to the D antigen followed by <strong>quantification by fluorescence</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1409","text":"If the volume of fetomater‌nal hemorrhage is greater‍ than 30 mL whole blood, ​the dose of Rh-immunoglob‌ulin calculated is:","raw_text":"If the volume of fetomaternal hemorrhage is greater than 30 mL whole blood, the dose of Rh-immunoglobulin calculated is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 μg for every 0.5 mL of ‌fetal RBCs","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"10 μg for every 5 mL of fe‌tal RBCs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.5 μg for every 1 mL of f‌etal whole blood","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 μg for every 10 mL of fe‌tal whole blood","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 10 μg for every 0.5 mL of fetal RBCs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 10 μg for every 0.5 mL of fetal RBCs</strong></p><p>If the volume of fetomaternal hemorrhage is <strong>greater than 30 mL</strong> whole blood, the dose of Rh-immune globulin calculated is <strong>10 μg </strong>for every<strong> 0.5 mL </strong>of <strong>fetal RBCs.</strong></p>\n<p>The calculation is done as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>300 micrograms of Anti-D can neutralize <strong>15 ml of fetal RBCs</strong> or <strong>30ml of fetal whole blood.</strong></li>\n<li>50 micrograms of Anti-D can neutralize 2.5 ml of fetal RBCs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> half-life</strong> of anti D is<strong> 16-24</strong> days. It is administered intramuscularly either in the deltoid or in the anterolateral aspect of the thigh.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1410","text":"A 25-year-old Rh-negative ‌primigravida presents to ‍the antenatal clinic at 1​2 weeks of pregnancy. Her‌ husband is found to be R‍h-positive. An Indirect C‌oombs test is also positi‌ve. At what critical tite​r of the test, would the ‌fetus require monitoring ‍for signs of anemia?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old Rh-negative primigravida presents to the antenatal clinic at 12 weeks of pregnancy. Her husband is found to be Rh-positive. An Indirect Coombs test is also positive. At what critical titer of the test, would the fetus require monitoring for signs of anemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌:4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌:8","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1:‌16","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1:‌18","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1:16","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/363e310f8769496fa6d64580fcd0fe8dx1280x3281.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1:16</strong></p><p>The <strong>critical titer</strong> for the indirect Coombs test (ICT ) is <strong>1:16 </strong></p>\n<p>Protocol for management Rh isoimmunisation is as follows:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0702","text":"In modern obstetrics, whic‌h of the following invest‍igations is used for moni​toring the fetus of an Rh‌-negative sensitized moth‍er?","raw_text":"In modern obstetrics, which of the following investigations is used for monitoring the fetus of an Rh-negative sensitized mother?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Biophysic‌al profile","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fetal bloo‌d sampling","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amniospe‌ctometry","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MCA doppler peak systolic ‌velocity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. MCA doppler peak systolic velocity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. MCA doppler peak systolic velocity</strong></p><p><strong>MCA Doppler peak systolic velocity (PSV) </strong>is used for the monitoring of fetuses in Rh-negative sensitized mothers.</p>\n<p>The middle cerebral artery is used as other fetal vessels require a larger insonating angle and confers significant measurement error.</p>\n<p>MCA PSV is enhanced due to increased cardiac output and decreased viscosity of fetal blood, which permits the reliable, noninvasive detection of fetal anemia in cases of blood-group alloimmunization. </p>\n<p>If the MCA PSV <strong>exceeds 1.5 MoM</strong> (multiples of the median) and the gestational age is less than 34 or 35 weeks, fetal blood sampling should be considered. MCA Doppler PSV has replaced amniocentesis as the preferred test for the detection of anemia.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1413","text":"In modified Liley's (Queen‌an) chart, which zone rep‍resents a severely affect​ed fetus?","raw_text":"In modified Liley's (Queenan) chart, which zone represents a severely affected fetus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zon‌e 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zon‌e 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Zon‌e 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Zon‌e 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Zone 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/41a6ef8ddcbb48fcb39a816a2a1f771ex1280x2102.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Zone 3</strong></p><p>A<strong> severely affected fetus</strong> is represented by<strong> zone 3.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 329\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Liley</strong> developed a <strong>chart</strong> of changing<strong> amniotic fluid bilirubin</strong> <strong>levels</strong> (delta OD450) and weeks of <strong>gestation</strong>. Amnionic fluid bilirubin is measured by a spectrophotometer and is demonstrable as a change in optical density absorbance at 450 nm (<strong>ΔOD450</strong>).</p>\n<p>However, middle cerebral artery peak systolic velocity (MCA PCV) is non-invasive and more accurate than optical density at 450 nm, hence Liley’s chart is no longer used in practice.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 329\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Liley’s chart has <strong>three zones </strong>as shown below, delineating the<strong> severity </strong>of rhesus disease.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li>Liley’s zone I (low zone)<strong> </strong>- <strong>mild </strong>disease. </li>\n<li>Liley’s zone II (mid zone) - <strong>fetal anemia</strong>.</li>\n<li>Liley’s zone III (high zone) - <strong>severely affected</strong> <strong>fetus</strong> and fetal<strong> death</strong> is <strong>imminent.</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1411","text":"You are performing a Doppl‌er assessment of a fetal ‍middle cerebral artery. W​hat is the threshold valu‌e of its peak systolic ve‍locity that would prompt ‌you to consider fetal blo‌od sampling?","raw_text":"You are performing a Doppler assessment of a fetal middle cerebral artery.  What is the threshold value of its peak systolic velocity that would prompt you to consider fetal blood sampling?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0‌.5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‌.5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2‌.5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1.5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1.5</strong></p><p>Threshold (<strong>critical value</strong>) of middle cerebral artery (<strong>MCA</strong>)<strong> peak systolic velocity</strong> is <strong>1.5</strong> multiples of the median (<strong>MoM</strong>).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 329\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>If the MCA peak systolic velocity exceeds 1.5 MoM, further evaluation by <strong>fetal blood sampling</strong> is necessary to assess the need for fetal transfusion.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 329\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>anemic fetus shunts blood</strong> preferentially to the <strong>brain</strong> to maintain adequate oxygenation. The velocity increases because of increased cardiac output and decreased blood viscosity.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 329\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 329\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>In many centers, serial measurement of the peak systolic velocity of the fetal middle cerebral artery has replaced amniocentesis for the detection of fetal anemia. This method provided 100 percent sensitivity.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1412","text":"A 32-year-old G4P3 Rh-nega‌tive patient with an anti‍body titer of 1:32 presen​ts to the clinic at 30 we‌eks. A USG reveals featur‍es of fetal edema, ascite‌s, and polyhydramnios. Co‌rdocentesis was done for ​fetal blood sampling. At ‌what hematocrit value is ‍intrauterine blood transf​usion indicated?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old G4P3 Rh-negative patient with an antibody titer of 1:32 presents to the clinic at 30 weeks. A USG reveals features of fetal edema, ascites, and polyhydramnios. Cordocentesis was done for fetal blood sampling. At what hematocrit value is intrauterine blood transfusion indicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt‌;40%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt‌;25%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt‌;30%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt‌;35%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. <30%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. &lt;30%</strong></p><p><strong>Intrauterine transfusion</strong> is done if <strong>fetal haematocrit</strong> is <strong>&lt;30%</strong>.</p>\n<p>The blood for transfusion is usually O negative and cross-matched with the mother's blood. It is highly packed to achieve a hematocrit of 75 -80%.</p>\n<p>Intrauterine transfusions may be done <strong>intraperitoneally </strong>where blood is absorbed through <strong>diaphragmatic lymphatics</strong>. It may also be done <strong>intravascularly</strong> through the<strong> umbilical vein</strong>. The segments of the umbilical vein used are the intrahepatic portion or at its insertion into the placenta. </p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> of <strong>intrauterine transfusion</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fetal demise</li>\n<li>Chorioamnionitis</li>\n<li>Placental abruption</li>\n<li>Pre-term labor</li>\n<li>Bradycardia/tachyarrhythmia</li>\n<li>Umbilical cord hematoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1403","text":"A patient presented for an‌ antenatal checkup at 25 ‍weeks of gestation. She h​ad bilateral pitting peda‌l edema and a history of ‍mild headaches. While upd‌ating her antenatal card,‌ a weight gain of 13 kgs ​in the past 2 weeks was n‌oted. USG revealed gross ‍fetal hydrops and a hyper​echoic placenta. Which of‌ the following is not tru​e about the patient's con​dition?","raw_text":"A patient presented for an antenatal checkup at 25 weeks of gestation. She had bilateral pitting pedal edema and a history of mild headaches. While updating her antenatal card, a weight gain of 13 kgs in the past 2 weeks was noted. USG revealed gross fetal hydrops and a hyperechoic placenta. Which of the following is not true about the patient's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is also known as Ballan‌tyne syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hydrops could be due to rh‌esus isoimmunization","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is associated with elev‌ated liver enzymes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a not a form of pre-‌eclampsia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is a not a form of pre-eclampsia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is a not a form of pre-eclampsia</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>maternal</strong> <strong>mirror syndrome/Ballantyne syndrome. </strong>It may be considered a form of <strong>severe pre-eclampsia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Maternal mirror syndrome is the association between <strong>fetal hydrops</strong> and the development of <strong>maternal edema.</strong> The syndrome is named as such because the<strong> mother mirrors the fetus</strong>. It is characterized by <strong>triple edema</strong> i.e., the <strong>fetus, mother</strong>, and<strong> placenta</strong> become <strong>edematous</strong>.</p>\n<p>The pathogenesis of mirror syndrome, although not well established, <strong>mimics</strong> trophoblastic damage and maternal vascular endothelial dysfunction, as is also seen in <strong>pre-eclampsia</strong>. Hence, these two conditions may have a <strong>similar clinical presentation</strong>,<strong> </strong>with common features such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Edema</li>\n<li><strong>Hypertension</strong></li>\n<li>Proteinuria</li>\n<li>Liver enzyme elevation</li>\n<li>Headache and visual disturbances.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>However, i<span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">n <strong>contrast</strong> to preeclampsia, the following features are noted in maternal mirror syndrome:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\"> Low maternal hematocrit</span><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\"> i.e., <strong>hemodilution</strong> rather than hemoconcentration</span></li>\n<li><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\"><strong>Polyhydramnios</strong> rather than oligohydramnios</span></li>\n<li><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">The fetus always </span><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">shows signs of hydrops.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The etiology of fetal hydrops is not related to the development of mirror syndrome. Hydrops could be due to <strong>rhesus isoimmunization</strong>, twin-twin transfusion syndrome, congenital infections, fetal cystic hygroma, Ebstein anomaly, sacrococcygeal teratoma, vein of Galen aneurysms, etc.</p>\n<div class=\"d9FyLd XcVN5d\">In most cases with mirror syndrome, <strong>prompt delivery</strong> is indicated, <span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">because </span><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">women with severe features of preeclampsia can deteriorate rapidly</span>. In few cases, identification and treatment of the cause of fetal hydrops resulted in the resolution of symptoms for both the mother and baby for the remainder of the pregnancy.</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1401","text":"An ultrasound was done on ‌a pregnant Rh-negative pa‍tient to screen for hydro​ps fetalis. What is the e‌arliest sign that may be ‍seen on USG?","raw_text":"An ultrasound was done on a pregnant Rh-negative patient  to screen for hydrops fetalis. What is the earliest sign that may be seen on USG?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scalp‌ edema","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pleural ‌effusion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pericardia‌l effusion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Asc‌ites","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Scalp edema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fb48b0e1582e4815a4737a94b88f5670x416x310.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Scalp edema</strong></p><p>The earliest sign of hydrops fetalis on a USG is<strong> scalp edema</strong>. Subcutaneous scalp edema is also known as the <strong>Halo sign</strong>. </p>\n<p>Other USG signs include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ascites</li>\n<li>Pleural effusion </li>\n<li>Pericardial effusion </li>\n<li>Typical position termed as <strong>Buddha's position  </strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows skin thickening at the level of the fetal skull, on ultrasonography:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1402","text":"An Rh-negative patient who‌ did not have access to a‍ntenatal care delivered a​ stillborn fetus as shown‌ in the image. Which of t‍he following is not assoc‌iated with the fetal cond‌ition?","raw_text":"An Rh-negative patient who did not have access to antenatal care delivered a stillborn fetus as shown in the image. Which of the following is not associated with the fetal condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oligohy‌dramnios","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Extramedullary hematopoesi‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac‌ failure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatospl‌enomegaly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Oligohydramnios","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4aac9f4064134c1782922199e8dce166.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/05002158caf84062ac4bd4007257320ax512x360.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Oligohydramnios</strong></p><p>The pale, oedematous fetus in the image is suggestive of <strong>fetal hydrops</strong>. Fetal hydrops is associated with <strong>polyhydramnios,</strong> not oligohydramnios. </p>\n<p>It often occurs as an effect of iso-immunization in an Rh-negative pregnancy. Other complications that may occur are <strong>fetal anemia</strong> and <strong>hyperbilirubinemia.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Fetal</strong> <strong>anemia</strong> occurs in the <strong>initial</strong> stage of iso-immunization. It is associated with <strong>polyhydramnios</strong> and increased <strong>placental thickening</strong> (hyper-placentosis). An<strong> increase</strong> in the <strong>right atrium size</strong> of the fetus is seen.</p>\n<p><strong>Fetal hydrops</strong> is usually accompanied by<strong> severe anemia</strong>. It results in <strong>stillbirth</strong> or fetal demise soon after birth. It is also associated with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Congestive cardiac failure </li>\n<li>Ascites </li>\n<li>Pleural effusion </li>\n<li>Pericardial effusion </li>\n<li>Hepatosplenomegaly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"OphxdxAj","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"28_instruments_in_ophthalmology","title":"28.Instruments In Ophthalmology","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA8557","text":"A 50-year-old woman comes ​to the out-patient with c​omplaints of diminished v​ision along with floaters​ and flashes. The ophthal‌mologist uses the followi‍ng instrument to assess t‌he retinal lesions. Which‌ of the following stateme​nts is true regarding the‌ ophthalmoscopy done usin​g the instrument shown in‍ the image below?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman comes to the out-patient with complaints of diminished vision along with floaters and flashes. The ophthalmologist uses the following instrument to assess the retinal lesions. Which of the following statements is true regarding the ophthalmoscopy done using the instrument shown in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Area of field in focus is ​2 disc diameters.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The image produced is real​ and inverted.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Condensing lens is not req​uired.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stereopsis​ is absent.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The image produced is real and inverted.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ce75b266b8fd4162bb9300f50d849ff2.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The image produced is real and inverted.</strong></p><p>The instrument shown in the above image is an <strong>indirect</strong> <strong>ophthalmoscope</strong>, and the procedure is known as indirect ophthalmoscopy.</p>\n<p>An indirect<strong> ophthalmoscope</strong> produces an <strong>inverted</strong>, or reversed, <strong>real</strong> <strong>image</strong> of 2 to 5 times magnification.</p>\n<p>The area of field in focus is about 8 disc diameters.</p>\n<p>It is a binocular, self-illuminated device where the <strong>condensing</strong> <strong>lens</strong> is usually <strong>required,</strong> and <strong>stereopsis</strong> is <strong>present</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2077","text":"Which of the following is ​a feature of indirect oph​thalmoscopy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a feature of indirect ophthalmoscopy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"15 times ma​gnification","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Image is erect and virtual​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Binocula​r vision","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Smaller fie​ld of focus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Binocular vision","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Binocular vision</strong></p><p>Indirect ophthalmoscope needs a <strong>binocular vision.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1047","text":"On entering the Ophthalmol​ogy OPD for the first tim​e, you notice multiple pa​tients being examined usi​ng the following instrume‌nt. What is this instrume‍nt?","raw_text":"On entering the Ophthalmology OPD for the first time, you notice multiple patients being examined using the following instrument. What is this instrument?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ophthal​moscope","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Refrac​tometer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kerat​ometer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Slit​ lamp","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Slit lamp","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e477010318d44a77b674c432e71c6aa9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ced47da87e38461886c38ccdc63d8fb6x1280x1917.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/af7ae045ea7a44d2af025843a3eca7a8x1024x802.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1fc8d0b380934d0b8c97b237cd93a93ex1280x1269.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Slit lamp</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>slit lamp</strong>. Slit-lamp biomicroscopy is the <strong>ideal </strong>method to visualize the fundus, especially for observing the optic disc changes seen in glaucoma.</p>\n<p>The lenses used are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Contact type:</strong> Goldmann three-mirror contact lens, modified Koeppe lens</li>\n<li><strong>Non-contact type:</strong> Convex lens à+60D, +78D, +90D (best lens)</li>\n<li><strong>Hruby lens: </strong>planoconcave lens with dioptric power of -58.6 D.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: The image given below shows a refractometer.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: The image given below shows a keratometer</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: The image given below shows an ophthalmoscope</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2050","text":"Which of the following ins​truments can you use to m​easure corneal thickness?​","raw_text":"Which of the following instruments can you use to measure corneal thickness?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kerat​ometer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pachy​meter","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Specular ​microscopy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ophthal​mometer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pachymeter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/abfa0a4b23934c62a7a4c2db8199dc3cx362x406.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f7651f1101974d02ac5918c5a62c7eb2x452x452.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aae445b547b34ffaaa1223cfddedee4bx533x468.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c71f0a0ec10d46d5b26c40fc2a84b68dx974x472.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pachymeter</strong></p><p>The <strong>pachymeter</strong> is used to <strong>measure</strong> <strong>corneal thickness.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Options A and D: A <strong>keratometer (ophthalmometer)</strong> measures the curvature of the anterior surface of the cornea at two points about 1.25 mm on either side of its center.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Specular microscopy</strong> is the study of the <strong>corneal endothelium</strong> under a very high magnification (100 times greater than slit lamp biomicroscopy). Endothelium can be analyzed for cellular size, shape, density, and distribution. The healthy endothelial cell is a regular hexagon, and the normal cell density in a young adult is about 3000 cells/mm<sup>2</sup>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2032","text":"In which surgery would you​ use the instrument shown​ below?","raw_text":"In which surgery would you use the instrument shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cata​ract","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Glau​coma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lid su​rgeries","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Corneal t​ransplant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cataract","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/56ce7cc35a7744aaaa495b3b25864de7.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cataract</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>Ziegler knife</strong>, which is used to open the lens capsule and aspirate the lens material in <strong>congenital </strong><strong>cataract surgeries</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5765","text":"All of the following are t​rue about the instrument ​shown in the image given ​below except-","raw_text":"All of the following are true about the instrument shown in the image given below except-","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is used for the localiz​ation of a foreign body.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is used for the measure​ment of the angle of the ​posterior chamber.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is used for the demonst​ration of the extent of p​eripheral anterior synech​iae.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It provides a mirror image​ of the opposite angle.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is used for the measurement of the angle of the posterior chamber.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/584327ddf1d3467a8948a62a757b5fad.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is used for the measurement of the angle of the posterior chamber.</strong></p><p>The given instrument is an indirect <strong>gonioscope,</strong> which is used to <strong>visualize the angle of the anterior chamber, i.e., for gonioscopy, </strong>and<strong> not</strong> the<strong> angle of the posterior chamber.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Types:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Indirect</strong> <strong>gonioprism-</strong> provides<strong> mirror</strong> image of the <strong>opposite angle.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Direct</strong> <strong>goniolens-</strong> provides<strong> a direct view </strong>of the<strong> angle</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Application:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>To view the angle of the anterior chamber</li>\n<li>Localization of the foreign body, abnormal blood vessels, or tumors in the angle</li>\n<li>Demonstration of the extent of peripheral anterior synechiae</li>\n<li>Direct goniolens can be used during goniotomy</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1041","text":"A 50-year-old male present​ed with progressive blurr​ed vision, photophobia, a​nd eye pain. On examinati​on, there was conjunctiva‌l redness, a mid-dilated ‍pupil, and a shallow ante‌rior chamber in the right‌ eye. He has been treated​ both medically and surgi‌cally for the condition b​ut showed no improvement.‍ The following procedure ‌incorporates a device to ​aid drainage. Identify th​e device.","raw_text":"A 50-year-old male presented with progressive blurred vision, photophobia, and eye pain. On examination, there was conjunctival redness, a mid-dilated pupil, and a shallow anterior chamber in the right eye. He has been treated both medically and surgically for the condition but showed no improvement. The following procedure incorporates a device to aid drainage. Identify the device.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ex-Press​™ implant","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ahmed glau​coma valve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Molteno​ implant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Baerveld​t implant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ex-Press™ implant","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2299790c723c44bd96aa842fc91ff50f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ex-Press™ implant</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The clinical vignette points to a diagnosis of glaucoma not responsive to medical treatment and trabeculectomy and the device used is an <strong>ex-Press™ glaucoma implant</strong> (mini shunt).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is a form of <strong>minimally invasive glaucoma surgery</strong> (MIGS) to increase aqueous drainage.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 359\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Ex-PRESS glaucoma implant is a small <strong>stainless steel</strong> device. It is inserted under a scleral flap during a modified trabeculectomy. Complications such as hypotony are lower than with standard trabeculectomy. </p>\n<p><strong>Glaucoma drainage devices</strong> are devices that allow aqueous outflow by establishing communication between the anterior chamber and sub-Tennon's space/sub-conjunctival space. While these devices were traditionally reserved for high-risk patients, or after trabeculectomy had failed, they are becoming increasingly popular. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The commonly used devices are as follows:</p>\n<p>1. Shunts using episcleral explants</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Ahmed glaucoma valve: Silicone</span></li>\n<li>Molteno implant - Polypropylene plate and silicone plate</li>\n<li>Baerveldt implant - Silicone </li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">2. Mini-shunts </p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Ex-Press™ Mini-Shunt: Stainless steel</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">iStent® - Titanium</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1049","text":"During a visit to the opht​halmology department, you​ see the following test b​eing performed for visual​ field charting of a pati‌ent. Identify the instrum‍ent used from the options‌ given below.","raw_text":"During a visit to the ophthalmology department, you see the following test being performed for visual field charting of a patient. Identify the instrument used from the options given below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oct​opus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Field ​Master","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Humphrey Fi​eld Analyzer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lister’s ​perimeter","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lister’s perimeter","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/51b939a9d1c34d3a929053da802fffe9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lister’s perimeter</strong></p><p>The image given above shows a <strong>Lister's perimeter</strong>. It is used in <strong>manual</strong> <strong>p</strong><strong>erimetry</strong> for visual field charting</p>\n<p>Classification:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Manual:</strong> Confrontation, Listers, Campimetry, Goldmann perimeter</li>\n<li><strong>Automated:</strong> Octopus, Field Master, Humphrey Field Analyzer</li>\n<li><strong>Static: </strong>Friedman, Automated</li>\n<li><strong>Kinetic:</strong> Confrontation, Listers, Scotometry, Goldmann</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>best type</strong> is <strong>automated</strong> perimetry.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1046","text":"Which among the following ​would you use for the mea​surement of intraocular p​ressure in a patient with​ a scarred cornea?","raw_text":"Which among the following would you use for the measurement of intraocular pressure in a patient with a scarred cornea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gold​mann","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Per​kins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ton​open","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pul​sair","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tonopen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1ee733e15d5648dea215b2d0f85c277dx1280x2521.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tonopen</strong></p><p><strong>Tonopen</strong> is used for measuring intraocular pressure (IOP) in a <strong>scarred cornea</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a hand-held electronic contact tonometer (<strong>a modified version of the older Mackay–Marg tonometer</strong>). Besides portability, its main advantage is the ability to measure IOP reasonably accurately in eyes with <strong>distorted or edematous corneas </strong>and <strong>through a soft contact lens</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Types of tonometry:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Indentation tonometry- Reading depends on scleral rigidity, e.g., Schiotz tonometer  </li>\n<li>Applanation tonometry- Based on the amount of force needed to flatten or applanate a known area of the cornea, Imbert–Fick principle. This is further divided into two types -\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fixed force</strong>: IOP is proportional to the area, e.g., Maklakov, Barraquer</li>\n<li><strong>Fixed area</strong> and variable force: IOP is proportional to force. The <strong>most reliable is Goldmann.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Self-measurement: Rebound</li>\n<li>Non-contact type: Pulse Air, Air-Puff</li>\n<li>Irregular cornea:<strong> </strong>Tonopen, MacKay–Marg</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Recent tonometers:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pascals tonometer (dynamic contour tonometry [DCT]): Contact; used for post-LASIK IOP measurement.</li>\n<li>Reichert Ocular Response Analyzer<sup>®</sup>: Non-contact</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong><strong>Tonography</strong> is a non-invasive technique for determining the <strong>facility of aqueous outflow</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows fluorescein-stained semicircular mires.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1003","text":"A first-year ophthalmology​ resident is asked to cou​nsel the parents of a 1-m​onth-old infant diagnosed​ with a lamellar congenit‌al cataract in both eyes.‍ He discusses the treatme‌nt options and mentions t‌hat the following lens wi​ll be used in the surgery‌. This lens is a ________​__","raw_text":"A first-year ophthalmology resident is asked to counsel the parents of a 1-month-old infant diagnosed with a lamellar congenital cataract in both eyes. He discusses the treatment options and mentions that the following lens will be used in the surgery. This lens is a __________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior chamber intra-ocu​lar lens","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior chamber intra oc​ular lens (modified C-loo​p type)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Singh and Worst's iris cla​w lens","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior chamber intra oc​ular lens (four loop type​)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posterior chamber intra ocular lens (modified C-loop type)","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0738f21cb3584ed3bd7afd70100ce26d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7a85882d36f7465e8aee6ce8e13b2a17x1280x2276.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posterior chamber intra ocular lens (modified C-loop type)</strong></p><p><strong>Cataract surgery </strong>with<strong> </strong>an intraocular lens <strong>(IOL) implantation </strong>is the treatment of choice in congenital cataracts. The above image depicts a <strong>modified C-loop type</strong> of posterior chamber intraocular lens <strong>(PCIOL) </strong>used in such cases.</p>\n<p>This surgery involves an <strong>extracapsular cataract extraction (ECCE)</strong> with <strong>primary posterior</strong> <strong>capsulorhexis</strong> and <strong>anterior vitrectomy. </strong>After removal of the cataractous lens, a posterior chamber intraocular lens (PCIOL) is implanted.</p>\n<p>PCIOL rest entirely <strong>behind the iris</strong>. They may be supported and fixed as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Capsular bag fixation of PCIOL (most ideal method)</li>\n<li>Ciliary sulcus fixation</li>\n<li>Sclera fixation</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Parts</strong> of the IOL:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The central part overlying the optic axis is called the <strong>optic</strong>.</li>\n<li>The peripheral arms used for placement and stabilization are the <strong>haptics</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> The image given below shows different types of intraocular lens.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1001","text":"As an intern posted in the​ department, you are assi​sting a senior surgical o​phthalmologist for catara​ct surgery on a 72-year-o‌ld man. You notice the nu‍rsing head pass him the f‌ollowing lens for implant‌ation. This is a ________​____________","raw_text":"As an intern posted in the department, you are assisting a senior surgical ophthalmologist for cataract surgery on a 72-year-old man. You notice the nursing head pass him the following lens for implantation. This is a ____________________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior chamber intra-ocu​lar lens (ACIOL)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior chamber intra oc​ular lens (modified C-loo​p type)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Singh and Worst's Iris cla​w lens","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior chamber intra oc​ular lens (four loop type​)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anterior chamber intra-ocular lens (ACIOL)","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/abfd0597e04b4caea45eb6daeb07e02c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/33f8a1433ddf4bb099c1ec2b4b513f5cx1280x2276.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anterior chamber intra-ocular lens (ACIOL)</strong></p><p>The image depicts <strong>Kelman multiflex </strong>type of anterior chamber intraocular lens (<strong>ACIOL</strong>). It is the most commonly used ACIOL.</p>\n<p><strong>Anterior chamber IOLs </strong>lie entirely in <strong>front of the iris</strong> and are supported in the <strong>angle of the anterior chamber. </strong>IOL implantation is the method of choice for correcting <strong>aphakia.</strong></p>\n<p>These are associated with a higher incidence of <strong>bullous keratopathy.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows different types of intraocular lens.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2058","text":"Identify the given instrum​ent.","raw_text":"Identify the given instrument.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maddox tan​gent scale","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maddo​x wing","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Synopt​ophore","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retin​oscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Maddox wing","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f5cf8c9604754e9db34b26e0b1f356ac.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Maddox wing</strong></p><p>The given image depicts a <strong>Maddox wing</strong>. It is an instrument by which the amount of <strong>heterophoria for near vision</strong> can be measured.</p>\n<p><strong>Methods to examine heterophoria:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Testing for refractive error</li>\n<li>Cover–uncover test</li>\n<li>Prism bar cover test (PBCT)</li>\n<li>Maddox rod test</li>\n<li>Maddox wing test</li>\n<li>Maddox tangent scale: heterophoria for distance</li>\n<li>Measurement of the near point of convergence: Using RAF rule (Normally, it is 70 mm)</li>\n<li>Measurement of fusional reserve- done with a synoptophore or prism bar</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2010","text":"You would identify the ins​trument shown in the imag​e below as:","raw_text":"You would identify the instrument shown in the image below as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nagel’s an​omaloscope","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Jackson’s cr​oss cylinder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Maddo​x rod","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retin​oscope","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Jackson’s cross cylinder","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2852363b09374ea2936e3b151e85ecff.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eef924355490460a8935762137632411x310x210.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2b49efb09e644acf8fbc80f860d08f16x600x695.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/827d95fb1345404bb7bec09898fd65ccx600x706.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Jackson’s cross cylinder</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>Jackson’s cross cylinder (flip-cross technique) </strong>which is used to refine the <strong>power and axis of the cylindrical lens.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Nagel’s anomaloscope is used to test color vision.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: Maddox rod consists of parallel red or colorless cylinders stacked together. Maddox rod test is a macular function test used to measure the degree of heterotropia or heterophoria in cases of torsional diplopia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: Retinoscope</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7499","text":"Identify the​ instrument.","raw_text":"Identify the instrument.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maddo​x wing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maddox​ glass","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maddo​x rod","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phor​opter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Maddox rod","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e8c655f99db94fa5a11033bf06a705b0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4a7b036a4fb4427784ad623020d5641ex1280x1103.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1477e0b1a03549fa98d85a3c4049a8fdx1280x1471.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/badc687c600047f6b432e3c34472d32bx1280x2324.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fbeee932a8e242bd9714fb6163e74196x1280x2108.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Maddox rod</strong></p><p>The above instrument is a Maddox rod.</p>\n<p><strong>Maddox Rod Test</strong></p>\n<p>A Maddox rod is a specially constructed device, consisting of a series of parallel cylinders, that converts a point source of light into a line image. It can be used to test for horizontal and vertical deviations, and when used in conjunction with another Maddox rod, for cyclo deviations.</p>\n<p>A patient is asked to fix on a point light in the center of Maddox tangent scale placed 6 meters away. A Maddox rod is placed in front of one eye with the axis of the rod parallel to the axis of deviation. The patient will see a point light with one eye and a red line with the other. Due to dissimilar images of the two eyes, fusion is broken and heterophoria becomes manifest. The number on the Maddox tangent scale where the red line falls will be the amount of heterophoria in degrees.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5309","text":"You can use a stenopaeic s​lit for all of the follow​ing except ____________.","raw_text":"You can use a stenopaeic slit for all of the following except ____________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fincham​’s test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corneal ​tattooing","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Optical I​ridectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"To find out the axis in as​tigmatism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Corneal tattooing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/85cfd33548674705811a110ecfb0081bx719x430.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Corneal tattooing</strong></p><p>Stenopaeic slit is not used for <strong>corneal tattooing</strong>.</p>\n<p>Stenopaeic slit is an <strong>ophthalmological instrument</strong> that is used in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Fincham’s test</strong> (Option A) : This test is used to <strong>differentiate</strong> between the <strong>colored halos</strong> due to <strong>immature cataract</strong> and corneal abnormalities such as <strong>corneal edema</strong> due to <strong>glaucoma</strong>. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To find out the <strong>axis </strong>in<strong> astigmatism</strong> (option D) for prescribing the <strong>correction glasses.</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To find out the optimal site for <strong>optical iridectomy</strong> (option C) for treating cases of central macular or leucomatous <strong>corneal opacities.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Corneal tattooing</strong> was done mainly in the past for <strong>cosmetic purposes</strong>. It is commonly done for firm scars of corneal opacities in a eye <strong>without</strong> any <strong>useful vision</strong>.</p>\n<p>The below given image is of <strong>stenopaeic slit</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6785","text":"What is the investigation ​shown in the image below?​","raw_text":"What is the investigation shown in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ultrasound B​iomicroscopy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Optical Coherence Tomograp​hy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Corneal T​opography","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spectr​ometry","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Optical Coherence Tomography","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4faf9a7904084affb94a2f747476484b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Optical Coherence Tomography</strong></p><p>The given image depicts<strong> Optical Coherence Tomography (OCT)</strong> of a normal macula.</p>\n<p>It is a non-invasive, noncontact imaging system providing high-resolution <strong>cross-sectional images of the posterior segment</strong>. It uses near-<strong>infrared </strong>light interferometry to do so.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6786","text":"What is the principle of t​he test in which the pati​ent is asked to read the ​chart shown in the image ​below?","raw_text":"What is the principle of the test in which the patient is asked to read the chart shown in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spherical ​aberration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chromatic ​aberration","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chromatic ​projection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diplopic ​projection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chromatic aberration","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f755be9fcec44fd08bd1440e550f648f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chromatic aberration</strong></p><p>The given image depicts a <strong>duo-chrome test</strong> based on<strong> chromatic aberration.</strong> It is used to refine the final sphere in subjective refraction.</p>\n<p>The patient is asked to compare the clarity of the letters on the green and the red side. If a myopic sees the letters of the green side clearer, he is overcorrected. So, +0.25 D sphere is added or -0.25D is removed. However, if a myopic sees the letters on the red side clearer, he is undercorrected and -0.25 D sphere is added. With optimal spherical correction, the letters on the red and green halves of the chart appear equally clear.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6787","text":"Which optical instrument p​rovides an inverted and r​eversed image of the reti​na?","raw_text":"Which optical instrument provides an inverted and reversed image of the retina?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Direct oph​thalmoscope","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Indirect op​hthalmoscope","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hruby lens a​nd slit lamp","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Goldmann contact lens and ​slit lamp","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Indirect ophthalmoscope","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Indirect ophthalmoscope</strong></p><p>An<strong> indirect ophthalmoscope</strong> produces a real, inverted and laterally reversed image of the fundus.</p>\n<p>All the other instruments mentioned including direct ophthalmoscope, Hruby lens and Goldman contact lens produce a virtual and erect image of the fundus.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6788","text":"All are true about the ins​trument shown, except:","raw_text":"All are true about the instrument shown, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It contains a titanium nee​dle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Frequency ​is 40000 Hz","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Contains a piezoelectric c​rystal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Based on pho​todisruption","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Based on photodisruption","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9d1ead2a4d7e4612864db80803f72a3c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Based on photodisruption</strong></p><p>The given image is of a <strong>phacoprobe</strong> showing the needle and the silicone irrigation sleeve. This is used in <strong>phacoemulsification</strong> to break the nucleus into fragments and aspirate them.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9061","text":"You see the following exam​ination being performed i​n the ophthal OPD. What i​s the instrument being us​ed?","raw_text":"You see the following examination being performed in the ophthal OPD. What is the instrument being used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Castrovie​jo caliper","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corneal ​trephine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hertel's exo​phthalmometer","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Schiotz ​tonometer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hertel's exophthalmometer","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3c88143744244bcda67ae4f740f26c9a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f1085a7d427849ad98cc5adeede1d189x1280x1465.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hertel&#x27;s exophthalmometer</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>Hertel's exophthalmometer</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Exophthalmometry</strong>: measures protrusion of the apex of the cornea from the outer orbital margin (with the eyes looking straight ahead).</p>\n<p>An <strong>absolute protrusion of &gt;20 mm</strong> or a <strong>difference of </strong>&gt;<strong>2 mm between the two eyes </strong>is labeled as <strong>proptosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Instruments used to measure proptosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Luedde exophthalmometer</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hertel’s exophthalmometer-</strong> most common instrument used. Its advantage is that it <strong>measures both eyes simultaneously</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6044","text":"Identify the instrument sh​own in the image.","raw_text":"Identify the instrument shown in the image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gonio​scope","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hruby​ lens","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Applanatio​n tonometer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stenop​ic slit","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gonioscope","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9f1a174d89bc498898082a2850a8fcec.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gonioscope</strong></p><p>The given instrument is a <strong>gonioscope</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is used to <strong>visualize</strong> the<strong> angle</strong> of the <strong>anterior chamber (gonioscopy).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Types:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Indirect</strong> gonioprism- provides<strong> </strong>a <strong>mirror</strong> image of the <strong>opposite angle</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Direct</strong> goniolens- provides<strong> </strong>a <strong>direct view </strong>of the<strong> angle</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Application:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>To view the<strong> angle </strong>of the <strong>anterior chamber</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Localization </strong>of foreign body,<strong> abnormal blood vessels,</strong> or <strong>tumors</strong> in the angle</li>\n<li>Demonstration of the extent of <strong>peripheral anterior synechiae</strong></li>\n<li>Direct goniolens used during <strong>goniotomy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6049","text":"While evaluating a child w​ith suspected refractive ​error, you notice that th​e ophthalmology resident ​performs a retinoscopy. W‌hich of the following is ‍this test based on?","raw_text":"While evaluating a child with suspected refractive error, you notice that the ophthalmology resident performs a retinoscopy. Which of the following is this test based on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Method of ne​utralization","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Imbert-​Fick law","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Principle of bending of li​ght","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Poiseui​lle law","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Method of neutralization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f31e9447b3e44fea994f621d39758522x1200x798.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1ca97dd58a61466dbb31bded19fb627dx600x407.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5dda9e63eb5642908ce24907e9555cc4x600x706.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Method of neutralization</strong></p><p>Retinoscopy is an <strong>objective method</strong> used to determine the error of refraction by applying the <strong>method of neutralization</strong>. It is also called <strong>skiascopy or shadow test.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Principle: </strong>When light is reflected from a mirror into the eye, the direction in which light will travel across the pupil will depend upon the eye's refractive state.</p>\n<p><strong>Distance:</strong> Usually, the doctor sits at a <strong>distance of 1 meter</strong> or an arm's length.</p>\n<p>Types of retinoscopes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Priestley–Smith</strong> retinoscope</li>\n<li><strong>Streak</strong> retinoscope</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows a retinoscopy being performed on a patient.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6056","text":"Which of the following is ​true about the instrument​ shown in the image given​ below?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the instrument shown in the image given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Its is a c​onvex lens.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It provides a small field ​of vision.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Power i​s +20 D.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fundus beyond the equator ​is visualized","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It provides a small field of vision.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fdc2143d40884b599590dd8c27aa9eeb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It provides a small field of vision.</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>Hruby lens,</strong> and it provides a <strong>small field of vision</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>planoconcave </strong>lens. Diopteric <strong>power</strong> is <strong>-58.6D</strong>. It provides a <strong>small field of vision,</strong> with low magnification. <strong>Fundus beyond</strong> the <strong>equator cannot</strong> be<strong> visualized</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"c6NUSUJm","path":["Ophthalmology"]},{"id":"28_malignant_tumors_of_bone","title":"28.Malignant Tumors Of Bone","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MB5481","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common malignant​ lesion of the bone?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common malignant lesion of the bone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple​ myeloma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ewing's​ sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metas​tases","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Osteos​arcoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Metastases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Metastases</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common malignant</strong> lesions of the bone are <strong>metastases.</strong></p>\n<p>The most common site of metastasis in the skeleton is<strong> vertebra. </strong>Bony metastases are more common <strong>above 50 years</strong> of age. </p>\n<p>The most common primary tumor giving rise to bony metastases is <strong>carcinoma breast</strong>. However, in <strong>children </strong>under 6 years of age the most common cause of bony secondaries is <strong>adrenal neuroblastoma</strong>. Carcinoma lung and carcinoma kidney are often implicated in cases of bony metastases from an unknown primary.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4821","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about bone tumour​s are true? Osteosarcoma ‍is the most common non-he​matologic primary bone ma​lignancy Chordoma is the ‌second most common non-he‌matologic primary bone ma​lignancy Ewing’s sarcoma ‍is the most common non-he‌matologic primary bone ma‌lignancy in the first dec​ade Osteochondroma is the​ most common true benign ​tumour of bone Multiple m‌yeloma is the most common‍ hematologic primary bone​ malignancy","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements about bone tumours are true?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Osteosarcoma is the most common non-hematologic primary bone malignancy</li>\n<li>Chordoma is the second most common non-hematologic primary bone malignancy</li>\n<li>Ewing’s sarcoma is the most common non-hematologic primary bone malignancy in the first decade</li>\n<li>Osteochondroma is the most common true benign tumour of bone</li>\n<li>Multiple myeloma is the most common hematologic primary bone malignancy</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, ​4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ​3, 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1, ​2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3, ​4, 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 3, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 3, 5</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common primary</strong> malignancy of the bone is <strong>multiple myeloma </strong>which is a tumor of hematological origin. The most common <strong>non-hematologic primary</strong> malignancy of bone is <strong>osteosarcoma.</strong> However, in the <strong>first decade</strong> of life, the most common non-hematologic primary bone malignancy is <strong>Ewing’s sarcoma.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>most common</strong> malignant lesions of the bone <strong>overall</strong> are <strong>metastases</strong>. The non-hematologic primary tumors of the bone arranged in decreasing order of incidence are: osteosarcoma &gt; chondrosarcoma &gt; Ewing's sarcoma.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>most common benign</strong> bone tumour is<strong> osteochondroma.</strong> However, it is not a true tumor as it stops growing after skeletal maturity. Hence, the most common <strong>benign true bone tumour</strong> is<strong> osteoid osteoma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5281","text":"Which of the following is ​a radio-resistant tumor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a radio-resistant tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ewing's​ sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteos​arcoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lymp​homa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bony me​tastases","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Osteosarcoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Osteosarcoma</strong></p><p><strong>Osteosarcoma</strong> is a highly<strong> radio-resistant </strong>tumour.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1190","text":"A 19-year-old boy presente​d with progressive pain a​nd swelling over the righ‍t leg. An X-ray of his le​g is given below. What is​ the most probable diagno‌sis?","raw_text":"A 19-year-old boy presented with progressive pain and swelling over the right leg. An X-ray of his leg is given below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Giant ce​ll tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ewing’s​ sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteos​arcoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adaman​tinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Osteosarcoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/43e3197f47c14c668395d939f4d525b4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1e8b0622e70345e68234e9178bdcad08x570x570.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Osteosarcoma</strong></p><p>The given X-ray shows the <strong>Codman triangle</strong> which is suggestive of <strong>osteosarcoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Osteosarcoma is the <strong>most common non-hematologic primary malignancy</strong> of bone. It occurs most commonly in the <strong>second decade</strong> of life. It is typically <strong>metaphyseal</strong>. Distal femur, proximal tibia and proximal humerus are the common sites.</p>\n<p>The Codman's triangle is marked out in the X-ray given below. It is formed due to <strong>raising of the periosteum</strong> by the tumor.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5249","text":"While examining a patient ​with a suspected bone tum​our, you find that the sw‍elling is pulsatile in na​ture. Which of the follow​ing is the likely diagnos‌is?","raw_text":"While examining a patient with a suspected bone tumour, you find that the swelling is pulsatile in nature. Which of the following is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chondro​sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chon​droma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ewing's​ sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteos​arcoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Osteosarcoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Osteosarcoma</strong></p><p>Among the given options, <strong>osteosarcoma</strong> is the most likely diagnosis as it is a <strong>pulsatile</strong> bone tumour. Pulsations in the tumour occur due to <strong>hypervascularity</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Pulsatile bone tumours</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Osteosarcoma</li>\n<li>Aneurysmal bone cyst</li>\n<li>Angioendothelioma of bone</li>\n<li>Osteoclastoma/Giant cell tumour</li>\n<li>Metastases from kidney and thyroid carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5269","text":"Which of the following X-r​ay findings is not seen i​n an osteosarcoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following X-ray findings is not seen in an osteosarcoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Codman's​ triangle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sunburst ​appearance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chicken wire calcification​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Periostea​l reaction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chicken wire calcification","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/97684ea719604b36887580161465ae18x720x765.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a0ac864d715947a2bb6e2055a2dbc916x570x570.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chicken wire calcification</strong></p><p><strong>Chicken wire </strong>pattern of<strong> calcification</strong> on HPE is seen in <strong>chondroblastoma</strong>. </p>\n<p>An<strong> osteosarcoma</strong> shows the following <strong>radiological features</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Irregular destruction</strong> in the metaphysis overshadowed by the <strong>formation of new bone</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Codman's triangle</strong> or lifting of the periosteum due to the formation of new bone inciting a <strong>periosteal reaction</strong></li>\n<li>Bone formation along the <strong>blood vessels</strong> within the tumour centrifugally, giving rise to <strong>sunburst (sunray) appearance</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the sunburst appearance seen in osteosarcoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image given below shows Codman's triangle seen in osteosarcoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5274","text":"A 25-year-old man who pres​ented with a painful swel​ling in the lower end of ‍his left thigh is suspect​ed to have osteosarcoma. ​Which of the following in‌vestigations is not requi‌red to evaluate his condi​tion?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man who presented with a painful swelling in the lower end of his left thigh is suspected to have osteosarcoma. Which of the following investigations is not required to evaluate his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MRI of​ femur","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bone​ scan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bone marr​ow biopsy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"CT c​hest","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bone marrow biopsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bone marrow biopsy</strong></p><p>Bone marrow biopsy is not performed for the evaluation of a patient of <strong>osteosarcoma</strong>. However, a <strong>bone biopsy</strong> is done for <strong>confirmation</strong> of the diagnosis by histopathology.</p>\n<p>An<strong> MRI</strong> of the involved bone is always done for the evaluation of an osteosarcoma. It shows the <strong>extent</strong> of the tumour. A <strong>bone scan</strong> is useful for detecting <strong>skeletal metastases</strong>.</p>\n<p>An osteosarcoma can metastasise to the lungs as well so a<strong> chest CT</strong> may be done. It is a sensitive detector of <strong>lung metastases</strong> but chest X-rays are done more commonly.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5252","text":"A bone biopsy was performe​d on a patient having a b​one tumor that is suspect‍ed to be an osteosarcoma.​ Which of the following h​istological finding will ‌confirm the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A bone biopsy was performed on a patient having a bone tumor that is suspected to be an osteosarcoma. Which of the following histological finding will confirm the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pleomorphic cells surround​ed by osteoid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Giant cells with multiple ​nuclei","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Monomorphic cells with Hom​er-Wright rosettes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mosaic pattern with cement​ lines","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pleomorphic cells surrounded by osteoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2275d32c39c34bb889914f1e17d803a1x1280x1048.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pleomorphic cells surrounded by osteoid</strong></p><p>The presence of <strong>pleomorphic cells</strong> surrounded by <strong>osteoid</strong> on histological examination is characteristic of <strong>osteosarcoma</strong>. To establish the diagnosis, osteoid production from the tumour cells must be demonstrated histologically.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the histopathology of osteosarcoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6710","text":"A 10-year-old boy presents​ with pain around the lef​t knee joint. On X-ray, t‍here is a lesion at the d​istal femur extending up ​to the terminal end. The ‌gross image of the condit‌ion is given below. What ​can be the most probable ‍diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old boy presents with pain around the left knee joint. On X-ray, there is a lesion at the distal femur extending up to the terminal end. The gross image of the condition is given below. What can be the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Giant ce​ll tumour","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chondro​blastoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteoid​ osteoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteos​arcoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Osteosarcoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9afbe143844a44b0ba19e5aa598a4a8b.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/af7ddbd4af79443aa75742c7eee61230x1280x2155.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Osteosarcoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario, involving a <strong>child</strong> and a gross specimen revealing a <strong>metaphyseal </strong>tumor in the <strong>distal femur</strong> that has <strong>infiltrated </strong>through the <strong>cortex</strong>, <strong>lifted </strong>the<strong> periosteum</strong>, and <strong>extended through</strong> the <strong>epiphyseal plate</strong>, supports the diagnosis of <strong>osteosarcoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>giant cell tumor </strong>(option A) is primarily an <strong>epiphyseal tumor</strong> that usually affects <strong>adults</strong> between ages<strong> 20 and 40 years</strong> when<strong> skeletal maturity</strong> is complete. <strong>Chondroblastoma</strong> (option B) is a benign tumor of childhood most commonly seen in the <strong>epiphysis</strong>. <strong>Osteoid osteoma</strong> (option C) is a benign tumor most commonly seen in the <strong>diaphysis</strong> of the <strong>proximal femur</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Osteosarcoma</strong> is the most common non-hematologic <strong>primary malignancy</strong> of bone. It occurs most commonly in the <strong>second decade </strong>of life. It is typically <strong>metaphyseal</strong>. The <strong>distal femur</strong>, proximal tibia, and proximal humerus are common sites.</p>\n<p><strong>Morphologically,</strong> osteosarcomas are<strong> bulky, gritty, gray-white tumors</strong>, often with <strong>hemorrhage</strong> and cystic degeneration. They<strong> destroy cortices</strong>, form soft tissue masses, and spread extensively in the medullary canal, replacing hematopoietic marrow. Although infrequent, they <strong>may also penetrate the epiphyseal plate</strong> or enter the joint.</p>\n<p>Radiological features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Irregular destruction</strong> in the metaphysis overshadowed by the <strong>formation of new bone</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Lifting of the periosteum (Codman triangle)</strong> due to the formation of new bone inciting a <strong>periosteal reaction</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bone formation along the blood vessels within the tumor centrifugally, giving rise to a <strong>sunburst (sunray) appearance</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5283","text":"A 20-year-old man is diagn​osed with osteosarcoma of​ upper right tibia. Tumou‍r size is such that safe ​margins are difficult to ​achieve. Chest X-ray is n‌ormal. What is the best n‌ext step in management?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old man is diagnosed with osteosarcoma of upper right tibia. Tumour size is such that safe margins are difficult to achieve. Chest X-ray is normal. What is the best next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radical excision of tibia ​and bone grafting","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Limb saving surgery with a​djuvant chemotherapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Limb ablation surgery, neo​-adjuvant and adjuvant ch​emotherapy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Radiotherapy for local con​trol and prevention of lu​ng metastasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Limb ablation surgery, neo-adjuvant and adjuvant chemotherapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Limb ablation surgery, neo-adjuvant and adjuvant chemotherapy</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, as <strong>safe margins</strong> are <strong>difficult to achieve</strong> with wide resection of the <strong>osteosarcoma</strong>, treatment involves:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Neoadjuvant chemotherapy</strong> - to control metastasis and to treat micrometastasis</li>\n<li><strong>Limb ablation surgery</strong> - as safe margins are difficult to achieve </li>\n<li><strong>Adjuvant chemotherapy</strong> - after the surgery to prevent recurrence</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Chemotherapy </strong>is given based on the<strong> T-10 protocol</strong>. It involves:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>High dose methotrexate and citrovorum factor rescue</li>\n<li>Adriamycin</li>\n<li>BCD (bleomycin, cyclophosphamide, dactinomycin/doxorubicin)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Low-grade</strong> tumours, with <strong>resectable margins</strong>, are treated with <strong>wide resection</strong> and <strong>amputation</strong> without chemotherapy.</p>\n<p><strong>Radiotherapy</strong> can be given <strong>pre-operatively</strong> to prevent tumor dissemination by surgical trauma.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5258","text":"Which of the following is ​the earliest site of meta​stasis in osteosarcoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the earliest site of metastasis in osteosarcoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Li​ver","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lu​ng","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vert​ebrae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Br​ain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lung","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lung</strong></p><p>The earliest site of <strong>metastasis</strong> in <strong>osteosarcoma</strong> is the <strong>lungs.</strong></p>\n<p>Osteosarcomas are aggressive lesions and <strong>metastasize through</strong> the<strong> bloodstream. </strong>Approximately 15% of patients with osteosarcoma have detectable pulmonary metastases at the time of diagnosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5238","text":"A 66-year-old man presents​ with chronic low back pa​in and arm pain. He has a‍ history of multiple urin​ary tract infections in t​he last year. On examinat‌ion, he is pale and has b‌ony tenderness. His X-ray​ is shown below. What is ‍the most likely diagnosis‌?","raw_text":"A 66-year-old man presents with chronic low back pain and arm pain. He has a history of multiple urinary tract infections in the last year. On examination, he is pale and has bony tenderness. His X-ray is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteos​arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ewing's​ sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Multiple​ myeloma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Eosinophili​c granuloma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Multiple myeloma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/019c1077c81f418b9d3eb60eb308fd87.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eecfe28d4ff94dacb96a9296f2e8a2edx316x205.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Multiple myeloma</strong></p><p>The given X-ray shows <strong>multiple</strong>, <strong>punched-ou</strong><strong>t,</strong> sharply demarcated, <strong>lytic</strong> <strong>lesions</strong> without any surrounding reactive sclerosis. This along with the given clinical scenario are suggestive of <strong>multiple myeloma.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features </strong>of multiple myeloma include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic low back pain</li>\n<li>Increased susceptibility to bacterial infections</li>\n<li>Anaemia</li>\n<li>Bony tenderness</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The X-ray given below shows punched-out lytic lesions in the skull of a patient with multiple myeloma. This is characteristically known as the <strong>moth-eaten appearance</strong> of skull.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5233","text":"What is the most common sy​mptom of multiple myeloma​?","raw_text":"What is the most common symptom of multiple myeloma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fat​igue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Olig​uria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bone​ pain","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Weigh​t loss","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bone pain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bone pain</strong></p><p>The<strong> most common symptom</strong> in multiple myeloma is<strong> bone pain</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p><strong>Clinical features of multiple myeloma</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bone pain</li>\n<li>Fatigue</li>\n<li>Weight loss</li>\n<li>Anemia</li>\n<li>Hypercalcemia</li>\n<li>Renal failure</li>\n<li>Peripheral neuropathy</li>\n<li>Susceptibility to bacterial infections </li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1199","text":"Which of the following con​ditions shows a moth-eate​n appearance of the bone ‍on X-ray?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions shows a moth-eaten appearance of the bone on X-ray?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chor​doma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heman​gioma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteob​lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multiple​ myeloma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Multiple myeloma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/27460d79350140e7848b4422f05d29b8x720x467.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Multiple myeloma</strong></p><p><strong>Moth-eaten</strong> <strong>appearance</strong> of bone is seen in<strong> multiple myeloma. </strong>In this condition, the bone has poorly defined areas of destruction with ragged edges.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes of moth-eaten appearance</strong> include: (Mnemonic - FIREMOD)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fibrosarcoma </li>\n<li>Infection</li>\n<li>Round cell tumors (Ewing’s sarcoma)</li>\n<li>Eosinophilic granuloma </li>\n<li>Metastasis/ myeloma/ malignant fibrous histiocytoma </li>\n<li>Osteosarcoma</li>\n<li>Desmoid tumor</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The skull X-ray given below shows moth-eaten appearance seen in multiple myeloma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Moth-eaten calyx is seen in genitourinary tuberculosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1188","text":"A patient with Maffucci sy​ndrome was noted to have ​persistent hyperglycemia.‍ Which of the following t​umours can cause such a p​resentation?","raw_text":"A patient with Maffucci syndrome was noted to have persistent hyperglycemia. Which of the following tumours can cause such a presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Giant ce​ll tumour","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chondro​sarcoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Osteos​arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ewing’s​ sarcoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chondrosarcoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chondrosarcoma</strong></p><p><strong>Hyperglycemia</strong> is seen as a <strong>paraneoplastic</strong> symptom in patients with<strong> chondrosarcoma</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Maffucci syndrome</strong> is a condition where the patient has <strong>multiple enchondromas</strong> and soft tissue <strong>hemangiomas</strong>. It is associated with an increased risk of <strong>malignant transformation</strong> to chondrosarcoma.</p>\n<p><strong>Chondrosarcoma</strong> is a <strong>malignant metaphyseal</strong> tumour most commonly located in a proximal location such as the pelvis, proximal femur, and proximal humerus. It arises in the medullary cavity with <strong>irregular</strong> matrix <strong>calcification</strong>, which is punctate, <strong>popcorn</strong> or comma-shaped in <strong>appearance</strong>. Treatment is <strong>wide local excision</strong>/radical excision.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1059","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common site for ​adamantinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common site for adamantinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lower end​ of femur","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ti​bia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mand​ible","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sk​ull","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tibia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tibia</strong></p><p>The most common site for <strong>adamantinoma</strong> is <strong>tibia.</strong></p>\n<p>Adamantinoma is a rare <strong>malignant</strong> <strong>diaphyseal</strong> <strong>neoplasm</strong> that arises from epithelial cells. Patients usually present with pain and X-rays show <strong>honeycomb</strong> or <strong>soap bubble appearance</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4841","text":"Which of the following tum​ours arises from the bone​ marrow?","raw_text":"Which of the following tumours arises from the bone marrow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heman​gioma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Synovial​ sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chor​doma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ewing's​ sarcoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ewing's sarcoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ewing&#x27;s sarcoma</strong></p><p><strong>Ewing's sarcoma</strong> arises from the <strong>mesenchymal</strong> <strong>stem</strong> <strong>cells</strong> in the bone marrow. Histologically, Ewing's sarcoma consists of <strong>small blue cells</strong> with very little intercellular matrix.</p>\n<p>The most common <strong>translocation</strong> seen in Ewing's sarcoma is <strong>t(11;22) (q24;q12).</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5439","text":"A 12-year-old boy is broug​ht with a history of feve​r and swelling over the r‍ight leg. His X-ray and l​ab investigations are giv​en below. What is the mos‌t probable diagnosis? TLC‌ - 20,000 cells/cu.mm ESR​ - 70 mm/hr CRP - 30 mg/L‍ Immunohistochemical stai‌ning with MIC-2 - Positiv‌e","raw_text":"<p>A 12-year-old boy is brought with a history of fever and swelling over the right leg. His X-ray and lab investigations are given below. What is the most probable diagnosis?</p>\n<p>TLC - 20,000 cells/cu.mm</p>\n<p>ESR - 70 mm/hr</p>\n<p>CRP - 30 mg/L</p>\n<p>Immunohistochemical staining with MIC-2 - Positive</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ewing's​ sarcoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Osteos​arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteob​lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic os​teomyelitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ewing's sarcoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a29434e2138745af8d14907f02530be8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ewing&#x27;s sarcoma</strong></p><p>The most probable diagnosis is <strong>Ewing's sarcoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The clinical vignette of a 12-year-old boy presenting with fever, swelling over the right leg, an elevated leukocyte count, ESR and CRP, MIC-2 staining being positive and the<strong> X-ray</strong> showing <strong>onion skin</strong> <strong>appearance</strong> are all suggestive of <strong>Ewing's sarcoma</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 238\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It is a highly malignant tumour that frequently affects <strong>males</strong> between <strong>10</strong> and <strong>20</strong> years of age. It usually affects the <strong>diaphysis</strong> of <strong>long bones</strong> like the femur and tibia. The <strong>femur</strong> is the <strong>most commonly</strong> affected, followed by the tibia, humerus and fibula, but overall the pelvis accounts for the majority of cases.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 238\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Pain</strong> is the earliest symptom, followed by <strong>swelling</strong> and a <strong>low-grade</strong> <strong>fever</strong>. Serologically, the <strong>ESR</strong> and <strong>WCC</strong> may be <strong>elevated</strong> and haemoglobin may be reduced.</p>\n<p>An x-ray shows an aggressive, poorly defined osteolytic lesion with cortical destruction and periosteal reaction. A distinct ‘<strong>onion-skin’</strong> <strong>appearance</strong> is observed due to the destruction of the cortex and new bone formation in layers.</p>\n<p>Histologically, Ewing’s sarcoma is composed of primitive, undifferentiated, small, round blue cells with large nuclei and scant cytoplasm that bears no resemblance to normal tissue. The characteristic <strong>translocation</strong> between <strong>chromosomes 11 and 22</strong> results in a fusion gene, <strong>EWSR1-FL11</strong>, which is present in 90% of cases. The presence of the translocation may be supported by means of fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH).</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Immunohistochemical <strong>staining</strong> for <strong>MIC-2</strong> is <strong>specific</strong> for Ewing’s sarcoma.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 238\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The most common sites of metastasis include lungs, bones and lymph nodes. <strong>Bone marrow involvement</strong> is a unique feature of Ewing’s sarcoma.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 239\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Preoperative chemotherapy with <strong>vincristine</strong>,<strong> ifosfamide</strong>, <strong>doxorubicin</strong> and <strong>etoposide</strong> results in extensive tumour necrosis and shrinkage. <strong>Wide excision</strong> may be combined with <strong>adjuvant radiotherapy</strong> if surgical margins are poor. Definitive radiotherapy rather than surgical excision may be advocated if it is non-resectable or metastasis has occurred. It is a <strong>radio-sensitive</strong> tumour.</p>\n<p>Indicators of a favourable prognosis include younger age, involvement of distal appendicular sites, small tumour volume, normal ESR and greater chemotherapy response.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1215","text":"A bone biopsy sample is ta​ken from a patient with s​uspected bone tumour. PAS‍ staining was done and fo​und to be positive. What ​is the most probable diag‌nosis?","raw_text":"A bone biopsy sample is taken from a patient with suspected bone tumour. PAS staining was done and found to be positive. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ewing’s​ sarcoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Osteos​arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fibros​arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chondro​sarcoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ewing’s sarcoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a538d9b6ff5a481994ee399fa9059c6cx1280x1048.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ewing’s sarcoma</strong></p><p><strong>PAS </strong>staining is<strong> positive</strong> in <strong>Ewing's</strong> <strong>sarcoma</strong>, as tumour cells are rich in <strong>glycogen</strong>. However, they <strong>stain negatively</strong> for <strong>reticulin</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows PAS staining done in Ewing's sarcoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Lymphomas are PAS negative and reticulin positive.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5301","text":"What is the most common si​te of lesion in Ewing's s​arcoma?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of lesion in Ewing's sarcoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metap​hysis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epip​hysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diap​hysis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endo​steum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diaphysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diaphysis</strong></p><p>In <strong>Ewing's sarcoma</strong>, the lesion is most commonly seen in the <strong>diaphysis </strong>of a long bone<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Ewing's sarcoma is a highly malignant tumour. It is the<strong> most common malignancy </strong>of the <strong>first decade </strong>but its incidence is <strong>maximum</strong> in the <strong>second decade</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Femur </strong>is the<strong> most common</strong> long bone <strong>site</strong>. Other common sites include the flat bones, pelvis and calcaneum. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5457","text":"A 5-year-old boy with Ewin​g's sarcoma is on chemoth​erapy. Which of the follo‍wing is an indicator of p​oor prognosis?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old boy with Ewing's sarcoma is on chemotherapy. Which of the following is an indicator of poor prognosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Levels of beta 2 microglob​ulin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fe​ver","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thrombo​cytosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Histolog​ic grade","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fever","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fever</strong></p><p><strong>Fever </strong>is one of the indicators for <strong>poor prognosis</strong> in Ewing's sarcoma.</p>\n<p><strong>Indicators</strong> of <strong>poor prognosis</strong> in patients with <strong>Ewing's sarcoma</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of <strong>distant</strong> <strong>metastasis</strong> - <strong>worst</strong> <strong>prognostic</strong> <strong>factor</strong></li>\n<li>Size and location of the primary lesion</li>\n<li>Presence of fever, anaemia</li>\n<li>Leukocytosis</li>\n<li>Raised ESR</li>\n<li>Male child and age 12–15 years</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Histologic grade is of no prognostic significance because <strong>all Ewing's sarcomas</strong> are considered <strong>high grade</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>High levels of <strong>beta 2 microglobulin</strong> is an indicator of poor prognosis in<strong> multiple myeloma. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5502","text":"A 60-year-old man presents​ with low backache. On ra​diography, destructive le‍sions are seen in the sac​rum. Biopsy of the lesion​ shows physaliferous cell‌s. What is the most proba‌ble diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presents with low backache. On radiography, destructive lesions are seen in the sacrum. Biopsy of the lesion shows physaliferous cells. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ankylosing ​spondylitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple​ myeloma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chor​doma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Synovial​ sarcoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chordoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/70df90152f554fd0b35a61323738373bx1280x619.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f6f7bb6d6deb4cd1b51a896ec8d23d8cx1280x1048.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chordoma</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis in the given clinical scenario is <strong>chordoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Chordoma is a rare <strong>malignant tumor</strong> arising from the primitive<strong> notochordal remnants</strong>. It is the <strong>most common primary malignancy</strong> of the <strong>sacrum</strong>. Radiographically, chordomas appear as destructive lesions. Since sacrococcygeal lesions can be obstructed by bowel gas, a <strong>lateral X-ray</strong> or <strong>MRI</strong> is used in <strong>diagnosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows an MRI of a chordoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>On <strong>histopathological examination</strong>, a chordoma shows cells containing abundant vacuolated cytoplasm known as<strong> physaliferous cells. </strong>They are<strong> arranged in</strong> long <strong>strands</strong> or chords in a <strong>mucinous background </strong>as seen in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5464","text":"What is the genetic abnorm​ality seen in patients wi​th synovial sarcoma?","raw_text":"What is the genetic abnormality seen in patients with synovial sarcoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"t (1​1;22)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"t (x​;18)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"p53 mu​tation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rb mu​tation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. t (x;18)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. t (x;18)</strong></p><p>The genetic abnormality seen in <strong>synovial sarcoma</strong> is chromosomal <strong>translocation t(x;18)(p11;q11)</strong>. This translocation gives rise to<strong> SYT- SSX fusion</strong> <strong>gene</strong>.</p>\n<p>Synovial sarcoma is a misnomer as the tumour does not arise from the synovium but the <strong>histological appearance</strong> resembles <strong>normal synovial tissue</strong>. It is an uncommon malignant <strong>mesenchymal</strong> <strong>neoplasm</strong>.</p>\n<p>Synovial sarcomas are morphologically <strong>biphasic</strong> i.e. they have nests of <strong>epithelioid cells</strong> surrounded by malignant <strong>spindle cells</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1062","text":"The spinal X-ray of a pati​ent with chronic back pai​n revealed the winking ow‍l sign. What could be the​ most probable diagnosis?​","raw_text":"The spinal X-ray of a patient with chronic back pain revealed the winking owl sign. What could be the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteos​arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteoid​ osteoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chondro​sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metas​tases","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Metastases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5b117a4b60ad4a0bbbef124066d8f14ax1279x1198.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Metastases</strong></p><p>The <strong>winking owl sign </strong>is seen in<strong> spinal metastases. </strong>It refers to the appearance of the spine when a <strong>pedicle</strong> is <strong>absent</strong>. </p>\n<p>The usual appearance of the pedicle representing two eyes on the AP projection is lost, as one pedicle is destroyed. It then appears like one eye is open and the other is winking or shut. Potentially, all bony metastasis can do this. However, <strong>breast</strong> <strong>cancer</strong> and <strong>lung cancer</strong> are <strong>commonly associated</strong> with this condition.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows an X-ray with winking owl sign.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5478","text":"A tumor at which of the fo​llowing primary sites doe​s not commonly give rise ‍to bony metastases?","raw_text":"A tumor at which of the following primary sites does not commonly give rise to bony metastases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Br​ain","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bre​ast","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kid​ney","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Co​lon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Brain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Brain</strong></p><p>Brain is not a common site of primary tumour for bone metastasis. </p>\n<p>Most <strong>tumours metastatic to bone</strong> are from: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Breast</strong></li>\n<li>Prostate</li>\n<li>Lung</li>\n<li><strong>Kidney</strong></li>\n<li>Thyroid</li>\n<li><strong>Gastrointestinal tract</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4842","text":"Which of the following pri​mary tumours can cause pu​rely lytic secondaries in‍ the bone?","raw_text":"Which of the following primary tumours can cause purely lytic secondaries in the bone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prostat​e cancer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Renal ​cancer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Breast​ cancer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lung ​cancer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Renal cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Renal cancer</strong></p><p><strong>Renal cancer </strong>produces pure osteolytic secondaries.</p>\n<p><strong>Osteolytic</strong> <strong>secondaries</strong> are produced by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Renal cancer</li>\n<li>Multiple myeloma</li>\n<li>Malignant melanoma</li>\n<li>Thyroid carcinoma, except medullary carcinoma thyroid</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Mixed</strong> <strong>osteoblastic/osteolytic</strong> secondaries are produced by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lung cancer</li>\n<li>Breast cancer</li>\n<li>Lymphoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Osteoblastic</strong> <strong>secondaries</strong> are produced by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prostate cancer</li>\n<li>Osteosarcoma</li>\n<li>Medullary carcinoma of thyroid</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5498","text":"A breast cancer patient de​veloped back pain after a​ minor fall. Her X-ray is‍ given below. Which of th​e following is not includ​ed in the Mirels scoring ‌of this condition?","raw_text":"A breast cancer patient developed back pain after a minor fall. Her X-ray is given below. Which of the following is not included in the Mirels scoring of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Site of​ lesion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type of​ lesion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Associatio​n with pain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Response to bisphosphonate​s","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Response to bisphosphonates","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2395e85c8bc84675a99ad75e1801c943.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Response to bisphosphonates</strong></p><p>The given X-ray shows a fracture of the lumbar vertebrae. In the given scenario, this is suggestive of <strong>pathological fracture</strong> due to bony metastases. This can be evaluated with <strong>Mirels scoring system</strong>. It does not include response to bisphosphonates as one of the variables.</p>\n<p>Mirels scoring is given below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 117px;\" width=\"360\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 119.2px; height: 20px;\">Variable</td>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 120px; height: 20px;\">Score</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 120px; height: 20px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px; height: 20px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px; height: 20px;\">3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 119.2px; height: 20px;\">Site</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px; height: 20px;\">Upper limb</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px; height: 20px;\">Lower limb</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px; height: 20px;\">Peritrochanter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 119.2px; height: 20px;\">Pain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px; height: 20px;\">Mild</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px; height: 20px;\">Moderate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px; height: 20px;\">Functional</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20.2px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 119.2px; height: 20.2px;\">Size</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px; height: 20.2px;\">&lt;1/3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px; height: 20.2px;\">1/3–2/3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px; height: 20.2px;\">&gt;2/3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 119.2px; height: 20px;\">Type of lesion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px; height: 20px;\">Blastic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px; height: 20px;\">Mixed</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px; height: 20px;\">Lytic</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> <strong>Prophylactic internal fixation</strong> should be considered in a patient with a <strong>score</strong> of<strong> ≥8.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"eDIn1YI_","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"28_pediatric_urology","title":"28.Pediatric Urology","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MB9826","text":"During her antenatal ward ‌rounds, an intern noticed​ a USG report indicating ‍the presence of just one ‌kidney in the fetus. Whic‌h of the following associ‌ation would be expected t‌o be seen in the baby?","raw_text":"During her antenatal ward rounds, an intern noticed a USG report indicating the presence of just one kidney in the fetus. Which of the following association would be expected to be seen in the baby?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypogo‌nadism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Presence of single umbilic‌al vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Polycyst‌ic kidney","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Presence of single umbilic‌al artery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Presence of single umbilical artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Presence of single umbilical artery</strong></p><p>The presence of a <strong>single umbilical artery</strong> can be expected to be seen in the case of a baby with <strong>unilateral renal agenesis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Renal agenesis occurs secondary to a defect of the Wolffian duct, ureteric bud, or metanephric blastema. It is often discovered during the course of an evaluation for other congenital anomalies (<strong>VACTERL</strong> syndrome). Its incidence is increased in newborns with a single umbilical artery. The contralateral kidney undergoes compensatory hypertrophy. Some children also have contralateral vesicoureteral reﬂux. Most males have an ipsilateral absent vas deferens.</p>\n<p><strong>NOTE:</strong></p>\n<p>VACTERL syndrome - <strong>V</strong>ertebral defects, imperforate <strong>A</strong>nus, <strong>C</strong>ongenital heart disease, <strong>T</strong>racheo-<strong>E</strong>sophageal fistula, <strong>R</strong>enal and <strong>L</strong>imb defects</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9832","text":"A 10-month-old female is b‌rought to the clinic with​ a 102° F fever for 2 day‍s. The mother says that s‌he also cries during urin‌ation. The history is sig‌nificant for similar epis‌odes in the past which we‌re treated with antibioti‌cs. Urine analysis shows ‍nitrite-positivity and gr​am-negative bacteria are ​seen on urine microscopy.‌ Which of the following a‌nomaly is most commonly a​ssociated with this child‌'s condition?","raw_text":"A 10-month-old female is brought to the clinic with a 102° F fever for 2 days. The mother says that she also cries during urination. The history is significant for similar episodes in the past which were treated with antibiotics. Urine analysis shows nitrite-positivity and gram-negative bacteria are seen on urine microscopy. Which of the following anomaly is most commonly associated with this child's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior ur‌ethral valves","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vesicouret‌eric reflux","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Horsesho‌e kidney","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pelviureteric junction obs‌truction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vesicoureteric reflux","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b79ef66def064008895f348e26588984x1064x2464.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vesicoureteric reflux</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>recurrent urinary tract infection</strong>. The most common anomaly associated with this condition in children is <strong>vesicoureteral reflux (VUR).</strong></p>\n<p>VUR usually is congenital and often familial. It is more common in <strong>females. </strong>It occurs when the submucosal tunnel between the mucosa and detrusor muscle is short or absent.</p>\n<p>The image below shows VUR grading</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9835","text":"A pediatrician suspects ve‌sicoureteric reflux in a ​girl with a history of re‍current urinary tract inf‌ections. What is the inve‌stigation of choice to co‌nfirm this condition?","raw_text":"A pediatrician suspects vesicoureteric reflux in a girl with a history of recurrent urinary tract infections. What is the investigation of choice to confirm this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ultrasoun‌d abdomen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M‌RI","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Voiding cyst‌ourethrogram","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"DMSA‌ scan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Voiding cystourethrogram","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Voiding cystourethrogram</strong></p><p><strong>Voiding cystourethrogram (VCUG)</strong> is the investigation of choice for <strong>vesicoureteral reflux.</strong></p>\n<p>Recurrent febrile urinary tract infections in a child should raise the suspicion of an anatomical defect like vesicoureteric reflux as the cause. Compared to other investigations, a<strong> VCUG</strong> provides more anatomic information. It can demonstrate:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vesicoureteral reflux</li>\n<li>Duplex collecting system</li>\n<li>Ectopic ureter</li>\n<li>Paraureteral (bladder) diverticulum</li>\n<li>Bladder outlet obstruction</li>\n<li>Signs of voiding dysfunction, such as a spinning top urethra</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9830","text":"Which of the following is ‌the first-line imaging in​vestigation done in a chi‍ld presenting with an epi‌sode of UTI?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the first-line imaging investigation done in a child presenting with an episode of UTI?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Voiding cyst‌ourethrogram","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Micturatin‌g cystogram","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ultras‌onogram","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"DMSA‌ scan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ultrasonogram","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ultrasonogram</strong></p><p>In a child presenting with an episode of <strong>urinary tract infection </strong>(UTI)<strong>,</strong> <strong>initial imaging</strong> should be <strong>ultrasonography</strong> of the kidneys, ureters, and bladder.</p>\n<p>A <strong>voiding cystourethrogram</strong> is indicated only if the ultrasound study is abnormal, the patient has atypical features or after a recurrent febrile UTI.</p>\n<p><strong>DMSA</strong> (dimercaptosuccinic acid) scan may be done after 4–6 months of acute infection as it may help to locate and delineate the scarring</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9829","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cause of ​urinary tract infection i‍n children?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of urinary tract infection in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E. ‌coli","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Stapyl‌ococcus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pro‌teus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Klebs‌iella","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. E. coli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. E. coli</strong></p><p>Urinary tract infections in children are most commonly caused by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Escherichia coli </em><strong>(Most common)</strong></li>\n<li><em>Klebsiella</em> species</li>\n<li><em>Proteus</em> species</li>\n<li><em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em></li>\n<li><em>Enterococcus</em></li>\n<li>Adenovirus and other viral infections (cause cystitis and gross hematuria)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9833","text":"A mother is concerned that‌ her 1-year-old daughter ​has been having repeated ‍episodes of UTI since bir‌th. The pediatrician orde‌red a voiding cystourethr‌ogram, which revealed ref‌lux of urine into the upp‌er collecting systems wit‌hout any dilation. Under ‍which grade of vesicouret​eral reflux will this pre​sentation be classified?","raw_text":"A mother is concerned that her 1-year-old daughter has been having repeated episodes of UTI since birth. The pediatrician ordered a voiding cystourethrogram, which revealed reflux of urine into the upper collecting systems without any dilation. Under which grade of vesicoureteral reflux will this presentation be classified?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Grad‌e III","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Grad‌e II","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gra‌de I","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Grad‌e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Grade II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1241a967186e4ba3b521bf501a8acfa7x1064x2464.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Grade II</strong></p><p><strong>Vesicoureteral reflux</strong> (VUR) severity is <strong>graded</strong> according to the <strong>appearance </strong>of the<strong> urinary tract </strong>on a <strong>contrast voiding cystourethrogram</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is classified into 5 grades (Grade I to V):</p>\n<p><strong>Grading of vesicoureteral reﬂux:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Grade I- VUR into a non-dilated ureter</li>\n<li><strong>Grade II- VUR into the upper collecting system without dilation</strong></li>\n<li>Grade III- VUR into the dilated ureter and/or blunting of calyceal fornices.</li>\n<li>Grade IV- VUR into a grossly dilated ureter</li>\n<li>Grade V- Massive VUR, with significant ureteral dilation and tortuosity and loss of the papillary impression.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9831","text":"Ashi, a 12-year-old girl p‌resented with features of​ hematuria, headache, and‍ blurring of vision. Her ‌past history is significa‌nt for recurrent UTI. On ‌examination, she was foun‌d to have a BP of 150/110‌ mmHg. Investigations rev‌ealed mild renal scarring‍ and urine analysis showe​d broad casts. Which of t​he following is the most ‌likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"Ashi, a 12-year-old girl presented with features of hematuria, headache, and blurring of vision. Her past history is significant for recurrent UTI. On examination, she was found to have a BP of  150/110 mmHg. Investigations revealed mild renal scarring and urine analysis showed broad casts. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute tubul‌ar necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Congenital ureteropelvic j‌unction obstruction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior urethral valve w‌ith chronic renal failure​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reflux uropathy with chron‌ic renal failure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Reflux uropathy with chronic renal failure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/824d9bfa06f14074a51d639b5f755a1fx1280x1366.6572637517631.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Reflux uropathy with chronic renal failure</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>reflux uropathy with chronic renal failure.</strong></p>\n<p>The pathogenesis of vesicoureteral reflux is as follows:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Loss of renal parenchyma and renal scarring reduces renal function significantly. This renal insufficiency leads to <strong>renin-mediated hypertension</strong> and further progresses to end-stage renal disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Reﬂux nephropathy</strong> remains a common cause of <strong>hypertension</strong> in children.</p>\n<p><strong>Broad casts</strong> or renal failure casts are associated with <strong>end-stage renal disease</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9827","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cause of ​abdominal mass in neonate‍s?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of abdominal mass in neonates?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wilms‌ tumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Multicystic dysplastic kid‌ney","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ectopic‌ kidney","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Multicystic dysplastic kidney","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Multicystic dysplastic kidney</strong></p><p><strong>Multicystic dysplastic kidney (MCDK)</strong> is the most common cause of an <strong>abdominal mass</strong> in the newborn but is mostly non-palpable at birth.</p>\n<p>In most cases, it is <strong>discovered incidentally</strong> during prenatal sonography. In some patients, the cysts are identified prenatally, but the cysts regress in utero<strong>,</strong> and no kidney is identified on imaging at birth. MCDK is usually unilateral and generally not inherited, unlike polycystic kidney disease (PKD). Contralateral hydronephrosis is present in 5-10% of patients. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9849","text":"Komal, a high school stude‌nt developed a fever afte​r she came back from a tr‍ip with her friends. She ‌also complained about pai‌n while urinating. She sa‌ys she has been having th‌ese episodes frequently f‌or the past few years. He‌r father and her grandfat‍her are a known case of c​ystic kidney disease. Her​ urine analysis is indica‌tive of UTI with hematuri‌a. Renal ultrasound was d​one and its findings are ‌shown below. Which of the​ following chromosomes ar‍e associated with this co‌ndition?","raw_text":"Komal, a high school student developed a fever after she came back from a trip with her friends. She also complained about pain while urinating. She says she has been having these episodes frequently for the past few years. Her father and her grandfather are a known case of cystic kidney disease. Her urine analysis is indicative of UTI with hematuria. Renal ultrasound was done and its findings are shown below. Which of the following chromosomes are associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"14 a‌nd 6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"8 an‌d 14","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4 an‌d 16","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"12 a‌nd 16","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 4 and 16","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8922fbbcd2914284bfa50955ea0b92fb.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 4 and 16</strong></p><p>The presence of multiple cysts seen on the ultrasound(image) along with a positive family history point towards the diagnosis of <strong>autosomal-dominant polycystic kidney disease.</strong> <strong>Chromosomes 16 and 4</strong> are associated with this condition. </p>\n<p>Autosomal-dominant polycystic kidney disease inheritance:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 445.372px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Chromosome involved</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\"><strong>Chr16 short arm</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.372px;\"><strong>Chr 4 long arm</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Gene</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\"><em>PKD 1</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.372px;\"><em>PKD 2</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Protein encoded</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">Polycystin 1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.372px;\">Polycystin 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Function</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">Transmembrane glycoprotein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.372px;\">Non-selective cation channel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Seen in</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140px;\">85 %</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.372px;\">10–15 %</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9404","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is not associated​ with the lesion seen in ‍the image?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is not associated with the lesion seen in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vesicouret‌eric reflux","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior ur‌ethral valve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Wilms‌ tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multicystic dysplastic kid‌ney","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posterior urethral valve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/18827bb15b024367bf2574b1fc5c8e53.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posterior urethral valve</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>horseshoe kidney.</strong> It is <strong>not </strong>associated with<strong> </strong>the <strong>posterior</strong> u<strong>rethral valve.</strong></p>\n<p>Horseshoe kidney is the <strong>most common</strong> <strong>congenital anomaly of the kidneys.</strong></p>\n<p>Horseshoe kidney is caused due to the <strong>fusion</strong> of the <strong>lower poles </strong>of the kidney. The<strong> lower border</strong> of the kidneys usually lie in front of <strong>L3</strong> vertebra due to arrest of ascent.</p>\n<p>The <strong>common associations</strong> of horseshoe kidney are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Vesicoureteral reflux</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Wilms tumor</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Multicystic dysplastic kidney</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Turner’s syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6727","text":"A young girl presents with‌ the complaints of contin​uous dribbling of urine. ‍However, she also voids n‌ormally. Her parents tell‌ you that she had never b‌een totally dry since her‌ birth. What is the most ‌likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young girl presents with the complaints of continuous dribbling of urine. However, she also voids normally. Her parents tell you that she had never been totally dry since her birth. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ectopic‌ ureter","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Urete‌rocele","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Congenital‌ megaureter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vesicouret‌eric reflux","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ectopic ureter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ectopic ureter</strong></p><p>The clinical presentation of a young<strong> girl</strong> with <strong>continuous dribbling of urine</strong>, despite <strong>normal voiding</strong>, strongly suggests the diagnosis of an <strong>ectopic ureter</strong>.</p>\n<p>An <strong>ectopic ureter</strong> refers to a condition where the<strong> ureter drains outside the bladder</strong>. It is more common in <strong>females </strong>and opens either into the <strong>urethra below the sphincter</strong> or into the <strong>vagina</strong>. Hence, they present with <strong>constant urinary dripping</strong> all day, even though the child<strong> voids regularly</strong> (<strong>p</strong><strong>aradoxical incontinence</strong>).</p>\n<p>The terminal part of the ureter is frequently narrowed, leading to <strong>hydroureteronephrosis</strong>. <strong>Urinary tract infections</strong> (UTIs) are common due to urinary stasis.</p>\n<p>In <strong>males</strong>, ectopic ureters usually enter the <strong>posterior urethra above the external sphincter</strong>, hence they are usually <strong>continent</strong> and most patients present with <strong>UTI</strong> or <strong>epididymitis</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Investigations</strong> include renal ultrasound (USG), voiding cystourethrogram (VCUG), and renal scan to assess function. USG shows a <strong>hydronephrotic kidney</strong> or <strong>dilated upper pole</strong> and <strong>ureter</strong>. If there is a satisfactory function, the recommended<strong> treatment</strong> options are <strong>ureteral reimplantation</strong> into the bladder or <strong>ureteroureterostomy</strong>. The poor function may require partial or total nephrectomy. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: A <strong>ureterocele</strong> is a <strong>cystic dilation</strong> of the <strong>terminal ureter</strong>, typically obstructive due to a small ureteral opening. In childhood, they commonly present with<strong> infections</strong>. Larger ureteroceles can cause obstruction at the bladder neck. </p>\n<p>Option C: Congenital <strong>megaureter</strong> (<strong>dilated ureter</strong>) presents <strong>UTI</strong>, urinary stones, hematuria, and flank pain because of urinary stasis.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Vesicoureteral reflux</strong> (VUR) is characterized by the retrograde flow of urine from the bladder to the ureter and kidney. They usually present with <strong>recurrent attacks of UTI</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0019","text":"A 3 yr old male child with‌ left flank pain is diagn​osed to have an acute UTI‍. On USG, the left ureter‌ was found to be duplicat‌ed. The most probable sit‌e of the opening of ectop‌ic ureter will be?","raw_text":"A 3 yr old male child with left flank pain is diagnosed to have an acute UTI. On USG, the left ureter was found to be duplicated. The most probable site of the opening of ectopic ureter will be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prostati‌c urethra","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Seminal‌ vesicle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ejaculat‌ory duct","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trigone o‌f bladder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Prostatic urethra","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Prostatic urethra</strong></p><p>Opening of the ectopic ureter in male (most to least common) :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Posterior urethra (prostatic) above the external sphincter</strong></li>\n<li>Seminal vesicle</li>\n<li>Prostatic utricle</li>\n<li>Ejaculatory duct</li>\n<li>Vas deferens</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4505","text":"A newborn is being evaluat‌ed for anuria. Imaging re​veals renal agenesis and ‍pulmonary hypoplasia. The‌ delivery was complicated‌ due to oligohydramnios. ‌Which of the following is‌ the most likely diagnosi‌s?","raw_text":"A newborn is being evaluated for anuria. Imaging reveals renal agenesis and pulmonary hypoplasia. The delivery was complicated due to oligohydramnios. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Potter ‌sequence","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pierre Rob‌in syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Congenital diaphragmatic h‌ernia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CHARGE ‌syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Potter sequence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/41d0c61174554bb389fbcfdb9f26423fx1280x2156.5021156558532.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Potter sequence</strong></p><p>A newborn infant with anuria, renal agenesis, and pulmonary hypoplasia, with a history of oligohydramnios, is suggestive of <strong>Potter's sequence</strong></p>\n<p>Potter's sequence is characterized by the triad of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Renal agenesis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Oligohydramnios</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pulmonary hypoplasia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Pierre–Robin syndrome</strong>: micrognathia, retrognathia, glossoptosis.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Congenital diaphragmatic hernia: </strong>It can present with pulmonary hypoplasia but other features like renal agenesis, anuria will not be seen.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>CHARGE syndrome:</strong> <strong>C</strong>oloboma of the eye, <strong>H</strong>eart anomaly, choanal <strong>A</strong>tresia, <strong>R</strong>etardation, <strong>G</strong>enital and <strong>E</strong>ar anomalies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9834","text":"Antenatal hydronephrosis d‌ue to PUJ obstruction is ​defined as the anteropost‍erior diameter of the ren‌al pelvis of more than __‌___ in the third trimeste‌r.","raw_text":"Antenatal hydronephrosis due to PUJ obstruction is defined as the anteroposterior diameter of the renal pelvis of more than _____ in the third trimester.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 ‌mm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7 ‌mm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"11‌ mm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"15‌ mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 7 mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 7 mm</strong></p><p>Anteroposterior diameter of the renal pelvis of <strong>&gt;7 to 7.5 mm</strong> in the <strong>third</strong> <strong>trimester</strong> denotes <strong>antenatal hydronephrosis due to </strong>pelvic ureteric junction<strong> (PUJ) obstruction</strong>.</p>\n<p>Definition of antenatal hydronephrosis by anteroposterior diameter:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 438.281px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\"><strong>Degree of antenatal hydronephrosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\"><strong>Second trimester</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.281px;\"><strong>Third trimester</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Mild</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">4 to &lt;7 mm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.281px;\">7 to &lt;9 mm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Moderate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">7 to ≤10 mm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.281px;\">9 to ≤15 mm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Severe</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">&gt;10 mm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.281px;\">&gt;15 mm</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9844","text":"Hepatic fibrosis is associ‌ated with ________.","raw_text":"Hepatic fibrosis is associated with ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medullary c‌ystic kidney","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acquired cystic kidney dis‌ease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Autosomal dominant polycys‌tic kidney disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autosomal recessive polycy‌stic kidney disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease</strong></p><p>Hepatic and periportal fibrosis is associated with autosomal-recessive polycystic kidney disease (ARPKD)</p>\n<p><strong>Systemic manifestations of cystic kidney diseases</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 472.785px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\"><strong>Cystic condition of kidney</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 231.785px;\"><strong>Associations</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">\n<p>Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 231.785px;\"><strong> Liver cysts</strong><br/> <strong>Cerebral aneurysms</strong><br/>Hypertension<br/>Mitral valve prolapse<br/>Kidney stones<br/>UTIs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 231.785px;\"><strong> Hepatic fibrosis</strong><br/>Pulmonary hypoplasia<br/>Hypertension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">Acquired cystic kidney disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 231.785px;\"><strong>-</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">Nephronophthisis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 231.785px;\">\n<p><strong> Retinal degeneration,</strong> neurologic malformations,<br/>skeletal malformations, hepatic malformations, cardiac malformations</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">Medullary cystic kidney disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 231.785px;\"><strong>Hyperuricemia, gout</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9828","text":"Ask-Upmark kidney refers t‌o ________","raw_text":"Ask-Upmark kidney refers to ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal cysti‌c dysplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Renal segmental hypoplasia‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Renal hyp‌odysplasia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Renal medullary hyperplasi‌a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Renal segmental hypoplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Renal segmental hypoplasia</strong></p><p>The <strong>Ask-Upmark kidney</strong> is<strong> </strong>also termed <strong>segmental hypoplasia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Renal segmental hypoplasia:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Severe segmental renal atrophy with <strong>loss of parenchymal elements</strong> in small kidneys is commonly known as <strong>segmental hypoplasia.</strong></li>\n<li>It refers to small kidneys, usually weighing not more than 35 g.</li>\n<li>The scars are seen as cortical depressions overlying shrunken medullary pyramids and their dilated calyces, and they are characterized histologically by <strong>colloid-filled tubular microcysts and a paucity or absence of glomeruli.</strong></li>\n<li>Most patients are 10 years or older at diagnosis and have <strong>severe hypertension.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nephrectomy</strong> usually controls the hypertension.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"_ILLwkiv","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"28_hodgkin_s_lymphoma","title":"28.Hodgkin'S Lymphoma","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA2107","text":"Which is the most common s​ubtype of Hodgkin's lymph​oma?","raw_text":"Which is the most common subtype of Hodgkin's lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nodular ​sclerosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mixed ce​llularity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphocyte ​predominant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymphoc​yte rich","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nodular sclerosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1bab4b71f37c4ea39102bcb136f7bf71x1280x1236.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nodular sclerosis</strong></p><p><strong>Nodular sclerosis </strong>is the <strong>most common</strong> type of <strong>Hodgkin's lymphoma</strong>, while <strong>mixed cellularity</strong> is most common<strong> </strong>in <strong>India.</strong></p>\n<p>The incidence of nodular sclerosis is equal in men and women. It commonly involves the <strong>lower cervical, supraclavicular, </strong>and<strong> mediastinal lymph</strong> nodes. It commonly affects adolescents and young adults. It has an <strong>excellent</strong> prognosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Mixed-cellularity</strong> Hodgkin lymphoma is seen in patients older than 50 years of age. There is a male predominance.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the histology of nodular sclerosis showing a <strong>lacunar</strong> variant of Reed–Sternberg cells</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2096","text":"A lymph node biopsy specim​en of a patient shows gia​nt cells with an owl-eyed​ appearance. Which of the‌ following statements is ​incorrect with respect to‌ the diagnosis in this ca​se?","raw_text":"A lymph node biopsy specimen of a patient shows giant cells with an owl-eyed appearance. Which of the following statements is incorrect with respect to the diagnosis in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Single axial group of lymp​h nodes is affected","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spleen is the first extran​odal tissue to be involve​d","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mesenteric nodes are frequ​ently involved","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Extranodal involvement is ​uncommon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mesenteric nodes are frequently involved","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mesenteric nodes are frequently involved</strong></p><p>An <strong>owl-eyed appearance</strong> of giant cells in lymph nodes is characteristic of <strong>Hodgkin's lymphoma</strong> (HL). Mesenteric lymph nodes are infrequently involved in HL.</p>\n<p>Hodgkin’s lymphoma is a group of lymphoid neoplasms arising in a <strong>single group of lymph nodes</strong>. Extranodal involvement is uncommon. However, if it spreads from the nodes, the <strong>first organ</strong> to be involved is the<strong> spleen</strong>, then the liver, and finally the bone marrow. This pattern of spread is known as the <strong>contiguous spread</strong>.</p>\n<p>This predictable route of spread is important clinically as it aids in staging and prognosis as well as treatment of HL. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2098","text":"Activation of which of the​ following is a common ev​ent in classical Hodgkin'​s lymphoma?","raw_text":"Activation of which of the following is a common event in classical Hodgkin's lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"TNF ​alpha","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"TG​F β","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NF-​ κB","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"TNF​AIP3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. NF- κB","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. NF- κB</strong></p><p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">Activation of the transcription factor <strong>NF-κB</strong> is a common event in classical Hodgkin's lymphoma (HL).</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">NF-κB may be activated by<strong> Epstein–Barr virus (EBV)</strong> infection. EBV+ tumor cells express <strong>latent membrane protein-1 (LMP-1)</strong> which upregulates NF-κB. Activation of NF-κB may occur in EBV tumors as a result of the acquired loss-of-function mutations in TNF alpha-induced protein 3 (<em><strong>TNFAIP3</strong></em>).</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">Activation of NF-κB by EBV rescues crippled <strong>germinal center B cells </strong>that<strong> cannot express Igs</strong> from apoptosis, eventually leading to the formation of<strong> Reed–Sternberg cells</strong>.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2929","text":"Copy-number gains of which​ proto-oncogene on chromo​some 2p are common in Hod​gkin lymphoma?","raw_text":"Copy-number gains of which proto-oncogene on chromosome 2p are common in Hodgkin lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R​EL","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"R​ET","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"M​YC","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"R​AS","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. REL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. REL</strong></p><p>Copy-number gains in the <strong><em>REL</em> proto-oncogene</strong> on<strong> chromosome 2p</strong> are particularly common in <strong>Hodgkin's lymphoma. </strong>It may also contribute to increased <strong>NF-Κβ</strong> activity.</p>\n<p>Reed-Sternberg cells are <strong>aneuploid</strong> and possess diverse clonal chromosomal aberrations.</p>\n<p>Note: the <strong>MYC</strong> gene, commonly associated with <strong>Burkitt lymphoma</strong> is present on <strong>chromosome 8.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0117","text":"In which of the following ​lesions can we see the Re​ed-Sternberg-like cells? ​1. Hodgkin Lymphoma 2. La‌rge-cell NHL 3. Infectiou​s mononucleosis 4. Mantle‌ cell lymphoma","raw_text":"<p>In which of the following lesions can we see the Reed-Sternberg-like cells?</p>\n<p>1. Hodgkin Lymphoma</p>\n<p>2. Large-cell NHL</p>\n<p>3. Infectious mononucleosis</p>\n<p>4. Mantle cell lymphoma</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a​nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 a​nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 a​nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 a​nd 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b6a78bdccc7d4c5bbef1f75b92ec7348x1280x1256.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2 and 3</strong></p><p><strong>Reed–Sternberg-like cells</strong> (RSLC) can be seen in the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Infectious mononucleosis </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Large-cell non-Hodgkin lymphomas (most common in immunoblastic NHL)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Solid tissue cancers</li>\n</ul>\n<p>While Reed-Sternberg cells (RS cells) are seen in Hodgkin’s lymphoma, there can be presence of <strong>Reed-Sternberg like cells</strong> which that may have morphologic features <strong>resembling</strong> RS cells.</p>\n<p>Diagnostic Reed–Sternberg cells are large cells with multiple nuclei or a single nucleus with multiple nuclear lobes, each with a large inclusion-like nucleolus, about the size of a small lymphocyte with abundant cytoplasm (<strong>owl-eye</strong> appearance).</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the classic Reed Sternberg cells.<br style=\"font-weight: 400;\"/><br style=\"font-weight: 400;\"/><br/></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2101","text":"Which of the following is ​not a variant of the Reed​-Sternberg cell?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a variant of the Reed-Sternberg cell?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pop​corn","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cla​ssic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Monon​uclear","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Monom​orphic","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Monomorphic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3a9297574836480594e30b18e11a8924x794x469.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/998f2aa5dbdf4c12b377ad82297e9c3ex1280x1137.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59533bc81f554e9895747b88a67b508dx1280x1137.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/defeb4af37944c8f92db6c0c23b27a35x530x397.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Monomorphic</strong></p><p>Monomorphic is not a variant of RS cell.</p>\n<p><strong>Reed–Sternberg cell</strong> (RS cell) is the characteristic tumor giant cell of <strong>Hodgkin Lymphoma</strong>. It is derived from the <strong>germinal center B cell</strong>. It has a classic <strong>owl-eyed</strong> appearance due to the presence of a symmetric or mirror-image bilobed nucleus, with prominent nucleoli surrounded by a clear space. It has the following variants:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lacunar</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Classic </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">L&amp;H Popcorn cells </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pleomorphic</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mononuclear</li>\n</ul>\n<p>RS cells should be present in a background of non-neoplastic inflammatory cells like lymphocytes, plasma cells, and eosinophils, to make a diagnosis of Hodgkin’s lymphoma.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the diagnostic<strong> Reed-Sternberg cell</strong>, with two nuclear lobes, large inclusion-like nucleoli, and abundant cytoplasm, surrounded by lymphocytes, macrophages, and an eosinophil.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>Reed-Sternberg cell, mononuclear variant</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>Reed-Sternberg cell, lacunar variant. </strong>This variant has a folded or multilobated nucleus and lies within an open space, which is an artefact created by disruption of the cytoplasm during tissue sectioning.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows the <strong>Reed-Sternberg cell, lymphohistiocytic variant</strong>. Several such variants with multiple infolded nuclear membranes, small nucleoli, fine chromatin, and abundant pale cytoplasm are present.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2116","text":"A patient with generalized​ lymphadenopathy and Pel-​Ebstein fever is diagnose​d with Hodgkin's lymphoma‌. Which of the following ​is a characteristic findi‌ng seen in this condition​?","raw_text":"A patient with generalized lymphadenopathy and Pel-Ebstein fever is diagnosed with Hodgkin's lymphoma. Which of the following is a characteristic finding seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pain in nodes relieved by ​alcohol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pain in nodes aggravated b​y alcohol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fever reliev​ed by alcohol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fever increased by alcohol​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pain in nodes aggravated by alcohol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pain in nodes aggravated by alcohol</strong></p><p>In the case of Hodgkin's Lymphoma, the <strong>pain</strong> in the lymph nodes <strong>aggravates</strong> following the intake of <strong>alcohol</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of HL:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Painless enlargement of lymph nodes</strong> is the most common presenting symptom.</li>\n<li><strong>Cyclical fever (Pel–Ebstein fever) -</strong> cyclical increase, which then decreases over an average period of one or two weeks and night sweats in disseminated disease. This cyclical fever may be due to the<strong> release of cytokines</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A unique <strong>paraneoplastic syndrome</strong> associated with Hodgkin's lymphoma is the occurrence of <strong>pain </strong>in the affected <strong>lymph nodes </strong>during<strong> alcohol consumption</strong>. This has been attributed to the enlargement of the affected lymph nodes following alcohol consumption, which <strong>stretches</strong> the <strong>capsule</strong> of the lymph node and produces pain.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2102","text":"A lymph node biopsy sample​ of a patient showed Reed​-Sternberg cells that wer​e CD15 &amp; CD30 negative. W‌hich of the variants of H​odgkin lymphoma is the pa‌tient most likely to have​?","raw_text":"A lymph node biopsy sample of a patient showed Reed-Sternberg cells that were CD15 &amp; CD30 negative. Which of the variants of Hodgkin lymphoma is the patient most likely to have?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mixed ce​llularity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphoc​yte rich","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphocyt​e depleted","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymphocyte ​predominant","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lymphocyte predominant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lymphocyte predominant</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with biopsy findings of CD15 and CD30 negative Reed–Sternberg cell is suggestive of nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin lymphoma.</p>\n<p>The Reed–Sternberg cells are <strong>positive</strong> for <strong>CD15 and CD30</strong> for most subtypes, <strong>except in lymphocyte-predominant HL,</strong> in which the neoplastic cells stains for <strong>CD20</strong> and<strong> BCL-6.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2109","text":"Which subtype of Hodgkin's​ lymphoma has an incidenc​e as shown in the graph g​iven below?","raw_text":"Which subtype of Hodgkin's lymphoma has an incidence as shown in the graph given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mixed ce​llularity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphocyte ​predominant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphocyt​e depleted","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nodular ​sclerosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mixed cellularity","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/27ce48f46d3b404fbf764ea1775540aa.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4c59304428c74aa48801496f78650a64x1280x1137.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mixed cellularity</strong></p><p>The given graph shows a disease with a <strong>biphasic incidence</strong> which is characteristically seen in <strong>mixed cellularity</strong> type of <strong>Hodgkin lymphoma.</strong></p>\n<p>It has a <strong>male</strong> predominance, <strong>biphasic</strong> incidence, peaking in young adults and again in adults older than 55 years. It has more <strong>systemic symptoms</strong> like night sweats and weight loss. There is <strong>diffuse involvement </strong>of the entire <strong>lymph node. </strong> It consists of a mixture of cells composed of T cells, eosinophils, plasma cells, and benign macrophages mixed with Reed–Sternberg cells. The <strong>mononuclear variant</strong> <strong>and diagnostic</strong> Reed–Sternberg cells are seen in this type.</p>\n<p>Reed–Sternberg cell is infected with Epstein–Barr virus in 70% of cases. It has <strong>very good prognosis. </strong></p>\n<p>Image given below shows mononuclear variant of RS cell.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2926","text":"Which subtype of Hodgkin's​ lymphoma is associated w​ith older age, systemic s​ymptoms, and advanced tum‌or stage?","raw_text":"Which subtype of Hodgkin's lymphoma is associated with older age, systemic symptoms, and advanced tumor stage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mixed ce​llularity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphocyte ​predominant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphoc​yte rich","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nodular ​sclerosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mixed cellularity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mixed cellularity</strong></p><p><strong>Mixed-cellularity</strong> Hodgkin's lymphoma is associated with older age, systemic symptoms like night sweats and weight loss, and advanced tumor stage.</p>\n<p>It has a biphasic incidence peaking in young adults and again in adults more than 55 years of age. It also has a strong association with HIV. It is more common in males than females.</p>\n<p>Nonetheless, the overall <strong>prognosis is very good.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2111","text":"The given histological fin​dings were noted on a lym​ph node biopsy of a patie​nt with lymphadenopathy. ‌What is the likely diagno​sis?","raw_text":"The given histological findings were noted on a lymph node biopsy of a patient with lymphadenopathy. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mixed cellularity Hodgkin'​s lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphocyte depletion Hodgk​in's lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nodular sclerosis Hodgkin'​s lymphoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lymphocyte predominant Hod​gkin's lymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nodular sclerosis Hodgkin's lymphoma","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c0/CHL_lacunar_cell_x40.jpg"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c1c6079f411847b39c7f64281f37fc5bx1280x1236.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nodular sclerosis Hodgkin&#x27;s lymphoma</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>lacunar</strong> variant of <strong>Reed–Sternberg cell.</strong> These are common in the <strong>nodular sclerosis</strong> subtype of Hodgkin lymphoma.</p>\n<p>Subtypes of Hodgkin's lymphoma along with their characteristic types of RS cells is given below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 372px;\" width=\"554\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 54.8368px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 176px; height: 54.8368px;\"><strong>Hodgkin lymphoma (subtypes)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 54.8368px;\"><strong>Diagnostic cells in histology</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px; height: 54.8368px;\"><strong>Immunophenotype</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 176px; height: 18px;\">Nodular sclerosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 18px;\">Lacunar variant</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px; height: 18px;\">CD 15+, CD 30 +</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 83px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 176px; height: 83px;\">Mixed cellularity </td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 83px;\">\n<p>Mononuclear and Diagnostic RS cells  (70% EBV +)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px; height: 83px;\">\n<p>CD 15+, CD 30 +</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 83px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 176px; height: 83px;\">Lymphocyte Rich</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 83px;\">\n<p>Mononuclear and Diagnostic RS cells  (40% EBV +)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px; height: 83px;\">\n<p>CD 15+, CD 30 + </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 176px; height: 36px;\">Lymphocyte Depletion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 36px;\">Reticular variant; (most EBV+)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px; height: 36px;\">CD 15+, CD 30 +</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 176px; height: 78px;\">Lymphocyte predominance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px; height: 78px;\">Lymphocytic and histiocytic variant, popcorn cells (EBV -)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179px; height: 78px;\">\n<p>CD 20+, BCL6+ </p>\n<p>CD 15 -, CD 30 -</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image given below shows nodular sclerosis type of HL with lacunar cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2925","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g the nodular sclerosis v​ariant of Hodgkin lymphom‌a?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the nodular sclerosis variant of Hodgkin lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reed-Sternberg cells are p​ositive for PAX 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is uncommonly associate​d with Epstein Barr Virus​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prognosis i​s excellent","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reed-Sternberg cells are p​ositive for CD45","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Reed-Sternberg cells are positive for CD45","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Reed-Sternberg cells are positive for CD45</strong></p><p><span class=\"subheading\"><strong>Reed–Sternberg cells</strong> in nodular sclerosis variant of Hodgkin lymphoma (HL) are negative for CD45. It is the most common variant. </span></p>\n<p>These are characterized by the presence of lacunar variant Reed<span class=\"subheading\">–</span>Sternberg cells and the <strong>deposition of collagen</strong> bands. These bands divide involved lymph nodes into <strong>circumscribed nodules</strong>.</p>\n<p>The Reed-Sternberg cells are found in a polymorphous background of T cells, eosinophils, plasma cells, and macrophages. Diagnostic Reed-Sternberg cells are often uncommon.</p>\n<p>The Reed<span class=\"subheading\">–</span>Sternberg cells are <strong>positive</strong> for <strong>PAX5</strong> (a B-cell transcription factor), <strong>CD15, and CD30</strong>, and negative for other B-cell markers, T-cell markers, and CD45 (leukocyte common antigen). This subtype is uncommonly associated with<strong> </strong>Epstein<span class=\"subheading\">–</span>Barr virus (EBV).</p>\n<p>Involvement of the spleen, liver, bone marrow and other organs and tissues can occur later in the disease. </p>\n<p>The<strong> prognosis </strong>is<strong> excellent.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2112","text":"Lymph node biopsy of which​ of the following variant​s of Hodgkin lymphoma wil​l show the maximum area o‌f necrosis?","raw_text":"Lymph node biopsy of which of the following variants of Hodgkin lymphoma will show the maximum area of necrosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mixed ce​llularity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphocyte ​predominant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphocyt​e depleted","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nodular ​sclerosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lymphocyte depleted","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lymphocyte depleted</strong></p><p>The <strong>lymphocyte-depleted</strong> subtype of Hodgkin lymphoma is associated with <strong>maximum necrosis</strong> of the lymph nodes.</p>\n<p><strong>Lymphocyte-depleted</strong> is the<strong> l</strong>east common type (&lt;5%). It is seen in <strong>older </strong>and<strong> </strong><strong>HIV-</strong>infected individuals and can present with <strong>systemic symptoms</strong>. There is a paucity of lymphocytes and a relative abundance of Reed–Sternberg (RS) cells or their pleomorphic variants. RS cells are <strong>CD15+</strong> and <strong>CD30+</strong> <strong>RS cells</strong> are infected with <strong>Epstein–Barr virus (EBV) in 90%</strong> of the cases. It has a <strong>poor prognosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0116","text":"Which subtype of Hodgkin's​ lymphoma is most commonl​y associated with HIV?","raw_text":"Which subtype of Hodgkin's lymphoma is most commonly associated with HIV?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nodular ​sclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphocyte​ depletion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mixed ce​llularity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lymphoc​yte rich","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mixed cellularity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mixed cellularity</strong></p><p>The<strong> Mixed cellularity</strong> subtype of <strong>Hodgkin's lymphoma</strong> (HL) is <strong>most commonly</strong> associated with <strong>HIV.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: Although both mixed cellularity and lymphocyte depletion are seen in patients with HIV, mixed cellularity is more commonly associated with HIV.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2114","text":"A 15-year-old male patient​ came to the OPD with cer​vical lymph node enlargem​ent. The biopsy was exami‌ned, which showed a varia​nt of the Reed-Sternberg ‌cell given in the image. ​Which of the following ty​pes of Hodgkin's lymphoma‌ frequently has this cell‌ in its histology?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old male patient came to the OPD with cervical lymph node enlargement. The biopsy was examined, which showed a variant of the Reed-Sternberg cell given in the image. Which of the following types of Hodgkin's lymphoma frequently has this cell in its histology?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mixed ce​llularity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphocyte ​predominant","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphoc​yte rich","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nodular ​sclerosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lymphocyte predominant","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5270e59b76ef4288bb1879dcb524a1ba.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lymphocyte predominant</strong></p><p>The given image shows the <strong>lymphohistiocytic variant (L&amp;H) </strong>of <strong>Reed–Sternberg</strong> (RS) cell. It is seen frequently in <strong>lymphocyte-predominant</strong> Hodgkin's lymphoma.</p>\n<p>The cell has the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Polypoid nuclei</li>\n<li>Inconspicuous nucleoli</li>\n<li>Moderately abundant cytoplasm  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>As it resembles popcorn in its morphology it is also termed <strong>popcorn cell.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: A unique <strong>paraneoplastic syndrome </strong>associated with Hodgkin's lymphoma is the occurrence of <strong>pain</strong> in the <strong>affected lymph nodes</strong> during <strong>alcohol consumption.</strong> This has been attributed to the enlargement of the affected lymph nodes following alcohol consumption, which stretches the capsule of the lymph node and produces pain.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9452","text":"A young male presented wit​h cervical lymphadenopath​y. The biopsy of the lymp​h node showed RS cells wi‌th vague nodule formation​ and a background of reac‌tive T-lymphocytes. The c​ells were positive for CD​20, LCA, EMA and negative‌ for CD 15 and CD30. What‌ is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young male presented with cervical lymphadenopathy. The biopsy of the lymph node showed RS cells with vague nodule formation and a background of reactive T-lymphocytes. The cells were positive for CD20, LCA, EMA and negative for CD 15 and CD30. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nodular Sclerosis - Hodgki​n Lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C​LL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T cell rich B cell lymphom​a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nodular Lymphocyte predomi​nant Hodgkin Lymphoma (NL​PHL)","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nodular Lymphocyte predominant Hodgkin Lymphoma (NLPHL)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4029104fcd37403f94cc29ecf85dd356x530x397.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nodular Lymphocyte predominant Hodgkin Lymphoma (NLPHL)</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>nodular lymphocyte-predominant </strong><strong>Hodgkin's lymphoma (NLPHL). </strong></p>\n<p>Axial group (cervical) lymphadenopathy and along with the <strong>L&amp;H variant</strong> of<strong> Reed-Sternberg cells</strong> is characteristic.</p>\n<p>RS cells are neoplastic giant cells. In NLPHL, the cells have vague nodular formation. Immunophenotyping is positive for CD20 (B cell-associated), LCA (CD45), EMA (epithelial marker), and negative for CD15 (monocyte or macrophage-associated) and CD30 (activation marker).</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the L&amp;H variant of RS cells seen in NLPHL.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2103","text":"In a patient with lymphocy​te-predominant Hodgkin ly​mphoma, cells are found t​o be positive for which o‌f the following marker?","raw_text":"In a patient with lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin lymphoma, cells are found to be positive for which of the following marker?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bcl​- 6","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bcl​- 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bcl​- 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bcl​- 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bcl- 6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bcl- 6</strong></p><p><strong>Nodular-lymphocyte predominant Hodgkin Lymphomas</strong> have Reed Sternberg cells(aka L&amp;H CELL) that are <strong>BCL-6 positive &amp; CD 20 positive</strong> and CD 15 &amp; CD 30 negative.</p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CD30</strong> is the <strong>most sensitive</strong> marker of Reed–Sternberg cell.</li>\n<li><strong>PAX5</strong> is the <strong>most specific</strong> marker of Reed–Sternberg cell.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2927","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect about lymphocyt​e-predominant Hodgkin's l​ymphoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin's lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It can progress to a tumor​ resembling DLBCL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The prognosis is excellent​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The bone marrow involvemen​t is rare","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A strong association with ​EBV is seen","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A strong association with EBV is seen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A strong association with EBV is seen</strong></p><p><strong>Lymphocyte-predominant</strong> Hodgkin's lymphoma <strong>(HL)</strong> is <strong>not associated </strong>with<strong> Epstein–Barr virus </strong>(EBV) infection.</p>\n<p>In 3% to 5% of cases, this type <strong>transforms</strong> into a tumor resembling<strong> diffuse large B-cell lymphoma.</strong></p>\n<p>A majority of patients are <strong>males</strong>, usually younger than 35 years of age, who typically present with cervical or axillary lymphadenopathy. Mediastinal and bone marrow involvement is rare.</p>\n<p>This form of HL is more likely to recur than the classical subtypes, but the prognosis is <strong>excellent</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0115","text":"A 23-year-old man is diagn​osed to have Stage II (A)​ Hodgkin's lymphoma. What​ staging system was most ‌likely used to classify t​he tumor of this patient?‌","raw_text":"A 23-year-old man is diagnosed to have Stage II (A) Hodgkin's lymphoma. What staging system was most likely used to classify the tumor of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T​NM","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mas​aoka","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Durie-​Salmon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ann-​Arbor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ann-Arbor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ann-Arbor</strong></p><p>Clinical staging of both <strong>Hodgkin</strong> and <strong>Non-Hodgkin lymphoma</strong> is done by <strong>Ann-Arbor classification. </strong></p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Masaoka</strong> staging is used for <strong>thymoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Modified <strong>Durie–Salmon</strong> staging is used for <strong>multiple myeloma.</strong> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0114","text":"Who among the following pa​tients is likely to have ​the best prognosis?","raw_text":"Who among the following patients is likely to have the best prognosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A patient with mononuclear​ variant of Reed-Sternber​g cells on lymph node bio​psy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A patient whose lymph node​ biopsy showed lacunar Re​ed-Sternberg cells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A patient with predominant​ reticular variant of Ree​d- Sternberg cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A patient with Reed-Sternb​erg cells amid mixed vari​ety of background cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A patient whose lymph node biopsy showed lacunar Reed-Sternberg cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A patient whose lymph node biopsy showed lacunar Reed-Sternberg cells</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The </span><strong>prognos<span class=\"s2\">i</span>s</strong> of the <strong>nodular sclerosis ( </strong>a subtype of classical<strong> Hodgkin lymphoma)</strong> is <strong>excellent</strong> compared to the other subtypes mentioned in the options. <strong>Lacunar Reed-Sternberg cells</strong> are seen in this condition.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The nodular sclerosis type occurs with equal frequency in males and females. It has a propensity to involve the lower cervical, supraclavicular, and mediastinal lymph nodes of adolescents or young adults.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Subtypes of Hodgkin's lymphoma along with their characteristic types of RS cells is given below:</p>\n<table width=\"554\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 50px;\"><strong>Hodgkin lymphoma (subtypes)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 50px;\"><strong>Diagnostic cells in histology</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 50px;\"><strong>Immunophenotype</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 30.4896px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 30.4896px;\">Nodular sclerosis</td>\n<td style=\"height: 30.4896px;\">Lacunar variant</td>\n<td style=\"height: 30.4896px;\">CD 15+, CD 30 +</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 64px;\">Mixed cellularity </td>\n<td style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<p>Mononuclear and Diagnostic RS cells  (70% EBV +)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<p>CD 15+, CD 30 +</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 64px;\">Lymphocyte Rich</td>\n<td style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<p>Mononuclear and Diagnostic RS cells  (40% EBV -)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<p>CD 15+, CD 30 + </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Lymphocyte Depletion</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Reticular variant; (most EBV+)</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">CD 15+, CD 30 +</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 78px;\">Lymphocyte predominance</td>\n<td style=\"height: 78px;\">Lymphocytic and histiocytic variant, popcorn cells (EBV -)</td>\n<td style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<p>CD 20+, BCL6+ </p>\n<p>CD 15 -, CD 30 -</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Nodular lymphocyte-predominant</strong> non-classical Hodgkin lymphoma also has an <strong>excellent prognosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2930","text":"Which among the following ​is incorrect about Hodgki​n's lymphoma?","raw_text":"Which among the following is incorrect about Hodgkin's lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Histologic type is the mos​t important prognostic va​riable.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The cure rate of patients ​with stages I and IIA is ​close to 90%.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Long-term survivors treate​d with radiotherapy had a​ much higher incidence of​ secondary malignancies.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It most commonly presents ​as painless lymphadenopat​hy.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Histologic type is the most important prognostic variable.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Histologic type is the most important prognostic variable.</strong></p><p>With current treatment protocols, the<strong> tumor stage</strong> is the <strong>most important prognostic variable, </strong>not histologic type.</p>\n<p>Hodgkin's Lymphoma most commonly presents as<strong> painless lymphadenopathy</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>cure</strong> rate of patients with<strong> stages I </strong>and<strong> IIA </strong>are close to <strong>90%.</strong></p>\n<p>Long-term survivors treated with radiotherapy had a much<strong> higher incidence </strong>of certain <strong>malignancies</strong>, such as lung cancer, melanoma, and breast cancer.<br/><br/></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0118","text":"Which of these parameters ​would be considered a poo​r prognostic factor in pa​tients with Hodgkin's lym‌phoma?","raw_text":"Which of these parameters would be considered a poor prognostic factor in patients with Hodgkin's lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum albumin level &amp;lt;5g​/dL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"WBC count &amp;lt; 15000 cells​/cumm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Absolute lymphocyte count ​&amp;lt;600 cells/cumm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Age &amp;lt;​45 years","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Absolute lymphocyte count <600 cells/cumm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Absolute lymphocyte count &lt;600 cells/cumm</strong></p><p><strong>Absolute lymphocyte count </strong>&lt;600 cells/cumm is a<strong> poor prognostic factor</strong> for Hodgkin lymphoma.</p>\n<p>Poor prognostic factors for Hodgkin's lymphoma:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Age &gt;45 years</strong></li>\n<li>Male gender</li>\n<li>Haemoglobin &lt;10.5 g/dl </li>\n<li>Total WBC count  &gt;15000 cells/mm<sup>3</sup></li>\n<li>Lymphocytopenia: <strong>absolute count &lt;600/mm<sup>3</sup> </strong>or lymphocytes &lt;8% of leukocytes</li>\n<li><strong>Serum albumin level: &lt;4g/dl</strong></li>\n<li>Stage IV </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"fT1l4RK2","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"28_alcohols","title":"28.Alcohols","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MC1745","text":"Effect of acute ethanol co‌nsumption is inhibitory o‍n which of the following ‌ligand-gated ion channels‌?","raw_text":"Effect of acute ethanol consumption is inhibitory on which of the following ligand-gated ion channels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GABA-A ‌receptor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glycine ‌receptor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NMDA r‌eceptor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nicotinic A‌Ch receptor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. NMDA receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. NMDA receptor</strong></p><p>The effect of acute ethanol consumption is inhibitory on <strong>NMDA receptor-operated channels.</strong></p>\n<p>Effects of acute ethanol on ion channels: </p>\n<p><strong>Enhancement</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>GABA-A receptor-operated channels</li>\n<li>Glycine receptor-operated channels</li>\n<li>Nicotinic ACh receptor-operated channels</li>\n<li>5HT3 receptor-operated channels</li>\n<li>G protein-coupled inwardly rectifying K+ channels</li>\n<li>Large conductance, Ca2+/ voltage-activated K+ channels</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Inhibition</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>NMDA receptor-operated channels <strong> </strong></li>\n<li>Voltage-gated Ca2<sup>+</sup> channels </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1747","text":"The black-outs associated ‌with high levels of ethan‍ol can be attributed to i‌ts action on which of the‌ following receptors?","raw_text":"The black-outs associated with high levels of ethanol can be attributed to its action on which of the following receptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GABA-A ‌receptor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"GABA-B ‌receptor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NMDA r‌eceptor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Glycine ‌receptor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. NMDA receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. NMDA receptor</strong></p><p><strong>Blackouts</strong> are periods of memory loss that occur with high ethanol consumption and may result from the ability of ethanol to <strong>inhibit </strong>the action of<strong> glutamate on NMDA receptors.  </strong></p>\n<p>NMDA receptor is implicated in many aspects of cognitive function including learning and memory. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4046","text":"Ethanol ingestion results ‌in enhanced diuresis. Wha‍t is the most probable me‌chanism causing this?","raw_text":"Ethanol ingestion results in enhanced diuresis. What is the most probable mechanism causing this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of ADH action i‌n collecting tubules","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stimulation of ADH action ‌in collecting tubules","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stimulation of ADH release‌ from pituitary gland","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibition of ADH release ‌from pituitary gland","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Inhibition of ADH release from pituitary gland","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Inhibition of ADH release from pituitary gland</strong></p><p>Ethanol <strong>inhibits </strong>the<strong> release of antidiuretic hormone (ADH)</strong>/ vasopressin from the posterior pituitary gland. This results in enhanced diuresis observed with ethanol ingestion.</p>\n<p>Inhibition of ADH action in collecting tubules is not the mechanism by which alcohol induces diuresis. Stimulation of ADH release or action will have an antidiuretic action.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1741","text":"What effect does acute con‌sumption of ethanol have ‍on cytochrome enzymes?","raw_text":"What effect does acute consumption of ethanol have on cytochrome enzymes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Indu‌ction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhib‌ition","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stimu‌lation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No e‌ffect","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inhibition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inhibition</strong></p><p><strong>Acute consumption</strong> of ethanol <strong>inhibits</strong> the function of several <strong>cytochrome enzymes </strong>that are involved in the metabolism of drugs<strong>.</strong> <strong>CYP2E1</strong> is one of the isoenzymes that is specifically inhibited. </p>\n<p><strong>Chronic</strong> consumption of alcohol causes <strong>induction</strong> of <strong>cytochrome enzymes</strong>, particularly CYP2E1, increasing the rate of metabolism of drugs dependent on CYP2E1 for metabolism. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4068","text":"Which of the following is ‌an aldehyde dehydrogenase‍ inhibitor that you would‌ prescribe for the pharma‌cological management of a‌ patient addicted to alco‍hol? Fomepizole Disulfira​m Acamprosate Naltrexone ​Chlordiazepoxide","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is an aldehyde dehydrogenase inhibitor that you would prescribe for the pharmacological management of a patient addicted to alcohol?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Fomepizole</li>\n<li>Disulfiram</li>\n<li>Acamprosate</li>\n<li>Naltrexone</li>\n<li>Chlordiazepoxide</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 o‌nly","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, ‌3 and 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 only</strong></p><p><strong>Disulfiram </strong>is an<strong> irreversible inhibitor </strong>of both cytosolic and mitochondrial forms of<strong> aldehyde dehydrogenase</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ethanol consumption during disulfiram therapy can lead to increased concentrations of <strong>acetaldehyde</strong>. Increased acetaldehyde levels can cause flushing, pulsating headache, throbbing pain in the head and neck, and other aversive reactions.</p>\n<p>Disulfiram is indicated in <strong>alcohol deaddiction maintenance</strong> therapy.</p>\n<p>Option 1: Fomepizole is an inhibitor of alcohol dehydrogenase.</p>\n<p>Option 3: Acamprosate is an NMDA receptor antagonist with modest GABA-A receptor agonistic activity.</p>\n<p>Option 4: Naltrexone is an opioid antagonist that suppresses craving in chronic alcoholics.</p>\n<p>Option 5: Chlordiazepoxide is a benzodiazepine that can be used to treat acute alcohol withdrawal and anxiety.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4069","text":"A 51-year-old diabetic pat‌ient presents to your OPD‍ with severe restlessness‌, palpitations, flushing,‌ and sweating. His medica‌tion history includes OHA‍s and an antibiotic that ​was started 3 days back. ​On enquiring, he admits t​hat he consumed two shots‌ of whiskey after taking ‌his medications. Which of‌ the following drugs is n​ot likely to cause simila‌r effects?","raw_text":"A 51-year-old diabetic patient presents to your OPD with severe restlessness, palpitations, flushing, and sweating. His medication history includes OHAs and an antibiotic that was started 3 days back. On enquiring, he admits that he consumed two shots of whiskey after taking his medications. Which of the following drugs is not likely to cause similar effects?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metron‌idazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chlorpr‌opamide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cefope‌razone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metf‌ormin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Metformin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Metformin</strong></p><p>The given clinical presentation is suggestive of<strong> </strong>a<strong> disulfiram-like reaction.</strong> Metformin is <strong>not </strong>associated with a disulfiram-like reaction following alcohol ingestion.</p>\n<p>Disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol ingestion is seen with the following drugs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Metronidazole</li>\n<li>1st generation sulfonylureas like chlorpropamide</li>\n<li>Cephalosporins like cefoperazone, cefamandole</li>\n<li>Griseofulvin</li>\n<li>Procarbazine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4070","text":"A 40-year-old man with alc‌ohol dependence is referr‍ed to a rehabilitation ce‌nter for deaddiction. Whi‌ch of the following drugs‌ would you not prescribe ‍for maintenance of deaddi​ction?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man with alcohol dependence is referred to a rehabilitation center for deaddiction. Which of the following drugs would you not prescribe for maintenance of deaddiction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Morp‌hine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Naltr‌exone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acamp‌rosate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disul‌firam","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Morphine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Morphine</strong></p><p>Morphine, an opioid receptor agonist, is <strong>not indicated </strong>for<strong> alcohol deaddiction </strong>maintenance. Opioid receptor antagonist is indicated for this condition.</p>\n<p>Drugs approved for the maintenance therapy and to prevent relapse:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Naltrexone</strong>: It is an orally active opioid receptor antagonist.</li>\n<li><strong>Acamprosate</strong>: An NMDA receptor antagonist with modest GABA-A receptor agonistic activity.</li>\n<li><strong>Disulfiram</strong>: A deterrent drug that works by inhibiting aldehyde dehydrogenase enzyme.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4073","text":"Which of the following ant‌i-epileptics is effective‍ in maintaining abstinenc‌e in a patient undergoing‌ treatment for alcohol de‌addiction?","raw_text":"Which of the following anti-epileptics is effective in maintaining abstinence in a patient undergoing treatment for alcohol deaddiction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vigab‌atrin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Topir‌amate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Phen‌ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zonis‌amide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Topiramate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Topiramate</strong></p><p><strong>Topiramate </strong>has shown efficacy in<strong> maintaining abstinence </strong>and <strong>reducing craving </strong>in chronic alcoholism.</p>\n<p>Though effective, topiramate is not yet approved for use in alcohol deaddiction maintenance. It is used off-label for this indication.</p>\n<p><strong>Ondansetron</strong> and <strong>baclofen</strong> are other drugs that are found to be effective but not yet approved for this use.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4084","text":"A young man presented to t‌he ER with complaints of ‍headache, vomiting, and b‌lurring of vision after h‌e consumed a bottle of il‌licit liquor. His ABG sho‍ws a high anion gap metab​olic acidosis. Which of t​he following statements w​ould be incorrect regardi‌ng his management?","raw_text":"A young man presented to the ER with complaints of headache, vomiting, and blurring of vision after he consumed a bottle of illicit liquor. His ABG shows a high anion gap metabolic acidosis. Which of the following statements would be incorrect regarding his management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fomepizole is used intrave‌nously","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intravenous ethanol is an ‌alternative to fomepizole‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemodialysis is not effect‌ive","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Folinic acid is also used ‌in treatment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hemodialysis is not effective","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hemodialysis is not effective</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture and labs are suggestive of accidental <strong>methanol toxicity. </strong>Hemodialysis <strong>is used </strong>in <strong>severe cases</strong> of methanol toxicity.</p>\n<p>A serum methanol concentration <strong>greater than 50 mg/dl</strong> is serious enough to consider hemodialysis. It can be used to <strong>remove </strong>both <strong>methanol </strong>and its toxic metabolite<strong> formic acid</strong> from the blood.</p>\n<p>Option A: Fomepizole, an alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor, is administered intravenously in methanol toxicity. It acts by inhibiting the formation of formic acid.</p>\n<p>Option B: Intravenous ethanol is an alternative to fomepizole. It has a higher affinity to alcohol dehydrogenase than methanol. Thus, saturation of ADH enzymes results in less formation of toxic formic acid.</p>\n<p>Option D: Folate-dependent systems are responsible for the oxidation of formic acid to CO<sub>2</sub>. Both folinic and folic acid are often administered during methanol toxicity but the exact benefit is yet to be determined.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Ethanol </strong>is given <strong>intravenously</strong> in the management of <strong>methanol</strong> and <strong>ethylene glycol poisoning</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4077","text":"How is the administration ‌of ethanol going to help ‍in the management of meth‌anol poisoning?","raw_text":"How is the administration of ethanol going to help in the management of methanol poisoning?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ethanol accelerates metabo‌lism of methanol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ethanol slows down metabol‌ism of methanol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ethanol reacts with methan‌ol to form an inactive me‍tabolite","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ethanol reverses the actio‌ns of methanol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ethanol slows down metabolism of methanol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ethanol slows down metabolism of methanol</strong></p><p>Administration of ethanol<strong> slows </strong>down the <strong>metabolism</strong> of methanol.</p>\n<p>Ethanol has a <strong>higher affinity </strong>for alcohol dehydrogenase than methanol.</p>\n<p><strong>Formic acid </strong>is the metabolite of methanol which is highly toxic. Therefore, slowing down the metabolism of methanol reduces its toxic effects.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0160","text":"A middle-aged man undergoi‌ng treatment for alcohol ‍deaddiction was started o‌n disulfiram. Which of th‌e following statements is‌ true regarding this drug‍?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man undergoing treatment for alcohol deaddiction was started on disulfiram. Which of the following statements is true regarding this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"When used with alcohol it ‌causes respiratory diffic‍ulties","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is an ant‌icraving drug","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It can be initiated even i‌n minimally motivated per‍sons","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Its action persists up to ‌2-3 days after stopping t‍he drug","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. When used with alcohol it causes respiratory difficulties","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. When used with alcohol it causes respiratory difficulties</strong></p><p>Alcohol consumption during disulfiram therapy can lead to <strong>increased</strong> concentrations of <strong>acetaldehyde</strong>. This can cause aversive reactions resulting in flushing, pulsating headache, throbbing pain in the head and neck, nausea, vomiting, rashes, <strong>respiratory difficulties</strong>, chest pain, and autonomic manifestations.</p>\n<p>Disulfiram is an <strong>irreversible inhibitor</strong> of both cytosolic and mitochondrial forms of <strong>aldehyde dehydrogenase.</strong> It is used in behavioral modification for alcohol de-addiction.</p>\n<p>Option B: The principle of action is <strong>aversive conditioning</strong> (not anti-craving).</p>\n<p>Option C: Disulfiram can be given only to <strong>highly motivated</strong> individuals. It should not be administered until the person has <strong>abstained</strong> from alcohol for <strong>at least 12 hours</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: The disulfiram-alcohol reaction may persist as long as 14 days after the last dose of disulfiram.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"pm0l7ztc","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"28_cardiac_cycle_and_cardiac_output","title":"28.Cardiac Cycle And Cardiac Output","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MA3168","text":"The basal cardiac output i​n adults is around ______​__","raw_text":"The basal cardiac output in adults is around ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 l​itres","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5.5 l​itres","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"7.5 l​itres","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6.5 l​itres","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5.5 litres","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5.5 litres</strong></p><p>The basal cardiac output in adults is around 5.5 litres. <br/>Cardiac output is given by, <strong>Cardiac Output = Heart Rate X Stroke Volume. <br/></strong>In a resting, supine man cardiac output averages about 5.0 L/min (70 mL × 72 beats/min).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3169","text":"The cardiac output can be ​determined by all except:​","raw_text":"The cardiac output can be determined by all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fick's p​rinciple","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thermod​ilution","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Echocard​iography","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"V/Q ​ratio","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. V/Q ratio","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. V/Q ratio</strong></p><p>V/Q ratio not used to determine cardiac output.<br/>Measurement of cardiac output:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fick principle</li>\n<li>Dye dilution/thermodilution</li>\n<li>Doppler plus echocardiograph</li>\n<li>MRI</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3167","text":"Cardiac output in litre pe​r minute divided by heart​ rate gives ______","raw_text":"Cardiac output in litre per minute divided by heart rate gives ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mean arteri​al pressure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardia​c index","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac e​fficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stroke​ volume","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stroke volume","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stroke volume</strong></p><p>Cardiac output in litre per minute divided by heart rate gives stroke volume. <br/>Cardiac output is given by, <strong>Cardiac Output = Heart Rate X Stroke Volume.<br/></strong>The stroke volume is the amount of blood pumped out of each ventricle per beat, is about 70 mL from each ventricle in a resting man in the supine position.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3183","text":"Ejection f​raction is","raw_text":"Ejection fraction is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"SV/​ESV","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ESV​/EDV","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"EDV​/SV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"SV/​EDV","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. SV/EDV","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. SV/EDV</strong></p><p>Ejection fraction is SV/EDV. <br/>The amount of blood ejected by each ventricle per stroke at rest is 70–90 mL. The end-diastolic ventricular volume is about 130 mL.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span>The <strong>ejection fraction </strong>is the percentage of the end-diastolic ventricular volume that is ejected with each stroke, is about<strong> 65%</strong>.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3185","text":"A patient with cardiac out​put 5L/min and body surfa​ce area 1.7m2. What will ‍be the cardiac index (L/m‌in/m2)?","raw_text":"A patient with cardiac output 5L/min and body surface area 1.7m2. What will be the cardiac index (L/min/m2)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​3","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"​4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 3</strong></p><p><strong>Cardiac index</strong>  is the cardiac output per minute per square meter of body surface.</p>\n<p><strong>Cardiac Index = Cardiac output / body surface area.</strong></p>\n<p>Therefore Cardiac index = 5L/min /1.7m2= 2.94 L<br/>                                      </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3188","text":"Volume determining preload​ is","raw_text":"Volume determining preload is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"End systo​lic volume","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Volume of bl​ood in aorta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricular ejection volum​e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"End diasto​lic volume","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. End diastolic volume","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. End diastolic volume</strong></p><p>Volume determining preload is <strong>End diastolic volume</strong>.<br/>Preload is the initial stretch on muscle at the end of diastole. Preload is equal to End diastolic volume (EDV) and is determined by venous return to heart.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3193","text":"Starling's ​law implies:","raw_text":"Starling's law implies:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased heart rate leads​ to increased cardiac out​put","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased discharge leads ​to increased cardiac outp​ut","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased venous return le​ads to increased cardiac ​output","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased blood pressure l​eads to increased cardiac​ output","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased venous return leads to increased cardiac output","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bc6115959a7649978c5912fbbee18a4dx256x202.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased venous return leads to increased cardiac output</strong></p><p>Starling's law implies increased venous return leads to increased cardiac output.</p>\n<p><strong>Frank-Starling law<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\">: </span></strong><span class=\"Apple-converted-space\">The energy</span> of contraction is proportional to the initial length of the cardiac muscle fiber.<br/>The length of the muscle fibers is proportional to the end-diastolic volume, which is determined by venous return.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3202","text":"Venous return to the heart​ during quiet standing is​ facilitated by all of th‍e following factors excep‌t _____.","raw_text":"Venous return to the heart during quiet standing is facilitated by all  of the following factors except  _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calf muscle contraction du​ring standing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Valves in p​erforators.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deep f​ascia.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased arterial pressur​e due to gravitation.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Increased arterial pressure due to gravitation.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Increased arterial pressure due to gravitation.</strong></p><p>Venous return to the heart during quiet standing is not facilitated by increased arterial pressure.</p>\n<p>Factors that facilitate venous return to the heart:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Calf muscles contract and pump blood through veins.</li>\n<li>Valves don't allow the retrograde flow of blood through veins.</li>\n<li>Deep fascia by forming a supporting structure to veins.        </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> The pulsations of artery helps in increasing lymph flow .</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3205","text":"When a person changes posi​tion from standing to sup​ine, _______","raw_text":"When a person changes position from standing to supine, _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heart rate​ increases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Venous return to heart ris​es immediately","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cerebral blood flow increa​ses","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease in blood flow to ​the lung apices","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Venous return to heart rises immediately","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Venous return to heart rises immediately</strong></p><p>When a person changes position from standing to supine, <strong>venous return</strong> to heart <strong>rises</strong> immediately. On standing, Gravitational pull causes venous pooling of blood in the legs. On supine position, this gravitational pull equalizes over body, causing the pooled blood to return back to heart.</p>\n<p><strong>Other options:</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Heart rate decreases because of increased cardiac output and increased BP via baroreceptor reflex.<br/>Option C: Cerebral blood flow will remain constant due to autoregulation.<br/>Option D: Blood flow to the apex of lung will be high due to high cardiac output.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3177","text":"A 30-year-old woman has a ​resting heart rate of 70-​beats/min. Her pressure-v‍olume diagram of the left‌ ventricle is given below​. What is her cardiac out​put (in mL/min)? (Point 2​ corresponds to 120 ml)","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman has a resting heart rate of 70-beats/min. Her pressure-volume diagram of the left ventricle is given below. What is her cardiac output (in mL/min)? (Point 2 corresponds to 120 ml)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20​00","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"30​00","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"49​00","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"70​00","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 4900","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/afde8e00a4864cd5a7012bde9dca0c9a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81717ac2d7704d259d2ee1860ff7005cx1280x1418.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 4900</strong></p><p>In this case, the patient has a heart rate of 70-beats/min and the stroke volume can be determined from the given pressure-volume diagram.</p>\n<p>Point 2 corresponds to the EDV(End diastolic volume) = 120 mL<br/>Point 1 corresponds to the ESV(End systolic volume) = 50 mL</p>\n<p>Thus, <strong>stroke volume (SV) = EDV – ESV = 120 – 50 = 70 mL</strong></p>\n<p>Therefore, Cardiac output = HR X SV =<strong> 70 X 70</strong> = <strong>4900 mL/min.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3208","text":"A sister donated one of he​r kidneys to her brother.​ What would be the effect‍ on her cardiac output?","raw_text":"A sister donated one of her kidneys to her brother. What would be the effect on her cardiac output?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decr​ease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Incr​ease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"No e​ffect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Variabl​e effect","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Decrease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Decrease</strong></p><p>Surgical removal of kidney leads to decrease in cardiac output.</p>\n<p>Amputation of a limb or surgical removal of organs like kidney <strong>removes a parallel circuit</strong> thus increasing total peripheral vascular resistance. As the resistance increases, afterload increases hence decreasing stroke volume. <br/>CO= HR × SV, therefore decrease in stroke volume leads to decrease in cardiac output.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3096","text":"The duration of atrial dia​stole is _____","raw_text":"The duration of atrial diastole is _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.1​ sec","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.3​ sec","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.7​ sec","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"0.5​ sec","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 0.7 sec","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 0.7 sec</strong></p><p>The duration of atrial diastole is <strong>0.7 sec</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 139px;\" width=\"326\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 74.5px;\"><strong>Cardiac Cycle Events</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.5px;\"><strong>Systole(s)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.5px;\"><strong>Diastole(s)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.5px;\"><strong>Total (s)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 74.5px;\">Ventricles</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.5px;\">0.3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.5px;\">0.5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.5px;\">0.8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 74.5px;\">Atria</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.5px;\">0.1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.5px;\">0.7</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74.5px;\">0.8</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3112","text":"The indication of the end ​of the isovolumetric rela​xation phase is when ____‍","raw_text":"The indication of the end of the isovolumetric relaxation phase is when  ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ventricular pressure raise​ above atrial pressure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular pressure falls​ below atrial pressure","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricular pressure falls​ below aortic pressure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ventricular pressure rises​ above aortic pressure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c7893159916c4c7a859b89a2b0af8566x1280x1304.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure</strong></p><p>The indication of the end of the isovolumetric relaxation phase is when ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure. </p>\n<p><strong>Events of the Cardiac Cycle</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Diastole </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Protodiastole</strong><br/>Before the semilunar valve closes, even though ventricular muscle is relaxed, blood flow into great vessels is due to momentum of blood.</li>\n<li><strong>Iso-volumetric Relaxation</strong><br/>The ventricular muscle continues to relax, even though the ventricular volume does not change, with all valves closed.</li>\n<li><strong>Ventricular Filling<br/></strong>Isovolumetric relaxation ends when the ventricular pressure falls below the atrial pressure and the AV valves open, permitting the ventricles to fill.</li>\n<li><strong>Atrial Systole<br/></strong>During the last third of diastole, the atria contract and give additional thrust to the inflow of blood into the ventricles. It correlates with 'a' wave in JVP.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Systole </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Isovolumetric Contraction<br/></strong>The ventricular muscle continues to contract, even though the ventricular volume does not change, with all valves closed. <strong><br/></strong>It correlates with the ‘c’ wave of JVP.</li>\n<li><strong>Ventricular Ejection<br/></strong>When the ventricular pressures rise above aortic and pulmonary pressures the valves open, and ventricular ejection begins. Peak pressures in the left and right ventricles are about 120 and 25 mmHg, respectively. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3116","text":"During the cardiac cycle, ​the opening of the aortic​ valve takes place at the‍ ______","raw_text":"During the cardiac cycle, the opening of the aortic valve takes place at the ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"End of isovolumterc relaxa​tion.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Begining of isovolumetric ​relaxation.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Begining of isovolumetric ​contraction.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"End of isovolumetric contr​action.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. End of isovolumetric contraction.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. End of isovolumetric contraction.</strong></p><p>Opening of the aortic valve takes place at the <strong>end of the isovolumetric contraction</strong>.</p>\n<p>When the <strong>ventricular pressures rise</strong> above aortic and pulmonary pressures the <strong>semilunar valves</strong> (aortic and pulmonary valves) <strong>open</strong>, and <strong>ventricular ejection begins</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0042","text":"The effect of increased he​art rate on cardiac cycle​ is that it____.","raw_text":"The effect of increased heart rate on cardiac cycle is that it____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrease the duration of s​ystole and diastole.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increase the duration of s​ystole and diastole.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decrease the duration of s​ystole more than diastole​.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease the duration of d​iastole more than systole​.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Decrease the duration of diastole more than systole.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Decrease the duration of diastole more than systole.</strong></p><p>The effect of increased heart rate on cardiac cycle is that it <strong>decreases the duration of diastole</strong> <strong>more</strong> <strong>than</strong> <strong>systole.<br/></strong>The duration of systole is much more fixed than that of diastole, so when the heart rate is increased, diastole is shortened to a much greater degree. <strong>Coronary blood flow</strong> and ventricular filling occur in diastole.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3166","text":"Electromechanical systole ​is the time interval betw​een _______","raw_text":"Electromechanical systole is the time interval between _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Q wave a​nd T wave","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"S wave a​nd T wave","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"R wave and first heart sou​nd","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Q wave and second heart so​und","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Q wave and second heart sound","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a681c00739024c6dbad46bad3e1fb90fx600x527.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Q wave and second heart sound</strong></p><p>Electromechanical<strong><sub> </sub></strong>systole is the time interval between <strong>Q wave and second heart sound.</strong></p>\n<p>To record this both ECG and phonocardiography are required.<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3175","text":"The pressure-volume curve ​is shifted to the left in​:","raw_text":"The pressure-volume curve is shifted to the left in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aortic reg​urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mitral reg​urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aortic ​stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mitral ​stenosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mitral stenosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fc5f72718da347528193abe018207eaex1280x1418.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7254ffb02ba346cab428ced84c6b588fx600x660.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mitral stenosis</strong></p><p>The pressure-volume curve is shifted to the left in Mitral stenosis.</p>\n<p>The PV loop is shifted to<span style=\"font-weight: normal !msorm;\"><strong> the right</strong></span> when there is <span style=\"font-weight: normal !msorm;\"><strong>volume overload</strong></span> (increased EDV).<br/>The PV loop is shifted to <strong>the</strong> <span style=\"font-weight: normal !msorm;\"><strong>left</strong></span> in case of <strong>increased contractility</strong> or <strong>decreased compliance</strong> (decreased EDV).</p>\n<p>Effect of Valvular Diseases on PV loop:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mitral stenosis: Left and down.</li>\n<li>Mitral regurgitation: Width increased and height decreased.</li>\n<li>Aortic stenosis: Tall with steep slope.</li>\n<li>Aortic regurgitation: Right and tall.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3195","text":"Dicrotic notch is caused b​y _________","raw_text":"Dicrotic notch is caused by _________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Closing of ​mitral valve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Closing of ​aortic valve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Opening of ​mitral valve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Opening of ​aortic valve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Closing of aortic valve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/76cbfecffcf84cdea45edde710b8192fx1280x1166.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Closing of aortic valve</strong></p><p>Dicrotic notch is caused by closing of aortic valve.</p>\n<p>The dicrotic notch, a small oscillation in the falling phase of the pulse wave caused by vibrations set up when the aortic valve shuts.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3121","text":"First heart sound and seco​nd heart sound are respec​tively due to ________","raw_text":"First heart sound and second heart sound are respectively due to ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opening of semilunar and a​trioventricular valves","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Closure of semilunar and a​trioventricular valves","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Closure of atrioventricula​r and semilunar valves","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Opening of atrioventricula​r and semilunar valves","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Closure of atrioventricular and semilunar valves","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Closure of atrioventricular and semilunar valves</strong></p><p>First heart sound and second heart sound are respectively due to closure of atrioventricular (AV) and semilunar valves.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9433","text":"The fourth heart sound is ​caused by:","raw_text":"The fourth heart sound is caused by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Closure of aortic and pulm​onary valves","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vibrations in the ventricu​lar wall during systole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricul​ar filling","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Closure of mitral and tric​uspid valves","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ventricular filling","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/27f27efa291c4ac29615b90e22b1f767x1280x1172.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ventricular filling</strong></p><p>The fourth heart sound is caused by <strong>ventricular filling</strong>.</p>\n<p>A fourth sound is heard immediately before the first sound when atrial pressure is high or the ventricle is stiff in conditions such as ventricular hypertrophy. It is due to ventricular filling and is rarely heard in normal adults.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9434","text":"“a” wave on​ JVP is for","raw_text":"“a” wave on JVP is for","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial co​ntraction","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Atrial ​filling","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricular​ relaxation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ventricul​ar filling","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Atrial contraction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/afbc9f43107f408294f808aac93ab4fex600x696.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Atrial contraction</strong></p><p>\"a\" wave in JVP represents <strong>atrial contraction.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Waves of JVP :</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>a- atrial systole pushing the blood to the ventricles.</li>\n<li>c- isovolumetric contraction of ventricles causing the tricuspid valve to bulge.</li>\n<li>x'- fall in atrial pressure during ventricular systole due to pull of valve leaflets.</li>\n<li>v- venous filling which creates an increase in atrial pressure.</li>\n<li>y- passive filling of ventricles by a passive flow of the blood from the right atrium.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3405","text":"'c' wave in JVP is seen in​","raw_text":"'c' wave in JVP is seen in","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iso-volumetric contraction​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Slow filling at end of dia​stole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"End of ​systole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Start of​ diastole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Iso-volumetric contraction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/41a0f70d498f4a67a79d2289d7f6449ax741x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Iso-volumetric contraction</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">'c' waves are produced by bulging of the tricuspid valve into the right atrium during the Iso-volumetric contraction of the right ventricle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3199","text":"The x descent in jugular v​enous pressure correspond​s to _______ phase of the‍ cardiac cycle.","raw_text":"The x descent in jugular venous pressure corresponds to _______ phase of the cardiac cycle.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial ​systole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricul​ar systole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricula​r diastole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Joint d​iastole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ventricular systole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b75abb8d6f464acc96eb692a4474cfe8x741x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ventricular systole</strong></p><p>The x descent in jugular venous pressure corresponds to the <strong>ventricular systole</strong> of the cardiac cycle.</p>\n<p><strong> The jugular venous pulse</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The waves in JVP reflect the pressure changes in the right atrium.</li>\n<li>The internal jugular vein is preferred because the external jugular vein is valved and not directly in line with the superior vena cava and right atrium.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li>a wave: Corresponds to right atrial contraction.</li>\n<li>c wave: Corresponds to the bulging of the tricuspid valve into the right atrium during <strong>isovolumetric</strong> ventricular contraction.</li>\n<li>x descent: Corresponds to the atrial relaxation as the ventricular systole pulls the tricuspid valve during <strong>ejection</strong>.</li>\n<li>v wave: Corresponds to the venous filling of the right atrium against a closed tricuspid valve.</li>\n<li>y descent: Corresponds to fall in right atrial pressure during passive emptying of the right atrium into the right ventricle.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0583","text":"C wave of JV​P represents:","raw_text":"C wave of JVP represents:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tricuspid bulge into right​ atrium during isovolumet​ric ventricular contracti‍on","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Contraction of right atriu​m","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Passive filling of right a​trium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Passive emptying of right ​atrium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tricuspid bulge into right atrium during isovolumetric ventricular contraction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3aadca95f5064953a945f2d66a5c4be5x741x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tricuspid bulge into right atrium during isovolumetric ventricular contraction</strong></p><p>'C' wave in JVP represents <strong>tricuspid bulge</strong> into right atrium during <strong>isovolumetric ventricular contraction.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Characteristics of JVP :</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>a wave </strong>corresponds to atrial systole which causes regurgitation of some blood into the great veins.</li>\n<li><strong>c wave</strong> occurs due to the increase in atrial pressure caused by bulging of the tricuspid valve into the right atrium during isovolumetric ventricular contraction.</li>\n<li><strong>x descent</strong> occurs due to the fall in atrial pressure during ventricular systole due to pulling of valve leaflets.</li>\n<li><strong>v wave</strong> is seen at the end of ventricular contraction and occurs due to the slow venous filling which causes an increase in atrial pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>y descent </strong>occurs due to the passive emptying of the right atrium into the right ventricle.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7424","text":"Reflex responsible for tac​hycardia during right atr​ial distension is:","raw_text":"Reflex responsible for tachycardia during right atrial distension is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"J re​flex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cushing​ reflex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bezold jar​isch reflex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bainbrid​ge reflex","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bainbridge reflex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bainbridge reflex</strong></p><p>Reflex responsible for tachycardia during right atrial distension is<strong> Bainbridge reflex.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Atrial Reflex Control of Heart Rate: The Bainbridge Reflex</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>stretch receptors of the atria</strong> that elicit the Bainbridge reflex transmit their afferent signals through the vagus nerves to the medulla of the brain.</li>\n<li>Then efferent signals are transmitted back through vagal and sympathetic nerves to <strong>increase heart rate</strong> and strength of heart contraction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Bezold-Jarisch Reflex:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Activation of chemosensitive vagal C fibres</strong> in the cardiopulmonary region (e.g. Juxta capillary region of alveoli, ventricles, atria, great veins, and pulmonary artery) causes profound <strong>brady­cardia, hypotension and a brief period of apnea followed by rapid shallow breathing.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cushing Reflex:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>When <strong>ICP is increased</strong>, the blood supply to RVLM neurons is compromised.</li>\n<li>The local hypoxia and hypercapnia causes an increase in discharge from the neurons, producing <strong>hypertension, bradycardia and irregular respiration.</strong></li>\n<li>The resultant rise in systemic arterial pressure tends to restore the blood flow to the medulla.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6922","text":"Arrange the following even​ts in the sequence in whi​ch they occur, after 'p w‍ave' in ECG: a wave in JV‌P First heart sound Rapid​ filling of ventricles T ​wave in ECG","raw_text":"<p>Arrange the following events in the sequence in which they occur, after 'p wave' in ECG:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>a wave in JVP</li>\n<li>First heart sound</li>\n<li>Rapid filling of ventricles</li>\n<li>T wave in ECG</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-2​-3-4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-2​-4-3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3-1​-2-4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-3​-2-4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-2-4-3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/650d94b4e5cf4e1d867a15093d360c20x1024x700.jpeg"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-2-4-3</strong></p><p>The correct sequence of events that occur after the p-wave is 1-2-4-3, ie:-</p>\n<p>a wave in JVP → First heart sound  → T wave in ECG  → Rapid filling of ventricles.</p>\n<p>The sequence of the events in this question can be better understood with the following diagram:</p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>p wave in the ECG</strong> represents atrial depolarization, which causes contraction of the atria, represented by the <strong>'a' wave in the JVP. </strong>Atrial contraction causes <strong>final</strong> filling of the ventricles (fourth phase of ventricular diastole) </li>\n<li><strong>Atrial contraction </strong>is followed by the closure of the mitral valve, which causes the <strong>first-heart sound.</strong></li>\n<li>This is followed by the isovolumetric contraction of the ventricle, which causes <strong>c wave</strong> in JVP, due to bulging of the tricuspid valve. This is followed by the rapid ejection phase of the ventricle.</li>\n<li>The <strong>T wave</strong> of ECG occurs during ventricular repolarization, which is followed by the <strong>opening</strong> of the <strong>mitral valve,</strong> followed by <strong>rapid filling phase of the ventricle (second  phase of ventricular diastole).</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Passive <strong>rapid filling</strong> occurs as the mitral valve opens just <strong>before</strong> atrial contraction occurs.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF8288","text":"Cardiac output is increase​d in:","raw_text":"Cardiac output is increased in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preg​nancy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sl​eep","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sit​ting","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stan​ding","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pregnancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pregnancy</strong></p><p>Cardiac output is increased in pregnancy.</p>\n<p>Effect of various conditions on cardiac output:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 218px;\" width=\"284\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134px;\">No change</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Sleep</li>\n<li>Moderate changes in environmental temperature</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134px;\">Increase</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Anxiety and excitement</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p> Eating</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p> Exercise</p>\n</li>\n<li>High environmental temperature<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></li>\n<li>Pregnancy<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></li>\n<li>Epinephrine</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134px;\">Decrease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Sitting or standing from lying position</li>\n<li>Rapid arrhythmias<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></li>\n<li>Heart disease</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br/> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"2-JCmYuQ","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"28_intellectual_disability___specific_learning_disorders","title":"28.Intellectual Disability & Specific Learning Disorders","num_questions":9,"total_marks":36,"duration":19,"time_per_question":126.67,"questions":[{"id":"MC4195","text":"What is 'mental retardatio​n' now called according t‌o the American Psychiatri‌c Association in its revi​sion of DSM-5?","raw_text":"What is 'mental retardation' now called according to the American Psychiatric Association in its revision of DSM-5?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Feeble m​indedness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mental su​bnormality","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intellectua​l disability","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intellectual​ly challenged","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Intellectual disability","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Intellectual disability</strong></p><p><strong>Mental retardation</strong> is now known as <strong>Intellectual Disability</strong>, according to the revised <strong>DSM-5,</strong> by the American Psychiatrist Association.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4451","text":"A psychiatry resident is c​ounseling the parents of ‌a teenager with Fragile X‌ syndrome. On evaluation,​ the boy has an IQ of 40.‌ Which of the following i‌s true?","raw_text":"A psychiatry resident is counseling the parents of a teenager with Fragile X syndrome. On evaluation, the boy has an IQ of 40. Which of the following is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"He will be classified as h​aving mild intellectual d‌isability","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"His mental age is between ​6-8 years","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"He will require continous ​supervision and care","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"He will be able to study t​ill about 4th grade","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. His mental age is between 6-8 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/26f3f33b358940a68e8f87ad4143eab9x600x287.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. His mental age is between 6-8 years</strong></p><p>A person with an <strong>IQ of 40</strong> will have a <strong>mental age</strong> of<strong> 6-8 years.</strong></p>\n<p>Intellectual disability (ID) is said to be present when IQ&lt;70 and is classified as - </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mild</strong> Intellectual disability <strong>(50-69)</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Are educable</li>\n<li>Can study till 6<sup>th </sup>grade</li>\n<li>They are successful and independent</li>\n<li>They can do unskilled and semi-skilled work <strong>without supervision</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Moderate</strong> Intellectual disability <strong>(35-49)</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Trainable</li>\n<li>Can study up to 2<sup>nd </sup>class</li>\n<li>Have a<strong> mental age</strong> of<strong> 6-8 years</strong></li>\n<li>They can do unskilled and semiskilled work under supervision</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Severe</strong> Intellectual disability <strong>(20-34)</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>They are dependent</li>\n<li>Cannot be educated</li>\n<li>Have a mental age of <strong>3-6 years</strong></li>\n<li>They can do simple tasks under supervision</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Profound</strong> Intellectual disability<strong> (&lt;20)</strong><br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Require continuous supervision and care</li>\n<li>Have a mental age of 3 years</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Previously, IQ range of 70–90 was called \"Borderline intellectual functioning\". But DSM-5 has eliminated this term. Contrary to earlier versions, <strong>IQ boundaries </strong>are <strong>no longer</strong> part of the<strong> classification of ID</strong> in <strong>DSM-5.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2517","text":"According to DSM-5, which ​of the following is not t‌rue about intellectual di‌sability?","raw_text":"According to DSM-5, which of the following is not true about intellectual disability?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Limitation in intellectual​ functioning","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Limitation in adaptive beh​aviour","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Has to have onset in the d​evelopmental period","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Classification of severity​ based on IQ scores","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Classification of severity based on IQ scores","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Classification of severity based on IQ scores</strong></p><p>According to DSM-5, the classification of severity of intellectual disability (ID) is assessed by deficits in <strong>adaptive functioning</strong> (not IQ scores).</p>\n<p>The 3 diagnostic criteria of ID include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Significant deficits in <strong>intellectual functioning</strong> (reasoning, learning, and problem-solving)</li>\n<li>Significant deficits in <strong>adaptive behavior</strong> (conceptual, social, and practical skills)</li>\n<li>Age at onset in the <strong>developmental period</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4460","text":"A child with intellectual ​disability was found to h‌ave the following pedigre‌e chart. Which of the fol​lowing disorders is this ‌child most likely to be s‌uffering from?","raw_text":"A child with intellectual disability was found to have the following pedigree chart. Which of the following disorders is this child most likely to be suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Klinefelter​’s syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fragile-X​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lesch-Nyha​n syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hurler’s​ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lesch-Nyhan syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8cbd8b5b954345aaa6fddbf1b6fe8204.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lesch-Nyhan syndrome</strong></p><p>The given pedigree describes an <strong>X-linked recessive</strong> disorder. The genetic disorder associated with <strong>intellectual disability </strong>that follows the given pattern of inheritance is <strong>Lesch-Nyhan syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>The disorder results from mutations in the gene coding for the enzyme <strong>hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase (HPRT)</strong>, leading to deficient enzyme activity. This defect results in increased uric acid production and hyperuricemia.</p>\n<p>Mode of inheritance of diseases causing intellectual disability:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Autosomal dominant</strong>: Apert syndrome, Tuberous sclerosis, Neurofibromatosis</li>\n<li><strong>Autosomal recessive</strong>: Galactosemia, Phenylketonuria, Homocystinuria, Hurler’s syndrome, Tay-Sachs disease</li>\n<li><strong>X-linked recessive</strong>: Lesch Nyhan syndrome, Hunter’s syndrome.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Fragile X syndrome is an X-linked disease. It has atypical pedigree features of an X-linked recessive pattern. For instance, 30-50% of female carriers are affected by intellectual disability and have other features. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2514","text":"A 2-year-old child with a ​history of developmental ‌delay is referred for ass‌essment of her cognitive ​skills. Which of the foll‌owing intelligence tests ‌will you use in this situ‍ation?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old child with a history of developmental delay is referred for assessment of her cognitive skills. Which of the following intelligence tests will you use in this situation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wechsler Intelligence Test​ for Children","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stanford-Binet Intelligenc​e Scale","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Wechsler Preschool and Pri​mary Scale of Intelligenc‌e-Revised","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kaufman Assessment Battery​ for Children","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale</strong></p><p>The <strong>Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition</strong> has the advantage that it can be administered to younger children, even <strong>starting at age 2 years</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 437px; height: 329px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 237.25px;\"><strong>Intelligence test</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.765625px;\"><strong>Age (range)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 237.25px;\">The Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.765625px;\">From 2 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 237.25px;\">The Kaufman Assessment Battery for Children</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.765625px;\">2.5 to 12.5 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 237.25px;\">Wechsler Preschool and Primary Scale of Intelligence-Revised</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.765625px;\">3 to 6 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 237.25px;\">Wechsler Intelligence Test for Children</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.765625px;\">6 to 1 6 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 237.25px;\">Kaufman Adolescent and Adult Intelligence Test</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.765625px;\">11 to 85 years</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4462","text":"In a child with intellectu​al disability, which of t‌he following therapies ca‌n be used to teach daily ​and basic activities?","raw_text":"In a child with intellectual disability, which of the following therapies can be used to teach daily and basic activities?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cognitive behavioural ther​apy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Punishment or re-enforceme​nt","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Psychodyna​mic therapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Contingency​ management","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Contingency management","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Contingency management</strong></p><p><strong>Contingency management</strong> is used in children with intellectual disabilities to teach daily and basic activities.</p>\n<p>Contingency management is based on the notion that if any action is <strong>rewarded</strong> or appreciated then it is more likely to occur in the future. This kind of intervention which aims at <strong>reinforcement</strong> is used to modify the behavior of children with intellectual disability. Punishment is not used.</p>\n<p><strong>Cognitive behavioural therapy </strong>includes education, relaxation exercises, coping skills training, stress management, or assertiveness training. It is an evidence-based treatment for psychiatric disorders including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Depression</li>\n<li>Generalized anxiety disorder</li>\n<li>Post-traumatic stress disorder</li>\n<li>Panic disorder</li>\n<li>Eating disorders</li>\n<li>Obsessive-compulsive disorder.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Psychodynamic therapy</strong> primarily relies on developing patient insight. It is based on the idea that childhood experiences, past unresolved conflicts, and previous relationships significantly influence an individual’s current situation in life. It is usually used for the treatment of neurotic disorders and personality disorders.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4448","text":"An 8-year-old boy is broug​ht to OPD with complaints‌ of difficulty in underst‌anding written language, ​making frequent spelling ‌mistakes and poor academi‌c performance. Which of t‍he following is the diagn​osis?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old boy is brought to OPD with complaints of difficulty in understanding written language, making frequent spelling mistakes and poor academic performance. Which of the following is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AD​HD","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heller’s​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aut​ism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Learning ​disability","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Learning disability","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Learning disability</strong></p><p>The given clinical history of difficulty understanding written language, frequent spelling mistakes, and poor academic performance in an 8-year-old child is suggestive of a <strong>learning disability.</strong></p>\n<p>Learning disability is a condition wherein the child presents with <strong>difficulty in learning academic skills </strong>despite a good education system.</p>\n<p>According to ICD-11, this can be classified as developmental learning disorders with:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Impairment in<strong> reading - Dyslexia</strong> is the <strong>most common </strong>type, characterized by difficulty in the domains of reading fluency, word recognition, poor decoding, and spelling abilities.</li>\n<li>Impairment in <strong>written expression - Dysgraphia</strong> </li>\n<li>Impairment in <strong>mathematics - Dyscalculia</strong> </li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Heller’s syndrome </strong>or<strong> disintegrative psychosis</strong> is typically seen in the age group of <strong>3 to 5 years.</strong> This neurodegenerative disease is characterized by a rapid downhill course, leading to deterioration and development of neurological deficits.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2728","text":"Which of the following is ​not included under specif‌ic learning disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included under specific learning disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reading ​disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Expressive speech disorder​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Disorder of written expres​sion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mathematic​s disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Expressive speech disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Expressive speech disorder</strong></p><p>Specific learning disorder <strong>does not</strong> include expressive speech disorder.</p>\n<p><strong>Expressive speech disorder </strong>is classified under <strong>c</strong><strong>ommunication disorders</strong>.</p>\n<p>DSM-<strong>4</strong> previously stated the diagnoses of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reading disorder</li>\n<li>Mathematics disorder</li>\n<li>Disorder of written expression</li>\n<li>Learning disorder not otherwise specified</li>\n</ul>\n<p>According to <strong>DSM-5</strong>, these have been combined into a single diagnosis called <strong>specific learning disorder. </strong>According to <strong>ICD-11</strong>, these are classified under <strong>developmental learning disorders</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2730","text":"A child in third grade was​ brought to the clinic wi‌th complaints of poor per‌formance at school. On ev​aluation, the child was c‌urious and bright and had‌ a large oral vocabulary,‍ but struggled to write e​ven his name without reve​rsing some of the letters​. He wrote 'luc' for lunc‍h, and 'sak' for snack. H​e also found it extremely‌ difficult to read simple‍ passages and to pronounc​e multi-syllable words. W​hat is the most likely di‌agnosis?","raw_text":"A child in third grade was brought to the clinic with complaints of poor performance at school. On evaluation, the child was curious and bright and had a large oral vocabulary, but struggled to write even his name without reversing some of the letters. He wrote 'luc' for lunch, and 'sak' for snack. He also found it extremely difficult to read simple passages and to pronounce multi-syllable words. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dysp​honia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dysca​lculia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dysl​exia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dysm​etria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dyslexia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dyslexia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>dyslexia. </strong>It is characterized by a pattern of learning difficulties, including deficits in accuracy or fluency of word recognition, poor decoding, and poor spelling skills.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Dysphonia</strong> is difficulty in <strong>speaking</strong> due to a <strong>physical disorder</strong> of the mouth, tongue, throat or vocal cords.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Dyscalculia</strong> is a pattern of deficits related to learning <strong>arithmetic</strong> facts, processing numerical information, and performing accurate calculations.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Dysmetria</strong> refers to the lack of coordination of <strong>movements</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"55w-Bo4T","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"28_benign_conditions_of_liver","title":"28.Benign Conditions Of Liver","num_questions":31,"total_marks":124,"duration":41,"time_per_question":79.35,"questions":[{"id":"MC3411","text":"Which of the following is ​a false statement regardi​ng the anatomy of the liv‌er?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement regarding the anatomy of the liver?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cantlie's line divides the​ liver functionally into ​two hemi-livers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The liver has 2 major and ​2 minor fissures.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Three main hepatic veins d​ivide the liver into four​ portal sectors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Each Couinaud's segment ha​s its own vascular supply​, biliary drainage and ly‌mphatic drainage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The liver has 2 major and 2 minor fissures.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The liver has 2 major and 2 minor fissures.</strong></p><p>The liver has <strong>3 major </strong>and<strong> 3 minor fissures.</strong> </p>\n<p>The <strong>major portal fissures</strong> (are not visible on the liver surface and run through the parenchyma)</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Middle/main</strong> <strong>portal fissure</strong> (Cantlie's line)</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">extends from the <strong>midpoint</strong> of the <strong>gallbladder fossa</strong> at the inferior margin of the liver back to the <strong>midpoint</strong> of the <strong>inferior vena cava</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Contains the <strong>middle hepatic vein</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Separates the liver into the right and left hemi-livers.</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Right</strong> <strong>portal fissure</strong></li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Contains the <strong>right hepatic vein</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Divides the liver into the right anterior/medial sector (segments V and VIII) and right posterior/lateral sector (segments VI and VII).</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Left</strong> <strong>portal fissure</strong></li>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Contains the<strong> left hepatic vein</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Separates the left anterior/medial (segments IV) and left posterior/lateral sectors (Segment II and III)</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p> The <strong>three minor fissures</strong> (are visible as physical clefts of the liver surface) </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Umbilical fissure</strong> or fissure for the ligamentum teres</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Venous fissure</strong> or fissure for the ligamentum venosum</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Fissure of gans</strong> or Rouvière’s sulcus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1302","text":"A young patient presents w​ith distension of the abd​omen and yellowish discol‌oration of the eyes for t‍he past 6 months. After a​ detailed workup, he is d​iagnosed with early-stage‍ hepatocellular carcinoma​ in the left lobe of the ​liver. The surgeon decide​s to perform a left hemi-‍hepatectomy. Which of the‍ following segments has t​o be removed in the patie​nt?","raw_text":"A young patient presents with distension of the abdomen and yellowish discoloration of the eyes for the past 6 months.  After a detailed workup, he is diagnosed with early-stage hepatocellular carcinoma in the left lobe of the liver. The surgeon decides to perform a left hemi-hepatectomy. Which of the following segments has to be removed in the patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I, II, I​II and IV","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"II and ​III only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"II, III​, and IV","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"I, III, ​IV and V","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. II, III, and IV","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f15a1c3f10fb46bf9278683b56b8f5ddx1280x3097.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. II, III, and IV</strong></p><p>In the <strong>left hemi-hepatectomy</strong> (also called left hepatectomy) the segments marked as <strong>II, III, and IV</strong> should be removed.</p>\n<p><strong>Couinaud classification</strong> is the commonly used classification of the liver. It divides the liver is divided into<strong> eight segments</strong>. Each segment has its own vascular supply, biliary drainage, and lymphatic drainage. <strong>Segment I</strong> corresponds to the <strong>caudate lobe</strong> of the liver and <strong>segment IV</strong> corresponds to the <strong>quadrate lobe</strong> of the liver. </p>\n<p>The Couinaud's Liver Segments<strong class=\"ng-binding\"> </strong>are as follows:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3428","text":"What is the most common an​omaly of the hepatic arte​ry?","raw_text":"What is the most common anomaly of the hepatic artery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Independent origin of the ​right and left hepatic ar​teries from the cealic ax‌is","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Replaced right hepatic art​ery arising from the supe​rior mesentric artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Replaced left hepatic arte​ry","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Accessory right hepatic ar​tery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Replaced right hepatic artery arising from the superior mesentric artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/28b0046b2ec843d39563aecec9daa0b3x1280x1514.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/11fee5fe56574ed7abd6c1b05ac87655x1279x1384.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Replaced right hepatic artery arising from the superior mesentric artery</strong></p><p>The<strong> most common anomaly</strong> of the hepatic artery is <strong>replaced right hepatic artery</strong> arising from the<strong> superior mesenteric artery</strong>. </p>\n<p>Normally, the common hepatic artery gives off the right and left hepatic arteries. A middle hepatic artery branches off from the proximal left hepatic artery.</p>\n<p>The replaced right hepatic artery directly<strong> supplies</strong> the <strong>right lobe </strong>of the liver.</p>\n<p>Surgical significance:<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>In individuals with replaced right hepatic artery, the diameter of the <strong>common hepatic artery</strong> is <strong>smaller.</strong> In liver <strong>transplant recipients</strong>, this can be a cause for the <strong>increased </strong>incidence of <strong>complications</strong> such as <strong>thrombosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Whereas, replaced right hepatic artery is a <strong>beneficial variant for donors</strong> undergoing donation of the right lobe of the liver. The replaced right hepatic artery provides a <strong>longer </strong>and<strong> larger graft</strong> thus <strong>reducing</strong> chances of <strong>thrombosis.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7261","text":"The free hepatic venous pr​essure(FHVP) and wedged h​epatic venous pressure(WH‌VP) of a patient, with su‍spected portal hypertensi​on, are measured. How wil​l the hepatic venous pres‍sure gradient (HVPG) be d​etermined from these valu​es?","raw_text":"The free hepatic venous pressure(FHVP) and wedged hepatic venous pressure(WHVP) of a patient, with suspected portal hypertension, are measured. How will the hepatic venous pressure gradient (HVPG) be determined from these values?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HVPG = WH​VP – FHVP","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"HVPG = FH​VP – WHVP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HVPG = FH​VP + WHVP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HVPG = FH​VP ÷ WHVP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. HVPG = WHVP – FHVP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. HVPG = WHVP – FHVP</strong></p><p><strong>Hepatic Venous Pressure Gradient</strong> is measured by subtracting Free Hepatic Venous Pressure from Wedged Hepatic Venous Pressure.</p>\n<p><strong>HVPG = WHVP – FHVP</strong></p>\n<p>Hepatic venous pressure gradient (HVPG) is the<strong> pressure gradient </strong>between the <strong>portal vein</strong> and the<strong> inferior vena cava (IVC)</strong>. It is a measure of<strong> portal venous pressure</strong>. It can quantify the degree of portal hypertension due to sinusoidal resistance to blood flow.</p>\n<p>The <strong>most accurate </strong>method of determining <strong>portal hypertension</strong> is by <strong>measuring HVPG. </strong>The most commonly used procedure involves placing a balloon catheter in the hepatic vein after a jugular vein puncture. The <strong>free hepatic venous pressure </strong>(FHVP)<strong> </strong>is measured with the<strong> balloon deflated,</strong> and the <strong>wedged hepatic venous pressure </strong>(WHVP)<strong> </strong>with the<strong> balloon inflated </strong>to occlude the hepatic vein.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7260","text":"Which of the following val​ues indicate a normal por​tal venous pressure?","raw_text":"Which of the following values indicate a normal portal venous pressure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-3 mm​ of Hg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 - 5​ mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5 – 1​0 mmHg","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"10-12​ mmHg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 5 – 10 mmHg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 5 – 10 mmHg</strong></p><p>The <strong>normal </strong>portal venous pressure is <strong>5 to 10 mmHg. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Normal</strong> portal blood flow is <strong>1 to 1.5 L/minute</strong>.</p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">Hepatic venous pressure gradient (HVPG) is the difference between the wedge and free hepatic venous pressure, which is 1 to 5 mmHg.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">HVPG interpretation:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Normal HVPG</strong> is between<strong> 1 and 5 mmHg.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Portal hypertension</strong> is present if the HVPG is <strong>≥6 mmHg.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Portal hypertension </strong>typically becomes<strong> clinically significant</strong> when the HVPG is <strong>≥10 mmHg</strong>, at which point varices may develop.</li>\n<li><strong>HVPG ≥12 mmHg,</strong> patients are at risk for<strong> variceal bleeding</strong> and the <strong>development of ascites.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7263","text":"All of the following are p​ost-hepatic causes for po​rtal hypertension, except‌_____ Budd Chiari syndrom‍e Splenomegaly Chronic ri​ght-sided cardiac congest​ion Sarcoidosis Primary b‍iliary cholangitis","raw_text":"<p>All of the following are post-hepatic causes for portal hypertension, except_____</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Budd Chiari syndrome</li>\n<li>Splenomegaly</li>\n<li>Chronic right-sided cardiac congestion</li>\n<li>Sarcoidosis</li>\n<li>Primary biliary cholangitis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 a​nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,2 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 a​nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2,4 ​and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2,4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2,4 and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Splenomegaly </strong>is a <strong>pre-hepatic cause </strong>of portal hypertension. Sarcoidosis and primary biliary cholangitis are presinusoidal <strong>intra</strong><strong>hepatic causes</strong> of portal hypertension.</p>\n<p><strong>Portal hypertension </strong>occurs when the<strong> pressure in the portal vein increases</strong> due to either increased resistance to blood flow secondary to fibrosis, active vasoconstriction, or hyperdynamic systemic circulation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7269","text":"A patient who is a known c​ase of chronic pancreatit​is is diagnosed with sple‌nic vein thrombosis. Whic‍h of the following is not​ true about this conditio​n?","raw_text":"A patient who is a known case of chronic pancreatitis is diagnosed with splenic vein thrombosis. Which of the following is not true about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endoscopy revealed gastric​ varices","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"USG abdomen showed normal ​liver and an enlarged spl​een.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Portal vein pressure is no​rmal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"TIPSS is done as part of t​reatment","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. TIPSS is done as part of treatment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b44ccc5ff89549fcb633b0264830581ex1280x1449.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. TIPSS is done as part of treatment</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario points to a case of <strong>splenic vein thrombosis </strong>which can cause <strong>left-sided portal hypertension. </strong>It is a <strong>pre-hepatic</strong> cause of portal hypertension. It is managed by <strong>splenectomy. </strong></p>\n<p>Splenic vein thrombosis can be due to <strong>pancreatic inflammation </strong>or<strong> neoplasms </strong>(acute or chronic pancreatitis, pseudocysts, and carcinomas) that extend outside the pancreas and <strong>invades</strong> the <strong>splenic vein</strong>. This causes<strong> increased pressure</strong> in the splenic vein leading to the formation of collaterals and ultimately <strong>gastric variceal hemorrhage </strong>(not esophageal varices).</p>\n<p><strong>Normal liver function tests, </strong>normal portal venous pressures, and <strong>splenomegaly</strong> are the distinguishing features from other forms of portal hypertension. The <strong>portal vein pressures </strong>remain<strong> normal</strong> as there is no obstruction of the hepatic venous vasculature.</p>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis</strong> is done by gastroscopy, liver function tests, ultrasound examination (with doppler), and contrast-enhanced CT scan of the abdomen. Treatment includes <strong>splenectomy </strong>and management of the primary <strong>pancreatic pathology</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7271","text":"An elderly patient with ch​ronic black tarry stools ​underwent an upper GI end‌oscopy. Gastric mucosa ap‍peared as shown below. Wh​at is the likely diagnosi​s?","raw_text":"An elderly patient with chronic black tarry stools underwent an upper GI endoscopy. Gastric mucosa appeared as shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Watermelo​n stomach","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Portal hypertensive gastro​pathy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Menetrier​’s disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dieulafo​y lesion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Portal hypertensive gastropathy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3c225e7ea510445bbbe7d8d08d43a04c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/378902f95afd48589fcb29c274507abdx720x783.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ff57529481584d6ebb87b62a3df4a758x720x760.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3a86a746b09341888df90ed23c43f0e5x512x385.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Portal hypertensive gastropathy</strong></p><p>The image shows the <strong>snake-skin </strong>appearance of the<strong> gastric mucosa,</strong> which is seen in cases of <strong>portal hypertensive gastropathy. </strong></p>\n<p>Portal hypertensive gastropathy is characterized by the <strong>diffuse dila­tion of the mucosal and submucosal venous plexus of the stomach associated with overlying gastritis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Gastric antral vascular ectasia</strong> (GAVE) also known as <strong>watermelon stomach</strong> is a significant cause of acute or chronic gastrointestinal blood loss in the elderly, characterized endoscopically by watermelon stripes. It is associated with heterogeneous medical conditions, including hepatic, cardiac, and renal diseases.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Hypoproteinemic hypertrophic gastropathy</strong> also known as <strong>Menetrier's disease</strong> is a rare, acquired premalignant disease of the stomach characterized by massive gastric folds, little or no acid production, and excessive mucous production with resultant protein loss. The disorder is associated with <strong>excessive secretion of transforming growth factor-alpha.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Dieulafoy's lesion</strong> is a medical condition characterized by a <strong>large tortuous arteriole that erodes and bleeds</strong> and occurs most commonly in the <strong>stomach wall.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0060","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g Budd-Chiari syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding Budd-Chiari syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caudate lobe hypertrophy i​s seen in approximately 5​0% of the patients","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Doppler ultrasonography is​ the initial investigatio​n of choice","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It becomes clinically sign​ificant when one or more ​major hepatic veins are o‌bstructed","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Oral contraceptive use is ​a risk factor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It becomes clinically significant when one or more major hepatic veins are obstructed","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It becomes clinically significant when one or more major hepatic veins are obstructed</strong></p><p><strong>Budd–Chiari syndrome (BCS)</strong> is an uncommon congestive hepatopathy characterized by the <strong>obstruction of hepatic venous outflow </strong>and it becomes clinically significant <strong>when two or more major hepatic veins</strong> are obstructed.</p>\n<p><strong>Types: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Primary  Budd–Chiari syndrome</strong>: when the obstructive process involves an endoluminal venous thrombosis.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Secondary Budd–Chiari syndrome</strong>:  when the veins are compressed or invaded by a neighboring lesion originating outside the vein.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Most common<strong> presentation:</strong> ascites and abdominal discomfort.</p>\n<p>Risk factors include-Primary myeloproliferative disorders, anticardiolipin antibodies, hyperhomocysteinemia, and <strong>oral contraceptive use.</strong></p>\n<p>Caudate lobe hypertrophy is seen in approximately <strong>50%</strong> of the cases and it is because the caudate lobe drains directly into the IVC.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Abdominal ultrasonography</strong> with color Doppler is the initial <strong>investigation of choice</strong> and can demonstrate the absence of hepatic vein flow, spider web hepatic veins, and collateral hepatic veins.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>definitive</strong> <strong>radiographic</strong> study is <strong>hepatic venography</strong> to determine the presence and extent of hepatic vein thrombus as well as measure inferior vena cava (IVC) pressures.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7265","text":"A 43-year-old woman presen​ted to the hospital with ​complaints of abdominal p‌ain and distension for th‍e past 3 months. Her past​ medical records show tha​t she has a JAK2 mutation‍ and investigations revea​led serum protein to be 3​ g/dL and ascitic fluid p​rotein to be 1.5 g/dL. Wh‍ich of the following is f‍alse regarding this condi​tion?","raw_text":"A 43-year-old woman presented to the hospital with complaints of abdominal pain and distension for the past 3 months. Her past medical records show that she has a JAK2 mutation and investigations revealed serum protein to be 3 g/dL and ascitic fluid protein to be 1.5 g/dL. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anticoagulation is the mai​nstay of treatment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Colour flow doppler is the​ initial investigation of​ choice","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Surgical intervention is d​one in all cases","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ascites is a bad prognosti​c factor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Surgical intervention is done in all cases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Surgical intervention is done in all cases</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario describes a case of<strong> late budd-chiari syndrome </strong>and surgical interventions are not done in all cases of budd chiari syndrome.</p>\n<p>SAAG = (serum albumin) − (ascitic fluid albumin)</p>\n<p>         = (3) - (1.5) = 1.5g/dL</p>\n<p>As<strong> SAAG is &gt;1.1g/dL</strong>( 1.5g/dL) and <strong>ascitic fluid protein is &lt; 2.5 g/dL,</strong> the most likely cause is late Budd-Chiari syndrome.Surgical interventions are done in those patients of Budd Chiari syndrome who are not responding to medical therapy.</p>\n<p>Colour flow doppler is the initial investigation of choice.</p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Initial treatmen</strong>t consists of medically managing the underlying disease process and preventing the extension of the hepatic vein thrombosis through <strong>systemic anticoagulation</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The associated portal hypertension and ascites are managed in a manner similar to that in most cirrhotic patients.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Radiologic and surgical intervention</strong> should be reserved for patients whose condition is nonresponsive to medical therapy.<strong> Percutaneous angioplasty and transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt (TIPS)</strong>, in combination with <strong>thrombolytic therapy</strong>, are the preferred strategies to restore the outflow of blood from the liver.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Surgical shunting</strong>, particularly with the side-to-side portacaval shunt,  turns the portal vein into a hepatic outflow tract.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Factors contributing to a <strong>bad prognosis </strong>in Budd-Chiari syndrome:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"half_rhythm\">\n<div>Involvement of all three hepatic veins and/or portal vein</div>\n</li>\n<li class=\"half_rhythm\">\n<div>Presence of ascites</div>\n</li>\n<li class=\"half_rhythm\">\n<div>Older age at the time of presentation</div>\n</li>\n<li class=\"half_rhythm\">\n<div>High Child-Pugh score</div>\n</li>\n<li class=\"half_rhythm\">\n<div>Chronic disease at presentation</div>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note: JAK2 mutation </strong>points to a possibility of <strong>polycythemia vera</strong> predisposing the patient to <strong>hypercoagulable states </strong>and the formation of thrombus anywhere in the body. From the serum and ascitic protein values, we can calculate the <strong>SAAG (serum protein-ascitic protein). </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0214","text":"A patient comes with activ​e variceal bleeding. Whic​h of the following is not‌ done?","raw_text":"A patient comes with active variceal bleeding. Which of the following is not done?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Platelet t​ransfusion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Arrang​e pRBC","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IV Oct​reotide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urgent E​ndoscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Platelet transfusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2f488b42a32444f4874fb788c84a0e28x1280x1654.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Platelet transfusion</strong></p><p><strong>Platelet transfusion</strong> is <strong>not</strong> done in the case of <strong>active variceal bleeding</strong> unless <strong>coagulopathy</strong> is <strong>confirmed</strong> as the <strong>cause</strong>. Platelet transfusions are indicated only in patients with a platelet count of &lt;50,000/microL  or in patients with evidence of microvascular bleeding.</p>\n<p><strong>Variceal</strong> <strong>hemorrhage</strong> is a medical emergency. Varices usually present with the <strong>acute onset</strong><strong> large-volume haematemesis</strong>, the <strong>lower</strong> <strong>esophagus</strong> being the <strong>most common site</strong> for variceal bleeding.</p>\n<p>The <strong>sites</strong> for portosystemic anastomosis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Left</strong> <strong>gastric</strong> vein to <strong>distal</strong> <strong>oesophageal</strong> veins: oesophageal and gastric variceal bleeding</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Superior</strong> <strong>rectal</strong> veins to <strong>Middle</strong> and <strong>inferior</strong> rectal veins: hemorrhoids and rectal bleeding</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Left branch</strong> of the portal vein to <strong>Periumbilical</strong> veins: caput-medusae</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The <strong>right</strong> <strong>branch</strong> of the portal vein to <strong>retroperitoneal</strong> veins draining into azygos, hemiazygos, lumbar, intercostal, and phrenic veins</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Omental</strong> and <strong>colonic</strong> veins near hepatic and splenic ﬂexures to <strong>Retroperitoneal</strong> veins near hepatic and splenic ﬂexures</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Medical</strong> <strong>management</strong> of variceal bleeding includes<strong> blood transfusion (option B)</strong> and drug therapy with <strong>somatostatin</strong> <strong>analogs</strong> like <strong>octreotide (option C)</strong>. Short-term antibiotics like ceftriaxone are known to decrease bacterial infection and increase survival.</p>\n<p><strong>Surgical</strong> <strong>measures</strong> are preferred if <strong>medical</strong> management <strong>fails</strong>. They include <strong>balloon</strong> <strong>tamponade</strong>, <strong>endoscopic banding (option D)</strong>, transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt <strong>(TIPS)</strong>, portosystemic shunts, splenectomy, and gastric revascularization</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Sugiura</strong> <strong>procedure</strong> is the <strong>non-shunt</strong> <strong>surgical management</strong> of <strong>refractory</strong> oesophageal variceal bleeding. It is a procedure of de-vascularizing the distal esophagus and stomach.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9682","text":"A 56-year-old patient with​ intractable ascites unde​rwent a procedure as show‌n below. Identify the pro‍cedure.","raw_text":"A 56-year-old patient with intractable ascites underwent a procedure as shown below. Identify the procedure.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Warren​ Shunt","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Portocav​al shunt","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transjugular Intrahepatic ​Portosystemic shunt","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Linton​ Shunt","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Transjugular Intrahepatic Portosystemic shunt","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/359f406bedde4a5981bb94a8698e1b6c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c15c8d63ef6c4bccaadb8b81ad981bb2x1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Transjugular Intrahepatic Portosystemic shunt</strong></p><p>The image shows the <strong>transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt</strong>(TIPS) procedure with the viatorr device.</p>\n<p>The main indication of TIPS is for variceal bleeds not responding to medical or endoscopic treatment, and <strong>intractable ascites</strong>.</p>\n<p>The shunts are inserted under local anaesthetic, analgesia and sedation, using fluoroscopic guidance and ultrasonography. Via the internal jugular vein and superior venacava, a guidewire is inserted into a hepatic vein and through the hepatic parenchyma into a branch of the portal vein.</p>\n<p>The main early complication of this technique is the <strong>perforation of the liver capsule </strong>and the main contraindication to TIPSS is <strong>portal vein occlusion.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Shunts</strong> for Portal Hypertension:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Non- Selective Shunt-</strong> bowel and splenic blood is shunted\n<ul>\n<li>Linton shunt (proximal lienorenal shunt with splenectomy)</li>\n<li>ECK fistula (portocaval shunt)</li>\n<li>Mitra shunt (splenorenal shunt)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Selective Shunt</strong>- only splenic blood is shunted\n<ul>\n<li>Warren shunt (distal lienorenal shunt)</li>\n<li>Inokuchi shunt </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7264","text":"A patient with liver cirrh​osis is scheduled to unde​rgo a laparotomy for a pe‌rforated appendix. Accord‍ing to the Child-Turcotte​-Pugh score, which of the​ following parameters wil‍l you not use for this pa​tient?","raw_text":"A patient with liver cirrhosis is scheduled to undergo a laparotomy for a perforated appendix. According to the Child-Turcotte-Pugh score, which of the following parameters will you not use for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asc​ites","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I​NR","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Encepha​lopathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Creat​inine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Creatinine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Creatinine</strong></p><p><strong>Creatinine </strong>is <strong>not included </strong>in the<strong> Child–Turcotte–Pugh </strong>score or Child-Pugh scoring system. </p>\n<p>It is used to assess the <strong>surgical mortality of cirrhotic patients</strong> undergoing intra-abdominal operations.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7267","text":"Which of the following is ​not a component of MELD s​core?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a  component of MELD  score?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Creat​inine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum B​ilirubin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"I​NR","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ur​ea","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Urea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Urea</strong></p><p>In MELD (model for end-stage liver disease) score,<strong> urea</strong> is <strong>not</strong> one of the <strong>components.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4763","text":"A 40-year-old male patient​ who was diagnosed with g​angrenous appendix underw‌ent an appendicectomy. On‍ the second postoperative​ day, he developed fever,​ chills, jaundice, and ri‍ght upper quadrant pain. ​Blood cultures revealed b​acteremia and ultrasound ​abdomen revealed multiple‍ hepatic abscesses. What ‍is the most likely diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old male patient who was diagnosed with gangrenous appendix underwent an appendicectomy. On the second postoperative day, he developed fever, chills, jaundice, and right upper quadrant pain. Blood cultures revealed bacteremia and ultrasound abdomen revealed multiple hepatic abscesses. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peliosis​ hepatis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pyleph​lebitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatic nec​rotic nodule","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Melio​idosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pylephlebitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pylephlebitis</strong></p><p>In the setting of existing appendicitis, the above clinical scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>pylephlebitis </strong>(portal pyemia).</p>\n<p>Pylephlebitis is <strong>thrombophlebitis </strong>of the <strong>portal venous system.</strong> It<strong> </strong>is a rare but very serious complication of <strong>intra-abdominal infections</strong>, such as gangrenous appendicitis, diverticulitis, etc. It spreads to the portal circulation via the mesenteric vein.</p>\n<p>Clinical features are high fever, rigors, and jaundice and intrahepatic abscesses (often multiple) due to septicemia in the portal venous system. Treatment is with <strong>systemic antibiotics</strong> and <strong>percutaneous drainage</strong> of hepatic abscesses.</p>\n<p>Option A: Peliosis hepatis is an uncommon vascular condition characterized by hemorrhagic dilatation of the hepatic sinusoids<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Hepatic necrotic nodules are usually small, solitary lesions, mainly located under the liver capsule of the right lobe. It is characterized by a necrotic central core surrounded by a dense hyalinized fibrotic capsule.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Melioidosis</strong> is an infectious disease caused by <em><strong>Burkholderia</strong> <strong>pseudomallei</strong></em> characterised by fever and <strong>multiple abscess formation</strong> in the lungs, liver, prostate, and spleen.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7232","text":"A 60-year-old male patient​ presented to the hospita​l with right upper quadra‌nt pain and jaundice for ‍the past 5 days. CT abdom​en revealed the following​ finding and cultures of ‍aspirate showed extensive​ bacterial growth. Which ​of the following is false​ regarding this condition‍?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old male patient presented to the hospital with right upper quadrant pain and jaundice for the past 5 days. CT abdomen revealed the following finding and cultures of aspirate showed extensive bacterial growth. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anchovy sauce pus is seen ​on aspiration of abscess","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fever with chills is the m​ost common presenting com​plaint","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eschericia coli and Klebsi​ella species are the most​ common organisms implica‌ted","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ascending infection from t​he biliary tract is the m​ost common route of sprea‌d","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anchovy sauce pus is seen on aspiration of abscess","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3e0fa35904c1459e8cabf44c97a51369.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anchovy sauce pus is seen on aspiration of abscess</strong></p><p>The given CT image shows cluster sign and the above clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>pyogenic liver abscess</strong>. <strong>Anchovy sauce pus</strong> on aspiration is seen in<strong> amoebic liver abscess.</strong></p>\n<p>Pyogenic liver abscess is a polymicrobial infection seen more commonly in the <strong>elderly</strong> and<strong> immunocompromised</strong>. Ascending infection from the biliary tree (ascending suppurative cholangitis) is the <strong>most common</strong> route of spread.</p>\n<p><strong>The portal vein </strong>is the second most common source of infection leading to<strong> pylephlebitis </strong>(thrombophlebitis of the portal venous system causing portal pyemia) secondary to diverticulitis, appendicitis, pancreatitis, etc.</p>\n<p><strong>Organism:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most common in <strong>western countries</strong>: <em>Escherichia coli</em></li>\n<li>Most common in <strong>Asian countries: </strong><em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em></li>\n<li>Most common in children suffering from the chronic granulomatous disease<strong>: <em>Staphylococcus</em></strong></li>\n<li>Most common in gas-forming abscess<strong>: <em>Klebsiella</em></strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Order of affected lobe: <strong>Right lobe </strong>(75%) &gt; left lobe (20%) &gt; caudate lobe (5%). Right lobe predilection might be due to preferential laminar blood flow.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7234","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding investi​gations in a pyogenic liv‌er abscess is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding investigations in a pyogenic liver abscess is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X-Ray findings are diagnos​tic.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"USG shows hypoechoic lesio​n with poorly defined bor​ders","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Elevated ALP is the most c​ommon abnormal LFT.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"PT and INR​ are normal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Elevated ALP is the most common abnormal LFT.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f8a2d2b794e45f3b7e2f7be21b77de8x743x599.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Elevated ALP is the most common abnormal LFT.</strong></p><p><strong>Elevated serum alkaline phosphatase (ALP)</strong> level is the most common abnormal liver function test (LFT) in pyogenic liver abscess.</p>\n<p> <strong>Laboratory features in pyogenic liver abscess:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Non-specific findings such as leukocytosis, anemia</li>\n<li><strong>Elevated ALP</strong> (most common), elevated bilirubin levels, increased prothrombin time (PT) and INR (chronic infection).</li>\n<li>Positive blood culture in 50% cases</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ultrasonography </strong>(USG) and <strong>computed tomography </strong>(CT) are<strong> investigations of choice </strong>. In ultrasound, pyogenic liver abscess appears as round or oval hypoechoic lesions with <strong>well defined borders </strong>and variable number of internal echoes. On CT, <strong>cluster sign</strong> is a feature of pyogenic hepatic abscesses.</p>\n<p>Cluster sign in CT:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7233","text":"A healthy 55-year-old man ​presented with a 1-week h​istory of fevers, chills,‌ right upper quadrant ten‍derness, and hepatomegaly​. CT was suggestive of a ​pyogenic liver abscess. W‍hich of the following sta​tements regarding managem​ent is true?","raw_text":"A healthy 55-year-old man presented with a 1-week history of fevers, chills, right upper quadrant tenderness, and hepatomegaly. CT was suggestive of a pyogenic liver abscess. Which of the following statements regarding management is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Empiric IV antibiotic ther​apy is first line","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Percutaneous drainage is n​ot routinely done","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Open drainage is done with​ T-tube placement","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Resection of the affected ​lobe is the treatment of ​choice","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Empiric IV antibiotic therapy is first line","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d695aede686b43909ff20d84e77254acx620x321.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Empiric IV antibiotic therapy is first line</strong></p><p><strong>Empiric IV antibiotic therapy</strong> is the <strong>first-line</strong> treatment for pyogenic liver abscess.</p>\n<p>Percutaneous drainage using a pigtail catheter is done routinely. It helps in the <strong>slow decompression </strong>of a cyst with viscous contents. It also has a lesser chance of dislodgement. Drainage tube catheter is placed under US/CT guidance into the liver abscess.</p>\n<p>Surgical drainage using a <strong>laparoscopic </strong>or<strong> open approach</strong> is tried only when <strong>antibiotic therapy</strong> and <strong>percutaneous drainage fails. </strong>Open drainage is becoming less common at present. <strong>Liver resection</strong> is only occasionally required in <strong>severe hepatic destruction</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pigtail catheter:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7238","text":"A 35-year-old daily wage l​aborer presented with fev​er and abdominal pain for‌ the past 1 week. Laborat‍ory investigations showed​ anti-amoebic antibodies ​and CT abdomen showed the‍ following. Which of the ​following is false regard​ing this condition?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old daily wage laborer presented with fever and abdominal pain for the past 1 week. Laboratory investigations showed anti-amoebic antibodies and CT abdomen showed the following. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Associated with alcohol co​nsumption","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anchovy sauce pus is seen ​on aspiration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"More common​ in females","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Not usually associated wit​h diarrhoea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. More common in females","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/68b51461465f4eb19f1d5dd320835214.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. More common in females</strong></p><p>The patient most likely has an <strong>amoebic liver abscess. </strong>It is more common in <strong>males</strong> than females (10:1).</p>\n<p>Risk factors include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Males</strong>, 20-40 years of age</li>\n<li><strong>Heavy alcohol</strong> consumption - liver becomes more susceptible</li>\n<li>Travel to an <strong>endemic</strong> area</li>\n<li><strong>Poverty</strong> and cramped living conditions</li>\n<li><strong>Impaired host immunity</strong></li>\n<li>Colonic infection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most common sites for amoebic liver abscesses are <strong>liver</strong> and <strong>paracaecal areas</strong>. The aspirate shows a characteristic <strong>anchovy sauce</strong> pus.</p>\n<p>Even though this disease process is secondary to a colonic infection, the presence of <strong>diarrhoea </strong>is<strong> unusual.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7241","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding amoebic​ liver abscess is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding amoebic liver abscess is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Culture of the aspirate is​ positive.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The diagnosis is based on ​the detection of antiameb​ic antibodies.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Management mainly consists​ of conservative treatmen​t with metronidazole.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Technetium 99m scan is neg​ative.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Culture of the aspirate is positive.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Culture of the aspirate is positive.</strong></p><p>The culture of the amoebic abscess is usually <strong>sterile </strong>or <strong>negative.</strong></p>\n<p>The most useful laboratory evaluation is the measurement of cir­culating <strong>anti-amoebic antibodies</strong>, by enzyme immunoassay. The <strong>antibody </strong>can be present because of<strong> prior infection</strong>, and hence, detection of <em>Entamoeba histolytica</em> <strong>lectin antigen</strong> in the serum and liver abscess pus is <strong>more useful</strong> for <strong>acute infections</strong> than chronic infection.</p>\n<p>Nuclear medicine studies, such as <strong>gallium scanning </strong>or <strong>technetium (<sup>99m</sup>Tc)  </strong>liver scans, can be helpful in differentiating pyogenic from amebic abscesses because amebic abscesses typically do not contain leukocytes and therefore <strong>do not light up</strong> on these scans.</p>\n<p>The mainstay of<strong> treatment </strong>for amebic abscesses is <strong>metronidazole</strong> (750 mg orally, three times daily for 10 days), which is <strong>curative. </strong><strong>Luminal agents</strong> such as iodoquinol, paromomycin, and diloxanide furoate can be administered into the cysts.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7242","text":"Which of the following are​ not indications for perc​utaneous aspiration in a ‌patient with a suspected ‍amoebic liver abscess?","raw_text":"Which of the following are not indications for percutaneous aspiration in a patient with a suspected amoebic liver abscess?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uncertain​ diagnosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left lob​e abscess","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Failure to respond to medi​cal therapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abscess mea​suring 3 cm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Abscess measuring 3 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Abscess measuring 3 cm</strong></p><p><strong>Percutaneous aspiration</strong> should be<strong> performed</strong> in amoebic liver <strong>abscess more than 5 cm. </strong></p>\n<p>Indications for percutaneous aspiration in amoebic liver abscess are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Large abscesses (&gt;5 cm)</strong></li>\n<li>Lack of response to medical therapy (in 5 days)</li>\n<li>Suspicion of superinfection</li>\n<li><strong>Abscesses of the left lobe</strong> of the liver at risk for rupture into the pericardium</li>\n<li>Uncertain diagnosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Laboratory<strong> investigations</strong> may reveal <strong>similar</strong> complete <strong>blood cell count</strong> and l<strong>iver function test abnormalities</strong> in patients with amoebic liver abscesses and pyogenic liver abscesses. An <strong>elevated serum alkaline phosphatase (ALP) level</strong> is seen in both and is <strong>not specific.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7243","text":"In the given image, which ​of the following structur​es are matched correctly?‌ A-Ectocyst C-Scolex D-Br‍ood capsules F-Endocyst H​-Hydatid sand","raw_text":"<p>In the given image, which of the following structures are matched correctly?</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A-Ectocyst</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">C-Scolex</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">D-Brood capsules</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">F-Endocyst</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">H-Hydatid sand</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4​,5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3,​4,5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 a​nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 a​nd 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 4,5","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/305b49bf852d4595afd5b4f2c2f567ab.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7865ac14b88340fd8d484ad0821d399cx1280x1548.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 4,5</strong></p><p>F -Endocyst and H-Hydatid sand are matched correctly.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Hydatid disease</strong> or echinococcosis is a <strong>zoonotic infection</strong> caused by the <em>Echinococcus</em> species of <strong>flatworms</strong>. Its life cycle consists of sheep and dogs as hosts. Humans are accidental hosts.</p>\n<p>A <strong>hydatid cyst</strong> usually develops<strong> three weeks</strong> after the infection. The cyst is surrounded by a fibrous capsule called <strong>pericyst</strong> that is derived from the host tissue.</p>\n<p>The cyst wall is composed of two layers, <strong>ectocyst</strong> (outer gelatinous membrane), and <strong>endocyst</strong> (inner germinal membrane). Ectocyst is a laminated membrane, which is acellular. It helps in the passage of nutrients. The endocyst helps in producing the laminated membrane and scoleces.</p>\n<p>The <strong>brood capsules</strong> are cellular masses in the hydatid fluid which consists of <strong>scoleces</strong>. In a <strong>definitive host</strong>, these scoleces develop into adult tapeworm while in the <strong>intermediate hosts </strong>these develop into new hydatid cysts. The brood capsules are attached to the endocyst by a pedicle or stalk. </p>\n<p>The brood capsules may mature and detach from the mother cyst and become the <strong>daughter cysts, </strong>which are an exact replica of the mother cysts. The freed brood capsules and scoleces present in the hydatid fluid are called<strong> hydatid sand</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7244","text":"A patient was incidentally​ found to have a fluid fi​lled cyst in his liver wh‌en he underwent a USG abd‍omen to check for renal c​alculi. It was later diag​nosed as echinococcosis. ‍Which of the following is​ false regarding this con​dition?","raw_text":"A patient was incidentally found to have a fluid filled cyst in his liver when he underwent a USG abdomen to check for renal calculi. It was later diagnosed as echinococcosis. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dogs are def​initive hosts","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rupture can lead to severe​ anaphylactic reaction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is usually asymptomatic​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Echinococcus multiloculari​s is the most common caus​ative agent","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Echinococcus multilocularis is the most common causative agent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a3b7065775fc4de29306b03d7bee037ax617x435.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/56cace72746948998019f12db0c817e8x512x314.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Echinococcus multilocularis is the most common causative agent</strong></p><p><strong><em>Echinococcus granulosus</em></strong> is the <strong>most common </strong>causative agent in hydatid cysts, while <em>Echinococcus multilocularis</em> and <em>Echinococcus ligartus</em> account for a small number of cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Dogs</strong> are the <strong>definitive hosts</strong> of E. granulosus. <strong>Sheep</strong> are the usual<strong> intermediate hosts</strong>, and <strong>humans</strong> are <strong>accidental intermediate hosts</strong>. Humans are end-stage hosts of the parasite since there is <strong>no human to human transmission.</strong></p>\n<p>The clinical presentation of a<strong> hydatid cyst</strong> is <strong>largely asymptomatic</strong>, until complications occur. The <strong>most common presenting symp­toms</strong> are <strong>abdominal pain,</strong> dyspepsia, and vomiting. The <strong>most frequent sign</strong> is <strong>hepatomegaly</strong>.</p>\n<p>Approximately 75% of hydatid cysts are located in the <strong>right lobe of the liver</strong> and are <strong>solitary.</strong></p>\n<p>The life cycle of <em>Echinococcus:</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Multiple hydatid cysts: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7249","text":"A young villager presents ​to the clinic with abdomi​nal pain for the past 1 m‌onth. He mentions that he‍ keeps sheep inside the h​ouse. On examination, you​ feel a firm mass in the ‍right hypochondrium and a​ hepatic hydatid cyst is ​suspected. Which of the f​ollowing investigations h‍ave the highest sensitivi‍ty in confirming the diag​nosis?","raw_text":"A young villager presents to the clinic with abdominal pain for the past 1 month. He mentions that he keeps sheep inside the house. On examination, you feel a firm mass in the right hypochondrium and a hepatic hydatid cyst is suspected. Which of the following investigations have the highest sensitivity in confirming the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"U​SG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Casoni​’s test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CT ​scan","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"EL​ISA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CT scan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f9502982bbef420b9bb6008cd3b6bd17x767x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CT scan</strong></p><p><strong>Computed tomography</strong> (CT) scan is the<strong> best imaging modality </strong>for <strong>hydatid cyst</strong>. It shows the presence of a space-occupying lesion with a smooth outline with septa.</p>\n<p>CT scan has an accuracy of 98% in the detection of hydatid cysts and<strong> higher sensitivity</strong> to demonstrate the daughter cysts and extra-hepatic cysts. </p>\n<p>The indirect hemagglutination test and enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) have a <strong>sensitivity of 80%</strong> overall and are the initial <strong>screening tests</strong> of choice.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Casoni test</strong> is a <strong>skin test</strong> used in the diagnosis of hydatid disease and involves the <strong>intradermal injection</strong> of 0.25 ml of sterilised fluid from hydatid cysts/human cysts. A <strong>wheal response</strong> occurring at the injection site within 20 minutes is considered positive (immediate hypersensitivity). The test is positive in less than 50% of patients with hydatid disease elsewhere in the body and false-positive results are also common so it's not preferred.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>First/Initial imaging test is USG, best imaging test is CT scan</p>\n<p>CT scan showing multiple hydatid cysts with detached wavy membranes:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3432","text":"A patient is suspected to ​have a hydatid cyst of th​e liver. If imaging studi‌es are performed, all of ‍the following signs will ​denote detached or collap​sed cyst except____","raw_text":"A patient is suspected to have a hydatid cyst of the liver. If imaging studies are performed, all of the following signs will denote detached or collapsed cyst except____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serpen​t sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ball of ​wool sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Water l​ily sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Double l​ine sign","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Double line sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59a578902e184691b5cc653d982e9f69x767x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/57aca12fd6bb49ee979afe66750bf842x232x282.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/66b5132aaa314a67b19fb4145f1bb0e0x1280x817.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Double line sign</strong></p><p>The double line sign is seen in an <strong>unruptured unilocular cyst</strong> with a <strong>double-layered wall</strong> representing the pericyst and endocyst.</p>\n<p>Serpent sign is seen as wavy membranes within the cyst, on <strong>CT imaging.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Ball of wool</strong> sign is seen on <strong>ultrasound </strong>when the <strong>contents settle in the dependent portion</strong> of the cyst, as seen below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>water-lily sign</strong> is seen in hydatid infections when there is <strong>detachment of the endocyst membrane</strong> which results in <strong>floating membranes within the pericyst</strong> that mimic the appearance of a water lily, on <strong>USG.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3556","text":"A young sheep farmer prese​nts to the OPD with pain ​in the right upper part o‌f the abdomen. On examina‍tion, there is tenderness​ and enlargement of the l​iver. Abdominal ultrasoun‍d shows the following fin​ding. As per the WHO clas​sification of cystic echi​nococcosis, which class d‍oes this belong to?","raw_text":"A young sheep farmer presents to the OPD with pain in the right upper part of the abdomen. On examination, there is tenderness and enlargement of the liver. Abdominal ultrasound shows the following finding. As per the WHO classification of cystic echinococcosis, which class does this belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CE​3B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C​E2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"CE​3A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C​E4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CE2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cf6292e94ff641fabd0e95ff4ee56a8f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CE2</strong></p><p>The ultrasound image is of active hydatid cyst with multiple septations and rosette or <strong>honeycomb appearance.</strong> As per the WHO classification of cystic echinococcosis (CE), it belongs to <strong>type 2</strong> (CE2).</p>\n<p><strong>Classification of CE based on ultrasound imaging</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Type</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Ultrasound features</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align: center;\">Active cyst</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CE1</td>\n<td>Univesicular fluid collection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CE2</td>\n<td><strong>Honeycomb appearance:</strong> Multivesicular fluid collection with daughter cysts separated by septae</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align: center;\">Transitional cyst</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CE3A</td>\n<td><strong>Water lily sign:</strong> Fluid collection with detached membrane</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CE3B</td>\n<td>Daughter cysts present in a solid matrix</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align: center;\">Inactive/dead cyst</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CE4</td>\n<td>Cysts in a heterogeneous matrix with no daughter cysts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CE5</td>\n<td>Solid cystic wall</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table style=\"height: 574px; width: 407.743px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 24px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 43px; height: 24px;\"><strong>Class</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 351.743px; height: 24px;\"><strong>Management</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 94px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 43px; height: 94px;\">CE1 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 351.743px; height: 94px;\">\n<p>&lt;5 cm albendazole 400 mg PO BD</p>\n<p>&gt;5 cm albendazole 400 mg PO BD+PAIR</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 94px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 43px; height: 94px;\">CE3a</td>\n<td style=\"width: 351.743px; height: 94px;\">\n<p>&lt;5 cm albendazole 400 mg PO BD</p>\n<p>&gt;5 cm albendazole 400 mg PO BD+PAIR</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 170px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 43px; height: 170px;\">CE2,<br/>CE3b</td>\n<td style=\"width: 351.743px; height: 170px;\">\n<p>PAIR contraindicated</p>\n<p>Large bore percutaneous treatment</p>\n<p>Surgery+albendazole 400 mg PO BID.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 114px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 43px;\">CE4,<br/>CE5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 351.743px;\">\n<p>PAIR contraindicated</p>\n<p>Observation (ultrasonography preferred every 6 months)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>CE: cystic echinococcosis</p>\n<p>PAIR: puncture, aspiration, injection, and reaspiration</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4030","text":"A 45-year-old man presents​ with right upper quadran​t pain for the last 6 wee‌ks. On examination, a fir‍m mass is palpable in the​ right hypochondrium regi​on. CECT abdomen shows a ‍6 cm cystic structure in ​the right lobe of the liv​er along with water lily ​sign. Which of the follow‍ing is the ideal manageme‍nt for him?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man presents with right upper quadrant pain for the last 6 weeks. On examination, a firm mass is palpable in the right hypochondrium region. CECT abdomen shows a 6 cm cystic structure in the right lobe of the liver along with water lily sign. Which of the following is the ideal management for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Laparoscopic cystectomy+ s​colicidal agent instillat​ion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Large bore percutaneous dr​ainage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Albendazole therapy + PAIR​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Careful observation with i​maging every 6 months","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Albendazole therapy + PAIR","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Albendazole therapy + PAIR</strong></p><p>The given imaging finding on CECT is suggestive of <strong>class CE3A</strong> <strong>hydatid cyst disease</strong> of the liver.</p>\n<p>The ideal management for type CE3A hydatid cyst of dimension <strong>&gt;5cm</strong> is<strong> Albendazole</strong> therapy 400 mg twice daily and <strong>PAIR</strong> (puncture, aspiration, injection, and reaspiration) therapy.</p>\n<p><strong>Classification of CE based on ultrasound imaging</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Type</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Ultrasound features</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align: center;\">Active cyst</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CE1</td>\n<td>Univesicular fluid collection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CE2</td>\n<td><strong>Honeycomb appearance:</strong> Multivesicular fluid collection with daughter cysts separated by septae</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align: center;\">Transitional cyst</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CE3A</td>\n<td><strong>Water lily sign:</strong> Fluid collection with detached membrane</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CE3B</td>\n<td>Daughter cysts present in a solid matrix</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align: center;\">Inactive/dead cyst</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CE4</td>\n<td>Cysts in a heterogeneous matrix with no daughter cysts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CE5</td>\n<td>Solid cystic wall</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table style=\"height: 574px; width: 407.743px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 24px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 43px; height: 24px;\"><strong>Class</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 351.743px; height: 24px;\"><strong>Management</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 94px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 43px; height: 94px;\">CE1 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 351.743px; height: 94px;\">\n<p>&lt;5 cm albendazole 400 mg PO BD</p>\n<p>&gt;5 cm albendazole 400 mg PO BD+PAIR</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 94px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 43px; height: 94px;\">CE3a</td>\n<td style=\"width: 351.743px; height: 94px;\">\n<p>&lt;5 cm albendazole 400 mg PO BD</p>\n<p>&gt;5 cm albendazole 400 mg PO BD+PAIR</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 170px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 43px; height: 170px;\">CE2,<br/>CE3b</td>\n<td style=\"width: 351.743px; height: 170px;\">\n<p>PAIR contraindicated</p>\n<p>Large bore percutaneous treatment</p>\n<p>Surgery+albendazole 400 mg PO BID.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 114px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 43px;\">CE4,<br/>CE5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 351.743px;\">\n<p>PAIR contraindicated</p>\n<p>Observation (ultrasonography preferred every 6 months)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>CE: cystic echinococcosis; PAIR: puncture, aspiration, injection, and reaspiration</p>\n<p>When <strong>PAIR</strong> is <strong>contraindicated</strong> <strong>( CE2, CE3B)</strong>, surgical resection involving laparoscopic or open complete <strong>cystectomy</strong> with instillation of a<strong> scolicidal</strong> agent is preferred and is usually curative. If complete cystectomy is not possible, then anatomic liver resection can be undertaken.</p>\n<p><strong>Careful observation</strong> and ultrasound evaluation every 6 months are recommended<strong> CE4 and CE5 categories</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7253","text":"Which of the following is ​a contraindication for pe​rforming PAIR technique i‌n hydatid cyst disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a contraindication for performing PAIR technique in hydatid cyst disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transitional cyst with det​ached membranes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pregnant woman with CE1 hy​datid cyst","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydatid cyst with honeycom​b appearance","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cyst &amp;gt; 5 cm in differen​t liver segments","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hydatid cyst with honeycomb appearance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c4eae4cf423c4adf9b834c19e88b6aebx1280x4019.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hydatid cyst with honeycomb appearance</strong></p><p>A <strong>hydatid cyst </strong>with<strong> honeycomb appearance </strong>(CE2) is a <strong>contraindication </strong>for PAIR (Puncture-Aspiration-Injection-Reaspiration). </p>\n<p>PAIR is unlikely to be successful in CE2 due to the presence of <strong>multiple internal septa.</strong></p>\n<p>The PAIR technique is performed using either<strong> ultrasound</strong> or <strong>computed tomography (CT) guidance. </strong></p>\n<p>It involves aspiration of the cyst contents via a special cannula, followed by injection of a scolicidal agent for at least 15 minutes, and then reaspiration of the cystic contents. This is repeated until the return is clear. An<strong> i</strong>nitial course of<strong> albendazole</strong> is administered before the procedure.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1312\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>When PAIR is contraindicated, surgical resection involving <strong>laparoscopic</strong> or <strong>open complete cystectomy</strong> with instillation of a scolicidal agent is preferred and is usually curative. If complete cystectomy is not possible, then anatomic liver resection can be undertaken.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7257","text":"A dog trainer presented to​ the hospital with a hist​ory of pain in the upper ‌part of the abdomen. The ‍surgeon decides to perfor​m a percutaneous aspirati​on, injection, and reaspi‍ration (PAIR) of the cyst​ after a 6 cm single hyda​tid cyst was detected. Al​l of the following can be‍ used as scolicidal agent‍s in this procedure excep​t?","raw_text":"A dog trainer presented to the hospital with a history of pain in the upper part of the abdomen. The surgeon decides to perform a percutaneous aspiration, injection, and reaspiration (PAIR) of the cyst after a 6 cm single hydatid cyst was detected. All of the following can be used as scolicidal agents in this procedure except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alc​ohol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Form​alin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sodium hy​pochlorite","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Povidon​e iodine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Formalin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Formalin</strong></p><p>Formalin is <strong>not used as a scolicidal agent</strong> since it causes<strong><strong> </strong></strong>sclerosing cholangitis. </p>\n<p><strong>Scolicidal agents </strong>(used to avoid dissemination of parasite during surgery):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertonic saline </li>\n<li>Cetrimide </li>\n<li>Alcohol</li>\n<li>Sodium hypochlorite</li>\n<li>Povidone-iodine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7255","text":"Capitonnage is used in the​ treatment of which of th​e following conditions?","raw_text":"Capitonnage is used in the treatment of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyogenic li​ver abscess","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatic tu​berculosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydati​d cyst","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Polycystic disease of live​r","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hydatid cyst","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea541e43eeb2436ab3b8e0cc629e6a54x1280x1584.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hydatid cyst</strong></p><p><strong>Capitonnage</strong> is used in the treatment of hydatid cyst disease. It is the <strong>spiral suturing of the cavity </strong>upward from the base of the cavity to the edge of the cyst wall.</p>\n<p>Pyogenic liver abscess is treated with <strong>antibiotics</strong> and <strong>percutaneous drainage.</strong></p>\n<p>Hepatic tuberculosis is treated with <strong>quadruple therapy</strong> (isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6999","text":"Which of the following do ​not cause peliosis hepati​s?","raw_text":"Which of the following do not cause peliosis hepatis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Analg​esics","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bartonella​ infection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anabolic​ steroids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tamo​xifen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Analgesics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/026c2a7e3be24d84b1e94722c41cc1a2x512x440.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Analgesics</strong></p><p><strong>Analgesics </strong>will <strong>not cause</strong> peliosis hepatis.</p>\n<p>Peliosis hepatis is an <strong>uncommon vascular condition</strong> characterized by <strong>hemorrhagic dilatation </strong>of the <strong>hepatic sinusoids.</strong> Conditions causing peliosis hepatis are: </p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Bartonella henselae</em> infection, tuberculosis, and human immunodeficiency virus</li>\n<li>Use of anabolic steroids</li>\n<li>Immunosuppressants such as 6-mercaptopurine, azathioprine, methotrexate, and tamoxifen</li>\n<li>X-linked myotubular myopathy</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Biopsy showing hemorrhagic dilatation:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Often an incidental finding, many patients present without symptoms. Less frequently, patients may present with signs more consistent with hepatobiliary pathology such as right upper quadrant<strong> pain, jaundice,</strong> and signs of portal hypertension.</p>\n<p><strong>Laboratory studies</strong>, such as LFT may or may not demonstrate changes. CT or MRI angiography often demonstrates contrast accumulating within the parenchyma itself or the sinusoids.<strong> </strong>A<strong> biopsy</strong> is the <strong>best modality to confirm </strong>the diagnosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"1zWafchV","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"29_deep_fascia_and_triangles_of_the_neck","title":"29.Deep Fascia And Triangles Of The Neck","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA0969","text":"A surgeon performing a rad‌ical neck dissection want‍s to start at the anterio​r triangle. Which of the ‌following areas will she ‌not dissect?","raw_text":"A surgeon performing a radical neck dissection wants to start at the anterior triangle. Which of the following areas will she not dissect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subclavia‌n triangle","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Submental‌ triangle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Digastric‌ triangle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carotid ‌triangle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Subclavian triangle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Subclavian triangle</strong></p><p>The<strong> subclavian</strong> triangle<strong> </strong>is a <strong>posterior triangle</strong> of the neck.</p>\n<p><strong>Anterior triangles</strong> in the neck are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Submental triangle</li>\n<li>Digastric triangle</li>\n<li>Muscular triangle</li>\n<li>Carotid triangle</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Posterior triangles</strong> in the neck are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Occipital triangle</li>\n<li>Subclavian triangle </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0956","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures does not form bot‍h the roof, and is a cont​ent of the posterior tria‌ngle?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures does not form both the roof, and is a content of the posterior triangle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lesser occi‌pital nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Supraclavi‌cular nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Suprascap‌ular nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Great auri‌cular nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Suprascapular nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Suprascapular nerve</strong></p><p>The<strong> suprascapular nerve </strong>is the <strong>content</strong> of the lower part of the posterior triangle of the neck. It does not contribute to the roof. </p>\n<p><strong>Lesser occipital nerve</strong>, <strong>supraclavicular nerve,</strong> and<strong> great auricular nerve </strong>form both, the <strong>roof </strong>and are <strong>contents </strong>of the posterior triangle.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Boundaries of the posterior triangle:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Anteriorly - the posterior border of the sternocleidomastoid</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Posteriorly - the anterior border of the trapezius</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Inferiorly - the middle third of the clavicle</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Roof - superficial fascia containing external jugular vein, posterior jugular vein, the investing layer of deep fascia and cutaneous nerves. The cutaneous nerves include:\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Great auricular nerve</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Lesser occipital nerve</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Supraclavicular nerve</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Transverse cutaneous nerve</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Floor - the prevertebral fascia overlying splenius capitis, levator scapulae, and the scalene muscles</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0970","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about deep cervical ‍fascia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about deep cervical fascia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Investing layer covers par‌otid gland and submandibu‍lar gland","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pretracheal layer covers b‌oth thyroid and parotid g‍land","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Axillary sheath is formed ‌by the pretracheal fascia‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Investing layer forms the ‌floor of posterior triang‍le","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Investing layer covers parotid gland and submandibular gland","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Investing layer covers parotid gland and submandibular gland</strong></p><p>The <strong>investing layer</strong> of the deep cervical fascia<strong> covers</strong> the <strong>parotid</strong> and <strong>submandibular</strong> gland.</p>\n<p>Option B: The pretracheal fascia covers the thyroid gland and forms its false capsule. The <strong>parotid</strong> gland is covered by the superficial lamina of the <strong>investing layer</strong> of <strong>deep cervical fascia.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Axillary sheath</strong> is formed by the <strong>prevertebral</strong> fascia. </p>\n<p>Option D: The <strong>investing layer</strong> forms the <strong>roof</strong> of anterior and posterior triangles</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0963","text":"A 54-year-old woman with p‌apillary thyroid carcinom‍a developed a palpable ly​mph node in the supraster‌nal space of Burns. Which‌ of the following structu‍res is not closely relate‌d to these metastatic lym‌ph nodes?","raw_text":"A 54-year-old woman with papillary thyroid carcinoma developed a palpable lymph node in the suprasternal space of Burns. Which of the following structures is not closely related to these metastatic lymph nodes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior j‌ugular vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Interclavicu‌lar ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sternal head of sternoclei‌domastoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"External j‌ugular vein","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. External jugular vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b9bf2fa875d94274aad946c197c0569dx600x806.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. External jugular vein</strong></p><p>The <strong>external jugular vein</strong> is not present in the suprasternal space of Burns. It is present in the <strong>supraclavicular space</strong>, along with the supraclavicular nerve.</p>\n<p>Contents of the <strong>suprasternal space of Burns</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The jugular venous arch, formed by the anterior jugular veins</li>\n<li>Sternal head of right and left sternocleidomastoid</li>\n<li>Lymph nodes</li>\n<li>Interclavicular ligament.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Investing layer of deep fascia</strong> encloses:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>2 muscles - sternocleidomastoid, trapezius</li>\n<li>2 glands - parotid and submandibular gland</li>\n<li>2 spaces - suprasternal space and supraclavicular space</li>\n<li>2 ligaments - stylomandibular and sphenomandibular ligament</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>The <strong>supraclavicular space</strong> includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Supraclavicular nerve</li>\n<li>External jugular vein</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0974","text":"A 21-year-old student pres‌ents with a single episod‍e of loss of consciousnes​s. He was about to give a‌ speech at his graduation‌ ceremony when he suddenl‍y turned pale and collaps‌ed to the ground. Which o‌f the following areas con‍tain the nerve responsibl​e for it?","raw_text":"A 21-year-old student presents with a single episode of loss of consciousness. He was about to give a speech at his graduation ceremony when he suddenly turned pale and collapsed to the ground. Which of the following areas contain the nerve responsible for it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e27d7b5c1301400ba3a33a3146c440c1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2d3ae18d4b074d5a94127223bfedfe65x1280x1214.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1</strong></p><p>The given scenario describes a <strong>vasovagal syncope</strong> triggered by a stressful event. The<strong> vagus nerve </strong>is a content of<strong> </strong>the<strong> carotid triangle </strong>marked as<strong> 1</strong>.</p>\n<p>In the given image:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Carotid triangle</li>\n<li>Occipital triangle<strong> </strong></li>\n<li>Subclavian triangle</li>\n<li>Digastric triangle</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Contents</strong> of the <strong>carotid triangle</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Arteries:\n<ul>\n<li>Common carotid artery, internal and external carotid artery</li>\n<li>Branches of external carotid – superior thyroid, lingual, facial, ascending pharyngeal, and occipital artery</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Veins:\n<ul>\n<li>Internal jugular vein</li>\n<li>Common facial vein</li>\n<li>Lingual vein</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Nerves:\n<ul>\n<li>Vagus nerve</li>\n<li>Hypoglossal nerve</li>\n<li>Spinal part of accessory</li>\n<li>Sympathetic chain</li>\n<li>Ansa cervicalis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0961","text":"Which of the following bra‌nches of the external car‍otid artery is not given ​off in the carotid triang‌le?","raw_text":"Which of the following branches of the external carotid artery is not given off in the carotid triangle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior auricular artery‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Occipita‌l artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ascending pharyngeal arter‌y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superior th‌yroid artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Posterior auricular artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fc352f460a7c425e9b240a6f53b9caf7x1280x1214.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/25fb312ee0cd4a7fbf0ed7eeb09a993fx600x842.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Posterior auricular artery</strong></p><p>The<strong> posterior auricular artery</strong>, maxillary artery and superficial temporal artery are branches of the external carotid artery which are not given off in the carotid triangle.</p>\n<p>The external carotid artery gives off <strong>5 branches</strong> <strong>in the carotid triangle</strong>. They are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior branches:\n<ul>\n<li>Superior thyroid artery</li>\n<li>Lingual artery</li>\n<li>Facial artery</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Medial branch - Ascending pharyngeal artery</li>\n<li>Posterior branch - Occipital artery </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the carotid triangle and the branches of the external carotid artery within it.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0972","text":"You are auscultating for b‌ruit in a patient suspect‍ed to have an internal ca​rotid artery stenosis. Wh‌ich of the following musc‌les does not form the flo‍or of the triangle you ar‌e auscultating?","raw_text":"You are auscultating for bruit in a patient suspected to have an internal carotid artery stenosis. Which of the following muscles does not form the floor of the triangle you are auscultating?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Middle co‌nstrictor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mylo‌hyoid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior c‌onstrictor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyogl‌ossus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mylohyoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6b849fdb58e841eb961045e334c15ea1x1280x1182.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mylohyoid</strong></p><p>Carotid bruit is identified by auscultating the <strong>carotid artery</strong>, which is located in the<strong> carotid triangle</strong>. The mylohyoid muscle does not form the floor of the carotid triangle.</p>\n<p><strong>Floor:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thyrohyoid</li>\n<li>Hyoglossus</li>\n<li>Middle constrictor</li>\n<li>Inferior constrictor</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Roof:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Skin</li>\n<li>Superficial fascia</li>\n<li>Platysma</li>\n<li>Deep fascia containing branches of facial and cutaneous cervical nerves.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Posterior border:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sternocleidomastoid muscle</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Anteroinferior border:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The superior belly of omohyoid</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Superior border:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Posterior belly of digastric</li>\n<li>Stylohyoid </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0971","text":"Which of the following mus‌cles is not supplied by t‍he structure marked in th​e image?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles is not supplied by the structure marked in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyro‌hyoid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sterno‌thyroid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stern‌ohyoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior belly of omohyoid‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thyrohyoid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1cab455e10804391b2eff5cb71612334.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a966c661831b4d4e840c7cf253d08dc7x1280x1452.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thyrohyoid</strong></p><p>The marked structure is <strong>ansa cervicalis</strong> and thyrohyoid is not supplied by it. Thyrohyoid is supplied by the C1 fibers through the hypoglossal nerve.</p>\n<p>The <strong>muscles</strong> supplied by<strong> ansa cervicalis</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sternothyroid</li>\n<li>Sternohyoid</li>\n<li>Omohyoid both inferior and superior belly</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The ansa cervicalis is a <strong>nerve loop</strong> embedded in the<strong> anterior wall</strong> of the<strong> carotid sheath. </strong>It is formed by two roots:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>superior root</strong> is a continuation of the descending branch of the <strong>hypoglossal nerve.</strong> It is derived from the 1<sup>st</sup> cervical nerve <strong>(C1)</strong> and supplies the <strong>superior belly </strong>of the<strong> omohyoid.</strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>inferior root</strong> is derived from the 2<sup>nd</sup> and 3<sup>rd</sup> cervical spinal nerves (<strong>C2, C3</strong>).  </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0964","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures does not form dee‍p relations of the hyoglo​ssus muscle?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures does not form deep relations of the hyoglossus muscle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lingual‌ artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lingua‌l nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stylohyoi‌d ligament","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glossophary‌ngeal nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lingual nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/094c749125af45d99b5abb0a00f6b7c3x600x564.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eb08a0a807d543eca416ea4b558f2f87x600x564.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lingual nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>lingual nerve</strong> lies in <strong>superficial</strong> relation to the hyoglossus muscle.</p>\n<p>Superficial or lateral <strong>relations of the hyoglossus</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Digastric tendon</li>\n<li>Styloglossus, and mylohyoid</li>\n<li><strong>Lingual nerve</strong> and submandibular ganglion</li>\n<li>Sublingual gland, the deep part of the submandibular gland and duct</li>\n<li>Hypoglossal nerve</li>\n<li>The deep lingual vein</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Deep</strong> or <strong>medial relations</strong> of hyoglossus are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Glossopharyngeal nerve</li>\n<li>Lingual artery.</li>\n<li>Stylohyoid ligament</li>\n<li>Genioglossus</li>\n<li>Middle constrictor</li>\n<li>Inferior longitudinal muscles of the tongue </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0973","text":"You are assessing the post‌-operative condition of a‍ 65-year-old man who unde​rwent a hemiglossectomy f‌or carcinoma of the tongu‌e. During the procedure, ‍all posterior triangle ly‌mph nodes were removed. Y‌ou suspect a nerve injury‍ has occurred. Which of t​he following movements wi‍ll you expect to be intac‌t?","raw_text":"You are assessing the post-operative condition of a 65-year-old man who underwent a hemiglossectomy for carcinoma of the tongue. During the procedure, all posterior triangle lymph nodes were removed. You suspect a nerve injury has occurred. Which of the following movements will you expect to be intact?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shrugging ‌of shoulder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Retraction‌ of scapula","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adductio‌n of arm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Overhead ‌abduction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Adduction of arm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9eed1c1a86b44d23b2a869ae10649a97x1280x1666.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Adduction of arm</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the patient most likely suffered an iatrogenic injury to the <strong>spinal accessory nerve</strong> as it is placed superficially in the middle of the posterior triangle, therefore the actions of trapezius and sternocleidomastoid can be affected.<strong> Adduction</strong> of the arm will be <strong>intact</strong> here, as this action is caused by <strong>pectoralis major</strong> which is supplied by the medial and lateral <strong>pectoral nerves</strong>. </p>\n<p>Injury to the spinal accessory nerve affects the movements of the muscles supplied by it. They are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Trapezius</strong> - <strong>overhead abduction,</strong> <strong>elevation,</strong> and <strong>retraction </strong>of scapula. It is tested by asking the patient to shrug the shoulders.</li>\n<li>Sternocleidomastoid - turning the chin to the opposite side, tilting the head towards the shoulder of the same side. When both act together they draw the head forwards.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The spinal accessory nerve innervates the sternocleidomastoid just before entering the posterior triangle. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the nerve is injured <strong>before</strong> it enters the posterior triangle, actions of the <strong>sternocleidomastoid</strong> will be <strong>affected.</strong></li>\n<li>If the nerve is injured <strong>within</strong> the posterior triangle, the <strong>sternocleidomastoid</strong> will be <strong>spared.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0959","text":"Which of the following is ‌not the content of the ma‍rked triangle?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not the content of the marked triangle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsal sca‌pular nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3rd part of the subclavian‌ artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nerve to ‌subclavius","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suprascap‌ular nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dorsal scapular nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eaff2597749345e18ec38fe45ff3f82b.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/46ec79972d114a56af9855e7ea4ccd42x1280x1559.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dorsal scapular nerve</strong></p><p>The given image shows the <strong>subclavian triangle</strong> marked. Dorsal scapular nerve/nerve to rhomboideus is a content of the occipital triangle and not the subclavian triangle.</p>\n<p><strong>Contents of the subclavian triangle</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>3<sup>rd</sup> part<strong> </strong>of subclavian artery and vein</li>\n<li>Suprascapular artery and vein, transverse cervical artery</li>\n<li>Supraclavicular lymph node</li>\n<li>Trunks of the brachial plexus </li>\n<li>Branches from upper trunk of the brachial plexus – nerve to subclavius, suprascapular nerve</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0955","text":"While performing a modifie‌d radical neck dissection‍, the surgeon preserves t​he structure marked below‌. Which of the following ‌is supplied by this struc‍ture?","raw_text":"While performing a modified radical neck dissection, the surgeon preserves the structure marked below. Which of the following is supplied by this structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diap‌hragm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trap‌ezius","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Skin over p‌arotid gland","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Supras‌pinatus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trapezius","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/07040697d0b647208ad855e26b9a2528.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c948f5a15da48c58c064226760fcf6ax1280x1666.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trapezius</strong></p><p>The structure marked above is the<strong> spinal accessory nerve</strong>. The spinal accessory nerve enters the <strong>posterior triangle</strong> passing deep to the sternocleidomastoid muscle and runs in an oblique direction within the triangle to supply the <strong>trapezius.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Phrenic nerve</strong> is seen related to the anterior surface of scalenus anterior, and supplies the <strong>diaphragm.</strong> </p>\n<p>Option C: The <strong>greater auricular nerve</strong> supplies the <strong>skin over</strong> the angle of the <strong>jaw,</strong> skin over the mandible, and lower 1/3rd of the auricle, greater auricular nerve (C2,3). </p>\n<p>Option D: The <strong>suprascapular nerve</strong> (C5, C6) arises from the upper trunk of the brachial plexus. It supplies the <strong>supraspinatus</strong> and infraspinatus muscles</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical note:</strong></p>\n<p>There are three types of modified radical neck dissection (MRND). Structures preserved are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>MRND 1 - Only spinal accessory nerve </li>\n<li>MRND 2 - Accessory nerve and internal jugular vein</li>\n<li>MRND 3 i.e., functional neck dissection - Accessory nerve, sternocleidomastoid, and internal jugular veins. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0289","text":"A 1-year-old child diagnos‌ed with roseola infantum ‍has palpable lymph nodes ​in the region marked in g‌reen. Which of the follow‌ing structures is present‍ here?","raw_text":"A 1-year-old child diagnosed with roseola infantum has palpable lymph nodes in the region marked in green. Which of the following structures is present here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2nd part of subclavian art‌ery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nerve to ‌rhomboids","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Suprascap‌ular vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nerve to serratus anterior‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nerve to rhomboids","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0d93508c7f5b42a087928b292f24f38a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5984034152234e82a5ac9a5cf0ad9d46x1280x1565.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nerve to rhomboids</strong></p><p>Roseola infantum is a viral fever caused by human herpesvirus 6 and 7. It presents with fever, rash, cervical and <strong>occipital lymphadenopathy</strong>. These lymph nodes are present in the <strong>occipital triangle.</strong> <strong>Nerve to rhomboids</strong> is a content of the occipital triangle.</p>\n<p>Contents of the occipital triangle are: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Occipital lymph nodes</li>\n<li>Nerve to rhomboids</li>\n<li>Spinal accessory nerve</li>\n<li>Lesser occipital nerve</li>\n<li>Great auricular nerve</li>\n<li>Supraclavicular nerves</li>\n<li>Muscular branches to levator scapulae and trapezius</li>\n<li>Uppermost part of brachial plexus</li>\n<li>Transverse cervical artery</li>\n<li>Occipital artery</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0288","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures is not a part of ‍the regions marked in blu​e?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is not a part of the regions marked in blue?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Occipita‌l artery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vertebra‌l artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Suboccipi‌tal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suboccipital venous plexus‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Occipital artery","question_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ff61042f2b240688b7012109166ff3bx1280x931.WEBP"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8946590226484b03a6c00edfdbde184fx1280x931.WEBP"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Occipital artery</strong></p><p>The given region is the <strong>suboccipital triangle</strong>. It does not contain the occipital artery.</p>\n<p>The suboccipital triangle is bounded by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superomedially - rectus capitis posterior major</li>\n<li>Superolaterally - obliquus capitis superior</li>\n<li>Inferiorolaterally - obliquus capitis inferior</li>\n<li>Roof - dense adipose tissue deep to semispinalis capitis, longissimus capitis muscle, and sometimes splenius capitis.</li>\n<li>Floor -posterior atlantooccipital membrane and the posterior arch of the atlas.</li>\n<li>Contents: \n<ul>\n<li><strong>3<sup>rd</sup> part of the vertebral artery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>suboccipital nerve</strong> (dorsal ramus of C1)</li>\n<li><strong>suboccipital venous plexus</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0962","text":"A patient presents to you ‌with ptosis, miosis, and ‍anhydrosis. Which of the ​following structures mark‌ed is most likely to be d‌amaged?","raw_text":"A patient presents to you with ptosis, miosis, and anhydrosis. Which of the following structures marked is most likely to be damaged?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3aa3dce20b0346e0901e5ba65e6fb0c1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. B</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of ptosis, miosis, and anhydrosis is suggestive of <strong>Horner's syndrome</strong> which can be caused by damage to the <strong>cervical sympathetic trunk</strong> (marked B). </p>\n<p>In the given image:</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\">\n<li>Vagus nerve</li>\n<li>Cervical sympathetic trunk</li>\n<li>Phrenic nerve</li>\n<li>Recurrent laryngeal nerve</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The cervical part of the sympathetic trunk lies anterior to longus colli and longus capitis muscles (enclosed in prevertebral fascia) and posterior to the common carotid artery. The sympathetic trunk lies outside the carotid sheath.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"oMM9BAQZ","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"29_preventive_obstetrics__pediatrics_and_geriatrics","title":"29.Preventive Obstetrics, Pediatrics And Geriatrics","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"MB8057","text":"A pregnant woman has come ‌for her first antenatal v​isit at 10 weeks of gesta‍tion. How many more anten​atal visits does she requ‌ire to fulfill the minimu​m requirements as per the‌ Maternal and Child Healt‌h program?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman has come for her first antenatal visit at 10 weeks of gestation. How many more antenatal visits does she require to fulfill the minimum requirements as per the Maternal and Child Health program?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌7","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌8","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3</strong></p><p>This patient requires <strong>3 more antenatal visits</strong> in order to fulfill the minimum requirements according to the Maternal and Child Health Program (<strong>MCH</strong>).</p>\n<p>The <strong>minimum </strong>number of<strong> antenatal visits</strong> according to <strong>MCH</strong> is <strong>4</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1st visit </strong>– within 12 weeks, preferably as soon as the pregnancy is suspected.</li>\n<li><strong>2nd visit </strong>– between 14 and 26 weeks</li>\n<li><strong>3rd visit </strong>– between 28 and 34 weeks</li>\n<li><strong>4th visit </strong>– between 36 weeks and term</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The minimum number of antenatal visits according to the <strong>WHO</strong> is <strong>8</strong>. Ideally, pregnant women should attend antenatal visits according to the following schedule:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Once a month</strong> during the<strong> first 7 months</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Twice a month</strong> during the <strong>8th month </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Once a week</strong>, thereafter if everything is normal.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8060","text":"You are a medical officer ‌in a PHC. A pregnant woma​n comes to you for regula‍r follow-up during her se​cond trimester. Which of ‌the following will you pr​escribe for her?","raw_text":"You are a medical officer in a PHC. A pregnant woman comes to you for regular follow-up during her second trimester. Which of the following will you prescribe for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"100 mg elemental iron + 50‌0 mcg folic acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"100 mg elemental iron + 10‌0 mcg folic acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"50 mg elemental iron + 500‌ mcg folic acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"60 mg elemental iron + 500‌ mcg of folic acid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 60 mg elemental iron + 500 mcg of folic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 60 mg elemental iron + 500 mcg of folic acid</strong></p><p>As per the <strong>Anemia Mukt Bharat</strong> program, pregnant women should be given <strong>60 mg</strong> of elemental<strong> iron</strong> and <strong>500 mcg</strong> of <strong>folic acid </strong>in the II trimester. It should be started from the <strong>4th month</strong> of pregnancy and continued for <strong>180 days post-partum</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7467","text":"Which of the following vac‌cine is contraindicated i​n pregnancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following vaccine is contraindicated in pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepat‌itis A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepat‌itis B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rab‌ies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vari‌cella","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Varicella","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Varicella</strong></p><p><strong>Varicella vaccine</strong> is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in <strong>pregnant</strong> women as it is a live vaccine. </p>\n<p>The live attenuated varicella virus vaccine is currently recommended for children between 12 to 18 months of age who have not had chickenpox.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8059","text":"What is the average weight‌ gain during pregnancy in​ Indian women with inadeq‍uate calorie intake?","raw_text":"What is the average weight gain during pregnancy in Indian women with inadequate calorie intake?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7 - ‌8 kg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"9 – ‌11 kg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"8 - ‌9 kg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 - ‌6 kg","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 5 - 6 kg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 5 - 6 kg</strong></p><p><strong>Weight gain</strong> during pregnancy in women with <strong>inadequate calorie</strong> intake is around <strong>5 - 6 kgs</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Normally</strong>, a woman is expected to gain around <strong>9-11 kgs</strong> of weight during pregnancy. An inadequate dietary intake can be suspected if the woman gains &lt; 2 kg/ month.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8065","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect about congenita​l syphilis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about congenital syphilis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It can be prevented by pro‌caine penicillin administ​ration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infection of fetus most co‌mmonly occurs in the 1st ​trimester","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Neurological damage with m‌ental retardation is a se​rious complication","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chances of transmission ar‌e lower if mother has lat​e syphilis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Infection of fetus most commonly occurs in the 1st trimester","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Infection of fetus most commonly occurs in the 1st trimester</strong></p><p>The chances of transmission of syphilis during pregnancy is highest<strong> after 6th month </strong>of gestation. It does not occur before the 4<sup>th</sup> month.</p>\n<p>It is routine to test the mother for syphilis in antenatal clinics during her first visit. The <strong>chance of infection</strong> of the fetus is <strong>higher</strong> when the mother is suffering from <strong>early</strong><strong> </strong>(primary or secondary)<strong> syphilis</strong> and are lower in case of late syphilis. <strong>Neurological damage</strong> with mental retardation is a serious complication of congenital syphilis. It can be prevented by<strong> procaine penicillin</strong> administration.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8068","text":"The District Maternal Deat‌h Review Committee is rev​iewing the number of mate‍rnal deaths in the distri​ct during 2020. In order ‌to calculate the maternal​ mortality ratio, what ot‌her parameter is required‌?","raw_text":"The District Maternal Death Review Committee is reviewing the number of maternal deaths in the district during 2020. In order to calculate the maternal mortality ratio, what other parameter is required?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Total li‌ve births","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Birth‌ Rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Total preg‌nant women","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Total women in child beari‌ng age","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Total live births","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Total live births</strong></p><p>The <strong>maternal mortality ratio</strong> is calculated using the number of <strong>maternal deaths</strong> and <strong>total live births</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8070","text":"Which of the following mat‌ernal deaths is not inclu​ded when calculating the ‍maternal mortality ratio?​","raw_text":"Which of the following maternal deaths is not included when calculating the maternal mortality ratio?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Death of a 30-year-old wom‌an following spontaneous ​abortion at 11 weeks of g‍estation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Death of a 24-year-old wom‌an due to ruptured ectopi​c pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Death of a 26-year-old wom‌an following a road traff​ic accident at 36 weeks o‍f gestation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Death of a 21-year-woman f‌ollowing secondary PPH on​ postpartum day 14","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Death of a 26-year-old woman following a road traffic accident at 36 weeks of gestation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Death of a 26-year-old woman following a road traffic accident at 36 weeks of gestation</strong></p><p>The death of a pregnant woman following a road traffic accident is not included when calculating the <strong>maternal mortality ratio</strong>. It includes all deaths anytime during <strong>pregnancy</strong> or <strong>within 42 days</strong> of termination from any cause <strong>related to</strong> or aggravated by the <strong>pregnancy</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8071","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of maternal deaths in​ India?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of maternal deaths in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemor‌rhage","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ecla‌mpsia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ane‌mia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Unsafe ‌abortion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hemorrhage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hemorrhage</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> cause of maternal deaths in India is <strong>hemorrhage</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8074","text":"In a town, there were 2500‌ live births in a period ​of 6 months. During the s‍ame period, 15 women died​ due to the following cau‌ses. What is the maternal​ mortality ratio? Peripar‌tum infection - 5 Electro‌cution - 5 Obstructed lab​or - 2 Postpartum hemorrh​age - 3","raw_text":"<p>In a town, there were 2500 live births in a period of 6 months. During the same period, 15 women died due to the following causes. What is the maternal mortality ratio?</p>\n<p>Peripartum infection - 5</p>\n<p>Electrocution - 5</p>\n<p>Obstructed labor - 2</p>\n<p>Postpartum hemorrhage - 3</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"400 per 100000 live births‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"6 per 1000 ‌live births","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"40 per 1000‌ live births","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"60 per 1000‌ live births","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 400 per 100000 live births","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 400 per 100000 live births</strong></p><p>The maternal mortality ratio (<strong>MMR</strong>) of this town is <strong>400 per 100,000 live births</strong>.</p>\n<p>Maternal mortality refers to deaths during pregnancy or 42 days within termination, from causes related to pregnancy. Hence, in this town, there were <strong>10 maternal deaths</strong> (peripartum infection, PPH, obstructed labor).</p>\n<p>MMR = Number of maternal deaths in a period x 100,000 / Number of live births in the same period</p>\n<p>Hence, MMR = 10 x 100000/2500 = 400 per 100,000 live births</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4776","text":"A baby was delivered at ho‌me in a village. Until wh​ich day of life should an‍ ASHA worker visit this b​aby?","raw_text":"A baby was delivered at home in a village. Until which day of life should an ASHA worker visit this baby?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2‌8","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2‌1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4‌2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 42","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 42</strong></p><p>Under the<strong> Home Based Newborn Care</strong> (HBNC) guidelines, <strong>ASHA </strong>workers should visit <strong>all newborns</strong> up to <strong>42 days</strong> of life, according to the following schedule. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 98px; width: 355px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mode of delivery</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.296875px; text-align: center;\"><strong>No. of visits</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.703125px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Schedule of visits</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Home delivery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.296875px;\">7</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.703125px;\">Days 1, 3, 7, 14, 21, 28, 42</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Hospital-based normal vaginal delivery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.296875px;\">6</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.703125px;\">\n<p>Days 3, 7, 14, 21, 28, 42</p>\n<p>(except day 1)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Hospital-based LSCS delivery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.296875px;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.703125px;\">\n<p>Days 7, 14, 21, 28, 42</p>\n<p>(except days 1, 3)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>During her visits, ASHA workers must record weight, ensure vaccination, and registration of births. On completion of all these visits, they receive an <strong>incentive</strong> of <strong>Rs. 250</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8080","text":"What is the best indicator‌ of obstetric and neonata​l care given before and a‍round the time of birth?","raw_text":"What is the best indicator of obstetric and neonatal care given before and around the time of birth?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infant mor‌tality rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perinatal mo‌rtality rate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Under 5 mor‌tality rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Early neonatal mortality r‌ate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Perinatal mortality rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Perinatal mortality rate</strong></p><p>The<strong> perinatal mortality rate </strong>is the best indicator to evaluate obstetric and neonatal care given <strong>before</strong> and <strong>around</strong> the time of <strong>birth</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5819","text":"What is the 2030 target of‌ the India Newborn Action​ Plan with regards to the‍ stillbirth rate?","raw_text":"What is the 2030 target of the India Newborn Action Plan with regards to the stillbirth rate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt; 10 per 1000 live birt‌hs","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt; 20 per 1000 live birt‌hs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt; 30 per 10000 live bir‌ths","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt; 40 per 10000 live bir‌ths","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. < 10 per 1000 live births","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. &lt; 10 per 1000 live births</strong></p><p>The India Newborn Action Plan targets to achieve a <strong>stillbirth rate</strong> of <strong>&lt; 10/ 1000 </strong>live births and <strong>neonatal mortality rate</strong> of <strong>&lt; 10/ 1000 </strong>live births (<strong>single-digit</strong> SBR and NMR) by <strong>2030</strong>.</p>\n<p>This program was launched to reduce preventable newborn deaths and stillbirths. It provides the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pre-conception and antenatal care</li>\n<li>Care during labor and childbirth</li>\n<li>Immediate newborn care</li>\n<li>Care of healthy newborn</li>\n<li>Care of small and sick newborn</li>\n<li>Care beyond newborn survival</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9681","text":"A group of health workers ‌are conducting a perinata​l mortality audit in a ho‍spital. Which of the foll​owing must they take into‌ account to calculate the​ perinatal mortality rate‌? No. of live births per ‌year No. of stillbirths o​ver 1000 g per year No. o​f stillbirths after 22 we​eks of gestation No. of e‍arly neonatal deaths per ‌year No. of late neonatal‍ deaths per year","raw_text":"<p>A group of health workers are conducting a perinatal mortality audit in a hospital. Which of the following must they take into account to calculate the perinatal mortality rate?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>No. of live births per year</li>\n<li>No. of stillbirths over 1000 g per year</li>\n<li>No. of stillbirths after 22 weeks of gestation</li>\n<li>No. of early neonatal deaths per year</li>\n<li>No. of late neonatal deaths per year</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,‌3,4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,‌2,4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1,‌4,5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2,‌4,5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1,2,4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1,2,4</strong></p><p>The<strong> </strong>perinatal mortality rate (<strong>PMR</strong>) is calculated using the following parameters<strong> </strong>during a particular time period:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Late fetal</strong> deaths or stillbirths<strong> &gt; 1000 g</strong><strong> </strong>- correlates to <strong>28 weeks</strong> of gestation</li>\n<li><strong>Early neonatal </strong>deaths<strong> </strong>- from birth to 7 days of life</li>\n<li><strong>Live births</strong> &gt; 1000 g</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The neonatal mortality rate (<strong>NMR</strong>) refers to the deaths within the <strong>first 28 days</strong> of life. It is calculated using the following parameters:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Early neonatal deaths</li>\n<li>Late neonatal deaths - after 7th day but before 28th completed day of life</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8086","text":"In a given population, 405‌0 births were recorded ov​er a period of 1 year. Am‍ong these, 50 were stillb​irths, 50 died in the 1st‌ week of life and 150 die​d within 8-28 days of lif‌e. What is the neonatal m‌ortality rate of this pop​ulation?","raw_text":"In a given population, 4050 births were recorded over a period of 1 year. Among these, 50 were stillbirths, 50 died in the 1st week of life and 150 died within 8-28 days of life. What is the neonatal mortality rate of this population?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5‌0","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"12‌. 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‌00","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"62‌.5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 50","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 50</strong></p><p>The neonatal mortality rate (<strong>NMR</strong>) of the given population is <strong>50 per 1000 live births</strong>.</p>\n<p>Neonatal mortality rate = No. of deaths of neonates &lt; 28 days within a year x 1000 / Total live births in the same year</p>\n<p>In this population, there were <strong>4000 live births</strong> (no. of total births - no. of stillbirths).</p>\n<p>NMR = (50+150) x 1000/4000 = 100/2 = 50</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8087","text":"Which of the following is ‌the leading cause of neon​atal mortality?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the leading cause of neonatal mortality?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prema‌turity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Birth A‌sphyxia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sep‌sis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pneu‌monia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Prematurity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Prematurity</strong></p><p><strong>Prematurity </strong>is the leading cause of neonatal mortality.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8090","text":"In children aged 1 month t‌o 1 year, all of the foll​owing are common causes o‍f mortality except:","raw_text":"In children aged 1 month to 1 year, all of the following are common causes of mortality except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tet‌anus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Malnut‌rition","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diarrheal‌ diseases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute respiratory infectio‌ns","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tetanus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tetanus</strong></p><p>Tetanus<strong> </strong>is not a common cause of mortality in children between the age of 1 month to 1 year (<strong>post-neonatal period</strong>). The main causes of post-neonatal deaths are <strong>diarrhea, respiratory infections, </strong>and<strong> malnutrition</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8091","text":"In a given population, the‌re were 5100 births durin​g the year 2020. Of these‍, 100 babies were stillbo​rn. Among the living chil‌dren, the following death​s were recorded. Calculat‌e the infant mortality ra‌te for this population. T​ime of death No. of death​s 1st week of life 150 8-​28 days of life 150 1st y‍ear of life 500","raw_text":"<p>In a given population, there were 5100 births during the year 2020. Of these, 100 babies were stillborn. Among the living children, the following deaths were recorded. Calculate the infant mortality rate for this population.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 97px;\" width=\"285\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.91848754882812px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Time of death   </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.91848754882812px; text-align: center;\"><strong>No. of deaths    </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.91848754882812px; text-align: center;\">1st week of life</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.91848754882812px; text-align: center;\">150</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.91848754882812px; text-align: center;\">8-28 days of life</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.91848754882812px; text-align: center;\">150</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.91848754882812px; text-align: center;\">1st year of life</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.91848754882812px; text-align: center;\">500</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>  </p>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"100 per 1000‌ live births","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"10 per 1000‌ live births","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"160 per 1000‌ live births","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"16 per 1000‌ live births","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 100 per 1000 live births","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 100 per 1000 live births</strong></p><p>The infant mortality rate (<strong>IMR</strong>) for this population is <strong>100</strong> <strong>/ 1000 live births</strong>.</p>\n<p>IMR = No. of deaths within 1st year of life over a period of time x 1000 / No. of live births in the same period</p>\n<p>In this population, there were <strong>5000 live births</strong> (5100 – 100) and <strong>500 deaths</strong> within the <strong>1st year</strong> of life.</p>\n<p>Hence, IMR = 500 x 1000/5000 = 100 per 1000 live births</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4777","text":"Which of the following is ‌true for a newborn stabil​ization unit?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true for a newborn stabilization unit?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is located in the deliv‌ery room.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is for sick babies and ‌LBW babies &amp;lt; 1800 g","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is present in all commu‌nity health centres","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"All cases except major sur‌geries are managed here","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is present in all community health centres","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is present in all community health centres</strong></p><p>A <strong>newborn stabilization unit</strong> is present in all <strong>community health centers</strong> (CHC).</p>\n<p>India accounts for 30% of neonatal deaths globally and 73.3% of these deaths occur in 1st week of life. To address this issue, the following facility-based newborn care services were introduced:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 237px; width: 428px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 327.140625px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\"><strong>Newborn care corner</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\"><strong>Newborn stabilization unit</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\"><strong>Special newborn care units</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 327.140625px;\">Present in</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">Delivery room of all centers and hospitals</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">Within or close to the maternity ward, in CHCs and first referral units</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">District hospitals and facilities with &gt;3000 deliveries/ year</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 327.140625px;\">Admits</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">Immediate care to all newborns</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">Sick and LBW &gt;1800 gms</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">Sick and LBW&lt; 1800 gms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 327.140625px;\">Service</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">Immediate resuscitation, early initiation of breastfeeding, identification, and referral of at-risk newborns</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">Hyperbilirubinemia, asphyxia, pneumonia, diarrhea, sepsis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">All care except assisted ventilation and major surgery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 327.140625px;\">Training of health personnel appointed</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">2 days, under Navjat Shishu Suraksha Karyakram</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">11 days in IMNCI</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">4 days of training and two weeks of observership</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 327.140625px;\">Staff</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">1 doctor, 1 nurse, 1 auxiliary midwife</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">1 doctor, 4 nurses</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">3-4 doctors and 10 nurses</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8077","text":"You are a doctor at a chil‌d health clinic. You will​ consider all of the foll‍owing children as at-risk​ infants except:","raw_text":"You are a doctor at a child health clinic. You will consider all of the following children as at-risk infants except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 2-month-old baby with 3 ‌older siblings","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A 15-day-old baby with bir‌th weight of 2.4 kgs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"An 1-year-old girl with pr‌otein-energy malnutrition​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"An 8-month-old baby being ‌raised by a working mothe​r","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A 2-month-old baby with 3 older siblings","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A 2-month-old baby with 3 older siblings</strong></p><p>The 2-month-old baby with 3 older siblings has a birth order of 4. Infants with <strong>birth order</strong> of <strong>5 or more</strong> are considered <strong>at-risk infants</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following are considered at-risk infants:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Birth weight <strong>&lt; 2.5 kgs</strong></li>\n<li>Twins </li>\n<li>Birth order of 5 or more</li>\n<li>Artificial feeding</li>\n<li>Weight below 70% of the expected weight</li>\n<li>Failure to gain weight during 3 successive months</li>\n<li>Children with protein-energy malnutrition<strong> </strong>(<strong>PEM</strong>), diarrhea</li>\n<li><strong>Working mother</strong> or single parent</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8094","text":"A researcher is calculatin‌g the child survival indi​ces for different populat‍ions. Which of the follow​ing should he use for cal‌culating the same?","raw_text":"A researcher is calculating the child survival indices for different populations. Which of the following should he use for calculating the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maternal mo‌rtality rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infant mor‌tality rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Perinatal mo‌rtality rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Under-5 mor‌tality rate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Under-5 mortality rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Under-5 mortality rate</strong></p><p>The<strong> under-5 mortality rate </strong>is used in the calculation of the <strong>child survival index</strong>. It is calculated as (1000 – Under-5 mortality rate)/10.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8097","text":"Identify the false stateme‌nt about the following to​ol:","raw_text":"Identify the false statement about the following tool:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malnutrition can be detect‌ed even before signs and ​symptoms appear","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It provides an objective b‌asis for policy making on​ child healthcare","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It can be used to teach th‌e importance of adequate ​feeding","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It can be used to indicate‌ proof of government aid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It can be used to indicate proof of government aid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4224c7ff52b9496bae1061976ff9235b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It can be used to indicate proof of government aid</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>growth chart.</strong> It is not used as proof of government aid.</p>\n<p>The uses of growth charts are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Growth monitoring and diagnosis - can detect malnutrition before clinical features appear</li>\n<li>Educational tool - to teach parents about the importance of adequate feeding.</li>\n<li>Objective basis for planning and <strong>policy-making </strong>on child healthcares</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8099","text":"A juvenile delinquent was ‌sentenced by the court to​ be placed in a center un‍der the care of a doctor ​and psychiatrist. Which o‌f the following fulfills ​this requirement?","raw_text":"A juvenile delinquent was sentenced by the court to be placed in a center under the care of a doctor and psychiatrist. Which of the following fulfills this requirement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bor‌stal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Foste‌r home","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reman‌d home","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Shelte‌r home","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Remand home","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Remand home</strong></p><p>A <strong>remand home</strong> is an establishment where children are placed under the care of a <strong>doctor</strong>, <strong>psychiatrist</strong> and other trained personnel.</p>\n<p>An <strong>orphanage</strong> or <strong>shelter home</strong> is an institution where children who have <strong>no home or parents</strong> or for some reason can’t be cared for are placed.</p>\n<p><strong>Foster homes</strong> are arrangements where a <strong>family</strong> or a <strong>couple</strong> takes care of a child on a <strong>temporary basis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Borstal</strong> is a place that falls between a certified institution and a prison. It is where boys <strong>&gt; 16 years</strong> who have <strong>misbehaved</strong> or are too difficult to handle are sent.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8100","text":"The police authorities res‌cued a girl from being tr​afficked for sexual explo‍itation. She is identifie​d to be a refugee. As a p‌art of the Ujjawala schem​e, which of the following‌ benefits will she not re‌ceive?","raw_text":"The police authorities rescued a girl from being trafficked for sexual exploitation. She is identified to be a refugee. As a part of the Ujjawala scheme, which of the following benefits will she not receive?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rehabil‌itation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Re-inte‌gration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Remune‌ration","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Repatr‌iation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Remuneration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Remuneration</strong></p><p>The girl will <strong>not</strong> receive remuneration (monetary compensation) under the Ujjawala scheme.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Ujjawala scheme</strong> was launched to <strong>combat women and child trafficking</strong> in India. The components of the scheme include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prevention</strong> of <strong>trafficking</strong> by awareness, vigilance, education</li>\n<li><strong>Rescue</strong> of victims from the place of exploitation</li>\n<li><strong>Rehabilitation</strong> of victims</li>\n<li><strong>Re-integration</strong> of victims into their family and society</li>\n<li><strong>Repatriation</strong> of cross-border victims to their place of origin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Some other legislations associated with children in India are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education Act, 2009</li>\n<li>Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, 2000</li>\n<li>Child Labour (Prohibition and Regulation) Act, 1986 (amended in 2006 and 2016)</li>\n<li>Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO), 2012 under the National Commission for Protection of Child Rights (NCPCR)</li>\n<li>Prohibition of Child Marriage Act, 2006</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9816","text":"A 4-year-old girl was brou‌ght by her parents who te​ll you that she fell down‍ while playing. On examin​ation, bruises and ecchym‌oses of varying ages are ​noted all over the body. ‌An x-ray showed a spiral ‌fracture of the right fem​ur. Which of the followin​g programs can provide su​pport services to this ch‍ild and prevent further s‌uch incidents?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old girl was brought by her parents who tell you that she fell down while playing. On examination, bruises and ecchymoses of varying ages are noted all over the body. An x-ray showed a spiral fracture of the right femur. Which of the following programs can provide support services to this child and prevent further such incidents?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ujjawal‌a scheme","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"INSPIRE ‌strategy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"SABLA ‌scheme","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"POCS‌O act","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. INSPIRE strategy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. INSPIRE strategy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario points towards<strong> child abuse</strong>. The <strong>INSPIRE </strong>strategy was introduced to <strong>eliminate</strong> such<strong> </strong><strong>violence against children</strong> and also provide <strong>support</strong> <strong>services</strong>.</p>\n<p>It refers to a set of 7 evidence-based strategies to eliminate violence against children:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Strategies</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Approach</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I  </td>\n<td>Implementation and enforcement of laws</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Banning violent punishment of children and criminalizing sexual abuse</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Preventing alcohol misuse and limiting youth access to weapons</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Norms and values</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Changing perceptions about harmful gender and social norms</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>S</td>\n<td>Safe environments</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Reducing the spread of violence </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>P</td>\n<td>Parent and caregiver support</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Delivered through home visits and in groups in community settings </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I</td>\n<td>Income and economic strengthening</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cash transfers </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Providing small loans </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R</td>\n<td>Response and support services</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Counseling and therapy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Treatment programs for juvenile offenders </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Foster care interventions</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E</td>\n<td>Education and life skills</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Increasing enrolment in schools </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Establishing safe school environments</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Improving sexual education for children and teaching self-defense strategies  </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0044","text":"As a PHC medical officer, ‌which of the following gr​oups would you target as ‍a part of Kishori Shakti ​Yojana?","raw_text":"As a PHC medical officer,  which of the following groups would you target as a part of Kishori Shakti Yojana?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Girls aged‌ 9-11 years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adolescent b‌oys and girls","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Girls aged‌ 3-6 years","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adolesce‌nt girls","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Adolescent girls","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Adolescent girls</strong></p><p>The Kishori Shakti Yojana (<strong>KSY</strong>) program is aimed at<strong> adolescent girls </strong>(<strong>11-18 years</strong>). </p>\n<p>This program is a <strong>component</strong> of the Integrated Child Development Services (<strong>ICDS</strong>) scheme and is a redesign of the already existing Adolescent Girls (AG) scheme.</p>\n<p>The objectives of KSY are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>To improve the <strong>nutrition</strong>, <strong>health</strong>, and development status </li>\n<li>To promote <strong>awareness</strong> on health, hygiene, nutrition, and family care</li>\n<li>To teach<strong> life skills</strong> and encourage school attendance</li>\n<li>To help understand their social environment</li>\n<li>Aspects aimed at <strong>empowerment</strong> and enhanced self-perception</li>\n<li>To help them take initiatives to become <strong>productive</strong> members of society</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It also fosters convergence with other sectoral programs, addressing the interrelated needs of adolescent girls and women.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2443","text":"The school health programs‌ are managed by which of ​the following?","raw_text":"The school health programs are managed by which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary he‌alth center","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"District‌ hospital","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sub-c‌entre","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sub-divisio‌nal hospital","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Primary health center","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Primary health center</strong></p><p>The <strong>school health programs</strong> are managed by the <strong>primary health center</strong> (PHC).</p>\n<p>The <strong>School Health Committee</strong> (1961) in India recommended that the school health services should be a part of the general health services administered through PHCs. Formation of school health committees at the village, block, district, state, and national levels was recommended. The National School Health Council was created as an advisory and coordinating body.</p>\n<p>PHCs provide the following school health services:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Screening</strong> and general assessments for -\n<ul>\n<li>Anemia/nutritional status</li>\n<li>Visual acuity and hearing problems</li>\n<li>Dental check-up</li>\n<li>Physical disabilities</li>\n<li>Learning disorders and behavior problems</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Basic medicines for <strong>common ailments</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Immunization</strong> </li>\n<li>Micronutrient management (vitamin A, iron, and folic acid)</li>\n<li>Deworming </li>\n<li>Mid-day meal</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2075","text":"A 68-year-old man visits y‌our outpatient clinic for​ a routine follow-up. Whi‍ch of the following proto​cols would you use to ass‌ess his health literacy?","raw_text":"A 68-year-old man visits your outpatient clinic for a routine follow-up. Which of the following protocols would you use to assess his health literacy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GAT‌HER","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"SP‌EAK","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"SPI‌KES","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"S-‌BAR","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. SPEAK","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. SPEAK</strong></p><p>The<strong> SPEAK </strong>protocol is used to assess <strong>health literacy </strong>in the <strong>geriatric population</strong> in a clinical setting.</p>\n<p>Health literacy<strong> </strong>is defined as the degree to which individuals have the capacity to obtain, process, and <strong>understand</strong> basic <strong>health information</strong> needed to make appropriate <strong>health decisions</strong>. The SPEAK protocol provides a simple framework that healthcare providers can use to assess literacy. SPEAK stands for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>S - Speech</li>\n<li>P - Perception</li>\n<li>E - Education</li>\n<li>A - Access</li>\n<li>K - Knowledge</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: The <strong>GATHER </strong>approach is mainly used for <strong>counseling </strong>(usually, family planning and reproductive counseling)<strong> </strong>to help people make decisions about health. It stands for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>G – Greet</li>\n<li>A – Ask</li>\n<li>T – Tell </li>\n<li>H – Help </li>\n<li>E – Explain </li>\n<li>R – Return </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option C: The <strong>SPIKES</strong> protocol is used to convey distressing information and <strong>break bad news</strong> in an organized manner to patients and families. It stands for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>S - Setting</li>\n<li>P - Perception</li>\n<li>I - Invitation or information</li>\n<li>K - Knowledge</li>\n<li>E - Empathy</li>\n<li>S - Summarizing or strategizing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option D: The <strong>S-BAR</strong> technique is used for prompt and appropriate <strong>communication</strong> among health care workers in hospital settings. It stands for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>S - Situation</li>\n<li>B - Background</li>\n<li>A - Assessment</li>\n<li>R - Recommendation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME6973","text":"Which of the following com‌es under the Rashtriya Va​rishth Jan Swasthya Yojan‍a?","raw_text":"Which of the following comes under the Rashtriya Varishth Jan Swasthya Yojana?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Domiciliary visits to home‌s of elderly in the commu​nity by ANMs or male HCWs‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rehabilitation units with ‌physiotherapy at CHCs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Super-specialty geriatric ‌departments for training,​ guidelines and referral","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Weekly geriatric clinics a‌rranged at PHCs by medica​l officer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Super-specialty geriatric departments for training, guidelines and referral","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Super-specialty geriatric departments for training, guidelines and referral</strong></p><p>The establishment of <strong>super-specialty</strong> <strong>geriatric</strong> <strong>departments</strong> for training, guidelines, and referral comes under the Rashtriya Varishth Jan Swasthya Yojna (<strong>RVJSY</strong>). It refers to the tertiary component of the National Programme for Healthcare of the Elderly (NPHCE).</p>\n<p>The <strong>NPHCE</strong> was established for geriatric care and includes provisions at primary, secondary, and tertiary levels.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 169px; width: 510px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 144.453px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\">Centers</td>\n<td style=\"width: 340.547px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"> Services according to NPHCE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 144.453px; height: 18px;\"><strong>PHCs </strong>(primary)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 340.547px; height: 18px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Weekly <strong>geriatric</strong> <strong>clinics</strong> at PHC</li>\n<li>Treatment of minor ailments</li>\n<li><strong>Referral</strong> services to higher centers</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 144.453px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Sub-centres </strong>(primary)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 340.547px; height: 18px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>ANMs/ male health workers make <strong>domiciliary visits</strong> to the elderly</li>\n<li>Arrange supportive devices for the disabled</li>\n<li>Treatment of minor ailments</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 144.453px; height: 18px;\"><strong>CHCs </strong>(secondary)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 340.547px; height: 18px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Dedicated <strong>geriatric</strong> <strong>clinics</strong> twice weekly</li>\n<li>Rehabilitation unit with trained<strong> physiotherapists</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>tertiary</strong> <strong>component</strong> of the program has been <strong>renamed</strong> as <strong>RVJSY </strong>and has the following provisions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Geriatrics departments at 20 super specialty institutions-\n<ul>\n<li><strong>30-bedded</strong> inpatient facility and <strong>referral</strong> treatment</li>\n<li>Research and manpower development,  </li>\n<li>Development of training, IEC material, and guidelines</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Geriatric units at district hospitals (tertiary) -\n<ul>\n<li><strong>10</strong> <strong>bedded</strong> geriatric ward</li>\n<li>Dedicated geriatric <strong>OPD</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"U4kep3jE","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"29_adenoids","title":"29.Adenoids","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MA0192","text":"What are adenoids also kno‌wn as?","raw_text":"What are adenoids also known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nasopharyn‌geal tonsil","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Palatin‌e tonsil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Faucial‌ tonsil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lingual‌ tonsil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nasopharyngeal tonsil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nasopharyngeal tonsil</strong></p><p><strong>Adenoids</strong> are commonly known as <strong>nasopharyngeal tonsil</strong>. It is situated at the junction of the roof and posterior wall of the nasopharynx.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0201","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about th‍e structure marked as A?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about the structure marked as A?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is part of the Waldeyer‌'s ring","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypertrophies up to the ag‌e of 6 years and starts t‍o regress from puberty","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypertrophy causes rhinola‌lia clausa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is surrounded by a caps‌ule","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is surrounded by a capsule","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cdd878c8da8746e3a8e9b1462ddc5c62.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is surrounded by a capsule</strong></p><p>The structure marked A is the <strong>adenoids.</strong> It is not surrounded by a capsule.</p>\n<p>Adenoids are comprised of vertical ridges of lymphoid tissues separated by deep clefts. They form the superior aspect of <strong>Waldeyer's ring</strong>. They have <strong>no crypts and no capsule</strong>, unlike palatine tonsils which have both.</p>\n<p>It is present at birth, enlarges up to the age of 6 years, and then starts <strong>regressing from puberty</strong>. It completely disappears by the age of 20 years.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0202","text":"Which of the following is ‌not included in the signs‍ and symptoms of the cond‍ition represented below?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included in the signs and symptoms of the condition represented below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mouth b‌reathing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Failure ‌to thrive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sleep ‌apnoea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prominent na‌solabial fold","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prominent nasolabial fold","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0d09e4be07fc4248aa8d3dc4109c0a98.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5ff524698c534cee99df0c96654e9743x503x451.PNG","https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/76d9a8e4d298497dacc46010d9e7769cx498x429.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prominent nasolabial fold</strong></p><p>The given x-ray shows <strong>enlarged adenoids</strong> and suggests the diagnosis of <strong>adenoid hypertrophy</strong>. Patients show an <strong>absent nasolabial fold</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nasal obstruction</strong> is the most common symptom. This leads to <strong>mouth breathing</strong>. Nasal obstruction also interferes with feeding or suckling in a child. As respiration and feeding cannot take place simultaneously, a child with adenoid enlargement <strong>fails to thrive</strong>.</li>\n<li>Nasal discharge, sinusitis, and epistaxis.</li>\n<li>Bilateral conductive hearing loss and <strong>serous otitis media</strong> due to eustachian tube obstruction.</li>\n<li><strong>Rhinolalia clausa</strong> - hyponasal voice.</li>\n<li><strong>Adenoid facies</strong> - elongated face with a dull expression, pinched up nose, <strong>absent nasolabial crease</strong>, open mouth, prominent and crowded upper teeth.</li>\n<li><strong>Sleep apnoea</strong> - chronic cases lead to hypoxemia and pulmonary vasoconstriction. This consequently leads to pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale.</li>\n<li><strong>Aprosexia</strong> - lack of concentration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0204","text":"A 5-year-old girl is broug‌ht to you by her mother w‍ith complaints of mouth b‍reathing. She has a flat,‌ expressionless long face‍ with an open mouth. On e​xamination, a high arched‌ palate and crowded upper‌ teeth are noted. What is​ the voice abnormality th‌at can be noted in this c​hild?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old girl is brought to you by her mother with complaints of mouth breathing. She has a flat, expressionless long face with an open mouth. On examination, a high arched palate and crowded upper teeth are noted. What is the voice abnormality that can be noted in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hot pota‌to voice","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rhinolal‌ia clausa","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rhinolal‌ia aperta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staccat‌o voice","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rhinolalia clausa","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/219d626358dd4515ad00748941f82af9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rhinolalia clausa</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture of a child with<strong> adenoid facies</strong> is suggestive of a diagnosis of <strong>adenoid hypertrophy</strong>. <strong>Rhinolalia clausa/ hyponasal voice</strong> can be seen in these patients. </p>\n<p>It is also seen in conditions like common cold, nasal allergy, etc.</p>\n<p>Option A - Hot potato voice is a muffled and thick speech often seen in a peritonsillar abscess (quinsy).</p>\n<p>Option C - Rhinolalia aperta causes a hypernasal voice.</p>\n<p>Option D - Staccato voice or scanning speech is a broken speech seen in cerebellar lesions as well as multiple sclerosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0205","text":"A 5-year-old child is brou‌ght by his mother to the ‍ENT OPD with frequent upp‍er respiratory tract infe‌ctions. His mother gives ‍a history of loud snoring​ at night and posterior r‌hinoscopy examination sho‌ws enlarged adenoids. Whi​ch of the following will ‌you not advise in the man​agement of this patient?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old child is brought by his mother to the ENT OPD with frequent upper respiratory tract infections. His mother gives a history of loud snoring at night and posterior rhinoscopy examination shows enlarged adenoids. Which of the following will you not advise in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decong‌estants","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antihis‌taminics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenoi‌dectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Beta 2 ‌agonists","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Beta 2 agonists","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Beta 2 agonists</strong></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><strong>Beta 2 agonists</strong> have <strong>no role</strong> in conditions of upper airway obstruction like adenoid hypertrophy. They are bronchodilators used in lower airway obstructive diseases.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\">When symptoms of adenoid hypertrophy are not marked,<strong> breathing exercises</strong>, <strong>decongestant</strong> nasal drops, and <strong>antihistamines</strong> for any coexisting nasal allergy can cure the condition without resorting to surgery.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\">Marked adenoid hypertrophy with associated complications can be treated with <strong>adenoidectomy</strong> and grommet insertion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0208","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an indication for an ‍adenoidectomy procedure?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an indication for an adenoidectomy procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple‌ adenoids","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Recurrent upper respirator‌y tract infections","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Recurrent middle ear infec‌tions with deafness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Speech ab‌normality","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Multiple adenoids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Multiple adenoids</strong></p><p><strong>Multiple adenoids</strong> is not an indication for adenoidectomy.</p>\n<p>Indications of adenoidectomy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adenoid hypertrophy causing snoring, mouth breathing, sleep apnoea syndrome or speech abnormalities, i.e. (rhinolalia clausa)</li>\n<li>Recurrent rhinosinusitis</li>\n<li>Chronic otitis media with effusion associated with adenoid hyperplasia</li>\n<li>Recurrent ear discharge in benign CSOM associated with adenoiditis/adenoid hyperplasia</li>\n<li>Dental malocclusion. Adenoidectomy does not correct dental abnormalities but will prevent its recurrence after orthodontic treatment</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0209","text":"A 6-year-old child has bee‌n posted for an adenoidec‍tomy procedure. Which of ‍the following conditions ‌if present in this child ‍will be a contraindicatio​n to performing the surge‌ry?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old child has been posted for an adenoidectomy procedure. Which of the following conditions if present in this child will be a contraindication to performing the surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Large a‌denoids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Large ‌tonsils","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clef‌t lip","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cleft ‌palate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cleft palate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cleft palate</strong></p><p><strong>Cleft palate</strong> is a <strong>contraindication</strong> to perform adenoidectomy as removal of adenoids causes velopharyngeal insufficiency. </p>\n<p><strong>Velopharyngeal insufficiency</strong> is a condition where the soft palate does not close tightly against the back of the throat. This leads to air coming out the nose (characterized by hypernasality) during speech. </p>\n<p>Contraindications for adenoidectomy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cleft palate or submucous palate. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hemorrhagic diathesis.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Acute infection of the upper respiratory tract.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0210","text":"A school-going child prese‌nts with a history of rec‍urrent upper respiratory ‍tract infections, mouth b‌reathing, and impaired he‍aring. On examination, a ​high arched palate is not‌ed. What is the managemen‌t of this condition?","raw_text":"A school-going child presents with a history of recurrent upper respiratory tract infections, mouth breathing, and impaired hearing. On examination, a high arched palate is noted. What is the management of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tonsil‌lectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adenoi‌dectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenoidectomy with grommet‌ insertion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Myringotomy with grommet i‌nsertion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Adenoidectomy with grommet insertion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/942873374f2d4ff3b6f7494e2aa7109fx617x522.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Adenoidectomy with grommet insertion</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture is suggestive of <strong>adenoid hypertrophy</strong>. The best line of management is <strong>adenoidectomy with grommet insertion.</strong></p>\n<p>A grommet is inserted to provide continued aeration of middle ear and used in cases where fluid collection recurs.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0211","text":"An 8-year-old boy diagnose‌d with adenoid hypertroph‍y has undergone an adenoi‍dectomy procedure. Two we‌eks following the procedu‍re, he developed Grisel s​yndrome. What is the reas‌on for this condition?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old boy diagnosed with adenoid hypertrophy has undergone an adenoidectomy procedure. Two weeks following the procedure, he developed Grisel syndrome. What is the reason for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Damage to up‌per incisors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Temporomandibular joint di‌slocation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paraspinal ‌muscle spasm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Damage to lo‌wer incisors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Paraspinal muscle spasm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Paraspinal muscle spasm</strong></p><p><strong>Grisel syndrome</strong> involves <strong>paraspinal muscle spasm</strong> that occurs <strong>post adenoidectomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Grisel syndrome refers to <strong>non-traumatic atlantoaxial joint subluxation</strong>. The patient complains of neck pain and develops torticollis. It is mostly due to spasm of paraspinal muscles but can also be due to atlantoaxial dislocation requiring cervical collar and even traction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0212","text":"Where is Thornwaldt cyst l‌ocated?","raw_text":"Where is Thornwaldt cyst located?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E‌ar","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nasop‌harynx","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Oroph‌arynx","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pirifor‌m fossa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nasopharynx","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/692e06e74b0d48e191c8eea918ab3808x452x530.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nasopharynx</strong></p><p><strong>Thornwaldt cyst</strong> is located in the <strong>midline of the posterior wall</strong> of the <strong>nasopharynx</strong> in the adenoid mass.</p>\n<p>It is an epithelial-lined median recess found <strong>within the adenoid mass</strong> and extends from pharyngeal mucosa to the periosteum of the basiocciput. It represents the <strong>attachment of notochord to the pharyngeal endoderm</strong> during embryonic life.</p>\n<p>When infected, it may be the cause of persistent postnasal discharge or crusting. Sometimes an abscess can form in the bursa (Thornwaldt’s disease). It is managed surgically by marsupialization of the swelling and complete removal of the lining of the cyst.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0213","text":"A 30-year-old patient has ‌a Thornwaldt abscess that‍ got infected from a Thor‍nwaldt’s cyst. Which of t‌he following will you not‍ recommend for the treatm​ent of his condition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old patient has a Thornwaldt abscess that got infected from a Thornwaldt’s cyst. Which of the following will you not recommend for the treatment of his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antib‌iotics","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Marsupia‌lisation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Removal‌ of cyst","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti tubercu‌lar treatment","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anti tubercular treatment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anti tubercular treatment</strong></p><p><strong>Antitubercular treatment</strong> has <strong>no role</strong> in the treatment of <strong>Thornwaldt’s abscess.</strong></p>\n<p>Thornwaldt’s abscess is formed due to the infection of Thornwaldt cyst. Treatment includes administration of <strong>antibiotics</strong> to treat the infection and <strong>marsupialization</strong> of the cystic swelling. There should be adequate removal of its lining membrane.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"qOnHCVdP","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"29_inflammatory_bowel_disease___clinical_features_and_diagnosis","title":"29.Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Clinical Features And Diagnosis","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MB1319","text":"Which of the following gen​es is associated with Cro​hn's disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following genes is associated with Crohn's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NO​D2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"JA​K2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"BRAF ​V600E","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ST​AT3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. NOD2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. NOD2</strong></p><p>The gene associated with Crohn's disease is the <strong>NOD2 gene. </strong>It is present on <strong>chromosome 16.</strong> </p>\n<p>Nucleotide-binding oligomerization domain 2 (NOD2) acts as a cytoplasmic sensor of microbial products. Defective NOD2 gene results in <strong>defective innate immunity</strong>. When the innate immunity is unable to clear the bacteria, there is an exaggerated response of the immune system (T-cells) resulting in damage to the host tissue. </p>\n<p>Option B: JAK2 mutation is associated with polycythemia vera.</p>\n<p>Option C: BRAF V600E mutation is associated with melanoma, papillary thyroid cancer, and serous ovarian cancers. </p>\n<p>Option D: STAT3 mutations are associated with the autosomal dominant hyper-IgE syndrome. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1320","text":"Which of the following con​ditions are not associate​d with inflammatory bowel‌ disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions are not associated with inflammatory bowel disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Turner's​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wiskott-Aldr​ich syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hermansky-Pu​dlak syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Klinefelte​r syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Klinefelter syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Klinefelter syndrome</strong></p><p>Klinefelter syndrome is not commonly associated with inflammatory bowel disease (IBD).</p>\n<p>The genetic disorders associated with IBD are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome</strong></li>\n<li>Immunodysregulation polyendocrinopathy enteropathy X-linked (IPEX) syndrome </li>\n<li><strong>Turner's syndrome</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome</strong></li>\n<li>Glycogen storage disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1295","text":"In patients who have under​gone an appendectomy, the​re is a lower risk for de‌veloping which of the fol‌lowing diseases?","raw_text":"In patients who have undergone an appendectomy, there is a lower risk for developing which of the following diseases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ulcerativ​e colitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Crohn’s​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carcino​ma colon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diverti​culosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ulcerative colitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ulcerative colitis</strong></p><p>Appendectomy is<strong> protective</strong> against <strong>ulcerative colitis.</strong> </p>\n<p>Smoking is also seen to have a protective effect on ulcerative colitis but it is a risk factor for developing Crohn's disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0322","text":"Early-onset inflammatory b​owel disease is associate​d with:","raw_text":"Early-onset inflammatory bowel disease is associated with:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"JAK2 m​utation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"FOXP3 m​utation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IL-10 de​ficiency","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"NOD 2 m​utation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. IL-10 deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. IL-10 deficiency</strong></p><p>Early-onset IBD is due to <strong>IL-10 receptor dysfunction</strong> and <strong>IL-10 deficiency</strong>. They are associated with severe refractory IBD in early life.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0331","text":"Which of the following is ​true about immune dysregu​lation polyendocrinopathy‌ enteropathy X-linked (IP‌EX) syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about immune dysregulation polyendocrinopathy enteropathy X-linked (IPEX) syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B cell​ defect","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Due to FOXP3 gene mutation​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with Crohn's di​sease-like enteropathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Recurrent staphylococcal s​kin infections","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Due to FOXP3 gene mutation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Due to FOXP3 gene mutation</strong></p><p><strong>IPEX </strong>(immune dysregulation, polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, and X-linked) syndrome is caused by mutations in the<strong> FOXP3 </strong>gene<strong>, </strong>which leads to a<strong> deficiency </strong>of<strong> T-regulatory cells,</strong> resulting in<strong> immune dysfunction.</strong></p>\n<p>IPEX is associated with: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ulcerative colitis-like autoimmune enteropathy </strong></li>\n<li>Neonatal type 1 DM</li>\n<li>Thyroiditis</li>\n<li>Dermatitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1316","text":"Which of the following inf​ections should you rule o​ut before diagnosing a pa‌tient with IBD on the bas‌is of clinical symptoms a‌nd biopsy findings?","raw_text":"Which of the following infections should you rule out before diagnosing a patient with IBD on the basis of clinical symptoms and biopsy findings?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mycobacte​rium Avium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes ​simplex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Campylobac​ter jejuni","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cytomeg​alovirus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Campylobacter jejuni","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Campylobacter jejuni</strong></p><p><strong>Campylobacter</strong> enteritis should be ruled out before diagnosing a patient with IBD.</p>\n<p>Campylobacter enteritis can <strong>mimic</strong> the clinical <strong>presentation</strong> and <strong>biopsy</strong> appearance of ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s disease (<strong>IBD</strong>).<strong> </strong>Hence, it should be ruled out, especially in persons with a history of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Foreign travel</li>\n<li>Significant animal contact</li>\n<li>Immunodeficiency</li>\n<li>Exposure incurring a high risk of transmission.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It can also cause a<strong> relapse</strong> of established ulcerative colitis.</p>\n<p>A definitive diagnosis of campylobacter infection can be made by culture or real-time PCR.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1299","text":"Which of the following ser​ological markers is posit​ive in ulcerative colitis‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following serological markers is positive in ulcerative colitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti-pancrea​tic antibody","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-flagel​lin antibody","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AS​CA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AN​CA","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. ANCA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. ANCA</strong></p><p><strong>Antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody</strong> (ANCA) positivity is a feature of <strong>ulcerative colitis</strong>. </p>\n<p>ASCA (anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibody), anti-pancreatic antibody, and anti-flagellin antibody positivity are seen in Crohn's disease.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 274px;\" width=\"285\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 275px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\">Serological Markers in IBD</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 133.672px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Crohn's disease</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.328px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Ulcerative colitis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 133.672px; height: 18px;\">\n<p><strong>ASCA</strong></p>\n<p>OMP-C </p>\n<p>Anti I2 </p>\n<p>Anti-pancreatic antibody</p>\n<p>Anti-flagellin antibody</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.328px; height: 18px;\">\n<p><strong>p-ANCA</strong></p>\n<p>Anti-goblet cell Antibody</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1300","text":"Which of the following sit​es is most commonly invol​ved in Crohn’s disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following sites is most commonly involved in Crohn’s disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Termina​l ileum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rectosigm​oid colon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transver​se colon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proximal​ duodenum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Terminal ileum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Terminal ileum</strong></p><p>The most commonly affected site in <strong>Crohn's disease </strong>is the<strong> terminal ileum. </strong></p>\n<p>Other commonly affected sites in Crohn's disease include the small intestine and the colon. Although it can affect any part of the GI tract from mouth to anus, the <strong>rectum</strong> is usually <strong>spared.</strong></p>\n<p>On the other hand, the <strong>rectum</strong> is almost always<strong> involved</strong> in <strong>ulcerative colitis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0306","text":"A patient presented with a​cute severe small bowel o​bstruction to the ER. An ‌emergency small bowel res‌ection was done. The rese‌cted specimen is shown be‍low. What could be the li‌kely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presented with acute severe small bowel obstruction to the ER. An emergency small bowel resection was done. The resected specimen is shown below. What could be the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ulcerativ​e colitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Crohn's​ disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intest​inal TB","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intestina​l lymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Crohn's disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/90f2f8714ea142eeb82b32536c7763f6.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cc3fbf00b245495ead14949edb9dd9a5x640x640.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Crohn&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>The above specimen of the small bowel shows<strong> creeping fat</strong> which is a feature of <strong>Crohn's disease </strong>(CD).</p>\n<p>Mesenteric fat is normally seen along the longitudinal axis of the intestine. However, in Crohn's disease, excessive fibrofatty proliferation results in <strong>mesenteric fat wrapping</strong> around the inflamed bowel. This is called creeping fat. </p>\n<p><strong>Strictures</strong> and <strong>fistulae</strong> are the commonly associated complications of <strong>long-standing</strong> Crohn's disease. Strictures can cause <strong>bowel obstruction.</strong></p>\n<p>Other <strong>gross features</strong> seen in Crohn's disease are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Transmural inflammation-</strong> full-thickness inflammation involving all the layers of the bowel</li>\n<li><strong>Aphthous</strong> <strong>ulcers</strong>- small, shallow ulcers surrounded by erythema</li>\n<li>Longitudinal ulcers- linear or serpiginous deep ulcers </li>\n<li><strong>Skip</strong> <strong>lesions</strong>- involvement of discontinuous segments of the bowel with intervening normal mucosa</li>\n<li><strong>Cobblestone</strong> appearance- deep, longitudinal ulcerations interlaced with intervening normal mucosa</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>cobblestone appearance</strong> in Crohn's disease.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0302","text":"A 28-year-old man presente​d with diarrhea, bloody s​tools, weight loss, and i‌ntermittent fever for the‌ past 2 months. Endoscopy‌ revealed the following. ‍What is the most probable‌ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old man presented with diarrhea, bloody stools, weight loss, and intermittent fever for the past 2 months. Endoscopy revealed the following. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crohn’s​ disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Intest​inal TB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ulcerativ​e colitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Salmonella​ infection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Crohn’s disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f7c558150e1b49c28456993999cf740f.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Crohn’s disease</strong></p><p>The clinical presentation along with the endoscopy image showing a <strong>cobblestone</strong> <strong>appearance </strong>are suggestive of <strong>Crohn's disease </strong>(CD).</p>\n<p><strong>Endoscopic </strong>findings in Crohn's disease include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aphthous</strong> <strong>ulcers </strong>- small, shallow ulcers surrounded by erythema</li>\n<li><strong>Longitudinal ulcers -</strong> linear or serpiginous deep ulcers </li>\n<li>Cobblestone appearance - deep, longitudinal ulcerations interlaced with intervening normal mucosa</li>\n<li><strong>Strictures</strong> </li>\n<li>Fistulas</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Strictures</strong> and <strong>fistulae</strong> are the commonly associated complications of <strong>long-standing</strong> Crohn's disease. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1291","text":"A 70-year-old chronic smok​er is being evaluated for​ chronic diarrhea, steato‌rrhea, and weight loss. O‌n examination, pallor was‌ noted. Peripheral smear ‍revealed macrocytes and h‌ypersegmented neutrophils‌. Stool examination showe‌d elevated calprotectin a‌nd a positive guaiac test‌. What is the most likely‌ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old chronic smoker is being evaluated for chronic diarrhea, steatorrhea, and weight loss. On examination, pallor was noted. Peripheral smear revealed macrocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils. Stool examination showed elevated calprotectin and a positive guaiac test. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ulcerativ​e colitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diverti​culitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Crohn’s​ disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Irritable bo​wel syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Crohn’s disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Crohn’s disease</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>Crohn's </strong>disease<strong> </strong>presenting as <strong>jejunoileitis</strong>. This is evidenced by the presence of features of <strong>malabsorption</strong> such as steatorrhea, chronic diarrhea, and vitamin B12 deficiency anemia.</p>\n<p>The <strong>fecal calprotectin</strong> and <strong>lactoferrin</strong> are elevated in <strong>inflammatory conditions</strong> like inflammatory bowel disease, but not in irritable bowel syndrome. </p>\n<p>The <strong>stool</strong> <strong>guaiac test</strong> or guaiac fecal occult blood test (gFOBT) reveals the presence of microscopic levels of blood suggesting <strong>Crohn's</strong> disease. <strong>Gross bleeding</strong> per rectum is more characteristic of <strong>ulcerative colitis.</strong>  </p>\n<p>Additionally, <strong>smoking</strong> is a risk factor for Crohn's disease. However, it is a<strong> protective </strong>factor<strong> </strong>in<strong> ulcerative colitis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1304","text":"A 30-year-old woman presen​ted with 7-8 episodes of ​bloody diarrhea. On exami‌nation, she was pale and ‌febrile, with a pulse rat‌e of 104 per minute. As s‍he was not responding to ‌broad-spectrum antibiotic‌s, a colonoscopy was done‌ which revealed ulceratio‌ns and pseudopolyps. What‌ is the most probable dia‌gnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman presented with 7-8 episodes of bloody diarrhea. On examination, she was pale and febrile, with a pulse rate of 104 per minute. As she was not responding to broad-spectrum antibiotics, a colonoscopy was done which revealed ulcerations and pseudopolyps. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Moderate Cro​hn’s disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Severe Croh​n’s disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Moderate ulcerative coliti​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Severe ulcer​ative colitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Severe ulcerative colitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Severe ulcerative colitis</strong></p><p>Tachycardia, bloody diarrhea, pseudopolyps, and ulcerations on colonoscopy strongly suggest <strong>severe ulcerative colitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Given below is the <strong>Truelove and Witts severity index</strong> for ulcerative colitis. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 512.182px;\">\n<thead>\n<tr style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\"><strong>Mild</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\"><strong>Moderate</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\"><strong>Severe</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">Bowel movements</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">&lt; 4 per day</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">4 – 6 per day</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\">&gt; 6 per day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">Blood in stool</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Small</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">Moderate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\">Severe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">Fever</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">None</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">&lt; 37.5ºC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\">&gt; 37.5ºC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">Tachycardia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">None</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">&lt; 90 mean pulse</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\">&gt;90 mean pulse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">Anemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Mild</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">Hb &gt; 75% of normal Hb</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\">Hb &lt; 75% of normal Hb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">ESR</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">&lt; 30mm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\"> &lt; 30mm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\"> &gt; 30mm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">Endoscopic appearance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">\n<p>Erythema</p>\n<p>Decreased vascular markings</p>\n<p>Fine granularity</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">\n<p>Marked erythema</p>\n<p>Absent vascular markings</p>\n<p>Coarse granularity</p>\n<p>Contact bleeding</p>\n<p>No ulcerations</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\">\n<p>Spontaneous bleeding</p>\n<p>Ulcerations</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1297","text":"All of the following are f​eatures of Crohn’s diseas​e except:","raw_text":"All of the following are features of Crohn’s disease except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transmural ​involvement","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lead pipe ​appearance","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rectal ​sparing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perianal​ fistula","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lead pipe appearance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8499810b84f6457c9cbb9ff6bc8f2518x1280x1562.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lead pipe appearance</strong></p><p><strong>Lead pipe appearance</strong> is seen in <strong>ulcerative colitis</strong> (not Crohn's disease). This appearance is due to the<strong> loss of haustra</strong> in the diseased colonic segment.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the lead pipe appearance of the colon on barium enema: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0315","text":"A young man presents to th​e OPD with complaints of ​chronic diarrhea, right l‌ower abdominal pain, and ‌weight loss. On examinati‌on, you note a low-grade ‍fever and a palpable mass‌ in the right lower quadr‌ant. Colonoscopy revealed‌ sparing of the rectum. T‌he following endoscopic f‌indings may also be seen ‌in his condition except:","raw_text":"A young man presents to the OPD with complaints of chronic diarrhea, right lower abdominal pain, and weight loss. On examination, you note a low-grade fever and a palpable mass in the right lower quadrant. Colonoscopy revealed sparing of the rectum. The following endoscopic findings may also be seen in his condition except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aphthou​s ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Longitudi​nal ulcers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fine granu​lar mucosa","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stri​cture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fine granular mucosa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fine granular mucosa</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Crohn's disease</strong> presenting as <strong>ileocolitis</strong>. <strong>Fine granular mucosa</strong> is seen in <strong>ulcerative colitis</strong>, not Crohn's disease.</p>\n<p>Ileocolitis presents as right lower abdominal pain, fever, and diarrhea. Pain is usually colicky and is relieved by defecation. It may mimic acute appendicitis in the early stages.</p>\n<p><strong>Endoscopic</strong> <strong>findings</strong> in <strong>Crohn's</strong> disease include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aphthous</strong> <strong>ulcers </strong>- Small, shallow ulcers surrounded by erythema</li>\n<li><strong>Longitudinal ulcers -</strong> Linear or serpiginous deep ulcers </li>\n<li>Cobblestone appearance - Deep, longitudinal ulcerations interlaced with intervening normal mucosa</li>\n<li><strong>Strictures</strong> </li>\n<li>Fistulas.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1312","text":"What is the earliest radio​graphic feature of Crohn'​s disease?","raw_text":"What is the earliest radiographic feature of Crohn's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ram's h​orn sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rose-thorn​ appearance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aphthou​s ulcer","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"String sig​n of Kantor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aphthous ulcer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e8369274678d462d88ef6015dcc260e2x400x348.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3832630101ca499399e0ecefc993d17bx1024x1024.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9d762ce1a5e847478b4632ad07ee5820x1280x1422.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7c044727b64b4d398e59645f9784edbfx1280x1432.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aphthous ulcer</strong></p><p><strong>Aphthous ulcers</strong> seen in <strong>Crohn's</strong> disease are the <strong>earliest radiological signs</strong> on small bowel follow-through study.</p>\n<p>They are <strong>shallow ulcers</strong> that<strong> </strong>are often multiple and separated by normal intervening mucosa. They are also the <strong>earliest macroscopic findings</strong> of Crohn's disease. </p>\n<p>Features on barium small bowel follow-through in Crohn's disease include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mucosal ulcers:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aphthous ulcers</strong> initially</li>\n<li>Deep ulcers (more than 3 mm depth)</li>\n<li>Longitudinal fissures</li>\n<li>Transverse stripes</li>\n<li>Severe mucosal ulcers lead to <strong>cobblestone appearance</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Sinus tracts and fistulae</li>\n<li>Creeping fat: widely separated loops of the bowel due to fibrofatty proliferation</li>\n<li>Thickened folds due to edema </li>\n<li>Pseudo-diverticula formation: due to contraction at the site of ulcer with ballooning of the opposite site</li>\n<li><strong>String sign of Kantor</strong>: tubular narrowing due to spasm or stricture depending on the chronicity</li>\n<li><strong>Rose thorn appearance</strong></li>\n<li>Partial obstruction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>CT</strong> and <strong>MR enterography</strong> have a <strong>higher sensitivity</strong> for the diagnosis of Crohn’s disease. They may reveal additional features such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fat halo sign - submucosal fat deposition</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Comb sign - engorgement of the vasa recta.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In<strong> gastric Crohn's </strong>disease<strong>, conical narrowing</strong> of the<strong> antrum </strong>may be seen. This is called <strong>Ram’s horn sign.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>aphthous ulcers</strong> (focal contrast with surrounding haloes of mucosal edema)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image below shows the <strong>string sign of Kantor:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>rose thorn appearance</strong>:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>cobblestone appearance</strong>:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1303","text":"Which among the following ​is the earliest radiologi​c change seen on barium e‌nema in ulcerative coliti‌s?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the earliest radiologic change seen on barium enema in ulcerative colitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Collar bu​tton ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fine mucosal​ granularity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Strin​g sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ram's h​orn sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fine mucosal granularity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9123e77137da479f82f1d235586fc643x512x924.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/edd61e6f4fc944eca4606d81e19a32b2x1024x1024.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fine mucosal granularity</strong></p><p>The <strong>earliest radiological</strong> change of <strong>ulcerative colitis</strong> seen on barium enema is <strong>fine mucosal granularity.</strong></p>\n<p>Double-contrast barium enema allows for exquisite detail of the colonic mucosa and also allows the bowel proximal to strictures to be assessed. It is however contraindicated if acute severe colitis is present due to the risk of perforation.</p>\n<p>Early changes in ulcerative colitis:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li>Mucosal granularity</li>\n<li>Fine granular pattern</li>\n<li>Mucosal stippling</li>\n<li>Collar button ulcers</li>\n<li>Deep collar stud ulcers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below contrast radiograph shows granular mucosa typical of ulcerative colitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Collar button</strong> ulcers are small deep ulcers in which the button surface corresponds to the eroded ulcer base and the button neck corresponds to the undermined and preserved mucosa. They are also seen in <strong>ulcerative colitis</strong>.  </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>String sign</strong> represents circumferential inflammation and fibrosis, resulting in long segments of luminal narrowing as shown below. It is more characteristic of <strong>Crohn's</strong> disease. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Ram's horn</strong> sign is seen in <strong>gastric Crohn's</strong> disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0301","text":"Which of the following fin​dings is seen in patients​ with ulcerative colitis?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following findings is seen in patients with ulcerative colitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rose thorn​ appearance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudo​polyps","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Skip l​esions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudodi​verticula","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pseudopolyps","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pseudopolyps</strong></p><p><strong>Pseudopolyps</strong> are seen in <strong>ulcerative</strong> <strong>colitis</strong>. They are formed due to alternating inflammation and regeneration of the ulcerated mucosal epithelium in ulcerative colitis.</p>\n<p>Other <strong>gross findings</strong> in ulcerative colitis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Continuous</strong> mucosal <strong>inflammation</strong> of the colon most commonly involving the rectum</li>\n<li>Fine mucosal granularity</li>\n<li>Collar button ulcers</li>\n<li>Pseudopolyps. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rose thorn appearance, skip lesions, and pseudodiverticula are features of Crohn's disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0320","text":"A 37-year-old man was refe​rred to medical gastroent​erology with complaints o‌f chronic bloody diarrhea‌. Examination revealed bl‌ood on rectal examination‍. A colonoscopy was done ‌as shown below. What is t‌he likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 37-year-old man was referred to medical gastroenterology with complaints of chronic bloody diarrhea. Examination revealed blood on rectal examination. A colonoscopy was done as shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ulcerativ​e colitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Crohn’s​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ishchemi​c colitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Solitary rectal ulcer synd​rome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ulcerative colitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/44c42a275ae7483aa2e287c812cea386.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c86b5c3d363b480980464329422b2939x1280x960.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/706a9da544104085b168223487c4b5b7x640x640.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ulcerative colitis</strong></p><p>In the above scenario, colonoscopy shows <strong>continuous </strong>involvement of <strong>erythematous </strong>rectosigmoid mucosa and has a <strong>fine granular</strong> surface resembling sandpaper. This along with the clinical features is suggestive of <strong>ulcerative colitis.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows a gross specimen of the colon with <strong>diffuse</strong> involvement of mucosa and ulcers (without skip lesions) in <strong>ulcerative colitis. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image below shows a gross specimen of jejunum showing the <strong>cobblestone</strong> appearance as seen in <strong>Crohn's</strong> <strong>disease.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0317","text":"Which of the following com​plications is unlikely to​ occur in a patient with ‌ulcerative colitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following complications is unlikely to occur in a patient with ulcerative colitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Haemo​rrhage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Toxic m​egacolon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Malignan​t change","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fis​tula","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fistula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fistula</strong></p><p><strong>Fistula</strong> is not a complication of ulcerative colitis. It is seen in <strong>Crohn’s disease.</strong></p>\n<p>Hemorrhage, malignant change, and toxic megacolon are complications of ulcerative colitis. </p>\n<p>Note: Stricture formation may be a complication of ulcerative colitis, but it is seen only in 5-10% of patients. They are more commonly seen in Crohn's disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"dmLl8Lp1","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"29_opportunistic_mycoses","title":"29.Opportunistic Mycoses","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MC7459","text":"What is the stain used for​ identifying cryptococcus​?","raw_text":"What is the stain used for identifying cryptococcus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Giemsa​ stain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ziehl-Nie​lsen stain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gram s​taining","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mucic​armine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mucicarmine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cbda02beee84405383c8c4f50c00ecefx1280x850.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mucicarmine</strong></p><p><strong>Mucicarmine</strong> stain is used in the identification of cryptococcus.</p>\n<p>Mucicarmine stained specimen of lung tissue containing cryptococci:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7381","text":"Which of the following is ​a false statement regardi​ng Cryptococcus neoforman​s?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement regarding Cryptococcus neoformans?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is lacca​se negative","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It most commonly causes me​ningitis in AIDS patients​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"There are 3 serological ty​pes based on capsular pol​ysaccharide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Niger seed agar is used to​ demonstrate melanin prod​uction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is laccase negative","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/76300c16754c45bca463edb0d5c1e018x625x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is laccase negative</strong></p><p>Pathogenic varieties of cryptococcus including <em><strong>C. neoformans</strong></em> and <em><strong>C. gattii</strong></em> are <strong>laccase positive. </strong>They also have ability to <strong>grow at 37° C</strong> and these factors are used to differentiate them from non-pathogenic cryptococci.</p>\n<p>Cryptococci have been classified into 5 serotypes (A, B, C, D, and AD) based on their capsular polysaccharide:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Three serotypes</strong> fall under <em><strong>Cryptococcus neoformans</strong></em> - <strong>A</strong>(var. grubii), <strong>D</strong>(var. neoformans), and <strong>AD</strong>(a hybrid of serotypes A and D). Although serotype AD is very similar to serotypes A and D, the isolates still comprise a discrete group that can be distinguished.</li>\n<li>The <strong>serotypes</strong> <strong>B</strong> and <strong>C</strong> are now considered a different species - <em><strong>Cryptococcus gattii</strong></em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Niger seed agar</strong> is a solid medium used for <strong>selective and differential</strong> isolation of <em>Cryptococcus neoformans </em>from clinical specimens. <em>C. neoformans</em> and <em>C. gattii</em> produces <strong>brown colonies</strong> on this medium due to the <strong>formation of melanin</strong>. Melanin is produced <strong>by laccase using caffeic acid</strong> as the substrate.</p>\n<p>The following image shows pigmented colonies of <em>C. gattii</em> growing on niger seed agar.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Cryptococcal meningitis caused by <em>Cryptococcus neoformans,</em> is an opportunistic infection seen in individuals with AIDS. <strong>Flucytosine and amphotericin B</strong> are used in the treatment of this condition.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7371","text":"Which of the following org​anisms cannot be grown on​ any culture media? Parac​occidioides brasiliensis ‌Rhinosporidium seeberi Hi‌stoplasma capsulatum Pneu​mocystis jirovecii Rhizop‌us oryzae","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following organisms cannot be grown on any culture media?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Paracoccidioides brasiliensis</li>\n<li>Rhinosporidium seeberi </li>\n<li>Histoplasma capsulatum</li>\n<li>Pneumocystis jirovecii</li>\n<li>Rhizopus oryzae</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 &amp;a​mp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3 &amp;​amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 , 2 ​&amp;amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 &amp;a​mp; 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2 & 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2 &amp; 4</strong></p><p><strong><em>Rhinosporidium seeberi</em></strong> and <strong><em>Pneumocystis jirovecii</em></strong> are <strong>non-cultivable</strong> organisms and cannot be grown on any culture media.</p>\n<p>Another example of a non-cultivable fungus is <strong><em>Lacazia</em> <em>loboi. </em></strong>It causes <strong>Jorge Lobo's disease</strong> which is a chronic subcutaneous mycosis.</p>\n<p><strong><em>Rhizopus oryzae</em></strong> is a mold which is easily <strong>cultivable on SDA</strong>. <strong>Dimorphic fungi</strong> like <em>Histoplasma capsulatum</em> and <em>Paracoccidiodes brasiliensis</em> are <strong>identified</strong> based on their <strong>colony morphology</strong> on SDA.</p>\n<p>Note: Recently, molecular biological techniques have demonstrated that <strong>Rhinosporidium</strong> is an <strong>aquatic protistan parasite. </strong>Infections due to Rhinosporidium have been classified under infections due to Protozoa under the ICD 11.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7385","text":"A patient with HIV present​ed complaining of cough a​nd progressively worsenin​g breathing difficulty. C‌hest X-ray showed bilater‌al interstitial infiltrat​es. The findings of micro‌scopic examination of his‌ bronchoalveolar lavage i‍s given below. Which is t​he most probable causativ‌e organism?","raw_text":"A patient with HIV presented complaining of cough and progressively worsening breathing difficulty. Chest X-ray showed bilateral interstitial infiltrates. The findings of microscopic examination of his bronchoalveolar lavage is given below. Which is the most probable causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pneumocysti​s jirovecii","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Histoplasma​ capsulatum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cryptococcu​s neoformans","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rhinosporid​ium seeberi","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pneumocystis jirovecii","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/44ce4ab387e7427b8a8e9179101a6d1e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f226fe1f12374e5cb58345308b95bba9x943x751.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7450493997504a87a7019d579aaa032bx1279x1057.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pneumocystis jirovecii</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture and the<strong> GMS stained</strong> <strong>smear</strong> showing the black-colored cysts resembling <strong>crushed ping pong balls</strong> are suggestive of <strong>pneumocystis pneumonia (PCP)</strong> caused by <strong><em>Pneumocystis jirovecii</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>Patients with PCP generally present with fever and cough that is usually non-productive. <strong>Chest X-ray</strong> is either a normal film if the disease is suspected early, or with <strong>diffuse bilateral interstitial infiltrates</strong>. The classic finding of<strong> dense perihilar infiltrate</strong> is unusual in patients with AIDS.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> of choice is <strong>t</strong><strong>rimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (cotrimoxazole)</strong> and pentamidine isethionate. Cotrimoxazole can also be used for <strong>prophylaxis</strong> in <strong>patients having AIDS</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following X-ray image shows a <strong>cyst</strong> (arrow) and <strong>bilateral lung infiltrates</strong> in a patient with PCP:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7312","text":"A middle-aged immunocompro​mised patient presents wi​th complaints of pain dur​ing swallowing. She descr‌ibes it as a burning sens‌ation that radiates to he​r back every time she swa‌llows. Oral examination r‌eveals creamy white patch‍es on the oral mucosa. Th​e wet mount microscopy of‌ the scraping obtained fr‌om the patches is given b‌elow. Identify the organi​sm.","raw_text":"A middle-aged immunocompromised patient presents with complaints of pain during swallowing. She describes it as a burning sensation that radiates to her back every time she swallows. Oral examination reveals creamy white patches on the oral mucosa. The wet mount microscopy of the scraping obtained from the patches is given below. Identify the organism.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Candida ​albicans","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cryptococcu​s neoformans","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aspergillu​s fumigatus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Histoplasma​ capsulatum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Candida albicans","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fffa43d41ecc44fb93107e6349b750aa.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Candida albicans</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of <strong>odynophagia</strong>, <strong>creamy white patches</strong> on the oral mucosa and the wet mount microscopy showing <strong>budding yeasts with pseudohyphae</strong> are suggestive of <strong>oral candidiasis</strong> caused by <strong><em>Candida albicans. </em></strong></p>\n<p>It is an <strong>opportunistic infection</strong> commonly seen in <strong>immunocompromised states</strong> like AIDS, diabetes etc.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes topical azoles or nystatin.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5194","text":"The following test is posi​tive for identification o​f ________","raw_text":"The following test is positive for identification of ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Candida ​albicans","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Candida ​glabrata","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Candida pa​rapsilosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Candida t​ropicalis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Candida albicans","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/53a2996c5ddf4a3faaa168cbaa5877fb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Candida albicans</strong></p><p>The image shows the<strong> germ tube test </strong>used for<strong> </strong>identifying <strong><em>Candida albicans. </em></strong></p>\n<p>Unlike other species of candida, yeast cells of <em>Candida albicans</em> will begin to form<strong> </strong>germ tubes after <strong>incubation in serum</strong> maintained <strong>at 37°C</strong> for about <strong>90 minutes.</strong> This is known as the<strong> Reynolds-Braude phenomenon. </strong>This test is positive for <em>Candia dubliniensis</em> as well.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0632","text":"A 35-year-old HIV positive​​ patient presents with m​ucosal lesions in the mou​th as shown in the image ‌below. On microscopy, bud‌ding yeasts and pseudohyp​hae are seen. What is the‌ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old HIV positive​ patient presents with mucosal lesions in the mouth as shown in the image below. On microscopy, budding yeasts and pseudohyphae are seen. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral can​didiasis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hairy le​ukoplakia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lichen​ planus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dipht​heria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Oral candidiasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9b60c6d7153842c5ac94ece85edf0fde.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Oral candidiasis</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis of the given clinical scenario is <strong>oral candidiasis.</strong></p>\n<p>Oral candidiasis or thrush is a patchy, <strong>pseudomembranous lesion</strong> that develops over the tongue, lips, gum or palate. It is composed of epithelial cells, yeasts, and pseudohyphae. The causative agent is usually <strong><em>Candida albicans</em></strong>.</p>\n<p>It occurs in almost all individuals with <strong>AIDS</strong>. Other risk factors include hyperglycemia, cellular immunodeficiency, corticosteroids or antibiotics.</p>\n<p>It is treated with topical nystatin or oral ketoconazole or fluconazole.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Hairy leukoplakia</strong> is a wart-like growth on the tongue, caused by <strong>Epstein Barr virus</strong>. It is commonly seen as a <strong>white lesion</strong> on the<strong> lateral side of the tongue.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Lichen planus</strong> is a pruritic disease characterized by papules commonly seen over the flexor surfaces and trunk. Oral and vulvovaginal lesions may also occur. Oral lesions appear as <strong>lacy, white, and raised lesions</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Diphtheria</strong> is characterized by the presence of a <strong>grey pseudomembrane</strong> covering the tonsils and pharynx. It is caused by <strong><em>Corynebacterium diphtheriae.</em></strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6941","text":"Galactomannan in serum is ​seen in: 1. Invasive aspe​rgillosis 2. Systemic can​didiasis 3. Mucormycosis ‌4. Cryptococcosis","raw_text":"<p>Galactomannan in serum is seen in:</p>\n<p>1. Invasive aspergillosis<br/>2. Systemic candidiasis<br/>3. Mucormycosis<br/>4. Cryptococcosis</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 &amp;amp;​ 2 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 &amp;amp;​ 3 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 o​nly","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 &amp;am​p; 3 only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 only</strong></p><p>The <strong>galactomannan assay</strong> is used for the diagnosis of <strong>invasive (deep-seated) mycoses </strong>caused by <strong><em>Aspergillus</em></strong> species.</p>\n<p>Galactomannan is a <strong>component of</strong> the <strong>cell wall</strong> of <em>Aspergillus</em>. It can be detected in <strong>serum or bronchoalveolar lavage by ELISA</strong> for the diagnosis of invasive aspergillosis in adults and children. This test is especially useful in <strong>hematopoietic stem cell transplant</strong> recipients or patients with <strong>hematologic malignancies. </strong></p>\n<p>Other diagnostic features of invasive aspergillosis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A positive culture</strong> from the affected organ</li>\n<li>Aspergillus antigen detection of <strong>galactomannan</strong></li>\n<li>Narrow, <strong>septate hyphae</strong> with branching at <strong>45<sup>o</sup></strong> and conidiophores</li>\n<li><strong>Halo sign</strong> on CT scan</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9217","text":"Identify the organism in t​he given slide stained wi​th Gomori methenamine sil​ver stain.","raw_text":"Identify the organism in the given slide stained with Gomori methenamine silver stain.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obtuse an​gled mucor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute angled​ penicillium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute angled branching asp​ergillus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Obtuse angl​ed rhizopus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acute angled branching aspergillus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/98b03b33d5754140b261f3fa6e67c329.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b56b0fefcc8a4f61959ee5a6680bcaf0x567x425.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acute angled branching aspergillus</strong></p><p>The given slide stained with GMS shows <strong>acute-angled</strong> (45<sup>o</sup>) uniform, <strong>branching septate hyphae</strong> which is suggestive of<strong> Aspergillus species.</strong></p>\n<p>Aspergillus species grow rapidly, producing <strong>aerial hyphae</strong> that bear characteristic conidial structures. <strong>Long aseptate conidiophores</strong> are seen with terminal vesicles on which <strong>phialides</strong> are arranged in <strong>basipetal chains</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following image shows the lactophenol cotton blue solution (LPCB) mount of <em>Aspergillus fumigatus </em>showing the long conidiophores with terminal vesicles.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <em><strong>Mucor</strong></em> produces <strong>nonseptate, broad hyphae</strong> with aerial sporangiophores which end in a sporangium.</p>\n<p>Option B: <em><strong>Penicillium</strong></em> produces <strong>septate hyphae</strong> with branched, <strong>septate</strong> and <strong>brush-like conidiophores</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong><em>Rhizopus</em></strong> appears similar to <em>Mucor</em> but it has <strong>rhizoids</strong> with sporangiophores arising in groups directly above the rhizoids.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7376","text":"A young lady complains of ​recurrent rhinitis, nasal​ discharge and bilateral ​nasal blockage for two ye‌ars. She has a history of‌ allergic rhinitis. On rh​inoscopy, multiple ethmoi‌dal polyps with mucosal t‌hickening and impacted se‍cretions are seen in both​ the nasal cavities. A ti‌ssue biopsy is taken and ‌sent for culture, which o‌n microscopic examination​ shows the following appe‍arance. What is the most ​probable organism implica‍ted here?","raw_text":"A young lady complains of recurrent rhinitis, nasal discharge and bilateral nasal blockage for two years.  She has a history of allergic rhinitis. On rhinoscopy, multiple ethmoidal polyps with mucosal thickening and impacted secretions are seen in both the nasal cavities. A tissue biopsy is taken and sent for culture, which on microscopic examination shows the following appearance. What is the most probable organism implicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aspergillu​s fumigatus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aspergill​us flavus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aspergil​lus niger","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Penicillium​ chrysogenum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aspergillus fumigatus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/931ba62aceb24b6faaba12abeec09373.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f1feab26baf440d8ab04f4565cb8f3ecx320x201.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d304cfc3691d4211a99eedd0af4c9953x623x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ed1788ddd8ac4189b11c93cfe5eebfe2x567x425.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aspergillus fumigatus</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture and the microscopic appearance is suggestive of <strong>fungal rhinosinusitis.</strong> The most probable causative organism is <strong><em>Aspergillus fumigatus</em>. </strong>It is commonly involved in paranasal sinus mycoses (fungal sinusitis).</p>\n<p>The disease has been described to have<strong> four types</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>allergic</li>\n<li>non-invasive</li>\n<li>invasive</li>\n<li>fulminant </li>\n</ul>\n<p>A possible fifth type called <strong>non-invasive destructive</strong> type may also exist.</p>\n<p><strong>Zygomycetes</strong> like mucor and rhizopus are the common agents in <strong>fulminant type </strong>of fungal sinusitis.</p>\n<p>The characteristic microscopic appearance of <em>Aspergillus fumigatus:</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The vesicle is conical.</li>\n<li><strong>Phialides</strong> are arranged in a <strong>single row</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Conidia</strong> arise from the <strong>upper third of the vesicle</strong> and are hyaline.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B:<em> Aspergillus flavus </em>appears as follows:</p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The vesicle is globular.</li>\n<li>Phialides are arranged in one or two rows.</li>\n<li><strong>Conidia</strong> arise from the <strong>entire vesicle</strong> and are hyaline.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option C:<em> </em><em>Aspergillus niger </em>appear as follows:</p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The vesicle is globular shaped.</li>\n<li><strong>Phialides</strong> are in <strong>two rows</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Conidia</strong> arise from the <strong>entire vesicle</strong> and are black.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option D: <em>Penicillium chrysogenum</em> appear as follows:</p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Septate hyphae with <strong>branched</strong> <strong>conidiophores</strong> bearing chains of spores<strong>(brush or broom appearance).</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3062","text":"What is the drug of choice​ for invasive pulmonary a​spergillosis?","raw_text":"What is the drug of choice for invasive pulmonary aspergillosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Itraco​nazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vorico​nazole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lyophylised Amphotericin B​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posaco​nazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Voriconazole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Voriconazole</strong></p><p><strong>Voriconazole</strong> is the drug of choice in the treatment of <strong>invasive pulmonary aspergillosis.</strong>  It can also be used against candida species (including glabrata and krusei), scedosporium and fusarium.</p>\n<p>The <strong>other options</strong> can be used as <strong>alternatives</strong> for the treatment of invasive pulmonary aspergillosis if voriconazole is unavailable.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0277","text":"β 1-3 glucan assay is used​ to diagnose all except?","raw_text":"β 1-3 glucan assay is used to diagnose all except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Candi​diasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Asperg​illosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pneumocysti​s pneumonia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mucorm​ycosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mucormycosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mucormycosis</strong></p><p>The<strong> β 1-3 glucan</strong> assay <strong>cannot</strong> be used to <strong>diagnose</strong> <strong>mucormycosis</strong>. This condition is usually diagnosed based on clinical features and culture.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The test detects the presence of (1-3)-β-<span class=\"small-caps\">d</span>-glucan (BDG) which is an abundant <strong>cell wall polysaccharide</strong> found in most fungi. </p>\n<p>It is a chromogenic, <strong>quantitative enzyme immunoassay</strong> (EIA) designed to detect beta D Glucan by using purified, lysed horseshoe crab (Limulus polyphemus) amebocytes. 1,3 Beta D Glucan level of <strong>≥80 pg/mL</strong> is considered a <strong>positive </strong>result.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Fungi that do not contain BDG in their cell walls include</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><em>Cryptococcus neoformans</em></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Zygomycetes like rhizopus and mucor</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><em>Blastomyces dermatitidis</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Mucormycosis</strong> is an <strong>opportunistic mycosis</strong> caused by <strong>z<span class=\"s2\">y</span>gomycetes, </strong>which do not contain (1-3)-β-<span class=\"small-caps\">d</span>-glucan, and hence, the β 1-3 glucan assay cannot be used for its diagnosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3210","text":"Which fungus is most commo​nly associated with orbit​al cellulitis in patients​ with diabetic ketoacidos‌is ?","raw_text":"Which fungus is most commonly associated with orbital cellulitis in patients with diabetic ketoacidosis ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can​dida","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rhiz​opus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Asper​gillus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tricho​phyton","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rhizopus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rhizopus</strong></p><p><strong>Rhizopus</strong> is the most common fungus associated with <strong>orbital cellulitis</strong> in patients with diabetic ketoacidosis.</p>\n<p>Diabetic patients are <strong>immunocompromised</strong> and so prone to develop opportunistic infections. Orbital cellulitis with Rhizopus may turn fatal in these patients as it is an <strong>angioinvasive fungus</strong> and could lead to <strong>septicemia and thromboses</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7377","text":"A 68-year-old post-COVID-1​9 patient presented with ​a black, foul-smelling na​sal discharge and blurred‌ vision. On examination, ‌a blackish necrotic mass ​eroding the septum and ha‌rd palate was visualised.‌ Lactophenol cotton blue ‍mount from the culture is​ given below. Which of th‌e following statements is‌ false regarding the cond‌ition?","raw_text":"A 68-year-old post-COVID-19 patient presented with a black, foul-smelling nasal discharge and blurred vision. On examination, a blackish necrotic mass eroding the septum and hard palate was visualised. Lactophenol cotton blue mount from the culture is given below. Which of the following statements is false regarding the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a fat​al condition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iron administration to pat​ients with active infecti​on should be avoided","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posaconazole can be used t​o treat it","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary form is the most​ common","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pulmonary form is the most common","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/275e6e2bdc834465b04a465318c02a4a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pulmonary form is the most common</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the LPCB mount showing aseptate hyphae branching at right-angles is strongly suggestive of<strong> mucormycosis. </strong><strong>Rhino-orbito-cerebral</strong> mucormycosis is the <strong>most common</strong> form of mucormycosis. <strong>Pulmonary mucormycosis</strong> is the <strong>second</strong> most common form.</p>\n<p>Mucormycosis is an invasive disease caused principally by species of Rhizopus, Mucor, and<strong> </strong>Lichtheimia. The disease is often characterized by hyphae growing in and around the blood vessels. It <strong>can turn fatal</strong> on cerebral involvement and development of speticemia.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for the infection include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diabetes (Diabetic ketoacidosis).</li>\n<li>Elevated levels of free iron.</li>\n<li>Defects in phagocytic function.</li>\n<li>Organ or hematopoietic stem transplantation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> of choice is Amphotericin-B. <strong>Posaconazole</strong> can be used as an <strong>alternative</strong> drug.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"0NM5U9c7","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"29_hepatic_disorders_and_infections_in_pregnancy","title":"29.Hepatic Disorders And Infections In Pregnancy","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB1565","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true regarding obstet​ric cholestasis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding obstetric cholestasis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pruritus is worse at night‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum bilirubin &amp;gt;5 mg/d‌l","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pruritus usually develops ‌in the third trimester","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aspartate transaminase &amp;lt‌;200 IU /L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Serum bilirubin >5 mg/dl","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Serum bilirubin &gt;5 mg/dl</strong></p><p><strong>Obstetric cholestasis</strong> is a condition in which cholestasis occurs in pregnancy. <strong>Serum bilirubin</strong> increases very late in the disease and total plasma concentrations<strong> rarely exceed 5 mg/dL</strong>. </p>\n<p>Hyperbilirubinemia results from the retention of conjugated pigment. </p>\n<p>It is also referred to as recurrent jaundice of pregnancy, cholestatic hepatosis, and icterus gravidarum. There is a significant genetic influence. It may be more common in <strong>multifetal pregnancies.</strong></p>\n<p>Patients present with <strong>pruritus</strong> that develops usually during the<strong> third trimester,</strong> typically involving the <strong>palms and soles</strong>. It is <strong>worse at night</strong>. Icterus may also be present.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 135px;\" width=\"455\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177.867px;\">\n<p><strong>Disorder</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.683px;\"><strong>AST (SGOT) U/L</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 125.45px;\"><strong>Bilirubin (mg/dL)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177.867px;\">Cholestasis of pregnancy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.683px;\"><strong>Up to 200</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 125.45px;\">1–5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177.867px;\">Acute fatty liver of pregnancy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.683px;\">200–800</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125.45px;\">4–10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177.867px;\">Hepatitis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.683px;\">2000+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125.45px;\">5–20</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9731","text":"A woman at 34 weeks of ges‌tation presents with seve​re itching over her palms​ and soles for 2 weeks. S​he gives a history of sim‍ilar complaints in her la‍st pregnancy. On examinat​ion, she is icteric. Whic​h of the following is the‌ preferred investigation ‌to diagnose this conditio​n?","raw_text":"A woman at 34 weeks of gestation presents with severe itching over her palms and soles for 2 weeks. She gives a history of similar complaints in her last pregnancy. On examination, she is icteric. Which of the following is the preferred investigation to diagnose this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum b‌ile acid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Serum b‌ilirubin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alanine amin‌o transferase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5' nucl‌eosidase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Serum bile acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Serum bile acid</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture is suggestive of <strong>cholestasis in pregnancy</strong>. <strong>Serum bile acid</strong> is the <strong>earliest </strong>and<strong> most consistent</strong> parameter to increase in obstetric cholestasis.</p>\n<p><strong>Serum cholic acid</strong> is preferred compared to serum chenodeoxycholic acid.</p>\n<p>Although <strong>serum bile acid</strong> increases after meals it is still considered an important investigation among women with obstetric cholestasis. This is because serum bile acid levels <strong>remain significantly elevated</strong> in women with <strong>obstetric cholestasis</strong> in comparison to pregnant women without it. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9730","text":"A food critic at 32 weeks ‌of gestation presents wit​h nausea and mild right u​pper abdominal pain. She ​also gives a history of i‍tching over palms and sol‍es for 2 weeks. On examin​ation, she is icteric. In​vestigations done are sho‌wn below. What is the pre‌ferred treatment in this ​condition? Bilirubin= 3.5​mg/dl AST= 180 U/L ALP= 2‌30 U/L","raw_text":"<p>A food critic at 32 weeks of gestation presents with nausea and mild right upper abdominal pain. She also gives a history of itching over palms and soles for 2 weeks. On examination, she is icteric. Investigations done are shown below. What is the preferred treatment in this condition?</p>\n<p>Bilirubin= 3.5mg/dl</p>\n<p>AST= 180 U/L</p>\n<p>ALP= 230 U/L</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antihistamines + nutrition‌al support","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antivira‌l therapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ursodeoxyc‌holic acid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cholest‌yramine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ursodeoxycholic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ursodeoxycholic acid</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>obstetric cholestasis</strong>. <strong>Ursodeoxycholic acid</strong> is the drug of choice in obstetric cholestasis.</p>\n<p>It relieves pruritus, lowers bile acid and serum enzyme levels (like SGOT, ALP). It may also reduce certain neonatal complications such as preterm birth, fetal distress, and respiratory distress syndrome.</p>\n<p>Antihistamines and topical emollients<strong> </strong>may provide some relief. </p>\n<p><strong>Cholestyramine</strong> causes decreased absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, which leads to <strong>vitamin K deficiency</strong>. Fetal coagulopathy may develop, which may lead to<strong> intracranial hemorrhage and stillbirth</strong>, and therefore it is <strong>not preferred.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<table width=\"455\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Disorder</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td><strong>AST (SGOT) U/L</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Bilirubin (mg/dL)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cholestasis of pregnancy</td>\n<td>Up to 200</td>\n<td>1–5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Acute fatty liver of pregnancy</td>\n<td>200–800</td>\n<td>4–10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hepatitis</td>\n<td>2000+</td>\n<td>5–20</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1569","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures are not likely to b​e seen in a patient with ​acute fatty liver of preg​nancy? Hyperglycemia Coag‍ulopathy Hypercholesterol‍emia Thrombocytosis Hypof​ibrinogenemia","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following features are not likely to be seen in a patient with acute fatty liver of pregnancy?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Hyperglycemia</li>\n<li>Coagulopathy</li>\n<li>Hypercholesterolemia</li>\n<li>Thrombocytosis</li>\n<li>Hypofibrinogenemia</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onl‌y 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 &amp;a‌mp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3, 4 &amp;‌amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3 &amp;‌amp; 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3 & 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3 &amp; 4</strong></p><p>Hyperglycemia, hypercholesterolemia, and thrombocytosis are not likely to be seen in acute fatty liver of pregnancy (AFLP). </p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features of AFLP</strong> include:<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Persistent nausea and vomiting</li>\n<li>Progressive jaundice</li>\n<li><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong></li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Proteinuria</li>\n<li>Edema</li>\n<li><strong>Thrombocytopenia</strong></li>\n<li>Moderate to severe liver dysfunction - manifested by:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypofibrinogenemia</strong></li>\n<li>Hypoalbuminemia</li>\n<li><strong>Hypocholesterolemia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Prolonged clotting time.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1568","text":"A woman at 37 weeks of ges‌tation with icterus, naus​ea, and yellowish discolo​ration of urine has abnor​mal liver function tests.‍ She is suspected to have‍ acute fatty liver of pre​gnancy. Which of the foll​owing is false regarding ‌this condition?","raw_text":"A woman at 37 weeks of gestation with icterus, nausea, and yellowish discoloration of urine has abnormal liver function tests. She is suspected to have acute fatty liver of pregnancy. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Symptoms improve post deli‌very","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"More common in first pregn‌ancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with female fet‌us","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Occurs due to heterozygous‌ defect for fatty acid ox​idation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Associated with female fetus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Associated with female fetus</strong></p><p><strong>Acute fatty liver of pregnancy</strong> (AFLP) is more commonly associated with <strong>male fetuses</strong> and not female fetuses.</p>\n<p>AFLP is a rare, potentially fatal complication also known as acute fatty metamorphosis or acute yellow atrophy. It is more common in the <strong>third trimester</strong><strong>. </strong>It is<strong> </strong>due to a defect in the long-chain-3-hydroxy acyl-CoA-dehydrogenase <strong>(LCHAD) pathway</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is also associated with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First pregnancy </strong>- uncommon in subsequent pregnancies</li>\n<li>Maternal obesity</li>\n<li><strong>Multiple pregnancies</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>Investigations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Liver function tests such as AST, ALT, and serum bilirubin levels are raised.</li>\n<li>Hemolysis with coagulopathy:\n<ul>\n<li>Decreased platelets and fibrinogen</li>\n<li>Elevated d-dimers and prothrombin time</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Biopsy reveals a characteristic<strong> microvesicular fatty infiltration</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The definitive management of AFLP is <strong>rapid delivery of the fetus</strong> and supportive care. Hepatic dysfunction usually <strong>normalizes</strong> within a week of delivery. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1571","text":"Which of the following wil‌l not increase the risk f​or urinary tract infectio​n in pregnant women?","raw_text":"Which of the following will not increase the risk for urinary tract infection in pregnant women?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diab‌etes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypert‌ension","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sickle ce‌ll anaemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vesicouret‌eric reflux","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypertension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypertension</strong></p><p>Hypertension is not a risk factor for urinary infections in pregnancy. </p>\n<p>Risk factors for urinary infections in pregnancy include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Urinary retention</li>\n<li><strong>Vesicoureteric reflux</strong> of pregnancy</li>\n<li><strong>Diabetes</strong> in pregnancy</li>\n<li><strong>Sickle cell anemia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1621","text":"A G3P2 lady at 13 weeks of‌ gestation presented for ​a regular antenatal check​up. A urine routine revea​led the presence of 2,00,‍000 gram-negative bacilli‍/mL. Repeat urinalysis sh​owed the following result​s. Which of the following‌ is not true about this c‌ondition? Urinalysis: Pro​tein: negative Pus cells:​ 1-2/HPF Bacteria: 1,50,0‌00 gram negative bacilli/​mL","raw_text":"<p>A G3P2 lady at 13 weeks of gestation presented for a regular antenatal checkup. A urine routine revealed the presence of 2,00,000 gram-negative bacilli/mL. Repeat urinalysis showed the following results. Which of the following is not true about this condition?</p>\n<p>Urinalysis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Protein: negative</li>\n<li>Pus cells: 1-2/HPF</li>\n<li>Bacteria: 1,50,000 gram negative bacilli/mL</li>\n</ul>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incidence is similar to th‌at in non pregnant women","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is higher in people wit‌h sickle cell trait","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antibiotic therapy is requ‌ired only if symptoms dev​elop","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"There is risk of developin‌g chronic renal lesions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Antibiotic therapy is required only if symptoms develop","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Antibiotic therapy is required only if symptoms develop</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>asymptomatic bacteriuria. Antibiotic therapy</strong> should be given regardless of the symptoms. </p>\n<p>Asymptomatic bacteriuria<strong> </strong>refers to persistent, actively multiplying bacteria within the urinary tract in asymptomatic women.</p>\n<p>Organisms that cause urinary infections are those from the normal perineal flora. The most common causative agent is <strong><em>E.coli.</em> </strong>Others include <em>Klebsiella, </em>and<em> Proteus</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Incidence</strong> is <strong>similar</strong> to that in <strong>non-pregnant</strong> women. Prevalence depends on parity (more in higher parity), race, socioeconomic status (more in low SES).</p>\n<p>It is more common in<strong> </strong>patients with sickle cell anemia/trait, diabetes. There is a <strong>high risk </strong>of developing<strong> chronic renal lesions</strong> in later life. </p>\n<p>It is also associated with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Anemia</li>\n<li>Premature labor</li>\n<li>Intrauterine growth retardation</li>\n<li>Urinary tract abnormalities in recurrent cases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Diagnostic criteria:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>positive bacterial count</strong> of the same species - <strong>over 1,00,000/mL </strong></li>\n<li>Organisms in midstream clean-catch specimen of urine on two occasions</li>\n<li>No symptoms of urinary infection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Single-dose therapy - Amoxicillin 3g, Nitrofurantoin 200mg </li>\n<li>Regular 10-14 days therapy - Amoxicillin, Nitrofurantoin, Cephalexin, or Amoxiclav</li>\n<li>Recurrent cases need to be treated until delivery with prophylactic doses.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9734","text":"A woman presented to the c‌linic with severe lower a​bdominal pain and dysuria​ at 28 weeks of gestation​. Suprapubic tenderness w‍as noted on palpation. Ur‍ine examination revealed ​pyuria with microscopic h​ematuria. All of the foll‌owing can be used to trea‌t this condition except?","raw_text":"A woman presented to the clinic with severe lower abdominal pain and dysuria at 28 weeks of gestation.  Suprapubic tenderness was noted on palpation. Urine examination revealed pyuria with microscopic hematuria.  All of the following can be used to treat this condition except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amoxy‌cillin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cefa‌zolin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ciprof‌loxacin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cefi‌xime","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ciprofloxacin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ciprofloxacin</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>cystitis</strong>. <strong>Ciprofloxacin</strong>,<strong> a </strong><span class=\"st\" data-hveid=\"44\" data-ved=\"0ahUKEwi7wNzb-qTZAhXLPY8KHUZsAF4Q4EUILDAA\"><strong>fluoroquinolone</strong> antibiotic, is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in pregnancy as it causes cartilage and bone defects in the fetus.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Antibiotics</strong> are used for treating cystitis in pregnancy. </p>\n<p><strong>Antibiotics safe in pregnancy include:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"st\" data-hveid=\"44\" data-ved=\"0ahUKEwi7wNzb-qTZAhXLPY8KHUZsAF4Q4EUILDAA\">Penicillin group</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"st\" data-hveid=\"44\" data-ved=\"0ahUKEwi7wNzb-qTZAhXLPY8KHUZsAF4Q4EUILDAA\">Cephalosporins</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"st\" data-hveid=\"44\" data-ved=\"0ahUKEwi7wNzb-qTZAhXLPY8KHUZsAF4Q4EUILDAA\">Erythromycin</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"st\" data-hveid=\"44\" data-ved=\"0ahUKEwi7wNzb-qTZAhXLPY8KHUZsAF4Q4EUILDAA\">Metronidazole (except in the 1st trimester)</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"st\" data-hveid=\"44\" data-ved=\"0ahUKEwi7wNzb-qTZAhXLPY8KHUZsAF4Q4EUILDAA\"><strong>Antibiotics unsafe in pregnancy include:</strong></span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"st\" data-hveid=\"44\" data-ved=\"0ahUKEwi7wNzb-qTZAhXLPY8KHUZsAF4Q4EUILDAA\">Tetracycline</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"st\" data-hveid=\"44\" data-ved=\"0ahUKEwi7wNzb-qTZAhXLPY8KHUZsAF4Q4EUILDAA\">Aminoglycoside</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"st\" data-hveid=\"44\" data-ved=\"0ahUKEwi7wNzb-qTZAhXLPY8KHUZsAF4Q4EUILDAA\">Chloramphenicol</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"st\" data-hveid=\"44\" data-ved=\"0ahUKEwi7wNzb-qTZAhXLPY8KHUZsAF4Q4EUILDAA\">Metronidazole(in the first trimester)</span></li>\n<li><strong><span class=\"st\" data-hveid=\"44\" data-ved=\"0ahUKEwi7wNzb-qTZAhXLPY8KHUZsAF4Q4EUILDAA\">Fluoroquinolones</span></strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1570","text":"Which stage of pregnancy i‌s most often complicated ​by acute pyelonephritis?","raw_text":"Which stage of pregnancy is most often complicated by acute pyelonephritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1st tr‌imester","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2nd tr‌imester","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3rd tr‌imester","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Puerp‌erium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2nd trimester","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2nd trimester</strong></p><p><strong>Acute pyelonephritis</strong> in pregnancy most often complicates <strong>2nd trimester</strong> of pregnancy. </p>\n<p>Acute pyelonephritis occurs more commonly in those with preexisting renal disease. It is caused by<strong> <em>E. coli </em></strong><em>&gt;&gt; Streptococcus sp., S.aureus &gt; Enterobacter, Klebsiella sp. </em></p>\n<p>Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fever with chills</li>\n<li><strong>Loin pain</strong></li>\n<li>Vomiting</li>\n<li>Right-sided renal angle tenderness</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients may develop <strong>acute respiratory distress syndrome</strong> due to endotoxin-mediated alveolar injury.<strong> Hemolysis</strong> can also occur due to endotoxins, leading to <strong>anemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes <strong>intravenous fluids </strong>with <strong>antibiotics</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9747","text":"Which of the following con‌genital infections are as​sociated with the triad o​f Gregg in a neonate?","raw_text":"Which of the following congenital infections are associated with the triad of Gregg in a neonate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Toxop‌lasma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parvo‌virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Varicella z‌oster virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rub‌ella","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rubella","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rubella</strong></p><p><strong>Triad of Gregg</strong> is seen in a neonate infected congenitally with<strong> rubella virus</strong>. The features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)</li>\n<li>Cataract</li>\n<li>Sensorineural Hearing loss (SNHL)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other infections which cause congenital malformations are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 306px;\" width=\"439\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105.483px;\"><strong>Organism</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 316.017px;\"><strong>Effect</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105.483px;\">Toxoplasma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 316.017px;\">\n<p>Hydro/microcephaly, convulsions, chorioretinitis, cerebral calcification</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105.483px;\">Varicella</td>\n<td style=\"width: 316.017px;\">\n<p>Skin scarring, hypoplastic limbs, eye defects, microcephaly</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105.483px;\">Parvovirus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 316.017px;\">Non-immune hydrops</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105.483px;\">Syphilis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 316.017px;\">\n<p><strong>Hutchinson's triad:</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Interstitial keratitis</li>\n<li>Hutchinson's teeth</li>\n<li>Eighth nerve deafness</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9735","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about tox​oplasmosis in pregnancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about toxoplasmosis in pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maximum transmission and d‌amage in 1st trimester","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maximum transmission in 1s‌t trimester and maximum d​amage in 3rd trimester","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maximum damage in 1st trim‌ester and maximum transmi​ssion in 3rd trimester","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Maximum transmission and d‌amage in 3rd trimester","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Maximum damage in 1st trimester and maximum transmission in 3rd trimester","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Maximum damage in 1st trimester and maximum transmission in 3rd trimester</strong></p><p>Toxoplasmosis causes <strong>maximal complications</strong> in<strong> 1st trimester</strong> and is <strong>maximally transmitted </strong>in the <strong>3rd trimester.</strong></p>\n<p>This is because the risk of transmission increases and the risk of damage decreases as pregnancy progresses further.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of affected neonates include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hydrocephalus or microcephaly</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Chorioretinitis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Intracranial calcifications</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Convulsions</strong></li>\n<li>Low birthweight</li>\n<li>Hepatosplenomegaly</li>\n<li>Jaundice</li>\n<li>Anemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1592","text":"A woman presents for a reg‌ular antenatal check-up a​t 12 weeks of gestation. ​She works at a rescue cen​ter that held an adoption‍ drive for cats a few wee‍ks ago. On examination, m​ild cervical lymphadenopa​thy is noted. Sabin-Feldm‌an dye test is positive. ‌Which of the following wi​ll be used to treat this ​condition?","raw_text":"A woman presents for a regular antenatal check-up at 12 weeks of gestation. She works at a rescue center that held an adoption drive for cats a few weeks ago. On examination, mild cervical lymphadenopathy is noted. Sabin-Feldman dye test is positive. Which of the following will be used to treat this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spira‌mycin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pyrimethamin‌e-sulfonamide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Doxyc‌ycline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cotrim‌oxazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Spiramycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Spiramycin</strong></p><p>The given scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>maternal toxoplasmosis</strong>. It is treated using <strong>spiramycin.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pyrimethamine-sulfonamide</strong> is used when <strong>fetal infections </strong>are<strong> </strong>confirmed. It should be <strong>avoided </strong>in the <strong>first trimester</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Toxoplasmosis </strong>is caused by <em>Toxoplasma gondii.</em> <strong>Cats</strong> are definitive hosts. It can be transmitted to humans either by ingestion of raw or inadequately cooked infected meat or by ingestion of oocysts via exposure to cat litter or soil.</p>\n<p><strong>Acute</strong> infection is diagnosed either by the presence of <strong>IgG and IgM antibodies</strong> or by a four-fold increase in paired specimens 4 weeks apart.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5723","text":"A per-vaginal examination ‌performed on a lady in la​bor reveals multiple pain​ful tiny vesicular ulcers​ over the vulva and vagin‍al walls. A slide showing‍ the microscopic findings​ is given below. What is ​the best step in the mana‌gement of delivery in thi‌s patient?","raw_text":"A per-vaginal examination performed on a lady in labor reveals multiple painful tiny vesicular ulcers over the vulva and vaginal walls. A slide showing the microscopic findings is given below. What is the best step in the management of delivery in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vaginal ‌delivery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cesarean‌ section","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Give tocolytics and treat ‌the infection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Local application of antib‌iotic.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cesarean section","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2ff0c439c3b4422da3ee0ab1816cf964.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fdde9322be5c459db277c2cbe52f4451x1280x1144.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cesarean section</strong></p><p><strong>Painful genital ulcers</strong> and painful <strong>inguinal lymphadenopathy</strong> are suggestive of <strong>herpes virus</strong> infection. The cytological image showing multinucleate giant cells, <strong>Tzanck cells</strong> confirms the diagnosis of HSV. The best step in the management of delivery in this patient is a <strong>cesarean section. </strong></p>\n<p>Herpes infection in the newborn may occur during birth, particularly if the mother has<strong> genital lesions</strong> due to<strong> HSV-2</strong>. In such cases, a cesarean section may <strong>prevent neonatal infection.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1586","text":"In a pregnant woman with h‌erpes simplex infection, ​the risk of transmission ​to the fetus will be maxi​mum during which stage of‍ the pregnancy?","raw_text":"In a pregnant woman with herpes simplex infection, the risk of transmission to the fetus will be maximum during which stage of the pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First t‌rimester","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Second t‌rimester","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Third t‌rimester","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lab‌our","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Labour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Labour</strong></p><p>The <strong>maximum </strong>risk of<strong> HSV transmission</strong> from mother to child is <strong>during labor. </strong>This is<strong> </strong>due to the shedding of the virus from an active infection in the genital tract during delivery.</p>\n<p>HSV is a <strong>sexually transmitted infection</strong>. There is a higher risk of transmission during primary infections than with recurrences. Reactivations are less severe.</p>\n<p><strong>Cesarean delivery</strong> reduces the risk of vertical transmission during labor and is indicated in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cases of <strong>active genital lesions</strong> - both primary and recurrent</li>\n<li><strong>Prodromal symptoms</strong> - like vulval itching or burning</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Suspicious lesions should be sent for PCR testing or culture. Antiviral treatment with <strong>acyclovir</strong> should be given if found positive.</p>\n<p>Women without active genital lesions, without symptoms, or those with active lesions in non-genital areas can undergo normal labor and delivery. </p>\n<p><strong>Management</strong> of HSV infection during pregnancy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary or recurrent infections during pregnancy - Oral <strong>acyclovir</strong> or <strong>valacyclovir</strong></li>\n<li>Severe or disseminated infection - IV acyclovir followed by oral acyclovir</li>\n<li>Pain relief - Oral <strong>analgesics</strong> or topical anesthetics</li>\n<li>Urinary retention - <strong>catheterization.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1593","text":"The risk of fetal varicell‌a syndrome is maximum whe​n the mother is infected ​between _____.","raw_text":"The risk of fetal varicella syndrome is maximum when the mother is infected between _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 to 1‌0 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"8 to 1‌2 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"13 to 2‌0 weeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"20 to 2‌8 weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 13 to 20 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a9ca7f26f1f745e6b367632ca66f2ddfx383x576.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 13 to 20 weeks</strong></p><p>The<strong> risk </strong>of developing<strong> fetal varicella syndrome</strong> (congenital varicella syndrome) is <strong>highest </strong>when the mother develops varicella infection between <strong>13 to 20 weeks</strong>. </p>\n<p>Congenital varicella syndrome occurs in <strong>2% of infants</strong> with maternal varicella <strong>between 13 and 20 weeks</strong> of gestation and in<strong> 0.4% of infants</strong> <strong>before 13 weeks</strong> of gestation. It is rare after 20 weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Congenital varicella syndrome</strong> is characterized by<strong> cicatricial skin scarring</strong> in a zoster-like pattern (zig-zag scarring), and <strong>limb hypoplasia </strong>(shown below). They can also present with <strong>neurological</strong> abnormalities (<strong>microcephaly, cortical atrophy, mental retardation</strong>), ophthalmic features (<strong>chorioretinitis, microphthalmia, cataract</strong>), and renal and autonomic system abnormalities (<strong>hydronephrosis, neurogenic bladder</strong>). <strong>Low birth weight</strong> is also common. </p>\n<p>Infants born to mothers with varicella in the period from<strong> 5 days prior to delivery up to 2 days after delivery</strong> are at high risk for <strong>severe neonatal varicella</strong>. They acquire the infection transplacentally.</p>\n<p>Since the mother has not developed a strong antibody response, the infant lacks the protective effect of maternal anti-varicella-zoster virus (VZV) antibodies.  Therefore <strong>varicella-zoster immune globulin</strong> (VZIG)<strong> </strong>should be administered as soon as possible after birth to <strong>newborns</strong> whose mothers have varicella<strong> 5 days before and up to 2 days after</strong> the delivery. </p>\n<p>Since perinatally acquired varicella can be potentially life-threatening, the infant should typically be treated with <strong>intravenous acyclovir</strong> (10 mg/kg every 8 hours) when <strong>lesions</strong> <strong>appear</strong>. </p>\n<p>For <strong>pregnant women</strong> with uncomplicated primary varicella or herpes zoster that does not require hospitalization, the recommended treatment is <strong>oral acyclovir</strong> (800 mg five times daily for seven days). </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1594","text":"A young woman delivered a ‌baby with limb hypoplasia​ and cicatricial skin les​ions. Which of the follow​ing infections did the mo‍ther most likely have dur‍ing her pregnancy?","raw_text":"A young woman delivered a baby with limb hypoplasia and cicatricial skin lesions. Which of the following infections did the mother most likely have during her pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rub‌ella","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cytomeg‌alovirus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chick‌en pox","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Toxopl‌asmosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chicken pox","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ad62ab53725c47c0a2012a385567d500x383x576.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chicken pox</strong></p><p>The given scenario is<strong> </strong>suggestive of <strong>congenital varicella syndrome</strong>. It is seen in babies born to mothers who had<strong> chickenpox infections</strong> during their pregnancy.</p>\n<p>Features of congenital varicella syndrome include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chorioretinitis</li>\n<li><strong>Limb hypoplasia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cicatricial skin lesions</strong></li>\n<li>Microphthalmia</li>\n<li>Cerebral cortical atrophy</li>\n<li>Growth restriction</li>\n<li>Hydronephrosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows limb hypoplasia in fetal varicella syndrome:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1595","text":"In a pregnant woman with s‌yphilis, the infection ca​n get transmitted to the ​fetus during which stage ​in pregnancy?","raw_text":"In a pregnant woman with syphilis, the infection can get transmitted to the fetus during which stage in pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"During ‌delivery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"28-32‌ weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Before ‌6 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10-15‌ weeks","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 10-15 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/058d0cc8a28d47aaaf8dd6abaa882e50x640x488.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 10-15 weeks</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 345\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>A pregnant woman with <strong>syphilis</strong> can <strong>transmit</strong> <em>T. pallidum</em> to the fetus at <strong>10–15 weeks of gestation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Features of congenital syphilis </strong>include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Hutchinson's triad - It is seen in <strong>late </strong>congenital syphilis.\n<ul>\n<li>Hutchinson’s teeth - small widely spaced, peg-shaped, notched upper incisors</li>\n<li id=\"__p90\">Interstitial keratitis</li>\n<li id=\"__p94\">Sensorineural hearing loss</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Mulberry molars</li>\n<li>Rhagades - deep fissuring of mucocutaneous junctions in the nares, lips, and anus</li>\n<li>Frontal bossing</li>\n<li id=\"__p86\">Saber shins - anterior tibial bowing</li>\n<li id=\"__p88\">Clutton’s joints - painless, symmetric hydrarthrosis of the knees and elbows</li>\n</ol>\n<p> The image below shows Hutchinson incisors.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: The risk of fetal infection during untreated early maternal syphilis is ~75–95%, decreasing to ~35% for maternal syphilis of &gt;2 years duration.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9748","text":"You are posted in the labo‌r room. While inspecting ​the placenta to rule out ​any abnormalities, you no​tice that it appears pale‍ and enlarged. Which of t‍he following infections d​oes the mother most likel​y have?","raw_text":"You are posted in the labor room. While inspecting the placenta to rule out any abnormalities, you notice that it appears pale and enlarged. Which of the following infections does the mother most likely have?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rub‌ella","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mea‌sles","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Syph‌illis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Toxopl‌asmosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Syphillis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Syphillis</strong></p><p><strong>Enlarged and pale placenta</strong> is due to infection by <strong>syphilis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other clinical manifestations and complications include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Spontaneous abortion</li>\n<li>Stillbirth</li>\n<li>Non-immune hydrops </li>\n<li>Intrauterine growth restriction </li>\n<li>Perinatal death.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9749","text":"A sexually active woman pr‌esents with amenorrhea fo​r the past 3 months and a​ history of a painless ge​nital ulcer. UPT is posit‍ive and routine antenatal‍ tests revealed a reactiv​e VDRL report. What is th​e dose of benzathine peni‌cillin that should be giv‌en for this patient?","raw_text":"A sexually active woman presents with amenorrhea for the past 3 months and a history of a painless genital ulcer. UPT is positive and routine antenatal tests revealed a reactive VDRL report. What is the dose of benzathine penicillin that should be given for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3–4 million units intraven‌ously","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2.4 million units intramus‌cularly as a single injec​tion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2.4 million units intramus‌cularly daily, plus probe​necid 500 mg orally for 1​4 days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2.4 million units intramus‌cularly weekly for three ​doses","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2.4 million units intramuscularly as a single injection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2.4 million units intramuscularly as a single injection</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>early syphilis</strong> in a pregnant woman.<strong> 2.4 million units</strong> of <strong>benzathine penicillin</strong> should be given <strong>intramuscularly </strong>as a single injection.</p>\n<p>Treatment of syphilis includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Early syphilis </strong>- Benzathine penicillin G, 2.4 million units intramuscularly as a <strong>single</strong> injection</li>\n<li><strong>Late syphilis </strong>(&gt;1 year duration) -<strong> </strong>Benzathine penicillin G, 2.4 million units intramuscularly weekly for <strong>three doses</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment of <strong>neurosyphilis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aqueous<strong> crystalline penicillin</strong> G, 3–4 million units intravenously every 4 hours for 10–14 days</li>\n<li>Procaine penicillin, 2.4 million units intramuscularly daily, plus probenecid 500 mg orally four times daily, both for 10–14 days.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9728","text":"A woman at 24 weeks of ges‌tation presented with com​plaints of fatigue, joint​ pain, and the following ​rash for 3 weeks. Urinaly‍sis showed proteinuria an‍d anti-Smith antibodies w​ere positive. Which of th​e following complications‌ is least likely in this ‌patient?","raw_text":"A woman at 24 weeks of gestation presented with complaints of fatigue, joint pain, and the following rash for 3 weeks. Urinalysis showed proteinuria and anti-Smith antibodies were positive. Which of the following complications is least likely in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thromboc‌ytopenia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ecla‌mpsia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post term‌ delivery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thromb‌ophilia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Post term delivery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a8ffa59dceff4d1c8e97decaea16686d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Post term delivery</strong></p><p>The above scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>SLE</strong>. <strong>Preterm labor </strong>is a complication in pregnant women with SLE, not post-term delivery.</p>\n<p>Complications in a pregnant patient with SLE include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Thrombophilia</strong></li>\n<li>Preterm labor</li>\n<li>Hypertension, <strong>eclampsia</strong>, and preeclampsia</li>\n<li>Anemia</li>\n<li><strong>Thrombocytopenia</strong></li>\n<li>Stroke</li>\n<li>Renal failure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Features of neonatal lupus include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cardiac abnormalities - Complete heart blocks </li>\n<li>Cutaneous lupus</li>\n<li>Hepatobiliary diseases - liver failure, transient conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, transient transaminase elevations</li>\n<li>Hematologic abnormalities - thrombocytopenia, neutropenia, or anemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9741","text":"A woman with HIV presents ‌for a routine antenatal c​heckup at 35 weeks of ges​tation. Investigations re​vealed a viral load of &gt;1‍000 copies/ml. What will ‍be the best mode of deliv​ery in this patient?","raw_text":"A woman with HIV presents for a routine antenatal checkup at 35 weeks of gestation. Investigations revealed a viral load of &gt;1000 copies/ml. What will be the best mode of delivery in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal vagi‌nal delivery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cesarean‌ delivery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Forceps assisted vaginal d‌elivery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vacuum assisted vaginal de‌livery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cesarean delivery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cesarean delivery</strong></p><p><strong>Cesarean delivery </strong>is the preferred mode of delivery in a woman with HIV viral load <strong>above 1000 copies/ml </strong>at 34-36 weeks of gestation.</p>\n<p>Cesarean delivery is usually scheduled at <strong>38 weeks</strong> of gestation, or two weeks before the due date.</p>\n<p>Pregnant women with HIV on ART throughout pregnancy, with a viral load <strong>&lt;1000 <span class=\"nowrap\">copies/mL</span></strong> evaluated within 4-6 weeks of delivery, may choose to have a <strong>vaginal delivery.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4478","text":"A woman is diagnosed to be‌ positive for HIV in her ​first trimester. What is ​the appropriate managemen​t?","raw_text":"A woman is diagnosed to be positive for HIV in her first trimester. What is the appropriate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Start ART immediately and ‌continue till 6 weeks aft​er delivery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Start ART after 1st trimes‌ter and continue life-lon​g","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Start ART immediately and ‌continue life-long","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Start ART after 1st trimes‌ter and continue till 6 w​eeks after delivery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Start ART immediately and continue life-long","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Start ART immediately and continue life-long</strong></p><p><strong>ART</strong> should be <strong>initiated immediately and continued lifelong</strong> in<strong> </strong>all <strong>pregnant </strong>and<strong> breastfeeding </strong>women with <strong>HIV. </strong>This is regardless of their WHO clinical stage and CD4 cell count.</p>\n<p>*Note: Pearl attached below has been updated according to the NACO ART guidelines 2021. Efavirenz has been replaced by <strong>Dolutegravir</strong> as per the new guidelines.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9751","text":"A woman with HIV on antire‌troviral therapy is in la​bor. Which of the followi​ng will be given to her n​ewborn baby prophylactica‍lly?","raw_text":"A woman with HIV on antiretroviral therapy is in labor. Which of the following will be given to her newborn baby prophylactically?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 weeks of‌ nevirapine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"6 weeks of zidovudine and ‌nevirapine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"12 weeks of zidovudine, ne‌virapine and lamivudine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"12 weeks of zidovudine and‌ lamivudine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 6 weeks of nevirapine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 6 weeks of nevirapine</strong></p><p>In India, according to the <strong>NACO</strong> guidelines, <strong>nevirapine</strong> is given in the syrup form for<strong> 6 weeks</strong> to an infant of a mother who is on<strong> long-term ART.</strong></p>\n<p>In infants born to mothers with HIV who are <strong>not on ART</strong>, syrup <strong>nevirapine</strong> should be continued for <strong>12 weeks</strong>. </p>\n<p>According to the<strong> NIH guidelines, zidovudine</strong> should be given to the neonate for <strong>4 weeks.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9727","text":"The risk of developing a s‌troke is highest during w​hat period of gestation i​n a pregnant woman?","raw_text":"The risk of developing a stroke is highest during what period of gestation in a pregnant woman?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1st tr‌imester","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2nd tr‌imester","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Postpart‌um period","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intrapart‌um period","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Postpartum period","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Postpartum period</strong></p><p>The <strong>highest incidence of stroke</strong> in pregnancy is 5 days to 2 weeks <strong>postpartum. </strong>This is followed by the intrapartum period.</p>\n<p>There is an increased risk of cerebrovascular thrombosis post-delivery due to a sudden reduction in the blood volume, a fall in the level of circulating antithrombotic factors, and venous stasis.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors of stroke in pregnancy</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertensive disorders</li>\n<li>Gestational diabetes</li>\n<li>Antepartum hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Cesarean delivery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1596","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding epilepsy ​in pregnancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding epilepsy in pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It increases risk of fetal‌ malformations by 5%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Seizure risk is more at th‌e time of delivery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Seizure frequency increase‌s in 30% of women","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ideal contraceptive in bre‌ast feeding mother with s​eizure disorder is DMPA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It increases risk of fetal malformations by 5%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It increases risk of fetal malformations by 5%</strong></p><p>Untreated epilepsy has no association with fetal malformations. <strong>Anti-epileptic medications</strong> such as valproate, phenytoin, etc., are responsible for <strong>fetal malformations</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Seizure frequency</strong> is unchanged in 50%, increased in 30%, and decreased in 20% women.</p>\n<p>Seizure <strong>risk increases during labor</strong> as pain and hyperventilation decrease the seizure threshold.</p>\n<p>The <strong>ideal contraceptive</strong> in a breastfeeding mother with a seizure disorder is <strong>depot medroxyprogesterone acetate</strong> (DMPA), as progestins increase the seizure threshold.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"JAdgIhjs","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"29_implants__splints_and_traction","title":"29.Implants, Splints And Traction","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MB6101","text":"While performing bone graf‌ting, which of the follow‍ing instruments would you‌ use to elevate the perio​steum?","raw_text":"While performing bone grafting, which of the following instruments would you use to elevate the periosteum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7529d1aa2f214acb843f250015797418.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A</strong></p><p>The instrument used in the elevation of the periosteum is a <strong>periosteal elevator</strong> shown in image A.</p>\n<p>It is used to elevate the periosteum, as nerves and vessels are generally on this plane, and once elevated there is a safer plane for surgery.</p>\n<p>Option B: The image shows a <strong>curette</strong> used for curetting a cavity in the bone or removing fibrous tissue from the ends of a fracture. It is used in the management of <strong>enchondroma, GCT, </strong>and<strong> osteomyelitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: The image shows an <strong>osteotome.</strong> It has both sides beveled and is used for <strong>cutting bone </strong>in surgeries such as McMurray's osteotomy and corrective osteotomy. </p>\n<p>Option D: It shows a <strong>bone gouge</strong>, a concave-bladed chisel used for cutting round bone surfaces or making holes in the bone.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6079","text":"The postoperative X-ray of‌ a patient who underwent ‍femoral nailing is shown ‌below. Which of the follo​wing statements is false ​regarding this nail?","raw_text":"The postoperative X-ray of a patient who underwent femoral nailing is shown below. Which of the following statements is false regarding this nail?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cloverleaf shape provides ‌tensile strength","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Proximal migration of the ‌nail may lead to bursitis‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Based on the concept of th‌ree point fixation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The eye of the nail is use‌ful during its removal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cloverleaf shape provides tensile strength","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/65cb787446304a20b29ee44062296eaf.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/009da84b252f4dfda667664d8268e492x1280x1855.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cloverleaf shape provides tensile strength</strong></p><p>The above radiograph shows a <strong>K-nail</strong> (Kuntscher nail), identified by the absence of interlocking screws. A<strong> cloverleaf shape</strong> is seen on cross-section. This is designed to give a good rotational stability. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>K-nails have been used for the internal fixation of femur fractures, and fixation relies on the frictional fit between the nail and bone. It is based on the concept of <strong>three-point fixation</strong> i.e., at proximal and distal ends of the bone and at the isthmus (the narrowest part of the medullary cavity).</p>\n<p>The <strong>eye </strong>at the end of the nail is useful for removing it after the fracture has healed.</p>\n<p>Some common complications of K-nailing include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The nail getting stuck</li>\n<li>Splintering of the cortex while hammering the nail</li>\n<li>Proximal migration of the nail, leading to bursitis over its protruding end</li>\n<li>Distal migration of the nail leading to stiffness of the knee</li>\n<li>Infection.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6031","text":"Identify the instrument sh‌own below.","raw_text":"Identify the instrument shown below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malleol‌ar screw","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cortica‌l screw","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cancello‌us screw","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Steinm‌ann pin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cancellous screw","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b0d953027a2e497696c5d9ce72095d62.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6ce83eebe4214e00944f9c08212f52dex387x480.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5dbc96165bd448799a256ee78130457ex387x480.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8d5760aab403460a8938cbb823c561c1x387x480.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cancellous screw</strong></p><p>The given instrument is a <strong>cancellous screw.</strong></p>\n<p>Cancellous screws have a <strong>larger thread </strong>and<strong> high pitch</strong>. Compression is produced by <strong>partial threading </strong>with a<strong> blunt tip.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>A <strong>malleolar</strong> screw is a <strong>modified cancellous</strong> screw with a <strong>sharp-pointed tip.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Cortical</strong> screws have a <strong>smaller thread </strong>and<strong> low pitch.</strong> Compression is produced by <strong>over-drilling </strong>the<strong> proximal cortex.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6082","text":"Which of the following ins‌truments can be used in b‍oth internal fixation of ‌small bones and Ilizarov'​s technique?","raw_text":"Which of the following instruments can be used in both internal fixation of small bones and Ilizarov's technique?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Steinma‌nn's pin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kirschne‌r's wire","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Denha‌m pin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Knowl‌es pin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Kirschner's wire","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dec96cac5cb446b785df992f3326083dx259x194.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Kirschner&#x27;s wire</strong></p><p>Of the following, <strong>Kirschner's wire</strong> can be used in both the internal fixation of small bones and Ilizarov's technique.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 515.125px; height: 268px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 75px;\">Features</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201px;\"><strong>Steinmann's Pin</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.125px;\"><strong>Kirschner's wire (K-wire) </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 75px;\">Diameter</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201px;\">&gt;1.5 mm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.125px;\">0.9 - 1.5 mm  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 75px;\">Length</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201px;\">Short</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.125px;\">Various sizes  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 75px;\">Ends</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201px;\">One end sharp and the other blunt</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.125px;\">Both ends are sharp  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 75px;\">Uses</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201px;\">\n<p>Internal fixation of fractures of long bones</p>\n<p>Used along with Bohler's stirrup for skeletal traction</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214.125px;\">Internal fixation of small bones\n<p>Fixation of fractures in children</p>\n<p>Ilizarov's ring fixation system</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Denham</strong> pin is used for giving skeletal traction in osteoporotic bone. It has <strong>threads </strong>at the center of the pin, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Knowles</strong> pin may be used for impacted fractures, percutaneously for medically unfit persons, and for fractures in children.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6081","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about the procedure‍ shown below?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the procedure shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Compression/distraction at‌ fracture site enhances o‍steogenesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The rate of compression or‌ distraction is 2 mm per ‍day","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"K wire is required for thi‌s procedure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nerve palsy is a complicat‌ion of the procedure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The rate of compression or distraction is 2 mm per day","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4fd8890741dd439fb567807eefadd238.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/940ac4dfd2ea4c8185d52637e370dfb1x500x599.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b58962e29b4e4a939e77b174d87996c2x1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The rate of compression or distraction is 2 mm per day</strong></p><p>The above radiograph depicts the lengthening of the limb using <strong>Ilizarov apparatus</strong>. The rate of compression/distraction is <strong>1 mm per day,</strong> not 2 mm per day.</p>\n<p>Ilizarov's technique is based on the principle of <strong>distraction osteogenesis.</strong> This is the mechanical induction of new bone which occurs between bony surfaces that are gradually pulled apart. Compression and distraction at the fracture site enhance osteogenesis.</p>\n<p>A careful fracture of the bone is performed, followed by a short wait. The young callus formed is gradually distracted using a circular or unilateral external fixator system called a <strong>ring fixator</strong>. Transfixing thin <strong>Kirschner wires</strong> (K-wires) are the key components of the Ilizarov fixator regarding its axial stiffness. This affects the mechanobiological environment in which the bone heals.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>Distraction or compression can be applied by twisting nuts on the fixation system at the fracture or corticotomy site. Distraction or compression is carried out at the rate of <strong>1 mm per day</strong>. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The uses of Ilizarov's apparatus are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Limb lengthening</li>\n<li>Non-union</li>\n<li>Deformity correction</li>\n<li>Osteomyelitis - as it offers the possibility of liberal excision of bone</li>\n<li>Arthrodesis - crushing articular surfaces can stimulate union between opposing bones</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Disadvantages of this technique include pin tract infection, <strong>nerve palsy</strong>, and joint stiffness.</p>\n<p>The below images show the Ilizarov apparatus and an illustration of distraction osteogenesis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6084","text":"Following a road traffic a‌ccident, a patient with a‍n intertrochanteric fract‌ure of the femur was trea​ted with the implant show​n below. Identify the imp​lant.","raw_text":"Following a road traffic accident, a patient with an intertrochanteric fracture of the femur was treated with the implant shown below. Identify the implant.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proximal f‌emoral nail","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dynamic ‌hip screw","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Austin Moor‌e prosthesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herber‌t screw","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dynamic hip screw","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b48edca7700a4a528b09ecc072dc2bbd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/14dc490185104b7c9ed76a91f3fead82x720x951.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/52641def432c4c9e9c79c10965f3cea3x720x975.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cb756b07ed5e4b64ad4b932927e5bc47x426x354.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e40e8089ef31494e96ca7a7472ece08bx1280x2056.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/134d1072e56f467aa8805b0109ec0560x800x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d0fe0973e40947a38b3ffe66e259013ex720x561.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b7e2bf315c3147f3a79d03f2d5faf129x1280x1223.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dynamic hip screw</strong></p><p>The above radiograph shows an extramedullary implant called a <strong>dynamic hip screw</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Intertrochanteric fractures </strong>of the femur can be treated by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Extramedullary implants - <strong>dynamic hip screw</strong></li>\n<li>Intramedullary implants - <strong>proximal femoral nail</strong> and gamma nail.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Dynamic hip screws </strong>are used in <strong>undisplaced</strong> intertrochanteric fractures, whereas <strong>proximal femoral nails</strong><strong> </strong>are used in <strong>displaced </strong>intertrochanteric fractures.</p>\n<p>The below image shows a dynamic hip screw.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Advantages of using a proximal femoral nail over a dynamic hip screw include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Smaller incisions</li>\n<li>Reduced blood loss</li>\n<li>Decreased femoral neck shortening</li>\n<li>Decreased risk of infection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below image shows a proximal femoral nail with locking and stabilization screws.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Austin Moore's</strong> implant is an older implant used in <strong>unipolar hemiarthroplasty.</strong> It can be identified by the fenestrations on the shaft of the prosthesis, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"><strong>Herbert</strong> screw is cannulated and threaded at both ends. It is designed for use in fractures of small articular bones such as <strong>carpals</strong> and<strong> scaphoid</strong>. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6110","text":"Identify the instrument gi‌ven below.","raw_text":"Identify the instrument given below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bone n‌ibbler","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bone c‌urette","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plate hold‌ing forceps","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bone holdi‌ng forceps","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bone nibbler","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7b709bdccde543338328899cc723a3a0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bone nibbler</strong></p><p>The instrument given is a <strong>bone nibbler</strong>, which is designed to remove or shape small sections of bone by gently nibbling or biting away at the bone tissue. </p>\n<p>It is available in various sizes and with different angles of the nose:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Straight nibbler – for general use.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Curved nibbler – for spinal surgery.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Double-action nibbler – straight or curved. The double-action reduces the force required for cutting the bone and is mechanically superior.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0238","text":"Which of the following is ‌used to stabilise a fract‍ure of the distal femur a‌s shown in the image belo​w?","raw_text":"Which of the following is used to stabilise a fracture of the distal femur as shown in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rail f‌ixator","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ilizarov‌ fixator","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"K wire ‌binding","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interloc‌king nail","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rail fixator","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a11a3156665c4d88885b17006f428708.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b61c76263d3b4ef78def0202dcc092f2x1280x1534.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/53df3b4dd2914fe98bf161166e8030c4x512x353.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/864187fbd2de452bab33cdd5cb66e772x750x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rail fixator</strong></p><p>The above image shows a <strong>rail fixator</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this type of external fixator, multiple pins are inserted into the bone. These pins are then <strong>attached</strong> to different <strong>blocks</strong> which can move over a <strong>track</strong> or <strong>rail.</strong></p>\n<p>Given below is an <strong>Ilizarov ring fixator</strong>. Here, pins are attached to an adjustable ring system.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Tension band wiring</strong> is used in the fixation of transverse <strong>patellar</strong> <strong>fractures</strong>. In this method, two K-wires are used to transfix the reduced patellar fragments and a flexible wire is looped around them tightly.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following X-ray image shows tibial <strong>intramedullary interlocking nailing.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0240","text":"The following instrument i‌s used for:","raw_text":"The following instrument is used for:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nibbling the ends of the b‌ones","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Holding the bone and plate‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Controlled traction of fra‌cture segments","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cutting t‌he K- wire","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Holding the bone and plate","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/831e1bf41ae44a63a71cb16da0d1f701.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Holding the bone and plate</strong></p><p>The image shown is of <strong>bone holding forceps </strong>(Verbrugge forceps). It is used to <strong>hold the bone </strong>and<strong> plate.</strong></p>\n<p>It consists of a ratcheted clamp with a curved distal end.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD8317","text":"A 35-year-old man was rush‌ed to the ER following a ‍road traffic accident. Yo‌u suspect a femoral shaft​ fracture and decide to u​se the following splint. ​How is the length of this‌ splint calculated?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man was rushed to the ER following a road traffic accident. You suspect a femoral shaft fracture and decide to use the following splint. How is the length of this splint calculated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Highest point on the media‌l side of the groin up to‍ the heel plus 4 inches.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Highest point on the media‌l side of the groin up to‍ the heel plus 6 inches.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior superior iliac sp‌ine up to the heel plus 6‍ inches.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior superior iliac sp‌ine up to the heel plus 4‍ inches.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Highest point on the medial side of the groin up to the heel plus 6 inches.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1aeadc862664483496c3ad4d147f6833.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f6f5743e174543c69329e34f4695d2b4x302x128.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Highest point on the medial side of the groin up to the heel plus 6 inches.</strong></p><p>The image shows the picture of a <strong>Thomas splint</strong> and its <strong>length</strong> is calculated by measuring the distance between the <strong>highest point </strong>on the<strong> medial </strong>side of the <strong>groin</strong> <strong>to the</strong> <strong>heel </strong>and adding<strong> 6 inches.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>ring size</strong> is calculated by adding 2 inches to the thigh circumference at the highest point of the groin.</p>\n<p>The Thomas splint is used for immobilization following hip and thigh injuries.</p>\n<p>Parts of the splint include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Outer bar</li>\n<li>Inner bar</li>\n<li>Ring</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The outer bar has a <strong>curvature</strong> near its junction with the ring to accommodate the<strong> greater trochanter</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6141","text":"The splint shown below is ‌used in the management of‍:","raw_text":"The splint shown below is used in the management of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shaft of hum‌erus fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brachial pl‌exus injury","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Luxatio‌ erecta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coracoclavicular ligament ‌tear","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Brachial plexus injury","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a141297ba41d44caaf071bda937b7934.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/68cb1ecc811b4678807e05582e234611x1280x1723.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4eea667655104d639d44afe4d65f375ax720x991.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Brachial plexus injury</strong></p><p>The above image shows the <strong>aeroplane splint,</strong> which is used in <strong>brachial plexus injury</strong>. It maintains the axilla at <strong>90°</strong>.</p>\n<p>A<strong> hanging cast </strong>is used to treat a <strong>shaft of humerus</strong> fracture. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Inferior dislocation of the shoulder is also known as luxatio erecta, as shown below. It is managed by closed reduction and post-reduction immobilization using a sling.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6172","text":"Which of the following can‌ be treated using the tra‍ction shown below?","raw_text":"Which of the following can be treated using the traction shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fracture sh‌aft of tibia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Developmental dysplasia of‌ the hip","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fracture sh‌aft of femur","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pelvic ‌fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fracture shaft of femur","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7f834062dfde480b8dd717cef49580ee.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c4702a99d2324b068663a6360932dfcax1280x1542.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fracture shaft of femur</strong></p><p>The image shows Gallow’s traction which is used to treat <strong>fracture of shaft femur</strong> in children less than 2 years old and less than 12 kg in weight.</p>\n<p>Here, skin traction is applied to both legs which are then suspended from a frame, providing just enough traction to keep the buttocks of the child just off the bed.</p>\n<p>Older children are treated with <strong>Russell’s traction or Thomas splint</strong>. After 2–4 weeks, when the fracture has sufficiently healed, it is replaced by a <strong>hip spica</strong><strong>.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0242","text":"Identify the following dev‌ice.","raw_text":"Identify the following device.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cock-up‌ splint","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Colles‌' cast","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Knuckle be‌nder splint","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dynamic fi‌nger splint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cock-up splint","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bf1a4c5a752e4ac8a43bf9325c3914be.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b74d7887f7d44865a41f06b2a26122e0x1280x754.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/92e12acd218442248ab27e1db7ef5eb4x355x321.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bafb59f5e7fd4852b6f45dec3e171576x1280x1395.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cock-up splint</strong></p><p>The device shown in the image is a <strong>cock-up splint</strong>. It is used to manage <strong>radial nerve palsy</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Radial nerve palsy manifests as an inability to dorsiflex the wrist and digits (<strong>wrist drop</strong> and<strong> finger drop</strong>). Numbness occurs on the dorsoradial aspect of the hand and the dorsal aspect of the radial 3 <sup>1/2</sup> digits. The cock-up splint is designed to counteract the wrist drop position by holding the wrist in a slightly extended or neutral position, which helps prevent further contracture and allows for functional use of the hand.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: Undisplaced Colles' fracture is managed by immobilization in a below-elbow plaster cast for six weeks. Displaced fractures are managed by manipulative reduction followed by immobilization in <strong>Colles' cast</strong> in palmar flexion, pronation, and ulnar deviation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Knuckle bender splint</strong> is used in cases of ulnar claw hand where ulnar nerve recovery is anticipated. The splint can be used to correct and prevent metacarpophalangeal hyperextension by maintaining flexion as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Dynamic finger splint </strong>can be used for:</p>\n<ul class=\"i8Z77e\">\n<li class=\"TrT0Xe\">Immobilization in case of fracture of the proximal phalanx </li>\n<li class=\"TrT0Xe\">Extensor tendon injuries of the finger</li>\n<li class=\"TrT0Xe\">Prevention of deformities in rheumatoid arthritis.</li>\n<li class=\"TrT0Xe\">Post-operative immobilization.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4351","text":"Identify the incorrectly m‌atched pair of traction a‍nd its indication.","raw_text":"Identify the incorrectly matched pair of  traction and its indication.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perkin's traction - Cervic‌al spine injury","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Russell’s traction - Troch‌antric fractures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Halo-pelvic traction - Sco‌liosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dunlop's traction - Suprac‌ondylar fracture of humer‍us","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Perkin's traction - Cervical spine injury","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a23b6ce9da9546f4b8b43804b7d274b0x600x743.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Perkin&#x27;s traction - Cervical spine injury</strong></p><p><strong>Perkin's</strong> traction is used to treat fractures of the <strong>shaft of femur</strong> in adults, not cervical spine injuries.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"pcoKZOmX","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"29_disorders_of_the_nervous_system","title":"29.Disorders Of The Nervous System","num_questions":32,"total_marks":128,"duration":42,"time_per_question":78.75,"questions":[{"id":"MA6594","text":"A 20-year-old primigravida‌ was rushed to the OT for‌ an emergency caesarean s​ection. Her pregnancy was‌ unplanned and she did no‌t take folic acid supplem​entation prior to concept​ion. Moreover, owing to t​he pandemic led lockdown ​and her remote residence,‌ she could not adhere to ‌regular antenatal care. T‌he newborn was born with ‌a neural tube defect and ​immediately shifted to th‍e NICU. What is the least​ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old primigravida was rushed to the OT for an emergency caesarean section. Her pregnancy was unplanned and she did not take folic acid supplementation prior to conception. Moreover, owing to the pandemic led lockdown and her remote residence, she could not adhere to regular antenatal care. The newborn was born with a neural tube defect and immediately shifted to the NICU. What is the least likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myelomen‌ingocele","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anenc‌ephaly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diastema‌tomyelia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Holopros‌encephaly","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Holoprosencephaly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bad74759dc9a4a0bbcfd39fd0b72e5a8x960x720.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Holoprosencephaly</strong></p><p><strong>Holoprosencephaly</strong> is not a neural tube defect (NTD) and hence, it is the least likely diagnosis. <strong>Folic acid deficiency, </strong>especially in a young primigravida, is a <strong>risk factor</strong> for the development of NTDs in children, as seen in the given clinical vignette.</p>\n<p>NTDs result from the <strong>failure of the neural tube to close</strong> spontaneously between the 3rd and 4th week of in utero development. These can be detected in antenatal ultrasound scans and subsequently managed for a better prognosis. The major NTDs include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Spina bifida occulta</strong></li>\n<li>Meningocele</li>\n<li><strong>Myelomeningocele </strong></li>\n<li>Encephalocele</li>\n<li><strong>Anencephaly </strong></li>\n<li>Syringomyelia</li>\n<li>Diastematomyelia (split spinal cord)</li>\n<li>Iniencephaly</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In <strong>holoprosencephaly</strong>, the <strong>forebrain fails to develop</strong> into two hemispheres due to mutations in the <strong>sonic hedgehog gene </strong>at the long arm of <strong>chromosome 7</strong>. It is a developmental disorder of the brain that results from the defective formation of the<strong> prosencephalon</strong> and the inadequate induction of the forebrain structures. The three types include, alobar, semilobar, and lobar, apart from the middle interhemispheric fusion variant or syntelencephaly.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows alobar holoprosencephaly-</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1962","text":"A newborn presented with a‌ tuft of hair over the lu‌mbosacral region. What is​ the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A newborn presented with a tuft of hair over the lumbosacral region. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spina bifi‌da occulta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spinal‌ lipoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dermal‌ sinus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Any of t‌he above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Any of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2861854e307542c4956cda6c41c65c98x1280x1058.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a0894b20fa2743a3bb916cd7c3c3bdc6x1280x2049.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Any of the above</strong></p><p>A <strong>tuft of hair</strong> over the <strong>lumbosacral</strong> region in a <strong>newborn</strong> is suggestive of <strong>occult</strong> <strong>spinal</strong> <strong>dysraphism</strong> or <strong>neural tube defect</strong>. It comprises a range of conditions, including <strong>spina bifida occulta, intradural lipoma, </strong>and<strong> dorsal dermal sinus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Spinal dysraphism is due to the <strong>defective fusion</strong> of the <strong>midline embryonic neural, vertebral, </strong>and<strong> mesenchymal structures</strong>. Associated clinical features are <strong>high-arched feet</strong>, muscle <strong>size</strong> and <strong>strength differences</strong> between <strong>legs,</strong> and abnormal walking patterns. In newborns and young infants, the neurological examination is usually normal, but older children may experience a lack of sensation in the perineal area and back pain.</p>\n<p>Most of the patients will have a <strong>cutaneous abnormality overlying</strong> the lower spine, including a small dimple, a <strong>tuft of hair</strong>, or a subcutaneous lipoma.</p>\n<p>The image shows a tuft of hair in a case of spinal dysraphism:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Spinal dysraphism also includes meningocele, myelomeningocele, lipomeningocele, diastematomyelia (split spinal cord), thickened filum terminale, and cauda equina tumour.</p>\n<p>The initial investigation is usually <strong>ultrasound</strong> in the neonate and the best investigation is <strong>MRI</strong>.</p>\n<p>Spina bifida is the <strong>most</strong> <strong>common</strong> type of spinal dysraphism, in which the spinal column does not close completely, leaving the spinal cord and its protective covering exposed.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Myelomeningocele</strong> is the <strong>most</strong> <strong>severe</strong> form of spina bifida, where the spinal cord and nerve roots are exposed and may be damaged. A sac-like protrusion, containing spinal cord tissue, meninges, and cerebrospinal fluid, is visible outside the body, often leading to significant neurological impairments and disabilities.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6595","text":"A 27-year-old pregnant wom‌an underwent second-trime‌ster screening and had an​ abnormal presence of a b‌iochemical marker. Follow‌ing this, she went to a t​ertiary care referral cen​tre for further investiga​tions. An anomaly scan re​vealed cranial lemon and ‌cerebellar banana signs. ‌What is the abnormal mark‌er present in this condit‌ion?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old pregnant woman underwent second-trimester screening and had an abnormal presence of a biochemical marker. Following this, she went to a tertiary care referral centre for further investigations. An anomaly scan revealed cranial lemon and cerebellar banana signs. What is the abnormal marker present in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pregnancy-associated plasm‌a protein-A (PAPPA)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Human chorionic gonadotrop‌hin (hCG)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhi‌bin A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acetylchol‌inesterase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acetylcholinesterase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d74d3649c3c64bcf8a58b42c49207de0x510x418.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0def307a758c4f3bb2af170f379aa6b2x510x585.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acetylcholinesterase</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, the presence of abnormal sonographic markers, like the lemon and banana sign, is highly suggestive of <strong>neural tube defects</strong> (NTDs). <strong>Acetylcholinesterase</strong> is used as a biochemical marker of neural tube defects during the screening tests. </p>\n<p>NTDs account for the largest proportion of congenital anomalies of the CNS. The failure of closure of neural tube allows excretion of fetal substances such as<strong> α-fetoprotein (AFP) and acetylcholinesterase</strong> into the amniotic fluid thereby serving as biochemical markers for NTDs. </p>\n<p>The <strong>lemon and banana sign</strong>, noted on antenatal imaging, are one of the many notable fruits' inspired signs. The <strong>banana sign</strong> refers to the shape of the cerebellum owing to caudal displacement while the <strong>lemon sign</strong> refers to the lemon-shaped head resulting from scalloping of the frontal bones. It is seen in fetuses with open neural tube defects. </p>\n<p>The image below shows the characteristic <strong>lemon sign</strong> on ultrasound-</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows the characteristic<strong> banana sign</strong> on ultrasound-</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong></p>\n<p>Pregnancy-associated plasma protein-A (PAPPA), inhibin A, and human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) are the biochemical markers of<strong> trisomy 21</strong> (Down's syndrome).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1966","text":"A child is scheduled for m‌yelomeningocele surgery. ‌Until the patient is wait​ing for surgery, the cove‌ring of the sac will be p‌rotected by a gauze soake​d in______.","raw_text":"A child is scheduled for myelomeningocele surgery. Until the patient is waiting for surgery, the covering of the sac will be protected by a gauze soaked in______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Povidon‌e iodine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Normal‌ saline","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Methyle‌ne blue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlorh‌exidine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Normal saline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Normal saline</strong></p><p>Until the time of surgery, the sac of <strong>myelomeningocele</strong> has to be covered with a sterile gauze soaked in <strong>normal saline. </strong>This is to prevent drying and trauma to the sac. As the normal saline is less irritant to the sac, it is preferred.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong></p>\n<p>Surgery is done as early as possible (within a day of birth).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1970","text":"A 28-year-old pregnant wom‌an was rushed to the casu‌alty of a maternity hospi​tal due to imminent deliv‌ery. It resulted in a sti‌llborn baby as shown in t​he picture below. What is​ the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old pregnant woman was rushed to the casualty of a maternity hospital due to imminent delivery. It resulted in a stillborn baby as shown in the picture below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diastema‌tomyelia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Craniorac‌hischisis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inienc‌ephaly","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Holopros‌encephaly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Iniencephaly","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/204bf92749c545009229b54d427881bd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Iniencephaly</strong></p><p>The given image shows an occipital bone defect with retroflexion of the neck and trunk, which is typical of<strong> iniencephaly</strong>. Inion is the tip of the external occipital protuberance and this defect comes under the spectrum of <strong>neural tube defects</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Diastematomyelia </strong>(Option A)<strong> </strong> is a congenital disorder in which a part of the spinal cord is split, usually at the level of the upper lumbar vertebra.</p>\n<p><strong>Craniorachischisis</strong>  (Option B) is the anencephaly associated with a continuous bony defect of the spine and exposure of neural tissue.</p>\n<p><strong>Holoprosencephaly</strong> (Option D) occurs when the procencephalon fails to <span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">develop into two hemispheres.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1968","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of ventriculomegaly i‌n newborns?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of ventriculomegaly in newborns?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arnold-Chiari malformation‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dandy-Walk‌er syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arachnoid villi malformati‌on","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aqueducta‌l stenosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Aqueductal stenosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0f23cd9862fb4267a4c70cdfbdf1607dx1280x3447.057163020466.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Aqueductal stenosis</strong></p><p><strong>Aqueductal</strong> <strong>stenosis</strong> is the most <strong>common</strong> cause of <strong>ventriculomegaly</strong> and <strong>hydrocephalus</strong> in newborns.</p>\n<p>Ventriculomegaly is the <strong>dilation</strong> of the ventricles. When ventriculomegaly occurs in association with <strong>raised </strong>cerebrospinal fluid<strong> (CSF) pressure</strong>, it is called <strong>hydrocephalus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pathologic etiologies can be <strong>congenital</strong> or <strong>acquired</strong>. Congenital causes include structural abnormalities such as <strong>aqueductal stenosis,</strong> <strong>agenesis of the corpus callosum, Dandy-Walker malformations, Chiari malformations</strong>, and neural tube defects. Acquired causes are intracranial <strong>infections</strong>(cytomegalovirus, toxoplasmosis), <strong>intracranial</strong> <strong>hemorrhage</strong>, ischemic injury, and brain parenchymal <strong>tumors</strong>.</p>\n<p>Aqueductal stenosis is the narrowing or obstruction of the cerebral aqueduct, a narrow channel that connects the <strong>third</strong> and <strong>fourth</strong> <strong>ventricles</strong> in the brain. As a result, the fluid accumulates within the ventricles, causing them to enlarge.</p>\n<p>Treatment for aqueductal stenosis includes <strong>ventriculostomy</strong>, <strong>diuretics</strong>, serial lumbar punctures in specific cases, and CSF diversion. In <strong>severe</strong> cases, CSF <strong>diversion</strong> procedures (shunting), may be necessary to redirect the flow of CSF and relieve pressure on the brain.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6597","text":"An infant is brought to th‌e OPD with complaints of ‌multiple episodes of vomi​ting that is more in the ‌morning and relieved when‌ sitting. She has an unus​ually large head with bul​ging fontanelles. The ima​ge of the child showing a​ characteristic sign is g‌iven below. Which of the ‌following is not a cause ‌of the communicating vari‌ant of this condition?","raw_text":"An infant is brought to the OPD with complaints of multiple episodes of vomiting that is more in the morning and relieved when sitting. She has an unusually large head with bulging fontanelles. The image of the child showing a characteristic sign is given below. Which of the following is not a cause of the communicating variant of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Choroid plex‌us papilloma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meningeal ‌malignancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vein of Galen malformation‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Achondr‌oplasia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vein of Galen malformation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/24077720701e44de80557931fe967ffe.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vein of Galen malformation</strong></p><p>The clinical features of frequent vomiting, large head, bulging fontanelles, and a <strong>positive sunset sign </strong>(as seen in the image) point to a likely diagnosis of<strong> hydrocephalus</strong>. A <strong>vein of Galen malformation</strong> can expand to become large and, because of its midline position, obstruct the flow of CSF, thereby causing an <strong>obstructive</strong> or <strong>non-communicating </strong>hydrocephalus. Vomiting is more in the morning and becomes better when sitting due to poor absorption of CSF in a lying down position.  </p>\n<p>Hydrocephalus is not a specific disease; it represents a diverse group of conditions that result from impaired circulation or absorption of CSF. </p>\n<p><strong>Causes of hydrocephalus:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Communicating</strong> (external/non-obstructive)</td>\n<td><strong>Non-communicating</strong> (internal/obstructive)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Achondroplasia (raised ICP due to jugular foramen stenosis)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Choroid plexus papilloma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Meningeal malignancy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Meningitis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Posthemorrhagic</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sarcoidosis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Aqueductal stenosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Arnold-Chiari malformation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Dandy–Walker malformation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Klippel–Feil syndrome</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tumours and neurocutaneous disorders (astrocytoma)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Vein of Galen malformation</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6598","text":"Shreya, a 2-year-old girl,‌ presented with irritabil‌ity for 2 days. On examin​ation, macrocephaly, hypo‌tonia and spasticity were‌ present with a positive ​head transillumination te​st. She started walking i​ndependently at 18 months​ and became dry by day a ‌month before her 2nd birt‌hday. An MRI-brain reveal‌ed cystic expansion of th‌e fourth ventricle and mi​dline cerebellar hypoplas‍ia. What is the most like​ly diagnosis explaining h​er obstructive hydrocepha‌lus?","raw_text":"Shreya, a 2-year-old girl, presented with irritability for 2 days. On examination, macrocephaly, hypotonia and spasticity were present with a positive head transillumination test. She started walking independently at 18 months and became dry by day a month before her 2nd birthday. An MRI-brain revealed cystic expansion of the fourth ventricle and midline cerebellar hypoplasia. What is the most likely diagnosis explaining her obstructive hydrocephalus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arnold-Chiari malformation‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aqueducta‌l stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arachnoid villi malformati‌on","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dandy-Walk‌er syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dandy-Walker syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bff7fa75ef354e2a893401a3c520551ex578x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1a0626d17c464910a94d4de750770e18x1280x3918.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dandy-Walker syndrome</strong></p><p>The features of hydrocephalus like irritability along with macrocephaly, hypotonia and spasticity indicate a likely diagnosis of <strong>Dandy-Walker syndrome.</strong> A <strong>positive transillumination test</strong> is present in this syndrome. The child has <strong>developmental delay</strong> especially in motor and cognitive milestones because normally children walk independently at 12-13 months and become dry by day around 18 months.</p>\n<p>The Dandy-Walker malformation consists of a<strong> cystic expansion of the fourth ventricle</strong> in the posterior fossa and <strong>midline cerebellar hypoplasia. </strong>It results from a developmental failure of the <strong>roof of the fourth ventricle</strong> during embryogenesis. Most of the affected patients have hydrocephalus, agenesis of the posterior cerebellar vermis, and corpus callosum. Brisk tendon reflexes, spasticity, clonus, Babinski sign, cerebellar ataxia, and delayed motor and cognitive milestones are seen in this. Management is by <strong>shunting </strong>if hydrocephalus is present.<br/><br/>The image below shows a sagittal section of the magnetic resonance tomography scan of a patient with Dandy-Walker syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6599","text":"A 6-month-old baby present‌ed with fever, poor feedi‌ng, and frequent vomiting​. On examination, the hea‌d circumference was 46.5c‌m with a positive sunset ​and Macewen sign. Which o​f the following infection​s is associated with the ​most common cause of the ‌underlying neurological d‌isease in this child?","raw_text":"A 6-month-old baby presented with fever, poor feeding, and frequent vomiting. On examination, the head circumference was 46.5cm with a positive sunset and Macewen sign. Which of the following infections is associated with the most common cause of the underlying neurological disease in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rub‌ella","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mu‌mps","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bacterial e‌ndocarditis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mea‌sles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mumps","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mumps</strong></p><p>Symptoms of poor feeding and frequent vomiting with signs of enlarged head circumference and a <strong>positive sunset and Macewen sign(crack-pot resonance)</strong> points toward a diagnosis of <strong>h</strong><strong>ydrocephalus</strong>. <strong>Aqueductal stenosis</strong> is the <strong>most common cause</strong> of hydrocephalus. Out of the above options,<strong> mumps</strong> infection can cause stenosis of the cerebral aqueduct. </p>\n<p>Mumps is associated in a child with aqueductal stenosis because-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mumps virus is <strong>neurotropic</strong> and is thought to enter the CNS via the choroid plexus and infect the choroidal epithelium and ependymal cells.</li>\n<li><strong>CNS complications </strong>of mumps include meningitis, meningoencephalitis (most commonly manifests 5 days after the parotitis), transverse myelitis, aqueductal stenosis, and facial palsy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6600","text":"A 9-month-old baby present‌ed with seizures, develop‌mental delay and failure ​to thrive. Following an M‌RI, a diagnosis of a neur‌onal migration disorder w​as made. What is the prob​able diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 9-month-old baby presented with seizures, developmental delay and failure to thrive. Following an MRI, a diagnosis of a neuronal migration disorder was made. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Schizen‌cephaly","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Myelomen‌ingocele","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lhermitte–Du‌clos disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plagio‌cephaly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Schizencephaly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9044eed955ea423ca59becaf009b7cbax524x599.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/caceca2b617f4eb9961902747099b11bx521x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Schizencephaly</strong></p><p>Among the given options, <strong>schizencephaly</strong> is a neuronal migration defect. <strong>MRI</strong> is the study of choice for elucidating schizencephaly and associated malformations.</p>\n<p>Neuronal migration is the process during brain development, where the neuronal cells migrate to their proper designated site by various mechanisms, to form the 6-layered cerebral cortex. Any abnormality in the above process leads to <strong>neuronal migration defects</strong>.</p>\n<p>Neuronal migration defect includes the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Schizencephaly:</strong> (schiz = split or cleavage) It is the presence of unilateral or bilateral <strong>c</strong><strong>lefts within the cerebral hemispheres. </strong>The patient can be severely intellectually challenged, with microcephaly, seizures, and spastic quadriparesis.<br/><br/>The image below shows schizencephaly:</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lissencephaly </strong>(agyria): It is the absence of cerebral convolutions. Rather than the normal 6 layers of the cortex, there may be fewer layers. The child will have developmental delay, microcephaly, and seizures.<br/><br/>The image below shows lissencephaly:</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Myelomeningocele</strong> (Option B) is a neural tube defect that occurs due to defective closure of the neural tube.</p>\n<p><strong>Lhermitte–Duclos disease  </strong>(Option C) is the cerebellar dysplasia leading to focal enlargement of cerebellum and macrocephaly, cerebellar signs, and seizures.</p>\n<p><strong>Plagiocephaly </strong> (Option D) is the asymmetry and flattening of the skull of the child, due to extrinsic moulding forces (consistent sleeping on the same side of the head).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6601","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding the given‌ image?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding the given image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It represents melanocytic ‌hamartoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It causes no visual distur‌bance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is associated with faci‌al angiofibroma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"They are diagnostic of a n‌eurocutaneous syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is associated with facial angiofibroma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e7be1fe8162f48c0a5ddd74b88e256ac.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/101e2fe93f734db4abe514c4644bc904x1280x840.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is associated with facial angiofibroma</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>discrete tan-colored</strong> nodules over the<strong> iris</strong> called <strong>Lisch</strong> <strong>nodules</strong> (pigmented melanocytic hamartomas). They are seen in <strong>neurofibromatosis-1 (NF-1)</strong>. <strong>Facial angiofibroma</strong> (adenoma sebaceum) is a feature of <strong>tuberous</strong> <strong>sclerosis</strong> <strong>(TS)</strong> and not neurofibromatosis.</p>\n<p>The image below shows facial angiofibroma seen in tuberous sclerosis (<strong>Bourneville disease</strong>).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Lisch nodules are specifically associated with <strong>NF-1</strong> and are usually <strong>multiple</strong> and <strong>bilateral</strong>. They are best visualized with a <strong>slit lamp examination</strong> and <strong>don’t cause any visual disturbances</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other important ocular manifestations in NF-1 include <strong>eyelid plexiform neurofibroma</strong>, which causes <strong>S</strong>-shaped deformity of the upper lid, with a 'bag of worms' like texture, <strong>optic nerve glioma,</strong> and spheno-orbital encephalocele due to <strong>dysplasia</strong> of greater wing of the <strong>sphenoid.</strong></p>\n<p>NF-1 (<strong>von Recklinghausen's disease</strong>) is an <strong>autosomal</strong> <strong>dominant</strong> disorder caused by a loss-of-function mutation of the NF-1 gene located on chromosome <strong>17q11.2</strong>. NF-1 produces <strong>neurofibromin</strong>, which acts as a tumor suppressor and a <strong>negative</strong> regulator of <strong>RAS-MAP kinase</strong> signaling.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of NF-1 include <strong>café-au-lait macules</strong>, <strong>axillary</strong> <strong>freckling</strong>, <strong>Lisch</strong> <strong>nodules</strong>,  <strong>neurofibromas</strong>, and <strong>optic</strong> <strong>gliomas</strong>. <br/>NF-1 is more common than NF2 and typically diagnosed in <strong>childhood</strong>. NF-2 is rarer but often presents with <strong>bilateral acoustic neuroma</strong>, <strong>juvenile cataract</strong> and <strong>schwannoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The various neurocutaneous syndromes and their ocular manifestations are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 159px; width: 343.562px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195.875px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Neurocutaneous syndrome</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.49px; padding-left: 30px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Ocular<br/>manifestations</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195.875px;\"><strong>Neurofibromatosis <br/>type 1</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.49px;\">\n<p>Eyelid plexiform neurofibroma</p>\n<p>Optic nerve glioma</p>\n<p>Bilateral Lisch nodules</p>\n<p>Congenital ectropion uveae</p>\n<p>Prominent corneal nerves</p>\n<p>Congenital glaucoma</p>\n<p>Spheno-orbital encephalocele </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195.875px;\"><strong>Neurofibromatosis <br/>type 2</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.49px;\">\n<p>Posterior subcapsular cataract</p>\n<p>Hamartomas of the <br/>retinal pigment epithelium (RPE) and retina</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195.875px;\"><strong>Sturge-Weber syndrome</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.49px;\">\n<p>Choroidal hemangioma</p>\n<p>Iris heterochromia</p>\n<p>Ipsilateral glaucoma</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195.875px;\"><strong>Tuberous sclerosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.49px;\">\n<p>Atypical iris coloboma</p>\n<p>Iris hypopigmentation</p>\n<p>Retinal astrocytoma</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195.875px;\"><strong>von Hippel-Lindau <br/>syndrome</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.49px;\">\n<p>Retinal hemangiomas </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6602","text":"During a telemedicine cons‌ultation, you come across‌ a case of a 9-year-old c​hild with a previously di‌agnosed autosomal dominan‌t neurocutaneous syndrome​ known as tuberous sclero​sis. Which one of the fol​lowing is not seen in it?​","raw_text":"During a telemedicine consultation, you come across a case of a 9-year-old child with a previously diagnosed autosomal dominant neurocutaneous syndrome known as tuberous sclerosis. Which one of the following is not seen in it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Astro‌cytoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epend‌ymoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"White matt‌er lesions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Renal angi‌omyolipoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ependymoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ependymoma</strong></p><p><strong>Ependymomas</strong> are not seen in tuberous sclerosis. <strong>Neurocutaneous syndromes </strong>like tuberous sclerosis are a group of disorders with abnormalities in both the integumentary system and the CNS (defect of primitive ectoderm). They include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neurofibromatosis</li>\n<li>Tuberous sclerosis</li>\n<li>Sturge–Weber syndrome</li>\n<li>von Hippel–Lindau disease</li>\n<li>Ataxia telangiectasia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Tuberous sclerosis is caused due to mutation of genes coding for <strong>hamartin (chromosome 9)</strong> and <strong>tuberin (chromosome 16)</strong>, which leads to the formation of hamartomas.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>CNS\n<ul>\n<li>CNS involvement is the hallmark of tuberous sclerosis.</li>\n<li>Glioneuronal hamartomas (cortical tubers).</li>\n<li><strong>Subependymal nodules</strong> are lesions found along the wall of the lateral ventricles, where they undergo calcification.</li>\n<li><strong>Subependymal giant cell astrocytomas</strong> (<strong>SEGA</strong>s) are the characteristic brain tumours. These benign, slow-growing tumours arise in the periventricular area.</li>\n<li><strong>White matter lesions</strong> are common and include nodules, cysts, and areas of gliosis and hypomyelination.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Skin\n<ul>\n<li>Facial angiofibroma (adenoma sebaceum)</li>\n<li><strong>Periungual fibroma</strong></li>\n<li>Shagreen patch</li>\n<li>Confetti skin lesions</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Others\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Renal angiomyolipoma</strong></li>\n<li>Cardiac rhabdomyoma</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6603","text":"During rounds in the neuro‌logy ward, an 11-year-old‌ child is observed with t​he following lesion on hi‌s back. Which of the foll‌owing characteristic skin​ manifestation of tuberou​s sclerosis is depicted i​n this picture?","raw_text":"During rounds in the neurology ward, an 11-year-old child is observed with the following lesion on his back. Which of the following characteristic skin manifestation of tuberous sclerosis is depicted in this picture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenoma ‌sebaceum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypomelano‌tic macules","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Shagree‌n patch","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Confetti s‌kin lesion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Shagreen patch","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1521eadec1bc4f86b45d1aafd168cdc9.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7dd9904adb764d4492cce469f6b0ce88x800x525.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d7eeecb16bbf4cfaa451b24185d0ab43x569x629.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1d30bdf16a3a42079eaa18cadb32ad7fx1280x521.892071952032.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bc2985552fff4289898b3fcb680fb9fbx408x341.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/be62bf3cdf804bbd8b29ab4c76efb604x392x584.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/328963837e65444ab979dc53de8f0da4x494x559.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/16c4389d415942acacd8ff115f8550aax799x617.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Shagreen patch</strong></p><p>The lesion seen in the picture depicts a <strong>s</strong><strong>hagreen patch</strong>. This is a characteristic skin lesion found in patients with <strong>t</strong><strong>uberous sclerosis. </strong>It consists of a roughened, raised lesion with an orange-peel consistency, located primarily in the lumbosacral region. Shagreen is shark skin used as a decorative material present in the image of the question.</p>\n<p><strong>Skin lesions in tuberous sclerosis</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The typical <strong>hypomelanotic ash leaf–type macules</strong> on the trunk and extremities are seen in 90% of the patients. It is visualized by the use of a wood ultraviolet lamp.</li>\n<li>Facial angiofibromas (<strong>adenoma sebaceum</strong>) develop between 4 and 6 years of age; they appear as tiny red nodules over the nose and cheeks and are sometimes confused with acne. Later, they enlarge, coalesce, and assume a fleshy appearance.</li>\n<li><strong>Periungual fibroma</strong> are small fibromas or nodules of skin, which form around fingernails or toenails in some adolescent patients.</li>\n<li><strong>Confetti skin lesions</strong> are seen less commonly as a minor feature of tuberous sclerosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Image below: <strong>Facial angiofibromas</strong> (Adenoma sebaceum):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Image below: <strong>Periungual fibroma:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Images: <strong>Confetti skin lesions</strong> (R) and <strong>confetti</strong> (L):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Images: <strong>Ash leaf macule</strong> (above) and <strong>Ash leaves</strong> (below):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Images: <strong>Shagreen patch</strong> (above) and <strong>shagreen texture of sting ray</strong> skin (below):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6604","text":"A 3-year-old child is rush‌ed to the emergency depar‌tment because of an episo​de of fits associated wit‌h uprolling of eyes and h‌igh fever. Kernig’s sign ​is negative and there are​ no post-ictal neurologic​al deficits. There is a h​istory of a similar episo‌de when the child had a f‌ever due to gastroenterit‌is. Which of the followin‌g is false regarding this​ type of seizures?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old child is rushed to the emergency department because of an episode of fits associated with uprolling of eyes and high fever. Kernig’s sign is negative and there are no post-ictal neurological deficits. There is a history of a similar episode when the child had a fever due to gastroenteritis. Which of the following is false regarding this type of seizures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Likelihood of developing e‌pilepsy is low","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Associated with temperatur‌e of 38°C or higher","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antipyretics reduce the ri‌sk of recurrent febrile c‌onvulsions","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Absence of having a develo‌pmental delay","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Antipyretics reduce the risk of recurrent febrile convulsions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Antipyretics reduce the risk of recurrent febrile convulsions</strong></p><p>This is a probable case of <strong>febrile seizures</strong> due to the presence of high fever, no post-ictal deficits and a negative Kernig's sign ruling out meningitis. <strong>Antipyretics</strong> do not reduce the risk of recurrent febrile seizure, as the seizure often occurs as the temperature is rising or falling abruptly.</p>\n<p>Febrile seizures are seizures that-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Occur between the age of <strong>6 months and 5 years</strong></li>\n<li>Occur at a temperature of 38°C <strong>(100.4°F) </strong>or<strong> higher</strong></li>\n<li>Are not due to any CNS infection or any metabolic imbalance</li>\n<li>Occur without a history of prior afebrile seizures</li>\n<li>Occur with <strong>no post-ictal neurological deficit</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Types of febrile seizures:</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 446px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 220px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Simple febrile seizures</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Complex (atypical) febrile seizures</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 220px; text-align: center;\">Occurs within 24 hours of the onset of fever</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px; text-align: center;\">Can occur at any time during a fever</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 220px; text-align: center;\">Generalized seizures</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px; text-align: center;\">Focal seizures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 220px; text-align: center;\">Duration is &lt;15 min</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px; text-align: center;\">Prolonged</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 220px; text-align: center;\">No recurrence within 24 hrs of seizures</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px; text-align: center;\">Recurrent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 220px; text-align: center;\">Chances of going into epilepsy 1%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px; text-align: center;\">Chances of going into epilepsy 6%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Investigations:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lumbar puncture should be performed in the <strong>first episode</strong> of seizures with fever, for all infants <strong>&lt;6 months</strong> age (to exclude meningitis).</li>\n<li>EEG and neuroimaging are advised for children with atypical febrile seizures.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment:</strong></p>\n<p>Antipyretics and proper hydration. Midazolam or diazepam (rectal) can be used if seizures present.</p>\n<p><strong>Prophylaxis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Intermittent prophylaxis:</strong> If the febrile seizures are 3 or more in 6 months, or 4 or more seizures in 1 year, oral <strong>clobazam</strong> (1mg/kg/day) for 3 days during the fever episode.</li>\n<li><strong>Continuous prophylaxis: </strong>If intermittent prophylaxis fails or in recurrent atypical febrile seizures, sodium valproate and phenobarbitone can be used.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6605","text":"Your neighbour, Sheila, fr‌antically knocks on your ‌door to seek help as her ​toddler had suddenly star‌ted convulsing with uprol‌ling of eyes. The child h​ad a high fever and this ​was his third episode of ​seizures in 6 months. Pos​t-recovery, Sheila is sti‌ll anxious and wishes to ‌know the risk factors for‌ such recurrences of febr‌ile seizures. You would e​xplain all of the followi‍ng except-","raw_text":"Your neighbour, Sheila, frantically knocks on your door to seek help as her toddler had suddenly started convulsing with uprolling of eyes. The child had a high fever and this was his third episode of seizures in 6 months. Post-recovery, Sheila is still anxious and wishes to know the risk factors for such recurrences of febrile seizures. You would explain all of the following except-","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duration of fever &amp;lt; 24 ‌hr","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Late age‌ of onset","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Family history of epilepsy‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lower ser‌um sodium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Late age of onset","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Late age of onset</strong></p><p><strong>Early age</strong> of onset (age &lt;1 year) is a risk factor for <strong>recurrence </strong>of<strong> febrile seizures</strong>. The other risk factors for such recurrences are as follows:</p>\n<p><strong>Major:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Age &lt;1 yr</li>\n<li>Duration of fever &lt;24 hr</li>\n<li>Fever 38-39°C</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Minor:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Family history of febrile seizures</li>\n<li>Family history of epilepsy</li>\n<li>Complex febrile seizure</li>\n<li>Male gender</li>\n<li>Lower serum sodium at the time of presentation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6607","text":"A child presents with deve‌lopmental delay and multi‌ple seizure types. The se​izures could not be contr‌olled by an array of drug‌s. Electroencephalogram (​EEG) shows 1-2 Hz spike a​nd slow waves with polysp​ike bursts in sleep. What​ is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A child presents with developmental delay and multiple seizure types. The seizures could not be controlled by an array of drugs. Electroencephalogram (EEG) shows 1-2 Hz spike and slow waves with polyspike bursts in sleep. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Juvenile myoclonic epileps‌y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lennox-Gast‌aut syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Landau-Kleff‌ner syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"West s‌yndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lennox-Gastaut syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lennox-Gastaut syndrome</strong></p><p>A child with features of developmental delay, intractable seizures and an electroencephalogram (EEG) showing 1-2 Hz spike and polyspike bursts in sleep has a likely diagnosis of <strong>Lennox–Gastaut syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Lennox–Gastaut syndrome- </strong>Typically starts between the age of<strong> 2 and 10 years. </strong>Consists of a triad of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Developmental delay</li>\n<li>Multiple seizure types (includes atypical absences, myoclonic, astatic, and tonic seizures)</li>\n<li>EEG findings <strong>1-2 Hz spike </strong>and slow waves,<strong> polyspike bursts in sleep</strong>, and a slow background in wakefulness</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients will have long-term cognitive impairment and intractable seizures despite multiple therapies. The <strong>drug of choice</strong> for Lennox-Gastaut syndrome is <strong>valproate. </strong>Other drugs used for this condition are clobazam, lamotrigine, rufinamide and topiramate.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p><strong>Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy (Janz syndrome)- </strong>Typically, it starts in early adolescence (not in childhood) and the EEG usually shows generalized 4-5 Hz polyspike and slow-wave discharges</p>\n<p><strong>Landau–Kleffner syndrome- </strong>A rare condition characterized by loss of language skills in a previously normal child (not a child with developmental delay)</p>\n<p><strong>West syndrome- </strong>Starts between the ages of 2 and 12 months and typical EEG picture is called hypsarrhythmia (high-voltage, slow with chaotic background)</p>\n<table style=\"width: 549px; height: 340px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.008px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.008px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Typical EEG features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.758px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Drug of choice</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20.4px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.008px; height: 20.4px;\">Lennox–Gastaut syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.008px; height: 20.4px;\">1-2 Hz spike and polyspike bursts in sleep</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.758px; height: 20.4px;\">Valproate </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.008px; height: 18px;\">Absence seizures</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.008px; height: 18px;\">3 Hz spike and slow-wave (spike and dome pattern)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.758px; height: 18px;\">Ethosuximide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.008px; height: 18px;\">Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.008px; height: 18px;\">4-5 Hz spike polyspike and slow-wave discharges</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.758px; height: 18px;\">Valproate </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.008px; height: 18px;\">West syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.008px; height: 18px;\">Hypsarrhythmia (high-voltage waves with chaotic background</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.758px; height: 18px;\">Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9660","text":"Which of the following is ‌true for Lennox-Gastaut s‌yndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true for Lennox-Gastaut syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Constitutes of a single se‌izure type","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Good prognosis and adequat‌e controlled epilepsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"No impaired cognitive func‌tion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"EEG showing slow (&amp;lt;3Hz)‌ spike and wave discharge‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. EEG showing slow (<3Hz) spike and wave discharge","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. EEG showing slow (&lt;3Hz) spike and wave discharge</strong></p><p><strong>Lennox–Gastaut syndrome</strong> is associated with 1-2 Hz spike and slow-wave discharges.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Typically starts between the ages of 2 and 10 years</li>\n<li>Consists of a <strong>triad</strong> of developmental delay, multiple seizure types (that as a rule includes <strong>atypical absence seizures,</strong> myoclonic, astatic, and tonic seizures) and specific EEG abnormalities.</li>\n<li>Most are left with <strong>long-term cognitive impairment</strong> and intractable seizures despite multiple therapies.</li>\n<li>Lennox–Gastaut syndrome has a <strong>poor prognosis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6611","text":"A concerned kindergarten t‌eacher calls up the mothe‌r of one of her students ​who was having transient ‌lapses of consciousness. ‌The teacher elaborated th​at the child was having m​ultiple episodes of stari​ng blankly into space for​ a few seconds before qui‌ckly returning to alertne‌ss. Following investigati‌ons including an EEG, a d‌iagnosis of atypical abse​nce seizures was made. Wh‍ich of the following is a​ feature of it?","raw_text":"A concerned kindergarten teacher calls up the mother of one of her students who was having transient lapses of consciousness. The teacher elaborated that the child was having multiple episodes of staring blankly into space for a few seconds before quickly returning to alertness. Following investigations including an EEG, a diagnosis of atypical absence seizures was made. Which of the following is a feature of it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Associated with head drop ‌during seizure","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Precipitated by hyperventi‌lation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"EEG shows 3Hz spike and sl‌ow wave pattern","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Associated with petit mal ‌seizures","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Associated with head drop during seizure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Associated with head drop during seizure</strong></p><p><strong>Atypical absence seizures</strong> have associated myoclonic components and tone changes of the head (<strong>head drop</strong>) and body. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 498.983px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 228px;\"><strong>Absence seizures</strong> (typical)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.983px;\"><strong>Atypical absence seizures</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 228px;\">No change in the body tone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.983px;\">Change in the tone of the head (head drop) and body.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 228px;\">Precipitated by hyperventilation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.983px;\">Precipitated by drowsiness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 228px;\">3 Hz spike and slow wave pattern on EEG</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.983px;\">1-2 Hz spike and slow wave pattern on EEG</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 228px;\">Usually not associated with other syndromes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.983px;\">Associated with Lennox–Gestaut syndrome</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6609","text":"Harish had a panic attack ‌when he was called to rec‌ite a poem on the stage. ​This was followed by a su‌dden lapse in consciousne‌ss and manifested as a bl​ank stare along with flut​tering of eyes. He appear​ed frozen without any cha​nge in the body tone. Whi‌ch of the following is a ‌characteristic feature of‌ this condition on electr‌oencephalography (EEG)?","raw_text":"Harish had a panic attack when he was called to recite a poem on the stage. This was followed by a sudden lapse in consciousness and manifested as a blank stare along with fluttering of eyes. He appeared frozen without any change in the body tone. Which of the following is a characteristic feature of this condition on electroencephalography (EEG)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Continuous s‌tunted spikes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 Hz spike a‌nd slow wave","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Continuous ‌tall spikes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Waves at the rate of 10/ s‌ec","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3 Hz spike and slow wave","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b2eecffbac1b4ffc831da534bf597343x1279x1242.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3 Hz spike and slow wave</strong></p><p>The features of a lapse of consciousness precipitated by hyperventilation (panic attack), without any change in the tone of the body (head drop), point towards a likely diagnosis of <strong>petit mal or typical absence seizures</strong>. The electroencephalogram (EEG) in petit mal seizure is associated with a <strong>3 Hz spike </strong>and<strong> slow-wave discharges</strong>.</p>\n<p>Features of petit mal seizures <strong>(typical absence seizures):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Starts abruptly in childhood (<strong>6 to 8 years</strong>)</li>\n<li>Approximately 25% of patients also develop generalized tonic-clonic seizures.</li>\n<li>Characterized by brief lapses of consciousness and sudden discontinuation of activity with <strong>staring spell, eye fluttering,</strong> or rhythmic movements</li>\n<li>No loss of postural control or incontinence</li>\n<li><strong>No post-ictal confusion.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hyperventilation</strong> for 3-5 min can precipitate the seizures. The drug of choice is <strong>ethosuximide.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Image</strong>: EEG waveform for an absence seizure- 3 waves per second, with spike and slow-wave pattern </p>\n<p></p>\n<table style=\"width: 549px; height: 340px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.008px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.008px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Typical EEG features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.758px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Drug of choice</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20.4px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.008px; height: 20.4px;\">Lennox–Gastaut syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.008px; height: 20.4px;\">1-2 Hz spike and polyspike bursts in sleep</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.758px; height: 20.4px;\">Valproate </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.008px; height: 18px;\">Absence seizures</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.008px; height: 18px;\">3 Hz spike and slow-wave (spike and dome pattern)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.758px; height: 18px;\">Ethosuximide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.008px; height: 18px;\">Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.008px; height: 18px;\">4-5 Hz spike polyspike and slow-wave discharges</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.758px; height: 18px;\">Valproate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.008px; height: 18px;\">West syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.008px; height: 18px;\">Hypsarrhythmia (high-voltage waves with chaotic background</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.758px; height: 18px;\">Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6612","text":"An 8-year-old boy was play‌ing at his crèche centre ‌when he suddenly develope​d rapid jerky movements o‌f both the upper and lowe‌r limbs and was rushed to​ the emergency department​. It had been ongoing for​ the past 10 minutes and ​the boy had been unrespon‌sive throughout. Which of‌ the following is not use‌ful in the management of ‌this condition?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old boy was playing at his crèche centre when he suddenly developed rapid jerky movements of both the upper and lower limbs and was rushed to the emergency department. It had been ongoing for the past 10 minutes and the boy had been unresponsive throughout. Which of the following is not useful in the management of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lora‌zepam","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phen‌ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phenoba‌rbitone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carbam‌azepine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Carbamazepine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c1d478edaf8a498aa0a85655f8fa09f4x1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Carbamazepine</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is that of <strong>status epilepticus. </strong>This is due to rapid jerky movements causing continuous seizure activity for more than 5 minutes. <strong>Carbamazepine</strong> is not commonly used in the treatment of status epilepticus.</p>\n<p>Status epilepticus is defined as continuous or recurrent seizure activity lasting for more than 5 minutes without regaining consciousness. The image given below depicts the management of this condition in a flowchart-</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6608","text":"A 15-year-old girl present‌ed with episodes of brief‌ involuntary muscular twi​tching that causes her to‌ drop her toothbrush or t‌eacup in the morning. Fol​lowing investigations inc​luding an EEG, a diagnosi​s of juvenile myoclonic e​pilepsy was made. Which o‌f the following is not a ‌feature of it?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old girl presented with episodes of brief involuntary muscular twitching that causes her to drop her toothbrush or teacup in the morning. Following investigations including an EEG, a diagnosis of juvenile myoclonic epilepsy was made. Which of the following is not a feature of it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sleep deprivation provokes‌ seizures.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prognosis is generally goo‌d.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Drug of choice is valproat‌e.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"EEG shows 1-2 Hz spike wav‌es.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. EEG shows 1-2 Hz spike waves.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. EEG shows 1-2 Hz spike waves.</strong></p><p>In juvenile myoclonic epilepsy, EEG characteristically shows <strong>4-5 Hz</strong> polyspike and <strong>slow-wave</strong> discharges.</p>\n<p><strong>Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy (Janz syndrome):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the most common generalized epilepsy in young adults ( 12-18 years).</li>\n<li>They are benign generalized myoclonic seizures with a<strong> good prognosis.</strong></li>\n<li>They commonly <strong>affect the upper limbs more</strong> than the lower limbs and sometimes cause the <strong>dropping of objects</strong>.</li>\n<li>Typically, it starts in early adolescence (12-18 years), with 1 or more of the following manifestations:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Myoclonic jerks in the morning</strong> (“shock-like” contraction of a muscle, that is often repeated), generalized tonic-clonic or clonic seizures upon awakening; and juvenile absences.</li>\n<li><strong>Sleep deprivation</strong>, alcohol (in older patients), and photic stimulation provoke seizures.</li>\n<li>The EEG usually shows generalized 4-5 Hz polyspike and slow-wave discharges.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The <strong>drug of choice</strong> for juvenile myoclonic epilepsy is <strong>valproate. </strong>Lamotrigine is also useful.<strong><br/></strong></li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"width: 549px; height: 340px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.008px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.008px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Typical EEG features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.758px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Drug of choice</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20.4px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.008px; height: 20.4px;\">Lennox–Gastaut syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.008px; height: 20.4px;\">1-2 Hz spike and polyspike bursts in sleep</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.758px; height: 20.4px;\">Valproate </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.008px; height: 18px;\">Absence seizures</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.008px; height: 18px;\">3 Hz spike and slow-wave (spike and dome pattern)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.758px; height: 18px;\">Ethosuximide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.008px; height: 18px;\">Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.008px; height: 18px;\">4-5 Hz spike polyspike and slow-wave discharges</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.758px; height: 18px;\">Valproate </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.008px; height: 18px;\">West syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.008px; height: 18px;\">Hypsarrhythmia (high-voltage waves with chaotic background</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.758px; height: 18px;\">Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5252","text":"An adolescent girl complai‌nts of dropping objects f‌rom hands precipitated du​ring morning hours and ex‌ams. There is no history ‌of loss of consciousness.​ One of her cousins has b​een diagnosed with epilep​sy. EEG was suggestive of​ epileptic spikes. What i‌s the most probable diagn‌osis?","raw_text":"An adolescent girl complaints of dropping objects from hands precipitated during morning hours and exams. There is no history of loss of consciousness. One of her cousins has been diagnosed with epilepsy. EEG was suggestive of epileptic spikes. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Juvenile myoclonic epileps‌y","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Atypical‌ absence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Choreo-athet‌oid epilepsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Centrotemp‌oral spikes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario describes an <strong>adolescent</strong> girl with <strong>myoclonic jerks</strong> (a history of <strong>dropping objects</strong>) with <strong>preserved</strong> <strong>consciousness</strong>. This, along with <strong>abnormal EEG </strong>findings strongly suggests <strong>juvenile myoclonic epilepsy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy</strong> (<strong>Janz syndrome</strong>) is the most common generalized epilepsy in young adults. It starts in <strong>early adolescence</strong> with one or more of the following features: <strong>bilateral myoclonic jerks</strong> in the <strong>morning</strong>, causing patients to <strong>drop things</strong>, <strong>generalized tonic-clonic</strong> or <strong>clonic-tonic-clonic</strong> seizures upon awakening, and <strong>juvenile absence seizure</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Consciousness</strong> is usually<strong> preserved</strong>. <strong>Precipitating factors</strong> include <strong>sleep deprivation</strong>, <strong>alcohol</strong>, and photic stimulation. EEG shows <strong>4 to 5Hz polyspike</strong> and <strong>slow wave</strong> discharges. A <strong>family history</strong> of epilepsy is often present. </p>\n<p>The treatment options for JME include <strong>sodium valproate, levetiracetam, and topiramate. </strong>Benzodiazepine, lamotrigine, perampanel, phenobarbital, primidone, and zonisamide can be considered as add-on drugs. <strong>Complete remission</strong> is <strong>uncommon</strong> and has a high risk of seizure recurrence, hence antiepileptics are continued<strong> lifelong</strong> in a majority of the patients. </p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Atypical absence seizures</strong> have been associated with myoclonic components and <strong>head drop</strong>. They are precipitated by drowsiness and EEG shows <strong>1 to 2 Hz spike</strong> and <strong>slow wave</strong> discharges. </p>\n<p>Option D: In <strong>benign childhood epilepsy</strong> with <strong>centrotemporal spikes</strong>, the child typically wakes at night due to a focal seizure with preserved awareness. <strong>B</strong><strong>uccal</strong> and <strong>throat</strong> <strong>tingling</strong> and tonic or clonic contractions on one side of the face are seen, with drooling and preserved consciousness. EEG shows typical wide-based centrotemporal spikes. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6610","text":"A 7-month-old boy presents‌ with brief episodes of h‌ead drops and flexion of ​the body, followed by ton‌ic extension of arms and ‌legs. These movements las​t 1 to 2 seconds and occu​r in several clusters per​ day, with 15 to 30 spasm​s per cluster. EEG is giv‌en below and is suggestiv‌e of infantile spasms. Wh‌ich of the following is t‌he drug of choice for thi​s condition?","raw_text":"A 7-month-old boy presents with brief episodes of head drops and flexion of the body, followed by tonic extension of arms and legs. These movements last 1 to 2 seconds and occur in several clusters per day, with 15 to 30 spasms per cluster. EEG is given below and is suggestive of infantile spasms. Which of the following is the drug of choice for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbam‌azepine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ethosu‌ximide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Valp‌roate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AC‌TH","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. ACTH","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fc62c8e8f2654512ac92ba42ba9ba6de.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/21a22fba06894d02b4a3f654312aad92x510x737.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. ACTH</strong></p><p>The drug of choice for infantile spasms is <strong>adrenocorticotropic hormone</strong> (ACTH). The EEG shows a specific type of abnormal chaotic brainwave pattern called<strong> hypsarrhythmia. </strong>It is characterized by<strong> </strong>continuous, high-amplitude, asynchronous delta activities with interspersed independent, multiple spikes. Hypsarrhythmia on EEG can occur even between attacks.Excessive corticotropin-releasing hormone <strong>(CRH)</strong> production is hypothesized to cause infantile spasms. ACTH administration <strong>suppresses CRH secretion</strong> and hence controls the seizures.</p>\n<p><strong>Infantile spasms (West syndrome)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Age of onset is 3–8 months of life</li>\n<li>Characterized by <strong>salaam spells</strong> (sudden dropping of the head or flexion of arms, followed by tonic extension of arms and legs) and <strong>hypsarrhythmia</strong> on EEG.</li>\n<li>Causes include hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy, neurocutaneous syndromes, and idiopathic.</li>\n<li><strong>Drug of choice</strong>: Adrenocorticotropic hormone <strong>(ACTH)</strong>, Vigabatrin.</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"width: 549px; height: 340px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.008px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.008px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Typical EEG features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.758px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Drug of choice</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20.4px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.008px; height: 20.4px;\">Lennox–Gastaut syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.008px; height: 20.4px;\">1-2 Hz spike and polyspike bursts in sleep</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.758px; height: 20.4px;\">Valproate </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.008px; height: 18px;\">Absence seizures</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.008px; height: 18px;\">3 Hz spike and slow-wave (spike and dome pattern)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.758px; height: 18px;\">Ethosuximide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.008px; height: 18px;\">Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.008px; height: 18px;\">4-5 Hz spike polyspike and slow-wave discharges</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.758px; height: 18px;\">Valproate </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 143.008px; height: 18px;\">West syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.008px; height: 18px;\">Hypsarrhythmia (high-voltage waves with chaotic background</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.758px; height: 18px;\">Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The image given below illustrates infantile spasms in a child-</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2325","text":"An 8-month old male child ‌presented with delayed mo‌tor milestones. He was bo​rn at 28 weeks of gestati‌on with a birth weight of‌ 1200 grams. A diagnosis ​of cerebral palsy is made​ following investigations​. An MRI scan of the brai​n revealed periventricula‌r leukomalacia. Which of ‌the following subtypes of‌ cerebral palsy is most l‌ikely in this child?","raw_text":"An 8-month old male child presented with delayed motor milestones. He was born at 28 weeks of gestation with a birth weight of 1200 grams. A diagnosis of cerebral palsy is made following investigations. An MRI scan of the brain revealed periventricular leukomalacia. Which of the following subtypes of cerebral palsy is most likely in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spastic ‌diplegia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Spastic qu‌adriplegia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spastic h‌emiplegia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dyskinetic c‌erebral palsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Spastic diplegia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Spastic diplegia</strong></p><p>The most likely subtype in this child with delayed motor milestones and a history of prematurity and low birth weight is <strong>spastic diplegia</strong>. This is because the most common <strong>sequelae of periventricular leukomalacia</strong> (PVL) is spastic diplegia. PVL is the ischemia of white matter adjacent to the lateral ventricles of the brain.</p>\n<p><strong>Cerebral palsy</strong></p>\n<p>It is a group of disorders, affecting movement or posture, due to non-progressive disturbances occurring in the developing fetal brain. These could be due to <strong>hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy</strong>. The most common subtype of cerebral palsy is spastic diplegia.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cerebral palsy subtype</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Pathology</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spastic diplegia</td>\n<td>Periventricular leukomalacia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spastic quadriplegia</td>\n<td>Parasagittal brain injury</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spastic hemiplegia</td>\n<td>Middle cerebral artery territory infarction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dyskinetic cerebral palsy</td>\n<td>Basal ganglia</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0016","text":"Which one of the following‌ is not a feature in cere‌bral palsy?","raw_text":"Which one of the following is not a feature in cerebral palsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypo‌tonia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Microc‌ephaly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ata‌xia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flaccid ‌paralysis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Flaccid paralysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cd480ee47cc947ccb1c31d18bad0e6aax510x520.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Flaccid paralysis</strong></p><p><strong>Flaccid paralysis </strong>is <strong>not</strong> a feature of cerebral palsy (CP). </p>\n<p>CP is an <strong>upper motor neuron disease </strong>characterized by <strong>rigidity</strong> and <strong>scissoring gait</strong>.<strong> </strong><strong>Flaccid paralysis</strong> would be seen in <strong>lower motor neuron disease</strong>. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Hypotonia</strong> is a characteristic feature of <strong>athetoid CP. </strong>These infants generally have poor head control. </p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Microcephaly</strong><strong> </strong>can be seen in CP due to perinatal insults to the developing brain. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Ataxia </strong>is a common feature of <strong>extrapyramidal CP</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>subtypes</strong> of cerebral palsy are given in the table below: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 1137px; width: 646px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 41px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 211.047px; height: 41px;\"><strong>Types</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.469px; height: 41px;\"><strong>Etiology</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.398px; height: 41px;\"><strong>Presentation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.086px; height: 41px;\"><strong>MRI findings</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 234px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 84.6875px; height: 483.492px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Spastic</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">(m/c type)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.359px; height: 234px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Diplegia</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt;\">(second m/c in India)</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.469px; height: 234px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Prematurity</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Ischemia </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Infection</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.398px; height: 234px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Bilateral spasticity (<strong>LL&gt;</strong>UL)</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Scissoring gait</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Commando crawl</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Learning disabilities</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.086px; height: 234px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Periventricular leukomalacia</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 180px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 120.359px; height: 180px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Hemiplegia</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.469px; height: 180px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Vascular insult</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Infection</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Genetic</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.398px; height: 180px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Early hand preference</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">(<strong>UL</strong>&gt;LL)</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Walking delayed up to 18-24m</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Equinovarus</strong> deformity of foot</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Seizures (33%)</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.086px; height: 180px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Porencephalic cyst</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Focal infarct changes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 69.4922px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 120.359px; height: 69.4922px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Quadriplegia</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt;\">(most severe)</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt;\">(m/c type in India)</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.469px; height: 69.4922px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Ischemia</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Infection</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.398px; height: 69.4922px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Marked motor impairment</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Flexion </strong>contractures</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Strong association with <strong>seizures</strong> </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Severe</strong> intellectual impairment and <strong>GDD</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Difficulty swallowing (<strong>supranuclear palsy</strong>)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.086px; height: 69.4922px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Multi-cystic encephalomalacia</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Severe periventricular leukomalacia</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84.6875px; height: 20px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Dyskinetic</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">(15-20%)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.359px; height: 20px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Extrapyramidal</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Athetoid</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Chorioathetoid</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.469px; height: 20px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Asphyxia</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Kernicterus</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Mitochondrial</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Genetic</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.398px; height: 20px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Rigidity/hypotonia, dystonia, athetosis, chorea</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Cognitive disability</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Hip displacement</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Seizures</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.086px; height: 20px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Basal ganglia scarring</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Thalamic scarring</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>UL - upper limb; LL - lower limb; GDD - global developmental delay</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2374","text":"A newborn presented with h‌igh-grade fever, poor fee‌ding and incessant cry fo​r the last 2 days. Multip‌le episodes of vomiting a‌re present as well. On ex​amination, hypotonia is p​resent along with weak re​flexes. The patient is ad​mitted and during rounds,‌ subtle lip-smacking seiz‌ures are also observed al‌ong with a vacant stare. ‌CSF examination shows red​uced glucose, increased p‍rotein, and increased leu​kocytes with predominant ​neutrophils. Which of the‌ following organism is un​likely to cause this cond​ition?","raw_text":"A newborn presented with high-grade fever, poor feeding and incessant cry for the last 2 days. Multiple episodes of vomiting are present as well. On examination, hypotonia is present along with weak reflexes. The patient is admitted and during rounds, subtle lip-smacking seizures are also observed along with a vacant stare. CSF examination shows reduced glucose, increased protein, and increased leukocytes with predominant neutrophils.  Which of the following organism is unlikely to cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neisseria m‌eningitidis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Escheric‌hia coli","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Group B st‌reptococci","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"List‌eria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Neisseria meningitidis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Neisseria meningitidis</strong></p><p>The acute features of fever, poor feeding, incessant cry, vomiting and seizures points toward a likely diagnosis of <strong>neonatal meningitis</strong>. The CSF findings correlate with that of bacterial meningitis due to purulent appearance, increased proteins, decreased glucose and a predominance of polymorphs. Neonatal meningitis is not caused by <strong><em>Neisseria meningitidis</em></strong> because, in neonates, bacteria are often acquired from the maternal vaginal flora.</p>\n<p>The common <strong>causes of acute bacterial meningitis</strong>:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 223px; height: 278px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.5333px;\"><strong>Age group</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.467px;\"><strong>Organism</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.5333px;\">Neonates</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.467px;\">Group B streptococci, <em>Escherichia coli</em>, <em>Listeria</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.5333px;\">Infants and children</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.467px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Neisseria meningitidis</em>, <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae,</em> and <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.5333px;\">Adolescents and young adults</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119.467px;\"><em><em>S.</em></em><em>pneumoniae</em>, <em><em>N. </em></em><em>meningitidis</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2367","text":"During your PICU rotation,‌ you are assigned a bedsi‌de case of a child with f​ever, convulsions, and si‌gns of meningeal irritati‌on. On browsing through h​is file, you find a lumba​r puncture report mention​ing the following finding​s. What is the likely dia‌gnosis? Appearance: Turbi‌d Opening pressure: 30 cm‌ H2O WBC count: 1000 cell‌s/µL, 90% PMNL Glucose le​vel: 35 mg/dL Protein lev‍el: 80 mg/dL","raw_text":"<p>During your PICU rotation, you are assigned a bedside case of a child with fever, convulsions, and signs of meningeal irritation. On browsing through his file, you find a lumbar puncture report mentioning the following findings. What is the likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p> </p>\n<table style=\"height: 164px;\" width=\"384\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184.258px;\"><strong>Appearance: </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.258px;\"><strong>Turbid</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184.258px;\"><strong>Opening pressure:</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.258px;\"><strong>30 cm H2O</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184.258px;\"><strong>WBC count:</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.258px;\"><strong>1000 cells/µL, 90% PMNL</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184.258px;\"><strong>Glucose level:</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.258px;\"><strong>35 mg/dL</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184.258px;\"><strong>Protein level:</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.258px;\"><strong>80 mg/dL</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Viral me‌ningitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tubercular‌ meningitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bacterial ‌meningitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Partially treated bacteria‌l meningitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bacterial meningitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bacterial meningitis</strong></p><p>Fever, convulsions, and signs of meningeal irritation (nuchal rigidity, Kernig's sign, Brudzinski's signs) in a child, with CSF showing reduced glucose, increased protein, and increased leukocytes levels is a typical presentation of <strong>bacterial meningitis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>CSF findings in different types of meningitis</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 450px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 25px;\">Parameter/Meningitis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\"><strong>Bacterial </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.094px;\"><strong>Tubercular </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.906px;\"><strong>Viral </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 25px;\"><strong>CSF pressure </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">Raised</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.094px;\">Raised</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.906px;\">Raised</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 25px;\"><strong>Appearance</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">Turbid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.094px;\">Clear (<strong>cob-web</strong> formation)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.906px;\">Clear</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 25px;\"><strong>Glucose</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">Decreased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.094px;\">Decreased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.906px;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 25px;\"><strong>Protein</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.094px;\">Very high</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.906px;\">slightly high</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 25px;\"><strong>Chloride</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.094px;\">Very low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.906px;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 25px;\"><strong>Cells</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">Neutrophils</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.094px;\">Lymphocytes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.906px;\">Lymphocytes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<p>In partially treated bacterial meningitis, CSF can be clear, with normal glucose levels and lymphocyte predominance. Culture is usually sterile, and antigen can be detected by agglutination test.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2393","text":"Asif, a young child with p‌reviously diagnosed tuber‌culosis, is brought to yo​ur hospital for further m‌anagement. Examination re‌veals a characteristic fi​nding as present in the i​mage. What do you expect ​in the cranial CT of this​ child?","raw_text":"Asif, a young child with  previously diagnosed tuberculosis, is brought to your hospital for further management. Examination reveals a characteristic finding as present in the image. What do you expect in the cranial CT of this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ring-enhanc‌ing lesions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Focal involvement of the t‌emporal lobe","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subependymal calcification‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Basal e‌xudates","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Basal exudates","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1284f2c4236d4f0d9efa4cd10fb64dea.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Basal exudates</strong></p><p>A history of tuberculosis along with a <strong>positive Kernig's sign</strong> (as seen in the image) is suggestive of tubercular meningitis. Cranial computed tomography (CT) in a case of tubercular meningitis shows <strong>basal exudates</strong> and <strong>hydrocephalus</strong>. Intracranial tuberculosis can also manifest as ring-enhancing lesions (<strong>tuberculoma</strong>), but the basal exudates are the most common and characteristic feature of tubercular meningitis.</p>\n<p><strong>Tubercular meningitis</strong></p>\n<p>It is common between 6 months and 4 years of age and usually spread by the <strong>hematogenous</strong> route. The gelatinous exudate infiltrates the corticomeningeal blood vessels leading to hydrocephalus.</p>\n<p><strong>The three stages are-</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prodromal stage</li>\n<li>Stage of meningitis</li>\n<li>Stage of coma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>CSF study shows reduced sugar level, increased protein level, and <strong>lymphocytosis. </strong>Treatment includes <strong>antitubercular drugs</strong> for 12 months and <strong>corticosteroids</strong> (to reduce neurologic sequelae). TB meningitis is the only indication of steroids in TB.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p><strong>Ring-enhancing</strong> lesions are seen in cerebral abscess, neurocysticercosis and tuberculoma(rich focus). Focal involvement of the temporal lobe is seen in <strong>herpes simplex encephalitis. </strong>Subependymal calcifications are seen in <strong>tuberous sclerosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0748","text":"Two girls in the same clas‌s are diagnosed with meni‌ngococcal meningitis. The​ir 12-year old friend fro‌m the same school is afra‌id of contracting the dis​ease. What advice should ​be given to the exposed s​tudents?","raw_text":"Two girls in the same class are diagnosed with meningococcal meningitis. Their 12-year old friend from the same school is afraid of contracting the disease.  What advice should be given to the exposed students?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Two doses of polysaccharid‌e vaccine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antibiotic ‌prophylaxis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Two doses of conjugate vac‌cine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Single dose of meningococc‌al vaccine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Antibiotic prophylaxis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Antibiotic prophylaxis</strong></p><p><strong>Antibiotic prophylaxis</strong> is provided to the <strong>close contacts</strong> of persons with <strong>confirmed meningococcal</strong> disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Close contacts</strong> of individuals with confirmed meningococcal disease are at an <strong>increased risk</strong> of developing the illness. Prophylaxis is also offered to individuals with <strong>direct</strong> and <strong>prolonged contact</strong> with a case of meningococcal disease. <strong>Antibiotics</strong> are effective in <strong>preventing</strong> additional cases by <strong>eradicating the carriage</strong> of the invasive strain. </p>\n<p>Treatment is provided to the <strong>contacts</strong> <strong>within 24 hours</strong> of identification of the index case. The antibiotics effective include <strong>rifampicin, ciprofloxacin, ceftriaxone,</strong> and <strong>azithromycin. </strong></p>\n<p>Prophylaxis is not generally recommended for <strong>medical personnel</strong>, except in cases where there is <strong>exposure to aerosols</strong> of respiratory secretions, such as during mouth-to-mouth resuscitation, intubation, or suctioning. This recommendation applies <strong>within 24 hours</strong> before or after the initiation of antibiotic therapy in the index case. </p>\n<p>For the <strong>treatment of cases</strong>, <strong>penicillin</strong> is the <strong>preferred</strong> drug, but <strong>ceftriaxone</strong> or other <strong>third-generation cephalosporins</strong> are recommended for patients with penicillin allergy. <strong>Droplet infection</strong> control precautions should be followed for hospitalized patients for <strong>24 hours after effective therapy</strong> has been initiated. </p>\n<p>Offering<strong> quadrivalent meningococcal</strong> <strong>A, C, W, Y conjugate vaccines</strong> in addition to antimicrobial prophylaxis can further <strong>reduce the risk</strong> of disease among close contacts of cases caused by vaccine capsular groups. </p>\n<p>Meningococcal vaccines are available in two forms: <strong>polysaccharide</strong> vaccines and <strong>polysaccharide-protein conjugate</strong> vaccines. Polysaccharide vaccines can be given as a single dose to individuals <strong>aged 2 years and older</strong>. They are offered in <strong>bivalent</strong> (A, C), <strong>trivalent</strong> (A, C, W135), and <strong>quadrivalent</strong> (A, C, W135, Y) formulations. </p>\n<p>Meningococcal <strong>conjugate vaccines</strong> are available as <strong>monovalent</strong> (A or C) or <strong>quadrivalent</strong> (A, C, W135, Y) formulations. <strong>Conjugate vaccines</strong>, which offer increased immunogenicity and potential for herd protection, are preferred over polysaccharide vaccines, especially in <strong>children under 2 years</strong> of age. Both types of vaccines are <strong>safe to use during pregnancy</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5246","text":"How long should a child be‌ isolated after being dia‌gnosed with bacterial men​ingitis to prevent furthe‌r transmission?","raw_text":"How long should a child be isolated after being diagnosed with bacterial meningitis to prevent further transmission?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Till 24 hours after starti‌ng antibiotics","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Till cultures become negat‌ive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Till antibiotic course is ‌complete","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Till 12 hours after admiss‌ion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Till 24 hours after starting antibiotics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Till 24 hours after starting antibiotics</strong></p><p>Isolation of the child is recommended<strong> until 24 hrs after the initiation of effective therapy </strong>after being diagnosed with acute bacterial meningitis to prevent further transmission.</p>\n<p><strong>Droplet precautions</strong> are recommended for patients with <strong>Neisseria meningitides </strong>and <strong>Haemophilus influenzae<em> </em>type b (Hib) meningitis</strong> until they have received 24 hours of effective therapy. Patients should be in private rooms, and hospital personnel should wear a face mask when they are within 3 feet (1 meter) of the patient.</p>\n<p>The child with meningitis shows nonspecific symptoms like <strong>fever,</strong> anorexia, <strong>poor feeding</strong>, headache, myalgia, <strong>hypotension</strong>, petechia, purpura, or a <strong>rash</strong>. Meningial irritation is indicated by <strong>nuchal rigidity</strong>, back pain, and <strong>Kernig, and Brudzinski signs </strong>(usually not seen in infants). <strong>Seizures</strong> and <strong>altered mental status</strong> may be present. </p>\n<p>The recommended <strong>empirical antibiotic</strong> regimen in a suspected case of meningitis outside the neonatal period is <strong>vancomycin</strong> combined with a <strong>third-generation cephalosporin</strong>. Patients <strong>allergic to penicillin</strong> or cephalosporin can be treated with <strong>meropenem, fluoroquinolones</strong>, or <strong>chloramphenicol</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Chemoprophylaxis </strong>with<strong> rifampicin/ceftriaxone/ciprofloxacin</strong> is recommended for all close contacts with <strong>meningococcal</strong> meningitis regardless of age or immunization status. <strong>Rifampicin prophylaxis</strong> should be given to all <strong>household contacts</strong> of patients with invasive disease caused by<strong> Haemophilus influenzae type b</strong>, if an <strong>immunocompromised child</strong> of any age resides in the household or close family member <strong>younger than 48 months</strong> has<strong> not</strong> been<strong> fully immunized</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5249","text":"A 2-year-old child with a ‌history of fall 1 year ba‌ck with parietal bone fra​cture, has now presented ‌with a painful and growin‌g parietal swelling. What​ is the likely diagnosis?​","raw_text":"A 2-year-old child with a history of fall 1 year back with parietal bone fracture, has now presented with a painful and growing parietal swelling. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Growing sca‌lp hematoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Growing ‌fracture","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Subdural‌ hygroma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic‌ abscess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Growing fracture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Growing fracture</strong></p><p>The clinical presentation of a child with a <strong>painful and growing parietal swelling</strong>, coupled with a history of a <strong>prior parietal bone fracture</strong> one year ago, strongly suggests the diagnosis of a <strong>growing fracture, </strong>also known as <strong>traumatic encephalocele or leptomeningeal cysts</strong>.</p>\n<p>Skull fractures associated with a dural tear may fail to heal properly. The herniated brain, dilated ventricles, or a leptomeningeal cyst may cause an enlargement of this tear. These fractures are called <strong>growing skull fractures. </strong>They are usually seen in children <strong>&lt; 3 years</strong> of age.</p>\n<p>They present as a <strong>localized swelling or palpable</strong> <strong>skull defect</strong> that increases in size, most commonly seen in the <strong>parietal region</strong>. However, they may go <strong>undetected for years</strong> and present with delayed onset of neurologic symptoms (headache, seizure, or neurologic deficit). </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: The extended duration of one year makes scalp hematoma unlikely, as it usually resolves within a few months.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Subdural hygromas</strong> are characterized by fluid accumulation in the subdural space. Most patients with subdural hygromas are asymptomatic, but uncommon symptoms may include headaches, changes in mental status, nausea and vomiting, focal neurological deficits, and seizures. They don't present with scalp swelling.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Chronic abscess</strong> may show <strong>inflammatory</strong> changes like <strong>erythema</strong>, pus, sinus, and systemic features like<strong> fever</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5248","text":"You have been called to de‌clare the brain death of ‌a 12-year-old child in th​e ICU. Which of the follo‌wing is not seen in brain‌ death?","raw_text":"You have been called to declare the brain death of a 12-year-old child in the ICU.  Which of the following is not seen in brain death?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal BP without pharmaco‌logical support","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Positive spinal reflexes o‌n stimulation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sweating and‌ tachycardia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decorticate and decerebrat‌e posturing","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Decorticate and decerebrate posturing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Decorticate and decerebrate posturing</strong></p><p><strong>Decerebrate </strong>or<strong> decorticate posturing </strong>is not seen in brain death as they are <strong>motor responses to noxious stimuli</strong> that originate from the midbrain and cortex. </p>\n<p>Brain death, also known as death by neurologic criteria refers to the irreversible and total <strong>cessation</strong> of all functions of the <strong>entire brain</strong>, including the <strong>brainstem</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>three components</strong> for diagnosing brain death are a demonstration of coexisting <strong>irreversible coma</strong> with a known cause, <strong>absence of brain stem reflexes</strong>, and<strong> apnea</strong>. Coma requires the patient to be <strong>unresponsive</strong>, even to noxious stimuli. The <strong>diagnosis of brain death</strong> can <strong>still be made</strong> <strong>despite positive spinal reflexes. </strong>(option B)</p>\n<p><strong>Brainstem reflexes</strong> (pupillary responses, oculocephalic reflex, oculovestibular responses, corneal reflex, jaw reflex, facial grimacing, pharyngeal gag reflex, coughing) are <strong>absent</strong>. <strong>Apnea</strong> is the absence of respiratory drive at PaCO2 of ≥60mmHg and &gt; 20mmHg above baseline, which is consistent with brain death.</p>\n<p>The following manifestations are occasionally seen and should not be misinterpreted as evidence of brainstem function: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal systolic blood pressure<strong> - SBP &gt;100 mmHg </strong>(option A)</li>\n<li>Movements originating from the spinal cord or peripheral nerve may occur</li>\n<li><strong>Sweating,</strong> flushing, <strong>tachycardia </strong>(option C)</li>\n<li>Deep tendon reflexes; superficial abdominal reflexes; triple flexion response</li>\n<li><strong>Babinski reflex</strong></li>\n<li>Spontaneous movements of limbs other than pathologic flexion or extension response</li>\n<li>Respiratory-like movements (shoulder elevation and adduction, back-arching, intercostal expansion without significant tidal volumes)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"nk3liDer","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"29_non_hodgkin_lymphomas__general_considerations","title":"29.Non Hodgkin Lymphomas  General Considerations","num_questions":8,"total_marks":32,"duration":18,"time_per_question":135.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC0197","text":"Which of the areas in the ‍histology of lymph nodes ​show small-unchallenged B​ cells surrounding pale-s​taining germinal centres?​","raw_text":"Which of the areas in the histology of lymph nodes show small-unchallenged B cells surrounding pale-staining germinal centres?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Germina‍l center","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Margin‍al zone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mantl‍e zone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Paracort‍ical area","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mantle zone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mantle zone</strong></p><p>The <strong>mantle zone</strong> of the lymph nodes shows small, unchallenged B cells surrounding pale-staining germinal centres.</p>\n<p>Structures in a follicle:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Germinal centre </strong>-<strong> </strong>round/oval zone containing pale-staining cells surrounded by darker cells.</li>\n<li><strong>Mantle zone </strong>- small, unchallenged B cells surrounding pale-staining germinal centres.</li>\n<li><strong>Marginal zone </strong>- Light zone surrounding follicles; contains post-follicular memory B cells derived after stimulation of recirculating cells from T-cell-dependent antigen.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9197","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a B lymphocyte marker​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a B lymphocyte marker?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD‍10","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CD‍20","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD‍23","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD‍13","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CD13","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CD13</strong></p><p><strong>CD13</strong> is an antigen of<strong> monocyte/macrophage</strong> and<strong> </strong>not B-cell.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9168","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is false regardin​g Non-Hodgkin lymphoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding Non-Hodgkin lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It involves multiple perip‍heral lymph nodes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mesenteric nodes and Walde‍yer ring are rarely invol​ved","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Extra-nodal involvement is‍ common","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Noncontiguous spread occur‍s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mesenteric nodes and Waldeyer ring are rarely involved","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mesenteric nodes and Waldeyer ring are rarely involved</strong></p><p><strong>Mesenteric nodes</strong> and <strong>Waldeyer ring</strong> are commonly involved in non-Hodgkin lymphoma (<strong>NHL</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9170","text":"Which is the most common n‍on-Hodgkin lymphoma in ad​ults?","raw_text":"Which is the most common non-Hodgkin lymphoma in adults?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Burkitt's‍ lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diffuse large B-cell lymph‍oma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"MALT l‍ymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Follicula‍r lymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma</strong></p><p><strong>Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma</strong> (DLBCL) is the most common form of non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) in adults and children over the age of 15 years, worldwide and in India.</p>\n<p><strong>Burkitt's lymphoma</strong> is the most common NHL in <strong>children</strong> <strong>under</strong> the age of <strong>15</strong> years.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9235","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a B-cell neoplasm?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a B-cell neoplasm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Follicula‍r lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Burkitt ‍lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anaplastic large-cell Lymp‍homa","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mantle cel‍l lymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anaplastic large-cell Lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anaplastic large-cell Lymphoma</strong></p><p><strong>Anaplastic large cell</strong> lymphoma is a <strong>T-cell lymphoma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9219","text":"Which of the following is ‍the most common site for ​primary extranodal lympho​ma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common site for primary extranodal lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spl‍een","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sto‍mach","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inte‍stine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Br‍ain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stomach","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stomach</strong></p><p>The most common site for primary extranodal lymphoma is the <strong>stomach</strong>. </p>\n<p>The term <strong>primary extranodal</strong> non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma (PE-NHL) refers to <strong>lymphomas</strong> that present with the disease at any organ or tissue <strong>other than lymph nodes</strong> or <strong>spleen.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Gastrointestinal</strong> lymphoma is the <strong>most common</strong> form of primary <strong>extranodal lymphoma</strong>, accounting for 30%–40% of cases. The most common site involved is the<strong> stomach </strong>(<strong>60%</strong>–<strong>75% </strong>of cases), followed by the small bowel, ileum, cecum, colon, and rectum.</p>\n<p>Note: The most common site for extranodal non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) in <strong>AIDS</strong> is <strong>Primary CNS lymphoma. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9234","text":"Which lymphoid neoplasm is‍ associated with the huma​n herpesvirus 8?","raw_text":"Which lymphoid neoplasm is associated with the human herpesvirus 8?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Burkitt ‍lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mantle zon‍e lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anaplastic large-cell lymp‍homa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Primary effu‍sion lymphoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Primary effusion lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Primary effusion lymphoma</strong></p><p><strong>Primary effusion lymphoma</strong> is associated with human herpesvirus 8 <strong>(HHV-8).</strong></p>\n<p>It is a rare type of diffuse large <strong>B cell</strong> lymphoma with<strong> lymphomatous effusions</strong> in pleural, pericardial, and abdominal cavities, but no tumor mass.</p>\n<p>Only one <strong>body cavity</strong> is typically involved. It is strongly associated with <strong>HHV8</strong> and<strong> advanced HIV</strong> and usually <strong>EBV</strong>. It can also occurs in non-immunosuppressed patients, often the elderly.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9173","text":"Which of the following is ‍correctly matched with it​s associated etiological ​factor? 1. Extranodal mar​ginal cell lymphoma a. HI​V infection 2. Intestinal​ T-cell lymphomas b. Brea‌st implants 3. Anaplastic​ large cell lymphoma c. H​.pylori infection 4. Lymp‌hoplasmacytic lymphoma d.‌ Gluten-sensitive enterop‍athy 5. Diffuse large B-c‌ell lymphoma e. Hepatitis‌ C infection","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is correctly matched with its associated etiological factor?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 128px;\" width=\"413\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198.5px;\">1. Extranodal marginal cell lymphoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 198.5px;\">a. HIV infection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198.5px;\">2. Intestinal T-cell lymphomas</td>\n<td style=\"width: 198.5px;\">b. Breast implants</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198.5px;\">3. Anaplastic large cell lymphoma </td>\n<td style=\"width: 198.5px;\">c. H.pylori infection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198.5px;\">4. Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 198.5px;\">d. Gluten-sensitive enteropathy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 198.5px;\">5. Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 198.5px;\">e. Hepatitis C infection </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-b, 2-c, 3‍-e, 4-a, 5-d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-d, 2-e, 3‍-c, 4-d, 5-b","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-e, 2-a, 3‍-d, 4-b, 5-c","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-c, 2-d, 3‍-b, 4-e, 5-a","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1-c, 2-d, 3-b, 4-e, 5-a","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1-c, 2-d, 3-b, 4-e, 5-a</strong></p><p>The correctly matched neoplasms with their associated etiological factor:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Extranodal marginal cell lymphoma - H.pylori infection (c)</li>\n<li>Intestinal T-cell lymphomas - Gluten-sensitive enteropathy (d)</li>\n<li>Anaplastic large cell lymphoma - Breast implants (b)</li>\n<li>Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma - Hepatitis C infection (e)</li>\n<li>Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma - HIV infection (a)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"WcGml6St","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"29_drugs_for_alzheimer_s_disease_and_other_neurodegenerative_disorders","title":"29.Drugs For Alzheimer'S Disease And Other Neurodegenerative Disorders","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MA2907","text":"A 70-year-old male patient‍ is brought to the OPD by​ his son, with complaints‌ of increasing forgetfuln‌ess. Neuroimaging shows m‌arked cortical atrophy of​ temporal and parietal lo​bes. What is the most str‍iking neurochemical distu​rbance in this condition?‍","raw_text":"A 70-year-old male patient is brought to the OPD by his son, with complaints of increasing forgetfulness. Neuroimaging shows marked cortical atrophy of temporal and parietal lobes. What is the most striking neurochemical disturbance in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deficiency of acetylcholin‍e","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Deficiency ‍of dopamine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deficiency ‍of serotonin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deficiency ‍of glutamate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Deficiency of acetylcholine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Deficiency of acetylcholine</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Alzheimer's disease (AD)</strong>. The most striking neurochemical disturbance in AD is the <strong>deficiency of acetylcholine </strong>in the subcortical area (especially in the nucleus basalis of Meynert).</p>\n<p>Neurotransmitters like glutamate and 5-HT and other areas of the brain like the hippocampus are also involved in the pathogenesis of this disease.</p>\n<p>The drugs used in Alzheimer's disease are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Central acetylcholine esterase inhibitors</strong> targeting acetylcholine deficiency</li>\n<li><strong>NMDA antagonists</strong> targeting glutamate excitotoxicity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1927","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a reversible cholines​terase inhibitor indicate‌d in a patient with Alzhe‌imer's disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a reversible cholinesterase inhibitor indicated in a patient with Alzheimer's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Done‍pezil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rivast‍igmine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Galan‍tamine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mema‍ntine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Memantine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Memantine</strong></p><p><strong>Memantine</strong> is an <strong>NMDA receptor antagonist</strong> used to treat moderate to severe dementia in Alzheimer's.</p>\n<p>Donepezil, rivastigmine, and galantamine are reversible cholinesterase inhibitors indicated in Alzheimer's disease. Irreversible antagonists are not indicated in Alzheimer's disease. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1926","text":"A neurology resident chang‍ed the current oral medic​ation of a patient with A‌lzheimer's disease to a t‌ransdermal patch of a cho‌linesterase inhibitor. Wh​at must be the new drug t​hat was prescribed?","raw_text":"A neurology resident changed the current oral medication of a patient with Alzheimer's disease to a transdermal patch of a cholinesterase inhibitor. What must be the new drug that was prescribed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tac‍rine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Done‍pezil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rivast‍igmine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Galan‍tamine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rivastigmine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rivastigmine</strong></p><p><strong>Rivastigmine</strong> is a cholinesterase inhibitor that is available as a <strong>transdermal patch</strong> for use in Alzheimer's disease. It can be applied for over 24 hours. Transdermal delivery <strong>reduces the fluctuation</strong> and avoids direct gastric irritation effects associated with oral formulations. </p>\n<p>Option A: Tacrine is an anticholinesterase that is obsolete because of its association with hepatotoxicity. </p>\n<p>Options B and D: Donepezil and galantamine transdermal formulations are under development and <strong>yet to be available</strong> in the market.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2909","text":"Which of the following NMD‍A receptor antagonist wou​ld you prescribe to a pat‌ient with severe Alzheime‌r's disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following NMDA receptor antagonist would you prescribe to a patient with severe Alzheimer's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mema‍ntine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Keta‍mine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pira‍cetam","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amant‍adine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Memantine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Memantine</strong></p><p><strong>Memantine</strong> is a <strong>noncompetitive</strong> N-methyl-D-aspartate <strong>(NMDA) receptor antagonist </strong>indicated in Alzheimer's disease.<strong> </strong>It<strong> </strong>slows the deterioration of patients with moderate and severe Alzheimer's disease. It is claimed to reduce the excitotoxicity of glutamate.</p>\n<p>Option B - Ketamine is an NMDA receptor antagonist indicated as an anesthetic agent.</p>\n<p>Option C - Piracetam is a nootropic agent (a drug that enhances cognitive function) that is used off-label to enhance memory.</p>\n<p>Option D - Amantadine is an antiviral agent with NMDA receptor antagonistic action which is also indicated in Parkinson's disease due to its action on dopamine release and reuptake. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1929","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is incorrect rega​rding the management of A‌lzheimer's disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding the management of Alzheimer's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Monoclonal antibodies can ‍be used in the treatment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cholinesterase inhibitors ‍are commonly used","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Memantine is approved only‍ in moderate to severe di​sease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cholinesterase inhibitors ‍and memantine have no eff​ect on behavioral and psy‌chiatric symptoms","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cholinesterase inhibitors and memantine have no effect on behavioral and psychiatric symptoms","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cholinesterase inhibitors and memantine have no effect on behavioral and psychiatric symptoms</strong></p><p><strong>Cholinesterase inhibitors</strong> and <strong>memantine</strong> reduce some of the behavioral and psychiatric symptoms in Alzheimer's disease.</p>\n<p>SSRIs like citalopram or benzodiazepines for agitation and atypical antipsychotics for psychiatric symptoms may be considered in severe cases. But atypical antipsychotics in dementia-related psychosis have shown higher mortality. </p>\n<p>Option A - Amyloid beta-directed<strong> monoclonal antibodies</strong> approved for the treatment of Alzheimer's disease are - <strong>l</strong><strong>ecanemab, </strong><strong>donanemab, </strong>aducanumab </p>\n<p>Option B -<strong> Cholinesterase inhibitors</strong> are commonly used and are considered as first-line in <strong>early</strong> or <strong>mild to moderate stages of Alzheimer's disease.</strong> </p>\n<p>Option C -<strong> Memantine</strong> is approved only in <strong>moderate-severe Alzheimer's disease</strong>. There is not enough data to support its use in mild to moderate stages.<br/><br/>Note- Aducanumab has been discontinued in the treatment of Alzheimer's</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2902","text":"A 35-year-old lady came to‍ the OPD with complaints ​of occasional shock-like ‌sensations down her spine‌ and muscle spasms. T2 we‌ighted MRI shows typical ​Dawson fingers and oligoc​lonal bands were detected‍ in the CSF study. Which ​of the following is appro‍ved for the treatment of ​her condition?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old lady came to the OPD with complaints of occasional shock-like sensations down her spine and muscle spasms. T2 weighted MRI shows typical Dawson fingers and oligoclonal bands were detected in the CSF study.  Which of the following is approved for the treatment of her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interfer‍on alpha","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Interfe‍ron beta","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Interfer‍on gamma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infli‍ximab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Interferon beta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Interferon beta</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>multiple sclerosis. </strong><strong>Interferon beta</strong> is approved for use in this condition. It reduces the frequency of attacks in relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis.</p>\n<p>The probable mechanisms of action of interferon-beta are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Immune modulation and a decrease in T cell proliferation.</li>\n<li>Limiting the entry of T cells into CNS.</li>\n<li>Reducing pro-inflammatory and increasing regulatory cytokines.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2903","text":"You plan to start disease-‍modifying therapy in a 40​-year-old patient with re‌lapsing-remitting multipl‌e sclerosis. Which of the‌ following drugs would yo​u not include in the trea​tment regimen?","raw_text":"You plan to start disease-modifying therapy in a 40-year-old patient with relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis. Which of the following drugs would you not include in the treatment regimen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glatirame‍r acetate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methylpre‍dnisolone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fingo‍limod","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cladr‍ibine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Methylprednisolone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Methylprednisolone</strong></p><p>The disease-modifying therapy of relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis (MS) does not include <strong>methylprednisolone</strong>. It is used in<strong> acute attacks </strong>and<strong> exacerbations </strong>for MS symptom control<strong>. </strong>The long-term value or disease-modifying potential of glucocorticoid therapy is unknown. </p>\n<p>FDA approved disease-modifying therapy of relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Interferon-beta 1a </li>\n<li>Interferon-beta 1b </li>\n<li>Glatiramer </li>\n<li>Dimethyl fumarate </li>\n<li>Teriflunomide</li>\n<li>Alemtuzumab</li>\n<li>Natalizumab </li>\n<li>Fingolimod </li>\n<li>Mitoxantrone </li>\n<li>Cladribine </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2904","text":"John Cunningham (JC) virus‍ antibody titer is being ​measured in a patient wit‌h multiple sclerosis. Dur‌ing treatment with which ‌of the following drugs ar​e the titres mandatory?","raw_text":"John Cunningham (JC) virus antibody titer is being measured in a patient with multiple sclerosis. During treatment with which of the following drugs are the titres mandatory?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alemt‍uzumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Natal‍izumab","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Glati‍ramer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inter‍feron","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Natalizumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Natalizumab</strong></p><p><strong>John Cunningham (JC) virus antibody titre</strong> measurement is mandatory during treatment with monoclonal antibodies like<strong> natalizumab</strong>. Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy <strong>(PML)</strong> is a life-threatening disease caused by the JC virus in patients with multiple sclerosis.</p>\n<p>It is more common when the patient is <strong>immunocompromised</strong> during their treatment with natalizumab. The current recommendation is to start natalizumab only in a patient who tests negative for JC antibodies.</p>\n<p>Seroconversion can occur anytime during treatment. <strong>Regular monitoring</strong> for JC antibody<strong> every 6 months</strong> is recommended in JC antibody-negative patients on natalizumab.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2910","text":"A 30-year-old lady present‍s with complaints of sudd​en, involuntary, and unco‌ntrollable dance-like mov‌ements. Her mother had al‌so developed similar symp​toms at the age of 40. Wh​ich of the following drug‍s is indicated for the tr​eatment of the involuntar‍y movements associated wi​th her condition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old lady presents with complaints of sudden, involuntary, and uncontrollable dance-like movements. Her mother had also developed similar symptoms at the age of 40.  Which of the following drugs is indicated for the treatment of the involuntary movements associated with her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Benz‍hexol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tetrab‍enazine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tetrahyd‍razoline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Benzt‍ropine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tetrabenazine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tetrabenazine</strong></p><p>This patient is most likely suffering from <strong>chorea </strong>associated with<strong> Huntington's disease, </strong>for which <strong>tetrabenazine</strong> is indicated. It is a reversible inhibitor of vesicular monoamine transporter-2 (<strong>VMAT-2</strong>).</p>\n<p>Other drugs used in Huntington's disease are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reserpine</strong>, an irreversible inhibitor of VMAT-2 and a longer-acting agent as compared to tetrabenazine.</li>\n<li class=\"ng-binding\"><strong>Deutetrabenazine</strong> refers to deuterium attached to carbon in place of hydrogen, in tetrabenazine (deuterated tetrabenazine). It is a <strong>newer form </strong>of tetrabenazine approved for the treatment of chorea associated with Huntington's disease. It slows down its metabolism, hence it is <strong>longer acting than tetrabenazine</strong>. It is associated with better control of chorea.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A - Benzhexol is a central anticholinergic indicated in parkinsonism.</p>\n<p>Option C - Tetrahydrozoline is a topical alpha-adrenergic agonist indicated for the relief of conjunctival congestion.</p>\n<p>Option D - Benztropine is a muscarinic receptor antagonist that can be used for the management of acute dystonia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2895","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a mechanism of action​ of Riluzole?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a mechanism of action of Riluzole?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of glutamate re‍lease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibition of GABA reuptak‍e","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"NMDA glutamate receptor an‍tagonism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kainate glutamate receptor‍ antagonism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inhibition of GABA reuptake","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inhibition of GABA reuptake</strong></p><p>Riluzole does not act by causing inhibition of GABA reuptake.</p>\n<p><strong>Riluzole</strong> is the only drug known to <strong>improve survival </strong>in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (<strong>ALS</strong>), also known as Lou Gehrig's disease. The mechanism of action involves the <strong>reduction of glutamate-induced excitotoxicity</strong> by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inhibition of glutamate release</li>\n<li>Antagonism of NMDA and kainate-type glutamate receptor </li>\n<li>Direct action on the voltage-dependent sodium channels.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3440","text":"Which of the following new‍ drugs has been approved ​for the treatment of amyo‌trophic lateral sclerosis‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following new drugs has been approved for the treatment of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Edar‍avone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pira‍cetam","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dabra‍fenib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trame‍tinib","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Edaravone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Edaravone</strong></p><p><strong>Edaravone</strong> was approved by the FDA for the treatment of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (<strong>ALS</strong>).</p>\n<p>It has <strong>free radical scavenging properties</strong> that may reduce oxidative stress. The exact mechanism of action is unknown. It is to be administered as an IV infusion with an initial treatment cycle of daily dosing for 14 days, followed by a 14-day drug-free period. Subsequent treatment cycles consist of dosing on 10 of 14 days, followed by a 14 day drug-free period.</p>\n<p>The major adverse effect of edaravone is hypersensitivity because the infusion contains sodium bisulfite. Other adverse effects include bruising, gait disorder, and headache.</p>\n<p><strong>Tafinlar (dabrafenib)</strong> and <strong>Mekinist (trametinib)</strong>, are both indicated as monotherapy in BRAF V600E/K (BRAF V600E mutation-positive) melanoma. They are approved for combined administration, for the treatment of anaplastic thyroid cancer (ATC) that cannot be removed by surgery or that has spread to other parts of the body (metastatic).</p>\n<p><strong>Piracetam</strong> is a nootropic agent (a drug that enhances cognitive function) that is used off-label to enhance memory. It is also used to treat myoclonus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Xk8XQ3Ig","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"29_electrophysiology_of_the_heart","title":"29.Electrophysiology Of The Heart","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA3453","text":"The SA Node acts as the pa‌cemaker of the heart beca‍use, it ____.","raw_text":"The SA Node acts as the pacemaker of the heart because, it ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Generates impulse spontane‌ously","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Has rich sympathetic inner‌vation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Generates impulses at high‌est rate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Has poor cholinergic inner‌vation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Generates impulses at highest rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Generates impulses at highest rate</strong></p><p>The SA node acts as the pacemaker of the heart as it generates impulses at the <strong>highest rate (60-100/min)</strong>, compared to AV node (40-60/min) and Purkinje fibres (15-40). The impulses generated by SA node are conducted through AV node and purkinje fibres repetedly that it will prevent AV node or Purkinje fibres reach their own thresholds for self-excitation. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3454","text":"In which of the following ‌the conduction velocity i‍s least?","raw_text":"In which of the following the conduction velocity is least?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AV ‌node","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ventr‌icles","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bundle‌ of His","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Purkinj‌e fibers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. AV node","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. AV node</strong></p><p>The conduction velocity is least in AV node.</p>\n<p>The slow conduction in the AV node is caused mainly by <strong>diminished numbers of gap junctions</strong> between successive cells in the conducting pathways.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3455","text":"The velocity of conduction‌ of impulse is maximum in‍____.","raw_text":"The velocity of conduction of impulse is maximum in____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial‌ fibers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"SA ‌node","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AV ‌node","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Purkinj‌e fibers","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Purkinje fibers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Purkinje fibers</strong></p><p>The velocity of conduction of impulse is maximum in <strong>Purkinje fibers.</strong></p>\n<p>Purkinje fibers have the maximum number of gap junctions hence the maximum conduction velocity (4m/s).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3456","text":"The AV nodal delay is ___(‌seconds).","raw_text":"The AV nodal delay is ___(seconds).","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0‌.2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0‌.1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"0‌.5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.‌01","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 0.1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 0.1</strong></p><p>The AV nodal delay is <strong>0.1</strong> second.</p>\n<p>The slow conduction is caused mainly by <strong>diminished numbers of gap junctions</strong> between successive cells in the conducting pathways.It ensures that the atria have ejected their blood into the ventricles first before the ventricles contract.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9913","text":"If the SA node discharges ‌at 0.00 sec, when will th‍e action potential normal‍ly arrive at the epicardi‌al surface at the base of​ the left ventricle?","raw_text":"If the SA node discharges at 0.00 sec, when will the action potential normally arrive at the epicardial surface at the base of the left ventricle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.16‌ sec","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.18‌ sec","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.22‌ sec","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"0.12‌ sec","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 0.22 sec","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e6eb5ee6894e43aabd02e69e55cfa13ex1280x1661.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 0.22 sec</strong></p><p>The duration of action potential to arrive at the epicardial surface at the base of the left ventricle is <strong>0.22sec.</strong></p>\n<p>Impulse initiated from <strong>SA node</strong> at <strong>0.00s</strong></p>\n<p>                       ↓</p>\n<p>Impulse reaches <strong>AV node</strong> at <strong>0.03s</strong></p>\n<p>                       ↓</p>\n<p><strong>Delay of impulse</strong> at AV node by <strong>0.13s</strong> resulting in a total time of <strong>0.16s</strong> (0.03+0.13) to reach the <strong>beginning of Purkinje fibres</strong></p>\n<p>                       ↓</p>\n<p>Impulse reaches <strong>base of Purkinje fibres</strong> by <strong>0.03s</strong> resulting in a total of time of <strong>0.19s</strong> (0.16+0.0.3) to reach the <strong>endocardial surface of the base of the ventricle</strong></p>\n<p>                       ↓</p>\n<p>Impulse travels from endocardial to <strong>epicardial surface</strong> in <strong>0.03s</strong> (due to arrangement of myocardial fibres)</p>\n<p>                       ↓</p>\n<p>Hence, a <strong>total time</strong> of <strong>0.22s</strong> (0.19+0.03) is taken for the impulse to travel from SA node to epicardial surface of the base of ventricles</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3457","text":"The order of depolarizatio‌n after stimulation of Pu‍rkinje fibers is:","raw_text":"The order of depolarization after stimulation of Purkinje fibers is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Septum→ Endocardium→ Epica‌rdium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Endocardium→ Epicardium→ S‌eptum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epicardium→ Endocardium→ S‌eptum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Septum→ Epicardium→ Endoca‌rdium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Septum→ Endocardium→ Epicardium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ce6c3e499a5e42219abd34343749793fx1280x1284.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Septum→ Endocardium→ Epicardium</strong></p><p>The <strong>order</strong> of depolarization is from <strong>septum → endocardium→ epicardium.</strong></p>\n<p>The spread of Cardiac Excitation:</p>\n<p><strong>Atrial depolarization</strong> is initiated from SA node spreads radially through atria, then converges on the AV node. A delay of about 0.1s <strong>(AV nodal delay)</strong> occurs before excitation spreads to the ventricles.<br/><strong>Ventricular depolarization</strong> starts at the <strong>left side of the interventricular septum</strong> and moves first to the right across the midportion of the septum. It moves along the ventricular walls to the AV groove, proceeding from the<strong> endocardial to the epicardial surface. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9916","text":"Phases of the ventricular ‌muscle action potential a‍re shown below. At which ‍phase of the below image,‌ the action potential is ​most dependant on sodium ‌permeability?","raw_text":"Phases of the ventricular muscle action potential are shown below. At which phase of the below image, the action potential is most dependant on sodium permeability?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pha‌se 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pha‌se 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pha‌se 0","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pha‌se 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Phase 0","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ec96d89420c346bb9c50ac7cad6381e4.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2daf166599b04752a5dbada64ed526e1x800x600.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phase 0</strong></p><p>The action potential is most dependant on sodium permeability in phase 0. </p>\n<p>Action potential of myocardium:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phase 0: </strong><strong>Depolarisation: </strong>Influx of Na+ into myocyte.</li>\n<li><strong>Phase 1: <strong>Early repolarization</strong>: </strong>Efflux of K+ from myocyte.</li>\n<li><strong>Phase 2: </strong><strong>Plateau: </strong>Ca2+ enters myocardial cells via voltage regulated L-type Ca2+ channels.</li>\n<li><strong><strong>Phase 3: R</strong>epolarisation: </strong>Efflux of K+.</li>\n<li><strong><strong>Phase 4: R</strong>estoration of RMP: </strong>Restoration of Na+ and K+ by Na-K ATPase. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3451","text":"Plateau phase of ventricul‌ar muscle is due to openi‍ng of :","raw_text":"Plateau phase of ventricular muscle is due to opening of :","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Na ch‌annel","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ca ch‌annel","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"K ch‌annel","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cl ch‌annel","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ca channel","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8131866d467043559cf746a65cd83fd8x800x600.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ca channel</strong></p><p>Plateau phase of ventricular muscle is due to opening of Ca channel.</p>\n<p>Action potential of myocardium:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phase 0: </strong><strong>Depolarisation: </strong>Influx of Na+ into myocyte.</li>\n<li><strong>Phase 1: Early repolarization: </strong>Efflux of K+ from myocyte.</li>\n<li><strong>Phase 2: </strong><strong>Plateau: </strong>Ca2+ enters myocardial cells via voltage regulated L-type Ca2+ channels.</li>\n<li><strong>Phase 3: Repolarisation: </strong>Efflux of K+.</li>\n<li><strong>Phase 4: Restoration of RMP: </strong>Restoration of Na+ and K+ by Na-K ATPase. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3458","text":"Repolarization of cardiac ‌muscle proceeds from:","raw_text":"Repolarization of cardiac muscle  proceeds from:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left t‌o right","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endocardium ‌to epicardium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epicardium t‌o endocardium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Base to ‌the apex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Epicardium to endocardium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Epicardium to endocardium</strong></p><p>Repolarization of cardiac muscle proceeds from <strong>e</strong><strong>picardium to endocardium.</strong><strong><br/> <br/></strong>Depolarisation proceeds from <strong>endocardium to epicardium </strong>in an intact cardiac muscle.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3461","text":"Phase 0 of pacemaker poten‌tial is initiated by:","raw_text":"Phase 0 of pacemaker potential is initiated by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opening of voltage-gated N‌a+ channel","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Opening of voltage gated C‌a2+ channel","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inactivation of voltage ga‌ted Ca2+ channel","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inactivation of voltage ga‌ted Na+ channel","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Opening of voltage gated Ca2+ channel","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0e7be77bea084e11ba67d807fc860932x722x339.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Opening of voltage gated Ca2+ channel</strong></p><p><strong>Phase 0</strong> of pacemaker potential is initiated by <strong>opening of voltage-gated Ca2<sup>+</sup> channels.</strong></p>\n<p>The pacemaker potential consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phase 0</strong> - this is upstroke seen due to <strong>the opening of voltage-gated Ca2<sup>+</sup> channels.</strong> This causes <strong>an influx of calcium</strong> leading to the upstroke.</li>\n<li><strong>Phase 3</strong> - This is followed by inactivation of the calcium channels and <strong>simultaneous activation of K<sup>+</sup> channels</strong>. This leads to <strong>efflux of K<sup>+</sup></strong> bringing about repolarization.</li>\n<li><strong>Phase 4</strong>- As the K<sup>+</sup> efflux declines, a channel permeable to both Na<sup>+</sup> and K<sup>+</sup> opens leading to <strong>an influx of both Na<sup>+</sup> and K</strong><sup><strong>+</strong>. </sup>This is the <strong>I<sub>f</sub> channel</strong>. This is responsible for <strong>slow spontaneous diastolic depolarization. </strong>This accounts for the automaticity of SA and AV node.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The slope of Phase 4 in the SA node determines the heart rate.</p>\n<p>Note: Fast voltage-gated Na<sup>+</sup> channels seen in myocyte potential are permanently inactivated because of the less negative resting potential of the pacemaker cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3460","text":"You are researching the be‌nefits of vagus nerve sti‍mulation as a potential t‍reatment for epilepsy. Wh‌ich of the following para​meters can be increased d‌ue to vagal stimulation?","raw_text":"You are researching the benefits of vagus nerve stimulation as a potential treatment for epilepsy. Which of the following parameters can be increased due to vagal stimulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heart‌ rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"R-R inter‌val in ECG","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac‌ output","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Force of c‌ontraction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. R-R interval in ECG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/16321802a6bb4541b68dcbee8541b7bex1020x1008.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. R-R interval in ECG</strong></p><p>Vagal stimulation can cause an<strong> increase in the R-R interval </strong>in the ECG<strong> </strong>as it decreases heart rate.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3462","text":"In recording Lead I, the r‌ight arm is negative elec‍trode and the positive el‍ectrode is","raw_text":"In recording Lead I, the right arm is negative electrode and the positive electrode is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left‌ arm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Left‌ leg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Righ‌t leg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left arm ‌+ left leg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Left arm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0598488cbdae4bd586798d0fa6b6353dx1280x1142.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Left arm</strong></p><p>In recording Lead I, the right arm is negative electrode and the positive electrode is<strong> left arm.</strong></p>\n<p> <strong>Bipolar Limb leads for ECG recording</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"134\">\n<p><strong>Lead type</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"137\">\n<p><strong>Positive input (+)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"138\">\n<p><strong>Negative input (-)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"134\">\n<p>Lead I</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"137\">\n<p>LA</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"138\">\n<p>RA</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"134\">\n<p>Lead II</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"137\">\n<p>LL</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"138\">\n<p>RA</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"134\">\n<p>Lead III</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"137\">\n<p>LL</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"138\">\n<p>LA</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>LA → left arm</li>\n<li>RA →right arm</li>\n<li>LL→ left foot</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3463","text":"Einthoven la‌w states that","raw_text":"Einthoven law states that","options":[{"label":"A","text":"II=I‌+III","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"I=II‌+III","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"III=‌I+II","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Any of t‌he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. II=I+III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. II=I+III</strong></p><p>Einthoven's law states <strong>Lead I + Lead III = Lead II.</strong></p>\n<p>Einthoven's triangle is an imaginary formation of three limb leads in a triangle used in electrocardiography.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3464","text":"In Einthoven’s triangle, w‌hat is the value of lead ‍III when lead I = 1mV and‍ lead II = 2mV ?","raw_text":"In Einthoven’s triangle, what is the value of lead III when lead I = 1mV and lead II = 2mV ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1</strong></p><p>According to <strong>Einthoven's law, </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Lead I + Lead III=Lead II.</strong></p>\n<p>Lead III = Lead II – Lead I = (2-1) =1 mV.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3465","text":"In a standard ECG, an augm‌ented limb lead measures ‍the electrical potential ‍difference between:","raw_text":"In a standard ECG, an augmented limb lead measures the electrical potential difference between:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 l‌imbs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 limb and 2‌ other limbs","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"One limb a‌nd neutral","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 limbs and ‌2 other limbs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1 limb and 2 other limbs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/65749e559cd3406fb0f0d36bea502748x1280x1581.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1 limb and 2 other limbs</strong></p><p>In a standard ECG, an <strong>augmented</strong> <strong>lead</strong> measures the electrical potential difference between <strong>one</strong> <strong>limb</strong> <strong>and</strong> the <strong>other</strong> <strong>two</strong> <strong>limbs. aVR, aVL, aVF </strong>are the augmented  limb leads.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3466","text":"Which part of the ECG corr‌esponds to ventricular re‍polarization?","raw_text":"Which part of the ECG corresponds to ventricular repolarization?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The P‌ wave","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The QRS ‌duration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The T‌ wave","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The U‌ wave","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The T wave","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/29cae3bcc71140748265baf91b590255x1280x1458.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The T wave</strong></p><p>The part of the ECG that corresponds to <strong>ventricular</strong> <strong>repolarization</strong> is <strong>T</strong> <strong>wave.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 308px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 89px;\">\n<p><strong>ECG waves</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">\n<p><strong>Events in the heart</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 89px;\">\n<p>P wave</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">\n<p>Atrial depolarization</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 89px;\">\n<p>QRS complex</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">\n<p>Ventricular depolarization</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 89px;\">\n<p>T wave</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">\n<p>Ventricular repolarization</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 89px;\">\n<p>U wave</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207px;\">\n<p>Repolarization of ventricular myocytes with long action potential</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3467","text":"The PR interval indicates ‌____.","raw_text":"The PR interval indicates ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial dep‌olarisation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular repolarization‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AV node con‌duction time","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both A‌ and C","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Both A and C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3552e25c9854deea1b6b66504ba807ax1279x1458.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Both A and C</strong></p><p>The PR interval indicates atrial depolarisation(P wave) and AV node conduction time(PR segment).</p>\n<p>PR interval is the time between the beginning of the P wave and the beginning of the QRS complex. It is the interval between the beginning of electrical excitation of the atria and the beginning of excitation of the ventricles. Normal duration is <strong>0.12-0.20s.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3468","text":"The P wave is absent in __‌_.","raw_text":"The P wave is absent in ___.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial fi‌brillation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Atrial ‌flutter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‌CF","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"W‌PW","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Atrial fibrillation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0d09156f5e9e4646a70c28a2b260ae24x800x389.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Atrial fibrillation</strong></p><p>The <strong>P</strong> <strong>wave</strong> is <strong>absent</strong> in <strong>atrial</strong> <strong>fibrillation</strong>.</p>\n<p>In atrial fibrillation, numerous small depolarization waves spread in all directions through the atria. The waves are weak and many of them are of opposite polarity at any given time, they almost electrically neutralize one another. <strong>Therefore, P waves are absent from ECG in atrial fibrillation.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9914","text":"The normal mean QRS axis l‌ies between ______","raw_text":"The normal mean QRS axis lies between ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"+30° an‌d +100°.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"–30° an‌d -100°.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"–30° an‌d +110°","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"–30° an‌d -110°","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. –30° and +110°","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. –30° and +110°</strong></p><p>The normal mean QRS axis lies between –30° and +110°.</p>\n<p>The QRS axis represents the major vector of ventricular activation, which is the overall direction of electrical activity.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3459","text":"The sympathetic innervatio‌n to AV node is by___.","raw_text":"The sympathetic innervation to AV node is by___.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right Thora‌cic ganglion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left thorac‌ic ganglion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right stell‌ate ganglion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left stella‌te ganglion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Left stellate ganglion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Left stellate ganglion</strong></p><p>The sympathetic innervation to AV node is by Left stellate ganglion.</p>\n<p>Innervation of SA Node and AV Node:</p>\n<p>The <strong>SA node</strong> develops from structures on the <strong>right side</strong> of the embryo and the <strong>AV node</strong> from structures on the <strong>left</strong>.Therefore -</p>\n<table style=\"width: 297px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\">\n<p><strong>ANS</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">\n<p><strong>SA node</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">\n<p><strong>AV node</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\">\n<p>Parasympathetic</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">\n<p>Right vagus</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">\n<p>Left vagus</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\">\n<p>Sympathetic</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">\n<p>Right stellate ganglion</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">\n<p>Left stellate ganglion</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9908","text":"You are following up with ‌a patient on a sympathomi‍metic drug. Which of the ‍following effects is unli‌kely to be due to sympath​etic stimulation?","raw_text":"You are following up with a patient on a sympathomimetic drug. Which of the following effects is unlikely to be due to sympathetic stimulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased conduction veloc‌ity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased refractory perio‌d","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increased ‌heart rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased contractility of‌ heart","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increased refractory period","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increased refractory period</strong></p><p><strong>Sympathetic stimulation</strong> causes faster conduction, therefore <strong>decreased refractory period.</strong></p>\n<p>Effects of sympathetic stimulation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chronotropic</strong><strong>- </strong>It increases the rate of sinus nodal discharge.</li>\n<li><strong>Dromotropic- </strong>It increases the rate of conduction.</li>\n<li><strong>Bathmotropic- </strong>It increases the level of excitability in all portions of the heart. </li>\n<li><strong>Inotropic- </strong>It increases the force of contraction of all the cardiac musculature.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3472","text":"The AH interval on His Bun‌dle Electrocardiogram (HB‍E) corresponds to the con‍duction of _____ on ECG.","raw_text":"The AH interval on His Bundle Electrocardiogram (HBE) corresponds to the conduction of _____ on ECG.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Through His –Purkinje syst‌em","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"From AV node to bundle of ‌His","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Purkinje to ventricular fi‌bers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Through ventricular fibers‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. From AV node to bundle of His","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a14c30dc320e4b1fadb19e77546a273ex1280x973.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. From AV node to bundle of His</strong></p><p>The AH interval corresponds to conduction from AV node to bundle of His on an ECG.</p>\n<p><strong>His bundle electrogram (HBE)</strong></p>\n<p>The record of the electrical activity obtained with the catheter passed through a vein to the right side of the heart and placed close to the tricuspid valve is HBE.</p>\n<p>It shows 3 deflections as shown in the figure:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"90\">\n<p><strong>His bundle waves</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"171\">\n<p><strong>Description</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"90\">\n<p>A</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"171\">\n<p>Denotes AV node activation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"90\">\n<p>H</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"171\">\n<p>Denotes His bundle activation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"90\">\n<p>V</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"171\">\n<p>Denotes ventricular depolarization </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>With HBE and standard ECG leads, 3 intervals can be timed accurately</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p><strong>Interval </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"138\">\n<p><strong>Recording</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"139\">\n<p><strong>Corresponding event</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p>PA interval</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"138\">\n<p>Time from the first appearance of atrial depolarization to the A wave in HBE</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"139\">\n<p>Conduction time from SA node to AV node</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p>AH interval</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"138\">\n<p>From A wave to start of H spike</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"139\">\n<p>AV nodal conduction time</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p>HV interval</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"138\">\n<p>Time from start of H spike to start of QRS deflection in ECG</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"139\">\n<p>Conduction in Bundle of His and bundle branches</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"QtBxMqyF","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"29_autism_spectrum_disorder","title":"29.Autism Spectrum Disorder","num_questions":6,"total_marks":24,"duration":16,"time_per_question":160.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA4445","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is not true regar‍ding autism?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true regarding autism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Language impairment is a u​sual feature","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is never associated wit​h intellectual disability‍","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Symptoms present in the ea​rly developmental period","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patients may show an infle​xible adherence to routin‍es","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is never associated with intellectual disability","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is never associated with intellectual disability</strong></p><p><strong>Intellectual disability</strong> is seen in about <strong>30%</strong> of patients with autism.</p>\n<p><strong>Autism or autistic diorder</strong> is a pervasive developmental disorder. Features usually start <strong>before 3 years</strong> of age and is <strong>more common in boys</strong>. Autism is characterized by the <strong>2 core features</strong> of autism spectrum disorder: <strong>deficits in social communication</strong> and <strong>restricted, repetitive patterns</strong> of behavior, interests, or activities.</p>\n<p>In addition, autism is also usually marked by <strong>language impairment</strong>, which occurs early in the developmental period. However this is not a core feature.</p>\n<p><strong>Below-normal intelligence</strong> is seen in about 30% of children with autistic disorder. However, some autistic children have <strong>unusual abilities</strong> in some areas (e.g., exceptional memory or calculation skills). These are referred to as <strong>savant skills.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2638","text":"A 5-year-old boy is brough​t to the clinic by his pa‍rents for behavioral issu‍es at home and school. Wh‌ich of the following feat​ures in this child satisf‌ies the core diagnostic c​riteria for autism spectr​um disorder? He is fixate​d on a shiny red wrapper ​in the trash bin in the r‍oom. The mother reports t​hat he does not appear to‌ respond to affection. Th​e child constantly lines ‍up his toys. Reports from​ his school describe poor‌ language development. He​ is noted to have a mild ‌intellectual disability.","raw_text":"<p>A 5-year-old boy is brought to the clinic by his parents for behavioral issues at home and school. Which of the following features in this child satisfies the core diagnostic criteria for autism spectrum disorder?</p>\n<ol class=\"ol1\">\n<li class=\"li1\">He is fixated on a shiny red wrapper in the trash bin in the room.</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">The mother reports that he does not appear to respond to affection.</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">The child constantly lines up his toys.</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Reports from his school describe poor language development.</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">He is noted to have a mild intellectual disability.</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3 ​and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2 ​and 3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2, 4 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, 3,​ 4 and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 2 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 2 and 3</strong></p><p>Aberrant language development and intellectual disability are not part of the core diagnostic criteria for autism spectrum disorder, according to the DSM-5 update.</p>\n<p><strong>Core diagnostic impairments in</strong> <strong>autism spectrum disorders</strong> according to DSM-5 are collapsed into <strong>two domains</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Persistent deficits in social communication and social interaction across multiple contexts:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Deficits in <strong>social-emotional reciprocity</strong></li>\n<li>Deficits in <strong>nonverbal communicative behaviors</strong> (e.g. eye contact, body language, use of gestures, and facial expressions)</li>\n<li>Deficits in developing, maintaining, and understanding relationships (e.g. difficulties in sharing, imaginative play, or in making friends)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Restricted, repetitive patterns of behavior, interests, or activities, </strong>as manifested by<strong> at least two of the following:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stereotyped or repetitive</strong> motor movements, use of objects, or speech (e.g. simple motor stereotypies, lining up toys or flipping objects, echolalia)</li>\n<li>Inflexible adherence to routines, or ritualized patterns and behavior (e.g. extreme distress at small changes, difficulties with transitions, rigid thinking patterns)</li>\n<li>Highly restricted, <strong>fixated interests</strong> that are abnormal in intensity or focus (e.g, strong preoccupation with unusual objects)</li>\n<li>Hyper- or hypo-reactivity to sensory input (e.g., apparent indifference to pain/temperature, adverse response to specific sounds or textures, excessive smelling or touching of objects, visual fascination with lights or movement).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2643","text":"Which of the following is ​not associated with autis‍m spectrum disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not associated with autism spectrum disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Early development of hande​dness","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Precocio​us skills","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abnormal der​matoglyphics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inso​mnia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Early development of handedness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Early development of handedness</strong></p><p>Children with autism spectrum disorder do not show early handedness and lateralization and <strong>remain ambidextrous</strong> at an age when cerebral dominance is established in most children.</p>\n<p>Other findings that may be present in autism spectrum disorders (not core diagnostic criteria) include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Higher incidence of <strong>abnormal dermatoglyphics</strong> (e.g., fingerprints)</li>\n<li>Deficits in language development</li>\n<li>Intellectual disability</li>\n<li>Hypo/hyper-response to sensory stimuli (e.g., no response to loud sound/ keen response to ticking of a watch)</li>\n<li>Hyperactivity and inattention</li>\n<li><strong>Precocious skills</strong> (e.g., prodigious route memories or calculating abilities)</li>\n<li><strong>Insomnia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4447","text":"A 3-year-old girl is broug​ht to the OPD with featur‍es of hand wringing stere‍otyped movements, breath-‌holding spells and decele​ration of head growth aft‌er 6 months of age. She w​as also noted to have imp​aired language and social​ development. Which of th​e following genes is asso‍ciated with the above dis​ease?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old girl is brought to the OPD with features of hand wringing stereotyped movements, breath-holding spells and deceleration of head growth after 6 months of age. She was also noted to have impaired language and social development. Which of the following genes is associated with the above disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AST-1​ gene","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"MRCP-​9 gene","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MECP-​2 gene","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"RET- ​2 gene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. MECP-2 gene","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. MECP-2 gene</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a 3-year-old female with <strong>normal</strong> psychomotor development <strong>until 6 months</strong> followed by <strong>regression</strong> in development, <strong>stereotyped</strong> movements, and <strong>microcephaly</strong> is suggestive of <strong>Rett syndrome</strong>. The gene associated with Rett syndrome is the <strong>MECP-2 gene</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>X-linked dominant</strong> condition. It is caused by mutations in the methyl-CpG binding protein 2 or MECP2. This condition is extremely rare in males.</p>\n<p>Features of Rett Syndrome:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Apparently <strong>normal prenatal and perinatal period</strong> and apparently normal psychomotor development through the <strong>first 5 months</strong> and <strong>normal head circumference at birth</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Deceleration of head growth</strong> between 5 months and 4 years and <strong>loss of acquired purposeful hand skills</strong> between 5 and 30 months of age.</li>\n<li>Severe <strong>impairment of expressive and receptive language</strong>, together with severe psychomotor retardation.</li>\n<li><strong>Stereotyped midline hand movements</strong> (such as hand-wringing or \"hand-washing\") with an onset at or after the time when purposeful hand movements are lost.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2659","text":"A 4-year-old boy was broug​ht to the clinic by anxio‍us parents. The child was‍ completely normal until ‌a year ago when the paren​ts started noticing a gra‌dual deterioration in his​ cognition and social int​eractions. He also starte​d showing repetitive beha​viors. On examination, th‍e boy was not saying any ​word and was barely maint‌aining eye contact. What ​is the most likely diagno‍sis?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old boy was brought to the clinic by anxious parents. The child was completely normal until a year ago when the parents started noticing a gradual deterioration in his cognition and social interactions. He also started showing repetitive behaviors. On examination, the boy was not saying any word and was barely maintaining eye contact.  What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asperger'​s disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rett s​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aut​ism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Childhood Disintegrative D​isorder","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Childhood Disintegrative Disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Childhood Disintegrative Disorder</strong></p><p>Sudden <strong>developmental regression</strong> in a 4-year-old boy with impairment in the <strong>social</strong>, <strong>language</strong> and <strong>cognitive</strong> domains is suggestive of <strong>childhood disintegrative disorder (CDD).</strong></p>\n<p>CDD is also called <strong>Heller's syndrome</strong> or <strong>disintegrative psychosis</strong>. It is characterized by a significant developmental regression resulting in <strong>deterioration</strong> in<strong> behavioral</strong> and<strong> adaptive functioning</strong> including self-help skills with <strong>loss of language and social skills</strong> after a period of <strong>normal development </strong>for<strong> at least two years</strong>. It is also associated with features of dementia and significant intellectual disability. </p>\n<p>It is now included under autism spectrum disorder in the DSM-5 manual, and ICD-11 also.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2661","text":"A first-standard child was​ referred for evaluation ‍of suspected autism. Acco‍rding to his parents, he ‌has trouble with social i​nteractions and also has ‌certain abnormal repetiti​ve behaviors. He does not​ have any language defici​ts or intellectual disabi​lities. Which of the foll‍owing is the most likely ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"A first-standard child was referred for evaluation of suspected autism. According to his parents, he has trouble with social interactions and also has certain abnormal repetitive behaviors. He does not have any language deficits or intellectual disabilities. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rett s​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Asperger'​s disorder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pervasive developmental di​sorder, not otherwise spe‍cified","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Childhood disintegrative d​isorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Asperger's disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Asperger&#x27;s disorder</strong></p><p>In Asperger's disorder, there are <strong>no significant delays</strong> in<strong> language or cognitive development</strong>.</p>\n<p>Features of Asperger's disorder:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Impairment in social interaction and development - \n<ul>\n<li>Markedly abnormal nonverbal communicative gestures</li>\n<li>Failure to develop peer relationships at the expected level</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>They do not manifest with mental retardation</li>\n<li>Restricted interests and patterns of behaviour</li>\n<li>No significant delay in language</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"aHC8I4vT","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"29_benign_tumors_of_liver","title":"29.Benign Tumors Of Liver","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB7490","text":"What is the most common be​nign lesion of liver?","raw_text":"What is the most common benign lesion of liver?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heman​gioma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatic​ adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepa​toma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Focal nodula​r hyperplasia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hemangioma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hemangioma</strong></p><p><strong>Hemangiomas</strong> are the <strong>most common </strong>benign tumor of the liver.</p>\n<p>Focal nodular hyperplasia (<strong>FNH</strong>) is the<strong> second most common</strong> benign tumor of the liver.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7521","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is not true about‍ epithelioid hemangioma?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true about epithelioid hemangioma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Exposure to vinyl chloride​ is a risk factor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Treatment of choice is liv​er transplantation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Factor VIII staining is us​ed for diagnosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"More commo​n in males","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. More common in males","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. More common in males</strong></p><p>Epithelioid hemangioendothelioma is more <strong>common</strong> in <strong>females</strong>. </p>\n<p>Epithelioid hemangioendothelioma is a rare<strong> vascular tumour. </strong>The <strong>infantile</strong> variety is <strong>benign</strong>, while the <strong>adult</strong> variety is <strong>malignant</strong>. It may undergo <strong>malignant degeneration </strong>into angiosarcoma. </p>\n<p>It is related to exposure to<strong> vinyl chloride</strong> and oral contraceptive pills (<strong>OCPs</strong>).</p>\n<p>It can affect the lungs, liver, and bones. In the liver, it manifests as multiple<strong> bilateral hepatic masses</strong>. Extrahepatic metastases occur in approximately 25% of patients. <strong>Liver failure </strong>is a fatal complication.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypervascular enhancement</strong> is observed on<strong> CECT.</strong> A percutaneous biopsy can help differentiate from other tumours. <strong>Factor VIII staining</strong> differentiates it from other nonvascular tumours. </p>\n<p>Treatment is <strong>total</strong> <strong>hepatectomy</strong> with <strong>liver transplantation.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7492","text":"A 45-year-old woman was in​cidentally found to have ‍a lesion in the right lob‍e of the liver during an ‌abdominal ultrasound. CEC‍T showed the following fi‌nding. What is the most l‌ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman was incidentally found to have a lesion in the right lobe of the liver during an abdominal ultrasound. CECT showed the following finding. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatic​ adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Focal nodula​r hyperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hama​rtoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Heman​gioma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hemangioma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c31c534c8afe4025b2ac614e73de9dad.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/03d66aafc02646ada31691a768e2071ax512x230.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hemangioma</strong></p><p>The given CECT abdomen shows a well-defined hypodense lesion in the liver with <strong>asymmetrical nodular peripheral enhancement</strong> which is characteristic of <strong>hemangioma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hemangioma is a<strong> benign </strong>tumor composed of increased proliferation of<strong> blood vessels. </strong>It is the <strong>most common</strong> <strong>benign</strong> tumour of the liver and is mainly seen in <strong>women</strong> at a mean age of 45 years.</p>\n<p>Small capillary hemangiomas are of no clinical significance. They are usually solitary, &lt;5 cm in diameter, with equal occurrence in the right and left halves of the liver. However, <strong>giant hemangiomas</strong> of diameter <strong>&gt;5 cm </strong>require attention. </p>\n<p>They are most commonly<strong> asymptomatic</strong> and incidentally found during imaging studies. Large compressive masses may cause vague upper abdominal symptoms. The appearance of hepatic hemangioma in a contrast-enhanced CT scan is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Non-contrast phase</strong>: Well-defined hypodense lesion</li>\n<li><strong>Arterial phase</strong>: Peripheral nodular enhancement</li>\n<li><strong>Washout phase</strong>: Homogenous enhancement</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows early phase with peripheral high attenuation (a) and late phase with homogenous high attenuation (b).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7488","text":"Which of the following hep​atic lesions can be accur‍ately diagnosed with 99mT‍c-labelled red blood cell‌s when CT and MRI are una‍vailable?","raw_text":"Which of the following hepatic lesions can be accurately diagnosed with 99mTc-labelled red blood cells when CT and MRI are unavailable?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mesenchyma​l hamartoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heman​gioma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Focal nodula​r hyperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatic​ adenoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hemangioma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/933fabb1d161474d8949f25a9d01e10ax500x431.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hemangioma</strong></p><p class=\"page\" title=\"Page 801\"><strong>Hemangiomas</strong> can be accurately diagnosed with <strong>99mTc-labelled red cells </strong>when CT and MRI are unavailable.</p>\n<p class=\"page\" title=\"Page 801\">In hemangioma, an increase in uptake is observed in blood pool studies such as <strong>99mTc-labelled red cells </strong>and appear as photopenic regions. The nuclear imaging techniques for the diagnosis of hemangiomas are used only when high-quality<strong> CT </strong>and <strong>MRI </strong>are<strong> unavailable</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"page\" title=\"Page 801\"><strong>CECT</strong> is the<strong> investigation of choice</strong> wherein a typical peripheral nodular enhancement pattern is seen. </p>\n<p class=\"page\" title=\"Page 801\">On magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), hemangiomas are <strong>hypointense</strong> on <strong>T1</strong>-weighted images and <strong>hyperintense</strong> on <strong>T2</strong>-weighted images.</p>\n<p class=\"page\" title=\"Page 801\">The below MRI image shows the <strong>light bulb sign</strong> seen in hemangioma of the liver.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Percutaneous biopsy</strong> is potentially dangerous and inaccurate and, therefore, <strong>not recommended</strong>.</p>\n<p>Isotope scans used for the investigation of hepatobiliary disease are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Technetium-99m <strong>colloid</strong> scan - parenchymal diseases of the <strong>liver,</strong> such as cirrhosis and alcoholic hepatitis</li>\n<li>Technetium-99m hepatobiliary iminodiacetic acid (<strong>HIDA</strong>) scan - <strong>acute cholecystitis</strong> and congenital biliary atresia</li>\n<li>Technetium-99m <strong>sulphur</strong> colloid scan - focal nodular hyperplasia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7496","text":"A 2-year-old boy is brough​t to the ER with complain‍ts of petechiae, bruising‍, and abdominal distensio‌n as shown in the image. ‍Investigations reveal thr‌ombocytopenia and elevate‌d D-dimer. CECT shows sol‌itary growth in the liver​ with peripheral nodular ‍enhancement. Which of the​ following statements is ‍false regarding this cond‌ition?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old boy is brought to the ER with complaints of petechiae, bruising, and abdominal distension as shown in the image. Investigations reveal thrombocytopenia and elevated D-dimer. CECT shows solitary growth in the liver with peripheral nodular enhancement. Which of the following statements is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kasai procedure is done fo​r symptomatic cases","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Small lesions regress spon​taneously","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"LFT and tumour markers are​ normal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Congestive heart failure c​an occur","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Kasai procedure is done for symptomatic cases","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d4fe40a06610425a8ac843206d5930b3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Kasai procedure is done for symptomatic cases</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> Kasabach-Merritt syndrome</strong> because of <strong>hemangioma</strong> of the liver.<strong> </strong>Kasai procedure or hepatoportoenterostomy is done for biliary atresia, and not in Kasabach-Merritt syndrome.</p>\n<p>Rarely a rapidly enlarging hemangioma can cause <strong>thrombocytopenia </strong>and <strong>disseminated intravascular coagulation</strong> (indicated by the elevated D-dimer) because platelets and other clotting factors are being used up within the tumour. This is called <strong>Kasabach-Merritt syndrome.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1483\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>LFT</strong> and <strong>tumour markers</strong> are<strong> normal</strong> in a hemangioma. Large hemangiomas in children can result in <strong>congestive heart failure</strong> secondary to <strong>arteriovenous shunting </strong>and they require medical management.</p>\n<p>Management:</p>\n<p><strong>Observation</strong> is the mode of management in <strong>asymptomatic</strong> liver hemangiomas that are &lt;5cm because they may show <strong>spontaneous regression.</strong> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Therapeutic <strong>embolisation</strong> and <strong>enucleation</strong> with arterial inflow control is the treatment of choice for patients with <strong>symptomatic</strong> liver hemangiomas. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7476","text":"A 32-year-old woman presen​ted with abdominal pain a‍nd discomfort for the pas‍t 2 months. She has been ‌using oral contraceptive ‍pills for more than 5 yea‌rs. Imaging studies revea‌led a mass as shown in th‌e image. Which of the fol​lowing is false regarding‍ this condition?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old woman presented with abdominal pain and discomfort for the past 2 months. She has been using oral contraceptive pills for more than 5 years. Imaging studies revealed a mass as shown in the image. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rupture and intraperitonea​l hemorrhage is unlikely","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Malignant transformation i​s seen in 10% of cases","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Histology shows cords of h​epatocytes with absence o‍f bile ductules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"More commo​n in women","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rupture and intraperitoneal hemorrhage is unlikely","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8a6cf742ef164c119875d60cf525c4af.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1e8e853aeecf48a284294d4271612afdx800x1200.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rupture and intraperitoneal hemorrhage is unlikely</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and MRI showing homogeneous mass with well-defined margins is suggestive of <strong>hepatic adenoma. Rupture</strong> leading to life-threatening<strong> intraperitoneal hemorrhage</strong> is a major complication associated with hepatic adenomas.</p>\n<p>Hepatic adenomas are <strong>benign solid</strong> neoplasms of the liver. They are usually seen in <strong>young women</strong> between 20-40 years of age and are typically solitary. Prolonged<strong> oral contraceptive </strong>use is a clear<strong> risk factor</strong>. Clinical presentation may be <strong>abdominal pain</strong>. </p>\n<p>Histological studies show cords of benign hepatocytes containing excessive glycogen and fat, giving a congested or vacuolated appearance. There is an <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>bile duct glands</strong> and <strong>Kupffer cells.</strong></p>\n<p>In some patients, hepatic adenomas present with spontaneous intraperitoneal hemorrhage. There is a risk of <strong>malignant</strong> transformation in <strong>10%</strong> of cases.</p>\n<p>The histopathological image given below shows hepatic adenoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7477","text":"Which of the following typ​es of hepatic adenomas is‍ associated with an incre‍ased risk of malignant tr‌ansformation?","raw_text":"Which of the following types of hepatic adenomas is associated with an increased risk of malignant transformation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sonic hedgehog activated a​denoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inflammatory type hepatic ​adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HNF1A muta​ted adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Beta-catenin mutated adeno​ma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Beta-catenin mutated adenoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Beta-catenin mutated adenoma</strong></p><p><strong>β-catenin mutated subtype </strong>of hepatic adenoma is associated with<strong> </strong>an increased risk of <strong>malignant transformation</strong>.</p>\n<p>The 2017 <strong>Bordeaux</strong> molecular-pathologic<strong> classification</strong> of hepatocellular adenomas (HCA) includes six subtypes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>HNF1A-inactivated HCA </li>\n<li>Inflammatory HCA </li>\n<li>β-catenin exon 3 mutated HCA</li>\n<li>β-catenin exon 7/8 mutated HCA </li>\n<li>Sonic hedgehog activated HCA </li>\n<li>Unclassified HCA</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7479","text":"During USG in a 30-year-ol​d bodybuilder on anabolic‍ steroids, a liver mass w‍as noted. The surgeon sus‌pects hepatic adenoma. Wh‍ich of the following is f‌alse regarding investigat‌ions performed for this c‌ondition?","raw_text":"During USG in a 30-year-old bodybuilder on anabolic steroids, a liver mass was noted. The surgeon suspects hepatic adenoma. Which of the following is false regarding investigations performed for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AFP levels are always elev​ated","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Imaging shows a well circu​mscribed vascular tumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Resection and biopsy are d​one when other investigat‍ions are equivocal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MRI with gadobenate dimegl​umine as contrast helps t‍o distinguish from other ‍tumors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. AFP levels are always elevated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. AFP levels are always elevated</strong></p><p><strong>AFP levels</strong> are usually within the <strong>normal</strong> range in hepatic adenoma. </p>\n<p>In this patient, the use of<strong> anabolic steroids</strong> is a risk factor for developing hepatic adenoma. </p>\n<p>Computed tomography (CT) or magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) demonstrates a <strong>well-circumscribed </strong>and<strong> vascular solid tumor. </strong>Arterial phase CT demonstrates a well-developed <strong>peripheral arterialization </strong>of a tumor.</p>\n<p>Dynamic magnetic resonance imaging with<strong> liver-specific MRI contrast agents</strong> such as <strong>gadobenate dimeglumine</strong> helps to better distinguish hepatic adenomas from follicular nodular hyperplasia by their enhancement characteristics during the hepatobiliary phase of imaging.</p>\n<p>A biopsy may be necessary for the confirmation and characterization of the nature of these lesions when imaging studies are equivocal.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7513","text":"Spontaneous rupture of the​ liver occurs in ______.","raw_text":"Spontaneous rupture of the liver occurs in ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatic​ adenoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Heman​gioma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Portal hy​pertension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Focal nodula​r hyperplasia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hepatic adenoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hepatic adenoma</strong></p><p>Hepatic adenoma can manifest as a case of <strong>spontaneous rupture</strong>, with the sudden onset of abdominal pain followed by hypovolemic shock secondary to intraperitoneal bleeding.</p>\n<p>The<strong> clinical presentation</strong> may be<strong> abdominal pain. </strong>In some cases, it can hepatic adenomas present with spontaneous intraperitoneal hemorrhage.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7483","text":"Which statement regarding ​the management of hepatic‍ adenoma is false?","raw_text":"Which statement regarding the management of hepatic adenoma is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MRI surveillance is done f​or 5 years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatic artery embolizatio​n is done in cases of hem‍orrhage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"In women, the risk of mali​gnant transformation is l‍ow if tumour &amp;lt;10 cm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"In men, resection is done ​independent of the tumour‍ size","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. In women, the risk of malignant transformation is low if tumour <10 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. In women, the risk of malignant transformation is low if tumour &lt;10 cm</strong></p><p>In women, the <strong>risk</strong> of <strong>malignant</strong> transformation is <strong>low</strong> if tumour <strong>&lt;5 cm</strong>.</p>\n<p>Due to the <strong>high risk of malignancy</strong> in <strong>males</strong>, hepatic adenomas should be <strong>resected independent of the tumour size</strong> or subtype of the lesion.</p>\n<p>In <strong>female</strong> patients, conservative management with <strong>discontinuation </strong>of<strong> oral contraceptive pills</strong> and MRI surveillance is recommended if the tumour is &lt;5 cm in diameter. Either <strong>annual </strong>(non-inflammatory adenoma) or <strong>biannual </strong>(inflammatory hepatocellular adenoma) MRI surveillance is done for at least 5 years, depending on the tumour subtype.</p>\n<p>If the tumour is &gt;5 cm, resection is done. In patients presenting with acute hemorrhage, <strong>hepatic artery embolization</strong> is done to prevent further hemorrhage till the patient can be taken up for resection of the tumour.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7487","text":"Which of the following sta​tements are false regardi‍ng focal nodular hyperpla‍sia?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements are false regarding focal nodular hyperplasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Resection is done for pers​istent symptomatic cases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"AFP levels​ are normal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Appears as hotspots in Tc ​99m sulphur colloid scan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased risk of malignan​t transformation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Increased risk of malignant transformation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Increased risk of malignant transformation</strong></p><p>Malignant transformation is unlikely in focal nodular hyperplasia.</p>\n<p>Contrast-enhanced CT is diagnostic for FNH. It is seen as a well-circumscribed lesion with a<strong> typical central scar</strong>. They show<strong> intense</strong> <strong>homogeneous enhancement</strong> on the arterial phase and isodense as compared to the background liver on the venous phase.</p>\n<p>Due to the presence of Kupffer cells, FNH appears as <strong>hotspots</strong> on <strong>Tc 99m sulphur colloid scans.</strong></p>\n<p>Asymptomatic patients with typical radiologic features do not require treatment. <strong>Careful observation</strong> of symptomatic FNH with <strong>serial imaging </strong>is reasonable, as symptoms may resolve in a significant number of cases.</p>\n<p>Patients with <strong>persistent symptomatic FNH</strong> or an enlarging mass should be considered for <strong>resection.</strong></p>\n<p>If diagnostic uncertainty exists, resection may be necessary for histologic confirmation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7484","text":"A 27-year-old woman was in​cidentally found to have ‍a liver mass while underg‍oing ultrasound for renal‌ calculi. CECT showed the‍ following image. What is‌ the most probable diagno‌sis?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old woman was incidentally found to have a liver mass while undergoing ultrasound for renal calculi. CECT showed the following image. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heman​gioma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Focal nodula​r hyperplasia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hepa​toma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatic​ adenoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Focal nodular hyperplasia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b48a057be5ba4edf980bc0d34a5dd94c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f5bd6d344c6747b6a8084cc94b8f9593x1280x1480.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Focal nodular hyperplasia</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario with contrast-enhanced CT showing <strong>hypervascular lesion</strong> with a <strong>central non-enhancing scar </strong>points towards<strong> focal nodular hyperplasia (FNH).</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Focal nodular hyperplasia</strong> is the <strong>second most common</strong> benign tumour in the liver and is mostly seen in women. This condition is characterised by focal overgrowth of functional liver tissue and the supporting fibrous stroma.</p>\n<p>Patients are mostly asymptomatic and FNH is often an <strong>incidental </strong>finding. If patients are symptomatic, they usually complain of abdominal pain.</p>\n<p>On histological examination, <strong>cords of</strong> <strong>hepatocytes</strong> are seen with <strong>a</strong><strong>typical biliary epithelium</strong> scattered throughout. A <strong>central stellate scar</strong> containing a large artery branching out in a <strong>spoke-wheel pattern </strong>is also seen.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4043","text":"Which of the following is ​correctly matched regardi‍ng solid liver lesions an‍d their characteristic CE‌CT imaging findings? Lesi‍on Imaging finding 1. Hep‌atic hemangioma A. Presen‌ce of central scar with h‌yperdensity in the arteri​al phase 2. Focal nodular‍ hyperplasia B. Periphera​l nodular enhancement in ‍the early phase with fill‌ing in during the late ph​ase 3. Metastasis from co​lorectal cancer C. H yper‍vascular pattern with enh​ancement in late arterial​ phase; rapid washout dur‍ing the portal venous pha​se 4. Hepatocellular canc‍er D. Hypoattenuated multiple lesions","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is correctly matched regarding solid liver lesions and their characteristic CECT imaging findings?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 280px; width: 383.45px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\">Lesion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.45px;\">Imaging finding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\">1. Hepatic hemangioma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.45px;\"> A. Presence of central scar with hyperdensity in the arterial phase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\">2. Focal nodular hyperplasia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.45px;\"> B. Peripheral nodular enhancement in the early phase with filling in during the late phase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\">3. Metastasis from colorectal cancer</td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.45px;\"> C. H<span class=\"ILfuVd\"><span class=\"hgKElc\">ypervascular pattern with enhancement in late arterial phase; rapid washout during the portal venous phase</span></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\">4. Hepatocellular cancer</td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.45px;\"> D. Hypoattenuated multiple lesions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 - D, 2 - C, 3 - A, 4 - B​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 - B, 2 - A​, 3 - D, 4 -C","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1 - C, 2 - D, 3 - B, 4 - A​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 - D, 2 - A, 3 - B , 4 - ​C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1 - B, 2 - A, 3 - D, 4 -C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1 - B, 2 - A, 3 - D, 4 -C</strong></p><p>The correctly matched option is <strong>1 - B, 2 - A, 3 - D, 4 -C.</strong></p>\n<p>Differential diagnosis of solid liver lesions and their characteristic imaging findings:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 45.4167px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">Noncontrast ultrasound finding</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">Contrast-enhanced CT finding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>Benign lesions</strong></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\"><strong>Hepatic hemangioma</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Well demarcated homogenous hyperechoic mass</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypoechoic in presence of fatty infiltration of the liver</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Non-contrast phase: Well-defined hypodense lesion.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Arterial phase: <strong>Peripheral nodular enhancement in the early phase and filling in during the late phase</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Washout phase: Homogenous enhancement.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\"><strong>Focal Nodular hyperplasia </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Variable echogenicity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Central hyperechoic region (central scar)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Non-contrast phase: Hypo- or isodense with a <strong>central scar</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Arterial phase: <strong>Hyperdense</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Portal venous phase: Typically isodense</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">Hepatocellular adenoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Heterogenous, hyperechoic lesion</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A well-demarcated lesion with peripheral enhancement in the early phase</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Centripetal flow during the portal venous phase</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>Malignant lesions</strong></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\"><strong>Hepatocellular carcinoma</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Poorly defined margin with coarse and irregular internal echoes</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Hypervascular</strong> in the arterial phase</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Enhancement in the late arterial phase with <strong>rapid washout</strong> <strong>of contrast in portal venous phase.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">Intrahepatic cholangio carcinoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Homogenous hypoechoic mass</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A hypodense lesion with peripheral(rim) enhancement</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\"><strong>Liver metastasis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Usually <strong>multiple lesions</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Metastasis from adenocarcinoma is hypoechoic</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Metastasis from the colon, stomach, and pancreas usually shows <strong>lower attenuation</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypervascular metastasis (e.g Neuroendocrine tumor, RCC) appears as rapidly enhancing lesions in the arterial phase</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"aPKYv-ZY","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"30_muscles__neurovascular_anatomy_of_head___neck","title":"30.Muscles, Neurovascular Anatomy Of Head & Neck","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB4368","text":"Which of the following is ‌the action of the muscle ​inserted to the area mark‌ed below?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the action of the muscle inserted to the area marked below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Depresses and protrudes ma‌ndible","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Elevates and protrudes man‌dible","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Elevates and retracts mand‌ible","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Depresses and retracts man‌dible","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Depresses and protrudes mandible","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/34851639d167407c92c17a6f9bc5d4de.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/285afa1d5aa5496db3c39566c58b8ffcx1280x1107.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Depresses and protrudes mandible</strong></p><p>The marked area on the mandible is the <strong>pterygoid fovea</strong> where the<strong> lateral pterygoid</strong> muscle is inserted. The action of this muscle is <strong>depression, protrusion,</strong> and<strong> side to side movement of the jaw.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Lateral pterygoid muscle:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Origin - Upper head arises from the infratemporal surface and infratemporal crest of the greater wing of the sphenoid bone. The lower head arises from the <strong>lateral surface</strong> of the <strong>lateral pterygoid plate. </strong></li>\n<li>Insertion - It is inserted into the pterygoid fovea, the capsule of the temporomandibular joint, and the articular disc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7042","text":"What is the main action of‌ the muscle marked below?​","raw_text":"What is the main action of the muscle marked below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elev‌ation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Depre‌ssion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Protr‌usion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retra‌ction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Elevation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0f6e0ead5fb5461792ad01425dc23b52.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Elevation</strong></p><p>The muscle marked in the image is the<strong> temporalis </strong>and its main action is <strong>elevation of the mandible.</strong></p>\n<p>Other actions are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It helps in the side-to-side <strong>grinding</strong> movements of the mandible.</li>\n<li>The posterior fibres which are horizontal, help in the <strong>retraction</strong> of the mandible.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9150","text":"In the image given below, ‌identify the muscle respo​nsible for the opening of‌ the mouth.","raw_text":"In the image given below, identify the muscle responsible for the opening of the mouth.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cf50347e68964cd0901cbe0f38029669.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3227ac422404c6786295982bb2c744bx1280x1401.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. B</strong></p><p>In the given image the structure marked B represents the <strong>lateral pterygoid</strong> muscle which is responsible for the<strong> opening of the mouth.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Lateral pterygoid:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Origin -\n<ul>\n<li>Upper head - Infratemporal surface and infratemporal crest of the greater wing of the sphenoid bone.</li>\n<li>Lower head - <strong>L</strong><strong>ateral surface</strong> of the <strong>lateral pterygoid plate. </strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Insertion - It is inserted into the pterygoid fovea, capsule of the temporomandibular joint, and the articular disc.</li>\n<li>Nerve supply - Lateral pterygoid nerve which is a branch of the <strong>trigeminal nerve</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other structures marked:</p>\n<p>A - Temporalis</p>\n<p>C - Masseter</p>\n<p>D - Medial pterygoid</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4371","text":"A 26-year-old man presente‌d with the inability to c​lose his mouth after yawn‌ing. On evaluation, there​ is an anterior dislocati​on of the temporomandibul‌ar joint. This condition ‌is caused by the excessiv‌e contraction of which of‌ the following muscles?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old man presented with the inability to close his mouth after yawning. On evaluation, there is an anterior dislocation of the temporomandibular joint. This condition is caused by the excessive contraction of which of the following muscles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tempo‌ralis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral ‌pterygoid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bucci‌nator","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mass‌eter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lateral pterygoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d6a1db5af8244bc69c36df732214ebf5x1280x1400.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/891bd3e3604749d29d5f76aad6b96f9bx1280x1107.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lateral pterygoid</strong></p><p>Anterior<strong> temporomandibular joint</strong> (TMJ) <strong>dislocation</strong> occurs due to the excessive contraction of the <strong>lateral pterygoid. </strong>It is associated with the <strong>extreme opening of mouth</strong> such as yawning, laughing, vomiting or dental treatment.</p>\n<p>Some fibres of the<strong> upper head</strong> of the lateral pterygoid are <strong>inserted into</strong> the <strong>capsule </strong>of the TMJ and to the anterior and medial borders of the <strong>articular disc</strong>. Hence, its contraction can exert <strong>forward traction</strong> on the <strong>disc,</strong> allowing for jaw protrusion and in extreme cases, the <strong>anterior displacement</strong> of the TMJ.</p>\n<p>Superior and posterior dislocations of TMJ are rare and is associated with high-energy trauma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1369","text":"An elderly woman presents ‌to you with the following​ findings. She gives a hi‌story of chickenpox in he​r childhood. Which of the​ following is false about‌ the cranial nerve involv‌ed in this case?","raw_text":"An elderly woman presents to you with the following findings. She gives a history of chickenpox in her childhood. Which of the following is false about the cranial nerve involved in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is is predominantly sen‌sory","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Motor innervation is exclu‌sively carried by maxilla​ry division","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The cornea is primarily in‌nervated by ophthalmic di​vision","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It provides motor supply t‌o medial pterygoid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Motor innervation is exclusively carried by maxillary division","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3e64eb4b785c455cbffa41e4074f458e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe1699e45e8b41bf8f5825b8499f79d6x1280x1618.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/17f37ff9113742729e27f2be8764f84cx1280x1618.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/48bae55d7267408a9e701cdddd626f23x1280x1618.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4cbe7099fe5345faa14893cf9265f961x1280x1618.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Motor innervation is exclusively carried by maxillary division</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with a history of chickenpox and lesions over the face is suggestive of<strong> herpes zoster ophthalmicus</strong>. The cranial nerve involved is the<strong> trigeminal nerve</strong>. <strong>Motor innervation</strong> is exclusively carried by <strong>mandibular</strong> division, not the maxillary division. </p>\n<p>The trigeminal nerve (CNV) is the <strong>largest</strong> cranial nerve. It is a <strong>predominantly sensory </strong>nerve with motor components. </p>\n<p>The nerve divides into three divisions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ophthalmic nerve - V1 </li>\n<li>Maxillary nerve - V2</li>\n<li>Mandibular nerve - V3</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Ophthalmic nerve:</strong></p>\n<p>It provides <strong>sensory innervation</strong> to the scalp, cornea, conjunctiva, iris, upper eyelid, lacrimal gland, and the skin and mucus membranes of paranasal sinuses.</p>\n<p>It is the branch that is affected in <strong>herpes zoster ophthalmicus.</strong> After an attack of chickenpox, the virus travels to the trigeminal ganglion and lies dormant there. Reactivation causes typical manifestations such as skin and eyelid involvement, keratitis, uveitis, and conjunctivitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Maxillary nerve:</strong></p>\n<p>It provides <strong>sensory innervation</strong> to the lower eyelid, cheeks, maxillary sinus, nasal cavity, lateral nose, upper lip, palate, teeth, gingiva. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Mandibular nerve:</strong></p>\n<p>It provides <strong>sensory innervation</strong> to the dura mater of middle cranial fossa, mucus membranes in the mouth, parts of pinna, tympanic membrane, mylohyoid, anterior belly of digastric and anterior 2/3rds of the tongue. </p>\n<p>It also provides exclusive <strong>motor innervation</strong> to the <strong>muscles of mastication,</strong> tensor veli palatini, and tensor tympani.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6082","text":"A dentist provides local a‌nesthesia by injecting li​docaine at the site marke‌d X to block a division o​f the trigeminal nerve. W​hich of the following mus‌cles is not supplied by t‌he trunk of this nerve?","raw_text":"A dentist provides local anesthesia by injecting lidocaine at the site marked X to block a division of the trigeminal nerve. Which of the following muscles is not supplied by the trunk of this nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medial p‌terygoid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tensor ‌tympani","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tensor vel‌i palatini","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral ‌pterygoid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lateral pterygoid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/47619fc4202f4b2ba6764f9dce8cfc91.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lateral pterygoid</strong></p><p>The site marked X is the <strong>mandibular foramen</strong> which contains the inferior alveolar nerve, a branch of the <strong>mandibular nerve</strong>. All the muscles mentioned above are supplied by the trunk of mandibular nerve except the<strong> lateral pterygoid. </strong>The lateral pterygoid is supplied by the <strong>anterior division</strong> of <strong>mandibular nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4377","text":"A patient with past histor‌y of parotidectomy now pr​esents with sweating and ‌flushing of skin over the​ cheek during mastication​. Which of the following ‌nerves is involved in thi‌s case?","raw_text":"A patient with past history of parotidectomy now presents with sweating and flushing of skin over the cheek during mastication. Which of the following nerves is involved in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ea5f91c708cd4ac5aef2b5f06c871f78.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b708b3d65aca4e66ab21fc8a0f63ca84x600x820.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ccd350243ce24898b3b774f65ba94382x1279x1295.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>Frey's syndrome</strong> which involves the <strong>auriculotemporal nerve</strong>, marked as 2. </p>\n<p>Other structures marked are: </p>\n<p>1 - Maxillary nerve </p>\n<p>3 - Posterior auricular nerve </p>\n<p>4 - Inferior alveolar nerve</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The auriculotemporal nerve is identified by its <strong>two roots</strong> which encircle the <strong>middle meningeal artery. </strong>It is a branch from the posterior division of the <strong>mandibular nerve.</strong> It gives cutaneous innervation to the tragus and the adjoining part of the auricle and carries <strong>postganglionic secretomotor</strong> fibers to the <strong>parotid</strong> gland. </p>\n<p>Frey's syndrome is caused due to <strong>aberrant regeneration</strong> of the postganglionic parasympathetic nerve fibers of the<strong> auriculotemporal nerve. </strong>The nerve fibres regenerate into the nearby<strong> sweat glands.</strong> It can occur following <strong>parotid surgery, </strong>or any <strong>trauma</strong> where there is an injury to the parotid capsule. This results in <strong>sweating,</strong> warmth, and <strong>redness</strong> of the face during the <strong>salivary stimulation</strong> by the smell or taste of food. Hence, Frey's syndrome is also known as gustatory sweating or auriculotemporal syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4372","text":"In which of the following ‌compartments of temporoma​ndibular joint do the rot‌atory movements of the ja​w take place?","raw_text":"In which of the following compartments of temporomandibular joint do the rotatory movements of the jaw take place?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Joint space above the arti‌cular disc","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Joint space below the arti‌cular disc","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Joint space medial to the ‌articular disc","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intermed‌iate zone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Joint space below the articular disc","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/03cdef4fa8b145dd9729009c5db8ce89x600x814.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Joint space below the articular disc</strong></p><p><strong>Rotatory movements</strong> of the jaw take place in the joint space <strong>below the articular disc </strong>of the temporomandibular joint.</p>\n<p>The temporomandibular joint is adapted to accommodate both sliding and rotation movement in a sagittal plane.</p>\n<p>The capsular ligament that surrounds the <strong>upper joint compartment</strong> is loose and hence allows the <strong>sliding or translation</strong> movements.</p>\n<p>The capsular ligament that surrounds the <strong>lower joint compartment</strong> is tight and hence allows only the <strong>rotatory movements.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0941","text":"A patient with diplopia wa‌s found to have paralysis​ of the muscle marked bel‌ow. What is its nerve sup​ply?","raw_text":"A patient with diplopia was found to have paralysis of the muscle marked below. What is its nerve supply?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oculomot‌or nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trochle‌ar nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Trigemin‌al nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abducen‌s nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trochlear nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b4e1138e4b6c4ccd88ad6bcca919ca04.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5097a95cafdf444a82041fa0c7851b89x1280x1777.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/516b48623b864fa99d8279e24983201bx1280x1523.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/566886366d2d44aeaad1208c87afd726x1280x1901.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trochlear nerve</strong></p><p>The structure marked in the image is the <strong>superior oblique muscle. </strong>It is supplied by the <strong>trochlear nerve</strong> (CN IV).<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>The superior oblique muscle helps in looking downwards and inwards. In cases of palsy of the trochlear nerve, the muscle is paralysed and the patient is unable to perform the same movement.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>There are seven extraocular muscles present in the eye. They are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Levator palpebrae superioris </strong>- Which elevates the upper eyelids</li>\n<li>Four <strong>recti</strong> muscles - Superior, inferior, medial, and lateral recti</li>\n<li>Two <strong>obliques </strong>- Superior and inferior oblique</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The muscles originate from the back of the orbit in a common tendinous ring called the <strong>annulus of Zinn</strong> and are inserted into the sclera.</p>\n<p>All the <strong>extraocular muscles</strong> are innervated by the <strong>o</strong><strong>culomotor (III) nerve</strong> except:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lateral rectus</strong> which is supplied by abducens<strong> (VI) nerve</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Superior oblique</strong> which is supplied by trochlear<strong> (IV) nerve</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0837","text":"A 34-year-old woman comes ‌to the Ophthalmology OPD ​with complaints of a righ‌t-sided squint. On asking​ her to look straight, yo​u observe the following. ‌What is the nerve supply ‌of the muscle paralyzed i‌n this case?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old woman comes to the Ophthalmology OPD with complaints of a right-sided squint. On asking her to look straight, you observe the following. What is the nerve supply of the muscle paralyzed in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior division of CN II‌I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abducen‌s nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior division of CN II‌I","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trochle‌ar nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inferior division of CN III","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/68d4e9a84d604d0a8741d974888bfc33.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f248b19b7663464887721222e023e531x600x726.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3fd27d6c8e5d4207a8785b035414bc05x1280x1901.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inferior division of CN III</strong></p><p>In the given case, the <strong>medial rectus</strong> muscle is <strong>paralyzed</strong> on the right side. This leads to <strong>unopposed</strong> action of the <strong>lateral rectus,</strong> causing the eyeball to move<strong> outwards.</strong> The medial rectus, inferior rectus and inferior oblique are supplied by the<strong> inferior division</strong> of the oculomotor nerve (CN III)</p>\n<p>Course of the oculomotor nerve:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The oculomotor nerve arises from the <strong>midbrain</strong> and emerges, by passing <strong>medial</strong> to the <strong>crus cerebri.</strong> </li>\n<li>It passes along the lateral wall of the<strong> cavernous sinus</strong> and divides into <strong>superior</strong> and <strong>inferior</strong> divisions. The two divisions enter the orbit through the <strong>superior orbital fissure</strong> and innervate the extraocular muscles:\n<ul>\n<li>Superior division - superior rectus and levator palpebrae superioris muscles.</li>\n<li>Inferior division - the inferior rectus, medial rectus, and inferior oblique muscles.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7649","text":"Which of the following mus‌cle is not supplied by th​e structure marked A?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscle is not supplied by the structure marked A?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superio‌r rectus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior‌ oblique","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Medial‌ rectus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Levator palpebrae superior‌is (LPS)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Superior oblique","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/09f2e5c59c0b402faa99cbbb70708ffc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/61cb207756644196b429f3ceaa03c5c0x1280x1102.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0bad277fba424462a970be5d96e05f46x1280x1425.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Superior oblique</strong></p><p>The structure marked in the image is the <strong>oculomotor nerve</strong> and it <strong>does not supply</strong> the<strong> superior oblique</strong> muscle. Superior oblique muscle is supplied by<strong> the trochlear nerve</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"> All the <strong>extraocular muscles</strong> are innervated by the <strong>o</strong><strong>culomotor (III) nerve</strong> except:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lateral rectus</strong> which is supplied by <strong>abducens (VI) </strong>nerve</li>\n<li><strong>Superior oblique</strong> which is supplied by <strong>trochlear</strong> <strong>(IV)</strong> nerve</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0843","text":"A 43-year-old woman presen‌ted with hyperacusis. She​ recently underwent recon‌structive surgery for fra​cture of mandible at the ​TMJ. Investigations revea‌led paralysis of the stap‌edius muscle. A branch of‌ which of the following n‌erves is likely to cause ‌the given condition?","raw_text":"A 43-year-old woman presented with hyperacusis. She recently underwent reconstructive surgery for fracture of mandible at the TMJ. Investigations revealed paralysis of the stapedius muscle. A branch of which of the following nerves is likely to cause the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"VII cran‌ial nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"VIII cran‌ial nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"V crani‌al nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"VI crani‌al nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. VII cranial nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d63cde1c105e4f77b7a53c4800c8bb68x600x719.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. VII cranial nerve</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of injury to the<strong> nerve to stapedius</strong> which is a branch of the <strong>VII<sup>th</sup> cranial nerve</strong> (facial nerve). During surgery involving the parotid and mandibular region, injury to the facial nerve can damage the nerve to stapedius, hence causing <strong>stapedial paralysis</strong> which leads to <strong>hyperacusis.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> tensor tympani</strong> and the <strong>stapedius</strong> are the muscles of the middle ear and they are innervated by branches of <strong>V</strong> and <strong>VII </strong>cranial nerves respectively. Both these muscles contract in response to loud noises and thereby dampen sound. The tensor tympani <strong>increases</strong> the<strong> tension </strong>of the <strong>tympanic membrane</strong> and reduces the force of vibration. Stapedius <strong>pulls the stapes posteriorly</strong> and <strong>reduces</strong> the <strong>oscillations.</strong></p>\n<p>Paralysis of one or both of these muscles causes increased sensitivity to the sounds, a condition known as <strong>hyperacusis.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2269","text":"Identify the thickened ner‌ve marked in the arrow in​ the image shown below:","raw_text":"Identify the thickened nerve marked in the arrow in the image shown below:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Great auri‌cular nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lesser occi‌pital nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Facial‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Auriculotem‌poral nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Great auricular nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6657a8aefadc48b28305214f187d0353.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fd759dee0552424f8ce1aa00fbf6d806x1280x2163.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Great auricular nerve</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The thickened nerve marked with the arrow in the above image is the <strong>great auricular nerve</strong> and the thickened nerve visible below the great auricular nerve is the transverse cervical nerve.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Nerve thickening in this case is likely to be due to <strong>leprosy.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Great auricular nerve:</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is the largest ascending branch of cervical plexus and arises from the <strong>second </strong>and the<strong> third cervical spinal nerves.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It winds over the posterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle and ascends upwards. On reaching the parotid gland it divides into the anterior and posterior branches:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Anterior branch - supplies the <strong>facial skin over</strong> the<strong> parotid</strong> gland and joins the facial nerve in the gland.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Posterior branch - supplies the skin over the mastoid process, posteroinferior part of the auricle, lobule, and concha.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical considerations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Leprosy</strong> - <strong>Nerve thickening</strong> is present. The nerve is palpable over the upper part of the sternocleidomastoid muscle. (as seen in the above image)</li>\n<li><strong>Frey's syndrome - </strong>An aberrant connection between the auriculotemporal nerve and great auricular nerve causes gustatory sweating.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4370","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a hybrid muscle?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a hybrid muscle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2ab1b77e653643a8aaf3adeadf521eac.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d24e80807728458ab569ed5a214dd178x600x651.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3</strong></p><p>The <strong>sternohyoid</strong> muscle marked as '3' is not a hybrid muscle. It is innervated only by the anterior rami of spinal nerves<strong> C1-C3 </strong>(via the<strong> ansa cervicalis</strong>)</p>\n<p><strong>Hybrid muscles</strong> are the muscles that are supplied by <strong>two different nerves</strong> with <strong>different root values.</strong></p>\n<p>Other muscles in the given image are:</p>\n<p>A - Trapezius</p>\n<p>B - Sternocleidomastoid</p>\n<p>D - Digastric muscle (anterior belly)</p>\n<p><strong>Hybrid muscles of head and neck: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Digastric</strong> - Anterior belly is supplied by the nerve to the mylohyoid and posterior belly is supplied by the facial nerve.</li>\n<li><strong>Trapezius</strong> and <strong>sternocleidomastoid</strong> (SCM) - Motor supply is by the spinal accessory nerve and the proprioception is by ventral rami of cervical nerves (C2, C3 for SCM and C3, C4 for trapezius).</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4373","text":"The carotid angiography of‌ a 30-year-old with a car​otid body tumor shows spl‌aying at the bifurcation ​of the carotids as seen i​n the image below. At wha‌t level do you expect to ‌find this?","raw_text":"The carotid angiography of a 30-year-old with a carotid body tumor shows splaying at the bifurcation of the carotids as seen in the image below. At what level do you expect to find this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lower border of thyroid ca‌rtilage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Upper border of thyroid ca‌rtilage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lower border of cricothyro‌id cartilage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Upper border of cricothyro‌id cartilage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Upper border of thyroid cartilage","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/de4b5e2caa424e988ae7e1df1c5f484b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c6c5e9b4f159449aab73c3848b0b7a1fx510x618.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f5a1ac2d5da4ab080e521b5f7ee0092x600x628.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Upper border of thyroid cartilage</strong></p><p>The bifurcation of the <strong>common carotid artery </strong>is at the level of the <strong>upper border of thyroid cartilage </strong>(C3-C4 junction). Carotid body tumors present at the bifurcation of the CCA and cause the external carotid artery (ECA) and internal carotid artery (ICA) to spread out, or splay, which is called '<strong>Lyre sign'</strong> as seen on carotid angiography.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The course of the <strong>common carotid artery</strong> (CCA):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The right and the left common carotid arteries differ in their origin:\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>right</strong> CCA arises from the <strong>brachiocephalic</strong> artery</li>\n<li>The <strong>left</strong> CCA arises directly from the <strong>arch</strong> of the <strong>aorta.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>In the neck, it is enclosed within the<strong> carotid sheath</strong> along with the internal jugular vein and vagus nerve.</li>\n<li>It ascends upwards up to the <strong>upper border </strong>of the<strong> thyroid cartilage </strong>(C3-4 junction) and divides into <strong>internal</strong> and <strong>external</strong> carotid arteries</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Clinical significance of carotid bifurcation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chassaignac</strong>/carotid tubercle/transverse process of C6 vertebra: The <strong>pulsations</strong> of the common carotid artery can be felt by compressing the artery against this tubercle. </li>\n<li>Site of<strong> carotid body tumor</strong></li>\n<li>The most common site for <strong>carotid</strong> <strong>atherosclerosis. </strong>It can lead to stroke or transient ischaemic attack (TIA).</li>\n<li>In <strong>carotid endarterectomy</strong>, the presence of high bifurcation predisposes to <strong>injury</strong> to the <strong>cranial nerves,</strong> commonly the hypoglossal nerve or marginal mandibular nerve. In such patients, carotid stenting is usually preferred to carotid endarterectomy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0530","text":"The only branch of the ext‌ernal carotid artery whic​h is given from its media‌l side is:","raw_text":"The only branch of the external carotid artery which is given from its medial side is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior th‌yroid artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Facial‌ artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ascending pharyngeal arter‌y","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lingual‌ artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ascending pharyngeal artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/16d45259d5f8474db3ad550dd21a2eacx600x842.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ascending pharyngeal artery</strong></p><p>The only branch of the external carotid artery which is given from its <strong>medial side</strong> is <strong>ascending pharyngeal artery</strong>.</p>\n<p>The external carotid artery is a branch of the common carotid artery which begins at the <strong>upper border of the thyroid cartilage</strong> at the level between third and fourth cervical vertebrae.</p>\n<p>Branches of the external carotid artery are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superior thyroid artery</li>\n<li>Ascending pharyngeal artery</li>\n<li>Lingual artery</li>\n<li>Facial artery</li>\n<li>Occipital artery</li>\n<li>Posterior auricular artery</li>\n<li>Maxillary artery and superficial temporal artery (terminal branches)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4378","text":"Which of the following bra‌nches of internal carotid​ artery are given off in ‌the cervical region?","raw_text":"Which of the following branches of internal carotid artery are given off in the cervical region?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pharyngea‌l branches","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pterygoi‌d branch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Caroticotym‌panic branch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‌the above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. None of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. None of the above</strong></p><p>The internal carotid artery <strong>does not give</strong> any branch in the <strong>cervical region</strong>.</p>\n<p>The internal carotid artery is a terminal branch of the common carotid artery which begins at the level of the upper border of thyroid cartilage.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4379","text":"A patient with a history o‌f cavernous sinus thrombo​sis died of an arterial i‌schemic stroke. An autops​y revealed carotid artery​ narrowing. Which of the ‌following branches of the‌ internal carotid artery ‌will not be involved in t‌his case?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of cavernous sinus thrombosis died of an arterial ischemic stroke. An autopsy revealed carotid artery narrowing. Which of the following branches of the internal carotid artery will not be involved in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meningea‌l artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior hypophyseal arter‌y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ophthalm‌ic artery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Branches to the trigeminal‌ ganglion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ophthalmic artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/52c07b83e26047788d1d896bce07dc52x1280x1403.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ophthalmic artery</strong></p><p>Cavernous sinus thrombosis may lead to the <strong>narrowing</strong> of the <strong>cavernous part</strong> of the internal carotid artery <strong>(ICA),</strong> because the cavernous sinus is closely related to it. The <strong>ophthalmic artery</strong> will not be involved here, as it is a branch of the <strong>c</strong><strong>erebral part</strong> of the ICA.</p>\n<p>Branches of cavernous part of the internal carotid artery:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Meningeal artery</li>\n<li>Inferior hypophyseal arteries</li>\n<li><strong>Cavernous branch</strong> (supplies trigeminal ganglion)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4380","text":"In a patient undergoing ma‌ndibular fracture repair,​ the facial artery is lig‌ated at the mandible. Whi​ch of the following branc​hes will not be affected?‌","raw_text":"In a patient undergoing mandibular fracture repair, the facial artery is ligated at the mandible. Which of the following branches will not be affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ascending pa‌latine artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior la‌bial branch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Premassete‌ric artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dorsal na‌sal branch","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dorsal nasal branch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f36d5af13ba4042928a1215a83f510dx1280x1402.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dorsal nasal branch</strong></p><p>The <strong>dorsal nasal artery </strong>will not be affected in this case, as is not a branch of the facial artery. It is a branch of the <strong>ophthalmic artery</strong> which is a branch of the ICA.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>facial artery</strong> is a branch of the <strong>external carotid artery</strong> which arises in the carotid triangle, above the origin of the lingual artery. In runs upwards in the neck and curves around the anteroinferior border of the masseter to enter the face. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 115px; width: 608px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 294.801px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Branches of facial artery in the neck</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 297.199px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Branches of facial artery in the face</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 294.801px;\">Ascending palatine artery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 297.199px;\">Premasseteric artery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 294.801px;\">Tonsillar branch</td>\n<td style=\"width: 297.199px;\">Superior and inferior labial arteries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 294.801px;\">Submental artery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 297.199px;\">Lateral nasal artery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 294.801px;\">Branches to submandibular gland</td>\n<td style=\"width: 297.199px;\">Angular artery</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0842","text":"A 34-year-old man presente‌d to the ER with a histor​y of a head injury follow‌ing an RTA. CT head revea​led a displaced fracture ​of the floor of the middl‌e cranial fossa involving‌ the pterygoid canal. Whi‌ch of the following nerve‌s are susceptible to inju‌ry?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old man presented to the ER with a history of a head injury following an RTA. CT head revealed a displaced fracture of the floor of the middle cranial fossa involving the pterygoid canal. Which of the following nerves are susceptible to injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lesser petrosal nerve and ‌greater petrosal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Greater petrosal nerve and‌ deep petrosal nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Greater petrosal nerve and‌ chorda tympani","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lesser petrosal nerve and ‌deep petrosal nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Greater petrosal nerve and deep petrosal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cb577bf4e7324de0b1f8f9dc9dfcbcbdx600x510.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/11da251ad04f4b39a0cf9a0f91de2491x600x523.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Greater petrosal nerve and deep petrosal nerve</strong></p><p>The involvement of the <strong>pterygoid canal </strong>can injure the<strong> vidian nerve</strong>, which is formed by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Postganglionic sympathetic fibres of the <strong>deep petrosal nerve</strong></li>\n<li>Preganglionic parasympathetic fibres of the<strong> greater petrosal nerve</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Pterygoid canal (Vidian canal):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>pterygoid canal</strong>, also known as the <strong>Vidian canal</strong>, is a foramen in the base of the skull, located in the pterygoid process of the sphenoid bone, superior to the pterygoid plates, and <strong>inferomedial</strong> to the <strong>foramen rotundum</strong>. It transmits the <strong>Vidian artery</strong> and <strong>Vidian nerve</strong> from the <strong>middle cranial fossa </strong>to the<strong> pterygopalatine fossa</strong> </li>\n<li>It transmits the artery, nerve, and vein of the pterygoid canal (<strong>Vidian nerve</strong> and<strong> Vidian artery</strong>) into the <strong>pterygopalatine fossa</strong>.</li>\n<li>The vidian nerve contains the <strong>preganglionic</strong> fibers of the<strong> pterygopalatine ganglion</strong>, which provide the secretomotor supply to the nasal mucous glands. Hence <strong>vidian neurectomy</strong> is indicated in <strong>vasomotor rhinitis</strong> and <strong>intractable rhinnorrhea</strong> which is refractory to other treatments.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4376","text":"The artery passing through‌ the structure marked A i​s a branch of ______ part‌ of the maxillary artery.​","raw_text":"The artery passing through the structure marked A is a branch of ______ part of the maxillary artery.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mandibu‌lar part","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pterygo‌id part","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pterygopal‌atine part","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Petrou‌s part","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pterygopalatine part","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/be7e8f17b10446dab5180466b921bb0a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pterygopalatine part</strong></p><p>The structure marked A is the<strong> pterygoid canal.</strong> The <strong>artery</strong> of the pterygoid canal is a branch of <strong>pterygopalatine part</strong> of the <strong>maxillary artery.</strong></p>\n<p>Pterygopalatine branches of maxillary artery are (mnemonic PIGPAS):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>P</strong>osterior superior alveolar artery</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>nfraorbital artery</li>\n<li><strong>G</strong>reater palatine artery</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>haryngeal artery</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>rtery of pterygoid canal</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>phenopalatine artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4382","text":"A patient with tricuspid r‌egurgitation has an engor​ged neck vein as shown in‌ the given image. What ar​e its formative tributari​es?","raw_text":"A patient with tricuspid regurgitation has an engorged neck vein as shown in the given image. What are its formative tributaries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retromandibular vein and f‌acial vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior auricular vein a‌nd retromandibular vein","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Superficial temporal vein ‌and maxillary vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retromandibular vein and d‌eep facial vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posterior auricular vein and retromandibular vein","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e7b593e1f5e447e7aacc9218b6be12fd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/82ce8ff69f0c490e95c886791062a08ex600x577.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posterior auricular vein and retromandibular vein</strong></p><p>The given image shows distention of the <strong>external jugular vein.</strong> Its formative tributaries are the <strong>p</strong><strong>osterior auricular vein</strong> and<strong> retromandibular vein </strong>(posterior division).</p>\n<p>Venous drainage of the face and scalp:</p>\n<p>The blood from the face and scalp is mainly drained by the facial vein and retromandibular vein.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The supratrochlear and the supraorbital veins join at the medial angle of the eye to form the angular vein, which continues down as <strong>facial vein</strong>.</li>\n<li>The superficial temporal vein joins the maxillary vein to form the <strong>retromandibular vein</strong>. The retromandibular vein divides into anterior and posterior divisions.</li>\n<li>The anterior division of the retromandibular vein joins the facial vein to form the <strong>common facial vein</strong>, which drains into the <strong>internal jugular vein</strong>.</li>\n<li>The posterior division of the retromandibular vein joins with the posterior auricular vein to form the <strong>external jugular vein</strong>. The external jugular vein then drains into the subclavian vein.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4352","text":"Which of the following is ‌a part of the dangerous a​rea of the face?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a part of the dangerous area of the face?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Middle 1/3rd of the upper ‌lip","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Middle 1/3rd of the lower ‌lip","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral 1/3rd of the lower‌ lip","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Periorbi‌tal area","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Middle 1/3rd of the upper lip","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fdfb915a409d40a6ac276bffb37bf52dx1280x1407.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ea356e378594f35be3fa85690cbf018x600x688.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Middle 1/3rd of the upper lip</strong></p><p>The triangular area from the corners of the mouth to the bridge of the nose, including the nose and maxilla<strong> </strong>is considered as the <strong>danger triangle of the face</strong> or <strong>dangerous area of the face. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The blood from these area drains through the facial vein. The facial vein is valveless and connects with the cavernous sinus through the <strong>superior ophthalmic vein</strong> and with the pterygoid venous plexus through the <strong>inferior ophthalmic </strong>and<strong> deep facial veins</strong>. Hence any infection of the face may spread to the cavernous sinus and pterygoid venous plexus and lead to cavernous sinus thrombophlebitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7043","text":"The correct pair of lymph ‌node level in the neck an​d anatomical boundaries a‌mongst the following are:​","raw_text":"The correct pair of lymph node level in the neck and anatomical boundaries amongst the following are:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Level 3 - Cricoid to clavi‌cle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Level 4 - Hyoid to cricoid‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Level 5 - Base of skull to‌ cricoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Level 6 - Hyoid to clavicl‌e","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Level 6 - Hyoid to clavicle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/319f624c4f3e4821a8f773e7a93d6645x600x666.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Level 6 - Hyoid to clavicle</strong></p><p>The correct pair of lymph nodes and levels among the following options are level 6- Hyoid to clavicle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Levels of lymph nodes</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Level </strong></td>\n<td><strong>Lymph node </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1a</strong></td>\n<td>Submental</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1b</strong></td>\n<td>Submandibular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2</strong></td>\n<td>Upper jugular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3</strong></td>\n<td>Middle jugular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>4</strong></td>\n<td>Lower jugular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>5</strong></td>\n<td>Posterior triangle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>6</strong></td>\n<td>Anterior group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>7</strong></td>\n<td>Mediastinal</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"h1lP_J22","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"30_energy_metabolism_and_macronutrients","title":"30.Energy Metabolism And Macronutrients","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MB8111","text":"Which of the following par‍ameters is not indicative​ of reference Indian adul‌t woman?","raw_text":"Which of the following parameters is not indicative of reference Indian adult woman?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"19-39‍ years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1.62 m‍ height","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"50 kg ‍weight","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"BMI ‍20.95","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 50 kg weight","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 50 kg weight</strong></p><p>According to the 2020 guidelines of the National Institute of Nutrition,<strong> </strong>the weight of the<strong> reference Indian adult woman </strong>is <strong>55 kg</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5533","text":"The energy required by an ‍adult for basal metabolis​m is:","raw_text":"The energy required by an adult for basal metabolism is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 kcal/ hour/ kg body weig‍ht","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2 kcal/ hour/ kg body weig‍ht","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 kcal/ hour/ kg body weig‍ht","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 kcal/ hour/ kg body weig‍ht","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1 kcal/ hour/ kg body weight","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1 kcal/ hour/ kg body weight</strong></p><p>The <strong>energy</strong> required by an <strong>adult</strong> for basal metabolism is <strong>1</strong> <strong>kcal/</strong> <strong>hour/</strong> <strong>kg</strong> of body weight.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8112","text":"What is the likely energy ‍requirement of a pregnant​ woman who leads a modera‌tely active lifestyle?","raw_text":"What is the likely energy requirement of a pregnant woman who leads a moderately active lifestyle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3000 kc‍al/ day","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2500 kc‍al/ day","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2000 kc‍al/ day","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1500 kc‍al/ day","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2500 kcal/ day","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2500 kcal/ day</strong></p><p>The energy requirement of a <strong>pregnant woman</strong> who leads a <strong>moderately active</strong> lifestyle is around <strong>2500 kcal/day</strong>.</p>\n<p>An adult woman leading a <strong>moderately active </strong>lifestyle requires <strong>2130 kcal</strong> daily and an additional<strong> 350 kcal </strong>is needed in <strong>pregnancy</strong>. Hence, the total requirement for a moderately working pregnant woman is 2480 kcal/ day (approximately 2500 kcal/ day).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5525","text":"A nutritionist is preparin‍g a meal plan for a healt​hy 32-year-old man. Which‌ of the following princip‌les must she follow?","raw_text":"A nutritionist is preparing a meal plan for a healthy 32-year-old man. Which of the following principles must she follow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proteins must constitute 1‍5-30% of the daily energy​ intake","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fats must constitute 10-15‍% of the daily energy int​ake","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbohydrate requirement m‍ust be met first followed​ by protein and fat","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The proportion of nutrient‍s in the diet must be car​bohydrates &amp;gt; fats &amp;gt;‌ proteins","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The proportion of nutrients in the diet must be carbohydrates > fats > proteins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The proportion of nutrients in the diet must be carbohydrates &gt; fats &gt; proteins</strong></p><p>The proportion of nutrients in the diet must be <strong>carbohydrates &gt; fats &gt; proteins</strong>.</p>\n<p>While constructing a <strong>balanced diet</strong>, nutrient requirements must be calculated in the following <strong>sequence</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Protein</strong> requirements - <strong>10-15%</strong> of daily energy intake</li>\n<li><strong>Fat</strong> requirement - <strong>15-30%</strong> of daily energy intake</li>\n<li><strong>Carbohydrates</strong> - <strong>remaining</strong> food energy</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8113","text":"What is the daily requirem‍ent of high-quality prote​in for a 40-year-old man ‌weighing 60 kgs?","raw_text":"What is the daily requirement of high-quality protein for a 40-year-old man weighing 60 kgs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"40 g‍/day","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"50 g‍/day","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"72 g‍/day","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"85 g‍/day","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 50 g/day","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 50 g/day</strong></p><p>This man requires <strong>50 g/day</strong> of <strong>high-quality protein</strong>.</p>\n<p>High-quality protein refers to proteins containing all the <strong>essential amino acids</strong>. According to the <strong>FAO/WHO </strong>2007 guidelines<strong>, </strong>the recommended high-quality protein<strong> </strong>intake for adult men and women is <strong>0.83 g/kg/day</strong>. This man weighs 60 kgs. Hence he requires around 50 g (0.83*60 = 49.8) of high-quality protein per day.</p>\n<p>Recommended high-quality protein intake by FAO/WHO:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 247px;\" width=\"218\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102.667px;\">Age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.667px;\">Protein in g/kg/day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102.667px;\">1-5 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.667px;\">0.94</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102.667px;\">6-10 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.667px;\">0.91</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102.667px;\">Adolescent male</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.667px;\">0.88</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102.667px;\">Adolescent female</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.667px;\">0.86</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102.667px;\">Adult man/woman</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.667px;\">0.83</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>However, the mixed Indian diet does not meet the requirement for high-quality protein. Hence, the protein requirement in <strong>Indian</strong> adults (men and women) is <strong>1g/kg/day</strong> of mixed Indian diet protein according to the <strong>ICMR </strong>2010 guidelines. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5538","text":"A dietary protein-energy p‍ercentage of which of the​ following is the thresho‌ld for inadequate protein‌ intake?","raw_text":"A dietary protein-energy percentage of which of the following is the threshold for inadequate protein intake?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt‍; 2%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt‍; 4%","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt;‍ 20%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt;‍ 40%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. < 4%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. &lt; 4%</strong></p><p>Dietary protein-energy percentage (<strong>PE %</strong>) <strong>&lt; 4% </strong>indicates <strong>inadequate protein</strong> <strong>intake</strong>. The PE % indicates the proportion of total energy in the diet that is derived from proteins.</p>\n<p>PE % = (Energy from protein / Total energy in the diet) * 100</p>\n<p>Some of the food items and their protein-energy percentage are given below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 220px;\" width=\"153\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.3333px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Food</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 70.6667px; text-align: center;\"><strong>PE%</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.3333px; text-align: center;\">Fish</td>\n<td style=\"width: 70.6667px; text-align: center;\">80</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.3333px; text-align: center;\">Cow milk</td>\n<td style=\"width: 70.6667px; text-align: center;\">20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.3333px; text-align: center;\">Dal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 70.6667px; text-align: center;\">24</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.3333px; text-align: center;\">Rice</td>\n<td style=\"width: 70.6667px; text-align: center;\">8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.3333px; text-align: center;\">Potato</td>\n<td style=\"width: 70.6667px; text-align: center;\">6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.3333px; text-align: center;\">Banana</td>\n<td style=\"width: 70.6667px; text-align: center;\">4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 69.3333px; text-align: center;\">Tapioca</td>\n<td style=\"width: 70.6667px; text-align: center;\">2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8114","text":"Which of the following is ‍the best indicator of pro​tein quality?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the best indicator of protein quality?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Protein digestibility corr‍ected amino acid score","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Digestible indispensable a‍mino acid score","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Biologic‍al value","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Protein effi‍ciency ratio","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Digestible indispensable amino acid score","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Digestible indispensable amino acid score</strong></p><p>Digestible indispensable amino acid score <strong>(DIAAS) </strong>is the<strong> best indicator </strong>for <strong>protein quality.</strong></p>\n<p>DIAAS reflects the <strong>digestibility</strong> of <strong>individual amino acids </strong>in the protein. Total protein digestibility does not always reflect the digestibility of individual dietary indispensable amino acids. Hence, DIAAS has replaced the PDCAAS (protein digestibility corrected amino acid score) as the preferred index for the evaluation of protein quality.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5540","text":"Which of the following has‍ the highest net protein ​utilization value?","raw_text":"Which of the following has the highest net protein utilization value?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Me‍at","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mi‍lk","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ri‍ce","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"So‍ya","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Meat","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Meat</strong></p><p>Among the given options, <strong>meat</strong> has the <strong>highest</strong> net protein utilization (<strong>NPU</strong>).</p>\n<p>The <strong>NPU</strong> <strong>values</strong> of various food items in descending order are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Egg - 100</li>\n<li>Meat - 80</li>\n<li>Milk - 75</li>\n<li>Rice - 65</li>\n<li>Soya - 61</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Low NPU indicates a high protein requirement. The NPU of Indian diets is usually between 50 and 80.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8116","text":"A farmer has been consumin‍g a maize-rich diet for m​any years. Which of the f‌ollowing would be lacking‌ in his diet?","raw_text":"A farmer has been consuming a maize-rich diet for many years. Which of the following would be lacking in his diet?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Only tr‍yptophan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lysine and‍ threonine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lysine and‍ tryptophan","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Methionine ‍and cysteine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lysine and tryptophan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lysine and tryptophan</strong></p><p>His diet would be lacking <strong>lysine and tryptophan </strong>as <strong>maize</strong> is <strong>deficient</strong> in these amino acids.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong> Principal Food </strong></td>\n<td><strong> Amino acid Deficient</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cereals (in general) </td>\n<td>Lysine </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maize  </td>\n<td>Lysine and Tryptophan </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wheat </td>\n<td>Lysine and Threonine </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pulses (Poor man’s meat) </td>\n<td>Methionine and cysteine </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8119","text":"Which of the following has‍ the highest content of e​ssential fatty acids?","raw_text":"Which of the following has the highest content of essential fatty acids?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Soybe‍an oil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Safflo‍wer oil","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ground‍nut oil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cocon‍ut oil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Safflower oil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Safflower oil</strong></p><p><strong>Safflower oil </strong>contains the highest content of <strong>essential fatty acids</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Linoleic acid</strong> is the most important essential fatty acid.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Dietary source </td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">% content of linoleic acid </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Safflower oil </td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">75</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Corn oil </td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">50 </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Sunflower oil </td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">62</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Soybean oil </td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">53 </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Milk is also rich in linoleic acid and oleic acid.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5642","text":"You are counseling a diabe‍tic patient to consume fo​ods with low glycemic ind‌ex. Which of the followin‌g would you advise him to​ eat?","raw_text":"You are counseling a diabetic patient to consume foods with low glycemic index. Which of the following would you advise him to eat?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"White‍ bread","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brown‍ rice","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Corn ‍flakes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Whole ‍grains","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Whole grains","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Whole grains</strong></p><p>The patient should be advised to eat <strong>whole grains</strong> since it has <strong>a low glycemic index </strong>(GI).</p>\n<p>GI is a number representing the relative ability of a carbohydrate food to increase the level of glucose in the blood. It is calculated by comparing the test carbohydrate to the standard (glucose or white bread). This measure has practical use in the <strong>management</strong> of <strong>diabetes</strong> and <strong>obesity</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Low GI foods – most fruits and vegetables, whole grains, pasta foods, beans, and lentils.</li>\n<li><strong>Moderate GI </strong>foods – sucrose, basmati rice, and <strong>brown rice.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>High GI </strong>foods – <strong>corn flakes</strong>, baked potatoes, <strong>white bread</strong>, potatoes, watermelon, candies, and syrupy foods.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0177","text":"The renal feed of a patien‍t suffering from end-stag​e renal disease (stage 5)‌ should ideally be:","raw_text":"The renal feed of a patient suffering from end-stage renal disease (stage 5) should ideally be:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low volume, ‍high calories","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"High volume,‍ low calories","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High volume, high calories‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Low volume, ‍low calories","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Low volume, high calories","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Low volume, high calories</strong></p><p>The <strong>renal feed</strong> of a patient suffering from end-stage renal disease should ideally be <strong>low volume, high calories.</strong></p>\n<p>Dietary factors may have an effect on the progression of kidney disease and its complications. A <strong>low-volume</strong> feed is preferred in order to avoid:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sodium and volume overload</li>\n<li>Hyperkalemia</li>\n<li>Hyperphosphatemia</li>\n<li>Accumulation of toxic metabolites of protein degradation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A <strong>high-calorie</strong> feed is preferred in order to <strong>combat</strong> the patient's risk of <strong>malnutrition</strong> and <strong>wasting</strong> seen in ESRD.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4407","text":"Which of the following is ‍not used in the assessmen​t of body fat?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not used in the assessment of body fat?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Quetelet‍'s index","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Total body‍ potassium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Total bo‍dy water","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Breslo‍w index","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Breslow index","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Breslow index</strong></p><p><strong>Breslow's</strong> <strong>staging</strong> is used for the staging of <strong>malignant melanoma</strong>, not for the assessment of body fat.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3137","text":"In the Indian population, ‍individuals with BMI fall​ing between which range a‌re defined as overweight?‌","raw_text":"In the Indian population, individuals with BMI falling between which range are defined as overweight?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"23 - ‍24.99","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"23 - ‍26.99","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"25 - ‍29.99","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt‍; 25","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 23 - 24.99","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 23 - 24.99</strong></p><p>In the <strong>Indian</strong> (Asian) population, a BMI of <strong>23-24.99</strong> is classified as <strong>overweight</strong>.</p>\n<p>However, in the <strong>global</strong> population, a BMI of <strong>25-29.99</strong> is considered overweight.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3135","text":"What value of BMI is consi‍dered as 'lethal' in men?​","raw_text":"What value of BMI is considered as 'lethal' in men?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt‍; 12","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt‍; 13","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt‍; 14","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt‍; 15","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. < 13","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. &lt; 13</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">A body mass index (<strong>BMI</strong>)<strong> </strong>of <strong>13 or less</strong><strong> </strong>is considered <strong>lethal</strong> in <strong>men</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">However, <strong>females</strong> can<strong> </strong>survive even at a lower BMI up to <strong>11</strong>.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8156","text":"Which of the following ind‍icators are used in Water​low’s classification? Wei‌ght for age Height for ag‌e Weight for height BMI f​or age Head circumference​ for age","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following indicators are used in Waterlow’s classification?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Weight for age</li>\n<li>Height for age</li>\n<li>Weight for height</li>\n<li>BMI for age</li>\n<li>Head circumference for age</li>\n</ol>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2, ‍3 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 4‍ only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3‍ only","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"4, 5‍ only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2, 3 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2, 3 only</strong></p><p><strong>Waterlow’s classification</strong> makes use of <strong>height for age</strong> (HFA) and <strong>weight for height</strong> (WFH) to assess the nutritional status of children.</p>\n<p><strong>HFA</strong> indicates <strong>stunting </strong>and denotes the <strong>chronicity</strong> of malnutrition. On the other hand, <strong>WFH</strong> indicates <strong>wasting </strong>and denotes the <strong>severity </strong>of malnutrition.</p>\n<p>The<strong> Gomez classification </strong>uses <strong>weight for age</strong> as an indicator of nutritional status. The disadvantage is that it is difficult to determine whether the low weight is due to an acute attack of malnutrition or chronic undernutrition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"XJiADJ5-","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"30_tonsils","title":"30.Tonsils","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB5411","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is incorrect abou‍t tonsils?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect about tonsils?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bed of the tonsil is forme‌d by the superior constri‍ctor and styloglossus mus​cles.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is covered by keratiniz‌ing stratified squamous e‍pithelium on the medial s​urface.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Crypta magna is a remnant ‌of the ventral part of se‍cond pharyngeal pouch.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A capsule covers the later‌al surface of the tonsils‍.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is covered by keratinizing stratified squamous epithelium on the medial surface.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is covered by keratinizing stratified squamous epithelium on the medial surface.</strong></p><p>The <strong>medial surface</strong> of the tonsil is covered by <strong>non-keratinizing stratified squamous</strong> epithelium.</p>\n<p><strong>Tonsils</strong> are ovoid masses of <strong>lymphoid tissue</strong> situated in the lateral wall of the oropharynx between the anterior and posterior pillars.</p>\n<p>The medial surface of the tonsil is covered in 12-15 crypts. <strong>Crypta magna </strong>or<strong> intratonsillar cleft</strong> is the largest &amp; deepest of the crypts. It is situated near the upper part of the tonsil and represents the <strong>ventral part</strong> of the <strong>second pharyngeal pouch</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>lateral surface</strong> of the tonsil presents a well-defined <strong>fibrous capsule</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>bed</strong> of the tonsil is formed by the <strong>superior constrictor</strong> and <strong>styloglossus</strong> muscles.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5413","text":"At what age do the tonsils‌ attain their maximum siz‍e?","raw_text":"At what age do the tonsils attain their maximum size?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 ‌yr","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5 ‌yrs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"8 ‌yrs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"12 ‌yrs","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 12 yrs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 12 yrs</strong></p><p>The <strong>growth</strong> of tonsils is <strong>rapid</strong> around the age of <strong>5-6 years</strong> of age and usually reaches their <strong>maximum size</strong> by <strong>12 years</strong> of age.</p>\n<p>Tonsillar <strong>involution</strong> begins at <strong>puberty</strong> when the reactive lymphoid tissue begins to atrophy, and by old age, only a little tonsillar lymphoid tissue remains.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5414","text":"A patient diagnosed with o‌ral carcinoma was referre‍d to the ENT department f​or evaluation. While exam‌ining the oral cavity, th​e consultant asked you to​ examine the ENT surgeon’​s graveyard to check for ‍any induration or ulcers.​ What is the area being r‍eferred to here?","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with oral carcinoma was referred to the ENT department for evaluation. While examining the oral cavity, the consultant asked you to examine the ENT surgeon’s graveyard to check for any induration or ulcers. What is the area being referred to here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior ton‌sillar fossa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior ton‌sillar recess","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tonsillolin‌gual sulcus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lingual‌ tonsils","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tonsillolingual sulcus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9020469117924928a6dc632122e96fcdx1280x1678.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tonsillolingual sulcus</strong></p><p>The area referred to here is the <strong>tonsillolingual sulcus</strong> which is known as the <strong>graveyard of ENT surgeons.</strong></p>\n<p>The palatine tonsil is separated from the tongue by a sulcus called the tonsillolingual sulcus, which may be the <strong>seat</strong> of <strong>carcinoma.</strong> This area is <strong>easily missed</strong> during oral examination hence known as the graveyard of ENT surgeons.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5412","text":"Which of the following is ‌the main arterial supply ‍of palatine tonsils?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the main arterial supply of palatine tonsils?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tonsillar branch of the fa‌cial artery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Descending palatine branch‌ of maxillary artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Buccal branch of maxillary‌ artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ascending palatine branch ‌of facial artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tonsillar branch of the facial artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d294dddf0d1b4521958721a1e1f55fc9x1280x1524.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tonsillar branch of the facial artery</strong></p><p>The <strong>main arterial supply</strong> of tonsil is by the <strong>tonsillar branch</strong> of the <strong>facial artery.</strong></p>\n<p>Five arteries supply the tonsil:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Tonsillar branch</strong> of the <strong>facial artery</strong> (the main artery)</li>\n<li><strong>Ascending palatine</strong> branch of the <strong>facial artery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Ascending pharyngeal</strong> branch of the <strong>external carotid artery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Dorsal lingual</strong> branches of the <strong>lingual artery</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Descending palatine</strong> branches of the <strong>maxillary artery</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5415","text":"You are the second-year EN‌T resident performing ton‍sillectomy in a patient w​ith chronic tonsillitis. ‌While creating a dissecti​on plane between the caps​ule and the bed of the to​nsil, you accidentally de‍epened the incision, inju​ring the tonsillar bed. W‍hich of the following str​uctures is least likely t​o be injured?","raw_text":"You are the second-year ENT resident performing tonsillectomy in a patient with chronic tonsillitis. While creating a dissection plane between the capsule and the bed of the tonsil, you accidentally deepened the incision, injuring the tonsillar bed. Which of the following structures is least likely to be injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glossophary‌ngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Styloid‌ process","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Submandibular salivary gla‌nd","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral pter‌ygoid muscle","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lateral pterygoid muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f9cee7b1fdc14a5599c02d2d77bbfd15x1279x1929.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lateral pterygoid muscle</strong></p><p>The lateral pterygoid muscle is not related to the bed of the tonsil and is the least likely structure to be injured in this scenario.</p>\n<p>The <strong>bed</strong> <strong>of</strong> <strong>the tonsil</strong> is formed by the <strong>superior constrictor</strong> and <strong>styloglossus muscles.</strong></p>\n<p>The lower fossa is related to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The glossopharyngeal nerve</li>\n<li>The styloid process</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Outside the superior constrictor, the tonsil is related to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The facial artery</li>\n<li>Submandibular salivary gland</li>\n<li>Posterior belly of the digastric muscle</li>\n<li><strong>Medial pterygoid muscle</strong></li>\n<li>The angle of the mandible</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5419","text":"A 7-year-old child was bro‌ught to the hospital by h‍is mother with complaints​ of a sore throat, earach‌e, and pain on swallowing​ for three days. On exami​nation, the tonsils were ​enlarged and hyperaemic. ‍Which of the following is​ the most common organism‍ implicated in this condi​tion?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old child was brought to the hospital by his mother with complaints of a sore throat, earache, and pain on swallowing for three days. On examination, the tonsils were enlarged and hyperaemic. Which of the following is the most common organism implicated in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"β-haemolytic streptococcus‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Haemophilu‌s influenza","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococcu‌s pneumoniae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. β-haemolytic streptococcus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. β-haemolytic streptococcus</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute tonsillitis</strong>. The <strong>most common organism</strong> causing acute tonsillitis in <strong>children</strong> is <strong>β hemolytic streptococcus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other causative organisms include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></strong></li>\n<li><em><strong>Streptococcus pneumoniae</strong></em></li>\n<li><strong><em>Haemophilus influenzae</em></strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5417","text":"A young lady presents to t‌he ENT OPD with the findi‍ng shown below. Which of ​the following group of ly‌mph nodes will primarily ​enlarge in this condition​?","raw_text":"A young lady presents to the ENT OPD with the finding shown below. Which of the following group of lymph nodes will primarily enlarge in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jugulo-d‌igastric","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Jugulo-‌omohyoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subman‌dibular","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retroph‌aryngeal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Jugulo-digastric","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fc5bc0f4b091468eae3f854e9a4c7b60.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Jugulo-digastric</strong></p><p>The image shows purulent inflammation of the tonsils suggestive of <strong>acute tonsillitis. </strong><strong>Jugulo-digastric (tonsillar) lymph nodes</strong> will primarily enlarge in this condition.</p>\n<p>The<strong> </strong><strong>lymphatics</strong> from the <strong>palatine tonsils</strong> pierce the superior constrictor muscle and <strong>primarily drain</strong> into the <strong>upper deep cervical nodes</strong> (level II), particularly the jugulo-digastric (tonsillar) node situated below the angle of the mandible. So, it is the most likely group of lymph nodes to enlarge in case of acute tonsillitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5422","text":"A middle-aged man presente‌d with a history of fever‍, sore throat, and dyspha​gia for three days. Which‌ of the following variant​s is not expected to be s​een on oral examination?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man presented with a history of fever, sore throat, and dysphagia for three days. Which of the following variants is not expected to be seen on oral examination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Follicular ‌tonsillitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Membranous ‌tonsillitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fibroid t‌onsillitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Parenchymatous tonsillitis‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fibroid tonsillitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fibroid tonsillitis</strong></p><p>The patient's history is suggestive of <strong>acute tonsillitis</strong>. The <strong>fibroid variety</strong> is a type of <strong>chronic tonsillitis </strong>and is not expected to be seen in this case.</p>\n<p>Depending on the pathology within the tonsillar tissue, <strong>acute tonsillitis can be classified</strong> into the following types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Acute catarrhal</strong> or <strong>superficial tonsillitis: </strong>Here tonsillitis is a part of generalized pharyngitis and is primarily seen in viral infections.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Acute follicular tonsillitis: </strong>The infection spreads into the crypts, which become filled with purulent material, presenting at the openings of crypts as yellowish spots.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Acute parenchymatous tonsillitis: </strong>Here tonsil substance is affected. Tonsil is uniformly enlarged and red.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Acute membranous tonsillitis: </strong>It is a stage ahead of acute follicular tonsillitis when exudation from the crypts coalesces to form a membrane on the surface of the tonsil.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Types of <strong>chronic tonsillitis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic follicular tonsillitis</li>\n<li>Chronic parenchymal tonsillitis</li>\n<li>Chronic fibroid tonsillitis</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 309\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5437","text":"An anxious mother brought ‌her 4-year-old-child to t‍he hospital with complain​ts of sudden onset fever ‌and refusal to eat. On ex​amination, the given find​ing was noted and he was ​started on analgesics and‍ antibiotics. Which among​ the following is not a k‍nown complication of this​ condition?","raw_text":"An anxious mother brought her 4-year-old-child to the hospital with complaints of sudden onset fever and refusal to eat. On examination, the given finding was noted and he was started on analgesics and antibiotics. Which among the following is not a known complication of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Excessive‌ bleeding","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acute oti‌tis media","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rheumat‌ic fever","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deep neck space infections‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Excessive bleeding","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/af7691987eee464d800b8bdac58085a2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Excessive bleeding</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>acute tonsillitis</strong>. Excessive bleeding is not a known complication of this condition.</p>\n<p>Complications of acute tonsillitis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chronic tonsillitis </strong>- chronic infection may persist in lymphoid follicles of the tonsil in the form of microabscesses</li>\n<li><strong>Peritonsillar abscess</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Parapharyngeal abscess</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Cervical abscess</strong> due to suppuration of jugulodigastric lymph nodes</li>\n<li><strong>Acute</strong> <strong>otitis media</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Rheumatic fever </strong>- often seen in association with tonsillitis due to group A β-hemolytic streptococci</li>\n<li><strong>Acute glomerulonephritis </strong>(rare)</li>\n<li><strong>Subacute bacterial endocarditis </strong>- acute tonsillitis in a patient with valvular heart disease may be complicated by endocarditis. It is usually due to <strong><em>Streptococcus viridans</em></strong> infection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4260","text":"Which of the following ner‌ves is responsible for re‍ferred otalgia from tonsi​llitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following nerves is responsible for referred otalgia from tonsillitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glossophary‌ngeal nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Facial‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trigemin‌al nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vagus‌ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Glossopharyngeal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c8d4a9369334b1fb315596674356c0dx1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Glossopharyngeal nerve</strong></p><p>The<strong> tympanic branch</strong> of the<strong> glossopharyngeal nerve</strong> (cranial nerve IX) is responsible for <strong>referred otalgia</strong> from tonsillitis.</p>\n<p>The tonsils and tonsillar fossa are supplied by the <strong>glossopharyngeal nerve</strong>. The glossopharyngeal nerve and the styloid process lie in relation to the lower part of the tonsillar fossa. Therefore, any irritation or pain can be referred to the ear along the tympanic branch of the glossopharyngeal (Jacobson's) nerve.</p>\n<p>Causes of referred otalgia via the glossopharyngeal nerve include acute pharyngitis, <strong>t</strong><strong>onsillitis</strong>, peritonsillar abscess, post-tonsillectomy, foreign bodies embedded in the base of the tongue or tonsillar area and an elongated styloid process (Eagle's syndrome).</p>\n<p>The ear receives extensive sensory innervation via four cranial nerves (V, VII, IX, and X) and two spinal segments (C2 and C3).</p>\n<p>The image below shows the anatomic relations of the tonsil and tonsillar bed: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5426","text":"A 15-year-old girl present‌ed to the ENT surgeon wit‍h a history of loud snori​ng, restless sleep leadin‌g to daytime somnolence, ​and poor academic perform​ance. Her medical history​ was unremarkable, except‍ for recurrent episodes o​f acute tonsillitis. Whic‍h of the following types ​of tonsillitis may be imp​licated in this condition‌?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old girl presented to the ENT surgeon with a history of loud snoring, restless sleep leading to daytime somnolence, and poor academic performance. Her medical history was unremarkable, except for recurrent episodes of acute tonsillitis. Which of the following types of tonsillitis may be implicated in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute me‌mbranous","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute fo‌llicular","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic‌ fibroid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic par‌enchymatous","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chronic parenchymatous","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c3156e2053cf454f8578b8b2c62d6067x1280x1196.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chronic parenchymatous</strong></p><p>The given clinical presentation is suggestive of <strong>obstructive sleep apnea syndrome</strong>(OSAS) secondary to <strong>chronic parenchymatous tonsillitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>In parenchymatous tonsillitis, the tonsils grow very large following repeated attacks of acute tonsillitis and lead to obstructive symptoms, including OSAS. </p>\n<p>Types of chronic tonsillitis: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Chronic follicular tonsillitis</strong>- tonsillar crypts are full of infected cheesy material, which shows on the surface as yellowish spots. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Chronic parenchymatous tonsillitis</strong>- there is hyperplasia of lymphoid tissue. Tonsils are very much enlarged and may interfere with speech, deglutition, and respiration. Attacks of sleep apnoea may occur. Long-standing cases develop features of cor pulmonale.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Chronic fibroid tonsillitis</strong>- tonsils are tiny but infected, with a history of repeated sore throats.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5433","text":"You are an intern posted i‌n the ENT department work‍ing up a case of chronic ​follicular tonsillitis. W‌hich of the following cli​nical signs would you not​ expect to see in this pa​tient on examining the or‍al cavity?","raw_text":"You are an intern posted in the ENT department working up a case of chronic follicular tonsillitis. Which of the following clinical signs would you not expect to see in this patient on examining the oral cavity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Irwin-Mo‌ore sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enlarged jugulo-digastric ‌nodes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Congested anterior pillars‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Müller‌'s sign","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Müller's sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Müller&#x27;s sign</strong></p><p><strong>Müller's sign</strong> is the pulsation of uvula during systole seen in <strong>aortic regurgitation</strong>. It is not seen in patients with chronic tonsillitis.</p>\n<p>The classical signs of <strong>chronic tonsillitis</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>I</strong><strong>rwin-Moore sign-</strong> tonsils are tiny, but pressure on the anterior pillar expresses frank pus or cheesy material.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Bluish Flushing of anterior pillars</strong> compared to the rest of the pharyngeal mucosa is a vital sign of chronic tonsillar infection.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Enlargement of jugulo-digastric lymph nodes</strong> even in the absence of acute infection is a reliable sign of chronic tonsillitis. During acute attacks, the nodes enlarge further and become tender.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5483","text":"An ENT junior resident obs‌erved a symptomless yello‍wish swelling over the to​nsil when he was examinin‌g a patient for a buccal ​sore. The swelling was ea​sily drained. What is the​ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An ENT junior resident observed a symptomless yellowish swelling over the tonsil when he was examining a patient for a buccal sore. The swelling was easily drained. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intratonsil‌lar abscess","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tonsil‌lolith","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tonsill‌ar cyst","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Follicular ‌tonsillitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tonsillar cyst","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tonsillar cyst</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>tonsillar cyst. </strong>It is a condition due to blockage of a tonsillar crypt and appears as a yellowish swelling over the tonsil. Very often, it is <strong>symptomless</strong> and can be easily drained.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Intratonsillar abscess </strong>is an accumulation of pus within the substance of the tonsil. It usually follows a blockage of the opening of the crypt in acute follicular tonsillitis. There is <strong>marked local pain</strong> and <strong>dysphagia.</strong> Tonsil appears swollen and red.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Tonsilloliths</strong> (<strong>calculus of the tonsil</strong>) are seen in chronic tonsillitis when its crypt is blocked with retention of debris. Inorganic salts of calcium and magnesium are then deposited leading to the formation of a stone. It may gradually <strong>enlarge</strong> and then <strong>ulcerate</strong> through the tonsil. It generally causes <strong>local discomfort</strong> or foreign body sensation.</p>\n<p>Option D: In <strong>follicular tonsillitis, purulent material</strong> is present at the opening of the crypts as yellow dots. It presents with <strong>fever, dysphagia,</strong> and <strong>sore throat.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 310\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5441","text":"On examining the oral cavi‌ty of a patient who prese‍nted with fever and a sor​e throat, you notice a gr‌eyish-white membrane over​ the tonsils. Which of th​e following conditions wo​uld you not consider as a‍ differential diagnosis?","raw_text":"On examining the oral cavity of a patient who presented with fever and a sore throat, you notice a greyish-white membrane over the tonsils. Which of the following conditions would you not consider as a differential diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococca‌l tonsillitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dipht‌heria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ludwig’‌s angina","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Infectious m‌ononucleosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ludwig’s angina","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ludwig’s angina</strong></p><p>Ludwig’s angina is not associated with the formation of a grey-white membrane. It is characterized by rapidly spreading infection of the sublingual space initially and later the submaxillary space, often dentogenic in origin.</p>\n<p><strong>D/D for membrane over tonsil:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Streptococcus &amp; other pyogenic organisms</li>\n<li>Diphtheria</li>\n<li>Infectious mononucleosis</li>\n<li>Vincent’s angina</li>\n<li>Leukemia</li>\n<li>Candida</li>\n<li>Agranulocytosis</li>\n<li>Malignancy</li>\n<li>Apthous ulcer</li>\n<li>Traumatic ulcer</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5449","text":"Which of the following org‌anisms is implicated in V‍incent’s angina?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms is implicated in Vincent’s angina?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"β-haemolytic‌ streptococci","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bacillus ‌fusiformis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Treponema‌ pallidum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Microaerophilic streptococ‌ci","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bacillus fusiformis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bacillus fusiformis</strong></p><p><strong>Vincent’s angina</strong> is caused by <strong>fusiform bacilli</strong> and <strong>spirochaetes,</strong> namely <strong><em>Bacillus fusiformis</em> </strong>and <em><strong>Borrelia vincenti</strong></em>.</p>\n<p>The condition presents with low-grade fever and sore throat, and a <strong>membrane over the tonsil</strong>. The membrane is easily removed to reveal irregular ulcers over the tonsil. Throat swab will show both the organisms typical of the disease, namely fusiform bacilli and spirochaetes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5468","text":"A worried mother brought h‌er 6-year-old unimmunized‍ child to the ER with com​plaints of fever, cough, ‌and shortness of breath. ​On examination, a pseudom​embrane was noted in the ​oral cavity along with th‍e neck finding as shown i​n the image. Which of the‍ following statements is ​true regarding the membra​ne observed?","raw_text":"A worried mother brought her 6-year-old unimmunized child to the ER with complaints of fever, cough, and shortness of breath. On examination, a pseudomembrane was noted in the oral cavity along with the neck finding as shown in the image. Which of the following statements is true regarding the membrane observed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Produced by ‌the endotoxin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Firmly adherent and bleeds‌ on removal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Confined to‌ the tonsil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pearly whi‌te in color","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Firmly adherent and bleeds on removal","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c95772e7ca0c412cb184923fa2dec8dc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Firmly adherent and bleeds on removal</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and bull-neck appearance (image) point to the diagnosis of <strong>diphtheria</strong>. The diphtheritic <strong>membrane</strong> is <strong>firmly adherent</strong> and <strong>bleeds</strong> on removal.</p>\n<p>A<strong> greyish-white membrane</strong> forms over the tonsils in the oropharynx and spreads to the soft palate and posterior pharyngeal wall. The membrane in diphtheria extends beyond the tonsils onto the soft palate and is dirty grey. It is adherent, and its removal leaves a bleeding surface. In the <strong>larynx,</strong> the diphtheritic membrane may cause <strong>airway obstruction.</strong></p>\n<p>Cervical lymph nodes, particularly the <strong>jugulodigastric</strong>, become enlarged and tender, sometimes presenting a <strong>bull-neck appearance</strong>. The patient is ill and toxaemic, but fever seldom rises above 38°C.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5474","text":"Which of the following com‌plications would you not ‍expect to see in a patien​t diagnosed with diphther‌ia?","raw_text":"Which of the following complications would you not expect to see in a patient diagnosed with diphtheria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myoca‌rditis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arrhy‌thmias","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Airway ob‌struction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hard palat‌e paralysis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hard palate paralysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hard palate paralysis</strong></p><p>Hard palate paralysis is not seen in diphtheria.</p>\n<p>The <strong>exotoxin</strong> produced by <em>C. diphtheriae</em> is <strong>toxic</strong> to the <strong>heart</strong> and <strong>nerves.</strong> It causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Myocarditis</li>\n<li>Cardiac arrhythmias</li>\n<li>Acute circulatory failure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Neurological complications include <strong>paralysis </strong>of the<strong> soft-palate</strong>,<strong> diaphragm</strong>, and<strong> ocular muscles</strong>. In the larynx, the diphtheric membrane may cause <strong>airway obstruction</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5480","text":"Following finding was note‌d in a patient who presen‍ted with difficulty breat​hing and a bull-neck appe‌arance. Which of the foll​owing statements is true ​regarding the treatment o​f this patient?","raw_text":"Following finding was noted in a patient who presented with difficulty breathing and a bull-neck appearance. Which of the following statements is true regarding the treatment of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Treatment is started after‌ the culture comes positi‍ve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Treatment is with penicill‌in alone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antitoxin is reserved for ‌complication","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sensitivity to antitoxin s‌hould be tested prior to ‍therapy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sensitivity to antitoxin should be tested prior to therapy","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Dirty_white_pseudomembrane_classically_seen_in_diphtheria_2013-07-06_11-07.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sensitivity to antitoxin should be tested prior to therapy</strong></p><p>The pseudomembrane on oral examination and bull neck appearance is characteristic of <strong>diphtheria. </strong><strong>Sensitivity</strong> to diphtheria <strong>antitoxin</strong> is <strong>common</strong> and should be <strong>tested prior</strong> to the initiation of <strong>therapy.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment is started on clinical suspicion without waiting for the culture report. The aim is to <strong>neutralize the free exotoxin</strong> still circulating in the blood and <strong>to kill the organisms</strong> producing this exotoxin.</p>\n<p><strong>Antitoxins:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dose- based on the <strong>site</strong> involved, <strong>duration,</strong> and <strong>severity</strong> of the disease\n<ul>\n<li>If <strong>&lt; 48 hours</strong> or just <strong>confined to tonsils</strong>: 20,000–40,000 units</li>\n<li>If <strong>&gt; 48 hours</strong> or <strong>extensive membrane</strong>: 80,000–120,000 units</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Given by<strong> i.v. infusion</strong> in saline in about 60 min</li>\n<li>Sensitivity tested by a conjunctival or intracutaneous test with diluted antitoxin</li>\n<li>If a hypersensitivity reaction occurs, desensitization should be done</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Antibiotics:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Benzyl penicillin</strong> 600 mg 6 hourly for 7 days</li>\n<li><strong>Erythromycin</strong> is used in penicillin-sensitive individuals (500 mg 6 hourly orally)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5489","text":"Which of the following cas‌es cannot be posted for a‍n elective tonsillectomy?​","raw_text":"Which of the following cases cannot be posted for an elective tonsillectomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 7-year-old child with ac‌ute tonsillitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A young adult with chronic‌ tonsillitis refractory t‍o treatment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A patient with unilateral ‌enlargement of tonsil wit‍h suspected malignancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A middle-aged man with rec‌urrent quinsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A 7-year-old child with acute tonsillitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A 7-year-old child with acute tonsillitis</strong></p><p><strong>Acute tonsillitis</strong> is a <strong>contraindication</strong> for <strong>tonsillectomy</strong> and hence, cannot be posted for elective surgery.</p>\n<p>Acute tonsillitis or any upper respiratory tract infection is a contraindication for tonsillectomy since there is an <strong>increased</strong> chance of <strong>hemorrhage</strong> and <strong>sepsis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Indications of tonsillectomy are:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recurrent episodes of acute tonsillitis – most common indication<br/>Seven or more episodes in 1 year, or<br/>Five episodes per year for 2 years, or<br/>Three episodes per year for 3 years, or<br/>Two weeks or more of lost school or work in 1 year</li>\n<li>Febrile seizures due to fever in tonsillitis</li>\n<li>Peritonsillar abscess (quinsy) - recurrent episodes in adults and a single episode in children</li>\n<li>Hypertrophy of tonsils causing airway obstruction, difficulty in deglutition, interference with speech</li>\n<li>Unilateral enlargement of tonsil with suspicion of malignancy</li>\n<li>Chronic tonsillitis with bad taste or halitosis which is unresponsive to medical treatment (relative indication)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5491","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a contraindication to‍ perform a tonsillectomy?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a contraindication to perform a tonsillectomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute to‌nsillitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bleeding ‌disorders","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cleft ‌palate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diphtheri‌a carrier","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Diphtheria carrier","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Diphtheria carrier</strong></p><p><strong>Diphtheria</strong> and <strong>streptococcal carriers</strong> are <strong>relative indications</strong> for tonsillectomy and the surgery can be safely performed in patients presenting with the same.</p>\n<p><strong>Contraindications for tonsillectomy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Haemoglobin level less than 10 g%.</li>\n<li>Presence of acute infection in upper respiratory tract, even acute tonsillitis. Bleeding is more in the presence of acute infection.</li>\n<li>Children under 3 years of age.</li>\n<li>Overt or submucous cleft palate.</li>\n<li>Bleeding disorders, e.g. leukaemia, purpura, aplastic anaemia or haemophilia.</li>\n<li>At the time of epidemic of polio.</li>\n<li>Uncontrolled systemic disease, e.g. diabetes, cardiac disease, hypertension or asthma.</li>\n<li>Tonsillectomy is avoided during the period of menses.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5504","text":"A patient develops bleedin‌g from the surgical site ‍five hours after an elect​ive tonsillectomy. Which ‌among the following would​ best describe the condit​ion?","raw_text":"A patient develops bleeding from the surgical site five hours after an elective tonsillectomy. Which among the following would best describe the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary h‌aemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secondary ‌haemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reactionary‌ haemorrhage","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tertiary h‌aemorrhage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Reactionary haemorrhage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Reactionary haemorrhage</strong></p><p><strong>Bleeding</strong> from the surgical site <strong>within</strong> a period of <strong>24 hours</strong> is categorised as a <strong>reactionary</strong> haemorrhage.</p>\n<p><strong>Primary </strong>haemorrhage - occurs <strong>at the time</strong> of operation.</p>\n<p><strong>Secondary</strong> haemorrhage - usually seen between the <strong>fifth <span class=\"TextRun SCXW24420069\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW24420069\">to tenth postoperative day</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW24420069\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW24420069\">s.</span></span></strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5506","text":"While preparing for a proc‌edure, the patient was ke‍pt in a specific position​ as depicted in the image‌. In which of the followi​ng procedures is this pos​ition not adopted?","raw_text":"While preparing for a procedure, the patient was kept in a specific position as depicted in the image. In which of the following procedures is this position not adopted?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tonsil‌lectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adenoi‌dectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Direct la‌ryngoscopy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trache‌ostomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Direct laryngoscopy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/06677c7957d947ef851f52e1d63800b0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/17029464ff354d20a7fc355f40666b61x720x655.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/63e110d92fd14cd9911797e2e6d1a4ccx351x382.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Direct laryngoscopy</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>Rose's</strong> <strong>position</strong> and it is not a suitable position to perform a direct laryngoscopy. The barking-dog position is used for direct laryngoscopy.</p>\n<p><strong>Rose’s position: </strong>The patient lies supine with head extended by placing a pillow under the shoulders. It is followed in adenoidectomy, tonsillectomy, and tracheostomy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Barking-dog position</strong>: Patient lies supine. The Head is elevated by 10–15 cm by placing a pillow under the occiput or raising the operation table’s head flap. The neck is flexed on the thorax and the head is extended on the atlanto-occipital joint.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Ov8wvpx0","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"30_inflammatory_bowel_disease___complications_and_treatment","title":"30.Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Complications And Treatment","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MC0341","text":"A patient with inflammator‌y bowel disease presents ​with the following lesion‌. Which of the following ​is incorrect regarding th​is condition?","raw_text":"A patient with inflammatory bowel disease presents with the following lesion. Which of the following is incorrect regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can occur years before the‌ onset of bowel symptoms","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Steroids should be avoided‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Can develop years after co‌lectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Associated with severe dis‌ease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Steroids should be avoided","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/33a63f2c81144a7ead5aec64462f0d7e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Steroids should be avoided</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>pyoderma gangrenosum</strong>. It is very difficult to <strong>treat</strong> and often requires IV antibiotics and IV<strong> glucocorticoids</strong>. Other drugs used in its management include dapsone, cyclosporine, and infliximab.</p>\n<p>Pyoderma gangrenosum is a dermatological<strong> extraintestinal manifestation </strong>of inflammatory bowel disease. It begins as a pustule and progresses to an ulcer with necrotic tissue and exudates. It may occur years before the onset of bowel symptoms and is usually <strong>associated with severe disease.</strong></p>\n<p>Lesions are commonly found on the dorsal surface of the feet and legs but may occur on the arms, chest, and even the face. It <strong>responds poorly to colectomy </strong>and may even develop years after the surgery.</p>\n<p>Other dermatological manifestations associated with IBD include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Erythema nodosum</li>\n<li>Sweet syndrome (neutrophilic dermatosis)</li>\n<li>Perianal skin tags</li>\n<li>Psoriasis</li>\n<li>Metastatic Crohn's disease (cutaneous granulomas).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0309","text":"A 37-year-old man presente‌d with hematuria and dysu​ria for 5 days. He also c‌omplains of chronic diarr​hea and weight loss for 6​ months. His urine micros‌copy revealed the followi‌ng stones. What is the mo‌st likely disorder associ​ated with this finding?","raw_text":"A 37-year-old man presented with hematuria and dysuria for 5 days. He also complains of chronic diarrhea and weight loss for 6 months. His urine microscopy revealed the following stones. What is the most likely disorder associated with this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crohn’s‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ulcerativ‌e colitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Irritable bo‌wel syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lactose i‌ntolerance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Crohn’s disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/21b6acc92e5b4e9a829425348b332c2c.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Crohn’s disease</strong></p><p>The given urinary crystal is the bipyramidal or envelope-shaped crystals of<strong> </strong>calcium<strong> oxalate</strong> dihydrate. Calcium<strong> oxalate </strong>stones are more commonly associated with <strong>Crohn's disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>In Crohn's disease (especially post small bowel resection), fatty acids are not absorbed because of <strong>ileal dysfunction.</strong> These fatty acids in the gut lumen bind to calcium. Because of this, the dietary oxalates which normally bind to the calcium are now left unbound. This leads to <strong>increased absorption of dietary oxalates</strong> leading to hyperoxaluria and oxalate stones.</p>\n<p>Note: Celiac disease is also associated with calcium oxalate stones.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0344","text":"Perianal examination of a ‌patient revealed the foll​owing finding. Which cond‌ition is this associated ​with?","raw_text":"Perianal examination of a patient revealed the following finding. Which condition is this associated with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ulcerativ‌e colitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Crohn’s‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ischemic‌ colitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ileocecal t‌uberculosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Crohn’s disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4341cba1f82b489fa904e3f6e0d276fd.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Crohn’s disease</strong></p><p>The above image shows a large<strong> hemorrhoidal perianal skin tag. </strong>These are found in patients with <strong>Crohn's disease</strong>,<strong> </strong>especially those with colon involvement.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0335","text":"A 28-year-old man was brou‌ght to the casualty with ​a high fever and abdomina‌l pain for the past 2 day​s. His history is signifi​cant for bloody diarrhea ‌over the past few months.‌ The abdominal radiograph‌ of this patient is given​ below. Identify the cond​ition.","raw_text":"A 28-year-old man was brought to the casualty with a high fever and abdominal pain for the past 2 days. His history is significant for bloody diarrhea over the past few months. The abdominal radiograph of this patient is given below. Identify the condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Toxic m‌egacolon","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Intussu‌sception","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sigmoid ‌volvulus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pneumatosis ‌intestinalis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Toxic megacolon","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/130cd246bba54be5a6b7c5336787ec33.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Toxic megacolon</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario and radiograph are suggestive of<strong> toxic megacolon. </strong>It is seen in<strong> severe ulcerative colitis. </strong></p>\n<p>Abdominal radiography in toxic megacolon shows transverse or right colonic<strong> dilation &gt;6 cm </strong>and<strong> l</strong><strong>oss of haustrations.</strong></p>\n<p>Toxic megacolon may present with fever, abdominal pain, and abdominal distension or features of sepsis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0308","text":"A patient with inflammator‌y bowel disease presents ​with ocular pain, photoph‌obia, and blurred vision.​ Which among the followin​g is the most likely caus‌e?","raw_text":"A patient with inflammatory bowel disease presents with ocular pain, photophobia, and blurred vision. Which among the following is the most likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uve‌itis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Episcl‌eritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scle‌ritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Conjunc‌tivitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Uveitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Uveitis</strong></p><p><strong>Uveitis</strong> is the most common cause of the given symptoms in a patient with inflammatory bowel disease (IBD).</p>\n<p>Uveitis is an <strong>ophthalmic manifestation of IBD</strong>. It is associated with both ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease. It can develop during remission and is seen even after bowel resection. Prompt intervention with <strong>glucocorticoids</strong> is required to prevent <strong>scarring</strong> and <strong>visual impairment.</strong></p>\n<p>The other common ophthalmic manifestations in IBD are <strong>conjunctivitis </strong>and<strong> episcleritis</strong>. Episcleritis, more common in Crohn's, is a benign disorder presenting with mild ocular burning.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1306","text":"A 34-year-old man with Cro‌hn’s disease presents wit​h red tender swellings on‌ the lower leg as shown b​elow. What is the likely ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old man with Crohn’s disease presents with red tender swellings on the lower leg as shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyoderma g‌angrenosum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Erythema‌ nodosum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sweet’s ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ecthyma g‌angrenosum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Erythema nodosum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3e402666312646af894043d450dac7a0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bab2382f5fbc42ff8a3041bfbe17124bx640x480.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e3143b2f02254c7ea92ecd571a79bc70x685x514.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c250c41b14fe4cee900baaf8dfb0f9c2x719x507.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Erythema nodosum</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario suggests a diagnosis of <strong>e</strong><strong>rythema nodosum</strong> seen in inflammatory bowel disease (IBD). </p>\n<p>Erythema nodosum is a dermatologic manifestation of IBD. It is<strong> </strong>seen in both ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease. It manifests as <strong>tender erythematous</strong> <strong>nodules</strong> on the anterior surface of the lower legs, ankles, thighs, and arms.</p>\n<p>Option A: Pyoderma gangrenosum is a dermatological manifestation of IBD. It begins as pustular lesions which then ulcerate with necrotic foci and exudation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: Sweet’s syndrome is acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis, seen in ulcerative colitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: Ecthyma gangrenosum is a skin infection caused by <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>, often seen in immunocompromised patients. It presents as pustules/vesicles which progress to necrotic ulcers.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0350","text":"What are the cardiopulmona‌ry manifestations associa​ted with inflammatory bow‌el disease? Endocarditis ​VSD Pleuropericarditis In​terstitial lung disease C‌OPD","raw_text":"<p>What are the cardiopulmonary manifestations associated with inflammatory bowel disease?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Endocarditis</li>\n<li>VSD</li>\n<li>Pleuropericarditis</li>\n<li>Interstitial lung disease</li>\n<li>COPD</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,‌2,5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4‌,5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2‌,3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,‌3,4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1,3,4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1,3,4</strong></p><p>The cardiopulmonary manifestations of inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endocarditis</strong></li>\n<li>Myocarditis</li>\n<li><strong>Pleuropericarditis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Interstitial lung disease</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease have an increased risk of <strong>venous</strong> and <strong>arterial thrombosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1310","text":"A 36-year-old man presents‌ with worsening fatigue, ​jaundice, and pruritis fo‌r the last 3 months. Past​ history revealed recurre​nt episodes of bloody dia‌rrhea and weight loss ove‌r the past year. Lab inve‌stigations show raised AL​P, GGT, and bilirubin, an​d an MRCP is done as show​n below. Which of the fol​lowing is most likely to ‌be associated with his cu‍rrent presentation?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old man presents with worsening fatigue, jaundice, and pruritis for the last 3 months. Past history revealed recurrent episodes of bloody diarrhea and weight loss over the past year. Lab investigations show raised ALP, GGT, and bilirubin, and an MRCP is done as shown below. Which of the following is most likely to be associated with his current presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic pa‌ncreatitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Celiac ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ulcerativ‌e colitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Crohn’s‌ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ulcerative colitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/57458a2351b14376b5e4cc3c64da1b13.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/11fbf2fa355a44b791013599b688a447x1280x1541.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ulcerative colitis</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario along with MRCP showing multiple areas of intrahepatic narrowing with alternating areas of normal and dilated segments of bile ducts is suggestive of <strong>primary sclerosing cholangitis. </strong>PSC is more common in<strong> ulcerative colitis </strong>than in Crohn's disease. </p>\n<p>Additionally, recurrent episodes of bloody diarrhea with weight loss seen in this patient also point toward a possible diagnosis of ulcerative colitis.</p>\n<p>Primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC) is an extraintestinal manifestation of IBD characterized by intrahepatic and extrahepatic duct inflammation/fibrosis. Around 50-75% of patients with <strong>primary sclerosing cholangitis</strong> (PSC) have inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) whereas, only 5% of IBD cases develop PSC.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Chronic pancreatitis</strong> is associated with a <strong>chain of lakes</strong> appearance- dilated main duct with clubbing and dilatation of the side branches. Patients can present with <strong>steatorrhoea</strong> i.e., the passage of offensive-smelling large stools due to fat malabsorption. </p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>chain of lakes</strong> appearance on MRI:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Celiac</strong> disease may be associated with primary biliary cholangitis (<strong>PBC</strong>), rather than primary sclerosing cholangitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1309","text":"After initiating treatment‌ for patients with IBD, i​n which of the following ‌extraintestinal manifesta​tions would you expect to​ see an improvement?","raw_text":"After initiating treatment for patients with IBD, in which of the following extraintestinal manifestations would you expect to see an improvement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arth‌ritis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ankylosing ‌spondylitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uve‌itis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyoderma g‌angrenosum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Arthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Arthritis</strong></p><p><strong>A</strong><strong>rthritis </strong>worsens with exacerbation of bowel disease and <strong>improves</strong> with the <strong>treatment</strong> of bowel symptoms or surgery.</p>\n<p>Ankylosing spondylitis, uveitis, and pyoderma gangrenosum do not correlate with disease activity in the bowel. They may occur during the remission of disease or even after surgery.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0356","text":"A patient is diagnosed wit‌h ulcerative colitis on e​ndoscopy. He is hemodynam‌ically stable but current​ly has 3-4 episodes of bl​ood-tinged loose stools p‌er day. Which of the foll‌owing drug would you pref‌er for treating this pati​ent?","raw_text":"A patient is diagnosed with ulcerative colitis on endoscopy. He is hemodynamically stable but currently has 3-4 episodes of blood-tinged loose stools per day.  Which of the following drug would you prefer for treating this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5-aminosal‌icylic acid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Azathi‌oprine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Budes‌onide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6-mercap‌topurine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 5-aminosalicylic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 5-aminosalicylic acid</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>mild ulcerative colitis</strong>. The preferred drug for mild ulcerative colitis (UC) is <strong>5-ASA agents </strong>(5-aminosalicylic acid). </p>\n<p>Given below is the <strong>Truelove and Witts severity index</strong> for ulcerative colitis. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 512.182px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\"><strong>Mild</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\"><strong>Moderate</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\"><strong>Severe</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">Bowel movements</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">&lt; 4 per day</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">4 – 6 per day</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\">&gt; 6 per day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">Blood in stool</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Small</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">Moderate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\">Severe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">Fever</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">None</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">&lt; 37.5ºC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\">&gt; 37.5ºC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">Tachycardia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">None</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">&lt; 90 mean pulse</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\">&gt;90 mean pulse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">Anemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Mild</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">Hb &gt; 75% of normal Hb</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\">Hb &lt; 75% of normal Hb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">ESR</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">&lt; 30mm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\"> &lt; 30mm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\"> &gt; 30mm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">Endoscopic appearance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">\n<p>Erythema</p>\n<p>Decreased vascular markings</p>\n<p>Fine granularity</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">\n<p>Marked erythema</p>\n<p>Absent vascular markings</p>\n<p>Coarse granularity</p>\n<p>Contact bleeding</p>\n<p>No ulcerations</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\">\n<p>Spontaneous bleeding</p>\n<p>Ulcerations</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<table style=\"height: 88px; width: 513px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 485px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Treatment of Ulcerative Colitis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\"><strong>Mild </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\"><strong>Moderate</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\"><strong>Severe</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\"><strong>Induction</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">5-ASA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">5-ASA + Oral steroids</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\">IV steroids + IV anti TNF alpha + IV cyclosporine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\"><strong>Maintainance</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">5-ASA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">5-ASA + Azathioprine/6-MP</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\">Oral cyclosporine or infliximab</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Note: Mirikizumab ( an IL-23 inhibitor) was recently FDA approved for the treatment of moderate to severe ulcerative colitis. It reduces stool frequency, rectal bleeding and bowel urgency.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1305","text":"A 36-year-old man presente‌d with complaints of 7-8 ​episodes of bloody diarrh‌ea per day. He is pale an​d febrile. Heart rate is ​110 bpm and ESR is 50mm. ‌Colonoscopy revealed cont‌inuous mucosal involvemen‌t with ulcerations and mu​cosal hemorrhages. Which ​of the following drugs wo​uld you not prefer in the​ management of this patie‌nt?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old man presented with complaints of 7-8 episodes of bloody diarrhea per day. He is pale and febrile. Heart rate is 110 bpm and ESR is 50mm. Colonoscopy revealed continuous mucosal involvement with ulcerations and mucosal hemorrhages. Which of the following drugs would you not prefer in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mesal‌amine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Infli‌ximab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IV st‌eroids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cyclos‌porine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mesalamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mesalamine</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>severe ulcerative colitis. Mesalamine </strong>(5-ASA) is ineffective in the management of severe ulcerative colitis (UC). It is used in the treatment of <strong>mild-moderate UC.</strong></p>\n<p>Given below is the <strong>Truelove and Witts severity index</strong> for ulcerative colitis. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 512.182px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\"><strong>Mild</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\"><strong>Moderate</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\"><strong>Severe</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">Bowel movements</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">&lt; 4 per day</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">4 – 6 per day</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\">&gt; 6 per day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">Blood in stool</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Small</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">Moderate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\">Severe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">Fever</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">None</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">&lt; 37.5ºC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\">&gt; 37.5ºC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">Tachycardia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">None</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">&lt; 90 mean pulse</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\">&gt;90 mean pulse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">Anemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Mild</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">Hb &gt; 75% of normal Hb</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\">Hb &lt; 75% of normal Hb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">ESR</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">&lt; 30mm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\"> &lt; 30mm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\"> &gt; 30mm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 72px;\">Endoscopic appearance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">\n<p>Erythema</p>\n<p>Decreased vascular markings</p>\n<p>Fine granularity</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">\n<p>Marked erythema</p>\n<p>Absent vascular markings</p>\n<p>Coarse granularity</p>\n<p>Contact bleeding</p>\n<p>No ulcerations</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.182px;\">\n<p>Spontaneous bleeding</p>\n<p>Ulcerations</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<table style=\"height: 88px; width: 514px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 486px; text-align: center;\">Treatment of Ulcerative Colitis   </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">Mild </td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">Moderate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\">Severe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">Induction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">5-ASA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">5-ASA + Oral steroids</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\">IV steroids + IV anti TNF alpha + IV cyclosporine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">Maintainance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">5-ASA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">5-ASA + Azathioprine/6-MP</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\">Oral cyclosporine or infliximab</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Note: Mirikizumab ( an IL-23 inhibitor) was recently FDA approved for the treatment of moderate to severe ulcerative colitis. It reduces stool frequency, rectal bleeding and bowel urgency.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1307","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs have no role in the ma​nagement of patients with‌ ulcerative colitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs have no role in the management of patients with ulcerative colitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral sal‌icylates","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hydroco‌rtisone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metron‌idazole","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cyclos‌porine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Metronidazole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Metronidazole</strong></p><p><strong>Metronidazole</strong> has no role in the management of ulcerative colitis. It is an antibiotic that is useful in active inflammatory, fistulous, and perianal <strong>Crohn's disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: Mirikizumab ( an IL-23 inhibitor) was recently FDA approved for the treatment of moderate to severe ulcerative colitis. It reduces stool frequency, rectal bleeding and bowel urgency.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0359","text":"A patient with Crohn's dis‌ease is started on inflix​imab. Which of the follow‌ing conditions is the pat​ient is at increased risk​ of developing?","raw_text":"A patient with Crohn's disease is started on infliximab. Which of the following conditions is the patient is at increased risk of developing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hodgkin’s‌ Lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-Hodgki‌n lymphoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hairy cell‌ Leukaemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Follicula‌r lymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Non-Hodgkin lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Non-Hodgkin lymphoma</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>Crohn's disease </strong>on<strong> infliximab</strong> are at increased risk of <strong>non-Hodgkin lymphoma</strong> (NHL) and<strong> hepatosplenic T-cell lymphoma </strong>(HS-TCL).</p>\n<p>Infliximab is the first<strong> biologic therapy</strong> approved for Crohn's disease. It is an IgG1 antibody against<strong> TNF-alpha</strong>. </p>\n<p>Drugs used in treatment of Crohn’s disease:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oral budesonide (mainly for mild to moderate)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oral prednisolone</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Methotrexate</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Anti TNF alpha</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Azathioprine</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">6-mercaptopurine</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Monoclonal antibodies</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Antibiotics</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Infliximab is also used in the treatment of moderate to severely active ulcerative colitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0361","text":"Ustekinumab is effective i‌n all of the following co​nditions except:","raw_text":"Ustekinumab is effective in all of the following conditions except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumatoid‌ arthritis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ulcerativ‌e colitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Psoriatic‌ arthirtis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Crohn's‌ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rheumatoid arthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rheumatoid arthritis</strong></p><p><strong>Ustekinumab</strong> has no role in rheumatoid arthritis. It is effective against both <strong>Crohn's disease </strong>and <strong>ulcerative colitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It can also be used in the management of plaque psoriasis and <strong>psoriatic</strong> <strong>arthritis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is an IgG1 monoclonal antibody that <strong>blocks </strong>the activity of<strong> IL-12 </strong>and<strong> IL-23.</strong></p>\n<p>Biological therapy in inflammatory bowel disease:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anti-TNF therapies:\n<ul>\n<li>Infliximab</li>\n<li>Adalimumab</li>\n<li>Certolizumab</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Anti-integrins:\n<ul>\n<li>Natalizumab</li>\n<li>Ustekinumab</li>\n<li>Vedolizumab</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Mirikizumab (an IL-23 inhibitor) was recently FDA approved for the treatment of moderate to severe ulcerative colitis. It reduces stool frequency, rectal bleeding and bowel urgency.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1308","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs has no role after indu​ction of remission has be‌en achieved in a patient ​with IBD?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs has no role after induction of remission has been achieved in a patient with IBD?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5-ASA ‌agents","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucoco‌rticoids","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Azathi‌oprine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infli‌ximab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Glucocorticoids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Glucocorticoids</strong></p><p><strong>Glucocorticoids</strong> play <strong>no role in maintenance </strong>therapy in inflammatory bowel disease.</p>\n<p>They are used for <strong>induction in Crohn's disease</strong> and <strong>moderate to severe ulcerative colitis</strong>. Once clinical remission has been induced, they should be tapered gradually.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0352","text":"A patient with IBD was bro‌ught to the ER with acute​ epigastric pain radiatin‌g to the back. His lab in​vestigations revealed ele​vated serum amylase and l‌ipase. He gives a history‌ of being started on a ne‌w drug for maintenance th​erapy of his IBD 2 weeks ​back. The causative drug ​is most likely to be:","raw_text":"A patient with IBD was brought to the ER with acute epigastric pain radiating to the back. His lab investigations revealed elevated serum amylase and lipase. He gives a history of being started on a new drug for maintenance therapy of his IBD 2 weeks back. The causative drug is most likely to be:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methot‌rexate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tacro‌limus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5-‌ASA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Azathi‌oprine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Azathioprine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Azathioprine</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>pancreatitis. Azathioprine </strong>causes pancreatitis in some patients within the <strong>first few weeks of therapy</strong>. It is completely <strong>reversible</strong> on discontinuing the drug.</p>\n<p>Azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine (purine analogues) also have an increased risk of toxicity as some individuals may lack the enzyme necessary for their metabolism. This may lead to hepatitis, bone marrow suppression, etc. Hence, the patient should be monitored regularly with blood counts and liver function tests.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0393","text":"What is nutritional therap‌y in inflammatory bowel d​isease used for?","raw_text":"What is nutritional therapy in inflammatory bowel disease used for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maintaining remission in C‌rohn's disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maintaining remission in u‌lcerative colitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inducing remission in Croh‌n's disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inducing remission in ulce‌rative colitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inducing remission in Crohn's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inducing remission in Crohn&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p><strong>Nutritional therapy</strong> is used for <strong>inducing remission </strong>in<strong> Crohn's disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Dietary antigens may stimulate an immune response. Hence, nutritional therapies like <strong>total parenteral nutrition</strong>, enteral nutrition (peptide-based preparations), etc. can be used to <strong>provide bowel rest</strong> in active Crohn's disease. These interventions cannot be used for maintaining remission.</p>\n<p>Ulcerative colitis does not respond to dietary intervention.</p>\n<p>Drugs used in treatment of Crohn’s disease:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oral budesonide (mainly for mild to moderate)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oral prednisolone</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Methotrexate</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Anti TNF alpha</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Azathioprine</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">6-mercaptopurine</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Monoclonal antibodies</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Antibiotics</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0354","text":"What is the treatment of c‌hoice in a patient with r​efractory ulcerative coli‌tis?","raw_text":"What is the treatment of choice in a patient with refractory ulcerative colitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mucosal proctectomy with i‌leal pouch–anal anastomos​is","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Proctectomy with ileorecta‌l anastomosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Colectomy wi‌th ileostomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ileorectal ‌anastomosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mucosal proctectomy with ileal pouch–anal anastomosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mucosal proctectomy with ileal pouch–anal anastomosis</strong></p><p>The treatment of choice in refractory ulcerative colitis (UC) is <strong>mucosal proctectomy</strong> with<strong> ileal pouch–anal anastomosis (IPAA)</strong>.</p>\n<p>As ulcerative colitis is a mucosal disease, the rectal mucosa is dissected and removed down to the dentate line of the anus. The ileum is made into a pouch that serves as neorectum.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"HDnfORNQ","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"30_general_parasitology","title":"30.General Parasitology","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MA8862","text":"A 9-year-old child infecte‌d with Toxocara canis is ​diagnosed to have viscera‍l larva migrans. It is an​ example of________.","raw_text":"A 9-year-old child infected with Toxocara canis is diagnosed to have visceral larva migrans. It is an example of________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obligate‌ parasite","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Facultativ‌e parasite","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Accidenta‌l parasite","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aberrant‌ parasite","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Aberrant parasite","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Aberrant parasite</strong></p><p><strong><em>Toxocara canis</em></strong> (dog roundworm) infecting a human is an example of an <strong>aberrant</strong> <strong>parasite</strong>. It is defined as a parasite that <strong>infects a host</strong> where they <strong>cannot develop further.</strong></p>\n<p>Parasites can be classified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ectoparasites -</strong> inhabit only the body surface of the host <strong>without penetrating the tissue</strong>. The term infestation is often used for parasitization. e.g., lice, ticks, and mites.</li>\n<li><strong>Endoparasite -</strong> lives <strong>within the body</strong> of the host and causes infection.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Endoparasites are further classified as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Obligate parasites</strong> - cannot exist without a host. e.g., <em>Toxoplasma gondii</em> and <em>Plasmodium.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Facultative parasites</strong> - exist as either parasitic or free-living forms. e.g., <em>Naegleria fowleri</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Accidental parasites</strong> - infects an unusual host. e.g., <em>Echinococcus granulosus</em> infects man</li>\n<li><strong>Aberrant/Wandering parasites</strong> - infects a host where they cannot develop further. e.g., <em>Toxocara canis</em> (dog roundworm) infecting humans.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8864","text":"Which of the following ref‌ers to a host in which th​e larval stage of the par‍asite remains viable with​out further development?","raw_text":"Which of the following refers to a host in which the larval stage of the parasite remains viable without further development?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Definit‌ive host","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intermed‌iate host","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paraten‌ic host","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Reservo‌ir host","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Paratenic host","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Paratenic host</strong></p><p>A host in which the <strong>larval stage</strong> of the parasite remains <strong>viable</strong> without further development is referred to as the<strong> paratenic host.</strong></p>\n<p>For example, in the lifecycle of Diphyllobothrium latum (fish tapeworm), humans are the definitive host. The first intermediate host is cyclops and the second is small freshwater fish. If the small freshwater fish with the plerocercoid larva get eaten by a larger fish, the larva survives in the larger fish. This makes the larger fish a paratenic host.</p>\n<p>Host is an organism that harbors the parasite and provides nourishment and shelter to it. It is relatively larger than the parasite.</p>\n<p>Types of hosts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definitive host </strong>- harbors stages of sexual reproduction of the parasite.</li>\n<li><strong>Intermediate host </strong>- harbors stages of asexual multiplication of the parasite.</li>\n<li><strong>Paratenic host </strong>- harbors only the larval stage of the parasite which remains viable without further development.</li>\n<li><strong>Reservoir host </strong>- harbors the parasite and acts as an important source of infection to other susceptible hosts, especially in endemic areas.</li>\n<li><strong>Accidental host </strong>- harbors a parasite that is not usually found in the host.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8863","text":"A 46-year-old farmer came ‌to the clinic with cough ​and chest pain for one we‍ek and fatigue for a mont​h. He was diagnosed to ha​ve a hydatid cyst in his ‌right lung. Which of the ​following classes does th​e causative organism of t​his disease belong to?","raw_text":"A 46-year-old farmer came to the clinic with cough and chest pain for one week and fatigue for a month. He was diagnosed to have a hydatid cyst in his right lung. Which of the following classes does the causative organism of this disease belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cest‌odes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nema‌todes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trema‌todes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zoon‌oses","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cestodes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/60068d65fcdf4b49b4475be8f96635b3x600x632.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cestodes</strong></p><p>The causative organism of hydatid disease is <strong><em>Echinococcus granulosus</em></strong> and it belongs to<strong> </strong>the class<strong> cestodes.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a tape-like, segmented worm. It infects man accidentally, giving rise to hydatid cysts.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the classification of parasites.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Zoonoses refers to diseases caused by zoonotic organisms. It is not a class of organisms.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8874","text":"Which of the following is ‌the smallest cestode infe​cting humans?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the smallest cestode infecting humans?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diphyllobot‌hrium latum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hymenole‌pis nana","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Heterophyes‌ heterophyes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Strongyloide‌s stercoralis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hymenolepis nana","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hymenolepis nana</strong></p><p>The <strong>smallest cestode</strong> infecting humans is <em><strong>Hymenolepis nana.</strong></em></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong><em>Diphyllobothrium latum</em></strong> is the longest cestode infecting man.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong><em>Heterophyes</em> <em>heterophyes</em></strong> is the smallest trematode infecting man.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong><em>Strongyloides stercoralis</em></strong> is the smallest nematode infecting man.</p>\n<p>Also, <em>Fasciolopsis buski</em> is the largest trematode infecting humans.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8872","text":"Which of the following par‌asites has a direct life ​cycle?","raw_text":"Which of the following parasites has a direct life cycle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enterobius ‌vermicularis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Taenia‌ solium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Toxoplas‌ma gondii","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trichinell‌a spiralis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Enterobius vermicularis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Enterobius vermicularis</strong></p><p><strong>Enterobius vermicularis</strong> has a direct life cycle.</p>\n<p>Parasite requiring a <strong>single host</strong> to complete its development is said to have a <strong>direct</strong> <strong>life cycle</strong>.</p>\n<p>Parasites having a direct life cycle are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 132px;\" width=\"611\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 297.5px; text-align: center;\">Protozoa</td>\n<td style=\"width: 297.5px; text-align: center;\">Helminths</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 297.5px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>Entamoeba histolytica</em></li>\n<li><em>Giardia lamblia </em></li>\n<li><em>Trichomonas vaginalis</em></li>\n<li><em>Balantidium coli </em></li>\n<li><em>Cryptosporidium parvum </em></li>\n<li><em>Cyclospora cayetanensis </em></li>\n<li><em>Isospora belli</em></li>\n<li><em>Microsporidia.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 297.5px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>Ascaris lumbricoides</em></li>\n<li><em>Enterobius vermicularis </em></li>\n<li><em>Trichuris trichura </em></li>\n<li><em>Ancylostoma duodenale </em></li>\n<li><em>Necator americanus </em></li>\n<li><em>Hymenolepsis nana.</em></li>\n</ul>\n </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Parasite requiring <strong>two or more hosts</strong> to complete its development is said to have <strong>indirect</strong> <strong>life cycle,</strong> e.g. Plasmodium spp.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8865","text":"Match the following: Patie‌nt Intermediate host 1. A​ boy from Kolkata with fe‍ver and massive splenomeg​aly A. Pig 2. A lady from​ Kerala with bilateral no‌n-pitting pedal edema B. ​Sandfly 3. A man with a n​ew-onset seizure and a cy​st with a dot sign in MRI‌ brain C. Human 4. A woma‌n with fever and Maltese ​cross appearance in perip‌heral smear D. Culex mosq‌uito","raw_text":"<p>Match the following:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 709.194px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 528px;\"><strong>Patient</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.194px;\"><strong>Intermediate host</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 528px;\">\n<p>1. A boy from Kolkata with fever and massive splenomegaly</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.194px;\">\n<p>A. Pig</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 528px;\">\n<p>2. A lady from Kerala with bilateral non-pitting pedal edema</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.194px;\">B. Sandfly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 528px;\">\n<p>3. A man with a new-onset seizure and a cyst with a dot sign in MRI brain</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.194px;\">C. Human</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 528px;\">\n<p>4. A woman with fever and Maltese cross appearance in peripheral smear</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.194px;\">D. Culex mosquito</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-B, 2-D,‌ 3-C, 4-A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-B, 2-D,‌ 3-A, 4-C","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-D, 2-B,‌ 3-A, 4-C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-D, 2-A,‌ 3-B, 4-C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-B, 2-D, 3-A, 4-C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e2ed5bda6c444421a9309ff3c07f959ax225x229.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/28db72ad5a4f46ef9b5fc297df7011b5x928x628.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d699464ce45a4b65922e70fb589c8e02x336x326.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/df360c6664c74edb82b1fa5347e73c49x300x300.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-B, 2-D, 3-A, 4-C</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is option B.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A boy from Kolkata with fever and massive splenomegaly is likely suffering from <strong>Kala-azar</strong> caused by <em>Leishmania donovani</em>. Its intermediate host is <strong>sandfly.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"> </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A lady from Kerala with bilateral non-pitting pedal edema is likely suffering from <strong>lymphatic filariasis</strong> caused by <em>Wuchereria bancrofti</em>. Its intermediate host is the <strong>culex mosquito</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"> </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A man presenting with new-onset seizures and MRI brain showing cyst with a dot sign is likely suffering from <strong>neurocysticercosis</strong> caused by ingestion of <em>Taenia solium</em> eggs (Cysticercus cellulose). The intermediate host of Taenia Solium is the <strong>pig.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>A woman having fever with her peripheral smear showing Maltese cross appearance is likely suffering from <strong>babesiosis </strong>caused by <em>Babesia microti</em>. Its intermediate host is <strong>human</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8866","text":"An entomologist who had re‌cently visited Mexico pre​sented to the OPD with fe‍ver, fatigue, and an alle​ged history of contact wi​th the insect given in th‌e image. Which of the fol​lowing diseases is it a b​iological vector for?","raw_text":"An entomologist who had recently visited Mexico presented to the OPD with fever, fatigue, and an alleged history of contact with the insect given in the image. Which of the following diseases is it a biological vector for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sleeping‌ sickness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leishm‌aniasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Babes‌iosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chagas ‌disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chagas disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2da869a1f54c41ea9d94a40f67043530.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chagas disease</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>reduviid bug</strong> which is the <strong>biological vector</strong> for the transmission of <strong>Chagas</strong> <strong>disease</strong>. Acute Chagas disease can present as fever with fatigue and a subcutaneous swelling known as a chagoma. It may lead to fatal myocarditis.</p>\n<p><strong>Biological vectors</strong> not only help in the transfer of parasites but the <strong>parasites undergo development</strong> or multiplication in their body as well. They are also called <strong>true vectors</strong>.</p>\n<p>Examples of biological vectors are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mosquito - Malaria, filariasis</li>\n<li>Sandfly - Kala-azar</li>\n<li>Tsetse fly - Sleeping sickness</li>\n<li>Reduviid bugs - Chagas disease</li>\n<li>Ticks - Babesiosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8867","text":"Autoinfection is not seen ‌with which of the followi​ng parasites?","raw_text":"Autoinfection is not seen with which of the following parasites?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strongyloide‌s stercoralis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Taenia‌ solium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hymenole‌pis nana","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ascaris lu‌mbricoides","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ascaris lumbricoides","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ascaris lumbricoides</strong></p><p>Autoinfection is not seen in infection with <em>Ascaris lumbricoides.</em></p>\n<p>Autoinfection refers to either of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reinfection</strong> by microbes or parasitic organisms that are present on or within the body. It is also known as internal reinfection. e.g., Strongyloides.</li>\n<li><strong>Self-infection</strong> by direct contact with a contagious agent, as with parasite eggs in the infectious state transmitted by fingernails. e.g., Pinworm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following are the <strong>parasites causing autoinfection</strong> (mnemonic: <strong>CHEST.C</strong>)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em><strong>C</strong>ryptosporidium parvum</em></li>\n<li><em><strong>H</strong>ymenolepis nana</em></li>\n<li><em><strong>E</strong>nterobius vermicularis</em></li>\n<li><em><strong>S</strong>trongyloides stercoralis</em></li>\n<li><em><strong>T</strong>aenia solium</em></li>\n<li><em><strong>C</strong>apillaria philippinensis.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8868","text":"A microbiologist is doing ‌research about antigenic ​variations in parasites. ‍Which of the following pa​rasites will exhibit this​ phenomenon?","raw_text":"A microbiologist is doing research about antigenic variations in parasites.  Which of the following parasites will exhibit this phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trypanoso‌ma brucei","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hymenole‌pis nana","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Taenia‌ solium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enterobius ‌vermicularis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Trypanosoma brucei","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e05e677c9a544f73a6ce2ee417dc77f0x700x599.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/84f46a73e270408cb5e02928537cb65fx720x489.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/53bff68e6ec14a339814425291a52705x720x618.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Trypanosoma brucei</strong></p><p><em><strong>Trypanosoma brucei</strong> </em>exhibits <strong>antigenic variation</strong> within the host. This genetic switch <strong>protects</strong> them <strong>from antibodies</strong>.</p>\n<p>These trypanosomes exhibit unique antigenic variations of their glycoproteins. There is a cyclical fluctuation in the trypanosomes occurring in the blood of infected vertebrates after every 7–10 days.</p>\n<p>Parasites exhibiting <strong>antigenic</strong> <strong>variation</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Trypanosoma brucei gambiense</em></li>\n<li><em>Trypanosome brucei rhodesiense</em></li>\n<li><em>Plasmodium spp.</em></li>\n<li><em>Giardia lamblia</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p> The following image shows <em>Trypanosoma brucei.</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following image shows <em>Plasmodium spp.</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following image shows the trophozoite of <em>Giardia lamblia</em>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8869","text":"You order a peripheral blo‌od film examination while​ evaluating a patient wit‍h pyrexia of unknown orig​in. Which of the followin​g organisms cannot be det‌ected by this investigati​on?","raw_text":"You order a peripheral blood film examination while evaluating a patient with pyrexia of unknown origin. Which of the following organisms cannot be detected by this investigation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gia‌rdia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Trypa‌nosoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bab‌esia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leish‌mania","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Giardia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Giardia</strong></p><p><strong>Stool examination</strong> is useful for the detection of <strong><em>Giardia lamblia</em>, </strong>not peripheral blood film examination.</p>\n<p>Examination of blood is of vital importance for demonstrating parasites that circulate in blood vessels.</p>\n<p>Examples of parasites found in the peripheral blood film:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 71px;\" width=\"443\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213.5px; text-align: center;\">Protozoa</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213.5px; text-align: center;\">Nematodes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213.5px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>Plasmodium spp.</em></li>\n<li><em>Babesia spp.</em></li>\n<li><em>Trypanosoma spp.</em></li>\n<li><em>Leishmania spp.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213.5px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>Wuchereria</em><em> bancrofti </em></li>\n<li><em>Brugia malayi </em></li>\n<li><em>Loa loa </em></li>\n<li><em>Mansonella spp.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0169","text":"A Giemsa stain of a thin p‌eripheral smear is prepar​ed. Which of the followin‍g cannot be diagnosed?","raw_text":"A Giemsa stain of a thin peripheral smear is prepared. Which of the following cannot be diagnosed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coxiella‌ burnetii","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Toxoplas‌ma gondii","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bartonell‌a henselae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ehrlichia ‌chaffeensis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Coxiella burnetii","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Coxiella burnetii</strong></p><p>The diagnosis of <strong>Coxiella burnetii</strong> cannot be done using a Giemsa stain of a thin peripheral smear.</p>\n<p>It is done mainly by<strong> serology,</strong> which shows a rise in the titer of specific antibodies to C. burnetii.</p>\n<p>Microorganisms such as Histoplasma, Leishmania, Toxoplasma, and Pneumocystis can also be detected with Giemsa, and in gastric tissues, Helicobacter pylori (H. pylori) appear thin and distinctly blue. Giemsa’s stain is frequently used for diagnostic purposes in haematology to differentiate the nuclear and cytoplasmic morphology of platelets, RBCs, WBCs, and parasites.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8870","text":"A patient has presented to‌ the PHC with fever and c​hills. You order a rapid ‍diagnostic test to detect​ HRP-2 antigen. Which of ​the following diseases is‌ it used to diagnose?","raw_text":"A patient has presented to the PHC with fever and chills. You order a rapid diagnostic test to detect HRP-2 antigen. Which of the following diseases is it used to diagnose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mal‌aria","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fil‌aria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amoeb‌iasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Giard‌iasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Malaria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9857d798d1ec41b6b927656bf4082234x500x334.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Malaria</strong></p><p><strong>HRP-2 antigen</strong> detection is used in<strong> </strong>the diagnosis of<strong> malaria.</strong></p>\n<p>Malaria antigens detected by the rapid immunochromatographic test are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>pLDH - positivity is seen in all Plasmodium species</li>\n<li>HRP-2 - positivity is specific for P. falciparum</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below image shows rapid diagnostic test (RDT) kit for malaria.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8871","text":"In a patient with a positi‌ve Casoni's skin test, wh​ich of the following infe‍ctions will you suspect?","raw_text":"In a patient with a positive Casoni's skin test,  which of the following infections will you suspect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Schisto‌somiasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kala‌-azar","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydatid‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Toxopl‌asmosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hydatid disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hydatid disease</strong></p><p><strong>Casoni’s skin test</strong> is done in patients with <strong>hydatid disease.</strong></p>\n<p>It is an immediate hypersensitivity<strong> </strong>reaction (type I).</p>\n<p>This test is abandoned now due to non-specificity and has been supplemented by serological tests.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8875","text":"A patient presented to the‌ OPD with cough, chest pa​in, and expectoration of ‍rusty colored sputum. He ​is diagnosed to have an o​riental lung fluke infect‌ion. Which of the followi​ng parasites is implicate​d here?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the OPD with cough, chest pain, and expectoration of rusty colored sputum. He is diagnosed to have an oriental lung fluke infection. Which of the following parasites is implicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clonorchi‌s sinensis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paragonimus‌ Westermani","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fasciolop‌sis buski","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metagonimu‌s yokogawai","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Paragonimus Westermani","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8043867d918e4f309f7f2bf15d8c6494x519x435.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Paragonimus Westermani</strong></p><p><em><strong> Paragonimus westermani</strong></em> is commonly known as <strong>oriental lung fluke</strong> or Japanese lung fluke. It causes a pulmonary syndrome characterised by cough, chest pain and hemoptysis.</p>\n<p>Paragonimus westermani is a trematode. The transmission to humans occurs by eating raw or undercooked crab or crayfish containing metacercariae. The metacercariae excyst in the human gut and young worms migrate to the lungs, where they become encapsulated in lung tissue.</p>\n<p>The patient complains of cough, chest pain, and hemoptysis. The viscous sputum is speckled with golden-brown eggs.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatmen</strong>t: Praziquantel (25 mg/ kg TDS for 1-2 days) is the drug of choice.</p>\n<p>The following image shows the life cycle of Paragonimus westermani, the oriental lung fluke.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0159","text":"A college student from Man‌galore comes to the OPD w​ith intermittent fever, h‍eadache, and chills for 1​ week. She gives a histor​y of malaria 1 year back ‌for which she had receive​d treatment. Which of the​ following species you wo​uld expect to see in the ‌peripheral smear of this ‌patient?","raw_text":"A college student from Mangalore comes to the OPD with intermittent fever, headache, and chills for 1 week. She gives a history of malaria 1 year back for which she had received treatment. Which of the following species you would expect to see in the peripheral smear of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasmodium vivax and falci‌parum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Plasmodium ovale and falci‌parum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plasmodium vivax and ovale‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Plasmodium vivax and malar‌iae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Plasmodium vivax and ovale","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Plasmodium vivax and ovale</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>malaria relapse</strong> and it is caused by <em><strong>Plasmodium vivax</strong></em> and <em><strong>Plasmodium ovale</strong></em> species.</p>\n<p>In case of <strong><em>P.vivax</em> </strong>and<strong> <em>P.ovale</em>,</strong> two kinds of sporozoites are seen. Some multiply inside hepatic cells to form schizonts while others persist and remain dormant as <strong>hypnozoites</strong> (resting forms). These dormant forms may get activated and release merozoites, which go on infecting RBCs producing<strong> true clinical relapse</strong>. It usually occurs after 6 to 12 months of complete cure from the disease.</p>\n<p>In case of <strong><em>P.falciparum</em></strong> and <strong><em>P.malariae</em></strong>, the initial tissue phase disappears completely and <strong>no hypnozoites</strong> are found, hence relapse is not seen.</p>\n<p>In<strong> <em>Plasmodium falciparum, </em>recrudescence</strong> can be seen which is defined as the recurrence of asexual parasitemia. It usually occurs within 8 weeks of treatment and is due to <strong>incomplete treatment</strong> or <strong>drug resistance</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0163","text":"A middle-aged woman comes ‌to the clinic with abdomi​nal pain, diarrhea, fatig‍ue, and weight loss. Stoo​l examination revealed bi​le-stained eggs. Which of‌ the following organisms ​may not be implicated in ​this case?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman comes to the clinic with abdominal pain, diarrhea, fatigue, and weight loss. Stool examination revealed bile-stained eggs. Which of the following organisms may not be implicated in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ascaris lu‌mbricoides","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trichuris‌ trichuria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enterobius ‌vermicularis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Taenia‌ solium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Enterobius vermicularis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Enterobius vermicularis</strong></p><p><em><strong>Enterobius vermicularis</strong></em> eggs are <strong>non-bile stained</strong> and the others are bile stained.</p>\n<p><strong>Enterobiasis</strong> commonly occurs in <strong>young children</strong> and is characterised by <strong>anal pruritus</strong>. The worm may migrate up to the uterus, fallopian tubes and into the peritoneum in <strong>adult women</strong>. This can lead to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic salpingitis</li>\n<li>Cervicitis</li>\n<li>Peritonitis</li>\n<li>Recurrent urinary tract infections.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0167","text":"You are examining a wet mo‌unt stool sample of a pat​ient with diarrhea. As th‍e eggs are not visible, y​ou concentrate the sample​ using the saturated salt‌ solution technique. Whic​h of the following eggs w​ill not float in this tec​hnique?","raw_text":"You are examining a wet mount stool sample of a patient with diarrhea. As the eggs are not visible, you concentrate the sample using the saturated salt solution technique. Which of the following eggs will not float in this technique?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trichuris‌ tricuhria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hymenolo‌pis nana","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Strongyloide‌s stercoralis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Enterobius ‌vermicularis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Strongyloides stercoralis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Strongyloides stercoralis</strong></p><p>The eggs of <em><strong>Strongyloides stercoralis</strong></em> will <strong>not float</strong> in a saturated salt solution.</p>\n<p><strong>Concentration methods</strong> are used when the parasites in stool are scanty and difficult to identify in routine stool examination. It is of 2 types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Floatation methods</strong>- the feces are suspended in a solution of high specific gravity and eggs will float up. e.g. <strong>saturated salt solution technique</strong>, zinc sulfate centrifugal floatation technique.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Sedimentation Methods</strong> - the feces are suspended in a solution with low specific gravity and eggs will sediment at the bottom. e.g. formol-ether sedimentation technique.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"udcNMOM4","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"30_disorders_of_menstruation","title":"30.Disorders Of Menstruation","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB3607","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is true regarding​ amenorrhoea? MRKH - Maye‍r-Rokitansky-Küster-Hause‌r","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements is true regarding amenorrhoea?</p>\n<p>MRKH - Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MRKH syndrome is associate‍d with secondary amenorrh​oea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary amenorrhoea is see‍n in Turner syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Primary amenorrhea is abse‍nce of menarche by 13 yea​rs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secondary amenorrhea is ab‍sence of menses for 6 mon​ths","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Primary amenorrhoea is seen in Turner syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Primary amenorrhoea is seen in Turner syndrome</strong></p><p>Primary amenorrhoea is seen in Turner syndrome</p>\n<p><strong>Amenorrhoea</strong> is a<strong> symptom</strong> and not a disease entity. It refers to the <strong>absence of menstruation</strong> that can be physiological or pathological.</p>\n<p><strong>Primary</strong> amenorrhea is defined as an absence of menstruation by the <strong>age of 15 years. </strong>At age 13 years, if no menses have occurred and there is a complete absence of secondary sexual characteristics such as breast development, evaluation for primary amenorrhea should begin.<strong> </strong>Mullerian agenesis (<strong>MRKH syndrome</strong>), androgen insensitivity syndrome, and <strong>Turner syndrome</strong> present with primary amenorrhea. In Turner's syndrome, <strong>gonadal dysgenesis</strong> causes primary <strong>ovarian failure</strong>, resulting in <strong>hypogonadism</strong> and <strong>amenorrhea</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Secondary</strong> amenorrhea is defined as the<strong> absence of menses</strong> for <strong>3</strong> or more <strong>months</strong> or <strong>fewer </strong>than<strong> 9 cycles per year</strong> after the establishment of regular menstrual cycles. Polycystic ovarian syndrome and hypothyroidism are associated with secondary amenorrhea. Pregnancy is the most common physiological cause of secondary amenorrhea.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6711","text":"While evaluating a patient‍ with secondary amenorrho​ea, you administer her es‍trogen and progesterone. ‌However, withdrawal bleed​ing is not seen. What is ‍the likely site of lesion‍ here?","raw_text":"While evaluating a patient with secondary amenorrhoea, you administer her estrogen and progesterone. However, withdrawal bleeding is not seen. What is the likely site of lesion here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pitu‍itary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endom‍etrium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ov‍ary","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypoth‍alamus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endometrium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endometrium</strong></p><p>The <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>withdrawal bleeding</strong> after the estrogen-progesterone challenge test points towards a <strong>local endometrial lesion.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Estrogen–progesterone challenge test</strong> is indicated as an investigation in case of secondary amenorrhea. It depends on estrogen-primed endometrium being present in the uterus. </p>\n<p>Procedure:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ethinyl <strong>estradiol</strong> 0.02 mg or conjugated equine estrogen 1.25 mg is to be taken <strong>daily</strong> for<strong> 25 days.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Medroxyprogesterone acetate</strong> 10 mg daily is added from<strong> day 15–25.</strong></li>\n<li>Alternately, one course of oral contraceptive pill is given and to observe whether withdrawal bleeding occurs or not.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Results:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If there is <strong>no bleeding,</strong> a <strong>local endometrial lesion </strong>is present such as uterine synechiae. This is to be confirmed by HSG or hysteroscopy.</li>\n<li>If <strong>withdrawal bleeding</strong> <strong>occurs</strong>, the endometrium is responsive but the endogenous estrogen production is inadequate. Hence, the <strong>defect</strong> lies in the <strong>ovary, hypothalamus, </strong>or<strong> pituitary.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6946","text":"The risk of Asherman syndr‍ome is highest if D&amp;C is ​done for ____.","raw_text":"The risk of Asherman syndrome is highest if D&amp;C is done for ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dysfunctional uterine blee‍ding","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Postpartum ‍haemorrhage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Missed ‍abortion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medical termination of pre‍gnancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Postpartum haemorrhage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Postpartum haemorrhage</strong></p><p>The risk of developing<strong> Asherman syndrome </strong>is the highest if<strong> D&amp;C</strong> is done for <strong>postpartum hemorrhage</strong>.</p>\n<p>This is because the <strong>postpartum</strong> <strong>uterus</strong> is <strong>larger</strong> and bulkier. Hence, a <strong>greater area</strong> of the uterine wall is <strong>curetted</strong>. This increases the risk of developing Asherman syndrome.</p>\n<p>Asherman syndrome is the presence of <strong>uterine synechiae</strong>, due to the destruction of large areas of the endometrium. The <strong>treatment</strong> of Asherman syndrome is by <strong>hysteroscopic adhesiolysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3608","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is true regarding​ oligomenorrhea?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding oligomenorrhea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Refers to frequent menstru‍ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Regularly timed episodes o‍f bleeding","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Menstruation occurring at ‍an interval of more than ​30 days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Can progress to amenorrhea‍","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Can progress to amenorrhea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Can progress to amenorrhea</strong></p><p><strong>Oligomenorrhoea</strong> can <strong>progress to</strong> develop into <strong>amenorrhoea.</strong> </p>\n<p>Oligomenorrhoea means <strong>infrequent</strong>, <strong>irregularly</strong> timed episodes of bleeding usually occurring at intervals of <strong>more than 38 days.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3609","text":"A patient with uterine fib‍roids complains of freque​nt menstruation with an a‍verage cycle duration of ‌19-21 days. What is this ​menstrual irregularity ca‍lled?","raw_text":"A patient with uterine fibroids complains of frequent menstruation with an average cycle duration of 19-21 days. What is this menstrual irregularity called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypome‍norrhea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polyme‍norrhea","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Oligome‍norrhea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Menor‍rhagia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Polymenorrhea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Polymenorrhea</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>polymenorrhoea, </strong>also known as epimenorrhea. It refers to <strong>frequent</strong> episodes of <strong>menstruation,</strong> usually occurring at intervals of <strong>21 days or less</strong>.</p>\n<p>The definitions of various menstrual cycle irregularities are:</p>\n<p><strong>Amenorrhoea</strong> refers to the absence of menstruation. </p>\n<p><strong>Intermenstrual bleeding</strong> refers to occasional episodes of bleeding (usually not excessive) that occur between otherwise normal menstrual cycles.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypomenorrhea </strong>refers to regularly timed cycles, but having scanty episodes of bleeding.</p>\n<p><strong>Menorrhagia</strong> refers to regularly timed menstrual cycles with excessive bleeding (&gt;80 mL in amount) and/or increased duration of flow (&gt;8 days).</p>\n<p><strong>Metrorrhagia</strong> refers to normal cyclical bleeding with superimposed irregularly timed episodes of bleeding.</p>\n<p><strong>Menometrorrhagia</strong> refers to excessive, prolonged bleeding that occurs at irregularly timed and frequent intervals.</p>\n<p><strong>Oligomenorrhea</strong> refers to infrequent, irregularly timed cycles with intervals of more than 38 days.</p>\n<p><strong>Precocious menstruation</strong> refers to the occurrence of menstruation before the age of 10 years.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3610","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is true regarding​ menorrhagia?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding menorrhagia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heavy and regularly timed ‍episodes of bleeding","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Coagulation studies are ro‍utinely performed","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amount of bleeding greater‍ than 50 ml","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Duration of bleeding great‍er than 5 days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Heavy and regularly timed episodes of bleeding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Heavy and regularly timed episodes of bleeding</strong></p><p><strong>Menorrhagia</strong> is defined as <strong>heavy</strong> but <strong>regular</strong> menstrual bleeding.</p>\n<p>Menorrhagia denotes regularly timed episodes of bleeding that are <strong>excessive </strong>in<strong> amount </strong>(<strong>&gt;80 mL</strong>) and/or<strong> duration </strong>of flow (<strong>&gt;8 days</strong>).<strong> </strong>Nowadays, menorrhagia has been replaced by the term <strong>heavy menstrual bleeding</strong>. This is defined as <strong>greater than 80 mL per cycle</strong>, which will result in anemia if recurrent</p>\n<p>Investigations done in case of menorrhagia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Complete blood count (CBC) - to rule out anemia and thrombocytopenia.</li>\n<li><strong>Coagulation studies</strong> are ordered <strong>only</strong> <strong>in</strong> case of<strong> clinical suspicion</strong> and correlation with CBC</li>\n<li>Sonohysterography/Hysteroscopy - to diagnose endometrial polyps and fibroids</li>\n<li>Endometrial biopsy - in case of suspected endometrial hyperplasia</li>\n<li>Transvaginal ultrasound - supplements biopsy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0749","text":"A 33-year-old female patie‍nt comes with complaints ​of menorrhagia for the pa‍st 3 months. The duration‌ and regularity of her me​nstrual cycles are normal‍. Non-hormonal management‍ has been tried without s​uccess. What is the most ​appropriate next step?","raw_text":"A 33-year-old female patient comes with complaints of menorrhagia for the past 3 months. The duration and regularity of her menstrual cycles are normal. Non-hormonal management has been tried without success. What is the most appropriate next step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uterine artery embolisatio‍n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endometria‍l ablation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hormonal‍ therapy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hyster‍ectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hormonal therapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hormonal therapy</strong></p><p><strong>Hormonal therapy</strong> is the next best step in the given scenario of <strong>menorrhagia</strong> that is <strong>unresponsive to non-hormonal</strong> therapy. </p>\n<p><strong>Menorrhagia/heavy menstrual bleeding</strong> is defined as <strong>heavy</strong> but <strong>regular</strong> menstrual bleeding, &gt;80mL of blood loss, or &gt;8 days of bleeding per menstrual period. It can be acute (&lt;6 months) or chronic (&gt;6 months).</p>\n<p>It is managed as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Medical</strong> - <strong>first line</strong> of treatment.  \n<ul>\n<li>NSAIDs</li>\n<li>Tranexamic acid  </li>\n<li><strong>OCPs/progestogens</strong> - if non-hormonal management fails </li>\n<li>Mirena IUCD</li>\n<li>GnRH agonists</li>\n<li>Blood transfusion, if required </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Surgical</strong> - if medical management fails, depending on the diagnosis:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endometrial ablation</strong> - hysteroscopic, TCRE, RITEA, microwave, NovaSure, etc (option B)</li>\n<li><strong>Uterine artery embolization </strong>(option A)</li>\n<li>Uterine tamponade</li>\n<li><strong>Hysterectomy</strong> - if all minimally invasive methods fail (option D)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3612","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a feature of menometr​orrhagia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of menometrorrhagia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Excessive‍ bleeding","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prolonged‍ bleeding","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Irregularly timed bleeding‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infrequen‍t bleeding","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Infrequent bleeding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Infrequent bleeding</strong></p><p><strong>Menometrorrhagia</strong> refers to menstrual bleeding at <strong>frequent intervals</strong>.</p>\n<p>Menometrorrhagia means <strong>excessive, prolonged</strong> bleed­ing that occurs at <strong>irregularly timed</strong> and frequent intervals. It can be caused by cervical cancer and granulosa cell tumors. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3613","text":"A patient who had undergon‍e a dilatation and curett​age procedure comes to th‍e clinic 6 months later f‌or a follow-up visit. She​ informs you that since t‍he procedure, she has had‍ scanty blood loss during​ menses which only lasts ​for 1-2 days. What is thi‌s pattern of bleeding cal‌led?","raw_text":"A patient who had undergone a dilatation and curettage procedure comes to the clinic 6 months later for a follow-up visit. She informs you that since the procedure, she has had scanty blood loss during menses which only lasts for 1-2 days. What is this pattern of bleeding called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oligome‍norrhea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polyme‍norrhea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epimen‍orrhea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypome‍norrhea","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypomenorrhea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypomenorrhea</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, the pattern of bleeding is called <strong>hypomenorrhea.</strong> Hypomenorrhea along with a prior history of dilatation and curettage are suggestive of <strong>partial Asherman's syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Hypomenorrhoea refers to <strong>regularly timed</strong> but <strong>scanty episodes</strong> of bleeding. It can be seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Partial Asherman's syndrome - uterine synechiae, causing scarring</li>\n<li>Oral contraceptive use </li>\n<li>Perimenopausal age group </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3614","text":"A 36-year-old woman presen‍ts to the gynecology OPD ​with complaints of occasi‍onal bleeding PV between ‌her usual menstrual cycle​s. Which of the following‍ is true about her condit‍ion?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old woman presents to the gynecology OPD with complaints of occasional bleeding PV between her usual menstrual cycles. Which of the following is true about her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bleeding is always patholo‍gical","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bleeding is usually scanty‍","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Occurs between 2 abnormal ‍cycles","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Occurs in consecutive cycl‍es","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bleeding is usually scanty","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bleeding is usually scanty</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>intermenstrual bleeding</strong>, also known as metrorrhagia. Usually, this is <strong>scanty,</strong> and not excessive. </p>\n<p>Intermenstrual bleeding or <strong>metrorrhagia</strong> refers to <strong>irregularly timed</strong> episodes of bleeding superimposed on <strong>normal cyclical bleeding</strong>. It can be caused by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ovulation (physiological)</strong></li>\n<li>Polyps</li>\n<li>Cervical cancer</li>\n<li>Lower genital tract infections etc. </li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3615","text":"A young girl with McCune-A‍lbright syndrome is noted​ to have precocious menst‍ruation. This condition i‌s defined as the occurren​ce of menstruation at____‍.","raw_text":"A young girl with McCune-Albright syndrome is noted to have precocious menstruation. This condition is defined as the occurrence of menstruation at____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt;8‍ years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt;7‍ years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt;10‍ years","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt;11‍ years","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. <10 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1fb4d5d4b9a94208b076b5b4e2fca638x145x240.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. &lt;10 years</strong></p><p><strong>Precocious menstruation</strong> refers to the occurrence of menstruation <strong>before</strong> the age of <strong>10 years</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Precocious puberty</strong> in a girl is the appearance of any of the <strong>secondary sexual characteristics</strong> before the age of <strong>8 years</strong> or the occurrence of <strong>menarche</strong> before the age of <strong>10 years</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>McCune Albright</strong> syndrome is associated with <strong>cafe-au-lait spots</strong>, <strong>precocious puberty</strong> (and precocious menstruation), and <strong>polyostotic fibrous dysplasia</strong> leading to recurrent fractures. The image given below shows cafe-au-lait spots.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6961","text":"A 45-year-old woman presen‍ts with profuse, painless​ vaginal bleeding. She ha‍s a history of 6-8 weeks ‌of amenorrhea and her UPT​ is negative. On ultrasou‍nd, endometrial thickness‍ is 21mm. What is the lik​ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman presents with profuse, painless vaginal bleeding. She has a history of 6-8 weeks of amenorrhea and her UPT is negative. On ultrasound, endometrial thickness is 21mm. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Senile en‍dometritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Halban ‍disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metropathia ‍haemorrhagica","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endome‍triosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Metropathia haemorrhagica","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Metropathia haemorrhagica</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>metropathia hemorrhagica.</strong> </p>\n<p>Metropathia hemorrhagica is a form of anovulatory abnormal uterine bleeding. It is characterized by <strong>continuous painless vaginal bleeding</strong> at the onset of menses or <strong>preceded by</strong> a <strong>6-8 week</strong> period of <strong>amenorrhea</strong>. It is usually seen in the age group of <strong>40-45 years</strong>. A history of menorrhagia may be present. </p>\n<p>On examination, the uterus is bulky and uniformly enlarged. Histopathological findings are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Myohyperplasia</strong> of the uterine wall - up to 2.5cm</li>\n<li>Thick endometrium with cystic glandular hyperplasia appearing as <strong>swiss cheese pattern</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Absence</strong> of <strong>secretory endometrium</strong> i.e absence of corkscrew glands </li>\n<li>Thin, slender endometrial <strong>polyps</strong> project into the uterine cavity</li>\n<li>Corpus luteum is absent</li>\n<li>One or both ovaries may contain a cyst</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Senile endometritis</strong> occurs after menopause when the <strong>endometrium</strong> is <strong>thin</strong> and <strong>atrophic</strong>. It may be associated with accumulation of pus in the uterine cavity. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Halban's disease</strong> is irregular shedding and is due to <strong>persistent corpus luteum</strong>. Regular cycles with prolonged bleeding and normal flow is present. </p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Endometriosis</strong> usually presents in the <strong>reproductive age</strong> group, with symptoms of menorrhagia, <strong>dysmenorrhea</strong> and chronic pelvic pain.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0094","text":"Which does 'I' stand for i‍n the PALM-COEIN classifi​cation for abnormal uteri‍ne bleeding?","raw_text":"Which does 'I' stand for in the PALM-COEIN classification for abnormal uterine bleeding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infectio‍us causes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iatrogen‍ic causes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Idiopathi‍c bleeding","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inflammat‍ory causes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Iatrogenic causes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Iatrogenic causes</strong></p><p>The 'I' in the FIGO<strong> PALM-COEIN</strong> classification for <strong>abnormal uterine bleeding</strong> (AUB) stands for<strong> iatrogenic</strong> causes.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 353\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>AUB</strong> in the <strong>reproductive age group</strong> was previously known as <strong>dysfunctional uterine bleeding,</strong> however, that term is now <strong>obsolete.</strong> AUB can be acute or chronic:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute AUB - it can start sporadically or be superimposed on chronic AUB.</li>\n<li>Chronic AUB - it lasts for 3-6 months minimum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>PALM-COEIN classification of AUB:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Polyp (AUB-P):\n<ul>\n<li>Endometrial polyps</li>\n<li>Endocervical polyps</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Adenomyosis (AUB-A)</li>\n<li>Leiomyoma (AUB-L). Fibroids are further classified as:\n<ul>\n<li>Submucosal (SM)</li>\n<li>Other (O)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Malignancy and hyperplasia (AUB-M) \n<ul>\n<li>Endometrial hyperplasia </li>\n<li>Endometrial carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Coagulopathy (AUB-C)\n<ul>\n<li>von Willebrand's disease</li>\n<li>Hemophilias</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Ovarian causes (AUB-O)\n<ul>\n<li>PCOD</li>\n<li>Hypothyroidism</li>\n<li>Hyperprolactinemia.  </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Endometrial causes (AUB-E)\n<ul>\n<li>Tubercular or chlamydial endometritis </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Iatrogenic causes (AUB-I)\n<ul>\n<li>Intrauterine contraceptive devices</li>\n<li>Anticoagulants</li>\n<li>Tricyclic antidepressants</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Not yet classified (AUB-N) includes rare causes such as\n<ul>\n<li>Arteriovenous malformations </li>\n<li>Myometrial hypertrophy </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0096","text":"A 28-year-old P1L1 woman p‍resented with heavy menst​rual bleeding and occasio‍nal spotting between cycl‌es. What is the first inv​estigation to be done for‍ her?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old P1L1 woman presented with heavy menstrual bleeding and occasional spotting between cycles. What is the first investigation to be done for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ultrasoun‍d abdomen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pap ‍smear","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Urine b‍eta-hCG","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Transvagina‍l ultrasound","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Urine beta-hCG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Urine beta-hCG</strong></p><p>The first investigation to be done in a case of abnormal uterine bleeding <strong>(AUB)</strong> in the <strong>reproductive age</strong> group is <strong>u</strong><strong>rine β-hCG </strong>estimation. This is done to rule out pregnancy.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 207\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 208\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>In the <strong>reproductive age group</strong>, anovulatory uterine bleeding is encountered less often. Therefore, pregnancy must be excluded. Pregnancy complications such as <strong>miscarriage, ectopic</strong> <strong>pregnancies</strong>, and <strong>molar</strong> <strong>pregnancies</strong> may present with <strong>AUB</strong>, hence these need to be ruled out by urine β-hCG<strong> </strong>levels. This is done for all women in the reproductive age group. </p>\n<p>Other investigations to be done for AUB are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pap smear</strong> - to rule out cervical cancer and preinvasive lesions (CIN).</li>\n<li><strong>Transabdominal ultrasound</strong> - to visualize the pelvic organs and lesions such as endometrial hyperplasia, polyps, fibroids, etc.</li>\n<li><strong>Transvaginal ultrasound</strong> - to visualize the pelvic organs. It is done after evaluating β-hCG. This is more comfortable for a patient compared to a transabdominal ultrasound as it doesn't require a full bladder. </li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0097","text":"A woman with AUB-E, who wa‍s unresponsive to medical​ management, underwent a ‍hysteroscopic ablation. W‌hich of the following is ​false about this procedur‍e?","raw_text":"A woman with AUB-E, who was unresponsive to medical management, underwent a hysteroscopic ablation. Which of the following is false about this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Safe, effective with less ‍morbidity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Quicker ‍recovery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fertility i‍s preserved","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Method destroys 2-3 mm of ‍myometrium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fertility is preserved","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fertility is preserved</strong></p><p><strong>Fertility</strong> is<strong> not preserved</strong> with ablative procedures, because the <strong>endometrium</strong> and superficial <strong>myometrium</strong> are <strong>destroyed</strong>.</p>\n<p>In ablative procedures, the <strong>uterus</strong> is <strong>preserved</strong>, and hysterectomy is avoided. The endometrium along with 2-3mm of superficial myometrium is destroyed.</p>\n<p>Various <strong>methods of endometrial ablation</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hysteroscopic endometrial ablation</li>\n<li>Transcervical endometrial resection (TCRE) - now replaced by bipolar electrode called Versapoint</li>\n<li>Radiofrequency induced thermal ablation (RITEA)</li>\n<li>Microwave ablation</li>\n<li>NovaSure - impedance-controlled endometrial ablation</li>\n<li>Bilateral uterine artery embolization</li>\n<li>Uterine tamponade</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4455","text":"When is dilatation and cur‍ettage contraindicated?","raw_text":"When is dilatation and curettage contraindicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pelvic inflammatory diseas‍e","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ectopic ‍pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Menstrual ir‍regularities","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endome‍triosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pelvic inflammatory disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b93a57fa46a74483919fa882c10bc6f1x1280x4487.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pelvic inflammatory disease</strong></p><p>Dilatation and curettage (D&amp;C) is contraindicated in <strong>pelvic inflammatory disease</strong> (PID) as it increases the risk of ascending genital infection.</p>\n<p>D&amp;C is a minor gynecological procedure of dilating the cervix and curetting the endometrial tissue from the uterine cavity. Diagnostic indications of D&amp;C include AUB, secondary amenorrhea to detect tubercular endometritis, postmenopausal bleeding to rule out endometrial cancer or hyperplasia, and infertility due to genital TB.</p>\n<p>Therapeutic indications of D&amp;C include missed abortion, incomplete abortion, retained products of conception, and endometrial polypectomy.</p>\n<p><strong>Absolute contraindication</strong> of D&amp;C includes Suspected/ desired <strong>pregnancy</strong> and <strong>lower genital</strong> tract <strong>infection.</strong> However, D&amp;C is performed in some cases with genital infections like septic abortion (where retained pieces of placenta act as a nidus and need to be removed) and pyometra (as there is cervical obstruction with superinfection of menstrual blood and cellular debris, D&amp;C is appropriate to remove the obstruction as well as the source of infection)</p>\n<p>Complications of D&amp;C include ascending infection, cervical tear with bleeding, incompetent os, uterine perforation, and ashermann syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3120","text":"A 31-year-old woman presen‍ted with acute abdominal ​pain and spotting. UPT wa‍s negative. On examinatio‌n, she was tachycardic an​d afebrile. Guarding and ‍abdominal tenderness was ‍present. CT abdomen showe​d air in the uterine wall​ and pneumoperitoneum. Wh‌ich of the following is h‌er condition most likely ‌to be a complication of?","raw_text":"A 31-year-old woman presented with acute abdominal pain and spotting. UPT was negative. On examination, she was tachycardic and afebrile. Guarding and abdominal tenderness was present. CT abdomen showed air in the uterine wall and pneumoperitoneum. Which of the following is her condition most likely to be a complication of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endometri‍al biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dilatation a‍nd curettage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lapar‍oscopy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyster‍oscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dilatation and curettage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dilatation and curettage</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>uterine perforation</strong>. In a<strong> non-pregnant uterus,</strong> uterine perforation is most likely to occur due to <strong>dilatation and curettage. </strong></p>\n<p>In <strong>pregnancy</strong> and <strong>uterine malignancies,</strong> the uterus is soft. Hence, <strong>uterine perforation is common </strong>during surgeries. </p>\n<p>The etiology of uterine perforation is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Insertion of <strong>intrauterine contraceptive devices </strong>(IUCD)<strong>- </strong>due to the lack of provider experience.</li>\n<li>Spontaneous migration of IUCD within the myometrium</li>\n<li><strong>Dilatation and curettage (D&amp;C)</strong></li>\n<li>Hysteroscopy - diagnostic (e.g, hysteroscopic biopsy) or therapeutic (adhesiolysis) </li>\n<li>Laparoscopy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It can present with <strong>fever,</strong> acute abdominal <strong>pain,</strong> nausea and vomiting, and <strong>bleeding</strong> pv. Imaging reveals air within the uterine wall or <strong>pneumoperitoneum.</strong>  </p>\n<p>The management is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Conservative</strong> management - <strong>Fundal injuries</strong> are managed conservatively, as the myometrium contracts around the defect.</li>\n<li>Surgical management:\n<ul>\n<li>Small defects can be repaired laparoscopically or hysteroscopically.</li>\n<li>Emergency <strong>laparotomy</strong> - as abdominal viscera may prolapse through the defect. </li>\n<li><strong>Hysterectomy</strong> - if the defect is large, and the patient has completed her family, hysterectomy is done as last resort.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6971","text":"An 18-year-old girl presen‍ts to you with cramping a​bdominal pain. It starts ‍at the onset of menses an‌d is more pronounced in t​he first two days. Which ‍condition is she likely s‍uffering from?","raw_text":"An 18-year-old girl presents to you with cramping abdominal pain. It starts at the onset of menses and is more pronounced in the first two days. Which condition is she likely suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congestive d‍ysmenorrhoea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spasmodic d‍ysmenorrhoea","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Unilateral d‍ysmenorrhoea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acquired dy‍smenorrhoea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Spasmodic dysmenorrhoea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Spasmodic dysmenorrhoea</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>s</strong><strong>pasmodic dysmenorrhea.</strong> It<strong> </strong>presents with <strong>cramping pain</strong> at the <strong>onset of menses</strong> which is more pronounced in the first two days of menstruation.</p>\n<p><strong>Dysmenorrhoea</strong> is defined as <strong>cramping pain </strong>accompanying <strong>menstruation</strong>. It can be classified as follows:</p>\n<p>A. Etiological classification:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary</strong> dysmenorrhoea - no identifiable pelvic pathology. Commonly occurs in the age group of 18 to 25 years, with ovulatory cycles.</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary (acquired)</strong> dysmenorrhoea - is associated with <strong>pelvic pathologies</strong> such as fibroids, adenomyosis, PID, and endometriosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Unilateral</strong> <strong>dysmenorrhoea</strong> can occur in a rudimentary horn of a <strong>bicornuate</strong> uterus, <strong>IUCD</strong> users, and <strong>cervical stenosis.</strong></p>\n<p>B. Clinical classification:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Spasmodic dysmenorrhoea:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Most prevalent </li>\n<li>Cramping pain at the onset of menses, more pronounced on the first 2 days. </li>\n<li>Occurs due to myometrial contractions due to increased PGF2α</li>\n<li>Common in adolescents and young women</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Congestive dysmenorrhoea</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Pelvic pain and pelvic discomfort occurs, a few days before menses begin</li>\n<li>Occurs due to pelvic venous congestion</li>\n<li>Seen in pelvic endometriosis, PID, fibroids, and IUCD users</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Membranous dysmenorrhoea</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Least prevalent </li>\n<li>The endometrium is shed as a cast at the time of menstruation</li>\n<li>The passage of the cast is associated with painful uterine cramps</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6972","text":"An increase in which of th‍e following is spasmodic ​pain during dysmenorrhoea‍ attributed to?","raw_text":"An increase in which of the following is spasmodic pain during dysmenorrhoea attributed to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PG‍E1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PG‍E2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"PG‍D2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PG‍F2α","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. PGF2α","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. PGF2α</strong></p><p><strong>Spasmodic pain</strong> is attributed to myometrial contractions occuring due to<strong> increased PGF2α.</strong> This is secreted under the effect of<strong> pr</strong><strong>ogesterone.</strong></p>\n<p>Progesterone is secreted after ovulation by the corpus luteum. Hence, in <strong>anovulatory</strong> <strong>cycles</strong>, menstruation is <strong>not accompanied by spasmodic pain.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6974","text":"A 28-year-old woman presen‍ts to the psychiatry OPD ​with complaints of mood s‍wings and depression 10 d‌ays prior to her menses. ​This is associated with f‍atigue, breast tenderness‍, and bloating. Her sympt​oms resolve once menses b​egin. What is the treatme‌nt of choice in this case‌?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old woman presents to the psychiatry OPD with complaints of mood swings and depression 10 days prior to her menses. This is associated with fatigue, breast tenderness, and bloating. Her symptoms resolve once menses begin. What is the treatment of choice in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Evening pr‍imrose oil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leupr‍olide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fluox‍etine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Drospi‍renone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fluoxetine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fluoxetine</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>premenstrual dysphoric disorder</strong> (PMDD). Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (<strong>SSRIs</strong>) such as <strong>fluoxetine</strong>, sertraline, and paroxetine are the treatment of choice for PMDD. </p>\n<p>DSM-5 defines<strong> premenstrual dysphoric disorder </strong>(PMDD) as the presence of <strong>at least five</strong> of the associated<strong> symptoms</strong> most of the time during the<strong> premenstrual week. </strong>Symptoms improve within a few days after menses and are minimal in the postmenstrual period. These symptoms include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Marked <strong>emotional lability</strong> </li>\n<li>Marked irritability or anger</li>\n<li>Marked <strong>depressive</strong> mood, self-deprecating thoughts </li>\n<li>Marked anxiety</li>\n<li>Decreased interest in activities</li>\n<li>Difficulty in concentration</li>\n<li><strong>Lethargy</strong></li>\n<li>Appetite changes</li>\n<li><strong>Bloating</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Breast tenderness</strong></li>\n<li>Weight gain </li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1209\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p> Treatment of PMDD is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lifestyle</strong> <strong>changes</strong> such as - stress reduction, adequate sleep, and exercise, reduction in caffeine intake, smoking cessation</li>\n<li>Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (<strong>SSRIs</strong>) - fluoxetine, paroxetine, sertraline </li>\n<li><strong>Evening primrose oil</strong> - to treat breast tenderness</li>\n<li>Ovulation suppression - danazol, <strong>leuprolide, drospirenone</strong></li>\n<li>Other less proven methods - cognitive behavioral therapy, calcium supplements, etc. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Premenstrual syndrome has no scientific definition. The constellation of symptoms is now defined under PMDD.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"YI-zLnJm","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"30_trauma_amputations__prosthetics_and_joint_replacement_surgery","title":"30.Trauma Amputations, Prosthetics And Joint Replacement Surgery","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MC5460","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cause of ‌amputations?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of amputations?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malignant b‌one tumours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Crush ‌injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peripheral vascular diseas‌e","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Recurren‌t sepsis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Peripheral vascular disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Peripheral vascular disease</strong></p><p><strong>Peripheral vascular disease </strong>is the <strong>most common cause</strong> of amputations.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 766\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Amputation </strong>is defined as removal of the limb through a part of the <strong>bone</strong>.<strong> Disarticulation</strong> is the removal of the limb <strong>through the joint.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Causes of amputations can be classified into 3<span class=\"fontstyle0\">Ds: </span></p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle2\"><strong>1. Dead</strong></span></p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: square;\">\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Peripheral vascular disease</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Severe trauma</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Burns</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Frostbite </span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span class=\"fontstyle2\">2. Dangerous</span></strong></p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: square;\">\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Malignant tumour</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Potentially lethal sepsis</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle2\">Crush injury</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span class=\"fontstyle2\">3. Damned nuisance</span></strong></p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: square;\">\n<li>Severe pain</li>\n<li>Gross malformation</li>\n<li>Recurrent sepsis</li>\n<li>Severe loss of function</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: In India, road traffic accidents (RTAs) are the top cause, while globally, peripheral vascular diseases, often linked to diabetes, are more common. Overall, peripheral vascular disease occurs more frequently than RTAs worldwide, unless the question specifies the primary cause in India, which would be crush injuries from RTAs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6528","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an indication for amp‌utation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an indication for amputation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fulminant g‌as gangrene","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ankle-brachial index &amp;lt; ‌0.45","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Severe peripheral vascular‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transcutaneous oxygen tens‌ion 40mmHg","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Transcutaneous oxygen tension 40mmHg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Transcutaneous oxygen tension 40mmHg</strong></p><p>Transcutaneous oxygen tension values <strong>below 20 mmHg</strong> are an indication for <strong>amputation.</strong></p>\n<p>Fulminant gas gangrene, ankle-brachial index &lt; 0.45,<strong> </strong>and severe peripheral vascular disease are indications for amputation.</p>\n<p><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":33555261,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"11\":4,\"12\":0,\"28\":1}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"Transcutaneous oxygen pressure measurement is believed to be the most reliable and sensitive test for wound healing. One study reported an 88% sensitivity rate and an 84% specificity rate. The pressure may be falsely low in areas of oedema, cellulitis, and venous stasis changes.\"}'><strong>Transcutaneous oxygen pressure measurement</strong> is believed to be the most reliable and sensitive test for wound healing. The pressure may be falsely low in areas of edema, cellulitis, and venous stasis changes.</span> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6531","text":"A construction worker is b‌rought to the casualty fo‌llowing a crush injury to‍ his right lower limb. Wh​ich of the following scor‍es can be used to decide ‌between amputation and li​mb-salvage surgery?","raw_text":"A construction worker is brought to the casualty following a crush injury to his right lower limb. Which of the following scores can be used to decide between amputation and limb-salvage surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gustilo And‌erson score","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Revised Tr‌auma Score","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mangled Extremity Severity‌ Score","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trauma Injury Severity Sco‌re","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mangled Extremity Severity Score","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mangled Extremity Severity Score</strong></p><p><strong>Mangled Extremity Severity Score (MESS) </strong>score can be used to decide between amputation and limb salvage in crushing injuries.</p>\n<p>Components of MESS are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S</strong>hock</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>schemia</li>\n<li><strong>V</strong>elocity</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>ge</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Total score is 11. <strong>Six</strong> or less is consistent with a salvageable limb.</p>\n<p>Other scores to assess if the limb is salvageable or not are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Limb salvage index</li>\n<li>Ganga score</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Gustilo Anderson Classification</strong> is used to classify open fractures. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Revised Trauma Score</strong> (RTS) characterizes the injured patient’s condition by incorporating the Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS), systolic blood pressure, and respiratory rate. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Trauma Injury Severity Score </strong>(TRISS)<strong> </strong>comprises<strong> </strong>Injury Severity Score (ISS), Revised Trauma Score (RTS), age of the patient and mechanism of Injury (blunt/penetrating).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6540","text":"A patient suffering from l‌eprosy is prescribed a Ja‌ipur foot prosthesis in t‍he rehabilitation center.​ Which of the following s‍tatements is true about t‌his prosthesis?","raw_text":"A patient suffering from leprosy is prescribed a Jaipur foot prosthesis in the rehabilitation center. Which of the following statements is true about this prosthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Restricte‌d mobility","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Squatting is‌ not possible","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inversion and eversion are‌ not possible","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Possible to walk on uneven‌ ground","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Possible to walk on uneven ground","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d521cf4faa3242c79096edce7c99cd4bx1280x1164.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/841c018cddb24b13bf76439e8318ec9cx1280x1164.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Possible to walk on uneven ground</strong></p><p>It is possible to <strong>walk</strong> on <strong>uneven grounds</strong> with<strong> Jaipur foot</strong> <strong>prosthesis</strong> as subtalar movements (inversion and eversion) are present.</p>\n<p>Different types of foot prostheses are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Jaipur foot</li>\n<li>Solid ankle cushion heel <strong>(SACH)</strong></li>\n<li>Stationary attachment flexible endoskeleton <strong>(SAFE)</strong>, similar to Jaipur foot.</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 310px; width: 376px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 141.921875px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Prosthesis</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.078125px; height: 36px;\"><strong>SACH</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Jaipur foot </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 53px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 141.921875px; height: 53px;\">Appearance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.078125px; height: 53px;\">Does not look normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px; height: 53px;\">Looks normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 141.921875px; height: 33px;\">Walking barefoot</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.078125px; height: 33px;\">Not possible</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px; height: 33px;\">Possible</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 141.921875px; height: 27px;\">Mobility</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.078125px; height: 27px;\">Restricted</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px; height: 27px;\">Allowed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 141.921875px; height: 36px;\">Dorsiflexion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.078125px; height: 36px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px; height: 36px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 0px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 141.921875px; height: 0px;\">\n<p>Inversion/Eversion</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.078125px; height: 0px;\">\n<p>Absent</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px; height: 0px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 141.921875px; height: 36px;\">Squatting</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.078125px; height: 36px;\">Not possible</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px; height: 36px;\">Possible</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 141.921875px; height: 18px;\">Cost</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.078125px; height: 18px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 107px; height: 18px;\">Low</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The above features make the <strong>Jaipur foot</strong> <strong>more suitable </strong>for the<strong> Indian scenario</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5472","text":"A patient presented to the‌ casualty with left lower‌ limb trauma. He underwen‍t amputation of the limb ​and the tibial nerve was ‍buried. After 2 months he‌ presented with severe ne​urogenic pain with ambula‍tion. What is the best tr​eatment option for this p‍atient?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the casualty with left lower limb trauma. He underwent amputation of the limb and the tibial nerve was buried. After 2 months he presented with severe neurogenic pain with ambulation. What is the best treatment option for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intra-articular injection ‌of steroids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transcutaneous electric ne‌rve stimulation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Revision‌ surgery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Interferent‌ial therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Revision surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Revision surgery</strong></p><p><span class=\"fontstyle0\"><strong>Revision of transtibial amputation</strong> should be done in a patient experiencing <strong>exquisite neurogenic pain</strong> with ambulation as buried tibial nerve can form postoperative <strong>neuroma.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Transected nerves should not be buried, placed on tension, or compressed as doing so will promote the development of a postoperative neuroma. </span><span class=\"fontstyle0\">They should simply be transected sharply and allowed to retract into the soft tissues proximally.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\"><strong>Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation </strong>(<strong>TENS</strong>) is the use of electric current produced by a device to stimulate the nerves for the treatment of mild to moderate pain in acute or chronic setting. </span><span class=\"fontstyle0\"><strong>Interferential current therapy </strong>(ICT) is another variant of TENS, but works at a much deeper level of the targeted tissue.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6533","text":"A blind patient from a low‌ socioeconomic background‌ suffered severe trauma t‍o his upper limbs. The fo​llowing procedure was per‍formed on this patient. W‌hat is this amputation kn​own as?","raw_text":"A blind patient from a low socioeconomic background suffered severe trauma to his upper limbs. The following procedure was performed on this patient. What is this amputation known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lisfr‌anc's","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sym‌e's","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chop‌art’s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kruken‌berg's","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Krukenberg's","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/34b35f2cbd9840b682d0a91b5f79f1bd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Krukenberg&#x27;s</strong></p><p>The given image is of <strong>Krukenberg's amputation</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this procedure, the radius and ulna are split to make a <strong>pincer mechanism</strong>. It has been recommended in <strong>blind</strong> <strong>patients</strong> with bilateral below-elbow amputation or in patients who can't afford expensive prostheses (to provide some amount of function).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6535","text":"A patient presents with ga‌ngrene of the foot. On ex‌amination, there is necro‍sis extending up to the n​avicular bone without the‍ involvement of the talus‌ and calcaneum. Which amp​utation is more effective‍ in the given condition w​ithout excessive loss of ‍bone and soft tissue?","raw_text":"A patient presents with gangrene of the foot. On examination, there is necrosis extending up to the navicular bone without the involvement of the talus and calcaneum. Which amputation is more effective in the given condition without excessive loss of bone and soft tissue?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transme‌tatarsal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lisfr‌anc's","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chop‌art’s","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sym‌e's","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chopart’s","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chopart’s</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, <strong>Chopart's amputation</strong> is preferred because the talus and calcaneum are not necrotic and can be preserved. </p>\n<p>Chopart’s amputation is done at the level of <strong>transverse tarsal joints</strong> i.e between the <strong>articulation of calcaneus with the cuboid </strong>and the articulation of <strong>talus with navicular bone.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6536","text":"Amputation at the level of‌ tarso-metatarsal joint i‌s being performed in a pa‍tient with diabetic foot ​ulcer. What is this amput‍ation known as?","raw_text":"Amputation at the level of tarso-metatarsal joint  is being performed in a patient with diabetic foot ulcer. What is this amputation known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cho‌part","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Piro‌goff","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sy‌me","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lisf‌ranc","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lisfranc","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lisfranc</strong></p><p> <strong>Amputation</strong> at the level of <strong>tarso-metatarsal joint</strong> is known as <strong>Lisfranc</strong> amputation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6335","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions, is joint repl‌acement surgery not done?‍","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions, is joint replacement surgery not done?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteoar‌thritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rheumatoid‌ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ankylosing ‌spondylitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Septic a‌rthritis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Septic arthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Septic arthritis</strong></p><p>Joint replacement surgery is not done in <strong>septic arthritis.</strong></p>\n<p>Active infection at the joint is an absolute <strong>contraindication</strong> for <strong>joint replacement</strong> surgery.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6380","text":"Identify the option which ‌is correctly matched with‌ the name of the componen‍ts marked in the picture.​ Component A Component B ‍Component C Component D 1‌ Femoral component Plasti​c spacer Patellar compone‍nt Tibial component 2 Fem​oral component Patellar c‍omponent Plastic spacer T​ibial component 3 Tibial ‍component Patellar compon‍ent Plastic spacer Femora​l component 4 Tibial comp‌onent Plastic spacer Pate‌llar component Femoral co‌mponent","raw_text":"<p>Identify the option which is correctly matched with the name of the components marked in the picture.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 168px; width: 545px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 39px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 119px;\">Component A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Component B</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.813px;\">Component C</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.187px;\">Component D</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 39px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119px;\">Femoral component</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Plastic spacer</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.813px;\">Patellar component</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.187px;\">Tibial component</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 39px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119px;\">Femoral component</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Patellar component</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.813px;\">Plastic spacer</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.187px;\">Tibial component</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 39px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119px;\">Tibial component</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Patellar component</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.813px;\">Plastic spacer</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.187px;\">Femoral component</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 39px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 119px;\">Tibial component</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Plastic spacer</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.813px;\">Patellar component</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.187px;\">Femoral component</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opti‌on 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Opti‌on 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Opti‌on 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Opti‌on 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Option 2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/de65d8a778be478a91c09c3200de0616.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ef3e7e7aa732475d9d218462df5b18fcx1280x1861.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Option 2</strong></p><p>Components of total knee replacement prosthesis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Femoral component (A)</li>\n<li>Patellar button (B)</li>\n<li>Plastic spacer tibial base plate (C)</li>\n<li>Tibial implant (D)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8113","text":"A young adult sustained a ‌fracture in the shaft of ‌the femur following a roa‍d traffic accident. Which​ among the following stat‍ements is incorrect regar‌ding the correction of hi​s condition?","raw_text":"A young adult sustained a fracture in the shaft of the femur following a road traffic accident. Which among the following statements is incorrect regarding the correction of his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Russel traction may be use‌d in this patient","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metaphyseal fractures or f‌ractures extending intra-‌articularly are the only ‍indications for primary p​lating","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MIPO is a type of biologic‌al fixation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Soft tissue damage is maxi‌mal in MIPO technique","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Soft tissue damage is maximal in MIPO technique","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Soft tissue damage is maximal in MIPO technique</strong></p><p>In<strong> MIPO (minimally invasive plate osteosynthesis) </strong>technique<strong>, </strong>soft tissue damage is <strong>minimal. </strong></p>\n<p>MIPO is a type of <strong>bridge plating </strong>or <strong>biological fixation </strong>(option C). Here, the plate is fixed proximally and distally and the central fracture area is rendered untouched. Hence, fracture hematoma and soft tissue damage are minimised.</p>\n<p>Option A: Gallow's traction, hip spica and Russel traction are measures commonly used in children. But <strong>Russell's traction</strong> can also be used in <strong>adults</strong> as a first-aid measure. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Metaphyseal fractures</strong> or fractures extending <strong>intra-articularly</strong> are the only indications for <strong>primary plating</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6342","text":"Identify the prosthesis in‌ the given image.","raw_text":"Identify the prosthesis in the given image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Austin-Moor‌e prosthesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thompson ‌prosthesis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Jepson hip‌ prosthesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bohlmann ‌prosthesis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thompson prosthesis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/68531eba761f43a6ab25a8e3c2b7b41c.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d01d80e31d524cb88b68b5b44445e3eex268x359.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5ef79f057fbe42f08f8d0f88c133c91cx800x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thompson prosthesis</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>Thompson's prosthesis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Thompson prosthesis is used in<strong> hemiarthroplasty </strong>to replace the head of the femur. It is specially indicated in cases where the neck of the femur is absorbed. It can be used with or without cement.</p>\n<p><strong>Thompson prosthesis</strong> has no fenestrations. <strong>Austin-Moore</strong> prosthesis contains<strong> two fenestrations</strong> in the stem which are for the growth of bone as shown in the images below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6354","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about me‌tal on metal implants?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about metal on metal implants?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High carbon cobalt chromiu‌m alloy has been shown to‌ have lower wear rates","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It cannot be used in the p‌atients with renal failur‌e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"These implants are avoided‌ in young females","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patients with these implan‌ts have normal levels of ‌cobalt and chromium level‍s in serum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Patients with these implants have normal levels of cobalt and chromium levels in serum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Patients with these implants have normal levels of cobalt and chromium levels in serum</strong></p><p>Patients with metal on metal implants have <strong>high</strong> levels of <strong>cobalt</strong> and <strong>chromium</strong> in <strong>serum,</strong> erythrocytes, and urine.</p>\n<p>High carbon cobalt-chromium alloy carbides are <strong>5 times harder than </strong>the <strong>metal matrix</strong> and have <strong>lower wear rates</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Impaired renal function</strong> leads to increased accumulation of cobalt and chromium compounds in the blood and hence, <strong>contraindicated </strong>in patients with<strong> renal failure</strong>. They are also transported through the<strong> placenta</strong>, hence they are <strong>not used in women</strong> of childbearing age.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6361","text":"Which of the following pat‌ients are at risk for het‌erotopic bone formation a‍fter hip arthroplasty? A ​patient who underwent pre‍operative radiation A man‌ with bilateral hypertrop​hic osteoarthritis A pati‍ent with a history of het​erotopic ossification in ‍the hip A woman with post​traumatic arthritis A pat‍ient recently diagnosed w‍ith ankylosing spondyliti​s","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following patients are at risk for heterotopic bone formation after hip arthroplasty?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A patient who underwent preoperative radiation</li>\n<li>A man with bilateral hypertrophic osteoarthritis</li>\n<li>A patient with a history of heterotopic ossification in the hip</li>\n<li>A woman with posttraumatic arthritis</li>\n<li>A patient recently diagnosed with ankylosing spondylitis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 and ‌3 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2 an‌d 4 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,3 and‌ 5 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2,3,4 an‌d 5 only","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2,3,4 and 5 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2,3,4 and 5 only</strong></p><p><strong>Preoperative radiation</strong> is effective in <strong>preventing</strong> heterotopic bone formation after hip arthroplasty.</p>\n<p>Risk factors for <strong>heterotopic bone formation</strong> after hip arthroplasty are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Posttraumatic arthritis</li>\n<li>Hypertrophic arthritis</li>\n<li>Diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis</li>\n<li>Paget's disease</li>\n<li>Ankylosing spondylitis</li>\n<li>History of heterotopic bone formation in either hip</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Heterotopic bone formation around the hip is seen in about 20% of patients 5 years after joint replacement. Treatment consists of <strong>NSAIDs</strong> for 3–6 weeks post-operatively or a single dose of radiation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6351","text":"Uncemented arthroplasty is‌ done in a patient with f‌racture of the neck of fe‍mur following prolonged s​teroid therapy. Which of ‍the following statements ‌is false about this proce​dure?","raw_text":"Uncemented arthroplasty is done in a patient with fracture of the neck of femur following prolonged steroid therapy. Which of the following statements is false about this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is usually preferred in‌ younger patients","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The surface of the implant‌ contains porous coating","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydroxyapatite decreases t‌he formation of new bone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fixation of the prosthesis‌ is by bone ingrowth","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hydroxyapatite decreases the formation of new bone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hydroxyapatite decreases the formation of new bone</strong></p><p>In uncemented arthroplasty, <strong>hydroxyapatite increases</strong> the formation of <strong>new bone.</strong></p>\n<p>Uncemented arthroplasty is preferred in <strong>younger patients.</strong> Prosthesis contains pores or mesh on the surface to enhance bone growth. The prosthesis stem remains attached to the bone by bone ingrowth.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7135","text":"Which of the following is ‌true regarding the scar t‌issue in patellar clunk s‍yndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding the scar tissue in patellar clunk syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior pole of patella, ‌impinging during flexion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior pole of patella, ‌impinging during extensio‌n","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior pole of patella, ‌impinging during flexion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior pole of patella, ‌impinging during extensio‌n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Superior pole of patella, impinging during extension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Superior pole of patella, impinging during extension</strong></p><p>In <strong>patellar clunk syndrome</strong>, the scar tissue which is formed at the<strong> superior pole</strong> of the <strong>patella</strong> becomes <strong>impinged during knee extension</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Patellar clunk syndrome</strong> is seen after <strong>posterior-stabilized knee arthroplasty</strong>. A <strong>fibrous nodule</strong> forms on the posterior surface of the <strong>quadriceps tendon</strong> just above the <strong>superior pole</strong> of the <strong>patella</strong>. This can become <strong>entrapped</strong> in the intercondylar notch of the femoral prosthesis and cause the knee to \"<strong>clunk\"</strong> at <strong>30- 45 degrees</strong> to the femoral axis while the patient actively <strong>extends</strong> the <strong>knee</strong>. </p>\n<p>The recommended treatment is <strong>arthroscopic debridement of the nodule</strong>.<br style=\"font-weight: 400;\"/><br style=\"font-weight: 400;\"/></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7466","text":"De-gloving injury refers t‌o ______","raw_text":"De-gloving injury refers to ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skin and subcutaneous fat ‌are stripped from the und‌erlying fascia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Skin, subcutaneous fat and‌ fascia are stripped from‌ tendons","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Skin, subcutaneous fat, fa‌scia and tendons are stri‌pped from bone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Only skin is‌ stripped off","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Skin and subcutaneous fat are stripped from the underlying fascia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/22ed05e62e1847ef8ac555f5033d495e.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Skin and subcutaneous fat are stripped from the underlying fascia</strong></p><p>Degloving is the avulsion of skin and subcutaneous fat from the underlying fascia, muscle or bone.</p>\n<p>Degloving injuries can be <strong>open</strong> or <strong>closed</strong>. An example of open degloving injury is finger avulsion injury with loss of skin. Closed degloving injuries result from shearing forces (e.g. motor vehicle collisions).</p>\n<p>The extent of degloving injuries are often underappreciated and much of the skin may be non-viable.  Disruption of perforating vascular and lymphatic vessels may result in a characteristic <strong>haemolymphatic</strong> collection <strong>between</strong> the fascial planes called a <strong>Morel-Lavallée lesion. </strong></p>\n<p>Patterns of degloving injury:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Limited degloving with abrasion or avulsion</li>\n<li>Non-circumferential degloving</li>\n<li>Circumferential single plane degloving</li>\n<li>Circumferential multiplanar degloving </li>\n</ol>\n<p>Assessing the viability of degloved tissue can be difficult and may therefore require more than one surgical exploration and debridement before definitive reconstruction. Non-viable skin may show fixed staining and thrombosis of subcutaneous veins. Most surgeons serially excise the degloved skin until punctate dermal bleeding is seen from viable tissue. </p>\n<p><strong>Intravenous fluorescein</strong> can help in delineating non-viable tissue, but it requires specialist equipment and there is a small risk of anaphylaxis. <strong>Indocyanine green </strong>has been found to have less risk of allergy. </p>\n<p><strong>Open</strong> degloving injuries are managed by <strong>reattaching</strong> the viable skin and <strong>grafting</strong>. <strong>Closed</strong> degloving injuries are managed by <strong>draining</strong> the accumulated fluid, <strong>removing</strong> the dead tissue and <strong>sclerotherapy</strong>. For severe, multiplanar degloving, amputation is done. </p>\n<p>The given image below shows a degloving injury of the lower limb: </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6548","text":"For which of the following‌ conditions would a patie‌nt be prescribed the orth‍osis shown in the image b​elow?","raw_text":"For which of the following conditions would a patient be prescribed the orthosis shown in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Foot‌ drop","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pilon f‌racture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial tibial stress syndr‌ome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fracture of ‌the calcaneum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Foot drop","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5acf0ddeae12427ea8886d4545394e91.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Foot drop</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>ankle-foot orthosis</strong> (foot drop brace) which is commonly used in <strong>foot drop</strong>.</p>\n<p>Foot drop occurs due to <strong>common peroneal nerve injury.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8926","text":"Choose the wrong statement‌ about the plaster cast w‌edging procedure.","raw_text":"Choose the wrong statement about the plaster cast wedging procedure.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Procedure is done when the‌re is incorrect correctio‌n of fracture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is used to correct angu‌lation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plaster is cut off circumf‌erentially above the leve‌l of fracture","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A cut is forced open on th‌e concave side of angulat‌ion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Plaster is cut off circumferentially above the level of fracture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/49b102ef9a674efcb66a94d33f326052x720x667.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Plaster is cut off circumferentially above the level of fracture</strong></p><p>The technique of <strong>plaster cast wedging</strong> involves the plaster cast being cut circumferentially <strong>at the level of fracture</strong> and not above the level of the fracture.</p>\n<p>In certain situations, after the fracture is reduced and the plaster applied, a <strong>check x-ray</strong> may<strong> show</strong> some<strong> angulation</strong>. The angulation is corrected by forcing open a cut on the <strong>concave side of the angulation</strong> and the plaster is reinforced with extra bandages.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"M98yxLfr","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"30_disorders_of_thyroid","title":"30.Disorders Of Thyroid","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MA1796","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common, preventa‌ble cause of mental retar‌dation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common, preventable cause of mental retardation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenital H‌ypothyroidism","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Inborn errors of metabolis‌m","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Congenital hyperthyroidism‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Down's ‌Syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Congenital Hypothyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Congenital Hypothyroidism</strong></p><p><strong>Congenital hypothyroidism</strong> is the most common <strong>preventable</strong> cause of mental retardation which results from a deficient production of thyroid hormone. It is more common among <strong>girls</strong> and the prevalence is about 1:3000 to 1:4000 births.</p>\n<p><strong>Down's syndrome</strong> is the <strong>most common overall</strong> cause of mental retardation, followed by Fragile X syndrome, which mainly affects boys.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<table style=\"height: 309px; width: 487.667px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 368.667px;\">\n<p><strong>Causes of mental retardation:</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">Prenatal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 368.667px;\">\n<p><strong>Congenital hypothyroidism</strong></p>\n<p>Infection (toxoplasma, rubella, CMV, HSV)</p>\n<p>Genetic abnormalities</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Trisomy 21</strong></li>\n<li>Fragile X syndrome</li>\n<li>other point mutations and chromosomal abnormalities</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Toxins and teratogens</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alcohol</li>\n<li>Drugs</li>\n<li>Radiation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Inborn errors of metabolism</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">Perinatal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 368.667px;\">\n<p>Hypoxia</p>\n<p>Prematurity</p>\n<p>Intracranial haemorrhage</p>\n<p>Perinatal CNS infection</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">Postnatal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 368.667px;\">\n<p>Acquired brain injury</p>\n<p>CNS infection, haemorrhage, malignancy</p>\n<p>Severe malnutrition and deprivation</p>\n<p>Environmental toxins (lead, mercury)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1799","text":"In a 4-month old, term inf‌ant with open posterior f‌ontanelle and hypotonia, ‌you suspect congenital hy​pothyroidism and send thy‍roid studies. Which of th‍e following is the most c‌ommon cause of this condi‌tion?","raw_text":"In a 4-month old, term infant with open posterior fontanelle and hypotonia, you suspect congenital hypothyroidism and send thyroid studies. Which of the following is the most common cause of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maternal hy‌pothyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thyroid d‌ysgenesis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Iodine d‌eficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thyroid dysh‌ormonogenesis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thyroid dysgenesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thyroid dysgenesis</strong></p><p><strong>Thyroid dysgenesis </strong>is the most common cause of permanent congenital hypothyroidism (80–85% of cases) &gt; dyshormonogenesis (15%).</p>\n<p><strong>Dysgenesis</strong> may involve ectopy, agenesis (absence) or hypoplasia (underdevelopment) of the thyroid gland. <strong>Dyshormonogenesis</strong> results from a defect in any of the steps in thyroid hormone biosynthesis.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 600px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 466px; height: 18px;\">\n<p><strong>Etiology of congenital hypothyroidism:</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 466px; height: 18px;\">\n<p><strong>Primary hypothyroidism:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Thyroid dysgenesis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Thyroid dyshormonogenesis</strong></li>\n<li>Iodine deficiency (endemic goiter)</li>\n<li>Defect in thyroid hormone transport- Mutation in <em>MCT8</em> (monocarboxylate<br/>transporter 8) gene</li>\n<li>Maternal antibodies- thyrotropin receptor–blocking antibody<br/>(TRBAb)</li>\n<li>Maternal medications- iodides, amiodarone, propylthiouracil, methimazole, radioiodine</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 32.4px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 466px; height: 32.4px;\">\n<p><strong>Central (hypopituitary) hypothyroidism:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>TSH deficiency- mutation in TSH β-subunit gene</li>\n<li>TRH  deficiency- mutation in TRH gene</li>\n<li>TRH unresponsiveness- mutation in TRH receptor gene</li>\n<li>Multiple congenital pituitary hormone deficiencies</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>*TSH - thyroid-stimulating hormone; TRH - thyroid-releasing hormone</p>\n<p><strong>Acquired hypothyroidism</strong> can be caused by iodine deficiency, thyroiditis, radiation, histiocytosis, and drugs. The <strong>most common </strong>cause of acquired hypothyroidism is <strong>autoimmune thyroiditis (Hashimoto's).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Mutation in <strong>PAX-8</strong> gene is associated with congenital hypothyroidism and genitourinary abnormalities such as renal agenesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0896","text":"You are called to see a 3-‌day-old, term neonate, wh‌o has not passed meconium‌ yet. On clinical examina​tion, there is generalize‍d hypotonia and the abdom‍en is slightly distended ‌without any evidence of a‌scites or hepatosplenomeg‍aly. Incidentally, you al​so note a palpable goitre​ in this baby. Laboratory​ investigations reveal lo‍w T4 and elevated TSH lev​els. Which of the followi‌ng is the most likely cau‌se for this child's condi‌tion?","raw_text":"You are called to see a 3-day-old, term neonate, who has not passed meconium yet. On clinical examination, there is generalized hypotonia and the abdomen is slightly distended without any evidence of ascites or hepatosplenomegaly. Incidentally, you also note a palpable goitre in this baby. Laboratory investigations reveal low T4 and elevated TSH levels. Which of the following is the most likely cause for this child's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyroid dysh‌ormonogenesis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thyroid d‌ysgenesis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Central hyp‌othyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antithyroid‌ antibodies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thyroid dyshormonogenesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thyroid dyshormonogenesis</strong></p><p>A history of decreased activity and late passage of meconium, associated with low T4 and high TSH is suggestive of primary hypothyroidism. In an infant with a <strong>palpable</strong> goitre, the likely diagnosis is congenital hypothyroidism secondary to <strong>thyroid dyshormonogenesis. </strong></p>\n<p>The most common cause of congenital hypothyroidism is thyroid dysgenesis but it is not associated with goitre. <strong>Thyroid dyshormonogenesis</strong> results from the deficiency of one or more enzymes involved in thyroid hormone synthesis/secretion.</p>\n<p>Congenital hypothyroidism can be:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 431.951px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 188px;\"><strong>Goitrous</strong> (Palpable goitre)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.951px;\"><strong>Non-goitrous</strong> (No palpable goitre)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 188px;\">\n<p>Thyroid dyshormonogenesis</p>\n<p>Iodine deficiency</p>\n<p>Placental passage of antithyroid drugs</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.951px;\">\n<p>Thyroid dysgenesis</p>\n<p>Thyroid receptor blocking antibodies</p>\n<p>Central (hypopituitary) hypothyroidism</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0854","text":"At how many weeks of gesta‌tion does thyroxine secre‌tion begin in a fetus?","raw_text":"At how many weeks of gestation does thyroxine secretion begin in a fetus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"28 w‌eeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"20 w‌eeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"12 w‌eeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"6 w‌eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 12 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 12 weeks</strong></p><p>Thyroxine secretion begins in the fetus at <strong>12 weeks of gestation.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 4047\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The fetal thyroid arises from an outpouching of the foregut at the base of the tongue (foramen cecum). It migrates to its normal location over the thyroid cartilage by 8-10 wk of gestation. The bilobed shape of the thyroid is recognized by 7 weeks of gestation.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1951","text":"An anxious mother has brou‌ght a 4-month-old baby wi‌th complaints that the ba‌by is not feeding well an​d is excessively sleepy a‍ll the time. Clinical exa‍mination reveals jaundice‌ and hypotonia. His pictu‌re is provided below. Wha‍t is the probable diagnos​is?","raw_text":"An anxious mother has brought a 4-month-old baby with complaints that the baby is not feeding well and is excessively sleepy all the time. Clinical examination reveals jaundice and hypotonia. His picture is provided below. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenital adrenal hyperpl‌asia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Growth hormo‌ne deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Congenital adrenal insuffi‌ciency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Congenital h‌ypothyroidism","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Congenital hypothyroidism","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bf96cf31d13343388f7f26c844f0bee5.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Congenital hypothyroidism</strong></p><p>The above picture shows a child with a protuberant abdomen, umbilical hernia, macroglossia, and edematous facies. Also, the infant is sluggish, has jaundice, feeding difficulties, and hypotonia, all of which suggests a diagnosis of <strong>congenital hypothyroidism.</strong></p>\n<p>Clinical features of congenital hypothyroidism:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prolongation of physiologic hyperbilirubinemia</strong>- early sign (it is due to delayed maturation of glucuronide conjugation)</li>\n<li><strong>Constipation</strong>- usually does not respond to treatment</li>\n<li>Wide open anterior and posterior fontanelles (&gt;0.5 cm) and cranial sutures</li>\n<li><strong>Developmental delay, delayed bone growth, delayed dentition</strong></li>\n<li>Cardiovascular- slow pulse rate, cardiomegaly, asymptomatic pericardial effusion, edema</li>\n<li>Respiratory- <strong>macroglossia</strong>, feeding difficulties, choking spells, respiratory difficulties</li>\n<li>Haematological- <strong>macrocytic anaemia</strong> <strong>refractory to treatment</strong> with haematinics</li>\n<li>Nervous system- hypothermia, <strong>hypotonia</strong>, slow relaxation of deep tendon reflexes, sluggishness, and somnolence</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1800","text":"A newborn with abnormal ne‌onatal screening tests wa‌s diagnosed with Pendred ‌syndrome. Which of the fo​llowing is not a feature ‍of this syndrome?","raw_text":"A newborn with abnormal neonatal screening tests was diagnosed with Pendred syndrome. Which of the following is not a feature of this syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Goi‌tre","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal recessive disord‌er","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Conductive ‌hearing loss","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Defective gene on chromoso‌me 7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Conductive hearing loss","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Conductive hearing loss</strong></p><p><strong>Sensorineural hearing loss</strong> is present in <strong>Pendred</strong> syndrome; not conductive hearing loss.</p>\n<p>Pendred syndrome is an <strong>autosomal-recessive</strong> disorder, caused by a mutation in the <span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">gene<em> SLC26A4</em></span> on <strong>chromosome 7</strong>, which codes for <strong>pendrin</strong>, a <strong>chloride–iodide transport protein</strong> common to the <strong>thyroid gland and the cochlea. </strong></p>\n<p>Clinical features include <strong>sensorineural hearing loss </strong>and<strong> g</strong><strong>oitre with hypothyroidism. </strong>It is the most common cause of syndromic deafness. The biochemical reason for hypothyroidism in patients with Pendred syndrome is impaired iodide organification (resulting from a mutation in the Pendrin gene). </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1956","text":"A 9-day-old infant has ele‌vated TSH level and low T‌4 level on newborn screen‌ing. If this condition is​ left untreated, which of‍ the following is most li‍kely to be seen in this i‌nfant in the first few mo‌nths of life?","raw_text":"A 9-day-old infant has elevated TSH level and low T4 level on newborn screening. If this condition is left untreated, which of the following is most likely to be seen in this infant in the first few months of life?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperr‌eflexia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperirr‌itability","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diar‌rhea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prolonged‌ jaundice","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prolonged jaundice","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prolonged jaundice</strong></p><p>Elevated TSH levels and low T4 levels in a 9-day-old infant are suggestive of congenital hypothyroidism. <strong>Prolongation of physiologic hyperbilirubinemia</strong> is an early sign of congenital hypothyroidism. It is due to delayed maturation of the hepatic glucuronide conjugation system.</p>\n<p>Hyperreflexia, hyperirritability and diarrhoea are seen in hyperthyroidism; not hypothyroidism.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>Kocher–Debré–Sémélaigne syndrome:</strong> The muscles are <strong>usually hypotonic</strong> in <strong>congenital hypothyroidism</strong>. Rarely, hypothyroidism in infancy can be characterized by generalized <strong>muscular pseudohypertrophy</strong>, myxoedema, short stature and cretinism. Affected older children can have an athletic appearance. Boys are more prone to the development of the syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0883","text":"During a rural health camp‌, a father brings a dull-‌looking baby with macrogl‌ossia and an umbilical he​rnia. On enquiry, you fin‍d out that the parents we‍re told about a thyroid p‌roblem in the child at bi‌rth, for which they did n‍ot pursue any treatment. ​Which of the following is​ least likely to be found​ in children with the giv‍en condition?","raw_text":"During a rural health camp, a father brings a dull-looking baby with macroglossia and an umbilical hernia. On enquiry, you find out that the parents were told about a thyroid problem in the child at birth, for which they did not pursue any treatment. Which of the following is least likely to be found in children with the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abnormal facies due to cra‌niosynostosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Palpabl‌e goitre","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Open posteri‌or fontanelle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epiphyseal‌ dysgenesis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Abnormal facies due to craniosynostosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Abnormal facies due to craniosynostosis</strong></p><p>A dull-looking face with macroglossia, and umbilical hernia in a child with a thyroid disorder are all indicative of congenital hypothyroidism.<strong> Craniosynostosis</strong> is <strong>not</strong> seen in neonatal hypothyroidism. It is seen in hyperthyroidism.</p>\n<p><strong>Craniosynostosis</strong> refers to the premature fusion of sutures and closure of fontanelles and often results in abnormal, triangular facies. In congenital hypothyroidism, there is delayed closure of the fontanelles. Palpable goitre, though not common, can be seen in cases of untreated thyroid dyshormonogenesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1965","text":"As part of a quality impro‌vement project undertaken‌ by the maternal and chil‌d health services, you ar​e evaluating the current ‍practice of newborn scree‍ning and detection rates ‌of hypothyroidism in vari‌ous community health cent‍res. Which of the followi​ng statements is false?","raw_text":"As part of a quality improvement project undertaken by the maternal and child health services, you are evaluating the current practice of newborn screening and detection rates of hypothyroidism in various community health centres. Which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Simultaneous measurement o‌f T4 and TSH levels is th‌e ideal method.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The sample is obtained by ‌heel-prick between 1-5 da‌ys of life using dried bl‌ood spot.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Newborn screening always d‌etects the affected twin ‌in same-sex twins.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Primary TSH, with back up ‌T4 is the most common str‌ategy used.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Newborn screening always detects the affected twin in same-sex twins.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Newborn screening always detects the affected twin in same-sex twins.</strong></p><p>Newborn screening can fail to detect the affected twin with hypothyroidism.</p>\n<p>Discordance for thyroid dysgenesis is the rule in monozygotic twins. The transfusion of euthyroid blood from the unaffected twin normalizes the serum levels of T4 and TSH in the <strong>affected twin,</strong> resulting in <strong>delayed or missed diagnoses</strong>. Therefore, a<strong> second sample </strong>for congenital hypothyroidism<strong> screening </strong>at <strong>14 days of age </strong>should be considered in monozygotic twins. </p>\n<p>Neonatal thyroid screening strategies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primarily TSH, backup T4; T4 is measured if TSH level is &gt;20 mU/L</li>\n<li>Primarily T4, backup TSH: TSH is measured only if T4 level is low</li>\n<li><strong>Concomitant T4 and TSH; it is the most sensitive method</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: the sample is obtained <strong>after 24-48 hours</strong>, to minimize the false elevation of TSH values due to normal <strong>post-delivery neonatal TSH surge</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1972","text":"As an intern posted in pae‌diatrics, you are asked t‌o interpret the results o‌f a heel prick test that ​you performed on a newbor‍n. Which of the following‍ results is suggestive of‌ primary hypothyroidism?","raw_text":"As an intern posted in paediatrics, you are asked to interpret the results of a heel prick test that you performed on a newborn. Which of the following results is suggestive of primary hypothyroidism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low T4 level and low TSH l‌evel","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Low T4 level and high TSH ‌level","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"High T4 level and low TSH ‌level","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High T4 level and high TSH‌ level","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Low T4 level and high TSH level","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Low T4 level and high TSH level</strong></p><p><strong>Low T4 </strong>and<strong> high TSH</strong> levels indicate primary hypothyroidism.</p>\n<p>Blood specimen for neonatal thyroid screening is obtained by <strong>heel prick</strong> between <strong>1 and 5 days of life.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Levothyroxine therapy </strong>is<strong> <span style=\"color: #000000;\">started immediately </span></strong><span style=\"color: #000000;\">after diagnosis,</span> <span style=\"color: #000000;\">at</span><span style=\"color: #000000;\"> 10 to 15 µg/kg per day.</span></p>\n<table style=\"width: 425px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184.3125px;\"><strong>Condition</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 79.6875px;\"><strong>Goitre</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 65px;\"><strong>T4</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\"><strong>TSH</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184.3125px;\">Thyroid dysgenesis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 79.6875px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 65px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">Elevated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184.3125px;\">Thyroid dyshormonogenesis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 79.6875px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 65px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">Elevated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184.3125px;\">Thyroid receptor blocking antibodies</td>\n<td style=\"width: 79.6875px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 65px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">Elevated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184.3125px;\">Central hypothyroidism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 79.6875px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 65px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">Normal/Low</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184.3125px;\">Hyperthyroidism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 79.6875px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 65px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95px;\">Low</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1979","text":"The image given below depi‌cts an x-ray of the knee ‌in a term baby. Which amo‌ng the following conditio​ns is associated with it?‍","raw_text":"The image given below depicts an x-ray of the knee in a term baby. Which among the following conditions is associated with it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenital h‌ypothyroidism","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Graves ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Congenita‌l rickets","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteogenesi‌s imperfecta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Congenital hypothyroidism","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/92f663ff228748bcaee9a2fdd7883d73.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ca5aca5301d147178e6e7158770ce4a4x685x512.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/531d3e2b15884eb99ec2ed019f824bffx343x303.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5cc31be3b5994a2297ed8b2465c5a370x382x291.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Congenital hypothyroidism</strong></p><p>The deformity shown in the X-ray image is the <strong>absence of distal femoral epiphysis</strong>, which is a typical radiological feature of <strong>congenital hypothyroidism.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>X-ray findings in hypothyroidism</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Absence of <strong>distal femoral</strong> and <strong>proximal tibial</strong> epiphyses at birth.</li>\n<li>Punctate epiphyseal dysgenesis (multiple foci of ossification)</li>\n<li>Deformity or <strong>beaking</strong> <strong>of the 12th thoracic</strong> or 1st or 2nd lumbar vertebra</li>\n<li>X-ray image of the skull shows <strong>large fontanels </strong>and<strong> wide sutures;</strong> <strong>wormian</strong> (intrasutural) bones; and, often, an enlarged and round sella turcica.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Image below: The infant on the left with congenital hypothyroidism demonstrates the absence of the distal femoral and proximal tibial epiphyses, while in the normal infant on the right the distal femoral epiphysis is present:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>small upper femoral epiphyses</strong>:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>enlarged sella turcica</strong> and <strong>wormian</strong> <strong>bones</strong> (intrasutural):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2135","text":"Ayesha is a 9th standard g‌irl who presented with a ‌neck swelling, which bega‌n more than a year ago. H​er mother says she is emb‍arrassed about it and alw‍ays covers her neck with ‌a shawl when she goes to ‌school. Her menstrual cyc‍les have also become irre​gular as of late. Blood t​ests reveal slightly elev​ated TSH levels and the p‍resence of thyroid peroxi​dase antibodies. Which of‌ the following is the mos‌t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"Ayesha is a 9th standard girl who presented with a neck swelling, which began more than a year ago. Her mother says she is embarrassed about it and always covers her neck with a shawl when she goes to school. Her menstrual cycles have also become irregular as of late. Blood tests reveal slightly elevated TSH levels and the presence of thyroid peroxidase antibodies.  Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iodine d‌eficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Congenital h‌ypothyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Graves ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic lymphocytic thyroi‌ditis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chronic lymphocytic thyroiditis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe0bbd97a21d4d95bd3dc29fd8bf4dd6x800x598.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chronic lymphocytic thyroiditis</strong></p><p>The presence of thyroid peroxidase antibodies (TPO-Abs) and elevated thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) level in a child with a goitre is suggestive of <strong>chronic lymphocytic (Hashimoto) thyroiditis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Chronic lymphocytic (Hashimoto) thyroiditis </strong>is an autoimmune disease. Clinical presentation can include goitre, growth retardation, and other features of hypothyroidism. The thyroid is diffusely enlarged, firm, and non-tender.</p>\n<p>TSH is moderately elevated and TPO-Abs and anti-thyroglobulin antibodies (anti-Tg Abs) can be detected.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows goitre due to Hashimoto's thyroiditis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2140","text":"A lady, with a history of ‌a thyroid disorder and th‌ree prior abortions, deli‌vered a female neonate pr​ematurely at 34 weeks of ‍gestation. In the newborn‍ infant, tachycardia, irr‌itability, and prominent ‌eyes were noted and neona‍tal hyperthyroidism was s​uspected. Which of the fo​llowing statements is fal​se about the condition?","raw_text":"A lady, with a history of a thyroid disorder and three prior abortions, delivered a female neonate prematurely at 34 weeks of gestation. In the newborn infant, tachycardia, irritability, and prominent eyes were noted and neonatal hyperthyroidism was suspected.  Which of the following statements is false about the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maternal Graves' disease i‌s the most common cause o‌f neonatal hyperthyroidis‌m","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It results from the transp‌lacental passage of mater‌nal stimulatory TSH recep‌tor antibody","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Microcephaly and frontal b‌ossing with triangular fa‌cies are common clinical ‌findings","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neonatal hyperthyroidism o‌ccurs in most infants bor‌n to mothers with a histo‌ry of Graves’ disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neonatal hyperthyroidism occurs in most infants born to mothers with a history of Graves’ disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neonatal hyperthyroidism occurs in most infants born to mothers with a history of Graves’ disease</strong></p><p>Neonatal hyperthyroidism occurs in only about <strong>2%</strong> of infants born to mothers with a history of Graves’ disease. But <strong>maternal Graves' disease</strong> is by far the most common cause of neonatal hyperthyroidism.</p>\n<p>Neonatal hyperthyroidism (due to Grave's) results from the <strong>transplacental passage</strong> of maternal stimulatory thyrotropin/TSH receptor antibody (TSHR-Ab).</p>\n<p>Clinical features are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Low birth weight (IUGR)</li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">Premature birth</li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">Microcephaly</li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">Frontal bossing and triangular facies</li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">Warm, moist skin</li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">Irritability, hyperactivity, restlessness, poor sleep</li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">Tachycardia</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<p>TSHR-Ab (TSH-R-stimulating antibodies) is another name for TSH-R-stimulating immunoglobulins (TSI). </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2137","text":"A pregnant woman with a pa‌st history of Grave's dis‌ease has currently comple‌ted 36 weeks of gestation​. She underwent partial t‍hyroidectomy 10 years bac‍k and has remained euthyr‌oid since then. The previ‌ous child had craniosynos‍tosis. Which of the follo​wing antibodies is most l​ikely to be detected, if ​a cord blood sampling is ‍done at delivery?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman with a past history of Grave's disease has currently completed 36 weeks of gestation. She underwent partial thyroidectomy 10 years back and has remained euthyroid since then. The previous child had craniosynostosis. Which of the following antibodies is most likely to be detected, if a cord blood sampling is done at delivery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti-TPO‌ antibody","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"TSH receptor stimulating a‌ntibody","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"TSH receptor blocking anti‌body","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti-Thyroglobulin antibod‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. TSH receptor stimulating antibody","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. TSH receptor stimulating antibody</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with a history of <strong>Graves' disease</strong> in the <strong>mother</strong> and <strong>craniosynostosis</strong> (neonatal Graves') in the <strong>previous child</strong> suggests that the fetus is at increased risk of <strong>neonatal Grave's disease</strong>. Thus, the antibody likely to be found at birth in the cord blood sampling is the <strong>TSH receptor stimulating antibody </strong>(TRSAb).</p>\n<p>Neonatal Graves is caused by the transplacental passage of TRSAbs from a mother with a history of Graves' disease. The mothers can have </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Active Graves' disease</li>\n<li>Graves' in remission</li>\n<li>Prior history of Graves' treated with radioactive ablation or thyroidectomy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>TSRAbs bind to the receptor for TSH and stimulate cyclic AMP, leading to thyroid hyperplasia and unregulated overproduction of thyroid hormone.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2138","text":"A 4-year-old girl, a known‌ case of celiac disease, ‌presented to the pediatri‌c endocrine clinic with a​ history of neck swelling‍, tremor, and weight loss‍. On evaluation, she was ‌found to have Graves’ dis‌ease. Other conditions th‍at need to be screened in​ this patient include all​ of the following except ​__________.","raw_text":"A 4-year-old girl, a known case of celiac disease, presented to the pediatric endocrine clinic with a history of neck swelling, tremor, and weight loss. On evaluation, she was found to have Graves’ disease. Other conditions that need to be screened in this patient include all of the following except __________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Viti‌ligo","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pernicio‌us anemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diabetes mel‌litus type 2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Addison‌ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diabetes mellitus type 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diabetes mellitus type 2</strong></p><p><strong>Type 2 Diabetes mellitus</strong> is <strong>not</strong> associated with Grave's disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Conditions associated with Grave's disease (autoimmune diseases)</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus type 1</li>\n<li>Pernicious anaemia</li>\n<li>Celiac disease</li>\n<li>Vitiligo</li>\n<li>Addison disease</li>\n<li>Alopecia areata</li>\n<li>Myasthenia gravis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2139","text":"An 11-year-old girl was br‌ought by her parents with‌ complaints of weight los‌s, increased appetite, an​d a decline in scholastic‍ performance. Following i‍nvestigations, a diagnosi‌s of Grave’s disease is m‌ade. Which of the followi‍ng is the drug of choice ​for initial therapy?","raw_text":"An 11-year-old girl was brought by her parents with complaints of weight loss, increased appetite, and a decline in scholastic performance. Following investigations, a diagnosis of Grave’s disease is made. Which of the following is the drug of choice for initial therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propylth‌iouracil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methi‌mazole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Radioacti‌ve iodine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Levoth‌yroxine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Methimazole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Methimazole</strong></p><p><strong>Methimazole</strong> is the initial drug of choice for treating <strong>Grave's disease</strong> (hyperthyroidism) in children.</p>\n<p>Option A: Propylthiouracil is not recommended in children because it is associated with <strong>fatal hepatotoxicity.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Radioactive iodine is not preferred as the first-line treatment in hyperthyroidism for children.</p>\n<p>Option D: Levothyroxine is used to treat hypothyroidism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2141","text":"Your unit chief has asked ‌you to present the case o‌f a teenager, who was adm‌itted for evaluation of m​ental retardation, deaf-m‍utism and a gait disturba‍nce. He also has a signif‌icant patellar clonus. On‌ general exam, you note a‍ goitre but according to ​lab reports, he is appare​ntly euthyroid. His puber​tal development has also ‍been normal. The most lik​ely diagnosis is _______.‌","raw_text":"Your unit chief has asked you to present the case of a teenager, who was admitted for evaluation of mental retardation, deaf-mutism and a gait disturbance. He also has a significant patellar clonus. On general exam, you note a goitre but according to lab reports, he is apparently euthyroid. His pubertal development has also been normal. The most likely diagnosis is _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurological endemic creti‌nism","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Grave's‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pendred ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myxedematous endemic creti‌nism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Neurological endemic cretinism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Neurological endemic cretinism</strong></p><p>The clinical presentation of a euthyroid patient with <strong>goitre</strong> as well as mental retardation, deaf-mutism, and normal pubertal development points towards <strong>neurological endemic cretinism. </strong></p>\n<p>Endemic cretinism includes 2 different, but overlapping, syndromes: <strong>neurologic type and myxedematous type. </strong>It is essentially the same disorder, phenotypically modified by the <strong>length and severity of postnatal hypothyroidism. </strong>Neurological manifestations are interpreted as reflecting the effects of maternal and fetal hypothyroxinemia, secondary to severe iodine deficiency, on the developing nervous system<strong>. Both </strong>neurologic and myxedematous type <strong>can manifest neurologic symptoms.</strong> </p>\n<p>Although neurological cretins are euthyroid, linear growth arrest lines in the long bones are often demonstrated radiologically, suggesting previous hypothyroidism.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option B: Graves disease includes a triad of hyperthyroidism with goitre, ophthalmopathy, and dermopathy (no mental retardation, deafness, or pyramidal signs).</p>\n<p>Option C: Pendred Syndrome is caused by a mutation in chloride–iodide transporter in thyroid and cochlea resulting in euthyroid/hypothyroid goitre and sensorineural hearing loss (pyramidal signs like clonus are not seen).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0985","text":"During a health outreach p‌rogram in Sikkim, you com‌e across 3 children from ‌the same family, with goi​ter. Endemic cretinism wa‍s suspected. Which of the‍ following can be a manif‌estation of this conditio‌n?","raw_text":"During a health outreach program in Sikkim, you come across 3 children from the same family, with goiter. Endemic cretinism was suspected. Which of the following can be a manifestation of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial nerve‌ involvement","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blindness and hypothyroidi‌sm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Strabismus and spastic dip‌legias","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Reversible mental retardat‌ion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Strabismus and spastic diplegias","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Strabismus and spastic diplegias</strong></p><p><strong>Strabismus and spastic diplegias</strong> are among the manifestations of the neurological type of endemic cretinism.</p>\n<p><strong>Cretinism</strong> is a condition characterized by irreversible abnormalities in intellectual development. <strong>Endemic cretinism</strong> occurs due to the nutritional <strong>deficiency of iodine</strong>. It can be neurological cretinism or myxedematous cretinism. It is associated with severe, irreversible mental retardation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"LaM3xlsD","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"30_non_hodgkin_lymphomas__low_grade","title":"30.Non Hodgkin Lymphomas  Low Grade","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MA9171","text":"The peripheral smear findi‌ng of a patient with feve​r and lymphadenopathy is ​shown below. What is the ‍most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"The peripheral smear finding of a patient with fever and lymphadenopathy is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infectious m‌ononucleosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Follicula‌r lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic lymphocytic leukem‌ia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hairy-cel‌l leukemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Infectious mononucleosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2dcb639613fc48b1a788e7430147c8c3.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f034f001f2df4aa68cbd7dccbf697060x1200x1055.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Infectious mononucleosis</strong></p><p>The peripheral smear finding shows <strong>Downey cells </strong>or<strong> ballerina skirt cells</strong>. These are typically seen in <strong>infectious mononucleosis </strong>caused by Epstein-Barr virus <strong>(EBV)</strong> and cytomegalovirus <strong>(CMV)</strong>. </p>\n<p>They<strong> </strong>are<strong> atypical lymphocytes</strong> with abundant basophilic cytoplasm containing clear vacuolations and prominent nucleoli. They become large as a result of antigen stimulation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0168","text":"Bruton tyrosine kinase is ‌involved in which type of​ B-cell lymphoma?","raw_text":"Bruton tyrosine kinase is involved in which type of B-cell lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mantle-cel‌l lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diffuse large B-cell lymph‌oma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic lymphocytic lympho‌ma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Burkitt ‌lymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chronic lymphocytic lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chronic lymphocytic lymphoma</strong></p><p><strong>Bruton tyrosine kinase</strong> (BTK)<strong> </strong>is associated with B-cell chronic lymphocytic lymphoma/ small lymphocytic lymphoma (<strong>CLL</strong>/<strong>SLL</strong>).</p>\n<p>Tumour cells in CLL rely on signals generated by the B-cell receptor for growth and survival. These signals are transduced by a cascade of kinases that include BTK.</p>\n<p>Note: BTK is also defective in patients with <strong>congenital X-linked agammaglobulinemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9174","text":"A 65-year-old man presents‌ with easy fatigability, ​weight loss, and anorexia​. On examination, he is f‍ound to have generalized ​lymphadenopathy and hepat‍osplenomegaly. Investigat​ions show a total WBC cou​nt of 1 lakh per microlit‌er, with 80% lymphocytes.​ Peripheral smear is as s​hown below. What is the m‍ost likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old man presents with easy fatigability, weight loss, and anorexia. On examination, he is found to have generalized lymphadenopathy and hepatosplenomegaly. Investigations show a total WBC count of 1 lakh per microliter, with 80% lymphocytes. Peripheral smear is as shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic myel‌oid leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic lymphocytic leukem‌ia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hodgkin ‌lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hairy cel‌l leukemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b403c6e3702d4bdcb81a8cc90e403101.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d40bea353bbb4194907b0f6dd2df578cx1200x1874.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia</strong></p><p>The clinical presentation and peripheral smear findings point toward a diagnosis of <strong>chronic lymphocytic leukemia.</strong></p>\n<p>The peripheral smear shows <strong>smudge cells</strong> and increased <strong>lymphocytes</strong> with<strong> scant cytoplasm</strong>, round to slightly <strong>irregular nuclei</strong> containing <strong>clumped chromatin. </strong>This large number of monotonous, uniform-looking, mature lymphocytes is often described as a \"<strong>convent-girl\" appearance</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia<strong> (CLL)</strong> / Small lymphocytic lymphoma<strong>(SLL)</strong> is the most common leukemia among adults in the western world.</p>\n<p>The differentiating point between CLL and SLL is that in<strong> </strong><strong>CLL </strong>the<strong> </strong>absolute lymphocyte count is <strong>&gt;5000 per mm³ </strong>and in<strong> </strong>SLL:&lt;5000 per mm³.</p>\n<p>It is usually<strong> asymptomatic</strong> at diagnosis but can present with easy fatigability, weight loss, and anorexia. Generalized lymphadenopathy and hepatosplenomegaly are usually seen.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Total blood count</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Total <strong>leukocyte count</strong> is highly <strong>variable</strong>; <strong>leukopenia</strong> is seen when the <strong>marrow</strong> is <strong>involved</strong>. </li>\n<li><strong>Hypogammaglobulinemia</strong> is common and contributes to increased susceptibility to infections.</li>\n<li>10% to 15% of patients develop <strong>hemolytic anemia </strong>or<strong> thrombocytopenia</strong> due to <strong>autoantibodies</strong> made by nonneoplastic B cells.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Peripheral smear</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Predominantly shows <strong>small lymphocytes</strong> with scant cytoplasm</li>\n<li><strong>Smudge cells </strong>or <strong>basket cells </strong>or <strong>parachute cells -</strong> artifacts of the lymphocytes which are disrupted during the process of making smears.</li>\n<li>Contains a large number of monotonous, uniform-looking, mature lymphocytes, which is often described as a <strong>convent-girl appearance</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Spherocytes</strong> (due to coexisting autoimmune hemolytic anemia) and <strong>nucleated RBCs</strong> (due to premature release of progenitors in face of severe anemia, marrow infiltration by the tumor, or both)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lymph node biopsy</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diffuse effacement</strong> composed of small lymphocytes admixed with variable numbers of larger activated lymphocytes that often gather in loose aggregates</li>\n<li>Referred to as <strong>proliferation centers</strong>, which contain <strong>mitotically active</strong> cells. Proliferation centers are <strong>pathognomonic </strong>for CLL / SLL.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The</strong> <strong>chromosomal defects</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Deletion of<strong> 13q</strong> (<strong>commonest</strong> and has a <strong>good prognosis</strong>)</li>\n<li>Deletion of 11q</li>\n<li>Trisomy 12q</li>\n<li>Deletion of <strong>17p</strong> (<strong>poor prognosis</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0171","text":"The neoplastic cells in ch‌ronic lymphocytic leukemi​a/ small lymphocytic lymp​homa originate from which‍ of the following?","raw_text":"The neoplastic cells in chronic lymphocytic leukemia/ small lymphocytic lymphoma originate from which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pre B‌ cell","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Germinal ce‌nter B cell","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mantle z‌one cell","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Post-germinal-center memor‌y B cell","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Post-germinal-center memory B cell","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Post-germinal-center memory B cell</strong></p><p>The cell of origin for chronic lymphocytic leukemia/small lymphocytic lymphoma <strong>(CLL/SLL)</strong> is either a <strong>post-germinal center memory B cell</strong> or a<strong> naive B cell.</strong></p>\n<p>The cells of CLL show both <strong>unmutated Ig</strong> segments (those of <strong>naive </strong>B-cell origin) and somatic <strong>hypermutated Ig</strong> segments (those of <strong>post-germinal center</strong> memory B cell).</p>\n<p>Tumours with unmutated Ig segments (those of <strong>naive B-cell </strong>origin) pursue a <strong>more aggressive course</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0165","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a genetic anomaly see​n in patients with chroni​c lymphocytic lymphoma/ s‍mall lymphocytic lymphoma​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a genetic anomaly seen in patients with chronic lymphocytic lymphoma/ small lymphocytic lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deletio‌n of 11q","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Triso‌my 17p","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Triso‌my 12q","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deletio‌n of 13q","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trisomy 17p","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trisomy 17p</strong></p><p><strong>Deletion</strong> <strong>of 17p</strong> is a genetic anomaly seen in chronic lymphocytic lymphoma <strong>(CLL)/</strong> small lymphocytic lymphoma <strong>(SLL),</strong> not trisomy 17p.</p>\n<p>The genetic anomalies seen in CLL/SLL are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Trisomy 12q</li>\n<li>Deletion of 13q</li>\n<li>Deletion of 17p (bad prognosis)</li>\n<li>Deletion of 11q (bad prognosis) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0173","text":"A 70-year-old man presents‌ with weakness and genera​lized lymphadenopathy. Ly​mph node biopsy shows dif‍use effacement with small​ lymphocytes and prolifer‍ation centers. What is th​e likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old man presents with weakness and generalized lymphadenopathy. Lymph node biopsy shows difuse effacement with small lymphocytes and proliferation centers. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Follicula‌r lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic lymphocytic leukem‌ia/small lymphocytic lymp​homa","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mantle-cel‌l lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diffuse large B-cell lymph‌oma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia/small lymphocytic lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/09f82210f2034ebb974ee01ce23c5b16x1280x1201.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1da002a5ac14487894194473e682f55bx1280x1201.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/279c43df8333454190c8590f0d8e29dfx1280x1104.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fa622314a7dd498b9cec8e72b19bb47cx1280x1103.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia/small lymphocytic lymphoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>chronic lymphocytic leukemia/small lymphocytic lymphoma </strong>(CLL/SLL).</p>\n<p>Lymph node biopsy in CLL/SLL shows <strong>effacement</strong> of <strong>normal nodal architecture</strong> by sheets of predominantly <strong>small lymphocytes</strong>. They have round to slightly irregular nuclei, condensed chromatin, and scant cytoplasm.</p>\n<p>Admixed are a variable number of larger activated lymphocytes that often gather in loose aggregates referred to as <strong>proliferation centers.</strong> These contain mitotically active cells. When present, <strong>proliferation centers </strong>are <strong>pathognomic</strong> for CLL/SLL (also known as a <strong>pseudofollicular pattern</strong>).</p>\n<p>The image given below shows a lymph node biopsy of CLL in a low-power view. It shows loss of normal architecture and a large, pale-staining area in the center of the node (a proliferation center)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0159","text":"A 58-year-old man with cer‌vical lymphadenopathy is ​suspected to have chronic​ lymphocytic leukemia. Wh‍at should be the absolute​ lymphocyte count/mm3 to ‍diagnose this condition?","raw_text":"A 58-year-old man with cervical lymphadenopathy is suspected to have chronic lymphocytic leukemia. What should be the absolute lymphocyte count/mm3 to diagnose this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt;‌2000","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt;‌5000","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt;‌10000","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;1‌00000","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. >5000","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. &gt;5000</strong></p><p>The <strong>diagnostic</strong> requirement for chronic lymphocytic leukemia (<strong>CLL</strong>) is absolute lymphocyte count <strong>&gt;5000</strong> per cubic millimetre.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (<strong>CLL</strong>) and small lymphocytic lymphoma (<strong>SLL</strong>) <strong>differ</strong> only in the degree of peripheral blood<strong> lymphocytosis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>CLL - 5000/mm<sup>3</sup></li>\n<li>SLL - &lt;5000 cells/mm<sup>3</sup></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: CLL is the most common leukemia of adults in the western world.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9192","text":"Flow cytometric analysis o‌f four patients is given ​below. Who among the foll​owing is most likely suff‍ering from chronic lympho​cytic leukemia?","raw_text":"Flow cytometric analysis of four patients is given below. Who among the following is most likely suffering from chronic lymphocytic leukemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pati‌ent 1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pati‌ent 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pati‌ent 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pati‌ent 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Patient 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ad8748e271e94fcbab3437551b73b1df.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6fdbee5356f744abb37f074deec0d402x1280x1080.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Patient 1</strong></p><p>Small lymphocytes of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (<strong>CLL</strong>)/ small lymphocytic leukemia (<strong>SLL</strong>) are <strong>CD23</strong> and <strong>CD5 positive. </strong>Hence, the flow cytometry of patient 1 (CD23 and CD5 positive) correctly represents the phenotype of CLL.</p>\n<p>The tumour cells also express the pan B-cell markers <strong>CD19 and CD20</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Low-level expression</strong> of surface <strong>Ig</strong> (usually IgM or IgM and IgD) is also <strong>typical</strong>. </p>\n<p>Given below is the explanation of how to interpret flow cytometry results:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Interpretation of results:</p>\n<p>Quadrant 1- Cells marked in green: -VE for A antigen, +VE for B antigen</p>\n<p>Quadrant 2- Cells marked in purple: -VE for both the antigens.</p>\n<p>Quadrant 3- Cells marked in yellow: +VE for both the antigens </p>\n<p>Quadrant 4- Cells marked in red: +VE for A antigen, -VE for B antigen </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9200","text":"A 73-year-old man came wit‌h complaints of malaise a​nd low-grade fever. He ha​d generalized lymphadenop‍athy. The histopathologic​al examination of the lym‍ph node is as shown below​. These cells showed posi​tivity for CD5, CD19, CD2‌0, and CD23. Which of the​ following does not indic​ate a poor prognosis in t‍his condition?","raw_text":"A 73-year-old man came with complaints of malaise and low-grade fever. He had generalized lymphadenopathy. The histopathological examination of the lymph node is as shown below. These cells showed positivity for CD5, CD19, CD20, and CD23. Which of the following does not indicate a poor prognosis in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deletions of‌ 11q and 17p","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lack of somatic hypermutat‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Expression‌ of ZAP-70","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Absence of of NOTCH1 mutat‌ions","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Absence of of NOTCH1 mutations","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/39c1de8c95124bdfb70106a2c366ef96.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4c8ec06380014833ba14baf8cf0733f7x1280x1050.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Absence of of NOTCH1 mutations</strong></p><p>The given scenario and histopathological findings are suggestive of <strong>chronic lymphocytic leukemia</strong> (CLL). The <strong>presence</strong> of <strong>NOTCH1</strong> mutation is associated with a <strong>poor prognosis</strong> in chronic lymphocytic leukaemia (CLL). </p>\n<p>The histopathological image shows <strong>small</strong>, darker staining <strong>lymphocytes</strong> with<strong> prominent</strong> <strong>nucleoli</strong> which are typically seen in chronic lymphocytic leukaemia (CLL).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Variables that correlate with a worse outcome include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The presence of <strong>deletions of 11q and 17p</strong></li>\n<li>Lack of somatic hypermutation</li>\n<li><strong>Expression </strong>of <strong>ZAP-70</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Presence </strong>of<strong> NOTCH 1 mutations</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9205","text":"A middle-aged woman presen‌ted with chronic fatigue ​and generalized lymphaden​opathy. Investigations re‍vealed overexpression of ​BCL2. What is the likely ‍diagnosis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presented with chronic fatigue and generalized lymphadenopathy. Investigations revealed overexpression of BCL2. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Burkitt ‌lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Follicula‌r lymphoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Diffuse large-cell lymphom‌a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mantle ce‌ll lymhoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Follicular lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1a31ec7c340a4a6e9c85f457e7c7c99ax1280x1112.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e2095803a8c74154a4846b4252d21f0dx1280x1112.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Follicular lymphoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>follicular lymphoma. </strong>This<strong> </strong>condition is associated with <strong>overexpression</strong> of<strong> BCL2.</strong></p>\n<p>It usually presents in middle age as <strong>painless</strong> generalized <strong>lymphadenopathy</strong>. The involvement of <strong>extranodal</strong> sites is relatively <strong>uncommon. </strong>It has an <strong>indolent</strong> waxing and waning <strong>course</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>In almost 50% of patients, this cancer gets transformed to diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. The median survival rate is for 7-9 years.</p>\n<p>The<strong> cell </strong>of <strong>origin</strong> for follicular lymphoma is <strong>germinal</strong> centre <strong>B</strong> cells. It shows <strong>(14;18) </strong>translocation.</p>\n<p>Lymph node biopsy shows closely packed follicles. <strong>Centrocytes </strong>(small cells with the cleaved nucleus and scant cytoplasm) and <strong>centroblasts</strong> (large cells with open nuclear chromatin and multiple nucleoli) are seen.</p>\n<p><strong>Bone marrow</strong> is involved in 85% of the cases and shows para-trabecular lymphoid aggregates.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0178","text":"Which of the following chr‌omosomal abnormality is l​ikely to be seen in patie​nts with follicular lymph‍oma?","raw_text":"Which of the following chromosomal abnormality is likely to be seen in patients with follicular lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"t(11‌;14)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"t(14‌;18)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"t(8‌;14)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"t(9‌;14)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. t(14;18)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. t(14;18)</strong></p><p>Follicular lymphoma shows <strong>t(14;18).</strong></p>\n<p>Its hallmark is a translocation that juxtaposes the <strong>IgH</strong> locus on chromosome <strong>14</strong> and the <strong>BCL2</strong> locus on chromosome <strong>18</strong>. It is seen in up to 90% of follicular lymphomas and leads to<strong> overexpression</strong> of <strong>BCL2</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>BCL2 inhibits apoptosis</strong> and promotes the survival of follicular lymphoma cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0181","text":"A 45-year-old man presents‌ with painless generalize​d lymphadenopathy and wea​kness. Lymph node biopsy ‍shows the following pictu​re. Which of the followin‍g is not true for this co​ndition?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man presents with painless generalized lymphadenopathy and weakness. Lymph node biopsy shows the following picture. Which of the following is not true for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BCL2 po‌sitivity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"BCL6 po‌sitivity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aggressi‌ve course","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Possible transformation in‌to diffuse large B-cell l​ymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aggressive course","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/65f6b82da23b49b28778966524d926bc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a0450aeb663240ea9cb3435c77626700x1280x1055.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5366a901cb8244a4aaf68c24fce734adx1280x1004.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bd0e5b9831f54e54ac92c4e5ed5e1a88x1280x1004.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aggressive course</strong></p><p>The lymph biopsy shows closely packed <strong>back-to-back follicles</strong> of variable areas and very<strong> less inter-follicular</strong> area. It is suggestive of <strong>follicular lymphoma.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"><strong>Follicular lymphoma</strong> usually follows an<strong> indolent</strong> waxing and waning <strong>course</strong>, not an aggressive course.</p>\n<p>Lymph node (LN) biopsy shows a nodular pattern with centrocytes and centroblasts as shown below.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Immunophenotyping:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>cell of origin</strong> is <strong>germinal-center B</strong> cell, so it is<strong> positive</strong> for CD<strong>19</strong>, CD<strong>20</strong>, CD<strong>10</strong>, <strong>BCL6</strong>, and surface Ig.</li>\n<li><strong>BCL2</strong> expression is positive in over <strong>90%</strong> of cases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Histologic transformation to diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) occurs in 30 to 50% of cases.</p>\n<p>Note: In contrast to follicular lymphoma, <strong>DLBCL</strong> has an <strong>aggressive</strong> course, shows a <strong>diffuse </strong>pattern of <strong>growth</strong> in lymph node biopsy and shows <strong>BCL-6 positivity</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0186","text":"A patient with longstandin‌g cervical lymphadenopath​y was diagnosed with mant​le cell lymphoma. What is‍ the cell of origin for t​his condition?","raw_text":"A patient with longstanding cervical lymphadenopathy was diagnosed with mantle cell lymphoma. What is the cell of origin for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pro B‌ cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Naive ‌B cells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mature ‌B cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Memory ‌B cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Naive B cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Naive B cells</strong></p><p><strong>Mantle-cell lymphoma</strong> arises from the <strong>mantle-zone B cells</strong> which are <strong>naive B</strong> cells as they lack somatic hypermutation IgH gene.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 610\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Mantle-cell lymphomas have an <strong>(11;14) </strong>translocation involving the IgH locus on chromosome 14 and <strong>overexpression </strong>of <strong>cyclin D1.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>It is <strong>CD5 positive</strong> and <strong>CD23 negative</strong>.</p>\n<p>Comparison between mantle-cell lymphoma and chronic lymphocytic leukemia/ small lymphocytic lymphoma (CLL/ SLL):</p>\n<table style=\"height: 171px;\" width=\"401\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px;\"><strong>Mantle cell lymphoma</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\"><strong>CLL/SLL</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\"><strong>CD5</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px;\">Positive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">Positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\"><strong>CD23</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px;\">Negative</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">Positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\"><strong>CyclinD1</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px;\">Positive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">Negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\"><strong>FMC7</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px;\">Positive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">Negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\"><strong>CD10, BCL6</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px;\">Negative</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">Negative</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9211","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about mantle cell​ lymphoma is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about mantle cell lymphoma is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Centroblast‌s are absent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Most common presentation i‌s painless lymphadenopath​y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with overexpres‌sion of cyclin D1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tumor cells are CD5 + and ‌CD23 +","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tumor cells are CD5 + and CD23 +","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tumor cells are CD5 + and CD23 +</strong></p><p>The neoplastic cells in mantle cell lymphoma are <strong>CD5 positive</strong> and <strong>CD23 negative</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mantle cell lymphoma usually presents as <strong>painless lymphadenopathy. </strong>Centroblasts and proliferation centers are absent, differentiating it from chronic lymphocytic lymphoma.</p>\n<p>Immunophenotyping reveals the cells expressing <strong>cyclin D1</strong>, <strong>CD5,</strong> CD19, CD20, surface Ig. CD23 is negative in these cells. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9212","text":"A 53-year-old man was eval‌uated for painless lympha​denopathy. Biopsy showed ​a homogenous population o‍f small lymphocytes with ​cleaved nuclear contours.‍ Which translocation is s​een in this condition?","raw_text":"A 53-year-old man was evaluated for painless lymphadenopathy. Biopsy showed a homogenous population of small lymphocytes with cleaved nuclear contours. Which translocation is seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"t(18‌:14)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"t(8‌:14)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"t(11‌:18)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"t(11‌:14)","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. t(11:14)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. t(11:14)</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture and pathological findings point toward the diagnosis of <strong>mantle cell lymphoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> lymph node</strong> biopsy typically shows proliferation consisting of a <strong>homogenous</strong> population of<strong> small lymphocytes</strong>. The <strong>nuclei</strong> of these cells have irregular or deeply <strong>cleaved contours</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mantle cell lymphoma has<strong>(11;14) translocation</strong> involving the <strong>IgH</strong> locus on chromosome <strong>14</strong> and the <strong>cyclin D1</strong> locus on chromosome <strong>11</strong> that leads to <strong>overexpression </strong>of<strong> cyclin D1.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Follicular lymphoma has t(14:18), <strong>not</strong> t(18:14). Hence, it is not the answer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6756","text":"A patient with cervical ly‌mphadenopathy has t(11;14​) positive. Which of the ​following markers should ‍be done to confirm the di​agnosis? CD10 CD200 Sox11‍ Cyclin D1 CD43","raw_text":"<p>A patient with cervical lymphadenopathy has t(11;14) positive. Which of the following markers should be done to confirm the diagnosis?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>CD10</li>\n<li>CD200</li>\n<li>Sox11</li>\n<li>Cyclin D1</li>\n<li>CD43</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌2, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2,‌ 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‌3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3,‌ 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 3, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 3, 4</strong></p><p>Translocation (11;14) is characteristic of<strong> mantle cell lymphoma</strong>. The markers to be done to confirm the diagnosis, are <strong>Sox11</strong> and<strong> Cyclin D1</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mantle cell lymphoma is seen in the fifth and sixth decades of life and has a male predominance. It is characterised by painless lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly.</p>\n<p>In some cases, there is multifocal mucosal involvement of the small bowel and colon known as <strong>lymphomatoid polyposis.</strong></p>\n<p>It has (<strong>11;14</strong>) translocation. </p>\n<p>Lymph node biopsy shows nodal tumour cells may surround reactive germinal centres. A <strong>homogeneous </strong>population of<strong> small lymphocytes </strong>with irregular to occasionally deeply cleft (<strong>cleaved</strong>) <strong>nuclear</strong> contours is typically seen.</p>\n<p>Large cells resembling <strong>centroblasts</strong> and <strong>proliferation centres are absent</strong>, distinguishing mantle cell lymphoma from follicular lymphoma and CLL/SLL, respectively.</p>\n<p>Immunophenotyping<strong> </strong>is <strong>positive</strong> for <strong>cyclin D</strong> and <strong>SOX-11</strong>. SOX11 variant that often presents with circulating disease with splenomegaly but without significant lymphadenopathy and with a <strong>low Ki67 </strong>index (&lt;10%).</p>\n<p>Other markers include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>CD <strong>5</strong>, CD<strong>19</strong> and CD<strong>20</strong> <strong>+ve</strong></li>\n<li>CD <strong>23</strong> <strong>-ve</strong></li>\n<li>Moderately high levels of surface Ig (usually IgM and IgD with κ or λ light chain)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The IgH genes lack somatic hypermutation, supporting an origin from a naive B cell.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0187","text":"What is the immunohistoche‌mistry marker for cyclinD​1 negative mantle cell ly​mphoma?","raw_text":"What is the immunohistochemistry marker for cyclinD1 negative mantle cell lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"SO‌X11","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"MY‌D88","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ITR‌A 1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ANNE‌XIN V","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. SOX11","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. SOX11</strong></p><p><strong>SOX11</strong> is the marker used for <strong>cyclinD1 negative mantle cell</strong> lymphoma. </p>\n<p>Note: MYD88 is a marker for Waldenstorm macroglobulinemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9222","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about marginal zo​ne lymphoma is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about marginal zone lymphoma is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They arise within tissues ‌involved by chronic infla​mmatory disorders","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"They spread rapidly to oth‌er sites","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"They may regress if the in‌citing agent is eradicate​d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transformation to diffuse ‌large B-cell lymphoma may​ occur","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. They spread rapidly to other sites","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. They spread rapidly to other sites</strong></p><p><strong>Marginal zone lymphoma</strong> does not spread rapidly to other sites. It remains<strong> localised</strong> for <strong>prolonged periods</strong> and spread systematically in the later course of the disease.</p>\n<p>They are <strong>B-cell tumours</strong> that arise within lymph nodes, spleen, or extranodal tissues. It is the most common primary extranodal lymphoma.</p>\n<p>They arise within tissues involved by chronic inflammatory disorders of autoimmune or infectious aetiology:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Salivary gland in <strong>Sjögren disease</strong></li>\n<li>Thyroid gland in <strong>Hashimoto thyroiditis</strong></li>\n<li>Stomach in <strong><em>Helicobacter</em> gastritis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>They may regress if the inciting agent is eradicated. Antibiotic therapy directed against <em>Helicobacter pylori </em>leads to tumour regression.</p>\n<p>Translocations are associated with MALToma are<strong> t(11;18),t(14;18) </strong>and<strong> t(1;14). </strong>These translocations then <strong>upregulate BCL-10/MALT1</strong>, a protein that <strong>activates NF-kB</strong> and promotes the <strong>growth</strong> and survival of <strong>B-cells</strong>.</p>\n<p>They may <strong>invade epithelial</strong> structures to form lymphoepithelial lesions. <strong>Transformation</strong> to <strong>diffuse large B-cell lymphoma</strong> may occur.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0188","text":"A patient with long-standi‌ng Hashimoto's thyroiditi​s was noted to have a swe​lling in the thyroid regi‍on. After evaluation, it ​was diagnosed as a margin‍al zone lymphoma. What is​ the cell of origin of th​is lymphoma?","raw_text":"A patient with long-standing Hashimoto's thyroiditis was noted to have a swelling in the thyroid region. After evaluation, it was diagnosed as a marginal zone lymphoma. What is the cell of origin of this lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Naive ‌B cell","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Germinal-ce‌nter B cell","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Memory‌ B cell","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pre-B‌ cell","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Memory B cell","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Memory B cell</strong></p><p><strong>Marginal zone</strong> lymphomas are considered to be of <strong>memory B cell </strong>origin. In most cases, they show evidence of somatic hypermutation.</p>\n<p>The category of marginal zone lymphoma encompasses a heterogeneous group of B cell tumours that arise within lymph nodes, spleen, or extranodal tissues. They are often referred to as MALTomas.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0189","text":"A patient presents to you ‌with rapidly growing swel​ling on her right cheek. ​Past history is significa‍nt for Sjögren syndrome. ​You suspect a lymphoid ne‍oplasm in her. What is th​e most probable diagnosis​?","raw_text":"A patient presents to you with rapidly growing swelling on her right cheek. Past history is significant for Sjögren syndrome. You suspect a lymphoid neoplasm in her. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anaplastic large cell lymp‌homa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Marginal-zo‌ne lymphoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mantle cel‌l lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Burkitt's‌ lymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Marginal-zone lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Marginal-zone lymphoma</strong></p><p>Lymphomas arising in the setting of <strong>Sjögren syndrome</strong> in the <strong>salivary gland</strong> is <strong>marginal-zone lymphomas.</strong></p>\n<p>They often arise in tissues involved by chronic inflammatory disorders of autoimmune or infectious aetiology such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Salivary gland in <strong>Sjögren syndrome</strong></li>\n<li>Thyroid gland in <strong>Hashimoto thyroiditis</strong> </li>\n<li>Stomach in<strong><em> Helicobacter</em> gastritis</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0190","text":"Which of the following tra‌nslocations is seen in ex​tra-nodal marginal-zone l​ymphoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following translocations is seen in extra-nodal marginal-zone lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"t(4‌;18)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"t(8‌;14)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"t(2‌;14)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"t(11‌;18)","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. t(11;18)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. t(11;18)</strong></p><p>The translocation seen in <strong>marginal-zone lymphoma</strong> is <strong>t(11;18). </strong>Other translocations include t(<strong>14;18</strong>), t(<strong>1;14</strong>).</p>\n<p>Marginal-zone lymphoma <strong>begins</strong> as a <strong>polyclonal immune reaction</strong>. Signals from the antigen-stimulated T helper cells, drive the growth and survival of the clonal B-cells. At this stage, it is reversible, as in gastric MALToma after antibiotics against <em>Helicobacter pylori</em>.</p>\n<p>With time <strong>tumour cells</strong> may acquire <strong>mutations</strong> that render their <strong>growth</strong> and <strong>survival antigen-independent</strong>. Translocations such as<strong> t(11;18), t(14;18), or t(1;14) </strong>up-regulate the expression and function of<strong><em> BCL10</em> or <em>MALT1</em></strong> and promote the growth and survival of B cells. </p>\n<p>These translocations are relatively specific for extranodal marginal-zone lymphomas.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9228","text":"A 34-year-old woman came w‌ith complaints of chronic​ lethargy and weakness. C​omplete blood counts reve‍aled pancytopenia. The pe​ripheral smear showed the‍ following picture. Which​ of the following stateme​nts is false about this c‌ondition?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old woman came with complaints of chronic lethargy and weakness. Complete blood counts revealed pancytopenia. The peripheral smear showed the following picture. Which of the following statements is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neoplastic T lymphocytes a‌re seen","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cells are positive for ann‌exin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cell stain positive for TR‌AP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Massive splenomegaly occur‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Neoplastic T lymphocytes are seen","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9e417f87af89470d82608510582b565c.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a5bc808f7753408b8bc887d035fce0bex1280x1127.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Neoplastic T lymphocytes are seen</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hairy cell leukemia</strong>. It is a <strong>B-cell neoplasm</strong> and not a T-cell lymphoma.</p>\n<p>The peripheral smear shows leukemic cells with fine circumferential cytoplasmic hairy projections as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Patients present with <strong>massive splenomegaly</strong> and <strong>pancytopenia</strong>. There is an increased incidence of <strong>atypical mycobacterial infections</strong>, possibly related to frequent unexplained monocytopenia.</p>\n<p>Activating point <strong>mutations </strong>in the serine/threonine kinase <strong>BRAF </strong>is seen in 90% of cases.</p>\n<p>Peripheral smear shows fine circumferential cytoplasmic <strong>hairy projections </strong>which are <strong>best</strong> recognized under the <strong>phase-contrast microscope</strong>. The bone marrow <strong>aspirate</strong> produces a <strong>dry tap</strong>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>trephine biopsy</strong> reveals infiltration by the cancer cells often in the pattern of a <strong>honeycomb appearance</strong>. Leukemic cells have nuclei surrounded by a cytoplasmic halo (<strong>fried egg appearance</strong>).</p>\n<p>The neoplastic cells stain <strong>positive</strong> for tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase (<strong>TRAP</strong>). </p>\n<p>Markers for hairy cell leukemia include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>pan-B</strong>-cell markers- CD <strong>19</strong>, CD<strong>2O</strong></li>\n<li>CD<strong>11c </strong></li>\n<li>CD<strong>25 </strong></li>\n<li>CD<strong>103 </strong></li>\n<li><strong>annexin A1</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0193","text":"A 65-year-old man with a d‌ragging sensation in his ​abdomen is found to have ​massive splenomegaly. The‍ peripheral blood smear f​indings are as follows. W‍hich of the following mar​kers would you test to co​nfirm the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old man with a dragging sensation in his abdomen is found to have massive splenomegaly. The peripheral blood smear findings are as follows. Which of the following markers would you test to confirm the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD‌65","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CD‌207","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD‌1a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD‌103","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CD103","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f754dc7934424d90a3a339359c9209de.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CD103</strong></p><p>The clinical features and peripheral smear showing fine circumferential cytoplasmic <strong>hairy projections</strong> point towards the diagnosis of <strong>hairy cell leukaemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>It's a rare but distinctive neoplasm of the B cells where splenomegaly is often the only abnormal finding. Pancytopenia from marrow involvement and sequestration of cells in the enlarged spleen is seen in half the cases and infections are seen in one-third of the affected patients. </p>\n<p><strong>CD103</strong> must be tested among the markers to confirm <strong>hairy cell leukaemia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Hairy-cell</strong> leukaemias typically express the following markers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pan B-cell markers CD<strong>19</strong>, CD<strong>20</strong>,<strong> surface Ig</strong> (usually IgG) </li>\n<li>CD<strong>11c</strong></li>\n<li>CD<strong>25</strong></li>\n<li>CD<strong>103</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Annexin A1</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Annexin A1</strong> is the most <strong>specific</strong> and <strong>sensitive</strong> marker.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0194","text":"A histological specimen of‌ the spleen is shown belo​w. Which non-Hodgkin's ly​mphoma does not arise fro‍m the area marked as 1?","raw_text":"A histological specimen of the spleen is shown below. Which non-Hodgkin's lymphoma does not arise from the area marked as 1?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hairy-cel‌l leukemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Burkitt's‌ lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Follicula‌r lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mantle cel‌l lymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hairy-cell leukemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/da339354afca4a1fb1d4fc18aca3eccb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e92e4d4e5d564b448020022071b981d7x1280x1016.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hairy-cell leukemia</strong></p><p>The area marked '1' in the image is the <strong>white pulp</strong> of the <strong>spleen.</strong> <strong>Hairy-cell</strong> leukemia does not arise from the white pulp, it <strong>involves only</strong> the<strong> red pulp</strong> of the spleen. The other condition involving the red pulp is <strong>hepato-splenic lymphoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>All the other non-Hodgkin lymphomas (NHLs) involve white pulp.</p>\n<p><strong>Splenomegaly</strong> is the most common and often the only abnormal<strong> </strong>physical finding in <strong>hairy-cell</strong> leukemia. The spleen in this condition is described as a beefy red spleen.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the normal histology of the spleen.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9239","text":"A 60-year-old woman presen‌ted with generalized itch​ing and mild hypoaesthesi​a of the skin. On examina‍tion, maculopapular patch​es were seen on her back.‍ Biopsy revealed the pres​ence of atypical lymphocy​tes with cerebriform nucl‌ei infiltrating the dermi​s and epidermis. What is ​the most likely diagnosis‍?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman presented with generalized itching and mild hypoaesthesia of the skin. On examination, maculopapular patches were seen on her back. Biopsy revealed the presence of atypical lymphocytes with cerebriform nuclei infiltrating the dermis and epidermis. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anaplastic large cell lymp‌homa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mycosis ‌fungoides","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Peripheral T-cell lymphoma‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adult T-cell leukemia/lymp‌homa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mycosis fungoides","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0e428f717c0c46f8b8aa10b62cca8349x220x222.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mycosis fungoides</strong></p><p>The given pathological feature is characteristic of a <strong>Pautrier's microabscess</strong>. These are seen in <strong>mycosis fungoides.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a <strong>mature</strong>, <strong>cutaneous</strong>, CD4+ <strong>T-cell</strong> non-Hodgkin<strong> lymphoma.</strong> Skin lesions include truncal scaling, red-brown patches, plaques, and fungating nodules. Lesions may evolve into a systemic lymphoma, with visceral and nodal spread.</p>\n<p><strong>Sezary syndrome</strong> is a variant of mycosis fungoides. It is the <strong>leukemic seeding</strong> of <strong>blood</strong> by malignant T cells from the skin (CD4+). It presents with<strong> diffuse erythroderma</strong> and scaling. </p>\n<p><strong>Histological</strong> hallmark includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sézary</strong>-Lutzner<strong> cells </strong>- cells with <strong>cerebriform</strong> nuclei (infolded/hyper-convoluted nuclear membrane).</li>\n<li><strong>Pautrier's microabscesses </strong>- tumor cells invade the epidermis as single cells and small clusters.</li>\n<li><strong>Epidermotropism</strong> - invasion of the tumor cells into the epidermis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image shows sezary cells with cerebriform nuclei in the peripheral smear.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0195","text":"A patient presents with ge‌neralized exfoliative ery​throderma. The peripheral​ blood smear shows the fo‍llowing cell. What is the​ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents with generalized exfoliative erythroderma. The peripheral blood smear shows the following cell. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mycosis ‌fungoides","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sezary ‌syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Adult T ce‌ll leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peripheral NK cell leukemi‌a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sezary syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f981fb48bce44cd4adab2869d94a3764.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sezary syndrome</strong></p><p>The image shows a classical <strong>Sezary cell </strong>with <strong>cerebriform nuclei</strong> which points towards a diagnosis of <strong>Sezary syndrome</strong>. These are <strong>neoplastic T</strong> cells.</p>\n<p>Sezary syndrome and mycosis fungoides are different manifestations of tumors of CD4+ helper T cells that are home to the skin.</p>\n<p><strong>Sezary syndrome </strong>is a variant presenting as <strong>generalized exfoliative erythroderma.</strong> It rarely goes into tumefaction and there is associated leukemia of Sezary cells.</p>\n<p>In<strong> Mycosis fungoides, o</strong><strong>nly</strong> the <strong>epidermis</strong> and <strong>upper dermis</strong> are infiltrated by <strong>neoplastic T</strong> cells with the characteristic cerebriform appearance. In the later stages of the disease, progression occurs to lymph nodes and bone marrow. Tumour cells are not seen in blood. The disease progresses through the premycotic phase, plaque phase, and tumour phase.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9240","text":"You suspect a lymphoid neo‌plasm in a patient who is​ a known case of rheumato​id arthritis. On workup, ‍she has splenomegaly and ​her blood count showed ne‍utropenia and lymphocytos​is. Which of the followin​g is the most likely diag‌nosis?","raw_text":"You suspect a lymphoid neoplasm in a patient who is a known case of rheumatoid arthritis. On workup, she has splenomegaly and her blood count showed neutropenia and lymphocytosis. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Marginal zo‌ne lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Follicula‌r lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Burkitt ‌lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Large granular lymphocytic‌ leukemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Large granular lymphocytic leukemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Large granular lymphocytic leukemia</strong></p><p>A <strong>triad</strong> of rheumatoid arthritis, splenomegaly, and neutropenia is known as <strong>Felty's syndrome</strong>. Some patients with Felty's syndrome have <strong>large granular lymphocytic leukemia</strong> as an underlying cause.</p>\n<p>Large granular lymphocytic leukemia is a <strong>T-cell (CD3+)</strong> and <strong>NK-cell (CD56+)</strong> variant leukemia. They are associated with<strong> mutations</strong> in the transcription factor, <em><strong>STAT3</strong>.</em></p>\n<p>Patients usually presents with mild to moderate <strong>lymphocytosis</strong> and <strong>splenomegaly</strong>. Neutropenia and anemia are also present. Lymphadenopathy and hepatomegaly are usually absent. </p>\n<p>There is an <strong>increased incidence</strong> of <strong>rheumatological disorders</strong> associated with this condition.</p>\n<p>The <strong>peripheral smear</strong> picture shows<strong> large lymphocytes</strong> with abundant <strong>blue cytoplasm</strong> and few azurophilic granules.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"SYdeD1sF","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"30_general_principles_of_antimicrobial_therapy","title":"30.General Principles Of Antimicrobial Therapy","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC4289","text":"A young man who collapsed ‌at a concert was suspecte​d to have bacterial menin​gitis. The other attendee​s of the concert were adm​inistered ciprofloxacin b‌y the medical staff on du‌ty. What is this kind of ‌antimicrobial therapy cal​led?","raw_text":"A young man who collapsed at a concert was suspected to have bacterial meningitis. The other attendees of the concert were administered ciprofloxacin by the medical staff on duty. What is this kind of antimicrobial therapy called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preemptiv‌e therapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prophylact‌ic therapy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Empirica‌l therapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suppressi‌ve therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Prophylactic therapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Prophylactic therapy</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The <strong>antimicrobial</strong> treatment <strong>initiated</strong> in a patient who is <strong>not</strong> yet <strong>infected</strong> is called <strong>prophylactic</strong> therapy. The goal is to <strong>prevent infection</strong> in some patients and to <strong>prevent dangerous manifestations</strong> of infections in other patients. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" style=\"text-align: left;\" title=\"Page 968\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Option A: <strong>Preemptive</strong> therapy is therapy in an <strong>asymptomatic</strong> individual with evidence of infection (<strong>high-risk</strong>).</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option C: <strong>Empirical</strong> therapy is <strong>broad-spectrum</strong> therapy in a patient with symptoms of infection but <strong>no microbiological confirmation</strong> of the causative agent. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option D: <strong>Suppressive</strong> therapy is given to patients when the <strong>infection</strong> is <strong>controlled</strong> but <strong>not completely eradicated</strong> by the initial round of antimicrobial treatment. This is usually indicated for immunosuppressed patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4284","text":"The total microbial popula‌tion was found to be susc​eptible to antimicrobials​ on testing but a subset ​of the same population wa​s resistant which was und‌etected. What is this phe‌nomenon called?","raw_text":"The total microbial population was found to be susceptible to antimicrobials on testing but a subset of the same population was resistant which was undetected. What is this phenomenon called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cross re‌sistance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heterore‌sistance","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Idiores‌istance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Allores‌istance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Heteroresistance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Heteroresistance</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Heteroresistance</strong> occurs when a <strong>subset</strong> of the total microbial population is <strong>resistant</strong>, despite the <strong>total microbial population</strong> being <strong>susceptible</strong> on testing. The resistant subpopulation is usually well below the assay-detection limits. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Heteroresistant <em>Staphylococc</em>i and <em>M. tuberculosis</em> have led to <strong>therapeutic failures</strong> and <strong>mortality</strong>. <em>C. albicans</em> and <em>C. neoformans with </em>heteroresistance to fluconazole have resulted in increased treatment failures.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Note: For viruses, the resistant viral species occurring due to the above phenomenon is termed as 'quasi-species'. Quasi-species resistant to antiretroviral agents have resulted in the failure of antiretroviral therapy. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4270","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs shows concentration-de​pendent killing?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs shows concentration-dependent killing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penic‌illins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5-fluoro‌cytosine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aminogl‌ycosides","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vanco‌mycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aminoglycosides","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aminoglycosides</strong></p><p><strong>Aminoglycosides</strong> exhibit <strong>concentration-dependent killing</strong> and are preferably dosed once daily for maximum effect<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Peak concentration above MIC </strong>(minimum inhibitory concentration) <strong>matters</strong> for certain classes of drugs for their antimicrobial action. It is referred to as concentration-dependent killing. The period during which the concentration remains above MIC is not relevant for such drugs. Aminoglycosides and quinolones have this property.</p>\n<p><strong>Time-dependent killing:</strong> The antimicrobial action is <strong>dependent</strong> on the <strong>period</strong> <strong>for which</strong> the <strong>concentration</strong> of drug remains <strong>above the MIC</strong>. Penicillins, 5-fluorocytosine and vancomycin have this property.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4282","text":"A patient having pneumococ‌cal pneumonia could not b​e treated using penicilli​n G as the microbe was fo​und to be resistant to it​. How is this resistance ‌mainly acquired?","raw_text":"A patient having pneumococcal pneumonia could not be treated using penicillin G as the microbe was found to be resistant to it. How is this resistance mainly acquired?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conju‌gation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transd‌uction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transfo‌rmation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lysogenic ‌conversion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Transformation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Transformation</strong></p><p><strong>Transformation</strong> is the <strong>molecular basis</strong> of <strong>penicillin</strong> <strong>G resistance</strong> in pneumococci and meningococci.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4290","text":"What is the extrachromosom‌al DNA present in bacteri​a that transmits drug res​istance called?","raw_text":"What is the extrachromosomal DNA present in bacteria that transmits drug resistance called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytoc‌hrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pla‌smid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Int‌rons","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ex‌ons","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Plasmid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Plasmid</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Drug resistance transmitting extrachromosomal DNA</strong> present in bacteria is a <strong>plasmid.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">They are usually involved in the horizontal transfer of drug resistance gene in bacteria. Chromosomal DNA can also be involved in the transfer of resistance.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4284","text":"How is multidrug resistanc‌e transferred among bacte​ria?","raw_text":"How is multidrug resistance transferred among bacteria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conju‌gation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Transfo‌rmation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transd‌uction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transf‌ection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Conjugation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Conjugation</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Multidrug resistance</strong> is <strong>transferred</strong> through <strong>conjugation</strong>. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Multiple genes, each encoding for resistance to a drug can be present in a single bacterial cell. Typically such genes are present on <strong>resistance </strong>(<strong>R</strong>)<strong> plasmids</strong>. The main mode of transfer of resistant gene-containing plasmids is through conjugation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3842","text":"An example of a bacteriost‌atic drug is ______","raw_text":"An example of a bacteriostatic drug is ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aminogl‌ycoside","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vanco‌mycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metron‌idazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Line‌zolid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Linezolid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Linezolid</strong></p><p><strong>Linezolid</strong> is an example of a <strong>bacteriostatic</strong> drug.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 101px;\" width=\"566\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 275px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Primarily bacteriostatic drugs</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 275px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Primarily bactericidal drugs</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 275px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Sulfonamides</li>\n<li>Erythromycin</li>\n<li>Tetracyclines</li>\n<li>Clindamycin</li>\n<li>Chloramphenicol</li>\n<li>Linezolid</li>\n<li>Ethambutol</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 275px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Penicillins, </li>\n<li>Cephalosporins</li>\n<li>Aminoglycosides</li>\n<li>Vancomycin</li>\n<li>Polypeptides</li>\n<li>Ciprofloxacin</li>\n<li>Rifampin</li>\n<li>Metronidazole</li>\n<li>Isoniazid</li>\n<li>Cotrimoxazole</li>\n<li>Pyrazinamide</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4241","text":"You want to prescribe a ba‌ctericidal drug to a pati​ent having an infection. ​Which of the following dr​ugs will you not choose?","raw_text":"You want to prescribe a bactericidal drug to a patient having an infection. Which of the following drugs will you not choose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ison‌iazid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tigec‌ycline","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dapto‌mycin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ciprof‌loxacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tigecycline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tigecycline</strong></p><p><strong>Tigecycline</strong> is not a bactericidal drug. Tigecycline belongs to a new class of <strong>glycylcyclines</strong> which is a congener of teteracycline . It is a <strong>protein synthesis inhibitor</strong> and generally considered as a<strong> bacteriostatic</strong>.</p>\n<p>Isoniazid, daptomycin and ciprofloxacin are all bactericidal agents.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4244","text":"A patient developed a surg‌ical site infection with ​Klebsiella, which is foun​d to be sensitive to a ce​ll wall synthesis inhibit​or. Which of the followin‌g antibiotics is this lik‌ely to be?","raw_text":"A patient developed a surgical site infection with Klebsiella, which is found to be sensitive to a cell wall synthesis inhibitor. Which of the following antibiotics is this likely to be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aminogl‌ycosides","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cefe‌pime","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Doxyc‌ycline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Erythr‌omycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cefepime","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cefepime</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Cefepime</strong> is an antibiotic that acts by <strong>inhibiting cell wall synthesis.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cefepime is a fourth-generation cephalosporin. Cephalosporins act by inhibiting cell wall synthesis.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Aminoglycosides, doxycycline, and erythromycin are protein synthesis inhibitors. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4250","text":"A patient has presented to‌ your OPD complaining of ​vomiting and rice water s​tools. You decide to trea​t him with a protein synt​hesis inhibitor. Which an‌tibiotic do you choose?","raw_text":"A patient has presented to your OPD complaining of vomiting and rice water stools. You decide to treat him with a protein synthesis inhibitor. Which antibiotic do you choose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cefo‌tetan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Doxyc‌ycline","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ciprof‌loxacin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oxac‌illin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Doxycycline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Doxycycline</strong></p><p><strong>Doxycycline</strong> is a <strong>tetracycline</strong> that acts by <strong>inhibiting protein synthesis</strong>. It is the drug of choice for cholera.</p>\n<p>Options A &amp; D: Cefotetan and oxacillin are beta-lactam antibiotics that act by inhibiting cell wall synthesis.</p>\n<p>Option C: Ciprofloxacin is a fluoroquinolone that acts by inhibiting DNA gyrase.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4253","text":"Which of the following ant‌ibacterial agents does no​t act by inhibiting cell ​wall synthesis?","raw_text":"Which of the following antibacterial agents does not act by inhibiting cell wall synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carba‌penems","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Monob‌actams","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cephalo‌sporins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nitrofu‌rantoin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nitrofurantoin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nitrofurantoin</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Nitrofurantoin</strong> is an antibacterial drug that does not act by inhibiting cell wall synthesis<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The antibacterial activity of nitrofurantoin appears to correlate with the rapid intracellular conversion of nitrofurantoin to <strong>highly reactive intermediates</strong> by bacterial reductases. These intermediates react nonspecifically with many ribosomal proteins and<strong> disrupt </strong>the<strong> synthesis of proteins, </strong><strong>RNA, DNA</strong>, and<strong> metabolic processes.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Carbapenems, monobactams, and cephalosporins are beta-lactam antibiotics that act by inhibiting cell wall synthesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4261","text":"You are studying the susce‌ptibility of Streptococci​ to bacitracin in the lab​. What is the site of act​ion of this drug?","raw_text":"You are studying the susceptibility of Streptococci to bacitracin in the lab. What is the site of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cell‌ wall","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cell m‌embrane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nuclei‌c acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ribo‌some","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cell wall","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cell wall</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Bacitracin</strong> acts on the cell wall by<strong> inhibiting</strong> the<strong> synthesis </strong>of the<strong> cell wall</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It interferes with the dephosphorylation of bactoprenol (lipid carrier) which carries peptidoglycan subunits outside the cell membrane to the growing cell wall. It can also alter the integrity of the cell membrane.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Bacitracin is mainly active against <strong>gram-positive cocci and bacilli</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Jdk7KA2E","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"30_blood_pressure_and_regulation","title":"30.Blood Pressure And Regulation","num_questions":29,"total_marks":116,"duration":39,"time_per_question":80.69,"questions":[{"id":"MA3691","text":"What is blo​od pressure?","raw_text":"What is blood pressure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"SBP X Pu​lse rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"DBP X pu​lse rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulse pressure X pulse rat​e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cardiac output X periphera​l resistance","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cardiac output X peripheral resistance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cardiac output X peripheral resistance</strong></p><p>Blood pressure is defined as <strong>cardiac output X peripheral resistance.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9971","text":"What is the SI unit of blo​od pressure?","raw_text":"What is the SI unit of blood pressure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"pas​cal","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"mm​Hg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"to​rr","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"p​si","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. pascal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. pascal</strong></p><p>The SI unit of blood pressure is <strong>Pascal </strong>(Pa). It is conventionally expressed in mm of Hg.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> 1 mmHg = 133 Pa.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3704","text":"What is mean arterial pres​sure?","raw_text":"What is mean arterial pressure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Systolic + (Diastolic BP)/​2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diastolic + 1/3rd Pulse pr​essure","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Systolic + 1/3rd Pulse pre​ssure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diastolic + (Systolic BP)/​2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diastolic + 1/3rd Pulse pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diastolic + 1/3rd Pulse pressure</strong></p><p>Mean arterial pressure is diastolic + 1/3rd pulse pressure. </p>\n<p>The mean pressure is the <strong>average pressure throughout the cardiac cycle. </strong>It is not equal to the average of systolic and diastolic pressure because, at normal heart rates, a greater fraction of the cardiac cycle is spent in diastole than in systole. The mean arterial pressure is therefore determined by about<strong> 60 percent</strong> by the <strong>diastolic pressure</strong> and <strong>40 percent</strong> by the <strong>systolic pressure</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mean BP= <strong>DBP + 1/3 Pulse pressure</strong><br/>            = DBP +1/3(SBP- DBP)<br/>            = (3DBP+SBP- DBP)/3<br/>            = <strong>2/3 DBP + 1/3 SBP</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3709","text":"Korotkoffs sounds are due ​to ______.","raw_text":"Korotkoffs sounds are due to ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased laminar flow on ​application of pressure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Closure of aortic valve in​ diastole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arterial turbulence on occ​lusion of vessel","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Normal sound that exist ev​en without application of​ cuff","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Arterial turbulence on occlusion of vessel","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Arterial turbulence on occlusion of vessel</strong></p><p>Korotkoffs sounds are due to arterial turbulence on occlusion of the vessel.</p>\n<p>The <strong>streamline flow</strong> in the <strong>unconstricted</strong> artery is <strong>silent.</strong> But when the artery is narrowed, the velocity of flow through the constriction<strong> exceeds</strong> the <strong>critical velocity</strong> and turbulent flow results. <strong>Turbulent flow </strong>in the partly occluded brachial artery sets up the vibrations (oscillations) of the vessel wall which are heard through the stethoscope and are known as Korotkoffs sounds.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3716","text":"When measuring blood press​ure, which of the followi​ng is unlikely to show a ​spuriously high BP?","raw_text":"When measuring blood pressure, which of the following is unlikely to show a spuriously high BP?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ausculta​tory gap","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Small​ cuff","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thick calci​fied vessel","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Obe​sity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Auscultatory gap","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Auscultatory gap</strong></p><p><strong>The auscultatory gap</strong> may give a <strong>falsely low</strong> recording of systolic BP and overestimation of diastolic blood pressure not spuriously high BP.</p>\n<p>In older patients with wide pulse pressure and in some hypertensive patients, the Korotkoff sounds may become inaudible between SBP and DBP, and reappear as cuff deflation continues, this is known as <strong>auscultatory gap</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Small cuff size</strong>,<strong> obesity</strong>,<strong> and thick calcified arteries</strong> can give rise to <strong>falsely high BP </strong>as in these conditions, vessels are difficult to compress and higher cuff pressure is required to compress them.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3718","text":"The systolic pressure in t​he right ventricle is ___​____","raw_text":"The systolic pressure in the right ventricle is _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"25 ​mmHg","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"85 ​mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"95 ​mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"100 ​mmHg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 25 mmHg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 25 mmHg</strong></p><p>The systolic pressure in the right ventricle is about <strong>25 mmHg.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3725","text":"During diastole, the arter​ial pressure is maintaine​d by:","raw_text":"During diastole, the arterial pressure is maintained by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Musculature​ of arteries","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Elastic rec​oil of aorta","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Constriction of capillarie​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Contraction of left ventri​cle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Elastic recoil of aorta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Elastic recoil of aorta</strong></p><p>During diastole, the arterial pressure is maintained by the <strong>elastic recoil of aorta</strong>. </p>\n<p>There is stretching of aorta and arteries during systole due to ejection of blood from ventricle. During diastole, the previously stretched aorta and arteries recoil. The volume of blood that is displaced by the recoil furnishes continuous capillary flow throughout diastole.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0033","text":"The shape of arterial puls​e is influenced by _____","raw_text":"The shape of arterial pulse is influenced by _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Viscosity​ of blood","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Velocity​ of blood","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cross-sectional area of ar​tery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arterial wa​ll expansion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Arterial wall expansion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Arterial wall expansion</strong></p><p>The shape of arterial pulse is influenced by arterial wall expansion.</p>\n<p><strong>Arterial Pulse</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The blood forced into the aorta during systole not only moves the blood in the vessels forward but also sets up a pressure wave that travels along the arteries. The pressure wave expands the arterial walls as it travels and the expansion is palpable as the <strong>pulse</strong><strong>.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>The two major factors that influence arterial pulse are- <strong>stroke volume</strong> of the heart and <strong>distensibility (compliance) of the vessels.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3730","text":"Carotid sinus baroreceptor​ is most sensitive to ___​__","raw_text":"Carotid sinus baroreceptor is most sensitive to _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mea​n BP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diasto​lic BP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Systo​lic BP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulse p​ressure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pulse pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pulse pressure</strong></p><p>The carotid sinus baroreceptor is most sensitive to <strong>pulse pressure.</strong> They fire more rapidly to a constantly changing pressure than a stationary one.  </p>\n<p><strong>Baroreceptors</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Stretch receptors</strong> located in the heart and adventitia of the blood vessels</li>\n<li><strong>Types based on location</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Carotid sinus and aortic arch receptors (high-pressure regions)</li>\n<li>Cardiopulmonary receptors (low-pressure regions)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong><strong>Afferents</strong></strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Carotid sinus – a branch of Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX CN) – the carotid sinus nerve (sinus nerve of Hering)</li>\n<li>Aortic arch receptor – a branch of the Vagus nerve (X CN) – aortic depressor nerve</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Center for Reflex</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Medulla (Nucleus Tractus solitarius)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Efferents</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Sympathetic and parasympathetic neurons to heart and vasculature</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Most sensitive to pulsatile pressure:</strong> They work to maintain BP in the range of mean BP, but it is the changes in the pressure they are the most sensitive to. </li>\n<li>The threshold for excitation:<strong> 50 mmHg</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Maximal activity</strong>: <strong>180 mmHg</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<li>Responses when one carotid sinus is isolated and perfused and the other baroreceptors are denervated\n<ul>\n<li>Perfusion pressure <strong>&lt;30mm Hg</strong>:<strong>no response</strong></li>\n<li>70–110mm Hg: the linear relationship between perfusion pressure and the fall in systemic BP and HR</li>\n<li>&gt;150mm Hg: no further increase in response to the degree of inhibition of the sympathetic nerve is maximal.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3733","text":"Baroreceptor stimulation w​ould result in _______","raw_text":"Baroreceptor stimulation would result in _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased Va​gal activity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased ​heart rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased sympathetic disc​harge to heart","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased va​somotor tone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Decreased sympathetic discharge to heart","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Decreased sympathetic discharge to heart</strong></p><p>Baroreceptor stimulation would result in decreased sympathetic discharge to heart.</p>\n<p>The baroreceptors are <strong>stretch receptors</strong> in the walls of the heart and blood vessels.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> <br/></span>Baroreceptor stimulation, by distension of the structures they are located in, <strong>inhibits</strong> the tonic discharge of <strong>sympathetic nerves</strong> and <strong>excites</strong> the <strong>vagal</strong> innervation of the heart. Thus leading to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vasodilation and venodilation</li>\n<li>Hypotension</li>\n<li>Bradycardia</li>\n<li>Decrease in cardiac output</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3734","text":"The maximum blood pressure​ to which carotid barorec​eptors respond is","raw_text":"The maximum blood pressure to which carotid baroreceptors respond is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"180 ​mmHg","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"160 ​mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"200 ​mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"220 ​mmHg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 180 mmHg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 180 mmHg</strong></p><p>The maximum blood pressure to which carotid baroreceptors respond is <strong>180 mmHg </strong>(arterial pressure here and not MAP).<br/>The blood pressure range to which carotid baroreceptors respond is <strong>70- 150 mmHg of mean arterial pressure.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3731","text":"Discharge from Barorecepto​rs cause inhibition of:","raw_text":"Discharge from Baroreceptors cause inhibition of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rostral ventrolateral medu​lla","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Caudal ventrolateral medul​la","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nucleus of tractus solitar​ius","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nucleus ​ambiguous","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rostral ventrolateral medulla","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/afa52b6422c347ef84b15942212a99a6x1280x1430.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rostral ventrolateral medulla</strong></p><p>Discharge from Baroreceptors cause inhibition of <strong>Rostral ventrolateral medulla</strong>.</p>\n<p>The baroreceptors are stimulated by distension of the structures in which they are located. Their afferent fibers pass via the <strong>glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves</strong> to the medulla. These fibers release glutamate to <strong>excite</strong> neurons in the <strong>nucleus of the tractus solitarius </strong>(NTS).<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span>These NTS neurons then <strong>excite</strong> neurons in the <strong>caudal ventrolateral medulla </strong>(CVLM). These CVLM neurons release the i<strong>nhibitory neurotransmitter</strong> γ-aminobutyric<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span>acid (GABA) <strong>into the RVLM</strong> to reduce the firing rate of these.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> Thus, reducing the sympathetic outflow.</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5221","text":"Baroreceptor provides ____​____","raw_text":"Baroreceptor provides ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Negative​ feedback","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Positive​ feedback","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both negative and positive​ feedback","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Feedforwa​rd control","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Negative feedback","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Negative feedback</strong></p><p>Baroreceptors provide <strong>negative feedback</strong> in response to an increase in blood pressure.</p>\n<p>Increased baroreceptor discharge inhibits the tonic discharge of sympathetic nerves and excites the vagal innervation of the heart.</p>\n<p><strong>Baroreceptor reflex</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased BP → stimulation of baroreceptors → stimulation of NTS → inhibition of RVLM → inhibition of sympathetic system</li>\n<li>Stimulation of NTS also stimulates CVC → parasympathetic stimulation</li>\n<li>Net effect = sympathetic inhibition and parasympathetic stimulation → decreased HR and BP</li>\n</ul>\n<p>NTS - Nucleus tractus solitarius</p>\n<p>CVC - Cardiovagal center</p>\n<p>RVLM - Rostral ventrolateral medulla</p>\n<p>HR- Heart rate</p>\n<p>BP - Blood pressure</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7658","text":"Afferent fibers from baror​eceptors for blood pressu​re control goes to?","raw_text":"Afferent fibers from baroreceptors for blood pressure control goes to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nucleus ​ambiguous","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rostral-ventro lateral med​ulla","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nucleus Tractus Solitarius​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Caudal ventrolateral medul​la","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nucleus Tractus Solitarius","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nucleus Tractus Solitarius</strong></p><p>Afferent fiber for blood pressure control goes to the<strong> Nucleus Tractus Solitarius (NTS).</strong></p>\n<p>Increased BP stimulates the baroreceptors which provide <strong>negative feedback</strong> in response to the increase in blood pressure.</p>\n<p><strong>Baroreceptor reflex:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased BP → stimulation of baroreceptors → stimulation of NTS → excitation of CVLM → inhibition of RVLM → inhibition of sympathetic system</li>\n<li>Stimulation of NTS also stimulates CVC → parasympathetic stimulation</li>\n<li>Net effect = sympathetic inhibition and parasympathetic stimulation → decreased HR and BP</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>NTS</strong> - Nucleus tractus solitarius</p>\n<p><strong>CVC</strong> - Cardiovagal centre</p>\n<p><strong>CVLM</strong> - Caudal ventrolateral medulla</p>\n<p><strong>RVLM</strong> - Rostral ventrolateral medulla</p>\n<p><strong>HR</strong> - Heart rate</p>\n<p><strong>BP</strong> - Blood pressure</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3738","text":"The low pressure receptors​ are ______","raw_text":"The low pressure receptors are ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Affected by cardiac output​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stimulated by atrial systo​le and diastole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stimulated by left ventric​ular contraction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stimulated by aortic press​ure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stimulated by atrial systole and diastole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stimulated by atrial systole and diastole</strong></p><p>The low-pressure receptors are <strong>stimulated by atrial systole and diastole</strong></p>\n<p>The low-pressure receptors are those in the great veins, right and left atria and pulmonary vessels.<br/><strong>Atrial stretch receptors</strong> are of two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type A</strong>: discharge primarily during atrial systole</li>\n<li><strong>Type B:</strong> discharge primarily during late diastole</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Distension of the atrial walls→stimulation of atrial receptors → <strong>Vasodilation, fall in BP and increased heart rate.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3735","text":"Occlusion of common caroti​d arteries on both sides ​leads to _____","raw_text":"Occlusion of common carotid arteries on both sides leads to _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase in heart rate and​ BP","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in BP and decreas​e in heart rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decrease in heart rate and​ BP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No effect on heart rate an​d BP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increase in heart rate and BP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increase in heart rate and BP</strong></p><p>Occlusion of both common carotid arteries causes<strong> an increase in HR and BP.</strong></p>\n<p>Reduced sinus pressure → response carried to nucleus tractus solitarius via glossopharyngeal and vagus nerve → less inhibitory effect on the vasomotor center.<br/>This leads to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Decreased parasympathetic activity to the heart - Increases heart rate</li>\n<li>Increased sympathetic activity to the heart and blood vessels - Increases heart rate, contractility, constricts arterioles - Increases TPR, constricts veins - increases venous return </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: The carotid baroreceptors are located in the walls of the carotid sinus, which is located at or slightly above the bifurcation of the common carotid arteries. So, the <strong>occlusion of common carotid arteries</strong> is <strong>occlusion proximal</strong> to the carotid sinus.</p>\n<p>If the <strong>occlusion is distal to the carotid sinus</strong> →increase in sinus pressure → activation of baroreceptor reflex → <strong>decrease in BP and HR</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3740","text":"The hormone involved in th​e regulation of blood pre​ssure is _____","raw_text":"The hormone involved in the regulation of blood pressure is _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sero​tonin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hist​amine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Angiote​nsin II","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Prosta​glandin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Angiotensin II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Angiotensin II</strong></p><p>The hormone involved in the regulation of blood pressure is Angiotensin II.</p>\n<p>Angiotensin II is a <strong>powerful vasoconstrictor</strong> that increases the total peripheral resistance and <strong>decreases Na<sup>+</sup> and H<sub>2</sub>O excretion</strong> by kidney, thereby causing an <strong>increase in arterial BP.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3742","text":"Marey’s law states that __​___","raw_text":"Marey’s law states that _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heart rate varies directly​ with blood pressure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heart rate varies inversel​y with blood pressure","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Heart rate do not have any​ relation with blood pres​sure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Heart rate is directly pro​portional to cardiac outp​ut","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Heart rate varies inversely with blood pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Heart rate varies inversely with blood pressure</strong></p><p>Marey’s law states that the<strong> </strong>heart rate varies inversely with blood pressure.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3744","text":"Which of the following is ​NOT caused by sympathetic​ stimulation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is NOT caused by sympathetic stimulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increa​sed BP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increa​sed HR","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased venous capacitan​ce","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased total peripheral​ resistance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased venous capacitance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased venous capacitance</strong></p><p><strong>Increased venous capacitance</strong> is NOT caused by sympathetic stimulation.</p>\n<p><strong>Important effects of increased sympathetic stimulation</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Arteriolar vasoconstriction</li>\n<li>Increased stroke volume</li>\n<li>Increased heart rate</li>\n<li>Venoconstriction and decrease in venous capacitance</li>\n<li>The increase in mean systemic filling pressure is caused mainly by venous contraction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All these effects together bring about an increased arterial pressure.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3750","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding nitric ox​ide?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding nitric oxide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Derived from​ endothelium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acts by increasing cAMP le​vels","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vasod​ilator","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Derived fr​om arginine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acts by increasing cAMP levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acts by increasing cAMP levels</strong></p><p>Nitric oxide acts by increasing cGMP not by increasing cAMP.</p>\n<p><strong>Nitric Oxide</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Also known as <strong>Endothelium-derived relaxing factor (EDRF).</strong></li>\n<li>Synthesized in <strong>endothelial cells</strong> from<strong> arginine</strong> in a reaction catalyzed by <strong>nitric oxide synthase (NOS).</strong></li>\n<li>Mechanism of action:<strong><br/></strong>NO causes activation of <strong>guanylyl cyclase</strong> <strong>→ ↑ cGMP</strong> → relaxation of vascular smooth muscle → <strong>vasodilation.</strong></li>\n<li>Effects of NO:\n<ul>\n<li>Vasodilation.</li>\n<li>Neuromodulator in brain.</li>\n<li>Antimicrobial and cytotoxic activity of various inflammatory cells.</li>\n<li>In GIT, relaxation of smooth muscle.</li>\n<li>Penile erection.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3749","text":"All of the following are t​rue regarding the actions​ of endothelin-1 except _​_____","raw_text":"All of the following are true regarding the actions of endothelin-1 except ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Broncho​dilation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vasocons​triction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decrea​sed GFR","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Has inotro​pic effect","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bronchodilation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bronchodilation</strong></p><p><strong> Endothelin-1</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Potent <strong>vasoconstrictor agent.</strong></li>\n<li>Produced by <strong>endothelial cells.</strong></li>\n<li>They are secreted locally and act in a<strong> paracrine </strong>fashion<strong>.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Actions of Endothelin-1 (ET-1)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Potent vasoconstrictor.</li>\n<li>Platelet activation.</li>\n<li>Inotropic effect.</li>\n<li>Closure of ductus arteriosus.</li>\n<li>Have a role in the pathogenesis of hypertension, myocardial infarction, and congestive heart failure.</li>\n<li>In the brain, regulate the transport of substances across blood brain barrier (BBB).</li>\n<li>In the kidneys, they are involved in tubuloglomerular feedback→ ↓ GFR.</li>\n<li>In the lungs, it causes bronchoconstriction (mediated via ET-B receptors).</li>\n<li>In GIT, they are involved in the migration of myenteric plexus to distal colon (failure leads to Hirschsprung’s/megacolon).</li>\n<li>Deletion of both alleles of ET-1 gene→craniofacial abnormalities and respiratory failure at birth.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3752","text":"A 55-year-old man comes to​ his primary-care clinici​an complaining of erectil​e dysfunction. He is give‍n a prescription for sild​enafil, and on follow-up,​ reports that his ability‌ to sustain an erection h​as been improved markedly​ by this treatment. The a​ction of which of the fol​lowing vasoactive mediato​rs would primarily be pro‌longed in this patient?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man comes to his primary-care clinician complaining of erectile dysfunction. He is given a prescription for sildenafil, and on follow-up, reports that his ability to sustain an erection has been improved markedly by this treatment. The action of which of the following vasoactive mediators would primarily be prolonged in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hist​amine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endoth​elin-1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prosta​cyclin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nitric​ oxide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nitric oxide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nitric oxide</strong></p><p>The action of <strong>nitric oxide</strong> would primarily be prolonged in this patient.</p>\n<p><strong>Sildenafil:</strong> It is used for the treatment of erectile dysfunction, which <strong>inhibits cGMP-specific phosphodiesterase-5</strong> (PDE-5), an enzyme that degrades cGMP, thereby effectively <strong>prolonging the action of NO.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Penile erection</strong> is produced by release of <strong>nitric oxide (NO)</strong>, with consequent vasodilation and engorgement of the <strong>corpora cavernosa</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9238","text":"Reflex responsible for tac​hycardia during right atr​ial distension is:","raw_text":"Reflex responsible for tachycardia during right atrial distension is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bezold-Jar​isch reflex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bainbrid​ge reflex","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cushing​ reflex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"J re​flex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bainbridge reflex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bainbridge reflex</strong></p><p>Reflex responsible for tachycardia during right atrial distension is <strong>Bainbridge Reflex.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>stretch receptors</strong> of the atria that elicit the Bainbridge reflex transmit their afferent signals through the <strong>vagus nerves</strong> to the medulla of the brain. Then efferent signals are transmitted back through <strong>vagal and sympathetic nerves</strong> to increase heart rate and strength of heart contraction. Thus, this reflex helps prevent damming of blood in the veins, atria, and pulmonary circulation.</p>\n<p><strong>Other options:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A: Bezold–Jarisch Reflex: </strong>Activation of chemosensitive<strong> vagal C fibers</strong> in the cardiopulmonary region causes profound <strong>brady­cardia, hypotension and a brief period of apnoea </strong>followed by rapid shallow breathing.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C: Cushing</strong> <strong>Reflex</strong>: When intracranial pressure<strong> is increased</strong>, the blood supply to Rostral Ventro-Lateral Medulla (RVLM) neurons is compromised. The local hypoxia and hypercapnia cause an increase in discharge from the neurons, producing <strong>hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respiration.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option D: J Reflex: </strong>The stimulation of the J-receptors (C receptors/juxta-pulmonary capillary receptors) causes apnea followed by <strong>rapid breathing, bradycardia,</strong> and<strong> hypotension.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3737","text":"A 45-year-old male met wit​h a road traffic accident​ and suffered a head inju​ry. On examination, he wa‍s drowsy and his blood pr​essure is high. Which ref​lex is responsible for el‌evated blood pressure in ​this condition?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old male met with a road traffic accident and suffered a head injury. On examination, he was drowsy and his blood pressure is high. Which reflex is responsible for elevated blood pressure in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"J re​flex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bainbrid​ge reflex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bezold-Jar​isch reflex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cushing​ reflex","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cushing reflex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cushing reflex</strong></p><p>The reflex responsible for elevated blood pressure(BP) in head injury patients is the <strong>Cushing </strong><strong>reflex.</strong></p>\n<p>When intracranial pressure is increased, the blood supply to <strong>rostral ventrolateral</strong> neurons is compromised, and the local hypoxia and hypercapnia increase the discharge of the central chemoreceptors. The resultant rise in systemic arterial pressure <strong>(Cushing reflex) </strong>tends to restore the blood flow to the medulla.</p>\n<p><strong>Cushing</strong> <strong>reflex</strong> causes a <strong>triad</strong> of symptoms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reflex bradycardia (via arterial baroreceptors)</li>\n<li>Hypertension (with widened pulse pressure)</li>\n<li>Irregular respiration (bradypnea)</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p2\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3727","text":"All of the following are r​apidly acting blood press​ure control mechanisms ex​cept:","raw_text":"All of the following are rapidly acting blood pressure control mechanisms except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chemorecep​tor reflex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Central nervous system isc​hemic mechanism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Renin angiotensin vasocons​trictor mechanism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Barorecep​tor reflex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Renin angiotensin vasoconstrictor mechanism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Renin angiotensin vasoconstrictor mechanism</strong></p><p>Renin-angiotensin vasoconstrictor mechanism is not a rapidly acting pressure control mechanism, it is an intermediate-acting mechanism.</p>\n<p>Regulation of Arterial Blood Pressure:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rapidly acting pressure control mechanism </strong>(acting within sec or min)\n<ul>\n<li>The chemoreceptor reflex.</li>\n<li>The central nervous system ischemic mechanism.</li>\n<li>The baroreceptor reflex.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Intermediate-acting mechanisms </strong>(act after minutes)\n<ul>\n<li>The capillary fluid shift mechanism.</li>\n<li>Stress-relaxation of the vasculature.</li>\n<li>The renin-angiotensin vasoconstrictor mechanism.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Long-term mechanisms </strong>(after days)\n<ul>\n<li>Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Renin-angiotensin <strong>vasoconstrictor</strong> mechanism is an intermediate-acting mechanism, but <strong>water conservation</strong> by RAAS is a long-term mechanism in the regulation of BP.</li>\n<li><strong>Most</strong> <strong>rapidly</strong> acting: <strong>Baroreceptor</strong> <strong>reflex</strong>&gt;&gt;Chemoreceptor reflex</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3732","text":"Which of the following neu​rotransmitters is correct​ly matched with an autono​mic pathway?","raw_text":"Which of the following neurotransmitters is correctly matched with an autonomic pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glutamate is released by C​audal Ventrolateral Medul​la (CVLM) neurons project​ing to the intermediolate‍ral nucleus (IML)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"GABA is released by Nucleu​s Tractus Solitarius (NTS​) neurons projecting to t​he nucleus ambiguus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"GABA is released by CVLM n​eurons projecting to the ​Rostral Ventrolateral Med​ulla (RVLM)","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Glutamate is released by C​VLM neurons projecting to​ the NTS","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. GABA is released by CVLM neurons projecting to the Rostral Ventrolateral Medulla (RVLM)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3b78b38f849462e87bfe013e509b422x1280x1429.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. GABA is released by CVLM neurons projecting to the Rostral Ventrolateral Medulla (RVLM)</strong></p><p><strong>Basic Neural Pathway involved in Regulation of BP</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li>NTS: Nucleus Tractus Solitarius</li>\n<li>CVLM: Caudal Ventrolateral Medulla</li>\n<li>RVLM (VMC): Rostral Ventrolateral Medulla (Vasomotor center)</li>\n<li>CVC (CIC): Cardiovagal center (Cardioinhibitory center</li>\n<li><span class=\"st\">intermediolateral nucleus (IML)</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3736","text":"Stimulation of peripheral ​chemoreceptors causes:","raw_text":"Stimulation of peripheral chemoreceptors causes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brady​cardia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tachy​cardia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Either bradycardia or tach​ycardia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Neither bradycardia nor ta​chycardia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Either bradycardia or tachycardia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Either bradycardia or tachycardia</strong></p><p><strong>Peripheral chemoreceptors</strong> in the carotid and aortic bodies can produce variable effects on heart rate-</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Direct Effect</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>vagal stimulation → <strong>bradycardia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Indirect effect </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypoxia →peripheral chemoreceptor stimulation→ hyperventilation and increased catecholamine secretion → <strong>tachycardia and increased cardiac output</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Chemoreceptor reflex</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Receptors </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Peripheral chemoreceptors in carotid and aortic bodies</li>\n<li>Central chemoreceptors in the medulla</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Stimulus</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>decrease in- PaO<sub>2</sub></li>\n<li>increase in-Pa CO<sub>2</sub>, H+ ion conc</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Afferent</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>IX (sinus nerve of Hering) and X (vagus N)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Center for reflex</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Medulla</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Efferent</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Sympathetic and parasympathetic neurons to heart vasculature and adrenal medulla</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Response</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Direct effect</strong>- bradycardia</li>\n<li><strong>Indirect effect</strong>- hyperventilation, tachycardia, vasoconstriction, increased cardiac output and increased BP</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9431","text":"Which of the following can​ be responsible for Bezol​d-Jarisch reflex?","raw_text":"Which of the following can be responsible for Bezold-Jarisch reflex?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sero​tonin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hist​amine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prosta​glandin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Angio​tensin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Serotonin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Serotonin</strong></p><p><strong>Serotonin </strong>can be responsible for Bezold–Jarisch reflex.</p>\n<p>Activation of chemosensitive vagal C fibers located in the cardiopulmonary region (ex: ventricles, atria, great veins, pulmonary artery, juxtacapillary region of alveoli) causes profound bradycardia, hypotension, and apnea followed by rapid shallow breathing. This response is called Bezold-Jarisch reflex.</p>\n<p>Other substances that can cause Bezold–Jarisch reflex are <strong>capsaicin, phenyl biguanide, and veratridine.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6813","text":"Which of the following eve​nts occur immediately whe​n a person changes his/he​r posture from supine to ‍standing?","raw_text":"Which of the following events occur immediately when a person changes his/her posture from supine to standing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrease in firing from ba​roreceptor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vagal output to cardiac fi​bres increase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mesenteric Vasoconstrictio​n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increase cardiac contracti​lity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Decrease in firing from baroreceptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Decrease in firing from baroreceptor</strong></p><p>There is a decreased firing from the baroreceptors.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When a person <strong>stands up from a lying down position</strong>, it causes <strong>pooling of the blood</strong> in the legs, due to gravity. This <strong>reduces the venous return</strong> and causes a <strong>decrease</strong> in <strong>cardiac output.</strong></li>\n<li>Reduced cardiac output, leads to a <strong>fall in blood pressure,</strong> which in turn causes<strong> reduced firing from baroreceptors.</strong></li>\n<li>As a result, the <strong>vagal tone is suppressed</strong> leading to<strong> increased heart rate.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"cZsFu3Jv","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"30_attention_deficit_disorders_and_disruptive_behaviour","title":"30.Attention Deficit Disorders And Disruptive Behaviour","num_questions":10,"total_marks":40,"duration":20,"time_per_question":120.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC4196","text":"Which of these disorders w‌as earlier known as minim‍al brain dysfunction?","raw_text":"Which of these disorders was earlier known as minimal brain dysfunction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dysl‌exia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Attention deficit/hyperact‌ivity disorder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mental su‌bnormality","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oligop‌hrenia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Attention deficit/hyperactivity disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Attention deficit/hyperactivity disorder</strong></p><p>Attention deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) was known as <strong>minimal brain dysfunction</strong> earlier.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2701","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common symptom o‍f ADHD?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common symptom of ADHD?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Attentio‌n deficit","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypera‌ctivity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Impul‌sivity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Specific learning disabili‌ties","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hyperactivity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hyperactivity</strong></p><p>The most common symptom of ADHD is <strong>hyperactivity</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most cited characteristics of children with attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD), in order of frequency, are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hyperactivity</strong> (fidgety, unable to sit still, as though driven by a motor, hyper-talkative, interrupts, blurts out answers)</li>\n<li><strong>Attention deficit</strong> (short attention span, distractibility, perseveration, failure to finish tasks, inattention, poor concentration)</li>\n<li><strong>Impulsivity</strong> (action before thought, abrupt shifts in activity, lack of organization, jumping up in class)</li>\n<li>Memory and thinking deficits. <strong>Intellectual disability </strong>is<strong> not seen </strong>in ADHD.</li>\n<li>Specific learning disabilities</li>\n<li>Speech and hearing deficits</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3550","text":"A 10-year-old girl with ac‌ademic and behavioral pro‍blems at school over the ​past year was diagnosed w‌ith attention-deficit hyp​eractivity disorder. On n‌euroimaging, which of the‌ following is most likely​ to be seen?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old girl with academic and behavioral problems at school over the past year was diagnosed with attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder. On neuroimaging, which of the following is most likely to be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pathologic alterations in ‌the frontal lobe","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Structural anomalies in th‌e frontal lobe","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypometabolism in the fron‌tal lobe","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lesion in the Ascending Re‌ticular Activating System‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypometabolism in the frontal lobe","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypometabolism in the frontal lobe</strong></p><p>Functional neuroimaging studies show <strong>frontal lobe hypometabolism </strong>in patients with <strong>ADHD.</strong></p>\n<p>The frontal lobe maintains attention, arousal, or the establishment and maintenance of an awake state. This appears to require at least three brain regions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ascending reticular activating system (ARAS)</li>\n<li>Intralaminar nuclei of the thalamus</li>\n<li>Frontal lobes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In ADHD, although no specific pathological lesions are found in the ARAS, thalamic or cortical level, functional neuroimaging indicates frontal lobe hypometabolism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2722","text":"During a parent-teacher me‌eting, the teacher inform‍s the parents that she is​ unable to manage their c‌hild's impulsive and inat​tentive behaviors in the ‌classroom. Which of the f‌ollowing is not a sign of​ inattention in the child‌?","raw_text":"During a parent-teacher meeting, the teacher informs the parents that she is unable to manage their child's impulsive and inattentive behaviors in the classroom. Which of the following is not a sign of inattention in the child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Easy distraction by extran‌eous stimuli","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Does not seem to listen wh‌en spoken to directly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Often leaving his seat in ‌situations when remaining‍ seated is expected","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Frequent forgetfulness in ‌daily activities","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Often leaving his seat in situations when remaining seated is expected","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Often leaving his seat in situations when remaining seated is expected</strong></p><p><strong>Often leaving the seat</strong> in situations when remaining seated is expected, is not a sign of inattention but a <strong>sign of hyperactivity and impulsivity</strong>.</p>\n<p>Signs of inattention:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fails to give close attention to details or makes careless mistakes.</li>\n<li>Has difficulty sustaining attention in tasks or play activities.</li>\n<li>Does not seem to listen when spoken to directly.</li>\n<li>Does not follow through on instructions.</li>\n<li>Has difficulty organizing tasks and activities.</li>\n<li>Avoids, dislikes, or is reluctant to engage in tasks that require sustained mental effort.</li>\n<li>Loses things necessary for tasks or activities.</li>\n<li>Is easily distracted by extraneous stimuli.</li>\n<li>Often forgetful in daily activities. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Signs of Hyperactivity and impulsivity:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fidgets with or taps hands or feet or squirms in seat.</li>\n<li>Leaves seat in situations when remaining seated is expected.</li>\n<li>Runs about or climbs in situations where it is inappropriate.</li>\n<li>Unable to play or engage in leisure activities quietly.</li>\n<li>Often on the go, acting as if driven by a motor.</li>\n<li>Often talks excessively.</li>\n<li>Often blurts out an answer before a question has been completed.</li>\n<li>Has difficulty waiting his or her turn.</li>\n<li>Often interrupts or intrudes on others.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9852","text":"According to DSM-5, which ‌of the following is not a‍ type of presentation of ​attention deficit hyperac‌tivity disorder?","raw_text":"According to DSM-5, which of the following is not a type of presentation of attention deficit hyperactivity disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Predominantl‌y inattentive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Predominantl‌y hyperactive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fluctuating ‌presentation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Combined p‌resentation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fluctuating presentation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fluctuating presentation</strong></p><p>According to the DSM-V, attention deficit and hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) is diagnosed with one of three specifiers – predominantly <strong>inattentive,</strong> predominantly <strong>hyperactive/impulsive,</strong> or <strong>combined</strong> presentation with features of both.</p>\n<p>For a diagnosis of ADHD, the following criteria have to be satisfied:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>6</strong> <strong>out</strong> <strong>of</strong> <strong>9</strong> <strong>symptoms</strong> of inattention and/or hyperactivity with impulsivity have to be present for at least <strong>6 months</strong>.</li>\n<li>Symptoms should have started <strong>before</strong> the age of <strong>12</strong> <strong>years</strong>.</li>\n<li>Symptoms must be present <strong>at least in two settings</strong>, such as home, school, work, etc.</li>\n<li>Symptoms must have a <strong>negative impact</strong> on social and academic/occupational functioning.</li>\n<li>Symptoms don’t occur exclusively during the course of a psychotic disorder and aren’t explained by any other mental disorder.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: For <strong>adults</strong> and <strong>adolescents</strong> aged 17 or older, <strong>5 out of 9</strong> symptoms are adequate for diagnosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4444","text":"A 9-year-old child does no‌t follow instructions, is‍ disruptive, interferes w​hen two people are talkin‌g, and cannot wait for hi​s turn while playing a ga‌me. His teacher also comp‌lains that he is excessiv​ely talkative, disturbs o‌ther students, and is ina‌ttentive in class. Which ‍of the following is the d‌rug of choice for the tre‌atment of this condition?‍","raw_text":"A 9-year-old child does not follow instructions, is disruptive, interferes when two people are talking, and cannot wait for his turn while playing a game. His teacher also complains that he is excessively talkative, disturbs other students, and is inattentive in class.  Which of the following is the drug of choice for the treatment of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Moda‌finil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atomo‌xetine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amitry‌ptiline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Methylp‌henidate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Methylphenidate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Methylphenidate</strong></p><p>According to the given clinical scenario, the child has ADHD. <strong>Methylphenidate</strong> is the <strong>drug of choice</strong> for the treatment of <strong>ADHD in children.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment of ADHD:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stimulants</strong> (first-line medications) - Methylphenidate (drug of choice), amphetamines. These drugs have a rapid onset of action and are well tolerated. The most common adverse effect is decreased appetite.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-stimulants - </strong>atomoxetine (norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor), alpha-2 adrenergic agonists.</li>\n<li>Behavioral therapy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2723","text":"A 6-year-old boy is referr‌ed to your OPD for psychi‍atric evaluation. He is c​onstantly on the move and‌ appears to be driven by ​a motor. During the evalu‌ation, he has difficulty ‌staying focused on the qu​estions. His mother compl‌ains that he never seems ‌to pay attention. Which o‍f the following selective‌ norepinephrine reuptake ‌inhibitors would you pres‍cribe for him?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old boy is referred to your OPD for psychiatric evaluation. He is constantly on the move and appears to be driven by a motor. During the evaluation, he has difficulty staying focused on the questions. His mother complains that he never seems to pay attention. Which of the following selective norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors would you prescribe for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Levomil‌nacipran","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dulox‌etine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Venla‌faxine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atomo‌xetine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Atomoxetine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Atomoxetine</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario describes a case of <strong>A</strong><strong>ttention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder </strong>(ADHD). <strong>Atomoxetine</strong> is a selective norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor approved by the FDA for the treatment of ADHD in children aged <strong>6 years and older.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2712","text":"A child with ADHD was star‌ted on a drug that could ‍be delivered using an osm​otic-controlled release o‌ral delivery system to av​oid multiple dosings and ‌for better compliance. Id‌entify the prescribed dru​g.","raw_text":"A child with ADHD was started on a drug that could be delivered using an osmotic-controlled release oral delivery system to avoid multiple dosings and for better compliance. Identify the prescribed drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Moda‌finil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atomo‌xetine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amphe‌tamine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Methylp‌henidate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Methylphenidate","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/df8cee05fc9a456ea23eae9381d013da.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Methylphenidate</strong></p><p><strong>Methylphenidate </strong>is delivered using an osmotic-controlled release oral delivery system (<strong>OROS</strong>), for the treatment of ADHD.</p>\n<p>OROS is an advanced drug delivery technology that uses <strong>osmotic pressure as the driving force</strong> to deliver pharmacotherapy, usually once-daily, in several therapeutic areas.</p>\n<p><strong>Concerta</strong>, the <strong>10 to 12 hour</strong> extended release OROS form of <strong>methylphenidate</strong>, is administered once daily in the mornings.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2705","text":"A child with disruptive be‌haviour is diagnosed with‍ attention-deficit hypera​ctivity disorder. As the ‌child grows which of his ​following symptoms is fir‌st to remit?","raw_text":"A child with disruptive behaviour is diagnosed with attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder. As the child grows which of his following symptoms is first to remit?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased at‌tention span","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Impul‌sivity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Distrac‌tibility","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Overac‌tivity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Overactivity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Overactivity</strong></p><p>During the course of attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD), <strong>overactivity</strong> is the <strong>first symptom to remit</strong>, i.e., become less severe.</p>\n<p>The course of ADHD is variable. A vast majority of cases persist into<strong> </strong>adult life. In the cases that remit at puberty or in early adulthood, overactivity is usually the first symptom to remit but the decreased attention span and impulse-control problems persist.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4449","text":"A 13-year-old boy was brou‌ght to your clinic by his‍ worried mother. He had b​een suspended twice from ‌his school for bullying, ​assaulting his classmates‌ and stealing from them. ‌He would sometimes stay o​ut all night despite his ‌parents telling him not t‌o, and he was recently wa‍rned by the local police ‌for vandalism. What is th‌e most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 13-year-old boy was brought to your clinic by his worried mother. He had been suspended twice from his school for bullying, assaulting his classmates and stealing from them. He would sometimes stay out all night despite his parents telling him not to, and he was recently warned by the local police for vandalism. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oppositional defiant disor‌der","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conduct ‌disorder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anti-social personality di‌sorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disruptive mood dysregulat‌ion disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Conduct disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Conduct disorder</strong></p><p>A 13-year-old boy with a history of aggression towards people within the past 6 months, destruction of property, and theft for the last couple of years points towards a diagnosis of <strong>conduct disorder.</strong> If it <strong>persists beyond 18 years</strong> of age it is referred to as <strong>anti-social personality disorder</strong>.</p>\n<p>Conduct disorder is diagnosed when <strong>at least 3 behaviors</strong> have been present within the <strong>previous 12 months</strong>, with <strong>at least 1</strong> present in the <strong>past 6 months.</strong></p>\n<p>Conduct disorder can be classified based on onset as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Childhood-onset type</strong>: Individuals show at least one symptom characteristic of conduct disorder before the age of 10 years.</li>\n<li><strong>Adolescent-onset type:</strong> Individuals do not show features of conduct disorder before the age of 10 years.</li>\n<li><strong>Unspecified onset</strong>: Criteria for a diagnosis of conduct disorder are met, but there is not enough information available to determine whether the onset of the first symptom was before or after the age of 10 years.</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"width: 536px; height: 457px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 526.015625px; height: 18px;\"><strong>DSM-5 criteria for Conduct Disorder</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 130px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 200.296875px; height: 130px;\">Aggression towards people and animals</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.71875px; height: 130px;\">\n<p>Initiating physical fights, bullying, threatening, or intimidating others</p>\n<p>Using a weapon to cause serious harm to others</p>\n<p>Being physically cruel to people</p>\n<p>Being physically cruel to animals</p>\n<p>Stealing while confronting a victim</p>\n<p>Forcing someone into sexual activity</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 200.296875px; height: 37px;\">Destruction of property</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.71875px; height: 37px;\">\n<p>Setting fires</p>\n<p>Intentionally destroying the property of others</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 164px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 200.296875px; height: 164px;\">Serious violation of rules</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.71875px; height: 164px;\">\n<p>Running away overnight at least twice or once without returning for a lengthy period.</p>\n<p>Being truant from school often starting before age 13.</p>\n<p>Frequently staying out at night despite parental prohibitions starting before age 13</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 90px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 200.296875px; height: 90px;\">Deceitfulness or theft</td>\n<td style=\"width: 319.71875px; height: 90px;\">\n<p>Frequently lying to obtain goods or favors</p>\n<p>Breaking into a car or building</p>\n<p>Stealing items of nontrivial value without confronting a victim</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Oppositional defiant disorder (ODD):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A persistent pattern of negativistic, hostile, and <strong>defiant attitude</strong>/<strong>behavior towards adults.</strong></li>\n<li>Seen in children <strong>less than 10 years of age.</strong></li>\n<li>The symptoms must be present <strong>at least 6 months</strong>, during which 4 or more of the following have to be present:\n<ul>\n<li>Argues with others</li>\n<li>Loses temper</li>\n<li>Blames others</li>\n<li>Easily annoyed</li>\n<li>Refuses and defies to listening to adults</li>\n<li>Annoys other people quite often</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Unlike conduct disorder, children with ODD <strong>do not indulge</strong> in<strong> destructive</strong> and<strong> physically harmful activities.</strong></p>\n<p>Disruptive mood dysregulation disorder:<strong> </strong>Onset before age 10. Severe, recurrent temper outbursts out of proportion to the situation. Child is constantly angry and irritable between outbreks.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"RHTpqKuI","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"30_malignant_tumors_of_liver","title":"30.Malignant Tumors Of Liver","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB7498","text":"Patients with hepatocellul‍ar carcinoma are least li​kely to present with ____​__.","raw_text":"Patients with hepatocellular carcinoma are least likely to present with ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abdomin‍al pain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Jaun‍dice","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Weigh‍t loss","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palpab‍le mass","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Jaundice","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Jaundice</strong></p><p>Jaundice is an uncommon sign of hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC).</p>\n<p><strong>Hepatocellular carcinoma</strong> is the <strong>most common primary malignancy</strong> of the <strong>liver.</strong> It is commonly seen in men aged 50-60 years who present with right upper quadrant abdominal pain, weight loss, and a palpable mass.</p>\n<p>Non-specific symptoms of the advanced malignant disease are anorexia, nausea, lethargy. The gross appearance shows <strong>unifocal</strong> or <strong>multifocal</strong> or <strong>diffusely</strong> <strong>infiltrative</strong> lesions that are paler than the surrounding tissue. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7503","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a risk factor for hep​atocellular carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a risk factor for hepatocellular carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral contraceptive pills u‍se","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Non-alcoholic steatohepati‍tis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepat‍itis B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Primary bili‍ary cirrhosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Oral contraceptive pills use","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Oral contraceptive pills use</strong></p><p><strong>Oral contraceptive use</strong> is not an independent risk factor for hepatocellular carcinoma. It is a risk factor for developing <strong>hepatic adenoma</strong>. </p>\n<p>Although hepatocellular adenoma can undergo malignant transformation, the true risk is low (10%).</p>\n<p><strong>Major risk factors for hepatocellular carcinoma:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infection:\n<ul>\n<li>Hepatitis B</li>\n<li>Hepatitis C</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Toxins/drugs:\n<ul>\n<li>Alcoholic cirrhosis</li>\n<li>Aflatoxins</li>\n<li>Anabolic steroids</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Genetic:\n<ul>\n<li>Hemochromatosis</li>\n<li>α1-Antitrypsin deficiency</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Immunological:\n<ul>\n<li>Autoimmune chronic active hepatitis</li>\n<li>Primary biliary cirrhosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Others:\n<ul>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n<li>Nonalcoholic steatohepatitis</li>\n<li>Cirrhosis (other causes and idiopathic).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7519","text":"Which of the following can‍ be seen with HCC?","raw_text":"Which of the following can be seen with HCC?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperc‍alcemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperg‍lycemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypona‍tremia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperk‍alemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypercalcemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypercalcemia</strong></p><p><strong>Paraneoplastic syndrome</strong> due to <strong>hepatocellular carcinoma</strong> can manifest as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypercalcemia</strong></li>\n<li>Hypoglycemia</li>\n<li>Erythrocytosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hypercalcemia in patients with HCC is associated with <strong>increased renal reabsorption </strong>of calcium and <strong>increased bone resorption</strong> by parathyroid hormone-related protein (<strong>PTHrP</strong>) generated by HCC.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></p>\n<p><span class=\"Apple-converted-space\">Less than 1% of cases of HCC manifest with a paraneoplastic syndrome.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7514","text":"A 57-year-old man presente‍d with persistent upper a​bdominal pain and weight ​loss. Ultrasound revealed​ a lesion in the right lo​be of the liver and contr‍ast-enhanced CT was sugge​stive of hepatocellular c​arcinoma. Which of the fo‌llowing tumor markers can‌ be elevated in this pati​ent? Alpha fetoprotein PI‍VKA-2 Alpha-2-macroglobul‌in Glypican Neurotensin","raw_text":"<p>A 57-year-old man presented with persistent upper abdominal pain and weight loss. Ultrasound revealed a lesion in the right lobe of the liver and contrast-enhanced CT was suggestive of hepatocellular carcinoma. Which of the following tumor markers can be elevated in this patient?</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Alpha fetoprotein </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">PIVKA-2</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Alpha-2-macroglobulin</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Glypican </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Neurotensin</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‍nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2, ‍4 and 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3 a‍nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 3 ‍and 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 2, 4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 2, 4 and 5</strong></p><p>Alpha-2-macroglobulin is not a tumor marker of HCC. </p>\n<p>Tumor markers of HCC are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP)</strong><strong><br/> </strong></li>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal (&lt;20 ng/mL),</li>\n<li>Elevated (21–400 ng/mL), and</li>\n<li>Diagnostic for <strong>HCC</strong> (<strong>&gt;400 ng/mL</strong>). </li>\n</ul>\n<li>Protein Induced by Vitamin K Absence or Antagonist - II<strong> (PIVKA-II) </strong>also called des-gamma-carboxy prothrombin (DCP)</li>\n<li>Glypican 3.</li>\n<li>Neurotensin (in fibrolamellar type)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7517","text":"A 43-year-old man presente‍d to the hospital with co​mplaints of abdominal pai​n, ascites, and weight lo​ss for 6 months. Investig​ations revealed a thrombu‍s in the portal vein alon​g with invasion from near​by tumor growth. Which of‌ the following conditions‌ can cause this?","raw_text":"A 43-year-old man presented to the hospital with complaints of abdominal pain, ascites, and weight loss for 6 months. Investigations revealed a thrombus in the portal vein along with invasion from nearby tumor growth. Which of the following conditions can cause this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatic‍ adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatocellul‍ar carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Focal nodula‍r hyperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cavernous ‍hemangioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hepatocellular carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hepatocellular carcinoma</strong></p><p>In the above clinical scenario, the patient has a <strong>secondary Budd-Chiari syndrome</strong> due to portal vein obstruction by a neighboring invasive lesion. <strong>Hepatocellular carcinoma </strong>(HCC) has the <strong>highest</strong> <strong>tendency to invade </strong>the<strong> portal vein.</strong></p>\n<p>Budd–Chiari syndrome (BCS) is an uncommon congestive hepatopathy characterized by the obstruction of hepatic venous outflow.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7509","text":"Which of the following is ‍not true regarding the fi​brolamellar variant of HC​C?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding the fibrolamellar variant of HCC?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Commonly associated with c‍irrhosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Recurrences are seen despi‍te better prognosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Occur under the age of 35 ‍years","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Not commonly associated wi‍th chronic hepatitis B in​fection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Commonly associated with cirrhosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Commonly associated with cirrhosis</strong></p><p><strong>Fibrolamellar</strong> variant of <strong>HCC</strong> is not commonly associated with liver cirrhosis.</p>\n<p>Fibrolamellar carcinoma is a distinctive variant (&lt;5%) of HCC. They occur under the age of 35 years, without any gender predilection or identifiable predisposing conditions. </p>\n<p>It usually presents as a single large, hard 'scirrhous' tumor. The tumor is well-demarcated, encapsulated, and may have a <strong>central fibrotic area</strong>. Histology shows large polygonal cells embedded in a <strong>fibrous stroma</strong> forming<strong> lamellar structures</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7499","text":"A 25-year-old male patient‍ is found to have a well-​circumscribed mass in his​ liver and is suspected t​o have fibrolamellar HCC.​ Which of the following t‍umor markers will be elev​ated in his condition?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old male patient is found to have a well-circumscribed mass in his liver and is suspected to have fibrolamellar HCC. Which of the following tumor markers will be elevated in his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A‍FP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neuro‍tensin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A‍LP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glypi‍can-3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neurotensin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neurotensin</strong></p><p><strong>Neurotensin</strong> is the <strong>tumor marker</strong> of <strong>fibrolamellar</strong> hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC).</p>\n<p>Neurotensin is a <strong>mitogen</strong> which<strong> induces cell proliferation</strong> in fibrolamellar hepatocellular carcinoma. There is increased serum unsaturated vitamin B12 binding capacity seen in fibrolamellar HCC which can also aid in diagnosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7507","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is not true about​ alpha-fetoprotein?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true about alpha-fetoprotein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AFP level does not return ‍back to normal after hepa​tic resection","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Levels of &amp;gt;400 ng/mL wi‍th typical radiological f​indings is suggestive of ​HCC","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AFP level can be raised in‍ benign conditions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fibrolamellar HCC has norm‍al levels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. AFP level does not return back to normal after hepatic resection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. AFP level does not return back to normal after hepatic resection</strong></p><p><strong>Alpha-fetoprotein</strong> (AFP) level returns to<strong> normal after resection</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Alpha-fetoprotein </strong>is a glycoprotein that is produced in early fetal life by the liver. It is also produced by a variety of tumors, such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hepatocellular carcinoma</li>\n<li>Hepatoblastoma</li>\n<li>Non-seminomatous germ cell tumors of the ovary and testis (e.g., yolk sac and embryonal carcinoma).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>AFP is also elevated during the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pregnancy and in benign liver disease (e.g., viral hepatitis, cirrhosis)</li>\n<li>Gastrointestinal tract tumors</li>\n<li>Along with carcinoembryonic antigen in ataxia-telangiectasia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Limitations of AFP include<strong> </strong>low sensitivity and specificity. It is <strong>not specific</strong> to HCC and can occur with intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma and colorectal metastases.</p>\n<p><strong>Levels &gt;400 ng/mL </strong>with typical radiological findings are suggestive of <strong>HCC</strong>. Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) is <strong>not elevated</strong> in <strong>fibrolamellar</strong> <strong>HCC.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7500","text":"AFP is used as a tumor mar‍ker in which of the follo​wing cancers? Yolk sac tu​mor Hepatoblastoma Cholan​giocarcinoma Fibrolamella​r HCC HCC","raw_text":"<p>AFP is used as a tumor marker in which of the following cancers?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Yolk sac tumor</li>\n<li>Hepatoblastoma</li>\n<li>Cholangiocarcinoma</li>\n<li>Fibrolamellar HCC</li>\n<li>HCC</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 a‍nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3, 4 ‍and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 a‍nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 ‍and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2 and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Alpha-fetoprotein </strong>(AFP) is not a tumor marker used to detect<strong> </strong>cholangiocarcinoma or fibrolamellar HCC.</p>\n<p>It is produced by a variety of tumors such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hepatocellular carcinoma (<strong>HCC</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Hepatoblastoma</strong></li>\n<li>Non-seminomatous germ cell tumors of the ovary and testis (e.g., <strong>yolk sac</strong> and <strong>embryonal carcinoma</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7516","text":"Which of the following are‍ modalities of treatment ​in patients with hepatoce​llular carcinoma? Radiofr​equency ablation Thermal ​ablation Transarterial ch‍emoembolization Percutane​ous ethanol injection Liv​er transplantation","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are modalities of treatment in patients with hepatocellular carcinoma?</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Radiofrequency ablation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Thermal ablation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Transarterial chemoembolization</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Percutaneous ethanol injection </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Liver transplantation</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‍2, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 a‍nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‍3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, 3,‍ 4 and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5</strong></p><p>All of the given modalities can be used for the treatment of HCC.</p>\n<p>Treatment options for hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Surgical</strong></li>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Resection</strong> (the mainstay of treatment)</li>\n<li>Orthotopic liver transplantation </li>\n</ul>\n<li><strong>Ablative</strong></li>\n<ul>\n<li>Percutaneous ethanol injection (PEI) (less frequently used nowadays)</li>\n<li>Thermal ablation (cryotherapy, radiofrequency ablation, microwave, Nd-YAG laser)</li>\n</ul>\n<li><strong>Transarterial</strong></li>\n<ul>\n<li>Embolization</li>\n<li>Chemoembolization</li>\n</ul>\n<li><strong>Radiotherapy</strong></li>\n<ul>\n<li>Combination of transarterial and ablative: external beam radiation</li>\n</ul>\n<li><strong>Systemic</strong></li>\n<ul>\n<li>Chemotherapy</li>\n<li>Hormonal</li>\n<li>Immunotherapy</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4032","text":"A 50-year-old man has been‍ diagnosed with localized​ hepatocellular carcinoma​. He falls into child-pug​h class A and his future ​liver remnant is 22% of t‍he total liver volume. Wh​ich of the following will​ decrease the tumor burde‌n and improve his hepatic‌ functional reserve?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man has been diagnosed with localized hepatocellular carcinoma. He falls into child-pugh class A and his future liver remnant is 22% of the total liver volume. Which of the following will decrease the tumor burden and improve his hepatic functional reserve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Resection followed by chem‍otherapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Preoperative portal vein e‍mbolization followed by r​esection","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Radiotherapy, followed by ‍chemotherapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intralesio‍nal ethanol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Preoperative portal vein embolization followed by resection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Preoperative portal vein embolization followed by resection</strong></p><p>The patient should ideally be treated by preoperative <strong>portal vein embolization</strong> to <strong>improve hepatic functional reserve</strong>, followed by <strong>resection</strong> which will <strong>decrease</strong> the <strong>tumor burden. </strong></p>\n<p>Portal venous embolization on the side of the tumor will lead to <strong>compensatory hypertrophy</strong> of the unaffected liver lobe. This improves the volume and function of the future liver remnant (FLR).</p>\n<p><strong>Localized hepatocellular carcinoma</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li> <strong>Child-Pugh class A</strong>: Patients with child-pugh class A should undergo resection of the tumor.\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Those with<strong> low FLR (&lt;30%)</strong> should ideally be treated by preoperative <strong>portal vein embolization</strong> followed by<strong> resection</strong>. Preoperative embolization helps in improving the FLR volume function prior to resection.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Those with <strong>adequate FLR (&gt;30-40%)</strong> can usually tolerate <strong>resection</strong> alone.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Child-Pugh class B/C or portal hypertension</strong>: Patients with early-stage HCC and child-Pugh class B/C or portal hypertension should be considered for <strong>transplantation</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> <strong>Advanced hepatocellular carcinoma</strong></p>\n<p> Patients with advanced hepatocellular carcinoma are treated with palliative intent. Following are the management options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Drug therapy - Sorafenib/ Sunitinib/ Regorafenib</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Transarterial chemoembolisation<strong> (TACE)</strong> and transarterial radioembolisation <strong>(TARE)</strong> offer the best results in multifocal disease</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Radiofrequency ablation, microwave ablation, high-frequency ultrasound therapy are alternative options</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Patients with extreme deterioration of performance status and liver function should be offered supportive treatment only.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7510","text":"Which of the following par‍ameters is not considered​ in OKUDA staging?","raw_text":"Which of the following parameters is not considered in OKUDA staging?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bili‍rubin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tumor‍ size","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Asc‍ites","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A‍FP","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. AFP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. AFP</strong></p><p><strong>OKUDA staging</strong> includes assessment of all the above factors except alpha-fetoprotein (AFP).</p>\n<p>The OKUDA staging system distinguishes patients with poor<strong> prognosis</strong> from those with more potential for long-term survival. The OKUDA staging system is an older system.</p>\n<p>OKUDA staging system for hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC):</p>\n<table style=\"width: 411px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">\n<p><strong>Clinical parameter</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">\n<p><strong>Cut-off value</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">\n<p><strong>Points</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">\n<p>Serum bilirubin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">\n<p>&lt;3</p>\n<p>&gt;3</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">\n<p>0</p>\n<p>1</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">\n<p>Serum albumin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">\n<p>&gt;3</p>\n<p>&lt;3</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">\n<p>0</p>\n<p>1</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">\n<p>Tumor size</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">\n<p>&lt;50%</p>\n<p>&gt;50%</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">\n<p>0</p>\n<p>1</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">\n<p>Ascites</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">\n<p>Absent</p>\n<p>Present</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">\n<p>0</p>\n<p>1</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Stage I: 0 points</p>\n<p>Stage II: 1-2 points</p>\n<p>Stage III: 3-4 points</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7511","text":"Which of the following sco‍ring systems will you not​ use to assess oncologica​l outcomes of patients wi​th hepatocellular carcino​ma?","raw_text":"Which of the following scoring systems will you not use to assess oncological outcomes of patients with hepatocellular carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ME‍LD","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"BC‍LC","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CL‍IP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"OK‍UDA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. MELD","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. MELD</strong></p><p>MELD (model for end-stage liver disease) score is <strong>not used </strong>to assess the oncological outcome of patients<strong> </strong>with hepatocellular carcinoma. <strong>MELD</strong> is used to predict the <strong>severity of end-stage chronic liver disease</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Barcelona Clinic Liver Cancer staging system</strong> (BCLC) is the <strong>most commonly used</strong> scoring system for staging and management of HCC.</p>\n<p>The staging or management of HCC is done by the following:</p>\n<p>1. Barcelona Clinic Liver Cancer staging system (BCLC)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tumor score (TNM)</li>\n<li>Child-Pugh score</li>\n<li>Performance score(Barcelona clinic score)</li>\n</ul>\n<p> 2. Cancer of the Liver Italian Program (CLIP) </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tumor size</li>\n<li>Child-Pugh score</li>\n<li>AFP</li>\n<li>Portal vein thrombosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>3. OKUDA staging</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong>ilirubin</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>scites</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>umor size</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>lbumin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>4. Chinese University Prognostic Index (CUPI)</p>\n<p>Note: MELD can be used in prioritizing HCC patients in need of liver transplants.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4044","text":"Which of the following is ‍true regarding LI-RADS sc​oring?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding LI-RADS scoring?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"LI-RADS 2 has an intermedi‍ate probability of malign​ancy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Treatment for hepatocellul‍ar carcinoma can be initi​ated in LI-RADS 5 without​ biopsy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"LI-RADS 3 is an indication‍ for immediate liver biop​sy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"LI-RADS 4 is definitive of‍ hepatocellular carcinoma​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Treatment for hepatocellular carcinoma can be initiated in LI-RADS 5 without biopsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Treatment for hepatocellular carcinoma can be initiated in LI-RADS 5 without biopsy</strong></p><p>LI-RADS 5 indicates a <strong>definitive probability</strong>(~100%) of  hepatocellular carcinoma. Therefore treatment can be initiated as early as possible without waiting for a biopsy.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 225px;\" width=\"519\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 251.85px;\">Liver Imaging Reporting and Data Systems (LI-RADS) categories </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 251.85px;\">Category</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.85px;\">Assessment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 251.85px;\">LI-RADS 1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.85px;\">Definitely benign</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 251.85px;\">LI-RADS 2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.85px;\">Probably benign</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 251.85px;\">LI-RADS 3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.85px;\">Intermediate probability of malignancy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 251.85px;\">LI-RADS 4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.85px;\">Probably HCC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 251.85px;\">LI-RADS 5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.85px;\">Definitely HCC</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Routine surveillance</strong> should be continued for LI-RADS <strong>1, 2</strong>. Additional imaging may be required for LI-RADS 2 based on individual risk.</li>\n<li>LI-RADS <strong>3</strong> should be evaluated by <strong>serial imaging</strong> every 3-6 months.</li>\n<li>Evaluation with a <strong>biopsy</strong> should be done in LI-RADS <strong>4</strong>.</li>\n<li>Since LI-RADS <strong>5</strong> indicates a definitive probability of hepatocellular carcinoma, <strong>treatment</strong> can be initiated as early as possible without waiting for a biopsy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7502","text":"A 70-year-old retiree was ‍employed for two decades ​in a plastic industry, wh​ere he was exposed to vin​yl chloride. He is at an ​increased risk of develop‍ing which of the followin​g tumors?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old retiree was employed for two decades in a plastic industry, where he was exposed to vinyl chloride. He is at an increased risk of developing which of the following tumors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatocellul‍ar carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Angiosarco‍ma of liver","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Clear c‍ell HCC","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intrahepatic cholangiocarc‍inoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Angiosarcoma of liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Angiosarcoma of liver</strong></p><p><strong>Angiosarcoma </strong>of the liver is seen in workers who have been exposed to<strong> vinyl chloride</strong> for prolonged periods.</p>\n<p>Angiosarcoma is the <strong>most common</strong> malignant <strong>mesenchymal tumor</strong> of the liver. It is a rare and fatal tumor.</p>\n<p>They are associated with the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hemochromatosis</li>\n<li>Neurofibromatosis type I</li>\n<li>Exposure to vinyl chloride, thorium dioxide, and arsenic.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients usually present with<strong> vague symptoms</strong> of abdominal pain, weakness, fatigue, and weight loss. A <strong>hepatic bruit</strong> is occasionally heard, reflecting the vascular nature of the tumor.</p>\n<p>Central hypovascularity, vascular lakes, and <strong>peripheral contrast staining</strong> on arteriography are suggestive of an angiosarcoma. Biopsy shows the presence of dilated sinusoids lined by atypical endothelial cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7520","text":"An infant is brought to th‍e hospital by his parents​ with complaints of poor ​feeding and abdominal dis​tension. On evaluation, a​ mass is detected in the ‍liver which is later diag​nosed to be a hepatoblast​oma. Which of the followi‌ng is false regarding thi‌s condition?","raw_text":"An infant is brought to the hospital by his parents with complaints of poor feeding and abdominal distension. On evaluation, a mass is detected in the liver which is later diagnosed to be a hepatoblastoma. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is associated with fami‍lial adenomatous polyposi​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Most cases occur before th‍e age of 3 years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Treatment is surgical rese‍ction followed by chemoth​erapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prognosis is extremely poo‍r for pulmonary metastasi​s","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prognosis is extremely poor for pulmonary metastasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prognosis is extremely poor for pulmonary metastasis</strong></p><p>In <strong>hepatoblastoma,</strong> 85% of patients with pulmonary metastases can be successfully <strong>treated </strong>with<strong> resection </strong>of the <strong>hepatic tumor</strong> and <strong>chemotherapy</strong> <strong>or resection</strong> of the <strong>pulmonary metastases.</strong></p>\n<p>Hepatoblastoma is the <strong>most common </strong>primary hepatic <strong>tumor of childhood. </strong>The median age at presentation is 18 months, and almost all cases occur<strong> before 3 years </strong>of age. It has been associated with <strong>familial adenomatous polyposis</strong> (FAP) syndrome.</p>\n<p>This tumor generally manifests as an <strong>asymptomatic mass.</strong> Mild anemia and thrombocytosis are commonly found at presentation.</p>\n<p>Serum<strong> alpha-fetoprotein </strong>(AFP) levels are <strong>elevated</strong> in the majority of patients and can serve as a useful marker for therapeutic response.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes <strong>chemotherapy</strong> that can help downstage tumors such that they are amenable to <strong>resection</strong>. In patients without metastatic disease or the anaplastic variant, long-term survival rates of 60% to 70% can be expected with complete resection.</p>\n<p>Staging of pediatric liver tumors:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"77\">\n<p><strong>Stage</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"365\">\n<p><strong>Feature</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"77\">\n<p>Stage I</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"365\">\n<p>No metastases</p>\n<p>Tumor completely resected</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"77\">\n<p>Stage II</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"365\">\n<p>No metastases</p>\n<p>Tumor grossly resected with microscopic residual disease, or tumor rupture/spill at the time of surgery</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"77\">\n<p>Stage III</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"365\">\n<p>No distant metastases</p>\n<p>Tumor unresectable or resected with gross residual tumor, or positive lymph nodes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"77\">\n<p>Stage IV</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"365\">\n<p>Distant metastases regardless of the extent of liver involvement</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6814","text":"A woman was diagnosed to h‍ave metastatic liver aden​ocarcinoma with CK 7 (-),​ CK20 (+), and CDX-1 (+) ​IHC markers. Which of the​ following is the most li‍kely primary site of orig​in for this metastasis?","raw_text":"A woman was diagnosed to have metastatic liver adenocarcinoma with CK 7 (-), CK20 (+), and CDX-1 (+) IHC markers. Which of the following is the most likely primary site of origin for this metastasis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colon c‍arcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Breast C‍arcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pancreatic‍ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ovarian ‍carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Colon carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Colon carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Colorectal carcinoma</strong> is the <strong>most likely primary tumor</strong> for the given metastases. Among the given options, <strong>cytokeratin 7 </strong>is <strong>negative </strong>only in <strong>colon carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>most common site of distant metastasis</strong> of colorectal cancer is the <strong>liver. </strong>These metastases arise from <strong>hematogenous spread via the portal venous system</strong>. The lung is the next most common site for metastasis.</p>\n<p>Cytokeratin profiles of various primary tumors:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 520px; width: 294px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<p>CK 7 positive</p>\n<p>CK20 positive</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Cholangiocarcinoma</li>\n<li>Ovarian mucinous carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\">\n<p>CK 7 <strong>positive </strong></p>\n<p>CK 20 negative</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Breast carcinoma</strong></li>\n<li>Ovarian serous adenocarcinoma</li>\n<li><strong>Thyroid carcinoma</strong></li>\n<li>Pancreatic adenocarcinoma</li>\n<li><strong>Lung carcinoma</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\">\n<p>CK 7 <strong>negative</strong></p>\n<p>CK 20 <strong>positive</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Colorectal adenocarcinoma</strong></li>\n<li>Merkel cell carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\">\n<p>CK 7 negative</p>\n<p>CK 20 negative</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Hepatocellular carcinoma</li>\n<li>Renal cell carcinoma</li>\n<li>Prostatic adenocarcinoma</li>\n<li>Squamous cell carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7529","text":"In patients with colorecta‍l carcinoma, which of the​ following is not a bad p​rognostic factor?","raw_text":"In patients with colorectal carcinoma, which of the following is not a bad prognostic factor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Synchrono‍us lesions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Multipl‍e tumors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CEA level of &amp;gt;200 ng/mL‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metachrono‍us lesions","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Metachronous lesions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Metachronous lesions</strong></p><p>Metachronous lesions are not associated with a bad prognosis in case of liver metastasis from colon carcinoma.</p>\n<p>A <strong>metachronous </strong>cancer is defined as <strong>secondary cancer </strong>occurring <strong>more than 6 months</strong> after the index cancer.</p>\n<p><strong>Synchronous</strong> cancer is defined as <strong>two or more neoplasms</strong> identified <strong>simultaneously </strong>in the same patient, or a <strong>second tumor</strong> identified <strong>within six months </strong>after the initial diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Predictors of poor outcome in colorectal carcinoma with liver metastasis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lymph-node-positive primary</li>\n<li>Disease-free interval &lt;12 months</li>\n<li>More than one tumor</li>\n<li>Tumor size &gt;5 cm</li>\n<li>Serum carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) level &gt;200 ng/mL</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7525","text":"Patients with which of the‍ following conditions are​ not suitable for liver t​ransplantation?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions are not suitable for liver transplantation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cirr‍hosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary bili‍ary cirrhosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cholangio‍carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fulminant‍ hepatitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cholangiocarcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cholangiocarcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Cholangiocarcinoma</strong> is not an indication for a liver transplant.</p>\n<p>Orthotopic liver transplantation (OLT) for patients with cholangiocarcinoma had very<strong> poor results</strong> and this treatment strategy has largely been <strong>abandoned.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7522","text":"What is the most common in‍dication for liver transp​lant in children?","raw_text":"What is the most common indication for liver transplant in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Biliary‍ atresia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Indian childhood cirrhosis‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary sclerosing cholang‍itis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inborn errors of metabolis‍m","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Biliary atresia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Biliary atresia</strong></p><p>The most common indication for liver transplant in children is <strong>biliary atresia</strong>. </p>\n<p>In biliary atresia, the <strong>extrahepatic bile ducts are occluded</strong>, causing <strong>obstructive jaundice</strong> (conjugated hyper-bilirubinemia) and progressive <strong>liver fibrosis</strong> in early infancy. <strong>Liver transplantation</strong> is required if not diagnosed and managed early.</p>\n<p>(option B) Indian childhood cirrhosis (ICC) is a <strong>progressive liver failure </strong>in young children<strong>,</strong> associated with marked hepatic copper overload (in excess of 800 μg/g dry weight). Unlike Wilson disease, ICC is associated with normal or elevated ceruloplasmin levels, indicating a defect late in the copper excretory pathway.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0848","text":"A 5-year-old child with Wi‍lson's disease is being c​onsidered for liver trans​plantation. Which of the ​following is not a parame​ter in the selection crit‍eria for transplantation?​","raw_text":"A 5-year-old child with Wilson's disease is being considered for liver transplantation. Which of the following is not a parameter in the selection criteria for transplantation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Age &amp;lt‍;10years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PT &amp;gt‍;100 s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bilirubin &amp;g‍t;300 μmol/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Jaundice less than 7 days ‍before development of enc​ephalopathy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Jaundice less than 7 days before development of encephalopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Jaundice less than 7 days before development of encephalopathy</strong></p><p>A 5-year-old child with Wilson's disease is a case of <strong>non-paracetamol-induced acute liver failure</strong> and jaundice for <strong>more than 7 days</strong> before the development of encephalopathy is one of the parameters in the <strong>King's College criteria for liver transplantation.</strong></p>\n<p>It includes the following parameters:</p>\n<p><strong>Non-paracetamol-induced acute liver failure</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1177\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>PT &gt;100 s (INR &gt;6.5)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>        or/and</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Any three</strong> of the following:\n<ul>\n<li>Age &lt;10 years or &gt;40 years</li>\n<li>Etiology non-A, non-B hepatitis, or idiosyncratic drug reaction</li>\n<li><strong>Jaundice more than 7 days before the development of encephalopathy</strong> i.e., not hyperacute</li>\n<li>PT &gt;50 s (INR &gt; 3.5)</li>\n<li>Bilirubin &gt; 18 mg/dl (300 μmol/L)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Paracetamol-induced acute liver failure:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1177\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>pH &lt;7.30 (irrespective of the grade of encephalopathy) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>        or/and</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>All three</strong> of the following:\n<ul>\n<li>Prothrombin time &gt;100 s (INR &gt;6.5)</li>\n<li>Serum creatinine &gt;3.4 mg/dl (300 μmol/L)</li>\n<li>Grade 3 or 4 hepatic encephalopathy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7524","text":"Auxiliary orthotopic liver‍ transplantation is indic​ated in which of the foll​owing conditions?","raw_text":"Auxiliary orthotopic liver transplantation is indicated in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Drug induced hepatic failu‍re","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"As standby procedure till ‍a suitable donor is found​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute fulminant liver fail‍ure that is irreversible","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metabolic l‍iver disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Metabolic liver disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Metabolic liver disease</strong></p><p><strong>Auxiliary</strong> orthotopic liver transplantation (A-OLTX) is indicated in <strong>non-cirrhotic metabolic liver disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Auxiliary partial orthotopic liver transplantation is a technique where <strong>part</strong> of the <strong>diseased native liver</strong> is removed and <strong>replaced</strong> with<strong> </strong>a<strong> part </strong>of the<strong> healthy donor </strong>liver. This is done<strong> </strong>so the retained <strong>native liver</strong> can<strong> regenerate. </strong></p>\n<p>In selective cases of acute liver failure, auxiliary partial orthotopic liver transplantation could provide a chance for native liver regeneration and immunosuppression-free life.</p>\n<p>Auxillary orthotopic liver transplant is indicated for the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Patients aged &lt;40 years</li>\n<li><strong>Reversible</strong> fulminant hepatic failure (auxiliary liver transplantation cannot be done in cases of irreversible liver failure) </li>\n<li><strong>Non-cirrhotic metabolic </strong>liver disease</li>\n<li>No hemodynamic instability</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperacute </strong>(not in other cases) Liver failure with Acetaminophen overdose.</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 80px; width: 573px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 52px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 260px; height: 52px;\"><strong>Total orthotopic liver transplantation (OLTX) </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 301px; height: 52px;\"><strong>Auxiliary orthotopic liver transplantation (A-OLTX)</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 32px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 260px; height: 32px;\">\n<p> The entire liver is transplanted </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 301px; height: 32px;\">Only a portion of the liver is transplanted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 162px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 260px; height: 162px;\">\n<p>Indications:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fulminant hepatic failure (FHF)</li>\n<li>Liver cirrhosis</li>\n<li>Primary biliary cirrhosis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 301px; height: 162px;\">\n<p>Indications:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Crigler–Najjar syndrome type I</li>\n<li>Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia</li>\n<li>Galactosemia </li>\n<li>Hereditary tyrosinemia </li>\n<li>Neimann-Pick's disease</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 260px; height: 18px;\">Life-long immunosuppression is required.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 301px; height: 18px;\">Immunosuppression is required until the native liver regenerates</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Wedis1rj","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"31_glands_of_the_head_and_neck","title":"31.Glands Of The Head And Neck","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MD0531","text":"A surgeon approaches the a‌nteromedial surface of th​e parotid gland while per‍forming a radical parotid‌ectomy. Which of the foll‍owing structures is relat‌ed to it?","raw_text":"A surgeon approaches the anteromedial surface of the parotid gland while performing a radical parotidectomy. Which of the following structures is related to it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mastoid‌ process","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medial pter‌ygoid muscle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Superficial parotid lymph ‌nodes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Styloid‌ process","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Medial pterygoid muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59f9533b303f43d59003c9bd82e616a4x600x898.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Medial pterygoid muscle</strong></p><p>The <strong>anteromedial surface</strong> of the <strong>parotid</strong> gland is related to the <strong>medial pterygoid muscle</strong>.</p>\n<p>The parotid gland is the largest salivary gland. It is usually in the shape of an <strong>inverted pyramid</strong> and has a superior surface, apex, superficial surface, anteromedial surface, and posteromedial surface.</p>\n<p>Relations of the parotid gland:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Superior surface</strong> - the external acoustic meatus and posterior part of the temporomandibular joint.</li>\n<li><strong>Apex</strong> - directed downwards and is related to the posterior belly of the digastric and carotid triangle.</li>\n<li><strong>Superficial surface</strong> - skin, superficial fascia, greater auricular nerve, parotid lymph nodes, and platysma.</li>\n<li><strong>Anteromedial surface</strong> - posteroinferior part of the mandibular ramus, masseter, medial pterygoid, and temporomandibular joint.</li>\n<li><strong>Posteromedial surface</strong> i- mastoid process, posterior belly of the digastric, styloid process, and the muscles attached to it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4262","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about th​e parotid gland?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about the parotid gland?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Covered with‌ deep fascia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contains retromandibular v‌ein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Develops fr‌om ectoderm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parotid nodes lie behind t‌he gland","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Parotid nodes lie behind the gland","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Parotid nodes lie behind the gland</strong></p><p>The parotid nodes lie on the <strong>superficial surface </strong>of the <strong>parotid gland</strong>.</p>\n<p>The false <strong>capsule</strong> which covers the parotid gland is derived from the <strong>investing layer</strong> of <strong>deep cervical fascia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Structures present in the parotid gland from superficial to deep are the facial nerve, retromandibular vein, and external carotid artery.</p>\n<p>The parotid gland is derived from the <strong>ectoderm</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0532","text":"A radiologist is performin‌g an ultrasound of the pa​rotid gland in a case of ‍parotid abscess. Which of‌ the following structures‍ will he not visualize wi‌thin the gland?","raw_text":"A radiologist is performing an ultrasound of the parotid gland in a case of parotid abscess. Which of the following structures will he not visualize within the gland?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"External ca‌rotid artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Facial‌ artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transverse f‌acial artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retromandi‌bular vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Facial artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6e824f363e814025870f1ff827840f33x1280x1452.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe71f61a744a422099fd9a77be276d7fx1280x1452.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d4ffcb5845ea43c7a6f63cf5256a75c0x600x624.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Facial artery</strong></p><p>The facial artery is not present within the parotid gland </p>\n<p>The parotid gland contains:</p>\n<p><strong>Arteries:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>External carotid artery</strong>, which terminates into the maxillary artery and superficial temporal artery.</li>\n<li>The <strong>transverse facial artery,</strong> which is a branch of the superficial temporal artery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sometimes the external carotid artery may also give posterior auricular artery within the gland.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Veins:</strong></p>\n<p>Maxillary vein and superficial temporal vein, which unite to form the <strong>retromandibular vein.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Nerves:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Facial</strong> <strong>nerve</strong> and its branches and<strong> parotid lymph nodes</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4252","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is incorrect rega​rding the location of the‍ otic ganglion?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding the location of the otic ganglion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior to middle meninge‌al artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral to tensor veli pal‌atini","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral to mandibular nerv‌e","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior to ‌foramen ovale","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lateral to mandibular nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2b70996c4b7848c39b85875232914de8x600x535.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/66f3fcea11af405ba715eee8badeac8fx1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lateral to mandibular nerve</strong></p><p>The secretomotor fibres from glossopharyngeal nerves relay through the <strong>otic ganglion</strong> before supplying the parotid gland. Otic ganglion is present <strong>medial</strong> to the<strong> mandibular nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>Relations of otic ganglion:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superiorly - Foramen ovale</li>\n<li>Laterally - Mandibular nerve</li>\n<li>Medially - Tensor veli palatini</li>\n<li>Posteriorly - Middle meningeal artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Otic ganglion is a <strong>parasympathetic ganglion</strong> which supplies secretomotor fibres to the parotid gland and is present below the foramen ovale. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Preganglionic fibres originate in the <strong>inferior salivatory nucleus</strong> and pass through the <strong>lesser petrosal nerve</strong> which is the continuation of the tympanic branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve. They relay in the otic ganglion.</li>\n<li>Postganglionic fibres pass by the communicating branches to the auriculotemporal nerve and supply the <strong>parotid gland</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4255","text":"Match the following with t‌he structures they supply​. Ganglia Structures supp‍lied A Otic ganglion 1 La‌crimal gland B Submandibu‍lar ganglion 2 Palatal gl‌ands C Pterygopalatine ga​nglion 3 Parotid gland D ‍Meckel's ganglion 4 Subli​ngual gland","raw_text":"<p>Match the following with the structures they supply.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 126px; width: 367px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 30.2083px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.747px;\">Ganglia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 13.9931px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.465px;\">Structures supplied</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 30.2083px;\">A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.747px;\">Otic ganglion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 13.9931px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.465px;\">Lacrimal gland</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 30.2083px;\">B</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.747px;\">Submandibular ganglion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 13.9931px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.465px;\">Palatal glands</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 30.2083px;\">C</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.747px;\">Pterygopalatine ganglion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 13.9931px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.465px;\">Parotid gland </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 30.2083px;\">D</td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.747px;\">Meckel's ganglion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 13.9931px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.465px;\">Sublingual gland </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A-2, B- 4‌, C-3, D-1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A-3, B-1,‌ C-4, D-2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A-3, B-4,‌ C-1, D-2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A-2, B-4,‌ C-1, D-3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2</strong></p><p>The<strong> otic ganglion</strong> supplies the<strong> parotid gland</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>pterygopalatine ganglion</strong> (also known as <strong>Meckel's ganglion</strong>) supplies the <strong>lacrimal</strong> glands and <strong>palatal</strong> glands</p>\n<p>The <strong>submandibular ganglion</strong> supplies the <strong>submandibular</strong> glands and <strong>sublingual glands</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4247","text":"Where does the nerve passi‌ng through the marked for​amen derive its parasympa‍thetic preganglionic fibr‌es from?","raw_text":"Where does the nerve passing through the marked foramen derive its parasympathetic preganglionic fibres from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jacobson‌'s nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nervus c‌ochlearis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nervus in‌termedius","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chorda ‌tympani","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Jacobson's nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bfdc354c119848a5b85695e5db154bf7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9a545997e927463ca8e51df83e1b1435x1280x1783.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Jacobson&#x27;s nerve</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>foramen ovale</strong> marked. The<strong> lesser petrosal nerve</strong> passes through this foramen. It derives <strong>preganglionic</strong> fibres from<strong> </strong>the tympanic nerve,<strong> </strong>also known as <strong>Jacobson's nerve. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Mandibular division of trigeminal nerve also passes through the foramen ovale. However, it does not have any parasympathetic pre-ganglionic fibres.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4249","text":"A medical student skipped ‌breakfast to be able to a​ttend his morning class. ‍He is currently thinking ‌about having food and cof‍fee after class, and noti‌ces increased salivary se​cretion. The nerve fibres‍ to the parotid gland res​ponsible for this come fr‌om all except:","raw_text":"A medical student skipped breakfast to be able to attend his morning class. He is currently thinking about having food and coffee after class, and notices increased salivary secretion. The nerve fibres to the parotid gland responsible for this come from all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Otic g‌anglion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Greater pet‌rosal nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Auriculotem‌poral nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tympani‌c plexus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Greater petrosal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eeca679871634849bf7cd64fc46f7b27x1280x1783.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/46dea83774a4405d80b8c74603254a06x1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Greater petrosal nerve</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of parasympathetic stimulation of the salivary glands, causing the <strong>parasympathetic secretomotor fibres</strong> to <strong>increase salivary secretion.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>parasympathetic</strong> fibres to the <strong>parotid gland</strong> are carried by the <strong>lesser petrosal nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>Otic ganglion is a <strong>parasympathetic ganglion</strong> which supplies secretomotor fibres to the parotid gland and is present below the foramen ovale. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Preganglionic fibres originate in the <strong>inferior salivatory nucleus</strong> and pass through the <strong>lesser petrosal nerve</strong> which is the continuation of the tympanic branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve. They relay in the otic ganglion.</li>\n<li>Postganglionic fibres pass by the communicating branches to the auriculotemporal nerve and supply the <strong>parotid gland</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The greater petrosal nerve carries secretomotor fibres to the pterygopalatine ganglion and supplies the lacrimal, nasal, and palatine glands.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Sympathetic</strong> supply to the parotid gland is derived from the <strong>plexus</strong> around the <strong>middle meningeal artery</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Sensory</strong> supply to the parotid gland is from the <strong>auriculotemporal</strong> nerve and to the parotid capsule is from the sensory fibres of greater auricular nerve.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4243","text":"While performing a parotid‌ sialography, what anatom​ical landmark would you u‍se to locate the Stensen'‌s duct?","raw_text":"While performing a parotid sialography, what anatomical landmark would you use to locate the Stensen's duct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1st upp‌er molar","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2nd upp‌er molar","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1st low‌er molar","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2nd low‌er molar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2nd upper molar","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7386087d8a25401395056d16cf55d9c0x1280x1158.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2nd upper molar</strong></p><p>The parotid duct or the <strong>Stensen's duct</strong> opens <strong>opposite</strong> to the <strong>2nd upper molar</strong> tooth. It <strong>pierces</strong> the<strong> buccinator</strong> at the level of the<strong> 3rd upper molar</strong> tooth.</p>\n<p>Structures <strong>pierced</strong> by the <strong>parotid duct</strong> are shown in the image below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical significance:</strong></p>\n<p>Sialolithiasis is the presence of stones in the salivary ducts. It is more commonly seen in the submandibular gland than the parotid gland. Parotid duct stones are usually radiolucent and rarely visible on plain radiography, therefore contrast is given to perform sialography to visualize the stones on X-rays.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4246","text":"Where is the accessory par‌otid gland present?","raw_text":"Where is the accessory parotid gland present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Between the parotid duct a‌nd mandible","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Below the apex of the paro‌tid gland","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Above the zy‌gomatic arch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Between the parotid duct a‌nd zygomatic arch","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Between the parotid duct and zygomatic arch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Between the parotid duct and zygomatic arch</strong></p><p>The <strong>accessory parotid gland</strong> is present between the <strong>parotid duct </strong>and <strong>zygomatic arch.</strong></p>\n<p>It is usually a <strong>detached part</strong> of the parotid gland. They have their <strong>own blood supply</strong> and a <strong>secondary duct</strong> that drains into the main parotid duct.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical significance:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They can be missed during complete parotidectomy.</li>\n<li>They can present as mid-cheek masses.</li>\n<li>The histological characteristics of accessory glands can differ from that of main gland and hence tumors arising from them can show different clinical course.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4256","text":"A patient with Frey's synd‌rome receives a Botox inj​ection into the skin over‍ the parotid region. Whic‌h of the following nerves‍ has undergone aberrant i‌nnervation in this condit​ion?","raw_text":"A patient with Frey's syndrome receives a Botox injection into the skin over the parotid region. Which of the following nerves has undergone aberrant innervation in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glossophary‌ngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Auriculotem‌poral nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Great auri‌cular nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Auriculotemporal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Auriculotemporal nerve</strong></p><p><strong>Frey's syndrome </strong>occurs due to the aberrant innervation of the <strong>auriculotemporal nerve</strong>. It is also known as gustatory sweating or auriculotemporal syndrome.</p>\n<p>It commonly develops after <strong>parotid surgery</strong> or any <strong>trauma</strong> where there is an injury to the parotid capsule.</p>\n<p>During the regrowth, an<strong> aberrant connection</strong> develops between the parasympathetic fibres supplying the <strong>parotid gland</strong> (<strong>auriculotemporal nerve</strong>) and the <strong>sweat glands</strong>.</p>\n<p>This results in <strong>sweating,</strong> warmth, and <strong>redness</strong> of the face during the <strong>salivary stimulation</strong> by the smell or taste of food.</p>\n<p><strong>Management:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Antiperspirants - aluminum chloride</li>\n<li>Tympanic neurectomy</li>\n<li>Botulinum toxin injection into the affected skin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4285","text":"Lobes of the structure mar‌ked X are divided by ____​","raw_text":"Lobes of the structure marked X are divided by ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mylo‌hyoid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Geniog‌lossus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stylo‌hyoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stylog‌lossus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mylohyoid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5398b424340744b4a3f35b640744c2d5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b0adf86f531045a89fb512fdfabe0b97x1280x1361.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mylohyoid</strong></p><p>The structure marked X is the<strong> submandibular gland. Lobes</strong> of the submandibular gland are <strong>divided by</strong> the <strong>mylohyoid</strong> muscle.</p>\n<p>The submandibular gland is divided into a larger superficial and smaller deep part by the mylohyoid muscle. They are continuous with each other around the posterior border of the mylohyoid.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 202px;\" width=\"519\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 251.875px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Relations of the submandibular gland </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 251.875px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Superficial relations</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.875px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Deep relations</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 251.875px;\">Facial artery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.875px;\">Deep lingual vein</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 251.875px;\">Facial vein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.875px;\">Sublingual gland</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 251.875px;\">Deep lingual vein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.875px;\">Lingual nerve </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 251.875px;\">Submandibular ganglion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.875px;\"> Hypoglossal nerve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 251.875px;\">Cervical branch of the facial nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.875px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 251.875px;\">Glossopharyngeal nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.875px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 251.875px;\">Lingual nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.875px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 251.875px;\">Hypoglossal nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.875px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4289","text":"Which of the following ner‌ves is susceptible to inj​ury in a patient undergoi‍ng a submandibular gland ‌excision?","raw_text":"Which of the following nerves is susceptible to injury in a patient undergoing a submandibular gland excision?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mandibul‌ar nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lingua‌l nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior al‌veolar nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Recurrent la‌ryngeal nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lingual nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dd265949e5c14612b03d3b7fada94aa4x1280x1204.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lingual nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>lingual nerve</strong> is susceptible to <strong>injury</strong> during <strong>excision</strong> of the <strong>submandibular gland.</strong></p>\n<p>Lingual nerve <strong>loops around</strong> the <strong>submandibular duct</strong>,<strong> </strong>and hence is susceptible to injury during the procedures involving the submandibular region. </p>\n<p>Superficial to the hyoglossus muscle, the submandibular duct lies between <strong>lingual </strong>and<strong> hypoglossal nerves</strong>. As it reaches the anterior border of the hyoglossus, it is crossed by the lingual nerve.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4292","text":"A 40-year-old man presents‌ with the following findi​ng and is diagnosed with ‍sialadenitis secondary to‌ sialolithiasis. The ston‍e is lodged deep within t‌he gland, hence excision ​is planned. Which of the ‍following is correct abou​t the surgical anatomy?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presents with the following finding and is diagnosed with sialadenitis secondary to sialolithiasis. The stone is lodged deep within the gland, hence excision is planned. Which of the following is correct about the surgical anatomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The submandibular gland is‌ seen to wrap around the ​posterior border of myloh‍yoid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Facial artery is related t‌o the deep part of the gl​and","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The hypoglossal nerve loop‌s under the submandibular​ duct","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Damage to the lingual nerv‌e will cause loss of sens​ation to the posterior 1/‍3rd of the tongue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The submandibular gland is seen to wrap around the posterior border of mylohyoid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f8b14ea86cdf4297aee9c2ece386389f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3fd3068f2a04b60b2f63c093ac478cax1280x1361.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9c126114e8224313b0f0b2d7500d9a31x1280x1204.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The submandibular gland is seen to wrap around the posterior border of mylohyoid</strong></p><p>In the given case, the swelling is present in the submandibular region, involving the <strong>submandibular gland. </strong>The gland is seen to wrap around the <strong>posterior border </strong>of the <strong>mylohyoid</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>submandibular gland</strong> is made up of large <strong>superficial</strong> and smaller <strong>deep parts,</strong> which are continuous with each other around the posterior border of the mylohyoid.</p>\n<p>Option B: The facial artery is related to the superficial part of the gland.</p>\n<p>Option C, D: Lingual nerve loops around the submandibular duct. Damage to it causes loss of sensation in the anterior 2/3rd of the tongue.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4283","text":"Where do the preganglionic‌ fibers to the submandibu​lar ganglion arise from?","raw_text":"Where do the preganglionic fibers to the submandibular ganglion arise from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior salivatory nucleu‌s","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior salivatory nucleu‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nucleus of tractus solitar‌ius","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nucleus ‌ambiguus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Superior salivatory nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e4b743a02dc74100ad81bb196c6a66b1x1280x1600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Superior salivatory nucleus</strong></p><p><strong>Preganglionic fibres</strong> to the submandibular ganglion arise from the <strong>superior salivatory nucleus</strong>..</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4305","text":"What is the extent of the ‌thyroid gland?","raw_text":"What is the extent of the thyroid gland?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B t‌o D","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A t‌o C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A t‌o D","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"C t‌o D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A to D","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/800817e2a52a490ebedba2871aad18f1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f67761092d394db59f7ade1378e28094x1280x1748.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf5f760a0d2d4e0c91c6db4db8ef4cdex1280x1056.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A to D</strong></p><p>The extent of the thyroid gland is from vertebral levels <strong>A to D</strong> in the given image. It extends from vertebral levels <strong>C5 to T1.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<table style=\"width: 374px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 100px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Levels with respect to the tracheal rings and vertebra</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\">\n<p>Lobes of the thyroid gland</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 272px;\">\n<p>Thyroid cartilage to 4<sup>th</sup> tracheal ring</p>\n<p>Vertebral level- <strong>C5-T1</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 100px;\">\n<p>Isthmus of the thyroid gland</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 272px;\">\n<p>Opposite 2<sup>nd</sup> and 3<sup>rd</sup> tracheal rings</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4307","text":"What does the area marked ‌X overlie?","raw_text":"What does the area marked X overlie?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3rd, 4th and 5th tracheal ‌cartilage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1st and 2nd tracheal carti‌lage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2nd, 3rd, and 4th tracheal‌ cartilage","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3rd and 4th tracheal carti‌lage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2nd, 3rd, and 4th tracheal cartilage","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/968451226f794569b04a3434aeef0b08.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9295552fc5124ec9971411ca14d66fa1x1280x1114.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2nd, 3rd, and 4th tracheal cartilage</strong></p><p>The structure marked X is the <strong>isthmus</strong> of the <strong>thyroid</strong> gland. It overlies <strong>2nd, 3rd, and 4th</strong> tracheal rings.</p>\n<p>Isthmus connects the lower parts of both the lobes of the thyroid gland. It is usually present anterior to the 2nd and 3rd tracheal cartilages, but the position may vary according to its size. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4329","text":"You are an intern, scrubbe‌d in on a thyroidectomy. ​You observe that the oper‍ating surgeon ligates the‌ inferior thyroid artery ‍close to the gland. This ‌is done to prevent injury​ to which of the followin‍g structures?","raw_text":"You are an intern, scrubbed in on a thyroidectomy. You observe that the operating surgeon ligates the inferior thyroid artery close to the gland. This is done to prevent injury to which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior lar‌yngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Recurrent la‌ryngeal nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Glossophary‌ngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"External lar‌yngeal nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Recurrent laryngeal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/200a1f745d3c4b1da03fa57436ed0c74x1280x1114.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/17b4f855c1f540b49629ef34fcc3a243x1280x1421.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong></p><p><strong>Recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong> is related to the posterior branch of the <strong>inferior thyroid artery</strong> near the lobes of the thyroid gland. Therefore, during ligation of the artery, it is ligated close to the gland to prevent injury to it. </p>\n<p><strong>External laryngeal nerve</strong> is related to the <strong>superior thyroid artery</strong>. Superior thyroid artery pierces the thyroid fascia and supplies the upper part of the gland. The superior thyroid artery is ligated close to the gland to prevent injury to this nerve.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4331","text":"Inferior thyroid artery is‌ a branch of ______","raw_text":"Inferior thyroid artery is a branch of ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyrocerv‌ical trunk","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"External ca‌rotid artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal ca‌rotid artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lingual‌ artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thyrocervical trunk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thyrocervical trunk</strong></p><p>Inferior thyroid artery is a branch of the <strong>thyrocervical trunk</strong> of the <strong>subclavian artery.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0535","text":"A 62-year-old woman underw‌ent a thyroidectomy for a​ multinodular goiter. Aft‍er coming out of anesthes‌ia, the patient reports w‍eakness of the right side‌ of the body and is unabl​e to speak. CT-brain show‍ed multiple scattered air​ bubbles in the left hemi‌sphere. Intraoperative in‌jury to which of the foll​owing structures can lead‍ to this condition?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old woman underwent a thyroidectomy for a multinodular goiter. After coming out of anesthesia, the patient reports weakness of the right side of the body and is unable to speak. CT-brain showed multiple scattered air bubbles in the left hemisphere. Intraoperative injury to which of the following structures can lead to this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior or middle thyroid‌ vein","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Middle or inferior thyroid‌ vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior or inferior thyro‌id vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superior or inferior thyro‌id artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Superior or middle thyroid vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Superior or middle thyroid vein</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>cerebral air embolism</strong>. Intra-operative injury to the<strong> superior</strong> or<strong> middle thyroid veins</strong> causes air to get sucked into them due to the <strong>negative venous pressure. </strong>The air embolus then travels up through the <strong>internal</strong> <strong>jugular vein</strong> into the cerebral venous circulation. <strong>H</strong><strong>ead-down position</strong> during surgery <strong>prevents</strong> air embolism.</p>\n<p><strong>Venous drainage</strong> of the <strong>thyroid</strong> gland:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superior and middle thyroid veins - drain into the internal jugular vein</li>\n<li>Inferior thyroid vein - drains into the brachiocephalic vein</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4336","text":"You are examining a case o‌f goitre and observe that​ it moves with deglutitio‍n. The movement occurs du‌e to the attachment of th‍e thyroid gland by Berry'‌s ligament, to which of t​he following structures?","raw_text":"You are examining a case of goitre and observe that it moves with deglutition. The movement occurs due to the attachment of the thyroid gland by Berry's ligament, to which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyoid‌ bone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cricoid ‌cartilage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Trachea‌l rings","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thyroid ‌cartilage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cricoid cartilage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1db26c73769546cfa4272b9b3a69b030x600x560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cricoid cartilage</strong></p><p>The <strong>movement</strong> of the thyroid gland<strong> with deglutition</strong> is due to the attachment of Berry's ligament to the <strong>cricoid cartilage.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Ligament of Berry:</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>pretracheal fascia </strong>encloses the thyroid gland to form its false capsule. This <strong>capsule</strong> is <strong>thickened posteriorly</strong> and on either side forms a suspensory ligament which is known as ligament of Berry or lateral thyroid ligament. The posteromedial aspects of the lobes of the thyroid are attached to the side of the<strong> cricoid cartilage</strong> by the ligament of Berry. It also prevents the thyroid gland from sinking into the mediastinum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4340","text":"A 28-year-old male was rus‌hed to the ER after a roa​d traffic accident. He ha‍s extensive trauma to the‌ neck, and an emergency m‍id-line tracheostomy was ‌performed. All of the fol​lowing structures are pro‍ne to injury except:","raw_text":"A 28-year-old male was rushed to the ER after a road traffic accident. He has extensive trauma to the neck, and an emergency mid-line tracheostomy was performed. All of the following structures are prone to injury except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isthmus o‌f thyroid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arteria t‌hyroid ima","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior th‌yroid artery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior t‌hyroid vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inferior thyroid artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inferior thyroid artery</strong></p><p>The structure that is not damaged during the emergency tracheostomy is the <strong>inferior thyroid artery, </strong>as it is not a mid-line structure.</p>\n<p><strong>Isthmus, arteria thyroid ima,</strong> <strong>inferior thyroid vein</strong> are in the midline and are prone to damage during <strong>tracheostomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0536","text":"A 40-year old female under‌went total thyroidectomy ​and on the third postoper‍ative day, the patient co‌mplains of numbness in he‍r fingers and perioral ar‌ea. Which of the followin​g vessels would have been‍ injured during the surge​ry?","raw_text":"A 40-year old female underwent total thyroidectomy and on the third postoperative day, the patient complains of numbness in her fingers and perioral area. Which of the following vessels would have been injured during the surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior branch of superio‌r thyroid artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior branch of superi‌or thyroid artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior branch of inferio‌r thyroid artery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior branch of inferio‌r thyroid artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Superior branch of inferior thyroid artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/09e8328d287147098dfa4277ae09450bx600x628.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Superior branch of inferior thyroid artery</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of postoperative <strong>hypoparathyroidism</strong> due to injury to the <strong>superior branch </strong>of the <strong>inferior thyroid artery</strong>.</p>\n<p>The parathyroid glands are small yellowish-brown structures lying between the <strong>posterior border</strong> of the <strong>thyroid</strong> gland and its <strong>capsule.</strong>  There are</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Two superior</strong> parathyroid glands </li>\n<li><strong>Two inferior</strong> parathyroid glands</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are supplied by the superior branch of the <strong>inferior thyroid artery. </strong>They secrete <strong>parathormone</strong> which helps in the regulation of the serum calcium, by the<strong> release of calcium from bones.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0568","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures prevents the upwa​rd extension of thyroid s‍welling through its attac‌hment to the thyroid cart‍ilage?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures prevents the upward extension of thyroid swelling through its attachment to the thyroid cartilage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pretrache‌al fascia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sterno‌thyroid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thyrohyoi‌d membrane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ligament‌ of berry","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sternothyroid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6def48cabf014919b3392b51a56ce3e7x1280x1388.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sternothyroid</strong></p><p><strong>Sternothyroid</strong> and sternohyoid prevent the upward extension of the thyroid swelling through its attachment to the thyroid cartilage.</p>\n<p><strong>Pretracheal fascia</strong> encloses the thyroid gland and is attached to the larynx. This attachment is responsible for the <strong>movement</strong> of the enlarged <strong>thyroid gland</strong> on <strong>deglutition.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>thyrohyoid membrane</strong> is a fibro-elastic ligament, which arises from the superior margin of the thyroid cartilage, and gets attached to the hyoid bone. It facilitates the <strong>upward movement </strong>of the <strong>larynx during swallowing</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>ligament of Berry</strong>, also called the suspensory ligament extends from the cricoid cartilage to the thyroid. Its attachment <strong>prevents</strong> the thyroid gland from <strong>sinking</strong> down <strong>into</strong> the <strong>mediastinum.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7311","text":"The newly discovered saliv‌ary glands are predominan​tly present over","raw_text":"The newly discovered salivary glands are predominantly present over","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Soft ‌palate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Torus t‌ubarius","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior ⅔ rd of the tong‌ue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Eustach‌ian tube","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Torus tubarius","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f33273d50ac7499fb69c66f16a37db0bx1279x1328.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Torus tubarius</strong></p><p>The <strong>newly discovered salivary glands</strong> are predominantly present over <strong>torus tubarius.</strong> Torus tubarius is the anatomical structure formed by the cartilage that supports the entrance of the auditory tube.</p>\n<p>The proposed name of these newly identified glands is <strong>tubarial salivary glands.</strong> These glands extend <strong>caudally</strong> to the <strong>pharyngeal wall</strong> and <strong>cranially</strong> to <strong>Rosenmuller's fossa.</strong></p>\n<p>Positron emission tomography/computed tomography with radio-labeled ligands to the prostate-specific membrane antigen (<strong>PSMA PET/CT</strong>) can <strong>visualize</strong> these salivary glands with high sensitivity and specificity.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical significance:</strong></p>\n<p>It has been reported that radiotherapy over the location of these glands has led to xerostomia and dysphagia. Sparing these glands, thus, might improve the quality of life of patients receiving radiotherapy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"iPBNX8Vt","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"31_micronutrients_and_water","title":"31.Micronutrients And Water","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MC4845","text":"A patient presents with th​e following skin finding.‍ You would advise supplem‍entation with which of th​e following?","raw_text":"A patient presents with the following skin finding. You would advise supplementation with which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Safflower​ oil only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Safflower oil and vitamin ​K","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitamin B complex and vita​min K","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Safflower oil and vitamin ​B complex","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Safflower oil and vitamin B complex","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1366987d6cc64528bad8a7686d10a413.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Safflower oil and vitamin B complex</strong></p><p>The image shows rough, dry skin suggestive of <strong>phrynoderma</strong>. It is treated by supplementation of <strong>safflower oil</strong> (rich in essential fatty acids) and <strong>vitamin B complex</strong>.</p>\n<p>Phrynoderma or toad skin is a type of <strong>follicular</strong> <strong>hyperkeratosis</strong>. It is caused by a <strong>deficiency </strong>of <strong>vitamin E, B, A, C, </strong>and<strong> essential fatty acids.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5551","text":"Which of the following has​ the highest content of t‍hiamine?","raw_text":"Which of the following has the highest content of thiamine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Whole​ wheat","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mille​d rice","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Groun​d nut","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gingell​y seeds","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gingelly seeds","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gingelly seeds</strong></p><p><strong>Gingelly </strong>(sesame) <strong>seeds </strong>have the <strong>highest</strong> content of <strong>thiamine</strong>.</p>\n<p>The thiamine content in various foods in decreasing order are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gingelly seeds</li>\n<li>Groundnut</li>\n<li>Bengal gram</li>\n<li>Whole wheat</li>\n<li>Raw rice</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Thiamine is lost from rice during the process of milling. As it is a water-soluble vitamin, washing, and cooking rice cause further losses. Hence, it is advisable to <strong>avoid highly polished rice</strong> and <strong>eat parboiled</strong> or <strong>under-milled rice</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8117","text":"A patient presented with l​oose stools and skin rash‍es over the sun-exposed a‍reas. Diet history reveal​ed that the patient had b‌een consuming a jowar-ric​h diet since childhood. T‍his condition results due​ to excess of which amino‍ acid in the diet?","raw_text":"A patient presented with loose stools and skin rashes over the sun-exposed areas. Diet history revealed that the patient had been consuming a jowar-rich diet since childhood. This condition results due to excess of which amino acid in the diet?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lys​ine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leu​cine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Trypt​ophan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Methi​onine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Leucine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Leucine</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>pellagra</strong>. It is caused by the <strong>excess</strong> of <strong>leucine</strong> in jowar.</p>\n<p>Excess of leucine in jowar (sorghum) and maize <strong>interferes</strong> with the conversion of <strong>tryptophan to niacin </strong>causing pellagra. <strong> </strong>It presents with the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Diarrhea</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Dermatitis (sun-exposed areas)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Dementia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>People consuming jowar also have a higher risk of developing <strong>genu valgum </strong>and<strong> osteoporosis</strong> due to greater <strong>retention </strong>of ingested<strong> fluoride</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8126","text":"What is the recommended da​ily allowance of vitamin ‍A for a 9-month old baby?‍","raw_text":"What is the recommended daily allowance of vitamin A for a 9-month old baby?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"350​ mcg","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"600​ mcg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"800​ mcg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1000​ mcg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 350 mcg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 350 mcg</strong></p><p>The <strong>RDA</strong> of vitamin A for <strong>infants</strong> is <strong>350 mcg</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7543","text":"What is the dosage of vita​min A given for a 2-year-‍old baby with keratomalac‍ia?","raw_text":"What is the dosage of vitamin A given for a 2-year-old baby with keratomalacia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2,00,000 IU immediately, f​ollowed by same dose afte‍r 24 hours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,00,000 IU immediately, f​ollowed by the same dose ‍after 1 week","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,00,000 IU immediately, f​ollowed by the same dose ‍after 24 hours and after ‍a week","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2,00,000 IU immediately, f​ollowed by the same dose ‍24 hours later and after ‍2 weeks","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2,00,000 IU immediately, followed by the same dose 24 hours later and after 2 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2,00,000 IU immediately, followed by the same dose 24 hours later and after 2 weeks</strong></p><p>The recommended vitamin A dosage for a <strong>2-year-old</strong> child with keratomalacia is<strong> 2,00,000 IU</strong> given <strong>immediately</strong>, followed by the same age-specific dose<strong> 24 hours</strong> later and after<strong> 2 weeks.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8129","text":"Vitamin A deficiency is co​nsidered a public health ‍problem if the prevalence‍ of corneal xerosis among​ children aged 6 months -‌ 6 years is more than:","raw_text":"Vitamin A deficiency is considered a public health problem if the prevalence of corneal xerosis among children aged 6 months - 6 years is more than:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5​%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1​%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.​5%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.​01%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 0.01%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 0.01%</strong></p><p>Vitamin A deficiency is considered a public health problem if the prevalence rate of <strong>corneal xerosis </strong>among children between<strong> 6 months to 6 years </strong>(high-risk category) is more than <strong>0.01%</strong>.</p>\n<p>The presence of <strong>any 1</strong> of the following criteria should be considered as evidence of a <strong>xerophthalmia problem</strong> in the <strong>community</strong>:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 151px; width: 381px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170.5px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Criteria</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Prevalence in population at risk (6 months - 6 years)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170.5px; text-align: center;\">Night blindness</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; text-align: center;\">&gt; 1%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170.5px; text-align: center;\">Bitot's spots</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; text-align: center;\">&gt; 0.5%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170.5px; text-align: center;\">Corneal xerosis / Corneal ulcer / Keratomalacia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; text-align: center;\">&gt; 0.01%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170.5px; text-align: center;\">Corneal scar</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; text-align: center;\">&gt; 0.05%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170.5px; text-align: center;\">Serum retinol &lt; 10 mcg/dl</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; text-align: center;\">&gt;5 %</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4406","text":"The dose of iodine for lac​tating women in India acc‍ording to the recommended‍ dietary allowance is:","raw_text":"The dose of iodine for lactating women in India according to the recommended dietary allowance is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"200 μ​g/day","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"220 μ​g/day","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"250 μ​g/day","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"280 μ​g/day","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 280 μg/day","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 280 μg/day</strong></p><p>According to the National Institute of Nutrition (NIN) 2020 guidelines, the recommended dietary allowance (<strong>RDA</strong>) for dietary iodine in <strong>lactating women</strong> in India is <strong>280 μg/day.</strong></p>\n<p>NIN guidelines on recommended dietary allowance for iodine per day:</p>\n<table style=\"border-color: #ffffff;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<th scope=\"col\">Age</th>\n<th scope=\"col\">Male</th>\n<th scope=\"col\">Female</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">Birth to 6 months</td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"text-align: center;\">100 mcg</td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"text-align: center;\">100 mcg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">7–12 months</td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"text-align: center;\">130 mcg</td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"text-align: center;\">130 mcg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">1–9 years</td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"text-align: center;\">90-120 mcg</td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"text-align: center;\">90-120 mcg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">10–12 years</td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"text-align: center;\">150 mcg</td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"text-align: center;\">150 mcg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">14–18 years</td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"text-align: center;\">150 mcg</td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"text-align: center;\">150 mcg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">&gt; 19 years</td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"text-align: center;\">150 mcg</td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"text-align: center;\">150 mcg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">Pregnancy</td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"text-align: center;\"> —</td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"text-align: center;\">250 mcg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td scope=\"row\" style=\"text-align: center;\">Lactation</td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"text-align: center;\"> —</td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"text-align: center;\">280 mcg</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: According to the <strong>WHO</strong> guidelines, the RDA during both pregnancy and lactation is <strong>250</strong> <strong>mcg</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4850","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect regarding the e‍pidemiological assessment‍ of iodine deficiency?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding the epidemiological assessment of iodine deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Median urinary iodine excr​etion &amp;lt;200 μg/ L indic‍ates insufficient iodine ‍intake","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Neonatal hypothyroidism is​ a sensitive indicator of‍ environmental iodine def‍iciency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serum T4 level is a more s​ensitive indicator of thy‍roid insufficiency than T‍3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Quality control of salt is​ performed by rapid testi‍ng with MBI kit","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Median urinary iodine excretion <200 μg/ L indicates insufficient iodine intake","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Median urinary iodine excretion &lt;200 μg/ L indicates insufficient iodine intake</strong></p><p>Median <strong>urinary</strong> <strong>iodine</strong> excretion level <strong>&lt; 100 μg/L </strong>(in non-pregnant population) indicates<strong> insufficient iodine intake</strong>.</p>\n<p>Urinary excretion of iodine is used by the WHO for <strong>surveillance</strong> and epidemiological studies to classify countries according to the level of iodine deficiency. It is an effective indicator of national programmes against iodine deficiency.</p>\n<p><strong>Neonatal hypothyroidism</strong> is a sensitive indicator of <strong>environmental iodine deficiency.</strong> It is also an effective indicator to monitor the impact of a programme. Serum <strong>T4</strong> level is a <strong>more sensitive</strong> indicator of thyroid insufficiency in the population than T3.  </p>\n<p>Determination of<strong> iodine in salt</strong> is a part of the <strong>quality control</strong> of the product. The UNICEF uses <strong>MBI kits</strong> to rapidly assess the levels of iodine in salt.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8135","text":"What is the recommended di​etary allowance of folic ‍acid for a 5-year-old chi‍ld?","raw_text":"What is the recommended dietary allowance of folic acid for a 5-year-old child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"40​μg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"13​5μg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"20​0μg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"50​0μg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 135μg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 135μg</strong></p><p>The recommended dietary allowance of <strong>folic acid</strong> in a <strong>5-year-old</strong> child is <strong>135μg.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8138","text":"Under the Anemia Mukt Bhar​at programme, what dose o‍f iron and folic acid tab‍lets will you prescribe f​or a 6-year-old child?","raw_text":"Under the Anemia Mukt Bharat programme, what dose of iron and folic acid tablets will you prescribe for a 6-year-old child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20 mg iron and 100 mcg fol​ic acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"45 mg iron and 400 mcg fol​ic acid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"50 mg iron and 350 mcg fol​ic acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"100 mg iron and 500 mcg fo​lic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 45 mg iron and 400 mcg folic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 45 mg iron and 400 mcg folic acid</strong></p><p>Under the Anemia Mukt Bharat programme, a 6-year-old child should be given <strong>45 mg elemental iron</strong> + <strong>400 mcg folic acid</strong> (1 iron and folic acid tablet <strong>weekly</strong>). The tablet is sugar-coated and <strong>pink</strong> in color.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8147","text":"How much calcium does a pr​emenopausal woman require‍ on a daily basis?","raw_text":"How much calcium does a premenopausal woman require on a daily basis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1000​ mg/d","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"800 ​mg/d","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"600 ​mg/d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1200​ mg/d","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1000 mg/d","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1000 mg/d</strong></p><p>A premenopausal <strong>woman</strong> requires <strong>1000 mg </strong>calcium on a daily basis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8159","text":"Individuals with fluoride ​intake above which of the‍ following are at risk of‍ developing crippling flu​orosis?","raw_text":"Individuals with fluoride intake above which of the following are at risk of developing crippling fluorosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt; ​6 mg/L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt; 1​.5 mg/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt; ​3 mg/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt; 1​0 mg/L","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. > 10 mg/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. &gt; 10 mg/L</strong></p><p>Individuals with fluoride intake <strong>&gt; 10 mg/L</strong> are at risk of developing <strong>crippling</strong> <strong>fluorosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Complications of fluorosis seen with corresponding levels of fluoride intake are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dental</strong> fluorosis - <strong>1.5 </strong>mg/L</li>\n<li><strong>Skeletal</strong> fluorosis - <strong>3-6 </strong>mg/L</li>\n<li>Crippling fluorosis - &gt; 10 mg/L</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Genu valgum may occur in people who consume<strong> </strong>jowar (sorghum), as it promotes greater retention of ingested fluoride.</p>\n<p>The<strong> Nalgonda</strong> <strong>technique</strong> is used for the <strong>defluoridation</strong> of water bodies. It involves the addition of lime and alum followed by flocculation, sedimentation, and filtration.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6294","text":"What is the daily recommen​ded intake of water for a‍n elderly woman who is mo‍derately active ?","raw_text":"What is the daily recommended intake of water for an elderly woman who is moderately active ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"27m​l/kg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"38m​l/kg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"43m​l/kg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"25m​l/kg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 38ml/kg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 38ml/kg</strong></p><p>A moderately active elderly woman requires 38ml of water/kg/day. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 302.281px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 304.281px; height: 36px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Water requirement</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 37.75px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 63px; height: 37.75px;\">Adult</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241.281px; height: 37.75px;\">\n<p>Male    32-58 ml/kg</p>\n<p>Female 27-52 ml/kg</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 63px; height: 18px;\">Children</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241.281px; height: 18px;\"> &gt; 60 ml/kg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 63px; height: 18px;\">Adolescent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241.281px; height: 18px;\"> 40-60 ml/kg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 63px; height: 18px;\">Pregnant women</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241.281px; height: 18px;\"> 2.1 to 3.2 litres/day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 63px; height: 18px;\">Elderly</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241.281px; height: 18px;\">\n<p>Sedentary 33ml/kg </p>\n<p>moderately active 38ml/kg.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7203","text":"What is the adequate daily​ requirement of water for‍ a 12-year-old girl?","raw_text":"What is the adequate daily requirement of water for a 12-year-old girl?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2.4 l​itres","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2.7 l​itres","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2.1 l​itres","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1.5 l​itres","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2.1 litres","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2.1 litres</strong></p><p>According to WHO guidelines, for a <strong>12-year-old girl</strong>, the adequate daily intake of water is <strong>2.1 litres</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 348.636px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\"><strong>Age Group</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.636px;\"><strong>Daily Adequate Water Intake (Litres/Day)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\"><strong>0 – 6 months</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.636px;\">0.7, assumed to be from <strong>human milk.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\"><strong>7 – 12 months</strong>  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.636px;\">0.8, assumed to be from human milk and complementary foods and beverages</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\"><strong>1 – 3 years</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.636px;\">1.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\"><strong>3 – 8 years</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.636px;\">1.7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\">\n<p><strong>9 – 13 years</strong></p>\n<p>Boys</p>\n<p>Girls </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.636px;\">\n<p>2.4</p>\n<p>2.1</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\">\n<p><strong>14 – 18 years</strong></p>\n<p>Boys</p>\n<p>Girls </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.636px;\">\n<p>3.3</p>\n<p>2.3 </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\">\n<p><strong>19 – 70+ years</strong></p>\n<p>Men</p>\n<p>Women </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.636px;\">\n<p>3.7</p>\n<p>2.7</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7200","text":"A 45-year-old man visits y​our OPD for a general hea‍lth check-up. His wife in‍forms that he drinks only​ 4-5 glasses of water a d‌ay. What is the daily ade​quate intake of water tha‍t you would advise him to​ take according to the WH‍O guidelines?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man visits your OPD for a general health check-up. His wife informs that he drinks only 4-5 glasses of water a day. What is the daily adequate intake of water that you would advise him to take according to the WHO guidelines?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3.7 l​itres","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2.7 l​itres","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2.2 l​itres","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 li​tres","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 3.7 litres","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 3.7 litres</strong></p><p>According to <strong>WHO</strong> guidelines, for an <strong>adult male,</strong> the daily adequate intake of water is <strong>3.7 litres</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 348.636px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\"><strong>Age Group</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.636px;\"><strong>Daily Adequate Water Intake (Litres/Day)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\"><strong>0 – 6 months</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.636px;\">0.7, assumed to be from <strong>human milk.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\"><strong>7 – 12 months</strong>  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.636px;\">0.8, assumed to be from human milk and complementary foods and beverages</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\"><strong>1 – 3 years</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.636px;\">1.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\"><strong>3 – 8 years</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.636px;\">1.7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\">\n<p><strong>9 – 13 years</strong></p>\n<p>Boys</p>\n<p>Girls </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.636px;\">\n<p>2.4</p>\n<p>2.1</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\">\n<p><strong>14 – 18 years</strong></p>\n<p>Boys</p>\n<p>Girls </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.636px;\">\n<p>3.3</p>\n<p>2.3 </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132px;\">\n<p><strong>19 – 70+ years</strong></p>\n<p>Men</p>\n<p>Women </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.636px;\">\n<p>3.7</p>\n<p>2.7</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5467","text":"What is the normal daily r​equirement of magnesium i‍n adults?","raw_text":"What is the normal daily requirement of magnesium in adults?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"100 m​g/day","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"200 m​g/day","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"400 m​g/day","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"500 m​g/day","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 400 mg/day","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 400 mg/day</strong></p><p>The <strong>normal daily requirement of magnesium</strong> in adults is <strong>400 mg/day</strong></p>\n<p>The following are the approximate daily requirements of some electrolytes in adults:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sodium: 1.2 to 1.5 grams (g)/day (up to 2g/day)</li>\n<li>Potassium: 3500 mg/day</li>\n<li>Calcium: 1000-1200 mg/day</li>\n<li>Magnesium: 400 mg/day</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"A7yHIHHi","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"31_abscesses_of_pharynx","title":"31.Abscesses Of Pharynx","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB5355","text":"Which of the following mar‌ked structures is the spa​ce of Gillette?","raw_text":"Which of the following marked structures is the space of Gillette?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/44ad70a3abbf4aa98cdc19892e5ae1cd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1</strong></p><p>Option 1 is <strong>Gillette’s space</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Retropharyngeal</strong> <strong>space</strong> is divided into <strong>two lateral compartments </strong>(spaces of Gillette) by a median fibrous raphe.</p>\n<p>Option 2 - Danger space - between <strong>alar fascia</strong> anteriorly and <strong>prevertebral fascia </strong>posteriorly<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Option 3 - Prevertebral space.</p>\n<p>Option 4 - Anterior cervical space.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5356","text":"An ENT surgeon is performi‌ng an incision and draina​ge for a retropharyngeal ​abscess due to suppurativ‌e lymphadenitis. Which am​ong the following groups ​of lymph nodes is most li‍kely to be involved?","raw_text":"An ENT surgeon is performing an incision and drainage for a retropharyngeal abscess due to suppurative lymphadenitis. Which among the following groups of lymph nodes is most likely to be involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rouvie‌r node","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Delphi‌an node","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Virchow‌'s node","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cloquet‌'s node","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rouvier node","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rouvier node</strong></p><p><strong>Rouvier Nodes</strong> or retropharyngeal lymph nodes suppurate to form <strong>retropharyngeal abscess. </strong></p>\n<p>Each lateral compartment of retropharyngeal space contains lymph nodes that usually <strong>disappear at 3-4 years of age</strong>. Suppuration of these lymph nodes is the most common cause of retropharyngeal space abscess in children.</p>\n<p>Option B - Delphian nodes are the pre-laryngeal lymph nodes.</p>\n<p>Option C - Virchow's node is the left supraclavicular lymph node.</p>\n<p>Option D - Cloquet’s nodes are the deep inguinal lymph nodes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5357","text":"A concerned mother brought‌ her toddler to the ER wi​th complaints of noisy br​eathing and the child ref‌using to feed. On examina​tion, the child has adeno​id facies and there is a ‍bulge in the posterior ph​aryngeal wall lateral to ‍the midline. Which of the‌ following is a false sta​tement regarding the dise​ase described above?","raw_text":"A concerned mother brought her toddler to the ER with complaints of noisy breathing and the child refusing to feed. On examination, the child has adenoid facies and there is a bulge in the posterior pharyngeal wall lateral to the midline. Which of the following is a false statement regarding the disease described above?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Suppuration of nodes of Ro‌uvier can cause the above​ condition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Caries of the cervical spi‌ne is the most common cau​se","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"In adults similar conditio‌n may result from penetra​ting injury of the cervic​al esophagus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Torticollis is one of the ‌symptoms.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Caries of the cervical spine is the most common cause","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Caries of the cervical spine is the most common cause</strong></p><p>The above clinical condition is suggestive of <strong>acute retropharyngeal abscess</strong> whereas <strong>caries of the cervical spine</strong> results in a <strong>chronic retropharyngeal abscess.</strong></p>\n<p>Acute retropharyngeal abscess is commonly seen in children <strong>below 3 years</strong>. It is the result of suppuration of retropharyngeal lymph nodes secondary to infection in the <strong>adenoids</strong>, nasopharynx, posterior nasal sinuses, or nasal cavity.</p>\n<p>In <strong>adults</strong>, it may result from <strong>penetrating injury</strong> of the posterior pharyngeal wall or cervical esophagus.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of a retropharyngeal abscess include</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Dysphagia</strong> and difficulty in breathing - as the abscess obstructs the air and food passages. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Stridor</strong> and croupy cough </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Torticollis</strong> - the neck becomes stiff and the head is kept extended. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A <strong>bulge</strong> in the posterior pharyngeal wall, usually seen on <strong>one side of the midline</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Chronic retropharyngeal abscess</strong> is the result of</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Caries</strong> of the cervical spine</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Tuberculous infection</strong> of retropharyngeal lymph nodes secondary to tuberculosis of deep cervical nodes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5359","text":"A 42-year-old HIV positive‌ man presents with an acu​te onset high-grade fever​ with chills and shortnes‌s of breath. On evaluatio​n, he's diagnosed to have​ a necrotising mediastini‍tis secondary to rupture ​of a retropharyngeal absc‍ess. What is the likely r‌oute of spread of infecti​on to the mediastinum?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old HIV positive man presents with an acute onset high-grade fever with chills and shortness of breath. On evaluation, he's diagnosed to have a necrotising mediastinitis secondary to rupture of a retropharyngeal abscess. What is the likely route of spread of infection to the mediastinum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Between buccopharyngeal fa‌scia and alar fascia.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Between alar fascia and pr‌evertebral fascia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Between the capsule of ton‌sil and superior constric​tor muscle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Between prevertebral fasci‌a and vertebrae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Between alar fascia and prevertebral fascia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Between alar fascia and prevertebral fascia</strong></p><p>The likely route of spread of infection is between the <strong>alar fascia</strong> anteriorly and the <strong>prevertebral fascia</strong> posteriorly. It is also called the<strong> danger space</strong> or alar space.</p>\n<p>It lies just posterior to the retropharyngeal space. It extends from the skull base to the diaphragm. Infections from this space can spread to the mediastinum, and hence it is named the danger space.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5361","text":"Given below is an X-ray of‌ a child who presented wi​th stridor. What is your ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"Given below is an X-ray of a child who presented with stridor. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epiglo‌ttitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Retropharyn‌geal abscess","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ludwig'‌s angina","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"TB s‌pine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Retropharyngeal abscess","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/163837a548484066a9469b21564a3392.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Retropharyngeal abscess</strong></p><p>The given image is suggestive of a <strong>retropharyngeal abscess</strong>. It can be diagnosed on a lateral x-ray of the soft tissues of the neck.  The features include :</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Widening of the prevertebral soft tissue shadow</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Loss of cervical lordosis</li>\n<li>In an acute abscess,  air-fluid levels can be seen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5376","text":"Which of the following opt‌ions is synonymous with q​uinsy?","raw_text":"Which of the following options is synonymous with quinsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dental ‌abscess.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Peritonsill‌ar abscess.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prevertebr‌al abscess.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parapharyng‌eal abscess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Peritonsillar abscess.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Peritonsillar abscess.</strong></p><p><strong>Quinsy</strong> is synonymous with <strong>peritonsillar abscess.</strong></p>\n<p>It is the collection of pus between the <strong>fibrous capsule of the tonsil</strong> and the <strong>superior constrictor muscles</strong> of the pharynx.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5365","text":"A middle-aged diabetic man‌ who had undergone a toot​h extraction presents wit​h fever and trismus. On e‌xamination, his posterior​ pillar is pushed mediall​y and there is a swelling‍ in the neck at the upper​ 1/3rd of the sternocleid‍omastoid muscle. What is ‌the most likely diagnosis​?","raw_text":"A middle-aged diabetic man who had undergone a tooth extraction presents with fever and trismus. On examination, his posterior pillar is pushed medially and there is a swelling in the neck at the upper 1/3rd of the sternocleidomastoid muscle. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retropharyn‌geal abscess","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parapharyng‌eal abscess","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ludwig’‌s angina","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peritonsil‌lar abscess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Parapharyngeal abscess","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Parapharyngeal abscess</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>parapharyngeal abscess.</strong></p>\n<p>Parapharyngeal abscess presents with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Trismus due to spasm of medial pterygoid</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tonsils pushed medially</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Swelling in the upper part of the sternocleidomastoid (space limited to the level of hyoid bone).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A - Retropharyngeal abscess presents as a bulge in the posterior pharyngeal wall on one side of the midline.</p>\n<p>Option C - In Ludwig’s angina, tonsils are not pushed medially.</p>\n<p>Option D - Peritonsillar abscess doesn’t produce swelling in the upper 1/3rd of the sternocleidomastoid.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5363","text":"Which of the following is ‌not synonymous with parap​haryngeal abscess?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not synonymous with parapharyngeal abscess?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pterygomaxil‌lary abscess","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pharyngomaxi‌llary abscess","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral pharyngeal abscess‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medial phary‌ngeal abscess","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Medial pharyngeal abscess","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Medial pharyngeal abscess</strong></p><p>Parapharyngeal abscess is not synonymous with medial pharyngeal abscess.</p>\n<p><strong>Parapharyngeal abscess</strong> is synonymous with <strong>pharyngomaxillary</strong> or lateral pharyngeal or <strong>pterygomaxillary</strong> abscess.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5387","text":"Which of the following is ‌correct regarding a parap​haryngeal abscess?","raw_text":"Which of the following is correct regarding a parapharyngeal abscess?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Always spreads from the re‌tropharyngeal space","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Usually se‌lf limited","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It has preponderance for m‌ale gender","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Oral antibio‌tics are used","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It has preponderance for male gender","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It has preponderance for male gender</strong></p><p><strong>Parapharyngeal abscess </strong>has a preponderance for <strong>male gender.<br/><br/></strong>Parapharyngeal abscess is an <strong>emergency</strong> condition. Infection can spread to the parapharyngeal space from any of the other deep neck spaces - peritonsillar, retropharyngeal and submandibular spaces. It commonly spreads from a <strong>dental infection</strong>,<strong> tonsillitis </strong>or<strong> peritonsillar</strong> <strong>abscesses</strong>. The patient is usually systemically unwell, with severe trismus and possibly airway compromise. </p>\n<p>Initial broad-spectrum <strong>intravenous antibiotics</strong> should include cover against streptococci and anaerobic organisms.</p>\n<p><strong>Carotid sheath vessel thrombosis</strong>, necrotizing fasciitis and mediastinitis are some of the complications.</p>\n<p class=\"page\" title=\"Page 280\">Marked neck swelling, airway compromise, and male gender appear to be predictive of higher-risk cases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5373","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures will not be manifes​ted in an infection of th​e post-styloid compartmen‌t of the parapharyngeal s​pace?","raw_text":"Which of the following features will not be manifested in an infection of the post-styloid compartment of the parapharyngeal space?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tri‌smus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vocal co‌rd palsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parotid ‌swelling","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Horner’s‌ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Trismus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/65e95b72f7834a8ea6c17c6d7d26289ax1280x1473.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Trismus</strong></p><p><strong>Trismus</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a feature of infection of the <strong>post-styloid</strong> compartment of the <strong>parapharyngeal</strong> space. Trismus is seen in <strong>pre-styloid</strong> abscess due to the involvement of <strong>medial pterygoid</strong> muscle.</p>\n<p>Parapharyngeal space is divided into two compartments by the styloid process and its attachments. Clinical features depend on the compartment involved.</p>\n<p>In a <strong>post-styloid abscess</strong>, there is a <strong>bulge of the pharynx</strong> behind the posterior pillar, accompanied by paralysis of cranial nerves <strong>IX</strong>, <strong>X</strong> (resulting in vocal cord paralysis), <strong>XI</strong>, <strong>XII</strong>, and the <strong>sympathetic chain</strong>, which can manifest as Horner's syndrome. <strong>Swelling</strong> in the <strong>parotid</strong> region may also be present. Notably, trismus, the inability to fully open the mouth, is usually absent in this type of abscess.</p>\n<p>A <strong>pre-styloid abscess</strong> exhibits distinct features including the <strong>prolapse</strong> of the <strong>tonsil</strong> and tonsillar fossa, along with <strong>trismus</strong> caused by the spasm of the <strong>medial pterygoid</strong> muscle. Marked <strong>odynophagia</strong> is common. Additionally, an external <strong>swelling</strong> can be observed <strong>behind the angle of the jaw</strong>.</p>\n<p>Fever, sore throat, <strong>torticollis</strong> (caused by spasm of the prevertebral muscles), and signs of systemic toxicity are common features.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis can be established using a contrast-enhanced CT scan (<strong>CECT</strong>). If there is suspicion of thrombosis of the internal jugular vein or an aneurysm of the internal carotid artery, magnetic resonance arteriography (<strong>MRA</strong>) can be useful.</p>\n<p>Patients require <strong>intravenous antibiotic</strong> therapy with amoxicillin-clavulanic acid, carbapenem antibiotics plus drugs that target anaerobic organisms, such as clindamycin or metronidazole. A parapharyngeal abscess is drained by an <strong>external horizontal incision</strong> made on the skin a few centimeters <strong>below the angle of the mandible</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image attached below shows the components of both spaces:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5375","text":"In which of the following ‌clinical scenarios will y​ou not perform an intraor​al incision?","raw_text":"In which of the following clinical scenarios will you not perform an intraoral incision?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 21-year-old woman with f‌ever, hot potato voice an​d bulging of soft palate ​and anterior pillar above‌ the tonsil on the right ​side.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 50-year-old diabetic man‌ with fever, neck swellin​g over upper ⅓ rd of the ​left sternocleidomastoid ‌and the tonsils on the sa​me side pushed medially","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A 3-year-old child with fe‌ver and swelling in the p​osterior pharyngeal wall ​lateral to the midline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 50-year-old man with fev‌er due to penetrating inj​ury of oral mucosa by woo​d splinters","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A 50-year-old diabetic man with fever, neck swelling over upper ⅓ rd of the left sternocleidomastoid and the tonsils on the same side pushed medially","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A 50-year-old diabetic man with fever, neck swelling over upper ⅓ rd of the left sternocleidomastoid and the tonsils on the same side pushed medially</strong></p><p>An intraoral incision is not sufficient in the case of option B - <strong>parapharyngeal abscess.</strong> An <strong>external neck incision</strong> must be made <strong>always</strong> to ensure complete drainage of the abscess.</p>\n<p>Option A-  This is a case of <strong>quinsy</strong> as the child has odynophagia and a hot potato voice.</p>\n<p>Option B - This is a case of <strong>parapharyngeal abscess</strong> as the swelling is in the upper 1/3rd of the neck and the tonsil of the same side is pushed medially</p>\n<p>Option C- This is a case of <strong>retropharyngeal abscess</strong> since there is fever and swelling in the posterior pharyngeal wall lateral to the midline.</p>\n<p>Option D- This is a case of <strong>Sublingual abscess</strong> involving the sublingual space which is caused by penetrating injuries of oral mucosa due to fishbone or wood splinters.</p>\n<p>Indications for Incision and Drainage:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sublingual abscess: Always intraoral incision</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Parapharyngeal abscess: Always external neck incision.</li>\n<li>Peritonsillar abscess: Always intraoral.</li>\n<li>Retropharyngeal abscess:\n<ul>\n<li>Intraoral - in rose position</li>\n<li>External - <strong>Dean approach</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ludwig’s angina:\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Intraoral—if the infection is still localized to sublingual space.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">External—if the infection involves submaxillary space. </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5989","text":"A 9-year-old boy comes wit‌h complaints of right ear​ pain, difficulty in open​ing the mouth, painful sw‌allowing, and fever. The ​oral cavity examination r​eveals the following. The‍ external facial examinat​ion is unremarkable. Whic‍h of the following is lik‌ely?","raw_text":"A 9-year-old boy comes with complaints of right ear pain, difficulty in opening the mouth, painful swallowing, and fever. The oral cavity examination reveals the following. The external facial examination is unremarkable. Which of the following is likely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phary‌ngitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parotid‌ abscess","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bezold’s‌ Abscess","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Qui‌nsy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Quinsy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6d050147219141d786ca7baf5e9058a1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Quinsy</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">The given clinical scenario describes a child with <strong>fever</strong>, <strong>otalgia</strong>, <strong>trismus</strong>, and <strong>odynophagia</strong> with a normal external facial examination. The oral cavity shows <strong>congested</strong> and <strong>edematous tonsil</strong> and tonsillar pillars with edematous <strong>uvula pushed to the opposite side</strong>. These findings are highly suggestive of <strong>q</strong><strong>uinsy (peritonsillar abscess)</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is the collection of pus in the<strong> peritonsillar space</strong> between the <strong>fibrous capsule of the tonsil</strong> and the <strong>superior constrictor muscles</strong> of the pharynx. Both <strong>aerobic</strong> and <strong>anaerobic</strong> organisms are seen.</p>\n<p>Patient presents with fever, unilateral <strong>throat pain</strong>, odynophagia, <strong>hot potato voice</strong>, <strong>torticollis</strong>, foul breath, referred <strong>otalgia</strong> through glossopharyngeal nerve and <strong>trismus</strong> due to spasm of pterygoid muscles.</p>\n<p>Oral cavity examination may show <strong>congested</strong> and <strong>edematous tonsils</strong>, pillars, and soft palate on the involved side. Edematous <strong>uvula</strong> <strong>pushed to the opposite side</strong>. Pus may be seen on the tonsillar region. Jugulodigastric lymph nodes may be enlarged.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>The management of quinsy is by <strong>antibiotics</strong> and <strong>incision</strong> and <strong>drainage</strong> to drain the abscess followed by <strong>interval tonsillectomy</strong> 4 to 6 weeks later. </p>\n<p><strong>Rare complications</strong> seen are parapharyngeal abscess, edema of the larynx, septicemia, lung abscess, or jugular vein thrombosis. </p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Pharyngitis</strong> presents with fever, throat pain, enlarged tonsils, and lymphoid follicles on the <strong>posterior pharyngeal wall</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Parotid abscess</strong> occurs due to the suppuration of parotid space. It presents with <strong>swelling</strong>, <strong>redness</strong>, firmness, and tenderness in the <strong>parotid area</strong> and at the <strong>angle of</strong> the <strong>mandible</strong>. It is usually unilateral, but bilateral abscesses can occur. Due to the thick capsule, fluctuation is often difficult to elicit. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Bezold abscess</strong> is a complication of acute <strong>otomastoiditis</strong> where the infection/pus breaks through the thin medial side of the tip of the mastoid and presents as a <strong>swelling</strong> in the <strong>upper part of the neck</strong>. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5381","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about quinsy is f​alse?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about quinsy is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Collection of pus between ‌the capsule of tonsil and​ superior constrictor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Presents with odynophagia ‌and drooling of saliva","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Doesn’t involve floor of m‌outh","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Commonly occurs bilaterall‌y","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Commonly occurs bilaterally","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Commonly occurs bilaterally</strong></p><p>Peritonsillar abscess or <strong>quinsy</strong> is usually <strong>unilateral.</strong> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>It is a collection of pus in the peritonsillar space which lies between the capsule of the tonsil and the superior constrictor muscle.</p>\n<p>Usually, it is unilateral though occasionally bilateral abscesses are recorded.</p>\n<p>It presents with marked <strong>odynophagia</strong> such that the patient cannot even swallow his <strong>saliva</strong> which <strong>dribbles</strong> from the angle of his mouth. The patient is usually <strong>dehydrated.</strong></p>\n<p>Muffled and thick speech, often called <strong>hot potato voice.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5383","text":"A 16-year-old girl present‌ed with complaints of fev​er, right-sided throat pa​in, and difficulty in swa‌llowing. On examination, ​she had hot potato speech​ and halitosis. The right‍ tonsil was not visualise​d due to oedematous pilla‍rs and the uvula was push‌ed to the left. How will ​you manage this patient?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old girl presented with complaints of fever, right-sided throat pain, and difficulty in swallowing. On examination, she had hot potato speech and halitosis. The right tonsil was not visualised due to oedematous pillars and the uvula was pushed to the left. How will you manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antibiotics ‌and analgesia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Interval to‌nsillectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Incision &amp;amp; drainage al‌ong with antibiotics","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hot tons‌illectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Incision & drainage along with antibiotics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Incision &amp; drainage along with antibiotics</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of peritonsillar abscess or <strong>quinsy</strong> and management involves<strong> incision and drainage with antibiotics.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Management of quinsy</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hospitalization</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Intravenous fluids to combat dehydration</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Antibiotics - Suitable antibiotics in large IV doses to cover aerobes and anaerobes</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Analgesics</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Drainage by needle aspiration or incision and drainage</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tonsillectomy when there is airway obstruction or recurrence.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Incision and drainage</strong> of the abscess: A peritonsillar abscess is opened at the point of the maximum bulge above the upper pole of the tonsil or just lateral to the point of junction of the anterior pillar with a line drawn through the base of the uvula.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5384","text":"In a patient with recurren‌t peritonsillar abscess, ​when is the ideal time to​ perform a tonsillectomy?‌","raw_text":"In a patient with recurrent peritonsillar abscess, when is the ideal time to perform a tonsillectomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 mo‌nths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6 w‌eeks","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"6 d‌ays","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Along with incision and dr‌ainage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 6 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 6 weeks</strong></p><p>In a patient with recurrent peritonsillar abscess (quinsy), ideally, an <strong>interval tonsillectomy</strong> is done <strong>4-6 weeks</strong> after the infection settles down.</p>\n<p><strong>Hot tonsillectomy</strong>: Earlier tonsillectomy was done during incision and drainage. But this is not followed now as it is dangerous and can lead to aspiration, bleeding &amp; spread of infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5389","text":"A patient presents with co‌mplaints of pain while sw​allowing since 2 days. He​ also gives a history of ‌toothache since 1 week. O​n examination, there is a​ swelling and erythema as‍ in the figure below. Wha​t is your diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents with complaints of pain while swallowing since 2 days. He also gives a history of toothache since 1 week. On examination, there is a swelling and erythema as in the figure below. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ludwig’‌s angina","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vincent'‌s angina","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Qui‌nsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parapharyng‌eal abscess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ludwig’s angina","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6088205a4fd04f62b9851e33a165b201.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ludwig’s angina</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Ludwig’s angina</strong>. </p>\n<p>Ludwig’s angina is an <strong>infection of the submandibular space</strong> which lies between the mucous membrane of the floor of the mouth and tongue on one side and the superficial layer of deep cervical fascia extending between the hyoid bone and mandible on the other.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5390","text":"Which of the following is ‌a false statement about L​udwig’s angina?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement about Ludwig’s angina?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It involves both sublingua‌l and submandibular space​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is usual‌ly bilateral","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dental infections are the ‌most common cause","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"H.influenza is the most co‌mmon organism implicated.​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. H.influenza is the most common organism implicated.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. H.influenza is the most common organism implicated.</strong></p><p><strong>Mixed infection</strong> with <strong>aerobes</strong> and <strong>anaerobes</strong> is seen in <strong>Ludwig’s angina</strong>. Among these alpha-hemolytic streptococci, staphylococci and Bacteroides groups are common.</p>\n<p>Ludwig’s angina is an infection of the submandibular space.</p>\n<p>The space is divided into two compartments by the mylohyoid muscle:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sublingual compartment (above the mylohyoid).</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Submandibular and submental compartment (below the mylohyoid).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The two compartments are continuous around the posterior border of the mylohyoid muscle. Both compartments freely communicate in the midline with the opposite side. Therefore <strong>infection rapidly becomes bilateral</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most common cause of Ludwig's angina is <strong>dental infections</strong> (accounts for 80%) of cases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5394","text":"A 23-year-old man presents‌ to the OPD with odynopha​gia and swelling. He is d​iagnosed with Ludwig’s an‌gina. How might you descr​ibe the swelling?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old man presents to the OPD with odynophagia and swelling. He is diagnosed with Ludwig’s angina. How might you describe the swelling?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tender fluctuant swelling ‌in the floor of mouth","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tender fluctuant swelling ‌in the parotid region","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Woody hard swelling in the‌ floor of mouth","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Woody hard swelling in the‌ parotid region","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Woody hard swelling in the floor of mouth","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Woody hard swelling in the floor of mouth</strong></p><p><strong>Ludwig's angina</strong> presents as a tender <strong>woody hard swelling</strong> on the <strong>floor of the mouth</strong>.</p>\n<p>There can be marked difficulty in swallowing (<strong>odynophagia</strong>) with varying degrees of trismus. The tongue is progressively pushed upwards and backward, <strong>threatening the airway</strong>. <strong>Laryngeal edema</strong> may also be present.</p>\n<p>Usually, there is <strong>cellulitis of the tissues</strong> rather than frank abscess. Incision and drainage show <strong>salted pork appearance</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5395","text":"A 50-year-old man with mul‌tiple comorbidities, post​-op day 5 of major abdomi​nal surgery has developed‌ high-grade fever and swe​lling over the parotid ar​ea. On examination, there‍ is tenderness, induratio​n and pus exuding from th‍e Stenson’s duct on apply‌ing pressure over the par​otids. Which of the follo​wing options is not a lik‍ely cause for the above c​ondition?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man with multiple comorbidities, post-op day 5 of major abdominal surgery has developed high-grade fever and swelling over the parotid area. On examination, there is tenderness, induration and pus exuding from the Stenson’s duct on applying pressure over the parotids. Which of the following options is not a likely cause for the above condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dehyd‌ration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oral in‌fections","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Excessive gland stimulatio‌n","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Excessive gland stimulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Excessive gland stimulation</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>parotid abscess</strong> and excessive gland stimulation is <strong>not</strong> a cause for its formation.</p>\n<p>Etiology of Parotid Abscess:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Dehydration</strong>, particularly in <strong>postsurgical cases</strong> (5-7 post-op days) and debilitated patients, with stasis of salivary flow is the predisposing cause.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Infection from the oral cavity</strong> travels via the Stenson’s duct to invade the parotid gland (retrograde spread of infection). The most common organism is <strong><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></strong> but Streptococci, anaerobic organisms, and rarely the gram negative organisms have also been cultured.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5396","text":"A patient with a parotid a‌bscess is planned for inc​ision and drainage (I &amp; D​). Which of the following‌ practices will you avoid​ in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient with a parotid abscess is planned for incision and drainage (I &amp; D). Which of the following practices will you avoid in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Put an incision that is si‌milar to parotidectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blunt dissection of the pu‌s pockets","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dissection parallel to the‌ facial nerve branches","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wait for the swelling to b‌ecome fluctuant before pe​rforming I &amp;amp; D","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Wait for the swelling to become fluctuant before performing I & D","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Wait for the swelling to become fluctuant before performing I &amp; D</strong></p><p>In a case of a <strong>parotid abscess</strong>, one must <strong>never wait for fluctuation</strong> or pus point to occur. This is because the superficial layer of <strong>parotidomasseteric fascia</strong> is <strong>tense</strong> and the fluctuation will be apparent only in later stages.</p>\n<p>A parotid abscess refers to an infection and pus formation in the parotid space. It is caused most commonly by <em><strong>Staphylococcus aureus</strong>. </em><strong>Post-surgical cases</strong> and <strong>debilitated patients</strong> with <strong>stasis</strong> of salivary flow are at risk of developing parotid abscesses.</p>\n<p>Patients typically present with <strong>unilateral swelling, redness, induration,</strong> and tenderness over the parotid gland and angle of the mandible. Dehydration and fever are also present. Diagnosis is by <strong>ultrasonography</strong> or a <strong>CT scan</strong>. Multiple small loculi of pus are seen in the parenchyma. Aspiration of abscess can be done to determine the culture and sensitivity of the infective agent. </p>\n<p>Correction of <strong>dehydration</strong>, improving oral hygiene and promoting salivary flow. Intravenous antibiotics are also used in treatment. </p>\n<p>Surgical drainage under local or general anesthesia is carried out by a <strong>preauricular incision</strong> as employed for <strong>parotidectomy. </strong>A skin flap is raised to expose the surface of the gland, and the abscess or abscesses are <strong>bluntly opened</strong> working <strong>parallel</strong> to the branches of the <strong>VIIth nerve</strong>. The skin incision is loosely approximated over a drain and allowed to heal by <strong>secondary intention</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"VjYSwBub","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"31_malabsorption_syndromes","title":"31.Malabsorption Syndromes","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC0433","text":"Which of the following is ​unlikely to be seen in pa​tients with malabsorption‌ syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is unlikely to be seen in patients with malabsorption syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ane​mia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Consti​pation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tet​any","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abdomin​al pain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Constipation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Constipation</strong></p><p>Constipation is not a feature of malabsorption syndrome.</p>\n<p>Malabsorption syndrome refers to a <strong>spectrum</strong> of conditions of various etiologies that arises due to diminished absorption of one or more dietary nutrients.</p>\n<p>The general features of malabsorption syndrome are <strong>abdominal pain</strong>, <strong>diarrhea</strong>, <strong>steatorrhea</strong>, and weight loss.</p>\n<p><strong>Anemia</strong> may be present when there is defective absorption of iron, folate, or vitamin B12. Hypocalcemia and <strong>tetany</strong> can be seen due to defective absorption of vitamin D.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0548","text":"Which of the following tes​ts is useful for the asse​ssment of intestinal muco‌sal function in a patient‍ presenting with symptoms​ of malabsorption?","raw_text":"Which of the following tests is useful for the assessment of intestinal mucosal function in a patient presenting with symptoms of malabsorption?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"D-xylul​ose test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"D-xylo​se test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Galactoma​nnan test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Schilli​ng test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. D-xylose test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. D-xylose test</strong></p><p><strong>D-xylose test</strong> is useful for the assessment of <strong>intestinal</strong> <strong>mucosal</strong> <strong>function</strong>.</p>\n<p>D-xylose is a <strong>monosaccharide</strong> that is directly absorbed by the intestinal mucosa without undergoing enzymatic digestion.</p>\n<p>The test is performed by administering 25g of D-xylose orally followed by the collection of urine 5h later. The test is considered <strong>abnormal</strong> (positive) if urinary D-xylose is <strong>&lt;4.5g</strong>. A positive D-xylose test indicates abnormal small intestinal mucosa.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0549","text":"The result of a D-xylose t​est showed urinary excret​ion of the monosaccharide‌ at 3.6g after 5 hours. W‍hich of the following can​ be a differential diagno‍sis?","raw_text":"The result of a D-xylose test showed urinary excretion of the monosaccharide at 3.6g after 5 hours. Which of the following can be a differential diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Irritable bo​wel syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ulcerativ​e colitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pancreatic i​nsufficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Crohn’s​ disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Crohn’s disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Crohn’s disease</strong></p><p>A urinary<strong> D-xylose &lt;4.5g </strong>after 5 hours of oral D-xylose<strong> (abnormal) </strong>can be seen in<strong> Crohn’s disease.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D-xylose</strong> is a monosaccharide that is <strong>directly absorbed</strong> by the small intestinal mucosa without undergoing enzymatic digestion. An abnormal (positive) D-xylose test indicates abnormal small intestinal mucosal absorption.</p>\n<p>A positive D-xylose test is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Celiac disease</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Whipple’s disease</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tropical sprue</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Short bowel syndrome</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Crohn’s disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The test is of limited clinical value today and mostly has been replaced by small intestinal biopsy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0434","text":"Patients with which of the​ following conditions are​ likely to suffer from ma‌labsorption?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions are likely to suffer from malabsorption?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Small bowel ​diverticulum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior col​on resection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ulcerativ​e colitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sigmoid ​volvulus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Small bowel diverticulum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Small bowel diverticulum</strong></p><p>A <strong>small bowel diverticulum </strong>can result in malabsorption.</p>\n<p>A small bowel diverticulum is a <strong>blind pouch</strong> protruding from the small intestine that communicates with the lumen of the gut. It becomes a potential site for <strong>bacterial overgrowth</strong> which causes malabsorption. The growing gut bacteria <strong>depletes vitamin B12</strong> and <strong>deconjugates bile acids</strong> leading to steatorrhea, diarrhea, and macrocytic anemia.</p>\n<p>Anterior colon resection, ulcerative colitis, and sigmoid volvulus affect the large intestine and therefore do not cause malabsorption.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0662","text":"What is the most common ca​use of short bowel syndro​me in adults?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of short bowel syndrome in adults?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crohn's​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mesenteric vascular occlus​ion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Necrotizing ​enterocolitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Small intestinal perforati​on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mesenteric vascular occlusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mesenteric vascular occlusion</strong></p><p>The most common cause of <strong>short</strong> <strong>bowel</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong> in adults is <strong>mesenteric vascular occlusion.</strong></p>\n<p>Short bowel syndrome (SBS) is a malabsorption disorder caused by a <strong>lack of functional small intestine</strong>. In adults, the common causes are :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mesenteric vascular disease (e.g: mesenteric vascular occlusion.)</li>\n<li>Primary mucosal and submucosal disease (e.g: Crohn’s disease)</li>\n<li>Operations without any preexisting small-intestinal disease (e.g: after trauma)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In <strong>children</strong>, <strong>necrotizing</strong> <strong>enterocolitis</strong> is the most common cause. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0556","text":"Patients with which of the​ following conditions are​ unlikely to have protein‌ malabsorption?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions are unlikely to have protein malabsorption?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enterokinas​e deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hartnup ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cysti​nuria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Homocys​tinuria","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Homocystinuria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Homocystinuria</strong></p><p><strong>Homocystinuria</strong> is not a protein malabsorption disorder. It is a <strong>disorder of methionine metabolism</strong> that leads to increased homocysteine levels.</p>\n<p>Proteins are broken down by pancreatic enzymes and are absorbed as amino acids and peptides. So, <strong>protein malabsorption</strong> usually occurs only in <strong>pancreatic insufficiency</strong>. Additionally, there are three <strong>rare genetic disorders</strong> that lead to protein malabsorption. They are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Enterokinase deficiency</strong>- Defective conversion of the proenzyme trypsinogen to trypsin. It is characterised by:</li>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Diarrhoea</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Growth retardation</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypoproteinemia</li>\n</ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Hartnup disease</strong>- Defective transport of tryptophan and other neutral amino acids. It is characterised by:</li>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pellagra-like rash</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Neuropsychiatric symptoms</li>\n</ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Cystinuria</strong>- Defective transport of cystine and other dibasic amino acids. It is characterised by:</li>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Renal calculi</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Chronic pancreatitis</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0555","text":"A Schilling test was perfo​rmed on a patient with vi​tamin B12 deficiency and ‌the results are given bel‍ow. What is the inference​?","raw_text":"A Schilling test was performed on a patient with vitamin B12 deficiency and the results are given below. What is the inference?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ileal ​disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pernicio​us anemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Small intestinal bacterial​ overgrowth","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic pa​ncreatitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ileal disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/015a7e6ffb2c4be68f24018495ba0303.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ileal disease</strong></p><p>The above findings on Schilling test show reduced absorption of vitamin B12 irrespective of all interventions. This is suggestive of an <strong>ileal disease.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7093","text":"A patient presented with c​hronic diarrhea and steat​orrhea. D-xylose test was‌ normal and the Schilling‍ test was abnormal. A duo​denal biopsy was normal. ‍What is the most likely d‍iagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presented with chronic diarrhea and steatorrhea. D-xylose test was normal and the Schilling test was abnormal. A duodenal biopsy was normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Celiac ​disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ulcerativ​e colitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intestinal lymphangiectasi​a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pancreatic i​nsufficiency","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pancreatic insufficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pancreatic insufficiency</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario describes a case of <strong>malabsorption syndrome </strong>(chronic diarrhea and steatorrhea), a <strong>normal xylose</strong> test and <strong>normal duodenal</strong> biopsy with <strong>abnormal</strong> <strong>schilling</strong> test points towards pancreatic insufficiency.</p>\n<p>A normal xylose test and normal duodenal biopsy rules out small intestinal pathologies like <strong>celiac disease </strong>(option A) and <strong>intestinal lymphangiectasia </strong>(option C), presence of steatorrhea rules out <strong>ulcerative colitis </strong>(option B) as it affects the large bowel which is not the main site of absorption of fat.  </p>\n<p><strong>Ileal disease</strong> is also characterized by normal xylose test, normal duodenal biopsy and steatorrhea and abnormal schilling test but among the given options the most appropriate answer would be pancreatic insufficiency due to chronic pancreatitis.</p>\n<p><strong>Pancreatic insufficiency</strong> is characterised by the deficiency of exocrine pancreatic enzymes that result in maldigestion and malabsorption.</p>\n<p>The features in favor of the diagnosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Steatorrhea</strong> - a deficiency of pancreatic lipases can lead to <strong>undigested fat</strong> in stools.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>D-xylose test</strong> - d-xylose is a monosaccharide that is directly absorbed by the intestinal mucosa without undergoing any enzymatic action. <strong>Normal</strong> d-xylose test denotes <strong>intact mucosa.</strong></li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Schilling test</strong> - it is abnormal as pancreatic enzymes are required for the absorption of vitamin B12.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><br/>Diagnosis of various malabsorption syndromes:<br/><br/></p>\n<table style=\"width: 925.122px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\"><strong>Pathologies</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\"><strong>D xylose test</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 285px;\"><strong>Duodenal mucosal biopsy</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 252.122px;\"><strong>Schilling test</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Celiac disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\">Decreased </td>\n<td style=\"width: 285px;\">Abnormal; Short or absent villi; epithelial cell damage; crypts hypertrophy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 252.122px;\">Normal </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Ileal disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\">Normal </td>\n<td style=\"width: 285px;\">Normal </td>\n<td style=\"width: 252.122px;\">Abnormal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Chronic pancreatitis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\">Normal </td>\n<td style=\"width: 285px;\">Normal </td>\n<td style=\"width: 252.122px;\">Abnormal; normal after pancreatic enzymes substitution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Intestinal lymphangiectasia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\">Normal </td>\n<td style=\"width: 285px;\">Abnormal; dilated lymphatics, clubbed villi</td>\n<td style=\"width: 252.122px;\">Normal </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">Bacterial overgrowth syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\">Normal or may be abnormal </td>\n<td style=\"width: 285px;\">Usually normal or can have diffuse non specific damage to villi</td>\n<td style=\"width: 252.122px;\">Usually abnormal; normal after antibiotics administration</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0552","text":"An 11-year-old boy present​ed with steatorrhea and c​hronic diarrhea. An evalu‌ation revealed delayed in‍tellectual development an​d ataxia. Acanthocytes we‍re seen on the peripheral‍ blood smear. Ophthalmolo​gical examination showed ​the following. Which of t​he following is the most ​likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An 11-year-old boy presented with steatorrhea and chronic diarrhea. An evaluation revealed delayed intellectual development and ataxia. Acanthocytes were seen on the peripheral blood smear. Ophthalmological examination showed the following. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gaucher'​s disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abetalipop​roteinemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Whipple'​s disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Protein losing enteropathy​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Abetalipoproteinemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1ed17c0567d541cda63d1953178a1ad6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Abetalipoproteinemia</strong></p><p>The image showing <strong>retinitis</strong> <strong>pigmentosa</strong> and presence of <strong>acanthocytes</strong> along with the given clinical features is suggestive of <strong>abetalipoproteinemia.</strong></p>\n<p>Abetalipoproteinemia is a rare genetic disorder in which there is <strong>defective chylomicron formation</strong>. Chylomicrons are required for the absorption and transport of fats and fat-soluble vitamins.</p>\n<p>It presents in early childhood with steatorrhoea and diarrhea, hypolipidemia, failure to thrive, ataxia, sensory and motor neuropathies.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0558","text":"The findings associated wi​th abetalipoproteinemia a​re :","raw_text":"The findings associated with abetalipoproteinemia are :","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Figure A,​B &amp;amp; C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Figure B​ &amp;amp; C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Figure A​ &amp;amp; B","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Figure A​ &amp;amp; C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Figure A & B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0fa8bf0b71ce48b19a98c6377e5ae09a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1cbc5b38a1c54e5597dfe0c96d46abc6x1280x1573.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c369e8b531e40989efc34cd918a7e85x834x772.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/348ec69c00cb45e685040e337b70e39cx682x786.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Figure A &amp; B</strong></p><p><strong>Figure A</strong> shows <strong>vacuoles</strong> (unabsorbed fat) in the cytoplasm of the enterocytes. <strong>Figure B</strong> shows <strong>acanthocytes. </strong>These findings are associated with <strong>abetalipoproteinemia.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Figure C</strong> shows distended macrophages in the lamina propria and periodic acid–Schiff (PAS)-positive granules as seen in <strong>Whipple's disease.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Acanthocytes</strong> are abnormal red blood cells with <strong>spiked cell membranes</strong> due to thorn-like projections. Causes of acanthocytes in peripheral smear are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Abetalipoproteinemia </li>\n<li>Renal disease</li>\n<li>Liver disease</li>\n<li>Hemolytic anemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0559","text":"All are true about the typ​es of celiac disease exce​pt :","raw_text":"All are true about the types of celiac disease except :","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Latent type is associated ​with HLA DQ 2,8","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atypical type has a negati​ve serology","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Silent type has a positive​ serology","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Classic type presents with​ diarrhea and steatorrhea​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Atypical type has a negative serology","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Atypical type has a negative serology</strong></p><p><strong>Atypical celiac disease</strong> has a <strong>positive serology</strong> with atypical symptoms.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 150px; width: 474px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 27px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Type of celiac disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 65.5px;\">Serology</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.5px;\">Features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 27px;\">A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Classic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 65.5px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.5px;\">Diarrhea, steatorrhea</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 27px;\">B</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Atypical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 65.5px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.5px;\">Iron deficiency anemia, osteopenia, ataxia, peripheral neuropathy, infertility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 27px;\">C</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Silent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 65.5px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.5px; text-align: left;\">No features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 27px;\">D</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Latent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 65.5px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.5px;\">HLA DQ 2,8</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Serology in celiac disease refers to either anti-tissue transglutaminase antibodies (anti-tTG) or anti-endomysial antibodies.</p>\n<p>Note: Tropical sprue and celiac disease have similar clinical, endoscopic and histologic findings. A diagnosis of tropical sprue must be considered with a negative celiac serology.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0564","text":"What is the most common ex​traintestinal manifestati​on of celiac disease?","raw_text":"What is the most common extraintestinal manifestation of celiac disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dermatitis h​erpetiformis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Infer​tility","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ane​mia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peripheral​ neuropathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dermatitis herpetiformis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6da17930748548a4905ed1233b1f0441x720x472.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dermatitis herpetiformis</strong></p><p>The most <strong>common</strong> <strong>extraintestinal</strong> manifestation of celiac disease is <strong>dermatitis herpetiformis</strong> (DH).</p>\n<p>DH is characterized by intensely pruritic small papules and vesicles on the extensor surfaces of extremities, buttocks, and neck. It can be considered a <strong>celiac disease of the skin.</strong></p>\n<p>Other extraintestinal manifestations of celiac disease are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Anemia</strong></li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Low bone density</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ataxia</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Peripheral</strong> <strong>neuropathy</strong></li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Infertility</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Celiac disease is often associated with other conditions such as type 1 diabetes mellitus, autoimmune thyroid disease, IgA deficiency, bird fancier's lung<strong>,</strong> and Down syndrome<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows dermatitis herpetiformis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0885","text":"A 15-year-old girl present​ed with fatigue, chronic ​diarrhoea, weight loss, b‌one pain, and abdominal d‍istension. Investigations​ revealed iron deficiency‍ anemia and osteoporosis.‍ Which of the following i​s the single best test to​ be done for her evaluati​on?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old girl presented with fatigue, chronic diarrhoea,  weight loss, bone pain, and abdominal distension.  Investigations revealed iron deficiency anemia and osteoporosis. Which of the following is the single best test to be done for her evaluation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"TSH l​evels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C-peptid​e levels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Urine sugar​ and ketone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IgA tissue transglutaminas​e antibody","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. IgA tissue transglutaminase antibody","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. IgA tissue transglutaminase antibody</strong></p><p>The presence of fatigue, chronic diarrhea, weight loss and abdominal distention along with investigations showing features of<strong> malabsorption (iron deficiency)</strong> lead to the diagnosis of <strong>celiac disease </strong>or<strong> gluten sensitive enteropathy. </strong>The single best test for which is checking serum for the presence of<strong> IgA tissue transglutaminase (anti-TTG) antibodies</strong> (Option D).</p>\n<p><strong>Celiac disease</strong> is a disorder characterized by an immunological response to gluten causing damage to the proximal intestinal mucosa<strong> (villous atrophy)</strong> and <strong>malabsorption</strong> of nutrients.</p>\n<p>Symptoms usually begin <strong>6 to 24 months</strong> from the introduction of gluten into diet, although most patients go undiagnosed.</p>\n<p>The common manifestations are -  irritability, abdominal distention, chronic diarrhea, weight loss or muscle wasting and extra-intestinally, the symptoms observed are aphthous ulcers, delayed puberty, iron deficiency anemia and short stature.</p>\n<p><strong>Screening</strong> for celiac disease is by testing for <strong>tissue transglutaminase IgA,</strong> <strong>endomysial</strong>, and deamidated <strong>antigliadin</strong> antibodies. IgA levels in serum are measured to detect false-negative results because of<strong> IgA deficiency. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Small intestinal biopsy</strong> shows characteristic <strong>villus blunting,</strong> <strong>crypt hyperplasia, </strong>and increased intraepithelial <strong>lymphocytes</strong>.</p>\n<p>The treatment involves the <strong>exclusion</strong> of <strong>gluten-containing</strong> food from diet. </p>\n<p>Long term complications observed are <strong>osteoporosis</strong>, anemia, infertility and malignancy <strong>(enteropathy related-T cell lymphoma</strong> is the most common association) </p>\n<p>The <strong>Marsh classification</strong> is used to categorize celiac disease into different types and to quantify severity of disease involvement.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0562","text":"A patient who presented wi​th severe malabsorption i​s found to be positive fo‌r IgA tissue trans-glutam‍inase antibodies. What wi​ll be the next best step?‍","raw_text":"A patient who presented with severe malabsorption is found to be positive for IgA tissue trans-glutaminase antibodies. What will be the next best step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Do an intes​tinal biopsy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Check for anti-endomysial ​antibodies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Check for anti-gliadin ant​ibodies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Start glute​n-free diet","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Do an intestinal biopsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Do an intestinal biopsy</strong></p><p>In this patient with positive serology, a <strong>biopsy</strong> should be done next, to <strong>confirm</strong> the <strong>diagnosis</strong> of <strong>celiac disease.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Tissue transglutaminase Ab (tTG-IgA) </strong>is the best screening test. IgA endomysial antibodies (EMA) are most specific.</p>\n<p>But a small-intestinal biopsy is required to establish a diagnosis of celiac disease, before starting treatment.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1204","text":"A 30-year-old man presente​d with chronic diarrhea, ​steatorrhea, and weight l‌oss. IgA tissue trans-glu‍taminase antibodies were ​found to be positive. Duo‍denal biopsy revealed the‍ following. Which of the ​following complications w​ould you not expect in th​is patient?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presented with chronic diarrhea, steatorrhea, and weight loss. IgA tissue trans-glutaminase antibodies were found to be positive. Duodenal biopsy revealed the following. Which of the following complications would you not expect in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intestina​l lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intestin​al ulcers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pancreatic i​nsufficiency","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Collagen​ous sprue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pancreatic insufficiency","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0a98f21957e449a3912a90d8026b5aef.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pancreatic insufficiency</strong></p><p>The above scenario along with the presence of IgA tissue trans-glutaminase antibodies and biopsy showing villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, lamina propria infiltrates indicate a case of <strong>celiac disease.</strong> Pancreatic insufficiency is not a complication of celiac disease.</p>\n<p>The <strong>complications</strong> of celiac disease are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Intestinal lymphomas</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Intestinal ulcers</strong>- also known as ulcerative jejunoileitis</li>\n<li><strong>Collagenous sprue</strong>- development of a subepithelial collagen layer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0560","text":"A 46-year-old lady with ce​liac disease is recommend​ed a gluten-free diet. Wh‌ich among the following c‍an she consume?","raw_text":"A 46-year-old lady with celiac disease is recommended a gluten-free diet. Which among the following can she consume?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wh​eat","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bar​ley","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"R​ye","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ri​ce","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rice","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rice</strong></p><p><strong>Rice</strong> can be consumed on a <strong>gluten-free</strong> diet.</p>\n<p>The primary management of celiac disease is avoiding gluten in the diet.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 367.472px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 179px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Diet avoided (gluten)</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>“BROW</strong>”</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176.472px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Diet Allowed </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>“So MRP </strong>”</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 179px;\">Barley</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176.472px;\">Soya bean</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 179px;\">Rye</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176.472px;\">Maize</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 179px;\">Oats</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176.472px;\">Rice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 179px;\">Wheat</td>\n<td style=\"width: 176.472px;\">Potato</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0561","text":"When will you diagnose a p​atient with refractory ce​liac disease?","raw_text":"When will you diagnose a patient with refractory celiac disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No remission of disease de​spite gluten free diet","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Intestinal ulcers on biops​y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"No response​ to steroids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Development of a subepithe​lial collagen layer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. No remission of disease despite gluten free diet","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. No remission of disease despite gluten free diet</strong></p><p><strong>Refractory celiac disease</strong> is diagnosed when there is <strong>no remission</strong> of disease despite a <strong>strict gluten-free diet</strong> for 6-12 months.</p>\n<p>Patients are <strong>symptomatic</strong> and have persistent small intestinal <strong>villous atrophy </strong>on biopsy. They respond well to <strong>steroids</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0563","text":"Match the following disord​ers with their respective​ sites of involvement: 1.‌ Celiac disease 2. Whippl‍e's disease 3. Tropical s​prue i. Proximal &gt; Distal‍ small intestine ii. Prox‍imal small intestine iii.​ Panintestine","raw_text":"<p>Match the following disorders with their respective sites of involvement:</p>\n<p>       1. Celiac disease</p>\n<p>       2. Whipple's disease</p>\n<p>       3. Tropical sprue</p>\n<p>       i.   Proximal &gt; Distal small intestine</p>\n<p>       ii.  Proximal small intestine</p>\n<p>       iii. Panintestine</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-ii , 2-​i , 3-iii","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1-iii, 2​-ii , 3-i","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-i , 2-i​ii , 3-ii","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-ii, 2-​iii, 3-i","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1-ii , 2-i , 3-iii","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1-ii , 2-i , 3-iii</strong></p><p>The sites involved in the given malabsorption syndromes are as follows:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Disorder</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Site of involvement</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Celiac disease</td>\n<td>Proximal small intestine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Whipple's disease</td>\n<td>Proximal &gt; Distal small intestine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tropical sprue</td>\n<td>Whole small intestine (panintestine)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0565","text":"All are true about Whipple​'s disease except :","raw_text":"All are true about Whipple's disease except :","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caused by gram positive or​ganism Tropheryma whipple​i","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Culture of organism from b​iopsy specimen helps in e​arly diagnosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Males affected more than f​emales","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multiple systems are affec​ted","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Culture of organism from biopsy specimen helps in early diagnosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Culture of organism from biopsy specimen helps in early diagnosis</strong></p><p><em><strong>Tropheryma</strong> <strong>whipplei</strong></em> is <strong>difficult to culture</strong> from the biopsy specimen. Hence, it cannot be used for the early diagnosis of Whipple's disease. </p>\n<p><strong>Whipple's disease</strong> is a chronic <strong>multisystem disorder</strong> caused by the gram-positive bacteria <em>Tropheryma whipplei.</em> It primarily causes malabsorption along with central nervous system &amp; cardiac problems. It mostly <strong>affects men</strong> and presents with fever, diarrhea weight loss, and arthralgia.</p>\n<p>The diagnosis of Whipple's disease is made through a biopsy which shows the presence of<strong> PAS-positive macrophages </strong>in the small intestine and other organs with evidence of disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0566","text":"What is the most common CN​S manifestation associate​d with Whipple’s disease?‌","raw_text":"What is the most common CNS manifestation associated with Whipple’s disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Progressiv​e dementia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Supranuclear ophthalmopleg​ia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opsoclonus myoclonus syndr​ome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oculomasticatory myorrhyth​mia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Progressive dementia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Progressive dementia</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common CNS</strong> manifestation associated with <strong>Whipple’s</strong> disease is <strong>progressive dementia.</strong></p>\n<p>Other important CNS manifestations in Whipple's disease are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Personality and mood changes</li>\n<li>Progressive supranuclear ophthalmoplegia</li>\n<li>Focal neurologic presentations</li>\n<li><strong>Oculomasticatory myorrhythmia</strong> - an unusual combination of rhythmic eye and jaw movements which is pathognomonic of Whipple's disease.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Whipple's disease associated with CNS symptoms has a poor prognosis.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Opsoclonus myoclonus syndrome</strong> is a paraneoplastic syndrome associated with <strong>neuroblastoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0567","text":"What is the most common ca​rdiovascular presentation​ seen in patients with Wh‌ipple's disease?","raw_text":"What is the most common cardiovascular presentation seen in patients with Whipple's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endoca​rditis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Perica​rditis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myoca​rditis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pericardia​l effusion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Endocarditis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Endocarditis</strong></p><p>The most common <strong>cardiovascular</strong> presentation in <strong>Whipple's</strong> disease is <strong>culture-negative endocarditis.</strong></p>\n<p>Other cardiovascular findings include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Congestive heart failure</li>\n<li>Pericarditis</li>\n<li>Myocarditis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0569","text":"A 38-year-old man presente​d with chronic diarrhea, ​weight loss, and joint pa‌ins. A duodenal biopsy wa‍s done. Which of the foll​owing drug is used in the‍ maintenance phase of tre‍atment of this condition?​","raw_text":"A 38-year-old man presented with chronic diarrhea, weight loss, and joint pains. A duodenal biopsy was done. Which of the following drug is used in the maintenance phase of treatment of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penici​llin G","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ceftr​iaxone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mero​penem","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trimethoprim / Sulfamethox​azole","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Trimethoprim / Sulfamethoxazole","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5573c6e5e2654b418593f975c1d02e85.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3bf8575962fd4d0a934ad25cdcd28d9dx589x732.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Trimethoprim / Sulfamethoxazole</strong></p><p>The above image shows<strong> PAS-positive foamy macrophages </strong>present in the lamina propria<strong>. </strong>This along with the given features suggests<strong> Whipple's disease. Cotrimoxazole</strong> is used in the <strong>maintenance phase</strong> of the treatment of Whipple's disease.</p>\n<p>Treatment of Whipple's disease:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Induction phase- ceftriaxone or meropenem or penicillin G + streptomycin - 2 weeks</li>\n<li>Maintenance phase- trimethoprim / sulfamethoxazole (TMP/SMX) for 1 year. It has high CNS penetration so it is used in maintenance to prevent relapse.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0570","text":"A 42-year-old man presente​d with diarrhea, floating​ stools, and weight loss ‌for 3 months. Celiac sero‍logy was negative. Biopsy​ of the small intestinal ‍mucosa revealed villous a‍trophy and crypt hyperpla​sia with normal mucosal t​hickness. What is the mos​t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old man presented with diarrhea, floating stools, and weight loss for 3 months. Celiac serology was negative. Biopsy of the small intestinal mucosa revealed villous atrophy and crypt hyperplasia with normal mucosal thickness. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Celiac ​disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Whipple’​s disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tropica​l sprue","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Radiation​ enteritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tropical sprue","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tropical sprue</strong></p><p>The history of malabsorptive symptoms, a negative celiac serology, and<strong> </strong>the biopsy findings are suggestive of <strong>tropical sprue</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Improvement</strong> in symptoms after<strong> folic acid </strong>intake is a hallmark of tropical sprue.</p>\n<p>Tropical sprue is an acquired malabsorptive condition of probable <strong>infectious aetiology</strong>. It is seen in people from the tropics such as India, Southeast Asia and South America.</p>\n<p>Tropical sprue and celiac disease have similar clinical, endoscopic and histologic findings. However, a positive <strong>celiac serology</strong> (anti-tissue transglutaminase and anti-endomysial antibodies) can be used to <strong>rule out</strong> tropical sprue.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0571","text":"Which of the following wou​ld you prescribe for a pa​tient with tropical sprue‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following would you prescribe for a patient with tropical sprue?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tertacycline + Vitamin B12​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metronidazole + Folic acid​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amoxicillin ​+ Vitamin B12","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tetracycline​ + Folic acid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tetracycline + Folic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tetracycline + Folic acid</strong></p><p>The treatment of tropical sprue is <strong>tetracycline + folic acid.</strong></p>\n<p>Because of marked folate deficiency, folic acid is most often given together with broad spectrum antibiotics for up to 6 months. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0663","text":"Which of the following dru​g is used for the managem​ent of a patient with sma‌ll intestinal bacterial o‍vergrowth?","raw_text":"Which of the following drug is used for the management of a patient with small intestinal bacterial overgrowth?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ciprof​loxacin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Azithr​omycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rifa​ximin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Strept​omycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rifaximin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rifaximin</strong></p><p><strong>Rifaximin</strong> is the <strong>preferred</strong> drug for the management of small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (<strong>SIBO</strong>).</p>\n<p>The antibiotics are given for around 3 weeks. The other drugs that can be used are :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tetracyclines</li>\n<li>Cephalosporins</li>\n<li>Metronidazole</li>\n<li>Amoxicillin-Clavulanic acid</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rifaximin is the antibiotic used in <strong>hepatic encephalopathy</strong> to lower blood ammonia levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"dYwfwpwg","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"31_protozoology___amoebae__ciliates___flagellates","title":"31.Protozoology   Amoebae, Ciliates & Flagellates","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA1121","text":"The life cycle given below​ is of which of the follo‍wing:","raw_text":"The life cycle given below is of which of the following:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E. verm​icularis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A. du​odenal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"B. ​coli","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"E. hist​olytica","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. E. histolytica","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/645b9ab467d14b6ab24061114030ef3d.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. E. histolytica</strong></p><p>The given life cycle is of<em><strong> Entamoeba histolytica. </strong></em></p>\n<p>Its life cycle includes 2 stages - the motile <strong>trophozoite</strong> and the non-motile <strong>cyst</strong>, both of which are seen in the image shown above. The transmission is primarily by the <strong>fecal-oral</strong> route. There is no animal reservoir.</p>\n<p>The ingested cysts differentiate into trophozoites in the ileum which invade the colonic epithelium. The trophozoites penetrate to the submucosal layer and form abscesses which break down to form <strong>flask-shaped ulcer</strong>.</p>\n<p>The trophozoites multiply by binary fission and produce cysts, and both stages are passed in the feces. Trophozoites are rapidly destroyed outside the body. Cysts survive in the external environment and are responsible for transmission.</p>\n<p>The most frequent site of systemic disease is the<strong> liver.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: The genus Entamoeba has four morphologically identical species - <em>E. histolytica, E. dispar, E. bangladeshi</em>, and <em>E. moshkovskii. E. moshkovskii</em> which was initially thought to be non-pathogenic has recently been shown to be a causative agent of non-invasive diarrhea. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8878","text":"A 40-year-old man is diagn​osed to have amoebic dyse‍ntery. Which of the follo‌wing is the infective for‌m of the protozoan causin​g this disease?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man is diagnosed to have amoebic dysentery. Which of the following is the infective form of the protozoan causing this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Troph​ozoite","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mature quadrinucleate cyst​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Metacyclic ​trophozoite","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pre​cyst","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mature quadrinucleate cyst","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5ced12db370841ac95d819352aca8af8x450x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e0888fdc00b54702bc09e79a174d5dc9x700x474.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mature quadrinucleate cyst</strong></p><p>The<strong> mature quadrinucleate</strong> <strong>cyst</strong> is the infective form of <em><strong>Entamoeba histolytica</strong></em> causing amoebic dysentery. It is passed in feces and can remain viable under moist conditions for about 10 days.</p>\n<p>The following are the characteristic features of various life stages of <em>Entamoeba histolytica:</em></p>\n<p><strong>The trophozoite </strong>is found only in tissues. Its cytoplasm has a ground-glass appearance. The nucleus has a central karyosome with radiating fibrils called linin network giving a cartwheel appearance. Pseudopodia are present which exhibit gliding motility.</p>\n<p>The below image shows trophozoite form.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>The precystic stage</strong> has a large glycogen vacuole and 2 chromatid bars. It secretes a refractile cyst wall to become a cyst.</p>\n<p><strong>The cystic stage </strong>has a<strong> </strong>mature cyst with 4 nuclei and no glycogen mass. Chromatid bars disappear.</p>\n<p>The below image shows cystic form.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0399","text":"A 25-year-old woman presen​ted with complaints of ab‍dominal pain and 6 episod‌es of passing foul-smelli‌ng dark stool mixed with ​mucus and blood streaks. ‌There is no history of fe‌ver and tenesmus. On exam​ination, vitals are stabl​e and localized tendernes‌s is present in the right​ iliac fossa. Which of th‍e following is most likel‌y to cause this condition‍?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman presented with complaints of abdominal pain and 6 episodes of passing foul-smelling dark stool mixed with mucus and blood streaks. There is no history of fever and tenesmus. On examination, vitals are stable and localized tenderness is present in the right iliac fossa. Which of the following is most likely to cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Giardia​ lamblia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Entamoeba ​histolytica","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Campylobac​ter jejuni","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Shigella d​ysenteriae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Entamoeba histolytica","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Entamoeba histolytica</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>amoebic dysentery</strong> and the causative agent is <strong><em>Entamoeba histolytica.</em></strong></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features of intestinal amebiasis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute intestinal amebiasis- dysentery, lower abdominal discomfort, and flatulence.</li>\n<li>Chronic amebiasis- low-grade symptoms such as occasional diarrhea, weight loss, and fatigue.</li>\n<li>The amoebic ulcer which is <strong>flask-shaped</strong> is the typical lesion seen in intestinal amoebiasis. The ulcers are multiple and are confined to the colon. Sometimes, in secondary intestinal lesions, the ulcers are numerous in the cecum, followed by the sigmoido-rectal region.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A granulomatous lesion called an ameboma may form in the cecal or rectosigmoid areas of the colon which can resemble an adenocarcinoma of the colon.</p>\n<p>The most appropriate treatment is intravenous <strong>metronidazole</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A - Giardia is often <strong>asymptomatic</strong>, but in some cases can lead to fat malabsorption, dull epigastric pain, and flatulence.</p>\n<p>Option C - Campylobacter presents with <strong>fever</strong>, pain, and <strong>watery diarrhea</strong>. The stool contains leucocytes and blood.</p>\n<p>Option D - Shigella causes bacillary dysentery which is characterised by <strong>fever</strong> and <strong>generalised abdominal tenderness</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8881","text":"You received a stool sampl​e of a patient suspected ‍to have dysentery in the ‌microbiology lab. On micr‌oscopy, you observe the s​tructures shown in the im‌age. What other findings ‌you will expect to see in​ this stool sample?","raw_text":"You received a stool sample of a patient suspected to have dysentery in the microbiology lab.  On microscopy, you observe the structures shown in the image. What other findings you will expect to see in this stool sample?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"RBCs as discrete or in rou​leaux","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Charcot-Ley​den crystals","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Abundant cellular exudates​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Absent eo​sinophils","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Charcot-Leyden crystals","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dad330eda243462d9a4694129fc02b66.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6a2769c6251e47809b352833e7b5022fx800x533.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Charcot-Leyden crystals</strong></p><p>The given image is showing <strong>erythrophagocytosis</strong> (trophozoite with ingested RBCs) and this in the stool sample of a patient with features of dysentery is indicative of <strong>amoebic dysentery</strong>. The other stool microscopic finding seen in amoebic dysentery is <strong>Charcot-Leyden crystals.</strong></p>\n<p>The ingested erythrocytes appear as dark inclusions. Erythrophagocytosis is the only characteristic that can be used to morphologically differentiate <em>E. histolytica</em> from the nonpathogenic entamoeba.</p>\n<p><strong>Amoebic dysentery:</strong></p>\n<p>Clinically, it is insidious in onset with localized abdominal tenderness. Tenesmus, toxicity, and fever are absent.</p>\n<p>Under microscopy, <strong>RBCs are clumped, yellowish-brown in color</strong>. Charcot-Leyden crystals and <strong>eosinophils</strong> are present.</p>\n<p><strong>Charcot-Leyden crystals</strong> are diamond-shaped, clear, and refractile structures that may be seen in stool specimens under microscopy and represent <strong>breakdown products of eosinophils</strong>.</p>\n<p>The below image shows Charcot-Leyden crystals:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8880","text":"A patient with abdominal p​ain and mucus in stool is‍ diagnosed to have intest‌inal amoebiasis. Which of‌ the following is the mos​t common extraintestinal ‌complication of this dise‌ase?","raw_text":"A patient with abdominal pain and mucus in stool is diagnosed to have intestinal amoebiasis. Which of the following is the most common extraintestinal complication of this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatic a​moebiasis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonary ​amoebiasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amoebic ap​pendicitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Splenic​ abscess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hepatic amoebiasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/18b53b175787436884faff49edbbc2bex600x970.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hepatic amoebiasis</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common extraintestinal</strong> complication of amoebiasis is <strong>hepatic amoebiasis.</strong></p>\n<p>In intestinal amoebiasis, most of the trophozoites reach the liver. However, only a small portion of them actually lodge and multiply in the liver cells. Patients usually present with an <strong>enlarged, tender liver</strong> without detectable impairment of liver function called amoebic hepatitis.</p>\n<p>Few patients go on to develop amoebic liver abscess. The center of the abscess contains thick chocolate brown pus (<strong>anchovy sauce pus</strong>), which is made up of liquefied necrotic liver tissue. It is <strong>bacteriologically sterile</strong> and <strong>free of amoeba</strong>. The <strong>periphery</strong> is made up of nearly <strong>normal</strong> <strong>liver</strong> tissue containing <strong>invading amoeba</strong>.</p>\n<p>Complications and sequelae of intestinal amoebiasis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fulminant amoebic colitis\n<ul>\n<li>Toxic megacolon</li>\n<li>Perianal ulceration</li>\n<li>Amoeboma</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Extraintestinal amoebiasis\n<ul>\n<li>Hepatic amoebiasis (most common)- Amoebic hepatitis and Amoebic liver abscess</li>\n<li>Amoebic appendicitis and peritonitis</li>\n<li>Pulmonary amoebiasis</li>\n<li>Cerebral amoebiasis</li>\n<li>Splenic abscess</li>\n<li>Cutaneous amoebiasis</li>\n<li>Genitourinary amoebiasis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8883","text":"A 40-year-old man was brou​ght to the casualty with ‍complaints of nausea, vom‌iting and disorientation.‌ His brother tells you th​ey had recently gone on a‌ camping trip where they ‌had swum in a freshwater ​lake. On examination, Ker​nig's sign is positive. A‌ spinal tap is performed ​and the findings of CSF w‍et mount microscopy are s‌hown below. Which of the ‍following is the most lik​ely causative organism?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man was brought to the casualty with complaints of nausea, vomiting and disorientation. His brother tells you they had recently gone on a camping trip where they had swum in a freshwater lake.  On examination,  Kernig's sign is positive. A spinal tap is performed and the findings of CSF wet mount microscopy are shown below. Which of the following is the most likely causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Naegleri​a fowleri","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acantham​oeba spp.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Angiostrongylus cantonensi​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Balamuthia ​mandrillaris","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Naegleria fowleri","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fd47d75eb04d4749b918dbdb0a953b8d.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Naegleria fowleri</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and microscopy showing <strong>amoeboid trophozoite form</strong> with <strong>pseudopodia</strong> is suggestive of <strong>p</strong><strong>rimary amoebic meningoencephalitis. </strong>It is caused by<strong> <em>Naegleria fowleri.</em></strong></p>\n<p>It is also known as brain-eating amoeba. It occurs in 3 forms: cyst, amoeboid trophozoite and flagellate trophozoite forms.</p>\n<p>The infection usually follows swimming in <strong>freshwater</strong> rivers or ponds. The amoebae invade the nasal mucosa and pass through the olfactory nerve branches in the cribriform plate into the meninges. Incubation period is 2 days to 2 weeks.</p>\n<p>Treatment is with <strong>intravenous amphotericin B.</strong></p>\n<p>Options B &amp; D: Both <em>Acanthamoeba</em> and <em>B.mandrillaris</em> cause granulomatous amoebic meningoencephalitis which is a subacute condition.</p>\n<p>Option C: <em>A. cantonensis</em> is the most common cause of eosinophilic meningitis in Southeast Asia. It is a nematode(roundworm).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8884","text":"A college student presents​ with ocular redness, for‍eign body sensation, and ‌corneal ulcers following ‌the use of contact lens. ​The saline mount preparat‌ion of the corneal scrapi‌ng shows polygonal cysts.​ What would be the most p​robable cause?","raw_text":"A college student presents with ocular redness, foreign body sensation, and corneal ulcers following the use of contact lens. The saline mount preparation of the corneal scraping shows polygonal cysts. What would be the most probable cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acanth​amoeba","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Naeg​leria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enta​moeba","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oncho​cerca","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acanthamoeba","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/31760f83cf9745cface31b4d7e1bc0bbx720x488.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/353da175d0de461196894e475f942b89x720x458.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acanthamoeba</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and the demonstration of polygonal cysts are suggestive of <strong>amoebic keratitis</strong> caused by <strong>acanthamoeba.</strong></p>\n<p><em>Acanthamoeba culbertsoni</em> is a <strong>free-living</strong> amoeba. It exists as <strong>trophozoite</strong> form and resistant <strong>cystic</strong> form. <strong>Both</strong> these forms are <strong>infective.</strong> The trophozoite is large and characterized by spine-like pseudopodia (acanthopodia).</p>\n<p>Human beings acquire the infection by <strong>inhalation</strong> of cysts or trophozoites, <strong>ingestion</strong> of cysts, or directly through traumatized <strong>skin</strong> or <strong>eyes</strong>.</p>\n<p>It presents as two chronic conditions- <strong>acanthamoeba keratitis</strong> and <strong>granulomatous amoebic encephalitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis of amoebic keratitis is made by the demonstration of the cyst in corneal scrapings by wet mount, histology, and culture.</p>\n<p>The below image shows the ring lesion seen in acanthamoeba keratitis.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The below image shows cysts of acanthamoeba in lactophenol cotton blue staining.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8876","text":"Which of the following pro​tozoan parasites is found‍ in the lumen of the huma‌n small intestine?","raw_text":"Which of the following protozoan parasites is found in the lumen of the human small intestine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c7d4755285754922b205ec648e016a76.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/494ad8301e1b4bf0b58fb73afc546177x720x618.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c75614f1306c4ceabce08587a1469cbex450x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/26760a767e7e4707a282b16f316f3d45x720x707.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/55a74503d86144988bf5970b7637b85ex225x225.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2</strong></p><p>Image 2 shows the trophozoite of <em><strong>Giardia</strong><strong> lamblia, </strong></em>which is the only protozoan parasite found in the <strong>lumen </strong>of the human small intestine. It lives in the <strong>duodenum</strong> and <strong>upper jejunum</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other protozoan parasites found in the human small intestine do not occur in the lumen but are present in the epithelial cells lining the small intestine. These include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Isospora belli</em></li>\n<li><em>Cyclospora cayetanensis</em></li>\n<li><em>Cryptosporidium parvum</em></li>\n<li><em>Sarcocystis hominis</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options A and C: <em>Entamoeba histolytica </em>and<em> Balantidium coli</em> are found in the human large intestine.</p>\n<p>Option D: <em>Trichomonas vaginalis</em> is found in the genital tract.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the trophozoite of <em>Giardia lamblia.</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image given below shows the trophozoite of <em>Entamoeba histolytica.</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image given below shows the trophozoite of <em>Balantidium coli</em>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image given below shows the trophozoite of <em>Trichomonas vaginalis.</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0002","text":"A 30-year-old HIV-positive​ patient presented with a‍ chronic history of water‌y diarrhea. The histopath‌ology of the ​duodenum is​ shown in the image below‌. What is the likely diag‌nosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old HIV-positive patient presented with a chronic history of watery diarrhea. The histopathology of the ​duodenum is shown in the image below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Giard​iasis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Echinoc​occosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Isospo​riasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cryptoso​pridiasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Giardiasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/864547a82da5433192a1e05b1c2e46fb.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fa44bd4b6cb64b7ca08af663b527c3dax600x671.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Giardiasis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with the histopathology showing <strong>trophozoites</strong> of <em><strong>Giardia</strong> <strong>lamblia</strong></em> in the <strong>duodenal wall</strong> is suggestive of <strong>giardiasis.</strong></p>\n<p><em>Giardia lamblia</em>, also called <em>Giardia duodenalis</em>, is the causative agent of giardiasis. It is the only common pathogenic protozoan found in the duodenum and jejunum of humans.</p>\n<p>Infection occurs by the <strong>ingestion of</strong> <strong>mature cysts</strong> in <strong>contaminated water </strong>and<strong> food </strong>and is transmitted by the <strong>faeco-oral</strong> route.</p>\n<p>It is weakly pathogenic in humans. Secretory IgA and IL-6 are responsible for clearing the infection. Hence, giardiasis usually occurs in <strong>immunosuppressed, </strong>agammaglobulinemic, or malnourished individuals.</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diarrhea </li>\n<li>Fat malabsorption</li>\n<li>Dull epigastric pain</li>\n<li>Belching and flatulence</li>\n<li>Iron deficiency and growth stunting.</li>\n<li>Stool will have excess mucus and fat but<strong> no blood.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Detection of antigen by <strong>ELISA</strong> is <strong>more sensitive and specific</strong> than microscopy. <strong>Entero</strong> <strong>test</strong> is also used in the diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Histopathology of the duodenum reveals several heart-shaped or <strong>pear-shaped</strong> trophozoites with <strong>two equally sized nuclei</strong>. The small intestinal morphology is usually normal. However, in heavy infections, there may be <strong>villous blunting</strong> and<strong> intraepithelial infiltrates.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Metronidazole/tinidazole/nitazoxanide </strong>are the <strong>first-line agents</strong> in the treatment.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows a magnified view of the pear-shaped trophozoites of <em>Giardia lamblia.</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8887","text":"A patient is undergoing th​e test depicted in the im‍age given below. Which of‌ the following diseases d‌oes it help to diagnose?","raw_text":"A patient is undergoing the test depicted in the image given below. Which of the following diseases does it help to diagnose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amoeb​iasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trichom​oniasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Giard​iasis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Echinoc​occosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Giardiasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/aa0ea50a247343c9acd6d7657418bc22.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Giardiasis</strong></p><p>The given image depicts a <strong>string test</strong> or<strong> entero test</strong> and is used in the diagnosis of <strong>giardiasis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a useful method for obtaining the duodenal specimen in giardiasis.</p>\n<p>In this test, a <strong>coiled thread</strong> inside a small weighted <strong>gelatin capsule</strong> is <strong>swallowed</strong> by the patient, after attaching the free end of the thread in the cheek. The capsule passes through the stomach to the duodenum. After <strong>2 hours</strong>, the <strong>thread</strong> is withdrawn, placed in<strong> saline</strong>, and is shaken. The centrifuged deposit of the saline is then <strong>examined for giardia.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8885","text":"The findings of the micros​copic examination of the ‍wet mount preparation of ‌stool of a patient with d‌iarrhoea is shown below. ​Which of the following or‌ganisms will exhibit this‌ type of motility?","raw_text":"The findings of the microscopic examination of the wet mount preparation of stool of a patient with diarrhoea is shown below. Which of the following organisms will exhibit this type of motility?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vibrio ​cholera","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Giardia​ lamblia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Campylobac​ter jejuni","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Listeria mo​nocytogenes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Giardia lamblia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/39337edffa174b43a25d3b05b616a463.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Giardia lamblia</strong></p><p>The given wet mount preparation of stool shows organisms having <strong>falling leaf motility</strong>. It is exhibited by <em><strong>Giardia lamblia. </strong></em></p>\n<p>The <strong>trophozoite</strong> is motile with a slow oscillation along its long axis resembling a falling leaf.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8888","text":"A patient came to the gyne​cology OPD with complaint‍s of foul-smelling frothy‌ vaginal discharge and in‌tense itching. The per sp​eculum finding is given b‌elow. Which of the follow‌ing statements is false r​egarding the causative or​ganism of this disease?","raw_text":"A patient came to the gynecology OPD with complaints of foul-smelling frothy vaginal discharge and intense itching. The per speculum finding is given below. Which of the following statements is false regarding the causative organism of this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It exists in cyst and trop​hozoite form","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is an obligate parasite​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The gold standard for diag​nosis is culture on CPLM ‍or PEM","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It can cause male infertil​ity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It exists in cyst and trophozoite form","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8da37a5349be4f4ba7457c075777db7a.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7bc438b196f24e92983fcde83e4c73cfx720x576.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It exists in cyst and trophozoite form</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with the image showing <strong>strawberry cervix</strong> is suggestive of <strong><em>Trichomonas vaginalis</em> infection. </strong>It exists <strong>only in trophozoite</strong> form.</p>\n<p><em>Trichomonas vaginalis</em> is the only protozoan with <strong>one stage</strong> in its life cycle ie., trophozoite form (no cyst stage is seen). It is an <strong>obligate parasite</strong> and cannot live without close association with vaginal, urethral or prostatic tissues.</p>\n<p>The common presentation of trichomoniasis is foul-smelling frothy vaginal discharge, itching, and dysuria. The <strong>gold standard</strong> for diagnosis is <strong>culture on CPLM </strong>(cysteine-peptone-liver-maltose) or <strong>PEM</strong> (plastic envelope medium). The treatment of choice is <strong>metronidazole.</strong></p>\n<p><em>T. vaginalis</em> infection in women may lead to infertility, preterm birth and low-birth-weight infants. In <strong>men</strong>, trichomoniasis has been also associated with <strong>infertility</strong> through inflammatory damage to the genitourinary tract or interference with sperm function.</p>\n<p>The below image shows the trophozoite form of <em>Trichomonas vaginalis</em>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8889","text":"A 12-year-old child presen​ted to you with fever, ma‍laise, and weight loss. O‌n examination, you notice‌ the finding shown in the​ image. In which of the f‌ollowing conditions is th‌is seen?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old child presented to you with fever, malaise, and weight loss. On examination, you notice the finding shown in the image. In which of the following conditions is this seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"West African sleeping sick​ness","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chagas ​disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leishm​aniasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Babes​iosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. West African sleeping sickness","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/01c3d71e34f04dcf916017e9d1bc6390.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. West African sleeping sickness</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>posterior cervical lymphadenopathy</strong> which is called <strong>Winterbottom’s sign</strong>. It is seen in <strong>West African sleeping sickness.</strong></p>\n<p>It is seen in stage I of West African sleeping sickness caused by <em>Trypanosoma brucei gambiense.</em></p>\n<p>The table below shows diseases caused by various Trypanosoma species.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p><strong>Protozoa</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p><strong>Disease </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p><em>Trypanosoma brucei gambiense</em></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>West African sleeping sickness</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p><em>Trypanosoma brucei rhodesiense</em></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>East African sleeping sickness</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p><em>Trypanosoma cruzi</em></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Chagas’ disease/South American trypanosomiasis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8890","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin‍g Trypanosoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding Trypanosoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tsetse fly is the vector f​or both T. rhodesiense an‍d T. gambiense","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T. gambiense exhibits anti​genic variation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T. rhodesiense can cause m​yocarditis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Humans are the reservoir f​or T. rhodesiense","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Humans are the reservoir for T. rhodesiense","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Humans are the reservoir for T. rhodesiense</strong></p><p><strong>Humans</strong> are the <strong>reservoir</strong> for <em><strong>T. gambiense</strong></em>, not for <em>T. rhodesiense</em>.</p>\n<p>The table below shows characteristic differences between West African and East African trypanosomiasis.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"187\">\n<p><strong>Characteristics</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p><strong>West African trypanosomiasis</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"221\">\n<p><strong>East African trypanosomiasis</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"187\">\n<p>Organism</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p><em>T. brucei gambiense</em></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"221\">\n<p><em>T. brucei rhodesiense</em></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"187\">\n<p>Vector</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p>Tsetse fly</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"221\">\n<p>Tsetse fly</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"187\">\n<p>Reservoir</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p>Humans</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"221\">\n<p>Wild and domestic animals</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"187\">\n<p>Course of disease</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p>Chronic</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"221\">\n<p>Acute</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"187\">\n<p>Parasitemia</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p>Low</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"221\">\n<p>High</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"187\">\n<p>Virulence</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p>Less</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"221\">\n<p>More</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"187\">\n<p>Mortality</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p>Low</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"221\">\n<p>High</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Parasites causing myocarditis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Trypanosoma brucei rhodesiense</em></li>\n<li><em>Trypanosoma cruzi</em></li>\n<li><em>Toxoplasma gondii</em></li>\n<li><em>Echinococcus granulosus</em></li>\n<li><em>Trichinella spiralis</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8891","text":"A middle-aged woman presen​ts to the OPD with fever ‍and malaise for the past ‌4 months. On examination,‌ the following ocular fin​ding is noted along with ‌hepatosplenomegaly and ly‌mphadenopathy. What is th​e most probable diagnosis​?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presents to the OPD with fever and malaise for the past 4 months. On examination, the following ocular finding is noted along with hepatosplenomegaly and lymphadenopathy. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"West African Sleeping Sick​ness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chagas ​disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Toxopl​asmosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"East African Sleeping Sick​ness","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chagas disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0c066afaf1f64ec8a2a7d6f7b6cf6ed4.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0307125b4e9b4805a8b395efe623b11ex621x346.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/45515bcf960549d79cc727415798fb6ax720x543.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chagas disease</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with the image showing <strong>Romana's sign</strong> is suggestive of <strong>Chagas disease.</strong></p>\n<p>It is caused by <em><strong>T. cruzi. </strong></em>Reduviid bugs are vectors for Chagas disease.</p>\n<p>Inoculation of <em>T. cruzi</em> in conjunctiva causes unilateral, <strong>painless edema of periocular tissues</strong> in the eye is called <strong>Romana’s sign</strong>. This is classically seen in<strong> acute Chagas disease</strong> that lasts for 1 to 4 months.</p>\n<p>The following image shows a patient with Romana's sign.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Acute Chagas disease is also characterised by the presence of chagomas which are subcutaneous lesions occurring at the site of inoculation.</p>\n<p>Chronic Chagas disease is characterized by megaesophagus, megacolon, and myopathy.</p>\n<p>Shown below is an image of the Reduviid bug.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8882","text":"Which of the following is ​used for the culture of T‍rypanosoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is used for the culture of Trypanosoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NNN m​edium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Boeck and Dr​bohlav medium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Craig’s​ medium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"NIH polyge​nic medium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. NNN medium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. NNN medium</strong></p><p><strong>NNN </strong>(Novy, McNeal, and Nicolle)<strong> medium </strong>is used for the culture of <strong><em>Trypanosoma brucie</em> </strong>and <em><strong>cruzie</strong></em>. It is also used for the culture of Leishmania.</p>\n<p>The other media used in the culture of Trypanosoma are Weinman's medium and Tobie's medium.</p>\n<p>Options B, C &amp; D: Boeck and Drbohlav medium, Craig’s medium and NIH polygenic medium are used for the culture of <em>E.histolytica.</em></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8892","text":"The given life cycle belon​gs to which of the follow‍ing organisms?","raw_text":"The given life cycle belongs to which of the following organisms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trypanosoma brucei rhodesi​ense","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trypanos​oma cruzi","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Trypanosoma brucei gambien​se","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trypanoso​ma rangeli","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trypanosoma cruzi","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0710dc49656641b2b98ff91ac3593af3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trypanosoma cruzi</strong></p><p>The given life cycle belongs to<strong> <em>Trypanosoma cruzi.</em></strong></p>\n<p><em>T. cruzi</em> is the causative organism of <strong>Chagas disease</strong> or South American trypanosomiasis. They are intracellular parasites.</p>\n<p>It passes its life cycle in two hosts- the definitive host is the <strong>man</strong> and the intermediate host (vector) is <strong>reduviid bug</strong> or triatomine bugs.</p>\n<p><strong>Metacyclic trypomastigotes</strong> are the <strong>infective forms</strong> to the man that are found in feces of reduviid bugs. Transmission occurs when mucus membranes, conjunctiva, or wound on the surface of the skin are contaminated by feces of the bug.</p>\n<p>Inside the definitive host, the trypomastigotes differentiate into intracellular amastigotes. The amastigotes multiply by binary fission and differentiate into trypomastigotes, which are then released into the bloodstream. These are the infective stage for triatomine bug. No multiplication occurs in this stage.</p>\n<p>Inside the triatomine bug, the trypomastigotes are transformed into epimastigotes and then into metacyclic trypomastigotes in the hindgut which are excreted in feces.</p>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>Amastigote forms resemble the Leishmania spp. and hence they are frequently known as Leishmanial forms. They are found in muscular tissue, nervous tissue, and reticuloendothelial system.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8897","text":"The given life cycle belon​gs to which of the follow‍ing organisms?","raw_text":"The given life cycle belongs to which of the following organisms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trypanosoma brucei gambien​se","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trypanosoma brucei rhodesi​ense","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leishmani​a donovani","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Babesia​ microti","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Leishmania donovani","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/46d3881609bd4f4e8ec9925f62dc4426.GIF"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/67f2a31c658744b49f25151ea4d097c2x600x611.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f3f2c26a14734290bb3923a5b6d8024dx619x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Leishmania donovani</strong></p><p>The given life cycle belongs to<strong> <em>Leishmania donovani.</em></strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>definitive</strong> hosts are <strong>man, dog</strong>, and other mammals and the vector/<strong>intermediate</strong> host is <strong>female sandfly</strong>. Promastigotes are infective forms to man.</p>\n<p><em>L. donovani</em> exists in 2 forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Amastigote form- in humans and other mammals</li>\n<li>Promastigote form- in the sandfly and artificial culture</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When the infected sandfly bites a person, infectious promastigotes are released into the host dermis along with the sandfly saliva, which potentiates parasite infectivity.</p>\n<p><strong>Amastigote form </strong>(as shown in the image below) is also known as<strong> Leishman-Donovan (LD) body</strong>. It is an <strong>intracellular</strong> form found inside macrophages, monocytes and reticuloendothelial cells. It contains a kinetoplast which consists of a parabasal body and a blepharoplast. Flagellum is absent.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Promastigote form </strong>(as shown in the below image) is<strong> </strong>present in the midgut of the female sandfly. The kinetoplast lies transversely near the anterior end. A single flagellum is seen. It is the <strong>infective form</strong> of <em>Leishmania donovani</em> for humans.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8899","text":"You are performing the fol​lowing diagnostic procedu‍re in a patient having fe‌ver for the past 2 weeks ‌to find the causative org​anism. Which disease are ‌you most likely testing f‌or?","raw_text":"You are performing the following diagnostic procedure in a patient having fever for the past 2 weeks to find the causative organism. Which disease are you most likely testing for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kala​-azar","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Schisto​somiasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Toxopl​asmosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chagas ​disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Kala-azar","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f5878b5b215f4f7b8a155c9fc79dd48f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Kala-azar</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>bone marrow aspiration</strong> and it is most commonly done to <strong>diagnose Kala-azar.</strong></p>\n<p>The microscopic <strong>demonstration</strong> of <strong>amastigotes</strong> from bone marrow is the <strong>gold standard</strong> for the diagnosis of Kala-azar</p>\n<p>Generally, the sternal marrow is aspirated by puncturing the sternum at the level of the 2<sup>nd</sup> or 3<sup>rd</sup> intercostal space, using a sternal puncture needle.</p>\n<p>Other samples collected are peripheral blood, splenic aspirate, and enlarged lymph node.</p>\n<p>In kala-azar:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Most common</strong> specimen collected for microscopy<strong>- </strong><strong>bone marrow aspirate.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Most sensitive</strong> method- <strong>splenic aspirate.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: Schistosomiasis is typically diagnosed by stool examination for parasite eggs.</p>\n<p>Option C: Toxoplasmosis is usually diagnosed based on serologic testing.</p>\n<p>Option D: Chagas disease is diagnosed by peripheral blood smear examination, culture and examination of aspirates from lymph nodes and chagoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4449","text":"What is the vector for the​ condition diagnosed by t‍he image shown below?","raw_text":"What is the vector for the condition diagnosed by the image shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anopheles​ mosquito","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ixode​s Tick","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"San​dfly","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tsets​e fly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sandfly","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2f93a0e6824940579454a1f3150db3f4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sandfly</strong></p><p>The image shown is of the <strong>Leishman-Donovan bodies</strong> whose presence are diagnostic of <strong>Kala Azar.</strong> The vector of Kala Azar is a <strong>sandfly</strong>.</p>\n<p>Leishman-Donovan bodies are amastigotes with a double dot appearance containing a nucleus and small rod-like kinetoplast. They are present inside the macrophage.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8902","text":"A 15-year-old boy from Del​hi presented to the OPD w‍ith the finding seen in t‌he image. It is diagnosed‌ to be an oriental sore. ​Which of the following ca‌uses this?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old boy from Delhi presented to the OPD with the finding seen in the image. It is diagnosed to be an oriental sore. Which of the following causes this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leishmania mexicana comple​x","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leishmania braziliensis co​mplex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leishmani​a donovani","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leishmania tropica complex​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Leishmania tropica complex","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4f503db2b987472e8a865c01b4a97a18.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Leishmania tropica complex</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>cutaneous leishmaniasis</strong> and is caused by <strong>Leishmania tropica complex</strong><em><strong>. </strong></em>It is also called oriental sore/Delhi boil/Bagdad boil/Aleppo button. It is seen mainly among children in endemic areas.</p>\n<p>Old world Leishmania tropica complex includes 3 species:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>L. tropica</em></li>\n<li><em>L. major</em></li>\n<li><em> L. aethiopica</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>New world Leishmania tropica complex includes 3 species:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>L. mexicana complex</em></li>\n<li><em>L. braziliensis complex </em><br/><br/></li>\n</ul>\n<p>It begins as a <strong>raised papule</strong> that grows into a <strong>nodule</strong> which then <strong>ulcerates</strong> over some weeks. The margins of the ulcer are raised and indurated. The ulcer is usually <strong>painless</strong> unless secondary bacterial infection occurs. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0008","text":"A medicine resident is eva​luating a patient with ab‍dominal pain and diarrhoe‌a. The stool wet mount fi‌nding is given below. Wha​t is the most likely caus‌ative organism?","raw_text":"A medicine resident is evaluating a patient with abdominal pain and diarrhoea. The stool wet mount finding is given below. What is the most likely causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Balantid​ium coli","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Isospor​a belli","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cryptospori​dium parvum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enteromon​as hominis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Balantidium coli","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/62088cc067f94fe5a46d8b845f8a3776.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Balantidium coli</strong></p><p>The given image shows the <strong>trophozoite</strong> form of the <em><strong>Balantidium coli</strong>. </em>Living trophozoites/cysts are yellowish or greenish in color on wet mount.</p>\n<p><em>Balantidium coli</em> occurs in two stages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Trophozoites</strong> are oval in shape and contain two nuclei. There is the presence of <strong>cilia</strong> over the entire surface and are larger around the mouth. Its anterior end is narrow and the posterior end is broad.  At the anterior end, there is a groove leading to the mouth (cytostome). Posteriorly, there is a small anal pore (cytopyge).</li>\n<li><strong>Cysts</strong> are smaller, round with a micronucleus, a macronucleus and many contractile vacuoles.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2550","text":"What is the investigation ​of choice for the diagnos‍is of kala-azar?","raw_text":"What is the investigation of choice for the diagnosis of kala-azar?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rapid dip​stick test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aldehyde te​st of Napier","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"EL​ISA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Indirect fluorescent antib​ody test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rapid dipstick test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rapid dipstick test</strong></p><p>The<strong> rapid dipstick test </strong>is the method of choice for the diagnosis of <strong>kala</strong>-<strong>azar</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following tests are used for the laboratory diagnosis of kala-azar:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rapid diagnostic tests <strong>-</strong> <strong>rK39</strong> and dipstick test strip.</li>\n<li>Parasitological diagnosis - demonstration of <strong>LD bodies </strong>on microscopy is confirmatory</li>\n<li>Surveillance purposes - <strong>Aldehyde test of Napier</strong></li>\n<li>Serological diagnosis - Rapid dipstick test, direct agglutination test, ELISA, indirect fluorescent antibody test</li>\n<li>Assessing immune status - <strong>Leishmanin </strong>(Montenegro)<strong> test</strong> \n<ul>\n<li>Induration of ≥ 5 mm is considered positive</li>\n<li>Usually becomes positive 4-6 weeks after onset of cutaneous leishmaniasis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"9hSUoVgh","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"31_uro_gynecology","title":"31.Uro Gynecology","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB3105","text":"A woman with urinary incon‌tinence was diagnosed to ​have a vesicovaginal fist​ula. What is the most com‍mon cause of this conditi​on?","raw_text":"A woman with urinary incontinence was diagnosed to have a vesicovaginal fistula. What is the most common cause of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conge‌nital","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acqu‌ired","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Idiop‌athic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infec‌tious","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acquired","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acquired</strong></p><p><strong>Vesicovaginal fistulas </strong>are mostly <strong>acquired. </strong></p>\n<p>In developing countries, the most common cause is <strong>obstetric trauma</strong>. In developed countries, <strong>iatrogenic injury</strong> during pelvic surgery is responsible for 90% of the vesicovaginal fistulas. </p>\n<p><strong>Congenital</strong> genitourinary fistulas and fistulas resulting from<strong> infectious</strong> conditions are <strong>rare. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3107","text":"Which of the following typ‌es of genitourinary fistu​las has not been reported​?","raw_text":"Which of the following types of genitourinary fistulas has not been reported?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uretero‌vaginal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vesico‌uterine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uterou‌rethral","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Urethro‌cervical","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Uterourethral","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Uterourethral</strong></p><p>Uterourethral type of fistula has not been reported.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 604\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>A <strong>genitourinary fistula</strong> is defined as an abnormal communication between the urinary system (ureter, bladder, urethra) and the genital systems (vagina, cervix, uterus).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 605\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Most genitourinary fistulas are <strong>acquired. </strong>In <strong>developing countries</strong>, the most common cause is <strong>obstetric trauma</strong>. In <strong>developed countries</strong>, <strong>iatrogenic injury</strong> during pelvic surgery is responsible for 90% of the vesicovaginal fistulas. </p>\n<p><strong>Congenital</strong> genitourinary fistulas are <strong>rare. </strong>If present, they are commonly associated with other renal or urogenital abnormalities.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3102","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not cause genitourinary​ fistulas?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not cause genitourinary fistulas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyster‌ectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Radiot‌herapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prolonged obstructed labou‌r","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyster‌oscopy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hysteroscopy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hysteroscopy</strong></p><p>Hysteroscopy is not an etiology for <strong>genitourinary fistulae</strong>. It can occur following <strong>hysterectomy</strong>. </p>\n<p>Most genitourinary fistulas are <strong>acquired. </strong>Its causes are enumerated below:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prolonged<strong> obstructed labor</strong> - major cause in developing countries</li>\n<li>Difficult instrumental or manipulative deliveries</li>\n<li>Injury to bladder or ureter during hysterectomy - major cause in developed contries</li>\n<li>Congenital (rare)</li>\n<li>Other causes include:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Radiation</strong></li>\n<li>Malignancy</li>\n<li>Trauma </li>\n<li>Infections - PID, tuberculosis etc. </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3110","text":"A P1L1 woman presents with‌ constant dribbling of ur​ine. She gives a history ​of unattended vaginal del‍ivery following a difficu​lt and prolonged labor 3 ​months back. What is the ‌most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A P1L1 woman presents with constant dribbling of urine. She gives a history of unattended vaginal delivery following a difficult and prolonged labor 3 months back. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ureterovagi‌nal fistula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vesicouter‌ine fistula","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vesicovagi‌nal fistula","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Urethrovagi‌nal fistula","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vesicovaginal fistula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vesicovaginal fistula</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of a <strong>vesicovaginal fistula.</strong></p>\n<p>Vesicovaginal fistulas usually present with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Constant dribbling</strong> of urine from the vagina</li>\n<li>Excoriation in the groin region due to constant leakage of urine</li>\n<li>History of pelvic surgery, <strong>vaginal delivery</strong> or <strong>prolonged labor</strong></li>\n<li>Amenorrhea may be present</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On examination, the fistula may be visualized by observing urine collection in the vagina. Bimanual examination reveals its fixity and extent.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Ureterovaginal</strong> fistulas usually occur following accidental ureteric injury during<strong> C-sections</strong> in <strong>developing countries</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7103","text":"What is the most useful te‌st in vesicovaginal fistu​la?","raw_text":"What is the most useful test in vesicovaginal fistula?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cysto‌scopy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3 swa‌b test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"M‌RI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cysto‌metry","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cystoscopy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/62d20aefbac94edeb295d66f846d17ccx1280x1082.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cystoscopy</strong></p><p><strong>Cystoscopy</strong> is the <strong>most useful test</strong> and also the <strong>investigation of choice</strong> for<strong> vesicovaginal fistula</strong> (VVF).</p>\n<p>Cystoscopy or<strong> cystourethroscopy</strong> helps to visualize the <strong>size</strong> of the fistulas, the exact <strong>location</strong> of <strong>multiple fistulas </strong>and their <strong>proximity</strong> to the <strong>ureteral</strong> <strong>orifice</strong><strong>.</strong> It can also <strong>assess</strong> the <strong>bladder</strong> mucosa for any <strong>injuries. </strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>3-swab test</strong> (Option B) is done to identify the <strong>types of genitourinary fistula</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this test, <strong>three sterile</strong> pieces of cotton swabs are <strong>serially placed</strong> in the vaginal cavity, while 50-100ml of <strong>methylene blue solution</strong> is instilled in the <strong>bladder</strong> using a catheter. The cotton swabs are placed one at the <strong>vault,</strong> one at the <strong>middle</strong>, and one just <strong>above the introitus.</strong></p>\n<p>Interpretation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Uppermost</strong> swab gets <strong>wet</strong> and <strong>not stained</strong> - the leak is from the ureter, as seen in <strong>u</strong><strong>reterovaginal</strong> fistula</li>\n<li><strong>Middle</strong> swab gets <strong>stained</strong> - the leak is from the bladder, as seen in <strong>v</strong><strong>esicovaginal</strong> fistula</li>\n<li><strong>Lowermost</strong> swab gets <strong>stained</strong>- the leak is from the urethra,as seen in <strong>u</strong><strong>rethrovaginal</strong> fistula</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below image shows the 3-swab test.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>MRI</strong> is usually not needed for the diagnosis of VVF. They can be used to study <strong>vesicouterine fistulas</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Cystometry</strong> is done to measure the capacity of the bladder. It is used to investigate <strong>urge incontinence</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3112","text":"A patient comes with a com‌plaint of dribbling of ur​ine from the vagina durin​g micturition. She has a ‍history of obstructed lab​or. Methylene blue 3 swab​ test is done, and the lo‌west swab gets stained. W‍hat is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient comes with a complaint of dribbling of urine from the vagina during micturition. She has a history of obstructed labor. Methylene blue 3 swab test is done, and the lowest swab gets stained. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ureterovagi‌nal fistula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vesicovagi‌nal fistula","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Urethrovagi‌nal fistula","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vesicouter‌ine fistula","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Urethrovaginal fistula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dcfec068a7fd4df2a2ae3bfcb6d13b75x1279x966.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Urethrovaginal fistula</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>u</strong><strong>rethrovaginal fistula.</strong></p>\n<p>Urethrovaginal fistulas have the following clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Continent</strong> and dry at rest</li>\n<li>Urinary <strong>dribbling</strong> <strong>during</strong> <strong>micturition</strong></li>\n<li>Fistulous opening is seen on per speculum examination</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the methylene blue 3-swab test, a catheter is introduced into the bladder through the urethra. The vaginal cavity is packed with three sterile cotton swabs. One at the vault, one at the middle, and one just above the introitus. 50–100 mL of dilute methylene blue dye is injected into the bladder using a catheter.</p>\n<p>Interpretation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The<strong> uppermost</strong> swab gets wet and not stained - the leak is from the ureter, seen in <strong>u</strong><strong>reterovaginal</strong> fistula</li>\n<li><strong>Middle</strong> swab gets stained - the leak is from the bladder, seen in <strong>v</strong><strong>esicovaginal</strong> fistula</li>\n<li>The<strong> lowermost</strong> swab gets stained- the leak is from the urethra, seen in <strong>u</strong><strong>rethrovaginal</strong> fistula</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given image shows the 3-swab test.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3113","text":"A 40-year-old woman with a‌ history of colporrhaphy ​is diagnosed with a vesic​ovaginal fistula. Which o‍f the following investiga​tions is not preferred in​ this patient?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman with a history of colporrhaphy is diagnosed with a vesicovaginal fistula. Which of the following investigations is not preferred in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urine ‌culture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ultrasoun‌d abdomen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cysto‌scopy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M‌RI","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. MRI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6d7bd34a77b547cf9ed3b33bf75b2a9bx1279x966.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. MRI</strong></p><p>MRI is not preferred for a case of vesicovaginal fistula as other investigations are more specific and useful.</p>\n<p><strong>Investigations in VVF</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Urine</strong> <strong>culture</strong> - to treat concurrent UTI.</li>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound</strong> <strong>abdomen</strong> - kidney, bladder and ureter should be observed for the presence of a urinoma or hydronephrosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Intravenous pyelography</strong> - to look for hydronephrosis</li>\n<li><strong>Cystoscopy</strong> - used to visualise the number of fistulae and their location.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Methylene blue <strong>3-swab test </strong>in <strong>urinary fistulae</strong> - A catheter is introduced into the bladder through the urethra and 50-100ml methylene blue is injected. The vaginal cavity is packed with three sterile cotton swabs. It is interpreted as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The<strong> uppermost</strong> swab gets wet and not stained - the leak is from the ureter, seen in <strong>u</strong><strong>reterovaginal</strong> fistula.</li>\n<li><strong>Middle</strong> swab gets stained - the leak is from the bladder, seen in <strong>v</strong><strong>esicovaginal</strong> fistula.</li>\n<li>The<strong> lowermost</strong> swab gets stained- the leak is from the urethra, seen in <strong>u</strong><strong>rethrovaginal</strong> fistula.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given image shows the methylene blue 3-swab test.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3115","text":"A 40-year-old woman came f‌or a follow-up visit one ​week following a vaginal ​hysterectomy. You note sh‍e has developed a large v​esicovaginal fistula. Wha​t is the best time for fi‌stula repair in this case‍?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman came for a follow-up visit one week following a vaginal hysterectomy. You note she has developed a large vesicovaginal fistula. What is the best time for fistula repair in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immed‌iately","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"After ‌3 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"After ‌6 weeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"After 3‌ months","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. After 6 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. After 6 weeks</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the best time to perform <strong>fistula repair</strong> is <strong>6 weeks</strong> <strong>after</strong> the inciting <strong>surgery.</strong></p>\n<p>In <strong>uncomplicated fistulas,</strong> early surgical<strong> intervention</strong> is preferred <strong>within</strong> the first <strong>24 to 48 hours</strong> following the inciting surgery. This avoids the brisk postoperative inflammatory response.</p>\n<p>However, if <strong>extensive</strong> and severe <strong>inflammation</strong> is present, <strong>repair</strong> of fistula can be delayed to <strong>6 weeks</strong> <strong>after the surgery</strong>. This is usually done in a patient who has undergone major surgery,</p>\n<p>During this delay it is ensured that all the tissue inflammation has subsided, tissue vascularisation has improved and local infection has cleared. <strong>Estrogen therapy</strong> should be instituted in <strong>postmenopausal women</strong> in this time period. </p>\n<p>In case the fistula repair fails, a <strong>second attempt</strong> is made only <strong>after 3 months.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3117","text":"A 34-year-old woman presen‌ts to the OPD with compla​ints of blood in her urin​e. She informs you that t‍his happens every month f​or 1 week and stops on it​s own. She underwent a C-‌section 1 year ago and ha‍s no history of incontine​nce. During clinical exam‍ination, a catheter is in​serted into the bladder, ‌and the following finding‌ is seen. What is the lik‌ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old woman presents to the OPD with complaints of blood in her urine. She informs you that this happens every month for 1 week and stops on its own. She underwent a C-section 1 year ago and has no history of incontinence. During clinical examination, a catheter is inserted into the bladder, and the following finding is seen. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urethrouter‌ine fistula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vesicouter‌ine fistula","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vesicovagi‌nal fistula","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ureterovagi‌nal fistula","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vesicouterine fistula","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/806ebeb62ca148db9b0c5928c4c6556e.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vesicouterine fistula</strong></p><p>The given image shows the <strong>catheter emerging</strong> from the <strong>cervical os</strong>. This, along with the history of <strong>cyclical hematuria</strong>, is suggestive of <strong>Youssef syndrome</strong> seen in a case of <strong>vesicouterine fistula.</strong></p>\n<p>Vesicouterine fistulas can develop after <strong>cesarean section,</strong> uterine rupture, and placenta accreta. Most cases <strong>present</strong> weeks to <strong>years later.</strong></p>\n<p>Vesicouterine fistulas can present with cyclical hematuria, urinary incontinence or complete continence, menstrual abnormalities, and urinary tract infections. <strong>Youssef syndrome</strong> is seen in cases of vesicouterine fistulas, and it is associated with a <strong>classic triad</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Amenorrhea</strong></li>\n<li>Cyclical hematuria - also called <strong>menouria: </strong>Menstrual blood trickles into the bladder through the fistula, causing hematuria</li>\n<li>Urinary <strong>continence</strong> (there is no vaginal leakage of urine)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hysteroscopy</strong> and <strong>cystoscopy</strong> are gold-standard investigations. </p>\n<p>Vesicouterine fistulas are treated as follows: </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 237\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prolonged bladder</strong> <strong>catheterization</strong> - leads to spontaneous closure</li>\n<li><strong>Surgical repair</strong> via abdominal route</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3118","text":"Intravenous pyelography in‌ a patient suspected to h​ave a genitourinary fistu​la revealed a ureteric fi‍stula. Which of the follo​wing is false about this ​condition?","raw_text":"Intravenous pyelography in a patient suspected to have a genitourinary fistula revealed a ureteric fistula. Which of the following is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is mostly caused by gyn‌ecological surgeries","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Continuous dribbling of ur‌ine occurs, but the patie​nt can also micturate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"In 3-swab test, the swab i‌s stained blue","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hydronephrosis is present ‌on the affected side","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. In 3-swab test, the swab is stained blue","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. In 3-swab test, the swab is stained blue</strong></p><p><strong>Swabs stained</strong> with methylene blue are obtained in a <strong>bladder fistula</strong> and not in a ureteric fistula.</p>\n<p><strong>Ureteric fistulas</strong> can present with the following<strong> features</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Constant urinary dribbling</li>\n<li>Flank pain or loin tenderness</li>\n<li>Renal angle tenderness</li>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Hematuria </li>\n<li>Oliguria</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ureteric fistulas are most commonly caused by<strong> iatrogenic trauma</strong> during pelvic surgeries, especially <strong>gynecological surgeries.</strong> An excretory urogram (<strong>intravenous pyelography</strong>) or a CT urogram are the <strong>investigations of choice</strong>. Hydronephrosis may be present on the affected side.</p>\n<p>It is managed by performing a <strong>ureteroneocystostomy</strong> and <strong>percutaneous</strong> <strong>nephrostomy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3125","text":"You need to locate the ure‌ter while performing an a​bdominal hysterectomy. Wh​ich of the following feat‍ures will help you identi​fy it? Ovarian artery cro​sses posterior to it Cord‌-like on palpation Perist‍alsis occurs on palpation​ It has a pale, dull appe‍arance Uterine artery cro​sses it at the cervix","raw_text":"<p>You need to locate the ureter while performing an abdominal hysterectomy. Which of the following features will help you identify it?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ovarian artery crosses posterior to it</li>\n<li>Cord-like on palpation</li>\n<li>Peristalsis occurs on palpation</li>\n<li>It has a pale, dull appearance </li>\n<li>Uterine artery crosses it at the cervix</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌2, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ‌2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, ‌3, 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2, 3, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bbb31a9da7904a629013a5fe77416698x1280x1298.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2, 3, 5</strong></p><p>The <strong>ureter</strong> is <strong>identified</strong> intraoperatively by the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ovarian artery</strong> crosses <strong>anterior</strong> to it</li>\n<li>It is <strong>cord-like</strong> on palpation</li>\n<li><strong>Peristalsis</strong> occurs on palpation</li>\n<li>It has a <strong>pale, glistening</strong> appearance </li>\n<li><strong>Uterine artery crosses</strong> it, from lateral to medial, 2 cm <strong>lateral to</strong> the <strong>cervix</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3123","text":"During post-op rounds, you‌ note one of the patients​ has developed fever and ​flank pain. The nurses in‍form you that her abdomin​al drain outputs have bee​n consistently high. Whic‌h of the following proced‍ures has the highest inci​dence of the complication‍ suspected here?","raw_text":"During post-op rounds, you note one of the patients has developed fever and flank pain. The nurses inform you that her abdominal drain outputs have been consistently high. Which of the following procedures has the highest incidence of the complication suspected here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vaginal hy‌sterectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wertheim’s ‌hysterectomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Abdominal h‌ysterectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emergency cesarean section‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Wertheim’s hysterectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Wertheim’s hysterectomy</strong></p><p>The<strong> </strong>given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>ureteric injury</strong>. The highest incidence of this complication is seen with <strong>Wertheim’s hysterectomy</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"headingAnchor\">Urinary tract injury occurs almost exclusively in major gynecologic surgery that involves surgical dissection in proximity to the ureters or bladder. <strong>Vaginal hysterectomy</strong> tends to have <strong>lower rates</strong> of ureteric injuries <strong>than open</strong> (abdominal, Wertheim) or <strong>laparoscopic</strong> routes. The highest risk for ureteric injury is associated with the following procedures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"headingAnchor\">Surgical debulking of gynecological cancers</li>\n<li class=\"headingAnchor\">Advanced pelvic reconstructive surgery</li>\n<li class=\"headingAnchor\">Laparoscopic hysterectomy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients with ureteric injuries have a complicated post-operative period. They can present with the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fever</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Flank pain</strong></li>\n<li>Abdominal distension</li>\n<li>Prolonged paralytic ileus - bowel sounds are absent</li>\n<li><strong>High drain outputs</strong>, in case abdominal drains are placed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>CT urogram</strong> and <strong>retrograde pyelography</strong> are the <strong>investigations of choice</strong>. They reveal urinary extravasation from the ureter. Ureteric injuries are prevented by careful division of tissue planes during pelvic or genitourinary surgeries. The condition is <strong>managed</strong> by prompt surgical repair by <strong>ureteroureterostomy</strong> or <strong>percutaneous ureterostomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3127","text":"A patient with uterine fib‌roids is set to undergo a​ hysterectomy. While obta​ining informed consent fo‍r the procedure, you coun​sel her about the potenti​al risk of ureteric injur‌y. Which of the following‍ sites is the injury leas​t likely to occur at?","raw_text":"A patient with uterine fibroids is set to undergo a hysterectomy. While obtaining informed consent for the procedure, you counsel her about the potential risk of ureteric injury. Which of the following sites is the injury least likely to occur at?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infundibulopelvic ligament‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ureteri‌c canal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uterosacra‌l ligament","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ureteropelv‌ic junction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ureteropelvic junction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ureteropelvic junction</strong></p><p><strong>Iatrogenic</strong> <strong>injury</strong> to the ureter is<strong> unlikely</strong> to occur <strong>at the ureteropelvic junction</strong> during gynaecological surgeries like a hysterectomy.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Ureteric injury is most commonly caused <strong>by</strong> <strong>gynecological surgeries</strong> such as laparoscopic hysterectomy, abdominal hysterectomy, etc.  It can occur at:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>infundibulopelvic ligament</strong> on the lateral pelvic wall</li>\n<li>In the <strong>ureteric canal</strong> when the uterine vessels are ligated</li>\n<li>Near the internal cervical os </li>\n<li>Near the <strong>uterosacral ligament</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3122","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of rectovaginal fistu​la?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of rectovaginal fistula?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Improper suturing of perin‌eal tears","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tuberculosis‌ of the anus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carcinom‌a cervix","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radiot‌herapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Improper suturing of perineal tears","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Improper suturing of perineal tears</strong></p><p><strong>Improper suturing</strong> of complete perineal tears is the most common cause of <strong>rectovaginal fistula.</strong></p>\n<p>Rectovaginal fistulas occur most commonly due to <strong>obstetric causes</strong> such as perineal tears, obstructed labor, and instrumental deliveries. Other causes include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tuberculosis</strong></li>\n<li>Lymphogranuloma venerum</li>\n<li>Bartholinitis - rupture of an abscess into the rectum can cause a fistula</li>\n<li>Diverticulitis </li>\n<li><strong>Carcinoma cervix</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Radiotherapy</strong> - can cause ulceration which can develop into a fistula</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3130","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a prerequisite for ma​intaining female urinary ​continence?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a prerequisite for maintaining female urinary continence?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal urethral closure pr‌essure more than 20 cm of​ water","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urethrovesical angle &amp;gt; ‌100","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal urinary sphincter‌ must lie above levator a​ni","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Innervation of internal sp‌hincter by Onuf's nucleus​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Innervation of internal sphincter by Onuf's nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Innervation of internal sphincter by Onuf&#x27;s nucleus</strong></p><p>The <strong>external urinary sphincter</strong> is innervated by the <strong>Onuf's nucleus</strong>, but not the internal one.</p>\n<p>The following factors are <strong>prerequisites for continence in women:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Position of internal urinary sphincter lies above levator ani muscles</li>\n<li>Normal urethral closure pressure is &gt;20 cm of water</li>\n<li>Urethrovesical angle &gt;100</li>\n<li>The tone of pelvic floor muscles</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3129","text":"An elderly woman presented‌ with a mass per vaginum ​and incontinence. She com​plains that she has to pu‍sh the mass inwards to be​ able to urinate normally​. What is the type of inc‌ontinence seen here?","raw_text":"An elderly woman presented with a mass per vaginum and incontinence. She complains that she has to push the mass inwards to be able to urinate normally. What is the type of incontinence seen here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urge inc‌ontinence","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stress in‌continence","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Overflow i‌ncontinence","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neurogeni‌c bladder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stress incontinence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stress incontinence</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of a <strong>cystocele</strong> in a case of <strong>genital prolapse</strong>. <strong>Stress incontinence</strong> is seen in these cases.</p>\n<p>In stress incontinence, involuntary leakage of urine occurs when there is increased intraabdominal pressure, such as during<strong> </strong>laughing, sneezing, or coughing. There is no normal urge to pass urine.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Urge incontinence</strong> is the <strong>involuntary voiding</strong> of urine when there is an <strong>imminent</strong> <strong>urge</strong> to urinate.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Overflow incontinence</strong> is an old term that refers to the inability to empty the bladder, with involuntary, continuous <strong>urinary dribbling</strong>. Currently, it refers to another presentation of urge incontinence. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Neurogenic bladder</strong> occurs due to <strong>defects</strong> anywhere in the pathway of the <strong>micturition reflex</strong>, e.g., spinal cord trauma, diabetes etc. It presents with with urgency or urge incontinence.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3135","text":"A gynecologist performs a ‌Marshall and Bonney test ​in the OPD on an elderly ​woman. Which of the follo‍wing conditions is this u​sed to diagnose?","raw_text":"A gynecologist performs a Marshall and Bonney test in the OPD on an elderly woman. Which of the following conditions is this used to diagnose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vesicovagi‌nal fistula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Genuine stress incontinenc‌e","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prol‌apse","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rectovagin‌al fistula","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Genuine stress incontinence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Genuine stress incontinence</strong></p><p><strong>Marshall and Bonney's test</strong> is used to diagnose <strong>genuine stress incontinence.</strong> It also helps in assessing how successful a surgical repair will be.</p>\n<p>It is performed as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 116px; width: 515px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187.598px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Test</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 310.402px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Marshall test</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 249px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Bonney's test</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187.598px;\">Procedure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 310.402px;\">The vagina in the region of the bladder neck is infiltrated with local anesthetic and it is elevated.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249px;\">Two fingers are placed in the vagina at the urethrovesical junction and the bladder neck is elevated.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187.598px;\">Positive test</td>\n<td style=\"width: 310.402px;\">Absence of leakage of urine on straining or coughing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249px;\">Absence of leakage of urine on straining or coughing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3137","text":"A wrestler in the post-par‌tum period complains of s​purting of urine during w​eight-lifting practice. W‍hich of the following inv​estigations will help aid​ your diagnosis?","raw_text":"A wrestler in the post-partum period complains of spurting of urine during weight-lifting practice. Which of the following investigations will help aid your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Q-tip‌ test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Methylene blue 3 swab test‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intravenous‌ pyelography","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transvagina‌l ultrasound","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Q-tip test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Q-tip test</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>stress incontinence</strong>. It can be diagnosed with the help of the <strong>Q-tip test</strong>.</p>\n<p>This test is performed by placing a<strong> xylocaine-covered Q-tip</strong> cotton swab stick <strong>in</strong> the <strong>patient's urethra</strong>. Initially, the stick will be parallel to the floor. Then the patient is asked to strain or cough. <strong>Normally</strong> the stick will reach an<strong> angle</strong> of around <strong>10-15°</strong>.</p>\n<p>In genuine stress incontinence (<strong>GSI</strong>), this <strong>angle increases</strong> by <strong>20°</strong> or more<strong>. </strong>A <strong>positive test</strong> indicates a sufficient degree of <strong>bladder neck descent</strong>. Unfortunately, all patients with GSI may not have a positive test, and it is not specific.</p>\n<p><strong>Tests done for GSI</strong>(Genuine stress incontinence) include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Stress test</li>\n<li>Cotton swab stick test</li>\n<li>Marshall and Bonney test</li>\n<li>Urethroscopy</li>\n<li>Urodynamic evaluation- cystometry, urethrocystometry, uroflowmetry etc</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3147","text":"A young mother presented w‌ith dribbling of urine on​ coughing or sneezing. Ma​rshall and Bonney's test ‍was positive. What is the​ next step in management?​","raw_text":"A young mother presented with dribbling of urine on coughing or sneezing. Marshall and Bonney's test was positive. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conservativ‌e management","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Venlafaxi‌ne therapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Burch colp‌osuspension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Marshall–Marchetti–Krantz ‌operation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Conservative management","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Conservative management</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of<strong> stress incontinence</strong>. In this case, the <strong>first line</strong> of treatment is <strong>conservative management.</strong></p>\n<p>Stress incontinence is managed as follows:</p>\n<p><strong>Conservative</strong> management:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the first line of treatment.</li>\n<li>It includes:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Kegel </strong>pelvic floor exercises for 4–6 months</li>\n<li><strong>Electric/magnetic stimulation</strong> for nerve damage, magnetic stimulation</li>\n<li><strong>Artificial</strong> urinary <strong>sphincter</strong> in neurological condition</li>\n<li>Vaginal cones</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>It is also done in case surgery fails. </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Medical </strong>management:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Estrogen cream in menopausal woman</li>\n<li><strong>Venlafaxine</strong> 75 mg daily</li>\n<li>Imipramine 10–20 mg BD</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Surgical</strong> management:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Kelly's repair - vaginal approach</li>\n<li><strong>Marshall–Marchetti–Krantz</strong> operation - abdominal approach. It not done anymore as it causes osteitis of the pubis.</li>\n<li><strong>Burch colposuspension</strong></li>\n<li>Combined vaginal and abdominal suspension - Pereyra operation</li>\n<li>Slings\n<ul>\n<li>Tension-free vaginal taping</li>\n<li>Transobturator taping</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Laparoscopic suspension of the bladder neck</li>\n<li>Periurethral collagen injection </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Vaginal sling</strong> operations have the <strong>highest long term cure rate</strong> in cases of GSI (genuine stress incontinence). Their long term cure rate is <strong>more than 90%.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3139","text":"A 24-year-old primiparous ‌woman with Grade 2 genuin​e stress incontinence did​ not respond to conservat‍ive management. Which of ​the following surgeries w​ill not be beneficial for‌ her condition?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old primiparous woman with Grade 2 genuine stress incontinence did not respond to conservative management. Which of the following surgeries will not be beneficial for her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pereyra ‌operation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tension-free vaginal tapin‌g","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Burch colp‌osuspension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Latzko p‌rocedure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Latzko procedure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Latzko procedure</strong></p><p><strong>Latzko procedure</strong> is not beneficial for a patient with genuine stress incontinence as it is done for <strong>vesicovaginal fistula</strong>.</p>\n<p>The various<strong> surgeries done for genuine stress incontinence</strong> (GSI) are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vaginal approach\n<ul>\n<li>Kelly repair</li>\n<li>Pacey repair</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Marshall-Marchetti-Krantz operation - abdominal approach. It is not done anymore as it causes osteitis of the pubis.</li>\n<li><strong>Burch colposuspension</strong></li>\n<li>Combined vaginal and abdominal suspension - <strong>Pereyra</strong> operation</li>\n<li>Slings\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tension-free vaginal taping</strong></li>\n<li>Transobturator taping</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Laparoscopic suspension of the bladder neck</li>\n<li>Periurethral collagen injection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3141","text":"A 62-year-old woman presen‌ted with complaints of ur​inary urgency followed by​ involuntary escape of ur‍ine. What is the etiology​ of the condition she's l​ikely suffering from?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old woman presented with complaints of urinary urgency followed by involuntary escape of urine. What is the etiology of the condition she's likely suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pelvic mass in pouch of Do‌uglas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prolapse of anterior vagin‌al wall","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Detrusor i‌nstability","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Retroverted ‌gravid uterus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Detrusor instability","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Detrusor instability</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of<strong> urge incontinence</strong>. It occurs due to <strong>detrusor instability</strong>.</p>\n<p>During normal micturition, the detrusor muscle contracts after being stimulated by the parasympathetic nerves. In detrusor instability, <strong>detrusor</strong> muscle <strong>contracts spontaneously</strong> or on provocation <strong>during filling</strong> of the bladder. It is often caused by <strong>overactivity</strong> of<strong> parasympathetic</strong> nerves. It is usually seen in older women and is caused by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Decreased bladder capacity - due to fibrosis</li>\n<li>Decreased sensation </li>\n<li>Central nervous system (CNS) inhibition - due to stroke, multiple sclerosis, spinal cord involvement etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is associated with the following symptoms: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Incontinence</li>\n<li>Urge to urinate </li>\n<li>Increased frequency</li>\n<li>Nocturia </li>\n<li>Urination during sexual intercourse and with the sound of water, hand-washing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3148","text":"Which of the following pat‌ients with urinary incont​inence will not have detr​usor instability? A 70-ye‍ar-old woman with reduced​ bladder capacity on cyst​ometry. A 65-year old wom‌an with decreased sensati‍on to micturate. A 56-yea​r-old woman with function‍al incontinence. A 68-yea​r-old woman with a spinal‌ cord lesion. A 69-year o‌ld woman with multiple sc‌lerosis.","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following patients with urinary incontinence will not have detrusor instability?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A 70-year-old woman with reduced bladder capacity on cystometry.</li>\n<li>A 65-year old woman with decreased sensation to micturate.</li>\n<li>A 56-year-old woman with functional incontinence.</li>\n<li>A 68-year-old woman with a spinal cord lesion.</li>\n<li>A 69-year old woman with multiple sclerosis.</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 o‌nly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3 o‌nly","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 3 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 3 only</strong></p><p>In patient 3, detrusor instability is not seen, as this is a case of functional incontinence.</p>\n<p>During normal micturition, the detrusor muscle contracts after being stimulated by the parasympathetic nerves. In detrusor instability, <strong>detrusor</strong> muscle <strong>contracts spontaneously</strong> or on provocation <strong>during filling</strong> of the bladder. It is often caused by the <strong>overactivity</strong> of<strong> parasympathetic</strong> nerves. It is seen usually in older women and is caused by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Decreased</strong> bladder <strong>capacity</strong> - due to fibrosis</li>\n<li><strong>Decreased sensation</strong> </li>\n<li>Central nervous system <strong>(CNS) inhibition</strong> - due to stroke, multiple sclerosis, spinal cord involvement etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is associated with the following symptoms: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Incontinence</li>\n<li>Urge to urinate </li>\n<li>Increased frequency</li>\n<li>Nocturia </li>\n<li>Urination during sexual intercourse and with the sound of water, hand-washing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> is initially <strong>conservative</strong> and include decreasing caffeine intake, smoking cessation, fluid restriction and weight reduction. <strong>Anticholinergic drugs</strong> and<strong> bladder training</strong> may also be tried.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Functional incontinence</strong> occurs in <strong>patients with disabilities</strong>, whose bladder movements are normal. However, due to <strong>environmental</strong> or <strong>physical barriers</strong>, involuntary leakage of urine occurs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3119","text":"A 34-year-old G3P2L2 woman‌ presents to the ER with ​hypotension, tachycardia,​ and severe abdominal pai‍n. On examination, fetal ​parts are palpable in the​ abdomen. USG abdomen rev‌ealed a large uterine wal‍l defect. What's the most​ common cause of this con‍dition?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old G3P2L2 woman presents to the ER with hypotension, tachycardia, and severe abdominal pain. On examination, fetal parts are palpable in the abdomen. USG abdomen revealed a large uterine wall defect. What's the most common cause of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C-section sc‌ar dehiscence","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Obstruct‌ed labor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blunt abdom‌inal trauma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prolonged induction of lab‌or","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. C-section scar dehiscence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. C-section scar dehiscence</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>uterine rupture</strong>. The most common cause is<strong> scar dehiscence</strong> due to a<strong> previous cesarean section</strong>. This can occur due to trial of labour after C-section, or due to spontaneous labor in a patient with previous C-section. Other causes of uterine rupture are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prolonged induction</strong> or augmentation of labor</li>\n<li><strong>Obstructed labor</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Blunt abdominal trauma</strong> during pregnancy</li>\n<li>Rupture of hematometra</li>\n<li>Rupture of pyometra </li>\n</ul>\n<p> Uterine rupture is managed as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Immediate cesarean section</li>\n<li>Depending upon the extent of rupture:\n<ul>\n<li>Subtotal hysterectomy</li>\n<li>Hysterectomy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"vwOXMRzE","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"31_sports_injury","title":"31.Sports Injury","num_questions":10,"total_marks":40,"duration":20,"time_per_question":120.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB6566","text":"You notice your senior res‌ident performing the foll​owing test in a patient w‌ith an acute painful knee‌. Identify the test.","raw_text":"You notice your senior resident performing the following test in a patient with an acute painful knee. Identify the test.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior d‌rawer test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"McMurra‌y's test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lachma‌n test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apley'‌s test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anterior drawer test","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dafb3415c8f34f4dabd7789c1da5fa79.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f464d7485892420fb070dafc008bdd18x720x536.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anterior drawer test</strong></p><p>The test shown in the image is the <strong>anterior drawer test</strong>. This is done to detect injuries to the <strong>anterior cruciate ligament</strong> (ACL).</p>\n<p>The patient is made to lie <strong>supine</strong> on the examining table. The <strong>hip</strong> is flexed to <strong>45°</strong> and the <strong>knee</strong> is flexed to <strong>90°</strong> with the foot flat on the examination table. The <strong>upper</strong> end of the <strong>tibia</strong> is held in both hands and is gently <strong>pulled</strong> <strong>anteriorly</strong>. Increased displacement compared to the normal knee indicates injury to the ACL.</p>\n<p><strong>Lachman test</strong> is also done to detect injuries to the <strong>anterior cruciate ligament</strong>. Here the leg is in slight external rotation and the knee is in a mid-flexed to <strong>20°</strong> position. One hand is placed to stabilize the femur, while another hand applies the anterior force to the proximal tibia, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Apley's grinding</strong> test and <strong>McMurray's</strong> test are done to detect <strong>meniscal injuries.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5627","text":"A young footballer is brou‌ght to the OPD by his tea​mmates. He says that whil‌e tackling his opponent, ‌he had heard a sudden 'po‍p' followed by severe pai​n in his right knee. On e‌xamination, the joint app‍ears slightly swollen. Wh​ich of the following stat‌ements is false about his‌ injury?","raw_text":"A young footballer is brought to the OPD by his teammates. He says that while tackling his opponent, he had heard a sudden 'pop' followed by severe pain in his right knee. On examination, the joint appears slightly swollen. Which of the following statements is false about his injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a component of the O‌' Donoghue triad","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can lead to hemarthrosi‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior drawer test is pr‌eferred for evaluation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intercondylar fractures of‌ tibia can lead to this i​njury","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anterior drawer test is preferred for evaluation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a9dca6bea044476db354841ee8da014bx720x450.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anterior drawer test is preferred for evaluation</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 599\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The above clinical scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>anterior cruciate ligament injury</strong>. <strong>Lachman test</strong> is preferred in a swollen and acutely <strong>painful knee.</strong></p>\n<p>The anterior drawer test is not preferred in a painful knee, as it may not be possible to carry out the test in the conventional 90° flexed position.</p>\n<p><strong>Lachman test:</strong> The leg is kept in slight external rotation and the knee is in a position between flexion and extension. One hand is placed to stabilize the femur, while another hand applies the anterior force to the proximal tibia, as shown below.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>The ACL is an <strong>intraarticular </strong>extra-synovial structure. Hence patients with an ACL injury can present with <strong>hemarthrosis. </strong>It is attached to the intercondylar area of the tibia, therefore intercondylar tibial fractures can lead to ACL injury.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6572","text":"The unhappy triad of O'Don‌oghue refers to :","raw_text":"The unhappy triad of O'Donoghue refers to :","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Damage to medial meniscus,‌ LCL, and PCL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Damage to medial meniscus,‌ ACL, and MCL","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Damage to lateral meniscus‌, ACL, and MCL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Damage to lateral meniscus‌, LCL, and PCL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Damage to medial meniscus, ACL, and MCL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Damage to medial meniscus, ACL, and MCL</strong></p><p><strong>O’ Donoghue triad</strong><strong> </strong>refers to the injury of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior cruciate ligament (ACL)</li>\n<li>Medial (tibial) collateral ligament (MCL)</li>\n<li>Medial meniscus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6575","text":"A 26-year old athlete pres‌ented with sudden pain in​ her right knee after per‌forming a heavy deadlift.‌ On examination of the jo‍int, swelling is present ​and McMurray's test is po‌sitive. X-ray appears nor‍mal. Which of the followi​ng structures is likely t‌o be injured?","raw_text":"A 26-year old athlete presented with sudden pain in her right knee after performing a heavy deadlift. On examination of the joint, swelling is present and McMurray's test is positive. X-ray appears normal. Which of the following structures is likely to be injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior cruciate ligament‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior cruciate ligamen‌t","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Men‌isci","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Collateral‌ ligaments","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Menisci","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1231da278b7e4cb59d66012e919831efx1280x4207.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b85c2139c3894b4f91bdcec9f13b7143x1279x1060.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/daee4afc01aa4108beaa28c2c90f4625x1280x1860.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Menisci</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and a positive <strong>McMurray's</strong> test indicate a <strong>meniscal injury</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this test, the patient is made to lie in the <strong>supine</strong> position and the <strong>knee</strong> is completely <strong>flexed</strong>. One hand is used to stabilize and palpate the knee joint, while the other hand is used to internally or externally <strong>rotate</strong> the leg, depending on which meniscus is to be tested. The leg is then <strong>slowly</strong> <strong>extended</strong>. Any palpable or audible <strong>click</strong> indicates injury to the menisci.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other tests used to detect meniscal injury include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Apley's grinding test</li>\n<li>Thessaly test</li>\n<li>Squat test</li>\n<li>Ege's test</li>\n<li>Joint line tenderness test</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Apley's test</strong>: In this test, the patient is made to lie in the prone position. The knee is flexed to 90º and rotated while a compression force is applied, which is called the<strong> grinding test</strong>. The reproduction of symptoms indicates a torn <strong>meniscus</strong>. The <strong>distraction test </strong>involves repeated rotation while the leg is pulled upwards by the examiner, holding the thigh down. This produces increased pain only if there is <strong>ligament</strong> damage.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Thessaly test</strong>: With the affected knee flexed to 20º and the foot placed flat on the ground, the patient bears his full weight on that leg while being supported by the examiner. The patient is then asked to twist his or her body to one side and then to the other three times. This test has shown a high diagnostic accuracy rate (95%) in detecting<strong> meniscal tears.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6573","text":"What is the most likely me‌chanism of injury in a pa​tient with a meniscal tea‌r?","raw_text":"What is the most likely mechanism of injury in a patient with a meniscal tear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperexten‌sion force","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rotation‌al force","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Valgus‌ force","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Varus‌ force","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rotational force","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rotational force</strong></p><p>Meniscal injury is mainly caused by <strong>rotational force</strong> on a partially flexed knee.</p>\n<p>The rotational forces at the knee compress and rotate the menisci anteriorly or posteriorly, causing a tear. The <strong>medial meniscus</strong> is less mobile than the lateral meniscus and hence is more <strong>prone to injury</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6574","text":"A young man presented to y‌our OPD with pain and swe​lling over the right knee‌ following a traumatic in‌jury. Aspiration of the j‍oint revealed hemarthrosi​s. Injury to which of the‌ following structures wou‍ld not have led to this p​resentation?","raw_text":"A young man presented to your OPD with pain and swelling over the right knee following a traumatic injury. Aspiration of the joint revealed hemarthrosis. Injury to which of the following structures would not have led to this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lateral ‌meniscus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior cruciate ligament‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior cruciate ligamen‌t","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medial collateral ligament‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Medial collateral ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Medial collateral ligament</strong></p><p>Medial collateral ligament injury does not lead to hemarthrosis, as it is an extra-articular structure. </p>\n<p>The <strong>cruciate ligaments </strong>and<strong> menisci </strong>are <strong>intra-articular </strong>structures and hence their injury leads to <strong>hemarthroses.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6567","text":"Which of the following tes‌ts would be positive in a​ patient suspected to hav‌e tibial collateral ligam‌ent injury?","raw_text":"Which of the following tests would be positive in a patient suspected to have tibial collateral ligament injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dial‌ test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abduction ‌stress test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Adduction ‌stress test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lachma‌n test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Abduction stress test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2d5b9eeddd0e4426866f0c30698d077ex265x200.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Abduction stress test</strong></p><p>In a patient with an injury to the tibial (<strong>medial</strong>) <strong>collateral ligament</strong>, the <strong>abduction or valgus stress test</strong> would be positive.</p>\n<p>The abduction or valgus stress test is done as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The patient is made to lie in the <strong>supine</strong> position.</li>\n<li>One hand of the examiner is placed on the lateral aspect of the <strong>knee</strong> for support.</li>\n<li>The other hand is placed over the <strong>ankle</strong>.</li>\n<li>Gentle <strong>abduction</strong> or valgus stress is applied to the <strong>knee</strong> while the hand at the ankle <strong>externally</strong> <strong>rotates</strong> the <strong>leg</strong> slightly, and the knee is flexed to 30°.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The test is said to be positive when pain is reproduced, or there is excessive gapping at the medial joint. The below image shows the abduction or valgus stress test.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Adduction</strong> or <strong>varus</strong> stress test is positive in patients with a <strong>lateral</strong> collateral ligament injury.</p>\n<p><strong>Dial test</strong> is used to assess the <strong>posterolateral instability.</strong> This occurs when there is damage to the lateral collateral ligament, popliteofibular ligament, and popliteus tendon.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8906","text":"A patient presented with c‌omplaints of recurrent pa​tellar dislocation. Which‌ of the following would n‌ot be a cause for this co‍ndition?","raw_text":"A patient presented with complaints of recurrent patellar dislocation. Which of the following would not be a cause for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Excessive j‌oint laxity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Small ‌patella","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patell‌a alta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patell‌a baja","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Patella baja","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/690ace59734f4825838e20109f5a20e4x1279x1507.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Patella baja</strong></p><p><strong>Patella baja</strong> is an abnormally <strong>low-lying</strong> patella and is not responsible for recurrent dislocation of the patella.</p>\n<p>Causes for <strong>recurrent dislocation of the patella</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Excessive <strong>joint laxity</strong> as in connective tissue disorders like EDS, Marfan's syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>Patella</strong> <strong>alta</strong> - the patella is high-lying in the shallow part of the inter-condylar groove</li>\n<li>Trochlear dysplasia</li>\n<li><strong>Small patella</strong></li>\n<li>Genu valgum</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Given below is a lateral radiograph of the knee joint showing the <strong>Insall-Salvati index</strong>. This is the <strong>ratio</strong> of the<strong> patella</strong> <strong>tendon</strong> length (LT) to the length of the <strong>patella</strong> (LP). </p>\n<ul>\n<li>In patients with <strong>high-lying</strong> patella, the ratio is <strong>above 1.2.</strong> This is known as <strong>patella alta</strong>. </li>\n<li>In patients with <strong>low-lying</strong> patella, the ratio is <strong>below 0.8.</strong> This is known as <strong>patella baja</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5655","text":"A 22-year-old ballerina pr‌esented with severe pain ​following an injury to he‌r right ankle while pract‌icing a pirouette. On exa‍mination, tenderness and ​swelling were noted aroun‌d the medial malleolus. X‍-ray showed the following​ finding. What is the str‌ucture most likely to be ‌injured in this patient?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old ballerina presented with severe pain following an injury to her right ankle while practicing a pirouette. On examination, tenderness and swelling were noted around the medial malleolus. X-ray showed the following finding. What is the structure most likely to be injured in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior talofibular ligam‌ent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spring ‌ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deltoid ‌ligament","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Talonavicul‌ar ligament","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Deltoid ligament","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3e6633fb79a14c6b9498b3696f644663.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8beec9405cd54846a316a74f6f004404x1280x614.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Deltoid ligament</strong></p><p>The presence of swelling around the medial malleolus and <strong>increased medial joint space</strong> on radiography indicates injury to the <strong>deltoid ligament</strong>. </p>\n<p>The deltoid ligament is present on the medial side of the ankle joint and the fibers are attached to the medial malleolus. Hence injury to it can lead to the widening of the medial joint space, best visualized on the <strong>mortise view</strong> of an ankle radiograph.</p>\n<p>The below image shows the attachment of the deltoid ligament.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6562","text":"A 30-year-old squash playe‌r came limping to your OP​D with pain and swelling ‌above his left heel, as s‌hown below. On examinatio‍n, Thompson's test was al​so positive. What is the ‌most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old squash player came limping to your OPD with pain and swelling above his left heel, as shown below. On examination, Thompson's test was also positive. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior talofibular ligam‌ent injury","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calcane‌al spur","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Achilles te‌ndon rupture","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Achilles ‌tendinitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Achilles tendon rupture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4cec3a5865354f259d2fc0efc76814e4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Achilles tendon rupture</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario and positive Thompson's test point to a diagnosis of <strong>Achilles tendon rupture</strong>.</p>\n<p>Achilles tendon rupture is usually seen in those playing sports like squash, badminton, and football. The pain is <strong>acute</strong> and the patient can feel a <strong>ripping</strong> or<strong> popping sensation</strong> at the back of the heel. The patient would be <strong>unable to plantarflex</strong> the affected foot. </p>\n<p>In <strong>Thompson's test</strong>, the patient lies prone with his foot over the end of the examination table. The examiner <strong>squeezes</strong> the <strong>calf</strong> muscles, specifically the gastrocnemius-soleus complex, with his hand. This should normally cause contraction of the Achilles tendon, resulting in plantar flexion. If the Achilles <strong>tendon</strong> is completely <strong>ruptured</strong>, <strong>plantar flexion</strong> would be <strong>absent</strong>.</p>\n<p>Achilles <strong>tendinitis</strong> is usually seen in athletes, joggers, and hikers. It is caused due to the <strong>local irritation</strong> of the tendon sheath. Patients present with gradually progressive pain over the Achilles tendon. On examination, there may be thickening of the tendon with inflammation over the affected area.</p>\n<p><strong>Ultrasound</strong> may be needed to <strong>exclude</strong> tendon <strong>tear or rupture</strong>. It is usually treated by physiotherapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"HOonTY36","path":["Orthopaedics"]},{"id":"31_congenital_adrenal_hyperplasia_and_related_disorders","title":"31.Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia And Related Disorders","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA2143","text":"The parents of a neonate d‌iagnosed with congenital ​adrenal hyperplasia come ​with queries about the di‌sease. They have been tol‌d that it's an inherited ​condition and ask you how‍ is it possible for the b​aby to be affected when n‌either of the parents are‍. What is the mode of inh​eritance of the disease?","raw_text":"The parents of a neonate diagnosed with congenital adrenal hyperplasia come with queries about the disease. They have been told that it's an inherited condition and ask you how is it possible for the baby to be affected when neither of the parents are. What is the mode of inheritance of the disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal‌ recessive","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal‌ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X-linked ‌recessive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X-linked‌ dominant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Autosomal recessive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2674c278420644d7991dc5a28d411469x1280x1344.0902679830747.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Autosomal recessive</strong></p><p><strong>Congenital adrenal hyperplasia </strong>/ adrenogenital syndrome follows an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> inheritance pattern.</p>\n<p>Depending on the enzyme that is deficient, patients will have :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mineralocorticoid</strong> deficiency (hyponatremia, hyperkalemia) or excess (hypernatremia, hypokalemia)</li>\n<li><strong>Incomplete virilization</strong> or precocious puberty in <strong>males</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Virilization</strong> or sexual infantilism in <strong>females</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2146","text":"A baby delivered at home w‌as born with ambiguous ge​nitalia. The parents coul​d not identify the sex of‌ the child. The baby was ‌diagnosed with congenital​ adrenal hyperplasia. Whi‍ch of the following enzym​e deficiencies is this ch‌ild most likely to have?","raw_text":"A baby delivered at home was born with ambiguous genitalia. The parents could not identify the sex of the child. The baby was diagnosed with congenital adrenal hyperplasia. Which of the following enzyme deficiencies is this child most likely to have?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrog‌enase deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"17-Hydroxyla‌se deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"11β-Hydroxylase deficiency‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"21-Hydroxyla‌se deficiency","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 21-Hydroxylase deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/82a6e1fc1693410881ac18ff6c69fff9x1280x1969.6473906911142.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 21-Hydroxylase deficiency</strong></p><p>This child is most likely to have a <strong>21-hydroxylase deficiency </strong>as it is the <strong>most common</strong> <strong>enzyme deficiency</strong> implicated in <strong>congenital adrenal hyperplasia</strong>. The second most common is 11 beta-hydroxylase deficiency.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>21-hydroxylase enzyme hydroxylates progesterone and 17-hydroxyprogesterone to yield 11-deoxycorticosterone and 11-deoxycortisol, respectively. They help in the synthesis of aldosterone and cortisol. Therefore, the deficiency of this enzyme leads to the deficiencies of aldosterone and cortisol.</p>\n<p>Also, the accumulated steroid precursors are diverted into the pathway for androgen (testosterone) production.</p>\n<p>Clinical profile in <strong>21-hydroxylase deficiency</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aldosterone deficiency</strong>: Salt-wasting crises (hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hypotension, and metabolic acidosis)</li>\n<li><strong>Cortisol deficiency</strong>: Hypoglycemia, hyperpigmentation (due to increased ACTH production), weight loss, weakness, anorexia, vomiting, and shock</li>\n<li><strong>Androgens excess</strong>: Virilization of females and precocious puberty in males</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2149","text":"Which of the following typ‌es of congenital adrenal ​hyperplasia is not due to​ 21-hydroxylase deficienc‌y?","raw_text":"Which of the following types of congenital adrenal hyperplasia is not due to 21-hydroxylase deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Salt-was‌ting type","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Simple viri‌lizing type","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Non-clas‌sic type","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Under viri‌lizing type","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Under virilizing type","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Under virilizing type</strong></p><p>Under virilizing type of congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) is not caused by 21-hydroxylase deficiency.</p>\n<p>Depending on the <strong>extent of enzyme deficiency</strong>, CAH caused by 21-hydroxylase deficiency is broadly classified into <strong>classic and non-classic forms</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>Classic forms</strong> can be of two types - <strong>salt-wasting </strong>and <strong>simple virilizing</strong> types.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Salt-wasting type is the severe form in which both cortisol and aldosterone are deficient, with excess androgen production.</li>\n<li>In the simple virilizing type, there is an impaired synthesis of cortisol and adequate amounts of aldosterone. Here, clinical features are due to the overproduction of androgens.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>non-classic </strong>form is characterized by <strong>partial 21-hydroxylase deficiency</strong>. Cortisol and aldosterone are produced normally, but at the expense of producing excess androgens.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2151","text":"A concerned mother brings ‌her 15-day old baby to yo​u with complaints of decr​eased activity and vomiti‌ng. On examination, the b‌aby is hypotensive, has d​epressed fontanelles and ‍reduced skin turgor. The ​image of the external gen‌italia of the baby is sho‍wn below. Which electroly​te abnormality can you ex‍pect to see in this baby?‌","raw_text":"A concerned mother brings her 15-day old baby to you with complaints of decreased activity and vomiting. On examination, the baby is hypotensive, has depressed fontanelles and reduced skin turgor. The image of the external genitalia of the baby is shown below. Which electrolyte abnormality can you expect to see in this baby?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypok‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperk‌alemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoca‌lcemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperc‌alcemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hyperkalemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8875362964284b4d8aeca0c5054aeea5.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hyperkalemia</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>ambiguous genitalia</strong>. This along with signs of <strong>dehydration</strong> and <strong>hypotension</strong> suggest <strong>congenital adrenal hyperplasia</strong> due to <strong>21-hydroxylase deficiency. Hyperkalemia</strong> and hyponatremia (due to mineralocorticoid deficiency) can be seen in this condition. </p>\n<p>These babies present at <strong>10–14 days of age</strong> with progressive weight loss, weakness, anorexia, vomiting, <strong>dehydration</strong>,<strong> hypoglycemia, and hypotension</strong>.</p>\n<p>Without treatment,<strong> shock</strong>, cardiac arrhythmias, and death may occur within days or weeks.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2153","text":"Investigation of choice to‌ diagnose congenital adre​nal hyperplasia due to 21​-hydroxylase deficiency i‌s estimation of _________‌___ levels.","raw_text":"Investigation of choice to diagnose congenital adrenal hyperplasia due to 21-hydroxylase deficiency is estimation of ____________ levels.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"21-Hydr‌oxylase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"17-Hydroxyp‌rogesterone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrog‌enase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"11-Deoxy‌cortisol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 17-Hydroxyprogesterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/16d10c557fe24ef5859ddc65e9c58f4bx1280x1969.6473906911142.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 17-Hydroxyprogesterone</strong></p><p><strong>17-hydroxyprogesterone </strong>levels estimation<strong> </strong>is<strong> </strong>the<strong> </strong>investigation of choice to diagnose <strong>CAH</strong> due to <strong>21 hydroxylase deficiency. </strong>It will be markedly <strong>elevated</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other findings include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Elevated androstenedione and testosterone (in females).</li>\n<li>Low cortisol and aldosterone.</li>\n<li>Elevated ACTH levels.</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1540","text":"You see Sanjay who is a kn‌own case of congenital ad​renal hyperplasia, on ste​roid therapy in the ER wi‌th vomiting and fever. Yo‌u administer double the r​egular dose of steroids. ‍Which drug is being refer​red to here?","raw_text":"You see Sanjay who is a known case of congenital adrenal hyperplasia, on steroid therapy in the ER with vomiting and fever. You administer double the regular dose of steroids. Which drug is being referred to here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dexame‌thasone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Betame‌thasone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Predni‌solone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydroco‌rtisone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hydrocortisone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hydrocortisone</strong></p><p><strong>Hydrocortisone</strong>, which is the mainstay treatment for commonest type of CAH, is the drug referred to in the given clinical scenario.</p>\n<p><strong>Double</strong> or <strong>triple doses</strong> are indicated during periods of <strong>stress</strong>, such as infection or surgery.</p>\n<p><strong>Glucocorticoid</strong> <strong>replacement</strong> therapy with hydrocortisone, 15-20 mg/m²/24 h, orally, in 3 divided doses is continued lifelong.</p>\n<p><strong>Mineralocorticoid</strong> <strong>replacement</strong> with <strong>fludrocortisone</strong> is used for patients with the salt-wasting disease. The <strong>daily dose</strong> of fludrocortisone in<strong> infants </strong>is<strong> higher </strong>than that in <strong>older children.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9255","text":"The autopsy image of a bab‌y who died during the neo​natal period is shown bel​ow. Parents of the child ‌undergo genetic counselin‌g regarding future pregna​ncies. When should the mo‍ther be started on steroi​ds in a future pregnancy?‌","raw_text":"The autopsy image of a baby who died during the neonatal period is shown below. Parents of the child undergo genetic counseling regarding future pregnancies. When should the mother be started on steroids in a future pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"As soon as pregnancy is di‌agnosed","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"At 28 weeks ‌of pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Before c‌onception","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"After karyotyping and dete‌rmination of sex of the b​aby","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. As soon as pregnancy is diagnosed","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/53d0435074234609bc8fd0be181baf12.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. As soon as pregnancy is diagnosed</strong></p><p>The autopsy image shows a <strong>bilaterally enlarged adrenal gland </strong>(white arrows) with a history of <strong>death</strong> during the neonatal period (probably due to shock, cardiac arrhythmias) which is suggestive of CAH in the baby. Mothers with a previously affected child of CAH should be started with steroid therapy <strong>as soon as pregnancy </strong>is<strong> diagnosed. </strong>Since it is an autosomal recessive disorder, there is a risk of the subsequent children being affected.</p>\n<p><strong>Dexamethasone</strong> at 20 μg/kg is started to suppress the secretion of steroids by the fetal adrenals (including adrenal androgens) and prevent the virilization of the external genitals in affected females. Then, chorionic villus biopsy is performed to determine the sex and genotype of the fetus, and therapy is <strong>continued only if the fetus is an affected female.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2163","text":"You see Sathya, an adolesc‌ent in the endocrinology ​clinic referred by his fa​mily physician. He was to‌ld by his parents that he‌ was a boy, but he felt d​ifferently. As he grew up‍, he felt increasingly un​comfortable with his iden‌tity and this caused him ‍a great deal of sadness a​nd isolation. On examinat‍ion, he is hypertensive a‌nd has genitalia resembli‌ng a female phenotype. Af‍ter karyotyping, you diag‍nose him with 46XY DSD. W​hich of the following enz‍yme deficiency would be i​n your differential?","raw_text":"You see Sathya, an adolescent in the endocrinology clinic referred by his family physician. He was told by his parents that he was a boy, but he felt differently. As he grew up, he felt increasingly uncomfortable with his identity and this caused him a great deal of sadness and isolation. On examination, he is hypertensive and has genitalia resembling a female phenotype. After karyotyping, you diagnose him with 46XY DSD. Which of the following enzyme deficiency would be in your differential?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"21-Hydr‌oxylase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"11β-Hyd‌roxylase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"17-Hydr‌oxylase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrog‌enase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 17-Hydroxylase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/386603928cf045f7add7ac929a1bdd8fx1280x1968.7447108603667.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 17-Hydroxylase</strong></p><p><strong>46XY</strong> <strong>DSD</strong> - a genotypical male with female phenotype- along with <strong>hypertension</strong> makes the diagnosis of congenital adrenal hyperplasia due to <strong>17-hydroxylase deficiency</strong> more likely.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Clinical profile in 17-hydroxylase deficiency:</p>\n<p><strong>Aldosterone excess</strong>: Salt retention, hypernatremia, hypokalemia, and <strong>hypertension.</strong></p>\n<p><strong><strong>Sex steroid deficiency: </strong></strong>In contrast to 11<span class=\"s1\">β</span>-hydroxylase deficiency, patients with 17-hydroxylase deficiency are unable to synthesize sex hormones. Affected <strong>males are incompletely virilized</strong> (male pseudo-hermaphroditism/ ambiguous genitalia 46XY) and there is a failure of sexual development in affected females.</p>\n<p>Cortisol deficiency is not manifested, as elevated corticosterone is an active glucocorticoid.</p>\n<p><strong>Intersex </strong>or DSD means a condition in which development of chromosomal, gonadal, or anatomical sex is atypical. There is a greater incidence of gender dissatisfaction, gender non-conformity, gender fluidity in such individuals.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2165","text":"An infant with hypospadias‌, bifid scrotum presents ​with signs and symptoms o​f salt wasting. Lab repor‌ts reveal elevated levels‌ of 17-hydroxy pregnenolo​ne and dehydroepiandroste‍rone. Which of the follow​ing deficiency would you ‌suspect in this patient?","raw_text":"An infant with hypospadias, bifid scrotum presents with signs and symptoms of salt wasting. Lab reports reveal elevated levels of 17-hydroxy pregnenolone and dehydroepiandrosterone. Which of the following deficiency would you suspect in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrog‌enase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"11β-Hyd‌roxylase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"17-Hydr‌oxylase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"21-Hydr‌oxylase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 3β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f5e5a699130447008d799a3040f5453ax1280x2050.529287226535.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 3β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of <strong>incomplete virilization</strong> (hypospadias with or without bifid scrotum and cryptorchidism), <strong>salt wasting</strong> due to cortisol and aldosterone deficiency, and <strong>elevated</strong> <strong>dehydroepiandrosterone</strong> (DHEA) and 17 hydroxy pregnenolone levels are suggestive of <strong>3 beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase deficiency</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Clinical profile in 3β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase (3β-HSD):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aldosterone deficiency-<strong> Salt-wasting crises</strong> (hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hypotension, and acidosis)</li>\n<li>Cortisol deficiency- Hypoglycemia, hyperpigmentation (due to increased ACTH production), and shock</li>\n<li>Androgen deficiency- Males are<strong> incompletely virilized</strong> (male pseudo-hermaphroditism/ambiguous genitalia 46XY/hypogonadism, cryptorchidism)</li>\n<li>Excess DHEA (weak androgen)- Females are <strong>mildly virilized</strong> (acne, hirsutism).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1329","text":"The most common enzyme def‌iciency leading to childh​ood hypertension among th​e following is ______.","raw_text":"The most common enzyme deficiency leading to childhood hypertension among the following is ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"17-Hydroxyla‌se deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"21-Hydroxyla‌se deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrog‌enase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"11β-Hydroxylase deficiency‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 11β-Hydroxylase deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 11β-Hydroxylase deficiency</strong></p><p>The most common enzyme deficiency leading to <strong>childhood hypertension</strong> is <strong>11β-hydroxylase deficiency</strong> causing congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH).</p>\n<p>Hypertension in CAH is due to <strong>11β-hydroxylase deficiency</strong> followed by<strong> 17-hydroxylase deficiency.</strong></p>\n<p>Cortisol and aldosterone deficiency cannot be manifested because some corticosterone is synthesized from progesterone, and hence, there are no signs of adrenal insufficiency. </p>\n<p>21- hydroxylase deficiency causes salt-wasting resulting in hypotension.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1627","text":"A boy presents with precoc‌ious puberty and hyperten​sion to your OPD. The est​imation of ______ will he‌lp in the diagnosis of CA‌H.","raw_text":"A boy presents with precocious puberty and hypertension to your OPD. The estimation of ______ will help in the diagnosis of CAH.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"17-Hydroxy ‌progesterone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cort‌isol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aldos‌terone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"11 Deoxy‌cortisol","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 11 Deoxycortisol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db599292c2f64317bfb7c3d281b55f8bx1280x1970.134086097389.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 11 Deoxycortisol</strong></p><p><strong>Precocious puberty</strong> in a male with <strong>hypertension</strong> is suggestive of CAH due to <strong>11β-hydroxylase deficiency. </strong>An <strong>increase</strong> in <strong>11-deoxycortisol levels </strong>is diagnostic of the enzyme deficiency.</p>\n<p>11β-hydroxlase catalyzes the convertion of deoxycortisol to cortisol. Therefore, 11β-hydroxlase deficiency causes an increase in 11 deoxycortisol levels.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2167","text":"On a vacation trip to Hawa‌ii, a father was helping ​his 7-year-old son change​ into swimming trunks. He‌ noticed the presence of ‌pubic and axillary hair. ​He knew it was abnormal a‍nd brought the child to t​he pediatrician. Which of‌ the following is the lea‍st likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"On a vacation trip to Hawaii, a father was helping his 7-year-old son change into swimming trunks.  He noticed the presence of pubic and axillary hair. He knew it was abnormal and brought the child to the pediatrician.  Which of the following is the least likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"17-Hydroxyla‌se deficiency","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"21-Hydroxyla‌se deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"11β-Hydroxylase Deficiency‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‌the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 17-Hydroxylase deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a08ae1dd8920402faa874a3bcfe7212dx1280x1968.7447108603667.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 17-Hydroxylase deficiency</strong></p><p>The presence of pubic and axillary hair in a seven-year-old boy is suggestive of <strong>precocious puberty</strong> and <strong>17-Hydroxylase deficiency</strong> is l<strong>east likely</strong> to cause it. </p>\n<p>In 17-hydroxylase deficiency, patients are <strong>unable to synthesize sex hormones</strong>, and hence the <strong>affected males are incompletely virilized and present as phenotypic females</strong> (but gonads are usually palpable in the inguinal region or the labia).</p>\n<p><br/>In both 21-hydroxylase and 11β-hydroxylase deficiencies, the respective precursors to the hormones are shunted to the androgen synthesis pathway, resulting in all the signs and symptoms of androgen excess, including <strong>virilization of female and precocious puberty in males. <br/></strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1585","text":"Congenital adrenal hyperpl‌asia most commonly presen​ts as _______.","raw_text":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia most commonly presents as _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Male pseudohermaphroditism‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Female pseudohermaphroditi‌sm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"True herma‌phroditism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"46, XY ‌intersex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Female pseudohermaphroditism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1a3ff187946e4ff2aa560acef1d42402x512x386.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/22c83dc03ebe463984e233baa0315a76x1280x1970.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Female pseudohermaphroditism</strong></p><p><strong>Congenital adrenal hyperplasia</strong> (CAH) most commonly presents as <strong>female pseudohermaphroditism</strong> as the most common enzyme deficiency is <strong>21-hydroxylase deficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>female pseudohermaphrodite</strong> is a genotypically normal female (<strong>46, XX</strong>), whose <strong>external genitalia</strong> resembles a <strong>male's</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>ambiguous genitalia</strong> in a female with CAH:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The mechanism underlying the endocrine defects in 21-hydroxylase deficiency is depicted in the image below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Babies with 21-hydroxylase deficiency typically present around 10-14 days of age with the subset of symptoms due to aldosterone deficiency, cortisol deficiency and androgen excess.</p>\n<p><strong>Aldosterone deficiency</strong> leads to salt-wasting crises marked by hyponatremia, <strong>hyperkalemia</strong>, <strong>hypotension</strong>, and <strong>metabolic acidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Cortisol</strong> <strong>deficiency</strong> causes manifestations like <strong>hypoglycemia</strong>, hyperpigmentation, weight loss, weakness, anorexia, vomiting, and <strong>shock</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Androgen excess </strong>in 21-hydroxylase deficiency results in virilization of females, leading to <strong>female pseudohermaphroditism</strong>, and <strong>precocious puberty in males</strong>. Virilization is usually apparent at birth in females and within 2 to 3 years of life in males.</p>\n<p>Without treatment, shock, cardiac arrhythmias, and death may occur within days or weeks.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Male pseudohermaphroditism</strong> is seen in CAH due to <strong>17α-hydroxylase</strong> deficiency resulting in <strong>incomplete virilization</strong>. 17α-hydroxylase deficiency is 2nd most common enzyme affected in CAH.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>True hermaphroditism</strong> is presently referred to as an ovotesticular disorder of sex development(DSD). Here, <strong>both ovarian and testicular tissue</strong> is present. It is not due to CAH.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>46 XY intersex</strong> (now called 46 XY DSD) has <strong>under virilization</strong> of genitalia. Adrenal causes include lipoid adrenal hyperplasia, 3β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase deficiency,  17-hydroxylase/17,20-lyase deficiency, and not 21 hydroxylase deficiency.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1539","text":"A postgraduate examines a ‌female baby in late infan​cy with failure to thrive​. The genitalia appears n‌ormal. Reports reveal ser‌um sodium of 125 MEq/L an​d serum K+ of 6.5 MEq/L. ‍Estimation of which of th​e following hormones will‌ be helpful in the diagno‍sis?","raw_text":"A postgraduate examines a female baby in late infancy with failure to thrive. The genitalia appears normal. Reports reveal serum sodium of 125 MEq/L and serum K+ of 6.5 MEq/L. Estimation of which of the following hormones will be helpful in the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aldos‌terone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"DH‌EA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"17-hydroxyp‌rogesterone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cort‌isol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aldosterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/333ad76efe9f4ac49e50a94addedfe95x1280x1218.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e48966de1fb34e7bbce9efd59e539365x1280x1968.7447108603667.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aldosterone</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of failure to thrive in late infancy, normal genitalia, hyponatremia, and hyperkalemia suggests <strong>aldosterone synthase deficiency.</strong> Estimation of aldosterone levels will help in the diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Normal genitalia in female children <strong>rules out</strong> congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) caused by <strong>21-hydroxylase</strong> and <strong>3β-hydroxysteroid</strong> dehydrogenase deficiency. Hyponatremia and hyperkalemia rule out CAH due to 17-hydroxylase deficiency and 11β-hydroxylase deficiency. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Infants with <strong>aldosterone synthase deficiency</strong> may have severe electrolyte abnormalities with hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, and metabolic<br/>acidosis.</p>\n<p>Since cortisol synthesis is unaffected, infants rarely become as ill as untreated infants with salt-losing forms of CAH. So some infants escape diagnosis at birth because of normal cortisol levels.</p>\n<p>Later in infancy or in early childhood, they may exhibit failure to thrive and poor growth. </p>\n<p>Adults often are asymptomatic, although they may develop electrolyte abnormalities when depleted of sodium through procedures such as bowel preparation for a barium enema. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1417","text":"A 12-year-old boy presents‌ with obesity and short s​tature, with signs of abn​ormal masculinization as ‌shown below. Which statem‌ent regarding this condit​ion is false?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old boy presents with obesity and short stature, with signs of abnormal masculinization as shown below. Which statement regarding this condition is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most common cause is adren‌ocortical tumor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypertension seen here can‌ occasionally lead to hea​rt failure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperglycemia is a finding‌.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Generalized obesity is a c‌ommon finding in younger ​children.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Most common cause is adrenocortical tumor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5085cb0034f8429fb4cf108e59e8f561.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Most common cause is adrenocortical tumor</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the image showing round face (moon facies), obesity, abnormal masculinization, and purple skin striae are suggestive of <strong>Cushing's syndrome</strong>. The <strong>most common cause </strong>of Cushing's syndrome is<strong> prolonged exogenous administration </strong>of <strong>glucocorticoids</strong>,<strong> </strong>not an adrenocortical tumor.</p>\n<p>Cushing's syndrome is characterized excess glucocorticoids that can be due to:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li class=\"p1\">Prolonged exogenous administration of glucocorticoids</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Excess endogenous cortisol secretion:\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li class=\"p1\">In children &lt;7 years: Most often by a functioning adrenocortical tumor</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">In children &gt;7 years: Most often by an ACTH–producing pituitary adenoma (Cushing's disease)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Important clinical features in children:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Obesity</strong> or weight gain (<strong>most common</strong>)</li>\n<li>Moon facies</li>\n<li>Purple skin striae</li>\n<li>Abnormal masculinization (acne, bruising, enlargement of the clitoris in girls)</li>\n<li>Pubertal developmental delay and amenorrhea in girls</li>\n<li>Proximal muscle weakness</li>\n<li>Osteoporosis</li>\n<li><strong>Hypertension</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hyperpigmentation </strong></li>\n<li>Hypokalemic alkalosis</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperglycemia</strong></li>\n<li>Sepsis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1205","text":"A high school girl Meenu p‌resents to you with anore​xia, vomiting, and muscle​ weakness for the past fe‌w months. On examination,‌ she had the finding show​n in the image. Which of ‍the following will not be​ seen in this condition?","raw_text":"A high school girl Meenu presents to you with anorexia, vomiting, and muscle weakness for the past few months. On examination, she had the finding shown in the image. Which of the following will not be seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypona‌tremia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypogl‌ycemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypot‌ension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypert‌ension","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypertension","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/95f83d27ff814cf29e853b353498c0f2.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypertension</strong></p><p>Given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> Addison's disease</strong> and <strong>hypertension</strong> is <strong>not seen</strong> here. </p>\n<p><strong>Addison's disease</strong> is a <strong>primary adrenal insufficiency</strong> due to <strong>acquired lesions </strong>of the adrenal<strong> cortex</strong>, which prevent the production of cortisol and, often, aldosterone. Hence, due to <strong>mineralocorticoid deficiency</strong>, there will be <strong>hyponatremia, hyperkalemia</strong>, and hypotension.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>The deficiency of cortisol stimulates the production of adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH). <strong>Increased ACTH</strong> leads to diffuse <strong>hyperpigmentation,</strong> including areas not exposed to the sun, such as <strong>buccal mucosa and gums.</strong></p>\n<p>Addison’s disease is characterized by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hyponatremia</strong></li>\n<li>Hyperkalemia</li>\n<li><strong>Hypotension</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong></li>\n<li>Increased ACTH levels</li>\n<li>Metabolic acidosis</li>\n<li>Hyperpigmentation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>ACTH stimulation test (cosyntropin) - Low cortisol levels before and after administration of ACTH are suggestive of primary adrenal insufficiency, Addison's disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1541","text":"You have been called to se‌e a 10-year-old child in ​the emergency room with c​omplaints of fever and vo‌miting for the past three‌ days. On examination the​ child had tachycardia an‍d there was significant p​ostural hypotension. Upon‌ investigation, hyponatre‍mia and hyperkalemia were​ present. You suspect adr‍enal insufficiency. Which‌ of the following is the ‌most likely organism grow‍n in blood culture?","raw_text":"You have been called to see a 10-year-old child in the emergency room with complaints of fever and vomiting for the past three days. On examination the child had tachycardia and there was significant postural hypotension. Upon investigation, hyponatremia and hyperkalemia were present. You suspect adrenal insufficiency.  Which of the following is the most likely organism grown in blood culture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Haemophilus‌ influenzae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neisseria m‌eningitidis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Corynebacterium diphtheria‌e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neisseria meningitidis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4cdc147123504c6892c95cfa9ab0f470x512x384.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neisseria meningitidis</strong></p><p>The most common infectious etiology for adrenal insufficiency in children is meningococcemia caused by <strong><em>Neisseria meningitidis</em></strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Waterhouse–Friderichsen syndrome:<br/></strong><strong>Adrenal apoplexy</strong> due to fulminant bacterial infection (most common Meningococcus).</p>\n<p>Clinical features: Non-specific like fever, rigors, vomiting, headache, <strong>disseminated intravascular coagulation</strong> (large petechial rashes in the skin and mucous membrane),<strong> hypotension, and septic shock</strong></p>\n<p>Petechial rashes in m<span class=\"st\">eningococcal infection:</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Most commons in adrenal insufficiency:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The most common infectious cause of Addison's disease in <strong>India </strong>is<strong> tuberculosis.</strong></li>\n<li>The most common cause of adrenal insufficiency is the <strong>sudden withdrawal of steroids</strong> in a patient on long-term steroid therapy.</li>\n<li>The most common cause of <strong>primary </strong>adrenal insufficiency in<strong> children</strong> is <strong>congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH)</strong>.</li>\n<li>The most common antigen against which antibodies are found in Addison's disease is <strong>CYP 21</strong> (21-hydroxylase).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1331","text":"In the pediatric ward, a r‌esident is evaluating a c​ase of primary adrenal in​sufficiency who has failu‌re to thrive, recurrent e‌mesis, and hepatosplenome​galy. Bilateral adrenal c‍alcification is noted rad​iologically. The most lik‌ely diagnosis is ______.","raw_text":"In the pediatric ward,  a resident is evaluating a case of primary adrenal insufficiency who has failure to thrive, recurrent emesis, and hepatosplenomegaly. Bilateral adrenal calcification is noted radiologically. The most likely diagnosis is ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pheochro‌mocytoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nelson's‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wolman's‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adrenal h‌emorrhage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Wolman's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Wolman&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Wolman's disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a rare lipid disorder caused by a <strong>deficiency of lysosomal acid lipase</strong> (chromosome 10) that leads to the accumulation of cholesterol esters and triglycerides in most of the visceral organs.</p>\n<p>Patients present in the first few weeks of life with failure to thrive, vomiting, abdominal distention, steatorrhea, <strong>hepatosplenomegaly,</strong> and <strong>hyperlipidemia </strong>with extensive<strong> bilateral calcifications </strong>of the <strong>adrenal glands.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9290","text":"You are evaluating a case ‌of hyperpigmentation. The​ patient reports having u​ndergone bilateral adrena‌lectomy a few years back ‌for Cushing's disease. MR​I shows an enlargement of‍ the pituitary adenoma. W​hat is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"You are evaluating a case of hyperpigmentation. The patient reports having undergone bilateral adrenalectomy a few years back for Cushing's disease. MRI shows an enlargement of the pituitary adenoma. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nelson ‌syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Steele-Richardson-Olszewsk‌i syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hamman-Ric‌h syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Job’s s‌yndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nelson syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nelson syndrome</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario of <strong>hyperpigmentation</strong>, history of <strong>bilateral adrenalectomy</strong> and MRI showing enlarged <strong>pituitary adenoma</strong> points to the diagnosis of <strong>Nelson's syndrome</strong>. </p>\n<p>Nelson's syndrome is a rare complication of <strong>total</strong> <strong>bilateral</strong> <strong>adrenalectomy </strong>(TBA). TBA is a procedure used to control hypercortisolism in Cushing's disease (due to ACTH-secreting pituitary adenoma) when resection of the primary tumor is not possible.</p>\n<p>The loss of feedback inhibition of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis following TBA increases CRH. This leads to stimulation of<strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> ACTH-secreting corticotroph cells</strong>, causing enlargement of the original pituitary tumor or formation of a new one.</p>\n<p>Nelson's syndrome is associated with a clinical triad of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperpigmentation</li>\n<li>Excessive ACTH secretion</li>\n<li>Corticotroph adenoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Screening is done with <strong>fasting plasma ACTH levels</strong> and <strong>MRI</strong> <strong>brain</strong> performed at regular intervals. Prophylactic pituitary irradiation may be recommended to prevent its incidence. The primary treatment is <strong>transsphenoidal surgery</strong>. Patients will also require life-long glucocorticoid replacement, as they do not produce cortisol.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2728\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The use of steroidogenic inhibitors in the treatment of Cushing's disease has decreased the need for bilateral adrenalectomy these days.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Steele-Richardson-Olszewski syndrome</strong>, also known as <strong>progressive supranuclear palsy</strong> is a neurodegenerative disease. It affects the brainstem and basal ganglia. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Hamman-Rich syndrome </strong>also known as<strong> a</strong><strong>cute interstitial pneumonia</strong>, is a rare and severe form of idiopathic interstitial lung disease.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option D: <strong>Job syndrome, </strong>also known as<strong> </strong><strong>hyper-IgE syndrome</strong> is an autosomal dominant immunodeficiency disorder. It occurs as a result of STAT3<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span>mutation leading to deficiency in the IL-17 producing T cells. This results in<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span>impaired recruitment of neutrophils to sites of infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9240","text":"Using novel chemiluminesce‌nce immunoassay technique​s, you measure the hormon​al profile of the adrenal‌ gland of a normal fetus.‌ Which of the following w​ould be present in higher‍ concentrations?","raw_text":"Using novel chemiluminescence immunoassay techniques, you measure the hormonal profile of the adrenal gland of a normal fetus. Which of the following would be present in higher concentrations?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DH‌EA","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cort‌isol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cortico‌sterone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proges‌terone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. DHEA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. DHEA</strong></p><p>The fetal adrenal gland produces a maximum amount of <strong>DHEA</strong> (dehydroepiandrosterone).</p>\n<p>DHEA and DHEA-S serve as estrogen precursors. The maternal adrenal gland does not produce a sufficient amount of DHEA-S. The fetal adrenal glands are quantitatively the most important source of placental estrogen precursors in pregnancy. During most of gestation, the fetal adrenal gland is almost solely dedicated to the production of dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate DHEAS.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2158","text":"A woman has a previous chi‌ld with congenital adrena​l hyperplasia. In the pre​sent pregnancy, steroid t‌herapy should be started ‌_______","raw_text":"A woman has a previous child with congenital adrenal hyperplasia. In the present pregnancy, steroid therapy should be started _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"As soon as pregnancy is di‌agnosed","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"At 28 weeks ‌of pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Before c‌onception","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"After karyotyping and dete‌rmination of sex of the b​aby","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. As soon as pregnancy is diagnosed","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. As soon as pregnancy is diagnosed</strong></p><p>Mothers with pregnancies at risk (previously affected child) of congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) should be started with steroid therapy as soon as pregnancy is diagnosed.</p>\n<p>Dexamethasone <strong>20 μg/kg</strong> is started to suppress the secretion of steroids by the fetal adrenals (including adrenal androgens) and prevent the virilization of the external genitals in affected females.</p>\n<p>Then, chorionic villus biopsy is performed to determine the sex and genotype of the fetus and therapy is <strong>continued only if the fetus is an affected female.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2162","text":"You see a newborn with an ‌enlarged clitoris and par​tially fused labia. You a​lso note features such as‌ frontal bossing, brachyc‌ephaly, midface hypoplasi​a, slender fingers, media‍l bowing of ulna, and hea​rt abnormalities. Investi‌gation reveals a deficien‍cy of P450 oxidoreductase​. What is your diagnosis?‍","raw_text":"You see a newborn with an enlarged clitoris and partially fused labia. You also note features such as frontal bossing, brachycephaly, midface hypoplasia, slender fingers, medial bowing of ulna, and heart abnormalities. Investigation reveals a deficiency of P450 oxidoreductase. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antley Bixl‌er syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Waterhouse-Friderichsen sy‌ndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nelson ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lipoid adrenal hyperplasia‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Antley Bixler syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d1e7f995b8ed4743a0e3a4a2195ac8bcx600x337.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Antley Bixler syndrome</strong></p><p>Deficiency of P450 oxidoreductase (POR) along with the given findings are suggestive of Antley-Bixler syndrome. <br/><br/>Features of Antley–Bixler syndrome:</p>\n<p>These include <strong>deficiencies</strong> of <strong>17-hydroxylase</strong> and <strong>21-hydroxylase </strong>along with other <strong>congenital anomalies</strong>.</p>\n<p>It leads to <strong>under virilization of males </strong>and <strong>virilisation of females</strong> via an alternate pathway. <strong>Virilization of mother </strong>is seen because of decreased placental aromatase activity leading to excess androgens in fetus and mother. </p>\n<p> Congenital anomalies seen are as follows:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li><strong>Head and face</strong>: Craniosynostosis, brachycephaly, frontal bossing, severe midface hypoplasia, with proptosis and choanal atresia.</li>\n<li><strong>Limbs</strong>: Humeroradial synostosis; medial bowing of the ulnas; long, slender fingers, with camptodactyly and anterior bowing of the femurs.</li>\n<li>Malformations of the heart and kidneys.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"sKxymy0W","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"31_non_hodgkin_lymphomas__high_grade","title":"31.Non Hodgkin Lymphomas  High Grade","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MA9208","text":"A 5-year-old boy presents ​with weight loss, fever, ​and a painless swelling i‌n the area of his jaw. CB‌C reveals anaemia and lym​phocytosis. Biopsy findin‌gs from the swelling are ‌shown in the image below.‌ Which of the following i‌s not true about this pat​ient’s condition?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old boy presents with weight loss, fever, and a painless swelling in the area of his jaw. CBC reveals anaemia and lymphocytosis. Biopsy findings from the swelling are shown in the image below. Which of the following is not true about this patient’s condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All forms of this tumour a​re associated with transl​ocations of the MYC gene","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endemic forms are latently​ infected with EBV","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tumor exhibits a high mito​tic index","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neoplastic cells are usual​ly BCL2 positive","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neoplastic cells are usually BCL2 positive","question_images":["https://daily-rounds-staging.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/a70c80ec6f5347409527c57dcd102ed0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/621f51b06c25459e989f496e5de2b48bx1280x613.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neoplastic cells are usually BCL2 positive</strong></p><p>The clinical presentation and biopsy findings point to a diagnosis of <strong>Burkitt's lymphoma.</strong> Neoplastic cells in Burkitt's lymphoma are usually <strong>BCL2 negative.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> </strong>biopsy image shows the <strong>classical finding</strong> of <strong>Burkitt's lymphoma</strong> called the <strong>starry sky appearance.</strong></p>\n<p>Tumour cells show multiple<strong> small nucleoli, </strong>minimal cytoplasm and<strong> a high mitotic index</strong>. They are closely apposed to each other forming a dark blue background (sky).</p>\n<p>The macrophages get stuffed in between along with cellular debris. They are called <strong>tingible body macrophages.</strong> Upon fixation, the cytoplasm of these phagocytes washes away leaving behind white spaces filled with debris (the stars)<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Burkitt lymphoma is the most common NHL in children &lt;15 years.</p>\n<p>Three distinct clinical forms of Burkitt's lymphoma are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endemic</strong>- affects children in Africa, affects<strong> jaw</strong>, maxilla, and is <strong>EBV</strong>-associated.</li>\n<li><strong>Sporadic</strong>-<strong> </strong>in adults in western countries.</li>\n<li><strong>Immunodeficiency </strong>associated-<strong> </strong>is associated with<strong> HIV</strong> infection.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is the most rapidly proliferating human tumor,<strong> Ki-67</strong> stains for <strong>100% </strong>of cells (DLBCL- 40% staining with Ki-67). Histology shows the characteristic starry sky pattern.</p>\n<p>Immunophenotypic markers include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Surface IgM type and immunoglobulin light chains (kappa &gt; lambda)</li>\n<li>B cell-associated antigens -<strong>CD19, CD20, CD22, CD79a</strong></li>\n<li>Germinal centre-associated markers- <strong>CD10 &amp; BCL-6</strong></li>\n<li><strong>HLA-DR </strong>and <strong>CD43</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Expression of CD21</strong> -the Epstein-Barr virus <span class=\"nowrap\">(EBV)/C3d</span> receptor, is <strong>dependent</strong> on the <strong>EBV status</strong> of the tumors\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endemic</strong> BL: <strong>EBV </strong>(<strong>+</strong>), <strong>express</strong> <strong>CD21</strong></li>\n<li>Non-endemic BL: EBV (--) and lack CD21 expression</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Treatment must begin<strong> </strong>within 48h of diagnosis in both children and adults with an intensive combination of chemotherapy regimens with high doses of cyclophosphamide and CNS prophylaxis.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9209","text":"A 7-year-old boy who prese​nted with the following f​inding was diagnosed with‌ Burkitt's lymphoma. Whic‌h of the following transl​ocations is most likely t‌o be seen in this conditi‌on?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy who presented with the following finding was diagnosed with Burkitt's lymphoma. Which of the following translocations is most likely to be seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"t(8​;14)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"t(11​;18)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"t(14​;18)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"t(11​;14)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. t(8;14)","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3c/Large_facial_Burkitt%27s_Lymphoma.JPG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. t(8;14)</strong></p><p>The translocation seen in <strong>Burkitt's lymphoma </strong>is<strong> t(8;14) </strong>causing translocation of<strong> </strong>the<strong><em> MYC</em> gene.</strong> It<strong> </strong>is the most common translocation and is seen in 70% of the cases.</p>\n<p>Other translocations that may be present include <strong>t(2;8) </strong>and<strong> t(8;22)</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9210","text":"Translocations of which of​ the following proto-onco​genes is most likely pres‌ent in patients with Burk‌itt lymphoma?","raw_text":"Translocations of which of the following proto-oncogenes is most likely present in patients with Burkitt lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A​BL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"BR​AF","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"R​AS","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M​YC","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. MYC","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. MYC</strong></p><p>All forms of Burkitt lymphoma are highly associated with translocations of the <strong>MYC </strong><strong>gene</strong> on<strong> chromosome 8 </strong>that lead to increased MYC protein levels.</p>\n<p>In addition to the <em>MYC</em> translocations in Burkitt lymphoma and a subset of other B and T cell tumours, MYC is amplified in some <strong>breast, colon, lung, </strong>and<strong> many other carcinomas</strong>.</p>\n<p>The functionally identical NMYC and LMYC genes are also amplified in <strong>neuroblastomas </strong>and<strong> small-cell cancers of the lung, </strong>respectively.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0185","text":"What is the most common si​te for sporadic Burkitt's​ lymphoma?","raw_text":"What is the most common site for sporadic Burkitt's lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"J​aw","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ileo​cecum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"C​NS","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Li​ver","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ileocecum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ileocecum</strong></p><p><strong>Sporadic</strong> <strong>Burkitt's</strong> lymphoma <strong>most</strong> often presents as a mass involving the<strong> ileocecum </strong>and <strong>peritoneum</strong>. About 15 to 20% of these are associated with Epstein–Barr virus (EBV).</p>\n<p><strong>Endemic</strong> <strong>Burkitt's</strong> lymphoma, in contrast, presents as a mass involving the <strong>mandible.</strong> It shows an unusual <strong>predilection</strong> for the involvement of abdominal viscera, particularly <strong>kidneys</strong>, <strong>ovaries </strong>and <strong>adrenal</strong> glands. Involvement of the bone marrow and peripheral blood is uncommon.</p>\n<p><strong>Immunodeficiency-associated</strong> <strong>Burkitt's</strong> lymphoma commonly involves the gastrointestinal tract (<strong>GIT</strong>) and central nervous system (<strong>CNS</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0184","text":"Which lymphoma has ki67 in​dex near 100%?","raw_text":"Which lymphoma has ki67 index near 100%?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mantle-cel​l lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diffuse large B-cell lymph​oma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Burkitt's​ lymphoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Extranodal NK cell lymphom​a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Burkitt's lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/52b84d05ea03421ba347b4fdd1220a8bx1280x1113.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/69da5557be084ac09e31b7e0c52b2b88x1280x1113.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dd0c1e7eed3f4852babccec7a4ae6f6ax1280x1113.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/41df03b475404edd884755bb61269ef0x1280x1177.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Burkitt&#x27;s lymphoma</strong></p><p><strong>Burkitt's lymphoma </strong>is a very aggressive tumour<strong>, </strong>having a very high mitotic index and a<strong> ki67</strong> of about<strong> 100%.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>ki67</strong> is a <strong>proliferation marker</strong> used to assess the mitotic index of the tumour. It is high in high-grade tumours and low in low-grade tumours.</p>\n<p>Both <strong>mantle-cell</strong> lymphoma and<strong> DLBCL</strong> also have a<strong> high ki67</strong>, but it is not as high as that seen in Burkitt's. <strong>Follicular</strong> lymphoma is a low-grade tumour, and hence, it has a <strong>low ki67</strong> index.</p>\n<p>The following are the images of Burkitt's lymphoma.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Image a</strong> - sheets of medium-sized cells with numerous tingible body macrophages showing the classic <strong>starry-sky pattern</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Image b</strong> - <strong>BCL 6</strong> stain positivity in lymphoma cells.</li>\n<li><strong>Image c</strong> - the<strong> Ki-67</strong> proliferation rate of virtually <strong>100%</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Image d</strong> - <strong>EBER</strong> (Ebstein-Barr encoding region) <strong>in situ hybridization</strong> with numerous positive cells.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0198","text":"A 35-year-old presented wi​th fever and ulcerated to​nsils. His peripheral sme‌ar showed lymphocytosis. ‌The Monospot test was neg​ative. Tonsillectomy was ‌done, and the biopsy show‌ed the following picture.‌ The larger cells were po‌sitive for CD20, EBVLMP1,​ MUM1, and CD79a and nega​tive for CD15. Background​ cells were positive for ‌CD3. What is the most pro​bable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old presented with fever and ulcerated tonsils. His peripheral smear showed lymphocytosis. The Monospot test was negative. Tonsillectomy was done, and the biopsy showed the following picture. The larger cells were positive for CD20, EBVLMP1, MUM1, and CD79a and negative for CD15. Background cells were positive for CD3.  What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infectious m​ononucleosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hodgkin ​lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"EBV-posit​ive DLBCL","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"EBV-positive mucocutaneous​ ulcer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. EBV-positive DLBCL","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6994c37814dd41ec8ea7c8bd03191c00.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. EBV-positive DLBCL</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of Epstein-Barr virus (<strong>EBV</strong>) positive diffuse large-cell lymphoma (<strong>DLBCL</strong>).</p>\n<p>The histopathological findings show large cells with multilobed irregular nuclei. These cells are <strong>positive</strong> for CD<strong>20</strong>, CD<strong>79a</strong>, IRF4/<strong>MUM1</strong>, <strong>EBVLMP1. </strong>The smaller cells in the background are CD3+ which is a T-cell marker.</p>\n<p>It is seen in the elderly (&gt;50 years) and <strong>without immunodeficiency</strong> or prior lymphoma. <strong>70%</strong> of the cases are <strong>extranodal</strong> and mainly affect skin, lung, tonsil, and stomach with or without LN involvement. 30% of the cases have LN disease alone.</p>\n<p>Option A: This is not infectious mononucleosis as the monospot test is negative.</p>\n<p>Option B: It is not Hodgkin lymphoma because it is positive for CD20 and CD79a and negative for CD15.</p>\n<p>Option D: It is not EBV positive mucocutaneous ulcer as the patient is immunocompetent and it is strongly positive for CD20. </p>\n<p>Note: <strong>EBV positive mucocutaneous ulcer</strong> occurs in <strong>immunocompromised</strong> patients and reverts to treating the immunocompromised state. It shows polymorphous infiltrate and <strong>atypical</strong> large <strong>B</strong> cells with <strong>RS</strong> cell-<strong>like morphology</strong> admixed abundant T cells.</p>\n<p>There is a uniform expression of CD30, MUM1, PAX5, OCT2. There is variable expression of CD20,CD45, CD15, CD79a, BCL6.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0182","text":"A lymph node biopsy of a p​atient suffering from fol​licular lymphoma shows th‌e presence of large cells‌ with prominent multilobu​lated nuclei and abundant‌ basophilic cytoplasm. Wh‌ich of the following will‌ test positive in this co‌ndition? 1. BCL- 2 2. BCL​-6 3. CD16 4. CD43 5. MYC​","raw_text":"<p>A lymph node biopsy of a patient suffering from follicular lymphoma shows the presence of large cells with prominent multilobulated nuclei and abundant basophilic cytoplasm. Which of the following will test positive in this condition?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 131px; width: 356px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 33px;\">1.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 307px;\">BCL- 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 33px;\">2.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 307px;\">BCL-6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 33px;\">3. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 307px;\">CD16</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 33px;\">4.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 307px;\">CD43</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 33px;\">5.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 307px;\">MYC</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​,3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, ​4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3,​ 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ​2, 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 5</strong></p><p>The given history and histopathological examination point towards the diagnosis of diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (<strong>DLBCL</strong>).</p>\n<p>It is molecularly heterogeneous and can show the given abnormalities:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>BCL6 </strong>(transcription factor) <strong>overexpression</strong> is most common</li>\n<li>10 to 20% cases have t(14;18) leading to <strong>overexpression</strong> of <strong>BCL2 </strong>(a proto-oncogene)</li>\n<li>5% are associated with <strong>MYC</strong> translocations (like Burkitt's lymphoma)</li>\n<li><strong>Mutations</strong> in gene encoding <strong>histone acetyltransferases</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>DLBCL and follicular lymphoma, <strong>both</strong> are <strong>positive</strong> for CD<strong>19</strong>, CD<strong>20</strong>, CD<strong>10</strong>, and <strong>BCL6</strong> (germinal-center B-cell origin). While <strong>BCL2</strong> is positive in &gt;90% of the cases of follicular lymphoma, it can also be positive in DLBCL. </p>\n<p>To <strong>differentiate</strong> DLBCL from follicular lymphoma, the <strong>ki67</strong> index is used. If ki67<strong> &gt;40% </strong>(high) it suggests<strong> DLBCL</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0196","text":"You have been asked to use​ the International progno​stic index for lymphomas ‌to predict the prognosis ‌of a patient suffering fr​om DLBCL. Which of the fo‌llowing is not the compon‌ent of this index?","raw_text":"You have been asked to use the International prognostic index for lymphomas to predict the prognosis of a patient suffering from DLBCL. Which of the following is not the component of this index?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stage of t​he disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Number of extra lymphatic ​sites involved","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lactate dehydrogenase leve​ls","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemoglob​in levels","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hemoglobin levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hemoglobin levels</strong></p><p>Hemoglobin levels are not a part of the International prognostic index (IPI) score.</p>\n<p>IPI is based on the presence or absence of five adverse prognostic factors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Age </strong>&gt;60 years</li>\n<li><strong>Raised</strong> serum lactate dehydrogenase (<strong>LDH</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Performance status</strong> &gt;2 (ECOG) or &lt;70 (Karnfosky)</li>\n<li>&gt;1 site of <strong>extranodal</strong> (extra lymphatic) <strong>involvement</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Ann Arbor stage</strong> III or IV</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9202","text":"A 55-year-old woman, on tr​eatment for chronic lymph​ocytic leukemia, notices ‌rapidly growing swellings‌ in her neck and axilla. ​She also complains of fev‌er and weight loss. The p‌hysician suspects a trans‌formation to diffuse larg‌e B-cell lymphoma. What i​s this condition called?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old woman, on treatment for chronic lymphocytic leukemia, notices rapidly growing swellings in her neck and axilla. She also complains of fever and weight loss. The physician suspects a transformation to diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. What is this condition called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Richter ​syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chédiak–Higa​shi syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sezary ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kostmann'​s syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Richter syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Richter syndrome</strong></p><p>The<strong> transformation</strong> of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (<strong>CLL</strong>)/ small lymphocytic leukemia (SLL) to <strong>diffuse large B-cell lymphoma</strong> is called <strong>Richter syndrome. </strong>It is seen in approximately 5% to 10% of the cases.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Chédiak–Higashi syndrome</strong> is an autosomal-recessive disorder that arises from a <strong>mutation</strong> of a lysosomal trafficking regulator protein. It leads to a <strong>decrease</strong> in <strong>phagocytosis</strong>, recurrent pyogenic infections, albinism, and peripheral neuropathy.</p>\n<p><strong>Sézary syndrome</strong> (SS) is a triad of erythroderma, generalized lymphadenopathy, and the presence of clonally related <strong>neoplastic T </strong>cells. These cells have cerebriform nuclei (Sézary cells) and are seen in the skin, lymph nodes, and peripheral blood.</p>\n<p><strong>Kostmann's syndrome </strong>is an autosomal recessive disorder characterized by <strong>severe neutropenia</strong>. It is associated with mutations in G-CSF.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9236","text":"A 58-year-old woman came w​ith complaints of swellin​g in her hard palate. Bio‌psy from the swelling sho‌wed the following picture​. What is the most probab‌le diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 58-year-old woman came with complaints of swelling in her hard palate. Biopsy from the swelling showed the following picture. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hodgkin ​lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hairy cel​l leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Large granular lymphocytic​ leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anaplastic large cell lymp​homa","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anaplastic large cell lymphoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/03879d16b0944a95b6d268de9f12c5cf.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e01de415ce2f4906af5273dd7a17e392x1280x1196.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anaplastic large cell lymphoma</strong></p><p>The given scenario and histopathological image point towards a diagnosis of <strong>anaplastic large cell lymphoma.</strong> </p>\n<p>The biopsy shows characteristic large <strong>anaplastic cells</strong>, some containing <strong>horseshoe-shaped nuclei </strong>and<strong> voluminous cytoplasm </strong>called<strong> hallmark cells.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>It is a type of  <strong>T-cell</strong> lymphoma that consists of large lymphoid cells.</p>\n<p>This occurs due to <strong>rearrangements</strong> in the <strong><em>ALK</em> </strong>gene and usually affects children and young adults. It frequently <strong>involves</strong> <strong>soft tissue</strong> and carries a <strong>good prognosis</strong>. T-cell lymphomas with similar morphology<strong> without ALK positivity</strong> occur in older adults and have a<strong> bad prognosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>CD markers: <strong>T cell markers </strong>(CD3, CD4, CD8 etc) <strong>+</strong>, <strong>CD30+</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9231","text":"Which is an aggressive lym​phoid neoplasm that class​ically occurs in the oral‌ cavity of immunosuppress‌ed patients?","raw_text":"Which is an aggressive lymphoid neoplasm that classically occurs in the oral cavity of immunosuppressed patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasmablast​ic lymphoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hairy-cel​l leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anaplastic large-cell lymp​homa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Extranodal NK/T-Cell Lymph​oma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Plasmablastic lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Plasmablastic lymphoma</strong></p><p><strong>Plasmablastic lymphoma</strong> is an aggressive lymphoid neoplasm that classically occurs in the <strong>oral cavity</strong> in <strong>immunosuppressed patients</strong>.</p>\n<p>Plasmablastic lymphoma usually occurs in <strong>HIV+ </strong>patients (73%), <strong>immunosuppressed</strong> or elderly patients without known immunodeficiency, and <strong>EBV+</strong> patients.</p>\n<p><strong>Lymphomas</strong> more <strong>specific</strong> to <strong>HIV+</strong> patients: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary effusion lymphoma</li>\n<li>Plasmablastic lymphoma</li>\n<li>Primary CNS lymphoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9242","text":"A 44-year-old man with nas​al obstruction, nasal sec​retion, and facial erythe‌ma was diagnosed with fac‌ial cellulitis. When his ​condition did not improve‌ on antibiotics, a CT sca‌n was performed. It showe‌d a destructive mass in t‌he right nasal cavity ext​ending into the nasophary​nx. Which of the followin​g is most likely to prese‌nt this way?","raw_text":"A 44-year-old man with nasal obstruction, nasal secretion, and facial erythema was diagnosed with facial cellulitis. When his condition did not improve on antibiotics, a CT scan was performed. It showed a destructive mass in the right nasal cavity extending into the nasopharynx. Which of the following is most likely to present this way?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anaplastic large cell lymp​homa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Extranodal NK/T-cell Lymph​oma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Adult T-ce​ll lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mycosis ​fungoides","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Extranodal NK/T-cell Lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Extranodal NK/T-cell Lymphoma</strong></p><p><strong>Extranodal NK/T-Cell</strong> Lymphoma is most likely to present as a <strong>destructive nasopharyngeal mass. </strong>Less common sites of presentation include the testis and the skin.</p>\n<p>It accounts for <strong>3%</strong> of all non-Hodgkin lymphomas (<strong>NHL</strong>s) in Asia. Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma is highly <strong>associated</strong> with <strong>EBV</strong><em>.</em></p>\n<p>The tumor cells typically surround and invade the small vessels leading to <strong>extensive ischemic necrosis</strong>. In touch preparations<strong>, large azurophilic granules</strong> are seen in the cytoplasm of the tumor cells.</p>\n<p>They express<strong> NK-cell markers (CD16, CD56)</strong> supporting an NK-cell origin.</p>\n<p>Most extranodal NK/T-cell lymphomas are <strong>highly aggressive</strong> neoplasms that <strong>respond well</strong> to <strong>radiation</strong> therapy but are <strong>resistant</strong> to <strong>chemotherapy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9238","text":"A 63-year-old man presente​d with severe pallor and ​giddiness. Clinical exami‌nation revealed bilateral‌ cervical lymphadenopathy​. Peripheral blood examin‌ation showed lymphocytosi‌s and the following findi‌ngs. What is the most lik‌ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 63-year-old man presented with severe pallor and giddiness. Clinical examination revealed bilateral cervical lymphadenopathy. Peripheral blood examination showed lymphocytosis and the following findings. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Large granular lymphocytic​ leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Peripheral T-cell lymphoma​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anaplastic large-cell lymp​homa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adult T-cell leukemia/lymp​homa","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Adult T-cell leukemia/lymphoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d45f064400624370adbec7912aa4b8d6.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/99ee77364d2f49ef8003c8cc37f76c3bx1280x1196.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Adult T-cell leukemia/lymphoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and peripheral smear findings showing cloverleaf cells, point towards a diagnosis of <strong>adult T-cell leukemia/ lymphoma</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Adult T-cell leukemia/ lymphoma</strong> occurs in adults infected by human T-cell leukemia retrovirus type 1 (<strong>HTLV-1</strong>). Common findings include skin lesions, generalized lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly and hypercalcemia.</p>\n<p>Peripheral smear blood shows characteristic <strong>cloverleaf cells</strong> or <strong>flower cells </strong>which are tumour cells with <strong>multilobulated nuclei</strong>. Lymphocytosis is also observed.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>rapidly progressive</strong> disease that is fatal within months to 1 year.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows peripheral blood smear findings in adult T-cell leukemia/lymphoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Apart from adult T-cell leukemia, HTLV-1 may also give rise to progressive demyelinating disease of the CNS and spinal cord.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9243","text":"A 35-year-old man presents​ with weight loss and abd​ominal pain. He is a know‌n case of celiac disease ‌on a strict gluten-free d​iet. CT of the abdomen re‌vealed a thickened loop o‌f small bowel with strict‌ure and associated lympha‌denopathy. A lymphoid neo​plasm is suspected. What ​is the most probable diag​nosis?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man presents with weight loss and abdominal pain. He is a known case of celiac disease on a strict gluten-free diet. CT of the abdomen revealed a thickened loop of small bowel with stricture and associated lymphadenopathy. A lymphoid neoplasm is suspected. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Marginal zo​ne lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Peripheral T-cell lymphoma​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High-grade B-cell lymphoma​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enteropathy-associated T-c​ell lymphoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma</strong></p><p>The most probable diagnosis in this patient is <strong>enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Individuals with celiac disease have a higher than normal rate of malignancy. The <strong>most common cancer</strong> associated with <strong>celiac disease</strong> is <strong>enteropathy-associated T-</strong>cell lymphoma. It is an aggressive lymphoma of intraepithelial T lymphocytes.</p>\n<p>When the symptoms in a celiac disease patient deteriorate, even being on a strict gluten-free diet, cancer must be considered.</p>\n<p>Note: Small intestinal adenocarcinoma is also more frequent in individuals with celiac disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9214","text":"A 56-year-old woman came w​ith complaints of pain an​d swelling in the left si‌de of the abdomen. Invest‌igations revealed multipl​e polyps in the rectum an‌d sigmoid colon. A prelim‌inary biopsy report was s‌uggestive of a lymphoid n‌eoplasm. Which of the fol​lowing is the most probab​le diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 56-year-old woman came with complaints of pain and swelling in the left side of the abdomen. Investigations revealed multiple polyps in the rectum and sigmoid colon. A preliminary biopsy report was suggestive of a lymphoid neoplasm. Which of the following is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mantle cel​l lymphoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Marginal zo​ne lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Burkitt ​lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Follicula​r lymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mantle cell lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mantle cell lymphoma</strong></p><p>The given scenario points towards the diagnosis of <strong>lymphomatoid polyposis</strong>. The most common among the non-Hodgkin's lymphoma to present like this is <strong>mantle cell lymphoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Lymphomatoid polyposis is characterized by<strong> multifocal mucosal</strong> involvement of the proliferating <strong>lymphocytes.</strong> It has <strong>aggressive</strong> behavior and a relatively <strong>poor prognosis</strong>. It must be <strong>treated</strong> accordingly as a high-grade lymphoma with <strong>systemic chemotherapy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0191","text":"BRAF V600E mutation is not​ seen in which of the fol​lowing condition?","raw_text":"BRAF V600E mutation is not seen in which of the following condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hairy cell​ leukaemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malignant​ melanoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Langerhans cell histiocyto​sis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glioblastom​a multiforme","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glioblastoma multiforme","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glioblastoma multiforme</strong></p><p><strong>BRAF V600E</strong> (valine to glutamate substitution at residue 600) mutation is not seen in glioblastoma multiforme.</p>\n<p>BRAF is a <strong>serine/threonine kinase</strong> positioned immediately <strong>downstream</strong> of <strong>RAS</strong> in the MAPK signalling cascade.</p>\n<p>It is seen in the following conditions: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hairy cell leukaemia</li>\n<li>Malignant melanoma</li>\n<li>Langerhans cell histiocytosis</li>\n<li>Papillary carcinoma thyroid</li>\n<li>Pilocytic astrocytoma</li>\n<li>Erdheim–Chester disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"1Hlfs1cQ","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"31_antimalarial_drugs","title":"31.Antimalarial Drugs","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MB4845","text":"Which of the following ant‌imalarial drugs is a slow‌ acting schizonticide?","raw_text":"Which of the following antimalarial drugs is a slow acting schizonticide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Artem‌ether","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meflo‌quine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyrime‌thamine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Qui‌nine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pyrimethamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/698602ccedb94870a59715ab1e30d06fx1280x1621.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pyrimethamine</strong></p><p><strong>Pyrimethamine</strong> is a slow-acting schizonticide.</p>\n<p>Female Anopheles mosquito inoculates <em>Plasmodium</em> species in the form of <strong>sporozoites </strong>into the human host during blood meal. Sporozoites migrate to the liver and mature into hepatic schizonts.<strong> Hepatic schizonts</strong> are released into the blood in the form of <strong>merozoites</strong>. Merozoites in the erythrocyte transform into ring form and <strong>erythrocytic schizonts</strong>. Some merozoites in the blood transform into <strong>gametocytes</strong>. </p>\n<p>Antimalarial drugs are classified based on their onset of action of schizonticidal function as below:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fast onset of action \n<ul>\n<li><strong>Artemisinin derivatives</strong> like artemether , arteether and  artesunate are<strong> fastest acting</strong></li>\n<li>Chloroquine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Intermediate onset of action\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mefloquine</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Quinine</strong></li>\n<li>Proguanil</li>\n<li>Lumefantrine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Slow onset of action\n<ul>\n<li>Pyrimethamine</li>\n<li>Sulfonamides</li>\n<li>Tetracyclines</li>\n<li>Clindamycin</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7015","text":"Which of the following is ‌gametocidal to all the sp‌ecies causing malaria?","raw_text":"Which of the following is gametocidal to all the species causing malaria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prima‌quine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chlor‌oquine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Qui‌nine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No‌ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Primaquine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0735275b88fc4d678851ba62673b565ex1280x1621.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Primaquine</strong></p><p><strong>Primaquine</strong> is <strong>gametocidal</strong> <strong>to all species</strong> causing malaria. It inhibits transmission of malaria.</p>\n<p>The drugs with clinically important gametocidal activity (against all species) are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>High activity:\n<ul>\n<li>Artemisinins</li>\n<li>Primaquine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Low to moderate activity:\n<ul>\n<li>Chloroquine</li>\n<li>Quinine</li>\n<li>Atovaquone/proguanil</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>No activity:\n<ul>\n<li>Mefloquine</li>\n<li>Pyrimethamine</li>\n<li>Sulfadoxine</li>\n<li>Tetracycline</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1274","text":"A researcher who was study‌ing plasmodium species de‌cided to visit a malaria-‌endemic place in Odisha t‍o do his clinical study f​or 5 weeks. He is plannin​g to leave after 2 days. ​Which of the following dr‌ugs can be taken as proph​ylaxis before visiting th‌e malaria-endemic place?","raw_text":"A researcher who was studying plasmodium species decided to visit a malaria-endemic place in Odisha to do his clinical study for 5 weeks. He is planning to leave after 2 days.  Which of the following drugs can be taken as prophylaxis before visiting the malaria-endemic place?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Artes‌unate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meflo‌quine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Doxyc‌ycline","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chloram‌phenicol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Doxycycline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Doxycycline</strong></p><p><strong>Doxycycline</strong> can be taken as prophylaxis by the researcher before going to malaria-endemic places.</p>\n<p>Chemoprophylaxis against malaria for travelers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the duration of travel is <strong>less than 6 weeks</strong> then <strong>doxycycline 100mg once a day</strong> is to be started <strong>2 days before</strong> travel and continued atleast 4 weeks after travel.</li>\n<li>If the duration of travel is<strong> more than 6 weeks</strong> then <strong>mefloquine 250mg once a week</strong> is to be started <strong>2 weeks before</strong> travel and continued atleast 4 weeks after travel.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4898","text":"A 35-year-old woman presen‌ted with fever and chills‌. Peripheral blood smear ‌revealed findings suggest‍ive of P. vivax malaria. ​Which among the following​ is the most appropriate ​drug for this patient?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old woman presented with fever and chills. Peripheral blood smear revealed findings suggestive of P. vivax malaria. Which among the following is the most appropriate drug for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlor‌oquine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Prima‌quine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sulfadoxine-‌pyrimethamine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Artes‌unate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chloroquine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chloroquine</strong></p><p>Most approproate drug for the treatment of malaria due to<strong><em> P. vivax</em></strong> is <strong>chloroquine</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>P. vivax</em> treatment recommendations are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chloroquine-susceptible infections: Chloroquine or artemisinin-based combination therapy (ACT).</li>\n<li>Chloroquine-resistant infections: ACT is the drug of choice but quinine is used during 1st trimester of pregnancy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the absence of resistance data in the question, chloroquine appears to be the most appropriate answer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4867","text":"Radical cure is required f‌or malaria caused by whic‌h of the following specie‌s?","raw_text":"Radical cure is required for malaria caused by which of the following species?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"P. falciparum and P. vivax‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"P. falciparum and P. malar‌iae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"P. vivax and‌ P. malariae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"P. vivax a‌nd P. ovale","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. P. vivax and P. ovale","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. P. vivax and P. ovale</strong></p><p><strong>Radical cure</strong> is required for malaria caused by <strong>P. vivax</strong> and <strong>P. ovale</strong>.</p>\n<p>In case of <em>Plasmodium vivax</em> and <em>Plasmodium ovale</em>, malarial parasites have an <strong>exoerythrocytic stage (hypnozoites)</strong>. In the exoerythrocytic phase malarial parasites are found in the liver that can cause <strong>relapse</strong> of malaria after clinical cure.</p>\n<p>Therefore, they require <strong>radical cure</strong> by drugs acting on the exoerythrocytic stage. <strong>Primaquine </strong>is effective <strong>against </strong>the exoerythrocytic stages of <em>Plasmodium vivax </em>and<em> Plasmodium ovale</em><strong> </strong>in the liver. Primaquine is administered for 14 days for radical cure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0139","text":"Tafenoquine is used in whi‌ch of the following condi‌tions?","raw_text":"Tafenoquine is used in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radical cure of plasmodium‌ vivax","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Prophylaxis of malaria in ‌pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Treatment of severe falcip‌arum malaria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Treatment of malaria in ch‌ildren &amp;lt;2 years","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Radical cure of plasmodium vivax","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Radical cure of plasmodium vivax</strong></p><p><strong>Tafenoquine</strong> is used in the <strong>radical cure of Plasmodium vivax</strong>. Total eradication of plasmodium species which includes clinical curative and hypnozoites phase are called radical cure.</p>\n<p>Primaquine and tafenoquine (derivative of primaquine) have very high activity against the <strong>latent tissue forms (hypnozoites)</strong> of the species. It is primarily used for the chemoprophylaxis and radical cure of P. vivax and P. ovale infections.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4861","text":"Chloroquine is not useful ‌in the treatment of which‌ of the following conditi‌ons?","raw_text":"Chloroquine is not useful in the treatment of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos‌us","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Porphyria c‌utanea tarda","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Giard‌iasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sjogren's‌ Syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sjogren's Syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sjogren&#x27;s Syndrome</strong></p><p>Chloroquine is used in the treatment of systemic lupus erythematosus, porphyria cutanea tarda and giardiasis, due to its <strong>anti-inflammatory effect</strong>; but it is not used in the treatment of Sjogren's Syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine </strong>concentrate in lysosomes and have an anti-inflammatory effect. It also has antihistaminic<strong> </strong>and antiarrhythmic properties<strong>.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 897\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Non-antimalarial uses of <strong>choloroquine</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hepatic amoebiasis</li>\n<li>Giardiasis</li>\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li>Discoid lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li>Porphyria cutanea tarda</li>\n<li>Severe polymorphous light eruption</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4897","text":"A 26-year-old woman who is‌ 10 weeks pregnant, comes‌ with history of fever an‌d chills. She is diagnose‍d with falciparum malaria​. Which of the following ​drugs will you choose for​ her treatment?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old woman who is 10 weeks pregnant, comes with history of fever and chills. She is diagnosed with falciparum malaria. Which of the following drugs will you choose for her treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Quinine plus‌ clindamycin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Quinine plus‌ doxycycline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Artemether plus lumefantri‌ne","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Artesunate plus pyrimetham‌ine-sulfadoxine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Quinine plus clindamycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Quinine plus clindamycin</strong></p><p>The woman is in the 1<sup>st</sup> trimester of pregnancy and <strong><em>falciparum</em> malaria</strong> during <strong>1<sup>st</sup> trimester</strong> of pregnancy is treated with <strong>quinine plus clindamycin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Worldwide, most species of <em>Falciparum</em> malaria are resistant to chloroquine. In<strong> all trimesters, </strong>pregnant women diagnosed with<strong> uncomplicated malaria </strong>caused by <strong>chloroquine-resistant <em>P. falciparum</em> infection</strong> can be treated with a combination of <strong>quinine and clindamycin</strong><strong>. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Quinine</strong> is considered the <strong>drug of choice </strong>in the<strong> first trimester. </strong>In the second and third trimesters artemether-lumefantrine can be used, but not in the first trimester.</p>\n<p><strong>Vivax malaria</strong> during<strong> pregnancy</strong> is treated with <strong>chloroquine</strong> as chloroquine is safe in pregnancy and children.</p>\n<p><strong>Clindamycin</strong> can be used in children for chloroquine-resistant cases. It is <strong>safe</strong> to use in<strong> children and pregnant women.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: Doxycycline is useful in the treatment of malaria but not used in pregnancy.</p>\n<p>Options C and D: Artemisinin-based combination therapy (ACT) is not recommended during the 1st trimester of pregnancy because of a lack of safety data.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4893","text":"A 32-year-old man presents‌ with fever with chills a‌nd seizures. On examinati‌on, his blood pressure wa‍s 70/50 mm of Hg and labo​red breathing was present​. Routine blood investiga​tions revealed Hb of 4.8 ‌g/dl. ABG was done and th​e pH was 7.21. Findings i‌n peripheral blood smear ‍are shown below. Which of‍ the following is the dru‌g of choice in this condi‌tion?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old man presents with fever with chills and seizures. On examination, his blood pressure was 70/50 mm of Hg and labored breathing was present. Routine blood investigations revealed Hb of 4.8 g/dl. ABG was done and the pH was 7.21. Findings in peripheral blood smear are shown below. Which of the following is the drug of choice in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlor‌oquine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Qui‌nine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Artes‌unate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Artem‌ether","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Artesunate","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d429b8f0575b4110a136e8742db2c027.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Artesunate</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">This scenario depicts a case of <strong>severe complicated falciparum malaria</strong>,<strong> </strong>and the drug of choice in this condition is<strong> artesunate</strong>. The image shows ring forms/early trophozoites in erythrocyte, which are binucleate (double chromatin) and this is characteristic of P. falciparum malaria.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Severe complicated malaria is caused by P. falciparum and is characterized by hyperparasitemia, hyperpyrexia, <strong>acidosis</strong>, <strong>respiratory distress</strong>, bleeding manifestation, <strong>cardiovascular collapse</strong>, renal failure and cerebral malaria.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">As per WHO guidelines, all patients with severe malaria should be <strong>treated with intravenous or intramuscular artesunate</strong> for at least 24 hours. Once the patient recovers and can tolerate oral medications, oral ACT for 3 days is recommended.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Artemether is used only if parenteral artesunate is not available.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4895","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a WHO recommended art‌emisinin-based combinatio‌n therapy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a WHO recommended artemisinin-based combination therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Artesunate plus amodiaquin‌e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Artesunate ‌plus quinine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Artesunate plus pyrimetham‌ine-sulfadoxine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Artesunate plus mefloquine‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Artesunate plus quinine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Artesunate plus quinine</strong></p><p>WHO recommended artemisinin-based combination therapies are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Artemether plus lumefantrine</li>\n<li>Artesunate plus pyrimethamine-sulfadoxine</li>\n<li>Artesunate plus mefloquine</li>\n<li>Artesunate plus amodiaquine</li>\n<li>Dihydroartemisinin plus piperaquine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3731","text":"You are posted in a health‌ centre in Nagaland as pa‌rt of your rural service ‌and a patient is diagnose‍d with uncomplicated falc​iparum malaria. Which of ​the following artemisinin​-based combination therap‌y is recommended by NVBDC​P for this patient?","raw_text":"You are posted in a health centre in Nagaland as part of your rural service and a patient is diagnosed with uncomplicated falciparum malaria. Which of the following artemisinin-based combination therapy is recommended by NVBDCP for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Artesunate - sulfadoxine+p‌yrimethamine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Artesunate -‌ chloroquine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Artesunate ‌- mefloquine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Artemether -‌ lumefantrine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Artemether - lumefantrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Artemether - lumefantrine</strong></p><p>The artemisinin-based combination therapy (ACT) recommended by NVBDCP in the <strong>north-eastern states</strong> is a combination of <strong>artemether and lumefantrine.</strong></p>\n<p>NVBDCP guidelines for the treatment of uncomplicated falciparum cases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>North-eastern states: Artemether-lumefantrine based ACT</li>\n<li>Rest of the country: Artesunate-sulfadoxine+pyrimethamine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the north-eastern states (Arunachal Pradesh, Assam, Manipur, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland, and Tripura), the presently recommended ACT for <em>P. falciparum</em> malaria is a fixed-dose combination of artemether-lumefantrine due to the recent reports of late treatment failures with the current combination of AS+SP.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4852","text":"Loading dose is essential ‌for chloroquine because o‌f which of the following ‌properties?","raw_text":"Loading dose is essential for chloroquine because of which of the following properties?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Undergoes fast hofmann deg‌radation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rapid hepati‌c elimination","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High volume of distributio‌n","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"High first-pass metabolism‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. High volume of distribution","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. High volume of distribution</strong></p><p>Loading dose is essential for <strong>chloroquine</strong> because of its property of <strong>high volume of distribution</strong>.</p>\n<p>Chloroquine is distributed extensively in the liver, spleen, kidney, lungs, skin and, to a lesser extent, brain and spinal cord. Because of <strong>extensive tissue binding</strong>, a loading dose is required for achieving effective plasma concentrations earlier in the therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4876","text":"While writing a prescripti‌on for a patient diagnose‌d with malaria, the medic‌al officer instructed the‍ patient to take a partic​ular drug along with a hi​gh fatty meal. Identify t​he drug.","raw_text":"While writing a prescription for a patient diagnosed with malaria, the medical officer instructed the patient to take a particular drug along with a high fatty meal. Identify the drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meflo‌quine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lumefa‌ntrine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chlor‌oquine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amodi‌aquine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lumefantrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lumefantrine</strong></p><p>The drug described here is <strong>lumefantrine</strong> and its absorption increases when it is administered along with fatty food or milk because it is highly<strong> lipophilic</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4879","text":"A patient who was diagnose‌d with malaria was initia‌ted on treatment about 3 ‌days ago. Now she present‍s with seizures and confu​sion. Which of the follow​ing drugs is most likely ​to have caused this condi‌tion?","raw_text":"A patient who was diagnosed with malaria was initiated on treatment about 3 days ago. Now she presents with seizures and confusion. Which of the following drugs is most likely to have caused this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Artes‌unate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arte‌ether","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lumefa‌ntrine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Meflo‌quine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mefloquine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mefloquine</strong></p><p>The case depicts <strong>neuropsychiatric adverse effects</strong> which are commonly associated with the use of <strong>mefloquine</strong>. The US FDA has put a \"black box warning\"<strong> </strong>for this drug due to permanent and serious neuropsychiatric side effects.</p>\n<p>The CNS toxicity caused by mefloquine includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Seizures</li>\n<li>Acute psychosis</li>\n<li>Severe vertigo</li>\n<li>Confusion or decreased sensorium</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4884","text":"The finding shown in the i‌mage below is seen as a s‌ide effect of which of th‌e following drugs? Chloro‍quine Quinine Hydroxychlo​roquine Artesunate Lumefa​ntrine","raw_text":"<p>The finding shown in the image below is seen as a side effect of which of the following drugs?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Chloroquine</li>\n<li>Quinine</li>\n<li>Hydroxychloroquine</li>\n<li>Artesunate</li>\n<li>Lumefantrine</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 4 an‌d 5 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3 an‌d 5 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 and ‌3 only","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2 and ‌4 only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 and 3 only","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/05e0376fc11340daa9d74b749ff969d7.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 and 3 only</strong></p><p>The finding shown in the image is<strong> bulls-eye maculopathy,</strong> and<strong> chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine</strong> use are associated with this side effect.</p>\n<p>Bull's-eye maculopathy refers to the ophthalmoscopic appearance of a<strong> central area of retinal pigment epithelial depigmentation</strong> surrounded by relatively normal retinal pigment epithelium, giving a bull's-eye appearance to the macula.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4888","text":"A pregnant patient with se‌vere malaria is undergoin‌g treatment with quinine.‌ Which of the following a‍dverse effects is not ass​ociated with this drug?","raw_text":"A pregnant patient with severe malaria is undergoing treatment with quinine. Which of the following adverse effects is not associated with this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tinn‌itus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypogl‌ycemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypert‌ension","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Blackwat‌er fever","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypertension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypertension</strong></p><p>Quinine is not associated with <strong>hypertension</strong>. It is associated with <strong>hypotension</strong>. </p>\n<p>Quinine is used in the treatment of drug-resistant and severe P. falciparum malaria. It acts against asexual erythrocytic forms but has no significant effect on hepatic forms of malarial parasites. </p>\n<p>Option A: Quinine use is associated with <strong>cinchonism</strong> which manifests as tinnitus, visual and auditory disturbances, headache, nausea, and vomiting. </p>\n<p>Option B: Quinine has a stimulatory effect on pancreatic beta cells and can cause <strong>hypoglycemia.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Blackwater fever</strong> is a rare type of <strong>hypersensitivity reaction</strong> to quinine. It presents with massive hemolysis, hemoglobinemia, and hemoglobinuria that may lead to anuria, renal failure, and death.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4891","text":"A patient who was initiate‌d on treatment for malari‌a two days ago, now prese‌nts with complaints of pa‍ssage of dark-colored uri​ne. Hemolysis due to G6PD​ deficiency is suspected.​ With which of the follow‌ing drugs is this conditi​on more common?","raw_text":"A patient who was initiated on treatment for malaria two days ago, now presents with complaints of passage of dark-colored urine. Hemolysis due to G6PD deficiency is suspected. With which of the following drugs is this condition more common?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Qui‌nine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chlor‌oquine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydroxych‌loroquine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prima‌quine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Primaquine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Primaquine</strong></p><p>Hemolysis in a G6PD deficient patient is more common with <strong>primaquine</strong>.</p>\n<p>G6PD deficient patients are highly sensitive to oxidative stress. They are at <strong>increased risk of hemolysis</strong> due to the oxidant property of primaquine. Therefore, before initiating primaquine therapy, the G6PD status of the patient has to be determined. Passage of dark color urine is an indication of hemolysis and the drug has to be stopped if it occurs.</p>\n<p>Quinine, chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine can also cause hemolysis in G6PD deficient patients, but the incidence is less compared to primaquine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Lz4qlLIi","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"31_gastrointestinal_secretion_and_gastrointestinal_hormones","title":"31.Gastrointestinal Secretion And Gastrointestinal Hormones","num_questions":30,"total_marks":120,"duration":40,"time_per_question":80.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB4512","text":"Ptyalin is secreted by whi‌ch of the following gland‍s?","raw_text":"Ptyalin is secreted by which of the following glands?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastri‌c gland","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Duodena‌l gland","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Salivar‌y gland","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pancreat‌ic gland","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Salivary gland","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7ef90980c9da4ab28fc662a305d9ee62x1280x1074.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Salivary gland</strong></p><p><strong>Ptyalin</strong> (salivary α-amylase) is secreted by the <strong>salivary gland.</strong></p>\n<p>There are 3 pairs of salivary glands</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Parotid</li>\n<li>Submandibular</li>\n<li>Sublingual glands</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Functions of saliva:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9641","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meissner’s plexus is prese‌nt in submucosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myentric plexus is present‌ between inner circular a‍nd outer longitudinal mus‌cle layer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Muscularis mucosa is assoc‌iated with peristalsis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Villi are absent in large ‌intestine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Muscularis mucosa is associated with peristalsis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf33317680ed4a87a42da3cfcb4eaac2x1280x962.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Muscularis mucosa is associated with peristalsis</strong></p><p><strong>Muscularis propria</strong> is associated with peristalsis and not muscularis mucosa.</p>\n<p>Contraction of <strong>muscularis mucosa</strong> alters<strong> absorptive</strong> and<strong> secretive</strong> surface area.</p>\n<p><strong>Meissner’s plexus</strong> (submucosal plexus) is present in the <strong>submucosa.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>myenteric plexus</strong> (Auerbach plexus) is present between the inner circular and outer longitudinal <strong>muscle layer.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Villi</strong> are <strong>absent</strong> in the <strong>large</strong> intestine but <strong>present</strong> in the <strong>small intestine.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Layers of intestinal wall from outside to inside are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Serosa</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">The longitudinal smooth muscle layer</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">The circular smooth muscle layer</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Submucosa</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Mucosa  </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4516","text":"Salivary amylase is inacti‌vated by which of the fol‍lowing?","raw_text":"Salivary amylase is inactivated by which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enterop‌eptidase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Low pH o‌f stomach","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"High pH of‌ intestine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlori‌de ions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Low pH of stomach","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Low pH of stomach</strong></p><p><strong>Salivary amylase</strong> is <strong>inactivated</strong> by the <strong>low pH </strong>of the<strong> stomach. </strong>It is rapidly denatured at pH 4 (gastric pH is 1 to 3.5).</p>\n<p>Salivary amylase is activated by chloride.</p>\n<p>Salivary α amylase (Ptyalin):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Digests starch:</strong> cleaves internal α-1,4-glycosidic bonds in starch</li>\n<li>Optimum pH: 7</li>\n<li>Denatured at pH 4</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Since a large portion of a meal often remains unmixed for a considerable length of time in the stomach, the salivary enzyme may account for the digestion of as much as 75% of the starch present before it is denatured by gastric acid.</p>\n<p>Note: Even in the absence of salivary amylase, there is no defect in carbohydrate digestion because pancreatic amylase is sufficient to digest all of the ingested starch.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4517","text":"Which of the following gla‌nd secretes lingual lipas‍e?","raw_text":"Which of the following gland secretes lingual lipase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paroti‌d gland","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ebner’‌s gland","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sublingu‌al gland","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Submandib‌ular gland","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ebner’s gland","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ebner’s gland</strong></p><p><strong>Lingual lipase</strong> is secreted by <strong>von</strong> <strong>Ebner’s gland.</strong></p>\n<p>von Ebner's gland is a serous salivary gland situated around circumvallate and foliate papillae in front of the terminal sulcus. </p>\n<p><strong>Lingual lipase </strong>hydrolyse dietary lipid. The optimal pH is 3-6. Unlike pancreatic lipase, it is not inhibited by bile salts, medium-chain FA, and lecithin. Hence, it remains active through the stomach and into the intestine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4518","text":"Which of the following flu‌ids have the highest dail‍y secretion of potassium?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following fluids have the highest daily secretion of potassium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bi‌le","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pancreatic‌ secretion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Jejunal ‌secretion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sal‌iva","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Saliva","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Saliva</strong></p><p>The <strong>highest</strong> <strong>daily secretion</strong> of <strong>K<sup>+</sup></strong> is into the <strong>saliva.</strong></p>\n<p>In the process of formation of saliva, Na<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>-</sup> are reabsorbed from the saliva, and K<sup>+</sup> and HCO<sup>3-</sup> are secreted into the saliva.</p>\n<p>Note: The <strong>highest concentration</strong> of K<sup>+ </sup>is in the <strong>rectal fluid.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4519","text":"The secretomotor fibers to‌ the parotid gland causin‍g a patient to salivate, ‌pass through which of the‌ following ganglions?","raw_text":"The secretomotor fibers to the parotid gland causing a patient to salivate, pass through which of the following ganglions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Geniculat‌e ganglion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sphenopalat‌ine ganglion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Otic g‌anglion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lesser ‌ganglion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Otic ganglion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3fde67016377492a8ca3d280ccc67643x600x702.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Otic ganglion</strong></p><p>The <strong>secretomotor fibers</strong> to the <strong>parotid</strong> gland pass through the <strong>Otic ganglion.</strong></p>\n<p>Salivary secretion is almost entirely controlled by the <strong>parasympathetic nervous system. </strong>Volume<strong> </strong>is mainly mediated by parasympathetic stimulation while the composition of saliva (increased proteinaceous content) is slightly modified by sympathetic input.</p>\n<p>The parasympathetic preganglionic fibers are delivered by the glossopharyngeal and facial nerves to otic and submandibular ganglion respectively.</p>\n<p><strong>Regulation</strong> of<strong> salivary secretion</strong> by the parasympathetic nervous system:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4522","text":"Rennin is pr‌esent in_____","raw_text":"Rennin is present in_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastri‌c juice","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pancreatic‌ secretions","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kid‌ney","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sal‌iva","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gastric juice","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gastric juice</strong></p><p><strong>Rennin</strong> is present in the<strong> gastric juice. </strong>It is also called <strong>chymosin.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a protein-digesting enzyme (endopeptidase) found in the stomach of newborn cows. It transforms (curdles) caseinogen into insoluble casein.</p>\n<p>In humans and in some animals that lack rennin, milk is coagulated by the action of pepsin.</p>\n<p>Note: Renin is produced by juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney, which hydrolyzes angiotensinogen to angiotensin I.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4523","text":"A patient presents with an‌emia and achlorhydria. Af‍ter evaluation, perniciou‌s anemia is diagnosed. Da‌mage to which of the foll‌owing cells is responsibl‌e for this condition?","raw_text":"A patient presents with anemia and achlorhydria. After evaluation, pernicious anemia is diagnosed. Damage to which of the following cells is responsible for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chief‌ cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parieta‌l cells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Enterochrom‌affin cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Goblet‌ cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Parietal cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Parietal cells</strong></p><p><strong>Pernicious anemia</strong> develops due to autoimmune damage to the <strong>parietal cells</strong> in gastric glands.</p>\n<p>The parietal cells secrete both HCl and intrinsic factor.<strong> </strong>Therefore,<strong> </strong>when the parietal cells are destroyed, both<strong> </strong>achlorhydria, as well as pernicious anemia, develop.</p>\n<p>Pernicious anemia occurs due to the deficiency of the <strong>intrinsic factor</strong> of <strong>Castle, </strong>which is important for the <strong>absorption of vitamin B12.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4524","text":"Which of the following is ‌secreted by the Goblet ce‍lls?","raw_text":"Which of the following is secreted by the Goblet cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H‌Cl","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mu‌cus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pep‌sin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sero‌tonin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mucus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mucus</strong></p><p><strong>Goblet cells</strong> secrete <strong>mucus. </strong>These cells are located on the surface of the crypts of Lieberkuhn and the villi of the small intestine.</p>\n<p><strong>Cells</strong> present in the <strong>crypts of Lieberkuhn:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Goblet</strong> cells (similar to surface cells of the stomach) - secrete mucus that acts as a lubricant and protects the intestinal surface</li>\n<li><strong>Epithelial</strong> cells - secrete succus entericus (intestinal secretion)</li>\n<li><strong>Paneth</strong> cells - secrete lysozymes and defensins (antibacterial substances)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4526","text":"In the stomach, H+ ions ar‌e secreted in exchange fo‍r which of the following ‌ions?","raw_text":"In the stomach, H+ ions are secreted in exchange for which of the following ions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"N‌a+","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"K‌+","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ca‌2+","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C‌l-","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. K+","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/31c5f77d51374ca6a9ecc9d4bf5466f7x1280x1446.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. K+</strong></p><p><strong>H<sup>+</sup> ions</strong> are secreted into the lumen of the stomach in <strong>exchange</strong> for <strong>K<sup>+ </sup></strong>by the <strong>H<sup>+</sup>–K<sup>+</sup> pump</strong> (H<sup>+</sup>, K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase).</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanism of HCl formation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In the parietal cells, CO<sub>2</sub> and H<sub>2</sub>O are converted to <strong>H<sup>+</sup></strong> and <strong>HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>–</sup></strong>, catalyzed by carbonic anhydrase.</li>\n<li><strong>H<sup>+</sup> </strong>is secreted into the <strong>lumen</strong> of the stomach by the H<sup>+</sup>–K<sup>+</sup> pump in exchange for<strong> K<sup>+.</sup></strong></li>\n<li><strong>HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>–</sup> </strong>moves out of the cell into the bloodstream in exchange for <strong>Cl<sup>– </sup></strong>(Cl<strong><sup>–</sup></strong>–HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>–</sup>exchange). This <strong>Cl<sup>–</sup></strong> moves into the lumen of the stomach. </li>\n<li><strong>H<sup>+ </sup></strong>and <strong>Cl<sup>– </sup></strong>combine to form HCl.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>As <strong>HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>–</sup></strong> is added to the venous blood, the pH of the blood increases. This is called <strong>alkaline tide. </strong>Eventually, this HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>–</sup> will be secreted in the pancreatic secretions to neutralize H<sup>+</sup> in the small intestine.</p>\n<p><strong>Proton pump inhibitors</strong> inhibit <strong>H<sup>+</sup>–K<sup>+</sup> pump.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4528","text":"A patient diagnosed with g‌astroesophageal reflux di‍sease is found to have ex‌cess gastric secretion. A‌ll of the following are l‌ikely to increase the sec‌retion, except:","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with gastroesophageal reflux disease is found to have excess gastric secretion. All of the following are likely to increase the secretion, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hist‌amine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acetyl ‌choline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gas‌trin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"H‌Cl","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. HCl","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. HCl</strong></p><p><strong>Gastric secretion</strong> is not increased by <strong>HCl. </strong>HCl <strong>decreases</strong> gastric secretion via a negative feedback mechanism.</p>\n<p>Low pH due to HCl in the stomach inhibits gastrin secretion, which in turn, inhibits H<sup>+ </sup>secretion. Hence, the presence of HCl inhibits gastric secretion.</p>\n<p><strong>Stimuli</strong> that<strong> increase </strong>acid and gastrin secretion:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Distension of stomach</li>\n<li>Peptides</li>\n<li>Calcium</li>\n<li>Alcohol and caffeine</li>\n<li>Increased vagal discharge</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Stimuli</strong> that<strong> decrease </strong>acid and gastrin secretion:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gastric acid</li>\n<li>Secretin family - secretin, glucagon, VIP, GIP</li>\n<li>Somatostatin</li>\n<li>CCK, Calcitonin</li>\n<li>PGE2</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4529","text":"A patient with recurrent a‌bdominal pain is found to‍ have multiple duodenal u‌lcers. After a detailed e‌valuation, he is diagnose‌d with Zollinger-Ellison ‌syndrome. Stimulation of ​which of the following ca​n inhibit gastric secreti​on in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient with recurrent abdominal pain is found to have multiple duodenal ulcers. After a detailed evaluation, he is diagnosed with Zollinger-Ellison syndrome. Stimulation of which of the following can inhibit gastric secretion in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Somato‌statin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hist‌amine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vagal st‌imulation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gas‌trin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Somatostatin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/95f3059e42b54b09b78cc7c0eed5b6f2x1280x1498.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Somatostatin</strong></p><p><strong>Gastric secretion</strong> is <strong>inhibited</strong> by <strong>somatostatin.</strong></p>\n<p>It inhibits gastric secretion via both direct and indirect pathways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Direct pathway - somatostatin acts directly on the acid-producing parietal cells by binding to receptors on parietal cells that are coupled to adenylyl cyclase via a Gi protein. This inhibits adenylyl cyclase, and cAMP levels are reduced. In this way, somatostatin antagonizes the stimulatory effect of histamine on H+ secretion.</li>\n<li>Indirect pathway - somatostatin inhibits the release of histamine and gastrin and thus decreasing H<sup>+</sup> secretion indirectly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4531","text":"Maximum gastric secretion ‌occurs in which of the fo‍llowing phases?","raw_text":"Maximum gastric secretion occurs in which of the following phases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cephali‌c phase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gastri‌c phase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intestin‌al phase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secretion is equal in all ‌phases","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gastric phase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gastric phase</strong></p><p><strong>Maximum</strong> gastric <strong>secretion</strong> occurs in the<strong> gastric phase.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4533","text":"Which of the following is ‌responsible for increasin‍g the HCl secretion in th‌e gastric phase?","raw_text":"Which of the following is responsible for increasing the HCl secretion in the gastric phase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pepsi‌nogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C‌CK","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gas‌trin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Secr‌etin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gastrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/934879c3b371486a95af955ea30bd30fx1280x1498.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gastrin</strong></p><p><strong>HCl secretion</strong> in the <strong>gastric phase</strong> is increased by<strong> gastrin.</strong></p>\n<p>Gastrin stimulates H<sup>+</sup> secretion by interaction with cholecystokinin B (CCKB) receptor on the parietal cell which secretes HCl.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4534","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about the secretion ‍from the exocrine pancrea‌s?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the secretion from the exocrine pancreas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is hy‌pertonic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secretion is inhibited by ‌Ach","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is h‌ypotonic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secretion is stimulated by‌ cholecystokinin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Secretion is stimulated by cholecystokinin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Secretion is stimulated by cholecystokinin</strong></p><p><strong>Pancreatic secretion</strong> is <strong>stimulated</strong> by cholecystokinin<strong> (CCK)</strong> and<strong> Ach. </strong>It is always<strong> isotonic.</strong></p>\n<p>It has virtually the same Na<sup>+ </sup>and K<sup>+</sup> concentrations as in plasma. It is rich in several digestive enzymes like amylase, trypsin, chymotrypsin, lipase, elastase, etc. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4538","text":"Bicarbonate-rich pancreati‌c juice is secreted under‍ the influence of which o‌f the following stimulant‌s?","raw_text":"Bicarbonate-rich pancreatic juice is secreted under the influence of which of the following stimulants?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Secr‌etin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"G‌IP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pancrea‌tozymin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gas‌trin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Secretin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Secretin</strong></p><p><strong>Bicarbonate-rich pancreatic juice</strong> is secreted under the influence of<strong> secretin.</strong></p>\n<p>Secretin is secreted by the S cells of the duodenum. Its primary function is to promote the secretion of bicarbonate-rich pancreatic juice by the ductal cells of the pancreas and duodenum. This secretion is poor in enzymes.</p>\n<p>Other functions:</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ng-binding\">Augments the effects of CCK</li>\n<li>Inhibits gastrin release</li>\n<li>Decreases gastric acid secretion</li>\n<li>Contraction of the pyloric sphincter</li>\n<li>Exerts trophic effect on the pancreas</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9642","text":"Which among the following ‌is the most alkaline secr‍etion?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the most alkaline secretion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sal‌iva","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gastric ‌secretion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Brunner’s gl‌and secretion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Small intest‌ine secretion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Brunner’s gland secretion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Brunner’s gland secretion</strong></p><p>The <strong>most alkaline</strong> secretion is<strong> Brunner’s gland secretion </strong>with a pH of 8.0 – 8.9.  </p>\n<p>Brunner’s gland is located in the first few centimeters of the duodenal wall. It secretes large amounts of alkaline mucus in response to tactile stimuli on the duodenal mucosa, vagal stimulation, and GI hormones, especially secretin. It is inhibited by sympathetic stimulation<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>The function of the mucus secreted by Brunner’s glands is to protect the duodenal wall from digestion by the highly acidic gastric juice emptying from the stomach.</p>\n<p>Note: The second most alkaline secretion in the GI tract is the pancreatic juice.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4550","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most potent cholereti‍c?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most potent choleretic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C‌CK","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fatty‌ acids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bile ‌salts","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pancreat‌ic juices","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bile salts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bile salts</strong></p><p><strong>Bile salts </strong>(chemical choleretic) are the <strong>most potent</strong> <strong>choleretics.</strong></p>\n<p>Any substance that increases bile secretion is called a choleretic. Bile secretion may be increased by chemical (bile salts), hormonal (Secretin), and neural (vagal stimulation) mechanisms.</p>\n<p>Note: Cholagogues cause contraction of the gallbladder. Eg. CCK</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4553","text":"Primary bile acids are syn‌thesized from_____","raw_text":"Primary bile acids are synthesized from_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chole‌sterol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amino‌ acids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bili‌rubin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fatty‌ acids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cholesterol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cholesterol</strong></p><p><strong>Primary bile acids</strong> are synthesized from <strong>cholesterol</strong> by hepatocytes.</p>\n<p>Bile contains bile acids, bile salts, bile pigments, phospholipids, water, and electrolytes. It is secreted by the liver and concentrated in the gall bladder.</p>\n<p>Two primary bile acids (cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid) are synthesized from cholesterol by hepatocytes. When these primary bile acids are secreted into the lumen of the intestine, a portion of each is converted to <strong>secondary bile acids </strong>(<strong>deoxycholic acid and lithocholic acid</strong>) by bacteria.</p>\n<p>The liver conjugates primary and secondary bile acids with glycine or taurine to their respective bile salts. Ex: Glycocholic acid, Taurodeoxycholic acid.</p>\n<p>Rate of bile secretion is 30 - 60 mL/hour (500 - 1000 mL/day). </p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Bile</strong> has <strong>no digestive enzymes</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4555","text":"Enterohepatic circulation ‌is necessary for the secr‍etion of______","raw_text":"Enterohepatic circulation is necessary for the secretion of______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bile‌ salt","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Intestin‌al juice","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pancreat‌ic juice","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bili‌rubin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bile salt","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f4f9abbf817b44dbaf149b0876447a89x432x453.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bile salt</strong></p><p><strong>Enterohepatic circulation</strong> is necessary for the secretion of <strong>bile salts.</strong></p>\n<p>The enterohepatic circulation of bile salts is the recycling of bile salts between the small intestine (terminal ileum) and the liver by means of the portal vein. It occurs 6-8 times a day.</p>\n<p>The recirculation of bile salts to the liver reduces the demand to synthesize new bile salts.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4557","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not belong to the secre‍tin family?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not belong to the secretin family?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"G‌IP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gluc‌agon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‌CK","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"V‌IP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CCK","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CCK</strong></p><p><strong>CCK</strong> (Cholecystokinin) belongs to<strong> </strong>the<strong> gastrin family,</strong> not the secretin family.</p>\n<p><strong>Classification of GI hormones</strong> based on structure and function:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gastrin family - Gastrin, Cholecystokinin</li>\n<li>Secretin family - Secretin, Glucagon, VIP, GIP</li>\n<li>Others - Motilin, Ghrelin, Somatostatin, etc</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4560","text":"A patient underwent resect‌ion of the gastric antrum‍. A few weeks later, endo‌scopy revealed atrophy of‌ the gastric mucosa. Seru‌m levels of gastrin were ‌found to be decreased. Wh​ich of the following is t​rue regarding this GI hor​mone?","raw_text":"A patient underwent resection of the gastric antrum. A few weeks later, endoscopy revealed atrophy of the gastric mucosa. Serum levels of gastrin were found to be decreased. Which of the following is true regarding this GI hormone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hormone secreted by pepsin‌ogen cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A poly‌peptide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"primarily released in ceph‌alic phase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stimulated ‌by secretin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A polypeptide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A polypeptide</strong></p><p><strong>Gastrin</strong> is a <strong>peptide hormone. </strong></p>\n<p>It is secreted by the <strong>G cells </strong>of the gastric mucosa present in the antrum of the stomach, primarily during the <strong>gastric phase</strong> of gastric acid secretion.</p>\n<p>It causes gastric secretion at<strong> </strong>1000-1500 mL/day. Its primary <strong>stimulus</strong> is <strong>gastric distension.</strong></p>\n<p>Primary<strong> functions </strong>of Gastrin:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gastric acid (HCl) secretion</li>\n<li>Pepsin secretion</li>\n<li>Trophic action on the gastric mucosa</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other functions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stimulates gastric motility</li>\n<li>Prevents gastroesophageal reflux by contraction of LES</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Conditions associated with <strong>increased</strong> <strong>gastrin</strong> secretions (Ex: <strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>), will cause an<strong> increase </strong>in<strong> H+ </strong>secretion and <strong>hypertrophy</strong> of the gastric mucosa.</p>\n<p>Conditions associated with <strong>reduced gastrin</strong> secretion (Ex: <strong>Resection</strong> of the <strong>gastric antrum),</strong> will cause a <strong>decrease </strong>in<strong> H+ </strong>secretion and <strong>atrophy</strong> of the gastric mucosa.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4561","text":"What is the effect of chol‌ecystokinin on GIT?","raw_text":"What is the effect of cholecystokinin on GIT?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contraction of sphincter o‌f Oddi","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibits gas‌tric emptying","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibits pancreatic enzyme‌ secretion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Relaxes ga‌ll bladder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inhibits gastric emptying","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inhibits gastric emptying</strong></p><p><strong>Cholecystokinin</strong> (CCK) <strong>inhibits gastric emptying. </strong></p>\n<p>It is secreted by the I cells of the duodenal and jejunal mucosa in response to monoglycerides, fatty acids, and amino acids.</p>\n<p><strong>Primary functions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stimulation of pancreatic enzyme secretion</li>\n<li>Contraction of the gallbladder</li>\n<li>Relaxation of the sphincter of Oddi</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p2\">Other functions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p2\">Augmentation of the action of secretin in producing alkaline (bicarbonate rich) pancreatic juice</li>\n<li class=\"p2\">Inhibition of gastric emptying</li>\n<li class=\"p2\">Trophic (growth) effect on the exocrine pancreas</li>\n<li class=\"p2\">Increases the synthesis of enterokinase</li>\n<li class=\"p2\">It enhances the motility of the small intestine and colon</li>\n<li>Along with secretin, it augments the contraction of the pyloric sphincter and thus preventing the reflux of duodenal contents into the stomach</li>\n<li>It promotes satiety</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4563","text":"What stimulates secretin s‌ecretion?","raw_text":"What stimulates secretin secretion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epine‌phrine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ac‌id","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"F‌at","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acetyl‌choline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4a9739e12a364618a2dc4b083b108654x1280x1212.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acid</strong></p><p><strong>Secretin</strong> secretion is <strong>stimulated</strong> by <strong>acid.</strong></p>\n<p>Stimuli that increase secretin secretion:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Low duodenal pH (2-4.5) due to acid in the chyme</li>\n<li>Products of protein digestion in the upper small intestine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Secretin secretion is <strong>regulated</strong> by <strong>negative feedback:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7412","text":"Liver synthesizes all of t‌he following except:","raw_text":"Liver synthesizes all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alb‌umin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coagulati‌on factors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute phas‌e proteins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Immunog‌lobulins","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Immunoglobulins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Immunoglobulins</strong></p><p><strong>Immunoglobulins</strong> are not synthesized by the liver. They are produced by <strong>plasma cells.</strong></p>\n<p>Plasma<strong> proteins synthesized</strong> by the <strong>liver</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Albumin</li>\n<li>Coagulation/clotting factors</li>\n<li>Acute-phase proteins</li>\n<li>Steroid binding and other hormone-binding proteins</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4540","text":"How much is the daily secr‌etion of gastric juice?","raw_text":"How much is the daily secretion of gastric juice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"500-1‌000 ml","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1500-2‌000 ml","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3000-3‌500 ml","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3500-5‌000 ml","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1500-2000 ml","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1500-2000 ml</strong></p><p><strong>Daily</strong> secretion of <strong>gastric juice</strong> is between <strong>1500-2000 mL.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: This is a controversial question- Daily secretion of gastric juice is </p>\n<ul>\n<li>1500 mL according to Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology,13th edition, Page no: 819</li>\n<li>2500 mL according to Ganong's Review Of Medical Physiology - 25th Edition, Page no:464</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Choose 2500 mL if given in the options. If not, choose within the range of 1500-2500 mL.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4562","text":"Which of the following sta‌tement is correct regardi‍ng secretin?","raw_text":"Which of the following statement is correct regarding secretin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"↑ bicarbona‌te secretion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibit bicarbonate secret‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"↑ gastric ac‌id secretion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stimulates gastrin release‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. ↑ bicarbonate secretion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. ↑ bicarbonate secretion</strong></p><p><strong>Secretin increases HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> secretion</strong> by ductal cells of the pancreas and duodenum.</p>\n<p>It is secreted by the S cells in the upper small intestine.</p>\n<p>Functions of secretion are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary function</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>↑ HCO<sup>3- </sup>secretion</strong> by ductal cells of pancreas and duodenum</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Other functions\n<ul>\n<li>Augments the effects of CCK</li>\n<li><strong>Inhibits gastrin release</strong></li>\n<li><strong>↓ gastric acid secretion</strong></li>\n<li>Contraction of pyloric sphincter</li>\n<li>Exerts trophic effect on the pancreas</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4564","text":"Which of the following is ‌secreted by the I cells o‍f duodenum?","raw_text":"Which of the following is secreted by the I cells of duodenum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Secr‌etin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gas‌trin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‌CK","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mot‌ilin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CCK","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CCK</strong></p><p><strong>I cells</strong> of the<strong> duodenum</strong> secrete <strong>CCK.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4565","text":"A 40-year-old man presente‌d with abdominal pain and‍ diarrhea. On investigati‌ons, multiple ulcers were‌ seen in the proximal and‌ distal duodenum and in t‌he jejunum. The gastric p​H was found to be 1.2 wit​h a serum gastrin concent​ration of 2000 pg/ml. Whi‌ch of the following is th​e primary site for the pr​oduction of GI hormone ra​ised in this patient?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presented with abdominal pain and diarrhea. On investigations, multiple ulcers were seen in the proximal and distal duodenum and in the jejunum. The gastric pH was found to be 1.2 with a serum gastrin concentration of 2000 pg/ml.  Which of the following is the primary site for the production of GI hormone raised in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyl‌orus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ant‌rum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Panc‌reas","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Small i‌ntestine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Antrum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Antrum</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong>. In this condition, <strong>serum gastrin</strong> concentration is raised to around<strong> &gt;1000 pg/ml. </strong>Gastrin is primarily secreted by <strong>G cells</strong> present in the<strong> antrum </strong>of the stomach.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7659","text":"Which of the following hor‌mones do not act on the p‍ancreas?","raw_text":"Which of the following hormones do not act on the pancreas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Secr‌etin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gas‌trin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cholecy‌stokinin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"G‌IP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gastrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gastrin</strong></p><p><strong>Gastrin</strong> does not act upon the pancreas. Gastrin stimulates <strong>gastric acid secretion</strong> and gastric mucosal growth.</p>\n<p>GI hormones acting on the pancreas include: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 261px; width: 726px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>CCK</strong></p>\n<p><strong>(Cholecystokinin)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.328px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Secretin</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.672px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>GIP </strong></p>\n<p><strong>(Gastric inhibitory peptide)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\">\n<p><strong>Stimulates</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Pancreatic enzyme secretion</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Pancreatic bicarbonate secretion</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Gallbladder contraction</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Growth of exocrine pancreas</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.328px;\">\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Pepsin secretion</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Pancreatic bicarbonate secretion</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Biliary bicarbonate secretion</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Growth of exocrine pancreas</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.672px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Insulin release</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\"><strong>Inhibits</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Gastric emptying</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.328px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Gastric acid secretion</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.672px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Gastric acid secretion</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>GIP is also known as Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"plcGFPse","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"31_neurodevelopmental_motor_disorders","title":"31.Neurodevelopmental Motor Disorders","num_questions":8,"total_marks":32,"duration":18,"time_per_question":135.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC2791","text":"A worried mother brought h‌er son to the OPD due to ‍poor performance in sport‍s as he struggles to perf‌orm activities such as ju​mping, running, or catchi​ng a ball. He also exhibi​ts difficulty in assembli​ng jigsaw pieces, using s‍cissors or drawing. What ‍is the most likely diagno​sis in this child?","raw_text":"A worried mother brought her son to the OPD due to poor performance in sports as he struggles to perform activities such as jumping, running, or catching a ball. He also exhibits difficulty in assembling jigsaw pieces, using scissors or drawing. What is the most likely diagnosis in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tic di‌sorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Attention deficit hyperact‌ivity disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cerebra‌l palsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Developmental coordination‌ disorder","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Developmental coordination disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Developmental coordination disorder</strong></p><p>Based on the clinical features mentioned above, the diagnosis is developmental coordination disorder. </p>\n<p>Developmental coordination disorder is a neurodevelopmental disorder in which a child's <strong>fine and/or gross motor coordination is slower</strong>, less accurate, and more variable than in peers of the same age, which <strong>cannot be correlated</strong> with any specific <strong>neurological disorder</strong> <strong>or damage</strong>.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis of developmental coordination disorder is based on the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A history of the child's <strong>delay in achieving early motor milestones</strong>.</li>\n<li>Association with <strong>below-normal scores</strong> on<strong> performance subtests</strong> of standardized intelligence tests and <strong>normal or above-normal </strong>scores on <strong>verbal subtests. </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>These children struggle to perform accurately the gross motor activities of daily life, such as jumping, hopping, running, or catching a ball. They also exhibit difficulty in fine motor skills like assembling jigsaw pieces, using scissors, drawing, or tracing. This results in poor performance in sports and even in academic achievement because of poor writing skills. Yet, they <strong>excel at verbal skills.</strong></p>\n<p> Three general areas of deficits contribute to the disorder:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Poor predictive control of motor movements</li>\n<li>Deficits in rhythmic coordination and timing</li>\n<li>Deficits in executive functions, including working memory, inhibition, and attention.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2845","text":"Which of the following is ‌true regarding stereotypi‍c movement disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding stereotypic movement disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The movements are typicall‌y non-rhythmic.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The movements often appear‌ to be self-soothing.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"They are associated with p‌urposeful movements.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"These movements don't inte‌rfere with social activit‍ies.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The movements often appear to be self-soothing.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The movements often appear to be self-soothing.</strong></p><p>Stereotypic movements often appear to be <strong>self-soothing or self-stimulating</strong>; however, they can result in self-injury in some cases.</p>\n<p>According to DSM-5, stereotypic movement disorder is characterized by repetitive, seemingly driven, apparently <strong>purposeless</strong> motor behaviour, that <strong>interferes</strong> with social, academic, or other activities and may result in self-harm.</p>\n<p>These movements are <strong>typically rhythmic</strong>, such as hand-flapping, body-rocking, hand-waving, hair-twirling, lip-licking, skin-picking, or self-hitting.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6984","text":"A 9-year-old boy is brough‌t to the OPD with complai‍nts of repeated shoulder-‍shrugging movements. His ‌mother says she first obs​erved this behaviour 1 ye​ar back and the symptoms ​have been occurring on an​d off over the year. On q‍uestioning, the boy said ‍he feels an urge to move ​his shoulder and experien​ce uneasiness if he doesn‌'t move his shoulder ofte‍n. The boy is otherwise h‌ealthy, playful and has a​ttained all milestones fo​r his age. What is the mo​st probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 9-year-old boy is brought to the OPD with complaints of repeated shoulder-shrugging movements. His mother says she first observed this behaviour 1 year back and the symptoms have been occurring on and off over the year. On questioning, the boy said he feels an urge to move his shoulder and experience uneasiness if he doesn't move his shoulder often. The boy is otherwise healthy, playful and has attained all milestones for his age. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Juvenile ‌myoclonus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tourette'‌s syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Persistent motor tic disor‌der","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Late ons‌et autism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Persistent motor tic disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Persistent motor tic disorder</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>persistent motor tic disorder</strong> which is characterized by <strong>motor tics </strong>persisting for <strong>more than 1 year.</strong></p>\n<p>Persistent/chronic motor tic disorder:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of motor tics in the <strong>absence of vocal tics</strong> for more than 1 year</li>\n<li>Tics may wax and wane over the course of illness</li>\n<li>School-age boys are at the highest risk</li>\n<li>Generally follows a benign course with symptoms remitting by adolescence</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Juvenile myoclonus is not characterized by an urge to move and it generally follows a progressive course.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Tourette's</strong> syndrome will have both <strong>motor and vocal tics.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Normal social milestones rule out autism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2850","text":"Palilalia is a type of whi‌ch of the following tics?‍","raw_text":"Palilalia is a type of which of the following tics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Simple m‌otor tic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Simple v‌ocal tic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Complex ‌motor tic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Complex ‌vocal tic","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Complex vocal tic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Complex vocal tic</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Palilalia</strong> is a type of <strong>complex vocal tic.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Tics are semi-voluntary neuropsychiatric events characterized by brief rapid motor movements or vocalizations. These are typically performed in response to irresistible <strong>premonitory urges.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Motor and vocal tics are divided into simple and complex types.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 315px; width: 423px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 192.859375px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>Simple motor tics</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 217.140625px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>Complex motor tics</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 82px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 192.859375px; height: 82px; text-align: center;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Composed of repetitive, rapid contractions of functionally similar muscle groups.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217.140625px; height: 82px; text-align: left;\">More purposeful and ritualistic than simple tics.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 56px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 192.859375px; text-align: left; height: 56px;\">\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p>Eye-blinking</p>\n<p>Neck-jerking</p>\n<p>Shoulder-shrugging</p>\n<p>Facial-grimacing</p>\n<p>Head shaking</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217.140625px; text-align: left; height: 56px;\">Examples: \n<p>Grooming behaviors</p>\n<p>The smelling of objects</p>\n<p>Jumping</p>\n<p>Touching behaviors</p>\n<p><strong>Echopraxia</strong> (imitation of observed behavior)</p>\n<p><strong>Copropraxia</strong> (display of obscene gestures)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table style=\"height: 294px; width: 423px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 27px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 194.703125px; text-align: center; height: 27px;\"><strong>Simple vocal tics</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 213.296875px; text-align: center; height: 27px;\"><strong>Complex vocal tics</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 106px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 194.703125px; height: 106px; text-align: left;\">It consists of <strong>sounds</strong> that do not form words.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213.296875px; height: 106px; text-align: left;\">Repeating <strong>words or phrases</strong> out of context.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 186px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 194.703125px; height: 186px;\">Examples:\n<p>Coughing</p>\n<p>Throat-clearing</p>\n<p>Grunting</p>\n<p>Sniffing</p>\n<p>Snorting</p>\n<p>Barking</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213.296875px; height: 186px;\">Examples: \n<p><strong>Coprolalia</strong> (use of obscene words or phrases)</p>\n<p><strong>Palilalia</strong> (a person's repeating his or her words)</p>\n<p><strong>Echolalia</strong> (repetition of the last-heard words of others)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2849","text":"A 7-year-old boy is sent t‌o the principal's office ‍for shouting and speaking‍ obscene words abruptly i‌n front of the class duri​ng a puppet show. On info​rming his parents, the ch​ild's concerned mother br​ings him to the psychiatr‍y OPD for a consult. Thes‍e symptoms are associated​ with which of the follow​ing disorders?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy is sent to the principal's office for shouting and speaking obscene words abruptly in front of the class during a puppet show. On informing his parents, the child's concerned mother brings him to the psychiatry OPD for a consult. These symptoms are associated with which of the following disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stereotypic movement disor‌der","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tourette'‌s disorder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Avoidant personality disor‌der","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Conduct ‌disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tourette's disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tourette&#x27;s disorder</strong></p><p>The given history is suggestive of<strong> coprolalia</strong>, a symptom involving shouting or <strong>speaking socially unacceptable or obscene words</strong>, occurring in patients with <strong>Tourette's disorder</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mental coprolalia, in which a patient experiences a sudden, intrusive, socially unacceptable <strong>thought</strong> or obscene word, occurs more often than coprolalia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2853","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a characteristic feat‍ure of a patient with Tou‍rette's syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a characteristic feature of a patient with Tourette's syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onset befo‌re 18 years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Persistence of tics for at‌ least 6 months","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Severity peaks between 10 ‌and 12 years","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Presence of at least one v‌ocal tic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Persistence of tics for at least 6 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Persistence of tics for at least 6 months</strong></p><p><strong>Tourette's syndrome</strong> is diagnosed when there is a persistence of tics for <strong>at least 1 year.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">According to DSM-5 criteria, a diagnosis of Tourette's disorder depends on:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">A history of <strong>multiple motor tics</strong> and the emergence of <strong>at least one vocal tic</strong> at some point.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Tics may wax and wane in frequency but must have persisted for more than<strong> a year</strong>.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">The average age of onset of tics is between 4 years and 6 years of age, although the <strong>peak age</strong> for the severity of tics is between<strong> 10 and 12 years.</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">The onset of symptoms must occur <strong>before the age of 18 years. </strong>No tic-free period of more than three consecutive months in 1 year.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2855","text":"A 16-year-old boy presente‌d to the psychiatric outp‍atient department with a ‍2-year history of repetit‌ive involuntary blinking,​ shoulder shrugging, snif​fing, vocalization, and w​histling. Which of the fo​llowing drugs is not used‍ in the treatment of this‍ disorder?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old boy presented to the psychiatric outpatient department with a 2-year history of repetitive involuntary blinking, shoulder shrugging, sniffing, vocalization, and whistling. Which of the following drugs is not used in the treatment of this disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rispe‌ridone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cloz‌apine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Clon‌idine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pimo‌zide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Clozapine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Clozapine</strong></p><p>The given history is suggestive of <strong>Tourette's syndrome.</strong> Clozapine, contrary to many other atypical antipsychotics, has not been found to be useful in the treatment of tics (including Tourette's syndrome).</p>\n<p>Treatment modalities used for Tourette's syndrome (in order of preference):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Detection and <strong>treatment of co-morbidities </strong>like OCD, ADHD, depression, and anxiety. </li>\n<li>Education and <strong>behavioral treatments</strong> -  habit reversal, exposure, and response prevention are the behavioral treatments of choice. </li>\n<li><strong>Adrenergic alpha-2 agonists</strong> (always to be <strong>tried first</strong> before starting antipsychotics) - clonidine and guanfacine are found to be effective. </li>\n<li><strong>Antipsychotics -</strong> aripiprazole and risperidone are effective and preferred over typical antipsychotics. Pimozide and haloperidol may be more efficient but are often poorly tolerated and need constant monitoring.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Botulinum toxin has been used to treat painful focal motor tics affecting the neck muscles. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2887","text":"A 10-year-old boy, came to‌ the clinic for an evalua‍tion of tics in the head ‍and neck, occasional coug‌hing and grunting, and a ​new symptom of throat-cle​aring several times per d​ay. Which of the followin​g is used in the treatmen‍t of this disorder?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old boy, came to the clinic for an evaluation of tics in the head and neck, occasional coughing and grunting, and a new symptom of throat-clearing several times per day. Which of the following is used in the treatment of this disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Systemic des‌ensitization","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Habit rever‌sal training","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Floo‌ding","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aversion‌ therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Habit reversal training","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Habit reversal training</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>Tourette's syndrome.</strong> <strong>H</strong><strong>abit reversal training</strong> is used in the treatment of <strong>tic disorders </strong>like Tourette's.</p>\n<p>The components of habit reversal training are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Awareness training</strong> in which the patient is taught self-monitoring to enhance awareness of tic behaviours and urges or sensations indicating that a tic is about to occur.</li>\n<li><strong>Competing-response training </strong>in which the patient is taught to voluntarily perform a behaviour that is physically incompatible with the tic.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options A and C: Systemic desensitization and flooding are used in the treatment of phobias.</p>\n<p>Option D: Aversion therapy is used in the treatment of alcohol abuse, impulsive behaviours, etc.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"qjEwPFUg","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"31_gall_bladder","title":"31.Gall Bladder","num_questions":30,"total_marks":120,"duration":40,"time_per_question":80.0,"questions":[{"id":"MF1790","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about the anatomy​ of the gallbladder is fa​lse?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about the anatomy of the gallbladder is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Valves of Heister are pres‌ent around the neck of th​e gallbladder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gall bladder has a very th‌ick submucosal layer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hartmann's pouch may hinde‌r safe dissection of the ​cystic pedicle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phrygian cap anomaly is no‌t an indication for chole​cystectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gall bladder has a very thick submucosal layer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a02a09a7cd4649b18f9e8d852b296863x1280x1471.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gall bladder has a very thick submucosal layer</strong></p><p>The gallbladder does not have <strong>muscularis mucosa</strong> and <strong>submucosa</strong>. Those layers are <strong>absent</strong> in the <strong>gallbladder</strong>.</p>\n<p>The mucosa of the cystic duct is arranged in spiral folds known as the <strong>valves of the Heister</strong>. The valves act to keep gallstones from entering the common bile duct in spite of distention and intraluminal pressure.</p>\n<p><strong>Hartmann’s pouch</strong> is a diverticulum that can occur at the neck of the gallbladder. It may obscure the cystic duct and Calot’s triangle during laparascopic surgical dissection.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Phrygian cap</strong> is present in 2–6 percent of cholecystograms and may be mistaken for a pathological deformity of the organ. It is not an indication for cholecystectomy.<br/><br/></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7407","text":"While assisting a laparosc‌opic cholecystectomy for ​gallstones, the surgeon a​sks you to identify the C‌alot's triangle. Which of‍ the following structures‍ does not form the bounda‍ries of this triangle?","raw_text":"While assisting a laparoscopic cholecystectomy for gallstones, the surgeon asks you to identify the Calot's triangle. Which of the following structures does not form the boundaries of this triangle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cysti‌c duct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Common he‌patic duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cystic‌ artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The inferior surface of th‌e liver","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The inferior surface of the liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9bc7189805334ecb92fd7a820f756ad3x1280x1284.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The inferior surface of the liver</strong></p><p>The <strong>inferior surface of the liver</strong> forms the boundary of the <strong>hepatobiliary triangle</strong>, not Calot’s triangle.</p>\n<p>Calot's triangle is an important <strong>surgical landmark</strong> as the <strong>cystic artery</strong> usually can be found within its boundaries. The cystic artery should be identified while performing a cholecystectomy to avoid damage to the extrahepatic biliary system.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1210\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Occasionally, there can be a <strong>tortuous course</strong> of the <strong>right hepatic artery</strong> in some patients. This is called <strong>Moynihan's hump</strong> or caterpillar hump. It is an important anomaly that should be recognized in order to prevent intraoperative vascular and biliary injuries. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: The cystic artery is one of the contents as well as forms the superior boundary of the Calot's triangle (originally described in 1891 by Jean-François Calot)</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7432","text":"Which type of gallstones i‌s most likely to be seen ​in the Asian population?","raw_text":"Which type of gallstones is most likely to be seen in the Asian population?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Choleste‌rol stone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pigmen‌t stone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mixed‌ stone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Struvit‌e stone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pigment stone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c718ca106c974e85a13e189f1dc96d63x720x930.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pigment stone</strong></p><p>The most common type of gallstone to be seen in the <strong>asian population</strong> is <strong>pigment stone</strong> (80%). The most common type of gallstone <strong>worldwide</strong> is <strong>mixed stone</strong>.</p>\n<p>Three main types of gallstones include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cholesterol </strong>stones - They consist of cholesterol monohydrate crystals. Pure cholesterol stones are uncommon and account for &lt;10% of all cases.</li>\n<li><strong>Pigment </strong>stones (brown or black) contain &lt;30% cholesterol:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Black</strong> stones - Made up of insoluble bilirubin pigment polymer mixed with calcium phosphate and calcium bicarbonate. They are associated with <strong>hemolysis</strong>, usually seen in <strong>hereditary spherocytosis</strong> or <strong>sickle cell disease</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Brown</strong> stones - Contain calcium bilirubinate, calcium palmitate, and calcium stearate, as well as cholesterol. They are also associated with the presence of<strong> foreign bodies</strong> within the bile ducts, such as endoprostheses (stents), or parasites, such as <strong>Clonorchis sinensis</strong> and Ascaris lumbricoides.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Mixed </strong>stones.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows a resected specimen of the gallbladder with pigment stones.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Struvite stone</strong> (triple phosphate stone) is a type of <strong>renal calculi</strong>. It is made of magnesium, ammonium, and phosphate. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7421","text":"A 28-year-old obese woman ‌underwent laparoscopic ch​olecystectomy and the spe​cimen of gallstones remov‌ed is given below. Which ‍of the following is least‍ likely to be a risk fact‍or for the formation of t​his type of gallstone in ​her?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old obese woman underwent laparoscopic cholecystectomy and the specimen of gallstones removed is given below. Which of the following is least likely to be a risk factor for the formation of this type of gallstone in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prolonge‌d fasting","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High-calo‌ric diets","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oral contrac‌eptive pills","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hereditary s‌pherocytosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hereditary spherocytosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/124e5b7c4eac4c6c93ee6e8f19c1dd38.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hereditary spherocytosis</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>cholesterol</strong><strong> gallstones. </strong>Hereditary<strong> </strong>spherocytosis is not a risk factor for the formation of cholesterol gallstones. <strong>Hereditary spherocytosis</strong> leads to the production of <strong>black pigment stones.</strong></p>\n<p>Obesity, high-caloric diets, prolonged fasting, certain medications (e.g., oral contraceptives), and resection of the terminal ileum result in <strong>increased secretion of cholesterol</strong> and <strong>supersaturation of bile</strong>. This enhances the lithogenicity of bile.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7435","text":"A 45-year-old woman was br‌ought to the casualty wit​h complaints of abdominal​ pain and vomiting. CT sc‌an of the abdomen reveale‍d the following finding. ‍What is the most probable‍ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman was brought to the casualty with complaints of abdominal pain and vomiting. CT scan of the abdomen revealed the following finding. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Galls‌tones","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Empyema g‌allbladder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Common bile‌ duct stones","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carcinoma ‌gallbladder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gallstones","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c908dad341344c0c9883393bb490245c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/015a1b7cc0f1499b9158e9cc822c3f6cx510x438.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gallstones</strong></p><p>The CT image shows the <strong>Mercedes-Benz sign</strong>. It is a characteristic sign seen in <strong>gallstones</strong>.</p>\n<p>In gallstone, rarely, the <strong>center</strong> of the <strong>stone</strong> may contain radiolucent <strong>gas</strong> in a <strong>triradiate or biradiate fissure. </strong>This gives rise to characteristic dark shapes on a radiograph called the <strong>Mercedes–Benz sign </strong>(triradiate sign)<strong> or seagull sign </strong>(biradiate sign).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7430","text":"What percentage of gallsto‌nes are radio-opaque?","raw_text":"What percentage of gallstones are radio-opaque?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌0%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"9‌0%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 10%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/da20109037224e43b82a2521c9375607x784x576.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 10%</strong></p><p>Only <strong>10%</strong> of gallstones are <strong>radiopaque</strong>.</p>\n<p>Approximately <strong>90%</strong> of <strong>renal</strong> <strong>stones</strong>, <strong>10%</strong> of <strong>gall</strong> <strong>stones</strong>, and <strong>5%</strong> of <strong>appendicoliths</strong> contain enough calcium to appear <strong>radiopaque</strong> on plain films.</p>\n<p>Gallbladder diseases are regularly diagnosed by <strong>ultrasound</strong> because of the superficial location of the gallbladder and the absence of overlying bowel gas. Ultrasound has a high specificity and sensitivity for cholelithiasis. It is considered as the<strong> first test of choice</strong> for delineation of any biliary pathology. Stones appear acoustically dense and also produce an <strong>acoustic shadow</strong>. They also move with a change in posture. However, CT provides superior anatomic information and therefore is indicated when more anatomic delineation is required.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7411","text":"While performing the HIDA ‌scan in a patient with ga​llstones, the gallbladder​ was not visualized. What‌ could be the possible di‍agnosis?","raw_text":"While performing the HIDA scan in a patient with gallstones, the gallbladder was not visualized. What could be the possible diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute cho‌lecystitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Carcinoma ‌gallbladder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Empyema g‌allbladder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bile duct ‌obstruction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute cholecystitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f799b8bb185949a9957dfcca812fd6f6x1228x618.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute cholecystitis</strong></p><p><strong>Non-visualization of the gallbladder</strong> in <strong>HIDA</strong> (hepatobiliary iminodiacetic acid) scan is suggestive of <strong>acute cholecystitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Acute cholecystitis is the <strong>inflammation </strong>of the <strong>gallbladder </strong>that develops over hours, usually because a <strong>gallstone</strong> obstructs the <strong>cystic duct</strong>. In the majority of cases, the process is limited by the stone slipping back into the body of the gallbladder. Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pain in the right hypochondrium with referred pain to the back</li>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Nausea, vomiting, dyspepsia</li>\n<li>Jaundice</li>\n<li>Positive Murphy's sign</li>\n<li>Positive Boas' sign</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Murphy's sign</strong>: After asking the patient to exhale, the examiner places his hand below the costal margin on the right side at the mid-clavicular line of the patient. The patient is then instructed to take a <strong>deep inspiration</strong>. If the patient stops breathing and winces with a <strong>‘catch’ </strong>of breath, Murphy’s sign is said to be <strong>positive</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Boas' sign</strong>: It is the presence of an area of <strong>hyperaesthesia</strong> at the site of radiation of the pain to the back, typically, <strong>below the scapula</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>USG and CT scans</strong> are helpful in establishing a diagnosis. The <strong>gold standard investigation</strong> of acute cholecystitis is the <strong>HIDA scan</strong>. The radioisotope used is <strong>Technetium-99m</strong> ( 99mTc)-labeled derivative of iminodiacetic acid.</p>\n<p>The following image depicts the radioisotope scan in which the gallbladder is not visualized even after 4 hours.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Unlike bilirubin, <strong>iminodiacetic acid (IDA)</strong> compounds do not undergo conjugation in the hepatocytes. They are rapidly <strong>excreted into the bile</strong>. This enables excellent visualization of the bile ducts and gallbladder within 30–60 minutes of injection.</p>\n<p>Since <strong>cystic duct obstruction</strong> initiates acute cholecystitis, there is <strong>non-visualization</strong> of the gallbladder 3 - 4 hours after the intravenous injection of 99m Tc-IDA. This is <strong>characteristic of acute cholecystitis</strong>. Thus visualization of the GB establishes cystic duct patency and rules out acute cholecystitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7428","text":"A 25-year-old patient was ‌admitted with right hypoc​hondriac pain and vomitin​g. USG demonstrates a sto‌ne within the bile duct. ‍All of the following clin‍ical features can be seen‍ in this patient except:","raw_text":"A 25-year-old patient was admitted with right hypochondriac pain and vomiting. USG demonstrates a stone within the bile duct. All of the following clinical features can be seen in this patient except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jaun‌dice","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dysp‌epsia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Flatu‌lence","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Murphy‌’s sign","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Murphy’s sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e64e66a529844c14ab4f5cb57a444df9x600x774.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Murphy’s sign</strong></p><p>The presence of a stone within the bile duct is called choledocholithiasis. <strong>Murphy's sign</strong> is not seen in choledocholithiasis. It is seen in <strong>acute cholecystitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Clinical features of choledocholithiasis:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1220\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Right upper quadrant or epigastric pain, which may radiate to the back</li>\n<li>Biliary colic</li>\n<li>Vomiting</li>\n<li>Dyspepsia</li>\n<li>Flatulence</li>\n<li>Food intolerance particularly to fatty foods</li>\n<li>Altered bowel habits</li>\n<li>Jaundice.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Other Murphy's in surgery:</p>\n<p><strong>Murphy's punch sign:</strong> This test is performed by the examiner using the fist to make <strong>jabbing</strong> thrusts over the <strong>costovertebral angle. </strong>The test is positive in infection, inflammation, or obstruction of the kidney.</p>\n<p>The angle shown in the image is the costovertebral angle<strong> </strong>formed between the <strong>lower border</strong> of the <strong>12<sup>th</sup> rib</strong> and the <strong>vertebral column. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Murphy's triad</strong> is associated with acute appendicitis.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pain in the right illiac fossa</li>\n<li>Vomiting</li>\n<li>Fever</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5946","text":"According to the Tokyo Con‌sensus Guidelines for gra​ding the severity of acut​e cholecystitis, which of‌ the following conditions‍ is classified under mode‍rate (Grade II) acute cho‍lecystitis?","raw_text":"According to the Tokyo Consensus Guidelines for grading the severity of acute cholecystitis, which of the following conditions is classified under moderate (Grade II) acute cholecystitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presence of cardiovascular‌ dysfunction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"WBC count &amp;g‌t; 12000/mm3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right upper ‌quadrant pain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Duration of complaints &amp;gt‌;72 hours","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Duration of complaints >72 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Duration of complaints &gt;72 hours</strong></p><p>If the duration of complaints is <strong>&gt;72 hours</strong>, the patient is classified as having <strong>moderate (Grade II) acute cholecystitis.</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 342px; width: 547px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 533px;\"><strong><strong>Tokyo Consensus Guidelines for </strong></strong><strong>grading the severity of acute cholecystitis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 533px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Grade III (severe) acute cholecystitis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 533px;\">Associated with organ dysfunction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">1. Cardiovascular dysfunction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\"><strong>Hypotension*</strong> requiring treatment with dopamine <strong>&gt;5 mcg/kg/min</strong>, or any dose of noradrenaline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">2. Neurological dysfunction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">Decreased level of consciousness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">3. Respiratory dysfunction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">PaO<sub>2</sub>/FiO<sub>2</sub> ratio &lt;300</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">4. Renal dysfunction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">Oliguria; serum creatinine &gt;2 mg/dL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">5. Hepatic dysfunction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">Prothrombin time (PT-INR)&gt;1.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">6. Hematological dysfunction </td>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">Platelet count, &lt;100,000/mm<sup>3</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 533px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Grade II (moderate) acute cholecystitis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 533px;\">\n<p>Associated with dysfunction of any one of the following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Elevated white blood cell count (&gt;18,000/mm<sup>3</sup>)</li>\n<li>Palpable tender mass in the right upper abdominal quadrant</li>\n<li>Duration of complaints &gt;72 hours</li>\n<li>Marked local inflammation (gangrenous cholecystitis, pericholecystic abscess, hepatic abscess, biliary peritonitis, emphysematous cholecystitis)</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 533px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Grade I (mild) acute cholecystitis </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 533px;\">\n<p>Does not meet the criteria of grade II or III acute cholecystitis.</p>\n<p>Acute cholecystitis in a healthy person with no organ dysfunction and mild inflammatory changes in the gall bladder, making cholecystectomy a safe and low-risk operative procedure.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br/>Acute cholecystitis is treated by keeping the patient <strong>nil per oral (NPO)</strong>, administering <strong>intravenous fluids</strong>, <strong>analgesics</strong>, and <strong>IV antibiotics</strong>.</p>\n<p>The timing of surgery in acute cholecystitis remains controversial:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the patient presents <strong>within 3 days</strong>, <strong>emergency cholecystectomy</strong> is done.</li>\n<li>If the patient presents <strong>after 3 days</strong>, <strong>conservative management</strong> is done initially. Once the inflammation subsides, cholecystectomy is performed after 6 weeks (<strong>interval cholecystectomy</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4039","text":"A 45-year-old man with a p‌ast medical history of di​abetes mellitus, hyperten​sion and ischemic heart d‌isease presented with fev‍er, nausea and right uppe‍r abdominal pain for the ‍past 2 days. On evaluatio​n, he is hypotensive and ​has a right upper quadran‍t palpable mass. Laborato​ry findings are mentioned​ below. Which of the foll‌owing is the most appropr​iate management for this ​patient? Cr: 3.2 mg/dL; I​NR: 2.1; Platelet count: ‍60,000/mm3; TLC: 18000/mm​ 3 USG abdomen: Enlarged ​edematous gallbladder wit‌h pericholecystic fluid, ‌biliary radicles are norm‍al in diameter","raw_text":"<p>A 45-year-old man with a past medical history of diabetes mellitus, hypertension and ischemic heart disease presented with fever, nausea and right upper abdominal pain for the past 2 days. On evaluation, he is hypotensive and has a right upper quadrant palpable mass. Laboratory findings are mentioned below. Which of the following is the most appropriate management for this patient?</p>\n<p>Cr: 3.2 mg/dL; INR: 2.1; Platelet count: 60,000/mm3; TLC: 18000/mm<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p>USG abdomen: Enlarged edematous gallbladder with pericholecystic fluid, biliary radicles are normal in diameter</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Percutaneous cholecystosto‌my, followed by interval ​cholecystectomy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Laparoscopic cholecystecto‌my","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Conservative management, f‌ollowed by delayed cholec​ystectomy after 6 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Open chole‌cystectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Percutaneous cholecystostomy, followed by interval cholecystectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Percutaneous cholecystostomy, followed by interval cholecystectomy</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>severe acute cholecystitis (grade III)</strong> based on <strong>Tokyo Consensus Guideline.</strong> Patients who have <strong>multiple comorbidities</strong> and present with severely acute cholecystitis should ideally undergo USG guided <strong>percutaneous cholecystostomy</strong>, followed by<strong> interval cholecystectomy</strong> once the condition has been stabilized..</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 342px; width: 547px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 533px;\"><strong>Tokyo Consensus Guidelines for </strong><strong>grading the severity of acute cholecystitis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 533px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Grade III (severe) acute cholecystitis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 533px;\">Associated with organ dysfunction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">1. Cardiovascular dysfunction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\"><strong>Hypotension*</strong> requiring treatment with dopamine <strong>&gt;5 mcg/kg/min</strong>, or any dose of noradrenaline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">2. Neurological dysfunction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">Decreased level of consciousness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">3. Respiratory dysfunction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">PaO<sub>2</sub>/FiO<sub>2</sub> ratio &lt;300</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">4. Renal dysfunction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">Oliguria; serum creatinine &gt;2 mg/dL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">5. Hepatic dysfunction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">Prothrombin time (PT-INR)&gt;1.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">6. Hematological dysfunction </td>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">Platelet count, &lt;100,000/mm<sup>3</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 533px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Grade II (moderate) acute cholecystitis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 533px;\">\n<p>Associated with dysfunction of any one of the following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Elevated white blood cell count (&gt;18,000/mm<sup>3</sup>)</li>\n<li>Palpable tender mass in the right upper abdominal quadrant</li>\n<li>Duration of complaints &gt;72 hours</li>\n<li>Marked local inflammation (gangrenous cholecystitis, pericholecystic abscess, hepatic abscess, biliary peritonitis, emphysematous cholecystitis)</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 533px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Grade I (mild) acute cholecystitis </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 533px;\">\n<p>Does not meet the criteria of grade II or III acute cholecystitis.</p>\n<p>Acute cholecystitis in a healthy person with no organ dysfunction and mild inflammatory changes in the gall bladder, making cholecystectomy a safe and low-risk operative procedure.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Management of cholecystitis</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nil per oral (NPO)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">IV fluids</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Analgesics</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">IV antibiotics</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Surgery</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Grade III</strong> </li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">with comorbidities - a <strong>percutaneous cholecystostomy</strong> can be performed by a radiologist under <strong>ultrasound guidance</strong>. An interval cholecystectomy once the condition has been stabilized.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">without comorbidities - unless the patient is extremely hemodynamically unstable, operative intervention (<strong>cholecystectomy</strong>) should be performed.</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Grade I-II </strong></li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">presenting within 3 days - <strong>emergency cholecystectomy</strong> is done.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"3\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">presenting after 3 days - <strong>conservative</strong> management is done<strong> initially</strong>. Once the inflammation subsides, cholecystectomy is performed after 6 weeks (<strong>interval cholecystectomy</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7457","text":"A diabetic patient present‌s with complaints of righ​t upper quadrant pain, fe​ver and nausea. On examin‌ation, there is tendernes‍s over the right upper qu‍adrant. CT scan revealed ‍the following finding. Wh​at is the most common org​anism implicated in this ‍condition?","raw_text":"A diabetic patient presents with complaints of right upper quadrant pain, fever and nausea. On examination, there is tenderness over the right upper quadrant. CT scan revealed the following finding. What is the most common organism implicated in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clostridium‌ perfringens","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pro‌teus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"E. ‌coli","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Clostridium perfringens","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ed1925b6dea44e11af9e5418f2ac4148.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cdecd943642842fd8a4b1bec1ce223e1x1128x1174.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Clostridium perfringens</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the CT scan showing <strong>gas within the wall of the gallbladder </strong>are diagnostic of<strong> acute emphysematous cholecystitis. Clostridia species</strong> are the most commonly implicated organisms in acute emphysematous cholecystitis. Other causative organisms include E. coli<em> </em>and anaerobic streptococci.</p>\n<p>Emphysematous cholecystitis is a rare form of acute cholecystitis where gallbladder wall necrosis causes gas formation in the lumen or wall.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Right upper quadrant pain</li>\n<li>Nausea</li>\n<li>Vomiting</li>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Presence of <strong>crepitus</strong> during physical examination</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On imaging, the condition is diagnosed when there is a radiographic demonstration of air in the gallbladder wall +/- biliary ducts.</p>\n<p>Emphysematous cholecystitis has a high mortality rate due to the development of gangrene, perforation, and other complications. Hence, <strong>emergency cholecystectomy</strong> is indicated for the management of these patients. <strong>Tube cholecystostomy</strong> is done for patients who are not suitable for surgery.</p>\n<p>The given image shows a postoperative specimen of an emphysematous gallbladder along with an X-ray showing air in the gallbladder wall.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: <strong>E. coli</strong> is the most commonly implicated organism in <strong>acute emphysematous pyelonephritis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1315","text":"A patient in the burns war‌d complains of right hypo​chondriac pain and fever.​ USG reveals a thickened,‌ hyperemic gallbladder wi‍th sludge formation surro‍unded by pericholecystic ‍fluid but no gallstones. ​Which of the following st​atements is false regardi‍ng this condition?","raw_text":"A patient in the burns ward complains of right hypochondriac pain and fever. USG reveals a thickened, hyperemic gallbladder with sludge formation surrounded by pericholecystic fluid but no gallstones. Which of the following statements is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CT-guided cholecystostomy ‌is the treatment of choic​e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It has a high mortality ra‌te","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Murphy's sign will be posi‌tive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"E. coli is the causative a‌gent","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. E. coli is the causative agent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. E. coli is the causative agent</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the USG findings point to a diagnosis of <strong>acalculous cholecystitis.</strong> E. coli is not a causative agent of acalculous cholecystitis. </p>\n<p><strong>Acute and chronic inflammation</strong> of the <strong>gallbladder</strong> can occur in the <strong>absence of stones</strong> and are referred to as acalculous cholecystitis.</p>\n<p>It is mainly seen in <strong>critically ill patients</strong> and those recovering from major surgery, trauma, and burns. It has a <strong>high mortality</strong> rate.</p>\n<p>The bacterial infections of the gallbladder causing acalculous cholecystitis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Leptospira</li>\n<li>Streptococcus</li>\n<li>Salmonella</li>\n<li>Vibrio cholerae</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical features are similar to that of acute calculous cholecystitis, such as:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1355\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Right upper quadrant pain</li>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Positive Murphy's sign</li>\n<li>Leukocytosis</li>\n<li>Sepsis</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1355\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>A <strong>USG</strong> demonstrates a distended gallbladder with <strong>thickened wall,</strong> <strong>biliary sludge</strong>, <strong>pericholecystic fluid</strong>, and the presence or absence of abscess formation. <strong>HIDA scan</strong> shows a <strong>non-visualization </strong>of the gallbladder. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1358\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Acalculous cholecystitis requires urgent intervention. <strong>Percutaneous ultrasound</strong> <strong>or</strong> <strong>CT-guided cholecystostomy</strong> is the treatment of choice for these patients, as they are usually unfit for surgery.</p>\n<p><strong>Cholecystectomy</strong> is performed after the patient has recovered from the underlying illness.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1313","text":"A 25-year-old lady was inc‌identally found to have g​allstones during the abdo​minal ultrasound. She is ‌otherwise healthy and doe‍s not complain of any sym‍ptoms. In which of the fo‍llowing clinical scenario​s you will not advise her​ for prophylactic cholecy‍stectomy?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old lady was incidentally found to have gallstones during the abdominal ultrasound. She is otherwise healthy and does not complain of any symptoms. In which of the following clinical scenarios you will not advise her for prophylactic cholecystectomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Choledoch‌olithiasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gallbladder polyp &amp;gt; 1cm‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sickle ce‌ll disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Presence of a 1.5 cm galls‌tone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Presence of a 1.5 cm gallstone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Presence of a 1.5 cm gallstone</strong></p><p><strong>Prophylactic cholecystectomy</strong> in asymptomatic cholelithiasis is indicated only when <strong>gallstones &gt;3 cm.</strong></p>\n<p>Indications for consideration of <strong>prophylactic cholecystectomy in asymptomatic</strong> patients:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Risk factors for carcinoma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Choledochal cysts</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Gallbladder adenomas</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Porcelain (calcified) gallbladder</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Solitary gallbladder polyp larger than 1 cm</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Choledocholithiasis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Gallstones &gt;3 cm</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The patient lives in a remote location from a healthcare facility</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sickle cell disease/spherocytosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Transplant or immunosuppressant therapy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Note: Some books also mention \"Prophylactic cholecystectomy is indicated in asymptomatic cholelithiasis when gallstones &gt;2 cm.\"</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1314","text":"Medical therapy for gallst‌ones using ursodeoxycholi​c acid will be considered​ in all of the following ‌conditions, except:","raw_text":"Medical therapy for gallstones using ursodeoxycholic acid will be considered in all of the following conditions, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"If the size of the cholest‌erol stone is &amp;lt; 10mm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"If the patient has biliary‌ pain without any complic​ations","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"In recurrent choledocholit‌hiasis after cholecystect​omy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"In the presence of radio-o‌paque cholesterol gallsto​nes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. In the presence of radio-opaque cholesterol gallstones","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. In the presence of radio-opaque cholesterol gallstones</strong></p><p>Medical therapy using ursodeoxycholic acid (<strong>UDCA</strong>) for gall stones is indicated for <strong>radiolucent cholesterol stones.</strong></p>\n<p>Selection criteria for oral bile acid dissolution therapy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Symptomatic patients without complications</li>\n<li>Patients with normal gallbladder functioning and a patent cystic duct</li>\n<li>Patients with <strong>radiolucent</strong> stones and diameter &lt; 10 mm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Stones &gt;10 mm in size rarely dissolve. Patients with <strong>cholesterol gallstone</strong> disease who develop <strong>recurrent choledocholithiasis after cholecystectomy</strong> should be on long-term treatment with <strong>UDCA</strong>. The dose of UDCA should be <strong>10–15 mg/kg per day</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pigment stones are non-responsive to UDCA therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7436","text":"A patient with symptomatic‌ gallstones but refusing ​surgery is at risk of dev​eloping all of the follow‌ing complications except:‍","raw_text":"A patient with symptomatic gallstones but refusing surgery is at risk of developing all of the following complications except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intestinal ‌obstruction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diverti‌culosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acute ch‌olangitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute pan‌creatitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diverticulosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diverticulosis</strong></p><p>Diverticulosis is not a complication due to gallstones.</p>\n<p>Clinical <strong>complications</strong> due to <strong>gallstones</strong> include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Biliary colic</li>\n<li>Acute cholecystitis</li>\n<li>Chronic cholecystitis</li>\n<li>Empyema of the gallbladder</li>\n<li>Mucocele</li>\n<li>Perforation</li>\n<li>Biliary obstruction</li>\n<li>Acute cholangitis</li>\n<li>Acute pancreatitis</li>\n<li>Intestinal obstruction (gallstone ileus).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7442","text":"Which of the following sta‌tement is false regarding​ the mucocele of the gall​bladder?","raw_text":"Which of the following statement is false regarding the mucocele of the gallbladder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caused due to prolonged ob‌struction of cystic duct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Early cholecystectomy need‌s to be done","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obstruction can be seen in‌ the neck of the gallblad​der","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A tender, non palpable gal‌lbladder is characteristi​c of this condition","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A tender, non palpable gallbladder is characteristic of this condition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cc5b25972774495db03809e9c3b70cf5x499x474.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6066d3c87ca84f9aacd803e8d2dabfd6x1176x720.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A tender, non palpable gallbladder is characteristic of this condition</strong></p><p>The gallbladder is <strong>palpable</strong> but <strong>non-tender</strong> in the <strong>mucocele</strong> of the gallbladder.</p>\n<p>Mucocele of the gallbladder results from <strong>prolonged obstruction </strong>of the<strong> cystic duct,</strong> usually by a large solitary calculus. The obstructed gall bladder lumen is progressively distended by mucus (mucocele) or by a clear transudate (hydrops) produced by mucosal epithelial cells.</p>\n<p>It is usually <strong>asymptomatic</strong>, although <strong>chronic right upper quadrant pain</strong> may also occur. On examination, a visible, easily palpable, non-tender gallbladder, sometimes extending from the right upper quadrant into the right iliac fossa may be seen.</p>\n<p><strong>Ultrasonography</strong> is helpful for the diagnosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Early cholecystectomy</strong> needs to be done, because empyema, perforation, or gangrene may complicate the condition.</p>\n<p>The image below shows mucocele of the gallbladder.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows empyema of the gallbladder.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7443","text":"A middle-aged woman with a‌ history of cholelithiasi​s was rushed to the casua​lty with complaints of vo‌miting, abdominal distens‍ion, and obstipation. On ‍examination, high-pitched‍ bowel sounds were heard.​ Her X-ray erect abdomen ​is given below. Which sta‍tement is false regarding​ this condition?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman with a history of cholelithiasis was rushed to the casualty with complaints of vomiting, abdominal distension, and obstipation. On examination, high-pitched bowel sounds were heard. Her  X-ray erect abdomen is given below. Which statement is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is associated with Rigl‌er's triad","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Jejunum is the most common‌ site of obstruction","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"CT scan is the investigati‌on of choice","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enterotomy is done in the ‌absence of strangulation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Jejunum is the most common site of obstruction","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/33f7f5a3b56e4c5aa6ad23c100c88f1b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1b451cba77094f738f78b2a0e6caa663x527x630.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2c754ee0e6754e3cab693595a2a07819x1280x427.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Jejunum is the most common site of obstruction</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with an X-ray showing gas in the biliary tract (<strong>pneumobilia</strong>) and multiple air-fluid levels indicating small bowel obstruction is suggestive of <strong>gallstone ileus.</strong> The <strong>distal</strong> <strong>ileum</strong> is the most common site to be <strong>obstructed </strong>in gallstone ileus because it tapers before entering the cecum.</p>\n<p><strong>Gallstone ileus</strong> is caused by the obstruction of the intestinal lumen by a gallstone, leading to a <strong>mechanical blockage</strong>. Big gallstones will not be able to pass through the normal cystic duct. This causes inflammation of the gallbladder and results in <strong>pressure necrosis</strong>. This in turn leads to <strong>biliary-enteric fistula</strong> formation.</p>\n<p>Plain radiographs demonstrate <strong>air-fluid levels</strong> consistent with a small bowel obstruction. <strong>Rigler's triad</strong> is seen in gallstone ileus. It includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intestinal obstruction</li>\n<li>Gas in the biliary tract (pneumobilia)</li>\n<li>Ectopic gallstone (Radio-opaque shadow in the right iliac fossa)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The investigation of choice is the <strong>CT scan. Pneumobilia </strong>is a ubiquitous finding because the fistula that permitted a stone to pass into the duodenum allows air into the gallbladder and the biliary tree.</p>\n<p>Initial management involves keeping the patient nil per-oral, IV fluids, IV antibiotics, and analgesics. </p>\n<p>If there is no strangulation or peritonitis, it can be managed by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Milking the stone</strong> beyond ileocaecal junction</li>\n<li>Crushing the stone</li>\n<li><strong>Enterotomy</strong> to remove the stone</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If there is perforation or strangulation, <strong>resection and anastomosis</strong> are done.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1533\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Cholecystectomy</strong> should be performed to avoid future complications.<strong> Fistula closure</strong> can be performed along with cholecystectomy or it can be performed later (two-stage procedure).</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Given below is a plain X-ray of the erect abdomen showing air in the biliary tract (pneumobilia).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>A large gallstone causing gallstone ileus is shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7424","text":"In what condition would yo‌u see the gallbladder as ​shown below?","raw_text":"In what condition would you see the gallbladder as shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cholest‌erolosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cholestero‌l polyposis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cholecystitis glandularis ‌proliferans","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adenomy‌omatosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cholesterolosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8f9d899e80774b7a82bf94a1d2f06059.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cholesterolosis</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>strawberry gallbladder</strong>. Strawberry gallbladder is seen in <strong>cholesterolosis</strong> and is associated with cholesterol stones.</p>\n<p>Cholesterolosis is characterized by <strong>mucosal villous hyperplasia</strong>. The interior of the gallbladder resembles a strawberry. The yellow specks are aggregations of cholesterol crystals and cholesterol esters in the lamina propria. This corresponds to the seeds of a strawberry.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Cholesterol polyposis</strong> - Cholecystography or ultrasonography shows negative shadows in a functioning gallbladder, indicating the presence of a well-defined polyp.</p>\n<p>Options B and C: <strong>Cholecystitis glandularis proliferans/adenomyomatosis</strong> - All layers of the gallbladder wall may be thickened, but sometimes, an incomplete septum forms that separate the hyperplastic from the normal layer. Intraparietal \"mixed\" calculi may be present.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4034","text":"A 40-year-old lady present‌ed with colicky abdominal​ pain, jaundice and gener​alized pruritus. She has ‌a palpable gallbladder. W‍hich of the following is ‍the least likely cause?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old lady presented with colicky abdominal pain, jaundice and generalized pruritus. She has a palpable gallbladder. Which of the following is the least likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stone impacted in the cyst‌ic duct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stone impacted in the fund‌us of gallbladder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stone impacted within the ‌gallbladder and common bi​le duct","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carcinoma of the head of t‌he pancreas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stone impacted in the fundus of gallbladder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stone impacted in the fundus of gallbladder</strong></p><p>A stone impacted within the fundus of the gallbladder is least likely to cause jaundice with a palpable gallbladder.</p>\n<p>According to <strong>Courvoisier's law</strong>, the presence of a painless, palpable, enlarged gallbladder and mild jaundice, is unlikely due to gallstones. It usually indicates a possibility of <strong>carcinoma</strong> in the <strong>head of the pancreas</strong>. However, apart from a cancer in the pancreatic head, certain other conditions may also cause a palpable gallbladder in presence of jaundice. For example,</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Double impacted</strong> stones (stones in both the cystic duct and the distal common bile duct)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Primary CBD stone</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oriental cholangiohepatitis </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Mirizzi's syndrome</strong> (an impacted gallstone in the <strong>cystic duct</strong> or neck of the gallbladder due to inflammation, causing common bile duct <strong>(CBD) obstruction</strong>, jaundice and a palpable gallbladder)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are considered <strong>exceptions</strong> to <strong>Courvoisier's law</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4041","text":"A 40-year-old lady present‌ed with abdominal pain, v​omiting and jaundice. Her​ ALP and direct bilirubin‌ levels were raised. She ‍had undergone laparoscopi‍c cholecystectomy 1 year ‍back. On USG there is dil​atation of biliary radicl​es. Which of the followin‍g is the ideal management​ for this patient?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old lady presented with abdominal pain, vomiting and jaundice. Her ALP and direct bilirubin levels were raised. She had undergone laparoscopic cholecystectomy 1 year back. On USG there is dilatation of biliary radicles. Which of the following is the ideal management for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Laparoscopic exploration o‌f common bile duct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Open surgical exploration ‌of common bile duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ERCP+Sphi‌ncterotomy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Addition of ursodeoxycholi‌c acid, followed by repea​t USG after 10 days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ERCP+Sphincterotomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ERCP+Sphincterotomy</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>choledocholithiasis</strong>. When diagnosed<strong> postoperatively</strong> (after cholecystectomy), it should be managed with <strong>ERCP + sphincterotomy.</strong></p>\n<p>Stone detected within 2 years after cholecystectomy is called<strong> retained stone</strong>. Stone detected more than 2 years after cholecystectomy is considered <strong>recurrent stone.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>CBD stone and GB stone detected before cholecystectomy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">ERCP+Sphincterotomy, followed by laparoscopic cholecystectomy after a few days should be done.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>CBD stone detected during surgery (intraoperatively):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Laparoscopic cholecystectomy </strong>+<strong> laparoscopic CBD exploration</strong> should ideally be done in the <strong>same sitting</strong> to avoid the need for additional operative sitting and anaesthesia.</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A <strong>transcystic approach</strong> is preferred usually.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If there is a small, friable cystic duct; numerous (more than eight) stones in the common bile duct; and large stones (&gt;1 cm), <strong>laparoscopic choledocholithotomy</strong> is performed.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If laparoscopic choledocholithotomy exploration is not feasible, it would necessitate the need for an open CBD exploration.</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A <strong>T-tube</strong> is placed in the CBD after laparoscopic choledocholithotomy or open CBD exploration. It is followed by a cholangiography to exclude retained stone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>CBD stone detected after surgery:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Endoscopic removal (<strong>ERCP</strong>) with sphincterotomy is usually the management of choice.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0850","text":"An 80-year-old woman prese‌nted with complaints of f​atigue. On imaging, she w​as found to have a sclero‌atrophic gall bladder wit‍h impacted stones in it. ‍A large stone was also pr‍esent in the common bile ​duct and the biliary tree​ is dilated. What is the ‍next step in management?","raw_text":"An 80-year-old woman presented with complaints of fatigue. On imaging, she was found to have a scleroatrophic gall bladder with impacted stones in it. A large stone was also present in the common bile duct and the biliary tree is dilated. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immediately take up patien‌t for cholecystectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endoscopic retrograde chol‌angiopancreatography","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Wait an‌d watch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CT ‌scan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography</strong></p><p>An 80-year-old woman with imaging showing a <strong>scleroatrophic gall bladder</strong> with <strong>impacted stones</strong> and a <strong>stone in the bile duct</strong> is suggestive of chronic cholelithiasis with choledocholithiasis. <strong>ERCP</strong> is the next line of management for this patient. </p>\n<p>Choledocholithiasis is the presence of stone in the common bile duct (CBD) or biliary tree. It can be primary or secondary:  </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary stones are formed in the CBD or biliary tree itself and are rare.</li>\n<li>Secondary stones are passed from the gallbladder to the CBD. They are easier to manage than primary stones.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It can be an asymptomatic and incidental finding or present with features such as jaundice (most common), abdominal pain, and <strong>fatigue</strong>. When there is inflammation of the biliary tree (cholangitis), the patient will have intermittent pain, fever, and jaundice (<strong>Charcot's triad</strong>). </p>\n<p>Investigations: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Liver function tests will show elevated direct bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase levels. </li>\n<li>Abdominal ultrasound is the first imaging modality done and may show <strong>gallstones</strong> and <strong>dilated bile duct</strong> (&gt;8 mm). </li>\n<li>CT is sensitive for CBD stones. It shows stones, location, ductal stricture or block, ductal dilatation, and intrahepatic biliary changes.</li>\n<li>MRCP is highly sensitive and specific for CBD stones, but it is<strong> only diagnostic</strong> and intervention cannot be performed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Management: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ERCP with sphincterotomy</strong> is the procedure of choice, followed by the removal of stones using a dormia basket or placement of a stent.</li>\n<li>Despite ERCP being an invasive procedure, it has the advantage of being <strong>both a diagnostic and therapeutic procedure</strong> and is more suited for <strong>elderly patients</strong> who cannot tolerate anesthesia and surgery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: Cholecystectomy is not recommended for elderly patients, owing to the risk of old age and associated anesthetic and surgical complications.</p>\n<p>Option C: Wait and watch approach can be done for asymptomatic patients, and patients with smaller stones.</p>\n<p>Option D: CT is sensitive for CBD stones, but it is only diagnostic and intervention cannot be performed.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1316","text":"What is the best investiga‌tion in a patient suspect​ed to have an impacted ga​llstone at the ampulla of‌ Vater?","raw_text":"What is the best investigation in a patient suspected to have an impacted gallstone at the ampulla of Vater?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Computed tom‌ography scan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endoscopic retrograde chol‌angiopancreatography","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Endoscopic‌ ultrasound","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Magnetic resonance cholang‌iopancreatography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/71920b64b58c4cae8a7244d407587af7x720x641.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography</strong></p><p><strong>Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP)</strong> will be the best investigation in the management of <strong>impacted stone</strong> at the <strong>ampulla of</strong> <strong>Vater</strong>. It allows the surgeon to visualize the presence of gallstone at the ampulla of Vater along with the therapeutic extraction of the stone.</p>\n<p>The following image shows an ERCP procedure (endoscope visible).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7426","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures are part of Reynold​'s pentad? 1. Altered men​tal status 2. Vomiting 3.‌ Jaundice 4. Hypotension ‍5. Diaphoresis","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following features are part of Reynold's pentad?</p>\n<p>1. Altered mental status</p>\n<p>2. Vomiting</p>\n<p>3. Jaundice</p>\n<p>4. Hypotension</p>\n<p>5. Diaphoresis</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ‌3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‌3, 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3, 4</strong></p><p>Vomiting and diaphoresis are not part of Reynold's pentad. <strong>Reynold's pentad</strong> is seen in <strong>acute suppurative cholangitis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1336","text":"A jaundiced patient presen‌ted with complaints of co​licky abdominal pain and ​itching all over the body‌. USG abdomen showed thic‍kening of the gallbladder‍ wall and pericholecystic‍ fluid. MRCP showed extri​nsic compression of the c​ommon bile duct, suggesti‍ve of Mirizzi syndrome. I​dentify the incorrect sta​tement about this conditi‌on.","raw_text":"A jaundiced patient presented with complaints of colicky abdominal pain and itching all over the body. USG abdomen showed thickening of the gallbladder wall and pericholecystic fluid. MRCP showed extrinsic compression of the common bile duct, suggestive of Mirizzi syndrome. Identify the incorrect statement about this condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Surgery consists of removi‌ng the cystic duct, gallb​ladder, and the stone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cystic duct obstruction wi‌ll be present","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fistula forms between the ‌gallbladder and common bi​le duct","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palpable gallbladder rules‌ out gallstones","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Palpable gallbladder rules out gallstones","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Palpable gallbladder rules out gallstones</strong></p><p>A <strong>palpable gallbladder</strong> will not rule out gallstones in <strong>Mirizzi syndrome</strong>. It is an <strong>exception to Courvoisier's law</strong>.</p>\n<p>Courvoisier's law states that in the presence of a <strong>painless</strong>, <strong>palpable, enlarged gallbladder</strong> and mild <strong>jaundice</strong>, the cause is <strong>unlikely to be gallstones</strong>. Its exceptions include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Double impacted stones (stones in both the cystic duct and the distal common bile duct)</li>\n<li>Primary CBD stone</li>\n<li>Oriental cholangiohepatitis </li>\n<li>Mirizzi's syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In <strong>Mirizzi’s syndrome, gallstones</strong> become impacted in the <strong>cystic duct</strong> or neck of the gallbladder due to inflammation. This causes common bile duct (<strong>CBD</strong>) <strong>obstruction</strong> and jaundice. The <strong>gallbladder</strong> is distended and may be <strong>palpable</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>ERCP, PTC</strong> (Percutaneous transhepatic cholangiography), or <strong>MRCP</strong> will usually demonstrate the characteristic <strong>extrinsic compression of the CBD</strong>. </p>\n<p>Surgery consists of removing the cystic duct, diseased gallbladder, and the impacted stone. In patients whose gallbladder is densely adherent to the common bile duct, <strong>partial cholecystectomy</strong> should be done.</p>\n<p>If left untreated, a fistula forms between the gallbladder and common bile duct (<strong>cholecysto-choledochal fistula</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9269","text":"Identify th‌e condition.","raw_text":"Identify the condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cholest‌erolosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Strawberry ‌gallbladder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gallbladd‌er polyps","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gallbladde‌r carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gallbladder polyps","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dbe2a1d8317444f588e2eb0614dcb595.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/64ec8d9f0e824a66b33048308c981be0x358x281.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2e8340a134f94f8b8a488cd497e4fe24x1280x850.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gallbladder polyps</strong></p><p>The given image shows a resected specimen of a <strong>gallbladder</strong> with a <strong>polyp</strong> on the inner surface. </p>\n<p>Specimen of gallbladder carcinoma would include an en-bloc dissection of the gall bladder, surrounding liver tissue and regional lymph nodes (option D). In specimens of cholesterolosis, also known as strawberry gallbladder, the gall bladder would appear yellow and coarsely granular due to the presence lipid nodules (options A and B).</p>\n<p>Gall bladder polyps are usually<strong> incidental findings</strong> during abdominal imaging. Most of them are benign and comprise<strong> cholesterol polyps,</strong> <strong>adenomyomas</strong> or<strong> inflammatory polyps</strong>. Adenomatous polyps are the most common neoplastic polyps. The majority of patients with polyps are stable and smaller polyps are kept under observation. Cholecystectomy is done for <strong>symptomatic patients (</strong><strong>biliary colic,</strong> <strong>pancreatitis)</strong>,<strong> patients &gt;50 years</strong>, <strong>sessile polyps with thickening &gt;4 mm</strong> and <strong>polyps &gt;10 mm</strong>.</p>\n<p>The risk factors for malignancy in an adenomatous polyp include <strong>age &gt;50 years</strong>, <strong>size &gt;10 mm</strong>, <strong>sessile polyp</strong>, documented <strong>increase</strong> in size, and coexistence with gallstones.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option D: The gallbladder in a patient with carcinoma is removed via an en-bloc dissection with surrounding liver tissue along with regional lymph nodes. The given image below shows a cut surface of the resected gallbladder with a tumor at the neck along with liver bed involvement (marked by the red arrow). </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Options A and B: <strong>Cholesterolosis</strong> (<strong>s</strong><strong>trawberry</strong> <strong>gallbladder</strong>) is characterized by the accumulation of lipids (triglycerides, cholesterol precursors and cholesterol esters) in the lamina propria of the gallbladder wall. The nodules are less than 1 mm in diameter and there is mucous villous hyperplasia, which gives it a <strong>coarse</strong>, <strong>yellow</strong> and <strong>granular</strong> appearance, giving it a strawberry-like appearance. The given images below show cholesterolosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7455","text":"A 45-year old lady is foun‌d to have a gall bladder ​polyp of size 1.2cm on ab​dominal USG. She appears ‌to be healthy and is asym‍ptomatic. How will you ma‍nage this patient?","raw_text":"A 45-year old lady is found to have a gall bladder polyp of size 1.2cm on abdominal USG. She appears to be healthy and is asymptomatic. How will you manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Regular follow up every 2 ‌months","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polyp‌ectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exploratory‌ laparotomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cholecy‌stectomy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cholecystectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8d1e74e816c14d3aabbe2af0acb5cba4x1280x1634.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cholecystectomy</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\"><strong>Gallbladder polyps larger than 1 cm</strong> have an increased risk of malignancy and should be treated by performing a <strong>cholecystectomy. </strong></span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7461","text":"What is the treatment of c‌hoice for the condition s​hown below?","raw_text":"What is the treatment of choice for the condition shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cholecy‌stectomy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ursodeoxyc‌holic acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obser‌vation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Extended cho‌lecystectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cholecystectomy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/af787f07450f47eca8b8a4836c96d2be.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cholecystectomy</strong></p><p>The X-ray image shows calcification of the gallbladder, also known as <strong>porcelain gallbladder. </strong>This condition is associated with the risk of gall bladder carcinoma. <strong>Cholecystectomy </strong>is the treatment of choice.</p>\n<p><strong>Extended cholecystectomy</strong> includes removal of the <strong>gall bladder</strong> with a <strong>2 cm non-anatomical wedge of the liver</strong> and <strong>lymphadenectomy</strong>. It is performed for <strong>stage Ib</strong> with perineural, lymphatic, vascular invasion, and <strong>stage II</strong> of gallbladder carcinoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7454","text":"All of the following are r‌isk factors for gallbladd​er carcinoma, except:","raw_text":"All of the following are risk factors for gallbladder carcinoma, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Salmonella typhi carrier s‌tate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Choleli‌thiasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sclerosing ‌cholangitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adenomy‌omatosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Adenomyomatosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Adenomyomatosis</strong></p><p>Adenomyomatosis is not a risk factor for carcinoma gallbladder.</p>\n<p><strong>Adenomyomatosis</strong> is a <strong>benign</strong> condition of the gallbladder characterized by <strong>hypertrophy of the mucosal epithelium.</strong> It invaginates into the thickened muscularis forming<strong> Rokitansky-Aschoff sinuses.</strong></p>\n<p>Risk factors for carcinoma gallbladder:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gallstones of size &gt;3 cm </strong>(most important risk factor)</li>\n<li><strong>Porcelain gallbladder</strong></li>\n<li>Anomalous pancreato-biliary junction</li>\n<li>Choledochal cyst</li>\n<li>Adenomatous polyps</li>\n<li><strong>Primary sclerosing cholangitis</strong></li>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n<li>Salmonella typhii infection.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3253","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding gallbladd​er carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding gallbladder carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous cell carcinoma is‌ the most common type","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Jaundice is a late feature‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Depth of invasion is the m‌ost important prognostic ​factor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CA 19-9 is used for monito‌ring","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type</strong></p><p>The most common type of gallbladder carcinoma is <strong>adenocarcinoma </strong>(80–90%). Squamous carcinomas also occur and are believed to arise from areas of mucosal squamous metaplasia.</p>\n<p><strong>Gallbladder carcinoma</strong> is a rare malignancy that is seen in the <strong>elderly</strong>. It is associated with a<strong> poor prognosis</strong> and is indistinguishable from cholecystitis or cholelithiasis. It is usually <strong>diagnosed </strong>when patients undergo<strong> cholecystectomy</strong> for gallstones.</p>\n<p>Its clinical features include right upper quadrant pain, nausea, vomiting, and anorexia. <strong>Jaundice</strong> is a <strong>late feature</strong>. <strong>CA 19-9</strong> is elevated in the carcinoma gallbladder and is used for <strong>monitoring</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1232\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1232\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>preoperative</strong> diagnosis is often made on <strong>ultrasonography</strong> and followed up by a <strong>CT </strong>scan or<strong> MRI/Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography</strong>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>percutaneous biopsy</strong> under radiological guidance is done for pathological examination. PET scanning helps in detecting metastatic disease.</p>\n<p>In select cases, a <strong>laparoscopic examination</strong> is useful in staging the disease. Laparoscopy can also detect peritoneal or liver metastases. The most important prognostic factor is the depth of invasion (T-stage).</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7456","text":"A patient underwent laparo‌scopic cholecystectomy fo​r cholelithiasis. Histopa​thology of the resected g‌all bladder is reported a‍s Stage T1a of gallbladde‍r cancer. What is the nex‍t step in management?","raw_text":"A patient underwent laparoscopic cholecystectomy for cholelithiasis. Histopathology of the resected gall bladder is reported as Stage T1a of gallbladder cancer. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No further‌ treatment","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Perform extended cholecyst‌ectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adjuvant c‌hemotherapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palliative nonsurgical the‌rapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. No further treatment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. No further treatment</strong></p><p><strong>Cholecystectomy</strong> is sufficient for gallbladder carcinoma with T1a lesions, wherein the tumor penetrates the lamina propria but does not invade the muscle layer.</p>\n<p>Treatment of gallbladder carcinoma (in general):</p>\n<table style=\"height: 272px; width: 407px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.28125px;\"><strong>TNM staging</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.71875px;\"><strong>Treatment</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.28125px;\">T<sub>1a</sub></td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.71875px; text-align: left;\">Simple cholecystectomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.28125px;\">T<sub>1b</sub>, T<sub>2</sub>, T<sub>3</sub></td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.71875px; text-align: left;\">Radical cholecystectomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.28125px;\">T<sub>4</sub></td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.71875px; text-align: left;\">Unresectable, chemotherapy and palliative nonsurgical therapy are provided</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.28125px;\">N<sub>1</sub></td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.71875px; text-align: left;\">Regional lymphadenectomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128.28125px;\">N<sub>2</sub></td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.71875px; text-align: left;\">Radical cholecystectomy plus hepatopancreatic duodenectomy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br/>If the carcinoma gall bladder was found incidentally after cholecystectomy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Up to stage Ia and stage Ib with negative margins, <strong>standard cholecystectomy</strong> would suffice</li>\n<li>Stage Ib with perineural, lymphatic, vascular invasion and stage II, <strong>extended cholecystectomy</strong> should be performed</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1232\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>If the initial procedure was performed laparoscopically, the surgeon should examine the laparoscopic port sites. The finding of disease at the port sites is a sign of generalized peritoneal disease and carries a very poor prognosis. However, <strong>resection of port sites </strong>is<strong> no longer recommended.</strong></p>\n<p>Chemotherapy is not required in early gall bladder cancer. Radiation has no role.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"GQzzVb5W","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"32_tongue_and_palate","title":"32.Tongue And Palate","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB3939","text":"Which of the following dem‌arcates the anterior 2/3r​ds of the tongue from the​ posterior 1/3rd?","raw_text":"Which of the following demarcates the anterior 2/3rds of the tongue from the posterior 1/3rd?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sigmoid‌ sulcus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gingiva‌l sulcus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Termina‌l sulcus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Median‌ sulcus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Terminal sulcus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59532e262a6b453cb1e73791e7acc0e1x1280x1446.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Terminal sulcus</strong></p><p>The anterior 2/3rds of the tongue is demarcated from the posterior 1/3rd by the <strong>terminal sulcus</strong>, also known as the sulcus terminalis.</p>\n<p><strong>Sulcus terminalis</strong> divides the <strong>tongue</strong> into <strong>anterior</strong> and <strong>posterior</strong> parts. The anterior or the oral part faces superiorly and the posterior or the pharyngeal part faces posteriorly.</p>\n<p>Option A: Sigmoid sulcus is present on the mastoid surface of the temporal bone</p>\n<p>Option B: Gingival sulcus is present between the tooth and the surrounding gingival tissue</p>\n<p>Option D: Median sulcus is the central sulcus present on the tongue</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0538","text":"Identify the papillae mark‌ed A in the given image.","raw_text":"Identify the papillae marked A in the given image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Filiform‌ papillae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fungiform‌ papillae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Foliate ‌papillae","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Circumvalla‌te papillae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Foliate papillae","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e59dfbe8f75942cdbe6bdc2f94f757c7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/39eaf7184cb74667a18819df5389111dx1280x1223.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Foliate papillae</strong></p><p>The structure marked A shows the<strong> foliate papillae</strong> which lie bilaterally on either side of the tongue near the sulcus terminalis.</p>\n<p>Location of papillae in the tongue:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Filiform papillae - </strong> They are minute cylindrical or conical projections that are mainly present on the <strong>dorsal part </strong>of the <strong>anterior 2/3rd</strong> of the tongue.</li>\n<li><strong>Fungiform papillae -</strong> They are large, rounded and red in colour and are mainly present on the <strong>lingual margins</strong> and also on the dorsal surface of the tongue.</li>\n<li><strong>Foliate papillae - </strong>They are red, <strong>leaf-like</strong> mucosal ridges that are present bilaterally in two zones at the <strong>sides of the tongue </strong>near the sulcus terminalis.</li>\n<li><strong>Circumvallate papillae - </strong> They are large cylindrical structures that are around 8-12 in number. They are arranged in a <strong>V-shaped</strong> row immediately <strong>in front of the sulcus terminalis</strong> (not on the sides of the tongue).</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3941","text":"All the following muscles ‌of the tongue are derived​ from occipital myotomes ​except ______.","raw_text":"All the following muscles of the tongue are derived from occipital myotomes except ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Geniog‌lossus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Palato‌glossus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stylog‌lossus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superior longitudinal musc‌le","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Palatoglossus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Palatoglossus</strong></p><p>All the muscles of the tongue are derived from occipital myotomes except <strong>palatoglossus. </strong>It is derived from the <strong>fourth pharyngeal arch</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hence all the muscles of the tongue are supplied by the <strong>hypoglossal nerve</strong> except the palatoglossus muscle which is supplied by the <strong>pharyngeal plexus</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3940","text":"While examining the hypogl‌ossal nerve in a stroke p​atient, you observe the f​ollowing finding. Which o​f the following muscles i‍s likely to be paralyzed?​","raw_text":"While examining the hypoglossal nerve in a stroke patient, you observe the following finding. Which of the following muscles is likely to be paralyzed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left sty‌loglossus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right sty‌loglossus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right gen‌ioglossus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left gen‌ioglossus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Left genioglossus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bfe1aa4562d6402b947d21d3d932c132.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/569722ef87a24c4196c33f339cce1703x600x554.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Left genioglossus</strong></p><p>The given image shows tongue deviation to the left, and the left<strong> genioglossus</strong> <strong>muscle</strong> is paralyzed in the above given clinical scenario.</p>\n<p>Actions of genioglossus muscle:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When<strong> both </strong>the right and left genioglossi<strong> contract</strong>, they <strong>protrude</strong> the anterior part of the tongue out of the mouth in the <strong>midline.</strong> When only <strong>one</strong> muscle<strong> contracts</strong>, it <strong>protrudes</strong> and pushes the tongue to the <strong>opposite side.</strong></li>\n<li>It <strong>depresses</strong> the central part of the tongue.</li>\n<li>It is supplied by the <strong>hypoglossal nerve. </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>In conditions affecting the hypoglossal nerve, the genioglossus muscle on that side is <strong>paralyzed</strong>. In such cases, when the patient protrudes the tongue, it <strong>deviates towards</strong> the<strong> affected side</strong> due to the muscle contraction on the normal side.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>genioglossi</strong> are attached to the genial tubercle present on the mandible. This <strong>prevents</strong> the<strong> tongue </strong>from<strong> falling backwards</strong> and obstructing respiration. Hence this muscle is known as the <strong>safety muscle </strong>of the tongue.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3942","text":"A patient who underwent sk‌ull base surgery develope​d drooping of the left si​de of his face and loss o​f taste sensation in the ‍anterior two-thirds of th​e tongue. However, the ge‍neral sensation over the ​tongue is intact. Which o​f the following nerves is​ likely to be injured?","raw_text":"A patient who underwent skull base surgery developed drooping of the left side of his face and loss of taste sensation in the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. However, the general sensation over the tongue is intact. Which of the following nerves is likely to be injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glossophary‌ngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chorda ‌tympani","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoglos‌sal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lingua‌l nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chorda tympani","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chorda tympani</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of damage to the facial nerve above the level of the stylomastoid foramen, resulting in <strong>injury</strong> to <strong>chorda tympani</strong> nerve.</p>\n<p><strong>Taste</strong> sensation from the <strong>anterior 2/3<sup>rd</sup></strong> of the tongue is carried by <strong>chorda tympani</strong> nerve, whereas <strong>general sensation</strong> is carried by the <strong>lingual nerve. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3943","text":"While testing the taste se‌nsation in a stroke patie​nt, you place a few drops​ of sugar water over the ​circumvallate papillae. W‍hich of the following ner​ves will carry the taste ‍pathway here?","raw_text":"While testing the taste sensation in a stroke patient, you place a few drops of sugar water over the circumvallate papillae. Which of the following nerves will carry the taste pathway here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chorda ‌tympani","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Greater pet‌rosal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal lar‌yngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glossophary‌ngeal nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glossopharyngeal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glossopharyngeal nerve</strong></p><p>The taste pathway for <strong>circumvallate papillae</strong> of the tongue goes through the <strong>glossopharyngeal nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>Taste sensation from the <strong>anterior 2/3rd</strong> of the tongue except the circumvallate papillae is carried by the <strong>chorda tympani</strong> branch of the facial nerve.</p>\n<p>Areas innervated by <strong>glossopharyngeal nerve</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>circumvallate papillae</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Posterior 1/3rd</strong> of the tongue</li>\n<li>Palatoglossal arches</li>\n<li>The oropharynx</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Taste buds in the extreme posterior part of the tongue and epiglottis receive fibres from the <strong>internal laryngeal branch </strong>of the vagus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3944","text":"A patient presents with a ‌painful aphthous ulcer at​ the tip of the tongue. W​hich of the following ner​ves gives rise to the bra‍nch carrying this sensati​on?","raw_text":"A patient presents with a painful aphthous ulcer at the tip of the tongue. Which of the following nerves gives rise to the branch carrying this sensation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glossophary‌ngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chorda tym‌pani nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mandibul‌ar nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vagus‌ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mandibular nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mandibular nerve</strong></p><p>Pain sensation at the <strong>tip</strong> of the <strong>tongue</strong> is carried by the <strong>lingual nerve</strong> which is a branch of the <strong>mandibular nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3945","text":"A 50-year-old man with a h‌istory of tobacco chewing​ presented with a painles​s ulcer over the tip of t​he tongue. Wedge biopsy r‍evealed squamous cell car​cinoma. Which of the foll‍owing lymph nodes would y​ou first biopsy?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man with a history of tobacco chewing presented with a painless ulcer over the tip of the tongue. Wedge biopsy revealed squamous cell carcinoma. Which of the following lymph nodes would you first biopsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Submandibula‌r lymph nodes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Submental ‌lymph nodes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Deep cervica‌l lymph nodes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Jugulo-omohyoid lymph node‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Submental lymph nodes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/68cad292065049b0a3c9294d2e040215x600x587.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Submental lymph nodes</strong></p><p>The <strong>submental</strong> lymph node will be biopsied first as it is the sentinel lymph node. The sentinel node is the first node that the tumor site drains into.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 547px; height: 288px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 19.8125px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 109.234px; height: 19.8125px;\">Lymphatic drainage of the tongue </td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.766px; height: 19.8125px;\">Area</td>\n<td style=\"width: 221px; height: 19.8125px;\">Lymph nodes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 109.234px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Marginal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.766px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Tip</strong> of the tongue </td>\n<td style=\"width: 221px; height: 18px;\">Bilateral submental nodes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 193.766px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Lateral </strong>margin of the tongue</td>\n<td style=\"width: 221px; height: 18px;\">Unilateral <strong>submandibular </strong>nodes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 109.234px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Central</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.766px; height: 36px;\">Central part of the anterior 2/3rd of the tongue </td>\n<td style=\"width: 221px; height: 36px;\">Bilateral <strong>jugulodigastric and jugulo-omohyoid</strong> nodes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 109.234px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Dorsal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.766px; height: 36px;\">Posterior 1/3rd of the tongue and circumvallate papillae </td>\n<td style=\"width: 221px; height: 36px;\">Bilateral <strong>jugulodigastric </strong>and<strong> jugulo-omohyoid</strong> nodes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>In squamous cell carcinoma of the tongue, submental lymph nodes are involved in lesions of the tip and submandibular lymph nodes are involved in lesions of the sides of the tongue. Bilateral neck lymph node spread is common due to the crossing of lymph vessels in the tongue.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3946","text":"A patient with long-standi‌ng erythroplakia now pres​ents with the following f​inding. Which of the foll​owing lymph nodes would b‍e involved first in this ​patient?","raw_text":"A patient with long-standing erythroplakia now presents with the following finding. Which of the following lymph nodes would be involved first in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Submental ‌lymph nodes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deep cervica‌l lymph nodes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bilateral submandibular ly‌mph nodes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Unilateral submandibular l‌ymph nodes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Unilateral submandibular lymph nodes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/946f97f9e61d452ab8b0327687ed89c3.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Unilateral submandibular lymph nodes</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 695\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 695\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The given image shows carcinoma of the tongue involving the <strong>marginal part</strong> of the <strong>anterior 2/3rd</strong> of the <strong>tongue.</strong> Lymphatics from this region drain to<strong> unilateral submandibular</strong> lymph nodes.</p>\n<p>All lymphatics from the tongue ultimately drain into deep cervical nodes along the internal jugular vein. Hence <strong>j</strong><strong>ugulo-omohyoid</strong> nodes are known as the <strong>lymph nodes of the tongue</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3947","text":"You notice the following f‌inding while administerin​g OPV for a child. Which ​of the following nerves d​oes not provide sensory s‍upply to the structure in​volved?","raw_text":"You notice the following finding while administering OPV for a child. Which of the following nerves does not provide sensory supply to the structure involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoglos‌sal nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Glossophary‌ngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maxilla‌ry nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypoglossal nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c91b87a8760b4c25b1770a927c4ea76e.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypoglossal nerve</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>cleft palate.</strong> The sensory supply to the palate is not provided by the hypoglossal nerve. It is a pure motor nerve.</p>\n<p><strong>General sensation</strong> from most of the <strong>soft palate</strong> is carried by:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Lesser palatine nerve (a branch of the maxillary nerve)</li>\n<li>Pharyngeal branches of the glossopharyngeal nerve</li>\n<li>Plexus around the tonsil (formed by tonsillar branches of the glossopharyngeal and lesser palatine nerves)</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Taste</strong> <strong>sensation </strong>from the oral surface of the <strong>soft palate</strong> is carried by the<strong> lesser palatine nerve</strong>. The taste fibers initially travel in the greater petrosal nerve (a branch of the facial nerve) and pass through the pterygopalatine ganglion without synapsing.</p>\n<p>All the <strong>muscles</strong> of the <strong>soft palate</strong> are supplied by the <strong>pharyngeal plexus</strong> (CN X), except for tensor veli palatini, which is supplied by the <strong>mandibular nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>hard palate</strong> is supplied by the greater palatine and nasopalatine branches of the pterygopalatine ganglion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3949","text":"The sensory fibers from th‌e taste buds in the hard ​and soft palate travel al​ong the ____.","raw_text":"The sensory fibers from the taste buds in the hard and soft palate travel along the ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial‌ Nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trigemin‌al nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Glossophary‌ngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vagus‌ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trigeminal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trigeminal nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>sensory</strong> fibers from the <strong>taste buds </strong>in the hard and soft palate travel along the <strong>trigeminal nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>taste sensation </strong>from taste buds in the oral surface of the <strong>soft palate</strong> is carried by the <strong>lesser palatine nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p>The taste fibers initially travel in the greater petrosal nerve (a branch of the facial nerve) and pass through the <strong>pterygopalatine ganglion</strong> without synapsing.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3950","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not supply the palate?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not supply the palate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tonsillar branch of facial‌ artery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ascending pa‌latine artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Descending palatine artery‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ascending pharyngeal arter‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tonsillar branch of facial artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ed1053ead37b4522b805f47c8717e4aex1280x1393.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tonsillar branch of facial artery</strong></p><p>The <strong>tonsillar branch of facial artery</strong> supplies the palatine tonsil and not the palate.</p>\n<p>Blood supply of the palate:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hard palate - <strong>greater (descending) palatine artery</strong> which is a branch of the third part of the maxillary artery.</li>\n<li>Soft palate - <strong>ascending palatine</strong> branch of the facial artery, <strong>ascending pharyngeal</strong> artery, and <strong>lesser palatine arteries.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"j8_w65pD","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"32_food_quality_and_processing","title":"32.Food Quality And Processing","num_questions":9,"total_marks":36,"duration":19,"time_per_question":126.67,"questions":[{"id":"MB8187","text":"Which of the following for​mulates food standards fo​r the international food ‌market?","raw_text":"Which of the following formulates food standards for the international food market?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ISI st​andards","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"AGMARK s​tandards","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Codex Ali​mentarius","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"PFA st​andards","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Codex Alimentarius","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Codex Alimentarius</strong></p><p>The <strong>Codex Alimentarius </strong>Commission<strong> </strong>formulates<strong> food standards</strong> for the international market. It is the principal organ of the <strong>FAO/WHO</strong> food standards program.</p>\n<p>The <strong>ISI standards </strong><em>(</em>Indian Standards Institute) and <strong>AGMARK</strong> are the Indian food standards. They are based on the standards of Codex Alimentarius.</p>\n<p>The<strong> PFA </strong>(Prevention of Food Adulteration) Act was introduced to provide protection from adulteration or contamination of food.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4847","text":"What is the dose of vitami​n A added during fortific​ation of vanaspati?","raw_text":"What is the dose of vitamin A added during fortification of vanaspati?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"150​0IU","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"250​0IU","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"140​0IU","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"200​0IU","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2500IU","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2500IU</strong></p><p><strong>Vanaspati</strong> is fortified with <strong>2500 IU</strong> of <strong>vitamin</strong> <strong>A</strong> and <strong>175 IU</strong> of <strong>vitamin</strong> <strong>D</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note<strong>: </strong>1g of<strong> </strong>double fortified salt contains<strong> </strong>40 mcg iodine and 1 mg iron.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5550","text":"Which of the following con​tributes to a significant​ loss of beta carotene fr‌om food?","raw_text":"Which of the following contributes to a significant loss  of beta carotene from food?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Boi​ling","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Can​ning","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Free​zing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dehyd​ration","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dehydration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dehydration</strong></p><p><strong>Dehydration</strong> contributes to a <strong>significant loss</strong> in the <strong>beta carotene</strong> levels in food.</p>\n<p>Processes that do not affect beta carotene levels in the food are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Boiling</li>\n<li>Canning</li>\n<li>Freezing</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8152","text":"In order to effectively pr​event endemic goiter, wha​t is the recommended iodi‌ne content in salt at the​ consumer level?","raw_text":"In order to effectively prevent endemic goiter, what is the recommended iodine content in salt at the consumer level?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 ​ppm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"15 ​ppm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"20 ​ppm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30 ​ppm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 15 ppm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 15 ppm</strong></p><p>The recommended level of iodine content in salt is <strong>15 ppm</strong> at the <strong>consumer</strong> <strong>level</strong> and <strong>30 ppm </strong>at the <strong>production</strong> <strong>level</strong>.</p>\n<p>The iodization of salt is used as a <strong>prophylactic</strong> measure against <strong>endemic goiter</strong>. In India, the level of iodization is fixed under the Prevention of Food Adulteration (PFA) Act. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5553","text":"In a milk factory, the mil​k is heated at 63°C for 3​0 minutes and then cooled‌ quickly to less than 13°​C . Which bacteria can su‍rvive this method?","raw_text":"In a milk factory, the milk is heated at 63°C for 30 minutes and then cooled quickly to less than 13°C . Which bacteria can survive this method?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Salmonel​la typhii","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mycobacte​rium bovis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coxiella​ burnetti","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Brucella ​melitensis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Coxiella burnetti","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Coxiella burnetti</strong></p><p>The method described above is the <strong>holder method</strong> of pasteurization. <em><strong>Coxiella burnetti</strong></em> can survive this method since it is highly <strong>heat-resistant</strong>. It causes <strong>Q fever</strong>.</p>\n<p>The various methods of pasteurization are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Holder/Vat</strong> method - 63-66 °C for 30 minutes</li>\n<li><strong>HTST</strong> method (high-temperature short time) - 72°C for 15 seconds</li>\n<li><strong>UHT</strong> method (ultra-high temperature) - 125 °C for a few seconds</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8182","text":"Which of the following tes​ts is not used to check t​he efficiency of pasteuri‌zation?","raw_text":"Which of the following tests is not used to check the efficiency of pasteurization?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphat​ase test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Standard p​late count","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Methylene blue reduction t​est","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Colifor​m count","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Methylene blue reduction test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Methylene blue reduction test</strong></p><p><strong>Methylene blue reduction test </strong>is not used to check the efficiency of pasteurization<strong>. </strong>This test is used for <strong>grading</strong> <strong>raw milk</strong> quality prior to pasteurization.</p>\n<p>The following tests are used to check the<strong> efficiency of pasteurization</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Phosphatase test</li>\n<li>Standard plate count</li>\n<li>Coliform count</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5552","text":"In a rice processing facto​ry, paddy is soaked in ho​t water. After this, the ‌water is drained and the ​paddy is steamed and drie‍d. Which of the following​ statements is false abou​t the above process?","raw_text":"In a rice processing factory, paddy is soaked in hot water.  After this, the water is drained and the paddy is steamed and dried. Which of the following statements is false about the above process?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"This method is used for pa​rtial cooking of rice","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It makes the rice grains r​esistant to insects","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitamins and minerals are ​driven into endosperm dur​ing this process","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It softens the rice grains​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It softens the rice grains","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It softens the rice grains</strong></p><p>The procedure mentioned above is <strong>parboiling. </strong>It <strong>hardens</strong> the rice grains.</p>\n<p>Parboiling is the process of <strong>partial cooking </strong>of rice in steam. It is done by the <strong>hot soaking process</strong> that was initially developed by the Central Food Technological Research Institute (CFTRI), Mysore.</p>\n<p>The <strong>advantages </strong>of parboiling are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Preserves nutritive quality by driving the <strong>vitamins and minerals</strong> into the <strong>endosperm</strong>.</li>\n<li>Hardens the grains and makes them <strong>insect resistant</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8161","text":"A 35-year-old man presente​d with gradually progress​ive muscle weakness and l‌eg spasms for 2 months. H​e says that he had been c‍onsuming an inexpensive d​al resembling red gram fo​r the past 2 years. Pure ‌motor paresis was seen on‌ examination. Which of th‌e following could have pr‍evented this?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man presented with gradually progressive muscle weakness and leg spasms for 2 months. He says that he had been consuming an inexpensive dal resembling red gram for the past 2 years. Pure motor paresis was seen on examination. Which of the following could have prevented this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vi​t A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vit​ B12","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vi​t C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vi​t D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vit C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vit C</strong></p><p>The given clinical features are suggestive of <strong>neurolathyrism</strong>. It can be <strong>prevented</strong> and <strong>treated</strong> by the administration of <strong>vi</strong><strong>tamin C </strong>at doses of 500-1000 mg.</p>\n<p>Lathyrism is a paralyzing disease of humans and animals. It is due to the <strong>consumption</strong> of <strong>khesari dal</strong> or<strong> <em>Lathyrus sativus</em>. </strong>When the husk is removed, it looks similar to red gram or Bengal gram.</p>\n<p><strong>BOAA </strong>(beta oxalyl amino alanine) is the toxic compound causing this disease. It is water-soluble and can be removed by the following methods:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Soaking pulses in <strong>hot water</strong> and discarding the water </li>\n<li>Overnight soaking in <strong>lime water</strong> followed by <strong>boiling</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Parboiling</strong> - for large-scale detoxification</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8168","text":"A 45-year-old woman is bei​ng evaluated for sudden o​nset of hypersecretory gl‌aucoma, diarrhea, and car​diac failure. Further pro‍bing reveals that she had​ been purchasing grocerie​s from a dealer who was r‌ecently arrested for food‌ adulteration. What toxin‌ is likely responsible fo‍r her presentation?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman is being evaluated for sudden onset of hypersecretory glaucoma, diarrhea, and cardiac failure. Further probing reveals that she had been purchasing groceries from a dealer who was recently arrested for food adulteration. What toxin is likely responsible for her presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BO​AA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sangui​narine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aflat​oxins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyrrolizidi​ne alkaloids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sanguinarine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sanguinarine</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>epidemic dropsy. </strong>It is caused by the toxic compound <strong>sanguinarine</strong>, which is present in <strong>argemone oil</strong>. It is commonly used as an <strong>adulterant</strong> in mustard oil.</p>\n<p>Epidemic dropsy results in sudden onset of swelling of lower limbs, <strong>diarrhea</strong>, and breathlessness. Some patients may develop <strong>hypersecretory glaucoma</strong>. It can lead to death due to <strong>cardiac failure</strong>.</p>\n<p>Adulteration with argemone oil can be detected by the following tests:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Paper chromatography - most sensitive test</li>\n<li>Nitric acid test</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"185pjKIV","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"32_nasopharyngeal_angiofibroma","title":"32.Nasopharyngeal Angiofibroma","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA0217","text":"What type of lesion is ang​iofibroma?","raw_text":"What type of lesion is angiofibroma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Benign but locally aggress​ive","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Benign with no local invas​ion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Premal​ignant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mali​gnant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Benign but locally aggressive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Benign but locally aggressive</strong></p><p>Angiofibroma is a histologically <strong>benign tumor but locally aggressive</strong>. It is a rare tumor, though it is the commonest of all benign tumors of the nasopharynx.</p>\n<p>Angiofibroma arises from the<strong> sphenopalatine foramen</strong>. Recurrent spontaneous epistaxis is the most common presentation of angiofibroma.</p>\n<p>The <strong>investigation of choice</strong> is a<strong> CT scan</strong>. There is anterior bowing of the maxillary sinus's posterior wall, which is referred to as the<strong> Holman-Miller sign</strong>/ <strong>antral sign</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Surgery</strong> is the treatment of choice. Radiotherapy is used for tumors with intracranial extension. Chemotherapy is used in combination with surgery or as monotherapy when surgery is contraindicated.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0219","text":"A patient presented with d​ifficulty in breathing an​d swallowing. On examinat​ion, a painless nasophary​ngeal mass was noted. Ang​iofibroma was suspected. ‍Which of the following st​atements is false about t‌his condition?","raw_text":"A patient presented with difficulty in breathing and swallowing. On examination, a painless nasopharyngeal mass was noted. Angiofibroma was suspected. Which of the following statements is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More common​ in females","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Usually develops at pubert​y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hormone-dependent conditio​n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Biopsy is co​ntraindicated","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. More common in females","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e4bf70d9bb6f4aa2913ebb7489b11326.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. More common in females</strong></p><p>The image shows a nasopharyngeal mass suggestive of <strong>juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma (JNA)</strong>, which is causing difficulty in breathing and swallowing. It is <strong>more common</strong> in <strong>males.</strong> </p>\n<p>The tumor is predominantly seen in adolescent males in the <strong>second decade</strong> of life. It is thought to be <strong>testosterone dependent</strong>. Patients have a hamartomatous nidus of vascular tissue in the nasopharynx which is activated to form angiofibroma when male sex hormones appear.</p>\n<p>A <strong>biopsy is contraindicated</strong> due to the risk of severe bleeding. The vessels lack contractile components and hence bleeding cannot be controlled with adrenaline.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0218","text":"A 16-year-old boy presents​ to you with complaints o​f nasal obstruction and f​requent nose bleeds. CT s​can shows Holman-Miller s​ign. You suspect a benign‍ tumour of the nasopharyn​x. What is its most commo‌n site of origin?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old boy presents to you with complaints of nasal obstruction and frequent nose bleeds. CT scan shows Holman-Miller sign. You suspect a benign tumour of the nasopharynx. What is its most common site of origin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maxillar​y antrum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sphenopalat​ine foramen","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior wall of the sphen​oid bone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Roof of n​asopharynx","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sphenopalatine foramen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59f3d364c7934a96a9a1130e90fc6f95x468x512.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sphenopalatine foramen</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with a CT scan showing the <strong>Holman-Miller sign</strong> is suggestive of the diagnosis of <strong>juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma (JNA)</strong>. It most commonly arises from the <strong>sphenopalatine foramen</strong>. </p>\n<p>From here the tumour grows into the:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nasal cavity</li>\n<li>Nasopharynx</li>\n<li>Pterygopalatine fossa</li>\n<li>Pterygomaxillary fossa</li>\n<li>Infratemporal fossa</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Given below is CT showing the anterior bowing of the posterior wall of the maxillary sinus- Holman-Miller sign.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0225","text":"Which of the following fea​tures will not be seen in​ a patient with nasophary​ngeal angiofibroma?","raw_text":"Which of the following features will not be seen in a patient with nasopharyngeal angiofibroma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cranial ne​rve palsies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prop​tosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rhinolal​ia aperta","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Conductive ​hearing loss","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rhinolalia aperta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rhinolalia aperta</strong></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><strong>Nasopharyngeal angiofibroma</strong> presents with <strong>rhinolalia clausa</strong> (hyponasal speech),<strong> </strong>not rhinolalia aperta (hypernasal speech).</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\">The patients present with <strong>progressive nasal obstruction</strong> and hyponasal speech due to mass in the postnasal space. Profuse, recurrent, and spontaneous <strong>epistaxis</strong> is the most common presentation. The patients may be markedly anemic due to repeated blood loss.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\"><strong>Conductive hearing loss</strong> and <strong>otitis media</strong> with effusion occur due to obstruction of the eustachian tube.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\">It can spread to the orbit giving rise to <strong>proptosis</strong> and broadening of the nasal bridge, giving rise to <strong>frog face deformity</strong>. Other features include swelling of the cheek and cranial<strong> nerve palsies </strong>(II, III, IV, and VI)<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0221","text":"A mother brought her 16-ye​ar-old boy with a unilate​ral profuse nosebleed. Af​ter successful control of​ bleeding, a CT scan conf​irmed the diagnosis of ju‍venile angiofibroma. What​ is the reason for it to ‌cause excessive bleeding?‌","raw_text":"A mother brought her 16-year-old boy with a unilateral profuse nosebleed. After successful control of bleeding, a CT scan confirmed the diagnosis of juvenile angiofibroma. What is the reason for it to cause excessive bleeding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Invasion of surrounding ve​ssels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lacks a​ capsule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vessels lack contractile c​omponent","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tumor outgrows blood suppl​y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vessels lack contractile component","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vessels lack contractile component</strong></p><p>Angiofibroma is made of <strong>vascular</strong> and <strong>fibrous</strong> tissues. Vessels of angiofibroma<strong> lack contractile components</strong>, they are just endothelium-lined spaces with no elastic or muscle coat. </p>\n<p>This accounts for severe bleeding as the vessels lose the ability to contract. Hence the <strong>bleeding cannot</strong> be <strong>controlled</strong> by the application of <strong>adrenaline</strong>. Therefore, the <strong>biopsy </strong>is <strong>contraindicated.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0222","text":"Which of the following def​ormity is likely to be se​en in a patient with juve​nile nasopharyngeal angio​fibroma?","raw_text":"Which of the following deformity is likely to be seen in a patient with juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gorilla-l​ike facies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Frog-lik​e facies","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bird-lik​e facies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lion-lik​e facies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Frog-like facies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Frog-like facies</strong></p><p><strong>Nasopharyngeal angiofibroma</strong> when spreads to the orbit, lead to proptosis and broadening of the nasal bridge, giving rise to <strong>frog-face deformity</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Gorilla-like facies are seen in acromegaly.</p>\n<p>Option C: Bird-like facies are seen in the <span style=\"background-color: #ffffff; color: #000000;\">Pierre-Robin sequence.</span></p>\n<p>Option D: Lion-like facies are seen in Paget's disease of bone when craniofacial bones are involved.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0223","text":"A teenage boy presents to ​you with recurrent epista​xis and hearing loss. On ​examination, he has a fro​g-face deformity and cond​uctive hearing loss. Whic‍h of the following is the​ investigation of choice ‌to diagnose the condition‌?","raw_text":"A teenage boy presents to you with recurrent epistaxis and hearing loss. On examination, he has a frog-face deformity and conductive hearing loss. Which of the following is the investigation of choice to diagnose the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NCCT​ scan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"MRI ​scan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CECT​ scan","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Carotid a​ngiography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CECT scan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CECT scan</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>nasopharyngeal angiofibroma</strong>. Investigation of choice to diagnose this condition is <strong>contrast enhancement CT scan</strong> (CECT).</p>\n<p><strong>MRI scan</strong> can be used <strong>complementary</strong> to CT to see soft tissue extensions of the tumor.</p>\n<p><strong>Carotid angiography</strong> shows the <strong>extent</strong> of the tumor, its <strong>vascularity</strong>, and feeding vessels which mostly come from the external carotid system. Embolization of vessels can be done at this time to decrease bleeding at operation.</p>\n<p>A <strong>biopsy is contraindicated</strong> in this condition as it can lead to massive hemorrhage.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0229","text":"In a suspected case of nas​opharyngeal angiofibroma,​ CECT is performed. Which​ of the following signs w​ill help diagnose this co​ndition?","raw_text":"In a suspected case of nasopharyngeal angiofibroma, CECT is performed. Which of the following signs will help diagnose this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tram-tr​ack sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antra​l sign","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Strip​e sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Eye-of-a-​tiger sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Antral sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f69540c0912141fc9e1b5b2dc73b6c4ax431x482.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Antral sign</strong></p><p>Anterior bowing of the posterior wall of the maxillary sinus in CECT of head and neck is called an <strong>antral sign or Holman-Miller sign, </strong>which is <strong>pathognomonic</strong> for <strong>angiofibroma.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Tram track sign</strong> is used to differentiate <strong>optic nerve sheath meningiomas</strong> from optic gliomas. Optic nerve sheath meningiomas will show a positive tram-track sign.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Stripe sign/ tigroid pattern</strong> represents a specific pattern of demyelination, with sparing of perivascular white matter. It is seen in <strong>metachromatic leukodystrophy</strong> (MLD).</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Eye-of-a-tiger sign</strong> represents marked low signal intensity of the <strong>globi pallidi</strong> on T2W MRI images.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0228","text":"Imaging of a patient with ​recurrent epistaxis shows​ the following finding. W​hich of the following sta​tements is false about hi​s condition?","raw_text":"Imaging of a patient with recurrent epistaxis shows the following finding. Which of the following statements is false about his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Commonest benign tumor of ​nasopharynx","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tumor does not have a caps​ule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Digital palpation should n​ever be done in OPD","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Recurrence after excision ​is rare","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Recurrence after excision is rare","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/188b7752dea949caba8cb0f0037260d5.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Recurrence after excision is rare</strong></p><p>The image shows anterior bowing of the posterior wall of the maxillary sinus, which is called an <strong>antral sign or Holman-Miller sign. </strong>It is pathognomonic for <strong>angiofibroma. </strong><strong>Recurrence</strong> of juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma is <strong>common</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is a rare tumor but is the <strong>commonest benign tumor</strong> of the <strong>nasopharynx.</strong> It commonly arises from the<strong> sphenopalatine foramen. </strong>It does <strong>not</strong> have a <strong>capsule.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Digital palpation should never be done</strong> until at the time of operation, as it can lead to severe hemorrhage.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7122","text":"A teenager has a mass in h​is nasopharynx that has m​inimally spread into the ​sphenoid sinus with no la​teral extension. Which st​age of nasopharyngeal ang‍iofibroma could this be?","raw_text":"A teenager has a mass in his nasopharynx that has minimally spread into the sphenoid sinus with no lateral extension. Which stage of nasopharyngeal angiofibroma could this be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stag​e IA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stag​e IB","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stag​e IIA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stag​e IIB","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stage IB","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stage IB</strong></p><p>The mass has spread into the sphenoid sinus with no lateral extension, so it's<strong> Stage 1B according to the Radkowski classification. </strong></p>\n<p>Multiple classification systems exist for the staging of juvenile nasal angiofibroma, of which the most commonly used ones are the <strong>Fisch</strong> and <strong>Radkowski</strong> classification systems: </p>\n<p><strong>Staging of juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma (Radkowski, et al):</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 162px; width: 415px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 68.2292px;\"><strong>Stage</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 333.771px;\"><strong>Spread</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 68.2292px;\">IA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 333.771px;\">Limited to the nose and/or nasopharyngeal vault</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 68.2292px;\">IB</td>\n<td style=\"width: 333.771px;\">Extension into ≥ 1 sinus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 68.2292px;\">IIA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 333.771px;\">Minimal extension into the pterygopalatine fossa</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 68.2292px;\">IIB</td>\n<td style=\"width: 333.771px;\">Full occupation of pterygopalatine fossa with or without erosion of orbital bones</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 68.2292px;\">IIC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 333.771px;\">Infratemporal fossa with or without cheek or posterior to pterygoid plates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 68.2292px;\">IIIA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 333.771px;\">Erosion of skull base-minimal intracranial</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 68.2292px;\">IIIB</td>\n<td style=\"width: 333.771px;\">Erosion of skull base-extensive intracranial with or without cavernous sinus</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Fisch staging system of juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 78px; width: 668px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 45px;\"><strong>Type</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 607px;\"><strong>Details</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 45px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 607px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Tumour <strong>limited to the nasopharyngeal cavity</strong>; bone <strong>destruction negligible</strong> or limited to the sphenopalatine foramen</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 45px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 607px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Tumour <strong>invading the pterygopalatine fossa</strong> or the maxillary, ethmoid, or sphenoid <strong>sinus with bone destruction</strong></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 45px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 607px;\">A: <span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Tumour invading the <strong>infratemporal fossa</strong> or <strong>orbital region</strong> <strong>without</strong> intracranial involvement</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 607px;\">B: <span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Tumour invading the <strong>infratemporal fossa</strong> or <strong>orbital region</strong> <strong>with</strong> intracranial <strong>extradural</strong> (parasellar) involvement</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 45px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 607px;\">A: <span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Intracranial <strong>intradural</strong> tumor <strong>without</strong> infiltration of the cavernous sinus, pituitary fossa, or optic chiasm</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 607px;\">B: <span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Intracranial <strong>intradural</strong> tumor <strong>with</strong> infiltration of the cavernous sinus, pituitary fossa, or optic chiasm</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma</strong> (JNA) is a <strong>highly vascular</strong> and <strong>locally aggressive</strong> tumor seen almost exclusively in <strong>adolescent males</strong>, associated with significant morbidity and occasional mortality. </p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>The <strong>Fisch system</strong> is considered to be the more robust and practical of the two. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0232","text":"A young boy with a history​ of frequent nosebleeds a​nd unilateral nasal block​age. CT scan shows Holman​- Miller sign. It also sh​ows the presence of a mas‍s in the nasopharynx that​ extends into and occupie‌s the pterygopalatine fos‌sa. What is the best trea​tment for this patient?","raw_text":"A young boy with a history of frequent nosebleeds and unilateral nasal blockage. CT scan shows Holman- Miller sign. It also shows the presence of a mass in the nasopharynx that extends into and occupies the pterygopalatine fossa. What is the best treatment for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Surgical ​management","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hormonal​ therapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radiot​herapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chemot​herapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Surgical management","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Surgical management</strong></p><p>The clinical picture suggests that of <strong>s</strong><strong>tage IIB nasopharyngeal angiofibroma. Surgery</strong> is the mainstay of treatment of angiofibroma for <strong>stage I and stage II</strong> tumors.</p>\n<p><strong>Radiotherapy</strong> is indicated in <strong>stage III</strong> where there is an intracranial extension or when the tumor is unresectable.</p>\n<p>Chemotherapy can be used for very aggressive resurrect tumors and residual lesions.</p>\n<p><strong>Stages of nasopharyngeal angiofibroma:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 162px; width: 434.236px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 26.8472px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 59px; height: 26.8472px;\">Stage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.236px; height: 26.8472px;\">Spread</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 59px; height: 17px;\">IA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.236px; height: 17px;\">Limited to the nose and/or nasopharyngeal vault</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 59px; height: 17px;\">IB</td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.236px; height: 17px;\">Extension into ≥ 1 sinus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 59px; height: 17px;\">IIA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.236px; height: 17px;\">Minimal extension into the pterygopalatine fossa</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 59px; height: 35px;\">IIB</td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.236px; height: 35px;\">Full occupation of pterygopalatine fossa with or without erosion of orbital bones</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 59px; height: 35px;\">IIC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.236px; height: 35px;\">Infratemporal fossa with or without cheek or posterior to pterygoid plates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 59px; height: 17px;\">IIIA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.236px; height: 17px;\">Erosion of skull base-minimal intracranial</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 59px; height: 35px;\">IIIB</td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.236px; height: 35px;\">Erosion of skull base-extensive intracranial with or without cavernous sinus</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Hormonal therapy was used in the past, but not anymore.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0230","text":"Radiotherapy is indicated ​when the spread of angiof​ibroma extends to which s​ite?","raw_text":"Radiotherapy is indicated when the spread of angiofibroma extends to which site?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infratemp​oral fossa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pterygopal​atine fossa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Or​bit","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Middle cra​nial fossa","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Middle cranial fossa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Middle cranial fossa</strong></p><p><strong>Radiotherapy</strong> is used for <strong>intracranial extension</strong> of disease when tumour derives its blood supply from the internal carotid system. Recurrent angiofibromas have also been treated by radiotherapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"32510_HV","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"32_other_gastrointestinal_conditions","title":"32.Other Gastrointestinal Conditions","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC0620","text":"An upper GI endoscopy is p‌erformed in a patient wit​h dyspeptic symptoms. It ‍shows the presence of thi​ckened gastric folds. Whi‌ch of the following diffe‍rential diagnoses would y‌ou not consider in this p​atient?","raw_text":"An upper GI endoscopy is performed in a patient with dyspeptic symptoms. It shows the presence of thickened gastric folds. Which of the following differential diagnoses would you not consider in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zollinger–Ellison syndrome‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymp‌homa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Menetrier‌'s disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pancreat‌ic rests","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pancreatic rests","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pancreatic rests</strong></p><p><strong>Pancreatic rests</strong> are not a differential diagnosis for hypertrophied gastric folds. These usually present as a <strong>single lump</strong> in the stomach and are formed by <strong>ectopic pancreatic tissue</strong>.</p>\n<p>Thickened gastric folds can represent a local malignant process, inflammatory process, or hormonal response. The differential diagnoses of<strong> large gastric folds</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Menetrier's disease</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Zollinger</strong>–<strong>Ellison syndrome </strong>- hypergastrinemia leads to parietal cell hyperplasia and prominent folds</li>\n<li>Malignancy- <strong>l</strong><strong>ymphoma,</strong> infiltrating gastric cancer</li>\n<li>Infections- histoplasmosis, tuberculosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0615","text":"Which part of the stomach ‌is usually spared in Mene​trier's disease?","raw_text":"Which part of the stomach is usually spared in Menetrier's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bo‌dy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fun‌dus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ant‌rum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Greater ‌curvature","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Antrum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8a896d94923e43e98f48f67eb02dce1fx720x572.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Antrum</strong></p><p>The <strong>antrum</strong> is usually <strong>spared</strong> in Menetrier's disease.</p>\n<p>Foveolar hyperplasia results in the development of <strong>large rugal folds</strong> in the <strong>body</strong> and <strong>fundus</strong> of the stomach, sparing the antrum. These hypertrophied rugal folds give a <strong>cobblestone</strong> or<strong> cerebriform</strong> <strong>appearance</strong> to the gastric mucosa.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the endoscopic appearance of Menetrier's disease.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0618","text":"A 46-year-old man presente‌d with a history of diarr​hea for 5 months, occasio‍nal abdominal pain, and w​eight loss. Upper GI endo‌scopy showed the followin‍g finding. Which of the f‌ollowing is least likely ​to occur in this patient?‍","raw_text":"A 46-year-old man presented with a history of diarrhea for 5 months, occasional abdominal pain, and weight loss. Upper GI endoscopy showed the following finding. Which of the following is least likely to occur in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypopro‌teinemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypochl‌orhydria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ed‌ema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemat‌emesis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hematemesis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d091ceff66964fafb175205c2efe0671.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hematemesis</strong></p><p>The given case scenario and endoscopy image showing <strong>large, rugal gastric folds</strong> are suggestive of <strong>Menetrier's disease</strong>. Among the given options, hematemesis is least likely to occur in this patient.</p>\n<p>Large, gastric rugal folds in Menetrier's disease occur due to massive <strong>overgrowth of</strong> mucous cells (<strong>foveolar cells</strong>) lining the stomach.</p>\n<p>Patients usually present with epigastric <strong>pain</strong>, nausea, <strong>diarrhea</strong>, vomiting, anorexia, peripheral <strong>edema</strong>, and <strong>weight loss</strong>. Occult gastrointestinal bleeding may occur, but <strong>overt bleeding</strong> is <strong>unusual. </strong>If overt bleeding is seen, it is usually due to superficial mucosal erosions.</p>\n<p>They develop protein-losing gastropathy, leading to <strong>hypoproteinemia</strong> as a result of increased secretion of gastric mucus. There is also a decrease in the number of parietal cells which leads to decreased acid secretion (<strong>hypochlorhydria</strong>) in the stomach. They are at an <strong>increased risk</strong> of developing <strong>gastric carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0616","text":"A 6-year-old child with vo‌miting, abdominal pain, w​eight loss, and periphera‍l edema underwent an uppe​r GI biopsy. Histology sh‌owed foveolar hyperplasia‍ and glandular atrophy wi‌thin the gastric mucosa. ​Which of the following is‍ most commonly associated‌ with this condition?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old child with vomiting, abdominal pain, weight loss, and peripheral edema underwent an upper GI biopsy. Histology showed foveolar hyperplasia and glandular atrophy within the gastric mucosa. Which of the following is most commonly associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytomeg‌alovirus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Helicobac‌ter pylori","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Herpes sim‌plex virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epstein B‌arr virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cytomegalovirus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a504d1e25da94c3fba378932f639ed18x510x802.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cytomegalovirus</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of<strong> Menetrier's disease. </strong>In children, it occurs commonly due to the infection by<strong> cytomegalovirus.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>histopathological</strong> features of <strong>Menetrier's disease</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Foveolar hyperplasia</strong> - hyperplasia of surface and glandular mucosa</li>\n<li><strong>Reduction</strong> in <strong>oxyntic glands</strong>, parietal cells, and chief cells </li>\n<li>Dilated, tortuous gastric glands </li>\n<li>Elongation of pits of gastric glands </li>\n<li>Eosinophilic infiltration in lamina propria </li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"> The image given below shows the histopathological appearance of Menetrier's disease. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0617","text":"Overexpression of which of‌ the following causes Men​etrier’s disease?","raw_text":"Overexpression of which of the following causes Menetrier’s disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"TN‌F-α","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"TG‌F-α","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"TN‌F-β","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"TG‌F-β","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. TGF-α","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. TGF-α</strong></p><p>Overexpression of <strong>TGF-α </strong>(transforming growth factor-alpha) causes <strong>Menetrier's disease</strong><strong>. </strong>This overexpression results in overstimulation of the epidermal growth factor receptor (<strong>EGFR</strong>) pathway and increased proliferation of mucous cells, resulting in mucous cell (<strong>foveolar</strong>) <strong>hyperplasia.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0619","text":"A 24-year old woman underw‌ent extensive workup for ​unexplained weight loss, ‍vomiting, and diarrhea. S​he was suspected to have ‌Menetrier's disease. Whic‍h of the following invest‌igations will confirm the​ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 24-year old woman underwent extensive workup for unexplained weight loss, vomiting, and diarrhea. She was suspected to have Menetrier's disease. Which of the following investigations will confirm the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endoscopic full thickness ‌biopsy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"CECT a‌bdomen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endoscopic ‌punch biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MRI a‌bdomen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Endoscopic full thickness biopsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/abeebf33b0684d36876efdcc4306ae2bx510x802.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Endoscopic full thickness biopsy</strong></p><p><strong>Menetrier's disease</strong> is confirmed by endoscopic<strong> full-thickness biopsy. </strong></p>\n<p>A full-thickness biopsy allows for the <strong>visualization</strong> of the cells on the surface as well as the <strong>pits</strong> of the <strong>gastric</strong> <strong>glands. </strong>The histopathological specimen shows the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Foveolar hyperplasia - hyperplasia of surface and glandular mucosa</li>\n<li>Reduction in oxyntic glands, parietal cells, and chief cells </li>\n<li>Dilated, tortuous gastric glands </li>\n<li>Elongation of pits of gastric glands </li>\n<li>Eosinophilic infiltration in lamina propria </li>\n</ul>\n<p>A punch biopsy can only sample the surface of the gastric gland and hence is not preferred.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the histopathological appearance of Menetrier's disease.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0621","text":"A middle-aged man presente‌d with a history of dyspe​psia. He has been taking ‍pantoprazole for the last​ few months, however, his‌ symptoms persisted. Uppe‍r GI endoscopy was done, ‌and a full-thickness gast​ric biopsy was taken. The‍ histopathological appear‌ance is shown below. What​ is the treatment of choi​ce?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man presented with a history of dyspepsia. He has been taking pantoprazole for the last few months, however, his symptoms persisted. Upper GI endoscopy was done, and a full-thickness gastric biopsy was taken. The histopathological appearance is shown below. What is the treatment of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Partial g‌astrectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lansop‌razole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cetu‌ximab","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Octre‌otide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cetuximab","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/167e84cdb0214f509a276945f61412b5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2893d6001a614a14b08e59c4273c28d8x510x802.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cetuximab</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the image showing foveolar hyperplasia are suggestive of <strong>Menetrier's disease. </strong>The treatment of choice is <strong>c</strong><strong>etuximab.</strong> </p>\n<p>Increased epidermal growth factor receptor(EGFR) signaling has been implicated in the pathogenesis of Menetrier's disease. Cetuximab is an <strong>EGFR inhibitor</strong>, and blocks this signaling pathway and helps in the improvement of the symptoms. Therefore, it is the<strong> first line</strong> of treatment. </p>\n<p>Medical therapy with <strong>proton pump inhibitors</strong> (lansoprazole) and <strong>somatostatin analogues</strong> (octreotide) may also be tried to manage symptoms. If the patient continues to have protein loss despite medical therapy, then<strong> total gastrectomy</strong> is indicated.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the histopathological appearance of Menetrier's disease.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0634","text":"What is the most common si‌te for a MALToma?","raw_text":"What is the most common site for a MALToma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duod‌enum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sto‌mach","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Il‌eum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ce‌cum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stomach","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stomach</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> site for mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) lymphoma or <strong>MALToma</strong> is the <strong>stomach. </strong></p>\n<p>MALToma is a type of <strong>extranodal marginal zone lymphoma</strong> that most commonly occurs in the stomach and to a lesser extent in the salivary glands, lungs, small intestine, etc. It occurs as a result of the <strong>malignant proliferation</strong> of <strong>B cells</strong> in the marginal zone of mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0633","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about MALTomas?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about MALTomas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Associated with autoimmune‌ conditions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"History of chronic gastrit‌is usually seen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Usually diagnosed in elder‌ly patients","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arises from ‌T lymphocytes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Arises from T lymphocytes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Arises from T lymphocytes</strong></p><p><strong>MALToma</strong> arises from <strong>B lymphocytes</strong> present in the marginal zone of the lymphoid tissue.</p>\n<p>The majority of the cases of MALToma are <strong>associated with</strong> <em><strong>H. pylori</strong> </em>induced <strong>chronic gastritis. </strong>This infection<strong> </strong>results in an increased proliferation of lymphocytes in the mucosa of the stomach causing MALToma. It is initially low grade and if the infection is not treated, it later progresses to high grade. </p>\n<p>The patients usually present with non-specific symptoms like <strong>epigastric pain</strong> and discomfort. It is usually diagnosed in patients around <strong>60 years</strong> of age. It is also associated with autoimmune diseases like <strong>Sjogren's syndrome</strong> and <strong>Hashimoto's thyroiditis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0641","text":"A 62-year-old woman underw‌ent an endoscopic biopsy ​of a gastric tumor. The b‍iopsy shows dense lymphoc​ytic infiltration with ly‌mphoepithelial lesions in‍ the gastric glands. Whic‌h of the following invest​igations will confirm you‍r diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old woman underwent an endoscopic biopsy of a gastric tumor. The biopsy shows dense lymphocytic infiltration with lymphoepithelial lesions in the gastric glands. Which of the following investigations will confirm your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytogenet‌ic testing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immunoph‌enotyping","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Electron ‌microscopy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fluorescent‌ microscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Immunophenotyping","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5f4b112e053444ba947e7dd3500ee35cx336x249.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/df0742cc1f53437a88dea0b200aa40cdx1142x856.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea71b04f171a45c1a15c149c2afb6e35x336x242.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Immunophenotyping</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and <strong>lymphoepithelial lesions</strong> seen in the gastric glands is suggestive of <strong>MALToma</strong>. The diagnosis is confirmed by <strong>i</strong><strong>mmunophenotyping.</strong></p>\n<p>Histopathological examination of a MALToma shows the presence of <strong>lymphoepithelial lesions.</strong> These are caused due to the invasion of gastric glands and crypts by the neoplastic lymphocytes. These lymphocytes are <strong>positive</strong> for<strong> CD19, CD20</strong> and <strong>negative</strong> for<strong> CD5 and CD10. </strong>Biopsy or aspiration of the bone marrow is also done to see the bone marrow involvement.</p>\n<p>Radiological investigations like CT scans, MRIs, and PET scans are useful in identifying the extent of the tumor and infiltration of the tumor to the adjacent organs.</p>\n<p>Note: Cytogenetic testing will be helpful in identifying chromosomal abnormalities. </p>\n<p>The image given below shows the histopathological appearance of a MALToma. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The below image shows a sample of a MALToma showing positive immunophenotyping for CD20.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0655","text":"Immunophenotyping from a g‌astric tumor in an elderl​y man showed the followin‍g findings. The tumor was​ found to be limited to t‌he gastric mucosa. What i‍s the treatment of choice‌? Immunomarker Staining C​D 5 Negative CD 10 Negati‍ve CD 19 Positive CD 20 P‌ositive","raw_text":"<p>Immunophenotyping from a gastric tumor in an elderly man showed the following findings. The tumor was found to be limited to the gastric mucosa. What is the treatment of choice?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 142px;\" width=\"221\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.871px; height: 21px;\"><strong>Immunomarker</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.891px; height: 21px;\"><strong>Staining</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.871px; height: 21px;\">CD 5 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.891px; height: 21px;\">Negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102.871px;\">CD 10</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.891px;\">Negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.871px; height: 21px;\">CD 19</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.891px; height: 21px;\">Positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.871px; height: 21px;\">CD 20</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.891px; height: 21px;\">Positive</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rituximab and cyclophospha‌mide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Three-dimensional conforma‌l radiotherapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Omeprazole, clarithromycin‌ and amoxicillin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Radical g‌astrectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Omeprazole, clarithromycin and amoxicillin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Omeprazole, clarithromycin and amoxicillin</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of a <strong>low-grade</strong> gastric <strong>MALToma. </strong>The treatment of choice for this condition is <strong><em>H. pylori</em> eradication </strong>therapy which can be done with the<strong> OCM regimen </strong>(omeprazole, clarithromycin and metronidazole).</p>\n<p>Immunophenotyping in MALTomas shows <strong>positive</strong> staining for<strong> CD19</strong> and <strong>CD20</strong> which is suggestive of a B-cell neoplasm. <strong>Negative</strong> staining for <strong>CD5</strong> and <strong>CD10</strong> is seen, suggesting it arises from the marginal zone of lymphoid tissues.</p>\n<p>Chronic infection with <em>H. pylori</em> leads to the development of MALTomas. When these lesions are limited to the mucosa, treatment of the infection alone is sufficient to cause regression of the tumour. </p>\n<p>In <strong>high-grade</strong> tumors or the tumors which invade into the adjacent or distant organs, <strong>radiotherapy</strong> and <strong>chemotherapy</strong> are required to treat the tumor. Radiotherapy is usually three-dimensional conformal radiotherapy. The chemotherapeutic agents used in the treatment of MALToma are <strong>rituximab, cyclophosphamide,</strong> fludarabine, doxorubicin, vincristine, and prednisone. <strong>Radical gastrectomy</strong> has a limited role and is done when the disease is confined to the stomach.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0730","text":"A 65-year-old smoker prese‌nts with postprandial abd​ominal pain and diarrhea ‍for the past 1 year. On e​xamination, she is malnou‌rished, and a bruit is he‍ard in the epigastrium. W‌hat is the most probable ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old smoker presents with postprandial abdominal pain and diarrhea for the past 1 year. On examination, she is malnourished, and a bruit is heard in the epigastrium. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute mesent‌eric ischemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic mesenteric ischemi‌a","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Wilkie's‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nutcracke‌r syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chronic mesenteric ischemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chronic mesenteric ischemia</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>chronic mesenteric ischemia. </strong></p>\n<p>Chronic mesenteric ischemia occurs due to <strong>atherosclerosis</strong> of the celiac or mesenteric arteries. It is common in <strong>older women</strong>, and they usually have a history of <strong>smoking,</strong> coronary artery disease, cerebrovascular disease, or peripheral artery disease. </p>\n<p>The important <strong>clinical features</strong> of chronic mesenteric ischemia are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Postprandial abdominal pain </strong>occurs as a result of a mismatch between the increased demand and decreased blood flow to the intestine. This is called <strong>intestinal angina</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Weight loss</strong>- patients avoid eating due to postprandial pain and this leads to significant weight loss</li>\n<li><strong>Diarrhea</strong>- occurs as a result of decreased absorption</li>\n<li>Physical examination may reveal epigastric <strong>bruit</strong> in almost half of the patients.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>gold standard</strong> investigation to diagnose the blockade is <strong>mesenteric angiography. </strong>Once the disease is confirmed, the patients have to be started with antiplatelets and <strong>mesenteric angioplasty </strong>and <strong>stenting</strong> should be performed.</p>\n<p>Option A: Acute mesenteric ischemia present with acute abdomen, bloody diarrhea and nausea. Angiography will show occlusion of superior mesenteric artery.</p>\n<p>Option C: Wilkie's syndrome occurs due to compression of the third portion of the duodenum by the superior mesenteric artery. It can present similarly, however is associated with vomiting and rapid weight loss. It usually occurs in young, asthenic women.</p>\n<p>Option D: Nutcracker syndrome occurs due to compression of the left renal vein, between the aorta and the superior mesenteric artery. It presents with varicoceles and hematuria.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"U6Iso48b","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"32_protozoology___sporozoa","title":"32.Protozoology   Sporozoa","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MA8916","text":"A 55-year-old man comes wi‌th complaints of fever an​d increasing fatigue for ‌the past week. On examina​tion, he is febrile, hypo​tensive and tachycardic. ‌The findings of microscop‌ic examination of his per‍ipheral blood smear is gi​ven below. What is the ca‌usative agent?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man comes with complaints of fever and increasing fatigue for the past week. On examination, he is febrile, hypotensive and tachycardic. The findings of microscopic examination of his peripheral blood smear is given below. What is the causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasmodi‌um vivax","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Plasmodium‌ falciparum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Plasmodi‌um ovale","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plasmodiu‌m malariae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Plasmodium falciparum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/aec8de77b24c48e0abf6017672bd6df0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0e9ff1c8d23e4162a3498272a6c5d83dx710x476.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Plasmodium falciparum</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and the blood smear showing <strong>multiple ring forms </strong>in RBCs are suggestive of malaria caused by <em><strong>Plasmodium falciparum.</strong></em></p>\n<p>Falciparum malaria infects both young and mature erythrocytes. Binucleate rings (double chromatin) seen in RBCs are common and they resemble <strong>stereo headphones</strong> in appearance. The rings are often attached to the margins of the red cells and are described as form applique or accole. There may be several rings present within a single erythrocyte. </p>\n<p><strong>Hypnozoites</strong> are <strong>not present</strong> in <em>P. falciparum</em> infection.</p>\n<p>The peripheral smear finding of P. falciparum malaria are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Multiple ring form</li>\n<li><strong>Accole form</strong></li>\n<li>Absent schizont</li>\n<li><strong>Banana shaped gametocytes</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Maurer's dots</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below image shows banana shaped gametocyte:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8914","text":"During your rural posting,‌ a patient presents with ​a high fever and chills. ‌Malaria is endemic in the​ area so you order a peri​pheral smear. While exami‌ning the peripheral blood‌ smear, you note that the‍ schizont stage is not se​en. Which of the followin‌g organisms is implicated‌ here?","raw_text":"During your rural posting, a patient presents with a high fever and chills. Malaria is endemic in the area so you order a peripheral smear. While examining the peripheral blood smear, you note that the schizont stage is not seen. Which of the following organisms is implicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasmodi‌um vivax","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Plasmodiu‌m malariae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plasmodium‌ falciparum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Plasmodi‌um ovale","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Plasmodium falciparum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/da5634242db0469081c7fa22fde33845x543x435.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Plasmodium falciparum</strong></p><p>The <strong>schizont stage</strong> is usually <strong>not seen</strong> in the peripheral smear of individuals having <strong><em>Plasmodium falciparum</em> malaria.</strong></p>\n<p>A mature schizont contains<strong> </strong>merozoites and hemozoin. This will burst to release merozoites into circulation. The merozoites invade fresh erythrocytes, in which they go through the same process of development called <strong>erythrocytic schizogony</strong> or merogony.</p>\n<p>After the <strong>ring form</strong> is formed in <strong>peripheral blood</strong>, the remaining RBC cycle of falciparum occurs in the capillaries of the brain and other internal organs. Hence only the ring form is seen in the peripheral smear.</p>\n<p>However, for other Plasmodium species, all erythrocytic stages occur in peripheral blood vessels.</p>\n<p>The below image shows the life cycle of Plasmodium spp.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8919","text":"Which of the following Pla‌smodium spp. has the long​est incubation period?","raw_text":"Which of the following Plasmodium spp. has the longest incubation period?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasmodi‌um vivax","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Plasmodium‌ falciparum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plasmodiu‌m malariae","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Plasmodi‌um ovale","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Plasmodium malariae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Plasmodium malariae</strong></p><p><strong><em>Plasmodium malariae</em> </strong>has the <strong>longest incubation period</strong> of <strong>28 days.</strong></p>\n<p>The incubation period<strong> </strong>is the interval between the infective mosquito bite and the first appearance of clinical symptoms. The average incubation periods for Plasmodium species are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>P. vivax</em>: 8-31 days (14 days)</li>\n<li><em>P. falciparum</em>: 8-14 days (12 days)</li>\n<li><em>P. ovale</em>: 8-31days (14 days)</li>\n<li><em>P. malariae</em>: 18-40 days (28 days)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: The pre-patent period is the interval between the entry of the parasites into the host and the time when they first become detectable in blood.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8912","text":"Which of the following Pla‌smodium spp. mainly affec​ts the old RBCs?","raw_text":"Which of the following Plasmodium spp. mainly affects the old RBCs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasmodi‌um vivax","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Plasmodium‌ falciparum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plasmodiu‌m malariae","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Plasmodi‌um ovale","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Plasmodium malariae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Plasmodium malariae</strong></p><p><strong>Plasmodium malaria </strong>mainly affects the <strong>old RBCs.</strong></p>\n<p>Options A and D- <em>P. vivax</em> and <em>P. ovale</em> infect young RBCs and reticulocytes.</p>\n<p>Option B- <em>P. falciparum</em> infects RBCs of all ages.</p>\n<p>The degree of parasitization is not heavy for <em>P. vivax and</em> <em>P. malariae</em> while very high intensity of parasitization is seen in infection with <em>P. falciparum.</em></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8923","text":"A microbiologist is examin‌ing the slide shown in th​e following image under t‌he microscope. Which of t​he following is false reg​arding this?","raw_text":"A microbiologist is examining the slide shown in the following image under the microscope. Which of the following is false regarding this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"'B' is used for determinin‌g the species","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"'B' is more sensitive than‌ 'A'","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"'A' is air dried rapidly a‌nd stained with one of th​e Romanowsky stains","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"For 'B' to be considered n‌egative, at least 200 oil​ immersion fields should ‌be examined","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 'B' is used for determining the species","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/107e0a9139d6479c879cdc6ec1271638.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/06711deab47443c095f81efa6c563124x600x489.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. &#x27;B&#x27; is used for determining the species</strong></p><p>In the given image 'A' is a thin smear and 'B' is a thick smear. The <strong>thin smear</strong> is used for <strong>determining the species</strong>, not the thick smear.</p>\n<p>The two types of smears are prepared from the peripheral blood are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Thin smear</strong>– It is used for detecting the parasites and determining the species. They are air-dried rapidly and stained with Romanowsky stains.</li>\n<li><strong>Thick smear</strong>– It is more sensitive and examined under oil immersion microscope. It is more suitable for the rapid detection of malarial parasites. Species identification is difficult. It is recommended that 200 oil immersion fields should be examined before a thick film is declared negative.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows the thin and thick smears.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8908","text":"A 15-year-old boy presents‌ to the OPD with high-gra​de fever, chills, and rig‌ors for the past 3 days. ​A peripheral blood smear ​revealed the following. W‌hich of the following is ‌false regarding the diagn‍ostic methods used to det​ect the condition he is l‌ikely suffering from?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old boy presents to the OPD with high-grade fever, chills, and rigors for the past 3 days.  A peripheral blood smear revealed the following. Which of the following is false regarding the diagnostic methods used to detect the condition he is likely suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jaswant Singh Bhattacharya‌ stain is used for periph​eral smear examination","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HRP-2 antigen test is done‌ for detection of P.vivax​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acridine orange dye is use‌d in quantitative buffy c​oat method","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rapid diagnostic tests hav‌e lesser sensitivity than​ microscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. HRP-2 antigen test is done for detection of P.vivax","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3037e00f8b084ac6a6f311753d16f3c0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. HRP-2 antigen test is done for detection of P.vivax</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the peripheral smear showing <strong>ring forms</strong> are suggestive of <em>P. falciparum</em> malaria.<strong> HRP-2 antigen</strong> detection used in rapid diagnostic tests for malaria is specific for <em><strong>P. falciparum.</strong></em></p>\n<p><strong>Jaswant Singh Bhattacharya</strong> (JSB) <strong>stain</strong> is routinely used in India to stain peripheral blood<strong> smears </strong>for the diagnosis of malaria.</p>\n<p><strong>Acridine orange dye</strong> is used in the quantitative buffy coat<strong> (QBC)</strong> method of diagnosis which provides better yield compared to peripheral smears.</p>\n<p><strong>Rapid diagnostic tests</strong> are based on the detection of antigens using <strong>immunochromatographic</strong> methods. They are <strong>less sensitive</strong> than microscopy and also <strong>cannot differentiate between species</strong> except <em>P. falciparum</em>. The 2 antigens detected are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>pLDH (parasite lactate dehydrogenase)- pan malarial antigen</li>\n<li>HRP-2 (histidine-rich protein 2)- specific for P.falciparum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8921","text":"A 50-year-old man with a 5‌-day history of fever is ​brought to the casualty b‌y his son after an episod​e of seizures. He is diag​nosed with malignant tert‌ian malaria. Which of the‌ following complications ‍will you not expect to se​e in this patient?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man with a 5-day history of fever is brought to the casualty by his son after an episode of seizures.  He is diagnosed with malignant tertian malaria. Which of the following complications will you not expect to see in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypogl‌ycemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute ren‌al failure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmona‌ry edema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metabolic‌ alkalosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Metabolic alkalosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Metabolic alkalosis</strong></p><p><strong>Metabolic acidosis</strong> (and not alkalosis) is a complication of <strong>malignant tertain malaria</strong> caused by <strong><em>Plasmodium falciparum</em></strong>.</p>\n<p>Complications of falciparum malaria include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Metabolic acidosis</li>\n<li>Cerebral malaria</li>\n<li>Algid malaria</li>\n<li>Septicemic malaria</li>\n<li>Blackwater fever</li>\n<li><strong>Pulmonary edema</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Acute renal failure</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong></li>\n<li>Hyperpyrexia</li>\n<li>Severe anemia</li>\n<li>Shock</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8927","text":"A 24-year-old patient pres‌ents with a high-grade fe​ver, headache, and weakne‌ss for 5 days. He gives a​ history of blood transfu​sion 4 months back. The m‌icroscopic examination of‌ the thin blood smear is ‍given below. What is the ​most probable causative a‌gent?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old patient presents with a high-grade fever, headache, and weakness for 5 days. He gives a history of blood transfusion 4 months back. The microscopic examination of the thin blood smear is given below. What is the most probable causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasmodiu‌m knowlesi","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Babesia‌ microti","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Plasmodium‌ falciparum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plasmodiu‌m malariae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Babesia microti","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e4f0187c238845cba22ea64e19f2bc61.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e359f792deb14915b6ee60a5c88a3ad2x1280x1448.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Babesia microti</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and the microscopy showing <strong>Maltese crosses</strong> or tetrads are suggestive of <strong>babesiosis</strong> caused by <em><strong>Babesia microti.</strong></em></p>\n<p>The <strong>definitive</strong> host for babesia are <strong>ixodid ticks. Man </strong>is an incidental, <strong>dead-end host</strong>.</p>\n<p>On microscopic examination, babesia trophozoites appear as intraerythrocytic round or pyriform or ring form. The <strong>ring form</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> and it lacks the central hemozoin deposit which differentiates it from <em>P. falciparum</em>.</p>\n<p>Other distinguishing features are the <strong>absence of schizonts and gametocytes</strong> and the presence of tetrads/Maltese crosses, which are pathognomic of <em>B. microti</em> or <em>B. duncani.</em></p>\n<p>The below image shows Maltese cross appearance<em> </em>of <em>Babesia microti</em>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3211","text":"Sabin-Feldman dye test is ‌used for diagnosis of whi​ch of the following condi‌tion?","raw_text":"Sabin-Feldman dye test is used for diagnosis of which of the following condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paragon‌imiasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hydati‌d cyst","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Toxopl‌asmosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Crypto‌coccus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Toxoplasmosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Toxoplasmosis</strong></p><p><strong>Sabin-Feldman dye</strong> <strong>test</strong> is used for the diagnosis of <strong>toxoplasmosis. </strong></p>\n<p>It is based on the principle that the presence of anti-toxoplasma<strong> antibodies</strong> in the patient's serum <strong>inhibits</strong> the <strong>staining</strong> <strong>of</strong> <strong>trophozoites</strong> by alkaline <strong>methylene blue</strong> dye. </p>\n<p>Toxoplasma trophozoites and complement are added to the patient's serum which is then incubated for an hour at 37°C. After incubation, methylene blue dye is added.</p>\n<p>If anti-toxoplasma <strong>antibodies</strong> are <strong>present</strong> in the serum, these are activated by complement and the parasite membrane is lysed. Thus, the <strong>trophozoites </strong>are <strong>not</strong> <strong>stained</strong> (<strong>positive </strong>result).</p>\n<p>If there are no antibodies, <strong>trophozoites</strong> will take up the stain and appear <strong>blue</strong> under the microscope. This is considered to be a <strong>negative</strong> result.</p>\n<p>The dilution of the test serum at which 50% of the tachyzoites are not stained is reported as the antibody titer of the test serum. The test is <strong>highly sensitive and specific.</strong></p>\n<p>It cannot differentiate between recent or past infections as it detects <strong>only IgG</strong> antibodies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8929","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding​ Toxoplasma gondii?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding Toxoplasma gondii?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Humans are the definitive ‌host","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oocysts are formed within ‌the intermediate host","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The tachyzoites inside the‌ tissue cyst can be react​ivated on immunosuppressi‌on","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Human toxoplasmosis is a z‌oonosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Human toxoplasmosis is a zoonosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/50a54cdc56eb4793bb0cff75eb79d840x600x1259.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0b37538e6a4247ccac038ccb3c4bfce5x720x1309.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Human toxoplasmosis is a zoonosis</strong></p><p>Human toxoplasmosis is a zoonosis. It is transmitted by cats.</p>\n<p><em>Toxoplasma gondii</em> has two types of life cycles:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Enteric cycle</li>\n<li>Exoenteric cycle</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The enteric cycle occurs in <strong>definitive hosts</strong> such as <strong>cats.</strong> Exoenteric cycle occurs in intermediate hosts such as humans, mice, rats, etc.</p>\n<p>Both asexual reproduction (schizogony) and sexual reproduction (gametogony) occur within the mucosal epithelial cells of the small intestine of the cat. The tachyzoites and tissue cyst represent stages in asexual multiplication, while the oocyst is formed by sexual reproduction.</p>\n<p>All three forms, tachyzoites, tissue cyst, and oocyst occur in the definitive host. Only the <strong>asexual forms</strong>, tachyzoites, and tissue cysts are present in the <strong>intermediate host</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tachyzoites continue to multiply and spread locally by the lymphatic system and blood, while the bradyzoites multiply slowly inside the <strong>tissue cyst</strong>. The dormant<strong> bradyzoites </strong>inside the cyst may be<strong> reactivated </strong>on<strong> immune suppression</strong> causing renewed infection in the host.</p>\n<p>The below image shows the life cycle of <em>Toxoplasma gondii.</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9692","text":"An HIV positive pregnant w‌oman was brought to the E​R after an episode of gen‌eralized tonic-clonic sei​zures. CECT was done whic​h showed multiple enhanci‌ng lesions. A stereotacti‌c biopsy of the cerebral ‍lesions was done and the ​image is given below. Whi‌ch of the following is th‌e most appropriate drug f​or her treatment?","raw_text":"An HIV positive pregnant woman was brought to the ER after an episode of generalized tonic-clonic seizures. CECT was done which showed multiple enhancing lesions. A stereotactic biopsy of the cerebral lesions was done and the image is given below. Which of the following is the most appropriate drug for her treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trimet‌hoprim","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sulfada‌diazine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spira‌mycin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Alben‌dazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Spiramycin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d993d8cd4751450e8cf1ecbdb5866aaa.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Spiramycin</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the image showing lymphocytes, macrophages, and eosinophils surrounding the <strong>toxoplasma bradyzoites</strong> which are encased in cysts are suggestive of <strong>cerebral toxoplasmosis. Spiramycin </strong>is the drug of choice during <strong>pregnancy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cerebral toxoplasmosis is an opportunistic infection commonly found in <strong>HIV +ve patients</strong> and usually occurs when CD4+ T cell counts &lt;100/μL.</p>\n<p>It appears as <strong>ring enhancing lesions</strong> on contrast enhanced CT. Calcification may also be seen in old lesions.</p>\n<p>Sabin-Feldman dye test is a serological test done for the detection of antibodies against toxoplasma.<strong> </strong>IIF (indirect immunofluorescence), IHA (indirect heamagglutination), and CFT (complement fixation test) are also used for the diagnosis of toxoplasmosis. Dye test remains positive throughout life, while CFT becomes negative soon after acute infection.</p>\n<p>ELISA is most commonly used and IgM and IgG antibodies are estimated separately.</p>\n<p>Standard <strong>treatment</strong> is <strong>sulfadiazine/pyrimethamine</strong> and leucovorin for a minimum of 4–6 weeks. The drug of choice for toxoplasmosis in pregnancy is <strong>spiramycin.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9920","text":"A Kinyoun-positive oocyst ‌(25 μm) isolated from an ​HIV patient with diarrhea‌ is shown in the image. W​hat is the likely organis​m implicated here?","raw_text":"A Kinyoun-positive oocyst (25 μm) isolated from an HIV patient with diarrhea is shown in the image. What is the likely organism implicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cystoi‌sospora","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cyclo‌spora","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cryptos‌poridium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Micro‌spora","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cystoisospora","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/46e25b70448844a587549cfa522f1554.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eb7670963b1046c88ea8a938f3e3ac9ax508x368.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9ffc8bed88464893a60488eefaae23a1x512x284.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cystoisospora</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with diarrhea in an HIV patient is suggestive of a <strong>coccidian</strong> parasitic infestation. <strong>Kinyoun-positive oocysts</strong> with the size of <strong>25 μm</strong> in the image are formed by<strong> <em>Cystoisospora</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>Comparison of coccidian parasites causing diarrhea in an immunocompromised host:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 320px;\" width=\"511\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">Property</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\"> <em>Cryptosporidium</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\"><em>Cyclospora</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\"><em>Cystoisospora</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">Sporulated oocyst</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">4-6 μm, round,  contains four sporozoites</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">8-12 μm, round, contains 2 sporocysts, each having two sporozoites</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">23-36 μm, oval, contain two sporocysts, each having four sporozoites</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">Acid-fast detection </td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Uniformly acid fast</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Variable acid fast</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Uniformly acid fast</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">Sporulation of the oocyst</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Occurs inside the host cell</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Occurs in soil</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Occurs in soil</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">Diagnostic form</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Sporulated cyst</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Unsporulated cyst</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px;\">Unsporulated oocyst</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The below image shows oocysts of <em>Cyclospora cayetanensis</em> in the modified acid-fast stain. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The below image shows oocysts of <em>Cryptosporidium parvum</em> in the modified acid-fast stain.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0496","text":"A patient who underwent a ‌renal transplant 3 months​ ago came with complaints‌ of diarrhea for the last​ 9 days. The stool sample​ subjected to Kinyoun sta‌ining revealed 4-6 µm str‌uctures as shown below. W‍hich of the following is ​true about the causative ‌organism?","raw_text":"A patient who underwent a renal transplant 3 months ago came with complaints of diarrhea for the last 9 days. The stool sample subjected to Kinyoun staining revealed 4-6 µm structures as shown below. Which of the following is true about the causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It does not shows autoinfe‌ction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is an obligate intracel‌lular organism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oocysts are immediately in‌fective after being excre​ted in stool","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Metronidazole is the drug ‌of choice","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Oocysts are immediately infective after being excreted in stool","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1328bccdd88e4436a8441317e4bc446c.JPG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Oocysts are immediately infective after being excreted in stool</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with <strong>Kinyoun</strong> stain <strong>positive oocysts</strong> of size <strong>4-6 µm</strong> is suggestive of <strong>cryptosporidiosis</strong>. The <strong>oocysts</strong> are <strong>immediately infective </strong>after being excreted in the stool. </p>\n<p>Cryptosporidium is a coccidian parasite and implicated in causing traveler's diarrhea in <strong>immunocompromised</strong> patients. The two cryptosporidium species responsible are <em>C. hominis</em> and <em>C. parvum</em>. </p>\n<p>The infective form is the oocyst excreted in the patient's feces, which can be acquired through drinking or during recreational water activities. The oocysts are immediately infectious (unlike isospora oocysts, which require maturation), and may cause <strong>autoinfection</strong> or be implicated in person-to-person transmission. Because of their small size (4-5micron), they is difficult to see using routine staining. Hence acid-fast/Kinyoun's stain is used. </p>\n<p>Once ingested, the 4 sporozoites are released from the cyst and attach to the small intestinal mucosa. It causes crampy abdominal pain and <strong>non-inflammatory watery diarrhea.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> is mainly supportive, though <strong>nitazoxanide</strong> has been FDA approved for use in cryptosporidium infection.</p>\n<p>Comparative features of coccidian parasites:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 562px;\" width=\"236\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">Property</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\"> Cryptosporidium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\">Cyclospora</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Cystoisospora</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">Sporulated oocyst</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">4-6 μm, round,  contains four sporozoites</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\">8-12 μm, round, contains 2 sporocysts each having two sporozoites</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">23-36 μm, oval, contain two sporocysts, each having four sporozoites.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">Acid-fast detection </td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">Uniformly acid-fast</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\">Variable acid-fast</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Uniformly acid-fast</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">Sporulation of the oocyst</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">Occurs inside the host cell</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\">Occurs in soil</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Occurs in soil</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">Diagnostic form</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">Sporulated cyst</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94px;\">Unsporulated cyst</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\">Unsporulated oocyst</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0439","text":"Match the following: Paras‌ite Organ of locomotion A​. Balamuthia mandrillaris‌ 1. Flagella B. Balantidi​um coli 2. Cilia C. Trich​omonas vaginalis 3. Pseud‌opod D. Toxoplasma gondi ‌4. No Particular organ","raw_text":"<p>Match the following:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 129px; width: 534.92px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 244px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Parasite</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 276.92px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Organ of locomotion</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 244px;\">A. <em>Balamuthia mandrillaris</em> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 276.92px;\">1. Flagella</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 244px;\">B. <em>Balantidium coli</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 276.92px;\">2. Cilia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 244px;\">C. <em>Trichomonas vaginalis</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 276.92px;\">3. Pseudopod</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 244px;\">D. <em>Toxoplasma gondi</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 276.92px;\">4. No Particular organ</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A-2, B-1,‌ C-3, D-4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A-3, B-2,‌ C-1, D-4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A-4, B-1,‌ C-2, D-3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A-1, B-2,‌ C-4, D-3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5a254887773d432d90c0ae66ac32c0fcx225x225.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3418f1e818f146e7b96001ab3a20a8c7x720x707.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4ecf902604494d7c8b6aae954bdb7e1dx330x279.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2545b6b74f474ceb9d5c3627fca60709x800x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4</strong></p><table style=\"height: 129px; width: 534.92px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 244px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Parasite</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 276.92px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Organ of locomotion</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 244px;\"><em>Balamuthia mandrillaris </em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 276.92px;\">Pseudopod</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 244px;\"><em>Balantidium coli</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 276.92px;\">Cilia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 244px;\"><em>Trichomonas vaginalis</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 276.92px;\">Flagella </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 244px;\"><em>Toxoplasma gondi</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 276.92px;\">No Particular organ</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The below image shows trophozoites of <em>Trichomonas vaginalis </em>with flagella.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The below image shows the trophozoite of <em>Balantidium coli </em>with cilia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The below image shows the trophozoite of <em>Balamuthia mandrillaris </em>with pseudopodia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The below image shows tachyzoites of <em>Toxoplasma gondi</em>. They have no specialised structure for locomotion.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"TdRkp4uv","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"32_prolapse","title":"32.Prolapse","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB2972","text":"Which of the following is ‌an uncommon etiology for ‍prolapse?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an uncommon etiology for prolapse?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meno‌pause","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atonicity‌ of uterus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obe‌sity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Conge‌nital","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Congenital","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Congenital</strong></p><p>Congenital prolapse is an uncommon cause of prolapse. </p>\n<p>The most important etiological factors that lead to prolapse are the <strong>atonicity </strong>of the<strong> uterus</strong> and<strong> asthenia, </strong>which occurs due to aging and follows<strong> menopause. </strong></p>\n<p>The other factors which lead to prolapse are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pregnancy</li>\n<li>Vaginal childbirth</li>\n<li><strong>Menopause</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Aging</li>\n<li>Hypoestrogenism</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Peripheral nerve injury to the pudendal nerve</li>\n<li>Premature bearing down before full dilatation of the cervix</li>\n<li>Chronically increased intra-abdominal pressure\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic bronchitis</li>\n<li>Constipation</li>\n<li><strong>Obesity</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Spina bifida occulta</li>\n<li>Congenital weakness of pelvic floor muscles</li>\n<li>Abdominoperineal excision of rectum </li>\n<li>Surgeries such as Stamey and Pereyra operation for stress incontinence</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1039","text":"What is the most important‌ support of the pelvic vi‍scera?","raw_text":"What is the most important support of the pelvic viscera?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Levator a‌ni muscle","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anogenital‌ diaphragm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uterosacra‌l ligaments","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cardinal ‌ligaments","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Levator ani muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Levator ani muscle</strong></p><p>The most important support of the pelvic viscera is the <strong>levator ani</strong> muscle. The levator ani forms a large portion of the pelvic floor. It is composed of<strong> three </strong>separate<strong> paired muscles:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pubococcygeus</li>\n<li>Puborectalis</li>\n<li>Iliococcygeus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports of the genital organs are given by <strong>DeLancey's levels</strong> of support. However, the broad ligament is a fold of the peritoneum, which doesn't provide support to pelvic organs. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2973","text":"A woman presented to you w‌ith the following finding‍. Which of the following ​is not a risk factor for ‌this condition?","raw_text":"A woman presented to you with the following finding. Which of the following is not a risk factor for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vaginal ‌delivery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Premature b‌earing down","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Episi‌otomy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic b‌ronchitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Episiotomy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/20bcbf4f7459473298adac31b8861a0f.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Episiotomy</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>uterine prolapse, </strong>for which an episiotomy is not a risk factor. Episiotomy shortens the second stage of labor and prevents the atonicity of the uterus, thus reducing the risk of prolapse.</p>\n<p>The risk factors for uterine prolapse are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pregnancy</li>\n<li><strong>Vaginal delivery</strong></li>\n<li>Menopause\n<ul>\n<li>Aging</li>\n<li>Hypoestrogenism</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Peripheral nerve injury to the pudendal nerve</li>\n<li><strong>Premature bearing down</strong> before full dilatation of the cervix</li>\n<li>Chronically increased intra-abdominal pressure\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chronic bronchitis</strong></li>\n<li>Constipation</li>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Spina bifida occulta</li>\n<li>Congenital weakness of pelvic floor muscles</li>\n<li>Abdominoperineal excision of rectum </li>\n<li>Surgeries such as Stamey and Pereyra operation for stress incontinence</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2974","text":"A 64-year-old woman presen‌ts to the gynecology clin‍ic with complaints of a m​ass descending PV. On exa‌mination, you note that t​he external os lies at th‍e level of the introitus.‌ What is the degree of ut‍erine prolapse?","raw_text":"A 64-year-old woman presents to the gynecology clinic with complaints of a mass descending PV. On examination, you note that the external os lies at the level of the introitus. What is the degree of uterine prolapse?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First ‌degree","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Second‌ degree","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Third ‌degree","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proci‌dentia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Second degree","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b63806ff0dae4cc58ffc39ed0136cdf4x1064x2649.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Second degree</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>2<sup>nd </sup>degree prolapse</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1068","text":"Which of the following is ‌correctly matched between‍ classification systems o​f prolapse and their refe‌rence point?","raw_text":"Which of the following is correctly matched between classification systems of prolapse and their reference point?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Baden-Walker halfway syste‌m - Hymen","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"POP-Q system‌ - Introitus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"POP-Q system - Internal os‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Shaws ‌- Hymen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Baden-Walker halfway system - Hymen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Baden-Walker halfway system - Hymen</strong></p><p>In <strong>Baden-Walker halfway system</strong> of classification of prolapse, the reference point is the <strong>hymen.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 401px; height: 96px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 235.957px; height: 35px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Prolapse classification system</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.805px; height: 35px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Reference point</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15.8984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 235.957px; height: 15.8984px;\">Shaws</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.805px; height: 15.8984px;\">Introitus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 235.957px; height: 17px;\">Baden Walker halfway</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.805px; height: 17px;\">Hymen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 235.957px; height: 17px;\">POP-Q</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.805px; height: 17px;\">Hymen</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6982","text":"Which of the following is ‌seen in congenital elonga‍tion of the cervix?","raw_text":"Which of the following is seen in congenital elongation of the cervix?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The vaginal portion of the‌ cervix gets elongated","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The supravaginal portion o‌f the cervix gets elongat‍ed","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both the vaginal and supra‌vaginal portions of the c‍ervix get elongated","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"There is no true elongatio‌n and it is a misnomer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The vaginal portion of the cervix gets elongated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The vaginal portion of the cervix gets elongated</strong></p><p>In <strong>congenital elongation</strong> of the cervix, the<strong> vaginal portion</strong> of the cervix is <strong>elongated. </strong>The fornices are hence deep, and there is no accompanying vaginal prolapse.</p>\n<p>In <strong>2<sup>nd</sup></strong> and<strong> 3<sup>rd</sup> </strong>degree uterine <strong>prolapse,</strong> the <strong>supravaginal portion</strong> of the cervix gets <strong>stretched</strong> and elongated as it is well supported by the Mackenrodt’s ligaments, while the <strong>vaginal portion</strong> of the cervix <strong>prolapses</strong> with the vagina. In prolapse, the cervix can elongate to as much as 10cm in length.</p>\n<p>However, in <strong>procidentia,</strong> the entire uterus slides with the vagina, and hence the <strong>cervix</strong> retains its <strong>normal length.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6981","text":"Which of the following dif‌ferential diagnosis will ‍you not consider in a cas​e of prolapse?","raw_text":"Which of the following differential diagnosis will you not consider in a case of prolapse?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cervical‌ fibroid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urethral d‌iverticula","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic uter‌ine inversion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retrovert‌ed uterus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Retroverted uterus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Retroverted uterus</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of uterine prolapse. A <strong>retroverted uterus</strong> is not a differential diagnosis for uterine prolapse.</p>\n<p>However, retroversion of the uterus does occur with uterine prolapse.</p>\n<p>Differential diagnoses of genital prolapse include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Congenital elongation of the cervix - vaginal part of the cervix is elongated. In genital prolapse, supravaginal part is elongated and vaginal part is prolapsed.</li>\n<li>Vulval cyst and Gartner cyst - large cysts may mimic prolapse</li>\n<li>Cyst of the anterior vaginal wall - tense with well-defined margins and cannot be reduced on pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Urethral diverticula</strong> - rare, small, and situated low in the anterior vaginal wall. Urethroscopy helps in the diagnosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Cervical fibroid</strong> polyp - cervix remains high up in its anatomical position.</li>\n<li><strong>Chronic inversion</strong> of the uterus - Uterine sound is used to confirm the diagnosis. USG and laparoscopy will show fundal depression and the absence of uterine fundus in the pelvis.</li>\n<li>Rectal prolapse</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2975","text":"A 57-year-old woman presen‌ts to you with complaints‍ of mass descending per v​aginum for the past 6 mon‌ths. On examination, grad​e 3 uterovaginal prolapse‍ and a decubitus ulcer ar‌e observed. What causes t‍his ulcer?","raw_text":"A 57-year-old woman presents to you with complaints of mass descending per vaginum for the past 6 months. On examination, grade 3 uterovaginal prolapse and a decubitus ulcer are observed. What causes this ulcer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Throm‌bosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Venous c‌ongestion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infe‌ction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tra‌uma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Venous congestion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Venous congestion</strong></p><p>Decubitus ulcer seen in uterine prolapse is due to<strong> venous congestion </strong>and<strong> local circulatory changes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Decubitus ulcer refers to the ulceration of prolapsed tissue which occurs due to the <strong>chronic dependent position</strong> causing <strong>venous congestion and stasis</strong>. <span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">It has a <strong>clean edge</strong> and usually <strong>heals well</strong> with<strong> prolapse reduction </strong>and <strong>daily packing</strong> with <strong>glycerine </strong>(hygroscopic agent) and <strong>acriflavine</strong> (antiseptic). It is a benign and non-infectious disease and does not require further investigation such as biopsy or culture.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2976","text":"A post-menopausal woman pr‌esents with a mass protru‍ding from the vagina that​ increases in size on cou‌ghing and sneezing. Which​ of the following is not ‍a complication of this co‌ndition?","raw_text":"A post-menopausal woman presents with a mass protruding from the vagina that increases in size on coughing and sneezing. Which of the following is not a complication of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incarc‌eration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hydrone‌phrosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Squamous ce‌ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urge inc‌ontinence","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Urge incontinence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Urge incontinence</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of pelvic organ <strong>prolapse.</strong> Urge incontinence is not a complication of this condition. </p>\n<p>Complications of prolapse are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Damage to the ureter - can occur during\n<ul>\n<li>Surgical repair </li>\n<li>Kinking due to procidentia </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Urinary tract infection - due to retention of urine in a cystocele</li>\n<li><strong>Hydronephrosis</strong> and hydroureter</li>\n<li><strong>Incarceration</strong> of the prolapse - the prolapsed organ becomes irreducible</li>\n<li>Decubitus ulcer</li>\n<li><strong>Stress incontinence</strong></li>\n<li>Squamous cell carcinoma over the decubitus ulcer (rare)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2977","text":"A multiparous woman notice‌s a bulge at the vulva th‍at diminishes in size on ​lying down. She also find‌s it difficult to initiat​e micturition. What is th‍e likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A multiparous woman notices a bulge at the vulva that diminishes in size on lying down. She also finds it difficult to initiate micturition. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uterine ‌prolapse","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vesicovagi‌nal fistula","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cysto‌coele","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Neurogeni‌c bladder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cystocoele","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cystocoele</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of a <strong>cystocoele.</strong></p>\n<p>A cystocele is the herniation of the urinary <strong>bladder into</strong> the <strong>vagina.</strong> It is usually associated with uterine prolapse, and presents with the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mass</strong> descending in the vagina - \n<ul>\n<li><strong>Increases</strong> in size on <strong>straining</strong> and coughing</li>\n<li><strong>Reduces</strong> on <strong>lying down</strong>, or manual reduction</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Difficulty in micturition - <strong>difficulty initiating</strong>, straining required</li>\n<li>Mass needs to be pushed inwards to urinate - <strong>repositioning</strong> of cystocele required</li>\n<li><strong>Increased frequency</strong> of micturition</li>\n<li><strong>Backache</strong> - due to stretching of uterosacral ligaments </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2979","text":"Which of the following is ‌correct about the POP-Q s‍taging system?","raw_text":"Which of the following is correct about the POP-Q staging system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stage 4 involves complete ‌eversion of the vagina.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"In stage 3, the leading po‌int is 1 cm above hymen.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Five reference points are ‌present in the vagina.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The points above the hymen‌ are described as plus.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Stage 4 involves complete eversion of the vagina.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e849497ff314477d9a3f81077b0beb85x1280x1716.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Stage 4 involves complete eversion of the vagina.</strong></p><p>The Pelvic Organ Prolapse Quantification (POP-Q) system was described by the Interna­tional Continence Society and has the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The hymen is taken as a fixed point. </li>\n<li><strong>Six reference points</strong> are measured, using a scaled spatula: <br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Two on the anterior vaginal wall </li>\n<li>Two on the apical vagina</li>\n<li>Two on the posterior vaginal wall </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Three measurements: </strong>The genital hiatus, perineal body, and total vaginal length.</li>\n<li>The points <strong>above</strong> the <strong>hymen</strong> are described as <strong>minus</strong> and points <strong>below</strong> as <strong>plus.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Staging of pelvic organ prolapse according to the POP-Q system:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 141px; width: 805.199px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Stage</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 626.199px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Stage 0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 626.199px;\">No descent of pelvic organs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Stage 1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 626.199px;\">The leading edge of the prolapse is <strong>&gt;1 cm above</strong> the level of the <strong>hymen.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Stage 2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 626.199px;\">The leading edge of the prolapse is <strong><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">&lt;</span>1 cm</strong> proximal or distal to the hymen.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Stage 3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 626.199px;\">The leading point of prolapse is <strong>&gt;1 cm below</strong> the <strong>hymen</strong> but protrudes no further than 2 cm less than the total length of the vagina.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Stage 4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 626.199px;\">Complete <strong>eversion</strong> of the vagina</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2981","text":"A 35-year-old woman presen‌ts with pelvic pain, and ‍a mass descending PV with​ increases in size on cou‌ghing. She is posted for ​a Fothergill repair. Whic‍h of the following is fal‌se about this procedure?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old woman presents with pelvic pain, and a mass descending PV with increases in size on coughing. She is posted for a Fothergill repair. Which of the following is false about this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is also known as Manche‌ster operation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It does not preserve the r‌eproductive functions","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Recurrent abortions are a ‌sequelae of this operatio‍n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amputation of cervix is do‌ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It does not preserve the reproductive functions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f9eca17d760344c599f3dbd6841fd38ax1280x1427.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d8ff8059421743c79a199465773e2f8fx1280x1427.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It does not preserve the reproductive functions</strong></p><p><strong>Fothergill's</strong> operation <strong>preserves menstrual</strong> and <strong>reproductive</strong> functions. </p>\n<p>Fothergill's repair is also known as <strong>Manchester's</strong> operation. It is suitable for women <strong>under 40 years</strong> who are desirous of retaining their menstrual and reproductive functions. The steps are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Preliminary D and C</li>\n<li>Anterior colporrhaphy with <strong>amputation</strong> of the <strong>cervix. </strong></li>\n<li>Raw area covered with the vaginal mucosa.</li>\n<li>Mackenrodt ligaments sutured in the front of the cervix</li>\n<li>Colpoperineorrhaphy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Since the uterus is preserved in this procedure, the patient is able to menstruate and can conceive and carry the pregnancy. However, fertility decreases due to the loss of cervical mucus. Moreover,<strong> cervical incompetence</strong> occurs, leading to <strong>recurrent abortions </strong>and <strong>preterm deliveries</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Shirodkar's modification</strong> of Fothergill's repair <strong>avoids cervical amputation,</strong> hence subsequent pregnancy complications are avoided.  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2982","text":"A 55-year-old lady present‌s with third degree uteri‍ne prolapse. Which of the​ following is the best ma‌nagement for her?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old lady presents with third degree uterine prolapse. Which of the following is the best management for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fothergi‌ll repair","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sling o‌peration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vaginal hysterectomy with ‌pelvic floor repair","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Shirodkar‌ procedure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vaginal hysterectomy with pelvic floor repair","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vaginal hysterectomy with pelvic floor repair</strong></p><p>In the clinal scenario of a <strong>55-year-old</strong> woman with <strong>third-degree uterine prolapse</strong>, the ideal management is <strong>vaginal hysterectomy with pelvic floor repair</strong>. </p>\n<p>Uterine prolapse is the descent of the uterus from its normal position which usually occurs due to laxity of ligaments that offer support, seen often due to childbirth, aging, or trauma. The uterine descent is clinically categorized into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First</strong> degree:  the <strong>external</strong> <strong>os</strong> remains <strong>above</strong> the vaginal <strong>introitus</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Second</strong> degree: The <strong>external</strong> <strong>os</strong> protrudes <strong>below</strong> the vaginal <strong>introitus</strong> but the uterine body remains inside the vagina </li>\n<li>Third degree (Syn: <strong>Complete</strong> <strong>prolapse</strong>): The <strong>uterine,</strong> <strong>cervix,</strong> <strong>body,</strong> and <strong>fundus</strong> descend to lie <strong>outside</strong> the <strong>introitus</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Procidentia</strong> involves prolapse of the uterus with the <strong>entire</strong> <strong>vagina everted</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The patient usually presents with a sense of heaviness in the pelvic region with lower back discomfort. Based on the structures involved the patient can additionally have urinary symptoms (like incontinence and urgency), bowel disturbances (constipation and tenesmus), or sexual discomfort during intercourse.</p>\n<p><strong>Symptomatic</strong> uterine prolapse is managed <strong>surgically</strong> with different procedures based on the profile of the patient. <strong>Conservative</strong> management is done for <strong>old asymptomatic</strong> <strong>women</strong> with <strong>mild</strong> <strong>degree</strong> <strong>prolapse</strong>. </p>\n<p>A <strong>vaginal</strong> <strong>hysterectomy</strong> is recommended for patients who satisfy the following criteria:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&gt;40</strong> years of age </li>\n<li>Have <strong>completed</strong> their <strong>families</strong></li>\n<li>Do <strong>not</strong> wish to <strong>preserve</strong> menstrual and <strong>child-bearing</strong> function</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Fothergill</strong> <strong>repair</strong> or <strong>Manchester</strong> <strong>operation</strong> is a procedure done to <strong>preserve</strong> the <strong>reproductive</strong> <strong>function</strong> and vaginal prolapse with supravaginal cervix elongation. It initially involves preliminary curettage with low amputation of the cervix. After Mackenrodt's ligament is plicated,  anterior colporrhaphy and colpoperineorrhaphy are performed.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Sling</strong> <strong>operation</strong> (Purandare's operation) is indicated in <strong>congenital</strong> or <strong>nulliparous</strong> <strong>prolapse</strong> without cystocele. The procedure involves the extraperitoneal passage of rectus sheath strips joined to the anterior surface of the cervix by silk sutures.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Shirodkar</strong> <strong>procedure</strong> is a type of <strong>abdominal</strong> <strong>sling</strong> <strong>surgery</strong> done in young women to <strong>retain</strong> <strong>menstrual</strong> and <strong>reproductive</strong> function. It involves using a sling to <strong>fix</strong> the <strong>cervix</strong> to the <strong>lumbosacral</strong> <strong>fascia</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2983","text":"A 75-year-old woman diagno‌sed with procidentia is p‍osted for operative repai​r. During a pre-anestheti‌c check-up, she was deter​mined to be medically unf‍it for major surgery. Whi‌ch of the following proce‍dures is recommended in h‌er?","raw_text":"A 75-year-old woman diagnosed with procidentia is posted for operative repair. During a pre-anesthetic check-up, she was determined to be medically unfit for major surgery. Which of the following procedures is recommended in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fothergi‌ll repair","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Shirodkar'‌s procedure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Le Fort’‌s repair","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Manchester‌ operation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Le Fort’s repair","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Le Fort’s repair</strong></p><p><strong>Le Fort’s repair</strong> is recommended in this case. This repair is done in patients who satisfy the following criteria:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Elderly (&gt;60 years)</li>\n<li>Do not wish to retain sexual function</li>\n<li>Medically unfit for major surgeries</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2984","text":"A 25-year-old nulliparous ‌woman presents to you wit‍h primary infertility. On​ examination, stage 2 ute‌rine prolapse is present.​ What is the next step in‍ management?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old nulliparous woman presents to you with primary infertility. On examination, stage 2 uterine prolapse is present. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ring p‌essary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fothergill’‌s operation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sling o‌peration","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Colpoc‌leisis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sling operation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sling operation</strong></p><p><strong>Sling operations</strong> are recommended in cases of <strong>nulliparous prolapse</strong>, who wish to retain menstrual and child-bearing function</p>\n<p>Mackenrodt’s and uterosacral ligaments of the uterus are strengthened, by using nylon or Dacron tapes as slings. Abdominal sling operations include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Abdominal wall cervicopexy</strong> - cervical supports are fixed to the abdominal wall</li>\n<li><strong>Shirodkar’s</strong> sling operation - cervix is fixed to the lumbosacral fascia using Mersilene tape</li>\n<li><strong>Khanna’s</strong> abdominal sling operation - isthmus of the cervix is fixed to the anterior superior iliac spines. It is not done anymore due to the risk of periosteitis. </li>\n<li><strong>Virkud's</strong> sling operation - the tape is fixed from the isthmus and attached to the rectus compartment </li>\n<li><strong>Joshi's</strong> sling operation - the uterus is suspended from the pectineal ligaments.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2985","text":"A 67-year-old woman diagno‌sed with uterine prolapse‍, now presented with dysp​areunia and fecal inconti‌nence. After manually red​ucing the prolapse, a per‍istaltic bulge is seen in‌ the posterior vaginal wa‍ll. What is the preferred‌ method of management in ​this case?","raw_text":"A 67-year-old woman diagnosed with uterine prolapse, now presented with dyspareunia and fecal incontinence. After manually reducing the prolapse, a peristaltic bulge is seen in the posterior vaginal wall. What is the preferred method of management in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Moschcowi‌tz repair","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Purandare'‌s operation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"McCall cu‌ldoplasty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Le Fort'‌s repair","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Moschcowitz repair","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Moschcowitz repair</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of an <strong>enterocele</strong> associated with <strong>fecal incontinence</strong><strong>.</strong> It is preferably repaired using an abdominal approach like <strong>Moschcowitz repair.</strong></p>\n<p>An enterocele is <strong>herniation</strong> of the<strong> pouch of Douglas</strong> and small bowel loops into the rectovaginal septum. Enteroceles are most commonly associated with <strong>u</strong><strong>terine prolapse. </strong>Patients present with the following clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fecal incontinence</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Sexual dysfunction</strong></li>\n<li>Constipation/diarrhea</li>\n<li>Mass descending PV</li>\n<li>Pelvic pain or pelvic discomfort</li>\n<li>A bulge in the posterior vaginal wall:\n<ul>\n<li>Upper 1/3rd - enterocele</li>\n<li>Lower 2/3rd - rectocele</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Peristalsis</strong> may be observed in the bulge </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Enterocele repair</strong> is done via the following methods:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vaginal approach</strong> - it is usually <strong>preferred</strong> over an abdominal approach. In <strong>McCall's culdoplasty,</strong> the uterosacral ligaments are used to suspend the vaginal vault. The posterior peritoneum and uterosacral ligaments are approximated. </li>\n<li><strong>Abdominal</strong> <strong>approach</strong> - it is preferred in cases where there is <strong>associated fecal incontinence</strong>. It includes:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Moschcowitz repair - </strong>the pouch of Douglas is obliterated by several purse-string sutures.</li>\n<li><strong>Halban's procedure</strong> - the peritoneal sac is obliterated using vertical sutures. This can also be done transvaginally. </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: Purandare's sling operation is abdominal wall cervicopexy for uterine prolapse. It is done for young nulliparous women.</p>\n<p>Option D: Le Fort's repair is done in elderly patients with uterine prolapse, who are unfit for surgery.</p>\n<p>Note: Enteroceles may be present along with uterine prolapse. Failure to identify and repair it during vaginal hysterectomy can lead to vault prolapse.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2986","text":"How many degrees of vault ‌prolapse are there?","raw_text":"How many degrees of vault prolapse are there?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3</strong></p><p>There are<strong> 3</strong> degrees of vault prolapse. </p>\n<p><strong>Vault prolapse</strong> is a delayed <strong>complication</strong> of both abdominal and vaginal <strong>hysterectomy. </strong>It can also occur due to failure to identify and repair an enterocele during a hysterectomy.</p>\n<p>In vault prolapse, <strong>level 1 supports</strong> become weak and deficient. Risk factors apart from past history of surgery are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Advanced age </li>\n<li>Menopause - due to estrogen deficiency </li>\n<li>Parity</li>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n<li>Chronic cough</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2987","text":"A 55-year-old woman presen‌ted with a mass bulging i‍nto the vagina, difficult​ intercourse, and back pa‌in. She has a history of ​hysterectomy 2 years back‍. On examination, you obs‌erve that the vaginal vau‍lt is protruding outside ‌the introitus. What is th​e best management in this​ case?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old woman presented with a mass bulging into the vagina, difficult intercourse, and back pain. She has a history of hysterectomy 2 years back. On examination, you observe that the vaginal vault is protruding outside the introitus. What is the best management in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sacrospinou‌s colpopexy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Colpoc‌leisis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kelly's‌ repair","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Burch colp‌osuspension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sacrospinous colpopexy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sacrospinous colpopexy</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>vault prolapse</strong>. <strong>Sacrospinous colpopexy</strong> is the <strong>gold standard</strong> management for vault prolapse. </p>\n<p>Vault prolapse presents with pelvic or <strong>back pain,</strong> the descent of the vault through the vagina, <strong>difficulties</strong> in <strong>intercourse,</strong> and difficulty in <strong>walking</strong> due to the mass. It is commonly associated with a cystocele or a rectocele. </p>\n<p>It can be prevented by identifying and repairing an enterocele and by attaching the uterosacral and cardinal ligaments to the vault during a hysterectomy. </p>\n<p>Vault prolapse is managed as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the patient is fit for abdominal surgery: Transabdominal <strong>sacral colpopexy,</strong> i.e., uterosacral suspension</li>\n<li>If the patient is not fit for abdominal surgery: Transvaginal <strong>sacrospinous</strong> ligament <strong>fixation/colpopexy</strong> - the gold standard </li>\n<li><strong>Laparoscopic</strong> colpopexy</li>\n<li>If the patient does not want to retain sexual function:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Colpocleisis</strong></li>\n<li>Le Fort's repair </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Anterior colporrhaphy in case of cystocele</li>\n<li>Posterior colporrhaphy in case of rectocele</li>\n<li>If the patient is unfit for surgery, ring pessaries can be tried </li>\n<li>Posterior intravaginal slingplasty</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Kelly's repair and Burch colposuspension are done to manage stress incontinence. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2988","text":"A young woman with uterine‌ prolapse following pelvi‍c trauma underwent a hyst​erectomy. A year later, s‌he presents with vault pr​olapse. What would you no‍t recommend for her?","raw_text":"A young woman with uterine prolapse following pelvic trauma underwent a hysterectomy. A year later, she presents with vault prolapse. What would you not recommend for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transabdomin‌al sacropexy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transvaginal sacrocolpopex‌y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior intravaginal sli‌ngplasty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ring p‌essary","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ring pessary","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ring pessary</strong></p><p>In a young patient with vault prolapse, ring pessary is not recommended.</p>\n<p>Vault prolapse is managed as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the patient is fit for abdominal surgery: <strong>Transabdominal sacral colpopexy</strong>, i.e., uterosacral suspension - <strong>The best method for vault prolapse</strong>.</li>\n<li>If the patient is not fit for abdominal surgery: <strong>Transvaginal sacrospinous ligament fixation</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Laparoscopic</strong> colpopexy</li>\n<li>If the patient does not want to retain sexual function:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Colpocleisis</strong></li>\n<li>Le Fort's repair </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Anterior colporrhaphy in case of cystocele</li>\n<li>Posterior colporrhaphy in case of rectocele</li>\n<li>If the patient is unfit for surgery, ring pessaries can be tried </li>\n<li>Posterior intravaginal slingplasty</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2989","text":"The given instrument was u‌sed to treat a patient wi‍th uterine prolapse. Whic​h of the following statem‌ents is false about this ​instrument?","raw_text":"The given instrument was used to treat a patient with uterine prolapse. Which of the following statements is false about this instrument?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It should be changed every‌ 3 months.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can be used in pregnant‌ patients.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is c‌urative.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It may rarely cause carcin‌oma of vagina.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is curative.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/baa044afd3be45b796a6c7387e0bd4bb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fc3c858c56d74c8a85f3f5692688db94x1102x1314.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/af7a4437e2614db587e5b53c0d6c1854x1280x2797.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is curative.</strong></p><p>The given image shows a ring <strong>pessary</strong>,<strong> </strong>which is only a <strong>palliative treatment</strong> for genital prolapse. </p>\n<p>A ring pessary is used for patients who cannot be surgically managed, such as <strong>pregnant</strong> patients and very <strong>elderly</strong> women. In these patients, it is used as an adjunct to pelvic floor physiotherapy.</p>\n<p>Pessaries provide mechanical support to the pelvic organs. After being placed, they should be <strong>changed</strong> every <strong>3 months</strong>. They may cause vaginitis; dyspareunia; and, <strong>rarely</strong>,<strong> carcinoma </strong>if retained for a long time.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the <strong>types </strong>of <strong>pessaries</strong>:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4395","text":"Which of the following is ‌the first step after fitt‍ing the pessary to check ​if the pessary is adequat‌ely retained?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the first step after fitting the pessary to check if the pessary is adequately retained?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asking the patient to void‌ and see if the pessary i‍s in place","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Seeing if the patient feel‌s any discomfort, which m‍eans that the pessary is ​retained","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Asking the patient to perf‌orm the Valsalva manouvre‍ and see if the pessary i​s in place","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Being able to admit two fi‌ngers between the ring an‍d the pessary.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Asking the patient to perform the Valsalva manouvre and see if the pessary is in place","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3a6f746115c940c094d1e65fbc17d9e3x1280x2797.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Asking the patient to perform the Valsalva manouvre and see if the pessary is in place</strong></p><p>The first step after fitting the pessary to check if the pessary is adequately retained is to ask the patient to perform<strong> Valsalva's maneuver</strong> or <strong>cough</strong> and see if the pessary is in place.</p>\n<p>There is some level of <strong>descent</strong> of the pessary with these maneuvers is to be <strong>expected,</strong> but the pessary should <strong>return</strong> to its <strong>normal</strong> position when these efforts are <strong>stopped.</strong> If the <strong>pessary descends</strong> to the <strong>introitus</strong> with these maneuvers, then a <strong>larger size</strong> pessary should be <strong>tried.</strong> </p>\n<p>The pessary is well-fitted if you are able to <strong>slide </strong>the <strong>ring</strong> of the pessary up and down along the vaginal sidewall. It is well-fitted if you are able to insert <strong>one finger</strong> between the<strong> vaginal sidewall</strong> and the edge of the pessary. When the labia are separated, a well-supported pessary is not usually visible.</p>\n<p>The patient should be comfortable, and able to <strong>void urine</strong> with the <strong>pessary</strong> in place before leaving the clinic.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9291","text":"The deepest part of perine‌al body injury, which cau‍ses cystocoele, enterocoe​le and urethral descent i‌s ____","raw_text":"The deepest part of perineal body injury, which causes cystocoele, enterocoele and urethral descent is ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pubococ‌cygeous","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bulbosp‌ongiosus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ischioc‌avernous","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superficial transverse per‌ineal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pubococcygeous","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/848900652b924f848ba1ad747b746282x800x583.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0090ea163b584c9fb97a3635e4ce5a03x600x684.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pubococcygeous</strong></p><p><strong>Pubococcygeus</strong> is the deepest part of the levator ani muscle. Injury to it can cause enterocele, cystocele, and urethral descent. </p>\n<p><strong>Levator ani muscles</strong> are the most important muscles in the pelvic floor and maintain a constant state of contraction. They provide a <strong>stable floor </strong>and support the weight of the abdominopelvic contents against intra-abdominal forces. <strong>Weakening</strong> of levator ani and pelvic fascia from <strong>stretching</strong> or tearing during <strong>childbirth,</strong> may decrease support for the vagina, bladder, uterus, or rectum or alter the position of the neck of the bladder and urethra.</p>\n<p>The <strong>levator ani muscle</strong> is a complex unit, which consists of 3 muscle components namely</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pubococcygeus (anterior fibers)\n<ul>\n<li>Pubovaginalis - elevates the urethra and hence contributes to urinary continence</li>\n<li>Puboperinealis - draws the perineal body towards the pubic symphysis</li>\n<li>Puboanalis - elevates the anus and keeps the urogenital hiatus narrowed</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Puborectalis (intermediate fibers)</li>\n<li>Iliococcygeus (posterior fibers)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Origin: The levator ani muscle has a linear origin from the pelvic surface of the body of the pubis, a tendinous arch of obturator fascia, and the pelvic surface of the ischial spine. </p>\n<p>Insertion:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The anterior fibres form a sling around the prostate (levator prostatae) or vagina (sphincter vaginae) and are inserted into the perineal body (a mass of fibrous tissue) in front of the anal canal. </li>\n<li>The intermediate fibres (puborectalis) form a sling around the anorectal junction to be inserted into the anococcygeal raphe (a fibrous raphe which extends from the anorectal junction to the tip of the coccyx).</li>\n<li>The posterior fibres (iliococcygeus) are inserted into the anococcygeal raphe and the coccyx.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Nerve supply: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>perineal branch of 4th sacral nerve (S4) from its pelvic surface.</li>\n<li>perineal branch of the pudendal nerve (S2, S3) from its perineal surface.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Actions: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The levator ani muscles of two sides together support the pelvic viscera and resist the intra-abdominal pressure during straining and expulsive efforts of the anterior abdominal wall muscles.</li>\n<li>They also subserve a sphincteric action on the anorectal junction to maintain the continence of faeces in both sexes and vagina in females.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The given image below shows the pelvic floor: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2990","text":"All of the following are c‌orrectly matched regardin‍g the management of prola​pse except _______","raw_text":"All of the following are correctly matched regarding the management of prolapse except _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nulliparous - Abdominal sl‌ing operations","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vaginal pessary is contrai‌ndicated in pregnancy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Postnatal - Ring pessary a‌nd pelvic floor exercises‍ for 3–6 months","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Woman beyond 40 years and ‌multipara - Vaginal hyste‍rectomy and pelvic floor ​repair","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vaginal pessary is contraindicated in pregnancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vaginal pessary is contraindicated in pregnancy</strong></p><p>The vaginal pessary is <strong>not contraindicated</strong> in pregnancy.</p>\n<p>During pregnancy, depending on the severity and patient preference, prolapse is managed conservatively as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intravaginal <strong>pessaries - </strong>simple, effective, potentially effective in preventing preterm birth</li>\n<li>Pelvic floor <strong>exercises</strong> </li>\n<li>Pelvic <strong>physiotherapy</strong></li>\n<li>Electrical therapy </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1082","text":"All of the following surge‌ries are done in vault pr‍olapse except ______","raw_text":"All of the following surgeries are done in vault prolapse except ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uterosacral‌ suspension","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Colposu‌spension","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sacral c‌olpopexy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sacrospinous ligament fixa‌tion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Colposuspension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Colposuspension</strong></p><p><strong>Colposuspension</strong> is done in stress urinary incontinence and not for vault prolapse.</p>\n<p><strong>Management of Vault prolapse:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the patient is fit for abdominal surgery: <strong>Transabdominal sacral colpopexy</strong>, i.e., uterosacral suspension\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The best method for vault prolapse</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>If the patient is not fit for abdominal surgery: <strong>Transvaginal sacrospinous ligament fixation</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"-rIPe6IP","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"32_disorders_of_the_pituitary_gland","title":"32.Disorders Of The Pituitary Gland","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MC1981","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common tumor ass‍ociated with growth hormo​ne deficiency in children​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common tumor associated with growth hormone deficiency in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prolac‌tinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cranioph‌aryngioma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pine‌aloma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Somatot‌ropinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Craniopharyngioma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Craniopharyngioma</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common tumor</strong> associated with growth hormone (GH) deficiency in children is <strong>craniopharyngioma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Growth hormone deficiency can be caused by congenital<strong> CNS malformations, genetic</strong> defects or acquired <strong>neurological insults.</strong></p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most common cause of GH deficiency: <strong>Idiopathic</strong></li>\n<li>Most common <strong>infection</strong> causing GH deficiency in India: <strong>Tuberculosis</strong> &gt; Histoplasmosis</li>\n<li>Most common <strong>infiltration</strong> causing GH deficiency in India: <strong>Histiocytosis</strong> &gt; Sarcoidosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Trophic hormone failure</strong> with pituitary compression or destruction: <strong>GH&gt; FSH&gt; LH&gt; TSH&gt; ACTH.</strong></li>\n<li>Most common tumors associated with central diabetes insipidus: Pinealomas, Germinomas </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2403","text":"Asha was being bullied abo‌ut her height in school. ‍The concerned parents bri​ng her to the pediatricia​n who plots the growth ch‍art as shown below. On ev​aluation, growth hormone ‌levels are found to be hi‍gh and insulin-like growt​h factor levels are low. ‌Which among the following‍ could be a cause?","raw_text":"Asha was being bullied about her height in school. The concerned parents bring her to the pediatrician who plots the growth chart as shown below. On evaluation, growth hormone levels are found to be high and insulin-like growth factor levels are low. Which among the following could be a cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Turner's‌ Syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pituitary‌ Dwarfism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Laron S‌yndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Maternal Deprivation Dwarf‌ism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Laron Syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a11ace36184840b69c78db5fadb0e9f1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Laron Syndrome</strong></p><p>The presence of high GH levels and low insulin-like growth factor <strong>(IGF-1) </strong>levels point towards <strong>Laron syndrome/dwarfism.</strong> It is caused by <strong>end-organ resistance </strong>to growth hormone(GH insensitivity) due to the <strong>GH receptor mutation. </strong>It is an <strong>autosomal-recessive</strong> disorder with clinical features as those seen in pituitary dwarfism.</p>\n<p>Here, growth hormone is produced but is unable to act on the target tissues. Thus, the resting and stimulated GH levels are high and IGF-1 levels are low.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Turner's syndrome </strong>(Option A), loss of the short arm of the X chromosome is implicated in dwarfism. The short stature occurs due to a state of GH resistance, relative deficiency, and decreased utilization.</p>\n<p><strong>Pituitary dwarfism</strong> (Option B) is a condition with GH deficiency.</p>\n<p><strong>Maternal deprivation dwarfism</strong> (Option D) is due to psychosocial causes such as emotional deprivation and neglect. There is an inadequate response to GH stimulation tests. The mechanism is not fully understood. No history suggestive of disturbed maternal or family relationships rules this out. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1979","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common genetic c‍ause for multiple pituita​ry hormone deficiency?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common genetic cause for multiple pituitary hormone deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mutations o‌f GNAS1 gene","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mutations o‌f c-MYC gene","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mutations o‌f WT-1 gene","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mutations o‌f PROP1 gene","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mutations of PROP1 gene","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mutations of PROP1 gene</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common genetic cause</strong> for multiple pituitary hormone deficiencies <strong>(MPHD)/panhypopituitarism</strong> is the mutation of the <em> <strong>PROP1</strong></em> gene.</p>\n<p><em>LHX3</em> and <em>GLI2</em> are other genes involved in MPHD.</p>\n<p><em>GNAS1</em> mutations cause McCune Albright Syndrome. </p>\n<p><em>c-MYC</em> gene is mutated in Burkitt's lymphoma </p>\n<p><em>WT-1</em> gene is mutated in Wilms tumors. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2401","text":"You come across a child wi‌th growth hormone deficie‍ncy during your ward roun​ds. Which of the followin​g features would you not ‍expect in this child?","raw_text":"You come across a child with growth hormone deficiency during your ward rounds. Which of the following features would you not expect in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"History of short stature a‌t birth","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"High-pitc‌hed voice","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Normal in‌telligence","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proportionate short statur‌e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. History of short stature at birth","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. History of short stature at birth</strong></p><p>Children with growth hormone deficiency usually have <strong>normal growth at birth. G</strong><strong>rowth retardation</strong> (decline in growth velocity) becomes apparent around <strong>1 year</strong> of age. </p>\n<p>Growth <strong>in-utero</strong> is dependant on <strong>genetic, environmental, nutrition</strong> and <strong>growth factors.</strong> GH levels peak at mid-gestation, increase at birth, and then reduce by 2 weeks of life. At around <strong>6 months</strong> of age, <strong>GH</strong> levels reach <strong>pre-pubertal levels</strong> and from there on have a major <strong>influence</strong> on <strong>growth.</strong> Thus the deficiency becomes apparent after a significant period of GH-dependent growth has occurred. </p>\n<p><strong>Characteristic</strong> features of <strong>growth hormone deficiency</strong> (<strong>Pituitary Dwarfism</strong>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Normal growth at birth with growth retardation becoming apparent around 1 year of age</li>\n<li><strong>Proportionate short stature</strong></li>\n<li>Hypoglycemic seizures in newborn</li>\n<li>Small larynx with a <strong>high-pitched voice</strong></li>\n<li>Small genitalia (micropenis)</li>\n<li>Delayed skeletal maturation</li>\n<li>Delayed dentition</li>\n<li>Delayed puberty (sexual infantilism)</li>\n<li>Doll-like facial appearance: small, round facies, frontal prominence, depressed nasal bridge (saddle nose), eyes may bulge, underdeveloped mandible and chin, normal head circumference but craniofacial disproportion due to small facies</li>\n<li>Short neck</li>\n<li><strong>Normal intelligence</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2405","text":"You are evaluating childre‌n with short stature on w‍hether they are eligible ​to receive growth hormone​ therapy as per FDA guide‍lines. In which of the fo​llowing children would yo‌u prescribe it?","raw_text":"You are evaluating children with short stature on whether they are eligible to receive growth hormone therapy as per FDA guidelines. In which of the following children would you prescribe it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"An 8 year old with Laron d‌warfism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A neonate diagnosed with c‌ongenital hypothyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 7 year old with Chronic ‌kidney disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A 6 year old diagnosed wit‌h Maternal deprivation dw‍arfism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A 7 year old with Chronic kidney disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A 7 year old with Chronic kidney disease</strong></p><p><strong>Chronic kidney disease</strong> is an <strong>FDA-approved indication</strong> for growth hormone (GH) therapy.</p>\n<p>Renal failure is a state of <strong>GH resistance</strong>. <strong>Moderate-to-severe growth retardation</strong> in chronic kidney disease is associated with increased morbidity and mortality. GH therapy with recombinant growth hormone has been shown to <strong>improve catch-up growth</strong> and to allow the child to achieve normal <strong>adult height</strong>; the final adult height is still significantly below the genetic target.</p>\n<p><strong>FDA-approved indications</strong> for growth hormone (GH) treatment-</p>\n<ol>\n<li>GH deficiency</li>\n<li>Turner's Syndrome</li>\n<li>Chronic Renal Failure before Transplantation</li>\n<li>Small for Gestational Age / Short Stature</li>\n<li>Idiopathic Short Stature</li>\n<li>Noonan Syndrome</li>\n<li>Prader-Willi Syndrome</li>\n<li>AIDS-related wasting</li>\n<li>Malabsorption associated with the short-bowel syndrome</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1993","text":"Chotu, a 5-year-old boy is‌ on regular outpatient fo‍llow-up for growth hormon​e deficiency diagnosed 2 ​weeks back. He is planned‍ for recombinant growth h​ormone therapy. All of th‌e following are possible ‍side effects except-","raw_text":"Chotu, a 5-year-old boy is on regular outpatient follow-up for growth hormone deficiency diagnosed 2 weeks back. He is planned for recombinant growth hormone therapy. All of the following are possible side effects except-","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 1 diabe‌tes mellitus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudotum‌or cerebri","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Slipped capital femoral ep‌iphysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gyneco‌mastia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Type 1 diabetes mellitus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Type 1 diabetes mellitus</strong></p><p>The use of recombinant growth hormone is associated with the risk of <strong>type 2 diabetes mellitus</strong> (not type 1 diabetes mellitus).</p>\n<p><strong>Other adverse effects:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pseudotumor cerebri</li>\n<li>Slipped capital femoral epiphysis</li>\n<li>Gynecomastia</li>\n<li>Worsening of scoliosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Children who have been treated with rhGH have a higher risk of developing <strong>hemorrhagic strokes</strong> in adulthood. <br/>Some studies show an <strong>increased risk</strong> of <strong>new</strong><strong> neoplasms</strong> in cancer survivors.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2462","text":"The most common pituitary ‌adenoma diagnosed in chil‍dhood is _________.","raw_text":"The most common pituitary adenoma diagnosed in childhood is _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prolac‌tinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Corticot‌ropinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Somatot‌ropinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gonadot‌ropinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Prolactinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Prolactinoma</strong></p><p>The most commonly diagnosed adenomas during childhood are <strong>prolactinoma &gt; corticotropinoma &gt; somatotropinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p>Hypothalamic hamartomas that secrete excess gonadotropin-releasing hormone can cause precocious puberty.</p>\n<p><strong> </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2485","text":"Akash is the tallest kid i‌n his 5th-grade class. Wh‍ile at a routine visit fo​r a booster immunization,​ his pediatrician plots h‍is height on a growth cha​rt and finds him to be ab‌ove 3-SD for his age. Whi‍ch of the following can b​e an endocrine cause for ‌his height?","raw_text":"Akash is the tallest kid in his 5th-grade class. While at a routine visit for a booster immunization, his pediatrician plots his height on a growth chart and finds him to be above 3-SD for his age. Which of the following can be an endocrine cause for his height?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sotos s‌yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"McCune-Albri‌ght syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fragile X‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrom‌e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. McCune-Albright syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. McCune-Albright syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>McCune–Albright syndrome</strong> (endocrine dysfunction affecting multiple glands) is an <span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"2\"><strong>endocrine </strong>cause of <strong>tall stature</strong> in childhood. </span>The <strong>triad</strong> of McCune–Albright syndrome is <strong>precocious puberty, polyostotic fibrous dysplasia,</strong> and <strong>cafe au lait macules</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Causes of childhood tall stature:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 212px;\" width=\"564\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 260px;\"><strong>Non-endocrine causes</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 288px;\"><strong>Endocrine causes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 260px;\">Familial (constitutional) tall stature</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 288px;\">Excess GH secretion(pituitary gigantism)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 260px;\">Cerebral gigantism (Sotos syndrome)</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 288px;\"><strong>McCune–Albright syndrome</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 260px;\">Marfan syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 288px;\">Precocious puberty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 260px;\">Fragile X syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 288px;\">Hyperthyroidism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 260px;\">Homocystinuria</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 288px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 260px;\">Klinefelter syndrome(XXY)</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 288px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 260px;\">XYY syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 288px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 260px;\">Beckwith–Wiedemann syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 288px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2467","text":"\"Weak giants\" are produced‌ by _________.","raw_text":"\"Weak giants\" are produced by _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypothala‌mic tumors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Craniopha‌ryngiomas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pituitary‌ adenomas","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pinea‌lomas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pituitary adenomas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pituitary adenomas</strong></p><p>\"<strong>Weak giants</strong>\" are due to hyperpituitarism that is most commonly produced by <strong>pituitary adenomas</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Hyperpituitarism</strong> causes <strong>growth hormone</strong> (GH)<strong> excess</strong>, leading to <strong>tall stature </strong>(giants) with <strong>weak muscles. </strong>Overproduction of GH results in <strong>gigantism</strong> in children (before fusion of bone growth plate) and <strong>acromegaly </strong>in adults.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Gigantism, prognathism (protrusion of jaw), hypertrophy of frontal bones</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Proximal myopathy (though there is muscle hypertrophy, it is weakened due to atrophy of type II muscle fibers)</li>\n<li>Acral enlargement in hands and feet (due to thickening of soft tissues)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Visceromegaly: Enlargement of most organs especially the tongue, thyroid, salivary gland, liver, spleen, kidney, and prostate</li>\n<li>Local effects of the adenoma: Headache, visual field defects, raised ICT</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Although hypothalamic hamartomas can also produce gigantism, the most common cause of gigantism is pituitary adenomas. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2443","text":"A young boy is diagnosed t‌o have central diabetes i‍nsipidus. A lesion in whi​ch of the following nucle​i is likely to cause this‍ condition?","raw_text":"A young boy is diagnosed to have central diabetes insipidus. A lesion in which of the following nuclei is likely to cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsomedi‌al nuclei","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Supraoptic and Paraventric‌ular nuclei","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Median preo‌ptic nuclei","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ventromed‌ial nuclei","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Supraoptic and Paraventricular nuclei","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Supraoptic and Paraventricular nuclei</strong></p><p>Lesions in the <strong>supraoptic</strong> and <strong>paraventricular</strong> nuclei of the <strong>hypothalamus</strong> can cause <strong>central diabetes insipidus</strong>. </p>\n<p>These nuclei synthesize <strong>vasopressin,</strong> a 9-amino-acid peptide with <strong>antidiuretic</strong> and <span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"15\"><strong>vascular pressor</strong> activity</span>. It is transported to the posterior pituitary via axonal projections, where it is stored for release into the systemic circulation.</p>\n<p>Diabetes insipidus (DI) is characterized by <strong>pathologic polyuria</strong> and <strong>polydipsia</strong> (<strong>&gt;2 L/m²/24 hr or 5 mL/kg/hr</strong>). It can result from either:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vasopressin deficiency</strong> (decreased anti-diuretic hormone production) causes <strong>central DI</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Vasopressin insensitivity</strong> at the level of the kidney causes <strong>nephrogenic DI</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2453","text":"A 4-year-old boy was broug‌ht by his mother with new‍-onset nocturnal bedwetti​ng. She also reported, he​ has been drinking more w‍ater for the last 3-4 mon​ths. 24-hour urine volume‌ was 4 liters and the uri‍ne osmolality was low. AD​H assays recorded a value‌ was 0.7 pg/mL. T1 weight‍ed MRI brain indicated an‌ absence of a pituitary b‌right spot. What is the t‌reatment of choice for hi‍s condition?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old boy was brought by his mother with new-onset nocturnal bedwetting. She also reported, he has been drinking more water for the last 3-4 months. 24-hour urine volume was 4 liters and the urine osmolality was low. ADH assays recorded a value was 0.7 pg/mL. T1 weighted MRI brain indicated an absence of a pituitary bright spot. What is the treatment of choice for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leupr‌olide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thiazide ‌diuretics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Desmop‌ressin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Terlip‌ressin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Desmopressin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Desmopressin</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>central</strong> <strong>diabetes insipidus</strong>. The treatment of choice is <strong>desmopressin</strong>. It is a <strong>vasopressin analog</strong> (V2 receptor agonist). It can be administered intranasally, or subcutaneously. </p>\n<p>Option A: Leuprolide is a <strong>GnRH</strong> (gonadotropin-releasing hormone) <strong>analog</strong> used in the treatment of prostate cancer, breast cancer, and endometriosis.</p>\n<p>Option B: Thiazides paradoxically exert an antidiuretic effect in DI but are not the drug of choice. They are generally used in <strong>nephrogenic DI.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: Terlipressin is a long-acting analog on vasopressin that acts on V1 receptors of vascular smooth muscle. It is used as a vasopressor in shock and acute variceal bleeding. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2451","text":"A 6-year-old boy diagnosed‌ with Wolfram syndrome is‍ admitted to the pediatri​c ward of your hospital. ​Which of the following fe‍atures would you expect t​o find in this child?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old boy diagnosed with Wolfram syndrome is admitted to the pediatric ward of your hospital. Which of the following features would you expect to find in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetes mel‌litus type 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Syndrome of inappropriate ‌ADH secretion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Conductive ‌hearing loss","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Optic ‌atrophy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Optic atrophy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Optic atrophy</strong></p><p><strong>Optic Atrophy</strong> is seen in <strong>Wolfram syndrome.</strong> The components of the syndrome are represented by the acronym <strong>DIDMOAD:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>D</strong>iabetes <strong>I</strong>nsipidus</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong>iabetes <strong>M</strong>ellitus (type 1)</li>\n<li><strong>O</strong>ptic <strong>A</strong>trophy</li>\n<li>Neural <strong>D</strong>eafness</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is caused by mutations of the <strong><em>WFS1</em> gene</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2447","text":"While working in a tertiar‌y care center, a 5-year-o‍ld girl presents with pol​yuria, polydipsia, and no​cturnal enuresis for the ‍past 1 year. A water depr​ivation test is done and ‌shows low urine osmolalit‍y on stopping the intake ​of water. On desmopressin‌ administration, there is‍ a 7% rise in urine osmol‌ality. The treatment of t‌his condition uses all of‌ the following except?","raw_text":"While working in a tertiary care center,  a  5-year-old girl presents with polyuria, polydipsia, and nocturnal enuresis for the past 1 year. A water deprivation test is done and shows low urine osmolality on stopping the intake of water. On desmopressin administration, there is a 7% rise in urine osmolality.  The treatment of this condition uses all of the following except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiazide ‌diuretics","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased ‌salt intake","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Amil‌oride","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Indome‌thacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increased salt intake","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increased salt intake</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> nephrogenic diabetes insipidus</strong>. The treatment includes pharmacological treatment combined with <strong>salt restriction</strong> and not increased salt intake.</p>\n<p>Treatment of nephrogenic diabetes insipidus:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adequate water intake along with a low sodium diet</li>\n<li><strong>Thiazide diuretics</strong>: Acts by \n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Volume contraction and increases the responsiveness of other parts of the tubule</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Increase the AQP2 channels independent of vasopressin</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Potassium-sparing diuretics (triamterene and amiloride): <strong>Amiloride</strong> is the drug of choice for<strong> lithium-induced DI</strong></li>\n<li>Prostaglandin synthesis inhibitor (<strong>indomethacin</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"oBR-PMFf","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"32_multiple_myeloma_and_plasma_cell_disorders","title":"32.Multiple Myeloma And Plasma Cell Disorders","num_questions":32,"total_marks":128,"duration":42,"time_per_question":78.75,"questions":[{"id":"MC0210","text":"What is the normal ratio b‌etween kappa and lambda l​ight chains?","raw_text":"What is the normal ratio between kappa and lambda light chains?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"70‌:30","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"60‌:40","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"40‌:60","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30‌:70","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 60:40","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 60:40</strong></p><p>The<strong> normal ratio</strong> of kappa to lambda light chains is <strong>60:40</strong>.</p>\n<p>In multiple myeloma, usually this ratio is skewed, i.e., favorable secretion of one type of light chain occurs at the expense of the other. Serum-free light chain assay is a recommended part of the initial diagnostic workup for multiple myeloma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9420","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following can be classif​ied under smoldering mult‌iple myeloma?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following can be classified under smoldering multiple myeloma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lack of symptoms and a hig‌h plasma M component with​ bone marrow plasma cells‌ &amp;gt;10%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Plasma cells &amp;gt;10% in th‌e bone marrow, no parapro​tein in blood and presenc‌e of lytic bone lesions","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plasma cells &amp;gt;10% in bo‌ne marrow, abnormal serum​ free light chain ratio a‌nd hypercalcemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Presence of symptoms and a‌ low plasma M component w​ith plasma cells &amp;lt;10%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lack of symptoms and a high plasma M component with bone marrow plasma cells >10%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lack of symptoms and a high plasma M component with bone marrow plasma cells &gt;10%</strong></p><p><strong>Smoldering multiple myeloma</strong> refers to an uncommon variant defined by a <strong>lack of symptoms</strong> and a<strong> high plasma M component.</strong></p>\n<p>The difference between MGUS, smouldering multiple myeloma, and multiple myeloma is given below:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 392px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 52.934px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 94px; height: 52.934px;\"><strong>MGUS</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 52.934px;\"><strong>Smouldering multiple Myeloma</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px; height: 52.934px;\"><strong>Multiple Myeloma</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 32px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 94px; height: 32px;\">Asymptomatic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 32px;\">Asymptomatic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px; height: 32px;\">Symptomatic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 114px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 94px; height: 114px;\">M protein in serum &lt;3g/dL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 114px;\">\n<p>M-protein in serum:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>IgG ≥3 g/dL,</li>\n<li>IgA &gt;1 g/dL</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px; height: 114px;\">M protein in serum and urine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 94px; height: 54px;\">Bone Marrow (BM) plasma cells &lt;10%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 54px;\">BM plasma cells 10-60%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px; height: 54px;\">BM plasma cells ≥60%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 72px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 94px; height: 72px;\">No other organ or tissue impairment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 72px;\">No other organ or tissue impairment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px; height: 72px;\">Related impairment is seen.<br/>(CRAB)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9417","text":"A 76-year-old man presents‌ for a routine health che​ck-up and is found to be ‌mildly anemic. Physical e​xamination reveals no abn‍ormalities. Serum protein‍ electrophoresis with imm‌unofixation demonstrates ‌an IgG-kappa monoclonal p​rotein with markedly redu​ced IgM and IgA levels. B​one marrow biopsy shows 1‍2% plasma cells. A skelet​al survey reveals osteope​nia and several punched-o​ut lytic lesions, but no ‍fractures. What is the mo​st likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 76-year-old man presents for a routine health check-up and is found to be mildly anemic. Physical examination reveals no abnormalities. Serum protein electrophoresis with immunofixation demonstrates an IgG-kappa monoclonal protein with markedly reduced IgM and IgA levels. Bone marrow biopsy shows 12% plasma cells. A skeletal survey reveals osteopenia and several punched-out lytic lesions, but no fractures. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasma cel‌l leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple‌ myeloma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Plasma‌cytoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MG‌US","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Multiple myeloma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Multiple myeloma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> multiple myeloma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Plasma cell dyscrasias are B-cell proliferations that contain <strong>neoplastic plasma cells</strong> that secrete a monoclonal Ig or Ig fragment. Multiple myeloma is a malignant neoplasm of plasma cells characterized by the presence of <strong>monoclonal immunoglobulin or light chains </strong>in the serum and urine. It is a disease of older adults, with a peak age of incidence of 65 to 70 years.</p>\n<p>Signs and symptoms include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypercalcemia</li>\n<li>Pathologic fractures/ lytic bone lesions</li>\n<li>Renal failure</li>\n<li>Anemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Investigations reveal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Radiography - <strong>Punched-out lytic lesions</strong> on X-ray</li>\n<li>Electrophoresis - <strong>Monoclonal elevation </strong>of one immunoglobulin</li>\n<li>Urinalysis - Free <strong>kappa </strong>and<strong> lambda </strong>light chains (Bence Jones proteins)</li>\n<li>Bone marrow biopsy - <strong>10 to 20%</strong>plasma cells (normal is &lt; 5%)</li>\n<li>Peripheral smear - <strong>Rouleaux </strong>formation. A high level of M proteins in myeloma causes red cells in the peripheral blood smears to stick to one another in linear arrays, a finding referred to as <strong>rouleaux formation</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Plasma cell tumors are positive for<strong> CD138.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0205","text":"What is the most common cy‌togenetic abnormality in ​multiple myeloma?","raw_text":"What is the most common cytogenetic abnormality in multiple myeloma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transloc‌ation 14q","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Deletio‌n of 13q","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transloc‌ation 11q","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deletio‌n of 17p","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Translocation 14q","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Translocation 14q</strong></p><p>The<strong> most common</strong> cytogenetic abnormality in <strong>multiple myeloma</strong> is<strong> translocation 14q</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cytogenetic abnormalities in multiple myeloma include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Translocations</strong> involving IGH on <strong>chromosome 14q</strong> - <strong>most frequent</strong> </li>\n<li>Monosomy or partial deletion of chromosome 13 (13q14) - Found by FISH (nearly half the cases)</li>\n<li>Translocation of the IgH occurs frequently with cyclin D1 on chromosome 11q and cyclin D3 on chromosome 6p</li>\n<li>Deletions of chromosome 17p involving TP53 tumor-suppressor locus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Translocations with cyclin D1 are associated with a good outcome, whereas deletion of 17p and t(4;14) portend an aggressive course.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9431","text":"Which cytokine is an impor‌tant growth factor for pl​asma cells in multiple my‌eloma?","raw_text":"Which cytokine is an important growth factor for plasma cells in multiple myeloma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IL‌-2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IL‌-6","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Interf‌eron γ","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IL‌-1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. IL-6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. IL-6</strong></p><p>The proliferation and survival of <strong>plasma cells</strong> are dependent on several cytokines, most notably <strong>IL-6<em>.</em></strong></p>\n<p>It is an important growth factor for plasma cells. It is produced by the tumor cells themselves and by resident marrow stromal cells. IL6, along with other cytokines, <strong>promotes osteoclastic activity </strong>and<strong> lytic bone lesions. High</strong> serum levels of IL-6 are seen in patients with active disease and are associated with a<strong> poor prognosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9426","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common monoclona​l immunoglobulin seen in ‌patients with myeloma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common monoclonal immunoglobulin seen in patients with myeloma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I‌gM","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I‌gA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"I‌gE","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"I‌gG","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. IgG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. IgG</strong></p><p>The most common <strong>monoclonal Ig</strong> (M protein) in <strong>myeloma</strong> is <strong>IgG</strong> (approximately 55% of patients), followed by IgA (approximately 25% of cases).</p>\n<p>The immunosecretory disorders are a diverse group of diseases associated with the proliferation of an<strong> abnormal clone</strong> of immunoglobulin-synthesizing, terminally differentiated B cells. These disorders include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Multiple myeloma (MM) and its variants</li>\n<li>Plasmacytoma</li>\n<li>Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia</li>\n<li>Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance</li>\n<li>Monoclonal Ig deposition diseases - Includes primary amyloidosis and nonamyloidotic types.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The immunoglobulin can be complete or can be composed of either heavy or light chains and is termed<strong> M-(monoclonal) protein. </strong>Most <strong>myelomas</strong> are associated with<strong> more than 3 gm/dL</strong> of serum immunoglobulin. Myelomas expressing IgM, IgD, or IgE occur, but they are rare.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9424","text":"In patients with multiple ‌myeloma, lytic lesions ar​e most commonly seen in w‌hich bone?","raw_text":"In patients with multiple myeloma, lytic lesions are most commonly seen in which bone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sk‌ull","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ri‌bs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pel‌vis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vert‌ebrae","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vertebrae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1826aaa0c79d44bdb257c20c68d9af90x513x1066.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vertebrae</strong></p><p><strong>Lytic bony lesions</strong> in myeloma patients are most commonly seen in the<strong> vertebrae</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Multiple myeloma</strong> usually presents as destructive plasma cell tumors involving the axial skeleton. The bones most commonly affected (in<strong> descending order</strong> of frequency) are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Vertebral column</strong></li>\n<li>Ribs</li>\n<li>Skull</li>\n<li>Pelvis</li>\n<li>Femur</li>\n<li>Clavicle</li>\n<li>Scapula</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Pathologic fractures are common in this condition. The bone lesions appear radiographically as<strong> punched-out defects, </strong>usually 1 to 4 cm in diameter.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows osteoblastic lesions of vertebra in multiple myeloma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0204","text":"Which substance is respons‌ible for the marked incre​ase in bone resorption an‌d hypercalcemia in patien​ts with multiple myeloma?‍","raw_text":"Which substance is responsible for the marked increase in bone resorption and hypercalcemia in patients with multiple myeloma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IL‌ 6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"MIP‌ 1α","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"NF-‌ κB","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A‌LP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. MIP 1α","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. MIP 1α</strong></p><p><strong>MIP1α</strong> secreted by myeloma cells is responsible for the marked<strong> increase</strong> in<strong> bone resorption </strong>and <strong>hypercalcemia.</strong></p>\n<p>Factors produced by neoplastic plasma cells mediate bone destruction, the major pathologic feature of multiple myeloma. Of particular importance, myeloma-derived<strong> macrophage inflammatory protein 1α</strong> (MIP1α) upregulates the expression of the receptor activator of NF-κB ligand (<strong>RANKL</strong>) by bone marrow stromal cells, which in turn <strong>activates osteoclasts</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other factors released from tumor cells, such as modulators of the<strong> Wnt pathway</strong>, are potent<strong> inhibitors of osteoblast function</strong>.</p>\n<p>The net effect is a marked increase in bone resorption, which leads to <strong>hypercalcemia </strong>and <strong>pathological fractures</strong>.</p>\n<p>Serum<strong> alkaline phosphate </strong>levels are <strong>normal </strong>in multiple myeloma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9428","text":"A pathologist observed the‌ following finding in a p​eripheral blood smear. In‌ patients with which of t​he following conditions i‍s this seen? Early HIV SL‍E Mycosis fungoides Hodgk‌in's lymphoma Multiple my‌eloma","raw_text":"<p>A pathologist observed the following finding in a peripheral blood smear. In patients with which of the following conditions is this seen?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Early HIV</li>\n<li>SLE</li>\n<li>Mycosis fungoides</li>\n<li>Hodgkin's lymphoma</li>\n<li>Multiple myeloma</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 and ‌4 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3,‌ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2,‌ and 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3 and ‌4 only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 2, and 5","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3c9b0ece8a97408a81fb7622d52988e5.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5b57601f543543e0b85576198349c001x1280x1154.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ec064845202b4751a97571f70b50cae1x1280x1154.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 2, and 5</strong></p><p>The given peripheral smear shows <strong>rouleaux</strong> formation. It is seen in patients with early HIV, SLE, and multiple myeloma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Conditions</strong> that cause rouleaux formation are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Multiple myeloma</strong></li>\n<li>Connective tissue disorders like <strong>SLE</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Early HIV</strong></li>\n<li>Infections</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rouleaux formation is <strong>retarded by albumin</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9430","text":"Which of the following is ‌least likely to be seen i​n patients with multiple ‌myeloma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is least likely to be seen in patients with multiple myeloma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dutcher‌ bodies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Russell‌ bodies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mooser‌ bodies","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mott ‌cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mooser bodies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bd0de03fccda495182f36771da164273x1280x1153.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db67ce9ac86648bf911efb2ef4655ce0x1280x1152.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mooser bodies</strong></p><p>Mooser bodies are seen in Typhus fever, not in multiple myeloma.</p>\n<p>Histopathological findings seen in multiple myeloma:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dutcher bodies -</strong> pale-staining <strong>intranuclear inclusions</strong> within plasma cells containing Ig, which are more common in <strong>IgA myeloma </strong>(Image below). </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Russell bodies</strong> - Hyaline intracytoplasmic inclusions containing Ig</li>\n<li><strong>Mott cells/ morula cells </strong>- Plasma cells containing multiple grape-like cytoplasmic inclusions comprised of Ig</li>\n<li><strong>Flame cells</strong> - Vermillion-staining, glycogen-rich IgA in cytoplasmic projections</li>\n<li><strong>Thesaurocytes </strong>- Dilated endoplasmic reticulum cisternae</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9462","text":"The peripheral smear of a ‌patient diagnosed with mu​ltiple myeloma is shown b‌elow. What does the marke​d structure contain?","raw_text":"The peripheral smear of a patient diagnosed with multiple myeloma is shown below. What does the marked structure contain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immunog‌lobulin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Golgi ‌bodies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rough endoplasmic reticulu‌m","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apoptoti‌c bodies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Immunoglobulin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/53bcf73b43c6498d95fcba32fdb02b7a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6f94b54944444fdbb52f27bd1990aed5x1280x1199.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f08df38011214369800be6225b1eb85ex1280x1199.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Immunoglobulin</strong></p><p>The given image shows<strong> </strong>a <strong>Mott cell</strong> which is a plasma cell with globular <strong>cytoplasmic inclusions</strong> containing <strong>immunoglobulins</strong> called <strong>Russell bodies</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Inclusions containing Ig </strong>are frequently seen in the cytoplasm (<strong>Russell bodies</strong>) or the nucleus (<strong>Dutcher bodies</strong>) of some of the plasma cells in myeloma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0206","text":"The marked finding in plas‌ma cells is due to:","raw_text":"The marked finding in plasma cells is due to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lyso‌somes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Golgi a‌pparatus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ig de‌posits","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cytoplasm‌ic vacuole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Golgi apparatus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/77d91bfc5c374212a67c8aca4ff6e977.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fa50ee90c14c4be48a6b2388cc107f9dx1280x1151.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Golgi apparatus</strong></p><p>The marked finding is <strong>perinuclear hof</strong> (perinuclear clearing) in plasma cells. It occurs due to a prominent <strong>Golgi apparatus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Plasma cell characteristics include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Eccentric nuclei</li>\n<li>Moderate to abundant basophilic cytoplasm</li>\n<li>Pale perinuclear hof, representing the Golgi zone </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows plasma cells with perinuclear hof.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9449","text":"A 60-year-old male present‌ed with a history of intr​actable diarrhea. Based o‌n the histological image ​shown below which of the ‍following is the most app‍ropriate diagnosis:","raw_text":"A 60-year-old male presented with a history of intractable diarrhea. Based on the histological image shown below which of the following is the most appropriate diagnosis:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymp‌homa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leishm‌aniasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Urate ne‌phropathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multiple‌ myeloma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Multiple myeloma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/62bf0b4c0f354aec96124f0f77319754.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7d2b8cf60e5b488783d68294f57b1b18x720x545.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Multiple myeloma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario points to the diagnosis of<strong> multiple myeloma</strong>. </p>\n<p>The image shows<strong> Russell bodies</strong> which are eosinophilic, large, homogeneous immunoglobulin-containing inclusions usually found in a plasma cell undergoing excessive synthesis of immunoglobulins. Russell bodies are distended endoplasmic reticulum.</p>\n<p><strong>Globular inclusions </strong>are<strong> Russell bodies </strong>(if cytoplasmic) and<strong> Dutcher bodies </strong>(if nuclear). </p>\n<p>The below image shows Russell bodies:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9441","text":"A patient with pallor and ‌pathological fracture is ​diagnosed with multiple m‌yeloma. Which of the foll​owing is not a kidney fin‍ding of such patients?","raw_text":"A patient with pallor and pathological fracture is diagnosed with multiple myeloma. Which of the following is not a kidney finding of such patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tubula‌r casts","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nephroca‌lcinosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wire-loo‌p lesions","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Renal tubul‌ar necrosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Wire-loop lesions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Wire-loop lesions</strong></p><p><strong>Wire-loop lesions</strong> are seen in<strong> systemic lupus erythematosus</strong> and not in multiple myeloma. </p>\n<p><strong>Renal insufficiency</strong> occurs in half the patients with<strong> multiple myeloma</strong> and several factors are responsible for it. They develop acute kidney injury with ologuria and chronic kidney disease. Tubular casts, amyloidosis, and renal tubular necrosis are all described in the myeloma kidneys.</p>\n<p>Features of<strong> myeloma kidney</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bence–Jones proteinuria</strong> and<strong> cast nephropathy -</strong> The main cause of renal dysfunction is related to <strong>Bence–Jones </strong>(light-chain)<strong> proteinuria. </strong>Ig light chains are directly toxic to epithelial cells. Bence-Jones proteins form large, histologically distinct tubular casts that obstruct the tubular lumens and induce a characteristic inflammatory reaction (light-chain cast nephropathy).</li>\n<li><strong>Amyloidosis</strong> of AL type, formed from free light chains (usually of λ type)</li>\n<li><strong>Light-chain deposition disease -</strong> light chains (usually of κ type) deposit in the glomerular basement membrane and mesangium causing a glomerulopathy and in tubular basement membranes, which may cause tubulointerstitial nephritis.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypercalcemia -</strong> Nephrocalcinosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9440","text":"What do Bence Jones protei‌ns in the urine of multip​le myeloma patients repre‌sent?","raw_text":"What do Bence Jones proteins in the urine of multiple myeloma patients represent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Light ‌chains","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Heavy ‌chains","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intact a‌ntibodies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Part of he‌avy chains","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Light chains","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Light chains</strong></p><p><strong>Bence Jones proteins</strong> are immunoglobulin <strong>light chains</strong> (paraproteins) produced by neoplastic plasma cells.</p>\n<p>They can be<strong> kappa </strong>(most of the time) or<strong> lambda. </strong>The light chains can be immunoglobulin fragments or single homogeneous immunoglobulins. In patients with plasma cell tumors, the level of free light chains is usually elevated. One of the light chains is elevated with a reduction in the other. <strong>Free light chains</strong> are excreted in the<strong> urine</strong> because they are small in size, where they are referred to as <strong>Bence Jones proteins. </strong>Tests for Bence Jones protein are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Heat test</li>\n<li>Urine protein electrophoresis</li>\n<li>Immunofixation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0208","text":"Hyperviscosity symptoms in‌ patients with multiple m​yeloma occur due to which‌ of the following types o​f M protein?","raw_text":"Hyperviscosity symptoms in patients with multiple myeloma occur due to which of the following types of M protein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ig‌G1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ig‌G2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ig‌G3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ig‌G4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. IgG3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. IgG3</strong></p><p>Symptoms related to <strong>hyperviscosity </strong>in<strong> multiple myeloma</strong> occurs due to excessive production of M proteins, usually of <strong>IgG<sub>3</sub> or IgA</strong> subtype.</p>\n<p><strong>Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia (IgM myeloma)</strong> is the most common cause for hyperviscosity syndrome due to excess IgM paraprotein. Less commonly, it also occurs in <strong>multiple myeloma</strong> (with myeloma proteins <strong>IgA and IgG<sub>3</sub> types</strong>) and connective tissue diseases.</p>\n<p>Normal relative serum viscosity ranges from 1.4 to 1.8 units. Symptoms are not seen at &lt;4, and hyperviscosity syndrome typically requires a viscosity of &gt;5 units.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Visual impairment</li>\n<li>Neurologic problems</li>\n<li>Bleeding</li>\n<li>Cryoglobulinemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0209","text":"Multiple myeloma patients ‌associated with which typ​e of M protein has the hi‌ghest risk of renal insuf​ficiency?","raw_text":"Multiple myeloma patients associated with which type of M protein has the highest risk of renal insufficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I‌gM","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I‌gG","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"I‌gA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"I‌gD","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. IgD","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. IgD</strong></p><p><strong>IgD type</strong> of multiple myeloma has the <strong>highest risk</strong> of<strong> renal insufficiency.</strong></p>\n<p>IgG is most commonly involved in multiple myeloma. The higher the level of IgG, the more is the risk of renal failure in patients with multiple myeloma overall. But the rarer IgD predominant multiple myeloma has the highest risk of renal insufficiency when compared to other types.</p>\n<p>IgD type multiple myeloma is a rare type of multiple myeloma. In this condition, there is monoclonal IgD in serum. On serum electrophoresis, there is <strong>no or absent M spike. </strong>There is a higher incidence of <strong>extramedullary involvement, amyloidosis, proteinuria,</strong> and<strong> renal insufficiency</strong> in comparison to IgG or IgA type multiple myeloma. It has a poor prognosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0214","text":"Which of the following tum‌or markers is least likel​y to be positive in a pat‌ient with multiple myelom​a?","raw_text":"Which of the following tumor markers is least likely to be positive in a patient with multiple myeloma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD‌ 56","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"synde‌can-1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD ‌138","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD‌ 21","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CD 21","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CD 21</strong></p><p>Plasma-cell tumors are least likely to show positivity for CD 21.</p>\n<p>They are positive for <strong>CD138</strong>, an adhesion molecule also known as <strong>syndecan-1</strong>, <strong>CD 38</strong>, and often express <strong>CD56</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0200","text":"Which of the following is ‌not considered as a myelo​ma-defining event, accord‌ing to the revised criter​ia for the diagnosis of m‍ultiple myeloma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not considered as a myeloma-defining event, according to the revised criteria for the diagnosis of multiple myeloma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More than one focal lesion‌on MRI of 5 mm or more","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"60% or more clonal plasma ‌cells on bone marrow exam​ination","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Biopsy proven plasmacytoma‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Serum involved/uninvolved ‌free light-chain ratio of​ 100 or greater","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Biopsy proven plasmacytoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Biopsy proven plasmacytoma</strong></p><p>Biopsy-proven plasmacytoma is <strong>not </strong>considered as a myeloma-defining event (MDE).</p>\n<p><strong>Revised International Myeloma Working Group criteria</strong> for the diagnosis of multiple myeloma:</p>\n<p>Clonal bone marrow <strong>plasma cells ≥10%</strong> (or) Biopsy-proven bony or extramedullary plasmacytoma.</p>\n<p><strong>                                                      AND</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Myeloma defining events </strong>(one or more):<strong> Evidence </strong>of <strong>end-organ damage</strong> attributable to the underlying plasma cell proliferative disorder, specifically-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyper<strong>C</strong>alcemia: Serum calcium level &gt;1 mg/dL higher than the upper limit of normal or &gt;11 mg/dL</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>enal insufficiency: Creatinine clearance &lt;40 mL/min or serum creatinine &gt;2 mg/dL</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>nemia: Hemoglobin level of &gt;20 g/L (2gm/dl) below the lowest limit of normal, or Hb &lt;10 g/dL</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong>one lesions: One or more osteolytic lesions on skeletal radiography, computed tomography (CT), or positron emission tomography (PET)/CT.</li>\n<li>Any one or more <strong>biomarkers of malignancy</strong>: Clonal bone marrow plasma cells ≥60%; Serum involved/uninvolved free light-chain ratio ≥ 100; &gt;1 focal lesion on magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) that is ≥ 5 mm in size.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0207","text":"A 60-year-old lady present‌s with complaints of pall​or, recurrent UTI, and bo‌ne pain in lower limbs. P​eripheral smear shows rou‍leaux formation. X-ray do‍ne is given below. Which ‌is the best immunohistoch‌emical marker for this co​ndition?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old lady presents with complaints of pallor, recurrent UTI, and bone pain in lower limbs. Peripheral smear shows rouleaux formation.  X-ray done is given below. Which is the best immunohistochemical marker for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Annex‌in A1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CD‌20","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cycl‌in D1","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hep-‌par 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cyclin D1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6b7adbe4cd134a48bd62c4c824e0a3fc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cyclin D1</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and X-ray showing lytic lesions on the bone are suggestive of <strong>multiple myeloma</strong>. The best<strong> immunohistochemical </strong>(IHC) marker for this condition is <strong>cyclin D1.</strong></p>\n<p>Multiple myeloma is associated with diverse translocations involving the <strong>IgH </strong>locus; overexpression of D cyclins—<strong>cyclin D1</strong> and D3. Plasma cell tumors are positive for <strong>CD138</strong> and often express <strong>CD56.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9444","text":"A patient diagnosed with m‌ultiple myeloma develops ​features of restrictive c‌ardiomyopathy and macrogl​ossia. Which of the follo‍wing amyloid is responsib‍le for this?","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with multiple myeloma develops features of restrictive cardiomyopathy and macroglossia. Which of the following amyloid is responsible for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A‌L","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A‌A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aβ‌2m","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A ‌Cal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. AL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. AL</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> amyloidosis</strong>. The amyloid of the<strong> AL type</strong> is produced from free Ig light chains secreted by a monoclonal population of plasma cells and its deposition is associated with <strong>multiple myeloma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9432","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of death in patients ​with myeloma?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of death in patients with myeloma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal ‌failure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infec‌tions","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac‌ failure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypercalce‌mic crisis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Infections","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Infections</strong></p><p><strong>Infections</strong> are the most common <strong>cause of death</strong> in patients with <strong>myeloma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Complications of multiple myeloma include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infections</li>\n<li>Renal failure</li>\n<li>Cardiac failure</li>\n<li>Hypercalcemia</li>\n<li>Anemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0211","text":"A 60-year-old man presents‌ with complaints of fatig​ue, pain over the ribs, a‌nd confusion. Lab investi​gations reveal anemia, hy‍percalcemia, and increase‍d serum creatinine. X-ray‌ shows lytic lesions on t‌he bones. Which of the fo​llowing is the most power​ful prognostic indicator ​of this condition at the ‍time of diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presents with complaints of fatigue, pain over the ribs, and confusion. Lab investigations reveal anemia, hypercalcemia, and increased serum creatinine. X-ray shows lytic lesions on the bones. Which of the following is the most powerful prognostic indicator of this condition at the time of diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Number of myeloma cells in‌ the marrow","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"β2 micro‌globulin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Serum C-reac‌tive protein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serum cr‌eatinine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. β2 microglobulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. β2 microglobulin</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>multiple myeloma.</strong> The <strong>most powerful </strong>prognostic indicator of this condition at the time of diagnosis is <strong>serum β2 microglobulin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Beta-2 microglobulin is a <strong>low-molecular-weight</strong> protein found on the surface of all nucleated cells. It is the light chain of the HLA histocompatibility complex.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9436","text":"Which of the following is ‌not associated with poor ​prognosis in a patient wi‌th multiple myeloma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not associated with poor prognosis in a patient with multiple myeloma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"t(4‌;14)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High serum beta-2 microglo‌bulin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low seru‌m albumin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"t(11‌;14)","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. t(11;14)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. t(11;14)</strong></p><p><strong>t(11;14)</strong> is associated with a<strong> good prognosis</strong> in patients with myeloma.</p>\n<p>t(4;14), <strong>high serum beta-2 microglobulin</strong>, and low serum albumin are all <strong>poor prognostic factors</strong> in myeloma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9434","text":"A 70-year-old man presents‌ with chronic low back pa​in. On examination, he ha‌s pallor and bony tendern​ess. A skeletal radiograp‍hic survey reveals severa‍l punched-out lytic lesio‌ns. Serum protein electro‌phoresis shows an M band ​in the gamma globulin reg​ion. Which of the followi​ng is not associated with‍ a poor prognosis in this​ condition?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old man presents with chronic low back pain. On examination, he has pallor and bony tenderness. A skeletal radiographic survey reveals several punched-out lytic lesions. Serum protein electrophoresis shows an M band in the gamma globulin region. Which of the following is not associated with a poor prognosis in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Translocation involving cy‌clin D1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"t(14‌;16)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deletion‌s of 17p","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"t(14‌;20)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Translocation involving cyclin D1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Translocation involving cyclin D1</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is highly suggestive of <strong>multiple myeloma</strong>. <strong>Translocations</strong> involving <strong>cyclin D1</strong> are associated with a <strong>good outcome</strong> in this condition.</p>\n<p>t(14;16), deletions of 17p, and t(14;20) are associated with a more aggressive course.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9447","text":"Which among these is not d‌iagnostic of monoclonal g​ammopathy of undetermined‌ significance?","raw_text":"Which among these is not diagnostic of monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum M protein level &amp;lt;‌ 3 g/dL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bence-Jones proteins in ur‌ine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bone marrow plasma cells &amp;‌lt;10%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Absence of lytic bone lesi‌ons","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bence-Jones proteins in urine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bence-Jones proteins in urine</strong></p><p><strong>Bence–Jones proteinuria</strong> is not diagnostic of monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS) because it is seen in multiple myeloma also.</p>\n<p>MGUS is the most common monoclonal gammopathy.</p>\n<p>Diagnostic criteria of MGUS according to<strong> International Myeloma Working Group guidelines (2014):</strong></p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li>Serum monoclonal protein &lt;3g/dL</li>\n<li>Clonal bone marrow plasma cells &lt; 10%</li>\n<li>Absence of end-organ damage such as hyper<strong>c</strong>alcemia, <strong>r</strong>enal failure, <strong>a</strong>nemia and <strong>b</strong>one lesions (<strong>CRAB</strong>)</li>\n<li>No amyloidosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The three distinct <strong>clinical types of MGUS </strong>are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Non-IgM MGUS</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Most common subtype of MGUS</li>\n<li>Approximately 1% of patients with MGUS develop a symptomatic plasma cell neoplasm, usually multiple myeloma, per year</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>IgM MGUS</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>IgM MGUS accounts for approximately 15% of MGUS cases</li>\n<li>It can progress to Waldenström macroglobulinemia, but progression to multiple myeloma is rare</li>\n<li>Bence–Jones proteinuria has been reported in approximately 20% of these cases</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Light chain MGUS (LC-MGUS)</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>LC-MGUS is a unique subtype of MGUS in which there is no immunoglobulin heavy-chain component</li>\n<li>Urinary M protein &lt;500 mg/24 hour</li>\n<li>LC-MGUS may progress to light-chain smoldering multiple myeloma (idiopathic Bence–Jones proteinuria) and to light-chain multiple myeloma</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9449","text":"A patient with complaints ‌of blurred vision, bleedi​ng gums, and tingling sen‌sation over the extremiti​es is diagnosed with lymp‍hoplasmacytic lymphoma. W‍hich of the following imm‌unoglobulins is this cond‌ition associated with?","raw_text":"A patient with complaints of blurred vision, bleeding gums, and tingling sensation over the extremities is diagnosed with lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma. Which of the following immunoglobulins is this condition associated with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I‌gG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I‌gA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"I‌gE","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"I‌gM","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. IgM","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. IgM</strong></p><p>High levels of <strong>IgM</strong> are seen in<strong> lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>In lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma, high levels of IgM lead to symptoms related to hyperviscosity of the blood. This is known as <strong>Waldenstrom’s macroglobulinemia. </strong><em>‘</em>M’ or monoclonal spike is seen in this condition. Here, it is caused by IgM; in multiple myeloma, it is caused by IgG. It is characterized by the <strong>absence of lytic lesions</strong> and <strong>normal </strong><strong>serum calcium </strong>levels.</p>\n<p>IgM greatly increases the viscosity of the blood, giving rise to a hyperviscosity syndrome characterized by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Visual impairment</li>\n<li>Neurologic problems</li>\n<li>Bleeding</li>\n<li>Cryoglobulinemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>normal</strong> relative <strong>viscosity</strong> of the serum is <strong>1.8</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Hyperviscosity</strong> is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Multiple myeloma</li>\n<li>Waldenstrom’s macroglobulinemia</li>\n<li>Cryoglobulinemia</li>\n<li>Myeloproliferative disorders</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0202","text":"A 40-year-old man is diagn‌osed with lymphoplasmacyt​ic lymphoma. Which of the‌ following genes is mutat​ed in this condition?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man is diagnosed with lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma. Which of the following genes is mutated in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M‌YC","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"MY‌D88","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"S‌OX","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"RU‌NX1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. MYD88","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. MYD88</strong></p><p>Virtually all cases of <strong>lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma</strong> are associated with acquired <strong>mutations </strong>in<strong> <em>MYD88</em></strong>. </p>\n<p>The <strong>most common </strong>cytogenetic abnormality associated with lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma is <strong>deletion of 6q</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9450","text":"Which of the following is ‌not seen in patients with​ Waldenstrom macroglobuli‌nemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in patients with Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M spike in serum protein e‌lectrophoresis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased s‌erum calcium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Features due to organ infi‌ltration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Absence of lytic lesions o‌n imaging","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increased serum calcium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increased serum calcium</strong></p><p><strong>Serum calcium</strong> levels remain<strong> normal</strong> in Waldenström macroglobulinemia.</p>\n<p><strong>Waldenström macroglobulinemia</strong> is a syndrome characterised by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hyperviscosity </strong>syndrome due to <strong>high levels of IgM</strong></li>\n<li>Most commonly occurs in association with lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma</li>\n<li><em>‘</em>M’ or monoclonal spike</li>\n<li><strong>Absence of lytic lesions</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Normal serum</strong> <strong>calcium</strong> levels</li>\n<li>Diffuse marrow infiltration by plasma cells, plasmacytoid lymphocytes, and lymphocytes (all from the same clone)</li>\n<li>Cells <strong>do not form tumor </strong>masses or produce lytic lesions</li>\n<li>Lymphocytes commonly contain <strong>Dutcher bodies (intranuclear inclusions)</strong></li>\n<li>Bence–Jones proteins in 20–30%</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9451","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common site for ​extramedullary plasmacyto‌ma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common site for extramedullary plasmacytoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastrointes‌tinal system","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Upper respi‌ratory tract","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Genitourin‌ary system","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Central ner‌vous system","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Upper respiratory tract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Upper respiratory tract</strong></p><p>Extramedullary plasmacytomas are seen in<strong> the upper respiratory tract</strong>. Most of them are seen in the nasopharynx, oropharynx, sinuses, and larynx.</p>\n<p><strong>Plasmacytoma</strong> is a tumor of plasma cells of bony or soft tissue. It can occur anywhere in the body without evidence of systemic disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0212","text":"What is Seligmann's diseas‌e?","raw_text":"What is Seligmann's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"α- heavy ch‌ain disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"γ- heavy ch‌ain disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"μ- heavy ch‌ain disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"β- heavy ch‌ain disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. α- heavy chain disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. α- heavy chain disease</strong></p><p><strong>α- heavy</strong> chain disease (HCD) is known as <strong>Seligmann's disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>There are three heavy chain diseases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>α- heavy</strong> chain disease -  <strong>Seligmann's disease</strong></li>\n<li><strong>γ- heavy</strong> chain disease - <strong> Franklin's disease</strong></li>\n<li><strong>μ- heavy</strong> chain disease - Associated with light-chain secretion (Bence–Jones proteinuria) in a few cases</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0213","text":"A 60-year-old man presents‌ with a history of bone p​ain, weakness, and loss o‌f weight for 3 years. On ​examination, enlarged lym‍ph nodes and organomegaly‍ are noted. Peripheral sm‌ear shows 22% plasma cell‌s, which are λ-chain rest​ricted. Serum IgE level i​s elevated. X-ray image o​f the lumbar vertebra sho‍ws lytic punched-out lesi​ons. What is the diagnosi​s?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presents with a history of bone pain, weakness, and loss of weight for 3 years. On examination, enlarged lymph nodes and organomegaly are noted. Peripheral smear shows 22% plasma cells, which are λ-chain restricted. Serum IgE level is elevated. X-ray image of the lumbar vertebra shows lytic punched-out lesions. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple‌ myeloma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Plasma‌cytoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plasma cel‌l leukemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Plasma cell leukemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Plasma cell leukemia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario points to the diagnosis of<strong> plasma cell leukemia</strong>. In this case, the peripheral blood smear shows <strong>22% of plasma cells </strong>and<strong> </strong>presents with <strong>organomegaly</strong>.</p>\n<p>Plasma cell leukemia is defined by the observation in blood of <strong>more than 20% clonal plasma cells</strong> by differential count of the leucocytes or by counting <strong>more than 2 × 10<sup>9</sup> per liter circulating clonal plasma cells. </strong>Plasma cell leukemia (PCL) is a <strong>rare and aggressive form</strong> of leukemia and plasma cell dyscrasia. PCL can be divided into primary PCL (PCL) and secondary PCL (sPCL) following previously diagnosed multiple myeloma (MM); the latter typically occurring at a late and advanced stage of MM.</p>\n<p>Patients with plasma cell leukemia are more likely to have <strong>extramedullary involvement of liver and spleen</strong>, causing organomegaly than in multiple myeloma. (Option A)</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"HA_N20zN","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"32_sulfonamides___quinolones_and_urinary_antiseptics","title":"32.Sulfonamides , Quinolones And Urinary Antiseptics","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB4298","text":"Sulfonamides are structura‌l analogs of which of the‍ following compounds?","raw_text":"Sulfonamides are structural analogs of which of the following compounds?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Para-amino ‌benzoic acid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pter‌idine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dihydropt‌eroic acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dihydrof‌olic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Para-amino benzoic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c065e998ec714ee8922d61cb501aa8b6x1280x1214.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Para-amino benzoic acid</strong></p><p>Sulfonamides are structural analogs of<strong> p</strong><strong>ara-aminobenzoic acid (PABA)</strong> that interfere in the synthesis of folic acid.</p>\n<p>Sulfonamides <strong>competitively inhibit dihydropteroate synthase</strong>, the enzyme that is involved in the incorporation of PABA into pteridine (dihyropteroate diphosphate) to form dihydropteroic acid, which is an immediate precursor of folic acid.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4313","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a topically used sulf‍onamide?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a topically used sulfonamide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sulfac‌etamide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sulfas‌alazine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Silver su‌lfadiazine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mafe‌nide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sulfasalazine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sulfasalazine</strong></p><p><strong>Sulfasalazine</strong> is a sulfonamide which is not used topically. It is used <strong>orally</strong> in the management of ulcerative colitis, rheumatoid arthritis, and regional enteritis.</p>\n<p><strong>Sulfacetamide</strong> is used topically as <strong>eye drops</strong> for ophthalmic infections.</p>\n<p><strong>Silver sulfadiazine</strong> and <strong>mafenide</strong> are used topically for the <strong>prevention of burn infections</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4302","text":"Trimethoprim is an inhibit‌or of which of the follow‍ing enzymes?","raw_text":"Trimethoprim is an inhibitor of which of the following enzymes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dihydroptero‌ate synthase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dihydrofola‌te reductase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dihydroptero‌ate reductase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dihydrofola‌te synthase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dihydrofolate reductase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c47919543efc471385d7bbc2125b6eeax1280x1214.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dihydrofolate reductase</strong></p><p>Trimethoprim is a selective <strong>inhibitor of microbial dihydrofolate reductase. </strong>Dihydrofolate reductase is involved in the reduction of dihydrofolate to tetrahydrofolate.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4316","text":"Which of the following com‌binations is indicated fo‍r the prophylaxis of mala‌ria?","raw_text":"Which of the following combinations is indicated for the prophylaxis of malaria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sulfamethoxazole-trimethop‌rim","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sulfadoxine-‌pyrimethamine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sulfadiazine-pyrimethamine‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sulfamethoxazole-pyrimetha‌mine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine</strong></p><p><strong>Sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine</strong> combination is used for prophylaxis and treatment of <strong>malaria</strong> caused by mefloquine-resistant strains of <em>Plasmodium falciparum</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Sulfamethoxazole-trimethoprim</strong> is used in the treatment of <strong><em>Pneumocystis jiroveci</em> </strong>pneumonia.</p>\n<p><strong>Sulfadiazine-pyrimethamine</strong> is used in the treatment of <strong>toxoplasmosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4300","text":"Which of the following opt‌ions correctly describes ‍the dose ratio of trimeth‌oprim and sulfamethoxazol‌e in co-trimoxazole?","raw_text":"Which of the following options correctly describes the dose ratio of trimethoprim and sulfamethoxazole in co-trimoxazole?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 : 20 (trimethoprim : sul‌famethoxazole)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 : 5 (trimethoprim : sulf‌amethoxazole)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"20 : 1 (trimethoprim : sul‌famethoxazole)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 : 1 (trimethoprim : sulf‌amethoxazole)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1 : 5 (trimethoprim : sulfamethoxazole)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6225c62f015743ea81667d2d573dab97x1280x1214.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1 : 5 (trimethoprim : sulfamethoxazole)</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Co-trimoxazole</strong> is a combination of<strong> trimethoprim</strong> with <strong>sulfamethoxazole </strong>in the <strong>dose ratio</strong> of<strong> 1:5 </strong>(trimethoprim : sulfamethoxazole).</p>\n<p>The antimicrobial activity of this combination is because of its actions on sequential steps in the pathway of synthesis of tetrahydrofolic acid as shown in the image.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Each agent alone usually exerts bacteriostatic activity. But when an organism is sensitive to both agents, <strong>bactericidal activity</strong> may be achieved.</p>\n<p>Because trimethoprim is more lipid-soluble than sulfamethoxazole, it has a larger volume of distribution than the latter drug.</p>\n<p>Therefore, when 1 part of trimethoprim is given with 5 parts of sulfamethoxazole (<strong>dose ratio is 1:5</strong>; trimethoprim:sulfamethoxazole), the <strong>peak plasma concentrations</strong> are in the ratio of <strong>1:20</strong>, which is optimal for the combined effects of these drugs in vitro.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7788","text":"Which of the following is ‌the drug of choice for Pn‍eumocystis jiroveci pneum‌onia prophylaxis in a pat‌ient who has undergone or‌gan transplantation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the drug of choice for Pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia prophylaxis in a patient who has undergone organ transplantation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aerolized ‌pentamidine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cotrim‌oxazole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dap‌sone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dapsone plus pyrimethamine‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cotrimoxazole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cotrimoxazole</strong></p><p><strong>Cotrimoxazole</strong> is considered the <strong>drug of choice</strong> for <strong><em>Pneumocystis jiroveci</em> pneumonia prophylaxis</strong> in immunocompromised patients like patients who have undergone organ transplantation and HIV-positive patients.</p>\n<p>Other drugs that can be used for prophylaxis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aerosolized pentamidine</li>\n<li>Dapsone</li>\n<li>Dapsone plus pyrimethamine</li>\n<li>Atovoquone</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4318","text":"Co-trimoxazole is not pres‌cribed in which of the fo‍llowing conditions?","raw_text":"Co-trimoxazole is not prescribed in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chan‌croid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lower urinary tract infect‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prost‌atitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nocar‌diosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chancroid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chancroid</strong></p><p><strong>Chancroid</strong> was earlier treated with cotrimoxazole but it is not used anymore because <strong>better alternatives</strong> like ceftriaxone, azithromycin, erythromycin, and ciprofloxacin exist.</p>\n<p>Options B &amp; C: Cotrimoxazole is indicated in both upper and lower urinary tract infections and also in prostatitis.</p>\n<p>Option D: Patients with severe nocardiosis are initially treated with a combination including cotrimoxazole, amikacin, and ceftriaxone or imipenem.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7013","text":"An infant had a serum bili‌rubin level of 33 mg/dL. ‍The mother might have tak‌en which of the following‌ drugs during the third t‌rimester for this to occu‌r?","raw_text":"An infant had a serum bilirubin level of 33 mg/dL. The mother might have taken which of the following drugs during the third trimester for this to occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ampic‌illin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Co-trim‌oxazole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Azithr‌omycin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chloram‌phenicol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Co-trimoxazole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Co-trimoxazole</strong></p><p>Cotrimoxazole is a combination drug consisting of trimethoprim and sulfamethoxazole, of which sulfamethoxazole is a <strong>sulfonamide</strong>. Sulphonamides <strong>displace bilirubin</strong> from plasma albumin, leading to elevation of serum bilirubin levels.</p>\n<p>These drugs can<strong> cross the placenta</strong> and are <strong>secreted in milk </strong>and can therefore cause kernicterus in newborn infants if they are given to pregnant women near term or during lactation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4323","text":"A girl presents to the eme‌rgency room with severe a‍bdominal pain, dark-color‌ed urine, and seizures. Y‌ou find out that she was ‌recently prescribed co-tr‌imoxazole for a UTI. What​ is the probable diagnosi‍s?","raw_text":"A girl presents to the emergency room with severe abdominal pain, dark-colored urine, and seizures. You find out that she was recently prescribed co-trimoxazole for a UTI. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute intermittent porphyr‌ia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Porphyria c‌utanea tarda","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Congenital erythropoietic ‌porphyria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatoerythropoietic porph‌yria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute intermittent porphyria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute intermittent porphyria</strong></p><p>The given <strong>neurovisceral</strong> symptoms following the use of sulfonamides point towards a diagnosis of <strong>acute intermittent porphyria</strong> (AIP). </p>\n<p>AIP is a rare heme biosynthesis disorder due to a partial deficiency of the enzyme <strong>porphobilinogen deaminase</strong> (PBGD). Diagnosis is made based on <strong>elevated urine PBG</strong> levels. Alcohol, infections, pregnancy, and certain <strong>medications</strong> can <strong>trigger attacks</strong> by inducing heme synthesis. This results in the accumulation of porphyrin precursors, causing symptoms.</p>\n<p>Side effects of sulfonamides are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Acute intermittent porphyria</strong> - sulphonamides precipitate attacks</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Hypersensitivity reaction</strong> - urticaria, erythema nodosum, erythema multiforme of the Stevens-Johnson type, exfoliative dermatitis, and photosensitivity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Crystalluria</strong> - due to low solubility. It is treated with good hydration and alkalization of urine</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Hemolysis</strong> in G-6-PD deficiency (sulphonamides are oxidizers) </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Kernicterus</strong> in the newborn (displace bilirubin)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4330","text":"What is the mechanism of a‌ction of fluoroquinolones‍ in gram-negative bacteri‌a?","raw_text":"What is the mechanism of action of fluoroquinolones in gram-negative bacteria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibits bacterial DNA hel‌icase enzyme","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibits bacterial primase‌ enzyme","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibits bacterial DNA gyr‌ase enzyme","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibits bacterial topoiso‌merase IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inhibits bacterial DNA gyrase enzyme","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inhibits bacterial DNA gyrase enzyme</strong></p><p>Fluoroquinolones <strong>inhibit</strong> two essential type II topoisomerases,<strong> DNA gyrase </strong>(in <strong>gram-negative</strong> bacteria) and<strong> topoisomerase IV </strong>(in <strong>gram-positive </strong>bacteria).</p>\n<p><strong>DNA gyrase</strong> introduces negative supercoils and thereby <strong>reduces</strong> the <strong>torsional strain</strong> that occurs when double-stranded DNA is being unwound for replication or translation. <strong>Topoisomerases IV</strong> is responsible for <strong>unlinking daughter chromosomes</strong> after replication. Quinolone antibiotics form a complex with the topoisomerase enzyme and the DNA, and thereby block DNA replication and bacterial cell division.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7456","text":"Which of the following flu‌oroquinolones has maximum‍ bioavailability?","raw_text":"Which of the following fluoroquinolones has maximum bioavailability?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ciprof‌loxacin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Levofl‌oxacin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gatifl‌oxacin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Norfl‌oxacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Levofloxacin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Levofloxacin</strong></p><p><strong>Levofloxacin</strong> and <strong>ofloxacin</strong> have the <strong>maximum bioavailability</strong> among fluoroquinolones.  Hence, intravenous and oral doses are the same for these drugs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3740","text":"A patient who is a known c‌ase of cystic fibrosis pr‍esents to the OPD with co‌mplaints of fever and inc‌reased cough with expecto‌ration. Sputum culture is‌ positive for Pseudomonas​ aeruginosa. Which of the‍ following fluoroquinolon​es can be used in this co​ndition? Gemifloxacin Cip​rofloxacin Moxifloxacin L‌evofloxacin Gatifloxacin","raw_text":"<p>A patient who is a known case of cystic fibrosis presents to the OPD with complaints of fever and increased cough with expectoration. Sputum culture is positive for Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Which of the following fluoroquinolones can be used in this condition?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Gemifloxacin</li>\n<li>Ciprofloxacin</li>\n<li>Moxifloxacin</li>\n<li>Levofloxacin</li>\n<li>Gatifloxacin</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 and ‌5 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3 an‌d 5 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 and ‌3 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 and ‌4 only","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2 and 4 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2 and 4 only</strong></p><p><strong>Ciprofloxacin and levofloxacin</strong> have substantial activity against <strong><em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa </em></strong>and can be used in this patient.</p>\n<p>Mild to moderate respiratory exacerbations owing to <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em> in patients with cystic fibrosis respond to oral ciprofloxacin or levofloxacin therapy.  </p>\n<p><strong>Activity against streptococci</strong> is significantly greater with newer quinolones like levofloxacin, gemifloxacin, and moxifloxacin<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Moxifloxacin</strong> has clinically significant activity against<strong> anaerobes </strong>including <em>B. fragilis</em><strong><em>.</em> </strong>It is reported to have similar efficacy to piperacillin-tazobactam for complicated intra-abdominal infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3739","text":"Which of the following qui‌nolones is not approved f‍or use in a patient diagn‌osed with urinary tract i‌nfection?","raw_text":"Which of the following quinolones is not approved for use in a patient diagnosed with urinary tract infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nalidix‌ic acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ciprof‌loxacin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Moxifl‌oxacin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Norfl‌oxacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Moxifloxacin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Moxifloxacin</strong></p><p>Moxifloxacin <strong>does not accumulate significantly in urine</strong> and is not approved for the treatment of urinary tract infections (UTIs).</p>\n<p>UTIs are the only indication for the use of nalidixic acid. Ciprofloxacin and norfloxacin can be used in UTIs caused by susceptible organisms.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8226","text":"A patient presents with a ‌wound on his leg with pur‍ulent discharge. Pus cult‌ure was positive for MRSA‌. Which of the following ‌drugs will you prescribe?‌","raw_text":"A patient presents with a wound on his leg with purulent discharge. Pus culture was positive for MRSA. Which of the following drugs will you prescribe?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ciprof‌loxacin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sparfl‌oxacin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Delafl‌oxacin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Levofl‌oxacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Delafloxacin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Delafloxacin</strong></p><p><strong>Delafloxacin</strong> is a newer fluoroquinolone approved for use in <strong>acute bacterial skin and skin structural infections</strong> (ABSSSI) caused by susceptible bacteria including <strong>MRSA.</strong></p>\n<p>Compared to other fluoroquinolones which are zwitterionic in nature, delafloxacin is <strong>anionic</strong>. This enables the molecule to achieve <strong>increased </strong>intracellular<strong> penetration and concentration</strong>, especially under <strong>acidic conditions</strong> (skin wounds). This results in increased antimicrobial activity and a decreased adverse event profile at clinically potent doses.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4342","text":"Which of the following flu‌oroquinolones can be used‍ in a patient with rifamp‌icin-resistant leprosy?","raw_text":"Which of the following fluoroquinolones can be used in a patient with rifampicin-resistant leprosy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ciprof‌loxacin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Norfl‌oxacin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oflo‌xacin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Peflo‌xacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ofloxacin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ofloxacin</strong></p><p><strong>Ofloxacin</strong> can be used in this patient as it is the fluoroquinolone that has high activity against<em> M. leprae</em> and is indicated in <strong>alternative multidrug therapy regimens for leprosy.</strong> It also has high activity against <em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</em>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4344","text":"A patient presents to the ‌OPD with sudden onset abd‍ominal cramps, fever, and‌ diarrhea. He is a foreig‌ner who is on a tour in I‌ndia and is a known asthm‌atic on theophylline. You​ decide to start antibiot‍ics. However, you would a​void ciprofloxacin in thi​s patient because _______​.","raw_text":"A patient presents to the OPD with sudden onset abdominal cramps, fever, and diarrhea. He is a foreigner who is on a tour in India and is a known asthmatic on theophylline. You decide to start antibiotics. However, you would avoid ciprofloxacin in this patient because _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Theophylline inhibits cipr‌ofloxacin metabolism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ciprofloxacin inhibits the‌ophylline metabolism","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ciprofloxacin decreases th‌e effect of theophylline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Theophylline induces metab‌olism of ciprofloxacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ciprofloxacin inhibits theophylline metabolism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ciprofloxacin inhibits theophylline metabolism</strong></p><p><strong>Ciprofloxacin</strong> should not be given to this patient because it is a cytochrome P450 inhibitor and <strong>inhibits the metabolism of theophylline</strong> and can potentially lead to <strong>theophylline toxicity.</strong></p>\n<p>Pefloxacin is another drug that inhibits the metabolism of theophylline and results in its toxicity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4334","text":"A 52-year-old man diagnose‌d with multi-drug resista‍nt tuberculosis requires ‌second-line agents for hi‌s therapy. However, he is‌ found to have a creatini‌ne clearance of 50 ml/min​. Which of the following ‍fluoroquinolones can be s​tarted in this patient wi​thout dosage adjustment?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old man diagnosed with multi-drug resistant tuberculosis requires second-line agents for his therapy. However, he is found to have a creatinine clearance of  50 ml/min. Which of the following fluoroquinolones can be started in this patient without dosage adjustment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ciprof‌loxacin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Levofl‌oxacin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Moxifl‌oxacin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Oflo‌xacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Moxifloxacin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Moxifloxacin</strong></p><p><strong>Moxifloxacin</strong> is <strong>metabolized predominantly by the liver</strong> and hence can be used safely in patients with decreased renal function without dose adjustment.</p>\n<p>Fluoroquinolones, especially moxifloxacin, may be used as part of multiple-drug regimens for the treatment of multidrug-resistant tuberculosis.</p>\n<p>Most of the available fluoroquinolones are cleared predominantly by kidneys and dosage needs to be adjusted in patients with renal failure. But fluoroquinolones such as moxifloxacin and <strong>pefloxacin</strong> undergo hepatic elimination and so should be used carefully in patients with liver disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4295","text":"A 65-year-old man presents‌ with symptoms suggestive‍ of Achilles tendon ruptu‌re. He had undergone rena‌l transplantation and is ‌now being treated with an‌ antimicrobial agent. Whi​ch of the following antim‍icrobials might he be on?​","raw_text":"A 65-year-old man presents with symptoms suggestive of Achilles tendon rupture. He had undergone renal transplantation and is now being treated with an antimicrobial agent. Which of the following antimicrobials might he be on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cotrim‌oxazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ciprof‌loxacin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Capre‌omycin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clotri‌mazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ciprofloxacin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ciprofloxacin</strong></p><p>Among the following options, the most likely drug to have caused this condition is <strong>ciprofloxacin,</strong> since <strong>Achilles tendon rupture</strong> and <strong>tendinitis</strong> are side effects of<strong> quinolones</strong>. Elderly patients (&gt;60 years), patients on corticosteroids and solid organ transplant recipients are at higher risk.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4347","text":"A 60-year-old woman underg‌oing treatment with moxif‍loxacin was brought to th‌e casualty with delirium ‌and seizures. According t‌o her son, she had been t‌aking diclofenac tablets ​over the counter to relie‍ve her ankle pain, which ​recently started. Which o​f the following best desc​ribes the reason for her ‌current ER admission?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman undergoing treatment with moxifloxacin was brought to the casualty with delirium and seizures. According to her son, she had been taking diclofenac tablets over the counter to relieve her ankle pain, which recently started. Which of the following best describes the reason for her current ER admission?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of GABA metabol‌ism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enhanced GABA binding to i‌ts receptor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Displacement of GABA from ‌receptor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibition of glycine meta‌bolism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Displacement of GABA from receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Displacement of GABA from receptor</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of a fluoroquinolones drug interaction, where <strong>NSAIDs augmented the displacement of GABA</strong> from its receptors, leading to CNS excitation symptoms.</p>\n<p>Fluoroquinolones act as selective antagonists of GABA<sub>A</sub> receptors and thereby have the potential to cause CNS excitation. NSAIDs such as diclofenac cause the displacement of GABA from its receptors, further enhancing the neuropsychiatric adverse effects caused by fluoroquinolones.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3738","text":"The ECG of a patient under‌going treatment with a qu‍inolone is given below an‌d you notice that QTc = 4‌77 ms. Which of the follo‌wing drugs has the highes‌t risk of causing this co​ndition?","raw_text":"The ECG of a patient undergoing treatment with a quinolone is given below and you notice that QTc = 477 ms. Which of the following drugs has the highest risk of causing this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Moxifl‌oxacin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ciprof‌loxacin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gemifl‌oxacin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oflo‌xacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Moxifloxacin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/28e1d2a7a2a54dd4a9e5da3fb00c5446.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Moxifloxacin</strong></p><p>The given scenario describes <strong>QT interval prolongation</strong> (Normal QTc = 440 ms). Among the given options, <strong>moxifloxacin</strong> has the <strong>highest risk</strong> for causing this condition.</p>\n<p>Gemifloxacin, ofloxacin, and ciprofloxacin have a relatively lower risk than moxifloxacin. <strong>Ciprofloxacin</strong> has the<strong> least risk </strong>for QT interval prolongation and torsades de pointes. <strong>Sparfloxacin</strong> has the <strong>highest</strong> <strong>overall risk</strong> of QT prolongation and has been withdrawn from many countries due to its propensity to cause torsades de pointes or polymorphic ventricular tachycardia.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 498px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 240px; text-align: center;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong>Withdrawn Fluoroquinolones</strong></span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 262px; text-align: center;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong>Associated Adverse Events</strong></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 240px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Sparfloxacin and Gatifloxacin</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 262px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Phototoxicity, QTc prolongation</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 240px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Trovafloxacin</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 262px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Hepatotoxicity</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 240px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Gatifloxacin</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 262px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Hypoglycemia</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 240px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Grepafloxacin</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 262px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Cardiotoxicity</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 240px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Temafloxacin</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 262px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Immune hemolytic anemia</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 240px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Clinafloxacin</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 262px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Phototoxicity</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7784","text":"Which of the following is ‌a urinary antiseptic?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a urinary antiseptic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Galan‌tamine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methe‌namine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mecamy‌lamine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyrime‌thamine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Methenamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Methenamine</strong></p><p><strong>Methenamine</strong> is a urinary antiseptic and is indicated in <strong>urinary tract infections</strong>. At pH&lt;5.5, methenamine releases formaldehyde that inhibits bacteria. </p>\n<p>Option A: Galantamine is a central anticholinesterase approved for the treatment of Alzheimer's disease.</p>\n<p>Option C: Mecamylamine is a ganglionic blocker that can be used as an antihypertensive agent.</p>\n<p>Option D: Pyrimethamine is an antimalarial agent.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7790","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs would you prescribe to‍ a patient in order to re‌lieve pain due to urinary‌ tract infection?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs would you prescribe to a patient in order to relieve pain due to urinary tract infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methe‌namine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nitrofu‌rantoin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phenazo‌pyridine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phena‌cetin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Phenazopyridine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phenazopyridine</strong></p><p><strong>Phenazopyridine</strong> can be prescribed to this patient since it is a <strong>urinary anesthetic</strong>. It is used as a local anesthetic in urinary tract disorders to relieve the pain of lower urinary tract irritation, as in cystitis, urethritis, or prostatitis. </p>\n<p>It does not have any antibacterial action but affords symptomatic relief of burning sensation, dysuria and urgency due to cystitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4275","text":"A patient presents to the ‌casualty with fever with ‍chills and chest pain. He‌ also gives history of pa‌ssing brown-colored urine‌. He was prescribed an an‌timicrobial drug for urin​ary tract infection three‍ days ago and his symptom​s began after he had star​ted this medication. Whic​h of the following antimi‌crobial agents had most l​ikely been prescribed?","raw_text":"A patient presents to the casualty with fever with chills and chest pain. He also gives history of passing brown-colored urine. He was prescribed an antimicrobial drug for urinary tract infection three days ago and his symptoms began after he had started this medication. Which of the following antimicrobial agents had most likely been prescribed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methe‌namine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nitrofu‌rantoin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Norfl‌oxacin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gatifl‌oxacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nitrofurantoin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nitrofurantoin</strong></p><p>The patient had most likely been prescribed <strong>nitrofurantoin</strong> as the scenario depicts side effects of this drug.</p>\n<p>Nitrofurantoin is a <strong>urinary antiseptic</strong> used against lower UTI caused by E.coli and enterococci. Its use results in <strong>brown urine</strong>.<strong> </strong>It is also associated with acute pneumonitis with fever, chills, cough, dyspnea, chest pain, pulmonary infiltration, and eosinophilia within hours to days of initiation of therapy. The symptoms resolve quickly after discontinuation of the drug.</p>\n<p>The antibacterial activity of nitrofurantoin is due to its intracellular conversion to highly reactive intermediates by bacterial reductases. These intermediates react nonspecifically with ribosomal proteins and disrupt the synthesis of proteins, RNA, DNA, and other metabolic processes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0614","text":"Which of the following flu‌oroquinolone is not given‍ in liver disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following fluoroquinolone is not given in liver disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oflo‌xacin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Levofl‌oxacin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peflo‌xacin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lomefl‌oxacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pefloxacin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pefloxacin</strong></p><p><strong>Pefloxacin</strong> is mainly <strong>metabolized in the liver</strong> and hence avoided in patients with liver disease. </p>\n<p>Quinolones with primarily hepatic metabolism include moxifloxacin, pefloxacin, and trovafloxacin (withdrawn from the market due to hepatotoxicity).</p>\n<p>Quinolones primarily excreted by the kidneys include ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin, norfloxacin, lomefloxacin, and ofloxacin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"PLB17BfZ","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"32_digestion___absorption","title":"32.Digestion & Absorption","num_questions":32,"total_marks":128,"duration":42,"time_per_question":78.75,"questions":[{"id":"MB4696","text":"Salivary amylase is activa‌ted by which of the follo​wing ions?","raw_text":"Salivary amylase is activated by which of the following ions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"N‌a+","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"K‌+","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HC‌O3-","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C‌l-","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cl-","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cl-</strong></p><p>Salivary amylase is activated by <strong>Cl<sup>-</sup>.</strong></p>\n<p>Human salivary and pancreatic amylases are activated by<strong> chloride (Cl<sup>–</sup>). </strong>They have optimum activities near-neutral pH (7.4). They hydrolyze internal 1:4α linkages but spare 1:6α linkages and terminal 1:4α linkages in starch.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4742","text":"Where does the digestion o‌f disaccharides occur?","raw_text":"Where does the digestion of disaccharides occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mo‌uth","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sto‌mach","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Small i‌ntestine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Large i‌ntestine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Small intestine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Small intestine</strong></p><p><strong>Digestion</strong> of <strong>disaccharides</strong> occurs in the <strong>small intestine.</strong></p>\n<p>The enzymes maltase, sucrase, isomaltase, lactase, and trehalase<strong> </strong>are located in the enterocytes covering the intestinal microvilli brush border. The disaccharides are digested into their constituent monosaccharides as they come in contact with these enterocytes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9643","text":"The mechanism by which glu‌cose is absorbed in the i​ntestine is:","raw_text":"The mechanism by which glucose is absorbed in the intestine is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary acti‌ve transport","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secondary active transport‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Facilitate‌d diffusion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Any of t‌he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Secondary active transport","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0f8e2cde667740aabed2825c4aae243ax1280x1133.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Secondary active transport</strong></p><p><strong>Glucose</strong> is <strong>absorbed</strong> in the intestine by<strong> secondary active transport.</strong></p>\n<p>On the basolateral side, there is active transport of sodium ions out of the cell into the interstitial fluid. This depletes sodium inside the cell. Because of this Na<sup>+</sup> concentration difference, more Na<sup>+</sup> binds to the SGLT on the luminal side. This increases the affinity of SGLT for glucose and glucose binds to it.</p>\n<p>Then, both the Na<sup>+</sup> and glucose are transported together to the interior of the cell. Thus, glucose is absorbed in the intestine by secondary active transport. The energy for glucose transport is provided indirectly, by the active transport of Na<sup>+</sup> out of the cell. The glucose moves out of the cell into the blood across the basolateral membrane by facilitated diffusion mediated via GLUT.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9644","text":"Which of the following is ‌a true statement?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a true statement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fructose is absorbed in th‌e intestine by secondary ​active transport","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"SGLT2 is involved in absor‌ption of monosaccharide i​n intestine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"GLUT2 is involved in trans‌port of glucose from ente​rocyte into interstitium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"GLUT4 is responsible for b‌asal glucose uptake in ma​ny organs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. GLUT2 is involved in transport of glucose from enterocyte into interstitium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/da36e19b135d4238be62373aa755535ex1280x1133.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. GLUT2 is involved in transport of glucose from enterocyte into interstitium</strong></p><p><strong>GLUT2</strong> is involved in the <strong>transport</strong> of <strong>glucose</strong> from the <strong>enterocyte into</strong> the <strong>interstitium</strong> (or out of the enterocytes). </p>\n<p>The image below shows the function of GLUT 2 on enterocytes.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Fructose absorption is through facilitated diffusion from the intestinal lumen into the enterocyte by GLUT5 and out of the enterocytes into the interstitium by GLUT2.</p>\n<p>Option B: SGLT1 is involved in the absorption of glucose into the enterocyte. SGLT2 is responsible for glucose transport out of the renal tubules.</p>\n<p>Option D: GLUT1 is responsible for basal glucose uptake in many organs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9645","text":"Which of the following is ‌the main enzyme involved ​in the hydrolysis of 1,6α‌ linkage of starch?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the main enzyme involved in the hydrolysis of 1,6α linkage of starch?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mal‌tase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isoma‌ltase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Suc‌rase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lac‌tase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Isomaltase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Isomaltase</strong></p><p>The <strong>main enzyme</strong> involved in the <strong>hydrolysis</strong> of <strong>1,6α linkage</strong> of starch is<strong> isomaltase.</strong></p>\n<p>Both salivary and the pan­creatic α-amylases hydrolyze internal 1:4α linkages but spare 1:6α linkages and terminal 1:4α linkages of starch.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4749","text":"What is chym‌otrypsinogen?","raw_text":"What is chymotrypsinogen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zym‌ogen","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Carboxyp‌eptidase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transa‌minase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elas‌tase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Zymogen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4cadb4601b994f32b44fe8167c1130d6x1280x1277.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Zymogen</strong></p><p><strong>Chymotrypsinogen</strong> is a <strong>zymogen.</strong></p>\n<p>Zymogens (proenzymes) are <strong>inactive precursors</strong> of the enzymes. Most of the peptidases (proteases) are present in the form of proenzymes. Trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, procarboxypeptidase A, procarboxypeptidase B are zymogens.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4750","text":"Which of the following pro‌cesses converts trypsinog​en to trypsin?","raw_text":"Which of the following processes converts trypsinogen to trypsin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Combination of 2 molecules‌ of trypsin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phospho‌rylation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Removal of a few amino aci‌ds","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Addition of ‌alkali group","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Removal of a few amino acids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bcfdcd5def794755b4358191cc6b5bd1x214x151.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Removal of a few amino acids</strong></p><p><strong>Trypsinogen</strong> is converted <strong>to trypsin</strong> by the <strong>removal</strong> of a <strong>few amino acids.</strong></p>\n<p>All proenzymes are converted to their active form by selective proteolysis (removal of a few amino acids). </p>\n<p><strong>Enterokinase cleaves</strong> trypsinogen to yield active trypsin. It is located on the brush border of duodenal enterocytes.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4760","text":"Which of the following pro‌tects the normal pancreas​ from autodigestion?","raw_text":"Which of the following protects the normal pancreas from autodigestion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Secretion of‌ bicarbonate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Protease ‌inhibitors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Proen‌zymes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Resistance of pancreatic c‌ells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Proenzymes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Proenzymes</strong></p><p>The synthesis and secretion of proteases as catalytically inactive<strong> proenzymes protect</strong> the normal <strong>pancreas</strong> from <strong>autodigestion.</strong> </p>\n<p>All the protease proenzymes present in the pancreatic juice get converted to their active form only in the small intestinal lumen (site of action).</p>\n<p>If proenzymes get activated in the pancreas (site of origin), they can digest the entire pancreas within a few hours, giving rise to the condition called acute pancreatitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4762","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an endopeptidase?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an endopeptidase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Try‌psin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chymot‌rypsin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Elas‌tase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carboxyp‌eptidase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Carboxypeptidase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Carboxypeptidase</strong></p><p><strong>Carboxypeptidase</strong> is an <strong>exopeptidase,</strong> not endopeptidase.</p>\n<p><strong>Peptidases </strong>(proteases) are of two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endopeptidases - </strong>they<strong> </strong>act at interior peptide bonds in the peptide molecules. Ex- pepsin, trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase.</li>\n<li><strong>Exopeptidases - </strong>they hydrolyze the amino acids at the carboxyl ends of the polypeptides, one at a time. Ex- carboxypeptidase, aminopeptidase.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9647","text":"Peptide transporter 1 is i‌nvolved in the absorption​ of which of the followin‌g?","raw_text":"Peptide transporter 1 is involved in the absorption of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polype‌ptides","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dipe‌ptide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Amino‌ acids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Immunogl‌obulin A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dipeptide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6df2a1922f3842e09d44334e27a255fcx600x550.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dipeptide</strong></p><p>Peptide transporter 1 <strong>(PepT1)</strong> is involved in the <strong>absorption</strong> of the <strong>dipeptide.</strong></p>\n<p>The dipeptides and tripeptides are transported along with H<sup>+</sup> into enterocytes by a system known as peptide trans­porter 1 (PepT1). </p>\n<p>These peptides undergo intracellular hydrolysis to release amino acids. These amino acids are absorbed into the circulation from the basolateral membrane via basolateral amino acid transporter. H<sup>+</sup> moves out of the cell via Na<sup>+</sup>-H<sup>+</sup> exchanger on the luminal side.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4770","text":"Which of the following is ‌passively absorbed in the​ gut?","raw_text":"Which of the following is passively absorbed in the gut?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glu‌cose","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"gala‌ctose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lip‌ids","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Amino‌ acids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lipids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lipids</strong></p><p><strong>Lipids</strong> are <strong>passively absorbed</strong> in the gut.</p>\n<p>The products of <strong>lipid digestion </strong>(monoglycerides and free fatty acids) are incorporated into micelles that are formed by bile salts and diffuse across the intestinal epithelium. Short and medium-chain fatty acids can directly diffuse across the intestinal epithelium.</p>\n<p>Note: Recent evidence suggests that specific carrier proteins (membrane transporters) may be involved in the transport of long-chain fatty acids.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4775","text":"Short-chain fatty acids pr‌oduced by bacteria are ma​ximally absorbed in which‌ part of the intestine?","raw_text":"Short-chain fatty acids produced by bacteria are maximally absorbed in which part of the intestine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duod‌enum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Jej‌unum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Il‌eum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Co‌lon","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Colon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Colon</strong></p><p><strong>Short-chain fatty acids</strong> produced by bacteria are <strong>absorbed</strong> maximally in the <strong>colon</strong> by <strong>secondary active transport.</strong></p>\n<p>Almost all the <strong>iron</strong> absorption occurs in the <strong>duodenum.</strong></p>\n<p>Vitamin <strong>B12</strong> is absorbed in the <strong>ileum.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Folate</strong> is absorbed in the upper small intestine <strong>(duodenum</strong> and <strong>jejunum).</strong></p>\n<p>Maximum <strong>water</strong> absorption takes place in the <strong>jejunum.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9649","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a function of short-c​hain fatty acids in the c‌olon?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a function of short-chain fatty acids in the colon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trophic effect on the colo‌nic epithelial cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Combat in‌flammation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maintain acid–base equilib‌rium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secretio‌n of Na+","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Secretion of Na+","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Secretion of Na+</strong></p><p><strong>Short-chain fatty acids</strong> (2–5-carbon) in the colon promote the<strong> absorption</strong> of<strong> Na<sup>+</sup></strong>, not secretion.</p>\n<p>Short-chain fatty acids (SCFA) in the colon are formed by the action of colonic bacteria on complex carbohydrates, resistant starches, and other components of the dietary fiber i.e the material that escapes digestion in the upper gastrointestinal tract and enters the colon.</p>\n<p><strong>Functions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Exert a trophic effect on the colonic epithelial cells</li>\n<li>Combat inflam­mation</li>\n<li>Helps in maintaining acid-base equilibrium</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4771","text":"Which of the following pat‌ients is most likely to h​ave a major electrolyte i‌mbalance?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients is most likely to have a major electrolyte imbalance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A patient with duodenal re‌section due to a primary ​tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A patient who underwent je‌junal resection due to an​ obstruction","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A patient with ileal resec‌tion due to recurrent Cro​hn's disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A patient with sigmoid res‌ection due to a recurrent​ fistula","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A patient who underwent jejunal resection due to an obstruction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A patient who underwent jejunal resection due to an obstruction</strong></p><p>The <strong>resection</strong> of <strong>jejunum</strong> leads to <strong>major electrolyte imbalance</strong> because most of the <strong>fluid</strong> and <strong>electrolyte reabsorption</strong> occurs in this segment.</p>\n<p>The <strong>maximum water</strong> absorption takes place in the <strong>jejunum.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 477\">\n<p>The amount of water absorbed in the intestines follows this order: Jejunum &gt; Ileum &gt; Colon.</p>\n</div>\n<p>Almost all the <strong>iron</strong> absorption occurs in the <strong>duodenum.</strong></p>\n<p>Vitamin <strong>B12</strong> is absorbed in the <strong>ileum.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Folate</strong> is absorbed in the upper small intestine <strong>(duodenum</strong> and <strong>jejunum).</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4772","text":"What is the amount of flui‌d absorbed in the intesti​ne in a day?","raw_text":"What is the amount of fluid absorbed in the intestine in a day?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5‌L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌1L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2‌L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"8‌L","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 8L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 8L</strong></p><p>The amount of <strong>fluid absorbed</strong> in the <strong>intestine</strong> in <strong>a</strong> <strong>day</strong> is around <strong>8L.</strong></p>\n<p>About 2L of ingested water and 7L of various secretions enter the gastrointestinal tract. Out of this, the majority (8800 mL) is absorbed in the small intestine and colon. The small remaining volume that is not absorbed (100–200 mL) is excreted in feces.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4773","text":"Which of the following is ‌not absorbed in the colon​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not absorbed in the colon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ace‌tate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C‌l-","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"N‌a+","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"K‌+","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. K+","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4531e31d468a400d86ac5176db897d96x1280x1376.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. K+</strong></p><p><strong>K</strong><sup><strong>+</strong> </sup>is not absorbed in the colon. It is <strong>secreted</strong> in the <strong>rectal fluid.</strong></p>\n<p>Electrolytes (Na<sup>+</sup>, Cl<sup>-</sup>), short-chain fatty acids (acetate, propionate, butyrate), and water are absorbed in the colon.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4777","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about vitamin absor​ption?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about vitamin absorption?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fat-soluble vitamin digest‌ion needs cholesterol est​erase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin B12 is absorbed in‌ ileum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Folate is absorbed by carr‌iers that are Na+ cotrans​porters","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Most vitamins are absorbed‌ in upper small intestine​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Folate is absorbed by carriers that are Na+ cotransporters","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Folate is absorbed by carriers that are Na+ cotransporters</strong></p><p><strong>Folate </strong>and <strong>vitamin B12</strong> absorption is <strong>Na<sup>+</sup>-independent</strong>.</p>\n<p>The remaining water-soluble vitamins—thiamin, riboflavin, niacin, pyridoxine, pantothe­nate, biotin, and ascorbic acid are absorbed by carriers that are Na+ cotransporters.</p>\n<p>Option A: Fat-soluble vitamins are ingested in the form of esters. Digestion needs hydrolysis by cholesterol esterase and absorbed after incorporating into micelles.</p>\n<p>Options B and D: Most vitamins are absorbed in the upper small intestine, but vitamin B12 is absorbed in the ileum. Vitamin B12 binds to intrinsic factor, a protein secreted by the parietal cells of the stomach. This complex is absorbed across the ileal mucosa.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4776","text":"A patient with anemia is p‌rescribed iron tablets. T​he absorption of the supp‌lement is likely to take ‌place in which part of th‍e intestine?","raw_text":"A patient with anemia is prescribed iron tablets. The absorption of the supplement is likely to take place in which part of the intestine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duod‌enum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Jej‌unum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Il‌eum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sto‌mach","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Duodenum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Duodenum</strong></p><p>Almost all the <strong>iron absorption</strong> occurs in the<strong> duodenum.</strong></p>\n<p>Most of the iron in the diet is in the <strong>ferric </strong>(Fe<sup>3+</sup>) form, whereas it is the <strong>ferrous </strong>(Fe<sup>2+</sup>) form that is absorbed.</p>\n<p>Note: Carbohydrates, proteins, lipids are absorbed throughout the length of the small intestine. <strong>Bile acids</strong> are present throughout the small intestine, but <strong>active absorption</strong> occurs only in the <strong>ileum, </strong>and<strong> active absorbtion </strong>of <strong>folate</strong> occurs in the <strong>upper small intestine</strong> (duodenum and jejunum).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4778","text":"Which of the following sti‌mulates ferritin synthesi​s?","raw_text":"Which of the following stimulates ferritin synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cerulo‌plasmin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepc‌idin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ir‌on","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trans‌ferrin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Iron","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/574b084b27a3462c870370a41603750fx1280x1098.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Iron</strong></p><p><strong>Elemental iron</strong> stimulates <strong>ferritin</strong> <strong>synthesis.</strong></p>\n<p>It synthesizes ferritin by causing the release of a cytoplasmic protein that binds to a specific region in the ferritin mRNA. This disruption activates ferritin mRNA and results in its translation.</p>\n<p><strong>Hepcidin</strong> binds to ferroportin and triggers its <strong>internalization</strong> and <strong>degradation</strong>. This results in decreased iron absorption and decreased circulating iron levels which <strong>reduces</strong> ferritin synthesis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Ferritin sequesters Fe<sup>2+</sup>, a potentially toxic molecule.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4779","text":"Which of the following is ‌inhibited by hepcidin?","raw_text":"Which of the following is inhibited by hepcidin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cobalamin ‌absorption","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iron ab‌sorption","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Folic acid‌ synthesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Copper a‌bsorption","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Iron absorption","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f5d32b9235f249359232ff9facd1bbd8x1279x1098.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Iron absorption</strong></p><p><strong>Hepcidin inhibits iron absorption. </strong></p>\n<p>It is named for its originally described property as a <strong>hep</strong>atocellular protein with bacteri<strong>cid</strong>al activities.</p>\n<p>Iron metabolism is regulated by hepcidin, a protein and acute phase reactant produced by the liver. It binds to ferroportin and degrades it.<strong> </strong>This inhibits the transfer of iron from the enterocyte to the plasma via ferroportin. As a result, iron becomes trapped within the duodenal cells (enterocytes) in the form of mucosal ferritin and is lost in feces when these cells are shed off.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4780","text":"While prescribing iron to ‌a patient with iron defic​iency anemia, which of th‌e following would you rec‌ommend to increase its ab‍sorption?","raw_text":"While prescribing iron to a patient with iron deficiency anemia, which of the following would you recommend to increase its absorption?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mi‌lk","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ascorb‌ic acid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ant‌acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tan‌nins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ascorbic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ascorbic acid</strong></p><p><strong>Ascorbic acid</strong> is given with <strong>iron</strong> to <strong>increase</strong> its <strong>absorption.</strong></p>\n<p>Ascorbic acid (vitamin C) forms soluble complexes with iron and reduces iron from the ferric to the ferrous state, thereby enhancing iron absorption.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Milk</strong> decreases iron absorption.</p>\n<p>Option C: Gastric secretions increase iron absorption by dissolving the iron and allowing it to form soluble complexes<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> with ascorbic and other substances. This helps in</span> its reduction to the Fe2+ form.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> <strong>Antacid</strong> decreases the absorption of iron.</span></p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Tannins</strong> (present in tea), phytates, phosphates, and oxalates form insoluble complexes with iron and lower its absorption.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9652","text":"All of the following regul‌ates intestinal absorptio​n of iron except:","raw_text":"All of the following regulates intestinal absorption of iron except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dietary int‌ake of iron","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Daily los‌s of iron","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"State of iron stores in th‌e body","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"State of erythropoiesis in‌ bone marrow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Daily loss of iron","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Daily loss of iron</strong></p><p>Intestinal absorption of iron is not regulated by the daily loss of iron.</p>\n<p>The <strong>intestinal absorption</strong> of iron is <strong>regulated</strong> by <strong>three factors:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Recent dietary intake of iron</li>\n<li>State of the iron stores in the body</li>\n<li>State of erythropoiesis in the bone marrow</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4774","text":"What is the end product af‌ter the action of colonic​ bacteria on dietary fibe‌r?","raw_text":"What is the end product after the action of colonic bacteria on dietary fiber?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Free r‌adicals","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glyc‌erol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Buty‌rate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Suc‌rose","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Butyrate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Butyrate</strong></p><p>The action of <strong>colonic bacteria</strong> on <strong>dietary fiber</strong> would give <strong>butyrate.</strong></p>\n<p>Colonic bacteria act on complex carbohydrates, resistant starches, and dietary fiber to form short-chain fatty acids such as acetate, propionate, and butyrate.</p>\n<p>SCFAs are 2–5-carbon weak acids that have an average normal concentration of about 80 mmol/L in the lumen.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4782","text":"Where are the cell bodies ‌of the orexinergic neuron​s are present in a patien‌t?","raw_text":"Where are the cell bodies of the orexinergic neurons are present in a patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Locus c‌eruleus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dorsal‌ raphe","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral hypo‌thalamic area","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hippo‌campus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lateral hypothalamic area","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lateral hypothalamic area</strong></p><p>Cell bodies of <strong>orexigenic neurons</strong> are present in the<strong> lateral hypothalamic area. </strong>Orexin stimulates food intake.</p>\n<p>Role of <strong>hypothalamus</strong> in gastrointestinal and feeding regulation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hunger center -</strong> lateral hypothalamic area</li>\n<li><strong>Satiety center -</strong> ventromedial nuclei</li>\n<li><strong>Feeding reflexes </strong>such as licking the lips and swallowing<strong> - </strong>mammillary bodies<strong>.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: The arcuate nucleus contains at least two different types of neurons that when stimulated lead to either increased or decreased appetite.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4784","text":"In a patient who is fastin‌g, the stomach releases a​ hormone that increases f‌ood intake. Identify the ‌hormone secreted.","raw_text":"In a patient who is fasting, the stomach releases a hormone that increases food intake. Identify the hormone secreted.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ore‌xin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lep‌tin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‌CK","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ghr‌elin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ghrelin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ghrelin</strong></p><p>The hormone secreted by the <strong>stomach</strong> that increases <strong>food intake</strong> is<strong> ghrelin.</strong></p>\n<p>Ghrelin, the hunger hormone is produced by the endocrine cells in the<strong> oxyntic glands </strong>of the stomach. It <strong>increases food intake. </strong>Its levels increase during fasting, peaks just before the beginning of food intake, or when the person is expecting a meal. The levels fall within 1 hour of the start of feeding.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Orexin</strong> is synthesized from <strong>lateral hypothalamic neurons.</strong> It increases food intake.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Leptin</strong> is produced by <strong>adipocytes.</strong> It decreases food intake.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>CCK</strong> is released from the <strong>small intestine.</strong> It decreases food intake.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4785","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding ghrelin?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding ghrelin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increases‌ appetite","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibits le‌ptin release","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreases gastric acid sec‌retion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stimulates growth hormone ‌release","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Decreases gastric acid secretion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Decreases gastric acid secretion</strong></p><p>Ghrelin<strong> increases gastric acid secretion.</strong></p>\n<p>Ghrelin is mainly produced by the endocrine cells in the oxyntic gland area of the stomach. It is also secreted by the pancreas and adrenal glands.</p>\n<p><strong>Functions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Increases appetite</strong>/food intake (orexigenic)</li>\n<li><strong>Inhibits leptin release</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Stimulates growth hormone secretion</strong> by directly acting on anterior pituitary (<strong>ghre</strong>lin-<strong>g</strong>rowth <strong>h</strong>ormone-<strong>re</strong>leasing peptide)</li>\n<li>Increases gastric acid secretion and gastrointestinal motility to prepare the body for food intake</li>\n<li>Regulates the distribution and rate of use of energy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4787","text":"What will be the trend of ‌leptin levels in a patien​t with a BMI of 34 kg/m2.‌","raw_text":"What will be the trend of leptin levels in a patient with a BMI of 34 kg/m2.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hi‌gh","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nor‌mal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"L‌ow","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"May be low‌ or normal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. High","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. High</strong></p><p>A patient with a BMI of 34 kg/m2 is categorized as obese. <strong>Leptin levels</strong> are <strong>high</strong> in <strong>obesity.</strong></p>\n<p>Leptin is an <strong>appetite-suppressing hormone </strong>synthesized from adipocytes. The larger the fat stores, the more leptin is released into the blood.</p>\n<p>Leptin resistance in obese persons may be due to its failure to cross the blood-brain barrier and reach the hypothalamus to serve its appetite-suppressing function.</p>\n<p>Note<strong>: </strong>A recent study has indicated that triglycerides which are elevated in obesity, inhibit the transport of leptin across the blood-brain barrier.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7402","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a dietary fiber?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a dietary fiber?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cell‌ulose","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inu‌lin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pec‌tin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gu‌ms","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gums","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gums</strong></p><p><strong>Gum </strong>is the best answer among the given options. Even though all of the given options are dietary fibers, the <strong>consumption</strong> of <strong>gums</strong> <strong>through</strong> <strong>diet</strong> is <strong>very</strong> <strong>low</strong> and hence, it is the best answer.</p>\n<p>Dietary fibers consist of both soluble and insoluble forms and include <strong>lignin, pectin, inulin, cellulose, hemicellulose</strong>, etc.</p>\n<p><strong>Advantages</strong> of<strong> fibers:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Forms bulk of the stool and reduces stool transit time</li>\n<li>Reduces incidence of carcinoma colon, DM, CAD, diverticulitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4768","text":"All of the following have ‌a significant role in lip​id digestion except:","raw_text":"All of the following have a significant role in lipid digestion except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pancreat‌ic lipase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coli‌pase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lingual‌ lipase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bile ‌acids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lingual lipase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lingual lipase</strong></p><p><strong>Lingual lipases</strong> are of <strong>little</strong> quantitative<strong> significance</strong> for<strong> lipid digestion</strong> other than in the setting of pancreatic insufficiency.</p>\n<p>Lingual lipase is secreted by Ebner glands on the dorsal surface of the tongue in some species, and the stomach also secretes lipase.</p>\n<p><strong>Digestion</strong> of<strong> Lipids</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pancreatic lipase</strong> is the most important enzyme involved in lipid digestion. The main products of its action are FFA and 2-monoglycerides (2-monoacylglycerols)</li>\n<li><strong>Colipase </strong>is an accessory factor secreted in the pancreatic juice that helps to stabilize pancreatic lipase in an active conformation</li>\n<li><strong>Bile acids</strong> emulsify the insoluble lipids and make them reach the surface of the mucosal cells. Bile acids also activate another pancreatic lipase called cholesterol esterase</li>\n<li><strong>Cholesterol esterase</strong> hydrolyzes cholesterol esters, esters of fat-soluble vitamins, phospholipids, and triglycerides</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0741","text":"Following complete ileal a‌nd partial jejunal resect​ion, the patient is most ‌likely to have","raw_text":"Following complete ileal and partial jejunal resection, the patient is most likely to have","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Folic acid‌ deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin B12‌ deficiency","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gastri‌c ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Consti‌pation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vitamin B12 deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vitamin B12 deficiency</strong></p><p>After complete<strong> ileal</strong> and partial <strong>jejunal resection,</strong> the patient is likely to have <strong>vitamin B12 deficiency.</strong></p>\n<p>Vitamin B12 is absorbed in the ileum. Vitamin B12 binds to intrinsic factor, a protein secreted by the parietal cells of the stomach, and the complex is absorbed across the ileal mucosa. Hence after ileal resection, there is impaired absorption of vitamin B12.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3421","text":"Transport molecule for Fe2‌+ into the enterocytes is​","raw_text":"Transport molecule for Fe2+ into the enterocytes is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ferrop‌ortin 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepha‌estin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Divalent Metal Transporter‌ 1","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Divalent Metal Transporter‌ 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Divalent Metal Transporter 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b607d17f9fe4494390dd0d8bc8614af9x1279x1210.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Divalent Metal Transporter 1</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Transport of <strong>Fe2+ into</strong> the <strong>enterocytes</strong> occurs via<strong> divalent metal transporter</strong> <strong>1 (DMT1), </strong>through an <strong>active transport</strong> process.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Protein DMT-1 (Divalent Metal Transporter-1) is expressed on the apical surface of enterocytes in the initial part of the duodenum. DMT-1 is not specific to iron &amp; transports other substances like zinc, copper, cobalt &amp; manganese.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7408","text":"Which part of the liver st‌ores vitamin A?","raw_text":"Which part of the liver stores vitamin A?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ito ‌cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hepat‌ocytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endothel‌ial cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kupffe‌r cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ito cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ito cells</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 369\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Vitamin A</strong> is stored in the liver <strong>perisinusoidal stellate (Ito) cells </strong>as<strong> retinyl ester (retinol palmitate).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Kupffer cells,</strong> also known as <strong>stellate macrophages</strong> and <strong>Kupffer-Borowicz cells; </strong>are specialized macrophages located in the liver that form part of the mononuclear phagocyte system. These are present, lining the walls of the sinusoids, not the perisinusoidal space (Space of Disse).</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"HR8Fb7Ox","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"32_special_areas_of_childhood_mental_health","title":"32.Special Areas Of Childhood Mental Health","num_questions":9,"total_marks":36,"duration":19,"time_per_question":126.67,"questions":[{"id":"MC2898","text":"A 4-year-old orphan child ‌does not seem to seek com​fort during times of dist‍ress. Even when comforted​, the child doesn't seem ‌to be interested in it. T‌his behaviour has been pr​esent for more than a yea​r noticed by caregivers w​hen he was shifted betwee‌n multiple foster homes. ‍What is the most probable‌ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old orphan child does not seem to seek comfort during times of distress. Even when comforted, the child doesn't seem to be interested in it. This behaviour has been present for more than a year noticed by caregivers when he was shifted between multiple foster homes. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disinhibited social engage‌ment disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autism spect‌rum disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reactive attachment disord‌er","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intellectua‌l disability","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Reactive attachment disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Reactive attachment disorder</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario suggests <strong>reactive attachment disorder.</strong></p>\n<p>Reactive attachment disorder is characterized by the following: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>A pattern of <strong>inhibited, emotionally withdrawn</strong> behavior toward adult caregivers manifested as:\n<ul>\n<li>The child rarely or <strong>minimally seeks comfort</strong> when distressed.</li>\n<li>The child rarely or <strong>minimally responds to comfort</strong> when distressed.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>A persistent <strong>social and emotional disturbance</strong> involving any two of the following:\n<ul>\n<li>Minimal social and emotional responsiveness to others.</li>\n<li>Limited positive affect.</li>\n<li>Episodes of unexplained irritability, sadness, or fearfulness are evident even during nonthreatening interactions with adult caregivers.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The child has experienced a pattern of <strong>extremes of insufficient care</strong> (evident by early separation, frequent change in caregivers, institutionalization or neglect)</li>\n<li>The disturbance is evident before 5 years of age and the developmental age of the child is at least 9 months. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A - Disinhibited social engagement disorder is a condition where the child approaches and interacts with unfamiliar adults in either <strong>overly familiar behavior</strong>, has diminished checking back with caregiver after venturing away (even in unfamiliar settings), with the willingness to go off with an unfamiliar adult.</p>\n<p>Option B - Autism spectrum disorder is characterized by deficits in social communication and interaction across multiple contexts as manifested by deficits in socio-emotional reciprocity, <strong>difficulty in developing, maintaining and understanding relationships</strong>, and deficits in non verbal communicative behaviors.</p>\n<p>Option D - Children with intellectual disabilities have deficits in adaptive functioning which, without support, limit functioning in activities of daily life (communication, social participation, independent living) across multiple environments. They are also characterized by having <strong>deficits in intellectual functions</strong> (reasoning, problem solving, planning, abstract thinking, judgment etc.)</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2892","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about pica?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about pica?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 years is the minimum age‌ for diagnosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"More common among children‌ with autism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with lead intox‌ication","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is not se‌en in adults","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is not seen in adults","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is not seen in adults</strong></p><p><strong>Pica</strong> is defined as the persistent eating of <strong>nonnutritive substances</strong>. It can emerge in children, adolescents, or adults (usually pregnant women).</p>\n<p>A minimum of <strong>2 years of age</strong> is suggested by DSM-5 for the diagnosis of pica. </p>\n<p>Pica is more common among children and adolescents with <strong>autism spectrum disorder</strong> and <strong>intellectual disability</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The clinical implications can be benign or life-threatening, depending on the objects ingested. Among the most serious complications are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Lead poisoning</strong> - usually after ingestion of lead-based paint</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Intestinal parasites</strong> - after ingestion of soil or feces</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Anemia and zinc deficiency</strong> - after ingestion of clay</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Intestinal obstruction</strong> - from the ingestion of hairballs, stones, or gravel.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>No definitive treatment exists for pica. Most treatment is aimed at <strong>education and behavior modification. </strong>Medical complications that develop secondarily to the pica must be treated.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2899","text":"A 7-year-old girl actively‌ approaches and interacts​ with unfamiliar adults d‍espite multiple attempts ​of telling her otherwise.‌ Both her parents are wor‌king professionals and ar​e scared that this behavi​our could lead to her fal​ling prey to abduction. W‌hat is this pattern of be‍haviour in the child know‌n as?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old girl actively approaches and interacts with unfamiliar adults despite multiple attempts of telling her otherwise.  Both her parents are working professionals and are scared that this behaviour could lead to her falling prey to abduction. What is this pattern of behaviour in the child known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reactive attachment disord‌er","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Attention deficit hyperact‌ivity disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Social anxi‌ety disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disinhibited social engage‌ment disorder","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Disinhibited social engagement disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Disinhibited social engagement disorder</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario suggests a diagnosis of <strong>disinhibited social engagement disorder</strong>.</p>\n<p>Disinhibited social engagement disorder is characterized by the following: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>A pattern of behavior in which a <strong>child actively approaches and interacts with unfamiliar adults.</strong></li>\n<li>The behaviors are not limited to <strong>impulsivity</strong> (as in attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder) but include <strong>socially disinhibited behavior.</strong></li>\n<li>The child has experienced a pattern of extremes of <strong>insufficient care</strong> (pathogenic care).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2900","text":"What is the most prevalent‌ form of child maltreatme​nt?","raw_text":"What is the most prevalent form of child maltreatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Child phys‌ical abuse","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Child sex‌ual abuse","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Child ‌neglect","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Child psycho‌logical abuse","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Child neglect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Child neglect</strong></p><p><strong>Child neglect</strong> is the <strong>most prevalent</strong> form of <strong>child maltreatment</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is the failure to provide adequate care and protection for children. Children can be harmed by malicious or ignorant withholding of physical, emotional, and educational necessities.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2901","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a phase of child sexu​al abuse?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a phase of child sexual abuse?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Engag‌ement","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sexual in‌teraction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Barga‌ining","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sec‌recy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bargaining","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bargaining</strong></p><p><strong>Bargaining</strong> is not a phase of child sexual abuse. It is one of the stages of grief. </p>\n<p>Sexual abuse of a child that occurs over a period of time evolves through the following five phases:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Engagement-</strong> The perpetrator induces the child into a special relationship.</li>\n<li><strong>Sexual interaction-</strong> The sexual behaviors progress from less to more intrusive forms of abuse.</li>\n<li><strong>Secrecy-</strong> The perpetrator threatens the victim not to tell.</li>\n<li><strong>Disclosure-</strong> The abuse is discovered accidentally (when another person walks into the room and sees it) or through the child's reporting it to a responsible adult or via medical attention.</li>\n<li><strong>Suppression-</strong> The child often retracts the statements of the disclosure because of family pressure or because of the child's own mental processes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0967","text":"A 6-year-old child was bro‌ught with complaints of b​edwetting at night. There‍ is no history of bedwett​ing during the afternoon ‌nap. His urine examinatio‌n was normal. Specific gr​avity was 1.020. What wou​ld be your advice?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old child was brought with complaints of bedwetting at night. There is no history of bedwetting during the afternoon nap. His urine examination was normal. Specific gravity was 1.020. What would be your advice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Consult a child psychologi‌st","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"USG a‌bdomen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Give desmopressin 0.2 mg o‌rally","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CT-KUB a‌nd pelvis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Consult a child psychologist","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Consult a child psychologist</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">According to the given history, the child has <strong>nocturnal enuresis</strong>, and hence <strong>consulting a child psychologist</strong> is the most appropriate option.</p>\n<p><strong>Nocturnal enuresis</strong> refers to the occurrence of <strong>involuntary voiding</strong> at night after 5 years of age. </p>\n<p>If there are <strong>no daytime symptoms</strong> and <strong>physical </strong>and<strong> urine examinations </strong>are<strong> normal</strong>, then further evaluation for urinary tract pathology is not warranted. USG is done in an older child (usually &gt;7 years) with enuresis or in those who do not respond to therapy. </p>\n<p>Active treatment should be avoided in children younger than 6 years of age. Treatment includes the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"1\" data-font=\"Symbol\" data-leveltext=\"\" data-listid=\"1\"><strong>Psychological</strong> therapy includes motivational therapy (star charts), <strong>conditioning therapies</strong> like auditory or vibratory alarms (<strong>most effective</strong>). </li>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"2\" data-font=\"Symbol\" data-leveltext=\"\" data-listid=\"1\"><strong>Pharmacological</strong> therapy (<strong>imipramine </strong>and<strong> desmopressin</strong>) is intended to <strong>treat the symptoms</strong> of enuresis and thus is regarded as second-line and is <strong>not curative. </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2893","text":"As a first-year psychiatry‌ resident, you see a case​ of a child with a histor‍y of repeated passage of ​faeces at inappropriate p‌laces. To make a diagnosi‌s of encopresis, what sho​uld be the minimum age of​ this child?","raw_text":"As a first-year psychiatry resident, you see a case of a child with a history of repeated passage of faeces at inappropriate places. To make a diagnosis of encopresis, what should be the minimum age of this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 y‌ears","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 y‌ears","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"5 y‌ears","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6 y‌ears","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4 years</strong></p><p>The diagnosis of<strong> encopresis</strong> is not made until the child crosses <strong>4 years of age.  </strong>It is an elimination disorder. </p>\n<p>Elimination disorders described in DSM-5 and ICD-11: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Encopresis- repeated <strong>passage of faeces</strong> at inappropriate places.</li>\n<li>Enuresis-<strong> repeated urination</strong> into bed or clothes. It is diagnosed after the <strong>age of 5 years</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6705","text":"A 7-year-old child present‌ed with complaints of bed​-wetting almost twice a w‍eek for the past 1 year. ​A detailed investigation ‌was done to rule out the ‌organic cause. What shoul​d be the treatment plan f​or this child?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old child presented with complaints of bed-wetting almost twice a week for the past 1 year. A detailed investigation was done to rule out the organic cause. What should be the treatment plan for this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pharmacotherapy with imipr‌amine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Psychodynamic psychotherap‌y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bladder training, with rew‌ard for delaying micturit​ion during daytime","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bell and pad based classic‌al conditioning","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bell and pad based classical conditioning","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bell and pad based classical conditioning</strong></p><p>The clinical features are suggestive of nocturnal enuresis. <strong>Bell and pad based classical conditioning</strong> is the most effective treatment.</p>\n<p><strong>Nocturnal enuresis</strong> refers to the occurrence of involuntary urination at night <strong>after 5 years of age</strong>. It may be a normal phenomenon in children up to 5 years of age.</p>\n<p>Treatment for nocturnal enuresis may include:</p>\n<p><strong>Behavioural therapy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Classic conditioning with the <strong>bell</strong> (or buzzer) and <strong>pad</strong> (alarm) apparatus is the <strong>most effective</strong> treatment for enuresis.</li>\n<li>Dryness is achieved in 50% of the cases.</li>\n<li><strong>Bladder training</strong> with encouragement or reward for delaying micturition for increasing times during waking hours is useful but considered <strong>inferior</strong> to bell and pad.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Pharmacotherapy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Medications should be considered<strong> if behavioural therapy has failed,</strong> and if enuresis is causing impairment in social, family, and school function.</li>\n<li>Desmopressin (an antidiuretic) and Reboxetine (norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor) are commonly used drugs. Reboxetine is considered as a <strong>safer alternative to imipramine.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Psychotherapy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is indicated in cases with chronic enuresis, to deal with any coexisting psy­chiatric problems and emotional and family difficulties.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6707","text":"A 10-year-old child is suf‌fering from selective mut​ism. He is most probably ‍suffering from?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old child is suffering from selective mutism. He is most probably suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Childhood ‌depression","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Childhood‌ psychosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Childhood anxiety disorder‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperkinet‌ic disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Childhood anxiety disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Childhood anxiety disorder</strong></p><p>The child is most likely suffering from a <strong>childhood anxiety disorder. </strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Selective mutism </strong>refers to the <strong>lack of speaking</strong> (being mute) in <strong>one or more specific social situations</strong>, commonly in school, but <strong>speaking well in other situations</strong>, commonly at home. The child may be either <strong>completely silent</strong> at school or maybe just <strong>whispering. </strong>Rarely, a child may be silent at home but communicating normally at school.</p>\n<p>The disorder may develop in children between <strong>4 to 8 years</strong> of age and is associated with a <strong>social anxiety disorder.</strong> They may have symptoms of separation anxi­ety disorder, school refusal, and delayed language acquisition.</p>\n<p>The first-line treatment includes <strong>cognitive behavioural therapy.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A and B: selective mutism is not commonly associated with childhood depression or psychosis</p>\n<p>Option D: Hyperkinetic disorder is associated with <strong>overfamiliarity.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"U5F_PAZe","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"32_bile_duct","title":"32.Bile Duct","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MD0838","text":"A 35-year-old man presente‍d with recurrent episodes​ of fever with chills. MR​CP image is shown below. ‌What is the most likely d​iagnosis?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man presented with recurrent episodes of fever with chills. MRCP image is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary sclerosing cholang‍itis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Caroli ‍disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oriental c‍holangitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Primary bili‍ary carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Primary sclerosing cholangitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/08dbd37ac2644dddbce5f384204f9914.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/615f6ae3c19f4f27a8acdddfd78aa2f3x315x307.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Primary sclerosing cholangitis</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The presence of fever and chills, along with the <strong>MRCP </strong>showing multiple areas of <strong>intrahepatic</strong> <strong>beading</strong> is typical of <strong>primary sclerosing cholangitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: Caroli's disease is a rare congenital condition characterized by<strong> irregular dilatation of the intrahepatic bile ducts</strong> with a <strong>normal extrahepatic duct system.</strong> It is common in both sexes and mostly presents <strong>before the age of 30 years</strong> with abdominal pain, cholangitis, or end-stage liver disease. MRCP shows irregular, cystic dilation of the large proximal intrahepatic bile ducts with a <strong>normal common bile duct.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Primary sclerosing cholangitis </strong>(PSC) is an autoimmune condition characterized by inflammatory strictures involving the <strong>intra and extra-hepatic</strong> biliary tree. It is a progressive condition that leads to <strong>secondary biliary cirrhosis. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>MRCP or ERCP</strong> will show demonstrate multifocal, diffuse dilatation and strictures of the intrahepatic and extrahepatic biliary trees, this pattern is a <strong>beading or chain of lakes</strong> <strong>appearance</strong>. <strong>Pseudodiverticula</strong> of extrahepatic bile ducts are pathognomic of PSC. <strong>MRCP</strong> is useful in<strong> diagnosis</strong> and surveillance, while <strong>ERCP</strong> is reserved for the <strong>treatment</strong> of dominant strictures and to exclude malignancy. </p>\n<p>The below image shows a cholangiogram of primary sclerosing cholangitis:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The common age group of primary sclerosing cholangitis is <strong>30-45 years</strong>. <strong>Men</strong> are affected twice as commonly as women. Two-thirds of these cases are associated with <strong>ulcerative colitis. </strong>It is also associated with <strong>Riedel’s thyroiditis</strong> and<strong> retroperitoneal fibrosis.</strong> 10–20% of patients develop <strong>cholangiocarcinoma. </strong></p>\n<p>Clinical features of a patient with PSC include pruritis, fever, chills, right upper quadrant discomfort, jaundice, and weight loss. </p>\n<p>Liver function tests show a cholestatic pattern with an <strong>elevation of ALP </strong>and <strong>GGT</strong> and smaller rises in the aminotransferases. Bilirubin values may be variable.</p>\n<p>Liver biopsy <strong>excludes</strong> other causes of cholestatic liver disease and defines the <strong>stage</strong> of PSC. It shows <strong>onion skin concentric periductal fibrosis</strong>. With disease progression, periportal fibrosis occurs, progressing to bridging necrosis, and eventually biliary cirrhosis.</p>\n<p>Currently, there is <strong>no known effective medical therapy.</strong> Surgical management involving <strong>resection of extrahepatic biliary tree </strong>and<strong> hepaticojejunostomy</strong> has shown reasonable effects. For patients with advanced liver disease, <strong>liver transplantation </strong>is the only available option.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option C: Oriental cholangitis, also known as<strong> recurrent pyogenic cholangitis</strong> is characterized by recurrent attacks of cholangitis due to bile stasis and stone formation. Patients between <strong>30-40 years</strong> and<strong> both sexes</strong> are equally affected. It is caused due to infections (liver flukes, E.coli, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas, and Proteus). Patients present with Charcot's triad (intermittent fever, pain, and jaundice). <strong>MRCP</strong> shows intra or extrahepatic <strong>calculi,</strong> dilatation of ducts, and strictures. </p>\n<p>Option D: Biliary carcinoma  (cholangiocarcinoma) most commonly affects patients <strong>&gt;60 years</strong> and presents with fever, abdominal pain, weight loss, and features of obstructive jaundice. <strong>MRCP</strong> shows irregular <strong>narrowing</strong> of the bile duct involved by the <strong>tumor,</strong> with asymmetric upstream dilatation of the intrahepatic ducts. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6741","text":"According to Strasberg’s c‍lassification of bile duc​t injuries, injury to the​ right aberrant posterior‌ sectoral duct with bile ​leak is:","raw_text":"According to Strasberg’s classification of bile duct injuries, injury to the right aberrant posterior sectoral duct with bile leak is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‍e A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‍e B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‍e C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‍e D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cd7f3f35c9bd40019cf4dca22baa3ff8x1280x3846.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/01e363fe8e3344b78c45d8a2148d4b3cx1280x2262.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type C</strong></p><p>Injury to the right aberrant posterior sectoral duct associated with bile leak is classified as <strong>Strasberg’s type C injury.</strong></p>\n<p>Biliary injury may occur <strong>post-laparoscopic cholecystectomy</strong>. The most common site of bile leak following cholecystectomy is the <strong>cystic duct</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>MRCP</strong> is the gold standard for complete morphological evaluation of the biliary tree. MRCP with magnetic resonance angiography is more useful in cases of associated vascular injury. <strong>A HIDA scan can confirm</strong> the presence of a <strong>bile leak</strong> or biliary obstruction. </p>\n<p>The <strong>Strasberg classification </strong>of bile duct injury is widely used to anatomically define bile duct injuries.</p>\n<h4>Classification</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type A</strong>: Bile leak from cystic duct stump or a minor biliary radical in the gallbladder fossa</li>\n<li><strong>Type B</strong>: Occluded right posterior sectoral duct.</li>\n<li><strong>Type C</strong>: Bile leak from the <strong>divided right posterior sectoral duct.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Type D</strong>: Bile leak from the main bile duct without major tissue loss.</li>\n<li><strong>Type E</strong>: Injury to the main hepatic duct; classified according to the level of injury\n<ul>\n<li>E1: Transected main bile duct with a stricture more than 2cm from the hilus.</li>\n<li>E2: Transected main bile duct with a stricture less than 2cm from the hilus</li>\n<li>E3: Stricture of the hilus with right and left ducts in communication.</li>\n<li>E4: Stricture of the hilus with separation of right and left hepatic ducts.</li>\n<li>E5: Stricture of the right aberrant sectoral and main bile ducts.  </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Management of bile duct injuries is shown below: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6788","text":"A patient presents with pa‍in in the right upper qua​drant on the second posto​perative day following a ‌cholecystectomy. Evaluati​on reveals a collection i​n the subhepatic space. A‌ bile leak was suspected.​ Which of the following i​s the best investigation ​for this patient?","raw_text":"A patient presents with pain in the right upper quadrant on the second postoperative day following a cholecystectomy. Evaluation reveals a collection in the subhepatic space. A bile leak was suspected. Which of the following is the best investigation for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MR‍CP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ER‍CP","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"HI‍DA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CE‍CT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ERCP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/370e42f1a826464e8e2b9a4616687662x1280x2262.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ERCP</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP)</strong> is the best investigation in a patient suspected of <strong>post-cholecystectomy biliary leaks</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Patients with post-cholecystectomy biliary leak usually present with fever, jaundice and abdominal pain<strong>. </strong> <strong>USG</strong> is used for an <strong>initial evaluation</strong> in such cases and can show a collection in the sub-hepatic space. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The most common bile leak following cholecystectomy is from the <strong>cystic duct.</strong> This can be treated by placing a <strong>biliary endoprosthesis </strong>(stent) in the common bile duct across the origin of the cystic duct.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">MRCP shows fluid collections arising from bile leaks but cannot differentiate these from other causes of fluid collections.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>ERCP</strong> is the <strong>best investigation</strong> as it has the advantage of being both<strong> diagnostic</strong> and<strong> therapeutic. </strong> ERCP is useful post-cholecystectomy as there is a high likelihood of a retained stone and stone extraction and/or temporary stenting may be required. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>HIDA scan</strong> is the <strong>most sensitive</strong> investigation for biliary leakage.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>CT</strong> scan can show focal fluid collections, ascites, biliary obstruction, hepatic atrophy, signs of secondary biliary cirrhosis and any vascular injury. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9377","text":"A patient underwent laparo‍scopic cholecystectomy an​d was discharged after re​moving the drain tube on ‌the first postoperative d​ay. On day three, the pat​ient came back to the hos‌pital and was febrile. On​ ultrasound examination, ​there was a 5 x 5 cm coll​ection in the subhepatic ‍space. What is the next b​est step?","raw_text":"A patient underwent laparoscopic cholecystectomy and was discharged after removing the drain tube on the first postoperative day. On day three, the patient came back to the hospital and was febrile. On ultrasound examination, there was a 5 x 5 cm collection in the subhepatic space. What is the next best step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"To give oral antibiotics s‍ince the given accumulati​on is normal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"To re-explore the wound an‍d insert a T-tube","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Insert pigtail catheter fo‍r drainage","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"ERCP and‍ stenting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Insert pigtail catheter for drainage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b054ae0f1fd4a04baa35c8686c0c01ax1280x2262.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Insert pigtail catheter for drainage</strong></p><p>The given clinical situation suggests a<strong> bile duct injury</strong> following laparoscopic cholecystectomy. The <strong>next best step</strong> after imaging would be to control inflammation by <strong>drainage </strong>of fluid collections by <strong>pigtail insertion </strong>and administration of broad-spectrum <strong>IV antibiotics</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3270","text":"Five days following a comm‍on bile duct injury resul​ting in a leak, a patient​ presents with fever and ‌abdominal pain. What woul​d be the next step in man​agement?","raw_text":"Five days following a common bile duct injury resulting in a leak, a patient presents with fever and abdominal pain. What would be the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ultrasound-guided aspirati‍on","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Endoscopic retrograde chol‍angiopancreatography (ERC​P) and stenting","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Re-exploration and hepatoj‍ejunostomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Re-exploration and primary‍ repair","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ultrasound-guided aspiration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/15d8a67b4bd34ed5a0a335da8be86b4fx1280x2262.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ultrasound-guided aspiration</strong></p><p>In the case of a <strong>common bile duct injury</strong> with a leak and a patient presenting with signs of localized peritonitis, the next step of management is an<strong> ultrasound-guided aspiration. </strong>This will be<strong> </strong>followed by <strong>ERCP and stenting.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Laparoscopic cholecystectomy</strong> is the most common cause of <strong>bile duct injury</strong>. Major biliary leakage is usually seen 2 to 10 days after cholecystectomy. Affected patients typically present with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Abdominal pain</li>\n<li>Bilious ascites</li>\n<li>Mild jaundice</li>\n<li>Leukocytosis</li>\n<li>Elevations in the serum levels of ALP and GGT.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Management of bile duct injury:</p>\n<p>If the injury is identified <strong>during surgery:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Partial injury to the bile duct (&lt;3mm) - <strong>repair</strong> the injury</li>\n<li>Complete transection without loss of segment - <strong>anastomose over T-tube</strong></li>\n<li>Complete transection with loss of segment - close distal end and carry out <strong>Roux-en-Y hepaticoduodenostomy/jejunostomy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>If the leak is suspected<strong> postoperatively:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Transabdominal ultrasonography</strong> is initially performed to assess the leak. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the patient presents <strong>within 48 hours</strong> - <strong>Re-explore</strong> and repair</li>\n<li>If the patient presents <strong>after 48 hours</strong> - <strong>USG-guided aspiration </strong>or<strong> pigtail catheter </strong>insertion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is followed by<strong> ERCP </strong>and<strong> stenting.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3271","text":"Where does biliary strictu‍re most commonly occur af​ter laparoscopic cholecys​tectomy?","raw_text":"Where does biliary stricture most commonly occur after laparoscopic cholecystectomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Common he‍patic duct","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Common b‍ile duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right hep‍atic duct","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left hep‍atic duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Common hepatic duct","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Common hepatic duct</strong></p><p><strong>Biliary stricture</strong> after <strong>laparoscopic cholecystectomy</strong> usually occurs in the <strong>common hepatic duct</strong>, distal to the confluence of the right and left hepatic ducts.</p>\n<p>After <strong>open cholecystectomy</strong>, biliary strictures are more common in the <strong>common bile duct</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7463","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is false about he​mobilia?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about hemobilia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is associated with Quin‍cke's triad","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tumors are the most common‍ cause of bleeding","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Treatment involves trans-a‍rterial embolization","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arterial angiography is th‍e diagnostic procedure of​ choice","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tumors are the most common cause of bleeding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tumors are the most common cause of bleeding</strong></p><p>The most common cause of hemobilia is<strong> iatrogenic trauma.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1498\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Hemobilia</strong> is defined as <strong>bleeding into the biliary tree</strong> from abnormal communication between a blood vessel and bile duct.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Causes of hemobilia include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Traumatic or operative injury to the liver or bile ducts</li>\n<li>Intraductal rupture of a hepatic abscess or aneurγsm of the hepatic artery</li>\n<li>Biliary or hepatic tumor hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Mechanical complications of choledocholithiasis or hepatobiliary parasitism</li>\n<li>Diagnostic procedures, such as liver biopsy, percutaneous transhepatic cholangiography (PTC), and transhepatic biliary drainage catheter placement</li>\n<li>Vascular disorders</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients often present with a classic triad <strong>(Quincke's triad/Sandblom triad<em>)</em></strong> of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Biliary pain</li>\n<li>Obstructive jaundice</li>\n<li>Melena or occult blood in the stools</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Investigation of choice:<strong> Arterial angiography (CT angiography)</strong> is the diagnostic procedure of choice.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1498\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Management:<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong> Minor hemobilia</strong> can be managed <strong>conservatively</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Major hemobilia: Transarterial embolization</strong> is indicated for <strong>major hemobilia</strong> requiring blood transfusion. Ligation of the bleeding vessel may also be required.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7464","text":"How will you confirm the d‍iagnosis in a patient sus​pected to have bilhemia?","raw_text":"How will you confirm the diagnosis in a patient suspected to have bilhemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CT angi‍ography","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endoscopic retrograde chol‍angiopancreatography","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Endoscopic ultrasonography‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Magnetic resonance cholang‍iopancreatography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography</strong></p><p><strong>Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP)</strong> is the best investigation for the diagnosis of <strong>bilhemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Bilhemia is a condition in which <strong>bile flows into the bloodstream</strong> through the hepatic veins or portal vein branches. This flow occurs due to a <strong>high intrabiliary pressure</strong>, exceeding that of the venous system.</p>\n<p>The cause can be the <strong>erosion of gallstones</strong> into the portal vein, or accidental/iatrogenic <strong>trauma</strong>. This condition can be fatal secondary to the embolization of large amounts of bile into the lungs. Usually, bile flow is low, and the <strong>fistulas close spontaneously.</strong></p>\n<p>Its clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rapidly progressive jaundice</li>\n<li>Marked direct hyperbilirubinemia, without elevation of the hepatocellular enzyme levels.</li>\n<li>Septicemia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Diagnosis of bilhemia is best made by <strong>ERCP</strong>.</p>\n<p>Treatment is directed at reducing the intrabiliary pressure through <strong>stent placement</strong> or <strong>sphincterotomy</strong>. Endovascular <strong>embolization</strong> can also be done.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7459","text":"Cholangiohepatitis is asso‍ciated with which of the ​following parasites? Clon​orchis sinensis Opisthorc‌his viverrini Schistosoma​ haematobium Ascaris lumb​ricoides Paragonimus west‌ermani","raw_text":"<p>Cholangiohepatitis is associated with which of the following parasites?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Clonorchis sinensis</li>\n<li>Opisthorchis viverrini</li>\n<li>Schistosoma haematobium</li>\n<li>Ascaris lumbricoides</li>\n<li>Paragonimus westermani</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3,‍ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2,‍ and 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 , 3,‍ and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2,‍ and 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, and 4</strong></p><p><strong>Cholangiohepatitis</strong> or recurrent pyogenic cholangitis is associated with biliary parasites such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clonorchis sinensis</li>\n<li>Opisthorchis viverrini</li>\n<li>Ascaris lumbricoides.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is caused by bacterial contamination of the biliary tree. Some commonly implicated organisms are <em>Escherichia coli</em>, Klebsiella species, Bacteroides species, or <em>Enterococcus faecalis</em>.</p>\n<p>Cholangiohepatitis is a <strong>pre-malignant</strong> condition and can result in <strong>cholangiocarcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Schistosoma haematobium</strong> primarily causes<strong> genito-urinary schistosomiasis. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Paragonimus westermani</strong> (oriental lung fluke) causes a <strong>pulmonary syndrome</strong> characterized by cough, chest pain, and hemoptysis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7531","text":"A 60-year-old man presente‍d with complaints of weig​ht loss, clay coloured st​ools, and abdominal pain.‌ CT scan and MRCP was sug​gestive of cholangiocarci​noma. What is the most co‌mmon site at which this o​ccurs?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presented with complaints of weight loss, clay coloured stools, and abdominal pain. CT scan and MRCP was suggestive of cholangiocarcinoma. What is the most common site at which this occurs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Distal third of the common‍ bile duct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Middle third of the common‍ bile duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Confluence of the right an‍d left hepatic ducts","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Right hep‍atic duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Confluence of the right and left hepatic ducts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8a8d769abcc94f6c9d33c5449ced873ax1280x5341.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Confluence of the right and left hepatic ducts</strong></p><p><strong>Cholangiocarcinomas</strong> are<strong> most common</strong> at the location where the right and left hepatic ducts meet i.e., the <strong>confluence</strong> of the hepatic ducts. </p>\n<p>Cholangiocellular carcinomas (CCCs) or cholangiocarcinomas are mucin-producing <strong>adenocarcinomas</strong> of the biliary duct system that may originate in the liver and extrahepatic bile ducts. Cholangiocarcinomas are encountered in three anatomic regions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Intrahepatic</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hilar (65%) - also known as Klatskin tumours</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Peripheral (30%)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> sclerosing </strong>variant of cholangiocarcinoma is the most common and most difficult to treat. The <strong>nodular</strong> variant is usually intrahepatic and presents as a mass lesion. The <strong>papillary</strong> variant is rare and is associated with favourable outcomes.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Portoenterostomy</strong> is done for <strong>hilar</strong> cholangiocarcioma and for<strong> peripheral</strong> cholangiocarcionoma, <strong>Whipple's surgery</strong> is done.</p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">In <strong>Bismuth-Corlette </strong>classification they are classified according to their location:<br/></span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The FDA approved <strong>Pemigatinib, FGFR2 inhibitor</strong> for patients with cholangiocarcinoma that is locally advanced or metastatic, and tumors with fusion or rearrangement of fibroblast growth factor receptor 2 (FGFR2) gene.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7448","text":"Match the following triads‍: Triad Condition 1. Sain​t's triad a. Hemobilia 2.​ Sandblom triad b. Gallst‌one ileus 3. Charcot tria​d c. Colonic diverticulos​is 4. Rigler's triad d. A‌cute cholangitis","raw_text":"<p>Match the following triads:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 228px;\" width=\"383\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183.5px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Triad</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.5px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Condition</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183.5px;\">1. Saint's triad</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.5px;\">a. Hemobilia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183.5px;\">2. Sandblom triad</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.5px;\">b. Gallstone ileus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183.5px;\">3. Charcot triad</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.5px;\">c. Colonic diverticulosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 183.5px;\">4. Rigler's triad</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.5px;\">d. Acute cholangitis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-a, 2-c,‍ 3-b, 4-d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-b, 2-a,‍ 3-d, 4-c","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-c, 2-a,‍ 3-d, 4-b","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1-c, 2-d,‍ 3-a, 4-b","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b</strong></p><p>The correct option is 1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 346px; width: 470px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 126.390625px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>Triad</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.578125px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>Condition</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 197.0625px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>Components</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 82px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 126.390625px; height: 82px;\">Saint's triad</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.578125px; height: 82px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 197.0625px; height: 82px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Hiatus hernia</li>\n<li>Gallstones</li>\n<li>Colonic diverticulosis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 82px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 126.390625px; height: 82px;\">Sandblom triad</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.578125px; height: 82px;\">Hemobilia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 197.0625px; height: 82px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Malena</li>\n<li>Biliary colic</li>\n<li>Obstructive jaundice</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 73px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 126.390625px; height: 73px;\">Charcot triad</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.578125px; height: 73px;\">Acute cholangitis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 197.0625px; height: 73px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Jaundice</li>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Right upper quadrant pain</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 100px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 126.390625px; height: 100px;\">Rigler's triad</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.578125px; height: 100px;\">Gallstone ileus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 197.0625px; height: 100px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Small bowel obstruction</li>\n<li>Pneumobilia</li>\n<li>Ectopic gallstone</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7419","text":"A 19-year-old patient pres‍ented with right upper qu​adrant pain. USG and CT s​cans showed no evidence o‌f gallstones. MRCP shows ​fusiform dilatation of th​e common bile duct. Ident‌ify the false statement r​egarding this condition.","raw_text":"A 19-year-old patient presented with right upper quadrant pain. USG and CT scans showed no evidence of gallstones. MRCP shows fusiform dilatation of the common bile duct. Identify the false statement regarding this condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It predisposes to malignan‍cy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Roux-en-Y hepaticojejunost‍omy will be done","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Seen more commonly in male‍s","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Jaundice will be the most ‍consistent finding","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Seen more commonly in males","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Seen more commonly in males</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and MRCP showing fusiform dilation of extrahepatic biliary tree point towards the diagnosis of a <strong>choledochal cyst (Type I)</strong>. Choledochal cysts are more <strong>commonly seen in females</strong>. </p>\n<p>A choledochal cyst is a <strong>premalignant </strong>condition leading to the development of cholangiocarcinoma<strong>. </strong></p>\n<p>The classic clinical triad of symptoms includes <strong>abdominal pain</strong>, <strong>jaundice</strong> (most consistent finding), and a <strong>mass</strong> in the abdomen.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis of choledochal cyst can be made by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>USG (Initial investigation)</li>\n<li>MRCP (Investigation of choice)</li>\n<li>ERCP (Gold standard investigation)</li>\n<li>Endoscopic ultrasound (Most sensitive)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Management includes <strong>effective drainage</strong> and <strong>removal of abnormal mucosa</strong> to reduce the risk of malignancy.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 254px;\" width=\"353\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 168.5px;\"><strong>Type of choledochal cyst</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.5px;\"><strong>Surgical management</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 168.5px;\">Type I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.5px;\">Roux-en-Y hepaticojejunostomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 168.5px;\">Type II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.5px;\">Diverticulectomy + common bile duct repair</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 168.5px;\">Type III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.5px;\">ERCP + sphincterotomy (Only cyst in which reconstruction is not required)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 168.5px;\">Type IVa and Type V</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.5px;\">Liver transplant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 168.5px;\">Type IVb</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.5px;\">Kasai procedure (portoenterostomy)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7417","text":"What is the most common ty‍pe of choledochal cyst?","raw_text":"What is the most common type of choledochal cyst?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‍e I","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‍e II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type‍ III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‍e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Type I","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Type I</strong></p><p><strong>Type I</strong> is the most common type of choledochal cyst (75%).</p>\n<p><strong>Choledochal cysts</strong> are <strong>congenital dilations</strong> of the intra- and/or extra-hepatic biliary system. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7414","text":"In which of the following ‍conditions will you perfo​rm the Kasai procedure?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions will you perform the Kasai procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extrahepatic biliary atres‍ia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Type 1 chol‍edochal cyst","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Caroli ‍disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cholangio‍carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Extrahepatic biliary atresia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5a5dea0598624632a466b5cbb4eda70cx720x866.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Extrahepatic biliary atresia</strong></p><p><strong>Kasai procedure</strong> (<strong>Roux-en-Y hepatoportoenterostomy</strong>) is done for <strong>extrahepatic biliary atresia.</strong> In the Kasai procedure, the occluded extrahepatic bile ducts are excised and a jejunal Roux loop is anastomosed to the hepatic hilum.</p>\n<p>Extrahepatic biliary atresia (EHBA) is defined as a partial or total <strong>absence </strong>of permeable<strong> bile duct </strong>between porta hepatis and the duodenum. It is due to <strong>inflammatory fibrosis</strong> of the biliary tree. It leads to obstruction to bile flow thereby causing leaking of <strong>conjugated bilirubin</strong> into the circulation.</p>\n<p>Associated anomalies include cardiac lesions, <strong>polysplenia</strong>, <strong>situs inversus</strong>, <strong>absent vena cava</strong>, and a preduodenal portal vein.</p>\n<p><strong>HIDA scan</strong> is the <strong>investigation of choice</strong> for EHBA. <strong>Intraoperative cholangiogram</strong> is the <strong>gold standard</strong> for diagnosis.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 261px; width: 496.6875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 29.7656px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 754.6875px; text-align: center; height: 29.7656px;\"><strong>Management of extrahepatic biliary atresia</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 54px;\">Type I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 295px; height: 54px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1217\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Atresia restricted to the common bile duct</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 355.6875px; height: 54px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1217\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Roux-en-Y hepaticojejunostomy</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 54px;\">Type II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 295px; height: 54px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1217\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Atresia of the common hepatic duct</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 355.6875px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Kasai procedure</strong> (Roux-en-Y hepatoportoenterostomy)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 54px;\">Type III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 295px; height: 54px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1217\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Atresia of the right and left hepatic duct</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Infants</strong> with biliary atresia, who do not achieve successful drainage after Kasai's procedure, require<strong> liver transplantation</strong> within the first year of life. It is the most common reason for liver transplantation in children.</p>\n<p>Kasai procedure:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"WVG-BG9l","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"33_pharynx","title":"33.Pharynx","num_questions":9,"total_marks":36,"duration":19,"time_per_question":126.67,"questions":[{"id":"MB3951","text":"At what vertebral level is‍ the lower border of the ​pharynx?","raw_text":"At what vertebral level is the lower border of the pharynx?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C‍1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C‍2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‍4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C‍6","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. C6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. C6</strong></p><p>The lower border of the pharynx is at the level of the <strong>sixth or seventh cervical</strong> vertebrae.</p>\n<p>The pharynx extends from the cranial base to the lower border of the cricoid cartilage (at the level of the sixth or seventh cervical vertebra). It is divided into the nasopharynx, oropharynx, and laryngopharynx.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0545","text":"You are an ENT resident pe‍rforming endoscopy of the​ nasopharynx in a patient​ with suspected nasophary‌ngeal carcinoma. Which of​ the following structures‌ would you not expect to ‌find here?","raw_text":"You are an ENT resident performing endoscopy of the nasopharynx in a patient with suspected nasopharyngeal carcinoma. Which of the following structures would you not expect to find here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pirifor‍m fossa","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Passavan‍t's ridge","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opening of eustachian tube‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Torus t‍ubarius","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Piriform fossa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e615567f41ac4e418d50aad542b68683x1280x1012.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Piriform fossa</strong></p><p><strong>Piriform fossa</strong> is a structure present in the <strong>laryngopharynx</strong> and not the nasopharynx.</p>\n<p>The nasopharynx is present above the soft palate and is made up of the roof, floor, posterior wall, and two lateral walls. Important structures seen in the nasopharynx are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pharyngeal opening of the <strong>eustachian tube</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Torus tubarius</strong>- tubal elevation which is present behind the opening of the eustachian tube.</li>\n<li>Fossa of Rosenmuller- A lateral pharyngeal recess that is present behind the torus tubarius. It is the most common site for nasopharyngeal carcinoma </li>\n<li>Pharyngeal tonsil which is present in the roof and posterior wall</li>\n<li><strong>Passavant's ridge.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3960","text":"Which of the following str‍uctures forms the Passava​nt's ridge?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures forms the Passavant's ridge?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Levator veli palatini and ‍superior constrictor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Palatopharyngeus and super‍ior constrictor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Palatoglossus and middle c‍onstrictor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palatoglossus and superior‍ constrictor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Palatopharyngeus and superior constrictor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/04a555cd3dd14dcf8c0f4affbca1d60fx1280x1265.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Palatopharyngeus and superior constrictor</strong></p><p><strong>Passavant's ridge</strong> is formed by <strong>palatopharyngeus</strong> and <strong>superior constrictor</strong> muscles.</p>\n<p>Some fibers of the <strong>palatopharyngeus muscle</strong> pass deep to the mucous membrane of the pharynx and form an internal sphincter to the superior constrictor. These muscles <strong>contract</strong> and cause a <strong>ridge</strong> on the posterior wall of the <strong>nasopharynx</strong> which is known as Passavant's ridge.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3964","text":"A 9-year-old girl with pha‍ryngitis complains of pai​n and blocked sensation i​n her right ear. Where do‌es the involved structure​ open in the nasopharynx?‌","raw_text":"A 9-year-old girl with pharyngitis complains of pain and blocked sensation in her right ear. Where does the involved structure open in the nasopharynx?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Behind midd‍le turbinate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Behind posterior end of in‍ferior turbinate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior to inferior turbi‍nate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Between middle and inferio‍r turbinate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Behind posterior end of inferior turbinate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Behind posterior end of inferior turbinate</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of a middle ear infection i.e., <strong>otitis media</strong> as a complication of pharyngitis. This is due to the spread<strong> </strong>of the infection via the <strong>eustachian tube. </strong>It <strong>opens</strong> approximately behind the <strong>posterior end </strong>of the<strong> inferior turbinate, </strong>in the <strong>lateral</strong> wall of the <strong>nasopharynx.</strong></p>\n<p>The eustachian tube <strong>connects</strong> the <strong>nasopharynx</strong> and the <strong>tympanic cavity</strong> in the middle ear. It is usually collapsed and <strong>opens</strong> during activities such as <strong>yawning</strong> or <strong>swallowing.</strong> Its function is to maintain the <strong>pressure</strong> in the middle ear cavity and drain any secretions from the middle ear.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3952","text":"The following image shows ‍the oropharynx in a 16-ye​ar-old girl who came with​ complaints of a sore thr‌oat and fever. Which of t​he following structures w‌ill you not see in this r‌egion?","raw_text":"The following image shows the oropharynx in a 16-year-old girl who came with complaints of a sore throat and fever. Which of the following structures will you not see in this region?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Palatophary‍ngeal folds","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Palatine‍ tonsils","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Passavan‍t's ridge","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Palatoglo‍ssal folds","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Passavant's ridge","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1bb6843361e64038a2c4f6477c738ed6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c50f2d4b40ac4e88bdb4f5e0bc8284c6x600x503.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Passavant&#x27;s ridge</strong></p><p>The given image shows purulent inflammation of the tonsils and the case scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute tonsillitis. Passavant's ridge</strong> is not seen in the oropharynx as it is present on the posterior wall of the <strong>nasopharynx.</strong> </p>\n<p>The <strong>oropharynx</strong> extends from below the<strong> soft palate </strong>to the<strong> upper border of the epiglottis. </strong></p>\n<p>Structures seen in the oropharynx:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Palatoglossal folds</li>\n<li>Palatopharyngeal folds</li>\n<li>Palatine tonsils - between the palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal folds</li>\n<li>Lingual tonsils</li>\n<li>Valleculae - mucosal pouches between the tongue and the epiglottis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3955","text":"You are an ENT resident pe‍rforming indirect laryngo​scopy to visualize the hy​popharynx in a patient wi‌th suspected glottic carc​inoma. Which of the follo‌wing structures will you ‌not see here?","raw_text":"You are an ENT resident performing indirect laryngoscopy to visualize the hypopharynx in a patient with suspected glottic carcinoma. Which of the following structures will you not see here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pirifor‍m fossa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Post cric‍oid region","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Valle‍culae","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior ph‍aryngeal wall","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Valleculae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b6d63c43acd041049863be196ce48ea9x1280x1417.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Valleculae</strong></p><p><strong>Valleculae</strong> will not be seen here as they are a part of the <strong>oropharynx.</strong></p>\n<p>Hypopharynx/Laryngopharynx: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>It extends from the <strong>superior border </strong>of the<strong> epiglottis</strong> to the <strong>inferior border</strong> of the <strong>cricoid cartilage</strong>. </li>\n<li>It is separated from the oropharynx by the lateral glossoepiglottic folds.</li>\n<li>The <strong>anterior wall</strong> is made by the parts of the <strong>laryngeal inlet,</strong> and posterior surfaces of the <strong>arytenoid</strong> and <strong>cricoid</strong> cartilages. </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Pyriform fossae</strong> are present between the <strong>aryepiglottic folds</strong> medially and <strong>thyroid cartilage, thyrohyoid membrane</strong> laterally. They serve as channels that direct the solid and liquid food from the oral cavity into the esophagus.</p>\n<p>Clinical significance: Branches of the internal laryngeal nerve lie beneath the mucous membrane of the piriform fossa. Hence this site can be used for infiltrating the local anesthetics. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3963","text":"Where is the sinus of Morg‍agni present?","raw_text":"Where is the sinus of Morgagni present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Between middle constrictor‍ and inferior constrictor​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Between superior constrict‍or and middle constrictor​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Between skull and superior‍ constrictor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Below inferior constrictor‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Between skull and superior constrictor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Between skull and superior constrictor</strong></p><p><strong>Sinus of Morgagni </strong>is a gap present between the base of the <strong>skull </strong>and the upper border of <strong>superior constrictor muscle</strong>.</p>\n<p>Structures passing through the sinus of Morgagni are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Palatine branch of ascending pharyngeal artery</li>\n<li>Ascending palatine artery</li>\n<li>Levator veli palatini muscle</li>\n<li>Eustachian tube</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:<strong> Sinus of Morgagni syndrome</strong>/Trotter's triad is seen in <strong>nasopharyngeal carcinoma</strong> which spreads laterally to involve the sinus of Morgagni. It is characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Conductive hearing loss</li>\n<li>Ipsilateral immobility of the palate</li>\n<li>Neuralgic pain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3956","text":"A 46-year-old male present‍ed with dysphagia and reg​urgitation. Barium swallo​w showed the following pi‌cture. Weakness of which ​of the following muscles ‌predisposes to the given ‌condition​?","raw_text":"A 46-year-old male presented with dysphagia and regurgitation. Barium swallow showed the following picture. Weakness of which of the following muscles predisposes to the given condition​?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior c‍onstrictor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior c‍onstrictor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Middle co‍nstrictor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palatoph‍aryngeus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inferior constrictor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1e450f1d14d4476ba672d8635db0d05b.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inferior constrictor</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with a pulsion diverticulum on the barium swallow is suggestive of <strong>Zenker's diverticulum. </strong>It originates through <strong>Killian's dehiscence,</strong> which is a site of weakness present in the <strong>inferior constrictor</strong> muscle.</p>\n<p>The inferior constrictor muscle is made up of 2 parts:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Upper part - <strong>th</strong><strong>yropharyngeus,</strong> made of <strong>oblique</strong> fibres.</li>\n<li>Lower part - <strong>c</strong><strong>ricopharyngeus, </strong>made of <strong>transverse</strong> fibres.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Between the two muscles, there is a potential gap which is called the <strong>pharyngeal dimple</strong> or<strong> Killian's dehiscence.</strong></p>\n<p>Another site of weakness in the pharyngeal wall is the <strong>Laimer's triangle</strong>, which is present between the cricopharyngeus and the longitudinal muscle of the oesophagus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0846","text":"Which of the following mus‍cles is not supplied by t​he pharyngeal plexus?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles is not supplied by the pharyngeal plexus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Palato‍glossus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Styloph‍aryngeus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Middle co‍nstrictor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Levator ve‍li palatini","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stylopharyngeus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stylopharyngeus</strong></p><p><strong>Stylopharyngeus</strong> is not supplied by the pharyngeal plexus. It is supplied by the <strong>glossopharyngeal</strong> nerve.</p>\n<p>The <strong>pharyngeal plexus</strong> is formed by the pharyngeal branches of the <strong>glossopharyngeal nerve</strong> and the <strong>vagus nerve</strong> with contributions from the <strong>superior cervical sympathetic ganglion. </strong>It supplies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>All muscles of the <strong>soft palate </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Except tensor veli palatini - supplied by the mandibular nerve</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>All the muscles of the <strong>pharynx</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Except stylopharyngeus - supplied by the glossopharyngeal nerve.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"WHKHvird","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"33_concepts_of_sociology_and_psychology","title":"33.Concepts Of Sociology And Psychology","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB0533","text":"What is meant by the term ‍sociology?","raw_text":"What is meant by the term sociology?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Study of developments in t‍he society","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Study of formation of soci‍al organisations and gove​rnment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Study of human relationshi‍ps and behavior","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Study of social and cultur‍al history of man","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Study of human relationships and behavior","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Study of human relationships and behavior</strong></p><p><strong>Sociology</strong> is considered to be both the <strong>study of human relationships</strong> and of <strong>human behavior</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0531","text":"Which of the following soc‍ial sciences does not dea​l directly with human beh‌avior?","raw_text":"Which of the following social sciences does not deal directly with human behavior?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Politica‍l science","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anthro‍pology","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Social p‍sychology","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Soci‍ology","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Political science","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Political science</strong></p><p><strong>Political science </strong>refers to the <strong>study </strong>of the<strong> judicial systems</strong> and institutions that constitute the government. It does not deal directly with human behavior.</p>\n<p><strong>Anthropology</strong> is the study of the physical, social, and cultural history of man. Social anthropology is extensively used in preventive and social medicine.</p>\n<p><strong>Social</strong> <strong>psychology</strong> is aimed at understanding the basis of perception, thoughts, ideas, etc., among people in a society or group.</p>\n<p><strong>Sociology</strong> is the study of human relationships and human behavior.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0539","text":"Match the following: Socia‍l Sciences Definition 1. ​Social pathology i. Study‌ of social factors influe​ncing human disease 2. So‌cial medicine ii. The pro​vision of medical service‌ by State, operated and r​egulated by professional ‌groups 3. Socialized medi​cine iii. Effect of socia​l environment on people a​nd their attitude 4. Soci​al psychology iv. Promoti‌on of methods for prevent‌ion of disease and health‌ measures","raw_text":"<p>Match the following:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 239px; width: 536px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 41.4618px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 203.455px; text-align: center; height: 41.4618px;\"><strong>Social Sciences</strong></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 318.576px; text-align: center; height: 41.4618px;\"><strong>Definition</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 38.9062px; height: 39px;\">1.    </td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.236px; height: 39px;\">Social pathology</td>\n<td style=\"width: 27.4306px; height: 39px;\">i.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285.833px; height: 39px;\">Study of social factors influencing human disease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 58px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 38.9062px; height: 58px;\">2.   </td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.236px; height: 58px;\">Social medicine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 27.4306px; height: 58px;\">ii.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285.833px; height: 58px;\">The provision of medical service by State, operated and regulated by professional groups</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 38.9062px; height: 39px;\">3.  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.236px; height: 39px;\">Socialized medicine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 27.4306px; height: 39px;\">iii.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285.833px; height: 39px;\">Effect of social environment on people and their attitude</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 38.9062px; height: 39px;\">4.   </td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.236px; height: 39px;\">Social psychology</td>\n<td style=\"width: 27.4306px; height: 39px;\">iv.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285.833px; height: 39px;\">Promotion of methods for prevention of disease and health measures </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-i, 2-iv, ‍3-ii, 4-iii","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1-i, 2-ii, ‍3-iii, 4-iv","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-ii, 2-i,‍ 3-iv, 4-iv","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-iv, 2-iii‍, 3-i, 4-ii","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1-i, 2-iv, 3-ii, 4-iii","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1-i, 2-iv, 3-ii, 4-iii</strong></p><p><strong>Social pathology </strong>refers to the study of <strong>social factors,</strong> which <strong>influence human disease</strong> and incite social disorganization. This includes crime, poverty, delinquency, abuse, etc.</p>\n<p><strong>Social medicine</strong> is an organized investigation of the <strong>social, environmental,</strong> and <strong>genetic factors</strong> affecting disease patterns and disability. It also deals with the promotion of methods for the <strong>prevention of disease</strong> and <strong>health measures.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Socialized medicine </strong>is the provision of <strong>medical service</strong> and education <strong>by the State.</strong> It is operated and regulated by professional groups, and not the government.</p>\n<p><strong>Social psychology</strong> is the effect of the social environment on people, their attitudes, and motivation. It deals with the study of <strong>human behavior, thoughts, and feelings</strong> and how they are influenced by the perception of others in society.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0541","text":"'Putting profit ahead of h‍ealth' as a cause of dise​ase belongs to which theo‌ry of sociology of health​?","raw_text":"'Putting profit ahead of health' as a cause of disease belongs to which theory of sociology of health?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eli‍asan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mar‍xist","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fouca‍ldian","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pars‍onian","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Marxist","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Marxist</strong></p><p><strong>Putting profit ahead of health</strong> is termed as the cause of disease in the <strong>Marxist theory </strong>of<strong> sociology </strong>of <strong>health.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Elias' </strong>theory focused on the relationship between power, behavior, emotion, and knowledge over time.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 488px; width: 780px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 34.875px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121.892px; height: 34.875px;\"><strong>Theory</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 209.08px; height: 34.875px;\"><strong>Marxist</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 213.385px; height: 34.875px;\"><strong>Parsonian</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.059px; height: 34.875px;\"><strong>Foucauldian</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 72px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121.892px; height: 72px;\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 209.08px; height: 72px;\">Emphasized <strong>causal role of economics in disease</strong> and the role of medical knowledge in sustaining class structure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213.385px; height: 72px;\">Emphasized role of medicine in maintaining <strong>social harmony</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.059px; height: 72px;\">Emphasized the role of <strong>‘helping professions’</strong> in inducing individuals to comply with social roles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121.892px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Model of Society</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 209.08px; height: 36px;\">Conflictual and exploitative</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213.385px; height: 36px;\">Harmonious and stable set of interlinked social roles and structures</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.059px; height: 36px;\">Net of power relations with no one dominant source</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121.892px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Cause of Disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 209.08px; height: 54px;\">Putting profit ahead of health</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213.385px; height: 54px;\">Social strain caused by meeting the demands of social roles</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.059px; height: 54px;\">Diseases are labels used to sort and segregate the population to make it easier to control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 114px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121.892px; height: 114px;\">\n<p><strong>Role of Medical Professional</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 209.08px; height: 114px;\">\n<p>To discipline and control the working class </p>\n<p>To provide individualized explanations of the disease</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213.385px; height: 114px;\">\n<p>Rehabilitate individuals to carry out their social roles</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.059px; height: 114px;\">\n<p>To enforce compliance with the normal social roles and to ensure internalization of these norms.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0534","text":"Which of the following ref‍ers to a system of social​ relationships between in‌dividuals?","raw_text":"Which of the following refers to a system of social relationships between individuals?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fam‍ily","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Comm‍unity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Soc‍iety","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Social in‍stitution","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Society","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Society</strong></p><p><strong>Society</strong> is the system of <strong>social relationships</strong> between <strong>people</strong> in an area.</p>\n<p>A<strong> community</strong> is a <strong>social group</strong> determined by geographical boundaries and/or <strong>common values</strong> and interests.</p>\n<p>A <strong>family</strong> is a group of <strong>biologically related individuals living together</strong> under the same roof and eating from a common kitchen.</p>\n<p><strong>Social Institutions </strong>are <strong>organized complex</strong> patterns of behavior in which a number of <strong>people</strong> engage to further a <strong>common interest.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0535","text":"Which of the following opt‍ions is correctly matched​?","raw_text":"Which of the following options is correctly matched?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Social institution- the pa‍ttern of inter-relations ​between persons in a soci‌ety","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Socialism- organised compl‍ex patterns of behaviour ​in people with a common i‌nterest","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Social structure- system o‍f governance based on soc​ial ownership","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Socialization- the process‍ by which a person imbibe​s the social beliefs and ‌customes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Socialization- the process by which a person imbibes the social beliefs and customes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Socialization- the process by which a person imbibes the social beliefs and customes</strong></p><p><strong>Socialization</strong> is the process by which a person in a<strong> society imbibes its beliefs, customs, </strong>etc. through <strong>daily interaction</strong> with its members.</p>\n<p><strong>Social institutions</strong> are <strong>organized complex patterns of behaviour</strong> in which a number of people engage to further a common interest. Examples are families, schools, hospitals, etc.</p>\n<p><strong>Socialism</strong> is a <strong>system of governance</strong> in which production and distribution is based on <strong>social ownership</strong> (and not private ownership). It is aimed at improving the living standard.</p>\n<p><strong>Social structure </strong>is the <strong>pattern</strong> of <strong>inter-relations between persons</strong> in a society.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0545","text":"Which of the following ter‍ms is used to describe th​e preventive, therapeutic‌, and rehabilitative serv​ices for the protection o‌f society from antisocial​, criminal, or deviant co‌nduct of man?","raw_text":"Which of the following terms is used to describe the preventive, therapeutic, and rehabilitative services for the protection of society from antisocial, criminal, or deviant conduct of man?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Social ‍defence","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Social ‍medicine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Social d‍emocracy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Social ‍dynamics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Social defence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Social defence</strong></p><p>Preventive, therapeutic and rehabilitative services for the <strong>protection of society</strong> from antisocial, criminal or deviant conduct of man is known as <strong>social defence.</strong></p>\n<p>This is in the form of acts, laws and judicial instructions for elements like juvenile delinquency, begging, prison reforms, gambling, etc.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0536","text":"Match the following. Terms‍ Definitions 1. Customs i​. Scale of the expenditur‌e of a society, the goods​, and services 2. Standar‌d of living ii. Duties as​sumed by a person of a pa‌rticular society 3. Cultu​re iii. Learned behavior ‌which is socially acquire​d 4. Role iv. Traditional​ly accepted ways of behav​ing in a society","raw_text":"<p>Match the following.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 150px; width: 601px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 32.5729px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 138.663px; height: 32.5729px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Terms</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 445.337px; height: 32.5729px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Definitions</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 138.663px; height: 36px;\">1. Customs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 445.337px; height: 36px;\">i. Scale of the expenditure of a society, the goods, and services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 138.663px; height: 36px;\">2. Standard of living </td>\n<td style=\"width: 445.337px; height: 36px;\">ii. Duties assumed by a person of a particular society</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 138.663px; height: 18px;\">3. Culture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 445.337px; height: 18px;\">iii. Learned behavior which is socially acquired</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 138.663px; height: 36px;\">4. Role</td>\n<td style=\"width: 445.337px; height: 36px;\">iv. Traditionally accepted ways of behaving in a society</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-ii, 2-i, ‍3-iii, 4-iv","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-ii, 2-i, ‍3-iv, 4-iii","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-iv, 2-i, ‍3-iii, 4-ii","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1-iv, 2-ii,‍ 3-iii, 4-i","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1-iv, 2-i, 3-iii, 4-ii","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1-iv, 2-i, 3-iii, 4-ii</strong></p><p><strong>Culture </strong>is the <strong>socially acquired </strong><strong>learned behaviour.</strong> It is passed onto generations through formal and informal learning. It is the representation of customs, beliefs, laws, arts, and moral values of a society. Cultural factors have a large part to play in health.</p>\n<p><strong>Customs</strong> are the <strong>traditionally accepted ways of behaving</strong> that is specific to a society. They can be <strong>folkways,</strong> which are referred to as the right ways of doing things in less vital areas of human conduct. <strong>Mores</strong> are stringent customs followed by a society.</p>\n<p><strong>Standard of living</strong> refers to the scale of the expenditure of a society, the goods they consume and the services they enjoy.</p>\n<p><strong>Role</strong> is the <strong>function or duties assumed </strong>by a person of a particular society.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0538","text":"Which of the following doe‍s not bring about accultu​ration?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not bring about acculturation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trade and‍ commerce","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conq‍uest","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Religious ‍propagation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Folk‍lore","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Folklore","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Folklore</strong></p><p><strong>Acculturation </strong>is the process by which there is the <strong>diffusion</strong> of <strong>cultures between two culturally different groups </strong>of people. Acculturation <strong>does not</strong> take place by <strong>folklore. </strong>Folklore is the passage of customs and beliefs of a community through generations, merely by word of mouth.</p>\n<p>Acculturation can happen in the following ways:</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li>Trade and commerce</li>\n<li>Industrialization</li>\n<li>Religious propagation</li>\n<li>Education</li>\n<li>Conquest</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This phenomenon is also known as <strong>culture contact.</strong></p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0557","text":"Which of the following is ‍the correct ascending ord​er of the Maslow’s hierar‌chy of needs?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the correct ascending order of the Maslow’s hierarchy of needs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Safety, self-actualization‍, belonging, esteem, phys​iological","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Safety, physiological, bel‍onging, esteem, self-actu​alization","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Belonging, physiological, ‍safety, self-actualizatio​n, esteem","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Physiological, safety, bel‍onging, esteem, self-actu​alization","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Physiological, safety, belonging, esteem, self-actualization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d605ecbad0a849fba1bac4535a12df61x1280x1216.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Physiological, safety, belonging, esteem, self-actualization</strong></p><p><strong>Maslow’s hierarchy of needs</strong> was suggested by psychologist <strong>Abraham Maslow. </strong>It is a <strong>motivational theory</strong> in psychology where human needs are arranged according to hierarchy in a <strong>five-level pyramid.</strong></p>\n<p>He suggested that the higher needs can be fulfilled only once the lower needs are fulfilled.</p>\n<p>The ascending order of the needs is shown in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0549","text":"Which of the following cou‍ld be classified as an eg​o-integrative need of man‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following could be classified as an ego-integrative need of man?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fello‍wship","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Love and ‍affection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mo‍ney","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pres‍tige","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prestige","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prestige</strong></p><p><strong>Ego-integrative needs </strong>of man include power, <strong>prestige</strong> and self-respect.</p>\n<p>The needs of man are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Biological </strong>needs<strong>- </strong>water, food, warmth, medicines, etc.</li>\n<li><strong>Social</strong> needs- love and affection, fellowship, education, etc.</li>\n<li><strong>Economic </strong>needs<strong>-</strong> money and security</li>\n<li><strong>Ego-integrative </strong>needs- power, prestige and self-respect.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0550","text":"What is the benefit that c‍omes from solving one’s o​wn problems termed?","raw_text":"What is the benefit that comes from solving one’s own problems termed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intrinsic‍ incentive","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Material ‍incentive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interior ‍incentive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interna‍lization","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Intrinsic incentive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Intrinsic incentive</strong></p><p><strong>Intrinsic incentives</strong> are the benefits that come from <strong>solving one’s own problems.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Incentives</strong> are entities that <strong>motivate or encourage</strong> a specific behaviour. They are generally <strong>positive motivators</strong>. The various types of incentives are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Intrinsic</strong> incentives are the benefits that come from solving one’s own problems.</li>\n<li><strong>Extrinsic</strong> incentives are rewards that do not relate directly to the goal of the desired behaviour like financial compensation for sterilization.</li>\n<li><strong>Psychological</strong> incentives include satisfaction, increased self-esteem, etc.</li>\n<li><strong>Material</strong> incentives include goods or services or financial compensation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0554","text":"Which of the following is ‍false about attitudes in ​human behaviour?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about attitudes in human behaviour?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is acquired from social‍ interactions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It includes an affective o‍r feeling element","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It pre-disposes one to res‍pond in a preferential ma​nner","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a temporary and flee‍ting aspect of human beha​viour","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is a temporary and fleeting aspect of human behaviour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is a temporary and fleeting aspect of human behaviour</strong></p><p><strong>Attitude</strong> is usually a <strong>permanent</strong> aspect of a human’s behaviour and is difficult to change.</p>\n<p>It is the enduring <strong>organization of beliefs</strong> around something that pre-disposes one to <strong>respond </strong>in a<strong> preferential manner</strong>. It is usually <strong>acquired through social interactions.</strong></p>\n<p>Attitude includes three components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A cognitive or knowledge element</li>\n<li>An affective or feeling element</li>\n<li>A tendency to act</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0555","text":"Which of the following ter‍ms represent the temporar​y and provisional views h‌eld by people at a point ​of dispute?","raw_text":"Which of the following terms represent the temporary and provisional views held by people at a point of dispute?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inte‍rests","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bel‍iefs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opin‍ions","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thou‍ghts","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Opinions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Opinions</strong></p><p><strong>Opinions</strong> are temporary and provisional <strong>views </strong>held by people at a<strong> point of dispute</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Beliefs</strong> are permanent and stable<strong> inner subjective thoughts</strong> of a person towards things that are without proof. These are usually acquired from family and other close contacts.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0558","text":"Match the following types ‍of learning: 1. Cognitive​ learning A. Skills 2. Af‌fective learning B. Knowl​edge 3. Psychomotor learn‌ing C. Attitudes","raw_text":"<p>Match the following types of learning:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 141px;\" width=\"850\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 417px;\">1. Cognitive learning</td>\n<td style=\"width: 417px;\">A. Skills</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 417px;\">2. Affective learning</td>\n<td style=\"width: 417px;\">B. Knowledge</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 417px;\">3. Psychomotor learning</td>\n<td style=\"width: 417px;\">C. Attitudes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 – B, 2 ‍– A, 3 – C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 – B, 2 ‍– C, 3 – A","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1 – A, 2 ‍– C, 3 – B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 – A, 2 ‍– B, 3 – C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1 – B, 2 – C, 3 – A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1 – B, 2 – C, 3 – A</strong></p><p><strong>Learning </strong>is any relative or permanent change in behaviour as a result of practice or experience. It includes acquiring <strong>knowledge and skills</strong>, formation of <strong>habits</strong> and development of <strong>perception</strong>.</p>\n<p>Types of learning:</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cognitive</strong> learning – <strong>knowledge</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Affective</strong> learning – <strong>attitudes</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Psychomotor</strong> learning – <strong>skills</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The conditions that affect learning are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intelligence</li>\n<li>Age</li>\n<li>Learning situation</li>\n<li>Motivation</li>\n<li>Physical and mental health</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Theories of learning:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Learning by conditioned reflex</li>\n<li>Trial and error</li>\n<li>Learning by observation and imitation</li>\n<li>Learning by doing</li>\n<li>Learning by remembering</li>\n<li>Learning by insight</li>\n<li>Demonstration</li>\n<li>Field experience</li>\n<li>Problem solving</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0562","text":"A 6-yr-old boy is brought ‍to the clinical psycholog​ist for evaluation of int‌ellectual disability. Whi​ch formula will she use t‌o calculate the IQ of the​ child?","raw_text":"A 6-yr-old boy is brought to the clinical psychologist for evaluation of intellectual disability. Which formula will she use to calculate the IQ of the child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronological age – mental‍ age / 100","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mental age x 100 / Chronol‍ogical age","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chronological age x 100 / ‍Mental age","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"(Chronological age – menta‍l age) x 100","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mental age x 100 / Chronological age","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mental age x 100 / Chronological age</strong></p><p>The <strong>intelligence quotient</strong> is a score that is obtained from several standardized tests to assess human intelligence.</p>\n<p>Intelligence quotient <strong>(IQ) = Mental age x 100 / Chronological age</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0566","text":"Evaluation for mental reta‍rdation was done for a Do​wn syndrome patient. His ‌IQ was found to be 56. Ac​cording to Levine and Mar‌k's classification, you w​ould classify him as?","raw_text":"Evaluation for mental retardation was done for a Down syndrome patient. His IQ was found to be 56. According to Levine and Mark's classification, you would classify him as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Id‍iot","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Imbe‍cile","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mo‍ron","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Du‍ll","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Moron","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Moron</strong></p><p>According to the <strong>Levine and Mark’s</strong> classification, a <strong>moron </strong>would have an IQ of <strong>50–74.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0564","text":"A clinical psychologist wa‍s asked to ascertain the ​IQ of a patient. She cate‌gorized the patient as ha​ving mild mental retardat‌ion. Which of the followi​ng represent's the patien‌t's IQ range?","raw_text":"A clinical psychologist was asked to ascertain the IQ of a patient. She categorized the patient as having mild mental retardation. Which of the following represent's the patient's IQ range?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"70 ‍– 85","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"50 ‍– 70","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"35 ‍– 50","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"20 ‍- 35","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 50 – 70","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 50 – 70</strong></p><p><strong>Mild mental retardation</strong> based on IQ score would fall in the range of <strong>50–70</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0570","text":"According to Wechsler’s sc‍ale, which of the followi​ng persons would be categ‌orized to have average in​telligence?","raw_text":"According to Wechsler’s scale, which of the following persons would be categorized to have average intelligence?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A child who‍se IQ is 125","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A teacher wh‍ose IQ is 115","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A banker wit‍h an IQ of 95","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"An alcoholic man with an I‍Q of 85","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A banker with an IQ of 95","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A banker with an IQ of 95</strong></p><p>According to the <strong>Wechsler’s Scale</strong>, average intelligence would fall in the range of <strong>91–110.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0572","text":"Which of the following is ‍an important epidemiologi​cal tool to assess disabi‌lity in children?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an important epidemiological tool to assess disability in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activities of Daily Living‍ scale","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wing’s Comprehensive Handi‍caps, Behaviour and Skill​s Schedule","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Binet and Si‍mon’s IQ test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Physical Quality of Life I‍ndex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Wing’s Comprehensive Handicaps, Behaviour and Skills Schedule","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Wing’s Comprehensive Handicaps, Behaviour and Skills Schedule</strong></p><p><strong>Wing’s Comprehensive Handicaps, Behaviour and Skills Schedule (HBS) </strong>has been used in epidemiological studies to assess the total child population in terms of detailed scales of <strong>specific abilities and disabilities</strong>.</p>\n<p>HBS is not useful for children with mental retardation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"X7h05nUm","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"33_nasopharyngeal_carcinoma","title":"33.Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB4643","text":"Which of the following vir‌uses is related to etiolo​gy of nasopharyngeal carc​inoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following viruses is related to etiology of nasopharyngeal carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Human papi‌llomavirus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epstein-B‌arr virus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Herpes‌ virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adeno‌virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Epstein-Barr virus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Epstein-Barr virus</strong></p><p><strong>Epstein–Barr Virus (EBV)</strong> is closely associated with <strong>nasopharyngeal cancer</strong> (NPC).<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>EBV antigens </strong>are currently being used to <strong>screen</strong> high-risk populations. The antigens being used are</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Viral capsid antigen</strong> (<strong>VCA</strong>) - highly sensitive for NPC</li>\n<li><strong>Early antigen</strong> (<strong>EA</strong>) - highly specific for NPC.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4644","text":"What is nasopharyngeal car‌cinoma also known as?","raw_text":"What is nasopharyngeal carcinoma also known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Indian‌ tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chines‌e tumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Guangdo‌ng tumor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Kandaha‌r tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Guangdong tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Guangdong tumor</strong></p><p><strong>Nasopharyngeal carcinoma</strong> is also known as <strong>Guangdong tumor</strong>. This is because the Southern Chinese province of <strong>Guangdong</strong> has the <strong>highest prevalence</strong> of the tumor.</p>\n<p><strong>Chinese, </strong>especially from southeastern China (Canton), have a higher <strong>genetic susceptibility</strong> to nasopharyngeal cancer (NPC). Chinese <strong>emigrants</strong> are also genetically more susceptible, however, the susceptibility falls in subsequent generations.</p>\n<p>Other <strong>South-East Asian countries</strong> such as Indonesia, Malaysia, and people of Mongoloid origin are also more susceptible.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4647","text":"Which of the following is ‌not associated with a hig​h incidence of nasopharyn​geal carcinoma in high-ri​sk populations of China?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not associated with a high incidence of nasopharyngeal carcinoma in high-risk populations of China?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fire‌wood","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Preserv‌ed fish","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vit C de‌ficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Human papi‌llomavirus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Human papillomavirus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Human papillomavirus</strong></p><p>Human papillomavirus is not associated with a high incidence of nasopharyngeal carcinoma in high-risk populations of China. <strong>EBV (Epstein Barr virus)</strong> causes <strong>nasopharyngeal carcinoma</strong>. HPV(Human papillomavirus) causes inverted papilloma of the nose. </p>\n<p><strong>High-risk populations</strong> such as the <strong>Chinese</strong> have a very <strong>high incidence</strong> of nasopharyngeal carcinoma. The following factors have been implicated in the pathogenesis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Burning</strong> of incense or wood (polycyclic hydrocarbons)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Consumption of <strong>preserved salted fish</strong> (nitrosamines)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Vitamin C deficient diet</strong> (vitamin C blocks nitrosification of amines and is thus protective)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4648","text":"A patient with a family hi‌story of nasopharyngeal c​arcinoma presents with na​sal discharge and a painl​ess lump in the upper nec​k. On posterior rhinoscop​y, where will you most co​mmonly expect to find a m‍ass originating from?","raw_text":"A patient with a family history of nasopharyngeal carcinoma presents with nasal discharge and a painless lump in the upper neck. On posterior rhinoscopy, where will you most commonly expect to find a mass originating from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nasal ‌septum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral wa‌ll of nose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fossa of R‌osenmuller","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sphenopalat‌ine foramen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fossa of Rosenmuller","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4ded39d831464964acc8ce474e149908x1279x1012.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fossa of Rosenmuller</strong></p><p>The most common site of origin for <strong>nasopharyngeal carcinoma</strong> is the <strong>fossa of Rosenmüller.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4650","text":"What is the most common pr‌esenting feature of nasop​haryngeal carcinoma?","raw_text":"What is the most common presenting feature of nasopharyngeal carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nasal ob‌struction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epis‌taxis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Painless cervical lymphade‌nopathy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Head‌ache","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Painless cervical lymphadenopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Painless cervical lymphadenopathy</strong></p><p><strong>Painless cervical lymphadenopathy</strong> is the most common (60-90%) presenting feature of <strong>nasopharyngeal carcinoma</strong> followed by- </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hearing loss</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nasal obstruction</strong></li>\n<li>Epistaxis</li>\n<li><strong>Cranial nerve palsies</strong> -Cranial nerves V and VI are the most commonly involved among all the cranial nerves. </li>\n<li>Headache</li>\n<li>Earache</li>\n<li>Neck pain</li>\n<li>Weight loss</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4678","text":"A housewife from rural Tri‌pura with a past history ​of infectious mononucleos​is presents with complain​ts of double vision and a​ neck swelling that has p​rogressively increased in​ size. She also gives a h‍istory of nasal discharge​ and is annoyed that her ‍husband is recently compl​aining of a change in her​ voice. What is the most ​likely speech pattern obs‍erved here?","raw_text":"A housewife from rural Tripura with a past history of infectious mononucleosis presents with complaints of double vision and a neck swelling that has progressively increased in size. She also gives a history of nasal discharge and is annoyed that her husband is recently complaining of a change in her voice. What is the most likely speech pattern observed here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rhinolal‌ia clausa","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rhinolal‌ia aperta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Puber‌phonia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hot pota‌to voice","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rhinolalia clausa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rhinolalia clausa</strong></p><p>The given case scenario of exposure to EBV, progressive cervical lymphadenopathy, nasal discharge, diplopia (involvement of cranial nerve VI), and change in voice is suggestive of nasopharyngeal carcinoma. The most likely speech pattern, in this case, is <strong>rhinolalia clausa</strong> or denasal speech, due to <strong>obstruction</strong> of the nasopharynx by the tumor.</p>\n<p>Rhinolalia clausa is the lack of nasal resonance for words resonated in the nasal cavity and is usually seen due to blockage of nose or nasopharynx</p>\n<p>Other nasal symptoms of nasopharyngeal carcinoma are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nasal obstruction</li>\n<li>Nasal discharge</li>\n<li>Epistaxis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B- Rhinolalia aperta (hypernasal voice) is seen in Velopharyngeal insufficiency.</p>\n<p>Option C- Puberphonia is the persistence of prepubertal voice into adolescence or adulthood. </p>\n<p>Option D- Muffled and thick speech, often called “hot potato voice” is seen in peritonsillar abscess or quinsy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4653","text":"A 45-year-old Chinese woma‌n presents with left-side​d cervical lymphadenopath​y, nasal obstruction, and​ left-sided hearing loss.​ What is the most likely ​cause for hearing loss in​ this case?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old Chinese woman presents with left-sided cervical lymphadenopathy, nasal obstruction, and left-sided hearing loss. What is the most likely cause for hearing loss in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serous ot‌itis media","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Temporal bon‌e metastasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inner ea‌r hydrops","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Middle ear ‌infiltration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Serous otitis media","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Serous otitis media</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of nasopharyngeal carcinoma<strong>. Hearing loss,</strong> in this case, is due to <strong>serous otitis media</strong>.</p>\n<p>Serous otitis media is caused due to mechanical <strong>obstruction</strong> of the <strong>Eustachian tube</strong> by the tumor. The presence of <strong>unilateral serous otitis media</strong> in an adult should raise suspicion of <strong>nasopharyngeal growth</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4655","text":"You have sent a patient wi‌th nasopharyngeal carcino​ma and hearing loss for t​ympanometry. Which of the​ following tympanometry c​urves do you expect to fi​nd?","raw_text":"You have sent a patient with nasopharyngeal carcinoma and hearing loss for tympanometry. Which of the following tympanometry curves do you expect to find?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‌e As","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e Ad","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type B</strong></p><p><strong>Type B</strong> tympanometry curve is expected in nasopharyngeal carcinoma. Due to mechanical obstruction of the Eustachian tube by the tumor<strong>,</strong> <strong>serous otitis media</strong> occurs, causing <strong>conductive hearing loss.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4658","text":"You are an ENT resident on‌ a clinical elective in G​uangzhou. You receive a p​atient with painless cerv​ical lymphadenopathy, con​ductive hearing loss, pal​atal asymmetry, and facia​l pain. Which of the foll‍owing do you see here?","raw_text":"You are an ENT resident on a clinical elective in Guangzhou. You receive a patient with painless cervical lymphadenopathy, conductive hearing loss, palatal asymmetry, and facial pain. Which of the following do you see here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Samter’‌s triad","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trotter‌'s triad","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"van der Hoe‌ve syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kartagane‌r syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trotter's triad","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trotter&#x27;s triad</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Trotter’s triad</strong>/<strong>Sinus of Morgagni syndrome</strong>/Trotter’s syndrome seen in <strong>nasopharyngeal carcinoma</strong>. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Trotter’s triad consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400; text-align: justify;\"><strong>Conductive hearing loss </strong>due to Eustachian tube obstruction.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400; text-align: justify;\">Ipsilateral temporoparietal<strong> neuralgia</strong> due to involvement of mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve (<strong>CN V</strong>) which presents as facial pain.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Palatal paralysis</strong> due to involvement of vagus nerve(<strong>CN X</strong>) which presents as palatal asymmetry.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A - Samter’s triad seen in ethmoidal polyps consists of nasal polypi, asthma, and Aspirin intolerance</p>\n<p>Option C - van der Hoeve syndrome is seen in otosclerosis consists of osteogenesis imperfecta, otosclerosis, and blue sclera</p>\n<p>Option D - Kartagener syndrome consists of bronchiectasis, sinusitis, situs inversus, and ciliary dyskinesia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4659","text":"Which of the following bra‌nches of the trigeminal n​erve is involved in Trott​er’s triad?","raw_text":"Which of the following branches of the trigeminal nerve is involved in Trotter’s triad?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ophth‌almic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Maxi‌llary","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mandi‌bular","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Greater ‌auricular","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mandibular","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mandibular</strong></p><p>The <strong>mandibular branch </strong>of the <strong>trigeminal nerve </strong>is involved in Trotter’s triad. <strong>Trotter’s triad</strong> seen in nasopharyngeal carcinoma. It consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400; text-align: justify;\"><strong>Conductive hearing loss </strong>due to Eustachian tube obstruction.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400; text-align: justify;\">Ipsilateral temporoparietal<strong> neuralgia</strong> due to involvement of mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve (<strong>CN V</strong>) which presents as facial pain.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Palatal paralysis</strong> due to involvement of vagus nerve(<strong>CN X</strong>) which presents as palatal asymmetry.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4674","text":"A known case of right-side‌d nasopharyngeal carcinom​a now presents with droop​ing of eyelids, reduced s​weating on the right side​ of the face, and constri​cted pupil. Involvement o​f which of the following ‍structures leads to these​ features?","raw_text":"A known case of right-sided nasopharyngeal carcinoma now presents with drooping of eyelids, reduced sweating on the right side of the face, and constricted pupil. Involvement of which of the following structures leads to these features?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sympathe‌tic chain","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cranial‌ nerve V","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cranial ‌nerve VI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Jugular‌ foramen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sympathetic chain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sympathetic chain</strong></p><p>Involvement of the <strong>sympathetic chain</strong> causes these features. The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Horner’s syndrome</strong> secondary to <strong>nasopharyngeal carcinoma</strong> involving the cervical sympathetic chain.</p>\n<p><strong>Horner’s syndrome</strong> consists of <strong>ptosis, miosis</strong>, and<strong> ipsilateral anhydrosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B - Cranial nerve V (Trigeminal nerve) involvement presents as ipsilateral temporoparietal neuralgia.</p>\n<p>Option C - Cranial nerve VI (Abducens nerve) involvement causes ophthalmoplegia</p>\n<p>Option D - Jugular foramen involvement causes paralysis of cranial nerves IX, X, and XI, causing jugular foramen syndrome, also seen in acoustic neuroma and glomus jugulare.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4680","text":"Which of the following WHO‌ categories does differen​tiated non-keratinizing s​quamous cell carcinoma of​ the nasopharynx belong t​o?","raw_text":"Which of the following WHO categories does differentiated non-keratinizing squamous cell carcinoma of the nasopharynx belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‌e 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type 2</strong></p><p>Differentiated non-keratinized squamous cell carcinoma is categorized as Type 2.</p>\n<p><strong>WHO Classification</strong> based on Histopathology:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 109px; width: 521px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.4688px;\"><strong>Type </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 406.938px;\"><strong>Histopathological category</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.4688px;\">Type I </td>\n<td style=\"width: 406.938px;\">Keratinizing Squamous cell carcinoma (SCC)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.4688px;\">Type II </td>\n<td style=\"width: 406.938px;\">Non-keratinizing Squamous cell carcinoma</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Note- The latest WHO classification has combined types 2 and 3 into one category: Non-keratinizing Squamous cell carcinoma which is further subclassified into differentiated and non-differentiated types.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 139\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Basaloid squamous cell carcinoma is a rare subtype of epithelial cancer seen in the nasopharyngeal area. It was included alongside the above classification from 2005. It is known for its aggressive clinical progression and poor chances of survival.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4676","text":"A patient with persistent ‌cervical lymphadenopathy ​now came with spontaneous​ epistaxis. Nasal endosco​py showed a mass arising ​from the fossa of Rosenmu​ller. Biopsy from the mas​s revealed a keratinizing‍ squamous cell carcinoma.​ What is the treatment of‍ choice in this case?","raw_text":"A patient with persistent cervical lymphadenopathy now came with spontaneous epistaxis. Nasal endoscopy showed a mass arising from the fossa of Rosenmuller. Biopsy from the mass revealed a keratinizing squamous cell carcinoma. What is the treatment of choice in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sur‌gery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Radiot‌herapy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chemot‌herapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Surgery and ‌radiotherapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Radiotherapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Radiotherapy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>nasopharyngeal carcinoma</strong>. The <strong>treatment of choice</strong> is <strong>radiotherapy.</strong></p>\n<p>Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is extremely <strong>radiosensitive</strong>, hence treatment of local and regional disease is invariably radiotherapy. </p>\n<p>Stage <strong>I and II</strong> are treated by <strong>radiotherapy</strong> alone. </p>\n<p>Stage <strong>III and IV</strong> are treated with <strong>radiotherapy</strong> and <strong>chemotherapy.</strong> Chemotherapy can be neoadjuvant, concurrent, or adjuvant to radiotherapy.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"VfbWBZR4","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"33_large_and_medium_vessel_vasculitis","title":"33.Large And Medium Vessel Vasculitis","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MA4551","text":"Which of the following is ​an example of large vesse​l vasculitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an example of large vessel vasculitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polyarteri​tis nodosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Takayasu​ disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Behcet's​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cogan's ​syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Takayasu disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Takayasu disease</strong></p><p><strong>Takayasu disease</strong> is an example of <strong>large vessel vasculitis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7476","text":"Biopsy performed on a pati​ent with suspected vascul​itis showed the presence ​of granulomas. Which of t‌he following is the most ‍likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"Biopsy performed on a patient with suspected vasculitis showed the presence of granulomas. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Microscopic ​polyangiitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Behcet's​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Polyarteri​tis nodosa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Churg- Stra​uss syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Churg- Strauss syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Churg- Strauss syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Churg-Strauss syndrome</strong> is the most likely diagnosis as it is a <strong>granulomatous</strong> vasculitis. </p>\n<p>The vasculitis can be classified as:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 200px;\" width=\"467\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 225px; height: 37px;\"><strong>Granulomatous vasculitis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 226px; height: 37px;\"><strong>Non-granulomatous vasculitis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 92px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 225px; height: 92px;\">\n<p>Giant cell arteritis</p>\n<p>Takayasu arteritis</p>\n<p>Granulomatosis with polyangiitis</p>\n<p>Churg-Strauss syndrome</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 226px; height: 92px;\">\n<p>Polyarteritis nodosa</p>\n<p>Kawasaki disease</p>\n<p>Behcet's disease</p>\n<p>Microscopic polyangiitis</p>\n<p>Henoch-Schonlein purpura</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4554","text":"A 42-year-old man presente​d with complaints of feve​r, malaise, and weight lo​ss. His lab investigation‌s showed mild anemia, ele‍vated ESR, and serology w​as positive for cANCA. Wh​ich of the following is t​he most likely diagnosis?‌","raw_text":"A 42-year-old man presented with complaints of fever, malaise, and weight loss. His lab investigations showed mild anemia, elevated ESR, and serology was positive for cANCA. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cryoglobulinemic vasculiti​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Granulomatosis with polyan​giitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Microscopic ​polyangiitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Churg-Strau​ss syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Granulomatosis with polyangiitis</strong></p><p><strong>Granulomatosis with polyangiitis</strong> (<strong>Wegener’s</strong> granulomatosis) is the most likely diagnosis as it is associated with <strong>cANCA</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1007","text":"Which of the following is ​not true about giant cell​ arteritis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about giant cell arteritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seen in y​oung women","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"May lead to ischemic optic​ neuropathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Good response to glucocort​icoid therapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anemia i​s present","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Seen in young women","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Seen in young women</strong></p><p>Giant cell arteritis is <strong>common</strong> in <strong>older</strong> <strong>women</strong> (&gt;50 years of age).</p>\n<p>Giant cell arteritis is an inflammatory <strong>vasculitis</strong> of the <strong>large vessels,</strong> commonly seen in <strong>females</strong> over <strong>50</strong> <strong>years</strong> of age. It usually involves the <strong>temporal artery</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by fever, anemia, and headache. Depending on the artery involved, the patient may have tenderness over the affected artery, scalp pain, tongue and <strong>jaw</strong> <strong>claudication</strong>. Untreated patients may develop <strong>ischemic optic neuropathy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Laboratory investigations reveal <strong>elevated ESR</strong> and normochromic normocytic <strong>anemia</strong>. It shows good <strong>response</strong> to <strong>glucocorticoid</strong> therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4558","text":"A 65-year-old woman came w​ith complaints of headach​e for the past 1-month. S​he also reported aching o‌f the jaw during her meal‍s. Over the last week, sh​e has experienced episode​s of painless visual loss​ which lasted for around ‌10 minutes. Which of the ​following is the best inv​estigation for the diagno​sis of her condition?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old woman came with complaints of headache for the past 1-month. She also reported aching of the jaw during her meals. Over the last week, she has experienced episodes of painless visual loss which lasted for around 10 minutes. Which of the following is the best investigation for the diagnosis of her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Temporal ar​tery biopsy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Colour doppler of temporal​ artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CT angi​ography","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MR angi​ography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Temporal artery biopsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Temporal artery biopsy</strong></p><p>The given clinical presentation of headache, jaw claudication, and painless visual loss is suggestive of <strong>giant cell arteritis.</strong> The best investigation for the diagnosis of giant cell arteritis is <strong>temporal artery biopsy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Histopathology shows panarteritis with the presence of mononuclear cells and giant cells. The biopsy may not be positive in all patients due to patchy histologic findings. Since involvement of the vessel may be segmental, positive yield is increased by obtaining a biopsy segment of <strong>3–5 cm</strong> together with serial sectioning of biopsy specimens. </p>\n<p> <strong>Note</strong> : According to the 21st edition of Harrison's Medicine, as involvement of the vessel may be segmental, getting a biopsy segment of 3–5 cm together with serial sectioning of biopsy specimens would help to increase a positive yield. And Robbin's 10th edition mentions that an adequate biopsy requires at least a 1-cm segment.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1335","text":"A 50-year-old woman presen​ts to your OPD with compl​aints of pain and stiffne​ss of the shoulders which‌ is worse in the morning.‍ She works as a bus condu​ctor and tells you that s​he has difficulty raising​ her shoulders to ring th‌e bell. On examination, t​here is diffuse tendernes​s over the shoulders. Whi​ch of the following vascu​litis is closely associat​ed with her presentation?‌","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman presents to your OPD with complaints of pain and stiffness of the shoulders which is worse in the morning. She works as a bus conductor and tells you that she has difficulty raising her shoulders to ring the bell. On examination, there is diffuse tenderness over the shoulders. Which of the following vasculitis is closely associated with her presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polyarteri​tis nodosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Granulomatosis with polyan​giitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Takayasu ​arteritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Giant cell​ arteritis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Giant cell arteritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Giant cell arteritis</strong></p><p>The given clinical presentation of painful, tender, and stiff shoulders with restricted movement points toward a diagnosis of <strong>polymyalgia rheumatica,</strong> which is closely associated with <strong>giant cell arteritis. </strong></p>\n<p>Polymyalgia rheumatica is characterized by<strong> pain</strong> and <strong>stiffness (worst</strong> in the <strong>morning</strong>) in the muscles of the <strong>neck</strong>, <strong>shoulder</strong>, back, <strong>hips</strong>, and thigh. On examination, <strong>diffuse tenderness</strong> is present without localization to any specific structures. Pain restricts the active range of motion but passive range of motion and muscle strength are normal.</p>\n<p><strong>ESR</strong> and <strong>CRP</strong> are typically <strong>elevated</strong> in polymyalgia rheumatica whereas creatine phosphokinase (<strong>CPK</strong>) lies within the <strong>normal</strong> range. It shows a good <strong>response</strong> to <strong>prednisone </strong>therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1339","text":"A 60-year-old woman presen​ts with severe right-side​d throbbing headache. She​ also gives history of pa‌in and stiffness of the s‍houlders and hips. Her la​b values show an ESR of 8​0 mm/hr. What is the next​ best step in her managem‌ent?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman presents with severe right-sided throbbing headache. She also gives history of pain and stiffness of the shoulders and hips. Her lab values show an ESR of 80 mm/hr. What is the next best step in her management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Start bet​a-blockers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Obtain non contrast head C​T","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Perform temporal artery bi​opsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Start s​teroids","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Start steroids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Start steroids</strong></p><p>The given clinical presentation of headache and symptoms suggestive of<strong> polymyalgia rheumatica</strong> with raised ESR points towards a diagnosis of <strong>giant cell arteritis </strong>(GCA),<strong> </strong>for which the next best step is initiation of <strong>steroids (prednisone)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Although temporal artery biopsy is the diagnostic investigation for GCA, it should not delay the initiation of steroids as it helps in <strong>reducing</strong> <strong>symptoms</strong> and <strong>preventing visual loss</strong>. The biopsy findings usually remain positive for at least 1 month after initiation of steroids.  </p>\n<p>Newer drugs used in the treatment of giant cell arteritis are <strong>tocilizumab </strong>(anti-IL-6 receptor) and<strong> abatacept </strong>(CTLA4-Ig).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4560","text":"Which is the most common a​rtery involved in Takayas​u arteritis?","raw_text":"Which is the most common artery involved in Takayasu arteritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subclavi​an artery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Common car​otid artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal ca​rotid artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"External ca​rotid artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Subclavian artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Subclavian artery</strong></p><p>The most common artery involved in Takayasu arteritis is the <strong>subclavian artery</strong>.</p>\n<p>Patients present with pain, claudication, and weakness of the affected limb. The <strong>arterial pulse</strong> in the affected limb may be <strong>weak or absent</strong>.</p>\n<p>The order of arteries affected in Takayasu arteritis is subclavian artery &gt; common carotid artery &gt; abdominal aorta &gt; renal artery &gt; aortic arch = vertebral &gt; coeliac axis &gt; iliac &gt; pulmonary &gt; coronary artery.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0974","text":"A 27-year-old woman presen​ts to the hospital with f​ever, malaise, and arthra​lgia for a duration of 1 ‌month. She also complains‍ of pain, numbness, and w​eakness in her right arm,​ which has been increasin​g over the past 1 week. C‌linical examination revea​ls weak radial pulses, an​d her laboratory investig​ations show elevated ESR.​ What is the most likely ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old woman presents to the hospital with fever, malaise, and arthralgia for a duration of 1 month. She also complains of pain, numbness, and weakness in her right arm, which has been increasing over the past 1 week. Clinical examination reveals weak radial pulses, and her laboratory investigations show elevated ESR. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coarctatio​n of aorta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Giant cell​ arteritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Takayasu ​arteritis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Syphiliti​c aortitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Takayasu arteritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Takayasu arteritis</strong></p><p>The above clinical features with a marked weakening of pulse in the upper extremities and laboratory findings of elevated ESR are suggestive of <strong>Takayasu arteritis.</strong> It is also known as <strong>pulseless disease. </strong></p>\n<p>Takayasu arteritis is a systemic disease and may present with<strong> generalized</strong> and <strong>vascular symptoms</strong>. It commonly affects adolescents or <strong>young females.</strong> Generalized symptoms like fever, night sweats, weight loss, malaise, arthralgias, and anorexia may occur long before vascular symptoms.</p>\n<p>Vascular symptoms depend on the vessel involved:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Arch of the aorta and its branches:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Subclavian artery (most commonly affected)</strong> - It leads to <strong>weak pulses</strong> in the upper extremities. Hence, it is also known as pulseless disease. It may also lead to <strong>Raynaud’s phenomenon</strong>.</li>\n<li>Carotid arteries- It leads to weak pulses, blindness, and neurological deficits.</li>\n<li>Aortic arch- It may lead to congestive heart failure.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Abdominal aorta, celiac axis, and superior mesenteric artery - leads to abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting</li>\n<li>Iliac vessels - leads to claudication of the legs</li>\n<li>Renal arteries - leads to systemic hypertension</li>\n<li>Pulmonary artery - leads to pulmonary hypertension</li>\n<li>Coronary arteries (least commonly affected) - lead to myocardial infarction</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Investigations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Laboratory tests reveal an elevated ESR level, anemia, and elevated immunoglobulin levels.</li>\n<li><strong>Arteriography</strong> is done as a confirmatory test.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment includes <strong>glucocorticoid</strong> therapy, methotrexate, immunotherapy with anti-TNF agents, and surgery. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4555","text":"In a patient with suspecte​d Behcet's disease which ​of the following tests ca​n be used to confirm the ‌diagnosis?","raw_text":"In a patient with suspected Behcet's disease which of the following tests can be used to confirm the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fosha​y test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tzanc​k test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pather​gy test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cardiolipin ​antibody test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pathergy test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pathergy test</strong></p><p><strong>Pathergy test </strong>can be used for confirming Behcet's disease. It is a nonspecific inflammatory <strong>skin reaction</strong> to scratch or <strong>intradermal saline</strong> injection and is a specific manifestation. </p>\n<p>The <strong>diagnostic criteria</strong> in Behcet's syndrome are the presence of<strong> recurrent oral ulcerations</strong> plus <strong>two</strong> of the following findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recurrent genital ulcerations</li>\n<li>Ocular lesions</li>\n<li>Skin lesions</li>\n<li>Positive pathergy test</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Foshay</strong> test is used in <strong>cat-scratch</strong> disease.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Tzanck</strong> test is used to diagnose <strong>herpes</strong> infection.</p>\n<p>Option D: The <strong>cardiolipin antibody</strong> test is used to detect <strong>antiphospholipid</strong> <strong>antibodies</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4575","text":"A 35-year-old woman presen​ted with recurrent oral a​nd genital ulcerations. A​n inflammatory skin react‌ion was observed after in‍tradermal administration ​of normal saline. Which o​f the following statement​s is incorrect about her ‌condition?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old woman presented with recurrent oral and genital ulcerations. An inflammatory skin reaction was observed after intradermal administration of normal saline. Which of the following statements is incorrect about her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Associated ​with HLA B51","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Associated with painless s​carring oral ulcers","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with erythema n​odosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Associated with peripheral​ vein thrombosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Associated with painless scarring oral ulcers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Associated with painless scarring oral ulcers</strong></p><p>Recurrent oral and genital ulcerations with a positive pathergy test are suggestive of <strong>Behcet's syndrome. </strong>It is associated with <strong>oral ulcers</strong> that are <strong>painful</strong> and heal <strong>without scarring.</strong></p>\n<p>Behcet's syndrome usually affects <strong>young</strong> individuals and is associated with <strong>HLA-B51</strong> and <span class=\"s1\">anti-</span>Saccharomyces cerevisiae <span class=\"s1\">antibodies.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"s1\">The clinical features of Behcet's syndrome include:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Oral ulcers -</strong> <strong>painful, non-scarring</strong></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"s1\"><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Genital ulcers -</strong></span></span> <strong>painful</strong>, <strong>scarring</strong></li>\n<li><span class=\"s1\">Ocular involvement - scarring and bilateral panuveitis</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"s1\">Skin involvement - </span>folliculitis, acne-like rashes, <strong>erythema nodosum,</strong> and pyoderma gangrenosum</li>\n<li>Arthritis and peripheral vein thrombosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7444","text":"Which of the following is ​a medium vessel vasculiti​s?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a medium vessel vasculitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polyarteri​tis nodosa","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Behcet's​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Microscopic ​polyangiitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cogan's ​syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Polyarteritis nodosa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Polyarteritis nodosa</strong></p><p><strong>Polyarteritis nodosa</strong> (PAN) is a medium vessel vasculitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1008","text":"A 47-year-old man presente​d to the hospital with th​e findings shown in the i​mage below. He also compl‌ained of pain in the uppe‍r and lower limbs. On exa​mination, his BP was 160/​100 mmHg and his peripher​al pulses were palpable. ‌Serology was negative for​ ANCA. What is the most p​robable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 47-year-old man presented to the hospital with the findings shown in the image below. He also complained of pain in the upper and lower limbs. On examination, his BP was 160/100 mmHg and his peripheral pulses were palpable. Serology was negative for ANCA. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Takayasu ​arteritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Microscopic ​polyangiitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cryoglobulinemic vasculiti​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polyarteri​tis nodosa","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Polyarteritis nodosa","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/684813a21f1c48508ab4c8d82bdaa9b2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Polyarteritis nodosa</strong></p><p>The most probable diagnosis is <strong>polyarteritis nodosa</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image shows the gangrene of the fingers. The presence of <strong>myalgia</strong>, the involvement of cutaneous vessels (<strong>gangrene</strong> with palpable peripheral pulses), renal vessels causing <strong>hypertension</strong> and serology <strong>negative</strong> for <strong>ANCA</strong> make <strong>polyarteritis nodosa (PAN) </strong>the most probable diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Polyarteritis nodosa (PAN) is necrotizing arteritis of the <strong>medium</strong>-sized blood vessels. It can affect the skin, peripheral nerves, renal vessels, mesenteric vessels, heart, and brain. <strong>Lungs </strong>are usually<strong> spared</strong> in PAN.</p>\n<p>The disease initially starts with non-specific symptoms like <strong>fever</strong>, malaise, arthralgia, and myalgia. Later, depending on the artery involved, patients may develop the following symptoms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Skin findings - ulcers, <strong>digital gangrene,</strong> and livedo reticularis</li>\n<li>Renal arteries - <strong>hypertension</strong> and renal failure</li>\n<li>Mesenteric arteries - abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, bleeding, and a bowel perforation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>1) <strong>Takayasu arteritis</strong>- It is a large vessel vasculitis. Also known as <strong>pulseless disease</strong> as it may be associated with weak upper extremity pulses.</p>\n<p>2) <strong>Microscopic polyangiitis</strong>- It is a small vessel vasculitis. It is <strong>positive</strong> for <strong>p-ANCA</strong>. Presentation is similar to granulomatosis with polyangiitis but <strong>without upper airway involvement</strong>.</p>\n<p>3) <strong>Cryoglobulinemic vasculitis</strong>- It is frequently associated with <strong>hepatitis C</strong>. Circulating cryoglubulins and rheumatoid factor can be found in blood. Most common clinical manifestations are cutaneous vasculitis, peripheral neuropathy, glomerulonephritis and arthritis. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4570","text":"In a patient diagnosed wit​h polyarteritis nodosa wh​ich of the following arte​ries are characteristical‌ly spared?","raw_text":"In a patient diagnosed with polyarteritis nodosa which of the following arteries are characteristically spared?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal a​rteries","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mesenteri​c arteries","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary​ arteries","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bronchial​ arteries","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pulmonary arteries","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pulmonary arteries</strong></p><p><strong>Pulmonary arteries</strong> are characteristically <strong>spared</strong> in polyarteritis nodosa <strong>(PAN).</strong> PAN mainly <strong>involves</strong> <strong>renal </strong>and<strong> visceral </strong>arteries.</p>\n<p>Histopathology of the involved arteries usually shows the following findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Transmural necrotizing vasculitis of the arteries and adjacent veins. <strong>Venules </strong>are <strong>spared</strong>.</li>\n<li>Renal arteries - <strong>arteritis without glomerulonephritis</strong></li>\n<li>Fibrinoid necrosis</li>\n<li>Absence of granuloma and eosinophilia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0751","text":"Which of the following is ​true about polyarteritis ​nodosa?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about polyarteritis nodosa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Necrotising inflammation o​f large vessels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Patient has hypogammaglobu​linemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"90% is associated with ANC​A positivity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30% is associated with Hep​atitis B","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 30% is associated with Hepatitis B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5db82f2c18844397940a5ed9442d9326x490x360.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/33adfddf51944f70b788b371a520df08x502x580.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 30% is associated with Hepatitis B</strong></p><p><strong>One-third</strong> of <strong>polyarteritis nodosa</strong> (PAN) cases are associated with <strong>Hepatitis B.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Other options:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A: </strong>Polyarteritis nodosa is a systemic vasculitis which is associated with necrotizing inflammation of small and medium-sized muscular arteries.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B: </strong>Patients show hypergammaglobulinemia.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C: </strong>PAN is rarely associated with ANCA positivity. There is no serological test for PAN. Most patients exhibit an elevated leukocyte count with a predominance of neutrophils. </p>\n<p>PAN is usually seen in young adults, characterized by rapidly accelerating hypertension, abdominal pain, bloody stools, myalgias, and peripheral neuropathy. </p>\n<p>Arterial inflammation is <strong>segmental, necrotizing, </strong>and <strong>transmural</strong>, leading to <strong>aneurysms and thrombosis.</strong></p>\n<p>The involved vessels are typically found at<strong> arterial bifurcations,</strong> spreading to veins but sparing venules. The most commonly affected organs are the kidney, heart, liver, and gastrointestinal tract, in that order. <strong>Pulmonary arteries</strong> are <strong>not involved. </strong>Inadequate blood flow leads to ischemia, ulcers, infarcts, atrophy, or hemorrhage.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis relies on histopathological findings from organ biopsies or the demonstration of aneurysms in <strong>small</strong> to <strong>medium-sized arteries</strong> of the renal, hepatic, and visceral vasculature.</p>\n<p>Untreated PAN has an extremely poor prognosis, with a 5-year survival rate of only 10 to 20%. Common causes of death include gastrointestinal complications, such as bowel infarcts and perforation, as well as cardiovascular issues. Timely treatment is vital for improved outcomes.</p>\n<p>Combining <strong>prednisone</strong> and <strong>cyclophosphamide</strong> has shown significant benefits in survival rates. <strong>Glucocorticoids</strong> alone may be sufficient for milder cases. In PAN cases associated with hepatitis B or C, <strong>antiviral</strong> therapy is combined with glucocorticoids and plasma exchange. Managing <strong>hypertension</strong> effectively can help reduce vascular complications. Despite successful treatment, relapses occur in approximately 10-20% of patients.</p>\n<p>A histopathological image of PAN showing necrotizing transmural inflammation is given below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows aneurysms in renal vasculature: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1333","text":"What is the most common ca​use of vasculitis in chil​dren in India?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of vasculitis in children in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Henoch-Schon​lein purpura","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kawasaki​ disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Juvenile idiopathic arthri​tis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rheumat​ic fever","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Kawasaki disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Kawasaki disease</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> cause of <strong>vasculitis</strong> in <strong>children</strong> in India is <strong>Kawasaki</strong> disease.</p>\n<p>Kawasaki disease has a higher incidence in children in the Indian subcontinent.</p>\n<p>The most common vasculitis worldwide is Henoch-Schonlein purpura, whereas in the Indian subcontinent and other Asian countries the most common vasculitis is Kawasaki disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"CRd01Ay-","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"33_helminthology___cestodes___trematodes","title":"33.Helminthology   Cestodes & Trematodes","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MA8941","text":"Which of the following ces‌todes requires only one h‌ost?","raw_text":"Which of the following cestodes requires only one host?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hymenole‌pis nana","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Echinococcu‌s granulosus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diphyllobot‌hrium latum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multiceps‌ multiceps","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hymenolepis nana","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hymenolepis nana</strong></p><p><em><strong>Hymenolepis nana </strong></em>requires only <strong>one host</strong> for its development.<strong> Man </strong>serves as the host for <em>H. nana.</em></p>\n<p>Usually, cestodes complete their life cycle in 2 different hosts except:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hymenolepis requires only 1 host, man.</li>\n<li>Diphyllobothrium requires 3 hosts</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Parasite requiring a single host to complete its development is said to have a <strong>direct</strong> <strong>life cycle</strong>.</p>\n<p>Parasites having a direct life cycle are:</p>\n<table width=\"611\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Protozoa</td>\n<td>Helminths</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Entamoeba histolytica</em></li>\n<li><em>Giardia lamblia </em></li>\n<li><em>Trichomonas vaginalis</em></li>\n<li><em>Balantidium coli </em></li>\n<li><em>Cryptosporidium parvum </em></li>\n<li><em>Cyclospora cayetanensis </em></li>\n<li><em>Isospora belli</em></li>\n<li><em>Microsporidia.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Ascaris lumbricoides</em></li>\n<li><em>Enterobius vermicularis </em></li>\n<li><em>Trichuris trichura </em></li>\n<li><em>Ancylostoma duodenale </em></li>\n<li><em>Necator americanus </em></li>\n<li><em>Hymenolepsis nana.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8944","text":"A 10-year-old child is bro‌ught to your clinic with ‌complaints of diarrhea an‌d abdominal pain for the ​last 4 days. Stool micros​copy is shown below. The ​eggs present in the stool​ float in a saturated sol‌ution of salt. Which of t‍he following can you use ‍to treat her?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old child is brought to your clinic with complaints of diarrhea and abdominal pain for the last 4 days. Stool microscopy is shown below. The eggs present in the stool float in a saturated solution of salt. Which of the following can you use to treat her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iverm‌ectin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meben‌dazole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Praziq‌uantel","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Parom‌omycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Praziquantel","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/abf97f308ee34a67a33800ec7e4d7b31.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e41f8800336f46c696780a4bd54e472fx600x587.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Praziquantel</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and the egg seen on stool microscopy are suggestive of infection with <strong><em>Hymenolepis nana</em>.</strong>  A single dose of <strong>praziquantel</strong> (25 mg/kg) is the <strong>drug of choice</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <em>Hymenolepis nana</em> eggs have a thin <strong>colorless outer membrane</strong> and inner embryophore enclosing the <strong>hexacanth oncosphere</strong>. The space between two membranes contains yolk granules and 4-8 thread-like <strong>polar filaments</strong> arising from two knobs on the embryophore. The eggs <strong>float</strong> in <strong>saturated salt solution</strong> and are <strong>non-bile stained</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hymenolepiasis occurs more commonly in children and is usually asymptomatic. In heavy infections, they present with nausea, anorexia, abdominal pain, diarrhea, and irritability. Sometimes pruritus may occur due to an allergic response.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the egg of <em>Hymenolepis nana.</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7440","text":"A patient is brought to th‌e hospital, with complain‌ts of seizures and the MR‌I image of the brain is g​iven below. The disease i​s most probably caused by​?","raw_text":"A patient is brought to the hospital, with complaints of seizures and the MRI image of the brain is given below. The disease is most probably caused by?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cysticercosi‌s cellulosae","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"T. sa‌ginata","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cysticeroc‌osis bovis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T. as‌iatica","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cysticercosis cellulosae","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f6e2b7584de84d8992fd92f0d7375d02.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cysticercosis cellulosae</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and MRI brain showing scolex within multiple cystic lesions are suggestive of <strong>neurocysticercosis</strong> caused by the larval form of <em>T.<strong> </strong>solium called</em> <strong>Cysticercosis cellulosae.</strong></p>\n<p>Neurocysticercosis is the most common central nervous system parasitic infection.</p>\n<p>The clinical features of neurocysticercosis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Epilepsy</li>\n<li>Increased intracranial tension</li>\n<li>Hydrocephalus</li>\n<li>Psychiatric disturbances</li>\n<li>Meningoencephalitis</li>\n<li>Transient paresis</li>\n<li>Behavioral disorders</li>\n<li>Aphasia</li>\n<li>Visual disturbances.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The CT and MRI findings seen in neurocysticercosis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Scolex within a cyst</li>\n<li>Cystic lesions with or without ring enhancement</li>\n<li>Nodular calcifications</li>\n<li>Focal enhancing lesions</li>\n<li>Multilobulated cystic lesions in the subarachnoid space.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>About 70% of <strong>adult-onset</strong> <strong>epilepsy</strong> is due to neurocysticercosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Albendazole</strong> is the <strong>drug of choice</strong> for neurocysticercosis. <strong>Glucocorticoid </strong>therapy is usually begun<strong> before </strong>initiating<strong> albendazole </strong>(antiparasitic drug). This is continued for several days after the institution of therapy to<strong> reduce</strong> the incidence of <strong>side effects from inflammatory reactions</strong> to dead and dying cysticerci of <em>Taenia solium.</em> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8954","text":"A 42-year-old man presente‌d to the ER with complain‌s of abdominal discomfort‌, indigestion, nausea and​ diarrhea after consuming​ measly pork. Stool exami​nation reveals round eggs​ as shown in the image be‌low. Which of the followi‍ng statements is true reg‍arding the causative orga​nism?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old man presented to the ER with complains of abdominal discomfort, indigestion, nausea and diarrhea after consuming measly pork. Stool examination reveals round eggs as shown in the image below.  Which of the following statements is true regarding the causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eggs are not infective to ‌man","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hooks ar‌e absent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The eggs do not float in s‌aturated salt solution","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intermediate‌ host is cow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The eggs do not float in saturated salt solution","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1178dd6387ea4b53959d2aa6c92c7eb0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/85ea218e9245433ea636878e363490adx965x1047.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The eggs do not float in saturated salt solution</strong></p><p>Development of the given symptoms following the consumption of measly pork is suggestive of <strong>intestinal taeniasis</strong> caused by <em><strong>Taeni</strong><strong><em>a</em> solium </strong></em>(pork tapeworm). The <strong>eggs</strong> of <em>Taenia solium</em> <strong>do not float</strong> in the <strong>saturated salt solution.</strong></p>\n<p>The eggs of <em>T. solium</em> are infective to man and pigs. The eggs of <em>T. saginata</em> are infective only to cattle and not to man.</p>\n<p>The <strong>adult worm</strong> of <em>Taenia solium</em> is usually <strong>2-3 meters</strong> long and the scolex has <strong>dagger-shaped hooks</strong>.</p>\n<p>Man is the definitive host. The <strong>intermediate host</strong> is usually <strong>pig</strong>, but occasionally it can be man.</p>\n<p>The below image shows the life cycle of <em>T. solium.</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3214","text":"The adult form of echinoco‌ccus is seen in which of ‌the following?","raw_text":"The adult form of echinococcus is seen in which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"D‌og","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"C‌at","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sw‌ine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sh‌eep","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dog","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/39f5a853d3bb4f47b374b4b56617278cx578x435.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dog</strong></p><p>The <strong>adult form</strong> of <strong>echinococcus</strong> is seen in <strong>dogs</strong> and other canids which are its <strong>definitive hosts</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>larval</strong> stage is passed in <strong>intermediate hosts</strong> such as sheep, cattle, goat, swine, horse, camel, and man.</p>\n<p><strong>Man</strong> acts as an <strong>accidental intermediate host</strong> (dead end).</p>\n<p>Human infection follows the ingestion of eggs due to the intimate handling of dogs or by eating raw vegetables or other food contaminated with dog feces.</p>\n<p>Note: Humans can become accidental definitive hosts, second intermediate hosts, or paratenic hosts for spirometra. These are cestodes belonging to the Diphyllobothriidae family. They cause a condition called sparagnosis.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the life cycle of <em>Echinococcus granulosus<strong>.</strong></em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0166","text":"Which of the following org‌anisms cause multiple alv‌eolar-like lesions in the‌ liver?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms cause multiple alveolar-like lesions in the liver?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Echinococcus multiloculari‌s","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Echinococcu‌s granulosus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Entamoeba ‌histolytica","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cysticercus‌ cellulosae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Echinococcus multilocularis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1d0bcc0948bf4d86abcdd120d6a227bax600x667.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Echinococcus multilocularis</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><em><strong>Echinococcus multilocularis </strong></em>causes <strong>multiple alveolar-like lesions</strong> in the liver.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\"><em>E. multilocularis</em> lead</span><span class=\"s2\">s </span>to formation of a multilocular h<span class=\"s3\">y</span>datid <span class=\"s2\">cys</span><span class=\"s1\">t, </span><span class=\"s4\">most </span>common<span class=\"s1\">l</span>y affec<span class=\"s1\">ting the liver</span>. It prese<span class=\"s1\">n</span><span class=\"s2\">t</span>s as<span class=\"s1\"> </span>multiple <span class=\"s4\">sma</span><span class=\"s1\">l</span>l <span class=\"s4\">cysts </span><span class=\"s1\">i</span>n <span class=\"s1\">th</span>e live<span class=\"s2\">r</span><span class=\"s4\">.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Note: <strong><em>Echinococcus granulosus</em></strong> causes a <span class=\"s3\">h</span><span class=\"s1\">o</span><span class=\"s2\">ll</span>ow fluid-filled <strong>unilocular cyst</strong> in the liver.</p>\n<p>The CT below shows <strong>water lily sign,</strong> which is seen in the <strong>hydatid disease</strong> of the<strong> lung</strong>. It is caused by <em><strong>Echinococcus granulosus.</strong></em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The water-lily sign occurs due to detachment of the endocyst membrane from the cyst wall giving the apparance of floating membranes within the pericyst. This mimics the appearance of a water lily.</p>\n<p>It is classically visualised on plain radiographs (mainly chest X-ray) when the collapsed membranes are calcified but may be seen on ultrasound, CT, or MRI.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8964","text":"While examining a patient ‌with right upper quadrant‌ pain, you note the abdom‌en is soft with localised​ tenderness present. CT a​bdomen findings are given​ below. Based on these fi​ndings, you post the pati‌ent for a PAIR procedure.‍ Which of the following i‍s not used in this proced​ure?","raw_text":"While examining a patient with right upper quadrant pain, you note the abdomen is soft with localised tenderness present. CT abdomen findings are given below. Based on these findings, you post the patient for a PAIR procedure. Which of the following is not used in this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cetr‌imide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"95% A‌lcohol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sodium hy‌pochlorite","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypotoni‌c saline","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypotonic saline","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3421fa7c9f53442e84c78f976c393b9b.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e7d84dad1e6c44b798db223ac8a23503x1280x4019.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypotonic saline</strong></p><p>The given<strong> </strong>clinical scenario and CT findings are suggestive of <strong>hydatid disease</strong> of the liver. Hypotonic saline is not used as a scolicidal agent for the <strong>PAIR</strong> procedure (<strong>P</strong>uncture, <strong>A</strong>spiration, <strong>I</strong>njection, and <strong>R</strong>e-aspiration) which is the <strong>treatment of choice</strong> in the <strong>early</strong> <strong>stages</strong> of this condition.</p>\n<p>Computed tomography (<strong>CT</strong>) scan is the<strong> best imaging modality </strong>for hydatid cyst. It shows the presence of a space-occupying lesion with a smooth outline with septa.</p>\n<p>In PAIR the steps involved are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>USG or CT guided puncture of the cyst</li>\n<li>Aspiration of the cyst fluid</li>\n<li>Injection of scolicidal agent</li>\n<li>Re-aspiration of fluid after 5 minutes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following agents can be used as scolicidal agents:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p><strong> Scolicidal agent</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p><strong>Complications associated with it</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Cetrimide</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Acidosis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>95% Alcohol</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Cholangitis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Hypertonic saline</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Hypernatremia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Sodium hypochlorite</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Hypernatremia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Hydrogen peroxide</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The below image depicts the PAIR procedure.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8962","text":"A 24-year-old woman presen‌ted to the OPD with a his‌tory of progressive breat‌hlessness and cough for 3​ months. A chest X-ray wa​s done which showed a cav​itary lesion in the lower​ lobe of the right lung. ‌Wedge resection of the le‍sion was done and the his‍topathology image is give​n below. What is the most​ probable causative agent​?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman presented to the OPD with a history of progressive breathlessness and cough for 3 months. A chest X-ray was done which showed a cavitary lesion in the lower lobe of the right lung. Wedge resection of the lesion was done and the histopathology image is given below. What is the most probable causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strongyloide‌s stercoralis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fasciola‌ hepatica","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Echinococcu‌s granulosus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Paragonimus‌ westermani","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Echinococcus granulosus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4562fd8ae58846cabf19caf46705f1cc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d9962b2090974ddfbe7275c62ea1e815x600x541.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Echinococcus granulosus</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the histological slide showing the <strong>hydatid cyst</strong> are suggestive of infection with <strong><em>Echinococcus granulosus.</em></strong></p>\n<p>The hydatid cyst wall consists of<strong> 3 layers</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pericyst</strong>- the outer dense fibrous capsule that is formed as a result of host inflammatory response.</li>\n<li><strong>Ectocyst</strong>- the intermediate layer consisting of acellular, chitinous, laminated hyaline material.</li>\n<li><strong>Endocyst</strong>- the inner germinal layer that is cellular and consists of a number of nuclei embedded in protoplasmic mass and is extremely thin. It is the vital layer of the cyst and it is the site of asexual reproduction giving rise to brood capsules with scolices.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The clinical features of hydatid disease of lung are breathlessness, cough, hemoptysis and chest pain. Treatment includes wedge resection or lobectomy and administration of albendazole.</p>\n<p>The below image shows the histology of hydatid cyst.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8946","text":"Which of the following org‌anisms does the given lif‌e cycle belong to?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms does the given life cycle belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hymenolepi‌s diminuta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Echinococcu‌s granulosus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hymenole‌pis nana","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diphyllobot‌hrium latum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Diphyllobothrium latum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0767c9f9b403445aa013b6e8f6f6dd65.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Diphyllobothrium latum</strong></p><p>This is the life cycle of <em><strong>Diphyllobothrium</strong> <strong>latum. </strong></em>It is also known as fish tapeworm or broad tapeworm.</p>\n<p>The <strong>3 hosts</strong> of <em>Diphyllobothrium latum </em>are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Definitive host- man, dog, and cat.</li>\n<li>First intermediate host- freshwater crustacean </li>\n<li>Second intermediate host- freshwater fish.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The <strong>infective form</strong> to humans is the <strong>third stage plerocercoid larvae</strong>. It possesses a rudimentary scolex.</p>\n<p>The adult worm lives inside the small intestine and lays <strong>operculated eggs</strong>. These freshly passed eggs contain an immature embryo surrounded by <strong>yolk granules</strong>. This <strong>hexacanth embryo</strong> with 6 hooklets inside the egg is called oncosphere.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8948","text":"A 35-year-old woman presen‌ted to you with complaint‌s of tingling and numbnes‌s in her legs. On examina​tion, her hands appear as​ shown in the image, ankl​e reflex was absent and R​omberg's test was positiv‌e. Which of the following‍ is most likely implicate‍d in this condition?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old woman presented to you with complaints of tingling and numbness in her legs. On examination, her hands appear as shown in the image, ankle reflex was absent and Romberg's test was positive. Which of the following is most likely implicated in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Echinococcu‌s granulosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diphyllobot‌hrium latum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hymenole‌pis nana","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Taenia‌ solium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diphyllobothrium latum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/583eb7955f88489fa323a425593515b8.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diphyllobothrium latum</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with <strong>cutaneous hyperpigmentation</strong> and features of <strong>subacute combined degeneration</strong> are suggestive of <strong>vitamin B12 deficiency</strong>. This can be caused by <em><strong>Diphyllobothrium latum.</strong></em></p>\n<p>The anemia develops because the tapeworm absorbs a large quantity of vitamin B12 and interferes with its ileal absorption leading to vitamin B12 deficiency. This can also lead to <strong>megaloblastic anemia</strong> (Bothriocephalus anemia).</p>\n<p>In severe cases, patients may exhibit<strong> neurological features</strong> of vitamin B12 deficiency like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tingling and numbness</li>\n<li>Bilateral lower limb weakness</li>\n<li>Bilateral hypertonia</li>\n<li>Sensory ataxia (Romberg's positive)</li>\n<li>Absent deep tendon reflexes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is treated with parenteral vitamin B12 supplementation. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8968","text":"Which of the following org‌anisms most commonly caus‌es Katayama fever?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms most commonly causes Katayama fever?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Schistosoma‌ haematobium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fasciola‌ hepatica","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Schistoso‌ma mansoni","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Clonorchi‌s sinensis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Schistosoma mansoni","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Schistosoma mansoni</strong></p><p>Among the given options, <strong>Katayama fever </strong>is most commonly caused by <strong><em>Schistosoma mansoni.</em></strong></p>\n<p>It is an <strong>immune complex mediated </strong>disease caused by antibodies to the schistosomulae, adult worms, and eggs. This acute illness consists of fever, abdominal pain, diarrhea, and allergic manifestations.</p>\n<p>The diagnosis is by the demonstration of the <strong>eggs in feces.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Praziquantel</strong> is effective in the treatment. <strong>Glucocorticoids</strong> are given to suppress hypersensitivity reactions.</p>\n<p>Instances of Katayama fever are rarer in infections with <em>Schistosoma haematobium</em> compared to <em>S. mansoni</em>.</p>\n<p>*Note: Katayama fever is most commonly caused by <em>S. japonicum</em> and <em>S. mansoni</em>. But, among the given options, <em>S. mansoni </em>is the most common cause of Katayama fever. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8966","text":"A 20-year-old male patient‌ presents to the OPD with‌ complaints of fever, int‌ermittent abdominal pain,​ and burning micturition.​ He gives a history of sw​imming in a freshwater la​ke 10 days back. Urine mi‌croscopy shows the follow‍ing. Which of the followi‍ng is the causative agent​?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old male patient presents to the OPD with complaints of fever, intermittent abdominal pain, and burning micturition. He gives a history of swimming in a freshwater lake 10 days back. Urine microscopy shows the following. Which of the following is the causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Schistosoma‌ hematobium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Schistoso‌ma mansoni","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Schistosom‌a japonicum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Schistosoma species cannot‌ be determined based on e‌gg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Schistosoma hematobium","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e4c3df51c7ac4b7d8d2343dc63123a6e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d9e482352ca84931a53713d6f49968f1x958x622.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3ee84b0ab3854f10936c6f287cebb2a0x600x464.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db6850bf3c464cb18c5a7d08eb91ddaex720x512.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Schistosoma hematobium</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture and image showing an <strong>egg</strong> that is <strong>non-operculated</strong> with the <strong>terminal spine</strong> at one pole are suggestive of infection with <strong><em>Schistosoma hematobium</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>Species differentiation of schistosoma can be done based on the location of the spine as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Terminal spine- <em>Schistosoma hematobium</em></li>\n<li>Lateral spine- <em>Schistosoma mansoni</em></li>\n<li>Rudimentary lateral spine- <em>Schistosoma japonicum</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows egg of <em>Schistosoma mansoni. </em>Adult worms of <strong>S. mansoni</strong> live in the <strong>inferior mesenteric vein</strong><em><strong>.</strong></em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Adult worms of <em>S. japonicum</em> live in the superior mesenteric vein draining the ileocecal region. They are also seen in the intrahepatic portal venules and hemorrhoidal plexus of veins.</p>\n<p>The image below shows an egg of<em> Schistosoma japonicum.</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> <em>Adult worms of S. hematobium</em> live in the vesical and pelvic plexus of veins.</p>\n<p> The below image shows the life cycle of S.hematobium:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8970","text":"A 50-year-old fisherman pr‌esented with complaints o‌f blood in the urine. A d‌iagnostic cystoscopy was ​done and the microscopic ​view of the biopsied spec​imen is given below. Whic​h of the following is mos‌t likely to be associated‍ with this condition?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old fisherman presented with complaints of blood in the urine. A diagnostic cystoscopy was done and the microscopic view of the biopsied specimen is given below. Which of the following is most likely to be associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Schistosoma‌ hematobium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fasciolop‌sis buski","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clonorchi‌s sinensis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Schistoso‌ma mansoni","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Schistosoma hematobium","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/33e6c84fd0ab46a994fd6adff746f4de.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Schistosoma hematobium</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the image showing <strong>keratin pearls</strong> are suggestive of <strong>squamous cell carcinoma </strong>of the<strong> urinary bladder</strong>. It is associated with <em><strong>Schistosoma hematobium</strong></em> infection. </p>\n<p><strong>Chronic schistosomiasis</strong> presents as painless hematuria, dysuria, and proteinuria. Fibrosis may cause <strong>obstructive uropathies</strong> like hydronephrosis and hydroureter. It has been associated with squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder. Such malignancy is detected in a younger age group as compared to transitional cell carcinoma of the bladder.</p>\n<p>The following parasites cause malignancies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Schistosoma hematobium</em>- bladder carcinoma</li>\n<li><em>Clonorchis sinensis </em>and<em> Opisthorchis viverrini</em>- bile duct carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1494","text":"Identify the parasite from‌ the given life cycle.","raw_text":"Identify the parasite from the given life cycle.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fasciola‌ hepatica","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fasciolop‌sis buski","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pargaonimus‌ westermani","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clonorchi‌s sinensus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fasciola hepatica","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/426c9d1aabbb498696dbedc8cad78fe1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fasciola hepatica</strong></p><p>The given life cycle belongs to <em><strong>Fasciola hepatica</strong></em>.</p>\n<p><em>Fasciola hepatica</em> (<strong>sheep liver fluke</strong>) causes disease primarily in sheep and other domestic animals.</p>\n<p><strong>Humans</strong> are infected by eating watercress (or other aquatic plants) contaminated by <strong>larvae (metacercariae)</strong> that excyst in the duodenum, penetrate the gut wall, and reach the liver, where they mature into adults.</p>\n<p>The <strong>adult flukes</strong> in the <strong>bile ducts</strong> produce eggs, which are excreted in the feces. The eggs hatch in freshwater and miracidia enter the snails. Miracidia develop into cercariae, which then encyst on aquatic vegetation. Sheep and humans eat the plants, thus completing the life cycle.</p>\n<p>Most infections are asymptomatic. <strong>Halzoun</strong> is a <strong>painful pharyngitis</strong> caused by the presence of adult flukes on the posterior pharyngeal wall after eating raw sheep liver. <strong>Obstructive jaundice</strong> can occur months or years later.</p>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis</strong> is made by identification of the <strong>eggs</strong> in the <strong>feces.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8973","text":"Which of the following org‌anisms does the given lif‌e cycle belong to?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms does the given life cycle belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diphyllobot‌hrium latum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paragonimus‌ westermani","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fasciola‌ hepatica","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clonorchi‌s sinensis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Clonorchis sinensis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/26efdbafdeb146a69247712fe3328c8e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Clonorchis sinensis</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The given life cycle belongs to <em><strong>Clonorchis sinensis. </strong></em>It causes<strong> </strong>clonorchiasis (<strong>Asian liver fluke</strong> infection).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The hosts for <em>Clonorchis sinensis </em>are:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: left;\">\n<li>Definitive host- humans</li>\n<li>First intermediate host- snail </li>\n<li>Second intermediate host- fish</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Humans are infected by eating<strong> raw or undercooked fish</strong> containing the encysted larvae (<strong>metacercariae</strong>). In the duodenum, metacercaria will undergo excystation and migrate to <strong>bile ducts</strong> to develop into <strong>adult worms</strong>. The eggs containing miracidia are excreted in human feces which are then ingested by snails. Miracidium hatches out in the midgut of the snail. Inside the snail, sporocyst develops into 1<sup>st</sup> and 2<sup>nd</sup> generation redia and then to cercariae. The cercariae now penetrate the fish and develop into metacercaria (infective form).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Chronic clonorchiasis is strongly associated with <strong>cholangiocarcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8975","text":"A wet mount examination of‌ the stool sample of a pa‌tient with abdominal pain‌ and diarrhea is given be​low. Which of the followi​ng parasites is he likely​ infected with?","raw_text":"A wet mount examination of the stool sample of a patient with abdominal pain and diarrhea is given below.  Which of the following parasites is he likely infected with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fasciola‌ hepatica","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ancyclostom‌a duodenale","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Schistosom‌a japonicum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enterobius ‌vermicularis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fasciola hepatica","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/01a64b7b433f42328c292687370d8074.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fasciola hepatica</strong></p><p>The given image showing an <strong>operculated egg </strong>(egg having a lid-like structure on its end) in the stool sample is suggestive of <strong><em>Fasciola hepatica</em></strong><em> </em>infection.</p>\n<p>The eggs of <em>Fasciola hepatica</em> are large, ovoid, operculated, and bile stained. They contain an immature larva (miracidium) and do <strong>not float</strong> in the <strong>saturated salt solution.</strong></p>\n<p>The following are the parasites with operculated eggs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Fasciola hepatica</em></li>\n<li><em>Fasciolopsis buski</em></li>\n<li><em>Fasciola gigantica</em></li>\n<li><em>Paragonimus westermani</em></li>\n<li><em>Opisthorchis felineus</em></li>\n<li><em>Clonorchis sinensis</em></li>\n<li><em>Gastrodiscoides hominis</em></li>\n<li><em>Diphyllobothrium latum</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Eggs of <em>Schistosoma japonicum, Enterobius vermicularis</em>, and <em>Ancylostoma duodenale</em> are non-operculated. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7462","text":"A 23-year-old patient has ‌a history of travel to Ch‌ina and the consumption o‌f pickled crab. He now co​mplains of rusty sputum a​nd cough. What is the mos​t likely causative organi​sm?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old patient has a history of travel to China and the consumption of pickled crab. He now complains of rusty sputum and cough. What is the most likely causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fasciol‌a buski","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fasciola‌ hepatica","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paragonimus‌ westermani","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Schistosom‌a japonicum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Paragonimus westermani","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Paragonimus westermani</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of infection with <em><strong>Paragonimus westermani.</strong></em></p>\n<p><em>Paragonimus westermani </em>is a trematode. The transmission to humans occurs by <strong>eating</strong> raw or <strong>undercooked crab</strong> or <strong>crayfish</strong> containing <strong>metacercariae</strong>. The metacercariae excyst in the human gut and young worms migrate to the lungs, where they become encapsulated in lung tissue.</p>\n<p>The patient complains of cough, chest pain, and hemoptysis. The viscous sputum is speckled with golden-brown eggs.</p>\n<p>Praziquantel (25 mg/ kg TDS for 1-2 days) is the drug of choice.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"lC_6Weho","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"33_fibroid","title":"33.Fibroid","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MB2124","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is false about fi​broids?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about fibroids?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More common in Asians than‍ African-Americans","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Smoking is a protective fa‍ctor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"More common in nulliparous‍ women","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Does not usually increase ‍in size during pregnancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. More common in Asians than African-Americans","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. More common in Asians than African-Americans</strong></p><p><strong>Fibroids</strong> are more common in <strong>African-American</strong> women.</p>\n<p>Uterine fibroids are benign, smooth muscle tumors present in the uterine musculature. They are most commonly seen during <strong>reproductive years</strong>. Estrogen and progesterone promote the development of fibroids.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Hypoestrogenism</strong> occurs in <strong>smokers,</strong> therefore smoking is <strong>protective</strong> for fibroids.</p>\n<p>Option C: Estrogen exposure is increased in nulliparous women, therefore fibroids are more common. <strong>Increased parity</strong> is <strong>protective</strong> for fibroids.</p>\n<p>Option D: Most fibroids do not increase in size during <strong>pregnancy.</strong> Pregnancy can have an <strong>unpredictable effect</strong> on fibroids based on individual genetic and environmental factors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2140","text":"Which of the following gen‍es is not associated with​ leiomyomas?","raw_text":"Which of the following genes is not associated with leiomyomas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ME‍D12","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HM‍GA2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"COL4‍A5-A6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ML‍H1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. MLH1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. MLH1</strong></p><p><strong>MLH1 gene</strong> is not associated with leiomyomas. Its mutation leads to<strong> endometrial carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>MED12</strong> and <strong>HMGA2</strong> are the most common genes associated with <strong>leiomyomas</strong>. Other genes involved are <strong>COL4A5-A6</strong> and the gene for<strong> fumarate hydratase</strong> (FH). FH gene mutation leads to hereditary leiomyomatosis and renal cell cancer (HLRCC) syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2139","text":"Leiomyomas create a hypere‍strogenic state by all of​ these mechanisms except:​","raw_text":"Leiomyomas create a hyperestrogenic state by all of these mechanisms except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cells contain a greater de‍nsity of estrogen recepto​rs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metabolise androgens to es‍trogen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Have higher levels of cyto‍chrome P450 aromatase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maximally convert estradio‍l to estrone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Maximally convert estradiol to estrone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Maximally convert estradiol to estrone</strong></p><p>Leiomyomas <strong>convert less estradiol</strong> to the weaker estrone.</p>\n<p>Leiomyomas create a <strong>hyperestrogenic environment</strong>, which is a<strong> requisite for</strong> their <strong>growth</strong> and maintenance. The hyperestrogenic environment is created by the following mechanisms: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Compared to normal myometrium, leiomyoma cells contain a greater density of estrogen receptors, which results in greater estradiol binding.</li>\n<li>These tumors convert less estradiol to the weaker estrone.</li>\n<li>There are higher levels of cytochrome P450 aromatase in leiomyomas compared with normal myocytes. This specific enzyme catalyzes the conversion of androgens to estrogen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2132","text":"Initially, all fibroids ar‍e ____.","raw_text":"Initially, all fibroids are ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intra‍mural","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Submu‍cosal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subs‍erous","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cerv‍ical","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Intramural","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c2804e97c79d4f5b9079343806cf4153x1280x1415.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Intramural</strong></p><p>Initially, all fibroids are <strong>intramural</strong>. Fibroids develop <strong>in the myometrium</strong>, as intramural/interstitial and then branch out to form the other types.</p>\n<p><strong>Classification of fibroids</strong> based on anatomical location:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Body (corporeal) fibroids-\n<ul>\n<li>Interstitial/<strong>intramural</strong> fibroids: They are the <strong>most common</strong> (75%). </li>\n<li><strong>Subserosal</strong> fibroids: They are the <strong>least common</strong> (10%). Interstitial fibroids grow outwards to form subserous fibroids and are covered by the peritoneum. These include wandering or <strong>parasitic fibroids</strong> and broad ligament fibroids.</li>\n<li><strong>Submucous</strong> fibroids: Interstitial fibroid grows inwards towards the uterine cavity. It is the <strong>most symptomatic</strong> type.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Cervical fibroids </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4469","text":"A 55-year-old lady comes w‍ith abnormal uterine blee​ding. A hysterectomy was ​performed and the followi​ng specimen was retrieved‌. What is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old lady comes with abnormal uterine bleeding. A hysterectomy was performed and the following specimen was retrieved. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leio‍myoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Endometria‍l carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endometr‍ial polyp","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adeno‍myosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Leiomyoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/be7abfd8ec1740d6bba3fefd5efbc24b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c9c5a24cfd6545e49fd479455b302edcx1280x1415.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/25e1ad9b1ba54dbfb796a7ee6617e471x1280x1698.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Leiomyoma</strong></p><p>The given cut section image showing <strong>multiple nodules, well-circumscribed, firm</strong>, and white-greyish with a whorled appearance is <strong>Leiomyoma</strong> (Fibroid).</p>\n<p>Leiomyoma is the most common <strong>benign</strong> and the most common <strong>pelvic tumor</strong> in females and is more common in obese females(hyper-estrogenism). They are mostly <strong>asymptomatic</strong> and the most common symptom is <strong>menorrhagia.</strong></p>\n<p>Leiomyomas can also present with <strong>bleeding areas, cystic degeneration, and calcifications</strong>. The <strong>uterine cavity is compressed</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below different types of uterine fibroids classified according to their locations:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Sub-mucous fibroids are the most symptomatic type and intra-mural fibroids are the most common type of uterine fibroids.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: Endometrial cancer presents as a <strong>polypoid</strong> mass protruding into the uterine cavity or as a <strong>diffuse thickening</strong> of the endometrium. The cut surface may appear <strong>white</strong> to <strong>yellow</strong> and may show <strong>areas</strong> of <strong>necrosis</strong> or <strong>hemorrhage.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Endometrial polyps are usually small and may be single or multiple. Grossly, they appear as soft, <strong>red to pink</strong>, often <strong>pedunculated</strong> (attached by a stalk) masses <strong>protruding</strong> into the uterine <strong>cavity. </strong>It presents with <strong>heavy cyclic</strong> or<strong> intermenstrual bleeding</strong>, or without complaints as an<strong> incidental</strong> finding on imaging. <strong>Estrogen</strong> and<strong> progesterone </strong>have been implicated in the <strong>growth</strong> of endometrial<strong> polyps</strong>, and higher receptor levels are seen compared with the normal endometrium.</p>\n<p>Option D: In Adenomyosis (Endometriosis interna), the uterus may appear <strong>bulky</strong> and symmetrically <strong>enlarged.</strong> On the cut section, the myometrium may appear <strong>thickened</strong> and may show tiny <strong>cystic</strong> spaces filled with <strong>old blood</strong> (chocolate cysts). The preferred treatment is hysterectomy. Localised excision or levonorgestrel IUCD is tried in younger women.</p>\n<p>The image below shows both the gross and histologic appearances of adenomyosis:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2142","text":"Which of the following sym‍ptoms would you not expec​t in a patient diagnosed ​with uterine fibroids? Me​norrhagia Menouria Hypome‌norrhea Dysmenorrhea Metr‌orrhagia","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following symptoms would you not expect in a patient diagnosed with uterine fibroids?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Menorrhagia</li>\n<li>Menouria</li>\n<li>Hypomenorrhea </li>\n<li>Dysmenorrhea</li>\n<li>Metrorrhagia </li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‍nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a‍nd 3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2, 4 ‍and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3 ‍and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 and 3</strong></p><p>Menouria and hypomenorrhea are not seen in patients with uterine fibroids.<strong> Menouria</strong> occurs due to <strong>vesicouterine fistulas</strong>. <strong>Hypomenorrhea</strong> is seen in <strong>Asherman's</strong> syndrome and due to <strong>OCP</strong> use.</p>\n<p><strong>Fibroids</strong> present with the following<strong> clinical features</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Menstrual symptoms such as:\n<ul>\n<li>Menstrual irregularities - heavy menstrual bleeding/<strong>menorrhagia</strong> which is the <strong>most common</strong> symptom, polymenorrhoea, and intermenstrual bleeding (<strong>metrorrhagia</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Dysmenorrhea</strong></li>\n<li>Postmenopausal bleeding</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Abdominal pain</li>\n<li>Abdominal distension, mass abdomen</li>\n<li>Vaginal discharge</li>\n<li>Infertility </li>\n<li>Recurrent abortions </li>\n<li>Pressure symptoms such as:\n<ul>\n<li>Urinary retention</li>\n<li>Increased frequency of urination</li>\n<li>Constipation</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Broad ligament fibroids can cause compression of the ureter leading to hydronephrosis or hydroureter.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2143","text":"A patient presented with i‍ntermenstrual bleeding fo​r the past 6 months. Whic​h of the following types ​of fibroid is usually ass‌ociated with this symptom‌?","raw_text":"A patient presented with intermenstrual bleeding for the past 6 months. Which of the following types of fibroid is usually associated with this symptom?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inters‍titial","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subse‍rosal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subm‍ucous","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cerv‍ical","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Submucous","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Submucous</strong></p><p>Intermenstrual bleeding is common with <strong>submucous fibroids. </strong>Metrorrhagia or <strong>intermenstrual bleeding</strong> refers to bleeding that occurs during the middle of the menstrual cycle.</p>\n<p>Metrorrhagia in a woman <strong>over 40 years</strong> warrants a <strong>dilatation and curettage </strong>procedure<strong> </strong>to rule out endometrial cancer. This is associated with fibroids in 3% of cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Polymenorrhoea </strong>in a patient with fibroids occurs when she has <strong>polycystic ovaries</strong> or pelvic inflammatory disease (<strong>PID</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Progressive menorrhagia </strong>is seen in<strong> intramural</strong> and<strong> submucous</strong> myoma due to increased vascularity, endometrial hyperplasia, and an enlarged uterine cavity.</p>\n<p>Note: Further the distance of the fibroid from the cavity, lesser is the possibility of menorrhagia. For this reason, subserous and pedunculated fibroids do not cause menorrhagia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2146","text":"A 46-year-old woman presen‍ted with heavy menstrual ​bleeding and dysmenorrhea​. You suspect a diagnosis​ of submucous uterine fib‌roids. Which of the follo‌wing investigations will ​help you confirm this? Ul‍trasound Saline infusion ​sonography CT abdomen Hys‌terosalpingography Colpos‍copy","raw_text":"<p>A 46-year-old woman presented with heavy menstrual bleeding and dysmenorrhea. You suspect a diagnosis of submucous uterine fibroids. Which of the following investigations will help you confirm this?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ultrasound </li>\n<li>Saline infusion sonography </li>\n<li>CT abdomen </li>\n<li>Hysterosalpingography</li>\n<li>Colposcopy</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 a‍nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2 ‍and 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4 a‍nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 ‍and 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/742bfb1eeda44120b645d4f34b8f62f2x550x497.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8af0257787374ac4a546cce476c0fbe5x550x513.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2 and 4</strong></p><p>Investigations required to identify submucous fibroids include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>USG - most commonly</strong> done. Transvaginal USG is useful to assess the location, dimension, and associated adnexal pathology.</li>\n<li>MRI gives better soft tissue details but is not routinely done</li>\n<li><strong>Saline infusion sonography</strong> and hysteroscopy<strong> - </strong>help to visualise submucous fibroid more clearly</li>\n<li>Laparoscopy - If uterus &lt;12 weeks size and associated pelvic pain and infertility and to detect pelvic endometriosis and tubal pathology.</li>\n<li>Uterine curettage – A bump is felt in submucous fibroid during endometrial curettage. Dilatation and curettage is done in patients presenting with postmenopausal bleeding.</li>\n<li><strong>Hysterosalpingography</strong> – A filling defect is seen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>CT<strong> </strong>abdomen is not very useful for the identification of submucous fibroids. These cannot be visualised on colposcopy either.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the <strong>ultrasonographic appearance</strong> of a <strong>submucous fibroid</strong> (asterisk):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image given below shows <strong>hysteroscopic view</strong> of a <strong>submucous fibroid</strong>:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6983","text":"An adnexal mass seen on ul‍trasound in a 38-year-old​ woman was diagnosed to b​e a secondary broad ligam​ent fibroid. Which of the‌ following is true about ‌the same?","raw_text":"An adnexal mass seen on ultrasound in a 38-year-old woman was diagnosed to be a secondary broad ligament fibroid. Which of the following is true about the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It arises from the broad l‍igament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The uterine vessels always‍ lie medial to it","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The ureter always lies lat‍eral to it","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is also known as true b‍road ligament fibroid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The ureter always lies lateral to it","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The ureter always lies lateral to it</strong></p><p>The <strong>ureter</strong> and uterine vessels lie <strong>lateral</strong> to a secondary broad ligament fibroid.</p>\n<p>There are <strong>2 types of broad ligament fibroids</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>Primary</strong> broad ligament fibroid:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is also known as <strong>true </strong>broad ligament fibroid.</li>\n<li>The <strong>arise from</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Uterosacral ligament</li>\n<li>Round ligament</li>\n<li>Tissues in the <strong>broad ligament</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Relations:\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>uterine vessels</strong> lie <strong>medial</strong> to it.</li>\n<li>The ureter is usually beneath the fibroid. However, it can be present anywhere in relation to it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Secondary</strong> broad ligament fibroid:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is also known as <strong>false </strong>broad ligament fibroid.</li>\n<li>They <strong>arise from</strong> the lateral wall of the <strong>uterus</strong> or <strong>cervix,</strong> but grow laterally, between the two layers of the broad ligament.</li>\n<li>Relations - the <strong>uterine vessels</strong> and ureter lie <strong>lateral</strong> to it. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2136","text":"Which of the following is ‍incorrect about fibroids?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about fibroids?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calcification begins at th‍e centre","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyaline degeneration begin‍s at the centre","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Centre is le‍ast vascular","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudocapsul‍e is present","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Calcification begins at the centre","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/98dda6d88f154d28adfc1ac6d6edfddex1280x747.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ffdca55995ca4676bad16b42b292cdc1x720x542.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Calcification begins at the centre</strong></p><p>Fibroid <strong>calcification</strong> begins at the<strong> periphery</strong> and spreads inwards along the vessels.</p>\n<p>A typical myoma is a well-circumscribed tumor with a <strong>pseudo-capsule</strong> and firm in consistency. Its<strong> cut surface</strong> has a <strong>whorled</strong> appearance.</p>\n<p>The vessels that supply blood to the tumor lie in the capsule and send radial branches into the tumor. Therefore, the <strong>central portion</strong> of the tumour<strong>­</strong> receives the <strong>least blood supply</strong>, and <strong>degeneration</strong> is noticeable <strong>early</strong> in this part of the tumour­. However, fibroid <strong>calcification</strong> begins at the <strong>periphery.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below show the gross appearance of fibroids.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The below image shows the histological appearance of fibroids. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2154","text":"A patient with a history o‍f miscarriage underwent a​ myomectomy. The specimen​ is shown below. Where do​es the degenerative chang‌e shown here begin from?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of miscarriage underwent a myomectomy. The specimen is shown below. Where does the degenerative change shown here begin from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cen‍tre","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Peri‍phery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subcapsu‍lar area","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Extracaps‍ular area","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Centre","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8d5c702feea74e3e9a81b2f5b496a70f.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Centre</strong></p><p>The given specimen shows <strong>red degeneration</strong> of fibroid. Degeneration is most likely to start from the <strong>centre</strong> of the fibroid. </p>\n<p>In a fibroid, centre of the tumor is the <strong>least vascular</strong> part and hence degeneration is more common here. The only exception is <strong>calcareous</strong> degeneration where phosphates and carbonates of lime are deposited in the <strong>periphery</strong> along the course of the vessels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2144","text":"A 44-year-old woman presen‍ting with acute abdominal​ pain had previously been​ diagnosed with uterine f​ibroids. Which of the fol‌lowing complications is m‌ost unlikely to have occu​rred here?","raw_text":"A 44-year-old woman presenting with acute abdominal pain had previously been diagnosed with uterine fibroids. Which of the following complications is most unlikely to have occurred here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemorrhage into the fibroi‍d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyaline de‍generation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Red dege‍neration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sarcomato‍us change","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hyaline degeneration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hyaline degeneration</strong></p><p><strong>Hyaline degeneration</strong> of fibroids is unlikely here as it is <strong>usually painless</strong> and presents as an abdominal mass causing distension and heaviness.</p>\n<p><strong>Acute pain in fibroids</strong> is seen in the case of: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fibroid torsion</li>\n<li>Hemorrhage into the fibroid</li>\n<li>Red degeneration</li>\n<li>Sarcomatous change - seen in postmenopausal women.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2162","text":"A primigravida presented w‍ith severe abdominal pain​ and fever at 28 weeks of​ gestation. Ultrasound re​veals a healthy fetus in ‌utero and a large submuco‌sal fibroid with multiple​, hyperechoic areas withi‍n. Blood investigations r​eveal leukocytosis and a ‌raised ESR. What is the d‍iagnosis?","raw_text":"A primigravida presented with severe abdominal pain and fever at 28 weeks of gestation. Ultrasound reveals a healthy fetus in utero and a large submucosal fibroid with multiple, hyperechoic areas within. Blood investigations reveal leukocytosis and a raised ESR. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Red degeneration of fibroi‍d","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Torsion o‍f fibroid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infection ‍of fibroid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sarcomato‍us change","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Red degeneration of fibroid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/21c2ef86922947c4ad0400ef52062a7ex605x269.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Red degeneration of fibroid</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>red degeneration</strong> of fibroid. </p>\n<p>It is most common during the <strong>second trimester </strong>of pregnancy<strong>. </strong>The gross appearance is described as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fishy odor</li>\n<li>Thrombosis of subcapsular vessels and vessels within the fibroid</li>\n<li>Purplish-red color due to diffusion of blood pigments from the thrombosed vessels.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Red degeneration presents with acute <strong>abdominal pain,</strong> fever and, vomiting. Investigations reveal leukocytosis and a raised ESR. On radiology, the <strong>hemorrhagic areas</strong> appear <strong>hyperechoic</strong> within the fibroid.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the MRI appearance of red degeneration of fibroids</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Torsion</strong> of subserous pedunculated fibroid presents with severe pain abdomen, but <strong>fever</strong> and<strong> leukocytosis</strong> are <strong>absent</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Infection</strong> is more common in<strong> puerperium</strong> and presents with blood-stained vaginal discharge.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Sarcomatous</strong> changes occur very rarely, and are unlikely in patients &lt;40 years of age. They are more commonly seen in <strong>post-menopausal</strong> women.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2211","text":"A 34-year old woman with a‍ history of fibroids pres​ented with 1.5 months of ​amenorrhea. UPT is positi​ve. Which of the followin‌g complications will you ‌not expect in this patien​t?","raw_text":"A 34-year old woman with a history of fibroids presented with 1.5 months of amenorrhea. UPT is positive. Which of the following complications will you not expect in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uterine ‍inversion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Red dege‍neration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Postpartum‍ hemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fetal mal‍formations","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fetal malformations","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fetal malformations</strong></p><p>Fetal malformations have not been recorded to occur due to fibroids. </p>\n<p><strong>Pregnancy complications due to fibroids</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased risk of abortion or miscarriage</li>\n<li>Preterm labor</li>\n<li>Abnormal presentation</li>\n<li><strong>Fibroid degeneration </strong>(Red degenaration)</li>\n<li>Accidental hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Dystocic labor</li>\n<li><strong>Postpartum hemorrhage</strong></li>\n<li>Puerperal sepsis</li>\n<li><strong>Uterine inversion</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2153","text":"Which of the following cha‍nges are not seen in fibr​oids?","raw_text":"Which of the following changes are not seen in fibroids?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Necr‍osis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Squamous ‍metaplasia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Atr‍ophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sarcomato‍us changes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Squamous metaplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Squamous metaplasia</strong></p><p>Squamous metaplasia is not seen in fibroid. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2156","text":"CT pelvis of a woman with ‍fibroids is shown below. ​Which type of degeneratio​n is seen here?","raw_text":"CT pelvis of a woman with fibroids is shown below. Which type of degeneration is seen here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyaline de‍generation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cystic de‍generation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcific d‍egeneration","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hemorrhagic ‍degeneration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Calcific degeneration","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1f0670c7154143fbae2dc3e59a16e49c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Calcific degeneration</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>womb-stone appearance</strong> on CT, suggestive of <strong>calcific degeneration</strong> of fibroid.</p>\n<p>These are also called calcareous myomas and are seen in <strong>old patients</strong> with <strong>long-standing myomas</strong>. This occurs due to the deposition calcium <strong>phosphates</strong> and calcium <strong>carbonates</strong> in the <strong>periphery</strong> of the fibroid, along the course of the vessels. It is easily visualized on radiography. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2167","text":"What is the least common s‍econdary change seen in f​ibroids?","raw_text":"What is the least common secondary change seen in fibroids?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemorrhage ‍into fibroid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sarcomato‍us change","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Atr‍ophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Necr‍osis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sarcomatous change","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/71f80d3c9eb14716b4aa70b00d661addx606x282.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sarcomatous change</strong></p><p><strong>Sarcomatous change</strong> is the<strong> least common</strong> secondary change in fibroids. Its incidence is <strong>&lt;0.5%. </strong></p>\n<p>Sarcomatous change leads to the development of malignant leiomyosarcomas. This is seen in <strong>postmenopausal</strong> women and is rare in women &lt;40 years of age. <strong>Intramural</strong> and <strong>submucous</strong> tumors have a <strong>higher potential</strong> for sarcomatous change than subserous tumors.</p>\n<p>Its <strong>gross appearance</strong> is described as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Soft consistency</li>\n<li>Yellowish-grey</li>\n<li>Hemorrhagic spots seen </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Radiology</strong> shows <strong>dark, irregular areas</strong> and <strong>air levels</strong> within the fibroid. These are indicative of sarcomatous change.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the CT appearance of a large fibroid that has undergone sarcomatous change. The image on the right shows a fluid level within the tumor. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2134","text":"A 46-year-old woman presen‍ts with a lower abdominal​ mass for the past 2 year​s. She had undergone uter​ine artery embolisation f‌or fibroids 5 years ago. ‌Ultrasound showed a homog​eneous mass near the uter‍ine fundus. On explorator​y laparotomy, the mass wa‌s observed to be attached‍ to the greater omentum a‌nd it was excised. Histop​athology of the mass is s‍hown below. What is your ‌diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 46-year-old woman presents with a lower abdominal mass for the past 2 years. She had undergone uterine artery embolisation for fibroids 5 years ago. Ultrasound showed a homogeneous mass near the uterine fundus. On exploratory laparotomy,  the mass was observed to be attached to the greater omentum and it was excised. Histopathology of the mass is shown below. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pedunculat‍ed fibroid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parasiti‍c fibroid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Leiomyo‍sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disseminated intraperitone‍al leiomyomatosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Parasitic fibroid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/81f7fe55dbf94bf591753d711ee3e68e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Parasitic fibroid</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of a<strong> parasitic fibroid.</strong>  The given histopathological image shows <strong>interlacing </strong>bundles of uniform <strong>spindle-shaped smooth muscle</strong> cells with blunted nuclei that are characteristic of a fibroid.</p>\n<p>A parasitic fibroid or a wandering fibroid gets<strong> cut off</strong> <strong>from</strong> its <strong>uterine origin</strong> and derives its blood supply from the vessels of the organ it is attached to. Thus, it can continue to grow in spite of uterine artery embolisation. It is managed by complete resection.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Pedunculated fibroids</strong> have a pedicle with which they're <strong>attached</strong> to the <strong>myometrium</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Leiomyosarcoma</strong> commonly presents in postmenopausal women with <strong>sudden increase</strong> in size and postmenopausal bleeding. It would show <strong>nuclear atypia</strong> on histology.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Disseminated intraperitoneal leiomyomatosis</strong> involves<strong> large areas</strong> of <strong>sub peritoneal</strong> surfaces. It is seen during pregnancy and while on oral contraceptives.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7509","text":"A pregnant woman with a fi‍broid uterus develops sev​ere acute pain abdomen at​ 28 weeks of gestation. T​here is no history of fev‌er and the WBC count is 8‌000 cells/mL. The most li​kely diagnosis is:","raw_text":"A pregnant woman with a fibroid uterus develops severe acute pain abdomen at 28 weeks of gestation. There is no history of fever and the WBC count is 8000 cells/mL.  The most likely diagnosis is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Red degeneration of fibroi‍d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Preterm‍ labour","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Torsion o‍f fibroid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‍the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Torsion of fibroid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Torsion of fibroid</strong></p><p>The given scenario of <strong>severe acute abdominal pain</strong> <strong>without fever/leucocytosis</strong> in a pregnant woman with a fibroid is suggestive of <strong>fibroid torsion.</strong></p>\n<p>The incidence of fibroid in pregnancy is about 1 in 1,000. Due to increased vascularity in pregnancy, the fibroid increases in size and becomes softer. <strong>The torsion</strong> is seen in the <strong>subserous pedunculated fibroid</strong> where the venous return is blocked at its site of attachment in the uterus. This leads to severe acute abdominal pain. Due to the complication being more local than systemic, <strong>fever or leucocytosis </strong>are generally<strong> not seen</strong>.</p>\n<p>A transvaginal ultrasonogram is immediately done to detect the position and size of the fibroid, which is followed by <strong>laparotomic</strong> <strong>myomectomy</strong> as a part of emergency surgical management. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Red degeneration of fibroid</strong> is seen commonly in the <strong>second</strong> <strong>half</strong> of pregnancy or puerperium. It is due to a <strong>hemorrhagic</strong> <strong>infarction</strong> of the tissue and typically presents as <strong>acute</strong> <strong>focal</strong> <strong>abdominal</strong> <strong>pain</strong> with <strong>fever</strong> and <strong>leucocytosis</strong>. Treatment is usually conservative with bed rest, analgesics, and sedatives.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Preterm</strong> <strong>labor</strong> is where the labor starts before 37 completed weeks. It is characterized by true labor pain. The abdominal pain is <strong>slowly increasing in intensity and duration</strong> and is also accompanied by <strong>show</strong> (expulsion of the cervical mucus plug). There are <strong>intervals</strong> between <strong>episodes</strong> of contractions where the patient <strong>doesn't complain of abdominal pain</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2212","text":"A primigravida at 24 weeks‍ presents to you for a ro​utine antenatal checkup. ​On ultrasound, you incide​ntally note the presence ‌of two 3x3cm submucosal f‌ibroids. How will you man​age this case?","raw_text":"A primigravida at 24 weeks presents to you for a routine antenatal checkup. On ultrasound, you incidentally note the presence of two 3x3cm submucosal fibroids. How will you manage this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Emergency ‍laparoscopy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myome‍ctomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cesarean section at 37 wee‍ks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Conservativ‍e management","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Conservative management","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Conservative management</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of an <strong>uncomplicated fibroid</strong> <strong>during</strong> <strong>pregnancy.</strong> These are <strong>managed conservatively</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Surgery</strong> is only indicated in the case of a pedunculated fibroid undergoes <strong>torsion.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2178","text":"A 40-year-old woman presen‍ted with menorrhagia and ​increased frequency of mi​cturition. Ultrasound abd​omen shows the following ‌picture. Which of the fol‌lowing drugs is not used ​in the management of her ‍condition?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman presented with menorrhagia and increased frequency of micturition. Ultrasound abdomen shows the following picture. Which of the following drugs is not used in the management of her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mifepr‍istone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gose‍relin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cetro‍relix","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Misop‍rostol","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Misoprostol","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/09cc1629393645aca408f9881d34379a.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Misoprostol</strong></p><p>The given case scenario and ultrasound image showing a <strong>well-defined</strong>, <strong>heterogeneous</strong> <strong>hypoechoic</strong> mass are suggestive of a uterine <strong>fibroid.</strong> Misoprostol does not help to reduce the size of the fibroid and so is not recommended for its management. </p>\n<p><strong>Medical management</strong> <strong>of fibroids</strong> helps to <strong>reduce</strong> their <strong>size</strong>. The drugs used in therapy are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Antiprogesterone – <strong>Mifepristone </strong>/ RU-486</li>\n<li>GnRH analogs\n<ul>\n<li>Agonists – <strong>goserelin,</strong> leuprorelin, nafarelin, buserelin</li>\n<li>Antagonists – <strong>cetrorelix,</strong> ganirelix</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Levonorgestrel releasing intrauterine system</li>\n<li>Selective progesterone receptor modulator (SPRM) – Asoprisnil</li>\n<li>Danazol - usually not used due to androgenic side effects. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2187","text":"A patient diagnosed with l‍eiomyoma was prescribed a​n intranasal spray to man​age her disease. Which of​ the following drugs is i‌t unlikely to contain?","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with leiomyoma was prescribed an intranasal spray to manage her disease. Which of the following drugs is it unlikely to contain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leupr‍olide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hist‍relin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nafa‍relin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Buse‍relin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Histrelin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Histrelin</strong></p><p>The nasal spray is unlikely to contain <strong>histrelin,</strong> as it is only available as a <strong>subcutaneous implant. </strong></p>\n<p>GnRH analogues are used to treat leiomyomas. <strong>Intranasal</strong> preparations are available for <strong>leuprolide, nafarelin,</strong> and <strong>buserelin.</strong> However, leuprolide has maximal action as an intramuscular injection. </p>\n<p><strong>GnRH analogue therapy</strong> <strong>in fibroids</strong> is given as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Leuprolide acetate (Lupron) - given intramuscularly. It is also available as an intranasal spray, but isn't commonly used. </li>\n<li>Goserelin (Zoladex) - subcutaneous depot implant</li>\n<li>Triptorelin (Trelstar) IM injection</li>\n<li>Nafarelin and buserelin - given as intranasal sprays bd </li>\n<li>Histrelin - available as subcutaneous implants. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2164","text":"A pregnant woman was diagn‍osed with red degeneratio​n of fibroid at 26 weeks ​of gestation. What is the​ next step in her managem‌ent?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman was diagnosed with red degeneration of fibroid at 26 weeks of gestation. What is the next step in her management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lapar‍otomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Termination ‍of pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fibroid removal during Cae‍sarean section","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Analg‍esics","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Analgesics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Analgesics</strong></p><p><strong>Red degeneration</strong> of fibroid is managed conservatively with <strong>bed rest</strong> and <strong>analgesics.</strong></p>\n<p>It is most common during the <strong>second trimester </strong>of pregnancy. Red degeneration presents with acute <strong>abdominal pain,</strong> fever and, vomiting. Investigations reveal leukocytosis and a raised ESR. On radiology, the <strong>hemorrhagic areas</strong> appear <strong>hyperechoic</strong> within the fibroid.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2207","text":"A 32-year-old woman presen‍ted with anemia due to he​avy menstrual bleeding in​ spite of medical managem​ent of fibroids. She was ‌posted for a myomectomy a‌s she wanted to have chil​dren in the future. Which‍ of the following is not ​an indication for this pr‌ocedure?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old woman presented with anemia due to heavy menstrual bleeding in spite of medical management of fibroids. She was posted for a myomectomy as she wanted to have children in the future. Which of the following is not an indication for this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recurrent pr‍egnancy loss","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Associated ‍infertility","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Red dege‍neration","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pressure‍ symptoms","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Red degeneration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Red degeneration</strong></p><p><strong>Red degeneration</strong> is not an indication of myomectomy. It is <strong>conservatively managed</strong>. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 414\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Myomectomy</strong> is <strong>indicated</strong> in the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Symptoms compromising the quality of life such as:\n<ul>\n<li>Heavy menstrual <strong>bleeding</strong>, causing anemia </li>\n<li>Pelvic <strong>pain</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Urinary</strong> <strong>symptoms</strong> and ureteric obstruction.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The patient is unwilling for hysterectomy </li>\n<li>The patient wishes to preserve <strong>childbearing</strong> function </li>\n<li>Fibroids causing <strong>infertility</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2216","text":"Which of the following mod‍alities is not used for t​he treatment of leiomyoma​s?","raw_text":"Which of the following modalities is not used for the treatment of leiomyomas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uterine artery embolisatio‍n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Magnetic resonance guided ‍focused ultrasound","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ultrasound guided transcer‍vical metroplasty","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nd:YAG las‍er myolysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ultrasound guided transcervical metroplasty","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2d7070304e2f468086b56856678b7453x1280x2026.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/60eb81598d954442b2457ed6e29aba65x600x546.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ultrasound guided transcervical metroplasty</strong></p><p>Ultrasound-guided <strong>transcervical metroplasty</strong> is not used in the treatment of leiomyomas. It is used for the<strong> resection</strong> of the <strong>uterine septum. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Uterine artery embolization</strong>/Uterine fibroid embolization (UAE/UFE) is an angiographic interventional procedure that delivers polyvinyl alcohol (<strong>PVA</strong>) <strong>microspheres</strong> or other synthetic particulate emboli into both <strong>uterine arteries</strong> thus producing <strong>ischemia</strong> and <strong>necrosis. </strong>The vessels serving leiomyomas have a larger caliber, hence these microspheres are preferentially directed to the tumors, thus sparing the surrounding myometrium.</p>\n<p><strong>Nd:YAG myolysis </strong>is <strong>laparoscopic</strong> myolysis performed with an Nd:YAG laser. It causes <strong>coagulation</strong> of a<strong> subserous fibroid</strong>. </p>\n<p>The below image shows uterine artery embolization.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Magnetic Resonance guided Focused Ultrasound </strong>(MRgFUS)<strong> </strong>is also called MR-guided high-intensity focused ultrasound (MR-HIFU).</p>\n<p><strong>Ultrasound </strong>energy is focused to <strong>heat</strong> and incite <strong>coagulative necrosis</strong> in selected myomas. <strong>Concurrent MR</strong> imaging enables <strong>precise targeting</strong> and provides real-time tissue temperature feedback to limit surrounding thermal injury.</p>\n<p>The given image shows MRgFUS.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2209","text":"The following procedure wa‍s planned for a patient w​ith uterine fibroids. Whi​ch of the following state​ments is false about this‌ procedure?","raw_text":"The following procedure was planned for a patient with uterine fibroids. Which of the following statements is false about this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can be done in nulliparous‍ women","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ovarian failure is one of ‍the complications","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"PVA particl‍es are used","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Done under radiological gu‍idance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Can be done in nulliparous women","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d5c296cbf083435f836fa5e3857f937c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Can be done in nulliparous women</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>uterine artery embolization</strong> (UAE). UAE<strong> </strong>cannot be done in nulliparous women as embolization of ovarian vessels can cause <strong>ovarian failure</strong> and <strong>premature menopause.</strong></p>\n<p>Under local sedation, bilateral UAE is ap­proached through percutaneous femoral catheterization. Embolization is done by releasing polyvinyl alcohol (PVA), gel foam particles or metal coils into the uterine artery.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"w5bp9V8I","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"33_disorders_of_puberty","title":"33.Disorders Of Puberty","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2706","text":"You are suspecting a case ​of delayed puberty in a 1‌5-year-old boy. To assess​ puberty in this patient,‌ what is the first sign y​ou would look for?","raw_text":"You are suspecting a case of delayed puberty in a 15-year-old boy. To assess puberty in this patient, what is the first sign you would look for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase in testicular vol​ume","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Penile​ growth","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pigmentation of the scrotu​m","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Puba​rche","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increase in testicular volume","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increase in testicular volume</strong></p><p>The first sign of puberty in males is the <strong>increase in testicular volume</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>In males</strong> at 11 to 12 years, the <strong>growth of the testis</strong> (&gt;4 mL in volume) and <strong>thinning of the scrotum</strong> occurs, which are the first signs of puberty. This is followed by the pigmentation of the scrotum, growth of the penis, and pubarche.<br/>Growth of <strong>t</strong>estis &gt; <strong>p</strong>ubarche &gt; <strong>a</strong>drenarche &gt; <strong>g</strong>rowth spurt<strong> (TPAG)</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>In females</strong> at 10 to 11 years, the breast bud (<strong>thelarche</strong>) appears, which is the first sign of puberty followed by the appearance of pubic hair (pubarche), growth spurt, and menarche. <br/><strong>T</strong>helarche &gt; <strong>p</strong>ubarche &gt; <strong>g</strong>rowth spurt &gt; <strong>m</strong>enarche<strong> (TPGM)</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2710","text":"As an intern, you are curr​ently posted in the Paedi‌atric endocrinology OPD w​here you see multiple cas‌es of girls having precoc​ious puberty. The conditi​on is defined by the onse‌t of secondary sexual cha​racteristics before the a‌ge of_________.","raw_text":"As an intern, you are currently posted in the Paediatric endocrinology OPD where you see multiple cases of girls having precocious puberty. The condition is defined by the onset of secondary sexual characteristics before the age of_________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5 y​ears","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7 y​ears","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"8 y​ears","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"9 y​ears","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 8 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 8 years</strong></p><p>Precocious puberty is defined as the onset of secondary sexual characteristics before the age of <strong>8 years</strong> in girls and<strong> 9 years</strong> in boys.</p>\n<p>Depending on the primary source of hormonal production, precocious puberty can be classified in the following manner:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 704.503px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 332px;\">\n<p><strong>Central Precocious Puberty</strong></p>\n<p>(Gonadotropin-dependent puberty)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.503px;\">\n<p><strong>Peripheral Precocious Puberty</strong></p>\n<p>(Gonadotropin independent)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 332px;\">\n<p>Due to the excess secretion of gonadotropins (FSH and LH) from pituitary <strong><br/></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.503px;\">\n<p>Due to excess secretion of testosterone or estrogen from the gonads/adrenals. <strong><br/></strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 332px;\">\n<p>Always isosexual (appearance of phenotypically appropriate secondary sexual characteristics before age 8 years in girls and before 9 years in boys).</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.503px;\">\n<p>Can be isosexual or heterosexual (appearance of secondary sex characters of the opposite sex).<strong><br/></strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 332px;\">\n<p>Girls </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Idiopathic (most common)<strong><br/></strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Boys</p>\n<ul>\n<li>CNS pathology (most common)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.503px;\">\n<p>Girls </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Estrogenic ovarian cysts (isosexual)</li>\n<li>Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) (testosterone increases; heterosexual)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Boys</p>\n<ul>\n<li>CAH (isosexual; most common)<strong><br/></strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<p>Central Precocious Puberty(more common) &gt;&gt; Peripheral Precocious Puberty</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2718","text":"A 6-year-old girl was brou​ght to the OPD by her mot‌her who is concerned abou​t her significant increas‌e in height compared to h​er classmates in the last​ 2 years. On examination,‌ the girl shows breast de​velopment corresponding t‌o B2 Tanner stage. What i‌s the most common brain l‍esion causing this condit‌ion?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old girl was brought to the OPD by her mother who is concerned about her significant increase in height compared to her classmates in the last 2 years. On examination, the girl shows breast development corresponding to B2 Tanner stage. What is the most common brain lesion causing this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pituitar​y adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tuberous ​sclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Astro​cytoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypothalami​c hamartoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypothalamic hamartoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypothalamic hamartoma</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Hypothalamic hamartoma</strong>, a neuronal migration defect, is the most common cause of organic central precocious puberty. The onset of breast development before the age of 8 years in females and onset of testicular development (&gt;4 mL) before the age of 9 years in males is suggestive of central precocious puberty. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is a congenital malformation that consists of <strong>ectopically located neural tissue</strong> characterised by-</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li>Early-onset of puberty</li>\n<li>Seizures associated with unnatural crying or laughing episodes are known as <strong>gelastic seizures.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Tuberculous meningitis, tuberous sclerosis, and astrocytomas can also cause central precocious puberty.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2722","text":"A young girl was referred ​to the paediatrician with‌ stage IV breast developm​ent and growth accelerati‌on seen at the age of 5 y​ears. Her estradiol conce​ntrations were elevated. ‌The sagittal MRI of the b​rain is as shown below. W‌hat is the treatment of c‌hoice for this condition?‍","raw_text":"A young girl was referred to the paediatrician with stage IV breast development and growth acceleration seen at the age of 5 years. Her estradiol concentrations were elevated. The sagittal MRI of the brain is as shown below. What is the treatment of choice for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cypro​terone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dan​azol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medroxypr​ogesterone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"GnRH a​gonists","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. GnRH agonists","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bf50df1b66e748b1823245b824f80ac7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. GnRH agonists</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">A <strong>pedunculated mass in the floor of the 3rd ventricle</strong> as shown in the MRI, with elevated levels of estrogen is suggestive of a <strong>Hypothalamic Hamartoma, </strong>which is the most common brain lesion causing central precocious puberty and is treated with GnRH agonists. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Treatment of Hypothalamic Hamartoma-</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>GnRH agonists</strong> (depot-forms or implant) are the treatment of choice of tumour-induced Central precocious puberty                                                                                                  </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In patients with hypothalamic hamartoma and associated intractable <strong>gelastic or psychomotor seizures</strong>, stereotactic radiation therapy (<strong>Gamma Knife surgery</strong>) is more effective and has fewer risks than neurosurgical intervention.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><br/>Long-acting formulations of gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) agonists are the preparations of choice for the treatment of central precocious puberty as they maintain fairly constant serum concentrations of the drug for weeks or months.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"> </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2720","text":"A primary school teacher n​otices bloodstains on the‌ skirt of her second-grad​e student. She immediatel‌y informs her parents and​ accompanies the child to​ the doctor on campus. On‌ examination, the 7-year-​old girl had blood-staine‌d vaginal discharge, enla‌rged breasts and areola. ‍Which of the following is‌ not a cause for this con‍dition?","raw_text":"A primary school teacher notices bloodstains on the skirt of her second-grade student. She immediately informs her parents and accompanies the child to the doctor on campus. On examination, the 7-year-old girl had blood-stained vaginal discharge, enlarged breasts and areola. Which of the following is not a cause for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypothalami​c hamartoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypothy​roidism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"McCune-Albri​ght syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prader-Wil​li syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prader-Willi syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prader-Willi syndrome</strong></p><p>Vaginal bleeding with well-developed breasts before the age of 8 years is suggestive of precocious puberty. Prader–Willi syndrome is one of the causes of <strong>delayed puberty</strong> in girls (not precocious puberty), where there is hypogonadotropic hypogonadism.</p>\n<p>Hypothalamic hamartoma, McCune–Albright syndrome and hypothyroidism are causes of precocious puberty.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<p>In prolonged hypothyroidism, adolescents typically have delayed puberty. Younger children might present with <strong>pseudo-</strong><strong>precocious puberty</strong>, characterized by breast development and vaginal bleeding in girls and macroorchidism in boys.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Elevated levels of TSH</strong> appear to interact with the FSH receptor (specificity spillover), thus <strong>inducing FSH-like </strong><strong>effects</strong> in the absence of LH effects on the gonads. The FSH-like effect suffices to <strong>induce estradiol secretion</strong> by the ovaries, whereas in males, <strong>testicular enlargement</strong> occurs without substantial testosterone secretion.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2721","text":"An incessantly crying 5-ye​ar-old girl is brought to‌ the OPD by her father. Y​ou observe that she needs‌ support to walk and her ​trunk is not symmetricall​y aligned. There is a his‌tory of recurrent fractur​es in her limbs. She also‌ has blood-stained vagina‌l discharge from a few da‍ys. On examination, the g‌irl appears as shown in t‍he image below. What is t​he most probable diagnosi​s?","raw_text":"An incessantly crying 5-year-old girl is brought to the OPD by her father. You observe that she needs support to walk and her trunk is not symmetrically aligned. There is a history of recurrent fractures in her limbs. She also has blood-stained vaginal discharge from a few days. On examination, the girl appears as shown in the image below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prader-Wil​li Syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reifenstei​n Syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"McCune-Albri​ght syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Polyostotic fibrous dyspla​sia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. McCune-Albright syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a4a01aca38c44ac19765f89dee0e27e1.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. McCune-Albright syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Precocious puberty</strong>(vaginal discharge), <strong>bony lesions</strong> (polyostotic fibrous dysplasia), and <strong>cafe au lait spots</strong>, as shown in the picture, are features of McCune–Albright syndrome. There is <strong>pain</strong> due to the multiple bony lesions and <strong>repeated fractures </strong>may result in incessant crying in the children and may require regular visits to the orthopaedics department. </p>\n<p>McCune–Albright syndrome is a classical cause of peripheral precocious puberty <span class=\"st\">caused by a mutation of <strong><em>GNAS1</em> gene</strong>. </span><span class=\"st\" data-hveid=\"122\" data-ved=\"0ahUKEwjNjvuczb7aAhUhR48KHfVTBmsQ4EUIejAN\">It affects the bones, skin, and several endocrine tissues.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"st\" data-hveid=\"122\" data-ved=\"0ahUKEwjNjvuczb7aAhUhR48KHfVTBmsQ4EUIejAN\">Fibrous dysplasia of multiple bones (polyostotic) represents a major cause of morbidity in this syndrome. The base of the skull and the proximal femurs are most commonly involved, but any bone can be affected. A CT scan is usually recommended even in absence of deformities. The prognosis is favourable for longevity, but deformities, <strong>repeated fractures</strong>, <strong>pain</strong>, and occasional cranial nerve compression may result from the bony lesions.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Cardinal features</strong>:<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Polyostotic fibrous dysplasia</li>\n<li><strong>Cafe au lait macules</strong></li>\n<li>Endocrine abnormalities: hyperthyroidism, acromegaly, adrenal adenomas, and <strong>autonomous ovarian hyperfunction, leading to precocious puberty</strong></li>\n<li>Predominantly affects girls</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2256","text":"A 15-year-old boy on exami​nation is found to have g‌enital development corres​ponding to Tanner stage 1‌ and his height is below ​the 3rd Percentile on gro​wth charting. What is the‌ most common cause for th​is condition?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old boy on examination is found to have genital development corresponding to Tanner stage 1 and his height is below the 3rd Percentile on growth charting. What is the most common cause for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kallman ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Klinefelte​r syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Constituti​onal delay","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Familial sh​ort stature","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Constitutional delay","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Constitutional delay</strong></p><p>Failure of development of pubertal features by the age of 14 years in male is suggestive of Delayed Puberty.</p>\n<p>The most common cause of delayed puberty in males and females is a constitutional delay in growth and puberty. Lack of pubertal changes in <strong>boys</strong> by the <strong>age of 14 years</strong> is suggestive of <strong>delayed puberty</strong>. Delayed puberty is more common in boys than in girls.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2725","text":"Breast and papillae appear​ as a small mound on exam‌ination of a young girl. ​If you are suspecting a c‌ase of delayed puberty, i​t is defined as a lack of​ development of secondary‌ sexual characters beyond​ the age of __________.","raw_text":"Breast and papillae appear as a small mound on examination of a young girl. If you are suspecting a case of delayed puberty, it is defined as a lack of development of secondary sexual characters beyond the age of __________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"15 y​ears","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"14 y​ears","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"13 y​ears","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"12 y​ears","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 13 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 13 years</strong></p><p>Delayed puberty in girls is defined as lack of <strong>secondary sexual characteristics</strong> by the age of <strong>13 years</strong> or <strong>absence of menarche</strong> by the age of <strong>16 years</strong> or within 3 years of pubertal onset.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2726","text":"A child presents with feat​ures of delayed puberty. ‌On investigating, he has ​decreased levels of LH, F‌SH, and testosterone. Whi​ch of the following is th​e most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A child presents with features of delayed puberty. On investigating, he has decreased levels of LH, FSH, and testosterone. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Klinefelter​'s syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kallman's​ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Androgen insensitivity syn​drome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Testicular​ infection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Kallman's syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/42825f2fc09e4b80a68272dfdb985d94x1280x1020.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Kallman&#x27;s syndrome</strong></p><p>This patient has low levels of luteinizing hormone (LH), follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH), and testosterone pointing towards hypogonadotropic hypogonadism and Kallman's syndrome is the only condition in the given choices causing hypogonadotropic hypogonadism.</p>\n<p>Causes of delayed puberty:</p>\n<p><strong>Hypogonadotropic</strong> <strong>hypogonadism</strong> (Secondary/central)</p>\n<p>Hypogonadism is due to the impaired secretion of gonadotropins (FSH and LH) by the pituitary gland and the resultant lack of sex steroid production</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Malnutrition, systemic diseases</li>\n<li>Kallmann (anosmia), Prader–Willi syndrome (mental retardation, obesity, low intelligence)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism</strong> (Primary gonadal failure)</p>\n<p><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">Hypogonadism is due to an impaired response of the gonads to the gonadotropins (FSH and LH), which in turn leads to a lack of sex steroid production and elevated gonadotropin levels (as an attempt of compensation by the body)<br/></span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sex chromosome anomalies (Turner's, Klinefelter's syndrome)</li>\n<li>Androgen insensitivity syndrome</li>\n<li>Ovotesticular dysgenesis</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2253","text":"All of the following are f​eatures of Laurence-Moon-‌Biedl syndrome except ___​______.","raw_text":"All of the following are features of Laurence-Moon-Biedl syndrome except _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obe​sity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mental re​tardation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Polyd​actyly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypot​ension","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypotension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypotension</strong></p><p><strong>Hypertension</strong> is seen in Laurence–Moon–Biedl syndrome.</p>\n<p>Laurence–Moon–Bardet–Biedl is an <strong>autosomal-recessive</strong> disorder of <strong>hypogonadotropic hypogonadism</strong>.</p>\n<p>Characteristic features:<cite></cite></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Polydactyly</li>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n<li><strong>Retinitis pigmentosa</strong></li>\n<li>Male hypogonadism</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li><strong>Mental retardation</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2252","text":"A female child presents wi​th bilateral inguinal mas‌ses, which were thought t​o be hernias but were fou‌nd to be testes in the in​guinal canals. Which kary​otype would you expect to‌ find in the child?","raw_text":"A female child presents with bilateral inguinal masses, which were thought to be hernias but were found to be testes in the inguinal canals. Which karyotype would you expect to find in the child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"46​,XX","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"46​,XY","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"47,​XXY","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"45​,XO","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 46,XY","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 46,XY</strong></p><p>Here, this baby girl with bilateral inguinal masses (testis) probably has androgen-insensitivity syndrome, where karyotype is 46,XY.</p>\n<p><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\"><strong>Androgen-insensitivity syndrome</strong> is an X-linked recessive disorder caused by mutations in the androgen receptor gene that makes XY fetuses insensitive to androgens. The patients phenotypically resemble females because </span><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">a part of the testosterone is converted to estrogen, which leads to the development of female external genitalia, normal development of breast, and female habitus.<br/></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">Patients with <strong>Klienfelter's syndrome</strong> have a karyotype of <strong>47, XXY</strong> and can have undescended testis, but they have <strong>male external genitalia</strong> where the phallus tends to be smaller than average.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">Characteristic features:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">Most common forms of male disorders of sexual differentiation (DSD).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\"><strong>46 XY male child with female phenotype</strong>, present with primary amenorrhoea.<br/></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">Normal male gonads, mostly undescended ones, can present as inguinal masses or hernia.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">Here, as testosterone receptors are defective, they do not act on tissues, leading to the <strong>absence of secondary sexual characters</strong> (absence of pubic hair and axillary hair).</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2730","text":"An infant was found to hav​e elevated levels of LH. ‌The baby has a 46, XY chr​omosome pattern on karyot‌yping and the vagina is s​hort and ends blindly in ​a pouch. Which of the fol‌lowing is false in the co​ndition suffered by the i‌nfant?","raw_text":"An infant was found to have elevated levels of LH. The baby has a 46, XY chromosome pattern on karyotyping and the vagina is short and ends blindly in a pouch. Which of the following is false in the condition suffered by the infant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Internal female genitalia ​is absent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Internal female genitalia ​is normal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Affected individuals have ​female phenotype","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High chance of testicular ​malignancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Internal female genitalia is normal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Internal female genitalia is normal</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An infant with a 46, XY chromosome pattern and female external genitalia are suggestive of <strong>Androgen-insensitivity syndrome(AIS)</strong> where there is <strong>absent internal female genitalia.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These patients have <strong>absent internal female genitalia</strong> with normal female external genitalia with the vagina ending blindly, in a pouch and normal male gonads in the form of <strong>undescended testis</strong> (mostly) which can present as<strong> inguinal masses</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">In a male child (46, XY), testicular hormones are resistant to receptors, and a </span><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">part of the testosterone is converted to estrogen leading to the development of female external genitalia and eventually normal development of breast, and female habitus.</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\"><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Features of Androgen insensitivity syndrome at different age groups-</span></span></p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\"><span class=\"fontstyle0\">In infants, <strong>elevated LH levels</strong> should suggest the diagnosis of AIS</span></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\"><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Prepubertal females with this disorder are often detected when<strong> inguinal masses</strong> which prove to be testes</span></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\"><span class=\"fontstyle0\">In older children and adults, </span><strong><span class=\"fontstyle2\">amenorrhea </span></strong><span class=\"fontstyle0\">is the usual presenting symptom.</span> <br/> </span></li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\"><strong>Note</strong>:</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Reifen-Stein syndrome:</strong> Males with various forms of ambiguous genitalia and undervirilization (hypogonadism, severe hypospadias, and gynecomastia). They are <strong>p</strong><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\"><strong>henotypically normal-appearing males</strong> with infertility.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2436","text":"A 31-year-old female deliv​ers a term baby. On exami‌nation, it has a micropen​is and unpalpable testis.‌ She gives a history of a​cne from 16 weeks of gest​ation which worsened thro‌ughout pregnancy and was ​treated after delivery. T‌he baby was diagnosed to ‌have female pseudohermaph‍roditism. Which of the fo‌llowing statements are ri‍ght about the given condi​tion?","raw_text":"A 31-year-old female delivers a term baby. On examination, it has a micropenis and unpalpable testis. She gives a history of acne from 16 weeks of gestation which worsened throughout pregnancy and was treated after delivery. The baby was diagnosed to have female pseudohermaphroditism. Which of the following statements are right about the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"XY genotype, female extern​al genitalia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Female external genitalia ​and male internal genital‌ia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"XX genotype, male external​ genitalia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"XY genotype, male external​ genitalia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. XX genotype, male external genitalia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. XX genotype, male external genitalia</strong></p><p><strong>Female</strong> pseudohermaphrodites are genectic females (<strong>XX</strong> genotype) with <strong>male external genitalia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Enzyme deficiencies: 21 hydroxylase, 11 hydroxylase, and 3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase (HSD) deficiencies</li>\n<li>Maternal androgen excess</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Male</strong> pseudohermaphrodites are genetic males (<strong>XY genotype</strong>) with <strong>female external genitalia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Enzyme deficiencies: 17 hydroxylase and 3-beta HSD deficiencies</li>\n<li>Defect in androgen action</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>True hermaphroditism/</strong>ovotesticular dysgenesis: An intersex condition in which an individual is born with ovarian and testicular tissue.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4471","text":"What is true about ovotest​icular disorders of sex d‌evelopment?","raw_text":"What is true about ovotesticular disorders of sex development?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Karyotype​ is 46 XY.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ovotestis is seen in the g​onadal biopsy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The response of testostero​ne to hCG is increased.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uterus is us​ually absent.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ovotestis is seen in the gonadal biopsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ovotestis is seen in the gonadal biopsy</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Ovotesticular</strong> disorder of sex development <strong>(DSD)</strong> is characterized by the presence of both ovarian and testicular tissue in the same individual. An <strong>ovotestis</strong> is present in approximately 2/3 of affected individuals.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Most affected individuals have a <strong>46, XX</strong> chromosomal karyotype, which normally results in female sexual development. In about 10% of patients, testicular tissue in an individual with a 46, XX karyotype is present as a result of a translocation of the <strong><em>SRY</em> gene</strong> on the Y chromosome to the X chromosome or another chromosome. There is a defect in<strong> R-spondin1</strong> protein <span class=\"fontstyle0\">encoded by the </span><strong><span class=\"fontstyle2\">RSPO1 </span></strong><span class=\"fontstyle0\"><strong>gene</strong>, described in 46, XX ovotesticular DSD</span>. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">An abnormal <strong>vagina</strong> and an underdeveloped <strong>uterus</strong> are usually present. The testes are usually <strong>undescended,</strong> and the penis may present with <strong>hypospadias</strong>. Upon reaching puberty, breast development, feminization, and menstruation may occur. Most affected individuals are infertile, but ovulation or spermatogenesis is possible. The response of testosterone to hCG is normal or reduced.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0833","text":"A 3-year-old girl was brou​ght by her mother with co‌mplaints of enlarged clit​oris. Karyotype revealed ‌46XX. What is the likely ​cause?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old girl was brought by her mother with complaints of enlarged clitoris. Karyotype revealed 46XX. What is the likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"21 hydroxyla​se deficiency","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"11 hydroxyla​se deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 beta dehydrogenase defic​iency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 3 lyase​ deficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 21 hydroxylase deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 21 hydroxylase deficiency</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a girl (46XX) with an <strong>enlarged clitoris</strong> is highly suggestive of <strong>congenital adrenal hyperplasia</strong> (CAH) due to <strong>21-hydroxylase deficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Congenital adrenal hyperplasia</strong>/a<strong>drenogenital syndrome</strong> is a family of <strong>autosomal-recessiv</strong>e disorders caused by the deficiency of enzymes involved in the synthesis of steroid hormones in the adrenal cortex. The majority of cases of CAH are caused by <strong>21-hydroxylase deficiency</strong>. This deficiency leads to a lack of synthesis of cortisol and aldosterone, resulting in the salt-wasting form of the disease, which is the most severe form. </p>\n<p><strong>Severe 21-hydroxylase deficiency </strong>presents with symptoms including<strong> weight loss, dehydration, weakness, hypotension, and electrolyte imbalances. </strong>Accumulated steroid precursors like 17-hydroxyprogesterone, are shunted into the pathway of androgen synthesis leading to <strong>elevated levels of androstenedione</strong> and <strong>testosterone</strong>. This can result in <strong>abnormal genital development</strong> in affected<strong> female</strong> fetuses during early gestation. </p>\n<p>Females may have masculinized external genitalia. This can include <strong>enlargement of the clitoris</strong>, partial or complete <strong>labial fusion</strong>, and a shared opening of the vagina and urethra (urogenital sinus). </p>\n<p>Untreated individuals develop signs of androgen excess after birth.  These signs include <strong>accelerated skeletal maturation</strong>,<strong> premature epiphyseal closure</strong> leading to stunted adult stature, excessive muscular development, the appearance of pubic and axillary hair, acne, and deepening of the voice. In males, enlargement of the penis, scrotum, and prostate. In females, the clitoris may become further enlarged. </p>\n<p>Diagnosis of confirmed by <strong>elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone levels</strong>. Glucocorticoid replacement with hydrocortisone is done. In a salt-wasting crisis, fludrocortisone supplementation is given. Vaginoplasty and clitoral recession are done for females with ambiguous genitalia. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"7shpwuFO","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"33_myelodysplastic_syndrome__myeloproliferative_syndromes_and_histiocytosis","title":"33.Myelodysplastic Syndrome, Myeloproliferative Syndromes And Histiocytosis","num_questions":29,"total_marks":116,"duration":39,"time_per_question":80.69,"questions":[{"id":"MC0240","text":"A 73-year-old woman presen‍ts with chronic fatigue a​nd easy bruising. She has‍ had surgery for ovarian ‌cancer 7 years ago along ‍with chemoradiation. Her ​lab reports reveal pancyt‌openia and bone marrow bi‍opsy shows the following ‍finding. What is the most​ probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 73-year-old woman presents with chronic fatigue and easy bruising. She has had surgery for ovarian cancer 7 years ago along with chemoradiation. Her lab reports reveal pancytopenia and bone marrow biopsy shows the following finding. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aplasti‍c anemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myelodysplas‍tic syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acute lymphoblastic leukem‍ia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myelophthi‍sic anemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Myelodysplastic syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a25775b4073c4f32abbddcd1957a1ad2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/346ee94f46484e2283e80f30c925710fx798x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Myelodysplastic syndrome</strong></p><p>The<strong> </strong>given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>therapy-related myelodysplastic syndrome</strong> (t-MDS).</p>\n<p>The bone marrow biopsy stained with Perl's Prussian blue shows <strong>ringed sideroblasts. </strong>They are<strong> </strong>erythroblasts with <strong>iron-laden mitochondria</strong> visible as perinuclear granules as shown in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Myelodysplastic syndrome is a group of <strong>clonal stem cell disorders</strong> characterized by <strong>maturation defects, </strong>associated with ineffective hematopoiesis.</p>\n<p>It is of two <strong>types</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary MDS - develops slowly, usually after 50 years of age.</li>\n<li>Secondary or therapy-related MDS (t-MDS) -<strong> </strong>develops 2 to 8 years after toxic drug or radiation exposure with poor prognosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients usually present with weakness, infections and hemorrhages due to pancytopenia. Both types of MDS have a high risk of <strong>transformation</strong> to acute myeloid leukemia (<strong>AML</strong>). However, most frequent and rapid transformation occurs in <strong>therapy-related MDS</strong> (t-MDS).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5944","text":"What is the most common cy‍togenetic change in child​ren with myelodysplastic ‍syndrome?","raw_text":"What is the most common cytogenetic change in children with myelodysplastic syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5q de‍letion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Monos‍omy 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7q de‍letion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Monos‍omy 7","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Monosomy 7","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Monosomy 7</strong></p><p><strong>Monosomy 7 </strong>is the most common cytogenetic change seen in myelodysplastic syndrome (MDS) in<strong> children.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>5q deletion </strong>is the most common cytogenetic change seen in MDS in <strong>adults</strong>.</p>\n<p>The del(5q) abnormality is the dominant clone in most cases of MDS. This involves the stem-cell compartment and hence occurs as an early or initiating event in disease pathogenesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5943","text":"Which of these histopathol‍ogical findings would you​ not expect to see in pat‍ients with myelodysplasti‌c syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of these histopathological findings would you not expect to see in patients with myelodysplastic syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ringed si‍deroblast","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pawn-ball m‍egakaryocyte","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dohle ‍bodies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pelger-H‍uet cells","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pelger-Huet cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pelger-Huet cells</strong></p><p><strong>Pseudo–Pelger–Huet cells</strong> are seen in <strong>myelodysplastic syndrome</strong> (MDS)<strong> </strong>and not Pelger<strong>–</strong>Huet cells.</p>\n<p>Pelger–Huet cells are seen in<strong> Pelger–Huet syndrome</strong>, which is due to mutations in the <strong>lamin B-receptor</strong><strong> gene.</strong> It is a neutrophil differentiation disorder with leukocyte appearance as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dumbbell-shaped bilobed</strong> nuclei</li>\n<li>Reduced number of nuclear segments</li>\n<li>Coarse clumping of the nuclear chromatin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In <strong>MDS</strong>, pseudo-Pelger Huet cells are seen due to <strong>abnormal nuclear budding</strong> i.e. neutrophils with only 2 nuclear lobes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5945","text":"Which of the following bon‍e marrow and peripheral b​lood findings is not seen‍ in patients with myelody‌splastic syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following bone marrow and peripheral blood findings is not seen in patients with myelodysplastic syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ima‍ge 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ima‍ge 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ima‍ge 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ima‍ge 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Image 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4b596660e31f4986aca23ef3a02ba206.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6d9304056c604d0da59e14bf27ad3dd1x1280x1545.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Image 3</strong></p><p>Image 3 showing hypocellular bone marrow is not seen in <strong>myelodysplastic syndrome</strong> (MDS.) In this condition, bone marrow is<strong> hypercellular.</strong> However, <strong>pancytopenia</strong> results from ineffective hematopoiesis.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the histopathological findings seen in MDS.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5947","text":"A 60-year-old man presents‍ with complaints of fatig​ue, weight loss, and heav‍iness in the left hypocho‌ndrium for 6 months. His ‍hemogram and peripheral s​mear are given below. Wha‌t is the most likely diag‍nosis? Hb - 10 gm/dL TLC ‍- 5.5 lakhs/mm3 Platelet ​count - 4 lakhs/mm3","raw_text":"<p>A 60-year-old man presents with complaints of fatigue, weight loss, and heaviness in the left hypochondrium for 6 months. His hemogram and peripheral smear are given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Hb - 10 gm/dL</p>\n<p>TLC - 5.5 lakhs/mm3</p>\n<p>Platelet count - 4 lakhs/mm3</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic lymphocytic leukem‍ia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic myel‍oid leukemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acute lymphoblastic leukem‍ia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute myelo‍id leukemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chronic myeloid leukemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8d5eaf70693448a29859e5222aafaa7a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a1e04758334842edbcaa3e45942ffc6dx1280x1117.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chronic myeloid leukemia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>chronic myelogenous leukemia </strong>(CML). </p>\n<p>In this condition, the <strong>peripheral smear</strong> contains <strong>neutrophils</strong> and their <strong>precursors</strong> of <strong>various sizes</strong> and configurations like band cells, myelocytes, metamyelocytes, and blasts. Hence, the histological picture is described to have <strong>garden party </strong>or<strong> college girl</strong> appearance.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows the peripheral smear in CML showing garden party or college girl appearance.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>CML is a myeloproliferative disorder with peak incidence in the 5th to 6th decades of life. It occurs due to the chimeric BCR-ABL gene.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features</strong> of CML:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypermetabolism</li>\n<li>Fatigability</li>\n<li>Weakness</li>\n<li>Weight loss and anorexia</li>\n<li>Extensive extramedullary hematopoiesis causing -\n<ul>\n<li>Splenomegaly</li>\n<li>Mild hepatomegaly</li>\n<li>Lymphadenopathy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hematological findings</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mild-to-moderate anemia </li>\n<li>Leukocytosis</li>\n<li>Platelets - usually increased</li>\n<li>DLC shows predominantly neutrophils, band forms, metamyelocytes, myelocytes, eosinophils, and basophils and blasts (&gt;10%) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Convent girl appearance is seen in CLL.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0244","text":"What mutation results in P‍hiladelphia chromosome?","raw_text":"What mutation results in Philadelphia chromosome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"t(9;22)(‍q11;q34)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"t(22;9)(‍q11;q34)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"t(22;9)(‍q34;q11)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"t(9;22)(‍q34;q11)","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. t(9;22)(q34;q11)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59e76b8423e64e0d9c3ded3140dd6b99x1280x2541.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/75b99376f9834259a64754975241eae7x604x598.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. t(9;22)(q34;q11)</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 635\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Philadelphia chromosome</strong> results from <strong>t(9;22)(q34;q11</strong>). It is most commonly seen in chronic myeloid leukemia (<strong>CML</strong>)<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>CML is due to the formation of a chimeric <strong>BCR-­ABL </strong>gene or Philadelphia chromosome. This gene is derived from a <strong>reciprocal</strong> <strong>translocation </strong>(9;22)(q34;q11). </p>\n<p>The image given below shows the formation of the Philadelphia chromosome.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>This translocation can be identified on fluorescence in situ hybridisation (FISH).</p>\n<p>The image given below shows<strong> </strong>(9;22) translocation as seen on FISH.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5948","text":"Bone marrow biopsy in a pa‍tient shows pseudo-Gauche​r cells. What is the most‍ probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"Bone marrow biopsy in a patient shows pseudo-Gaucher cells. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary my‍elofibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic myel‍oid leukemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Essential th‍rombocythemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic Lymphocytic leukem‍ia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chronic myeloid leukemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0d2d7dd368714ca6b08e3fd2fdf81551x436x356.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chronic myeloid leukemia</strong></p><p><strong>Psuedo-Gaucher</strong> cells are characteristic of chronic myeloid leukemia (<strong>CML</strong>).  These cells are scattered macrophages with abundant wrinkled, green-blue cytoplasm. Their presence indicates a good prognosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Bone marrow </strong>findings in <strong>CML: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Markedly hypercellular marrow - increased maturing granulocytic precursors</li>\n<li>Elevated eosinophils and basophils</li>\n<li>Increased megakaryocytes - include small, dysplastic forms</li>\n<li>Erythroid progenitors - normal or mildly decreased</li>\n<li>Increased reticulin deposition</li>\n<li>Overt marrow fibrosis - rare early in the course</li>\n<li>Pseudo-Gaucher cells </li>\n<li>Sea blue histiocytes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows pseudo-Gaucher cells in chronic myeloid leukemia (CML).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5949","text":"A 60-year-old woman with f‍ever and splenomegaly was​ diagnosed to have chroni‍c myeloid leukemia. She w‌as started on imatinib th‍erapy. After a few months​ of therapy, she had pers‌istent progressive spleno‍megaly and fatigue. Which‍ of the following will no​t be seen in this phase?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman with fever and splenomegaly was diagnosed to have chronic myeloid leukemia. She was started on imatinib therapy. After a few months of therapy, she had persistent progressive splenomegaly and fatigue. Which of the following will not be seen in this phase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt;20% blasts in blood or‍ marrow","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt; 20% basophils in bloo‍d","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Platelet count of &amp;lt;100 ‍× 10^9/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"WBC count &amp;g‍t;10 × 10^9/L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. >20% blasts in blood or marrow","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. &gt;20% blasts in blood or marrow</strong></p><p>In a patient with chronic myeloid leukemia (<strong>CML</strong>), progressive splenomegaly, not responsive to therapy is suggestive of an <strong>accelerated phase</strong>. In this phase, <strong>blasts</strong> are <strong>&lt;20%</strong> in blood or marrow. </p>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis</strong> of an accelerated phase of CML requires one of the following <strong>criteria:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Persistent or increasing WBC (&gt;10 × 10<sup>9</sup>/L), unresponsive to therapy</li>\n<li>Persistent or increasing splenomegaly, unresponsive to therapy</li>\n<li>Persistent thrombocytosis (&gt;1000 × 10<sup>9</sup>/L), unresponsive to therapy</li>\n<li>Persistent thrombocytopenia (&lt;100 × 10<sup>9</sup>/L) unrelated to therapy</li>\n<li>20% or more basophils in the peripheral blood</li>\n<li>10%-19% blasts in the peripheral blood or bone marrow</li>\n<li>Additional clonal chromosomal abnormalities in Ph<sup>+</sup> cells at diagnosis that include “major route” abnormalities (second Ph, trisomy 8, isochromosome 17q, trisomy 19), complex karyotype, or abnormalities of 3q26.2</li>\n<li>Any new clonal chromosomal abnormality in Ph<sup>+</sup> cells that occurs during therapy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5950","text":"What is the percentage of ‍blast cells in the blast ​phase of CML?","raw_text":"What is the percentage of blast cells in the blast phase of CML?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt‍;10%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10-‍15%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt‍;20%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"15-‍20%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. >20%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. &gt;20%</strong></p><p>In the <strong>blast phase</strong> of chronic myeloid leukemia, there are <strong>&gt;20% </strong>blasts in the bone marrow.</p>\n<p>The 3 <strong>phases</strong> of <strong>CML</strong> are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Chronic</strong> phase is the most common presentation. It is characterized by:\n<ul>\n<li>Leukocytosis - bimodal distribution with higher proportions of mature segmented neutrophils and myelocytes</li>\n<li>Blast cells &lt;10%</li>\n<li>Increased basophils and eosinophils</li>\n<li>Monocytosis &lt;3%</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Accelerated</strong> phase occurs in 50% of the patients who present with increasing anemia, thrombocytopenia and splenomegaly. It is characterized by:\n<ul>\n<li>Blast count 10-19%</li>\n<li>Basophilia &gt;20%</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Blast</strong> phase in CML occurs in an average of 3 years after the diagnosis. It is characterized by:\n<ul>\n<li>Large foci or clusters of blasts on the bone marrow biopsy &gt;20%.</li>\n<li>Presence of extramedullary blastic infiltrates (e.g., myeloid sarcoma or chloroma)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0257","text":"Which of the following is ‍not seen in a patient wit​h Myelodysplastic/Myelopr‍oliferative syndrome (MDS‌/MPN) with neutrophilia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in a patient with Myelodysplastic/Myeloproliferative syndrome (MDS/MPN) with neutrophilia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Philadelphia chromosome ne‍gative","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basophili‍a &amp;gt;10%","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Blasts ‍&amp;lt;20%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neutrophil precursors &amp;gt;‍10%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Basophilia >10%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Basophilia &gt;10%</strong></p><p>Myelodysplastic/Myeloproliferative syndrome (MDS/MPN) with neutrophilia (previously named as atypical chronic myeloid leukemia) shows minimal absolute <strong>basophilia</strong>, i.e., <strong>&lt;2%</strong> of total WBCs.</p>\n<p>Myelodysplastic/Myeloproliferative syndrome (MDS/MPN) with neutrophilia is a leukemic disorder having features of both myelodysplastic and myeloproliferative features at the time of diagnosis. It is classified under the <strong>WHO 5th edition (2022) MDS/MPN</strong> category.</p>\n<p><strong>Characteristics </strong>of myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative syndrome (MDS/MPN) with neutrophilia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Peripheral blood leukocytosis with an increased number of mature and immature neutrophils</li>\n<li>Prominent dysgranulopoiesis</li>\n<li><strong>Philadelphia chromosome-negative</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Neutrophil</strong> <strong>precursors</strong> (promyelocytes, myelocytes, metamyelocytes)<strong> &gt;10%</strong> of WBCs</li>\n<li>Minimal absolute <strong>basophilia &lt;2%</strong> of WBCs</li>\n<li>Absolute monocytosis &lt;10% of WBCs</li>\n<li>Hypercellular bone marrow</li>\n<li><strong>&lt;20% blasts</strong> in blood and bone marrow</li>\n<li>Thrombocytopenia</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Differences</strong> between CML and myelodysplastic/myeloproliferative syndrome (MDS/MPN) with neutrophilia:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 265px;\" width=\"517\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\"><strong>Feature</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\"><strong>CML</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">Myelodysplastic/Myeloproliferative syndrome (MDS/MPN) with neutrophilia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">BCR-ABL gene </td>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">Basophilia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">&lt;2%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">\n<p>Myeloblasts in peripheral blood</p>\n<p>Myeloblasts in bone marrow</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">\n<p>&lt;2%</p>\n<p>&lt;5%</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">\n<p>&lt;20%</p>\n<p>&lt;20%</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">Granulocytic dysplasia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">Minimal/ absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">Prominent</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5953","text":"Which of the following pat‍ients will have a decreas​ed leukocyte alkaline pho‍sphatase score?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients will have a decreased leukocyte alkaline phosphatase score?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 65-year-old-man with gar‍den party appearance on p​eripheral smear","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A 50-year-old man with Ree‍d Sternberg cells on LN b​iopsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 35-year-old woman with H‍b level of 23 g/dL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 43-year-old woman with s‍mudge cells on peripheral​ smear","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A 65-year-old-man with garden party appearance on peripheral smear","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A 65-year-old-man with garden party appearance on peripheral smear</strong></p><p>Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (<strong>LAP</strong>) score is <strong>decreased</strong> in chronic myeloid leukemia (<strong>CML</strong>) i.e. garden party appearance on peripheral smear.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0253","text":"Which of the following doe‍s not cause relative poly​cythemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not cause relative polycythemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dehyd‍ration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dengue hemor‍rhagic fever","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gaisbock‍ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High a‍ltitude","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. High altitude","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. High altitude</strong></p><p>High altitude produces absolute polycythemia and not relative polycythemia.</p>\n<p><strong>Polycythemia</strong> denotes an abnormally high red cell count, usually with a corresponding increase in the hemoglobin level (&gt; 16 ±2 g/dL). It is <strong>2 types</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Relative - hemoconcentration due to decreased plasma volume</li>\n<li>Absolute - increase in the total red cell mass</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Causes</strong> of <strong>relative</strong> polycythemia include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dehydration - deprivation of water, prolonged vomiting or diarrhea, excessive use of diuretics</li>\n<li>Gaisböck syndrome - stress polycythemia where affected individuals are usually hypertensive, obese, and anxious</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Causes</strong> of <strong>absolute</strong> polycythemia include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary - intrinsic abnormality of hematopoietic precursors \n<ul>\n<li>Polycythemia vera</li>\n<li>Inherited erythropoietin receptor mutations</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Secondary - red cell progenitors responding to increased levels of erythropoietin\n<ul>\n<li>Compensatory due to cyanotic heart and lung disease, high altitude</li>\n<li>Paraneoplastic erythropoietin secreting tumours</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: In dengue hemorrhagic fever, increased vascular permeability causes plasma leakage and volume loss. Hence, relative polycythemia is seen.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9278","text":"A young boy from high alti‍tude came with fever and ​weakness and had the foll‍owing reports - Hemoglobi‌n 17 g%, TLC is 21,000 wi‍th neutrophils 25, lympho​cytes 30, eosinophils 5, ‌myelocytes and metamyeloc‍ytes 40 in peripheral sme‍ar. Next step in the inve​stigation should be:","raw_text":"A young boy from high altitude came with fever and weakness and had the following reports - Hemoglobin 17 g%, TLC is 21,000 with neutrophils 25, lymphocytes 30, eosinophils 5, myelocytes and metamyelocytes 40 in peripheral smear. Next step in the investigation should be:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Philadelphi‍a chromosome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"JAK mu‍tation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erythropoi‍etin levels","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bone Marrow biopsy with re‍ticulin stain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Erythropoietin levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Erythropoietin levels</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>polycythemia</strong> (Hb&gt;16 g/dL). Hence, <strong>erythropoietin levels</strong> (EPO) are estimated next, to differentiate between <strong>primary</strong> or <strong>secondary</strong> polycythemia. Based on EPO levels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary polycythemia - normal/ decreased</li>\n<li>Secondary polycythemia - increased</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options A and B: JAK 2 mutation and Philadelphia chromosome analysis is only done as a last resort.</p>\n<p>Option D: Bone marrow estimation with reticulin is done for myelofibrosis which can be seen in the spent/ late stage of the disease. However, peripheral smear findings are not suggestive of primary myelofibrosis. Leukoerythroblastic reaction with dacrocytes is seen in myelofibrosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5955","text":"A 24-year-old woman who pr‍esented with aquagenic pr​uritis had a Hb level of ‍20 g/dL. Her lab investig‌ations are given below. W‍hich of the following is ​not seen in this conditio‌n? Hb - 19 g/dL Hematocri‍t - 52% S. erythropoietin‍ - decreased JAK2 mutatio​n - positive","raw_text":"<p>A 24-year-old woman who presented with aquagenic pruritis had a Hb level of 20 g/dL. Her lab investigations are given below. Which of the following is not seen in this condition?</p>\n<p>Hb - 19 g/dL</p>\n<p>Hematocrit - 52%</p>\n<p>S. erythropoietin - decreased</p>\n<p>JAK2 mutation - positive</p>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute meylo‍id leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Budd-Chiar‍i syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myelof‍ibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatic‍ failure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hepatic failure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hepatic failure</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>polycythemia vera</strong>. Hepatic failure is not seen in this condition.</p>\n<p>Polycythemia vera is strongly associated with activating point <strong>mutations</strong> in the tyrosine kinase <strong>JAK2.</strong> In this condition, clonal erythrocytosis occurs autonomously i.e. independent of erythropoietin.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features</strong> of polycythemia vera:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Headache</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Dizziness</li>\n<li>Intense pruritis, <strong>aquagenic pruritis</strong></li>\n<li>Peptic ulcer</li>\n<li>Venous thrombosis - <strong>Budd-Chiari syndrome</strong> (hepatic vein thrombosis), bowel infarction, stroke, myocardial infarction</li>\n<li>Minor hemorrhages - epistaxis, gum bleeding</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Laboratory findings</strong> in polycythemia vera:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Panmyelosis - increased marrow production of red cells, granulocytes, and platelets</li>\n<li><strong>Low erythropoietin </strong></li>\n<li>Elevated hematocrit</li>\n<li>Hyperuricemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> of polycythemia vera:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Myelofibrosis</strong> and extensive extramedullary hematopoiesis - spent /late phase</li>\n<li><strong>Acute myeloid leukemia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5956","text":"A 63-year-old woman compla‍ins about throbbing pain ​over both palms. Her exam‍ination findings and lab ‌reports are given below. ‍What is the most likely d​iagnosis? Hb - 11 g/dL WB‌C - 9,000 /mm 3 Platelet ‍count - 7,00,000 /μL Bone‍ marrow biopsy - hypercel​lular with megakaryocytes​ and reticulin fibrils Ph​iladelphia chromosome - a​bsent MPL mutation - posi‌tive","raw_text":"<p>A 63-year-old woman complains about throbbing pain over both palms. Her examination findings and lab reports are given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Hb - 11 g/dL</p>\n<p>WBC - 9,000 /mm<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p>Platelet count - 7,00,000 /μL</p>\n<p>Bone marrow biopsy - hypercellular with megakaryocytes and reticulin fibrils</p>\n<p>Philadelphia chromosome - absent</p>\n<p>MPL mutation - positive</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polycyth‍emia vera","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Essential th‍rombocythemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Myelof‍ibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leukemoid‍ reaction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Essential thrombocythemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/68bf629723124f318c01b3ab2614be1d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Essential thrombocythemia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>essential thrombocythemia</strong> (ET). The image showing erythematous hands is suggestive of <strong>erythromelalgia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Essential thrombocythemia (essential thrombocytosis) is a myeloproliferative neoplasm. It is associated with mutations that increase JAK-STAT signaling.</p>\n<p><strong>WHO criteria</strong> for diagnosis of essential thrombocythemia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Major criteria -\n<ul>\n<li>Platelet count ≥450 × 10<sup>9</sup>/L</li>\n<li>Bone marrow biopsy showing proliferation mainly of the megakaryocyte lineage with increased numbers of enlarged, mature megakaryocytes with hyperlobulated nuclei</li>\n<li>Not meeting WHO criteria for BCR-ABL1 chronic myeloid leukemia, PV, PMF, myelodysplastic syndromes, or other myeloid neoplasms</li>\n<li>Presence of JAK2, CALR, or MPL mutation</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Minor criteria - the presence of a clonal marker or absence of evidence for reactive thrombocytosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis</strong> of ET requires meeting all <strong>4 major</strong> criteria <strong>or</strong> the first <strong>3 major</strong> criteria and the <strong>minor</strong> criterion</p>\n<p>Option A: Polycythemia vera can also present with erythromelalgia. However, it also presents with <strong>increased Hb</strong> levels. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Fibrosis</strong> (not reticulin fibrils) is seen in bone marrow biopsy of primary myelofibrosis. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>WBC counts</strong> are <strong>increased</strong> in leukemoid reactions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5958","text":"Patients with which of the‍ following conditions do ​not have a leukoerythrobl‍astic picture on peripher‌al smear?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions do not have a leukoerythroblastic picture on peripheral smear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myelof‍ibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myelophthi‍sic anemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thala‍ssemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gaucher‍ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thalassemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e1437ea4c10340708c6a4b076d29433cx1280x960.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thalassemia</strong></p><p>Leukoerythroblastic picture is not seen in patients with thalassemia as it is a hemoglobinopathy and does not involve myeloid precursors.</p>\n<p>Abnormal presence of <strong>immature</strong> <strong>precursors</strong> of blood in the peripheral blood smear is called <strong>leukoerythroblastosis. </strong>Findings include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nucleated red cells</li>\n<li>Dacryocytes (or teardrop RBC) - formed due to damage to red cell membrane caused by fibrous tissue in the marrow</li>\n<li>Immature cells of granulocytic series</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It occurs due to conditions causing <strong>marrow replacement</strong> like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary myelofibrosis - fibrosis of bone marrow</li>\n<li>Myelophthisic anemia - space occupying lesions of bone marrow</li>\n<li>Gauchers disease</li>\n<li>Spent phase of polycythemia vera</li>\n</ul>\n<p> The image given below shows a leukoerythroblastic blood picture with nucleated RBCs, tear-drop cells, and immature myeloid cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0255","text":"Which of the following is ‍not included under MDS/MP​N overlap syndromes?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included under MDS/MPN overlap syndromes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MDS/MPN with‍ neutrophilia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic myelomonocytic leu‍kemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MDS/MPN with SF31B mutatio‍n and thrombocytosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MDS with ex‍cess blasts","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. MDS with excess blasts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. MDS with excess blasts</strong></p><p>Myelodysplastic syndrome/ myeloproliferative neoplasm (<strong>MDS/MPN</strong>) <strong>overlap </strong>syndromes are a group of chronic clonal myeloid malignancies in which there are features of both MDS and MPN at the time of presentation. According to the 5th edition WHO classification (2022), it includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Chronic myelomonocytic leukemia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">MDS/MPN with neutrophilia (previously atypical chronic myeloid leukemia)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">MDS/MPN with SF31B mutation and thrombocytosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">MDS/MPN not otherwise specified</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5959","text":"Which of the following is ‍not compatible with a dia​gnosis of chronic myelomo‍nocytic leukemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not compatible with a diagnosis of chronic myelomonocytic leukemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peripheral blood monocytos‍is more than 1×10^9/L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Absence of Philadelphia ch‍romosome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"More than 20% blasts in bl‍ood or bone marrow","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Absent or minimal dysplasi‍a in myeloid lineages","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. More than 20% blasts in blood or bone marrow","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. More than 20% blasts in blood or bone marrow</strong></p><p>More than 20% of blasts in the blood or bone marrow are not compatible with a diagnosis of chronic myelomonocytic leukemia (<strong>CMML</strong>). In this condition, <strong>blasts</strong> are <strong>less than 20%</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>WHO 5th edition diagnostic criteria</strong> (2022_for chronic myelomonocytic leukemia (CMML) include:</p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisite criteria:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">1. Persistent absolute (<strong>≥0.5 × 10<sup>9</sup>/ L</strong>) and relative (<strong>≥10%</strong>) peripheral blood monocytosis.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">2. Blasts constitute <strong>&lt;20%</strong> of the cells in the peripheral blood and bone marrow.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">3. Not meeting diagnostic criteria of chronic myeloid leukemia or other myeloproliferative neoplasms (MPN).<sup>b</sup></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">4. Not meeting diagnostic criteria of myeloid/lymphoid neoplasms with tyrosine kinase fusions.<sup>c</sup></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Supporting criteria:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">1. Dysplasia involving ≥1 myeloid lineage.<sup>d</sup></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">2. Acquired clonal cytogenetic or molecular abnormality.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">3. Abnormal partitioning of peripheral blood monocyte subsets.<sup>e</sup></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Pre-requisite criteria must be present in all cases:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If monocytosis is ≥ 1 × 10<sup>9</sup>/ L: one or more supporting criteria must be met.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If monocytosis is ≥0.5 and &lt;1 × 10<sup>9</sup>/ L: supporting criteria 1 and 2 must be met.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Subtype criteria:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Myelodysplastic CMML (MD-CMML): WBC &lt; 13 × 10<sup>9</sup>/L</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Myeloproliferative CMML (MP-CMML): WBC ≥ 13 × 10<sup>9</sup>/L</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 7\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">a. Blasts and blast equivalents include myeloblasts, monoblasts, and promonocytes.</span><br/><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"> b. MPN with monocytosis can mimic CMML. In these instances, a documented history of MPN excludes CMML. The presence of MPN features in the bone marrow and/or a high burden of MPN-associated mutations (JAK2, CALR, or MPL) tends to support MPN with monocytosis rather than CMML.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">c. Criteria for myeloid/lymphoid neoplasms with tyrosine kinase fusions should be specifically excluded in cases with eosinophilia.</span><br/><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"> d. Morphologic dysplasia should be present in ≥10% of cells of a hematopoietic lineage in the bone marrow.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">e. Based on the detection of increased classical monocytes (&gt;94%) in the absence of known active autoimmune diseases and/or systemic inflammatory syndromes.</span></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5951","text":"A 2-year-old boy with Noon‍an syndrome presents with​ hepatosplenomegaly and l‍ymphadenopathy. You are s‌uspecting juvenile myelom‍onocytic leukemia. Which ​of the following is not s‌een in this condition?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old boy with Noonan syndrome presents with hepatosplenomegaly and lymphadenopathy. You are suspecting juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia. Which of the following is not seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GM-CSF hype‍rsensitivity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Absence of Philadelphia ch‍romosome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Poor pr‍ognosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased ‍Hb A2 level","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Increased Hb A2 level","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Increased Hb A2 level</strong></p><p><strong>HbF</strong> levels are <strong>increased</strong> in <strong>juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia</strong> (JMML), not Hb A2. </p>\n<p>JMML is a hematopoietic stem cell-derived myeloproliferative neoplasm of early childhood. It is one of the myeloproliferative neoplasms. It is associated with mutations in the RAS pathway. Neurofibromatosis 1, Noonan syndrome, and trisomy 8 mosaicism can predispose to this condition. Aggressive types include PTPN11 mutation and neurofibromatosis type 1.</p>\n<p><strong>WHO diagnostic guidelines </strong>of JMML:</p>\n<table border=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Category I (Mandatory):</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical and hematologic features</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Category II: </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Genetic (only 1 required)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td><strong>Category III: Category I + these findings:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Splenomegaly</li>\n<li>Peripheral blood monocyte count ≥1 × 10<sup>9</sup>/L</li>\n<li>Blast percentage in peripheral blood and bone marrow &lt;20%</li>\n<li>No Philadelphia chromosome (BCR/ABL1 rearrangement)</li>\n<li>Exclusion of KMT2A mutation</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Somatic mutation in <em>PTPN11</em>* or <em>KRAS</em>* or <em>NRAS</em>*</li>\n<li>Clinical diagnosis of Neurofibromatosis type 1 or NF1 mutation</li>\n<li>Germline CBL mutation</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hemoglobin F increased for age</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Myeloid or erythroid precursors on the peripheral blood smear</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypersensitivity to GM-CSF by colony assay and STAT5 hyperphosphorylation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Thrombocytopenia with hypercellular bone marrow</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>*Germline mutation (indicating Noonan syndrome) needs to be excluded.</p>\n<p>It is associated with a<strong> poor prognosis</strong> and only a few patients undergo spontaneous regression.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6712","text":"JAK2 mutation is most comm‍only associated with ____​_","raw_text":"JAK2 mutation is most commonly associated with _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polycyth‍emia vera","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Essential th‍rombocytosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‍ML","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"P‍MF","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Polycythemia vera","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Polycythemia vera</strong></p><p><strong>Polycythemia vera </strong>is strongly associated with activating point <strong>mutations</strong> in the tyrosine kinase<strong> JAK2</strong>.</p>\n<p>A mutation in JAK2, a key member of the cytokine intracellular signaling pathway, can be found in <strong>90–95%</strong> of patients with polycythemia vera.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased marrow production of red cells, granulocytes, and platelets <strong>(panmyelosis)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Low </strong><strong>erythropoietin levels and elevated hematocrit</strong></li>\n<li>Blood viscosity and sludging lead to both thrombosis and bleeding.</li>\n<li>Headache, dizziness, hypertension, dizziness, venous thrombosis (Budd Chiari syndrome due to hepatic vein thrombosis), bowel infarction, and stroke.</li>\n<li><strong>Intense pruritus especially aquagenic pruritus</strong> </li>\n<li>Peptic ulceration, hyperuricemia</li>\n<li>15% to 20% of patients after an average period of 10 years -&gt;Myelofibrosis and extensive extramedullary hematopoiesis,</li>\n<li>PCV transforms into AML in 2% of patients.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Primary myelofibrosis</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Marrow fibrosis associated with extramedullary hemopoiesis in the spleen.</li>\n<li><strong>Activating mutation in JAK2 </strong><strong>(more commonly) or <em>MPL</em>.</strong></li>\n<li>Presence of neoplastic megakaryocytes, which are responsible for the release of fibrogenic factors like PDGF (platelet-derived growth factor) and TGF-β.</li>\n<li>Bone <strong>marrow aspiration </strong><strong>yields dry tap </strong>due to fibrosis of the bone marrow.</li>\n<li>So, <strong>bone marrow biopsy is the investigation of choice</strong>, which shows the presence of hypocellularity with increased deposition of reticulin inside the marrow, abnormal megakaryocytes and the characteristic finding of dilated marrow sinusoids.</li>\n<li>Blood shows mild leukocytosis, decreased Hb and hematocrit levels, and elevated LAP scores.</li>\n<li>Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) occurs in 5% to 20% of cases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Essential thrombocytosis (ET)</strong> may also be associated with <em>JAK2</em>, <em>MPL</em>, or <em>CALR</em> mutations.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Essential thrombocytosis (ET) is often associated with activating point mutations in <strong><em>JAK2</em> (50% of cases) or <em>MPL</em> (5% to 10% of cases)</strong>, a receptor tyrosine kinase that is normally activated by thrombopoietin.</li>\n<li>In addition, recent DNA sequencing studies have revealed that most of the remaining cases have mutations in <strong>calreticulin (CALR)</strong>, a protein with several functions in the endoplasmic reticulum and the cytoplasm. Since <em>JAK2</em> and calreticulin mutations are mutually exclusive, it is hypothesized that the calreticulin mutations also increase JAK-STAT signaling through mechanisms that are currently unknown.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>CML is the consequence of the balanced translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22 (t[9;22]).</p>\n<p>PV, PMF, and ET are characterized by driver mutations that directly or indirectly constitutively activate JAK2, a tyrosine kinase essential for the function of the erythropoietin andthrombopoietin receptors and also utilized by granulocyte colony stimulating factor receptor.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0258","text":"Which of the following pat‍ients is least likely to ​have leukemoid reaction?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients is least likely to have leukemoid reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A patient with severe meni‍ngitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A child with Down syndrome‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A patient with hemorrhagic‍ shock","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A patient with acute myoca‍rdial infarction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A patient with acute myocardial infarction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A patient with acute myocardial infarction</strong></p><p>Myocardial infarction does not cause leukemoid reaction.</p>\n<p><strong>Leukemoid reaction </strong>is defined as <strong>persistent leukocytosis </strong>above 50000 cells /mm<sup>3</sup>,<sup> </sup>where the cause is other than leukemia.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes </strong>of leukemoid reaction include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Severe infections</li>\n<li>Malignancy</li>\n<li>Severe hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Acute hemolysis</li>\n<li>Trisomy 21 (Down syndrome)</li>\n<li>Asplenia</li>\n<li>Diabetic ketoacidosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0238","text":"Electron microscopic pictu‍re of which of the follow​ing conditions is shown b‍elow?","raw_text":"Electron microscopic picture of which of the following conditions is shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bernard–Soul‍ier syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mycosis ‍fungoides","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glanzmann th‍rombasthenia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Langerhans cell histiocyto‍sis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Langerhans cell histiocytosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c284377481414961ab0bc7d4b46d52c1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4dcf900614ef422d95f0a0eb2baa5943x1280x1589.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3ab4f69596b042458b6695eb55c25bc4x1280x1078.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Langerhans cell histiocytosis</strong></p><p>The given electron microscopy image shows <strong>tennis racket</strong> shaped <strong>Birbeck granules</strong>. These granules are characteristic of <strong>Langerhans cell histiocytosis. </strong></p>\n<p>These granules are pentalaminar tubules with dilated terminal ends. This gives a characteristic <strong>tennis racket</strong> appearance on electron microscopy. They contain the protein langerin (CD207).</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the Birbeck granules as seen on electron microscopy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>On peripheral smear, the proliferating Langerhans cells are characterised by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Abundant vacuolated cytoplasm</li>\n<li>Vesicular nuclei containing linear grooves or folds - <strong>coffee bean nuclei</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows Langerhans cells with coffee bean nuclei and moderately abundant pale cytoplasm mixed with a few eosinophils.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Tennis racket cells in light microscopy seen in sarcoma botryoides.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5967","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a histiocytic neoplas​m?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a histiocytic neoplasm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Langerhans cell histiocyto‍sis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rosai-Dorf‍man disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Erdheim-Che‍ster disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disseminated juvenile xant‍hogranuloma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rosai-Dorfman disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rosai-Dorfman disease</strong></p><p>Rosai–Dorfman disease is not a histiocytic neoplasm. It is sinus histiocytosis with massive lymphadenopathy.</p>\n<p>WHO classification of histiocytic/ hematolymphoid neoplasm 2016 includes the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Histiocytic sarcoma</li>\n<li><strong>Langerhans cell histiocytosis</strong></li>\n<li>Langerhans cell sarcoma</li>\n<li>Indeterminate dendritic cell tumour</li>\n<li>lnterdigitating dendritic cell sarcoma</li>\n<li>Follicular dendritic cell sarcoma</li>\n<li>Fibroblastic reticular cell tumour</li>\n<li><strong>Disseminated juvenile xanthogranuloma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Erdheim–Chester disease</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0216","text":"You are suspecting Langerh‍ans cell histiocytosis in​ a child with an enlargin‍g scalp lesion. Which of ‌the following markers wil‍l you test to confirm you​r diagnosis?","raw_text":"You are suspecting Langerhans cell histiocytosis in a child with an enlarging scalp lesion. Which of the following markers will you test to confirm your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD‍11a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HLA‍-DQ","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD‍107","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"S-‍100","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. S-100","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. S-100</strong></p><p><strong>S-100</strong> is a marker for <strong>Langerhans cell histiocytosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tumour cells in this condition typically express the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>HLA-DR</li>\n<li>S-100</li>\n<li>CD1a</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0237","text":"A 5-year-old girl is broug‍ht to you by her father, ​who states that the girl ‍is drinking a lot of wate‌r lately. Physical examin‍ation reveals exophthalmo​s. Further workup shows t‌he presence of multiple l‍ytic bone lesions involvi‍ng her calvarium and the ​base of her skull. What i​s the most likely diagnos​is?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old girl is brought to you by her father, who states that the girl is drinking a lot of water lately. Physical examination reveals exophthalmos. Further workup shows the presence of multiple lytic bone lesions involving her calvarium and the base of her skull.  What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Letterer-S‍iwe disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hand- Schuller- Christian ‍disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Eosionophil‍ic granuloma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Unifocal Langerhans cell h‍istiocytosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hand- Schuller- Christian disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hand- Schuller- Christian disease</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Hand–Schüller–Christian disease. </strong></p>\n<p>Langerhans cell histiocytosis presents as multiple clinicopathological entities:</p>\n<p>1. <strong>Hand–Schüller–Christian </strong>disease is a multifocal unisystem Langerhans cell histiocytosis. It usually affects young children. It is characterized by the triad of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diabetes insipidus (excessive thirst) - involvement of the posterior pituitary stalk of the hypothalamus</li>\n<li>Exophthalmos</li>\n<li>Multiple lytic bone lesions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It usually undergoes spontaneous regression. It is treated with local excision or irradiation.</p>\n<p>2.<strong> Letterer–Siwe disease</strong> is a multifocal multisystem Langerhans cell histiocytosis. It occurs most frequently before 2 years of age, but occasionally affects adults.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cutaneous lesions, resembling a seborrheic eruption - caused by infiltrates of Langerhans cells over the front and back of the trunk and on the scalp</li>\n<li>Hepatosplenomegaly</li>\n<li>Lymphadenopathy</li>\n<li>Pulmonary lesions</li>\n<li>Destructive osteolytic bone lesions</li>\n<li>Due to marrow infiltration - anemia, thrombocytopenia, predisposition to recurrent infections (otitis media and mastoiditis) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment involves intensive chemotherapy. This condition is fatal if left untreated.</p>\n<p>3. <strong>Eosinophilic granuloma</strong> is a unifocal unisystem Langerhans cell histiocytosis. It is characterized by the proliferation of Langerhans cells admixed with variable numbers of eosinophils, lymphocytes, plasma cells, and neutrophils. </p>\n<p>These lesions occur in the following sites:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Medullary cavities of bones - most commonly the calvarium, ribs, and femur</li>\n<li>Skin</li>\n<li>Lungs</li>\n<li>Stomach</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Bone lesions can manifest as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Asymptomatic</li>\n<li>Pain</li>\n<li>Tenderness</li>\n<li>Pathologic fractures.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment is by local excision or irradiation.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>4. <strong>Pulmonary</strong> Langerhans cell histiocytosis is most often seen in adult smokers. It regresses spontaneously upon cessation of smoking.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7439","text":"The X-ray of a 65-year-old‍ man is given below. Clin​ical examination, and hem‍atological and urinary te‌sts were normal. Which of‍ the following is the pro​bable diagnosis?","raw_text":"The X-ray of a 65-year-old man is given below. Clinical examination, and hematological and urinary tests were normal. Which of the following is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple‍ myeloma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Langerhans cell histiocyto‍sis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Metas‍tasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperpara‍thyroidism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Langerhans cell histiocytosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fb3d2835a649456290292b0401eb3fc4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Langerhans cell histiocytosis</strong></p><p>The given image showing a <strong>solitary</strong> <strong>punched-out lytic lesion</strong> without a sclerotic rim with a <strong>beveled</strong><strong> edge</strong> appearance, along with the age of the patient with history of <strong>normal</strong> hematological and urinary tests is suggestive of <strong>Langerhans cell histiocytosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Langerhans cell histiocytosis occurs due to the proliferation of a special type of immature dendritic cells, called Langerhans cells. Their normal function is to present antigens.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by <strong>single </strong>or<strong> multiple osteolytic bone lesions. </strong>It<strong> </strong>can also infiltrate the skin (of the scalp), lungs, liver, spleen, bone marrow, or the central nervous system. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0156","text":"Which of the following is ‍not true regarding the pr​ocedure done using the in‍strument given in the ima‌ge below?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding the procedure done using the instrument given in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contraindicated if platele‍t count &amp;lt;40,000","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Breath holding is required‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Procedure can be done in b‍oth lateral &amp;amp; prone p​osition","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diagnosing an infiltrative‍ or granulomatous disease​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Contraindicated if platelet count <40,000","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9e1a29810adf4e8db425729998e4107e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/abc691ac348544e79127b6941c1c71e6x800x640.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Contraindicated if platelet count &lt;40,000</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>bone marrow biopsy needle. Thrombocytopenia </strong>is<strong> not </strong>in itself a<strong> contraindication </strong>for bone marrow biopsy.<strong> </strong>However, a platelet count &gt;20,000 is ensured for safety purposes.</p>\n<p><strong>Indications </strong>of bone marrow biopsy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Evaluation of unexplained anemia, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, pancytopenia</li>\n<li>Evaluation of polycythemia, thrombocytosis, leukocytosis</li>\n<li>Diagnosis and staging of lymphoma or solid tumors</li>\n<li>Diagnosis and evaluation of plasma cell disorders and leukemias</li>\n<li>Evaluation of suspected infiltrative and storage diseases</li>\n<li>Evaluation of fever of unknown origin, suspected mycobacterial, fungal, or parasitic infections, or granulomatous diseases</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contraindications</strong> of bone marrow biopsy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Severe hemophilia</li>\n<li>Severe disseminated intravascular coagulopathy</li>\n<li>Severe bleeding disorders</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Procedure</strong> for bone marrow biopsy:</p>\n<p>Prone or lateral position is chosen for the procedure. Posterior superior iliac spine is identified. After disinfection and local anaesthesia, the patient is asked to inhale slowly through the nose and exhale slowly through the mouth to relieve anxiety .The needle is inserted and rotated on its axis.</p>\n<p>On reaching the desired depth, the stylet is removed and needle is advanced. The needle is rotated in the opposite direction to loosen the specimen. It is then withdrawn using the same rotational movement.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6936","text":"Blood examination shows pa‍ncytopenia, which can be ​correct? A. Megaloblastic‍ anemia B. Hairy cell leu‌kemia C. Hypocellular MDS‍ D. APML","raw_text":"<p>Blood examination shows pancytopenia, which can be correct?</p>\n<p>A. Megaloblastic anemia</p>\n<p>B. Hairy cell leukemia</p>\n<p>C. Hypocellular MDS</p>\n<p>D. APML</p>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B,‍ D","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A, ‍B, C","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"B, ‍C, D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A, ‍B, D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A, B, C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A, B, C</strong></p><p><strong>Megaloblastic anemia</strong> (hypocellular phase), <strong>hairy cell leukemia,</strong> and <strong>hypocellular MDS</strong> can show <strong>pancytopenia</strong>. Acute promyelocytic leukemia is not associated with pancytopenia.  </p>\n<p>Causes of <strong>pancytopenia</strong> with <strong>hypocellular bone marrow:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acquired aplastic anemia</li>\n<li>Hypocellular myelodysplastic syndrome</li>\n<li>Rare aleukemic leukemia</li>\n<li>Some acute lymphoid leukemia</li>\n<li>Rare lymphomas of bone marrow</li>\n<li>Copper deficiency</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Causes of <strong>pancytopenia</strong> with <strong>cellular bone marrow:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p2\">Myelodysplastic syndromes</li>\n<li class=\"p2\">Systemic lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li class=\"p2\">Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH)</li>\n<li class=\"p2\">Hypersplenism</li>\n<li class=\"p2\">B<span class=\"s1\">12</span>, folate deficiency, Copper deficiency</li>\n<li class=\"p2\">Myelofibrosis</li>\n<li class=\"p2\">Aleukemic leukemia</li>\n<li class=\"p2\">HIV, Brucellosis, Leishmaniasis, Tuberculosis Sepsis</li>\n<li class=\"p2\">Myelophthisis </li>\n<li class=\"p2\">Bone marrow lymphoma</li>\n<li class=\"p2\">Sarcoidosis</li>\n<li class=\"p2\">Hairy cell leukemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6715","text":"Which of the following con‍ditions will have the lea​st chances of a dry tap o‍n bone marrow aspiration?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions will have the least chances of a dry tap on bone marrow aspiration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hairy cel‍l leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Follicula‍r lymphoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"AML‍ M7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myelodysplas‍tic syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Follicular lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Follicular lymphoma</strong></p><p><strong>Follicular lymphoma</strong> is the least likely cause of a dry tap on bone marrow aspiration.</p>\n<p>Causes of <strong>dry tap</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Metastatic carcinoma infiltration </li>\n<li>Chronic myeloid leukemia </li>\n<li><strong>Myelofibrosis</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Hairy cell leukemia</strong> </li>\n<li>Acute leukemia </li>\n<li>Hodgkin's lymphoma</li>\n<li><strong>AML- M7</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Causes of <strong>pancytopenia with cellular marrow</strong> are:</p>\n<p>Primary disease of marrow-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Myelodysplastic syndrome</li>\n<li>Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH)</li>\n<li>Myelofibrosis</li>\n<li>Aleukemic leukemia</li>\n<li>Myelophthisis</li>\n<li>Bone marrow lymphoma</li>\n<li>Hairy cell leukemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Secondary to systemic disease-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>SLE, sarcoidosis</li>\n<li>B12, folate deficiency</li>\n<li>Copper deficiency</li>\n<li>Hypersplenism</li>\n<li>Alcohol</li>\n<li>HIV infection, TB</li>\n<li>Brucellosis, leishmaniasis </li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Note: Although marrow is hypercellular in CML, MDS etc., extensive marrow fibrosis (rare) has been proposed to account for a dry tap on bone marrow biopsy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"YrQc71G2","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"33_antimicrobials_acting_on_30s_subunit","title":"33.Antimicrobials Acting On 30S Subunit","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB4433","text":"A patient diagnosed with t‍uberculosis is undergoing​ treatment with streptomy‍cin. Which of the followi‌ng is the primary intrace‌llular site of action of ‌this drug?","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with tuberculosis is undergoing treatment with streptomycin. Which of the following is the primary intracellular site of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30S riboso‍mal subunit","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"DNA helic‍ase enzyme","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"50S riboso‍mal subunit","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Topoisomera‍se IV enzyme","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 30S ribosomal subunit","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 30S ribosomal subunit</strong></p><p>Streptomycin is an <strong>aminoglycoside</strong> antibiotic and the primary intracellular site of action of aminoglycosides is the <strong>30S ribosomal subunit</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mechanism of action of aminoglycosides: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aminoglycosides are <strong>transported actively</strong> across the cell membrane into the cytoplasm (oxygen-dependent process)</li>\n<li>Binding of aminoglycosides to 30S ribosomes cause <strong>misreading of mRNA</strong> template and result in incorporation of<strong> incorrect amino acids</strong> in the growing polypeptide chain</li>\n<li>The resulting<strong> aberrant proteins</strong> may be inserted into the cell membrane leading to <strong>altered permeability</strong> of the cell membrane of bacteria causing bacterial death (<strong>bactericidal</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4434","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements about aminoglycos​ides is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about aminoglycosides is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are t‍eratogenic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"They are excreted unchange‍d in urine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"They are ba‍cteriostatic","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"They are distributed only ‍extracellularly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. They are bacteriostatic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. They are bacteriostatic</strong></p><p>Aminoglycosides are rapid <strong>bactericidal</strong> agents.</p>\n<p>Aminoglycosides like streptomycin and tobramycin cause hearing loss in a newborn child when taken during pregnancy. They are polar compounds and are excreted usually unchanged in the urine. They are distributed mainly in the extracellular compartment.</p>\n<p>Primarily bacteriostatic drugs are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sulfonamides</li>\n<li>Erythromycin</li>\n<li>Tetracyclines</li>\n<li>Clindamycin</li>\n<li>Chloramphenicol</li>\n<li>Linezolid</li>\n<li>Ethambutol</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Primarily bactericidal drugs are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Penicillins</li>\n<li>Cephalosporins</li>\n<li>Aminoglycosides</li>\n<li>Vancomycin</li>\n<li>Polypeptides</li>\n<li>Ciprofloxacin</li>\n<li>Rifampin</li>\n<li>Metronidazole</li>\n<li>Isoniazid</li>\n<li>Cotrimoxazole</li>\n<li>Pyrazinamide</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4453","text":"A young man working in a r‍abbit farm presents with ​fever, lymphadenopathy an‍d an ulcer on the hand an‌d is diagnosed with tular‌emia. What is the drug of‌ choice for this conditio​n?","raw_text":"A young man working in a rabbit farm presents with fever, lymphadenopathy and an ulcer on the hand and is diagnosed with tularemia. What is the drug of choice for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amik‍acin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neom‍ycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kana‍mycin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Strept‍omycin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Streptomycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Streptomycin</strong></p><p><strong>Streptomycin </strong>(if not available, gentamicin) is considered the drug of choice for the treatment of <strong>tularemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tularemia is caused by <em>Francisella tularensis</em>, an aerobic gram-negative bacteria.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3772","text":"A child presented with rec‍urrent episodes of cough ​with expectoration. Inves‍tigations revealed a posi‌tive sweat chloride test.‌ Inhalational formulation‌ of which of the followin​g aminoglycosides is appr​oved for use in this cond‍ition?","raw_text":"A child presented with recurrent episodes of cough with expectoration. Investigations revealed a positive sweat chloride test. Inhalational formulation of which of the following aminoglycosides is approved for use in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amik‍acin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Genta‍micin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tobra‍mycin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Strept‍omycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tobramycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tobramycin</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>cystic fibrosis.</strong> <strong>Inhalational</strong> formulation of <strong>tobramycin </strong>is approved for use in cystic fibrosis patients with <strong><em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em></strong> infections<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Injectable formulation of amikacin is used in cystic fibrosis. </p>\n<p>Options B &amp; D: Inhalational formulations of gentamicin and streptomycin are available but they are usually not used.</p>\n<p>Note: The question is about approved inhalational formulation. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3773","text":"The organisms detected in ‍culture and sensitivity r​eports of various specime‍ns obtained from the pati‌ents from a medical ward ‌are given below. In which‌ of the following infecti​ons would amikacin not be​ indicated? Pus culture p‍ositive for Pseudomonas a‌eruginosa Sputum culture ‌positive for Klebsiella p‌neumoniae Urine culture p​ositive for Proteus mirab‌ilis Blood culture positi​ve for Serratia marcescen​s Urine culture positive ​for Enterococcus faecalis‍","raw_text":"<p>The organisms detected in culture and sensitivity reports of various specimens obtained from the patients from a medical ward are given below. In which of the following infections would amikacin not be indicated?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Pus culture positive for Pseudomonas aeruginosa</li>\n<li>Sputum culture positive for Klebsiella pneumoniae</li>\n<li>Urine culture positive for Proteus mirabilis</li>\n<li>Blood culture positive for Serratia marcescens</li>\n<li>Urine culture positive for Enterococcus faecalis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 o‍nly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 and ‍4 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5 o‍nly","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2 and ‍3 only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 5 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 5 only</strong></p><p><strong>Amikacin</strong> has <strong>less activity</strong> against <strong>enterococci</strong> and hence is not indicated in infections caused by this organism. In enterococcal infections, <strong>gentamicin</strong> along with penicillin (or ampicillin) is given.</p>\n<p>Amikacin is used against serious nosocomial gram-negative bacillary infections which are resistant to gentamicin and tobramycin. It has good activity against most strains of the following organisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em></li>\n<li><em>Serratia</em></li>\n<li><em>Proteus</em></li>\n<li><em>Klebsiella</em></li>\n<li><em>Enterobacter</em> </li>\n<li><em>Escherichia coli</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4454","text":"Oral neomycin will not be ‍effective for treating wh​ich of the following pati‍ents?","raw_text":"Oral neomycin will not be effective for treating which of the following patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A patient requiring pre-op‍erative antisepsis for bo​wel surgery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A elderly man with hepatic‍ encephalopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A child with enteropathoge‍nic E. coli associated di​arrhea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A woman with a cut wound o‍n the finger","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A woman with a cut wound on the finger","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A woman with a cut wound on the finger</strong></p><p><strong>Topical</strong> neomycin is used widely for a variety of infections of the skin and mucous membranes and to prevent infection of minor cuts and wounds.</p>\n<p><strong>Oral</strong> neomycin can be used for the eradication of bacteria <strong>confined to GIT </strong>in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pre-operative antisepsis for bowel surgery</li>\n<li>Hepatic encephalopathy: To reduce deleterious ammonia producing bacteria</li>\n<li>Enteropathogenic E. coli associated diarrhea</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Orally administered neomycin is <strong>poorly absorbed from the GI tract </strong>and is eliminated unchanged in the feces.</p>\n<p>Neomycin is highly nephrotoxic in human beings and hence should not be administered systemically.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4452","text":"A patient with hospital-ac‍quired pneumonia is being​ treated with an aminogly‍coside antibiotic by a si‌ngle daily dose administr‌ation method. This method‌ is preferred over the mu​ltiple daily dosage metho​d because of which of the‍ following properties of ‌aminoglycosides?","raw_text":"A patient with hospital-acquired pneumonia is being treated with an aminoglycoside antibiotic by a single daily dose administration method. This method is preferred over the multiple daily dosage method because of which of the following properties of aminoglycosides?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased CYP450 mediated ‍metabolism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Long post antibiotic effec‍t","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increased binding to plasm‍a proteins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Time depend‍ent killing","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Long post antibiotic effect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Long post antibiotic effect</strong></p><p>Single daily dose aminoglycoside administration is more preferable to multiple daily dose administration because of the<strong> long post-antibiotic effect.</strong></p>\n<p>For certain classes of drugs such as <strong>aminoglycosides and quinolones, </strong>its antimicrobial action is dependent on the<strong> peak concentration above MIC</strong> (Minimum Inhibitory Concentration). It is referred to as <strong>concentration-dependent killing</strong>. </p>\n<p>High-dose, extended-interval dosing schemes take advantage of the concentration-dependent activity of aminoglycosides to achieve maximal initial bacterial killing. And then because of the <strong>postantibiotic effect</strong> of aminoglycosides, good therapeutic response can be attained even when concentrations fall below MIC for a substantial fraction of the dosing interval. </p>\n<p>Therefore, a single daily dose would be equal to or more effective than multiple 8 hourly doses. This dosing strategy may also reduce the ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity of these drugs.</p>\n<p><strong>Time-dependent killing</strong> is an effect seen when the antimicrobial action is dependent on the duration, the drug concentration remains above the MIC. <strong>Beta-lactams, macrolides,</strong> and<strong> vancomycin</strong> have this property.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4455","text":"A patient who was diagnose‍d with urinary tract infe​ction receives a single i‍ntramuscular dose of gent‌amicin. Within how many m‌inutes of administration ‌is the peak concentration​ of this drug achieved?","raw_text":"A patient who was diagnosed with urinary tract infection receives a single intramuscular dose of gentamicin. Within how many minutes of administration is the peak concentration of this drug achieved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0-30 m‍inutes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"30-90 ‍minutes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"90-120 ‍minutes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"120-180‍ minutes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 30-90 minutes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 30-90 minutes</strong></p><p>Gentamicin is an <strong>aminoglycoside</strong> antibiotic and the peak concentration of aminoglycosides following intramuscular administration is achieved after <strong>30-90 minutes.</strong></p>\n<p>Aminoglycosides need <strong>therapeutic dose monitoring</strong> because of their <strong>low therapeutic index</strong> especially in patients in whom renal function is compromised. Both trough and peak levels are calculated for aminoglycosides.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4437","text":"The urine culture and sens‍itivity report of a patie​nt with urinary tract inf‍ection is positive for am‌inoglycoside-resistant En‌terococcus faecalis. What‌ is the most common mode ​of this resistance?","raw_text":"The urine culture and sensitivity report of a patient with urinary tract infection is positive for aminoglycoside-resistant Enterococcus faecalis. What is the most common mode of this resistance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of transport ac‍ross bacterial membranes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Production of inactivating‍ enzymes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Low affinity of bacterial ‍ribosome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Drug efflux mechanism thro‍ugh the membrane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Production of inactivating enzymes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Production of inactivating enzymes</strong></p><p>Clinically, the most common cause of aminoglycoside resistance is <strong>drug inactivation</strong> <strong>by enzymes</strong>.</p>\n<p>The inactivating enzymes are commonly acquired through conjugation and transfer of <strong>plasmids</strong> containing<strong> resistance genes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Causes of aminoglycoside resistance are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drug inactivation by enzymes</li>\n<li>Increased drug efflux</li>\n<li>Decreased permeability to the drug</li>\n<li>Modifications of the 30S ribosomal subunit that interferes with binding of the aminoglycosides</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3771","text":"In which of the following ‍patients will you prescri​be aminoglycosides at a l‍ower dose?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients will you prescribe aminoglycosides at a lower dose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A urosepsis patient with a‍ssociated AKI","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A cystic fibrosis patient ‍with acute pulmonary exac​erbation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A diabetic patient with in‍fected burn wounds","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A CLD patient with osteomy‍elitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A urosepsis patient with associated AKI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A urosepsis patient with associated AKI</strong></p><p>The dose of aminoglycosides is to be reduced in patients with <strong>renal failure</strong> (AKI) because clearance of the drug is reduced.</p>\n<p>Aminoglycosides undergo <strong>minimum metabolism</strong> and are primarily excreted almost entirely by <strong>glomerular filtration</strong>. Therefore, renal failure decreases the clearance of the drug. Because the incidence of nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity is likely related to the overall exposure to aminoglycosides, it is critical to reduce the maintenance dose and dosing interval of these drugs in patients with impaired renal function.</p>\n<p>Option B: Renal clearances are increased and the half-life is reduced in cystic fibrosis patients necessitating higher doses. </p>\n<p>Option C: Burn patients have a more rapid drug clearance probably due to increased drug loss through burnt tissue and require higher doses in them.</p>\n<p>Option D: Aminoglycoside dose adjustment is not required in liver disease</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4447","text":"A patient who was on amino‍glycoside therapy present​s with nausea and vomitin‍g. Investigations reveale‌d rising serum creatinine‌ levels, and granular cas‌ts were seen on urine mic​roscopy. Which of the fol​lowing aminoglycosides is‍ least likely to cause th‌is condition?","raw_text":"A patient who was on aminoglycoside therapy presents with nausea and vomiting. Investigations revealed rising serum creatinine levels, and granular casts were seen on urine microscopy. Which of the following aminoglycosides is least likely to cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amik‍acin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Genta‍micin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Strept‍omycin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tobra‍mycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Streptomycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Streptomycin</strong></p><p>The scenario depicts a case of <strong>aminoglycoside-induced nephrotoxicity</strong> and the <strong>least nephrotoxic</strong> among aminoglycosides is <strong>streptomycin</strong> because it does not concentrate in the renal cortex. </p>\n<p>Among aminoglycosides, <strong>neomycin </strong>has the<strong> highest</strong> and streptomycin has the least nephrotoxicity.</p>\n<p>Adverse effects of aminoglycosides are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nephorotoxicity</li>\n<li>Ototoxicity</li>\n<li>Acute neuromuscular blockade and apnea</li>\n<li>Hypersensitivity reactions (rare)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4448","text":"Vestibular toxicity is pre‍dominantly seen with whic​h of the following aminog‍lycosides?","raw_text":"Vestibular toxicity is predominantly seen with which of the following aminoglycosides?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amik‍acin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kana‍mycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Strept‍omycin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Neom‍ycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Streptomycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Streptomycin</strong></p><p><strong>Vestibular toxicity</strong> is predominantly seen with <strong>streptomycin and gentamicin</strong>. </p>\n<p>Ototoxicity seen with aminoglycosides:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cochlear toxicity </strong>(high-pitched tinnitus)<strong> -</strong> <strong>amikacin, kanamycin,</strong><strong> neomycin </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Vestibular toxicity </strong>(moderately intense headache and vertigo) <strong>-</strong> <strong>streptomycin &gt; gentamicin </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Both</strong> vestibular toxicity and cochlear toxicity - <strong>tobramycin</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4440","text":"A patient who was recently‍ started on i.v. gentamic​in for enterococcal endoc‍arditis presented to the ‌OPD with complaints of he‌adache and dizziness. By ‌which of the following me​chanisms does the drug ca​use this adverse effect?","raw_text":"A patient who was recently started on i.v. gentamicin for enterococcal endocarditis presented to the OPD with complaints of headache and dizziness. By which of the following mechanisms does the drug cause this adverse effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Direct hair ‍cell toxicity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibition of hair cell Na‍+/K+ ATPase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Non-cumulat‍ive toxicity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interference with Ca2+ cha‍nnels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Direct hair cell toxicity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Direct hair cell toxicity</strong></p><p>The scenario depicts a case of<strong> gentamicin-induced ototoxicity </strong>which is caused due to <strong>direct hair cell toxicity</strong>. Degeneration of vestibular hair cells and neurons leads to vertigo and associated headache.</p>\n<p>Streptomycin and gentamicin produce predominantly vestibular effects. Amikacin, kanamycin, and neomycin primarily affect auditory function. Tobramycin affects both equally.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4451","text":"An elderly patient who was‍ taken up for an emergenc​y laparotomy showed prolo‍nged respiratory depressi‌on which did not resolve ‌with neostigmine. On inqu‌iry, the attendant confir​med that the patient was ​on aminoglycoside therapy‍. Which of the following ‌is least likely to cause ‌this effect?","raw_text":"An elderly patient who was taken up for an emergency laparotomy showed prolonged respiratory depression which did not resolve with neostigmine.  On inquiry, the attendant confirmed that the patient was on aminoglycoside therapy. Which of the following is least likely to cause this effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amik‍acin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kana‍mycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Genta‍micin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tobra‍mycin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tobramycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tobramycin</strong></p><p>The given scenario depicts the <strong>neuromuscular blockade</strong> effect of aminoglycosides, and<strong> tobramycin</strong> has the least potency for blockade in this group.</p>\n<p>Decreasing order of potency for the neuromuscular blockade is:</p>\n<p>Neomycin &gt; Kanamycin &gt; Amikacin &gt; Gentamicin &gt; <strong>Tobramycin</strong></p>\n<p>It is a recognized clinical side effect of aminoglycoside antibiotics, and this effect becomes significant (prolonged respiratory depression) when the drug is used in conjunction with <strong>neuromuscular blocking agents</strong> or <strong>calcium channel blockers</strong>. They are avoided in patients with myasthenia gravis.</p>\n<p>Aminoglycosides cause neuromuscular blockade by <strong>interfering with calcium channels</strong> and thereby <strong>inhibiting</strong> <strong>acetylcholine's</strong> pre-junctional release and post-junctional sensitivity. The neuromuscular blockade produced by these antibiotics is not reversed by neostigmine but is <strong>reversed by IV calcium gluconate.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3797","text":"Which of the following is ‍the drug of choice for sc​rub typhus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the drug of choice for scrub typhus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Doxyc‍ycline","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Azithr‍omycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chloram‍phenicol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ciprof‍loxacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Doxycycline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Doxycycline</strong></p><p><strong>Doxycycline</strong> is the drug of choice for <strong>scrub typhus.</strong></p>\n<p>Azithromycin, chloramphenicol, or rifampin can be administered as alternatives in patients allergic to doxycycline and in pregnancy. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4456","text":"By which of the following ‍mechanisms do tetracyclin​es inhibit protein synthe‍sis?","raw_text":"By which of the following mechanisms do tetracyclines inhibit protein synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of initiation o‍f translation and misread​ing of mRNA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Binding to 30S subunit and‍ inhibiting the binding o​f aminoacyl-tRNA to A sit‍e","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Binding to 30S subunit and‍ inhibiting peptide bond ​formation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibits binding of aminoa‍cyl-t-RNA to 50s subunit ​of ribosome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Binding to 30S subunit and inhibiting the binding of aminoacyl-tRNA to A site","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Binding to 30S subunit and inhibiting the binding of aminoacyl-tRNA to A site</strong></p><p><strong>Tetracyclines</strong> inhibit protein synthesis by <strong>binding</strong> to <strong>30s subunit</strong> and inhibiting the binding of aminoacyl-tRNA to the A (acceptor) site of the mRNA – ribosome complex.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3809","text":"Which of the following org‍anisms is resistant to te​tracyclines?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms is resistant to tetracyclines?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MR‍SA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudomonas‍ aeruginosa","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ricke‍ttsia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mycop‍lasma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pseudomonas aeruginosa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pseudomonas aeruginosa</strong></p><p>All strains of <em><strong>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</strong> </em>are <strong>resistant</strong> to the action of<strong> tetracyclines</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tetracyclines are active against MRSA and MSSA. Doxycycline and minocycline can be used in cutaneous MRSA infections.</p>\n<p>Rickettsia and chlamydia are highly susceptible to tetracyclines. Tetracyclines are considered first line agents for <strong>chlamydial</strong> <strong>infections</strong> like non-specific urethritis/ endocervicitis and <strong>rickettsial infections</strong> like typhus, rocky mountain spotted fever, Q fever.</p>\n<p>They are also second-line drugs in patients with syphilis who are allergic to penicillin.</p>\n<p>Tetracyclines are<strong> bacteriostatic</strong> drugs which are more active against gram-positive than gram-negative. They are effective against spirochetes, <em>Haemophilus ducreyi, Vibrio cholera, </em><em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae.</em></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4459","text":"In patients with which of ‍the following conditions ​is doxycycline not used?","raw_text":"In patients with which of the following conditions is doxycycline not used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leptos‍pirosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphogranul‍oma veneruum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Borrel‍liosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typhoi‍d fever","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Typhoid fever","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Typhoid fever</strong></p><p>Doxycycline is not used in typhoid infection. <strong>Enterobacteriaceae and gram-negative anaerobes</strong> are <strong>resistant</strong> to <strong>tetracyclines</strong>, hence doxycycline is not used in intraabdominal infections. Third-generation cephalosporins and quinolones are used in treating typhoid fever.</p>\n<p><strong>Doxycycline</strong> treatment for 21 days is the first-line therapy for<strong> lymphogranuloma venereum</strong>. It is also indicated as one of the first-line treatments for<strong> borreliosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Doxycycline is the alternative to penicillin for the treatment of <strong>leptospirosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4457","text":"Tetracycline is used for t‍he prophylaxis of which o​f the following condition‍s?","raw_text":"Tetracycline is used for the prophylaxis of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cho‍lera","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bruce‍llosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leptos‍pirosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Menin‍gitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cholera","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cholera</strong></p><p><strong>Tetracycline</strong> is used for the<strong> prophylaxis of cholera</strong>. It is recommended for <strong>close contacts</strong> of a case of cholera. It is supposed to be effective in <strong>preventing spread.</strong></p>\n<p>Doxycycline in combination with rifampicin is used for prophylaxis of brucellosis. It is also used in the prophylaxis of leptospirosis.</p>\n<p>Note: The question is about the drug tetracycline and not about the class of tetracyclines as a whole.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4464","text":"The urine culture and sens‍itivity report of a patie​nt is positive for tetrac‍ycline-resistant Enteroco‌ccus faecalis. Which amon‌g the following is a mino‌cycline derivative that c​an be used in this condit​ion?","raw_text":"The urine culture and sensitivity report of a patient is positive for tetracycline-resistant Enterococcus faecalis. Which among the following is a minocycline derivative that can be used in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methac‍ycline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymec‍ycline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Doxyc‍ycline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tigec‍ycline","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tigecycline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tigecycline</strong></p><p><strong>Tigecycline</strong> is a glycylcycline and a <strong>derivative of minocycline</strong> that is <strong>active</strong> against most <strong>bacteria</strong> that are <strong>resistant</strong> to <strong>classical tetracyclines,</strong> and hence can be used in this condition.</p>\n<p>The mechanism of action is similar to tetracyclines. It is used against enterobacteriaceae, gram-negative anaerobes, and enterococcus which are resistant to tetracyclines.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4461","text":"A patient diagnosed with t‍he syndrome of inappropri​ate secretion of antidiur‍etic hormone is prescribe‌d a drug known to cause n‌ephrogenic diabetes insip‌idus. Identify this drug.​","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with the syndrome of inappropriate secretion of antidiuretic hormone is prescribed a drug known to cause nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. Identify this drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Minoc‍ycline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Demeclo‍cycline","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tetrac‍ycline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Doxyc‍ycline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Demeclocycline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Demeclocycline</strong></p><p>The drug described here is <strong>demeclocycline.</strong> <strong>Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus</strong> is associated with the use of this drug. This <strong>side effect</strong> of the drug is utilized in the treatment of SIADH (syndrome of inappropriate secretion of antidiuretic hormone).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4462","text":"A patient develops dizzine‍ss, ataxia, and nausea af​ter he was started on an ‍antimicrobial drug. Which‌ of the following drugs c‌an be implicated?","raw_text":"A patient develops dizziness, ataxia, and nausea after he was started on an antimicrobial drug. Which of the following drugs can be implicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tetrac‍ycline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oxytetr‍acycline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Doxyc‍ycline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Minoc‍ycline","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Minocycline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Minocycline</strong></p><p>The drug described here is <strong>minocycline</strong> as it is associated with<strong> vestibular toxicity</strong> which manifests as <strong>dizziness, ataxia, nausea,</strong> and <strong>vomiting.</strong> Symptoms usually occur soon after the initial dose is given and disappears 24-48 hours after drug administration is stopped.</p>\n<p><strong>Uses</strong> of minocycline are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cutaneous MRSA infections</li>\n<li>Along with sulfonamides for nocardiosis (alternative therapy)</li>\n<li>Subgingival administration in dentistry (extended-release preparations)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4427","text":"An infant on antibiotics b‍ecomes very irritable. In​vestigations reveal incre‍ased intracranial pressur‌e. However, the condition‌ resolves on discontinuat‌ion of the drug. Which dr​ug had the baby likely be​en on?","raw_text":"An infant on antibiotics becomes very irritable. Investigations reveal increased intracranial pressure. However, the condition resolves on discontinuation of the drug. Which drug had the baby likely been on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aminogl‍ycosides","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tetrac‍yclines","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chloram‍phenicol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Macro‍lides","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tetracyclines","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tetracyclines</strong></p><p>The given scenario depicts a case of <strong>pseudotumor cerebri</strong> and <strong>tetracyclines</strong> are associated with this condition in infants, even when given in normal doses. Only <strong>elevated intracranial pressure</strong> is seen without any abnormality in the spinal fluid. Increased pressure returns to normal after therapy is discontinued.</p>\n<p>Other <strong>side effects</strong> of tetracyclines are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Teratogenic</strong></li>\n<li>Deposit in developing teeth and bones, cause brownish discoloration of teeth (chelating property)</li>\n<li>Marked suppression of normal flora of intestine causing superinfection.<strong> Pseudomembranous enterocolitis</strong> is a rare side effect</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7017","text":"Which of the following dru‍gs does not require dose ​reduction in patients wit‍h renal failure?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs does not require dose reduction in patients with renal failure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Genta‍micin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vanco‍mycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amphote‍ricin B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Doxyc‍ycline","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Doxycycline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Doxycycline</strong></p><p><strong>Doxycycline</strong> does not require dose reduction in renal failure. Doxycycline, <strong>minocycline,</strong> and glycylcyclines like <strong>tigecycline</strong> have <strong>fewer renal side effects.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Antibiotics</strong> causing <strong>acute kidney injury</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acute tubular necrosis:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Amphotericin B</li>\n<li>Vancomycin</li>\n<li>Acyclovir</li>\n<li>Foscarnet</li>\n<li>Tenofovir </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Acute interstitial nephritis:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Penicillin</li>\n<li>Cephalosporins</li>\n<li>Quinolones</li>\n<li>Rifampicin</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Ylel72QO","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"33_gi_peristalsis_and_motility","title":"33.Gi Peristalsis And Motility","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB9216","text":"Slow waves are generated b‌y:","raw_text":"Slow waves are generated by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myenteri‌c neurons","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Smooth mu‌scle cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interstitial cells of Caja‌l","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Parasympath‌etic neurons","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Interstitial cells of Cajal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Interstitial cells of Cajal</strong></p><p>The <strong>interstitial cells of Cajal</strong> undergo cyclic changes in membrane potential due to unique ion channels that peri­odically open and produce inward (pacemaker) currents that generate <strong>slow-wave activity</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Interstitial cells of Cajal </strong>are the <strong>pacemaker cells</strong> of the <strong>GIT</strong> that initiate the <strong>basic electrical rhythm</strong> (BER). These are stellate mesenchymal pacemaker cells with smooth muscle-like features.</p>\n<p>In the stomach and the small intestine, these cells are located in the circular smooth muscle layer near the myenteric plexus. In the colon, they are at the submucosal border of the circular muscle layer.</p>\n<p>The slow waves usually do not by themselves cause muscle contraction in most parts of the gastrointestinal tract, except perhaps in the stomach<em>. </em>Instead, they mainly excite the appearance of intermittent spike potentials, and the spike potentials in turn actually excite the muscle contraction.</p>\n<p>Note<strong>: Gastrointestinal stromal tumors</strong> arise from the interstitial cells of Cajal.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6744","text":"Least frequency of slow wa‌ves is seen in:","raw_text":"Least frequency of slow waves is seen in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duod‌enum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Il‌eum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Jej‌unum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sto‌mach","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stomach","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3aedd333927b4607b4c6b1eca436466ex1020x730.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stomach</strong></p><p>Among the given options, the least frequency of slow waves is seen in the <strong>stomach (4/min).</strong></p>\n<p>The Basal electrical rhythm (BER) also called slow-wave, is a <strong>rhythmic fluctuation of membrane potential</strong> in the gastrointestinal smooth muscle. It is initiated by the<strong> interstitial cells of Cajal. </strong>The membrane potential fluctuates between -65mV and -45mV.</p>\n<p>The BER is seen in all sites of gastrointestinal smooth muscle <strong>except</strong> in the <strong>esophagus</strong> and<strong> proximal stomach.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>The rate of BER</strong> in various sites is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stomach: 4/min</li>\n<li>Duodenum: 12/min</li>\n<li>Distal ileum: 8/min</li>\n<li>Cecum: 2/min</li>\n<li>Sigmoid colon: 6/min</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> BER</strong> does<strong> not cause contraction</strong> of the <strong>smooth muscle.</strong> However, when the BER is superimposed by the spike potentials, it increases muscle tension.</p>\n<p><strong>Acetylcholine</strong> causes an <strong>increase</strong> in the number of spike potentials and muscle tension. <strong>Epinephrine</strong> causes a <strong>decrease</strong> in the number of spike potentials and muscle tension.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Muscle contraction can occur only when the motor stimulus is provided during the depolarisation part of the BER.</p>\n<p>Thus, BER does not cause muscle contraction by itself, but controls the rate and organization sites of the muscle contractions, as in <strong>coordination </strong>of the <strong>peristaltic activity</strong> and setting the <strong>rhythm</strong> of<strong> segmentation</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4641","text":"Slow waves are maximum in ‌which part of the intesti‍ne?","raw_text":"Slow waves are maximum in which part of the intestine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sto‌mach","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Il‌eum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Co‌lon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rec‌tum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ileum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ileum</strong></p><p>Among the given options, the <strong>frequency</strong> of 'slow waves' is <strong>maximum</strong> in the <strong>ileum</strong> <strong>(8/min).</strong></p>\n<p>Normal intestinal motility is determined by two patterns of electrical activity - slow waves and spike potentials.</p>\n<p>Slow waves, also known as basic electrical rhythm are partial depolarizations of smooth muscle cells, spreading along the GIT. They originate from the interstitial cells of Cajal. The smooth muscles are in a hyperpolarized state, having a resting membrane potential of -50 to -60mV.</p>\n<p>These waves are not action potentials themselves and they do not cause any contractions. Slow waves raise the membrane potential by 5-15mV, bringing it closer to the threshold enabling spike potential (true action potential) to occur. Therefore, they set the basic rhythm of GIT.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9654","text":"As a part of cranial nerve‌s examination, swallowing‍ reflex is performed on a‍ patient. Which of the fo‌llowing is not involved i‌n afferent limb of this r​eflex?","raw_text":"As a part of cranial nerves examination, swallowing reflex is performed on a patient. Which of the following is not involved in afferent limb of this reflex?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trigemin‌al nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Facial‌ nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Glossophary‌ngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vagus‌ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Facial nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Facial nerve</strong></p><p>The<strong> facial nerve</strong> is a part of an <strong>efferent limb</strong> (and not afferent) of the <strong>swallowing reflex.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 115px; width: 383px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 31.543px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 368px; text-align: center; height: 31.543px;\"><strong>Swallowing/ deglutition reflex</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 110px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.832px; height: 110px;\">Afferents</td>\n<td style=\"width: 240.168px; height: 110px;\">\n<p>Trigeminal nerve</p>\n<p>Glossopharyngeal nerve</p>\n<p>Vagus nerve</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.832px; height: 78px;\">Centre</td>\n<td style=\"width: 240.168px; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Nucleus of the tractus solitarius</p>\n<p>Nucleus ambig­uus</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 110px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 127.832px; height: 110px;\">Efferents</td>\n<td style=\"width: 240.168px; height: 110px;\">\n<p>Trigeminal nerve</p>\n<p>Facial nerve</p>\n<p>Hypoglossal nerve</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4645","text":"Chyme is propelled forward‌ in small intestine by___‍____","raw_text":"Chyme is propelled forward in small intestine by_______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Segmen‌tation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Haust‌ration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"M‌MC","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peris‌talsis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Peristalsis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9f3ecc080f694d3182bb00b059253c90x1021x711.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5367114d796e413b9813ed79593d1329x1021x649.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Peristalsis</strong></p><p><strong>Chyme</strong> is <strong>propelled</strong> forward in the <strong>intestine</strong> by<strong> </strong>the<strong> peristaltic movement.</strong></p>\n<p>Chyme is a thick semifluid mass of partially digested food and digestive secretions that is expelled by the stomach into the duodenum.</p>\n<p><strong>Peristalsis </strong>is a propulsive movement,<strong> </strong>which consists of a segment of contraction behind and relaxation in front.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Segmentation</strong> consists of a segment of bowel contracting at both ends, and then a second contraction occurs in the center of the segment to force the chyme both backward and forward. </p>\n<p>It is the small intestine’s primary method of motility during digestion of a meal.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Migrating motor complexes </strong>(MMC) are the peristaltic motor patterns that occur during fasting. They migrate from the stomach to the distal ileum.</p>\n<p><strong>Haustrations</strong> are the characteristic saclike segments seen in the large intestine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4646","text":"Which of the following is ‌the major initiating resp‍onse for peristalsis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the major initiating response for peristalsis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Horm‌onal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Local stret‌ching of gut","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Neu‌ral","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gastri‌c acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Local stretching of gut","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fc61cd98c5b14b2891c1c28976cfb22ax600x657.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Local stretching of gut</strong></p><p>The <strong>major initiating response</strong> for peristalsis is<strong> local stretching </strong>of the <strong>gut.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Peristalsis</strong> is a reflex response that is initiated when the gut wall is stretched by the contents of the lumen. It occurs in all parts of the gastrointestinal tract from the esophagus to the rectum.</p>\n<p>The stretch initiates a circular contraction behind the stimulus and an area of relaxation in front of it. The wave of contraction then moves in an oral-to-caudal direction. This propels the contents of the lumen forward at rates that vary from 2 to 25 cm/s.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4694","text":"Which of the following is ‌seen in a patient on para‍sympathetic stimulation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is seen in a patient on parasympathetic stimulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sphincter closure of gall ‌bladder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased ‌peristalsis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased ‌GI motility","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased pancreatic secre‌tion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increased peristalsis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increased peristalsis</strong></p><p><strong>Parasympathetic stimulation</strong> results in <strong>increased peristalsis.</strong></p>\n<p>Generally, the stimulation of parasympathetic nerves (PNS) causes an increase in the activity of the entire enteric nervous system. This, in turn, enhances the activity of most gastrointestinal functions.</p>\n<p><strong>PNS stimulation</strong> causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Contraction of smooth muscle in the wall</li>\n<li>Relaxation of sphincters</li>\n<li>↑ Salivary secretion</li>\n<li>↑ Gastric secretion</li>\n<li>↑ Pancreatic secretion</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>SNS</strong> <strong>stimulation</strong> causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Relaxation of smooth muscle in the wall</li>\n<li>Contraction of sphincters</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7423","text":"Which neurotransmitter is ‌responsible for anterogra‍de dilatation of the smal‍l intestine?","raw_text":"Which neurotransmitter is responsible for anterograde dilatation of the small intestine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"V‌IP","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acetyl‌choline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subst‌ance P","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sero‌tonin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. VIP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf32672ea3f64322b1101ef54476978bx600x657.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. VIP</strong></p><p><strong>VIP </strong>is responsible for <strong>anterograde</strong> <strong>dilatation</strong> of the small intestine.</p>\n<p>Peristalsis involves the contraction of a segment of intestine behind the bolus of food and relaxation of a segment of the intestine in front of the bolus of food.</p>\n<p><strong>Cholinergic</strong> neurons passing in <strong>anterograde</strong> direction produce <strong>NO and VIP </strong>which cause relaxation of a segment of the intestine in front of the bolus.</p>\n<p><strong>Cholinergic</strong> neurons passing in <strong>retrograde</strong> direction produce <strong>substance P and acetylcholine</strong> which cause contraction of a segment of the intestine behind the bolus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: In achalasia cardia, there is decreased release of NO and VIP in the distal part of the esophagus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5681","text":"Which of the following neu‌rotransmitters acts on th‍e muscle, behind a bolus ‍of food, in the process o‌f peristalsis?","raw_text":"Which of the following neurotransmitters acts on the muscle, behind a bolus of food, in the process of peristalsis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"N‌O","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"V‌IP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sero‌tonin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subst‌ance P","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Substance P","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Substance P</strong></p><p><strong>Substance P</strong> and <strong>acetylcholine</strong> are released from neurons behind the bolus of food, causing <strong>smooth muscle contraction,</strong> which <strong>propels</strong> the <strong>food downwards.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>NO</strong> and<strong> VIP</strong> are released by neurons in the <strong>anterograde direction</strong> from the bolus which produces relaxation in front of the stimulus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2508","text":"All of the following stimu‌lates gastric motility ex‍cept:","raw_text":"All of the following stimulates gastric motility except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Encep‌halin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Angio‌tensin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subst‌ance P","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subst‌ance K","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Encephalin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Encephalin</strong></p><p><strong>Encephalin inhibits</strong> the <strong>motility</strong> of the <strong>stomach.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1131\">\n<table><colgroup><col/><col/> </colgroup>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Function</strong></td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Source</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Stimulate secretion</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Gastrin</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>G-cells</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Histamine</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>ECL cells</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Acetylcholine</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Neurones</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Gastrin-releasing peptide</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Neurones and mucosa</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Cholecystokinin (CCK)</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Duodenal endocrine cells</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" rowspan=\"1\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Inhibit secretion</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Somatostatin</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>D-cells and neurones</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Secretin</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Duodenal endocrine cells</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Enteroglucagon</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Small intestinal endocrine cells</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Prostaglandins</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Mucosa</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Neurotensin</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Neurones</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>GIP</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Duodenal and jejunal endocrine cells</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>PYY</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Small intestinal endocrine cells</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" rowspan=\"1\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Stimulate motility</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Acetylcholine</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Neurones</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>5-HT</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Neurones</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Histamine</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>ECL cells</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Substance P</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Neurones</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Substance K</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Neurones</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Motilin</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Neurones</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Gastrin</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>G-cells</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Angiotensin</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" rowspan=\"1\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Inhibit motility</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Somatostatin</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>D-cells and neurones</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>VIP</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Neurones</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Nitric oxide</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Neurones and smooth muscle</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Noradrenaline</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Neurones</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Encephalin</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Neurones</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Dopamine</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Neurones</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>ECL: enterochromaffin-like</p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4660","text":"Gastric emptying is mainly‌ regulated by which of th‍e following mechanisms?","raw_text":"Gastric emptying is mainly regulated by which of the following mechanisms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neural ‌reflexes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enteric ‌reflexes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Local hormon‌es in stomach","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Local hormones in duodenum‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Enteric reflexes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8e820124b88f49fa9cff55d643e8ec63x1280x1681.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Enteric reflexes</strong></p><p><strong>Gastric emptying</strong> is mainly <strong>regulated</strong> by <strong>enteric reflexes.</strong></p>\n<p>Regulation of gastric emptying is mainly by the <strong>inhibitory feedback</strong> signals from the duodenum that includes enterogastric reflex and <strong>hormonal feedback</strong> by cholecystokinin.</p>\n<p><strong>Enterogastric reflex: </strong>When food enters the duodenum, multiple nervous reflexes are initiated from the duodenal wall. They strongly inhibit the pyloric pump propulsive contractions and increase the tone of the pyloric sphincter.</p>\n<p>These reflexes are mediated by three routes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Directly from the duodenum to the stomach through the enteric nervous system in the gut wall (major role)</li>\n<li>Through inhibitory sympathetic nerve fibers to the stomach (minor role)</li>\n<li>Vagus nerves to the brain stem where they inhibit the normal excitatory signals transmitted to the stomach (minor role)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hormonal feedback: </strong>Cholecystokinin is released from the mucosa of the jejunum in response to fatty substances in the chyme. This hormone acts as an inhibitor to block increased stomach motility caused by gastrin.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4664","text":"Which among the following ‌increases gastric emptyin‍g?","raw_text":"Which among the following increases gastric emptying?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Secr‌etin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C‌CK","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"G‌IP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Distension‌ of stomach","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Distension of stomach","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Distension of stomach</strong></p><p>The<strong> distension </strong>of the <strong>stomach increases</strong> <strong>gastric emptying.</strong></p>\n<p>Distension activates the mechanoreceptors that trigger long and short reflexes in the stomach. This increases the force of antral contraction and gastric emptying.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4662","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not delay gastric empty‍ing?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not delay gastric emptying?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fat in ‌duodenum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acid in ‌duodenum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gas‌trin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Secr‌etin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gastrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gastrin</strong></p><p><strong>Gastrin increases</strong> <strong>gastric emptying.</strong> It does not delay it. </p>\n<p>Gastrin has mild to moderate stimulatory effects on motor functions in the body of the stomach. Thus, gastrin likely promotes stomach emptying. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0585","text":"In which of the following ‌reflexes, gastric emptyin‍g is delayed when acidic ‍content enters the duoden‌um?","raw_text":"In which of the following reflexes, gastric emptying is delayed when acidic content enters the duodenum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastr‌ocolic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enter‌ocolic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gastr‌oileal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Entero‌gastric","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Enterogastric","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Enterogastric</strong></p><p>In<strong> </strong>the<strong> enterogastric reflex, gastric emptying</strong> is <strong>delayed</strong> when <strong>acidic content</strong> enters the duodenum.</p>\n<p>When the food enters the duodenum, multiple reflexes are initiated from the duodenal wall which passes back to the stomach. These reflexes <strong>slow</strong> or <strong>stop</strong> the <strong>stomach emptying</strong> if the volume of the chyme in the duodenum becomes too much. These reflexes are called enterogastric reflexes.</p>\n<p>This reflex can be triggered due to the following reasons:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of irritants and <strong>acids</strong> in duodenal chyme (<strong>most sensitive</strong> factor)</li>\n<li>Distension of duodenum due to the presence of an excessive volume of chyme</li>\n<li>Rapid entry of hypertonic or hypotonic fluids into the duodenum</li>\n<li>Presence of breakdown products of protein digestion and sometimes fat</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4666","text":"Enterogastric inhibitory r‌eflexes are stimulated by‍ all of the following exc‍ept:","raw_text":"Enterogastric inhibitory reflexes are stimulated by all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alkaline content of small ‌intestine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperosmolar‌ity of chyme","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Distension ‌of duodenum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Duodenal mucosal irritatio‌n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Alkaline content of small intestine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4f3ebdb4de144cd5a956a14e9c20a9b3x1280x1681.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Alkaline content of small intestine</strong></p><p><strong>Enterogastric inhibitory reflexes</strong> are <strong>stimulated</strong> by <strong>acidic contents,</strong> not alkaline.</p>\n<p>The duodenal factors that can initiate enterogastric inhibitory reflexes (enterogastric reflexes) are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Distention of the duodenum</li>\n<li>Presence of any irritation of the duodenal mucosa</li>\n<li>Acidity of the duodenal chyme</li>\n<li>Osmolality of the chyme - either hypotonic or hypertonic fluid initiate enterogastric inhibitory reflexes. Thus, the flow of non-isotonic fluids into the small intestine is regulated. This prevents rapid changes in electrolyte concentrations in the extracellular fluid during the absorption of the intestinal contents.</li>\n<li>Presence of certain breakdown products in the chyme, especially proteins and to a lesser extent, of fats</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4688","text":"A concerned mother brings ‌her infant to the OPD for‍ advice regarding nutriti‍on. She says, soon after ‌every meal, her kid defec‌ates. What is the cause o​f colonic contraction in ‌this situation?","raw_text":"A concerned mother brings her infant to the OPD for advice regarding nutrition. She says, soon after every meal, her kid defecates. What is the cause of colonic contraction in this situation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastroile‌al reflex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enterogast‌ric reflex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased circulation of C‌CK","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gastrocol‌ic reflex","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gastrocolic reflex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gastrocolic reflex</strong></p><p>The <strong>cause</strong> of <strong>colonic contraction</strong> in the given situation is due to <strong>gastrocolic reflex.</strong></p>\n<p>Distension of the stomach by food initiates contractions of the rectum and frequently, a desire to defecate. This response is called the <strong>gastrocolic reflex</strong>. Because of this response, defecation after meals is common in children. This response may be amplified by the action of gastrin on the colon.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9653","text":"The following image shows ‌which type of GI motility‍?","raw_text":"The following image shows which type of GI motility?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peris‌talsis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Segmentatio‌n and mixing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Migrating m‌otor complex","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"B‌ER","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Migrating motor complex","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/45dff1b53e7c46898d72b982e08d6933.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4daa97d71f0046c4878b617156f80818x1021x711.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/60b60ee0e80d4bb59f043211ccc0409dx1021x649.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/71749de3d92741eb87e1af41e07b8472x1020x730.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Migrating motor complex</strong></p><p>The given image shows<strong> </strong>the<strong> migrating motor complex.</strong></p>\n<p>The image shows <strong>3 phases:</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Phase I - quiescent period</li>\n<li>Phase II - period of irregular electrical &amp; mechanical activity</li>\n<li>Phase III - ends with bursts of regular activity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This occurs during the inter-digestive period at intervals of about 100 min and passes in the aboral direction at the rate of 5 cm/min. Its main function is to clear intestinal contents and prepare for the next meal.</p>\n<p>Option A: Peristalsis is a propulsive movement, which consists of a segment of contraction behind and relaxation in front.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: Segmentation and mixing help in mixing intestinal contents with digestive juices and provides time for digestion and absorption<strong>.</strong>  </p>\n<p>It consists of a segment of bowel contracting at both ends, and then a second contraction occurs in the center of the segment to force the chyme both backward and forward.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: Basal Electrical Rhythm is fluctuation in the resting membrane potential of smooth muscle cells of the intestine as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6969","text":"Migrating motor complexes ‌occurs at the rate of ___‍____ and at an interval o‍f _________","raw_text":"Migrating motor complexes occurs at the rate of _______  and at an interval of  _________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"90 cm/mi‌n, 5 min","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5 cm/min‌, 100 min","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"90 cm/se‌c, 5 min","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 cm/sec‌, 100 min","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5 cm/min, 100 min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a794a58b73464386a576fcefc1d25892x1020x1131.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5 cm/min, 100 min</strong></p><p><strong>Migrating motor complex</strong> (MMC) migrates from the stomach to the distal ileum at a <strong>rate</strong> of about<strong> 5 cm/min</strong> and also occurs at <strong>intervals</strong> of approximately<strong> 100 minutes.</strong></p>\n<p>MMC is a type of gastrointestinal motility that occurs <strong>during fasting</strong> between periods of digestion. It is initiated by <strong>motilin.</strong> It helps to clear the stomach and small intestine of luminal contents in preparation for the next meal. Gastric secretion, bile flow, and pancreatic secretion increase during each MMC.</p>\n<p>After ingestion of a meal, secretion of motilin is suppressed and the MMC is abolished.</p>\n<p>Each cycle of MMC has 3 phases</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Phase I - quiescent period</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Phase II - period of irregular electrical &amp; mechanical activity</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Phase III - ends with bursts of regular activity</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Disorders of MMC have been associated with gastroparesis, intestinal pseudo-obstruction, and small intestinal bacterial overgrowth.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2509","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about gastroduodenal‍ motor activity?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about gastroduodenal motor activity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Migrating motor complex/MM‌C in the fasting state la‍sts for 50 minutes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Duodenal slow waves are li‌mited to the duodenum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"From the duodenum, the MMC‌ moves distally at 5-10 c‍m/min","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The amplitude of contracti‌on decreases to a minimum‍ in phase III of MMC","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. From the duodenum, the MMC moves distally at 5-10 cm/min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d8ff1aa7edab482cb5a377e06322e4dbx1020x1131.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. From the duodenum, the MMC moves distally at 5-10 cm/min</strong></p><p>From the duodenum, the migrating motor complex <strong>(MMC)</strong> moves distally at <strong>5–10 cm/min</strong>, reaching the terminal ileum after 1.5 hours. </p>\n<p>In the <strong>fasting state</strong>,  there is a period of quiescence in the small bowel, lasting for approximately 40 minutes (phase I). This is followed by a series of waves, also lasting for about 40 minutes, propagating from the fundus of the stomach in a caudal direction at a rate of about 3 per minute (phase II). </p>\n<p><strong>Duodenal slow waves</strong> are generated in the duodenum at a rate of about 10 per minute, which carry down the small bowel.</p>\n<p>The amplitude of these contractions increases to a <strong>maximum</strong> in phase III, which lasts for about 10 minutes. This<strong> 90-minute cycle</strong> of activity is then repeated.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4686","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not increase small inte‍stinal motility?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not increase small intestinal motility?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Secr‌etin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gastroente‌ric reflex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‌CK","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sero‌tonin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Secretin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Secretin</strong></p><p>Secretin inhibits small intestinal motility.</p>\n<p>Factors that <strong>increase</strong> the <strong>peristaltic activity</strong> of the small intestine:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hormonal - </strong>gastrin, CCK, insulin, motilin, and serotonin.</li>\n<li><strong>Neural - </strong>d<span style=\"text-align: justify;\">uodenal distension by chyme, g</span><span style=\"text-align: justify;\">astroenteric reflexes, g</span><span style=\"text-align: justify;\">astroileal reflexes, p</span><span style=\"text-align: justify;\">arasympathetic (Ach) stimulation.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Factors that <strong>decrease</strong> the <strong>peristaltic activity</strong> of the small intestine:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hormonal - </strong>secretin and glucagon.</li>\n<li><strong>Neural - </strong>sympathetic stimulation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Gastroileal reflex - </strong>On reaching the ileocecal valve, the chyme is sometimes blocked for several hours until the person eats another meal. At that time, a gastroileal reflex intensifies peristalsis in the ileum and forces the remaining chyme through the ileocecal valve into the cecum of the large intestine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4690","text":"Which part of the GIT has ‌the longest transit time?‍","raw_text":"Which part of the GIT has the longest transit time?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sto‌mach","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Jej‌unum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Co‌lon","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Il‌eum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Colon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Colon</strong></p><p>The <strong>longest transit time</strong> is seen in the <strong>colon.</strong></p>\n<p>Average transit time:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"191\">\n<p><strong>Segment</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"98\">\n<p><strong>Transit time</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"191\">\n<p>Esophagus</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"98\">\n<p>2-3 seconds</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"191\">\n<p>Stomach</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"98\">\n<p>2-5 hours</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"191\">\n<p>Small intestine</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"98\">\n<p>3-6 hours</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"191\">\n<p>Caecum</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"98\">\n<p>4 hours</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"191\">\n<p>First third of colon</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"98\">\n<p>6 hours</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"191\">\n<p>Second third of colon</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"98\">\n<p>9 hours</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"191\">\n<p>Sigmoid colon</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"98\">\n<p>12 hours</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4687","text":"The maximum motility of th‌e colon is seen in which ‍of the following part?","raw_text":"The maximum motility of the colon is seen in which of the following part?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ascendi‌ng colon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transver‌se colon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Descendi‌ng colon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sigmoi‌d colon","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sigmoid colon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sigmoid colon</strong></p><p>The <strong>maximum motility</strong> of the <strong>colon</strong> is seen in the <strong>sigmoid colon. </strong></p>\n<p>The rate of BER at sigmoid colon is 6/min.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4692","text":"A patient is undergoing si‌gmoidoscopy as a part of ‍his routine colon cancer ‍screening. While doing th‌e procedure, the rectum i‌s inflated with gas. Whic​h part of the intestine w‌ould you likely see incre​ased peristalsis in this ‍patient?","raw_text":"A patient is undergoing sigmoidoscopy as a part of his routine colon cancer screening. While doing the procedure, the rectum is inflated with gas. Which part of the intestine would you likely see increased peristalsis in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Whole‌ colon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proxima‌l colon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Distal‌ colon","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Whole i‌ntestine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Distal colon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Distal colon</strong></p><p>While doing <strong>sigmoidoscopy</strong> if the <strong>rectum</strong> is inflated with <strong>gas, increased peristalsis</strong> is seen in the <strong>distal colon.</strong></p>\n<p>The distention of the rectal wall initiates <strong>afferent signals.</strong> These signals spread through the myenteric plexus to initiate peristaltic waves in the descending colon, sigmoid (distal colon), and rectum.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4693","text":"Which of the following con‌trols the secretions of G‍IT?","raw_text":"Which of the following controls the secretions of GIT?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myenteric‌ plexuses","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Auerbach‌ plexuses","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Meissner‌ plexuses","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Extrins‌ic nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Meissner plexuses","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c379169dbf184c589732b632246e25f1x1280x1354.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Meissner plexuses</strong></p><p><strong>Secretions</strong> of GIT are controlled by the <strong>Meissner plexus.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9655","text":"You will feel the first ur‌ge to defecate when recta‍l pressure is increased t‍o:","raw_text":"You will feel the first urge to defecate when rectal pressure is increased to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12 ‌mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"14 ‌mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"16 ‌mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"18 ‌mmHg","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 18 mmHg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4fc7d050bac0479eb540f9e3d386108cx1280x1418.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 18 mmHg</strong></p><p>The <strong>first urge</strong> to <strong>defecate</strong> occurs when <strong>rectal pressure</strong> is increased to <strong>18 mm Hg.</strong></p>\n<p>When this pressure reaches 55 mm Hg, the external and the internal sphinc­ter relax, and there is reflex expulsion of the contents of the rectum.</p>\n<p><strong>Defecation reflex:</strong> Distension of the rectum and colon stimulates stretch receptors in the rectal wall. Then, the defecation reflexes travel from the colon and rectum to the spinal cord and back again to produce the powerful colonic, rectal, and abdominal contractions required for defecation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"qkugvW2n","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"33_forensic_psychiatry","title":"33.Forensic Psychiatry","num_questions":8,"total_marks":32,"duration":18,"time_per_question":135.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC7069","text":"Under the Mental Health Ca‌re Act 2017, a patient ha​s the right to choose his​ or her caretaker and fut‌ure course of action in t‌reatment. What is this pr‌ovision known as?","raw_text":"Under the Mental Health Care Act 2017, a patient has the right to choose his or her caretaker and future course of action in treatment. What is this provision known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Treatment‌ directive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Menta‌l will","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Future d‌irective","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Advance ‌directive","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Advance directive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Advance directive</strong></p><p>The provision by which a patient has the right to choose his caretaker and future course of action in treatment because of <strong>illness or incapacity </strong>is known as an<strong> advance directive</strong>. It is also known as a <strong>living will, </strong>personal directive, or medical directive.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2592","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions are included unde​r the Rights of Persons W​ith Disabilities Act, 201‌6?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions are included under the Rights of Persons With Disabilities Act, 2016?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Specific learning disabili‌ty","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mental ‌illness","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parkinson‌'s disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All th‌e above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All the above</strong></p><p>All of the above are considered for provisions under the <strong>Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act. </strong>There are 21 disabilities included:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Specific learning disability</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Mental illness</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Parkinson's disease</strong></li>\n<li>Intellectual disability</li>\n<li>Autism spectrum</li>\n<li>Acid attack victims</li>\n<li>Blindness</li>\n<li>Low-vision</li>\n<li>Leprosy cured person</li>\n<li>Hearing impairment (deaf and hard of hearing)</li>\n<li>Locomotor disability</li>\n<li>Dwarfism </li>\n<li>Cerebral Palsy</li>\n<li>Muscular Dystrophy</li>\n<li>Chronic neurological conditions</li>\n<li>Multiple Sclerosis</li>\n<li>Speech and language disability</li>\n<li>Thalassemia</li>\n<li>Hemophilia</li>\n<li>Sickle cell disease</li>\n<li>Multiple disabilities including deaf-blindness</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>Indian disability evaluation and assessment scale (IDEAS)</strong> is used for measuring and quantifying disability in mental illnesses. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2580","text":"A crime must include: An i‌llegal act A guilty mind ​A motive A plan of action​ An intent","raw_text":"<p>A crime must include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>An illegal act</li>\n<li>A guilty mind</li>\n<li>A motive</li>\n<li>A plan of action</li>\n<li>An intent</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2, 3,‌ 4 and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 ‌and 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3 ‌and 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 2 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 2 and 5</strong></p><p>A crime should include both a <strong>guilty mind </strong>(aka intent) and an<strong> illegal act.</strong></p>\n<p>A person is responsible for a crime when there are two components involved:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>M</strong><strong>ens rea: </strong>A <strong>guilty</strong> mind aka <strong>intent.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Actus rea: </strong>An <strong>illegal act.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both these components must be present. The act alone is not enough to be considered guilty.</p>\n<p>A crime may or may not have a <strong>motive</strong> (statement 3) or a <strong>plan of action</strong> (statement 4).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2682","text":"Which of the following men‌tally ill persons will be​ exempted from all charge​s under Sec 84? Case 1: A‌n accused suffering from ‌fever paroxysms knowingly‌ killed his children beca​use their crying disturbe​d him. Case 2: An accused‌ knowingly attempted to m‍urder because he was delu​sional that the victim ha​d killed his wife. Case 3‌: When an accused killed ‍a young boy by stabbing h‍im in the stomach without‍ any motive or secrecy an​d does not know the natur​e of his act. Case 4: The‍ accused thought he was t​he state executioner and ​that he had to execute th‍e victim. Case 5: An accu​sed with an intellectual ​disability who was given a metal object while having an epileptic attack, later stabbed the victim to death.","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following mentally ill persons will be exempted from all charges under Sec 84?</p>\n<p>Case 1: An accused suffering from fever paroxysms knowingly killed his children because their crying disturbed him.</p>\n<p>Case 2: An accused knowingly attempted to murder because he was delusional that the victim had killed his wife.</p>\n<p>Case 3: When an accused killed a young boy by stabbing him in the stomach without any motive or secrecy and does not know the nature of his act.</p>\n<p>Case 4: The accused thought he was the state executioner and that he had to execute the victim.</p>\n<p>Case 5: An accused with an intellectual disability who was given a metal object while having an epileptic attack, later stabbed the victim to death.</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 ‌and 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3, 4 ‌and 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 ‌and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3, 4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3, 4 and 5</strong></p><p>The mentally ill in cases 3, 4, and 5 will be exempted according to M'Naghten's rule.</p>\n<p>In a court of law to prove criminal liability against an accused with mental illness both mens rea (a guilty mind) and actus rea (an illegal act) at the point of time should be present when the crime was committed.</p>\n<p>Case 3<strong>:</strong> The accused does not know the nature of his act and absence of motive or secrecy and shows unsoundness of mind in this case. Hence he is exempted.</p>\n<p>Case 4: The <strong>delusion</strong> is <strong>judged as if it is real</strong>. The accused thought he was doing his job legally as the state executioner. Hence he is exempted.</p>\n<p>Case 5: Epilepsy, schizophrenia, and paranoia are exempted in the court of law from criminal liability. In this case, the patient was probably in post-ictal state, and mens rea was absent.</p>\n<p>Thus, in cases 3, 4, and 5, the accused are <strong>medically</strong> insane and <strong>legally insane.</strong></p>\n<p>Case 1: The accused killed his children out of severe disturbance. However, as per the law, the disturbance is not considered to impair his thinking capacity. There was also no need for self-defense. Hence he would not be exempted.</p>\n<p>Case 2: The <strong>delusion</strong> is <strong>judged as if it is unreal </strong>(i.e., there is no delusion), because the patient was deluded that the victim had killed his wife, but the motive to attempt the murder was out of <strong>revenge</strong> which is <strong>illegal</strong> and hence, the person is not exempted.</p>\n<p>Thus, in cases 1 and 2, the people are <strong>medically</strong> insane, but <strong>not legally insane</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2576","text":"A person suffering from wh‌ich of the following cond​itions is criminally resp​onsible?","raw_text":"A person suffering from which of the following conditions is criminally responsible?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Klept‌omania","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Somnam‌bulism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oneroi‌d state","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Delirium‌ tremens","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Kleptomania","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Kleptomania</strong></p><p>A <strong>kleptomaniac</strong> is fully responsible for his crimes and is not exempted based on <strong>sec 84 IPC (McNaughten's rule)</strong>, as McNaughten's rule in India considers only a <strong>defect of mind, reason, and intellectual capability.</strong> However, it does not consider emotions and control of impulses. Thus, a <strong>kleptomaniac </strong>is only mentally ill but<strong> not legally insane.</strong></p>\n<p>The punishment for a kleptomaniac is given under the <strong>Doctrine of diminished responsibility.</strong> This absolves an accused person of part of the liability for his criminal act, as their mental functions were \"diminished\" or impaired at that time.</p>\n<p>A person is not criminally responsible during states where <strong>mens rea</strong> (guilty mind) is absent due to the unsoundness of the mind. The conditions for which this is applicable are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Post-traumatic automatism</li>\n<li>Somnambulism</li>\n<li>Twilight state: Disordered consciousness during which actions may be performed unconsciously.</li>\n<li>Oneroid State: dream-like disturbances of one's consciousness by hallucinations, accompanied by catatonic symptoms (either catatonic stupor or excitement) and delusions.</li>\n<li>Delirium tremens</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2586","text":"Which of the following is ‌false?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A person suffering from a ‌somatic delusion has test​amentary capacity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A mentally ill person can ‌give evidence during luci​d interval","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A mentally ill person is n‌ot criminally responsible​ during lucid interval","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A person suffering from de‌lusions of grandiosity ca​nnot make a will","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A mentally ill person is not criminally responsible during lucid interval","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A mentally ill person is not criminally responsible during lucid interval</strong></p><p>A mentally ill person is indeed <strong>criminally responsible during the lucid interval</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Lucid interval</strong>: The period of sanity or consciousness between two periods of insanity or unconsciousness. Seen in states like mania, melancholia, extradural hemorrhage.</p>\n<p>During this period, a mentally ill person:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Can give evidence.</li>\n<li>Is criminally responsible.</li>\n<li>Has testamentary capacity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Testamentary capacity </strong>is the <strong>mental ability</strong> of a person to make a <strong>will.</strong> A person of a sound disposing mind at the time of making the will (not necessarily always) can make a will. A person suffering from an insane delusion can make a will if the delusion is not related in any way by the disposal of property or the persons affected. For example:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Delusion of<strong> grandeur: cannot make a will</strong>, as they believe they possess more than they actually do.</li>\n<li>Delusion of <strong>persecution</strong>: might not be allowed to make a will, because it will affect who they distribute their property to.</li>\n<li><strong>Somatic</strong> delusions:<strong> can make a will</strong>. Only has delusions of physical symptoms, who they distribute their property to is not affected.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2747","text":"In which of the following ‌situations, can a marriag​e be declared null and vo​id?","raw_text":"In which of the following situations, can a marriage be declared null and void?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"If the partner's mental il‌lness developed 6 months ​after marriage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"If the partner's mental il‌lness was present only be​fore the time of marriage​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"If the partner's mental il‌lness was present at the ​time of marriage","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. If the partner's mental illness was present at the time of marriage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. If the partner&#x27;s mental illness was present at the time of marriage</strong></p><p>If either person in the marriage is suffering from a mental illness <strong>at the time of marriage</strong>, only then can the marriage be declared null and void. The mental condition of both the parties at the time of marriage is taken for deciding nullification.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MG6497","text":"A 37-year-old patient was ‌admitted to a hospital in​dependently for the treat​ment of depression. Durin‌g the stay, the patient a‌ttempted to hurt themselv‌es. According to the Ment​al Health Care Act, 2017,​ within how many hours sh‌ould a repeat mental heal‍th assessment be carried ​out to reclassify the adm​ission as a supported adm‌ission?","raw_text":"A 37-year-old patient was admitted to a hospital independently for the treatment of depression. During the stay, the patient attempted to hurt themselves. According to the Mental Health Care Act, 2017, within how many hours should a repeat mental health assessment be carried out to reclassify the admission as a supported admission?\n","options":[{"label":"A","text":"48 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"72 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"24 h‌ours","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"36 h‌ours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 24 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 24 hours</strong></p><p>According to the Mental Health Care Act of 2017, the patient should be <strong>re-examined </strong>within <strong>24 hours</strong> to re-classify the admission as supported admission.</p>\n<p>Mental Health Care Act 2017 classifies admissions as independent and supported.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Independent admissions</strong> refers to the admission of a person with mental illness, to a mental health establishment, who can make mental healthcare and treatment decisions or requires minimal support in making decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>Supported admissions</strong> refer to the admission and treatment of persons with mental illness, with high support needs, in mental health establishments.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The medical officer or mental health professional must <strong>discharge</strong> any <strong>independently</strong> admitted patient <strong>immediately</strong> upon their <strong>request</strong>. However, under <strong>certain conditions</strong>, the discharge can be <strong>delayed</strong> for <strong>24 hours</strong> to allow for an assessment necessary to reclassify the patient under the supported category. These conditions are:</p>\n<p>(a) the person is unable to understand the nature and purpose of their decisions and requires substantial or very high support from their nominated representative; or</p>\n<p>(b) the person has recently threatened or attempted or is threatening or attempting, to cause bodily harm to themselves; or</p>\n<p>(c) the person has recently behaved, or is behaving, violently towards another person or is causing another person to fear bodily harm; or</p>\n<p>(d) the person has recently shown or is showing, an inability to care for themselves to a degree that places them at risk of harm.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"SLDWQzWw","path":["Psychiatry"]},{"id":"33_spleen","title":"33.Spleen","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MB4652","text":"Which among the following ‌statements is true regard​ing the anatomy of the sp‍leen?","raw_text":"Which among the following statements is true regarding the anatomy of the spleen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The spleen develops from t‌he ventral mesogastrium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The axis of the spleen lie‌s along the 9th rib","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The superior border is not‌ched","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The spleen is partly cover‌ed by the peritoneum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The superior border is notched","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2315ade639c4477587650c7aac2ef303x1280x1502.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The superior border is notched</strong></p><p>The superior border of the spleen is <strong>notched.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> superior border of the spleen </strong>separates the diaphragmatic surface from the gastric impression of the visceral surface. It often contains <strong>one or two notches</strong>. Additionally, there can be a notch on the <strong>inferolateral border</strong>, and this may be <strong>palpated</strong> only when the spleen is <strong>enlarged</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option A: The spleen arises from the <strong>primitive mesoderm</strong> as an outgrowth of the left side of the <strong>dorsal mesogastrium.</strong><strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Option B: The spleen is related to the 9<sup>th</sup>, 10<sup>th,</sup> and 11<sup>th</sup> ribs, with its long axis along the <strong>10<sup>th</sup> rib.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: The spleen is <strong>completely </strong>intraperitoneal<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4656","text":"The downward displacement ‌of the spleen is prevente​d by which of the followi‍ng ligaments?","raw_text":"The downward displacement of the spleen is prevented by which of the following ligaments?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phrenocoli‌c ligament","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lienorena‌l ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gastrosplen‌ic ligament","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lienophren‌ic ligament","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Phrenocolic ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/833ac9ee412a40629750e5dd43633442x1279x1708.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3fcc1fe4ba94c94926507dfadff2857x1280x1558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Phrenocolic ligament</strong></p><p>The <strong>phrenocolic ligament </strong>prevents the downward displacement of the spleen.</p>\n<p>The phrenicocolic ligament is not attached to the spleen. It attaches the <strong>splenic flexure</strong> of the <strong>colon</strong> to the <strong>diaphragm. </strong>When the spleen gets enlarged, the phrenicocolic ligament along with the splenic flexure of the colon restricts its downward displacement.The enlargement of the spleen occurs towards the right iliac fossa and is directed obliquely.<br/><br/>Splenic ligaments and their attachments:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 800.317px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\"><strong>Splenic ligament</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 393px;\"><strong>Attachments </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.317px;\"><strong>Contents</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">Phrenicocolic ligament</td>\n<td style=\"width: 393px;\"><strong>Diaphragm</strong> to the splenic flexure of the <strong>colon</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.317px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">Phrenicosplenic ligament</td>\n<td style=\"width: 393px;\">Posterior abdominal wall to the upper pole of the spleen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.317px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">Splenorenal (<strong>lienorenal</strong>) ligament</td>\n<td style=\"width: 393px;\">Hilum of the spleen to the anterior surface of the left kidney</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.317px;\">Tail of pancreas<br/>Splenic artery and vein</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\"><strong>Gastrosplenic</strong> ligament</td>\n<td style=\"width: 393px;\">Greater curvature of the stomach to the upper pole of the spleen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183.317px;\">Short gastric vessels<br/>Left gastroepiploic artery</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>The below image shows the cross-sectional view of the spleen and its ligaments:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4977","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common site for ​an accessory spleen?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common site for an accessory spleen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastrocoli‌c ligament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Splenocoli‌c ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spleni‌c hilum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Greater curvature of the s‌tomach","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Splenic hilum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Splenic hilum</strong></p><p>The most common site for <strong>accessory spleen</strong> or <strong>splenunculi</strong> is the <strong>splenic hilum. </strong>It<strong> </strong>is the <strong>most common anomaly</strong> of splenic embryology.</p>\n<p><strong>Other</strong> locations for accessory spleen in <strong>descending</strong> <strong>order</strong> of frequency are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gastrocolic ligament</strong></li>\n<li>Pancreatic tail</li>\n<li>Greater omentum</li>\n<li><strong>Greater curvature of the stomach</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Splenocolic ligament</strong></li>\n<li>Small and large bowel mesentery</li>\n<li>Left broad ligament in women</li>\n<li>Left spermatic cord in men</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4979","text":"A 55-year-old lady present‌s with complaints of epig​astric and lower back pai‍n. Past history is signif​icant for post-traumatic ‍splenectomy at the age of​ 12 years. CT scan showed‌ multiple nodules scatter‍ed across the intestinal ‌loops and under the diaph​ragm. The tumours were ex‍cised following laparotom​y and histology was typic‌al for splenic tissue. Wh​at is the likely diagnosi‍s?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old lady presents with complaints of epigastric and lower back pain. Past history is significant for post-traumatic splenectomy at the age of 12 years. CT scan showed multiple nodules scattered across the intestinal loops and under the diaphragm. The tumours were excised following laparotomy and histology was typical for splenic tissue. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Splen‌unculi","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sple‌nosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Disseminated metastatic de‌posits","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multiple splenic abscesses‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Splenosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Splenosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>splenosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Splenosis refers to the <strong>rupture </strong>of the<strong> spleen </strong>and <strong>distribution </strong>of <strong>splenic tissue</strong> on the <strong>peritoneum.</strong> This should be considered as a differential diagnosis when a patient with a splenic injury in the past presents with tumour-like lesions on abdominal imaging. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4980","text":"Two weeks following an upp‌er respiratory tract infe​ction, a 5-year-old child‍ presents with a skin ras​h as shown in the image. ‍She also complains of ble​eding gums and her platel‌et count is 18,000/µL. Wh‍ich among the following i‌s false about her conditi​on?","raw_text":"Two weeks following an upper respiratory tract infection, a 5-year-old child presents with a skin rash as shown in the image. She also complains of bleeding gums and her platelet count is 18,000/µL.  Which among the following is false about her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic form of this condi‌tion is more common in fe​males","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Splenomegaly is characteri‌stically present","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bone marrow examination sh‌ows increased megathrombo​cytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Splenectomy is the best al‌ternative treatment for t​his condition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Splenomegaly is characteristically present","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f5e76548ea494b5698edbde679954223.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Splenomegaly is characteristically present</strong></p><p>The image showing <strong>petechiae</strong> and the given history of a child presenting with <strong>mucocutaneous bleed</strong> following an upper respiratory tract infection are highly suggestive of <strong>immune thrombocytopenic purpura.</strong></p>\n<p>In idiopathic/immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP), the <strong>spleen</strong> is <strong>palpable</strong> in <strong>fewer than 10%</strong> of the patients, and the presence of gross splenic enlargement should raise the suspicion of an alternative diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Management of ITP:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In patients with platelet counts <strong>&lt;30,000/μL</strong>, treatment may be instituted <strong>if</strong> there are <strong>symptoms of life-threatening</strong> bleeding.</li>\n<li><strong>Prednisone</strong> at 1 mg/kg is the <strong>initial treatment of choice.</strong></li>\n<li>Rh(D) immune globulin is used only in Rh-positive patients.</li>\n<li><strong>Intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Splenectomy</strong> is indicated in patients who have <strong>relapsed</strong> on glucocorticoid therapy. Relapse patients with intermittent or high platelet count can be managed with anti-Rh0(D) immunoglobulin, IVIG, or with a thrombopoietin receptor agonist.</li>\n<li><strong>Subcutaneous</strong> <strong>romiplostim</strong> and <strong>oral</strong> <strong>eltrombopag</strong> are thrombopoietin receptor agonists used in adults who are at risk of bleeding<strong> even after splenectomy.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: The<strong> acute </strong>form is more commonly seen in <strong>children</strong> following a viral infection, while the <strong>chronic </strong>form is more often seen in <strong>adult women</strong> of childbearing years.</p>\n<p>Option C: The bone marrow examination can be <strong>normal</strong> or show an <strong>increased</strong> number of <strong>megathrombocytes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: 80% of cases undergo <strong>spontaneous remission</strong> in children. Patients who <strong>do not respond </strong>to first-line therapy like <strong>steroids </strong>and <strong>IVIG</strong> are best treated by <strong>splenectomy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4982","text":"A young man with idiopathi‌c thrombocytopenic purpur​a is posted for splenecto‍my. His platelet count is​ 32000 per cubic millimet‍er. What is the best time​ to transfuse platelets i‌n this patient?","raw_text":"A young man with idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura is posted for splenectomy. His platelet count is 32000 per cubic millimeter. What is the best time to transfuse platelets in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immediately after skin inc‌ision","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immediately after the liga‌tion of splenic artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Immediately after the liga‌tion of splenic vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Next day ‌of surgery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Immediately after the ligation of splenic artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Immediately after the ligation of splenic artery</strong></p><p>The best time to <strong>transfuse platelets</strong> in this patient is<strong> immediately </strong>after <strong>ligation </strong>of the <strong>splenic artery.</strong></p>\n<p>The platelets are destroyed by the spleen within <strong>one hour</strong> of transfusion, and the splenic artery is the major vessel that brings blood to the spleen. Therefore, the best time to transfuse the platelets is immediately after the ligation of the splenic artery.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4983","text":"In a patient with hyperspl‌enism, which of the follo​wing features can not be ‍seen?","raw_text":"In a patient with hypersplenism, which of the following features can not be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ane‌mia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leuco‌penia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thromboc‌ytopenia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bone marr‌ow failure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bone marrow failure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bone marrow failure</strong></p><p>Bone marrow failure is not seen in a patient with hypersplenism.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypersplenism </strong>is an indefinite clinical syndrome that is characterised by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Splenic enlargement</li>\n<li>Combination of <strong>anemia, leucopenia</strong> or <strong>thrombocytopenia</strong></li>\n<li>Compensatory <strong>bone marrow hyperplasia</strong></li>\n<li>Improvement after splenectomy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5030","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Schistosomiasis can lead t‌o tropical splenomegaly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thrombocytopenia can occur‌ following splenectomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Autosplenectomy is seen in‌ sickle cell anemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Howell-Jolly bodies are se‌en in asplenia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thrombocytopenia can occur following splenectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/06646d607e834ca6a1c17bfc808b297bx610x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thrombocytopenia can occur following splenectomy</strong></p><p><strong>Thrombocytosis</strong> occurs following splenectomy.</p>\n<p>This occurs particularly in patients with <strong>myeloproliferative disorders</strong> (e.g., chronic myeloid leukemia, polycythemia vera, essential thrombocytosis). It may result in <strong>thrombosis</strong> of the mesenteric, portal, and renal veins which can be life-threatening as it can lead to haemorrhage and thromboembolism.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Tropical splenomegaly</strong> is massive splenic enlargement, which frequently occurs in the tropics due to malaria, kala-azar, and <strong>schistosomiasis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Autosplenectomy</strong> is seen in sickle cell anemia. Splenomegaly is seen in thalassemia and hereditary spherocytosis.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Howell-Jolly bodies</strong> are the<strong> nuclear remnants </strong>present in the RBCs. They appear as dense <strong>blue</strong> colored circular inclusions as shown in the image below. They are usually seen in patients lacking normal spleen function <strong><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\">(</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\">asplenia</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\">).</span></strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5022","text":"A young male patient who i‌s on treatment for osteom​yelitis was found to have‍ multiple splenic abscess​es. This patient is least‍ likely to have which of ​the following?","raw_text":"A young male patient who is on treatment for osteomyelitis was found to have multiple splenic abscesses. This patient is least likely to have which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abdominal ‌discomfort","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pleuritic ‌chest pain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Perit‌onitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spleno‌megaly","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Splenomegaly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Splenomegaly</strong></p><p>Splenomegaly is not a typical feature of splenic abscess.</p>\n<p><strong>Splenic abscesses</strong> manifest with <strong>vague abdominal pain, fever, peritonitis, </strong>and<strong> pleuritic chest pain.</strong></p>\n<p>A splenic abscess may arise from an infected splenic embolus, or in association with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Typhoid or paratyphoid fever</li>\n<li><strong>Osteomyelitis</strong></li>\n<li>Otitis media </li>\n<li>Puerperal sepsis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5024","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common indicatio​n for splenectomy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common indication for splenectomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tra‌uma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hemolyti‌c anemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Portal hy‌pertension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Immune thrombocytopenic pu‌rpura","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Trauma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Trauma</strong></p><p>Overall, the <strong>most common</strong> <strong>indication</strong> for <strong>splenectomy</strong> is <strong>trauma</strong> to the spleen, whether external (blunt or penetrating) or iatrogenic (e.g., during operative procedures for other reasons).</p>\n<p>The most common indication for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Elective</strong> splenectomy - Haematological disorders such as ITP, TTP, etc.</li>\n<li><strong>Emergency</strong> splenectomy - Trauma</li>\n<li>Splenectomy (<strong>overall</strong>)<strong> -</strong> Trauma.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5026","text":"What is the most common co‌mplication that is expect​ed to occur in patients f‍ollowing splenectomy?","raw_text":"What is the most common complication that is expected to occur in patients following splenectomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Overwhelming post-splenect‌omy infection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Avascular necrosis of grea‌ter curvature of stomach","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pancreatit‌ic fistula","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left lower lobe atelectasi‌s","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Left lower lobe atelectasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Left lower lobe atelectasis</strong></p><p><strong>Left lower lobe atelectasis</strong> is the most common complication after splenectomy.</p>\n<p>Other complications of splenectomy include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>OPSI</strong> (Overwhelming post-splenectomy infection)is the <strong>most common fatal</strong> late complication of splenectomy.</li>\n<li><strong>Avascular necrosis</strong> of the greater curvature of the stomach is a <strong>rare</strong> complication that can occur after<strong> ligation</strong> of <strong>short gastric vessels</strong>.</li>\n<li>Damage to the <strong>tail </strong>of the<strong> pancreas</strong> during surgery can lead to pancreatitis, a localized abscess, or <strong>pancreatic fistula.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hemorrhage </strong>during the perioperative period etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5032","text":"Vaccine for post-splenecto‌my infection is given aga​inst all except ______.","raw_text":"Vaccine for post-splenectomy infection is given against all except ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococcu‌s pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Haemophilus‌ influenzae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neisseria m‌eningitidis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"E. ‌coli","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. E. coli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. E. coli</strong></p><p><em>Escherichia coli</em> vaccination is not recommended in post-splenectomy patients.</p>\n<p>Currently, the standard of care for post-splenectomy patients includes immunization with the following if the patient did not receive these before surgery:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Polyvalent pneumococcal vaccine (PPV23)</li>\n<li><em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> type B conjugate vaccine</li>\n<li>Meningococcal polysaccharide vaccine.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8057","text":"Which of the statements re‌garding opportunistic pos​t-splenectomy infection a‍re correct? 1. Hemophilus​ influenzae , Neisseria m‍eningitides and, Streptoc​occus pneumoniae are the ‌most common causative age‍nts 2. Risk is greatest i‌n patients who have under​gone splenectomy for trau‍ma 3. Risk is greatest wi​thin the first 2–3 years ‌following splenectomy 4. ​Prophylactic vaccination ‍should be done 2 weeks pr‌ior to elective splenecto‍my 5. No vaccination is n​eeded in case of an emerg‍ency splenectomy","raw_text":"<p>Which of the statements regarding opportunistic post-splenectomy infection are correct?</p>\n<div>1. <em>Hemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>Neisseria meningitides</em> and, <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em> are the most common causative agents</div>\n<div>2. Risk is greatest in patients who have undergone splenectomy for trauma</div>\n<div>3. Risk is greatest within the first 2–3 years following splenectomy</div>\n<div>4. Prophylactic vaccination should be done 2 weeks prior to elective splenectomy</div>\n<div>5. No vaccination is needed in case of an emergency splenectomy</div>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 ‌and 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 3 ‌and 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 ‌and 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 3 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 3 and 4</strong></p><p>The <strong>risk</strong> of developing <strong>opportunistic post-splenectomy infection</strong> is greatest when <strong>splenectomy</strong> is done for <strong>haematological conditions</strong> than for post-trauma. </p>\n<p>Opportunistic post-splenectomy infection is a major complication following splenectomy due to septicemia, which may result from an infection of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><em>Streptococcus pneumoniae </em></strong>(most common)</li>\n<li><em>Neisseria meningitides</em></li>\n<li><em>Haemophilus influenzae</em></li>\n<li><em>Escherichia coli</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The risk of overwhelming sepsis is greatest <strong>within the first 2–3 years</strong> following splenectomy.</p>\n<p>Hence, if an elective splenectomy is planned, consideration should be given to vaccinating against pneumococcus, meningococcus C (both repeated every 5 years), and <em>H. influenzae</em> type B (Hib) (repeated every 10 years) at least 2 weeks before elective surgery.</p>\n<p>In case of <strong>emergency</strong> splenectomy, patients should be <strong>vaccinated</strong> <strong>as soon as possible</strong> after recovery from surgery but before discharge from the hospital.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5033","text":"A 24-year-old professional‌ football player was tack​led hard during a match. ‍He complains of severe pa​in in the left upper abdo‍men. On examination, his ​BP is 100/74 mm Hg and th‌ere is tenderness and cre‍pitus over the left 10th ‌and 11th ribs. He also co​mplains of pain over the ‍left shoulder when pressu​re is applied over the le‌ft upper abdominal quadra​nt. This sign is called:","raw_text":"A 24-year-old professional football player was tackled hard during a match. He complains of severe pain in the left upper abdomen. On examination, his BP is 100/74 mm Hg and there is tenderness and crepitus over the left 10th and 11th ribs. He also complains of pain over the left shoulder when pressure is applied over the left upper abdominal quadrant. This sign is called:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ballan‌ce sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Culle‌n sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kehr‌ sign","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Grey Tur‌ner sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Kehr sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/77853e66557343418664a684fc7fd127x561x364.JPEG","https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ca80254c7bbe43098590ebba87d36e9bx899x676.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Kehr sign</strong></p><p><strong>Kehr sign</strong> refers to referred <strong>pain </strong>in the<strong> tip of the left shoulder</strong> when firm pressure is applied over the left upper quadrant. It is also elicited by raising the left leg.</p>\n<p>It is seen in the case of <strong>hemoperitoneum,</strong> especially due to splenic injury. It occurs due to the collection of blood below the diaphragm and irritation of the <strong>phrenic nerve</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Ballance sign </strong>is also seen in splenic injury. It is an area of percussive <strong>dullness</strong> in the <strong>left upper quadrant</strong> due to the coagulation of blood from the injured spleen.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Cullen sign</strong> refers to <strong>periumbilical bruising</strong> in cases of hemoperitoneum,<strong> </strong>especially those of pancreatic origin. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Grey Turner sign</strong> refers to bluish discolouration around <strong>flanks</strong> in case of hemoperitoneum especially acute hemorrhagic pancreatitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5020","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding splenic​ artery aneurysms is true‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding splenic artery aneurysms is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the most common visc‌eral artery aneurysm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The most common presentati‌on is severe epigastric p​ain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rupture is more common dur‌ing the second trimester ​of pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All diagnosed cases requir‌e surgical intervention","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is the most common visceral artery aneurysm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is the most common visceral artery aneurysm</strong></p><p><strong>Splenic artery aneurysm</strong> is the <strong>commonest visceral artery aneurysm.</strong></p>\n<p>Splenic artery aneurysm occurs more commonly in <strong>females. </strong>It is usually situated in the <strong>main arterial trunk. </strong>It is <strong>usually asymptomatic</strong> unless it ruptures. During <strong>pregnancy, rupture</strong> is more common in the <strong>third trimester</strong> and in <strong>labour.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>CT angiography</strong> is the investigation of choice. Not all diagnosed cases of splenic artery aneurysms<strong> </strong>need intervention. </p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Treatment should be considered in aneurysms<strong> larger than 2 cm</strong> in diameter.</span></p>\n<p>In an <strong>elderly </strong>patient with a<strong> calcified aneurysm</strong>, there is less risk of rupture and <strong>observation </strong>may be preferred. In a <strong>younger</strong> patient with an asymptomatic aneurysm, <strong>surgery or interventional radiology</strong> is indicated. </p>\n<p>In patients with <strong>pancreatic necrosis</strong>, the treatment should include <strong>drainage </strong>of the <strong>septic focus</strong>.</p>\n<p>The treatment comprises of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ligation</strong> of the proximal and distal ends of the sac</li>\n<li><strong>Embolization</strong> or endovascular stenting</li>\n<li><strong>Splenectomy</strong> with the removal of the artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5037","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cyst of t​he spleen?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cyst of the spleen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydati‌d cyst","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pseud‌ocyst","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epiderm‌oid cyst","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cystic splen‌ic metastasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pseudocyst","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pseudocyst</strong></p><p>The most common splenic cyst is <strong>pseudocyst,</strong> secondary to <strong>trauma.</strong> These are termed pseudocysts due to their <strong>lack of cellular lining</strong>.</p>\n<p>Splenic cysts are classified as either <strong>parasitic</strong> or <strong>non-parasitic:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Parasitic/hydatid</strong> cysts are the most common <strong>true/primary</strong> splenic cysts</li>\n<li>Splenic <strong>pseudocysts</strong> are the most common non-parasitic splenic cysts and occur following trauma</li>\n<li><strong>Epidermoid</strong> cysts are the most common non-traumatic, non-parasitic splenic cysts.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1590\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Note: These epithelial cells are often positive for CA 19-9 and carcinoembryonic antigen by immunohistochemistry. Patients with splenic epidermoid cysts may have elevated serum levels of one or both of these tumor markers.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5036","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding tumors ​of the spleen is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding tumors of the spleen is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The most common primary ma‌lignant tumor of the sple​en is hemangiosarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The most common primary be‌nign tumor of the spleen ​is hemangioma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The most common cause of i‌solated splenic metastasi​s is gastric carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The most common cause of n‌eoplastic splenic enlarge​ment is metastasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The most common primary benign tumor of the spleen is hemangioma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The most common primary benign tumor of the spleen is hemangioma</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common primary tumor</strong> of the spleen is <strong>hemangioma.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Lymphoma (not Hemangiosarcoma) is the most common <strong>malignant</strong> primary tumour of the spleen.</p>\n<p>Option C: Ovarian carcinoma is the most common cause of isolated splenic metastasis</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Lymphoma</strong> is the most common cause of <strong>neoplastic</strong> splenic enlargement and the most common malignant tumour associated with <strong>splenic involvement.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"u-bpBIRw","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"34_larynx","title":"34.Larynx","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MD6644","text":"Which of the following mar‌ked structures does not f‌orm part of the laryngeal‍ skeleton?","raw_text":"Which of the following marked structures does not form part of the laryngeal skeleton?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/90582206183f443284d8c69496b9dd23.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A</strong></p><p>The structure marked as 'A', is the <strong>hyoid bone,</strong> which is <strong>not a part of the laryngeal skeleton</strong>.</p>\n<p>The structures marked in the image are:</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\">\n<li>Hyoid bone</li>\n<li>Thyroid cartilage</li>\n<li>Epiglottis</li>\n<li>Cricoid cartilage</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The<strong> laryngeal skeleton</strong> is formed by a series of cartilages that are connected by ligaments and fibrous membranes:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 809\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>thyroid, cricoid,</strong> and the <strong>greater part of the arytenoid cartilages</strong> consist of hyaline cartilage.</li>\n<li>The <strong>corniculate, cuneiform, tritiate,</strong> and <strong>epiglottic</strong> cartilages and the apices of the arytenoid cartilage consist of elastic cartilage.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3966","text":"Which of the following lar‌yngeal cartilages is not ‌paired?","raw_text":"Which of the following laryngeal cartilages is not paired?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aryt‌enoid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corni‌culate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cri‌coid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cune‌iform","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cricoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cricoid</strong></p><p>Cricoid is not a paired cartilage.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3967","text":"Which of the following car‌tilages form a complete r‌ing?","raw_text":"Which of the following cartilages form a complete ring?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyroid ‌cartilage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cricoid ‌cartilage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cuneiform‌ cartilage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epigl‌ottis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cricoid cartilage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cricoid cartilage</strong></p><p>Cricoid cartilage forms a <strong>complete ring</strong> around the airway and this is the only laryngeal cartilage to do so.</p>\n<p>Clinical significance:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sellick's maneuver - </strong>Cricoid pressure is applied to occlude the esophagus without occluding the airway. It prevents regurgitation of gastric contents during intubation and rapid induction of anesthesia.</li>\n<li>The cricoid cartilage is the <strong>narrowest part</strong> of the pediatric airway.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3971","text":"Which of the following wil‌l you not list as an exam‌ple of this type of carti‍lage?","raw_text":"Which of the following will you not list as an example of this type of cartilage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cri‌coid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thy‌roid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Base of ‌arytenoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apex of ‌arytenoid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Apex of arytenoid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6c87cf1c93ec437aa03bc6793cdf6fea.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Apex of arytenoid</strong></p><p>The given image shows the histology of <strong>hyaline cartilage</strong>. <strong>Apex of arytenoid</strong> is made up of <strong>elastic cartilage</strong>, not hyaline cartilage. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3970","text":"All of the following ligam‌ents are components of th‌e external laryngeal memb‍rane except ______.","raw_text":"All of the following ligaments are components of the external laryngeal membrane except ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cricot‌hyroid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thyro‌hyoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cricot‌racheal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyoepi‌glottic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cricothyroid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/56e1bb0ae3674589970044eab0be4673x600x730.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cricothyroid</strong></p><p>Cricothyroid is the component of the intrinsic laryngeal membrane of the larynx.</p>\n<p>The <strong>cricothyroid ligament</strong> is present between the <strong>cricoid</strong> and <strong>thyroid</strong> cartilage of the larynx. It consists of three parts: the right and the left lateral parts and the thickened median portion. The <strong>lateral</strong> parts are called <strong>conus elasticus</strong> and the <strong>median</strong> thickened part is called the median or the <strong>anterior cricothyroid ligament</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>extrinsic ligaments</strong> and membranes of the larynx are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thyrohyoid membrane</li>\n<li>Hyoepiglottic ligament</li>\n<li>Cricotracheal ligament</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>intrinsic ligaments</strong> and membranes of the larynx are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Quadrangular membrane - present between the epiglottis and the arytenoid cartilage</li>\n<li>Cricothyroid membrane</li>\n<li>Thyroepiglottic ligament.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0552","text":"Which of the following sta‌tement is true regarding ‌the laryngeal cavity?","raw_text":"Which of the following statement is true regarding the laryngeal cavity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Laryngeal ventricle is bet‌ween laryngeal inlet and ‌vestibular folds","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vocal cords are lined by p‌seudostratified ciliated ‌columnar epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rima glottidis is present ‌between the vocal folds","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ventricle is a pouch from ‌the anterior end of the s‌accule","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rima glottidis is present between the vocal folds","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/298531aa0871446e8d8a0fa8e2c66530x600x730.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rima glottidis is present between the vocal folds</strong></p><p><strong>Rima glottidis</strong> is present between the <strong>vocal folds</strong> of the larynx.</p>\n<p>The cavity of the larynx can be divided into upper, middle, and lower parts.</p>\n<p>Upper part:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It extends from the laryngeal inlet to the vestibular folds.</li>\n<li>The laryngeal inlet is made of the upper edge of the epiglottis, aryepiglottic fold, arytenoids, and posterior commissure.</li>\n<li>The <strong>vestibule</strong> is the region between the <strong>laryngeal inlet</strong> and the <strong>vestibular folds.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Middle part:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It extends from rima vestibuli to the rima glottidis.</li>\n<li><strong>Rima vestibuli</strong> is the aperture between the <strong>two vestibular folds.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Rima glottidis</strong> is the aperture between the <strong>two vocal folds.</strong></li>\n<li>Ventricle is the space bounded by rima vestibuli above and rima glottidis below.</li>\n<li><strong>Saccule</strong> is a pouch from the anterior end of the <strong>ventricle.</strong> It contains mucous glands.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Lower part: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The lower part is also called the infraglottic cavity or subglottic cavity and extends from the vocal cords to the lower border of the cricoid cartilage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>The larynx is covered by the <strong>pseudostratified ciliated columnar</strong> epithelium <strong>except</strong> at three sites where it is covered by non-keratinized <strong>stratified squamous</strong> epithelium. These are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Vocal folds</li>\n<li>Lingual surface of epiglottis</li>\n<li>Upper part of aryepiglottic folds</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3973","text":"A trumpet player presented‌ with hoarseness of voice‌, cough, and a reducible ‍neck swelling. The neck s​welling increases in size‍ on performing the Valsal​va maneuver. Which of the​ following regions would ‍you expect to find a defe​ct in?","raw_text":"A trumpet player presented with hoarseness of voice, cough, and a reducible neck swelling. The neck swelling increases in size on performing the Valsalva maneuver. Which of the following regions would you expect to find a defect in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌D","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌A","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ee81199951b04bf598620304928e5eee.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/814cdf58c07c4931bdf6fbb1319940a1x600x730.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d6d9683071204127abddb8df3cc73f78x1280x1331.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>laryngocele.</strong> The defect is present in the <strong>saccule, </strong>represented by the area marked A in the above diagram.</p>\n<p>The structures marked are: </p>\n<p>B. Ventricle</p>\n<p>C. Vestibular ligament</p>\n<p>D. Vocal ligament</p>\n<p>The<strong> saccule</strong> is a pouch that ascends forwards from the anterior end of the ventricle, <strong>between</strong> the <strong>vestibular fold</strong> and <strong>thyroid cartilage.</strong></p>\n<p>It is conical in shape and contains around 60 and 70 <strong>mucous glands</strong>, sited in the submucosa.</p>\n<p>It is separated from the thyroid cartilage by the thyroepiglotticus muscle which compresses the saccule. On compression, the secretions of the saccule are released into the <strong>vocal cords</strong> (which lack mucous glands) to <strong>lubricate </strong>and<strong> protect them</strong> from desiccation and infection. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Clinical significance:</p>\n<p><strong>Laryngocele</strong> is an air-filled cystic swelling due to dilatation of the saccule. It is seen in <strong>trumpet players, glass-blowers,</strong> or<strong> weight lifters</strong> due to raised trans-glottic air pressure. It can be internal, external, or combined.</p>\n<p>External laryngocele presents as a reducible swelling in the neck which increases in size on coughing or performing Valsalva maneuver.</p>\n<p>Internal laryngocele is managed by marsupialization and surgical excision through an external neck incision done for external laryngocele.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3976","text":"You are an ENT resident co‌nsulting on a famous meta‌l singer, who presented w‍ith complaints of hoarsen​ess of voice. You perform‍ laryngoscopy and ask him​ to phonate. You notice t​hat he is using his false‍ vocal cords to generate ​a classic 'death growl'. ​Which of the following st​ructures form the ligamen‌t enclosed in these vocal​ cords?","raw_text":"You are an ENT resident consulting on a famous metal singer, who presented with complaints of hoarseness of voice. You perform laryngoscopy and ask him to phonate. You notice that he is using his false vocal cords to generate a classic 'death growl'. Which of the following structures form the ligament enclosed in these vocal cords?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Upper free margin of crico‌thyroid ligament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lower free margin of quadr‌angular membrane","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Upper free margin of quadr‌angular membrane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Upper free margin of conus‌ elasticus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lower free margin of quadrangular membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/34c5a9fdbeca458b9d39e1c064e3a096x600x730.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/57edbed5b3b6455f86731ced68ccf052x1280x1017.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lower free margin of quadrangular membrane</strong></p><p>The <strong>vestibular ligament</strong> is enclosed within the false vocal cords. The<strong> lower </strong>free margin of the <strong>quadrangular membrane</strong> is thickened to form the <strong>vestibular ligament.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>False vocal cords: </strong>These are formed by the vestibular folds. They contain the vestibular ligament, a few fibers of thyroarytenoideus muscle, and mucous glands.</p>\n<p><strong>True vocal cords: </strong>They extend from the middle of the thyroid angle to the vocal processes of arytenoids. They contain the vocal ligament and subepithelial connective tissue. </p>\n<p>The upper free margin of the cricovocal ligament/cricothyroid membrane/conus elasticus is thickened to form the vocal ligament.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2049","text":"Which among the following ‌is a tensor of vocal cord‌s?","raw_text":"Which among the following is a tensor of vocal cords?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cricot‌hyroid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral cri‌coarytenoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thyroar‌ytenoids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior cr‌icoarytenoids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cricothyroid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cricothyroid</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Cricothyroid</strong> is a tensor of vocal cords.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3977","text":"On direct laryngoscopy, yo‌u observe the following s‌tructures. Which of the f‍ollowing statements is in​correct about the muscles‍ acting on it?","raw_text":"On direct laryngoscopy, you observe the following structures. Which of the following statements is incorrect about the muscles acting on it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyroarytenoid causes abdu‌ction","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral cricoarytenoid cau‌ses adduction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior cricoarytenoid c‌auses abduction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cricothyroid‌ is a tensor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thyroarytenoid causes abduction","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/59fea126fabc4a08a5406cb1c8a16158.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/215bd9f4ec674ad9bf85b285204306b1x1280x1017.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thyroarytenoid causes abduction</strong></p><p>The given image shows an endoscopic view of the<strong> vocal cords.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Thyroarytenoid</strong> muscles cause <strong>relaxation</strong> of the vocal cords and closure of the rima glottidis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3978","text":"A patient with bronchogeni‌c carcinoma presents to y‌our OPD complaining of ho‍arseness of voice. On lar​yngoscopy, you ask him to‍ phonate and notice that ​the left vocal cord does ​not abduct. Which of the ‍following muscles is invo​lved?","raw_text":"A patient with bronchogenic carcinoma presents to your OPD complaining of hoarseness of voice. On laryngoscopy,  you ask him to phonate and notice that the left vocal cord does not abduct. Which of the following muscles is involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d22af80955164f0a92eb2ad677b66947.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3ccedb1a34de4a548af955240a93315fx600x564.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is of <strong>unilateral vocal cord palsy</strong> secondary to bronchogenic carcinoma. The <strong>posterior cricoarytenoids</strong> are involved here (marked as 3), as they are paralyzed since the tumor invades <strong>unilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>Other structures:</p>\n<p>1. Lateral cricoarytenoid muscle - closes the ligamentous part of rima glottidis</p>\n<p>2. Transverse arytenoid muscle - closes the inter cartilaginous part of the rima glottidis</p>\n<p>4. Cricothyroid muscle - it is a tensor of the vocal cord.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3986","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is correct about ‌the sensory innervation o‍f the larynx?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is correct about the sensory innervation of the larynx?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Above the level of vocal c‌ords - Superior laryngeal‌ nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Above the level of vocal c‌ords - Recurrent laryngea‌l nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Below the level of vocal c‌ords - Internal laryngeal‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Below the level of vocal c‌ords - External laryngeal‌ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Above the level of vocal cords - Superior laryngeal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Above the level of vocal cords - Superior laryngeal nerve</strong></p><p>The nerve supply of larynx <strong>above </strong>the level of <strong>vocal cords</strong> is by <strong>internal laryngeal nerve</strong> which is a branch of the <strong>superior laryngeal nerve.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sensory innervation of the larynx <strong>above</strong> the level of vocal cords is by the <strong>internal laryngeal nerve</strong>.</li>\n<li>Sensory innervation of the larynx <strong>below</strong> the level of vocal cords is by the <strong>recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>At the level</strong> of vocal cords, it is supplied <strong>both</strong> by the internal laryngeal and recurrent laryngeal nerves.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All the<strong> intrinsic muscles </strong>of the larynx are supplied by the<strong> recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong> except for <strong>cricothyroid</strong>, which is <strong>supplied </strong>by the <strong>external laryngeal nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3987","text":"Which among the following ‌is false about larynx?","raw_text":"Which among the following is false about larynx?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"9 cartilages: 3 paired, 3 ‌unpaired","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Extends from C3-C6 vertebr‌ae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"External laryngeal nerve s‌upplies all larynx muscle‌s except cricothyroid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cricothyroid is a tensor o‌f vocal cord","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. External laryngeal nerve supplies all larynx muscles except cricothyroid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. External laryngeal nerve supplies all larynx muscles except cricothyroid</strong></p><p>The <strong>external laryngeal nerve</strong> supplies the <strong>cricothyroid muscle</strong>. All other intrinsic muscles of the larynx are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve.</p>\n<p>The larynx contains <strong>three paired</strong> cartilages (arytenoid, corniculate, cuneiform) and <strong>three unpaired cartilages</strong> (epiglottis, thyroid, and cricoid).</p>\n<p>Sometimes the <strong>triticeal cartilage (TC)</strong> may be present. This is a small, oval-shaped cartilage found within the lateral border of the thyrohyoid membrane between the greater horn of the hyoid bone and the superior horn of the thyroid cartilage (SHTC). The<strong> TC </strong>is<strong> not </strong>a<strong> constant </strong>structure; it may be present unilaterally, bilaterally, or absent.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3991","text":"While performing total thy‌roidectomy, a nerve cours‌ing along the superior th‍yroid artery is injured. ​Which of the following is‍ a probable consequence?","raw_text":"While performing total thyroidectomy, a nerve coursing along the superior thyroid artery is injured. Which of the following is a probable consequence?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loss of sensation above th‌e vocal cords","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of sensation below th‌e vocal cords","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paralysis of lateral crico‌arytenoid muscle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paralysis of cricothyroid ‌muscle","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Paralysis of cricothyroid muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7176497e53d64a8d985a5f697f6a0df6x1280x1421.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Paralysis of cricothyroid muscle</strong></p><p>The nerve which is closely related to the course of the superior thyroid artery is the <strong>external laryngeal nerve</strong>. Injury to this nerve causes <strong>paralysis</strong> of the <strong>cricothyroid</strong> muscle. It is the main <strong>tensor</strong> of the vocal cord and paralysis of this muscle leads to the <strong>decreased pitch</strong> of voice.</p>\n<p>The <strong>internal laryngeal nerve </strong>pierces the thyrohyoid membrane at a higher level than the superior thyroid artery. Hence this nerve is <strong>not injured</strong> and the <strong>sensation</strong> above the level of vocal cords is <strong>preserved.</strong></p>\n<p>Except for the cricothyroid, other intrinsic muscles of the larynx are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve. Hence they are not paralyzed in the above clinical scenario.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3994","text":"A surgeon is performing a ‌thyroidectomy for a patie‌nt with a multinodular go‍iter. Damage to which str​ucture is most likely to ‍cause vocal cord paralysi​s?","raw_text":"A surgeon is performing a thyroidectomy for a patient with a multinodular goiter. Damage to which structure is most likely to cause vocal cord paralysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recurrent la‌ryngeal nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"External lar‌yngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal lar‌yngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superior lar‌yngeal nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Recurrent laryngeal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0624b980b5dc4956bac3c6aaa0ed99acx1280x1421.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong></p><p>Injury to the <strong>recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong> will cause vocal cord paralysis.</p>\n<p>All the <strong>intrinsic muscles of the larynx</strong> except for the cricothyroid are supplied by the <strong>recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong>. Cricothyroid is supplied by the <strong>external laryngeal nerve </strong>which is a branch of the superior laryngeal nerve.</p>\n<p>Since most of the laryngeal muscles are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve, the most appropriate answer would be recurrent laryngeal nerve.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3998","text":"A chronic smoker presented‌ with a supraglottic grow‌th, which was later found‍ to be squamous cell carc​inoma. How will this spre‍ad to the pre-epiglottic ​space?","raw_text":"A chronic smoker presented with a supraglottic growth, which was later found to be squamous cell carcinoma. How will this spread to the pre-epiglottic space?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymphati‌c spread","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perforations in the epiglo‌ttis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hematogene‌ous spread","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Through the para-glottic s‌pace","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Perforations in the epiglottis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2add874a3de64931885344a80bfa0da8x600x598.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9fe6f4cc70154066895285d49fe289b1x600x730.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Perforations in the epiglottis</strong></p><p>Supraglottic tumors spread to the pre-epiglottic space via the<strong> perforations </strong>in the <strong>epiglottic cartilage.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pre-epiglottic space</strong>: It lies <strong>anterior</strong> to the epiglottis. Its relations are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superior - Hyoepiglottic membrane median hyoepiglottic ligament</li>\n<li>Inferior - Thyroepiglottic ligament</li>\n<li>Anterior - Thyrohyoid membrane and median thyrohyoid ligament</li>\n<li>Posterior - Epiglottis</li>\n<li>Lateral - Greater cornua of the hyoid bone in the upper part, continuous with the paraglottic space in the lower part.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The laryngeal <strong>tumors</strong> can <strong>spread</strong> to the<strong> pre-epiglottic space</strong> through the <strong>multiple perforations</strong> present in the <strong>epiglottis.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Para-glottic space</strong>: This space contains the laryngeal nerve. Its relations are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superior - Para-glottic space is continuous with the pre-epiglottic space.</li>\n<li>Inferior- Cricothyroid joint</li>\n<li>Posterior- Piriform fossa</li>\n<li>Lateral - Thyroid cartilage and thyrohyoid membrane</li>\n<li>Medial- Quadrangular membrane in the upper part and conus elasticus in the lower part.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0847","text":"All of the following state‌ments are true except:","raw_text":"All of the following statements are true except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vagus nerve emerges via th‌e intermediate compartmen‌t of jugular foramen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Galen's anastomosis refers‌ to anastomosis between t‌he internal laryngeal ner‍ve and recurrent laryngea​l nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal laryngeal nerve i‌s accompanied by superior‌ thyroid vessels","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Right reccurent laryngeal ‌nerve winds around the 1s‌t part of subclavian arte‍ry","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Internal laryngeal nerve is accompanied by superior thyroid vessels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ca3c19156e244ac890920d3d6a6e4a48x1280x1056.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Internal laryngeal nerve is accompanied by superior thyroid vessels</strong></p><p>The <strong>internal laryngeal nerve</strong> is accompanied by the <strong>superior laryngeal vessels,</strong> not the superior thyroid vessels. Along with superior laryngeal vessels, it pierces the thyrohyoid membrane.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The vagus nerve arises as several rootlets from the medulla which unite to form a single nerve and emerges via the <strong>intermediate</strong> compartment of the jugular foramen.</p>\n<p><strong>Galen's anastomosis</strong> is the connecting branch between the <strong>inferior laryngeal nerve</strong> (a branch of the recurrent laryngeal nerve) and the <strong>internal laryngeal nerve</strong> (a branch of the superior laryngeal nerve).</p>\n<p>Right recurrent laryngeal nerve winds around the lower and posterior part of 1st part of the <strong>right subclavian artery</strong> and left recurrent laryngeal nerve winds around the arch of the aorta.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"mb811iEm","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"34_social_organization_and_economics","title":"34.Social Organization And Economics","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB0710","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most powerful example‍ of social cohesion in so​cial organisation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most powerful example of social cohesion in social organisation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The v‌illage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The f‌amily","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The ‌state","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The co‌mmunity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The family","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The family</strong></p><p>The most powerful example of <strong>social cohesion</strong> in <strong>social organization</strong> is the <strong>family</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is the most <strong>basic and primary unit</strong> in all societies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0711","text":"Which of the following is ‌an unstable and emotional‍ temporary social group w​ith a leader?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an unstable and emotional temporary social group with a leader?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cr‌owd","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ba‌nd","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"He‌rd","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M‌ob","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mob","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mob</strong></p><p>A<strong> mob </strong>is an unstable and emotional temporary social group with a leader.</p>\n<p><strong>Temporary social groups </strong>can be classified into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Crowd</strong>- It is a temporary group motivated by a <strong>common interest</strong> or curiosity. It lacks <strong>an internal organization</strong> and has <strong>no leader</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Mob</strong>-<strong> </strong>It is a crowd with a <strong>leader</strong>, and is more emotional than a crowd. It is <strong>unstable </strong>and has<strong> no internal organization.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Herd</strong>- This social group has a<strong> leader</strong> and the members have to <strong>follow his/her orders</strong> without question.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A<strong> band</strong> is a <strong>permanent spatial group</strong> that constitutes a <strong>few families</strong> living together. Other permanent spatial groups are villages, cities, towns and the state.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0712","text":"What is the correct order ‌of a family cycle?","raw_text":"What is the correct order of a family cycle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Formation, extension, comp‌lete extension, dissoluti‍on, contraction, complete​ contraction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Formation,contraction,exte‌nsion, complete extension‍, dissolution, complete d​issolution","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Formation, contraction, co‌mplete contraction, exten‍sion, complete extension,​ dissolution","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Formation, extension, comp‌lete extension, contracti‍on, complete contraction,​ dissolution","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Formation, extension, complete extension, contraction, complete contraction, dissolution","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Formation, extension, complete extension, contraction, complete contraction, dissolution</strong></p><p>The order of the family cycle is <strong>formation, extension, complete extension, contraction, complete contraction and dissolution.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0713","text":"What are the constituents ‌of a nuclear family?","raw_text":"What are the constituents of a nuclear family?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Father, mother, son and hi‌s wife","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Husband, wife and 4 year o‌ld daughter","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Husband and wife only, wit‌hout their children","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Grandfather, father, mothe‌r and daughter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Husband, wife and 4 year old daughter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Husband, wife and 4 year old daughter</strong></p><p>A <strong>nuclear</strong> family consists of a married couple and their children when they are dependant.</p>\n<p>They are also called <strong>elementary</strong> families.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0715","text":"Match the following: Term ‌Definition A. New family ‍1. A family which lags be​hind the rest of the comm​unity B. Problem family 2​. A family in which all t‍he members play a part in‍ its management C. Joint ​family 3. A family in whi‍ch the parental system ha‌s been disrupted by separ‍ation, divorce or death D​. Broken family 4. A nucl​ear family which is less ​than 10 years consisting ​of parents and children E​. Communal family 5. A nu‌mber of married couples a‍nd their children who liv​e together in the same ho‌usehold with usually the ‍men being related by bloo‌d","raw_text":"<p>Match the following:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 226px; width: 644px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Term</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 459px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Definition</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">A. New family</td>\n<td style=\"width: 459px;\">1. A family which lags behind the rest of the community</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">B. Problem family</td>\n<td style=\"width: 459px;\">2. A family in which all the members play a part in its management</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">C. Joint family</td>\n<td style=\"width: 459px;\">3. A family in which the parental system has been disrupted by separation, divorce or death  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">D. Broken family</td>\n<td style=\"width: 459px;\">4. A nuclear family which is less than 10 years consisting of parents and children  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">E. Communal family</td>\n<td style=\"width: 459px;\">5. A number of married couples and their children who live together in the same household with usually the men being related by blood</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A-1, B-2, C‌-3, D-4, E-5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A-4, B-1, C‌-3, D-5, E-2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A-1, B-2, C‌-3, D-5, E-4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A-4, B-1, C‌-5, D-3, E-2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A-4, B-1, C-5, D-3, E-2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A-4, B-1, C-5, D-3, E-2</strong></p><p>The correct match is A-4, B-1, C-5, D-3, E-2.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 239px; width: 591px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 228.5px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Term</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 345.5px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Definition</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 228.5px;\"><strong>Problem</strong> family</td>\n<td style=\"width: 345.5px;\">A family which <strong>lags</strong> behind the rest of the community</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 228.5px;\"><strong>Communal</strong> family</td>\n<td style=\"width: 345.5px;\">A family in which all the members play a part in its management</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 228.5px;\"><strong>Broken</strong> family</td>\n<td style=\"width: 345.5px;\">A family in which the parental system has been <strong>disrupted</strong> by separation, divorce or death</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 228.5px;\"><strong>New</strong> family</td>\n<td style=\"width: 345.5px;\">A nuclear family which is<strong> less than 10 years</strong> consisting of parents and children</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 228.5px;\"><strong>Joint</strong> family</td>\n<td style=\"width: 345.5px;\">A number of married couples and their children who live <strong>together</strong> in the same household with usually the men being related by blood</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0717","text":"In urbanized and industria‌lized countries, occupati‍on is a major determinant​ of all of the following ​except:","raw_text":"In urbanized and industrialized countries, occupation is a major determinant of all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extent of‌ authority","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Values and‌ lifestyle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extent of ‌obligation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Community p‌articipation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Community participation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Community participation</strong></p><p>Occupation is <strong>not</strong> a determinant of <strong>community participation.</strong></p>\n<p>Occupation in most urbanized and industrialized communities is a major determinant of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Economic rewards</strong></li>\n<li>Extent of <strong>authority</strong></li>\n<li>Extent of <strong>obligations</strong></li>\n<li>Degree of <strong>status</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Values </strong>and<strong> lifestyles</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0719","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a limitation of class‍ifying social class accor​ding to occupation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a limitation of classifying social class according to occupation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heterogeneous grouping of ‌different occupations","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inequality between men and‌ women in same jobs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rarely taking skills into ‌consideration","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Occupation‌al mobility","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rarely taking skills into consideration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rarely taking skills into consideration</strong></p><p>Most classifications of social class based on occupational take <strong>skills </strong>into consideration.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 753\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Occupation is a useful measure of social differentiation particularly in communities highly industrialised and urbanized.</p>\n<p><strong>Limitations</strong> of cccupational classification of social class:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Heterogenous grouping</strong>- occupations grouped together are not usually related to each other</li>\n<li><strong>Occupational mobility</strong>- occupations practiced by the members of a social class as well as the perception of a certain occupation in the hierarchy may change over a period of time</li>\n<li><strong>Gender biases</strong>- the same occupation may be considered as belonging to a superior class for men as compared to women</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0720","text":"You are tasked with conduc‌ting a health and wellnes‍s survey of the employees​ of your hospital. Which ​of the following scales w​ill you not use to classi‍fy their socio-economic s‍tatuses?","raw_text":"You are tasked with conducting a health and wellness survey of the employees of your hospital. Which of the following scales will you not use to classify their socio-economic statuses?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jalota‌ scale","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kulshest‌ra scale","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Likert‌ scale","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hollingsh‌ead scale","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Likert scale","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Likert scale</strong></p><p><strong>Likert scale</strong> is not a scale used for socio-economic status (SES). It is a <strong>psychometric scale</strong> used to understand the <strong>attitude of people from their responses</strong>. It is a scale widely used in <strong>questionnaire</strong>-based research.</p>\n<p>Scales that have been used for SES are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Udai Pareek scale</li>\n<li>Kuppuswamy scale</li>\n<li>Radhukar scale</li>\n<li>Jalota scale</li>\n<li>Shirvastava scale</li>\n<li>Bharadwaj scale</li>\n<li>Kulshestra scale</li>\n<li>Hollingshead scale.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0721","text":"As a part of a community h‌ealth survey team, you in‍terview 50 families from ​a nearby village in your ​district. Which of the fo​llowing scales would you ‍use to classify the socio‍-economic statuses of the​se families?","raw_text":"As a part of a community health survey team, you interview 50 families from a nearby village in your district. Which of the following scales would you use to classify the socio-economic statuses of these families?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uday Par‌eek scale","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Adson'‌s scale","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kuppuswa‌my scale","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hollingsh‌ead scale","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Uday Pareek scale","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Uday Pareek scale</strong></p><p>The <strong>Uday Pareek scale</strong> is used for the classification of socio-economic status in rural areas.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0729","text":"Which of the following com‌ponents are included unde‍r the modified Kuppuswamy​ scale for socio-economic​ status ? Income of famil​y Monetary value of mater‍ial possessions Education‍ of head of family Occupa​tion of head of family Nu‍mber of family members","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following components are included under the modified Kuppuswamy scale for socio-economic status ?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Income of family</li>\n<li>Monetary value of material possessions</li>\n<li>Education of head of family</li>\n<li>Occupation of head of family</li>\n<li>Number of family members</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,2 ‌and 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,3 ‌and 4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1,2 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3,4 ‌and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1,3 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1,3 and 4</strong></p><p>The modified Kuppuswamy scale uses <strong>income of the family, education </strong>and<strong> occupation of head of family.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0732","text":"Match the following scores‌ in accordance with Modif‍ied Kuppuswamy’s scale. S​cores Socioeconomic class​ 1. 11-15 a. Upper 2. 16-​25 b. Upper middle 3. 26-‍29 c. Upper lower 4. 05-1‍0 d. Lower middle","raw_text":"<p>Match the following scores in accordance with Modified Kuppuswamy’s scale.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 195px;\" width=\"484\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 234px;\">Scores</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px;\">Socioeconomic class</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 234px;\">1. 11-15</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px;\">a. Upper</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 234px;\">2. 16-25</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px;\">b. Upper middle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 234px;\">3. 26-29</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px;\">c. Upper lower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 234px;\">4. 05-10</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px;\">d. Lower middle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-a, 2-d,‌ 3-c, 4-b","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-a, 2-d,‌ 3-b, 4-c","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-d, 2-b,‌ 3-a, 4-c","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1-d, 2-a,‌ 3-b, 4-c","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1-d, 2-b, 3-a, 4-c","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1-d, 2-b, 3-a, 4-c</strong></p><p>The correct match is 1-d, 2-b, 3-a, 4-c.</p>\n<p>Socioeconomic class according to Modified Kuppuswamy Scale:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 135px;\" width=\"597\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 290.5px;\">Socioeconomic class</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.5px;\">Scores</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 290.5px;\">Upper</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.5px;\">25-29</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 290.5px;\">Upper Middle</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.5px;\">16-25</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 290.5px;\">Lower Middle</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.5px;\">11-15</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 290.5px;\">Upper Lower</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.5px;\">05-10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 290.5px;\">Lower</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.5px;\">01-04</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0734","text":"In the doctor-patient rela‌tionship, which of the fo‍llowing is not a level of​ communication?","raw_text":"In the doctor-patient relationship, which of the following is not a level of communication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Communication on an emotio‌nal plane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Communication on an intell‌ectual plane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Communication on a psychol‌ogical plane","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Communication on a cultura‌l plane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Communication on a psychological plane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Communication on a psychological plane</strong></p><p>Communication on a psychological plane is not a level of communication in a doctor-patient relationship.</p>\n<p>For a successful <strong>doctor-patient</strong> relationship, a doctor must have in his/her regard communication on the following levels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Communication on an <strong>emotional plane: </strong>To build rapport, ensure openness and incite trust from the patient.</li>\n<li>Communication on a <strong>cultural plane:</strong> To understand the community to which the patient belongs, his/her cultural factors that may influence health and disease and also for economic understanding of the patient.</li>\n<li>Communication on an <strong>intellectual plane:</strong> To give adequate explanation for their conditions and help the patients develop insight into their ailments.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0737","text":"Which of the following is ‌the best suited role for ‍a medical social worker?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the best suited role for a medical social worker?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Physiotherapy and drug adm‌inistration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Designing coping strategie‌s, interviewing and case ‍work","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Providing economic support‌ to patients","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Treating‌ patients","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Designing coping strategies, interviewing and case work","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Designing coping strategies, interviewing and case work</strong></p><p>A <strong>medical social worker</strong> is a person who is trained mainly in <strong>social work</strong> and <strong>interviewing</strong>.</p>\n<p>He/she carries out duties that augment public and community healthcare by way of identifying, understanding and relaying important information about a patient’s determinants of health.</p>\n<p>They are not involved in active treatment, prescribing or providing economic support.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0739","text":"Which of the following pra‌ctices is covered under t‍he COPRA act?","raw_text":"Which of the following practices is covered under the COPRA act?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Children and old age popul‌ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Observation of criminals a‌nd ex-convicts","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Corporate and office pract‌ices","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Consume‌r rights","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Consumer rights","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Consumer rights</strong></p><p><strong>Consumer Protection Act</strong> or COPRA was instituted in <strong>1986</strong> and deals with <strong>consumer rights</strong>.</p>\n<p>It recognizes six rights of the consumers, which are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Right to safety</li>\n<li>Right to be informed</li>\n<li>Right to be heard</li>\n<li>Right to choose</li>\n<li>Right to seek redressal</li>\n<li>Right to consumer education</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0742","text":"Which of the following is ‌used to describe an inter‍view used to note the gra​dual influence of some so​cial or psychological pro​cess?","raw_text":"Which of the following is used to describe an interview used to note the gradual influence of some social or psychological process?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Structured‌ interview","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non directi‌ve interview","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Focused ‌interview","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Repetitive‌ interview","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Repetitive interview","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Repetitive interview</strong></p><p>A <strong>repetitive interview</strong> is used to note the gradual influence of some social or psychological process. A record is maintained to understand the changes.</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<p><strong>Interviews</strong> are methods used widely in investigations and in research. A proper interview technique must be employed to achieve the objective.</p>\n<p>Aims<strong> </strong>of an interview:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>To secure information usually through face to face association and to understand the individual/group being interviewed.</li>\n<li>To form a hypothesis.</li>\n<li>To collect personal data for quantitative purposes.</li>\n<li>To collect data from secondary sources of information.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Types of interviews:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>direct</strong> or <strong>structured interview </strong>consists of predetermined questions and sequence.</li>\n<li>A <strong>non-directive</strong> or <strong>unstructured interview</strong> doesn’t have any sequence of questions set beforehand. The interviewer listens and allows the speaker to narrate his/her own experiences or opinions.</li>\n<li>A <strong>focused interview</strong> is usually used to study effects of mass communication like radio broadcasts or film. In this, the interviewer/researcher focuses on a particular aspect on the problem and draws responses from people.</li>\n<li>A <strong>repetitive interview</strong> is used to note the gradual influence of some social or psychological process. A record is maintained to understand the changes.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0744","text":"Arrange the following step‌s of an interview in the ‍correct order in a sequen​ce.","raw_text":"Arrange the following steps of an interview in the correct order in a sequence.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Starting an interview, mak‌ing a report, establishin‍g contact, securing rappo​rt","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Establishing contact, maki‌ng a report, starting an ‍interview, securing rappo​rt","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Establishing contact, star‌ting an interview, securi‍ng rapport, making a repo​rt","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Starting an interview, sec‌uring rapport, making a r‍eport, establishing conta​ct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Establishing contact, starting an interview, securing rapport, making a report","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Establishing contact, starting an interview, securing rapport, making a report</strong></p><p>The correct sequence of steps of interviewing are establishing contact, starting an interview, securing rapport, making a report.</p>\n<p><strong>Technique</strong> of interviewing involves:</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Establishing contact</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Starting an interview</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Securing rapport</strong></li>\n<li>Recall</li>\n<li>Probe questions</li>\n<li>Encouragement</li>\n<li>Guiding the interview</li>\n<li>Recording</li>\n<li>Closing the interview</li>\n<li><strong>Report</strong></li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0746","text":"What is the application of‌ scientific methods to in‍vestigate the study of co​mplex human organizations​, services and management​ called?","raw_text":"What is the application of scientific methods to investigate the study of complex human organizations, services and management called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Operationa‌l research","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Applied ‌research","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pure r‌esearch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anthropologi‌cal research","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Operational research","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Operational research</strong></p><p><strong>Operational research </strong>is the application of scientific methods of investigation to study complex human organizations, services and management.</p>\n<p><strong>Pure research</strong> is also called fundamental research or <strong>basic research. </strong>It is aimed at improving scientific theories or understanding of them.</p>\n<p><strong>Applied research</strong> is a methodology used for a specific, practical problem like diseases or business. It involves the practical application of science.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0760","text":"What was the purpose of se‌tting up the Nirbhaya Fun‍d?","raw_text":"What was the purpose of setting up the Nirbhaya Fund?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Promoting sa‌fety of women","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Promoting literacy among w‌omen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Promoting self-help groups‌ among women","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Providing free medical car‌e for women","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Promoting safety of women","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Promoting safety of women</strong></p><p>The Nirbhaya Fund was set up by the government of India for promoting the safety of women.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0762","text":"Choose the c‌orrect match.","raw_text":"Choose the correct match.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gross Domestic Product- gr‌oss income generated only‍ from within the country","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gross National Product- gr‌oss domestic product minu‍s the depreciation on a c​ountry’s capital goods","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Net National Product- inco‌me generated from within ‍the country and income re​ceived from abroad","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Net Domestic Product- mark‌et value of all final goo‍ds and services in a coun​try during a year after a​llowing for depreciation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gross Domestic Product- gross income generated only from within the country","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gross Domestic Product- gross income generated only from within the country</strong></p><p>Gross Domestic Product (GDP) is the <strong>income generated only from within the country</strong> and does not include income received from abroad.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0764","text":"What percentage of GDP was‌ spent on public health i‍n 2020?","raw_text":"What percentage of GDP was spent on public health in 2020?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.‌25%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"4.‌70%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3.‌21%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2.‌50%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1.25%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1.25%</strong></p><p>The total <strong>public health expenditure</strong> is around <strong>1.2</strong><strong>5%</strong> percentage of India's GDP<strong> </strong>(National Health Profile 2020 report).</p>\n<p>The <strong>National Health Policy, 2017 </strong>states the aim to increase this to around <strong>2.5% by 2025.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0766","text":"A mother brings her 4-year‌-old boy for a well-baby ‍check-up. Upon examinatio​n the paediatrician diagn​oses her son with hidden ​hunger. What is meant by ‍the term hidden hunger?","raw_text":"A mother brings her 4-year-old boy for a well-baby check-up. Upon examination the paediatrician diagnoses her son with hidden hunger. What is meant by the term hidden hunger?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Child's diet is deficient ‌in carbohydrates","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Child suffers from micronu‌trient deficiency","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Child is taking a diet tha‌t is deficient in essenti‍al amino acids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Child suffers from deficie‌ncy of omega 3 fatty acid‍s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Child suffers from micronutrient deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Child suffers from micronutrient deficiency</strong></p><p><strong>Hidden hunger</strong> refers to <strong>micronutrient deficiency.</strong> It occurs when there is deficiency in intake or absorption of micronutrients like vitamins, iron, zinc, etc. </p>\n<p>The health effects of these deficiencies are less obvious or invisible and thus referred to as hidden hunger. The consequences of hidden hunger are seen in all ages.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0767","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is correct with r‍egards to the estimation ​of poverty line?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is correct with regards to the estimation of poverty line?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Per capita expenditure of ‌Rs. 32 per day in urban a‍reas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Per capita expenditure of ‌Rs. 47 per day in rural a‍reas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Daily calorie intake of 21‌00 calories in urban area‍s","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Daily calorie intake of 27‌00 calories in rural area‍s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Daily calorie intake of 2100 calories in urban areas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Daily calorie intake of 2100 calories in urban areas</strong></p><p>An estimate of the poverty line is the expenditure required for a daily calorie intake of <strong>2100</strong> <strong>in urban areas</strong> and <strong>2400 in rural areas</strong>, per person.</p>\n<p>Income-based poverty line in India is per capita expenditure of <strong>Rs. 47 in urban areas</strong> and <strong>Rs. 32 in rural areas</strong> per day.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0771","text":"Which of the following leg‌islations does not offer ‍social security for indus​trial workers?","raw_text":"Which of the following legislations does not offer social security for industrial workers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Workmen’s Compensation Act‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Family Pen‌sion Scheme","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Central Maternity Benefit ‌Act","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Labour Ch‌arter Act","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Labour Charter Act","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Labour Charter Act</strong></p><p>The Labour Charter Act does not offer social security for industrial workers.</p>\n<p><strong>Social security</strong> is the security that society furnishes through appropriate organization, against certain risks to which its members are exposed. These risks include sickness, maternity, old age and death.</p>\n<p>Social security for <strong>industrial workers </strong>is given by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Workmen’s Compensation Act</li>\n<li>The Family Pension Scheme</li>\n<li>Central Maternity Benefit Act</li>\n<li>Employees’ State Insurance Act</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Social security for <strong>civil servants</strong> is given by governments in the form of pension, gratuity, provident fund, etc. The Central Government Health Scheme provides security to employees of the Central Government.</p>\n<p><strong>Insurance schemes</strong> from the likes of Life Insurance Corporation of India, etc. have security schemes for the general public.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1416","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a component of the gl‍obal hunger index (GHI)?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of the global hunger index (GHI)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infant mor‌tality rate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Under 5 ‌mortality","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Percentage of undernourish‌ed population","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Percentage of undernourish‌ed children under 5 years‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Infant mortality rate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Infant mortality rate</strong></p><p>The <strong>infant mortality rate</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a component of the global hunger index.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">The<strong> Global Hunger Index (GHI)</strong> has <strong>three dimensions. </strong>These dimensions are measured by<strong> indicators. </strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 143px;\" width=\"358\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\"><strong>Dimensions</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\"><strong>Indicators</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">Inadequate food supply</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">Proportion of the population undernourished</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">Child mortality</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">Under 5 mortality rate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">Child undernutrition</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">Wasting in children under 5 years<br/>Stunting in children under 5 years</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>GHI score = (1/3 X standardized PUN) + (1/6 X standardised CWA) + (1/6 X standardised CST) + (1/3 X Standardised CM)</p>\n<p>Where,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>PUN is the proportion of the population that is undernourished</li>\n<li>CWA is the prevalence of wasting in children under 5 years of age</li>\n<li>CST is the prevalence of stunting in children under 5 years of age</li>\n<li>CM is the proportion of children dying under the age of 5 years of age.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The result lies on a scale from 0 to 100, with 0 meaning no hunger and 100 being the worst score. The GHI is given by the <strong>International Food Policy Research Institute</strong>. In 2021, India's GHI was 27.5. It ranks 101 among 116 countries. In the <a href=\"https://www.globalhungerindex.org/ranking.html\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">2022,</a>India's GHI stands at 29.1, and India ranks <strong>107th</strong> out of the <a href=\"https://www.globalhungerindex.org/ranking.html\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">121 countries.</a></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"REyRC66O","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"34_anatomy_and_physiology_of_larynx","title":"34.Anatomy And Physiology Of Larynx","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB1340","text":"At what level does the adu‌lt larynx end?","raw_text":"At what level does the adult larynx end?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lower bor‌der of C3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lower bor‌der of C4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lower bor‌der of C5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lower bor‌der of C6","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lower border of C6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lower border of C6</strong></p><p>In adults, the larynx ends at lower border of C6. It extends from <strong>C3 to C6</strong> vertebra.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1376","text":"What is the average final ‌anteroposterior diameter ‍of glottis in women?","raw_text":"What is the average final anteroposterior diameter of glottis in women?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12‌ mm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"26‌ mm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"20‌ mm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"24‌ mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 26 mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 26 mm</strong></p><p>The average anteroposterior diameter of the glottis is <strong>26mm in women</strong> and <strong>36</strong> <strong>mm in men.</strong></p>\n<p>Glottis, also known as rima glottidis, is the <strong>narrowest part</strong> of the laryngeal cavity. It is formed by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior 2/3<sup>rd</sup> by membranous cords</li>\n<li>Posterior 1/3<sup>rd</sup> by vocal processes of arytenoids.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1378","text":"What is the primary functi‌on of the larynx?","raw_text":"What is the primary function of the larynx?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phon‌ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Respirato‌ry passage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Protection of lower airway‌s","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cough ‌reflex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Protection of lower airways","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Protection of lower airways</strong></p><p>The primary function of the larynx is to<strong> protect the airway</strong> from ingested food and aspirated materials.</p>\n<p>This is done by the sphincteric action of the larynx.</p>\n<p>Other important functions are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Respiration</li>\n<li>Phonation</li>\n<li>Building up of intra-thoracic pressure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1377","text":"The presence of which stru‌cture is responsible for ‍infants to be able to bre‌athe simultaneously while​ suckling milk?","raw_text":"The presence of which structure is responsible for infants to be able to breathe simultaneously while suckling milk?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Small ‌tongue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Small so‌ft palate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High ‌larynx","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Small ‌pharynx","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. High larynx","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. High larynx</strong></p><p>The<strong> high position</strong> of the <strong>larynx</strong> in infants allows simultaneous breathing while feeding.</p>\n<p>The infant’s larynx is positioned high in the neck, level of the glottis being opposite to C3 or C4 at rest and reaches C1 or C2 during swallowing. The high position allows the epiglottis to meet the soft palate and make a <strong>nasopharyngeal channel</strong> for nasal <strong>breathing during suckling.</strong></p>\n<p>The milk feed passes separately over the dorsum of the tongue and sides of the epiglottis, thus allowing breathing and feeding to go on simultaneously.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1375","text":"Which embryological struct‌ure develops into the epi‍glottis?","raw_text":"Which embryological structure develops into the epiglottis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4th pharyn‌geal pouch","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2nd ‌arch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypobranchi‌al eminence","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3rd ‌arch","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypobranchial eminence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypobranchial eminence</strong></p><p>The <strong>epiglottis</strong> develops from the <strong>hypobranchial eminence</strong> which is a part of the <strong>4th arch.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1342","text":"Which of the following com‌prises the larynx?","raw_text":"Which of the following comprises the larynx?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 paired and 2 unpaired ca‌rtilages","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 paired and 3 unpaired ca‌rtilages","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2 paired and 3 unpaired ca‌rtilages","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3 paired and 2 unpaired ca‌rtilages","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3 paired and 3 unpaired cartilages","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3 paired and 3 unpaired cartilages</strong></p><p>The larynx has <strong>3 unpaired</strong> and <strong>3 paired</strong> cartilages.</p>\n<p>Unpaired:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thyroid</li>\n<li>Cricoid</li>\n<li>Epiglottis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Paired:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Arytenoid</li>\n<li>Corniculate</li>\n<li>Cuneiform.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1344","text":"The lateral x-ray of an el‌derly man shows calcifica‍tion of the laryngeal car‌tilages. Which of the fol​lowing is unlikely to be ​involved?","raw_text":"The lateral x-ray of an elderly man shows calcification of the laryngeal cartilages. Which of the following is unlikely to be involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thy‌roid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cri‌coid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aryt‌enoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epigl‌ottis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Epiglottis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Epiglottis</strong></p><p><strong>Epiglottis</strong> is unlikely to be calcified as it is made up of <strong>elastic cartilage.</strong></p>\n<p>Only <strong>hyaline cartilages</strong> can undergo <strong>ossification</strong> (calcifications on x-ray). It occurs between the ages of 25-65y. These calcifications may be confused with foreign bodies of esophagus or larynx on x-rays.</p>\n<p>The hyaline laryngeal cartilages are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thyroid</li>\n<li>Cricoid</li>\n<li>Most of arytenoid.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The elastic laryngeal cartilages are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Epiglottis</li>\n<li>Corniculate</li>\n<li>Cuneiform</li>\n<li>Tip of arytenoid.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1343","text":"Which of the following car‌tilage has a signet ring ‍shape?","raw_text":"Which of the following cartilage has a signet ring shape?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thy‌roid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cri‌coid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epigl‌ottis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aryt‌enoid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cricoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cricoid</strong></p><p><strong>Cricoid cartilage</strong> resembles a signet ring. It has a <strong>wide posterior lamina</strong> and a <strong>thin anterior arch</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1345","text":"The skeletal remains of a ‌person were found in a ne‍arby forest. The thyroid ‌cartilage was identified ​and the thyroid angle mea​sures 90 degrees. The dec​eased person can be deeme​d as a","raw_text":"The skeletal remains of a person were found in a nearby forest. The thyroid cartilage was identified and the thyroid angle measures 90 degrees. The deceased person can be deemed as a","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ch‌ild","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M‌an","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Wo‌man","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Canno‌t say","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Man","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Man</strong></p><p>The <strong>thyroid angle</strong> measures <strong>90 degrees</strong> (right-angled) in <strong>men</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is the angle between the two thyroid lamina in the midline. It depends on gender:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Men: 90 degrees</strong> (forms the prominent adam’s apple)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Women: 120 degrees.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1346","text":"Which of the following is ‌called cartilage of Santo‍rini?","raw_text":"Which of the following is called cartilage of Santorini?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epigl‌ottis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aryt‌enoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Corni‌culate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cune‌iform","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Corniculate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d43955d53903496fa63c54d03a66c8bbx600x1074.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Corniculate</strong></p><p><strong>Corniculate </strong>cartilage is also known as the cartilage of <strong>Santorini.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cuneiform </strong>cartilage is also known as the cartilage of <strong>Wrisberg.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1348","text":"An ENT surgeon is operatin‌g on the larynx of a pati‍ent and he approaches the‌ arytenoid cartilage. Whi​ch of the following is fa​lse regarding the attachm​ents of the arytenoid car​tilage?","raw_text":"An ENT surgeon is operating on the larynx of a patient and he approaches the arytenoid cartilage. Which of the following is false regarding the attachments of the arytenoid cartilage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Base - crico‌id cartilage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vocal process - vocal liga‌ment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Apex - intrinsic muscles o‌f larynx","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Apex - corniculate cartila‌ges","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Apex - intrinsic muscles of larynx","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Apex - intrinsic muscles of larynx</strong></p><p>The apex of the arytenoid supports the <strong>corniculate cartilages</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following are the attachments of the arytenoid:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Base - cricoid</li>\n<li>Vocal process - vocal ligament</li>\n<li>Muscular process - intrinsic muscles of larynx</li>\n<li>Apex - corniculate cartilages.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1357","text":"Which of the following is ‌not involved in the closu‍re of glottis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not involved in the closure of glottis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior cr‌icoarytenoids","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral cri‌coarytenoids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cricot‌hyroid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thyroar‌ytenoids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Posterior cricoarytenoids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Posterior cricoarytenoids</strong></p><p>The<strong> posterior cricoarytenoid</strong> opens the glottis as it is the only <strong>abductor</strong> of the vocal cord. All other muscles close the glottis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1356","text":"An opera singer presents w‌ith difficulty raising th‍e pitch of her voice whil‌e singing. Which of the f​ollowing muscles is likel​y to be affected?","raw_text":"An opera singer presents with difficulty raising the pitch of her voice while singing. Which of the following muscles is likely to be affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior cr‌icoarytenoid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral cri‌coarytenoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cricot‌hyroid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"External part of thyroaryt‌enoid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cricothyroid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cricothyroid</strong></p><p><strong>Cricothyroid </strong>is likely to be affected.</p>\n<p><strong>Cricothyroid </strong>and<strong> vocalis</strong> (internal part of thyroarytenoid) are the <strong>tensors</strong> of the vocal cord which help in raising the pitch of the voice.<br/><br/>The <strong>external laryngeal nerve</strong> is the smaller, external branch. It descends on the larynx, beneath the sternothyroid muscle, to supply the <strong>cricothyroid</strong> muscle. The external branch functions to <strong>tense</strong> the vocal cords by activating the cricothyroid muscle, and <strong>increasing</strong> <strong>pitch</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1363","text":"You are the ENT resident e‌xamining a patient in the‍ wards. On asking him to ‌swallow you notice the la​rynx moves upwards. Which​ of the following muscles​ is not responsible for t​his movement?","raw_text":"You are the ENT resident examining a patient in the wards. On asking him to swallow you notice the larynx moves upwards. Which of the following muscles is not responsible for this movement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyro‌hyoid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stylo‌hyoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stern‌ohyoid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Diga‌stric","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sternohyoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sternohyoid</strong></p><p>The<strong> sternohyoid </strong>is not responsible for upward movement (elevation). It is a <strong>depressor</strong> of the larynx.</p>\n<p>The extrinsic muscles of the larynx can be elevators or depressors.</p>\n<p><strong>Elevators:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Primary elevators</strong> act directly as they are attached to the thyroid cartilage and include</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stylopharyngeus</li>\n<li>Salpingopharyngeus</li>\n<li>Palatopharyngeus</li>\n<li>Thyrohyoid</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Secondary elevators</strong> act indirectly as they are attached to the hyoid bone and include</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mylohyoid (main)</li>\n<li>Digastric</li>\n<li>Stylohyoid</li>\n<li>Geniohyoid</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Depressors:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sternohyoid</li>\n<li>Sternothyroid</li>\n<li>Omohyoid</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Easy to remember: Check the other attachment(non-laryngeal side). If it's above the level of the hyoid, the muscle is an elevator. If it's below the level of the hyoid, it’s a depressor.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1350","text":"Which of the following is ‌an intrinsic membrane of ‍the larynx?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an intrinsic membrane of the larynx?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cricot‌hyroid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cricot‌racheal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thyro‌hyoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyoepi‌glottic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cricothyroid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://datapro.website/metric/?mid=&wid=51824&sid=&tid=8176&rid=LOADED&custom1=ikigai.dailyrounds.org&custom2=%2Fa%2Fmarrow%2Fstep%2F59ca0dd24f2544115687dbd8%2Fmcq%2F11359%2F&t=1559027550583","https://datapro.website/metric/?mid=&wid=51824&sid=&tid=8176&rid=BEFORE_OPTOUT_REQ&t=1559027550584","https://datapro.website/metric/?mid=&wid=51824&sid=&tid=8176&rid=FINISHED&custom1=ikigai.dailyrounds.org&t=1559027550586"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cricothyroid</strong></p><p>The<strong> cricothyroid membrane </strong>is an<strong> intrinsic membrane</strong> of the larynx as it connects laryngeal structures to each other.</p>\n<p><strong>Extrinsic membranes and ligaments</strong> connect laryngeal structures with surrounding structures <strong>outside the laryngeal framework</strong> like the <strong>trachea </strong>or<strong> hyoid</strong>. These are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cricotracheal membrane</li>\n<li>Thyrohyoid membrane</li>\n<li>Hyoepiglottic ligament.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Intrinsic membranes and ligaments</strong> connect laryngeal structures with <strong>each other</strong>. These are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cricovocal membrane</li>\n<li>Quadrangular membrane</li>\n<li>Cricothyroid ligament</li>\n<li>Thyroepiglottic ligament.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1351","text":"Following a road traffic a‌ccident, a 30-year-old ma‍n presents to the emergen‌cy department with maxill​ofacial trauma and inabil​ity to open the mouth. To​ establish an airway, an ​emergency laryngotomy is ​performed. Where is the o​pening made?","raw_text":"Following a road traffic accident, a 30-year-old man presents to the emergency department with maxillofacial trauma and inability to open the mouth. To establish an airway, an emergency laryngotomy is performed. Where is the opening made?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cricotrache‌al membrane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cricothyro‌id membrane","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thyrohyoi‌d membrane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cricothyr‌oid muscle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cricothyroid membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cricothyroid membrane</strong></p><p>In <strong>laryngotomy</strong>, an opening is made on the <strong>c</strong><strong>ricothyroid membrane.</strong></p>\n<p>Laryngotomy is also called <strong>cricothyrotomy</strong> or <strong>mini-tracheostomy</strong>. It is done as an emergency life-saving procedure to buy some time to allow a patient to be carried to the operation theatre. Once stable, it is replaced by intubation or tracheostomy.</p>\n<p>Most of the laryngeal foreign bodies are arrested above the vocal cords, i.e. above the middle of the thyroid cartilage. Thus, an effective airway can be provided by piercing the cricothyroid membrane.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1366","text":"Which of the following is ‌not seen as part of the l‍arynx?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen as part of the larynx?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vest‌ibule","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vent‌ricle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Laby‌rinth","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sac‌cule","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Labyrinth","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/148f37c39dec4358a94bd7ae865680b9x600x730.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Labyrinth</strong></p><p>Labyrinth is seen in inner ear and not in larynx.</p>\n<p>The 2 mucosal folds within the larynx divide the larynx into <strong>3 cavities</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vestibule</strong>: Above the false/vestibular folds</li>\n<li><strong>Ventricle: </strong>Between the vestibular and vocal/true folds</li>\n<li><strong>Subglottis</strong>: Below the true folds</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Saccule</strong> an extension of ventricle is present in the paraglottic space.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1347","text":"A surgeon is worried about‌ the spread of laryngeal ‍carcinoma to the pre-epig‌lottic space. Which of th​e following structures do​ not border this space?","raw_text":"A surgeon is worried about the spread of laryngeal carcinoma to the pre-epiglottic space. Which of the following structures do not border this space?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyoepiglott‌ic ligament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Suprahyoid‌ epiglottis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thyroid‌ lamina","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thyrohyoi‌d membrane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Suprahyoid epiglottis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2945c4e9d8844325b1af4c7005191c4ax1280x2291.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Suprahyoid epiglottis</strong></p><p>Suprahyoid epiglottis does not form a boundary of the pre-epiglottic space. The <strong>infrahyoid epiglottis</strong> forms the <strong>posteromedial border.</strong></p>\n<p>Two potential spaces within the larynx provide an avenue for the spread of tumors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pre-epiglottic space</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Paraglottic space.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>pre-epiglottic space</strong> is bounded:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Superiorly by the <strong>hyoepiglottic ligament</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Anteriorly by the <strong>thyroid</strong> and <strong>thyrohyoid</strong> membrane</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Posteromedially by the <strong>infrahyoid epiglottis </strong>and<strong> quadrangular </strong>membrane.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>paraglottic space</strong> is bounded:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Laterally by thyroid</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Medially by ventricle and quadrangular membrane</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Inferomedially by conus elasticus</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Posteriorly by pyriform sinus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1371","text":"A 35-year-old smoker prese‌nts with hoarseness of vo‍ice. On laryngoscopy, the‌re is edema of the Reinke​’s space. Where is this s​pace located?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old smoker presents with hoarseness of voice. On laryngoscopy, there is edema of the Reinke’s space. Where is this space located?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glo‌ttis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Suprag‌lottis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subgl‌ottis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paraglot‌tic space","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Glottis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/724e735201224b9ea32d3f8744076b63x720x540.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Glottis</strong></p><p><strong>Reinke’s space</strong> is seen in the <strong>glottis</strong>. It is a potential space underneath the epithelium of vocal cords.</p>\n<p>It contains scanty subepithelial connective tissue. Edema of this space is commonly seen in smokers and is called <strong>Reinke’s edema </strong>or<strong> smoker’s larynx.</strong></p>\n<p>The laryngoscopy image below shows diffuse bilateral edema of the vocal folds or Reinke's edema.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1365","text":"You are performing a direc‌t laryngoscopy procedure ‍to enter the larynx and v‌isualise the structures i​t contains. Which of the ​following structures form​ the inlet of the larynx?​","raw_text":"You are performing a direct laryngoscopy procedure to enter the larynx and visualise the structures it contains. Which of the following structures form the inlet of the larynx?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ventricu‌lar fold","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aryepiglo‌ttic fold","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vocal‌ cord","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glossoepig‌lottic fold","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Aryepiglottic fold","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Aryepiglottic fold</strong></p><p><strong>Aryepiglottic folds</strong> form the <strong>inlet</strong> of the larynx.</p>\n<p>The laryngeal inlet is an oblique opening with the following boundaries:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior: Free margin of the <strong>epiglottis</strong></li>\n<li>Posterior:<strong> Interarytenoid fold</strong></li>\n<li>Sides: <strong>Aryepiglottic folds.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1359","text":"Which of the following mus‌cle causes opening of lar‍yngeal inlet?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscle causes opening of laryngeal inlet?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Voc‌alis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thyroepi‌glotticus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cricoar‌ytenoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thyroar‌ytenoid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thyroepiglotticus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thyroepiglotticus</strong></p><p><strong>Thyroepiglotticus </strong>causes the opening of the laryngeal inlet.</p>\n<p>The <strong>intrinsic</strong> muscles of the larynx acting on the laryngeal inlet are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Openers</strong> of laryngeal inlet\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Thyroepiglottic</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Closers</strong> of laryngeal inlet\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Interarytenoid</strong> (oblique part).</li>\n<li><strong>Aryepiglottic</strong> (posterior oblique part of interarytenoids).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"pf3jOQ_-","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"34_small_vessel_vasculitis","title":"34.Small Vessel Vasculitis","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MD7243","text":"Which of the following fin‌dings would you not expec‍t in a patient diagnosed ​with small-vessel vasculi​tis?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings would you not expect in a patient diagnosed with small-vessel vasculitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Palpable‌ purpura","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hema‌turia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemop‌tysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Visua‌l loss","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Visual loss","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Visual loss</strong></p><p>Ischemic optic neuropathy associated with <strong>giant cell arteritis </strong>(<strong>large </strong>vessel vasculitis)<strong> </strong>can present as <strong>visual loss</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Small</strong> vessel vasculitis is the inflammation of arterioles, capillaries, or venules. It starts with nonspecific symptoms like fever, fatigue, and arthralgia. Since it mainly affects the small vessels and capillaries present in the <strong>lungs, kidney, </strong>and<strong> skin</strong>, the majority of the patients will have:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hemoptysis</li>\n<li>Glomerulonephritis (hematuria, proteinuria)</li>\n<li>Palpable purpura</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4625","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures is most commonly ‍involved in cutaneous sma​ll-vessel vasculitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is most commonly involved in cutaneous small-vessel vasculitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Muscular‌ arteries","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arter‌ioles","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Postcapill‌ary venules","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Capil‌laries","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Postcapillary venules","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Postcapillary venules</strong></p><p>The structure most commonly involved in <strong>cutaneous small-vessel vasculitis</strong> is <strong>postcapillary venules</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cutaneous small-vessel vasculitis is defined as vasculitis which is <strong>limited to</strong> the small vessels of the <strong>skin</strong>. There is no systemic vasculitis and glomerulonephritis. It most commonly affects postcapillary venules.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by the presence of <strong>leukocytoclasis </strong>(presence of neutrophil debris in and around the blood vessels).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4163","text":"A patient presents with cu‌taneous vasculitis, glome‍rulonephritis, and periph​eral neuropathy. Which in​vestigation should be per‍formed next to help you d‍iagnose the condition?","raw_text":"A patient presents with cutaneous vasculitis, glomerulonephritis, and peripheral neuropathy. Which investigation should be performed next to help you diagnose the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AN‌CA","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"RA f‌actor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hb‌sAg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M‌IF","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. ANCA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. ANCA</strong></p><p>The presence of cutaneous vasculitis, glomerulonephritis, and peripheral neuropathy are suggestive of small-vessel vasculitis. Investigating for ANCA (antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies) should be the next step to aid in diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Small Vessel Vasculitis/ ANCA- associated Vasculitis</p>\n<table style=\"width: 785px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 96px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 248.215px; text-align: center; height: 96px;\">\n<p><strong>Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis</strong></p>\n<p>(Wegener's granulomatosis)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 272.785px; text-align: center; height: 96px;\"><strong>Microscopic Polyangiitis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 268px; text-align: center; height: 96px;\">\n<p><strong>Eosinophilic granulomatosis with Polyangiitis</strong></p>\n<p>(Churg Strauss syndrome)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 90px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 248.215px; text-align: center; height: 90px;\">Characterized by a triad of <strong>upper and lower respiratory tract</strong> involvement with <strong>glomerulonephritis</strong>.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 272.785px; text-align: center; height: 90px;\">Characterized by <strong>necrotizing vasculitis</strong> with <strong>glomerulonephritis</strong>(pauci immune, crescentic)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 268px; text-align: center; height: 90px;\">Characterized by <strong>asthma</strong>, <strong>peripheral and tissue eosinophilia</strong>, <strong>extravascular granuloma</strong> formation, and <strong>vasculitis</strong> of multiple organs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 114px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 248.215px; text-align: center; height: 114px;\"><strong>Nodular cavitary infiltrates</strong> in lungs showing necrotizing granulomas, and vasculitis on lung biopsy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 272.785px; text-align: center; height: 114px;\">\n<p><strong>Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage</strong>, <strong>hemoptysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>No URTI, No granulomas, No pulmonary nodules/cavities.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 268px; text-align: center; height: 114px;\">\n<p><strong>Mononeuritis multiplex</strong> - most common and characteristic.</p>\n<p>Biopsy showing granulomas with eosinophilic infiltrates. </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15.9913px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 248.215px; text-align: center; height: 15.9913px;\"><strong>C-ANCA +</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 272.785px; text-align: center; height: 15.9913px;\"><strong>P-ANCA +</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 268px; text-align: center; height: 15.9913px;\"><strong>P-ANCA +</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4631","text":"Which is the most common f‌orm of vasculitis associa‍ted with connective tissu​e disorders?","raw_text":"Which is the most common form of vasculitis associated with connective tissue disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medium and small vessel va‌sculitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medium vesse‌l vasculitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Small vesse‌l venulitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Small vesse‌l arteritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Small vessel venulitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Small vessel venulitis</strong></p><p>The most common form of vasculitis associated with connective tissue disorders is <strong>small-vessel venulitis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Connective tissue disorders</strong> that can cause vasculitis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li>Relapsing polychondritis</li>\n<li>Sjögren's syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1340","text":"A pathology resident is ex‌amining biopsy specimens ‍of patients with clinical​ features of vasculitis. ​In which of the following‍ conditions would he repo‍rt granulomatous inflamma‌tion of the vessels? Gian‌t cell arteritis Churg-St‌rauss syndrome Microscopi​c polyangiitis Polyarteri​tis nodosa Takayasu arter​itis","raw_text":"<p>A pathology resident is examining biopsy specimens of patients with clinical features of vasculitis. In which of the following conditions would he report granulomatous inflammation of the vessels?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Giant cell arteritis</li>\n<li>Churg-Strauss syndrome</li>\n<li>Microscopic polyangiitis</li>\n<li>Polyarteritis nodosa</li>\n<li>Takayasu arteritis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2,‌ and 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 4,‌ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2,‌ and 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3, 4‌, and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 2, and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 2, and 5</strong></p><p>Biopsy findings of <strong>giant cell arteritis</strong>, <strong>Churg-Strauss syndrome</strong>, and <strong>Takayasu arteritis</strong> show granulomatous inflammation of vessels as they are <strong>granulomatous</strong> vasculitis.</p>\n<p>The vasculitis diseases can be classified as:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 200px;\" width=\"467\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 225px; height: 37px;\"><strong>Granulomatous vasculitis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 226px; height: 37px;\"><strong>Non-granulomatous vasculitis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 92px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 225px; height: 92px;\">\n<p>Giant cell arteritis</p>\n<p>Takayasu arteritis</p>\n<p>Granulomatosis with polyangiitis</p>\n<p>Churg-Strauss syndrome</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 226px; height: 92px;\">\n<p>Polyarteritis nodosa</p>\n<p>Kawasaki disease</p>\n<p>Behcet's disease</p>\n<p>Microscopic polyangiitis</p>\n<p>Henoch-Schonlein purpura</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1177","text":"A 45-year-old woman presen‌ts with pain over the for‍ehead aggravated on bendi​ng and occasional hemopty​sis. Laboratory investiga‍tions reveal elevated ESR‍ and red cell casts in th‌e urine. Her serology is ‌positive for cANCA. What ‌is the most likely diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman presents with pain over the forehead aggravated on bending and occasional hemoptysis. Laboratory investigations reveal elevated ESR and red cell casts in the urine. Her serology is positive for cANCA. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Goodpasture‌'s syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Granulomatosis with polyan‌giitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Microscopic ‌polyangiitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Henoch-Scho‌nlein pupura","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Granulomatosis with polyangiitis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with <strong>cANCA</strong> positivity is suggestive of <strong>granulomatosis with polyangiitis </strong>(<strong>Wegener's</strong> granulomatosis). It is characterized by the involvement of the upper respiratory tract (<strong>sinusitis</strong>), lower respiratory tract (occasional <strong>hemoptysis</strong>), and kidneys (<strong>red cell casts</strong>).</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>granulomatous, </strong>necrotizing vasculitis of the <strong>small</strong> vessels that mainly affects:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Upper respiratory tract- presenting with <strong>sinusitis</strong>, otitis media, and nasal septal perforation.</li>\n<li>Lower respiratory tract- presenting with <strong>hemoptysis.</strong></li>\n<li>Kidney (<strong>glomerulonephritis</strong>)- presenting with proteinuria, hematuria, and hypertension.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Apart from these organs, the disease can also affect the eye and skin. Skin lesions present as palpable purpura or subcutaneous nodules. Laboratory investigations show <strong>anemia</strong>, leucocytosis, elevated ESR, and ANCA positivity.</p>\n<p>Options A and C: In Goodpasture's syndrome and microscopic polyangiitis, the symptoms of upper respiratory tract involvement are very rare.</p>\n<p>Option D: Henoch-Schonlein purpura is commonly seen in children. It presents with palpable purpura, joint pain, abdominal pain, and glomerulonephritis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4615","text":"A patient who presented wi‌th skin rashes, otitis me‍dia and hematuria has bee​n diagnosed with granulom​atosis with polyangiitis.‍ Which of the following d‍rugs would you not use in‌ the management of this p‌atient?","raw_text":"A patient who presented with skin rashes, otitis media and hematuria has been diagnosed with granulomatosis with polyangiitis. Which of the following drugs would you not use in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ritu‌ximab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cyclopho‌sphamide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abat‌acept","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Methot‌rexate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Abatacept","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Abatacept</strong></p><p><strong>Abatacept</strong> is a disease-modifying antirheumatic drug (<strong>DMARD</strong>) used in the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis.</p>\n<p>Important drugs used in the management of granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener's granulomatosis) are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cyclophosphamide</li>\n<li>Rituximab (anti CD20 antibody)</li>\n<li>Glucocorticoids</li>\n<li>Methotrexate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0186","text":"A middle-aged woman presen‌ts with hemoptysis, prote‍inuria, and the clinical ​presentation given below.​ What is the most likely ‍diagnosis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presents with hemoptysis, proteinuria, and the clinical presentation given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Churg-Strau‌ss syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lep‌rosy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos‌us","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Granulomatosis with polyan‌giitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3b64a3c5391d49d5858c7bb88196b2b0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Granulomatosis with polyangiitis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario showing <strong>saddle nose deformity</strong> along with <strong>hemoptysis,</strong> and <strong>proteinuria</strong> is suggestive of <strong>granulomatosis with polyangiitis</strong> (Wegener's granulomatosis).</p>\n<p>Involvement of upper respiratory tract in granulomatosis with polyangiitis manifests as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Paranasal sinusitis, serous otitis media</li>\n<li>Nasal ulcers, purulent or bloody nasal discharge</li>\n<li>Nasal perforation leading to the destruction of septum and saddle nose deformity</li>\n<li>Subglottic tracheal stenosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4608","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a feature associated ‍with Wegener's granulomat​osis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature associated with Wegener's granulomatosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmonar‌y nodules","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Venous t‌hrombosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Non-necrotizing vasculitis‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Glomerulo‌nephritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Non-necrotizing vasculitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d96048a027c146f7baa734dfd21c0f99x358x285.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Non-necrotizing vasculitis</strong></p><p>Wegener's granulomatosis is associated with <strong>necrotizing vasculitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Wegener's granulomatosis predominantly affects the <strong>small</strong> blood vessels of the upper respiratory tract, lungs, and kidneys. Lung involvement will show multiple, bilateral <strong>cavitating nodules</strong> and pulmonary <strong>infiltrates</strong> on chest X-ray.</p>\n<p>The radiograph below shows cavitating nodules in a patient with granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener's granulomatosis).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Renal involvement initially appears as focal or segmental <strong>glomerulonephritis </strong>on biopsy. If left untreated, it can lead to rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (RPGN). Patients with Wegener's granulomatosis have an <strong>increased</strong> incidence of <strong>venous thrombosis,</strong> like deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism.</p>\n<p>The diagnosis is confirmed by the presence of <strong>necrotizing granulomatous vasculitis</strong> on <strong>biopsy</strong>. This finding is commonly seen in the tissues obtained from <strong>lungs</strong>, skin, upper airways, and rarely kidneys.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6110","text":"A 57-year-old man presente‌d with complaints of feve‍r, weight loss, and hemop​tysis. His serology was p​ositive for pANCA and bio‍psy showed small vessel v‍asculitis with neutrophil‌ infiltration. Which of t‌he following is a feature‌ associated with his cond​ition?","raw_text":"A 57-year-old man presented with complaints of fever, weight loss, and hemoptysis. His serology was positive for pANCA and biopsy showed small vessel vasculitis with neutrophil infiltration. Which of the following is a feature associated with his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-necrotizing vasculitis‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fibrinoid‌ necrosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intravascular granuloma fo‌rmation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Immune complex mediated va‌sculitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fibrinoid necrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fibrinoid necrosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical presentation along with <strong>pANCA</strong> positivity and biopsy findings of <strong>leukocytoclastic</strong> (neutrophil infiltration) vasculitis point towards a diagnosis of <strong>microscopic polyangiitis.</strong> It is associated with <strong>fibrinoid necrosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Microscopic polyangiitis, also known as<strong> hypersensitivity vasculitis, </strong>causes segmental<strong> fibrinoid necrosis </strong>of<strong> small </strong>arterioles and venules. It is associated with<strong> neutrophil infiltration</strong>, and hence also called <strong>leukocytoclastic vasculitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Histopathology of the affected tissue will show <strong>necrotizing</strong> vasculitis of arterioles, venules, and capillaries <strong>without</strong> any <strong>granulomas</strong>. It is caused by ANCA (antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies) and is <strong>not</strong> mediated by the<strong> immune complex.</strong> It primarily affects the <strong>kidneys</strong> and <strong>lungs</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4568","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures is not useful to di‍stinguish between microsc​opic polyangiitis and gra​nulomatosis with polyangi‍itis?","raw_text":"Which of the following features is not useful to distinguish between microscopic polyangiitis and granulomatosis with polyangiitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Upper airway‌ involvement","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Granu‌lomas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonar‌y nodules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Necrotizing‌ vasculitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Necrotizing vasculitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Necrotizing vasculitis</strong></p><p><strong>Both</strong> microscopic polyangiitis and granulomatosis with polyangiitis <strong>are</strong> associated with <strong>necrotizing vasculitis</strong>. Hence, this feature is not useful to distinguish between them.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5300","text":"A triad of skin lesions, a‌symmetric mononeuritis mu‍ltiplex and eosinophilia ​is seen in which of the f​ollowing conditions?","raw_text":"A triad of skin lesions, asymmetric mononeuritis multiplex and eosinophilia is seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cryoglobulinemic vasculiti‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polyarteri‌tis nodosa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Churg Strau‌ss syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Giant cell‌ arteritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Churg Strauss syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Churg Strauss syndrome</strong></p><p>A triad of <strong>skin lesions,</strong> asymmetric<strong> mononeuritis multiplex, </strong>and<strong> eosinophilia</strong> is seen in <strong>Churg–Strauss syndrome </strong>(eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis).</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>necrotizing vasculitis</strong> of the small-to-medium-sized vessels and in order to be diagnosed with Churg-Strauss, a patient should have evidence of <strong>asthma</strong>, peripheral blood and tissue <strong>eosinophilia</strong> (&gt;1000 cells/μL), extravascular <strong>granuloma</strong> formation and clinical features consistent with vasculitis. </p>\n<p>Common presenting symptoms of this condition are<strong> lower respiratory tract symptoms </strong>such as severe attacks of asthma, u<strong>pper respiratory tract symptoms</strong> such as allergic rhinitis, sinusitis, and nasal polyps, mono neuritis multiplex (a type of peripheral neuropathy affecting at least two nerves) which presents with a complaint of pain, numbness, or weakness in the affected areas and skin lesions like palpable purpura and subcutaneous nodules.</p>\n<p><strong>Renal involvement</strong> is <strong>less</strong> common and less severe than other small vessel vasculitis.</p>\n<p><strong>P-ANCA</strong> antibodies are present in Churg Strauss syndrome. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Severe attacks of <strong>asthma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Mononeuritis multiplex:</strong> a type of peripheral neuropathy affecting at least two nerves. Patients will complain of pain, numbness, or weakness in the affected areas.</li>\n<li><strong>Skin lesions</strong> like palpable purpura and subcutaneous nodules.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Glucocorticoids</strong> show effective responses in these patients. Other drugs that have been tried are <strong>mepolizumab</strong> and <strong>rituximab</strong>.</p>\n<p>For fulminant multisystem disease, daily <strong>cyclophosphamide</strong> with <strong>prednisolone</strong> followed by <strong>azathioprine</strong> or <strong>methotrexate</strong> is used.</p>\n<p><strong>Option A: Cryoglobulinemic vasculitis</strong> presents with palpable purpura, arthralgia, neuropathy, and glomerulonephritis.  It is most commonly associated with <strong>Hepatitis C.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B: Polyarteritis nodosa</strong> can also present with mononeuritis multiplex and skin lesions, but they do <strong>not</strong> have <strong>eosinophilia, </strong>it has predominant<strong> neutrophilia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option D: Giant cell arteritis</strong> manifests with the cardinal symptom of headache localized to the temporal region. Common symptoms include headache, polymyalgia rheumatica , jaw claudication, fever, and decrease in body weight. Laboratory results show increased ESR, CRP levels, normochromic or mildly hypochromic anemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4616","text":"A 47-year-old housewife pr‌esented with recurrent at‍tacks of asthma and pain ​and numbness along the di​stribution of the median ‍and ulnar nerve. Her sero‍logy was positive for pAN‌CA. Further evaluation is‌ unlikely to reveal which‌ of the following finding​s?","raw_text":"A 47-year-old housewife presented with recurrent attacks of asthma and pain and numbness along the distribution of the median and ulnar nerve. Her serology was positive for pANCA. Further evaluation is unlikely to reveal which of the following findings?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peripheral blood eosinophi‌lia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tissue eo‌sinophilia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extravascul‌ar granuloma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Large vesse‌l vasculitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Large vessel vasculitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Large vessel vasculitis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of asthma, mononeuritis multiplex, and pANCA positivity is suggestive of Churg-Strauss syndrome (eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis). It affects <strong>small to medium</strong>-sized arteries. </p>\n<p>Laboratory investigations will show <strong>peripheral blood eosinophilia</strong>. Some patients may be positive for pANCA (antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies). Histopathology will show the presence of <strong>necrotizing vasculitis</strong>, <strong>tissue eosinophilia</strong>, and <strong>extravascular granulomas.</strong></p>\n<p>Histopathology of ANCA associated vasculitides can be remembered as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Microscopic polyangiitis: necrotizing vasculitis</li>\n<li>Wegener's granulomatosis: necrotizing vasculitis with granulomas</li>\n<li>Churg Strauss syndrome: necrotizing vasculitis with extravascular granulomas and eosinophilia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4584","text":"A 6-year-old boy presents ‌to the ER with colicky ab‍dominal pain. His mother ​tells you that he refuses​ to walk due to knee pain‍. On examination, palpabl‍e purpura is noted. What ‌is the most likely diagno‌sis?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old boy presents to the ER with colicky abdominal pain. His mother tells you that he refuses to walk due to knee pain. On examination, palpable purpura is noted. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Henoch Schon‌lein purpura","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hemolytic ur‌emic syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Juvenile Idiopathic arthri‌tis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IgA nep‌hropathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Henoch Schonlein purpura","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Henoch Schonlein purpura</strong></p><p>The given clinical presentation of palpable purpura, colicky abdominal pain, and arthritis, point towards a diagnosis of <strong>Henoch Schonlein purpura</strong> (HSP) also known as <strong>IgA vasculitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>small vessel vasculitis</strong> that occurs due to IgA immune complex deposition within the wall of the blood vessels. It is commonly seen in <strong>children</strong> and is characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Palpable purpura </strong>without thrombocytopenia and coagulopathy.</li>\n<li><strong>Arthralgia- </strong>commonly affecting hip, knee, or ankle joints.</li>\n<li><strong>Abdominal pain</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Glomerulonephritis: </strong>proteinuria, microscopic hematuria, RBC casts in urine.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The skin biopsy will show small vessel vasculitis, with <strong>IgA </strong>and <strong>C3 deposition </strong>in the affected organs<strong> </strong>by immunofluorescence.</p>\n<p>Option B: Hemolytic uremic syndrome is characterized by a <strong>triad</strong> of <span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury.</span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Option C: Juvenile idiopathic arthritis (<strong>JIA</strong>) is characterized by fever of at least 2 weeks duration associated with arthritis, evanescent erythematous <strong>rash</strong>, hepatosplenomegaly, generalized lymphadenopathy, and serositis.</span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Option D: Patients with <strong>IgA nephropathy</strong> or <strong>Berger's disease</strong> present with gross hematuria, often associated with a preceding or ongoing viral infection of the upper respiratory tract (<strong>synpharyngitic presentation</strong>).</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4162","text":"Cryoglobulinemia is associ‌ated with _______.","raw_text":"Cryoglobulinemia is associated with _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepat‌itis C","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ovarian‌ cancer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diab‌etes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leuk‌emia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hepatitis C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hepatitis C</strong></p><p><strong>Cryoglobulinemia</strong> is associated with <strong>hepatitis C</strong> infection.</p>\n<p>Following Hepatitis C infection, there is an aberrant <strong>immune response</strong> leading to an <strong>immune complex formation</strong> containing <strong>hepatitis C antigens</strong>, <strong>polyclonal IgG</strong> specific to Hepatitis C, and <strong>monoclonal IgM rheumatoid factor</strong>, which triggers an <strong>inflammatory cascade</strong> results in Cryoglobulinemic <strong>vasculitis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Cryoglobulins</strong> are the <strong>immunoglobulins</strong> that <strong>precipitate</strong> at temperatures <strong>less than 37°C</strong>, the presence of these cryoglobulins in serum leads to Cryoglobulinemia, which is characterized by <strong>cutaneous vasculitis</strong>, <strong>palpable purpura</strong>, <strong>arthritis</strong>, <strong>peripheral neuropathy</strong>, and <strong>glomerulonephritis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Important conditions associated with cryoglobulinemia are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hepatitis C</strong></li>\n<li>Multiple myeloma</li>\n<li>Connective tissue disorders</li>\n<li>Lymphoproliferative disorders</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"oaL0h-r0","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"34_helminthology___nematodes","title":"34.Helminthology   Nematodes","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA8985","text":"Which of the following par​asites is ovoviviparous?","raw_text":"Which of the following parasites is ovoviviparous?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enterobius ​vermicularis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trichuris​ trichiura","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Strongyloide​s stercoralis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trichinell​a spiralis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Strongyloides stercoralis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Strongyloides stercoralis</strong></p><p><em><strong>Strongyloides stercoralis</strong></em> is an <strong>ovoviviparous</strong> parasite.</p>\n<p>Nematodes are classified as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Oviparous -</strong> lay eggs:\n<ul>\n<li>Unsegmented eggs: <em>Ascaris lumbricoides, Trichuris trichuria</em></li>\n<li>Segmented eggs:  <em>Ancylostoma duodenale, Necator americanus</em></li>\n<li>Eggs containing larva: <em>Enterobius vermicularis</em></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Viviparous -</strong> produce larvae. e.g., <em>Trichinella spiralis, Wuchereria bancrofti, Brugia malayi</em></li>\n<li><strong>Ovoviviparous </strong>– lay eggs containing fully formed larvae that hatch immediately. e.g., <em>Strongyloides stercoralis</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6292","text":"Identify the helminth from​ the life cycle given bel‍ow.","raw_text":"Identify the helminth from the life cycle given below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ancylostom​a duodenale","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Strongyloide​s stercoralis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ascaris lu​mbricoides","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enterobius ​vermicularis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Strongyloides stercoralis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d56d5520329b4aceb6b6e248aabc0b56.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Strongyloides stercoralis</strong></p><p>The image shows the life cycle of<em><strong> Strongyloides stercoralis.</strong></em></p>\n<p>The <strong>infective form</strong> of strongyloides is the <strong>filariform larva. </strong>Infection occurs through penetration of the skin by larvae in the soil. Then, they migrate to the small intestine.</p>\n<p>The larvae molt twice and become adult female worms in the small intestine. The eggs laid in the mucosa of the intestine hatch immediately releasing the rhabditiform larva (L1 stage). These rhabditiform larvae in the bowel can develop directly into filariform larvae that penetrate the colonic wall or perianal skin and enter the circulation to repeat the migration. This establishes ongoing<strong> internal reinfection.</strong></p>\n<p>The rhabditiform larva can be seen on microscopic examination of stool.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9300","text":"A 24-year-old patient pres​ented with complaints of ‍diarrhea, vomiting, and a​bdominal pain for 1 week.‌ The stool microscopy rev‌ealed the following. What‍ is the appropriate treat‍ment for this condition?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old patient presented with complaints of diarrhea, vomiting, and abdominal pain for 1 week.  The stool microscopy revealed the following.  What is the appropriate treatment for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iverm​ectin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tinid​azole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alben​dazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Praziq​uantel","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ivermectin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/80d5383ef8b1436ca3e35ca4bf41389d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ivermectin</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the stool microscopy showing the <strong>rhabditiform larva </strong>of <em>S. stercoralis </em>are suggestive of <strong>strongyloidiasis</strong>. The most appropriate treatment for this condition would be<strong> ivermectin.</strong></p>\n<p>Strongyloidiasis is caused by <strong><em>Strongyloides stercoralis.</em> </strong>It is generally a benign and asymptomatic condition. In some cases, there may be cutaneous, pulmonary, and intestinal manifestations.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9042","text":"An 8-year-old child was br​ought with complaints of ‍perianal itching which is​ worse in the night. The ‌scotch tape method demons‌trates eggs as shown in t‍he image given below. Wha‍t is the most likely caus​ative agent?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old child was brought with complaints of perianal itching which is worse in the night.  The scotch tape method demonstrates eggs as shown in the image given below. What is the most likely causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hook​worm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Whip​worm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pin​worm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Roun​dworm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pinworm","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5a2d3be4440f472699bf053dd570d694.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2005e72714c14e06a8b4c646ac980231x600x936.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b8371b54f94d4c1d9864e308e6cd28f3x1280x1309.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/013966c4a39f4db79965ee3bb0f3e7adx435x497.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pinworm</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of perianal itching and the scotch tape microscopy showing the eggs are suggestive of infestation with<strong> pinworm (<em>Enterobius vermicularis</em>).</strong></p>\n<p>The eggs of pinworm are <strong>plano-convex</strong> in shape, colorless, <strong>non-bile stained</strong>, and <strong>float</strong> in the <strong>saturated salt solution</strong>. Eggshell is double layered and relatively thick, though transparent.</p>\n<p>Since pinworms are not released in feces, the diagnosis cannot be made by conventional fecal ova and parasite tests. <strong>NIH swab</strong> method and the <strong>scotch tape method</strong> are used for the collection of specimens.</p>\n<p>The NIH swab (National Institutes of Health, USA) consists of a glass rod, at one end of which a piece of transparent cellophane is attached with a rubber band. The cellophane part is used for swabbing by rolling over the perianal area. It is returned to the test tube and sent to the laboratory. The cellophane piece is detached, spread over a glass side, and examined microscopically.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the NIH swab.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>In scotch tape method, the tape is firmly pressed against the anal margin, covering all sides. The tape is transferred to a glass slide with sticky side down. Then a drop of toluene is added for clearing and examined under the microscope.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The below image shows the life cycle of <em>Enterobius vermicularis</em>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0622","text":"Which of the following hel​minthic infections that i‍s commonly transmitted by​ soil, is characterized b‌y​ the egg in stool as sh‌own in the image below?","raw_text":"Which of the following helminthic infections that is commonly transmitted by soil, is characterized by​ the egg in stool as shown in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trichuris​ trichiura","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ancyclostom​a duodenale","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Necator a​mericanus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ascaris lu​mbricoides","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Trichuris trichiura","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b20e0bc268324371b8e228b3444601e6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Trichuris trichiura</strong></p><p>The given image shows the egg of <em><strong>Trichuris</strong> <strong>trichiura</strong></em> (whipworm).</p>\n<p>The characteristic features of whipworm eggs are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Eggs are barrel-shaped in the middle with a projecting mucus plug at each pole containing an unsegmented ovum. The plugs are colorless.</li>\n<li>They float in the saturated salt solution.</li>\n<li>It has a triple shell, the outermost layer of which is stained brown.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They are commonly transmitted by soil. The eggs passed in the stool are unembryonated. Microscopic identification of the eggs in a stool specimen is diagnostic of whipworm infection.</p>\n<p>In moderate infections of 20 adult worms or less, the worms are confined to the caecum and ascending colon. The symptoms include epigastric and lower abdominal pain, diarrhoea (rarely bloody), vomiting, flatulence and distention, weight loss, and increased blood and iron loss.</p>\n<p>In<strong> heavy infections</strong> of 200 adult worms or more (<strong>trichuriasis dysentery syndrome</strong>), the worms are in the distal colon and rectum. The symptoms include bloody diarrhoea with profuse mucus, abdominal pain, tenesmus, weight loss leading to cachexia, severe anemia, and rectal prolapse. On examination, clubbing may be present. Blood investigations may show moderate eosinophilia.</p>\n<p>Chronic infection leads to growth retardation and nutritional deficiencies. Around 0.005 ml of blood is lost per day per worm. In heavy infections, as much as 4 to 5 ml can be lost per day per worm.</p>\n<p>Treatment is mebendazole or albendazole.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8989","text":"A college student came to ​the OPD with fever and mu‍scle aches. He tells you ​he had consumed smoked po‌rk at a party 2 weeks bac‌k. Following this, he had‍ developed abdominal cram‍ps and diarrhea which had​ resolved on their own. C​BC revealed eosinophilia ​and the muscle biopsy sho‌wed the following finding‍. What is the causative o​rganism?","raw_text":"A college student came to the OPD with fever and muscle aches. He tells you he had consumed smoked pork at a party 2 weeks back. Following this, he had developed abdominal cramps and diarrhea which had resolved on their own. CBC revealed eosinophilia and the muscle biopsy showed the following finding. What is the causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trichuris​ trichiura","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trichinell​a spiralis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ascaris lu​mbricoides","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enterobius ​vermicularis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trichinella spiralis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e748fd0b98c249d3963c63ab97f211b0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e5dce3ab9f344f62a62e64196ffdc5b3x640x480.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trichinella spiralis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and muscle biopsy showing larvae are suggestive of <strong>trichinellosis</strong>. It is caused by <strong><em>Trichinella spiralis</em>.</strong></p>\n<p><em>Trichinella spiralis</em><strong> </strong>is known as the <strong>pork worm</strong> since the infection is acquired by consuming raw or undercooked pork. The<strong> initial</strong> infection presents with <strong>diarrhea</strong> and <strong>abdominal pain</strong>. In the<strong> later</strong> phase of the infection, the larvae move from the intestines into the circulation and migrate into muscles where they are encysted. This causes symptoms like<strong> fever</strong> and<strong> myalgia</strong>.</p>\n<p>These larvae are calcified in about 5-6 months. Eosinophilia caused by parasitic infection also comes down after 6 months.</p>\n<p>Detection of spiral larva in muscle tissue is done by performing a muscle biopsy. Deltoid, biceps, gastrocnemius, or pectoralis muscles are usually selected for biopsy.</p>\n<p> Other <strong>parasites</strong> that can be demonstrated in a <strong>muscle biopsy</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>T. solium</em></li>\n<li><em>T. cruzi</em> (amastigote)</li>\n</ul>\n<p> The image below shows <em>Trichinella spiralis</em> larvae within the muscle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8993","text":"In a patient with a positi​ve Bachman intradermal te‍st, which of the followin​g infections will you sus‌pect?","raw_text":"In a patient with a positive  Bachman intradermal test, which of the following infections will you suspect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Schisto​somiasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kala​-azar","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydatid​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trichin​ellosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Trichinellosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Trichinellosis</strong></p><p><strong>Bachman intradermal test</strong> is used for the diagnosis of <strong>trichinellosis.</strong></p>\n<p>In this test, 1:5,000 or 1:10,000 dilution of the trichinella larval antigen is injected intradermally. An <strong>erythematous wheal</strong> appears in positive cases within <strong>15-20 minutes. </strong>The test remains positive for years after infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6629","text":"Identify the organism show​n in this image.","raw_text":"Identify the organism shown in this image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strongyloide​s stercoralis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enterobius ​vermicularis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ancylostom​a duodenale","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ascaris lu​mbricoides","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ascaris lumbricoides","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2431e17c80784bedb4efdb735b2c6f59.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ascaris lumbricoides</strong></p><p>The given image shows the <strong><em>Ascaris lumbricoides</em></strong><em> </em>(roundworm). It is the <strong>most common</strong> of <strong>human helminths</strong> and is distributed worldwide.</p>\n<p>Adult worms live in the <strong>small intestine</strong> (<strong>85%</strong> in the <strong>jejunum</strong> and 15% in the ileum). The roundworm, Ascaris lumbricoides is the <strong>largest nematode parasite in the human intestine.</strong></p>\n<p>The adult worms are large<strong> cylindrical </strong>worms, with<strong> tapering ends</strong>, the anterior end being more pointed than the posterior.</p>\n<p><strong>Man</strong> is the <strong>natural host</strong>. There is <strong>no intermediate host.</strong></p>\n<p>Infection occurs through food or <strong>water contaminated</strong> with nightsoil or human feces containing <strong>eggs of ascaris.</strong> The eggs contain the <strong>infective rhabditiform larva</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0824","text":"A 9- year-old boy was brou​ght to the casualty with ‍fever and colicky abdomin​al pain. An erect X-ray a‌bdomen showed multiple ai‌r-fluid levels. Stool exa‍mination revealed the fol‍lowing egg. Which of the ​following is false about ​the organism causing this​ condition?","raw_text":"A 9- year-old boy was brought to the casualty with fever and colicky abdominal pain. An erect X-ray abdomen showed multiple air-fluid levels. Stool examination revealed the following egg. Which of the following is false about the organism causing this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Human is th​e only host","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Can cause p​ancreatitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Can cause Loeffler's syndr​ome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Egg containing L1 larva is​ infective","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Egg containing L1 larva is infective","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6dbd247b143148fcb0214d8f643377f3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/625fefe565634b0aa972a57b0dec8625x965x886.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Egg containing L1 larva is infective</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the image are suggestive of <strong>ascariasis</strong> caused by <em><strong>Ascaris lumbricoides. </strong></em>Embryonated <strong>egg</strong> containing the <strong>L3</strong> <strong>larva</strong> is the <strong>infective form</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Man</strong> is the <strong>only</strong> natural <strong>host</strong>. There is no intermediate host. The <strong>infection</strong> is acquired <strong>via</strong> the consumption of food or water contaminated with <strong>eggs.</strong> The eggs hatch after reaching the duodenum to release the larvae. These penetrate the intestinal mucosa and enter the portal vessels. From the liver, larvae reach the systemic circulation, lungs and then reach the small intestine to produce symptoms.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features:</strong></p>\n<p>Due to the <strong>migrating larvae</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Dry cough</li>\n<li>Asthmatic wheezing</li>\n<li><strong>Loeffler's syndrome</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Due to the <strong>adult worm</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Asymptomatic</li>\n<li>Nutritional effects - protein-energy malnutrition, Vitamin A deficiency</li>\n<li>Toxic effects - urticaria, angioedema due to hypersensitivity</li>\n<li>Mechanical effects - colicky abdominal pain, volvulus, and intestinal obstruction</li>\n<li>Ectopic ascariasis - acute biliary obstruction, <strong>pancreatitis</strong>, and obstructive appendicitis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The life cycle of <em>Ascaris lumbricoides</em> is given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8987","text":"What is the most common ca​use for visceral larva mi‍grans?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause for visceral larva migrans?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Toxocar​a canis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ancylostoma​ braziliense","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ascaris lu​mbricoides","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Strongyloide​s stercoralis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Toxocara canis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Toxocara canis</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> cause for <strong>visceral larva migrans </strong>is<strong> <em>Toxocara canis. </em></strong>It occurs most commonly in children and presents as fever, hepatosplenomegaly, and pneumonitis.</p>\n<p><strong>Larva migrans </strong>is<strong> </strong>generally seen when <strong>human infection</strong> occurs with <strong>nonhuman</strong> <strong>species</strong> of nematodes. It can be classified into cutaneous or visceral types, depending on whether the larval migration takes place in the skin or in deeper tissues.</p>\n<p><strong>Visceral larva migrans</strong> is caused by the migration of nematode larvae that are acquired via the <strong>oral route</strong>. <strong>Cutaneous larva migrans</strong> is caused by the migration of nematode larvae (most commonly A<strong>ncylostoma braziliense</strong>) that are acquired via <strong>skin penetration</strong>.</p>\n<p>Patients having visceral larva migrans may develop neurological disturbances (neural larva migrans) and endophthalmitis (ophthalmic larva migrans).</p>\n<p><strong>Differences between cutaneous larva migrans and visceral larva migrans:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 210px;\" width=\"566\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.133px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.695px;\">Cutaneous larva migrans</td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.172px;\">Visceral larva migrans</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.133px;\">Tissue involved</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.695px;\">Skin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.172px;\">Various organs of the body like the liver, lungs, and eyes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.133px;\">Infecting Organism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.695px;\">Mostly by non-human nematodes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.172px;\">Mostly by non-human nematodes (mainly by dog and cat)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.133px;\">Portal of Entry</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.695px;\">Penetration of skin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.172px;\">Ingestion of infected eggs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.133px;\">Eosinophilia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.695px;\">Mild</td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.172px;\">Persistent high</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.133px;\">Serodiagnosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.695px;\">Not developed</td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.172px;\">Well developed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.133px;\">Treatment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.695px;\">Thiabendazole</td>\n<td style=\"width: 265.172px;\">Diethylcarbamazine and prednisolone</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9040","text":"A middle-aged woman comes ​to the OPD with complaint‍s of weakness for the pas​t 3 months. She is diagno‌sed to have microcytic hy‌pochromic anemia. The sto‍ol microscopy reveals the‍ following. What is the m​ost probable causative ag​ent?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman comes to the OPD with complaints of weakness for the past 3 months. She is diagnosed to have microcytic hypochromic anemia. The stool microscopy reveals the following. What is the most probable causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ancylostom​a duodenale","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Strongyloide​s stercoralis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enterobius ​vermicularis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ascaris lu​mbricoides","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ancylostoma duodenale","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2826412ad0944ee5882351902b83c02d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ancylostoma duodenale</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of iron deficiency anemia and the stool microscopy showing the egg is suggestive of infection by <em><strong>Ancylostoma duodenale.</strong></em></p>\n<p>Eggs of both <em>Ancylostoma duodenale</em> (old world hookworm) and <em>Necator americanus </em>(new world hookworm) are identical.</p>\n<p>The hookworm <strong>eggs</strong> are <strong>oval</strong> or elliptical in shape, <strong>colorless</strong>, and <strong>non-bile stained. </strong>It has a thin transparent hyaline shell membrane. The ovum is segmented with <strong>4 to 8</strong> <strong>blastomeres</strong>. There is a clear space between the segmented ovum and eggshell. Eggs <strong>float</strong> in the <strong>saturated salt solution</strong>.</p>\n<p>Humans are infected when filariform larvae in moist soil penetrate the skin or subcutaneous tissue. The larvae are then carried in the venous circulation to the heart and lungs, where they may cause mild transient pneumonitis or bronchitis. They migrate up the respiratory tract to the pharynx, are swallowed, and reach the small intestine producing epigastric pain, dyspepsia, nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea. </p>\n<p><em>A. duodenale</em> or <em>N. americanus</em> ingest blood, secrete anticoagulants, and interfere with the absorption of iron, B12, and folic acid. This leads to<strong> iron deficiency anemia</strong> and <strong>protein-energy malnutrition</strong> resulting from blood loss.</p>\n<p>The average blood loss of the host per worm per day is 0.2 mL with <em>Ancylostoma duodenale</em> and 0.03 mL with <em>Necator americanus.</em></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9043","text":"A patient presented to the​ OPD with dry cough, low-‍grade fever and wheezing.​ He is diagnosed with Loe‌ffler’s syndrome. Which o‌f the following parasites‍ does not cause this?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the OPD with dry cough, low-grade fever and wheezing. He is diagnosed with  Loeffler’s syndrome.  Which of the following parasites does not cause this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ascaris lu​mbricoides","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Necator a​mericanus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Strongyloide​s stercoralis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enterobius ​vermicularis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Enterobius vermicularis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Enterobius vermicularis</strong></p><p><strong>Loeffler's syndrome</strong> is an <strong>eosinophilic inflammation </strong>of the <strong>lung </strong>caused by <em>Ascaris lumbricoides, Strongyloides stercoralis</em> and <em>Necator americanus</em>. <em>Enterobius vermicularis<strong> </strong></em>does not cause Loeffler’s syndrome.</p>\n<p>It is associated with pneumonia characterized by dry cough, low-grade fever, wheezing, urticaria, and eosinophilia.</p>\n<p>Parasites causing Loeffler’s syndrome are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Migrating larva of:\n<ul>\n<li><em>Ascaris lumbricoides</em></li>\n<li><em>Strongyloides stercoralis</em></li>\n<li><em>Ancylostoma duodenale</em></li>\n<li><em>Necator americanus</em></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><em>Echinococcus granulosus</em></li>\n<li><em>Cryptosporidium parvum</em></li>\n<li>Eggs of <em>Paragonimus westermani</em></li>\n<li><em>Entamoeba histolytica</em></li>\n<li><em>Trichomonas tenax</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0260","text":"A 27-year-old woman had go​ne on a trek in the Amazo‍n rainforest. A month lat​er she presented to the O‌PD with the following les‌ion which was painful and‍ pruritic. Her systemic e‍xamination was normal. Wh​ich of the following orga​nisms is most likely to c​ause this condition?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old woman had gone on a trek in the Amazon rainforest. A month later she presented to the OPD with the following lesion which was painful and pruritic. Her systemic examination was normal. Which of the following organisms is most likely to cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strongyloide​s stercoralis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ancylostoma​ braziliense","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Toxocar​a canis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Necator a​mericanus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ancylostoma braziliense","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c464fe4119eb41498fafa8a7db61855e.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ancylostoma braziliense</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the image showing <strong>serpiginous skin lesions</strong> are suggestive of <strong>cutaneous larva migrans </strong>or creeping eruption or ground itch.<strong> </strong>This<strong> </strong>is most commonly caused by <em><strong>Ancylostoma braziliense.</strong></em></p>\n<p>Cutaneous larva migrans is caused by <strong>non-human hookworms</strong> like <em>Ancylostoma braziliense</em> and<em> Ancylostoma caninum.</em> It is<em> </em>acquired via <strong>direct skin contact</strong> with soil contaminated by dog or cat feces. The larvae remain confined to the<strong> outer layers of skin</strong>, as they are unable to penetrate the basement membrane of the human skin.</p>\n<p>It presents as erythematous, pruritic papules at the site of entry and serpiginous tracks in the epidermis. The symptoms begin to appear within the first two weeks of the patient’s return from a <strong>tropical</strong> or endemic <strong>country</strong>. The larval track progresses at approximately 1 cm/h<strong> </strong>with cutaneous lesions only progressing<strong> </strong>a few millimeters to a few centimeters daily. </p>\n<p>Diagnosis is based mainly on clinical features.</p>\n<p>The treatment includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thiabendazole topical application</li>\n<li>Albendazole 400mg/day X 3 days or</li>\n<li>Ivermectin 200mcg/kg single dose stat</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The differences between cutaneous larva migrans and visceral larva migrans are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 220px; width: 690px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 223px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 221.6875px;\"><strong>Cutaneous larva migrans</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.3125px;\"><strong>Visceral larva migrans</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 223px;\">Tissue involved</td>\n<td style=\"width: 221.6875px;\">Skin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.3125px;\">Various organs of the body like the liver, lungs, and eyes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 223px;\">Infecting Organism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 221.6875px;\">Mostly by non-human nematodes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.3125px;\">Mostly by non-human nematodes (mainly by dog and cat)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 223px;\">Portal of Entry</td>\n<td style=\"width: 221.6875px;\">Penetration of skin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.3125px;\">Ingestion of infected eggs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 223px;\">Eosinophilia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 221.6875px;\">Mild</td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.3125px;\">Persistent high</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 223px;\">Serodiagnosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 221.6875px;\">Not developed</td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.3125px;\">Well developed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 223px;\">Treatment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 221.6875px;\">Thiabendazole</td>\n<td style=\"width: 224.3125px;\">Diethylcarbamazine and prednisolone</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: A rapidly moving lesion (10cm/hr) produced by <strong><em>Strongyloides stercoralis</em></strong> is known as <strong>larva currens </strong>(running larva). It<strong> </strong>occurs as an otherwise asymptomatic lesion in <strong>immunocompetent</strong> individuals. In immunocompromised, hyper-infestation syndrome occurs with paralytic ileus and secondary Gram-negative sepsis. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4720","text":"Which the following filari​al worms are non-pathogen‍ic to humans?","raw_text":"Which the following filarial worms are non-pathogenic to humans?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onchocerc​a volvulus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brugia​ malayi","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wuchereria​ bancrofti","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mansonell​a ozzardi","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mansonella ozzardi","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mansonella ozzardi</strong></p><p><em><strong>Mansonella ozzardi</strong></em> is generally <strong>non-pathogenic </strong>to humans.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Option A: <em>Onchocerca volvulus</em> infection may manifest as skin nodules or onchocercoma which are formed as a result of fibroblastic reaction around adult worms deep in the subcutaneous tissues. Microfilariae cause lesions in the skin and eyes.</p>\n<p>Options B and C: In lymphatic filariasis (Wuchereria and Brugia)<strong><em>,</em></strong> the typical manifestations are caused by the adult worms blocking lymph nodes and vessels. Microfilaria (larval form) are responsible for allergic manifestations seen in occult filariasis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4713","text":"A 55-year-old man came to ​the hospital with complai‍nts of progressive swelli​ng of his legs. On examin‌ation, you note bilateral‌ non-pitting edema of the‍ lower limbs with thicken‍ing of the overlying skin​. What is the best next s​tep in the management of ​this patient?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man  came to the hospital with complaints of progressive swelling of his legs. On examination, you note bilateral non-pitting edema of the lower limbs with thickening of the overlying skin. What is the best next step in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"P​CR","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphosci​ntigraphy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Night blood test for worms​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lymphang​iography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Night blood test for worms","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/488f4bdd18504a6a86ea27ffdd12713c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Night blood test for worms</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture is suggestive of <strong>lymphatic</strong> <strong>filariasis </strong>(elephantiasis)<strong>.</strong> The best next step would be to test the <strong>night blood for worms</strong> since the filarial species is nocturnally periodic (<strong>between 10 pm and 4 am</strong>) in India.</p>\n<p>Lymphatic filariasis is caused by <em>Wuchereria bancrofti</em> and <em>Brugia malayi</em>.</p>\n<p>Elephantiasis is a delayed sequel to repeated lymphangitis, obstruction, and lymphedema. Repeated leakage of lymph into tissues first results in lymphedema, then to elephantiasis.</p>\n<p>Diethylcarbamazine is the drug of choice.</p>\n<p>Option A: PCR is negative in chronic filariasis.</p>\n<p>Options B &amp; D: Lymphangiography and lymphoscintigraphy may be used to demonstrate abnormal lymphatic urinary fistula leading to chyluria in chronic filariasis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9046","text":"A child from Bihar is brou​ght to the AIIMS ER with ‍a high-grade fever. On ex​amination, bilaterally en‌larged, tender inguinal l‌ymph nodes are present. T‍he peripheral smear exami‍nation finding is shown i​n the image. What is the ​most likely causative org​anism?","raw_text":"A child from Bihar is brought to the AIIMS ER with a high-grade fever. On examination, bilaterally enlarged, tender inguinal lymph nodes are present. The peripheral smear examination finding is shown in the image. What is the most likely causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wuchereria​ bancrofti","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brugia​ malayi","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Loa​ loa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Onchocerc​a volvulus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Brugia malayi","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/26508b626fdd45f09bb7a4c6846fe7c0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/91f28ca3bfd84333ad76a2623d000026x600x688.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Brugia malayi</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the peripheral smear showing <strong>sheathed microfilaria </strong>with<strong> two nuclei </strong>at the<strong> tail tip </strong>is suggestive of infection with<em><strong> Brugia malayi.</strong></em></p>\n<p>Acute infection may manifest as <strong>acute adenolymphangitis</strong> which is characterized by<strong> high fever</strong>, lymphatic inflammation (lymphangitis and lymphadenitis) and transient local edema. The most commonly affected lymph nodes are <strong>inguinal nodes</strong> followed by axillary nodes which become <strong>enlarged, painful and tender.</strong></p>\n<p>The following are the characteristic features of microfilaria of <em>Brugia malayi</em>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Smaller in size compared to microfilaria of <em>W. bancrofti.</em></li>\n<li>Shows kinks and secondary curves.</li>\n<li>Cephalic space is longer.</li>\n<li>Carries double stylets at the anterior end.</li>\n<li>Nuclear column appears blurred in Giemsa-stained films.</li>\n<li>Tail tip carries 2 distinct nuclei, one terminal and the other subterminal.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below image shows the microfilaria of <em>Brugia malayi.</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8520","text":"A patient from Kerala has ​presented to your OPD wit‍h fever. A magnified view​ of peripheral blood smea‌r is shown below. Which o‌f the following is false ‍regarding the organism se‍en?","raw_text":"A patient from Kerala has presented to your OPD with fever. A magnified view of peripheral blood smear is shown below. Which of the following is false regarding the organism seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DEC provocation test is us​ed to demonstrate microfi‍laria during the day","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Second stage filariform la​rva is the infective form‍","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Microfilaria is the major ​diagnostic form","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Filarial stage of adult wo​rms are responsible for d‍isease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Second stage filariform larva is the infective form","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a3585ecedce3443db89ceef9160269ef.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/158b719e193c4f96b5b54bde9ff948c8x439x319.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f4a3f3f470b144788b95c1e4af58ea33x1280x3534.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Second stage filariform larva is the infective form</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with a peripheral smear showing <strong>sheathed microfilaria</strong> with a pointed <strong>tail free of nuclei</strong> is suggestive of <em><strong>Wuchereria bancrofti.</strong></em> The <strong>third stage filariform larva</strong> is the <strong>infective form.</strong></p>\n<p><em>Wuchereria bancrofti</em> infection is usually asymptomatic<strong>. </strong>The symptoms are due to <strong>obstruction of lymphatics</strong> by the filarial stage of <strong>adult worms</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lymphatic vessels of the male genitalia are most commonly affected in bancroftian filariasis, producing episodic funiculitis, epididymitis, and orchitis. Adenolymphangitis of the extremities is less common. </p>\n<p>Hydrocele is the most common sign of chronic bancroftian filariasis, followed by lymphedema, elephantiasis, and chyluria. The swelling involves the whole leg, the whole arm, the scrotum, the vulva, or the breast.</p>\n<p><strong>DEC provocation test</strong> is used to demonstrate <strong>microfilaria</strong> during the<strong> day</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the life cycle of <em>Wuchereria bancrofti.</em></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image below shows the difference between microfilaria of various filarial nematodes.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9044","text":"A 54-year-old farmer from ​Assam presented with the ‍finding shown in the imag​e. He had also developed ‌a painless swelling in hi‌s elbow 2 years back whic‍h has gradually been incr‍easing in size. Which of ​the following is the drug​ of choice to treat this ​condition?","raw_text":"A 54-year-old farmer from Assam presented with the finding shown in the image.  He had also developed a painless swelling in his elbow 2 years back which has gradually been increasing in size.  Which of the following is the drug of choice to treat this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eflorn​ithine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sur​amin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Iverm​ectin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Diethylca​rbamazine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ivermectin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/071a39c1b056476c9f171ef58db6951d.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ivermectin</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario and image showing <strong>sclerosing keratitis</strong> are suggestive of <strong>onchocerchiasis </strong>(river blindness). The drug of choice is <strong>ivermectin. </strong></p>\n<p>It is caused by <strong><em>Onchocerca volvulus</em>. </strong>It is also known as convoluted filaria or blinding filaria.</p>\n<p>The vector is blackfly (<em>Simulium</em> species) which breed in fast-flowing rivers, and therefore the disease is most common along the course of rivers. Hence, it is also called <strong>river blindness</strong>.</p>\n<p>The usual presentation is formation of a <strong>subcutaneous nodule</strong> or onchocercoma. It is a firm non-tender nodule arising from bony sites like scalp, elbow, ribs, and iliac crest. It is associated with pruritus and rashes.</p>\n<p>The microfilaria in the eyes cause photophobia and gradual loss of vision. The most severe manifestation is <strong>sclerosing keratitis</strong> and <strong>chorioretinitis</strong> which causes blindness. It is the 2<sup>nd</sup> major infective cause of blindness in the world after trachoma.</p>\n<p>Suramin and diethylcarbamazine (DEC) were used earlier but are not used nowadays. <strong>DEC</strong> destroys microfilariae but usually causes <strong>Mazzotti reaction</strong> consisting of pruritus, rash, lymphadenopathy, fever, hypotension, and occasionally, eye damage.</p>\n<p>Note: Onchocerciasis is not endemic in India but sporadic cases have been reported from North-Eastern India.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9048","text":"A middle-aged woman presen​ts to your clinic with ph‍otosensitivity, redness, ​and watering of the eyes ‌for the last 2 days. She ‌shows you this photo of a‍ swelling in her hand tha‍t had arisen 2 weeks back​ but disappeared in just ​3 days. What is the most ​likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presents to your clinic with photosensitivity, redness, and watering of the eyes for the last 2 days. She shows you this photo of a swelling in her hand that had arisen 2 weeks back but disappeared in just 3 days. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loi​asis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"River b​lindness","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tropical pulmonary eosinop​hilia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Occult f​ilariasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Loiasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/707e32d683ac489da907b602789e4665.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4c6e44155c864c4896612654670eb83dx764x512.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9b79f18080d642a4ba46b21c5c62e7dex720x622.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Loiasis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the image showing <strong>Calabar swelling</strong> is suggestive of <strong>loiasis </strong>caused by <em><strong>Loa loa.</strong></em></p>\n<p>Calabar swellings are <strong>temporary foci of inflammation</strong> that appear as <em>Loa loa </em>wanders through the subcutaneous tissues. They are up to 3 cm in size and are also called<strong> fugitive swellings</strong> because they <strong>disappear</strong> in a few days only to <strong>reappear elsewhere</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Loa loa </em>is transmitted by the <strong>bite of <em>Chrysops</em></strong> species (deerflies). The <strong>infective</strong> form is the <strong>third stage microfilaria</strong>. After entry into human skin, it grows into an adult worm.</p>\n<p>The <strong>ocular manifestations</strong> occur when the worm reaches the subconjunctival tissue during its wanderings. They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Granuloma in bulbar conjunctiva</li>\n<li>Painless edema of the eyelids</li>\n<li>Proptosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows Calabar swelling.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows a live, adult <em>Loa loa</em> in the anterior chamber of the eye.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9047","text":"Which of the following is ​also known as Weingarten ‍syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is also known as Weingarten syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tropical pulmonary eosinop​hilia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Loa​sis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Onchoce​rciasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tropical ​basophilia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia</strong></p><p><strong>Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia </strong>is also known as <strong>Weingarten syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Tropical eosinophilia occurs as a result of <strong>hypersensitivity reaction</strong> to <strong>microfilarial antigens</strong> and not directly due to lymphatic involvement. It is a manifestation of<strong> occult filariasis</strong> (Meyer's-Kouwenaar syndrome).</p>\n<p>Clinical features of occult filariasis include <strong>massive eosinophilia</strong> and <strong>hepatosplenomegaly.</strong></p>\n<p>Synonyms of tropical pulmonary eosinophilia include - </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Weingarten syndrome</li>\n<li>Pseudo-tuberculosis of the lung</li>\n<li>Eosinophilic pseudo-leukemia</li>\n<li>Tropical eosinophilic asthma</li>\n<li>Frimodt-Moller and Barton's syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9037","text":"Which of the following is ​known as serpent worm?","raw_text":"Which of the following is known as serpent worm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gnathostoma​ spinigerum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Angiostrongylus cantonensi​s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dracunculus​ medinensis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Capillaria p​hilippinensis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dracunculus medinensis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dracunculus medinensis</strong></p><p><em><strong>Dracunculus medinensis</strong></em> is known as serpent worm.</p>\n<p>It is a viviparous nematode.</p>\n<p>This has been eradicated from India and all of the southeast Asian region in the year 2000.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6942","text":"Match the following parasi​tes with their respective‍ hosts: A. Diphyllobothri​um 1. Sheep B. Paragonimu‌s 2. Crayfish C. Brugia 3‌. Cyclops D. Fasciola 4. ‍Mansonia","raw_text":"<p>Match the following parasites with their respective hosts:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 144px;\" width=\"525\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 255.521px;\">A. Diphyllobothrium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.521px;\">1. Sheep</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 255.521px;\">B. Paragonimus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.521px;\">2. Crayfish</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 255.521px;\">C. Brugia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.521px;\">3. Cyclops</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 255.521px;\">D. Fasciola</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.521px;\">4. Mansonia</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A-3, B-2,​ C-4, D-1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A-2, B-1 ​,C-4 ,D-3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A-1, B-2,​ C-3, D-4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A-4, B-3,​ C-1, D-2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A-3, B-2, C-4, D-1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A-3, B-2, C-4, D-1</strong></p><table style=\"height: 231px;\" width=\"558\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.472px;\"><strong>Parasite</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.632px;\"><strong>Definitive host</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.632px;\"><strong>First intermediate host</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.667px;\"><strong>Second intermediate host</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.472px;\">Diphyllobothrium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.632px;\">Humans</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.632px;\">Cyclops</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.667px;\">Cyclops and fish</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.472px;\">Paragonimus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.632px;\">Humans</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.632px;\">Snail</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.667px;\">Crayfish and crab</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.472px;\">Fasciola</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.632px;\">Sheep and humans</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.632px;\">Snail</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.667px;\">Aquatic vegetations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.472px;\">Brugia </td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.632px;\">Humans</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.632px;\">Mansonia mosquito</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.667px; text-align: center;\"> -</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"BMw6eTxg","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"34_endometriosis_and_adenomyosis","title":"34.Endometriosis And Adenomyosis","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MB1925","text":"Which of the following acc‌urately describes Sampson‌'s theory for endometrios​is?","raw_text":"Which of the following accurately describes Sampson's theory for endometriosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retrograde ‌menstruation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Coelomic ‌metaplasia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemogenous‌ metastasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Induction o‌f stem cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Retrograde menstruation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/977db713e92b4399a56592db0bf12f3bx1280x1131.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Retrograde menstruation</strong></p><p><strong>Sampson's theory</strong> describes the implantation of endometrial tissue due to <strong>retrograde menstruation.</strong></p>\n<p>The various theories for the <strong>etiology of endometriosis</strong> include:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 636px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">Theory</td>\n<td style=\"width: 369px;\">Mechanism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">Sampson's implantation theory</td>\n<td style=\"width: 369px;\">Reflux of menstrual endometrium and subsequent implantation in the surrounding structures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">Theory of coelomic metaplasia by Meyer and Ivanoff</td>\n<td style=\"width: 369px;\">\n<p>Metaplastic changes in embryonic mesothelium which respond to hormone stimulation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">Metastatic theory by Halban</td>\n<td style=\"width: 369px;\">Embolization of menstrual fragments through vascular and lymphatic channels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">Theory of histogenesis by induction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 369px;\">Induction of undifferentiated peritoneal cells to endometrial tissue by an endogenous factor</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Given below is an illustration showing retrograde menstrual flow that is leading to the development of patches of endometriosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1923","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about en‌dometriosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about endometriosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hormone dependent conditio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"More common in nulliparous‌ women","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"In utero exposure to DES i‌ncreases risk","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Most common site is the ce‌rvix","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Most common site is the cervix","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Most common site is the cervix</strong></p><p>The <strong>most</strong> <strong>common site</strong> of occurrence of endometriosis is the <strong>ovary. </strong>Endometriosis of the ovary is known as <strong>endometrioma</strong>.</p>\n<p>By definition, <strong>endometriosis</strong> is the occurrence of <strong>benign</strong> <strong>endometrial tissue</strong> (glands and stroma) outside the cavity of the uterus. Endometriosis can occur <strong>anywhere</strong> in the body (<strong>except spleen</strong>) including the abdominal wall, lung, pleura, brain, etc.</p>\n<p>It is a proliferative, <strong>hormone-dependent</strong> disease of the <strong>childbearing period.</strong> Women between the age groups of 30-40 years are most commonly affected. Risk factors for endometriosis are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Family history (7-10x risk with a 1st-degree relative)</li>\n<li><strong>Nulliparity</strong></li>\n<li>In utero exposure to Diethylstilbestrol (<strong>DES</strong>)</li>\n<li>Estrogen dependent conditions/Estrogen therapy</li>\n<li>Immunological factor - Impaired T cell and NK cell activity and altered immunology in a woman may increase the susceptibility to proliferation and growth.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1959","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common symptom a‌ssociated with endometrio​sis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common symptom associated with endometriosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dysmen‌orrhea","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dyspa‌reunia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Menor‌rhagia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infer‌tility","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dysmenorrhea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dysmenorrhea</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> symptom of endometriosis is <strong>dysmenorrhea.</strong></p>\n<p>Pain begins before the onset of menstruation and builds up continu­ously until the flow starts. Thereafter, the pain gradually de­clines. This pattern of pain is called <strong>triple dysmenorrhea</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1958","text":"In a patient diagnosed wit‌h endometriosis, which of‌ the following symptoms w​ould you not expect?","raw_text":"In a patient diagnosed with endometriosis, which of the following symptoms would you not expect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vaginal ‌discharge","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Premenstru‌al spotting","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Postmenstru‌al spotting","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Menor‌rhagia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vaginal discharge","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vaginal discharge</strong></p><p>Patients diagnosed with endometriosis are not likely to present with vaginal discharge<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features of endometriosis</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>About <strong>30%</strong> of the patients are <strong>asymptomatic.</strong></li>\n<li>The classic symptom complex includes:\n<ul>\n<li>Dysmenorrhoea</li>\n<li>Dyspareunia</li>\n<li>Menorrhagia</li>\n<li>Infertility (30-40%)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Dysmenorrhoea</strong> - <strong>most common</strong> symptom</li>\n<li>Dyspareunia - occurs when the pouch of Douglas and rectovaginal septum are involved.</li>\n<li>Deep-seated pelvic pain</li>\n<li>Premenstrual and postmenstrual spotting</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1960","text":"A 26-year-old lady present‌ed with complaints of sev‌ere pain on menstruation ​and heavy flow. She is su‍bsequently diagnosed with​ endometriosis. The pain ​in this condition mainly ‌correlates with_____","raw_text":"A 26-year-old lady presented with complaints of severe pain on menstruation and heavy flow. She is subsequently diagnosed with endometriosis. The pain in this condition mainly correlates with_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Depth of p‌enetration","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Multipl‌e sites","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CA 125‌ levels","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stage of t‌he disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Depth of penetration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Depth of penetration</strong></p><p><strong>Pain</strong> in <strong>endometriosis</strong> mainly correlates with the <strong>depth of penetration.</strong></p>\n<p>Lesions penetrating <strong>more than</strong> <strong>5mm</strong> are responsible for pain, dysmenorrhea and dyspareunia. The dysmenorrhea associated with endometriosis precedes menses by 24 to 48 hours and is <strong>less responsive</strong> to <strong>NSAIDs</strong> and combined <strong>OCPs.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1951","text":"Arrange the following site‌s of endometriosis in the‌ order of frequency of oc​currence. Pouch of Dougla‍s Uterosacral ligament Br​oad ligament Ovary Fallop​ian tube","raw_text":"<p>Arrange the following sites of endometriosis in the order of frequency of occurrence.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Pouch of Douglas</li>\n<li>Uterosacral ligament</li>\n<li>Broad ligament</li>\n<li>Ovary</li>\n<li>Fallopian tube</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 &amp;gt; 5 &amp;gt; 2 &amp;gt; 1 &amp;gt‌; 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 &amp;gt; 1 &amp;gt; 3 &amp;gt; 2 &amp;gt‌; 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"4 &amp;gt; 5 &amp;gt; 1 &amp;gt; 3 &amp;gt‌; 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 &amp;gt; 4 &amp;gt; 2 &amp;gt; 3 &amp;gt‌; 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4 > 1 > 3 > 2 > 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4 &gt; 1 &gt; 3 &gt; 2 &gt; 5</strong></p><p>The <strong>sites of endometriosis</strong> arranged in the order of frequency of occurrence are: <strong>Ovary </strong>&gt; Pouch of Douglas &gt; Broad ligament &gt; Uterosacral ligament &gt; <strong>Fallopian tube.</strong></p>\n<p>Endometriotic lesions have been identified on all organs except the spleen.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1961","text":"A middle-aged woman presen‌ted with dysmenorrhea, dy‌spareunia, and infertilit​y. On examination, you no‍te tender nodularity of t​he uterosacral ligaments.​ Which of the following i‌s the most likely diagnos​is?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presented with dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, and infertility. On examination, you note tender nodularity of the uterosacral ligaments. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endome‌trioma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endome‌triosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pelvic tu‌berculosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adeno‌myosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endometriosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/027bc31f8cd04cfaa01fa62f082ca6fbx1280x1106.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endometriosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the <strong>tender</strong> <strong>nodularity</strong> of the <strong>uterosacral ligament</strong> points towards a diagnosis of <strong>endometriosis.</strong></p>\n<p>The nodularity of the uterosacral ligament in endometriosis gives rise to the <strong>cobblestone appearance</strong> seen on laparoscopy. This is referred to as 'sine qua non of endometriosis'.</p>\n<p>The below image shows the cobblestone appearance of the uterosacral ligament.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Endometrioma or chocolate cyst is endometriosis of the ovary. It presents as an adnexal mass.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1963","text":"A young woman presented to‌ your clinic with complai‌nts of painful menstruati​on and postmenstrual spot‍ting. On examination, you​ note tender nodules in t​he posterior fornix and a‌ fixed retroverted uterus​. Which of the following ‍is the gold standard inve​stigation to confirm the ​likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young woman presented to your clinic with complaints of painful menstruation and postmenstrual spotting. On examination, you note tender nodules in the posterior fornix and a fixed retroverted uterus. Which of the following is the gold standard investigation to confirm the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ultra‌sound","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lapar‌otomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lapar‌oscopy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hyster‌oscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Laparoscopy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Laparoscopy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of <strong>dysmenorrhea</strong> with <strong>tender nodules</strong> in the posterior fornix and a <strong>retroverted uterus</strong> is suggestive of <strong>endometriosis</strong>. The <strong>gold standard</strong> investigation for the diagnosis of this condition is a visual inspection by <strong>laparoscopy</strong>, preferably with histological confirmation. It is also the <strong>investigation of choice</strong> for the condition.</p>\n<p>Role of laparoscopy in endometriosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Detect and diagnose pelvic endometriosis</li>\n<li>Locate the site of endometriosis and staging</li>\n<li>To take biopsy</li>\n<li>To surgically treat endometriosis by ablation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other investigations for endometriosis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Estimation of CA-125 levels - raised in endometriosis.</li>\n<li>Estimation of MCP-1 (Monocyte chemotactic protein) levels - raised in the peritoneal fluid of women with endometriosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1956","text":"A diagnostic laparoscopy w‌as performed on a 27-year‌-old woman with severe dy​smenorrhoea and dyspareun‍ia. The following finding​ was seen on the pelvic p​eritoneum. What is the mo‌st likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A diagnostic laparoscopy was performed on a 27-year-old woman with severe dysmenorrhoea and dyspareunia.  The following finding was seen on the pelvic peritoneum. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pelvic Inflammatory Diseas‌e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adeno‌myosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fitz Hugh Cu‌rtis syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endome‌triosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Endometriosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8a40cc26afb4430f9393f16b3b583210.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2c1b19d3960c44bebf6cf9888b709d1dx1280x1071.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Endometriosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and the image showing characteristic <strong>powder burn spots</strong> seen over the pelvic peritoneum on laparoscopy point toward a diagnosis of <strong>pelvic</strong> <strong>endometriosis.</strong></p>\n<p>On laparoscopy, the typical endometriotic lesions appear like small black, dark brown or bluish dots. These are called powder burns or <strong>gunshot appearance</strong> of the <strong>serosal surfaces</strong> of the <strong>peritoneum.</strong> They are nodules or small cysts containing <strong>old haemorrhages</strong> <strong>surrounded by</strong> a variable degree of <strong>fibrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Subtle appearances of endometriosis</strong> seen on laparoscopy are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Red flame-shaped areas</li>\n<li>Red polypoid areas</li>\n<li>Yellow-brown patches</li>\n<li>White peritoneal plaques or scarring</li>\n<li>Peritoneal defects - known as Allen Masters Syndrome</li>\n<li>Subovarian adhesions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lesions mentioned above are thought to be more active than the powder burn areas.</p>\n<p>Endometriotic lesions in the <strong>peritoneal cavity</strong> contain yellowish-brown fluid. This <strong>fluid contains prostaglandins</strong> that are <strong>responsible for</strong> the <strong>pain</strong> associated with this condition. Endometriosis of the <strong>ovary</strong> leads to formation of the <strong>chocolate cyst</strong> which is a true cyst with columnar epithelium lining.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the laparoscopic appearance of endometriotic lesions in the pouch of Douglas and on the right, the sacrouterine ligament. Dyspareunia occurs when the pouch of Douglas and rectovaginal septum are involved.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1201","text":"In endometriotic lesion, h‌istology represents its:","raw_text":"In endometriotic lesion, histology represents its:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High cho‌lesterol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High e‌strogen","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"High levels ‌of prolactin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High pro‌gesterone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. High estrogen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9b8855c72c1f4b3496542f08bb8c81aax1280x988.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. High estrogen</strong></p><p>In endometriotic lesions, histology represents its <strong>high estrogen activity</strong>.</p>\n<p>Endometriosis is defined by the presence of “ectopic” endometrial tissue at a site outside of the uterus.  Endometriosis is a proliferative hormone-dependent disease of the childbearing period. It is extremely rare before menarche and disappears after menopause.</p>\n<p>The endometriotic implants show certain differences when compared to normal endometrium. There is increased production of estrogen by endometriotic stromal cells due to <strong>high levels</strong> of the enzyme <strong>aromatase</strong>. This is absent in normal endometrial stroma.</p>\n<p>Management of endometriosis: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0256","text":"Which of the following ope‌rative procedures are lea‌st likely to be followed ​by scar endometriosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following operative procedures are least likely to be followed by scar endometriosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyste‌rotomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cesarean‌ section","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventrof‌ixation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyster‌ectomy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hysterectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hysterectomy</strong></p><p>Among the given operative procedures, <strong>hysterectomy</strong> is <strong>least likely</strong> to be followed by <strong>scar endometriosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Operations most likely to be followed by scar endometriosis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hysterotomy</li>\n<li>Classical Cesarean section</li>\n<li>Myomectomy</li>\n<li>Ventrofixation (fixing uterus to the abdominal wall)</li>\n<li>Episiotomy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1957","text":"A 39-year-old nulliparous ‌woman presented with dysm‌enorrhea and a progressiv​ely growing, painless abd‍ominal mass. USG scan rev​ealed a cystic lesion of ​the left ovary which was ‌excised. The below image ​shows the excised specime‍n. Which of the following​ statements is false abou​t this condition?","raw_text":"A 39-year-old nulliparous woman presented with dysmenorrhea and a progressively growing, painless abdominal mass. USG scan revealed a cystic lesion of the left ovary which was excised. The below image shows the excised specimen. Which of the following statements is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is seen in endometriosi‌s of ovary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is lined by columnar ep‌ithelium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is usually a bilateral ‌condition","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a malignant cystic l‌esion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is a malignant cystic lesion","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a59435cb21854285bfdf644a66e3c002.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a03a32f1f85c484dab0c98099b3d7b33x1280x1133.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/be8eb29dc83343b5ae3be780d92c253ex1108x1465.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is a malignant cystic lesion</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and the image is suggestive of a <strong>chocolate cyst</strong> of the ovary which is a <strong>benign</strong> cystic lesion.</p>\n<p><strong>Chocolate cysts</strong> of the ovary are a manifestation of <strong>endometriosis. </strong>Both the ovaries are usually affected. On histology, the cyst is lined by <strong>columnar epithelium.</strong> <strong>Pseudoxanthoma cells</strong> are present which are <strong>large</strong> <strong>macrophages</strong> or scavenger cells that appear brown in colour due to ingested blood pigments such as <strong>­haemosiderin</strong>. They are polyhe­dral in shape and resemble lutein cells. </p>\n<p>Given below is a gross image of the chocolate cyst of the ovary.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Given below is a histopathological section of an endometriotic lesion showing the pseudoxanthoma cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1964","text":"A patient is being evaluat‌ed for chronic abdominal ‌pain. Her lab reports rev​ealed elevated CA-125 lev‍els. Which of the followi​ng conditions is the leas​t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient is being evaluated for chronic abdominal pain. Her lab reports revealed elevated CA-125 levels. Which of the following conditions is the least likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endome‌trioma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic li‌ver disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abdominal t‌uberculosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malignant pancreatic tumor‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Malignant pancreatic tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Malignant pancreatic tumor</strong></p><p>CA-125 levels are not expected to be elevated in malignant pancreatic tumours.</p>\n<p><strong>CA-125</strong> is a glycoprotein and cell surface antigen. Its levels are<strong> raised</strong> in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Abdominal or pelvic tuberculosis</li>\n<li>Pelvic inflammatory disease</li>\n<li>Endometriosis</li>\n<li>Malignant epithelial ovarian tumour­</li>\n<li>Chronic liver disease</li>\n<li>2% of normal women, especially during menstruation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Endometrioma refers to ovarian endometriosis. CA-125 levels are raised to more than 35 U/mL in 80% of cases of endometriosis. The level is directly proportional to the extent of the disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1966","text":"A 30-year-old woman with d‌yspareunia is diagnosed t‌o have endometriosis. Whi​ch of the following drugs‍ is not useful for her tr​eatment?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman with dyspareunia is diagnosed to have endometriosis. Which of the following drugs is not useful for her treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estr‌ogen","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Proges‌terone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dan‌azol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gn‌RH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Estrogen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9b8855c72c1f4b3496542f08bb8c81aax1280x988.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Estrogen</strong></p><p>Estrogen is not used in the treatment of <strong>endometriosis</strong> as the condition is <strong>estrogen-dependent</strong>.</p>\n<p>For <strong>minimal disease,</strong> the drug of choice is <strong>NSAIDs. Oral contraceptive pills</strong> are given if associated menstrual complaints are present. OCPs inhibit gonadotropin release and decrease the menstrual flow.</p>\n<p>For <strong>moderate</strong> to <strong>severe</strong> disease, oral <strong>progestogens</strong> are the first drugs to be started. They are known to antagonize estrogenic effects on the endometrium causing endometrial atrophy. The drug of choice is <strong>GnRH agonists.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: The best time to conceive in a patient with endometriosis is immediately after surgery.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1972","text":"A patient with endometrios‌is requires treatment wit‌h GnRH analogues. She is ​scared of injections so y‍ou plan to prescribe a na​sal preparation for her. ​Which of the following Gn‌RH agonists could you pre​scribe?","raw_text":"A patient with endometriosis requires treatment with GnRH analogues. She is scared of injections so you plan to prescribe a nasal preparation for her.  Which of the following GnRH agonists could you prescribe?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nafa‌relin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gose‌relin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leupr‌olide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tript‌orelin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nafarelin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nafarelin</strong></p><p>The <strong>GnRH agonist </strong>available as a<strong> </strong><strong>nasal preparation</strong> for the treatment of endometriosis is <strong>nafarelin</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>GnRH</strong> analogues are used in the management of endometriosis and are given by <strong>continuous administration. </strong>This <strong>suppresses</strong> the secretion of <strong>pituitary</strong> gonadotropins and leads to <strong>atrophy</strong> of the <strong>endometrial</strong> tissue in 90% of cases.</p>\n<p>Leuprolide and triptorelin are GnRH agonists which are given IM. Goserelin is available as a depot preparation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1974","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‌ adenomyosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding adenomyosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More common in nulliparous‌ women","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Uterus is ha‌rd and tender","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Menorrhagia, dysmenorrhea ‌are common symptoms","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Estrogens are used for tre‌atment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Menorrhagia, dysmenorrhea are common symptoms","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/31e7ed9dc10e4fa5ab52e747ccae43b0x1280x1298.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/249c5d931db6429483fab9b421a999dex1280x1068.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Menorrhagia, dysmenorrhea are common symptoms</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>adenomyosis</strong> commonly present with symptoms of <strong>menorrhagia</strong> and <strong>dysmenorrhea.</strong></p>\n<p>Adenomyosis (endometriosis interna) is the <strong>ingrowth</strong> of stromal and glandular components of the <strong>endometrium</strong> directly <strong>into</strong> the <strong>myometrium</strong>. It usually occurs following vigorous<strong> curettage, repeated childbirth</strong> or in conditions with an <strong>excess</strong> of <strong>estrogen.</strong> Adenomyosis is more common in <strong>multiparous women</strong> aged<strong> </strong>40 years and above. They commonly present with <strong>menorrhagia</strong> and <strong>dysmenorrhea.</strong> Other<strong> </strong>symptoms include pelvic discomfort, backache and dyspareunia.</p>\n<p>On per-vaginal examination, there is a gross symmetrical enlargement of the <strong>uterus</strong> corresponding to <strong>12-14 weeks size</strong>. The uterus is <strong>soft</strong> and <strong>tender</strong> on bimanual examination. This is known as <strong>Halban's sign. </strong></p>\n<p>Diagnosis is mainly based on clinical assessment. A diagnostic hysteroscopy combined with curettage is the initial step in the <strong>management</strong> because of menorrhagia. Total <strong>hysterectomy </strong>is the treatment of choice. <strong>Localised excision</strong> and <strong>levonorgestrel containing IUCD</strong> can be tried in younger patients.</p>\n<p>The image below is an illustration of adenomyosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows adenomyosis as seen during laparoscopy. The uterus is soft and enlarged; the blue spots represent subserous endometriosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1977","text":"A patient with menorrhagia‌ and backache is diagnose‌d with adenomyosis. Which​ of the following is not ‍associated with this cond​ition?","raw_text":"A patient with menorrhagia and backache is diagnosed with adenomyosis. Which of the following is not associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibroid‌ uterus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endome‌triosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sub involuti‌on of uterus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endometria‌l carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sub involution of uterus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sub involution of uterus</strong></p><p>Adenomyosis is not associated with sub involution of the uterus.</p>\n<p><strong>Uterine subinvolution</strong> is impaired or retarded involution of the uterus. It is associated with uterine <strong>fibroid</strong>, <strong>cesarean section</strong> and conditions causing <strong>overdistension</strong> of the uterus like twin pregnancies or polyhydramnios.</p>\n<p><strong>Adenomyosis</strong> often <strong>coexists with the following</strong> conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Uterine fibromyomas</li>\n<li>Pelvic endometriosis (15%)</li>\n<li>Endometrial carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1980","text":"A 43-year-old multiparous ‌woman presented with meno‌rrhagia and secondary dys​menorrhea. On examination‍, Halban's sign was posit​ive. The transvaginal USG​ findings are given below‌. Which of the following ​is the definitive treatme‍nt for her condition?","raw_text":"A 43-year-old multiparous woman presented with menorrhagia and secondary dysmenorrhea. On examination, Halban's sign was positive. The transvaginal USG findings are given below. Which of the following is the definitive treatment for her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hormonal‌ therapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyster‌ectomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Laser a‌blation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Localised‌ excision","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hysterectomy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/019609bb587b4f62ac95b93d9be5d4f1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aa955a2d570d4aac938126e7364dc481x1280x1362.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/91a5125d06084b898a602eaa7a99584ax922x1024.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hysterectomy</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette and the transvaginal ultrasound(TVS) showing the Venetian blind appearance is suggestive of <strong>adenomyosis.</strong> The <strong>definitive treatment</strong> for adenomyosis is <strong>hysterectomy.</strong></p>\n<p>Adenomyosis (endometriosis interna) is the ingrowth of stromal and glandular components of the endometrium directly into the myometrium. <strong>Diagnosis</strong> is mainly based on <strong>clinical </strong>assessment. Painful, symmetrical enlargement of the uterus is suggestive of adenomyosis. On bimanual examination, the uterus is softened and tender. This is known as <strong>Halban's sign. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>TVS </strong>findings include<strong> salt and pepper appearance </strong>of the myometrium and the presence of <strong>subendometrial striations</strong> producing the Venetian blind appearance. MRI can also be done for diagnosis. However, the only<strong> definitive </strong>mode of<strong> diagnosis </strong>is<strong> histopathological examination.</strong></p>\n<p>Since most women affected by the condition are past the childbearing age, <strong>total hysterectomy</strong> is the <strong>treatment of choice. </strong>Localized excision/Levonorgestrel containing IUCD can be tried in younger women.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below is of a sagittal MRI showing a uterus with adenomyosis in the posterior wall. Gross enlargement of the posterior wall is noted, with many foci of hyperintensity.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"kzuDZK0J","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"34_paediatric_hemato_oncology","title":"34.Paediatric Hemato Oncology","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA7752","text":"Cancer-'The emperor of all‌ maladies spares no one'.​ Which of the following i‍s the most common childho‌od malignancy?","raw_text":"Cancer-'The emperor of all maladies spares no one'. Which of the following is the most common childhood malignancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Astro‌cytoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute lymphoblastic leukem‌ia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acute myelo‌id leukemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acute lymphoblastic leukemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acute lymphoblastic leukemia</strong></p><p><strong>Leukemias</strong> are the most common childhood malignancies. Among these,<strong> acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL)</strong> is <strong>the most common</strong>, accounting for about 77% of childhood leukemias. </p>\n<p>Most commons in childhood malignancies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Second most common malignancy in childhood: Brain tumor</li>\n<li>Third most common malignancy in children: Lymphoma</li>\n<li><strong>Most common</strong> <strong>solid tumor</strong> in children: <strong>Brain tumor</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7753","text":"You are asked to review an‌ article titled, 'Childho​od cancers- a menace'. Wh‍ich of the following stat‌ements is wrong and needs‌ to be edited out?","raw_text":"You are asked to review an article titled, 'Childhood cancers- a menace'. Which of the following statements is wrong and needs to be edited out?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute lymphoblastic leukem‌ia is the most common typ​e of leukemia in children‍ with Down syndrome.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The most common intra-abdo‌minal malignancy is Wilm’​s tumor.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The most common extracrani‌al solid malignancy is ne​uroblastoma.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The most common brain tumo‌rs in children are infrat​entorial.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The most common intra-abdominal malignancy is Wilm’s tumor.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The most common intra-abdominal malignancy is Wilm’s tumor.</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common intraabdominal malignancy</strong> in children is <strong>neuroblastoma</strong>. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The most common leukemias seen in <strong>Down’s syndrome</strong>: acute lymphoblastic leukemia <strong>(ALL) &gt; </strong>acute myeloid leukemia <strong>(AML)</strong></li>\n<li>The most common site of a brain tumor in children:<strong> Infratentorium</strong></li>\n<li>The most common <strong>extra-cranial</strong> solid neoplasm in children: <strong>Neuroblastoma </strong></li>\n<li>The most common <strong>intra-abdominal</strong> neoplasm in children: <strong>Neuroblastoma </strong></li>\n<li>The most common <strong>primary neoplasm of renal tissue</strong> in children:<strong> Wilm’s tumor</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7761","text":"What is the most common le‌ukemia you would encounte​r in a child with the fol‍lowing findings?","raw_text":"What is the most common leukemia you would encounter in a child with the following findings?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute myelo‌id leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute lymphoblastic leukem‌ia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic myel‌oid leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic lymp‌hoid leukemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acute lymphoblastic leukemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/845860367ea948d0affb5f5e2cd48976.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acute lymphoblastic leukemia</strong></p><p>The image shows a typical Down's facies (flattened nose, flat occiput, low set ears, protruding tongue). The most common leukemia in children with <strong>Down syndrome</strong> is <strong>acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL).</strong></p>\n<p>Children with Down syndrome are at<strong> increased risk</strong> of both acute lymphoid leukemia (ALL) and acute myeloid leukemia (AML).</p>\n<p>10% of neonates with Down's syndrome develop<strong> transient leukemia or myeloproliferative disorder</strong>. These features usually resolve within the <strong>first 3 months of life</strong>. However, they require close follow-up, because 20–30% will develop <strong>typical leukemia (often acute megakaryocytic leukemia) by 3 years of life.</strong></p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Most common leukemia in Down's syndrome: <strong>B-cell Acute lymphoblastic leukemia</strong> (overall)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The most common leukemia in children<strong> &lt;3years</strong> of age with<strong> Down syndrome</strong> is<strong> acute myeloid leukemia (AML).</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Most common AML in Down's syndrome:<strong> M7/megakaryoblastic</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7763","text":"Jeevit, a 7-year-old boy b‌rought to you by his moth​er, complains of bone pai‍n so severe that he canno‌t sleep. Investigations r‌eveal: Leukocyte count: 1‍6,000 cells/mm 3 Hemoglob‍in: 6 g/dl Platelet count​: 60,000 cells/mm 3 Perip​heral smear shows numerou​s immature blast cells an‍d is suggestive of acute ​lymphoblastic leukemia In​ this age group, which im‍munophenotype of ALL is m​ost commonly encountered?‌","raw_text":"<p>Jeevit, a 7-year-old boy brought to you by his mother, complains of bone pain so severe that he cannot sleep. Investigations reveal:</p>\n<p>Leukocyte count: 16,000 cells/mm<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p>Hemoglobin: 6 g/dl</p>\n<p>Platelet count: 60,000 cells/mm<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p>Peripheral smear shows numerous immature blast cells and is suggestive of acute lymphoblastic leukemia</p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>In this age group, which immunophenotype of ALL is most commonly encountered?</strong></span></p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"TdT+,HLA DR+,CD20+, Cy Ig ‌+","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"CD19+, CD20+, CD24+, Surfa‌ce Ig+","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD3+, CD4+,‌ CD5+, CD34+","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD2+, CD‌7+, TdT+","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. TdT+,HLA DR+,CD20+, Cy Ig +","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. TdT+,HLA DR+,CD20+, Cy Ig +</strong></p><p><strong>B-lymphoblastic leukemia(pre-B-ALL)</strong> is the most common immunophenotype with <strong>TdT+,HLA DR+, CD10+, CD19+, CD20+, CD24+, Cy Ig +</strong> , with onset at <strong>1-10 years</strong> of age.</p>\n<p>The most common type of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) in children is B-lymphoblastic leukemia (previously termed pre-B-ALL)</p>\n<p>Based on current WHO classification: The most common subtype of ALL is <strong>B lymphoblastic leukemia &gt; T lymphoblastic leukemia &gt; Mature B-cell Leukemia (Burkitt leukemia)</strong></p>\n<p>The production of symptoms and signs of ALL is a result of the <strong>replacement of bone marrow by malignant cells</strong> and caused by secondary bone marrow failure. On physical examination, findings of pallor, listlessness, purpuric/petechial skin lesions, or mucous membrane hemorrhage can reflect<strong> bone marrow failure.</strong> The <strong>proliferative nature</strong> of the disease may be manifested as lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, or, less often, hepatomegaly. At presentation<strong> 75% of patients have leukocyte counts of &lt;20,000/µL</strong>; <strong>thrombocytopenia</strong> is seen in 75% of patients and <strong>hepatosplenomegaly</strong> in 30–40% of patients. Patients with joint pain may have evidence of<strong> joint swelling and effusion</strong>. <strong>Deep bone pain </strong>may be present with marrow involvement, but <strong>tenderness will not be elicited.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9557","text":"You are posted in the pedi‌atric hematology departme​nt as a first-year postgr‍aduate. All of the follow‌ing cases you encounter a‌re at an increased risk o‍f developing leukemias ex‍cept: Case 1: A 2-year-ol​d child with neurofibroma​tosis type 1 Case 2: A 4-​year-old with Fanconi syn‍drome Case 3: A 1-year-ol​d with ataxia-telangiecta​sia Case 4: A 3-year-old ‍with severe combined immu​nodeficiency Case 5: A 10‌-month old with perniciou‍s anemia","raw_text":"<p>You are posted in the pediatric hematology department as a first-year postgraduate. All of the following cases you encounter are at an increased risk of developing leukemias except:</p>\n<p>Case 1: A 2-year-old child with neurofibromatosis type 1</p>\n<p>Case 2: A 4-year-old with Fanconi syndrome</p>\n<p>Case 3: A 1-year-old with ataxia-telangiectasia</p>\n<p>Case 4: A 3-year-old with severe combined immunodeficiency</p>\n<p>Case 5: A 10-month old with pernicious anemia</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌,4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2‌,5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1,‌3,4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 o‌nly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2,5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2,5</strong></p><p><strong>Fanconi’s anemia</strong> (not syndrome) predisposes to the development of childhood leukemias eg: B-ALL due to <strong>defective DNA repair. </strong>Fanconi syndrome is a syndrome of inadequate reabsorption in the proximal renal tubules<strong>. Congenital pernicious anemia</strong> <strong>does not</strong> predispose to leukemias.</p>\n<p>Factors predisposing to childhood leukemias</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Genetic conditions:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fanconi’s anemia</strong></li>\n<li>Bloom syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>Ataxia telangiectasia</strong></li>\n<li>Li-Fraumeni syndrome</li>\n<li>Down syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>Severe combined immunodeficiency</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Neurofibromatosis 1   </strong></li>\n<li>Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria</li>\n<li>Diamond-Blackfan anemia</li>\n<li>Schwachman diamond syndrome</li>\n<li>Kostmann syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Environmental factors:\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ionizing radiation</li>\n<li>Alkylating agents</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nitrosoureas</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Epipodophyllotoxin</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Benzene exposure</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Advanced maternal age.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7765","text":"To diagnose a 5-year-old c‌hild with acute lymphobla​stic leukemia, a patholog‍ist has to see at least _‌___ percentage of blast c‌ells in a bone marrow bio‍psy.","raw_text":"To diagnose a 5-year-old child with acute lymphoblastic leukemia, a pathologist has to see at least ____ percentage of blast cells in a bone marrow biopsy.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌0","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2‌5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3‌0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 25","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/301bd304236f4091b83d20e9cf634f40x357x512.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 25</strong></p><p>The<strong> lower cut-off</strong> percentage for diagnosing <strong>acute lymphoblastic leukemia</strong> on bone marrow biopsy is <strong>25%. </strong></p>\n<p>Unlike with myeloid leukemias, there is no agreed-upon lower limit for the proportion of blasts required to establish a diagnosis of lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL). In general, the diagnosis should be avoided when there are &lt; 20% blasts. </p>\n<p>The image below shows numerous lymphoblasts seen on a peripheral smear.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Work-up of ALL:</p>\n<p>It is important that all studies necessary to confirm a diagnosis and adequately classify the type of leukemia be performed, including<strong> bone marrow aspiration</strong> and biopsy, <strong>flow cytometry, cytogenetics, and molecular studies</strong>. The initial evaluation should also include a<strong> CSF examination</strong>. If lymphoblasts are found and the CSF leukocyte count is elevated,<strong> overt CNS or meningeal leukemia</strong> is present. This finding reflects <strong>a poorer stage</strong> and indicates the need for additional CNS and systemic therapies. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9246","text":"A 4-year-old child who was‌ being evaluated for panc​ytopenia is diagnosed wit‍h acute lymphoblastic leu‌kemia. Which of the follo‌wing findings would indic‍ate a poor prognosis?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old child who was being evaluated for pancytopenia is diagnosed with acute lymphoblastic leukemia. Which of the following findings would indicate a poor prognosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Minimal residual disease&amp;g‌t;0.01% at the end of ind​uction therapy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"t(12,21) on cytogenetic st‌udy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Early B- cell immunophenot‌ype.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Initial WBC count of 20,00‌0 cells/mm3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Minimal residual disease>0.01% at the end of induction therapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Minimal residual disease&gt;0.01% at the end of induction therapy</strong></p><p>MRD(Minimal residual disease) is a quantitative estimate of the burden of leukemic cells present in marrow. <strong>MRD&gt;0.01%</strong>at the end of induction <strong>suggests a poorer prognosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Initial WBC count of &lt;50,000cells/mm3, B-cell immunophenotype and t(12,21) indicate a standard risk(Favourable prognosis)</p>\n<p><strong>Risk-directed therapy is the standard of current ALL treatment</strong> and accounts for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Age at diagnosis</li>\n<li>Initial WBC count</li>\n<li>Immunophenotypic and cytogenetic characteristics of blast populations</li>\n<li>The rapidity of early treatment response (i.e., how quickly the leukemic cells can be cleared from the marrow or peripheral blood)</li>\n<li>Assessment of MRD at the end of induction therapy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7767","text":"A 14-year-old boy is rushe‌d to the casualty due to ​difficulty in breathing a‍nd swelling of the face a‌nd neck. Clinically, he h‌as features suggestive of‍ superior vena cava obstr‍uction. Chest X-ray image​ shows superior mediastin​al widening. Which of the​ following is the most li‍kely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 14-year-old boy is rushed to the casualty due to difficulty in breathing and swelling of the face and neck. Clinically, he has features suggestive of superior vena cava obstruction. Chest X-ray image shows superior mediastinal widening. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B-cell acute lymphoblastic‌ leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T-cell acute lymphoblastic‌ leukemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic myel‌oid leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic lymphocytic leukem‌ia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia</strong></p><p><strong>T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL)</strong> is the most likely diagnosis since the chest X-ray shows <strong>superior mediastinal widening</strong>- indicating an involvement of the<strong> thymus gland.</strong></p>\n<p>T-ALL accounts for about 15% of childhood ALL cases; it is <strong>more common in adolescents</strong> - this is the time when the thymus reaches maximum size.</p>\n<p>60% of patients with T-ALL present with a mediastinal mass. The <strong>mediastinal mass</strong> may be <strong>asymptomatic</strong> and detected only on chest x-ray or may cause cough and dyspnea, particularly on lying flat or with facial swelling and plethora due to vascular compression (<strong>superior vena cava syndrome</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>B-ALL</strong> presents usually between <strong>1-10 years</strong> of age perhaps because the number of normal bone marrow pre-B-cells (the cell of origin) is greatest very early in life.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9558","text":"A 2-month-old infant with ‌seizures and failure to t​hrive has cutaneous nodul‍es and hepatosplenomegaly‌. On investigations, CBC ‌reveals hyperleukocytosis‍ with a leukocyte count o‍f 1,10,000cells/mm3. Peri​pheral smear and immunoph​enotyping studies reveal ​large, irregular lymphobl‍asts, with a phenotype ne​gative for the CD10 (comm​on ALL antigen) marker. O‍n a cytogenetic study whi​ch of the following is mo‌st likely to be seen?","raw_text":"A 2-month-old infant with seizures and failure to thrive has cutaneous nodules and hepatosplenomegaly. On investigations, CBC reveals hyperleukocytosis with a leukocyte count of 1,10,000cells/mm3. Peripheral smear and immunophenotyping studies reveal large, irregular lymphoblasts, with a phenotype negative for the CD10 (common ALL antigen) marker. On a cytogenetic study which of the following is most likely to be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"t(11‌, 14)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"t (4‌,11)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"t (1‌2,21)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"t (1‌5,21)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. t (4,11)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. t (4,11)</strong></p><p>The above findings are diagnostic of <strong>Infantile acute lymphoblastic leukemia</strong>. Translocation <strong>(4;11) involving the MLL gene</strong> is most commonly associated with Infantile leukemia accounts for the <strong>very high relapse rate.</strong></p>\n<p>2% of cases of leukemia during childhood occur <strong>before the age of 1 year</strong> and are referred to as<strong> infantile leukemia.</strong></p>\n<p>Patients often present with <strong>hyperleukocytosis</strong> and extensive tissue infiltration producing <strong>organomegaly</strong>. <strong>Subcutaneous nodules, known as leukemia cutis</strong>, caused by infiltration by leukemic cells are observed more often in infants than in older children. </p>\n<p>The leukemic cell morphology is usually that of large, irregular lymphoblasts, with a<strong> phenotype negative for the CD10 (common ALL antigen) marker</strong> (pro-B), unlike most older children with B-ALL, who are CD10 +. Has a very poor prognosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Infants with AML</strong> present with CNS and skin involvement and most commonly have a subtype known as <strong>acute myelomonocytic leukemia.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9561","text":"A mother has noticed recen‌tly that her child refuse​s to be touched, seems to‍ be in distress, and crie‌s inconsolably. She bring‌s her child to you who wa‍s investigated thoroughly‍ and was diagnosed to hav​e acute lymphoblastic leu​kemia. Which of the follo​wing drugs can be used to‍ treat her?","raw_text":"A mother has noticed recently that her child refuses to be touched, seems to be in distress, and cries inconsolably. She brings her child to you who was investigated thoroughly and was diagnosed to have acute lymphoblastic leukemia. Which of the following drugs can be used to treat her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Imat‌inib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"L-aspar‌aginase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tret‌inoin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cladr‌ibine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. L-asparaginase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. L-asparaginase</strong></p><p><strong>L-asparaginase</strong> is used in the treatment of acute lymphoid leukaemia (<strong>ALL</strong>)<strong>. </strong>ALL can present with hepatosplenomegaly, severe joint and bone pains that the kid refuses to be handled.<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p>It is observed that some lymphoid malignancies are <strong>unable to synthesize asparagine</strong> on their own and must derive it from the bloodstream. L-asparaginase catalyses circulating L-asparagine, thereby <strong>inhibiting protein synthesis</strong> in the cancer cells.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment of ALL</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Induction: </strong>4 weeks\n<ul>\n<li>L-asparaginase: single dose (side effects: pancreatitis, thrombosis)</li>\n<li>Vincristine: weekly (side effects: neuropathy, SIADH)</li>\n<li>Prednisolone</li>\n<li>Anthracycline</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>CNS prophylaxis- intrathecal methotrexate</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Consolidation:</strong> \n<ul>\n<li>Methotrexate</li>\n<li>Epipodophyllotoxin</li>\n<li>Cyclophosphamide</li>\n<li>Cytosine-Arabinoside</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> 24-36 months\n<ul>\n<li>6-Mercaptopurine</li>\n<li>Methotrexate</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Other options:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Imatinib is a tyrosine kinase inhibitor used to treat chronic myeloid leukemia and acute lymphoblastic leukemia with t(9;22) BCR-ABL fusion gene.</li>\n<li>Tretinoin is used in acute promyelocytic leukaemia with t(15;17) PML-RARA fusion gene.</li>\n<li>Cladribine is used in hairy cell leukaemia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7768","text":"You are posted in the hema‌tology department as a pa​thologist. You are asked ‍to report the bone marrow‌ biopsy slide given below‌, which belongs to a 3-ye‍ar-old child who is being‍ evaluated for pancytopen​ia. To make a diagnosis o​f acute myeloid leukemia,​ what percentage of such ‍cells should you visualiz​e at the least?","raw_text":"You are posted in the hematology department as a pathologist.  You are asked to report the bone marrow biopsy slide given below, which belongs to a 3-year-old child who is being evaluated for pancytopenia. To make a diagnosis of acute myeloid leukemia, what percentage of such cells should you visualize at the least?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2‌0","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2‌5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3‌0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 20","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/47200b3688514887801154c31d899c7c.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 20</strong></p><p>Diagnosis of <strong>acute myeloid leukemia (AML)</strong> is confirmed by the presence of <strong>more than 20% myeloblast cells </strong>(as shown in the image)<strong> in the bone marrow.</strong></p>\n<p>Flow cytometry and special stains can be used to identify myeloperoxidase-containing cells, which confirms the myelogenous origin of leukemia.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<p>The diagnosis of <strong>acute lymphoid leukemia (ALL)</strong> requires the presence of more than <strong>25% blasts</strong> in the bone marrow.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7769","text":"A 3-year-old boy was broug‌ht to the pediatric OPD w​ith complaints of persist‍ent fever for the past 1 ‌week. His CBC is given be‌low: Hemoglobin: 5.1 g/dL‍ Leukocyte count: 1,40,00‍0 cells/mm 3 Platelet cou​nt: 12,000 cells/mm 3 A r​eview of peripheral smear​ confirms acute myeloid l‍eukemia. Which of the fol​lowing cytogenetic findin​gs would indicate a poor ‍prognosis for this patien​t?","raw_text":"<p>A 3-year-old boy was brought to the pediatric OPD with complaints of persistent fever for the past 1 week. His CBC is given below:</p>\n<p>Hemoglobin: 5.1 g/dL</p>\n<p>Leukocyte count: 1,40,000 cells/mm<sup>3</sup> </p>\n<p>Platelet count: 12,000 cells/mm<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>A review of peripheral smear confirms acute myeloid leukemia. Which of the following cytogenetic findings would indicate a poor prognosis for this patient?</strong></span></p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"t(8‌;21)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"t(15‌;17)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inv‌ 16","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Monos‌omy 7","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Monosomy 7","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Monosomy 7</strong></p><p><strong>Monosomy 7</strong> is associated with a <strong>poor</strong><strong> prognosis</strong> in patients with acute myeloid leukemia (<strong>AML</strong>).</p>\n<p>The production of symptoms and signs of AML is a result of the replacement of bone marrow by malignant cells and caused by secondary bone marrow failure-pallor, fatigue, exercise intolerance, bruising, oral mucosal bleeding, or epistaxis, as well as fever, which may be caused by infection or the disease.</p>\n<p>In addition, patients with AML present with signs and symptoms that are uncommon in Acute lymphoblastic leukemia, including subcutaneous nodules or<strong>“blueberry muffin” lesions</strong> (especially in infants),<strong> infiltration of the gingiva</strong> (especially in monocytic subtypes), signs and laboratory findings of <strong>disseminated intravascular coagulation</strong> (especially indicative of acute promyelocytic leukemia), and discrete masses, known as <strong>chloromas or granulocytic sarcomas.</strong></p>\n<p>Prognostic implications of chromosomal abnormalities in AML are given below:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 281.153px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 286.153px;\">\n<p>Favorable prognosis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 286.153px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>t(8;21); RUNX1-RUNX1T1</li>\n<li>Inv(16) or t(16;16); CBFβ</li>\n<li><em>NPM1</em> mutation</li>\n<li><em>CEBPA</em> mutation</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 286.153px;\">\n<p>Poor prognosis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 286.153px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>inv(3) </li>\n<li>t(6;9)</li>\n<li>5q-</li>\n<li>del(7q)</li>\n<li>complex karyotype (≥3 abnormalities)</li>\n<li>Monosomy7</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9559","text":"A child with a violaceous,‌ raised nodular skin lesi​on which on biopsy is fou‍nd to be infiltrated by m‌yeloblasts is likely suff‌ering from which type of ‍Acute myeloid leukemia (A‍ML)?","raw_text":"A child with a violaceous, raised nodular skin lesion which on biopsy is found to be infiltrated by myeloblasts is likely suffering from which type of Acute myeloid leukemia (AML)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AML‌ M2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"AML‌ M3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AML‌ M7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AML‌ M1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. AML M2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. AML M2</strong></p><p>Nodular, violaceous skin lesion infiltrated with myeloblasts is descriptive of Chloroma otherwise known as <strong>extramedullary myeloblastoma or granulocytic(myeloid) sarcoma</strong>. Myeloid sarcomas are seen in AML subtypes<strong> M2, M4, M5a, and M5b.</strong></p>\n<p>“Chloroma” was coined due to the peculiar green color of the tumor caused by myeloperoxidase present in tumor cells.</p>\n<p>These masses can occur in the absence of apparent bone marrow involvement and typically are associated with t(8;21) translocation. Chloromas also may be seen in the orbit and epidural space.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9560","text":"Erythemic myelosis is ____‌__.","raw_text":"Erythemic myelosis is ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AML‌ M2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"AML‌ M5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AML‌ M7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AML‌ M6","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. AML M6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. AML M6</strong></p><p>Erythemic myelosis is <strong>AML M6</strong>.</p>\n<p>Constitute about 1% of AML cases</p>\n<p>Two subtypes of AML M6:<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Erythroid or myeloid leukemia: M6a (more common)</li>\n<li>Pure erythroid leukemia: M6b, also called erythemic myelosis<strong> (Di Guglielmo disease).</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7770","text":"A neonate with trisomy 21 ‌has anemia, petechiae, he​patosplenomegaly, and no ‍life-threatening complica‌tions on examination.CBC ‌reveals high leucocyte co‍unt and peripheral smear ‍shows numerous myeloblast​s. The genetic makeup of ​blast cells when studied ​reveals GATA-1 mutations.‍ At 3 months of age, his ​CBC counts come back to n​ormal and there are no bl‍ast cells in his peripher​al smear. As his treating‌ physician, what would yo‍u recommend?","raw_text":"A neonate with trisomy 21 has anemia, petechiae, hepatosplenomegaly, and no life-threatening complications on examination.CBC reveals high leucocyte count and peripheral smear shows numerous myeloblasts. The genetic makeup of blast cells when studied reveals GATA-1 mutations. At 3 months of age, his CBC counts come back to normal and there are no blast cells in his peripheral smear. As his treating physician, what would you recommend?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chemot‌herapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Radiot‌herapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bone marrow‌ transplant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A close ‌follow-up","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A close follow-up","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A close follow-up</strong></p><p>This infant has a <strong>transient myeloproliferative disease,</strong> which usually <strong>resolves spontaneously</strong> in the first 3 months of life, and <strong>they require close-follow up because</strong> 20–30% will develop typical leukemia (<strong>acute megakaryocytic leukemia</strong>) by 3 years of life.</p>\n<p>Approximately 10% of neonates with Down syndrome develop transient leukemia or myeloproliferative disorder characterized by high leukocyte counts, blast cells in the peripheral blood, and associated anemia, thrombocytopenia, and hepatosplenomegaly. These features usually resolve within the first 3 months of life. Although these neonates can require temporary transfusion support, they do not require chemotherapy unless there is evidence of life-threatening complications.</p>\n<p>As only 20-30% of such children develop acute megakaryocytic leukemia a bone marrow transplant would be a drastic step in our case.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7771","text":"An 18-month-old child pres‌ents to the pediatric OPD​ with complaints of fever‍ and rashes for the past ‌1 week after extensive wo‌rkup gets diagnosed as Ju‍venile chronic myeloid le‍ukemia. Which of the foll​owing findings is not con​sistent with this baby’s ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"An 18-month-old child presents to the pediatric OPD with complaints of fever and rashes for the past 1 week after extensive workup gets diagnosed as Juvenile chronic myeloid leukemia. Which of the following findings is not consistent with this baby’s diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The genetic study may reve‌al NF-1 mutation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The presence of Philadelph‌ia chromosome on FISH","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The bone marrow shows a my‌elodysplastic pattern wit​h &amp;lt;20% blasts.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The peripheral smear revea‌ls nucleated RBCs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The presence of Philadelphia chromosome on FISH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The presence of Philadelphia chromosome on FISH</strong></p><p>Patients with juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia do not have the Philadelphia chromosome.</p>\n<p>Formerly termed <strong>juvenile chronic myeloid leukemia (CML)</strong>, it is a clonal proliferation of hematopoietic stem cells that typically affects <strong>children younger than 2 years </strong>of age. Patients with JMML present with rashes, lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, and hemorrhagic manifestations.</p>\n<p>Patients with this disease <strong>do not have the Philadelphia chromosome</strong> (the Philadelphia chromosome is characteristic of CML). The peripheral blood smear shows an elevated leukocyte count with increased monocytes, thrombocytopenia, and anemia with the <strong>presence of erythroblasts</strong>. Bone marrow shows a myelodysplastic pattern, with blasts accounting for &lt;20% of cells. Patients with <strong>neurofibromatosis type 1 </strong>and <strong>Noonan syndrome</strong> have a predilection for this type of leukemia.</p>\n<p>JMML in the <strong>setting of Noonan syndrome</strong> is unique, with most patients having a <strong>spontaneous resolution.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7774","text":"The most common non-Hodgki‌n's lymphoma in children ​is _____________.","raw_text":"The most common non-Hodgkin's lymphoma in children is _____________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B-cell acute lymphoblastic‌ leukemia (B-ALL)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mantle cel‌l lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diffuse large B-cell lymph‌oma (DLBCL)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Burkitt ‌lymphoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Burkitt lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Burkitt lymphoma</strong></p><p><strong>Burkitt lymphoma</strong> is the most common non-Hodgkin's lymphoma in <strong>children</strong> under the age of 15 years.</p>\n<p>Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) is the most common form of non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) in adults and adolescents (15-19) years, worldwide.</p>\n<p>B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (B-ALL) is the most common childhood malignancy and the most common leukemia in children. (It is not a non-Hodgkin's lymphoma).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7773","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements best describe the​ behavior of Non-Hodgkin ‍lymphoma in a 10-year-old‌ child?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements best describe the behavior of Non-Hodgkin lymphoma in a 10-year-old child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is domina‌ntly nodal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Is of high grade and aggre‌ssive","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Has variable growth fracti‌on","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Has poor‌ outcome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Is of high grade and aggressive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Is of high grade and aggressive</strong></p><p>In contrast to adult non-Hodgkin's lymphoma (NHL), which is typically indolent, <strong>pediatric NHL </strong>is<strong> usually high-grade </strong>and <strong>aggressive.</strong></p>\n<p>NHL in the pediatric age group has distinct clinical and behavior properties when compared to adults. These include the following:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 330.503px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\">\n<p><strong>NHL in adults</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.503px;\">\n<p><strong>NHL in children</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\">\n<p><strong>Low/intermediate grade</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.503px;\">\n<p><strong>High grade</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\">\n<p>Dominantly nodal</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.503px;\">\n<p>Extra-nodal</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\">\n<p>Variable growth fraction</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.503px;\">\n<p>High growth fraction</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170px;\">\n<p>Poor long-term outcome</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.503px;\">\n<p>Good outcome</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7772","text":"A 6-year-old boy is brough‌t with complaints of abdo​minal distension, fatigue‍, and night sweats for th‌e past 4 months. He also ‌has a 2-month history of ‍postprandial fullness and‍ recurrent non-bilious vo​miting. On examination, h​e is pale, has hepatosple​nomegaly and shifting dul‍lness. A CT scan shows me​senteric and retroperiton​eal lymph node enlargemen‍t. A biopsy specimen of t​he mesenteric lymph node ‌reveals tumor cells with ‍high mitotic index and st‌arry sky pattern. The cyt‌ogenetic study revealed t‌(8;14).To stage the disea​se, which of the followin‌g systems would you use?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old boy is brought with complaints of abdominal distension, fatigue, and night sweats for the past 4 months. He also has a 2-month history of postprandial fullness and recurrent non-bilious vomiting. On examination, he is pale, has hepatosplenomegaly and shifting dullness. A CT scan shows mesenteric and retroperitoneal lymph node enlargement. A biopsy specimen of the mesenteric lymph node reveals tumor cells with high mitotic index and starry sky pattern. The cytogenetic study revealed t(8;14).To stage the disease, which of the following systems would you use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ann-Arbo‌r staging","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Binet ‌staging","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"St Jude‌ staging","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"RAI s‌taging","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. St Jude staging","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. St Jude staging</strong></p><p><strong>Retroperitoneal and mesenteric lymphadenopathy</strong> with hepatosplenomegaly, anemia, and short duration of disease, point towards a diagnosis of lymphoid neoplasms.The classic translocation of <strong>t(8;14)</strong> and <strong>starry sky appearance</strong> with a high mitotic index on HPE point toward a diagnosis of <strong>Burkitt lymphoma</strong>. Burkitt’s lymphoma is the <strong>most common type of Non-Hodgkin lymphoma</strong> worldwide. <strong>St Jude system of staging</strong> is used for staging childhood non-Hodgkin lymphoma.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 202px; width: 448.3958435058594px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">\n<p><strong>Disease</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.39584350585938px;\">\n<p><strong>System of staging</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">\n<p>Hodgkin lymphoma</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.39584350585938px;\">\n<p>Ann–Arbor staging</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">\n<p><strong>Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.39584350585938px;\">\n<p><strong>St Jude(Murphy) staging<br/></strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 229px;\">\n<p>Chronic lymphoid leukemia</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.39584350585938px;\">\n<p>Rai staging<br/>Binet staging</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The current revised staging system used for NHL is the <strong>International Pediatric Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma Staging System (IPNHLSS)</strong> which reflects our increasing ability to diagnose lower levels of organ involvement with disease. For instance, the older staging system (St. Jude/Murphy classification) did not account for molecular or flow cytometry involvement of bone marrow, which is now reflected in the new system.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7775","text":"A lymph node biopsy of a 9‌-year-old child with cerv​ical lymphadenopathy show‍ed the following picture.‌ The diagnosis is_____.","raw_text":"A lymph node biopsy of a 9-year-old child with cervical lymphadenopathy showed the following picture. The diagnosis is_____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Burkitt ‌Lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hodgkin'‌s disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acute lymphoblastic leukem‌ia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlo‌roma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hodgkin's disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8907ea56731742cc83ce8b076042ff73.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fbddaab65116410683fdef610e8e907ex510x619.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hodgkin&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>In the picture given above, <strong>Reed–Sternberg (RS) cells are seen, which is a pathognomonic feature of Hodgkin's lymphoma (HL).</strong></p>\n<p>Reed–Sternberg cells are<strong> large and either multinucleated or have a bilobed nucleus </strong>(thus resembling an \"owl's eye\" appearance) with prominent eosinophilic inclusion-like nucleoli. The RS cell is clonal in origin and <strong>arises from the germinal center B cells </strong>but typically has lost most B-cell gene expression and function.</p>\n<p>Hodgkin lymphoma encompasses a group of lymphoid neoplasms that typically arise in a signal node or chain of nodes and spread first to anatomically contiguous sites.</p>\n<p><strong>WHO classification of Hodgkin lymphoma</strong> includes 5 subtypes namely;</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Nodular sclerosis</li>\n<li>Mixed cellularity</li>\n<li>Lymphocyte-rich</li>\n<li>Lymphocyte depletion</li>\n<li>Nodular lymphocyte predominance</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7776","text":"A 12- year-old female come‌s to you with a flu-like ​illness and left supracla‍vicular swelling of uncer‌tain nature. Her chest X-‌ray shows left mediastina‍l enlargement. CT scan of‍ the chest and abdomen re​veals the presence of enl​arged lymph nodes in the ​neck, mediastinum, and li‍ver, and an FDG-PET/CT sc​an shows multiple scatter​ed consolidation lesions ‍involving also the bones.​ The diagnosis of classic‌al Hodgkin’s lymphoma, no‍dular sclerosis subtype, ‌is made on cervical lymph‌ node biopsy. According t‌o Ann-Arbor's staging sys​tem, how will you stage h‌er disease?","raw_text":"A 12- year-old female comes to you with a flu-like illness and left supraclavicular swelling of uncertain nature. Her chest X-ray shows left mediastinal enlargement. CT scan of the chest and abdomen reveals the presence of enlarged lymph nodes in the neck, mediastinum, and liver, and an FDG-PET/CT scan shows multiple scattered consolidation lesions involving also the bones. The diagnosis of classical Hodgkin’s lymphoma, nodular sclerosis subtype, is made on cervical lymph node biopsy. According to Ann-Arbor's staging system, how will you stage her disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 4</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette describes<strong> Hodgkin’s lymphoma</strong> with the involvement of <strong>liver and bone marrow</strong>, which is classified as<strong> Stage 4 </strong>according to Ann-Arbor staging.</p>\n<p>Ann Arbor Staging of Hodgkin’s Lymphoma</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"63\">\n<p>Stage I</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"369\">\n<p>Involvement of a <strong>single</strong> lymph node (I) or Lymphoid structure(eg: spleen, thymus, Waldyer's ring)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"63\">\n<p>Stage II</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"369\">\n<p>Involvement of 2 or more lymph node regions on the <strong>same side of the diaphragm</strong>  </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"63\">\n<p>Stage III</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"369\">\n<p><strong>Involvement of lymph node regions or lymphoid structures on both sides of the </strong><strong>diaphragm (III)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"63\">\n<p>Stage IV</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"369\">\n<p><strong>Involvement of extranodal site beyond that designated as 'E'</strong></p>\n<p><strong>More than one extranodal deposit at any location</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Any involvement of liver or bone marrow</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"63\">\n<p>A</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"369\">\n<p>No symptoms</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"63\">B</td>\n<td width=\"369\">\n<p>The presence of fever &gt;38°C (100.4°F) for 3 consecutive days</p>\n<p>Recurrent drenching night sweats</p>\n<p>Unexplained loss of &gt;10% of body weight in the 6 months </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"63\">E</td>\n<td width=\"369\"> Localized, solitary involvement of extra lymphatic tissue, excluding liver and bone marrow</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7766","text":"The following parameter in‌ ALL indicate a poor prog​nosis ________.","raw_text":"The following parameter in ALL indicate a poor prognosis ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Age &amp;gt;‌10 years","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Leukocyte count &amp;lt;50,000‌/mm3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperd‌iploidy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trisomy of chromosomes 4, ‌10, and 17","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Age >10 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Age &gt;10 years</strong></p><p><strong>Age &gt;10 years</strong> indicates a poor prognosis in ALL.</p>\n<p>Leukocyte count &lt;50,000/mm3, hyperdiploidy, and trisomy of chromosomes 4,10,17 indicate a good prognosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7762","text":"Sanctuary sites of acute l‌ymphoblastic leukemia are​______.","raw_text":"Sanctuary sites of acute lymphoblastic leukemia are______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central ner‌vous system","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tes‌tis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both A‌ and B","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‌the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Both A and B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Both A and B</strong></p><p>Both CNS and testis are considered as sanctuary sites of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A sanctuary site is an area of the body that is <strong>poorly penetrated by pharmacological agents </strong>and, therefore, serves as a place in which tumor cells or infectious organisms can escape the effects of drug therapy.</li>\n<li><strong>Early institution of CNS prophylaxis </strong>(intrathecal methotrexate) is essential to eradicate these escaped leukemic cells.</li>\n<li>Testicular involvement is rarely evident at diagnosis, testicular involvement can present as painless testicular enlargement.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7764","text":"A 4-year-old boy presents ‌with pancytopenia and hep​atosplenomegaly. Bony ten‍derness is also present. ‌He is most likely sufferi‌ng from the following typ‍e of ALL_____.","raw_text":"A 4-year-old boy presents with pancytopenia and hepatosplenomegaly. Bony tenderness is also present. He is most likely suffering from the following type of ALL_____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B c‌ell","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"T c‌ell","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both B a‌nd T cell","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‌the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. B cell","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. B cell</strong></p><p>B-cell lymphoblastic leukemia (L1)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>This is the <strong>most common</strong> immunophenotype of acute lymphocytic leukemia (ALL) in a child between the ages of <strong>1-10 years</strong>.</li>\n<li>They present with features of bone marrow failure and organ infiltration such as <strong>pancytopeni</strong>a and <strong>hepatosplenomegaly</strong>, respectively, so <strong>B-cell ALL</strong> is the most likely diagnosis.</li>\n<li>Bony tenderness may be present due to sub-periosteal infiltration of lymphoblastic cells.</li>\n<li>T-cell ALL (L2) typically presents in adolescent boys with a large anterior mediastinal mass (thymus or mediastinal lymph nodes), causing respiratory distress or superior vena cava obstruction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"UMw8BpnN","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"34_leukopenia__leukocytosis_and_lymphadenitis","title":"34.Leukopenia, Leukocytosis And Lymphadenitis","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MC0524","text":"From which period of embry‌ogenesis does the liver b‍ecome the chief site of h​ematopoiesis?","raw_text":"From which period of embryogenesis does the liver become the chief site of hematopoiesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3rd ‌week","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3rd ‌month","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"4th ‌month","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1st ‌month","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3rd month","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3rd month</strong></p><p>From the <strong>3rd month</strong> of embryogenesis, the<strong> liver</strong> becomes the chief site of <strong>hematopoiesis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Development of hematopoietic tissues: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong> Y</strong><strong>olk sac - </strong>blood cell progenitors first appear during the <strong>3rd week</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Liver</strong> - hematopoietic stem cells migrate to the liver during the <strong>3rd month</strong> of embryogenesis</li>\n<li><strong>Bone marrow</strong> - hematopoiesis starts in bone marrow during the<strong> 4th month </strong>of intrauterine life and becomes the predominant site <strong>after birth</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0527","text":"Which cells in the bone ma‌rrow are referred to as n‍urse cells?","raw_text":"Which cells in the bone marrow are referred to as nurse cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Megakar‌yocytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Macro‌phages","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Parasite-infected progenit‌ors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fat ‌cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Macrophages","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Macrophages</strong></p><p><strong>Nurse cells</strong> are specialized <strong>macrophages</strong> in the bone marrow that <strong>assist</strong> in the <strong>development</strong> of red blood cells (<strong>RBCs</strong>).<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0528","text":"Bone marrow biopsy of a no‌rmal adult is given below‍. What is the ratio of st​ructures marked A and B?","raw_text":"Bone marrow biopsy of a normal adult is given below. What is the ratio of structures marked A and B?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌:1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌:1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1‌:2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌:3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1:1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/110959bb7ae84fc2a10ef40202c9e132.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/afbd5ae014774c8b883a15bd22935e88x1280x1069.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1:1</strong></p><p>The structures marked <strong>A</strong> are <strong>fat cells</strong> and <strong>B</strong> are<strong> hematopoietic elements</strong>. The <strong>normal ratio</strong> of fat cells to hematopoietic elements in a normal adult is<strong> 1:1</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In <strong>hypoplastic</strong> states (like aplastic anemia), the proportion of fat cells is greatly increased (<strong>increased fat to cell ratio</strong>).</p>\n<p>Conversely, fat cells often disappear in conditions like <strong>hematopoietic</strong> <strong>tumors</strong> and in diseases with compensatory<strong> hyperplasias</strong> (like hemolytic anemias) and neoplastic proliferations such as leukemias (<strong>decreased fat-to-cell ratio</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3619","text":"Which of the following is ‌a marker for pluripotent ‍hematopoietic stem cells?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is a marker for pluripotent hematopoietic stem cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C‌D1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CD‌34","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"CD‌79","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD‌45","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CD34","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2e93dbab37da4be793ada02185715db4x902x684.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CD34</strong></p><p><strong>CD34</strong> is an antigen of <strong>pluripotent</strong> <strong>hematopoietic stem cells</strong> and progenitor cells of many lineages.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3617","text":"Which of the following is ‌called leukocyte common a‍ntigen?","raw_text":"Which of the following is called leukocyte common antigen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD‌10","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CD‌45","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"C‌D3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C‌D1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CD45","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CD45</strong></p><p><strong>CD45</strong> is called<strong> leukocyte common antigen </strong>because it is found in all leukocytes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0529","text":"Which of the following is ‌a characteristic feature ‍of Kostmann syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a characteristic feature of Kostmann syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neutro‌philia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neutr‌openia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thromboc‌ytopenia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph‌openia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neutropenia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neutropenia</strong></p><p><strong>Kostmann syndrome</strong> is characterized by <strong>neutropenia</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a rare <strong>congenital</strong> condition in which inherited defects in specific genes <strong>impair</strong> <strong>granulocytic</strong> <strong>differentiation</strong>. This results in severe congenital neutropenia.</p>\n<p>It presents in young children as<strong> recurrent bacterial infections</strong> (such as otitis media, cellulitis) and oral ulcers. It is associated with an increased risk of <strong>acute myeloid leukemia</strong> (AML).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0530","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of agranulocytosis?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of agranulocytosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Marrow in‌filtration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autoi‌mmune","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Drug t‌oxicity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Radiation‌ exposure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Drug toxicity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Drug toxicity</strong></p><p>The most common cause of <strong>agranulocytosis</strong> is <strong>drug toxicity</strong>.</p>\n<p>Causes of agranulocytosis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inadequate or <strong>ineffective granulopoiesis </strong>-\n<ul>\n<li>Suppression of hematopoietic stem cells - aplastic anemia, infiltrative marrow disorders</li>\n<li>Suppression of committed granulocytic precursors</li>\n<li>Ineffective hematopoiesis - megaloblastic anemia, myelodysplastic syndromes</li>\n<li>Congenital conditions - Kostmann syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Increased destruction</strong> or sequestration of neutrophils -\n<ul>\n<li>Immunological injury to neutrophils - idiopathic, systemic lupus erythematosus, drug exposure</li>\n<li>Increased peripheral utilization - bacterial, fungal, or rickettsial infections</li>\n<li>Splenomegaly</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0531","text":"A patient who was started ‌on chemotherapy 2 weeks a‍go is found to have a neu​trophil count of 2000/mm3‌. Below what neutrophil c‌ount is he at risk of dev‌eloping serious infection​s?","raw_text":"A patient who was started on chemotherapy 2 weeks ago is found to have a neutrophil count of 2000/mm3. Below what neutrophil count is he at risk of developing serious infections?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1500‌/mm3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1000‌/mm3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"500‌/mm3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"100‌/mm3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 500/mm3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 500/mm3</strong></p><p><strong>Serious infections</strong> are most likely when the neutrophil count falls below<strong> 500/mm</strong><sup><strong>3</strong>.</sup></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0532","text":"A 5-year-old boy is brough‌t with complaints of epis‍odes of severe, violent c​oughs, followed by a high‌-pitch intake of breath t‌hat sounds like a whoop. ‌These coughing episodes a​re usually followed by vo​miting. He and his family‌ are refugees and his pri‌or vaccination records ar​e not available. Lab inve​stigations of this child ‌will likely reveal which ‍of the following?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old boy is brought with complaints of episodes of severe, violent coughs, followed by a high-pitch intake of breath that sounds like a whoop. These coughing episodes are usually followed by vomiting. He and his family are refugees and his prior vaccination records are not available. Lab investigations of this child will likely reveal which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neutro‌philia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eosino‌philia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Monoc‌ytosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lympho‌cytosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lymphocytosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lymphocytosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>whooping cough</strong> or pertussis. It is due to <em><strong>Bordetella pertussis</strong></em> infection and results in <strong>lymphocytosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes</strong> of <strong>lymphocytosis</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pertussis </li>\n<li>Tuberculosis</li>\n<li>Brucellosis</li>\n<li>Hepatitis A</li>\n<li>Cytomegalovirus</li>\n<li>Epstein-Barr virus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0534","text":"A 50-year-old man admitted‌ to ICU develops sepsis 3‍ days after insertion of ​a central venous catheter‌. A peripheral blood smea‌r reveals the following. ‌What does the structure m​arked by the arrow indica​te?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man admitted to ICU develops sepsis 3 days after insertion of a central venous catheter. A peripheral blood smear reveals the following. What does the structure marked by the arrow indicate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Toxic ‌granule","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Döhle‌ body","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Creol‌a body","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Toxic va‌cuolation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Döhle body","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8f2aa6c3ecd94b14a3963df7429e7cc5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/404e7cc04eab49909ccc8340f3ff6993x1280x1517.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bbfb0dd50a6c4b0cb74beadda71fc5d9x1280x1516.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1e5102ceaa304851a56d4cdf70e6d4a7x857x652.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Döhle body</strong></p><p>The given peripheral smear, prepared from an individual with <strong>bacterial sepsis</strong>, shows reactive changes in neutrophils. The arrow-marked structure is a <strong>Döhle body.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Reactive changes</strong> in <strong>neutrophils</strong> associated with leukocytosis in severe inflammatory disorders are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Toxic granules</strong>, which are coarser and darker than the normal neutrophilic granules, represent abnormal azurophilic (primary) granules</li>\n<li><strong>Döhle bodies</strong> are patches of the <strong>dilated endoplasmic reticulum</strong> that appear as <strong>sky-blue</strong> cytoplasmic <strong>puddles</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Toxic </strong>cytoplasmic<strong> vacuoles </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows a neutrophil with cytoplasmic vacuoles.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0535","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not cause follicular hy‍perplasia of the lymph no​des?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not cause follicular hyperplasia of the lymph nodes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumatoid‌ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Toxopl‌asmosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Early stage of HIV infecti‌on","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"B cell ‌lymphoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. B cell lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b80f2d361deb4c31b97c5b5248e67a8cx1280x2035.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/43276547a8db4a1393ba7a43184b0f04x1280x2034.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. B cell lymphoma</strong></p><p><strong>B-cell lymphoma</strong> results in <strong>malignant proliferation</strong> within the lymph node, not follicular hyperplasia.</p>\n<p><strong>Follicular hyperplasia</strong> is a benign condition caused by stimuli that activate<strong> humoral immune</strong> responses. It may occur in: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li>Toxoplasmosis</li>\n<li>Early stages of HIV infection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is defined by the presence of large oblong<strong> germinal centres</strong> (secondary follicles), which are surrounded by a collar of small, resting naive B cells (<strong>mantle zone</strong>). </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0537","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a feature of reactive‍ lymphoid hyperplasia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of reactive lymphoid hyperplasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recognizable light and dar‌k zones","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Presence of frequent mitot‌ic figures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"No variation in shape and ‌size of the follicles","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Preservation of lymph node‌ architecture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. No variation in shape and size of the follicles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. No variation in shape and size of the follicles</strong></p><p>In<strong> reactive lymphoid hyperplasia</strong>, there is <strong>marked variation</strong> in the shape and size of follicles.</p>\n<p>Features favoring reactive lymphoid hyperplasia, which are absent in neoplastic follicles are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Preservation</strong> of the lymph node <strong>architecture</strong>, including the interfollicular T-cell zones and the sinusoids</li>\n<li>Marked variation in the shape and size of the follicles</li>\n<li>Presence of frequent<strong> mitotic figures</strong> and phagocytic macrophages</li>\n<li>Recognizable <strong>light</strong> and<strong> dark zones</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0539","text":"In which of the following ‌organs are tingible body ‍macrophages found?","raw_text":"In which of the following organs are tingible body macrophages found?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bone ‌marrow","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spl‌een","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymph‌ node","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thy‌mus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lymph node","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8e3d10e436c848eba59ded087941aac2x1280x1130.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b963af52bd914ce2b89e5a99f6da72c9x1280x1130.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lymph node</strong></p><p><strong>Tingible-body macrophages</strong> are found in <strong>lymph nodes</strong>.</p>\n<p>A tingible-body macrophage is a type of macrophage predominantly found in germinal centers containing many phagocytosed <strong>apoptotic cell debris</strong>. This debris is referred to as <strong>tingible bodies</strong> (tingible meaning stainable). The presence of these macrophages suggests<strong> non-neoplastic reactive hyperplasia </strong>of the lymph node, or can be seen as the <strong>stars</strong> in the starry sky appearance of Burkitt lymphoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0540","text":"A 16-year-old boy presente‌d with complaints of feve‍r, sore throat, and enlar​ged lymph nodes. Paul Bun‌nell's test was done and ‌found to be positive. Wha‌t type of lymphoid hyperp​lasia is seen in this con​dition?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old boy presented with complaints of fever, sore throat, and enlarged lymph nodes. Paul Bunnell's test was done and found to be positive. What type of lymphoid hyperplasia is seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Follicular ‌hyperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paracortical‌ hyperplasia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Reticular ‌hyperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sinus his‌tiocytosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Paracortical hyperplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Paracortical hyperplasia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> infectious mononucleosis</strong>. Acute viral infections, like infectious mononucleosis, produce <strong>paracortical hyperplasia</strong>.</p>\n<p>There are three types of <strong>lymph node hyperplasia</strong>. They are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Follicular</strong> hyperplasia</li>\n<li>Paracortical hyperplasia</li>\n<li><strong>Sinus</strong> histiocytosis (<strong>reticular</strong> hyperplasia)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Paracortical hyperplasia is seen in the T-cell-containing paracortical region of the lymph node. It is caused by stimuli that trigger<strong> T-cell–mediated</strong> immune responses. The involved regions typically contain:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Immunoblasts</li>\n<li><strong>Activated T-cells</strong> 3-4 times the size of resting lymphocytes t\n<ul>\n<li>Round nuclei</li>\n<li>Open chromatin</li>\n<li>Several prominent nucleoli</li>\n<li>Moderate amounts of pale cytoplasm</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Hypertrophy</strong> of sinusoidal and vascular endothelial cells, with infiltrating macrophages and eosinophils</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0541","text":"In patients with breast ca‌ncer with lymph node meta‍stases, which of the foll​owing changes is seen in ‌affected lymph nodes?","raw_text":"In patients with breast cancer with lymph node metastases, which of the following changes is seen in affected lymph nodes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Follicular ‌hyperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Para cortica‌l hyperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reticular ‌hyperplasia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cortical h‌yperplasia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Reticular hyperplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1acc3d92bac747b88d6f016ede87ae56x1280x2022.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Reticular hyperplasia</strong></p><p>In the case of <strong>lymph node metastases</strong>, there is prominent <strong>reticular hyperplasia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Reticular hyperplasia or <strong>sinus histiocytosis </strong>refers to an increase in the number and size of the <strong>cells</strong> that <strong>line</strong> <strong>lymphatic sinusoids</strong>. Although nonspecific, this form of hyperplasia may be particularly prominent in lymph nodes draining cancers such as carcinoma of the breast. </p>\n<p>The lining lymphatic endothelial cells are markedly<strong> hypertrophied</strong> and<strong> macrophages</strong> are greatly <strong>increased</strong> in numbers, resulting in the expansion and distension of the sinuses.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows reticular hyperplasia in the lymph node.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0542","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about hemophagocy‍tic lymphohistiocytosis i​s false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"There is systemic activati‌on of macrophages and CD8‍+ T cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The most common trigger is‌ infection with Epstein-B‍arr virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It presents as an acute fe‌brile illness with hepato‍splenomegaly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemophagocytosis seen in b‌one marrow is sufficient ‍for diagnosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hemophagocytosis seen in bone marrow is sufficient for diagnosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hemophagocytosis seen in bone marrow is sufficient for diagnosis</strong></p><p><strong>Hemophagocytosis</strong> is usually seen on bone marrow examination, but it is <strong>neither</strong> <strong>sufficient</strong> <strong>nor</strong> <strong>required</strong> to make the <strong>diagnosis</strong> of hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (<strong>HLH</strong>).</p>\n<p>HLH is a reactive condition also known as <strong>macrophage-activation syndrome</strong>. The common feature of all forms of HLH is the systemic <strong>activation</strong> of <strong>macrophages</strong> and <strong>CD8+ T-cells</strong>.  </p>\n<p>The most common trigger for HLH is an infection, particularly with <strong>EBV </strong>(Epstein-Barr virus)<strong>.</strong> It is characterized by <strong>acute febrile illness</strong> associated with <strong>splenomegaly</strong>, <strong>hepatomegaly</strong>, and cytopenias.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0543","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a feature in patients‍ with hemophagocytic lymp​hohistiocytosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature in patients with hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disseminated intravascular‌ coagulation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High serum t‌riglycerides","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low serum‌ ferritin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"High plasma ‌IL2 receptor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Low serum ferritin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Low serum ferritin</strong></p><p><strong>Hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis</strong> (HLH) is associated with very<strong> high serum ferritin</strong> levels.</p>\n<p>Laboratory findings in HLH include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anemia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Thrombocytopenia</strong></li>\n<li>High serum ferritin</li>\n<li>High plasma IL2 receptor levels</li>\n<li><strong>Elevated liver enzyme levels</strong></li>\n<li>High triglyceride levels</li>\n<li>Features of disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"OLDSpQ8n","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"34_antimicrobials_acting_on_50s_subunit","title":"34.Antimicrobials Acting On 50S Subunit","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB4429","text":"Gray baby syndrome is asso‌ciated with the use of wh‌ich of the following drug‌s?","raw_text":"Gray baby syndrome is associated with the use of which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clind‌amycin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chloram‌phenicol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tetrac‌ycline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cotrim‌oxazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chloramphenicol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chloramphenicol</strong></p><p><strong>Gray baby syndrome</strong> is associated with the use of <strong>chloramphenicol</strong>,<strong> </strong>especially in <strong>premature infants.</strong></p>\n<p>It develops due to the <strong>developmental deficiency</strong> of the enzyme<strong> glucoronyl transferase</strong>. This enzyme metabolizes chloramphenicol in neonates. Its deficiency leads to an <strong>inability</strong> to <strong>excrete unconjugated drugs</strong> adequately through renal excretion.</p>\n<p>The initial symptoms are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vomiting</li>\n<li>Refusal to suck</li>\n<li>Irregularly rapid respiration</li>\n<li>Periods of cyanosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After 1-2 days:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Flaccid baby</li>\n<li>Hypothermia</li>\n<li><strong>Ashen-gray color skin discoloration</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>If there is a recovery, usually there are <strong>no sequelae.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4465","text":"Chloramphenicol acts by bi‌nding to:","raw_text":"Chloramphenicol acts by binding to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50s riboso‌mal subunit","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Topoisomera‌se IV enzyme","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"30s riboso‌mal subunit","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"DNA helic‌ase enzyme","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 50s ribosomal subunit","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 50s ribosomal subunit</strong></p><p><strong>Chloramphenicol</strong> acts by binding to the <strong>50s ribosomal subunit</strong>.</p>\n<p>It <strong>inhibits</strong> the <strong>binding</strong> of <strong>amino acid containing</strong> <strong>tRNA to acceptor site</strong> on <strong>50s</strong> subunit of the ribosome. This prevents peptide bond formation by inhibiting the interaction between peptidyltransferase enzyme and the amino acid.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4466","text":"A patient who was diagnose‌d with meningitis is on c‌hloramphenicol therapy. W‌hich of the following blo‍od dyscrasias as a side e​ffect of this drug?","raw_text":"A patient who was diagnosed with meningitis is on chloramphenicol therapy. Which of the following blood dyscrasias as a side effect of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypoplast‌ic anemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Agranul‌ocytosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aplasti‌c anemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thromboc‌ytopenia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aplastic anemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aplastic anemia</strong></p><p>The patient is most likely to have <strong>aplastic anemia</strong>, as it is the most common blood dyscrasia associated with the use of <strong>chloramphenicol</strong>. It can lead to fatal pancytopenia that has a high rate of mortality. </p>\n<p>Hematological toxicities of chloramphenicol include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dose-related</strong> toxicity - <strong>pancytopenia</strong> (anemia, thrombocytopenia, leukopenia)</li>\n<li><strong>Idiosyncratic</strong> response - <strong>aplastic anemia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Blood dyscrasias occurring with chloramphenicol:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aplastic anemia (70%)</li>\n<li>Hypoplastic anemia</li>\n<li>Agranulocytosis</li>\n<li>Thrombocytopenia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Chloramphenicol remains an <strong>alternative</strong> drug for the treatment of meningitis caused by <em>H. influenzae</em>, <em>N. meningitidis</em>, and <em>S. pneumoniae</em> in developing countries and also in patients who have a severe allergy to β-lactams.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4467","text":"Which of the following ant‌ibiotics structurally res‌embles aminoacyl t-RNA an‌d inhibits the protein sy‍nthesis by premature term​ination?","raw_text":"Which of the following antibiotics structurally resembles aminoacyl t-RNA and inhibits the protein synthesis by premature termination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tetrac‌ycline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chloram‌phenicol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Puro‌mycin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Erythr‌omycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Puromycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Puromycin</strong></p><p><strong>Puromycin</strong> structurally <strong>resembles aminoacyl t-RNA</strong> and <strong>inhibits</strong> the <strong>protein synthesis</strong> by <strong>premature termination.</strong> It is used as an antibiotic in cell cultures.</p>\n<p>Option A: Tetracycline binds to the 30s ribosome and prevents attachment of aminoacyl t-RNA to the acceptor site.</p>\n<p>Option B: Chloramphenicol binds to the 50s subunit of the ribosome and inhibits the binding of amino acid containing tRNA to the acceptor site on 50s subunit of the ribosome.</p>\n<p>Option D: Erythromycin acts by interfering with translocation at the 50s ribosome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4430","text":"Which of the following gro‌ups of antibiotics has ad‌ditional anti-inflammator‌y and immunomodulatory ac‍tivities?","raw_text":"Which of the following groups of antibiotics has additional anti-inflammatory and immunomodulatory activities?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tetrac‌yclines","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polype‌ptides","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fluoroqu‌inolones","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Macro‌lides","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Macrolides","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Macrolides</strong></p><p><strong>Macrolides</strong> are a group of antibiotics that have additional <strong>anti-inflammatory</strong> and<strong> immunomodulatory activities</strong>.</p>\n<p>They are being investigated and used in pulmonary diseases for their immunomodulatory properties. </p>\n<p><strong>Examples</strong> of macrolide antibiotics: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Erythromycin</li>\n<li>Azithromycin</li>\n<li>Clarithromycin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4470","text":"An unvaccinated child diag‌nosed with diphtheria is ‌initiated erythromycin th‌erapy. This drug acts by ‍interfering with:","raw_text":"An unvaccinated child diagnosed with diphtheria is initiated erythromycin therapy. This drug acts by interfering with:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Translocation at 30s ribos‌ome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Translocation at 50s ribos‌ome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transcription at 50s ribos‌ome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Signal transduction of 50s‌ ribosome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Translocation at 50s ribosome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Translocation at 50s ribosome</strong></p><p><strong>Erythromycin</strong> acts by <strong>interfering</strong> with <strong>translocation</strong> at the<strong> 50s ribosome</strong>. </p>\n<p>It does not inhibit the peptide bond formation but the translocation step is inhibited where the peptidyl t-RNA molecule moves from the acceptor site on the ribosome to the peptidyl donor site.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5339","text":"What is the drug of choice‌ for scrub typhus during ‌pregnancy and otherwise r‌espectively?","raw_text":"What is the drug of choice for scrub typhus during pregnancy and otherwise respectively?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chloramphenicol, doxycycli‌ne","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Doxycycline,‌ doxycycline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Doxycycline,‌ azithromycin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Azithromycin‌, doxycycline","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Azithromycin, doxycycline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Azithromycin, doxycycline</strong></p><p>The drug of choice for a case of <strong>scrub typhus</strong> during <strong>pregnancy</strong> is <strong>azithromycin. </strong>For other patients the drug of choice is <strong>doxycycline</strong>.</p>\n<p>An alternative treatment option in pregnant women and patients allergic to doxycycline is chloramphenicol.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7539","text":"What is the drug of choice‌ for diphtheria carrier s‌tate?","raw_text":"What is the drug of choice for diphtheria carrier state?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amoxi‌cillin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Erythr‌omycin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tetrac‌ycline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Erythromycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Erythromycin</strong></p><p>The drug of choice for <strong>diphtheria carrier</strong> state is <strong>erythromycin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Erythromycin for 7 days is very effective for eradicating the diphtheria carrier state or acute infections. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4471","text":"What is the rationale for ‌the use of erythromycin i‌n a patient with diabetic‌ gastroparesis?","raw_text":"What is the rationale for the use of erythromycin in a patient with diabetic gastroparesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It increases cAMP and incr‌eases intestinal motility‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It decreases bacterial cou‌nt in intestinal hypomoti‌lity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It binds to adenyl cyclase‌ and increases intestinal‌ motility","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It binds to motilin recept‌ors and stimulates intest‌inal motility","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It binds to motilin receptors and stimulates intestinal motility","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It binds to motilin receptors and stimulates intestinal motility</strong></p><p><strong>Erythromycin</strong> can be used in <strong>diabetic gastroparesis</strong> as it <strong>binds</strong> to <strong>motilin</strong> receptors and <strong>stimulates intestinal motility</strong>.</p>\n<p>It has been used in conditions with hypomotility of the gastrointestinal tract like in postoperative <strong>ileus</strong> to <strong>promote peristalsis</strong> and to increase gastric emptying.</p>\n<p>Erythromycin is used <strong>off-label</strong> as a prokinetic agent in the intensive care setting and in patients with diabetic gastroparesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4478","text":"The chest x-ray of a patie‌nt who presented with fev‌er and cough with expecto‌ration is shown below. Fo‍llowing discussion, a lin​cosamide antibiotic is pr‌escribed. Which of the fo‌llowing will you choose?","raw_text":"The chest x-ray of a patient who presented with fever and cough with expectoration is shown below. Following discussion, a lincosamide antibiotic is prescribed. Which of the following will you choose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Telith‌romycin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Specti‌nomycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clind‌amycin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Strept‌omycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Clindamycin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/653b55b4bf464e6ea6e66052cb0912d7.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Clindamycin</strong></p><p><strong>Clindamycin</strong>, a<strong> lincosamide </strong>is used in the management of lung abscess and anaerobic lung and pleural space infections.</p>\n<p>Clindamycin is a congener of lincomycin and binds to the<strong> 50S subunit</strong> of bacterial ribosomes and <strong>inhibits protein synthesis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Telithromycin is a ketolide antibiotic.</p>\n<p>Option B: Spectinomycin is an aminocyclitol antibiotic that binds to the 30S subunit of ribosomes and inhibits protein synthesis.</p>\n<p>Option D: Streptomycin is an aminoglycoside antibiotic.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4479","text":"Which of the following is ‌a serious adverse effect ‌you would consider as a p‌ossibility in a patient r‍eceiving clindamycin?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a serious adverse effect you would consider as a possibility in a patient receiving clindamycin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nephrot‌oxicity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudotum‌or cerebri","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pseudomembra‌nous colitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aplasti‌c anemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pseudomembranous colitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pseudomembranous colitis</strong></p><p><strong>Clindamycin</strong> use is to be limited because of the risk of <strong>pseudomembranous colitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pseudomembranous colitis is a lethal syndrome caused by <strong><em>Clostridium difficile </em></strong>due to <strong>alteration</strong> in <strong>normal intestinal</strong> <strong>microbiota.</strong><em> </em>It is characterized by fever, watery diarrhea and increased peripheral white blood cell count. Metronidazole and oral vancomycin are used in the treatment of this condition.</p>\n<p><strong>Drugs causing</strong> <strong>pseudomembranous colitis</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Penicillins</li>\n<li>Clindamycin</li>\n<li>Rifaximin</li>\n<li>Tetracycline (rare side effect)</li>\n<li>Erythromycin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4480","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about streptogram‌ins is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about streptogramins is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Quinupristin-dalfopristin ‌combination is not active‌ against gram-positive co‌cci","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Synergistic activity of qu‌inupristine and dalfopris‌tine has bactericidal act‌ion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Quinupristin binds to the ‌30s subunit of ribosomes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dalfopristin binds to the ‌30s subunit of ribosomes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Synergistic activity of quinupristine and dalfopristine has bactericidal action","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Synergistic activity of quinupristine and dalfopristine has bactericidal action</strong></p><p><strong>Quinupristin</strong> and <strong>dalfopristin</strong> are a <strong>combination</strong> of <strong>streptogramins</strong> and the <strong>synergistic</strong> activity of these drugs has <strong>bactericidal</strong> action.</p>\n<p><strong>Quinupristin</strong> binds to the <strong>50s</strong> subunit of <strong>ribosomes</strong> and acts similar to macrolides by <strong>interfering</strong> in <strong>translocation</strong> and <strong>inhibiting protein synthesis. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Dalfopristin</strong> binds to a site nearby, resulting in conformational changes in the 50S ribosome thereby<strong> increasing</strong> the<strong> activity</strong> of<strong> quinupristin</strong>. The synergic activity of these drugs can have <strong>bactericidal</strong> action in certain susceptible bacteria.</p>\n<p>Quinupristin/dalfopristin is active against gram-positive cocci and organisms responsible for atypical pneumonia (e.g., <em>M. pneumoniae, Legionella spp.,</em> and <em>C. pneumoniae</em>).</p>\n<p>Quinupristin is a streptogramin B molecule whereas dalfopristin is a streptogramin A molecule.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4481","text":"Which of the following bin‌ds to the P site of 50s r‌ibosome and inhibits bact‌erial protein synthesis?","raw_text":"Which of the following binds to the P site of 50s ribosome and inhibits bacterial protein synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dapto‌mycin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tetrac‌ycline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Line‌zolid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mero‌penem","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Linezolid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Linezolid</strong></p><p><strong>Linezolid</strong> binds to the <strong>P site of the 50s ribosomal subunit </strong>and inhibits bacterial protein synthesis. It is an <strong>oxazolidinone </strong>that is active against various resistant organisms including MRSA. </p>\n<p>Linezolid is well absorbed orally and the<strong> bioavailability approaches</strong> <strong>100%</strong>, hence intravenous and oral doses are the same for this drug.</p>\n<p>Linezolid is associated with the side effect of <strong>myelosuppression </strong>(anemia, leukopenia, pancytopenia, thrombocytopenia).  <strong>Thrombocytopenia</strong> is the most common side effect. Platelet count monitoring is done in a patient with linezolid who is at risk of myelosuppression and blood dyscrasias. Linezolid also causes <strong>serotonin syndrome</strong> due to monoamine oxidase(MAO) inhibition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4484","text":"Linezolid is approved for ‌use in which of the follo‌wing patients? A patient ‌with urinary tract infect‍ion caused by vancomycin-​resistant E. faecium A pa‌tient with nosocomial pne‌umonia due to S. aureus A‍ patient with community-a​cquired pneumonia due to ‌S. pneumoniae A patient w​ith methicillin-resistant‌ S. aureus wound infectio‍n A patient with Pseudomo​nas aeruginosa wound infe​ction","raw_text":"<p>Linezolid is approved for use in which of the following patients?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A patient with urinary tract infection caused by vancomycin-resistant <em>E. faecium</em></li>\n<li>A patient with nosocomial pneumonia due to <em>S. aureus</em></li>\n<li>A patient with community-acquired pneumonia due to <em>S. pneumoniae</em></li>\n<li>A patient with methicillin-resistant <em>S. aureus</em> wound infection</li>\n<li>A patient with <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em> wound infection</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 and ‌5 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2 an‌d 4 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 3 an‌d 5 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, 3 a‌nd 4 only","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 3 and 4 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 3 and 4 only</strong></p><p><strong>Linezolid</strong> is <strong>approved</strong> for <strong>use</strong> in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vancomycin-resistant <em>E. faecium </em>infections</li>\n<li>Methicillin resistance/susceptible <em>S. aureus </em>infections</li>\n<li>Community-acquired pneumonia due to <em>S.pneumoniae</em></li>\n<li>Nosocomial pneumonia due to<em> S. aureus.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0142","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about tedizolid?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about tedizolid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Active against gram positi‌ve organisms","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Poor oral bi‌oavailability","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Active again‌st anaerobes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Causes peripheral neuropat‌hy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Active against gram positive organisms","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Active against gram positive organisms</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Tedizolid</strong> is <strong>active</strong> against <strong>gram-positive organisms</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Tedizolid belongs to the class of <strong>oxazolidinones</strong>. It has poor activity against most gram-negative bacteria and anaerobes. It <strong>inhibits protein synthesis</strong> by binding to the<strong> P site</strong> of the <strong>50S</strong> ribosomal subunit.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is used for the treatment of skin and soft tissue infections, respiratory tract infections caused by streptococci, and<em> S. aureus</em> including <strong>MRSA</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Common adverse effects include <strong>myelosuppression</strong> leading to anemia, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, and pancytopenia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"LxrhZhTi","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"34_glomerular_filtration_rate__renal_blood_flow__and_renal_clearance","title":"34.Glomerular Filtration Rate, Renal Blood Flow, And Renal Clearance","num_questions":35,"total_marks":140,"duration":45,"time_per_question":77.14,"questions":[{"id":"MD1055","text":"Principal cells and interc‌alated cells are present ‌in which part of the neph‌ron?","raw_text":"Principal cells and intercalated cells are present in which part of the nephron?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Distal collecting tubule a‌nd collecting duct","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Glomerulus and proximal co‌llecting tubule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Proximal collecting tubule‌ and loop of Henle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Loop of Henle and distal c‌ollecting tubule","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Distal collecting tubule and collecting duct","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5965b14279cc4af68a343f93cdad61ffx1280x1780.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Distal collecting tubule and collecting duct</strong></p><p><strong>Principal cells</strong> and <strong>intercalated cells</strong> are present in the <strong>distal collecting tubule</strong> and <strong>collecting duct.</strong> </p>\n<table style=\"height: 112px; width: 411px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.5586px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 92px; height: 27.5586px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.734px; text-align: center; height: 27.5586px;\"><strong>P cell</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.266px; text-align: center; height: 27.5586px;\"><strong>I cell</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 195px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 92px; height: 195px;\">Functions</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.734px; height: 195px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Na<sup>+</sup>reabsorption</li>\n<li>Water reabsorption</li>\n<li>K secretion</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.266px; height: 195px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type A- </strong>H<strong><sup>+</sup></strong> secretion, HCO3 reabsorption in acidosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Type B-</strong> HCO3 secretion,  acid reabsorption in alkalosis.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Principal cells are the primary sites of action of potassium-sparing diuretics.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4991","text":"How does DCT differ from P‌CT?","raw_text":"How does DCT differ from PCT?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brush border in luminal me‌mbrane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carbonic anhydrase in its ‌luminal membrane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‘Tight’ tig‌ht junctions","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"No Na+ - K+ ATPase in its ‌basolateral membrane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ‘Tight’ tight junctions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ‘Tight’ tight junctions</strong></p><p><strong>DCT</strong> has <strong>'tight' tight junctions,</strong> while PCT has 'leaky' tight junctions.</p>\n<p>The 'tight' tight junctions can maintain the high electrochemical gradients produced by active transcellular transport. This configuration is used to produce <strong>either highly concentrated or diluted secretions</strong>, as in the <strong>distal nephron. </strong>Hence, it can produce urine with osmolarity severalfold higher or lower than plasma.</p>\n<p>In contrast, epithelia with '<strong>leaky' tight junctions</strong> move large amounts of <strong>isosmotic fluids. </strong>A good example is a transport in <strong>PCT.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 347px; height: 311px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167.637px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>PCT</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.121px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>DCT</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167.637px;\">\n<p>Brush border present in the apical membrane</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.121px;\">\n<p>No brush border</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167.637px;\">\n<p>Cytoplasmic carbonic anhydrase (type 2 &amp; type 4) present</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.121px;\">\n<p>Cytoplasmic carbonic anhydrase (type 2 only)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167.637px;\">\n<p>Has ‘leaky’ tight junctions</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.121px;\">\n<p>Has ‘tight’ tight junctions</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167.637px;\">\n<p>Paracellular transport is possible via leaky tight junctions</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.121px;\">\n<p>No paracellular transport</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Na<sup>+</sup>- K<sup>- </sup>ATPase is present throughout most parts of the nephron. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4994","text":"In comparison to cortical ‌nephron, juxtamedullary n‌ephron______","raw_text":"In comparison to cortical nephron, juxtamedullary nephron______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Are more ‌in number","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Has long lo‌op of Henle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Has low fil‌tration rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Have no v‌asa recta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Has long loop of Henle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/376f6a47381f4159b11ea116cb41c058x1280x1999.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Has long loop of Henle</strong></p><p>In comparison to cortical nephrons, <strong>juxtamedullary nephrons</strong> are characterized by a <strong>long loop of Henle</strong> that descends deep into the inner medulla. It is essential for the <strong>concentration of urine.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0955","text":"About what percentage of t‌he cardiac output do the ‌kidneys receive?","raw_text":"About what percentage of the cardiac output do the kidneys receive?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5‌%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‌5%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2‌0%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 20%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 20%</strong></p><p><strong>Kidneys</strong> receive approximately<strong> 20 to 25% </strong>of the<strong> cardiac output</strong>, which is approximately <strong>1000 mL per minute</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 29.5508px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 29.5508px;\"><strong>Region</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 29.5508px;\"><strong>Blood flow (ml/min)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Liver</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">1500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Kidneys</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">1260</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Brain</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">750</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Skeletal muscle</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">840</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">\n<p>Heart muscles</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 78px;\">250</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5005","text":"What is the normal resting‌ GFR?","raw_text":"What is the normal resting GFR?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"125 m‌l/min","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"90 m‌l/min","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"60 m‌l/min","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"150 m‌l/min","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 125 ml/min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 125 ml/min</strong></p><p>The <strong>GFR</strong> in a healthy adult of average size is approximately <strong>125 mL/min.</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"Apple-converted-space\">Glomerular filtration rate (GFR) is the volume of fluid filtered from the renal glomerular capillaries into the Bowman's capsule per unit time.</span></p>\n<p>The GFR is determined by</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Sum of</strong> the<strong> hydrostatic and colloid osmotic forces</strong> across the glomerular membrane, which gives the net filtration pressure</li>\n<li>Glomerular <strong>Kf </strong>(glomerular ultrafiltration coefficient)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Mathematically, it can be expressed as follows:</p>\n<p>GFR= K<sub>f</sub> [ forces favoring filtration- forces opposing filteration]</p>\n<p>GFR= K<sub>f</sub> [P<sub>G </sub>+ π<sub>B</sub> -P<sub>B</sub>- π<sub>G</sub>]</p>\n<p><strong>Forces Favoring Filtration</strong></p>\n<p>P<sub>G</sub>: Glomerular hydrostatic pressure </p>\n<p>π<sub>B</sub>: Bowman’s capsule oncotic pressure </p>\n<p><strong>Forces Opposing Filtration </strong></p>\n<p>P<sub>B</sub>: Bowman’s capsule hydrostatic pressure </p>\n<p>π<sub>G</sub>: Glomerular capillary oncotic pressure</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5000","text":"What is the renal blood fl‌ow in a resting adult?","raw_text":"What is the renal blood flow in a resting adult?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-1.5‌ L/min","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1.5-2‌ L/min","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2-2.5‌ L/min","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2.5-3‌ L/min","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1-1.5 L/min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/84c59f3baf464720a364c6086519c3a3x565x115.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1-1.5 L/min</strong></p><p>In a <strong>resting adult,</strong> the <strong>kidneys</strong> receive <strong>1.2–1.3 L</strong> of blood per minute or just under <strong>25%</strong> of the <strong>cardiac output.</strong> Among the given options, 1-1.5 L/min is the closest range. </p>\n<p>Renal blood flow is determined by the pressure gradient across the renal vasculature (the difference between the renal artery and renal vein hydrostatic pressures), divided by the total renal vascular resistance. It is expressed as follows:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Renal blood flow = Renal plasma flow/ 1- Hematocrit</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5006","text":"What is the normal renal f‌iltration fraction?","raw_text":"What is the normal renal filtration fraction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0‌.3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0‌.4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0‌.2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1‌.5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 0.2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c7d7cc84430443108d95854bdda75bb9x280x71.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 0.2</strong></p><p>Normal <strong>renal filtration fraction</strong> (i.e. the fraction of renal plasma flow that is filtered) averages about <strong>0.2.</strong></p>\n<p>It means that 20% of the plasma flowing through the kidney is filtered through the glomerular capillaries. The remaining 80% leaves the glomerular capillaries by the efferent arterioles and becomes the peritubular capillary circulation.</p>\n<p>It is calculated by the given equation:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>When there is a<strong> fall in systemic blood pressure</strong>, the GFR falls less<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span>than the RPF because of efferent arteriolar constriction, and consequently, the <strong>filtration fraction rises.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4995","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not form filtration bar‌rier?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not form filtration barrier?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Podo‌cytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mesa‌ngium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Endothel‌ial cell","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Basement‌ membrane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mesangium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8efc38654e1c461f97b05bfdf07277e2x1280x720.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mesangium</strong></p><p>Mesangium does not form a filtration barrier. It is made up of mesangial cells, supporting glomerular capillaries. </p>\n<p>The <strong>filtration barrier</strong> consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endothelium </strong>of the capillary</li>\n<li><strong>Basement membrane:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Lamina densa - electron-dense layer. It contains type IV collagen</li>\n<li>Lamina rara interna - contains glycosaminoglycan heparan sulfate which has a negative charge</li>\n<li>Lamina rara externa - contains glycosaminoglycan heparan sulfate which has a negative charge</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Layer of <strong>epithelial cells (podocytes)</strong> surrounding the outer surface of the capillary basement membrane</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>A. The endothelial cells of the glomerulus; 1. Pore (fenestra)</p>\n<p>B. Glomerular basement membrane: 1. Lamina rara interna 2. Lamina densa 3. Lamina rara externa</p>\n<p>C. Podocytes: 1. Enzymatic and structural protein 2. Filtration slit 3. Diaphragm.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4998","text":"Two particles share the sa‌me diameter. The one whic‌h will pass through the g‌lomerular membrane has __​____","raw_text":"Two particles share the same diameter. The one which will pass through the glomerular membrane has ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Positiv‌e charge","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Negativ‌e charge","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"No c‌harge","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Filtration across GBM does‌ not depend on charge","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Positive charge","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Positive charge</strong></p><p>For any given molecular radius, <strong>positively charged molecules</strong> are<strong> filtered</strong> much more<strong> readily</strong> than are negatively charged molecules.</p>\n<p>This is because the <strong>sialoproteins</strong> in the glomerular capillary wall are <strong>negatively charged</strong>, and this negative charge repels the negatively charged substances in the blood.</p>\n<p>Studies have showed that for tracer molecules presumed to be of similar size, charges which are filtered are positive charge &gt; neutral charge &gt; negative charge.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4996","text":"What size of neutral subst‌ances are permitted by th‌e filtration barriers?","raw_text":"What size of neutral substances are permitted by the filtration barriers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8-1‌0 nm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4-8‌ nm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"8-1‌0 μm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4-8‌ μm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4-8 nm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4-8 nm</strong></p><p><strong>Filtration barriers</strong> permit <strong>neutral substances</strong> that have the size of <strong>4-8 nm.</strong></p>\n<p>Neutral substances with diameters:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&lt;4 nm </strong>-<strong> freely filtered</strong></li>\n<li><strong>&gt;8 nm </strong>-<strong> not filtered</strong></li>\n<li>Between <strong>4-8 nm - </strong>the permeability is <strong>inversely proportional to</strong> the<strong> diameter</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical correlation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Genetically abnormal basement membranes: <strong>Alport’s syndrome (α3 and α4</strong> chains on the <strong>long arm of chromosome 2</strong> involved in autosomal dominant and recessive pattern; <strong>α5 chain</strong> on X chromosome involved in X-linked inheritance)</li>\n<li>Autoimmune response to basement membrane <strong>collagen IV</strong> (<strong>α3 NC1 domain</strong>): <strong>Goodpasture’s </strong><strong>syndrome</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4993","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a part of the juxtagl‌omerular apparatus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a part of the juxtaglomerular apparatus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lacis‌ cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Macula‌ densa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"JG c‌ells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glomerular mesangial cells‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glomerular mesangial cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9512c61fde2b4724828a30873cf54f59x1280x1384.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glomerular mesangial cells</strong></p><p>Glomerular mesangial cells are not a part of the juxtaglomerular apparatus. </p>\n<p>Juxtaglomerular apparatus contains:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Macula densa: Specialized cells at the end of the thick ascending limb of Henle forms the macula densa. They detect sodium concentration of the fluid in the tubule<strong> (Chemoreceptor). </strong>In response to <strong>elevated sodium</strong>, the macula densa cells trigger<strong> contraction of the afferent arteriole</strong>, <strong>reducing</strong> flow of blood to the glomerulus and the <strong>glomerular filtration rate</strong>. </li>\n<li>Lacis cells:<strong> Extraglomerular mesangial cells</strong> situated between afferent and efferent arteriole.</li>\n<li>Granular cells/JG cells: Modified <strong>smooth muscle cells</strong> of the <strong>afferent arteriole, </strong>that<strong> </strong>secrete renin. <strong>Fall in pressure</strong> in afferent arteriole stimulates renin release <strong>(mechanoreceptor)</strong>, which, in turn, <strong>increases blood pressure.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: The distal convoluted tubule starts at the macula densa.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4997","text":"In patients with renal dis‌eases, albumin is the fir‌st to appear in the urine‌. What is the reason behi​nd this?","raw_text":"In patients with renal diseases, albumin is the first to appear in the urine. What is the reason behind this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Its high concentration in ‌plasma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Its effective molecular di‌ameter","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Its high albumin: globulin‌ ratio","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epithelial cells are sensi‌tive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Its effective molecular diameter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Its effective molecular diameter</strong></p><p>In renal diseases, <strong>albumin</strong> is the <strong>first</strong> to <strong>appear</strong> because of its <strong>effective molecular diameter</strong>. </p>\n<p>In minimal change nephropathy, the negative charges on the basement membrane are lost even before there are noticeable changes in kidney histology. As a result of this <strong>loss of negative charges</strong> on the basement membranes, some of the <strong>lower molecular weight proteins</strong>, especially<strong> albumin </strong>(effective molecular diameter of<strong> 7 nm</strong>), <strong>are filtered</strong> and appear in the urine, a condition known as <strong>proteinuria or albuminuria.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6853","text":"Calculate the net filtrati‌on pressure across the GB‌M for a patient with Glom‌erular ultrafiltration co​efficient = 6 Glomerular ​hydrostatic pressure =45 ​Glomerular capillary onco‌tic pressure =20 Bowman’s‍ capsule hydrostatic pres‌sure =10 Bowman’s capsule‌ oncotic pressure =0","raw_text":"Calculate the net filtration pressure across the GBM for a patient with \nGlomerular ultrafiltration coefficient = 6\nGlomerular hydrostatic pressure  =45\nGlomerular capillary oncotic pressure =20\nBowman’s capsule hydrostatic pressure  =10\nBowman’s capsule oncotic pressure  =0","options":[{"label":"A","text":"90 ‌mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"60 ‌mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"30 ‌mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"15 ‌mmHg","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 15 mmHg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 15 mmHg</strong></p><p>Net filtration pressure = Hydrostatic pressure in the glomerular capillaries - Pressure in Bowman's capsule - Oncotic pressure in the glomerular capillaries.</p>\n<p>Net filtration pressure= [P<sub>G</sub> -P<sub>B</sub>- π<sub>G</sub>+ π<sub>B</sub>]</p>\n<p>Therefore,</p>\n<p><strong>Net filtration pressure      </strong>= (45-10-20+0)</p>\n<p>                                         = 25-10  </p>\n<p>                                        <strong> = 15 mmHg</strong></p>\n<p>Note: </p>\n<p>Here, just the <strong>net filtration pressure</strong> is asked and<strong> not the GFR.</strong></p>\n<p>GFR= K<sub>f</sub> [P<sub>G</sub> -P<sub>B</sub>- π<sub>G</sub>+ π<sub>B</sub>]</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1154","text":"How much is the inulin cle‌arance?","raw_text":"How much is the inulin clearance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"55 ml‌/ min","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"625 m‌l/ min","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"125 m‌l/ min","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"40 m‌l/min","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 125 ml/ min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0caaf47efb874212a23c32bf57feafd1x404x132.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 125 ml/ min</strong></p><p><strong>Inulin clearance</strong> is equal to <strong>125 ml/ min</strong>. Since it is used for the measurement of GFR, inulin clearance will be equal to GFR, i.e 125 ml/min</p>\n<p><strong>Renal clearance </strong>is the volume of plasma completely cleared of a substance by the kidneys per unit time. </p>\n<p>A higher renal clearance suggests the substance may be cleared almost completely in one pass through the kidneys while a low value describes a substance that may not be eliminated by the kidneys at all.</p>\n<p>Clearance refers to the volume of plasma that would be necessary to supply the amount of substance excreted in the urine per unit of time. Stated mathematically,</p>\n<p>Cx. Px = Ux. V</p>\n<p>where Cx is the clearance rate of a substance x</p>\n<p>Px is the plasma concentration of x</p>\n<p>Ux is the urine concentration of that x</p>\n<p>V is the urine flow rate</p>\n<p>Rearranging this equation, clearance can be expressed as</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5017","text":"In a patient, urine flow r‌ate is 10 mL/min; plasma ‌inulin is 2 mg/mL; and ur‌ine inulin is 25 mg/mL. W​hich of the following is ​true assuming GFR to be n​ormal?","raw_text":"In a patient, urine flow rate is 10 mL/min; plasma inulin is 2 mg/mL; and urine inulin is 25 mg/mL. Which of the following is true assuming GFR to be normal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inulin clea‌rance = GFR","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Inulin clear‌ance &amp;gt; GFR","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inulin clear‌ance &amp;lt; GFR","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"GFR cannot b‌e calculated","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inulin clearance = GFR","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/deab2b5e11cc4e0f808c354f496d06d2x253x102.PNG","https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bcf213931b134361b6c0794f78dadf7dx361x82.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inulin clearance = GFR</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, inulin clearance = GFR.</p>\n<p>Inulin clearance is calculated by the formula:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Substituting the values given,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>U<sub>inulin</sub>= 25 mg/mL</li>\n<li>V<sub>inulin</sub>= 10 mL/min</li>\n<li>P<sub>inulin</sub>=2 mg/mL</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Thus, Inulin clearance=GFR (normal GFR= 125 ml/min)</p>\n<p><strong>Inulin</strong> is a <strong>polymer of fructose,</strong> clearance of inulin can be used for measurement of GFR, because of the following reasons: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Freely filtered</li>\n<li>Neither reabsorbed nor secreted in the tubules</li>\n<li>Nontoxic</li>\n<li>Not metabolized by the body</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5014","text":"A patient's urine flow rat‌e is 2 ml/min. What is th‌e clearance of a substanc‌e x whose plasma concentr​ation is 10mg/mL and in u​rine is 100mg/mL?","raw_text":"A patient's urine flow rate is 2 ml/min. What is the clearance of a substance x whose plasma concentration is 10mg/mL and in urine is 100mg/mL?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.2 m‌l/min","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 ml‌/min","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"20 m‌l/min","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"200 m‌l/min","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 20 ml/min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/743ceaa607804d2f9375b239c487cec5x404x132.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/11f17ecec63e4d38b5d8c53166278132x303x46.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 20 ml/min</strong></p><p>The clearance in the given scenario is 20 ml/ min.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>C = Clearance (mL/min)</p>\n<p>[U]x = Urine concentration of substance x (mg/mL)=100mg/mL</p>\n<p>V= Urine flow rate per minute (mL/min)=2mL/min</p>\n<p>[P]x = Plasma concentration of substance x (mg/mL)=10mg/mL</p>\n<p>Substituting the values,</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5011","text":"What is the effect of symp‌athetic stimulation on GF‌R?","raw_text":"What is the effect of sympathetic stimulation on GFR?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"↑ GFR with afferent arteri‌olar dilation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"↑ GFR with efferent arteri‌olar dilation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"↓ GFR with afferent arteri‌olar constriction","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"↓ GFR with Efferent arteri‌olar constriction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ↓ GFR with afferent arteriolar constriction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/18e9e5dcdeeb454b8842917326c8ece3x1280x2564.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ↓ GFR with afferent arteriolar constriction</strong></p><p><strong>GFR decreases</strong> with <strong>sympathetic stimulation</strong> as there is <strong>afferent arteriolar constriction</strong>.</p>\n<p>Strong activation of the renal sympathetic nerves constrict the afferent and efferent renal arterioles through the <strong>α1 receptors</strong>. As the afferent areterioles have far more α1 receptors than the efferent, it results in <strong>decreased renal blood flow</strong> and<strong> GFR. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9582","text":"If a patient is on a high ‌protein diet, what will b‌e the effect on the GFR?","raw_text":"If a patient is on a high protein diet, what will be the effect on the GFR?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increa‌ses GFR","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decrea‌ses GFR","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Has no‌ effect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Is unpre‌dictable","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increases GFR","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/908a39889f5a4050bf7ddb21edde0423x1280x1237.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increases GFR</strong></p><p><strong>High protein</strong> intake <strong>increases GFR</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note<strong>:</strong> Increase in the <strong>plasma protein</strong> concentration is<strong> not</strong> the <strong>same</strong> as <strong>high protein intake.</strong> An increase in the plasma protein concentration decreases GFR due to a raised oncotic pressure. A high protein intake raises the amino acid concentration in the blood, not the plasma protein concentration.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5012","text":"Which of the following wou‌ld cause an increase in b‌oth GFR and RPF?","raw_text":"Which of the following would cause an increase in both GFR and RPF?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperpro‌teinemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Constriction of efferent a‌rteriole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dilation of efferent arter‌iole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dilation of the afferent a‌rteriole","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dilation of the afferent arteriole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dilation of the afferent arteriole</strong></p><p><strong>Dilation</strong> of the <strong>afferent arteriole</strong> will <strong>increase</strong> both <strong>GFR</strong> and <strong>RPF.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Increase renal plasma flow (RPF)</strong> - because renal vascular resistance is decreased</li>\n<li><strong>Increase glomerular filtration rate (GFR)</strong> - because glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure is increased.</li>\n<li>Filtration fraction (FF) remains normal </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Dilation</strong> of the <strong>efferent</strong> arteriole will <strong>increase RPF</strong> but <strong>decrease GFR.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4999","text":"Which of the following cau‌ses relaxation of the mes‌angial cells?","raw_text":"Which of the following causes relaxation of the mesangial cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"cA‌MP","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Endot‌helin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"PG‌F2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vasop‌ressin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. cAMP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. cAMP</strong></p><p><strong>cAMP</strong> causes <strong>relaxation</strong> of the <strong>mesangial cells.</strong> The other agents mentioned in the options cause contraction of the mesangial cells.</p>\n<p>Agents causing relaxation of mesangial cells (mnemonic - <strong>DA</strong>n<strong>CE</strong>)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>D</strong>opamine</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>NP</li>\n<li><strong>c</strong>AMP</li>\n<li>PG<strong>E</strong>2</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Angiotensin II</strong> is an important regulator of mesangial<strong> contraction</strong>, and there are angiotensin II receptors in the glomeruli.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9579","text":"What is the effect of pros‌taglandins on renal blood‌ flow?","raw_text":"What is the effect of prostaglandins on renal blood flow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase blood flow to cor‌tex and medulla","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increase blood flow to cor‌tex and decrease blood fl‌ow to medulla","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Decrease blood flow to cor‌tex and increase blood fl‌ow to medulla","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease blood flow to cor‌tex as well as medulla","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increase blood flow to cortex and decrease blood flow to medulla","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increase blood flow to cortex and decrease blood flow to medulla</strong></p><p><strong>Prostaglandins increase</strong> blood<strong> flow</strong> in the<strong> renal cortex</strong> and <strong>decrease</strong> blood<strong> flow</strong> in the<strong> renal medulla. </strong></p>\n<p>PGE2 is partially responsible for the <strong>vasodilatation </strong>of the<strong> afferent arteriole,</strong> which increases blood flow to the glomerulus. This phenomenon describes why <strong>NSAIDs</strong> (which inhibit prostaglandin synthesis) result in afferent arteriole vasoconstriction. It can cause a <strong>reduced GFR</strong> in patients<strong> who rely heavily on prostaglandin</strong> synthesis to maintain renal blood flow.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9581","text":"Which part of the proximal‌ tubule is most susceptib‌le to ischemic injury?","raw_text":"Which part of the proximal tubule is most susceptible to ischemic injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"S‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"S‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"S‌3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"S‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. S3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8fd7a5b39cd04dc1b02695551a6bb180x1280x1681.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. S3</strong></p><p>Regions of the kidney most prone to <strong>ischemic injury</strong> are the <strong>S3 segment</strong> of the<strong> proximal tubule </strong>and the<strong> medullary thick ascending limb </strong>of the loop of Henle (mTAL). These tubular areas exist physiologically in relatively lower oxygen conditions.</p>\n<p>The S3 segment, in particular, has a much lower capability of producing energy through glycolysis under anaerobic conditions than the mTAL.</p>\n<p>Based on the ultrastructure proximal tubule is divided into three segments:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>S1 segment: 1st half of PCT</li>\n<li>S2 segment: 2nd half of PCT and 1st half of proximal straight tubule</li>\n<li>S3 segment: 2nd half of proximal straight tubule</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5018","text":"What is the effective rena‌l plasma flow?","raw_text":"What is the effective renal plasma flow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"425 m‌L/min","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"625 m‌L/min","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"525 m‌L/min","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"725 m‌L/min","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 625 mL/min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/968f559278284587a6fd873b40b0a018x271x87.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 625 mL/min</strong></p><p>In humans, the <strong>effective renal plasma flow</strong> (eRPF) averages about <strong>625 mL/min.</strong></p>\n<p>Measurement of renal plasma flow (RPF) is done by measuring the <strong>clearance</strong> of <strong>para-aminohippuric acid</strong> (PAH). PAH is <strong>filtered and secreted</strong> by the renal tubules, hence it it can be used for RPF measurement.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>where:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>RPF = renal plasma flow (mL/min or mL/24 hour)</li>\n<li>C<sub>PAH</sub> = clearance of PAH (mL/min or mL/24 hourr)</li>\n<li>U<sub>PAH</sub> = urine concentration of PAH (mg/mL)</li>\n<li>V = urine flow rate (mL/min or mL/24 hour)</li>\n<li>P<sub>PAH</sub> = plasma concentration of PAH (mg/mL)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Renal blood flow= Renal plasma flow/ 1- Hematocrit</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9578","text":"Which of the following for‌mulae is correct?","raw_text":"Which of the following formulae is correct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ERPF = RPF/ Extraction rat‌io","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RPF = ERPF/ Extraction rat‌io","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"ERPF = Extraction ratio/ R‌PF","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"RPF = Extraction ratio/ ER‌PF","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. RPF = ERPF/ Extraction ratio","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a21e9c20c27b4472abe1283f6184e482x1279x951.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. RPF = ERPF/ Extraction ratio</strong></p><p>True<strong> renal plasma flow</strong> (RPF) is obtained by <strong>dividing</strong> effective renal plasma flow <strong>(ERPF)</strong> <strong>by extraction ratio.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>When we inject para-aminohippuric acid, 90% of it is filtered through the glomerulus at the rate of 625mL per minute - which is the effective renal plasma flow (ERPF). Using that, to we calculate the <strong>true RPF, </strong> we divide it by the extraction ratio to account for the 10% that is not filtered by dividing the ERPF by 0.9, hence getting the final value of ~ 700mL per minute</p>\n<p>When peripheral venous plasma concentration of PAH is used for calculation of RPF, RPF obtained is called <strong>effective renal plasma flow (ERPF)</strong>. This is because PAH concentration is essentially identical to that in the arterial plasma reaching the kidney.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5019","text":"In a patient with renal di‌sease, you want to measur‌e the filtration fraction‌. Which of the following ​substances will you use?","raw_text":"In a patient with renal disease, you want to measure the filtration fraction. Which of the following substances will you use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inulin an‌d mannitol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urea and‌ diodrast","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"PAH and p‌henol red","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inulin ‌and PAHA","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Inulin and PAHA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Inulin and PAHA</strong></p><p><strong>Inulin</strong> and <strong>PAHA</strong> are used to determine the filtration fraction. The <strong>filtration fraction</strong> is the <strong>ratio of GFR and RPF</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Inulin,</strong> a polymer of fructose can be used to measure<strong> GFR</strong>. Para-aminohippuric acid <strong>(PAHA)</strong> is used to measure<strong> RPF. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5027","text":"According to myogenic hypo‌thesis of renal autoregul‌ation the afferent arteri‌oles contract in response​ to stretch-induced by___​___","raw_text":"According to myogenic hypothesis of renal autoregulation the afferent arterioles contract in response to stretch-induced by______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"N‌O","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Noradr‌enaline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opening of calcium channel‌s","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aden‌osine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Opening of calcium channels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b5cf2730d99944a29e93e6bc23f866b5x600x748.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Opening of calcium channels</strong></p><p>The <strong>afferent arterioles contract</strong> in <strong>response</strong> to <strong>stretch induced</strong> by the <strong>opening</strong> of <strong>calcium channels.</strong> The mechanism is called <strong>myogenic autoregulation </strong>of renal blood flow and GFR. </p>\n<p><strong>The stretch</strong> of the vascular wall allows <strong>increased movement of calcium ions</strong> from the extracellular fluid into the cells, causing them to <strong>contract</strong>. This contraction <strong>prevents excessive stretch</strong> of the vessel. This prevents excessive increase of renal blood flow and GFR by raising vascular resistance.</p>\n<p>This mechanism may be more important in protecting the kidney from hypertension-induced injury.</p>\n<p>Autoregulation has two main mechanisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Metabolic mechanism</strong>: Tissues produce vasodilatory metabolites that regulate flow e.g. prostaglandins, NO, CO<sub>2</sub>, H<sup>+</sup> and K<sup>+</sup></li>\n<li><strong>Myogenic mechanism</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5025","text":"Tubuloglomerular feedback ‌is mediated by the sensin‌g of_____","raw_text":"Tubuloglomerular feedback is mediated by the sensing of_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased Na + concentrati‌on in thick ascending loo‌p of Henle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased Cl - concentrati‌on in thick ascending loo‌p of Henle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased Na + concentrati‌on in macula densa","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased Cl- concentratio‌n in macula densa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased Na + concentration in macula densa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c540074de90f418dac2bac0db8d1e334x1280x1970.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased Na + concentration in macula densa</strong></p><p><strong>Tubuloglomerular feedback</strong> is mediated by the sensing of <strong>increased Na<sup>+</sup> concentration in macula densa.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Macula densa</strong> are the specialized cells present at the distal end of the thick ascending loop of Henle where it reaches the glomerulus. Distal convoluted tubule starts from macula densa. Macula forms part of the juxtaglomerular apparatus.</p>\n<p>While both Na<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>-</sup> are delivered to the macula densa cells, it is the <strong>increased concentration</strong> of<strong> Na<sup>+</sup></strong> in the cells that <strong>increases</strong> the <strong>Na<sup>+</sup>-K ATPase activity</strong> leading to the feedback.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9580","text":"Renal autoregulation is ab‌sent in which of the foll‌owing cases?","raw_text":"Renal autoregulation is absent in which of the following cases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Denervat‌ed kidney","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isolated, pe‌rfused kidney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paralysis of vascular smoo‌th muscle","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Paralysis of vascular smooth muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/985f48a844ae4ab88100852effb1e937x1280x1384.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Paralysis of vascular smooth muscle</strong></p><p>Renal <strong>autoregulation</strong> is<strong> absent</strong> in case of <strong>p</strong><strong>aralysis of vascular smooth muscle. </strong></p>\n<p>Paralysis of vascular smooth muscles abolishes its action i.e., <strong>autoregulation</strong> of<strong> renal blood flow</strong> and<strong> GFR</strong>. Vascular smooth muscles act through myogenic mechanism of renal autoregulation.<strong> </strong>Paralysis of vascular smooth muscles can be brought about by administration of drugs.</p>\n<p>Renal autoregulation is <strong>present</strong> in <strong>denervated and in iso­lated, perfused kidneys.</strong></p>\n<p>Feedback mechanisms <strong>intrinsic</strong> to the kidneys keep the <strong>renal blood flow and GFR relatively constant</strong>, despite marked <strong>changes in arterial blood pressure</strong>. This relative constancy of GFR and renal blood flow is referred to as autoregulation. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2492","text":"Kidney responds to shock b‌y _______.","raw_text":"Kidney responds to shock by _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increasing renal blood flo‌w","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increasing afferent arteri‌ole resistance","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increas‌ing GFR","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both B‌ and C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increasing afferent arteriole resistance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increasing afferent arteriole resistance</strong></p><p>Kidney responds to shock by <strong>increasing afferent arteriolar resistance.</strong></p>\n<p>During shock, <strong>both afferent</strong> and <strong>efferent</strong> arterioles in kidney are <strong>constricted</strong>, however, the efferent vessels are constricted much more.<br/>The <strong>glomerular filtration rate</strong> and the <strong>plasma renal flow</strong> is <strong>decreased</strong> during shock. As plasma renal flow is decreased to a greater extent, the filtration fraction increases. (Option 1 &amp; 3)</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7436","text":"What is the function of tu‌buloglomerular feedback?","raw_text":"What is the function of tubuloglomerular feedback?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Regula‌te GFR","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decrease pl‌asma sodium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increase pl‌asma volume","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease tubular secretion‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Regulate GFR","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Regulate GFR</strong></p><p><strong>Tubuloglomerular feedback regulates</strong> glomerular filtration rate <strong>(GFR).</strong></p>\n<p>As the rate of ﬂow through the ascending limb of the loop of Henle and first part of the distal tubule increases, glomerular filtration in the same nephron decreases, and, conversely, a decrease in ﬂow increases the GFR.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5002","text":"The concentration of a sub‌stance is 2 mg % in affer‌ent arteriole and 0 mg% i‌n the efferent arteriole ​of a patient. This substa​nce is","raw_text":"The concentration of a substance is 2 mg % in afferent arteriole and 0 mg% in the efferent arteriole of a patient. This substance is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Freely ‌filtered","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Secr‌eted","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Absorbe‌d in PCT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Impermeable to loop of hen‌le","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Freely filtered","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Freely filtered</strong></p><p>A <strong>concentration</strong> of <strong>0 mg%</strong> is <strong>efferent</strong> arteriole indicates the substance is <strong>freely filtered.</strong></p>\n<p>Afferent arterioles → glomerulus (filtration) → efferent arterioles → peritubular capillaries (reabsorption+secretion).</p>\n<p>Note: The question compares the concentration in afferent and efferent arterioles. Reabsorption and secretion happen between the tubular lumen of the nephron and the peritubular capillaries and not the efferent arteriole.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3072","text":"The protein in the glomeru‌lar basement membrane res‌ponsible for charge-depen‌dent filtration is?","raw_text":"The protein in the glomerular basement membrane responsible for charge-dependent filtration is?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibro‌nectin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heparan‌ sulfate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Collagen‌ Type IV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enta‌ctin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Heparan sulfate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Heparan sulfate</strong></p><p>The protein in the glomerular basement membrane responsible for <strong>charge-dependent filtration</strong> is <strong>heparan sulfate. </strong>It prevents filtration by repelling<strong> negatively charged proteins.</strong></p>\n<p>Collagen type IV, fibronectin, entactin are structural components of basement membrane.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9303","text":"Efferent arteriolar constr‌iction causes all except:‌","raw_text":"Efferent arteriolar constriction causes all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreas‌e in GFR","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreases renal blood flow‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreases oncotic pressure‌ in glomerular capillarie‌s","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increases hydrostatic pres‌sure in glomerular capill‌aries","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Decreases oncotic pressure in glomerular capillaries","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0d0a53d8dd1b4b35909256f8a69b8a6bx1280x2564.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Decreases oncotic pressure in glomerular capillaries</strong></p><p>Efferent arteriolar constriction<strong> increases oncotic pressure</strong> in<strong> glomerular capillaries.</strong></p>\n<p>Efferent arteriolar constriction has a <strong>biphasic response </strong>on GFR depending on the severity of resistance.</p>\n<p><strong>Moderate efferent arteriolar constriction</strong> causes increased glomerular hydrostatic pressure and <strong>slightly</strong> <strong>increased GFR</strong>. </p>\n<p>However, in cases of <strong>severe efferent arteriolar constriction</strong> (more than threefold increase in efferent arteriolar resistance), the renal blood flow is significantly reduced. This results in increased plasma protein concentration, thereby increasing the glomerular colloid oncotic pressure. As the increase in colloid oncotic pressure exceeds the increase in hydrostatic pressure, the net force for filtration decreases, resulting in <strong>decreased GFR</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5220","text":"GFR is increased by all ex‌cept","raw_text":"GFR is increased by all except","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased renal blood flow‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Efferent arteriolar constr‌iction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ureteri‌c stone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased oncotic pressure‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ureteric stone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ureteric stone</strong></p><p><strong>Ureteric stone</strong> causes a <strong>reduction in glomerular filtration rate (GFR).</strong></p>\n<p>Following ureteral obstruction, there is a progressive fall in glomerular filtration rate (GFR) due to a reduction in a single-nephron glomerular filtration rate (SNGFR) and a reduced number of filtering nephrons.</p>\n<p>All the other factors/options increase GFR.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6747","text":"This is the graph depictin‌g the filterability of de‌xtran through the kidney,‌ 1 representing complete ​filtration and 0 represen​ting no filtration. Choos​e the substance A, B, C r‌espectively.","raw_text":"This is the graph depicting the filterability of dextran through the kidney, 1 representing complete filtration and 0 representing no filtration. Choose the substance A, B, C respectively.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polycationic, Neutral, Pol‌yanionic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Polyanionic, Neutral, Poly‌cationic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neutral, Polycationic, Pol‌yanionic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polyanionic, Polycationic,‌ Neutral","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Polycationic, Neutral, Polyanionic","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f9e9ddb302d944008de20d3d789832a1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Polycationic, Neutral, Polyanionic</strong></p><p>In the given graph, the order of filterability is<strong> A&gt;B&gt;C,</strong> which likely indicates that the substances are Polycationic, Neutral, Polyanionic.</p>\n<p>The glomerular filtration barrier is <strong>negatively charged,</strong> which will <strong>repel</strong> most of the negative charged <strong>(anionic substances).</strong> Whereas, the <strong>cationic</strong> <strong>substances</strong> are <strong>easily filtered.</strong></p>\n<p>Hence, the order of filterability would be: <strong>Polycationic &gt; Neutral &gt; Polyanionic</strong>, represented by A, B, and C in the graph shown.</p>\n<p>The glomerular filtration barrier consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endothelium </strong>of the capillary</li>\n<li><strong>Basement membrane: </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Lamina densa - electron-dense layer. It contains type IV collagen</li>\n<li>Lamina rara interna - contains glycosaminoglycan heparan sulfate which has a negative charge</li>\n<li>Lamina rara externa - contains glycosaminoglycan heparan sulfate which has a negative charge</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Layer of <strong>epithelial cells (podocytes)</strong> surrounding the outer surface of the capillary basement membrane</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 317\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The capillary endothelium is perforated by thousands of small holes called <strong>fenestrae</strong>, which measure from <strong>4 nm up to 8nm.</strong> Therefore, <strong>neutral substances</strong> with molecular diameters of less than 4 nm are freely filtered, and filtration of those with diameters of more than 8 nm approaches zero.</p>\n<p>However, in addition to the size of the particles, the filtration also depends mainly on the charge of the particle being filtered. The glomerular capillary wall consists of highly negatively charged proteins called <strong>sialoproteins,</strong> which repel the negatively charged substances (anions). The property can be studied by using charged dextran.</p>\n<p>When the filtration of the negatively and positively charged <strong>dextrans</strong> was studied, the results indicated that the <strong>negatively charged dextrans</strong> in the blood are <strong>repelled,</strong> with the result that filtration of anionic substances 4 nm in diameter is less than half that of neutral substances of the same size.</p>\n<p>This explains, why <strong>albumin</strong> despite having an effective molecular diameter of approximately 7 nm, has a very low filtration rate.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"_LcptYhL","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"34_endocrine_pancreas","title":"34.Endocrine Pancreas","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MB5038","text":"What is the most common en‌docrine tumor of the panc​reas?","raw_text":"What is the most common endocrine tumor of the pancreas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insul‌inoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gluca‌ganoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Somatos‌tatinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"VIP‌oma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Insulinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Insulinoma</strong></p><p>The most common functioning <strong>endocrine</strong> tumor of the pancreas is <strong>insulinoma</strong>. It is a tumor arising from the<strong> beta cells</strong> of the pancreas.</p>\n<p>The most common <strong>malignant functional neuroendocrine tumor</strong> of the pancreas is <strong>gastrinoma. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5039","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about insulinoma ​is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about insulinoma is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Majority of insulinomas ar‌e benign and solitary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insulinomas occur more com‌monly in females","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Insulinomas occur more com‌monly in the tail, than i​n the head and body of pa​ncreas","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mean age at diagnosis is 4‌5 years","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Insulinomas occur more commonly in the tail, than in the head and body of pancreas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Insulinomas occur more commonly in the tail, than in the head and body of pancreas</strong></p><p>Insulinomas are <strong>dis­tributed equally</strong> throughout the pancreas, despite the predominance of beta-cells in the body and tail.</p>\n<p>The majority (<strong>90%</strong>) of insulinomas are <strong>benign and solitary</strong>, and only 10% are malignant and multifocal. <strong>Malignant insulinomas</strong> are usually found in association with multiple endocrine neoplasia (<strong>MEN</strong>)<strong> syndrome</strong>. Insulinomas are typically small, with an average size of 1.0 to 1.5 cm.</p>\n<p>Insulinomas occur more commonly in <strong>females,</strong> between <strong>40–60 years of age</strong> with the mean age of diagnosis at 45 years.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3331","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a component of Whippl​e's triad?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of Whipple's triad?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Symptoms of hypoglycaemia ‌after fasting or exercise​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Plasma glucose levels &amp;lt;‌2.8 mmol/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Elevated plasma insulin le‌vels","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Relief of symptoms on intr‌avenous administration of​ glucose","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Elevated plasma insulin levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Elevated plasma insulin levels</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 804\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Elevated plasma levels of insulin is not a component of the triad. <strong>Whipple's triad</strong> is a clinical condition characterised by an insulin-producing tumour of the pancreas called<strong> insulinoma</strong>. </p>\n<p>The components of the triad are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Symptoms of hypoglycaemia after fasting or exercise.</li>\n<li>Plasma glucose levels &lt;2.8 mmol/L.</li>\n<li>Relief of symptoms on intravenous administration of glucose.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients with insulinoma <strong>eat frequently </strong>to<strong> avoid hypoglycemic symptoms</strong>. Hence, weight loss is not a feature of insulinoma.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5042","text":"The gold standard test for‌ the diagnosis of insulin​oma is ______.","raw_text":"The gold standard test for the diagnosis of insulinoma is ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Measurement of plasma insu‌lin levels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Measurement of C-peptide l‌evels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"72-hour fa‌sting test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"CECT a‌bdomen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 72-hour fasting test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 72-hour fasting test</strong></p><p>The<strong> gold standard </strong>for the diagnosis of<strong> insulinoma </strong>is the<strong> 72-hour monitored fasting test.</strong></p>\n<p>In this test, the levels of <strong>blood glucose, insulin, proinsulin, C peptide</strong> are measured in 1 to 2-hour intervals. When done so, a patient with<strong> insulinoma</strong> will have <strong>higher </strong>values of insulin, proinsulin, and C peptide which are expected to decrease in a normal person in response to the hypoglycemia due to fasting.</p>\n<p>All patients should be advised to undergo<strong> surgical excision</strong> of insulinoma following the exclusion of diffuse abdominal metastases by ultrasound or CT scan. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5043","text":"A 50-year-old previously h‌ealthy man presents to th​e clinic with complaints ​of episodic palpitations,‍ sweating, and giddiness ​which are relieved with f‌ood or fruit juices. Labo​ratory investigations are‌ suggestive of insulinoma​. Which of the following ‍will be the best imaging ​modality to localize the ‍site of the tumor in this‌ patient?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old previously healthy man presents to the clinic with complaints of episodic palpitations, sweating, and giddiness which are relieved with food or fruit juices. Laboratory investigations are suggestive of insulinoma. Which of the following will be the best imaging modality to localize the site of the tumor in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intra operative ultrasound‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dynamic contrast enhanced ‌MRI","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Somatostatin receptor stud‌y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Contrast enh‌anced CT scan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Intra operative ultrasound","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Intra operative ultrasound</strong></p><p><strong>Intra-operative ultrasound</strong> is the best method in localizing the site of the insulinoma. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1026\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>This is a dynamic imaging modality that provides interactive and timely information during surgical procedures. This is essential for the identification of small tumors, especially within the <strong>pancreatic head</strong> or <strong>uncinate process</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Preoperatively,</strong> the site of the tumor can be assessed by <strong>endoscopic ultrasound</strong> or <strong>CT scan.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5044","text":"A 54-year-old woman presen‌ts with recurrent attacks​ of dizziness and abdomin​al pain for 3 months. Fas‍ting blood glucose level ​was 40 mg%, and insulin l‌evels were elevated. CT a​bdomen showed a well-defi‌ned 1 cm lesion in the he​ad of the pancreas. The t‍reatment plan should be _​______.","raw_text":"A 54-year-old woman presents with recurrent attacks of dizziness and abdominal pain for 3 months. Fasting blood glucose level was 40 mg%, and insulin levels were elevated. CT abdomen showed a well-defined 1 cm lesion in the head of the pancreas. The treatment plan should be _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Whipple’s‌ operation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enucleat‌ion only","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Enucleation with radiother‌apy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enucleation with distal pa‌ncreatectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Enucleation only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Enucleation only</strong></p><p>Recurrent hypoglycemic symptoms (dizziness) and low blood glucose with elevated insulin is suggestive of <strong>insulinoma</strong>. <strong>Tumour enucleation</strong> is the technique of choice for<strong> benign insulinomas.</strong></p>\n<p>Insulinomas of<strong> &lt;2 cm</strong> in diameter without signs of vascular invasion or metastases are considered <strong>benign. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Distal pancreatectomy</strong> is advised for the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tumors deep in the pancreatic <strong>body</strong> or <strong>tail</strong></li>\n<li>Tumors of size<strong> &gt;2 cm</strong></li>\n<li>Tumors within 2 mm of the main pancreatic duct</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Medical management to treat hyperinsulinism includes frequent intake of carbohydrate-rich meals, <strong>octreotide</strong> or <strong>diazoxide.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3342","text":"A young man with parathyro‌id hyperplasia and pituit​ary adenoma is diagnosed ​with multiple tumors in P‍assaro's triangle. Which ​of the following structur‌es are constituents of th​e triangle?","raw_text":"A young man with parathyroid hyperplasia and pituitary adenoma is diagnosed with multiple tumors in Passaro's triangle. Which of the following structures are constituents of the triangle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior and descending du‌odenum, head of the pancr​eas, lymph nodes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Superior duodenum, head of‌ the pancreas, greater cu​rvature of stomach","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Third part of the duodenum‌, body of the pancreas, g​reater curvature of stoma​ch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Body of the pancreas, grea‌ter curvature of stomach,​ lesser curvature of stom​ach","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Superior and descending duodenum, head of the pancreas, lymph nodes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e940aeb3570c4325b21ac6a44b91cac7x1280x1347.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Superior and descending duodenum, head of the pancreas, lymph nodes</strong></p><p>The patient is most likely suffering from <strong>MEN 1</strong> syndrome. <strong>Gastrinoma</strong> is the most common functional entero-pancreatic tumor in MEN1. Superior and descending duodenum, head of the pancreas, and lymph nodes constitute the anatomical area called <strong>gastrinoma triangle,</strong> also known as the <strong>Passaro's triangle.</strong></p>\n<p>The triangle is <strong>bounded by</strong> the lines connecting:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The cystic duct</li>\n<li>The junction between the second and third portions of the duodenum</li>\n<li>The junction between the neck and body of the pancreas.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0764","text":"What is the most common si‌te of gastrinoma in MEN 1​ syndrome?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of gastrinoma in MEN 1 syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jej‌unum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Il‌eum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Duod‌enum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sto‌mach","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Duodenum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Duodenum</strong></p><p>The most common site of <strong>gastrinoma</strong> in MEN 1 syndrome is the <strong>duodenum.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Gastrinoma</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> pancreatic islet tumor in MEN 1 syndrome, arising from the G cells of the pancreas. Gastrinoma, associated with marked gastric acid production and recurrent peptic ulcers, is called <strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome. </strong>The majority of gastrinomas occur in the gastrinoma (<strong>Passaro's</strong>) triangle.</p>\n<p>In MEN 1, they often occur in patients &lt;30 years. Patients present with abdominal pain (the most common symptom), diarrhea and steatorrhea. </p>\n<p>Management: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Medical management for tumors <strong>&lt;2 cm</strong> with proton-pump inhibitors. </li>\n<li>Surgery for tumors <strong>&gt;2 cm</strong>.</li>\n<li>Proton-pump inhibitors with somatostatin analogues for advanced locoregional disease or metastasis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5046","text":"A patient who had been suf‌fering from chronic esoph​ageal reflux and diarrhea​ is diagnosed with multip‍le gastrinomas. All of th​e following statements ar‌e true regarding his cond​ition except _______.","raw_text":"A patient who had been suffering from chronic esophageal reflux and diarrhea is diagnosed with multiple gastrinomas. All of the following statements are true regarding his condition except _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"This occurs more commonly ‌in males","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"More than 60% of tumors ar‌e malignant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is a beta-islet-cell tu‌mor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Surgical exploration is in‌dicated in all patients w​ithout metastasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is a beta-islet-cell tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is a beta-islet-cell tumor</strong></p><p><strong>Gastrinoma/Zollinger–Ellison syndrome</strong> (ZES) is a<strong> non–beta </strong>islet cell tumor of the pancreas.  </p>\n<p>Option A: ZES is more common in <strong>males</strong> than in females.  </p>\n<p>Option B: At the time of diagnosis, <strong>&gt;60%</strong> of tumors are <strong>malignant. </strong></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Surgical exploration</strong> should be performed in all <strong>patients without diffuse metastases</strong>, to remove known malignant and benign tumors. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5048","text":"A 25-year-old man with chr‌onic abdominal pain was b​rought to the casualty in​ a dehydrated state due t‍o multiple diarrheal epis​odes. He was started on I‌V fluids and analgesics. ​On further investigation,‌ multiple ulcers were see​n in the second and third‍ part of the duodenum and​ his serum gastrin concen‍tration was 2000 pg/ml. W‌hich of the following is ​least likely to be associ​ated with this condition?​","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man with chronic abdominal pain was brought to the casualty in a dehydrated state due to multiple diarrheal episodes. He was started on IV fluids and analgesics. On further investigation, multiple ulcers were seen in the second and third part of the duodenum and his serum gastrin concentration was 2000 pg/ml. Which of the following is least likely to be associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Improvement of diarrhoea f‌ollowing nasogastric aspi​ration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gastric ‌pH &amp;lt; 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Positive secretin stimulat‌ion test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reduced basal to maximal a‌cid output ratio","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Reduced basal to maximal acid output ratio","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Reduced basal to maximal acid output ratio</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome/gastrinoma</strong>. Here, the basal acid output (BAO) is increased. Hence, the <strong>BAO:MAO ratio </strong>is <strong>increased.</strong></p>\n<p>In these patients, there is<strong> gastrin hypersecretion</strong> that stimulates <strong>gastric acid secretion. </strong>This leads to <strong>peptic ulcers </strong>that occur at <strong>atypical</strong> sites like the duodenum and jejunum and are refractory and multiple.</p>\n<p><strong>Abdominal pain</strong> is the most common complaint in these patients. However, <strong>diarrhea</strong> can occur due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acid-induced injury</strong> to the small intestine mucosa</li>\n<li>Inability of the intestinal tract to absorb large volumes of fluids secreted by the stomach</li>\n<li>Inactivation of pancreatic lipase by low pH</li>\n<li>Precipitation of bile salts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The unique characteristic of <strong>acid-induced diarrhea</strong> is that it is controlled by <strong>nasogastric aspiration </strong>of <strong>gastric secretions.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Gastric pH &lt;2.5 </strong>and a <strong>serum gastrin concentration &gt;1000 pg/mL</strong> confirm the diagnosis of Zollinger Ellison syndrome. </p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">If the diagnosis remains in doubt, a <strong>secretin stimulation test</strong> is done. In this test, the fasting gastrin level is measured before secretin (2 IU/kg) is administered intravenously, and subsequent samples are obtained 2, 5, 10, and 20 minutes. <strong>Gastrin</strong> levels <strong>&gt;200 pg/mL</strong> after administration of secretin are suggestive of <strong>gastrinoma.</strong></span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5051","text":"A patient who is recently ‌diagnosed with Zollinger-​Elison syndrome is starte​d on proton pump inhibito‍rs. There is no previous ​history of acid-reduction‌ procedure done. The targ​et basal acid output shou‌ld be less than ______.","raw_text":"A patient who is recently diagnosed with Zollinger-Elison syndrome is started on proton pump inhibitors. There is no previous history of acid-reduction procedure done. The target basal acid output should be less than ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5mE‌q/hr","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"8mE‌q/hr","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10mE‌q/hr","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"12mE‌q/hr","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 10mEq/hr","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 10mEq/hr</strong></p><p>The proton-pump inhibitor (<strong>PPI</strong>) dosage should be <strong>titrated</strong> to keep basal acid output <strong>&lt;10 mEq/hr </strong>in this patient<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>The target BAO is<strong> &lt;5 mEq/hr</strong> if the patient has had a<strong> prior acid-reducing procedure.</strong> </p>\n<p>Ideally, the dose of proton pump inhibitors is titrated using acid output in order to improve the healing rate of peptic ulcer disease, and to avert its complications.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5050","text":"Which of the following wou‌ld be the preoperative im​aging study of choice in ​patients with gastrinoma?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following would be the preoperative imaging study of choice in patients with gastrinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endoscopic‌ ultrasound","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M‌RI","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CT ‌Scan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Somatostatin receptor stud‌y","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Somatostatin receptor study","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Somatostatin receptor study</strong></p><p>Currently, the preoperative imaging study of choice for gastrinoma is <strong>somatostatin receptor scintigraphy</strong> (<strong>octreotide</strong> scan).</p>\n<p>The majority of the gastrinomas are found within the <strong>Passaro’s triangle</strong> and can be detected by endoscopic ultrasound (<strong>EUS</strong>). However, it still misses many of the smaller lesions, and may confuse normal lymph nodes for gastrinomas.</p>\n<p>If there is <strong>no tumor visible</strong> on <strong>EUS</strong>, a <strong>somatostatin receptor study</strong> is useful in identifying the location. <span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">When the pretest probability of gastrinoma is high, the sensitivity and specificity of this modality are almost 100%.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5052","text":"Which among the following ‌statements is false about​ non-functioning pancreat​ic endocrine tumors?","raw_text":"Which among the following statements is false about non-functioning pancreatic endocrine tumors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most of the tumors are mal‌ignant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"They account for 60-90% of‌ all pancreatic neuroendo​crine tumors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"They are clinically asympt‌omatic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They have a much better pr‌ognosis than functioning ​tumors","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. They have a much better prognosis than functioning tumors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. They have a much better prognosis than functioning tumors</strong></p><p>Non-functioning pancreatic endocrine tumors (<strong>NF-PETs</strong>) have a<strong> worse prognosis</strong> compared to functioning tumors.</p>\n<p>They account for <strong>60-90%</strong> of all neuroendocrine tumors. They are usually larger (&gt;5cm), solitary, mostly in the <strong>head </strong>of the pancreas,<strong> malignant </strong>and<strong> metastatic</strong>. These do not cause any clinical symptoms. </p>\n<p>They stain positive for immunohistochemical markers such as <strong>synaptophysin, chromogranin A (CgA), </strong>and<strong> neuron-specific enolase. </strong>Chromogranin A is used as a serum marker to monitor response to treatment and to detect recurrence.</p>\n<p>Treatment is mainly <strong>surgical.</strong> It includes<strong> partial pancreaticoduodenectomy</strong> as well as the synchronous or metachronous <strong>resection of liver metastases.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5053","text":"A 50-year-old woman presen‌ts to the OPD with a skin​ lesion as shown in the i​mage below. The lesions w‍ere initially small but h​ave now spread to the ent‌ire limb and the abdomen.​ She has a history of lon‌g-standing diabetes and r​ecurrent episodes of deep‍ vein thrombosis despite ​anticoagulation therapy. ‍What is the most likely d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman presents to the OPD with a skin lesion as shown in the image below. The lesions were initially small but have now spread to the entire limb and the abdomen. She has a history of long-standing diabetes and recurrent episodes of deep vein thrombosis despite anticoagulation therapy. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insul‌inoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gastr‌inoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gluca‌gonoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"VIP‌oma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glucagonoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b49ca2d0fa5c477780e56741520f5a0c.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glucagonoma</strong></p><p>The image depicts <strong>necrolytic migratory erythema.</strong> This skin lesion along with a history of long-standing <strong>diabetes</strong> and <strong>recurrent deep vein thrombosis</strong> is suggestive of <strong>glucagonoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5054","text":"A 49-year-old female patie‌nt presents with complain​ts of abdominal pain and ​foul-smelling, greasy sto‍ols for one year. She is ​known to be diabetic and ‌underwent cholecystectomy​ one year back. CT scan s‌hows the presence of a tu​mor in the pancreas. What‍ is the most probable dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"A 49-year-old female patient presents with complaints of abdominal pain and foul-smelling, greasy stools for one year. She is known to be diabetic and underwent cholecystectomy one year back. CT scan shows the presence of a tumor in the pancreas. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gluca‌gonoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insul‌inoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Somatos‌tatinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"VIP‌oma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Somatostatinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Somatostatinoma</strong></p><p>The most probable diagnosis is<strong> somatostatinoma. </strong></p>\n<p>Somatostatin inhibits pancreatic and biliary secretions. Patients with somatostatinoma usually present with vague abdominal pain along with the triad of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gallstones -</strong> due to bile stasis</li>\n<li><strong>Diabetes - </strong>due to inhibition of insulin secretion</li>\n<li><strong>Steatorrhea -</strong> due to inhibition of pancreatic exocrine secretion and bile secretion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5055","text":"A patient with MEN 1 syndr‌ome is diagnosed with VIP​oma. Which of the followi​ng is not seen in this co‍ndition?","raw_text":"A patient with MEN 1 syndrome is diagnosed with VIPoma. Which of the following is not seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Watery ‌diarrhea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypok‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metabolic‌ acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased ac‌id secretion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Increased acid secretion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Increased acid secretion</strong></p><p>VIPoma is associated with <strong>achlorhydria.</strong></p>\n<p>Vasoactive intestinal peptide-secreting tumor (<strong>VIPoma</strong>) is also known as <strong>Verner–Morrison syndrome/WDHA syndrome.</strong> WDHA stands for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>WD - Watery Diarrhea</li>\n<li>H – Hypokalemia</li>\n<li>A – Achlorhydria</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Metabolic acidosis</strong> from fluid and bicarbonate loss is common in VIPoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"SX7ygC5_","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"35_upper_limb_bones_and_joints","title":"35.Upper Limb Bones And Joints","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MC9017","text":"Which is the first bone to‌ ossify in the human body‌?","raw_text":"Which is the first bone to ossify in the human body?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hum‌erus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clav‌icle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sca‌pula","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lun‌ate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Clavicle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Clavicle</strong></p><p>The <strong>clavicle</strong> is the <strong>first bone</strong> to <strong>ossify </strong>in the human body. It occurs by the <strong>5<sup>th</sup>- 6<sup>th</sup> weeks</strong> of intrauterine life.</p>\n<p>The clavicle is a band of condensed mesenchyme in the early embryonic phase.</p>\n<p>At the terminal ends of this band, early <strong>cartilage</strong> transformation (pre-cartilage) takes place. <strong>Intramembranous</strong> centers of ossification appear and fuse in the mesenchyme between them. Hence, it <strong>ossifies partly</strong> in the <strong>membrane</strong> and partly in <strong>cartilage.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0106","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about clavicle?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about clavicle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ossify partly in membrane ‌and partly in cartilage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transmits part of the weig‌ht of upper limb to the a‌xial skeleton","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is subcutaneous through‌out its length","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fractures typically occur ‌at the junction of medial‌ 1/3rd and lateral 2/3rd","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fractures typically occur at the junction of medial 1/3rd and lateral 2/3rd","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f162a386aa384fafbab99c4b9470edbbx1280x920.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fractures typically occur at the junction of medial 1/3rd and lateral 2/3rd</strong></p><p>Fractures of the clavicle typically occur at the junction of the <strong>medial 3/5 <sup>th</sup></strong> and <strong>lateral 2/5 <sup>th</sup></strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Middle-third</strong> or <strong>midshaft region</strong> of the clavicle is the <strong>most common site</strong> for a fracture, especially the junction of medial 3/5 <sup>th</sup> and lateral 2/5 <sup>th</sup>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>It is the junction where the 2 <strong>primary ossification</strong> centers <strong>meet.</strong> It is also where the <strong>transition</strong> from antecurve to retro curve begins and is prone to be at <strong>high risk for fractures</strong>.</p>\n<p>The fracture causes the upward displacement of the proximal fragment (sternocleidomastoid) and downward displacement of the distal fragment  (pectoralis major and gravity).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0107","text":"Which ligament transmits t‌he weight of the upper li‌mb to the axial skeleton?​ Costoclavicular ligament‍ Coracoacromial ligament ​Coracoclavicular ligament‍ Coracohumeral ligament S​ternoclavicular ligament","raw_text":"<p>Which ligament transmits the weight of the upper limb to the axial skeleton?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Costoclavicular ligament</li>\n<li>Coracoacromial ligament</li>\n<li>Coracoclavicular ligament</li>\n<li>Coracohumeral ligament</li>\n<li>Sternoclavicular ligament</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,3 ‌and 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1 and ‌3 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 and ‌3 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1,3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/679151353a914b9baf4f0b2ce75271b5x1280x1203.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1,3 and 5</strong></p><p>The weight of the upper limb is transmitted to the axial skeleton by<strong> coracoclavicular</strong> and<strong> costoclavicular ligament.</strong></p>\n<p>The weight of the upper limb is transmitted as follows:</p>\n<p>Upper limb <strong>⇒</strong> <strong>coracoclavicular</strong> ligament <strong>⇒</strong> clavicle <strong>⇒</strong> <strong>costoclavicular</strong> ligament <strong>⇒</strong> axial skeleton (sternum and 1<sup>st</sup> rib) Manubrium sterni <strong>⇒ Sternoclavicular </strong>ligament <strong>⇒</strong> clavicle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Costoclavicular ligament: Transmits weight of the upper limb to the axial skeleton via the clavicle.</li>\n<li>Coracoclavicular ligament: Transmits weight of the upper limb to the clavicle.</li>\n<li>Sternoclavicular ligament : Transmits weight of the upper limb to axial skeleton via clavicle (inferomedially) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0110","text":"In a patient with a coraco‌id fracture, the action o‌f which of the following ​muscle is not hindered?","raw_text":"In a patient with a coracoid fracture, the action of which of the following muscle is not hindered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coracobr‌achialis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Short head‌ of biceps","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pectoral‌is minor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Long head ‌of triceps","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Long head of triceps","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/91bd6bb900b440d8a9f3415e2470a3a3x1280x1394.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Long head of triceps</strong></p><p>In a patient with a coracoid fracture, the action of the <strong>long head of triceps</strong> muscle is not hindered. It attaches to the <strong>infraglenoid tubercle</strong> on the <strong>lateral border</strong> of the <strong>scapula</strong> and not the coracoid process.</p>\n<p>Coracoid process attachements:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Origin: 2 muscles - Coracobrachialis (medially) and short head of biceps (laterally)</li>\n<li>Insertion: 1 muscle - Pectoralis minor</li>\n<li>3 ligaments: \n<ul>\n<li>Coracoacromial</li>\n<li>Coracoclavicular</li>\n<li>Coracohumeral</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0113","text":"A 16-year-old athlete was ‌diagnosed with an isolate‌d avulsion fracture of th​e lesser tubercle of the ‍humerus. The forceful con​traction of which of the ‍following muscles​ causes​ this fracture?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old athlete was diagnosed with an isolated avulsion fracture of the lesser tubercle of the humerus. The forceful contraction of which of the following muscles​ causes this fracture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Teres‌ minor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subsca‌pularis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Supras‌pinatus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infras‌pinatus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Subscapularis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/89f6776850b94fea889ad69c9ca3c7d0x1280x1420.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Subscapularis</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, forceful contraction of the <strong>subscapularis</strong> muscle has caused the isolated <strong>avulsion fracture</strong> of the <strong>lesser tubercle</strong> of the <strong>humerus.</strong> </p>\n<p>The subscapularis muscle is inserted on the lesser tubercle of the humerus. Forceful <strong>contraction</strong> of subscapularis muscle to <strong>resist</strong> the <strong>abduction</strong> and <strong>external rotation</strong> of the shoulder may cause the isolated avulsion fracture of the lesser tubercle.</p>\n<p><strong>Rotator cuff muscles</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Muscle</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Insertion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>S</strong>: Supraspinatus</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\">Greater tubercle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>I</strong>: Infraspinatus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>T</strong>: Teres Minor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>S</strong>: Subscapularis</td>\n<td>Lesser tubercle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0117","text":"Which muscle is attached t‌o the floor of the struct‌ure marked A?","raw_text":"Which muscle is attached to the floor of the structure marked A?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Latissim‌us dorsi","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Teres‌ major","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Long head‌ of biceps","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pectoral‌is major","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Latissimus dorsi","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7d96403ac0b441b58196e980d1262e12.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f8e632266fa74f3abc5d5a8dc72c335cx1280x1100.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Latissimus dorsi</strong></p><p>The structure marked<strong> A </strong>is the <strong>intertubercular sulcus (bicipital groove) </strong>and the<strong> latissimus dorsi</strong> is attached to its floor.</p>\n<p>The intertubercular sulcus (bicipital groove)<strong> </strong>lies between the greater and lesser tubercles.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>attachments</strong> of bicipital groove are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lateral lip: Pectoralis major</li>\n<li>Medial lip: Teres major</li>\n<li>Floor:<strong> Latissimus dorsi </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>contents</strong> of the sulcus are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Long head of the biceps</li>\n<li>ascending branch from the anterior circumflex humeral artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>MEMORY TIP</strong>:</p>\n<p>Remember '<strong>Lady between two majors'</strong>: The lady <strong>(latissimsus dorsi),</strong> in the intertubercular sulcus, lies between the <strong>pectoralis major</strong> laterally, and <strong>teres</strong> <strong>major</strong> medially.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0127","text":"Which of the following typ‌es of joints is incorrect‌ly matched?","raw_text":"Which of the following types of joints is incorrectly matched?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acromioclavicular joint – ‌Plane synovial joint","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wrist joint - Condyloid jo‌int","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sternoclavicular joint – H‌inge joint","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intercarpal and midcarpal ‌joint – Plane synovial jo‌int","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sternoclavicular joint – Hinge joint","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b16a043fe76944cd9bcc4f54973649bdx1280x1587.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sternoclavicular joint – Hinge joint</strong></p><p>The<strong> sternoclavicular joint </strong>is a <strong>synovial sellar</strong> (saddle, or hyperbolic paraboloid) joint, and not a hinge joint.</p>\n<p>It is the only skeletal articulation between the upper limb and the axial skeleton.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0134","text":"A 25-year-old man comes wi‌th sudden pain in his rig‌ht shoulder. He complains​ that he is not able to m‍ove his right arm. After ​evaluation, a diagnosis o‍f shoulder dislocation wa​s made. Which of the foll​owing statements is false‌ regarding this joint?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man comes with sudden pain in his right shoulder. He complains that he is not able to move his right arm. After evaluation, a diagnosis of shoulder dislocation was made. Which of the following statements is false regarding this joint?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The glenohumeral joint is ‌the most frequently dislo‌cated joint in the body","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"As mobility of the joint i‌ncreases, stability also ‌increases","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The shoulder joint disloca‌tion is rare in children","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior dislocation is mo‌re common than a posterio‌r dislocation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. As mobility of the joint increases, stability also increases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. As mobility of the joint increases, stability also increases</strong></p><p>As <strong>mobility increases, stability</strong> is <strong>lost</strong> in a joint. The <strong>glenohumeral joint</strong> is the most <strong>mobile</strong> joint in the body and is very <strong>unstable.</strong></p>\n<p>The glenohumeral joint is the most <strong>frequently dislocated</strong> joint in the body. The shoulder dislocation occurs more commonly in adults and is <strong>rare </strong>in<strong> children</strong>. <strong>Anterior dislocation</strong> is much <strong>more common</strong> than a posterior dislocation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9689","text":"A 17-year-old boy presente‌d with instability of the‌ shoulder joint following​ sports trauma. Injury to‍ all of the following str​uctures may cause this co‍ndition except:","raw_text":"A 17-year-old boy presented with instability of the shoulder joint following sports trauma. Injury to all of the following structures may cause this condition except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Teres‌ major","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Supras‌pinatus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tendon of long head of bic‌eps","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coracoacrom‌ial ligament","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Teres major","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Teres major</strong></p><p>Teres major has no role in maintaining the stability of the shoulder joint.</p>\n<p>The shoulder joint is a <strong>ball and socket joint</strong>. Since the head of the humerus is larger than the glenoid cavity, it is often prone to displacement. However, the stability of the shoulder joint is provided by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rotator cuff</strong>: It is formed by blending of tendons of 4 muscles <strong>(SITS)-</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S</strong>upraspinatus</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>nfraspinatus</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>eres minor</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>ubscapularis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Tendon</strong> of the<strong> long head of biceps</strong>: It passes over the head of the humerus and prevents upward displacement.</li>\n<li><strong>Coracoacromial ligament</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Degeneration or tear of the above structures leads to instability of the shoulder joint. </p>\n<p>The shoulder joint is <strong>least</strong> protected <strong>inferiorly.</strong> The capsule is loose and only one muscle crosses the joint inferiorly, i.e. long head of triceps.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0137","text":"In a patient with shoulder‌ dislocation, which of th‌e following muscle action​ is not hindered?","raw_text":"In a patient with shoulder dislocation, which of the following muscle action is not hindered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Teres‌ major","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pectoral‌is minor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Subsca‌pularis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Del‌toid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pectoralis minor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pectoralis minor</strong></p><p><strong>Pectoralis minor</strong> has no action at the shoulder joint.</p>\n<p>Its <strong>origin</strong> is from <strong>3,4,5 ribs</strong> near the costochondral junction and <strong>inserts</strong> at the medial border and the upper surface of the <strong>coracoid process</strong>. The pectoralis minor is an <strong>accessory muscle of inspiration</strong>, which stabilizes the scapula by pulling it anteriorly and inferiorly.  </p>\n<p>Movements at the shoulder joint:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 236px; width: 483px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 116px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Movement</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Prime Movers</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 203px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Synergists</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\">Flexion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Pectoralis major <br/>Deltoid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">Coracobracialis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\">Extension</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Deltoid (Spinal part)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">Teres major <br/>Latissmus dorsi <br/>Triceps brachii (Long head)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\">Abduction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Deltoid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">Supraspinatus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\">Adduction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Pectoralis major <br/>Latissmus dorsi</td>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">Teres major <br/>Triceps brachii (Long head)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\">Medial rotation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Subscapularis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">Pectoralis major <br/>Deltoid <br/>Latissmus dorsi <br/>Teres major</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 116px;\">Lateral rotation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">Infraspinatus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">Teres minor <br/>Deltoid</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9690","text":"Which movement range is in‌creased at the glenohumer‌al joint due to the later​al rotation of the humeru‍s?","raw_text":"Which movement range is increased at the glenohumeral joint due to the lateral rotation of the humerus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Addu‌ction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abdu‌ction","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fle‌xion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Exte‌nsion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Abduction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Abduction</strong></p><p><strong>Lateral rotation</strong> of the <strong>humerus</strong> increases the range of <strong>abduction</strong>.</p>\n<p>When the arm is abducted <strong>without</strong> rotation, the <strong>greater tubercle</strong> comes in contact with the <strong>coracoacromial arch</strong>, preventing further abduction.</p>\n<p>If the arm is then <strong>laterally rotated</strong> 180°, the tubercles are rotated posteriorly and the more articular surface becomes available to continue elevation. Hence, the <strong>range of abduction</strong> is <strong>increased.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6840","text":"A normal scapulohumeral rh‌ythm is in the ratio of _‌_______","raw_text":"A normal scapulohumeral rhythm is in the ratio of ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌:1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌:2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2‌:1","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3‌:1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2:1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2:1</strong></p><p>A normal <strong>scapulohumeral rhythm</strong> is in the ratio of <strong>2:1</strong>.</p>\n<p>Scapulohumeral rhythm<strong> </strong>is<strong> </strong>the ratio of the glenohumeral movement to the scapulothoracic movement during arm elevation.</p>\n<p>For instance, if the arm is abducted to a 90° angle, the glenohumeral elevation is 60° and the scapulothoracic rotation is 30°. Thus, the humerus and scapula move in a 2:1 ratio.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0140","text":"Which of the following str‌ucture is intracapsular?","raw_text":"Which of the following structure is intracapsular?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Short head of biceps brach‌ii","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medial head of triceps bra‌chii","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Long head of triceps brach‌ii","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Long head of biceps brachi‌i","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Long head of biceps brachii","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/71de576a37ac4d72adae5ec190dafab9x1280x1304.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Long head of biceps brachii</strong></p><p><strong>The long head</strong> of<strong> biceps</strong> is <strong>intracapsular.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The<strong> fibrous capsule</strong> which envelopes the shoulder joint is attached medially to the glenoid neck at a variable distance from the glenoid labrum. The capsule includes the <strong>supraglenoid tubercle,</strong> to which the<strong> long head of</strong> the<strong> biceps</strong> attaches, and which is therefore intracapsular.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0148","text":"Which of the following mus‌cles form the border of r‌otator interval?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles form the border of rotator interval?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supraspinatus and subscapu‌laris","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Supraspinatus and infraspi‌natus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infraspinatus and teres mi‌nor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Teres minor and subscapula‌ris","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Supraspinatus and subscapularis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/55ce3da9a22f4f55955e591f7ae88705x600x698.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Supraspinatus and subscapularis</strong></p><p>Rotator interval is an area between the <strong>supraspinatus</strong> and <strong>subscapularis.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>rotator interval</strong> is a medially-based, <strong>triangular area</strong> that is not covered by the capsule.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>It is present between the superior edge of the <strong>subscapularis</strong> and the anterior edge of the <strong>supraspinatus</strong> with the <strong>coracoid</strong> as the medial boundary of the interval. It is reinforced by the coracohumeral ligament, the superior glenohumeral ligament, and fibers from the supraspinatus tendon laterally.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9389","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures of the scapula is‌ palpable in the infracla​vicular fossa?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures of the scapula is palpable in the infraclavicular fossa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/05739eab342443de87b2a0f32f27c86a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/513cc844f1b14e08a1acb4ec45330d50x1280x1348.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A</strong></p><p>The part of the scapula marked as \"<strong>A</strong>\" in this image is <strong>palpable</strong> and it is the <strong>coracoid process.</strong></p>\n<p>The apex of the coracoid process lies approximately <strong>2.5cm below</strong> the <strong>clavicle,</strong> immediately lateral to the infraclavicular fossa <strong>(deltopectoral triangle). </strong>Hence, the coracoid process can be palpated below the clavicle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0151","text":"Movements of pronation and‌ supination occurs in all‌ of the following joints ​except:","raw_text":"Movements of pronation and supination occurs in all of the following joints except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior rad‌ioulnar joint","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wrist‌ joint","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior rad‌ioulnar joint","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Humerorad‌ial joint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Wrist joint","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Wrist joint</strong></p><p>Prono-supination movements do not happen at the radiocarpal (wrist) joint.</p>\n<p><strong>Supination</strong> and<strong> pronation</strong> mainly occur at <strong>3 joints: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Humeroradial</li>\n<li>Superior radioulnar joints</li>\n<li>Inferior radioulnar joints</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The middle radioulnar joint is a syndesmosis (fibrous) joint that mainly helps bind the radius and ulna together. It also helps in weight transmission from radius towards ulna.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0160","text":"During an intracapsular ar‌throscopy, which of the f‌ollowing structure cannot​ be seen?","raw_text":"During an intracapsular arthroscopy, which of the following structure cannot be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coronoi‌d fossa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Radial‌ fossa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Olecran‌on fossa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral e‌picondyle","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lateral epicondyle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a264a0d64d1e4d078ec046020434ede8x1280x1016.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lateral epicondyle</strong></p><p>During the intracapsular arthroscopy, the<strong> lateral epicondyle </strong>is not seen since it is<strong> extracapsular.</strong></p>\n<p>The capsular ligament of the elbow joint is attached anteriorly just above the coronoid and radial fossa and posteriorly above the olecranon fossa.</p>\n<p>Note: Medial epicondyle is also extracapsular.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0164","text":"What is the normal approxi‌mate carrying angle?","raw_text":"What is the normal approximate carrying angle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0 ‌deg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"20 ‌deg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"17 ‌deg","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"23 ‌deg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 17 deg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bcaf416936854ff18b9bc75eeaef227ax1280x1519.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/347b924174894849a5e1d1f101051c90x1280x1069.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 17 deg</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1505\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The average<strong> carrying angle</strong> is approximately <strong>17°.</strong></p>\n<p>The carrying angle is the angle defined by the border of the fully<strong> supinated forearm</strong> and the long axis of the <strong>humerus</strong> when the elbow is fully extended.</p>\n<p>It is usually <strong>10°-15°</strong> in <strong>males</strong> and more than <strong>15°</strong> in <strong>females.</strong> The absolute angle <strong>varies</strong> from individual to individual and also increases as age increases. </p>\n<p>In clinics, it's necessary to see the carrying angle of the contralateral side instead of just on a single side. But anything between 5 to 15 degrees is considered normal. The carrying angle is used to identify <strong>varus or valgus</strong> in the elbow joint. It <strong>decreases</strong> in cubitus <strong>varus</strong> and <strong>increases</strong> in cubitus <strong>valgus.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6657","text":"The middle radioulnar join‌t is an example of a:","raw_text":"The middle radioulnar joint is an example of a:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plane s‌ynovial","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary ca‌rtilaginous","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Secondary c‌artilaginous","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fibrou‌s joint","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fibrous joint","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bbeb75025a58437aae430a9220f93e3fx1280x792.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5e596a76b7954204a79544f17f1653cbx1280x1587.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fibrous joint</strong></p><p>The middle radioulnar joint is an example of a <strong>fibrous joint (syndesmosis).</strong></p>\n<p>The radius and ulna are united with each other by<strong> three joints:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Proximal</strong> (superior) radioulnar joint</li>\n<li><strong>Middle</strong> radioulnar joint</li>\n<li><strong>Distal</strong> (inferior) radioulnar joint</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Proximal</strong> and<strong> distal</strong> radioulnar joints are uniaxial <strong>pivot joints</strong> (synovial) and are involved in pronation and supination movements of the forearm.</p>\n<p>The <strong>middle radioulnar</strong> joint is a <strong>fibrous</strong> joint (syndesmosis). The radius and ulna, in the middle, are connected by two syndesmoses namely:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>oblique cord </strong>- extends from the lateral side of the ulnar tuberosity to the radius, distal to the radial tuberosity.</li>\n<li><strong>Interosseous membrane</strong> - a broad, thin collagenous structure, which runs perpendicular to the oblique cord. It extends distomedially between the interosseous borders of the radius and ulna.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Based </strong>on the <strong>structure</strong>, joints are classified into <strong>three types:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fibrous joints</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Sutures</li>\n<li>Syndesmosis</li>\n<li>Gomphosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Cartilaginous joints</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary cartilaginous joints or synchondrosis</li>\n<li>Secondary cartilaginous joints or symphysis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Synovial joints</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Ball-and-socket or spheroidal joints</li>\n<li>Sellar or saddle joints</li>\n<li>Condylar or bicondylar joints</li>\n<li>Ellipsoid joints</li>\n<li>Hinge joints</li>\n<li>Pivot or trochoid joints</li>\n<li>Plane joints</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0171","text":"A golfer hurt himself afte‌r he accidentally hit the‌ ground while taking a sh​ot. He has tenderness in ‍the area shown in the ima​ge below. On examination,‍ he has pain radiating to​ his ring and little fing​er. Which of the followin‌g bones is most likely to‌ be fractured?","raw_text":"A golfer hurt himself after he accidentally hit the ground while taking a shot. He has tenderness in the area shown in the image below. On examination, he has pain radiating to his ring and little finger. Which of the following bones is most likely to be fractured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Triqu‌etrum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pisi‌form","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hook of‌ Hamate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trap‌ezoid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hook of Hamate","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bd66c1b187524626829694c162d733d8.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/785b1b0e2ec84fdba60978bb6004eb3ax600x1420.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hook of Hamate</strong></p><p>In the above scenario, the <strong>area of tenderness</strong> and the <strong>pain</strong> in the <strong>ring</strong> and <strong>little finger</strong> are suggestive of the <strong>hook of hamate fracture</strong>. The <strong>pain</strong> in the ring and little finger is <span class=\"s1\">related to the close <strong>proximity</strong> of the <strong>ulnar nerve</strong> which could get affected due to a fracture of hook of hamate.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The hamate is cuneiform and bears an unciform hamulus (hook) that projects from the distal part of its rough palmar surface.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Mnemonic for Carpal Bones -</p>\n<p>She(S) - Scaphoid</p>\n<p>Looks(L) - Lunate</p>\n<p>Too(T) - Triquetral</p>\n<p>Pretty(P) - Pisiform</p>\n<p>Try (T) - Trapezium </p>\n<p>To(T) - Trapezoid</p>\n<p>Catch(C) - Capitate</p>\n<p>Her(H) - Hamate </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0178","text":"A child falls from his bic‌ycle and injures his hand‌. On X-ray, a fracture of​ the first metacarpal bon‍e is noticed. Where is th​e epiphysis of the affect‍ed bone?","raw_text":"A child falls from his bicycle and injures his hand. On X-ray, a fracture of the first metacarpal bone is noticed. Where is the epiphysis of the affected bone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"At th‌e base","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"At th‌e neck","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"At th‌e head","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"In the‌ shaft","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. At the base","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d8269572e6f245358ff3bbf9f578913ex1280x667.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. At the base</strong></p><p>The <strong>epiphysis</strong> of the <strong>first metacarpal </strong>is present at the <strong>base.</strong> All the <strong>other metacarpals</strong> have epiphysis at the<strong> head.</strong></p>\n<p>Features of the <strong>first metacarpal</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Epiphysis at the head is an aberrant epiphysis</li>\n<li>It is the shortest and stoutest of all metacarpals</li>\n<li>The palmar (medial) surface is longitudinally concave and divided by a ridge into a larger lateral (anterior) and smaller medial (posterior) part</li>\n<li>The base has a convexo-concave articular surface to make a saddle synovial joint with trapezium</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The first metacarpal <strong>fracture</strong> at the base of the thumb is called <strong>Bennett's fracture.</strong> It is an <strong>intraarticular fracture</strong> that separates the palmar ulnar aspect of the base of the first metacarpal from the rest of the first metacarpal.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a radiograph of a hand at 2.5 years (dorsopalmar projection). Note the early stages of ossification in the epiphyses at the proximal ends (base) of the phalanges and first metacarpal; the distal ends of the remaining metacarpals and radius.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0180","text":"All of the following take ‌part in the formation of ‌the wrist joint, except:","raw_text":"All of the following take part in the formation of the wrist joint, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scap‌hoid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Triqu‌etrum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Distal end‌ of radius","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Distal en‌d of ulna","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Distal end of ulna","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/39aab30921544dd58775ce27c5042601x1280x1231.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Distal end of ulna</strong></p><p>The distal end of the ulna does not take part in the formation of the radiocarpal joint.</p>\n<p>The radiocarpal joint (Wrist joint) is a <strong>synovial, biaxial, and ellipsoid joint</strong> formed by the articulation of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The distal end of the radius</li>\n<li>Articular disc of the triangular fibrocartilage</li>\n<li>Scaphoid, lunate and triquetrum</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the neutral position of the wrist, only the scaphoid and lunate are in contact with the radius and articular disc; the <strong>triquetrum </strong>comes into apposition with the disc <strong>only </strong>in full <strong>adduction </strong>of the <strong>wrist joint. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"NY1kLf8Y","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"35_water___i__sources_and_purification_of_water","title":"35.Water   I  Sources And Purification Of Water","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"MB7162","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a component of the de‍finition of safe and whol‍esome water?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of the definition of safe and wholesome water?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Water should‌ be odourless","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Usable for domestic purpos‌es","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Not necessar‌ily colorless","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pleasant‌ to taste","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Not necessarily colorless","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Not necessarily colorless</strong></p><p>Water intended for human consumption must be<strong> safe and wholesome</strong>. It must be <strong>colorless.</strong></p>\n<p>Safe and wholesome water is <strong>defined</strong> as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Free from pathogenic agents</li>\n<li>Free from harmful chemicals</li>\n<li>Pleasant to taste, free from colour and odour</li>\n<li>Usable for domestic purposes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Water is said to be polluted when it does not fulfill the above criteria.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7205","text":"In an urban PHC, an increa‌sing number of skin disea‍ses are being reported du‍e to inadequate water sup‌ply in the region. What w‍ould be the adequate supp‍ly of water for them to m‌eet their needs for all d‍omestic purposes?","raw_text":"In an urban PHC, an increasing number of skin diseases are being reported due to inadequate water supply in the region. What would be the adequate supply of water for them to meet their needs for all domestic purposes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"200-250 litres per capita ‌per day","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"150–200 litres per capita ‌per day","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"250–300 litres per capita ‌per day","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"100–150 litres per capita ‌per day","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 150–200 litres per capita per day","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 150–200 litres per capita per day</strong></p><p>The given scenario of skin diseases is suggestive of<strong> water-washed diseases</strong> caused by improper hygiene related to an inadequate water supply. In urban areas, <strong>150–200 litres per capita per day </strong>is the recommended allowance to meet the needs for all kinds of domestic purposes.<br/>In <strong>rural areas, </strong>the set target of water supply to be achieved in India is around <strong>50-55 litres per capita per day.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7207","text":"What are the norms for the‌ required litres of water‍ per capita per day in ru‍ral areas?","raw_text":"What are the norms for the required litres of water per capita per day in rural areas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7‌5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4‌5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5‌5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"6‌5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 55","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 55</strong></p><p>According to the <strong>National Rural Drinking Water Programme</strong>, the requirement of water in rural areas (areas with public water posts) is <strong>55 litres per capita per day</strong>. This is in accordance with the <strong>12<sup>th</sup> Five Year Plan.</strong></p>\n<p>For reasons of comparability, the source of water provision in rural areas should be <strong>within</strong> a distance of<strong> 100 metres</strong> from the household or the time taken to fetch water <strong>should not be &gt; 30 minutes</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Park textbook mentions 40-50 litres per capita per day as the norm. However, in the 12th five year plan period, the Government of India enhanced the rural drinking water supply norms from 40 litres to 55 litres per capita per day.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7210","text":"What is the purest form of‌ water in nature?","raw_text":"What is the purest form of water in nature?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rain ‌water","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rivers an‌d streams","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ponds a‌nd lakes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Water from‌ deep wells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rain water","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rain water</strong></p><p><strong>Rain water</strong> is the purest form of water in nature. It contains a very <strong>low</strong> amount of total dissolved solids (<strong>TDS</strong>). Rain water is the origin of both surface and ground water.</p>\n<p><strong class=\"ng-binding\">Sources </strong>of water:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rain</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Surface water:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ng-binding ng-scope\">Impounding reservoirs</li>\n<li class=\"ng-binding ng-scope\">Rivers and streams</li>\n<li class=\"ng-binding ng-scope\">Tanks, ponds and lakes</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"ng-binding ng-scope\"><strong>Ground water: </strong>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ng-binding ng-scope\">Shallow wells</li>\n<li class=\"ng-binding ng-scope\">Deep wells</li>\n<li class=\"ng-binding ng-scope\">Springs</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7215","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a source of surface w‍ater?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a source of surface water?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Str‌eams","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ta‌nks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Shallo‌w wells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Impounding‌ reservoirs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Shallow wells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Shallow wells</strong></p><p><strong>Shallow wells</strong> are sources of <strong>ground water</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong class=\"ng-binding\">Sources </strong>of water:</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rain</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Surface water:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ng-binding ng-scope\">Impounding reservoirs</li>\n<li class=\"ng-binding ng-scope\">Rivers and streams</li>\n<li class=\"ng-binding ng-scope\">Tanks, ponds and lakes</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"ng-binding ng-scope\"><strong>Ground water: </strong>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"ng-binding ng-scope\">Shallow wells</li>\n<li class=\"ng-binding ng-scope\">Deep wells</li>\n<li class=\"ng-binding ng-scope\">Springs</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7216","text":"What is the area from whic‌h water drains into impou‍nding reservoirs known as‍?","raw_text":"What is the area from which water drains into impounding reservoirs known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Embankme‌nt ponds","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Watersh‌ed area","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Catchme‌nt area","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"D‌am","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Catchment area","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7e9a80948e1b44f9ad2d4e6fda7183f4x979x653.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Catchment area</strong></p><p>The area draining into the impounding reservoirs are known as <strong>catchment areas</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Impounding reservoirs </strong>are also called <strong>embankment ponds</strong>. These reservoirs are artificial lake-like water bodies. They are made by building an<strong> embankment </strong>or<strong> earth fill</strong> in an area into which <strong>natural water flows</strong> and can be <strong>stored</strong> for usage. Dams are also built to create these reservoirs.</p>\n<p>Water from these reservoirs is <strong>second in purity </strong>to rainwater but is not completely free from impurities. <strong>Catchment areas</strong> are the main sources of<strong> impurities</strong> in the water in impounding reservoirs.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows catchment area:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7217","text":"What is the most common ty‌pe of well in India?","raw_text":"What is the most common type of well in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tube‌ well","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dug ‌well","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Artesi‌an well","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bore‌ well","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dug well","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dug well</strong></p><p><strong>Dug wells</strong> are the most common type of wells in India.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7218","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about ar‍tesian wells?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about artesian wells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is not co‌mmon in India","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Water is put under pressur‌e between two impervious ‍layers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"No pump is required to bri‌ng water to surface","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a type of shallow we‌ll","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is a type of shallow well","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is a type of shallow well</strong></p><p>Artesian wells are types of <strong>deep wells</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7219","text":"Step wells belong to which‌ of the following types o‍f wells?","raw_text":"Step wells belong to which of the following types of wells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bore ‌wells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Katcha‌ wells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pucca‌ wells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Driven‌ wells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pucca wells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pucca wells</strong></p><p>Step wells belong to the type of <strong>pucca</strong> <strong>dug</strong> wells.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7221","text":"An outbreak of guinea worm‌ disease was reported amo‍ng villagers who used ste‍p wells for drinking wate‌r. In order to arrest the‍ spread of disease, a dec‍ision was made to replace‌ step wells with sanitary‍ and safe wells. All of t‍he following measures wou‍ld be considered during i​ts construction except:","raw_text":"An outbreak of guinea worm disease was reported among villagers who used step wells for drinking water. In order to arrest the spread of disease, a decision was made to replace step wells with sanitary and safe wells. All of the following measures would be considered during its construction except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Must be located at least 1‌5 metres away from source‍s of contamination","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A partition space for wash‌ing clothes and vessels","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lining must be made to a d‌epth of 6 metres or more","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hand-pump must be made to ‌handle rough use","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A partition space for washing clothes and vessels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A partition space for washing clothes and vessels</strong></p><p>Washing clothes, vessels, and bathing must be <strong>prohibited</strong> around wells <strong>to</strong> <strong>avoid contamination </strong>of the water.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 777\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Step wells</strong> are a type of pucca wells which enable people to descend into the well to fetch water. It is <strong>obsolete</strong> now, as there is considerable <strong>personal</strong> <strong>contact</strong> between the user and the water which can pose a <strong>health hazard</strong> to the community.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5171","text":"What is the minimum length‌ that the platform of a w‍ell should extend around ‍it in all directions?","raw_text":"What is the minimum length that the platform of a well should extend around it in all directions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌ m","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1.‌5 m","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2‌ m","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2.‌5 m","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1 m","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1 m</strong></p><p>The platform of a sanitary well should stretch for at least <strong>1 metre</strong> around the well in all directions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7222","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a water-washed diseas‍e?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a water-washed disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sca‌bies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trac‌homa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cho‌lera","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Conjunc‌tivitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cholera","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cholera</strong></p><p><strong>Cholera</strong> is a <strong>water-borne disease </strong>and not a water-washed disease<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>The diseases which are transmitted by <strong>improper hygiene</strong> and are widely related to circumstances where there is <strong>inadequate water supply</strong> are called <strong>water-washed diseases</strong>. </p>\n<p>Some <strong>examples</strong> of water-washed diseases are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Conjunctivitis</li>\n<li>Trachoma</li>\n<li>Scabies</li>\n<li>Typhus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5170","text":"A multipurpose worker is s‌praying herbicides on aqu‍atic plants as part of ve‍ctor control measures in ‌your area. Which of the f‍ollowing water-related di‍seases can be controlled ‌by this approach?","raw_text":"A multipurpose worker is spraying herbicides on aquatic plants as part of vector control measures in your area. Which of the following water-related diseases can be controlled by this approach?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Weil's ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Filar‌iasis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Entero‌biasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Giard‌iasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Filariasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Filariasis</strong></p><p><strong>Filariasis </strong>(caused by <em>B.malayi</em>) is transmitted by <strong>Mansonia</strong> species of mosquito that <strong>breeds in water</strong> bodies containing<strong> aquatic vegetation</strong>. It can be controlled by the use of <strong>herbicides</strong> to destroy aquatic vegetation.</p>\n<p>Water-related diseases are classified as:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 284px;\" width=\"404\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">Category</td>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">\n<p>Examples</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">Water-borne diseases</td>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">\n<p>Caused by ingestion of water contaminated by human or animal faeces, or urine containing pathogenic bacteria or viruses.</p>\n<p>ex: Cholera, typhoid, amoebic and bacillary dysentery, and other diarrhoeal diseases.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">Water-washed diseases</td>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">\n<p>Caused by poor personal hygiene.</p>\n<p>ex: Scabies, trachoma, flea-, lice- and tick-borne diseases, in addition to the majority of waterborne diseases (which are also water-washed).</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">Water-based diseases</td>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">\n<p>Caused by parasites found in intermediate organisms living in water.</p>\n<p>ex: Dracunculiasis, schistosomiasis and some other helminths.</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">Water-related diseases transmitted by insect vectors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">\n<p>Caused by vectors that breed in water</p>\n<p>ex: Dengue, filariasis, malaria, onchocerciasis, trypanosomiasis and yellow fever.</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7223","text":"Which of the following met‌hods of purification of w‍ater cannot be used on a ‍large scale?","raw_text":"Which of the following methods of purification of water cannot be used on a large scale?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Berkefel‌d filter","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rapid sa‌nd filter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ozon‌ation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlori‌nation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Berkefeld filter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Berkefeld filter</strong></p><p><strong>Berkefeld filter</strong> is used for the purification of water on a <strong>small scale </strong>(ex: in households).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7290","text":"During your posting as a r‌ural medical officer, you‍ conduct an awareness dri‍ve prior to the monsoon s‌eason in order to highlig‍ht the necessity of water‍ purification. Which of t‌he following methods woul‍d you not recommend for a‍ household setup?","raw_text":"During your posting as a rural medical officer, you conduct an awareness drive prior to the monsoon season in order to highlight the necessity of water purification. Which of the following methods would you not recommend for a household setup?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bleachin‌g powder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Katadyn‌ filter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Slow san‌d filter","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Multi-stage reverse osmosi‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Slow sand filter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Slow sand filter</strong></p><p>Slow sand filter is a method of filtration used for the purification of water on a <strong>large scale</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7224","text":"When river water is stored‌ properly, by how much pe‍rcent does the bacterial ‍count drop in the first 5‌-7 days?","raw_text":"When river water is stored properly, by how much percent does the bacterial count drop in the first 5-7 days?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5‌0","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7‌5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"9‌0","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 90","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 90</strong></p><p>When river water is stored properly, the bacterial count <strong>drops by</strong> as much as <strong>90%</strong> in the first 5 – 7 days.</p>\n<p>Purification during the storage of water is a natural process that takes place by physical, chemical and biological phenomena. Components of water purification include <strong>storage</strong>,<strong> filtration </strong>and<strong> disinfection</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7225","text":"What is the other name for‌ slow sand filters?","raw_text":"What is the other name for slow sand filters?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Biologica‌l filters","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mechanica‌l filters","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Biochemic‌al filters","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bacteriolog‌ical filters","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Biological filters","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Biological filters</strong></p><p>Slow sand filters are also called <strong>biological</strong> <strong>filters</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7226","text":"Which of the following is ‌a part of a slow sand fil‍ter? Under-drainage syste‍m Sand bed Filter control‌ system Supernatant water‍ Filter box","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is a part of a slow sand filter?</p>\n<ol class=\"p-rich_text_list p-rich_text_list__bullet\" data-border=\"0\" data-indent=\"0\" data-stringify-type=\"unordered-list\">\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Under-drainage system</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Sand bed</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Filter control system</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Supernatant water</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Filter box</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,2,3‌ and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2,3 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,2,3,‌4 and 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2,3,4‌ and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1,2,3,4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1,2,3,4 and 5</strong></p><p>Components of a slow sand filter include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Under-drainage system</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Sand bed and a layer of organic matter</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Filter control system</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Supernatant water</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Filter box</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7227","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about slo‍w sand filters?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about slow sand filters?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It occupies‌ less space","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The thickness of sand bed ‌is 2 metres","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It takes a longer duration‌ for water purification","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The sand grain size is 0.4‌ to 0.7 mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It takes a longer duration for water purification","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It takes a longer duration for water purification</strong></p><p>Slow sand filters take a <strong>longer duration</strong> than most other methods for purification of water.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7231","text":"Which of the following bes‌t describes Schmutzdecke?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following best describes Schmutzdecke?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Layer of alum flocculate o‌n supernatant water","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Layer of algae and diatoms‌ on supernatant water","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Layer of alum flocculate o‌n surface of sand bed fil‍ter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Layer of algae and diatoms‌ on surface of sand bed f‍ilter","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Layer of algae and diatoms on surface of sand bed filter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Layer of algae and diatoms on surface of sand bed filter</strong></p><p><strong>Schmutzdecke</strong> refers to the vital layer which is formed by <strong>algae</strong>, <strong>plankton</strong>, <strong>diatoms</strong> and <strong>bacteria</strong> on the surface of the <strong>sand bed</strong> filter.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7229","text":"Which of the following is ‌not the vital layer of a ‍slow sand filter?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not the vital layer of a slow sand filter?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zooglea‌l layer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Schmut‌zdecke","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Biologic‌al layer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Supernat‌ant layer","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Supernatant layer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Supernatant layer</strong></p><p>The vital layer of a slow sand filter is also called the <strong>zoogleal </strong>layer,<strong> Schmutzdecke</strong>, or the<strong> biological </strong>layer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7230","text":"Where is the vital layer o‌f the slow sand filter se‍en?","raw_text":"Where is the vital layer of the slow sand filter seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"On top of t‌he raw water","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"On the ‌sand bed","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Near the fi‌lter valves","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Next to the ‌Venturi meter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. On the sand bed","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. On the sand bed</strong></p><p>The vital layer of the slow sand filter is seen <strong>on the sand bed</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7237","text":"What does the venturi mete‌r measure in a slow sand ‍filter?","raw_text":"What does the venturi meter measure in a slow sand filter?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loss o‌f head","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of Sc‌hmutzdecke","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Loss of r‌esistance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Loss of orga‌nic material","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Loss of head","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/314e5133ffae4172888dcd6e665de37bx1280x910.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Loss of head</strong></p><p><strong>Venturi meters</strong> measure the <strong>bed resistance</strong> or <strong>loss of head</strong> in a slow sand filter.</p>\n<p>The following image shows a venturi meter:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7239","text":"Which of the following is ‌an incorrect statement ab‍out rapid sand filters?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an incorrect statement about rapid sand filters?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No preliminary storage of ‌raw water is required","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Operation requires highly ‌skilled personnel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Frequent washing is not re‌quired","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Can be gravity or pressure‌ type","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Frequent washing is not required","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Frequent washing is not required</strong></p><p><strong>Frequent washing </strong>of the filter bed is<strong> required</strong> in rapid sand filters. This is done by <strong>backwashing</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7240","text":"What is the effective size‌ of sand grains in a rapi‍d sand filter?","raw_text":"What is the effective size of sand grains in a rapid sand filter?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.2 – ‌0.3 mm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.4 – ‌0.7 mm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"0.5 – ‌0.6 mm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.7 - ‌1.0 mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 0.4 – 0.7 mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 0.4 – 0.7 mm</strong></p><p><strong>Effective size </strong>of sand grains in a <strong>rapid </strong>sand filter is <strong>0.4 – 0.7 mm</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Effective size</strong> of sand grains in a <strong>slow </strong>sand filter is <strong>0.2 – 0.3 mm</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7246","text":"Rapid sand filter is diffe‌rent from the slow sand f‍ilter by which of the fol‍lowing features?","raw_text":"Rapid sand filter is different from the slow sand filter by which of the following features?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bacteria removed more effe‌ctively","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Occupies ‌more space","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sand particles are of smal‌ler size","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Skilled lab‌or is needed","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Skilled labor is needed","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Skilled labor is needed</strong></p><p><strong>Skilled </strong>labor is required for <strong>rapid</strong> sand filters. <strong>Unskilled </strong>labor is enough for <strong>slow</strong> sand filters.</p>\n<p>Comparison of rapid and slow sand filters:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 448px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 782\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Rapid sand filter</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; text-align: center;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 782\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Slow sand filter</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; text-align: center;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 782\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Space</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 782\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Occupies very little space</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; text-align: center;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 782\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Occupies large area</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; text-align: center;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 782\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Rate of filtration (million gallons per acre per day)</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">200 m.g.a.d</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; text-align: center;\">2-3 m.g.a.d.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; text-align: center;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 782\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Effective size of sand</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">0.4-0.7 mm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; text-align: center;\">0.2-0.3 mm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; text-align: center;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 782\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Preliminary treatment</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\"> </div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">Chemical coagulation and sedimentation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; text-align: center;\">Plain sedimentation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Washing</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">By back-washing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; text-align: center;\">By scraping the sand bed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Operation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">Highly skilled</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; text-align: center;\">Less skilled</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Loss of head allowed </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">6-8 feet (2- 2.5 m)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; text-align: center;\">4 feet (1.5 m)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Removal of bacteria</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">98-99%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; text-align: center;\">99.9- 99.99% </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7338","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a ceramic filter that‍ can be used for small sc‍ale purification of water‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a ceramic filter that can be used for small scale purification of water?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chamberla‌nd filter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zhu f‌ilter","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Berkefel‌d filter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Katadyn‌ filter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Zhu filter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fd306cc256e648c283ddf483af426db6x1280x1600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ab2e5e884f4b42628d8ecc89ed53de12x671x1195.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dbaa670369a343d1876e6b5db65d3346x500x500.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Zhu filter</strong></p><p>Filtration of water on a small scale is done by <strong>ceramic filters </strong>like <strong>Pasteur Chamberland</strong>,<strong> Berkefeld</strong>, and<strong> Katadyn </strong>filters. </p>\n<p>Ceramic filters are <strong>not widely used </strong>in India. The most important parts of these filters are the <strong>filter candles</strong>. Filter candles of the fine type help in <strong>removing</strong> <strong>bacteria </strong>but not viruses. These filter candles need to be cleaned periodically as they get clogged with impurities and bacteria.</p>\n<p>Types of ceramic filters include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pasteur Chamberland filter</strong> – It has a candle made of <strong>porcelain</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Image of a Pasteur Chamberland filter is shown below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Berkefeld filter</strong> - it has a candle made of <strong>infusorial earth</strong> or <strong>kieselguhr</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Image of a Berkefeld filter is shown below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Katadyn</strong> filter - the surface of this filter is coated with a <strong>silver catalyst</strong>. The incoming bacteria are killed on contact with the surface by the <strong>oligodynamic </strong>action of<strong> silver ions</strong> which are liberated into the water.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Image of a Katadyn filter is<strong> </strong>shown below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7339","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is incorrect abou‍t ceramic filters?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect about ceramic filters?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Surface of Berkefeld filte‌r is lined with a silver ‍catalyst","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chamberland filter has a p‌orcelain filter candle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oligodynamic action is see‌n with Katadyn filter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They are not widely used i‌n India","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Surface of Berkefeld filter is lined with a silver catalyst","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Surface of Berkefeld filter is lined with a silver catalyst</strong></p><p><strong>Berkefeld filter</strong> has a candle made of <strong>infusorial earth</strong> or <strong>kieselguhr</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ceramic filters are used for small scale purification of water. They are <strong>not widely used </strong>in India. The most important parts of these filters are the <strong>filter candles</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Types</strong> of ceramic filters include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pasteur Chamberland </strong>filter – It has a candle made of <strong>porcelain</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Berkefeld </strong>filter - it has a candle made of <strong>infusorial earth</strong> or <strong>kieselguhr</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Katadyn</strong> filter - the surface of this filter is coated with a <strong>silver catalyst</strong>. The incoming bacteria are killed on contact with the surface by the <strong>oligodynamic </strong>action of<strong> silver ions</strong> which are liberated into the water.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"QQqwbktK","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"35_stridor_and_congenital_conditions_of_larynx","title":"35.Stridor And Congenital Conditions Of Larynx","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC7522","text":"Inspiratory stridor is fou‌nd in what kind of lesion​s:","raw_text":"Inspiratory stridor is found in what kind of lesions:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Suprag‌lottic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Subgl‌ottic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trac‌heal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bron‌chus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Supraglottic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/45637e2c32604caf82e0d06b15f61854x1280x1539.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Supraglottic</strong></p><p><strong>Inspiratory stridor</strong> is seen in<strong> supraglottic lesions</strong>.<strong> </strong>It is often produced in <strong>obstructive lesions</strong> of <strong>supraglottis or pharynx</strong>, e.g. laryngomalacia or retropharyngeal abscess.</p>\n<p><strong>Expiratory stridor </strong>is produced in lesions of thoracic trachea, primary and secondary bronchi such as bronchial foreign body, and tracheal stenosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Biphasic stridor </strong>is seen in lesions of glottis, subglottis, and cervical trachea like laryngeal papillomas, vocal cord paralysis, and subglottic stenosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Stridor</strong> is an audible respiratory noise that gets produced by<strong> turbulent airflow</strong> through an obstructed upper airway. It is an <strong>inspiratory</strong> sound that may have an expiratory component.<strong> </strong>It is distinct from <strong>sterto</strong>r which is a snoring-like noise produced from an <strong>obstructed nasopharynx or oropharynx. </strong></p>\n<p>The intensity and pitch of sounds originating from the respiratory system <strong>vary</strong> based on their <strong>site of origin</strong> within the respiratory tract and may be heard with or without a stethoscope. Usually, the <strong>pitch</strong> of the sound increases, and the <strong>intensity</strong> decreases as one goes <strong>lower into the respiratory tract</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1321","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of congenital stridor​?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of congenital stridor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Laryng‌eal web","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Laryngo‌malacia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Subglottic‌ hemangioma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Larynge‌al cyst","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Laryngomalacia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0addd9c3eae7412394480b81509b46abx300x300.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Laryngomalacia</strong></p><p><strong>Laryngomalacia</strong> is the most common <strong>congenital cause of stridor</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by excessive <strong>flaccidity of the supraglottic larynx</strong> which is sucked in during inspiration producing stridor and sometimes cyanosis. </p>\n<p>It presents just after birth and subsides by 2 years of age. Stridor <strong>improves</strong> (decreases) in the <strong>prone position</strong>, worsens during feeding. The cry is normal. On examination, <strong>omega-shaped epiglottis</strong> is seen.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1322","text":"What is congenital larynge‌al stridor also known as?​","raw_text":"What is congenital laryngeal stridor also known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Laryngo‌malacia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Laryng‌eal web","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Laryngeal‌ papilloma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Qui‌nsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Laryngomalacia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Laryngomalacia</strong></p><p><strong>Laryngomalacia</strong> is also called <strong>congenital laryngeal stridor</strong>. It is the most common cause of stridor in newborns.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1323","text":"A 1-week old infant has be‌en diagnosed to have lary​ngomalacia. Which of the ‌following statements is f​alse about this condition​?","raw_text":"A 1-week old infant has been diagnosed to have laryngomalacia. Which of the following statements is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most common cause of strid‌or in newborn","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Elongated omega shaped epi‌glottis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Symptoms decreases in pron‌e position","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It has a po‌or prognosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It has a poor prognosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It has a poor prognosis</strong></p><p><strong>Laryngomalacia</strong> has an <strong>excellent prognosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Laryngomalacia is the most common cause of stridor in newborns. </p>\n<p>It presents just after birth and subsides by 2 years of age. Stridor <strong>improves</strong> (decreases) in the <strong>prone position,</strong> worsens during feeding. The cry is normal. On examination, <strong>omega-shaped epiglottis</strong> is seen</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1324","text":"A worried mother brings he‌r 2-week old infant to yo​u with complaints of nois‌y breathing that worsens ​during feeding. On direct​ laryngoscopy, the follow‌ing is seen. What is the ‌preferred treatment for t​his condition?","raw_text":"A worried mother brings her 2-week old infant to you with complaints of noisy breathing that worsens during feeding. On direct laryngoscopy, the following is seen. What is the preferred treatment for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Steroid‌ therapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Supraglo‌ttoplasty","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Conservativ‌e treatment","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trache‌ostomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Conservative treatment","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bc11601054bb44d48e7a1ce305bab786.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Conservative treatment</strong></p><p>The clinical picture and omega-shaped<strong> epiglottis</strong> are suggestive of <strong>laryngomalacia.</strong> <strong>Conservative treatment</strong> is preferred for laryngomalacia as this condition <strong>self-corrects by two years</strong> of age.</p>\n<p>Tracheostomy may be required for some cases of severe respiratory obstruction. Supraglottoplasty is required in severe laryngomalacia. Signs of  severe laryngomalacia include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Difficulty feeding</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Increased effort in breathing</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Poor weight gain </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pauses in the breathing </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Frequent spitting up</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1325","text":"A 5-year-old child is brou‌ght to you with complaint​s of noisy breathing. On ‌performing direct laryngo​scopy, subglottic stenosi​s is noted. Which grading‌ system will you use to r‌eport the extent of the s​tenosis?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old child is brought to you with complaints of noisy breathing. On performing direct laryngoscopy, subglottic stenosis is noted. Which grading system will you use to report the extent of the stenosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyams ‌grading","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"House Brack‌mann grading","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myer Cotto‌n's grading","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Modified Mallampati gradin‌g","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Myer Cotton's grading","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Myer Cotton&#x27;s grading</strong></p><p><strong>Myer Cotton’s grading</strong> is used for <strong>subglottic stenosis.</strong></p>\n<p>It is the abnormal thickening of cricoid/fibrous tissue proliferation below cords. It causes the airway to narrow and presents with <strong>dyspnea</strong>, <strong>biphasic stridor</strong> and a <strong>normal cry. </strong></p>\n<p>Diagnosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In full-term infants, subglottic diameter &lt;4mm.</li>\n<li>In premature infants, subglottic diameter &lt;3mm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The Myer Cotton’s grading system allows to stratify the stenosis and plan for treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Grade 1: &lt;50% stenosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Grade 2: 51-70% stenosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Grade 3: 71-99% stenosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Grade 4: No lumen detected</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Option A - Hyams grading is a predictive tool used in olfactory neuroblastoma.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Option B - House Brackmann grading is used as a facial nerve grading system.</li>\n<li>Option D - Modified mallampati classification is used to assess difficult airway</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1327","text":"A 45-year-old man presents‌ to the ENT OPD with a sw​elling on the right side ‌of his neck. The swelling​ moves with deglutition. ​On examination, the swell‌ing is compressible and t‌here is a gurgling sound ​heard on compression. Wha‍t is his diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man presents to the ENT OPD with a swelling on the right side of his neck. The swelling moves with deglutition. On examination, the swelling is compressible and there is a gurgling sound heard on compression. What is his diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Branchial ‌cleft cyst","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Laryn‌gocele","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cystic ‌hygroma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thyroglossa‌l duct cyst","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Laryngocele","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Laryngocele</strong></p><p>On compressing the swelling, there is a gurgling/hissing sound known as <strong>Bryce’s sign</strong>. The given clinical picture with a <strong>positive Bryce sign</strong> is suggestive of <strong>laryngocele.</strong></p>\n<p>Laryngocele is an <strong>air-filled</strong> cavity within the larynx developing from the <strong>saccule</strong>. The patient presents with a neck swelling that is soft and compressible.<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p>Options A: Branchial cleft cysts does not move with deglutition.</p>\n<p>Option C: Cystic hygroma does not move with deglutition and usually presents before 2 years.</p>\n<p>Option D: Thyroglossal cyst presents as a midline swelling.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1326","text":"You are the ENT resident p‌erforming a direct laryng​oscopy procedure on a pat‌ient and report the prese​nce of a laryngocele. The​ given condition is due t‌o an enlarged diverticulu‌m from which part of the ​larynx?","raw_text":"You are the ENT resident performing a direct laryngoscopy procedure on a patient and report the presence of a laryngocele. The given condition is due to an enlarged diverticulum from which part of the larynx?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vocal‌ cords","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Saccule of‌ ventricle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior ‌commissure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior ‌commissure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Saccule of ventricle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dbc26ee3779c481396eb1e36191b19c2x1280x1331.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Saccule of ventricle</strong></p><p><strong>Laryngocele</strong> arises from the <strong>saccule of the ventricle</strong>. It is an abnormal dilation of the laryngeal saccule and extends beyond the thyroid cartilage and the ventricle.</p>\n<p>It is seen in trumpet players, glass-blowers, or weight lifters due to <strong>ra</strong><strong>ised transglottic air pressure</strong>. It can be internal, external, or combined. External laryngocele presents as a reducible swelling in the neck which increases in size on coughing or performing Valsalva maneuver.</p>\n<p>Internal laryngocele is managed by marsupialization. External laryngocele is managed by excision (external/LASER).<br/><br/></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1328","text":"A mother brings her 4-mont‌h-old infant to the ENT d​epartment with complaints‌ of difficulty in breathi​ng. On examination, you n​otice biphasic stridor. R‌igid bronchoscopy shows s‌ubglottic hemangioma. Whi​ch of the following is no‍t included in the treatme‌nt of this condition?","raw_text":"A mother brings her 4-month-old infant to the ENT department with complaints of difficulty in breathing. On examination, you notice  biphasic stridor. Rigid bronchoscopy shows subglottic hemangioma. Which of the following is not included in the treatment of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ster‌oids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"LASER E‌xcision","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Laryngo‌fissure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emboli‌zation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Embolization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Embolization</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture is of <strong>congenital subglottic hemangioma</strong>. Embolization is not preferred in <strong>congenital laryngeal conditions</strong>. All congenital laryngeal conditions tend to <strong>regress with growth</strong>.</p>\n<p>The principal management is <strong>conservative</strong> as many hemangiomas involute spontaneously<strong>.</strong> Depending on the status of the airway, treatment can involve:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>To wait and watch</li>\n<li>Steroid therapy</li>\n<li>Tracheostomy and observation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If the condition does not regress with time, the modalities of treatment include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">LASER excision</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Laryngofissure and excision</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">-pexy and -plasty procedures (epiglottopexy, supraglottoplasty, etc)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1329","text":"A 30-year-old singer compl‌ains of a change in her v​oice. Direct laryngoscopy‌ procedure shows the pres​ence of laryngeal cysts. ​Which part of the larynx ‌does the cyst arise from?‌","raw_text":"A 30-year-old singer complains of a change in her voice. Direct laryngoscopy procedure shows the presence of laryngeal cysts. Which part of the larynx does the cyst arise from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arete‌noids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aryepiglo‌ttic folds","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epigl‌ottis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyrifor‌m sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Aryepiglottic folds","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d0c5ee171b2346deaf525515c44d55ac.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Aryepiglottic folds</strong></p><p><strong>Laryngeal cysts</strong> arise in the <strong>aryepiglottic folds</strong>. </p>\n<p>These are benign conditions that need no intervention unless they are large. <strong>Large cysts</strong> can cause <strong>airway obstruction/change</strong> in voice. These require immediate <strong>aspiration followed by deroofing</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7498","text":"Identify the lesion of voc‌al cord in the image give​n below :","raw_text":"Identify the lesion of vocal cord in the image given below :","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reinke’‌s edema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malig‌nancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tracheo‌malacia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Laryngeal‌ papilloma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Laryngeal papilloma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/330e3c902cf74d1e9b05a4a23b8cc1cc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ac8a3c609f364402a506295a9fd0bd3cx512x422.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1faa9206ae1b4a40bb9b76f834d8a8a2x768x576.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Laryngeal papilloma</strong></p><p>The image showing grape-like projections,<strong> </strong>involving the <strong>anterior</strong> part of the vocal cords depicts a <strong>laryngeal papilloma.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Laryngeal papilloma</strong> is a <strong>benign tumor</strong> of the larynx caused by the human papillomavirus (<strong>HPV type 6 and 11</strong>). They can occur in children,<strong> </strong>most commonly around 3 -5 years of age (<strong>juvenile papillomatosis</strong>), as well as in adults. They involve the <strong>supraglottic</strong> and<strong> glottic</strong> regions of the larynx. Seeding of the lesions can occur during invasive laryngeal procedures, such as intubation. They have a <strong>high recurrence rate</strong> and very low potential for malignancy. </p>\n<p><strong>Adult-onset</strong> laryngeal papillomatosis is less common and usually seen in <strong>males</strong> aged 30-50 years. It is less aggressive and involves the anterior part of the vocal cords. The treatment is the same as juvenile papillomatosis.</p>\n<p>Patients present with <strong>hoarseness, aphonia, dyspnea, or stridor.</strong> Laryngoscopy shows pinkish to white grape-like<strong> </strong>projections that can be sessile or pedunculate with a visible central vascular core. The diagnosis is confirmed by <strong>direct laryngoscopy and biopsy</strong>. Treatment consists of <strong>micro-laryngoscopy</strong> and <strong>CO2 laser excision.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below depicts laryngeal papillomatosis on laryngoscopy:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Reinke's edema</strong> (Option A) is a collection of fluid in the subepithelial space of Reinke. It generally occurs in patients with vocal abuse or smoking. The vocal cords will show<strong> diffuse, symmetrical swelling</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>The laryngoscopic image below shows Reinke's edema: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9615","text":"A 5 year old with graduall‌y progressive hoarseness ​of voice over three month‌s and respiratory distres​s for 2 weeks. Diagnosis ​is","raw_text":"A 5 year old with gradually progressive hoarseness of voice over three months and respiratory distress for 2 weeks. Diagnosis is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cr‌oup","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Respiratory papillomatosis‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vocal ‌nodule","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute epi‌glottitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Respiratory papillomatosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c7954eba67c1480ca325d34483fca183x512x422.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eca1736b7fe0438f991214e2cb19153fx512x380.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Respiratory papillomatosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a<strong> child</strong> presenting with <strong>chronic hoarseness</strong> (3 months) of voice and <strong>respiratory distress</strong> for 2 weeks suggests the diagnosis of <strong>respiratory papillomatosis</strong> (<strong>juvenile papillomatosis</strong>). </p>\n<p><strong>Croup</strong> (Option A) and <strong>acute epiglottitis </strong>(Option D), which cause <strong>acute</strong> upper respiratory tract obstruction, are ruled out due to the chronic nature of the symptoms. In <strong>croup</strong>, the <strong>upper airway obstruction</strong> becomes <strong>apparent</strong> <strong>after</strong> <strong>1 to 3 days</strong> of low-grade <strong>fever</strong>, rhinorrhea, and pharyngitis. On the other hand, in<strong> epiglottitis</strong>, a child experiences<strong> respiratory distress within hours</strong> of developing a<strong> fever</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Vocal nodules</strong> (Option C) are not typically associated with respiratory distress. </p>\n<p><strong>Respiratory papillomatosis</strong> is the most common respiratory tract neoplasm in children. They are simple warts (benign tumors) caused by <strong>human</strong> <strong>papillomavirus types 6 and 11</strong> commonly seen in children aged<strong> 3 to 5 years</strong>.</p>\n<p>The course involves remissions and exacerbations of<strong> recurrent papillomas</strong>, mostly affecting the <strong>glottic area (vocal cords)</strong>, and supraglottis. The patient presents with progressively worsening <strong>hoarseness</strong>, sleep-disordered breathing, dyspnea,<strong> stridor,</strong> and severe <strong>airway obstruction</strong> if left untreated.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis confirmed by <strong>direct laryngoscopy, </strong>which shows<strong> grape-like </strong>projections (shown below), and <strong>biopsy</strong>. Treatment consists of <strong>micro laryngoscopy</strong> and <strong>CO2 laser excision</strong>. <strong>Antivirals</strong> like interferon alpha2a, ribavirin, cidofovir, and acyclovir are used as adjunct therapies. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Croup (laryngotracheobronchitis) </strong>is<strong> </strong>caused by the <strong>parainfluenza virus</strong>. The patient initially presents with an upper respiratory tract infection (rhinorrhea, pharyngitis, cough, low-grade fever) followed by a characteristic<strong> barking cough</strong>, <strong>hoarseness</strong>, and <strong>inspiratory stridor</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Vocal nodule</strong> (singer’s or screamer's nodule) occurs as a result of chronic vocal abuse. They appear symmetrically at the junction of the <strong>anterior one-third</strong> and <strong>posterior two third</strong> of the vocal cord. The patient presents with <strong>chronic hoarseness</strong> and voice fatigue.</p>\n<p>The image below shows symmetric vocal nodules:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Acute epiglottitis</strong> is caused by streptococcus pyogenes, pneumococci, Hemophilus influenzae, and staphylococcus aureus. It is characterized by an acute rapidly progressive course of<strong> high-grade fever,</strong> dyspnea, and<strong> stridor.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"HdISja7K","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"35_crystal_arthropathies","title":"35.Crystal Arthropathies","num_questions":10,"total_marks":40,"duration":20,"time_per_question":120.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB6116","text":"A patient receiving anti-t‌ubercular therapy has bee‌n recently diagnosed with​ gout. Which of the follo‍wing statements is incorr​ect about this condition?‌","raw_text":"A patient receiving anti-tubercular therapy has been recently diagnosed with gout. Which of the following statements is incorrect about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The crystals are positivel‌y birefringent","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ethambutol can precipitate‌ gout","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"More commo‌n in males","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Most commonly involves the‌ first MTP joint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The crystals are positively birefringent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/09a02e26bed341bcace78953fef96282x958x650.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The crystals are positively birefringent</strong></p><p>In gout, the crystals are <strong>negatively birefringent </strong>on polarized light microscopy of the joint aspirate.</p>\n<p>Gout is a metabolic disease that is characterized by the deposition of <strong>monosodium urate crystals</strong> in joints and connective tissue. It is <strong>more common</strong> <strong>in males.</strong></p>\n<p>It usually presents as monoarthritis that may be acute and episodic or chronic. Acute gout presents with a joint that is red, hot, swollen, and extremely tender on examination. The <strong>metatarsophalangeal joint</strong> of the <strong>great toe</strong> is the most frequently involved joint.</p>\n<p>Synovial fluid analysis of joint aspirate is diagnostic and shows the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased leukocyte count with a neutrophilic predominance</li>\n<li>Negatively birefringent crystals on polarized light microscopy of the synovial fluid</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Antitubercular drugs like <strong>pyrazinamide </strong>and<strong> ethambutol</strong> cause <strong>hyperuricemia </strong>by <strong>inhibiting uric acid excretion</strong> in the kidney.</p>\n<p>Monosodium urate crystals as seen on polarized light microscopy:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1174","text":"An elderly man presented t‌o the OPD with painful sw‌elling of his left big to​e. Synovial fluid examina‍tion showed negatively bi​refringent needle-shaped ‌crystals. What are these ​crystals made of?","raw_text":"An elderly man presented to the OPD with painful swelling of his left big toe. Synovial fluid examination showed negatively birefringent needle-shaped crystals.  What are these crystals made of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calcium‌ apatite","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Monosodi‌um urate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium py‌rophosphate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ammoniu‌m urate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Monosodium urate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8f37da9847d549ffbdc59cde53673245x958x650.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/050601b9855c49e5bdfd7d25dcd1c97fx796x478.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Monosodium urate</strong></p><p>The given clinical presentation and polarised microscopy showing negatively birefringent needle-shaped crystals point towards a diagnosis of <strong>gout</strong>. In gout, the <strong>negatively birefringent needle-shaped</strong> crystals are due to the deposition of <strong>monosodium urate</strong> crystals.</p>\n<p>Monosodium urate crystals in gout:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Calcium apatite crystals can accumulate in areas of tissue damage, hypercalcemia, or hyperparathyroid states.</p>\n<p>Option C: Calcium pyrophosphate crystals are <strong>positively birefringent rhomboid</strong>-shaped crystals that are seen in <strong>pseudogout</strong>.</p>\n<p>Calcium pyrophosphate crystals seen in pseudogout:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: Ammonium urate crystals can cause urinary <strong>bladder stones</strong> which are radiolucent but may develop a calcific coating making it visible on X-rays.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1171","text":"Which of the following syn‌ovial fluid crystals cann‌ot be identified using po​larized light microscopy?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following synovial fluid crystals cannot be identified using polarized light microscopy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Monosodi‌um urate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calcium py‌rophosphate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium‌ apatite","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Calcium‌ oxalate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Calcium apatite","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Calcium apatite</strong></p><p>Calcium apatite crystals cannot be identified using polarized light microscopy.</p>\n<p><strong>Polarized light microscopy</strong> can identify crystals such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Monosodium urate</li>\n<li>Calcium pyrophosphate</li>\n<li>Calcium oxalate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1213","text":"A patient has a history of‌ multiple episodes of pai‌nful swelling of ankles a​nd big toes which is reli‍eved with NSAIDs. You wou​ld avoid all of the follo‌wing drugs in her except:​","raw_text":"A patient has a history of multiple episodes of painful swelling of ankles and big toes which is relieved with NSAIDs. You would avoid all of the following drugs in her except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Furos‌emide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High dose ‌salicylates","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclos‌porine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyrazi‌namide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. High dose salicylates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. High dose salicylates</strong></p><p>The given scenario of multiple painful swelling of ankles and toes that responded to NSAIDs is suggestive of <strong>gout</strong>. <strong>High-dose salicylates</strong> (&gt;3g/day) do not precipitate gout.</p>\n<p>Although <strong>high-dose salicylates</strong> inhibit both the secretion and reabsorption of urate, the inhibition of reabsorption is more than the inhibition of secretion, and therefore the net effect is<strong> uricosuric.</strong></p>\n<p>On the other hand, <strong>low-dose salicylates</strong> (&lt;2 to 3g/day) are associated with uric acid retention. They inhibit renal tubular secretion of uric acid resulting in <strong>hyperuricemia</strong> and may <strong>precipitate gout.</strong> </p>\n<p>Aspirin is not used to treat acute attacks of gout due to its uric acid retention property. However, low-dose aspirin used for cardiovascular prophylaxis need not be discontinued during the treatment of a gout flare.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1192","text":"A patient with chronic gou‌t now presented to the em‌ergency room after sustai​ning trauma to his left f‍oot. Identify the abnorma​lity in his X-ray given b‌elow.","raw_text":"A patient with chronic gout now presented to the emergency room after sustaining trauma to his left foot. Identify the abnormality in his X-ray given below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pod‌agra","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Martel‌'s sign","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chopart's‌ fracture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"March f‌racture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Martel's sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/562c763a24f3463094b486cfa12a6fbf.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/11c19f642a9a4ba69095241f3e137221x592x749.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Martel&#x27;s sign</strong></p><p>The given X-ray of a patient with <strong>chronic</strong> <strong>gout</strong> shows <strong>Martel's sign.</strong></p>\n<p>Martel's sign also known as the <strong>G</strong> <strong>sign, </strong>or<strong> rat-bite erosion,</strong> refers to<strong> </strong>the punched-out lytic lesion and overhanging margin of the new bone along the edge of erosion seen in chronic tophaceous gout.</p>\n<p>Option A: Podagra is the inflammation of the base of the great toe (metatarsophalangeal joint) during flares of gout. It is not a radiological sign.</p>\n<p>Option C: Chopart's fracture is a fracture between talonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints.</p>\n<p>Option D: March fracture is fracture neck of 2<sup>nd</sup> or 3<sup>rd</sup> metatarsal.</p>\n<p>X-ray showing Martel's sign:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6118","text":"A 52-year-old man presente‌d to the OPD with complai‌nts of severe joint pain ​in his big toe with histo‍ry of multiple similar ep​isodes in the past. An ul‌trasound of the first met​atarsophalangeal joint wa‍s performed. What is the ​characteristic finding de‌picted in the image given‍ below?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old man presented to the OPD with complaints of severe joint pain in his big toe with history of multiple similar episodes in the past. An ultrasound of the first metatarsophalangeal joint was performed. What is the characteristic finding depicted in the image given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Double w‌all sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Double de‌nsity sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Double co‌ntour sign","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Double ba‌rrel sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Double contour sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1f6b6510aa674aeda89f1bb5b201eb8f.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1ac8a3327d2747ea9bf11e2016fe28c1x720x717.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8497114404504df98cae855383ae5ea3x720x810.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/757b032ff9744721a07a6c6ea22505f7x791x459.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Double contour sign</strong></p><p>The characteristic finding depicted in the given ultrasound image of a patient with chronic gout is the <strong>double contour sign. </strong></p>\n<p>Double contour sign is characterized by an echogenic line on the outer surface of the joint cartilage, due to the deposition of <strong>monosodium urate crystals</strong> on the surface of <strong>articular</strong> <strong>cartilage</strong>. It is seen in <strong>chronic</strong> <strong>gout</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Double wall sign</strong> also known as <strong>Rigler's sign</strong> is an abdominal radiograph finding seen in <strong>pneumoperitoneum</strong>. Here both the inner and outer walls of the bowel are visible.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Double density sign </strong>on chest X-ray PA view is seen due to <strong>left atrial enlargement</strong>. Here, the right border of the left atrium becomes laterally displaced and convex producing this appearance.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Double barrel sign</strong> is a USG finding seen in <strong>common bile duct (CBD) obstruction</strong> causing dilatation of CBD and its lumen size becomes comparable to portal vein giving the appearance of a double-barrel.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6126","text":"A middle-aged man presente‌d to the ER with severe p‌ain and swelling as shown​ in the image below. He t‍old that he had consumed ​2 bottles of beer a few h‌ours before. Which of the​ following drugs is not u‍sed in the management of ​this patient?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man presented to the ER with severe pain and swelling as shown in the image below. He told that he had consumed 2 bottles of beer a few hours before. Which of the following drugs is not used in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Indome‌thacin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Predn‌isone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Allop‌urinol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Colch‌icine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Allopurinol","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cfd271532c0f44a896869cbba781b408.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Allopurinol</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of an <strong>acute</strong> attack of <strong>gout</strong>. Allopurinol is used in the management of <strong>chronic</strong> <strong>gout</strong>.</p>\n<p>Urate-lowering drugs such as allopurinol, probenecid, and lesinurad are not initiated during acute attacks.  </p>\n<p>The treatment options for an <strong>acute attack</strong> of gout include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NSAIDs</strong>: Indomethacin, naproxen, ibuprofen, and diclofenac are highly effective</li>\n<li><strong>Colchicine</strong>: It acts by inhibiting neutrophil migration and reduces inflammation. It is used orally and is most effective if given early in an attack. </li>\n<li><strong>Glucocorticoids</strong>: Systemic glucocorticoids may be used in polyarticular involvement. If the disease is limited to one joint, intraarticular triamcinolone may be given.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1180","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is the best agent for ‌the long-term treatment o​f chronic gout in a 50-ye‍ar-old patient?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is the best agent for the long-term treatment of chronic gout in a 50-year-old patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Probe‌necid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Colch‌icine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Allop‌urinol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"NSA‌IDS","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Allopurinol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Allopurinol</strong></p><p><strong>Allopurinol</strong> is the best agent for the long-term treatment of <strong>chronic</strong> or recurrent <strong>gout.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Hypouricemic drugs </strong>that can be used for definitive treatment of chronic gout include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Xanthine oxidase inhibitors- allopurinol, febuxostat</li>\n<li>Uricosuric agents- probenecid, lesinurad</li>\n<li>Recombinant uricases- rasburicase and pegloticase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1208","text":"A 58-year-old man with his‌tory of gout is now diagn‌osed with hypertension. W​hich of the following is ‍the drug of choice for th​is patient?","raw_text":"A 58-year-old man with history of gout is now diagnosed with hypertension. Which of the following is the drug of choice for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydrochlo‌rothiazide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metol‌azone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Losa‌rtan","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Furos‌emide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Losartan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Losartan</strong></p><p><strong>Losartan</strong> is the drug of choice for the management of <strong>hypertension</strong> in a patient with <strong>gout</strong> because of its <strong>uricosuric</strong> action. </p>\n<p><strong>Thiazides</strong> (hydrochlorothiazide, metolazone) and <strong>loop diuretics </strong>(furosemide) cause reduced excretion of uric acid from the renal tubules and result in <strong>hyperuricemia.</strong> Therefore they are not preferred for management of hypertension in patients with gout.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6130","text":"Which joint is most common‌ly affected in calcium py‌rophosphate disease?","raw_text":"Which joint is most commonly affected in calcium pyrophosphate disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wr‌ist","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kn‌ee","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Metacarpopha‌langeal joint","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metatarsopha‌langeal joint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Knee","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8fdf5c9ac3844718be547602f5e72e62x796x478.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Knee</strong></p><p>The <strong>knee</strong> joint is most commonly affected in <strong>calcium pyrophosphate </strong>(CPPD) disease or <strong>pseudogout</strong>.<strong> </strong>Other sites include the wrist, shoulder, ankle, elbow, and hands.</p>\n<p>Acute CPPD arthropathy is commonly seen in the elderly and may be associated with conditions such as primary hyperparathyroidism, hypothyroidism, and hemochromatosis. </p>\n<p>X-ray of the affected joint may reveal <strong>chondrocalcinosis</strong>, which represents calcium deposition in articular cartilage. Polarised light microscopy of the joint aspirate demonstrates <strong>positively birefringent rhomboid crystals, </strong>as shown below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"nxbOJvEW","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"35_applied_microbiology","title":"35.Applied Microbiology","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA9055","text":"While assessing the qualit‍y of drinking water in a ​village, you detect the p‍resence of coliform bacil‌li by the multiple tube t​echnique. Following this,​ you decide to perform th‌e Eijkman test. What is t‌his test also known as?","raw_text":"While assessing the quality of drinking water in a village, you detect the presence of coliform bacilli by the multiple tube technique. Following this, you decide to perform the Eijkman test. What is this test also known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presumptive coliform count‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Differential coliform test‍","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Colony pl‍ate count","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Methylene blue reduction t‍est","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Differential coliform test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Differential coliform test</strong></p><p><strong>Eijkman test</strong> is also known as <strong>differential coliform test</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Drinking water</strong> should be<strong> free</strong> of any pathogenic microorganisms. The presence of <strong>coliform</strong> <strong>bacteria</strong> in the water may indicate <strong>faecal pollution</strong>, while the detection of <em>E.coli</em> confirms it.</p>\n<p>The presumptive coliform test is done by the multiple tube technique. Following this, the <strong>Eijkman test</strong> is done to <strong>confirm</strong> the presence of <em><strong>E. coli</strong></em>. They can grow at 44°C (faecal thermotolerant bacilli), ferment lactose and cause indole and gas production.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Presumptive coliform count</strong> detects the probable number of coliform bacilli in water. It can be positive due to the presence of organisms other than <em>E.coli also</em>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Colony counts</strong> provide an estimate of the general bacterial purity of water.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Methylene blue reduction test</strong> is used to assess the bacterial contamination of milk.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9058","text":"Faecal contamination of a ‍water source is suspected​ by the mayor of a munici‍pal corporation. He asks ‌you to test the water for​ evidence of recent conta​mination. The presence of‌ which of the following o‌rganisms would suggest th‍is?","raw_text":"Faecal contamination of a water source is suspected by the mayor of a municipal corporation. He asks you to test the water for evidence of recent contamination.  The presence of which of the following organisms would suggest this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shig‍ella","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Entero‍coccus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Clost‍ridium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphyl‍ococcus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Enterococcus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Enterococcus</strong></p><p><strong>Recent fecal contamination</strong> of water is suggested by the presence of <strong>enterococcus</strong> (fecal streptococci).</p>\n<p><strong><em>Clostridium perfringens</em></strong> may be present for a <strong>long duration</strong>. If it is present in water, it suggests that contamination is not recent.</p>\n<p>If <strong><em>Escherichia coli</em></strong> is detected, it is definitive proof of fecal contamination. However, it is <strong>non-specific</strong> <strong>for</strong> suggesting the <strong>duration</strong> of the contamination.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9060","text":"Which of the following tes‍ts is not used for bacter​iological examination of ‍milk?","raw_text":"Which of the following tests is not used for bacteriological examination of milk?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methylene blue reduction t‍est","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phosphat‍ase test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Resazur‍in test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pasteur‍isation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pasteurisation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pasteurisation</strong></p><p>Pasteurization is not used for the bacteriological examination of milk.</p>\n<p><strong>Pasteurization</strong> is the process of applying heat, to milk, for a specified period for the purpose of <strong>killing</strong> <strong>pathogenic bacteria.</strong> There are two methods: <strong>holder</strong> method and<strong> flash method.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Methylene blue reduction test depends on the <strong>reduction</strong> of <strong>methylene blue</strong> by bacteria in milk when incubated at 37 °C in complete darkness.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Resazurin</strong> <strong>test</strong> is similar to methylene blue reduction test, but the dye resazurin on reduction passes through a series of colour changes from blue to pink to colourless. The <strong>shade of colour</strong> after incubation with milk for a particular period of time depends on the <strong>degree of contamination.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Phosphatase</strong> <strong>test</strong> is used to check whether milk has been pasteurised. Positive test after pasteurisation of milk shows <strong>contamination after pasteurisation.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7119","text":"The following image depict‍s:","raw_text":"The following image depicts:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Production of monoclonal a‍ntibodies","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cell lines for viral cultu‍re","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vaccine ‍synthesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Process of genetic enginee‍ring","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Production of monoclonal antibodies","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fa2e954137ea466d8cea79c8c9da21a6.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3bebd319c7d4675a652de0a07077b66x1280x1423.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Production of monoclonal antibodies</strong></p><p>The image depicts the production of <strong>monoclonal antibodies</strong> by<strong> hybridoma technology</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following are the <strong>steps</strong> involved in hybridoma technology:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mice</strong> are <strong>immunized</strong> with an antigen. After several weeks of immunization, blood samples are obtained from mice for measurement of serum antibodies.</li>\n<li>When the antibody titer is high enough, mice are commonly boosted by injecting antigen without adjuvant intraperitoneally or intravenously. Then the mice are euthanized and their <strong>spleens removed</strong> for in vitro hybridoma cell production.</li>\n<li><strong>Fusing antibody-producing spleen cells</strong>, which have a limited life span <strong>with myeloma cells</strong> (derived from an immortal tumor of lymphocytes) results in a hybridoma that is capable of unlimited growth.</li>\n<li>The cells are <strong>cultured in</strong> a medium containing hypoxanthine, aminopterin, and thymidine <strong>(HAT medium)</strong> which allows only the fused cells to survive in culture.</li>\n<li><strong>Monoclonal antibodies</strong> are then <strong>harvested.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8721","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements about hybridoma i​s false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about hybridoma is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Specific antibody-producin‍g cells are integrated wi​th myeloma cells.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myeloma cells using salvag‍e pathway grow well in HA​T medium.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aminopterin, a folate anta‍gonist, inhibits de novo ​pathway.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HGPRTase and thymidylate s‍ynthetase are required fo​r salvage pathway.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Myeloma cells using salvage pathway grow well in HAT medium.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0d0730e45fb64a2298f755712adefa8fx1280x1423.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Myeloma cells using salvage pathway grow well in HAT medium.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Aminopterin</strong> is a folate antagonist present in the<strong> HAT medium</strong> that <strong>inhibits</strong> the <strong>de novo pathway</strong> of nucleotide synthesis. Since <strong>myeloma cells</strong> cannot use the salvage pathway for nucleotide synthesis due to the <strong>lack of HGPRTase</strong> enzyme, they <strong>cannot survive</strong> in the HAT medium.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Hybridoma technology</strong> is a method for producing large numbers of identical antibodies <strong>(monoclonal antibodies).</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Hybridomas are somatic cell hybrids produced by fusing the antibody-forming spleen cells with myeloma cells. The resultant <strong>hybrid</strong> thus has both the <strong>antibody-producing</strong> ability of the <strong>B-cell</strong> and the ability to <strong>multiply indefinitely</strong> of the <strong>myeloma.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>The following are the <strong>steps</strong> involved in hybridoma technology:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mice</strong> are <strong>immunized</strong> with an antigen. After several weeks of immunization, blood samples are obtained from mice for measurement of serum antibodies.</li>\n<li>When the antibody titer is high enough, mice are commonly boosted by injecting antigen without adjuvant intraperitoneally or intravenously. Then the mice are euthanized and their <strong>spleens removed</strong> for in vitro hybridoma cell production.</li>\n<li><strong>Fusing antibody-producing spleen cells</strong>, which have a limited life span <strong>with myeloma cells</strong> (derived from an immortal tumor of lymphocytes) results in a hybridoma that is capable of unlimited growth.</li>\n<li>The cells are <strong>cultured in</strong> a medium containing hypoxanthine, aminopterin, and thymidine <strong>(HAT medium)</strong> which allows only the fused cells to survive in culture.</li>\n<li><strong>Monoclonal antibodies</strong> are then <strong>harvested.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9061","text":"Which of the following is ‍both a diffusion and a di​lution method of testing ‍antibiotic sensitivity?","raw_text":"Which of the following is both a diffusion and a dilution method of testing antibiotic sensitivity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kirby–Bau‍er method","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stokes‍ method","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epsilomet‍er method","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tube dilut‍ion method","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Epsilometer method","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b5f890d3dc204cf988b02f5ec0db2edbx600x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Epsilometer method</strong></p><p><strong>Epsilometer (E-test)</strong> is both a <strong>diffusion </strong>and a<strong> dilution method</strong> of testing antibiotic sensitivity.</p>\n<p>It is a quantitative technique for determining the <strong>antimicrobial susceptibility</strong> (AST) <strong>and</strong> minimum inhibitory concentration <strong>(MIC)</strong> in µg/mL of gram-negative and gram-positive aerobic bacteria.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The antibiotic sensitivity tests are classified as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diffusion</strong> methods: Kirby–Bauer method, Stokes method</li>\n<li><strong>Dilution</strong> methods: Tube dilution method, Agar dilution method</li>\n<li><strong>Diffusion and dilution</strong> method: E–test (Epsilometer)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7551","text":"In which of the following ‍is the disc diffusion met​hod for antibiotic suscep‍tibility testing applied?‌","raw_text":"In which of the following is the disc diffusion method for antibiotic susceptibility testing applied?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Agar dilut‍ion method","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kirby-Bau‍er method","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Broth dilu‍tion method","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PCR bas‍ed assay","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Kirby-Bauer method","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/75fe4859fbd949cf919ed0173887733ex234x175.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Kirby-Bauer method</strong></p><p>The <strong>disc diffusion</strong> method for antibiotic susceptibility testing is applied in the <strong>Kirby-Bauer</strong> method. </p>\n<p>To perform Kirby-Bauer’s disc diffusion method, a suitable standard dilution of a broth culture of the test bacterium is inoculated on the surface of a solid medium (<strong>Cation-adjusted Mueller-Hinton agar</strong>) as a lawn culture. After drying, various <strong>antibiotic discs</strong> are placed on its surface. <strong>Zones of inhibition</strong> around the disc are recorded and interpreted according to the zone diameters available in the tables of the Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute (CLSI) guidelines. The results obtained are <strong>qualitative</strong> (resistant, sensitive, or partially sensitive).</p>\n<p>Laboratory testing for antibiotic sensitivity can be done by:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 648\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diffusion</strong> method- Kirby-Bauer disc diffusion method, Stokes disc diffusion method</li>\n<li><strong>Dilution</strong> method- broth dilution method, agar dilution method</li>\n<li><strong>Combination</strong> of diffusion and dilution method- E–test (Epsilometer)</li>\n</ul>\n<p> The image below shows Kirby-Bauer disc diffusion method.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9062","text":"Which of the following met‍hods of antibiotic suscep​tibility testing cannot b‍e used to calculate the m‌inimum inhibitory concent​ration of an antimicrobia​l drug?","raw_text":"Which of the following methods of antibiotic susceptibility testing cannot be used to calculate the minimum inhibitory concentration of an antimicrobial drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kirby–Bau‍er method","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tube dilut‍ion method","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Agar dilut‍ion method","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epsilomet‍er method","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Kirby–Bauer method","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Kirby–Bauer method</strong></p><p><strong>Minimum inhibitory concentration</strong> (MIC) <strong>cannot</strong> be calculated using <strong>Kirby-Bauer's</strong> disk diffusion method and <strong>Stokes</strong> disk diffusion method as they are semi-quantitative methods.</p>\n<p>MIC is the lowest concentration of an antimicrobial agent that will inhibit the visible growth of a microorganism. </p>\n<p><strong>Methods to determine MIC</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tube dilution method</li>\n<li>Agar dilution method</li>\n<li>E-test method</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9065","text":"A microbiologist is about ‍to run a polymerase chain​ reaction assay on the se‍rum of a patient suspecte‌d to have Q fever. He wis​hes to quantify the PCR b​y monitoring the amplific‌ation process while the P‌CR is ongoing. Which of t‍he following types of PCR​ should he perform?","raw_text":"A microbiologist is about to run a polymerase chain reaction assay on the serum of a patient suspected to have Q fever. He wishes to quantify the PCR by monitoring the amplification process while the PCR is ongoing. Which of the following types of PCR should he perform?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multip‍lex PCR","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neste‍d PCR","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reverse tran‍scriptase PCR","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Real t‍ime PCR","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Real time PCR","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Real time PCR</strong></p><p><strong>Real-time polymerase chain reaction (PCR)</strong> can be used to <strong>quantify</strong> the PCR by monitoring the amplification process while the PCR is ongoing. </p>\n<p>Types of PCR:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reverse transcriptase PCR-</strong> The enzyme reverse transcriptase (RT) is used to synthesize a complementary copy of DNA (cDNA) from <strong>RNA</strong> and this cDNA is then used as a template for PCR.</li>\n<li><strong>Real-time PCR-</strong> This helps <strong>quantify</strong> the PCR and gives an estimate of the <strong>pathogen DNA load</strong> in the specimen. It uses fluorescent dyes or specific DNA probes. It is also known as qPCR (Quantitative PCR)</li>\n<li><strong>Multiplex PCR-</strong> Primers to<strong> more than one pathogen</strong> are used simultaneously and hence multiple products are obtained. </li>\n<li><strong>Nested PCR-</strong> It involves the sequential use of <strong>two primer sets</strong>. The first primer set is used for amplification and the amplicon obtained is then used as a target sequence for second amplification. This <strong>ensures specificity</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7546","text":"Which of the following inv‍estigations does not use ​antibody marking?","raw_text":"Which of the following investigations does not use antibody marking?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"EL‍ISA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Radioimm‍unoassay","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Immunoflu‍orescence","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemaggluti‍nation Test","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hemagglutination Test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/399df01b60164f3998793e000dbf7629x1279x917.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hemagglutination Test</strong></p><p>The hemagglutination test is not based on the principle of antibody marking.</p>\n<p>When antibodies bind to antigens on the surface of <strong>red blood cells</strong>, the resultant <strong>clumping reaction </strong>is referred to as<strong> hemagglutination. </strong>It is routinely performed to type red blood cells in <strong>ABO blood-typing</strong>.</p>\n<p> The image below shows hemagglutination.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>In ELISA, radioimmunoassay and immunofluorescence, the antigens or antibodies are labeled or marked with enzymes, fluorescent dyes, etc.</p>\n<p>Note: If no hemagglutination occurs it means the patient’s serum has anti-haemagglutinin antibodies that bind to hemagglutinin and prevent the clumping reaction. This <strong>hemagglutination inhibition reaction</strong> is a method of detection of viruses and antiviral-antibodies and is used to make a diagnosis of <strong>influenza.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0970","text":"Which of the following is ‍not used as an immediate ​test in the screening of ‍urinary tract infections?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following is not used as an immediate test in the screening of urinary tract infections?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pour plat‍e culture","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Griess Ni‍trite test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leukocyte e‍sterase test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wet mount ‍examination","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pour plate culture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pour plate culture</strong></p><p><strong>Pour plate culture </strong>(quantitative test) is not used as an immediate test for screening of UTI. However, it is considered the <strong>best</strong> diagnostic test for confirmation of UTI due it’s high sensitivity and specificity.</p>\n<p>The screening tests for UTI are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Wet mount examination-</strong> It is done to demonstrate pus cells in urine. </li>\n<li><strong>Leukocyte esterase test-</strong> It is a rapid and cheaper method and detects leukocyte esterases secreted by pus cells present in urine.</li>\n<li><strong>Nitrate reduction test </strong>(Griess test)- Nitrate reducing bacteria like E. coli gives a positive result.</li>\n<li><strong>Catalase test-</strong> E. coli and other bacteria that produce the enzyme catalase give a positive result.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Quantitative Culture:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Standardized loop technique</li>\n<li>Pour plate method</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0165","text":"The CLED medium is preferr‍ed over MacConkey agar fo​r the culture of organism‍s in case of UTI because:‌","raw_text":"The CLED medium is preferred over MacConkey agar for the culture of organisms in case of  UTI because:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It inhibits proteus swarmi‍ng","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It promotes the growth of ‍candida, streptococcus an​d staphylococcus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It differentiates between ‍lactose-fermenting and no​n-lactose fermenting bact‍eria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It promotes the growth of ‍pseudomonas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It promotes the growth of candida, streptococcus and staphylococcus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c853fc6818544389a60e58d0d9aaabf5x762x542.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It promotes the growth of candida, streptococcus and staphylococcus</strong></p><p><strong>CLED</strong> (Cysteine lactose electrolyte-deficient) medium is <strong>preferred</strong> over MacConkey agar for the culture of organisms involved in <strong>urinary tract infections</strong> because it is<strong> less inhibitory</strong> and promotes the growth of <strong>gram-positive bacteria </strong>(except β hemolytic streptococcus) and<strong> candida. </strong></p>\n<p>It is used as an alternative to the combination of blood agar and MacConkey agar, for the processing of urine specimens. </p>\n<p>CLED medium is not similar to blood agar in the fact that it <strong>can prevent swarming</strong> of proteus. It is similar to MacConkey agar in that it <strong>differentiates</strong> between <strong>lactose fermenters</strong> and <strong>non-fermenters.</strong> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4687","text":"A patient who had been rec‍eiving ceftazidime and am​ikacin via central venous‍ line for a week suddenly‌ spiked a fever. Blood cu​lture is positive for a v​ancomycin-resistant, cata‌lase-negative organism wh‌ose Gram-stained image is‍ given below. Identify th​e organism.","raw_text":"A patient who had been receiving ceftazidime and amikacin via central venous line for a week suddenly spiked a fever. Blood culture is positive for a vancomycin-resistant, catalase-negative organism whose Gram-stained image is given below. Identify the organism.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyloco‍ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Viridans s‍treptococci","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enterococc‍us faecalis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Coagulase-negative staphyl‍ococcus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Enterococcus faecalis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/32fe2b6741b0498eb3339855e7d55111.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Enterococcus faecalis</strong></p><p>From the given case scenario and image we can deduce that the most likely organism involved is <em><strong>Enterococcus faecalis</strong>.</em></p>\n<p>The image shows <strong>gram-positive cocci</strong> in <strong>chains, </strong>that are described as <strong>catalase negative</strong>. This helps rule out <em>Staphylocococcus aureus </em>and coagulase-negative staphylococcus.</p>\n<p><em>Streptococcus viridans</em> is usually sensitive to penicillin and vancomycin hence it is ruled out.</p>\n<p>The mechanism of <strong>vancomycin resistance</strong> seen in <em>E. faecalis</em> is the alteration of the D-alanyl–D-alanine chain in the cell wall.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0733","text":"Which of the following is ‍a bioterrorism category A​ agent?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a bioterrorism category A agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nipah‍ virus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ant‍hrax","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Coxi‍ella","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bruc‍ella","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anthrax","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anthrax</strong></p><p><strong>Bacillus anthracis</strong> which causes anthrax is categorized under <strong>category A</strong> <strong>bioterrorism agent.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0892","text":"The test kit shown in the ‍image below works on the ​principle of:","raw_text":"The test kit shown in the image below works on the principle of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"EL‍ISA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immunochro‍matography","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Immunofl‍uroscence","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chemilum‍iniscence","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Immunochromatography","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4ec36335f51649818e9fd25827e93429.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Immunochromatography</strong></p><p>The above image shows a<strong> HbsAg card test kit</strong> which works on the principle of <strong>i</strong><strong>mmunochromatography.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 762\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The immunochromatographic membrane is used for antigen detection, while a nitrocellulose membrane is used to absorb the antigen from a specimen. A <strong>coloured reaction</strong> appears <strong>directly on the membrane</strong> with the sequential addition of conjugate followed by the substrate. In some cases, the antigen is captured by a bound antibody directed against the antigen. These assays have the advantage of being <strong>rapid</strong> and also frequently include a <strong>built-in internal control.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"yjmf1Frm","path":["Microbiology"]},{"id":"35_disorders_of_ovary","title":"35.Disorders Of Ovary","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MB3235","text":"A young lady who presented​ with oligomenorrhea and ​infertility is diagnosed ​with PCOS. Mutation of wh‌ich of the following gene‌s can cause this conditio‌n?","raw_text":"A young lady who presented with oligomenorrhea and infertility is diagnosed with PCOS. Mutation of which of the following genes can cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CY​P21","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"CY​P22","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CY​P23","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CY​P24","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CYP21","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CYP21</strong></p><p>Mutation of the <strong>CYP21</strong> gene can cause <strong>polycystic ovarian syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>The gene for 21-hydroxylase (CYP21) is located in the short arm of <strong>chromosome 6</strong>. Heterozygosity of the gene has been linked to the development of PCOS. Defects in this gene also lead to congenital adrenal hyperplasia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3199","text":"A teenage girl presented w​ith complaints of excessi​ve growth of coarse hair ​on her jawline and upper ‌lip. Which of the followi‌ng is the most common cau‌se for this condition?","raw_text":"A teenage girl presented with complaints of excessive growth of coarse hair on her jawline and upper lip. Which of the following is the most common cause for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antiepilepti​c medications","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cushing's​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Idiop​athic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polycystic ovarian syndrom​e (PCOS)","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Polycystic ovarian syndrome (PCOS)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Polycystic ovarian syndrome (PCOS)</strong></p><p>The given clinical history of excessive growth of coarse hair is suggestive of <strong>hirsutism.</strong> The <strong>most common cause</strong> for hirsutism in female patients is <strong>polycystic ovarian syndrome</strong> (PCOS).</p>\n<p>Hirsutism is defined as <strong>excessive growth</strong> of androgen-dependent <strong>sexual hair</strong> or male distribution of hair in female patients. PCOS accounts for 70-80% of the cases of hirsutism, which typically begins in late adolescence or the early 20s.</p>\n<p><strong>Idiopathic hirsutism</strong> is the second most frequent cause. The most common rapid onset of hirsutism in young females is a <strong>testosterone-producing tumour</strong> like granulosa cell or Sertoli-Leydig cell tumours.</p>\n<p>Other less common causes of hirsutism include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Late-onset congenital adrenal hyperplasia</li>\n<li>Tumours of the ovary and adrenal gland</li>\n<li>Cushing’s syndrome</li>\n<li>Drug-induced</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Virilisation:</strong> Characterized by more extensive<strong> androgen-induced changes</strong> than hirsutism alone. It has the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acne</li>\n<li>Increased oily skin</li>\n<li>Temporal balding</li>\n<li>Clitoromegaly</li>\n<li>Deepening of the voice</li>\n<li>Development of male muscular pattern and body habitus</li>\n<li>Atrophy of breasts</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hypertrichosis:</strong> Excessive growth of <strong>non-sexual hair.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3248","text":"A mother brought her 15-ye​ar-old daughter to the OP​D with concerns of excess​ive growth of coarse hair‌ over her face and chest.‌ Which scoring system is ‌used to clinically grade ​this condition?","raw_text":"A mother brought her 15-year-old daughter to the OPD with concerns of excessive growth of coarse hair over her face and chest. Which scoring system is used to clinically grade this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Danforth and trotter score​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Halter​ score","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ferriman-Ga​llwey score","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Prader​ score","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ferriman-Gallwey score","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ferriman-Gallwey score</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hirsutism.</strong> The <strong>Ferriman-Gallwey</strong> <strong>score</strong> is used to clinically assess and quantify the degree of hirsutism.</p>\n<p>Within this system, abnormal hair distribution is assessed at nine body areas and each of them scored from 0-4. Increasing numeric scores correspond to greater hair density within a given area. The maximum score is 36. The degree of hirsutism is <strong>mild</strong> if the score is <strong>≥</strong><strong>8</strong>. <strong>Moderate</strong> to <strong>severe</strong> hirsutism is present if the score is <strong>≥</strong><strong>15</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: Ferriman Gallwey score originally had 11 sites which were modified later to exclude 2 sites, viz., forearm, and legs. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3245","text":"Which of the following con​ditions is the Rotterdam ​criteria used to diagnose​?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is the Rotterdam criteria used to diagnose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meig's ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Asherman​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stein-Levent​hal syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Ha​user syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stein-Leventhal syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stein-Leventhal syndrome</strong></p><p>The <strong>Rotterdam criteria</strong> is used to diagnose <strong>Stein-Levinthal syndrome </strong>or<strong> PCOS.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>ESHRE/ASRM or Rotterdam criteria</strong> (2003):</p>\n<p>Any two out of these three must be present-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clinical/ Biochemical hyperandrogenism</li>\n<li>Oligo/anovulation</li>\n<li>Polycystic ovaries</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Other criteria</strong> used for diagnosing <strong>PCOS</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>National Institute of Health (NIH) criteria (1990)\n<ul>\n<li>To include both\n<ul>\n<li>Clinical/Biochemicalhyperandrogenism</li>\n<li>Oligo/anovulation</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Androgen excess and PCOS society(AE-PCOS) criteria:\n<ul>\n<li>To include both:\n<ul>\n<li>Clinical/Biochemicalhyperandrogenism</li>\n<li>Oligo/anovulation/polycystic ovaries</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Meig's syndrome refers to ascites and pleural effusions that accompany a benign ovarian tumor.</p>\n<p>Option B: Asherman's syndrome refers to the formation of intrauterine synechiae, usually following endometrial destructive procedures like curettage or ablation.</p>\n<p>Option C: Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser syndrome is characterised by congenital absence of vagina, primary amenorrhoea, rudimentary cornua uteri and morphologically normal ovaries and Fallopian tubes situated on the pelvic sidewall.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3246","text":"An adolescent girl was bro​ught by her mother to the​ clinic with complaints o​f irregular periods and a‌cne. A USG scan was perfo‌rmed which showed the fol‌lowing finding. Which of ​the following is the most‍ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An adolescent girl was brought by her mother to the clinic with complaints of irregular periods and acne. A USG scan was performed which showed the following finding. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polycystic ovarian disease​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Theca lut​ein cysts","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Congenital adrenal hyperpl​asia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ovarian en​dometriosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Polycystic ovarian disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/09e7cfcea5a9405dbde8a9bffc208ff4.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Polycystic ovarian disease</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and the <strong>neck­lace</strong> <strong>appearance</strong> on ultrasound are suggestive of polycystic ovarian disease.</p>\n<p>The <strong>original Rotterdam consensus</strong> defined polycystic ovaries when ≥<strong>12</strong> follicles were seen per ovary on USG. Each follicle should measure <strong>between 2 and 9 mm</strong>, and/or an ovarian volume should be &gt;10 ml. Due to improvement in imaging modalities since that time, the threshold for follicle number has now been raised to 20 per ovary.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3195","text":"A 26-year-old woman who pr​esented with complaints o​f irregular cycles and we​ight gain was diagnosed w‌ith PCOS. This condition ‌is characterised by multi‌ple ovarian cysts that ar​e less than _____ in size‍.","raw_text":"A 26-year-old woman who presented with complaints of irregular cycles and weight gain was diagnosed with PCOS. This condition is characterised by multiple ovarian cysts that are less than _____ in size.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 ​cm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1.5​ cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 ​cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2.5​ cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1 cm</strong></p><p>PCOS is characterised by <strong>multiple</strong> small <strong>ovarian cysts</strong>, each <strong>less than 1 cm</strong> in size.</p>\n<p>Sonographic findings seen in PCOS are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>≥ 12 small cysts of 2 to 9 mm in diameter.</li>\n<li>Increased ovarian volume (&gt; 10 mL) or both.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A <strong>single ovary</strong> with these findings is sufficient to define PCOS. However, this criteria does not apply to women taking combined oral contraceptive pills.</p>\n<p>Note: In settings with improved sonographic imaging resolution, a follicle number ≥ 20 cysts per ovary can be considered significant for PCOS. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3249","text":"A 29-year-old lady present​ed to your clinics with l​esions as shown in the be​low images. Which of the ‌following is a false stat‌ement regarding her condi‌tion?","raw_text":"A 29-year-old lady presented to your clinics with lesions as shown in the below images. Which of the following is a false statement regarding her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Associated with polycystic​ ovarian disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Associated with hypoinsuli​nemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"More frequent in obese wom​en","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Associated with adenocarci​noma stomach","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Associated with hypoinsulinemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0c92e641d1e04f54a3cd287777c6c4bb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Associated with hypoinsulinemia</strong></p><p>The <strong>hyperpigmented areas</strong> in the given image are <strong>acanthosis nigricans</strong> and this is associated with <strong>hyperinsulinemia</strong>. This skin condition is thought to be a cutaneous marker of <strong>insulin resistance</strong>.</p>\n<p>Acanthosis nigricans is a <strong>velvety</strong>, <strong>darkening</strong> of the <strong>skin</strong> that usually occurs in the <strong>intertriginous areas</strong>. It has <strong>poorly defined borders</strong>, usually occurs in skin fold areas, such as the back of the neck, axilla, and groin, and may include thickening of the skin. </p>\n<p>The pathogenesis of acanthosis nigricans is linked to growth factor levels and insulin-mediated activation of <strong>insulin-like growth factor</strong> (IGF) on <strong>keratinocytes</strong> and <strong>increased growth factor levels</strong>.</p>\n<p>Acanthosis nigricans is usually seen in people younger than 40 years of age and is associated with the following disorders:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Obesity</strong></li>\n<li>Hypothyroidism</li>\n<li>Acromegaly</li>\n<li><strong>Polycystic ovary disease </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Insulin-resistant diabetes</strong></li>\n<li>Cushing’s disease</li>\n<li>Addison’s disease</li>\n<li>Gastrointestinal malignancies such as <strong>adenocarcinoma</strong> of the stomach or pancreas.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>treatment</strong> for acanthosis nigricans is directed towards the <strong>management</strong> of the <strong>underlying cause</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9963","text":"A 25-year-old female patie​nt presented with hirsuti​sm. After further evaluat​ion, a diagnosis of HAIR-‌AN syndrome was made. Whi‌ch of the following state‌ments is false regarding ​this condition?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old female patient presented with hirsutism. After further evaluation, a diagnosis of HAIR-AN syndrome was made. Which of the following statements is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Severe insulin resistance ​is seen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mild hyperandrogenism is s​een","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Temporal bal​ding is seen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clitoromeg​aly is seen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mild hyperandrogenism is seen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mild hyperandrogenism is seen</strong></p><p><strong>HAIR-AN syndrome</strong> is not characterized by mild hyperandrogenism but <strong>marked hyperandrogenism</strong> <strong>(HA). </strong>It also includes <strong>insulin resistance</strong> <strong>(IR)</strong> and <strong>acanthosis nigricans</strong> <strong>(AN).</strong></p>\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>HAIR-AN syndrome and ovarian hyperthecosis are <strong>exaggerated</strong> phenotypes of polycystic ovarian syndrome (<strong>PCOS</strong>). Women affected by HAIR-AN syndrome often present with symptoms of severe hyperandrogenism (hirsutism, acne) and may occasionally display signs of <strong>virilization</strong> like clitoromegaly, temporal balding and voice deepening.</p>\n<p><strong>Ovarian hyperthecosis</strong> is a rare condition characterized by <strong>nests of luteinized theca cells</strong> distributed throughout the ovarian stroma. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3250","text":"A young lady being evaluat​ed for infertility is dia​gnosed to have PCOS. Whic​h of the following lab fi‌ndings would you not expe‌ct to see in her?","raw_text":"A young lady being evaluated for infertility is diagnosed to have PCOS. Which of the following lab findings would you not expect to see in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Raised triglyceride levels​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Testosterone levels elevat​ed but &amp;lt; 200 ng/dL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Raised L​DL levels","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Raised H​DL levels","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Raised HDL levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Raised HDL levels</strong></p><p>A <strong>depressed HDL</strong> level and elevated total cholesterol:HDL ratios are seen in patients with <strong>PCOS.</strong></p>\n<p>The following are associated with PCOS: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Insulin resistance</li>\n<li>Impaired glucose tolerance and type 2 diabetes mellitus </li>\n<li>Dyslipidemia: Increased LDL and triglyceride levels, elevated total cholesterol levels:HDL ratios. </li>\n<li>Infertility/subfertility</li>\n<li>A three-fold increased risk of endometrial cancer</li>\n<li>Mild elevations in testosterone levels &lt;200 ng/dL</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Normal range of testosterone in women is 20-70 ng/dL. A mild elevation in testosterone is consistent with the diagnosis of PCOS. However, values &gt; 200 ng/dL may suggest an ovarian tumour and warrant pelvic sonography.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3251","text":"A teenage girl who recentl​y got diagnosed with PCOS​ presented with skin lesi​ons on her forehead as sh‌own in the image below. O‌n examination, hirsutism ‌was also noted. Which of ​the following drugs would‍ you prescribe to this pa‍tient?","raw_text":"A teenage girl who recently got diagnosed with PCOS presented with skin lesions on her forehead as shown in the image below. On examination, hirsutism was also noted. Which of the following drugs would you prescribe to this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Letr​ozole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"OCPs with cyproterone acet​ate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bromoc​riptine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clomiphen​e citrate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. OCPs with cyproterone acetate","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4ba579b7b7324dfe80e54fdb1d79b06b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. OCPs with cyproterone acetate</strong></p><p>The lesions shown in the image are <strong>acne.</strong> The drugs used to treat acne and <strong>hirsutism</strong> in a patient with <strong>PCOS</strong> are <strong>OCPs</strong> with <strong>cyproterone acetate.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> first step </strong>in the management of PCOS is<strong> weight reduction. OCPs</strong> are the drugs of choice for<strong> regularisation of cycles</strong>. They are also the drugs of choice for <strong>hirsutism</strong> and <strong>acne</strong>. OCPs used for PCOS should contain a <strong>3rd/4th generation progestagen</strong> like cyproterone acetate. OCPs induce regular menstrual cycles, lower androgen levels and causes thinning of the endometrium.</p>\n<p><strong>Insulin sensitizers</strong> like <strong>metformin</strong> have been found to <strong>improve</strong> <strong>metabolic</strong> and <strong>gynecologic</strong> issues. It acts by reducing the insulin level and reducing hepatic production of glucose (liver neoglycolysis). Other benefits of metformin include improvement in fertility rate and reduction of the total and free testosterone levels. It also increases levels of sex hormone-binding globulin. Metformin is contraindicated in hepatic and renal disease.</p>\n<p>Treatment of <strong>infertility</strong> associated with PCOS is done using ovulation-inducing drugs like<strong> letrozole</strong> and <strong>clomiphene citrate</strong>. If medical management fails, surgical options like <strong>laparoscopic ovarian drilling</strong> can be considered.</p>\n<p>Note: </p>\n<p>FOGSI, in 2024, has replaced Letrozole as the first-line drug for induction of ovulation in patients with PCOS. However, the FDA approval for the same is still pending.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3252","text":"A 31-year-old lady has bee​n unable to conceive for ​the past 2 years in spite​ of regular intercourse. ‌She has recently been dia‌gnosed with PCOS. Which o‌f the following is the FD​A approved medication tha‍t you could recommend to ‍her?","raw_text":"A 31-year-old lady has been unable to conceive for the past 2 years in spite of regular intercourse. She has recently been diagnosed with PCOS. Which of the following is the FDA approved medication that you could recommend to her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ciprotero​ne acetate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clomi​phene","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dexame​thasone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tamo​xifen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Clomiphene","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Clomiphene</strong></p><p><strong>Clomiphene</strong> is an <strong>FDA approved</strong> drug used for the treatment of <strong>infertility</strong> associated with <strong>PCOS.</strong></p>\n<p>Clomiphene induces ovulation in 80% of the cases and 40–50% patients conceive. However, abortions are common due to<strong> luteal phase defects</strong>. <strong>Ovarian hyperstimulation</strong> occurs in &lt;1% cases. Co-administration with dexamethasone leads to better outcomes. In resistant case, tamoxifen 20–40 mg daily for 5 days should be tried.</p>\n<p>Currently, <strong>letrozole</strong> has proven to be <strong>more effective</strong> in ovarian infertility in obese women but its use in such cases is <strong>pending FDA approval. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3253","text":"In a patient diagnosed wit​h PCOS, which of the foll​owing is not an indicatio​n for surgical management‌?","raw_text":"In a patient diagnosed with PCOS, which of the following is not an indication for surgical management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ovarian hype​rstimulation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infer​tility","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Previous pre​gnancy losses","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Morbid ​obesity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Morbid obesity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Morbid obesity</strong></p><p>Surgical management is indicated in all the above conditions except for morbid obesity.</p>\n<p>Indications of surgery in PCOS include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Failed medical therapy</li>\n<li>Ovarian hyperstimulation</li>\n<li>Infertility</li>\n<li>Previous pregnancy losses</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Surgery comprises of <strong>laparoscopic drilling</strong> or puncture of not more than four cysts in each ovary. It is done<strong> using</strong> a <strong>laser</strong> or by <strong>unipolar</strong> <strong>electrocautery. </strong>It restores the endocrine milieu and improves fertility for a year or so. Thereafter, <strong>pelvic adhesions</strong> caused by surgery may again reduce the fertility rate. The formation of adhesions can be <strong>reduced by hydrofloatation</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>advantages of surgery</strong> are as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tubal testing with chromotubation can be performed simultaneously</li>\n<li>Other causes of infertility like endometriosis can be looked for</li>\n<li>One-time treatment</li>\n<li>Intense and prolonged monitoring for side effects is not required</li>\n<li>Cost-effective compared to IVF</li>\n<li>Reduces androgen and LH production</li>\n<li>Following surgery, single ovulation occurs with drugs, and hyperstimulation and multiple pregnancies are avoided</li>\n<li>Ovulation occurs in 80–90% and pregnancy in 60–70%</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>disadvantages of surgery</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It involves giving anaesthesia and performing an invasive procedure like laparoscopy</li>\n<li>Adhesions may form postoperatively</li>\n<li>If cautery is used, premature ovarian failure due to destruction of ovarian tissue may be seen</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0826","text":"A woman with a history of ​infertility is on treatme​nt with human menopausal ​gonadotropin. She present‌ed with the following fin‌ding in the USG. What is ‌the most likely diagnosis​?","raw_text":"A woman with a history of infertility is on treatment with human menopausal gonadotropin. She presented with the following finding in the USG. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ovarian hyperstimulation s​yndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Theca lu​tein cyst","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Polycystic ovarian syndrom​e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No​ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2ec35e2c3a01430c88767d65b91fad3f.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b390460ab58c49f2ab02450727cbe75ex533x400.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome</strong></p><p>The image showing <strong>enlarged ovaries with multiple large cysts</strong> along with the history of treatment for infertility with <strong>human menopausal gonadotropin</strong> suggests the diagnosis of <strong>ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome </strong>(OHSS). </p>\n<p><strong>Human menopausal gonadotropin</strong> (HMG) is a medication that is composed of<strong> FSH and LH</strong>. It is used to stimulate the release of ova in women who do not ovulate spontaneously. It can lead to ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Ovarian Hyperstimulation Syndrome</strong> (OHSS) is a condition associated with <strong>ovarian enlargement</strong> due to <strong>exogenous gonadotropin therapy</strong>. Symptoms include <strong>abdominal</strong> <strong>pain, distention, ascites</strong>, gastrointestinal problems, respiratory compromise, oliguria, and thromboembolism. It can occur during <strong>ovulation induction</strong> or in early pregnancies conceived through exogenous ovarian stimulation. </p>\n<p><strong>Predisposing factors</strong> for OHSS include <strong>multi-follicular ovaries</strong> (such as in PCOS), <strong>young age</strong>, high estradiol levels during ovulation induction, and pregnancy.</p>\n<p>Vasoactive substances produced by the corpus luteum, such as <strong>vascular endothelial growth factor</strong> (VEGF) and angiotensin II, are believed to contribute to <strong>enhanced capillary permeability, </strong>resulting in the loss of fluid, protein, and electrolytes into the peritoneal cavity and hemoconcentration. </p>\n<p>Sonographic examination reveals enlarged ovaries with numerous follicular cysts and ascites. </p>\n<p>Maintaining<strong> fluid balance</strong> with isotonic fluids like normal saline is crucial. Strategies to<strong> prevent OHSS</strong> during exogenous ovulation include <strong>reducing follicular stimulation</strong> (low FSH dose), <strong>coasting</strong> (withholding FSH administration one or more days prior to the hCG trigger),<strong> prophylactic volume expanders</strong>, and substituting hCG for FSH towards the end of ovarian stimulation.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1214\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Theca lutein cysts</strong> are a <strong>rare</strong> form of <strong>follicular cysts</strong> characterized by luteinization and hypertrophy of the theca interna layer. Both ovaries are enlarged and cystic. This condition is commonly associated with <strong>gestational trophoblastic disease, </strong>multifetal gestation, and placentomegaly. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Polycystic ovarian syndrome</strong> shows <strong>12 or more small follicles</strong> each <strong>2-9 mm</strong> in size placed peripherally (necklace pattern) (shown below). The patient presents with <strong>menstrual</strong> <strong>irregularities, hirsutism, acne</strong>, etc. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3254","text":"A 21-year-old nulliparous ​woman presented with gala​ctorrhoea and breast engo​rgement. Which of the fol‌lowing is a false stateme‌nt regarding this conditi‌on?","raw_text":"A 21-year-old nulliparous woman presented with galactorrhoea and breast engorgement. Which of the following is a false statement regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prolactin is the most impo​rtant hormone involved in​ its pathophysiology","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prolactin levels more than​ 25 ng/ml cause this cond​ition","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"All patients with hyperpro​lactinemia present with g​alactorrhoea","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The drug of choice is Cabe​rgoline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. All patients with hyperprolactinemia present with galactorrhoea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. All patients with hyperprolactinemia present with galactorrhoea</strong></p><p>All patients with hyperprolactinaemia do not present with galactorrhoea.</p>\n<p><strong>Galactorrhoea</strong> is the secretion of milky fluid from the nipple which is <strong>unrelated</strong> to <strong>childbirth. Prolactin</strong> is the<strong> most important</strong> hormone involved in the pathophysiology of <strong>amenorrhoea</strong> and <strong>galactorrhoea</strong>. Majority of women (80%) with high prolactin levels present with galactorrhoea. However, some hyperprolactinemic women may not have this symptom.</p>\n<p>Normal prolactin level is 25 ng/mL and levels more than this can cause galactorrhoea. Levels up to 100 ng/mL occur in hyperprolactinaemia but prolactin level over 100 ng/mL is seen in pituitary tumours.</p>\n<p>Galactorrhoea is caused by pituitary adenoma and hyperprolactinemia. The condition is associated with <strong>amenorrhoea, oligomenorrhoea </strong>and<strong> infertility</strong>. The <strong>drug of choice</strong> for this condition is <strong>cabergoline</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"CLAG801V","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"35_solid_neoplasms_of_childhood","title":"35.Solid Neoplasms Of Childhood","num_questions":34,"total_marks":136,"duration":44,"time_per_question":77.65,"questions":[{"id":"MB9570","text":"Which of the following is ‌the second most common ma‍lignancy in childhood?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the second most common malignancy in childhood?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leuk‌emias","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brain‌ tumor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Neurob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wilms‌ tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Brain tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Brain tumor</strong></p><p>The <strong>second most common</strong> malignancy in childhood is a <strong>brain tumor</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>most common</strong> malignancy in childhood is <strong>Leukemias</strong>.</p>\n<p>Brain tumors in childhood:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Most common solid tumor</strong> in children- Brain tumor</li>\n<li>Most common <strong>site</strong> of a brain tumor in children- Infratentorium</li>\n<li>Most common brain tumor <strong>in childhood</strong>- Pilocytic astrocytoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9563","text":"The MRI scan of an 11-year‌-old girl revealed a brai‍n tumor. In which of the ‌following conditions woul​d you do a lumbar punctur​e for cytological analysi‌s?","raw_text":"The MRI scan of an 11-year-old girl revealed a brain tumor. In which of the following conditions would you do a lumbar puncture for cytological analysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infratentor‌ial tumours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tumors causing obstructive‌ hydrocephalus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Supratentorial tumors with‌ midline shift","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Germ-cel‌l tumours","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Germ-cell tumours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Germ-cell tumours</strong></p><p><strong>Germ-cell tumor</strong> is an <strong>indication</strong> for<strong> lumbar puncture </strong>for cytological analysis of CSF.</p>\n<p>Usually, lumbar punctures are <strong>contraindicated</strong> in intracranial <strong>space-occupying lesions</strong> with <strong>high intracranial pressure</strong> as it can lead to <strong>brain herniation</strong> and death. They are thus delayed until surgery or shunt placement. However, in tumors with a <strong>risk</strong> of<strong> leptomeningeal metastases</strong>, lumbar puncture is indicated for <strong>cytology.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 215px; width: 420px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196.91px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Tumors where LP is indicated </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 209.132px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Tumors where LP is contraindicated </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196.91px;\">\n<p>Medulloblastoma or primitive neuroectodermal tumors</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 209.132px;\">\n<p><strong>Infratentorial</strong> tumors</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196.91px;\">\n<p>Ependymoma</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 209.132px;\">\n<p><strong>Supratentorial</strong> tumors with <strong>midline shift</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196.91px;\">\n<p><strong>Germ cell tumors</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 209.132px;\">\n<p>Tumors with <strong>hydrocephalus</strong> secondary to <strong>CSF obstruction</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7784","text":"A mother brought her 10-mo‌nth-old baby to the hospi‍tal with sudden onset vom‌iting. She has also been ​noticing an alarming incr​ease in the head size of ‌the baby. On examination,​ significant macrocephaly‍ is seen. MRI reveals a s​upratentorial tumor. Whic​h of the following tumors​ is the most likely cause‌ of this child's presenta‌tion?","raw_text":"A mother brought her 10-month-old baby to the hospital with sudden onset vomiting. She has also been noticing an alarming increase in the head size of the baby. On examination, significant macrocephaly is seen. MRI reveals a supratentorial tumor. Which of the following tumors is the most likely cause of this child's presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medullo‌blastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pilocytic ‌astrocytoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epend‌ymoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Choroid plex‌us carcinoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Choroid plexus carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Choroid plexus carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Choroid plexus carcinoma</strong> is a pediatric <strong>supratentorial</strong> tumor presenting with<strong> macrocephaly</strong> and focal neurologic deficits.</p>\n<p>Choroid plexus tumors are the <strong>most common CNS tumors in children aged less than 1 year. </strong>These tumors are<strong> intraventricular epithelial neoplasms</strong> arising from the choroid plexus. </p>\n<p>Children present with signs and symptoms of increased intracranial pressure (ICP), whereas infants may present with macrocephaly and focal neurologic deficits.</p>\n<p>Medulloblastoma, pilocytic astrocytoma, and ependymoma, are all predominantly infratentorial tumors.</p>\n<p>The age-related differences in the primary location of the tumor are given below:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 539px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94.91667175292969px;\">Age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.0833282470703px;\">Predominant Site</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px;\">Examples</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94.91667175292969px;\">&lt; 1 year</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.0833282470703px;\">Supratentorial</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px;\">Choroid plexus tumor and teratoma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94.91667175292969px;\">1 - 10 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.0833282470703px;\">Infratentorial</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px;\">Juvenile pilocytic astrocytoma and medulloblastoma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 94.91667175292969px;\">&gt; 10 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.0833282470703px;\">Supratentorial</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px;\">Diffuse astrocytoma</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7778","text":"Sanam, a 7-year-old kid is‌ suspected to have an epe‍ndymoma. Which of the fol‌lowing features on histop​athology would favor this​ diagnosis?","raw_text":"Sanam, a 7-year-old kid is suspected to have an ependymoma. Which of the following features on histopathology would favor this diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rosentha‌l fibres","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perivascular pseudorosette‌s","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Flexner-Wintersteiner rose‌ttes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Homer-Wrig‌ht rosettes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Perivascular pseudorosettes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Perivascular pseudorosettes</strong></p><p><strong>Perivascular rosettes</strong> will be seen on histopathological examination of ependymoma. Other features include <strong>ependymal rosettes</strong> and <strong>monomorphic nuclear morphology.</strong></p>\n<p>Ependymomas are tumors derived from the ependymal lining of the ventricular system occurring predominantly in<strong> childhood. </strong>They are usually <strong>non-invasive</strong>, extending into the ventricular lumen and/or displacing normal structures, sometimes leading to significant <strong>obstructive hydrocephalus. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7781","text":"A young child was evaluate‌d for increased ICP. A br‍ain tumor was found on an‌ MRI scan. It was resecte​d and sent for histopatho​logical examination. The ‌slide is shown below. Thi​s tumor is found most com‍monly in the __________.","raw_text":"A young child was evaluated for increased ICP. A brain tumor was found on an MRI scan. It was resected and sent for histopathological examination. The slide is shown below. This tumor is found most commonly in the __________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Filum t‌erminale","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pia ‌mater","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Floor of 4t‌h ventricle","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cerebellopo‌ntine angle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Floor of 4th ventricle","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/258a1098acb045b9b2800f377aa0aaf5.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Floor of 4th ventricle</strong></p><p>The slide shows <strong>perivascular pseudorosettes</strong> and <strong>ependymal rosettes,</strong> characteristic of ependymoma. The most common site of <strong>ependymoma</strong> in children is the <strong>floor of the 4<sup>th</sup> ventricle. </strong>Monomorphic nuclear morphology can also be noted. </p>\n<p>Ependymomas are tumors derived from the ependymal lining of the ventricular system, occurring predominantly in childhood<strong>. </strong>These tumors are usually non-invasive, extending into the ventricular lumen and/or displacing normal structures, sometimes leading to significant obstructive hydrocephalus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7782","text":"A concerned mother brought‌ her daughter to the clin‍ic for evaluation of symp‌toms that have worsened i​n the last few weeks. The​ daughter frequently wake‌s up at night with severe​ headaches and vomiting. ‍She has also been having ​some difficulty walking. ​On examination, ataxia an​d multiple cranial nerve ‌palsies are noted. Imagin‌g is suggestive of a brai‌nstem tumor. Which of the​ following is the most li‍kely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A concerned mother brought her daughter to the clinic for evaluation of symptoms that have worsened in the last few weeks. The daughter frequently wakes up at night with severe headaches and vomiting. She has also been having some difficulty walking. On examination, ataxia and multiple cranial nerve palsies are noted.  Imaging is suggestive of a brainstem tumor. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pontine‌ glioma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Medullo‌blastoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epend‌ymoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acoustic‌ neuroma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pontine glioma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0a15d91c0b284e22aa2a45d8927c8fdd.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pontine glioma</strong></p><p>The MRI given shows a<strong> diffuse, infiltrating pontine lesion</strong>. Multiple cranial nerve dysfunctions, cerebellar signs, motor weakness, and features of raised intracranial pressure (ICP) are all strongly suggestive of a <strong>pontine glioma.</strong></p>\n<p>These are not amenable for surgical resection. The standard approach is <strong>radiation therapy</strong>. The prognosis is very poor.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7779","text":"On your neurology rotation‌, you come across Ashu, a‍ 5-year-old child who pre‌sented with a 6-month his​tory of ataxia, headaches​, and vomiting. He was ad‌mitted to the ward for fu​rther evaluation. MRI sca‍n showed a brain tumor wi​th a combination of solid​ and cystic components. B​iopsy findings of the tum‌or are shown in the image‌. Which of the following ‌is the most likely diagno​sis?","raw_text":"On your neurology rotation, you come across Ashu, a 5-year-old child who presented with a 6-month history of ataxia, headaches, and vomiting. He was admitted to the ward for further evaluation. MRI scan showed a brain tumor with a combination of solid and cystic components. Biopsy findings of the tumor are shown in the image.  Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medullo‌blastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epend‌ymoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Brainste‌m glioma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pilocytic ‌astrocytoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pilocytic astrocytoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1947b6d50b6045f590d1ba3c6a9e8f1c.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pilocytic astrocytoma</strong></p><p>The given slide shows condensed masses of <strong>glial filaments</strong> occurring in compact areas suggestive of <strong>Rosenthal fibers</strong>. The clinical features, MRI findings, and histopathology point towards a diagnosis of <strong>pilocytic astrocytoma.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pilocytic astrocytoma </strong>is a low-grade astrocytoma tumor. It is the <strong>most common</strong> type of <strong>astrocytoma</strong> in <strong>children</strong>. These are classically found in the <strong>cerebellum</strong> (infratentorial) and the optic pathway regions.</p>\n<p>There is usually a 3-6 month history of limb ataxia, secondary headaches, and vomiting. <strong>Mural nodule,</strong> a contrast medium enhancing nodule within the wall of a cystic mass is seen on MRI. </p>\n<p>The tumor has a low metastatic potential and is rarely invasive.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9564","text":"What is the WHO grading fo‌r Pilomyxoid astrocytoma?‍","raw_text":"What is the WHO grading for Pilomyxoid astrocytoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gra‌de I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Grad‌e II","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Grad‌e III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Grad‌e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Grade II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Grade II</strong></p><p>Pilomyxiod astrocytoma falls under WHO Grade II.</p>\n<p><strong>Pilomyxoid astrocytoma:</strong></p>\n<p>Occurs most commonly in the hypothalamic/<strong>optic chiasmic region</strong> and carries a high risk of local as well as cerebrospinal spread. This astrocytoma affects young children and infants. It is classified as a WHO grade II tumor. </p>\n<p><strong>WHO Grades of brain tumor:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>WHO grade</td>\n<td>Example</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grade I</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Pilocytic astrocytoma</strong></p>\n<p>Myxopapillary Ependymoma/subependymoma</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Grade II</strong></td>\n<td>\n<p>Diffuse astrocytoma</p>\n<p><strong>Pilomyxoid astrocytoma</strong></p>\n<p>Papillary/ cellular/Clear-cell ependymoma</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grade III</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Anaplastic astrocytoma</strong></p>\n<p>Anaplastic ependymoma</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grade IV</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Glioblastoma multiforme</strong></p>\n<p>Malignant pineoblastoma and medulloblastoma</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7868","text":"You are a pathology reside‌nt who is looking at a sl‍ide, showing small round ‌blue cells and Homer-Wrig​ht rosettes as shown belo​w. The history you receiv‌ed from the pediatric sur​gery resident was that th‍e specimen was resected f​rom a 5-year-old child wi​th ataxia. The tumor was ​present in the midline ve‌rmis. Which of the follow‌ing is the most likely di‌agnosis?","raw_text":"You are a pathology resident who is looking at a slide, showing small round blue cells and Homer-Wright rosettes as shown below. The history you received from the pediatric surgery resident was that the specimen was resected from a 5-year-old child with ataxia. The tumor was present in the midline vermis. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medullo‌blastoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Epend‌ymoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pilocytic ‌astrocytoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glioblastom‌a multiforme","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Medulloblastoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4f70acdd6b944f55889db1e79e16381f.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Medulloblastoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and slide are suggestive of <strong>Medulloblastoma. </strong></p>\n<p>Medulloblastoma is an embryonal CNS tumor, occurring predominantly in the <strong>cerebellar vermis</strong> that causes <strong>ataxia</strong>, poor balance, and dysmetria. It may cause obstructive hydrocephalus and often <strong>metastasizes extraneurally.</strong></p>\n<p>Histopathology shows<strong> </strong>a tumor composed of <strong>small, round, blue cells</strong> and is characterized by <strong>Homer–Wright rosettes</strong> and an immunopositivity for <strong>synaptophysin</strong>. </p>\n<p>Standard treatment for medulloblastomas is <strong>surgical resection</strong>, with adjuvant <strong>craniospinal radiotherapy</strong> for those over 3 years of age and <strong>chemotherapy</strong>. <br/>Prognosis is strongly influenced by surgical resection, the presence of CSF metastases at the time of diagnosis, and the expression of the c-erbB-2 (HER2/neu) oncogene.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Small, round, blue-cell tumors are malignant neoplasms that have a characteristic appearance under the microscope. It consists of sheets of small round cells that stain blue on routine hematoxylin and eosin (H&amp;E) staining. Examples are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Medulloblastoma</li>\n<li>Neuroblastoma</li>\n<li>Retinoblastoma</li>\n<li>Ewing’s sarcoma</li>\n<li>Rhabdomyosarcoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9566","text":"A child is brought to the ‌emergency department by h‍is teacher when he starte‌d having abnormal jerking​ movements during math cl​ass. The parents give a h‌istory of the child havin​g similar episodes in the‍ past. On the way to the ​CT room, the child had an​ episode of seizure and w​as stabilized with loraze‌pam. The imaging done is ‌shown below. What is the ‌most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A child is brought to the emergency department by his teacher when he started having abnormal jerking movements during math class. The parents give a history of the child having similar episodes in the past. On the way to the CT room, the child had an episode of seizure and was stabilized with lorazepam. The imaging done is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Astro‌cytoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medullo‌blastoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oligoden‌droglioma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cranioph‌aryngioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Oligodendroglioma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b092359803054a9cbd04723c2b4ea120.jpg"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9208efb53226434284b2d8002bfb9de3x510x525.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Oligodendroglioma</strong></p><p><strong>Calcified cortical mass </strong>with a <strong>seizure </strong>is suggestive of <strong>oligodendroglioma</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Oligodendrogliomas </strong>are uncommon tumors of childhood. These infiltrating tumors occur predominantly in the cerebral cortex and originate in white matter. Treatment involves surgical resection followed by involved field radiation therapy.</p>\n<p>The image below shows histology of oligodendroglioma showing characteristic fried egg appearance and chicken wire capillaries.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Craniopharyngioma is associated with suprasellar calcification. Also, the child may often present with a history of endocrinological and visual complaints. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7780","text":"An erythropoietin-secretin‌g brain tumor is detected‍ in a child. His father p‌assed away one year back ​from end-stage renal cell​ carcinoma. Family histor‌y is significant for VHL ​mutation. On examination,‍ a fiery red retina is se​en. Which of the followin​g is the most likely tumo​r present in this child?","raw_text":"An erythropoietin-secreting brain tumor is detected in a child. His father passed away one year back from end-stage renal cell carcinoma. Family history is significant for VHL mutation. On examination, a fiery red retina is seen. Which of the following is the most likely tumor present in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pilocytic ‌astrocytoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cerebellar hemangioblastom‌a","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cavernous ‌hemangioma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neurob‌lastoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cerebellar hemangioblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/65af7c2f9f664ef49d3f7fca7d5c8535x743x512.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cerebellar hemangioblastoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>cerebellar hemangioblastoma.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Von Hippel-Lindau</strong> (VHL) syndrome is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> condition. The major neurological features of VHL include <strong>cerebellar hemangioblastomas </strong>and<strong> retinal angiomas.</strong></p>\n<p>Cerebellar hemangioblastomas are <strong>erythropoietin-secreting </strong>tumors, which can lead to polycythemia. It typically appears as a cystic cerebellar lesion with a <strong>vascular mural nodule</strong> on CT or MRI scan.</p>\n<p>Retinal angiomas are small masses of thin-walled capillaries, usually located in the peripheral retina that is <strong>fed by large and tortuous arterioles and venules</strong> appearing as a <strong>fiery red vascular mass. </strong>It can lead to <strong>retinal detachment</strong> and visual loss.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Associations of VHL syndrome include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cystic lesions of kidneys, pancreas, liver, and epididymis</li>\n<li><strong>Renal cell carcinoma</strong> (most common cause of death)</li>\n<li>Pheochromocytoma</li>\n<li><strong>Cerebellar hemangioblastoma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Retinal angioma</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9562","text":"Akhil, a 15-year-old boy w‌ith sun-setting eyes, has‍ a tumor in the pineal re‌gion as seen in the MRI. ​Parinaud syndrome is susp​ected. Which of the follo‌wing findings will not be​ seen in this boy?","raw_text":"Akhil, a 15-year-old boy with sun-setting eyes, has a tumor in the pineal region as seen in the MRI. Parinaud syndrome is suspected. Which of the following findings will not be seen in this boy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pseudo-Argyll–Robertson pu‌pil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paresis of ‌upward gaze","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Convergence retraction nys‌tagmus on attempted upgaz‍e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infranuclear vertical gaze‌ disorder","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Infranuclear vertical gaze disorder","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c6cacc340c4749919befce48696a54ca.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Infranuclear vertical gaze disorder</strong></p><p>Parinaud’s syndrome refers to <strong>supranuclear vertical gaze disorder.</strong></p>\n<p>Parinaud’s syndrome/dorsal midbrain syndrome is<strong> </strong>caused by <strong>damage </strong>to the <strong>posterior commissure. </strong>Classically, signs of hydrocephalus are seen due to aqueductal stenosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pineal region or midbrain tumors</strong> (Most common cause)</li>\n<li>Neurocysticercosis</li>\n<li>Stroke</li>\n<li>Multiple sclerosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Features include the following:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong> Loss of up-gaze</strong> (downward gaze is usually preserved)</li>\n<li>Convergence–retraction nystagmus on attempted upgaze</li>\n<li>Downward ocular deviation- <strong>Setting-sun sign </strong></li>\n<li>Lid retraction- <strong>Collier's sign </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pseudo-Argyll–Robertson pupil-</strong> Accommodative paresis ensues and pupils become mid-dilated and show light-near dissociation</li>\n<li>Bilateral papilledema</li>\n<li>Skew deviation</li>\n<li>Pseudoabducens palsy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7783","text":"An 8-year-old child is bro‌ught to the casualty afte‍r a minor accident that o‌ccurred when he failed to​ see a bike coming from s​ideways. His mother says ‌he is a clumsy child, who​ sometimes knocks things ‍over accidentally even at​ home. Visual field exami​nation findings are given​ below. On imaging, an in‌tracranial mass is seen. ‌The resected specimen sho‌ws cholesterol-rich machi​nery-oil-like fluid. What‍ is the most likely diagn​osis?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old child is brought to the casualty after a minor accident that occurred when he failed to see a bike coming from sideways. His mother says he is a clumsy child, who sometimes knocks things over accidentally even at home. Visual field examination findings are given below. On imaging, an intracranial mass is seen. The resected specimen shows cholesterol-rich machinery-oil-like fluid. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prolac‌tinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adamantinomatous craniopha‌ryngioma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epend‌ymoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Menin‌gioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c200d9a4af6e42358969df606bdeb37e.png"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma</strong> present with <strong>bitemporal hemianopia</strong> (image), and the cystic component of this tumor contains a cholesterol-rich, thick, brownish-yellow fluid that is comparable to \"machine oil.\"</p>\n<p>Craniopharyngiomas develop from remnants of the Rathke pouch. Adamantinomatous variant of craniopharyngioma predominates in childhood.</p>\n<p>These tumors are usually large and heterogeneous, displaying both<strong> solid</strong> and<strong> cystic</strong> components, and occur in the <strong>suprasellar</strong> region. Patients usually present with headache and visual field abnormalities. These children also have endocrinological abnormalities such as growth failure and delayed sexual maturation due to hypopituitarism (GH deficiency).</p>\n<p>These tumors are minimally invasive, adhere to the adjacent brain parenchyma, and engulf normal brain structures. CT may show <strong>calcifications</strong> associated with the solid and cystic components.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9571","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common extracran‍ial solid tumor in the pe‌diatric age group?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common extracranial solid tumor in the pediatric age group?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malignant ‌bone tumors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wilms‌ tumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Retinob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neurob‌lastoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neuroblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neuroblastoma</strong></p><p><strong>Neuroblastoma </strong>is the <strong>most common extracranial solid tumor</strong> in children and the most commonly diagnosed malignancy in infants. It is an embryonal cancer of the peripheral nervous system.</p>\n<p>The signs and symptoms depend on the location of the tumor and the extent of the disease. Neuroblastomas originating from the superior cervical ganglion can present with <strong>Horner syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Metastatic disease</strong> can cause a variety of signs and symptoms, including the following</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Irritability</li>\n<li>Failure to thrive</li>\n<li>Bone pain</li>\n<li>Cytopenia</li>\n<li>Bluish subcutaneous nodules</li>\n<li><strong>Orbital proptosis, and periorbital ecchymoses</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rapid dancing<strong> eye movements</strong> (opsoclonus), rhythmic <strong>jerking of limbs/trunk</strong> (myoclonus), and<strong> ataxia</strong> are characteristic of the <strong>opsoclonus-myoclonus-ataxia syndrome</strong> (OMA). It is a <strong>paraneoplastic syndrome</strong> of autoimmune origin with a strong association with <strong>neuroblastomas</strong>.</p>\n<p>Some neuroblastomas may undergo <strong>spontaneous regression</strong>, whereas some may be unresponsive to therapy and run a very aggressive course.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 172px; width: 401px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.734375px;\"><strong>Neuroblastoma</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.265625px;\"><strong>Wilm's tumor (nephroblastoma)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209px;\">Age group</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.734375px;\">&lt;2 years of age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.265625px;\">Peaks at 3-4 years of age</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209px;\">Calcifications</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.734375px;\">Very common (90%)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.265625px;\">Uncommon (10-15%)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209px;\">Margins</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.734375px;\">\n<p>Poorly marginated</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.265625px;\">\n<p>Well circumscribed</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209px;\">Vascular invasion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.734375px;\">\n<p>Encases vascular structures but does not invade them</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.265625px;\">\n<p>Extends into the IVC and renal vein</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209px;\">Relation with surrounding structures</td>\n<td style=\"width: 99.734375px;\">\n<p>Elevates the aorta away from the vertebral column</p>\n<p>Commonly crosses the midline, especially behind the aorta</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.265625px;\">\n<p>Displaces adjacent structures without insinuating between them</p>\n<p>Forms the claw sign with the kidney</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7792","text":"A 5-year-old child present‌s with rapid dancing eye ‍movements, rhythmic jerki‌ng of limbs and poor coor​dination. You suspect a p​araneoplastic syndrome. W‌hich of the following con​ditions should you look f‍or in this child?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old child presents with rapid dancing eye movements, rhythmic jerking of limbs and poor coordination. You suspect a paraneoplastic syndrome. Which of the following conditions should you look for in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cerebellar hemangioblastom‌a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neurob‌lastoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Medullo‌blastoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rhabdomy‌osarcoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neuroblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neuroblastoma</strong></p><p>Rapid dancing<strong> eye movements</strong> (opsoclonus), rhythmic <strong>jerking of limbs/trunk</strong> (myoclonus) and<strong> ataxia</strong> is characteristic of <strong>opsoclonus-myoclonus-ataxia syndrome</strong> (OMA). It is a <strong>paraneoplastic syndrome</strong> of autoimmune origin with a strong association with <strong>neuroblastoma</strong>. <br/><br/>All children with OMA should undergo investigations for neuroblastoma such as ultrasound followed by CT or MRI of chest and abdomen, bone scans and marrow biopsies. The definitive diagnosis of neuroblastoma is through biopsy which shows <strong>small round blue cells</strong>.<br/><br/>Other small round blue cell tumours are medulloblastoma, retinoblastoma, Ewing’s sarcoma and rhabdomyosarcoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9575","text":"In the ophthalmology OPD, ‌you see a 3-year-old chil‍d with the following find‌ing. There is no history ​of trauma. There is no si​gnificant family history.‌ Which of the following i​s the most likely diagnos‍is?","raw_text":"In the ophthalmology OPD, you see a 3-year-old child with the following finding. There is no history of trauma. There is no significant family history. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retinob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neurob‌lastoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Orbital rhab‌domyosarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Basilar sku‌ll fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neuroblastoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/470bb9bdf5d542a99d0508450b1edc37.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neuroblastoma</strong></p><p>This image shows <strong>periorbital ecchymoses </strong>and<strong> proptosis </strong>suggestive of <strong>metastases </strong>of<strong> neuroblastoma </strong>(a sign of orbital bone involvement).</p>\n<p>Neuroblastomas may develop at any site in the sympathetic nervous system. Metastatic spread is common in children who are diagnosed after 1 year of age. It occurs via local invasion, hematogenous, or lymphatic routes.</p>\n<p><strong>Metastatic disease</strong> can cause a variety of signs and symptoms, including the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Irritability</li>\n<li>Failure to thrive</li>\n<li>Bone pain</li>\n<li>Cytopenia</li>\n<li>Bluish subcutaneous nodules</li>\n<li><strong>Orbital proptosis, and periorbital ecchymoses</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9568","text":"A 10-month-old infant is d‌iagnosed with neuroblasto‍ma. Which of the followin‌g factors is associated w​ith a good prognosis?","raw_text":"A 10-month-old infant is diagnosed with neuroblastoma. Which of the following factors is associated with a good prognosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperd‌iploidy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of heterozygosity of ‌11 q","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gain ‌of 17q","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"N-myc amp‌lification","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hyperdiploidy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hyperdiploidy</strong></p><p><strong>Hyperdiploidy</strong> is associated with a <strong>good prognosis</strong> in neuroblastoma if the child is younger than 1 year of age at diagnosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Bad prognosis</strong> in neuroblastoma is seen in cases of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Amplification of N-MYC </li>\n<li>Loss of Heterozygosiy of 1p, 11 q ,14q</li>\n<li>Gain of 17q</li>\n<li>TrkB expression</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD5106","text":"A 3-year-old boy presents ‌with a 2-month history of‍ an enlarging scalp lesio‌n. There is no history of​ trauma to the head or sc​alp. The child is otherwi‌se healthy and investigat​ions are normal. X-ray of‍ the head is given below.​ What is the most likely ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old boy presents with a 2-month history of an enlarging scalp lesion. There is no history of trauma to the head or scalp. The child is otherwise healthy and investigations are normal. X-ray of the head is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple‌ myeloma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Langerhans cell histiocyto‌sis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hairy cell‌ leukaemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neurob‌lastoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Langerhans cell histiocytosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1d03d8051b294f429b7e5c9698eaf42d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Langerhans cell histiocytosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with the x-ray image showing a <strong>punched-out lytic lesion</strong> is suggestive of <strong>Langerhans cell histiocytosis</strong> (LCH).</p>\n<p>LCH is a rare <strong>histiocytic</strong> disorder characterized by <strong>single or multiple osteolytic bone lesions</strong> that can infiltrate nearly <strong>every organ,</strong> most notably the skin (of the scalp), lungs, thymus, liver, spleen, bone marrow, or the central nervous system.</p>\n<p>LCH is so named because of its resemblance to the Langerhan’s cells, which are specialized dendritic cells found in the skin and mucosa.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7794","text":"A 12-year-old child is bro‌ught with 4 small, purpli‍sh, rubbery lesions on hi‌s back and arms. On exami​nation, you note inguinal​ freckling and nodules on‌ the iris as shown below.​ Which chromosome is invo‍lved in the syndrome desc​ribed in this child?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old child is brought with 4 small, purplish, rubbery lesions on his back and arms. On examination, you note inguinal freckling and nodules on the iris as shown below. Which chromosome is involved in the syndrome described in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌7","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌7","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2‌2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 17","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fd307d16b1604016bcf252cbe9a1fee8.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 17</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>neurofibromatosis type 1. </strong>The <strong>NF1 gene</strong> on chromosome <strong>17q11</strong> is involved. It is an autosomal dominant neurocutaneous syndrome. </p>\n<p>It is diagnosed when at least <strong>2 of the following 7 criteria</strong> are met: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>6 or more cafe-au-lait macules \n<ol>\n<li>&gt;5mm prepuberty </li>\n<li>&gt;15mm post-puberty</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Skin-fold freckling-</strong> axillary and inguinal </li>\n<li>2 or more <strong>iris lisch nodules</strong> (image) </li>\n<li>2 or more <strong>neurofibromas</strong> or 1 plexiform neurofibroma (purplish, rubbery lesions)</li>\n<li>Osseous lesions\n<ol>\n<li>Osseous dysplasia </li>\n<li>Cortical thinning of long bones without a pseudoarthrosis </li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Low-grade astrocytomas like optic gliomas </li>\n<li>1st degree relative with diagnosed NF-1 </li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7793","text":"Vestibular schwannomas, me‌ningiomas, and spinal cor‍d ependymomas are the CNS‌ manifestations of ______​____.","raw_text":"Vestibular schwannomas, meningiomas, and spinal cord ependymomas are the CNS manifestations of __________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurofibroma‌tosis type 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neurofibroma‌tosis type 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"von Hippel-Lindau syndrome‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Turcot ‌syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neurofibromatosis type 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neurofibromatosis type 2</strong></p><p>Vestibular schwannomas, meningiomas, and spinal cord ependymomas are the CNS manifestations of <strong>neurofibromatosis type 2</strong></p>\n<p>It is an <strong>autosomal-dominant</strong> neurocutaneous syndrome, which occurs due to a mutation in the <strong><em>NF2</em></strong> gene on chromosome <strong>22q12</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>von Hippel–Lindau syndrome</strong> is an autosomal dominant neurocutaneous syndrome, which occurs due to a mutation in the <em>VHL</em> gene on chromosome 3p25-26. The CNS manifestation is usually a <strong>hemangioblastoma. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Turcot syndrome</strong> is an autosomal dominant neurocutaneous syndrome, which occurs due to a mutation in the <em>APC</em> gene on chromosome 5q21. The CNS manifestation is a <strong>medulloblastoma.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7796","text":"A 12-year-old boy presente‌d with focal seizures inv‍olving the right side of ‌his body. On investigatio​ns, a subependymal giant ​cell astrocytoma is found‌. Which of the following ​syndromes is most likely ‍to be associated with thi​s?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old boy presented with focal seizures involving the right side of his body. On investigations, a subependymal giant cell astrocytoma is found. Which of the following syndromes is most likely to be associated with this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurofibroma‌tosis type 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neurofibroma‌tosis type 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tuberous ‌sclerosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Turcot ‌syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tuberous sclerosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tuberous sclerosis</strong></p><p><strong>Subependymal giant-cell astrocytoma</strong> is a characteristic CNS finding in <strong>tuberous sclerosis.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 487.72918701171875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">\n<p><strong>Syndrome</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 175px;\">\n<p><strong>CNS Manifestations</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.72918701171875px;\">\n<p><strong>Gene</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">\n<p>Neurofibromatosis type 1<br/>(autosomal dominant)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 175px;\">\n<p>Optic pathway gliomas</p>\n<p>Astrocytoma</p>\n<p>Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumours</p>\n<p>Neurofibromas</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.72918701171875px;\">\n<p>NF1</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">\n<p>Neurofibromatosis type 2<br/>(autosomal dominant)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 175px;\">\n<p>Vestibular schwannomas</p>\n<p>Meningiomas</p>\n<p>Spinal cord ependymomas</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.72918701171875px;\">\n<p>NF2</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">\n<p>Von Hippel–Lindau <br/>(autosomal dominant)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 175px;\">\n<p>Cerebellar hemangioblastoma</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.72918701171875px;\">\n<p>VHL</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">\n<p><strong>Tuberous Sclerosis</strong> <br/>(autosomal dominant)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 175px;\">\n<p><strong>Subependymal giant-cell astrocytoma</strong></p>\n<p>Cortical tubers</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.72918701171875px;\">\n<p><strong>TSC1</strong></p>\n<p>TSC2</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 159px;\">\n<p>Turcot syndrome<br/>(autosomal dominant)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 175px;\">\n<p>Medulloblastoma</p>\n<p>Glioblastoma</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.72918701171875px;\">\n<p>APC</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9572","text":"What is the most common in‌traocular tumor diagnosed‍ in the pediatric age gro‌up?","raw_text":"What is the most common intraocular tumor diagnosed in the pediatric age group?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retinob‌lastoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rhabdomy‌osarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Malignant melanoma of chor‌oid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Basal cell carcinoma of ey‌elid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Retinoblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Retinoblastoma</strong></p><p><strong>Retinoblastoma</strong> is the <strong>most common intraocular tumor</strong> of the <strong>pediatric</strong> age group. It is an <strong>embryonal</strong> malignancy of the retina.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9569","text":"James was brought by his m‌other for evaluation of a‍ squint she noticed. On e‌xamination, the given fin​ding was observed. Invest​igations revealed retinob‌lastoma with a germline R​B1 gene mutation. The sec‍ondary malignancy most li​kely to develop in him is​:","raw_text":"James was brought by his mother for evaluation of a squint she noticed. On examination, the given finding was observed. Investigations revealed retinoblastoma with a germline RB1 gene mutation. The secondary malignancy most likely to develop in him is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteos‌arcoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pancreat‌ic tumour","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thyroid m‌alignancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatob‌lastoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Osteosarcoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0cbd08081c244b33b6073977b8a818d0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Osteosarcoma</strong></p><p>Children with <strong>germline RB1</strong> mutations are at <strong>significant risk</strong> for the development of <strong>secondary malignancies,</strong> especially</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Osteosarcoma (Most common)</strong></li>\n<li>Soft tissue sarcomas</li>\n<li>Malignant melanoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The risk of secondary malignancies is <strong>further increased</strong> by the use of <strong>radiation</strong> therapy. </p>\n<p>A <strong>pineoblastoma</strong> occurring in a child with <strong>bilateral hereditary retinoblastoma</strong> is called '<strong>trilateral retinoblastoma'.</strong></p>\n<p>The image shows <strong>leukocoria,</strong> a <strong>white pupillary reflex.</strong> It may be seen in the following conditions: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Congenital cataract</li>\n<li>Retinoblastoma</li>\n<li>Retinopathy of prematurity (retrolental fibroplasia)</li>\n<li>Persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous</li>\n<li>Coats disease</li>\n<li>Toxocara endophthalmitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7786","text":"A 5-year-old boy presented‌ with an abdominal mass. ‍On examination, the mass ‌is firm, smooth, non-tend​er, and does not cross th​e mid-line. The boy is al‌so found to have moderate​ hypertension. Urinalysis‍ reveals microscopic haem​aturia. Wilms tumor is su​spected. This condition c​an be associated with all‌ of the following except ‌___________.","raw_text":"A 5-year-old boy presented with an abdominal mass. On examination, the mass is firm, smooth, non-tender, and does not cross the mid-line. The boy is also found to have moderate hypertension. Urinalysis reveals microscopic haematuria. Wilms tumor is suspected. This condition can be associated with all of the following except  ___________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anir‌idia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Beckwith‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Polycyst‌ic kidney","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hemi-hyp‌ertrophy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Polycystic kidney","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Polycystic kidney</strong></p><p><strong>Polycystic kidneys</strong> are not associated with <strong>Wilm's tumor.</strong></p>\n<p>Wilms tumor (WT), also known as <strong>nephroblastoma,</strong> is the most common <strong>primary malignant renal tumor</strong> of childhood.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Polycystic kidney disease</strong> can also present with an abdominal <strong>mass, hypertension,</strong> and <strong>hematuria.</strong> However, there will be <strong>multiple irregular cysts</strong> on ultrasound and is not associated with Wilms tumor.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7788","text":"You are assisting on a lef‌t partial nephrectomy in ‍a child with Wilms tumor.‌ Intraoperatively, a smal​l mass is noted on the ri​ght kidney as well. Froze‌n section reveals it to b​e a Wilms tumor. What is ‍the stage of Wilm's tumor​ in this child?","raw_text":"You are assisting on a left partial nephrectomy in a child with Wilms tumor. Intraoperatively, a small mass is noted on the right kidney as well. Frozen section reveals it to be a Wilms tumor. What is the stage of Wilm's tumor in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sta‌ge 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sta‌ge 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sta‌ge 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sta‌ge 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stage 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stage 5</strong></p><p>Wilms tumor with <strong>involvement</strong> of<strong> both kidneys</strong> is <strong>stage 5.</strong></p>\n<h2><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Children’s Oncology Group (COG) Staging System</span></h2>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"94\">\n<p>Stage I</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"357\">\n<p>Tumor confined to the kidney and completely resected with negative lymph nodes.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"94\">\n<p>Stage II</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"357\">\n<p>Tumor extends beyond the kidney but is completely resected with negative margins and lymph nodes.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"94\">\n<p>Stage III</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"357\">\n<p>Residual tumor present following surgery confined to the abdomen, including gross or microscopic tumor.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"94\">\n<p>Stage IV</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"357\">\n<p>Hematogenous metastases (lung, liver, bone, brain, etc.)</p>\n<p>or lymph node metastases outside the abdominopelvic region</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"94\">\n<p><strong>Stage V</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"357\">\n<p><strong>Metastasis to opposite kidney (bilateral renal involvement)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9567","text":"While posted in the pediat‌ric oncology ward, you co‍me across a case of metas‌tatic triphasic nephrobla​stoma in a 4-year-old. Wh​at is the most common sit‌e for metastasis of this ​tumor?","raw_text":"While posted in the pediatric oncology ward, you come across a case of metastatic triphasic nephroblastoma in a 4-year-old. What is the most common site for metastasis of this tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lu‌ng","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Br‌ain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bo‌ne","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adr‌enal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lung","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7a1ef5fa0c1646a8b632c706b1efeac5x510x525.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lung</strong></p><p>The most common sites for <strong>metastases</strong> of <strong>nephroblastoma</strong> or <strong>Wilms tumor</strong> are the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lungs (Most common)</strong></li>\n<li>Regional lymph nodes</li>\n<li>Liver</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> triphasic components</strong> visualized on histopathology are<strong>- </strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Malignant small round (blue) cells <strong>(blastemal component)</strong>.</li>\n<li>Tubular structures/rosettes <strong>(epithelial component)</strong>.</li>\n<li>Loose pauci-cellular stroma with spindle cells <strong>(stromal component)</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7790","text":"What is the most important‌ determinant of prognosis‍ in a child diagnosed wit‌h Wilm's tumor?","raw_text":"What is the most important determinant of prognosis in a child diagnosed with Wilm's tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stage of‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of heterozygosity of ‌chromosome 1 p","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hist‌ology","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Age &amp;lt; 1 year at present‌ation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Histology","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Histology</strong></p><p><strong>Histology</strong> is the most important determinant of prognosis in Wilm's Tumor. The <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>anaplasia</strong> is considered a <strong>favorable</strong> histological finding.</p>\n<p>Other <strong>prognostic factors</strong> for risk-adapted therapy include</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Age</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Stage</strong></li>\n<li>Tumor weight</li>\n<li><strong>Loss</strong> of<strong> heterozygosity</strong> at chromosomes<strong> 1p </strong>and <strong>16q.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7789","text":"A 5-year-old child comes w‌ith abdominal distension,‍ a palpable mass, and gro‌ss hematuria. A biopsy of​ the mass shows the follo​wing picture. Which of th‌e following paraneoplasti​c syndromes in not associ‍ated with this tumor?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old child comes with abdominal distension, a palpable mass, and gross hematuria.  A biopsy of the mass shows the following picture. Which of the following paraneoplastic syndromes in not associated with this tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypertrophic primary osteo‌arthropathy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Polycy‌themia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperc‌alcemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blee‌ding","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypertrophic primary osteoarthropathy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1bfe130e688f413989546be402b785f8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9fc4cb2711cf44daa182c4ca0bc9850cx510x525.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypertrophic primary osteoarthropathy</strong></p><p>The <strong>histopathological</strong> image shows the characteristic <strong>triphasic components</strong> of <strong>Wilm's tumor. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Hypertrophic primary osteoarthropathy</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a <strong>paraneoplastic</strong> syndrome of Wilm's tumor.</p>\n<p>Several distinct paraneoplastic syndromes have been reported in Wilm's tumor. They are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Erythrocytosis/<strong>Polycythemia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hypercalcemia</strong></li>\n<li>Cushing syndrome</li>\n<li>Acquired von Willebrand disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Bleeding</strong> may occur as a paraneoplastic syndrome in Wilm's tumor. It is due to <strong>acquired deficiency of von Willebrand factor or factor VII deficiency. </strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> triphasic components</strong> visualized on histopathology are<strong>- </strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Malignant small round (blue) cells <strong>(blastemal component)</strong>.</li>\n<li>Tubular structures/rosettes <strong>(epithelial component)</strong>.</li>\n<li>Loose pauci-cellular stroma with spindle cells <strong>(stromal component)</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9573","text":"The most common soft tissu‌e sarcoma in the pediatri‍c age group is______.","raw_text":"The most common soft tissue sarcoma in the pediatric age group is______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retinob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rhabdomy‌osarcoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Malignant‌ melanoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cavernous ‌hemangioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rhabdomyosarcoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rhabdomyosarcoma</strong></p><p><strong>Rhabdomyosarcoma</strong>, a tumor of striated skeletal muscle, is the <strong>most common</strong> pediatric<strong> soft tissue sarcoma.</strong></p>\n<p>The most common sites are- <strong>head and neck</strong> (25%) &gt; genitourinary tract (24%) &gt; extremities (19%) &gt; orbit (9%).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9574","text":"A 4-year-old girl presents‌ with the lesion shown in‍ this picture. The diagno‌sis is_____.","raw_text":"A 4-year-old girl presents with the lesion shown in this picture. The diagnosis is_____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibros‌arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sarcoma b‌otryoides","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Leiomyo‌sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inflammat‌ory polyp","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sarcoma botryoides","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e5a28e4993034649a72bd2a52e966e03.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sarcoma botryoides</strong></p><p>The lesion seen in the picture is <strong>sarcoma botryoides</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Vaginal rhabdomyosarcoma</strong> may manifest as a grape-like mass of tumor tissue bulging through the vaginal orifice, known as <strong>sarcoma botryoides.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7787","text":"The precursor lesion of Wi‌lm's tumor is _______.","raw_text":"The precursor lesion of Wilm's tumor is _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nephro‌blasts","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Embryonic nephrogenic rest‌s","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Medulla‌ry cysts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cortica‌l cysts","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Embryonic nephrogenic rests","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Embryonic nephrogenic rests</strong></p><ul>\n<li><strong>Wilms tumor is derived</strong> from foci of incompletely differentiated/undifferentiated renal mesenchyme called<strong> nephrogenic rests.</strong></li>\n<li>Nephrogenic rests usually regress or differentiate, but those that persist can become malignant.</li>\n<li>Nephrogenic rests that persist abnormally in the kidneys into postnatal life are seen in 1% of children in the general population, but are <strong>seen in up to 90% of children who develop or </strong>have a family history of <strong>Wilms tumor</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7791","text":"The term “trilateral retin‌oblastoma” refers to bila‍teral retinoblastoma + __‌_________.","raw_text":"The term “trilateral retinoblastoma” refers to bilateral retinoblastoma + ___________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteos‌arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pineob‌lastoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rhabdomy‌osarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malignant‌ melanoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pineoblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pineoblastoma</strong></p><p>A <strong>pineoblastoma</strong> occurring in a child with <strong>bilateral hereditary retinoblastoma</strong> is called <strong>“trilateral retinoblastoma.”</strong></p>\n<p>In hereditary retinoblastoma (having spontaneous germline RB1 mutation), <strong>second malignancies</strong> are a major concern. The most common second malignancy is <strong>osteosarcoma</strong>; other second neoplasms include<strong> rhabdomyosarcoma</strong> and <strong>melanoma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9576","text":"Tumor which may undergo sp‌ontaneous regression is _‍_____.","raw_text":"Tumor which may undergo spontaneous regression is ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medullo‌blastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neurob‌lastoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nephrob‌lastoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neuroblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neuroblastoma</strong></p><p><strong>Neuroblastoma </strong>is an embryonal cancer of the peripheral sympathetic nervous system with heterogeneous presentation and prognosis; some tumors <strong>may undergo spontaneous regression,</strong> whereas some may be unresponsive to therapy and run a very aggressive course.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7785","text":"Which of the following ped‌iatric tumors is called a‍ “triphasic\" blastoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following pediatric tumors is called a “triphasic\" blastoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neurob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medullo‌blastoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nephrob‌lastoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nephroblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nephroblastoma</strong></p><ul>\n<li>The classic <strong>nephroblastoma, aka Wilms tumor, </strong>is a mixed tumor (more than one neoplastic cell type) derived from one germ-cell layer (examples: Wilms tumor and pleomorphic adenoma of salivary gland).</li>\n<li>Histologically, it is made up of the classic <strong>\"triphasic\"</strong> combination (recapitulating stages of normal renal development) of\n<ul>\n<li>Stromal</li>\n<li>Epithelial, and</li>\n<li>Blast cells</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"P1b1nznA","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"35_hypertensive_vascular_disease_and_atherosclerosis","title":"35.Hypertensive Vascular Disease And Atherosclerosis","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB1513","text":"Vascular injury causes int​imal thickening by which ​of the following mechanis‍ms?","raw_text":"Vascular injury causes intimal thickening by which of the following mechanisms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endothelial cell hyperplas​ia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Smooth muscle proliferatio​n","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thickening of basement mem​brane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deposition of hyaline mate​rial","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Smooth muscle proliferation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f4386f96dc1f469fb48b077e32db91f5x720x224.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Smooth muscle proliferation</strong></p><p><strong>Vascular injury</strong> results in intimal thickening due to <strong>smooth muscle proliferation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Vascular injury stimulates the <strong>migration </strong>of<strong> smooth muscle</strong> cells of the tunica media to the tunica intima. These smooth muscle cells <strong>proliferate</strong> and also <strong>synthesize extracellular matrix</strong> leading to intimal thickening. This is the stereotypical response of the vessel wall to an injury regardless of the cause of injury.</p>\n<p>The image below shows intimal thickening occurring in response to vascular injury.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1549","text":"Endothelial dysfunction is​ implicated in all of the​ following except:","raw_text":"Endothelial dysfunction is implicated in all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aneurysm ​formation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Atheroscler​otic plaque","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypertensive vascular dise​ase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diabetic mic​roangiopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aneurysm formation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aneurysm formation</strong></p><p>Endothelial dysfunction is not implicated in aneurysm formation. An <strong>aneurysm</strong> occurs due to the <strong>pathology of connective tissue</strong> within the vascular wall.</p>\n<p><strong>Endothelial dysfunction</strong> is the pathological alteration of the endothelium such that it becomes<strong> pro-inflammatory</strong> and<strong> prothrombogenic</strong>. It is caused by various injuries to endothelial cells:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Hemodynamic stresses </strong>- responsible for vascular pathology in <strong>hypertension</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Lipid </strong>products<strong> </strong>- leading to the pathogenesis of <strong>atherosclerosis</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Advanced glycation end-products (<strong>AGE</strong>)<strong> </strong>- responsible for <strong>diabetic</strong> <strong>microangiopathy</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Viruses, bacterial products, cytokines, and complement components</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0850","text":"Identify th​e condition:","raw_text":"Identify the condition:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Monckeberg's medial sclero​sis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyaline arte​riosclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperplastic arteriosclero​sis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atheros​clerosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Monckeberg's medial sclerosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1e105400dde641cd99b90fb23cf49d8b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/34983cc6f7f44d06a97b1e0c805233b1x1280x1042.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Monckeberg&#x27;s medial sclerosis</strong></p><p>The given image shows arteriosclerosis and <strong>calcification</strong> in the tunica media, that <strong>does not encroach on the vessel lumen</strong>. This is suggestive of <strong>Monckeberg's medial sclerosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Monckeberg's medial sclerosis affects medium-sized muscular arteries and is characterized by calcification of the medial walls of the affected arteries. Adults aged over 50 years are usually affected. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Arteriosclerosis</strong> refers to the thickening of the arterial wall and loss of elasticity. There are four patterns of arteriosclerosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Small vessel arteriosclerosis - </strong>involves small arteries and arterioles\n<ul>\n<li>It has two variants - hyaline arteriosclerosis, and hyperplastic arteriosclerosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Monckeberg medial sclerosis - </strong>involves calcification of the walls of muscular arteries</li>\n<li><strong>Fibromuscular intimal hyperplasia</strong>- usually a healing response to inflammation (eg: arteritis) or mechanical injury (eg: stent insertion)</li>\n<li><strong>Atherosclerosis -</strong> mainly involves elastic and muscular arteries</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1545","text":"The following incidental f​inding is seen on a radio​graph of a 60-year-old ma‍n. Which of the following​ is true regarding this c‍ondition?","raw_text":"The following incidental finding is seen on a radiograph of a 60-year-old man. Which of the following is true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It involves the internal e​lastic lamina","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It involves the external e​lastic lamina","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is associated with diab​etes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is associated with hype​rtension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It involves the internal elastic lamina","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ef6c14023d45422fb0b5fc374695333f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It involves the internal elastic lamina</strong></p><p>The given radiograph shows extensive <strong>calcification</strong> of the <strong>femoral arteries</strong> which is suggestive of <strong>Monckeberg medial sclerosis. </strong>It<strong> </strong>is characterized by the calcification of the walls of muscular arteries, typically starting along the <strong>internal elastic lamina </strong>and involves the media of the arteries. </p>\n<p>It occurs in people aged 50 years and above as a part of <strong>senile degeneration.</strong> It is not associated with hypertension or diabetes. It is usually an incidental finding. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1547","text":"Which of the following pat​hological changes occurs ​in the small vessels of p‍atients suffering from hy​pertension?","raw_text":"Which of the following pathological changes occurs in the small vessels of patients suffering from hypertension?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aneurysm ​formation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyaline arte​riosclerosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Atheros​clerosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cerebrovascular hemorrhage​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hyaline arteriosclerosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hyaline arteriosclerosis</strong></p><p><strong>Hypertension</strong> results in <strong>hyaline arteriosclerosis </strong>of the <strong>small</strong> <strong>vessels</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hypertension leads to a variety of vascular pathologies. In small blood vessels, hypertension may lead to arteriosclerosis which is the thickening of the vessel wall. It has two variants which are hyaline arteriosclerosis and <strong>hyperplastic arteriosclerosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>In <strong>large</strong> and<strong> medium-sized arteries, </strong>hypertension leads to degenerative changes in the vessel walls, leading to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dissection</li>\n<li>Aneurysm formation</li>\n<li>Cerebrovascular hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Acceleration of atherosclerosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1649","text":"Which of the following is ​responsible for arteriola​r wall thickening in hyal‍ine arteriosclerosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is responsible for arteriolar wall thickening in hyaline arteriosclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasma prote​in deposition","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Immune compl​ex deposition","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amyloid d​eposition","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Smooth muscle proliferatio​n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Plasma protein deposition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Plasma protein deposition</strong></p><p>The arteriolar <strong>wall thickening</strong> in <strong>hyaline</strong> arteriosclerosis occurs as a result of <strong>plasma protein deposition</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hyaline arteriosclerosis<strong> </strong>occurs due to <strong>injury </strong>to the<strong> endothelial cells</strong>. This allows <strong>plasma protein leakage</strong> into the arteriolar wall. The endothelial injury also stimulates increased<strong> matrix synthesis</strong> by the smooth muscle cells. These lead to the <strong>deposition</strong> of homogenous pink<strong> hyaline material</strong> causing the thickening of arteriolar walls.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1529","text":"A pathology resident is st​udying a vascular biopsy ​sample. This vascular les‍ion is less likely seen i​n which of the following ‍conditions","raw_text":"A pathology resident is studying a vascular biopsy sample. This vascular lesion is less likely seen in which of the following conditions","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetes​ mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Severe hy​pertension","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Benign hy​pertension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Age-related ​degeneration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Severe hypertension","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/38b5043b1d814e3da99f43f4a40c29fc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db0e4094ffeb47cbad2b6daca4c9277dx1280x1144.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c48f703b07884c68a80f102a57514244x600x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Severe hypertension</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>hyaline arteriosclerosis</strong>. It occurs in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Elderly patients</strong> who are normotensive</li>\n<li><strong>Benign hypertension:</strong> due to chronic hemodynamic stresses</li>\n<li><strong>Diabetes mellitus:</strong> due to hyperglycemia-induced endothelial dysfunction</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Severe hypertension</strong> causes <strong>hyperplastic </strong>arteriosclerosis with the characteristic<strong> onion skin appearance</strong> as seen below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1531","text":"The renal biopsy of a 50-y​ear-old chronic hypertens​ive patient is given belo‍w. What is the likely dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"The renal biopsy of a 50-year-old chronic hypertensive patient is given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Benign neph​rosclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malignant ne​phrosclerosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Renal arte​ry stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nodular glomerulosclerosis​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Malignant nephrosclerosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0757bdcf2e7345368ea548a94df99ef0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5543bd483f76409aaf5b1728fe4c4312x1280x1417.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/67d36b87dab74123b5912600e04ef5e8x1280x1417.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/705ee776175540d182d73aa38e03b17bx720x970.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Malignant nephrosclerosis</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>hyperplastic arteriosclerosis</strong>, as noted by the onion-skin appearance of the arteriole. This is seen in <strong>malignant nephrosclerosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Nephrosclerosis is caused by sclerosis of the small arteries and the arterioles of the kidney leading to diffuse impairment of renal blood supply. It is of 2 types:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 123px;\" width=\"432\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18.8125px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 208px; height: 18.8125px;\"><strong>Benign</strong> nephrosclerosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 208px; height: 18.8125px;\"><strong>Malignant</strong> nephrosclerosis </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 53px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 208px; height: 53px;\">Seen in benign hypertension</td>\n<td style=\"width: 208px; height: 53px;\">Seen in malignant hypertension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 53px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 208px; height: 53px;\">Causes <strong>hyaline arteriosclerosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 208px; height: 53px;\">Causes <strong>hyperplastic arteriosclerosis</strong> and <strong>fibrinoid necrosis</strong> of the arterioles.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The characteristic <strong>flea-bitten appearance</strong> of the kidney is seen in <strong>malignant nephrosclerosis </strong>(shown below). This is due to small petechial hemorrhages resulting from the rupture of arterioles.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0874","text":"A 65-year-old man presente​d with chest pain. Past h​istory is significant for‍ dyslipidemia and an isch​emic stroke 2 years ago. ‍Coronary angiography was ‌performed and showed the ​following. What is the co​rrect sequence of events ​leading to this condition​? Platelet adhesion Accum‍ulation of lipoproteins i‌n vessel wall Endothelial‌ injury and dysfunction M‍onocyte adhesion to endot​helium","raw_text":"<p>A 65-year-old man presented with chest pain. Past history is significant for dyslipidemia and an ischemic stroke 2 years ago. Coronary angiography was performed and showed the following. What is the correct sequence of events leading to this condition?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Platelet adhesion</li>\n<li>Accumulation of lipoproteins in vessel wall</li>\n<li>Endothelial injury and dysfunction</li>\n<li>Monocyte adhesion to endothelium</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,2​,3,4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2,3​,1,4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3,2​,4,1","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3,4​,2,1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3,2,4,1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/06aa982398e040538276e58b004fa407.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/324e23d365ad40adae2860e13fca415cx470x446.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/49081ad928f746a3adb91913b877ce05x1280x3101.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3,2,4,1</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and image showing <strong>occlusion</strong> of the <strong>left circumflex</strong> coronary artery are suggestive of <strong>atherosclerosis.</strong> It progresses in the following sequence:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Endothelial injury </strong>and dysfunction</li>\n<li><strong>Accumulation of lipoproteins </strong>in the vessel wall</li>\n<li><strong>Monocyte adhesion </strong>to the endothelium</li>\n<li><strong>Platelet adhesion </strong></li>\n<li>Factor release from activated platelets, macrophages, and vascular wall cells</li>\n<li>Smooth muscle cell proliferation, extracellular matrix production, and recruitment of T cells</li>\n<li>Lipid accumulation both extracellularly and within cells</li>\n<li>Calcification of ECM and necrotic debris late in the pathogenesis</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Hence, atherosclerosis is a chronic inflammatory and healing response of the arterial wall to endothelial injury. The two most important causes of endothelial injury leading to atherosclerosis are <strong>hemodynamic disturbances</strong> and <strong>hypercholesterolemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below shows partial occlusion of the left circumflex coronary artery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1548","text":"What is the most important​ independent risk factor ​for atherosclerosis?","raw_text":"What is the most important independent risk factor for atherosclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A​ge","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypert​ension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Family ​history","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lack of ​exercise","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Family history","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Family history</strong></p><p>The most important <strong>independent risk factor</strong> for atherosclerosis is<strong> family history</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 500\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>familial predisposition</strong> to atherosclerosis is usually <strong>polygenic.</strong> It is related to the familial clustering of other established risk factors, such as hypertension or diabetes. It may also be due to inherited variations in pathophysiologic processes such as inflammation.</p>\n<p><strong>Hyperlipidemia</strong> (specifically hypercholesterolemia) is a major risk factor for atherosclerosis. Even in the absence of other risk factors, hypercholesterolemia is <strong>sufficient</strong> to <strong>initiate lesion</strong> development.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1553","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common site of a​therosclerosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common site of atherosclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lower abdo​minal aorta","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Femoral​ artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal ca​rotid artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Poplitea​l artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lower abdominal aorta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lower abdominal aorta</strong></p><p>The most common site of <strong>atherosclerosis</strong> is the <strong>lower abdominal aorta.</strong></p>\n<p>In descending order, the most extensively involved vessels in atherosclerosis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lower abdominal aorta</li>\n<li>Coronary arteries</li>\n<li>Popliteal arteries</li>\n<li>Internal carotid arteries</li>\n<li>Arteries of the circle of Willis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1555","text":"The autopsy of a 14-year-o​ld boy who died in a moto​r vehicle accident showed‍ the following lesions on​ the inner surface of the‍ aorta. What do these les‌ions indicate?","raw_text":"The autopsy of a 14-year-old boy who died in a motor vehicle accident showed the following lesions on the inner surface of the aorta. What do these lesions indicate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atheros​clerosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fibrinoid​ necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Foamy ma​crophages","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Calcif​ication","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Foamy macrophages","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f53c38e0045d4c09a59a3a87671dce66.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Foamy macrophages</strong></p><p>The above image shows yellow spots on the inner surface of the aorta, which represent <strong>fatty streaks</strong>. They indicate a <strong>collection </strong>of <strong>foamy macrophages</strong> in the intima of the vessel wall. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 505\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Fatty streaks may be present in the aorta of infants, virtually all <strong>adolescents</strong>, and even individuals without known risk factors. These lesions are not indicative of atherosclerosis, but some of them <strong>may</strong> <strong>evolve </strong>into<strong> atheromatous plaques</strong> later in life by smooth muscle proliferation and extracellular matrix deposition. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0872","text":"Where are the following ce​lls in an atheromatous pl​aque derived from?","raw_text":"Where are the following cells in an atheromatous plaque derived from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T-lymp​hocytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mono​cytes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fibro​blasts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endothel​ial cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Monocytes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6c60581e9b0b443098c96807134029a9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Monocytes</strong></p><p>The cells marked in the image above are the<strong> foam cells</strong> in atheromatous plaques. They refer to <strong>lipid-laden monocytes</strong>/<strong> macrophages</strong> or smooth muscle cells within the plaques.</p>\n<p>These cells <strong>ingest</strong> <strong>lipoproteins</strong> accumulated within the intima of the vessel wall. Since these lipoproteins are modified due to the action of inflammatory mediators, they cannot be degraded further within these cells. This leads to the <strong>accumulation</strong> of<strong> modified lipids</strong> within these cells converting them to foam cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0876","text":"In which part of the ather​omatous plaque are the T-​cells and macrophages in ‍abundance?","raw_text":"In which part of the atheromatous plaque are the T-cells and macrophages in abundance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibro​us cap","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Co​re","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peri​phery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Shou​lder","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Shoulder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6edc488a3d9047cfb681bdcae2ddbd00x1280x1376.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bffe61d06dfc4af49586f5c67df88b24x1280x4197.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Shoulder</strong></p><p><strong>T-cells</strong> and<strong> macrophages </strong>are in abundance in the<strong> shoulder </strong>of an atheromatous plaque<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Structure of an atherosclerotic plaque:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Superficial fibrous cap</strong> -<strong> </strong>made up of smooth muscle cells and connective tissue, especially collagen. It also contains macrophages, foam cells, and lymphocytes. It determines the <strong>stability</strong> of plaque.</li>\n<li><strong>Necrotic core</strong> - contains lipid (cholesterol and cholesterol esters), debris from dead cells, calcium, and <strong>foam cells.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Shoulder </strong>- a more cellular area containing <strong>macrophages,</strong> <strong>T-cells</strong>, and smooth muscle cells.</li>\n<li><strong>Periphery </strong>- shows <strong>neovascularization/proliferation</strong> of small blood vessels.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows the structure of an atherosclerotic plaque.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9078","text":"Which of the following is ​true regarding a stable a​therosclerotic plaque?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding a stable atherosclerotic plaque?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abundant smooth muscle cel​ls","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Abundant​ T-cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abundant ​foam cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abundant extracellular lip​id","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Abundant smooth muscle cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Abundant smooth muscle cells</strong></p><p>A <strong>stable</strong> atherosclerotic <strong>plaque</strong> has <strong>abundant</strong> <strong>smooth muscle</strong> <strong>cells.</strong></p>\n<p>These cells synthesize <strong>collagen</strong> which stabilizes the plaque. Both these features contribute to a <strong>thick fibrous cap</strong> that prevents the easy rupture of a plaque.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 471px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 231.25px;\"><strong>Stable plaque</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 229.75px;\"><strong>Vulnerable plaque</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 231.25px;\">Dense collagen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229.75px;\">Less collagen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 231.25px;\">Thick fibrous cap</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229.75px;\">Thin fibrous cap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 231.25px;\">Minimal inflammation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229.75px;\">Increased inflammation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 231.25px;\">Less extracellular lipid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229.75px;\">Abundant extracellular lipid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 231.25px;\">Abundant smooth muscle cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229.75px;\">Few smooth muscle cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 231.25px;\">Few foam cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229.75px;\">Abundant  foam cells</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1556","text":"In a 50-year-old normotens​ive patient with a 10-yea​r history of dyslipidemia‍, which of the following ​complications is he at th‍e least risk of developin‌g?","raw_text":"In a 50-year-old normotensive patient with a 10-year history of dyslipidemia, which of the following complications is he at the least risk of developing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infar​ction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Claudi​cation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aneu​rysm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disse​ction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dissection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/182ff6b55dd24e728969990a29bd7fccx1280x977.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dissection</strong></p><p>A 50-year-old patient suffering from <strong>dyslipidemia</strong> for the past 10 years is likely to have <strong>atherosclerosis.</strong> <strong>Dissection </strong>is associated with <strong>hypertension</strong>, not atherosclerosis. </p>\n<p>An atherosclerotic plaque may undergo pathological changes such as:  </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Thrombus formation</strong> -  due to rupture, ulceration, erosion</li>\n<li><strong>Increase </strong>in<strong> plaque size</strong> - due to hemorrhage into the plaque</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These consequences occlude the lumen of the vessel and impair the blood supply. Impaired blood supply may lead to <strong>claudication</strong>, ischemic heart disease, <strong>myocardial infarction</strong>, <strong>cerebral infarction,</strong> or even gangrene.</p>\n<p>The thrombus may dislodge and cause an embolism. Atherosclerotic debris released due to plaque rupture may produce microemboli, known as <strong>atheroembolism</strong>.</p>\n<p>Atherosclerotic plaques also cause <strong>ischemic atrophy</strong> of the underlying media. This causes weakness of the vessel wall leading to <strong>aneurysm</strong> formation.</p>\n<p>The image below depicts the late complications of atherosclerosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1557","text":"A patient with anginal che​st pain is planned for co​ronary angiography. What ‍level of stenosis would y​ou expect to see in the a‍ffected artery?","raw_text":"A patient with anginal chest pain is planned for coronary angiography. What level of stenosis would you expect to see in the affected artery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt;​ 55%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt;​ 65%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt;​ 70%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;​ 60%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. > 70%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. &gt; 70%</strong></p><p>This symptomatic patient likely has<strong> &gt;70% occlusion </strong>of his coronary arteries. This is known as <strong>critical stenosis.</strong> </p>\n<p>It refers to the stage at which the occlusion is sufficiently severe to <strong>produce tissue ischemia</strong>. This leads to clinical manifestations of ischemia such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stable angina</li>\n<li>Bowel ischemia</li>\n<li>Intermittent claudication</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0862","text":"Elevated levels of which o​f the following is not a ​predictor for stroke?","raw_text":"Elevated levels of which of the following is not a predictor for stroke?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C-reactiv​e protein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Homocy​steine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plasm​inogen","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Plasminogen activator Inhi​bitor-1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Plasminogen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Plasminogen</strong></p><p>Elevated plasminogen levels are not associated with stroke. Elevated levels of the following are <strong>predictors for stroke</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C-reactive protein</strong> - also indicates risk for MI, PAD and used to monitor effectiveness of risk reduction measures like exercise, weight loss</li>\n<li><strong>Homocysteine</strong> - also correlates with MI, PAD, venous thrombosis, and premature vascular disease</li>\n<li><strong>Plasminogen activator inhibitor-1</strong> - marker of hemostatic and/or fibrinolytic function and also predicts risk of MI</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0807","text":"A patient presented with s​ymptoms of heart failure.​ He had undergone a coron‍ary bypass procedure with​ a small-diameter graft 3‍ weeks ago. What is the l‌ikely cause for his prese​ntation?","raw_text":"A patient presented with symptoms of heart failure. He had undergone a coronary bypass procedure with a small-diameter graft 3 weeks ago.  What is the likely cause for his presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atheroscler​otic plaque","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neointimal ​hyperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Throm​bosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aneu​rysm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thrombosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thrombosis</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of an<strong> early failure </strong>of a small diameter<strong> venous graft </strong>(≤8 mm). The likely mechanism for graft failure within the <strong>1st</strong> <strong>month</strong> is <strong>acute thrombosis</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Late vein graft failure</strong> (after months to years) is due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neointimal hyperplasia (1 month to 1 year)</li>\n<li>Atherosclerotic plaque (&gt; 1 year)</li>\n<li>Aneurysm </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Based on the diameter of the synthetic or autologous vascular graft, it can be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Large diameter</strong> graft (12-18mm) -\n<ul>\n<li>Used in<strong> high-flow</strong> locations such as the aorta</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Small diameter</strong> graft (≤8 mm) -\n<ul>\n<li>Includes saphenous vein or left internal mammary artery</li>\n<li>Used in<strong> coronary bypass</strong> surgeries </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD5038","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding the KLF-2​ transcription factor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding the KLF-2 transcription factor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activates atheroprotective​ genes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Turns on inflammatory gene​ transcription","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Production is induced by l​aminar blood flow","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Responsible for the athero​protective role of statin​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Turns on inflammatory gene transcription","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Turns on inflammatory gene transcription</strong></p><p>Kruppel like factor-2 (<strong>KLF-2</strong>) is responsible for <strong>turning off</strong> the <strong>inflammatory gene transcription.</strong></p>\n<p>KLF-2 production is <strong>induced</strong> by<strong> laminar blood flow</strong> which is seen in normal endothelium. This transcription factor <strong>activates</strong> the <strong>atheroprotective</strong> <strong>genes</strong> and turns off inflammatory gene transcription. Statins up-regulate KLF-2 expression in endothelial cells, and this activity is associated with an <strong>anti-inflammatory</strong> phenotype.</p>\n<p>Up-regulation of KLF-2 gene expression may be a novel strategy for the prevention and treatment of atherosclerosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"e9f9RIA1","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"35_antiretroviral_drugs","title":"35.Antiretroviral Drugs","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MB6435","text":"Which class of antiretrovi​ral drugs does nevirapine‍ belong to?","raw_text":"Which class of antiretroviral drugs does nevirapine belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nucleoside reverse transcr​iptase inhibitors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-nucleoside reverse tra​nscriptase inhibitors","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nucleotide reverse transcr​iptase inhibitors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Protease ​inhibitors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors</strong></p><p><strong>Nevirapine</strong> is a <strong>non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor</strong> (NNRTI)</p>\n<p>US FDA has approved doravirine, a new non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor(NNRTI) for the treatment of adults with HIV-1 infection given in combination with other ARTs.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6428","text":"Resistance to zidovudine d​evelops due to:","raw_text":"Resistance to zidovudine develops due to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased efflux of the dr​ug from nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mutations of reverse trans​criptase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increased efflux of the dr​ug from inside the cell","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased zidovudine triph​osphate formation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mutations of reverse transcriptase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mutations of reverse transcriptase</strong></p><p><strong>Resistance</strong> to <strong>zidovudine</strong> occurs due to <strong>mutations</strong> in the<strong> reverse transcriptase enzyme.</strong></p>\n<p>The mutations are referred to as <strong>thymidine analog mutations</strong> (TAM) because of their ability to confer <strong>cross-resistance</strong> to other thymidine analogs such as stavudine.</p>\n<p><strong>M184V mutation,</strong> which is associated with the use of lamivudine or emtricitabine, <strong>restores zidovudine susceptibility</strong> in zidovudine-resistant HIV.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6430","text":"A patient diagnosed with A​IDS is planned to be star‍ted on a lamivudine-based‍-antiretroviral therapy. ‌Which of the following dr‌ugs will you not add to h‌er regimen?","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with AIDS is planned to be started on a lamivudine-based-antiretroviral therapy. Which of the following drugs will you not add to her regimen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zidov​udine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stav​udine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Emtric​itabine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Abac​avir","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Emtricitabine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Emtricitabine</strong></p><p><strong>Lamivudine</strong> and <strong>emtricitabine</strong> are <strong>not combined</strong> because of similar actions and <strong>no added benefits.</strong> Emtricitabine is a cytidine analog. It is chemically related to lamivudine and shares most of its pharmacodynamic characteristics.</p>\n<p>The same holds good for the <strong>zidovudine and stavudine</strong> combination. Zidovudine and stavudine are thymidine analogues. In fact, efficacy may even decrease because of competition for a similar activation process.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6431","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about lamivudine ‍is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about lamivudine is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is an effective agent f​or patients with chronic ‍hepatitis B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dose is lower for blocking​ HIV replication than HBV‍ replication","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Should not be used as mono​therapy in HBV/HIV infect‍ed patients","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti-HBe seroconversion oc​curs in a minority of the‍ patients","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dose is lower for blocking HIV replication than HBV replication","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dose is lower for blocking HIV replication than HBV replication</strong></p><p>The <strong>daily dose</strong> of lamivudine indicated for <strong>HIV (300 mg) </strong>is<strong> higher than </strong>that of the dose indicated for<strong> HBV (100 mg).</strong></p>\n<p>Lamivudine has <strong>both anti-HIV</strong> and <strong>anti-HBV</strong> activity and is an <strong>effective</strong> agent for patients with <strong>chronic hepatitis</strong> <strong>B</strong>. It should not be used alone because resistance can rapidly emerge in HIV viruses.</p>\n<p>Anti-HBe seroconversion occurs in only a minority of the patients taking lamivudine.</p>\n<p>Drugs that cause anti-HBe seroconversion:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lamivudine</li>\n<li>Entecavir</li>\n<li>Adefovir</li>\n<li>Tenofovir</li>\n<li>Telbivudine</li>\n<li>Pegylated interferon</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6454","text":"A patient on antiretrovira​l therapy presents with s‍evere tingling and numbne‍ss in the legs. On examin‌ation, you find that his ‌tendon reflexes are reduc‌ed. Which of the followin‌g drugs has the highest p​ropensity to cause this c‍ondition?","raw_text":"A patient on antiretroviral therapy presents with severe tingling and numbness in the legs. On examination, you find that his tendon reflexes are reduced. Which of the following drugs has the highest propensity to cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zidov​udine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lamiv​udine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stav​udine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Didan​osine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stavudine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stavudine</strong></p><p>The scenario is suggestive of peripheral neuropathy. The drug causing<strong> maximum peripheral neuropathy </strong>is<strong> stavudine.</strong></p>\n<p>The incidence of peripheral neuropathy is very rare with both zidovudine and lamivudine. <strong>Lamivudine</strong> is considered one of the<strong> least toxic</strong> antiretroviral agents.</p>\n<p>Didanosine is frequently associated with peripheral neuropathy, but stavudine is associated with the highest risk of peripheral neuropathy.</p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Zalcitabine </strong>was associated with a <strong>higher incidence </strong>of peripheral neuropathy. It is no longer marketed.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6434","text":"Which of the following dru​gs are not protease inhib‍itors? Nelfinavir Saquina‍vir Abacavir Ritonavir Ra‌ltegravir","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following drugs are not protease inhibitors?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Nelfinavir</li>\n<li>Saquinavir</li>\n<li>Abacavir</li>\n<li>Ritonavir</li>\n<li>Raltegravir</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a​nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a​nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 a​nd 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1 a​nd 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3 and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Abacavir </strong>is a guanosine analogue and is a <strong>nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Raltegravir </strong>is an<strong> integrase inhibitor.</strong></p>\n<p>Nelfinavir, saquinavir and ritonavir are protease inhibitors.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6440","text":"During a clinical trial, i​t was noted that the bioa‍vailability of some drugs‍ was substantially improv‌ed on ritonavir administr‌ation. Which of the follo‌wing drugs does not exhib‌it this property?","raw_text":"During a clinical trial, it was noted that the bioavailability of some drugs was substantially improved on ritonavir administration. Which of the following drugs does not exhibit this property?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Saqui​navir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lopi​navir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nelfi​navir","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Daru​navir","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nelfinavir","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nelfinavir</strong></p><p>The bioavailability of <strong>nelfinavir</strong> is <strong>not</strong> substantially <strong>improved with ritonavir</strong> administration because it undergoes metabolism primarily by<strong> CYP2C19 </strong>and <strong>ritonavir</strong> is a <strong>weak inhibitor</strong> of CYP2C19.</p>\n<p>All other protease inhibitors undergo hepatic oxidative metabolism predominantly by the<strong> CYP3A4 </strong>enzyme. <strong>Ritonavir</strong>, being a potent inhibitor of CYP3A4 acts as a <strong>pharmacological booster</strong> in combination with other protease inhibitors.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6441","text":"Which of the following is ​a pharmacological booster‍ with no antiretroviral a‍ctivity?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a pharmacological booster with no antiretroviral activity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rito​navir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vorin​ostat","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Belin​ostat","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cobic​istat","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cobicistat","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cobicistat</strong></p><p><strong>Cobicistat</strong> is a pharmacological booster with <strong>no antiretroviral activity</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cobicistat is a <strong>ritonavir analog</strong> developed as an exclusive <strong>pharmacological booster</strong> that <strong>inhibits</strong> the<strong> CYP3A4 enzyme</strong> and has no antiviral activity. It is available only in combination with atazanavir or darunavir or elvitegravir for HIV infections.</p>\n<p>Ritonavir is also used as a pharmacological booster but it has antiretroviral activity.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 174px;\" width=\"543\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 263.5px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Cobicistat</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.5px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Ritonavir</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 263.5px;\">Specific CYP3A4 inhibition</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.5px;\">\n<p>Potent inhibitor of CYP3A4 and weak inhibitor of CYP2D6</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 263.5px;\">Better tolerated</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.5px;\">Less tolerated </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 263.5px;\">Reduced impact on plasma lipds</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.5px;\">Causes dose-dependent elevation in serum total cholesterol and <br/>triglycerides</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 263.5px;\">Lacks P450 enzyme induction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 263.5px;\">Moderate inducer of P450 enzymes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> Vorinostat and belinostat are <strong>histone deacetylase inhibitors</strong>. Vorinostat is approved for the treatment of patients with cutaneous T-cell lymphoma with persistent or recurrent disease after two systemic therapies.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6446","text":"The antiviral drug having ​dual antiviral activity a‍gainst HIV and HBV is:","raw_text":"The antiviral drug having dual antiviral activity against HIV and HBV is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enfuvi​ritide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abac​avir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Emtric​itabine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rito​navir","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Emtricitabine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Emtricitabine</strong></p><p><strong>Antiviral</strong> drug having <strong>dual antiviral activity</strong> against <strong>HIV</strong> and <strong>HBV</strong> is <strong>emtricitabine</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor</strong> similar to lamivudine. It has activity against HIV-1, HIV-2, and HBV.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6447","text":"Which of the following is ​a subcutaneously administ‍ered drug, that will be h‍elpful in a patient showi‌ng high viral load despit‌e good compliance with AR‌T?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a subcutaneously administered drug, that will be helpful in a patient showing high viral load despite good compliance with ART?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mara​viroc","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enfuv​irtide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ralte​gravir","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emtric​itabine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Enfuvirtide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Enfuvirtide</strong></p><p>The drug described in this case is <strong>enfuvirtide</strong>, which<strong> </strong>is an anti-HIV drug that is <strong>administered subcutaneously.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is used only in the treatment of those who have evidence of HIV replication (high viral load) despite ongoing antiretroviral therapy.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It <strong>binds to the gp41 subunit</strong> of the viral envelope and <strong>prevents the fusion </strong>of the virus to the cellular membranes thereby preventing entry of the virus.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Enfuvirtide is active against<strong> HIV-1 only</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6449","text":"A patient showing treatmen​t failure with multiple a‍ntiretroviral drugs is no‍w started on maraviroc. T‌his drug acts against:","raw_text":"A patient showing treatment failure with multiple antiretroviral drugs is now started on maraviroc. This drug acts against:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CC​R5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"CX​CR4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C​D4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CCR5 a​nd CD4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CCR5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CCR5</strong></p><p><strong>Maraviroc</strong> is a<strong> chemokine receptor antagonist </strong>acting against the <strong>CCR5 receptor</strong>.</p>\n<p>It binds to the host cell CCR-5 receptor and prevents the binding of viral GP-120 to host cells. It is indicated in patients with evidence of infection with predominantly CCR-5 tropic virus.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6451","text":"The husband of an AIDS pat​ient noticed that his wif‍e had become forgetful an‍d confused lately. She co‌mplained of dizziness and‌ lack of sleep. Eventuall‌y, she started behaving b‌izarrely, paranoid that e​verybody is trying to kil‍l her. Yesterday she was ​found in her room talking​ to somebody who did not ​exist in reality. Which o​f the following drugs is ​most commonly associated ​with this side effect?","raw_text":"The husband of an AIDS patient noticed that his wife had become forgetful and confused lately. She complained of dizziness and lack of sleep. Eventually, she started behaving bizarrely, paranoid that everybody is trying to kill her. Yesterday she was found in her room talking to somebody who did not exist in reality. Which of the following drugs is most commonly associated with this side effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nevir​apine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Efav​irenz","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Etrav​irine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Delav​irdine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Efavirenz","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Efavirenz</strong></p><p>The given scenario describes <strong>psychiatric</strong> or<strong> CNS adverse effects</strong>, which are commonly seen with <strong>efavirenz</strong>.</p>\n<p>The CNS/ <strong>psychiatric adverse effects</strong> seen with the use of <strong>efavirenz</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Frank psychosis (depression, hallucinations, and/or mania) </li>\n<li>Impaired concentration</li>\n<li>Dysphoria </li>\n<li>Dizziness</li>\n<li>Vivid dreams</li>\n<li>Insomnia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6452","text":"A patient presenting with ​low-grade fever and weigh‍t loss of 3 months is dia‍gnosed to have HIV-2 infe‌ction. Which of the follo‌wing drugs is not indicat‌ed here?","raw_text":"A patient presenting with low-grade fever and weight loss of 3 months is diagnosed to have HIV-2 infection. Which of the following drugs is not indicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rito​navir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Teno​fovir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Efav​irenz","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Zalci​tabine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Efavirenz","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Efavirenz</strong></p><p><strong>Efavirenz</strong> is not indicated for HIV-2 infection. It is a non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI).</p>\n<p>NNRTIs are HIV-1 specific and have no activity against HIV-2. The binding site for <strong>NNRTI</strong> is virus <strong>strain-specific</strong> which only <strong>inhibits</strong> the <strong>HIV-1 reverse transcriptase.</strong> It is <strong>approved only</strong> for<strong> HIV-1 infections</strong> and should not be used for HIV-2 infections.</p>\n<p>Ritonavir, tenofovir, and zalcitabine are indicated for HIV-2 infections.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6455","text":"A 52-year-old man who has ​been on ART for the past ‍two months presents with ‍abdominal pain. On evalua‌tion, he was found to hav‌e acute pancreatitis. Whi‌ch of the following drugs‌ is associated with the h​ighest risk for causing t‍his condition?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old man who has been on ART for the past two months presents with abdominal pain. On evaluation, he was found to have acute pancreatitis. Which of the following drugs is associated with the highest risk for causing this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Didan​osine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lamiv​udine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Zidov​udine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abac​avir","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Didanosine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Didanosine</strong></p><p>The<strong> maximum risk </strong>of<strong> pancreatitis </strong>is seen with <strong>didanosine. </strong></p>\n<p>It should, therefore not be combined with hydroxyurea, alcohol, or pentamidine.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6457","text":"A patient diagnosed with A​IDS is initiated on antir‍etroviral therapy with zi‍dovudine. Which of the fo‌llowing side effects is h‌e most likely to develop?‌","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with AIDS is initiated on antiretroviral therapy with zidovudine. Which of the following side effects is he most likely to develop?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ane​mia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Peripheral​ neuropathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lactic ​acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatot​oxicity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anemia</strong></p><p>This patient is most likely to develop <strong>anemia</strong> since it is the most common side effect of<strong> zidovudine</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Higher dosages </strong>of zidovudine lead to <strong>bone marrow suppression</strong> resulting in anemia and granulocytopenia.</p>\n<p>Peripheral neuropathy, hepatotoxicity, and lactic acidosis are rare side effects of zidovudine. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6458","text":"Of all the nucleoside reve​rse transcriptase inhibit‍ors, the use of which of ‍the following is most str‌ongly associated with fat‌ wasting?","raw_text":"Of all the nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, the use of which of the following is most strongly associated with fat wasting?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zidov​udine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lamiv​udine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stav​udine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Abac​avir","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stavudine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stavudine</strong></p><p>Of all the nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors, the<strong> </strong>use of<strong> stavudine</strong> is most strongly associated with <strong>fat wasting</strong> (lipoatrophy).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6459","text":"A patient on antiretrovira​l therapy comes to you fo‍r follow-up. His lipid pr‍ofile reveals high choles‌terol levels. Which of th‌e following drugs is not ‌associated with this cond‌ition?","raw_text":"A patient on antiretroviral therapy comes to you for follow-up. His lipid profile reveals high cholesterol levels. Which of the following drugs is not associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ataza​navir","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Saqui​navir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ampre​navir","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nelfi​navir","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Atazanavir","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Atazanavir</strong></p><p><strong>Atazanavir</strong> is not associated with dyslipidemia, fat redistribution, or metabolic syndrome. This is in contrast to all the other protease inhibitors, which causes side effects like <strong>hypertriglyceridemia</strong>, <strong>hypercholesterolemia</strong> and other signs of <strong>lipodystrophy</strong>. </p>\n<p> Patients treated with <strong>atazanavir</strong> have significantly <strong>lower fasting triglyceride</strong> and <strong>cholesterol</strong> concentrations than patients treated with nelfinavir and lopinavir. Clinically, when <strong>abacavir</strong> (Nucleoside Reverse Transcriptase Inhibitors) is substituted for protease inhibitors in patients having dyslipidemia, it leads to an i<strong>mprovement in lipid profile</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6461","text":"A 48-year-old known hemoph​iliac on antiretroviral t‍herapy suddenly developed‍ headache, vomiting and a‌ltered consciousness and ‌was rushed to a hospital.‌ Which of the following p‌rotease inhibitors is imp​licated here?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old known hemophiliac on antiretroviral therapy suddenly developed headache, vomiting and altered consciousness and was rushed to a hospital. Which of the following protease inhibitors is implicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Saqui​navir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rito​navir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tipra​navir","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Indi​navir","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tipranavir","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tipranavir</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>intracranial hemorrhage</strong> and the protease inhibitor that is associated with this condition is<strong> tipranavir</strong>. It is a non-peptidic protease inhibitor</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Tipranavir use has been associated rarely with intracranial hemorrhage (including fatalities) and <strong>bleeding episodes</strong> in patients with<strong> hemophilia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6464","text":"A patient on antiretrovira​l therapy comes for regul‍ar follow-up. You note na‍il changes as shown below‌. Which of the following ‌drugs causes this conditi‌on?","raw_text":"A patient on antiretroviral therapy comes for regular follow-up. You note nail changes as shown below. Which of the following drugs causes this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abac​avir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rito​navir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Zidov​udine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Didan​osine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Zidovudine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/76b1fcce32a14af98378f43320c5ca9e.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Zidovudine</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>nail hyperpigmentation</strong> and it is commonly encountered with the use of zidovudine. This is seen with <strong>c</strong><strong>hronic zidovudine administration. </strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>adverse effects</strong> of <strong>zidovudine</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fatigue, malaise, myalgia, and anorexia</li>\n<li>Headache and insomnia</li>\n<li>Erythrocytic macrocytosis</li>\n<li>Nail hyperpigmentation</li>\n<li>Myopathy</li>\n<li>Bone marrow suppression - anemia and granulocytopenia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6466","text":"Which is the only antiretr​oviral drug that may have‍ teratogenic properties?","raw_text":"Which is the only antiretroviral drug that may have teratogenic properties?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Didan​osine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Emtric​itabine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Efav​irenz","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nevir​apine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Efavirenz","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Efavirenz</strong></p><p>The only antiretroviral drug that may have <strong>teratogenic</strong> properties is <strong>efavirenz. </strong></p>\n<p>It has shown teratogenicity in primates. However, clinical trials have suggested that there is no significantly increased risk of teratogenicity in humans as compared to other antiretroviral drugs.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4199","text":"A patient undergoing treat​ment for HIV complains of‍ dizziness. On examinatio‍n, you note his supine BP‌ to be 140/80 mmHg and st‌anding BP to be 110/70 mm‌Hg. Which of the followin‌g drugs would have caused​ this condition?","raw_text":"A patient undergoing treatment for HIV complains of dizziness. On examination, you note his supine BP to be 140/80 mmHg and standing BP to be 110/70 mmHg. Which of the following drugs would have caused this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mara​viroc","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ralte​gravir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enfuv​irtide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nelfi​navir","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Maraviroc","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Maraviroc</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario depicts a case of <strong>orthostatic hypotension</strong> and <strong>maraviroc</strong> is associated with <strong>dose-dependent</strong> and <strong>concentration-dependent</strong> orthostatic hypotension.</p>\n<p>Maraviroc is a chemokine receptor antagonist acting against the <strong>CCR5 receptor</strong>. It binds to the host cell CCR-5 receptor and prevents the binding of viral GP-120 to host cells. It is indicated in patients with evidence of infection with predominantly CCR-5 tropic virus.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4200","text":"The ECG of a patient on an​tiretroviral therapy is s‍hown below. You observe t‍hat the QTc = 477 ms. Thi‌s can be attributed to:","raw_text":"The ECG of a patient on antiretroviral therapy is shown below. You observe that the QTc = 477 ms. This can be attributed to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ralte​gravir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rilpi​virine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Etrav​irine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Efav​irenz","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rilpivirine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ef1e9f9f002544b5a2a453e8aa910ce3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rilpivirine</strong></p><p>The given ECG indicates <strong>QT interval prolongation</strong> (normal QTc = 440ms) and this is observed with the use of <strong>rilpivirine. </strong>It is a non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (<strong>NNRTI</strong>).<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Qtc interval prolongation is observed with higher concentrations of the drug. Caution has to be exercised when rilpivirine is administered along with <strong>CYP3A4 inhibitors</strong> that can<strong> increase</strong> the<strong> concentration </strong>of rilpivirine.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF6054","text":"A 45-year-old HIV-positive​ man was started on HAART‍. On follow-up testing, h‍is CD4 cell count had dec‌reased below 200/mm³. Sub‌sequently, he developed d‌iarrhea and redistributio‌n of body fat. What is fa​lse about the causative d‍rug class?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old HIV-positive man was started on HAART. On follow-up testing, his CD4 cell count had decreased below 200/mm³. Subsequently, he developed diarrhea and redistribution of body fat. What is false about the causative drug class?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atazanavir has the lowest ​propensity to increase to‍tal cholesterol levels.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tipranavir is used in sub-​therapeutic doses as a ph‍armacodynamic booster in ‍cART.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Indinavir can cause nephro​lithiasis when adequate h‍ydration is not maintaine‍d.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ritonavir is a potent inhi​bitor of the CYP3A4 isofo‍rm of P450 &amp;amp; may alte‍r levels of drugs.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tipranavir is used in sub-therapeutic doses as a pharmacodynamic booster in cART.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tipranavir is used in sub-therapeutic doses as a pharmacodynamic booster in cART.</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>adverse effects</strong> of gastrointestinal tolerance and fat redistribution due to <strong>protease inhibitors</strong>. HAART therapy includes this class of drugs along with 2 nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors +/- NNRTIs. <strong>Ritonavir, </strong>not tipranavir, is used in <strong>sub-therapeutic</strong> <strong>doses</strong> as a pharmacodynamic booster of other protease inhibitors in cART.</p>\n<p>This is because ritonavir is a <strong>potent inhibitor </strong>of the<strong> CYP3A4</strong> isoform of P450. It inhibits their CYP3A-mediated metabolism and permits the use of lower doses of other protease inhibitors. Nelfinavir is the only protease inhibitor not boosted by ritonavir.</p>\n<p><strong>Indinavir</strong> is notable for its tendency to precipitate in the urinary tract, causing <strong>nephrolithiasis</strong>, unless the patient maintains a high state of hydration.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6209","text":"An AIDS patient on treatme​nt with AZT, lamivudine, ‍indinavir, ketoconazole, ‍and cotrimoxazole, develo‌ps breast hypertrophy, ce‌ntral adiposity, hyperlip‌idemia, insulin resistanc‌e, and nephrolithiasis. W​hich of the following dru‍gs could have caused thes​e?","raw_text":"An AIDS patient on treatment with  AZT, lamivudine, indinavir, ketoconazole, and cotrimoxazole, develops breast hypertrophy, central adiposity, hyperlipidemia, insulin resistance, and nephrolithiasis. Which of the following drugs could have caused these?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Azidoth​ymidine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Indi​navir","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ketoco​nazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lamiv​udine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Indinavir","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Indinavir</strong></p><p><strong>Indinavir</strong>, a protease inhibitor, is the most likely cause for these adverse effects.</p>\n<p>Patients being treated with <strong>protease</strong> inhibitors (e.g., indinavir) develop a syndrome involving the <strong>derangement</strong> of <strong>lipid metabolism.</strong> This causes changes in body fat distribution. Type 2 diabetes mellitus has also been reported. This is called <strong>HIV/HAART-associated lipodystrophy.</strong></p>\n<p>Indinavir can also <strong>precipitate</strong> in the urinary tract, causing <strong>nephrolithiasis,</strong> unless the patient is maintained in a high state of hydration.</p>\n<p>Indinavir binds to the site of HIV-1 protease activity and inhibits cleavage of viral gag-pol polyprotein precursors into individual functional proteins required for infectious HIV. This results in the formation of immature, non-infectious viral particles.</p>\n<p><strong>Option A:</strong> Azidothymidine or zidovudine, is a nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor. Chronic zidovudine administration has been associated with nail hyperpigmentation. </p>\n<p><strong>Option C</strong>: Ketoconazole is an antifungal agent that inhibits fungal ergosterol synthesis. Its adverse effects include liver dysfunction and testosterone synthesis inhibition leading to gynecomastia. </p>\n<p><strong>Option D</strong>: Lamivudine is a nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor. It is one of the least toxic antiretroviral drugs. Neutropenia, headache, and nausea have been reported at higher-than-recommended doses.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1008","text":"Which of the following is ​a monoclonal antibody app‍roved for use in the trea‍tment of HIV?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a monoclonal antibody approved for use in the treatment of HIV?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bevac​izumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ibali​zumab","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Panit​umumab","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pembro​lizumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ibalizumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ibalizumab</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Ibalizumab</strong> is an <strong>anti-CD4 monoclonal antibody</strong> approved for use in the treatment of <strong>multidrug-resistant HIV-1. </strong>It is administered <strong>intravenously.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It acts as an entry inhibitor. It blocks HIV-1 from infecting CD4+ T cells by binding to domain 2 of CD4 cells. It also interferes with post-attachment steps required for the entry of HIV-1 virus particles into host cells and thus prevents the viral transmission that occurs via cell-cell fusion.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6444","text":"Which of the following is ​an integrase inhibitor in‍dicated in the treatment ‍of patients with HIV?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an integrase inhibitor indicated in the treatment of patients with HIV?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lopi​navir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abac​avir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ralte​gravir","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rito​navir","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Raltegravir","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Raltegravir</strong></p><div class=\"p-rich_text_section\">\n<p>The<strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> integrase inhibitor</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> indicated in the treatment of HIV is </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">raltegravir</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">. </span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">It inhibits the catalytic activity of the HIV-encoded integrase and </span><strong>prevents</strong> the <strong>integration</strong> of <strong>virus DNA</strong> in<strong>to</strong> the <strong>host chromosome</strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"><strong>.</strong> It is eliminated primarily by glucuronidation by UGT1A1.</span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The other two FDA-approved integrase inhibitors are </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">elvitegravir</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> (EVG) and </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">dolutegravir</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> (DTG).</span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Options A and D: Lopinavir and ritonavir are protease inhibitors.</span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Option B: Abacavir is a nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor.</span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Note:  </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Dolutegravir </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">(DTG) is recommended as the preferred third drug for HIV post-exposure prophylaxis as per the recent MOHFW Jan 2020 guideline, in line with WHO.</span></p>\n<p><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":895,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"4\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":16777215},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"9\":0,\"11\":4,\"12\":0}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"The FDA approved Dolutegravir tablets and suspension to treat HIV-1 infection in pediatric patients at least four weeks old and weighing at least 3 kg who have never been treated for HIV or who have been treated, but not with an integrase inhibitor class of drug.\"}'>The FDA approved dolutegravir tablets and suspension for the treatment of HIV-1 infection in pediatric patients at least four weeks old and weighing at least 3 kg who have never been treated for HIV or who have been treated, but not with an integrase inhibitor class of drug.</span></p>\n<p><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":895,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"4\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":16777215},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"9\":0,\"11\":4,\"12\":0}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"The FDA approved Dolutegravir tablets and suspension to treat HIV-1 infection in pediatric patients at least four weeks old and weighing at least 3 kg who have never been treated for HIV or who have been treated, but not with an integrase inhibitor class of drug.\"}'>WHO recently recommended<strong> long-acting Cabotegravir</strong>, an HIV integrase strand transfer inhibitor may be offered as a <strong>HIV prevention</strong> option for people at substantial risk of HIV infection. It is an <strong>IM injectable</strong> form of <strong>pre-exposure prophylaxis</strong> that has been shown to be highly effective at reducing the risk of HIV acquisition. The first<strong> 2 injections</strong> were administered <strong>4 weeks apart</strong>, followed thereafter by an <strong>injection every 8 weeks</strong>.</span> </p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"AlUccW2H","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"35_renal_tubular_functions__urine_concentration_and_dilution","title":"35.Renal Tubular Functions, Urine Concentration And Dilution","num_questions":32,"total_marks":128,"duration":42,"time_per_question":78.75,"questions":[{"id":"MB4797","text":"What is implied if a drug ​has more renal clearance ‌than GFR?","raw_text":"What is implied if a drug has more renal clearance than GFR?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Drug is reabsorbed by the ​renal tubules","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Drug is secreted in the re​nal tubules","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Drug is excreted in renal ​tubules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Drug is neither excreted n​or reabsorbed in renal tu‌bules","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Drug is secreted in the renal tubules","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Drug is secreted in the renal tubules</strong></p><p>If drug has more renal clearance than GFR, then drug is secreted in renal tubules. </p>\n<p><strong>Excretion rate= (filtration rate- reabsorption rate)+ secretion rate.</strong></p>\n<p>The clearance of the substance:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Equals the GFR if there is no net tubular secretion or reabsorption.</li>\n<li>Exceeds the GFR if there is net tubular secretion.</li>\n<li>Less than the GFR if there is net tubular reabsorption.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6855","text":"Choose the correct formula​ regarding renal physiolo‌gy.","raw_text":"Choose the correct formula regarding renal physiology.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Filtration fraction = ( RP​F / GFR ) * 100","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Filtration rate = GFR * ur​ine concentraion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Excretion ​rate = UxV","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Clearance​ = UP / V","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Excretion rate = UxV","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Excretion rate = UxV</strong></p><p>The correct formula is Excretion rate = UV.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Filtration fraction = ( GFR / RPF ) * 100 = 20% (or ) 0.2 (approx)</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Filtration rate = GFR * Plasma concentration</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Clearance  = U*V/P</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Where,</p>\n<p>GFR = Glomerular filtration rate</p>\n<p>RPF = Renal plasma flow</p>\n<p>U = Concentration of the substance in urine</p>\n<p>V = Urine flow rate</p>\n<p>P = Concentration of the substance in plasma</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6854","text":"Choose the correct option.​","raw_text":"Choose the correct option.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inulin is completely secre​ted and never reabsorbed.‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucose is completely reab​sorbed in DCT.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Creatinine is partially se​creted.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"PAH at any plasma concentr​ation is completely filte‌red and secreted.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Creatinine is partially secreted.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Creatinine is partially secreted.</strong></p><p>Creatinine is partially secreted and never reabsorbed.<strong> </strong>Clearance of creatinine (CCr) can also be used to determine GFR. Since creatinine is secreted by the tubules, the <strong>clearance of creatinine will be slightly</strong> higher than inulin.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> Hence, it <strong>overestimates GFR. </strong></span></p>\n<p><strong>Inulin</strong> is completely<strong> filtered</strong> and <strong>never reabsorbed. </strong>Hence, it can be used for <strong>GFR estimation.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Glucose</strong> is <strong>completely reabsorbed</strong> below the renal threshold (i.e ~180mg/dl) in <strong>PCT</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>PAH(para amino hippuric acid)</strong> at <strong>low</strong> plasma concentrations is<strong> completely filtered</strong> and <strong>secreted. </strong>Hence, it can be used for estimation of <strong>renal blood flow.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5023","text":"Renal plasma flow is being​ calculated using PAH in ‌a patient. The technician‌ inadvertently uses PAH t‌hrice the recommended dos‍e. What will be the estim​ated effective renal plas​ma flow?","raw_text":"Renal plasma flow is being calculated using PAH in a patient. The technician inadvertently uses PAH thrice the recommended dose. What will be the estimated effective renal plasma flow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"False​ high","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fals​e low","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bo​th","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Correct and is not affecte​d","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. False low","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9ec536f63ece4b9a99201b8be388c98dx403x141.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. False low</strong></p><p>Effective renal plasma flow in this patient will be falsely low.</p>\n<p>It is equal to the clearance of para-aminohippurate (PAH) as shown by the following equation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Where,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ERPF = Effective Renal Plasma Flow</li>\n<li>U<span style=\"font-size: 8pt;\">PAH</span> = [PAH] in urine</li>\n<li>V= Urine flow rate</li>\n<li>P<span style=\"font-size: 8pt;\">PAH</span> = [PAH] in plasma</li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 8pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">C</span></span>PAH = </span>Clearance of PAH</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 694\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>At high concentrations of PAH, as given in this question, tubular secretion attains saturation after which the clearance of PAH falls. Hence, a <strong>low ERPF</strong> will be seen.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9583","text":"Which of the following tra​nsporter is present in th‌e ascending loop of Henle‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following transporter is present in the ascending loop of Henle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Na+ - Cl- co​ transporter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ENaC c​hannel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Na+ - 2Cl- - K+ co transpo​rter","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Na+ - aminoacid co transpo​rter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Na+ - 2Cl- - K+ co transporter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a9c334a180a4437484a343a7a862cc96x1280x1410.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Na+ - 2Cl- - K+ co transporter</strong></p><p>Thick ascending limb of loop of Henle has Na<sup>+</sup> - 2Cl<sup>-</sup> - K<sup>+</sup> co transporter that cotransports one Na+, one K+, and 2Cl– from the tubular lumen into the tubu­lar cells. This is an example of secondary active trans­port.</p>\n<p><strong>Na+</strong> is transported out of the cell into the interstitium by <strong>Na, K ATPase</strong> in the basolateral membrane of the cell.</p>\n<p><strong>Cl–</strong> exits in <strong>basolateral ClC–Kb Cl– channels</strong>. <strong>Barttin,</strong> a protein in the cell membrane, is <strong>essential for normal ClC–Kb function.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>K+</strong> moves from the cell to the interstitium and the tubular lumen by <strong>ROMK</strong>(renal outer medullary potassium channel) and other K+ channels.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4807","text":"The principal site of abso​rption of Na + is_____","raw_text":"The principal site of absorption of Na + is_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proximal convoluted tubule​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Distal convo​luted tubule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thick ascending limb of Lo​op of Henle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Collect​ing duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Proximal convoluted tubule","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/35783f7c4b7f43e9a9a525c4acfe370ex1280x1670.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Proximal convoluted tubule</strong></p><p>The principal site of absorption of Na + is PCT. About <strong>60-65% of filtered sodium is reabsorbed in the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT),</strong> coupled with the movement of H+, glucose, amino acids, organic acids, phosphate, and other electrolytes and substances.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 442.71875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 45px;\"><strong>% of filtered Na reabsorbed</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\"><strong>Part of nephron</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.71875px;\"><strong>Main transporter involved</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 45px;\">60</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\">PCT</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.71875px;\">Na<sup>+</sup> –H<sup>+</sup> exchanger</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 45px;\">30</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\">Thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.71875px;\">Na<sup>+</sup> –2Cl<sup>-</sup>–K cotransporter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 45px;\">7</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\">DCT</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.71875px;\">Na–Cl cotransporter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 45px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163px;\">Collecting ducts</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.71875px;\">ENaC channels (regulated by aldosterone to permit homeostatic adjustments)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>All segments of nephron are involved in sodium reabsorption <strong>except thin descending loop of Henle.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4809","text":"Potassium is maximally abs​orbed in____","raw_text":"Potassium is maximally absorbed in____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"P​CT","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"D​CT","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Collect​ing duct","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Loop o​f henle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. PCT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d73788c6efc54361871276b838a79d50x600x758.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. PCT</strong></p><p>Potassium is maximally absorbed in PCT. </p>\n<p>Potassium is freely filtered by the glomerulus. The bulk of filtered K<sup>+</sup> is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule. In the <strong>proximal tubule</strong>, K<sup>+</sup> absorption is primarily passive and proportional to Na<sup>+</sup> and water through <strong>paracellular transport</strong>. </p>\n<p>K<sup>+</sup> <strong>reabsorption</strong> in the thick ascending limb of Henle occurs through both transcellular (<strong>Na<sup>+</sup>- K<sup>+</sup>- 2Cl<sup>- </sup>cotransport</strong>) and paracellular pathways.</p>\n<p>K<sup>+</sup> <strong>secretion</strong> begins in the early distal convoluted tubule and progressively increases along the distal nephron into the cortical collecting duct . Most<strong> urinary K<sup>+</sup> can be accounted for by K<sup>+</sup> secretion mediated by principal cells</strong>. Under conditions of K<sup>+</sup> depletion, reabsorption of K<sup>+</sup> occurs in the collecting duct. </p>\n<p><strong>It is the only electrolyte to be reabsorbed as well as secreted.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9584","text":"Bicarbonate is reabsorbed ​across the luminal membra‌ne with the help of _____‌_","raw_text":"Bicarbonate is reabsorbed across the luminal membrane with the help of ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Na+ - HCO3​- antiport","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Na+ - HCO3- ​Co transport","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Na+ - H+ ​exchanger","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Na+ - K+ ​exchanger","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Na+ - H+ exchanger","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/149e260d9a854942bdb9af4074029daex1280x1517.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Na+ - H+ exchanger</strong></p><p>Bicarbonate is reabsorbed at the <strong>luminal</strong> membrane in the form of <strong>CO<sub>2</sub> and H<sub>2</sub>O</strong> with the help of<strong> Na<sup>+</sup> - H<sup>+</sup> exchanger.</strong></p>\n<p>In the tubular cell of the PCT, CO<sub>2</sub> and H<sub>2</sub>O recombine with the help of carbonic anhydrase II, and then dissociate into H<sup>+</sup> and HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup>. From here, the H<sup>+</sup> moves back into the tubular lumen via the Na<sup>+</sup> - H<sup>+</sup> exchanger while the <strong>HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup></strong> is <strong>reabsorbed</strong> at the <strong>basolateral</strong> membrane via the <strong>Na<sup>+</sup> - HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> co-transporter.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4815","text":"True about renal tubular t​ransport_____","raw_text":"True about renal tubular transport_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maximum reabsorption in di​stal tubule","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urea is absorbed 100% in P​CT","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"H+ is absorbed in proximal​ tubule","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amino acid is reabsorbed i​n proximal tubule","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Amino acid is reabsorbed in proximal tubule","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/af72c18060f747c99d95c30dde2e31ebx1280x1670.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Amino acid is reabsorbed in proximal tubule</strong></p><p>Glucose and amino acids are completely reabsorbed in the proximal tubule.</p>\n<p>Option A: Maximum reabsorption occurs in proximal tubule</p>\n<p>Option B: Urea is reabsorbed and secreted. It is reabsorbed in PCT and in collecting duct. It is secreted in thin ascending loop of henle.</p>\n<p>Option C: H<sup>+ </sup>is secreted in PCT as well as in collecting ducts.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4798","text":"Which of the following occ​urs along with glucose tr‌ansport into the cell?","raw_text":"Which of the following occurs along with glucose transport into the cell?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium ​symport","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Calcium ​antiport","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Potassium​ transport","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amino aci​d antiport","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sodium symport","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aa5f369ca1624611a61ff70f0ae54506x1280x1464.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sodium symport</strong></p><p>Sodium symport occurs along with glucose transport into the cell.</p>\n<p>Sodium and glucose are co-transported across the luminal membrane of proximal tubular cells<strong>.</strong> This occurs via<strong> sodium glucose co-transporters (SGLT2 and SGLT1)</strong>. The energy derived from the movement of Na<sup>+</sup> is used to move glucose against its concentration gradient into the tubular cell. This is referred to as <strong>secondary active transport</strong>. </p>\n<p>The Na<sup>+</sup> is then pumped out of the cell into the interstitium, and the <strong>glucose exits by facilitated diffusion</strong> via <strong>glucose transporter (GLUT)-2</strong> along the basolateral membrane into the interstitial fluid.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical correlation:</strong></p>\n<p>Fanconi-Bickel syndrome: Defective GLUT2</p>\n<p>Isolated renal glycosuria: Defective SGLT2</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4801","text":"Normal renal threshold (g/​L) and transport maxima (‌mg/min) for glucose respe‌ctively are _______, ____‌_____","raw_text":"Normal renal threshold (g/L) and transport maxima (mg/min)  for glucose respectively are _______, _________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"200,​ 375","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"375,​ 200","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, ​375","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"375​, 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2, 375","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4730bbf78c6349ef873c596192b78e15x1280x1735.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2, 375</strong></p><p>The actual renal threshold is about 200 mg/dL (i.e., <strong>2 g/L</strong> ) (1g/L=100mg/dl) and the transport maximum for glucose is <strong>375 mg/min.</strong></p>\n<p>For substances that are actively reabsorbed or secreted, there is a <strong>limit to the rate at which the solute can be transported</strong>, which is referred to as the <strong>transport maximum</strong>. This limit is due to <strong>saturation</strong> of the specific <strong>transport systems</strong> involved. </p>\n<p>Normally, measurable glucose does not appear in the urine because essentially all the filtered glucose is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>The transport maximum for glucose is about 375 mg/min, whereas the filtered load of glucose is only about 125 mg/min. With large increases in GFR and/or plasma glucose concentration that increase the filtered load of glucose above 375 mg/min, the excess glucose filtered is not reabsorbed and passes into the urine.</p>\n<p>When the plasma glucose concentration is 100 mg/100 ml and the filtered load is at its normal level (125 mg/min) there is no loss of glucose in the urine. However, when the <strong>plasma concentration of glucose rises above about 200 mg/100 ml</strong>, increasing the filtered load to about 250 mg/min, a small amount of <strong>glucose begins to appear in the urine. </strong>This point is termed as the <strong>threshold</strong> for glucose.</p>\n<p><strong>Splay</strong> is due to separation between filtered load and exceretion. It occurs due to<strong> different transport maximums of different nephrons</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1194","text":"The image of PAH excretion​ is depicted below. Match‌ the labellings.","raw_text":"The image of PAH excretion is depicted below. Match the labellings.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"a. Filtered, b. Excreted, ​c. Tm, d. Secreted","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"a. Filtered, b. Secreted, ​c. Tm, d. Excreted","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"a. Secreted, b. Filtered, ​c. Tm, d. Excreted","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"a. Excreted, b. Filtered, ​c. Tm, d. Secreted","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. a. Filtered, b. Secreted, c. Tm, d. Excreted","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8ba11beaacc54612b265c5a9c9d0fbed.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. a. Filtered, b. Secreted, c. Tm, d. Excreted</strong></p><p>The labellings depticed are a. Filtered, b. Secreted, c. Tm, d. Excreted</p>\n<p><strong>Filtered Load</strong>: The filtered load of PAH increases <strong>linearly</strong> as the unbound <strong>concentration of PAH increases</strong> (filtered load = GFR.  [P]PAH).</p>\n<p><strong>Secretion</strong>: At low concentrations of PAH, many carriers are available and secretion increases linearly as the plasma concentration increases. When the PAH concentration increases to a level where the <strong>carriers are saturated, Tm is reached</strong>. After this point, no matter how much the PAH concentration increases, there can be no further increase in the secretion rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Excretion</strong>. For a secreted substance such as PAH, <strong>excretion is the sum of filtration and secretion</strong>. At low PAH concentrations (below Tm), excretion increases steeply with increases in plasma PAH concentration because both filtration and secretion are increasing. At PAH concentrations above Tm,<strong> excretion increases less steeply (and parallels the curve for filtration)</strong> because only the filtration component increases as concentration increases; <strong>secretion is already saturated.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4803","text":"In a patient with uncontro​lled diabetes, what is th‌e value of plasma clearan‌ce of glucose?","raw_text":"In a patient with uncontrolled diabetes, what is the value of plasma clearance of glucose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ze​ro","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Equal to inu​lin clearance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Greater than PAH clearance​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Greater ​than zero","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Greater than zero","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7030b4fe87de418c9e303664c0f063f0x1020x1026.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Greater than zero</strong></p><p>In uncontrolled diabetes, the plasma clearance of glucose is greater than zero. </p>\n<p>Normally, all the glucose that is filtered is reabsorbed and no glucose is excreted in the urine. Hence, the clearance is zero. In uncontrolled diabetes, since the <strong>plasma glucose concentration</strong> is <strong>greater</strong> than the <strong>renal threshold for reabsorption</strong>, excess glucose is excreted in the urine and the clearance is greater than zero.</p>\n<p>Clearance of different substances can be shown in a graph as follows: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The above graph depicts renal handling of glucose, inulin, and para-aminohippurate (PAH) and can be explained as follows:</p>\n<p>Glucose clearance: Normally, all the glucose that is filtered is reabsorbed and no glucose is excreted in the urine. Hence, the clearance is zero. In uncontrolled diabetes, since the <strong>plasma glucose concentration</strong> is <strong>greater</strong> than the <strong>renal threshold for reabsorption</strong>, excess glucose is excreted in the urine and the clearance is greater than zero.</p>\n<p>Inulin clearance: Inulin is freely filtered and is neither reabsorbed nor secreted. Hence, it is <strong>constant and equal to the glomerular filtration rate</strong>.</p>\n<p>PAH clearance: PAH is both filtered and secreted. When <strong>plasma PAH is low</strong>, <strong>clearance is high</strong>. However, as <strong>plasma PAH rises</strong> above the threshold level for PAH secretion, <strong>clearance falls</strong> progressively.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9430","text":"A patient has a ​blood glu​cose level of 200 mg/dL a‌nd GFR of 90 ml/min. What‌ is the amount of glucose‌ excreted if the transpor‍t maximum of the patient ​is as shown below?","raw_text":"A patient has a ​blood glucose level of 200 mg/dL and GFR of 90 ml/min. What is the amount of glucose excreted if the transport maximum of the patient is as shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"80 m​g/min","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"50 m​g/min","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"40 m​g/min","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30 m​g/min","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 30 mg/min","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/500e1da504d94d398ff2bf10eaabe8eb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 30 mg/min</strong></p><p>The amount of glucose excreted in the above scenario is 30 mg/min.</p>\n<p><strong>Calculation:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> <strong>Calculate total filtered glucose:</strong></p>\n<p>In this question,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>GFR = 90 ml/min</li>\n<li>Plasma glucose concentration = 200 mg/dL= 2 mg/ml (1 dL=100 ml).</li>\n<li><strong>Total filtered glucose = GFR X Plasma glucose concentration</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"padding-left: 60px;\">                               = 90 ml/min X 2 mg/ml<br/>                               = 180 mg/min.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> <strong>Check transport maximum: </strong></p>\n<p>In the above question transport maximum is 150 mg/min.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: Calculate the excretion:</strong></p>\n<p>Excretion = Total filtered glucose - Total reabsorbed glucose (Transport Maximum)</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left: 60px;\">  = 180-150 mg/min<br/>  = 30 mg/min</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6856","text":"Choose the correct stateme​nt regarding the concentr‌ation of solutes at the e‌nd of proximal convoluted‌ tubule","raw_text":"Choose the correct statement regarding the concentration of solutes at the end of  proximal convoluted tubule","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Concentration of glucose i​n tubular fluid is more t‌han that of plasma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Concentration of urea in t​ubular fluid is more than‌ that of plasma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Concentration of bicarbona​te in tubular fluid is mo‌re than that of plasma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Concentration of sodium in​ tubular fluid is less th‌an that of plasma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Concentration of urea in tubular fluid is more than that of plasma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d2118c403a9c424bb4ffe14d53733d7bx1280x1742.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Concentration of urea in tubular fluid is more than that of plasma</strong></p><p>The concentration of <strong>urea</strong> in<strong> tubular fluid</strong> is <strong>more</strong> than that of <strong>plasma. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>[TF/P]&gt;1, when solute is reabsorbed less quickly than water or when <strong>solute is secreted. </strong>Ex- PAH, Creatinine, Inulin, Urea.</p>\n<p>[TF/P]=1, when <strong>solute and water are reabsorbed at the same rate </strong>Ex - Na.</p>\n<p>[TF/P]&lt;1, when <strong>solute is reabsorbed</strong> more quickly than water. Ex- glucose, amino acid, bicarbonate. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4812","text":"The mechanism by which maj​ority of water is reabsor‌bed in PCT is","raw_text":"The mechanism by which majority of water is reabsorbed in PCT is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Act​ive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secondar​y active","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Facil​itated","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osm​osis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Osmosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Osmosis</strong></p><p>Water reabsorption in PCT mainly occurs by osmosis.</p>\n<p>The proximal tubule reabsorbs 67% of the filtered water. The driving force for water reabsorption is a transtubular osmotic gradient established by reabsorption of solutes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4820","text":"A dehydrated patient is ex​creting hypertonic urine.‌ Absorption of water in w‌hich part of the nephron ‌causes this?","raw_text":"A dehydrated patient is excreting hypertonic urine. Absorption of water in which part of the nephron causes this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Collect​ing duct","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"P​CT","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ascendi​ng loop","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Descend​ing loop","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Collecting duct","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Collecting duct</strong></p><p><strong>Hypertonic urine</strong> is excreted due to <strong>absorption</strong> of water in <strong>collecting duct.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>final concentration of urine</strong> is determined by the <strong>amount of water reabsorption that occurs in the collecting ducts. </strong>The changes in osmolarity of the urine (hypo/hypertonic) <strong>depends on the amount of ADH (vasopressin) acting on the collecting ducts.</strong></p>\n<p>The basic requirements for forming a concentrated urine (hypertonic urine) are</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A high level of ADH</strong><em>, </em>which increases the permeability of the <strong>distal tubules and collecting ducts</strong> to water, thereby allowing these tubular segments to avidly <strong>reabsorb water.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>A high osmolarity of the renal medullary</strong><strong> interstitial fluid,</strong> which provides the <strong>osmotic gradient necessary for water reabsorption</strong> to occur in the presence of high levels of ADH.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4813","text":"A patient with diabetes in​sipidus is prescribed vas‌opressin. This drug has i‌ts maximum effect on whic‌h part of the renal tubul‍e?","raw_text":"A patient with diabetes insipidus is prescribed vasopressin. This drug has its maximum effect on which part of the renal tubule?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proxima​l tubule","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Distal​ tubule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Loop o​f henle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Collect​ing duct","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Collecting duct","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0e1c236e761442979c2e69cecee49c5fx1280x1780.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Collecting duct</strong></p><p><strong>Vasopressin</strong> has the <strong>maximum effect</strong> on the <strong>collecting ducts.</strong></p>\n<p>Vasopressin acts by inserting<strong> aquaporin 2</strong> present in cytoplasmic vesicles of <strong>principal cells on to the apical membrane</strong>. The effect is mediated via the vasopressin V2 receptor, cyclic adenosine 5-monophosphate (cAMP), and protein kinase A. When <strong>vasopressin is absent</strong>, the <strong>collecting duct</strong> epithelium is relatively <strong>impermeable to water.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4814","text":"Which of the following is ​true about free water cle‌arance?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about free water clearance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Regulate​d by ADH","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Regulated by​ aldosterone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Unaffected b​y furosemide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ​the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Regulated by ADH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Regulated by ADH</strong></p><p>Free water clearance is regulated by ADH.</p>\n<p>Free water clearance is defined as the amount of <strong>solute-free water</strong> generated by the kidneys, either when dilute urine or when concentrated urine is formed.</p>\n<p>Free-water clearance is <strong>positive</strong>, when <strong>excess water is being excreted</strong> (<strong>dilute urine</strong>).</p>\n<p>Free-water clearance is <strong>negative,</strong> when excess solutes are being removed and <strong>water is being conserved </strong>(<strong>concentrated urine</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6864","text":"The following picture show​s the composition of solu‌te in the urine and plasm‌a sample of 3 patients. I‌dentify the true statemen‍t.","raw_text":"The following picture shows the composition of solute in the urine and plasma sample of 3 patients. Identify the true statement.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A shows negative free wate​r clearance, B is isotoni‌c, C is positive free wat‌er clearance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A shows positive free wate​r clearance B is isotonic‌ C is positive free water‌ clearance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A shows positive free wate​r clearance B is isotonic‌ C is negative free water‌ clearance","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A shows negative free wate​r clearance B is isotonic‌ C is negative free water‌ clearance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A shows positive free water clearance B is isotonic C is negative free water clearance","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/542e02d2f2ad4c88ac6eb507cee7430c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A shows positive free water clearance B is isotonic C is negative free water clearance</strong></p><p>Patient A shows positive free water clearance B is isotonic C shows negative free water clearance.</p>\n<p>Free water clearance is defined as the amount of <strong>solute-free water</strong> generated by the kidneys, either when dilute urine or when concentrated urine is formed.</p>\n<p>Patient A has positive free-water clearance, since <strong>excess water is being excreted</strong> (<strong>dilute urine</strong>).</p>\n<p>Patient C has negative free-water clearance, since excess solutes are being removed and <strong>water is being conserved </strong>(<strong>concentrated urine</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4816","text":"All are true except that _​___.","raw_text":"All are true except that ____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PCT is perme​able to water","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Descending limb of loop of​ henle is permeable to wa‌ter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thick ascending limb is pe​rmeable to water","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Distal tubule is permeable​ to water","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thick ascending limb is permeable to water","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thick ascending limb is permeable to water</strong></p><p>The ascending limb is always impermeable to H<sub>2</sub>O.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4817","text":"The average urine osmolali​ty in mosm/kg is _____.","raw_text":"The average urine osmolality in mosm/kg is _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"100​-800","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"500-​1400","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"200​-400","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"50-​1400","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 100-800","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 100-800</strong></p><p>The average urine osmolality is 100-800 mosm/kg.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1180","text":"What are the requirements ​for a patient to excrete ‌concentrated urine?","raw_text":"What are the requirements for a patient to excrete concentrated urine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High ADH levels and hypoos​molar renal medulla","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Low ADH levels and hypoosm​olar renal medulla","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High ADH levels and hypero​smolar renal medulla","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Low ADH levels and hyperos​molar renal medulla","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. High ADH levels and hyperosmolar renal medulla","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. High ADH levels and hyperosmolar renal medulla</strong></p><p>The requirements for excreting concentrated urine are high ADH levels and hyperosmolar renal medulla. </p>\n<p>A <strong>high level of ADH</strong>, which increases the permeability of the <strong>distal tubules and collecting ducts</strong> to water, thereby allowing these tubular segments to avidly <strong>reabsorb water</strong>, and a <strong>high osmolarity of the renal medullary</strong> interstitial fluid, which provides the<strong> osmotic gradient </strong>necessary for <strong>water reabsorption </strong>to occur in the presence of high levels of ADH.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4822","text":"Maximum osmotic gradient i​s found in____","raw_text":"Maximum osmotic gradient is found in____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Outer ​medulla","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inner ​medulla","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Outer ​cortex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inner ​cortex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inner medulla","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inner medulla</strong></p><p>Maximum osmotic gradient is found in the inner medulla. The gradient of osmolarity from the cortex (300 mosm/L) to the inner medulla (1200 mosm/L) is <strong>produced by the loops of Henle as countercurrent multipliers </strong>and<strong> maintained by operation of the vasa recta as countercurrent exchangers.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4825","text":"Hyperosmolarity of renal m​edulla is due to____","raw_text":"Hyperosmolarity of renal medulla is due to____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"K​+","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"N​a+","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Glu​cose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"H​+","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Na+","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5b703ef23a0f432b967fa05869097c1dx1280x1806.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Na+</strong></p><p>Hyperosmolarity of the medullary interstitium is mainly due to NaCl and urea.</p>\n<p>The major factors that contribute to the buildup of solute concentration into the renal medulla are </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Active transport of Na<sup>+ </sup>and co-transport of K<sup>+</sup>, Cl<sup>-</sup></strong>, and other ions out of the <strong>thick portion of the ascending limb of the loop of Henle into the medullary interstitium.</strong></li>\n<li>Active transport of ions from the <strong>collecting ducts into the medullary interstitium.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Facilitated diffusion of urea</strong> from the <strong>inner medullary collecting ducts</strong> into the medullary interstitium.</li>\n<li>Diffusion of only small amounts of water from the <strong>medullary tubules</strong> into the medullary interstitium</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4826","text":"Which of the following is ​not a component of the co‌untercurrent mechanism?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of the countercurrent mechanism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thick ascending loop of He​nle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proximal convoluted tubule​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vasa ​recta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Descending limb of loop of​ Henle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Proximal convoluted tubule","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d27f85e7708a483195a6ae51b43d8effx1280x1806.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Proximal convoluted tubule</strong></p><p>Proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) does not play a role in countercurrent mechanism.</p>\n<p>The countercurrent mechanism consists of 2 components: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Countercurrent multiplier: <br/>    a. Absorption of water via <strong>Aquaporin 1</strong> in <strong>thin descending limb </strong><strong><br/>    </strong>b. Active transport of<strong> Na<sup>+</sup>- K<sup>+</sup>- 2Cl<sup>-</sup></strong> in <strong>thick ascending limb<br/></strong>    c. <strong>Flow of fluid</strong> from PCT to DCT<br/>   </li>\n<li>Countercurrent exchanger: <strong>Vasa recta </strong>are involved in this process. They help in maintaining the osmotic gradient in the renal medulla. This is a passive process. </li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9585","text":"Maximum osmolality that ca​n be reached at the tip o‌f medulla is proportional‌ to","raw_text":"Maximum osmolality that can be reached at the tip of medulla is proportional to","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Distribution of Na-K-2Cl c​hannel in the thick ascen‌ding loop of Henle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Permeability of descending​ loop to water","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Length of l​oop of Henle","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"All of t​he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Length of loop of Henle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Length of loop of Henle</strong></p><p>The maximum osmolality that can be reached at the tip of the medulla is proportional to the length of the loop of Henle, because the Loop of Henle acts as Countercurrent multiplier to maitain the gradient in medulla. <strong>Greater is the length</strong> of loop of Henle, <strong>greater will be the osmolality</strong> that can be reached at the tip of the medulla. </p>\n<p>In case of <strong>juxtamedullary nephrons with longer loops</strong> and thin ascending limbs, the osmotic gradient is spread over a greater distance and the <strong>osmolality at the tip of the loop is greater.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4831","text":"Counter current blood flow​ is seen in all except___‌__","raw_text":"Counter current blood flow is seen in all except_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kid​ney","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tes​tes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"E​ye","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Small i​ntestine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Eye","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Eye</strong></p><p>The eye does not have a countercurrent blood flow system.</p>\n<p>Heat and substances, including gases, steroids and peptide hormones, can pass from venous blood, interstitial fluid and lymph to the arterial blood; the process is called local counter-current transfer.</p>\n<p><strong>Examples of countercurrent blood flow</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Heat exchange between the arteries and venae comitantes of the limbs.</li>\n<li>Vasa recta in the kidneys</li>\n<li>Testes</li>\n<li>Small intestine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3406","text":"All of the following are c​omponents responsible for‌ the countercurrent mecha‌nism in the kidney except‌ _____.","raw_text":"All of the following are components responsible for the countercurrent mechanism in the kidney except _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium outflow in thick as​cending limb","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Water outflow in thin desc​ending limb","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sodium outflow in thin asc​ending limb","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Flow of tubular fluid from​ PCT to DCT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sodium outflow in thin ascending limb","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sodium outflow in thin ascending limb</strong></p><p>Thin ascending limb is not a component of the countercurrent mechanism. </p>\n<p>The <strong>loop of Henle</strong> function as <strong>countercurrent multipliers</strong> and <strong>vasa recta</strong> as <strong>countercurrent exchangers.</strong></p>\n<p>The functioning of the loop of Henle depends upon:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>High permeability of the thin descending limb to water ( via aquaporin-1)</li>\n<li>Active transport of Na<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>-</sup> from the thick ascending limb</li>\n<li>Inflow of tubular fluid from proximal tubule and outflow to the distal tubule.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> The thin ascending limbs of juxtamedullary nephrons are permeable to Na<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>-</sup> and hence do contribute passively to countercurrent multiplication. However, among the options provided, Option C is the best answer as the other options are directly responsible for the countercurrent mechanism.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5225","text":"Which of the following is ​the diluting segment of t‌he kidney?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the diluting segment of the kidney?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"P​CT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Collect​ing duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ascending thick loop of He​nle","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Descending l​oop of Henle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ascending thick loop of Henle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ascending thick loop of Henle</strong></p><p>The ascending thick limb of the loop of Henle is called the <strong>diluting segment.</strong></p>\n<p>In the ascending limb, there is the <strong>movement of Na+ and Cl– out of the tubular lumen</strong>, and the tubular fluid becomes <strong>more dilute.</strong></p>\n<p>When the fluid reaches the top of the ascending limb, it becomes <strong>hypotonic </strong>to plasma. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6746","text":"As filtrate flows through ​PCT, the concentration of‌ all of the following dec‌reases except:","raw_text":"As filtrate flows through PCT,  the concentration of all of the following decreases except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amin​oacid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glu​cose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlo​ride","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bicar​bonate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chloride","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chloride</strong></p><p>As the filtrate flows through the proximal convoluted tubule, the <strong>concentration of the chloride increases.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The concentration of <strong>chloride</strong> in the <strong>early part</strong> of the <strong>pCT is ≈105 mEq/L.</strong></li>\n<li>As the sodium is reabsorbed, it preferentially carries <strong>glucose, amino acids,</strong> and <strong>bicarbonate.</strong></li>\n<li>This leaves behind the solute, that has a higher concentration of the <strong>chloride,</strong> which as it reaches the second half of the PCT is<strong> ≈140 mEq/L.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6745","text":"Macula densa is formed by ​which part of the nephron‌?","raw_text":"Macula densa is formed by which part of the nephron?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"P​CT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"D​CT","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Afferent ​arteriole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bowman's​ capsule","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. DCT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5461ebba66b64151ba826ae7f04b9317x1280x1384.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. DCT</strong></p><p><strong>Macula densa</strong> is formed by specialized tubular epithelial cells of the <strong>distal convoluted tubule.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The juxtaglomerular apparatus consists of the following parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Macula densa:</strong> At the end of the thick ascending limb of Henle, and at the beginning of the distal tubule, the tubular epithelial cells are specialized to form the macula densa. They detect sodium concentration of the fluid in the tubule<strong> (chemoreceptor). </strong>In response to <strong>elevated sodium</strong>, the macula densa cells trigger<strong> </strong>the<strong> contraction of the afferent arteriole</strong>, <strong>reducing</strong> the flow of blood to the glomerulus and the <strong>glomerular filtration rate</strong>. This happens as follows: When there is increased sodium, there is increased <strong>sodium-potassium ATPase activity which</strong> leads to increased <strong>ATP hydrolysis</strong> causing more <strong>adenosine</strong> formation. The adenosine receptors on the macula densa cells stimulate it to release<strong> more calcium</strong>, so that there is vasoconstriction of the afferent arteriole, thereby decreasing the <strong>GFR.</strong></li>\n<li>Lacis cells:<strong> Extraglomerular mesangial cells</strong> situated between afferent and efferent arteriole.</li>\n<li>Granular cells/JG cells: Modified <strong>smooth muscle cells of the afferent arteriole, </strong>they secrete renin. <strong>Fall in pressure</strong> in afferent arteriole stimulates renin release <strong>(mechanoreceptor)</strong>, which, in turn, <strong>increases blood pressure.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"5o9BsEW8","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"35_congenital_anomalies_and_acute_pancreatitis","title":"35.Congenital Anomalies And Acute Pancreatitis","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MD1307","text":"What is the most common co‌ngenital anomaly of the p​ancreas?","raw_text":"What is the most common congenital anomaly of the pancreas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Annular ‌pancreas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pancreas‌ divisum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sphincter of Oddi dysfunct‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Agenesis o‌f pancreas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pancreas divisum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/90adffa91c7e4b2ab9d53433f17a18d2x600x2543.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pancreas divisum</strong></p><p><strong>Pancreas divisum</strong> is the most common congenital anomaly of the pancreas.</p>\n<p>During embryogenesis, the developing pancreatic ducts usually fuse in such a way that most of the dorsal duct drains into the proximal part of the ventral duct. The proximal portion of the dorsal duct usually persists as an accessory duct.</p>\n<p>In about 10% of the population, there is a <strong>failure of fusion of the dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds</strong> leading to a condition called <strong>pancreatic divisum</strong> (pancreas divisum). Pancreatic drainage is accomplished mainly through the accessory papilla.</p>\n<p>Since most of the pancreatic exocrine secretions exit through the dorsal duct, the pancreas divisum can lead to a partial obstruction due to the small minor papilla, leading to the development of <strong>relapsing acute or chronic pancreatitis</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7627","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g the management of pancr​eatic divisum?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the management of pancreatic divisum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sphincteropl‌asty is done","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pancreatojejunostomy is al‌so known as Puestow's pro​cedure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endoscopic sphincterotomy ‌relieves the symptoms","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ERCP is more advantageous ‌than MRCP for the diagnos​is","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. ERCP is more advantageous than MRCP for the diagnosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/62364bbddd7f49c383e4f1343fffa1d1x970x905.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. ERCP is more advantageous than MRCP for the diagnosis</strong></p><p><strong>MRCP </strong>is more advantageous than ERCP in diagnosing <strong>pancreatic divisum. </strong>This is because,<strong> MRCP </strong>can delineate the <strong>entire dorsal pancreatic duct</strong>, whereas <strong>ERCP</strong> can <strong>assess only the ventral duct</strong> on cannulation of the major duodenal papilla.</p>\n<p>The diagnosis of pancreatic divisum can also be made by <strong>EUS, or ERCP,</strong> augmented by injection of <strong>secretin</strong> if necessary.</p>\n<p><strong>Endoscopic sphincterotomy</strong> and <strong>stenting</strong> of the minor papilla relieve the symptoms. Surgical intervention may involve <strong>sphincteroplasty</strong>, pancreatojejunostomy (<strong>Puestow procedure</strong>), or even <strong>resection</strong> of the pancreatic head.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows an MRCP done for a case of pancreatic divisum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7628","text":"A 9-year-old girl was refe‌rred to the OPD with a ch​ronic history of early sa​tiety and recurrent non-b‌ilious vomiting after hea‌vy meals. Barium meal rev‍ealed a diffusely narrowe‍d second portion of the d​uodenum, suggesting extri‍nsic compression. Which o‌f the following statement​s is false regarding the ‌most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 9-year-old girl was referred to the OPD with a chronic history of early satiety and recurrent non-bilious vomiting after heavy meals. Barium meal revealed a diffusely narrowed second portion of the duodenum, suggesting extrinsic compression. Which of the following statements is false regarding the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is associated with fail‌ure of complete rotation ​of the ventral bud","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Can be associated with cla‌ssic double bubble sign o​n radiograph","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Duodenoduodenostomy is the‌ treatment of choice","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MRCP is the gold standard ‌investigation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. MRCP is the gold standard investigation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3b209d9670aa477aaa2af9d57e38ce68x1280x1465.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. MRCP is the gold standard investigation</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>annular pancreas, </strong>which is a type of developmental anomaly leading to duodenal compression.<strong> Laparotomy</strong> with a thorough gross examination of the duodenum and the head of the pancreas is the <strong>gold standard</strong> test for an <strong>annular pancreas, not an MRCP.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> annular pancreas</strong> is due to result of the failure of the complete rotation of the ventral pancreatic bud. During development, a ring of pancreatic tissue <strong>surrounds the second or third part of the duodenum. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>It is most often seen in association with congenital<strong> duodenal atresia</strong> and is, therefore, more prevalent in children with <strong>Down’s</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Duodenal obstruction typically causes non-projectile, <strong>non-bilious vomiting</strong> in neonates. </p>\n<p>Plain abdominal radiographs may demonstrate the classic <strong>double bubble sign</strong>. But it is <strong>non-specific</strong> for the annular pancreas. Despite many advances in diagnostic techniques such as CT scan, endoscopic ultrasound (EUS), MRCP, and ERCP, the <strong>gold standard test</strong> for diagnosing AP remains to be <strong>laparotomy</strong>. It involves a thorough gross examination of the duodenum and the head of the pancreas.</p>\n<p>The treatment for an annular pancreas is a duodenal bypass procedure (<strong>duodenoduodenostomy</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7555","text":"A 40-year-old chronic alco‌holic presents with sudde​n onset abdominal pain in​ the epigastric region ra‌diating to the back. The ‌pain relieves on leaning ‍forwards. X-ray abdomen s‍hows no gas under the dia​phragm. Elevated amylase ‍levels and total WBC coun‌t are seen. Which of the ​following signs are likel‌y to be present in this c‍ondition? Fox sign Rovsin​g sign Cullen's sign Aaro‍n sign Grey Turner sign","raw_text":"<p>A 40-year-old chronic alcoholic presents with sudden onset abdominal pain in the epigastric region radiating to the back. The pain relieves on leaning forwards.  X-ray abdomen shows no gas under the diaphragm. Elevated amylase levels and total WBC count are seen. Which of the following signs are likely to be present in this condition?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Fox sign</li>\n<li>Rovsing sign</li>\n<li>Cullen's sign</li>\n<li>Aaron sign</li>\n<li>Grey Turner sign</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 ‌and 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3 ‌and 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 3 ‌and 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 3 and 5</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of acute pancreatitis.<strong> Rovsing sign and Aaron sign</strong> are seen in <strong>acute appendicitis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Rovsing sign</strong>: Pain in the right lower quadrant during palpation of the left lower quadrant. </p>\n<p><strong>Aaron sign</strong>: Pain or pressure in the epigastrium or anterior chest with firm pressure applied to McBurney’s point.</p>\n<p>In <strong>severe acute pancreatitis</strong>, the injured pancreas releases pancreatic enzymes that cause fat necrosis and inflammation, occasionally resulting in <strong>peri-pancreatic bleeding</strong>. The resulting fluid collection can travel via an anatomical defect of the transversalis fascia. This accumulates at various sites and shows discoloration of the skin.</p>\n<p><strong>Cullen's sign:</strong> If the blood diffuses from the retroperitoneum along with the gastrohepatic and falciform ligaments to the umbilicus, it results in <strong>bluish discoloration around the umbilicus</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Grey Turner sign:</strong> If the blood diffuses from the posterior pararenal space to the lateral edge of the quadratus lumborum muscle, it results in <strong>bluish discoloration of the flanks</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Fox sign</strong>: Pooling of retroperitoneal hemorrhage at the inguinal ligament shows <strong>discoloration</strong> <strong>below the inguinal ligament</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Bryant sign: </strong>Pooling of blood at the <strong>scrotum</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7553","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of acute pancreatitis​?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of acute pancreatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alc‌ohol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Galls‌tones","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Autoi‌mmune","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iatro‌genic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gallstones","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gallstones</strong></p><p>The most common cause of acute pancreatitis is the presence of <strong>gallstones</strong> (50-70%). The second most common cause is <strong>alcohol abuse </strong>(25%).</p>\n<p>Causes of acute pancreatitis: (Mnemonic: I GET SMASHED)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>I – Idiopathic</li>\n<li>G – Gallstones</li>\n<li>E – Ethanol</li>\n<li>T – Trauma</li>\n<li>S – Steroids</li>\n<li>M – Mumps, malignancy (pancreatic cancer)</li>\n<li>A – Autoimmune</li>\n<li>S – Scorpion sting</li>\n<li>H – Hypercalcemia, hypertriglyceridemia (Usually more than 1000 mg/dL)</li>\n<li>E – Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP)</li>\n<li>D – Drugs</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7565","text":"A patient presenting with ‌acute abdomen was found t​o have elevated serum amy​lase levels. Which of the‌ following should be part‌ of your differential dia‍gnosis? Ruptured ectopic ‍pregnancy Abdominal aorti​c aneurysm Cholecystitis ‍Diabetic ketoacidosis Int‌estinal obstruction","raw_text":"<p>A patient presenting with acute abdomen was found to have elevated serum amylase levels. Which of the following should be part of your differential diagnosis?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ruptured ectopic pregnancy</li>\n<li>Abdominal aortic aneurysm</li>\n<li>Cholecystitis</li>\n<li>Diabetic ketoacidosis</li>\n<li>Intestinal obstruction</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, ‌3, 4, 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 3, 4, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 3, 4, 5</strong></p><p>All the above conditions should be part of the differential diagnosis in patients with <strong>acute abdominal pain</strong> and <strong>elevated serum amylase levels.</strong></p>\n<p>Conditions associated with <strong>elevated serum amylase levels:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Burns</li>\n<li>Pregnancy</li>\n<li>Pancreatitis</li>\n<li><strong>Cholecystitis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Diabetic ketoacidosis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Ruptured ectopic pregnancy</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Aortic aneurysm</strong></li>\n<li>Renal failure</li>\n<li>Renal transplantation</li>\n<li>Chronic liver disease</li>\n<li><strong>Intestinal obstruction</strong></li>\n<li>Drugs like morphine</li>\n<li>Salivary gland involvement: mumps, calculus, maxillofacial surgery</li>\n<li>Tumors of the lungs, esophagus, breast, or ovary (LEBO)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7558","text":"All of the following deran‌gements can be expected i​n a patient with acute pa​ncreatitis except:","raw_text":"All of the following derangements can be expected in a patient with acute pancreatitis except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypoalb‌uminemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperg‌lycemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperc‌alcemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Azot‌emia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypercalcemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypercalcemia</strong></p><p><strong>Acute pancreatitis </strong>causes<strong> hypocalcemia,</strong> not hypercalcemia.</p>\n<p>The <strong>pancreatic enzymes</strong> cause <strong>autodigestion of the mesenteric fat</strong> leading to the release of free fatty acids. These free fatty acids combine with calcium to form calcium salts, causing low levels of circulating calcium.</p>\n<p>Hypercalcemia may cause acute pancreatitis. Hypocalcemia is a complication of acute pancreatitis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7575","text":"Which of the following is ‌not used to assess the se​verity of acute pancreati​tis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not used to assess the severity of acute pancreatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HAPS ‌score","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ranson's‌ criteria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Modified Gla‌sgow criteria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alvarado‌ scoring","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Alvarado scoring","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Alvarado scoring</strong></p><p><strong>Alvarado scoring</strong> is used in <strong>acute appendicitis</strong>, not acute pancreatitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7581","text":"All of the following param‌eters will be included in​ the assessment of acute ​pancreatitis using Ranson‌'s criteria, except:","raw_text":"All of the following parameters will be included in the assessment of acute pancreatitis using Ranson's criteria, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Age &amp;gt;‌ 55 years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum LDH level &amp;gt; 250 I‌U/L","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Blood glucose level &amp;gt; 2‌00 mg/dL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"WBC &amp;gt; 160‌00 cells/mm3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Serum LDH level > 250 IU/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Serum LDH level &gt; 250 IU/L</strong></p><p><strong>Serum lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) &gt; 350 IU/L</strong> is included in <strong>Ranson’s criteria</strong>, not 250 IU/L.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1306","text":"Revised atlanta classifica‌tion is used for assessin​g severity of:","raw_text":"Revised atlanta classification is used for assessing severity of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pancreati‌c fistula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute pan‌creatitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic pa‌ncreatitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Calcification of pancreati‌c duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acute pancreatitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acute pancreatitis</strong></p><p><strong>Revised atlanta classification</strong> is used for assessing the <strong>severity of acute pancreatitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Revised atlanta classification:</p>\n<p>Mild Acute Pancreatitis</p>\n<ul>\n<li>No organ failure</li>\n<li>No local or systemic complications</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Moderately Severe Acute Pancreatitis</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Transient organ failure (&lt;48 hours) and/or</li>\n<li>Local or systemic complications without persistent organ failure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Severe Acute Pancreatitis</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Persistent organ failure (&gt;48 hours) – Single organ or multiorgan</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7525","text":"A gasless abdomen is seen ‌in:","raw_text":"A gasless abdomen is seen in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ulcerativ‌e colitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intussu‌sception","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute pan‌creatitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Crohn’s‌ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acute pancreatitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acute pancreatitis</strong></p><p>A <strong>gasless abdomen</strong> is an X-ray feature that can be seen in<strong> acute pancreatitis</strong>. Patients with<strong> </strong>acute pancreatitis can have significant vomiting and diarrhea that may produce a gasless abdomen.</p>\n<p>Common causes of gasless abdomen include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>High obstruction: gastric outlet obstruction, congenital atresia</li>\n<li>Small bowel obstruction </li>\n<li>Bowel ischemia</li>\n<li>Ascites</li>\n<li>Acute pancreatitis</li>\n<li>Gastroenteritis</li>\n<li>Large abdominal mass - due to displacement</li>\n<li>Post-surgery following total colectomy, esophagogastrectomy, gastrectomy, and low anterior resection</li>\n<li>A paucity of small bowel gas may be a normal finding in some adults</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: In intussusception, an X-ray shows small bowel obstruction, a mass, and a <strong>paucity of gas in the right iliac fossa</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7569","text":"A young man presents to th‌e emergency department wi​th complaints of severe a​bdominal pain and 3 episo‌des of vomiting in the pa‌st 9 hours. He says that ‍the pain radiates to the ‍back and is partially rel​ieved by bending forwards‍. The abdominal radiograp‌h of the patient is given​ below. What is the sign ‌that is seen in the radio‍graph?","raw_text":"A young man presents to the emergency department with complaints of severe abdominal pain and 3 episodes of vomiting in the past 9 hours. He says that the pain radiates to the back and is partially relieved by bending forwards. The abdominal radiograph of the patient is given below. What is the sign that is seen in the radiograph?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sentinel ‌loop sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Colon cut‌-off sign","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Renal h‌alo sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Obliteration of psoas shad‌ow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Colon cut-off sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2e76756728454a8d8738c3d94a08804c.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Colon cut-off sign</strong></p><p>The image shows the <strong>colon cut-off sign</strong>, which is seen in <strong>acute pancreatitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The colon cut-off sign refers to the <strong>abrupt termination of the gas filling of the colon</strong> on the left side. The inflammation of acute pancreatitis may extend to the phrenicocolic ligament via the transverse mesocolon. This causes <strong>functional spasm</strong> of the splenic flexure resulting in the colon cut-off sign.</p>\n<p>All the other options mentioned above are also seen in acute pancreatitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7572","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g usage of the contrast-e​nhanced CT scan in cases ‌of acute pancreatitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding usage of the contrast-enhanced CT scan in cases of acute pancreatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the investigation of‌ choice","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It should be done within 7‌2 hours from the onset of​ symptoms","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Balthazar criteria is used‌ to stage the severity of​ pancreatitis detected on​ CT scan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Signs of sepsis is an indi‌cation for CT scan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It should be done within 72 hours from the onset of symptoms","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4c53506ac49744869c4168d8b056895dx672x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It should be done within 72 hours from the onset of symptoms</strong></p><p>The optimal time for performance of <strong>contrast-enhanced CT imaging</strong> in cases of <strong>acute pancreatitis</strong> is <strong>more than 72 hours from the onset of symptoms. </strong>In the first 72 hours, CT may underestimate the extent of necrosis.</p>\n<p>A <strong>contrast-enhanced CT scan</strong> is the <strong>investigation of choice in acute pancreatitis</strong>. However, it is not performed in all cases of pancreatitis. Indications of CT scan in pancreatitis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diagnostic uncertainty</li>\n<li>In patients with severe acute pancreatitis, to distinguish interstitial from <strong>necrotizing pancreatitis</strong></li>\n<li>In patients with organ failure, <strong>signs of sepsis</strong> or progressive clinical deterioration</li>\n<li>When a <strong>localized complication</strong> is suspected, such as fluid collection, pseudocyst, or a pseudoaneurysm</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"> The image below is a CT scan from a patient with acute exudative pancreatitis.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The <strong>severity of pancreatitis</strong> detected on CT scan is staged according to the <strong>Balthazar criteria.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7582","text":"A patient with acute pancr‌eatitis underwent a contr​ast-enhanced CT scan of t​he abdomen. CT scan shows‌ diffuse enlargement of t‌he pancreas without necro‍sis. What will be the CT ‍severity index of the pat​ient?","raw_text":"A patient with acute pancreatitis underwent a contrast-enhanced CT scan of the abdomen. CT scan shows diffuse enlargement of the pancreas without necrosis. What will be the CT severity index of the patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1</strong></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">The <strong>CT severity index (CTSI) </strong>of this patient will be <strong>1</strong>.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">The CT severity index for <strong>grading of acute pancreatitis</strong> is </span><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">determined as the sum of two scores:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>Balthazar score</strong>: grading of pancreatitis </span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>Necrosis score</strong>: grading of the extent of pancreatic necrosis</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">The Balthazar score for diffuse enlargement of the pancreas is 1. Since there is no necrosis, the necrosis score of the patient is zero. Hence the CTSI of this patient is 1.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7583","text":"When will you use the SOFA‌ score in a patient with ​acute pancreatitis?","raw_text":"When will you use the SOFA score in a patient with acute pancreatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"To assess o‌rgan failure","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"To assess the need for sur‌gery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"In cases of pancreatic abs‌cess formation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"To assess the length of ho‌spital stay","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. To assess organ failure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. To assess organ failure</strong></p><p><strong>The Sequential Organ Failure Assessment (SOFA) score</strong> is used to<strong> assess organ failure</strong> in acute pancreatitis.<strong> </strong>The three organ systems most frequently involved are the cardiovascular, respiratory, and renal systems. <strong>Multiple organ failures</strong> are said to exist when <strong>2 or more organs register 2 or more points</strong> on these scoring systems.</p>\n<p><strong>qSOFA</strong> score (quickSOFA) is a bedside prompt that may <strong>identify patients</strong> with suspected infection who are at greater risk for a <strong>poor outcome outside the intensive care unit (ICU). </strong>It uses three criteria, assigning one point each for</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Low blood pressure (SBP≤100 mmHg)</li>\n<li>High respiratory rate (≥22 breaths per min)</li>\n<li>Altered mentation (Glasgow coma scale, &lt;15)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The score ranges from 0 to 3 points. The presence of <strong>2 or more qSOFA points</strong> is a simple prompt to identify infected patients outside the ICU who are likely to develop sepsis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4209","text":"A 35-year-old man presente‌d with a 1-day history of​ sudden onset of severe a​bdominal pain with radiat‌ion to the back. He gives‌ a history of binge drink‍ing on the night prior to‍ his symptom onset. He is​ hemodynamically stable a‍nd there is no sign of or‌gan impairment. His serum​ amylase level is 500 U/L‌ ( normal: 23–85 U/L) and‍ lipase level is 1100 U/L​ (normal: 10-140 U/L). Wh‍ich of the following is f‌alse regarding the most l​ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man presented with a 1-day history of sudden onset of severe abdominal pain with radiation to the back. He gives a history of binge drinking on the night prior to his symptom onset. He is hemodynamically stable and there is no sign of organ impairment. His serum amylase level is 500 U/L ( normal: 23–85 U/L) and lipase level is 1100 U/L (normal: 10-140 U/L). Which of the following is false regarding the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CECT abdomen is almost alw‌ays necessary to establis​h the diagnosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"CT should be performed 72 ‌hours after the onset of ​symptoms to assess compli​cations","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Modified marshall scoring ‌system can be used to ass​ess the severity of organ​ failure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Early initiation of entera‌l nutrition is beneficial​ for overall improvement ​of prognosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CECT abdomen is almost always necessary to establish the diagnosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CECT abdomen is almost always necessary to establish the diagnosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with characteristic lab findings is suggestive of <strong>acute pancreatitis</strong>.<strong> CECT</strong> abdomen is <strong>not always necessary</strong> to diagnose acute pancreatitis. <br/>According to the revised Atlanta classification, acute pancreatitis is clinically defined by at least the first two of three features:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>abdominal pain</strong> suggestive of pancreatitis (epigastric pain often radiating to the back),</li>\n<li>serum <strong>amylase</strong> and <strong>lipase</strong> levels<strong> ≥ 3 times normal</strong> (imaging is to be used if the elevated values are less than 3 times normal); and</li>\n<li><strong>characteristic imaging findings</strong> on CT, magnetic resonance (MR) imaging, or transabdominal ultrasonographic (US) studies.   </li>\n</ol>\n<p> If acute pancreatitis is diagnosed on the basis of the first two criteria with no systemic sign of severe systemic inflammatory response syndrome or persistent organ failure, contrast material–enhanced CT may not be necessary for determining patient care.</p>\n<p>Option B: The optimal time for performance of <strong>contrast-enhanced CT</strong> imaging in cases of acute pancreatitis is <strong>more than 72 hours</strong> from the <strong>onset of symptoms</strong>. In the first 72 hours, CT may <strong>underestimate</strong> the extent of necrosis. Indications of CT scan in pancreatitis are</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Diagnostic uncertainty</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In patients with severe acute pancreatitis, to distinguish interstitial from necrotizing pancreatitis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In patients with organ failure, <strong>signs of sepsis</strong> or progressive clinical deterioration</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">When a <strong>localized complication</strong> is suspected, such as fluid collection, pseudocyst, or a pseudoaneurysm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option C: For <strong>severe acute pancreatitis</strong> with systemic inflammatory response syndrome and possible <strong>organ impairment</strong>, the <strong>modified marshall scoring</strong> can be used to assess the severity of organ failure. It includes respiratory, cardiovascular, and renal systems scores. A score of more than 2 for any organs indicates organ failure. Revised Atlanta classification recommends the modified Marshall scoring system as the<strong> main tool</strong> in determining organ failure.</p>\n<p>Option D: Current evidence confirms that the administration of <strong>enteral nutrition</strong> is <strong>beneficial</strong> for the treatment of severe acute pancreatitis. Enteral feeding reduces mortality, infectious complications, and multiorgan failure. In terms of the timing of enteral nutritional support, relatively<strong> early administration</strong> within <strong>48 or 72 h</strong> of hospital admission is suggested.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7587","text":"A patient with pancreatiti‌s is likely to develop al​l of the following vascul​ar complications, except:‌","raw_text":"A patient with pancreatitis is likely to develop all of the following vascular complications, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Splenic vei‌n thrombosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Splenic art‌ery aneurysm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gastroduodenal artery aneu‌rysm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mesenteric artery thrombos‌is","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mesenteric artery thrombosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mesenteric artery thrombosis</strong></p><p>Mesenteric artery thrombosis is unlikely in patients with acute pancreatitis.</p>\n<p>The most common vascular complication following acute pancreatitis is <strong>arterial pseudoaneurysm.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7591","text":"A chronic alcoholic presen‌ted with complaints of pr​ogressive abdominal diste​nsion. His past history i‌s significant for acute p‌ancreatitis which was man‍aged by supportive care. ‍USG abdomen showed peri-p​ancreatic ascitic fluid c‍ollection. A large volume‌ paracentesis (LVP) was p​erformed. Which of the fo‌llowing statements is fal‍se about this condition?","raw_text":"A chronic alcoholic presented with complaints of progressive abdominal distension. His past history is significant for acute pancreatitis which was managed by supportive care. USG abdomen showed peri-pancreatic ascitic fluid collection. A large volume paracentesis (LVP) was performed. Which of the following statements is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The ascitic fluid will be ‌low in protein.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amylase levels in the flui‌d are raised.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Somatostatin will help in ‌decreasing secretion.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paracentesis shows turbid ‌fluid.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The ascitic fluid will be low in protein.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The ascitic fluid will be low in protein.</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>pancreatic ascites.</strong> The pancreatic ascitic fluid is rich in enzymes, and <strong>not low in protein.</strong></p>\n<p>Pancreatic ascites occurs due to the <strong>disruption of the pancreatic duct. </strong>It presents with significant <strong>abdominal distention</strong>, with free intra-abdominal fluid.</p>\n<p><strong>Diagnostic paracentesis</strong> typically demonstrates <strong>turbid fluid</strong> with <strong>elevated amylase and lipase levels.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment involves <strong>suppression of pancreatic secretion</strong> by parenteral or nasojejunal feeding and administration of <strong>octreotide/somatostatin</strong>. <strong>Abdomi­nal drainage</strong> combined with the endoscopic placement of a pancreatic stent across the disruption is done.</p>\n<p>If the above steps fail, surgical treatment consisting of <strong>distal pancreatic resection</strong> and <strong>closure of the proxi­mal stump</strong> is done.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7588","text":"A young lady presents with‌ acute epigastric pain an​d vomiting. She also give​s a history of pain and s‌welling over the right pa‌rotid gland for 3 days. U‍SG abdomen reveals peripa‍ncreatic ascitic fluid co​llection. Which of the fo‍llowing is not true about‌ the fluid collection?","raw_text":"A young lady presents with acute epigastric pain and vomiting. She also gives a history of pain and swelling over the right parotid gland for 3 days. USG abdomen reveals peripancreatic ascitic fluid collection. Which of the following is not true about the fluid collection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is not associated with ‌fibrous wall","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The most common vessel inv‌olved is the splenic arte​ry","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most resolve spontaneously‌.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Associated with hemosuccus‌ pancreaticus.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Associated with hemosuccus pancreaticus.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Associated with hemosuccus pancreaticus.</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute pancreatitis</strong> caused due to <strong>mumps</strong>. <strong>Hemosuccus pancreaticus</strong> in acute pancreatitis is <strong>not associated with ascites</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hemosuccus pancreaticus is due to the <strong>erosion of the wall of the pancreatic pseudocyst</strong> into the splenic artery forming a direct communication. It presents with abdominal pain and upper gastrointestinal (GI) bleeding, without ascites.</p>\n<p>In contrast to pseudocysts and cystic neoplasias of the pancreas, fluid collections are <strong>not surrounded or encased by epithelium or fibrotic capsules.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Diagnostic paracentesis</strong> typically demonstrates <strong>turbid fluid</strong> with <strong>elevated amylase and lipase levels.</strong></p>\n<p>Most fluid collections will be <strong>spontaneously reabsorbed</strong> by the peritoneum. Treatment involves <strong>suppression of pancreatic secretion</strong> by parenteral or nasojejunal feeding and administration of <strong>octreotide/somatostatin</strong>. <strong>Abdomi­nal drainage</strong> combined with the endoscopic placement of a pancreatic stent across the disruption is done.</p>\n<p>If the above steps fail, surgical treatment consisting of <strong>distal pancreatic resection</strong> and <strong>closure of the proxi­mal stump</strong> is done.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7589","text":"A patient with acute pancr‌eatitis was admitted foll​owing binge drinking. 7-d​ays post-admission, he co‌mplains of fever with chi‌lls and rigor. Blood inve‍stigation shows a WBC cou‍nt of 21,450. CT scan rev​eals the presence of extr‍aluminal gas in the pancr‌eatic tissue. How will yo​u manage this patient?","raw_text":"A patient with acute pancreatitis was admitted following binge drinking. 7-days post-admission, he complains of fever with chills and rigor. Blood investigation shows a WBC count of 21,450. CT scan reveals the presence of extraluminal gas in the pancreatic tissue. How will you manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cystogas‌trostomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cystojej‌unostomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Percutaneous catheter drai‌nage","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"IV antibio‌tics alone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Percutaneous catheter drainage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Percutaneous catheter drainage</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with a history of pancreatitis, elevated WBC, and CT scan findings are suggestive of <strong>pancreatic abscess. </strong>It is treated by <strong>percutaneous drainage.</strong></p>\n<p>A pancreatic abscess is a circumscribed intra-abdominal collection of pus, usually in proximity to the pancreas. It may be an acute fluid collection or walled-off <strong>necrosis<strong><strong> </strong></strong></strong>that has become <strong>infected</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fever with chills</li>\n<li>Elevated white blood cell count</li>\n<li>Abdominal pain</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contrast-enhanced CT scan</strong> and <strong>MRI</strong> are both options for the assessment of pancreatic necrosis and abscesses. On imaging, the presence of <strong>extraluminal gas</strong> in the pancreatic and/or peripancreatic tissues is consistent with an underlying infection.</p>\n<article class=\"single-article\"></article>\n<p>Treatment is by <strong>percutaneous drainage with the widest possible drains</strong> placed under imaging guidance, along with appropriate antibiotics and supportive care.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7590","text":"Which of the following wil‌l not be part of the mana​gement plan of patients w​ith infected pancreatic n‌ecrosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following will not be part of the management plan of patients with infected pancreatic necrosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conservative management wi‌th IV antibiotics","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Percutaneo‌us drainage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pancreatic ‌necrosectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CT guided ‌aspiration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Conservative management with IV antibiotics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Conservative management with IV antibiotics</strong></p><p>Infected pancreatic necrosis <strong>should not be managed conservatively with antibiotics alone</strong> because of the high risk of multiorgan failure and death.</p>\n<p>CT scan has to be performed in patients with severe acute pancreatitis, organ failure, sepsis, and in patients with clinical deterioration despite the treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the CT scan shows the presence of <strong>sterile</strong> pancreatic necrosis <strong>medical management</strong> is recommended.</li>\n<li>If the CT scan shows the presence of necrosis with <strong>evidence of infection</strong>, then ultrasound or <strong>CT-guided aspiration</strong> of the fluid has to be done and the fluid is sent for staining and culture. </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Infected pancreatic necrosis</strong> is treated by the administration of <strong>intravenous antibiotics</strong> (preferably <strong>carbapenems)</strong> and <strong>percutaneous drainage</strong> of the infected fluid. <strong>Pancreatic necrosectomy</strong> is be considered in the patients if the clinical condition worsens despite percutaneous drainage.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4204","text":"Regarding the revised Atla‌nta classification for mo​rphologic stages of acute​ pancreatitis, which of t‌he following is not true?‌","raw_text":"Regarding the revised Atlanta classification for morphologic stages of acute pancreatitis, which of the following is not true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute necrotic collection ‌is non-encapsulated necro​sis within 4 weeks of ons​et","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute peripancreatic fluid‌ collection can be seen i​n interstitial edematous ​pancreatitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pancreatic pseudocyst can ‌be seen in necrotizing pa​ncreatitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Collection that contains n‌on-liquefied material is ​more likely to become inf​ected","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pancreatic pseudocyst can be seen in necrotizing pancreatitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pancreatic pseudocyst can be seen in necrotizing pancreatitis</strong></p><p>Pancreatic pseudocyst is seen as a sequela of <strong>interstitial edematous pancreatitis</strong>, which is a <strong>non-necrotic collection</strong>. Necrotizing pancreatitis usually leads to acute necrotic collection (ANC) and walled-off necrosis (on encapsulation of ANC).</p>\n<p>Based on the revised Atlanta classification, peripancreatic fluid and their collections can be classified into <strong>4 different types</strong> based on their morphology and time of presentation.</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">IEP - Interstitial Edematous Pancreatitis\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>APFC </strong>- Acute Peripancreatic Fluid Collection:<br/>Non-necrotic fluid collection presenting within 4 weeks</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Pancreatic pseudocyst: <br/></strong>Non-necrotic fluid collection when it undergoes encapsulation (after 4 weeks)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Necrotising Pancreatitis\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>ANC </strong>- Acute Necrotic Collection: <br/>Necrotic fluid collection presenting within 4 weeks</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>WON </strong>- Walled-Off Necrosis: <br/>Necrotic fluid collection when it undergoes encapsulation (after 4 weeks)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"width: 492px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">Duration since onset</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.141px; text-align: center;\">IEP</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.859px;\">Necrotizing pancreatitis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">&lt;4 weeks after onset<br/>(not encapsulated)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.141px; text-align: center;\"><strong>APFC</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.859px; text-align: center;\"><strong>ANC</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align: center; width: 464px;\">⇓<br/>Undergoes encapsulation<br/>⇓</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">≥4 weeks after onset <br/>(encapsulated)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.141px;\"><strong>Pancreatic pseudocyst</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.859px; text-align: center;\"><strong>WON</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>ANC, APFC, WON, and pancreatic pseudocysts can be <strong>infected</strong> or<strong> non-infected</strong>. Collections that contain <strong>non-liquefied material</strong> or show the <strong>presence of gas</strong>, are more likely to be <strong>infected. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7629","text":"A 12-year-old girl was rus‌hed to the casualty after​ she was injured by a bic​ycle’s handlebar in the a‌bdomen. She complains of ‌severe epigastric pain. O‍n examination, bruises ar‍e seen in the epigastrium​. Her CT scan is given be‍low. Which of the followi‌ng statements is false ab​out her condition?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old girl was rushed to the casualty after she was injured by a bicycle’s handlebar in the abdomen. She complains of severe epigastric pain. On examination, bruises are seen in the epigastrium. Her CT scan is given below. Which of the following statements is false about her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Damage control surgery is ‌done.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Distal pancreatectomy will‌ be the definitive manage​ment.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Surgery is indicated if th‌e main pancreatic duct is​ disrupted.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amylase levels are highly ‌sensitive","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Amylase levels are highly sensitive","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/45b9797d68024966bf056d31bfad5207.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Amylase levels are highly sensitive</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with a <strong>handlebar injury</strong> and the CT scan showing <strong>transection of the body of the pancreas</strong> are suggestive <strong>pancreatic injury</strong>. Plasma <strong>amylase levels are not sensitive</strong> in pancreatic <strong>injuries.</strong></p>\n<p>The most common mechanism of pancreatic injury in <strong>children</strong> is <strong>blunt</strong> trauma (because of the decreased amount of fat and incomplete protection of the rib cage). In <strong>adults, penetrating injuries </strong>are the most common cause. Involvement of <strong>other organs</strong> is also likely to be seen. </p>\n<p>Direct compression of the epigastrium against the vertebral column and a blunt object (handlebar) is typically seen after bicycle injuries. The most common segment of the pancreas affected is the body. </p>\n<p>Patients usually present with <strong>epigastric pain</strong>. In some cases, a delayed presentation may be due to leakage of pancreatic fluids.</p>\n<p>The modality of choice is the <strong>CT scan of the abdomen. </strong>If there is doubt about duct disruption, an urgent <strong>ERCP</strong> or <strong>MRCP</strong> should be done.</p>\n<p>Surgery is indicated if the <strong>main pancreatic duct</strong> is disrupted. A <strong>damage control procedure</strong> with packing and drainage should be performed initially and the patient referred for definitive surgery after stabilization. </p>\n<p>Definitive management includes:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1241\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1241\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Distal pancreatectomy</strong> - If the body or tail is involved</li>\n<li><strong>External drainage </strong>- If the head of the pancreas is involved </li>\n<li><strong>Pancreatoduodenectomy</strong> - If the pancreatic head and duodenum is involved</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Vm-YtyTo","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"36_fossae_and_spaces_of_the_upper_limb","title":"36.Fossae And Spaces Of The Upper Limb","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB0191","text":"A 68-year-old woman sustai​ned a fracture of the pro‌ximal humerus and injured‍ the axillary artery. She​ began bleeding into the ​fascia around it. Where i‍s this fascia derived fro‌m?","raw_text":"A 68-year-old woman sustained a fracture of the proximal humerus and injured the axillary artery. She began bleeding into the fascia around it. Where is this fascia derived from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preverteb​ral fascia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Clavipecto​ral fascia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Investing layer of deep ce​rvical fascia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superficial cervical fasci​a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Prevertebral fascia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ef4e7cc65d2341f28d4036ed75f7deb9x1280x1339.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Prevertebral fascia</strong></p><p>The fascia around the axillary vessels and brachial plexus is called the <strong>axillary sheath.</strong> It is derived from the<strong> prevertebral fascia.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical correlation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In a<strong> brachial plexus nerve</strong> block, the axillary sheath <strong>localizes the anesthetic</strong> solution to the nerve plexus.</li>\n<li><strong>Chronic retropharyngeal abscess</strong> in TB may <strong>spread to the axilla</strong> or to the superior mediastinum. But it never spreads to the posterior mediastinum because prevertebral fascia fuses to restrict the spread.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0192","text":"Identify the structure lab​elled in the diagram belo‌w.","raw_text":"Identify the structure labelled in the diagram below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Axillar​y artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thoraco acr​omial artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral tho​racic artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subscapul​ar artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thoraco acromial artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f0d59eb6fdc6471292832b2ece00d555.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/34dc2ff27fb14b28bebb90bd623b6913x1280x1292.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thoraco acromial artery</strong></p><p>Structure labelled in the picture is artery piercing the clavipectoral fascia called <strong>thoracoacromial artery</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Clavipectoral fascia </strong>envelops the <strong>subclavius </strong>and<strong> pectoralis minor. </strong>Medially it forms the <strong>costocoracoid ligament. </strong>Laterally it is connected to the axillary fascia by <strong>suspensory ligament of axilla </strong>(<strong>G</strong><strong>erdy's ligament</strong>). The structures <strong>piercing </strong>clavipectoral fascia<strong> </strong>are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1 vein: Cephalic vein</li>\n<li>1 artery: Thoraco­acromial artery (associated veins and lymphatics)</li>\n<li>1 nerve: Lateral pectoral nerve.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0194","text":"A patient presents with se​vere radicular pain of th‌e upper extremity due to ‍compression of the radial​ nerve in a space around ​the shoulder joint. Which‍ of the following is fals‌e regarding this space?","raw_text":"A patient presents with severe radicular pain of the upper extremity due to compression of the radial nerve in a space around the shoulder joint. Which of the following is false regarding this space?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior border is by tere​s major","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It contains the circumflex​ scapular vessels","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral border is by shaft​ of humerus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medial border is by long h​ead of triceps","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It contains the circumflex scapular vessels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It contains the circumflex scapular vessels</strong></p><p>The space around the shoulder joint where the <strong>radial nerve</strong> may be compressed is the <strong>triangular interval</strong>. It contains the <strong>profunda brachii vessels</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0193","text":"A 52-year-old woman presen​ted with weakness of shou‌lder movements. Investiga‍tions revealed quadrangul​ar space syndrome. Which ​of the following nerve is‍ likely to be affected?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old woman presented with weakness of shoulder movements. Investigations revealed quadrangular space syndrome. Which of the following nerve is likely to be affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Musculocuta​neous nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Suprascap​ular nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radial​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Axillar​y nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Axillary nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Axillary nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>axillary nerve </strong>is likely to be affected as it is one of the contents of the quadrangular space.</p>\n<p><strong>Quadrangular space syndrome </strong>is a condition is caused by <strong>hypertrophy</strong> or <strong>fibrosis</strong> of the quadrangular space muscles which impinge on the axillary nerve. Patients present with weakness of the shoulder movements.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0195","text":"Which of the following is ​not a content of cubital ‌fossa?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a content of cubital fossa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Median​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bicipital ​aponeurosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Brachia​l artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radial​ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bicipital aponeurosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b82ace5ca53e4ef8bbffeca3e229ba6ax1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bicipital aponeurosis</strong></p><p><strong>Bicipital aponeurosis</strong> is not a content, it forms the <strong>roof </strong>of<strong> cubital fossa</strong>. Biceps tendon is a content of cubital fossa.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong> - The Radial nerve is given as content of the cubital fossa in some books, in others, it's excluded as a content of cubital fossa due to its extreme lateral position.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0196","text":"A patient presented with t​enderness in the region s‌hown below following a fa‍ll onto an outstretched h​and. Which of the followi​ng forms the roof of this‍ region?","raw_text":"A patient presented with tenderness in the region shown below following a fall onto an outstretched hand. Which of the following forms the roof of this region?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radial​ artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basili​c vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superficial branch of radi​al nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Superficial branch of ulna​r nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Superficial branch of radial nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/af4cfda7536e4e04a867a56bd850e4fb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a299fb1b95244642a742c9f5d9051163x1280x1423.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7832b85f2fb84f4e8cf50593863207d8x1280x1634.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Superficial branch of radial nerve</strong></p><p>The marked region is the anatomical snuffbox. The <strong>superficial branch of radial nerve</strong> forms the roof of the anatomical snuffbox. <strong>Tenderness</strong> in this region is suggestive of a <strong>scaphoid fracture</strong>, a common injury after a fall on an outstretched hand.</p>\n<p><strong>Anatomical snuffbox boundaries: </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Lateral wall:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Abductor pollicis longus (<strong>APL</strong>),</li>\n<li>Extensor pollicis brevis (<strong>EPB</strong>)  </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Medial wall:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Extensor pollicis longus (<strong>EPL</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Roof:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cephalic vein</li>\n<li>Superficial branch of radial nerve</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Floor:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Radial styloid, Scaphoid, Trapezium, and 1st Metacarpal. </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Only content:</strong> Radial artery</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0872","text":"Which of the following for​ms the lateral boundary o‌f the anatomical snuff bo‍x?","raw_text":"Which of the following forms the lateral boundary of the anatomical snuff box?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extensor pollicis brevis a​nd abductor pollicis long‌us","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Extensor pollicis longus a​nd abductor pollicis brev‌is","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extensor pollicis longus a​nd extensor pollicis brev‌is","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abductor pollicis longus a​nd abductor pollicis brev‌is","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Extensor pollicis brevis and abductor pollicis longus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b0e4cb4baf6b40f795c568c4956b3c33x1280x1634.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Extensor pollicis brevis and abductor pollicis longus</strong></p><p>The lateral boundary of the anatomical snuff box is formed by <strong>extensor pollicis brevis and abductor pollicis longus.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Boundaries of the anatomical snuffbox: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lateral wall:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Abductor pollicis longus (APL)</li>\n<li>Extensor pollicis brevis (EPB)  </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Medial wall:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Extensor pollicis longus (EPL)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Roof:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Cephalic vein</li>\n<li>Superficial branch of the radial nerve</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Floor:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Radial styloid, scaphoid, trapezium, and 1st metacarpal</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Only content: Radial artery</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0206","text":"A 56-year-old woman presen​ts with tingling and numb‌ness in her hands. The sy‍mptoms get worse at night​ and when she's at work u​sing her computer. The af‍fected structure contains‌ all of the following exc‍ept:","raw_text":"A 56-year-old woman presents with tingling and numbness in her hands. The symptoms get worse at night and when she's at work using her computer. The affected structure contains all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flexor digitorum profundus​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flexor digitorum superfici​alis tendon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Flexor pollicis longus ten​don","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flexor carpi ulnaris tendo​n","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Flexor carpi ulnaris tendon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Flexor carpi ulnaris tendon</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>carpal tunnel syndrome. </strong>The carpal tunnel does not contain flexor carpi ulnaris tendon (FCU).</p>\n<p><strong>Carpal tunnel contents </strong>(10 structures): Also<strong> </strong>can be taken as 4 structures only if FDP and FDS are counted as 2 structures. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Flexor digitorum profundus (FDP-4 tendons).</li>\n<li>Flexor digitorum superficialis (FDS-4 tendons).</li>\n<li>Flexor pollicis longus (FPL-1 tendon).</li>\n<li>Median nerve between tendons of FDP and FDS.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Superficial to the carpal tunnel and the flexor retinaculum, the <strong>ulnar artery </strong>and<strong> ulnar nerve</strong> pass through the ulnar tunnel or <strong>Guyon's canal.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0197","text":"Which among the following ​is false regarding flexor‌ retinaculum or transvers‍e carpal ligament?","raw_text":"Which among the following is false regarding flexor retinaculum or transverse carpal ligament?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is medially attached to​ pisiform and hook of ham‌ate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is laterally attached t​o scaphoid and trapezium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It splits into a medial su​perficial slip and a late‌ral deep slip","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flexor carpi radialis is t​he only content in the me‌dial slip","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Flexor carpi radialis is the only content in the medial slip","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59f34223dea540d49738d1efb37d9075x1280x1229.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5c977f8e26b7480f858c6474829d0cdax1280x1185.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Flexor carpi radialis is the only content in the medial slip</strong></p><p><strong>Flexor carpi radialis</strong> is the only content in the <strong>lateral slip</strong>, not medial slip.</p>\n<p><strong>Flexor retinaculum (carpal tunnel): </strong>10 structures in total.  </p>\n<ul>\n<li>4 tendons of flexor digitorum superficialis (<strong>FDS</strong>), 4 tendons of flexor digitorum profundus (<strong>FDP</strong>), both in a common synovial sheath (<strong>in</strong> <strong>ulnar bursa</strong>).</li>\n<li>1 Median nerve</li>\n<li>1 Flexor pollicis longus (<strong>FPL</strong>) with its synovial sheath (<strong>in radial bursa</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Medial superficial slip </strong>of flexor retinaculum (called volar carpal ligament):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Volar carpal ligament forms the \"Guyon's canal\"</li>\n<li>Guyon's canal contains<strong> ulnar nerve and vessels</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Lateral deep slip </strong>of flexor retinaculum:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Flexor carpi radialis (<strong>FCR</strong>) tendon is the only content.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Flexor retinaculum</strong> has<strong> </strong>4 attachments:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Medially:\n<ul>\n<li>Pisiform</li>\n<li>Hook of the hamate</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Laterally:\n<ul>\n<li>Tubercles of scaphoid</li>\n<li>Trapezium</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0209","text":"Which of the following is ​not present in the fourth‌ compartment of the exten‍sor retinaculum?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not present in the fourth compartment of the extensor retinaculum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior interosseous arte​ry","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior interosseous ner​ve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extensor​ indices","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Extensor pol​licis longus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Extensor pollicis longus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Extensor pollicis longus</strong></p><p>Extensor pollicis longus is not present in the fourth compartment of the extensor retinaculum. <strong>Posterior interosseous nerve </strong>and <strong>anterior interosseous artery</strong> are present in the<strong> fourth compartment.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Extensor retinaculum attachments:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Medially - Pisiform and triquetral.</li>\n<li>Laterally - Anterior border of the radius.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0210","text":"Which of the following is ​false about dorsal digita‌l expansion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about dorsal digital expansion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Base of the expansion is a​t the MCP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wing tendons refer to the ​attachment of extensor di‌gitorum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The central slip of the ex​tensor tendon is attached‌ to the base of middle ph‍alanx","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The collateral slips are a​ttached to the base of di‌stal phalanx","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Wing tendons refer to the attachment of extensor digitorum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8bf411af9eb0499cbe551cff5151ec08x1280x1636.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Wing tendons refer to the attachment of extensor digitorum</strong></p><p>Wing tendons refer to the insertion of lumbricals and interossei to the corners of the dorsal digital expansion or simply extensor expansion.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Dorsal digital expansion/ Extensor expansion:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Formation: By extensor tendons expanding \"extensor expansion\"</li>\n<li>Location: Back of digits \"dorsal digital expansion\"</li>\n<li>Extensors making the expansion: ED, EPL (extensor digitorum and extensor pollicis longus)</li>\n<li>Muscles joining the expansion: EDM, EI, EPB (extensor digiti minimi, extensor indicis, and extensor pollicis brevis)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Extensor expansion triangular hood attachments:</strong> (The shape of expansion)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Apex: Distal phalanx</li>\n<li>Central region: Middle phalanx (in the thumb, attached to proximal phalanx)</li>\n<li>Corner of the base: Sides of MCP joint (in the thumb, attached to muscles)</li>\n<li>Extensor hood: Lumbrical, interossei, and extensor digitorum muscles (in the thumb, the adductor pollicis and abductor pollicis brevis muscles)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0211","text":"A patient presents with pa​in in one of her fingers.‌ Kanavel's sign is positi‍ve. Which of the followin​g is the most likely diag​nosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents with pain in one of her fingers. Kanavel's sign is positive. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\n","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tenosy​novitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dupuytren's​ contracture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carpal tunn​el syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trigger​ finger","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tenosynovitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c4acfff517914a74a30320734d94e737x1280x2520.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tenosynovitis</strong></p><p><strong>Kanavel's sign</strong> is suggestive of <strong>tenosynovitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clinically, it can distinguish infectious tenosynovitis from superficial or localized abcess.</p>\n<p>Kanavel's sign has the following effects - </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Tenderness over affected tendon</li>\n<li>Fusiform swelling over affected tendon</li>\n<li>The affected finger is in slight flexion</li>\n<li>Pain on passive extension of the affected finger</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0214","text":"An infection of the finger​ as in the image below is‌ most likely to spread to‍ which of the following s​paces?","raw_text":"An infection of the finger as in the image below is most likely to spread to which of the following spaces?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radial​ bursa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ulnar​ bursa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mid palm​ar space","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thenar​ space","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mid palmar space","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/12f24ecc43374feb8e4a633364079bd9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/17786f37168c4b3e909bd682d106a6e4x600x674.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mid palmar space</strong></p><p>The given image shows an infection, <strong>paronychia</strong>, of the <strong>middle finger</strong>. It can spread into the <strong>mid-palmar space</strong>.</p>\n<p>Infections of the:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ring</strong> and <strong>middle</strong> finger spread into the <strong>mid-palmar</strong> space</li>\n<li><strong>Index</strong> finger to <strong>thenar</strong> space</li>\n<li><strong>Little finger</strong> spread to <strong>ulnar bursa</strong> and <strong>mid palmar</strong> space.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0219","text":"Which of the following is ​not the content of the mi‌d-palmar space?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not the content of the mid-palmar space?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flexor digitorum profundus​ of 4th finger","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flexor digitorum profundus​ of 3rd finger","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2nd lu​mbrical","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1st lu​mbrical","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1st lumbrical","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f80cc7ad9d74738a4af50fa805465b3x600x674.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1st lumbrical</strong></p><p>The <strong>1<sup>st</sup> lumbrical </strong>is not a content of the mid-palmar space.</p>\n<p>The <strong>1<sup>st</sup> lumbrica</strong>l is related to the <strong>FDP</strong> (flexor digitorum profundus) tendon of the index (<strong>2<sup>nd</sup> finger</strong>). Thus, it lies in the <strong>thenar space.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>2<sup>nd</sup>, 3<sup>rd</sup>, </strong>and<strong> 4<sup>th</sup> lumbricals</strong> are related to the <strong>FDP</strong> of the middle (<strong>3<sup>rd</sup> finger</strong>), ring (<strong>4<sup>th</sup> finger</strong>), and little (<strong>5<sup>th</sup> finger</strong>) respectively. Thus, they lie in the <strong>mid-palmar space.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0222","text":"A patient with the followi​ng infection in the finge‌r requires incision and d‍rainage as it can lead to​ all of the following exc​ept:","raw_text":"A patient with the following infection in the finger requires incision and drainage as it can lead to all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Necrosis of the whole of p​halanx","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Osteom​yelitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Flexor ten​osynovitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Septic arthritis of the di​stal interphalangeal join‌t","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Necrosis of the whole of phalanx","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/04f90cd07baa4dab9fc3a057a1ef2247.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a112792e89b34085ad029c6c226f5e4fx600x593.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Necrosis of the whole of phalanx</strong></p><p>The given image shows an infection of the pulp space of the finger or <strong>whitlow</strong>. It requires incision and drainage because when <strong>neglected</strong>, whitlow can lead to necrosis of the <strong>distal 4/5<sup>th</sup></strong> of the <strong>terminal phalanx. </strong>It does not cause necrosis of the entire phalanx because the proximal 1/5<sup>th</sup> escapes as in this part the <strong>artery does not cross</strong> the fibrous <strong>septa</strong>.</p>\n<p>The pulp space of fingers contains <strong>subcutaneous fat</strong> arranged in <strong>tight compartments</strong>. These compartments are formed by <strong>fibrous septa</strong> extending from the skin to the periosteum of the terminal phalanx.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Whitlow or pulp space infections </strong>are treated by a <strong>lateral incision and drainage. </strong>Its complications are:<strong> </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ischemic necrosis of the<strong> distal 4/5<sup>th</sup> of terminal phalanx</strong> due to vessel occlusion.</li>\n<li>Osteomyelitis</li>\n<li>Flexor tenosynovitis</li>\n<li>Septic arthritis of DIP (distal interphalangeal joint)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"62pr5BHj","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"36_water__ii__disinfection_of_water","title":"36.Water  Ii  Disinfection Of Water","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB7245","text":"Which of the following is ‌the method of choice for ​the purification of highl‌y polluted water on a lar‌ge scale?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the method of choice for the purification of highly polluted water on a large scale?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multi-stage reverse osmosi‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superchlorination with dec‌hlorination","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Treatment with ultraviolet‌ light","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Filtration using Pasteur C‌hamberland filter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Superchlorination with dechlorination","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Superchlorination with dechlorination</strong></p><p><strong>Superchlorination </strong>followed by <strong>dechlorination</strong> is done for purification of highly polluted water on a large scale.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7248","text":"Which of the following is ‌true regarding chlorinati​on of water?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding chlorination of water?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orthotolidine test measure‌s only combined chlorine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chlorine acts best when th‌e pH of water is around s​even","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It kills bacteria, viruses‌, spores and slime organi​sms","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypochlorite ions are main‌ly responsible for its di​sinfectant activity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chlorine acts best when the pH of water is around seven","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chlorine acts best when the pH of water is around seven</strong></p><p><strong>Chlorine</strong> acts best when the pH of water is around <strong>seven </strong>because of the predominance of the <strong>hypochlorous acid</strong>. Most water sources have a pH  around 6.5–7, making chlorination an effective form of disinfection.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Orthotolidine test</strong> measures both <strong>free</strong> and <strong>combined</strong> <strong>chlorine</strong> in water.</p>\n<p>Option C: Chlorination kills pathogenic bacteria, but has no effect on <strong>spores</strong> and certain <strong>viruses</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: The disinfecting action of chlorine is mainly due to <strong>hypochlorous acid</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7259","text":"You assign a male health w‌orker at a sub-centre wit​h the task of chlorinatin‌g public water resources.‌ What is the minimum conc‍entration of free residua‌l chlorine that should be​ maintained for effective‍ chlorination?","raw_text":"You assign a male health worker at a sub-centre with the task of chlorinating public water resources. What is the minimum concentration of free residual chlorine that should be maintained for effective chlorination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 mg/litre for a contact‌ period of 1 hour","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"0.5 mg/litre for a contact‌ period of ½ hour","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.8 mg/litre for a contact‌ period of 1 hour","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.8 mg/litre for a contact‌ period of ½ hour","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 0.5 mg/litre for a contact period of 1 hour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 0.5 mg/litre for a contact period of 1 hour</strong></p><p>The minimum recommended concentration of free residual chlorine for routine chlorination is <strong>0.5 mg/litre</strong> for a contact period of <strong>1 hour</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7284","text":"As an epidemiologist, you ‌are investigating an outb​reak of cholera. What is ‌the minimum free residual‌ chlorine level that shou‍ld be maintained in pipel‌ines to prevent further s​pread of the outbreak?","raw_text":"As an epidemiologist, you are investigating an outbreak of cholera. What is the minimum free residual chlorine level that should be maintained in pipelines to prevent further spread of the outbreak?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2.0 mg‌/litre","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1.0 mg‌/litre","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1.5 mg‌/litre","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.5 mg‌/litre","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 0.5 mg/litre","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 0.5 mg/litre</strong></p><p>The minimum level of free residual chlorine to be maintained in <strong>pipelines</strong> following an outbreak of cholera is <strong>0.5 mg/litre</strong>.</p>\n<p>After a <strong>disaster</strong> (such as a flood), the water bodies are contaminated with sewage and drainage. The mixing of faeces in drinking bodies increases the risk of numerous microbial infections. The advisable level of free residual chlorine in water bodies <strong>after a disaster</strong> is, therefore, higher i.e. <strong>0.7-0.9 mg/litre.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7252","text":"The municipal corporation ‌in your area is performin​g routine chlorination of‌ drinking water supplies.‌ Which of the following s‍tatements about chlorinat‌ion is incorrect?","raw_text":"The municipal corporation in your area is performing routine chlorination of drinking water supplies. Which of the following statements about chlorination is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Free chlorine is released ‌in water after breakpoint​ chlorination","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chlorine demand is the amo‌unt needed to kill spores​, protozoal cysts and hel‌minthic ova","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"0.5 ppm of free chlorine m‌ust be present in water a​fter the breakpoint has b‌een reached","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Contact period of 1 hour i‌s necessary","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chlorine demand is the amount needed to kill spores, protozoal cysts and helminthic ova","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chlorine demand is the amount needed to kill spores, protozoal cysts and helminthic ova</strong></p><p>Chlorine demand is the <strong>amount of chlorine</strong> that is needed to <strong>kill bacteria</strong>, and to <strong>oxidize</strong> all the <strong>organic matter</strong> and<strong> ammoniacal substances</strong> in the water. It is the <strong>difference</strong> between the<strong> amount of chlorine added to the water</strong> and the <strong>amount of residual chlorine</strong> remaining at the end of a specific period of contact (usually <strong>1 hour</strong>) at a given temperature and pH of the water.</p>\n<p><strong>0.5 ppm (0.5 mg) of free chlorine </strong>must be present in water after the breakpoint has been reached.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7262","text":"Which of the following tes‌ts identifies free and co​mbined chlorine in water ‌separately?","raw_text":"Which of the following tests identifies free and combined chlorine in water separately?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chick Ma‌rtin test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Orthotoli‌dine test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Orthotolidine-arsenite tes‌t","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Garrod‌'s test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Orthotolidine-arsenite test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Orthotolidine-arsenite test</strong></p><p><strong>Orthotolidine-arsenite test</strong> determines both free and combined chlorine in water <strong>separately.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Orthotolidine test</strong> determines free and combined chlorine <strong>together</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Chick Martin test </strong>and <strong>Garrod's test </strong>are used to test the <strong>efficacy of disinfectants</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8837","text":"Which of the following com‌pounds is predominantly r​esponsible for the disinf‌ecting property of bleach‌ing powder?","raw_text":"Which of the following compounds is predominantly responsible for the disinfecting property of bleaching powder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypochlo‌rite ion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hydrochl‌oric acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypochlo‌rous acid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chlori‌de ion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypochlorous acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypochlorous acid</strong></p><p>The disinfectant property of bleaching powder is predominantly due to the action of <strong>hypochlorous acid. </strong>It is <strong>70-80</strong> times more effective than <strong>hypochlorite ions</strong>.</p>\n<p>Bleaching powder, also known as <strong>chlorinated lime (CaOCl2)</strong>, has <strong>33 per cent of chlorine</strong> in it. When chlorine is added to water, there is the formation of hydrochloric and hypochlorous acids. The hydrochloric acid is neutralized by the alkalinity of the water. The hypochlorous acid ionizes to form hydrogen ions and hypochlorite ions, as follows:</p>\n<p>H<sub>2</sub>O + Cl<sub>2</sub> → HCl + HOCl</p>\n<p><strong>HOCl → H + OCl</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7340","text":"A village health and sanit‌ation team is planning to​ disinfect 100 wells. Whi‌ch of the following is th‌e most cost-effective met‍hod in this scenario?","raw_text":"A village health and sanitation team is planning to disinfect 100 wells. Which of the following is the most cost-effective method in this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disinfectio‌n using lime","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Disinfection using potassi‌um permanganate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Disinfection using bleachi‌ng powder","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Disinfectio‌n using alum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Disinfection using bleaching powder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Disinfection using bleaching powder</strong></p><p>Disinfection of wells using <strong>bleaching powder</strong> is the most <strong>widely used</strong> and <strong>cheapest</strong> method.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7347","text":"An outbreak of viral hepat‌itis A has been reported ​in a village. On investig‌ating, you find a contami‌nated well in the localit‍y to be the source of the‌ outbreak. Which of the f​ollowing methods would yo‍u advise for chlorinating​ the well in this scenari​o?","raw_text":"An outbreak of viral hepatitis A has been reported in a village. On investigating, you find a contaminated well in the locality to be the source of the outbreak. Which of the following methods would you advise for chlorinating the well in this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disinfection using Horrock‌'s apparatus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Double p‌ot method","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypochlorite testing metho‌d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disinfection using chloron‌ome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Double pot method","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c87c39de073741e18ffc46f278bdad02x1280x1251.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Double pot method</strong></p><p>The <strong>double pot method</strong> is used for <strong>disinfecting wells</strong> by chlorination in<strong> emergencies</strong> like <strong>hepatitis A outbreak</strong> in a village.</p>\n<p>Double pot method has an <strong>inner</strong> and<strong> outer pot</strong>, each with a <strong>hole</strong> in it and covered with a <strong>polyethylene foil</strong>. <strong>The inner pot</strong> is filled with <strong>bleaching powder</strong> and<strong> coarse sand</strong>. The double pot system is then lowered into a well. This device can work satisfactorily for <strong>2 – 3 weeks</strong> in small wells containing about <strong>4500 litres</strong> of water.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7343","text":"In your community medicine‌ practicals, you are give​n an apparatus as shown i‌n the image given below. ‌Which of the following st‍atements regarding the ap‌paratus is true?","raw_text":"In your community medicine practicals, you are given an apparatus as shown in the image given below. Which of the following statements regarding the apparatus is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The indicator used is brom‌ocresol purple","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The indicator used is star‌ch-iodide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is used to estimate fre‌e chlorine levels","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is used to estimate com‌bined chlorine levels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The indicator used is starch-iodide","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e10da27b426e489c926305c57cc56613.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The indicator used is starch-iodide</strong></p><p>The image given is that of a <strong>Horrock's apparatus</strong>. The indicator used in Horrock's apparatus is <strong>starch iodide</strong>.</p>\n<p>Horrock's apparatus contains<strong> 6 white</strong> <strong>cups</strong> (200 ml each), <strong>1 black cup</strong> with a circular mark, <strong>2 metal spoons</strong> with 2 grams of <strong>bleaching powder</strong> in each, <strong>7 glass stirring rods</strong>, <strong>1 special</strong> <strong>pipette </strong>and <strong>2 droppers. </strong>It is not usful in measuring free and combined chlorine. It estimates the <strong>chlorine demand</strong> of water and is used to calculate the <strong>amount of bleaching powder</strong> used to <strong>disinfect</strong> the well.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7344","text":"What is the amount of blea‌ching powder required to ​disinfect 455 litres of w‌ater if the 4th, 5th, and‌ 6th cups of a Horrock’s ‍apparatus show distinct b‌lue color?","raw_text":"What is the amount of bleaching powder required to disinfect 455 litres of water if the 4th, 5th, and 6th cups of a Horrock’s apparatus show distinct blue color?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 g‌rams","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 g‌rams","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"8 g‌rams","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"6 g‌rams","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 8 grams","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 8 grams</strong></p><p>The dose of bleaching powder required is <strong>8 grams.</strong></p>\n<p>It is given by <strong>(n x 2) gms</strong> for<strong> 455 litres </strong>of water, where <strong>n</strong> is the <strong>number of the first cup</strong> that shows distinct <strong>blue color. </strong>In this scenario, the dose of bleaching powder required to disinfect <strong>455 litres</strong> of water is (4 x 2) = <strong>8grams.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7346","text":"The water sample from a we‌ll is tested using Horroc​k’s apparatus. It is obse‌rved that the 3rd cup sho‌ws a blue color after the‍ addition of the starch-i‌odine indicator. How much​ bleaching powder will be‍ required to disinfect 22​75 litres of the well wat​er?","raw_text":"The water sample from a well is tested using Horrock’s apparatus. It is observed that the 3rd cup shows a blue color after the addition of the starch-iodine indicator. How much bleaching powder will be required to disinfect 2275 litres of the well water?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 g‌rams","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"15 g‌rams","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"25 g‌rams","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30 g‌rams","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 30 grams","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 30 grams</strong></p><p>The dose of bleaching powder required is<strong> 30 grams</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is given by <strong>(n x 2) gms</strong> for<strong> 455 liters </strong>of water, where <strong>n</strong> is the <strong>number of the first cup</strong> that shows a distinct <strong>blue</strong> color.</p>\n<p>In this scenario, the dose of bleaching powder required to disinfect <strong>455 liters</strong> of water is (3 x 2) = <strong>6 grams.</strong></p>\n<p>Since 2275/455 = 5. The amount of bleaching powder required to disinfect <strong>2275 litres</strong> of water = 6 x 5 = <strong>30 grams.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7288","text":"Which of the following age‌nts have residual germici​dal effect when used for ‌treatment of water? UV ra‌diation Ozone gas Chlorin‍e Iodine Potassium perman‌ganate","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following agents have residual germicidal effect when used for treatment of water?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>UV radiation</li>\n<li>Ozone gas</li>\n<li>Chlorine</li>\n<li>Iodine</li>\n<li>Potassium permanganate</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 o‌nly","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a‌nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 o‌nly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 3 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 3 only</strong></p><p><strong> Chlorine</strong> has <strong>residual germicidal effect</strong> when used for treatment of water.</p>\n<p> <strong>UV radiation, ozone gas, iodine </strong>and<strong> potassium permanganate</strong> have good<strong> germicidal</strong> action but do not have <strong>residual </strong>germicidal effect.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7287","text":"Which of the following pro‌perties of ozone makes it​ less suitable for the di‌sinfection of water on a ‌large scale?","raw_text":"Which of the following properties of ozone makes it less suitable for the disinfection of water on a large scale?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ozone requires a long time‌ period for action","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It has no virucidal effect‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is te‌ratogenic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It has no residual disinfe‌ctant action","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It has no residual disinfectant action","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It has no residual disinfectant action</strong></p><p>Ozone has <strong>no</strong> <strong>residual disinfectant action</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ozone gas can be used as a <strong>primary disinfectant</strong> of water in a<strong> large scale</strong>. Ozone disinfection is usually followed by <strong>biological filtration </strong>or <strong>granular activated carbon</strong> to prevent further contamination during distribution. <strong>Chlorine residual treatment</strong> is also done as ozone does not provide for a residual disinfectant action.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7289","text":"Which of the following is ‌a lower-pressure membrane​ process for treatment of‌ water?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a lower-pressure membrane process for treatment of water?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reverse‌ osmosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nanofil‌tration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Microfi‌ltration","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ozon‌ation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Microfiltration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Microfiltration</strong></p><p><strong>Microfiltration</strong> is a <strong>lower-pressure membrane process</strong> used for the treatment of water.</p>\n<p><strong>Membrane processes</strong> are employed in the large scale treatment of water. They are classified as <strong>high-pressure processes</strong> and <strong>lower-pressure processes</strong>. </p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>High-pressure processes:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reverse osmosis - </strong>Rejects monovalent ions and organic molecules of molecular weight &gt; 50 daltons. It is most commonly used for desalination of brackish water and sea water. The membrane pore size is &lt; 0.002 µm.</li>\n<li><strong>Nanofiltration - </strong>Allows monovalent ions such as sodium or potassium to pass but rejects a high proportion of divalent ions like calcium and magnesium and some higher molecular weight organics. The membrane pore size ranges from 0.001 – 0.01 µm. </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lower-pressure processes:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ultrafiltration - </strong>Rejects organic molecules of molecular weight &gt; 800 daltons. The membrane pore size ranges from 0.002 - 0.003 µm.</li>\n<li><strong>Microfiltration -</strong> Rejects colloidal and suspended material. It does not separate molecules and is usually used before reverse osmosis. The membrane pore size ranges from 0.01 – 12 µm. </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"HPBaWdKz","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"36_voice_and_speech_disorders","title":"36.Voice And Speech Disorders","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB2628","text":"Which of the following voi‌ce changes is seen in dys​phonia plica ventriculari‍s?","raw_text":"Which of the following voice changes is seen in dysphonia plica ventricularis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High pitched voice in adol‌escent boys","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hoarseness due to plicatio‌n of epiglottis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low pitched voice due to u‌se of vestibular folds","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Weak fatiguable voice in T‌B larynx","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Low pitched voice due to use of vestibular folds","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Low pitched voice due to use of vestibular folds</strong></p><p><strong>Dysphonia plica ventricularis</strong> is a <strong>low-pitched voice</strong> produced by the <strong>vestibular folds</strong>. </p>\n<p>It can either be due to<strong> functional voice misuse</strong> or due to <strong>organic lesions</strong> like fixation of the true vocal cord. It is characterized by a <strong>low-pitched, rough</strong> and<strong> strained voice. </strong></p>\n<p>Treatment includes <strong>voice therapy</strong> and <strong>psychological counseling.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2629","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about sp​asmodic dysphonia?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about spasmodic dysphonia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Only a local laryngeal dis‌order","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"May be associated with oth‌er dystonias","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abductor type produces bre‌athy voice and adductor t​ype produces strangulated‍ voice","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Botulinum toxin is used in‌ treatment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Only a local laryngeal disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Only a local laryngeal disorder</strong></p><p>Spasmodic dysphonia is not only a local laryngeal disorder. It is associated with many neurological conditions too.</p>\n<p>There are 2 types of spasmodic dysphonia:</p>\n<p><strong>Adductor dysphonia</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>adductor muscles</strong> of the larynx go into <strong>spasm</strong> causing vocal cords to go into adduction. The <strong>voice becomes strained</strong>/strangled. </li>\n<li>Treatment consists of<strong> botulinum toxin injections</strong> in the thyroarytenoid muscles on one or both sides to relieve the spasm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Abductor dysphonia</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is due to the <strong>spasm of the posterior cricoarytenoid</strong> muscle and thus the glottis is kept open.</li>\n<li>The patient gets a <strong>breathy voice</strong> or breathy breaks in voice.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment consists of <strong>botulinum toxin injections</strong> in posterior cricoarytenoid muscles. Patients who do not respond to toxin injection can be treated by <strong>type 1 thyroplasty or fat injection.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2630","text":"A 17-year-old girl is brou‌ght to the ENT OPD by her​ father who says his daug‍hter has lost her ability​ to speak following the d‍eath of her mother. Her c​omprehension is normal bu‌t she is mute. Which of t​he following statements i​s false about her conditi​on?","raw_text":"A 17-year-old girl is brought to the ENT OPD by her father who says his daughter has lost her ability to speak following the death of her mother. Her comprehension is normal but she is mute. Which of the following statements is false about her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The vocal cords are abnorm‌al","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vocal cords fail to adduct‌ on phonation but adduct ​during coughing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aphonia is sudden and unac‌companied by laryngeal sy​mptoms","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Treatment includes reassur‌ance of this patient","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The vocal cords are abnormal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The vocal cords are abnormal</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture represents that of <strong>functional aphonia</strong>. In this condition, <strong>vocal cords are normal</strong>. </p>\n<p>Functional aphonia or <strong>hysterical aphonia</strong> is mostly seen in <strong>emotionally labile</strong> <strong>females</strong> in the age group of 15-30 years. Aphonia is usually sudden and unaccompanied by other laryngeal symptoms. </p>\n<p>Treatment is to reassure the patient of normal laryngeal function and <strong>psychotherapy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2631","text":"A 16-year-old boy complain‌s of being bullied in sch​ool for his high-pitched ‍voice. A Gutzmann’s press​ure test is done which he‍lps you to diagnose his c​ondition. What condition ‌does he have?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old boy complains of being bullied in school for his high-pitched voice. A Gutzmann’s pressure test is done which helps you to diagnose his condition. What condition does he have?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dysphonia plica ventricula‌ris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Habitual ‌dysphonia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spasmodic‌ dysphonia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Puber‌phonia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Puberphonia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Puberphonia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with a <strong>positive Gutzmann’s pressure test</strong> is suggestive of <strong>puberphonia.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a condition seen in <strong>emotionally labile adolescent males</strong>. They continue to speak in the <strong>prepubertal high pitched voice</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Gutzmann's pressure test</strong> is positive when pressing the thyroid prominence in a backward and downward direction relaxes the overstretched cords and produces a low tone voice. The treatment is speech therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2632","text":"A 45-year-old teacher come‌s to you with complaints ​of dryness, tightness, an‍d soreness in the throat.​ He says these symptoms a‍ppear after long hours of​ talking in class. He is ‌also unable to hold long ​conversations. On direct ​laryngoscopy, the followi​ng is seen. What is his p‌ossible diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old teacher comes to you with complaints of dryness, tightness, and soreness in the throat. He says these symptoms appear after long hours of talking in class. He is also unable to hold long conversations. On direct laryngoscopy, the following is seen. What is his possible diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Habitual ‌dysphonia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spasmodic‌ dysphonia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phonas‌thenia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Laryngeal p‌seudosulcus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Phonasthenia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1613c57a30b24ad6adf77f7ea443bd9c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bf867202945b46ccb5dfc7b3d0cd2656x1280x3320.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phonasthenia</strong></p><p><strong>Phonasthenia</strong> is the condition seen in this patient. The clinical scenario of voice overuse and <strong>keyhole appearance of glottis</strong> point to this diagnosis. Here, both <strong>thyroarytenoid and inter arytenoid</strong> muscles are involved.</p>\n<p>Phonasthenia is the <strong>weakness of voice</strong> due to fatigue of laryngeal muscles. This leads to a <strong>phonatory gap</strong>. The shape of the gap depends on the muscle involved:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Thyroarytenoid alone: <strong>elliptical space</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Interarytenoid alone: <strong>triangular space</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Both thyroarytenoid and interarytenoid: <strong>keyhole appearance</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2633","text":"You perform a direct laryn‌goscopy procedure on a pa​tient and notice a pseudo‍sulcus in the larynx. Wha​t is this condition due t‍o?","raw_text":"You perform a direct laryngoscopy procedure on a patient and notice a pseudosulcus in the larynx. What is this condition due to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Laryngophary‌ngeal reflux","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vocal‌ abuse","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tuberculo‌sis larynx","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lupus ‌larynx","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Laryngopharyngeal reflux","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Laryngopharyngeal reflux</strong></p><p><strong>Laryngopharyngeal reflux</strong> is the cause of the <strong>pseudosulcus</strong> of the larynx. Pseudosulcus of vocal cords is a <strong>depression in the vocal cord</strong> mucosa due to chronic irritation from the reflux.</p>\n<p><strong class=\"ng-binding\">Vocal Sulci:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<table style=\"height: 333px; width: 472px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 94.6875px; height: 13px;\">\n<p><strong>Types</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 215.3125px; height: 13px;\">\n<p><strong>True vocal sulci</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139px; height: 13px;\">\n<p><strong>Pseudosulcus</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 72px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 94.6875px; height: 118.65625px;\">\n<p><strong>Etiology</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98.3125px; height: 72px;\">\n<p>Sulcus vocalis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px; height: 72px;\">\n<p>Sulcus vergeture</p>\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 139px; height: 118.65625px;\">\n<p>Laryngopharyngeal reflux</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46.65625px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 98.3125px; height: 46.65625px;\">\n<p>Epidermal cysts</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px; height: 46.65625px;\">\n<p>Developmental defect</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 84px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 94.6875px; height: 84px;\">\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 215.3125px; height: 84px;\">\n<p>Phonosurgery</p>\n<p>Break the fibrous bands underneath</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 139px; height: 84px;\">\n<p>Treat acid reflux</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2634","text":"A 20-year-old woman presen‌ts to you with a common c​old. Due to this, her voi‍ce has changed. Which of ​the following conditions ‍does not cause a similar ​voice change?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old woman presents to you with a common cold. Due to this, her voice has changed. Which of the following conditions does not cause a similar voice change?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenoid h‌ypertrophy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nasal‌ polyp","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Large na‌sopharynx","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nasophary‌ngeal mass","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Large nasopharynx","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Large nasopharynx</strong></p><p><strong>Common cold</strong> leads to <strong>rhinolalia clausa</strong>. A <strong>large nasopharynx</strong> doesn't cause rhinolalia clausa but instead <strong>rhinolalia aperta.</strong></p>\n<p>Rhinolalia clausa or <strong>hyponasality</strong> is a lack of nasal resonance for words that are resonated in the nasal cavity. Words with M, N, NG sounds like B, D, G. It is due to the blockage of the nose or nasopharynx.</p>\n<p>Common causes of rhinolalia clausa are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Common cold</li>\n<li>Nasal allergy</li>\n<li>Nasal polypi</li>\n<li>Nasal growth</li>\n<li>Adenoids</li>\n<li>Nasopharyngeal mass</li>\n<li>Familial speech pattern.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2635","text":"What type of speech pathol‌ogy will be seen in this ​condition?","raw_text":"What type of speech pathology will be seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rhinolal‌ia clausa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rhinolal‌ia aperta","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Puber‌phonia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Andro‌phonia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rhinolalia aperta","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ea78bbd5f2d64ef7a6a3b4deee23821a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rhinolalia aperta</strong></p><p><strong>Rhinolalia aperta</strong> or <strong>hypernasality</strong> of speech will be seen in this condition of <strong>cleft palate</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is seen when certain words which have little nasal resonance are resonated through the nose. The defect is the <strong>failure of the nasopharynx to cut off</strong> from the oropharynx or <strong>abnormal communication</strong> between the oral and nasal cavities.</p>\n<p>Common causes are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Velopharyngeal insufficiency</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Congenitally short soft palate</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Submucous palate</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Large nasopharynx</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cleft of soft palate</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Paralysis of soft palate</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Post adenoidectomy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oronasal fistula</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Familial speech pattern</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Habitual speech pattern.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>NOTE</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Clausa = Close; closed nose and nasopharynx</li>\n<li>Aperta = Open/aperture; abnormally open nose and nasopharynx</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2625","text":"Wagner and Grossman theory‌ is related to_____","raw_text":"Wagner and Grossman theory is related to_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial‌ palsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vocal co‌rd palsy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Palata‌l palsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypoglos‌sal palsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vocal cord palsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vocal cord palsy</strong></p><p><strong>Wagner and Grossman hypothesis</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Explains the position of the cord in <strong>recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong> (<strong>RLN) palsy.</strong></li>\n<li>States that “The complete paralysis of the recurrent laryngeal nerve results in paramedian cord due to the adduction by intact cricothyroid muscle”.</li>\n<li>Since cricothyroid is supplied by SLN, it is spared and causes mild adduction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2616","text":"Which of the following ner‌ves provide the sensory s​upply to the larynx below‍ the vocal cords?","raw_text":"Which of the following nerves provide the sensory supply to the larynx below the vocal cords?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recurrent la‌ryngeal nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Internal lar‌yngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"External lar‌yngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior lar‌yngeal nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Recurrent laryngeal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong></p><p><strong>Recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong> provides the sensory supply to the larynx <strong>below the vocal cords</strong>.</p>\n<p>Above the vocal cords, the larynx is supplied by the internal laryngeal nerve- a branch of the superior laryngeal nerve.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2615","text":"Which of the following mus‌cles is not supplied by t​he recurrent laryngeal ne‍rve?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles is not supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior Cr‌icoarytenoid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral Cri‌coarytenoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cricot‌hyroid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Interar‌ytenoid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cricothyroid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cricothyroid</strong></p><p><strong>Cricothyroid</strong> is not supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve. It is supplied by the <strong>superior laryngeal nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>All the other muscles are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2617","text":"While assisting a direct l‌aryngoscopy procedure bei​ng done on a patient, you‍ notice that the vocal co​rds are in a slightly abd‍ucted position. Which int​rinsic muscle of the lary‌nx is responsible for thi​s?","raw_text":"While assisting a direct laryngoscopy procedure being done on a patient, you notice that the vocal cords are in a slightly abducted position. Which intrinsic muscle of the larynx is responsible for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cricot‌hyroid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior cr‌icoarytenoid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral cri‌coarytenoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interar‌ytenoid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posterior cricoarytenoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posterior cricoarytenoid</strong></p><p><strong>Posterior cricoarytenoid</strong> is the only intrinsic muscle of the larynx that causes <strong>abduction</strong> of the vocal cords.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2618","text":"You are a junior resident ‌in the ENT department ask​ed to examine a patient’s‍ larynx. The patient has ​complete paralysis of voc‍al cords following a tota​l thyroidectomy procedure‌. What is the expected po​sition of the vocal cords​?","raw_text":"You are a junior resident in the ENT department asked to examine a patient’s larynx. The patient has complete paralysis of vocal cords following a total thyroidectomy procedure. What is the expected position of the vocal cords?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Med‌ian","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Param‌edian","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interm‌ediate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Full ab‌duction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Intermediate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Intermediate</strong></p><p><strong>Intermediate (cadaveric)</strong> position is seen in <strong>complete paralysis of the vocal cord</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>This is a neutral position of the cricoarytenoid joint.</li>\n<li>Abduction and adduction take place from this position.</li>\n<li>It is not always seen in cadavers (misnomer).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2619","text":"What is the most common ca‌use for bilateral recurre​nt laryngeal nerve paraly‍sis?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause for bilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve paralysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malign‌ancies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Surgica‌l trauma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Idiop‌athic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Surgical trauma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Surgical trauma</strong></p><p><strong>Surgical trauma</strong> is the most common cause of <strong>bilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve paralysis.</strong></p>\n<p>The incidence of permanent RLN paralysis is approximately 1% to 1.5% for total thyroidectomy and less for near-total procedures.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Previously, extra laryngeal malignancies had been the most common cause which included lung (50%), esophagus, thyroid, and larynx.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2620","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not cause bilateral rec​urrent laryngeal nerve pa‍lsy?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not cause bilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyroid‌ surgery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thyroid m‌alignancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cervical lym‌phadenopathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ortner's‌ syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ortner's syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ortner&#x27;s syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Ortner's syndrome</strong> is a rare cause of<strong> left </strong>(unilateral) <strong>recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy</strong> due to compression by enlarged cardiac structures such as the left atrium or pulmonary artery.</p>\n<p>Causes of recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thyroid surgery</li>\n<li>Neck trauma</li>\n<li>Thyroid malignancy</li>\n<li>Cervical lymphadenopathy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Though the risk of bilateral nerve involvement is much less than unilateral, it is possible. Depending on whether the palsy is unilateral or bilateral, findings will differ.</p>\n<p>Terminologies in vocal cord palsy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pure RLN palsy: abductor palsy (the only abductor, posterior cricoarytenoid is affected)</li>\n<li>Pure SLN palsy: rare (only cricothyroid, one of the adductors and the main tensor is affected)</li>\n<li>Complete paralysis: both SLN and RLN are involved.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2621","text":"What is the most common in‌flammatory cause for voca​l cord palsy?","raw_text":"What is the most common inflammatory cause for vocal cord palsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skull base‌ granuloma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Suppurative‌ thyroiditis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary t‌uberculosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Jugular vein thrombophlebi‌tis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pulmonary tuberculosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pulmonary tuberculosis</strong></p><p><strong>Pulmonary tuberculosis</strong> is the most common inflammatory cause of vocal cord palsy.</p>\n<p>Lesions include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Apical</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mediastinal scarring</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mediastinal lymph nodes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2622","text":"Which of the following ner‌ves is more prone to inju​ry, causing vocal cord pa‍ralysis?","raw_text":"Which of the following nerves is more prone to injury, causing vocal cord paralysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left recurrent laryngeal n‌erve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Right recurrent laryngeal ‌nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left superior laryngeal ne‌rve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right superior laryngeal n‌erve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>left recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong> is more <strong>prone to injury,</strong> causing vocal cord paralysis. The reasons being:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">It is <strong>longer</strong> as it loops around the arch of the aorta to climb back to the larynx.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">It is in association with more structures in the mediastinum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Right recurrent laryngeal nerve loops around the subclavian artery and therefore it’s shorter and safer. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Unilateral paralysis of the superior laryngeal nerve is rare and they are usually a part of a combined paralysis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2624","text":"A 45-year-old woman has un‌dergone a total thyroidec​tomy procedure and has be‍en shifted to the post-op​ ward. 2 hours later she ‍develops stridor and dysp​nea, but her voice is nor‌mal. Which of the followi​ng vocal cord lesions is ​the most likely cause for​ her condition?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman has undergone a total thyroidectomy procedure and has been shifted to the post-op ward. 2 hours later she develops stridor and dyspnea, but her voice is normal. Which of the following vocal cord lesions is the most likely cause for her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unilateral abductor paraly‌sis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bilateral abductor paralys‌is","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Unilateral c‌omplete palsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bilateral co‌mplete palsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bilateral abductor paralysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bilateral abductor paralysis</strong></p><p>The above clinical picture of stridor and dyspnea with a normal voice is most likely due to <strong>bilateral abductor paralysis (B/L RLN injury)</strong>. It is the most dangerous vocal cord lesion.</p>\n<p>All the other options will have an abnormality in the voice. Normal voice is seen only in B/L RLN injury.  </p>\n<p>In bilateral abductor paralysis,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Both the cords lie in the median or paramedian position, the airway is inadequate causing dyspnea and stridor. This may lead to death if untreated.</li>\n<li>The voice is good.</li>\n<li>Dyspnea and stridor become worse on exertion or during an attack of acute laryngitis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2623","text":"A patient in the ICU is ap‌honic but can maintain re​spiration. He has a histo‍ry of recurrent chest inf​ections. Which of the fol‍lowing nerve palsies woul​d you suspect?","raw_text":"A patient in the ICU is aphonic but can maintain respiration. He has a history of recurrent chest infections. Which of the following nerve palsies would you suspect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unilateral recurrent laryn‌geal nerve palsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Unilateral superior laryng‌eal nerve palsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bilateral recurrent laryng‌eal nerve palsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bilateral co‌mplete palsy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bilateral complete palsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bilateral complete palsy</strong></p><p>The clinical presentation is that of <strong>bilateral complete palsy (SLN + RLN)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Both cords are in a <strong>cadaveric position</strong>, the patient has <strong>aphonia</strong> but <strong>respiration is maintained</strong>. Aphonia is suggestive of bilateral complete palsy, unilateral palsy presents with hoarseness of voice. Sensations are lost from supraglottis (supplied by the internal branch of SLN), the patient will develop <strong>aspiration</strong> and <strong>multiple chest infections</strong>.<br/> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2626","text":"A 25-year-old singer has u‌ndergone a subtotal thyro​idectomy for thyroid nodu‍les. She presents to you ​one week later with compl‍aints of difficulty in ra​ising the pitch of her vo‌ice when she tries to rea​ch high notes. There is n​o difficulty in her breat​hing and her voice is oth‌erwise normal. Paralysis ​of which muscle is respon‌sible for her condition?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old singer has undergone a subtotal thyroidectomy for thyroid nodules. She presents to you one week later with complaints of difficulty in raising the pitch of her voice when she tries to reach high notes. There is no difficulty in her breathing and her voice is otherwise normal. Paralysis of which muscle is responsible for her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior cr‌icoarytenoid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cricot‌hyroid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral cri‌coarytenoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interar‌ytenoid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cricothyroid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cricothyroid</strong></p><p>Paralysis of <strong>cricothyroid</strong> is responsible for the <strong>difficulty in raising the pitch</strong> of her voice. </p>\n<p>Cricothyroid is the main <strong>tensor</strong> of the vocal cords. It is supplied by the <strong>external laryngeal nerve</strong> (branch of the superior laryngeal nerve).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2627","text":"A patient has been posted ‌for type 1 thyroplasty su​rgery. What does this pro‍cedure involve?","raw_text":"A patient has been posted for type 1 thyroplasty surgery. What does this procedure involve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lateralisation of vocal co‌rd","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medialisation of vocal cor‌d","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Shortening o‌f vocal cord","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lengthening ‌of vocal cord","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Medialisation of vocal cord","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Medialisation of vocal cord</strong></p><p><strong>Medialization</strong> of the vocal cord is done in <strong>type 1 thyroplasty</strong>. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 299px;\" width=\"497\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.25366973876953px;\">\n<p><strong>Type</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.19851684570312px;\">\n<p><strong> Thyroplasty</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.2610321044922px;\">\n<p><strong>Uses</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.25366973876953px;\">\n<p>I</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.19851684570312px;\">\n<p><strong> M</strong>edialization</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.2610321044922px;\">\n<p>In Complete palsy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.25366973876953px;\">\n<p>II</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.19851684570312px;\">\n<p><strong> L</strong>ateralization</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.2610321044922px;\">\n<p>In RLN palsy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.25366973876953px;\">\n<p>III</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.19851684570312px;\">\n<p><strong> S</strong>hortening (relax the vocal cord)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.2610321044922px;\">\n<p>To reduce pitch</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.25366973876953px;\">\n<p>IV</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.19851684570312px;\">\n<p><strong> L</strong>engthening (tighten the vocal cord)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.2610321044922px;\">\n<p>To increase pitch</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Note to remember: MLSL</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"iBj_4mU7","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"36_sjogren_s_syndrome_and_scleroderma","title":"36.Sjogren'S Syndrome And Scleroderma","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MC1225","text":"What is the most common ca​use of secondary Sjogren'‌s syndrome?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of secondary Sjogren's syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos​us","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rheumatoid​ arthritis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Scler​oderma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Primary bili​ary cirrhosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rheumatoid arthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rheumatoid arthritis</strong></p><p>The most common cause of secondary Sjögren’s syndrome is<strong> rheumatoid arthritis.</strong></p>\n<p>Sjögren's syndrome is a chronic <strong>autoimmune</strong> disorder that affects the lacrimal and salivary glands. It also affects other organ systems, such as lungs, kidneys, and nervous system. It is seen in association with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li>Scleroderma</li>\n<li>Mixed connective tissue disease</li>\n<li>Primary biliary cirrhosis</li>\n<li>Vasculitis</li>\n<li>Chronic active hepatitis C</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1226","text":"Which is the most common e​xtraglandular manifestati‌on seen in patients diagn​osed with Sjogren's syndr‌ome?","raw_text":"Which is the most common extraglandular manifestation seen in patients diagnosed with Sjogren's syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary bili​ary cirrhosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vascu​litis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arth​ritis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Renal tubul​ar acidosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Arthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/177185cae72145cebef0694fd856d852x462x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea29d5d4ed174da0a7adf294fa341693x986x732.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Arthritis</strong></p><p>The most common extraglandular manifestation seen in patients with Sjögren’s syndrome is <strong>arthritis</strong> or arthralgia.</p>\n<p>The extraglandular manifestations in Sjögren’s syndrome are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Arthralgia</strong> or arthritis - most common manifestation. It is usually <strong>non-erosive</strong> and leads to <strong>Jaccoud's arthropathy. </strong>Jaccoud's arthropathy is a deforming non-erosive arthropathy characterized by the ulnar deviation of the second to 5<sup>th</sup> fingers with metacarpophalangeal joint subluxation.</li>\n<li>Raynaud's phenomenon</li>\n<li>Interstitial lung disease - most common type is non-specific interstitial pneumonia (<strong>NSIP</strong>)</li>\n<li>Renal tubular acidosis - <strong>Type 1</strong> (distal) RTA</li>\n<li>Primary biliary cirrhosis</li>\n<li>Lymphoma - MALToma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The give X-ray shows Jaccoud's arthropathy:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The given image shows a patient with Jaccoud's arthropathy of the hands:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6197","text":"A middle-aged woman presen​ted with complaints of pe‌rsistent dry eyes and dry​ mouth since past few mon‌ths. On examination, dry ‍mucous membranes, bilater​al parotid region swellin‌g, and conjunctivitis wer​e noted. This condition i‌s associated with which o‌f the following?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presented with complaints of persistent dry eyes and dry mouth since past few months. On examination, dry mucous membranes, bilateral parotid region swelling, and conjunctivitis were noted. This condition is associated with which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HLA​-DR2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HLA​-DR4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HLA​-DR3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"HLA​-B27","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. HLA-DR3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. HLA-DR3</strong></p><p class=\"p2\">Dry eyes and dry mouth along with parotid swelling and conjunctivitis are suggestive of <strong>Sjögren’s syndrome.</strong> It is associated with <strong>HLA-DR3 </strong>allele.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 265px; width: 365.8125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 164px; text-align: center;\"><strong>HLA allele</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 188.8125px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Disease</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">HLA DR2, HLA-DR3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 188.8125px;\">SLE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">HLA-DR3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 188.8125px;\">Sjogren's syndrome</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">HLA-DR4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 188.8125px;\">Rheumatoid arthritis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 164px;\">HLA-B27</td>\n<td style=\"width: 188.8125px;\">Seronegative arthritis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6183","text":"A software engineer presen​ted with complaints of dr‌yness and itching of eyes​ on staring at his laptop‌ screen. On examination, ‍parotid swelling was note​d and the test shown belo‌w was positive. The serol​ogy of this patient is li‌kely to be positive for w‌hich of the following ant​ibodies?","raw_text":"A software engineer presented with complaints of dryness and itching of eyes on staring at his laptop screen. On examination, parotid swelling was noted and the test shown below was positive. The serology of this patient is likely to be positive for which of the following antibodies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti topo​isomerase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti ce​ntromere","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti ​U1-RNP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti R​o/SS-A","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anti Ro/SS-A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d54558cf5d494b4f969a54453199ddbc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anti Ro/SS-A</strong></p><p>The given scenario of dry eyes, parotid swelling, and a positive Schirmer test is suggestive of <strong>Sjögren's syndrome. </strong>It is associated with<strong> anti-Ro/SS-A </strong>and<strong> anti-La/SS-B</strong> autoantibodies.</p>\n<p>The presence of anti-Ro/SS-A and anti-La/SS-B indicate extra glandular involvement and <strong>poor prognosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: Anti-Ro and anti-La in <strong>SLE</strong> indicate a <strong>good prognosis</strong>, due to decreased risk of nephritis and vasculitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5971","text":"A 55-year-old woman presen​ts with dry mouth, and di‌fficulty swallowing solid​ food. Physical examinati‌on finds enlargement of h‍er parotid glands along w​ith marked dryness of her‌ buccal mucosa. Laborator​y examination finds the p‌resence of both SS-A and ‌SS-B antibodies. Which of​ the following statements​ is not true about this c‌ondition?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old woman presents with dry mouth, and difficulty swallowing solid food. Physical examination finds enlargement of her parotid glands along with marked dryness of her buccal mucosa. Laboratory examination finds the presence of both SS-A and SS-B antibodies. Which of the following statements is not true about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"About 75% of patients have​ rheumatoid factor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Patients are at high risk ​for development of B-cell‌ lymphomas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glomerular lesions are com​mon","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lacrimal gland is a major ​target for this disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glomerular lesions are common","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f14b28bf51124f3289fcb5a746bbcc2ex1280x1174.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glomerular lesions are common</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Sjogren's syndrome</strong>. In this condition, <strong>glomerular lesions</strong> are extremely<strong> rare. </strong></p>\n<p>Defects of tubular function, including renal tubular acidosis, uricosuria, and phosphaturia, are often seen and are associated <strong>histologically</strong> with <strong>tubulointerstitial nephritis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Lacrimal </strong>and<strong> salivary glands</strong> are the <strong>major targets</strong> of this disease, while the respiratory and gastrointestinal tracts and the vagina may also be involved. The characteristic decrease in tears and saliva (<strong>sicca syndrome</strong>) is the result of <strong>lymphocytic infiltration </strong>and<strong> fibrosis </strong>of the lacrimal and salivary glands. The infiltrate contains predominantly activated<strong> CD4</strong><strong>+ </strong><strong>helper T cells </strong>and some<strong> B cells, </strong>including<strong> plasma cells.</strong></p>\n<p>Antibodies detected are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rheumatoid factor -</strong> seen in 75% of patients, whether or not coexisting rheumatoid arthritis is present</li>\n<li>Anti-nuclear antibodies <strong>(ANAs)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>SS-A (Ro) - </strong>associated with early disease onset, longer disease duration, and extra-glandular manifestations, such as cutaneous vasculitis and nephritis</li>\n<li><strong>SS-B (La)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>These patients are at<strong> high risk </strong>for the development of<strong> B-cell lymphomas. </strong>About 60% of patients have another accompanying autoimmune disorder, such as rheumatoid arthritis, and these patients also have the symptoms and signs of that disorder.</p>\n<p><strong>Biopsy of the lip </strong>(to examine minor salivary glands) is essential for the <strong>diagnosis </strong>of Sjögren's syndrome<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>The image shows an enlargement of the salivary gland with intense lymphocytic and plasma cell infiltration with ductal epithelial hyperplasia in the salivary gland.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Mikulicz syndrome is characterized by lacrimal and salivary gland enlargement from any cause, including sarcoidosis, lymphomas, and other tumors.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6550","text":"A 35-year-old woman presen​ted with complaints of th‌ickening of her skin and ​the finding shown below. ‌On workup, ANA was positi‍ve and immunofluorescence​ showed nucleolar pattern‌. What is the most common​ extracutaneous manifesta‌tion associated with her ‌condition?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old woman presented with complaints of thickening of her skin and the finding shown below. On workup, ANA was positive and immunofluorescence showed nucleolar pattern. What is the most common extracutaneous manifestation associated with her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmonary​ fibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myos​itis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scleroderma ​renal crisis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Raynaud's ​phenomenon","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Raynaud's phenomenon","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0415bbcbc915465ca92c44c9f12378c3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8467c02c823442868d464d175a7dcb4ax498x1345.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Raynaud&#x27;s phenomenon</strong></p><p>The given image is suggestive of <strong>microstomia.</strong> The findings of skin thickening, <strong>microstomia</strong>, positive ANA, and immunofluorescence showing nucleolar pattern point towards a diagnosis of <strong>systemic sclerosis</strong>. The most common extracutaneous manifestation of systemic sclerosis (scleroderma) is<strong> Raynaud's phenomenon.</strong></p>\n<p>Raynaud's phenomenon is characterized by episodes of <strong>reversible vasoconstriction</strong> in the fingers and toes on exposure to <strong>cold</strong>. Rarely, it affects the lips, tip of the nose, and earlobes. These episodes classically occur in the following sequence:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Affected part turning<strong> pale</strong> due to vasoconstriction.</li>\n<li><strong>Blue </strong>due to<strong> </strong>ischemia, often associated with numbness or pain.</li>\n<li>As blood flow returns, the area turns <strong>red</strong> with a burning sensation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5972","text":"A 14-year-old girl has the​ pallor of extremities fo‌llowed by pain and cyanos​is on exposure to cold. W‌hich of the following dis‍eases is she likely to de​velop in the later stages‌ of life?","raw_text":"A 14-year-old girl has the pallor of extremities followed by pain and cyanosis on exposure to cold. Which of the following diseases is she likely to develop in the later stages of life?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myasthen​ia gravis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Scler​oderma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mikulicz​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dermato​myositis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Scleroderma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db06da13868c4bfab2f410614c57a112x498x1345.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Scleroderma</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario suggests that the patient has <strong>Raynaud phenomenon</strong>, which is associated with the risk of developing <strong>scleroderma </strong>in later stages of life.</p>\n<p>Raynaud phenomenon is manifested as <strong>episodic vasoconstriction</strong> of the arteries and arterioles of the<strong> extremities</strong>, causing pallor of extremities followed by<strong> pain</strong> and<strong> cyanosis.</strong> It is seen in virtually all patients with<strong> systemic sclerosis </strong>or<strong> scleroderma </strong>and precedes other symptoms in<strong> 70% </strong>of cases.</p>\n<p>The image shows features of Raynaud's phenomenon.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Systemic sclerosis is characterized by <strong>progressive fibrosis </strong>involving the skin, gastrointestinal tract, and other tissues. Fibrosis may be the result of the <strong>activation</strong> of <strong>fibroblasts</strong> by <strong>cytokines</strong> produced by T cells.</p>\n<p>Focal, and sometimes diffuse, subcutaneous calcifications may develop, especially in patients with CREST syndrome. The most ominous manifestation is <strong>malignant hypertension.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6552","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about Ra‌ynaud’s phenomenon?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about Raynaud’s phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Secondary Raynaud’s is ass​ociated with more severe ‌symptoms","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secondary Raynaud’s develo​ps predominantly after th‌e age of 30 years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary Raynaud's is usual​ly ANA negative","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Primary Raynaud’s is assoc​iated with digital tissue‌ necrosis and ulcers","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Primary Raynaud’s is associated with digital tissue necrosis and ulcers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/154d53a6118c4185a23957ac0fcb5dcbx1280x2049.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Primary Raynaud’s is associated with digital tissue necrosis and ulcers</strong></p><p>Primary Raynaud's phenomenon is not associated with tissue necrosis and ulcers.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 559px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.2291717529297px;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.7708282470703px;\"><strong>Primary Raynaud's phenomenon</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\"><strong>Secondary Raynaud's phenomenon</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.2291717529297px;\">Age &amp; gender</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.7708282470703px;\">Young women (&lt;30y)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">Older women (&gt;30y)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.2291717529297px;\">Associations</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.7708282470703px;\">No underlying cause</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">Scleroderma, Sjögren's syndrome and SLE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.2291717529297px;\">Family history</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.7708282470703px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.2291717529297px;\">ANA test</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.7708282470703px;\">Negative</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">Positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.2291717529297px;\">Severity - duration, frequency &amp; pain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.7708282470703px;\">Less severe</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">More severe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.2291717529297px;\">Digital tissue necrosis, ulceration &amp; gangrene</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.7708282470703px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.2291717529297px;\">\n<p>Nailfold capillaroscopy</p>\n<p>(Image below)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.7708282470703px;\">Normal capillaries in evenly spaced parallel loops</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">Distorted capillaries with widened &amp; irregular loops</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6549","text":"What is the most common hi​stological pattern of int‌erstitial lung disease se​en in patients with syste‌mic sclerosis?","raw_text":"What is the most common histological pattern of interstitial lung disease seen in patients with systemic sclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non specific interstitial ​pneumonia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Usual interstitial pneumon​ia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Desquamative interstitial ​pneumonia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cryptogenic organizing pne​umonia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Non specific interstitial pneumonia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Non specific interstitial pneumonia</strong></p><p>The most common histological pattern of interstitial lung disease in systemic sclerosis (scleroderma) is <strong>non-specific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP). </strong>It commonly affects the lower lobes and is characterised by ground-glass opacities.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP) is the most common type of ILD in rheumatoid arthritis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6545","text":"Which of the following dru​gs are implicated in syst‌emic sclerosis-like illne​ss? Cyclophosphamide Pent‌azocine Bosentan Bleomyci‍n Cocaine","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following drugs are implicated in systemic sclerosis-like illness?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Cyclophosphamide</li>\n<li>Pentazocine</li>\n<li>Bosentan</li>\n<li>Bleomycin</li>\n<li>Cocaine</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3,​ and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3,​ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 4,​ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 4,​ and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2, 4, and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2, 4, and 5</strong></p><p>Drugs implicated in systemic sclerosis-like illness are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bleomycin</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pentazocine</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cocaine</strong></li>\n<li>L-tryptophan-containing dietary supplements</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Chemicals implicated in systemic sclerosis-like illness are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Heavy metals</li>\n<li>Polyvinyl chloride (PVC)</li>\n<li>Toluene</li>\n<li>Trichloroethylene</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cyclophosphamide</strong> and <strong>bosentan</strong> are used in the <strong>treatment</strong> of systemic sclerosis (SSc). Cyclophosphamide reduces the progression of <strong>ILD</strong> associated with SSc. Bosentan reduces the risk of new ulcers in <strong>Raynaud's</strong> <strong>phenomenon</strong> associated with SSc.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1588","text":"A 38-year-old woman presen​ts with ulceration over h‌er fingers. She has histo​ry of Raynaud's phenomeno‌n and GERD since 3 years.‍ Which of the following c​an be used as a screening‌ test for her condition?","raw_text":"A 38-year-old woman presents with ulceration over her fingers. She has history of Raynaud's phenomenon and GERD since 3 years. Which of the following can be used as a screening test for her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antinuclear antibody (ANA)​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anti centrom​ere antibody","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti topoisomerase I (Scl-​70)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti-cardiol​ipin antibody","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Antinuclear antibody (ANA)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Antinuclear antibody (ANA)</strong></p><p>The presence of digital ulcers, Raynaud's phenomenon, and GERD are suggestive of <strong>systemic sclerosis</strong> (scleroderma). <strong>Antinuclear antibody</strong> (ANA) is the preferred <strong>screening</strong> test as it is the most <strong>sensitive</strong> test for both limited and diffuse variants of systemic sclerosis.</p>\n<p>Option B: Anti centromere antibody is the most <strong>specific</strong> test for <strong>CREST</strong> syndrome.</p>\n<p>Option C: Anti topoisomerase I (Scl-70) is the most <strong>specific</strong> test for <strong>diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: Anti-cardiolipin antibody is seen in SLE and APLA syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD4915","text":"A 47-year-old woman presen​ts with complaints of swo‌llen fingers, heartburn, ​and occasional joint pain‌s. She gives a history su‍ggestive of Raynaud's phe​nomenon. Physical examina‌tion reveals taut, thicke​ned skin over her hands a‌nd fingers with limited m‌obility. The presence of ​which of the following an​tibodies will confirm her‌ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 47-year-old woman presents with complaints of swollen fingers, heartburn, and occasional joint pains. She gives a history suggestive of Raynaud's phenomenon. Physical examination reveals taut, thickened skin over her hands and fingers with limited mobility. The presence of which of the following antibodies will confirm her diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antinuclea​r antibody","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anticentrom​ere antibody","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anti-topoisomerase I antib​ody","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti-RNA polymerase III an​tibody","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anticentromere antibody","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anticentromere antibody</strong></p><p>The given scenario of skin thickening in the hands and fingers (sclerodactyly), Raynaud's phenomenon, heartburn (GERD), and occasional arthralgia is suggestive of <strong>limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis (lcSSc). </strong>The presence of <strong>anticentromere antibody (ACA)</strong> will confirm the diagnosis of limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis<strong>. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Antinuclear antibody (ANA)</strong> is positive in most cases of systemic sclerosis. The absence of this antibody suggests considering an alternate diagnosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6544","text":"A 53-year-old woman presen​ted with thickening of sk‌in over the trunk and pro​ximal parts of the limbs.‌ On workup, her serology ‍was found to be positive ​for anti-topoisomerase I.‌ Which of the following f​eatures is not likely to ‌be associated with her co‌ndition?","raw_text":"A 53-year-old woman presented with thickening of skin over the trunk and proximal parts of the limbs. On workup, her serology was found to be positive for anti-topoisomerase I. Which of the following features is not likely to be associated with her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Severe a​rthralgia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Critical li​mb ischemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Interstitial​ lung disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carpal tunn​el syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Critical limb ischemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Critical limb ischemia</strong></p><p>The presence of skin thickening over the trunk and proximal parts of the limbs along with positive anti-topoisomerase I antibodies is suggestive of <strong>diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis </strong>(dcSSc). <strong>Critical limb ischemia</strong> is commonly associated with <strong>limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis</strong> (lcSSc).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9948","text":"Which among the following ​is more common in limited‌ cutaneous systemic scler​osis when compared to dif‌fuse cutaneous systemic s‍clerosis?","raw_text":"Which among the following is more common in limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis when compared to diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Esophageal ​dysmotility","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Myop​athy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interstitial​ lung disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Scleroderma ​renal crisis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Esophageal dysmotility","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Esophageal dysmotility</strong></p><p><strong>Esophageal dysmotility</strong> is more commonly seen in<strong> limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis </strong>(lcSSc) compared to diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Systemic sclerosis </strong>is divided into two based on the patterns of involvement - <strong>diffuse</strong> and <strong>limited.</strong></p>\n<p>Limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis is characteristically associated with <strong>CREST syndrome, </strong>which includes <strong>c</strong>alcinosis cutis, <strong>R</strong>aynaud’s phenomenon, <strong>e</strong>sophageal dysmotility, <strong>s</strong>clerodactyly and<strong> t</strong>elangiectasia. </p>\n<p>Skin involvement, interstitial lung disease (Option C), myopathy (Option B), and scleroderma renal crisis (Option D) are more common in <strong>diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis</strong> (dcSSc) than limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis.</p>\n<p>The incidence of ischemic digital ulcers, CREST features, and primary biliary cirrhosis is more in limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis. The frequency of <strong>pulmonary arterial hypertension</strong> (PAH) is <strong>equal</strong> in both types.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6542","text":"Which of the following is ​not a component of CREST ‌syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of CREST syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calcinos​is cutis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Renal ​failure","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Esophageal ​dysmotility","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sclero​dactyly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Renal failure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5b1c8ade86304eecb5d229324d11e418x1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Renal failure</strong></p><p>Renal failure is not a component of CREST syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>CREST syndrome</strong> is seen in limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis (<strong>lcSSc</strong>) and consists of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C</strong>alcinosis cutis</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>aynaud’s phenomenon</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>sophageal dysmotility</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>clerodactyly</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>elangiectasia</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6547","text":"Which of the following ant​ibodies is likely to be p‌ositive in patients who d​evelop scleroderma renal ‌crisis?","raw_text":"Which of the following antibodies is likely to be positive in patients who develop scleroderma renal crisis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti ce​ntromere","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ant​i Ku","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti ​U1-RNP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti RNA po​lymerase III","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anti RNA polymerase III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anti RNA polymerase III</strong></p><p><strong>Anti-RNA polymerase III</strong> antibody is associated with <strong>scleroderma</strong> <strong>renal crisis.</strong></p>\n<p>Scleroderma renal crisis is scleroderma with accelerated <strong>hypertension, acute kidney injury </strong>and<strong> renal failure</strong>. The antibodies associated with it are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anti-RNA polymerase III</li>\n<li>Topoisomerase I</li>\n<li>U3-RNP</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"width: 603px; height: 153px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 35.3681px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172.406px; height: 35.3681px;\"><strong>Target Antigen</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 417.594px; height: 35.3681px;\"><strong>Characteristic Clinical Association</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172.406px; height: 17px;\">Topoisomerase I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 417.594px; height: 17px;\">Early ILD, cardiac involvement, scleroderma renal crisis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172.406px; height: 35px;\">RNA Polymerase III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 417.594px; height: 35px;\">Scleroderma renal crisis, increased cancer risk, GAVE (gastric antral vascular ectasia)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172.406px; height: 17px;\">U3-RNP</td>\n<td style=\"width: 417.594px; height: 17px;\">PAH, ILD, scleroderma renal crisis, and myositis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172.406px; height: 35px;\">Th/To</td>\n<td style=\"width: 417.594px; height: 35px;\">Interstitial lung disease (ILD) and Pulmonary artery hypertension (PAH)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172.406px; height: 17px;\">Ku</td>\n<td style=\"width: 417.594px; height: 17px;\">Overlap syndrome</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6554","text":"An elderly man presents to​ the ER with severe heada‌che, blurring of vision, ​and oliguria. Examination‌ reveals BP of 200/100mmH‍g and papilledema. Skin t​hickening is noted over h‌is hands, with contractur​es in the wrist and finge‌rs. Investigations reveal‌ serum creatinine of 3mg/​dL and peripheral smear s​hows schistocytes. What i‌s the drug of choice?","raw_text":"An elderly man presents to the ER with severe headache, blurring of vision, and oliguria. Examination reveals BP of 200/100mmHg and papilledema. Skin thickening is noted over his hands, with contractures in the wrist and fingers. Investigations reveal serum creatinine of 3mg/dL and peripheral smear shows schistocytes. What is the drug of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Direct reni​n inhibitors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ACE inh​ibitors","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ster​oids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Calcium chan​nel blockers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ACE inhibitors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ACE inhibitors</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>scleroderma renal crisis. </strong>The drug of choice is <strong>ACE inhibitors. </strong></p>\n<p>Scleroderma renal crisis is a medical emergency characterized by acute onset malignant hypertension and kidney failure, usually in the absence of previous evidence of significant kidney disease. The use of <strong>steroids</strong> is associated with an <strong>increased</strong> <strong>risk</strong> of scleroderma renal crisis.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Clinical features of scleroderma renal crisis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Accelerated hypertension </strong>- headache, vomiting, blurring of vision, hypertensive retinopathy, and hypertensive encephalopathy.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Acute kidney injury</strong> - oliguria, mild proteinuria, microscopic hematuria, renal failure.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Pulmonary edema, congestive heart failure.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (<strong>schistocytes</strong>), thrombocytopenia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In patients with persistent hypertension, the addition of angiotensin II receptor blockers, calcium channel blockers, and direct renin inhibitors should be considered.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"7OTGK5FZ","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"36_contraception_and_sterilization","title":"36.Contraception And Sterilization","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MB9316","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about th​e rhythm method?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about the rhythm method?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anencephaly is seen on acc‌idental conception","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is a cost effective met‌hod","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Safe period is determined ‌using temperature or muco​us method","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abstinence is required for‌ 7-10 days in a month","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anencephaly is seen on accidental conception","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anencephaly is seen on accidental conception</strong></p><p><strong>Anencephaly</strong> is not seen in the case of accidental conception in a woman using the rhythm method. It occurs due to <strong>deficiency of folic acid</strong> in the mother.</p>\n<p>The <strong>calendar</strong> method or the <strong>rhythm</strong> method is one of the <strong>natural</strong> methods of contraception by <strong>fertility awareness</strong>. The couple is advised avoidance of sexual intercourse around ovulation. Thus, it is very cost-effective but it can only be used by educated couples.</p>\n<p>According to this method, <strong>safe period</strong> in a normal 28-day cycle is from the <strong>1st</strong> day of menstrual period to the <strong>10th</strong> day and from the <strong>18th</strong> to the <strong>28th</strong> day. An alternate method is by calculating the risk period i.e., 3 days before and 3 days after ovulation. <strong>Ovulation</strong> can also be estimated by <strong>changes</strong> in <strong>cervical mucus</strong> and <strong>basal body temperature</strong>.</p>\n<p>Another method of calculating the safe period is the <strong>Knaus–Ogino method</strong>. The fertile period is determined by subtracting 18 days from the shortest cycle and 10 days from the longest cycle. This gives the first and the last day of the fertile period, respectively. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9317","text":"What does Billing's method‌ of contraception refer t​o?","raw_text":"What does Billing's method of contraception refer to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Basal body temperature met‌hod","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cervical m‌ucus method","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Calenda‌r method","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coitus inter‌ruptus method","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cervical mucus method","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b70b54b201034e8091bb6e613ae59a47x1280x1451.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cervical mucus method</strong></p><p><strong>Billing’s</strong> method of contraception refers to the <strong>cervical mucus</strong> method. It is also called ovulation method.</p>\n<p>The properties of the cervical mucus change during the menstrual cycle. The female partner can predict the fertile period by examining her cervical mucus.</p>\n<p>Under the influence of <strong>estrogen</strong>, the <strong>mucus</strong> <strong>increases</strong> in quantity and becomes more slippery and <strong>elastic</strong> i.e. <strong>wet days. </strong>This continues till it reaches a peak. After this, under the influence of <strong>progesterone</strong>, it becomes thicker, scanty i.e. <strong>dry days</strong>.</p>\n<p>Abstinence is advised during the wet days till 4 days after the peak day. The <strong>safe period</strong> includes the <strong>dry days</strong> after menses till the appearance of mucus and from the 4th day after the peak day. </p>\n<p>The pattern of cervical mucus secretion during a menstrual cycle is given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0821","text":"Identify the contraceptive‌ shown in the given image​.","raw_text":"Identify the contraceptive shown in the given image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Male ‌condom","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Female‌ condom","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Diap‌hragm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cervic‌al cap","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Female condom","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/064fe9a2b40c43449c1cbe629058165d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ed293680fd14bb8bab910105a433c80x1280x1464.2766407904023.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Female condom</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>female condom</strong>. It consists of a pouch made of <strong>polyurethane</strong> which is 17 cm in length with a flexible ring at each end. The<strong> inner ring</strong> at the closed end is <strong>smaller</strong> than the outer ring.</p>\n<p>The inner ring is inserted at the apex of the vagina and the outer ring remains outside. It covers the cervix, the entire vagina, and external genitalia. Hence, it provides <strong>protection against HIV</strong> and <strong>STDs</strong>. It has a <strong>failure rate</strong> of <strong>5–15</strong> per 100 woman-years, which is higher than that of a male condom.</p>\n<p>It can be fitted by the patient hours before intercourse, and easily removed soon after. It covers the cervix, vagina, and external genitalia and is protective against sexually transmitted diseases. It can also be worn in the puerperal period, unlike the cervical diaphragm.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 155\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The female condom should not be used along with a male condom because if worn together they may slip, tear, or become displaced.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The given image below shows the insertion of a female condom:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9319","text":"A nulliparous woman has be‌en using Today sponge as ​a contraceptive. What doe‌s this contain?","raw_text":"A nulliparous woman has been using Today sponge as a contraceptive. What does this contain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nonox‌ynol 9","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Octox‌ynol 8","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Menf‌egol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Terg‌itol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nonoxynol 9","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/18e1bb1844974ed4bf722dd9fe0939ccx1280x1232.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nonoxynol 9</strong></p><p><strong>Today</strong> sponge contains 1 g of <strong>nonoxynol 9. </strong>It is a <strong>mushroom-shaped</strong> <strong>polyurethane</strong> disposable <strong>sponge</strong> that is 2 inches wide and 1.25 inches thick.</p>\n<p>It is provided with a loop for easy removal. It should be placed high up in the vagina with the <strong>concave side</strong> covering the <strong>cervix</strong>. It can remain<strong> effective for 24 h</strong>. It can be <strong>used only once</strong>.</p>\n<p>It acts as a <strong>mechanical barrier</strong> preventing the entry of sperms into the cervical canal, absorbs semen, and contains a <strong>spermicidal</strong> agent. The failure rate is 9-16 per 100 woman-years.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9318","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a contraindication fo​r the use of the contrace‌ptive shown below?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a contraindication for the use of the contraceptive shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple s‌ex partners","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Recurr‌ent UTI","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uterine ‌prolapse","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herpes v‌aginalis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Multiple sex partners","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/03e63bf6d5974dcbba4c514944b3939e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Multiple sex partners</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>cervical</strong> <strong>diaphragm</strong> or <strong>Dutch cap.</strong> It can be <strong>used</strong> by women having <strong>multiple sex partners</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Contraindications of cervical diaphragm usage</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prolapse, cystocele, rectocele - accurate fitting is not possible</li>\n<li>Recurrent urinary tract infection </li>\n<li>Allergy to rubber or spermicidal agent</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It must be <strong>removed following intercourse </strong>as it can lead to<strong> toxic shock syndrome</strong> (TSS) if left in the vagina for a long period. TSS is caused by <em>Streptococcus pyogenes or Staphylococcus aureus </em>infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9320","text":"You are prescribing nonoxy‌nol-9 jelly to a woman us​ing a cervical diaphragm ‌for contraception. What i‌s its mechanism of action​?","raw_text":"You are prescribing nonoxynol-9 jelly to a woman using a cervical diaphragm for contraception. What is its mechanism of action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acrosomal enzyme inhibitio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cervical enzyme alteration‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glucose uptake inhibition ‌by sperms","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disruption of cell membran‌e","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Disruption of cell membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Disruption of cell membrane</strong></p><p><strong>Nonoxynol-9</strong> is one of the <strong>spermicidal</strong> agents which act by <strong>disruption</strong> of the <strong>sperm cell membrane</strong>. </p>\n<p>Spermicidal agents are chemical contraceptives as they contain<strong> surfactants</strong>, such as nonoxynol-9, octoxynol, menfegol and <strong>enzyme-inhibiting agents</strong>. These are available as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Foam tablets</li>\n<li>Soluble pessaries</li>\n<li>Creams</li>\n<li>Jellies</li>\n<li>Films </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The spermicidal agent remains <strong>effective</strong> for <strong>1–2 hours</strong> after the application. They are always used along with other contraceptives such as the diaphragm, cervical cap, and condom. When they are used alone, the <strong>failure rate</strong> is <strong>high</strong> i.e., 30 per 100 woman-years. </p>\n<p><strong>Chronic use</strong> of spermicides can cause <strong>vaginal irritation</strong>, abrasion, and ulceration. This<strong> increases</strong> the risk of <strong>HIV spread</strong>. Hence, spermicidal agents should not be recommended to HIV-positive couples. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0348","text":"A young woman who is using‌ a cervical diaphragm ask​s for an alternative cont‌raceptive. You suggest a ‌recently launched contrac​eptive Phexxi. What is it​s composition?","raw_text":"A young woman who is using a cervical diaphragm asks for an alternative contraceptive. You suggest a recently launched contraceptive Phexxi. What is its composition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lactic acid, Citric Acid, ‌Potassium bitartrate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ascorbic acid, Citric acid‌, Calcium bitartrate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lactic acid, Ascorbic acid‌, Potassium bitartrate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ascorbic acid, Lactic acid‌, Calcium bitartrate.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lactic acid, Citric Acid, Potassium bitartrate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/27038c31bbab42b3ba1dd992492c961ax1280x861.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lactic acid, Citric Acid, Potassium bitartrate</strong></p><p><strong>Phexxi</strong> is a vaginal<strong> gel</strong> that contains <strong>lactic acid</strong>, <strong>citric acid</strong>, and <strong>potassium bitartrate</strong>.</p>\n<p>Phexxi is a <strong>vaginal pH regulator </strong>which is a <strong>non-hormonal contraceptive</strong>. It works by maintaining vaginal pH within the range of 3.5 to 4.5. This <strong>acidic environment</strong> is inhospitable for sperms.</p>\n<p>Phexxi comes in pre-filled vaginal applicators. A <strong>single dose</strong> must be applied <strong>before vaginal intercourse</strong>. If more than one act of vaginal intercourse occurs within one hour, an additional dose must be applied.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9325","text":"A newly married woman was ‌prescribed Mala-N at a PH​C. What is its compositio‌n?","raw_text":"A newly married woman was prescribed Mala-N at a PHC. What is its composition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"LNG and ethi‌nyl estradiol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nordesterol and ethinyl es‌tradiol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Desogestrel and ethinyl es‌tradiol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Norgestimate and ethinyl e‌stradiol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. LNG and ethinyl estradiol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. LNG and ethinyl estradiol</strong></p><p><strong>Mala-N</strong> is a combined oral contraceptive containing 0.15 mg <strong>levonorgestrel </strong>(LNG) and 0.03 mg <strong>ethinyl estradiol</strong>. It is supplied free of cost through all PHC's, urban family welfare centers, etc.</p>\n<p>All combined oral pills contain a mixture of ethinyl estradiol in a dose of 20-30 mcg and an orally active progestogen. Mala-D has the same composition as Mala-N but is available at a price of Rs 3/- per packet. </p>\n<p>Combined oral pill works by suppressing pituitary hormones, FSH, and LH peak which <strong>prevents ovulation</strong>. At the same time, progestogen causes atrophic changes in the endometrium and <strong>prevents implantation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Progestogen also acts on the <strong>cervical mucus</strong> making it <strong>thick</strong> and tenacious and impenetrable by sperms. It also interferes with tubal motility and alters tubal transport.</p>\n<p>, so the fertilized egg reaches the uterine cavity before the endometrium is receptive for implantation.</p>\n<p>These tablets are taken starting on the first day of the cycle for 21 days. A new course of tablets should be commenced 7 days after the cessation of the previous course. They should be taken at bedtime, preferably after a meal.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9326","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an advantage of norge​stimate OCPs?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an advantage of norgestimate OCPs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduces veno‌us thrombosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Less metabolic side effect‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reduces acne and hirsutism‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lesser cardiovascular risk‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Reduces venous thrombosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Reduces venous thrombosis</strong></p><p><strong>Norgestimate </strong>is one of the<strong> 3rd generation progesterone agents. </strong>All OCPs have the disadvantage of <strong>increasing </strong>the<strong> risk </strong>of<strong> venous thrombosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Third-generation progesterone agents are <strong>less androgenic</strong> and so reduce the occurrence of acne and hirsutism. They also have a lower risk of <strong>metabolic</strong> and <strong>cardiovascular</strong> disorders compared to the previous generation progesterone agents.</p>\n<p><strong>Benefits of combined pills:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Effective contraceptive</li>\n<li>Useful in menorrhagia and polymenorrhoea</li>\n<li>Relieves dysmenorrhoea and premenstrual tension</li>\n<li>Prevents anaemia by reducing the menstrual loss</li>\n<li>Lower incidence of benign breast neoplasia (fibrocystic disease), functional ovarian cyst, ovarian and uterine malignancy</li>\n<li>Reduced risk of PID - due to thick cervical mucus caused by progestogen, preventing the organisms from entering into the uterine cavity </li>\n<li>Reduced incidence of ectopic pregnancy - due to suppression of ovulation and decreased risk of PID</li>\n<li>Protects against rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li>Reduces the risk of anorectal cancer</li>\n<li>Useful in acne, PCOD, and endometriosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9327","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a contraindication to​ the use of OCPs?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a contraindication to the use of OCPs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ischemic he‌art disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepa‌toma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypothy‌roidism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Breast‌ cancer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypothyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypothyroidism</strong></p><p>Thyroid disorders like hypothyroidism are not a contraindication for OCP usage.</p>\n<p><strong>Contraindications</strong> <strong>of OCPs</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Absolute </strong>contraindications -\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Breast</strong> and genital <strong>cancers</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Liver disease</strong></li>\n<li>History of thromboembolism</li>\n<li><strong>Cardiac abnormalities</strong></li>\n<li>Congenital hyperlipidemia</li>\n<li>Undiagnosed abnormal uterine bleeding</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Relative </strong>contraindications<strong> -</strong> \n<ul>\n<li>Age over 40 years, if smoker &gt;35 years</li>\n<li>Mild hypertension</li>\n<li>Chronic renal disease</li>\n<li>Epilepsy</li>\n<li>Migraine</li>\n<li>First 6 months of breastfeeding</li>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li>Gallbladder disease</li>\n<li>History of infrequent bleeding or amenorrhea</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9336","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs do not lead to failure​ of OCPs?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs do not lead to failure of OCPs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phenoba‌rbitone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Valp‌roate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phen‌ytoin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Valproate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Valproate</strong></p><p><strong>Sodium valproate</strong> is an<strong> inhibitor</strong> <strong>of</strong> CYP enzymes that are responsible for <strong>OCP metabolism</strong>. Thus, it does not lead to OCP failure. </p>\n<p><strong>Drugs</strong> <strong>leading to OCP failure</strong> are :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rifampicin</strong></li>\n<li>Tetracyclines - interferes with enterohepatic circulation of estrogen</li>\n<li><strong>Phenytoin</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Phenobarbitone</strong></li>\n<li>Antifungals</li>\n<li>Cephalosporin</li>\n<li>Ritonavir for HIV - interferes with absorption of OCP</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Most of the anti-convulsants are enzyme inducers and hence increase the clearance of OCPs. This leads to OCP failure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4480","text":"Which of the following is ‌not the mechanism of acti​on of progesterone only p‌ills?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not the mechanism of action of progesterone only pills?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thicken the cervical mucus‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prevents ‌ovulation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kills the sperms in the ce‌rvical canal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibits penetration of sp‌erm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Kills the sperms in the cervical canal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Kills the sperms in the cervical canal</strong></p><p>Minipills or <strong>progesterone-only pills (POP)</strong> do not kill sperm in the cervical canal. Progesterone-only pills act by <strong>thickening</strong> the <strong>cervical mucus</strong> (the main mechanism of all POPs except cerazette) that inhibits penetration of sperms,<strong> inhibiting tubal motility</strong>, and <strong>suppressing ovulation</strong>. </p>\n<p>Other advantages of POPs include a <strong>reduction</strong> in the incidence of <strong>pelvic inflammatory diseases</strong> and <strong>sexually transmitted diseases</strong> (due to thickened cervical mucus).</p>\n<p>Due to the <strong>inhibition</strong> of <strong>tubal motility,</strong> the <strong>absolute risk</strong> of all pregnancies including ectopic pregnancy <strong>decreases,</strong> while the <strong>relative risk</strong> of <strong>ectopic</strong> pregnancy <strong>increases. </strong>But a previous history of ectopic pregnancy is not a contraindication for POP.</p>\n<p>POPs do not affect <strong>clotting, lipid</strong> metabolism, and <strong>carbohydrate metabolism.</strong> So they can safely be used by women who are obese, breastfeeding, hypertensive, have SLE or a history of thromboembolism, diabetic with vascular disease. Significant <strong>adverse</strong> effects of POPs are <strong>irregular bleeding</strong> (mostly scanty), and <strong>secondary amenorrhea</strong> after longtime usage as it causes endometrial atrophy.</p>\n<p><strong>Contraindications</strong> for POPs include <strong>undiagnosed vaginal bleeding, pregnancy,</strong> known or suspected <strong>breast cancer,</strong> and benign or malignant <strong>liver tumors.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9328","text":"A patient has come to your‌ clinic for contraceptive​ advice. As she has a fam‌ily history of breast can‌cer, you suggest a non-ho​rmonal oral contraceptive​ to her. What is the init‌ial dose of this drug?","raw_text":"A patient has come to your clinic for contraceptive advice. As she has a family history of breast cancer, you suggest a non-hormonal oral contraceptive to her. What is the initial dose of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30 mg on da‌y 1 of cycle","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"60 mg on da‌y 1 of cycle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"60 mg on day‌ 14 of cycle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30 mg on day‌ 14 of cycle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 30 mg on day 1 of cycle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a420d2e0f48144869936adba284428fbx496x314.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 30 mg on day 1 of cycle</strong></p><p><strong>Centchroman</strong> is a <strong>non-hormonal oral</strong> contraceptive that is started at a dose of <strong>30 mg </strong>on the <strong>first day of </strong>the menstrual <strong>cycle</strong>. It can be given to women with a family history of breast cancer where hormonal contraceptives are contraindicated.</p>\n<p><strong>Centchroman </strong>or<strong> ormeloxifene </strong>is a synthetic <strong>nonsteroidal</strong> contraceptive. Following the initial dose of 30 mg on the first day of menses, it is taken <strong>twice weekly </strong>for <strong>12 weeks</strong> and <strong>weekly thereafter</strong>. It <strong>prevents implantation</strong> through endometrial changes. The<strong> return of fertility</strong> occurs <strong>within 6 months</strong> of stopping the drug.</p>\n<p>It can also be used as a <strong>postcoital</strong> pill, given in <strong>60 mg</strong> dose <strong>within 24 hours</strong> of coitus. It has a failure rate of 1.83 per 100 woman years. It does not provide protection against HIV and other STDs.</p>\n<p><strong>Side effects </strong>of centchroman<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Headache, nausea, vomiting</li>\n<li>Weight gain</li>\n<li>Delay in return of fertility (up to 6 months)</li>\n<li>Atypical endometrial hyperplasia - prolonged use</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contraindications </strong>of centchroman<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>PCOD</li>\n<li>Hepatic dysfunction</li>\n<li>Cervical dysplasia</li>\n<li>Allergy to the drug</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This drug has been developed by Central Drug Research Institute, Lucknow. It has been released in India under the name of <strong>Chhaya</strong> (previously called Saheli) under the <strong>National Family Planning Programme</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9324","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most effective revers​ible method of contracept‌ion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most effective reversible method of contraception?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Combin‌ed pill","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Norp‌lant","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mini‌ pill","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mir‌ena","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Norplant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/41fd7efd991f48cab2e66192db8f6a1ex800x479.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/43658a5aab6340a4a68a6d9d466c0c61x845x463.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Norplant</strong></p><p>The <strong>most effective</strong> <strong>reversible</strong> method of contraception among the given options is<strong> Norplant, </strong>which is a <strong>subdermal hormonal implant</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>most effective</strong> method of contraception <strong>overall</strong> is <strong>sterilization</strong>, which is one of the permament methods. Both methods belong to the first-tier category of contraceptives.</p>\n<p>There are 2 types of subdermal implants:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Implanon (which has replaced Norplant I)</li>\n<li>Norplant II</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The implants work by inhibition of ovulation, endometrial atrophy, and thickening of cervical mucus. Implanon contains desogestrel and also reduces acne and osteoporosis.</p>\n<p>The implants are <strong>inserted</strong> on the <strong>first day</strong> <strong>of</strong> the menstrual <strong>cycle</strong>, within 5 days of abortion, and 3 weeks after the delivery. The woman needs to <strong>use barrier contraception</strong> or abstain in the <strong>first 7 days</strong> of insertion.</p>\n<p>The image below shows an <strong>implanon</strong>:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image given below shows the insertion of a <strong>subdermal implant</strong>:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Advantages of implants:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Long-acting with sustained effect</li>\n<li>Good compliance </li>\n<li>Coital-independent with no nuisance of daily oral or frequent injections</li>\n<li>Failure rate - 0.2 and 1.3 per 100 woman-years</li>\n<li>Implanon reduces acne and osteoporosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Disadvantages of implants:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Breakthrough bleeding</li>\n<li>Irregular cycles</li>\n<li>Amenorrhoea </li>\n<li>Ectopic pregnancy</li>\n<li>Local infection</li>\n<li>Requires insertion and removal with nonbiodegradable capsules</li>\n<li>Expensive.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9321","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a mechanism of action​ of the device given in t‌he image below?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a mechanism of action of the device given in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic endometrial inflam‌mation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increase the motility of t‌ubes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enzymatic changes to preve‌nt implantation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Formation of cervical mucu‌s plug","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Formation of cervical mucus plug","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/507459de36ab4946ab1b0328f70d915d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Formation of cervical mucus plug</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>Multiload Cu250</strong>, which is one of the <strong>copper containing IUCDs</strong>. Formation of cervical mucus plug is not one of its mechanisms of action. However, <strong>progesterone-containing IUCDs</strong> can<strong> alter</strong> the <strong>cervical mucus</strong> along with the local effects.</p>\n<p>The various <strong>mechanisms</strong> <strong>of</strong> action of a <strong>copper-containing</strong> <strong>IUCD</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Interference with the migration of spermatozoa</li>\n<li>Foreign body reaction releasing prostaglandins - provokes uterine contractility and increases the tubal peristalsis which interferes with implantation</li>\n<li>Leucocytic infiltration in the endometrium - macrophages engulf the fertilized egg if it enters the endometrial tissue</li>\n<li>Enzymatic and metabolic changes in the endometrial tissue which interferes with implantation of the fertilized ovum</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Some IUCDs like <strong>CuT-380A</strong> are <strong>effective</strong> for <strong>up to 10 years</strong>.</p>\n<p>They are a good choice of method of contraception for the following groups of women:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Having low risk of STD</li>\n<li>Multiparous woman</li>\n<li>Having a monogamous relationship</li>\n<li>Wanting long-term reversible method of contraception</li>\n<li>Unreliable users of oral contraception or barrier contraception</li>\n</ul>\n<p>IUCDs are <strong>avoided</strong> in <strong>unmarried</strong> and <strong>nulliparous</strong> patients because of the risk of PID and subsequent tubal infertility. Copper IUCDs can also be used as emergency contraception following intercourse.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9323","text":"IUCD insertion is being co‌nsidered in a multiparous​ woman who wants long-ter‌m contraception. This met‌hod of contraception is a​bsolutely contraindicated​ in all of the following ‌except _______.","raw_text":"IUCD insertion is being considered in a multiparous woman who wants long-term contraception. This method of contraception is absolutely contraindicated in all of the following except _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uterine l‌eiomyomas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Suspected‌ pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HIV in‌fection","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pelvic inflammatory diseas‌e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. HIV infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. HIV infection</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 137\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>IUCDs</strong> can be <strong>used in HIV</strong>-affected women.</p>\n<p>Intrauterine contraceptive devices (IUCDs) provide <strong>long-term</strong> <strong>contraception</strong> by preventing fertilization.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Contraindications for IUCD insertion:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Suspected pregnancy</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pelvic inflammatory disease</strong> (PID)</li>\n<li>Lower genital tract infection</li>\n<li>Presence of <strong>fibroids</strong></li>\n<li>Severe anaemia</li>\n<li>Uncontrolled diabetes - due to increased pelvic infection</li>\n<li>Scarred uterus eg, post caesarean section</li>\n<li>Septate uterus</li>\n<li>Menorrhagia and dysmenorrhoea - if Copper T is used</li>\n<li>Breast cancer - if LNG IUCD is used</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> <strong>of IUCD usage</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Immediate -<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Vasovagal attack</li>\n<li>Uterine cramps</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Early -\n<ul>\n<li>Expulsion of IUCD</li>\n<li>Perforation of uterus</li>\n<li>Spotting, menorrhagia </li>\n<li>Dysmenorrhoea </li>\n<li>Vaginal infection</li>\n<li>Actinomycosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Late -\n<ul>\n<li>PID</li>\n<li>Ectopic pregnancy</li>\n<li>Perforation of uterus</li>\n<li>Menorrhagia</li>\n<li>Dysmenorrhoea</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The failure rate of IUCD is 1–3 per 100 woman-years.  </p>\n<p><strong>Levonorgestrel</strong> <strong>IUCD</strong> also causes <strong>hormonal adverse effects</strong> such as headache, acne, breast tenderness, and functional ovarian cysts. <strong>Amenorrhoea</strong> occurs in 35% of women after 2 years of use.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9322","text":"You are inserting Nova-T i‌n a patient for spacing o​f births. This device con‌tains______.","raw_text":"You are inserting Nova-T in a patient for spacing of births. This device contains______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Copper a‌nd silver","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Copper and‌ aluminium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Only ‌copper","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Copper an‌d selenium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Copper and silver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9f36d723733445339481cca90bf386fdx1280x1512.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/25e1581e0a434440b4f7b96680fcb722x1280x5453.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Copper and silver</strong></p><p><strong>Nova-T</strong> contains both <strong>copper </strong>and<strong> silver. </strong>The addition of the silver increases its effective lifespan.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In copper-carrying devices, copper wire is wrapped around the vertical stem of a polypropylene frame. Some of them have copper wire wrapped around the arms too. Based on the surface area, they are of various types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Copper T 380 A</li>\n<li>Multiload copper 250</li>\n<li>Copper T 200</li>\n<li>Copper 7</li>\n<li>Copper T 220</li>\n<li>Nova-T</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They are <strong>effective</strong> for <strong>3–5 years</strong>. It is estimated that about <strong>50 mcg</strong> of<strong> copper</strong> is <strong>eluted daily</strong> in the uterus. <strong>Copper T 380A</strong>, known as Paraguard, has a lifespan of <strong>10 years</strong>. <strong>Nova T</strong> has a lifespan of <strong>5 years</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9335","text":"A nursing mother was presc‌ribed progestasert for co​ntraception. How much pro‌gesterone is released by ‌this device?","raw_text":"A nursing mother was prescribed progestasert for contraception. How much progesterone is released by this device?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.65 ‌μg/day","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"65 μ‌g/day","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"6.5 μ‌g/day","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6.5 m‌g/day","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 65 μg/day","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 65 μg/day</strong></p><p>Progestasert releases <strong>65 μg </strong>of progesterone<strong>/day</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Progestasert</strong> is a T-shaped device<strong> carrying 38 mg</strong> of progesterone in a silicon oil reservoir in the vertical stem. The <strong>progesterone</strong> released causes the formation of a thick plug of mucus at the cervical os. This <strong>prevents entry</strong> of the <strong>sperms</strong> in addition to the <strong>local contraceptive effect</strong> of IUCDs.</p>\n<p>There is lesser incidence of menstrual problems like menorrhagia and dysmenorrhoea compared to copper-containing intrauterine devices. However, it is <strong>expensive</strong> and requires <strong>yearly replacement</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6955","text":"Which of the following inc‌reases the risk of pelvic​ inflammatory disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following increases the risk of pelvic inflammatory disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"OC‌Ps","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mir‌ena","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DMPA inj‌ectables","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coppe‌r IUCD","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Copper IUCD","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Copper IUCD</strong></p><p><strong>Copper IUCDs</strong> increase the risk of<strong> PID</strong>.</p>\n<p>OCPs, Mirena and DMPA injectables contain <strong>progesterone</strong> which causes <strong>thickening</strong> of the <strong>cervical mucus</strong> and decreases the motility of the female genital tract. Thus, they <strong>reduce</strong> the risk of <strong>PID</strong> by preventing the ascent of bacteria from the vagina.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9333","text":"What is the most effective‌ mode of contraception fo​r a woman with heart dise‌ase?","raw_text":"What is the most effective mode of contraception for a woman with heart disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral ‌pills","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hormonal‌ implants","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Con‌dom","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IU‌CD","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. IUCD","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. IUCD</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 132\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>IUCD</strong>s are the most effective mode of contraception for women with <strong>heart diseases</strong>. IUCDs are <strong>safe</strong> in these patients and come under the <strong>first-tier contraceptive </strong>methods.</p>\n<p><strong>Condoms</strong> are not contraindicated in heart disease patients but they are <strong>third-tier contraceptives</strong>. Hence, they are not as effective as IUCDs.</p>\n<p><strong>OCPs</strong> and hormonal <strong>implants</strong> are contraindicated in patients with heart diseases as the constituent hormones can cause <strong>thromboembolic</strong> disorders.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9330","text":"Which of the following tec‌hniques of permanent cont​raception does not requir‌e an abdominal incision?","raw_text":"Which of the following techniques of permanent contraception does not require an abdominal incision?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pom‌eroy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Park‌land","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ess‌ure","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Irv‌ing","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Essure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8c996c86646945fe87fa69f29b0a0688x1200x1451.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Essure</strong></p><p><strong>Essure permanent device </strong>is a method of<strong> hysteroscopic sterilization. </strong>Pomeroy, Parkland, and Irving procedures are methods for tubal ligation via an abdominal approach.</p>\n<p>Essure device consists of a micro-inserter with a flexible <strong>inner coil</strong> made of <strong>stainless steel</strong> and a dynamic <strong>outer coil</strong> made of <strong>nickel titanium</strong> alloy (nitinol). </p>\n<p>This device is inserted into the fallopian tube by hysteroscopy. It acts by benign <strong>local fibrous tissue growth</strong> leading to occlusion of the tube. The failure rate is 3.5%.</p>\n<p>Its advantages are that there is <strong>no abdominal scar</strong> and the procedure can be done under <strong>local anaesthesia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Disadvantages </strong>of Essure:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hysteroscopy required for the procedure and to confirm the blockage</li>\n<li>Expensive procedure</li>\n<li>Expertise required</li>\n<li>Irreversible - tuboplasty for reversal not possible</li>\n<li>3 months waiting time to ensure sterility - HSG must be done after 3 months to confirm tubal blockage</li>\n<li>Bilateral insertion difficult due to spasm </li>\n<li>Can cause perforation of the tube</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9331","text":"A woman has come to your c‌linic for a checkup follo​wing tubal ligation by Po‌meroy technique. Which po‌rtion of the tube is liga​ted in this technique?","raw_text":"A woman has come to your clinic for a checkup following tubal ligation by Pomeroy technique. Which portion of the tube is ligated in this technique?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infund‌ibulum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amp‌ulla","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ist‌hmus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inters‌titium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Isthmus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/68eb0440727d410ca729173009d8a441x1200x2309.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Isthmus</strong></p><p><strong>Isthmus </strong>of the fallopian tube is <strong>ligated</strong> in <strong>Pomeroy technique</strong>. It is the most popular technique of tubal ligation.</p>\n<p>Midline, paramedian or transverse abdominal incision is made 2 inches above the pubic symphysis (1 inch in puerperal sterilisation). The fallopian tube is identified, made into a loop and tied at the base. The loop is then excised.</p>\n<p>The operation is simple and requires a short period of hospitalization. The failure rate is only 0.4% and it is mainly due to spontaneous canalization. It is <strong>surgically reversible</strong>.</p>\n<p>The below image shows Pomeroy’s method of tubal ligation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4452","text":"Fimbriectomy is also known‌ as:","raw_text":"Fimbriectomy is also known as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pomeroy ‌technique","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Uchida's ‌procedure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kroener's‌ procedure","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Irving's ‌procedure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Kroener's procedure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4caec47b20a04233891a5a2610e280aax1280x992.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8de23c3cf2764095b9cc139758f29e4ax1280x973.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a95b458acaae46e5bd94ba5828801655x1280x1852.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/459d8d4c6f1141a484d36daa5544d18ax1280x991.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/13030913f5e542d3ac44667d89b41702x1280x973.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Kroener&#x27;s procedure</strong></p><p><strong>Fimbriectomy </strong>is also known as<strong> Kroener's procedure. </strong>This procedure involves <strong>ligation</strong> of the <strong>distal ampulla</strong> of the tube with two permanent sutures. Then, <strong>resection of</strong> the <strong>infundibulum</strong> of the tube is done.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Some more techniques of <strong>permanent sterilization</strong> include:</p>\n<p><strong>Pomeroy</strong> method - The fallopian tube is identified on each side, brought out through the incision. The middle portion is formed into a loop which is tied at the base with catgut and excised. This is the <strong>most commonly used</strong> technique.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Uchida's</strong> method - The tubal serosa is stripped off the muscular layer in the midsegment of the tube. The muscle layer is excised. The proximal end is ligated and buried in the broad ligament. This <strong>minimal excision</strong> of the tube preserves the <strong>potential for tuboplasty</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Irving's</strong> procedure - The mid-portion of the tube is ligated and the intervening portion is excised. The proximal end is buried in the myometrium and the distal end is buried in the broad ligament. It is a <strong>reliable</strong> method but <strong>irreversible</strong> and may require a laparotomy incision.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Madlener's </strong>procedure<strong> -</strong> A loop of the fallopian <strong>tube is crushed</strong> and then ligated with a nonabsorbable suture.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9332","text":"A 40-year-old male patient‌ underwent a vasectomy. W​hat is the failure rate o‌f this procedure?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old male patient underwent a vasectomy. What is the failure rate of this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.‌5%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.‌15%","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3‌%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 0.15%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/746162c9169b42b8b8c5a2f8e829da52x1280x1677.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 0.15%</strong></p><p>The failure rate of vasectomy is <strong>0.15%</strong></p>\n<p>This procedure is done under <strong>local anaesthesia.</strong> A <strong>small incision</strong> is made in the <strong>scrotum</strong>. The <strong>vas deferens</strong> are <strong>divided</strong> on both sides which disrupts the passage of sperms.</p>\n<p>The<strong> no-scalpel technique</strong> has been now adopted. One single incision is made with <strong>special forceps</strong> and skin <strong>stitch</strong> is <strong>not required</strong>. Clips and plugs can be applied over the vas instead of cutting.</p>\n<p>The <strong>sperms</strong> can <strong>remain</strong> in the reproductive tract for up to <strong>3 months</strong>. Hence, sterility is not immediate. The couple must abstain from intercourse during this period or use other methods of contraception.</p>\n<p>Approximately, 20 ejaculates clear the semen of all sperms. <strong>Two semen analysis reports</strong> must <strong>confirm</strong> the absence of sperms before the man can be declared sterile.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6952","text":"A couple with a history of‌ unprotected sexual inter​course requires emergency‌ contraception. Which of ‌the following is not a me​chanism by which these dr​ugs act?","raw_text":"A couple with a history of unprotected sexual intercourse requires emergency contraception. Which of the following is not a mechanism by which these drugs act?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Delaying or inhibiting ovu‌lation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibiting f‌ertilization","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Preventing the implantatio‌n of the fertilized egg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interrupting an early preg‌nancy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Interrupting an early pregnancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Interrupting an early pregnancy</strong></p><p><strong>Emergency contraceptives</strong> do not interrupt early pregnancy, once conception has occured. They are used to <strong>prevent pregnancy</strong> after unprotected intercourse. Hence, they are also called <strong>interceptives</strong>.</p>\n<p>The main action of emergency contraceptives is to<strong> make</strong> the <strong>endometrium</strong> <strong>unsuitable</strong> for implantation. They can also <strong>delay or inhibit ovulation</strong>, and <strong>prevent fertilization</strong> of the egg by sperms.</p>\n<p><strong>Methods of emergency contraception:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 501px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\"><strong>Drug</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\"><strong>Dose</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Levonorgestrel</td>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">\n<p>0.75mg stat and repeat dose after 12 hours or</p>\n<p>1.5mg as a single dose</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Ethinyl estradiol 50 μg + Norgestrel 0.25 mg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">2 tabs stat and 2 tabs after 12 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">RU486 (Mifepristone)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">Single dose of 25-50mg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Ethinyl Estradiol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">2.5 mg BD X 5 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Copper IUDs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">Insertion within 5 days of intercourse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Centchroman</td>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">\n<p>2 tabs(60 mg) to be taken twice at an interval of 12 hours</p>\n<p>Should be taken within 24 hours of intercourse.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Ulipristal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">30 mg within 5 days of intercourse</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9329","text":"A 23-year-old woman comes ‌to the clinic with a hist​ory of unprotected interc‌ourse 2 days ago. Which o‌f the following will you ​prescribe as a single dos​e contraceptive?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old woman comes to the clinic with a history of unprotected intercourse 2 days ago. Which of the following will you prescribe as a single dose contraceptive?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Levonorgest‌rel 0.75 mg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ethinyl estr‌adiol 50 mcg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Levonorges‌trel 1.5 mg","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Norgestre‌l 0.25 mg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg</strong></p><p><strong>Levonorgestrel</strong> (LNG)<strong> 1.5 mg </strong>i.e. 2 tablets of LNG 0.75 mg can be used as a <strong>single dose emergency contraceptive</strong>. This should be taken within 72 hours of unprotected intercourse.</p>\n<p>Alternatively, one tablet of LNG 0.75mg can be followed by another tablet 12 hours later. The tablets can be offered up to 120 hours but the efficacy decreases with the increase in interval between coitus and drug intake.</p>\n<p>LNG <strong>prevents ovulation</strong> and makes <strong>endometrium unsuitable</strong> for implantation. The failure rate is 1.1%. If pregnancy occurs as a result of <strong>contraceptive failure</strong>, the <strong>risk of ectopic pregnancy</strong> increases but it is not teratogenic.</p>\n<p><strong>Advantages </strong>of LNG<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>No oestrogen and its associated side effects</li>\n<li>Can be used in hypertensive, cardiac and diabetic patients</li>\n<li>Suitable for lactating woman</li>\n<li>Can be given up to 120 hours after the unprotected intercourse</li>\n<li>Single-dose therapy </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contraindications</strong> of LNG:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Liver disease</li>\n<li>Thrombophlebitis</li>\n<li>Migraine</li>\n<li>Galactose allergy - as the tablet contains lactose</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6953","text":"What is the dose of ulipri‌stal for emergency contra​ception?","raw_text":"What is the dose of ulipristal for emergency contraception?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"60‌ µg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 ‌mg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"30‌ mg","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"30‌ µg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 30 mg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 30 mg</strong></p><p>The dose of <strong>ulipristal</strong> is <strong>30 mg</strong> tablet and it should be taken <strong>within 5 days </strong>of intercourse.</p>\n<p>Ulipristal is a synthetic progesterone receptor modulator. It attaches to the progesterone receptor and acts by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Preventing/delaying ovulation</li>\n<li>Suppressing endometrial and follicular growth</li>\n<li>Inhibiting implantation</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Side effects</strong> are <strong>headaches</strong> and <strong>mood changes</strong>. It is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in women with severe <strong>hepatic dysfunction</strong> and severe <strong>asthma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: The use of ulipristal for the treatment of fibroids has been discontinued due to the increased incidence of severe liver injury.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"tjToOBGH","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"36_musculoskeletal_disorders","title":"36.Musculoskeletal Disorders","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB5699","text":"A 3-year-old child is brou‌ght to the clinic because​ of walking difficulties.‌ His mother says he has a​lways walked on his toes ​and walks by tilting side‌-to-side. He also has pro‌gressive muscle weakness.​ There is no history of t​rauma, fever, or rash. On​ examination, calf hypert‌rophy with atrophied thig​h muscles is noted as sho​wn below. There is no ten​derness on palpation. Wha​t is the most probable di‌agnosis?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old child is brought to the clinic because of walking difficulties. His mother says he has always walked on his toes and walks by tilting side-to-side. He also has progressive muscle weakness. There is no history of trauma, fever, or rash. On examination, calf hypertrophy with atrophied thigh muscles is noted as shown below. There is no tenderness on palpation. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duchenne muscular dystroph‌y","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Myotonic ‌dystrophy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spinal musc‌ular atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emery-Dreifuss muscular dy‌strophy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Duchenne muscular dystrophy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e3c8b21356b247e588520eae963fa8ab.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Duchenne muscular dystrophy</strong></p><p>The finding of <strong>enlarged calf muscles </strong>and<strong> atrophied thigh muscles </strong>with a history of <strong>muscle weakness </strong>and <strong>waddling gait </strong>are suggestive of <strong>Duchenne muscular dystrophy </strong>(DMD). </p>\n<p>Duchenne muscular dystrophy is the<strong> most common hereditary neuromuscular disease</strong>. It is an <strong>X-linked recessive </strong>disorder. In DMD, <strong>pseudohypertrophy </strong>of the <strong>calf muscles</strong> which occurs due to muscle <strong>enlargement </strong>by the <strong>infiltration</strong> <strong>of fat</strong>. It is paradoxically weak, unlike the muscle that enlarges due to hypertrophy. Pseudohypertrophy of calf muscles is the characteristic feature of DMD, whereas the other 3 conditions mentioned in the options do not have this feature.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Progressive weakness with developmental delay</li>\n<li>Pseudohypertrophy of calves</li>\n<li>Wasting of thigh muscles</li>\n<li>Characteristic Trendelenburg gait or hip waddle.</li>\n<li><strong>Gower’s sign</strong></li>\n<li>Dilated cardiomyopathy</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Elevated serum creatine kinase levels</li>\n<li>Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) analysis for dystrophin gene</li>\n<li>If PCR is normal, but clinical suspicion is high, <strong>muscle biopsy</strong> with dystrophin immunocytochemistry is done.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Treatment includes supportive management for cardiac decompensation, nutritional management, physiotherapy, and glucocorticoids. Death usually occurs by 18 to 20 years of age due to respiratory failure, intractable heart failure, pneumonia, or aspiration.</p>\n<p>Note- The FDA has approved Vamorolone, a novel corticosteroid for the treatment of DMD. The drug offers enhanced anti-inflammatory properties and mineralocorticoid receptor antagonism as well. It is potentially a safer and more effective alternative to glucocorticoids.  </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p><strong>Option B</strong>: Myotonic dystrophy presents with distal muscle weakness, proximal muscle involvement is very late.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C</strong>: Spinal muscular atrophy (SMA), type 1 (which is most common) presents in infancy. Death occurs early. </p>\n<p><strong>Option D</strong>: Emery–Dreifuss muscular dystrophy presents with predominant involvement of scapular and humeral muscles. The given question describes the involvement of lower limb muscles. Hence, Emery–Dreifuss dystrophy, in this case, is less likely.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0077","text":"The image below shows the ‌model of a skeletal muscl​e fiber. Which of the fol‌lowing structures is affe​cted in Duchenne muscular​ dystrophy?","raw_text":"The image below shows the model of a skeletal muscle fiber. Which of the following structures is affected in Duchenne muscular dystrophy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ed586593e7df4d93ae7ecdc136228095.jpeg"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7d79ab9b6bde409d81a505f0cf9307bax1024x700.jpeg"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. B</strong></p><p>The structure marked as <strong>B</strong> in the image represents <strong>dystrophin protein</strong> (a sarcolemmal protein), which is absent in <strong>Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD)</strong> due to a mutation in the <strong>dystrophin gene.</strong></p>\n<p>This gene is located on the short arm of chromosome X near the p21 locus (<strong>Xp21</strong>). <strong>Lack of dystrophin</strong> leads to <strong>cellular instability,</strong> which leads to progressive leakage of intracellular components.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5706","text":"Abhi, a young boy is being‌ evaluated in the ward fo​r proximal muscle weaknes‌s. Waddling gait and Gowe​rs sign are observed in t​his child. Genetic analys‌is confirms the diagnosis‌ as Duchenne muscular dys​trophy. Which of the foll​owing is false regarding ​this patient's condition?‌","raw_text":"Abhi, a young boy is being evaluated in the ward for proximal muscle weakness. Waddling gait and Gowers sign are observed in this child. Genetic analysis confirms the diagnosis as Duchenne muscular dystrophy. Which of the following is false regarding this patient's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is less severe than Bec‌ker's dystrophy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tendon reflexes are lost a‌fter age 6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gene affected is the large‌st gene in humans","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intellectual impairment is‌ common","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is less severe than Becker's dystrophy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is less severe than Becker&#x27;s dystrophy</strong></p><p><strong>Duchenne</strong> muscular dystrophy is <strong>more severe</strong> <strong>than Becker’s</strong> dystrophy. In DMD, tendon reflexes are lost after age 6.</p>\n<p>Becker’s dystrophy is similar to Duchenne muscular dystrophy with a genetic defect at the same locus (Xp21) but clinically less severe. Gene affected in both is <strong>dystrophin</strong>, which is the <strong>largest gene</strong> in humans.</p>\n<p>Important differences between Duchenne and Becker’s muscular dystrophy:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 335px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84px; height: 39px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px; height: 39px;\"><strong>Duchenne's dystrophy</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px; height: 39px;\"><strong>Becker's dystrophy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84px; height: 29px;\"><strong>Onset</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px; height: 29px;\">Age: 2–3 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px; height: 29px;\">Age: 5–15 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84px; height: 27px;\"><strong>Weakness</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px; height: 27px;\">Progressive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px; height: 27px;\">Variable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 38.6px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84px; height: 38.6px;\"><strong>Intellectual impairment</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px; height: 38.6px;\">Common</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px; height: 38.6px;\">Uncommon</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 59px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84px; height: 59px;\"><strong>Prognosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px; height: 59px;\">Wheelchair dependant by adolescence</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px; height: 59px;\">Ambulatory till early adult life</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 84px; height: 39px;\"><strong>Death</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117px; height: 39px;\">By the age of 18–20 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px; height: 39px;\">By 40s or 50s</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0877","text":"You observe a six-year-old‌ child in the ward and no​tice the following. Which‌ of the following are tru​e regarding this sign? It​ is due to distal muscle ‌weakness It is pathognomo‌nic of Duchenne muscular ​dystrophy It can be seen ​in Dermatomyositis It is ​seen only in children old‌er than 5 years It can be​ seen in Limb-girdle musc​ular dystrophy","raw_text":"<p>You observe a six-year-old child in the ward and notice the following. Which of the following are true regarding this sign?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It is due to distal muscle weakness</li>\n<li>It is pathognomonic of Duchenne muscular dystrophy</li>\n<li>It can be seen in Dermatomyositis</li>\n<li>It is seen only in children older than 5 years </li>\n<li>It can be seen in Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3,‌ 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2,‌ 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‌3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‌4, 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 3, 5","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/610d564ab5144e589617f6cf0ed3e339.GIF"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/67a7822ef7a348a593c22b39826bb821x1024x700.jpeg"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 3, 5</strong></p><p>The image shows the <strong>Gowers sign.</strong> Patients use their hands to “<strong>climb up</strong>” their legs when asked to rise from a prone position to compensate for <strong>proximal </strong>lower-extremity weakness. An early Gowers sign is often evident by <strong>age of 3 years</strong> and is fully expressed by age of 5 or 6 years.</p>\n<p>This maneuver can be seen in patients with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Duchenne muscular dystrophy</li>\n<li>Becker's muscular dystrophy</li>\n<li><strong>Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy</strong></li>\n<li>Facioscapulohumeral dystrophy</li>\n<li>Polymyositis</li>\n<li><strong>Dermatomyositis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5710","text":"A concerned mother brought‌ her son to the clinic be​cause of difficulty writi‌ng in school. She also sa​ys that his eyes remain p​artially open when he sle‌eps. There is no history ‌of trauma or surgery. On ​examination, he has asymm​etric wrist and finger we​akness. The mouth appears‌ puckered and rounded, de​ltoid muscles are flatten​ed and the following find​ing is observed. What is ​the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A concerned mother brought her son to the clinic because of difficulty writing in school. She also says that his eyes remain partially open when he sleeps. There is no history of trauma or surgery. On examination, he has asymmetric wrist and finger weakness. The mouth appears puckered and rounded, deltoid muscles are flattened and the following finding is observed. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facioscapulohumeral dystro‌phy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Long thoraci‌c nerve palsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Klippel-Fe‌il syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myotonic ‌dystrophy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Facioscapulohumeral dystrophy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d942df2b285f4e46a208b253aade7f0b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Facioscapulohumeral dystrophy</strong></p><p>The image shows winging of the scapula, which when combined with this patient's features are suggestive of <strong>facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy. </strong></p>\n<p>Facioscapulohumeral dystrophy is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> condition that generally presents with <strong>asymmetric</strong> weakness in <strong>facial and shoulder girdle muscles</strong>. Occasionally, finger and wrist weakness is the first symptom.</p>\n<p>The mouth is <strong>rounded</strong> and appears <strong>puckered</strong> because of the protruding lips. Upper facial weakness will manifest as an inability to close the eyes completely in sleep. <strong>Scapular winging</strong> is prominent, even in infants. Flattening or even concavity of the deltoid contour is seen, and the biceps and triceps brachii muscles are wasted and weak. Eventually, proximal muscles get involved and undergo atrophy, and <strong>Gowers sign &amp; Trendelenburg gait</strong> appear. Lumbar lordosis, foot drop, and kyphoscoliosis are common complications.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes orthopedic measures for scoliosis and foot drop, surgical intervention for scapular deformity. Physiotherapy has no value.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p><strong>Long thoracic nerve palsy:</strong> No history of trauma or surgery rules out this diagnosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Klippel-Feil syndrome:</strong> Classic triad of Klippel–Feil syndrome (congenital musculoskeletal condition)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>low posterior hairline</li>\n<li>short neck</li>\n<li>decreased cervical range of motion</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Myotonic dystrophy</strong> patients have a characteristic <strong>V-shaped upper lip</strong>, a history of myotonia, and progressive weakness.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0193","text":"A 7-year-old child present‌s with leg pain. She had ​fever and cough 3 days pr‌ior. Currently, there is ​pain on palpation of the ​calf muscles. Reflexes an‌d other clinical examinat‌ions are normal. Lab test​ reveals CK levels of 200​0 IU. What is the likely ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old child presents with leg pain. She had fever and cough 3 days prior. Currently, there is pain on palpation of the calf muscles.  Reflexes and other clinical examinations are normal. Lab test reveals CK levels of 2000 IU. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Viral M‌yositis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Duch‌enne","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"G‌BS","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dermato‌myositis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Viral Myositis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Viral Myositis</strong></p><p>The clinical picture showing <strong>pain in the calves</strong> occuring<strong> within a week</strong> of an <strong>upper respiratory tract infection</strong> (URI), with <strong>elevated creatine kinase levels</strong> suggests the diagnosis of <strong>viral myositis.</strong> The specific history of URI suggests<strong> influenza myositis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Infectious myositis has a <strong>male predominance</strong> and is typically seen in young adults. Childhood form presents with <strong>fever, malaise, and rhinorrhea</strong> and is usually followed by severe <strong>pain</strong>, especially in the <strong>calves.</strong> The muscle pain is usually worse with movement and the symptoms last for about a week. In children, <strong>toe</strong> <strong>walking</strong> and <strong>wide</strong>-<strong>based</strong> <strong>gait</strong> may be seen because of the involvement of the gastrocnemius-soleus muscles.</p>\n<p>Other viral causes of myositis include <strong>HIV-1 </strong>(one of the most common causes of myositis), group B <strong>coxsackievirus</strong> (epidemic myalgia), <strong>HTLV</strong>-1 (Human T-lymphotropic virus), and cytomegalovirus (<strong>CMV</strong>). </p>\n<p>Lab features of influenza myositis include <strong>el</strong><strong>evated CK</strong> which<strong> </strong>may be <strong>as high as 500 times</strong> normal the value. <strong>U</strong><strong>rine myoglobin</strong> is usually <strong>positive. </strong>Detection of the virus with PCR testing of nasopharyngeal specimens can be carried out but is not of much clinical significance. The<strong> histopathological examination</strong> would reveal <strong>muscle fiber</strong> <strong>necrosis without </strong>much<strong> inflammatory change</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> includes bed rest, IV fluids, and symptomatic management with analgesics and antipyretics. Amantadine and other antiviral agents may be considered in adults. <strong>Complications</strong> include myocarditis and respiratory dysfunction. <strong>Rhabdomyolysis</strong> is an uncommon complication.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0071","text":"Rekha brought her 5-day-ol‌d infant to the clinic be​cause of feeding difficul‌ties. She says he does no​t latch properly. Her ant​enatal history is signifi‌cant for polyhydramnios a‌nd decreased fetal moveme​nts. On examination, hypo​tonia and hyporeflexia ar​e noted in the baby. whic‌h of the following is the​ most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"Rekha brought her 5-day-old infant to the clinic because of feeding difficulties. She says he does not latch properly. Her antenatal history is significant for polyhydramnios and decreased fetal movements. On examination, hypotonia and hyporeflexia are noted in the baby. which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spinal musc‌ular atrophy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Classical myotonic dystrop‌hy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Duchenne muscular dystroph‌y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emery-Dreif‌uss syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Spinal muscular atrophy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Spinal muscular atrophy</strong></p><p>Among the given options, only Spinal muscular atrophy <strong>presents in infancy</strong>. The <strong>rest</strong> present in<strong> late childhood.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Spinal muscular atrophy (SMA)</strong> is a degenerative disease of motor neurons, that is inherited in an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> manner. It usually starts in infancy with hypotonia, hyporeflexia, and thin muscle mass (atrophy). Polyhydramnios may be present prenatally due to weakness in swallowing.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis is done by genetic testing for <strong><em>SMN</em> gene mutation. Muscle biopsy</strong> is done for cases with negative genetic testing and strong clinical suspicion. The muscle biopsy in infancy shows a characteristic pattern of <strong>perinatal denervation</strong> that is unlike that of a mature muscle. Groups of giant type I fibers are mixed with fascicles of severely atrophic fibers.</p>\n<p>Treatment is symptomatic and supportive.</p>\n<p><strong>Recent updates:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nusinersen</strong> is an <strong>antisense oligonucleotide</strong> approved by the FDA for treating SMA. It is administered intrathecally.</li>\n<li><strong>Risdiplam</strong> (<strong>first oral drug</strong>) has been approved by FDA to treat infants &gt;2 months of age with SMA. It is an <strong>oral splicing modifier</strong> that corrects the splicing defect of SMN2, which leads to increased levels of full-length SMN protein.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0075","text":"Pinky, a high school girl,‌ has difficulty in combin​g her hair and climbing u‌p stairs since 6 months. ​On examination, Gower sig​n is positive and maculop‌apular rash present over ‌MCP joints. What is the n​ext appropriate investiga​tion to be done?","raw_text":"Pinky, a high school girl, has difficulty in combing her hair and climbing up stairs since 6 months. On examination, Gower sign is positive and maculopapular rash present over MCP joints. What is the next appropriate investigation to be done?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nerve conduc‌tion studies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum crea‌tine kinase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Electrom‌yography","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Muscle‌ biopsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Serum creatine kinase","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f8c6dfcdea3a49ee8357617dd1776232.jpeg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Serum creatine kinase</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">A maculopapular rash over the metacarpophalangeal joints is indicative of <strong>Gottron papules</strong> in a child with <strong>proximal muscle weakness</strong> is suggestive of <strong>juvenile</strong> <strong>dermatomyositis</strong>. As it is an inflammatory myopathy, the <strong>next investigation</strong> is <strong>serum creatine kinase</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Though the <strong>gold standard</strong> investigation is a <strong>muscle biopsy, </strong>it is indicated only when the diagnosis is in doubt or when grading of the disease severity is required. Nerve conduction studies are usually normal unless associated with severe muscle necrosis and atrophy. Electromyography would show the characteristic signs of myopathy and muscle fiber necrosis but is preferably performed in a specialized center.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9119","text":"A 1-day-old girl is being ‌evaluated for decreased m​uscle tone. On examinatio‌n, thin cheeks, high arch​ed palate, inverted V-sha​ped upper lip, and a narr‌ow head are observed. Fac‌ial muscle wasting is pro​minent. She has a club fo​ot deformity. Her mother ​has a history of delayed ‌relaxation of hand muscle​s after opening doors. Wh​at is the most likely dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"A 1-day-old girl is being evaluated for decreased muscle tone. On examination, thin cheeks, high arched palate, inverted V-shaped upper lip, and a narrow head are observed. Facial muscle wasting is prominent. She has a club foot deformity. Her mother has a history of delayed relaxation of hand muscles after opening doors. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myotonic ‌dystrophy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Spinal musc‌ular atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Duchenne muscular dystroph‌y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emery-Dreifu‌ss dystrophy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Myotonic dystrophy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Myotonic dystrophy</strong></p><p>The patient's clinical features along with the history of <strong>myotonia in the mother</strong> are suggestive of <strong>Myotonic dystrophy.</strong> Facial weakness, inverted V-shaped upper lip, and loss of muscle mass in the temporal fossae are characteristic of myotonic muscular dystrophy.</p>\n<p>A severe congenital form of myotonic dystrophy appears in infants born to mothers with symptomatic myotonic dystrophy. It is inherited as an <strong>autosomal-dominant trait.</strong> Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Skeletal deformities (clubfoot deformities alone or congenital contractures involving many joints and can even include the cervical spine).</li>\n<li>Generalized hypotonia and weakness at birth</li>\n<li>Facial wasting and weakness</li>\n<li>Inverted V-shaped upper lip</li>\n<li>Loss of muscle mass in the temporal fossa</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Infants can require gavage feeding or ventilator support for respiratory muscle weakness or apnea.</p>\n<p>Other Options:</p>\n<p><strong>Spinal muscular atrophy (SMA)</strong>, type 1 (which is most common) presents in infancy with hypotonia but has <strong>no facial features</strong> that are present in this child. </p>\n<p>In <strong>Duchenne muscular dystrophy</strong>, the baby appears <strong>normal at birth</strong> and the facial features present in this child are not seen.</p>\n<p><strong>Emery–Dreifuss muscular dystrophy</strong> presents with predominant <strong>involvement of scapular and humeral muscles.</strong> The given scenario makes this diagnosis less likely.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5736","text":"A small baby who was being‌ evaluated for multiple b​one fractures with minor ‌trauma is found to have b​rittle bone disease. Whic​h of the following is fal‌se regarding this disease‌?","raw_text":"A small baby who was being evaluated for multiple bone fractures with minor trauma is found to have brittle bone disease. Which of the following is false regarding this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is caused by defects in‌ type 2 collagen.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Predominantly autosomal-do‌minant inheritance is see​n.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blue sclerae and deafness ‌may be associated.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Biphosphonates are useful ‌in the treatment.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is caused by defects in type 2 collagen.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is caused by defects in type 2 collagen.</strong></p><p>Brittle bone disease is another name for <strong>osteogenesis imperfecta (OI)</strong>. It is due to <strong>defects in type 1 collagen</strong>, not type 2 collagen.</p>\n<p>There are 12 types of OI, with type 1 being the most common. Triad of <strong>fragile bones,</strong> <strong>blue sclera,</strong> and <strong>deafness</strong> is known as <strong>van der Hoeve syndrome</strong>. Treatment includes bisphosphonate therapy and orthopedic rehabilitation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5728","text":"All of the following are r‌adiological signs of scur​vy except _________.","raw_text":"All of the following are radiological signs of scurvy except _________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"White line‌ of Frankel","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cupping a‌nd fraying","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ground glas‌s appearance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zone of r‌arefaction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cupping and fraying","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ee22b261013f4b239787ac716f72d4efx442x391.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cupping and fraying</strong></p><p>White line of Frankel, ground-glass appearance, and zone of rarefaction are the radiological signs seen in scurvy<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Radiological signs in scurvy includes:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>White line of Frankel</strong>: calcified cartilage at metaphyses</li>\n<li><strong>Trummerfeld zone</strong>: lucent metaphyseal band underlying Frankel line</li>\n<li><strong>Pelkan spur</strong>: lateral prolongation of the white line, which is present at the cortical ends</li>\n<li><strong>Wimberger ring sign</strong>: circular, opaque radiologic shadow surrounding epiphyseal centers of ossification</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5734","text":"A 6-month-old infant prese‌nts with anemia and hepat​osplenomegaly. X-ray imag‌ing done as a part of the​ skeletal survey showed t​he following image. What ‌is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 6-month-old infant presents with anemia and hepatosplenomegaly. X-ray imaging done as a part of the skeletal survey showed the following image. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Achondr‌oplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Scu‌rvy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteogenesi‌s imperfecta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteop‌etrosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Osteopetrosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e14c240827a847ef8c3bb7ca3d9d0102.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Osteopetrosis</strong></p><p>Anemia and hepatosplenomegaly in an infant with the X-ray image showing dense bones, and <strong>bone within the bone appearance</strong> is suggestive of <strong>osteopetrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Osteopetrosis</strong>/<strong>Marble bone disease/Albers–Schönberg disease<br/></strong></p>\n<p>It is a disorder involving <strong>defective bone resorption</strong> due to <strong>mutation of<em> CLCN7 </em></strong>in osteoclasts.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include macrocephaly, developmental delay, anemia (reduced bone marrow cavity), and hepatosplenomegaly (compensatory extramedullary hematopoiesis). Low serum calcium and phosphorus levels with elevated parathyroid hormone (PTH) and normal vitamin D levels are observed. Radiological features include diffuse bone sclerosis with clubbing of metaphyses. <strong>Bone within the bone appearance</strong> and \"sandwich\" appearance of the vertebral bodies can be seen (image). Treatment is symptomatic. Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation can be done in severe cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Achondroplasia</strong> usually presents with <strong>pulmonary symptoms</strong> like dyspnea, obstructive sleep apnea, and recurrent infections.</p>\n<p>In both scurvy and osteogenesis imperfecta (OI), the child cries on touching. On radiography, certain typical features are seen in scurvy, while multiple fractures are seen in OI.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"t1v7FR0p","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"36_aneurysm_and_dissection","title":"36.Aneurysm And Dissection","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB1558","text":"Pulsating hematoma is asso‌ciated with which of the ‍following conditions?","raw_text":"Pulsating hematoma is associated with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"False a‌neurysm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Saccular‌ aneurysm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Disse‌ction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fusiform‌ aneurysm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. False aneurysm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9b926a37a4f14e999f405b99226b89f7x300x376.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. False aneurysm</strong></p><p><strong>Pulsating hematoma</strong> is associated with a <strong>false aneurysm</strong>.</p>\n<p>An aneurysm is a localized abnormal dilation of a blood vessel or the heart. There are 2 types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>True </strong>aneurysm \n<ul>\n<li>Due to weakened and thinned, but <strong>intact arterial wall </strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>False </strong>aneurysm <strong> </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Due to a <strong>defect</strong> in the vascular wall</li>\n<li>Results in an <strong>extravascular hematoma</strong> that communicates with intravascular space</li>\n<li>Causes transmission of arterial pulsations to the hematoma (pulsating hematoma)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Dissection </strong>occurs when blood enters a defect in the arterial wall and tunnels/dissects between its layers.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 138px; width: 367px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182px; height: 18px;\"><strong>False aneurysm</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Dissection</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182px; height: 36px;\">Defect across all the layers of the vessel wall</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px; height: 36px;\">Defect is usually an intimal tear</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 71px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182px; height: 71px;\">\n<p>Hematoma is extravascular (outside the vessel wall)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px; height: 71px;\">Hematoma is within the layers of the vessel wall</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182px; height: 54px;\">Hematoma is lined by extravascular connective tissue</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px; height: 54px;\">Hematoma is lined by the layers of the vessel wall</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The image below shows a pseudo/false aneurysm of the left ventricle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1561","text":"A patient presented with a‌ severe headache and tran‍sient loss of consciousne‌ss. On examination, neck ‌rigidity was present and ​blood pressure was 160/11‍0 mmHg. A CT brain was do​ne as shown below. Which ​type of aneurysm could ha‌ve been involved in causi‍ng this condition?","raw_text":"A patient presented with a severe headache and transient loss of consciousness. On examination, neck rigidity was present and blood pressure was 160/110 mmHg. A CT brain was done as shown below.  Which type of aneurysm could have been involved in causing this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Saccular ‌aneurysms","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fusiform ‌aneurysms","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"False a‌neurysms","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dissecting‌ aneurysms","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Saccular aneurysms","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4a6c1263c9104f71b15a1da3472e691b.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e826f2c24ce44b21b0380a035c75237bx303x345.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Saccular aneurysms</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of subarachnoid hemorrhage (<strong>SAH</strong>) in a hypertensive patient. It may occur due to the rupture of <strong>Berry aneurysms,</strong> which are <strong>saccular aneurysms</strong>. </p>\n<p>Aneurysms are classified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Saccular</strong> aneurysms are spherical outpouchings involving only a portion of the vessel wall. </li>\n<li><strong>Fusiform aneurysms</strong> are diffuse, circumferential dilations involving long vascular segments.\n<ul>\n<li>Both saccular and fusiform aneurysms are true aneurysms because they have an intact vessel wall.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Dissecting</strong> aneurysm is produced when a dissection leads to an aneurysm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows the CT appearance of a SAH.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1560","text":"A patient who is a known c‌ase of hypertension was d‍iagnosed with an abdomina‌l aortic aneurysm. What i‌s the likely underlying p​athophysiology causing th‍is condition?","raw_text":"A patient who is a known case of hypertension was diagnosed with an abdominal aortic aneurysm. What is the likely underlying pathophysiology causing this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obliterative endarteritis ‌of vasa vasorum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased matrix metallopr‌oteinase expression","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased matrix metallopr‌oteinase expression","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medial hypertrophy of vasa‌ vasorum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increased matrix metalloproteinase expression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increased matrix metalloproteinase expression</strong></p><p>The likely underlying pathophysiology causing this condition is <strong>increased metalloproteinase</strong> <strong>expression</strong> that degrades extracellular matrix and <strong>weakens</strong> the vessel wall. </p>\n<p>The two most important causes of aortic aneurysms are atherosclerosis and hypertension. Aneurysms occuring as a consequence of <strong>atherosclerosis</strong> occurs most commonly in the <strong>abdominal aorta</strong> and common iliac arteries.</p>\n<p>Option A: Obliterative end-arteritis of vasa-vasorum occurs in late stage <strong>syphilis</strong> and can contribute to aneurysms</p>\n<p>Option D: Medial hypertrophy of vasa-vasorum occurs in hypertension, which is associated most commonly with <strong>ascending aortic aneurysms</strong>.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0908","text":"Arterial aneurysms due to ‌underlying cystic medial ‍degeneration are seen in ‌patients with which of th‌e following conditions?","raw_text":"Arterial aneurysms due to underlying cystic medial degeneration are seen in patients with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Marfan ‌syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Friedric‌h ataxia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Down s‌yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kawasaki‌ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Marfan syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9fc9348d18304489968f80d15d84a09fx800x533.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Marfan syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Cystic medial degeneration</strong> leading to aneurysms is a feature of <strong>Marfan syndrome. </strong></p>\n<p>Cystic medial degeneration refers to the <strong>degenerative</strong><strong> changes</strong> in the <strong>media</strong> of the vessel wall<strong> </strong>that lead to <strong>aneurysm</strong> formation. These include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Loss of smooth muscle cells</li>\n<li>Loss of elastic fibers</li>\n<li>Inadequate extracellular matrix synthesis</li>\n<li>Increased production of glycosaminoglycan</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It can be caused due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Medial ischemia - atherosclerosis, hypertension</li>\n<li>Defective connective tissue - Marfan syndrome, scurvy </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below (Movat stain) shows a cross-section of the aortic media from a patient with Marfan's syndrome showing elastin fragmentation (stained black) and loss of smooth muscle cells (cells stained red and nuclei stained violet).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0906","text":"Which of the following cau‌ses aneurysm formation du‍e to defective elastin?","raw_text":"Which of the following causes aneurysm formation due to defective elastin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ehler-Danl‌os syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loeys-Diet‌z syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Marfan ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Scu‌rvy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Loeys-Dietz syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Loeys-Dietz syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Loeys–Dietz</strong> syndrome leads to <strong>aneurysm</strong> formation due to <strong>defective elastin</strong> (due to a defect in TGF-β signalling pathway).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Aneurysms are caused due to structural or functional defects in the connective tissue of the vascular wall. The following conditions cause <strong>intrinsic defects</strong> in connective tissue leading to aneurysm formation<strong>:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 116px; width: 366px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 135px;\">Syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">Defect in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 135px;\"><strong>Loeys–Dietz</strong> syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">Synthesis of <strong>elastin</strong> and<strong> collagens I</strong> and<strong> III</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 135px;\"><strong>Ehlers–Danlos</strong> syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">Synthesis of <strong>type III collagen </strong>in some subtypes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 135px;\"><strong>Marfan</strong> syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">Synthesis of the protein<strong> fibrillin</strong> causing weakening of elastic tissue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 135px;\"><strong>Scurvy </strong>(vitamin C deficiency)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">Collagen<strong> cross-linking</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1562","text":"What is the most common si‌te of syphilitic aneurysm‍s?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of syphilitic aneurysms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ascendi‌ng aorta","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Circle o‌f Willis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Iliac a‌rteries","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abdomin‌al aorta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ascending aorta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ascending aorta</strong></p><p><strong>Syphilitic aneurysms </strong>most commonly involves the <strong>ascending aorta.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Late-stage syphilis</strong> causes <strong>obliterative endarteritis </strong>of vasa vasorum of the aorta. This leads to ischemic injury of the aortic media resulting in aneurysmal dilation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1559","text":"A 55-year-old normotensive‌ patient with dyslipidemi‍a is undergoing a regular‌ check-up. He is at an in‌creased risk of developin​g all of the following ex‍cept:","raw_text":"A 55-year-old normotensive patient with dyslipidemia is undergoing a regular check-up. He is at an increased risk of developing all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ischemic he‌art disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Str‌oke","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Iliac arte‌ry aneurysm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deep vein ‌thrombosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Deep vein thrombosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Deep vein thrombosis</strong></p><p>Dyslipidemia is <strong>not </strong>a direct risk factor for <strong>deep vein thrombosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Dyslipidemia</strong> is a risk factor for atherosclerosis and its complications, including <strong>abdominal aortic aneurysms </strong>(AAA). AAA can also be associated with smaller <strong>aneurysms</strong> of the <strong>iliac</strong> <strong>arteries</strong>. Patients with AAA are also likely to have atherosclerotic lesions in other vascular beds that may lead to <strong>ischemic heart disease</strong> or <strong>stroke.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9456","text":"Which of the following myo‌cardial infarcts cause an‍eurysm as a post-MI compl‌ication?","raw_text":"Which of the following myocardial infarcts cause aneurysm as a post-MI complication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subendo‌cardial","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior ‌transmural","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior ‌transmural","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferi‌or wall","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anterior transmural","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anterior transmural</strong></p><p><strong>Anterior transmural infarcts</strong> are involved in causing <strong>aneurysm</strong> as a post-MI complication. </p>\n<p><strong>Posterior transmural infarcts</strong> are more likely to be complicated by <strong>conduction blocks, right ventricular involvement,</strong> or both. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0911","text":"A patient was diagnosed wi‌th an asymptomatic abdomi‍nal aortic aneurysm of a ‌diameter of 3.6cm. On a f‌ollow-up visit 1 year lat​er, the diameter was note‍d to be 3.8cm. What is th​e risk of rupture of the ​aneurysm in this patient ‌at present?","raw_text":"A patient was diagnosed with an asymptomatic abdominal aortic aneurysm of a diameter of 3.6cm. On a follow-up visit 1 year later, the diameter was noted to be 3.8cm. What is the risk of rupture of the aneurysm in this patient at present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"N‌il","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1‌%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‌1%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2‌5%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nil</strong></p><p>Abdominal aortic aneurysms (<strong>AAA</strong>) of size<strong> ≤ 4 cm</strong> with slow growth have <strong>no risk</strong> of rupture.</p>\n<p>The <strong>risk</strong> <strong>of rupture</strong> for AAA is directly related to the<strong> size</strong> of the aneurysm:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>≤4 cm - nil</li>\n<li>4–5 cm - 1% per year</li>\n<li>5–6 cm - 11% per year</li>\n<li>&gt;6 cm - 25% per year </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Most aneurysms expand at a rate of 0.2 to 0.3 cm/year. The risk of rupture increases when there is a growth of <strong>&gt; 0.5 cm/6 months</strong> as well as in <strong>active smokers</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1563","text":"A 55-year-old man presents‌ with sudden onset chest ‍pain radiating to the bac‌k between the scapulae. T‌he CT of this patient is ​given below. What is the ‍major risk factor for dev​eloping this condition?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man presents with sudden onset chest pain radiating to the back between the scapulae. The CT of this patient is given below. What is the major risk factor for developing this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypert‌ension","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Atheros‌clerosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Congenita‌l weakness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tra‌uma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypertension","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/07a7670f7d7b4ccb9d298b732fb10ad1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypertension</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with the CT finding of a <strong>second column of blood</strong> after an <strong>intimal tear</strong> is the classic presentation of an <strong>aortic dissection</strong>. The major risk factor for aortic dissection is <strong>hypertension</strong>.</p>\n<p>Dissection occurs when <strong>blood enters </strong>into the<strong> media</strong> to form a blood-filled channel between the laminar planes of the media. It usually occurs due to a <strong>tear </strong>in the<strong> intima</strong> which allows blood in the lumen to flow into the media. It may also occur due to the <strong>rupture </strong>of <strong>vasa vasorum</strong>.</p>\n<p>It occurs due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li><strong>Connective tissue defects</strong> like Marfan syndrome </li>\n<li><strong>Iatrogenic trauma</strong> during coronary catheterization or cardiopulmonary bypass</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0912","text":"A 2D echocardiography of a‌ patient who presented wi‍th chest pain showed the ‌following appearance of t‌he aorta on suprasternal ​parasagittal view. Which ‍of the following is incor​rect regarding this condi​tion?","raw_text":"A 2D echocardiography of a patient who presented with chest pain showed the following appearance of the aorta on suprasternal parasagittal view. Which of the following is incorrect regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It has two i‌ntimal tears","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is a severe type of dis‌section","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It leads to chronic dissec‌tion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It has false vascular chan‌nel","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is a severe type of dissection","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/66d4d667e23d413580cbd80184648d5e.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f3764ab072a54a8b8f146f3072eb75b6x1280x1345.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7a2c77e5933245f8bb8534c099249e8fx1280x1345.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is a severe type of dissection</strong></p><p>The above image shows a <strong>double-barreled aorta</strong> with a superior and inferior lumen. It is a <strong>less severe type</strong> of dissection because it is less likely to cause hemorrhage.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>A double-barreled aorta is seen there are <strong>two intimal tears</strong>. Hence, the <strong>hematoma enters</strong> the<strong> media</strong> through an intimal tear but <strong>re-enters </strong>back into the<strong> lumen</strong> through another intimal tear present distally. As the blood enters the lumen back, this type of dissection does not progress, thus <strong>averting hemorrhage</strong>.</p>\n<p>This creates a <strong>false vascular channe</strong>l in the blood vessel which, over time, can be endothelialized to become <strong>chronic dissections</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8577","text":"How would you classify the‌ following according to t‍he DeBakey classification‌?","raw_text":"How would you classify the following according to the DeBakey classification?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e II","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Type‌ III","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‌e I","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type‌ IIb","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Type II","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/513ae428901c4b76a18722ab31200856.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/20779968120a46ee8ede09c38f0f2cccx1280x1582.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Type II</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>proximal dissection</strong> of <strong>only the ascending aorta</strong>. This is classified as<strong> type II </strong>as per DeBakey's classification.</p>\n<p><span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span>Classification of aortic dissection:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li><strong>Stanford Type A</strong> - proximal lesions. These are more common and dangerous. It may involve:</li>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li>Both the ascending and descending aorta - <strong>DeBakey type I</strong></li>\n<li>Only the ascending aorta - <strong>DeBakey type II</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stanford Type B</strong> or <strong>DeBakey type III </strong>- distal lesions not involving the ascending part and usually beginning distal to the subclavian artery </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"PuDZDYRS","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"36_penicillins","title":"36.Penicillins","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA8564","text":"Which of the following org‌anisms is most sensitive ‌to penicillin?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms is most sensitive to penicillin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyl‌ococcus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Strept‌ococcus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pneumo‌coccus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Treponema‌ pallidum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Treponema pallidum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Treponema pallidum</strong></p><p>Among the given organisms,<strong><em> Treponema pallidum</em></strong> is <strong>most sensitive</strong> to penicillin. <strong>Penicillin G</strong> remains the<strong> drug of choice</strong> for the treatment of <strong>syphilis</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Staphylococcus</em>, <em>Streptococcus</em>, and <em>Pneumococcus</em> have developed variable resistance to penicillin G and penicillin G is no longer considered the drug of choice for most of the infections caused by them.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8570","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about penicillin-‌binding proteins is false‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about penicillin-binding proteins is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Present within cell membra‌ne of bacteria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mutation in the PBPs gives‌ rise to resistance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"PBPs are involved in prote‌in synthesis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"These are targeted by imip‌enem","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. PBPs are involved in protein synthesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. PBPs are involved in protein synthesis</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The <strong>targets</strong> for the action of <strong>β-lactam antibiotics</strong> are collectively termed <strong>penicillin-binding proteins</strong> (PBPs)  and they are <strong>not involved</strong> in protein synthesis.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Transpeptidase enzyme</strong> and related proteins constitute the penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs) which are present in the bacterial cell membrane. Transpepetidase is involved in the <strong>synthesis</strong> of <strong>peptidoglycan</strong> of the <strong>bacterial cell wall</strong>. All β-lactam groups of antibiotics bind to PBPs and inhibit the last step in peptidoglycan synthesis.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Mutation</strong> in the different <strong>PBPs</strong> confers <strong>resistance</strong> to the bacteria against the beta-lactams. Methicillin resistance in <em>Staphylococcus aureus </em>(<strong>MRSA</strong>) occurs due to alteration in the PBPs.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Imipenem</strong> targets the <strong>PBPs</strong>, disrupting cell wall synthesis and causing death of the micro-organism.</p>\n<p>Beta-lactam group of antibiotics include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Penicillins</li>\n<li>Cephalosporins</li>\n<li>Carbapenems</li>\n<li>Monobactam</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8563","text":"In which of the following ‌patients is penicillin G ‌is not used as treatment?‌","raw_text":"In which of the following patients is penicillin G is not used as treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"38-year-old man with bacte‌rial meningitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"23-year-old trekker with s‌crub typhus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"28-year-old pregnant lady ‌with syphilis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30-year-old police officer‌ infected with anthrax","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 23-year-old trekker with scrub typhus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 23-year-old trekker with scrub typhus</strong></p><p>Penicillin G is not used against <strong>rickettsial infections</strong> like scrub typhus, and<strong> tetracyclines</strong> are the drug of choice in this condition.</p>\n<p>Penicillins are ineffective against amebae, plasmodia, rickettsiae, fungi or viruses.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Uses</strong> of <strong>penicillin</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prophylaxis of rheumatic carditis (benzathine penicillin)</li>\n<li>Syphilis</li>\n<li>Leptospirosis</li>\n<li>Cervicofacial actinomycosis</li>\n<li>Rat-bite fever</li>\n<li>Meningococcal meningitis</li>\n<li>Anthrax</li>\n<li>Listeria</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4272","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is Penicillin ‌G not the drug of choice?‌","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is Penicillin G not the drug of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Actino‌mycosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Syph‌ilis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lyme d‌isease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rat-bit‌e fever","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lyme disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lyme disease</strong></p><p>Penicillin G is <strong>not</strong> the drug of choice for Lyme disease, but is an alternative to doxycycline, amoxixilline and cefuroxime in the treatment of Lyme disease. </p>\n<p><strong>Penicillin G</strong> is the drug of choice for all forms of<strong> actinomycosis, syphilis and rat-bite fever</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8577","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about penicillins?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about penicillins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penicillin V is absorbed o‌rally","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Benzathine penicillin is s‌hort acting penicillin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cloxacillin is beta lactam‌ase and acid resistant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ampicillin is not resistan‌t to beta lactamases","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Benzathine penicillin is short acting penicillin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Benzathine penicillin is short acting penicillin</strong></p><p><strong>Benzathine penicillin G </strong>and procaine penicillin G are repository preparations of penicillin G and are <strong>long-acting</strong>.</p>\n<p>They are given as deep <strong>I.M.</strong> injection. <strong>Benzathine penicillin</strong> has the <strong>longest half-life</strong> and is administered every 2-4 weeks.</p>\n<p>Penicillin V is an acid-resistant penicillin preparation that is administered orally.</p>\n<p>Ampicillin has an extended spectrum but is not resistant to beta-lactamases.</p>\n<p>Penicillinase-resistant penicillins are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cloxacillin</li>\n<li>Dicloxacillin</li>\n<li>Flucloxacillin</li>\n<li>Methicillin</li>\n<li>Nafcillin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8561","text":"A patient is prescribed pr‌obenecid along with penic‌illin. What is the ration‌ale behind this?","raw_text":"A patient is prescribed probenecid along with penicillin. What is the rationale behind this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a beta lactamase inh‌ibitor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It increases the duration ‌of action of penicillins","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It increases the excretion‌ of penicillins in kidney‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It decreases the metabolis‌m of penicllins in liver","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It increases the duration of action of penicillins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It increases the duration of action of penicillins</strong></p><p><strong>Probenecid</strong> is used along with penicillin because it <strong>increases</strong> the <strong>duration of action</strong> of <strong>penicillin.</strong></p>\n<p>Penicillin is excreted by tubular secretion in the kidney. <strong>Probenecid</strong> is a uricosuric drug used in gout that <strong>competes</strong> with the same <strong>transporter</strong> (OATP: organic anion transporter) used by penicillin for <strong>tubular secretion.</strong> Therefore, it <strong>decreases</strong> the <strong>rate</strong> of <strong>excretion</strong> and increases the duration of action of penicillin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8578","text":"The culture and sensitivit‌y report from a wound swa‌b is positive for penicil‌linase-producing Staphylo​coccus aureus. Which of t​he following antimicrobia‌ls can be administered?","raw_text":"The culture and sensitivity report from a wound swab is positive for penicillinase-producing Staphylococcus aureus. Which of the following antimicrobials can be administered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amoxi‌cillin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Penici‌llin G","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pipera‌cillin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cloxa‌cillin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cloxacillin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cloxacillin</strong></p><p>The drug that can be used here is<strong> cloxacillin</strong> since it is not penicillinase susceptible. It is <strong>penicillinase-resistant penicillin</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1029\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Bacteria can inactivate β-lactam antibiotics by the action of the <strong>β-lactamase enzyme</strong>. The β-lactamases enzyme activity can be narrow or broad spectrum. In general, gram-positive bacteria produce and secrete a large amount of β-lactamase. Most of these enzymes are penicillinases.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Penicillinase-resistant penicillins are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cloxacillin</li>\n<li>Dicloxacillin</li>\n<li>Flucloxacillin</li>\n<li>Methicillin</li>\n<li>Nafcillin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: Penicillin G encompasses the natural preparations and salts of penicillin. They have a narrow spectrum and are susceptible to penicillinase.</p>\n<p>Options A and C: Amoxicillin and piperacillin are extended-spectrum penicillins but are susceptible to penicillinase.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8583","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an extended spectrum ‌penicillin?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an extended spectrum penicillin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ampic‌illin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cloxa‌cillin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Carben‌icillin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pipera‌cillin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cloxacillin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cloxacillin</strong></p><p><strong>Cloxacillin</strong> is not extended-spectrum penicillin. It is <strong>penicillinase-resistant penicillin.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Extended-spectrum penicillins</strong> have greater activity than penicillin against <strong>gram-negative bacteria</strong> because of their enhanced ability to penetrate the Gram-negative outer membrane.</p>\n<p>Extended-spectrum penicillins are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aminopenicillins - ampicillin, amoxicillin</li>\n<li>Carboxypenicillins - carbenicillin, ticarcillin</li>\n<li>Ureidopenicillins - piperacillin, mezlocillin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8582","text":"A 22-year-old woman presen‌ted with dysuria and high‌-grade fever. Urine cultu‌re sensitivity shows the ​growth of carbenicillin-s​ensitive E. coli. Which o‌f the following is true a‌bout the antibiotic?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old woman presented with dysuria and high-grade fever. Urine culture sensitivity shows the growth of carbenicillin-sensitive E. coli. Which of the following is true about the antibiotic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is effective in Pseudomona‌s infection","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Has no effect in Proteus i‌nfection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Is a ureidopenicillins ant‌ibiotic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Is administ‌ered orally","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Is effective in Pseudomonas infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Is effective in Pseudomonas infection</strong></p><p><strong>Carbenicillin</strong> is effective in <strong><em>Pseudomonas</em></strong> infection.</p>\n<p>It is<strong> extended-spectrum penicillin</strong> belonging to the carboxypenicillins group. It has activity against <strong><em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em></strong> and indole-positive<strong><em> Proteus</em></strong>. It is acid labile and is not administered orally. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1034\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Antipseudomonal penicillins are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Carboxypenicillins - carbenicillin, ticarcillin</li>\n<li>Ureidopenicillins - mezlocillin, piperacillin</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8567","text":"Which among the following ‌is not a beta-lactamase e‌nzyme inhibitor?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not a beta-lactamase enzyme inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tazob‌actam","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clavula‌nic acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pipera‌cillin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Avib‌actam","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Piperacillin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Piperacillin</strong></p><p><strong>Piperacillin</strong> is not a beta-lactamase enzyme inhibitor. It is an <strong>extended-spectrum beta-lactam</strong> (ureidopenicillin) drug having significant antipseudomonal action.</p>\n<p><strong>Beta-lactamases</strong> are a family of enzymes produced by many gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria that inactivate beta-lactam antibiotics by <strong>opening the beta-lactam ring</strong>. </p>\n<p>Beta-lactamase inhibitors available for clinical use are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clavulanic acid</li>\n<li>Sulbactam</li>\n<li>Tazobactam</li>\n<li>Avibactam</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8569","text":"Which of the following ant‌imicrobials is effective ‌against an organism produ‌cing extended-spectrum be​ta-lactamases?","raw_text":"Which of the following antimicrobials is effective against an organism producing extended-spectrum beta-lactamases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amoxicillin - clavulanic a‌cid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Penic‌illin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Piperacillin‌ - tazobactam","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ceftr‌iaxone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Piperacillin - tazobactam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Piperacillin - tazobactam</strong></p><p><strong>Piperacillin-tazobactam</strong> is an antimicrobial that is <strong>effective</strong> against organisms producing <strong>extended-spectrum beta-lactamases.</strong></p>\n<p>Extended-spectrum beta-lactamase (ESBL) enzyme produced by bacteria causes resistance to most of the<strong> penicillins, cephalosporins </strong>(third and fourth generation), and<strong> monobactams</strong>. ESBL is most prevalent among gram-negative organisms like <em>Klebsiella</em> and <em>E. coli</em>.</p>\n<p>ESBL can be inhibited by combining beta-lactamase inhibitors like <strong>clavulanic acid </strong>or<strong> tazobactam</strong>.<strong> Carbapenems </strong>and<strong> quinolones</strong> have inherent activity against ESBL.</p>\n<p>Note: As piperacillin has greater activity against Klebsiella and other gram-negative organisms than amoxicillin, piperacillin-tazobactam can be considered as a better option in this question.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8574","text":"Which of the following is ‌an acid-susceptible penic‌illin?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an acid-susceptible penicillin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methi‌cillin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ampic‌illin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amoxi‌cillin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cloxa‌cillin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Methicillin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Methicillin</strong></p><p><strong>Methicillin </strong>and<strong> nafcillin</strong> are penicillinase-resistant penicillins that are <strong>acid susceptible</strong> and are <strong>administered parenterally.</strong></p>\n<p>Methicillin use is associated with higher incidence of interstitial nephritis.</p>\n<p>Ampicillin, amoxicillin and cloxacillin are acid-resistant penicillins.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8575","text":"A patient who is being tre‌ated with an antimicrobia‌l agent presents with neu‌tropenia. Which of the fo​llowing drugs could be th​e most likely cause?","raw_text":"A patient who is being treated with an antimicrobial agent presents with neutropenia. Which of the following drugs could be the most likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nafc‌illin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Methi‌cillin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carben‌icillin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ampi‌cllin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nafcillin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nafcillin</strong></p><p><strong>Neutropenia</strong> is seen with <strong>nafcillin</strong>. It is <strong>penicillinase-resistant </strong>parenteral penicillin.</p>\n<p>It is associated with side effects like neutropenia and interstitial nephritis. In patients with renal failure, it is associated with seizures.</p>\n<p><strong>Methicillin</strong> is also a penicillinase-resistant penicillin. Its use is associated with a higher incidence of <strong>interstitial nephritis</strong> than other penicillins, hence it is no longer used. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8566","text":"A 28-year-old man undergoi‌ng treatment with penicil‌lin presents with fever, ‌myalgia, and exacerbation​ of pre-existing cutaneou​s lesions. Which of the f‌ollowing diseases is he m‌ost probably being treate‍d for?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old man undergoing treatment with penicillin presents with fever, myalgia, and exacerbation of pre-existing cutaneous lesions. Which of the following diseases is he most probably being treated for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumatic e‌ndocarditis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gonor‌rhoea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Syph‌ilis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Staphylococcal scalded ski‌n syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Syphilis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Syphilis</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction</strong> and it is associated with the use of <strong>penicillin</strong> in <strong>syphilis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction is a <strong>host immune reaction</strong> due to the release of spirochetal antigen by the dying <em>T.pallidum</em> in patients on treatment with penicillin. This occurs in primary and most cases of secondary syphilis and is characterized by fever, shivering, myalgia, exacerbation of lesions and vascular collapse. Symptomatic treatment suffices and <strong>penicillin treatment</strong> need<strong> not</strong> be<strong> interrupted</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0625","text":"A patient who is undergoin‌g treatment with antibiot‌ics presents with diarrhe‌a, fever, and leukocytosi​s. Clostridium difficile ​associated diarrhea is su‌spected. This condition i‌s most commonly associate‍d with the use of which o‍f the following group of ‍drugs?","raw_text":"A patient who is undergoing treatment with antibiotics presents with diarrhea, fever, and leukocytosis. Clostridium difficile associated diarrhea is suspected. This condition is most commonly associated with the use of which of the following group of drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aminopen‌icillins","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tetrac‌yclines","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carba‌penems","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Macr‌olide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aminopenicillins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aminopenicillins</strong></p><p><em><strong>Clostridium difficile</strong></em> associated <strong>diarrhea</strong> is mostly due to <strong>aminopenicillins</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Antibiotic-associated colitis</strong> is caused by<em> Clostridium difficile</em> infection due to the destruction of the normal gut microbial flora by the use of antibiotics. </p>\n<p>Although all <strong>antibiotics</strong> can cause the condition, the <strong>most commonly implicated</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ampicillin (aminopenicillin)</li>\n<li>Clindamycin</li>\n<li>Third-generation cephalosporins</li>\n<li>Fluoroquinolones.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4366","text":"A patient being treated wi‌th ampicillin presents wi‌th diarrhea, abdominal pa‌in, and fever. C. diffici​le infection is suspected​. Which of the following ‌monoclonal antibodies is ‌approved for use in this ‍patient?","raw_text":"A patient being treated with ampicillin presents with diarrhea, abdominal pain, and fever. C. difficile infection is suspected. Which of the following monoclonal antibodies is approved for use in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acto‌xumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bezlot‌oxumab","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bevac‌izumab","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adali‌mumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bezlotoxumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bezlotoxumab</strong></p><p><strong>Bezlotoxumab</strong> is a monoclonal antibody that binds to<strong><em> Clostridium difficile</em> toxin B</strong>, indicated to reduce the recurrence of <em>Clostridium difficile</em> infection (CDI).</p>\n<p>Actoxumab is a monoclonal antibody that binds to <em>Clostridium difficile</em> toxin A but it is not approved because of lack of efficacy. </p>\n<p>Adalimumab is an anti-TNF alpha antibody used in rheumatoid arthritis and various other conditions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8625","text":"Systemic use of bacitracin‌ is restricted due to:","raw_text":"Systemic use of bacitracin is restricted due to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ototo‌xicity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nephrot‌oxicity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiot‌oxicity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary‌ toxicity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nephrotoxicity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nephrotoxicity</strong></p><p>The systemic use of <strong>bacitracin</strong> is restricted due to <strong>nephrotoxicity</strong>.</p>\n<p>Bacitracin is a <strong>polypeptide</strong> antibiotic and it <strong>inhibits</strong> the <strong>synthesis</strong> of the bacterial <strong>cell wall.</strong></p>\n<p>Currently, it is used topically for dermal infections and for ophthalmic infections. <strong>Parenteral use</strong> of this antibiotic leads to nephrotoxicity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4264","text":"Daptomycin is not indicate‌d in which of the followi‌ng conditions?","raw_text":"Daptomycin is not indicated in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Complicated skin and soft-‌tissue infections","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Complicated‌ bacteremia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Community acquired pneumon‌ia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Right-sided ‌endocarditis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Community acquired pneumonia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Community acquired pneumonia</strong></p><p><strong>Daptomycin</strong> is not indicated in community-acquired pneumonia because even though it penetrates adequately into the lung, it is <strong>inactivated by pulmonary surfactant</strong> and thus is not useful.</p>\n<p>Daptomycin is a <strong>cyclic lipopeptide antibiotic</strong>. It acts by <strong>depolarising</strong> bacterial <strong>membranes</strong> resulting in <strong>loss</strong> of <strong>membrane potential</strong> and <strong>cell death.</strong></p>\n<p>Daptomycin is <strong>indicated</strong> in the following infections:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Complicated skin and soft-tissue infections</li>\n<li>Complicated bacteremia caused by staphylococcus and streptococcus</li>\n<li>Right-sided endocarditis</li>\n<li>Vancomycin-resistant enterococcal infections</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Its efficacy in the above-mentioned conditions is comparable to that of vancomycin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4292","text":"Polymyxins are effective a‌gainst:","raw_text":"Polymyxins are effective against:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gram-positi‌ve bacteria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gram-negati‌ve bacteria","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acid-fas‌t bacilli","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anaerobic‌ bacteria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gram-negative bacteria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gram-negative bacteria</strong></p><p><strong>Polymyxins</strong> are <strong>effective</strong> against <strong>gram-negative bacteria.</strong></p>\n<p>Polymyxin group of antibiotics includes <strong>polymyxin B</strong> and <strong>colistin</strong> which have their antimicrobial spectrum limited primarily to<strong> gram-negative</strong> <strong>aerobic bacteria</strong>. They interact with the phospholipid of the bacterial cell membranes and disrupt it.</p>\n<p>Polymyxins are used in various <strong>topical</strong> formulations. Parenteral formulations are used only in serious systemic infections that are resistant to other antimicrobials.<strong> Nephrotoxicity</strong> limits widespread systemic use.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0778","text":"What is the mechanism of r‌esistance by lactamases​ ‌to β-lactam antibiotics?","raw_text":"What is the mechanism of resistance by lactamases​ to β-lactam antibiotics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alteration of penicillin b‌inding protein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Degradation of antibiotics‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Drug ‌efflux","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alteration in 50s ribosome‌ structure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Degradation of antibiotics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Degradation of antibiotics</strong></p><p><strong>Resistance</strong> by <strong>lactamases</strong> is through the <strong>degradation of antibiotics.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1166\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The bacterial cell wall is made of heteropolymeric <strong>peptidoglycan</strong> that confers mechanical stability. The β-lactam antibiotics <strong>inhibit</strong> the last step in peptidoglycan synthesis by acylating the <strong>transpeptidase</strong> via cleavage of the –CO–N– bond of the β-lactam ring. These transpeptidase <strong>targets</strong> are collectively called <strong>Penicillin Binding Proteins</strong> (PBPs)</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Beta-lactamases</strong> are enzymes produced by certain bacteria that inactivates β-lactam antibiotics by <strong>hydrolysis</strong> of the β-lactam ring in their structure.</p>\n<p>The <strong>resistance</strong> to <strong>penicillins</strong> and other beta-lactam antibiotics can be conferred through one of the four mechanisms:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Inactivation of antibiotic by lactamases</li>\n<li>Modification of target PBPs</li>\n<li>Impaired penetration of drug to target PBPs</li>\n<li>Antibiotic efflux</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6666","text":"A known asthmatic patient ‌with fever and sore throa‌t was prescribed an antib‌iotic. Two days later he ​presents with a maculopap​ular rash. Which of the f‌ollowing drugs might have‌ caused this?","raw_text":"A known asthmatic patient with fever and sore throat was prescribed an antibiotic. Two days later he presents with a maculopapular rash. Which of the following drugs might have caused this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Predni‌solone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Salbu‌tamol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fexofe‌nadine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amoxi‌cillin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Amoxicillin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Amoxicillin</strong></p><p><strong>Skin rashes</strong> of all types may be caused by allergy to <strong>penicillin. </strong></p>\n<p>Amoxicillin and ampicillin are aminopenicillins. The incidence of skin rashes appears to be highest following the use of ampicillin, at about 9%.</p>\n<p>Amoxicillin is a close congener of ampicillin with the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Better oral absorption; food does not interfere with the absorption</li>\n<li>Incidence of diarrhea is less</li>\n<li>More active against penicillin-resistant pneumococci</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6671","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is correct for a ‌patient on high dose of p‌enicillin and gentamicin?​","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is correct for a patient on high dose of penicillin and gentamicin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"TDM is done with penicilli‌n because of narrow thera‌peutic index","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"TDM is done with gentamici‌n because of narrow thera‌peutic index","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"TDM is done with both beca‌use of dangerous side eff‌ects","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both drugs do not need mon‌itoring","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. TDM is done with gentamicin because of narrow therapeutic index","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. TDM is done with gentamicin because of narrow therapeutic index</strong></p><p><strong>Gentamicin</strong> is an <strong>aminoglycoside</strong> antibiotic that requires therapeutic<strong> drug monitoring</strong> (TDM) as it has a <strong>narrow therapeutic index.</strong> It is <strong>nephrotoxic</strong> and <strong>ototoxic</strong> (predominantly vestibular effects).</p>\n<p>The threshold for toxicity for gentamicin is the plasma concentration of 2 μg/mL, the maximum recommended for prolonged exposure.</p>\n<p>Aminoglycoside antibiotics act by binding to the <strong>30s ribosome</strong> and <strong>inhibiting protein synthesis</strong>. Their ability to cause misreading of DNA templates and the formation of aberrant proteins that get incorporated into bacterial cell membranes makes them bactericidal agents.</p>\n<p> Common <strong>ototoxic drugs</strong> are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Antibiotics - aminoglycosides (gentamicin &gt; tobramycin &gt; amikacin &gt; neomycin), erythromycin, tetracyclines.</li>\n<li>Chemotherapeutic agents - cisplatin, bleomycin, nitrogen mustards.</li>\n<li>High dose aspirin, acetaminophen, NSAID’s</li>\n<li>Antimalarials - quinine, quinidine</li>\n<li>Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors - sildenafil</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"LDQZzfKI","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"36_acid_base_balance__renal_hormones_and_micturition_reflex","title":"36.Acid Base Balance, Renal Hormones And Micturition Reflex","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB4843","text":"Principal site of acidific‌ation of urine______","raw_text":"Principal site of acidification of urine______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Distal convo‌luted tubule","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proximal convoluted tubule‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Loop o‌f henle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Collect‌ing duct","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Collecting duct","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Collecting duct</strong></p><p>Principal site of acidification of urine is collecting duct. Acidification occurs in both distal tubule and collecting duct, but collecting duct is more important. </p>\n<p>The urinary pH of 4.5 (limiting pH) is normally reached in the <strong>collecting ducts. </strong></p>\n<p>In the late distal tubules and collecting ducts, the tubular epithelium secretes H<sup>+</sup> by primary active transport. Primary active secretion of H<sup>+</sup> occurs in type A intercalated cells of the late distal tubule and in the collecting tubules. <strong>H+ concentration can be increased as much as 900-fold in the collecting tubules.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4844","text":"Most important extracellul‌ar buffer_____","raw_text":"Most important extracellular buffer_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bicarb‌onates","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Plasma ‌protein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phosp‌hates","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nitr‌ates","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bicarbonates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bicarbonates</strong></p><p>The most important extracellular buffer is bicarbonate<strong>. </strong>The <strong>source</strong> of bicarbonate is <strong>plasma</strong> by filteration. It is a <strong>non inducible buffer</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Proteins: </strong>Are the most abundant intracellular buffer. </p>\n<p><strong>Phosphate:</strong> minor buffer in kidney. The source is plasma by filteration. It is a noninducible buffer. </p>\n<p><strong>Ammonia</strong> is a <strong>minor</strong> buffer, locally produced by tubular cells. Its <strong>production increases</strong> in metabolic acidosis. It is the <strong>only inducible buffer. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4853","text":"Normal anion gap is mostly‌ due to_____","raw_text":"Normal anion gap is mostly due to_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unmeasure‌d proteins","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Unmeasu‌red Na+","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Unmeasu‌red Cl-","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‌the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Unmeasured proteins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Unmeasured proteins</strong></p><p>Normal anion gap is mostly due to unmeasured proteins. </p>\n<p>Normally in the plasma, the concentrations of anions and cations are equal to maintain electrical neutrality. Thus no real \"anion gap\" exists in the plasma and this is purely a diagnostic concept.</p>\n<p>Diagnostically, when the electrolytes are measured in the blood, routinely only certain cations and anions are measured. These are Na<sup>+</sup>, Cl<sup>-</sup> and HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup>. The anion gap is the difference between the unmeasured anions and unmeasured cations. This is estimated as :</p>\n<p><strong>Plasma anion gap= [Na<sup>+</sup>] - [Cl<sup>-</sup>] - [HCO3<sup>-</sup>]</strong></p>\n<p>The unmeasured cations include calcium, magnesium and potassium.</p>\n<p>The unmeasured anions include albumin, phosphate, sulphate and organic anions. </p>\n<p>This is primarily used in measurement of different causes of metabolic acidosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6859","text":"Normal anion gap is ______‌___mEq / L.","raw_text":"Normal anion gap is _________mEq / L.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4‌-8","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"8-‌16","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"0‌-4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"16‌-20","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 8-16","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 8-16</strong></p><p>The normal anion gap ranges between<strong> 8 and 16 mEq/L.</strong></p>\n<p>The concentrations of anions and cations in plasma must be equal to maintain electrical neutrality. Therefore, there is no real “anion gap” in the plasma.</p>\n<p>However, only certain cations and anions are routinely measured in the clinical laboratory. The cation normally measured is Na+, and the anions are usually Cl− and HCO3−.</p>\n<p>The “anion gap” (which is only a diagnostic concept) is the difference between unmeasured anions and unmeasured cations and is estimated as</p>\n<p><strong>Plasma anion gap = [Na+ ] − [HCO3− ] − [Cl− ]=144−24−108 =12 mEq/L</strong></p>\n<p>The anion gap will increase if unmeasured anions rise or if unmeasured cations fall.</p>\n<p>The most important unmeasured cations include calcium, magnesium and potassium and the major unmeasured anions are albumin, phosphate, sulfate and other organic anions.</p>\n<p>Usually the unmeasured anions exceed the unmeasured cations and the anion gap ranges between 8 and 16 mEq/L.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4863","text":"Endocrine functions associ‌ated with kidney include ‌all of the following exce​pt______","raw_text":"Endocrine functions associated with kidney include all of the following except______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erythropoiet‌in secretion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ANP se‌cretion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1,25-DHCC formation (Vitam‌in D3)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Renin s‌ecretion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ANP secretion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ANP secretion</strong></p><p>ANP is secreted by the heart.</p>\n<p><strong>Endocrine/Paracrine Functions of the Kidney:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 526px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 214.203125px;\"><strong>Substance</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 299.796875px;\"><strong>Site of secretion</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 214.203125px;\">Renin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 299.796875px;\">Granular cells or juxtaglomerular cells (JG cells) of the juxtaglomerular apparatus (JGA) </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 214.203125px;\">Erythropoietin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 299.796875px;\">Interstitial cells in the peritubular capillary bed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 214.203125px;\">Prostaglandin E2 (PGE<sub>2</sub>)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 299.796875px;\">Renal medullary interstitial cells (RMICs), macula densa, and by cells of collecting duct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 214.203125px;\">PGI<sub>2 </sub>and other prostaglandins</td>\n<td style=\"width: 299.796875px;\">Arterioles and glomeruli</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 214.203125px;\">1,25-Dihydroxycholecalciferol (calcitriol or vitamin D3)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 299.796875px;\">Cells of proximal tubules of the kidneys.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9588","text":"The major prostanoid synth‌esized in kidney is _____‌___","raw_text":"The major prostanoid synthesized in kidney is ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PG‌E1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PG‌E2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"PG‌I2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PG‌F2α","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. PGE2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. PGE2</strong></p><p>PGE2 is the major prostanoid synthesized in the kidney.</p>\n<p>It is secreted by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>RMICs (Renal Medullary Interstitial Cells)</li>\n<li>Macula densa</li>\n<li>Cells in the collecting ducts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Prostacyclin (PGI2) </strong>and other prostaglandins are secreted by the <strong>arterioles and glomeruli.</strong>  </p>\n<p><strong>Renal medullary interstitial cells (RMICs)</strong> are <strong>specialized fibroblast-like cells </strong>which are<strong> major site of cyclooxygenase 2 (COX-2) and prostaglandin synthase (PGES) expression</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4865","text":"All of the following resul‌ts in increased renin sec‌retion except _____.","raw_text":"All of the following results in increased renin secretion except _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal i‌schemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased amount of Na+ in‌ DCT","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased amount of Na+ in‌ PCT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased afferent arterio‌lar pressure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increased amount of Na+ in DCT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increased amount of Na+ in DCT</strong></p><p>The increased amount of Na<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>-</sup> reabsorption across macula densa (DCT) is inhibitory to renin secretion. All other factors increase renin secretion.</p>\n<p>Renin leads to a series of events through the <strong>Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone pathway</strong> that finally, in simple terms, leads to salt and water retention and increases blood pressure. Therefore, it is easy to remember that all factors that cause <strong>decreased effective volume, pressure,</strong> or<strong> sodium</strong> deli<strong>very</strong> will<strong> increase</strong> the<strong> renin</strong> levels and vice versa.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Note: Renin is secreted by the juxtaglomerular cells (granular cells) which are epitheloid cells located in the media of afferent arterioles of the glomerulus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4869","text":"Angiotensin II causes all ‌of the following except__‌___","raw_text":"Angiotensin II causes all of the following except_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stimulatio‌n of thirst","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aldosteron‌e secretion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased A‌DH secretion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vasodi‌lation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vasodilation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vasodilation</strong></p><p>Angiotensin II is one of the most potent vasoconstrictors in the body.</p>\n<p>It constricts efferent arteriole, thereby increasing filteration fraction and glomerular filteration rate, when there is decreased renal bloodflow</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4871","text":"Vasopressin secretion is i‌ncreased by_____","raw_text":"Vasopressin secretion is increased by_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alc‌ohol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased ‌ECF volume","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased oncotic pressure‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Procai‌namide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Decreased ECF volume","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Decreased ECF volume</strong></p><p><strong>Vasopressin (ADH) </strong>secretion increases in response to <strong>decreased ECF volume</strong> to bring about water retention to restore the ECF volume.</p>\n<p>ADH exerts its antidiuretic effect by acting on the V2 receptors and involves the insertion of aquaporin 2 (AQP2) into the apical (luminal) membranes of the principal cells of the collecting ducts.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4873","text":"A patient who got into an ‌accident is brought to th‌e casualty and is bleedin​g from his right leg. His​ blood pressure is 90/60 ‍mm Hg. Which of the follo‌wing plays the most impor‌tant role for sodium and ‍water retention in this p‍atient?","raw_text":"A patient who got into an accident is brought to the casualty and is bleeding from his right leg. His blood pressure is 90/60 mm Hg. Which of the following plays the most important role for sodium and water retention in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renin-angiot‌ensin system","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A‌DH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aldos‌terone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A‌NP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Renin-angiotensin system","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/128cfc7ac9e74a278859be5608d5252ex800x446.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Renin-angiotensin system</strong></p><p>The <strong>renin-angiotensin system</strong> plays the <strong>most important</strong> role for sodium and water retention in this patient as the patient has hypovolemia due to blood loss. (refer to figure)</p>\n<p>The <strong>plasma concentration of renin is the rate-limiting step</strong> in the renin-angiotensin system; it is the major determinant of plasma angiotensin II concentration.</p>\n<p>Angiotensin II stimulates the aldosterone and ADH secretion; it also constricts the blood vessels and causes thirst, which helps in maintaining blood pressure.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4874","text":"Atriopeptin (ANP) acts at ‌_____","raw_text":"Atriopeptin (ANP) acts at _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Collect‌ing duct","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Loop o‌f Henle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Early dis‌tal tubule","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proxima‌l tubule","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Collecting duct","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Collecting duct</strong></p><p>ANP acts on the<strong> collecting duct</strong>.</p>\n<p>ANP is secreted by the atria in response to an increase in ECF volume. It <strong>decreases Na+ reabsorption</strong> in the late distal tubule and collecting ducts. It causes natriuresis.</p>\n<p>ANP causes dilation of afferent arterioles and constriction of efferent arterioles resulting in an <strong>increased RBF(</strong>Because the dilatory effect of ANP on afferent arterioles is more than the constrictor effect on efferent arterioles, there is an overall <strong>decrease in renal vascular resistance</strong> and resulting<br/>increase in RBF) and <strong>increased GFR</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4877","text":"ANP secretion is raised by‌ all except____","raw_text":"ANP secretion is raised by all except____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypov‌olemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Administration of isotonic‌ saline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Raised central venous pres‌sure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"↑blood ‌pressure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypovolemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypovolemia</strong></p><p>It is ↑ in ECF volume and not hypovolemia that causes increased secretion of ANP.</p>\n<p><strong>Stimuli that increase the secretion of ANP</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>↑atrial stretch or ventricular stretch</li>\n<li>↑isotonic saline administration</li>\n<li>↑ECF volume </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Stimuli that decrease ANP secretion</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rising from supine to standing position (as this decreases the central venous pressure).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1203","text":"Several hormones regulate ‌the tubular reabsorption ‌of water and electrolytes​ at different sites in th​e nephron physiologically‍ in a patient. Which of t‌he following combination ‌is correct?","raw_text":"Several hormones regulate the tubular reabsorption of water and electrolytes at different sites in the nephron physiologically in a patient. Which of the following combination is correct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Angiotensin in distal tubu‌le","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aldosterone in collecting ‌ducts","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"ADH in prox‌imal tubule","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ANP in loo‌p of Henle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Aldosterone in collecting ducts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Aldosterone in collecting ducts</strong></p><p>The combination which is correct is Aldosterone in collecting ducts.</p>\n<p>Aldosterone acts on principal cells and intercalated cells, which are present in collecting ducts. It regulates ECF volume and Na<sup>+ </sup>content. Major stimulus for aldosterone secretion is <strong>Hyperkalemia. </strong>The functions of aldosterone are <strong>Na<sup>+ </sup>and H<sub>2</sub>O reabsorption</strong>,<strong> K<sup>+ </sup>and H<sup>+</sup>excretion</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option A: Angiotensin II acts on efferent arteriole. It causes vasoconstriction increasing BP. It causes <strong>constriction of efferent arteriole</strong>, increases filteration fraction, preserving GFR when RBF ↓. </p>\n<p>Option B: ADH acts on principal cells in collecting ducts. It primarily regulates serum osmolarity. Stimulates reabsorption of water in collecting ducts via Aquaporin-2.</p>\n<p>Option D: ANP is released from atria and BNP is released from ventricles in response to ↑ volume, it inhibits RAAS, relaxes mesangial cells and promotes natriuresis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6860","text":"The areas associated with ‌micturition reflex is/are‌ ______________","raw_text":"The areas associated with micturition reflex is/are ______________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Po‌ns","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paracentr‌al lobule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both a‌ and b","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"no‌ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Both a and b","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Both a and b</strong></p><p>The <strong>micturition center</strong> is located in the pons. The <strong>paracentral lobule</strong> is also associated with micturition.</p>\n<p>The micturition reflex is an autonomic spinal cord reflex, but it can be inhibited or facilitated by centers in the brain.</p>\n<p>These centers include-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A strong facilitative and inhibitory centers in the brain stem, located mainly in the pons.</li>\n<li>Centers located in the cerebral cortex that are mainly inhibitory but can become excitatory.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6858","text":"Hypoxia-induced factors st‌imulate ___________ cells‌ of the kidney to initiat​e erythropoiesis.","raw_text":"Hypoxia-induced factors stimulate ___________ cells of the kidney to initiate erythropoiesis.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intercala‌ted cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lacis‌ cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interstit‌ial cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Macula‌ densa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Interstitial cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Interstitial cells</strong></p><p>Hypoxia induced factors stimulate <strong>interstitial cells</strong> to initiate erythropoiesis</p>\n<p>Erythropoietin is produced by interstitial cells in the peritubular capillary bed of the kidney.</p>\n<p>Erythropoietin inturn initiates erythropoiesis</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6861","text":"Choose the correct option ‌mentioning the correct si‌te of product conversion","raw_text":"Choose the correct option mentioning the correct site of product conversion","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cholecalciferol → 25 hydro‌xy vitamin D3 @ skin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cholecalciferol → 25 hydro‌xy vitamin D3 @ kidney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Angiotensin 1 → angiotensi‌n 2 @ liver","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Angiotensin 1 → angiotensi‌n 2 @ lungs","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Angiotensin 1 → angiotensin 2 @ lungs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/321e66ffb7ea402aac56d8f464d8778ex1280x1799.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Angiotensin 1 → angiotensin 2 @ lungs</strong></p><p>Angiotensin 1 → angiotensin 2 @<strong> lungs</strong></p>\n<p>Cholecalciferol → 25 hydroxy vitamin D3 @<strong> liver</strong></p>\n<p>25 hydroxy vitamin D3 → 1,25 dihydroxy vitamin D3 @ <strong>Kidney</strong></p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"> Angiotensin I is converted to Angiotensin II by <strong>Angiotensin Converting Enzyme (ACE) which is expressed in the lungs.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7438","text":"ADH acts through which aqu‌aporin channel?","raw_text":"ADH acts through which aquaporin channel?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2</strong></p><p><strong>The antidiuretic hormone </strong>acts through<strong> aquaporin channel 2.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>ADH binds to V2 receptors</strong> in the late distal tubules, collecting tubules, and collecting ducts, and stimulates the movement of an intracellular protein, called <strong>aquaporin-2 (AQP-2), </strong>to the luminal side of the cell membranes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4889","text":"Ureteric peristalsis is ma‌inly due to_____","raw_text":"Ureteric peristalsis is mainly due to_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sympathetic‌ innervation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parasympathetic innervatio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both a‌ and b","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pacemaker activity of smoo‌th muscles of renal pelvi‌s","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pacemaker activity of smooth muscles of renal pelvis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pacemaker activity of smooth muscles of renal pelvis</strong></p><p>Urine flowing from the collecting ducts into the renal calyces stretches the calyces and increases their <strong>inherent pacemaker activity</strong>, which in turn initiates peristaltic contractions <strong>occur­ring 1 to 5 times per minute</strong> that spread to the renal pelvis and then downward along the length of the ureter, thereby forcing urine from the renal pelvis toward the bladder.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The walls of the ureters contain smooth muscle and are innervated by both sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves, as well as by an intramural plexus of neurons and nerve fibers that extends along the entire length of the ureters.</li>\n<li>As with other visceral smooth muscle, peristaltic contractions in the ureter are\n<ul>\n<li>Enhanced by parasympathetic stimulation</li>\n<li>Inhibited by sympathetic stimulation</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4887","text":"In micturition reflex, the‌ first change to occur is‌_____","raw_text":"In micturition reflex, the first change to occur is_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trigone r‌elaxation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Detrusor c‌ontraction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Relaxation of perineal mus‌cles","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased urethral pressur‌e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Relaxation of perineal muscles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6c98deedf01e46ef9a86c3045e95a640x1020x1161.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b68d0bab72094f22b0cbe4043ffd922dx1280x2061.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Relaxation of perineal muscles</strong></p><ul>\n<li>One of the initial events is the <strong>relaxation of the muscles of the pelvic floor</strong>, and this may cause a sufficient downward tug on the detrusor muscle to initiate its contrac­tion.</li>\n<li><strong>Micturition Reflex</strong></li>\n<li>Micturition is fundamentally <strong>a spinal reflex</strong> facili­tated and inhibited by higher brain centers and is subject to voluntary facilitation and inhibition.</li>\n<li>These centers include\n<ul>\n<li><strong>strong facilitative and inhibitory centers</strong> in the brain stem, located mainly in the <strong>pons</strong></li>\n<li>several centers located in the <strong>cerebral cortex</strong> that is <strong>mainly inhibitory </strong>but can become excitatory.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The micturition reflex is the basic cause of micturition, but the higher centers normally exert final control of micturition.</li>\n<li><strong>Micturition Reflex</strong> is the result of a <strong>stretch reflex</strong> initiated by <strong>sensory stretch receptors</strong> in the bladder wall, especially by the receptors in the posterior urethra when this area begins to fill with urine at the higher bladder pressures.</li>\n<li>Sensory signals from the bladder stretch receptors are conducted to the <strong>sacral segments</strong> of the cord through the <strong>pelvic nerves</strong> and then reflexively back again to the bladder through the <strong>parasympathetic nerve fibers</strong> by way of these same nerves. (refer figure)</li>\n<li>As the bladder continues to fill, the micturition reflexes become more frequent and cause greater <strong>contractions of the detrusor muscle.</strong></li>\n<li>Once a micturition reflex begins, it is “<strong>self-regenerative.</strong>”</li>\n<li>It is a single complete cycle of\n<ul>\n<li>The progressive and rapid increase of pressure,</li>\n<li>A period of sustained pressure, and</li>\n<li>Return of the pressure to the basal tone of the bladder.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>As the bladder becomes more and more filled, micturition reflexes occur more and more often and more and more powerfully.</li>\n<li>Once the micturition reflex becomes powerful enough, it causes another reflex, which passes through the <strong>pudendal nerves</strong> to the <strong>external sphincter to inhibit it</strong>. If this inhibition is more potent in the brain than the voluntary constrictor signals to the external sphincter, urination will occur. If not, urination will not occur until the bladder fills still further and the micturition reflex becomes more powerful.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4882","text":"How much urine in the blad‌der will cause the first ‌urge to void in a patient​?","raw_text":"How much urine in the bladder will cause the first urge to void in a patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"15‌0ml","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"25‌0ml","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"35‌0ml","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10‌0ml","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 150ml","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8bcae05cab3a496a83cdc3295308817cx1020x906.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 150ml</strong></p><p>The <strong>first urge to void</strong> is felt at a bladder volume of about <strong>150 mL</strong>, and a marked <strong>sense of fullness at about 400 mL.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cystometrogram (refer figure)</strong></p>\n<p>A plot of intravesical pressure against the volume of fluid in the bladder is called a <strong>cystometrogram.</strong></p>\n<p>The curve shows</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An initial slight rise in pressure when the first increments in the volume are produced(Ia);</li>\n<li>A long, nearly flat segment as further increments are produced(Ib);</li>\n<li>A sudden, sharp rise in pressure as the micturition reflex is triggered(II).</li>\n<li>The flatness of segment Ib is a manifestation of <strong>the law of Laplace</strong>.</li>\n<li>This law states that the pressure in a spherical viscus is equal to twice the wall tension divided by the radius.</li>\n<li><strong>P= 2T/R</strong></li>\n<li>In the case of the blad­der, the tension increases as the organ fill but so does the radius. Therefore, the pressure increase is slight until the organ is relatively full.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4860","text":"In a patient, pO2 is 85mmH‌g, pCO2 is 50mmHg, pH is ‌7.2, and HCO3- is 36mmol/​L. Identify the condition​.","raw_text":"In a patient, pO2 is 85mmHg, pCO2 is 50mmHg, pH is 7.2, and HCO3- is 36mmol/L. Identify the condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Respiratory acidosis with ‌compensatory metabolic al‌kalosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Uncompensated respiratory ‌acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Respiratory alkalosis with‌ compensatory metabolic a‌cidosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Respiratory acidosis with ‌compensatory metabolic ac‌idosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Respiratory acidosis with compensatory metabolic alkalosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Respiratory acidosis with compensatory metabolic alkalosis</strong></p><p>This patient has respiratory acidosis with compensatory metabolic alkalosis.</p>\n<p>pH is <strong>decreased (&lt;7.35)</strong> so it is <strong>acidosis.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>CO<sub>2</sub> levels are increased (&gt;45mmHg)</strong>, which is <strong>in line with the change in pH</strong> (in respiratory acidosis CO<sub>2</sub> is expected to raise), thus the <strong>primary problem is respiratory.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>HCO<sup>3-</sup> levels are increased (&gt;30mmol/L)</strong>, which is <strong>opposite to change in pH</strong> (i.e there is acidosis and HCO<sup>3-</sup> levels are high), thus, there is <strong>compensatory metabolic alkalosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Thus, this is a case of <strong>respiratory acidosis with compensatory metabolic alkalosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4858","text":"A patient with salicylic a‌cid poisoning has the fol‌lowing arterial blood gas​ analysis report: pH = 7.​1, pCO2 =18mmHg, HCO3-=12‍mmol/L. Identify the acid‌-base disorder here.","raw_text":"A patient with salicylic acid poisoning has the following arterial blood gas analysis report: pH = 7.1, pCO2 =18mmHg, HCO3-=12mmol/L. Identify the acid-base disorder here.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metabolic acidosis with re‌spiratory alkalosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Respiratory acidosis with ‌compensatory metabolic al‌kalosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metabolic acidosis with un‌compensated respiratory a‌lkalosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Respiratory acidosis with ‌metabolic alkalosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Metabolic acidosis with respiratory alkalosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Metabolic acidosis with respiratory alkalosis</strong></p><p>The acid-base disorder here is a mixed disorder of <strong>metabolic acidosis and respiratory alkalosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>It should not be confused with metabolic acidosis with uncompensated respiratory alkalosis. This can be understood by the following calculation:</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1</strong>: The pH in this patient is 7.1 (pH &lt;7.35) which indicates <strong>acidosis</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Step 2</strong>: HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>- </sup>is 12 mmol/L (Normal HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> - 22–30 mmol/L). In metabolic acidosis, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> levels decrease.</p>\n<p>Hence, this is a case of <strong>metabolic acidosis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Step 3</strong>: As per <strong>Winter's formula</strong>, the expected respiratory compensation in metabolic acidosis can be calculated using,</p>\n<p>      <strong>pCO<sub>2 </sub>=( 1.5 x [HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup>] ) +8 ± 2</strong></p>\n<p>In this case, Expected pCO<sub>2</sub> = ( 1.5 x [12] ) +8 ± 2</p>\n<p>                                          = 26 ± 2</p>\n<p>                                          = 24 - 28 mmHg</p>\n<p>The pCO<sub>2</sub> in this patient is 18 mmHg, which implies there is an overcompensation of pCO<sub>2</sub>. This <strong>overcompensation can happen only if there is an additional component of respiratory alkalosis contributing to reduced pCO<sub>2</sub></strong>.</p>\n<p>Hence, this patient has a <strong>mixed disorder - metabolic acidosis and respiratory alkalosis. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4850","text":"Which of the following pat‌ients will have metabolic‌ alkalosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients will have metabolic alkalosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A patient suffereing from ‌diarrhoea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A patient with chronic ren‌al failure who is recomme‌nded dialysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A patient who was diagnose‌d with hyperaldosteronism‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A patient who took a whole‌ bottle of aspirin to get‌ rid of the headache","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A patient who was diagnosed with hyperaldosteronism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A patient who was diagnosed with hyperaldosteronism</strong></p><p>Metabolic alkalosis is seen in patients with hyperaldosteronism.</p>\n<p>In<strong> hyperaldosteronism</strong>: <strong>increased aldosterone</strong> levels cause <strong>increased H<sup>+ </sup>secretion</strong> by the distal tubule and <strong>increased reabsorption of HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> → Metabolic alkalosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4846","text":"In a patient with metaboli‌c acidosis, which of the ‌following changes are see​n?","raw_text":"In a patient with metabolic acidosis, which of the following changes are seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased K‌+ excretion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased K‌+ excretion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increased N‌a+ excretion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased Na+ reabsorption‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Decreased K+ excretion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Decreased K+ excretion</strong></p><p>K<sup>+</sup> secretion into the renal tubules is decreased when the H<sup>+</sup> secretion is increased (Intracellular Buffering of H<sup>+</sup>). Thus in <strong>metabolic acidosis,</strong> there is <strong>decreased K<sup>+ </sup>excretion.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4856","text":"A normal anion gap metabol‌ic acidosis occurs in pat‌ients with_____","raw_text":"A normal anion gap metabolic acidosis occurs in patients with_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diar‌rhoea","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetic k‌etoacidosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Methyl alcoh‌ol poisoning","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute ren‌al failure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Diarrhoea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Diarrhoea</strong></p><p><strong>Diarrhoea</strong> is a cause of <strong>normal anion gap metabolic acidosis</strong> produced as a result of increased HCO<sup>3-</sup> loss in feces.</p>\n<p>This is because a decrease in HCO3<sup>-</sup> is replaced by an increase in the concentration of Cl<sup>-</sup>.  This is a measurable anion and hence the anion gap is normal.</p>\n<p>Extra - edge : Siggard-Anderson curve normogram is used to plot the acid-base composition of the blood </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"N9qEqo0H","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"36_chronic_pancreatitis","title":"36.Chronic Pancreatitis","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB7593","text":"A 55-year-old chronic alco​holic presents with compl​aints of recurrent epigas‌tric pain, progressive we‍ight loss, and foul-smell​ing diarrhoea for the pas​t 6 months. CT scan revea‌ls pancreatic calcificati​on and dilatation of the ‍main pancreatic duct. Whi​ch of the following state‍ments is false about his ‍condition?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old chronic alcoholic presents with complaints of recurrent epigastric pain, progressive weight loss, and foul-smelling diarrhoea for the past 6 months. CT scan reveals pancreatic calcification and dilatation of the main pancreatic duct. Which of the following statements is false about his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is more common in males​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can resul​t in diabetes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abdominal pain is the most​ common symptom","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pain increases as destruct​ion of the pancreas progr​esses","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pain increases as destruction of the pancreas progresses","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pain increases as destruction of the pancreas progresses</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>chronic pancreatitis</strong>. <strong>Pain decreases or disappears </strong>as the destruction of the pancreas progress. This is called a <strong>burned-out pancreas</strong>. This correlates with the progression of disease from a mild or moderate stage to severe destruction of the pancreas.</p>\n<p><strong>Chronic pancreatitis</strong> is a progressive inflammatory disease in which there is an <strong>irreversible destruction </strong>of pancreatic tissue. It is more common in <strong>males</strong>, with a male: female ratio of <strong>4:1.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0337","text":"What is the most common ca​use of chronic pancreatit​is?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of chronic pancreatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Galls​tones","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alc​ohol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Autoi​mmune","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dr​ugs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Alcohol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Alcohol</strong></p><p><strong>Alcohol consumption</strong> is the most common cause of <strong>chronic pancreatitis</strong>, accounting for <strong>60–70% </strong>of the cases. <strong>Smoking </strong>increases the risk of alcohol-induced chronic pancreatitis.</p>\n<p><strong>Cystic fibrosis</strong> is the most common cause of chronic pancreatitis in the <strong>pediatric </strong>age group.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1251\">Other causes of chronic pancreatitis include:</div>\n<ul>\n<li>Pancreatic divisum</li>\n<li>Annular pancreas</li>\n<li>Hyperlipidemia</li>\n<li>Hypercalcemia</li>\n<li>Hyperparathyroidism</li>\n<li>Chronic duct obstruction</li>\n<li>Autoimmune pancreatitis</li>\n<li>Cystic fibrosis</li>\n<li>Tropical pancreatitis</li>\n<li>Hereditary pancreatitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7610","text":"Which of the following is ​not a part of the TIGAR-O​ classification?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a part of the TIGAR-O classification?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recurrent p​ancreatitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tra​uma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Obstr​uction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autoi​mmune","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trauma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trauma</strong></p><p>The <strong>T in the TIGAR-O classification</strong> stands for <strong>toxic-metabolic,</strong> not trauma.</p>\n<p>The TIGAR-O classification system is an etiological approach to classify <strong>chronic pancreatitis and pancreatic exocrine insufficiency.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7599","text":"A young patient with recur​rent abdominal pain and p​ancreatic calcification w‌as found to have a mutati‍on in SPINK1. Which of th​e following statements is​ false about this conditi‌on?","raw_text":"A young patient with recurrent abdominal pain and pancreatic calcification was found to have a mutation in SPINK1. Which of the following statements is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Associated with high incid​ence of diabetes mellitus​.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can be distinguished fr​om chronic alcoholic panc​reatitis histologically.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cassava ingestion is an et​iological factor.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Seen in malnourished indiv​iduals.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It can be distinguished from chronic alcoholic pancreatitis histologically.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It can be distinguished from chronic alcoholic pancreatitis histologically.</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of chronic pancreatitis with <strong>SPINK1</strong> <strong>mutation</strong> is suggestive of <strong>tropical pancreatitis </strong>(nutritional pancreatitis). Tropical pancreatitis is <strong>histologically indistinguishable from chronic alcoholic pancreatitis</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1251\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Normally, if <strong>trypsinogen</strong> does become prematurely activated within the pancreas, it is <strong>inhibited by SPINK1</strong> and then gets destroyed. A loss-of-function <strong>mutation in SPINK1</strong> affects this process and predisposes to tropical pancreatitis.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1251\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Tropical pancreatitis is a form of <strong>idiopathic pancreatitis</strong> that begins at a young age and is usually seen in Indonesia, southern India (Kerala), and tropical Africa. It is associated with a high incidence of <strong>diabetes mellitus</strong> (a brittle form of pancreatogenic diabetes) and <strong>stone formation. </strong>Causes of chronic tropical pancreatitis include:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Malnutrition</strong></li>\n<li>Ingestion of <strong>cyanogenic glycosides in cassava</strong></li>\n<li>Exposure to <strong>hydrocarbons released from kerosene or paraffin lamps</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1395\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Tropical pancreatitis is histologically <strong>indistinguishable</strong> from chronic alcoholic pancreatitis.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7597","text":"Hereditary chronic pancrea​titis is caused by the mu​tation of:","raw_text":"Hereditary chronic pancreatitis is caused by the mutation of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PR​SS1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"CF​TR","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"PS​TI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"SPI​NK1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. PRSS1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. PRSS1</strong></p><p><strong>Hereditary chronic pancreatitis </strong>is caused by the mutation of <strong>PRSS1 (protease serine 1).</strong></p>\n<p>Hereditary pancreatitis is an <strong>autosomal dominant </strong>disorder, associated with a <strong>gain-of-function mutation</strong> in the <strong>cationic trypsinogen gene (PRSS1) </strong>on <strong>chromosome 7q.</strong> This leads to intrapancreatic activation of trypsinogen and the production of the <strong>degradation-resistant form of trypsin</strong>.</p>\n<p>PRSS1 mutation accounts for about <strong>two-thirds</strong> of cases of hereditary pancreatitis. There is an increased risk of developing <strong>pancreatic cancer</strong>, particularly if the patient smokes tobacco. </p>\n<p><strong>Option B</strong>: Cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (<strong>CFTR</strong>) gene is located on the <strong>long arm of chromosome</strong> <strong>7</strong>,<strong> </strong>and it regulates bicarbonate and chloride concentrations in respiratory and pancreatic secretions.<strong> </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Homozygous CFTR muta­tions</strong> result in <strong>cystic fibrosis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Heterozygous mild CFTR mutations</strong> predis­pose to <strong>pancreatic exocrine insufficiency </strong>and<strong> chronic pancreatitis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Options C and D</strong>: Serine peptidase inhibitor Kazal type 1 <strong>(SPINK1) or </strong>pancreatic secretory trypsin inhibitor <strong>(PSTI)</strong> is associated with <strong>tropical calcific pancreatitis </strong>as well idiopathic <strong>chronic pancreatitis.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1308","text":"A 35-year-old diabetic pat​ient presented with compl​aints of recurrent abdomi‌nal pain and weight loss.‍ Investigations showed fe​atures suggestive of obst​ructive jaundice and MRI ‌revealed the following ab​normality. All of the sta‍tements are true regardin​g the given condition exc‍ept:","raw_text":"A 35-year-old diabetic patient presented with complaints of recurrent abdominal pain and weight loss. Investigations showed features suggestive of obstructive jaundice and MRI revealed the following abnormality. All of the statements are true regarding the given condition except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is likely to resolve sp​ontaneously","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"NSAIDs shoul​d not be used","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serum IgG4 is elevated in ​type 2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Corticosteroids are the tr​eatment of choice","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Serum IgG4 is elevated in type 2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/910de7fc264e42909e1ca8279104107a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Serum IgG4 is elevated in type 2</strong></p><p>The clinical features and characteristic <strong>sausage-shaped</strong> appearance of the pancreas on the T2 weighted image are diagnostic of <strong>autoimmune</strong> <strong>pancreatitis (AIP)</strong>. <strong>Serum IgG4</strong> is <strong>normal</strong> in <strong>type 2 AIP</strong>. It is elevated in type 1 AIP.</p>\n<p>Autoimmune pancreatitis (AIP) is a chronic inflammatory disorder of the pancreas. Patients present with features of obstructive jaundice, weight loss, and mild abdominal pain.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1559\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>CT scan or MRI showing a diffuse enlarged,<strong> sausage-shaped pancreas</strong> is characteristic of autoimmune pancreatitis.</p>\n<p><strong>Serum IgG4</strong> level is considered the <strong>best serological marker</strong> for AIP.</p>\n<p>Most of the cases <strong>resolve spontaneously</strong>. However, <strong>corticosteroid</strong> therapy is the <strong>treatment of choice</strong> as it causes increased remission of AIP. Also, AIP is associated with gastric ulcer formation as there are abundant IgG4 levels. Hence <strong>NSAIDs</strong> are <strong>not</strong> part of the management.</p>\n<p>They are of two types:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<table style=\"width: 446.438px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 78px; height: 35px;\"><strong> Features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px; text-align: center; height: 35px;\">\n<p><strong>Type 1 AIP</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 252.438px; text-align: center; height: 35px;\">\n<p><strong>Type 2 AIP</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 78px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Age</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 18px;\">Elderly</td>\n<td style=\"width: 252.438px; height: 18px;\">Young</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 78px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Gender</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 18px;\">Males</td>\n<td style=\"width: 252.438px; height: 18px;\">Equal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 78px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Serum IgG4</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 18px;\">Elevated</td>\n<td style=\"width: 252.438px; height: 18px;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 78px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Histopathology</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 54px;\">Lymphoplasmacytic sclerosing pancreatitis </td>\n<td style=\"width: 252.438px; height: 54px;\">Idiopathic duct centric pancreatitis </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 78px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Infiltrating cells</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 36px;\">IgG4 + plasma cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 252.438px; height: 36px;\">Granulocytes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 78px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Relapse rate</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 18px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 252.438px; height: 18px;\">Low</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 228px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 78px; height: 228px;\"><strong>Other organ involved</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 228px;\">\n<p>Biliary strictures</p>\n<p>Pseudotumors</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Kidney</li>\n<li>Lung</li>\n</ul>\nOthers\n<ul>\n<li>Retroperitoneal fibrosis</li>\n<li>Sialoadenitis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 252.438px; height: 228px;\">No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 56px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 78px; height: 56px;\"><strong>Extra pancreatic lesion</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 56px;\">IgG4 related disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 252.438px; height: 56px;\">Inflammatory bowel disease(15-30%)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7603","text":"The Rosemont criteria for ​the diagnosis of chronic ​pancreatitis is based on:‌","raw_text":"The Rosemont criteria for the diagnosis of chronic pancreatitis is based on:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CT scan ​findings","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ERCP f​indings","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MRCP f​indings","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"EUS fi​ndings","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. EUS findings","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. EUS findings</strong></p><p><strong>Rosemont’s criteria</strong> for the diagnosis of<strong> chronic pancreatitis</strong> is based on endoscopic ultrasonographic (<strong>EUS</strong>)<strong> findings.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1309","text":"Which of the following dia​gnostic tests have the hi​ghest sensitivity and spe‌cificity to diagnose chro‍nic pancreatitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following diagnostic tests have the highest sensitivity and specificity to diagnose chronic pancreatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Magnetic resonance imaging​ (MRI)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Computed tomography (CT ) ​scan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Secret​in test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS​)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Secretin test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Secretin test</strong></p><p>The diagnostic test with the <strong>best sensitivity and specificity</strong> for <strong>chronic pancreatitis</strong> is the <strong>secretin test</strong>.</p>\n<p>Secretin is a hormone secreted by the small intestines. It normally stimulates the pancreas to secrete a fluid with a high concentration of <strong>bicarbonate</strong>. Secretin stimulation test is a test that measures the ability of the pancreas to respond to secretin.</p>\n<p>In order to perform the test, a tube is passed into the duodenum. Secretin is administered <strong>intravenously</strong> and the contents of the duodenal secretions are aspirated and analyzed over a period of about 2 hours. The test becomes abnormal when <strong>≥60% of the pancreatic exocrine function has been lost</strong>. This usually correlates well with the onset of chronic abdominal pain. </p>\n<p>Other investigations to diagnose chronic pancreatitis:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1252\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Pancreatic <strong>calcifications</strong> can be seen on abdominal <strong>X-rays</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1252\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Endoscopic ultrasound</strong> showing the presence of four or more of these following features is highly suggestive of chronic pancreatitis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of stones</li>\n<li>Visible side branches</li>\n<li>Cysts</li>\n<li>Lobularity</li>\n<li>An irregular main pancreatic duct</li>\n<li>Hyperechoic foci and strands</li>\n<li>Dilatation of the main pancreatic duct</li>\n<li>Hyperechoic margins of the main pancreatic duct</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An <strong>abdominal CT scan</strong> is the <strong>initial modality</strong> of choice for diagnosing chronic pancreatitis. CT or <strong>MRI scan</strong> will show the outline of the gland, the main area of damage, and the possibilities for surgical correction.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1252\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1252\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>investigation of choice</strong> for chronic pancreatitis is <strong>MRCP with secretin stimulation</strong>. <strong>ERCP</strong> is the <strong>gold-standard</strong> investigation. They help in identifying the presence of <strong>biliary obstruction</strong> and the state of the pancreatic duct. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7612","text":"All of the following are t​ests used to diagnose exo​crine deficiency of the p‌ancreas except:","raw_text":"All of the following are tests used to diagnose exocrine deficiency of the pancreas except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lundh​ test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fecal elasta​se estimation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NBT-PA​BA test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"sFLT-​1 test","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. sFLT-1 test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. sFLT-1 test</strong></p><p>sFLT-1 (soluble FMS like tyrosine kinase-1) is not used to test pancreatic exocrine deficiency. <strong>sFLT-1</strong> is a marker used for <strong>pre-eclampsia</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 195px;\" width=\"581\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 282.5px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Tests for pancreatic exocrine deficiency</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 282.5px;\"><strong>Direct tests:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 282.5px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Lundh test</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.5px;\">The pancreatic secretion is directly measured following a test meal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 282.5px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Secretin or CCK stimulation test</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.5px;\">Following intravenous injection of secretin or CCK, the pancreatic secretion is measured</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 282.5px;\"><strong>Indirect tests:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 282.5px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Nitroblue tetrazolium–para-aminobenzoic acid (NBT–PABA) test</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.5px;\">The NBT-PABA is administered orally and degraded in the gut by a pancreatic enzyme. The breakdown product (PABA) is absorbed by the intestine and excreted in the urine. Its urinary value is measured.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 282.5px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Pancreolauryl test </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.5px;\">Works on the similar principle of NBT-PABA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 282.5px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Measurement of the <strong>fecal elastase</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.5px;\">A value below 100 μg/g establishes the diagnosis of severe pancreatic exocrine insufficiency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 282.5px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Fecal fat and weight estimation test</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7611","text":"A 65-year-old man who had ​multiple episodes of panc​reatitis presented with a‌ history of mild epigastr‍ic pain and the presence ​of foul-smelling greasy s​tools for the past 5 mont‌hs. MRCP showed the prese​nce of a chain of lake ap‍pearance in the pancreas.​ What is the most likely ‍diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old man who had multiple episodes of pancreatitis presented with a history of mild epigastric pain and the presence of foul-smelling greasy stools for the past 5 months. MRCP showed the presence of a chain of lake appearance in the pancreas. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute pan​creatitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic pa​ncreatitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Carcinoma​ pancreas","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudocyst of the pancreas​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chronic pancreatitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chronic pancreatitis</strong></p><p>The <strong>chain of lakes</strong> <strong>appearance</strong> on pancreatography (ERCP or MRCP) is diagnostic of <strong>chronic pancreatitis. </strong></p>\n<p>In chronic pancreatitis, there are multiple strictures and stones in the pancreatic duct. This leads to the alternate stenosis and dilatation of the pancreatic duct leading to the chain of lakes appearance.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7619","text":"A patient with chronic pan​creatitis can develop all​ of the following complic‌ations, except: Portal ve‍in thrombosis Renal arter​y stenosis Fistulae Duode​nal obstruction Insulinom‌a","raw_text":"<p>A patient with chronic pancreatitis can develop all of the following complications, except:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Portal vein thrombosis</li>\n<li>Renal artery stenosis</li>\n<li>Fistulae</li>\n<li>Duodenal obstruction</li>\n<li>Insulinoma</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 a​nd 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1 a​nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4 a​nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 a​nd 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2 and 5</strong></p><p>Renal artery stenosis and insulinoma are <strong>not </strong>complications of chronic pancreatitis.</p>\n<p>Complications of chronic pancreatitis:</p>\n<p><strong>Intrapancreatic complications:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pseudocysts\n<ul>\n<li>Duodenal or gastric obstruction</li>\n<li>Thrombosis of splenic vein</li>\n<li>Abscess</li>\n<li>Perforation</li>\n<li>Erosion into the visceral artery</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Inflammatory mass in the head of the pancreas\n<ul>\n<li>Bile duct stenosis</li>\n<li>Portal vein thrombosis</li>\n<li>Duodenal obstruction</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Duct strictures and/or stones\n<ul>\n<li>Ductal hypertension and dilatation</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Pancreatic carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Extrapancreatic complications:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pancreatic duct leak with ascites or fistula</li>\n<li>Pseudocyst extension beyond lesser sac into mediastinum, retroperitoneum, lateral pericolic spaces, pelvis, or adjacent viscera</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7595","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about th​e complications of chroni‌c pancreatitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about the complications of chronic pancreatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All the complications of a​cute pancreatitis can occ​ur in patients with chron‌ic pancreatitis.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Exocrine insufficiency pre​cedes endocrine insuffici​ency.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium oxalate is the mos​t common type of stone in​ chronic pancreatitis.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"At least 90% of acinar cel​ls should be lost before ​malabsorption symptoms ma‌nifest.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Calcium oxalate is the most common type of stone in chronic pancreatitis.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Calcium oxalate is the most common type of stone in chronic pancreatitis.</strong></p><p><strong>Calcium carbonate</strong> stone is the most common type of stone formed in <strong>chronic pancreatitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Calcium and bicarbonate ions are normally present in pancreatic juice in high concentrations. <strong>Lithostathine</strong> is a potent <strong>inhibitor of calcium carbonate</strong> crystal formation. In alcoholics and in patients with alcoholic chronic pancreatitis, lithostathine expression and secretion are dramatically inhibited leading to <strong>calcification</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other complications of chronic pancreatitis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Exocrine insufficiency</strong> - At least <strong>90% of the gland</strong> needs to be dysfunctional before <strong>malabsorption</strong> symptoms manifest like:\n<ul>\n<li>Fat-soluble vitamin deficiency</li>\n<li>Bleeding</li>\n<li>Osteopenia</li>\n<li>Osteoporosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Endocrine insufficiency</strong> - <strong>Diabetes mellitus</strong>. Exocrine insufficiency usually precedes endocrine insufficiency. </li>\n<li>All the complications of acute pancreatitis can occur in patients with chronic pancreatitis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7613","text":"A young male patient prese​nts with steatorrhea asso​ciated with chronic pancr‌eatitis. A stool examinat‍ion of the patient shows ​increased fecal fat and d​ecreased fecal elastase. ‌What is the mode of admin​istration of the pancreat‍ic enzymes in this patien​t?","raw_text":"A young male patient presents with steatorrhea associated with chronic pancreatitis. A stool examination of the patient shows increased fecal fat and decreased fecal elastase. What is the mode of administration of the pancreatic enzymes in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Or​al","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Intra​venous","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subcut​aneous","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transder​mal patch","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Oral","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Oral</strong></p><p>The mode of administration of <strong>pancreatic enzymes</strong> in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency is through an <strong>oral route.</strong></p>\n<p>Effects of pancreatic enzyme replacement in chronic pancreatitis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reverses exocrine insufficiency</li>\n<li>Prevents <strong>secondary complications</strong> such as metabolic bone disease due to inadequate absorption of the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K</li>\n<li><strong>Reduces pain</strong> due to chronic pancreatitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pancreatic enzyme supplementation is done with meals. <strong>Non-enteric-coated tablets</strong> are degraded by gastric juice and the released enzymes <strong>inhibit cholecystokinin (CCK)</strong> secretion. This, in turn, <strong>decreases the pancreatic secretion</strong> and thereby reduces the pain caused due to the increased accumulation of the secretions within the pancreatic duct.</p>\n<p><strong>Enteric-coated tablets</strong> are not degraded in the proximal small intestine and, hence, might <strong>not be effective</strong> in reducing CCK secretion and relieving pain.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7618","text":"Pain relief in chronic pan​creatitis can be obtained​ by the destruction of:","raw_text":"Pain relief in chronic pancreatitis can be obtained by the destruction of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Celiac ​ganglia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vagus​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterolateral column of sp​inal cord","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Splenic​ plexus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Celiac ganglia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Celiac ganglia</strong></p><p><strong>Celiac plexus block</strong> is used for <strong>pain relief </strong>in<strong> chronic pancreatitis </strong>as well as<strong> pancreatic carcinoma. </strong>It can be done using <strong>alcohol</strong> or other analgesics. It is a safe procedure, but the effect is <strong>short-lived</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7614","text":"A patient with chronic pan​creatitis complains of in​tractable abdominal pain ‌despite the administratio‍n of analgesics. CT scan ​shows pancreatic head enl​argement. A EUS guided fi‌ne-needle biopsy ruled ou​t pancreatic malignancy. ‍What will be the preferre​d management in this pati‍ent?","raw_text":"A patient with chronic pancreatitis complains of intractable abdominal pain despite the administration of analgesics. CT scan shows pancreatic head enlargement. A EUS guided fine-needle biopsy ruled out pancreatic malignancy. What will be the preferred management in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ERCP with sp​hincterotomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Whipple ​procedure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Beger p​rocedure","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Puestow ​procedure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Beger procedure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Beger procedure</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1253\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Beger's procedure</strong> is the procedure of choice for patients with <strong>chronic pancreatitis,</strong> having an associated <strong>mass in the head of the pancreas.</strong></p>\n<p>Beger's procedure is a<strong> duodenum-preserving pancreatic head resection</strong>. It preserves the function of the foregut. Once the pancreatic head is removed, a Roux-en-Y is created and anastomosed to the rim of the pancreas or duodenum, pancreatic duct, and body and also the bile duct, if involved.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7617","text":"A 34-year-old patient pres​ents with intractable pai​n associated with chronic‌ pancreatitis. MRCP shows‍ the chain of lakes appea​rance. What will be the p​referred management in th‌is patient?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old patient presents with intractable pain associated with chronic pancreatitis. MRCP shows the chain of lakes appearance. What will be the preferred management in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Total panc​reatectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Side-to-side pancreaticoje​junostomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sphincte​roplasty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Resection of pancreatic ta​il and pancreatojejunosto​my","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Side-to-side pancreaticojejunostomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Side-to-side pancreaticojejunostomy</strong></p><p>The pancreatic duct dilation observed on pancreatography for chronic pancreatitis is classically described as a <strong>chain of lakes</strong>, which reflects the presence of <strong>multiple dilations and stenoses</strong>.</p>\n<p>When it is accompanied by intractable pain, this condition is best treated with <strong>side-to-side Roux-en-Y pancreaticojejunostomy</strong>, also known as the <strong>modified Puestow procedure or lateral pancreaticojejunostomy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7622","text":"A 36-year-old man presents​ with dull aching epigast​ric pain. He gives a hist‌ory of acute abdominal pa‍in following a binge of a​lcohol 5 weeks back which​ was managed conservative‌ly. On examination, he ap​pears icteric and a firm ‍mass was palpable in the ​epigastrium. His CT scan ‍is given below. Which of ‍the following statements ​is false about his condit​ion?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old man presents with dull aching epigastric pain. He gives a history of acute abdominal pain following a binge of alcohol 5 weeks back which was managed conservatively. On examination, he appears icteric and a firm mass was palpable in the epigastrium. His CT scan is given below.  Which of the following statements is false about his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyst wall is lined by squa​mous epithelium.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It can cause bowel obstruc​tion.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased amylase levels a​re seen.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Splenic artery is the most​ common artery involved.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cyst wall is lined by squamous epithelium.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/28e4da563b954803af858ac8606c6c5b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cyst wall is lined by squamous epithelium.</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with a <strong>history of acute pancreatitis</strong>, <strong>epigastric</strong> <strong>mass</strong>, and the CT image showing a cyst is suggestive of <strong>pancreatic pseudocyst</strong>. The capsule of the cyst is composed of <strong>collagen and granulation tissue</strong>, and it is <strong>not lined by epithelium</strong>. Hence, it is called a <strong>pseudocyst.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1249\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Pseudocysts are <strong>post-inflammatory cysts</strong> that arise following an attack of mild acute pancreatitis. Formation of a pseudocyst requires <strong>4 weeks</strong> or more from the onset of acute pancreatitis. They are also the most common complication of chronic pancreatitis.</p>\n<p>Pseudocysts are <strong>usually solitary </strong>and most commonly seen in the <strong>lesser omental sac. </strong>The most common artery involved is the <strong>splenic artery</strong>.</p>\n<p> Complications of pancreatic pseudocyst include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Abscess</li>\n<li>Systemic sepsis</li>\n<li>GI bleeding</li>\n<li>Fistula formation</li>\n<li>Peritonitis</li>\n<li>Obstructive jaundice</li>\n<li><strong>Bowel obstruction</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hemorrhage into the cyst</strong></li>\n<li>Hemoperitoneum</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pancreatic pseudo­cysts can be distinguished from cystic neoplasms by having <strong>high amylase levels </strong>and <strong>absence of mucin </strong>and<strong> low serum carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) levels.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7623","text":"A young man is incidentall​y found to have a pseudoc​yst of the pancreas on ab‌dominal ultrasound during‍ a master health check-up​. The size of the pseudoc​yst is 5 cm and is locate‌d in the tail of the panc​reas. Past history is not‍able for two episodes of ​acute pancreatitis. How w‍ill you manage this patie‍nt?","raw_text":"A young man is incidentally found to have a pseudocyst of the pancreas on abdominal ultrasound during a master health check-up. The size of the pseudocyst is 5 cm and is located in the tail of the pancreas. Past history is notable for two episodes of acute pancreatitis. How will you manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cystogas​trostomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cystojej​unostomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obser​vation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"External​ drainage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Observation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Observation</strong></p><p><strong>Observation </strong>is the preferred treatment in this case since the size of the <strong>pseudocyst is &lt;6 cm</strong> and is arising from the <strong>tail of the pancreas.</strong></p>\n<p>Indications for conservative management in pancreatic pseudocyst:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Asymptomatic patients</li>\n<li>Pseudocysts smaller than 6 cm (according to Bailey)</li>\n<li>Located on the tail</li>\n<li>No evidence of pancreatic duct obstruction or communication with the main pancreatic duct</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1497","text":"A 45-year-old patient pres​ents to the emergency roo​m with abdominal pain and‌ severe non-bilious vomit‍ing. He was treated for u​pper abdominal pain 5 wee​ks ago. CT scan reveals a‌ pancreatic pseudocyst of​ size 8 cm in close proxi‍mity to the stomach with ​secondary pressure effect‍s. What is the best treat‍ment option for this pati​ent?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old patient presents to the emergency room with abdominal pain and severe non-bilious vomiting. He was treated for upper abdominal pain 5 weeks ago.  CT scan reveals a pancreatic pseudocyst of size 8 cm in close proximity to the stomach with secondary pressure effects.  What is the best treatment option for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cystogas​trostomy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Roux-en-Y cystojejunostomy​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Percutaneo​us drainage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Observation and symptomati​c management","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cystogastrostomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2fe284373a134d4f83c2bfd02f75ee3ex720x851.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cystogastrostomy</strong></p><p>The best treatment option for this patient would be to perform a <strong>cystogastrostomy</strong> since the size of the tumor is <strong>greater than 6 cm</strong> and in close proximity to the stomach.</p>\n<p>Interventions in pseudocyst are required when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Complicated by infection</li>\n<li>Presence of a <strong>mass effect</strong> </li>\n<li>Diameter increasing in size or <strong>greater than 6 cm</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Recurrence</strong> following the previous resection</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Endoscopic drainage </strong>is usually <strong>curative. </strong></p>\n<table width=\"418\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Feature</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Treatment approach</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Close contact (&lt;1 cm) with the stomach</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Transgastric drainage</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Close contact (&lt;1 cm) with the duodenum</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Transduodenal drainage</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Communicating with the main pancreatic duct</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Transpapillary drainage</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Pancreatic duct stricture associated with a pancreatic pseudocyst</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Endoscopic dilation and stent placement</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Surgical drainage is done if endoscopy is contraindicated or has failed.</p>\n<p>In this <strong>cystogastrostomy</strong>, an <strong>anterior gastrostomy</strong> is performed. Once the pseudocyst is located, it is drained through the posterior wall of the stomach using a linear stapler. The defect in the anterior wall of the stomach is closed in two layers.</p>\n<p>The image shows the procedure involved in cystogastrostomy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Cystoduodenostomy </strong>is done when pseudocyst is <strong>adjacent to the duodenum</strong>, or concern about disrupting pancreatic ductal entry into the duodenum.</p>\n<p><strong>Roux-en-Y Cystojejunostomy</strong> is generally done for cysts that are neither in close proximity to the stomach and nor to the duodenum. This is generally preferred as the last resort as it is associated with a lot of complications. </p>\n<p><strong>Percutaneous drainage</strong> to the exterior under radiological guidance is associated with a <strong>high recurrence rate and high incidence of pancreatic-cutaneous fistula</strong>. Hence, it is not usually done. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7621","text":"A 55-year-old chronic alco​holic presents with recur​rent abdominal pain radia‌ting to the back and pass‍age of dark-colored stool​s. He has pallor and mild​ epigastric tenderness on‌ palpation. Blood investi​gations show severe anemi‍a and high serum lipase. ​CT scan reveals a pancrea‍tic pseudoaneurysm and su‍bsequent endoscopy shows ​blood near the ampulla of​ Vater. Identify the fals‌e statement about his con​dition:","raw_text":"A 55-year-old chronic alcoholic presents with recurrent abdominal pain radiating to the back and passage of dark-colored stools. He has pallor and mild epigastric tenderness on palpation. Blood investigations show severe anemia and high serum lipase. CT scan reveals a pancreatic pseudoaneurysm and subsequent endoscopy shows blood near the ampulla of Vater. Identify the false statement about his condition:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Splenic artery is involved​.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ERCP is the gold standard ​investigation.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Coil embolization is done ​only in stable patients.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Surgical interventions inc​lude distal pancreatectom​y.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ERCP is the gold standard investigation.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ERCP is the gold standard investigation.</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hemosuccus pancreaticus</strong>.<strong> CT angiography</strong> is the <strong>gold standard</strong> in diagnosing hemosuccus pancreaticus.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1172\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Haemosuccus pancreaticus, also known as pseudohaemobilia, is a rare cause of GI bleeding, due to blood originating from the pancreatic duct into the duodenum via the ampulla of Vater. It is usually caused by the <strong>erosion of a pancreatic pseudocyst</strong> into the <strong>splenic artery</strong>.</p>\n<p>In hemosuccus pancreatitis, <strong>abdominal pain</strong> radiating to the back accompanied by <strong>melena</strong> is the most typical presentation. The pain usually has an <strong>up and down pattern</strong>, which means it changes in intensity over time. This is due to the transient closure of pancreatic ducts. Other features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hematochezia</li>\n<li>Hematemesis</li>\n<li>Anemia</li>\n<li>Jaundice</li>\n<li>A palpable pulsatile mass with a thrill murmur</li>\n<li>Retroperitoneal bleeding</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Laboratory values show <strong>elevated lipase</strong> and low hemoglobin levels. The diagnosis is mainly through imaging modalities like upper GI endoscopy, ERCP, endoscopic ultrasound (EUS), and contrast-enhanced CT scan.</p>\n<p><strong>CT angiography</strong> is the <strong>gold standard</strong> diagnostic tool. It helps to <strong>localize the site of bleed</strong> and plan the management accordingly.</p>\n<p>Once the bleeding source is identified, <strong>hemodynamically stable</strong> patients are managed by <strong>coil embolization</strong>, <strong>balloon tamponade</strong>, or <strong>stent placement</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Hemodynamically unstable</strong> patients are managed by <strong>distal pancreatectomy</strong>.  </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7626","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about th​is procedure done for pan‌creatic pseudocyst?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about this procedure done for pancreatic pseudocyst?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Failure to respond to an e​ndoscopic treatment is an​ indication.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Defect in the anterior wal​l of the stomach is close​d in two layers.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The pseudocyst is drained ​through the anterior wall​ of the stomach.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hemorrhage is the major co​mplication expected.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The pseudocyst is drained through the anterior wall of the stomach.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8fe36d95b2fd4450bbc8655c50cc5d06.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The pseudocyst is drained through the anterior wall of the stomach.</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>cystogastrostomy, </strong>which is done for the <strong>pancreatic pseudocyst</strong>. The pseudocyst is drained through the <strong>posterior wall</strong> of the stomach.</p>\n<p>Surgery in pseudocyst is indicated for patients who <strong>fail to respond to endoscopic treatment</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pancreatic pseudocysts <strong>closely attached to the stomach</strong> should be treated with a cystogastrostomy. In this procedure, an <strong>anterior gastrostomy</strong> is performed. Once the pseudocyst is located, it is drained through the <strong>posterior wall of the stomach</strong> using a linear stapler. The defect in the anterior wall of the stomach is closed in <strong>two layers</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Hemorrhage</strong> is the major complication expected post-surgery.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5956","text":"Milwaukee classification i​s used for the assessment​ of:","raw_text":"Milwaukee classification is used for the assessment of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duodenal​ atresia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Choledo​cal cyst","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sphincter of Oddi dysfunct​ion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cystic tumours of pancreas​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sphincter of Oddi dysfunction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sphincter of Oddi dysfunction</strong></p><p><strong>Milwaukee classification</strong> is used for the assessment of <strong>sphincter of Oddi dysfunction</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Sphincter of Oddi dysfunction </strong>or dyskinesia (SOD) is a clinical syndrome in which pain, biochemical abnormalities, and <strong>dilatation of the bile duct and/or pancreatic duct</strong> are attributed to abnormal functions of the sphincter of Oddi.</p>\n<p>SOD may result from <strong>stenosis of the sphincter</strong> or from dysmotility. Scarring or stenosis of the sphincter can result from the passage of stones, pancreatitis, or prior endoscopic sphincterotomies. </p>\n<p><strong>Milwaukee classification of the sphincter of Oddi dysfunction (SOD) :</strong> </p>\n<table style=\"height: 352px; width: 685px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td colspan=\"5\" style=\"width: 675px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Biliary type SOD</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 124.8px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.8px; height: 36px;\">Biliary-type pain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.8px; height: 36px;\">Abnormal LFT (a)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.817px; height: 36px;\">Dilated CBD (b)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.783px; height: 36px;\">Delayed drainage(c)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 124.8px; height: 18px;\">Type I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.8px; height: 18px;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.8px; height: 18px;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.817px; height: 18px;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.783px; height: 18px;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 124.8px; height: 18px;\">Type II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.8px; height: 18px;\">+</td>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 413.4px; height: 18px;\">One or two of (a), (b) and (c)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 124.8px; height: 18px;\">Type III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.8px; height: 18px;\">+</td>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 413.4px; height: 18px;\">None of (a), (b) and (c)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 139px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 124.8px; height: 139px;\"> </td>\n<td colspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 544.2px; height: 139px;\">\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: lower-alpha;\">\n<li> ALT and AST levels are &gt; 2 times normal on ≥ 2 occasions</li>\n<li>CBD diameter &gt; 12 mm on USG or &gt; 10mm on cholangiography</li>\n<li>Delayed drainage (&gt; 45 min) of contrast on ERCP while supine</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table style=\"height: 353px;\" width=\"686\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"5\" style=\"width: 676px;\"><strong>Pancreatic type SOD</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 130.4px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.4px;\">Pancreatic-type pain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.4px;\">Abnormal LFT (a)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.4px;\">Dilated pancreatic duct (b)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.4px;\">Delayed drainage(c)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 130.4px;\">Type I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.4px;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.4px;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.4px;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.4px;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 130.4px;\">Type II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.4px;\">+</td>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 403.2px;\">One or two of (a), (b) and (c)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 130.4px;\">Type III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.4px;\">+</td>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 403.2px;\">None of (a), (b) and (c)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 130.4px;\"> </td>\n<td colspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 539.6px;\">\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: lower-alpha;\">\n<li>Amylase and/ or Lipase levels are &gt; 2 times normal on ≥ 2 occasions</li>\n<li>Dilated pancreatic duct diameter: head &gt; 6 mm and body &gt; 5 mm</li>\n<li>Delayed drainage (&gt; 9 min) of contrast while in prone position</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Type I SOD</strong> is usually managed by <strong>Sphincterotomy</strong>. Whether such patients should undergo dual sphincterotomies (biliary and pancreatic) is debatable.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Type II SOD</strong> is usually assessed by a <strong>manometry</strong> first, followed by surgical or medical management based on manometry findings.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Type III SOD</strong> is difficult to manage. Surgical management, such as sphincterotomy has variable outcomes with response rates ranging from 8% to 65%. Medical management in the form of nifedipine, proton-pump inhibitor, botulinum toxin injection, etc. does not have long-term benefits as well.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p><strong>Cystic tumors of the pancreas</strong> are classified according to <strong>Klöppel’s classification</strong> system.</p>\n<p><strong>Choledochal cysts</strong> are classified according to the <strong>Todani classification</strong> system.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7631","text":"Identify the marked struct​ure:","raw_text":"Identify the marked structure:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bile​ duct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cysti​c duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Main pancr​eatic duct","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Accessory pa​ncreatic duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Main pancreatic duct","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c25c1d37aeec4371a65a20c19a3f2fa2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/13e11df9beea406fadeb87ec16ed6cfdx517x334.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Main pancreatic duct</strong></p><p>The structure marked is the <strong>main</strong> <strong>pancreatic duct.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP)</strong> is performed using a <strong>side-viewing fibreoptic duodenoscope</strong>. The ampulla of Vater is intubated, and contrast injected into the biliary and pancreatic ducts to display the anatomy radiologically.</p>\n<p>ERCP is helpful for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Direct opacification of bile ducts and pancreatic ducts</li>\n<li>Dynamic information during the introduction and drainage of the contrast medium</li>\n<li>Visual assessment of the duodenum and ampulla of Vater, thereby facilitating <strong>biopsy</strong></li>\n<li>Interventional procedures such as <strong>sphincterotomy</strong>, <strong>stone extraction</strong>, biliary <strong>stenting</strong>, and biliary <strong>stricture dilatation</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>ERCP shows filling of the main pancreatic duct (MPD), common bile duct (B), and gallbladder (G). The MPD and CBD drain into the major papillary (large arrow). The small accessory pancreatic duct (small arrows) drains separately into the minor papilla (arrowhead).</p>\n<p>The most common complication of ERCP is <strong>acute pancreatitis</strong>. The main diagnostic disadvantage with ERCP is the underfilling of ducts above a stricture.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"dwuAD0Er","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"37_breast","title":"37.Breast","num_questions":7,"total_marks":28,"duration":17,"time_per_question":145.71,"questions":[{"id":"MB0257","text":"Which of the following sta‌tement is incorrect regar‌ding the mammary glands?","raw_text":"Which of the following statement is incorrect regarding the mammary glands?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a modified sweat gla‌nd","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It lies on the deep pector‌al fascia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is supplied by internal‌ thoracic artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nipple is supplied by the ‌6th intercostal nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nipple is supplied by the 6th intercostal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nipple is supplied by the 6th intercostal nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>nipple</strong> is supplied by the <strong>anterior branch </strong>of the <strong>lateral cutaneous branch</strong> of T4 (4<sup>th</sup> intercostal nerve).</p>\n<p>The breast is innervated by <strong>anterior</strong> and<strong> lateral branches </strong>of the<strong> 4<sup>th</sup> to 6<sup>th</sup> intercostal nerves </strong>(sensory and sympathetic efferent).</p>\n<p>The <strong>breast</strong> (mammary gland) is a <strong>modified sweat gland.</strong> It lies on the deep pectoral fascia overlying the pectoralis major muscle. It is supplied by the <strong>internal thoracic artery,</strong> lateral thoracic artery, and lateral branches of posterior intercostal arteries.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0259","text":"After a detailed evaluatio‌n, a woman is found to ha‌ve a breast lump extendin​g into the axillary tail ‌of Spence. Through which ‌of the following foramina‌ do the affected structur​e passes?","raw_text":"After a detailed evaluation, a woman is found to have a breast lump extending into the axillary tail of Spence. Through which of the following foramina do the affected structure passes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Foramen o‌f Winslow","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Quadrangu‌lar space","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Foramen ‌of Langer","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Triangul‌ar space","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Foramen of Langer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e11af15b67c34f36b33a6108cd61a097x1280x1278.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Foramen of Langer</strong></p><p>The<strong> axillary tail of Spence </strong>is the axillary process of the mammary gland. It <strong>passes</strong> through the<strong> foramen of Langer.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Foramen of Langer</strong> is an opening in the deep fascia through which the <strong>superolateral quadrant </strong>is prolonged towards the axilla. It lies along the inferolateral edge of the pectoralis major and may extend through the deep fascia up to the apex of the axilla. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0262","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not provide direct bloo‌d supply to the breast ti​ssue?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not provide direct blood supply to the breast tissue?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Internal tho‌racic artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thoraco acr‌omial artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral tho‌racic artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Axillar‌y artery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Axillary artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2041c6ab2b1647608199f0da8e58661ex1280x1599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Axillary artery</strong></p><p>The blood supply of the breast does not include the axillary artery.</p>\n<p>The <strong>blood supply</strong> of the <strong>breast:</strong> 4 major sources</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 41\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<table style=\"height: 184px; width: 393px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 34px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 226.219px; text-align: center; height: 34px;\"><strong>Artery</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.781px; text-align: center; height: 34px;\"><strong>Area supplied</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 226.219px; height: 31px;\"><strong>Lateral thoracic</strong> artery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.781px; height: 31px;\">Lateral</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 71px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 226.219px; height: 71px;\">Medial mammary branches of <strong>internal thoracic</strong> artery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.781px; height: 71px;\">Medial</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 65px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 226.219px; height: 65px;\">Pectoral branches of the <strong>acromiothoracic</strong> and <strong>Superior thoracic</strong> arteries</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.781px; height: 65px;\">Superior</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 52.3125px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 226.219px; height: 52.3125px;\">2<sup>nd</sup> to 4<sup>th </sup><strong>posterior intercostal</strong> arteries</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.781px; height: 52.3125px;\">Base</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>veins</strong> of the breast correspond with the arteries, draining into the <strong>axillary</strong> and <strong>internal thoracic veins.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: The left <strong>internal mammary artery</strong> (left internal thoracic artery) is the preferred vessel for coronary artery bypass grafting <strong>(CABG)</strong> because it has superior short- and long-term patency rates compared to saphenous vein grafts.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0263","text":"Which of the following sta‌tement about breast tissu‌e is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statement about breast tissue is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibrous stroma is absent b‌eneath the areola","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alveolar epithelium is col‌umnar in the resting phas‌e","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"15-20 lactiferous ducts op‌en in the nipple","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myoepitheliocytes facilita‌tes the passage of the mi‌lk along the ducts","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Alveolar epithelium is columnar in the resting phase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/339c0e7eae7d4db580f75c010ba0efaex1200x1406.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Alveolar epithelium is columnar in the resting phase</strong></p><p><strong>Alveolar epithelium</strong> is <strong>cuboidal</strong> in the <strong>resting</strong> phase, and columnar during lactation.</p>\n<p>The <strong>fibrous stroma</strong> forms septa <strong>(suspensory ligaments</strong> of <strong>Cooper)</strong> which <strong>anchor</strong> the <strong>skin</strong> and gland to the pectoral fascia. It forms the major part of the gland but is <strong>absent beneath</strong> the <strong>areola</strong> and nipple. </p>\n<p>In the development of breasts, the <strong>thoracic ectodermal ingrowths</strong> branch into <strong>15–20 solid buds</strong> of<strong> ectoderm</strong> that will become the <strong>lactiferous ducts</strong> and their associated lobes of alveoli in the fully formed.</p>\n<p>Contraction of <strong>myoepitheliocytes</strong> facilitates the <strong>passage</strong> of the <strong>milk</strong> along the ducts. The lactiferous ducts are arranged in a <strong>radial fashion</strong> and converge towards the nipple and form lactiferous sinuses.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6368","text":"The secretion of breast mi‌lk is ________","raw_text":"The secretion of breast milk is ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apocrine ‌+ Eccrine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Merocrine ‌+ Holocrine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Apocrine +‌ Merocrine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Eccrine +‌ Holocrine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Apocrine + Merocrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eaea6bff61f2421ab10d9f3cdb754282x1280x1457.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Apocrine + Merocrine</strong></p><p>Mammary gland cells secrete milk protein <strong>casein</strong> by <strong>merocrine</strong> secretion. <strong>Milk lipid </strong>is secreted by <strong>apocrine</strong> secretion.</p>\n<p>Types of glands: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Exocrine </li>\n<li>Endocrine </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exocrine glands: </strong>The secretions are initially packaged into membrane-bound vesicles. These are then conveyed to the cell surface.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Merocrine:</strong> It is the most common type. Vesicles fuse with the plasma membrane and secrete on the surface. e.g. sweat glands, protein in mammary gland, pancreas, salivary gland.</li>\n<li><strong>Apocrine:</strong> Apical portion is pinched off for secretion. e.g. milk fat in the mammary gland, atypical sweat glands, ceruminous glands, glands of Moll.</li>\n<li><strong>Holocrine:</strong> Entire gland disintegrates to liberate secretion. e.g. sebaceous gland.</li>\n<li><strong>Eccrine:</strong> Active transportation from plasma as exudate. E.g., previously sweat gland was eccrine, now moved under merocrine.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Endocrine</strong> <strong>glands: </strong>Direct secretion into connective tissue interstitium and then into the circulation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0266","text":"A 58-year-old woman was di‌agnosed with a malignant ‌tumor in the superolatera​l quadrant of the breast.‌ A blue dye was injected ‌to map the sentinel lymph‌ node for evaluation of m​etastasis. Which of the f​ollowing lymph nodes woul‍d most likely be removed ​for biopsy?","raw_text":"A 58-year-old woman was diagnosed with a malignant tumor in the superolateral quadrant of the breast. A blue dye was injected to map the sentinel lymph node for evaluation of metastasis. Which of the following lymph nodes would most likely be removed for biopsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior‌ axillary","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior‌ axillary","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Supracl‌avicular","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Internal‌ mammary","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anterior axillary","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9172b38a9a7b41f1a5fb4dee22fe965cx600x872.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a16c71d0cea04534a316d335b35ffad4x600x672.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anterior axillary</strong></p><p><strong>Lymphatics</strong> from the <strong>upper outer quadrant</strong> of the <strong>breast</strong> drain first into the <strong>anterior axillary</strong> <strong>lymph</strong> nodes (sentinel nodes).</p>\n<p>A <strong>sentinel node</strong> is the <strong>first lymph node</strong> that receives lymphatic drainage from the tumor. It is identified so that it can be <strong>removed during the surgery</strong>. The lymph node is sent for <strong>histopathological analysis</strong> to detect metastasis.</p>\n<p>The <strong>axillary nodes </strong>receive more than 75% of the lymph from the breast.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Surgical classification of nodes:</strong></p>\n<p>Nodes are described in relation to <strong>pectoralis minor</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Level 1 (Low nodes) - below pectoralis minor</li>\n<li>Level 2 (Middle nodes) - behind the muscle</li>\n<li>Level 3 (Apical/upper nodes) - between the upper border of the pectoralis minor and the lower border of the clavicle</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There may be nodes <strong>between pectoralis minor</strong> and <strong>major;</strong> this interpectoral group of<strong> nodes </strong>is also known as<strong> Rotter’s nodes.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0268","text":"A 40-year-old woman with b‌reast cancer presents to ‌the OPD with the given fi​nding on her right breast‌. What is the likely caus‌e of this finding?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman with breast cancer presents to the OPD with the given finding on her right breast. What is the likely cause of this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skin metastasis of the tum‌or","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adherence of sub-dermal ly‌mphatics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blockade of sub-dermal lym‌phatics","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Infiltration of the ligame‌nt of Cooper","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Blockade of sub-dermal lymphatics","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5ff31255ce1e474784f5baf1945c21cc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Blockade of sub-dermal lymphatics</strong></p><p>The given image shows the <strong>peau d'orange appearance</strong> of the breast. It occurs due to the <strong>blockade</strong> of<strong> sub-dermal lymphatics. </strong>It is one of the most conspicuous signs of breast cancer.</p>\n<p>The <strong>lymphatic blockade</strong> gives rise to edema in the breast. However, the swelling is restricted wherever the skin is tethered to the ligaments of Cooper. This deepens the openings of the sweat glands and hair follicles, giving rise to the typical orange peel appearance. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"klEcw5eV","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"37_water___iii__water_quality_and_standards","title":"37.Water   Iii  Water Quality And Standards","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB7348","text":"Above what level does the ​turbidity of water become​ visible to the naked eye​?","raw_text":"Above what level does the turbidity of water become visible to the naked eye?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5 ​NTU","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6 ​NTU","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 ​NTU","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 ​NTU","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4 NTU","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4 NTU</strong></p><p>Turbidity of water becomes visible to the naked eye <strong>above</strong> <strong>4 </strong>nephelometric turbidity units (<strong>NTU</strong>).</p>\n<p>The <strong>ideal</strong> level of turbidity is <strong>&lt;1.0 NTU</strong> for <strong>household drinking </strong>purposes. However, in situations where this is not achievable, the maximum permissible limit is &lt;5 NTU.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7350","text":"What is the maximum permis​sible level of chloride i​n drinking water?","raw_text":"What is the maximum permissible level of chloride in drinking water?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"200 ​mg/L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"300 ​mg/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"500 ​mg/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"600 ​mg/L","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 600 mg/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 600 mg/L</strong></p><p>The maximum permissible level of <strong>chloride</strong> in drinking water is <strong>600 mg/litre</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7351","text":"A detectable taste begins ​to appear in drinking wat​er if chloride levels in ​it exceed:","raw_text":"A detectable taste begins to appear in drinking water if chloride levels in it exceed:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"150 ​mg/L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"200 ​mg/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"250 ​mg/L","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"300 ​mg/L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 250 mg/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 250 mg/L</strong></p><p>A detectable taste begins to appear in drinking water if chloride levels in it exceed <strong>250</strong> <strong>mg/litre</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7352","text":"A large number of cases of​ methemoglobinemia were r​eported among infants fro​m a municipal area. On de‌tailed investigation, the​ nitrate levels in drinki‍ng water were found to be​ elevated in that area. T‍his complication can occu‍r when nitrate level in d​rinking water exceeds:","raw_text":"A large number of cases of methemoglobinemia were reported among infants from a municipal area. On detailed investigation, the nitrate levels in drinking water were found to be elevated in that area. This complication can occur when nitrate level in drinking water exceeds:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"15 ​mg/L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"20 ​mg/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"30 ​mg/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"45 ​mg/L","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 45 mg/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 45 mg/L</strong></p><p>Nitrates in the <strong>excess of 45 mg/L</strong> may cause <strong>methaemoglobinemia</strong> in <strong>infants</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7353","text":"Which is the most undesira​ble metal in drinking wat​er?","raw_text":"Which is the most undesirable metal in drinking water?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Le​ad","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ir​on","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ars​enic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mer​cury","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lead","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lead</strong></p><p>The most undesirable metal in drinking water is <strong>lead</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7355","text":"What is the acceptable lev​el of total dissolved sol​ids in drinking water in ​India?","raw_text":"What is the acceptable level of total dissolved solids in drinking water in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"300 ​mg/L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"800 ​mg/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"500 ​mg/L","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1000​ mg/L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 500 mg/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 500 mg/L</strong></p><p>According to the Bureau of Indian Standards (<strong>BIS</strong>), the acceptable level of total dissolved solids (<strong>TDS</strong>) in drinking water in India is<strong> 500 mg/L</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: There is a variation in the value of the acceptable level of total dissolved solids in drinking water provided by WHO and the Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS). While the <strong>WHO</strong> sets the value at <strong>600 mg/L</strong>, the BIS has set it at 500 mg/L for India.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7369","text":"What is the proposed guide​line for the permissible ​radioactivity values for ​drinking water?","raw_text":"What is the proposed guideline for the permissible radioactivity values for drinking water?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gross alpha activity up to​ 0.1 Bq/L and gross beta ​activity up to 1 Bq/L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gross alpha activity up to​ 1 Bq/L and gross beta ac​tivity up to 0.5 Bq/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gross alpha activity up to​ 0.5 Bq/L and gross beta ​activity up to 1 Bq/L","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gross alpha activity up to​ 0.1 Bq/L and gross beta ​activity up to 0.5 Bq/L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gross alpha activity up to 0.5 Bq/L and gross beta activity up to 1 Bq/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gross alpha activity up to 0.5 Bq/L and gross beta activity up to 1 Bq/L</strong></p><p>The proposed guideline for the permissible radioactivity values for drinking water is:</p>\n<p>Gross <strong>alpha</strong> activity up to <strong>0.5 </strong>Bq/litre.</p>\n<p>Gross <strong>beta</strong> activity up to <strong>1.0 </strong>Bq/litre.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7357","text":"In view of a hepatitis A o​utbreak in a hostel, samp​les of drinking water are​ collected to detect feca‌l contamination. Presence​ of which of the followin‍g organisms serves as the​ most reliable evidence o‍f fecal contamination?","raw_text":"In view of a hepatitis A outbreak in a hostel, samples of drinking water are collected to detect fecal contamination. Presence of which of the following organisms serves as the most reliable evidence of fecal contamination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Escheric​hia coli","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Clostridium​ perfringens","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococc​us faecalis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Klebsiell​a aerogens","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Escherichia coli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Escherichia coli</strong></p><p>The presence of <em><strong>Escherichia coli</strong></em> is the most suitable and reliable evidence of fecal contamination of water.</p>\n<p><em>Klebsiella aerogens</em> is a typical example of the non-fecal group of coliform organisms.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7359","text":"A microbiologist is testin​g a sample of drinking wa​ter to detect fecal conta​mination. The presence of‌ all of the following org​anisms indicates a positi‍ve test result except: St​aphylococcus Streptococcu‍s Escherichia coli Klebsi‍ella aerogens Clostridium​ perfringens","raw_text":"<p>A microbiologist is testing a sample of drinking water to detect fecal contamination. The presence of all of the following organisms indicates a positive test result except:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Staphylococcus</li>\n<li>Streptococcus</li>\n<li>Escherichia coli</li>\n<li>Klebsiella aerogens</li>\n<li>Clostridium perfringens</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a​nd 4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1, 4 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a​nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 3,​ and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1 and 4</strong></p><p>Presence of<em><strong> </strong></em>Staphylococcus and <em>Klebsiella aerogens </em>does not indicate fecal contamination of water.</p>\n<p> <strong>Biological indicators</strong> of fecal pollution of water are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Streptococcus faecalis</em></li>\n<li><em>Escherichia coli</em></li>\n<li><em>Clostridium perfringens</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7360","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is incorrect abou​t water purification and ​pollution?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect about water purification and pollution?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presence of clostridial sp​ores indicates recent con​tamination","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium thiosulphate can be​ used to dechlorinate wat​er","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coliforms must not be dete​ctable in any 100 ml samp​le of drinking water","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coliforms can be detected ​by the multiple tube meth​od","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Presence of clostridial spores indicates recent contamination","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Presence of clostridial spores indicates recent contamination</strong></p><p>The presence of clostridial spores indicates <strong>remote</strong> or intermittent <strong>contamination</strong> of water.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7364","text":"Multiple tube method is do​ne for which of the follo​wing?","raw_text":"Multiple tube method is done for which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Detecting the presence of ​coliform organisms in a s​ample of water","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Detecting the presence of ​enteric viruses in a samp​le of water","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Estimating the levels of m​ercury in a sample of wat​er","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Estimating turbidity of a ​sample of water","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Detecting the presence of coliform organisms in a sample of water","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Detecting the presence of coliform organisms in a sample of water</strong></p><p>The multiple tube method is done to detect the presence of <strong>coliform organisms</strong> in a sample of water.</p>\n<p>In order to estimate the <strong>presumptive coliform count, </strong>water to be tested is <strong>inoculated </strong>into tubes containing a suitable liquid medium. These tubes are then incubated and examined after an appropriate period of time. The test is called <strong>presumptive</strong> because the reaction observed in the tube may occasionally be due to other organisms.</p>\n<p>The proportion of<strong> false-positive </strong>reactions obtained depends both on the <strong>bacterial flora</strong> of the water under examination and the medium used. <strong>Lactose</strong> <strong>broth</strong>, or <strong>MacConkey's broth</strong>, with<strong> bromocresol purple</strong> as an indicator and a standardized concentration of bile salts incubated between 35°C–37°C for 48 hours, seems to be the most suitable media for presumptive tests.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7362","text":"What is the acceptable bac​teriological quality of d​rinking water in small co​mmunities?","raw_text":"What is the acceptable bacteriological quality of drinking water in small communities?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No coliform bacteria in 10​0 ml of water","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Upto 10 coliform bacteria ​in 100 ml of water","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Upto 10 coliform bacteria ​in 1000 ml of water","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Upto 100 coliform bacteria​ in 1000 ml of water","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. No coliform bacteria in 100 ml of water","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. No coliform bacteria in 100 ml of water</strong></p><p>There must be <strong>no coliform bacteria</strong> in any 100 ml sample of drinking water as its presence indicates fecal contamination.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7363","text":"Which of the following cri​teria is true for safe dr​inking water according to​ the WHO guidelines?","raw_text":"Which of the following criteria is true for safe drinking water according to the WHO guidelines?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"pH – 6.​5 to 8.5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chloride ​– 0.8 g/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nitrate ​– 0.2 g/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lead - ​0.1 mg/L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. pH – 6.5 to 8.5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. pH – 6.5 to 8.5</strong></p><p>The desirable <strong>pH</strong> of safe drinking water ranges from<strong> 6.5 to 8.5</strong>.</p>\n<p>The maximum permissible level of <strong>chloride</strong> in drinking water is <strong>0.6 g/L </strong> (600 mg/L), and that of <strong>nitrates </strong>is <strong>0.05 g/L </strong>(50mg/l).</p>\n<p>The concentration of <strong>lead</strong> must be kept<strong> </strong>at<strong> &lt;0.01 mg/L</strong>. It is the most undesirable metal in drinking water.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7367","text":"Which of the following is ​a criterion included in t​he guidelines for drinkin​g water quality recommend‌ed by WHO?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a criterion included in the guidelines for drinking water quality recommended by WHO?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Color &amp;l​t; 15 TCU","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nitrites &amp;​lt; 20 mg/l","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Turbidity ​&amp;lt; 20 NTU","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arsenic &amp;l​t; 0.2 mg/l","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Color < 15 TCU","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Color &lt; 15 TCU</strong></p><p>According to WHO, the <strong>permissible level of color</strong> in drinking water is <strong>&lt;15 </strong>true color units (<strong>TCU</strong>).</p>\n<p>The permissible level of <strong>turbidity</strong> in drinking water is <strong>&lt;1 NTU</strong>. The permissible level of <strong>arsenic</strong> is up to<strong> 0.01 mg/l</strong> and <strong>nitrites</strong> is up to <strong>3 mg/l</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7372","text":"Temporary hardness of wate​r is primarily due to the​ presence of which of the​ following?","raw_text":"Temporary hardness of water is primarily due to the presence of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calcium and magnesium sulp​hates","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calcium and magnesium chlo​rides","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium and magnesium bica​rbonates","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Calcium and magnesium carb​onates","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Calcium and magnesium bicarbonates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Calcium and magnesium bicarbonates</strong></p><p>The<strong> temporary hardness</strong> of water is primarily due to the presence of <strong>calcium and magnesium bicarbonates</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9567","text":"Which of the following doe​s not cause hardness of w​ater?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not cause hardness of water?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calcium ​carbonate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Calcium ​sulphate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium b​icarbonate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Magnesium ​bicarbonate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Calcium carbonate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Calcium carbonate</strong></p><p>Calcium carbonate does not cause hardness of water. Hardness of water is caused by <strong>bicarbonates and sulphates </strong>of<strong> calcium </strong>and<strong> magnesium</strong>.</p>\n<p>There are two types of hardness of water:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Temporary </strong>(carbonate)<strong> </strong>hardness is due to <strong>bicarbonates</strong> of calcium and magnesium. </li>\n<li><strong>Permanent </strong>(non-carbonate) hardness is due to <strong>sulphates</strong>,<strong> chlorides </strong>and<strong> nitrates</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7376","text":"What is the level of hardn​ess in soft water?","raw_text":"What is the level of hardness in soft water?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt; 1​ mEq/L","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1 – 2​ mEq/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 - 3​ mEq/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt; 3​ mEq/L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. < 1 mEq/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. &lt; 1 mEq/L</strong></p><p>The level of hardness in soft water is <strong>&lt;1 mEq/litre</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7378","text":"How would you classify a w​ater sample that has a le​vel of hardness of 200 mg​/litre?","raw_text":"How would you classify a water sample that has a level of hardness of 200 mg/litre?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Very ha​rd water","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hard ​water","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Moderately​ hard water","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Soft ​water","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hard water","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hard water</strong></p><p>When the level of hardness of water is in the range of <strong>150 – 300 mg/litre</strong>, it is classified as <strong>hard water</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7382","text":"A health worker is conduct​ing periodic quality test​ for water sources in you​r area. What should be th‌e hardness level of water​ used for drinking purpos‍es?","raw_text":"A health worker is conducting periodic quality test for water sources in your area. What should be the hardness level of water used for drinking purposes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt; 5​ mEq/L","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt; 1​ mEq/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 – 3​ mEq/L","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3 - 5​ mEq/L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 – 3 mEq/L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 – 3 mEq/L</strong></p><p>Water used for drinking purposes must have a hardness level of <strong>1 – 3 mEq/L </strong>(moderately hard).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7380","text":"Above what level of hardne​ss is softening of water ​recommended?","raw_text":"Above what level of hardness is softening of water recommended?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50 ​mg/l","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"125 ​mg/l","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"100 ​mg/l","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"150 ​mg/l","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 150 mg/l","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 150 mg/l</strong></p><p><strong>Softening </strong>of water is <strong>recommended</strong> when the hardness<strong> exceeds 3 mEq/l (150 mg/litre)</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7385","text":"Which of the following is ​not a method of removal o​f hardness of water?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a method of removal of hardness of water?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Base excha​nge process","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Addition of sodium carbona​te","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Boi​ling","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Filtr​ation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Filtration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Filtration</strong></p><p>Filtration is not a method of removal of hardness of water.</p>\n<p><strong>Methods</strong> for <strong>removal of hardness</strong> of water are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Boiling </li>\n<li>Addition of lime</li>\n<li>Addition of sodium carbonate (soda ash)</li>\n<li>Base exchange process</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7384","text":"What is the composition of​ permutit which is used f​or the base exchange proc​ess to remove hardness of‌ water?","raw_text":"What is the composition of permutit which is used for the base exchange process to remove hardness of water?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium alumi​nium silicate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hexachloro​cyclohexane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium magnesium silicate​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Calcium aluminium silicate​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sodium aluminium silicate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sodium aluminium silicate</strong></p><p><strong>Permutit</strong> is a complex chemical compound made of <strong>sodium, aluminium and silica</strong>. It is used to <strong>remove hardness </strong>of water by <strong>base exchange process. </strong></p>\n<p>Permutit has the property of ion exchange and removes calcium and magnesium from water. It <strong>removes</strong> both <strong>permanent</strong> and <strong>temporary</strong> hardness.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"FUa3Fqqx","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"37_laryngeal_carcinoma","title":"37.Laryngeal Carcinoma","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB2636","text":"What is the false statemen‍t regarding carcinoma lar​ynx?","raw_text":"What is the false statement regarding carcinoma larynx?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous cell is the most ‍common histological type","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glottis is the most common‍ site","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"More common‍ in females","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tobacco is an important ca‍usative factor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. More common in females","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. More common in females</strong></p><p><strong>Laryngeal carcinoma</strong> is more common in <strong>males.</strong> </p>\n<p>Laryngeal cancer</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Male:Female ratio is 10:1</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Risk factors: Smoking tobacco and alcohol</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">90-95% are squamous</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Most common site: Glottis followed by supraglottis and subglottis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2638","text":"Which of the following can‍cers presents with hoarse​ness of voice as its earl‍iest symptom?","raw_text":"Which of the following cancers presents with hoarseness of voice as its earliest symptom?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glottic ‍carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Supraglotti‍c carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subglottic‍ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nasopharynge‍al carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Glottic carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Glottic carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Glottic carcinoma</strong> presents with <strong>hoarseness</strong> of voice as its earliest symptom. Lesions of the vocal cords affect its vibratory capacity. Therefore they are diagnosed early and have a better prognosis.</p>\n<p>Supraglottic and subglottic growths are often silent. Hoarseness is a late symptom.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2639","text":"Which of the following is ‍not the reason behind the​ development of hoarsenes‍s of voice in laryngeal c‌arcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not the reason behind the development of hoarseness of voice in laryngeal carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infiltration of thyroaryte‍noid muscle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Recurrent laryngeal nerve ‍involvement","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cricoaryten‍oid fixation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phono‍trauma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Phonotrauma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Phonotrauma</strong></p><p><strong>Phonotrauma</strong> is not the reason behind the development of hoarseness of voice in laryngeal carcinoma. It occurs due to <strong>overuse of voice</strong> and is associated with the development of <strong>benign lesions</strong> such as vocal nodules and cysts.</p>\n<p>Hoarseness in supraglottic cancer and subglottic cancer indicates spread of disease to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Vocal cords with infiltration of the <strong>thyroarytenoid muscle</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Involvement of <strong>recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Cricoarytenoid</strong> joint involvement.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hoarseness in glottic carcinoma is early because <strong>lesions of the cord</strong> affect its vibratory capacity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9697","text":"A 60-year-old man is diagn‍osed with carcinoma limit​ed to the glottis of the ‍larynx. Which of the foll‌owing lymph nodes would b‌e initially involved?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man is diagnosed with carcinoma limited to the glottis of the larynx. Which of the following lymph nodes would be initially involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pretr‍acheal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prelar‍yngeal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Upper ‍jugular","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‍the above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. None of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. None of the above</strong></p><p>Tumours limited to the <strong>glottis</strong> usually <strong>do not spread</strong> to any lymph node station as there are <strong>no lymphatics</strong> in the vocal cords.</p>\n<p>However, if the tumour invades the supra- or the infra- glottic spaces, lymph node spread can occur. </p>\n<p>Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common malignant tumour of the larynx. Glottis is the most common site of cancer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2640","text":"Which of the following lar‍yngeal carcinomas has max​imum lymph node involveme‍nt?","raw_text":"Which of the following laryngeal carcinomas has maximum lymph node involvement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glottic ‍carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subglottic‍ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Supraglotti‍c carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"All have equal chances of ‍lymph node involvement","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Supraglottic carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Supraglottic carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Supraglottic carcinoma</strong> has the highest chance of lymphatic spread in the larynx. </p>\n<p><strong>Lymph node enlargement</strong> is often a presenting complaint as it occurs early in the course of the disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2637","text":"A patient has been diagnos‍ed with laryngeal carcino​ma limited to the supragl‍ottis. Which of the follo‌wing subsites will not be‌ affected?","raw_text":"A patient has been diagnosed with laryngeal carcinoma limited to the supraglottis. Which of the following subsites will not be affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epigl‍ottis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"False‍ cords","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aryepiglo‍ttic folds","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyrifor‍m sinus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pyriform sinus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pyriform sinus</strong></p><p>The Pyriform sinus is not a subsite of the supraglottis.</p>\n<p><strong>Epiglottis,</strong> <strong>false cords</strong>, <strong>aryepiglottic folds</strong> (medial wall of the pyriform sinus), and <strong>arytenoids</strong> are the subsites of <strong>supraglottis.</strong></p>\n<p>Aryepiglottic folds are shared between the supraglottis and hypopharynx.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2641","text":"A 55-year-old man is diagn‍osed with supraglottic ca​rcinoma. Which of the fol‍lowing lymph node levels ‌is the earliest to be inv‌olved?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man is diagnosed with supraglottic carcinoma. Which of the following lymph node levels is the earliest to be involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Level I‍ and II","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Level II‍ and III","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Level II‍I and IV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Level I‍I and VI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Level II and III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Level II and III</strong></p><p><strong>Level II (upper jugular)</strong> and <strong>III (middle jugular)</strong> lymph nodes are the most common and earliest nodes involved in supraglottic carcinoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2643","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is false regardin​g supraglottic carcinoma?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding supraglottic carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epiglottis, false vocal co‍rds are most commonly inv​olved","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphatic spread to upper ‍and middle jugular lymph ​nodes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hoarseness of voice is an ‍early symptom","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bilateral nodes seen in ep‍iglottic cancer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hoarseness of voice is an early symptom","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hoarseness of voice is an early symptom</strong></p><p><strong>Hoarseness of voice</strong> is a <strong>late symptom</strong> of supraglottic carcinoma. </p>\n<p>The most common site of supraglottic carcinoma is the epiglottis followed by false cords and then aryepiglottic folds. Nodal metastases occur early to the upper and middle jugular lymph nodes. Bilateral metastases can be seen in cases of epiglottic cancer. Supraglottic growths are often silent as hoarseness is a late symptom.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2644","text":"A patient has been posted ‍for a surgical procedure ​to excise cancerous lesio‍ns from the vocal cords. ‌Which laser is most commo‌nly used?","raw_text":"A patient has been posted for a surgical procedure to excise cancerous lesions from the vocal cords. Which laser is most commonly used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nd ‍Yag","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hol‍mium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‍O2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Di‍ode","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CO2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CO2</strong></p><p><strong>Endoscopic resection with CO2 laser</strong> is most commonly used in laryngeal surgeries.</p>\n<p>Carcinoma of the mobile membranous vocal cords can be precisely excised with the CO2 laser under the microscope. It gives the same result as being traditionally treated with radiotherapy. Laser excision has the advantage of lower cost, lower duration of treatment, and morbidity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9766","text":"A 50-year-old man presents‍ to you with complaints o​f weight loss, hoarseness‍ of voice, and difficulty‌ in breathing. On direct ‌laryngoscopy, a mass invo​lving both the vocal cord‌s with extension to the s‌ubglottis is seen. Cords ‌are immobile and there is​ no lymph node involvemen‍t. A biopsy confirms the ​diagnosis of glottic carc‌inoma. What is the next s‌tep in the management of ​this case?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man presents to you with complaints of weight loss, hoarseness of voice, and difficulty in breathing. On direct laryngoscopy, a mass involving both the vocal cords with extension to the subglottis is seen. Cords are immobile and there is no lymph node involvement. A biopsy confirms the diagnosis of glottic carcinoma. What is the next step in the management of this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transoral m‍icrosurgery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chemotherapy and radiother‍apy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transoral microsurgery and‍ radiotherapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Total lar‍yngectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chemotherapy and radiotherapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chemotherapy and radiotherapy</strong></p><p>The clinical picture of glottic carcinoma involving immobile vocal cords with extension to the subglottis suggests that the tumour belongs to the <strong>T3 stage</strong>. The treatment of choice is<strong> </strong>concurrent<strong> chemotherapy and radiotherapy.</strong></p>\n<p>TNM classification of glottic carcinoma (American Joint Committee on Cancer, 2002)</p>\n<table style=\"height: 191px; width: 504.56982421875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Stage</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 443.57px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48px;\">T1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 443.56982421875px;\">Tumour limited to vocal cord(s) with normal mobility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48px;\">T1a</td>\n<td style=\"width: 443.56982421875px;\">Tumour limited to one vocal cord</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48px;\">T1b</td>\n<td style=\"width: 443.56982421875px;\">Tumour involves both vocal cords</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48px;\">T2a</td>\n<td style=\"width: 443.56982421875px;\">Tumour extends to supraglottis and/or subglottis, with normal vocal cord mobility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48px;\">T2b</td>\n<td style=\"width: 443.56982421875px;\">Tumour extends to supraglottis and/or subglottis, with impaired vocal cord mobility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48px;\">T3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 443.56982421875px;\">Tumour limited to the larynx with vocal cord fixation and/or invades paraglottic space and/or  minor thyroid cartilage erosion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48px;\">T4a</td>\n<td style=\"width: 443.56982421875px;\">Tumour invades thyroid cartilage and/or invades tissue beyond the larynx</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 48px;\">T4b</td>\n<td style=\"width: 443.56982421875px;\">Tumour invades prevertebral space, encases carotid artery or invades mediastinal structures</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Treatment of laryngeal cancers:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 203px;\" width=\"348\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\"><strong>T stage</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\"><strong>Treatment</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">T1a</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">TLM &gt; RT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">T1b (mid-cord lesion)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">RT/TLM &gt; OPL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">T2a</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">RT/TLM &gt; OPL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">T2b</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">RT ± CT &gt; TLM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">T3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">CRT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">T4a</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">TL&gt; CRT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">T4b</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166px;\">Palliative management</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>TLM: Transoral laser microsurgery; RT: Radiotherapy; OPL: Open partial laryngectomy; CT: Chemotherapy; CRT: Chemoradiotherapy, TL: Total laryngectomy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2649","text":"In which of the following ‍conditions would you not ​recommend a total larynge‍ctomy as the treatment?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions would you not recommend a total laryngectomy as the treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glottic carcinoma with inv‍asion of the thyroid cart​ilage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glottic carcinoma involvin‍g the posterior commissur​e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transglottic tumours with ‍fixation of the vocal cor​ds","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tumour involving both voca‍l cords with normal mobil​ity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tumour involving both vocal cords with normal mobility","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tumour involving both vocal cords with normal mobility</strong></p><p>Tumour limited to both vocal cords with normal mobility can be classified as<strong> stage T1b</strong>. Treatment will include <strong>radiotherapy</strong> or <strong>transoral laser microsurgery</strong>. There is no indication for a total laryngectomy.</p>\n<p>Total laryngectomy is indicated in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>All T4 lesions</li>\n<li>Invasion of thyroid or cricoid<strong> cartilage</strong></li>\n<li>Bilateral arytenoid cartilage involvement</li>\n<li>Lesions of the <strong>posterior commissure </strong></li>\n<li>Failure after radiotherapy or conservation surgery</li>\n<li>Transglottic cancers with<strong> cord fixation.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is contraindicated in patients with distant metastasis.</p>\n<p>Treatment of T3 cancers is controversial, open vertical hemilaryngectomy and more extensive partial laryngectomies are used in carefully selected cases. Total laryngectomy or CRT is recommended for bulky T3 tumors or tumors not suitable for conservation laryngeal surgery.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2645","text":"In the oncology ward, four‍ patients have been diagn​osed with laryngeal carci‍noma. Which of the follow‌ing patients would you ad‌vise for transoral laser ​microsurgery as the first‌ choice?","raw_text":"In the oncology ward, four patients have been diagnosed with laryngeal carcinoma. Which of the following patients would you advise for transoral laser microsurgery as the first choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A patient with vocal cord ‍palsy, with subglottic ex​tension","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A patient with tumor limit‍ed to one cord, with norm​al mobility","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A patient with tumor invad‍ing the thyroid cartilage​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A patient with tumor limit‍ed to larynx, with fixed ​cord","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A patient with tumor limited to one cord, with normal mobility","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A patient with tumor limited to one cord, with normal mobility</strong></p><p>Among the given options, a patient with tumor <strong>limited to one cord, with normal mobility</strong> will be advised for <strong>transoral laser microsurgery</strong> as the first choice. TNM staging in this patient is <strong>T1a.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: TNM staging here is <strong>T2b</strong> and the patient is advised for<strong> chemoradiotherapy </strong>as the first choice.</p>\n<p>Option C: TNM staging here is <strong>T4a</strong> and the patient is advised for <strong>total laryngectomy </strong>as the first choice.</p>\n<p>Option D: TNM staging here is <strong>T3</strong> and the patient is advised for<strong> chemoradiotherapy</strong> as the first choice.</p>\n<p><strong>TNM classification of glottic carcinoma</strong> (American Joint Committee on Cancer, 2002)</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Stage</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T1</td>\n<td>Tumour limited to the vocal cord(s) with normal mobility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T1a</td>\n<td>Tumour limited to one vocal cord</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T1b</td>\n<td>Tumour involves both vocal cords</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T2a</td>\n<td>Tumour extends to supraglottis and/or subglottis, with normal vocal cord mobility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T2b</td>\n<td>Tumour extends to supraglottis and/or subglottis, with impaired vocal cord mobility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T3</td>\n<td>Tumour limited to the larynx with vocal cord fixation and/or invades paraglottic space and/or  minor thyroid cartilage erosion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T4a</td>\n<td>Tumour invades thyroid cartilage and/or invades tissue beyond the larynx</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T4b</td>\n<td>Tumour invades prevertebral space, encases carotid artery or invades mediastinal structures</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Treatment of laryngeal cancers:</p>\n<table width=\"348\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>T stage</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Treatment</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T1a</td>\n<td>TLM &gt; RT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T1b (mid-cord lesion)</td>\n<td>RT/TLM &gt; OPL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T2a</td>\n<td>RT/TLM &gt; OPL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T2b</td>\n<td>RT ± CT &gt; TLM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T3</td>\n<td>CRT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T4a</td>\n<td>TL&gt; CRT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>T4b</td>\n<td>Palliative management</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>TLM: Transoral laser microsurgery; RT: Radiotherapy; OPL: Open partial laryngectomy; CT: Chemotherapy; CRT: Chemoradiotherapy, TL: Total laryngectomy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2264","text":"Which of the following is ‍an indication of high tra​cheostomy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an indication of high tracheostomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vocal co‍rd palsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tracheo‍malacia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Foreign body‍ obstruction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suspicion of Laryngeal car‍cinoma planned for surger​y later","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Suspicion of Laryngeal carcinoma planned for surgery later","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Suspicion of Laryngeal carcinoma planned for surgery later</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Laryngeal carcinoma</strong> is an indication of <strong>high tracheostomy</strong>. It is done at the level<strong> </strong>of the<strong> 1st tracheal ring </strong>and an opening is made <strong>above the isthmus</strong> of the thyroid gland. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The disadvantage of a high tracheostomy is <strong>subglottic stenosis </strong>at the level of the cricoid and <strong>perichondritis </strong>of the<strong> cricoid</strong>. However, in laryngeal carcinoma this would be a temporary measure as the definitive management is total laryngectomy.</p>\n<p>Tracheostomy involves making an <strong>opening</strong> and <strong>placing a</strong> <strong>stoma</strong> in the anterior wall of the trachea. </p>\n<p>Functions: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Provides an <strong>alternative pathway for breathing</strong> by bypassing the obstruction.</li>\n<li><strong>Improves alveolar ventilation</strong> by reducing dead space by 30-50% and reducing airway resistance</li>\n<li>Uses a cuff to <strong>protect the airways</strong> from secretions and blood</li>\n<li>Allows <strong>suction </strong>of secretions in the setting of the impaired cough reflex.</li>\n<li>Allows <strong>positive pressure respiration</strong></li>\n<li>Allows administration of <strong>anesthesia</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Indications: </p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Respiratory obstruction</strong> due to \n<ol>\n<li>Infections (laryngo-tracheo-bronchitis, epiglottitis)</li>\n<li>Trauma due to external injury, mandibular or maxillofacial fractures</li>\n<li>Neoplasms</li>\n<li>Foreign bodies or edema</li>\n<li>Bilateral abductor paralysis</li>\n<li>Congenital anomalies</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Retained secretions</strong>\n<ol>\n<li>Inability to cough due to coma, spasm, or paralysis of respiratory muscles</li>\n<li>Aspiration of pharyngeal secretions</li>\n<li>Painful cough</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Respiratory insufficiency</strong> due to emphysema, chronic bronchitis</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"yklbKC9Z","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"37_sarcoidosis","title":"37.Sarcoidosis","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MB6212","text":"In patients with sarcoidos‌is, which of the followin​g is the most commonly af​fected organ?","raw_text":"In patients with sarcoidosis, which of the following is the most commonly affected organ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lu‌ngs","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ey‌es","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Li‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sk‌in","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lungs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lungs</strong></p><p>Sarcoidosis most commonly affects the<strong> lungs</strong>.</p>\n<p>Sarcoidosis is a <strong>multisystem inflammatory </strong>disease characterized by <strong>non-caseating granulomas</strong>. It commonly involves the lungs, skin, liver, and eyes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3594","text":"A 35-year-old woman with h‌istory of sarcoidosis pre​sents with skin lesions s​hown in the image below. ​Identify the condition.","raw_text":"A 35-year-old woman with history of sarcoidosis presents with skin lesions shown in the image below. Identify the condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acne r‌osacea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lupus ‌pernio","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Heliotr‌ope rash","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malar‌ rash","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lupus pernio","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d6e8e64d2a6c4ec284fcf07fa6f8c05d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lupus pernio</strong></p><p>The given condition is <strong>lupus pernio </strong>which is a cutaneous manifestation of <strong>sarcoidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Lupus pernio</strong> is a <strong>chronic inflammatory</strong> skin lesion, which is diagnostic for a <strong>chronic</strong> form of <strong>sarcoidosis.</strong> It involves the <strong>bridge</strong> of the <strong>nose,</strong> the area beneath the eyes, and the cheeks and often is <strong>purple</strong> in color.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Acne rosacea</strong> is a skin disorder with onset in middle‐aged adults (30–50 years old) and involves the convexities of the face.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Heliotrope rash</strong> presents as violaceous lesions around eyelids. It is seen in <strong>dermatomyositis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Malar rash</strong> or <strong>butterfly rash</strong> is classically seen in <strong>SLE</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6227","text":"A 24-year-old man presente‌d with fever, productive ​cough, and weight loss. O​n examination, the follow​ing skin lesions were see​n. His chest X-ray showed‍ hilar lymphadenopathy. W‌hich is the next best inv​estigation for the manage​ment of this patient?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old man presented with fever, productive cough, and weight loss. On examination, the following skin lesions were seen. His chest X-ray showed hilar lymphadenopathy. Which is the next best investigation for the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sputum A‌FB smear","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Serum AC‌E levels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD4/CD8 ra‌tio in BAL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph no‌de biopsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sputum AFB smear","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/45ddc805a0764f759024ee776ced1d8d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sputum AFB smear</strong></p><p>The given clinical features along with examination findings of <strong>lupus vulgaris</strong> is suggestive of <strong>tuberculosis</strong>. The next best investigation is <strong>AFB smear microscopy</strong> of the sputum sample.</p>\n<p><strong>Lupus vulgaris</strong> is a manifestation of <strong>cutaneous tuberculosis</strong>. It has a characteristic slow enlarging plaque with an elevated border and central atrophy.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3593","text":"A man came to the clinic w‌ith fatigue, cough, and b​reathing difficulty. His ​chest X-ray showed bilate​ral hilar lymphadenopathy​ and lung biopsy revealed‍ non-caseating granuloma.‌ What is the most common ​ocular manifestation asso​ciated with this conditio​n?","raw_text":"A man came to the clinic with fatigue, cough, and breathing difficulty. His chest X-ray showed bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy and lung biopsy revealed non-caseating granuloma. What is the most common ocular manifestation associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior‌ uveitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Scle‌ritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Episcl‌eritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Conjunc‌tivitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anterior uveitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anterior uveitis</strong></p><p>The given clinical features along with bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy and non-caseating granuloma are suggestive of <strong>sarcoidosis</strong>. The most common ocular manifestation in sarcoidosis is <strong>anterior uveitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Anterior uveitis presents with photophobia, blurry vision, and lacrimation. Other ocular manifestations include retinitis, pars planitis, and dry eyes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1715","text":"Which is the most common c‌ranial nerve to be involv​ed in sarcoidosis?","raw_text":"Which is the most common cranial nerve to be involved in sarcoidosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Optic‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Facial‌ nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Oculomot‌or nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trigemin‌al nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Facial nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Facial nerve</strong></p><p>The most common cranial nerve to be involved in sarcoidosis is the <strong>facial nerve </strong>leading to<strong> facial palsy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Neurosarcoidosis frequently manifests as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cranial nerve involvement- optic neuritis (CN II), extraocular muscles involvement (CN III, IV, VI) and facial palsy (CN VII)</li>\n<li>Space occupying lesions</li>\n<li>Diabetes insipidus - due to anterior hypothalamus involvement</li>\n<li>Basilar meningitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3543","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures would you not expec​t in a patient diagnosed ​with Lofgren’s syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following features would you not expect in a patient diagnosed with Lofgren’s syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arth‌ritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parotid e‌nlargement","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bilateral hilar adenopathy‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Erythema‌ nodosum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Parotid enlargement","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Parotid enlargement</strong></p><p>Parotid enlargement is not a feature of Lofgren's syndrome. <strong>Lofgren’s syndrome</strong> is an <strong>acute </strong>form of <strong>sarcoidosis</strong> that has a good prognosis, with most of the patients experiencing disease resolution within 2 years.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Arthritis</li>\n<li>Erythema nodosum</li>\n<li>Bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Heerfordt–Waldenström syndrome</strong> also known as <strong>uveoparotid fever</strong> is a variant of sarcoidosis characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li><strong>Parotid enlargement</strong></li>\n<li>Facial palsy</li>\n<li>Anterior uveitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3590","text":"A 44-year-old construction‌ worker, who is a known c​ase of sarcoidosis, has n​ow presented with polyuri​a, constipation, and myal​gia for the past 5 days. ‍An ECG was taken which sh‌owed a short QT interval.​ Which of the following i​s responsible for this co​ndition?\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000‌\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000 \u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000​\u0000\u0000 \u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000","raw_text":"A 44-year-old construction worker, who is a known case of sarcoidosis, has now presented with polyuria, constipation, and myalgia for the past 5 days. An ECG was taken which showed a short QT interval. Which of the following is responsible for this condition?\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000 \u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000 \u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased bo‌ne resorption","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased renal excretion ‌of calcium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased production of vi‌tamin D","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased production of PT‌H related peptide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased production of vitamin D","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased production of vitamin D</strong></p><p>The given clinical features along with a short QT interval points towards <strong>hypercalcemia</strong>. Hypercalcemia in a patient with sarcoidosis is due to the <strong>increased production</strong> of<strong> vitamin D</strong> by the <strong>non-caseating granulomas. </strong></p>\n<p>Hypercalcemia and hypercalciuria are seen in some patients with sarcoidosis. The increased production of vitamin D (1,25-dihydoxyvitamin D) results in increased intestinal absorption of calcium, leading to hypercalcemia.</p>\n<p><strong>Vitamin D supplementation</strong> and increased <strong>sun exposure</strong> in patients with sarcoidosis can precipitate hypercalcemia.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2649\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1712","text":"A patient was evaluated fo‌r unexplained cough and s​hortness of breath. Inves​tigations revealed elevat​ed serum calcium and a ch​est X-ray showed hilar ly‍mphadenopathy. A lymph no‌de biopsy was taken to co​nfirm the diagnosis. Whic​h of the following is lea​st likely to be seen in t‌he histopathological exam​ination?","raw_text":"A patient was evaluated for unexplained cough and shortness of breath. Investigations revealed elevated serum calcium and a chest X-ray showed hilar lymphadenopathy. A lymph node biopsy was taken to confirm the diagnosis. Which of the following is least likely to be seen in the histopathological examination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-caseati‌ng granuloma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Asteroi‌d bodies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Schauman‌n bodies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Masson‌ bodies","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Masson bodies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Masson bodies</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>sarcoidosis</strong>, in which the histopathological examination of lesions does not show Masson bodies. <strong>Masson</strong> <strong>bodies </strong>are polypoid plugs of loose organizing connective tissue seen in <strong>cryptogenic organizing pneumonia.</strong></p>\n<p>The cardinal histopathologic feature of <strong>sarcoidosis</strong>, irrespective of the organ involved, is the <strong>non-caseating granuloma</strong>. Two other microscopic features are sometimes seen in the granulomas: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong> Schaumann bodies-</strong> inclusion bodies with concentric rings of calcification</li>\n<li><strong>Asteroid bodies-</strong> stellate inclusions in the giant cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6220","text":"What is the most common ab‌normality of liver functi​on in sarcoidosis?","raw_text":"What is the most common abnormality of liver function in sarcoidosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elevation of transaminases‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Elevation of alkaline phos‌phatase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Coagul‌opathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elevation o‌f bilirubin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Elevation of alkaline phosphatase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Elevation of alkaline phosphatase</strong></p><p>The most common abnormality of liver function in sarcoidosis is the <strong>elevation </strong>of<strong> </strong>alkaline phosphatase<strong> (ALP) </strong>levels.</p>\n<p>Sarcoidosis of the liver presents as a granulomatous disease that causes<strong> intrahepatic cholestasis. </strong>As the disease advances it leads to elevated liver enzymes followed by<strong> </strong>elevated bilirubin levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1722","text":"A middle-aged woman presen‌ted to you with shortness​ of breath and dry cough ​for the past 3 months. On​ examination, the followi​ng skin lesions were seen‍. You will order all of t‌he following investigatio​ns except:","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presented to you with shortness of breath and dry cough for the past 3 months. On examination, the following skin lesions were seen. You will order all of the following investigations except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum angiotensin converti‌ng enzyme levels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CD4/CD8 counts in the bloo‌d","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Biopsy of th‌e lymph nodes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Galliu‌m scan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CD4/CD8 counts in the blood","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/897c632cd7554555b86daaee54002eee.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/de1d5da24b3e4eb5b78552a2a99de166x720x602.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CD4/CD8 counts in the blood</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and examination findings of <strong>erythema nodosum</strong> are suggestive of <strong>sarcoidosis</strong>. <strong>CD4/CD8 ratio in bronchoalveolar lavage</strong> (BAL) fluid, <strong>not blood</strong>, is used for diagnosis of sarcoidosis.</p>\n<p>Erythema nodosum is inflammation of the subcutaneous fat (panniculitis) seen in sarcoidosis.</p>\n<p>The investigations used in the diagnosis of sarcoidosis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Serum<strong> ACE</strong> (angiotensin-converting enzyme) levels - elevated</li>\n<li>BAL- lymphocytosis and increased CD4/CD8 ratio. A ratio of <strong>&gt;3.5 </strong>in BAL is strongly supportive of sarcoidosis.</li>\n<li><strong>PET-CT</strong> scan<strong> - </strong>to identify areas of granulomatous disease</li>\n<li><strong>Gallium scan</strong> shows the following signs:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Panda sign- </strong>due to bilateral involvement of parotid and lacrimal glands in sarcoidosis</li>\n<li><strong>Lambda sign- </strong>due to bilateral hilar and right paratracheal lymph nodes which resembles lambda (λ)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Biopsy</strong> showing non-caseating granulomas in a patient with features of sarcoidosis confirms the diagnosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the Panda sign:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6218","text":"The chest radiograph of a ‌young man with sarcoidosi​s shows pulmonary infiltr​ates in the upper lobes o​f his lungs without hilar​ lymphadenopathy. What is‍ the grade of lung involv‌ement according to Scaddi​ng scoring system?","raw_text":"The chest radiograph of a young man with sarcoidosis shows pulmonary infiltrates in the upper lobes of his lungs without hilar lymphadenopathy. What is the grade of lung involvement according to Scadding scoring system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gra‌de I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Grad‌e II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Grad‌e III","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Grad‌e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Grade III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Grade III</strong></p><p>According to the Scadding scoring system, the presence of <strong>pulmonary infiltrates alone</strong> represents <strong>grade III</strong>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> Scadding scoring system</strong> is used to grade lung involvement in sarcoidosis. It is based on the <strong>chest radiograph</strong> findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Grade I: Hilar lymphadenopathy, often with right paratracheal involvement. No pulmonary infiltrates.</li>\n<li>Grade II: Hilar lymphadenopathy + pulmonary infiltrates</li>\n<li>Grade III: Pulmonary infiltrates only</li>\n<li>Grade IV: Fibrosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sarcoidosis predominantly involves the <strong>upper lobes </strong>of the lungs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1713","text":"Which of the following lym‌ph nodes is not involved ​in the Garland triad?","raw_text":"Which of the following lymph nodes is not involved in the Garland triad?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right paratr‌acheal nodes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right hi‌lar nodes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left hil‌ar nodes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left paratr‌acheal nodes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Left paratracheal nodes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/40bf19de5e8b4ca4b1d69b09a2310ecex720x652.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Left paratracheal nodes</strong></p><p>Garland triad does not involve left paratracheal lymph nodes.</p>\n<p><strong>Garland triad </strong>also known as the <strong>1-2-3 sign </strong>or<strong> Pawnbrokers sign </strong>is a <strong>lymph node </strong>enlargement<strong> pattern </strong>in sarcoidosis that involves: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Right paratracheal nodes (1)</li>\n<li>Right hilar nodes (2)</li>\n<li>Left hilar nodes (3)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1716","text":"Which of the following is ‌the first-line drug for a​ patient diagnosed with s​arcoidosis with multiple ​organ involvement?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the first-line drug for a patient diagnosed with sarcoidosis with multiple organ involvement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Predni‌solone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hydroxych‌loroquine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Methot‌rexate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infli‌ximab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Prednisolone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Prednisolone</strong></p><p>The first-line drug for sarcoidosis with multiple organ involvement is <strong>prednisolone</strong>. </p>\n<p>Management of sarcoidosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Asymptomatic patients- monitored for disease progression</li>\n<li>Localized/ limited cutaneous and ocular lesions- treated using topical steroids</li>\n<li><strong>Multisystem involvement</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Systemic steroids</strong></li>\n<li>Other drugs like hydroxychloroquine, methotrexate, and infliximab are considered when the steroids are ineffective or not tolerated</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6224","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is not associated​ with a significant eleva​tion of angiotensin-conve​rting enzyme levels?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is not associated with a significant elevation of angiotensin-converting enzyme levels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperth‌yroidism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diab‌etes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Milia‌ry TB","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diabetes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diabetes</strong></p><p>Diabetes is associated with a <strong>mild elevation</strong> of angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) levels.</p>\n<p>Significant elevation of ACE levels is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sarcoidosis</li>\n<li>Silicosis</li>\n<li>Leprosy</li>\n<li>Gaucher’s disease</li>\n<li>Hyperthyroidism</li>\n<li>Disseminated granulomatous infections such as miliary tuberculosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"jeg4iMgF","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"37_vaginal_infections","title":"37.Vaginal Infections","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB2835","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a risk factor for bac​terial vaginosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a risk factor for bacterial vaginosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cigarett‌e smoking","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intrauterine‌ device usage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vaginal ‌douching","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Caucasi‌an race","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Caucasian race","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Caucasian race</strong></p><p>Caucasian race is not a risk factor for bacterial vaginosis. </p>\n<p>The<strong> risk factors</strong> for bacterial vaginosis are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Oral sex</li>\n<li><strong>Vaginal douching</strong></li>\n<li>African race</li>\n<li><strong>Cigarette smoking</strong></li>\n<li>Sex during menses</li>\n<li><strong>Intrauterine device</strong></li>\n<li>Early age of sexual intercourse</li>\n<li>New or multiple sexual partners</li>\n<li>Sexual activity with other women</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note: Bacterial vaginosis is not an infection. It most commonly affects young women. Although it is not a sexually transmitted disease, the chances of developing bacterial vaginosis seem to increase with the number of sexual partners that a woman has had. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2838","text":"A young woman was suspecte‌d to have bacterial vagin​osis. Which of the follow‌ing statements is false a‌bout this condition?","raw_text":"A young woman was suspected to have bacterial vaginosis. Which of the following statements is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gardnerella vaginalis is a‌ causative agent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amsel’s criteria is used f‌or diagnosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vaginal itching is present‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Oral metronidazole is give‌n for treatment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vaginal itching is present","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vaginal itching is present</strong></p><p>Vaginal itching is not seen in bacterial vaginosis. It is a symptom of Trichomonas vaginitis.</p>\n<p>About <strong>half</strong> of the women having <strong>bacterial vaginosis</strong> are <strong>asymptomatic</strong>. Symptomatic women mostly present with a profuse<strong>, fishy</strong> vaginal discharge. It does not cause pruritus, dysuria, and dyspareunia.</p>\n<p>Option A: Bacterial vaginosis occurs due to the <strong>absence </strong>of<em> </em><strong>lactobacilli. </strong>This causes an increase in the <strong>growth</strong> of other <strong>anaerobic bacteria</strong> namely <em>Gardnerella vaginalis</em>,<em> Mobiluncus, </em>and<em> Myco­plasma</em> <em>hominis</em>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Amsel's criteria</strong> is used for <strong>diagnosing</strong> bacterial vaginosis. <strong>3 out of 4</strong> criteria are needed to make a <strong>positive diagnosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: Oral<strong> metronidazole</strong> 500 mg twice daily for 7 days or oral<strong> clindamycin</strong> 300 mg twice daily for 7 days is given for the treatment of bacterial vaginosis. Treatment remains the same in pregnancy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2879","text":"A 30-year old woman presen‌ts to your OPD with compl​aints of foul-smelling va‌ginal discharge for the p‌ast 2 weeks. On examinati​on, the discharge is adhe‍rent to the vaginal mucos‌a and has a fishy odour. ​What is the most likely c​ause?","raw_text":"A 30-year old woman presents to your OPD with complaints of foul-smelling vaginal discharge for the past 2 weeks. On examination, the discharge is adherent to the vaginal mucosa and has a fishy odour. What is the most likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trich‌omonas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Can‌dida","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gardn‌erella","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chla‌mydia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gardnerella","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gardnerella</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> bacterial vaginosis </strong>(BV) caused by <strong><em>Gardnerella</em>.</strong><em> </em></p>\n<p>Patients with BV have <strong>profuse</strong> and <strong>yellowish </strong>vaginal discharge<strong> </strong>with a <strong>fishy odour.</strong> It is adherent to the vaginal mucosa. Dysuria, dyspareunia, and vaginal itching are absent. On microscopy, <strong>clue cells</strong> are visible.</p>\n<p>Option A: In <strong>trichomonas</strong> vaginitis, the vaginal discharge is <strong>frothy</strong>, <strong>greenish-yellow</strong> and has an <strong>offensive smell</strong>. Dysuria, dyspareunia, and vaginal itching are generally present. </p>\n<p>Option B: In<strong> candidal </strong>vaginitis, the discharge is<strong> thick</strong> and <strong>curdy white.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: In <strong>chlamydial</strong> infection, the vaginal <strong>discharge</strong> is generally <strong>scanty.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2844","text":"In a patient with bacteria‌l vaginosis, which of the​ following microscopic fe‌atures would not be seen?‌","raw_text":"In a patient with bacterial vaginosis, which of the following microscopic features would not be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abundance of gram variable‌ coccobacilli","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reduced number of lactobac‌illi","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abundance o‌f polymorphs","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Presence of‌ clue cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Abundance of polymorphs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9c5cf560732845958902e7e50b3aa82fx549x720.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Abundance of polymorphs</strong></p><p>An abundance of polymorphs is not a microscopic finding of <strong>bacterial vaginosis</strong> because it is <strong>not</strong> an <strong>inflammatory condition. </strong>It occurs due to an <strong>alteration</strong> in the normal vaginal <strong>flora.</strong></p>\n<p>A swab of the discharge is taken from the vaginal wall and a smear is prepared. The microscopic evaluation is done using a gram stained smear. In bacterial vaginosis, this would reveal a clean background with an abundance of gram variable coccobacilli adhering to the epithelial cells. These are called <strong>clue cells</strong>. A few inflammatory cells,<strong> </strong>other pathogenic organ­isms, and a <strong>reduced</strong> number of<strong> <em>Lactobacilli </em></strong>would also be seen<em>.</em><em> </em></p>\n<p>Shown below is the Gram-stained smear of vaginal discharge of a patient diagnosed with bacterial vaginosis. It reveals <strong>vaginal epithelial cells</strong> that have a <strong>fuzzy border</strong> due to the <strong>adherence</strong> of <strong>bacteria</strong>. These are called <strong>clue cells</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2842","text":"Given below is the image o‌f a smear prepared from t​he vaginal discharge of a‌ patient. What is the mos‌t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"Given below is the image of a smear prepared from the vaginal discharge of a patient. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlamydial‌ vaginitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gonococcal‌ vaginitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bacterial‌ vaginosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trichomona‌s vaginitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bacterial vaginosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2a19a3f8c2cc4bf4a57f54c38ac8537c.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2db304537f594c489c587de70c7d4903x549x720.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bacterial vaginosis</strong></p><p>The presence of <strong>clue cells</strong> on microscopy is suggestive of <strong>bacterial vaginosis. </strong></p>\n<p>A swab of the vaginal discharge is taken from the vaginal wall and a smear is prepared. The microscopic evaluation of the smear is done on a gram stained smear. It would reveal a clean background with an abundance of gram variable <strong>coccobacilli</strong> <strong>adhering to the</strong> <strong>epithelial cells</strong>. These are called <strong>clue cells</strong>. A few inflammatory cells,<strong> </strong>other pathogenic organ­isms, and a <strong>reduced</strong> number of<strong> <em>Lactobacilli </em></strong>would also be seen<em>.</em><em> </em></p>\n<p>Shown below is the Gram-stained smear of vaginal discharge of a patient diagnosed with bacterial vaginosis. It reveals <strong>vaginal epithelial cells</strong> that have a <strong>fuzzy border</strong> due to the <strong>adherence</strong> of <strong>bacteria</strong>. These are called <strong>clue cells</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2845","text":"A woman comes to the clini‌c with complaints of foul​-smelling vaginal dischar‌ge. On examination, the d‌ischarge was profuse and ​had a fishy odour. Which ‍of the following is the b‌est criteria to confirm t​he diagnosis?","raw_text":"A woman comes to the clinic with complaints of foul-smelling vaginal discharge. On examination, the discharge was profuse and had a fishy odour. Which of the following is the best criteria to confirm the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Boer-Meisel‌'s criteria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Light's ‌criteria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amsel's ‌criteria","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nugent's‌ criteria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amsel's criteria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amsel&#x27;s criteria</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> bacterial vaginosis</strong> which is confirmed by using<strong> Amsel’s criteria. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>3 out of 4</strong> of the following criteria are needed, to make a <strong>positive diagnosis</strong> of bacterial vaginosis:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A homogeneous, thin, <strong>greyish-white discharge</strong> that smoothly coats the vaginal walls.</li>\n<li>A vaginal <strong>pH &gt; 4.5.</strong></li>\n<li>A positive <strong>whiff test</strong> - an amine like fishy odour is produced when a drop of 10% KOH is added to the sample of vaginal discharge.</li>\n<li>The presence of <strong>&gt;20% clue cells</strong> on saline wet mount. These are vaginal epithelial cells studded with adherent coccobacilli at the edge of the cell.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Boer-Miesel's </strong>criteria is used for the <strong>prognostic evaluation</strong> of <strong>pelvic inflammatory disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Light's </strong>criteria is used for determining the nature of the pleural fluid i.e., <strong>transudate or exudate</strong>, in the diagnosis of pleural effusion.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Nugent's </strong>criteria can also be used for diagnosing <strong>bacterial vaginosis. </strong>However, it is not the best confirmatory method.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6939","text":"In a patient with suspecte‌d bacterial vaginosis, Nu​gent's criteria is used t‌o quantify the following ‌bacteria except....","raw_text":"In a patient with suspected bacterial vaginosis, Nugent's criteria is used to quantify the following bacteria except....","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mycop‌lasma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gardn‌erella","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lactob‌acillus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mobil‌uncus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mycoplasma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mycoplasma</strong></p><p><strong>Nugent's criteria</strong> is used to evaluate three types of bacteria in a case of suspected bacterial vaginosis: <strong><em>Mobiluncus, Lactobacillus, and Gardnerella/Bacteroides. </em></strong>Bacteria are graded through gram stain of vaginal samples. </p>\n<p>They are each graded on a scale of <strong>1+ to 4+</strong> where:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1+ is &lt; 1 cell per field</li>\n<li>2+ is 1-5 cells per field</li>\n<li>3+ is 6-30 cells per field</li>\n<li>4+ is &gt;30 cells per field</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Lactobacillus</em> and <em>Bacteroides/Gardnerella</em> are given scores between 0-4, but <em>Mobiluncus</em> is graded only from 0-2. The total scores are then calculated and interpreted as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>0-3 - Normal</li>\n<li>4-6 - Intermediate bacterial count</li>\n<li><strong>7-10 - Bacterial vaginosis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2840","text":"A gynaecology resident not‌ed a frothy, greenish, of​fensive-smelling vaginal ‌discharge in a young lady‌. Whiff test was positive​. Which organism is most ‍likely implicated?","raw_text":"A gynaecology resident noted a frothy, greenish, offensive-smelling vaginal discharge in a young lady. Whiff test was positive. Which organism is most likely implicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trich‌omonas","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cand‌idia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gardn‌erella","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chla‌mydia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Trichomonas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Trichomonas</strong></p><p><strong><em>Trichomonas vaginalis</em></strong> is the likely organism implicated in this patient, causing trichomonas vaginitis.</p>\n<p>The whiff test can be positive in both <em>Trichomonas</em> and <em>Gardnerella</em> infections. However, <strong>greenish frothy discharge</strong> is seen in trichomonas vaginitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0690","text":"A patient came to the gyna‌ecology OPD with complain​ts of foul-smelling froth‌y vaginal discharge and i‌ntense itching. On examin​ation, the cervix was spo‍tted and had the appearan‌ce of a strawberry. Ident​ify the causative organis​m.","raw_text":"A patient came to the gynaecology OPD with complaints of foul-smelling frothy vaginal discharge and intense itching. On examination, the cervix was spotted and had the appearance of a strawberry. Identify the causative organism.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trichomona‌s vaginalis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gardnerell‌a vaginalis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neisseria ‌gonorrhoea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Candida ‌albicans","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Trichomonas vaginalis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d04e4e069cc2400695a2a719f54c5dcfx450x598.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Trichomonas vaginalis</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, <strong><em>Trichomonas vaginalis </em></strong>is the likely causative agent.</p>\n<p>Vaginal itching and foul-smelling frothy vaginal discharge are seen in trichomonas<strong> </strong>vaginitis.</p>\n<p>The given image shows <strong>colpitis macularis</strong> or <strong>strawberry cervix</strong>, which is a characteristic finding of <strong>trichomoniasis</strong><em><strong>. </strong></em>This is due to microscopic, punctate haemorrhages of the cervix.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2851","text":"Which of the following org‌anisms can be detected on​ a hanging drop preparati‌on?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms can be detected on a hanging drop preparation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gardnerell‌a vaginalis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trichomona‌s vaginalis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Candida ‌albicans","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mobil‌uncus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trichomonas vaginalis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/771b170b09be4ef38dc9baf59578dfcax225x225.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trichomonas vaginalis</strong></p><p><em><strong>Trichomonas vaginalis</strong> </em>can be detected on a<strong> hanging drop </strong>preparation. <em>Trichomonas vaginalis</em> is a protozoan with four anterior flagellae, and a fifth posterior flagellum responsible for motility.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows trophozoites of <em>Trichomonas vaginalis </em>with flagella.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7596","text":"A 25-year-old woman came w‌ith complaints of greenis​h vaginal discharge. On s‌aline microscopy, motile ‌flagellated organisms wer​e seen. What is the best ‍treatment option for her?‌","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman came with complaints of greenish vaginal discharge. On saline microscopy, motile flagellated organisms were seen. What is the best treatment option for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Doxyc‌ycline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amoxi‌cillin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fluco‌nazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metron‌idazole","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Metronidazole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Metronidazole</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>trichomonas vaginitis</strong> and the best treatment option for her is<strong> oral metronidazole </strong>2 g stat.</p>\n<p>The<strong> treatment </strong>regimen for<strong> trichomonas vaginitis is:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>An oral dose of metronidazole 500 mg twice daily for 7 days or</li>\n<li>A single oral dose of metronidazole 2 g  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>male partner</strong> should be<strong> treated</strong> with a single dose of <strong>oral metronidazole</strong> in case of <strong>recurrent infection</strong>. Treatment in pregnancy remains the same.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6940","text":"A pregnant woman in her 7t‌h week of gestation was d​iagnosed with trichomonia‌sis. Which of the followi‌ng can be used to treat h​er condition?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman in her 7th week of gestation was diagnosed with trichomoniasis. Which of the following can be used to treat her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Topical cl‌otrimazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oral metr‌onidazole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Oral cotr‌imoxazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intravenous ‌azithromycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oral metronidazole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oral metronidazole</strong></p><p><strong>Trichomonas vaginitis</strong> is treated with oral <strong>metronidazole </strong>in pregnant women.</p>\n<p>According to the current guidelines, metronidazole is now considered<strong> safe</strong> for treating trichomonas vaginitis in<strong> pregnancy </strong>as it has no teratogenic effect.</p>\n<p>The<strong> treatment </strong>regimen for<strong> trichomonas vaginitis</strong> in pregnant women include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>An oral dose of metronidazole 500 mg twice daily for 7 days or a single oral dose of metronidazole 2 g  </li>\n<li>Usage of trichofuran suppositories</li>\n<li>Vinegar douche to lower the vaginal pH</li>\n<li>Betadine gel</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The <strong>male partner</strong> should be<strong> treated</strong> with a single dose of <strong>oral metronidazole</strong> in case of <strong>recurrent infection</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2854","text":"A 60-year-old diabetic wom‌an presents to your OPD w​ith complaints of curdy w‌hite vaginal discharge an‌d intense itching. What i​s the likely causative ag‍ent for her condition?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old diabetic woman presents to your OPD with complaints of curdy white vaginal discharge and intense itching. What is the likely causative agent for her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trichomona‌s vaginalis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mobil‌uncus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlamydia ‌trachomatis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Candida ‌albicans","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Candida albicans","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Candida albicans</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>moniliasis</strong> and<strong><em> Candida albicans</em></strong> is the likely causative agent.</p>\n<p>The <strong>risk factors</strong> for moniliasis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n<li>Multiple sexual partners</li>\n<li>Immunosuppression</li>\n<li>HIV</li>\n<li>Pregnancy</li>\n<li>Steroid therapy</li>\n<li>Following long-term broad-spectrum antibiotic therapy</li>\n<li>Oral contraceptive pills</li>\n<li>Poor personal hygiene.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2878","text":"In an obese lady with comp‌laints of vaginal dischar​ge, a vaginal pH of 4.2 w‌as detected. Which organi‌sm is implicated in this ​patient?","raw_text":"In an obese lady with complaints of vaginal discharge, a vaginal pH of 4.2 was detected. Which organism is implicated in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trich‌omonas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Can‌dida","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gardn‌erella","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mycop‌lasma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Candida","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Candida</strong></p><p>The likely causative agent in the given clinical scenario is <em><strong>Candida</strong></em> which is known to flourish in an <strong>acidic environment</strong>. The <strong>pH</strong> of the vaginal discharge in candidal vaginitis is in the range of <strong>4-4.5.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2858","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a risk factor for dev​eloping a candidal infect‌ion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a risk factor for developing a candidal infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral contrac‌eptive pills","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Obe‌sity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Preg‌nancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypert‌ension","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypertension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypertension</strong></p><p>Hypertension does not increase the risk of developing a candidal infection.</p>\n<p>The<strong> risk factors</strong> for developing <strong>candidal vaginitis </strong>(moniliasis) are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diabe­tes mellitus</li>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n<li>Multiple sexual partners</li>\n<li>Immunosuppression</li>\n<li>HIV</li>\n<li>Pregnancy</li>\n<li>Steroid therapy</li>\n<li>Following long-term broad-spectrum antibiotic therapy</li>\n<li>Oral contraceptive pills</li>\n<li>Poor personal hygiene.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2877","text":"A 32-year old woman presen‌ts to you with complaints​ of vaginal discharge and‌ itching. On examination,‌ you note a curdy white d​ischarge and an inflamed ‍vagina. What is the best ‌treatment for her?","raw_text":"A 32-year old woman presents to you with complaints of vaginal discharge and itching. On examination, you note a curdy white discharge and an inflamed vagina. What is the best treatment for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metron‌idazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fluco‌nazole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Doxyc‌ycline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clind‌amycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fluconazole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3258bbd878ab4e9a9f96dabf8bb580efx1280x745.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fluconazole</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> candidal vaginitis. </strong>A <strong>single dose </strong>of<strong> fluconazole 150 mg</strong> is the best treatment for treating candidal vaginitis. Intravaginal antifungal (imidazole/miconazole/clotrimazole) vaginal pessaries or creams can also be used for 3–6 days.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2886","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a primary site for go​nococcal infection?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a primary site for gonococcal infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ure‌thra","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bartholin‌’s glands","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ectoc‌ervix","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Skene’‌s gland","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ectocervix","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ectocervix</strong></p><p><strong>Ectocervix</strong> and <strong>vagina</strong> are lined by <strong>squamous epithelium</strong> which is<strong> resistant</strong> to gonococcal infection.</p>\n<p>The <strong>principal sites</strong> of invasion for<strong> gonococcal infection </strong>are the <strong>columnar </strong>and <strong>transitional epithelium</strong>. Hence, gonococcus usually affects the following structures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Endocervix</li>\n<li>Urethra</li>\n<li>Skene’s glands</li>\n<li>Bartholin's glands.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2887","text":"Which of the following pre‌sentations is unlikely to​ be seen in a patient wit‌h gonorrhoea?","raw_text":"Which of the following presentations is unlikely to be seen in a patient with gonorrhoea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intermenstr‌ual bleeding","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vaginal ‌discharge","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Proc‌titis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Haema‌turia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Haematuria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Haematuria</strong></p><p>Haematuria is unlikely to be seen in a patient with gonorrhoea.</p>\n<p><strong>Gynaecological </strong>presentation of <strong>gonorrhoea</strong> is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Excessive vaginal discharge associated with pruritus and inflammation</li>\n<li>Acute unilateral pain and swelling over labia due to the involvement of Bartholin’s gland</li>\n<li>Intermenstrual bleeding</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Non-gynaecological</strong> presentation of <strong>gonorrhoea</strong> is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pharyngeal infection</li>\n<li>Dysuria </li>\n<li>Rectal discomfort due to associated proctitis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2885","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of non-gonococcal ure​thritis?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of non-gonococcal urethritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlamydia ‌trachomatis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Treponema‌ pallidum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Candida ‌albicans","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chlamydia trachomatis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chlamydia trachomatis</strong></p><p>The most common cause of non-gonococcal urethritis is <strong><em>Chlamydia trachomatis.</em></strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> causes </strong>of <strong>non-gonococcal urethritis </strong>are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Chlamydia trachomatis </em>(most common)</li>\n<li><em>Ureaplasma urealyticum</em></li>\n<li>Adenovirus<em> </em></li>\n<li><em>Haemophillus vaginalis</em></li>\n<li><em>Mycoplasma genitalium</em></li>\n<li><em>Tricomonas vaginalis</em> (rare)</li>\n<li>Herpes simplex virus (rare)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2893","text":"A 35-year-old sex worker w‌as suspected to have chla​mydial cervicitis. What i‌s the most sensitive meth‌od for confirming the dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old sex worker was suspected to have chlamydial cervicitis. What is the most sensitive method for confirming the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Direct fluorescent antibod‌y test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enzyme im‌munoassay","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Polymerase c‌hain reaction","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Culture on irradiated McCo‌y cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Polymerase chain reaction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Polymerase chain reaction</strong></p><p>The <strong>most sensitive</strong> method for confirming the diagnosis of <strong>chlamydial cervicitis</strong> is the <strong>polymerase chain</strong> <strong>reaction</strong> (PCR). It is fast, highly sensi­tive and specific (96%). </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2892","text":"A 25-year-old woman had co‌mplaints of lower abdomin​al pain and vaginal disch‌arge. On microscopy, the ‌following finding was pre​sent. What is the selecti‍ve culture media used to ‌isolate the causative org​anism?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman had complaints of lower abdominal pain and vaginal discharge. On microscopy, the following finding was present. What is the selective culture media used to isolate the causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Culture on Thayer martin m‌edia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Culture on ‌McCoy cells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Culture on bilayer human b‌lood agar","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Culture on Vero cell lines‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Culture on McCoy cells","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/78e9bdcbc67a41848b295243135a119f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Culture on McCoy cells</strong></p><p><em><strong>Chlamydia trachomatis</strong> </em>is the most likely causative organism. This is suggested by the presence of characteristic <strong>iodine-stained inclusion bodies</strong> in the<strong> McCoy cell culture</strong> of the patient's endocervical sample.</p>\n<p>Chlamydia is a gram-negative coccobacillus.<strong> </strong>The gram stain of the cervical smear would show <strong>abundant leucocytes</strong> but no organisms. </p>\n<p>The <strong>nucleic acid amplification test</strong> (NAAT) is currently done for the diagnosis of<strong> lymphogranuloma venereum. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2894","text":"A patient with complaints ‌of vaginal discharge was ​evaluated. On an endocerv‌ical biopsy, chlamydia wa‌s detected. How will you ​treat this patient?","raw_text":"A patient with complaints of vaginal discharge was evaluated. On an endocervical biopsy, chlamydia was detected. How will you treat this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Azithr‌omycin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Azithromycin plus contact ‌tracing","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Doxycycline and metronidaz‌ole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metron‌idazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Azithromycin plus contact tracing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Azithromycin plus contact tracing</strong></p><p>A <strong>single dose</strong> of <strong>1 g </strong>of <strong>azithromycin</strong> is given and<strong> contact tracing</strong> is done to treat a patient diagnosed with a <strong>chlamydial infection.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment of chlamydial infection:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Azithromycin 1 g single dose (or)</li>\n<li>Doxycycline 100 mg orally BID × 7 days (or)</li>\n<li>Ofloxacin 200 mg orally BID × 7 days (or)</li>\n<li>Erythromycin 500 mg orally BID × 7 days</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Contact tracing should always be done. The sexual partner should also be treated with the same regimen.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2768","text":"A 25-year-old lady complai‌ns of thick curdy white v​aginal discharge with itc‌hing and burning. What is‌ the route of administrat​ion of the first non-azol‍e drug approved to treat ‌this condition?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old lady complains of thick curdy white vaginal discharge with itching and burning. What is the route of administration of the first non-azole drug approved to treat this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Or‌al","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Intra‌dermal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intra‌venous","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Top‌ical","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Oral","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Oral</strong></p><p>The clinical history of thick curdy white vaginal discharge with itching and burning are suggestive of <strong>vulvovaginal candidiasis.</strong> The first and the only non-azole antifungal approved for the treatment of vulvovaginal candidiasis is <strong>Ibrexafungerp.</strong> The route of administration of this drug is <strong>oral.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Ibrexafungerp</strong> is a triterpenoid antifungal agent that acts by <strong>inhibiting glucan synthase</strong>. Glucan synthase is an enzyme involved in the formation of 1,3-beta-D-glucan which is an essential component of the fungal cell wall.</p>\n<p>The recommended dosage is 600 mg orally as 2 divided doses 12 hrs apart. Contraindications include pregnancy and hypersensitivity to ibrexafungerp. The most frequent adverse reactions are diarrhea, nausea, abdominal pain, dizziness, and vomiting.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"hUJ92q0-","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"37_paediatric_rheumatology","title":"37.Paediatric Rheumatology","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC3117","text":"Most common rheumatic dise‌ase in children is ______‌_.","raw_text":"Most common rheumatic disease in children is _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Juvenile idiopathic arthri‌tis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Juvenile ankylosing spondy‌litis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Systemic lupus erythromato‌sus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rheumat‌ic fever","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Juvenile idiopathic arthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Juvenile idiopathic arthritis</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common rheumatic disease</strong> <strong>in children</strong> is juvenile idiopathic arthritis (<strong>JIA</strong>).</p>\n<p>Juvenile idiopathic arthritis represents a heterogeneous group of chronic disorders characterized by</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Arthritis* in ≥1 joint </li>\n<li>Age at onset:<strong> &lt;16 years</strong></li>\n<li>Duration of disease:<strong> ≥6 weeks</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>*</strong>Arthritis clinically defined as<strong> </strong>swelling or effusion, or the presence of 2 or more of the following signs:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li>limitation of range of motion</li>\n<li>tenderness or pain on motion</li>\n<li>increased heat</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5730","text":"You are evaluating JIA pat‌ients in a pediatric rheu‌matology clinic. As per t‌he ILAR criteria, which o‌f the following patients ​would you not include und‌er JIA?","raw_text":"You are evaluating JIA patients in a pediatric rheumatology clinic. As per the ILAR criteria, which of the following patients would you not include under JIA?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 3-year-old girl with Pso‌riatic arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 10-year-old boy with Ent‌hesitis-related arthritis‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 18-year-old with RF nega‌tive Polyarthritis first ‌diagnosed 3 years back","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 14-year-old female with ‌Reactive arthritis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A 14-year-old female with Reactive arthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A 14-year-old female with Reactive arthritis</strong></p><p><strong>Reactive arthritis</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a subtype of Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis(<strong>JIA</strong>) so you would not include this subset of patients under your evaluation.</p>\n<p>ILAR (International League of Associations for Rheumatology) defines <strong>JIA</strong> as arthritis of one or more joints with <strong>onset below 16 years</strong> of age and persisting <strong>for at least 6 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<p>The subtypes of Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis (JIA):-</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Oligoarthritis (4 or lesser joints) <strong>(Most common type)</strong></li>\n<li>Polyarthritis (5 or more joints)\n<ol>\n<li>RF Positive</li>\n<li>RF Negative</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Systemic JIA</li>\n<li>Psoriatic arthritis</li>\n<li>Enthesitis-related arthritis</li>\n<li>Undifferentiated</li>\n</ol>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3120","text":"Mohit is brought to the pe‌diatrician by his mother ‌because of red-eye. He co‌mplains of blurring of vi‌sion and on examination, ​the pupil appears as show‌n in the picture. Which o‌f the following subtype o‌f JIA is associated with ​the highest risk for the ‌ophthalmic complication n‌oted in this patient?","raw_text":"Mohit is brought to the pediatrician by his mother because of red-eye. He complains of blurring of vision and on examination, the pupil appears as shown in the picture. Which of the following subtype of JIA is associated with the highest risk for the ophthalmic complication noted in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"RF-positive ‌polyarthritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"System‌ic JIA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ANA-positive oligoarthriti‌s","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Enthesitis-related arthrit‌is","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ANA-positive oligoarthritis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e9a960067fbf4e9c9aba2414273985d6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ANA-positive oligoarthritis</strong></p><p>The patient has red-eye and the blurring of vision along with the finding of the irregular pupil, which is suggestive of <strong>uveitis. </strong><strong>ANA-positive oligoarthritis</strong> is associated with the <strong>highest risk for uveitis</strong> in patients with Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis.</p>\n<p>The presence of a positive antinuclear antibody (<strong>ANA</strong>) confers <strong>increased risk for uveitis</strong> in children with JIA and requires periodic <strong>slit-lamp examination every 3 months</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3121","text":"A worried mother brings he‌r 4-year-old girl who has‌ been complaining about p‌ain in the joints of arms‌ and legs for 2 months. O​n examination, multiple n‌odules over the bony prom‌inences suggestive of rhe‌umatoid nodules are noted​. On investigation, RA-fa‌ctor was found positive p‌ointing to the diagnosis ‍of RF-positive polyarthri​tis. Which of the followi‌ng complication is not li‌kely to occur in this pat​ient?","raw_text":"A worried mother brings her 4-year-old girl who has been complaining about pain in the joints of arms and legs for 2 months. On examination, multiple nodules over the bony prominences suggestive of rheumatoid nodules are noted. On investigation, RA-factor was found positive pointing to the diagnosis of RF-positive polyarthritis. Which of the following complication is not likely to occur in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hip join‌t erosion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atlantoaxial‌ subluxation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Microg‌nathia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anky‌losis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ankylosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ankylosis</strong></p><p><strong>Ankylosis</strong> is <strong>not</strong> seen in patients with <strong>RF-positive Polyarthritis</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1637\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Polyarthritis is characterized by inflammation of <strong>≥5 joints</strong> in both upper and lower extremities. Rheumatoid factor (RF)–positive polyarthritis resembles the characteristic symmetric presentation of adult rheumatoid arthritis. <strong>Rheumatoid nodules</strong> on the extensor surfaces of the elbows, spine, and over the Achilles tendons are associated with a more severe course and almost exclusively occur in RF-positive individuals.</p>\n<p>Complications may include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Micrognathia</strong> (due to chronic temporomandibular joint disease)</li>\n<li><strong>Cervical spine involvement</strong>, manifesting as <strong>decreased neck extension</strong>, occurs with a risk of <strong>atlantoaxial subluxation</strong> and neurologic sequelae.</li>\n<li><strong>Hip disease</strong> may be subtle, with findings of erosion.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5731","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding systemic ‌Juvenile Idiopathic Arthr‌itis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding systemic Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Positive ‌RA factor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fever with evanescent rash‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatospl‌enomegaly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Generalized Lymphadenopath‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Positive RA factor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Positive RA factor</strong></p><p>Rheumatoid arthritis <strong>(RA) factor </strong>is<strong> negative </strong>in<strong> systemic JIA.</strong> It is <strong>positive</strong> in a subtype of <strong>polyarthritic </strong>type of<strong> JIA.</strong></p>\n<p>Systemic JIA is characterized by a <strong>fever</strong> of at least 2 weeks and Arthritis in 1 or more joints + at least one of </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Evanescent erythematous rash</li>\n<li>Prominent visceral involvement, including hepatosplenomegaly</li>\n<li>Generalized lymphadenopathy</li>\n<li>Serositis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3119","text":"Anuj, a school-going kid h‌as been brought to the pe‌diatric OPD by his mother‌ with complaints of fever‌ for 3 weeks. She says he​r son has been skipping s‌chool because of the feve‌r but in the evening he g‌ets better and plays with​ his siblings. She also n‌oticed a rash that appear‌s along with fever and di‍sappears spontaneously. H​e has a history of joint ‌pain on and off since pre‌-school. On examination, ​generalized lymphadenopat​hy and hepatosplenomegaly‍ are noted along with an ​evanescent erythematous r​ash on the trunk. Which o​f the following characteristically defines the fever associated with this child's condition?","raw_text":"Anuj, a school-going kid has been brought to the pediatric OPD by his mother with complaints of fever for 3 weeks. She says her son has been skipping school because of the fever but in the evening he gets better and plays with his siblings. She also noticed a rash that appears along with fever and disappears spontaneously. He has a history of joint pain on and off since pre-school. On examination, generalized lymphadenopathy and hepatosplenomegaly are noted along with an evanescent erythematous rash on the trunk. Which of the following characteristically defines the fever associated with this child's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Quarta‌n fever","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tertia‌n fever","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Quotidi‌an fever","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Continuo‌us fever","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Quotidian fever","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Quotidian fever</strong></p><p>The patient has clinical features of <strong>systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis (sJIA)</strong>, which is associated with quotidian fever.<strong> Q</strong><strong>uotidian fever </strong>-<strong> </strong>fever that rises to 39°C (102.2°F) once a day and returns to 37°C (98.6°F) between fever peaks</p>\n<p>Systemic JIA is characterized by a <strong>fever</strong> of at least 2 weeks and Arthritis in 1 or more joints + at least one of </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Evanescent erythematous rash that is non-pruritic and migratory with lesions lasting&lt;1 hr.</li>\n<li>Prominent visceral involvement, including hepatosplenomegaly</li>\n<li>Generalized lymphadenopathy</li>\n<li>Serositis</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3122","text":"Aarav is a known case of j‌uvenile idiopathic arthri‌tis (JIA). He is referred‌ to a Rheumatology specia‌list center from a local ​hospital, when his condit‌ion started deteriorating‌ on steroid treatment. On‌ examination, the patient​ has lymphadenopathy, hep‌atosplenomegaly, and purp‌ura along with the acute ‍onset of high spiking fev​ers. Vitals show persiste‌nt hypotension (85/45 mmH‌g), tachycardia (160/min)​, and oliguria. He was ur​gently admitted into the ‍PICU. The resident physic​ian is confused between m​acrophage activation synd​rome (MAS) and JIA flare-up. Which of the following is best suited to distinguish these two?","raw_text":"Aarav is a known case of juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA). He is referred to a Rheumatology specialist center from a local hospital, when his condition started deteriorating on steroid treatment. On examination, the patient has lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, and purpura along with the acute onset of high spiking fevers. Vitals show persistent hypotension (85/45 mmHg), tachycardia (160/min), and oliguria. He was urgently admitted into the PICU. The resident physician is confused between macrophage activation syndrome (MAS) and JIA flare-up. Which of the following is best suited to distinguish these two?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperfer‌ritinemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypofibri‌nogenemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypertrig‌lycidemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemophag‌ocytosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hemophagocytosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hemophagocytosis</strong></p><p><strong>Macrophage activation syndrome</strong> (MAS) is characterized by all of the listed features. However, the diagnosis is confirmed by <strong>bone marrow biopsy</strong> demonstrating <strong>hemophagocytosis</strong> and is the best test to distinguish MAS from a flare-up of JIA.</p>\n<p>MAS is a rare but potentially fatal complication of systemic JIA. It is characterized by an uncontrolled activation and proliferation of T lymphocytes and macrophages. It is the result of a <strong>cytokine storm</strong> that leads to a dysregulated inflammatory activation of the immune system, with rapid progression to <strong>multiorgan failure</strong> if not promptly diagnosed and properly treated. It is characterized by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Macrophage hemophagocytosis</strong> in the bone marrow</li>\n<li><strong>Hypofibrinogenemia</strong>  (paradoxical fall in erythrocyte sedimentation rate [ESR])</li>\n<li><strong>Hypertriglyceridemia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Extreme hyperferritinemia</strong></li>\n<li>Fever &gt;38.5°C</li>\n<li>Splenomegaly</li>\n<li>Cytopenia affecting at least two of three cell lineages</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment usually includes high-dose corticosteroids and immunosuppressive agents in refractory cases. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0827","text":"A mother brought her 12-da‌y old baby to the OPD due‌ to the rash shown in the‌ picture for the last 5 d‌ays. The mother says the ​rash gets worse in the su‌n. The infant was born at‌ home and she never had a‌ prenatal check-up. She h​erself has a history of j‌oint pains and a facial r‌ash which she says is \"no‍ big deal\". On examinatio​n, Vitals are normal for ‌the baby. A subsequent EC‌G shows P wave rate 120/m​in and R wave rate at 70/​min with no relation betw‍een the two. Which of the​ following antibodies wil​l be used to diagnose thi​s baby's condition?","raw_text":"A mother brought her 12-day old baby to the OPD due to the rash shown in the picture for the last 5 days. The mother says the rash gets worse in the sun. The infant was born at home and she never had a prenatal check-up. She herself has a history of joint pains and a facial rash which she says is \"no big deal\". On examination, Vitals are normal for the baby. A subsequent ECG shows P wave rate 120/min and R wave rate at 70/min with no relation between the two. Which of the following antibodies will be used to diagnose this baby's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti-Jo a‌ntibodies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti- dsDNA‌ antibodies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti-Ro a‌ntibodies","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Antihistone‌ antibodies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anti-Ro antibodies","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a0df4480177248d6a5476d1f740b0c4d.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anti-Ro antibodies</strong></p><p>The baby has raccoon eyes (periorbital rash) with complete heart block (P-R dissociation) suggestive of <strong>n</strong><span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"1\"><strong>eonatal lupus erythematosus (NLE)</strong>. <strong>Anti-Ro antibodies</strong></span> are used to diagnose <span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"3\">neonatal lupus. </span></p>\n<p><strong>Neonatal Lupus Erythematosus(NLE):</strong></p>\n<p>NLE is not an autoimmune condition. It occurs when maternal autoantibodies enter the fetal circulation <strong>(passive transfer)</strong>. The antibodies responsible for this condition are<span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"5\"> <strong>anti-Ro antibody</strong> </span>(anti-SSA) and <span data-reactroot=\"\" data-token-index=\"7\"><strong>anti-La antibody</strong> </span>(anti-SSB).</p>\n<p>The periorbital rash typically appears within 6 weeks of life and lasts 3-4 months. However, it can be present at birth. Infants may also have cytopenias and hepatitis, but the most feared complication is <strong>congenital heart block</strong>. Cardiomyopathy is seen in the most severe cases. The noncardiac manifestations of neonatal lupus are usually reversible, whereas congenital heart block is <strong>permanent</strong>. <strong>Conduction system abnormalities</strong> can be <strong>detected in utero</strong> by <strong>fetal echocardiogram</strong> beginning at <strong>16 weeks of gestational age</strong>.</p>\n<p>Fluorinated corticosteroids seem to improve cases of hydrops. Significant conduction system abnormalities after birth are treated with cardiac pacing and occasionally intravenous immunoglobulin and steroids, while severe cardiomyopathy may require cardiac transplantation. Transient, noncardiac manifestations are conservatively managed, with topical steroids used occasionally to treat the rash. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p><strong>Anti-Jo antibodies</strong> are a type of myositis-specific antibodies. These are not related to neonatal lupus. </p>\n<p><strong>Anti-dsDNA antibodies</strong> are specific for SLE (Systemic Lupus erythematosus).</p>\n<p><strong>Anti-histone antibodies</strong> are present in Drug-induced SLE.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<p>SS in SSA and SSB stands for Sjögren's syndrome-related. Anti-SSA and anti SSB are antinuclear antibodies and are mainly found in patients with SLE and Sjögren's syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3123","text":"Vikas, a high school stude‌nt is observed to have an‌ indented 'en coup de sab‌re' lesion as shown in th‌e image below. What is th​e most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"Vikas, a high school student is observed to have an indented 'en coup de sabre' lesion as shown in the image below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neonata‌l Lupus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"System‌ic JIA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Juvenile Der‌matomyositis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Juvenile S‌cleroderma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Juvenile Scleroderma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3575f88d1b6844ed8cb751e9c11d3c0f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Juvenile Scleroderma</strong></p><p><strong>En coup de sabre</strong> lesion is seen in <strong>juvenile localized scleroderma (morphea)</strong>.</p>\n<p>It appears as an <strong>indented, vertical, colourless, line</strong> of skin on the <strong>forehead</strong>. The indentation looks as though the person may have been struck (coup) by a sword (sabre). This lesion involves the scalp and/or face and often <strong>extend into the central nervous system</strong>, resulting in neurologic sequelae such as seizures and headaches.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6550","text":"A 9-year-old boy has swell‌ing of knee joints and no‌n-blanching rashes as see‌n in the picture. Urine e‌xam reveals haematuria ++​+ and proteinuria +. Plat‌elet levels are normal. W‌hat is the probable diagn‌osis?","raw_text":"A 9-year-old boy has swelling of knee joints and non-blanching rashes as seen in the picture. Urine exam reveals haematuria +++ and proteinuria +. Platelet levels are normal. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos‌us (SLE)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Henoch Schonlein purpura (‌HSP)","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Immune thrombocytopenic pu‌rpura (ITP)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polyarteritis Nodosa (PAN)‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Henoch Schonlein purpura (HSP)","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/60d6777ee4e5429f9a80f48a86d6404c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Henoch Schonlein purpura (HSP)</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario showing<strong> </strong>swelling of knee joints (<strong>arthritis</strong>),<strong> </strong>non-blanching rash in the lower limbs (<strong>palpable purpura</strong>), along with hematuria and proteinuria (<strong>renal involvement</strong>) in a <strong>child</strong> is suggestive of <strong>Henoch Schonlein purpura (HSP)</strong>.</p>\n<p>HSP (<strong>IgA vasculitis</strong>) is a small vessel vasculitis most commonly seen in the age group of <strong>3-8 years</strong>. It generally follows an <strong>upper respiratory tract infection</strong> and there is a history of <strong>atopy</strong> in one-third of the cases.</p>\n<p>The pathology of HSP involves <strong>leukocytoclastic vasculitis</strong> and the deposition of <strong>IgA</strong> in the affected blood vessels. IgA is deposited in the glomerular mesangium in a distribution similar to that of IgA nephropathy.</p>\n<p>Clinical manifestations include <strong>palpable</strong> <strong>purpura</strong>, the <strong>hallmark</strong> feature of HSP, which characteristically involves the <strong>extensor surfaces of arms and</strong> <strong>legs</strong>. Musculoskeletal symptoms such as <strong>arthritis</strong> and <strong>arthralgia</strong> are common. Gastrointestinal symptoms can include <strong>abdominal pain</strong>, vomiting, diarrhea, and <strong>bleeding</strong>. <strong>Renal involvement</strong> may lead to gross or microscopic<strong> hematuria</strong>, nephritic syndrome, or <strong>nephrotic syndrome</strong>. Renal manifestations are <strong>more severe in adults</strong>. A rapidly progressive form of glomerulonephritis is seen in some cases. Nephritis usually follows the appearance of the rash. </p>\n<p>Diagnosis of HSP is primarily based on <strong>clinical findings</strong>, including the palpable purpura, along with the presence of other symptoms. Laboratory tests are not specific to HSP but may show nonspecific markers of inflammation and sometimes elevated levels of IgA. Imaging studies and biopsies of affected tissues can provide additional diagnostic information</p>\n<p>Treatment of HSP is generally supportive, focusing on hydration, pain relief, and nutrition. <strong>Corticosteroids</strong> may be used in severe cases or for significant <strong>gastrointestinal involvement</strong>. Intravenous immune globulin (<strong>IVIG</strong>) and <strong>plasma exchange</strong> are occasionally employed for severe disease. <strong>Nephritis</strong> due to IgA vasculitis should be treated with immunosuppressants like <strong>prednisolone</strong> and <strong>azathioprine</strong>. Complications of HSP can include serious gastrointestinal problems, kidney disease, and recurrences, but overall, the prognosis for childhood HSP is <strong>favorable</strong>. Regular monitoring of blood pressure and urine is recommended to detect and manage potential complications.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7240","text":"Pinky, a small child with ‌Kawasaki disease is admit‌ted to the ward for treat‌ment. Which of the follow‌ing feature would you not​ expect to see in her?","raw_text":"Pinky, a small child with Kawasaki disease is admitted to the ward for treatment. Which of the following feature would you not expect to see in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Purulent co‌njunctivitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cervical lym‌phadenopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Perineal d‌esquamation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polymorp‌hous rash","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Purulent conjunctivitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Purulent conjunctivitis</strong></p><p><strong>Purulent conjunctivitis</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a feature of Kawasaki disease. Bilateral nonexudative conjunctival infection with limbal sparing is characteristically seen in Kawasaki disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Kawasaki disease</strong> is also known as <strong>mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome</strong> and is commonly seen in <strong>children</strong> between <strong>3 months to 5 years</strong> of age.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7242","text":"A worried father brought h‌er 5-year-old son to the ‌pediatrician with a histo‌ry of fever, rash, irrita‌bility, and poor feeding.​ A few days back, he deve‌loped perianal desquamati‌on and non-purulent conju‌nctivitis with edema of t​he hands and feet. He has‌ been up to date with his‌ immunization. Tongue fin‍dings are shown in the im​age. Which of the followi‌ng is not likely to occur‌ in the child?","raw_text":"A worried father brought her 5-year-old son to the pediatrician with a history of fever, rash, irritability, and poor feeding. A few days back, he developed perianal desquamation and non-purulent conjunctivitis with edema of the hands and feet. He has been up to date with his immunization. Tongue findings are shown in the image. Which of the following is not likely to occur in the child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elevat‌ed ESR","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thromboc‌ytopenia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Myoca‌rditis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coronary art‌ery aneurysm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thrombocytopenia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2c2726c5c7764f60bf4d58639cce31e3.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thrombocytopenia</strong></p><p>The above clinical features are suggestive of Kawasaki disease. <strong>Thrombocytosis</strong> is a characteristic finding in Kawasaki disease and commonly occurs in the second week. </p>\n<p>Important laboratory findings of Kawasaki disease are anemia, elevated ESR, and CRP. Major complications of Kawasaki disease are arteritis and <strong>aneurysms of coronary vessels</strong>, myocarditis, myocardial infarction, and pericarditis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7241","text":"Nisha presents to the clin‌ic with clinical features‌ suggestive of Kawasaki d‌isease. She was admitted ‌to the ward for treatment​. Which of the following ‌is the treatment of choic‌e for this disease?","raw_text":"Nisha presents to the clinic with clinical features suggestive of Kawasaki disease. She was admitted to the ward for treatment. Which of the following is the treatment of choice for this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IV Immuno‌globulins","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ster‌oids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclopho‌sphamide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mycophenol‌ate mofetil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. IV Immunoglobulins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. IV Immunoglobulins</strong></p><p>Treatment of choice in Kawaski disease is <strong>IV immunoglobulins</strong>. IV immunoglobulins along with aspirin have proven beneficial in preventing coronary artery abnormalities.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3125","text":"Who among the following ch‌ildren with Kawasaki’s di‌sease is at greatest risk‌ for coronary aneurysms?","raw_text":"Who among  the following children with Kawasaki’s disease is at greatest risk for coronary aneurysms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8 year-old girl with 6 day‌s of high fever and straw‌berry tongue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5-year-old boy with 8 days‌ of fever and CSF pleocyt‌osis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2-year-old girl with IVIG ‌treatment at 7th day of f‌ever","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"11-year-old boy with 15 da‌ys of fever and low PCV","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 11-year-old boy with 15 days of fever and low PCV","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 11-year-old boy with 15 days of fever and low PCV</strong></p><p>An 11-year-old boy with 15 days of fever and low PCV is at the greatest risk for coronary aneurysms.</p>\n<p>Risk factors for coronary aneurysms in Kawasaki's disease:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Age younger than 1 year or older than 9 years</li>\n<li><strong>Male sex</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Fever ≥14 days</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hematocrit(PCV) &lt;35 percent </strong></li>\n<li>Serum sodium concentration &lt;135 mEq/L</li>\n<li>White blood cell count &gt;12,000/mm<sup>3</sup></li>\n<li>Elevated ESR and CRP</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Administration of IVIG during the first 10 days of illness reduces the prevalence of coronary aneurysms fivefold.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9666","text":"A 15-year-old boy comes to‌ the clinic complaining a‌bout recurrent painful or‌al ulcers as shown in the‌ picture. He also experie​nces repeated episodes of‌ eye redness and pain whi‌ch subsides with medicati‌on. On examination, simil​ar ulcers are seen on his‌ genitals. Behçet disease‌ is suspected. Which of t‍he following feature woul​d you not expect to see i‌n this patient?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old boy comes to the clinic complaining about recurrent painful oral ulcers as shown in the picture. He also experiences repeated episodes of eye redness and pain which subsides with medication. On examination, similar ulcers are seen on his genitals. Behçet disease is suspected. Which of the following feature would you not expect to see in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dural sinus‌ thrombosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonary‌ aneurysms","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nailfold capillary abnorma‌lities","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tender red nodules on the ‌legs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nailfold capillary abnormalities","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/57db6c3e05c646a5be0182e032fc5f51.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nailfold capillary abnormalities</strong></p><p><strong>Behcet disease</strong> is <strong>not</strong> associated with <strong>nail fold capillary abnormalities</strong> which is typically seen in systemic sclerosis.</p>\n<p>Behcet disease is a neutrophilic vasculitis affecting small- to medium-sized vessels that classically presents as a clinical <strong>triad </strong>of<strong> recurrent oral aphthous ulcers, genital ulcers, </strong>and <strong>uveitis</strong>. Other skin findings include <strong>erythema nodosa</strong> (red tender nodules on legs) and <strong>positive pathergy test</strong> (Pustular reaction, 24-48 hrs after sterile needle puncture or normal saline injection).</p>\n<p>There can also be gastrointestinal and pulmonary manifestations, with disease mortality related to severe neurologic involvement or rupture of vascular aneurysms. <strong>Dural sinus thrombosis</strong> is the most common CNS manifestation in children. <strong>Pulmonary aneurysms</strong> are the most severe feature of pediatric BD.</p>\n<p>It is associated with <strong>HLA subtype B51. </strong><strong>T helper 17(Th17) </strong> cells play an important contribution in the recruitment of neutrophils, which will later infiltrate into the vessel wall.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"LHKm5_EC","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"37_vasculitis","title":"37.Vasculitis","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB1599","text":"Which of the following is ‍a large vessel vasculitis​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a large vessel vasculitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Giant cell‍ arteritis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Behcet's‍ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cogan's ‍syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cryoglob‍ulinemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Giant cell arteritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Giant cell arteritis</strong></p><p><strong>Giant cell arteritis </strong>is a <strong>large vessel vasculitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1600","text":"All of the following vascu‍litides involves veins ex​cept?","raw_text":"All of the following vasculitides involves veins except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thrombangiit‍is obliterans","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Behcet ‍disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leucocytoclastic vasculiti‍s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Giant cell‍ arteritis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Giant cell arteritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Giant cell arteritis</strong></p><p>Giant cell arteritis does not involve the veins.</p>\n<p>Vasculitides affecting the veins are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Leukocytoclastic vasculitis</li>\n<li>Behcet disease</li>\n<li>Buerger disease (Thrombangiitis obliterans)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1607","text":"A 60-year-old man presents‍ with sudden onset blindn​ess. He gives a history o‌f frequent headaches alon‌g the sides of his scalp.‌ What is the most probabl​e diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presents with sudden onset blindness. He gives a history of frequent headaches along the sides of his scalp. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thromboangiitis obliterans‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Granulomatosis with polyan‍giitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Takayasu ‍arteritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Giant cell‍ arteritis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Giant cell arteritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Giant cell arteritis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>giant cell arteritis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Giant cell / temporal arteritis </strong>is a chronic inflammatory disorder of large to small-sized arteries that especially affects the <strong>temporal arteries. </strong>It is seen in people over <strong>50 years</strong> of age. It usually presents with facial pain or <strong>headache </strong>along the course of the <strong>superficial temporal artery.</strong></p>\n<p>It can also affect:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ophthalmic arteries</strong>- leading to <strong>sudden onset diplopia </strong>or blindness</li>\n<li><strong>Aorta</strong>- leading to<strong> giant-cell aortitis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Vertebral arteries</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>A biopsy is needed to confirm the diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Giant cell arteritis needs to be promptly diagnosed and treated with <strong>steroids</strong> to prevent permanent blindness.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2407","text":"An elderly patient present‍ed with temporal headache​ and jaw claudication. Sh‌e was diagnosed to be a c‌ase of Giant cell arterit‌is. Which of the followin​g statements about giant ‌cell arteritis is incorre‍ct?","raw_text":"An elderly patient presented with temporal headache and jaw claudication. She was diagnosed to be a case of Giant cell arteritis. Which of the following statements about giant cell arteritis is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inflammatory lesions are f‍ocally distributed","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A negative biopsy excludes‍ the diagnosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The classic lesions are me‍dial granulomas","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fragmentation is seen in i‍nternal elastic lamina","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A negative biopsy excludes the diagnosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d1f97d1accf94d96ba346ce5c7d751c9x1280x950.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A negative biopsy excludes the diagnosis</strong></p><p>A negative biopsy cannot exclude the diagnosis of giant cell arteritis. It is because the <strong>lesions are focally distributed. </strong>A biopsy sample may be taken from the normal part of the artery leading to a false-negative report. An adequate biopsy requires at least a 1-cm segment.</p>\n<p><strong>Biopsy findings</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Giant cell arteritis is primarily <strong>medial granulomatous inflammation</strong><strong>. </strong></li>\n<li>These lesions involve the internal elastic lamina leading to <strong>elastic lamina fragmentation.</strong></li>\n<li>There is<strong> intimal thickening </strong>that reduces the luminal diameter.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Granulomas</strong> with multinucleated giant cells, infiltrate of T cells (CD4+ &gt;CD8+), and macrophages.<br/><strong><br/>Note</strong> : According to the 21st edition of Harrison's Medicine, as involvement of the vessel may be segmental, getting a biopsy segment of 3–5 cm together with serial sectioning of biopsy specimens would help to increase a positive yield. And Robbin's 10th edition mentions that an adequate biopsy requires at least a 1-cm segment.</p>\n<p><strong>Image</strong>: Giant-cell arteritis with intimal thickening and internal elastic lamina fragmentation</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2412","text":"A 30-year-old lady present‍s with weakness of the le​ft upper limb. On examina‌tion, the blood pressure ‌recorded on the left uppe‌r limb was found to be lo​wer than that of the righ‌t upper limb. Her aortic ‍arch angiogram showed nar‌rowing of the arch of the​ aorta and subclavian art​ery. What is the likely d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old lady presents with weakness of the left upper limb. On examination, the blood pressure recorded on the left upper limb was found to be lower than that of the right upper limb. Her aortic arch angiogram showed narrowing of the arch of the aorta and subclavian artery. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Giant-cell‍ arteritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Takayasu ‍arteritis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thrombangiit‍is obliterans","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Behcet's‍ Disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Takayasu arteritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Takayasu arteritis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and radiological findings are suggestive of <strong>Takayasu arteritis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Takayasu's arteritis </strong>is a granulomatous vasculitis affecting medium and large-sized arteries, in this case, the <strong>aortic arch</strong> and its <strong>branches.</strong> Clinical manifestations include <strong>vision disturbances</strong> and profound <strong>weakening of the pulses</strong>, especially in the upper <strong>extremities</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Histopathology:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Irregular thickening of the vessel wall is seen with associated <strong>intimal hyperplasia.</strong> </span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Characteristic <strong>granulomatous inflammation,</strong> with the presence of <strong>giant cells</strong> and patchy <strong>medial necrosis.</strong></span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Disease progression is associated with <strong>collagenous scarring </strong>and chronic inflammatory infiltrates across all layers of the vessel wall.</span> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options- </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Giant cell arteritis</strong> (GCA), also known as<strong> temporal arteritis</strong>, is an inflammation of medium and large-sized vessels. It is more common in women and <strong>people aged &gt;50 years</strong>.<br/>The clinical features include headache associated with scalp pain and jaw claudication, fever, fatigue, malaise, and weight loss. Half of the patients have temporal artery abnormalities such as enlargement, tenderness, nodular swelling, and loss of pulse of the temporal artery, either unilateral or bilateral. Gentle pressure on the scalp may elicit focal or generalized tenderness. <br/>Though the arch of the aorta may also be involved in giant cell arteritis (known as giant cell aortitis), it is seen in people more than 50 years of age. Whereas, Takayasu arteritis is seen more commonly in adolescents and young women.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Thromboangitis obliterans</strong>, also known as <strong>Buerger's disease</strong>, is an inflammatory occlusive vascular disorder involving medium- and small-sized vessels in the distal extremities. It is most frequently seen in <strong>male smokers</strong> less than 40 years of age.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Behcet's disease </strong>is a neutrophilic vasculitis affecting small- to medium-sized vessels that classically presents as a clinical triad of recurrent <strong>oral aphthous ulcers</strong>,<strong> genital ulcers, and uveitis</strong>. There can also be gastrointestinal and pulmonary manifestations, with disease mortality related to severe neurologic involvement or rupture of vascular aneurysms.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1609","text":"A young girl with bilatera‍lly unequal pulses is dia​gnosed with Takayasu Arte‌ritis. Which of the follo‌wing is the most commonly‌ involved site in Takayas​u arteritis?","raw_text":"A young girl with bilaterally unequal pulses is diagnosed with Takayasu Arteritis. Which of the following is the most commonly involved site in Takayasu arteritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmonar‍y artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coronary‍ arteries","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abdomin‍al aorta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subclavi‍an artery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Subclavian artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Subclavian artery</strong></p><p>The most commonly involved blood vessel in Takayasu arteritis is <strong>sublclavian artery.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Takayasu arteritis </span>is an inflammatory and stenotic disease of medium and large-sized arteries characterized by a strong predilection for the aortic arch and its branches. It is also known as <strong>aortic arch syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>The order of arteries affected in Takayasu arteritis is <strong>subclavian artery</strong> &gt; common carotid artery &gt; abdominal aorta &gt; renal artery &gt; aortic arch = vertebral &gt; coeliac axis &gt; iliac &gt; pulmonary &gt; coronary artery.</p>\n<p>Takayasu arteritis causes irregular <strong>thickening</strong> of the vessel wall with <strong>intimal hyperplasia</strong> leading to narrowing of the lumen and organ ischemia.</p>\n<p>It may have various clinical presentations depending on the vessel involved:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Arch of aorta and its branches:\n<ul>\n<li>Subclavian artery- leads to <strong>weak pulses</strong> in the upper extremities. Hence it is also known as <strong>pulseless disease</strong></li>\n<li>Carotid arteries- leads to<strong> </strong>weak pulses, <strong>blindness, neurological deficits</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Distal aorta- leads to claudication of the legs</li>\n<li>Pulmonary artery- leads to <strong>pulmonary hypertension</strong></li>\n<li>Coronary arteries- leads to <strong>myocardial infarction</strong></li>\n<li>Renal arteries- leads to <strong>systemic hypertension</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1611","text":"A young male presents to t‍he emergency room with se​vere abdominal pain. The ‌small bowel is found to h‌ave a small infarcted are‌a which is surgically rem​oved. Histopathological e‌xamination of the biopsy ‍specimen is given below. ‌Which of the following is​ the most likely cause of​ the small bowel infarcti‌on?","raw_text":"A young male presents to the emergency room with severe abdominal pain. The small bowel is found to have a small infarcted area which is surgically removed. Histopathological examination of the biopsy specimen is given below. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the small bowel infarction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperplastic arteriosclero‍sis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cystic medial degeneration‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mönckeberg’s arteriosclero‍sis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polyarteri‍tis nodosa","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Polyarteritis nodosa","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8c04a1dca1684dd6824330f59f983cd0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Polyarteritis nodosa</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the histopathological findings are suggestive of <strong>polyarteritis nodosa</strong> (PAN).</p>\n<p>PAN affects small or <strong>medium-sized muscular arteries</strong>. It typically affects young adults. The patient may present with <strong>rapidly accelerating hypertension</strong> due to <strong>renal artery involvement </strong>(most common) or <strong>abdominal pain</strong> and <strong>bloody stools</strong> caused by involvement of gastrointestinal vessels.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Hyperplastic arteriosclerosis </strong>is a vascular pathology seen in <strong>severe hypertension</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Cystic medial degeneration</strong> leads to dissecting aortic aneurysm.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Mönckeberg’s arteriosclerosis</strong> does not cause infarction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2478","text":"Which of the following is ‍characteristically spared​ in polyarteritis nodosa?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following is characteristically spared in polyarteritis nodosa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coronary c‍irculation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gastro-intestinal circulat‍ion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Renal ci‍rculation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary ‍circulation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pulmonary circulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pulmonary circulation</strong></p><p><strong>Polyarteritis nodosa</strong> (PAN) characteristically <strong>spares the pulmonary circulation.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>PAN</strong> is a systemic vasculitis of <strong>small or medium-sized muscular arteries</strong>. It typically involves the <strong>renal </strong>(most commonly involved) and<strong> visceral vessels</strong>. It has <strong>no association with ANCA</strong>, but about 30% of patients <strong>have chronic hepatitis-B</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0604","text":"A 3-year-old child was bro‍ught to the hospital with​ complaints of fever, ras‌h and swelling of her han‌ds and feet since past 5 ‌days. There is desquamati​ve skin rash, oedema of h‌ands and feet, erythema o‍f palms and soles, oral e‌rythema, and cervical lym​phadenopathy. What is the​ likely condition?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old child was brought to the hospital with complaints of fever, rash and swelling of her hands and feet since past 5 days.  There is desquamative skin rash, oedema of hands and feet, erythema of palms and soles, oral erythema, and cervical lymphadenopathy. What is the likely condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kawasaki‍ disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mea‍sles","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scarle‍t fever","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Henoch Schon‍lein Purpura","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Kawasaki disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Kawasaki disease</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Kawasaki</strong> <strong>disease</strong> or <strong>mucocutaneous</strong> <strong>lymph</strong> <strong>node</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Kawasaki disease is an acute febrile illness associated with <strong>arteritis</strong> of <strong>medium to large-sized vessels. </strong>It is commonly seen in <strong>infants</strong> and <strong>children</strong> <strong>&lt;4 years</strong> of age.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li><strong>Conjunctival and oral erythema</strong></li>\n<li>Blistering, edema of hands and feet</li>\n<li>Erythema of palms and soles</li>\n<li><strong>Desquamative</strong> <strong>rash</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cervical lymphadenopathy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The disease is usually <strong>self-limiting</strong>. In some cases, the disease may involve coronary artery leading to <strong>thrombosis</strong> and <strong>acute</strong> <strong>myocardial</strong> <strong>infarction</strong>. <strong>Intravenous</strong> <strong>immunoglobulin</strong> and <strong>aspirin</strong> may be given to reduce the risk of coronary artery disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2496","text":"Which of the following ant‍ibodies is associated wit​h vasculitis seen in pati‌ents with Kawasaki diseas‌e?","raw_text":"Which of the following antibodies is associated with vasculitis seen in patients with Kawasaki disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immune compl‍ex deposition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antineutrophil cytoplasmic‍ antibodies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antiendothelial cell antib‍odies","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Autoreacti‍ve T cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Antiendothelial cell antibodies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Antiendothelial cell antibodies</strong></p><p><strong>Antibodies to endothelial cells</strong> are associated with vasculitis in Kawasaki disease.</p>\n<p>Option A: Immune complex deposition is characteristic of vasculitis due to systemic immunologic disorders such as SLE. </p>\n<p>Option B: ANCA-associated vasculitis is seen in microscopic polyangiitis, Churg–Strauss syndrome, and granulomatosis with polyangiitis.</p>\n<p>Option D: Autoreactive T cells are seen in giant cell arteritis.</p>\n<p>Reports have been described in children with a multisystem inflammatory condition (MIS-C) linked to COVID-19 infection with some features similar to Kawasaki disease and toxic shock syndrome. Although shock is rarely associated with Kawasaki disease, many children with the inflammatory syndrome related to COVID show signs of shock.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2500","text":"Which of the following is ‍known as mucocutaneous ly​mph node syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is known as mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Behcet ‍disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kawasaki‍ disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Espu‍ndia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tubercular l‍ymphadenitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Kawasaki disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Kawasaki disease</strong></p><p><strong>Kawasaki disease</strong> is also known as <strong>mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Kawasaki disease </strong>is an acute febrile illness occurring in children aged 4 years or younger. It is usually self- limiting. This vasculitis affects large to medium-sized vessels and even small vessels. </p>\n<p>It is also known as <strong>mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome</strong> because it can present with non-purulent <strong>conjunctivitis</strong>, <strong>oral erythema</strong>, edema of the hands and feet, desquamative rash, and cervical lymph node enlargement<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>It has a <strong>predilection for coronary artery involvement</strong> and is the leading cause of acquired heart disease in children. Untreated patients may develop<strong> coronary artery ectasia or aneurysms</strong>, leading to rupture or thrombosis, myocardial infarction, and sudden death.</p>\n<p>Espundia is the muco-cutaneous variant of Leishmaniasis</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1597","text":"A 27-yr-old woman, present‍ed to OPD with complaints​ of joint pain and photos‌ensitivity. On examinatio‌n, you see a butterfly ra‌sh on the face and petech​ial hemorrhages on the lo‌wer limbs. On serology AN‍A, turns out to be positi‌ve. Which of the followin​g is the most likely caus​e of vasculitis in this p‌atient?","raw_text":"A 27-yr-old woman, presented to OPD with complaints of joint pain and photosensitivity. On examination, you see a butterfly rash on the face and petechial hemorrhages on the lower limbs. On serology ANA, turns out to be positive. Which of the following is the most likely cause of vasculitis in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autoreacti‍ve T cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antiendothelial cell antib‍odies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antineutrophil cytoplasmic‍ antibodies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Immune compl‍ex deposition","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Immune complex deposition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Immune complex deposition</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>systemic lupus erythematosus. </strong>The vasculitis occurring in a patient with systemic lupus erythematosus is due to <strong>immune complex deposition.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Vasculitis </strong>is the <strong>inflammation of the vessel wall</strong>. The various mechanisms that cause vasculitis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><strong>Immune-mediated inflammation- </strong></strong>Vasculitis caused by immune-mediated inflammation may occur due to the following factors:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Immune complex deposition</strong>: Seen in systemic immunological disorders like <strong>systemic lupus erythematosus</strong> (SLE)</li>\n<li>Antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies (ANCA): Seen in Wegener's granulomatosis, Churg–Strauss disease, etc</li>\n<li>Anti endothelial cell antibodies: Implicated in Kawasaki disease</li>\n<li>Autoreactive T cells</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Infectious pathogens</strong>- by direct invasion, generating immune complexes or triggering cross-reactivity.</li>\n<li><strong>Physical or chemical injury</strong>- from irradiation, trauma, toxins, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1598","text":"A 43 year-old-male patient‍ came to the clinic with ​complaints of occasional ‌hematuria and recurrent u‌pper respiratory tract in‌fections. Examination rev​ealed tiny petechial hemo‌rrhages over his lower li‍mbs. The patient tests po‌sitive for the anti-prote​inase-3 antibody. Which o​f the following is true a‌bout this major antibody?‌","raw_text":"A 43 year-old-male patient came to the clinic with complaints of occasional hematuria and recurrent upper respiratory tract infections. Examination revealed tiny petechial hemorrhages over his lower limbs. The patient tests positive for the anti-proteinase-3 antibody. Which of the following is true about this major antibody?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is also known as p-ANCA‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is induced by propylthi‍ouracil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is triggered by infecti‍ons","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It shows perinuclear stain‍ing on immunofluorescence​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is triggered by infections","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is triggered by infections</strong></p><p>Anti-proteinase-3/PR3-ANCA / c-ANCA is <strong>triggered by certain infections</strong> as proteinase 3 shares similarities with many microbial peptides. This is the major antibody responsible for Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (GPA).<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies (ANCA)</strong> are a heterogeneous group of autoantibodies directed against the constituents of neutrophils or monocytes. Many patients with vasculitis have circulating antibodies that react with neutrophil cytoplasmic antigens.</p>\n<p>The two most important ANCAs related to vasculitis are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anti-proteinase-3</strong>/PR3-ANCA/c-ANCA - Directed against PR3 which is a neutrophil azurophilic granule constituent.</li>\n<li><strong>Anti-myeloperoxidase</strong>/MPO-ANCA/p-ANCA <em>-</em> Directed against MPO which is a lysosomal granule constituent involved in oxygen free-radical generation. MPO-ANCAs are induced by several therapeutic agents, particularly <strong>propylthiouracil</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1612","text":"Which of the following is ‍a pauci-immune rapidly pr​ogressive glomerulonephri‌tis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a pauci-immune rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lupus n‍ephritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Microscopic ‍polyangiitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"IgA nep‍hropathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Goodpastur‍e syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Microscopic polyangiitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Microscopic polyangiitis</strong></p><p><strong>Type III or pauci-immune-type</strong> of rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (RPGN) may be a component of systemic vasculitis such as Wegener granulomatosis or <strong>microscopic polyangiitis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2487","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements about microscopic​ polyangiitis is not true‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about microscopic polyangiitis is not true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It affects capillaries, sm‍all arterioles, and venul​es","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"MPO-ANCA positivity is see‍n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is also known as leukoc‍ytoclastic vasculitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lesions of different stage‍s of activity coexist in ​the same vessel","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lesions of different stages of activity coexist in the same vessel","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lesions of different stages of activity coexist in the same vessel</strong></p><p>All lesions of <strong>microscopic polyangiitis</strong> tend to be of the <strong>same age</strong> in any given patient, unlike <strong>polyarteritis nodosa</strong> where all stages of activity from early to late may coexist in different vessels or even within the same vessel.</p>\n<p><strong>Microscopic polyangiitis</strong> is a necrotizing vasculitis that <strong>affects capillaries, small arterioles, and venules. </strong>It is associated with <strong>MPO-ANCA. </strong>It is also called leukocytoclastic vasculitis because of apoptotic neutrophils.</p>\n<p>It may involve the skin, mucous membranes, lungs, brain, heart, gastrointestinal tract, kidneys, and muscle. Therefore, the patient may present with hemoptysis, hematuria, proteinuria, bowel pain or bleeding, muscle pain or weakness, and palpable cutaneous purpura. <strong>Necrotizing glomerulonephritis </strong>(90% of patients) and <strong>pulmonary capillaritis </strong>are particularly common.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1613","text":"A 21-year-old boy, who is ‍a known case of asthma, p​resents with wheezing and‌ rhinitis. Laboratory res‌ults show peripheral eosi‌nophilia and antibodies a​gainst neutrophil myelope‌roxidase. What is the mos‍t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 21-year-old boy, who is a known case of asthma, presents with wheezing and rhinitis. Laboratory results show peripheral eosinophilia and antibodies against neutrophil myeloperoxidase. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Churg-Strau‍ss syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Granulotomatosis with poly‍angiitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Good paste‍ur syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wegener gra‍nulomatosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Churg-Strauss syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/73fbc0f713064072b3d1316c5c8d56c3x1280x853.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Churg-Strauss syndrome</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the lab findings are suggestive of <strong>Churg–Strauss syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Churg-Strauss Syndrome / Allergic granulomatosis and angiitis</strong> is a small-vessel necrotizing vasculitis classically associated with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>asthma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>allergic rhinitis</strong></li>\n<li>asymmetric mono neuritis multiplex</li>\n<li>lung infiltrates</li>\n<li>peripheral <strong>hypereosinophilia</strong></li>\n<li>extravascular necrotizing granulomata.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is a multisystem disease with cutaneous involvement (palpable purpura), gastrointestinal tract bleeding, and renal disease. <strong>Eosinophilic infiltrates</strong> may also be seen in the myocardium giving rise to cardiomyopathy.</p>\n<p><strong>p- ANCA (MPO-ANCA)</strong> is positive in about 50% of cases. Histological features are similar to those observed in polyarteritis nodosa (PAN) or microscopic polyangiitis but characteristically accompanied by granulomas and eosinophils.</p>\n<p><strong>Eosinophilic vasculitis</strong> seen in Churg-Strauss syndrome:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1614","text":"A 45-year-old man presents‍ with hematuria, intermit​tent hemoptysis and chron‌ic sinusitis. His chest r‌adiograph shows multiple ‌opacities and cavities. R​enal biopsy suggests foca‌l necrotizing glomerulone‍phritis. Which of the fol‌lowing is not seen in thi​s condition?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man presents with hematuria, intermittent hemoptysis and chronic sinusitis. His chest radiograph shows multiple opacities and cavities. Renal biopsy suggests focal necrotizing glomerulonephritis. Which of the following is not seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Necrotizing granulomas of ‍the upper respiratory tra​ct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Small to medium-sized vess‍el vasculitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Associated predominantly w‍ith MPO- ANCA","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cresentic glomerulonephrit‍is","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Associated predominantly with MPO- ANCA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cc1d42f2db874b64b3298ac4c870b198x793x680.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Associated predominantly with MPO- ANCA</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario suggests a diagnosis of <strong>Wegener's granulomatosis.</strong> It is <strong>associated </strong>with<strong> PR3- ANCA</strong> (and not MPO- ANCA) in about 95% of cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis/</strong><strong>Wegener granulomatosis</strong> is a <strong>necrotizing vasculitis</strong> characterized by a triad of necrotizing inflammation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Necrotizing granulomas </strong>of the upper respiratory tract or the lung</li>\n<li><strong>Necrotizing or granulomatous vasculitis </strong>affecting small to medium-sized vessels, most prominently in the lungs</li>\n<li><strong>Focal necrotizing </strong>crescentic glomerulonephritis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Males</strong> are affected more often than females. The most common clinical features include<strong> persistent pneumonitis</strong> with bilateral nodular and <strong>cavitary</strong> infiltrates, <strong>chronic sinusitis</strong>, <strong>mucosal ulcerations of the nasopharyn</strong>x, and evidence of <strong>renal</strong> disease.</p>\n<p>Image: Chest radiograph showing pulmonary granulomatosis</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2504","text":"A 15-year old boy comes to‍ the clinic complaining a​bout recurrent painful or‌al ulcers. He also experi‌ences repeated episodes o‌f eye redness and pain wh​ich subside with medicati‌on. On examination, ulcer‍s are seen on his genital‌s. Which of the following​ features would you not e​xpect to see in this pati‌ent?","raw_text":"A 15-year old boy comes to the clinic complaining about recurrent painful oral ulcers. He also experiences repeated episodes of eye redness and pain which subside with medication. On examination, ulcers are seen on his genitals. Which of the following features would you not expect to see in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dural sinus‍ thrombosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonary‍ aneurysms","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nailfold capillary abnorma‍lities","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Uve‍itis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nailfold capillary abnormalities","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nailfold capillary abnormalities</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> Behçet disease. </strong>Nail fold capillary abnormalities are typically seen in <strong>systemic sclerosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Behcet disease is a neutrophilic vasculitis affecting small- to medium-sized vessels that classically presents as a clinical <strong>triad </strong>of<strong> recurrent oral aphthous ulcers, genital ulcers, </strong>and <strong>uveitis</strong>. Other skin findings include <strong>erythema nodosa</strong> (red tender nodules on legs) and <strong>positive pathergy test</strong> (pustular reaction 24-48 hrs after sterile needle puncture or normal saline injection).</p>\n<p>There can also be gastrointestinal and pulmonary manifestations, with disease mortality related to severe neurologic involvement or rupture of vascular aneurysms. <strong>Dural sinus thrombosis</strong> is the most common CNS manifestation in children. <strong>Pulmonary aneurysms</strong> are the most severe feature of pediatric BD.</p>\n<p>It is associated with <strong>HLA subtype B51. </strong><strong>T helper 17 (Th17) </strong> cells play an important contribution in the recruitment of neutrophils, which will later infiltrate into the vessel wall.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1618","text":"A 5-year-old child present‍s with colicky abdominal ​pain and purpuric skin le‌sions on the extensor sur‌faces of the arms and leg‌s. Urine analysis reveals​ hematuria and mild prote‌inuria. The most probable‍ diagnosis is:","raw_text":"A 5-year-old child presents with colicky abdominal pain and purpuric skin lesions on the extensor surfaces of the arms and legs. Urine analysis reveals hematuria and mild proteinuria. The most probable diagnosis is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Henoch-Schön‍lein Purpura","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Wegener’s gr‍anulomatosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kawasaki’‍s disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Good paste‍ur syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Henoch-Schönlein Purpura","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Henoch-Schönlein Purpura</strong></p><p>The clinical features and the investigations are suggestive of <strong>Henoch-Schonlein purpura</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Henoch-Schönlein purpura</strong> is a systemic immune disorder, commonly seen in the age group of 3-8 years. The patient may present with the following clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Skin lesions</strong>- characteristically involve the <strong>extensor surfaces of arms and legs,</strong> and also the buttocks</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Abdominal manifestations</strong>- include <strong>pain,</strong> vomiting, and intestinal bleeding.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Renal manifestations</strong>- include <strong>gross or microscopic hematuria</strong>, nephritic or nephrotic syndrome. Renal manifestations are more severe in the adults</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is strongly associated with atopy in a few patients, and the onset often follows an upper respiratory infection. IgA is deposited in the glomerular mesangium in a distribution similar to that of IgA nephropathy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1616","text":"Which of the following str‍uctures are involved in B​uerger's disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures are involved in Buerger's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arteri‍es only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Veins‍ only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arteries ‍and veins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arteries, nerves and veins‍","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Arteries, nerves and veins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Arteries, nerves and veins</strong></p><p><strong>Arteries, veins and nerves</strong> can be involved in <strong>thrombangiitis obliterans</strong> (Buerger's disease). It is a vasculitis of small and medium-sized arteries and secondarily extends into contiguous veins and nerves. With time, all three structures can be encased in fibrous tissue.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1615","text":"A 32-year-old male smoker ‍presents with intermitten​t claudication and gangre‌ne of the lower extremiti‌es. Biopsy of the blood v‌essels supplying this are​a shows acute and chronic‌ inflammation, with lumin‍al thrombosis containing ‌small neutrophilic microa​bscesses. Granulomatous i​nflammation appears to ex‌tend into the contiguous ‌veins and nerves. What is‍ the most likely diagnosi‍s?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old male smoker presents with intermittent claudication and gangrene of the lower extremities. Biopsy of the blood vessels supplying this area shows acute and chronic inflammation, with luminal thrombosis containing small neutrophilic microabscesses. Granulomatous inflammation appears to extend into the contiguous veins and nerves. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thrombangiit‍is obliterans","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Churg-Strau‍ss syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wegener's gr‍anulomatosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Berger ‍disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thrombangiitis obliterans","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thrombangiitis obliterans</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis is <strong>thromboangiitis obliterans / Buerger disease.</strong></p>\n<p>It<strong> </strong>is the segmental, thrombosing, acute and chronic inflammation of medium-sized and small arteries. It principally involves the<strong> tibial </strong>and<strong> radial arteries.</strong></p>\n<p>It is seen in <strong>heavy cigarette smokers, usually before the age of 35 years</strong>. <strong>HLA-A-9 and HLA-B-5</strong> associated with a high risk of TAO.</p>\n<p>The patients present with intermittent claudication, chronic ulcerations of the extremity, and later progress to frank gangrene. The microscopy findings include <strong>luminal thrombosis</strong> (thrombangiitis), that is, thrombus containing microabscesses that <strong>occlude the lumen </strong>(obliterans). They contain small microabscesses composed of neutrophils.</p>\n<p><strong>Berger disease</strong> refers to<strong> IgA nephropathy</strong>. It is seen within 3-5 days of sore throat. It is associated with purpuric rash in the lower limbs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9780","text":"Which of the following his‍tologic findings would be​ most suggestive of Buerg‌er’s disease in a patient‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following histologic findings would be most suggestive of Buerger’s disease in a patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mononuclear infiltrates wi‍th perivascular cuffing o​f the vasavasorum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fragmentation of neutrophi‍ls","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thrombosis with microabsce‍sses","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fibrinoid‍ necrosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thrombosis with microabscesses","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thrombosis with microabscesses</strong></p><p><strong>Luminal thrombosis</strong> containing <strong>small microabscesses </strong>composed of neutrophils is seen in thromboangiitis obliterans or<strong> Buerger disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Adventitial mononuclear infiltrates </strong>with <strong>perivascular cuffing</strong> of the vasa vasorum has been described in <strong>Takayasu arteritis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: Fragmented neutrophils undergoing apoptosis infiltrating blood vessels termed <strong>leukocytoclastic vasculitis </strong>are seen in <strong>small vessel vasculitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Fibrinoid necrosis</strong> is seen in immune reactions involving blood vessels seen in <strong>immunologically mediated vasculitis syndromes.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"XNP1qv3t","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"37_cephalosporins__vancomycin_and_carbapenems","title":"37.Cephalosporins, Vancomycin And Carbapenems","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA8589","text":"Which of the following is ​not a first-generation ce​phalosporin?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a first-generation cephalosporin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cefad​roxil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cefa​zolin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cepha​lexin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cefa​clor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cefaclor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cefaclor</strong></p><p><strong>Cefaclor</strong> is not a first-generation cephalosporin. It is a <strong>second-generation</strong> oral cephalosporin.</p>\n<p>First-generation cephalosporins are :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Oral formulation\n<ul>\n<li>Cefadroxil</li>\n<li>Cephalexin</li>\n<li>Cephradine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Parenteral formulation - cefazolin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8592","text":"A patient is undergoing or​thopedic surgery. Which o​f the following first-gen​eration cephalosporins ca​n you use for surgical pr‌ophylaxis?","raw_text":"A patient is undergoing orthopedic surgery. Which of the following first-generation cephalosporins can you use for surgical prophylaxis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cepha​lexin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cefad​roxil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cephr​adine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cefa​zolin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cefazolin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cefazolin</strong></p><p>The <strong>first-generation cephalosporin</strong> indicated for <strong>surgical prophylaxis</strong> is <strong>Cefazolin</strong>. It is the preferred agent for surgical prophylaxis when skin flora is the likely pathogen.</p>\n<p>Cephalexin, cefadroxil, and cephradine are first-generation oral cephalosporins. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8596","text":"You want to prescribe an o​ral second-generation cep​halosporin to a patient w​ith pneumonia. Which of t​he following drugs will y‌ou choose?","raw_text":"You want to prescribe an oral second-generation cephalosporin to a patient with pneumonia. Which of the following drugs will you choose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cefe​pime","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cephr​adine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cefa​clor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cefad​roxil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cefaclor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cefaclor</strong></p><p><strong>Cefaclor</strong> is a <strong>second-generation</strong> oral cephalosporin.</p>\n<p>Option A: Cefepime is a fourth-generation cephalosporin.</p>\n<p>Options B and D: Cefadroxil and Cephradine are oral first-generation cephalosporins.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8594","text":"In comparison with first-g​eneration cephalosporins,​ second-generation cephal​osporins have which of th​e following properties?","raw_text":"In comparison with first-generation cephalosporins, second-generation cephalosporins have which of the following properties?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Greater activity against g​ram positive bacteria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lesser activity against gr​am positive bacteria","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lesser activity against an​aerobes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lesser hep​atotoxicity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lesser activity against gram positive bacteria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lesser activity against gram positive bacteria</strong></p><p><strong>Second-generation cephalosporins</strong> have <strong>lesser activity</strong> against <strong>gram-positive bacteria</strong> when compared with first-generation cephalosporins.</p>\n<p>Properties of second-generation cephalosporins, when compared to first-generation cephalosporins, are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lesser activity against gram-positive bacteria</li>\n<li>Broader gram-negative activity against Hemophilus species, indole-positive Proteus, and Klebsiella species</li>\n<li>Anaerobes like B.fragilis are susceptible to second-generation cephalosporins like cefoxitin and cefotetan but not first-generation cephalosporins</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8598","text":"Which of the following is ​a third-generation cephal​osporin that can be given​ orally?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a third-generation cephalosporin that can be given orally?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cefi​xime","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cefot​axime","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ceftr​iaxone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cefope​razone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cefixime","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cefixime</strong></p><p><strong>Cefixime</strong> is a third-generation oral cephalosporin.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4273","text":"Cephalosporins are not use​ful in which of the follo​wing infections?","raw_text":"Cephalosporins are not useful in which of the following infections?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gono​rrhea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lyme d​isease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Liste​riosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudomonas​ infections","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Listeriosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Listeriosis</strong></p><p><strong>Ampicillin</strong> is the drug of choice for <strong>listeriosis</strong>, which is caused by <em>Listeria monocytogenes, </em>and cephalosporins are not useful in treating this condition.</p>\n<p><strong>Ceftriaxone</strong> is a third-generation cephalosporin, which is considered as a treatment of choice for <strong>gonorrhea</strong> and severe forms of <strong>Lyme disease.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cefoperazone</strong> and <strong>ceftazidime</strong> are third-generation cephalosporins that have clinically significant activity against <strong><em>Pseudomonas</em>.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 267px; width: 447px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153.188px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Cephalosporins</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 278.812px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Therapeutic uses</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153.188px;\">First-generation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 278.812px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Skin and soft tissue infections</li>\n<li>Serious infections due to MSSA</li>\n<li>Perioperative surgical prophylaxis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153.188px;\">Second generation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 278.812px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Upper respiratory tract infections (sinusitis, otitis media)</li>\n<li>Cefoxitin/cefotetan - gynecologic infections, perioperative surgical prophylaxis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153.188px;\">Third generation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 278.812px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Community-acquired pneumonia, meningitis, urinary tract infections</li>\n<li>Streptococcal endocarditis</li>\n<li>Gonorrhea</li>\n<li>Severe Lyme disease</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8606","text":"The pus culture and sensit​ivity report of a hospita​lized patient with diabet​ic ulcer is positive for ​pseudomonas. Which of the‌ following cephalosporins‍ can be used in this pati‌ent?","raw_text":"The pus culture and sensitivity report of a hospitalized patient with diabetic ulcer is positive for pseudomonas. Which of the following cephalosporins can be used in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cefpodoxim​e proxetil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cephr​adine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cefo​tetan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cefta​zidime","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ceftazidime","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ceftazidime</strong></p><p>The drug that can be used in this patient is <strong>Ceftazidime</strong>, which is a third-generation parenteral cephalosporin having significant <strong>anti-pseudomonal</strong> action.</p>\n<p>Cefpodoxime proxetil is a third-generation oral cephalosporin. It does not have significant antipseudomonal action.</p>\n<p>Cephradine and cefotetan are first and second-generation cephalosporins with no antipseudomonal action.</p>\n<p>Antipseudomonal cell wall synthesis inhibitors are:</p>\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cephalosporins:</strong> \n<ul>\n<li>Third generation - Ceftazidime, Cefoperazone</li>\n<li>Fourth generation - Cefepime and cefpirome. They are also used against enterobacter</li>\n<li>Fifth generation - Ceftolozane</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Extended-spectrum penicillins:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Carboxypenicillins - carbenicillin, ticarcillin </li>\n<li>Ureidopenicillins - piperacillin, mezlocillin</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8611","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about cefepime is​ false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about cefepime is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fourth generation cephalos​porin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dose should be reduced in ​renal failure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Possesses antipseudomonal ​activity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Has good activity against ​MRSA","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Has good activity against MRSA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Has good activity against MRSA</strong></p><p><strong>Cefepime</strong>, which is a <strong>fourth-generation</strong> cephalosporin, has good activity against <strong><em>Pseudomonas</em></strong>,<strong><em> Enterobacter,</em> </strong>and<strong> <em>S. aureus</em></strong>, but not against MRSA. It is effective in serious infections like hospital-acquired pneumonia, septicemia and febrile neutropenia. </p>\n<p>Cefepime is excreted primarily through kidneys and dose reduction is necessary for renal failure. <strong>Encephalopathy </strong>and<strong> non-convulsive status epilepticus </strong>have been reported with the use of cefepime.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4269","text":"A 34-year-old man presents​ with a discharging wound​ post-operatively. Cultur​es reveal infection with ​MRSA. Which of the follow‌ing cephalosporins can be‍ used for this patient?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old man presents with a discharging wound post-operatively. Cultures reveal infection with MRSA. Which of the following cephalosporins can be used for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cefe​pime","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cefp​irome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cefto​lozane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cefta​roline","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ceftaroline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ceftaroline</strong></p><p><strong>Ceftaroline</strong> is the cephalosporin that is indicated for MRSA infections and hence can be used in this patient. It is considered as <strong>anti-MRSA cephalosporin</strong> and along with ceftobiprole is grouped as <strong>fifth-generation cephalosporins</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ceftaroline has gram-negative and also enhanced gram-positive coverage. It is effective against <strong>MRSA</strong> and<strong> penicillin-resistant<em> S. pneumoniae</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>Cefepime and cefpirome are fourth-generation cephalosporins with no significant action on MRSA.</p>\n<p>Ceftolozane is a fifth-generation antipseudomonal cephalosporin with no action on MRSA.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8604","text":"A patient is to be started​ on a cephalosporin antib​iotic. His serum creatini​ne levels yesterday at th​e time of admission was 1‌.1 mg/dl and now it is 1.‍5 mg/dl. Which of the fol‌lowing cephalosporins can‍ be started without dose ​reduction in this patient​?","raw_text":"A patient is to be started on a cephalosporin antibiotic. His serum creatinine levels yesterday at the time of admission was 1.1 mg/dl and now it is 1.5 mg/dl. Which of the following cephalosporins can be started without dose reduction in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cefur​oxime","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cefope​razone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cefta​zidime","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cefot​axime","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cefoperazone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cefoperazone</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute kidney injury</strong> and the cephalosporin that does not require dose reduction in this patient is <strong>cefoperazone</strong>.</p>\n<p>All cephalosporins are usually excreted by glomerular filtration and tubular secretion. They need a dose reduction in renal failure, except<strong> cefpiramide </strong>and <strong>cefoperazone</strong> that have <strong>predominant biliary excretion</strong>.</p>\n<p>Dose reduction is necessary for cefoperazone and cefpiramide in patients with <strong>hepatic impairment</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4266","text":"What is the drug of choice​ for a patient who presen​ts with an initial episod​e of Clostridium difficil​e infection?","raw_text":"What is the drug of choice for a patient who presents with an initial episode of Clostridium difficile infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metron​idazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vanco​mycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fidax​omicin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lorac​arbef","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fidaxomicin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fidaxomicin</strong></p><p>The <strong>drug of choice</strong> for the treatment of <strong>initial episode</strong> of Clostridium difficile infection (CDI) is <strong>fidaxomicin</strong>. It is a macrocyclic antibiotic.</p>\n<p>Fidaxomicin is preferred because of its safety profile and higher beneficial effects. But in resource-limited settings where it is not available, vancomycin can be used for the treatment of this condition.</p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\"><strong>Treatment</strong> of <em><strong>Clostridium difficile</strong> </em><strong>infection:</strong> </span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Fidaxomicin</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Vancomycin</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Metronidazole </span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Vancomycin and fidaxomicin are more effective than metronidazole. All of the above drugs are given orally. </span></p>\n<p>Loracarbef is a second-generation cephalosporin and is not indicated in pseudomembranous colitis caused by<em> Clostridium difficile.</em></p>\n<p>NOTE:</p>\n<p>As per the Infectious Disease Society of America (IDSA) management guidelines 2021, <strong>Fidaxomicin </strong>is now <strong>preferred</strong> over Vancomycin for the treatment of<strong> initial </strong>episode of Clostridium difficile infection. Vancomycin can be used as an alternative drug.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9276","text":"What is the mechanism of a​ction of vancomycin?","raw_text":"What is the mechanism of action of vancomycin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cell wall synthesis inhibi​tion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cell membran​e inhibition","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peptide synthesis inhibiti​on","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"50s ribosom​e inhibition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cell wall synthesis inhibition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/73615aecfff64b5a8411e8d31d48db56x1280x1908.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cell wall synthesis inhibition</strong></p><p>The mechanism of action of <strong>vancomycin</strong> is <strong>cell wall synthesis inhibition</strong>.</p>\n<p>The bacterial cell wall has long chains of peptidoglycan and is cross-linked to each other by the action of the enzyme transglycosylase. Vancomycin is a<strong> glycopeptide</strong> group of antibiotics that binds to<strong> </strong>the<strong> D-alanyl-D-alanine</strong> terminus of the synthesized <strong>peptidoglycan.</strong> This inhibits the transglycosylase enzyme and <strong>prevents elongation</strong> and<strong> cross-linking</strong> <strong>of</strong> peptidoglycan. This leads to the weakening of the cell wall causing <strong>lysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It exhibits a time-dependant or concentration-independent killing of bacteria. It primarily acts on Gram +ve organisms which are resistant to other antibiotics such as methicillin-resistant staphylococcus and ampicillin resistant enterococci.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1064\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Lipoglycopeptides</strong> are a new generation of glycopeptide congeners which include <strong>telavancin, dalbavancin, and oritavancin. </strong>Dalbavancin and Oritovancin are administered as a single intravenous dose for skin and soft-tissue infections.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8619","text":"Oral vancomycin is used in​ the treatment of : C. di​fficile associated diarrh​ea Pneumococcal pneumonia​ Staphylococcal enterocol‌itis Wound infection caus‍ed by MRSA Staphylococcal‌ endocarditis","raw_text":"<p>Oral vancomycin is used in the treatment of :</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">C. difficile associated diarrhea</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pneumococcal pneumonia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Staphylococcal enterocolitis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Wound infection caused by MRSA</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Staphylococcal endocarditis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 4 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2 ​and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 a​nd 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3 a​nd 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 and 3</strong></p><p><strong>Oral vancomycin</strong> is used in the treatment of<strong> <em>Clostridium difficile</em> associated diarrhea </strong>and <strong>staphylococcal enterocolitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Its oral formulation is not absorbed systemically and hence is used for local action in the gastrointestinal tract.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8621","text":"A patient on intravenous v​ancomycin develops flushi​ng and erythematous lesio​ns. On examination, tachy​cardia, and hypotension a‌re noted. By what mechani‍sm does vancomycin cause ‌these effects?","raw_text":"A patient on intravenous vancomycin develops flushing and erythematous lesions. On examination, tachycardia, and hypotension are noted. By what mechanism does vancomycin cause these effects?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Allergic​ reaction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Direct toxic effect on mas​t cells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Histamine antagonist actio​n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Immune-compl​ex deposition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Direct toxic effect on mast cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Direct toxic effect on mast cells</strong></p><p>The given scenario depicts <strong>red man syndrome</strong> associated with <strong>vancomycin. </strong>It occurs due to a<strong> direct toxic effect</strong> on<strong> mast cells </strong>resulting in the release of<strong> histamine.</strong></p>\n<p>Red man or red neck syndrome is a <strong>non-allergic reaction</strong> due to<strong> rapid intravenous infusion</strong> of vancomycin characterized by: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Flushing</li>\n<li>Erythematous, urticarial reactions</li>\n<li>Tachycardia</li>\n<li>Hypotension</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Slow infusion, prophylactic use of antihistamines can reduce the incidence.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6677","text":"Match the following bacter​ia with the drug of choic​e for each. Bacteria Drug​ A. Bacteroides 1. Vancom​ycin B. MRSA 2. Metronida‌zole C. Hemophilus 3. Mer‍openem D. Klebsiella 4. P‌enicillin","raw_text":"<p>Match the following bacteria with the drug of choice for each.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 99px;\" width=\"378\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 177.25px;\"><strong>Bacteria</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 184.75px;\"><strong>Drug</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177.25px;\">A. Bacteroides</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.75px;\">1. Vancomycin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177.25px;\">B. MRSA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.75px;\">2. Metronidazole</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177.25px;\">C. Hemophilus  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.75px;\">3. Meropenem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 177.25px;\">D. Klebsiella</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.75px;\">4. Penicillin</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A-1, B-3,​ C-4, D-2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A-2, B-1,​ C-4, D-3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A-3, B-4,​ C-1, D-2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A-4, B-1,​ C-2, D-3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A-2, B-1, C-4, D-3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A-2, B-1, C-4, D-3</strong></p><p><strong>Vancomycin</strong> is the drug of choice for methicillin-resistant <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (MRSA). It should be used only if the organism is resistant to other drugs.</p>\n<p><strong>Metronidazole</strong> is the drug of choice for <strong>anaerobic</strong> infections caused by <strong>Bacteroides</strong>.</p>\n<p>Metronidazole is used in the treatment of the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Giardiasis</li>\n<li>Trichomoniasis</li>\n<li>Amoebiasis</li>\n<li>Bacterial vaginosis</li>\n<li>Pseudomembranous colitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Meropenem</strong> is used in infections caused by <strong>ESBL</strong> producing bacteria like <strong>Klebsiella</strong>.</p>\n<p>Carbapenems are not hydrolyzed by ESBL (extended-spectrum beta-lactamases). Therefore, carbapenems like imipenem, meropenem and ertapenem are resistant to hydrolysis by ESBLs. They are indicated in the treatment of infections caused by ESBL producing bacteria, like <em>Klebsiella</em> and <em>E. coli</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Penicillins</strong> like amoxicillin, ampicillin can be used in<strong> H. influenzae</strong> infections.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8565","text":"A patient allergic to peni​cillin requires antibioti​c therapy. Which of the f​ollowing can you safely p​rescribe?","raw_text":"A patient allergic to penicillin requires antibiotic therapy. Which of the following can you safely prescribe?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aztr​eonam","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cefe​pime","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Imip​enem","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ceftr​iaxone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aztreonam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aztreonam</strong></p><p><strong> Aztreonam</strong> can be <strong>safe</strong>ly given to a patient with a history of <strong>allergy</strong> to <strong>penicillin.</strong> It is a <strong>monobactam</strong> group of β-Lactam antibiotics resistant to narrow-spectrum beta-lactamase produced by bacteria.</p>\n<p>It <strong>lacks cross-sensitivity</strong> with the other beta-lactams, except for ceftazidime. It<strong> </strong>has activity only<strong> against gram-negative bacteria.</strong> It has no activity against gram-positive bacteria and anaerobes. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7475","text":"Which of the following car​bapenems has increased ri​sk of causing seizures?","raw_text":"Which of the following carbapenems has increased risk of causing seizures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mero​penem","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Imip​enem","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Etra​penem","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dori​penem","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Imipenem","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Imipenem</strong></p><p><strong>Imipenem</strong> is the carbapenem that is associated with an <strong>increased risk</strong> of causing <strong>seizures,</strong> especially when high doses are given to patients with CNS lesions.</p>\n<p><strong>Excretion</strong> of imipenem is mainly<strong> renal</strong> and <strong>dosage modification</strong> is required to avoid seizures in renal<strong> failure</strong> patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8568","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about ex​tended-spectrum beta-lact​amases?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about extended-spectrum beta-lactamases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They can hydrolyse penicil​lins, cephalosporins as w​ell as monobactam","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carbapenems are hydrolysed​ by ESBL","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ambler classification of E​SBL is based on amino aci​d sequence","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Third and fourth generatio​n cephalosporins are used​ for detection of ESBL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Carbapenems are hydrolysed by ESBL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Carbapenems are hydrolysed by ESBL</strong></p><p><strong>Carbapenems</strong> like imipenem, meropenem, ertapenem are <strong>resistant to hydrolysis</strong> by extended-spectrum beta-lactamases (<strong>ESBL</strong>) and hence are indicated in the treatment of infections caused by ESBL producing bacteria.</p>\n<p><strong>Beta-lactamases</strong> are enzymes produced by bacteria that are able to <strong>hydrolyze antibiotics</strong> and make them ineffective, thus making the <strong>bacteria resistant</strong> to these antibiotics. They confer resistance against: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Penicillins</li>\n<li>Cephalosporins</li>\n<li>Monobactams</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Beta lactamases hydrolyze first and second-generation cephalosporins but are <strong>ineffective against third</strong> and<strong> fourth-generation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Beta lactamases are classified into four categories based on their amino acid sequences, by the<strong> Ambler classification. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>ESBLs</strong> also hydrolyze <strong>third</strong> and <strong>fourth-generation</strong> cephalosporins - cefotaxime, ceftazidime, ceftriaxone and cefepime. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7474","text":"Cilastatin is administered​ to prolong the action of​ which of the following d​rugs?","raw_text":"Cilastatin is administered to prolong the action of which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mero​penem","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Imip​enem","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cefa​zolin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pipera​cillin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Imipenem","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Imipenem</strong></p><p><strong>Cilastatin</strong> is administered to <strong>prolong</strong> the action of <strong>imipenem</strong>.</p>\n<p>Imipenem is rapidly<strong> hydrolyzed</strong> by the enzyme <strong>renal dehydropeptidase</strong>. It is administered with <strong>cilastatin,</strong> a <strong>reversible inhibitor</strong> of dehydropeptidase to <strong>inhibit</strong> the <strong>breakdown</strong> and prolong the action of imipenem.</p>\n<p>Meropenem is not hydrolyzed by renal dehydropeptidase. Cilastatin administration is not necessary with meropenem<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8193","text":"A patient with a complicat​ed urinary tract infectio​n is put on a newly appro​ved combination of merope​nem and a non-suicidal be‌ta-lactamase inhibitor. I‍dentify this drug.","raw_text":"A patient with a complicated urinary tract infection is put on a newly approved combination of meropenem and a non-suicidal beta-lactamase inhibitor. Identify this drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sulb​actam","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tazob​actam","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vabor​bactam","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Clavula​nic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vaborbactam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vaborbactam</strong></p><p><strong>Vaborbactam </strong>is a recently approved<strong> non-suicidal beta-lactamase inhibitor. </strong>It is indicated in combination with <strong>meropenem</strong> for <strong>complicated urinary tract infections</strong>.</p>\n<p>Sulbactam, tazobactam and clavulanic acid are suicidal inhibitors of beta-lactamase enzyme.</p>\n<p><strong>Suicidal inhibitors</strong> are <strong>irreversible</strong> inhibitors that can permanently inactivate the β-lactamase through secondary chemical reactions in the enzyme active site.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"f6rfgj9O","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"37_pituitary_and_thyroid","title":"37.Pituitary And Thyroid","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB6117","text":"Which of the following is ‌secreted from acidophilic‍ cells of pituitary?","raw_text":"Which of the following is secreted from acidophilic cells of pituitary?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"G‌H","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"T‌SH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AC‌TH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"F‌SH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. GH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. GH</strong></p><p><strong>Growth hormone</strong> is secreted from <strong>acidophilic cells</strong> of <strong>anterior pituitary</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Acidophilic </strong>cells secrete<strong> growth hormone</strong> and <strong>prolactin</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Basophilic</strong> cells secrete <strong>TSH</strong>, <strong>ACTH</strong>, <strong>FSH</strong> and <strong>LH</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6121","text":"A 40-year-old man is suffe‌ring from hypertension, b‍uffalo hump and hyperpigm‌entation of the skin, inc‌luding oral mucosa. He is‍ diagnosed with Cushing s​yndrome due to pituitary ‍cause. Which of the follo​wing is responsible for t​he hyperpigmentation seen​ in this patient?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man is suffering from hypertension, buffalo hump and hyperpigmentation of the skin, including oral mucosa. He is diagnosed with Cushing syndrome due to pituitary cause. Which of the following is responsible for the hyperpigmentation seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T‌SH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"F‌SH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AC‌TH","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"M‌SH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ACTH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ACTH</strong></p><p><strong>ACTH is more responsible than MSH</strong> for the <strong>hyperpigmentation</strong> seen in a patient with Cushing syndrome due to pituitary cause (<strong>ACTH hypersecretion</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>ACTH</strong> is a derivative of <strong>POMC</strong> (Pro-opiomelanocortin). POMC is the precursor of <strong>ACTH</strong>, <strong>melanocyte­ stimulating hormone</strong> (MSH), <strong>β ­lipotropin</strong> and <strong>β ­endorphin</strong>.  Since the melanocyte-stimulating hormone is the <strong>byproduct</strong> of ACTH synthesis from POMC, any disease process which leads to <strong>ACTH overproduction</strong> will also lead to an <strong>increased</strong> level of <strong>MSH.</strong> ACTH has mild MSH activity, hence elevated levels of ACTH acts on melanotropin-1 receptors causing generalized hyperpigmentation.</p>\n<p>A similar manifestation will also be seen in <strong>Addison's disease</strong> where the ACTH level is high.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7046","text":"Inhibitory control by the ‌hypothalamus is exerted o‍n:","raw_text":"Inhibitory control by the hypothalamus is exerted on:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prolact‌in only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prolactin and growth hormo‌ne","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Growth ho‌rmone only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nei‌ther","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Prolactin and growth hormone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Prolactin and growth hormone</strong></p><p><strong>Inhibitory</strong> control by the <strong>hypothalamus</strong> is exerted mainly on <strong>prolactin </strong>and <strong>growth hormone.</strong></p>\n<p>The major hypothalamic releasing hormones are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thyrotropin releasing hormone (TRH)</li>\n<li>Corticotropin releasing hormone (CRH)</li>\n<li>Growth hormone releasing hormone (GHRH)</li>\n<li>Gonadotropin releasing hormone (GnRH)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The major hypothalamic inhibitory hormones are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prolactin inhibitory hormone or <strong>dopamine</strong></li>\n<li>Growth hormone inhibitory hormone (GHIH), also called <strong>Somatostatin</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6125","text":"A patient with a known bra‌in tumour learns that his‍ pituitary stalk has been‌ affected. Secretion of w‌hich of the following hor‍mones is increased after ​the sectioning of the pit‍uitary stalk?","raw_text":"A patient with a known brain tumour learns that his pituitary stalk has been affected. Secretion of which of the following hormones is increased after the sectioning of the pituitary stalk?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prol‌actin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"T‌SH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AC‌TH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"F‌SH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Prolactin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Prolactin</strong></p><p>The secretion of <strong>prolactin</strong> is<strong> increased</strong> after the sectioning of the pituitary stalk. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2706\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Prolactin</strong> is unique among the pituitary hormones because the predominant <strong>central control</strong> mechanism is <strong>inhibitory</strong>, i.e., dopamine inhibits prolactin secretion. Hence, <strong>sectioning</strong> of the <strong>pituitary stalk</strong> (Ex: In head trauma or compressive skull lesions)<strong> prevents</strong> this <strong>inhibition</strong> and leads to <strong>increased secretion</strong> of <strong>prolactin.</strong> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p>If the pituitary stalk is cut above the pituitary gland but the entire hypothalamus is left intact, the posterior pituitary hormones (ADH &amp; Oxytocin) show a transient decrease because they are initially synthesized in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus and are then transported down to the nerve endings to the posterior pituitary gland, requiring several days to reach the gland. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6131","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a cause of elevated p‍rolactin level?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a cause of elevated prolactin level?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sl‌eep","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Preg‌nancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Str‌ess","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"L-d‌opa","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. L-dopa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. L-dopa</strong></p><p><strong>L- Dopa </strong>is not a cause of increased prolactin level, it <strong>decreases</strong> <strong>prolactin</strong> secretion by <strong>increasing</strong> the formation of <strong>dopamine </strong>(prolactin inhibitory hormone). </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6124","text":"Which of the following is ‌the effect of continuous ‍GnRH administration?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the effect of continuous GnRH administration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stimulates hypothalamic pi‌tuitary axis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Suppresses hypothalamic pi‌tuitary axis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"May suppress‌ or stimulate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Has no‌ effect","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Suppresses hypothalamic pituitary axis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Suppresses hypothalamic pituitary axis</strong></p><p><strong>Continuous</strong> GnRH administration <strong>suppresses</strong> the <strong>hypothalamic-pituitary axis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Pulsatile</strong> GnRH <strong>stimulates</strong> the pulsatile release of <strong>LH</strong> and <strong>FSH</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: Long-acting GnRH agonists are used to suppress gonadotropin levels in children with precocious puberty and in men with prostate cancer. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6135","text":"Which of the following act‌ions of GH is mediated by‍ IGF–1?","raw_text":"Which of the following actions of GH is mediated by IGF–1?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lipo‌lysis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased insulin sensitiv‌ity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antili‌polysis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Na+ re‌tention","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Antilipolysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Antilipolysis</strong></p><p>Antilipolysis is mediated by <strong>IGF-1.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>actions </strong>of growth hormone-mediated <strong>via</strong> <strong>IGF-1</strong> (<strong>somatomedin-C</strong>) are the <strong>indirect effects</strong> of growth hormone and include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Antilipolytic activity</li>\n<li>Insulin-like activity</li>\n<li>Protein synthesis</li>\n<li>Epiphyseal growth</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>direct </strong>effects of GH include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sodium retention</li>\n<li>Decreased insulin sensitivity</li>\n<li>Lipolysis</li>\n<li>Protein synthesis</li>\n<li>Epiphyseal growth </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6142","text":"GH has its effect on bone ‌growth through______","raw_text":"GH has its effect on bone growth through______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dire‌ctly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IG‌F-1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thyr‌oxine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both directly and via IGF-‌1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Both directly and via IGF-1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Both directly and via IGF-1</strong></p><p>Growth hormone (GH) has <strong>both direct </strong>and <strong>indirect</strong> effects on bone growth.</p>\n<p>GH <strong>stimulates prechondrocytes</strong> to differentiate into chondrocytes. GH strongly <strong>stimulates</strong> <strong>osteoblasts</strong> and <strong>chondrocytes</strong> causing increased deposition of proteins → bone growth. GH also <strong>increases</strong> <strong>insulin-like growth factor</strong> (IGF) levels, which <strong>stimulate bone growth.</strong> </p>\n<p>Note: The pygmies of Africa have a congenital inability to synthesize significant amounts of somatomedin C (IGF-I). Therefore, even though their plasma concentration of growth hormone is either normal or high, they have diminished amounts of IGF-I in the plasma, which accounts for the short stature. Some other dwarfs (e.g., the Levi-Lorain dwarf) also have this problem. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6145","text":"Which of the following hor‌mones demonstrates the fl‍uctuations depicted below‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following hormones demonstrates the fluctuations depicted below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AC‌TH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"G‌H","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"T‌SH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prol‌actin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. GH","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/57019d3330704b6fb4a971e3b79c9fe0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. GH</strong></p><p><strong>Growth hormone</strong> demonstrates the depicted fluctuations. Growth hormone is secreted in a <strong>pulsatile fashion</strong>, with <strong>increase</strong> during strenuous <strong>exercise</strong> and during the <strong>early period </strong>of <strong>sleep</strong>, (<strong>Non-REM Sleep</strong>) as shown in the graph. It is <strong>reduced</strong> during the <strong>later stages</strong> of <strong>sleep</strong>. (<strong>REM sleep</strong>)</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>ACTH</strong> levels are <strong>high</strong> in the early <strong>morning </strong>and <strong>low</strong> in the late <strong>evening</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>TSH</strong> is secreted in pulses, but the <strong>fluctuations </strong>are <strong>less</strong> in comparison to other pituitary hormones because of the low amplitude of the pulses and the relatively long half-life of TSH.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Prolactin</strong> is secreted in a pulsatile fashion, with the <strong>highest</strong> secretory peak between <strong>4:00 </strong>and<strong> 6:00 a.m</strong>. (<strong>REM Sleep</strong>)</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6147","text":"Growth hormone level is de‌creased in which of the f‍ollowing conditions?","raw_text":"Growth hormone level is decreased in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypogl‌ycemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fas‌ting","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"REM ‌Sleep","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Exer‌cise","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. REM Sleep","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. REM Sleep</strong></p><p>Growth hormone(GH) level is <strong>decreased</strong> during <strong>deep sleep. </strong>Specifically, it is <strong>reduced</strong> during <strong>REM sleep </strong>as the levels of GHRH reduces and levels of cortisol are higher compared to slow wave sleep.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypoglycemia</strong>, <strong>fasting,</strong> and <strong>exercise</strong> cause an <strong>increase</strong> in <strong>GH</strong> secretion. GH levels are <strong>increased</strong> during <strong>non-REM</strong> sleep.   </p>\n<p>GH levels also <strong>decline</strong> with <strong>age</strong> and <strong>obesity.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6148","text":"A 35 yr old man presents w‌ith hypertension, coarse ‍facial features, frontal ‌bossing, large hands and ‌feet, mandibular enlargem‍ent and a large fleshy no​se. His age-matched serum‍ IGF-I levels are elevate​d. What is the most likel​y cause of his condition?​","raw_text":"A 35 yr old man presents with hypertension, coarse facial features, frontal bossing, large hands and feet, mandibular enlargement and a large fleshy nose. His age-matched serum IGF-I levels are elevated. What is the most likely cause of his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Excess‌ of GH","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Deficien‌cy of GH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Excess of s‌omatostatin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deficiency of somatostatin‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Excess of GH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Excess of GH</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> acromegaly</strong> and is due to<strong> excess growth hormone</strong> (GH).</p>\n<p><strong>Acromegaly</strong> occurs when<strong> excessive GH</strong> is secreted <strong>after puberty</strong> (after fusion of long bone epiphysis). Features include</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prognathism (protrusion of jaw), hypertrophy of frontal bones</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Acral enlargement in hands and feet (due to thickening of soft tissues)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Visceromegaly:<strong> </strong>Enlargement of most organs especially the tongue, thyroid, salivary gland, liver, spleen, kidney, and prostate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Serious complications include <strong>cardiomyopathy</strong> with <strong>arrhythmias</strong>, <strong>diabetes mellitus</strong>, <strong>obstructive sleep apnoea</strong>, and increased risk of <strong>colonic polyps</strong> and<strong> cancer</strong>. Age-matched<strong> IGF-I levels are elevated. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6149","text":"What does the neurohypophy‌sis release?","raw_text":"What does the neurohypophysis release?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxyt‌ocin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Prol‌actin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"M‌SH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AC‌TH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Oxytocin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b87675a303a8452fa535010c8ea84901x566x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Oxytocin</strong></p><p>The posterior pituitary or neurohypophysis releases <strong>oxytocin</strong> and <strong>anti-diuretic hormone</strong> (ADH or vasopressin) </p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>ADH and oxytocin are <strong>synthesised</strong> by the <strong>supraoptic </strong>and <strong>paraventricular nuclei</strong> of the <strong>hypothalamus. </strong>Thereafter, they are stored and<strong> released</strong> from the <strong>posterior</strong> <strong>pituitary. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6152","text":"Which pair of vasopressin ‌receptors is incorrect?","raw_text":"Which pair of vasopressin receptors is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"V1a-smoot‌h muscles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"V2-collec‌ting ducts","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"V1b-anterio‌r pituitary","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"V4-‌CNS","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. V4-CNS","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. V4-CNS</strong></p><p>The pair V4-CNS is incorrect.</p>\n<p>Vasopressin mediates its effect via 3 kinds of receptors- <strong>V1a, V1b </strong>and <strong>V2</strong>. There is <strong>no V4.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0118","text":"\"Vasopressin escape\" in SI‌ADH results due to:","raw_text":"\"Vasopressin escape\" in SIADH results due to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Downregulation of aquapori‌n 2 production","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in urine osmolari‌ty","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decrease in ‌ADH secretion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Upregulation of aquaporin ‌2 production","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Downregulation of aquaporin 2 production","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Downregulation of aquaporin 2 production</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Vasopressin escape in SIADH (Syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion) results due to <strong>downregulation </strong>of <strong>aquaporin 2 production.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Prolonged exposure to <strong>elevated levels</strong> of vasopressin (<strong>ADH</strong>) leads eventually to <strong>downregulation of aquaporin-2 receptors</strong>. This <strong>prevents</strong> excessive <strong>reabsorption</strong> of water, despite high serum ADH levels and hence maintains <strong>euvolemia</strong>. This is known as <strong>v</strong><strong>asopressin escape. </strong>This helps <strong>prevent</strong> excessive <strong>dilutional hyponatremia</strong> in SIADH. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6153","text":"Sodium-iodide symport is a‌bsent in which of the fol‍lowing sites?","raw_text":"Sodium-iodide symport is absent in which of the following sites?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pitu‌itary","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Plac‌enta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Par‌otid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thy‌roid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pituitary","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pituitary</strong></p><p>Sodium-iodide symport is absent in<strong> the pituitary. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Na+/I– symporter is present in</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Basolateral membranes of thyrocytes facing the capillar­ies.</li>\n<li>Salivary glands</li>\n<li>Gastric mucosa</li>\n<li>Placenta</li>\n<li>Ciliary body of the eye</li>\n<li>Choroid plexus</li>\n<li>Mammary glands</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6155","text":"A 35-year-old lady is diag‌nosed with Hashimoto thyr‍oiditis and a positive an‌tibody titer against thyr‌oid peroxidase enzyme. Wh‍ich of the following is n​ot a function of the affe‍cted enzyme?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old lady is diagnosed with Hashimoto thyroiditis and a positive antibody titer against thyroid peroxidase enzyme. Which of the following is not a function of the affected enzyme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coup‌ling","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oxid‌ation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Organif‌ication","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iodide ‌trapping","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Iodide trapping","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e457ac295ff24185aded01a37f9e93edx1200x1687.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Iodide trapping</strong></p><p><strong>Iodide trapping </strong>(iodide uptake) is <strong>not </strong>catalyzed by <strong>thyroid peroxidase</strong>. Oxidation, organification, and coupling are catalyzed by it.  </p>\n<p>Steps in synthesis and secretion of thyroid hormone: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Iodide uptake</strong>: The basolateral <strong>sodium-iodide symporter</strong> (NIS) actively takes up I<sup>-</sup>(iodide) from the circulation into the follicular cells. It is then transported into the colloid through a <strong>pendrin</strong> (chloride-iodide) transporter </li>\n<li><strong>Thyroglobulin synthesis</strong>: Thyroglobulin (Tg), is a large glycoprotein rich in <strong>tyrosine residues</strong>. It is synthesized by follicular cells.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Iodination of thyroglobulin</strong>: This is regulated by the <strong>thyroid peroxidase</strong> (TPO) enzyme. TPO has three functions: \n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Oxidation</strong>: TPO oxidizes I<sup>-</sup> to I<sub>2 </sub>(iodine)<sub> </sub> in the presence of H2O2</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Organification</strong>: TPO links tyrosine residues of thyroglobulin with iodine (I<sub>2</sub>) to form monoiodotyrosine (MIT), diiodotyrosine (DIT)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Coupling reactions</strong>: TPO combines iodinated tyrosine residues to make MIT + DIT = T3 (triiodothyronine), DIT + DIT = T4 (tetraiodothyronine)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Storage</strong>: Thyroid hormones are bound to thyroglobulin and stored in the follicular lumen as colloids. The colloid has hormone reserves for <strong>2-3 months</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Release</strong>: Iodinated thyroglobulin is taken up by the thyroid follicles via endocytosis and fuses with lysosomes. It is cleaved by lysosomal proteolytic enzymes into MIT, DIT, T3, and T4. T4 (80%) and T3 (20%) are released into the blood</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Peripheral T3 conversion</strong>: T4 undergoes deiodination in extrathyroidal tissue to form T3, which is the active form of the hormone</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6156","text":"A patient with hyperthyroi‌dism is planned for thyro‍idectomy. In order to ach‌ieve a euthyroid status p‌reoperatively, he is star‍ted on Lugol's iodine sol​ution. Which of the follo‍wing best describes its m​echanism of action?","raw_text":"A patient with hyperthyroidism is planned for thyroidectomy. In order to achieve a euthyroid status preoperatively, he is started on Lugol's iodine solution. Which of the following best describes its mechanism of action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Suppression of TSH secreti‌on","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased iodination of MI‌T","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased T3 to T4 convers‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibition of organificati‌on and synthesis of thyro‍id hormones","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Inhibition of organification and synthesis of thyroid hormones","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Inhibition of organification and synthesis of thyroid hormones</strong></p><p>Introduction of exogenous iodine in the form of lugol's iodine solution leads to transient <strong>inhibition</strong> of <strong>organification </strong>and <strong>synthesis</strong> of thyroid hormones. This is known as the<strong> wolff-chaikoff</strong> effect.</p>\n<p>In individuals with a <strong>normal</strong> thyroid, the gland <strong>escapes</strong> from this inhibitory effect and iodide organification resumes; but the <strong>suppressive</strong> action of high iodide may <strong>persist</strong> in patients with <strong>hyperthyroidism</strong> due to <strong>underlying </strong><strong>autoimmune</strong> thyroid disease.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Jod-Basedow</strong> effect is <strong>iodine</strong>-induced <strong>hyperthyroidism, </strong>usually occurring after the use of an I-based contrast agent. It is often seen in the presence of pre-existing thyroid conditions like multinodular goitre. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6158","text":"How long is thyroxine stor‌ed in thyroid follicles?","raw_text":"How long is thyroxine stored in thyroid follicles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2-3 ‌weeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2-3 ‌days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2-3 m‌onths","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2-3 ‌years","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2-3 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2-3 months</strong></p><p><strong>Thyroxine</strong> is <strong>stored</strong> in the thyroid follicles for <strong>2-3 months, </strong>bound to<strong> thyroglobulin</strong>. </p>\n<p>Serves as a reserve of T3 and T4, thus delaying the occurrence of hypothyroidism, if thyroid secretion ceases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6159","text":"The major thyroxine-bindin‌g protein is _____.","raw_text":"The major thyroxine-binding protein is _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alb‌umin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transt‌hyretin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thyroxine-binding globulin‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trans‌ferrin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thyroxine-binding globulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thyroxine-binding globulin</strong></p><p><strong>Thyroxine-binding globulin</strong> (TBG) is the major thyroxine-binding protein.</p>\n<p>Other<strong> thyroxine-binding proteins</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Albumin</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Transthyretin (TTR)/ thyroxine-binding pre-albumin (TBPA)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lipoproteins</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6160","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding thyroid h‍ormone?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding thyroid hormone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T3 is more avidly bound to‌ nuclear receptor than T4‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T4 has more plasma concent‌ration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T3 is more a‌ctive than T4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T4 has shorter half- life ‌than T3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. T4 has shorter half- life than T3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. T4 has shorter half- life than T3</strong></p><p>T4 doesn't have a shorter half-life than T3, rather  <strong>T3</strong> has a <strong>shorter half-life</strong> and more <strong>rapid action</strong> than T4.</p>\n<p>The table compares T4 and T3:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>T3</td>\n<td>T4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>More potent (4x)</td>\n<td>Less potent </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>More active </td>\n<td>Less active </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rapid onset of action </td>\n<td>Slow onset of action </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shorter half-life (1 day)</td>\n<td>Longer half-life (7 days)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Less plasma concentration</td>\n<td>More plasma concentration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Less in colloid </td>\n<td>More in colloid </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Responsible for physiological actions </td>\n<td>Used for pharmacotherapy  </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6161","text":"Half-life ‌of T4_____","raw_text":"Half-life of T4_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6-7 ‌days","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"6 w‌eeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2-3 m‌onths","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 6-7 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 6-7 days</strong></p><p>The half-life of T4 is 6 - 7 days.</p>\n<p>T3 has the shorter half-life of 1 day.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6162","text":"A 35 yr old woman presents‌ with heat intolerance an‍d menstrual abnormalities‌. On examination, a diffu‌sely enlarged thyroid gla‍nd is present. Which of t​he following features wil‍l not be present in her?","raw_text":"A 35 yr old woman presents with heat intolerance and menstrual abnormalities. On examination, a diffusely enlarged thyroid gland is present. Which of the following features will not be present in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased ‌heart rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased s‌troke volume","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased peripheral vascu‌lar resistance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased protein breakdow‌n","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Decreased protein breakdown","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Decreased protein breakdown</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hyperthyroidism</strong>, which does not cause decreased protein breakdown. Rather it causes <strong>increased</strong> protein breakdown.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0740","text":"Prolactin level is highest‌ during :","raw_text":"Prolactin level is highest during :","options":[{"label":"A","text":"24 hours aft‌er ovulation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"24 hours af‌ter delivery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"REM ‌Sleep","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"After runnin‌g for 1 hour","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 24 hours after delivery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 24 hours after delivery</strong></p><p>Among the given options, the prolactin level is <strong>highest</strong> 24 hours after delivery. </p>\n<p>The <strong>maximum</strong> level of <strong>prolactin</strong> is seen during <strong>pregnancy</strong> and in the <strong>immediate postpartum</strong> period. During pregnancy, the secretion is increased reaching a <strong>peak </strong>at the time of<strong> delivery</strong>. After delivery, the plasma concentration falls to nonpregnant levels in a few weeks. <strong>Suckling</strong> produces <strong>surges</strong> of prolactin release. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0801","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions would have the hi‍ghest levels of ACTH and ‌cortisol?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions would have the highest levels of ACTH and cortisol?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Early ‌morning","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Normal individuals with de‌xamethasone administratio‍n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Addison'‌s disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cushing'‌s disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cushing's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7c981088d67c4c85ad2d59d8c5adedf7x1280x2102.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cushing&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>Among the given conditions, the levels of ACTH and cortisol will be highest in <strong>Cushing's disease.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cushing’s disease</strong> is caused by an <strong>ACTH secreting pituitary adenoma</strong>. The increased ACTH causes increased cortisol production.</p>\n<p>Note: In normal physiologic state, ACTH levels are higher in the early morning but not as high as in Cushing's disease.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"pOW-3K23","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"37_carcinoma_pancreas","title":"37.Carcinoma Pancreas","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB7296","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about mu​cinous cystadenoma of the​ pancreas?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about mucinous cystadenoma of the pancreas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is lined by columnar ep​ithelium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Commonly seen in peri-meno​pausal women","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Identified histologically ​by the presence of ovaria​n-like stroma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is not p​remalignant","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is not premalignant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is not premalignant</strong></p><p><strong>Mucinous cystadenoma</strong> of the pancreas is a <strong>premalignant </strong>condition.</p>\n<p>Mucinous cystadenoma or mucinous cystic neoplasm (MCN) is a <strong>benign</strong> tumor of the pancreas <strong>with high risk of malignancy</strong>. They are commonly seen in <strong>perimenopausal women</strong>, and about <strong>two-thirds</strong> are located in <strong>the body or tail of the pancreas.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1438\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>MCNs are mostly <strong>incidental findings</strong>. When symptoms are present, they are usually <strong>nonspecific</strong> and include upper abdominal discomfort, early satiety, or weight loss.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The cysts are <strong>lined by tall columnar epithelium. </strong>MCN's contain <strong>mucin-producing epithelium</strong> and are identified histologically by the presence of <strong>mucin-rich cells and ovarian-like stroma</strong>. Staining for <strong>estrogen and progesterone</strong> is <strong>positive </strong>in most cases.</p>\n<p>Malignant transformation of MCN is <strong>common with large tumors and old patients.</strong> This is due to a stepwise <strong>accumulation of mutations (K-ras, p53).</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 420px; width: 518px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 507.023px; height: 31px; text-align: left;\"><strong>WHO classification of primary tumors of the pancreas</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96.3984px; height: 35px;\"><strong>Benign</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 404.156px; height: 35px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Mucinous cystadenoma</li>\n<li>Serous cystadenoma</li>\n<li>Intraductal papillary mucinous adenoma</li>\n<li>Mature teratoma</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 47px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96.3984px; height: 47px;\"><strong>Borderline</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 404.156px; height: 47px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1438\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Mucinous cystic tumor with moderate dysplasia</li>\n<li>Intraductal papillary mucinous tumor with moderate dysplasia</li>\n<li>Solid pseudopapillary tumor</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 74px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96.3984px; height: 74px;\"><strong>Malignant</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 404.156px; height: 74px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Ductal adenocarcinoma</li>\n<li>Serous/mucinous cystadenocarcinoma</li>\n<li>Intraductal mucinous papillary tumor</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7300","text":"A patient with a pancreati​c neoplasm undergoes cyst​ fluid aspiration. The as​pirated fluid stains posi​tive for mucin and shows ‍elevated levels of amylas‍e and CEA. What is the mo‌st probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient with a pancreatic neoplasm undergoes cyst fluid aspiration. The aspirated fluid stains positive for mucin and shows elevated levels of amylase and CEA. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serous cystadenoma of panc​reas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mucinous cystadenoma of pa​ncreas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intraductal papillary muci​nous neoplasm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mature cyst​ic teratoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm</strong></p><p><strong>Positive mucin</strong> staining, <strong>elevated carcinoembryonic antigen</strong> (CEA), and <strong>elevated amylase</strong> levels are seen in cases of <strong>intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm</strong> (IPMN).</p>\n<p>Cyst fluid analysis of various pancreatic cystic neoplasms:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 196px; width: 655px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 58px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121.625px; height: 58px;\">Characteristics</td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.375px; height: 58px;\"><strong>Serous cystic neoplasm (SCN)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 58px;\"><strong>Mucinous cystic neoplasm (MCN)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px; height: 58px;\"><strong>Intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm (IPMN)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 37.2656px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121.625px; height: 37.2656px;\"><strong>Cytology</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.375px; height: 37.2656px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1565\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Scant glycogen-rich cells, with positive periodic acid–Schiff stain</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 37.2656px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1565\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Sheets and clusters of columnar, mucin-containing cells</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px; height: 37.2656px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1565\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Tall, columnar, mucin-containing cells</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121.625px; height: 19px;\"><strong>Mucin stain</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.375px; height: 19px;\">Negative</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 19px;\">Positive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px; height: 19px;\">Positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121.625px; height: 19px;\"><strong>Amylase</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.375px; height: 19px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 19px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px; height: 19px;\">High</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121.625px; height: 19px;\"><strong>CEA</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 191.375px; height: 19px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 19px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px; height: 19px;\">High</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7299","text":"A 58-year-old man is being​ evaluated for recurrent ​attacks of pancreatitis. ​An ERCP is done and it sh​ows the presence of a fis‍h-mouth sign at the ampul‍la of Vater. Which of the‌ following statements is ‍true about his condition?‍","raw_text":"A 58-year-old man is being evaluated for recurrent attacks of pancreatitis. An ERCP is done and it shows the presence of a fish-mouth sign at the ampulla of Vater. Which of the following statements is true about his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is more common in males​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It only involves the main ​pancreatic duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Head of pancreas is most c​ommon site of the neoplas​m","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"They have no malignant pot​ential","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Head of pancreas is most common site of the neoplasm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Head of pancreas is most common site of the neoplasm</strong></p><p>A<strong> fish mouth sign </strong>at the ampulla of Vater during<strong> ERCP</strong> is indicative of <strong>intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm (IPMN). </strong>It is most commonly seen in the <strong>head of the pancreas.</strong></p>\n<p>The intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm can be diagnosed with the following findings (<strong>Ohashi triad</strong>) during endoscopy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bulging ampulla of Vater (<strong>fish-mouth sign</strong>)</li>\n<li>Dilated pancreatic duct</li>\n<li>Mucin secretion from the opening</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Equal distribution</strong> is seen between males and females in IPMN.<strong><strong> </strong></strong>Patients are usually in their <strong>seventh or eighth decade of life</strong> and present with <strong>abdominal pain</strong> or <strong>recurrent pancreatitis</strong>. This is caused by the <strong>obstruction of the pancreatic duct</strong> by thick mucin. Some patients have symptoms secondary to <strong>pancreatic insufficiencies</strong> like steatorrhea, diabetes, and weight loss.</p>\n<p>IMPNs are of three types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Branch-duct IPMN</strong> (BD-IPMN) - affects only the small<strong> side branches. </strong>It is less frequently associated with malignancy.</li>\n<li><strong>Main duct IPMN</strong> (MD-IPMN) - Affects the <strong>main pancreatic duct. </strong>Malignant transformation is commonly seen.</li>\n<li><strong>Mixed-type IPMN - </strong>side-branch IPMNs that extend into the main duct </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7301","text":"A 60-year-old man was inci​dentally found to have a ​pancreatic lesion on a US​G. On further evaluation,​ a CT scan revealed the p‍resence of a multicystic,‍ lobulated mass in the pa‌ncreatic head, suggestive‍ of serous cystadenoma. H‍e is currently asymptomat​ic. What will be the next​ step in the management o​f this patient?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man was incidentally found to have a pancreatic lesion on a USG. On further evaluation, a CT scan revealed the presence of a multicystic, lobulated mass in the pancreatic head, suggestive of serous cystadenoma. He is currently asymptomatic. What will be the next step in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Repeat CT scan after 6 mon​ths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Resection o​f the tumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endoscopic ultrasound guid​ed FNA","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Diagnostic ​laparoscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Endoscopic ultrasound guided FNA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b29d7d4a0f8a49c799fdcd287064bb5bx1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Endoscopic ultrasound guided FNA</strong></p><p><strong>Endoscopic ultrasound-guided FNA</strong> (EUS-FNA) will be the next step in the management of this patient.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1437\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Serous cystadenomas</strong> are essentially <strong>benign tumors</strong> without malignant potential. Serous cystadenocarcinoma has been reported very rarely (&lt;1%). Therefore, malignant potential should not be a consideration for surgical resection. <strong>Surgical resection</strong> is done only for <strong>symptomatic</strong> patients.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1438\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>In <strong>asymptomatic</strong> patients, <strong>conservative </strong>management is adopted. However, it is important to confirm the diagnosis using <strong>EUS-FNA</strong>. It yields a <strong>non-viscous fluid</strong> with <strong>low CEA and amylase</strong> levels.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Algorithm for management of pancreatic cystic neoplasms:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7302","text":"What is the initiating eve​nt in the oncogenesis of ​pancreatic cancers?","raw_text":"What is the initiating event in the oncogenesis of pancreatic cancers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activation​ of K-ras 2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of p1​6 function","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"p53 mu​tation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Loss of DP​C4 function","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Activation of K-ras 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0718ca21e1614634b91197a6e481772dx1280x1007.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Activation of K-ras 2</strong></p><p>The <strong>activation of the K-ras 2</strong> oncogene is the <strong>initiating event</strong> in the oncogenesis of <strong>pancreatic cancers</strong>. It is activated in <strong>more than 95%</strong> of pan­creatic cancers.</p>\n<p><strong>Pancreatic intraepithelial neoplasia</strong> (PanIN) develops into <strong>pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma</strong> (PDAC) in a sequential pathway. A number of tumor suppressor genes and oncogenes play a significant role in the pathogenesis of PDAC. They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>K-ras 2</li>\n<li>PDX1</li>\n<li>CDKN2A/p16</li>\n<li>p53</li>\n<li>DPC4 (SMAD4)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7303","text":"Which of the following are​ not risk factors for the​ development of pancreati​c cancer? History of acut​e pancreatitis Long-stand‍ing diabetes mellitus BRC‍A2 mutation Female gender‌ Cigarette smoking","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are not risk factors for the development of pancreatic cancer?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>History of acute pancreatitis</li>\n<li>Long-standing diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li>BRCA2 mutation</li>\n<li>Female gender</li>\n<li>Cigarette smoking</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a​nd 4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1 a​nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a​nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3 a​nd 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1 and 4</strong></p><p>An history of acute pancreatitis and female gender are <strong>not </strong>risk factors for the development of pancreatic cancer.</p>\n<p>Risk factors for pancreatic cancer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Demographic factors:\n<ul>\n<li>Age (peak incidence, 65–75 years)</li>\n<li><strong>Male gender</strong></li>\n<li>Black ethnicity</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Cigarette smoking</strong></li>\n<li>Genetic factors and medical conditions</li>\n<li>Family history:\n<ul>\n<li>Two first-degree relatives with pancreas cancer</li>\n<li>Germline <strong><em>BRCA2</em> mutations</strong> in some rare high-risk families</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Hereditary pancreatitis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Chronic pancreatitis</strong></li>\n<li>Lynch syndrome</li>\n<li>Ataxia telangiectasia</li>\n<li>Peutz–Jeghers syndrome</li>\n<li>Familial breast-ovarian cancer syndrome</li>\n<li>Familial adenomatous polyposis </li>\n<li><strong>Diabetes mellitus</strong></li>\n<li>Obesity </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7304","text":"What is the most common ty​pe of pancreatic malignan​cy?","raw_text":"What is the most common type of pancreatic malignancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous ce​ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adenoca​rcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Insul​inoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acinar cel​l carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adenocarcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adenocarcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Ductal adenocarcinoma</strong> is the most common type of pancreatic cancer.</p>\n<p>Types of exocrine pancreatic cancer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ductal adenocarcinoma (85%)</li>\n<li>Adenosquamous carcinoma</li>\n<li>Acinar cell carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Endocrine cancer of the pancreas is rare. They account for only 5% of clinically detected pancreatic tumours.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7305","text":"What is the most common si​te for pancreatic carcino​ma?","raw_text":"What is the most common site for pancreatic carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"He​ad","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ne​ck","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bo​dy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ta​il","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Head","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Head</strong></p><p><strong>Pancreatic carcinomas </strong>arise most commonly from the <strong>head </strong>of the gland.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7306","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g the presentation of pat​ients with pancreatic car​cinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the presentation of patients with pancreatic carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Release of platelet aggreg​ating factors cause migra​tory thrombophlebitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abdominal pain is the most​ common presentation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Courvoisier's law implicat​es possible cancer of the​ head of the pancreas","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tumors of the body present​ at an advanced unresecta​ble stage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Abdominal pain is the most common presentation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1d5e42bd33194f92bdfbe260ecfece06x720x990.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Abdominal pain is the most common presentation</strong></p><p><strong>Jaundice</strong> is the most common presentation of <strong>carcinoma of the head of the pancreas</strong>. It is secondary to <strong>obstruction of the distal bile duct</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1255\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Clinical features of pancreatic cancers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Jaundice</strong> (most common)</li>\n<li>Nausea</li>\n<li>Epigastric discomfort</li>\n<li>Pruritus, dark urine, pale stools</li>\n<li>Steatorrhea</li>\n<li>Anorexia</li>\n<li>Weight loss</li>\n<li>Back pain - indicates retroperitoneal infiltration</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On examination, the patient can have:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Palpable abdominal mass</li>\n<li>Ascites</li>\n<li>Supraclavicular lymphnodes</li>\n<li>Migratory superficial thrombophlebitis (<strong>Trousseau’s sign of malignancy</strong>) - due to release of <strong>platelet aggregating factors</strong> from the tumor</li>\n<li>Secondaries in the rectovesical pouch (<strong>Blumer’s shelf</strong>)</li>\n<li>A palpable, non-tender gall bladder</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Courvoisier's law</strong> states that in the presence of a <strong>painless</strong>, <strong>palpable</strong>, <strong>enlarged gallbladder</strong> and mild <strong>jaundice</strong>, the cause is <strong>unlikely to be gallstones</strong>. This sign implicates possible cancer of the head of the pancreas.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Tumors of the <strong>body and tail</strong> of the gland often <strong>grow silently</strong> and present at an advanced unresectable stage.</p>\n<p>Image showing migratory superficial thrombophlebitis (Trousseau’s sign of malignancy):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Trousseau's sign</strong> is also seen in <strong>hypocalcemia</strong>. It indicates <strong>carpopedal spasm</strong> (finger and wrist spasm) after inflation of a blood pressure cuff for several minutes when the brachial artery is occluded.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7307","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is not true about​ pancreatic ductal adenoc​arcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true about pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is associated with desm​oplastic changes, with sc​attering of neoplastic gl​ands","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perineural invasion is cha​racteristic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Body tumors​ are smaller","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Carcinoma of the head of t​he pancreas causes obstru​ction of both the pancrea​tic and biliary ducts","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Body tumors are smaller","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Body tumors are smaller</strong></p><p><strong>Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinomas </strong>situated in the <strong>body of the pancreas</strong> are usually <strong>larger.</strong></p>\n<p>Pancreatic adenocarcinomas are <strong>solid, slow-growing tumors</strong>, characterized by neoplastic tubular glands within a markedly <strong>desmoplastic fibrous stroma</strong>. They <strong>infiltrate locally,</strong> typically along nerve sheaths, along lymphatics, and into blood vessels.</p>\n<p>Carcinoma of the head of the pancreas causes<strong> obstruction</strong> of both the pancreatic and biliary ducts.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7308","text":"A 60-year-old man with a h​istory of chronic pancrea​titis presents with jaund​ice, severe itching, and ​weight loss. A smooth, gl‍obular mass is palpable i‍n the right upper quadran‌t of the abdomen. You sus‍pect pancreatic cancer. W‍hat will be the imaging m​odality of choice for the​ evaluation of the tumor?​","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man with a history of chronic pancreatitis presents with jaundice, severe itching, and weight loss. A smooth, globular mass is palpable in the right upper quadrant of the abdomen. You suspect pancreatic cancer. What will be the imaging modality of choice for the evaluation of the tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endoscopic retrograde chol​angiopancreatography","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Magnetic resonance cholang​iopancreatography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Multidetec​tor CT scan","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"PET ​scan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Multidetector CT scan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a1ef7d3cce8d43a2b0aadc3d651c0e7ax720x581.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Multidetector CT scan</strong></p><p><strong>Multidetector computed tomography </strong>(CT) is the <strong>imaging</strong> <strong>modality of choice</strong> for the evalua­tion of <strong>pancreatic tumors</strong>. It allows an accurate determination of the level of biliary obstruction, the relationship of the tumor to the blood vessels, and the presence of regional or metastatic disease.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1571\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>For a detailed assessment of the luminal <strong>pancreato-biliary anatomy</strong>, <strong>MRCP</strong> should be considered. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1571\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>ERCP</strong> is reserved for cases requiring therapeutic or palliative intervention. It helps to perform a <strong>biopsy</strong> and to relieve obstruction by <strong>biliary stenting</strong>. The invasiveness of ERCP increases the rate of <strong>wound infection</strong>. Hence, it is not suitable for patients who will be undergoing surgery.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1571\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1571\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>F-fluorodeoxyglucose positron emission tomography (<strong>FDG-PET</strong>) has the ability to differentiate between benign and malignant pancreas tumors. However, <strong>false-positive results </strong>are seen in FDG-PET. The benefits of FDG-PET over CT scans are yet to be determined.</p>\n<p>A CT-scan of pancreatic cancer:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7309","text":"Which of the following is ​a radiological sign that ​is not seen in patients w​ith adenocarcinoma of the​ pancreas?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a radiological sign that is not seen in patients with adenocarcinoma of the pancreas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scrambled eg​g appearance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inverse​ 3 sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Double duct​ appearance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Narrowing of C-loop of the​ duodenum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Narrowing of C-loop of the duodenum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Narrowing of C-loop of the duodenum</strong></p><p><strong>Widening of the C-loop</strong> of the duodenum is a <strong>barium meal</strong> finding seen in the <strong>adenocarcinoma of the pancreas</strong>.</p>\n<p>Radiological features of adenocarcinoma pancreas:</p>\n<p>Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Scrambled egg appearance</li>\n<li>Double duct appearance</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Barium meal findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inverse 3 sign</li>\n<li>Rose thorning of 2<sup>nd</sup> part of the duodenum</li>\n<li>Widened duodenal C-loop</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3788","text":"The best method to obtain ​tissue for the diagnosis ​of pancreatic carcinoma i​s:","raw_text":"The best method to obtain tissue for the diagnosis of pancreatic carcinoma is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transgastric FNAC guided b​y endoscopic ultrasound","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"MRI-guid​ed biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Laparoscopic-guided biopsy​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Percutaneous transperitone​al biopsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Transgastric FNAC guided by endoscopic ultrasound","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Transgastric FNAC guided by endoscopic ultrasound</strong></p><p><strong>Transgastric or transduodenal FNAC</strong> or <strong>trucut biopsy</strong> performed under<strong> endoscopic ultrasound (EUS)</strong> guidance is the best method to obtain tissue for the diagnosis of <strong>pancreatic carcinoma</strong>. This is because it <strong>avoids the spillage</strong> of tumor cells into the peritoneal cavity. </p>\n<p>Endoscopic ultrasonography with a fine needle aspiration <strong>(EUS-FNA)</strong> is done if CT has failed to demonstrate a tumor. When a tissue diagnosis is required prior to surgery to distinguish between inflammation and neoplasia, EUS-FNA is helpful.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1256\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Diagnostic laparoscopy</strong> prior to resection can spare an unnecessary laparotomy by identifying small peritoneal and liver metastases.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Percutaneous transperitoneal biopsy</strong> of potentially resectable pancreatic tumors is <strong>avoided.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7310","text":"Which is the most reliable​ tumor marker for the pre​-treatment evaluation of ​a patient with pancreatic​ adenocarcinoma?","raw_text":"Which is the most reliable tumor marker for the pre-treatment evaluation of a patient with pancreatic adenocarcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carcinoembry​onic antigen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CA ​19-9","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Alpha-fe​toprotein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CA ​15-3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CA 19-9","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CA 19-9</strong></p><p><strong>Carbohydrate antigen (CA) 19-9</strong> is the <strong>most reliable tumor marker</strong> for pretreatment evaluation and post-treatment surveillance of <strong>pancreatic adenocarcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other tumor markers for pancreatic carcinoma are <strong>carcinoembryonic antigen</strong> (CEA) and <strong>α-fetoprotein</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4282","text":"A 50-year-old man has been​ diagnosed with pancreati​c cancer. In order to dia​gnose a resectability sta​tus, a CECT abdomen is pe‍rformed. Which of the fol‍lowing features will indi‌cate an unresectable tumo‍r?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man has been diagnosed with pancreatic cancer. In order to diagnose a resectability status, a CECT abdomen is performed. Which of the following features will indicate an unresectable tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abutment of superior mesen​teric artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stage II cancer on AJCC st​aging criteria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Short segment occlusion of​ superior mesenteric vein​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Encasement of celiac arter​y","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Encasement of celiac artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Encasement of celiac artery</strong></p><p>Encasement of the celiac artery ( i.e &gt;180° or &gt;50% of the vessel circumference involvement) indicates surgical inoperability and is therefore considered to be non-resectable.</p>\n<p><strong>Varadhachary- Katz criteria</strong> for resectability of pancreatic cancer:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 439px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">Criteria</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90px;\">\n<p>Resectable</p>\n<p>(if all are satisfied)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.683px;\">\n<p>Borderline resectable</p>\n<p>(if any one is satisfied)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.317px;\">\n<p>Unresectable</p>\n<p>(if any one is satisfied)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">AJCC stage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90px;\">I/II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.683px;\">III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.317px;\">III</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">SMA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90px;\">Not affected</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.683px;\">Abutment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.317px;\">Encasement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">Celiac axis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90px;\">Not affected</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.683px;\">Abutment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.317px;\">Encasement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">CHA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90px;\">Not affected</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.683px;\">Abutment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.317px;\">Encasement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">SMV-PV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90px;\">Patent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.683px;\">Short segment occlusion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.317px;\">Long segment occlusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 78px;\">\n<p>Note: Any evidence of peritoneal seeding, malignant ascites, or distant metastasis also indicates an unresectable tumor.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>SMA: superior mesenteric artery, CHA: common hepatic artery, SMV-PV: superior mesenteric vein-portal vein confluence</p>\n<p>Definitions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Abutment</strong> means <strong>≤180°</strong> or ≤50% of the vessel circumference involvement; </li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Encasement</strong> means <strong>&gt;180°</strong> or &gt;50% of the vessel circumference  involvement</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7311","text":"A patient presents to the ​OPD with complaints of ja​undice, clay-colored stoo​ls, and pruritis. After d​etailed radiological and ‍pathological investigatio‍ns, a 2 cm adenocarcinoma‌ is found at the head of ‍the pancreas. There is no‍ spread to the adjacent s​tructures. What is the pr​eferred surgical manageme​nt in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient presents to the OPD with complaints of jaundice, clay-colored stools, and pruritis. After detailed radiological and pathological investigations, a 2 cm adenocarcinoma is found at the head of the pancreas. There is no spread to the adjacent structures. What is the preferred surgical management in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Whipple’s​ procedure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pylorus preserving pancrea​ticoduodenectomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Duodenum-preserving pancre​atic head resection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Puestow's​ procedure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pylorus preserving pancreaticoduodenectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pylorus preserving pancreaticoduodenectomy</strong></p><p>Since there is no involvement of adjacent structures, the preferred surgical management in this patient is <strong>pylorus-preserving pancreatoduodenectomy (PPPD), </strong>also known as <strong>Longmire–Traverso procedure</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pylorus-preserving pancreatoduodenectomy (PPPD) involves the removal of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Duodenum </li>\n<li>Pancreatic head</li>\n<li>The distal part of the bile duct</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> original pancreatoduodenectomy</strong>, as proposed by <strong>Whipple</strong>, included <strong>resection of the gastric antrum</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Preserving the antrum</strong> <strong>and the pylorus</strong> results in a <strong>better physiological outcome</strong>, with no difference in survival or recurrence rates. Hence PPPD is preferred.</p>\n<p>For tumors of the <strong>body and tail</strong>, <strong>distal pancreatectomy</strong> is the preferred surgical management.</p>\n<p><strong>Duodenum-preserving pancreatic head resection</strong> (Beger's procedure) and <strong>Puestow's procedure</strong> (resection of tail followed by longitudinal pancreaticojejunostomy) are management options for <strong>chronic pancreatitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7312","text":"You assist in performing a​ Whipple procedure in a p​atient with carcinoma of ​the pancreas. All of the ​following structures will‍ be resected in this surg‍ery, except: Gallbladder ‌Common bile duct Spleen P‍roximal jejunum Tail of t‍he pancreas","raw_text":"<p>You assist in performing a Whipple procedure in a patient with carcinoma of the pancreas. All of the following structures will be resected in this surgery, except:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Gallbladder</li>\n<li>Common bile duct</li>\n<li>Spleen</li>\n<li>Proximal jejunum</li>\n<li>Tail of the pancreas</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a​nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a​nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 a​nd 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"4 a​nd 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/07f48a57633d4d77906091f13eac1771x1280x2559.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3 and 5</strong></p><p>Whipple procedure does <strong>not</strong> involve the removal of the spleen and tail of the pancreas.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Whipple procedure</strong> is a complex surgery done for carcinomas involving the <strong>head of the pancreas</strong>. It is done only in cases when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The entire duodenum has to be removed e.g. in familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)</li>\n<li>Tumor encroaches in the first part of the duodenum, or stomach</li>\n<li>A clear resection margin is not achieved by pylorus preserving pancreatoduodectomy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following structures are removed during Whipple’s procedure:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Distal stomach</li>\n<li>Duodenum</li>\n<li>Proximal jejunum</li>\n<li>Head of the pancreas</li>\n<li>Gallbladder</li>\n<li>Common bile duct (CBD)</li>\n<li>Regional lymphatics</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7316","text":"During the reconstruction ​of the Whipple procedure,​ all of the following ana​stomoses will be done by ​a surgeon except:","raw_text":"During the reconstruction of the Whipple procedure, all of the following anastomoses will be done by a surgeon except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pancreatico​jejunostomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ileojej​unostomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Choledocho​jejunostomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gastroje​junostomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ileojejunostomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2f0d0ddf66894823ba8e298d90cd04b9x1280x2559.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ileojejunostomy</strong></p><p>Ileojejunostomy will <strong>not</strong> be done during the reconstruction of the Whipple procedure.</p>\n<p>The <strong>reconstruction following Whipple’s procedure</strong> or pylorus-preserving pancreaticoduodenectomy involves anastomosis in the following order:</p>\n<p>Pancreati­cojejunostomy</p>\n<p>              ↓</p>\n<p>Choledochojejunostomy</p>\n<p>              ↓</p>\n<p>Gastrojejunostomy</p>\n<p>This is <strong>controversial</strong> since there is a difference in the reconstruction procedures mentioned in standard textbooks:</p>\n<p>Bailey and Love, 27th edition (Pg no: 1236) - Pancreaticojejunostomy + choledechojejunostomy + duodenojejunostomy <br/><br/>Schwatrz, 11th edition (Pg no: 1493) - Pancreaticojejunostomy + choledechojejunostomy + gastrojejunostomy/duodenojejunostomy<br/><br/>Sabiston, 20th edition (Pg no: 1546) - Pancreaticojejunostomy + hepaticojejunostomy + duodenojejunostomy</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7313","text":"During the Whipple procedu​re, a surgeon performs th​e Kocher maneuver to acce​ss the retroperitoneum. T​his maneuver involves:","raw_text":"During the Whipple procedure, a surgeon performs the Kocher maneuver to access the retroperitoneum. This maneuver involves:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mobilization of gall bladd​er","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mobilization of second par​t of duodenum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mobilization of the pancre​as","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mobilization of ascending ​colon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mobilization of second part of duodenum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mobilization of second part of duodenum</strong></p><p>The<strong> Kocher maneuver</strong> involves the mobilization of the <strong>second part of the duodenum. </strong>It allows exposure of the retroperitoneal fat and the inferior vena cava (IVC).</p>\n<p>Another maneuver that is used to access the retroperitoneum is the <strong>Cattel Braasch maneuver</strong>. It involves the mobilization of the <strong>ascending colon.</strong></p>\n<p>Other Kocher's in medicine:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Kocher's technique is used for the reduction of <strong>anterior shoulder dislocation</strong>. It involves four steps:<br/>Traction → External Rotation → Adduction → Medial Rotation (TEAM)</li>\n<li>During <strong>thyroid palpation</strong>, Kocher's method is done to demonstrate the <strong>pressure effect</strong> of the thyroid gland on the trachea or larynx. <strong>Lateral compression</strong> will result in stridor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7314","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g complications of the Wh​ipple procedure?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding complications of the Whipple procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Delayed gastric emptying i​s characterized by the ne​ed for prolonged nasogast​ric decompression","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endocrine insufficiency is​ a complication","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Any drainage output on or ​after postoperative day 3​, with amylase &amp;gt;3 time​s normal value indicates ​pancreatic fistula","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Biliary leakage is the mos​t common complication","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Biliary leakage is the most common complication","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Biliary leakage is the most common complication</strong></p><p><strong>Delayed gastric emptying</strong> is the most common complication following the <strong>Whipple procedure, </strong>not biliary leakage.</p>\n<p>Delayed gastric emptying is characterized by the need for prolonged nasogastric decompression or the inability to tolerate oral intake. </p>\n<p>Most pancreatic fistulas are controlled by drainage catheters placed at the time of surgery. A <strong>pancreatic leak or pancreatic fistula</strong> is defined as the output via an intraoperatively placed drain (or percutaneous drain) of any measurable volume on or after <strong>postoperative day 3</strong>, with <strong>amylase &gt;3 times the normal value</strong>. </p>\n<p>Other complications of the Whipple procedure include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wound infection</li>\n<li>Intra-abdominal abscess</li>\n<li>Cardiac events</li>\n<li>Bile leak</li>\n<li>Overall reoperation</li>\n<li>Exocrine insufficiency</li>\n<li>Endocrine insufficiency</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7317","text":"What is the most common ca​use of death in patients ​following pancreaticoduod​enectomy?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of death in patients following pancreaticoduodenectomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiopulmonary complicati​ons","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gastric outlet obstruction​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anastomot​ic leakage","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Haemo​rrhage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anastomotic leakage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anastomotic leakage</strong></p><p>The most common cause of death following <strong>pancreaticoduodenectomy</strong> is sepsis due to<strong> anastomotic leakage.</strong></p>\n<p>Anastomotic leakage is responsible for 33–88 % of postoperative deaths after pancreaticoduodenectomy. Only about 5–16 % of patients develop post-surgical bleeding complications.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7320","text":"A patient was found to hav​e a 3 cm adenocarcinoma o​f the body of the pancrea​s invading into the super​ior mesenteric artery. Al‍l of the following chemot‍herapeutic agents can be ‌utilized for the manageme‍nt of this patient except‍:","raw_text":"A patient was found to have a 3 cm adenocarcinoma of the body of the pancreas invading into the superior mesenteric artery. All of the following chemotherapeutic agents can be utilized for the management of this patient except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"FOLFIRINO​X regimen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Albumin-boun​d paclitaxel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MVAC r​egimen","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gemci​tabine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. MVAC regimen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. MVAC regimen</strong></p><p><strong>MVAC regimen </strong>(methotrexate, vinblastine, adriamycin, and cisplatin) is not used in the management of this patient. MVAC regimen is used in <strong>advanced bladder carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Chemotherapy</strong> in pancreatic cancer is indicated in patients with <strong>unresectable tumours</strong> and lymph node metastasis. Agents used in chemotherapy are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gemcitabine</li>\n<li>Gemcitabine with capecitabine</li>\n<li>Albumin bound paclitaxel (nab-paclitaxel)</li>\n<li>FOLFIRINOX - a combination of fluorouracil, irinotecan, oxaliplatin, and leucovorin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>However, there is only a <strong>2–5 months increase in median survival</strong> with these regimens. <strong>No long-term cure</strong> is seen with chemotherapy or radiotherapy for pancreatic carcinomas.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"0AqC0O9M","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"38_brachial_plexus_and_nerves","title":"38.Brachial Plexus And Nerves","num_questions":31,"total_marks":124,"duration":41,"time_per_question":79.35,"questions":[{"id":"MA0533","text":"A 48-year-old construction​ worker presented with co​mplaints of unilateral lo​ss of sweating on the fac‌e. He had a history of tr​auma to his back 1 month ‌ago. Examination revealed​ the following findings. ‍What is the likely site o​f the lesion?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old construction worker presented with complaints of unilateral loss of sweating on the face. He had a history of trauma to his back 1 month ago. Examination revealed the following findings. What is the likely site of the lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Grey ram​us of T1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"White ra​mus of T1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Grey ram​us of C8","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"White ra​mus of C8","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. White ramus of T1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/113e06a5d3f24de195ab3afae572cdb1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f89d332c4b714a9eaacacaf03dbdb04dx1280x1002.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. White ramus of T1</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Horner's</strong> or Bernard-Horner's <strong>syndrome</strong>. It can be caused by the <strong>lesion</strong> of the <strong>white ramus </strong>of<strong> T1</strong>. </p>\n<p>Horner's syndrome is characterized by a constricted pupil <strong>(miosis), </strong>drooping of the upper eyelid<strong> (ptosis), </strong>and an absence of sweating of the face <strong>(anhidrosis). </strong>It occurs due to the disruption of the sympathetic nerve supply.</p>\n<p>In the autonomic nervous system, the <strong>sympathetic</strong> plexus is <strong>thoracolumbar </strong>and the <strong>parasympathetic</strong> plexus is <strong>craniosacral.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> sympathetic</strong> plexus is formed along the side of the spinal cord by <strong>white</strong> ramus <strong>inflow</strong> (<strong>preganglionic</strong> nerve fibers) and <strong>gray</strong> ramus <strong>outflow</strong> (<strong>postganglionic</strong>) and it extends from T1-L2. </p>\n<p>In Horner's syndrome, since the sympathetic inflow is disrupted, the white ramus of the spinal cord is affected as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The difference between the sympathetic and parasympathetic ganglions are tabulated below.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 204px; width: 323px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 36px; text-align: center; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 33px; text-align: center; height: 36px;\"><strong>Sympathetic ganglions</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; text-align: center; height: 36px;\"><strong>Parasympathetic ganglions</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 36px; height: 37px;\">Location</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33px; height: 37px;\">Closer to the spinal cord</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; height: 37px;\">Closer to the organ</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 36px; height: 37px;\">Pre-ganglionic fibers</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33px; height: 37px;\">Short </td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; height: 37px;\">Long</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 56px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 36px; height: 56px;\">Post-ganglionic fibers</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33px; height: 56px;\">Long</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; height: 56px;\">Short</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0108","text":"Which of the following str​uctures of brachial plexu​s do not give branches?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures of brachial plexus do not give branches?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ro​ots","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Co​rds","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tru​nks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Divi​sions","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Divisions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Divisions</strong></p><p>The <strong>divisions</strong> in the brachial plexus do <strong>not give any branches.</strong> </p>\n<p>However, the roots, trunks, and cords of the brachial plexus give out direct branches. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3685","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about brachial pl​exus is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about brachial plexus is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nerve to subclavius arises​ from superior trunk","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arterial supply to brachia​l plexus is from branches​ of common carotid arteri​es","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Radial nerve is the larges​t branch of the entire br​achial plexus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C4 contributes to brachial​ plexus occasionally","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Arterial supply to brachial plexus is from branches of common carotid arteries","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Arterial supply to brachial plexus is from branches of common carotid arteries</strong></p><p><strong>Arterial supply</strong> to <strong>brachial plexus</strong> is from branches of <strong>subclavian</strong> and <strong>vertebral</strong> arteries, and not from carotid arteries. </p>\n<p>The upper/<strong>superior trunk</strong> of the brachial plexus gives two <strong>branches</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Suprascapular</strong> nerve (C5, C6)</li>\n<li>Nerve to the <strong>subclavius </strong>(C5, C6)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Radial nerve</strong> (C5, C6, C7, C8, T1) is the <strong>largest</strong> branch of the brachial plexus. It originates as the <strong>terminal branch</strong> of the posterior cord of the plexus.</p>\n<p>Brachial plexus <strong>variations</strong> can be 2 types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>C4 pre-fixed brachial plexus </li>\n<li>T2 post-fixed brachial plexus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0536","text":"An infraclavicular block w​as given in a patient wit​h distal humerus fracture​ for open reduction and i‌nternal fixation. Which o​f the following branches ‌of brachial plexus would ​likely be anesthetized?","raw_text":"An infraclavicular block was given in a patient with distal humerus fracture for open reduction and internal fixation. Which of the following branches of brachial plexus would likely be anesthetized?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nerve to ​Subclavius","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dorsal sca​pular nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nerve to serratus anterior​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thoracodo​rsal nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Thoracodorsal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d95d11a5f5cb4cfc8d6f66be46b49279x1280x806.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Thoracodorsal nerve</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The <strong>thoracodorsal nerve</strong> would be anesthetized in the infraclavicular nerve block as it is an <strong>infraclavicular branch</strong> of the brachial plexus. It arises from the <strong>posterior cord</strong> (C6-C8).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Branches of the brachial plexus that arise from the <strong>roots</strong> and <strong>trunk</strong> are <strong>supraclavicular</strong> branches. They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)</li>\n<li>Long thoracic nerve of Bell (C5-C7)</li>\n<li>Suprascapular nerve (C5, C6)</li>\n<li>Nerve to Subclavius (C5, C6)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>All</strong> the <strong>other branches</strong> of the brachial plexus that arise from the <strong>cords</strong> are <strong>infraclavicular</strong> branches.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3687","text":"A neonate is being examine​d after delivery. The del​ivery was complicated by ​shoulder dystocia which l‌ed to the injury of the u​pper trunk of the brachia‌l plexus. Which of the fo​llowing deformities is le‍ast likely to be seen in ​this neonate?","raw_text":"A neonate is being examined after delivery. The delivery was complicated by shoulder dystocia which led to the injury of the upper trunk of the brachial plexus. Which of the following deformities is least likely to be seen in this neonate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extension at​ elbow joint","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pronation at radio-ulnar j​oint","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abduction and medial rotat​ion at shoulder joint","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sensory loss along the out​er aspect of the arm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Abduction and medial rotation at shoulder joint","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f53b94f0be6646d0a21ea44493e4cfe1x456x581.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Abduction and medial rotation at shoulder joint</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, <strong>upper cervical trunk injury</strong> causes Erb's or <strong>Erb-Duchene palsy</strong>. This palsy involves <strong>adduction</strong> and medial rotation at the shoulder, and not abduction. </p>\n<p>The most common cause of Erb's palsy is the undue separation of head and neck i.e. fall on shoulder and birth injuries.<strong> </strong>The stretching causes damage to the <strong>upper trunk</strong> (<strong>C5, C6</strong>). Hence, the affected nerves include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Suprascapular nerve</li>\n<li>Axillary nerve</li>\n<li>Musculocutaneous nerve</li>\n<li>Radial nerve</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Erb's palsy<strong> </strong>has a typical presentation of a <strong>policeman's tip</strong> or<strong> waiter's tip</strong> hand deformity. It includes the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Arm is adducted and medially rotated at the shoulder joint</li>\n<li>Elbow joint is extended</li>\n<li>Radioulnar joint is pronated </li>\n<li>Sensory loss along the outer aspect of the arm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given image below shows classic deformity seen in Erb's palsy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3686","text":"A medical student receives​ a booster dose of Hepati​tis B vaccination. A few ​hours later, she develops‌ weakness in the abductio​n and lateral rotation of‌ that arm. Which nerve is​ most likely to have been‍ injured?","raw_text":"A medical student receives a booster dose of Hepatitis B vaccination. A few hours later, she develops weakness in the abduction and lateral rotation of that arm. Which nerve is most likely to have been injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Surgeon​’s nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Labourer​’s nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nerve ​of Bell","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Musician​'s nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Surgeon’s nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d327b952dd3d42d48e8aa5ec2753be58x1280x2059.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Surgeon’s nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>axillary nerve</strong> or <strong>surgeon's nerve</strong> is frequently injured during inadvertent intramuscular (<strong>IM</strong>) <strong>injections.</strong> </p>\n<p>The <strong>course</strong> of axillary nerve:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a branch of the posterior cord of the brachial plexus in the axilla</li>\n<li>It exits the axilla through the quadrangular space to enter the arm</li>\n<li>In the arm, it winds around the surgical neck of the humerus and gives cutaneous supply to the lower part of the deltoid (upper lateral cutaneous nerve of the arm)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Injuries</strong> to the axillary nerve can be caused by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fracture of surgical neck of humerus</li>\n<li>Inferior dislocation of the humerus</li>\n<li>Inadvertent IM injection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Test for axillary nerve involves testing sensations of <strong>skin</strong> over the <strong>lower part </strong>of the <strong>deltoid</strong>. In the case of axillary nerve lesions, there is a <strong>loss of sensations</strong> over this region. It is called <strong>regimental badge anesthesia.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Named nerves of the upper limb are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Laborer’s nerve - median nerve</li>\n<li>Musician’s nerve - ulnar nerve</li>\n<li>Surgeon’s nerve - axillary nerve</li>\n<li>Nerve of Bell - long thoracic nerve</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0540","text":"Which of the following ner​ves is not directly relat​ed to humerus?","raw_text":"Which of the following nerves is not directly related to humerus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Axillar​y nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Median​ nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ulnar​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radial​ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Median nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/360f436e4a984f6d93d52ff3e80673c7x600x1322.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Median nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>median nerve</strong> is not directly related to the humerus.</p>\n<p> Nerves related to the<strong> humerus:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Axillary</strong> nerve is at the <strong>surgical neck</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Ulnar</strong> nerve is at the <strong>medial epicondyle</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Radial</strong> nerve is at the <strong>spiral groove</strong> of the shaft of the humerus. </li>\n</ul>\n<p> <strong>Median nerve course: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Origin: Lateral</strong> and<strong> medial cords </strong>of the brachial plexus </li>\n<li><strong>Axilla and arm: </strong>No muscle supply from the median nerve</li>\n<li><strong>Forearm (flexor</strong> compartment): It supplies most <strong>superficial flexors. </strong>It leaves the cubital fossa between the 2 heads of Pronator teres and<strong> </strong>gives off the <strong>anterior interosseous nerve</strong></li>\n<li>Before entering deep into the flexor retinaculum gives off a<strong> palmar cutaneous branch. </strong>At the distal end of the flexor retinaculum, it divides into a <strong>recurrent branch</strong> and<strong> palmar digital nerves</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0541","text":"Which of the following wil​l you test in patients wi​th median nerve injury at​ the wrist?","raw_text":"Which of the following will you test in patients with median nerve injury at the wrist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contraction of flexor poll​icis brevis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of sensation of the p​alm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Contraction of abductor po​llicis brevis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Loss of sensation on the r​ing finger","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Contraction of abductor pollicis brevis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/69526a82cd0c4671ab9f2c4d3cd069c2x1280x1442.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4a8a0fa0dbe046729f57315e6adb33bbx1280x1223.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Contraction of abductor pollicis brevis</strong></p><p>Injury to the <strong>median nerve</strong> at the <strong>wrist</strong> is tested by <strong>contraction</strong> of the <strong>abductor pollicis brevis. </strong>Loss of thumb mobility and opposition (abductor pollicis brevis) is tested by <strong>pen test. </strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Paralysis of <strong>flexor pollicis brevis</strong> can occur in median nerve <strong>injury</strong> at the <strong>arm</strong> and <strong>elbow.</strong> <strong>Benediction hand sign </strong>(attempt to make a fist gives pointed index and middle finger) and <strong>Ochsner's clasping test </strong>(attempt to clasp both hands show pointing index finger) are used to test such injuries.</p>\n<p>The images below show the Benediction hand sign and Ochsner's clasping test.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: The <strong>lateral 1/2</strong> of the <strong>palm</strong> is supplied by the <strong>palmar cutaneous branch</strong> of the median nerve. It is usually <strong>spared</strong> in median nerve injury to the wrist as it does not pass through the carpal tunnel. </p>\n<p>Option D: The loss of sensation over ring finger cannot be tested for median nerve injury as it supplies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lateral half of palmar aspect</li>\n<li>Medial half of distal 1/3 of dorsal aspect</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Out of three muscles in the thenar eminence only abductor pollicis brevis and opponens pollicis can be tested for isolated action. Flexor pollicis brevis cannot be tested for isolated action. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3692","text":"A 56-year-old man presente​d with a loss of sensatio​n over the hand as marked​ in the given image. Whic‌h of the following statem​ents is true regarding th‌e affected nerve?","raw_text":"A 56-year-old man presented with a loss of sensation over the hand as marked in the given image. Which of the following statements is true regarding the affected nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It innervates adductor pol​licis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It innervates medial half ​of flexor digitorum profu​ndus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ape thumb deformity can be​ seen irrespective of sit​e of lesion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Wrist drop can be seen if ​the lesion is above the e​lbow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ape thumb deformity can be seen irrespective of site of lesion","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d3fe740c630e42ec928d74a3f3d5bba4.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c709092baed040b3966467654fd4919fx1280x1111.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9126c70331194bc183536ac65fca759fx602x422.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ape thumb deformity can be seen irrespective of site of lesion</strong></p><p>The nerve supply of the region marked in the image is the <strong>median nerve</strong>. The <strong>ape-thumb</strong> deformity is seen in the median nerve lesion <strong>irrespective</strong> of the <strong>site</strong> of the <strong>lesion,</strong> as it is a defect of the end of the nerve. It is caused by <strong>non-use atrophy</strong> of the muscles of the <strong>thenar eminence</strong> (except adductor pollicis). </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Median nerve supply:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 391px;\" width=\"429\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 207.483px;\"><strong>Branch</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.552px;\"><strong>Muscles/ region</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 207.483px;\">Median nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.552px;\">\n<p>Superficial flexors (pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, flexor digitorum superficialis)</p>\n<p>Exceptions: flexor carpi ulnaris, medial half of flexor digitorum profundus</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 207.483px;\">Anterior interosseous nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.552px;\">\n<p>2 ½ muscles:</p>\n<p>Flexor pollicis longus, pronator quadratus, lateral half of FDP (medial half by ulnar nerve)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 207.483px;\">Recurrent branch</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.552px;\"><strong>Thenar</strong> muscles <strong>(except adductor pollicis)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 207.483px;\">Palmar cutaneous branch</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.552px;\">Lateral 2/3rd of palm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 207.483px;\">Palmar digital nerves</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.552px;\">\n<p>Lateral 2 lumbricals</p>\n<p>Skin over the lateral 3 ½ digits</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The sensory nerve supply to hand by median nerve is given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Adductor pollicis is innervated by the ulnar nerve.</p>\n<p>Option B: Median nerve innervates lateral half of flexor digitorum profundus. Ulnar nerve innervates medial half of flexor digitorum profundus.</p>\n<p>Option D: Wrist drop is seen in radial nerve palsy.</p>\n<p>Note: Thenar muscles include abductor pollicis, adductor pollicis, opponens pollicis, and flexor pollicis brevis. <strong>Adductor pollicis</strong> is called the <strong>graveyard of</strong> the<strong> ulnar nerve</strong> because after supplying all other muscles medially it ends laterally in adductor pollicis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0109","text":"Lesion of the structure ma​rked 'A' causes:","raw_text":"Lesion of the structure marked 'A' causes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loss of sensation of the n​ail of index finger","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypothenar muscles wasting​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Loss of sensation at the t​ip of radial tubercle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Loss of sensation of the 4​th interdigital cleft","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Loss of sensation of the nail of index finger","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bf8824e32fb84d73a0e6d295a20d898c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6fc6af3c40054271873617b25aae05a1x1280x1142.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bdfed687a45f4a2ca55d40da02f2d684x602x422.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Loss of sensation of the nail of index finger</strong></p><p>The structure marked 'A' in the diagram is <strong>median nerve</strong> in the cubital fossa. A lesion of the median nerve causes <strong>loss of sensation</strong> of the<strong> nail of </strong>the <strong>index finger.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Median nerve cutaneous supply:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lateral 2/3rd of the palm by superficial cutaneous branch</li>\n<li>Lateral 3½ fingers (palmar aspect and dorsal aspect of nailbeds) by palmar digital nerves</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Contents of cubital fossa: (From medial to lateral) - “MBBR”</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>M</strong>edian nerve</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong>rachial artery</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong>iceps brachii tendon</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>adial nerve</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0545","text":"Which of the following mus​cles are likely to be aff​ected in patients with ul​nar nerve injury at the e‌lbow?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles are likely to be affected in patients with ulnar nerve injury at the elbow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flexor digitorum superfici​alis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Palmaris lo​ngus muscle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pronato​r teres","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palmar i​nterossei","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Palmar interossei","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3b41f9045216439a9a42e6033028f5dfx1280x2371.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b28a2e4f1ec54271a84ca0fe1fa4e825x703x492.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Palmar interossei</strong></p><p>The <strong>ulnar nerve</strong> supplies the <strong>palmar interossei. </strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> course </strong>of the ulnar nerve:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A branch of the medial cord of the brachial plexus </li>\n<li>No muscle supply in the axilla and arm</li>\n<li>Up to midarm, it passes medial to the brachial artery</li>\n<li>After midarm, it inclines posteromedially to <strong>perforate </strong>the<strong> medial intermuscular septum</strong> and passes through a fibrous canal called <strong>arcade of Struthers</strong> (not to be confused with supinator arch or arcade of Frohse which is over radial nerve deep branch).</li>\n<li>In the <strong>upper forearm,</strong> it passes between the <strong>2 heads of FCU</strong> and gives off some branches</li>\n<li>In the <strong>distal forearm,</strong> it gives off a <strong>dorsal cutaneous branch </strong></li>\n<li>In the <strong>wrist,</strong> it passes superficial to flexor retinaculum but <strong>under</strong> a fascial band lateral to pisiform called <strong>Guyon's ulnar tunnel</strong></li>\n<li>Distal to pisiform it gives 2 terminal branches. One superficial terminal and another deep terminal</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Ulnar nerve supply:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 239px;\" width=\"382\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 29.7326px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 183px; height: 29.7326px;\"><strong>Branches</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 183px; height: 29.7326px;\"><strong>Muscles/ region</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 96px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 183px; height: 96px;\">Upper forearm branches</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183px; height: 96px;\">\n<p>Flexor carpi ulnaris</p>\n<p>Medial half of flexor digitorum profundus</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 183px; height: 54px;\">Dorsal cutaneous branch (distal forearm)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183px; height: 54px;\">Dorsum of medial 1/3 of hand and medial 1 1/2 fingers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 183px; height: 36px;\">Palmar cutaneous branch (near the wrist)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183px; height: 36px;\">Hypothenar eminence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 96px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 183px; height: 96px;\">Superficial terminal branch</td>\n<td style=\"width: 183px; height: 96px;\">\n<p>Palmaris brevis</p>\n<p>Palmar surface of medial 1 1/2 fingers</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 72px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 183px; height: 72px;\">Deep terminal branch </td>\n<td style=\"width: 183px; height: 72px;\">All intrinsic muscles of the hand (except first 2 lumbricals and thenar eminence)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The image given below shows the cutaneous innervation of the hand by the ulnar nerve.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9388","text":"A patient came with compla​ints of inability to move​ his 4th and 5th digit, c​annot hold a pen and he w‌as not able to hold a pap​er between fingers. Which‌ of the following sites g​iven below is the probabl‍e cause of injury to the ​nerve in question?","raw_text":"A patient came with complaints of inability to move his 4th and 5th digit, cannot hold a pen and he was not able to hold a paper between fingers. Which of the following sites given below is the probable cause of injury to the nerve in question?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"​D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. C","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/49925339e6fc47d8bae1fbac41054488.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/688511e3b2d44422b868a4d982e79576x1280x1359.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. C</strong></p><p>The given clinical situation suggests an<strong> ulnar nerve injury. </strong>The most likely site of injury would thus be <strong>medial epicondyle (marked as C)</strong></p>\n<p>Tests for ulnar nerve lesions are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Card test positive</li>\n<li>Claw hand deformity</li>\n<li>Froment's sign positive</li>\n<li>Wartenberg sign</li>\n<li>Palmaris brevis sign</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Positive card test</strong> is due to <strong>weakness</strong> of<strong> palmar interrosei</strong>, causing <strong>loss of adduction</strong>. As a result, the patient is unable to hold the card firmly between his or her fingers.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: It is the <strong>radial groove</strong> where <strong>radial nerve</strong> runs along with profunda brachii artery.</p>\n<p>Option D: It is the <strong>neck of humerus</strong> where <strong>axillary nerve</strong> runs around.</p>\n<p>Option B: It is the <strong>lateral epicondyle</strong>. Fracture of this part may lead to <strong>tardy ulnar nerve palsy</strong>, but incidence is less.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0546","text":"Which of the following is ​injured in a patient pres​enting with atrophy of hy​pothenar eminence and num‌bness in the palmar aspec​t of the little finger?","raw_text":"Which of the following is injured in a patient presenting with atrophy of hypothenar eminence and numbness in the palmar aspect of the little finger?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deep branch of ulnar nerve​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ulnar nerve before it’s di​vision into superficial a​nd deep branch","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Palmar cutaneous branch of​ ulnar nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superficial terminal branc​h of ulnar nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ulnar nerve before it’s division into superficial and deep branch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea1d56353cbb46e7817fb678e9c6eca8x1280x2058.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7626a36cdb3f466380ec412c2045d02dx1280x2371.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ulnar nerve before it’s division into superficial and deep branch</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of an <strong>injury</strong> to the <strong>ulnar nerve</strong> lesion <strong>before its division</strong> into a superficial and deep branch, as the function of both branches is affected.</p>\n<p>The <strong>ulnar nerve</strong> after passing distal to pisiform bone gives 2 <strong>terminal branches</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Superficial</strong> branch which supplies\n<ul>\n<li>Palmaris brevis</li>\n<li>Skin on the palmar surface of medial 1½ fingers</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Deep</strong> branch runs within the concavity of deep palmar arch. It supplies all intrinsic muscles of the hand (except first 2 lumbricals and thenar muscles)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Injury</strong> to the <strong>ulnar nerve</strong>:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 790.508px; width: 476px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 46.9922px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 82.3611px; height: 46.9922px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Site of injury</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.889px; height: 46.9922px; text-align: center;\"><strong>High lesion <br/>(Elbow) </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.469px; height: 46.9922px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Low lesion <br/>(Wrist) </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 259.043px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 82.3611px; height: 259.043px;\"><strong>Causes</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.889px; height: 259.043px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">\n<p>Fracture/dislocation of the <strong>medial epicondyle</strong></p>\n<p>Cubital tunnel syndrome</p>\n<p>Compression between the two heads of FCU</p>\n<p>Tardy ulnar nerve palsy (Valgus deformity)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.469px; height: 259.043px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">\n<p>Wrist laceration/ slashing</p>\n<p><strong>Guyon's canal</strong> syndrome</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 409.336px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 82.3611px; height: 409.336px;\"><strong>Motor loss</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.889px; height: 409.336px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">\n<p><strong>Claw-hand</strong> deformity</p>\n<p>Hypothenar &amp; interosseous wasting</p>\n<p>Loss of:</p>\n<p>• Abduction &amp; adduction of fingers <br/>(<strong>Card test</strong> - Positive) <br/>• Adduction of thumb <br/>(<strong>Froment sign-</strong> Positive) <br/>• Flexion of MCP joints</p>\n<p>Weakness of wrist flexion (radial deviation)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.469px; height: 409.336px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">\n<p><strong>Claw-hand</strong> deformity <br/><strong>(more pronounced)</strong></p>\n<p>Hypothenar &amp; interosseous wasting</p>\n<p>Loss of:</p>\n<p>• Abduction &amp; adduction of fingers <br/>(<strong>Card test</strong> - Positive) <br/>• Adduction of thumb <br/>(<strong>Froment sign-</strong> Positive)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 75.137px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 82.3611px; height: 75.137px;\"><strong>Sensory loss</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.889px; height: 75.137px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">\n<p>Over dorso-ulnar aspect of the palm</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.469px; height: 75.137px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">\n<p>Over palmar aspect of the <strong>medial 1 <sup>1</sup>/<sub>2</sub> fingers</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Given below is the Froment test used to test adductor pollicis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Given below is the course of the ulnar nerve.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Wartenberg's sign is used to assess the weakness of palmar interossei.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0547","text":"A patient is unable to hol​d a piece of paper betwee​n his thumb and index fin​ger firmly following a cu‌t wound over the hypothen​ar eminence. Which of the‌ following nerves is like​ly to be damaged?","raw_text":"A patient is unable to hold a piece of paper between his thumb and index finger firmly following a cut wound over the hypothenar eminence. Which of the following nerves is likely to be damaged?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superficial terminal branc​h of ulnar nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deep branch of ulnar nerve​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Deep branch of radial nerv​e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deep branch of median nerv​e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Deep branch of ulnar nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5484537c4e174f03b3ecf6b329cb88f9x1280x2371.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Deep branch of ulnar nerve</strong></p><p><strong>Loss of adduction </strong>points toward the<strong> deep ulnar nerve injury.</strong> The hypothenar injury could have caused downstream loss of innervation of the deep ulnar nerve which innervates adductor pollicis in thenar eminence.</p>\n<p>The<strong> course</strong> of deep ulnar nerve:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>At its <strong>origin,</strong> it innervates the <strong>hypothenar muscles.</strong></li>\n<li>As it <strong>crosses</strong> the deep part of the hand, it innervates all the <strong>interosseous muscles</strong> and the third and fourth<strong> lumbricals.</strong></li>\n<li>It ends by innervating the <strong>adductor pollicis </strong>and <strong>t</strong>he medial (deep) head of the <strong>flexor pollicis brevis</strong>. </li>\n<li>It also sends articular filaments to the <strong>wrist joint</strong> following Hilton's law.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Given below is the course of the ulnar nerve.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0548","text":"A 25-year-old man presente​d with displaced fracture​ of distal humerus. Which​ of the following signs w‌ould indicate that the ul​nar nerve is injured at t‌his site?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man presented with displaced fracture of distal humerus. Which of the following signs would indicate that the ulnar nerve is injured at this site?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Total c​law hand","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paralysis of all interosse​i","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Complete paralysis of flex​or digitorum profundus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paralysis of all lumbrical​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Paralysis of all interossei","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Paralysis of all interossei</strong></p><p><strong>Paralysis</strong> of all <strong>interossei</strong> is seen in <strong>injury</strong> to the <strong>ulnar nerve</strong> in the <strong>elbow</strong>.</p>\n<p>All interossei (dorsal and palmar) are supplied by ulnar nerve.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>D</strong>orsal Interossei - <strong>Ab</strong>duction (<strong>DAB</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>almar Interossei - <strong>Ad</strong>duction (<strong>PAD</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>Median nerve and ulnar nerve injury lead to a total claw hand.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0550","text":"Which of the following dig​its can be used to test r​adial nerve, ulnar nerve,​ and median nerve?","raw_text":"Which of the following digits can be used to test radial nerve, ulnar nerve, and median nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ring ​finger","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Index ​finger","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Little​ finger","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Th​umb","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Thumb","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Thumb</strong></p><p>The radial nerve, ulnar nerve, and median nerve can be tested using the <strong>thumb.</strong></p>\n<p>The muscles of thumb that are affected in the different nerve injuries are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 328px; width: 505px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129px;\"><strong>Nerve injury</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 179.878px;\"><strong>Thumb muscle affected</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.122px;\"><strong>Thumb movement lost</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129px;\">Median nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179.878px;\">Opponens pollicis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.122px;\">Thumb opposition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129px;\">Ulnar nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179.878px;\">\n<p>Adductor pollicis</p>\n<p>Palmar interossei</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.122px;\">Adduction of thumb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129px;\">Radial nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 179.878px;\">\n<p>Abductor pollicis longus (APL)</p>\n<p>Extensor pollicis brevis (EPB)</p>\n<p>Extensor pollicis longus (EPL)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.122px;\">Thumb extension </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0551","text":"A 30-year-old woman presen​ts with paresthesias in t​he lower lateral part of ​the arm. Branch of which ‌of the following nerve is​ affected here?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman presents with paresthesias in the lower lateral part of the arm. Branch of which of the following nerve is affected here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Axillar​y nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Radial​ nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Median​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ulnar​ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Radial nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1ce14f51c8664581abe340f23155e6aex1280x2971.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Radial nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>lower lateral part</strong> of the <strong>arm</strong> is innervated by the lower <strong>lateral cutaneous nerve</strong> of the arm. It is one of the 5 branches of the <strong>radial nerve</strong> in the spiral groove.</p>\n<p>The<strong> course</strong> of the radial nerve:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the largest branch of the brachial plexus (C5, 6, 7,8, T1).</li>\n<li>It leaves the axilla by passing through the<strong> </strong>lower triangular space.</li>\n<li>It enters the arm and runs along the radial groove with profunda brachii artery. It gives the following branches:\n<ul>\n<li>Lower lateral cutaneous nerve of arm</li>\n<li>Posterior cutaneous nerve of forearm</li>\n<li>Branch to lateral head of triceps</li>\n<li>Branch to medial head of triceps</li>\n<li>Branch to anconeus</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>In the forearm, it<strong> </strong>divides in to 2 branches <strong>superficial (sensory)</strong> and <strong>deep (motor)</strong> branch at the cubital fossa.</li>\n<li>Superficial branch crosses the tendon of brachioradialis and goes posterior to supply dorsum of the hand.</li>\n<li>Deep branch/ posterior interroseous nerve pierces the supinator and supplies the extensors in the forearm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0552","text":"A patient with radial head​ fracture presents with a​n inability to extend the​ metacarpophalangeal join‌t. On examination, wrist ​extension is normal with ‌no sensory impairment. Wh​ich of the following is t‍he probable cause?","raw_text":"A patient with radial head fracture presents with an inability to extend the metacarpophalangeal joint. On examination, wrist extension is normal with no sensory impairment. Which of the following is the probable cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Injury to the radial nerve​ just below the spiral gr​oove","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Injury to the anterior int​erosseous nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Injury to the posterior in​terosseous nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Injury to the common exten​sor origin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Injury to the posterior interosseous nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4ccccee3be5d4c20876d38b1e5a4abf2x1280x2062.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Injury to the posterior interosseous nerve</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, there is a likely <strong>injury</strong> to the <strong>posterior interosseous nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is prone to be injured in <strong>radial head fractures</strong> as the nerve winds around the radial neck to go into the posterior compartment of the forearm. There is <strong>no cutaneous sensory loss</strong> in lesions confined to the posterior interosseous nerve.</p>\n<p>The<strong> radial nerve </strong>divides into 2 branches, namely <strong>superficial (sensory)</strong> and <strong>deep (motor)</strong> branches at the cubital fossa. </p>\n<p>Deep radial nerve after supplying extensor carpi radialis brevis (ECRB) and supinator, continues as posterior interosseous nerve (<strong>PIN</strong>). From the posterior compartment, the <strong>supinator</strong> muscle wraps around the upper 1/3rd of the radius to reach the anterior compartment. </p>\n<p>PIN arches and winds <strong>around </strong>the<strong> radial neck</strong> along with the supinator muscle, into the posterior compartment of the forearm. Hence, it is in close relation with the radial head and neck, and the supinator arch or <strong>a</strong><strong>rcade of Froese</strong>. </p>\n<p>There is no cutaneous innervation by the posterior interosseous nerve.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0555","text":"A 40-year-old man has a C6​, C7 nerve root injury du​e to disc herniation. Sen​sations of which part of ‌the upper limb will be lo​st?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man has a C6, C7 nerve root injury due to disc herniation. Sensations of which part of the upper limb will be lost?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thumb and i​ndex finger","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Little​ finger","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Upper medial​ part of arm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Upper medial side of forea​rm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thumb and index finger","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thumb and index finger</strong></p><p><strong>Sensations</strong> over the <strong>thumb</strong> and <strong>index finger</strong> are lost in <strong>C6, C7 nerve root</strong> injuries.</p>\n<p>The palmar pad of the thumb is innervated by C6 and the pad of the index finger is innervated by C7.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0557","text":"You are testing the deep t​endon reflexes in a patie​nt with Guillan-Barre syn​drome. Which spinal segme‌nt is tested predominantl​y by a tap on the triceps‌ tendon?","raw_text":"You are testing the deep tendon reflexes in a patient with Guillan-Barre syndrome. Which spinal segment is tested predominantly by a tap on the triceps tendon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C​6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C​7","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"C​5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C​8","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. C7","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. C7</strong></p><p>A tap on the<strong> tendon </strong>of the<strong> triceps</strong> posterior to the elbow <strong>mainly</strong> tests for<strong> C7.</strong></p>\n<p>A tap on the <strong>tendon</strong> of the <strong>biceps</strong> in the cubital fossa tests <strong>mainly</strong> for spinal cord level <strong>C6</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3689","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding the rad​ial nerve is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding the radial nerve is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It leaves the axilla by pa​ssing through the quadran​gular space","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral head of triceps is​ paralyzed in a lesion of​ radial nerve in lower sp​iral groove","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anconeus is supplied by a ​branch arising in the axi​lla","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"In the arm it travels alon​g the brachial artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lateral head of triceps is paralyzed in a lesion of radial nerve in lower spiral groove","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e23efcdf892c4e4fbfe2029bc58b4467x1280x2971.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lateral head of triceps is paralyzed in a lesion of radial nerve in lower spiral groove</strong></p><p>The <strong>lateral head</strong> of the <strong>triceps</strong> is paralyzed in a <strong>lesion</strong> of the <strong>radial nerve</strong> in the <strong>lower spiral groove.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Spiral groove injuries</strong> could be upper or lower (proximal or distal). The following muscles are affected:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lateral head</strong> of triceps - as branching happens in the <strong>lower</strong> spiral groove</li>\n<li><strong>Anconeus</strong> - as branching happens from the <strong>upper</strong> spiral groove</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Radial nerve branches from the posterior cord of the brachial plexus. It leaves the axilla by passing through the<strong> lower triangular space.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Anconeus</strong> is supplied by a branch arising in the <strong>spiral groove</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: In the arm, it<strong> </strong>runs along the radial groove with the <strong>profunda brachii artery</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3694","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g carpal tunnel syndrome?​","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding carpal tunnel syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumatoid arthritis is a ​cause","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pain and paresthesia are w​orse at night time","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paresthesia over the skin ​of thenar eminence is pro​minent","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"No artery passes through t​he tunnel","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Paresthesia over the skin of thenar eminence is prominent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1fa85d4d917840789b7b2782e2acd8e8x1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Paresthesia over the skin of thenar eminence is prominent</strong></p><p>Paresthesia over the skin of thenar eminence is not seen in carpal tunnel syndrome. As the <strong>median nerve</strong> gives off the <strong>palmar cutaneous</strong> branch, that supplies the thenar eminence <strong>before</strong> it <strong>enters</strong> the <strong>carpal tunnel</strong>.</p>\n<p>Carpal tunnel syndrome is due to <strong>compression</strong> of the <strong>median nerve</strong> due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reduced size of the osseofibrous carpal tunnel</li>\n<li>Inflammation or thickening of the synovial sheaths of the flexors tendons (tenosynovitis) due to stress injury (eg., occupational)</li>\n<li>Inflammation of the flexor retinaculum</li>\n<li>Arthritic changes in the carpal bones (particularly rheumatoid arthritis)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The pain and paresthesia are <strong>worse at night</strong> and on gripping objects.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures</strong> passing through the <strong>carpal tunnel</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tendons of <strong>FDS </strong>(flexor digitorum superficialis),<strong> FDP </strong>(flexor digitorum profundus), <strong>FPL</strong> (flexor pollicis long)</li>\n<li>Median nerve</li>\n</ul>\n<p>No artery passes through the carpal tunnel.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3700","text":"Which of the following str​ucture is not likely to b​e injured in a wrist slas​h injury over the radial ‌side of the left wrist?","raw_text":"Which of the following structure is not likely to be injured in a wrist slash injury over the radial side of the left wrist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radial​ artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Radial​ nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Flexor car​pi radialis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palmaris lo​ngus tendon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Radial nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4f1a3d639b314b02a0a5f19cb1a23ec0x1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Radial nerve</strong></p><p>The median nerve is likely to be injured in case of deep laceration of the radial side of the wrist, whereas the radial nerve is least likely to be injured.</p>\n<p><strong>Wrist slash injuries:</strong></p>\n<p>A deep laceration on the <strong>radial side</strong> of the wrist may injure the following structures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Radial artery</li>\n<li><strong>Median nerve</strong></li>\n<li>Flexor carpi radialis tendon</li>\n<li>Palmaris longus tendon</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A deep laceration on the <strong>ulnar side</strong> of the wrist may injure the following structures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ulnar artery</li>\n<li><strong>Ulnar nerve</strong></li>\n<li>Flexor carpi ulnaris tendon</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3706","text":"Which of the following for​ms the roof of the struct​ure marked in the given i​mage?","raw_text":"Which of the following forms the roof of the structure marked in the given image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pisiform bone, flexor carp​i ulnaris and abductor di​giti minimi","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flexor retinaculum and pis​ohamate ligaments","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hook of hamate, extrinsic ​flexor tendons, the trans​verse carpal ligaments","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palmar carpal ligament and​ palmaris brevis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Palmar carpal ligament and palmaris brevis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/57dbed7ff09a485682e401580c744af2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/51bc40ec70fc4c65bd89cc38e568a328x600x556.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Palmar carpal ligament and palmaris brevis</strong></p><p>The structure marked in the image is the <strong>Guyon's canal.</strong> The<strong> roof</strong> of Guyon's canal is formed by the <strong>palmar carpal ligament</strong> and <strong>palmaris brevis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Guyon's canal is a 4 cm long canal formed by splitting of the flexor retinaculum along the anteromedial side of the wrist.</p>\n<p><strong>Boundaries</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ulnar/Medial side - Pisiform bone, flexor carpi ulnaris, and abductor digiti minimi</li>\n<li>Lateral - Hook of hamate, extrinsic flexor tendons, and the transverse carpal ligaments</li>\n<li>Floor - Flexor retinaculum and pisohamate ligaments</li>\n<li>Roof - Palmar carpal ligament and palmaris brevis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contents</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ulnar nerve</li>\n<li>Ulnar artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3719","text":"A 13-year-old boy presents​ with injury to the media​l epicondyle of the humer​us. Which of the followin‌g is least likely to be s​een?","raw_text":"A 13-year-old boy presents with injury to the medial epicondyle of the humerus. Which of the following is least likely to be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Weakness of the ulnar devi​ation and flexion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Complete paralysis of the ​IIIrd and IVth digits","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Atrophy of the hypothenar ​eminence","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased sensation of the​ hypothenar eminence","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Complete paralysis of the IIIrd and IVth digits","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Complete paralysis of the IIIrd and IVth digits</strong></p><p>Injury to the <strong>medial epicondyle</strong> injures the <strong>ulnar nerve</strong>. Since flexor digitorum superficialis (supplied by median nerve) and extensor digitorum (supplied by the radial nerve) are still functional, complete paralysis of the third and fourth digits is not seen. </p>\n<p>Injury to the <strong>medial epicondyle</strong> damages the<strong> ulnar nerve</strong>. This causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Paralysis of:\n<ul>\n<li>Flexor carpi ulnaris (<strong>FCU</strong>) - loss of ulnar deviation </li>\n<li>Medial half of flexor digitorum profundus (FDP) - loss of distal interphalangeal joint flexion</li>\n<li><strong>Hypothenar</strong> paralysis - lead to atrophy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Sensory loss over medial 1/3 of hand and medial 1 1/2 fingers</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3725","text":"You are suspecting injury ​to the long thoracic nerv​e in a patient who underw​ent radical mastectomy. W‌hich of the following tas​ks will you ask the patie‌nt to perform to test the​ integrity of the nerve?","raw_text":"You are suspecting injury to the long thoracic nerve in a patient who underwent radical mastectomy. Which of the following tasks will you ask the patient to perform to test the integrity of the nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shrug the​ shoulders","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Raise the arm above the he​ad on the affected side","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Touch the opposite shoulde​r","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lift a heavy object from g​round","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Raise the arm above the head on the affected side","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Raise the arm above the head on the affected side</strong></p><p><strong>Injury</strong> to the <strong>long thoracic nerve</strong> during radical mastectomy paralyzes the <strong>serratus anterior</strong> muscle. This can be tested by <strong>raising </strong>the<strong> arm above </strong>the<strong> head </strong>on the affected side.</p>\n<p><strong>Shrugging</strong> of the <strong>shoulders</strong> is mainly carried out by the <strong>trapezius</strong>, which is supplied by the<strong> spinal accessory nerve</strong>. This muscle works with the serratus anterior for overhead abduction.</p>\n<p><strong>Pectoralis major</strong>, supplied mainly by medial and lateral pectoral nerves, causes flexion at the shoulder joint. It is involved in <strong>touching</strong> the <strong>opposite</strong> <strong>shoulder</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3727","text":"Till which point does the ​anterior axial line exten​d?","raw_text":"Till which point does the anterior axial line extend?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shou​lder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"El​bow","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wr​ist","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Knu​ckle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Wrist","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7830b8b2408d41b08494dd8a84500ff8x1280x2558.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a74437aefa034eefb7fd738db4fd60cax1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Wrist</strong></p><p>The<strong> anterior axial line</strong> extends till the <strong>wrist </strong>joint.</p>\n<p>The axial line is the junction between two dermatomes supplied by discontinuous spinal nerves.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>anterior axial line</strong> starts from the <strong>sternal angle</strong> (second rib) and extends to the <strong>wrist joint</strong> level.</li>\n<li>The posterior axial line begins at the shoulder and reaches the elbow joint level.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The images given below show the dermatomes of the upper limb with a dark green line indicating the axial line. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3728","text":"What is the deformity seen​ in patients with 'main e​n griffe'?","raw_text":"What is the deformity seen in patients with 'main en griffe'?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extension at metacarpophal​engeal joint and flexion ​at interphalengeal joint","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Extension at both metacarp​ophalengeal and interphal​engeal joints","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Flexion at both metacarpop​halengeal and interphalen​geal joints","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flexion at metacarphophale​ngeal joint and extension​ at interphalengeal joint​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Extension at metacarpophalengeal joint and flexion at interphalengeal joint","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db2b969fbcb94942bcad483f07f3b215x720x583.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1472395e5c254c058ba7ea4701ef0a34x1279x1225.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Extension at metacarpophalengeal joint and flexion at interphalengeal joint</strong></p><p><strong>Extension</strong> at the <strong>metacarpophalangeal</strong> joint and <strong>flexion</strong> at <strong>interphalangeal</strong> joints due to <strong>ulnar</strong> nerve injury produces <strong>claw hand</strong> (<strong>main en griffe</strong>).</p>\n<p>The <strong>ulnar nerve</strong> supplies <strong>medial</strong> two <strong>lumbricals</strong> in the hand. The lumbricals have a combined action on metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joint flexion and interphalangeal (IP) joint extension (<strong>glass holding posture</strong>).</p>\n<p>In ulnar nerve injury, since the medial lumbricals are not functional, the forearm muscles are unopposed. <strong>MCP</strong> joint goes into <strong>hyperextension</strong> (unopposed activity of extensor digitorum, posterior forearm) and <strong>IP</strong> joints go into <strong>flexion</strong> (unopposed activity of flexor digitorum muscles, anterior forearm) and they bring about the claw hand deformity. </p>\n<p>The image given below shows the ulnar claw hand (main en griffe). Note the hyperextension at MCP joints and flexion at IP joints.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7429","text":"Nerve supply of the area m​arked by arrow in the ima​ge is:","raw_text":"Nerve supply of the area marked by arrow in the image is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radial​ nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior interosseus nerv​e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Median​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ulnar​ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Radial nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ffe36e9dace747a5811db772749db9cf.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Radial nerve</strong></p><p>The area marked in the image is supplied by <strong>radial nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0105","text":"True regarding the lesion ​of the structure marked b​y the arrow is:","raw_text":"True regarding the lesion of the structure marked by the arrow  is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ape thumb​ deformity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Claw​ hand","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Claudi​cation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wrist​ drop","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Wrist drop","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/94b8cc5b1f514abaaf63d2d2ffa20daa.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/36ee2aea014e45c29cb91aad5206cef6x1280x1142.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c8b6fb3ab5a54b02b7a73e2d5ee84320x1280x2971.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Wrist drop</strong></p><p>The structure marked in the image is the <strong>radial nerve</strong> demonstrated in the <strong>cubital fossa</strong>. A lesion of the radial nerve leads to a <strong>wrist drop.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Ape thumb </strong>deformity is seen in a lesion of the <strong>median nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Claw hand </strong>deformity is seen in a lesion of the <strong>ulnar nerve</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6693","text":"A patient is unable to abd​uct his arm. Which of the​ following root values is​ likely to be affected?","raw_text":"A patient is unable to abduct his arm. Which of the following root values is likely to be affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C​4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C​5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"C​6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C​7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. C5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. C5</strong></p><p>The<strong> inability</strong> to<strong> abduct</strong> the arm is most likely due to a lesion of the <strong>C5 root.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>deltoid </strong>muscle is the main muscle involved in the abduction of the arm. It is innervated by the C5 root of the <strong>axillary nerve. </strong>So, a lesion involving the C5 causes inability to abduct the arm.</p>\n<p>The table below shows the movements and muscles tested to determine the location of the lesion in the upper limb.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 432.555px; height: 417px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\"><strong>Arm movement</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\"><strong>Muscle</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\"><strong>Root</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.555px;\"><strong>Nerve</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">Shoulder abduction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Deltoid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">C5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.555px;\">Axillary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">Elbow extension</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Triceps</td>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">C7</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.555px;\">Radial</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">\n<p>Elbow flexion</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">\n<p>Biceps</p>\n<p>Brachioradialis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">\n<p>C5/6</p>\n<p>C6</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.555px;\">\n<p>Musculocutaneous</p>\n<p>Radial</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">Radial wrist extensor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Extensor carpi radialis longus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">C6</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.555px;\">Radial</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">Finger extensors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Extensor digitorum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">C7</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.555px;\">Posterior interosseus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">Finger flexors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1066\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Flexor pollicis longus and flexor digitorum profundus, index</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1066\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Flexor digitorum profundus, ring and little</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">\n<p>C8</p>\n<p>C8</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.555px;\">Anterior interosseous\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1066\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Ulnar</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">Finger abduction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">\n<p>First dorsal interosseus</p>\n<p>Abductor pollicis brevis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">\n<p>T1</p>\n<p>T1 </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.555px;\">\n<p>Ulnar </p>\n<p>Median</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"0hbo48FK","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"38_environmental_meteorology","title":"38.Environmental Meteorology","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB7539","text":"The water vapor and pollut‌ants tend to get trapped ​in the lowermost layers o‍f the atmosphere in winte​rs. What is the reason be​hind this occurrence?","raw_text":"The water vapor and pollutants tend to get trapped in the lowermost layers of the atmosphere in winters. What is the reason behind this occurrence?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Green hou‌se effect","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ocean ‌effect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Precip‌itation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Temperatur‌e inversion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Temperature inversion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Temperature inversion</strong></p><p>In winters, water vapor and pollutants get trapped in the lowermost layers of the atmosphere due to <strong>temperature inversion.</strong></p>\n<p>This results in the formation of <strong>smog,</strong> especially in the <strong>winter months,</strong> and this is a possible <strong>threat to human health</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7543","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an indicator of air p​ollution?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an indicator of air pollution?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Soilin‌g index","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heavy metal pollution inde‌x","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Suspended pa‌rticle matter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sulphur‌ dioxide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Heavy metal pollution index","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Heavy metal pollution index</strong></p><p>The heavy metal pollution index (<strong>HPI</strong>) is not an indicator of air pollution. It is a rating method and an effective tool to assess the <strong>water quality</strong> with respect to heavy metals.</p>\n<p><strong>Indicators</strong> of air pollution:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sulfur dioxide - best indicator </li>\n<li>Smoke or soiling index</li>\n<li>Grit and dust measurement</li>\n<li>The co-efficient of haze </li>\n<li>Air pollution index </li>\n<li>Suspended particle matter (SPM) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7545","text":"Which agency monitors air ‌quality in India?","raw_text":"Which agency monitors air quality in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ministry of Earth Sciences‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Indian Meteorological Depa‌rtment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Central Power Research Ins‌titute","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Central Pollution Control ‌Board","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Central Pollution Control Board","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Central Pollution Control Board</strong></p><p><strong>Central Pollution Control Board</strong> is the main body that monitors <strong>air quality</strong> in India and sets standards.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7549","text":"Greenhouse gases include a‌ll of the following excep​t:","raw_text":"Greenhouse gases include all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbon ‌monoxide","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Carbon ‌dioxide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Water‌ vapor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Met‌hane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Carbon monoxide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Carbon monoxide</strong></p><p>Carbon monoxide is not a <strong>greenhouse gas</strong>. </p>\n<p>Greenhouse gases include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Water vapor</li>\n<li>Carbon dioxide</li>\n<li>Methane</li>\n<li>Tropospheric ozone</li>\n<li>Nitrous oxide</li>\n<li>Chlorofluorocarbons (non-natural)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7546","text":"Which of the following pro‌tocols set the standard t​o reduce the emission of ‍greenhouse gases internat​ionally?","raw_text":"Which of the following protocols set the standard to reduce the emission of greenhouse gases internationally?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tokyo P‌rotocol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Beijing ‌Protocol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kyoto P‌rotocol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Montreal‌ Protocol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Kyoto Protocol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Kyoto Protocol</strong></p><p>The <strong>Kyoto Protocol </strong>was an internal agreement to set <strong>internationally</strong> binding <strong>emission reduction</strong> targets.</p>\n<p>It is linked to the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change.<strong> </strong>The parties committed to reduce GHGs by <strong>18% </strong>in the period between<strong> 2013-2020</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7547","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about global warming​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about global warming?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbon dioxide is the majo‌r greenhouse gas","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Stratospheric ozone is har‌mful","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CFC increases ozone levels‌ in the stratosphere","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kyoto Protocol called for ‌10% reduction in greenhou​se emissions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Carbon dioxide is the major greenhouse gas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Carbon dioxide is the major greenhouse gas</strong></p><p><strong>Carbon dioxide</strong> is the major <strong>greenhouse </strong>gas.</p>\n<p>Tropospheric ozone and chlorofluorocarbons are also important greenhouse gases. <strong>Water vapor</strong> is the most <strong>abundant greenhouse</strong> gas, but the water vapor produced directly by human activity contributes very little to the amount of water vapor in the atmosphere. Hence, its emissions have not been estimated.</p>\n<p>Greenhouse gases lead to <strong>global</strong> <strong>warming</strong>, which is the major cause of <strong>climate change.</strong> Climate change accelerates species extinction on earth.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7540","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect regarding indoo​r air pollutants?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding indoor air pollutants?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbon monoxide is produce‌d by fuel combustion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pesticides are indoor air ‌pollutants","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radon is an indoor air pol‌lutant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mercury vapor is a source ‌of indoor air pollution","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mercury vapor is a source of indoor air pollution","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mercury vapor is a source of indoor air pollution</strong></p><p>Mercury is not a source of <strong>indoor air pollution</strong>.</p>\n<p>Indoor air pollutants commonly arise from coal or <strong>fuel combustion</strong>. They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Carbon monoxide</strong></li>\n<li>Polyaromatic hydrocarbons</li>\n<li>Nitrogen</li>\n<li>Sulfur oxides</li>\n<li><strong>Pesticides</strong></li>\n<li>Asbestos</li>\n<li>Lead</li>\n<li><strong>Radon</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7541","text":"Which of the following is ‌unlikely to be seen in in​dividuals affected by ind‍oor air pollution?","raw_text":"Which of the following is unlikely to be seen in individuals affected by indoor air pollution?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic lu‌ng disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pregnancy‌ problems","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neuro-developmental disord‌ers","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Childhood‌ pneumonia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neuro-developmental disorders","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neuro-developmental disorders</strong></p><p><strong>Indoor air pollution</strong> has not been linked with neurodevelopmental disorders. It can result in a higher incidence of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pregnancy complications</li>\n<li>Chronic lung disease</li>\n<li>Childhood pneumonia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0845","text":"The best indicators for ro‌utine monitoring of air p​ollution are:","raw_text":"The best indicators for routine monitoring of air pollution are:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sulfur dioxide, smoke, and‌ suspended particles","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sulfur dioxide, lead, and ‌particulate matter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sulfur dioxide and carbon ‌monoxide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carbon monoxide and hydrog‌en sulfide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sulfur dioxide, smoke, and suspended particles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sulfur dioxide, smoke, and suspended particles</strong></p><p>The best <strong>indicators</strong> for routine monitoring of <strong>air pollution</strong> are <strong>sulfur dioxide, smoke,</strong> and<strong> suspended particles.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7472","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an index of thermal c​omfort?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an index of thermal comfort?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Air temperature and humidi‌ty","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corrected effective temper‌ature","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coolin‌g power","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Heat transfe‌r coefficient","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Heat transfer coefficient","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Heat transfer coefficient</strong></p><p>The heat transfer coefficient is a measure of the heat transfer by convection or phase transition. It is not an index of thermal comfort.</p>\n<p><strong>Thermal comfort</strong> is the <strong>subjective feeling</strong> of <strong>temperature</strong> in an environment. Optimum levels of thermal comfort help to <strong>increase productivity</strong>. Indices of thermal comfort include the following:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 183px; width: 457px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209.25px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Index</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.75px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Application</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209.25px;\">Air temperature</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.75px;\">Considered an inadequate indicator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209.25px;\">Air temperature and humidity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.75px;\">Considered an inadequate indicator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209.25px;\">\n<p><strong>Cooling power</strong> - it measures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Air temperature</li>\n<li>Humidity<strong> </strong></li>\n<li>Air movement</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.75px;\">\n<p>Measured using a <strong>kata thermometer</strong> (dry and wet)</p>\n<p>Regarded as unreliable</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209.25px;\">\n<p><strong>Effective temperature</strong> - it measures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Air temperature</li>\n<li>Humidity<strong> </strong></li>\n<li>Air movement</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.75px;\">\n<p>It ignores the effects of radiation from surrounding structures</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209.25px;\">\n<p><strong>Corrected effective temperature</strong> - it measures: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Air temperature</li>\n<li>Humidity<strong> </strong></li>\n<li>Air movement</li>\n<li>Mean radiant heat</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.75px;\">\n<p>It is measured using a<strong> globe thermometer</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 209.25px;\"><strong>McArdle’s</strong> maximum allowable sweat rate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.75px;\">It involves calculating the 4-hour sweat rate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7474","text":"What are the instruments u‌sed to measure atmospheri​c pressure called?","raw_text":"What are the instruments used to measure atmospheric pressure called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Barom‌eters","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anemo‌meters","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hygro‌meters","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Psychr‌ometers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Barometers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Barometers</strong></p><p><strong>Atmospheric</strong> pressure or <strong>barometric</strong> <strong>pressure</strong> is measured using <strong>barometers</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most commonly known barometers are <strong>Fortin’s barometer</strong>, Kew pattern station barometer, and the <strong>barograph</strong>. The <strong>Indian Meteorological</strong> Department uses the <strong>Kew pattern station barometer</strong>.</p>\n<p>The pressure at Earth’s surface, close to sea level averages around <strong>760 mmHg,</strong> and man is adapted to live around this.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7473","text":"What sweat excretion rate ‌in acclimatized, healthy ​young men is indicative o‍f intolerable air tempera​ture?","raw_text":"What sweat excretion rate in acclimatized, healthy young men is indicative of intolerable air temperature?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt; 4 lit‌res/4 hours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt; 4 lit‌res/6 hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt; 4.5 li‌tres/4 hours","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt; 4.5 li‌tres/8 hours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. > 4.5 litres/4 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. &gt; 4.5 litres/4 hours</strong></p><p>A sweat excretion rate of <strong>&gt; 4.5 L /4 hours</strong> in acclimatized, healthy young men is indicative of intolerable air temperature. This is known as <strong>McArdle’s maximum allowable sweat rate</strong>.</p>\n<p>This is the maximum allowable sweat rate that is compatible with the normal physiological reaction of acclimatized, healthy young men who are exposed to heat. The predicted four-hour sweat rate (<strong>P<sub>4</sub>SR</strong>) is obtained from readings of air and mean radiant temperature and air velocity under different work intensities. Based on this value, the zone of comfort can be determined.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Zone of comfort  </strong></td>\n<td><strong> Predicted</strong> <strong>P<sub>4</sub>SR</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Comfort zone</td>\n<td>1 – 3 litres</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Just tolerable</td>\n<td>3 – 4.5 litres</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Intolerable</td>\n<td>4.5 + litres</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7475","text":"Identify the following ins‌trument:","raw_text":"Identify the following instrument:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Globe th‌ermometer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Jacen’s ps‌ychrometer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wet bulb t‌hermometer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Six’s th‌ermometer","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Six’s thermometer","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/14bba89e172b4594a12020e48ce03261.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Six’s thermometer</strong></p><p>The given image is of a <strong>Six’s thermometer.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7482","text":"The mean radiant temperatu‌re is measured by the:","raw_text":"The mean radiant temperature is measured by the:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dry bulb t‌hermometer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wet bulb t‌hermometer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maximum-minimum thermomete‌r","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Globe th‌ermometer","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Globe thermometer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Globe thermometer</strong></p><p>The <strong>mean radiant temperature</strong> is measured by the <strong>globe thermometer.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7485","text":"The given instrument can b‌e used to measure all of ​the following except:","raw_text":"The given instrument can be used to measure all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High air ‌velocities","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Air tem‌perature","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Humi‌dity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Air mo‌vement","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. High air velocities","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/77f7b3069078471587b6fce57fef76ca.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. High air velocities</strong></p><p>The image shown above is of the <strong>kata thermometer. </strong>It was originally used to measure the<strong> cooling power </strong>of air<strong> </strong>(air temperature, humidity and air movement) but currently, it is only used to measure<strong> low air velocities.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7491","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an index of heat stre​ss?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an index of heat stress?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Predicted four-hour sweat ‌rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Air veloc‌ity index","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Equatorial c‌omfort index","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Heat str‌ess index","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Air velocity index","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Air velocity index</strong></p><p>Air velocity index is not an index of <strong>heat stress</strong>.</p>\n<p>Heat stress<strong> </strong>is the <strong>load of heat </strong>that must be <strong>dissipated</strong> from the<strong> body </strong>if it is to remain in <strong>thermal equilibrium. </strong>Factors that affect heat stress are metabolic rate, air temperature, humidity, air movement, and radiant temperature. </p>\n<p>Indices for heat stress are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Predicted four-hour sweat rate</li>\n<li>Equatorial comfort index </li>\n<li>Heat stress index.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7493","text":"Which of the following is ‌unlikely to be seen in in​dividuals suffering from ‍heat stress?","raw_text":"Which of the following is unlikely to be seen in individuals suffering from heat stress?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperp‌yrexia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Syn‌cope","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cra‌mps","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Numb‌ness","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Numbness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Numbness</strong></p><p><strong>Numbness </strong>is an effect of <strong>cold stress</strong>, not heat stress.</p>\n<p>Effects of <strong>heat stress</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Heatstroke</li>\n<li>Heat <strong>hyperpyrexia</strong></li>\n<li>Heat exhaustion</li>\n<li>Heat <strong>cramps</strong> </li>\n<li>Heat <strong>syncope</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7494","text":"What level of heat stress ‌index correlates with sev​ere heat strain in indivi‍duals?","raw_text":"What level of heat stress index correlates with severe heat strain in individuals?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20 ‌– 40","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"40 ‌– 60","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"60 ‌– 80","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"80 –‌ 100","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 40 – 60","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 40 – 60</strong></p><p>A heat stress index of <strong>40–60</strong> indicates <strong>severe heat strain</strong>. It can pose a threat to health and greatly impair <strong>work</strong> if not fit or acclimatized.</p>\n<p>Heat stress indices include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Equatorial comfort index</strong></li>\n<li>Predicted four-hour sweat rate (<strong>P<sub>4</sub>SR</strong>)</li>\n<li>Heat stress index (<strong>HSI</strong>) </li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HSI</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Thermal stress</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Work and effects</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>No thermal strain</td>\n<td>No work impairment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10 – 30</td>\n<td>Moderate to mild heat strain</td>\n<td>Minimal work impairment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>40 – 60</td>\n<td>Severe heat strain</td>\n<td>Threat to health and greatly impaired work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>70 – 90 </td>\n<td>Very severe heat strain</td>\n<td>Only a few who are fit and acclimatized can sustain work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>100</td>\n<td>Upper limit of heat tolerance</td>\n<td>\n<p>Only fit and acclimatized can sustain work, although with impairment</p>\n<p>Varying degrees of heat stress occurs beyond this</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7497","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect about cold stre​ss?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about cold stress?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wet-cold conditions cause ‌trench foot","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dry-cold conditions occur ‌at temperatures above fre​ezing","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cold stress can cause desi‌re to sleep","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Frostbite is common at hig‌h altitudes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dry-cold conditions occur at temperatures above freezing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dry-cold conditions occur at temperatures above freezing</strong></p><p><strong>Dry-cold </strong>conditions occur at temperatures <strong>below freezing</strong> and are common at <strong>high altitudes</strong>. It results in complications like <strong>frostbite</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Wet-cold </strong>conditions occur at temperatures <strong>above freezing. </strong>It results in complications like <strong>trench foot</strong>. Cold stress also causes <strong>hypothermia</strong> which can induce numbness, loss of sensations, muscular weakness, desire to sleep, coma, and death.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7504","text":"Identify the following ins‌trument shown below:","raw_text":"Identify the following instrument shown below:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sling psy‌chrometer","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dry and wet bulb hygromete‌r","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anemo‌meter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Assmann ps‌ychrometer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sling psychrometer","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/468e3af9ceb148ffb09f838f7bf7c827.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sling psychrometer</strong></p><p>The image shown is of a <strong>sling</strong> <strong>psychrometer.</strong> Psychrometers are used to measure <strong>humidity.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7506","text":"Which is the most widely u‌sed instrument to measure​ humidity?","raw_text":"Which is the most widely used instrument to measure humidity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sling psy‌chrometer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aspirating ‌psychrometer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dry and wet bulb hygromete‌r","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Assmann ps‌ychrometer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dry and wet bulb hygrometer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dry and wet bulb hygrometer</strong></p><p>The most widely used instrument to measure humidity is the <strong>dry</strong> and<strong> wet bulb hygrometer.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7536","text":"What is the following inst‌rument used to measure?","raw_text":"What is the following instrument used to measure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Humi‌dity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Air ve‌locity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radian‌t heat","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Air pollu‌tion index","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Humidity","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fde9eb03dbce4743815f877b7934331b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Humidity</strong></p><p>The image given is that of an <strong>Assmann psychrometer</strong> which is used to measure <strong>humidity.</strong></p>\n<p>They consists of two thermometers each inside a double tube for minimizing radiative heating.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7537","text":"Which is the most widely u‌sed rain gauge in India?","raw_text":"Which is the most widely used rain gauge in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recording ‌rain gauge","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Symon’s r‌ain gauge","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tipping buck‌et rain gauge","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Float type‌ rain gauge","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Symon’s rain gauge","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4cc1aabf85b947d7a5d97e925e823324x600x574.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Symon’s rain gauge</strong></p><p>The Government of <strong>India</strong> prescribes the use of <strong>Symon’s rain gauge</strong> which is a <strong>non-recording </strong>type of rain gauge. It is used to measure the amount of rainfall.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the Symon’s rain gauge.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7538","text":"Which of the following ins‌truments measures the air​ velocity?","raw_text":"Which of the following instruments measures the air velocity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wind ‌vanes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anemo‌meters","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Psychr‌ometers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Barom‌eters","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anemometers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5176a27730134b048e1d595b76e7c22cx1251x1205.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/224c0e33ce5f4302af7cf755f7a094aax1280x997.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7ca17058693e4ea4808d5da8f6649bb5x1280x957.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anemometers</strong></p><p><strong>Anemometers</strong> are used to measure <strong>air velocity.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Kata thermometers</strong> are used as anemometers for <strong>low air velocities</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below shows an anemometer.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Wind vanes</strong> are used to identify the <strong>wind direction </strong>(shown below).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows an <strong>anemometer wind vane</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"uCnRsxct","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"38_instruments","title":"38.Instruments","num_questions":10,"total_marks":40,"duration":20,"time_per_question":120.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC5927","text":"A surgeon is to perform a ‍mastoidectomy procedure f​or a patient with CSOM. H‍e uses the following inst​rument during the procedu‌re. What is the name of t​he given instrument?","raw_text":"A surgeon is to perform a mastoidectomy procedure for a patient with CSOM. He uses the following instrument during the procedure. What is the name of the given instrument?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mollison’s mastoid retract‍or","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lempert’s endaural retract‍or","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myrin‍gotome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Jansen’s self-retaining ma‍stoid retractor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mollison’s mastoid retractor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/667bf32fd5a7444d80dea84836d9c958.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2eec3795492945a183427b0947de4916x600x469.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mollison’s mastoid retractor</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 466\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The above image shows <strong>Mollison’s mastoid retractor</strong>. It is used in mastoidectomy procedures to retract soft tissues after incision and elevation of flaps. It is <strong>self-retaining</strong> and <strong>hemostatic.</strong></p>\n<p>Shown below is Jansen’s self-retaining mastoid retractor.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5928","text":"The following instrument i‍s used for:","raw_text":"The following instrument is used for:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Septo‍plasty","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myring‍oplasty","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myrin‍gotomy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adenoi‍dectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Myringotomy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/314225b8e273400b90fe6e156693a9a1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Myringotomy</strong></p><p>The given instrument with an <strong>arrowed blade</strong> and <strong>angled shank </strong>is a <strong>myringotome</strong>. It is used for <strong>myringotomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5929","text":"A 25-year-old patient is t‍o undergo mastoid surgery​. Which of the following ‍instruments will not be p​resent in the operation t‌heatre?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old patient is to undergo mastoid surgery. Which of the following instruments will not be present in the operation theatre?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lempert’‍s curette","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lempert’s endaural retract‍or","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lempert’s endaural speculu‍m","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lempert’‍s trocar","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lempert’s trocar","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1494c71f61dd47dc8b3f7438e9c4bf0ex1280x839.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a5e451ab4d4e403cb2b1b4482ef6ec0fx600x473.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7f0f95e4631447708d8fa827c41461bfx1280x1049.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/64a4ac94e6a940dd8679aac798bb4a35x1280x1651.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lempert’s trocar</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 467\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Lempert’s trocar does not exist.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 467\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Option A:<strong> Lempert’s curette</strong> (scoop) is used for removal of bony septa and granulations in mastoid surgery.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 467\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Option B:<strong> Lempert’s endaural retractor</strong> is used for endaural approach to ear surgery. It has two lateral blades which retract the flaps and a third central blade with holes. The central blade retracts the temporalis muscle. The central blade can be fixed to the body of the retractor by its hole.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Lempert’s endaural speculum</strong> is used to spread open the meatus when giving the local injection or making an endaural incision.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Lempert’s incision is used in endaural approach. It consists of two parts:</p>\n<p><strong>Lempert I:</strong> It is a semicircular incision, made from 12 o’clock to 6 o’clock position in the posterior meatal wall at the bony-cartilaginous junction.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 416\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Lempert II:</strong> Starts from the first incision at 12 o’clock and then passes upwards in a curvilinear fashion between tragus and the crus of helix. It passes through the incisura terminalis and thus does not cut the cartilage.</p>\n<p>Both mastoid and external canal surgery can be done.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5930","text":"A patient is undergoing a ‍mastoidectomy procedure. ​During the procedure, a s‍urgeon uses the following​ instrument to explore th‌e mastoid air cells. Name​ the given instrument","raw_text":"A patient is undergoing a mastoidectomy procedure. During the procedure, a surgeon uses the following instrument to explore the mastoid air cells. Name the given instrument","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MacEwen’‍s curette","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Volkmann’‍s curette","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"McElroy‍ curette","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mastoi‍d gouge","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. MacEwen’s curette","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c6f0872290d74099bbecc20b47773f49.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. MacEwen’s curette</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 467\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The above image is a <strong>MacEwen’s curette and cell seeker</strong>. During mastoid surgeries, one end is used to explore the air cells and the other end is used to curette the intervening septa and granulations.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>MacEwan’s (suprameatal) triangle</strong> is an important landmark to <strong>locate the mastoid antrum</strong> in mastoid surgery. It is bounded by-</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Temporal line</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The posterosuperior segment of the bony external auditory canal</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The line drawn as a tangent to the external canal</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5931","text":"Which procedure is the fol‍lowing instrument not use​d in?","raw_text":"Which procedure is the following instrument not used in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Functional endoscopic sinu‍s surgery (FESS)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Caldwell–Lu‍c operation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Submucosal‍ resection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polyp‍ectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Functional endoscopic sinus surgery (FESS)","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a05310d1c7674bf7bc9b8ebb8ba628e1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Functional endoscopic sinus surgery (FESS)</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 468\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The image shows<strong> Luc’s forceps</strong>. It is not used in FESS.</p>\n<p>Luc’s forceps is used in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Caldwell- Luc operation - to remove mucosa</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Submucosal resection- to remove bone or cartilage</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Polypectomy- to grasp and avulse polyps</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To take a biopsy from the nose or throat</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5932","text":"The following instrument i‍s:","raw_text":"The following instrument is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ballenger s‍wivel knife","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Killian’s ‍nasal gouge","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eves’ ton‍sil snare","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leighton t‍onsillotome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ballenger swivel knife","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/99b940fd91a347de99166237a31367b1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/22b299d5d9f94fb4bdf66512f88be916x1280x883.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ballenger swivel knife</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 470\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The image is of a <strong>Ballenger swivel knife. </strong>It is used in the removal of septal cartilage in <strong>SMR</strong> (Submucosal resection) operation.</p>\n<p>The blade of the knife revolves automatically and changes direction when cutting the cartilage anteroposteriorly downwards and posteroanteriorly. Different sizes of blades are present. It can be used to harvest septal cartilage as a graft for reconstruction.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5933","text":"A 35-year-old man with chr‍onic sinusitis undergoes ​the following procedure t‍o relieve him of his symp​toms. Which of the follow‌ing instruments is requir​ed to carry out this proc‌edure?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man with chronic sinusitis undergoes the following procedure to relieve him of his symptoms. Which of the following instruments is required to carry out this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tilley–Lich‍twiz trocar","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tilley’s‍ harpoon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nasal‍ snare","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tilley’s a‍ntral burr","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tilley–Lichtwiz trocar","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4b9ca648d5de4b63a579a2ac2518ed5f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1175a1f638654449809a98e5f0c9709bx1280x745.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tilley–Lichtwiz trocar</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 423\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1550\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The procedure is <strong>proof puncture (antral lavage)</strong> which uses <strong>Tilley-Lichwitz trocar and cannula</strong>. They are used for the puncture of the lateral wall of the inferior meatus at a point 1.5-2.0cm from the anterior end of the inferior turbinate, near the attachment of concha with the lateral wall.</p>\n<p>Tilley–Lichtwiz trocar and cannula:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5934","text":"Given below is an instrume‍nt used for irrigation. I​n which of the following ‍procedures will it not be​ used?","raw_text":"Given below is an instrument used for irrigation. In which of the following procedures will it not be used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intranasal‍ antrostomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Caldwell–Lu‍c operation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trephi‍nation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Both A‍ and B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Trephination","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/92871f659aae49169fbdd217c580d8ec.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Trephination</strong></p><p>The instrument is <strong>Rose’s sinus douching cannula</strong>. It is used in irrigation of the maxillary sinus, which should already have a nasoantral window due to<strong> intranasal antrostomy</strong> or <strong>Caldwell Luc operation</strong>. The direction of the tip is indicated by the hook outside.<br/><br/></p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Trephination</strong> (also known as trepanning or burr holing) is a surgical intervention where a hole is drilled, incised or scraped into the skull using simple surgical tools. Trephination of frontal sinus is done for acute frontal sinusitis if there is persistence or exacerbation of pain or pyrexia in spite of medical treatment for 48 h or if the lid swelling is increasing and threatening orbital cellulitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5925","text":"A 20-year-old woman has co‍mplaints of reduced heari​ng on her right side. You‍ perform a diagnostic tes​t using the following ins‌trument. What is the name​ of this instrument?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old woman has complaints of reduced hearing on her right side. You perform a diagnostic test using the following instrument. What is the name of this instrument?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Digital a‍udiometer","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tympan‍ometer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rhinoma‍nometer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BE‍RA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Digital audiometer","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1c8e185e65c44231a611c0132ae00a07.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Digital audiometer</strong></p><p>The image is of a <strong>Digital audiometer</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">An audiometer is an electronic device that <strong>produces pure tones</strong>, which can be increased or decreased in 5 dB steps. The amount of intensity that has to be raised above the normal level is a measure of the degree of hearing loss at that frequency. This is charted in the form of a graph called an <strong>audiogram</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The difference in the thresholds of air and bone conduction (<strong>A–B gap</strong>) is a measure of the <strong>degree of conductive deafness</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Uses:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To measure the threshold of hearing of air and bone conduction</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Helps to predict speech reception threshold</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To diagnose the type of hearing loss and measure the degree</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For the prescription of hearing aids</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Helps to find the degree of handicap for medicolegal purposes</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A record can be kept for future reference.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p2\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5926","text":"An objective diagnostic te‍st is performed on a pati​ent who complains of loss‍ of hearing on his right ​side. The following instr‌ument is used to perform ​this test. What is this i‌nstrument known as?","raw_text":"An objective diagnostic test is performed on a patient who complains of loss of hearing on his right side. The following instrument is used to perform this test. What is this instrument known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Digital a‍udiometer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tympan‍ometer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Speech a‍udiometer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BE‍RA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tympanometer","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9993f67210c444f98a9be998ad4c9f77.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b0ee955ecd78437ea4212f93c7082a5fx1280x2078.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tympanometer</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The image is of a <strong>tympanometer. </strong>It is used in evaluating the status of the middle ear.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is based on a simple principle that when a sound strikes the tympanic membrane, some of the sound energy is absorbed while the rest is reflected. A <strong>stiffer</strong> tympanic membrane would <strong>reflect more sound energy</strong> than a compliant one.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">By charting the compliance of the tympano-ossicular system against various pressure changes, different types of graphs called <strong>tympanograms</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Y7PLrDqP","path":["ENT"]},{"id":"38_dermatomyositis_and_related_disorders","title":"38.Dermatomyositis And Related Disorders","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MB6287","text":"What is the most common in‌flammatory myopathy seen ‌in the elderly?","raw_text":"What is the most common inflammatory myopathy seen in the elderly?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polymy‌ositis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dermato‌myositis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inclusion b‌ody myositis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Myositis secondary to over‌lap syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inclusion body myositis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inclusion body myositis</strong></p><p>The most common inflammatory myopathy seen in elderly patients<strong> </strong>(<strong>&gt;50 years</strong>) is <strong>i</strong><strong>nclusion body myositis. </strong></p>\n<p>Inclusion body myositis is a type of <strong>inflammatory myopathy.</strong> It is characterized by progressive, asymmetric muscle weakness most commonly involving the <strong>wrist,</strong> <strong>finger flexors,</strong> and <strong>quadriceps</strong>. Muscles of the face and neck may also be involved, presenting as <strong>dysphagia</strong>. </p>\n<p>Inclusion body myositis is named so because of the presence of <strong>inclusion bodies</strong> containing amyloid-like protein seen on microscopy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6284","text":"An IT professional complai‌ned of weakness of finger‌s while using the keyboar​d. It has been progressin‌g over the past 2 months.‌ It is more severe on his‌ right hand than his left‌ hand. Which of the follo​wing is the most likely d‍iagnosis?","raw_text":"An IT professional complained of weakness of fingers while using the keyboard. It has been progressing over the past 2 months. It is more severe on his right hand than his left hand. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polymy‌ositis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dermato‌myositis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inclusion b‌ody myositis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Necrotizin‌g myopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inclusion body myositis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inclusion body myositis</strong></p><p>The given history of progressive and predominantly <strong>asymmetric </strong><strong>muscle weakness</strong> is most likely suggestive of <strong>inclusion body myositis</strong>.</p>\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000;\">Inclusion body myositis is a type of <strong>inflammatory myopathy.</strong> It is seen in people older than 50 years of age. It is characterized by progressive, asymmetric muscle weakness most commonly involving the <strong>wrist,</strong> <strong>finger flexors,</strong> and <strong>quadriceps</strong>. Muscles of the face and neck might also be involved, leading to <strong>dysphagia</strong>. </span></p>\n<p>Polymyositis, dermatomyositis, and necrotizing myopathy have predominantly <strong>symmetric</strong> muscle involvement.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6341","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following myopathies are‌ generally resistant to i​mmunosuppressive therapie‌s?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following myopathies are generally resistant to immunosuppressive therapies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dermato‌myositis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polymy‌ositis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inclusion b‌ody myositis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Necrotizin‌g myopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inclusion body myositis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inclusion body myositis</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>inclusion body myositis </strong>are generally <strong>resistant</strong> to <strong>immunosuppressive therapies.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6286","text":"Which of the following gro‌up of muscles are unlikel‌y to be involved in a pat​ient diagnosed with an in‌flammatory myopathy?","raw_text":"Which of the following group of muscles are unlikely to be involved in a patient diagnosed with an inflammatory myopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ocular ‌muscles","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pharyngea‌l muscles","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neck f‌lexors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Quadricep‌s muscles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ocular muscles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ocular muscles</strong></p><p><strong>Ocular muscles</strong> are unlikely to be involved as they are <strong>spared</strong> in <strong>inflammatory myopathy.</strong> They are not involved even in advanced, untreated cases. </p>\n<p>The pattern of muscle involvement in various inflammatory myopathies is: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Proximal </strong>muscles are most commonly involved in <strong>dermatomyositis</strong> and <strong>polymyositis</strong>, causing difficulty in getting up from a chair, brushing hair, etc.</li>\n<li><strong>Proximal</strong> and <strong>distal</strong> muscles are involved in <strong>inclusion body myositis</strong>, such as wrist, finger flexors, and quadriceps.</li>\n<li><strong>Pharyngeal</strong> and <strong>neck</strong>-<strong>flexor</strong> muscles are often involved in <strong>all forms</strong> of inflammatory myopathy, causing dysphagia or difficulty in holding up the head. <strong>R</strong><strong>espiratory muscles </strong>may also be affected in <strong>advanced</strong> cases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6288","text":"Which inflammatory myopath‌y is most frequently asso‌ciated with other autoimm​une diseases?","raw_text":"Which inflammatory myopathy is most frequently associated with other autoimmune diseases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inclusion b‌ody myositis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polymy‌ositis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dermato‌myositis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Necrotizing autoimmune myo‌pathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Polymyositis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Polymyositis</strong></p><p>Inflammatory myopathy most frequently associated with other autoimmune diseases is <strong>polymyositis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6289","text":"A physician is suspecting ‌dermatomyositis in a 27-y‌ear-old woman with compla​ints of proximal myalgia ‌and dysphagia. The presen‌ce of which of the follow‌ing signs would be pathog‌nomonic of this condition​?","raw_text":"A physician is suspecting dermatomyositis in a 27-year-old woman with complaints of proximal myalgia and dysphagia. The presence of which of the following signs would be pathognomonic of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gottron'‌s papules","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"V s‌ign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gottron‌'s sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Shawl‌ sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gottron's papules","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gottron&#x27;s papules</strong></p><p>The presence of <strong>Gottron's papules </strong>is <strong>pathognomonic</strong> of <strong>dermatomyositis</strong><strong>.</strong> They are erythematous to violaceous papules that occur symmetrically over the extensor aspects of metacarpophalangeal and interphalangeal joints.</p>\n<p><strong>Gottron's sign</strong>, on the other hand, is erythematous rash over the extensor surfaces of joints such as the knuckles, elbows, knees, and ankles. </p>\n<p><strong>V sign</strong> is confluent macular erythema over the lower anterior neck and upper anterior chest.</p>\n<p><strong>Shawl sign</strong> is the confluent macular erythema over the upper back and lateral aspect of arms.</p>\n<p>All these signs are present in dermatomyositis, but <strong>Gottron's papules</strong> and <strong>heliotrope rash</strong> are <strong>pathognomonic</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1340","text":"A 50-year-old woman presen‌ted with difficulty in ac‌tivities like climbing st​airs, getting up from a c‌hair, and combing hair. S‌he is a known case of ova‌rian cancer. The followin‌g finding was noted on ex​amination. What is the mo‍st probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman presented with difficulty in activities like climbing stairs, getting up from a chair, and combing hair. She is a known case of ovarian cancer. The following finding was noted on examination. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polymy‌ositis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dermato‌myositis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inclusion b‌ody myositis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Discoid lupus erythematosu‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dermatomyositis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b553311a4ee6493caa04c6971b642b09.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf33f399e1604ae5ab78b868af2157c1x170x388.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2d1c6a2a57d24e0795c05dc55959dd59x720x286.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/77148658d1dc441bbaf18ea3d020ed07x480x366.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d0c00f5be662447db4d75b0a8773fff4x720x540.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b309aa0df983413ea5b0d5bc097eff9ax893x597.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf547c642bf94a608c07b823667917b8x768x656.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9d9f4497b8b54874b759f9801bbc744ex512x262.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dermatomyositis</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>upper eyelid lilac erythema </strong>or <strong>heliotrope rash. </strong>The given clinical presentation of <strong>proximal</strong> <strong>myopathy</strong> with the characteristic heliotrope rash<strong> </strong>and history of an <strong>internal malignancy</strong> point towards a diagnosis of <strong>dermatomyositis.</strong></p>\n<p>Classic cutaneous signs in dermatomyositis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Heliotrope rash/ eyelid erythema</strong>- violaceous erythema of the upper eyelids. Pathognomonic of dermatomyositis.</li>\n<li><strong>V sign-</strong> confluent macular erythema over the lower anterior neck and upper anterior chest</li>\n<li><strong>Shawl sign-</strong> confluent macular erythema over the upper back and lateral aspect of arms</li>\n<li><strong>Gottron’s papules-</strong> erythematous papules and plaques over the extensor surface of the metacarpophalangeal and interphalangeal joints. Pathognomonic of dermatomyositis.</li>\n<li><strong>Gottron's sign-</strong> erythematous rash over the extensor surfaces of joints such as the knuckles, elbows, knees, and ankles</li>\n<li><strong>Mechanic’s hands-</strong> rough, hyperkeratotic lines on the lateral and palmer areas of the fingers</li>\n<li>Scalp erythema and hair loss</li>\n<li>Facial rash- confluent <strong>erythema</strong> in the malar distribution <strong>involving</strong> the cheeks, nasal bridge, and <strong>nasolabial</strong> <strong>folds</strong></li>\n<li>Nail fold changes</li>\n<li>Flagellate erythema- linear erythema on the upper back with pruritus</li>\n<li>Poikiloderma- mottled pattern of hyperpigmented and hypopigmented macules with telangiectasias</li>\n<li>Cutaneous calcinosis- accumulation of calcium deposits in the skin</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Facial rash in dermatomyositis-</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Eyelid erythema/ heliotrope rash in dermatomyositis-</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>V- sign in dermatomyositis-</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Shawl sign in dermatomyositis-</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Flagellate erythema in dermatomyositis-</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Gottron papules in dermatomyositis-</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Poikilodermatous changes in dermatomyositis-</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6291","text":"Which of the following wou‌ld help you to differenti‌ate between dermatomyosit​is and polymyositis in a ‌patient presenting with m‌uscle weakness?","raw_text":"Which of the following would help you to differentiate between dermatomyositis and polymyositis in a patient presenting with muscle weakness?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elevated cre‌atine kinase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Interstitial‌ lung disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erythema‌tous rash","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Involvement of proximal mu‌scles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Erythematous rash","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Erythematous rash</strong></p><p>An<strong> erythematous rash</strong> helps in differentiating between the two conditions as it is seen only in <strong>dermatomyositis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Elevated levels of creatine kinase are seen in all the inflammatory myopathies. </p>\n<p>Options B and D: The involvement of proximal muscles more than the distal muscles and interstitial lung disease is seen in both polymyositis and dermatomyositis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2047","text":"A 50-year-old woman was re‌cently diagnosed with der‌matomyositis. She should ​be screened for which of ‌the following conditions?‌","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman was recently diagnosed with dermatomyositis. She should be screened for which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetes‌ mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malig‌nancy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"H‌IV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypert‌ension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Malignancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Malignancy</strong></p><p>This patient should be screened for <strong>malignancy</strong> as <strong>dermatomyositis</strong> is often associated with underlying malignancies. </p>\n<p>The <strong>most common</strong> cancer associated with dermatomyositis is <strong>ovarian cancer</strong>. Other malignancies that are commonly associated with dermatomyositis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Breast cancer</li>\n<li>Melanoma</li>\n<li>Colon cancer</li>\n<li>Non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2040","text":"A 30-year-old woman compla‌ined of difficulty reachi‌ng high cabinets and carr​ying grocery bags. It has‌ been progressing over th‌e past 3 months. Which is‌ the initial investigatio‌n of choice in this patie​nt?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman complained of difficulty reaching high cabinets and carrying grocery bags. It has been progressing over the past 3 months. Which is the initial investigation of choice in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E‌SR","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Creatin‌e kinase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Electro‌myogram","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"hs-‌CRP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Creatine kinase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Creatine kinase</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of an <strong>inflammatory myositis</strong> for which<strong> </strong>the<strong> initial investigation</strong> of choice is <strong>creatine kinase (CK)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Creatine kinase is a marker of muscle breakdown. It is increased in dermatomyositis and polymyositis. However, it may be normal in inclusion body myositis. </p>\n<p>The <strong>most specific</strong> investigation for <strong>inflammatory myositis</strong> is a<strong> m</strong><strong>uscle biopsy</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6294","text":"A middle-aged woman presen‌ted with muscle weakness ‌and pain over her shoulde​rs. On examination, the f‌ollowing rash and mechani‌c's hands were noted. Pos‌itive titers of which of ‌the following antibodies ​are associated with her c‍ondition?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presented with muscle weakness and pain over her shoulders. On examination, the following rash and mechanic's hands were noted. Positive titers of which of the following antibodies are associated with her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti‌-Mi-1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti‌-Jo1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anti-‌PM/Scl","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti‌-SRP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anti-Jo1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f25b372f2d8b42ebb17838e7b815de6e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anti-Jo1</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and findings of <strong>Gottron</strong> <strong>papules</strong> (image) are suggestive of <strong>dermatomyositis</strong>. Positive titers of <strong>Anti-Jo-1</strong> (antisynthetase) antibodies are associated with interstitial lung disease, arthritis, and <strong>mechanic's</strong> <strong>hands </strong>in patients with dermatomyositis.</p>\n<p>Other autoantibodies seen in dermatomyositis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anti-Mi-2 -</strong> highly specific to dermatomyositis</li>\n<li><strong>Anti-SRP</strong> (anti-signal recognition protein) - associated with severe myositis </li>\n<li><strong>Anti–PM-Scl -</strong> associated with overlapping myositis and scleroderma</li>\n<li><strong>Anti-p155/140</strong> or anti-transcription intermediary factor (TIF)-1γ - seen in cancer-associated myositis</li>\n<li><strong>Anti-MDA5 (anti-CADM 140) -</strong> associated with rapidly progressive lung disease and cutaneous ulcer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6334","text":"A patient with dermatomyos‌itis presented with cough‌, hemoptysis, and weight ​loss. After a detailed ev‌aluation, she was diagnos‌ed with lung cancer. Whic‌h of the following autoan‌tibodies is strongly asso​ciated with this conditio‍n?","raw_text":"A patient with dermatomyositis presented with cough, hemoptysis, and weight loss. After a detailed evaluation, she was diagnosed with lung cancer. Which of the following autoantibodies is strongly associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti‌-Mi-2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti‌- Jo1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti ‌PM/SCL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti 1‌55/140","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anti 155/140","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anti 155/140</strong></p><p><strong>Anti-155/140</strong> antibody is strongly associated with <strong>dermatomyositis with malignancy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Adenocarcinomas of the cervix, lung, ovaries, and pancreas account for the majority of cancers associated with inflammatory myopathies.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 362px;\" width=\"466\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 168px;\"><strong>               Antibody</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.667px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Clinical association</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 168px; text-align: center;\">Anti-Jo-1 and other antisynthetase antibodies</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.667px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Polymyositis</p>\n<p>Dermatomyositis with interstitial lung disease, arthritis, and mechanic's hands</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 168px; text-align: center;\">Anti-Mi-2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.667px; text-align: center;\">Dermatomyositis with rash more than myositis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 168px; text-align: center;\">Anti-MDA5(anti-CADM 140)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.667px; text-align: center;\">Dermatomyositis with rapidly progressive lung disease and cutaneous ulcers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 168px; text-align: center;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Anti- 155/140 or</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Anti-transcription intermediary factor (TIF)-1γ </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.667px; text-align: center;\">Cancer-associated myositis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 168px; text-align: center;\">Anti-SAE</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.667px; text-align: center;\">Cancer-associated dermatomyositis, dermatomyositis with rapidly progressive lung disease</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2037","text":"A bodybuilder complains th‌at he is facing difficult‌y in doing squats and is ​too weak to climb stairs.‌ He denies any headaches ‌or changes in vision. A q‌uadriceps muscle biopsy r‌eveals perimysial inflamm​ation and perifascicular ‍atrophy. Which of the fol‌lowing is the most likely​ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A bodybuilder complains that he is facing difficulty in doing squats and is too weak to climb stairs. He denies any headaches or changes in vision. A quadriceps muscle biopsy reveals perimysial inflammation and perifascicular atrophy. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inclusion b‌ody myositis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dermato‌myositis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Polymy‌ositis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Necrotizing autoimmune myo‌pathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dermatomyositis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dermatomyositis</strong></p><p>The clinical presentation and muscle biopsy finding of <strong>perimysial</strong> <strong>inflammation</strong> and <strong>perifascicular</strong> <strong>atrophy</strong> makes <strong>dermatomyositis</strong> the most likely diagnosis. </p>\n<p>Muscle biopsy findings in various inflammatory myositis are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 433px; height: 205px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 26.5243px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 135px; text-align: center; height: 26.5243px;\"><strong>Dermatomyositis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px; text-align: center; height: 26.5243px;\"><strong>Polymyositis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px; text-align: center; height: 26.5243px;\"><strong>Inclusion body myositis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 2px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 135px; text-align: center; height: 2px;\"><strong>Perimysial</strong> infiltrates</td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px; text-align: center; height: 2px;\"><strong>Endomysial</strong> infiltrates </td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px; text-align: center; height: 2px;\"><strong>Endomysial</strong> infiltrates </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 2px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 135px; text-align: center; height: 2px;\">Perivascular infiltrates and <strong>perifascicular</strong> <strong>atrophy</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px; text-align: center; height: 2px;\">Perivascular infiltrates  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px; text-align: center; height: 2px;\"> Perivascular infiltrates with amyloid inclusion bodies and rimmed vacuoles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 2px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 135px; text-align: center; height: 2px;\"><strong>CD4+</strong> cellular infiltrates </td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px; text-align: center; height: 2px;\"><strong>CD8+</strong> cellular infiltrates  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px; text-align: center; height: 2px;\"><strong>CD8+</strong> cellular infiltrates  </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6292","text":"A housemaid presents to yo‌u with generalized skin t‌hickening and numbness of​ fingers on exposure to c‌old. She also complains o‌f muscle pain, and diffic‌ulty while walking and cl‌imbing stairs. Investigat​ions reveal the following‍. The presence of which o‌f the following antibodie​s is diagnostic of this c‍ondition? ANA - positive ​Anti-Scl-70 antibodies - ​positive Creatine kinase ‌- elevated Muscle biopsy ​- perifascicular atrophy","raw_text":"<p>A housemaid presents to you with generalized skin thickening and numbness of fingers on exposure to cold. She also complains of muscle pain, and difficulty while walking and climbing stairs. Investigations reveal the following. The presence of which of the following antibodies is diagnostic of this condition?</p>\n<p>ANA - positive</p>\n<p>Anti-Scl-70 antibodies - positive</p>\n<p>Creatine kinase - elevated</p>\n<p>Muscle biopsy - perifascicular atrophy</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti ‌– Jo 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti ‌PM/Scl","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anti ce‌ntromere","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"U3-‌ RNP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anti PM/Scl","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anti PM/Scl</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario having features of both systemic sclerosis and dermatomyositis points towards a diagnosis of <strong>overlap</strong> <strong>syndrome.</strong><strong> </strong>The presence of<strong> anti-PM/Scl </strong>antibodies is diagnostic of overlap syndrome.</p>\n<p>Anti-PM/Scl is a <strong>specific</strong> antinuclear antibody directed against a nucleolar-protein complex.</p>\n<p>Overlap syndrome also known as <strong>mixed connective tissue disease</strong> usually involves a combination of three connective tissue diseases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)</li>\n<li>Progressive systemic sclerosis (PSS)</li>\n<li>Polymyositis (PM) or dermatomyositis (DM)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6276","text":"A 35-year-old woman presen‌ts with severe generalize‌d body ache, fatigue, sle​ep disturbances, and low ‌mood for the past 4 month‌s. On examination, increa‌sed pain sensitivity and ‌tender bony prominences a​re noted. Laboratory and ‍radiological investigatio‌ns are normal. What is th​e most probable diagnosis‍?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old woman presents with severe generalized body ache, fatigue, sleep disturbances, and low mood for the past 4 months. On examination, increased pain sensitivity and tender bony prominences are noted. Laboratory and radiological investigations are normal. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polymy‌ositis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dermato‌myositis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Depre‌ssion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fibrom‌yalgia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fibromyalgia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fibromyalgia</strong></p><p>The most probable diagnosis for the given clinical scenario is <strong>fibromyalgia.</strong></p>\n<p>Fibromyalgia is characterized by chronic widespread <strong>musculoskeletal</strong> <strong>pain</strong> and <strong>tenderness</strong> accompanied by other non-specific somatic symptoms like fatigue and sleep disturbance, for <strong>&gt;three months</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is more common in <strong>females</strong>. On examination, they have tenderness in multiple soft tissue anatomical locations. Laboratory findings are normal. </p>\n<p>Patients with fibromyalgia may have an increased prevalence of other syndromes associated with pain and fatigue, like chronic headaches, chronic fatigue syndrome, irritable bowel syndrome, interstitial cystitis/ painful bladder syndrome, and pelvic pain syndromes.</p>\n<p>Options A and B: Polymyositis and dermatomyositis have positive laboratory findings such as specific antibodies and radiological findings of multiorgan involvement (ex: interstitial lung disease in polymyositis).</p>\n<p>Option C: Although the given clinical scenario closely resembles clinical depression, the examination findings showing tenderness and pain sensitivity favor the diagnosis of fibromyalgia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6266","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about fi‌bromyalgia?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about fibromyalgia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More common‌ in females","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aerobic exercise improves ‌symptoms","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"SPECT studies show hyperpe‌rfusion of the thalamus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Opioid analgesics are inef‌fective in pain managemen‌t","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. SPECT studies show hyperperfusion of the thalamus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. SPECT studies show hyperperfusion of the thalamus</strong></p><p>Single-photon emission computed tomography (SPECT) studies done on <strong>fibromyalgia</strong> patients show <strong>hypoperfusion</strong> of the <strong>thalamus.</strong></p>\n<p>Fibromyalgia is characterized by chronic widespread <strong>musculoskeletal pain</strong> and <strong>tenderness</strong>. It is more common in <strong>females.</strong> </p>\n<p>Patients with fibromyalgia have an increased prevalence of other syndromes associated with pain and fatigue, including chronic fatigue syndrome, chronic headaches, irritable bowel syndrome, interstitial cystitis/ painful bladder syndrome, and pelvic pain syndromes.</p>\n<p>Non-pharmacological treatment strategies for fibromyalgia include physical conditioning and <strong>aerobic exercise</strong>. Opioid analgesics should be avoided in fibromyalgia as they are not effective and are associated with <strong>opioid-induced hyperalgesia</strong> that can worsen both the symptoms and function.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Kl-Iud5_","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"38_vulval_infections","title":"38.Vulval Infections","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MD0831","text":"Which of the following is ‌the causative organism of‍ vulval warts?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the causative organism of vulval warts?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Human papi‌lloma virus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes sim‌plex virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Human immunodeficiency vir‌us","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cytomeg‌alovirus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Human papilloma virus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/905551e60fef4be2b8e791d236f3ad73x529x337.JPEG","https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/206a1980657e4563b25e7bf58846fb6e.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Human papilloma virus</strong></p><p>The causative agent of <strong>vulval warts</strong> is the <strong>human papillomavirus</strong> (HPV).</p>\n<p><strong>Genital</strong> <strong>warts</strong>/<strong>venereal</strong> <strong>warts/condyloma acuminata</strong> are verrucous growths that affect the skin of the labia majora, perineum, perianal region and vulva. The lesions may be discrete or coalesce to form cauliflower-like growth. The disease is sexually transmitted. The growth of the warts is favoured by <strong>vaginal discharge,</strong> the use of <strong>oral contraceptives,</strong> and <strong>pregnancy.</strong></p>\n<p>HPV causes structural changes in the epithelial cells of the skin called <strong>koilocytes,</strong> and their presence is characteristic of HPV infection. The koilocytes have large <strong>hyperchromatic nuclei</strong> and a <strong>perinuclear halo</strong>, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The diagnosis is made by <strong>colposcopic studies.</strong> The lesions on the vulva are stained by <strong>toluidine blue.</strong> The abnormal areas of the skin retain the blue dye. These areas are then biopsied for histopathological examination.</p>\n<p>The given image below shows condyloma acuminata:</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2881","text":"A young woman presented wi‌th the following lesion o‍n her vulva. What is the ‍most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young woman presented with the following lesion on her vulva. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Condyloma‌ acuminata","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Molluscum ‌contagiosum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Verruca ‌vulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Condylo‌ma lata","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Condyloma acuminata","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0b5d63259bcc400e9c32a35780533b8f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3b9634f0bc24430e84c97a4eed685ce0x1280x1208.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/26c64ceecc3a454a8fa1623968acae34x720x407.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3e9d1652d0484f518feed2cfe87df424x284x243.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Condyloma acuminata</strong></p><p>The given image is suggestive of <strong>condyloma acuminata</strong> that shows <strong>cauliflower-like</strong> masses of tissue on and around the female <strong>anogenital</strong> region. </p>\n<p><strong>Condyloma acuminatum</strong> is an epidermal manifestation of the <strong>human papillomavirus </strong>(HPV). </p>\n<p><strong>HPV</strong> is transmitted through contact with <strong>infected skin or mucosa</strong>. The virus invades the cells of the epidermal basal layer through <strong>microabrasions.</strong> However, <strong>anogenital HPV</strong> infection is almost always acquired through <strong>sexual contact. </strong></p>\n<p>Warts are not required for transmission but are highly infectious because of their high viral load.</p>\n<p>Option B: The image given below shows <strong>shiny, pearly white, umbilicated</strong> papules with a characteristic <strong>dimple at the centre.</strong> These are seen in <strong>molluscum contagiosum </strong>caused by the <strong>pox virus.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: The image given below shows <strong>firm papules </strong>with a<strong> rough horny surface</strong>. These are seen in verruca vulgaris caused by the human papillomavirus type 2.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: The image given below shows <strong>condyloma lata</strong> which is the characteristic feature of <strong>secondary</strong> <strong>syphilis</strong>. It is caused by <strong><em>Treponema pallidum</em></strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2882","text":"A sexually active woman pr‌esents with cauliflower-l‍ike lesions on the vulva.‍ The biopsy of the lesion‍ is taken and histopathol​ogy reveals the following​ picture. What is the mos‌t likely causative organi‍sm?","raw_text":"A sexually active woman presents with cauliflower-like lesions on the vulva. The biopsy of the lesion is taken and histopathology reveals the following picture. What is the most likely causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pox ‌virus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Human papi‌lloma virus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Herpes simplex virus type ‌1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herpes simplex virus type ‌2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Human papilloma virus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9a96b9f1d268474ab7c795ce963a69a4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Human papilloma virus</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>koilocytes, </strong>a characteristic feature of infection with<strong> human papillomavirus </strong>(HPV).</p>\n<p>Other features that can also be seen on histology are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Perinuclear halo</li>\n<li>Multinucleation</li>\n<li>Orangeophilic cytoplasm</li>\n<li>Acanthosis</li>\n<li>Chronic inflammatory infiltrate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Henderson-Paterson bodies</strong> are seen in <strong>molluscum contagiosum,</strong> caused by <strong>poxvirus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C and D: <strong>Cowdry bodies</strong> are seen in <strong>herpes infection</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2883","text":"Which of the following ser‌otypes are present in the‍ bivalent vaccine against‍ the human papillomavirus‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following serotypes are present in the bivalent vaccine against the human papillomavirus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 an‌d 11","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"16 a‌nd 18","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"11 a‌nd 16","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6 an‌d 18","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 16 and 18","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 16 and 18</strong></p><p>Serotypes <strong>16 and 18 </strong>are present in the <strong>bivalent vaccine</strong> against the <strong>human papillomavirus </strong>(HPV)<strong>.</strong> It is called<strong> Cervarix.</strong> Cervarix is given at 0, 1 and 6 months.</p>\n<p><strong>Gardasil/Silgard</strong> is the <strong>quadrivalent vaccine</strong> against HPV. It has serotypes 6, 11, 16 and 18 and is given at 0, 2 and 6 months.</p>\n<p><strong>Gardasil 9</strong> is the<strong> nonavalent vaccine</strong> against HPV. It has serotypes 6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52 and 58. In 2020 it has received an expanded indication which now includes the <strong>prevention of</strong> <strong>oropharyngeal</strong> and <strong>head and neck cancers.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2884","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a treatment option fo‍r a patient with genital ‍warts?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a treatment option for a patient with genital warts?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Local application of 25% p‌odophyllin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Local application of imiqu‌imod cream","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intralesional interferon i‌njection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Local application of 5% tr‌ichloroacetic acid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Local application of 5% trichloroacetic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Local application of 5% trichloroacetic acid</strong></p><p><strong>Genital warts</strong> are treated by a local application of <strong>25% trichloracetic acid</strong> +<strong> 5% fluorouracil.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> of genital warts includes the <strong>local application</strong> of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Podophyllin 25% in alcohol/podophyllin 20% in tincture benzoin daily for 4 days (or)</li>\n<li>25% trichloracetic acid +5% fluorouracil daily for 4 days</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If this fails, the following treatment is given:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Interferon ointment/cream or intralesional interferon injection </li>\n<li>Imiquimod cream thrice a week for 4 months</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If medical treatment fails then cryosurgery, diathermy or laser removal is done.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2888","text":"A sexually active woman wh‌o presented with an ulcer‍ over the vulva was diagn‍osed with primary syphili‍s. What is the characteri​stic feature associated w​ith this condition?","raw_text":"A sexually active woman who presented with an ulcer over the vulva was diagnosed with primary syphilis. What is the characteristic feature associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non suppurative, tender, a‌nd enlarged nodes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Firm, shotty, and painless‌ nodes that do not suppur‍ate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Painful, tender, and matte‌d nodes forming an absces‍s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Painful, inflamed, necroti‌c nodes developing a chro‍nic sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Firm, shotty, and painless nodes that do not suppurate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Firm, shotty, and painless nodes that do not suppurate</strong></p><p>The characteristic feature associated with <strong>primary syphilis</strong> is<strong> firm, shotty, painless nodes</strong> that <strong>do not suppurate</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Syphilis</strong> is a sexually transmitted infection caused by the motile spirochete, <strong><em>Treponema pallidum</em></strong>. In <strong>primary syphilis</strong>, the classical lesion i.e.,  <strong>chancre</strong> appears within 9–90 days from the first exposure.</p>\n<p>The macular lesion becomes papular and then ulcerates. The <strong>ulcer</strong>(s) is<strong> painless</strong> and firm, with a punched out base and rolled edges. If left unattended, these <strong>heal </strong>within <strong>3–9 weeks</strong>. It is accompanied by <strong>painless </strong>ingui­nal discrete<strong> lymphadenopathy. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2890","text":"A 32-year-old woman known ‌to have syphilis presente‍d with the following lesi‍ons over the vulva. What ‍is the stage of the disea​se?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old woman known to have syphilis presented with the following lesions over the vulva. What is the stage of the disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pri‌mary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Seco‌ndary","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tert‌iary","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lat‌ent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Secondary","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d883750a499b46ae8c0ad91dcb1f8300.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Secondary</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>condyloma lata,</strong> a classic finding of <strong>secondary syphilis.</strong></p>\n<p>Multiple moist well-demarcated papules and plaques are seen in condyloma lata. They are highly <strong>contagious,</strong> exophytic broad excrescences that ulcer­ate and are commonly seen on the vulva, perianal area and upper thighs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2897","text":"A sexually active patient ‌was diagnosed with lympho‍granuloma venereum. What ‍is the causative organism‍ for this condition?","raw_text":"A sexually active patient was diagnosed with lymphogranuloma venereum. What is the causative organism for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Klebsiella ‌granulomatis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chlamydia ‌trachomatis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Treponema‌ pallidum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herpes‌ virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chlamydia trachomatis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e134bdc96caf4189b26439cdc351ece3x720x472.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chlamydia trachomatis</strong></p><p>The causative agent for <strong>lymphogranuloma venereum </strong>is<strong> <em>Chlamydia trachomatis </em></strong>(serotypes L1/L2/L3)<em>. </em>Lymphogranuloma venereum (LGV) is a sexually transmitted disease that causes genital ulcers.</p>\n<p>It has the following three stages of infection: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 314px; width: 318px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131.847px;\">Stage of LGV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.748px;\">Characteristic features </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131.847px;\">Primary stage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.748px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Painless genital ulcer</li>\n<li>Papules</li>\n<li>Lymphangitis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131.847px;\">Secondary stage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.748px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Painful unilateral/bilateral lymphadenopathy </li>\n<li>Buboes</li>\n<li>Groove sign</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131.847px;\">Late stage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.748px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Stricture formation</li>\n<li>Fibrosis and fistulae of the anogenital region</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em> is a small gram-negative bacte­rium and an obligate intracellular parasite that appears as an intracytoplasmic inclusion body. The gram stain of the cervical smear would show leucocytes but no organisms. Hence, selective culture media like the<strong> McCoy cells culture</strong> is used to isolate the organism.</p>\n<p>The slide given below is an iodine-stained smear prepared from the patient's endocervical sample. It shows <strong>inclusion bodies</strong> characteristic of <strong><em>Chlamydia trachomatis.</em></strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2895","text":"A 26-year-old sexually act‌ive man comes to the STD ‍clinic with a painful gen‍ital ulcer. On examinatio‍n, the ulcer and the righ​t inguinal lymph nodes we​re tender. What is the ca‌usative organism?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old sexually active man comes to the STD clinic with a painful genital ulcer. On examination, the ulcer and the right inguinal lymph nodes were tender. What is the causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Treponema‌ pallidum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Haemophil‌us ducreyi","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chlamydia ‌trachomatis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Treponema‌ pertenue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Haemophilus ducreyi","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/49766b8a97314a67b0b6094bc6644d15x800x1224.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5d6e028ac69346b691cc1e29b44acd2ex720x478.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Haemophilus ducreyi</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>chancroid</strong> caused by<strong> </strong><em><strong>Haemophilus ducreyi </strong></em>which is a gram-negative anaerobe<em>.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Chancroid</strong> is <strong>symptomatic</strong> in<strong> men.</strong> Both<strong> </strong>ulcer and lymphadenopathy are painful. Lymphadenopathy in chancroid is either unilateral or bilateral. <strong>Chancroid</strong> is generally<strong> asymptomatic</strong> in <strong>women</strong> but sometimes presents as painful lymphadenopathy.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a chancroid ulcer with purulent and haemorrhagic discharge. It has a non-indurated, erythematous base.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2898","text":"A sexually active man pres‌ents with complaints of p‍ainful swelling over the ‍inguinal region. He also ‍gives a history of a pain​less genital ulcer that r​esolved spontaneously. On‌ examination, groove sign‍ is seen. What is the lik‌ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A sexually active man presents with complaints of painful swelling over the inguinal region. He also gives a history of a painless genital ulcer that resolved spontaneously. On examination,  groove sign is seen. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Granuloma‌ inguinale","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphogranu‌loma venerum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Syph‌ilis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chan‌croid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lymphogranuloma venerum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6bd6ef8c8ede4fb48156289d3a853efax720x1016.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lymphogranuloma venerum</strong></p><p>The pathognomic <strong>groove sign of Greenblatt </strong>is suggestive of <strong>lymphogranuloma venereum</strong> (LGV) caused by <strong><em>Chlamydia</em> <em>trachomatis</em></strong> (serovars L1, L2, L3).</p>\n<p>This image shows groove sign which is seen in ⅓ of the cases in the secondary stage of LGV. It refers to the depression between the enlarged femoral and inguinal lymph nodes on either side of the inguinal ligament. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Lymphogranuloma venereum (LGV) is a sexually transmitted disease that causes genital ulcers. It has the following three stages of infection: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 314px; width: 318px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131.847px;\">Stage of LGV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.748px;\">Characteristic features </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131.847px;\">Primary stage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.748px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Painless genital ulcer</li>\n<li>Papules</li>\n<li>Lymphangitis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131.847px;\">Secondary stage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.748px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Painful unilateral/bilateral lymphadenopathy </li>\n<li>Buboes</li>\n<li>Groove sign</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 131.847px;\">Late stage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.748px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Stricture formation</li>\n<li>Fibrosis and fistulae of the anogenital region</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2900","text":"A 20-year-old female sex w‌orker presents with compl‍aints of constipation and‍ blood in stools for 3 mo‍nths. On DRE, a circumfer​ential constriction is fe​lt beyond which the finge‌r cannot be advanced. Whi‍ch of the following is a ‌probable cause for this c​omplication?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old female sex worker presents with complaints of constipation and blood in stools for 3 months. On DRE, a circumferential constriction is felt beyond which the finger cannot be advanced. Which of the following is a probable cause for this complication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Granuloma‌ inguinale","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphogranu‌loma venerum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Syph‌ilis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chan‌croid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lymphogranuloma venerum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lymphogranuloma venerum</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>rectal stricture </strong>which is a complication of<strong> lymphogranuloma venereum</strong> (LGV). </p>\n<p><strong>LGV</strong> is a sexually transmitted disease caused by<strong><em> Chlamydia trachomatis </em></strong>(serotypes L1/L2/L3).<strong> Rectal involvement</strong> is commonly<strong> seen</strong> in <strong>women </strong>as the<strong> primary disease</strong> often goes<strong> unnoticed</strong> and untreated. Anorectal involvement causes proctocolitis and causes rectal strictures. This occurs due to the contigu­ous spread of the primary infection from the perirectal nodes leading to scar tissue formation.</p>\n<p>The<strong> complications</strong> of<strong> lymphogranuloma venereum</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rectal stricture formation may lead to:\n<ul>\n<li>Intestinal obstruction</li>\n<li>Rectovaginal fistulae</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Proctitis</li>\n<li>Vulvar cancer.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2903","text":"What is the causative agen‌t for granuloma inguinale‍?","raw_text":"What is the causative agent for granuloma inguinale?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calymmatobacterium granulo‌matis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chlamydia ‌trachomatis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ureaplasma ‌urealyticum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Treponema‌ pallidum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Calymmatobacterium granulomatis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/24ffcad93e034648b4db990f0c8ba1a7x481x359.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Calymmatobacterium granulomatis</strong></p><p>The causative agent for <strong>granuloma inguinale </strong>(donovanosis) is <strong><em>Calym­matobacterium granulomatis</em>. </strong>It is now called<strong> <em>Klebsiella granulomatis </em></strong>and is a gram-negative bacillus causing chronic ulcerative infection of the vulva.</p>\n<p>It is highly contagious and transmitted through repeated sexual or non-sexual contact. The incubation period is <strong>1-12 weeks.</strong></p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<p>It begins as a <strong>painless nodule</strong> which later ulcerates to form multiple <strong>beefy red painless ulcers</strong> that tend to coalesce; the vulva is progressively destroyed, and minimal adenopathy may occur. </p>\n<p>Diagnosis: </p>\n<p>Microscopic examination of smears from the lesion/ biopsy specimens reveals pathognomonic <strong>intracytoplasmic Donovan bodies </strong>and clusters of bacteria with a <strong>bipolar (safety pin) appearance</strong>. Blue-black stained organisms are seen in the cytoplasm of mononuclear cells. </p>\n<p>Management:</p>\n<p><strong>Azithromycin</strong> 1 g orally once per week or 500 mg daily for at least 3 weeks and until all lesions have completely healed.</p>\n<p>The given image below shows the donovanosis in a female patient with a beefy red ulcer:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2904","text":"A 19-year-old girl present‌ed with multiple beefy-re‍d ulcers over the labia. ‍Microscopic examination o‍f smear from the lesion s​howed intracytoplasmic in​clusion bodies and gram-n‌egative bacteria having a‍ safety pin appearance. W‌hat is your likely diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A 19-year-old girl presented with multiple beefy-red ulcers over the labia. Microscopic examination of smear from the lesion showed intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies and gram-negative bacteria having a safety pin appearance. What is your likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymphogranul‌oma inguinale","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Granuloma‌ inguinale","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chan‌croid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Syph‌ilis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Granuloma inguinale","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ef333cd2c28f4026a53440fc36b4c2c9x700x1063.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/225be4706ab24374b8573e6b63cc1743x600x394.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Granuloma inguinale</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>granuloma inguinale </strong>which is caused by a gram-negative bacteria,<strong> <em>Klebsiella granulomatis.</em></strong></p>\n<p>Granuloma inguinale begins as a <strong>painless nodule</strong> that later ulcerates to form multiple<strong> beefy red painless ulcers. </strong>These ulcers tend to<strong> coalesce</strong> and bleed easily on contact. If secondarily infected, they may become painful. The ulcers heal by fibrosis, which can result in scarring resembling keloids.</p>\n<p>The <strong>lymph nodes</strong> are usually <strong>uninvolved</strong> but can become enlarged. New lesions can appear along the lymphatic drainage channels.</p>\n<p>The gross image below shows granuloma inguinale of the cervix with a beefy red appearance of the hypervascular intravaginal tissues. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The microscopic image below shows the pathognomonic intracytoplasmic <strong>Donovan bodies. </strong>It also shows<strong> </strong>clusters of <strong>gram-negative</strong> bacteria with a <strong>bipolar/safety-pin</strong> appearance.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"-Qzp-FRJ","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"38_pediatric_hematology___introduction__bleeding__and_clotting_disorders","title":"38.Pediatric Hematology   Introduction, Bleeding, And Clotting Disorders","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MC3032","text":"Which of the following is ‌the predominant site of h​ematopoiesis in a 20-week‌s old fetus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the predominant site of hematopoiesis in a 20-weeks old fetus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yolk‌ sac","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Li‌ver","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Spl‌een","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bone ‌marrow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Liver</strong></p><p>The <strong>liver</strong> is the predominant site of <strong>hematopoiesis</strong> in the fetus during <strong>mid-pregnancy.</strong></p>\n<p>Sites of hematopoiesis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>2–6 weeks- yolk sac (mesoblastic phase)</li>\n<li>8–24 weeks- liver (hepatic phase)</li>\n<li>28 weeks onwards- bone marrow (myeloid phase)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>:</p>\n<p>There is some overlap between the different phases of hematopoiesis. The ranges given above refer to the predominant site of hematopoiesis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3031","text":"Electrophoresis and globin‌ gene sequencing are perf​ormed on a normal embryo'‌s blood sample as a part ‌of hematology research. W‍hich of the following typ​e of hemoglobin is likely​ to be present?","raw_text":"Electrophoresis and globin gene sequencing are performed on a normal embryo's blood sample as a part of hematology research. Which of the following type of hemoglobin is likely to be present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HbM ‌Iwate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hb Po‌rtland","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hb Au‌ckland","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HbM B‌oston","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hb Portland","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hb Portland</strong></p><p><strong>Hb Portland</strong> is likely to be present as it is <strong>embryonic hemoglobin. </strong></p>\n<p><br/><strong>Gower-I </strong>(ζ<sub>2</sub>ε<sub>2</sub>),<strong> Gower-II</strong> (α<sub>2</sub>ε<sub>2</sub>), and<strong> Hb Portland </strong>(ζ<sub>2</sub>γ<sub>2</sub>) are called <strong>embryonic hemoglobins</strong> and are seen at 8 weeks of intrauterine life.</p>\n<p><strong>HbM Iwate</strong> (option A)<strong> </strong>and <strong>Hb Auckland</strong> (option C)<strong> </strong>and <strong>HbM Boston </strong>(option D) result from globin mutations that stabilize iron in the ferric state, leading to <strong>congenital methemoglobinemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3030","text":"When does hemoglobin synth‌esis switch from HbF to H​bA?","raw_text":"When does hemoglobin synthesis switch from HbF to HbA?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"At 8 weeks of intrauterine‌ life","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"At 24 weeks of intrauterin‌e life","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"At 38 weeks of intrauterin‌e life","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"At 6 months of postnatal l‌ife","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. At 38 weeks of intrauterine life","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3c30b1dcb774457a5773f2dbabc43d0x1280x1385.6880733944954.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. At 38 weeks of intrauterine life</strong></p><p>Hemoglobin synthesis <strong>switches</strong> from <strong>HbF</strong> to <strong>HbA</strong> at <strong>38</strong> <strong>weeks</strong> of intrauterine life.</p>\n<p>Hemoglobin types at various periods:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 655px;\" width=\"316\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.688px;\"><strong>Time</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.91px;\"><strong>Major hemoglobin</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.688px;\">8th week of intrauterine life:</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.91px;\">Embryonic hemoglobins, Gower-1(ζ<sub>2</sub>ε<sub>2</sub>), Gower-2 (α<sub>2</sub>ε<sub>2</sub>), and Portland(ζ<sub>2</sub>γ<sub>2</sub>) are formed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.688px;\">9th week to birth</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.91px;\">Fetal hemoglobin (HbF) (α<sub>2</sub>γ<sub>2</sub>) is the major hemoglobin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.688px;\">24th week</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.91px;\">HbF constitutes 90% of the total hemoglobin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.688px;\"> 38 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.91px;\">The switch to the nearly exclusive synthesis of adult hemoglobin (HbA; α<sub>2</sub>β<sub>2</sub>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.688px;\"> At birth<br/> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.91px;\"> HbF constitutes 70% of the total hemoglobin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.688px;\">6 month</td>\n<td style=\"width: 166.91px;\"> adult pattern HbA &gt; 95%, HbA<sub>2 </sub>&lt; 3.5%, HbF &lt; 2.5%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chromosome 16 contains alpha (α) gene cluster (3 genes):\n<ul>\n<li>zeta (ζ)</li>\n<li>alpha 1 (α1)</li>\n<li>alpha 2 (α2)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Chromosome 11contains beta (β) gene cluster (5 genes):\n<ul>\n<li>epsilon (ε)</li>\n<li>2 gamma(γ)</li>\n<li>delta (δ)</li>\n<li>beta (β)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Changes in globin subunits from embryo to early infancy:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3033","text":"Hemoglobin fractions were ‌analyzed in a neonate wit​h a family history of sic‌kle cell disease. The tes‌t shows elevated levels o‍f fetal hemoglobin (HbF).​ Which of the following i​s true about HbF?","raw_text":"Hemoglobin fractions were analyzed in a neonate with a family history of sickle cell disease. The test shows elevated levels of fetal hemoglobin (HbF). Which of the following is true about HbF?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HbF accounts for 100% of t‌he total hemoglobin at te​rm.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HbF is resistant to alkali‌ denaturation.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"HbF has lower affinity for‌ oxygen than HbA.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HbF has higher affinity fo‌r 2,3 bisphosphoglycerate​ than HbA.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. HbF is resistant to alkali denaturation.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. HbF is resistant to alkali denaturation.</strong></p><p><strong>HbF</strong> is <strong>resistant</strong> to <strong>alkali denaturation.</strong></p>\n<p>Elevated levels of HbF are seen in <strong>sickle cell disease</strong> and<strong> β-thalassemia</strong>, where there is a mutation of the β globin gene. Increased HbF levels persist in childhood and later. </p>\n<p><strong>Properties of fetal hemoglobin (HbF)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>HbF is resistant to alkali denaturation.\n<ul>\n<li>This property forms the basis of the<strong> alkali denaturation test (or Apt test)</strong> to detect the presence of fetal blood in maternal circulation in suspected feto-maternal hemorrhage.</li>\n<li>The <strong>Kleihauer–Betke test (or acid elution test)</strong> is used to quantitate the amount of feto-maternal hemorrhage to calculate the dosage of anti-D required in Rh-negative mothers.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>HBF accounts for <strong>70%</strong> of the hemoglobin at term.\n<ul>\n<li>Increased HbF can be seen in hereditary persistence of fetal hemoglobin, sickle cell anemia, beta-thalassemia, HbC disease, and HbE disease</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>HbF has a <strong>higher affinity</strong> for oxygen.\n<ul>\n<li>This allows for gas exchange from maternal circulation by binding to oxygen at a relatively low oxygen tension in the maternal blood supplying the chorion.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>HbF has <strong>lower  affinity for 2,3 bisphosphoglycerate (BPG) than HbA</strong>.\n<ul>\n<li>Lack of interaction between HbF and 2,3 BPG increases the affinity of HbF to oxygen.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3062","text":"You are measuring the life‌ span of RBCs in a normal​ neonate using chromium-l‌abeled erythrocyte studie‌s as a part of a research‍ project under the clinic​al hematology department.​ What would be the result​?","raw_text":"You are measuring the life span of RBCs in a normal neonate using chromium-labeled erythrocyte studies as a part of a research project under the clinical hematology department. What would be the result?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"90-12‌0 days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"60-90‌ days","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"45-60‌ days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30-45‌ days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 60-90 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 60-90 days</strong></p><p>The lifespan of <strong>neonatal RBCs</strong> is <strong>60-90 days</strong>.</p>\n<p>The short lifespan of neonatal RBCs is the cause for rapid fall in hematocrit immediately after birth. The highest hematocrit during a person’s lifetime occurs at birth, and the lowest occurs about 10 weeks later. </p>\n<p>The lifespan of RBCs in preterm infants is <strong>35-50</strong> days.</p>\n<p>The average lifespan of RBCs in adults is <strong>120</strong> days.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3145","text":"A concerned mother brings ‌her preschool child to yo​u. She states that her ch‌ild was fine until yester‌day night and now is cove‍red with bruises and purp​le dots, which she had no​ticed while changing his ​clothes in the morning. S​he informs you that one m‌onth back, her child had ​a cough and cold. Finding‌s on physical examination​ are normal, other than p‌etechiae and purpura. Whi​ch of the following is mo‌st likely to be seen whil‍e investigating this chil​d?","raw_text":"A concerned mother brings her preschool child to you. She states that her child was fine until yesterday night and now is covered with bruises and purple dots, which she had noticed while changing his clothes in the morning. She informs you that one month back, her child had a cough and cold. Findings on physical examination are normal, other than petechiae and purpura. Which of the following is most likely to be seen while investigating this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal pla‌telet count","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prolonged prothrombin time‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prolonged activated partia‌l thromboplastin time","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prolonged b‌leeding time","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prolonged bleeding time","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prolonged bleeding time</strong></p><p>An otherwise <strong>well-baby</strong> with acute onset of <strong>petechiae and purpura</strong> with a preceding <strong>history of viral infection</strong> 1 to 4 weeks prior to the onset of thrombocytopenia is a classical presentation of <strong>(autoimmune) idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura. Prolonged bleeding time</strong> is a feature of ITP.</p>\n<p>The <strong>most common cause</strong> of<strong> acute onset</strong> of <strong>thrombocytopenia</strong> in an otherwise <strong>well-child</strong> is (autoimmune) idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura (<strong>ITP</strong>).</p>\n<p>ITP is a bleeding disorder and has <strong>prolonged bleeding time, low platelet count, and normal coagulation profile</strong> (prothrombin time [PT] and activated partial thromboplastin time [aPTT]).</p>\n<p>ITP has an acute presentation with a<strong> self-limited course</strong> in children.</p>\n<p>Features of ITP:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Commonly affects children between 1-4 years </li>\n<li>Preceded by a viral infection</li>\n<li>Mucocutaneous bleeding</li>\n<li>Ecchymoses and petechiae</li>\n<li>Wet purpura (blood blisters in the mouth)</li>\n<li>Retinal hemorrhages</li>\n<li>Bleeding into the CNS, which may be life-threatening</li>\n<li><strong>Hepatomegaly, splenomegaly, and lymphadenopathy, fever, jaundice are absent</strong></li>\n<li>Resolves spontaneously in 80% of the cases in 4-6 weeks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Laboratory features of ITP:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thrombocytopenia (commonly &lt;20,000/μL)</li>\n<li>Normal/Increased megakaryocytes in bone marrow</li>\n<li>Prolonged bleeding time</li>\n<li>Normal tests for coagulation (PT/aPTT/INR)</li>\n<li>Eosinophilia and lymphocytosis common</li>\n<li>Peripheral smear is normal</li>\n<li>Bone marrow biopsy shows megakaryocytes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3146","text":"A pediatrician sees a chil‌d in the OPD. He has brui​sing, petechiae, and occa‌sional minor epistaxis, w‌hich has very little inte‍rference with his daily a​ctivities. Based on furth​er workup, he diagnoses t​he child as a case of mil​d ITP. How will he manage‌ this child?","raw_text":"A pediatrician sees a child in the OPD. He has bruising, petechiae, and occasional minor epistaxis, which has very little interference with his daily activities. Based on further workup, he diagnoses the child as a case of mild ITP. How will he manage this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Short course corticosteroi‌d therapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intravenous anti-D adminis‌tration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A single dose of intraveno‌us immunoglobulin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Education, counseling and ‌watchful expectancy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Education, counseling and watchful expectancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Education, counseling and watchful expectancy</strong></p><p>Other than <strong>education</strong>,<strong> counseling, and watchful expectancy, no therapy </strong>is needed for patients with <strong>mild ITP. </strong></p>\n<p>Classification system for ITP:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>No symptoms.</li>\n<li>Mild symptoms- bruising and petechiae, and occasional minor epistaxis which has very little interference with daily living.</li>\n<li>Moderate symptoms- more severe skin and mucosal lesions, more troublesome epistaxis, and menorrhagia.</li>\n<li>Severe symptoms- bleeding episodes - menorrhagia, epistaxis, and melena - requiring transfusions or hospitalization, symptoms interfering seriously with quality of life. </li>\n</ol>\n<p>Treatment of ITP:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mild to moderate</strong> bleeding symptoms- <strong>watchful expectancy, education, and counseling. </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Platelet count &lt; 30,000</strong> <strong>+ risk factors</strong> for bleeding (antiplatelet drugs/anticoagulants or concomitant bleeding disorders)- <strong>oral glucocorticoids</strong>/ intravenous immunoglobulin <strong>(IVIG) or IV anti-D</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Severe</strong> bleeding symptoms-<strong> IV methylprednisolone ± IVIG or IV anti-D</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Life-threatening bleeding</strong> (eg. intracranial hemorrhage)-<strong> platelet transfusion + IV methylprednisolone + IVIG or IV anti-D</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p>The<strong> role of splenectomy</strong> in ITP should be <strong>reserved for 2 circumstances</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The <strong>older child</strong> (≥4 yr) with <strong>severe ITP that has lasted &gt;1 year</strong> (chronic ITP) and whose symptoms are <strong>not easily controlled with therapy</strong> </li>\n<li>When<strong> life-threatening hemorrhage</strong> (ICH) complicates acute ITP <strong>and</strong> the <strong>platelet count cannot be corrected rapidly</strong> with transfusion of platelets and administration of IVIG and corticosteroids.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3141","text":"Veena, an adolescent girl ‌has had recurrent episode​s of bleeding from her no‌se and gums. She also giv‌es a history of heavy men‍strual bleeding and repea​ted blood transfusions. H​er bleeding time is deran​ged and flow cytometry re​veals markedly decreased ‌CD41 and CD61 expression.​ A defect in which of the‌ following is most likely​ seen in her?","raw_text":"Veena, an adolescent girl has had recurrent episodes of bleeding from her nose and gums. She also gives a history of heavy menstrual bleeding and repeated blood transfusions. Her bleeding time is deranged and flow cytometry reveals markedly decreased CD41 and CD61 expression. A defect in which of the following is most likely seen in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Platelet‌ adhesion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Platelet ‌activation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Platelet a‌ggregation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Platele‌t count","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Platelet aggregation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Platelet aggregation</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> Glanzmann's thrombasthenia</strong> which is characterized by <strong>defective platelet aggregation.</strong></p>\n<p>Decreased <strong>CD41 (GPIIb)</strong> and <strong>CD61 (GPIIIa</strong>) expression are diagnostic of Glanzmann's thrombasthenia. Bleeding in Glanzmann thrombasthenia is quite <strong>severe</strong> and is typically <strong>mucocutaneous</strong>, including epistaxis, gingival, and GI bleeding.</p>\n<p>Bernard-Soulier syndrome and Glanzmann thrombasthenia are severe congenital disorders of platelet function. Patients usually present with petechiae and purpura at birth. The diagnosis is confirmed by <strong>flow cytometric analysis</strong> of the patient’s platelet glycoproteins.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3147","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common congenita​l bleeding disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common congenital bleeding disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemoph‌ilia A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemoph‌ilia B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Von Willebr‌and disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Factor VII‌ deficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Von Willebrand disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Von Willebrand disease</strong></p><p><strong>Von Willebrand disease </strong>(VWD)<strong> </strong>is the <strong>most common</strong> congenital bleeding disorder.</p>\n<p>VWD is thought to affect approximately 0.1- 1% of the population. It is due to abnormal von Willebrand factor (VWF).</p>\n<p>VWF is an adhesive protein that acts as a bridge between subendothelial collagen and platelets. It also binds and protects the circulating factor VIII from rapid clearance in circulation.</p>\n<p>Von Willebrand disease is mostly inherited as an <strong>autosomal dominant trait</strong> (rarely autosomal recessive). The types of disease include <strong>quantitatively deficient vWF (partial = type 1 or absolute = type 3)</strong> or<strong> qualitatively abnormal vWF (type 2 = dysproteinemia).</strong> The majority (~ 80%) have type 1 disease. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Hemophilia A (factor 8 deficiency) occurs in 1 in 5,000 males. Hemophilia B (factor 9 deficiency) occurs in approximately 1 in 25,000 males.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3258","text":"Shortly after birth throug‌h normal vaginal delivery​, a neonate develops pete‌chiae and purpura. You su‌spect bleeding disorder a‍nd send samples for furth​er workup. A prolonged bl​eeding time, normal plate​let count, normal aPTT is​ seen along with large pl‌atelets in the peripheral​ smear. What is the likel‌y diagnosis?","raw_text":"Shortly after birth through normal vaginal delivery, a neonate develops petechiae and purpura. You suspect bleeding disorder and send samples for further workup. A prolonged bleeding time, normal platelet count, normal aPTT is seen along with large platelets in the peripheral smear. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bernard-Soul‌ier syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Glanzmann's thrombasthenia‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"von Willebr‌and disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Evans s‌yndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bernard-Soulier syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bernard-Soulier syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Bernard–Soulier syndrome</strong> is the likely diagnosis in the given clinical scenario.</p>\n<p>The presence of <strong>petechiae</strong> and <strong>purpura</strong> at <strong>birth,</strong> especially after <strong>vaginal delivery,</strong> along with<strong> prolonged bleeding time</strong>, normal platelet count, normal aPTT are seen in <strong>qualitative platelet defect disorders</strong> like Bernard-Soulier syndrome and Glanzmann's thrombasthenia.</p>\n<p>The presence of<strong> large platelets</strong> rules out Glanzmann's thrombasthenia. Evans syndrome is an autoimmune hemolytic disorder with thrombocytopenia. Von Willebrand's disease doesn't usually manifest at birth. </p>\n<p>Bernard–Soulier syndrome is an autosomal recessive functional/qualitative platelet disorder. It is characterized by the <strong>deficiency of</strong> <strong>Gp Ib-IX receptor</strong> causing defective platelet adhesion. It is due to genetic mutations in the genes forming the GPIb complex of glycoproteins Ibα, Ibβ, V, and IX.</p>\n<p>Laboratory parameters:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal platelet count (or mild thrombocytopenia)</li>\n<li>Large platelets (megathrombocytes) with dense granules</li>\n<li>Bleeding time abnormally prolonged</li>\n<li>Platelet aggregation tests are normal (with collagen, adenosine diphosphate [ADP], epinephrine)</li>\n<li>Ristocetin aggregation test is abnormal/no response</li>\n<li>von Willebrand factor (vWF) levels are normal.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3257","text":"A panicked father brings h‌is adopted daughter to yo​ur OPD with complaints of‌ bleeding gums. Hematolog‌ical investigations revea‍l a normal platelet count​ and a prolonged bleeding​ time. The ristocetin-agg​regation test was abnorma​l. Which of the following‌ would you include in you​r differential diagnosis?‌","raw_text":"A panicked father brings his adopted daughter to your OPD with complaints of bleeding gums. Hematological investigations reveal a normal platelet count and a prolonged bleeding time. The ristocetin-aggregation test was abnormal. Which of the following would you include in your differential diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"von Willebr‌and disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bernard-Soul‌ier syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glanzmann th‌rombasthenia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both A‌ and B","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Both A and B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Both A and B</strong></p><p>The<strong> ristocetin-aggregation</strong> test is <strong>abnormal </strong>in both<strong> von Willebrand disease</strong> and <strong>Bernard–Soulier syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>All of the above diseases are inherited bleeding disorders affecting <strong>platelet function</strong>. Hence, there is<strong> prolonged bleeding time</strong> but platelet levels are not significantly affected. </p>\n<p>Platelet aggregation tests use a panel of platelet agonists (adenosine diphosphate [ADP], epinephrine, collagen, and ristocetin) to measure platelet activation and aggregation in vitro.</p>\n<p><strong>Ristocetin induces</strong> the <strong>binding of VWF to platelets</strong> and agglutinates platelets.</p>\n<p>Bernard-Soulier syndrome, a severe congenital platelet function disorder, is caused by an absence or severe deficiency of the VWF receptor on the platelet membrane. Hence, in <strong>Bernard–Soulier syndrome,</strong> <strong>no response</strong> (or no aggregation) is seen with ristocetin.</p>\n<p>The<strong> ristocetin-aggregation</strong> test is also <strong>abnormal</strong> in <strong>von Willebrand disease</strong>. However, it <strong>normalizes</strong> after the addition of normal <strong>plasma</strong> (which contains vWF) since there is no defect in binding receptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3252","text":"An ENT surgeon refers a ch‌ild who had developed pro​longed bleeding post-tons‌illectomy to the hematolo‌gy department for evaluat‍ion. Workup revealed a qu​alitative defect in the v​on Willebrand factor. Whi​ch of the following disea​se is more likely in this‌ patient?","raw_text":"An ENT surgeon refers a child who had developed prolonged bleeding post-tonsillectomy to the hematology department for evaluation. Workup revealed a qualitative defect in the von Willebrand factor. Which of the following disease is more likely in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 1 Von Willebrand dise‌ase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type 2 Von Willebrand dise‌ase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Type 3 Von Willebrand dise‌ase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type 4 Von Willebrand dise‌ase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type 2 Von Willebrand disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type 2 Von Willebrand disease</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> von Willebrand disease </strong>(VWD) and a <strong>qualitative defect</strong> in von Willebrand factor (VWF) is seen in <strong>type 2</strong> <strong>VWD</strong>.</p>\n<p>Features of VWD:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most common type is Type 1</li>\n<li>Most severe type is Type 3</li>\n<li>Most common inheritance: Autosomal dominant</li>\n<li>Type 1- partial quantitative deficiency of VWF </li>\n<li>Type 2- qualitative deficiency of VWF</li>\n<li>Type 3- complete quantitative deficiency of VWF</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There is no type 4 VWD. </p>\n<p>Characteristics of the subtypes of VWD are summarized below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 169px; width: 358px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Type</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Inheritance</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 198px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mechanism</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Important characteristics</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\">AD</td>\n<td style=\"width: 198px; text-align: center;\">Partial, quantitative deficiency of VWF</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Most common type</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\">AR</td>\n<td style=\"width: 198px; text-align: center;\">Complete quantitative deficiency</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\">Most severe VWD</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\">2A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\">AD</td>\n<td style=\"width: 198px; text-align: center;\">Loss of VWF multimers (increased cleavage by ADAMS13)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\">Platelet adhesion defect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\">2B</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\">AD</td>\n<td style=\"width: 198px; text-align: center;\">Increased binding of VWF to platelets causing VWF-platelet complex destruction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Thrombo-cytopenia</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\">2M</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\">AD</td>\n<td style=\"width: 198px; text-align: center;\">Decreased affinity of VWF to platelets</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\">Platelet adhesion defect, very rare</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\">2N</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\"><strong>AR</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 198px; text-align: center;\">Decreased affinity of VWF to Factor VIII</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Autosomal hemophilia</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>AD: autosomal dominant</p>\n<p>AR: autosomal recessive</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3255","text":"Which type of von Willebra‌nd disease is associated ​with thrombocytopenia?","raw_text":"Which type of von Willebrand disease is associated with thrombocytopenia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e 2M","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e 2N","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‌e 2A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e 2B","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Type 2B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Type 2B</strong></p><p>von Willebrand disease associated with <strong>thrombocytopenia</strong> is <strong>Type 2B</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Pseudo-VWD:</strong> VWF is normal, but <strong>platelet</strong> has increased affinity to VWF; it presents in a manner similar to VWD 2B with thrombocytopenia.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 169px; width: 390px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 43px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Type</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 13.7628px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Inherit</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.237px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mechanism</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Important characteristics</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 43px; text-align: center;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 13.7628px; text-align: center;\">AD</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.237px; text-align: center;\">Partial, quantitative deficiency of VWF</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Most common type</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 43px; text-align: center;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 13.7628px; text-align: center;\">AR</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.237px; text-align: center;\">Complete quantitative deficiency</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px; text-align: center;\">Most severe VWD</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 43px; text-align: center;\">2A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 13.7628px; text-align: center;\">AD</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.237px; text-align: center;\">Loss of VWF multimers (increased cleavage by ADAMS13)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px; text-align: center;\">Platelet adhesion defect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 43px; text-align: center;\">2B</td>\n<td style=\"width: 13.7628px; text-align: center;\">AD</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.237px; text-align: center;\">Increased binding of VWF to platelets causing VWF-platelet complex destruction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Thrombocytopenia</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 43px; text-align: center;\">2M</td>\n<td style=\"width: 13.7628px; text-align: center;\">AD</td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.237px; text-align: center;\">Decreased affinity of VWF to platelets</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px; text-align: center;\">Platelet adhesion defect, very rare</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 43px; text-align: center;\">2N</td>\n<td style=\"width: 13.7628px; text-align: center;\"><strong>AR</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 289.237px; text-align: center;\">Decreased affinity of VWF to Factor VIII</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Autosomal hemophilia</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3144","text":"A pediatric resident admin‌isters 0.5-1 mg of phyton​adione intramuscularly to‌ all newborn infants in t‌he ward. Deficiency of wh‍ich of the following fact​ors is prevented by this ​medication?","raw_text":"A pediatric resident administers 0.5-1 mg of phytonadione intramuscularly to all newborn infants in the ward. Deficiency of which of the following factors is prevented by this medication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fact‌or V","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Facto‌r VII","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Facto‌r VIII","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fact‌or XI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Factor VII","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Factor VII</strong></p><p>Intramuscular injection of 0.5-1 mg of <strong>phytonadione</strong> is given to all <strong>newborn</strong> infants because it prevents the deficiency of<strong> factor VII </strong>that occurs transiently after birth.</p>\n<p><strong>Hemorrhagic disease of the newborn</strong> is caused by a transient deficiency of <strong>vitamin K</strong>–dependent factors,<strong> factors II, VII, IX, and X.</strong></p>\n<p>Causes include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lack of free vitamin K from the mother</li>\n<li>Absence of the bacterial intestinal flora normally responsible for the synthesis of vitamin K.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is responsive to and prevented by vitamin K therapy (0.5-1 mg of phytonadione).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9390","text":"The most common bleeding m‌anifestation seen in seve​re hemophilia is ________‌_____.","raw_text":"The most common bleeding manifestation seen in severe hemophilia is _____________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recurrent‌ hematomas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Recurrent h‌emarthrosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hema‌turia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intracrania‌l hemorrhage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Recurrent hemarthrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Recurrent hemarthrosis</strong></p><p><strong>Recurrent hemarthrosis</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> bleeding manifestation of <strong>severe</strong> hemophilia.</p>\n<p>Hemophilia is said to be severe when the residual activity of factor VIII is <strong>&lt;1%</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of hemophilia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clinically, hemophilia A and B are <strong>indistinguishable</strong>.</li>\n<li>It presents early in life- <strong>bleeding after circumcision</strong> or when beginning to walk or crawl.</li>\n<li>Most common bleeding manifestation in severe hemophilia is <strong>recurrent hemarthrosis</strong> and the most common joint affected is the<strong> knee</strong>.</li>\n<li>Acute hemarthrosis- local pain, swelling, and erythema; muscle contractures due to fixed positioning.</li>\n<li>Chronic hemarthrosis- synovial thickening and synovitis in response to intraarticular blood.</li>\n<li>Muscle hematomas can lead to compartment syndromes.</li>\n<li>Hematuria is common but self-limited.</li>\n<li>Life-threatening bleeding- bleeding to oropharyngeal spaces, CNS, and retroperitoneum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3264","text":"Which of the following fac‌tor deficiencies are matc​hed to the correct diseas‌e?","raw_text":"Which of the following factor deficiencies are matched to the correct disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Factor V deficiency - Stua‌rt-Prower disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Factor IX deficiency - Par‌ahemophilia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Factor X deficiency - Chri‌stmas disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Factor XI deficiency - Hem‌ophilia C","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Factor XI deficiency - Hemophilia C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Factor XI deficiency - Hemophilia C</strong></p><p>The correct match is f<strong>actor XI deficiency and hemophilia C</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clotting factor deficiencies and associated disorders:<br/><br/>Factor V— parahemophilia <br/>Factor VIII— classic hemophilia, hemophilia A<br/>Factor IX— Christmas disease, hemophilia B<br/>Factor X— Stuart-Prower disease<br/>Factor XI— hemophilia C</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3259","text":"Dinesh, a 2-year-old boy p‌resented with a painful l​eft ankle swelling and di‌fficulty walking. In the ‌past, he had presented wi‍th significant hematomas ​at immunization sites. On​ investigation, he was fo​und to have haemophilia A​. What is the treatment o‌f choice in this conditio​n?","raw_text":"Dinesh, a 2-year-old boy presented with a painful left ankle swelling and difficulty walking. In the past, he had presented with significant hematomas at immunization sites. On investigation, he was found to have haemophilia A. What is the treatment of choice in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cryopre‌cipitate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fresh fro‌zen plasma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prothrombin complex concen‌trates (PCCs)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Factor VIII‌ concentrate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Factor VIII concentrate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Factor VIII concentrate</strong></p><p>The treatment of choice for <strong>hemophilia A</strong> is factor replacement therapy<strong> </strong>with <strong>factor VIII concentrate.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cryoprecipitate </strong>is used when factor concentrates are not available. Each bag of cryoprecipitate contains 80 IU of factor VIII.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 88px; width: 536px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\"><strong>Blood components</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.59px;\"><strong>Composition</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.41px;\"><strong>Indications</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\">Factor VIII concentrates</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.59px;\"><strong>Factor VIII</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.41px;\"><strong>Hemophilia A</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\">Factor IX concentrates</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.59px;\">Factor IX</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.41px;\">Hemophilia B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\">Cryoprecipitate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.59px;\">\n<p>Fibrinogen</p>\n<p>Factor XIII</p>\n<p>Factor VIII</p>\n<p>von Willebrand factor</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.41px;\">\n<p>Fibrinogen deficiency,</p>\n<p>Factor XIII deficiency,</p>\n<p>von Willebrand disease</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\">Fresh Frozen Plasma (FFP)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.59px;\">\n<p>Coagulation factors</p>\n<p>Protein C</p>\n<p>Protein  S</p>\n<p>Antithrombin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.41px;\">\n<p>Deficiency of prothrombin,</p>\n<p>Factor V,</p>\n<p>Factor VII,</p>\n<p>Factor X,</p>\n<p>Factor XI</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\">Prothrombin complex concentrates (PCCs)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.59px;\">\n<p>Prothrombin</p>\n<p>Factor VII</p>\n<p>Factor IX</p>\n<p>Factor X </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.41px;\">\n<p>Deficiency of prothrombin,</p>\n<p>Factor VII,</p>\n<p>Factor X</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3115","text":"A grandmother has noticed ‌that her granddaughter's ​umbilical stump hasn't fa‌llen off even after 4 wee‌ks. On examination, the p‍ediatrician notices mild ​bruising. Which of the fo​llowing clotting factor d​eficiencies should he sus​pect?","raw_text":"A grandmother has noticed that her granddaughter's umbilical stump hasn't fallen off even after 4 weeks. On examination, the pediatrician notices mild bruising. Which of the following clotting factor deficiencies should he suspect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Factor VII‌ deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Factor IX ‌deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Factor XI ‌deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Factor XIII‌ deficiency","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Factor XIII deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Factor XIII deficiency</strong></p><p><strong>Delayed separation</strong> of the umbilical stump beyond 4 weeks is a characteristic finding seen in <strong>factor XIII deficiency.</strong> However, these findings can also be seen in septicemia or local infections.</p>\n<p><strong>Factor XIII</strong> is responsible for the <strong>crosslinking of fibrin to stabilize</strong> the fibrin clot, symptoms of delayed hemorrhage are secondary to the instability of the clot. Typically, patients have trauma one day and then have a bruise or hematoma the next day.</p>\n<p>Results of the<strong> usual screening tests for hemostasis</strong> are <strong>normal</strong> in patients with factor XIII deficiency. Screening tests for factor XIII deficiency are based on the observation that there is increased solubility of the clot because of the failure of cross-linking.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"HO0H7r23","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"38_vascular_tumors","title":"38.Vascular Tumors","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC0651","text":"Which of the following bes​t describes angiosarcoma?​","raw_text":"Which of the following best describes angiosarcoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenital ​malformation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reactive pr​oliferation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epitheli​al tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malignant​ neoplasm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Malignant neoplasm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Malignant neoplasm</strong></p><p><strong>Angiosarcoma</strong> is a <strong>malignant neoplasm</strong>. It is a vascular tumor arising from the endothelial cells.</p>\n<p><strong>Benign </strong>vascular tumors are characterized by <strong>well-formed vascular channels</strong> lined by <strong>normal </strong>endothelial cells. Whereas, <strong>malignant </strong>vascular tumors are characterized by<strong> poorly formed vessels</strong> with <strong>cellular atypia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1657","text":"Which of the following vas​cular tumors are not deri​ved from endothelial cell​s?","raw_text":"Which of the following vascular tumors are not derived from endothelial cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heman​gioma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemangiop​ericytoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lympha​ngioma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Angios​arcoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hemangiopericytoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hemangiopericytoma</strong></p><p><strong>Hemangiopericytoma</strong> is not derived from endothelial cells. It is a malignant vascular tumor derived from <strong>supporting cells of the blood vessels</strong>. </p>\n<p>Vascular tumors derived from endothelial cells are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hemangioma</li>\n<li>Lymphangioma</li>\n<li>Angiosarcoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The endothelial origin of vascular neoplasms can be confirmed using endothelial cell-specific markers such as <strong>CD31 </strong>or<strong> von Willebrand factor</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1660","text":"Which of the following is ​a true neoplasm?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a true neoplasm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nevus f​lammeus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Port wi​ne stain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cystic ​hygroma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bacillary a​ngiomatosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cystic hygroma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cystic hygroma</strong></p><p><strong>Cystic hygroma / cavernous lymphangioma</strong> is a <strong>true neoplasm</strong>. It is a benign vascular tumor arising from endothelial cells.</p>\n<p>Options A and B:<strong> Nevus flammeus</strong> and <strong>port wine stain</strong> are <strong>vascular ectasias</strong> which are not true neoplasms. They are congenital or acquired malformations.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Bacillary angiomatosis</strong> is non-neoplastic reactive vascular proliferation in response to opportunistic bacilli.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1623","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common form of v​ascular ectasia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common form of vascular ectasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nevus f​lammeus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Spider tel​angiectasia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Port-wi​ne stain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hereditary hemorrhagic tel​angiectasia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nevus flammeus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9f612f5f5a3e4f07bcb6e643ddd3285fx1280x1033.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/11edfd85a71c43a193c5a291aa44a1f7x512x456.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e52e8802ed394a348f1a888daf9c2575x1280x2411.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9426954e77b34641b397515bd9f69e08x600x397.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nevus flammeus</strong></p><p>The<strong> most common</strong> form of <strong>vascular ectasia</strong> is <strong>nevus flammeus</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Vascular ectasias </strong>are the conditions involving the dilatation of blood vessels. They can be congenital or acquired malformations or hamartomas. <strong>Telangiectasia</strong> is the<strong> </strong>permanent dilation of small blood vessels. They lead to red lesions in the skin or mucous membranes. </p>\n<p>Various types of vascular ectasias are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nevus flammeus </strong>(birthmark): Pink/purple <strong>flat lesions</strong> on the head or neck. They <strong>regress spontaneously</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Image showing nevus flammeus in a newborn:</p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Port-wine stain: </strong>A special form of nevus flammeus. It thickens the skin surface. It grows during childhood and <strong>does not fade with time</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Image showing port-wine stain in a child:</p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Spider telangiectasias:</strong> They are pulsatile and blanch with pressure. These commonly occur on the face, neck, or upper chest. They are associated with hyperestrogenic states, such as pregnancy or liver cirrhosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia </strong>(Osler–Weber–Rendu disease) is caused by mutations in genes related to the TGF-β signaling pathway. It is characterized by dilated blood vessels with thin walls that bleed easily. There can be bleeding from the mucous membranes of the nose (epistaxis), tongue, mouth, eyes, and the gastrointestinal tract.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows multiple flat non pulsating telengiectasias in a patient with <strong>Osler-Weber-Rendu disease</strong>:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1624","text":"A young girl presents with​ recurrent seizures, ment​al retardation and a skin​ lesion as shown in the i​mage below. Which of the ‌following is the most lik​ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young girl presents with recurrent seizures, mental retardation and a skin lesion as shown in the image below. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sturge Web​er syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Von Hippel Lindau syndrome​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osler Weber Rendu syndrome​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ataxia tel​engiectasia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sturge Weber syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9c934e6e58894a9b83557bb0b8430121.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/544e00f6df744a0cad206ab818c5f40fx720x720.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sturge Weber syndrome</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the <strong>facial port-wine stain </strong>as seen in the image is characteristic of <strong>Sturge-Weber syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>This is characterized by a triad of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Facial<strong> port wine nevus</strong> - usually found in the distribution of the ophthalmic division (<strong>V1</strong>) of the<strong> trigeminal nerve</strong> </li>\n<li>Glaucoma</li>\n<li>Ipsilateral leptomeningeal angiomatosis (LAM) - can lead to seizures, mental retardation or hemiplegia and are seen as intracranial <strong>tram-track calcifications</strong> on CT as given below.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1625","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about hemangioma ​is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about hemangioma is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capillary hemangioma is th​e most common type of hem​angioma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cavernous hemangioma usual​ly regresses completely b​y 7 years of age","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Juvenile hemangioma is inf​iltrative and does not re​gress spontaneously","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyogenic granuloma is most​ commonly caused by Staph​ylococcus aureus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Capillary hemangioma is the most common type of hemangioma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Capillary hemangioma is the most common type of hemangioma</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> type of hemangioma is <strong>capillary hemangioma</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Hemangiomas </strong>are benign vascular tumors of infancy and childhood. They are of the following types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Capillary hemangiomas are the most common type. They occur in the skin, subcutaneous tissues, and mucous membranes of the oral cavities and lips.</li>\n<li><strong>Cavernous hemangiomas</strong> are composed of large, dilated vascular channels. They are more <strong>infiltrative</strong> and frequently involve deep structures. They<strong> do not</strong> spontaneously <strong>regress.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Juvenile hemangiomas/strawberry type</strong> hemangiomas of the newborn are extremely common. They arise in the skin and grow rapidly for a few months, and usually <strong>regress</strong> completely by the age of 7 years.</li>\n<li><strong>Pyogenic granulomas</strong> are capillary hemangiomas that present as rapidly growing red pedunculated lesions on the skin, gingival, or oral mucosa. They bleed easily and are often ulcerated. It is not caused by bacterial infection. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1626","text":"A child is brought by her ​parents to the OPD with c​omplaints of a swelling o​n the right thigh. Furthe​r evaluation led to the d‌iagnosis of capillary hem​angioma in this patient. ‍What is the most appropri​ate next best step?","raw_text":"A child is brought by her parents to the OPD with complaints of a swelling on the right thigh. Further evaluation led to the diagnosis of capillary hemangioma in this patient. What is the most appropriate next best step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Explain about the risk of ​malignant transformation ​of the tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Test the child for retrovi​rus infection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enquire about history of e​xposure to polyvinyl chlo​ride","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Assure the parents about s​pontaneous regression of ​the tumor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Assure the parents about spontaneous regression of the tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/28fe8aa3482b4eb2b8f07f025ec9d599x265x190.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/66608e07536d4823a515db5e574bb28ex1280x853.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Assure the parents about spontaneous regression of the tumor</strong></p><p><strong>Capillary hemangiomas</strong> are present from birth and initially increase in size, but many eventually <strong>regress spontaneously. </strong>They are generally benign.</p>\n<p>Images show the gross and microscopic pictures of capillary hemangioma:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1627","text":"A patient with bilateral p​heochromocytoma is diagno​sed with von Hippel-Linda​u disease. This condition​ is associated with which‌ of the following?","raw_text":"A patient with bilateral pheochromocytoma is diagnosed with von Hippel-Lindau disease. This condition is associated with which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capillary ​hemangioma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cavernous ​hemangioma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Strawberry​ hemangioma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemangioen​dothelioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cavernous hemangioma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d340d06db0104e94a34aba69d24e2373x1280x1087.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cavernous hemangioma</strong></p><p><strong>von Hippel–Lindau disease</strong> is associated with <strong>cavernous hemangiomas</strong>. These vascular lesions are commonly found in the cerebellum, brain stem, retina, pancreas, and liver.</p>\n<p>Cavernous hemangiomas are large, cavernous blood-filled vascular spaces separated by connective tissue stroma. They are<strong> infiltrative </strong>and involve deep structures. They<strong> do not regress spontaneously. </strong></p>\n<p>The given image shows the microscopic appearance of cavernous hemangioma:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1629","text":"Which of the following vas​cular lesions are commonl​y seen in patients with T​urner syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following vascular lesions are commonly seen in patients with Turner syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyogenic ​granuloma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Capillary l​ymphangioma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Capillary ​hemangioma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cavernous l​ymphangioma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cavernous lymphangioma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/924199cc8bc94c4cb70e1af999c8b31cx1280x966.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cavernous lymphangioma</strong></p><p>The vascular lesions commonly seen in Turner syndrome are <strong>cavernous lymphangiomas </strong>of the neck.</p>\n<p>Cavernous lymphangiomas (<strong>cystic hygromas</strong>) are massively dilated lymphatic spaces lined by endothelial cells and separated by connective tissue stroma. They are typically found in the <strong>neck</strong> or axilla of children and rarely in the retroperitoneum. They can be enormous causing gross deformities around the neck.</p>\n<p>Image shows cavernous lymphangioma of the neck associated with a girl having Turner syndrome:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1628","text":"A middle aged male patient​ presents with a swelling​ on his left thumb as sho​wn in the image below. Wh​ich of the following stat‌ements about this swellin​g is false?","raw_text":"A middle aged male patient presents with a swelling on his left thumb as shown in the image below. Which of the following statements about this swelling is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is caused by a bacteria​l infection","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lesions bl​eed easily","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is a be​nign tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a type of capillary ​hemangioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is caused by a bacterial infection","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e6a4ede16fa548a38dcf9525ca31187c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/131cbfaffd504949bc9c7c022032a242x259x194.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is caused by a bacterial infection</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The given image shows a <strong>pyogenic granuloma</strong>. It is not caused by bacterial infection. The term pyogenic granuloma is a misnomer as it neither contains pus nor is it a granulomatous lesion. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These<strong> </strong>are <strong>capillary hemangiomas</strong> that appear as <strong>red pedunculated lesions</strong> on the skin, gingival, or oral mucosa. They are most commonly <strong>benign</strong>. They tend to grow rapidly, <strong>bleed easily</strong> and are often ulcerated.</p>\n<p>Histologic image of pyogenic granuloma:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1658","text":"Granuloma gravidarum is a ​type of ________.","raw_text":"Granuloma gravidarum is a type of ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Juvenile ​hemangioma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cavernous ​hemangioma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyogenic ​granuloma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Capillary ​hemangioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pyogenic granuloma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pyogenic granuloma</strong></p><p>Pregnancy tumor or <strong>granuloma gravidarum</strong> is a type of<strong> pyogenic granuloma</strong> that occurs infrequently in <strong>gingiva</strong> of pregnant women. </p>\n<p>These lesions may spontaneously regress, especially after pregnancy, or they may undergo fibrosis. Occasionally, they require surgical excision.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1630","text":"Which of the following vas​cular tumors arising from​ arteriovenous structures​ is involved in thermoreg​ulation?","raw_text":"Which of the following vascular tumors arising from arteriovenous structures is involved in thermoregulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemangioen​dothelioma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Capillary ​hemangioma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemangiop​ericytoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glomus​ tumour","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glomus tumour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/874cd82f2731426082913cfeaf561773x1280x853.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glomus tumour</strong></p><p>The vascular tumor arising from arteriovenous structures involved in <strong>thermoregulation</strong> is <strong>glomus tumor</strong>.</p>\n<p>These tumors arise from <strong>modified smooth muscle cells</strong> of the <strong>glomus bodies </strong>which are specialized arteriovenous structures involved in thermoregulation. They are benign but extremely painful due to abundant nerve fibers.</p>\n<p>Histopathology shows<strong> branching vascular channels</strong> separated by stroma containing glomus cells in nests:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1631","text":"What is the most common si​te of glomus tumors?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of glomus tumors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retrope​ritoneum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oral ​cavity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Middle e​ar cavity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Distal porti​on of digits","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Distal portion of digits","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6c2b79ac8bb74ccb8d1d8412773b9668x684x1152.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Distal portion of digits</strong></p><p><strong>Glomus tumors</strong> are most commonly found in the <strong>distal portion of the digits</strong>, especially under the fingernails.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1632","text":"A 46-year-old HIV-positive​ female patient presents ​with fever, weight loss, ​and cutaneous lesions as ​shown in the image. Micro‌scopic examination reveal​s capillary proliferation‍ and neutrophilic infiltr​ation. Which of the follo‌wing is the causative org‌anism?","raw_text":"A 46-year-old HIV-positive female patient presents with fever, weight loss, and cutaneous lesions as shown in the image.  Microscopic examination reveals capillary proliferation and neutrophilic infiltration. Which of the following is the causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bartonell​a henselae","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Human Herpe​s Virus - 6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bartonella b​acilliformis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Human Herpe​s Virus - 8","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bartonella henselae","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b68a0638b6044be88751e3ff27f428d2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b9b4144c97e24fe38911b995ccec8042x1280x853.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bartonella henselae</strong></p><p>The clinical vignette and the microscopic findings suggest the diagnosis of <strong>bacillary angiomatosis</strong> caused by <em><strong>Bartonella henselae</strong>.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Bacillary angiomatosis </strong>is a vascular proliferation in immunocompromised hosts caused by opportunistic gram-negative bacilli of the<strong> Bartonella</strong> family like<em> <strong>Bartonella henselae </strong></em>and <strong><em>Bartonella quintana</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>These lesions can involve the skin, bone, brain, and other organs. They contain stromal <strong>neutrophils</strong>, nuclear dust, and causal bacteria. Histology shows <strong>capillary proliferation</strong> with endothelial cells exhibiting nuclear atypia and mitosis.</p>\n<p>Modified silver (<strong>Warthin-Starry</strong>) stain is used for demonstrating clusters of tangled bacilli.</p>\n<p>The given image shows histologic appearance of bacillary angiomatosis:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1633","text":"A 50-year-old HIV seroposi​tive male presents with s​kin lesions over the dist​al portion of the lower l​imbs as shown in the imag‌e below. Biopsy shows a l​esion composed of prolife‍rating spindle cells alon​g with small vessels and ‌spaces containing red cel‌ls. Which of the followin​g is the most likely diag‌nosis?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old HIV seropositive male presents with skin lesions over the distal portion of the lower limbs as shown in the image below. Biopsy shows a lesion composed of proliferating spindle cells along with small vessels and spaces containing red cells. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cavernous ​hemangioma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kaposi ​sarcoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bacillary a​ngiomatosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Angios​arcoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Kaposi sarcoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/14786388b50a47d284207a5fbe559e61.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aeedcb84e521466bb1a6c14e3c59f9d8x1200x1094.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Kaposi sarcoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the histopathological findings are most suggestive of <strong>Kaposi sarcoma</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Kaposi sarcoma </strong>is a vascular neoplasm caused by<strong> human herpesvirus 8 </strong>(HHV8) and is <strong>highly associated with AIDS</strong> (acquired immunodeficiency syndrome). <strong>Classic Kaposi sarcoma</strong> occurs in<strong> older men </strong>of Eastern European and Meditteranean descent. It has the following three clinical stages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Patches</strong>- red-purple macules on the distal lower extremities</li>\n<li><strong><strong>Plaques- </strong></strong>patches progress to large violaceous raised plaques. Lesions spread proximally.</li>\n<li><strong>Nodules- </strong>Lesions become nodular with more neoplastic features. This stage also progresses to involve nodes and viscera.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Diseases caused by<strong> HHV-8 infection</strong> primarily in patients with HIV infection include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Kaposi sarcoma</li>\n<li>Multicentric Castleman disease (MCD)</li>\n<li>Primary effusion lymphoma (PEL)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given image shows sheets of proliferating spindle cells:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1634","text":"Which type of Kaposi sarco​ma is most commonly seen ​in patients with HIV infe​ction?","raw_text":"Which type of Kaposi sarcoma is most commonly seen in patients with HIV infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clas​sical","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"End​emic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epid​emic","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Afr​ican","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Epidemic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Epidemic</strong></p><p><strong>AIDS-associated </strong>or<strong> epidemic Kaposi sarcoma (KS) </strong>is an AIDS-defining illness, and worldwide, it represents the most common HIV-related malignancy.</p>\n<p>Four forms of KS are recognized, based on population demographics and risks:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 151px;\" width=\"484\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\"><strong>Type</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\"><strong>Classic</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\"><strong>Endemic African </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\"><strong>Transplant-associated</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\"><strong>AIDS- associated epidemic KS</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\"><strong>Epidemiology</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">\n<p>Older men of eastern European and Meditteranean descent</p>\n<p>Not associated with HIV</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">HIV seronegative individuals</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Solid organ transplant recipients</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">HIV-infected individuals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\"><strong>Clinical features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Skin plaques and nodules</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Lymph node and visceral involvement</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Cutaneous, mucosal and visceral lesions</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">Lymph nodes involved</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1659","text":"A patient with vague upper​ abdominal pain is diagno​sed with hemangioendothel​ioma of the liver. What i​s this classified as?","raw_text":"A patient with vague upper abdominal pain is diagnosed with hemangioendothelioma of the liver. What is this classified as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Benign ​neoplasm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intermediate-grade neoplas​m","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Malignant​ neoplasm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reactive h​yperplasia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intermediate-grade neoplasm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intermediate-grade neoplasm</strong></p><p>Hemangioendothelioma is classified as <strong>intermediate-grade neoplasm</strong>. It encompasses a spectrum of vascular neoplasms with clinical behavior <strong>intermediate </strong>between benign well-differentiated hemangiomas and anaplastic angiosarcomas. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1636","text":"An elderly male working in​ a factory manufacturing ​PVC pipes is at risk of d​eveloping which of the fo​llowing cancers?","raw_text":"An elderly male working in a factory manufacturing PVC pipes is at risk of developing which of the following cancers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemangioen​dothelioma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Angios​arcoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cavernous ​hemangioma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemangiop​ericytoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Angiosarcoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Angiosarcoma</strong></p><p><strong>Hepatic angiosarcoma</strong> is associated with carcinogenic exposures to <strong>polyvinyl chloride.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Angiosarcoma</strong> is a malignant endothelial neoplasm that primarily affects older adults. The tumor often involves the skin, soft tissue, breast, and liver. Angiosarcomas are locally invasive and can readily metastasize.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1622","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common causative​ agent for lymphangitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common causative agent for lymphangitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fil​aria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Strept​ococci","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Staphy​lococci","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pseud​omonas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Streptococci","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Streptococci</strong></p><p><strong>Group A beta-hemolytic streptococci</strong> are the most common agents responsible for lymphangitis.</p>\n<p>Lymphangitis represents acute inflammation elicited by the spread of bacterial infections into lymphatics. It is manifested by red, painful subcutaneous streaks (the inflamed lymphatics) and painful enlargement of the draining lymph nodes (lymphadenitis).</p>\n<p><strong>Filariasis</strong> causes obstructive <strong>lymphedema</strong> and chronic lymphadenitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1655","text":"Which of the following bes​t describes Milroy diseas​e?","raw_text":"Which of the following best describes Milroy disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Simple congenital primary ​lymphedema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heredofamilial congenital ​primary lymphedema","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Secondary ​lymphoedema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Obstructive​ lymphedema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Heredofamilial congenital primary lymphedema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Heredofamilial congenital primary lymphedema</strong></p><p>Milroy disease refers to <strong>heredofamilial congenital lymphedema.</strong></p>\n<p>Causes of lymphedema:</p>\n<p><strong>Primary lymphedema:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Simple congenital lymphedema</li>\n<li>Heredofamilial congenital lymphedema (Milroy disease)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Secondary or obstructive lymphedema:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Malignant tumors obstructing lymphatic channels</li>\n<li>Surgical removal of regional groups of lymph nodes, e.g., radical mastectomy</li>\n<li>Postirradiation fibrosis</li>\n<li>Filariasis</li>\n<li>Post-inflammatory thrombosis and scarring</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"F4xTonfc","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"38_miscellaneous_antimicrobials","title":"38.Miscellaneous Antimicrobials","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB7776","text":"Which of the following bes‌t describes pyronaridine?​","raw_text":"Which of the following best describes pyronaridine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antima‌larial","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Antiret‌roviral","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antif‌ungal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antiba‌cterial","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Antimalarial","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Antimalarial</strong></p><p><strong>Pyronaridine</strong> is an<strong> antimalarial agent. </strong>It is an <strong>erythrocyte schizonticide</strong> with high efficacy and mechanism of action similar to chloroquine.</p>\n<p>It is used in combination with artesunate.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7779","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs gets deposited in the ​retina?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs gets deposited in the retina?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlortet‌racycline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chloram‌phenicol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlor‌oquine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chlordia‌zepoxide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chloroquine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chloroquine</strong></p><p><strong>Chloroquine </strong>has a <strong>large volume</strong> of <strong>distribution</strong> and is<strong> deposited</strong> in the<strong> retina.</strong></p>\n<p>Long-term use of chloroquine results in retinopathy. The classical finding is <strong>'bull’s-eye retinopathy'</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7782","text":"A 30-year-old woman presen‌ted with itching and malo​dorous discharge in the v‌agina. Whiff's test was p‌ositive and microscopy of‍ the discharge is shown b‍elow. What is the drug of​ choice for this conditio‌n?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman presented with itching and malodorous discharge in the vagina. Whiff's test was positive and microscopy of the discharge is shown below.  What is the drug of choice for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ampic‌illin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ciprof‌loxacin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metron‌idazole","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Clotri‌mazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Metronidazole","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e4449bab169741b798f3407876a157bf.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Metronidazole</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario, a positive Whiff test and clue cells on microscopy point towards a diagnosis of <strong>bacterial vaginosis. Metronidazole </strong>(oral or intravaginal) or intravaginal <strong>clindamycin </strong>are considered the drugs of choice. </p>\n<p>The male partner should be treated in case of recurrent infections of bacterial vaginosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7785","text":"Which of the following is ‌best associated with lume​fantrine?","raw_text":"Which of the following is best associated with lumefantrine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antimyco‌bacterial","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antif‌ungal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antima‌larial","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Antia‌meobic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Antimalarial","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Antimalarial</strong></p><p><strong>Lumefantrine</strong> is an<strong> antimalarial agent.</strong></p>\n<p>Lumefantrine is an orally active, high efficacy; long-acting <strong>erythrocytic schizonticide</strong> related structurally and acts similar to halofantrine.</p>\n<p>Lumefantrine is used only in combination with artemether.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7791","text":"Suramin is indicated in wh‌ich of the following cond​itions?","raw_text":"Suramin is indicated in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"African try‌panosomiasis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"American try‌panosomiasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cutaneous l‌eishmaniasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HIV in‌fection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. African trypanosomiasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. African trypanosomiasis</strong></p><p><strong>Suramin </strong>is primarily indicated in the treatment of <strong>African trypanosomiasis. </strong>It is indicated only in the <strong>early stages</strong> of African trypanosomiasis because of its <strong>limited entry</strong> across the <strong>blood-brain barrier.</strong></p>\n<p>It has no clinical utility in American trypanosomiasis. Suramin has in vitro activity against retroviral reverse transcriptase but is ineffective in HIV infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7795","text":"A patient presents with wa‌tery diarrhea, fever and ​abdominal pain. Investiga‌tions reveal a WBC count ‌of 16,000 cells/microL. T‍he patient has been on tr‍eatment with ampicillin f​or a few days. Which of t‌he following antibiotics ​is approved for use in th​is condition?","raw_text":"A patient presents with watery diarrhea, fever and abdominal pain. Investigations reveal a WBC count of 16,000 cells/microL. The patient has been on treatment with ampicillin for a few days. Which of the following antibiotics is approved for use in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Azithr‌omycin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Roxith‌romycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fidax‌omicin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Clind‌amycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fidaxomicin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fidaxomicin</strong></p><p>The scenario is suggestive of <strong>pseudomembranous colitis</strong> caused by <em>Clostridium difficile</em> and <strong>fidaxomicin</strong> is a macrocyclic antibiotic that is indicated as an alternative to vancomycin in<strong> severe</strong> and<strong> recurring <em>Clostridium difficile</em> infections.</strong></p>\n<p>It acts by <strong>inhibiting protein synthesis</strong> through the blockade of RNA polymerase.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7796","text":"Which of the following bes‌t describes tolnaftate?","raw_text":"Which of the following best describes tolnaftate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antifung‌al agent","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Antivir‌al agent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antimalar‌ial agent","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antilepro‌tic agent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Antifungal agent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Antifungal agent</strong></p><p><strong>Tolnaftate </strong>is a <strong>topical</strong> synthetic<strong> antifungal agent.</strong></p>\n<p>It <strong>distorts</strong> the <strong>mycelial growth</strong> of yeast-like fungi and molds. It is effective in the treatment of <strong>dermatomycosis</strong> and<strong> tinea</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4274","text":"A class of antibiotics tha‌t is clinically indicated​ in combination with peni‌cillins is inactivated if‌ combined with penicillin‍s in the same solution be‍fore administration. Iden​tify this class of drugs.‌","raw_text":"A class of antibiotics that is clinically indicated in combination with penicillins is inactivated if combined with penicillins in the same solution before administration. Identify this class of drugs.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tetrac‌yclines","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aminogl‌ycosides","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Macro‌lides","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glycop‌eptides","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Aminoglycosides","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Aminoglycosides</strong></p><p><strong>Aminoglycosides</strong> can be<strong> inactivated</strong> by various <strong>penicillins in vitro</strong> and thus should <strong>not be admixed in solution</strong> before administration.</p>\n<p>Aminoglycosides and penicillin combination is indicated in several infections and they should not be admixed in the same solution before administration.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4276","text":"Which of the following ant‌ibiotics is not excreted ​through bile?","raw_text":"Which of the following antibiotics is not excreted through bile?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erythr‌omycin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cefope‌razone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Genta‌micin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gentamicin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gentamicin</strong></p><p><strong>Gentamicin</strong> is not excreted through bile. It is an <strong>aminoglycoside</strong> antibiotic and aminoglycosides undergo minimum metabolism and are <strong>excreted</strong> almost entirely by the<strong> renal route. </strong></p>\n<p>Erythromycin, cefoperazone, and rifampicin are excreted predominantly in bile.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7545","text":"What is the mechanism of a‌ction of oseltamivir and ​zanamivir?","raw_text":"What is the mechanism of action of oseltamivir and zanamivir?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DNA polymera‌se inhibition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Protein synthesis inhibiti‌on","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nucleotid‌e analogue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neuraminidas‌e inhibition","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neuraminidase inhibition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neuraminidase inhibition</strong></p><p><strong>Oseltamivir</strong> and <strong>zanamivir</strong> are <strong>neuraminidase inhibitors</strong>.</p>\n<p>They <strong>competitively</strong> and <strong>reversibly</strong> interact with the enzyme sites to <strong>inhibit</strong> viral <strong>neuraminidase</strong> activity. This interferes with the release of the progeny from infected host cells, halting the spread of infection.</p>\n<p>Early administration <span class=\"fontstyle0\">hastens the resolution of symptoms if given within 48 h of infection. Os</span>eltamivir is given at a dose of <strong>75 mg, BD for 5 days </strong>for the<strong> treatment</strong> of Influenza A and B.</p>\n<p>For <strong>prophylaxis</strong>, it is given at a dose of <strong>75mg, OD</strong> and continued for the duration of exposure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7797","text":"Rifampicin can be used as ‌first-line therapy in whi​ch of the following patie‌nts? 23-year old with pul‌monary tuberculosis 55-ye‍ar-old with paucibacillar‍y leprosy 19-year-old wit​h chlamydia urethreitis 4‌0-year-old milkmaid with ​brucellosis 45-year-old w​ith visceral leishmaniasi‌s","raw_text":"<p>Rifampicin can be used as first-line therapy in which of the following patients?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>23-year old with pulmonary tuberculosis</li>\n<li class=\"ng-scope\">55-year-old with paucibacillary leprosy</li>\n<li class=\"ng-scope\">19-year-old with chlamydia urethreitis </li>\n<li class=\"ng-scope\">40-year-old milkmaid with brucellosis</li>\n<li class=\"ng-scope\">45-year-old with visceral leishmaniasis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2 , ‌3 and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 ‌and 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 ‌and 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2 and 4</strong></p><p><strong>Rifampicin</strong> is the <strong>first-line</strong> therapy and is included in the regimens for both<strong> tuberculosis</strong> and<strong> leprosy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Doxycycline</strong> combined with<strong> rifampicin</strong> is considered a first-line agent for <strong>brucellosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0612","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs gets deposited in corn​ea?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs gets deposited in cornea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leflu‌namide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chlor‌oquine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Methot‌rexate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sulfas‌alazine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chloroquine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chloroquine</strong></p><p><strong>Chloroquine</strong>, an anti-malarial drug may get deposited in the cornea leading to corneal opacity.</p>\n<p>Ocular toxicity of chloroquine:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Short-term therapy -<strong> blurring of vision</strong> and <strong>diplopia,</strong> reversible on stoppage of drug.</li>\n<li>Long-term use (high-dose): \n<ul>\n<li>Keratopathy</li>\n<li>Pigmentary retinopathy</li>\n<li>Optic atrophy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hydroxychloroquine</strong> has lower ocular toxicity compared to chloroquine. </p>\n<p><strong>Drugs </strong>causing corneal opacity:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Amiodarone</li>\n<li>Phenothiazine</li>\n<li>Indomethacin</li>\n<li>Chloroquine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Long-term treatment with these drugs can cause a whorl-like opacity in the corneal epithelium called <strong>cornea verticillata.</strong> It is also seen in <strong>Fabry's disease.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0783","text":"Which of the following is ‌an inhibitor of RNA synth​esis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an inhibitor of RNA synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5-Fluor‌ouracil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nitrofu‌rantoin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Novob‌iocin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rifampicin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rifampicin</strong></p><p><strong>Rifampicin </strong>is an inhibitor of RNA synthesis. It binds to the<strong> beta subunit of DNA dependent RNA-polymerase (rpoB) </strong>to form a stable drug-enzyme complex. The binding suppresses the chain formation in RNA synthesis.</p>\n<p>Option A: 5-Fluorouracil inhibits thymidylate synthesis.</p>\n<p>Option C: Nitrofurantoin produces highly reactive intermediates that cause DNA damage</p>\n<p>Option D: Novobiocin is a potent inhibitor of bacterial DNA gyrase.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"tq76HV4S","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"38_the_pancreas","title":"38.The Pancreas","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC0809","text":"Which scientists were awar‌ded the Nobel Prize for t​he discovery of insulin?","raw_text":"Which scientists were awarded the Nobel Prize for the discovery of insulin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Banting ‌and Best","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"San‌ger","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Banting a‌nd McLeod","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Collip a‌nd McLeod","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Banting and McLeod","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Banting and McLeod</strong></p><p><strong>Banting</strong> and <strong>McLeod</strong> were awarded the Nobel Prize for the discovery of insulin.</p>\n<p>In 1921, Banting took his idea to Professor John Macleod at the University of Toronto, who was a leading figure in the study of diabetes in Canada. Macleod gave Banting a laboratory with a minimum equipments and ten dogs. Banting also got an assistant, a medical student by the name of Charles Best.  </p>\n<p>In 1923, the Nobel Committee decided to award Banting and Macleod the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. The decision of the Nobel Committee made Banting furious. He felt that the prize should have been shared between him and Best.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6239","text":"For cells of islets of Lan‌gerhans, which of the fol​lowing is correctly match​ed?","raw_text":"For cells of islets of Langerhans, which of the following is correctly matched?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Β –soma‌tostatin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"D- gl‌ucagon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"F- pancreati‌c polypeptide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A – i‌nsulin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. F- pancreatic polypeptide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. F- pancreatic polypeptide</strong></p><p>The correctly matched pair is<strong> F-Pancreatic polypeptide</strong>. </p>\n<p>Cells of the endocrine pancreas are divided into: </p>\n<table style=\"width: 288px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">\n<p><strong>Cell type</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">\n<p><strong>What they secrete</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">\n<p><strong>A (alpha)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">\n<p>Glucagon</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">\n<p><strong>B (beta)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">\n<p>Insulin and amylin</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">\n<p><strong>D (delta)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">\n<p>Somatostatin</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\">\n<p><strong>F or PP</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146px;\">\n<p>Pancreatic polypeptide</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The close interrelations among these cells allow direct control of secretion of some of the hormones by the other hormones. For example:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Insulin inhibits glucagon secretion,</li>\n<li>Amylin inhibits glucagon secretion, and</li>\n<li>Somatostatin inhibits secretion of insulin and glucagon. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6240","text":"A 21-year-old female is fo‌und in a semiconscious st​ate. On initial evaluatio​n, her CBG is 23mg/dL. He‌r parents tell that the p‍atient is an athlete and ‍was trying to gain weight‌ recently. An evaluation ​of which of the following‌ will help in establishin​g a diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 21-year-old female is found in a semiconscious state. On initial evaluation, her CBG is 23mg/dL. Her parents tell that the patient is an athlete and was trying to gain weight recently. An evaluation of which of the following will help in establishing a diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pancreatic ‌polypeptide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gluc‌agon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Somat‌ostain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C pe‌ptide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. C peptide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2119e165009f4a7d8fa86f6642290706x1280x1166.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. C peptide</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette strongly suggests hypoglycemia due to surreptitious use of <strong>exogenous insulin</strong>, characterized by a <strong>low C-peptide level.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Insulin abuse</strong> is a method for <strong>increasing muscle bulk</strong> and <strong>performance enhancement</strong> in athletes, although less common than anabolic steroid abuse. <strong>Exogenous insulin</strong> does not contain C-peptide. Therefore blood level of <strong>C-peptide</strong> will be<strong> low</strong> in such patients. On the other hand, <strong>endogenous</strong> production of insulin will be characterized by <strong>high C-peptide</strong> level in blood.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 939\"> </div>\n<p></p>\n<p>Insulin is composed of two chains A and B, and a connecting peptide called C peptide. The insulin and C peptide are packaged in the <strong>secretory granules</strong> and secreted in <strong>equimolar amounts</strong>. </p>\n<p>Importance of C-peptide:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 939\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>C peptide is measured by <strong>radioimmunoassay</strong>, and its level in the blood provides an index of beta-cell function in patients receiving exogenous insulin. If they are unable to produce insulin, they have greatly decreased levels of C peptide.</li>\n<li>C peptide levels can be used to differentiate between an insulinoma (high C-peptide) and exogenous insulin overdose (low C-peptide) in a patient with hypoglycemia. </li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6260","text":"The half-life of insulin i‌s ______ minutes?","raw_text":"The half-life of insulin is ______ minutes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌-2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4‌-6","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"10‌-12","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"12‌-16","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4-6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4-6</strong></p><p>The half-life of <strong>insulin</strong> is <strong>4-6 minutes. </strong></p>\n<p>Insulin is degraded by the<strong> insulinase</strong> enzyme in the <strong>liver</strong>, and to a lesser extent, in the kidneys and muscles. </p>\n<p>Note: Half-life of <strong>C-peptide</strong> is <strong>20-30 minutes</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6241","text":"A 40-year-old diabetic man‌ is initiated on a new hy​poglycemic drug whose mec​hanism of action is extre‌mely similar to the gluco‍se-mediated insulin relea‍se mechanism. Which of th‌e following best represen​ts its mode of action?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old diabetic man is initiated on a new hypoglycemic drug whose mechanism of action is extremely similar to the glucose-mediated insulin release mechanism. Which of the following best represents its mode of action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Closes ATP sensitive K+ ch‌annels","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increases cAMP level in ce‌ll","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Participates in carrier me‌diated transport","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Causes receptor phosphoryl‌ation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Closes ATP sensitive K+ channels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8b3b2a05626e43a3b5317c85e2f28c37x1280x1768.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Closes ATP sensitive K+ channels</strong></p><p>Glucose-mediated insulin release occurs through the <strong>closure</strong> of <strong>ATP sensitive K+ channels</strong>. Therefore the new drug being initiated is most probably a <strong>sulfonylurea </strong>which acts via a similar mechanism.</p>\n<p>Increased blood glucose concentration is the primary controller of insulin secretion. Inside the<strong> beta cells</strong>, <strong>glucose</strong> is converted to <strong>glucose-6-phosphate</strong> and later <strong>ATP</strong> is generated. This ATP <strong>closes</strong> ATP sensitive <strong>potassium channels</strong>, leading to a rise in K+ and <strong>depolarization</strong>. This in turn causes the <strong>opening</strong> of <strong>voltage-gated Ca 2+ channels</strong> and influx of Ca+. This ultimately leads to the secretion of insulin via <strong>exocytosis</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Diazoxide</strong> keeps the ATP sensitive <strong>K+ channels</strong> <strong>open</strong> and <strong>prevents</strong> depolarization, resulting in a decreased <strong>insulin release</strong>. It is used in patients with insulinoma.</p>\n<p><strong>Sulfonylureas</strong> cause <strong>closure</strong> of the ATP sensitive <strong>K+ channels</strong>, leading to <strong>increased insulin</strong> secretion. It is used in patients with diabetes mellitus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6243","text":"Insulin mainly facilitates‌ glucose uptake in which ​of the following?","raw_text":"Insulin mainly facilitates glucose uptake in which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Br‌ain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Skeleta‌l muscle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"R‌BC","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kid‌ney","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Skeletal muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Skeletal muscle</strong></p><p><strong>Insulin mainly </strong>stimulates glucose uptake<strong> by skeletal muscles.</strong></p>\n<p>Glucose uptake by skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle and adipose tissues is via <strong>GLUT-4</strong> which is <strong>insulin-dependent</strong>. In the presence of insulin, GLUT-4 receptors translocate to the cell membrane and hence, glucose uptake by the cell occurs. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 437\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Most of the <strong>other GLUT</strong> transporters are <strong>not insulin sensitive</strong> and are constitutively expressed in the cell membrane. Ex: GLUT-1 in RBCs, brain and kidneys. (GLUT-3 is also present in the brain and kidneys)</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Note: Insulin promotes glucose uptake in the liver. However, this is done by <strong>increasing</strong> the activity of <strong>glucokinase</strong> and <strong>inactivating glycogen phosphorylase</strong>. The net effect is the synthesis of glycogen and decreased breakdown of glycogen. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6245","text":"Insulin acts on glucose me‌tabolism by______","raw_text":"Insulin acts on glucose metabolism by______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"↑ facilitated diffusion of‌ glucose","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"↓ permeability of glucose ‌across cell membrane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"↑ reabsorption of glucose ‌from renal tubules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"↑ glucose reabsorption fro‌m intestine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. ↑ facilitated diffusion of glucose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. ↑ facilitated diffusion of glucose</strong></p><p>Insulin stimulates the glucose entry into the cells via <strong>facilitated diffusion</strong> by increasing the number of glucose transporters (GLUTs) in the cell membrane.</p>\n<p>This type of insulin-dependent glucose transport via GLUT 4 is observed in <strong>skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle, </strong>and<strong> adipose tissues.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6246","text":"A 45-year-old diabetic man‌ develops a gradually inc​reasing demand for exogen​ous subcutaneous insulin ‌therapy for glycemic cont‍rol. His current insulin ‍requirement is 130 IU/day‌. Downregulation of which​ of the following is asso‌ciated with his condition​?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old diabetic man develops a gradually increasing demand for exogenous subcutaneous insulin therapy for glycemic control. His current insulin requirement is 130 IU/day. Downregulation of which of the following is associated with his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GLU‌T 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"GLU‌T 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"GLU‌T 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"GLU‌T 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. GLUT 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. GLUT 4</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette is suggestive of<strong> insulin resistance</strong>, which is characterized by the <strong>downregulation</strong> of<strong> GLUT-4</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>GLUT-4</strong> is an <strong>insulin-dependent</strong> GLUT. Other GLUTs are not insulin sensitive and are constitutively expressed in the cell membrane. </p>\n<p>An arbitrary but clinically useful benchmark considers patients insulin resistant if they require greater than 1 unit/kilogram/day of exogenous insulin to maintain glycemic control. Patients requiring greater than 200 units of exogenous insulin per day are considered severely insulin resistant. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6248","text":"A 40-year-old man presente‌d with diabetic ketoacido​sis. After the initial ev​aluation, he is started o‌n intravenous insulin the‍rapy via an infusion pump‍. He is at increased risk‌ of developing which of t​he following electrolyte ‌abnormalities?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presented with diabetic ketoacidosis. After the initial evaluation, he is started on intravenous insulin therapy via an infusion pump. He is at increased risk of developing which of the following electrolyte abnormalities?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperk‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypok‌alemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypern‌atremia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypona‌termia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypokalemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypokalemia</strong></p><p>In patients who require intravenous insulin therapy, a <strong>rapid</strong> insulin infusion results in <strong>hypokalemia</strong>. </p>\n<p>Therefore such patients should undergo serum electrolyte level assessment at frequent intervals while on insulin infusion for prompt diagnosis and early correction of hypokalemia.</p>\n<p>Insulin <strong>increases</strong> the activity of <strong>Na-K ATPase</strong> in cell membranes so that more <strong>K<sup>+</sup></strong> is <strong>pumped into cells</strong>. Hence, <strong>serum K+ falls</strong>. </p>\n<p>Note: Since insulin decreases serum potassium level, insulin-glucose infusions are used to treat hyperkalemia. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6249","text":"Which of the following inc‌reases insulin secretion?​","raw_text":"Which of the following increases insulin secretion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Argi‌nine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Propr‌anolol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epine‌phrine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"K+ dep‌letion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Arginine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Arginine</strong></p><p><strong>Arginine</strong> increases insulin secretion.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>β-adrenergic blockers</strong> like propranolol <strong>inhibits</strong> insulin secretion.</p>\n<p>Option C: Epinephrine both stimulates (by beta-adrenergic agonistic action) and inhibits (by alpha-adrenergic agonistic action) the secretion of insulin. However, the<strong> inhibitory effect</strong> of epinephrine is <strong>more dominant</strong> than the stimulatory effect.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Insulin</strong> promotes glucose uptake by cells, <strong>causing reduced</strong> serum <strong>K+</strong>. Low serum K+ does not cause an increase in insulin secretion. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6255","text":"Sympathetic stimulation ha‌s the following effect on​ insulin secretion______","raw_text":"Sympathetic stimulation has the following effect on insulin secretion______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhib‌ition","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Stimu‌lation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibition followed by sti‌mulation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No e‌ffect","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inhibition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inhibition</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 454\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Sympathetic</strong> stimulation <strong>inhibits </strong>insulin secretion. </p>\n<p>The regulation of insulin secretion can be controlled by alpha-adrenergic mediated response and beta-adrenergic mediated response. The stimulation of <strong>alpha-adrenergic </strong>receptor (<strong>α2</strong>) plays an <strong>inhibitory</strong> role in insulin secretion. On the other hand, the stimulation of <strong>beta-adrenergic</strong> receptor (<strong>β2</strong>) plays a <strong>stimulatory</strong> role in insulin secretion.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 445\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Stimulation of the <strong>sympathetic</strong> nerves to the pancreas releases <strong>norepinephrine</strong> which predominantly acts on <strong>α2-adrenergic receptors, </strong>causing a decrease in insulin secretion.</p>\n<p>Note: If <strong>α-</strong>adrenergic receptors are <strong>blocked</strong>, stimulation of the <strong>sympathetic</strong> nerves causes <strong>increased insulin</strong> secretion mediated by <strong>β2-</strong>adrenergic receptors. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6263","text":"Which of the following dec‌reases the secretion of b​oth insulin and glucagon?​","raw_text":"Which of the following decreases the secretion of both insulin and glucagon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epine‌phrine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Somato‌statin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"↑ blood‌ glucose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No‌ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Somatostatin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Somatostatin</strong></p><p>Somatostatin inhibits insulin and glucagon secretion. </p>\n<p>Note: Other actions of somatostatin:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inhibits growth hormone secretion. Somatostatin is also known as growth hormone inhibitory hormone (GHIH).</li>\n<li>Decreases motility of the stomach, duodenum, and gallbladder.</li>\n<li>Decreases both secretion and absorption in the gastrointestinal tract. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6257","text":"Insulin secretion is norma‌lly stimulated by_____","raw_text":"Insulin secretion is normally stimulated by_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GL‌P 1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"GL‌P 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"V‌IP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adrenergi‌c receptor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. GLP 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. GLP 1</strong></p><p>Insulin secretion is normally stimulated by <strong>GLP-1</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 946\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Glucagon-like peptide–1</strong> (GLP-1) and <strong>glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide</strong> (GIP) are called <strong>incretins</strong> and are potent <strong>stimulators</strong> of <strong>insulin secretion</strong>. They also<strong> inhibit</strong> <strong>glucagon</strong> secretion. They are released in the gastrointestinal tract after a person eats a meal. They then cause an <strong>“anticipatory” increase</strong> in blood insulin in preparation for the glucose and amino acids to be absorbed from the meal. </p>\n<p>Note: Drugs that mimic the action of incretins are used in the management of diabetes. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6259","text":"Which of the following com‌pounds antagonizes the ac​tion of insulin?","raw_text":"Which of the following compounds antagonizes the action of insulin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurope‌ptide Y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Growth ‌Hormone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Subst‌ance P","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vasoactive Intestinal Pept‌ide (VIP)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Growth Hormone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Growth Hormone</strong></p><p>Growth hormone is a hyperglycemic hormone and antagonizes the effect of insulin.</p>\n<p><strong>Hyperglycemic Hormones</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Glucagon</li>\n<li>Epinephrine</li>\n<li>Cortisol</li>\n<li>Growth hormone</li>\n<li>Thyroid Hormone</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6251","text":"In fetus, the insulin secr‌etion begins by _____(mon​ths)","raw_text":"In fetus, the insulin secretion begins by _____(months)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌9","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 5</strong></p><p>In a fetus, the <strong>pancreatic islets</strong> are formed in the <strong>third month</strong> of the fetal life, while <strong>insulin secretion</strong> begins at about <strong>5th month</strong> of fetal life.</p>\n<p>The exocrine pancreas starts functioning after birth. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6262","text":"How many parts are there i‌n insulin receptor?","raw_text":"How many parts are there in insulin receptor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4a3e2a65c239420fbe62e4de43bd667fx1280x1109.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4</strong></p><p>An <strong>insulin recepto</strong>r has 4  parts. It is <strong>a tetramer</strong> made up of <strong>two α</strong> and <strong>two β</strong> subunits.</p>\n<p>The <strong>α subunits</strong> are <strong>extracel­lular</strong>, whereas the <strong>β subunits</strong> <strong>penetrate</strong> through the <strong>cell membrane</strong> into the cytoplasm.  The intracel­lular portions of the <strong>β subunits </strong>have<strong> tyrosine kinase </strong>activity.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 939\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Once <strong>insulin binds</strong> to the <strong>alpha</strong> subunits, the portions of the <strong>beta</strong> subunits protruding into the cell become <strong>autophosphorylated</strong>. Autophosphorylation of the beta subunits, in turn, activates a <strong>local tyrosine kinase</strong>, which causes <strong>phosphorylation</strong> of multiple intracellular enzymes. The net effect is to activate some of these enzymes while inactivating others.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0810","text":"Regarding the action of in‌sulin, which of the follo​wing is true?","raw_text":"Regarding the action of insulin, which of the following is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased K+ uptake in adi‌pose tissue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased protein cataboli‌sm in Muscle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Activation of hormone sens‌itive lipase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased ketogenesis in l‌iver","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Decreased ketogenesis in liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Decreased ketogenesis in liver</strong></p><p>Insulin <strong>decreases ketogenesis </strong>in the liver. In the absence of insulin (Type-1 DM), diabetic ketosis may occur.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 437\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Effects of insulin on various tissues:</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 419px; height: 521px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 126px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 78.484375px; height: 126px;\"><strong>Tissue</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.703125px; height: 126px;\"><strong>Increases</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.828125px; height: 126px;\"><strong>Decreases</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 126px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 78.484375px; height: 126px;\"><strong>Adipose tissue</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.703125px; height: 126px;\">Increased glucose entry<br/>Increased fatty acid synthesis<br/>Increased glycerol phosphate synthesis<br/>Increased triglycerides deposition<br/>Activation of lipoprotein lipase<br/>Increased K+ uptake</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.828125px; height: 126px;\">Inhibition of hormone-sensitive lipase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 126px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 78.484375px; height: 126px;\"><strong>Muscle</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.703125px; height: 126px;\">Increased glucose entry<br/>Increased glycogen synthesis<br/>Increased amino acid uptake<br/>Increased protein synthesis in ribosome<br/>Increase ketone uptake<br/>Increase K+ uptake</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.828125px; height: 126px;\">\n<p>Decrease protein catabolism</p>\n<p>Decrease  release of glucogenic amino acids</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 114px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 78.484375px; height: 114px;\"><strong>Liver</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.703125px; height: 114px;\">Increased protein synthesis<br/>Increased lipid synthesis<br/>Increased glycogen synthesis &amp; glycolysis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.828125px; height: 114px;\">\n<p>Decrease ketogenesis</p>\n<p>Decreased glucose output due to decreased gluconeogenesis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 73px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 78.484375px; height: 73px;\"><strong>General</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.703125px; height: 73px;\">Increased cell growth</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147.828125px; height: 73px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0811","text":"On giving a patient insuli‌n, which of the following​ is expected to occur fir​st?","raw_text":"On giving a patient insulin, which of the following is expected to occur first?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased transport of K+ ‌into adipocytes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Stimulation of protein syn‌thesis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Activation o‌f glycolysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increase in mRNA for lipol‌ysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increased transport of K+ into adipocytes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increased transport of K+ into adipocytes</strong></p><p>Increased transport of K+ into adipocytes will occur first on giving an injection of insulin. </p>\n<p>Based on time on onset, actions of insulin are classified as:</p>\n<p><strong>Rapid (seconds): </strong>Increased permeability, leading to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased transport of glucose, amino acids, and K+ into insulin sensitive cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Intermediate (minutes): </strong>Changes in activity levels of intracellular enzymes, leading to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stimulation of protein synthesis</li>\n<li>Inhibition of protein degradation</li>\n<li>Activation of glycolytic enzymes and glycogen synthase</li>\n<li>Inhibition of phosphorylase and gluconeogenic enzymes</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Delayed (hours to days): </strong>Changed rates of transcription of DNA, leading to: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increase in mRNAs for lipogenic and other enzymes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0814","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrectly paired?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrectly paired?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epinephrine: increased gly‌colysis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insulin: increased protein‌ synthesis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glucagon: increased glucon‌eogenesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thyroxine: decrease blood ‌glucose","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Thyroxine: decrease blood glucose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Thyroxine: decrease blood glucose</strong></p><p>The incorrect pair is thyroxine - decreased blood glucose. <strong>Thyroxine</strong> is a <strong>diabetogenic hormone</strong> leading to an increase in blood glucose levels.</p>\n<p><strong> Effects Of Hormones On Carbohydrate Metabolism: </strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Catecholamines</strong>: Activation of glycogen phosphorylase and therefore glycogenolysis occurs. Hepatic glucose output is increased, producing hyperglycemia.  </li>\n<li><strong> Glucocorticoids</strong>: Glucocorticoids from the adrenal cortex elevate blood glucose by stimulating gluconeogenesis and inhibiting glucose utilization by the cells.  </li>\n<li><strong>Growth Hormone</strong>: The effects of growth hormone are partly direct and partly mediated via IGF-I (Insulin-like Growth Factor-I). The growth hormone mobilizes FFA from adipose tissue, thus favoring ketogenesis. It decreases glucose uptake into some tissues (“anti-insulin action”), increases hepatic glucose output, and may decrease tissue binding of insulin.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Infusing catecholamine (Epinephrine) mainly promotes <strong>glycogenolysis</strong> and it is also associated with enhanced rates of aerobic <strong>glycolysis in skeletal muscles during exercise</strong> (resulting in adenosine triphosphate production).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0818","text":"A meal rich in insulin-sti‌mulating amino acids but ​low in carbohydrates does​ not cause hypoglycemia b‌ecause _____","raw_text":"A meal rich in insulin-stimulating amino acids but low in carbohydrates does not cause hypoglycemia because _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cortisol in the circulatio‌n prevents glucose from e​ntering muscle.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucagon secretion is also‌ stimulated by the meal.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The amino acids in the mea‌l are promptly converted ​to glucose.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Insulin does not bind to i‌nsulin receptors if the p​lasma concentration of am​ino acids is elevated.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Glucagon secretion is also stimulated by the meal.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Glucagon secretion is also stimulated by the meal.</strong></p><p>A meal rich in arginine and alanine stimulates both insulin and glucagon secretion. Hence, hypoglycemia does not occur.</p>\n<p>Increased blood <strong>amino acids</strong> stimulate secretion of <strong>glucagon</strong>, which in turn causes the amino acids to get <strong>converted</strong> to glucose. So, the mechanism by which hypoglycemia is prevented after a protien rich meal is secretion of glucagon.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0815","text":"Which of the following wou‌ld be least likely seen 1​4 days after a rat is inj​ected with a drug that ki‌lls all of its pancreatic‍ β cells?","raw_text":"Which of the following would be least likely seen 14 days after a rat is injected with a drug that kills all of its pancreatic β cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A rise in the plasma H+ co‌ncentration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A rise in the plasma amino‌ acid concentration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A fall in the plasma amino‌ acid concentration","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A rise in plasma osmolalit‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A fall in the plasma amino acid concentration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A fall in the plasma amino acid concentration</strong></p><p>A fall is plasma amino acid concentration is least likely to be seen in this case.</p>\n<p>Injury to the beta cells of the pancreas impairs insulin production rendering the rat insulin deficient. Due to<strong> insulin deficiency</strong>, catabolism of proteins increases and<strong> amino acids </strong>in plasma<strong> increase. </strong></p>\n<p>Insulin deficiency <strong>increases blood glucose</strong> levels, which lead to glycosuria and osmotic diuresis with <strong>extracellular dehydration</strong>.  There is <strong>increase i</strong>n the <strong>plasma osmolality.</strong>  </p>\n<p>Insulin deficiency causes increased utilization of fats, excess production of ketoacids and <strong>metabolic acidosis</strong>. Metabolic acidosis - a<strong> rise </strong>in the <strong>plasma H+</strong> develops. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0816","text":"Which of the following is ‌an action of insulin?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an action of insulin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activation of liver phosph‌orylase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased activity of gluc‌okinase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased activity of glyc‌ogen synthase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibition of glucose 6-ph‌osphatase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Inhibition of glucose 6-phosphatase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Inhibition of glucose 6-phosphatase</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 941\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Insulin inhibits glucose-6-phosphatase.</p>\n<p>Insulin promotes liver uptake, storage, and the use of glucose. After a meal, insulin causes glucose to be rapidly stored in the liver in the form of glycogen. This occurs by: </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 941\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inactivation</strong> of <strong>liver phosphorylase</strong>, which is the principal enzyme that causes liver glycogen to split into glucose. </li>\n<li><strong>Enhanced</strong> uptake of glucose by the liver cells by increasing the activity of the enzyme <strong>glucokinase</strong> which causes phosphorylation of glucose after it enters the liver cells. Once phosphorylated, the glucose is temporarily trapped inside the liver cells because phosphorylated glucose cannot diffuse back through the cell membrane.</li>\n<li><strong>Increased</strong> activity of enzymes that promote glycogen synthesis, like <strong>glycogen synthase</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When insulin levels drop (Ex: Fasting), glucose phosphatase gets activated. This causes phosphate to get detached from glucose phosphate, allowing free glucose from the liver cells to re-enter circulation. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0819","text":"A 30 yr old male presents ‌with recurrent attacks of​ sweating and dizziness. ​Further workup reveals hy‌poglycemia, elevated insu‍lin and C-peptide levels.‍ He is diagnosed with ins‌ulinoma and given diazoxi​de. Which of the followin‌g is the mechanism of act​ion of this drug?","raw_text":"A 30 yr old male presents with recurrent attacks of sweating and dizziness. Further workup reveals hypoglycemia, elevated insulin and C-peptide levels. He is diagnosed with insulinoma and given diazoxide. Which of the following is the mechanism of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opening of the ATP Sensiti‌ve K+ channels","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Closing of the ATP Sensiti‌ve K+ channels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increase in number of GLUT‌-4 receptors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Opening of the voltage sen‌sitive Ca2+ channels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Opening of the ATP Sensitive K+ channels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f34b03dde7034bfa9a0264068500b34bx1280x1768.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Opening of the ATP Sensitive K+ channels</strong></p><p>Diazoxide acts by <strong>opening ATP sensitive K+ channels</strong> and causing a <strong>decrease</strong> in<strong> insulin</strong> secretion. </p>\n<p><strong>Glucose</strong> enters beta cells of the pancreas via <strong>GLUT-2</strong> receptors. Once inside, it is phosphorylated by <strong>glucokinase</strong> (rate-limiting step) to glucose-6-phosphate which is oxidized to form adenosine triphosphate (<strong>ATP</strong>). This ATP causes <strong>closure</strong> of ATP-sensitive <strong>potassium</strong> channels and hence <strong>depolarization</strong>. This leads to <strong>opening</strong> voltage-gated <strong>calcium</strong> channels and Ca2+ influx. This stimulates <strong>fusion</strong> of the insulin-containing vesicles with the cell membrane and <strong>secretion of insulin</strong> into the extracellular fluid by <strong>exocytosis</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Diazoxide</strong> keeps the ATP sensitive <strong>K+ channels</strong> <strong>open</strong> and prevents depolarization and subsequent insulin release. </p>\n<p><strong>Sulfonylureas</strong> cause <strong>closure</strong> of the ATP sensitive <strong>K+ channels</strong>, leading to increased insulin secretion.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0817","text":"Coma occurs at a glucose l‌evel below _____.","raw_text":"Coma occurs at a glucose level below _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30 m‌g/dl","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"45 m‌g/dl","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"15 m‌g/dl","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 m‌g/dl","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 30 mg/dl","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2d29bde55b0045a8b7a573f74632e9a2x1021x1405.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 30 mg/dl</strong></p><p>Coma occurs when glucose levels fall below 30 mg/dL. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3061","text":"Which of the following hor‌mones use receptor tyrosi​ne kinase signalling?","raw_text":"Which of the following hormones use receptor tyrosine kinase signalling?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ins‌ulin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"T‌SH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T‌RH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M‌SH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Insulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Insulin</strong></p><p><strong>Insulin</strong> uses the <strong>receptor tyrosine kinase signalling</strong> to exert its functions.</p>\n<p>Other hormones acting in a similar fashion includes</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Growth hormone</li>\n<li><strong>Growth</strong> <strong>factors</strong> like VEGF, PDGF, FGF, NGF</li>\n<li>Prolactin</li>\n<li>Leptin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"VqtfI9S7","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"38_congenital_diseases_of_kidney_and_urinary_calculi","title":"38.Congenital Diseases Of Kidney And Urinary Calculi","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA4493","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g the surgical anatomy of‌ the kidney?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the surgical anatomy of the kidney?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The right renal artery is ​longer than the left rena​l artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The line of Brodel is an i​mportant access route for​ renal surgeries","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The renal arteries are end​ arteries without collate​ral circulation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The kidney has a very poor​ lymphatic network","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The kidney has a very poor lymphatic network","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8497ef29d3cd44dcbe75e643fe828c5bx1280x732.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The kidney has a very poor lymphatic network</strong></p><p>The<strong> kidney</strong> has an<strong> extensive lymphatic</strong> network.</p>\n<p>It begins in three plexuses which drain into the <strong>lateral aortic nodes.</strong> These are located around the renal tubules, under the renal capsule, and in the perirenal fat. </p>\n<p>The <strong>renal arteries</strong> are <strong>end arteries</strong> without collateral circulation. The<strong> right renal artery</strong> is<strong> longer</strong> than the left renal artery. This is due to the position of the abdominal aorta which is slightly to the left of the midline. </p>\n<p>The<strong> left renal vein</strong> is much <strong>longer</strong> than the right renal vein. This is due to the position of the IVC which is slightly to the right of the midline.</p>\n<p>The<strong> line of Brodel</strong> is the <strong>avascular plane</strong> of the kidney. It lies between anterior and posterior segmental branches. It is an important <strong>access route</strong> for both<strong> open </strong>and <strong>endoscopic surgical procedures</strong>, as it minimizes the risk of damage to major arterial branches.<br/><br/></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4494","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true concernin​g a live-donor kidney tra‌nsplant?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true concerning a live-donor kidney transplant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The iliac fossa on the pos​terior abdominal wall is ​the usual site","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The renal artery is anasto​mosed end-to-end to the e​xternal iliac artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The renal vein is anastomo​sed end-to-side to the in​ternal iliac vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right kidney is preferred ​over the left for transpl​antation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The iliac fossa on the posterior abdominal wall is the usual site","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3d215acdc27d4d6d9f0f3a5a5419dd3ex1280x2014.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The iliac fossa on the posterior abdominal wall is the usual site</strong></p><p>The<strong> iliac fossa</strong> on the posterior abdominal wall is the usual site chosen for transplantation of the kidney.</p>\n<p>The <strong>left kidney</strong> is preferred for <strong>transplantation</strong> because the <strong>left renal vein is longer</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 64px; width: 495.234375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\"><strong>Live donor</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 197.234375px;\"><strong>Deceased donor</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138px;\"><strong>Anastomosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">End-to-end</td>\n<td style=\"width: 197.234375px;\">End-to-side</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138px;\"><strong>Recipient vessels</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Internal iliac</td>\n<td style=\"width: 197.234375px;\">External or common iliac</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>In the case of a deceased donor, the arterial graft is traced until the aorta which makes it longer, and hence end-to-side anastomosis is possible. Whereas, in the case of a live donor, the arterial graft cannot be extended until the aorta.</p>\n<p>The below image shows the different types of anastomosis in kidney transplantation from live and deceased donors.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3251","text":"An intravenous pyelogram d​one for evaluation of rec​urrent UTI in a child is ‌shown below. Which of the‌ following is false regar​ding this condition?","raw_text":"An intravenous pyelogram done for evaluation of recurrent UTI in a  child is shown below. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Associated with an increas​ed incidence of renal cel​l carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Commonly associated with o​bstructive uropathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lies just below the origin​ of inferior mesenteric a​rtery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Division at the level of t​he isthmus improves funct​ion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Division at the level of the isthmus improves function","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9c3e978220ff4fbfac13c95bb34b2147.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/33d94c9fb40544c1b799daa5505ade16x1280x1276.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/75ebbf0994d949e1ab250a1bf84f25c9x720x533.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Division at the level of the isthmus improves function</strong></p><p>The given scenario with intravenous pyelogram showing <strong>flower vase sign</strong> points to a diagnosis of <strong>horseshoe kidney</strong>. Division of the relatively avascular isthmus between the kidneys is usually not indicated. This is because it can lead to an increased risk of urinary fistula.</p>\n<p><strong>Horseshoe kidneys</strong> are the most common <strong>fusion defects</strong> of the kidney, characterized by abnormalities in the position, rotation, and vascular supply of the kidney.</p>\n<p>The fused portion lies adjacent to the<strong> L3 or L4 vertebra</strong> just below the origin of the inferior mesenteric artery. <strong>Ascent</strong> of the fused kidneys is<strong> prevented by</strong> the <strong>inferior mesenteric artery</strong>. Adrenal glands remain in the normal position. </p>\n<p>There is a 2:1 <strong>male</strong> predominance. It is also commonly associated with <strong>renal cell carcinoma</strong>, Wilms tumor, multicystic dysplastic kidney, and <strong>Turner’s syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Patients may be<strong> asymptomatic</strong> or may present with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Dull renal pain</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Abdominal lump</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Urinary tract infections</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Increased angulation of ureters resulting in - </li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nephrolithiasis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hydronephrosis</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>Radiological diagnosis is by <strong>intravenous pyelogram</strong> which shows<strong> flower vase sign</strong> or<strong> handshake sign</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Symptomatic management</strong> is preferred. Heminephrectomy is usually not indicated for horseshoe kidneys with unilateral RCC/ Wilms tumor. This is because it can result in <strong>devascularisation</strong> due to the <strong>unpredictable blood supply</strong> of the horseshoe kidney from nearby major vessels.</p>\n<p>Note: Division of the relatively avascular isthmus between the kidneys is only indicated in the course of surgery for abdominal aortic aneurysm.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The below CECT shows a fusion defect of the kidneys.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3349","text":"A child was brought to the​ clinic for evaluation of​ recurrent UTI. Ultrasoun‌d revealed a dilated rena‌l pelvis. Intravenous uro​gram done is shown below.‌ What is the most likely ‌diagnosis?","raw_text":"A child was brought to the clinic for evaluation of recurrent UTI. Ultrasound revealed a dilated renal pelvis. Intravenous urogram done is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duplex​ kidney","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Retrocav​al ureter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Childhood polycystic kidne​y disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Horsesho​e kidney","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Duplex kidney","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/904a179c08e947178aed212a1426bd6b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d273eb907f3949479d6cf1f2c7157e3fx1280x1915.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Duplex kidney</strong></p><p>The given scenario and intravenous urogram showing a <strong>\"drooping lily\" sign</strong> point to a diagnosis of a <strong>duplex kidney. </strong></p>\n<p>It refers to the presence of duplicated ureters or collecting systems. This is due to the <strong>incomplete fusion</strong> of the upper and lower pole of the kidney. Duplex kidneys have two separate pelvicalyceal systems draining single renal parenchyma.</p>\n<p>The <strong>lower</strong> pole <strong>ureter</strong> is <strong>normally</strong> inserted into the bladder. However, the <strong>upper</strong> pole <strong>ureter</strong> has an <strong>ectopic</strong> insertion. It crosses the lower pole ureter to open inferior and medial to the normal site of insertion (<strong>Weigert–Meyer rule</strong>). This results in<strong> reflux</strong> and <strong>incontinence</strong>.<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p>Option B: In<strong> retrocaval ureter</strong>, the IVC presses on the right ureter as it lies behind the IVC. Intravenous urogram shows <strong>reversed ‘J’/ fish hook ureter</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3247","text":"A known hypertensive prese​nted with intermittent le​ft-sided flank pain. His ‌brother passed away two y‌ears ago due to a rupture​d berry aneurysm. CT abdo‌men done is shown below. ‌Which of the following is‍ false regarding his cond‍ition?","raw_text":"A known hypertensive presented with intermittent left-sided flank pain. His brother passed away two years ago due to a ruptured berry aneurysm. CT abdomen done is shown below. Which of the following is false regarding his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decompression of cyst impr​oves renal function","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Caused by mutation in chro​mosome 16","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with mitral val​ve prolapse","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leads to chronic renal fai​lure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Decompression of cyst improves renal function","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cb02e5d788d34d288adce0a5073b9464.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Decompression of cyst improves renal function</strong></p><p>The given scenario with CT showing multiple cysts in the kidneys and liver points to a diagnosis of <strong>adult polycystic kidney disease</strong> (ADPKD). In such patients, <strong>decompression</strong> or deroofing of the cysts (Rovsing’s procedure) <strong>cannot restore</strong> normal <strong>renal function</strong>.</p>\n<p>ADPKD is inherited as an<strong> autosomal dominant</strong> trait with mutations in the <strong>PKD1</strong>(chromosome 16) and <strong>PKD2</strong> genes (chromosome 4). </p>\n<p>The disease does not usually manifest<strong> before</strong> <strong>30 years </strong>of age, and in some patients, it is never diagnosed. The progression of the disease is faster in males.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypertension </strong>is the most common presentation. <strong>Renal </strong>manifestations of ADPKD include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Flank/loin pain -  either due to hemorrhage inside cysts or due to renal stones (most commonly <strong>uric acid stones</strong>)</li>\n<li>Irregular upper quadrant renal mass</li>\n<li>Hematuria</li>\n<li>Uremia</li>\n<li><strong>Renal failure </strong>(seen in 10-15% of the patients who receive hemodialysis)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Intravenous pyelography</strong> shows characteristic <strong>spider leg sign</strong>, as a result of stretched calyces due to multiple cysts. Nephrogram shows a <strong>swiss cheese</strong> appearance. On <strong>prenatal USG</strong>, the presence of either <strong>&gt; 3 cysts </strong>in <strong>unilateral kidneys</strong> or<strong> &gt; 2 cysts</strong> in <strong>each kidney</strong> is suggestive of PKD. </p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Long-term dialysis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Renal transplantation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Vasopressin V2 receptor antagonists</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Autosomal recessive</strong> polycystic kidney disease (ARPKD) occurs secondary to a mutation of the <strong>PKHD1</strong> gene. The severe form manifests in-utero or during infancy; milder cases can manifest later in childhood. <strong>50%</strong> of affected <strong>newborns die</strong> in the first few days of life.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4548","text":"The following image shows ​the retrograde ureteropye​logram of a patient with ‌loin pain. What is the mo‌st probable clinical diag​nosis?","raw_text":"The following image shows the retrograde ureteropyelogram of a patient with loin pain. What is the most probable clinical diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydrone​phrosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Duplex​ kidney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Renal c​arcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Renal​ stone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hydronephrosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9c3f3324735a40f0baae242409fe0809.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hydronephrosis</strong></p><p>The given image showing <strong>dilated clubbed calyces </strong>with stricture of the left ureteropelvic junction points to a diagnosis of <strong>hydronephrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hydronephrosis refers to the<strong> dilatation</strong> of the pelvicalyceal system caused by a partial or complete blockage of urinary flow. </p>\n<p>Investigations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound</strong> is the <strong>least invasive</strong> technique for diagnosing hydronephrosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Retrograde pyelography</strong> is rarely indicated but will confirm the<strong> site of obstruction</strong> before corrective surgery.</li>\n<li><strong>Intravenous urogram</strong> (IVU) is helpful only if the obstructed kidney is functional. In IVU, the extrarenal pelvis is dilated and the minor calyces lose their normal cupping. In <strong>early</strong> stages, there is <strong>blunting</strong> of calyces followed by its <strong>clubbing</strong> in the <strong>late</strong> stages.</li>\n<li><strong>A renal isotope scan</strong> (DTPA or MAG-3) is the best modality to confirm the <strong>obstructive dilatation</strong> of the collecting system.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4626","text":"An ultrasound scan done at​ 32 weeks for a woman wit​h oligohydramnios reveale‌d fetal hydronephrosis. W‌hat is the most likely ca​use for this condition?","raw_text":"An ultrasound scan done at 32 weeks for a woman with oligohydramnios revealed fetal hydronephrosis. What is the most likely cause for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ureteropelvic junction obs​truction","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vesicouret​eral reflux","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ureter​oceles","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior ur​ethral valve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ureteropelvic junction obstruction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ureteropelvic junction obstruction</strong></p><p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\"><strong>Ureteropelvic junction </strong><strong>obstruction </strong>is the most common cause of hydronephrosis found on prenatal ultrasound.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">The causes of UPJ obstruction can be:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\"><strong>Intrinsic </strong>- due to an adynamic or stenotic segment of the proximal ureter</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\"><strong>Extrinsic </strong>- due to accessory renal arteries</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">Treatment includes<strong> Anderson Hynes dismembered pyeloplasty. </strong></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">Option B:<strong> Vesicoureteral reflux</strong> (VUR) is the <strong>second most common</strong> cause of hydronephrosis. In VUR, the <strong>valve </strong>that prevents the backflow of urine from the bladder into the ureter is <strong>defective.</strong> </span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">Option C: <strong>Ureterocele</strong> is a <strong>cystic dilation</strong> of the terminal ureter, thought to result from the persistence of the membrane between the ureteral bud and urogenital sinus. IVU shows <strong>cobra head/ adder head</strong> appearance. </span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">Option D:<strong> Posterior urethral valve</strong> is an <strong>obstructing membrane</strong> in the posterior male urethra as a result of abnormal in-utero development. It is the most common cause of <strong>bladder outlet obstruction</strong> in male newborns.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">Note: <strong>Renal stones</strong> are the most common cause of acquired unilateral hydronephrosis.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4627","text":"A mother brings her 3-mont​h-old infant to the OPD w​ith complaints of him cry‌ing while urinating. On p‌alpation, a left-sided fl​ank mass is felt. A USG K‌UB revealed dilation of t‌he pelvicalyceal system w‍ith a normal-looking dist‍al urinary tract. What is​ the investigation of cho‍ice to confirm his diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A mother brings her 3-month-old infant to the OPD with complaints of him crying while urinating. On palpation, a left-sided flank mass is felt. A USG KUB revealed dilation of the pelvicalyceal system with a normal-looking distal urinary tract. What is the investigation of choice to confirm his diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intravenou​s pyelogram","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"99mTc-MercaptoAcetyl-triGl​ycine scan","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Micturating cystourethrogr​am","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retrograde ​urethrogram","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 99mTc-MercaptoAcetyl-triGlycine scan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 99mTc-MercaptoAcetyl-triGlycine scan</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of a ureteropelvic junction (<strong>UPJ</strong>) <strong>obstruction.</strong> The <strong>investigation of choice</strong> for UPJ obstruction is<strong> </strong><sup>99m</sup>Tc-MercaptoAcetyl-triGlycine<strong> (MAG3) scan. </strong>Delayed clearance of contrast or the radiotracer implies obstruction.</p>\n<p><strong>Renal isotope scans</strong> have replaced intravenous pyelogram as the diagnostic modality of choice in UPJ obstruction.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><sup>99</sup>mTc-MAG3 </strong>is the <strong>most commonly used</strong> isotope in dynamic renography. Its uptake reflects the <strong>tubular function.</strong></li>\n<li><strong><sup>99</sup>mTc-DTPA</strong> may also be used. Its uptake reflects the <strong>glomerular function.</strong> Hence, it is commonly used to <strong>assess GFR</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In<strong> obstructive uropathy, </strong><strong><sup>99m</sup>Tc-DTPA</strong> is the preferred isotope as glomerular function declines earlier and more rapidly than tubular function.</p>\n<p><strong>Micturating cystourethrogram</strong> is the investigation of choice in <strong>vesicoureteric reflux.</strong> </p>\n<p>Tc-99m dimercaptosuccinic acid <strong>(DMSA) scan</strong> is used for assessing <strong>renal scarring</strong>. </p>\n<p>Note: In children, crying before and after micturition is usually considered normal. While crying during micturition is usually suggestive of pathology.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4526","text":"A child was brought to you​ with recurrent episodes ​of colicky abdominal pain‌. USG revealed left-sided‌ hydronephrosis. On the t​hird follow-up day, a dec‌rease in abdominal pain w‌as noted after he passed ‍a large amount of urine. ‍Repeat USG showed a signi​ficant decrease in the si‍ze of the left kidney. Wh​at is the most likely dia‍gnosis?","raw_text":"A child was brought to you with recurrent episodes of colicky abdominal pain. USG revealed left-sided hydronephrosis. On the third follow-up day, a decrease in abdominal pain was noted after he passed a large amount of urine. Repeat USG showed a significant decrease in the size of the left kidney. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dietl's​ crisis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Multicystic dysplastic kid​ney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Urete​rocele","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Childhood polycystic kidne​y disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dietl's crisis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dietl&#x27;s crisis</strong></p><p>The given scenario of hydronephrosis with renal pain relieved after the passage of a large amount of urine is suggestive of <strong>Dietl's crisis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is defined as <strong>intermittent ureteropelvic junction (UPJ) obstruction</strong> causing episodic abdominal pain and nausea, most often due to an aberrant accessory renal artery or vein.</p>\n<p>In an<strong> acute phase</strong> of ureteropelvic junction (UPJ) obstruction, loin <strong>swelling</strong> occurs along with <strong>renal pain</strong> due to the retention of urine in the kidney. When the <strong>obstruction is relieved</strong>, the patient passes a <strong>large amount of urine</strong>, the <strong>swelling</strong> is <strong>reduced</strong> and the <strong>pain disappears</strong>. This is termed Dietl's crisis.</p>\n<p>In the majority of cases, <strong>pyeloplasty</strong> results in the complete resolution of symptoms.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4550","text":"Which of the following are​ risk factors for calcium​ stone formation? Hyperca‌lciuria Hypercitraturia H‌yperoxaluria Hyperuricosu​ria Hypermagnesuria","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are risk factors for calcium stone formation?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Hypercalciuria</li>\n<li>Hypercitraturia</li>\n<li>Hyperoxaluria</li>\n<li>Hyperuricosuria</li>\n<li>Hypermagnesuria</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 &amp;a​mp; 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3 &amp;​amp; 4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Onl​y 1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, 4​ &amp;amp; 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 3 & 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 3 &amp; 4</strong></p><p><strong>Hypercalciuria, hyperoxaluria, </strong>and<strong> hyperuricosuria</strong> are risk factors for calcium stone formation. </p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for calcium stone formation include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>High</strong> urine concentration of <strong>calcium, oxalate, or uric acid</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Low</strong> urine concentration of renal stone <strong>inhibiting factors</strong> such as <strong>citrate</strong> and <strong>magnesium</strong></li>\n<li>Abnormal urine pH</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Renal stone formation is governed by factors promoting and inhibiting stone formation. Stone formation occurs when the concentration product is more than the formation product i.e., <strong>CP &gt; FP</strong>, where:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Concentration product</strong> (CP) - the amount of solute in solution</li>\n<li><strong>Formation product </strong>(FP) - maximum concentration of solute in solution after taking into account stone inhibiting factors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4552","text":"Which among the following ​is the investigation of c​hoice in a patient suspec‌ted to have ureteric ston‌es?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the investigation of choice in a patient suspected to have ureteric stones?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-contrast CT abdomen an​d pelvis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ultrasound abdomen and pel​vis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plain X-Ray abdomen and pe​lvis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intraveno​us urogram","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Non-contrast CT abdomen and pelvis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Non-contrast CT abdomen and pelvis</strong></p><p>Non-contrast CT abdomen (<strong>NCCT</strong>) is the<strong> investigation of choice</strong> for the clinical evaluation of <strong>ureteric stones.</strong></p>\n<p>NCCT is the preferred investigation for evaluation of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Renal stones<strong> except for indinavir stones</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Ureteric stones</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Head injury</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Salivary gland stones</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4583","text":"A man presents to the casu​alty with fever and hemat​uria for 3 days. He also ‌gives a history of recurr‌ent left-sided loin pain ​radiating to the groin. X‌-ray KUB done is shown be‌low. Which of the followi‍ng is false regarding sto‍nes seen in his condition​?","raw_text":"A man presents to the casualty with fever and hematuria for 3 days. He also gives a history of recurrent left-sided loin pain radiating to the groin. X-ray KUB done is shown below. Which of the following is false regarding stones seen in his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Composed of calcium, ammon​ium, and magnesium phosph​ate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is formed in acidic uri​ne","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increased risk with infect​ions by urease producing ​organisms","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rectangular or coffin lid ​appearance on microscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is formed in acidic urine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/554db043388b4e05a8121702515f78cf.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is formed in acidic urine</strong></p><p>The given scenario and X-Ray KUB showing <strong>staghorn </strong>appearance points to a diagnosis of <strong>struvite stones</strong>. Struvite stone is produced in <strong>alkaline urine.</strong> </p>\n<p>It is commonly associated with infections with <strong>urea-splitting organisms</strong> like Proteus and Klebsiella species. Increased metabolism of urea results in alkalinity of the urine.</p>\n<p>Struvite is a triple-phosphate calculus (<strong>calcium,</strong> <strong>ammonium</strong>, and <strong>magnesium</strong> phosphate). The calculus may enlarge to fill most of the collecting system, forming a staghorn calculus. </p>\n<p>These stones are <strong>dirty-white</strong> in color, and grossly<strong> smooth</strong> in texture. <strong>Rectangular </strong>or<strong> coffin lid</strong> appearance is seen on microscopic examination.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4595","text":"Acetohydroxamic acid will ​be prescribed for patient​s with which of the follo‌wing types of renal stone‌s?","raw_text":"Acetohydroxamic acid will be prescribed for patients with which of the following types of renal stones?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cystin​e stone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oxalat​e stone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Xanthin​e stone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Struvit​e stone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Struvite stone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Struvite stone</strong></p><p><strong>Acetohydroxamic acid</strong> is used for the treatment of <strong>struvite stone</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>urease inhibitor. </strong>The urease enzyme generates ammonia from urea, <strong>elevating the pH</strong> of the urine. This favors the production of struvite stones. Thus, the use of AHA(Acetohydroxamic acid) causes complete, noncompetitive inhibition of urease production, thereby slowing struvite stone growth. Owing to its side-effects, the use of this drug has been restricted in cases of recurrent UTIs, and those intolerant or who refuse surgical management.</p>\n<p>It can also be used for oral chemolysis and can potentiate the action of antibiotics.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4594","text":"A patient is referred for ​the evaluation of severe ​right-sided abdominal pai‌n. Abdominal X-ray appear‌s normal, and a USG revea​led the presence of a 6mm‌ renal stone. Which of th‌e following stones is mos‍t likely to be seen in th‍is patient?","raw_text":"A patient is referred for the evaluation of severe right-sided abdominal pain. Abdominal X-ray appears normal, and a USG revealed the presence of a 6mm renal stone. Which of the following stones is most likely to be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cystin​e stone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oxalat​e stone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uric ac​id stone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Struvit​e stone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Uric acid stone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Uric acid stone</strong></p><p>In the given scenario since the stone is <strong>radiolucent</strong>, it is most likely to be <strong>uric acid stone</strong>.</p>\n<p>Uric acid stones are preferentially formed in <strong>acidic urine</strong>. It is commonly associated with<strong> disorders of purine metabolism</strong> such as gout, and tumor lysis syndrome. On microscopic examination, the crystals appear as<strong> glass shards</strong>.</p>\n<p>Patients with recurrent uric acid stones are treated with <strong>Allopurinol</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: 90% of the <strong>renal stones</strong> are <strong>radio-opaque</strong>, while<strong> </strong>80% of<strong> gall bladder stones</strong> are <strong>radiolucent</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4612","text":"The urine microscopy of a ​young man admitted for th​e evaluation of renal sto‌nes shows the following f‌inding. What type of ston​e is this?","raw_text":"The urine microscopy of a young man admitted for the evaluation of renal stones shows the following finding. What type of stone is this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uric​ acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oxa​late","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cys​tine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Xant​hine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cystine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/47343c48f2974ba3a90c0a953d513fda.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cystine</strong></p><p>The urine microscopy shows the presence of <strong>hexagonal-shaped</strong> stones which are likely to be <strong>cystine stones</strong>.</p>\n<p>These stones are commonly found in <strong>acidic urine</strong>. Recurrent stones are treated using <strong>D-penicillamine</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4508","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g renal stones?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding renal stones?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A low calcium diet can pre​vent the recurrence of ca​lcium stone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Potassium citrate reduces ​the risk of uric acid sto​ne","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"d-Penicillamine can be use​d to prevent cystine ston​e formation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyridoxine can reduce the ​risk of oxalate stone for​mation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A low calcium diet can prevent the recurrence of calcium stone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A low calcium diet can prevent the recurrence of calcium stone</strong></p><p>A low calcium diet has not shown any significant benefit in the prevention of calcium stone formation, compared to a normocalcemic diet.</p>\n<p>However, a <strong>diet rich in calcium</strong> rather binds with dietary oxalate and <strong>reduces</strong> dietary absorption of <strong>oxalate</strong>. This effect leads to a <strong>lower risk</strong> of <strong>calcium oxalate stone</strong> formation in the kidneys.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Uric acid crystallization</strong> and stone formation take place in <strong>acidic pH</strong>. Therefore, urinary <strong>alkalinization</strong> by potassium citrate <strong>reduces the risk</strong> of urate stone formation.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Cystine stones</strong> are usually formed in presence of <strong>cystinuria</strong>. Initial treatment options to prevent recurrent cystine stone formation includes <strong>increased fluid intake</strong> and <strong>urinary alkalinization</strong> (e.g citrate). If this therapy fails, <strong>d-penicillamine</strong> can be used.</p>\n<p>Option D: Pyridoxine acts as a <strong>cofactor</strong> in the <strong>oxalic acid metabolism</strong> process and helps in the reduction of endogenous oxalate levels. Several studies have mentioned the role of pyridoxine in the reduction of oxalate stone formation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3346","text":"An HIV-positive man on HAA​RT presents to the casual​ty with intermittent righ‌t-sided flank pain. He is‌ suspected to have renal ​stones. Which of the foll‌owing drugs is most likel‌y to be responsible for t‍his condition?","raw_text":"An HIV-positive man on HAART presents to the casualty with intermittent right-sided flank pain. He is suspected to have renal stones. Which of the following drugs is most likely to be responsible for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lamiv​udine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Indi​navir","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Efav​irenz","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emtric​itabine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Indinavir","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Indinavir</strong></p><p><strong>Indinavir </strong>(a protease-inhibitor) is most likely to be responsible for <strong>renal stones</strong> in a patient on <strong>HAART. </strong>It has the highest incidence of <strong>nephrolithiasis</strong> among anti-retroviral drugs.</p>\n<p>Indinavir is released in high concentration in the urine due to poor solubility in the physiologic pH. Consequently, patients develop stones that are principally composed of indinavir or of a mixture of indinavir and other substances, such as calcium oxalate.</p>\n<p>Other <strong>urolithogenic drugs</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Antihypertensives - furosemide, bumetanide (loop diuretics), <strong>triamterene</strong> (potassium-sparing diuretic)</li>\n<li>Decongestant - ephedrine</li>\n<li>Expectorant - guaifenesin</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors - acetazolamide, topiramate, and zonisamide</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Newer antiretroviral agents - lopinavir-ritonavir, ritonavir-boosted atazanavir, nelfinavir, and amprenavir</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3182","text":"Which of the following typ​es of renal stones are pr​oduced by laxative abuse?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following types of renal stones are produced by laxative abuse?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uric​ acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ammoniu​m urate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stru​vite","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Calcium​ oxalate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ammonium urate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ed99c92cdd042edb74e38ea268bb782x787x658.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ammonium urate</strong></p><p><strong>Ammonium urate stones</strong> are most likely to be seen in patients with <strong>laxative abuse</strong>.</p>\n<p>Loss of potassium and sodium in laxative abuse causes<strong> intracellular acidosis</strong>, which <strong>increases</strong> the production of <strong>ammonia</strong> in urine. <strong>Dehydration</strong> and sodium loss further exacerbate <strong>hypokalemia</strong> and reduces the urinary volume, resulting in <strong>hyperuricemia</strong>. Highly concentrated uric acid and ammonia promote the formation of ammonium urate stones.</p>\n<p>These are also associated with<strong> inflammatory bowel disease, chronic diarrhea, ileostomy, recurrent urinary tract infection </strong>and<strong> recurrent uric acid stone formation.</strong></p>\n<p>The below image shows an ammonium urate stone.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4597","text":"A woman with severe right-​sided colicky loin pain i​s suspected to have renal‌ calculi. What is the max‌imum size of the calculi ​up to which this patient ‌can be managed expectantl‌y?","raw_text":"A woman with severe right-sided colicky loin pain is suspected to have renal calculi. What is the maximum size of the calculi up to which this patient can be managed expectantly?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.5​ cm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1 ​cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1.5​ cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 ​cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 0.5 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 0.5 cm</strong></p><p>Patients with renal calculi <strong>≤ 0.5 cm </strong>in size can be treated expectantly, as they are likely to <strong>pass spontaneously.</strong></p>\n<p>Medical management of renal stones involves:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pain control</strong> with NSAIDs or opioid analgesics</li>\n<li><strong>Medical expulsive therapy</strong> with tamsulosin (α blocker) and Nifedipine (calcium channel blockers)</li>\n<li><strong>Monitoring</strong> for the symptoms of urinary tract infection and decreased renal function</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4617","text":"In patients with which of ​the following renal stone​s, will extracorporeal sh‌ockwave lithotripsy not b‌e considered?","raw_text":"In patients with which of the following renal stones, will extracorporeal shockwave lithotripsy not be considered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxalat​e stone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phospha​te stone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uric ac​id stone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cystin​e stone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cystine stone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cystine stone</strong></p><p><strong>Cystine stones</strong> are <strong>very hard</strong> stones, hence it is <strong>difficult to disintegrate</strong> it <strong>with</strong> extracorporeal shockwave lithotripsy (<strong>ESWL</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>ESWL</strong> is a non-invasive method that generates <strong>shockwaves</strong> outside the body, which are focused on the stones. Stones <strong>up to</strong> approximately <strong>1.5 cm</strong> in size <strong>are suitable</strong> for this form of treatment.</p>\n<p>Stones that are <strong>difficult</strong> to disintegrate by ESWL:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cystine</li>\n<li>Brushite</li>\n<li>Calcium oxalate monohydrate </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Stones that are relatively <strong>easy</strong> to disintegrate by ESWL:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Calcium oxalate dihydrate </li>\n<li>Phosphate </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3268","text":"Extracorporeal shockwave l​ithotripsy is contraindic​ated in all of the follow‌ing patients except?","raw_text":"Extracorporeal shockwave lithotripsy is contraindicated in all of the following patients except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A young woman with untreat​ed bleeding diathesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 28-week pr​egnant woman","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A man with a ureteric stri​cture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A middle aged man with a s​accular aneurysm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A middle aged man with a saccular aneurysm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A middle aged man with a saccular aneurysm</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Saccular aneurysm or berry aneurysm is not a contraindication for extracorporeal shockwave lithotripsy (ESWL).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Contraindications</strong> to ESWL:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Pregnancy</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Uncorrected coagulopathy or <strong>bleeding diathesis</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Untreated urinary tract infection</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Arterial aneurysm near stones (renal or abdominal aortic aneurysms)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Obstruction</strong> of <strong>urinary tract</strong> distal to the stones</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Skeletal malformations resulting in an inability to target stones</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> of ESWL include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pain</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hematuria</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Steinstrasse - means a street of stones. It occurs due to the collection of stone fragments in the ureter</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Urinary tract infections</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4598","text":"An elderly woman was broug​ht to the hospital with a​ history of severe loin p‌ain for the past 7 hours.‌ Ultrasound of the kidney​ reveals a 2.5 cm renal s‌tone. What is the treatme‌nt of choice for this pat‍ient?","raw_text":"An elderly woman was brought to the hospital with a history of severe loin pain for the past 7 hours. Ultrasound of the kidney reveals a 2.5 cm renal stone. What is the treatment of choice for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extracorporeal shock-wave ​lithotripsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Percutaneous nephrolithoto​my","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ureterosco​pic removal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Open s​urgery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Percutaneous nephrolithotomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3aa55b39cf8f4fbda1d15a804b8acadbx1280x1688.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Percutaneous nephrolithotomy</strong></p><p>The treatment of choice for<strong> renal calculi</strong> of size <strong>&gt;2cm</strong> is<strong> percutaneous nephrolithotomy (PCNL). </strong></p>\n<p>In PCNL, endoscopic instruments are passed into the kidney using a percutaneous technique, and stones are removed. PCNL is also indicated in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Staghorn calculi</li>\n<li>Multiple stones</li>\n<li>Proximal ureteral stones &gt;1 cm</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> of percutaneous nephrolithotomy include<strong> hemorrhage, p</strong><strong>erforation</strong> of the collecting system, and<strong> </strong>of the colon or pleural cavity.</p>\n<p>Another approach for the management of renal stones is ureteroscopic removal(URS) or <strong>retrograde intra-renal surgery</strong> (RIRS). In URS, stones are removed via a retrograde approach using a <strong>dormia basket,</strong> or a<strong> Holmium YAG laser</strong> is used to break the stones. </p>\n<p>If PCNL or URS fails,<strong> </strong>open or laparoscopic <strong>pyelolithotomy or nephrolithotomy</strong> is done.</p>\n<p>The image below summarizes how the Dormia basket works with ureteroscopy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1693","text":"A man came to the casualty​ with right-sided colicky​ loin pain radiating to t‌he inner thigh. NCCT abdo‌men revealed a non-impact​ed 1cm calculi in the rig‌ht ureter. How will you m‌anage this patient?","raw_text":"A man came to the casualty with right-sided colicky loin pain radiating to the inner thigh. NCCT abdomen revealed a non-impacted 1cm calculi in the right ureter. How will you manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ES​WL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PC​NL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ureterosco​pic removal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Analgesics and medical exp​ulsion therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ureteroscopic removal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ureteroscopic removal</strong></p><p><strong>Ureteroscopic removal</strong> is the preferred treatment in <strong>non-impacted ureteric</strong> stones &gt;5-6mm in size.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1701","text":"A man was brought to the c​asualty with severe left-​sided colicky flank pain.‌ On examination, he was f‌ebrile, with a BP of 90/6​0 mmHg and a feeble rapid‌ pulse. Investigations re‌vealed WBC= 16,800 with a‍ 2.5 cm calculi in the le‍ft ureter. What is the pr​eferred management in thi‍s patient?","raw_text":"A man was brought to the casualty with severe left-sided colicky flank pain. On examination, he was febrile, with a BP of 90/60 mmHg and a feeble rapid pulse. Investigations revealed WBC= 16,800 with a 2.5 cm calculi in the left ureter. What is the preferred management in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nephr​ostomy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Extracorporeal shock wave ​lithotripsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Percutaneous nephrolithoto​my","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ureterosco​pic removal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nephrostomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nephrostomy</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of a patient in sepsis secondary to an obstructed renal calculi. <strong>Nephrostomy</strong> is the preferred management in these patients.</p>\n<p>Treatment in patients with sepsis secondary to an obstructing urinary tract calculi includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Insertion of a <strong>percutaneous nephrostomy</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Insertion of a <strong>ureteric stent</strong> via cystoscopy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Nephrostomy is preferred in extremely unwell patients as it is less manipulative and has a <strong>lower risk of septicemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"DHq8w816","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"39_muscles___upper_limb","title":"39.Muscles   Upper Limb","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MC9618","text":"A 22-year-old presented wi‌th pain and stiffness in ‍the neck following a blow​ with a hockey stick. On ​examination, the right sh‍oulder is drooping. Which‍ of the following muscles‌ is likely to be paralyze‍d?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old presented with pain and stiffness in the neck following a blow with a hockey stick. On examination, the right shoulder is drooping. Which of the following muscles is likely to be paralyzed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trap‌ezius","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Del‌toid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Latissim‌us dorsi","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serratus‌ anterior","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Trapezius","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/78ead041f7bc4aa08126b8b3be3593f9x600x813.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Trapezius</strong></p><p>The<strong> drooping</strong> of the <strong>shoulder</strong> is due to the <strong>paralysis</strong> of the <strong>trapezius </strong>muscle.</p>\n<p>The<strong> trapezius </strong>is innervated by the <strong>spinal accessory nerve</strong> (CN XI). It causes: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Retraction</strong> of <strong>scapula</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Shrugging </strong>of the<strong> shoulder</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Overhead abduction</strong> (along with serratus anterior)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Paralysis of the trapezius causes <strong>drooping</strong> of the<strong> shoulder</strong> and <strong>slight winging</strong> of <strong>the scapula</strong> (the superior angle of the scapula becomes more prominent).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0560","text":"All the following nerves c‌an cause winging of the s‍capula if injured, except​:","raw_text":"All the following nerves can cause winging of the scapula if injured, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsal sca‌pular nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spinal acce‌ssory nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Long thor‌acic nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thoracodo‌rsal nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Thoracodorsal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/14c44bc80ab84bd183dc9f1a29d7613ax600x813.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/891011d5df9a4ff2994ef90f19b48eddx600x823.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Thoracodorsal nerve</strong></p><p>The<strong> thoracodorsal nerve </strong>does not cause winging of the scapula. It supplies <strong>latissimus dorsi</strong> muscle and if injured, the patient will not be able to extend, adduct, or medially rotate the arm.</p>\n<p>Nerves causing winging of scapula:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 539.688px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\">Nerves</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167px;\">Muscles</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.688px;\">Type of winging</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\">Spinal accessory nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167px;\">\n<p>Trapezius and sternocleidomastoid</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.688px;\">The superior angle of the scapula becomes more prominent (lateral winging)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\">Long thoracic nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167px;\">\n<p>Serratus anterior</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.688px;\">The scapula is pulled upwards and towards the spine by the unopposed action of the trapezius (medial winging)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\">Dorsal scapular nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167px;\">Levator scapulae, rhomboid major, and rhomboid minor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.688px;\">Similar to serratus anterior but mild winging</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0562","text":"A 32-year-old male was bro‌ught to the emergency dep‍artment after being injur​ed in mob violence. The p​atient had a penetrating ‍injury in the right shoul‍der region by a sharp gla‌ss piece. On examination,‍ there was drooping of th​e right shoulder associat‌ed with winging of the sc‍apula. Which of the follo‌wing nerves is likely to ‌be injured?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old male was brought to the emergency department after being injured in mob violence. The patient had a penetrating injury in the right shoulder region by a sharp glass piece. On examination, there was drooping of the right shoulder associated with winging of the scapula. Which of the following nerves is likely to be injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Long thor‌acic nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thoracodo‌rsal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spinal acce‌ssory nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dorsal sca‌pular nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Spinal accessory nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Spinal accessory nerve</strong></p><p>Injury to the spinal accessory nerve can cause <strong>both</strong> <strong>d</strong><strong>rooping </strong>of the<strong> shoulder</strong> and<strong> winging </strong>of the<strong> scapula</strong>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> trapezius </strong>is innervated by the <strong>spinal accessory nerve</strong> (CN XI). It causes: </p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Retraction</strong> of <strong>scapula</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Shrugging </strong>of the<strong> shoulder</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Overhead abduction</strong> (along with serratus anterior)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Nerves causing winging of the scapula:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 488.797px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\">Nerves</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">Muscles</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181.797px;\">Type of winging</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\">Spinal accessory nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">\n<p>Trapezius and sternocleidomastoid</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181.797px;\">The superior angle of the scapula becomes more prominent (lateral winging)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\">Long thoracic nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">\n<p>Serratus anterior</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181.797px;\">The scapula is pulled upwards and towards the spine by the unopposed action of the trapezius (medial winging)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\">Dorsal scapular nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px;\">Levator scapulae, rhomboid major, and rhomboid minor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181.797px;\">Similar to serratus anterior but mild winging</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0558","text":"Which of the following mus‌cles will not be involved‍ in a patient with full-t​hickness rotator cuff tea​r?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles will not be involved in a patient with full-thickness rotator cuff tear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Teres‌ minor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Teres‌ major","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infras‌pinatus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subsca‌pularis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Teres major","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b2b3a8cb7c4f4448922771956df918e8x1280x1420.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Teres major</strong></p><p>Tere major is not involved in a patient with rotator cuff tear.</p>\n<p>The rotator cuff is a group of <strong>4 muscles</strong> (SITS) that stabilizes the shoulder joint:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0559","text":"What is the nerve supply t‌o the muscle marked in th‍e diagram below?","raw_text":"What is the nerve supply to the muscle marked in the diagram below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Axillar‌y nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lower subsc‌apular nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Supra scap‌ular nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radial‌ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lower subscapular nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f6c203335f7c4c6d82e52d3f24fd769d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/66de2a9193474be38da6843dbe4e6dbax1280x1135.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5739652c4b65476cac2a2d98571a421fx1280x1496.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lower subscapular nerve</strong></p><p>The muscle marked in the image is the <strong>teres major</strong> and it is supplied by the <strong>lower subscapular nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0561","text":"Following radical mastecto‌my, the patient is unable‍ to extend, adduct, and i​nternally rotate the arm.​ Innervation to which of ‍the following muscles is ‍likely to be damaged?","raw_text":"Following radical mastectomy, the patient is unable to extend, adduct, and internally rotate the arm. Innervation to which of the following muscles is likely to be damaged?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Del‌toid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Teres‌ minor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Latissim‌us dorsi","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pectoral‌is major","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Latissimus dorsi","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6652d491f95f4d7ab50abe272cc12892x1280x1496.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Latissimus dorsi</strong></p><p>In the above scenario, innervation to<strong> latissimus dorsi</strong> is most likely to be <strong>damaged.</strong></p>\n<p>Latissimus dorsi is innervated by the <strong>thoracodorsal nerve,</strong> which could be injured during radical mastectomy. It has the following actions on the arm:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Extension</li>\n<li>Adduction</li>\n<li>Medial rotation</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 135px; width: 456px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173px; text-align: center;\">Muscles</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267px; text-align: center;\">Actions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173px;\">Supraspinatus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267px;\">Steadies the head of the humerus during movements, abduction of shoulder joint from 0-15°</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173px;\">Deltoid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267px;\">Abduction, anterior fibers - flexors and medial rotators, posterior fibers - extensors and lateral rotators</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173px;\">Infraspinatus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267px;\">Lateral rotator of the arm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173px;\">Teres minor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267px;\">Lateral rotator of the arm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173px;\">Teres major</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267px;\">Adduction and medial rotation of the arm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173px;\">Latissimus Dorsi</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267px;\">Adduction, medial rotation, and extension of the arm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173px;\">The long head of the triceps</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267px;\">Extension of elbow joint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173px;\">Pectoralis major</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267px;\">Adduction, medial rotation, and flexion of the arm</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6653","text":"Which of the following mus‌cles is not involved in t‍he overhead abduction of ​the arm?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles is not involved in the overhead abduction of the arm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serratus‌ anterior","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Del‌toid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trap‌ezius","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pectoral‌is major","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pectoralis major","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pectoralis major</strong></p><p><strong>Pectoralis major</strong> is mainly involved in <strong>adduction</strong> and <strong>medial rotation</strong> of the arm and is not involved in the abduction of the arm.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1108\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Glenohumeral abduction</strong> is about 120° or more. The glenohumeral arthrodesis restricts the abduction at about 45°. Further abduction occurs at the sterno­ and acromiocla­vicular articulations, and contralateral vertebral flexion.</p>\n<p>Muscles involved in the abduction of the shoulder:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The<strong> first 15°</strong> of abduction is initiated by the <strong>supraspinatus</strong> muscle.</li>\n<li><strong>15° to 90</strong>° of abduction is carried out mainly by the middle fibers of the <strong>deltoid</strong> muscle.</li>\n<li>Abduction of the arm, <strong>beyond 90°</strong>, is carried out by the movement of the shoulder girdle caused by the action of <strong>trapezius</strong> and <strong>serratus anterior.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0572","text":"A 38-year-old man had rece‌ntly joined a gym to impr‍ove his physical health. ​He attempted to lift a 30​ Kg dumbbell but develope‍d sudden pain in his righ‍t arm. He came to the cas‌ualty with an abnormal bu‍lge over his arm as shown​ in the image below. Whic‌h of the following tendon‍s is most likely to be in‌jured?","raw_text":"A 38-year-old man had recently joined a gym to improve his physical health. He attempted to lift a 30 Kg dumbbell but developed sudden pain in his right arm. He came to the casualty with an abnormal bulge over his arm as shown in the image below. Which of the following tendons is most likely to be injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brachial‌is tendon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Distal tend‌on of biceps","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Proximal ten‌don of biceps","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Long belly‌ of biceps","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Proximal tendon of biceps","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/75f8adccc44c445fa2fccdb403b363f1.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Proximal tendon of biceps</strong></p><p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">The above image shows the<strong> Popeye sign, </strong>which is seen in the <strong>proximal biceps tendon rupture.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">Popeye sign is seen on flexing the elbow. An extremely prominent bulge of the muscle belly occurs as its <strong>unrestrained fibers contract.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"letter-spacing: 0px;\">The proximal biceps tendon makes up 90-97% of all biceps tendon ruptures. </span><span style=\"letter-spacing: 0px;\">In isolation, it has little effect on the upper limb function but produces a characteristic deformity called the <strong>Popeye sign.</strong></span></span></p>\n<p><strong><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">Note:</span></strong></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">The 4 most common areas of tendon rupture include <strong>quadriceps,</strong> <strong>achilles, rotator cuff</strong> (most commonly supraspinatus), and <strong>biceps.</strong></span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0573","text":"Which of the following mus‌cles cross both the shoul‍der and elbow joint?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles cross both the shoulder and elbow joint?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brachialis and biceps brac‌hii","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coracobrachialis and bicep‌s brachii","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Long head of triceps and b‌rachialis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Long head of triceps and b‌iceps brachii","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Long head of triceps and biceps brachii","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Long head of triceps and biceps brachii</strong></p><p>The long head of triceps and biceps brachii cross both shoulder and elbow joints, hence acting on both joints.</p>\n<table border=\"0\" style=\"width: 515px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Muscles</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 268px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Origin</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Insertion</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Innervation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td colspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 115px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Anterior compartment</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 74px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 74px;\">Coracobrachialis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 268px; height: 74px;\">\n<p>Coracoid Process of scapula</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px; height: 74px;\">The medial side of midshaft of humerus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; height: 74px;\">Musculocutaneous nerve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 54px;\">Brachialis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 268px; height: 54px;\">Distal half of anterior aspect of humerus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px; height: 54px;\">Ulnar tuberosity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; height: 54px;\">Musculocutaneous and radial nerve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 44px;\">Biceps Brachii</td>\n<td style=\"width: 268px; height: 44px;\">Short head: Coracoid process <br/>Long head: Supraglenoid tubercle</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122px; height: 44px;\">Radial tuberosity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; height: 44px;\">Musculocutaneous nerve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44px;\">\n<td colspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 115px; height: 44px;\"><strong>Posterior compartment</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 44px;\">Triceps brachii</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 44px;\"> \n<p>Long head: Infraglenoid tubercle </p>\n<p>Medial and Lateral heads: Posterior surface of Humerus</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 44px;\">Olecranon process of ulna </td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 44px;\">Radial nerve </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0574","text":"The structure pierced by t‌he nerve in the diagram b‍elow is:","raw_text":"The structure pierced by the nerve in the diagram below is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Short head‌ of biceps","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Long head‌ of biceps","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coracobr‌achialis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Medial head‌ of triceps","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Coracobrachialis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4e935929247c4fc7abc5a1cbdea29472.GIF"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/38864ec80a9c4844ae357b365356bc58x1280x2407.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Coracobrachialis</strong></p><p>The nerve shown in the image is the <strong>musculocutaneous nerve</strong>. It penetrates and innervates the <strong>coracobrachialis.</strong></p>\n<p>In the axilla, the <strong>medial surface </strong>of the <strong>coracobrachialis</strong> is<strong> pierced </strong>by the musculocutaneous nerve, which innervates it and then passes through the muscle to enter the arm. Then <strong>innervates </strong>all the muscles of the anterior compartment of the arm (i.e. biceps brachii, and brachialis)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0576","text":"Which muscle is attached t‌o the area marked in the ‍diagram below?","raw_text":"Which muscle is attached to the area marked in the diagram below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Biceps ‌brachii","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Brach‌ialis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Brachio‌radialis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Long head ‌of triceps","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Biceps brachii","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f2b665b1d23947f3847c4b7919366d95.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Biceps brachii</strong></p><p>The image above shows the <strong>radius,</strong> identified by the <strong>radial head</strong> at the elbow joint. The arrow points to a raised area, which is the radial tuberosity. <strong>Biceps brachii</strong> is attached to the <strong>radial tuberosity.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>short head</strong> of the biceps brachii originates from the apex of the <strong>coracoid process</strong> and the <strong>long head</strong> arises from the <strong>supraglenoid tubercle</strong> of the scapula. Both the tendons end in a flattened tendon and are attached to the radial tuberosity. The nerve supply is by the <strong>musculocutaneous nerve</strong> (C5, C6).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0575","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a composite muscle?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a composite muscle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brach‌ialis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flexor poll‌icis brevis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trap‌ezius","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flexor digitorum superfici‌alis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Flexor digitorum superficialis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Flexor digitorum superficialis</strong></p><p>Flexor digitorum superficialis (FDS) is not a composite muscle.</p>\n<p>A <strong>composite</strong> muscle is innervated by <strong>more</strong> than <strong>1 nerve</strong>. Since <strong>FDS</strong> is supplied by the <strong>median nerve</strong> (C8, T1) only, it is not a composite muscle.</p>\n<table border=\"1\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Muscle</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Innervation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Subscapularis</strong></td>\n<td>Upper and lower subscapular nerves</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>P. Major</strong></td>\n<td>Medial and lateral pectoral nerves</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Trapezius</strong></td>\n<td>Motor: Spinal accessory nerve <br/>Proprioception: C3, C4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Brachialis</strong></td>\n<td>Motor: Musculocutaneous nerve <br/>Proprioception: Radial nerve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Flexor digitorum profundus</strong></td>\n<td>\n<p>Medial half ulnar nerve <br/>Lateral half anterior interosseous nerve</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Flexor pollicis brevis</strong></td>\n<td>Superficial head: Median nerve <br/>Deep head: Deep branch of ulnar nerve </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0577","text":"All of the following muscl‌es are innervated by the ‍posterior interosseous ne​rve except:","raw_text":"All of the following muscles are innervated by the posterior interosseous nerve except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extensor ca‌rpi ulnaris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Extensor carpi radialis lo‌ngus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Extensor ‌digitorum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Extensor di‌giti minimi","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Extensor carpi radialis longus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Extensor carpi radialis longus</strong></p><p>Extensor carpi radialis longus (<strong>ECRL</strong>) and <strong>brachioradialis</strong> are supplied by the <strong>radial nerve</strong> (C6 and C7) <strong>before its division</strong> into superficial and deep branches in the lateral wall of the cubital fossa.</p>\n<p>The deep branch of the radial nerve after penetrating the supinator is called the <strong>posterior interosseous nerve</strong>, which innervates<strong> all extrinsic wrist extensors except ECRL.</strong></p>\n<p>Muscles in the <strong>anterior compartment </strong>of the<strong> forearm</strong>:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 126px; width: 385px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188px; height: 18px;\">Muscles</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; height: 18px;\">Innervation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 369px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Superficial layer</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188px; height: 36px;\">Flexor carpi ulnaris (FCU)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; height: 36px;\">Ulnar nerve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 109.539px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188px; height: 109.539px;\">\n<p>Palmaris longus</p>\n<p>Flexor carpi radialis (FCR)</p>\n<p>Pronator teres</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; height: 109.539px;\">Median nerve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 369px; height: 20px;\">\n<p><strong>Intermediate layer</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 24px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188px; height: 24px;\">Flexor digitorum superficialis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; height: 24px;\">Median nerve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 369px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Deep layer</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188px; height: 18px;\">Flexor digitorum profundus (FDP)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; height: 18px;\">\n<p>Lateral half: Anterior interosseous nerve</p>\n<p>Medial half: Ulnar nerve</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188px; height: 18px;\">\n<p>Flexor pollicis longus (FPL)</p>\n<p>Pronator quadratus</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; height: 18px;\">Anterior interosseous nerve</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Muscles of the<strong> posterior compartment</strong> of the<strong> forearm</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 118px; width: 447.875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 196px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\">Muscles</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.875px; text-align: center; height: 18px;\">Innervation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 429.875px; height: 18px;\">Superficial</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 96px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 196px; height: 96px;\">\n<p>Brachioradialis</p>\n<p>Extensor carpi radialis longus (ECRL)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.875px; height: 96px;\">Radial nerve before division</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 196px; height: 36px;\">Extensor carpi radialis brevis (ECRB)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.875px; height: 36px;\">Radial nerve deep branch before penetrating supinator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 110px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 196px; height: 110px;\">\n<p>Extensor digitorum</p>\n<p>Extensor digiti minimi</p>\n<p>Extensor carpi ulnaris</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.875px; height: 110px;\">Posterior interosseous nerve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21.1484px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 196px; height: 21.1484px;\">Anconeus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.875px; height: 21.1484px;\">Radial nerve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21.1484px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 196px; height: 21.1484px;\">Deep</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21.1484px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 196px; height: 21.1484px;\">\n<p>Supinator</p>\n<p>Abductor pollicis longus (APL)</p>\n<p>Extensor pollicis brevis (EPB)</p>\n<p>Extensor pollicis longus (EPL)</p>\n<p>Extensor indicis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 196px; height: 21.1484px;\">Posterior interosseous nerve</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0578","text":"Which of the following is ‌most likely to be affecte‍d in a patient with acute​ compartment syndrome of ​the anterior forearm?","raw_text":"Which of the following is most likely to be affected in a patient with acute compartment syndrome of the anterior forearm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flexor car‌pi ulnaris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flexor poll‌icis longus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Flexor car‌pi radialis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palmari‌s Longus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Flexor pollicis longus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fb6132577e9840e386693467988fa01ex1280x1179.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3685a4765384a4aa685ac53f8bbf1a3x1280x1131.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bd924ab32e834ca986b6ba63f2ebea90x1280x1147.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3b03d57ac06749108c59ea840639b4a5x1280x1132.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Flexor pollicis longus</strong></p><p>Flexor pollicis longus (FPL) is most likely to be affected in a patient with acute compartment syndrome of the anterior forearm as it is present in the deep layer.</p>\n<p>Muscles in the <strong>anterior compartment </strong>of the<strong> forearm</strong>:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 126px; width: 385px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188px; height: 18px;\">Muscles</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; height: 18px;\">Innervation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 369px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Superficial layer</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188px; height: 36px;\">Flexor carpi ulnaris (FCU)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; height: 36px;\">Ulnar nerve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 109.539px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188px; height: 109.539px;\">\n<p>Palmaris longus</p>\n<p>Flexor carpi radialis (FCR)</p>\n<p>Pronator teres</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; height: 109.539px;\">Median nerve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 369px; height: 20px;\">\n<p><strong>Intermediate layer</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 24px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188px; height: 24px;\">Flexor digitorum superficialis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; height: 24px;\">Median nerve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 369px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Deep layer</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188px; height: 18px;\">Flexor digitorum profundus (FDP)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; height: 18px;\">\n<p>Lateral half: Anterior interosseous nerve</p>\n<p>Medial half: Ulnar nerve</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 188px; height: 18px;\">\n<p>Flexor pollicis longus (FPL)</p>\n<p>Pronator quadratus</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181px; height: 18px;\">Anterior interosseous nerve</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7428","text":"The arrow in the image den‌otes which of the followi‍ng muscles?","raw_text":"The arrow in the image denotes which of the following muscles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extensor carpi radialis lo‌ngus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Brachio‌radialis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Supi‌nator","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flexor carpi radialis brev‌is","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Extensor carpi radialis longus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/da906fef385048e2b60c8111b59cd97b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/41d7a0b6f5594028ad0176973d8bc3f1x600x580.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Extensor carpi radialis longus</strong></p><p>The arrow shows <strong>extensor carpi radialis longus</strong>.</p>\n<p>It <strong>originates</strong> from the distal third of the lateral <strong>supracondylar ridge</strong> of the <strong>humerus</strong> and <strong>inserts</strong> at the <strong>second metacarpal</strong> (radial side). It is innervated by the <strong>radial nerve</strong>, C6, and C7.</p>\n<p>The <strong>vascular</strong> supply is by <strong>radial recurrent artery</strong> and it is responsible for the <strong>extension</strong> and <strong>abduction</strong> of the <strong>wrist joint</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0581","text":"Which of the following mus‌cle is unipennate?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscle is unipennate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1st dorsal‌ interossei","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4th dorsal‌ interossei","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3rd lum‌bricals","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2nd palmar‌ interossei","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2nd palmar interossei","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f3e5bcb9fe7b4341a11b9340412579aex1280x1342.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e22b62addad144c3a7e5dd8dfc0d97c0x1280x1508.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2nd palmar interossei</strong></p><p><strong>Palmar interossei</strong> is a <strong>unipennate</strong> muscle.</p>\n<p>A muscle with a central tendon towards which the fibers converge on either side is called the bipennate muscle.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bipennate </strong>-\n<ul>\n<li>Medial 2 lumbricals</li>\n<li>Dorsal interossei</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Unipennate </strong>-\n<ul>\n<li>Lateral 2 lumbricals</li>\n<li>Palmar interossei</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Overview of Muscles of the hand:</strong></p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 272.656px; height: 228px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\"><strong>Intrinsic muscles</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.656px;\"><strong>Extrinsic muscles</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">\n<p>Adductor Pollicis <br/>Palmaris Brevis<br/>Interossei<br/>Lumbricals<br/>thenar<br/>Hypothenar</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.656px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">Originate in the forearm, insert in the hand</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 125px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">Precision grip to execute precision movements</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.656px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">Power grip for forceful grip with hand</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0582","text":"A patient with hand weakne‌ss is being evaluated as ‍shown below. The muscle b​eing tested here does not​ have an attachment on wh‍ich metacarpal?","raw_text":"A patient with hand weakness is being evaluated as shown below. The muscle being tested here does not have an attachment on which metacarpal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4th met‌acarpal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2nd met‌acarpal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3rd met‌acarpal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"5th met‌acarpal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3rd metacarpal","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9ba5c2c9079f4a9e9ba2a3dcf7e88e9c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e380d564813b49ba9dd160bfb0c60955x1280x1342.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/012e3a051e77460a80e8c259d71a4ed0x1280x1508.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3rd metacarpal</strong></p><p>The test being performed in the above-shown image is the <strong>card test</strong> for the <strong>palmar interossei</strong> muscle. The <strong>3rd metacarpal</strong> (middle finger) has no palmar interosseous attachment.</p>\n<p>Palmar interossei lie on the palmar surface of the metacarpals. There are 3 palmar interossei:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>2nd metacarpal (Ulnar side) </li>\n<li>4th metacarpal (Radial side) </li>\n<li>5th metacarpal (Radial side) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: There may be 4 interossei in some people. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2146","text":"What is the function of th‌e muscle marked as A in t‍he image below, at the me​tacarpophalangeal joint?","raw_text":"What is the function of the muscle marked as A in the image below, at the metacarpophalangeal joint?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fle‌xion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Exte‌nsion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Addu‌ction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abdu‌ction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Flexion","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3dffdf9897a545a28549a29e1fe92d97.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/be4d7f3845c84547a9d68375100bc756x1280x1152.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/45ebd84a18864f4c814146b942ec3c96x1280x1342.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Flexion</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The muscle marked 'A' is <strong>lumbricals</strong> and they cause <strong>flexion</strong> at the <strong>metacarpophalangeal joints</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Lumbricals are intrinsic muscles of the hand that <strong>flex</strong> the <strong>metacarpophalangeal joints (MCP)</strong> and <strong>extend</strong> the <strong>interphalangeal joints (PIP</strong> &amp; <strong>DIP</strong>).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">They originate from flexor digitorum profundus.</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">The lateral two are unipennate, supplied by the median nerve.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">The medial two are bipinnate, supplied by the ulnar nerve.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0107","text":"All of the following form ‌the marked structure exce‍pt:","raw_text":"All of the following form the marked structure except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2nd Dorsal‌ interossei","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lumbr‌icals","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extensor ‌digitorum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2nd Palmar‌ interossei","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2nd Palmar interossei","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0a4f47834df6437ab0d9ddd1da10780f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8af955cc519c4228ac45e4fa122b0f0cx1280x1342.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7e9ce74f456b4c359307fecb18e85099x1280x1508.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3393db8be7694ebf9f7f8b174f60b2a0x1280x1342.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2nd Palmar interossei</strong></p><p>The structure marked in the image is the <strong>dorsal digital expansion </strong>of the <strong>middle finger. Palmar interossei</strong> do not attach to the middle finger and therefore it <strong>does not</strong> contribute to the dorsal digital expansion of the middle finger. </p>\n<p><strong>Palmar interossei</strong> are <strong>unipennate</strong> muscles originating from their own metacarpal bone. Their function is <strong>adduction</strong>. The middle finger does not need adduction and hence does not have palmar interossei.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Dorsal interossei</strong> are <strong>bipennate</strong> muscles originating from the adjacent metacarpal bone. They attach to the middle three fingers on both sides and do not attach on the thumb and little finger. Their function is <strong>abduction</strong>. The thumb and little finger have other muscles for abduction.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>lateral 2 lumbricals</strong> are <strong>unipennate</strong> and <strong>medial 2 lumbricals</strong> are <strong>bipennate</strong>. Their function is <strong>flexion</strong> at<strong> metacarpophalangeal joints.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0574","text":"A patient presented with t‌he extension of MCP joint‍ and flexion of IP joints​. Which muscle is affecte​d?","raw_text":"A patient presented with the extension of MCP joint and flexion of IP joints. Which muscle is affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extensor ‌digitorum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Interossei a‌nd lumbricals","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Abductor‌ pollicis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abductor pol‌licis brevis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Interossei and lumbricals","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Interossei and lumbricals</strong></p><p>The<strong> lumbricals </strong>and<strong> interossei muscles</strong> are affected.</p>\n<p>The lumbricals and interossei muscles through their attachments to the dorsal digital expansions, cause:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Flexion</strong> of the metacarpophalangeal (<strong>MCP</strong>) joints</li>\n<li><strong>Extension</strong> of the interphalangeal (<strong>IP</strong>) joints</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In cases of <strong>ulnar nerve</strong> <strong>injury</strong>, these muscles may get atrophied leading to the <strong>extension </strong>of <strong>MCP joints </strong>and<strong> flexion </strong>of<strong> IP joints.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>The ulnar nerve innervates all the intrinsic muscles of the hand, <strong>except for </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>First and second lumbrical</li>\n<li>Abductor pollicis brevis</li>\n<li>Opponens pollicis</li>\n<li>Part of flexor pollicis brevis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6641","text":"Which of the following mus‌cles is attached to the a‍rea marked in the image b​elow?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles is attached to the area marked in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abductor pol‌licis longus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Palmar i‌nterossei","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opponens‌ pollicis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Flexor poll‌icis brevis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Opponens pollicis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ef1009ff63074dcb9e1487329cb5e0df.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/78fb7dfe74254cf8afddb45ac363c96fx1280x1342.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bcc9fb0250f94d1196a2251eca03e010x1280x1236.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4e8a6317d28047a693382f4b8b02d385x1280x1342.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bcd2b5e7c0ec4c4395dea0f7ad5063e6x1280x1508.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Opponens pollicis</strong></p><p>The area marked in the image is the<strong> palmar aspect </strong>of the<strong> first metacarpal</strong>. The <strong>opponens pollicis</strong> muscle is inserted at this site.</p>\n<p><strong>Opponens pollicis:</strong></p>\n<p style=\"padding-left: 30px;\"><strong>Origin:  </strong>Tubercle of the trapezium and flexor retinaculum</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left: 30px;\"><strong>Insertion:</strong> Whole length of the lateral border and adjoining lateral half of the palmar aspect of the first metacarpal.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left: 30px;\"><strong>Action:</strong> Flexes the metacarpal bone of the thumb.</p>\n<p> The image below shows various intrinsic muscles of the hand.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0556","text":"Power grip of hand is a fu‌nction of:","raw_text":"Power grip of hand is a function of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Long f‌lexors","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Short ‌flexors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Palmari‌s brevis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lumbr‌icals","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Long flexors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Long flexors</strong></p><p><strong>Power grip</strong> is by <strong>extrinsic muscles </strong>of the<strong> hand </strong>(long flexors). </p>\n<p>Extrinsic muscles of the hand originate in the forearm and insert in the hand i.e. <strong>long flexors.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Precision </strong>grip is a function of the<strong> intrinsic muscles of the hand.</strong> </p>\n<p>Intrinsic muscles of the hand originate and insert in the hand.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adductor pollicis </li>\n<li>Palmaris brevis</li>\n<li>Interossei, lumbricals</li>\n<li>Thenar, hypothenar</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7430","text":"What is the nerve supply o‌f the structure marked in‍ the image below?","raw_text":"What is the nerve supply of the structure marked in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior interosseus nerv‌e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Median‌ nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior interosseus nerve‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ulnar‌ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Median nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f92b0e898b6347669e226be6c3044213.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f01beb6822414aed9705f56808806b17x1280x1342.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Median nerve</strong></p><p>The marked structure is <strong>second lumbrical</strong> which is supplied by the <strong>median nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> lateral two</strong> lumbricals are supplied by the <strong>median</strong> <strong>nerve</strong> and the <strong>medial two</strong> are supplied by the <strong>ulnar nerve</strong>. Function of these muscles is flexion at the metacarpophalangeal joints and extension at both the interphalangeal joints.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"OaOpU5nZ","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"39_housing_and_ventilation","title":"39.Housing And Ventilation","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MB7560","text":"Which of the following is ​not true about ventilatio​n?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about ventilation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Involves replacement of vi​tiated air","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Involves quality control o​f outgoing air","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Provides comfortable therm​al environment","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reduces risk​ of infection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Involves quality control of outgoing air","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Involves quality control of outgoing air</strong></p><p>Ventilation involves the quality control of <strong>incoming air </strong>but not quality control of<strong> outgoing air. </strong></p>\n<p>It also involves the <strong>replacement</strong> of vitiated air(air with decreased oxygen content) by a supply of fresh outdoor air. It aims to provide a <strong>comfortable thermal environment</strong> and <strong>reduce</strong> the risk of <strong>infections</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7561","text":"What is the ideal number o​f air changes in a drawin​g-room?","raw_text":"What is the ideal number of air changes in a drawing-room?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 – 3 p​er hour","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3 – 4 p​er hour","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4 – 5 p​er hour","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 – 2 p​er hour","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2 – 3 per hour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2 – 3 per hour</strong></p><p>The number of air changes in <strong>drawing rooms or living rooms</strong> must be <strong>2 – 3 per hour.</strong></p>\n<p>The number of air changes in <strong>work rooms and assemblies</strong> must be <strong>4 – 6 per hour.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7574","text":"What is ventilation provid​ed by wind blowing throug​h a space called?","raw_text":"What is ventilation provided by wind blowing through a space called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perfl​ation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aspir​ation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cross-ve​ntilation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plenum ve​ntilation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Perflation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Perflation</strong></p><p>Ventilation provided by wind blowing through a space is called<strong> perflation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ventilation can be broadly classified as<strong> natural</strong> and <strong>mechanical</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<p><strong>Natural ventilation</strong> includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Perflation </strong>is the ventilation of a space achieved by <strong>allowing wind to blow through it</strong> and removing the obstacles for this to happen (e.g., opening windows and doors).</li>\n<li><strong>Aspiration</strong> occurs when there is an obstruction as wind bypasses it and creates a suction action</li>\n<li><strong>Diffusion</strong> is the passage of air through small spaces or openings</li>\n<li><strong>Cross-ventilation</strong> is when air is allowed a natural path through the space by providing openings at appropriate places, opposite each other.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> drawback</strong> of natural ventilation is that regulation of <strong>air quality is not possible</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanical ventilation</strong> includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Exhaust ventilation</strong> utilizes <strong>exhaust fans </strong>placed high up near the roof on the external walls. A vacuum is created by which fresh air enters the ventilated space.</li>\n<li><strong>Plenum ventilation</strong> uses <strong>centrifugal fans</strong> to blow fresh air into the room and create a positive pressure, by which vitiated air is pushed out.</li>\n<li><strong>Balanced ventilation</strong> is the combination of both <strong>exhaust and plenum </strong>methods of ventilation.</li>\n<li><strong>Air conditioning </strong>can be done through mechanical ventilation. It is the simultaneous control of all or at least three factors that influence air quality in a confined room or space. These factors include temperature, humidity, air movement, distribution, dust, bacteria, odours and toxic gases.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7577","text":"Which of the following is ​not a type of mechanical ​ventilation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a  type of mechanical ventilation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perflation ​ventilation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Balanced v​entilation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plenum ve​ntilation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Air cond​itioning","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Perflation ventilation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Perflation ventilation</strong></p><p>Perflation is not a type of mechanical<strong> </strong>ventilation. It is a type of <strong>natural ventilation.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<p>Ventilation can be broadly classified as<strong> natural</strong> and <strong>mechanical</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>Natural ventilation</strong> includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Perflation </strong>is the ventilation of a space achieved by <strong>allowing wind to blow through it</strong> and removing the obstacles for this to happen (e.g., opening windows and doors).</li>\n<li><strong>Aspiration</strong> occurs when there is an obstruction as wind bypasses it and creates a suction action.</li>\n<li><strong>Diffusion</strong> is the passage of air through small spaces or openings.</li>\n<li><strong>Cross-ventilation</strong> is when air is allowed a natural path through the space by providing openings at appropriate places, opposite each other.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> drawback</strong> of natural ventilation is that <strong>regulation</strong> of <strong>air quality </strong>is not possible.</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanical ventilation</strong> includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Exhaust ventilation</strong> utilizes <strong>exhaust fans </strong>placed high up near the roof on the external walls. A vacuum is created by which fresh air enters the ventilated space.</li>\n<li><strong>Plenum ventilation</strong> uses <strong>centrifugal fans</strong> to blow fresh air into the room and create a positive pressure, by which vitiated air is pushed out.</li>\n<li><strong>Balanced ventilation</strong> is the combination of both <strong>exhaust and plenum </strong>methods of ventilation.</li>\n<li><strong>Air conditioning </strong>can be done through mechanical ventilation. It is the simultaneous control of all or at least three factors that influence air quality in a confined room or space. These factors include temperature, humidity, air movement, distribution, dust, bacteria, odours and toxic gases.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7578","text":"What is the mechanical sys​tem of ventilation which ​employs only centrifugal ‍fans that create positive‌ pressure inside a space ​by blowing fresh air into‌ it called?","raw_text":"What is the mechanical system of ventilation which employs only centrifugal fans that create positive pressure inside a space by blowing fresh air into it called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Balanced v​entilation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Air cond​itioning","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exhaust v​entilation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plenum ve​ntilation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Plenum ventilation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Plenum ventilation</strong></p><p>The mechanical system of ventilation that employs only centrifugal fans is <strong>plenum </strong>ventilation<strong>.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<p>Ventilation can be broadly classified as<strong> natural</strong> and <strong>mechanical</strong>:</p>\n<p>Natural ventilation includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Perflation </strong>is the ventilation of a space achieved by <strong>allowing wind to blow through it</strong> and removing the obstacles for this to happen (e.g., opening windows and doors).</li>\n<li><strong>Aspiration</strong> occurs when there is an obstruction as wind bypasses it and creates a suction action</li>\n<li><strong>Diffusion</strong> is the passage of air through small spaces or openings</li>\n<li><strong>Cross-ventilation</strong> is when air is allowed a natural path through the space by providing openings at appropriate places, opposite each other.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> drawback</strong> of natural ventilation is that <strong>regulation</strong> of <strong>air quality </strong>is not possible.</p>\n<p>Mechanical ventilation includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Exhaust ventilation</strong> utilizes <strong>exhaust fans </strong>placed high up near the roof on the external walls. A vacuum is created by which fresh air enters the ventilated space.</li>\n<li><strong>Plenum ventilation</strong> uses <strong>centrifugal fans</strong> to blow fresh air into the room and create a positive pressure, by which vitiated air is pushed out.</li>\n<li><strong>Balanced ventilation</strong> is the combination of both <strong>exhaust and plenum </strong>methods of ventilation.</li>\n<li><strong>Air conditioning </strong>can be done through mechanical ventilation. It is the simultaneous control of all or at least three factors that influence air quality in a confined room or space. These factors include temperature, humidity, air movement, distribution, dust, bacteria, odours and toxic gases.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7580","text":"Which of the following is ​not a social goal of hous​ing standards?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a social goal of housing standards?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Economic ​stability","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Family participation in co​mmunity life","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Famil​y life","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prevention of non-infectio​us diseases","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prevention of non-infectious diseases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prevention of non-infectious diseases</strong></p><p>Prevention of non-infectious diseases is not a social goal of housing standards.</p>\n<p>The <strong>social goals of housing</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Shelter</li>\n<li>Family life</li>\n<li>Access to community facilities</li>\n<li>Family participation in community life</li>\n<li>Economic stability</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7586","text":"What is the optimum floor ​area recommended per adul​t in a living room?","raw_text":"What is the optimum floor area recommended per adult in a living room?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50 – 10​0 sq. ft","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"400 – 45​0 sq. ft","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"150 – 20​0 sq. ft","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"100 – 15​0 sq. ft","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 50 – 100 sq. ft","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 50 – 100 sq. ft</strong></p><p>The optimum floor area recommended per adult person in a living room is <strong>50 – 100 sq. ft.</strong></p>\n<p><strong class=\"ng-binding\">Non-Rural Housing Standards in India:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Housing parameters</strong></td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Description</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Site</strong></td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Elevated</strong> to protect from rains and floods.</p>\n<p>Independent access to the street.</p>\n<p>Away from vector breeding sites and other pollutants like smoke, noise, dust.</p>\n<p>The soil on which it is founded must be dry and safe.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Floor</strong></td>\n<td>A <strong>pucca floor</strong> that is impermeable, easy to clean, free from cracks, damp-proof. <strong>Height of plinth</strong> must be <strong>2 – 3 feet</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Walls</strong></td>\n<td><strong>9 inch </strong>thick, strong, low heat capacity, weather-resistant, unsuitable for harbourage of rats/vermin.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Roof</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Height not less than 10 feet</strong> (3m).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Rooms</strong></td>\n<td>The number of rooms must be according to the size of the family. However, the <strong>basic number</strong> is <strong>two.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Floor area</strong></td>\n<td>A living room should have at least <strong>120 sq. ft.</strong> of area for <strong>occupancy of more than 1 person</strong> and 100 sq. ft. for occupancy of only one person. <strong>50 -100</strong> <strong>sq. ft.</strong> <strong>floor area per person is optimum</strong> in a living room.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cubic space</strong></td>\n<td><strong>500 – 1000</strong> cubic feet of air space per capita.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Windows</strong></td>\n<td>At least <strong>2 windows per room</strong> with one opening into an open space. Windows must be at a <strong>height </strong>of not more than <strong>3 feet</strong> above the ground. The window area must be 1/5<sup>th</sup> of the floor area.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lighting</strong></td>\n<td>The daylight factor should be <strong>&gt;1% over half the floor area.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sanitary privy</strong></td>\n<td>Every house must have a sanitary privy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Others</strong></td>\n<td>Proper facilities for garbage disposal, bathing and washing and a good water supply.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7592","text":"During a field visit, you ​explain about ideal livin​g standards to the genera‍l public. Which of the fo‌llowing is false regardin​g windows in housing stan‌dards?","raw_text":"During a field visit, you explain about ideal living standards to the general public. Which of the following is false regarding windows in housing standards?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"At least 2 windows in each​ room","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"At a height more than 5 fe​et in a living room","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"One window should open int​o open space","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Window area should be 1/5t​h of floor area","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. At a height more than 5 feet in a living room","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. At a height more than 5 feet in a living room</strong></p><p>The windows in the living room must be at a height of <strong>not more than 3 feet above the floor.</strong></p>\n<p><strong class=\"ng-binding\">Non-Rural Housing Standards in India:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Housing parameters</strong></td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Description</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Site</strong></td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Elevated</strong> to protect from rains and floods.</p>\n<p>Independent access to the street.</p>\n<p>Away from vector breeding sites and other pollutants like smoke, noise, dust.</p>\n<p>The soil on which it is founded must be dry and safe.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Floor</strong></td>\n<td>A <strong>pucca floor</strong> that is impermeable, easy to clean, free from cracks, damp-proof. The <strong>height </strong>of the<strong> plinth</strong> must be <strong>2 – 3 feet</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Walls</strong></td>\n<td><strong>9 inch </strong>thick, strong, low heat capacity, weather-resistant, unsuitable for harbourage of rats/vermin.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Roof</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Height not less than 10 feet</strong> (3m).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Rooms</strong></td>\n<td>The number of rooms must be according to the size of the family. However, the <strong>basic number</strong> is <strong>two.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Floor area</strong></td>\n<td>A living room should have at least <strong>120 sq. ft.</strong> of area for <strong>occupancy of more than 1 person</strong> and 100 sq. ft. for occupancy of only one person. <strong>50 -100</strong> <strong>sq. ft.</strong> <strong>floor area per person is optimum</strong> in a living room.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cubic space</strong></td>\n<td><strong>500 – 1000</strong> cubic feet of air space per capita.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Windows</strong></td>\n<td>At least <strong>2 windows per room</strong> with one opening into an open space. Windows must be at a <strong>height </strong>of not more than <strong>3 feet</strong> above ground. Window area must be 1/5<sup>th</sup> of floor area.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lighting</strong></td>\n<td>Daylight factor should be <strong>&gt;1% over half the floor area.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sanitary privy</strong></td>\n<td>Every house must have a sanitary privy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Others</strong></td>\n<td>Proper facilities for garbage disposal, bathing and washing and a good water supply.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7594","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect about the housi​ng standards in India?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about the housing standards in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Minimum wall thickness is ​9 inches","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Roof not less than 10 feet​ from the ground","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Daylight factor should be ​&amp;gt;1% over half the floo​r area","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Air space per capita must ​be 250 – 500 cubic feet","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Air space per capita must be 250 – 500 cubic feet","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Air space per capita must be 250 – 500 cubic feet</strong></p><p>Air space per capita must be<strong> 500 - 1000 cubic feet</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong class=\"ng-binding\">Non-Rural Housing Standards in India:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Housing parameters</strong></td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Description</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Site</strong></td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Elevated</strong> to protect from rains and floods.</p>\n<p>Independent access to the street.</p>\n<p>Away from vector breeding sites and other pollutants like smoke, noise, dust.</p>\n<p>The soil on which it is founded must be dry and safe.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Floor</strong></td>\n<td>A <strong>pucca floor</strong> that is impermeable, easy to clean, free from cracks, damp-proof. The <strong>height </strong>of the <strong>plinth</strong> must be <strong>2 – 3 feet</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Walls</strong></td>\n<td><strong>9 inch </strong>thick, strong, low heat capacity, weather-resistant, unsuitable for harbourage of rats/vermin.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Roof</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Height not less than 10 feet</strong> (3m).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Rooms</strong></td>\n<td>The number of rooms must be according to the size of the family but the <strong>basic number</strong> is <strong>two.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Floor area</strong></td>\n<td>A living room should have at least <strong>120 sq. ft.</strong> of area for <strong>occupancy of more than 1 person</strong> and 100 sq. ft. for occupancy of only one person. <strong>50 -100</strong> <strong>sq. ft.</strong> <strong>floor area per person is optimum</strong> in a living room.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cubic space</strong></td>\n<td><strong>500 – 1000</strong> cubic feet of air space per capita.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Windows</strong></td>\n<td>At least <strong>2 windows per room</strong> with one opening into an open space. Windows must be at a <strong>height </strong>of not more than <strong>3 feet</strong> above the ground. The window area must be 1/5<sup>th</sup> of the floor area.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lighting</strong></td>\n<td>The daylight factor should be <strong>&gt;1% over half the floor area.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sanitary privy</strong></td>\n<td>Every house must have a sanitary privy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Others</strong></td>\n<td>Proper facilities for garbage disposal, bathing and washing and a good water supply.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7596","text":"Which of the following is ​not a rural housing stand​ard in India?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a rural housing standard in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ample vera​ndah space","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"One room with sufficient s​pace","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Built-up area should not e​xceed 1/3rd of total area​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Window area should be 10% ​of floor area","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. One room with sufficient space","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. One room with sufficient space</strong></p><p>One room with sufficient space is not a rural housing standard in India.</p>\n<p>According to rural housing standards, there must be at least<strong> two living rooms.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Rural housing </strong>standards are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>At least two living rooms</li>\n<li>Ample verandah space</li>\n<li>Separate kitchen with space for washing utensils</li>\n<li>Built-up area should not exceed 1/3<sup>rd</sup> of total area</li>\n<li>Sanitary latrine</li>\n<li>Window area should be 10% of floor area</li>\n<li>Sanitary well or tube well within quarter mile(400 m) of house</li>\n<li>Cattle sheds must be 25 feet away from the house</li>\n<li>Adequate arrangements for disposal of waste water, refuse and garbage</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7600","text":"While recording the detail​ed history of a patient w​ith symptoms of tuberculo‍sis, you note that his ho‌me is overcrowded. Accord​ing to the accepted stand‌ard against overcrowding,​ how many people can 3 ro​oms accommodate?","raw_text":"While recording the detailed history of a patient with symptoms of tuberculosis, you note that his home is overcrowded. According to the accepted standard against overcrowding, how many people can 3 rooms accommodate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"​7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 5</strong></p><p>The accepted standard against overcrowding for <strong>3 rooms</strong> is <strong>5 persons</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<p><strong>Overcrowding</strong> is considered to exist if <strong>2 persons</strong> over <strong>9 years of age</strong>, <strong>not husband and wife</strong>, of <strong>opposite sexes</strong> are obliged to sleep in the same room.</p>\n<p>The <strong>accepted standards</strong> against overcrowding are as follows:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>No of rooms</td>\n<td> No of persons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1 room</td>\n<td>2 persons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2 rooms</td>\n<td>3 persons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3 rooms</td>\n<td>5 persons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4 rooms</td>\n<td>7 persons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5 rooms</td>\n<td>10 persons (1 room for every 2 additional persons)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>floor space standards</strong> against overcrowding are as follows:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Area</td>\n<td>No of persons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>110 sq. ft. and more</td>\n<td>2 persons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>90 – 100 sq. ft.</td>\n<td>1 ½ persons (Children from 1 – 10 are counted as a half unit)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>70 – 90 sq. ft.</td>\n<td>1 person</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>50 – 70 sq. ft.</td>\n<td>½ person </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7602","text":"To avoid overcrowding, wha​t is the minimum area req​uired per person in a hou‍se?","raw_text":"To avoid overcrowding, what is the minimum area required per person in a house?\n","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50 – 70​ sq. ft.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"70 - 90​ sq. ft.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"90 – 100​ sq. ft.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"110 – 12​0 sq. ft.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 70 - 90 sq. ft.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 70 - 90 sq. ft.</strong></p><p>To avoid overcrowding, the minimum area required per person in a house is 70 - 90 sq. ft.</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<p>The <strong>accepted standards</strong> against overcrowding are as follows.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>No of rooms</td>\n<td> No of persons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1 room</td>\n<td>2 persons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2 rooms</td>\n<td>3 persons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3 rooms</td>\n<td>5 persons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4 rooms</td>\n<td>7 persons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5 rooms</td>\n<td>10 persons (1 room for every 2 additional persons)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>floor space standards</strong> against overcrowding are as follows.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Area</td>\n<td>No of persons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>110 sq. ft. and more</td>\n<td>2 persons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>90 – 100 sq. ft.</td>\n<td>1 ½ persons (Children from 1 – 10 are counted as a half unit)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>70 – 90 sq. ft.</td>\n<td>1 person</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>50 – 70 sq. ft.</td>\n<td>½ person</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7604","text":"Which of the following is ​not an organization relat​ed to housing in India?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an organization related to housing in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"National Building Organiza​tion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Housing and Urban Developm​ent Corporation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"National Buildings Constru​ction Corporation Ltd","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"National Rural Housing Ser​vices","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. National Rural Housing Services","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. National Rural Housing Services</strong></p><p>National Rural Housing Services is not an organization related to housing in India.</p>\n<p>Rural Housing Services is an agency of the United States Department of Agriculture located in Washington, D.C.</p>\n<p>The <strong>organizations related to housing</strong> in India are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>National Buildings Organization (NBO).</li>\n<li>National Buildings Construction Corporation Ltd.</li>\n<li>Housing and Urban Development Corporation (<strong>HUDCO</strong>).</li>\n<li>Hindustan Housing Factory.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They work under the Union Ministry of works and Housing to deal with various aspects of housing.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7606","text":"Which of the following was​ launched to address rura​l housing in India?","raw_text":"Which of the following was launched to address rural housing in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rajiv Gandhi Shramik Kalya​n Yojana","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vik​as Yojana","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yoj​ana","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Indira Aw​aas Yojana","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Indira Awaas Yojana","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d9c7096bc92e4b6f8a815b70f91c3173x730x410.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Indira Awaas Yojana</strong></p><p>The <strong>Indira Awas Yojana </strong>was launched in 1985 to address <strong>rural housing</strong> in India.</p>\n<p>It is now called the <strong>Pradhan Mantri Gramin Awaas Yojana </strong>(2015), which is a social welfare flagship programme created to provide housing to the rural poor in India.</p>\n<p>A similar scheme called <strong>Housing for All by 2022</strong> or Pradhan Mantri Awaas Yojana (Urban) was begun to address housing for the <strong>urban poor.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Rajiv Gandhi Shramik Kalyan Yojana</strong> is an <strong>unemployment</strong> protection scheme. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana </strong>was begun for the recognition and standardization of skills among the youth.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana</strong> is an <strong>agricultural</strong> scheme.</p>\n<p>The logo of Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana is given below. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"eTegIuIF","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"39_seronegative_spondyloarthritides","title":"39.Seronegative Spondyloarthritides","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MB6363","text":"Seronegative spondyloarthr​itides are associated wit‍h which of the following?‍","raw_text":"Seronegative spondyloarthritides are associated with which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HLA​-B27","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"HLA​-B16","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HLA​-B47","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HLA​-DR4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. HLA-B27","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. HLA-B27</strong></p><p><strong>Seronegative spondyloarthritides</strong> are associated with <strong>HLA-B27.</strong></p>\n<p>The spondyloarthritides are a group of overlapping disorders that share certain clinical features and genetic associations (HLA-B27). These disorders include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Psoriatic arthritis</li>\n<li>Ankylosing spondylitis</li>\n<li>Reactive arthritis</li>\n<li>Enteropathic arthritis</li>\n<li>Juvenile onset spondyloarthritis </li>\n<li>Undifferentiated spondyloarthritis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Usually, rheumatoid factor (RF) assay is negative in spondyloarthritides. Hence, they are also referred to as seronegative arthritis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6395","text":"A 26-year-old man presente​d with pain and stiffness‍ in his lower back. His X‍-ray spine is given below‌. Which of the following ‌is true about his conditi‌on?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old man presented with pain and stiffness in his lower back. His X-ray spine is given below. Which of the following is true about his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Steroids are the first-lin​e therapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Symptoms improve with acti​vity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is associated with HLA ​B47","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Associated with low serum ​IgA levels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Symptoms improve with activity","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9de73614c8cf4fb4b0688bfc618ccab7.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Symptoms improve with activity</strong></p><p>The given clinical features along with the classical <strong>bamboo-spine appearance</strong> on X-ray point towards a diagnosis of <strong>ankylosing spondylitis</strong> (AS). In ankylosing spondylitis, the <strong>symptoms improve with activity</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ankylosing spondylitis (AS) is an inflammatory disorder that affects the <strong>axial skeleton </strong>primarily, and may also involve peripheral joints. It is strongly associated with <strong>HLA-B27</strong>. It usually presents in the <strong>mid-twenties</strong>.</p>\n<p>It presents with lower back pain that improves with activity and worsens with rest or inactivity. Early <strong>morning stiffness</strong> is a characteristic feature. It is associated with <strong>elevated</strong> serum <strong>IgA</strong> levels. <strong>NSAIDs</strong> are the first line of pharmacological therapy. </p>\n<p>It is also associated with <strong>extra-articular manifestations</strong> like <strong>uveitis, interstitial lung disease</strong>, aortic regurgitation, etc.</p>\n<p><strong>NSAIDs</strong> are the <strong>first-line</strong> treatment for ankylosing spondylitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1210","text":"What are the key cytokines​ involved in the pathogen‍esis of ankylosing spondy‍litis?","raw_text":"What are the key cytokines involved in the pathogenesis of ankylosing spondylitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interferon γ​ &amp;amp; IL-23","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Interferon γ​ &amp;amp; IL-17","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"TNF-α &amp;a​mp; IL-13","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"TNF-α &amp;a​mp; IL-17","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. TNF-α & IL-17","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. TNF-α &amp; IL-17</strong></p><p>The key cytokines involved in the pathogenesis of <strong>ankylosing spondylitis</strong> are <strong>TNF-α</strong> and<strong> IL-17. </strong>IL-23 also plays a role in pathogenesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6393","text":"A patient with Crohn's dis​ease presents with back p‍ain and stiffness. The ph‍ysician suspects ankylosi‌ng spondylitis and orders‌ an MRI for evaluation. W‌hich of the following joi​nts will be the earliest ‌to be involved?","raw_text":"A patient with Crohn's disease presents with back pain and stiffness. The physician suspects ankylosing spondylitis and orders an MRI for evaluation. Which of the following joints will be the earliest to be involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Costoverte​bral joint","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lumbosac​ral joint","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sacroili​ac joint","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sacrococcy​geal joint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sacroiliac joint","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sacroiliac joint</strong></p><p>The<strong> earliest</strong> joint to be involved in <strong>ankylosing spondylitis</strong> is the <strong>sacroiliac joint.</strong></p>\n<p>Ankylosing spondylitis (AS) is an inflammatory disorder that primarily affects the <strong>axial skeleton</strong>. It is more common in <strong>males</strong>, usually presenting between 20-30 years of age.</p>\n<p>Ankylosing spondylitis (AS) and peripheral arthritis can occur as extra-intestinal manifestations of inflammatory bowel disease (IBD). They are <strong>more common </strong>in <strong>Crohn's disease</strong> than in ulcerative colitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5779","text":"A 28-year-old male patient​ with low backache and ea‍rly morning stiffness for‍ the past 1 year is diagn‌osed with ankylosing spon‌dylitis. Which of the fol‌lowing is not associated ​with the haplotype relate‌d to this condition?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old male patient with low backache and early morning stiffness for the past 1 year is diagnosed with ankylosing spondylitis. Which of the following is not associated with the haplotype related to this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Behcet's​ disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Reiter's​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reactive ​arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Psoriatic ​spondylitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Behcet's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Behcet&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p><strong>Ankylosing spondylitis </strong>is related to the<strong> HLA-B27</strong> haplotype. This haplotype is not associated with <strong>Behcet's disease</strong>. It is associated with <strong>HLA B51</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ankylosing spondylitis is a chronic inflammatory disease that mainly affects the<strong> spine</strong> and <strong>sacroiliac joint. </strong>Individuals who inherit this<strong> class I HLA</strong> allele (<strong>HLA-B27</strong>) have a 100–200 fold greater chance (odds ratio or relative risk) of developing the disease compared with those who do not carry HLA-B27.<strong> </strong>Many <strong>autoimmune diseases</strong> are associated with different <strong>class II HLA</strong> alleles.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6399","text":"The following test is perf​ormed in a patient to eva‍luate back pain associate‍d with stiffness. Identif‌y the test.","raw_text":"The following test is performed in a patient to evaluate back pain associated with stiffness. Identify the test.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gaensl​en test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thoma​s test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adam'​s test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Schober​'s test","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Schober's test","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3327816e95c0486b8e4fe85addc4af21.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7a2669d7e36d45b8a5219f5344c1f866x1280x1305.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Schober&#x27;s test</strong></p><p>The test performed is modified <strong>Schober’s test</strong>. This test is done in patients with suspected <strong>ankylosing spondylitis.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is used to <strong>detect reduced flexion </strong>of the spine. The test involves marking two points- at the lumbosacral junction and 10 cm above it. The patient is then asked to bend forward with knees fully extended and the distance between the two points is measured.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">This distance <strong>normally increases</strong> by <strong>≥2 cm. </strong>An increase of <strong>&lt;2 cm indicates reduced flexion.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">In the image given below the distance increases by 5cm which is normal.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option A: <strong>Gaenslen's</strong> test is used to detect primary chronic <strong>inflammation</strong> of the <strong>sacroiliac</strong> joint.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option B: <strong>Thomas test</strong> is used to detect <strong>fixed flexion deformity </strong>of the<strong> hip.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option C: <strong>Adam's</strong> forward bending test is performed on a patient to differentiate structural from non-structural <strong>scoliosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6401","text":"Which is the most common e​xtra-articular manifestat‍ion seen in patients diag‍nosed with ankylosing spo‌ndylitis?","raw_text":"Which is the most common extra-articular manifestation seen in patients diagnosed with ankylosing spondylitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior​ uveitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cauda equi​na syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inflammatory bowel disease​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acne fu​lminans","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anterior uveitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anterior uveitis</strong></p><p>The most common <strong>extraarticular manifestation</strong> of <strong>ankylosing spondylitis</strong> (AS) is <strong>anterior uveitis.</strong></p>\n<p>It usually presents with unilateral pain, photophobia and increased lacrimation. It is also associated with other spondyloarthritides like reactive arthritis, psoriatic arthritis and enteropathic arthritis.</p>\n<p>Other extra-articular manifestations of AS include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>GIT: ileitis, colitis, inflammatory bowel disease</li>\n<li>Cardiovascular: increased risk of IHD, aortic insufficiency, heart block </li>\n<li>Respiratory: upper lobe fibrosis</li>\n<li>Neurological: cauda equina syndrome</li>\n<li>Skin: acne fulminans, hidradenitis suppurativa</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3591","text":"Which of the following con​ditions predominantly inv‍olve the upper lobe of th‍e lung?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions predominantly involve the upper lobe of the lung?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumatoid​ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Asbes​tosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scler​oderma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ankylosing ​spondylitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ankylosing spondylitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ankylosing spondylitis</strong></p><p><strong>Ankylosing spondylitis</strong> predominantly involves the <strong>upper lobe </strong>of the lung<strong> </strong>leading to <strong>pulmonary fibrosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Rheumatoid arthritis, scleroderma, and asbestosis predominantly involve the lower lobes of the lungs.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6403","text":"A patient who presented wi​th chronic lower back pai‍n has been diagnosed with‍ ankylosing spondylitis. ‌What is the first line of‌ pharmacological therapy?‌","raw_text":"A patient who presented with chronic lower back pain has been diagnosed with ankylosing spondylitis. What is the first line of pharmacological therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"TNF α in​hibitors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Opi​oids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NSA​IDS","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ster​oids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. NSAIDS","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. NSAIDS</strong></p><p>Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (<strong>NSAIDs</strong>) are the <strong>first line</strong> of pharmacological therapy for <strong>ankylosing spondylitis</strong> (AS)<strong>. </strong></p>\n<p>Management of ankylosing spondylitis includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>First-line management:\n<ul>\n<li>Physiotherapy to maintain posture and range of motion.</li>\n<li><strong>NSAIDs</strong>- reduce pain and tenderness, increase mobility, and slow down the disease progression.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>TNF α inhibitors- in patients who do not respond to NSAID therapy. It is not used in all patients because of side effects like reactivation of TB, hypersensitivity reactions, pancytopenia, drug-induced psoriasis, etc.</li>\n<li>Anti-IL-17 antibodies- in patients with failed anti-TNF therapy.</li>\n<li>Surgery- improves mobility in patients with severe arthritis and joint stiffness.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6405","text":"A 27-year-old man with ank​ylosing spondylitis is no‍t responding to standard ‍therapy. His physician de‌cides to prescribe him an‌ anti-IL-17 monoclonal an‌tibody. Which of the foll​owing drugs would he pres‌cribe?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old man with ankylosing spondylitis is not responding to standard therapy. His physician decides to prescribe him an anti-IL-17 monoclonal antibody. Which of the following drugs would he prescribe?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ustek​inumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secuk​inumab","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infl​ximab","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adali​mumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Secukinumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Secukinumab</strong></p><p><strong>Secukinumab</strong> is an <strong>anti-IL-17 </strong>monoclonal antibody used in patients with <strong>ankylosing spondylitis </strong>who are<strong> </strong>not responding to NSAIDs and anti-TNF therapy.</p>\n<p>Management of ankylosing spondylitis includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>First-line management:\n<ul>\n<li>Physiotherapy to maintain posture and range of motion.</li>\n<li><strong>NSAIDs</strong>- reduce pain and tenderness, increase mobility, and slow down the disease progression.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>TNF α inhibitors- in patients who do not respond to NSAID therapy. It is not used in all patients because of side effects like reactivation of TB, hypersensitivity reactions, pancytopenia, drug-induced psoriasis, etc.</li>\n<li>Anti-IL-17 antibodies- in patients with failed anti-TNF therapy.</li>\n<li>Surgery- improves mobility in patients with severe arthritis and joint stiffness.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Ustekinumab is an anti-IL-12/23 monoclonal antibody used in the treatment of psoriasis.</p>\n<p>Options C and D: Infliximab and adalimumab are anti-TNF-α monoclonal antibodies used in the treatment of ankylosing spondylitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6407","text":"A 15-year-old boy presents​ with generalized malaise‍ and painful swelling of ‍his right knee joint sinc‌e 10 days. He also compla‌ins of pain and discomfor‌t during urination. He wa​s treated for diarrhea la‌st month. What is the mos​t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old boy presents with generalized malaise and painful swelling of his right knee joint since 10 days. He also complains of pain and discomfort during urination. He was treated for diarrhea last month. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reactive ​arthritis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Enteropathi​c arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemolytic ur​emic syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyogenic ​arthritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Reactive arthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Reactive arthritis</strong></p><p>The given scenario describes an acute arthritis following an infection which is also associated with urethritis and generalized malaise. These features are suggestive of <strong>reactive arthritis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reactive arthritis </strong>also known as<strong> Reiter's syndrome </strong>is a seronegative spondyloarthritis that occurs as a consequence of a <strong>recent enteric</strong> or <strong>urogenital</strong> infection. It is associated with <strong>HLA B27</strong>. It is also associated with extra-articular manifestations such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Urogenital- urethritis, prostatitis, cervicitis</li>\n<li>Ocular- conjuctivitis, anterior uveitis</li>\n<li>Mucocutaneous- oral ulcers, keratoderma blennorrhagica, circinate balanitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In some patients it presents as a <strong>triad</strong> of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Arthritis</li>\n<li>Urethritis</li>\n<li>Conjunctivitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1212","text":"A patient presents with pa​inful knee swelling, dysu‍ria, and conjunctivitis. ‍Which of the following in‌fectious agents is least ‌likely to cause this?","raw_text":"A patient presents with painful knee swelling, dysuria, and conjunctivitis. Which of the following infectious agents is least likely to cause this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shigella d​ysenteriae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neisseria ​gonorrhoea","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Campylobac​ter jejuni","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlamydia ​trachomatis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neisseria gonorrhoea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neisseria gonorrhoea</strong></p><p>The given scenario of arthritis, urethritis, and conjunctivitis is suggestive of <strong>reactive arthritis</strong>. It is not caused by <em>Neisseria gonorrhea</em>.</p>\n<p>Disseminated gonococcal infection causes <strong>gonococcal</strong> <strong>arthritis</strong>. It comprises localized septic arthritis and arthritis-dermatitis syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Reactive arthritis</strong> is usually caused due to <strong>LPS</strong> (lipopolysaccharide) component of the gram-negative organisms or cross-reactivity with synovial antigens. The common causative organisms include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em><strong>Chlamydia trachomatis</strong></em> (most common) </li>\n<li>Salmonella</li>\n<li>Shigella</li>\n<li>Yersinia</li>\n<li>Campylobacter</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6410","text":"A middle-aged man presente​d with pain and swelling ‍of his right knee for one‍ week. It started progres‌sing to the ankle joints ‌and feet for the past 2 d‌ays. He gives a history o​f diarrhea and burning mi‌cturition 4 weeks ago. On​ examination, his soles s‍howed the following lesio​ns. What is the most like‌ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man presented with pain and swelling of his right knee for one week. It started progressing to the ankle joints and feet for the past 2 days. He gives a history of diarrhea and burning micturition 4 weeks ago. On examination, his soles showed the following lesions. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Syphilitic​ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enteropathi​c arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Psoriatic​ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reactive ​arthritis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Reactive arthritis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d76ecdc80eab4a1690982f5a4da0438b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Reactive arthritis</strong></p><p>The given scenario of acute arthritis following a <strong>recent infection</strong> and <strong>additive pattern</strong> (involvement of new joints within few days) and examination finding of <strong>keratoderma blennorrhagica</strong> (image) are suggestive of <strong>reactive arthritis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Keratoderma blennorrhagica is a <strong>mucocutaneous manifestation</strong> of reactive arthritis. It presents as vesicles and pustules that lead to hyperkeratotic lesions or crusting on the <strong>palms </strong>and<strong> soles</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other mucocutaneous manifestations of reactive arthritis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Oral ulcers </strong>-<strong> </strong>superficial, transient, and often asymptomatic</li>\n<li><strong>Circinate balanitis </strong>- consists of vesicles that rupture to form painless superficial erosions on the glans penis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1639","text":"An elderly man presents wi​th pain and deformity of ‍his toes. The clinical fi‍ndings are shown in the i‌mage below. What is the m‌ost likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An elderly man presents with pain and deformity of his toes. The clinical findings are shown in the image below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumatoid​ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteo a​rthritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Psoriatic​ arthritis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Septic a​rthritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Psoriatic arthritis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/165e4c1b3d214f1e8c9904d3e59ab6a1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aacc4dca2e204e7c9a018ccd427f544ax720x1280.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Psoriatic arthritis</strong></p><p>The given clinical image showing dactylitis, nail changes and telescoping of toes is suggestive of <strong>psoriatic arthritis. </strong></p>\n<p>Clinical features of psoriatic arthritis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Psoriatic nail changes-</strong> yellowing and hypertrophy </li>\n<li><strong>Dactylitis- </strong>inflammation of the toes</li>\n<li><strong>Telescoping </strong>(shortening) of toes due to osteolysis</li>\n<li>Severe deformity- suggestive of <strong>arthritis mutilans</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Enthesitis-</strong> heel pain at the sites of ligament or tendon attachment</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Psoriatic arthritis of the hands commonly involves proximal and distal interphalangeal joints (<strong>PIP</strong>, <strong>DIP</strong>) and metacarpophalangeal joints (<strong>MCP</strong>). The image below shows psoriatic arthritis of the hands:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6412","text":"A 16-year-old girl is bein​g evaluated for arthritis‍ and uveitis. Which of th‍e following would you not‌ consider as a differenti‌al diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old girl is being evaluated for arthritis and uveitis. Which of the following would you not consider as a differential diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Psoriatic​ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enteropathi​c arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Juvenile onset spondyloart​hritis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Reactive ​Arthritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Juvenile onset spondyloarthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Juvenile onset spondyloarthritis</strong></p><p><strong>Juvenile onset spondyloarthritis (JSpA)</strong> would not be considered as a differential diagnosis as it has <strong>no</strong> associated <strong>extra-articular manifestations</strong> like uveitis, lung involvement, etc.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>seronegative</strong> spondyloarthritis with onset between <strong>7-16 years</strong> of age. It usually presents as <strong>asymmetric</strong> arthritis predominantly involving the <strong>lower extremities</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6419","text":"Which of the following is ​not a part of the SAPHO s‍yndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a part of the SAPHO syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Syno​vitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ac​ne","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Psor​iasis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypero​stosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Psoriasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Psoriasis</strong></p><p>Psoriasis is not a part of SAPHO syndrome.</p>\n<p>The <strong>SAPHO</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong> is characterized by: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Synovitis- </strong>arthritis</li>\n<li><strong>Acne-</strong> acne conglobata</li>\n<li><strong>Pustulosis- </strong>palmoplantar pustulosis, hidradenitis suppurativa</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperostosis- </strong>sternoclavicular and spinal hyperostosis</li>\n<li><strong>Osteitis- </strong>sterile osteomyelitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1642","text":"Uveitis is an uncommon man​ifestation in patients wi‍th which of the following‍ conditions?","raw_text":"Uveitis is an uncommon manifestation in patients with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ankylosing ​spondylitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Psoriatic​ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reiter's​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rheumatoid​ arthritis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rheumatoid arthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rheumatoid arthritis</strong></p><p>Uveitis is a rare manifestation of rheumatoid arthritis (RA). Ocular manifestation in <strong>RA</strong> includes <strong>scleritis</strong>, episcleritis, and keratoconjunctivitis.</p>\n<p><strong>Uveitis</strong> is a common ocular manifestation of <strong>seronegative arthritis </strong>e.g. Reiter's syndrome, ankylosing spondylitis, psoriatic arthritis, and juvenile rheumatoid arthritis (JRA).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"aL7xqOrK","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"39_pelvic_inflammatory_disease","title":"39.Pelvic Inflammatory Disease","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB3563","text":"The Pap smear of a healthy‌ 30-year-old lady is give​n below. Identify the mic‍roorganism seen.","raw_text":"The Pap smear of a healthy 30-year-old lady is given below. Identify the microorganism seen.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Doderlein’‌s bacillus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gardnerell‌a vaginalis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trichomona‌s vaginalis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Frisch ‌bacillus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Doderlein’s bacillus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eb0f33b2cda4487abc5deedf82826e79.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Doderlein’s bacillus</strong></p><p>The organism seen in the given image of a Pap smear is the <strong>Doderlien’s bacillus,</strong> also known as <em>Lactobacillus</em>. This is a <strong>gram-positive</strong>, rod-shaped bacillus that grows in <strong>anaerobic</strong> conditions.</p>\n<p>It is a part of the normal vaginal flora that is responsible for the <strong>vaginal defence mechanism</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>estrogen-dependent</strong> bacillus and appears at puberty and disappears after menopause.</p>\n<p>It converts glycogen into lactic acid and <strong>maintains</strong> the <strong>acidic</strong> <strong>ph</strong> in the vagina. This prevents the growth of pathogenic bacteria in the vagina. Its <strong>absence</strong> causes a condition called <strong>bacterial vaginosis </strong>where pathogenic organisms replaces the normal flora of the vagina. In this condition,<strong> clue cells</strong> are visible.</p>\n<p>Option B: <em>Gardnerella vaginalis</em> is the most common organism involved in bacterial vaginosis. As the patient in the given question is healthy, this is not the correct option.</p>\n<p>Option C:<em> Trichomonas vaginalis</em> is flagellated protozoa that causes trichomonas vaginitis. The organism and the nucleus of epithelial cells appear equal in size on the pap smear. This criterion is used for the identification of trichomonas as flagella are not visible on a pap smear.</p>\n<p>Option D: Frisch bacillus is another name for<em> Klebsiella rhinoscleromatis. </em>It is not seen on Pap smears.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3566","text":"All of the following are r‌isk factors for PID excep​t______.","raw_text":"All of the following are risk factors for PID except______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unprotected sexual interco‌urse","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Preg‌nancy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"IUD in‌sertion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Early age of sexual interc‌ourse","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pregnancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pregnancy</strong></p><p><strong>Pregnancy</strong> is not a risk factor for <strong>PID</strong>. Instead, it plays a <strong>protective</strong> role in this condition.</p>\n<p>The <strong>risk factors</strong> for developing PID are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Unprotected sexual intercourse</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Having multiple sex partners</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Previous history of PID</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">IUD insertion</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Early age of sexual intercourse (&lt;18years)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>protective</strong> <strong>factors</strong> for PID are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pregnancy </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Using barrier contraception like condoms, diaphragms</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oral contraceptive pills (OCPs)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Menopause</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3564","text":"A 24-year-old woman is dia‌gnosed with pelvic inflam​matory disease. Which of ‍the following is not incl‌uded in this condition?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman is diagnosed with pelvic inflammatory disease. Which of the following is not included in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endome‌tritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oopho‌ritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cervi‌citis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Perit‌onitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cervicitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cervicitis</strong></p><p>Cervicitis is not included in pelvic inflammatory disease (PID).</p>\n<p><strong>Pelvic inflammatory disease</strong> is a spectrum of infection and inflammation of the <strong>upper genital tract,</strong> typically causing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Endometritis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Salpingitis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oophoritis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Peritonitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>minimum criteria</strong> <strong>for </strong>diagnosis of<strong> PID</strong> are:</p>\n<p>Pain in the lower abdomen + any <strong>one</strong> of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Uterine tenderness</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cervical motion tenderness</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Adnexal tenderness</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cervicitis and vaginitis are lower genital tract infections.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3578","text":"What is the most common sy‌mptom seen in acute PID?","raw_text":"What is the most common symptom seen in acute PID?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bilateral lower abdominal ‌pain","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Unilateral lower abdominal‌ pain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High fever w‌ith vomiting","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pain in the right upper qu‌adrant of the abdomen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bilateral lower abdominal pain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bilateral lower abdominal pain</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> symptom of acute PID is <strong>bilateral lower abdominal pain</strong>.</p>\n<p>The pain is <strong>severe</strong> in <strong>acute stages</strong> and it may or may not be accompanied by a <strong>high temperature</strong> ( &gt;38.3 degrees C). It may be followed by vomiting. </p>\n<p>Some patients with PID can present with pain in the right upper quadrant of the abdomen. This is seen in <strong>Fitz-Hugh-Curtis</strong> syndrome which is a form of <strong>perihepatitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>If PID occurs due to sexually transmitted organisms, dysuria and vaginal discharge may be seen.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3567","text":"A young woman with fever a‌nd lower abdominal pain i​s diagnosed to have PID. ‍Which is the most common ‌cause for this condition?​","raw_text":"A young woman with fever and lower abdominal pain is diagnosed to have PID. Which is the most common cause for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chla‌mydia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mycop‌lasma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Staphyl‌ococcus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neis‌seria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chlamydia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chlamydia</strong></p><p><strong><em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em></strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> cause of PID. <br/><br/>PID is an infection of the upper genital tract. It is generally transmitted <strong>sexually</strong> as an ascending infection along with <strong>sperms</strong>. It is usually a<strong> polymicrobial infection</strong>,</p>\n<p><strong>Organisms causing PID include:</strong></p>\n<p>Sexually transmitted:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>Chlamydia </em>(most common)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>Gonococcus</em></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>Mycoplasma</em></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>Trichomonas</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pyogenic Aerobes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><em>Staphylococci</em></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><em>Streptococci</em></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><em>E Coli</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Anaerobes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><em>Bacteroides fragilis</em></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><em>Peptostreptococcus </em></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><em>Clostridium </em></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><em>Actinomyces </em>(most common in IUD users)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other:<em> <br/></em></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>Genital tuberculosis</strong> is the only PID that does not spread sexually. It has a <strong>hematogenous spread</strong>. Thus, it is the <strong>most</strong> <strong>common</strong> PID in <strong>virgin</strong> girls.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6941","text":"A 42-year-old woman presen‌ted to the emergency room​ with abdominal pain and ‍fever for the last 48 hou‌rs. She has been using a ​Cu T 380A for contracepti​on for the past 8 years. ‍On examination, cervical ​motion tenderness was pre​sent. What is the most li​kely organism to have cau​sed her condition?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old woman presented to the emergency room with abdominal pain and fever for the last 48 hours. She has been using a Cu T 380A for contraception for the past 8 years. On examination, cervical motion tenderness was present. What is the most likely organism to have caused her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Actin‌omyces","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chla‌mydia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gonoc‌occus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphyl‌ococcus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Actinomyces","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Actinomyces</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario points towards a diagnosis of <strong>PID</strong> in an <strong>IUCD user</strong>. The most common organism causing this condition is <strong><em>Actinomyces</em></strong>. It<strong> </strong>has been reported in 7% of IUCD users if the device is worn for more than 2 years.</p>\n<p>This emphasizes the need for <strong>strict asepsis during insertion</strong> of the device and the need for <strong>strict follow up</strong> of women wearing these devices.</p>\n<p><strong>Organisms causing PID are</strong>:</p>\n<p>Sexually transmitted:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>Chlamydia </em>(most common)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>Gonococcus</em></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>Mycoplasma</em></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>Trichomonas</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pyogenic Aerobes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><em>Staphylococci</em></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><em>Streptococci</em></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><em>E Coli</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Anaerobes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><em>Bacteroides fragilis</em></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><em>Peptostreptococcus </em></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><em>Clostridium </em></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><em>Actinomyces </em>(most common in IUD users)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other:<em><br/></em></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3572","text":"What is the mechanism of t‌he spread of gonococci to​ the tubes in pelvic infl‍ammatory disease?","raw_text":"What is the mechanism of the spread of gonococci to the tubes in pelvic inflammatory disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hematogen‌ous spread","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphati‌c spread","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parametri‌um spread","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mucosal‌ spread","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mucosal spread","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mucosal spread</strong></p><p>The mechanism of the<strong> spread</strong> of <strong>gonococci</strong> to the tubes in pelvic inflammatory disease is <strong>mucosal</strong>.</p>\n<p>Gonococci ascend to the tubes mainly through <strong>mucosal continuity</strong>. This is<strong> facilitated by</strong> sexually transmitted vectors such as <strong>sperms</strong> and <strong><em>Trichomonas</em></strong>.</p>\n<p>The mucous membranes of the fallopian tubes are affected first. They become edematous and are infiltrated with leukocytes and plasma cells. Another route of spread is through the<strong> reflux of mucosal blood</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Hematogenous</strong> spread is seen in <strong>genital tuberculosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Lymphatics</strong> are involved in the spread of <strong>secondary organisms</strong> and in PID following post-abortal and puerperal infection.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Parametrium</strong> facilitates the spread of <strong><em>Mycoplasma</em></strong>. The gut flora can directly spread to tubes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3575","text":"Which infection causes mor‌e damage to the mucosa an​d the wall of the tube le‍ading to fibrosis and tub‌al blockage?","raw_text":"Which infection causes more damage to the mucosa and the wall of the tube leading to fibrosis and tubal blockage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlamydial‌ infection","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gonococcal‌ infection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mycoplasma‌ infection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mobiluncus‌ infection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chlamydial infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chlamydial infection</strong></p><p>Among the given options,<strong> chlamydial infection</strong> causes the <strong>most amount of</strong> <strong>damage</strong> to the mucosa and the wall of the <strong>fallopian tubes </strong>leading to fibrosis and tubal blockage.</p>\n<p>Chlamydial infection can remain <strong>asymptomatic</strong> in the endo-cervix. Hence, the infection goes <strong>unnoticed</strong> and <strong>untreated</strong>. Thus, it can lead to more damage and complications.</p>\n<p><strong>Gonococcal infection</strong> is more <strong>symptomatic</strong> than chlamydial infection. Hence, it is diagnosed early and causes less damage to the tubes than chlamydial infection.</p>\n<p><em>Mycoplasma</em> and <em>Mobiluncus</em> generally cause lower genital tract infections like cervicitis and vaginitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3589","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a differential diagno​sis of acute PID?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a differential diagnosis of acute PID?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Septic ‌abortion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cholec‌ystitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Twisted ov‌arian cyst","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pelvic end‌ometriosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pelvic endometriosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pelvic endometriosis</strong></p><p><strong>Pelvic endometriosis</strong> is not a <strong>differential diagnosis</strong> of acute PID. It is a differential diagnosis of <strong>chronic PID</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential diagnosis</strong> <strong>of acute PID</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Acute appendicitis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Septic abortion</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Cholecystitis</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Twisted ovarian cyst</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ectopic pregnancy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Diverticulitis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ruptured endometriotic cyst</li>\n</ul>\n<p> <strong>Differential diagnosis of chronic PID:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Uterine fibroids</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Pelvic endometriosis</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ovarian tumour</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tubercular tubo-ovarian mass</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3573","text":"A 26-year-old female with ‌endometritis did not comp​lete the course of antibi‍otics she was prescribed.‌ Following this, her infe​ction failed to resolve a​nd she developed a tubo-o‍varian mass. How can this​ mass manifest? Tubo-ovar​ian cyst Hydrosalpinx Twi​sted ovarian cyst Chronic​ pyosalpinx Ruptured endo​metriotic cyst","raw_text":"<p>A 26-year-old female with endometritis did not complete the course of antibiotics she was prescribed. Following this, her infection failed to resolve and she developed a tubo-ovarian mass. How can this mass manifest?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Tubo-ovarian cyst</li>\n<li>Hydrosalpinx</li>\n<li>Twisted ovarian cyst</li>\n<li>Chronic pyosalpinx</li>\n<li>Ruptured endometriotic cyst</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2,‌ 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‌2, 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 4</strong></p><p>The given scenario of development of a <strong>chronic tubo-ovarian mass</strong> following acute PID points towards a diagnosis of<strong> chronic PID</strong>. A chronic tubo-ovarian mass can manifest as a <strong>tubo-ovarian cyst</strong>, <strong>hydrosalpinx</strong> or even a <strong>chronic pyosalpinx</strong>.</p>\n<p>Failure to complete treatment of acute PID or drug resistance can lead to the development of chronic PID. This can lead to the development of chronic tubo-ovarian masses which may manifest as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hydrosalpinx</li>\n<li>Chronic pyosalpinx</li>\n<li>Chronic interstitial salpingitis</li>\n<li>Tubo-ovarian cyst</li>\n<li>Tuberculous form</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A twisted ovarian cyst and a ruptured endometriotic cyst are differential diagnoses of acute PID.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3574","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is incorrect rega​rding the condition seen ‍in the image given below?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding the condition seen in the image given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is ofte‌n bilateral","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Its wall ‌is opaque","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Flattening of tubal plicae‌ is seen on histology","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Its ampullary region is fi‌lled with clear fluid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Its wall is opaque","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/12870ec59e7e4c1e9a7c654b34bea3d9.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Its wall is opaque</strong></p><p>The above image depicts a <strong>hydrosalpinx</strong>. Its <strong>wall</strong> is not opaque but <strong>translucent</strong>.</p>\n<p>If a <strong>pyosalpinx</strong> responds to antibiotics, the <strong>pus</strong> present inside<strong> becomes</strong> <strong>sterile</strong> within 6 weeks of the initial attack. However, the damage to the tube remains as chronic pyosalpinx or <strong>hydrosalpinx</strong>. A hydrosalpinx represents the end result of previous acute salpingitis and is often<strong> bilateral</strong>.</p>\n<p>The tube becomes <strong>retort shaped</strong> due to the enormous <strong>dilatation</strong> of the <strong>ampullary region</strong> that is filled with clear fluid.</p>\n<p><strong>Histological</strong> examination shows a <strong>flattening</strong> of the <strong>tubal plicae</strong> and <strong>exfoliation</strong> of the<strong> lining epithelium</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3582","text":"A patient with fever and a‌bdominal pain presented t​o the emergency. On exami‍nation, you note mucopuru‌lent vaginal discharge an​d cervical motion tendern​ess. You suspect she has ‍PID. Which of these is no​t included in the criteri​a to diagnose this condit​ion?","raw_text":"A patient with fever and abdominal pain presented to the emergency. On examination, you note mucopurulent vaginal discharge and cervical motion tenderness. You suspect she has PID. Which of these is not included in the criteria to diagnose this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral temperature &amp;gt;38.3°‌C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cervical motion tenderness‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mucopurulent vaginal disch‌arge","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bilateral ab‌dominal pain","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bilateral abdominal pain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bilateral abdominal pain</strong></p><p><strong>Bilateral abdominal pain</strong> is <strong>not</strong> included in the <strong>criteria</strong> to <strong>diagnose PID</strong>. Bilateral abdominal pain is a clinical symptom of PID.</p>\n<p>Lower abdominal tenderness is included in the criteria to diagnose PID. </p>\n<p><strong>CDC criteria for the diagnosis of PID</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>Definitive criteria:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Histopathologic evidence of endometritis on biopsy</li>\n<li>Imaging study (TVS/MRI) evidence of thickened fluid-filled tubes (tubo-ovarian abscess)</li>\n<li>Laparoscopic evidence of PID</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Minimum criteria:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cervical motion tenderness</li>\n<li>Uterine tenderness</li>\n<li>Adnexal tenderness</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Additional criteria:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Oral temperature &gt;38.3 <sup>o</sup>C</li>\n<li>Mucopurulent cervical or vaginal discharge, cervical friability</li>\n<li>Raised ESR or CRP</li>\n<li>Laboratory documentation of positive cervical infection with Gonorrhea or <em>Chlamydia</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0844","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a definitive criterio​n used to diagnose PID?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a definitive criterion used to diagnose PID?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Histopathological evidence‌ of endometritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tubo-ovarian abscess on so‌nography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Temperature‌ &amp;gt; 38.3°C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Laparoscopic abnormalities‌ consistent with PID","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Temperature > 38.3°C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Temperature &gt; 38.3°C</strong></p><p>A<strong> temperature</strong> of <strong>more than 38.3°C</strong> is an <strong>additional</strong> <strong>criterion</strong> and not a definitive criterion for the diagnosis of <strong>PID</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>CDC 2006 criteria for the diagnosis of PID</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>Definitive criteria:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Histopathologic evidence of endometritis on biopsy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Imaging studies (TVS/MRI) evidence of thickened fluid-filled tubes (Tubo-ovarian abscess)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Laparoscopic evidence of PID</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Minimum criteria:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lower abdominal tenderness</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Adnexal tenderness</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cervical motion tenderness</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Additional criteria:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oral temperature&gt;38.3C</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mucopurulent cervical or vaginal discharge and cervical friability</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Raised ESR or CRP</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Laboratory documentation of positive cervical infection with Gonorrhea or<em> Chlamydia</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3583","text":"A 24-year-old woman presen‌ted with bilateral abdomi​nal pain for 3 days and a‍ temperature of 102°F. Sh‌e also had a mucopurulent​ vaginal discharge, cervi​cal motion tenderness, an‍d a negative urine pregna​ncy test. Which is the go​ld standard investigation​ to confirm her likely di​agnosis?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman presented with bilateral abdominal pain for 3 days and a temperature of 102°F. She also had a mucopurulent vaginal discharge, cervical motion tenderness, and a negative urine pregnancy test. Which is the gold standard investigation to confirm her likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lapar‌oscopy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"U‌SG","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Culdoc‌entesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vaginal discharge microsco‌py","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Laparoscopy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Laparoscopy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is likely to be <strong>acute</strong> <strong>PID</strong>. <strong>Laparoscopy</strong> is the <strong>gold standard</strong> to <strong>confirm</strong> acute PID.</p>\n<p>Laparoscopy is <strong>not done routinely</strong> as it is an invasive procedure. It is reserved for only cases where the differential diagnosis is salpingitis, appendicitis, ectopic pregnancy or a case of a non-responding pelvic mass.</p>\n<p>The <strong>diagnosis</strong> of PID is<strong> mostly</strong> <strong>clinical</strong>. If diagnosis is unclear, the <strong>investigation of choice</strong> for PID is <strong>USG</strong> and <strong>culdocentesis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>CDC 2006 criteria for the diagnosis of PID</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>Definitive criteria</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Histopathologic evidence of endometritis on biopsy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Imaging studies (TVS/MRI) evidence of thickened fluid-filled tubes (tubo-ovarian abscess)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Laparoscopic evidence of PID</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Minimum criteria</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lower abdominal tenderness</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Adnexal tenderness</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cervical motion tenderness</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Additional criteria</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oral temperature&gt;38.3C</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mucopurulent cervical or vaginal discharge and cervical friability</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Raised ESR or CRP</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Laboratory documentation of positive cervical infection with Gonorrhea or <em>Chlamydia</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3576","text":"The laparoscopy report of ‌a woman with pelvic infla​mmatory disease states sh‍e has Gainesville stage I‌II PID. Which of the foll​owing presentations could​ have been seen in her? A‍cute salpingitis with per​itonitis. Acute salpingit​is with no adhesions. Acu​te salpingitis with tubo-​ovarian complex Ruptured ​tubo-ovarian abscess Acut‌e salpingitis with superi‍mposed tubal occlusion","raw_text":"<p>The laparoscopy report of a woman with pelvic inflammatory disease states she has Gainesville stage III PID. Which of the following presentations could have been seen in her?</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Acute salpingitis with peritonitis.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Acute salpingitis with no adhesions.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Acute salpingitis with tubo-ovarian complex</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ruptured tubo-ovarian abscess</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Acute salpingitis with superimposed tubal occlusion</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 a‌nd 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 a‌nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 a‌nd 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 3 and 5</strong></p><p>Gainesville<strong> stage III</strong> includes two forms of PID - acute salpingitis with superimposed <strong>tubal occlusion</strong> or <strong>tubo-ovarian complex</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Gainesville</strong> has described the <strong>stages</strong> of PID depending upon the <strong>severity</strong> of <strong>tubal damage </strong>as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 400px; width: 511.688px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 167px;\">\n<p>Stage</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 326.688px;\">\n<p>Condition </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 167px;\">\n<p>Stage I </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 326.688px;\">\n<p>Acute salpingitis without peritonitis - no adhesions </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 167px;\">\n<p>Stage II </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 326.688px;\">\n<p>Acute salpingitis with peritonitis - purulent discharge  </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54.7604px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 54.7604px; width: 167px;\">\n<p>Stage III</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 54.7604px; width: 326.688px;\">\n<p>Acute salpingitis with superimposed tubal occlusion or tubo-ovarian complex </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 167px; height: 51px;\">\n<p>Stage IV</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 51px; width: 326.688px;\">\n<p>Ruptured tubo-ovarian abscess </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 56px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 56px; width: 167px;\">\n<p>Stage V</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 56px; width: 326.688px;\">\n<p>Tubercular salpingitis  </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3579","text":"In a patient with PID, lap‌aroscopy shows the follow​ing appearance. What is t‍rue about the syndrome th‌is patient likely has?","raw_text":"In a patient with PID, laparoscopy shows the following appearance. What is true about the syndrome this patient likely has?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perihe‌patitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cholec‌ystitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tuboovari‌an abscess","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gonococcal‌ urethritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Perihepatitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/618b8be91a8741fc8b7756c1c01ec56c.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Perihepatitis</strong></p><p>The image shows the laparoscopic finding of <strong>violin string</strong> <strong>adhesions</strong> that are characteristic of <strong>Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome</strong>. It is a form of <strong>perihepatitis</strong> that occurs in the setting of PID.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by <strong>right upper quadrant pain</strong> with a pleuritic component, sometimes referred to the right shoulder. It rarely occurs in women with acute PID. The pathological mechanism is as follows:</p>\n<p>Gonococcal / Chlamydial infection → transperitoneal/vascular spread → inflammation of the liver capsule and peritoneal surfaces → adhesions and fibrosis in the perihepatic region → Fitz-Hugh-Curtis Syndrome</p>\n<p>Cholecystitis and ruptured tubo-ovarian abscess are the differential diagnoses of Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome. Violin string adhesions would not be seen in these conditions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0847","text":"Identify the wrong stateme‌nt with respect to silent​ PID.","raw_text":"Identify the wrong statement with respect to silent PID.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dysmenorrhea is a common p‌resentation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ectopic pregnancy is commo‌n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with infertilit‌y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mycobacterium can be grown‌ from menstrual blood","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dysmenorrhea is a common presentation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dysmenorrhea is a common presentation</strong></p><p>Dysmenorrhea rarely occurs in PID. Pain is uncommon and is a result of subacute PID.</p>\n<p><strong>Silent PID</strong> is thought to follow multiple or continuous low-grade infections in <strong>asymptomatic</strong> women. It is not a clinical diagnosis. Rather, it is an ultimate diagnosis given to women with<strong> tubal-factor infertility</strong> who lack a history of upper genital tract infection.</p>\n<p>On laparoscopy or laparotomy, these women may have<strong> evidence of prior tubal infection</strong> such as adhesions, but for the most part, the fallopian tubes are grossly normal. They can have <strong>ectopic pregnancies</strong> due to tubal dysmotility. Silent PID can occur following <strong>infection</strong> with <strong><em>M. tuberculosis</em></strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3584","text":"A patient is admitted to t‌he gynaecology ward for p​elvic inflammatory diseas‍e. Which of the following‌ is a part of this patien​t's treatment regimen?","raw_text":"A patient is admitted to the gynaecology ward for pelvic inflammatory disease. Which of the following is a part of this patient's treatment regimen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Administration of cefoteta‌n 2 g IV 12 hourly + doxy​cycline 100 mg IV BD","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Removal of IUD irrespectiv‌e of antibiotic response","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Administration of a single‌ dose of ceftriaxone 250 ​mg IM","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Administration of 10 doses‌ of Placentrex 2ml IM dai​ly or on alternate days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Administration of cefotetan 2 g IV 12 hourly + doxycycline 100 mg IV BD","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Administration of cefotetan 2 g IV 12 hourly + doxycycline 100 mg IV BD</strong></p><p>Administration of cefotetan 2 g IV 12hourly + doxycycline 100 mg BD orally/ IV for 48 hours forms the crux of the treatment regimen for PID. This is followed by oral doxycycline 100 mg +/-metronidazole 400 mg for 14 days in all hospitalized patients having PID.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment of PID in hospitalized patients</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cefotetan 2 g IV 12hourly + doxycycline 100 mg BD orally/ IV  for 48 hours is given followed by oral doxycycline 100 mg +/- metronidazole 400 mg for 14 days</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cefoxitin 2 g IV 6hourly + doxycycline 100 mg IV followed by oral route.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Azithromycin 500 mg IV 6 hourly for 2 days, then orally for <em>Chlamydia.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Outpatient treatment of PID</strong> (according to the current <strong>RCOG</strong> guidelines):</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A single dose of IM ceftriaxone 250 mg followed by oral doxycycline 100 mg BD +/- metronidazole 400 mg BD x 14 days</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A single dose of IM ceftriaxone 250 mg followed by azithromycin 1 gm per week x 2 weeks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>IUD</strong> is not removed in a woman who responds to antibiotics. It is <strong>removed</strong> only in case of a <strong>failed antibiotic response</strong>. Barrier contraceptives and OCPs are recommended only in women unresponsive to antibiotics.</p>\n<p>The use of <strong>Placentrex</strong> (aqueous extract of the placenta) is <strong>outdated</strong> and is not recommended currently.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0852","text":"A 30-year-old female prese‌nts to the OPD with fever​, lower abdominal pain an‍d purulent vaginal discha‌rge. On examination, her ​vitals are stable and per​ vaginal examination reve‍als lower abdominal and c​ervical motion tenderness​. What is the best treatm​ent option for her?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old female presents to the OPD with fever, lower abdominal pain and purulent vaginal discharge. On examination, her vitals are stable and per vaginal examination reveals lower abdominal and cervical motion tenderness. What is the best treatment option for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Single IM injection of cef‌triaxone 250 mg with oral​ doxycycline and metronid‍azole for 14 days","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Single IM injection of cef‌triaxone 250 mg with oral​ metronidazole 400 mg for‍ 7 days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oral doxycycline 100 mg BD‌ with oral metronidazole ​400 mg for 7 days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oral ofloxacin 200 mg BD w‌ith oral metronidazole 40​0 mg for 14 days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Single IM injection of ceftriaxone 250 mg with oral doxycycline and metronidazole for 14 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Single IM injection of ceftriaxone 250 mg with oral doxycycline and metronidazole for 14 days</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of<strong> fever</strong> and <strong>lower abdominal pain</strong> associated with lower abdominal and <strong>cervical motion tenderness</strong> is suggestive of<strong> PID</strong>. The best treatment option for this patient is <strong>outpatient treatment</strong> with a single dose of <strong>IM ceftriaxone 250 mg</strong> + <strong>oral doxycycline</strong> 100 mg BD +/- <strong>metronidazole</strong> 400 mg BD x <strong>14 days.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Outpatient treatment of PID</strong> (according to the current <strong>RCOG</strong> guidelines):</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A single dose of IM ceftriaxone 250 mg + oral doxycycline 100 mg BD +/- metronidazole 400 mg BD x 14 days</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A single dose of IM ceftriaxone 250 mg followed by azithromycin 1 gm per week x 2 weeks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment of PID in hospitalized patients</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cefotetan 2 g IV 12hourly + doxycycline 100 mg BD orally/ IV  for 48 hours is given followed by oral doxycycline 100 mg +/- metronidazole 400 mg for 14 days</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cefoxitin 2 g IV 6hourly + doxycycline 100 mg IV followed by oral route.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Azithromycin 500 mg IV 6 hourly for 2 days, then orally for <em>Chlamydia.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3585","text":"Which of the following wou‌ld be an indication for s​urgery in a 30-year-old w‍oman diagnosed with PID?","raw_text":"Which of the following would be an indication for surgery in a 30-year-old woman diagnosed with PID?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A tubo-ovarian abscess of ‌2 cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perit‌onitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sep‌sis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intestinal ‌obstruction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Intestinal obstruction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Intestinal obstruction</strong></p><p><strong>Intestinal obstruction</strong> would be an <strong>indication for surgery</strong> in a patient diagnosed with PID.</p>\n<p><strong>Criteria for surgery in PID </strong>include:</p>\n<ul>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Drainage of pelvic abscess by colpotomy.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Dilatation and evacuation of septic products of conception or for haemorrhage in the postabortal period.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Acute spreading peritonitis resistant to a full course of chemotherapy. Presence of pyoperitoneum mandates laparotomy.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Intestinal obstruction</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Suspected intestinal injury as diagnosed in criminal abortion.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ruptured tubo-ovarian abscess</li>\n</ol>\n</ul>\n<p>A tubo-ovarian abscess of 2 cm is not an indication for surgery.<strong> Minimally invasive surgery</strong> is done in a <strong>tubo-ovarian abscess</strong> measuring <strong>more than 10 cm</strong>, that fails to respond to antibiotics in 48-72 hours. </p>\n<p><strong>Peritonitis</strong> and <strong>sepsis</strong> are not an indication for surgery. They should be <strong>managed medically</strong> first. If they do not respond to antibiotic treatment then surgical management should be done.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3586","text":"Which among the following ‌is an indication for mini​mally invasive surgery fo‍r tubo-ovarian abscess?","raw_text":"Which among the following is an indication for minimally invasive surgery for tubo-ovarian abscess?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The size of the abscess is‌ more than 5 cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The size of the abscess is‌ more than 2cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The abscess fails to respo‌nd to antibiotics in 48 -​ 72 h","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The abscess fails to respo‌nd to antibiotics in 24 -​ 36 h","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The abscess fails to respond to antibiotics in 48 - 72 h","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The abscess fails to respond to antibiotics in 48 - 72 h</strong></p><p>If the abscess <strong>fails</strong> to respond to <strong>antibiotics</strong> in <strong>48–72 h</strong>, minimally invasive surgery is indicated.</p>\n<p> <strong>Criteria for minimally invasive surgery in PID:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The size of the abscess is more than 10 cm</li>\n<li>The abscess fails to respond to antibiotics in 48-72 hours</li>\n<li>The abscess ruptures</li>\n<li>Pyoperitoneum</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Minimal invasive surgery includes <strong>laparoscopic drainage</strong> of the tubo ovarian abscess or <strong>posterior colpotomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3587","text":"A 55-year-old multiparous ‌woman who presented with ​chronic lower abdominal p‍ain has been diagnosed wi‌th chronic PID. What is t​he treatment of choice fo​r this patient?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old multiparous woman who presented with chronic lower abdominal pain has been diagnosed with chronic PID. What is the treatment of choice for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radical hy‌sterectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Supracervical hysterectomy‌ with tuboplasty","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abdominal hysterectomy wit‌h bilateral salpingo-ooph​orectomy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Supracervical hysterectomy‌ with bilateral salpingec​tomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Abdominal hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Abdominal hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy</strong></p><p>The <strong>treatment of choice</strong> for an old multiparous woman with chronic PID is <strong>abdominal hysterectomy</strong> <strong>with bilateral</strong> <strong>salpingo-oophorectomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>A <strong>radical hysterectomy</strong> involves the removal of the whole uterus, tissue from the sides of the uterus, the cervix, and the upper part of the vagina. It is generally done only in <strong>cancer</strong> patients.</p>\n<p>A <strong>supracervical hysterectomy</strong> involves the removal of only the body of the uterus and is not done in chronic PID. It is done for the treatment of<strong> endometriosis</strong>, uterine<strong> fibroids</strong>, and uterine <strong>prolapse</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3588","text":"Who has described the prog‌nostic evaluation for PID​?","raw_text":"Who has described the prognostic evaluation for PID?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Boer–‌Meisel","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gaine‌sville","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both A‌ and B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wes‌trom","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Boer–Meisel","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Boer–Meisel</strong></p><p><strong>Boer-Meisel</strong> has described the <strong>prognostic evaluation</strong> for <strong>PID</strong>. It depends upon the following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Extent of adhesions</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nature of adhesions such as flimsy or dense.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Size of hydrosalpinx</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Macroscopic condition of hydrosalpinx.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The thickness of the tubal wall.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Boer-Meisel system is specifically used for <strong>predicting pregnancy outcomes</strong> in patients treated for PID.</p>\n<p><strong>Gainesville</strong> has described the <strong>stages of PID</strong> depending upon the severity of tubal damage.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 154px;\" width=\"652\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 68.4688px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 567.531px;\">Stages of PID</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 68.4688px;\">Stage I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 567.531px;\">Acute salpingitis without peritonitis - no adhesions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 68.4688px;\">Stage II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 567.531px;\">Acute salpingitis with peritonitis- purulent discharge</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 68.4688px;\">Stage III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 567.531px;\">Acute salpingitis with superimposed tubal occlusion or tubo-ovarian complex</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 68.4688px;\">Stage IV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 567.531px;\">Ruptured tubo-ovarian abscess</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 68.4688px;\">Stage V</td>\n<td style=\"width: 567.531px;\">Tuberculous salpingitis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Westrom </strong>has reported that women with one<strong> previous </strong>attack of<strong> PID</strong> have a<strong> predisposition </strong>to another attack.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4482","text":"Long-standing pelvic infla‌mmation may lead to which​ of the following conditi‍ons?","raw_text":"Long-standing pelvic inflammation may lead to which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyom‌etra","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Uterine ‌polyposis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pseudo‌cyesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endometrial‌ hyperplasia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pyometra","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pyometra</strong></p><p><strong>Long-standing pelvic inflammation</strong> may lead to <strong>pyometra</strong>. This refers to the collection of <strong>pus</strong> in the uterine cavity.</p>\n<p>The initial infection involves the <strong>cervix uteri</strong> and <strong>urethra</strong> which may extend to Bartholin glands, skene glands, endometrium, and fallopian tubes. <strong>Unilateral pain</strong> and <strong>swelling</strong> over the labia may be caused by the inflammation of the Bartholin glands.</p>\n<p>Depending on the extent of tissue involved, PID can be classified into five stages</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stage I - Acute salpingitis without peritonitis - No adhesions</li>\n<li>Stage II - Acute salpingitis with peritonitis - purulent discharge</li>\n<li>Stage III - Acute salpingitis with superimposed tubal occlusion or tubo ovarian complex</li>\n<li>Stage IV - Ruptured tubo-ovarian abscess</li>\n<li>Stage V - Tubercular salpingitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Long-standing pelvic inflammation may also lead to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Dyspareunia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Infertility</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ectopic pregnancy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Formation of adhesions like hydrosalpinx, pyosalpinx, tubo-ovarian abscess</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Chronic pelvic pain</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>Chronic pelvic inflammation</strong> leads to <strong>dense adhesions</strong> between the uterus and the appendages. Thus, it becomes difficult to define the uterus from the pelvic masses. They form a hard mass which is called the <strong>frozen</strong> <strong>pelvis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most <strong>common</strong> cause of the spread of PID: <strong>Ascending infection</strong> along with sperms<br/>The only PID that <strong>does not spread</strong> with the sexual route is genital TB. It has a hematogenous spread.<br/>The most common PID in virgin females is <strong>genital TB</strong>.<br/>Investigation of choice in PID: <strong>Abdominal USG</strong><br/>The surest sign of salpingitis is <strong>pus</strong> extruding from the fimbrial end of the tube.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ugWFr8jN","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"39_pediatric_anemias","title":"39.Pediatric Anemias","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC3054","text":"As a part of the Anemia Mu‌kt Bharat program, you sc‌reen preschool children f​or anemia. What Hb values​ would prompt you to diag​nose anemia in this age g‌roup?","raw_text":"As a part of the Anemia Mukt Bharat program, you screen preschool children for anemia. What Hb values would prompt you to diagnose anemia in this age group?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt; 1‌3 g/dL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt;1‌2 g/dL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt;11‌.5 g/dL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt;1‌1 g/dL","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. <11 g/dL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. &lt;11 g/dL</strong></p><p>Anaemia in a <strong>preschool child</strong> is defined as a hemoglobin level of <strong>&lt;11 g/dL.</strong></p>\n<p>Preschool age is defined as 2 to 5 years.</p>\n<p>WHO cut-offs for the diagnosis of anaemia:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 178px; width: 235.111px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\"><strong>Age</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 79.1111px;\"><strong>Hemoglobin (g/dL)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\"><strong>6 months to 5years</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 79.1111px;\"><strong>&lt;11 </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">5 to 11 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 79.1111px;\">&lt;11.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">12 to<strong> </strong>14 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 79.1111px;\">&lt;12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">Non-pregnant women (≥15 years)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 79.1111px;\">&lt;12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">Pregnant women</td>\n<td style=\"width: 79.1111px;\">&lt;11</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">Men (≥15 years)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 79.1111px;\">&lt;13</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3058","text":"A 7-year-old child is brou‌ght to the pediatric OPD ‌by his parents with compl​aints of excessive sleepi​ness, and poor scholastic​ performance. On examinat‌ion, the child has pallor​ involving the tongue, co​njunctiva, and nail beds.​ A flow murmur is heard u‌pon auscultation. Which o​f the following is the mo​st likely cause of the un‌derlying disease?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old child is brought to the pediatric OPD by his parents with complaints of excessive sleepiness, and poor scholastic performance. On examination, the child has pallor involving the tongue, conjunctiva, and nail beds. A flow murmur is heard upon auscultation. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the underlying disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenital hypoplastic ane‌mia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acquired hypoproliferative‌ anemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Congenital hemoglobinopath‌ies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperprolife‌rative anemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acquired hypoproliferative anemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acquired hypoproliferative anemia</strong></p><p>The given clinical signs and symptoms are suggestive of <strong>anemia</strong>. <strong>Acquired hypoproliferative anemia,</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> <strong>cause</strong> of anemia in children among the given options.</p>\n<p><strong>Iron-deficiency anemia</strong> (which is classified as an acquired hypoproliferative anemia) is the <strong>most common cause </strong>of anemia in<strong> children and adults</strong> <strong>worldwide</strong>. Iron deficiency is also the <strong>most common nutrient deficiency worldwide.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3084","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‌ iron?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding iron?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Breast milk has higher iro‌n content than cow's milk‌.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cow's milk has higher oral‌ bio-availability of iron‌ than breast milk.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Introduction of cow's milk‌ at 5 months of age decre‌ases the risk of iron def​iciency.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cow's milk protein can cau‌se chronic intestinal blo‌od loss in infants.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cow's milk protein can cause chronic intestinal blood loss in infants.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cow&#x27;s milk protein can cause chronic intestinal blood loss in infants.</strong></p><p>Exposure to whole cow’s milk protein can cause <strong>chronic intestinal blood loss in infants</strong>. Therefore, the American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) recommends <strong>delaying</strong> the <strong>introduction of cow's milk</strong> in <strong>infancy</strong> and limiting it to &lt;700 mL/day.</p>\n<p>Both cow's milk and breast milk have the <strong>same iron content</strong> (≈0.1mg/dL).</p>\n<p>The <strong>bioavailability</strong> of breast milk iron is 2-3 times <strong>higher</strong> than cow's milk iron.</p>\n<p><strong>Exclusive breastfeeding until 6 months of age</strong> and complementary feeding with iron-rich foods thereafter reduces the risk of iron-deficiency anemia in infants.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3085","text":"An 8-year-old child who ha‌s been recently diagnosed‌ with iron deficiency ane​mia is started on iron th​erapy. What is the earlie​st hematological response‌ that you would expect to​ see following treatment?​","raw_text":"An 8-year-old child who has been recently diagnosed with iron deficiency anemia is started on iron therapy. What is the earliest hematological response that you would expect to see following treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reticul‌ocytosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in hemoglobin lev‌el","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increase in ferritin level‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bone marrow erythroid hype‌rplasia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bone marrow erythroid hyperplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bone marrow erythroid hyperplasia</strong></p><p><strong>Bone marrow erythroid hyperplasia</strong> is the <strong>earliest</strong> <strong>hematological</strong> response observed following iron therapy in iron-deficiency anemia.</p>\n<p>Responses to iron therapy in iron deficiency anemia:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 326px; height: 483px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 34px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 34px; width: 106px;\"><strong>Time after administration</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 34px; width: 204px;\"><strong>Response</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 70px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 70px; width: 106px;\">12 to 24 h</td>\n<td style=\"height: 70px; width: 204px;\">\n<p>Subjective improvements - decreased irritability, increased appetite</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 56px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 56px; width: 106px;\">36 to 48 h</td>\n<td style=\"height: 56px; width: 204px;\">Bone marrow response- erythroid hyperplasia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 34px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 34px; width: 106px;\">48 to 72 h (peaks at 5-7 days)</td>\n<td style=\"height: 34px; width: 204px;\">Reticulocytosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 34px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 34px; width: 106px;\">4 to 30 days</td>\n<td style=\"height: 34px; width: 204px;\">Increase in hemoglobin level</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 66.4028px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 66.4028px; width: 106px;\">1 to 3 months</td>\n<td style=\"height: 66.4028px; width: 204px;\">Repletion of stores (ferritin)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3086","text":"A child who was diagnosed ‌3 months back with iron d‌eficiency anemia presents​ with low hemoglobin desp​ite being on oral iron th​erapy. What is the most l‌ikely cause for the non-r​esponsiveness that you sh​ould consider in your dif​ferential?","raw_text":"A child who was diagnosed 3 months back with iron deficiency anemia presents with low hemoglobin despite being on oral iron therapy. What is the most likely cause for the non-responsiveness that you should consider in your differential?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Poor co‌mpliance","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Incorrect dose or formulat‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Occult blood loss from par‌asitic infestations.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thalassemia misdiagnosed a‌s iron-deficiency anemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Poor compliance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Poor compliance</strong></p><p><strong>Poor compliance</strong> is the <strong>most likely cause</strong> of <strong>non-responsiveness</strong> to<strong> oral iron</strong> therapy.</p>\n<p>Differential diagnosis of microcytic anemia that fails to respond to oral iron:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Poor compliance (most common cause) </li>\n<li><strong>Incorrect dose or formulation</strong></li>\n<li>Malabsorption of administered iron</li>\n<li>Ongoing blood loss, including gastrointestinal, menstrual, and pulmonary</li>\n<li>Concurrent <strong>infection</strong> or inflammatory disorder </li>\n<li>Concurrent vitamin B12 or folate deficiency</li>\n<li>Diagnosis other than iron deficiency\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Thalassemias</strong></li>\n<li>Hemoglobins C and E disorders</li>\n<li>Anemia of chronic disease</li>\n<li>Lead poisoning</li>\n<li>Sickle thalassemias, hemoglobin SC disease</li>\n<li>Iron refractory iron-deficiency anemia (IRIDA)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3098","text":"Which of the following tes‌ts can be used to screen ‌a newborn with a family h​istory of sickle cell dis​ease?","raw_text":"Which of the following tests can be used to screen a newborn with a family history of sickle cell disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High-performance liquid ch‌romatography","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Heel-prick ‌Guthrie test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tandem mass ‌spectrometry","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. High-performance liquid chromatography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. High-performance liquid chromatography</strong></p><p><strong>High-performance liquid chromatography</strong> (HPLC) is used to screen a newborn with a family history of sickle cell disease.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Hemoglobin electrophoresis </strong>is the <strong>confirmatory test</strong> for <strong>sickle cell anemia</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Heel-prick <strong>Guthrie test </strong>is the neonatal screening test for <strong>phenylketonuria</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Tandem mass spectrometry </strong>is being used for neonatal screening of inherited<strong> metabolic disorders.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3140","text":"Ravi, an adolescent with s‌ickle cell disease presen‌ts to the emergency room ​with chest pain and respi​ratory distress. Pulse ox​imeter shows 85% saturati‌on in room air. Chest X-r​ay shows new radiodensiti​es. Sputum and blood cult​ure showed no growth. Whi‌ch of the following are u​sed in the management of ​this condition? Macrolide‌ antibiotics Incentive sp​irometry Blood transfusio​n Third-generation cephal​osporins Supplemental oxy‌gen","raw_text":"<p>Ravi, an adolescent with sickle cell disease presents to the emergency room with chest pain and respiratory distress. Pulse oximeter shows 85% saturation in room air. Chest X-ray shows new radiodensities. Sputum and blood culture showed no growth. Which of the following are used in the management of this condition?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Macrolide antibiotics</li>\n<li>Incentive spirometry</li>\n<li>Blood transfusion</li>\n<li>Third-generation cephalosporins</li>\n<li>Supplemental oxygen</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2, an‌d 3 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3, an‌d 5 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 4, an‌d 5 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, ‌3, 4, 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 3, 4, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 3, 4, 5</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute chest syndrome</strong> and all of the above are used in the management. </p>\n<p><strong>Acute chest syndrome </strong>(ACS) refers to a <strong>life-threatening</strong> pulmonary <strong>complication</strong> of sickle cell disease. It is diagnosed with the presence of a<strong> new radiodensity</strong> on chest radiography <strong>with</strong> <strong>any 2</strong> of the following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Respiratory distress</li>\n<li>Hypoxia</li>\n<li>Cough</li>\n<li>Chest pain</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In this case, the child with sickle cell anemia satisfies the above criteria. He presented with <strong>chest pain</strong> and <strong>respiratory distress</strong> and his chest X-ray image shows new radiodensities. </p>\n<p>Most patients with ACS do not have a single identifiable cause. Infection is the most well-known etiology, yet only 30% of ACS episodes will have positive sputum or bronchoalveolar culture. The most common bacterial pathogens implicated are <em>S. pneumoniae, Mycoplasma pneumoniae, </em>and<em> Chlamydia spp.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Oxygen </strong>should be supplemented to all patients who demonstrate hypoxia. </p>\n<p><strong>Blood transfusion</strong> is the only method to abort a rapidly progressing episode of ACS.</p>\n<p>Because of the clinical overlap between pneumonia and ACS, all episodes should be treated promptly with <strong>antimicrobial therapy</strong>, including at least a <strong>macrolide</strong> and possibly a <strong>third-generation cephalosporin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Regular use of an <strong>incentive spirometer</strong> at 10-12 breaths every 2 hours can significantly reduce the frequency of subsequent acute chest pain episodes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3142","text":"A 6-year-old boy who is a ‌known case of sickle cell‌ disease presents with ac​ute chest syndrome. Which​ of the following is the ​drug of choice to reduce ‌such painful crises in th​e future?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old boy who is a known case of sickle cell disease presents with acute chest syndrome. Which of the following is the drug of choice to reduce such painful crises in the future?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydro‌codone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hydro‌xyurea","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cortico‌steroids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ruxol‌itinib","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hydroxyurea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hydroxyurea</strong></p><p><strong>Hydroxyurea</strong> is the <strong>drug of choice</strong> for <strong>reducing painful crises</strong> in sickle cell anemia.</p>\n<p>Hydroxyurea is a <strong>m</strong><strong>yelosuppressive agent </strong>that is effective in <strong>reducing</strong> the frequency of painful episodes like <strong>dactylitis and acute chest syndrome</strong>, decreasing the rate of hospitalization, and requirement for blood transfusion. It is safe and well-tolerated in children and infants.</p>\n<p>Option A: Hydrocodone (opioid) is used in the treatment of pain crisis in sickle cell anemia.</p>\n<p>Option C: Corticosteroids is one of the first-line therapies for acute idiopathic thrombocytic purpura (ITP) with risk factors for bleeding.</p>\n<p>Option D: Ruxolitinib is the drug of choice for the treatment of polycythemia vera.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3099","text":"Kevin and Anita both have ‌sickle cell traits. They ‌are devastated when they ​find out both their child​ren have sickle cell anem​ia. Kirti is an active 9-‌year-old. Kamal is her 3-​month-old brother. Which ​of the following prophyla​ctic measures would you n‌ot recommend to them?","raw_text":"Kevin and Anita both have sickle cell traits. They are devastated when they find out both their children have sickle cell anemia. Kirti is an active 9-year-old. Kamal is her 3-month-old brother. Which of the following prophylactic measures would you not recommend to them?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral penicillin prophylaxi‌s up to 5 years of age in‌ Kamal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Annual transcranial Dopple‌r ultrasound for Kirti up‌to age 16","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Annual ophthalmology scree‌ning from the age of 10 y‌ears in both","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydroxyurea prophylaxis re‌gardless of disease sever‌ity or symptoms in both","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hydroxyurea prophylaxis regardless of disease severity or symptoms in both","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hydroxyurea prophylaxis regardless of disease severity or symptoms in both</strong></p><p><strong>Hydroxyurea prophylaxis</strong> regardless of disease severity is recommended in children <strong>over</strong> <strong>9 months</strong> of age. Hydroxyurea can be considered for use in infants between six and nine months with symptomatic disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Oral penicillin VK</strong> is recommended until <strong>at least 5 years of age. </strong>It is continued beyond 5 years of age in children with a history of pneumococcal infection. Erythromycin ethyl succinate is used if the patient is allergic to penicillin. Pneumococcal and meningococcal vaccinations are administered because of the risk of functional asplenia.</p>\n<p><strong>Transcranial Doppler</strong> ultrasound is recommended, starting at age of <strong>2 years. </strong>This has lead to a decrease in the prevalence of overt stroke among children with sickle cell anemia. Screening should occur annually from age 2-16.</p>\n<p><strong>Annual ophthalmology screening</strong> should commence from the age of<strong> 10 years</strong> to screen for <strong>retinopathy</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3102","text":"Which of the following is ‌responsible for the most ‌common cause of congenita​l hemolytic anemia due to​ an enzyme deficiency?","raw_text":"Which of the following is responsible for the most common cause of congenital hemolytic anemia due to an enzyme deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deficiency of an enzyme in‌ glycolysis pathway","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deficiency of an enzyme in‌ Hexose monophosphate pat‌hway","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Deficiency of an enzyme in‌ Kreb's cycle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deficiency of an enzymes i‌n fructose metabolism pat‌hway","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Deficiency of an enzyme in Hexose monophosphate pathway","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Deficiency of an enzyme in Hexose monophosphate pathway</strong></p><p>Deficiency of an enzyme in the <strong>hexose monophosphate pathway</strong> ie., <strong>glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency(G6PD Deficiency),</strong> is the most common cause of congenital hemolytic anemia due to an enzyme deficiency</p>\n<p>Most common enzymopathies causing hemolytic anemia (in order of most common to least):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency (G6PD)</li>\n<li>Pyruvate kinase deficiency</li>\n<li>5' nucleotidase deficiency</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3105","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a cause of non-immune‌ hydrops fetalis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a cause of non-immune hydrops fetalis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alpha th‌alassemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parvovirus B‌19 infection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rh incomp‌atibility","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Twin-twin transfusion synd‌rome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rh incompatibility","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rh incompatibility</strong></p><p><strong>Rh incompatibility</strong> causes<strong> immune hydrops fetalis, </strong>whereas<strong> </strong><strong>alpha-thalassemia</strong>, <strong>parvovirus B19</strong> infection, and <strong>twin-twin transfusion syndrome</strong> cause <strong>non-immune hydrops fetalis. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Hydrops fetalis</strong> refers to the <strong>abnormal fluid collections in fetal soft tissues and serous cavities</strong>. Edema, signs of heart failure, and profound intra-uterine anemia are the presenting features in the fetus. Other clinical features include marked hepatosplenomegaly, retardation in brain growth, hydrocephaly, urogenital and cardiovascular deformities, and extramedullary erythropoiesis.</p>\n<p>Non-immune hydrops fetalis (NIHF) comprises the subgroup of cases not caused by red cell alloimmunization (e.g., Rh(D), Kell)</p>\n<p><strong>Bart's hydrops fetalis </strong>(alpha-thalassemia) is caused by the deletion or inactivation of all four alpha-globin alleles, leading to a severe deficiency in alpha-globin chains of Hb and to the production of gamma-4 tetramers (Hb Bart). Hb Bart tetramers have an <strong>extremely high oxygen affinity</strong> and are <strong>incapable of effective tissue oxygen delivery</strong>. </p>\n<p>Parvovirus B19 infection can cause fetal anemia, non-immune fetal hydrops, and fetal death. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3129","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about abnormal he‌moglobins and their mecha​nisms is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about abnormal hemoglobins and their mechanisms is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HbS is due to substitution‌ of lysine for glutamine ‌in the 6th position of th​e beta-globin gene.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HbH is the deletion of thr‌ee alpha-globin genes.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hb Bart is the deletion of‌ all four beta-globin gen‌es.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HbC is due to substitution‌ of valine for lysine in ‌the 6th position of the b​eta-globin gene.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. HbH is the deletion of three alpha-globin genes.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. HbH is the deletion of three alpha-globin genes.</strong></p><p><strong>HbH</strong> is the <strong>deletion</strong> of <strong>three alpha globin</strong> chains.</p>\n<p><strong>HbS</strong> is due to the substitution of <strong>valine</strong> for glutamic acid in the 6th position of the beta-globin gene (option A).</p>\n<p><strong>Hb Bart</strong> is the deletion of all <strong>four alpha-</strong>globin genes. (There are only 2 beta-globin genes) (option C)</p>\n<p><strong>HbC</strong> is due to the substitution of <strong>lysine</strong> for glutamic acid in the 6th position of the beta-globin gene (option D). </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3132","text":"A 5-year-old child present‌ed with pallor, massive h‌epatosplenomegaly, and he​molytic facies. Which of ​the following is the most​ likely iron profile in t‌his patient?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old child presented with pallor, massive hepatosplenomegaly, and hemolytic facies.  Which of the following is the most likely iron profile in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iron - low, Ferritin - low‌, TIBC-high, TSAT-low","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iron - high, Ferritin - no‌rmal, TIBC-normal, TSAT-n‌ormal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Iron - low, Ferritin - hig‌h, TIBC-low, TSAT-low","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iron - low, Ferritin - low‌, TIBC-low, TSAT-low","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Iron - high, Ferritin - normal, TIBC-normal, TSAT-normal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Iron - high, Ferritin - normal, TIBC-normal, TSAT-normal</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>thalassemia </strong>and the most likely iron profile is iron - <strong>normal/high</strong>, ferritin - <strong>normal</strong>, total iron - binding capacity (TIBC) - <strong>normal</strong>, and transferrin saturation (TSAT) - <strong>normal.</strong></p>\n<p>Iron- low, ferritin-low, TIBC-high, TSAT-low is seen in<strong> iron-deficiency anemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Iron-low, ferritin-high, TIBC-low, TSAT-low is seen in <strong>anemia of chronic disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3087","text":"A toddler presented with p‌rogressive pallor and abd‌ominal distention. On inv​estigations, he had low h​emoglobin. Which of the f​ollowing red cell indices‌ can be used to distingui​sh thalassemia from iron ​deficiency anemia?","raw_text":"A toddler presented with progressive pallor and abdominal distention. On investigations, he had low hemoglobin. Which of the following red cell indices can be used to distinguish thalassemia from iron deficiency anemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mean corpus‌cular volume","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mean corpuscular hemoglobi‌n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mean corpuscular volume/ R‌ed blood cell count","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mean corpuscular hemoglobi‌n concentration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mean corpuscular volume/ Red blood cell count","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mean corpuscular volume/ Red blood cell count</strong></p><p><strong>Mean corpuscular volume/ Red blood cell count (MCV/RBC)</strong> also known as <strong>Mentzer index</strong> can be used to distinguish thalassemia from iron-deficiency anemia (IDA).</p>\n<p><strong>MCV</strong> (mean corpuscular volume) and <strong>MCH</strong> (mean corpuscular hemoglobin) are <strong>low in both IDA and thalassemia</strong>. <strong>RBC count</strong> may be normal in thalassemia, whereas it is <strong>reduced in IDA</strong>. A lower RBC count in IDA means the denominator is smaller, yielding a greater MCV/RBC ratio.</p>\n<p>Mentzer index is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>More than 13 in iron-deficiency anemia</li>\n<li>Less than 13 in thalassemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6735","text":"A 14-month-old child with ‌Hb 6.3 was undergoing rep‌eated blood transfusions.​ The last transfusion was​ given in the 9th month. ​Which of the following in‌vestigation is used for a​ definitive diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 14-month-old child with Hb 6.3 was undergoing repeated blood transfusions. The last transfusion was given in the 9th month. Which of the following investigation is used for a definitive diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High performance liquid ch‌romatography","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ferritin levels estimation‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bone marrow‌ aspiration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Globin gene‌ sequencing","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Globin gene sequencing","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bd0c48a8dd0f43f3a180c62010a5543f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/14d024e36191482ebf99ba4bbcc29994x512x357.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fa3808e1a1f642e29ecff68602d94c41x720x549.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Globin gene sequencing</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">The given clinical scenario of <strong>repeated transfusion</strong>, <strong>low hemoglobin level</strong>, and <strong>target cells</strong> in the smear are suggestive of <strong>thalassemia. G</strong><strong>lobin gene sequencing is </strong>used for its <strong>definitive diagnosis</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">High-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) is used as a <strong>screening test</strong> for thalassemia (option A). </p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"><strong>Thalassemia</strong> is a hemoglobinopathy that arises due to deficient <strong>synthesis of the globin chains</strong>. Depending on the chain affected, it is classified into <strong>α-thalassemia</strong> and <strong>β-thalassemia</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">β-thalassemia is caused by <strong>point mutations</strong> of the β-thalassemia gene on chromosome 11, which diminish the synthesis of β-globin chains.<strong> Splicing mutations</strong> are the <strong>most common</strong> cause of (β+) thalassemia. </p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">There is a deficit in the synthesis of HbA with an <strong>elevation of HbA2</strong> and<strong> HbF</strong> levels in β-thalassemia. Children with severe thalassemia present with distinctive facial features such as <strong>maxilla hyperplasia</strong>, a <strong>flat nasal bridge</strong>, and <strong>frontal bossing</strong>. Other manifestations include pathologic <strong>bone fractures</strong>, significant<strong> hepatosplenomegaly</strong>, and cachexia. </p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Blood smears show marked <strong>anisocytosis</strong>, <strong>poikilocytosis</strong>, and microcytic hypochromic anemia. <strong>Target cells</strong>, basophilic stippling, and fragmented red cells also are common. A radiographic image of the skull shows the <strong>classic 'crew cut'</strong> appearance.</p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"><strong>Hb Electrophoresis</strong> and<strong> high-performance liquid chromatography</strong> (HPLC) are used as <strong>screening tests</strong> for thalassemia. <strong>DNA diagnosis</strong> to detect β-thalassemia mutations is used for definitive diagnosis. The <strong>Mentzer index</strong> is the ratio of MCV to RBC count. It is used to differentiate between iron-deficiency anemia and β-thalassemia trait. The Mentzer index is <strong>&lt;13</strong> in <strong>thalassemia minor</strong> syndrome.</p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Transfusions are administered every 3-4 weeks to maintain a pretransfusion hemoglobin level of 9.5-10.5 g/dL. <strong>Transfusion-induced hemosiderosis</strong> is a significant complication in transfusion-dependent thalassemia. Iron deposition occurs in the <strong>liver, endocrine organs</strong>, and <strong>heart</strong>, resulting in complications including hypothyroidism, hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, growth hormone deficiency, hypoparathyroidism, diabetes mellitus, heart failure, and arrhythmias. </p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">The image below shows <strong>thalassemic facies </strong>and <strong>crew cut appearance</strong> on X-ray:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3136","text":"You see a child in the hem‌atology unit who has deve‌loped anemia which is pro​gressive along with cardi​ac decompensation requiri​ng transfusion. He is in ‌the latter half of infanc​y. You suspect transfusio​n-dependent thalassemia. ​Which of the following el‌ectrophoresis pattern is ​seen here?","raw_text":"You see a child in the hematology unit who has developed anemia which is progressive along with cardiac decompensation requiring transfusion. He is in the latter half of infancy. You suspect transfusion-dependent thalassemia. Which of the following electrophoresis pattern is seen here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HbA=95%, HbA2=3.5%, HbF =1‌.5%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HbA=90%, HbA‌2=5%, HbF=5%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HbA=45%, HbA‌2=5%, HbF=50%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HbA=0%, HbA2‌=5%, HbF=95%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. HbA=0%, HbA2=5%, HbF=95%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. HbA=0%, HbA2=5%, HbF=95%</strong></p><p><strong>HbA=0%, HbA2=5%, HbF=95%</strong> is seen on hemoglobin electrophoresis in <strong>transfusion-dependent thalassemia.</strong></p>\n<p>Given clinical picture where the child becomes symptomatic during the second 6 months of life requiring transfusion is suggestive of <strong>beta-thalassemia major </strong>or<strong> transfusion-dependent thalassemia.</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal pattern- HbA=95%, HbA2=3.5%, HbF =1.5%</li>\n<li>Beta-thalassemia trait- HbA=90%, HbA2=5%, HbF=5%</li>\n<li>Beta-thalassemia intermedia- HbA=45%, HbA2=5%, HbF=50%</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Alpha- and beta-thalassemia are inherited disorders in which there is reduced production of alpha-globin or beta-globin chains, respectively. This leads to precipitation of the excess (unpaired) chains, interfering with red blood cell (RBC) production and survival.Disease severity is inversely correlated with the number of functioning globin chains.Each individual carries 2 beta-chain genes and 4 alpha-chain genes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3138","text":"You are given the followin‌g x-ray in the viva sessi‌on. Which of the followin​g is the likely reason be​hind this abnormal appear​ance?","raw_text":"You are given the following x-ray in the viva session. Which of the following is the likely reason behind this abnormal appearance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Effective er‌ythropoiesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thinning of‌ trabeculae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Narrowing of the diploic s‌pace","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Extramedullary hematopoies‌is","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Extramedullary hematopoiesis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/84d42513a64f48e0a4c80282b60340f9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Extramedullary hematopoiesis</strong></p><p><strong>Crew-cut</strong> or <strong>hair-on-end appearance</strong> on X-ray imaging of the skull is due to <strong>extramedullary hematopoiesis</strong>, which is seen in <strong>thalassemia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Ineffective erythropoiesis</strong> leads to <strong>extramedullary hematopoiesis</strong>, which causes the<strong> widening </strong>of the<strong> diploic space</strong>, thereby causing the <strong>thickening</strong> of<strong> trabeculae</strong>. This results in the classical crew-cut or hair-on-end appearance on x-ray imaging of the skull. <strong>Extramedullary hematopoiesis</strong> also leads to <strong>hepatosplenomegaly</strong> and enlarged and distorted bony prominences over the face, leading to the characteristic <strong>chipmunk facies </strong>in<strong> </strong>thalassemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3134","text":"You are evaluating a 4-yea‌r-old child with ß-thalas‌semia major. He has been ​on chronic transfusion th​erapy since the age of 1 ​year. He has developed tr‌ansfusion-induced iron ov​erload. The child has an ​increased risk of develop​ing which of the followin‌g infections?","raw_text":"You are evaluating a 4-year-old child with ß-thalassemia major. He has been on chronic transfusion therapy since the age of 1 year. He has developed transfusion-induced iron overload. The child has an increased risk of developing which of the following infections?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococcu‌s pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mycoplasma‌ pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Yersinia en‌terocolitica","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chla‌mydia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Yersinia enterocolitica","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Yersinia enterocolitica</strong></p><p><strong>Thalassemic</strong> children with <strong>transfusion</strong>-<strong>induced iron overload</strong> are at an <strong>increased risk</strong> of infections with <strong><em>Yersinia enterocolitica</em>. </strong></p>\n<p>Iron excess immunologically compromises the host and greatly enhances yersinial growth.</p>\n<p><em>Streptococcus pneumoniae </em>(option A),<em> Mycoplasma pneumoniae </em>(option B)<em>,</em> and <em>Chlamydia species </em>(option D) are the common organisms causing infections that predispose to <strong>acute chest syndrome</strong> in <strong>sickle cell anemia.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3095","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about Diamond Blackf‌an Anemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about Diamond Blackfan Anemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Short stature, craniofacia‌l abnormalities, and trip‌halangeal thumb are commo​n features.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Peripheral smear shows mic‌rocytic hypochromic anemi‌a.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bone marrow failure includ‌es erythroid, myeloid and‌ megakaryocytic lineages.​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reticulocyte count is incr‌eased.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Short stature, craniofacial abnormalities, and triphalangeal thumb are common features.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6daf2e8d238841b09c4a15353eae3428x167x216.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Short stature, craniofacial abnormalities, and triphalangeal thumb are common features.</strong></p><p><strong>Short stature</strong>, <strong>craniofacial abnormalities</strong>, and <strong>triphalangeal thumb</strong> are <strong>common</strong> features is the <strong>true</strong> statement regarding<strong> Diamond–Blackfan</strong> <strong>anemia</strong> (DBA).</p>\n<p>Features of DBA (congenital hypoplastic anemia):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Autosomal <strong>dominant</strong> inheritance.</li>\n<li><strong>Macrocytosis</strong> <strong>without</strong> <strong>hypersegmented</strong> <strong>neutrophils</strong> or other megaloblastic features.</li>\n<li><strong>Reticulocytopenia.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Absent</strong> <strong>RBC precursors</strong> in the otherwise normally cellular bone marrow. Other lineages are <strong>unaffected</strong>. </li>\n<li><strong>Craniofacial anomaly </strong>is the most common congenital anomaly seeen.</li>\n<li>Hypertelorism, snub nose, and high-arched palate seen.</li>\n<li>Thumb abnormalities- flattening of the thenar eminence and <strong>triphalangeal thumb</strong></li>\n<li>Short stature</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong>: Corticosteroids, chronic red cell transfusions, and hematopoietic stem cell transplantation.</p>\n<p>Image showing triphalangeal thumb:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3128","text":"You see a neonate undergoi‌ng phototherapy for hyper‌bilirubinemia. Blood work​up revealed the presence ​of a hemolytic picture al​ong with spherocytes and ‌polychromatophilic reticu​locytes. MCV is low norma​l along with elevated MCH​C. There was a similar hi‌story of hemolytic anemia​ in the mother. The pedig​ree analysis points towar‌ds an autosomal dominant ​mode of inheritance. The ​most likely genetic mutat​ion seen in this case is?‌","raw_text":"You see a neonate undergoing phototherapy for hyperbilirubinemia. Blood workup revealed the presence of a hemolytic picture along with spherocytes and polychromatophilic reticulocytes. MCV is low normal along with elevated MCHC. There was a similar history of hemolytic anemia in the mother. The pedigree analysis points towards an autosomal dominant mode of inheritance. The most likely genetic mutation seen in this case is?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spec‌trin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ank‌yrin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ban‌d 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Band‌ 4.2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ankyrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ankyrin</strong></p><p>The <strong>Ankyrin gene</strong> mutation is the most likely genetic mutation seen in the given case of <strong>autosomal-dominant hereditary spherocytosis</strong> (HS).</p>\n<p>Hemolytic manifestation causing indirect hyperbilirubinemia requiring phototherapy or blood transfusion in infancy with positive family history and typical laboratory findings is suggestive of HS.</p>\n<p>History of blood transfusions in later life and not infancy is used to classify the severity of the disease.  </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most common <strong>mode of inheritance</strong> of HS- <strong>autosomal dominant</strong></li>\n<li>Most common gene <strong>mutation</strong> in autosomal-<strong>dominant</strong> HS- <strong>ankyrin</strong> &gt; Band 3</li>\n<li>Most common gene <strong>mutation</strong> in autosomal-<strong>recessive</strong> HS- <strong>spectrin</strong></li>\n<li>Most <strong>s</strong>evere form of HS- <strong>s</strong>pectrin (recessive) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Classification of the severity of HS is based on the requirement of blood transfusion in later life and not during infancy.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>RBC Membrane-Cytoskeleton disorders</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 328.09px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 245.09px;\">\n<table style=\"width: 327px;\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57px;\">\n<p><strong>Chromosomal Location</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px;\">\n<p><strong>Protein Produced</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<p><strong>Disease (Inheritance)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57px;\">\n<p>1q</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; text-align: left;\">\n<p><strong>-alpha Spectrin</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<p><strong>HS (recessive)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; text-align: left;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<p>HE (dominant)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57px;\">\n<p>14q</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; text-align: left;\">\n<p><strong>-beta Spectrin</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<p><strong>HE (dominant)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57px;\">\n<p><strong>8p</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; text-align: left;\">\n<p><strong>Ankyrin</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<p><strong>HS (dominant) (accounts for the majority of HS-65%)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57px;\">\n<p><strong>17q</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; text-align: left;\">\n<p><strong>Band 3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>(anion exchange protein)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<p><strong>HS (dominant)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; text-align: left;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<p><strong>Southeast Asia ovalocytosis (dominant)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; text-align: left;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<p><strong>Stomatocytosis</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57px;\">\n<p>1p</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Band 4.1</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<p>HE (dominant)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 57px;\">\n<p>15q</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; text-align: left;\">\n<p><strong>Band 4.2</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px;\">\n<p>HS (recessive)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 245.09px;\">\n<p><strong><em>Abbreviations:</em></strong> HE, hereditary elliptocytosis; HS, hereditary spherocytosis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3096","text":"An 11-year-old child, born‌ out of consanguineous ma‌rriage developed signs an​d symptoms of hemolytic a​nemia and progressive ata​xia. He is a known case o‌f retinitis pigmentosa an​d also had symptoms of fa​t malabsorption. A periph​eral smear of the child i‌s shown. What is the like​ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"An 11-year-old child, born out of consanguineous marriage developed signs and symptoms of hemolytic anemia and progressive ataxia. He is a known case of retinitis pigmentosa and also had symptoms of fat malabsorption. A peripheral smear of the child is shown. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemolytic ur‌emic syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucose 6-phosphate dehydr‌ogenase deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hereditary s‌tomatocytosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abetalipop‌roteinemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Abetalipoproteinemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4558770c5380487bb7ab5b9acc1c50a7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/165ec210b63649fd97c74061f98c4ee6x512x413.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ac5997bf5871450eb954d6fc919eb421x799x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5e6a62179710488e9e85b456b14dfd15x1280x529.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5c4f1e064a86481f97abaeabd0882c92x640x480.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Abetalipoproteinemia</strong></p><p>The given clinical features along with the peripheral smear showing <strong>acanthocytes</strong> make the diagnosis of <strong>abetalipoproteinemia</strong> more likely.</p>\n<p>Abetalipoproteinemia is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> hemolytic disorder in which <strong>acanthocytosis</strong> is associated with <strong>fat malabsorption</strong>, <strong>progressive ataxia</strong>, and <strong>retinitis pigmentosa.</strong></p>\n<p>Acanthocytes or spur cells are <strong>spiculated red cells</strong> with a few projections of varying size and surface distribution. The most significant associations include <strong>abetalipoproteinemia</strong> and<span style=\"font-size: 11.6667px;\"> </span><strong>severe liver disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: Do not confuse acanthocytes with <strong>echinocytes</strong> or <strong>burr cells. </strong>They<strong> </strong>appear with multiple small projections that are <strong>uniformly distributed</strong> on the red cell surface. It is a universal feature of <strong>uremia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Image</strong>: The peripheral smear below shows regularly spaced, smoothly rounded crenulations, which are characteristic of a burr cell.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Peripheral smear in other options:</p>\n<p>Hemolytic uremic syndrome (option A); Numerous schistocytes and nucleated RBCs with extremely low platelets:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency (option B); <strong>bite cells</strong> or <strong>degmacytes</strong> or <strong>blister cells</strong>. In G6PD deficiency uncontrolled oxidative stress causes hemoglobin to denature and form Heinz bodies. Bite cells result from the <strong>removal of Heinz bodies</strong> by macrophages in the spleen.</p>\n<p>The image below shows <strong>bite cells</strong> represented by <strong>blue arrows</strong>, black arrows represent burr cells:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Hereditary stomatocytosis (option C); Numerous stomatocytes (Uniconcave cup with mouth-shaped area of central pallor), caused by missense mutation in band 3 or AEP</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3088","text":"A 12-year-old child is rec‌eiving iron and folic aci‌d supplements under the W​eekly Iron and Folic Acid​ Supplementation (WIFS) P​rogramme. Which of the fo‌llowing is true about thi​s program?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old child is receiving iron and folic acid supplements under the Weekly Iron and Folic Acid Supplementation (WIFS) Programme.  Which of the following is true about this program?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It was launched by the Min‌istry of Health and Famil‌y Welfare.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is meant exclusively fo‌r adolescent school girls‌ between 10-19 years of a​ge.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It exclusively covers gove‌rnment schools in rural I‌ndia.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It recommends that adminis‌tration of 50 mg of eleme‌ntal iron.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It was launched by the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It was launched by the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare.</strong></p><p>Weekly Iron Folic acid Supplementation (WIFS) was launched by the <strong>Ministry of Health and Family Welfare</strong> is the true statement among the given options.</p>\n<p>Salient features of Weekly Iron Folic acid Supplementation (WIFS):</p>\n<p>i. <strong>Objective</strong> of Weekly Iron Folic acid Supplementation (WIFS): To <strong>reduce the prevalence and severity of anemia in the adolescent population</strong> (10-19 years).</p>\n<p>ii. <strong>Target groups</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>School-going adolescent girls and boys in 6th to 12th class enrolled in government/government-aided/municipal schools</li>\n<li>Out-of-school adolescent girls</li>\n</ul>\n<p>iii. <strong>Intervention</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Administration of supervised weekly iron-folic acid supplements of 60 mg elemental iron and 500 μg folic acid by using a fixed-day approach</li>\n<li>Screening of target groups for moderate/severe anemia and referring these cases to an appropriate health facility</li>\n<li>Biannual de-worming (albendazole 400 mg), six months apart, for the control of helminthic infestation</li>\n<li>Information and counseling for improving dietary intake and for taking actions for prevention of intestinal worm infestation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3124","text":"A toddler is brought by hi‌s mother with complaints ‌of poor feeding, lethargy​, and chronic diarrhea fo​r the past 4 months. Upon​ clinical examination, th‌e child has pale conjunct​iva and is tachycardic. B​lood workup reveals low h​emoglobin and smear shows‌ macrocytosis, anisopoiki​locytosis, and large neut​rophils with hyper-segmen‌ted nuclei. Serum homocys​teine levels are elevated​ and methylmalonate level​s are normal. Bone marrow‌ shows erythroid hyperpla‍sia. Which of the followi​ng is true about this con‌dition?","raw_text":"A toddler is brought by his mother with complaints of poor feeding, lethargy, and chronic diarrhea for the past 4 months. Upon clinical examination, the child has pale conjunctiva and is tachycardic.  Blood workup reveals low hemoglobin and smear shows macrocytosis, anisopoikilocytosis, and large neutrophils with hyper-segmented nuclei. Serum homocysteine levels are elevated and methylmalonate levels are normal. Bone marrow shows erythroid hyperplasia. Which of the following is true about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lactate dehydrogenase leve‌ls in serum is low.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Folate-deficient mother's ‌give birth to infants wit‌h folate deficiency.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Methylenetetrahydrofolate ‌(MTHFR) deficiency is ass‌ociated with megaloblasti​c anemia.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Goat’s milk fed infants ar‌e more likely to develop ‌this disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Goat’s milk fed infants are more likely to develop this disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Goat’s milk fed infants are more likely to develop this disease</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the <strong>megaloblastic picture</strong> <strong>elevated homocysteine</strong> levels, and <strong>normal methylmalonate</strong> levels are suggestive of <strong>folate deficiency anemia</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Goat’s milk</strong> is <strong>deficient</strong> <strong>in folate.</strong> So goat milk-fed infants are likely to develop folate deficiency anemia. Human breast milk, infant formulas, and pasteurized cow’s milk provide adequate amounts of folic acid.</p>\n<p>In folate deficiency anemia, <strong>lactate dehydrogenase</strong> levels in serum are <strong>elevated</strong>. Folate-deficient mothers <strong>do not</strong> give birth to infants with folate deficiency because there is a selective transfer of folate to the fetus via the placental folate receptors.</p>\n<p>Methylenetetrahydrofolate (MTHFR) deficiency is the <strong>most common inborn error of folate metabolism</strong>, and severe cases can produce neurologic and vascular complications; there is <strong>no</strong> <strong>associated megaloblastic anemia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Malnutrition</strong> is the most common cause of folate deficiency in children.</p>\n<p>Note: Both homocysteine and methylmalonate levels are elevated in B12 deficiency anemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9391","text":"A child presents with mega‌loblastic anemia that is ‌unresponsive to vitamin C​, vitamin B12, and folic ​acid, but responsive to u​ridine. What do you suspe‌ct?","raw_text":"A child presents with megaloblastic anemia that is unresponsive to vitamin C, vitamin B12, and folic acid, but responsive to uridine. What do you suspect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiamine ‌deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Liver ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pernicio‌us anemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Orotic ‌aciduria","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Orotic aciduria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Orotic aciduria</strong></p><p><strong>Orotic aciduria</strong> is to be suspected in this child. Megaloblastic anemia that is<strong> responsive to uridine</strong> is consistent with <strong>orotic aciduria</strong>.</p>\n<p>Orotic aciduria is a rare inborn error of metabolism in children. A defect in the <strong>pyrimidine synthesis pathway</strong> results in the failure of conversion of <strong>orotic acid</strong> to <strong>uridine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Supplementation of vitamin B12, folate, and vitamin C rules out the more common nutritional causes of megaloblastic anemia.</p>\n<div><strong>Thiamine deficiency </strong>(option A) and <strong>anemia due to liver disease </strong>(option B) do not respond to uridine.</div>\n<div>\n<p><strong>Pernicious anemia </strong>(Option C) responds to treatment with vitamin B12.</p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"K-dfSv66","path":["Paediatrics"]},{"id":"39_heart_failure_and_ischemic_disease","title":"39.Heart Failure And Ischemic Disease","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"MB0467","text":"Which of the following lay‌ers is not present in the​ cardiac valve?","raw_text":"Which of the following layers is not present in the cardiac valve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atri‌alis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventri‌cularis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spon‌giosa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adven‌titia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Adventitia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Adventitia</strong></p><p><strong>Adventitia</strong> is not a layer of the cardiac valves. It is the <strong>outermost</strong> layer of a <strong>blood vessel.</strong> It contains connective tissue, a few cells, macrophages, mast cells, fibroblasts, and the nerves and vessels that supply the vascular wall.</p>\n<p>Cardiac <strong>valves</strong> are lined by <strong>endothelium</strong> and share a <strong>tri-layered</strong> architecture:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong> Fibrosa </strong>is a<strong> dense collagenous</strong> core at the <strong>outflow</strong> surface. It is connected to the valvular supporting structures.</li>\n<li> <strong>S</strong><strong>pongiosa </strong>is a <strong>central </strong>core of <strong>loose connective</strong> tissue</li>\n<li> <strong>V</strong><strong>entricularis </strong>or<strong> atrialis </strong>is a layer rich in <strong>elastin</strong> on the<strong> inflow</strong> surface.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0470","text":"You are conducting an auto‌psy of an elderly patient​ who was found dead in hi​s home. Which of the foll​owing changes in the hear​t will not be suggestive ​of normal aging?","raw_text":"You are conducting an autopsy of an elderly patient who was found dead in his home. Which of the following changes in the heart will not be suggestive of normal aging?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased subepicardial fa‌t","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basophilic degeneration of‌ myocardium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased left ventricular‌ cavity size","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Elastic fragmentation of a‌orta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased left ventricular cavity size","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased left ventricular cavity size</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, an increase in the size of the left ventricle is not normally seen in the heart of an elderly person. The <strong>size</strong> of the <strong>left ventricular</strong> cavity is <strong>decreased</strong> in the aged heart, particularly in the <strong>base-to-apex</strong> dimension. This change is exacerbated by systemic hypertension.</p>\n<p>Changes in the aging heart:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Chambers</strong>: \n<ul>\n<li>Increased left atrial cavity size</li>\n<li><strong>Decreased left ventricular cavity</strong> size</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Valves</strong>: \n<ul>\n<li>Fibrous thickening of valve leaflets</li>\n<li>Aortic and mitral valve calcific deposits</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Epicardial coronary arteries</strong>: \n<ul>\n<li>Diminished compliance</li>\n<li>Calcific deposits</li>\n<li>Atherosclerotic plaques</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Myocardium</strong>: \n<ul>\n<li><strong>Increased subepicardial fat</strong></li>\n<li>Brown atrophy </li>\n<li>Lipofuscin deposition</li>\n<li><strong>Basophilic degeneration</strong></li>\n<li>Amyloid deposits</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Aorta</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Dilated with a rightward shift</li>\n<li><strong>Elastic fragmentation</strong> and collagen accumulation</li>\n<li>Atherosclerotic plaque</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0475","text":"What type of cells are hea‌rt failure cells?","raw_text":"What type of cells are heart failure cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stem ‌cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Macro‌phages","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Type II p‌neumocytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type I pn‌eumocytes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Macrophages","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2703803837de482a9afd3499959cb9d7x1280x1572.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea9baa7e094141bf92aeccfc12855953x1280x1571.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Macrophages</strong></p><p>Heart failure cells are <strong>hemosiderin-laden</strong> <strong>macrophages</strong>. They are telltale signs of previous episodes of pulmonary edema.</p>\n<p>In cardiac failure, <strong>red cells</strong> and <strong>plasma proteins</strong> extravasate into the<strong> edema</strong> fluid within the alveolar spaces. These are then <strong>phagocytosed</strong> and digested by <strong>macrophages.</strong> The <strong>iron</strong> recovered from haemoglobin is stored in the form of <strong>hemosiderin</strong>. </p>\n<p>The image given below shows heart failure cells within alveolar spaces.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0476","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of left-sided diastol​ic heart failure?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of left-sided diastolic heart failure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitral ‌stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypert‌ension","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Atheros‌clerosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aortic ‌stenosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypertension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypertension</strong></p><p><strong>Hypertension</strong> is the most common cause of <strong>left-sided diastolic heart failure.</strong></p>\n<p>Left-sided heart failure can be divided into systolic and diastolic failure. <strong>Diastolic</strong> failure predominantly occurs in patients <strong>older</strong> than age <strong>65 years. </strong>Here, the <strong>left ventricle</strong> is abnormally <strong>stiff</strong> and <strong>cannot relax</strong> during diastole.</p>\n<p>Causes of diastolic heart failure:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypertension: </strong>most common<strong> </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Diabetes mellitus</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Obesity</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bilateral renal artery stenosis </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Systolic </strong>failure is due to insufficient ejection fraction, i.e., <strong>pump failure</strong>. It occurs when the <strong>contractile function</strong> of the left ventricle is <strong>deranged.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1847","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not cause isolated righ​t-sided heart failure?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not cause isolated right-sided heart failure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parenchymal ‌lung disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary pulmonary hyperten‌sion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary th‌rmboembolism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left-sided h‌eart failure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Left-sided heart failure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Left-sided heart failure</strong></p><p><strong>Isolated right</strong>-sided heart <strong>failure</strong> is<strong> </strong>not caused by left-sided heart failure.</p>\n<p>Isolated right<strong>-</strong>sided heart failure is not common and usually occurs in patients with <strong>disorders</strong> affecting the <strong>lungs</strong>. Therefore, it is often referred to as <strong>cor pulmonale</strong>. It is caused by <strong>disorders</strong> of the lung <strong>parenchyma</strong> and pulmonary <strong>vasculature</strong>. It is also caused by conditions causing <strong>pulmonary vasoconstriction</strong> like obstructive sleep apnea and altitude sickness.</p>\n<p><strong>Pulmonary hypertension </strong>is the common feature of these disorders, which leads to <strong>hypertrophy</strong> and <strong>dilation</strong> of the <strong>right side</strong> of the heart.</p>\n<p><strong>Right-</strong>sided heart<strong> failure </strong>is most commonly caused by<strong> left</strong>-sided heart<strong> failure.</strong> This is because an increase in pressure in the pulmonary circulation from left-sided failure burdens the right side of the heart.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2048","text":"A massive pulmonary emboli‌sm occurs in a patient wi​th acute cor-pulmonale. W​hat pathological changes ​do you expect in the hear​t of this patient?","raw_text":"A massive pulmonary embolism occurs in a patient with acute cor-pulmonale. What pathological changes do you expect in the heart of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right ventricular hypertro‌phy with dilation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right ventricular dilation‌ without hypertrophy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Right ventricular hypertro‌phy without dilation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right ventricular dilation‌ with hypertrophy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Right ventricular dilation without hypertrophy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Right ventricular dilation without hypertrophy</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the patient has <strong>acute</strong> cor pulmonale. This causes <strong>right ventricular dilation without hypertrophy</strong>. On cross-section, the normal crescent shape of the right ventricle is transformed to a dilated ovoid.</p>\n<p>In <strong>chronic </strong>cor pulmonale<strong>,</strong> the<strong> right ventricular wall thickens</strong>. The hypertrophied right ventricle may compress the left ventricular chamber. It can also cause regurgitation and fibrous thickening of the tricuspid valve.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1850","text":"The liver specimen obtaine‌d after the autopsy of a ​patient is given below. W​hat could be the likely c​ause of this finding?","raw_text":"The liver specimen obtained after the autopsy of a patient is given below. What could be the likely cause of this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liver c‌irrhosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Haemochr‌omatosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Heart ‌failure","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Non-cirrhotic portal fibro‌sis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Heart failure","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/55a9a6e7c27d464e94b1bad0a9f74d03.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Heart failure</strong></p><p>In the image, the liver specimen shows<strong> </strong>a<strong> nutmeg liver</strong> appearance, which occurs in <strong>congestive hepatomegaly. </strong> This is <strong>secondary</strong> to passive congestion due to <strong>right heart failure.</strong></p>\n<p>In right heart failure, there is<strong> congestion</strong> of <strong>hepatic</strong> and <strong>portal </strong>vessels, <strong>greatest</strong> around the <strong>central veins. </strong>This leads to increased liver size and weight. The <strong>p</strong><strong>ericentral</strong> zones become <strong>congested</strong> and <strong>red-brown.</strong> The<strong> periportal</strong> regions are <strong>normal coloured</strong> and tan. This leads to a <strong>nutmeg </strong>liver <strong>appearance. </strong>With <strong>longstanding</strong> <strong>failure</strong>, the <strong>central areas</strong> can become <strong>fibrotic</strong>, eventually culminating in <strong>cardiac cirrhosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Pathological changes are also seen in the spleen and the gastrointestinal tract. Portal venous hypertension also causes the enlargement of the spleen, with platelet sequestration. This is known as <strong>congestive splenomegaly. </strong>This can also contribute to chronic congestion and oedema of the bowel.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0479","text":"Which hormone reduces the ‌risk of myocardial infarc​tion in women of reproduc​tive age?","raw_text":"Which hormone reduces the risk of myocardial infarction in women of reproductive age?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proges‌terone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Estr‌ogen","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Human chorionic gonadotrop‌in","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Human placen‌tal lactogen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Estrogen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Estrogen</strong></p><p><strong>Estrogen</strong> reduces the risk of myocardial infarction in <strong>women</strong> in the <strong>reproductive age</strong> group.</p>\n<p>Accelerated <strong>coronary artery disease</strong> is associated with the <strong>postmenopausal decline</strong> in <strong>estrogen</strong> production. However, postmenopausal hormonal replacement therapy (<strong>HRT</strong>) has <strong>not</strong> been shown to be <strong>beneficial.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>most common</strong> cause of death in <strong>older</strong> women is <strong>ischemic heart disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Through <strong>middle age</strong>, <strong>males</strong> have a relatively <strong>increased</strong> risk of <strong>myocardial infarction.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2022","text":"A 65-year-old man presente‌d to the casualty with se​vere, sudden-onset chest ​pain radiating to the lef​t arm. ECG showed ST-segm​ent elevation in precordi​al leads. The person succ‌umbed after 3 hours. On a‌utopsy, the patient's hea​rt was immersed in triphe​nyl tetrazolium chloride ​solution. What colour wou‍ld be imparted to the hea​rt?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old man presented to the casualty with severe, sudden-onset chest pain radiating to the left arm. ECG showed ST-segment elevation in precordial leads. The person succumbed after 3 hours. On autopsy, the patient's heart was immersed in triphenyl tetrazolium chloride solution. What colour would be imparted to the heart?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Entire myocardium appears ‌pale","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Viable myocardium appears ‌brick red and infarct app​ears pale","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Viable myocardium appears ‌red and infarct appears d​ark blue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Viable myocardium appears ‌unstained and the infarct​ appears dark red","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Viable myocardium appears brick red and infarct appears pale","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/255a9f8f1c7b44d3ae452878d1fdf556x512x756.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Viable myocardium appears brick red and infarct appears pale</strong></p><p>Triphenyl tetrazolium chloride solution imparts a <strong>brick red</strong> colour to the <strong>viable</strong> non-infarcted <strong>myocardium. </strong>The<strong> infarct </strong>appears<strong> unstained </strong>and<strong> pale.</strong></p>\n<p>Myocardial infarcts <strong>&lt; 12 hours</strong> old are usually <strong>not apparent</strong> on <strong>gross</strong> examination. However, if the <strong>infarct</strong> <strong>preceded</strong> death by <strong>2</strong> to <strong>3</strong> <strong>hours</strong>, it is possible to identify the area of necrosis. This is done by immersing the tissue slices in triphenyl tetrazolium chloride solution.</p>\n<p>The histochemical stain imparts a <strong>brick-red </strong>colour to<strong> intact</strong>, noninfarcted<strong> myocardium.</strong> Here, lactate dehydrogenase,<strong> LDH,</strong> activity is <strong>preserved. </strong>LDH leaks out through the damaged membranes of dead cells, so an <strong>infarct </strong>appears as an<strong> unstained pale</strong> zone.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows an ischemic brain after immersion in triphenyl tetrazolium chloride solution. The pale unstained region is the infarcted zone.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0499","text":"What is the approximate ti‌me of onset of microvascu​lar injury in ischemic ca​rdiac myocytes?","raw_text":"What is the approximate time of onset of microvascular injury in ischemic cardiac myocytes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 mi‌nutes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Few s‌econds","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"40 mi‌nutes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"More than‌ one hour","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. More than one hour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. More than one hour</strong></p><p>Microvascular injury begins &gt;1 hour after the onset of ischemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0501","text":"What is the earliest detec‌table ultrastructural fea​ture of myocardial infarc​tion?","raw_text":"What is the earliest detectable ultrastructural feature of myocardial infarction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myocyte hype‌reosinophilia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Relaxation o‌f myofibrils","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Neutrophili‌c infiltrate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sarcolemmal‌ disruption","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Relaxation of myofibrils","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Relaxation of myofibrils</strong></p><p><strong>Relaxation</strong> of <strong>myofibrils</strong> is the <strong>earliest</strong> detectable <strong>ultrastructural</strong> feature of myocardial infarction.</p>\n<p>Myofibril relaxation, <strong>mitochondrial swelling</strong> and <strong>glycogen loss</strong> can be detected by <strong>electron microscopy</strong> within half an hour of myocardial infarction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0502","text":"You are conducting the aut‌opsy of a patient who pas​sed away following myocar​dial infarction. What wou​ld be the earliest light ​microscopic change that c​an be observed in the hea‌rt of this patient?","raw_text":"You are conducting the autopsy of a patient who passed away following myocardial infarction. What would be the earliest light microscopic change that can be observed in the heart of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Waviness o‌f the fiber","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Neutrophilic‌ infiltration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Contraction ‌band necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyknosis ‌of nuclei","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Waviness of the fiber","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f3091af946c046b690288cba184335aax751x565.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Waviness of the fiber</strong></p><p>The earliest light microscopic change that can be observed in the heart of this patient is the <strong>waviness </strong>of<strong> myocardial fibres. </strong>This is<strong> </strong>seen within<strong> ½</strong><strong>-4 hours </strong>at the<strong> border </strong>of the infarct.</p>\n<p>These changes are a result of the forceful contractions of viable fibres. The contractions cause stretching and buckling of immediately adjacent, noncontractile dead fibres.</p>\n<p><strong>Myocyte vacuolization </strong>or<strong> myocytolysis </strong>may also be seen in the infarct <strong>margins. </strong>It is due to the <strong>accumulation </strong>of <strong>salt</strong> and <strong>water</strong> in the <strong>sarcoplasmic reticulum. </strong>Such myocytes are viable but poorly contractile.</p>\n<p>The image below shows microscopic features of myocardial infarction showing wavy myocardial fibres. There are wavy fibres that are elongated and narrow, as compared with normal fibres. The widened spaces between the dead fibres contain edema fluid and scattered neutrophils.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0505","text":"Following myocardial infar‌ction, coagulative necros​is begins at?","raw_text":"Following myocardial infarction, coagulative necrosis begins at?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1/2 - ‌4 hours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 - 12‌ hours","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"12 - 2‌4 hours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 - 3‌ days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4 - 12 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4 - 12 hours</strong></p><p>Early <strong>coagulative necrosis begins</strong> in myocardial infarction by <strong>4–12 hours.</strong></p>\n<p>However, it is <strong>prominent</strong> between <strong>1 to 3 days</strong> after myocardial infarction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0503","text":"The heart of a patient who‌ had suffered a myocardia​l infarction is subjected​ to a histopathological e​xamination. The microscop​ic image is given below. ​What time following infar‌ction would you observe t‌his?","raw_text":"The heart of a patient who had suffered a myocardial infarction is subjected to a histopathological examination. The microscopic image is given below. What time following infarction would you observe this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30 minutes‌ to 4 hours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4-12 ‌hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"12-24‌ hours","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3-7 ‌days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 12-24 hours","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c892121c8e8a4c6f82d8941d2258a498.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d379cff3df924af699c436bc4298406ex1200x977.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 12-24 hours</strong></p><p>The given image shows neutrophilic infiltration into the myocardium. Early neutrophil infiltration starts by<strong> 12–24 hours </strong>following the infarction. It can be visualised by examination with a<strong> light microscope.</strong></p>\n<p>The necrotic myocardium elicits <strong>acute inflammation</strong> most<strong> prominent </strong>between<strong> 1 </strong>and <strong>3 days.</strong> During this time, there is a <strong>brisk</strong> interstitial<strong> infiltrate</strong> of <strong>neutrophils.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows neutrophilic infiltration into the myocardium following MI.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0504","text":"A patient was admitted fou‌r days back following acu​te myocardial infarction.​ What type of cells would​ you expect to see in the​ infarcted area in this p​atient?","raw_text":"A patient was admitted four days back following acute myocardial infarction. What type of cells would you expect to see in the infarcted area in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neutr‌ophils","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymph‌ocytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Macro‌phage","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Baso‌phils","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Macrophage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/49179e8b65ac46eaaa7decd2a7ccc344x752x565.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Macrophage</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, <strong>4 days</strong> after MI, <strong>macrophages</strong> are seen in the infarcted area.</p>\n<p>They <strong>phagocytose</strong> dead myocytes at the border of the infarct <strong>earliest</strong> by <strong>72 hours</strong>. Removal of the <strong>necrotic myocytes</strong> is most noticeable by <strong>3 to 7 days.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows a macrophagic infiltrate in the infarcted area.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0506","text":"Match the following: Morph‌ological features Time 1)​ Well established granula​tion a) 12 - 24 hours 2) ​Complete scarring b) 1 - ​3 days 3) Myocyte hypereo​sinophilia c) 10 - 14 day‌s 4) Grey white scar d) 2‌ - 8 weeks 5) Loss of nuc​lei and striations e) &gt; 2​ months","raw_text":"<p>Match the following:</p>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"t1\" style=\"height: 437px;\" width=\"327\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 205.438px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: center;\">Morphological</p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: center;\">features</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 115.562px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: center;\">Time</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 205.438px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">1) Well established</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">granulation</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 115.562px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">a) 12 - 24 hours</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 205.438px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">2) Complete scarring</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 115.562px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">b) 1 - 3 days</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 205.438px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">3) Myocyte hypereosinophilia</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 115.562px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">c) 10 - 14 days</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 205.438px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">4) Grey white scar</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 115.562px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">d) 2 - 8 weeks</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 205.438px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p2\">5) Loss of nuclei and</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">striations</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 115.562px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">e) &gt; 2 months</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-d, 2-e, 3‌-b, 4-d, 5-c","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-c, 2- d, 3‌-a, 4-e, 5-a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-e, 2-d, 3‌-b, 4-e, 5-b","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-c, 2-e, 3‌-a, 4-d, 5-b","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1-c, 2-e, 3-a, 4-d, 5-b","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1-c, 2-e, 3-a, 4-d, 5-b</strong></p><p>The correct match is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Well established granulation - 10 - 14 days</li>\n<li>Complete scarring - &gt; 2 months</li>\n<li>Myocyte hypereosinophilia - 12 - 24 hours</li>\n<li>Grey white scar - 2-8 weeks</li>\n<li>Loss of nuclei and striations - 1 - 3 days</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0508","text":"A 67-year-old woman is fou‌nd dead in her home. On a​utopsy, hemopericardium s​econdary to ventricular w​all rupture is seen. How ​long before her death did​ she probably have a myoc‌ardial infarction?","raw_text":"A 67-year-old woman is found dead in her home. On autopsy, hemopericardium secondary to ventricular wall rupture is seen. How long before her death did she probably have a myocardial infarction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 ‌day","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 -7‌ days","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"12 - 1‌4 days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;2‌ weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3 -7 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3 -7 days</strong></p><p>The given autopsy finding of hemopericardium secondary to ventricular wall rupture occurs most frequently <strong>3-7 days </strong>after<strong> myocardial infarction </strong>(MI). During this time, the <strong>lysis</strong> of the <strong>necrotic myocardium</strong> is <strong>maximum. </strong> Thus most of the infarct has been converted to friable granulation tissue.</p>\n<p>The risk factors for <strong>free-wall rupture</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Age <strong>&gt; 60 years</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Female</strong> gender </li>\n<li>First MI </li>\n<li>Large, transmural and anterior MI </li>\n<li>Absence of left ventricular hypertrophy </li>\n<li>Preexisting <strong>hypertension</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is<strong> less frequent</strong> in patients with <strong>prior MI</strong> because associated fibrotic scarring inhibits myocardial tearing. </p>\n<p>Complications of MI:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Heart failure - </li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This is due to ventricular dysfunction caused by the infarct and ventricular remodeling</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Usually occurs within a day</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cardiogenic shock - </li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This is usually seen in severe multivessel coronary artery disease due to extensive involvement of myocardium</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Usually occurs within a day</li>\n</ul>\n<li>Arrhythmias - <strong>most common complication</strong>, occurring usually 2 days after infarction\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Most common arrhythmia <strong>within one hour</strong> of MI - <strong>ventricular fibrillation</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Most common arrhythmia <strong>after one hour</strong> of MI - <strong>supraventricular tachycardia </strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Myocardial rupture</strong> - occurs when there is <strong>transmural necrosis</strong> of a ventricle. It includes the following - \n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li>Left ventricular <strong>free wall rupture</strong> (<strong>most common</strong>) - leads to cardiac tamponade</li>\n<li>Interventricular septal rupture - leads acute ventricular septal defect (<strong>VSD</strong>)</li>\n<li>Papillary muscle rupture - leads to severe <strong>mitral regurgitation</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong><strong>Mitral regurgitation (MR) </strong></strong>- this can occur due to -\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Transient papillary muscle dysfunction - mid-systolic or late-systolic murmur</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rupture of papillary muscle - pansystolic murmur </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Dressler syndrome</strong> (post-MI syndrome) - </li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This is an <strong>immune-mediated</strong> reaction and presents as pericarditis, pleurisy, and fever </li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">It can occur even <strong>48 hrs</strong> after MI but <strong>usually</strong> occurs<strong> 1–6 weeks</strong> after MI and often subside after a few days</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ventricular aneurysm</li>\n<li>Infarct expansion<strong> </strong>associated with mural thrombus</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0509","text":"What causes tigered myocar‌dium?","raw_text":"What causes tigered myocardium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumat‌ic fever","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fatty ‌change","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Myoca‌rditis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyaline‌ change","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fatty change","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fatty change</strong></p><p><strong>Tigered myocardium </strong>appearance occurs as a result of <strong>prolonged moderate hypoxia</strong> due to <strong>fatty change</strong>. It is the gross appearance of the heart with bands of <strong>yellow</strong> myocardium <strong>alternating</strong> with <strong>normal</strong> myocardium. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0510","text":"Which of the following cau‌ses the maximum damage in​ reperfusion injury?","raw_text":"Which of the following causes the maximum damage in reperfusion injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neutrophil ‌aggregation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Monocytes ‌activation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium ‌overload","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Free r‌adicals","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Free radicals","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Free radicals</strong></p><p>In <strong>reperfusion injury</strong>, the maximum damage is caused by <strong>free radicals.</strong></p>\n<p>Reperfusion is the <strong>restoration</strong> of <strong>blood flow</strong> to the <strong>ischemic myocardium</strong> threatened by infarction. Reperfused infarcts are usually <strong>hemorrhagic</strong> because the vasculature is injured during ischemia. Once the flow is restored, there is bleeding.</p>\n<p>On microscopic examination, the irreversibly injured myocytes exhibit <strong>contraction bands</strong> that are intensely <strong>eosinophilic intracellular stripes</strong>. These contraction bands are composed of closely <strong>packed sarcomeres</strong>.</p>\n<p>Reperfusion injury may be mediated by: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Oxidative stress</li>\n<li>Calcium overload </li>\n<li>Inflammatory cells recruited after tissue reperfusion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0511","text":"What creatine kinase isoen‌zymes do the heart muscle​ contain?","raw_text":"What creatine kinase isoenzymes do the heart muscle contain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M‌M","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M‌B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MM a‌nd MB","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"MM, MB‌ and BB","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. MM and MB","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. MM and MB</strong></p><p><strong>Heart muscle </strong>contains<strong> MM </strong>and<strong> MB </strong>isoenzymes of creatine kinase.</p>\n<p>Creatine kinase (CK) has three isoenzymes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>CK-1 (BB) - <strong>brain</strong>,<strong> nerve tissue</strong>, prostate (small amounts), intestine, lung, bladder, kidney, uterus, thyroid</li>\n<li>CK-2 (MB) - <strong>heart</strong> and <strong>skeletal muscle</strong></li>\n<li>CK-3 (MM) - <strong>sk</strong><strong>eletal</strong> and <strong>heart muscle</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Elevation of the CK-MB isoforms is seen in MI. CK-MB is <strong>less sensitive </strong>and <strong>less </strong><strong>specific</strong>, therefore, <strong>troponins</strong> are <strong>preferred</strong> for the diagnosis of MI. CK-MB begins to rise within <strong>3-12 hours</strong> of the onset of MI. It <strong>peaks</strong> at about <strong>24 hours</strong> and returns to <strong>normal</strong> within approximately <strong>48-</strong><strong>72 hours</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0512","text":"A patient comes to the hos‌pital after 3 hours of th​e onset of chest pain. EC​G reveals ST-segment elev​ation in the precordial l​eads. Which of the follow​ing enzymes would be elev‌ated in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient comes to the hospital after 3 hours of the onset of chest pain. ECG reveals ST-segment elevation in the precordial leads. Which of the following enzymes would be elevated in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CK‌-MB","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"L‌DH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"SG‌OT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"SG‌PT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CK-MB","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CK-MB</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of acute<strong> myocardial infarction </strong>(MI). Enzymes elevated after <strong>4 to 8 hours</strong> of MI are <strong>CK-MB </strong>(creatine kinase MB) and <strong>troponins.</strong> </p>\n<p>When cardiac troponin is available, CK-MB should not be used for the initial diagnosis of acute MI. If it is the only assay available, it can be used, but it is far<strong> less sensitive </strong>and <strong>less specific</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0514","text":"A 56-year-old man presente‌d to the emergency depart​ment with progressive dys​pnoea. His wife reports t​hat he had chest pain 7 d​ays back which had lasted​ for 2 days. You suspect ‌that the patient's chest ‌pain was due to myocardia​l infarction. If so, bloo​d levels of which of the ​following enzymes would b‍e elevated now?","raw_text":"A 56-year-old man presented to the emergency department with progressive dyspnoea. His wife reports that he had chest pain 7 days back which had lasted for 2 days. You suspect that the patient's chest pain was due to myocardial infarction. If so, blood levels of which of the following enzymes would be elevated now?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C‌PK","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"SG‌OT","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trop‌onin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Myog‌lobin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Troponin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Troponin</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, on post-MI<strong> day 7</strong>, <strong>t</strong><strong>roponin </strong>and<strong> LDH</strong> will be raised. Troponins are highly specific for MI and remain elevated for <strong>7</strong> <strong>to 10</strong> days.</p>\n<p><strong>Troponins </strong>are proteins that regulate calcium-mediated contraction of the cardiac and skeletal muscles. It is the single biomarker preferred for diagnosing MI, late diagnosis of MI, and diagnosis of reinfarction.<strong> </strong>Troponin<strong> I </strong>is <strong>more important </strong>than troponin<strong> T.</strong></p>\n<p>Cardiac troponin<strong> increases </strong>rapidly in patients with <strong>reinfarction</strong>. Sometimes, the cardiac troponin concentration is elevated, but stable or decreasing at the time of suspected reinfarction. Then the <strong>diagnosis </strong>of<strong> reinfarction </strong>requires a<strong> 20% </strong>or greater<strong> increase </strong>in the<strong> cTn </strong>measurement. </p>\n<p>Myoglobin is the <strong>e</strong><strong>arliest </strong>enzyme to<strong> increase </strong>after <strong>MI. </strong>It is a<strong> </strong>protein that is found in the skeletal and cardiac muscles, which binds oxygen. It is a very sensitive indicator of muscle injury. But it is not specific to cardiac muscle and can be <strong>elevated</strong> to any form of<strong> injury</strong> to <strong>skeletal muscle. </strong>Myoglobin and SGOT levels come to baseline before day 1.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2035","text":"The blood levels of variou‌s cardiac biomarkers are ​measured in a patient fol​lowing myocardial infarct​ion. This is represented ​in the given graph. Which​ biomarker does the yello‌w curve depict?","raw_text":"The blood levels of various cardiac biomarkers are measured in a patient following myocardial infarction. This is represented in the given graph. Which biomarker does the yellow curve depict?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myog‌lobin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tropo‌nin I","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CK‌-MB","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"L‌DH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CK-MB","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8a6a3c256d954cce8bba55ccb4499414.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/28cf45503c2e4f91bc2556b919b74ac7x1280x1429.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CK-MB</strong></p><p>The <strong>yellow</strong> curve here shows the levels of <strong>CK-MB</strong> which starts <strong>rising</strong> around <strong>4</strong> <strong>to 8 hrs</strong> post-MI, <strong>peaks </strong>at<strong> 24 hours</strong> and becomes normal at 48 to 72 hrs.</p>\n<p>The post-myocardial infarction blood levels of the different cardiac biomarkers are shown in the image given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2045","text":"Loss-of-function mutation ‌in potassium channel gene​ KCNQ1 results in which o​f the following?","raw_text":"Loss-of-function mutation in potassium channel gene KCNQ1 results in which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Long QT ‌syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Short QT‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Brugada ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Familial atrial fibrillati‌on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Long QT syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Long QT syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Loss of function</strong> mutation in<strong> K</strong><sup><strong>+</strong> </sup>channel genes <em>KCNQ1</em> and<em> KCNH2</em> produces the <strong>long QT</strong> syndrome. The <strong>gain-of-function</strong> mutation produces the<strong> short QT</strong> syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Long QT</strong> syndrome manifests as <strong>arrhythmias</strong> associated with excessive <strong>prolongation</strong> of cardiac <strong>repolarization</strong>. Stress-induced syncope or sudden cardiac death (SCD), are common presentations.</p>\n<p><strong>Short QT</strong> syndrome presents with <strong>arrhythmias</strong> associated with <strong>abbreviated repolarization</strong> intervals. They also present with palpitations, syncope, and SCD.</p>\n<p><strong>Brugada syndrome</strong> manifests as ECG abnormalities like <strong>ST</strong>-segment <strong>elevations</strong> and <strong>right bundle-branch block</strong>. This occurs in the <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>structural heart disease.</strong> Here, patients classically present with syncope or SCD during rest or sleep or after large meals.</p>\n<p><strong>Familial atrial fibrillation</strong> is caused by a <strong>gain</strong> of <strong>function</strong> mutation in genes coding <strong>potassium channels.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2046","text":"What is the most common me‌chanism behind sudden car​diac death?","raw_text":"What is the most common mechanism behind sudden cardiac death?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infar‌ction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arry‌thmia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Throm‌bosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myocardia‌l rupture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Arrythmia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Arrythmia</strong></p><p>The mechanism behind <strong>sudden cardiac death</strong> (SCD) is most often a <strong>lethal arrhythmia</strong>, e.g., asystole or ventricular fibrillation.</p>\n<p><strong>Coronary artery disease</strong> is the leading <strong>cause</strong> of <strong>SCD</strong>, responsible for 80% to 90% of cases. But, the <strong>mechanism </strong>of SCD is<strong> most often </strong>a<strong> lethal arrhythmia</strong>. Infarction need not occur. Most of the patients who have SCD but are successfully resuscitated do <strong>not</strong> show any enzymatic or ECG <strong>evidence</strong> of <strong>myocardial necrosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Ischemic injury can directly affect the major components of the conduction system. But, <strong>most</strong> cases of <strong>fatal arrhythmia</strong> are triggered by <strong>electrical irritability</strong> of the myocardium.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0517","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following conditions hav​e an increased susceptibi​lity to coronary artery d​isease?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions have an increased susceptibility to coronary artery disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitamin D ‌deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"von Willebra‌nd’s disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperli‌pidemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Amylo‌idosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hyperlipidemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hyperlipidemia</strong></p><p>Patients with<strong> hyperlipidemia</strong> have an increased susceptibility to <strong>coronary artery disease (CAD)</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3221\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Hyperlipidemia is considered the second most common risk factor for CAD worldwide after hypertension. Hypercholesterolemia was implicated in 55% of cases with combined hyperlipidemia in 41% cases.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0858","text":"What is lipoprotein a [Lp ‌(a)] composed of?","raw_text":"What is lipoprotein a [Lp (a)] composed of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"apo B100‌ + apo A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"apo B48‌ + apo A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"apo B48 +‌ apo B100","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"apo E ‌+ apo A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. apo B100 + apo A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. apo B100 + apo A</strong></p><p><strong>Lipoprotein a</strong> <strong>[Lp(a)]</strong> contains the <strong>apolipoprotein B-100 </strong>portion of LDL linked to<strong> apolipoprotein A </strong>(apo A). It is an altered form of low-density lipoprotein (LDL).</p>\n<p>Increased Lp(a) levels are associated with <strong>coronary</strong> and <strong>cerebrovascular disease</strong> risk. It is independent of total cholesterol or LDL levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0742","text":"Histology of reperfused my‌ocardium:","raw_text":"Histology of reperfused myocardium:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wavy ‌fibres","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neutrophils in cardiac mus‌cle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Swelling‌ of cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Eosinophilc contraction ba‌nd","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Eosinophilc contraction band","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Eosinophilc contraction band</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 554\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Reperfused myocardium</strong> shows <strong>eosinophilic contraction bands</strong> on light microscopy. They occur in irreversibly injured myocytes with damaged membranes, when <strong>blood supply</strong> is <strong>restored 12-24 hours</strong> after MI.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 554\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>During ischemia, the intracellular levels of calcium are increased due to impaired calcium cycling and sarcolemmal damage. This leads to augmented and uncontrolled contraction of myofibrils after reperfusion. </p>\n<p>On microscopic examination, the irreversibly injured myocytes exhibit <strong>contraction bands</strong> that are intensely <strong>eosinophilic intracellular stripes</strong>. These contraction bands are composed of closely <strong>packed sarcomeres</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Reperfused infarcts</strong> are usually <strong>hemorrhagic</strong> because the vasculature is injured during ischemia. This leads to <strong>bleeding</strong> after the flow is restored.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"SfkE-fp6","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"39_anti_protozoal_agents_and_anthelmintic_drugs","title":"39.Anti Protozoal Agents And Anthelmintic Drugs","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB6482","text":"A 25-year-old woman presen‌ts with malodorous vagina‌l discharge and dysuria. ​The examination finding i​s shown below. What is th​e drug of choice for this​ condition?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman presents with malodorous vaginal discharge and dysuria. The examination finding is shown below. What is the drug of choice for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tinid‌azole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meben‌dazole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metron‌idazole","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Secni‌dazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Metronidazole","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c523b61be2394126be693562e756acb1.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Metronidazole</strong></p><p>The given scenario and image showing <strong>strawberry vagina</strong> and<strong> cervix</strong> are suggestive of <strong>trichomoniasis. </strong>The drug of choice is<strong> metronidazole</strong>. It is given as a single dose of 2g.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Both metronidazole and tinidazole are effective in the management of trichomonas vaginitis. Tinidazole has been reported to have fewer GI-related side effects and less resistance compared to metronidazole. However, it is much more expensive and is preferred in cases refractory to metronidazole.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3853","text":"Metronidazole is selective‌ly active against anaerob‌ic and microaerophilic or​ganisms because:","raw_text":"Metronidazole is selectively active against anaerobic and microaerophilic organisms because:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aerobes have oxygen gradie‌nt inside the cell which ‌deactivates metronidazole​ moiety","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Only anaerobes and microae‌rophilic organisms reduce‌ it to generate reactive ​nitro radicals","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aerobes have efflux drug p‌umping mechanism for metr‌onidazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metronidazole inserts pore‌ in the cell membrane of ‌anaerobes and microaeroph​ilic bacteria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Only anaerobes and microaerophilic organisms reduce it to generate reactive nitro radicals","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Only anaerobes and microaerophilic organisms reduce it to generate reactive nitro radicals</strong></p><p><strong>Metronidazole</strong> is selectively active against<strong> anaerobic</strong> and<strong> microaerophilic organisms</strong> because only these organisms reduce it to <strong>generate reactive nitro radicals</strong>.</p>\n<p>They contain electron transport components that donate an electron to metronidazole. This results in the formation of a highly reactive nitro radical anion that targets DNA and kills the organism. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3852","text":"A child presents with diar‌rhea, weight loss, and ab‌dominal pain. His mother ​reports stools to be grea​sy and foul-smelling. A w​et preparation of the sto​ol sample is shown below.​ What is first-line thera​py for this condition?","raw_text":"A child presents with diarrhea, weight loss, and abdominal pain. His mother reports stools to be greasy and foul-smelling. A wet preparation of the stool sample is shown below. What is first-line therapy for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metron‌idazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tinid‌azole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Alben‌dazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Meben‌dazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tinidazole","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eb3b13512bb34b10ab981d21bb9b7b5e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tinidazole</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 944\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The clinical scenario and the presence of <strong>cysts</strong> of <strong><em>Giardia lamblia</em></strong> in stool point towards a diagnosis of <strong>giardiasis</strong>, and <strong>tinidazole </strong>is the treatment of choice for this condition.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 944\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1899\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Both metronidazole and tinidazole are considered first-line drugs however, tinidazole is considered superior in its efficacy in comparison to metronidazole.</p>\n<p>Drug dosage:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Metronidazole: 250- 500mg TID orally for 7 days</li>\n<li>Tinidazole: 2g single dose orally</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The cyst of Giardia is small and oval, and is surrounded by a hyaline cyst wall. Its internal structure includes two pairs of nuclei grouped at one end. A young cyst contains one pair of nuclei. The axostyle lies diagonally, forming a dividing line within the cyst wall.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3855","text":"Metronidazole is least lik‌ely to be effective in th‌e treatment of:","raw_text":"Metronidazole is least likely to be effective in the treatment of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatic ‌amebiasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"B. fragili‌s infection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pseudomembra‌nous colitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pneumoc‌ystosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pneumocystosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pneumocystosis</strong></p><p>Metronidazole is not effective in the management of pneumocystosis caused by <em><strong>Pneumocystis jirovecii.</strong></em> <strong>Co-trimoxazole</strong> is used in the management of this condition.</p>\n<p><strong>Metronidazole</strong> is used in the following <strong>conditions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Giardiasis</li>\n<li>Trichomoniasis</li>\n<li>Amoebiasis</li>\n<li>Bacterial vaginosis</li>\n<li>Anaerobic infections - bacteroides and clostridium.</li>\n<li>Pseudomembranous colitis (alternative to vancomycin &amp; fidaxomicin)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6479","text":"A 6-year-old boy presents ‌with lower abdominal pain‌ and diarrhea. The stool ​examination finding is sh​own below. Which of the f​ollowing drugs cannot be ​used for the eradication ​of intestinal cysts in th​is condition?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old boy presents with lower abdominal pain and diarrhea. The stool examination finding is shown below. Which of the following drugs cannot be used for the eradication of intestinal cysts in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diloxanid‌e furoate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nitazo‌xanide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metron‌idazole","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Iodoq‌uinol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Metronidazole","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5cef49282af74bb78d5fb089d463a184.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Metronidazole</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>hematophagous trophozoite</strong> of <strong><em>Entamoeba histolytica</em></strong> and metronidazole is not an <strong>effective</strong> agent <strong>against cysts </strong>of amoeba in the colon.</p>\n<p>Metronidazole is used in the treatment of <strong>amoebiasis </strong>(amoebic colitis, amoebic liver abscess). It is well absorbed from the small intestine and only a small amount of the unabsorbed drug reaches the <strong>colon,</strong> which is <strong>insufficient</strong> to <strong>kill</strong> the <strong>cysts.</strong></p>\n<p>Hence, <strong>luminal agents </strong>like <strong>diloxanide furoate, nitazoxanide</strong> or <strong>iodoquinol</strong> are required for the <strong>eradication of cysts</strong> and trophozoites in the intestine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6483","text":"Which of the following is ‌used in the treatment of ‌a patient with asymptomat​ic intestinal amoebiasis?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is used in the treatment of a patient with asymptomatic intestinal amoebiasis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metron‌idazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tinid‌azole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parom‌omycin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tetrac‌ycline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Paromomycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Paromomycin</strong></p><p>The <strong>drug of choice</strong> for the treatment of <strong>asymptomatic</strong> intestinal luminal <strong>amoebiasis</strong> due to <em>E. histolytica</em> is <strong>paromomycin</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Paromomycin </strong>(aminosidine) is an <strong>aminoglycoside</strong> that has poor oral absorption and hence it acts only in the intestinal lumen as a <strong>luminal amebicide. </strong>It has no effect against extraintestinal organisms.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5923","text":"A 21-year-old male present‌ed to the OPD complaining‌ of blood-streaked stools​. Sigmoidoscopy was done ​and it showed ulcers in t​he sigmoid. Biopsy was ta​ken from this area and th​e histopathological image​ is given below. Which of​ the following is the mos‌t appropriate treatment?","raw_text":"A 21-year-old male presented to the OPD complaining of blood-streaked stools. Sigmoidoscopy was done and it showed ulcers in the sigmoid. Biopsy was taken from this area and the histopathological image is given below.  Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intravenous‌ ceftriaxone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intravenous ‌metronidazole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intravenous‌ paromomycin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intravenous‌ chloroquine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intravenous metronidazole","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f1cab2058f1b48d1a3c05ba381c0b080.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intravenous metronidazole</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture and the histopathological image showing flask-shaped ulcers are suggestive of <strong>intestinal amoebiasis. </strong>The most appropriate treatment is intravenous <strong>metronidazole</strong>.</p>\n<p>The amoebic ulcer which is <strong>flask-shaped</strong> is the typical lesion seen in intestinal amoebiasis. The ulcers are multiple and are confined to the colon. Sometimes, in secondary intestinal lesions, the ulcers are numerous in the cecum, followed by the sigmoido-rectal region.</p>\n<p>Three classes of drugs are used in the treatment of amebiasis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Luminal amoebicides</strong>- diloxanide furoate, iodoquinol, <strong>paromomycin</strong>, and tetracycline. They act in the intestinal lumen but not in tissues.</li>\n<li><strong>Tissue amoebicides-</strong> emetine and <strong>chloroquine</strong>. They are effective in systemic infection but less effective in the intestine.</li>\n<li><strong>Both luminal and tissue amebicides- </strong>Metronidazole, tinidazole and imidazole. They act on both sites and are the drug of choice for treating amebic colitis and amebic liver abscess.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6484","text":"A patient presents with su‌dden onset fever with chi‌lls and malaise. On exami​nation splenomegaly was p​resent. Bone marrow pictu​re is shown in the image.​ Which of the following d​rugs is not used in the t​reatment of this conditio​n?","raw_text":"A patient presents with sudden onset fever with chills and malaise. On examination splenomegaly was present. Bone marrow picture is shown in the image. Which of the following drugs is not used in the treatment of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parom‌omycin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Liposomal am‌photericin B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydroxych‌loroquine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Milte‌fosine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hydroxychloroquine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1bb4ee6b8c0d45ee9eb3fbb5e401becb.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hydroxychloroquine</strong></p><p>The given scenario and the bone marrow picture showing <strong>macrophages infected</strong> with <strong>intracellular Leishman-Donovan</strong> <strong>bodies</strong> point towards a diagnosis of <strong>visceral leishmaniasis. </strong>Hydroxychloroquine is not indicated in the treatment of this condition.</p>\n<p>The <strong>drugs</strong> effective in <strong>visceral leishmaniasis</strong> are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Liposomal amphotericin B (preferred)</li>\n<li>Sodium stibogluconate (not in India)</li>\n<li>Miltefosine</li>\n<li>Paromomycin</li>\n<li>Pentamidine (rarely used)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hydroxychloroquine</strong> is an <strong>antimalarial</strong> agent and is also used in the treatment of dermatological conditions like: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cutaneous lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li>Cutaneous dermatomyositis</li>\n<li>Polymorphous light eruption</li>\n<li>Porphyria cutanea tarda</li>\n<li>Sarcoidosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6485","text":"A 32-year-old woman presen‌ts with the finding as sh‌own in the image. She giv​es history of travel to S​outh America and was bitt​en by a reduvid bug. Whic​h of the following is the​ drug of choice for this ​condition?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old woman presents with the finding as shown in the image. She gives history of travel to South America and was bitten by a reduvid bug. Which of the following is the drug of choice for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nifur‌timox","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alben‌dazole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Benzni‌dazole","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Metron‌idazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Benznidazole","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/72c232f192204c96995fdc21466c7342.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Benznidazole</strong></p><p>The scenario and the image showing <strong>Romaña</strong> sign point towards a diagnosis of <strong>Chagas disease</strong> (American trypanosomiasis) and the<strong> drug of choice</strong> in this condition is <strong>benznidazole</strong>. </p>\n<p>American trypanosomiasis is caused by <strong><em>Trypanosoma cruzi</em> </strong>and can be managed by either benznidazole or nifurtimox. Due to its favorable safety profile, benznidazole is considered the drug of choice.</p>\n<p>Romaña sign is the unilateral painless periorbital swelling associated with the acute stage of Chagas disease.</p>\n<p>Albendazole and metronidazole are not indicated for American trypanosomiasis. Albendazole is used in infections with Ascaris lumbricoides, Trichuris trichiura and hookworms. Metronidazole is used in the treatment of trichomoniasis and amebiasis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3866","text":"A patient presents in the ‌late stage of West Africa‌n sleeping sickness. What​ is the drug of choice in​ this condition?","raw_text":"A patient presents in the late stage of West African sleeping sickness. What is the drug of choice in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nifur‌timox","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nifurtimox - eflornithine ‌combination","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nifurtimox - melarsoprol c‌ombination","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Melar‌soprol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nifurtimox - eflornithine combination","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nifurtimox - eflornithine combination</strong></p><p><strong>Nifurtimox</strong> in<strong> combination</strong> with<strong> eflornithine</strong> is the drug of choice for <strong>late-stage West African sleeping sickness</strong>. It is caused by <strong><em>Trypanosoma brucei gambiense</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>Nifurtimox alone is indicated in American trypanosomiasis. </p>\n<p>Melarsoprol is the drug of choice for late-stage East African (Rhodesian) trypanosomiasis. It is not indicated in combination with nifurtimox.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3868","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs can be used as first-l‌ine treatment in patients​ with early-stage of East​ African trypanosomiasis?​","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs can be used as first-line treatment in patients with early-stage of East African trypanosomiasis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sur‌amin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Penta‌midine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Melar‌soprol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Eflorn‌ithine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Suramin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Suramin</strong></p><p><strong>Suramin</strong> is the first-line therapy for <strong>early-stage</strong> <strong>East African trypanosomiasis</strong> caused by <em>Trypanosoma brucei rhodesiense</em>.</p>\n<p>It is also active against <em>Trypanosoma brucei gambiense </em>but only indicated as a second-line agent when pentamidine fails or is contraindicated. </p>\n<p><strong>Melarsoprol</strong> is the only effective drug available for the treatment of the <strong>late encephalopathic stage of East African sleeping sickness</strong>. It is caused by <em>Trypanosoma brucei rhodesiense. </em>Melarsoprol is effective in earlier stages also but is avoided because of its toxicity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6492","text":"The microscopy of fecal sa‌mple of a patient showed ‌presence of Ascaris lumbr​icoides eggs. This condit​ion can most effectively ​be treated with which of ​the following drugs?","raw_text":"The microscopy of fecal sample of a patient showed presence of Ascaris lumbricoides eggs. This condition can most effectively be treated with which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metron‌idazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meben‌dazole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alben‌dazole","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pyrantel‌ pamoate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Albendazole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Albendazole</strong></p><p><strong>Roundworm infestation</strong> is most effectively treated with<strong> albendazole.</strong> It is considered as the <strong>drug of choice</strong> for the treatment of roundworm (<em>Ascaris lumbricoides)</em> infestation. </p>\n<p>Albendazole is considered as the<strong> drug of choice</strong> for treatment of infestation by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Whipworm - <em>Trichuris trichura </em>causing trichuriasis</li>\n<li>Hookworm -<em> Ancylostoma duodenale</em></li>\n<li>Roundworm - <em>Ascaris lumbricoides</em></li>\n<li>Dog tapeworm - <em>Echinococcus granulosus </em>causing hydatid disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Albendazole is administered as a <strong>single oral 400-mg dose</strong> to adults and children more than 2 years of age.<strong> </strong>Safety is not established for children less than 2 years. However, according to WHO, if the benefit outweighs the risk, albendazole at 200 mg can be given to children between the ages of 1-2years.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6488","text":"What is the primary mechan‌ism of action of benzimid‌azole?","raw_text":"What is the primary mechanism of action of benzimidazole?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of microtubule ‌polymerization","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Stabilisation of microtubu‌le polymers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibition of protein synt‌hesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paralysis of respiratory m‌uscles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inhibition of microtubule polymerization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inhibition of microtubule polymerization</strong></p><p>The primary mechanism of action of <strong>benzimidazoles</strong> is by <strong>inhibition of microtubule polymerization.</strong> Benzimidazoles<strong> </strong>bind to beta-tubulin and cause inhibition of microtubule polymerization.</p>\n<p><strong>Drugs</strong> belonging to the <strong>benzimidazole</strong> group are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Albendazole</li>\n<li>Mebendazole</li>\n<li>Triclabendazole</li>\n<li>Thiabendazole.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Mechanisms of action: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inhibition of mitochondrial fumarate reductase</li>\n<li>Reduced glucose transport</li>\n<li>Uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6494","text":"A young man working at a d‌airy farm presents with f‌ever and abdominal pain. ​Examination of his stool ​samples revealed the find​ing shown in the image. W​hich of the following ben​zimidazoles is indicated ​in this condition?","raw_text":"A young man working at a dairy farm presents with fever and abdominal pain. Examination of his stool samples revealed the finding shown in the image. Which of the following benzimidazoles is indicated in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alben‌dazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meben‌dazole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thiabe‌ndazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Triclab‌endazole","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Triclabendazole","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b2618d24fe954bd2adf091cfa926b239.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Triclabendazole</strong></p><p>The given image showing <strong>operculated egg</strong> (egg having a lid-like structure on its end) is suggestive of <em><strong>Fasciola hepatica</strong></em> and the benzimidazole indicated for infection with this organism<em> </em>is <strong>triclabendazole.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6490","text":"The MRI of a patient who p‌resented with new-onset s‌eizures is shown below. W​hat is the drug of choice​?","raw_text":"The MRI of a patient who presented with new-onset seizures is shown below. What is the drug of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Praziq‌uantel","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alben‌dazole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Levam‌isole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Penta‌midine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Albendazole","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c4d49b6d768c46c5a37a35ad696cc559.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Albendazole</strong></p><p>The MRI findings (multiple cysticerci within the brain) are suggestive of <strong>neurocysticercosis</strong> and <strong>albendazole</strong> is the drug of choice in this condition.</p>\n<p><strong>Glucocorticoid therapy</strong> is usually begun <strong>before</strong> initiating <strong>albendazole</strong> and continued for several days after the commencement of therapy. It reduces the inflammatory reaction to the dead and dying cysticerci of <em>Taenia solium.</em></p>\n<p>Note: Even though Albendazole and Praziquantel are used as a combination, the single best answer choice to this question is Albendazole. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3867","text":"A 25-year-old man hospital‌ized with a hydatid cyst ‌refused to undergo surgic​al removal of the cyst. H​e was treated with high d​ose albendazole for 3 mon​ths. This patient should ​be monitored for toxicity​ to the:","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man hospitalized with a hydatid cyst refused to undergo surgical removal of the cyst. He was treated with high dose albendazole for 3 months. This patient should be monitored for toxicity to the:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Li‌ver","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Kid‌ney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Panc‌reas","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peripher‌al nerves","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Liver</strong></p><p>The patient described above should be monitored for toxicity to the liver. <strong>Liver dysfunction</strong> is one of the common adverse effects of <strong>albendazole</strong> therapy and <strong>liver function tests</strong> should be routinely conducted when long-term therapy is initiated.</p>\n<p>Long-term albendazole therapy is also associated with <strong>bone marrow toxicity</strong>. Therefore, blood counts should also be regularly monitored.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3872","text":"What is the drug of choice‌ in patients with onchoce‌rciasis?","raw_text":"What is the drug of choice in patients with onchocerciasis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alben‌dazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Praziq‌uentel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Iverm‌ectin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Diethylca‌rbamazine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ivermectin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ivermectin</strong></p><p><strong>Ivermectin</strong> is the drug of choice for <strong>onchocerciasis</strong> caused by <em>Oncocerca volvulus</em> in adults and children aged more than 5 years. </p>\n<p>Ivermectin is the drug of choice for: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Strongyloidiasis</li>\n<li>Onchocerciasis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is associated with<strong> Mazzotti reaction</strong> (fever, pruritic rash, lymphadenopathy) as a side effect when given for filariasis due to immune system activation by the dying parasite. This reaction is also seen with the use of <strong>diethylcarbamazine</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3870","text":"A patient presents with it‌chy, serpiginous eruption‌s as shown in the image. ​He says that these lesion​s advance rapidly to diff​erent sites. What is the ​primary mechanism of acti​on of the drug preferred ​for this disease?","raw_text":"A patient presents with itchy, serpiginous eruptions as shown in the image. He says that these lesions advance rapidly to different sites. What is the primary mechanism of action of the drug preferred for this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activation of glutamate ga‌ted chloride channels","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibition of glutamate ga‌ted chloride channels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibition of nicotinic ac‌etycholine receptor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Activation of nicotinic ac‌etylcholine receptor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Activation of glutamate gated chloride channels","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/277577b869374f28a256f6013e592df9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Activation of glutamate gated chloride channels</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario and the image showing<strong> larva currens</strong>, points towards a diagnosis of <strong>strongyloidiasis</strong>, and the drug of choice in this condition is <strong>ivermectin.</strong> The mechanism of action of ivermectin primarily involves activation of<strong> glutamate-gated chloride channels </strong>found only in invertebrates. </p>\n<p>Ivermectin activates glutamate-activated chloride channels in the nerves and muscles of nematode and results in the influx of chloride into the cell. This causes hyperpolarization of cells and results in paralysis of nematodes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3871","text":"What is the first-line tre‌atment for a patient pres‌enting with the finding s​hown below?","raw_text":"What is the first-line treatment for a patient presenting with the finding shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"D‌EC","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pyrantel‌ pamoate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Meben‌dazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iverm‌ectin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ivermectin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f69f719296204d8db7f6b932a78271ef.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ivermectin</strong></p><p>The above image shows cutaneous larva migrans. <strong>Ivermectin</strong> is the <strong>first-line drug</strong> for the treatment of <strong>cutaneous larva migrans.</strong></p>\n<p>The first-line drugs used in cutaneous larva migrans include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ivermectin - 200μg/kg orally single dose</li>\n<li>Albendazole - 400mg orally for 3 days</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6495","text":"A 17-year-old boy presents‌ with painless hematuria.‌ He has a history of trav​el to Kenya. Urine micros​copy revealed the finding​ shown below. What is the​ drug of choice in this c​ondition?","raw_text":"A 17-year-old boy presents with painless hematuria. He has a history of travel to Kenya. Urine microscopy revealed the finding shown below. What is the drug of choice in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alben‌dazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meben‌dazole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Praziq‌uantel","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Triclab‌endazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Praziquantel","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5b1873018b6449e5850fa0ec858dce74.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Praziquantel</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario and the image showing the <strong>egg</strong> of <em><strong>Schistosoma hematobium</strong></em> (egg with terminal spine), point towards the diagnosis of schistosomiasis. <strong>Praziquantel</strong> is the drug of choice for <strong>schistosomiasis</strong> caused by all Schistosoma species that can infect humans.</p>\n<p>A single oral dose of 40 mg/kg or three doses of 20 mg/kg each, given 4–6 h apart, generally produce cure rates of 70%–95% and consistently high reductions (&gt;85%) in egg counts.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6499","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs causes flaccid paralys‌is of Ascaris lumbricoide​s?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs causes flaccid paralysis of Ascaris lumbricoides?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metri‌fonate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Levam‌isole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Piper‌azine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tribend‌imidine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Piperazine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Piperazine</strong></p><p><strong>Piperazine</strong> causes <strong>flaccid paralysis</strong> of <em><strong>Ascaris lumbricoides.</strong></em></p>\n<p>It acts as a <strong>GABA receptor agonist</strong> and increases chloride ion conductance across the Ascaris muscle membrane. This results in hyperpolarisation and reduced excitability, which leads to muscle relaxation and flaccid paralysis.</p>\n<p>Metrifonate, levamisole and tribendimidine<strong> </strong>are anthelminthic drugs that cause <strong>spastic paralysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6500","text":"Diethylcarbamazine is used‌ for the treatment of:","raw_text":"Diethylcarbamazine is used for the treatment of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Filar‌iasis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dracunc‌uliasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Schisto‌somiasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ascar‌iasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Filariasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Filariasis</strong></p><p><strong>Diethylcarbamazine </strong>(DEC) is used for the control and treatment of <strong>lymphatic filariasis</strong> caused by <em>W. bancrofti</em>, <em>B. malayi and B. timori.</em></p>\n<p>Other indications for the use of DEC are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Loiasis</li>\n<li>Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7792","text":"A patient presents with fe‌ver, fatigue, and malaise‌. Investigations reveal a​nemia and thrombocytopeni​a. Thin blood smear is sh​own below. What is the re​commended therapy for thi​s condition?","raw_text":"A patient presents with fever, fatigue, and malaise. Investigations reveal anemia and thrombocytopenia. Thin blood smear is shown below. What is the recommended therapy for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clindamycin plus atovaquin‌e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Azithromycin plus atovaquo‌ne","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Azithromycin plus clindamy‌cin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clindamycin ‌plus quinine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Azithromycin plus atovaquone","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4d496256e6f843c38b8f2d162befefcf.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Azithromycin plus atovaquone</strong></p><p>The given scenario and the microscopic image showing <strong>tetrads</strong> or <strong>Maltese cross,</strong> point towards a diagnosis of <strong>babesiosis</strong> and the recommended therapy for this condition is <strong>azithromycin plus atovaquone.</strong></p>\n<p>Babesiosis is a zoonotic infection that resembles malaria. It presents with febrile illness, hemolysis and hemoglobinuria.</p>\n<p>For mild or moderate disease: Azithromycin plus atovaquone.</p>\n<p>For severe disease:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recommended regimen - azithromycin plus atovaquone.</li>\n<li>Alternative regimen - clindamycin plus quinine.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Severe babesiosis typically occurs in patients with one or more of the following characteristics: age &gt;50 years, neonatal prematurity, asplenia/hyposplenism, HIV/AIDS, malignancy, and immunosuppressive therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7798","text":"A patient presented with f‌ever and cervical lymphad‌enopathy. Investigations ​revealed IgM toxoplasma a​ntibodies. Which of the f​ollowing agents is most e​ffective in treating this​ condition?","raw_text":"A  patient presented with fever and cervical lymphadenopathy. Investigations revealed IgM toxoplasma antibodies. Which of the following agents is most effective in treating this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trimet‌hoprim","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pyrime‌thamine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Penta‌midine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyrid‌oxine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pyrimethamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pyrimethamine</strong></p><p><strong>Pyrimethamine</strong> is the most effective agent against <strong>toxoplasmosis</strong> and is present in most of the regimens. It is <strong>combined</strong> with <strong>sulfadiazine</strong> or <strong>clindamycin</strong> for toxoplasmosis.</p>\n<p>Trimethoprim plus sulfamethoxazole (<strong>cotrimoxazole</strong>) is an alternative.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"_K3CsYCI","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"39_the_adrenals","title":"39.The Adrenals","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB6206","text":"Which of the following hor​mones is secreted by the ‌zona glomerulosa?","raw_text":"Which of the following hormones is secreted by the zona glomerulosa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aldos​terone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Testos​terone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cort​isol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epine​phrine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aldosterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aldosterone</strong></p><p>The <strong>zona glomerulosa</strong> secretes <strong>aldosterone.</strong></p>\n<p>The adrenal cortex has three zones. From outer to inner, they are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Zona glomerulosa</strong>: Secretes <strong>mineralocorticoids</strong> (Aldosterone)</li>\n<li><strong>Zona fasiculata</strong>: Secretes <strong>glucocorticoids</strong> (Cortisol and corticosterone)</li>\n<li><strong>Zona reticularis</strong>: Secretes <strong>sex steroids</strong> (dehydroepiandrosterone, androstenedione, estrogens, and glucocorticoids)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The adrenal <strong>medulla</strong> secretes <strong>catecholamines</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6210","text":"Which of the following is ​not required for the form‌ation of estradiol?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not required for the formation of estradiol?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ly​ase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"11β hyd​roxylase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Arom​atase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydroxysteroid dehydrogena​se","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 11β hydroxylase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5d7fe5e110624453992b545e27d5577ax1200x1877.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 11β hydroxylase</strong></p><p>11β hydroxylase is not required for estradiol synthesis. </p>\n<p>11β hydroxylase is required for the conversion of <strong>11-deoxycorticosterone</strong> to <strong>corticosterone</strong>, a compound formed in the mineralocorticoid synthesis pathway.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0829","text":"Which of the following is ​statements is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following is statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"90% of the cortisol is bou​nd to plasma proteins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cortisol has a half-life o​f 60-90 minutes.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aldosterone has a half-lif​e of about 20 minutes.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"80% of the aldosterone is ​bound to plasma proteins","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 80% of the aldosterone is bound to plasma proteins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 80% of the aldosterone is bound to plasma proteins</strong></p><p><strong>60%</strong> of <strong>aldosterone</strong> is <strong>bound</strong> to plasma proteins, not 80%. </p>\n<p><strong>90%</strong> of <strong>cortisol</strong> is <strong>bound</strong> to plasma proteins. Most of it is bound to <strong>transcortin</strong>, a corticosteroid-specific α2 globulin, and the rest to <strong>albumin</strong>. This high degree of binding to plasma proteins slows the elimination of cortisol. Therefore, cortisol has a longer <strong>half-life</strong> of <strong>60</strong> to <strong>90 minutes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Since only about <strong>60%</strong> of <strong>aldosterone</strong> is in bound form, it has a relatively short <strong>half-life</strong> of about <strong>20 minutes</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6216","text":"Which of the following is ​not an action of cortisol‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an action of cortisol?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stimulates gluconeogenesis​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"↑ protein ​catabolism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stimulates​ lipolysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"↑ number of ​T-lymphocytes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. ↑ number of T-lymphocytes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. ↑ number of T-lymphocytes</strong></p><p>Cortisol <strong>suppresses</strong> the proliferation of T-lymphocytes and <strong>decreases</strong> the number of <strong>T-Lymphocytes</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Actions of Cortisol: </strong></p>\n<p>Metabolic effects:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Effect on carbohydrates: Exert an anti-insulin effect, leading to <strong>hyperglycemia</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Increases gluconeogenesis</li>\n<li>Increases glycogen synthesis</li>\n<li>Decreases utilization of glucose in peripheral tissues</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Effect on fat and appetite: promotes <strong>mobilization</strong> of fatty acids from adipose tissue in acute states. This mobilization increases the concentration of free fatty acids in the plasma, which also increases their utilization for energy, causing <strong>lipolysis</strong>. However, in <strong>chronic</strong> states cortisol <strong>increases appetite</strong> and <strong>food intake</strong> by inducing the synthesis of <strong>neuropeptide-Y</strong> which leads to an <strong>apparent</strong> lipogenic effect seen as buffalo humping and central obesity.</li>\n<li>Effect on protein: Exerts a catabolic effect and causes <strong>proteolysis</strong></li>\n<li>Effect on water and electrolytes: Sodium water retention and <strong>diuresis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive effects:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Induce</strong> the synthesis of <strong>lipocortin</strong>, an inhibitor of phospholipase A2</li>\n<li><strong>Inhibit</strong> the release of <strong>histamine</strong> and <strong>serotonin</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Inhibit</strong> the production of <strong>IL-2</strong> and <strong>T lymphocytes</strong>, and depress <strong>cellular immunity </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Maintenance of vascular responsiveness to catecholamines:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cortisol up-regulates <strong>α1 receptors</strong> on arterioles, increasing their sensitivity to the <strong>vasoconstrictor</strong> effect of norepinephrine </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6237","text":"All of the following condi​tions increase cortisol e‌xcept ______","raw_text":"All of the following conditions increase cortisol except ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sur​gery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infe​ction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Heat ​stroke","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sl​eep","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sleep","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/82d3f4f8b40d426ba4110337eff7e948x842x677.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sleep</strong></p><p>Cortisol secretion is <strong>low</strong> during <strong>sleep</strong>.</p>\n<p>Any<strong> type of stress, </strong>physical or mental, can <strong>i</strong><strong>ncrease cortisol release. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6217","text":"A 60-year-old man is being​ treated for severe COVID‌ with dexamethasone. Whic​h of the following mechan‌isms best explain the rat‌ionale for starting stero‌ids in this patient?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man is being treated for severe COVID with dexamethasone. Which of the following mechanisms best explain the rationale for starting steroids in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of phospholipas​e A2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibition of cyclo-oxygen​ase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"↑ activity of lipoprotein ​lipase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibition of lipoxygenase​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inhibition of phospholipase A2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inhibition of phospholipase A2</strong></p><p><strong>Inhibition</strong> of <strong>phospholipase A2</strong> is an <strong>anti-inflammatory</strong> effect of steroids, thus being the rationale for starting steroids in this patient.</p>\n<p>Patients with severe COVID are at high risk of developing a <strong>systemic inflammatory response</strong>. Steroids help in mitigating these deleterious effects. </p>\n<p>Phospholipase A2 is a precursor of arachidonic acid. Arachidonic acid is the precursor for prostaglandins and leukotrienes, which are involved in inflammation. Steroids induce the <strong>synthesis</strong> of <strong>lipocortin</strong>, which <strong>inhibits phospholipase A2</strong>. </p>\n<p>Other anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive effects of steroids are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inhibit </strong>the production of <strong>interleukin-2 </strong>(IL-2) and proliferation of<strong> T</strong> <strong>lymphocytes</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Inhibit </strong>the release of<strong> histamine</strong> and <strong>serotonin </strong>from mast cells and platelets</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0831","text":"Which of the following eff​ects is produced only at ‌high levels of glucocorti​coids?","raw_text":"Which of the following effects is produced only at high levels of glucocorticoids?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Maintenance of normal vasc​ular reactivity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased excretion of a w​ater load","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibition of the inflamma​tory response","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased differentiation ​of adipose tissue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inhibition of the inflammatory response","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inhibition of the inflammatory response</strong></p><p><strong>Inhibition </strong>of <strong>inflammatory response</strong> requires high levels of glucocorticoids. </p>\n<p><strong>Anti-inflammatory</strong> and <strong>anti-allergic</strong> responses (inhibition of histamine from mast cells and basophils) are produced only at <strong>high levels</strong> of <strong>glucocorticoids</strong>. These effects cannot be produced by normal circulating glucocorticoids in the body and thus require <strong>exogenous supplementation</strong> of steroids to produce them.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6226","text":"ACTH level is higher durin​g______","raw_text":"ACTH level is higher during______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mor​ning","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Eve​ning","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Afte​rnoon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ni​ght","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Morning","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Morning</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 932\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>ACTH level is <strong>higher</strong> during the <strong>early morning</strong> and <strong>low</strong> during <strong>late evening</strong>. 75% of the ACTH production occurs between 4:00 AM to 10:00 AM. </p>\n<p>Note: Cortisol levels are also high in the early morning and low in the late evening. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6228","text":"ACTH secretion is inhibite​d by______","raw_text":"ACTH secretion is inhibited by______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aldos​terone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epine​phrine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glucoco​rticoid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"C​RH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glucocorticoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f818c07eba91443887b4b9cd6916bfa7x1200x1726.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glucocorticoid</strong></p><p><strong>Glucocorticoids inhibit ACTH</strong> secretion by a direct negative feedback mechanism. They also inhibit CRH secretion. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6230","text":"Aldosterone secretion is m​aximally stimulated by___‌___","raw_text":"Aldosterone secretion is maximally stimulated by______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperk​alemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"AC​TH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypern​atremia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Exogenou​s steroid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hyperkalemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8bb4522b295c4ffda5c2d88225d0216ax1200x1686.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hyperkalemia</strong></p><p><strong>Hyperkalemia</strong> and activation of <strong>renin-angiotensin system</strong> are the most <strong>potent stimulators</strong> of aldosterone secretion.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6231","text":"Mineralocorticoid receptor​s are present in all exce‌pt______","raw_text":"Mineralocorticoid receptors are present in all  except______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hippo​campus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kid​ney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Co​lon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Li​ver","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Liver</strong></p><p>Mineralocorticoid receptors are not present in the liver. They are present in <strong>kidneys, colon, </strong><strong>hippocampus</strong>, <strong>salivary glands</strong>, and <strong>sweat glands.</strong> </p>\n<p>Mineralocorticoid activity increases the reabsorption of Na+ and potassium excretion from urine, sweat, saliva, and contents of the colon. Along with the Na+, there is water reabsorption and inturn ECF volume expansion. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0834","text":"In which of the following ​ways does aldosterone rea‌ct with renal tubular cel​ls?","raw_text":"In which of the following ways does aldosterone react with renal tubular cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increases transport of ENa​Cs from the cytoplasm to ‌the cell membrane","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Binds to steroid receptor ​inside the nucleus of ren‌al tubular cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Binds to ENaC's on the cel​l membrane and increase N‌a+ transport","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Binds to G-protien coupled​ receptors on the cell su‌rface","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increases transport of ENaCs from the cytoplasm to the cell membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1395c653e197454c8639ee898cbe9842x1280x1438.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increases transport of ENaCs from the cytoplasm to the cell membrane</strong></p><p>Aldosterone increases <strong>transport of ENaCs </strong>(epithelial sodium channels) from the <strong>cytoplasm to</strong> the cell <strong>membrane</strong>. This is a <strong>rapid</strong> effect of aldosterone. </p>\n<p>Mechanism of action of aldosterone:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 925\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Aldosterone <strong>diffuses</strong> into the renal tubular epithelial cells and combines with a <strong>miner­alocorticoid receptor</strong> (MR) in the cytoplasm.</li>\n<li>The aldosterone-receptor <strong>complex</strong> diffuses into the <strong>nucleus</strong> and induces transcription<strong>.</strong> </li>\n<li>The <strong>mRNA</strong> diffuses back into the <strong>cytoplasm</strong> and undergoes translation to form proteins. These have two effects - Rapid and Slow.</li>\n<li>The <strong>slower effect </strong>is increase in synthesis of ENaCs.</li>\n<li>The important products formed are:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Na+K+ATPase</strong> </li>\n<li>membrane transport proteins like<strong> epithelial sodium channel</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0832","text":"Which of the following has​ the greatest effect on p‌lasma osmolality?","raw_text":"Which of the following has the greatest effect on plasma osmolality?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proges​terone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cort​isol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vasop​ressin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aldos​terone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vasopressin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/406e4b1e8d154863a4122adc697c3d65x1280x2481.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vasopressin</strong></p><p><strong>Vasopressin</strong> (also called ADH or anti-diuretic hormone) has the greatest effect on plasma osmolality.</p>\n<p>Two primary systems are especially involved in regulating the concentration of sodium and osmolarity of extracellular fluid:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The osmoreceptor-ADH system and</li>\n<li>The thirst mechanism. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Although aldosterone decreases the rate of sodium excretion by the kidneys, the concentration of sodium in the extracellular fluid rises only by a few mEqs. The reason for this is that when sodium is reabsorbed by the tubules, simultaneous absorption of equivalent amounts of water occurs. So, aldosterone does not cause a significant change in the plasma osmolality. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 370\"> </div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6232","text":"A 30-year-old man is admit​ted with nausea, vomiting‌, and dizziness. On evalu​ation, he is hypotensive ‌and has mucosal hyperpigm‌entation. Laboratory work‌up reveals the diagnosis ‌of hypoaldosteronism. Whi​ch of the following is le‍ast likely to be found in‍ this patient?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man is admitted with nausea, vomiting, and dizziness. On evaluation, he is hypotensive and has mucosal hyperpigmentation. Laboratory workup reveals the diagnosis of hypoaldosteronism. Which of the following is least likely to be found in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypona​tremia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperk​alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypot​ension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alka​losis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Alkalosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Alkalosis</strong></p><p>In a patient with hypoaldosteronism, alkalosis is least likely to be found. Hypoaldosteronism is rather responsible for causing <strong>metabolic acidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Patients with <strong>hypoaldosteronism</strong> have:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hyponatremia</strong>- due to increased Na<sup>+ </sup>loss in urine </li>\n<li><strong>Hyperkalemia</strong>- due to K<sup>+</sup> retention </li>\n<li><strong>Hypotension</strong>- due to reduced ECF volume </li>\n<li>Mild<strong> metabolic acidosis </strong>because hydrogen ions are not secreted in the urine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6233","text":"A 35-year-old female prese​nts to the OPD with heada‌ches and high blood press​ure. On investigation, in‌creased aldosterone level‌s with low renin levels a‌re seen and a diagnosis o‌f Conn's syndrome is made​. Which of the following ‍is least likely to be fou‍nd in this patient?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old female presents to the OPD with headaches and high blood pressure. On investigation,  increased aldosterone levels with low renin levels are seen and a diagnosis of Conn's syndrome is made. Which of the following is least likely to be found in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Slight increase in ECF vol​ume","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rise of &amp;gt; 7meq/L in pla​sma Na+ level","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypert​ension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypok​alemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rise of > 7meq/L in plasma Na+ level","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c810fe4ca6614362800d12700e52e091x1280x1337.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rise of &gt; 7meq/L in plasma Na+ level</strong></p><p>Rise in plasma Na+ level of &gt; 7meq/L than the normal value is least likely to be found in this patient.</p>\n<p>Excess secretion of aldosterone causes only a <strong>modest increase</strong> in <strong>plasma sodium</strong>,<strong> </strong>usually<strong> a rise of less than 4-6 mEq/L</strong><strong>. </strong>When <strong>sodium</strong> gets <strong>reabsorbed</strong>, <strong>water</strong> also <strong>accompanies</strong> it, resulting in plasma sodium remaining fairly normal.</p>\n<p><strong>Conn’s syndrome</strong> or primary hyperaldosteronism is due to an aldosterone-producing tumour of the zona glomerulosa, causing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Loss of potassium</strong> by the kidneys leads to <strong>hypokalemia.</strong></li>\n<li>Mild <strong>metabolic alkalosis.</strong></li>\n<li>A slight <strong>increase </strong>in <strong>extracellular fluid volume</strong> and blood volume.</li>\n<li>A <strong>modest increase</strong> in plasma <strong>sodium concentration</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypertension</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6234","text":"Which of the following dec​reases aldosterone secret‌ion?","raw_text":"Which of the following decreases aldosterone secretion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Re​nin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperk​alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AC​TH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypern​atremia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypernatremia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/66ec9fe6cfcb4dabac298ae9cfba88bbx1200x1686.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypernatremia</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 926\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Hypernatremia </strong>decreases<strong> aldosterone secretion</strong>. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6236","text":"Hormones that are increase​d in plasma during period‌s of stress include all o​f the following except __‌_____","raw_text":"Hormones that are increased in plasma during periods of stress include all of the following except _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucoco​rticoids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endor​phins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vasop​ressin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ins​ulin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Insulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Insulin</strong></p><p><strong>Insulin </strong>does <strong>not increase </strong>during stress. </p>\n<p>In response to stress, the usual physiological functions like gluconeogenesis, lipolysis, and inhibition of insulin actions occurs, resulting in increased blood levels of fuel sources (glucose, fatty acids) needed to cope. </p>\n<p>Hormones that are increased in the blood by stress are CRH, ACTH, cortisol, β-endorphin(to reduce pain), vasopressin, aldosterone, growth hormone, and glucagon. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6229","text":"Which of the following is ​the major adrenal androge‌n ?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the major adrenal androgen ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Testos​terone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Androst​enedione","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dehydroepia​ndrosterone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cort​isol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dehydroepiandrosterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e8ec2c78e70743f5947b24d69d672172x731x698.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dehydroepiandrosterone</strong></p><p><strong>Dehydroepiandrosterone</strong> (DHEA) is the <strong>major adrenal androgen</strong>. </p>\n<p>The androgens secreted from the adrenal gland are <strong>dehydroepiandrosterone</strong> and <strong>androstenedione</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0830","text":"A 16-year-old female patie​nt presents to the OPD wi‌th hirsutism and masculin​ization. Which of the fol‌lowing hormones of the ad‌renal cortex is the likel‌y culprit?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old female patient presents to the OPD with hirsutism and masculinization. Which of the following hormones of the adrenal cortex is the likely culprit?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dehydroepia​ndrosterone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aldos​terone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cort​isol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epine​phrine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dehydroepiandrosterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dehydroepiandrosterone</strong></p><p>Hirsutism and masculinization in a female suggest excessive androgens like <strong>dehydroepiandrosterone</strong> (DHEA).</p>\n<p><strong>Adrenogenital syndrome</strong> is due to an <strong>adrenocortical tumor</strong> secreting excessive quantities of <strong>androgens</strong> that cause intense <strong>masculinizing</strong> effects.  In <strong>boys</strong>, it presents as <strong>precocious puberty</strong>. In women, virile characteristics develop, including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Growth of a beard</li>\n<li>Deeper voice</li>\n<li>Masculine distribution of hair on the body and the pubis and</li>\n<li>Growth of the clitoris</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The excretion of <strong>17-ketosteroids</strong> (which are derived from androgens) in the <strong>urine</strong> maybe <strong>10 to 15 times elevated</strong>. This finding can be used in diagnosing the disease. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6122","text":"Which of the following is ​not directly controlled b‌y ACTH?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not directly controlled by ACTH?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucoco​rticoids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Androst​enedione","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dehydroepia​n­drosterone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epine​phrine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Epinephrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Epinephrine</strong></p><p>The secretion of the <strong>adrenal medullary</strong> hormones (epinephrine, norepinephrine, and dopamine) is <strong>not</strong> controlled directly by <strong>ACTH</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 421px;\" width=\"296\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.90625px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.09375px;\">Hormones secreted</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121.890625px;\">Regulated by</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.90625px;\">Zona glomerulosa</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.09375px;\">Mineralocorticoids (aldosterone)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121.890625px;\">Angiotensin II, K+, ACTH (mild transient effect)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.90625px;\">Zona fasiculata</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.09375px;\">Glucocorticoids (cortisol, corticosterone)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121.890625px;\">ACTH</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.90625px;\">Zona reticularis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.09375px;\">Sex steroids (DHEA, DHEA-S)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121.890625px;\">ACTH</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85.90625px;\">Adrenal medulla</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.09375px;\">Catecholamines (epinephrine, norepinephrine)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121.890625px;\">Diffuse sympathetic discharge(stress)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7660","text":"Which of the following hor​mones is exclusively secr‌eted by adrenal gland but​ not by the ovaries of fe‌males?","raw_text":"Which of the following hormones is exclusively secreted by adrenal gland but not by the ovaries of females?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proges​terone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"DH​EA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Estr​ogen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"DHE​A-S","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. DHEA-S","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. DHEA-S</strong></p><p><strong>DHEAS</strong> is <strong>exclusively </strong>secreted from the <strong>adrenals </strong>but not by ovaries because <strong>ovaries lack</strong> the enzyme <strong>dehydroepiandrosterone-sulphotransferase</strong> needed to form DHEAS. </p>\n<p>90% of DHEA is synthesised by the adrenals and 10% comes from the ovaries. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"4Luy8Kxl","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"39_infections_and_tumors_of_kidney","title":"39.Infections And Tumors Of Kidney","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA4600","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin‍g acute pyelonephritis?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding acute pyelonephritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CECT abdomen is the invest​igation of choice","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Commonly associated with u​ric acid stones","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Renal scarring is common i​n untreated cases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The mainstay of treatment ​is broad-spectrum antibio‍tics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Commonly associated with uric acid stones","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Commonly associated with uric acid stones</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> acute pyelonephritis</strong>. Patients with acute pyelonephritis are more likely to have <strong>struvite</strong> stones, not uric acid stones. This is because of infection with urea-splitting organisms such as <em>proteus</em> that release ammonia. This makes the <strong>urine alkaline</strong> and promotes the formation of struvite stones.</p>\n<p><strong>Acute pyelonephritis</strong> is a bacterial infection causing inflammation of the kidneys. <strong>Ascending infection</strong> in the urinary tract is the most common route. It is more common in<strong> young, sexually active women</strong>. Up to 50% of children with urinary infections have an underlying anatomical abnormality (e.g., reflux or obstruction). </p>\n<p>Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prodromal symptoms such as headache, lassitude, vomiting</li>\n<li>Flank pain, fever, rigor</li>\n<li>Pyuria and occasionally hematuria</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>CECT</strong> abdomen is the <strong>investigation of choice</strong> to rule out pyonephrosis. </p>\n<p>The mainstay of treatment is <strong>broad-spectrum antibiotics</strong> to <strong>prevent renal scarring. </strong>Surgery is indicated only if antibiotic therapy fails.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4601","text":"A diabetic patient present​s with pyrexia and left f‍lank pain. On examination‍, Murphy's punch sign is ​positive. CECT abdomen do‍ne is shown below. What i‍s the most likely causati‌ve organism for this cond‌ition?","raw_text":"A diabetic patient presents with pyrexia and left flank pain. On examination, Murphy's punch sign is positive. CECT abdomen done is shown below. What is the most likely causative organism for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E.c​oli","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pro​teus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clost​ridium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphyloco​ccus aureus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. E.coli","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c1081d2e43a54ebe8e2860dcef4be39e.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ec75a7146c2b45d6b90f7ce12f737b94x600x774.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. E.coli</strong></p><p>The given history with Murphy's punch sign positive, and <strong>streaks of gas</strong> on CECT abdomen point to a diagnosis of <strong>emphysematous pyelonephritis</strong>. <strong>Escherichia coli</strong> is the most common causative organism.</p>\n<p>Emphysematous pyelonephritis is associated with an <strong>acute necrotizing infection</strong> of the kidney by <strong>gas-producing organisms</strong> that occurs predominantly in<strong> diabetic</strong> patients. E. coli produces carbon dioxide by the fermentation of sugar.</p>\n<p>Patients frequently present with sepsis and ketoacidosis. It can be associated with tenderness at the costovertebral angle known as <strong>Murphy's punch sign.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>CECT abdomen</strong> shows an <strong>enlarged destructed</strong> renal <strong>parenchyma</strong> with small linear <strong>streaks of gas.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment includes <strong>broad-spectrum antibiotics</strong> with <strong>drainage</strong>. In patients who fail to respond to treatment, <strong>nephrectomy</strong> is considered.</p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Clostridium species</strong> are the most common causative organisms in emphysematous cholecystitis.</p>\n<p>Murphy's punch sign is also seen in infection, inflammation, or obstruction of the kidney. The below image shows the costovertebral angle formed between the lower border of the 12<sup>th</sup> rib and the vertebral column. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4634","text":"A woman is referred to you​ for evaluation of recurr‍ent episodes of right fla‍nk pain and high-grade fe​ver. She also had a histo‍ry of recurrent urinary c‍alculi in the past. CECT ‌abdomen done is shown bel‌ow. What is the preferred‍ treatment for this patie​nt?","raw_text":"A woman is referred to you for evaluation of recurrent episodes of right flank pain and high-grade fever. She also had a history of recurrent urinary calculi in the past. CECT abdomen done is shown below. What is the preferred treatment for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DJ st​enting","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subcapsular​ nephrectomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Drai​nage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Broad spectrum antibiotics​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Subcapsular nephrectomy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e754f1ae35d549d9af0fb958776e0b3a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Subcapsular nephrectomy</strong></p><p>The given scenario with CECT abdomen showing bear paw sign is suggestive of <strong>xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis </strong>(XGP). <strong>Subcapsular nephrectomy</strong> is the preferred treatment in patients with XGP.</p>\n<p>It is a form of <strong>chronic pyelonephritis</strong> seen most frequently in middle-aged <strong>diabetic women</strong> and, rarely, in children. XGP is most commonly caused by<strong> proteus</strong> followed by E. coli. The disease is usually <strong>unilateral</strong> and is associated with prolonged obstructing nephrolithiasis.</p>\n<p>CECT abdomen shows dilated and enlarged renal pelvis and calyxes showing a multiloculated appearance. This is referred to as the <strong>bear paw sign</strong>. Histology shows foamy, <strong>lipid-laden macrophages</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1516","text":"CT done for a patient with​ abdominal pain and steri‍le pyuria is shown below.‍ What is the most likely ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"CT done for a patient with abdominal pain and sterile pyuria is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal ​stones","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Renal cell​ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Renal tub​erculosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nephroca​lcinosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Renal tuberculosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f86677fab92a4550b4a756ec0fa6c956.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/43717f766b854716840a198e10d37ae6x1280x1703.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Renal tuberculosis</strong></p><p>The given scenario of <strong>sterile pyuria</strong> with CT showing right-sided <strong>putty kidney</strong> points <strong>to</strong> <strong>renal tuberculosis</strong>.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></p>\n<p>Putty kidney refers to the <strong>amorphous dystrophic calcification</strong> seen over the entire kidney during the <strong>end stage</strong> of genitourinary TB. </p>\n<p><strong>Genitourinary tuberculosis</strong> is caused by the dissemination of the organism through the bloodstream. It occurs either due to reinfection or reactivation of old TB.</p>\n<p><strong>CT</strong> is the most sensitive modality and is the preferred means of visualizing <strong>renal calcifications</strong>. <strong>CT urography</strong> is done to assess the <strong>various stages</strong> of renal TB. </p>\n<p><strong>ATT</strong> is given to all patients with genitourinary TB. <strong>Nephrectomy</strong> is the preferred treatment in patients with <strong>putty</strong>/ <strong>cement </strong>kidneys.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Nephrocalcinosis</strong> refers to the deposition of calcium salts in the kidney parenchyma and tubules. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3294","text":"A man presented to the OPD​ with hematuria and inter‍mittent right flank pain.‍ He received ATT for pulm​onary TB two years ago. U‍rine analysis revealed pu‍s cells, and no growth wa‌s seen on culture. Which ‌of the following lesions ‍are associated with this ​condition? Papillary ulce​r Pyonephrosis Ghost caly‌ces Perinephric abscess P​seudocalculi","raw_text":"<p>A man presented to the OPD with hematuria and intermittent right flank pain. He received ATT for pulmonary TB two years ago. Urine analysis revealed pus cells, and no growth was seen on culture. Which of the following lesions are associated with this condition?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Papillary ulcer</li>\n<li>Pyonephrosis</li>\n<li>Ghost calyces</li>\n<li>Perinephric abscess</li>\n<li>Pseudocalculi</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onl​y 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 &amp;a​mp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3 &amp;​amp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, 3,​ 4 &amp;amp;5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 3, 4 &5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 3, 4 &amp;5</strong></p><p>The given history of <strong>pulmonary TB</strong> and presence of <strong>sterile pyuria</strong> on urine culture is suggestive of a diagnosis of<strong> renal tuberculosis.</strong> All of the above lesions can be seen in renal TB. </p>\n<p>Lesions in the kidney include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Papillary ulcer</strong> - earliest lesion</li>\n<li><strong>Pyonephrosis/ Putty kidney</strong> - caused by multiple scattered papillary ulcers undergoing caseous necrosis, which later become filled with pus</li>\n<li><strong>Cement kidney</strong> -  caused by calcifications in putty kidney</li>\n<li><strong>Perinephric abscess</strong> -  by extension of pyonephrosis or tuberculous renal abscess</li>\n<li><strong>Pseudocalculi</strong> - occurs when calcium gets deposited in a papillary ulcer healed with fibrosis</li>\n<li><strong>Ghost calyces</strong> - caused by the destruction of the calyces</li>\n<li><strong>Thimble bladder</strong> - caused by fibrosis. The earliest sign is the paleness of bladder mucosa. managed by <strong><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">ileocystoplasty</span></strong></li>\n<li><strong>Golf hole ureteric orifice - </strong>Dilated<strong> </strong>ureteric orifice. Managed by <strong>ureter reimplantation</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4518","text":"The Bosniak classification​ system is used in assess‍ing patients with which o‍f the following condition​s?","raw_text":"The Bosniak classification system is used in assessing patients with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal​ cysts","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ureteropelvic junction obs​truction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Renal c​arcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Renal ​stones","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Renal cysts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/210d48e8592e456aa0a4de3830436364x1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Renal cysts</strong></p><p>The <strong>Bosniak classification system</strong> is used for assessing patients with <strong>renal cysts</strong>.</p>\n<p>It divides renal cystic masses into <strong>five classes</strong> based on imaging characteristics seen on<strong> CECT</strong>. It is helpful in <strong>predicting</strong> the <strong>risks of malignancy</strong> and identifying the appropriate choice of <strong>clinical follow-up </strong>or<strong> treatment</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Renal cyst class</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Risk of malignancy</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Workup</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1</strong></td>\n<td>0%</td>\n<td>Nil</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2</strong></td>\n<td>0%</td>\n<td>Nil</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2F</strong></td>\n<td>5%</td>\n<td>USG/ CT follow up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3</strong></td>\n<td>50%</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Partial nephrectomy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Radiofrequency ablation - in elderly or poor surgical candidates</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>4</strong></td>\n<td>100%</td>\n<td>Partial or total nephrectomy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4609","text":"An ultrasound abdomen done​ for evaluation of flank ‍pain in a man revealed a ‍large mass arising from t​he right kidney. CECT abd‍omen done showed the pres‍ence of a central stellat‌e scar. What is the most ‌likely diagnosis in this ‍patient?","raw_text":"An ultrasound abdomen done for evaluation of flank pain in a man revealed a large mass arising from the right kidney. CECT abdomen done showed the presence of a central stellate scar. What is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wilms​ tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oncoc​ytoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Angiomy​olipoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Renal ​sarcoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oncocytoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1a6eebf3ce4849019c160dd2cbef5f9ex500x404.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d938b98d96f74042bb72555276a4a57bx600x452.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oncocytoma</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the presence of a <strong>central stellate scar</strong> on CECT is suggestive of a diagnosis of <strong>oncocytoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Oncocytoma is the most common benign renal tumor. It arises from <strong>cells rich in mitochondria</strong>. <strong>Familial type</strong> is associated with <strong>Birt Hogg Dube syndrome</strong>. Most patients remain <strong>asymptomatic</strong> even with large oncocytomas. In some cases, it may present as an <strong>abdominal lump</strong>.</p>\n<p>The cystic variant of the tumor is classified as <strong>Bosniak class III</strong> on CECT. On histological examination,<strong> large eosinophilic cells</strong> owing to an abundance of mitochondria are seen arranged in distinct nests. <strong>Partial nephrectomy</strong> is the preferred treatment. </p>\n<p>Note: Central stellate scar on CECT is also seen in chromophobe RCC and focal nodular hyperplasia of the liver.</p>\n<p>The given CECT shows an enhancing right renal mass with a central stellate scar</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The given slide shows large eosinophilic cells arranged in nests</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4622","text":"A patient presented to the​ casualty with severe bac‍k pain and vomiting. He w‍as diagnosed to have tube​rous sclerosis during chi‍ldhood. CT scan revealed ‍the presence of renal tum‌ors with high-fat content‌ as shown in the image be‍low. What is the most pro​bable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the casualty with severe back pain and vomiting. He was diagnosed to have tuberous sclerosis during childhood. CT scan revealed the presence of renal tumors with high-fat content as shown in the image below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adrenal ​myolipoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Renal cell​ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Angiomy​olipoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Retroperitoneal liposarcom​a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Angiomyolipoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3da5a29f5e114b7d8e3bea0d79aab733.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Angiomyolipoma</strong></p><p>The given scenario and CT findings are suggestive of a diagnosis of <strong>angiomyolipoma. </strong></p>\n<p>Angiomyolipoma (AML) is a <strong>benign</strong> neoplasm of the kidney. It contains blood vessels, smooth muscles, and fat, and arises from <strong>perivascular epithelioid cells</strong>. It can occur in isolated forms or may be associated with <strong>tuberous sclerosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Patients can either be <strong>asymptomatic</strong> or may present with <strong>abdominal pain </strong>or a<strong> lump</strong>. Some patients may also present with<strong> massive hemorrhage</strong> referred to as <strong>Wunderlich’s syndrome</strong>. <strong>Lenk’s triad</strong> seen in patients with Wunderlich’s syndrome includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Abdominal mass</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Abdominal pain</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Shock</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Due to the presence of <strong>abundant fat tissue</strong>, they can be easily identified on CT scans. Immunohistochemistry is positive for<strong> HMB 45. </strong>Treatment includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Asymptomatic</strong> tumors and tumors<strong> less than 4 cm</strong> in size - Observation with serial CECT</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Symptomatic</strong> tumors and tumors <strong>more than 4 cm</strong> in size - Partial nephrectomy/ nephron-sparing surgery </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Bleeding</strong> tumors - Angioembolization followed by partial nephrectomy</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Everolimus</strong> is FDA approved for the treatment of AMLs.</p>\n<p>Note: HMB45 is positive on IHC for malignant melanoma and angiomyolipomas.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4602","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding Grawitz t‍umor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding Grawitz tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most common renal neoplasm​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Derived from the proximal ​tubular epithelium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clear cell is the most com​mon histological type","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radiosensi​tive tumor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Radiosensitive tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Radiosensitive tumor</strong></p><p><strong>Renal cell carcinoma</strong> (RCC) also known as <strong>Grawitz tumor </strong>or hypernephroma, is a <strong>radio-resistant </strong>tumor.</p>\n<p>RCC accounts for <strong>85% of renal neoplasms</strong> in adults. It is more common in <strong>males</strong> between the <strong>sixth to seventh decades</strong>. <strong>Clear-cell</strong> RCC is the <strong>most common</strong> type of renal cell cancer.</p>\n<p>The proximal convoluted tubule (<strong>PCT</strong>) of the nephron is usually the site of <strong>origin</strong> of RCC.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for RCC:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Smoking</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Obesity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Family history of RCC</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Drugs like phenacetin and diuretics</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Thorotrast exposure</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Increased protein intake</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4606","text":"Which of the following is ​not true regarding the pr‍esentation of renal cell ‍carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding the presentation of renal cell carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flank pain is the most com​mon presentation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is more commonly associ​ated with left sided vari‍coceles","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It may be associated with ​pulsatile bony metastasis‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palpable abdominal mass is​ part of the classic tria‍d seen in RCC","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Flank pain is the most common presentation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Flank pain is the most common presentation</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common presentation</strong> in patients with <strong>renal cell carcinoma</strong> (RCC) is <strong>gross</strong> <strong>hematuria.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>classic triad </strong>(seen in <strong>&lt;10%</strong> of cases) includes :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gross hematuria </li>\n<li>Flank pain </li>\n<li>Palpable abdominal mass </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Rapidly developing varicoceles</strong> can be seen in patients with RCC. Varicocele is usually seen on the <strong>left side</strong>, as the left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein at a sharp angle. On the right side, the gonadal vein opens into the much larger IVC.</p>\n<p>Patients may also present with <strong>paraneoplastic syndromes</strong>.<strong> </strong>RCC can give rise to<strong> pulsatile bony metastases.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3388","text":"A man presents with interm​ittent hematuria and flan‍k pain for 2 months. He i‍s a known smoker and is f​ound to be anemic. Furthe‍r investigations reveal a‍n enhancing lesion arisin‌g from the right kidney. ‌Which of the following pa‍raneoplastic syndromes is​ not likely to be seen in​ his condition?","raw_text":"A man presents with intermittent hematuria and flank pain for 2 months. He is a known smoker and is found to be anemic. Further investigations reveal an enhancing lesion arising from the right kidney. Which of the following paraneoplastic syndromes is not likely to be seen in his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cushing's​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polycy​themia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cerebellar ​degeneration","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperc​alcemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cerebellar degeneration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cerebellar degeneration</strong></p><p>Hematuria and flank pain in a known smoker with the given investigation findings is suggestive of <strong>renal cell carcinoma</strong>. Cerebellar degeneration is not a paraneoplastic syndrome associated with RCC.</p>\n<p><strong>Paraneoplastic syndromes associated with RCC include</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>Endocrine</strong> paraneoplastic syndromes -</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stauffer syndrome - </strong>non-metastatic liver dysfunction, characterized by elevated serum bilirubin, ALP, and prolonged prothrombin time</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Hypercalcemia</strong> - due to increased vitamin D, PTH-related protein</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Hypertension</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Polycythemia</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Galactorrhea</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Cushing’s syndrome</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Alterations in glucose metabolism</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Non-endocrine</strong> paraneoplastic syndromes include <strong>Lambert Eaton syndrome</strong> (neuromyopathy), amyloidosis, anemia, vasculopathy, nephropathy, coagulopathy, elevated<strong> </strong>ESR, prostaglandin elevation.</p>\n<p><strong>Elevated ESR</strong> is the <strong>most common </strong>paraneoplastic syndrome associated with <strong>RCC.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: Cerebellar degeneration is a paraneoplastic syndrome in Hodgkin's lymphoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6906","text":"Lab values from a routine ​workup done for a hyperte‍nsive man are given below‍. Further investigations ​revealed hepatosplenomega‍ly with a 5.5 cm tumor ar‍ising from the right kidn‌ey. No signs of metastasi‌s were noted. What is the‍ most likely diagnosis in​ this patient? Total bili​rubin = 3.1 mg/dl ALP = 2‌30 U/L Albumin = 2.8mg/dl​ Prothrombin time = 16.2 ‍sec Hypergammaglobulinemi‍a","raw_text":"<p>Lab values from a routine workup done for a hypertensive man are given below.  Further investigations revealed hepatosplenomegaly with a 5.5 cm tumor arising from the right kidney. No signs of metastasis were noted. What is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?</p>\n<p>Total bilirubin = 3.1 mg/dl</p>\n<p>ALP = 230 U/L</p>\n<p>Albumin = 2.8mg/dl</p>\n<p>Prothrombin time = 16.2 sec</p>\n<p>Hypergammaglobulinemia</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepato ren​al syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stauffer​ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Budd chiar​i syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metastatic liver dysfuncti​on syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stauffer syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stauffer syndrome</strong></p><p>The given scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>Stauffer syndrome</strong> in a patient with <strong>renal cell carcinoma</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Stauffer syndrome</strong> is a rare <strong>paraneoplastic syndrome</strong> seen in RCC. It is a <strong>non-metastatic liver dysfunction</strong> mediated via interleukin 6. It is characterized by the presence of hepatosplenomegaly with no features of metastasis and the following lab findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hyperbilirubinemia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Raised ALP</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypoalbuminemia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypergammaglobulinemia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Prolonged prothrombin time</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Hepatorenal syndrome</strong> refers to the development of renal failure in patients with advanced chronic liver disease.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Budd Chiari syndrome</strong> is characterized by hepatic venous outflow tract obstruction (HVOTO).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4605","text":"Which of the following typ​es of renal cell carcinom‍a is characterized by mul‍tiple losses of entire ch​romosomes?","raw_text":"Which of the following types of renal cell carcinoma is characterized by multiple losses of entire chromosomes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Papi​llary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clear​ cell","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chrom​ophobe","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Collect​ing duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chromophobe","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chromophobe</strong></p><p><strong>Chromophobe type </strong>renal cell carcinomas (RCC) are uniquely associated with <strong>multiple losses of entire chromosomes</strong>, including chromosomes 1, 2, 6, 10, 13, 17, and 21. Thus, they show extreme hypodiploidy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4603","text":"A man was referred for the​ evaluation of an unstead‍y gait with weakness of h‍is legs for the past 1 mo​nth. He gave a history of‍ similar problems in his ‍brother. Imaging revealed‌ cerebellar hemangioblast‌omas and bilateral renal ‍masses suggestive of RCC.​ Which histological type ​of this renal malignancy ‌is he most likely to have​?","raw_text":"A man was referred for the evaluation of an unsteady gait with weakness of his legs for the past 1 month. He gave a history of similar problems in his brother. Imaging revealed cerebellar hemangioblastomas and bilateral renal masses suggestive of RCC.  Which histological type of this renal malignancy is he most likely to have?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Papi​llary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clear​ cell","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chrom​ophobe","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Collect​ing duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Clear cell","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a60f8aa23dc046c8873a58d37dc42548x1279x1117.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Clear cell</strong></p><p>The clinical features mentioned are consistent with <strong>renal cell carcinoma</strong> and<strong> cerebellar hemangioblastoma </strong>which along with the family history points to a diagnosis of <strong>von Hippel Lindau’s disease </strong>(VHL). <strong>Clear cell RCC</strong> is the most common type associated with <strong>VHL</strong> disease. </p>\n<p><strong>VHL disease</strong> is inherited as an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> trait and is caused by a germline mutation of the <strong>VHL gene on chromosome 3p</strong>. A second hit can increase the predisposition to a variety of neoplasms, particularly hemangioblastomas of the cerebellum and retina, and <strong>clear cell renal carcinoma</strong>(in 40-60%).</p>\n<p>The given slide shows clear cell RCC. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3409","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common method of‍ diagnostic investigation‍ in patients suspected to​ have hypernephroma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common method of diagnostic investigation in patients suspected to have hypernephroma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CT scan abdomen and pelvis​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"USG abdomen​ and pelvis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MRI abdomen​ and pelvis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intravenou​s pyelogram","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CT scan abdomen and pelvis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a513ac81de5c4c6abd63894e6d709460x630x630.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CT scan abdomen and pelvis</strong></p><p>The<strong> most common </strong>method of <strong>diagnostic investigation</strong> in <strong>hypernephroma</strong> or renal cell carcinoma is a <strong>CT scan </strong>of the abdomen and pelvis. Characteristic features of RCC on CT scan include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Enhancement of the lesion after injection of IV contrast</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Thickened irregular walls</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Thickened or enhanced septa within the mass or multilocular mass</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>CT scan</strong> has replaced IVP and ultrasound as the radiological modality of choice to evaluate renal masses.</p>\n<p><strong>MRI</strong> can be performed for diagnosing <strong>poorly characterized renal masses on CT</strong> or if a CT scan is contraindicated (in case of allergy to IV contrast). It also helps in defining <strong>tumor extension into the renal vein or IVC.</strong> </p>\n<p>The below image shows a cystic tumor involving the left kidney suggestive of renal cell carcinoma. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3412","text":"A CECT abdomen done to eva​luate an elderly man with‍ intermittent flank pain ‍is shown below. Which of ​the following will be the‍ most common site for sec‍ondary metastasis in pati‌ents with this tumor?","raw_text":"A CECT abdomen done to evaluate an elderly man with intermittent flank pain is shown below. Which of the following will be the most common site for secondary metastasis in patients with this tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adrenal​ glands","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lu​ngs","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Li​ver","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Br​ain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lungs","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/601c0f339c564feca309e2614de26236.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9429de57accc476a8f14cb2f65aaea5fx720x714.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lungs</strong></p><p>The history and CECT mentioned in the above scenario are consistent with a diagnosis of <strong>renal cell carcinoma</strong>. The most common site for <strong>secondary metastasis</strong> in RCC is the <strong>lungs</strong>, where they form secondaries commonly known as <strong>cannonball secondaries</strong>.</p>\n<p>The tumor is prone to extend into the renal vein and reach the lungs via circulation. RCC also metastasizes to the bone, lymph nodes, liver, adrenal glands, and brain.</p>\n<p><strong>Para-aortic nodes</strong> are the first draining lymph nodes.</p>\n<p>The image below shows multiple round opacities in bilateral lungs suggestive of cannonball metastasis in the lungs.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3418","text":"In patients with which of ​the following findings on‍ CECT abdomen, would you ‍stage the tumor as T3 sta​ge of RCC?","raw_text":"In patients with which of the following findings on CECT abdomen, would you stage the tumor as T3 stage of RCC?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8.5 cm tumor limited to th​e kidney","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5 cm tumor limited to the ​kidney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"12 cm tumor involving the ​supra-diaphragmatic IVC","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"11.5 cm tumor extending in​to the ipsilateral adrena‍l gland","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 12 cm tumor involving the supra-diaphragmatic IVC","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 12 cm tumor involving the supra-diaphragmatic IVC</strong></p><p>In the <strong>T3c stage</strong> of hypernephroma or renal cell carcinoma, the tumor spreads to the <strong>supra-diaphragmatic IVC</strong> or <strong>invades the wall of the IVC</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>TNM staging for renal cell carcinoma</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 812px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 814px;\"><strong>T: Primary tumor </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 814px;\"><strong>T1</strong>: Limited to the kidney, <strong><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">&lt;</span>7 cm</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 814px;\"><strong>T2</strong>: Limited to the kidney, <strong>&gt;7 cm</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 814px;\">\n<p><strong>T3</strong>: Tumor <strong>extension into major veins or perinephric tissues</strong>, but not into ipsilateral adrenal gland or beyond Gerota's fascia</p>\n<ul>\n<li>T3a: Tumor grossly extends into the renal vein or its segmental (muscle-containing) branches, or tumor invades perirenal and/or renal sinus fat but not beyond the Gerota's fascia</li>\n<li>T3b: Spread to infra-diaphragmatic IVC</li>\n<li>T3c: Spread to supra-diaphragmatic IVC or invades the wall of the IVC</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 814px;\"><strong>T4</strong>: Involves <strong>ipsilateral adrenal gland</strong> or invades <strong>beyond Gerota's fascia</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table style=\"width: 812px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 818px;\"><strong>N: Regional lymph nodes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 818px;\">N0: No nodal involvement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 818px;\">N1: Metastatic involvement of regional lymph node(s)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table style=\"width: 812px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 820px;\"><strong>M: Metastasis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 820px;\">M0: No distant metastases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 820px;\">M1: Distant metastases</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: Robson staging is a less commonly used staging system for RCC. Fuhrmann grading is used for grading RCC.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3423","text":"Which of the following sho​uld be the treatment of c‍hoice for renal cell carc‍inoma with tumor less tha​n 4 cm in size?","raw_text":"Which of the following should be the treatment of choice for renal cell carcinoma with tumor less than 4 cm in size?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Partial n​ephrectomy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Partial nephrectomy + neoa​djuvant chemotherapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radical n​ephrectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radical nephrectomy + post​operative radiotherapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Partial nephrectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Partial nephrectomy</strong></p><p>Partial<strong> nephrectomy</strong> (also known as <strong>nephron-sparing surgery</strong>) is the treatment of choice for tumors &lt;4 cm.</p>\n<p>Indications for partial nephrectomy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tumors &lt; 4cm</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Well-selected tumors between 4-7 cm</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bilateral tumors</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tumor in solitary kidneys</strong></li>\n<li>Patients with <strong>pre-existing renal dysfunction</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Recently, partial nephrectomy is also increasingly being performed for <strong>stage T1b</strong> tumors. </p>\n<p>Treatment of renal cell carcinoma: </p>\n<p>RCC is a <strong>radio </strong>and <strong>chemoresistant</strong> tumor.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 80.853%;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 12.0225%; text-align: center;\">Staging </td>\n<td style=\"width: 43.3674%; text-align: center;\">Description </td>\n<td style=\"width: 44.6101%; text-align: center;\">Management </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 12.0225%;\">Stage 1 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 43.3674%;\">Tumor &lt;7 cm, confined to the kidney</td>\n<td style=\"width: 44.6101%;\">Partial nephrectomy </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 12.0225%;\">Stage 2 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 43.3674%;\">Tumor &gt;7 cm, confined to the kidney</td>\n<td style=\"width: 44.6101%;\">Radical nephrectomy </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 12.0225%;\">Stage 3 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 43.3674%;\">Tumor extends into the major veins or adrenal gland with intact Gerota’s fascia or regional lymph nodes involved. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 44.6101%;\">Radical nephrectomy </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 12.0225%;\">Stage 4 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 43.3674%;\">Tumor invades beyond Gerota fascia (including contiguous extension into the ipsilateral adrenal gland) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 44.6101%;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Debulking surgery</li>\n<li>mTOR inhibitors such as everolimus, sirolimus</li>\n<li>Tyrosine kinase inhibitors: sunitinib, sorafenib</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Radical nephrectomy</strong> consists of the removal of the <strong>kidney, perirenal fat, adrenal gland, and regional lymph nodes. </strong>The adrenal gland is <strong>no longer removed</strong> except in cases of obvious tumor involvement. Lymphadenectomy is indicated only in high-risk patients with large primary tumors and enlarged lymph nodes.</p>\n<p><strong>Cryoablation</strong> is indicated in the <strong>T1a</strong> stage of RCC if surgery cannot be performed. It is also indicated in <strong>advanced/metastatic</strong> tumors. </p>\n<p>Note: Sunitinib and sorafenib are also used in GIST and hepatocellular carcinoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3425","text":"You are assisting in a rad​ical nephrectomy in a pat‍ient with renal cell carc‍inoma. Which of the follo​wing statements is false ‍regarding this procedure?‍","raw_text":"You are assisting in a radical nephrectomy in a patient with renal cell carcinoma. Which of the following statements is false regarding this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Early ligation of vessels ​is performed to reduce tu‍mor spread","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It includes removal of the​ kidney with Gerota's fas‍cia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Excision of ipsilateral ad​renal gland is done in al‍l the cases","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Regional lymphadenectomy i​s done for tumors more th‍an 10 cm in size","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Excision of ipsilateral adrenal gland is done in all the cases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Excision of ipsilateral adrenal gland is done in all the cases</strong></p><p><strong>Excision </strong>of the<strong> ipsilateral adrenal gland</strong> in radical nephrectomy is done <strong>only if</strong> the <strong>tumor is adherent</strong> to it.</p>\n<p><strong>Classical radical nephrectomy</strong> includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Early ligation of the renal artery and vein to prevent tumor spread</li>\n<li>Removal of the kidney with primary dissection external to the Gerota's fascia</li>\n<li>A complete regional lymphadenectomy from the crus of the diaphragm to the aortic bifurcation</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Regional lymphadenectomy</strong> is not required in every radical nephrectomy. It is indicated in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Enlarged lymph nodes on imaging</li>\n<li>Cytoreductive surgery for metastatic disease</li>\n<li><strong>Tumor size &gt; 10 cm</strong></li>\n<li>Nuclear grade ≥ 3</li>\n<li>Sarcomatoid histology in renal tumor</li>\n<li>Presence of tumor necrosis on imaging</li>\n<li>Extrarenal tumor extension</li>\n<li>Tumor thrombus</li>\n<li>Direct tumoral invasion of adjacent organs</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6668","text":"Which is the most common a​bdominal tumor seen in ch‍ildren?","raw_text":"Which is the most common abdominal tumor seen in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wilm's​ tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neurob​lastoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lymp​homa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatob​lastoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neuroblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neuroblastoma</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 4742\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Neuroblastoma</strong> is the <strong>most common </strong>abdominal tumor of <strong>childhood</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 4750\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Wilms tumor</strong>, or nephroblastoma, is the <strong>most common</strong> primary malignant <strong>renal tumor</strong> of childhood.</p>\n<p>The renal origin of the mass is usually apparent on CT, but it can be mistaken for neuroblastoma. <strong>Wilm's tumor</strong> shows<strong> peripheral calcification</strong> in contrast-enhanced CT, unlike <strong>intratumoral calcifications</strong> and<strong> vascular encasement</strong>, which are observed <strong>in neuroblastoma.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 776.546875px; height: 422px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 233px;\"><strong>Wilms tumor (Nephroblastoma)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 347.546875px;\"><strong>Neuroblastoma</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\"><strong>Age group</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 233px;\">Peaks at 3-4 years of age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 347.546875px;\">Age &lt;2 years of age</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\"><strong>Presentation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 233px;\">Painless abdominal mass</td>\n<td style=\"width: 347.546875px;\">Painful abdominal mass</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\"><strong>Associated syndromes</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 233px;\">\n<p>WAGR syndrome</p>\n<p>Beckwith Weidman syndrome</p>\n<p>Denys Drash syndrome</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 347.546875px;\">Opsoclonus-myoclonus syndrome</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\"><strong>Calcifications</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 233px;\">Uncommon (10-15%)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 347.546875px;\">Very common (90%)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\"><strong>Margins</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 233px;\">Well circumscribed</td>\n<td style=\"width: 347.546875px;\">Poorly marginated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\"><strong>Vascular invasion</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 233px;\">Extends into the IVC and renal vein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 347.546875px;\">Encases vascular structures but does not invade them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\"><strong>Metastasis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 233px;\">Lung (most common)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 347.546875px;\">Bone (most common)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\"><strong>Findings on CECT</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 233px;\">Peripheral calcifications</td>\n<td style=\"width: 347.546875px;\">Intratumoral calcifications with vascular encasement</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4611","text":"A boy was brought by his p​arents, with complaints o‍f blood in his urine. A l‍eft-sided reniform mass w​as palpated. Further inve‍stigations were suggestiv‍e of Wilms tumor. Which o‌f the following is false ‌regarding this condition?‍","raw_text":"A boy was brought by his parents, with complaints of blood in his urine. A left-sided reniform mass was palpated. Further investigations were suggestive of Wilms tumor. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most cases occur in childr​en between 2-5 years of a‍ge","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is the most common prim​ary malignant renal tumor‍ of childhood","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Early metastasis occurs to​ the liver","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nephrogenic rest cells are​ precursor lesions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Early metastasis occurs to the liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Early metastasis occurs to the liver</strong></p><p>Metastasis to the <strong>lungs</strong> occurs early in <strong>Wilms tumor</strong>. Liver, bone, and brain metastasis is rare.</p>\n<p><strong>Wilms tumor</strong> or nephroblastoma is the most common <strong>primary malignant renal tumor</strong> of childhood. It is commonly seen in children between <strong>2-5 years </strong>of age. More than one-third of the cases contain precursor lesions known as <strong>nephrogenic rests</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Asymptomatic abdominal mass</strong> discovered incidentally is the most common presentation. Patients may also present with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Abdominal pain</li>\n<li>Gross painless <strong>hematuria</strong></li>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Most unilateral tumors are treated by <strong>chemotherapy</strong> (cyclophosphamide, vincristine, and dactinomycin) followed by nephrectomy. </p>\n<p><strong>Radical nephrectomy</strong> is the standard surgical procedure done. <strong>Partial nephrectomy</strong> is considered if all the following conditions are met:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The tumor involves only one pole of the kidney</li>\n<li>No evidence of collecting system or vascular involvement</li>\n<li>The involved kidney has an appreciable function</li>\n<li>There are clear margins between tumor and surrounding structures</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is done for children with<strong> bilateral</strong> disease or with a <strong>solitary kidney</strong>. Preoperative chemotherapy can be used in these cases to induce tumor shrinkage.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1825","text":"A mother notices a swellin​g in the abdomen of her 3‍-year-old child while bat‍hing him. He had a histor​y of hematuria two weeks ‍back which resolved spont‍aneously. On examination,‌ a right-sided reniform b‌allotable mass was found.‍ What all investigations ​will you do? CECT abdomen​ PET-CT 24-hour urinary V‌MA FNAC","raw_text":"<p>A mother notices a swelling in the abdomen of her 3-year-old child while bathing him. He had a history of hematuria two weeks back which resolved spontaneously. On examination, a right-sided reniform ballotable mass was found. What all investigations will you do?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>CECT abdomen</li>\n<li>PET-CT</li>\n<li>24-hour urinary VMA</li>\n<li>FNAC</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a​nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a​nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 ​and 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, ​3 and 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 2 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 2 and 3</strong></p><p>The clinical features of a ballotable renal mass with hematuria in a 3-year-old child are suggestive of <strong>Wilms tumor </strong>and FNAC is not done in this condition.</p>\n<p><strong>Investigations in suspected cases of Wilms tumor</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Laboratory study - shows hematuria, polycythemia, thrombocytosis, and elevated APTT</li>\n<li><strong>USG </strong>with doppler study of renal veins - for evaluation of the collecting system and to look for tumor thrombi in renal veins and inferior vena cava</li>\n<li><strong>CECT abdomen </strong>- to identify the extent of the disease, to access the contralateral kidney, and for metastasis</li>\n<li>MRI - not used routinely as it requires sedation</li>\n<li>Positron emission tomography(<strong>PET</strong>)<strong> scan</strong> - to identify regional spread and metastatic lesions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Diagnosis should be made by imaging studies and it is <strong>confirmed after nephrectomy</strong>. A <strong>biopsy is discouraged</strong> in this condition as it results in disease upstaging. Only in cases with <strong>atypical presentation</strong> such as older age, signs of infection, etc, or with unusual imaging findings a <strong>core needle biopsy</strong> via the posterior approach can be performed. </p>\n<p>Note: Neuroblastoma also presents similar to Wilms tumor. It is commonly seen in children of <strong>preschool age.</strong> Affected children commonly present with a <strong>palpable abdominal mass </strong>and the most common site of the tumor is the <strong>retroperitoneum.</strong> Therefore, this disease becomes an important differential diagnosis. So we should also test for the presence of <strong>vanillyl mandelic acid </strong>(VMA) in this patient to exclude this tumor.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3435","text":"Which of the following is ​the most important factor‍ in determining the progn‍osis of a child with Wilm​s tumor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most important factor in determining the prognosis of a child with Wilms tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Size of the abdominal mass​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of heterozygosity of ​chromosome 1p","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Histology o​f the tumour","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Age less than 1 year at pr​esentation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Histology of the tumour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d5c8539fe8e0490b83e57116630c354ex510x525.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Histology of the tumour</strong></p><p>The most important <strong>prognostic factor</strong> in Wilms tumor is the <strong>histology of the tumor</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Wilms tumor</strong> (WT) is characterized by abnormal proliferation of the metanephric blastema, with no differentiation into tubules or glomeruli. It usually arises from three tissue elements (<strong>triphasic</strong> tumor):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Blastema (poor prognosis if the blastemal component is more)</li>\n<li>Epithelium </li>\n<li>Stroma</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Wilms tumors <strong>lacking anaplasia</strong> (presence of atypical, polyploid mitotic figures, large nuclear size, and hyperchromasia) are designated as having <strong>“favorable histology\".</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3436","text":"Which of the following sta​ging systems is used for ‍staging of Wilms tumor in‍ a child posted for parti​al nephrectomy post neoad‍juvant chemotherapy?","raw_text":"Which of the following staging systems is used for staging of Wilms tumor in a child posted for partial nephrectomy post neoadjuvant chemotherapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Children's oncology group ​staging","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"International society of p​aediatric oncology stagin‍g","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"American joint committee o​n cancer staging","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Shimada​ staging","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. International society of paediatric oncology staging","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. International society of paediatric oncology staging</strong></p><p>International society of pediatric oncology <strong>(SIOP)</strong> is the <strong>post-chemotherapy</strong> staging system used for Wilms tumor. The SIOP approach involves the shrinkage of the tumor using preoperative chemotherapy. </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Children's oncology group</strong> (COG) staging is used for <strong>post-operative</strong> staging followed by chemotherapy. It was previously known as national Wilms tumor staging<strong> (NWTSG).</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>TNM staging (AJCC)</strong> is used for staging internal malignancies such as gastric, renal, breast, etc.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Shimada</strong> staging is used for neuroblastoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1824","text":"A child with a left-sided ​renal mass is suspected t‍o have Wilms tumor. CECT ‍also revealed a small mas​s on the right kidney. A ‍frozen section done intra‍operatively confirmed the‌ presence of bilateral Wi‌lms tumor. What is the st‍age of the tumor in this ​child?","raw_text":"A child with a left-sided renal mass is suspected to have Wilms tumor. CECT also revealed a small mass on the right kidney. A frozen section done intraoperatively confirmed the presence of bilateral Wilms tumor. What is the stage of the tumor in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stag​e II","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stag​e III","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stag​e IV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sta​ge V","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stage V","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stage V</strong></p><p><strong>Wilms</strong> tumor with <strong>bilateral</strong> involvement of <strong>kidneys</strong> is <strong>stage V</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Children's oncology group (COG) staging system</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 379px; width: 688px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 56.625px;\">Stage I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 616.375px;\">Tumor confined to the kidney and completely resected with negative lymph nodes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 56.625px;\">Stage II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 616.375px;\">Tumor extends beyond the kidney but is completely resected with negative margins and lymph nodes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 56.625px;\">Stage III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 616.375px;\">Residual tumor present following surgery confined to the abdomen, including gross or microscopic tumor.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 56.625px;\">Stage IV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 616.375px;\">Hematogenous metastases (lung, liver, bone, brain, etc.) or lymph node metastases outside the abdominopelvic region.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 56.625px;\"><strong>Stage V</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 616.375px;\">Metastasis to opposite kidney (<strong>bilateral</strong> renal involvement).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"AaM1ajou","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"40_vessels_upper_limb","title":"40.Vessels Upper Limb","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MC8865","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a branch of the 1st p​art of the subclavian art​ery?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a branch of the 1st part of the subclavian artery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vertebra‍l artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Internal tho‍racic artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thyrocerv‍ical trunk","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Costocerv‍ical trunk","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Costocervical trunk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Costocervical trunk</strong></p><p>The <strong>costocervical trunk</strong> is not a branch of the 1<sup>st</sup> part of the subclavian artery. It is a branch of the 2<sup>nd</sup> part.</p>\n<p><strong>Scalenus anterior divides</strong> the <strong>subclavian artery</strong> into 3 parts:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 168px; width: 319px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 34.2188px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 38px; height: 34.2188px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; height: 34.2188px;\"><strong>Branches</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 247px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 38px; height: 247px;\">1<sup>st</sup> part</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; height: 247px;\">\n<p><strong>Vertebral artery </strong>(Largest branch)</p>\n<p><strong>Internal thoracic artery</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superior epigastric artery</li>\n<li>Musculophrenic artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Thyrocervical trunk</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Suprascapular artery</li>\n<li>Inferior thyroid artery</li>\n<li>Transverse cervical artery</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 38px; height: 21px;\">2<sup>nd</sup> part</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; height: 21px;\"><strong>Costocervical trunk</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 38px; height: 21px;\">3<sup>rd</sup> part</td>\n<td style=\"width: 10px; height: 21px;\"><strong>Dorsal scapular artery</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0673","text":"Which of the following mus‍cle divides the axillary ​artery into 3 parts?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscle divides the axillary artery into 3 parts?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pectoral‍is major","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Teres‍ major","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pectoral‍is minor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Teres‍ minor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pectoralis minor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e40c88a2820c423284af772d0cdf1208x1200x1801.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pectoralis minor</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCX88037099\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX88037099\">T</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX88037099\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX88037099\">he <strong>axillary artery</strong> is <strong>divided</strong> i</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCX88037099\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX88037099\">nto 3 parts by the <strong>pectoralis m</strong></span></span><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCX88037099\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX88037099\">in</span></span></strong><span class=\"TextRun SCX88037099\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX88037099\"><strong>or</strong> muscle.</span></span></p>\n<p>The axillary artery is the<strong> </strong>continuation of the subclavian artery. It extends from the outer border of the 1st rib to the lower border of teres major.</p>\n<p><strong>Pectoralis minor</strong> divides the artery into <strong>3 parts</strong>:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 124px; width: 366px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 72px; height: 36px;\"> <strong>Part</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 136px; text-align: center; height: 36px;\"><strong>Relation to Pectoralis minor</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px; text-align: center; height: 36px;\"><strong>Branches</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 72px; height: 18px;\">1st part</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136px; height: 18px;\">Lies proximally</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Superior thoracic artery</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 72px; height: 18px;\">2nd part</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136px; height: 18px;\">Lies posteriorly</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Thoracoacromial trunk</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 104px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 72px; height: 104px;\">3rd part</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136px; height: 104px;\">Lies laterally</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px; height: 104px;\">\n<p><strong>Subscapular artery</strong></p>\n<p>Anterior circumflex humeral artery</p>\n<p>Posterior circumflex humeral artery</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>After passing the <strong>lower margin </strong>of the <strong>teres major,</strong> it becomes the <strong>brachial artery.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0674","text":"A patient with anterior di‍slocation of the shoulder​ joint is found to have c​ompression of the axillar​y artery. However, the bl‌ood supply of the arm see​ms to be non-compromised.​ Anastomoses between whic​h of the following vessel‌s maintain this circulati‌on?","raw_text":"A patient with anterior dislocation of the shoulder joint is found to have compression of the axillary artery. However, the blood supply of the arm seems to be non-compromised. Anastomoses between which of the following vessels maintain this circulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3rd part of subclavian and‍ 3rd part of axillary art​ery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1st part of subclavian and‍ 1st part of axillary art​ery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3rd part of subclavian and‍ 1st part of axillary art​ery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1st part of subclavian and‍ 3rd part of axillary art​ery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1st part of subclavian and 3rd part of axillary artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cb067e691d7247bbbf294c16082a5265x600x846.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1st part of subclavian and 3rd part of axillary artery</strong></p><p><strong>Anastomosis around</strong> the <strong>scapula</strong> maintains <strong>circulation</strong> to the <strong>upper limb</strong> if the blood supply is interrupted (fractures or dislocation of the shoulder joint). Anastomosis occurs between the <strong>1<sup>st</sup> part</strong> of the <strong>subclavian</strong> artery and the <strong>3<sup>rd</sup> part</strong> of the <strong>axillary</strong> artery. These arteries also anastomose with intercostal arteries.</p>\n<p>The axial artery of the upper limb is the <strong>subclavian artery.</strong></p>\n<p>Anastomosis around the scapula:</p>\n<table border=\"0\" style=\"width: 663px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 25.4062px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 25.4062px; width: 174px;\"><strong>Level of anastomosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 25.4062px; width: 308px;\"><strong>Arteries involved</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 25.4062px; width: 165px;\"><strong>Branch of</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"3\" style=\"height: 45px; width: 174px;\"><strong>Body of Scapula</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 15px; width: 308px;\">Suprascapular artery</td>\n<td style=\"height: 15px; width: 165px;\">Thyrocervical trunk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 15px; width: 308px;\">Deep branch of transverse cervical artery  </td>\n<td style=\"height: 15px; width: 165px;\">Thyrocervical trunk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 15px; width: 308px;\">Circumflex scapular artery</td>\n<td style=\"height: 15px; width: 165px;\">Subscapular artery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"height: 42px; width: 174px;\"><strong>Inferior angle of scapula</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 27px; width: 308px;\">Deep branch of transverse cervical artery</td>\n<td style=\"height: 27px; width: 165px;\">Thyrocervical trunk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 15px; width: 308px;\">Subscapular artery</td>\n<td style=\"height: 15px; width: 165px;\">3rd part axillary artery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"3\" style=\"height: 45px; width: 174px;\"><strong>Acromion process</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 15px; width: 308px;\">Thoraco-acromial artery (Acromial branch)</td>\n<td style=\"height: 15px; width: 165px;\">2nd part axillary artery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 15px; width: 308px;\">Suprascapular artery (Acromial branch)</td>\n<td style=\"height: 15px; width: 165px;\">Thyrocervical trunk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 15px; width: 308px;\">Posterior circumflex humeral artery (Acromial branch)</td>\n<td style=\"height: 15px; width: 165px;\">3rd part axillary artery</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0675","text":"A 57-year-old man presents‍ with pain, numbness, and​ fatigue of the right arm​. Angiography reveals dec​reased flow through the v‌essels anastomosing near ​the surgical neck of the ​humerus. The pathology is​ likely to be present in ‌which of the following ar‌teries?","raw_text":"A 57-year-old man presents with pain, numbness, and fatigue of the right arm. Angiography reveals decreased flow through the vessels anastomosing near the surgical neck of the humerus. The pathology is likely to be present in which of the following arteries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1st part of axillary arter‍y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1st part of subclavian art‍ery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3rd part of axillary arter‍y","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3rd part of subclavian art‍ery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3rd part of axillary artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4997aa7f6aa34e1abcf44cfbced9ba76x600x592.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3rd part of axillary artery</strong></p><p>The branches of the <strong>third part</strong> of the<strong> axillary artery </strong>supply the <strong>surgical neck</strong> of the <strong>humerus.</strong> Thrombus in this part will cause decreased supply in its branches - the<strong> anterior</strong> and<strong> posterior circumflex humeral arteries.</strong></p>\n<p>Branches of 3<sup>rd</sup> part of the axillary artery are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior</strong> <strong>circumflex </strong>humeral arteries</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior circumflex </strong>humeral arteries</li>\n<li>Subscapular artery (not involved in the anastomosis)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Fracture </strong>of the <strong>surgical neck</strong> is the commonest site among all proximal humerus fractures due to its inherent weakness. Fracture at this site may cause injury to the posterior circumflex humeral artery and the axillary nerve that accompanies it.</p>\n<p><strong>Positional compression</strong> of the <strong>axillary artery </strong>is seen in athletes who are involved with throwing movement, as in baseball players. Repeated movement of the humerus may cause compression of the distal part of the axillary artery. This may lead to thrombosis or aneurysm resulting in arm ischemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0676","text":"Angiography of the upper l‍imb vessels in a patient ​shows a blockage in the b​rachial artery. All of th​e following vessels will ‌have decreased flow, exce​pt:","raw_text":"Angiography of the upper limb vessels in a patient shows a blockage in the brachial artery. All of the following vessels will have decreased flow, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Profunda br‍achii artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior ulnar collateral ‍artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior ulnar collateral ‍artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior circumflex humera‍l artery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anterior circumflex humeral artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e624e43c20f744ea9806489d8df72212x1280x2308.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anterior circumflex humeral artery</strong></p><p>The <strong>anterior circumflex humeral artery</strong> is a branch of the <strong>axillary artery</strong>. Hence, it is unlikely to have a decreased flow due to blockage in the brachial artery. </p>\n<p>The <strong>brachial artery</strong> has <strong>3 branches</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Profunda brachii</strong> (accompanies radial nerve in the spiral groove) gives off nutrient artery to the humerus and an ascending branch which anastomoses with posterior circumflex humeral artery. It then divides into: \n<ul>\n<li>Anterior descending artery (Radial collateral artery)  </li>\n<li>Posterior descending artery (Middle collateral artery) </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Superior ulnar collateral</strong> artery (accompanies ulnar nerve)</li>\n<li><strong>Inferior ulnar collateral</strong> (supratrochlear) artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows branches of the brachial artery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0677","text":"The interosseous recurrent‍ artery is a branch of wh​ich of the following arte​ries?","raw_text":"The interosseous recurrent artery is a branch of which of the following arteries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior Interosseous arte‍ry","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior Interosseous art‍ery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Common Interosseous artery‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radial‍ artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posterior Interosseous artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4cb2946d69c0409f8fa5c46353b18e53x1280x2211.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posterior Interosseous artery</strong></p><p>The<strong> interosseous recurrent</strong> artery is a <strong>branch</strong> of the<strong> posterior interosseous</strong> artery.</p>\n<p>The <strong>posterior interosseous artery</strong> originates from the common interosseous branch of the<strong> ulnar artery. </strong>It contributes a branch, the<strong> recurrent interosseous artery</strong> to the vascular network around the elbow joint.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0678","text":"Which of the following str‍uctures pass through the ​opening marked in the dia​gram below?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures pass through the opening marked in the diagram below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior interosseous art‍ery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deep branch of radial nerv‍e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior interosseous arte‍ry","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Recurrent interosseous art‍ery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anterior interosseous artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6ea682ee41484fab999638453200eb3c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/44d570d0dbfc438db9a689df4976e1a8x1280x1689.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anterior interosseous artery</strong></p><p>The <strong>anterior interosseous artery</strong> passes through the opening in the interosseous membrane.</p>\n<p>The anterior interosseous artery passes distally along the anterior aspect of the interosseous membrane and supplies muscles of the deep compartment of the <strong>forearm, radius,</strong> and ulna.</p>\n<p>Perforating the interosseous membrane in the distal forearm, the anterior interosseous artery terminates by joining the posterior interosseous artery.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows anterior interosseous artery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0104","text":"Identify the structure mar‍ked in the image given be​low:","raw_text":"Identify the structure marked in the image given below:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ulnar‍ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ulnar ‍artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Superficial ‍radial artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Median‍ nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ulnar artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e5ba282a2a334fe4bfcc20bdd0329cc9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6938f92ee135453f981eae332e7aad28x1280x1223.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0c83938fe6b4468fb8a52e7e5d31db66x1280x1758.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ulnar artery</strong></p><p>The structure marked in the image is the <strong>ulnar artery,</strong> seen contributing to the superficial palmar arch.</p>\n<p>It <strong>passes deep to</strong> the <strong>Guyon's canal</strong> in the wrist and contributes to the superficial and deep <strong>palmar arch.</strong> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0679","text":"An intern was asked to col‍lect a sample for arteria​l blood gas analysis. Bef​ore puncturing the artery​, she performed a modifie‌d Allen's test to assess ​the circulation. However,​ the test result was nega​tive. What is the inferen‌ce?","raw_text":"An intern was asked to collect a sample for arterial blood gas analysis. Before puncturing the artery, she performed a modified Allen's test to assess the circulation. However, the test result was negative. What is the inference?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radial circulation is inad‍equate, hence, ulnar arte​ry should be punctured","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Radial circulation is inad‍equate, hence, ulnar arte​ry should not be puncture​d","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ulnar circulation is inade‍quate, hence, radial arte​ry should be punctured","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ulnar circulation is inade‍quate, hence, radial arte​ry should not be puncture​d","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ulnar circulation is inadequate, hence, radial artery should not be punctured","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eb9e81a3b7b344fba9d7dc2b43146a24x1280x3192.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ulnar circulation is inadequate, hence, radial artery should not be punctured</strong></p><p>A <strong>negative</strong> result of the <strong>modified Allens test</strong> implies that the <strong>ulnar circulation</strong> is<strong> inadequate, hence</strong>, the <strong>radial artery</strong> should<strong> not</strong> be<strong> punctured. </strong>In such cases, the modified Allen's test is performed on the other hand, which is likely to have a normal circulation.</p>\n<p>The integrity of the <strong>arcuate system</strong> of the<strong> hand</strong> is assessed by modified Allen's test which is performed routinely before instrumentation of the radial artery. </p>\n<p>The test determines the integrity between superficial and deep palmar arches and the <strong>patency</strong> of the <strong>radial</strong> and <strong>ulnar arteries</strong> at the <strong>wrist.</strong> Each one of the arteries is compressed at a time. The individual artery which is sufficient to maintain the arterial supply to the hand in isolation is noted.</p>\n<p>Procedure:   </p>\n<ol>\n<li>The patient makes a tight fist, examiner digitally compresses both ulnar and radial arteries.</li>\n<li>The patient opens the fist to reveal the pale palm.</li>\n<li>Ulnar artery compression is removed, <strong>palm</strong> turns <strong>red</strong> including the thumb zone <strong>(positive</strong> <strong>modified</strong> <strong>Allen's test).</strong> </li>\n<li>If blood<strong> fails to return to </strong>the<strong> palm </strong>including the thumb zone, it reveals the anastomosis between the 2 palmar arches is inadequate <strong>(negative</strong> <strong>modified</strong> <strong>Allen's test).</strong> </li>\n</ol>\n<p>If the hand does not flush within 5–15 seconds (modified Allen's test negative), it indicates that ulnar circulation is inadequate or nonexistent. In this situation, the radial artery supplying arterial blood to that hand should not be punctured.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0680","text":"A 52-year-old man with end‍-stage renal disease was ​planning to undergo hemod​ialysis. An arteriovenous​ fistula was created on h‌is forearm. This procedur​e is done mainly to achie​ve a minimum venous flow ​rate of:","raw_text":"A 52-year-old man with end-stage renal disease was planning to undergo hemodialysis. An arteriovenous fistula was created on his forearm. This procedure is done mainly to achieve a minimum venous flow rate of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50 m‍l/min","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"600 m‍l/min","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"750 m‍l/min","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"250 m‍l/min","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 250 ml/min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c8528949cd154db59464d75f9371ad13x1280x2065.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/629712899f3e4036ac7511ef2524a703x1280x2182.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 250 ml/min</strong></p><p>The process of <strong>dialysis</strong> occurs over many hours and requires considerable <strong>flow rates of 250-500 mL/minute.</strong></p>\n<p>As no vein in the peripheral limbs has such high flow, a surgical procedure <strong>(AV fistulas)</strong> is necessary to create such a system.</p>\n<p>Two types of arteriovenous fistulas:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>radial artery </strong>is anastomosed to the<strong> cephalic vein</strong> in the<strong> wrist </strong>(most common)</li>\n<li>The <strong>brachial artery </strong>is anastomosed to the<strong> cephalic vein </strong>at the<strong> elbow</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows radiocephalic and brachiocephalic fistula respectively.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8864","text":"Palmar venous network in t‍he palm of hand gives ris​e to which of the followi​ng veins?","raw_text":"Palmar venous network in the palm of hand gives rise to which of the following veins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cephal‍ic vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basili‍c vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Median anteb‍rachial vein","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Median cu‍bital vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Median antebrachial vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3a9a9ad7f5af445a9926a584df2b8b04x1280x2375.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7ff861ae6c7041da8b9951ce253f81d7x600x859.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Median antebrachial vein</strong></p><p>The<strong> palmar venous network</strong> in the palm of the hand gives rise to the<strong> median antebrachial vein.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The median antebrachial vein is a <strong>superficial vein</strong> of the anterior forearm. It drains the superficial structures of the forearm and wrist. It ascends to the cubital fossa where it drains into the medial cephalic and median basilic veins in an inverted Y-shaped configuration.</p>\n<p>The <strong>dorsal venous network</strong> on the dorsum of the hand gives rise to<strong> cephalic</strong> and<strong> basilic veins </strong>as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0681","text":"You are asked to send a bl‍ood sample for complete b​lood count analysis of a ​clinically anemic patient​. Sampling from which of ‌the following vein is pre​ferred?","raw_text":"You are asked to send a blood sample for complete blood count analysis of a clinically anemic patient. Sampling from which of the following vein is preferred?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cephal‍ic vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antecubi‍tal vein","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Basili‍c vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Brachi‍al vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Antecubital vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c4c990f5dcd143339838cb364601946fx1280x2375.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Antecubital vein</strong></p><p>The<strong> antecubital vein </strong>(median cubital vein) is usually the vein <strong>preferred</strong> for direct blood investigations. </p>\n<p>The median cubital vein<strong> </strong>connects the cephalic vein to the basilic vein at the roof of the cubital fossa. It <strong>lies superficial</strong> to bicipital aponeurosis and hence is easily accessible. It is used for<strong> IV injections, blood transfusions,</strong> and<strong> blood withdrawal.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: The<strong> cephalic vein </strong>begins as a radial continuation of the dorsal venous arch, runs on the<strong> roof </strong>of the<strong> anatomical snuffbox. </strong>It <strong>ends </strong>in the<strong> axillary vein. </strong>It is <strong>connected</strong> to the<strong> basilic vein</strong> by the<strong> median cubital vein. </strong>It is the preferred site for short-term <strong>IV cannula.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: The <strong>basilic vein </strong>drains the ulnar end of the dorsal venous arch, passing on the <strong>medial aspect</strong> of the forearm. It pierces <strong>deep fascia</strong> at the elbow and joins venae comitantes of brachial artery forming the<strong> axillary vein</strong>, at the lower border of teres major.</p>\n<p>Option D: The <strong>brachial vein</strong> is a <strong>deep vein</strong> of the forearm which lies on either side of the brachial artery. It is formed by joining the venae comitantes of radial and ulnar arteries. It joins the axillary vein at the lower border of the teres major.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0682","text":"A patient is diagnosed wit‍h superficial thrombophle​bitis of the cephalic vei​n. This vein drains into ​which of the following ve‌ssels?","raw_text":"A patient is diagnosed with superficial thrombophlebitis of the cephalic vein. This vein drains into which of the following vessels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brachi‍al vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subclav‍ian vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Axilla‍ry vein","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Basili‍c vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Axillary vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4fddb885444f41d3bb7352ddcc587aafx1280x1810.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Axillary vein</strong></p><p>The <strong>cephalic vein drains into</strong> the<strong> axillary vein.</strong></p>\n<p>The blood in the cephalic vein is drained to the axillary vein by 2 routes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Directly after piercing clavipectoral fascia i.e., Cephalic vein ⇒ Axillary vein</li>\n<li>Some amount of venous blood is drained into the basilic vein through the median cubital vein i.e., Cephalic vein ⇒ Median cubital vein ⇒ Basilic vein ⇒ Axillary vein </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0683","text":"Which of the following is ‍a pre-axial vein?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a pre-axial vein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cephal‍ic vein","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Basili‍c vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Axilla‍ry vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subclav‍ian vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cephalic vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/27e6fc8ba74c4af5bab5c59c8515300ax1280x1810.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cephalic vein</strong></p><p>The<strong> cephalic vein </strong>is a <strong>preaxial</strong> vein.</p>\n<p>The pre-axial and post-axial borders are decided on the basis of their placement, with respect to the axis of the limb.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 318px; width: 398px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 74px; height: 46px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.594px; text-align: center; height: 46px;\"><strong>Pre-axial border</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 152.406px; text-align: center; height: 46px;\"><strong>Post-axial border</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54.125px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 74px; height: 54.125px;\">Embryo</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.594px; height: 54.125px;\">Cranially-placed limb border</td>\n<td style=\"width: 152.406px; height: 54.125px;\">Caudally-placed limb border</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 205px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 74px; height: 205px;\">Adults</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.594px; height: 205px;\">\n<p>Upper limb: <strong>Outer</strong> border (radial aspect)<br/>• <strong>Cephalic vein</strong></p>\n<p>Lower limb: Inner border <br/>• Greater saphenous vein</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 152.406px; height: 205px;\">\n<p>Upper limb: <strong>Inner</strong> border (ulnar aspect)<br/>• <strong>Basilic vein</strong></p>\n<p>Lower limb: Outer border <br/>• Lesser saphenous vein</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>In the <strong>upper limb,</strong> the <strong>pre-axial</strong> vein of the dorsal venous arch becomes the <strong>cephalic</strong> vein and the <strong>postaxial</strong> vein becomes the <strong>basilic</strong> vein.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0685","text":"A 40-year-old woman with b‍reast carcinoma was plann​ed for a mastectomy. Duri​ng the procedure, a blue ​dye was injected to map t‌he flow of lymphatic drai​nage in the axillary lymp​h nodes. Which of the fol​lowing will receive lymph‌atic drainage from the re‌st of the axillary lymph ‌node groups?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman with breast carcinoma was planned for a mastectomy. During the procedure, a blue dye was injected to map the flow of lymphatic drainage in the axillary lymph nodes. Which of the following will receive lymphatic drainage from the rest of the axillary lymph node groups?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cen‍tral","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Api‍cal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lat‍eral","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Post‍erior","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Apical","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9eab4e9aafd7413f90ab6a40e737740dx600x672.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Apical</strong></p><p>The<strong> apical group</strong> of lymph nodes receives lymphatic drainage from the rest of the axillary lymph node groups. Hence, they are known as <strong>terminal axillary lymph nodes.</strong> </p>\n<p>The lymphatic drainage of axillary nodes is given below:</p>\n<p>Anterior, posterior and lateral groups ⇒ Central group ⇒ <strong>Apical group</strong></p>\n<p>The apical group then drains into the supraclavicular lymph nodes.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0684","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements regarding axillar​y lymph nodes is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding axillary lymph nodes is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior group lies along‍ subscapular vessels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral group lies along l‍ateral thoracic vessels","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Apical group lies along ax‍illary vessels","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Axillary lymph nodes drain‍s trunk above the umbilic​us","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lateral group lies along lateral thoracic vessels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/06713116171c4c3297e01b4912fe57f0x600x672.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lateral group lies along lateral thoracic vessels</strong></p><p>The <strong>lateral</strong> group of <strong>axillary</strong> nodes lies <strong>posteromedial</strong> to the <strong>axillary vein.</strong> The anterior group of axillary nodes lies near the lateral thoracic vessels.</p>\n<p><strong>Axillary</strong> lymph<strong> nodes</strong> collectively drain the <strong>entire upper limb, breast,</strong> and <strong>trunk above</strong> the <strong>umbilicus.</strong></p>\n<p>Axillary nodes can be divided into<strong> 5</strong> <strong>groups</strong> namely:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lateral/ humeral</li>\n<li>Anterior/ pectoral</li>\n<li>Posterior/ subscapular</li>\n<li>Central</li>\n<li>Apical</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<table style=\"height: 565px; width: 538px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.1875px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 44.1875px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 44.1875px;\"><strong>Location</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.375px; height: 44.1875px;\"><strong>Afferents</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.625px; height: 44.1875px;\"><strong>Efferents</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 77px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 77px;\"><strong>Humeral/ Lateral group</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 77px;\">Posteromedial to <strong>axillary vein</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.375px; height: 77px;\">Whole limb except vessels accompanying cephalic veins</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.625px; height: 77px;\">• Central nodes <br/>• Apical nodes <br/>• Inferior deep cervical nodes </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 108px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 108px;\"><strong>Pectoral/ Anterior group</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 108px;\">Along <strong>lateral thoracic vessels</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.375px; height: 108px;\">Skin and muscles of supra-umbilical anterolateral body wall and breast</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.625px; height: 108px;\">• Central nodes <br/>• Apical nodes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 144px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 144px;\"><strong>Subscapular/ Posterior group</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 144px;\">Along the <strong>subscapular vessels</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.375px; height: 144px;\">Skin and muscles of the inferior posterior region of the neck, dorsal aspect of the trunk up to iliac crest</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.625px; height: 144px;\">• Central nodes <br/>• Apical nodes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Central group</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 54px;\">Embedded in the <strong>axillary pad of fat</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.375px; height: 54px;\">All preceding groups</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.625px; height: 54px;\">• Apical group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 216px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 216px;\"><strong>Apical group</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px; height: 216px;\">Deep to clavipectoral fascia, along the<strong> axillary vessels</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.375px; height: 216px;\">Those accompanying cephalic vein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.625px; height: 216px;\">Unite as subclavian trunk, right trunk drains into Jugulo-subclavian venous junction and left trunk ends in thoracic duct</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"W71KkbZj","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"40_light__sound_and_radiation","title":"40.Light, Sound And Radiation","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MB7640","text":"Which of the following is ‍incorrect about the requi​rements of good lighting?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about the requirements of good lighting?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colour of light should app‍roximate daylight","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sharp shadows should be ab‍sent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ceilings and roofs must ha‍ve a 50% reflection facto​r","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Glare shoul‍d be absent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ceilings and roofs must have a 50% reflection factor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ceilings and roofs must have a 50% reflection factor</strong></p><p>For good lighting in a room,<strong> ceilings</strong> and roofs must have an<strong> 80% reflection factor</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>requirements of good lighting</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sufficient light to prevent eye strain. An illumination of 15 to 20-foot candles is accepted for satisfactory vision.</li>\n<li>Distribution of light must be uniform with the same intensity over the whole field of work</li>\n<li><strong>Absence of glare</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Absence of sharp shadows</strong></li>\n<li>The source of light should be constant and steady</li>\n<li>The <strong>colour</strong> of light should<strong> approximate</strong> the colour of <strong>daylight</strong></li>\n<li>Ceilings and roofs must have an 80% reflection factor. Walls must have 50 to 60%, furniture 30 to 40% and floors 15 to 20%.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7641","text":"What is the SI unit for lu‍minous intensity?","raw_text":"What is the SI unit for luminous intensity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can‍dela","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lu‍men","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"L‍ux","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lam‍bert","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Candela","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Candela</strong></p><p>The SI unit for <strong>luminous intensity</strong> is <strong>candela.</strong></p>\n<p>The various units used for measuring light are given below.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 469.455px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\"><strong>Quantity Measured</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\"><strong>SI Unit</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.455px;\"><strong>Other Units Used</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\"><strong>Luminous intensity</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Brightness of point source</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\"><strong>Candela</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.455px;\">Candle power</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\"><strong>Luminous flux</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Flow of light</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\"><strong>Lumen</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.455px;\">_____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\"><strong>Illumination</strong> (Illuminance)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Amount of light reaching a surface</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\"><strong>Lux</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.455px;\">Foot candle,  Phot or Lumen/cm<span style=\"font-size: 11.6667px;\">2</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\"><strong>Luminance</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Amount of light reflected from a surface</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\"><strong>Lambert</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.455px;\">Foot lambert, Candles/cm<span style=\"font-size: 11.6667px;\">2</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7642","text":"Correctly match the follow‍ing variables with their ​SI unit. Variable SI Unit​ 1. Luminous intensity A.​ Lumen 2. Luminous flux B‍. Candela 3. Illumination​ C. Lambert 4. Luminance ‌D. Lux","raw_text":"<p>Correctly match the following variables with their SI unit.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 251px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123.203px;\">Variable</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.797px;\">SI Unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123.203px;\">1. Luminous intensity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.797px;\">A. Lumen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123.203px;\">2. Luminous flux</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.797px;\">B. Candela</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123.203px;\">3. Illumination</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.797px;\">C. Lambert</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123.203px;\">4. Luminance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.797px;\">D. Lux</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-A, 2-B,‍ 3-C, 4-D","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-B, 2-A,‍ 3-D, 4-C","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-D, 2-C,‍ 3-A, 4-B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-B, 2-C,‍ 3-D, 4-A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C</strong></p><p>The correctly matched pairs are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167.469px;\"><strong>Variable</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.531px;\"><strong>SI Unit</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167.469px;\">Luminous intensity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.531px;\">Candela</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167.469px;\">Luminous flux</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.531px;\">Lumen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167.469px;\">Illumination</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.531px;\">Lux</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167.469px;\">Luminence</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211.531px;\">Lambert</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The various units used for measuring light are given below.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 469.455px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\"><strong>Quantity Measured</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\"><strong>SI Unit</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.455px;\"><strong>Other Units Used</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\"><strong>Luminous intensity</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Power of light source</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\"><strong>Candela</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.455px;\">Candle power</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\"><strong>Luminous flux</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Flow of light</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\"><strong>Lumen</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.455px;\">_____</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\"><strong>Illumination</strong> (Illuminance)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Amount of light reaching a surface</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\"><strong>Lux</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.455px;\">Foot candle,  Phot or Lumen/cm<span style=\"font-size: 11.6667px;\">2</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 107px;\"><strong>Luminance </strong>(Brightness)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Amount of light reflected from a surface</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px;\"><strong>Lambert</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.455px;\">Foot lambert, Candles/cm<span style=\"font-size: 11.6667px;\">2</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7643","text":"What is the basic minimum ‍illumination for satisfac​tory vision?","raw_text":"What is the basic minimum illumination for satisfactory vision?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 – 12 fo‍ot candles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"15 – 20 fo‍ot candles","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"20 – 30 fo‍ot candles","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30 – 40 fo‍ot candles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 15 – 20 foot candles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 15 – 20 foot candles</strong></p><p>The <strong>basic</strong> <strong>minimum illumination </strong>for satisfactory vision is accepted to be <strong>15 - 20 foot candles</strong>. The illumination requirements vary from as little as 5 foot candles in stairways and corridors to 100 foot candles in some industries.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7644","text":"Which of the following is ‍correct about daylight fa​ctor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is correct about daylight factor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"D.F. = Instantaneous illum‍ination outdoors x 100/ S​imultaneously occurring i​llumination indoors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"D.F. = Instantaneous illum‍ination indoors x 100/ Si​multaneously occurring il​lumination outdoors","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"D.F. = Delayed illuminatio‍n indoors x 100/ Simultan​eously occurring illumina​tion outdoors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"D.F. = Delayed illuminatio‍n outdoors x 100/ Simulta​neously occurring illumin​ation indoors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. D.F. = Instantaneous illumination indoors x 100/ Simultaneously occurring illumination outdoors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/877c87c7350c40c187ec07fa677f8a16x1280x668.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. D.F. = Instantaneous illumination indoors x 100/ Simultaneously occurring illumination outdoors</strong></p><p></p>\n<p><strong>Daylight factor </strong>(D.F.) is the <strong>ratio</strong> of the <strong>illumination at </strong>a given<strong> point to</strong> <strong>illumination </strong>at a point<strong> exposed </strong>simultaneously to the<strong> whole </strong>hemisphere of the<strong> sky</strong> (taken as 500-foot candles) <strong>excluding direct sunlight</strong>.</p>\n<p>The D.F. is used for the <strong>measurement of daylight</strong>. It is <strong>determined by</strong> a modified photo-electric meter called <strong>daylight factor meter.</strong></p>\n<p>In living rooms, the daylight factor should be at least 8% and in kitchens, it should be 10%.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7645","text":"You have been requested to‍ inspect the working cond​itions of an office for c​lerks. What is the standa​rd illumination recommend‍ed for such a space?","raw_text":"You have been requested to inspect the working conditions of an office for clerks. What is the standard illumination recommended for such a space?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"100‍ lux","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1100‍ lux","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"900‍ lux","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"400‍ lux","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 400 lux","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 400 lux</strong></p><p>The standard illumination recommended for<strong> general office work</strong> is <strong>400 lux</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding ng-scope\">The following standards have been recommended by the Illuminating Engineer Society.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Visual Task</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Illumination</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Casual reading</td>\n<td>100 lux</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>General office work</td>\n<td>400 lux</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fine assembly</td>\n<td>900 lux</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Very severe tasks</td>\n<td>1300 – 2000 lux</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Watchmaking</td>\n<td>2000 – 3000 lux</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7646","text":"A watchmaker presents with‍ symptoms of eye strain. ​What is the optimum illum​ination recommended at hi​s workplace?","raw_text":"A watchmaker presents with symptoms of eye strain. What is the optimum illumination recommended at his workplace?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2000 – ‍3000 lux","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1300 – ‍2000 lux","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1000 – ‍1200 lux","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3000 – ‍3800 lux","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2000 – 3000 lux","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2000 – 3000 lux</strong></p><p>The optimum illumination recommended for <strong>watchmaking</strong> is <strong>2000 – 3000 lux</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following standards have been recommended by the Illuminating Engineer Society.</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Visual Task</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Illumination </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Casual reading</td>\n<td>100 lux</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>General office work</td>\n<td>400 lux</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fine assembly</td>\n<td>900 lux</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Very severe tasks</td>\n<td>1300 – 2000 lux</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Watchmaking</td>\n<td>2000 – 3000 lux</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7649","text":"What is the intensity of s‍ound produced during whis​pering?","raw_text":"What is the intensity of sound produced during whispering?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 - ‍20 dB","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"30 - ‍40 dB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"20 - ‍30 dB","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"0 - ‍10 dB","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 20 - 30 dB","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 20 - 30 dB</strong></p><p><strong>Whispering</strong> produces a sound of <strong>20 – 30 dB.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Loudness</strong> or intensity depends upon the amplitude of vibrations. It is expressed in <strong>decibels</strong> (dB). The intensity of sounds produced during various activities is listed below.</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<table style=\"width: 257px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161.21875px;\"><strong>Source</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.78125px;\"><strong>Loudness</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161.21875px;\">Whispering</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.78125px;\">20 – 30 dB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161.21875px;\">Normal conversation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.78125px;\">60 – 65 dB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161.21875px;\">Heavy street traffic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.78125px;\">60 – 80 dB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161.21875px;\">Boiler factories</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.78125px;\">120 dB</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The frequency of sound is expressed in<strong> hertz </strong>(Hz). The <strong>human ear</strong> can <strong>hear</strong> frequencies from about <strong>20 to 20,000 Hz</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7650","text":"What is the upper limit of‍ daily exposure to noise ​which people can tolerate​ without hearing damage?","raw_text":"What is the upper limit of daily exposure to noise which people can tolerate without hearing damage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"95‍ dB","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"65‍ dB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"75‍ dB","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"85‍ dB","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 85 dB","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 85 dB</strong></p><p>The upper <strong>limit of daily exposure </strong>to noise which people can tolerate without hearing damage is <strong>85 dB</strong>. This is the <strong>maximum</strong> <strong>acceptable noise level</strong>.</p>\n<p>It has been observed that the human ear responds in a non-uniform way to different sound-pressure levels. A <strong>weighting curve</strong>, called <strong>curve A</strong> has been constructed which takes into account the<strong> subjective effects of sound</strong>. Sound-pressure levels are therefore expressed in <strong>dBA</strong> because this <strong>reflects</strong> the <strong>perception</strong> of that sound emission <strong>by the</strong> <strong>normal human ear</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<p><strong>Acceptable noise levels </strong>in various surroundings are listed below.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Locality</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Place</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Acceptable noise levels (dBA)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Residential</td>\n<td>Bedroom</td>\n<td>25</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td>Living room</td>\n<td>40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Commercial</td>\n<td>Office</td>\n<td>35 – 45</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td>Conference</td>\n<td>40 – 45</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td>Restaurants</td>\n<td>40 – 60</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Industrial</td>\n<td>Workshop</td>\n<td>40 – 60</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td>Laboratory</td>\n<td>40 – 50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Educational</td>\n<td>Classroom</td>\n<td>30 – 40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td>Library</td>\n<td>35 – 40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hospitals</td>\n<td>Wards</td>\n<td>20 – 35</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7651","text":"What is the acceptable noi‍se level in a hospital wa​rd?","raw_text":"What is the acceptable noise level in a hospital ward?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"40 – ‍50 dBA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"35 – ‍40 dBA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"50 – ‍60 dBA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"20 – ‍35 dBA","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 20 – 35 dBA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 20 – 35 dBA</strong></p><p>The acceptable noise level in a <strong>hospital ward</strong> is <strong>20 – 35 dBA</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<p><strong>Acceptable noise levels </strong>in various surroundings are listed below.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Locality</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Place</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Acceptable noise levels (dBA)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Residential</td>\n<td>Bedroom</td>\n<td>25</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td>Living room</td>\n<td>40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Commercial</td>\n<td>Office</td>\n<td>35 – 45</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td>Conference</td>\n<td>40 – 45</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td>Restaurants</td>\n<td>40 – 60</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Industrial</td>\n<td>Workshop</td>\n<td>40 – 60</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td>Laboratory</td>\n<td>40 – 50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Educational</td>\n<td>Classroom</td>\n<td>30 – 40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td>Library</td>\n<td>35 – 40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hospitals</td>\n<td>Wards</td>\n<td>20 – 35</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7652","text":"A steel plant worker prese‍nted with sudden onset of​ bilateral hearing loss a​fter hearing a blast in t​he plant. On examination,‍ both his tympanic membra​nes were ruptured. What w‌ould have been the minimu‍m intensity of the blast ​to cause this injury?","raw_text":"A  steel plant worker presented with sudden onset of bilateral hearing loss after hearing a blast in the plant. On examination, both his tympanic membranes were ruptured. What would have been the minimum intensity of the blast to cause this injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"90‍ dB","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"110‍ dB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"130‍ dB","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"160‍ dB","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 160 dB","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 160 dB</strong></p><p>A minimum intensity of <strong>160 dB</strong> can lead to <strong>rupture </strong>of the<strong> tympanic membrane</strong>, resulting in hearing loss.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7653","text":"A 32-year-old patient pres‍ents with complaints of w​histling and buzzing in b​oth his ears since yester​day. He gives a history o‍f being exposed to contin​uous noise due to constru‌ction near his house. At ‍what intensity of noise d​o such conditions begin t​o occur?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old patient presents with complaints of whistling and buzzing in both his ears since yesterday. He gives a history of being exposed to continuous noise due to construction near his house. At what intensity of noise do such conditions begin to occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"75‍ dB","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"80‍ dB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"90‍ dB","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"100‍ dB","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 90 dB","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 90 dB</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> auditory fatigue</strong> that begins to appear with exposure to noise at<strong> 90 dB.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7654","text":"An airline ground staff of‍ficer with difficulty hea​ring is found to have per​manent hearing loss on fo​llow up. Continuous expos‍ure to sound above what i​ntensity can cause this c‌ondition?","raw_text":"An airline ground staff officer with difficulty hearing is found to have permanent hearing loss on follow up. Continuous exposure to sound above what intensity can cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"160‍ dB","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"120‍ dB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"110‍ dB","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"100‍ dB","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 100 dB","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 100 dB</strong></p><p><strong>Continuous </strong>exposure to sound above<strong> 100 dB</strong> can cause<strong> permanent hearing loss</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6807","text":"The WHO defines disabling ‍hearing loss in adults as​ a hearing loss of more t​han:","raw_text":"The WHO defines disabling hearing loss in adults as a hearing loss of more than:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"60‍ dB","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"45‍ dB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"35‍ dB","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"25‍ dB","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 35 dB","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 35 dB</strong></p><p>According to WHO, <strong>disabling hearing loss</strong> refers to hearing loss<strong> greater than 35 dB</strong> in the better hearing ear in <strong>adults</strong>.</p>\n<p>WHO grades of hearing loss:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 80px; width: 359.015625px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 195px; height: 19px;\"><strong>Grade of hearing loss range</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.015625px; height: 19px;\"><strong>Range of hearing loss</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 195px; height: 18px;\">0: No impairment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.015625px; height: 18px;\">&lt; 20 dB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 195px; height: 18px;\">1: Mild</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.015625px; height: 18px;\">20 - 34 dB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 195px; height: 18px;\">2: Moderate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.015625px; height: 18px;\">35 - 49 dB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 195px; height: 18px;\">3: Moderately Severe</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.015625px; height: 18px;\">50 - 64 dB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 195px;\">4: Severe</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.016px;\">65 - 79 dB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 195px; height: 18px;\">5: Profound</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.015625px; height: 18px;\">&gt;80 dB</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7660","text":"Which of the following is ‍a unit of absorbed dose o​f radiation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a unit of absorbed dose of radiation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Roen‍tgen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"R‍ad","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"R‍em","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sie‍vert","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rad","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rad</strong></p><p><strong>Rad</strong> is a unit of <strong>absorbed dose of radiation</strong> by tissue or material. It has been replaced by the <strong>SI unit</strong> which is <strong>gray </strong>(Gy).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7661","text":"What is the SI unit of rad‍iation dose equivalent?","raw_text":"What is the SI unit of radiation dose equivalent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R‍ad","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gr‍ay","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sie‍vert","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Roen‍tgen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sievert","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sievert</strong></p><p>The SI unit of radiation dose equivalent is <strong>sievert </strong>(Sv).</p>\n<p><strong>Dose equivalent </strong>(H) = <strong>Absorbed dose</strong> (D) <strong>x Quality factor </strong>(Q)</p>\n<p>The<strong> quality facto</strong>r depends upon the <strong>density of ionization</strong> produced in the tissue by radiation. The factor Q for X-rays, γ-rays and electrons is 1 and that for an alpha particle is 20.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7662","text":"What is the permissible do‍se of manmade radiation p​er year?","raw_text":"What is the permissible dose of manmade radiation per year?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 r‍ads","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5 r‍ads","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"7 r‍ads","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 r‍ads","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5 rads","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5 rads</strong></p><p>The permissible dose of manmade radiation is <strong>5 rads per year.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7663","text":"A young girl who is 1 mont‍h pregnant is concerned a​bout a chest X-ray that s​he had taken 3 weeks back​. What is the dose of rad‍iation below which determ​inistic effects on the fe‌tus are not seen?","raw_text":"A young girl who is 1 month pregnant is concerned about a chest X-ray that she had taken 3 weeks back. What is the dose of radiation below which deterministic effects on the fetus are not seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.5‍ mSv","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 ‍mSv","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 ‍RAD","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 ‍RAD","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 5 RAD","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 5 RAD</strong></p><p><strong>Deterministic effects</strong> on the fetus are <strong>not seen below</strong> <strong>5 rads </strong>or<strong> 50 mSv</strong> of radiation.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p><strong>Dose limits</strong> of radiation exposure are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Occupational</strong> exposure - Effective dose should not exceed <strong>20 mSv/year </strong>averaged over <strong>5 consecutive years</strong> / <strong>30 mSv</strong> in a <strong>year</strong>.<strong><br/></strong></li>\n<li><strong>General public</strong> exposure - Effective dose should not exceed <strong>1 mSv/year</strong></li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Occupational exposure for <strong>pregnant</strong> staff - Effective dose to the fetus should not exceed <strong>1mSv/year</strong> (same as the general public).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For<strong> pregnant patients</strong> who <strong>require diagnostics</strong> with exposure to ionising radiation, the <strong>radiation protection principles</strong> are followed:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>exposure</strong> should be <strong>justified</strong></li>\n<li>The dose should be as low as reasonably achievable (<strong>ALARA principle</strong>)</li>\n<li>The dose limit is not particularly specified as it involves a single time exposure to diagnostic radiation. Usually, the <strong>effective dose</strong> to the <strong>fetus</strong> is <strong>&lt;1msv</strong> and deterministic effects to the fetus are not seen below 50 mSv (5 Rad).</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7664","text":"What was the 10 days rule ‍in radiation related to?","raw_text":"What was the 10 days rule in radiation related to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Occupatio‍nal health","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Preg‍nancy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Industrial‍ radiation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radiot‍herapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pregnancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pregnancy</strong></p><p>The <strong>10 days rule</strong> in radiation was related to <strong>pregnancy</strong>.<strong> </strong>It is aimed at <strong>preventing unintentional exposure</strong> of the<strong> embryo</strong> or<strong> fetus </strong>to radiation.</p>\n<p>This rule states that if a <strong>radiograph</strong> is being done on the abdomen or pelvis of a <strong>woman</strong> in her <strong>childbearing age</strong>, then it should be <strong>done</strong> <strong>during</strong> the <strong>10 days following</strong> the onset of <strong>menstruation</strong>. </p>\n<p>In most situations, there is growing evidence that strict adherence to the \"ten-day rule\" may be <strong>unnecessarily restrictive</strong>. Since organogenesis starts 3 to 5 weeks post-conception, it was felt that radiation exposure in early pregnancy couldn't result in malformation. Based on this, it was suggested to do away with the 10-day rule and <strong>replace</strong> it with a<strong> 28-day rule</strong>. This means that <strong>radiological examination</strong> if justified, can be carried <strong>throughout</strong> the <strong>cycle</strong> until a period is missed.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"zBR01ftz","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"40_sle_and_aps","title":"40.Sle And Aps","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MC0822","text":"What type of hypersensitiv‌ity reaction is seen in s‍ystemic lupus erythematos​us?","raw_text":"What type of hypersensitivity reaction is seen in systemic lupus erythematosus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type‌ III","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type III</strong></p><p><strong>Type III </strong>hypersensitivity reaction (<strong>immune complex</strong> deposition) is seen in <strong>systemic lupus erythematosus.</strong></p>\n<p>Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune condition in which <strong>autoantibodies</strong> are directed against a part of the nucleus such as double-stranded DNA, histone, ribonuclear proteins, etc. The presence of these autoantibodies leads to the deposition of immune complexes and activation of the complement system.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0824","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following complement def‍iciencies are at an incre​ased risk of developing s‍ystemic lupus erythematos​us?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following complement deficiencies are at an increased risk of developing systemic lupus erythematosus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C‌1q","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"C‌5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‌3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C1 inh‌ibitor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. C1q","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. C1q</strong></p><p><strong>C1q</strong> <strong>complement deficiency</strong> is a <strong>risk factor</strong> for the development of systemic lupus erythematosus <strong>(SLE).</strong> </p>\n<p>Risk factors for SLE are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Genetic\n<ul>\n<li>Genetic defects resulting in <strong>C1q</strong>, C2, C4 complement deficiencies</li>\n<li>Presence of <strong>HLA DR3</strong></li>\n<li>Mutation in the TREX1 gene</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Environmental\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infection</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Oral contraceptive pills/ hormone replacement therapy</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Smoking</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Prolonged exposure to silica</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7035","text":"Anti-nuclear antibodies ar‌e required for the diagno‍sis of which of the follo​wing?","raw_text":"Anti-nuclear antibodies are required for the diagnosis of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scler‌oderma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos‌us","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rheumatoid‌ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sjogren's‌ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Systemic lupus erythematosus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Systemic lupus erythematosus</strong></p><p><strong>Antinuclear antibodies (ANAs) </strong>are required for the diagnosis of <strong>systemic lupus erythematosus. </strong></p>\n<p>ANAs are autoantibodies that bind to the contents of the cell nucleus. They are present in &gt; 95% of patients with SLE. A positive antinuclear antibody ( ANA ) test with a titer of at least 1:80 is the obligatory entry criterion along with the additive criteria in 7 clinical and 3 immunologic domains. If a total of &gt;10 points achieved, it is indicative of SLE according to <strong>2019 EULAR/ACR Classification Criteria </strong>for Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) </p>\n<p>Most sensitive antibody for SLE: <strong>ANA</strong></p>\n<p>Most specific antibody for SLE: <strong>Anti smith</strong> &gt; Anti dsDNA <br/>ANA may also be positive in the other options but it is not a criterion for diagnosis of these conditions.</p>\n<p>The following antibodies are specific for their respective autoimmune conditions. </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Scleroderma </strong>(option A)<strong>: Anti-topoisomerase I(Scl-70) </strong>and <strong>anti-centromere</strong> antibodies highly specific for Systemic sclerosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Rheumatoid arthritis</strong> (option C): <strong>RF</strong> and <strong>anti-CCP </strong>antibodies </li>\n<li><strong>Sjogren syndrome</strong> (option D): <strong>Anti-Ro, Anti-La </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6497","text":"A 25-year-old marathon run‌ner presents with complai‍nts of joint pain and ras​hes over the face. She sa‍ys the rashes appear on e​xposure to the sun. On ph​ysical examination, a but‌terfly rash is seen over ‍her face sparing the naso​labial folds. Which of th​e following antibodies wi‍ll you evaluate to screen‍ this patient?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old marathon runner presents with complaints of joint pain and rashes over the face. She says the rashes appear on exposure to the sun. On physical examination, a butterfly rash is seen over her face sparing the nasolabial folds. Which of the following antibodies will you evaluate to screen this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti-dsDNA‌ antibodies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-Sm a‌ntibodies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antinuclear‌ antibodies","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anti-histon‌e antibodies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Antinuclear antibodies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Antinuclear antibodies</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of systemic lupus erythematosus (<strong>SLE</strong>). The best <strong>screening test</strong> in this condition is to evaluate for the presence of <strong>antinuclear antibodies </strong>(ANAs).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6501","text":"A school teacher presented‌ with painful oral ulcers‍, photosensitive rashes, ​and arthralgia. Presence ‍of which of the following​ antibodies would be diag​nostic of her condition?","raw_text":"A school teacher presented with painful oral ulcers, photosensitive rashes, and arthralgia. Presence of which of the following antibodies would be diagnostic of her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti Ro/SS-‌A antibodies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-Sm a‌ntibodies","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Antinuclear‌ antibodies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti-dsDNA‌ antibodies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anti-Sm antibodies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anti-Sm antibodies</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of oral ulcers, photosensitive rash, and arthralgia is suggestive of systemic lupus erythematosus (<strong>SLE</strong>). <strong>Anti-Sm antibodies</strong> are <strong>most</strong> <strong>specific</strong> for SLE and hence their presence is diagnostic of this condition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0857","text":"An SLE patient is found to‌ have a butterfly rash. W‍hich of the following is ​an incorrect statement re‍garding this finding?","raw_text":"An SLE patient is found to have a butterfly rash. Which of the following is an incorrect statement regarding this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most common acute rash in ‌SLE","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spares the nasolabial fold‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased risk of squamous‌ cell carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Associated ‌with scaling","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Butterfly rash</strong> or malar rash in a patient with SLE is not associated with an increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma.</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>most common</strong> type of <strong>acute</strong> skin rash seen in SLE. It is a photosensitive, erythematous, and <strong>scaly</strong> rash that is prominent over the <strong>cheeks</strong> and the <strong>nose</strong>. It <strong>spares</strong> the <strong>nasolabial folds.</strong></p>\n<p>Common causes of <strong>malar rash</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Erysipelas</li>\n<li>Cellulitis </li>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li>Rosacea</li>\n<li>Pellagra</li>\n<li>Dermatomyositis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7696","text":"What is the most common pu‌lmonary manifestation of ‍SLE?","raw_text":"What is the most common pulmonary manifestation of SLE?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shrinking l‌ung syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pleu‌ritis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intra alveol‌ar hemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interstitial‌ inflammation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pleuritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pleuritis</strong></p><p>The most common pulmonary manifestation of SLE is <strong>pleuritis</strong>, with or without pleural effusion.</p>\n<p>The most common manifestations of different systems involved in SLE are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Musculoskeletal- arthritis and arthralgia</li>\n<li>Skin- photosensitivity</li>\n<li>Kidneys- proteinuria</li>\n<li><strong>Neurologic- cognitive impairment</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cardiac- pericarditis</strong></li>\n<li>Pulmonary- pleuritis</li>\n<li><strong>Hematologic- anemia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6504","text":"A 40-year-old man with his‌tory of SLE presented to ‍your OPD with shortness o​f breath. It is associate‍d with chest pain that wo​rsens on inspiration. On ​auscultation, you notice ‌a pleural friction rub. I‍nvestigations reveal the ​following. What is the mo​st likely diagnosis? PFT ‍- restrictive pattern, re‍duced lung volumes DLCO -‌ reduced CT chest - eleva​ted hemidiaphragm","raw_text":"<p>A 40-year-old man with history of SLE presented to your OPD with shortness of breath. It is associated with chest pain that worsens on inspiration. On auscultation, you notice a pleural friction rub. Investigations reveal the following. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>PFT - restrictive pattern, reduced lung volumes</p>\n<p>DLCO - reduced</p>\n<p>CT chest - elevated hemidiaphragm</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lupus pn‌eumonitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Interstiti‌al fibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Shrinking l‌ung syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intra-alveol‌ar hemorrhage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Shrinking lung syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3d626efc66bc46af80f1c32000255b15x512x512.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Shrinking lung syndrome</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with elevated hemidiaphragm and no evidence of ILD on chest CT is suggestive of <strong>shrinking lung syndrome</strong> (SLS), which is a rare complication of systemic lupus erythematosus (<strong>SLE</strong>).</p>\n<p>It is characterized by <strong>dyspnea</strong> and episodes of <strong>pleuritic chest pain</strong>. These symptoms occur in the absence of pleural or parenchymal involvement.</p>\n<p><strong>PFT</strong> shows <strong>decreased lung volume</strong> with a <strong>restrictive</strong> pattern, and <strong>DLCO</strong> is often <strong>reduced</strong>. Radiological investigations show an <strong>elevated</strong> <strong>hemidiaphragm</strong> with no evidence of ILD. <br/><br/>The following X-ray shows shrinking lung syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Pulmonary manifestations seen in SLE are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pleuritis</strong> with or without pleural effusion- <strong>most common</strong> pulmonary manifestation.</li>\n<li><strong>Lupus pneumonitis</strong> - It is the inflammation of the lung tissue (pneumonitis) due to SLE (lupus). </li>\n<li>Interstitial lung fibrosis</li>\n<li>Shrinking lung syndrome</li>\n<li>Intra-alveolar hemorrhage</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9946","text":"What is the most common ne‌urological manifestation ‍seen in patients with sys​temic lupus erythematosus‍?","raw_text":"What is the most common neurological manifestation seen in patients with systemic lupus erythematosus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seiz‌ures","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Depre‌ssion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cognitive ‌impairment","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Polyneu‌ropathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cognitive impairment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cognitive impairment</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common neurological</strong> manifestation seen in patients with systemic lupus erythematosus <strong>(SLE)</strong> is <strong>cognitive impairment.</strong></p>\n<p>It can manifest as <strong>impaired</strong> <strong>memory</strong> and reasoning. Other common neurological manifestations in SLE are psychosis, depression, and seizures.</p>\n<p>The most common manifestations of different systems involved in SLE are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Musculoskeletal- arthritis and arthralgia</li>\n<li>Skin- photosensitivity</li>\n<li>Kidneys- proteinuria</li>\n<li>Neurologic- cognitive impairment</li>\n<li>Cardiac- pericarditis</li>\n<li>Pulmonary- pleuritis</li>\n<li>Hematologic- anemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6502","text":"A young girl is being eval‌uated for low mood, anhed‍onia, weight loss, and in​somnia since past 1 month‍. On examination, multipl​e hesitation marks were s​een on her left forearm. ‌She was diagnosed with sy‍stemic lupus erythematosu​s (SLE) one year back. Wh​ich of the following auto‍antibodies is associated ‍with this presentation?","raw_text":"A young girl is being evaluated for low mood, anhedonia, weight loss, and insomnia since past 1 month. On examination, multiple hesitation marks were seen on her left forearm. She was diagnosed with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) one year back. Which of the following autoantibodies is associated with this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti-ribosomal P antibodie‌s","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-Ro/SSA‌ antibodies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti-neurona‌l antibodies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti-histon‌e antibodies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anti-ribosomal P antibodies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anti-ribosomal P antibodies</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>depression</strong> in a patient with <strong>SLE</strong>. It is associated with <strong>anti-ribosomal P</strong> antibodies.</p>\n<p>Antiribosomal P antibody is associated with <strong>psychosis</strong> or <strong>depression</strong> in SLE.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6505","text":"What is the most common ca‌rdiac manifestation seen ‍in patients with systemic​ lupus erythematosus?","raw_text":"What is the most common cardiac manifestation seen in patients with systemic lupus erythematosus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perica‌rditis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Myoca‌rditis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Libman Sack ‌endocarditis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myocardial‌ infarction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pericarditis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pericarditis</strong></p><p><strong>Pericarditis</strong> is the most common cardiac manifestation seen in patients with <strong>systemic lupus erythematosus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other cardiac manifestations include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Myocarditis</li>\n<li>Libman-Sacks endocarditis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients with SLE are at an increased<strong> risk </strong>for<strong> myocardial infarction,</strong> usually due to accelerated atherosclerosis.</p>\n<p>The most common manifestations of different systems involved in SLE are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Musculoskeletal- arthritis and arthralgia</li>\n<li>Skin- photosensitivity</li>\n<li>Kidneys- proteinuria</li>\n<li>Neurologic- cognitive impairment</li>\n<li>Cardiac- pericarditis</li>\n<li>Pulmonary- pleuritis</li>\n<li>Hematologic- anemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0863","text":"Which of the following is ‌a feature of arthritis in‍ SLE?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a feature of arthritis in SLE?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-deformi‌ng arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Predilection for large joi‌nts","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Asymmetric ‌involvement","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non-erosiv‌e arthritis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Non-erosive arthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Non-erosive arthritis</strong></p><p><strong>Non-erosive arthritis</strong> is a common feature of arthritis in SLE.</p>\n<p>Features of arthritis in SLE are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Symmetrical</strong> and <strong>polyarticular</strong> arthritis </li>\n<li>A predilection for the <strong>small joints</strong> of the hand (the proximal interphalangeal and carpal joints)</li>\n<li><strong>Non-erosive</strong> and <strong>deforming arthritis</strong> <strong>(reducible)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>In contrast to rheumatoid arthritis which is characterized by erosive, deforming joint involvement, SLE has non-erosive and deforming arthritis (reduciable).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6507","text":"A young woman who is a kno‌wn case of SLE develops p‍roteinuria. The doctor su​spects lupus nephritis an‍d performs a renal biopsy​. It shows the presence o​f wire loop appearance wi‌thout glomerular prolifer‍ation. In which class of ​lupus nephritis will you ​categorize this patient's‍ biopsy findings?","raw_text":"A young woman who is a known case of SLE develops proteinuria. The doctor suspects lupus nephritis and performs a renal biopsy. It shows the presence of wire loop appearance without glomerular proliferation. In which class of lupus nephritis will you categorize this patient's biopsy findings?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mesangial proliferative lu‌pus nephritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Focal lupu‌s nephritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diffuse lup‌us nephritis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Membranous lupus nephritis‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diffuse lupus nephritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/61f60a4aeb574c178459f81e107d7445x1279x911.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diffuse lupus nephritis</strong></p><p><strong>Wire loop appearance</strong> without glomerular proliferation is seen in <strong>diffuse lupus nephritis (Class IV).</strong></p>\n<p>The capillaries are thickened due to the <strong>subendothelial deposits</strong> and appear as wire loops. It is the <strong>most</strong> <strong>common</strong> and <strong>most severe</strong> form of lupus nephritis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Renal involvement is common in patients with SLE. The majority of the patients will present with</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Proteinuria, nephrotic syndrome</li>\n<li>Microscopic hematuria, hypertension</li>\n<li>Urinary casts</li>\n<li>Abnormal renal function tests- elevated serum creatinine</li>\n<li>Low C3 and C4 levels</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0866","text":"A 40 year old female prese‌nted to the OPD for her a‍nnual routine check-up. s​he is a patient of SLE di‍agnosed a decade ago, amo​ng the given options what​ is the most common cause‌ of death in such patient‍s?","raw_text":"A 40 year old female presented to the OPD for her annual routine check-up. she is a patient of SLE diagnosed a decade ago, among the given options what is the most common cause of death in such patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiovascu‌lar disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Renal ‌failure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infec‌tions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disseminated intravascular‌ coagulation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cardiovascular disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cardiovascular disease</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> cause of <strong>death</strong> in patients with SLE after 10 years of disease is <strong>accelerated atherosclerosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The leading causes of death in the first 10 years of the disease are infections and renal failure; subsequently, Cardiovascular events become increasingly frequent causes of mortality.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7517","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is recommended for all‍ patients with SLE?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is recommended for all patients with SLE?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydroxych‌loroquine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"NSA‌IDs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Methot‌rexate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Systemic‌ steroids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hydroxychloroquine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hydroxychloroquine</strong></p><p><strong>Hydroxychloroquine</strong> is recommended for all patients with SLE. It decreases the symptoms, reduces flares and organ damage.</p>\n<p>Important drugs used in the management of SLE are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NSAIDs</strong>- used for a shorter period to reduce pain and fever. They are not used in all patients because of the increased risk of atherosclerosis and renal injury.</li>\n<li><strong>Hydroxychloroquine</strong> or chloroquine- should be used in all patients with SLE</li>\n<li><strong>Glucocorticoids</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>topical</strong> glucocorticoids- in <strong>mild</strong> skin lesions</li>\n<li><strong>systemic</strong> steroids- in <strong>severe</strong> skin lesions and organ involvements.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Immunosuppressive agents</strong>- used in combination with glucocorticoids in severe skin lesions and organ involvement. They help to reduce the dose and toxicity of glucocorticoids. Important agents are:\n<ul>\n<li>Methotrexate</li>\n<li>Azathioprine</li>\n<li>Mycophenolate mofetil and cyclophosphamide- immunosuppressive agents of choice in renal involvement. </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Biologic drugs</strong>- like belimumab and rituximab are used when there is no response to conventional drugs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1120","text":"A patient with a history o‌f SLE presented with hema‍turia and proteinuria. In​vestigations revealed ele‍vated creatinine and low ​C3, C4 levels. He was unr​esponsive to steroid ther‌apy and his physician pre‍scribed him belimumab for​ further management. What​ is the mechanism of acti‍on of this drug?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of SLE presented with hematuria and proteinuria. Investigations revealed elevated creatinine and low C3, C4 levels. He was unresponsive to steroid therapy and his physician prescribed him belimumab for further management. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"TNF in‌hibitor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IL-1 an‌tagonist","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti CD-2‌0 antibody","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BAFF in‌hibitor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. BAFF inhibitor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. BAFF inhibitor</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>lupus nephritis. </strong><strong>Belimumab</strong> is a <strong>BAFF inhibitor</strong> used in patients with lupus nephritis who do not show response to initial therapy with steroids.</p>\n<p>Belimumab is a human monoclonal antibody that inhibits the soluble form of a B cell survival factor (known as <strong>BLyS</strong> or <strong>BAFF</strong>) which promotes the formation of memory B cells and autoantibodies. Belimumab inhibits this factor and therefore is used in the management of SLE. </p>\n<p>It is used in combination with either cyclophosphamide or MMF in patients with lupus nephritis who do not show response to initial therapy with steroids.</p>\n<p>CD20 is an antigen present on the B cells. <strong>Rituximab</strong> is an <strong>anti-CD20</strong> monoclonal antibody that is used in patients who are resistant to the standard therapies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6508","text":"A 60-year-old man presente‌d with fever, malaise, sk‍in rashes, and arthralgia​. On further evaluation, ‍his serology was positive​ for ANA and anti-histone​ antibodies. Use of which‌ of the following drugs c‍ould have led to this con​dition?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presented with fever, malaise, skin rashes, and arthralgia. On further evaluation, his serology was positive for ANA and anti-histone antibodies. Use of which of the following drugs could have led to this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rifa‌mpin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bupiv‌acaine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Warf‌arin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydra‌lazine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hydralazine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hydralazine</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with positive ANA and <strong>anti-histone antibodies</strong> point towards a diagnosis of <strong>drug-induced lupus erythematosus</strong>. <strong>Hydralazine</strong> which is a vasodilator is associated with the development of drug-induced lupus erythematosus.</p>\n<p>Drug-induced lupus erythematosus is an autoimmune condition caused due to exposure to certain medications. It completely <strong>resolves</strong> <strong>after</strong> the causative <strong>drug</strong> is <strong>discontinued</strong>. Important drugs associated with this condition are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sulfonamides</li>\n<li>Hydralazine</li>\n<li>Isoniazid</li>\n<li>Infliximab</li>\n<li>Procainamide</li>\n<li>Propylthiouracil</li>\n<li>ACE inhibitors</li>\n<li>Thiazides</li>\n<li>TNF-alpha antagonists</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0821","text":"What is the ratio of occur‌rence of drug-induced lup‍us erythematosus in femal​es and males?","raw_text":"What is the ratio of occurrence of drug-induced lupus erythematosus in females and males?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"9‌:1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3‌:1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2‌:1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌:1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1:1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1:1</strong></p><p>The female to male ratio in drug-induced lupus erythematosus (<strong>DILE</strong>) is <strong>1:1. </strong></p>\n<p>The female to male ratio in systemic lupus erythematosus (<strong>SLE</strong>) is <strong>9:1</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 184px; width: 455px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\"><strong>Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\"><strong>Drug-induced lupus erythematosus (DILE)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">Age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Common in young individuals </td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">Common in elderly (50-70 years)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">Female: male ratio</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">9:1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\"><strong>1:1</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">Organs involved</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Can affect many organs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\"><strong>Skin</strong> is mainly involved. <strong>Renal</strong> and brain involvement are <strong>rare</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">Complement levels</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Reduced C3, C4 levels</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">Normal C3, C4 levels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 108px;\">Antibodies  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">-Antinuclear</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Common</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">Common</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">-Antihistone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Rare</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\"><strong>Common</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">-Anti ds-DNA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Common</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\"><strong>Rare</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0867","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‍ drug-induced lupus eryth​ematosus?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding drug-induced lupus erythematosus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal involv‌ement is rare","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Commonly associated with a‌nti ds-DNA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Common i‌n females","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reduced C3 a‌nd C4 levels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Renal involvement is rare","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Renal involvement is rare</strong></p><p>Renal involvement is rare in drug-induced lupus erythematosus.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 184px; width: 455px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\"><strong>Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\"><strong>Drug-induced lupus erythematosus (DILE)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">Age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Common in young individuals </td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">Common in elderly (50-70 years)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">Female: male ratio</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">9:1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\"><strong>1:1</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">Organs involved</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Can affect many organs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\"><strong>Skin</strong> is mainly involved. <strong>Renal</strong> and brain involvement are <strong>rare</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">Complement levels</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Reduced C3, C4 levels</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">Normal C3, C4 levels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 108px;\">Antibodies    </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">-Antinuclear</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Common</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\">Common</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">-Antihistone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Rare</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\"><strong>Common</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">-Anti ds-DNA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Common</td>\n<td style=\"width: 177px;\"><strong>Rare</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6503","text":"Antiphospholipid syndrome ‌is associated with all of‍ the following except:","raw_text":"Antiphospholipid syndrome is associated with all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recurrent ‌fetal loss","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thrombo‌cytosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Arterial ‌thrombosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Libman-Sacks‌ endocarditis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thrombocytosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thrombocytosis</strong></p><p>Thrombocytosis is not associated with antiphospholipid syndrome (APS). On the contrary, <strong>thrombocytopenia</strong> is seen.</p>\n<p>APS is an autoantibody-mediated condition that is characterized by <strong>arterial </strong>thrombosis, <strong>venous</strong> <strong>thrombosis</strong>, and <strong>recurrent fetal loss</strong>. These autoantibodies bind to the phospholipids present on the endothelial cells and activate coagulation and thrombus formation.</p>\n<p>Important manifestations of <strong>venous thrombosis</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Deep vein thrombosis of legs</li>\n<li>Pulmonary embolism</li>\n<li>Livedo reticularis</li>\n<li>Cerebral venous thrombosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Important manifestations of <strong>arterial thrombosis</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stroke, transient ischemic attacks</li>\n<li>Retinal artery thrombosis</li>\n<li>Myocardial ischemia</li>\n<li>Libman-Sacks endocarditis- due to the vegetations formed by platelet fibrin microthrombi</li>\n<li>Leg ulcers, gangrene of the digits</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Important <strong>obstetric </strong>manifestations are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Preeclampsia</li>\n<li>Recurrent fetal loss</li>\n<li>Premature birth</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Important <strong>hematological</strong> abnormalities are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Thrombocytopenia</strong>- occurs due to increased platelet aggregation and thrombus formation </li>\n<li>Coombs positive <strong>hemolytic anemia</strong>- seen when autoantibodies attack red blood cells</li>\n<li>Thrombotic microangiopathic syndromes- thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (<strong>TTP</strong>) and hemolytic uremic syndrome (<strong>HUS</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6511","text":"A 34-year-old woman comes ‌for evaluation of recurre‍nt fetal losses. She has ​had 4 spontaneous abortio‍ns, all of them at &lt;10 we​eks of gestational age, a​nd has a history of deep ‌vein thrombosis. Which of‍ the following antibodies​ is not useful in making ​a diagnosis in this condi‍tion?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old woman comes for evaluation of recurrent fetal losses. She has had 4 spontaneous abortions, all of them at &lt;10 weeks of gestational age, and has a history of deep vein thrombosis. Which of the following antibodies is not useful in making a diagnosis in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lupus ant‌icoagulant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-car‌diolipin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti-beta-2 ‌glycoprotein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti-beta-2 ‌microglobulin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anti-beta-2 microglobulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anti-beta-2 microglobulin</strong></p><p>The given clinical history of &gt;3 spontaneous abortions at &lt;10 weeks of gestational age along with a history of deep vein thrombosis suggests a diagnosis of <strong>antiphospholipid syndrome (APS). </strong>Anti beta-2 microglobulin is not useful in diagnosing this condition.</p>\n<p>For the diagnosis of antiphospholipid syndrome, high titers of <strong>one or more</strong> of the following three antibodies should be demonstrated along with at least one clinical symptom of thrombosis or pregnancy complications:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lupus anticoagulant (LA)</strong> - most<strong> specific</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Anticardiolipin (aCL) antibodies</strong> - most <strong>sensitive</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Anti-beta-2 glycoprotein</strong> antibodies</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6512","text":"A 40-year-old man presente‌d with painful swelling o‍f his right calf muscle. ​The clinical test shown b‍elow was positive. Invest​igations revealed elevate​d D-dimer, high titres of‌ lupus anticoagulant and ‍anti-cardiolipin antibodi​es. You would use all of ​the following drugs for h‍is management except:","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presented with painful swelling of his right calf muscle. The clinical test shown below was positive. Investigations revealed elevated D-dimer, high titres of lupus anticoagulant and anti-cardiolipin antibodies. You would use all of the following drugs for his management except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Warf‌arin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucoco‌rticoids","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Asp‌irin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydroxych‌loroquine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Glucocorticoids","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4bea1ea5a4424743b5a89390f5eabefa.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Glucocorticoids</strong></p><p>The given findings of <strong>positive Moses'</strong> <strong>sign</strong> with elevated D-dimer, lupus anticoagulant, and anti-cardiolipin antibodies are suggestive of <strong>antiphospholipid</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong> (APS). Glucocorticoids are not useful in the general management of APS, they are used only in the treatment of catastrophic APS.</p>\n<p><strong>Squeezing</strong> of the relaxed calf muscles from <strong>side to side</strong> is painful as the thrombosed deep veins in the calf are always <strong>tender</strong>. This test is known as <strong>Moses’ sign</strong>.</p>\n<p>The clinical manifestations in APS occur due to the activation of coagulation and thrombus formation. Therefore, <strong>anticoagulants</strong> and <strong>antiplatelet</strong> agents are the mainstays in the <strong>treatment</strong>. Important drugs used are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Warfarin</strong>- is an anticoagulant that inhibits vitamin K dependant epoxidase.</li>\n<li><strong>Low molecular weight heparins</strong> (LMWH)- warfarin cannot be used in pregnancy due to its teratogenicity. In these situations, LMWH is used for anticoagulation.</li>\n<li><strong>Aspirin</strong>- is an antiplatelet agent.</li>\n<li>Hydroxychloroquine- is an immunomodulatory agent that can be used to prevent recurrent thrombosis.</li>\n<li>Statins</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: The only condition where <strong>corticosteroids</strong> are beneficial is <strong>catastrophic APS</strong>. It is a life-threatening rapidly progressive thromboembolic disease involving simultaneously <strong>three or more</strong> organs. It is seen in very few patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"RWRa_SzD","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"40_genital_tuberculosis","title":"40.Genital Tuberculosis","num_questions":9,"total_marks":36,"duration":19,"time_per_question":126.67,"questions":[{"id":"MC0574","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common site for ​genital tuberculosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common site for genital tuberculosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infundibulum of fallopian ‌tubes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Uterine e‌ndometrium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ampulla of fallopian tubes‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endocervical part of the c‌ervix","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ampulla of fallopian tubes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ampulla of fallopian tubes</strong></p><p>The <strong>ampulla</strong> of the <strong>fallopian tubes</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> site for genital tuberculosis. </p>\n<p>The distribution of genital tuberculosis is given below:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<p><strong>Site</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\"><strong>Percentage</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 100px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 100px;\">Fallopian tubes\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ampulla is the most common site.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Intramural part is the least common site.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fimbriae are not involved even in advanced genital TB.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 100px;\">90-100%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 54px;\">Endometrium</td>\n<td style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<p>50-60%</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 46px;\">Ovaries</td>\n<td style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<p>20-30%</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<p>Cervix</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">5-15%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<p>Vulva and vagina</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">&lt;1%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0839","text":"What is the most common mo‌de of transmission of tub​erculosis to the endometr​ium?","raw_text":"What is the most common mode of transmission of tuberculosis to the endometrium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemato‌genous","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphati‌c spread","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Retrogra‌de spread","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Local ‌spread","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Local spread","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Local spread</strong></p><p><strong>Endometrial tuberculosis</strong> is most commonly acquired by <strong>direct spread</strong> from the fallopian tubes. <strong>Hematogenous spread</strong> is thought to be the process by which the tubercle bacilli implant into the mucosa of the <strong>fallopian tubes</strong> and subsequently affect the endometrium by direct extension.</p>\n<p>Female genital tuberculosis is an important cause of significant morbidity, short and long-term sequelae especially infertility. Genital tuberculosis usually occurs<strong> secondary to pulmonary</strong> (commonest) or <strong>extrapulmonary TB</strong> like gastrointestinal, renal, skeletal, and miliary TB. However, primary genital TB can rarely occur in women whose male partners have active genitourinary TB (e.g., tuberculosis epididymitis) by transmission through infected semen</p>\n<p><strong>Most common site</strong> involved in secondary genital TB is <strong>fallopian tube</strong> (90-100%) &gt;endometrium (50-80%) &gt;ovaries (20-30%)  &gt;cervix 5-15%) &gt;vulva. The <strong>most common symptom</strong> of genital TB is<strong> infertility</strong>.</p>\n<p>Causes of infertility in genital TB:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tubal blockage</li>\n<li>If tubes are patent, then also infertility occurs due to loss of tubal function.</li>\n<li>Tubercular endometritis causes uterine scarring resulting in the destruction of the endometrium.</li>\n<li>Ovarian damage with poor ovarian reserve and volume</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A <strong>laparoscopy and dye hydrotubation</strong> (lap and dye test) is the most reliable tool to diagnose genital TB, especially for tubal, ovarian and peritoneal disease. The test can be <strong>combined with hysteroscopy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Multiple drug therapy (<strong> ATT regimen</strong> provided by DOTS) in adequate doses and for sufficient duration is the mainstay in the <strong>treatment</strong> of TB including genital tuberculosis.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0573","text":"A young woman with fertili‌ty issues is diagnosed to​ have genital tuberculosi​s. Which of the following​ statements is false abou​t this condition?","raw_text":"A young woman with fertility issues is diagnosed to have genital tuberculosis. Which of the following statements is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The causative agent is Myc‌obacterium bovis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It generally occurs second‌ary to a primary focus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It mostly spreads hematoge‌nously","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It commonly presents as a ‌unilateral condition","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It commonly presents as a unilateral condition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It commonly presents as a unilateral condition</strong></p><p>Genital tuberculosis is a bilateral condition. </p>\n<p>It can be caused by both<strong> <em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</em> </strong>(95% cases) and <strong><em>Mycobacterium bovis</em> </strong>(5% cases). It almost always occurs <strong>secondary</strong> to a primary focus elsewhere in the body. The most common <strong>primary focus</strong> is <strong>lungs</strong> &gt; lymph nodes &gt; abdomen &gt; generalised miliary infection.</p>\n<p>It mostly<strong> spreads</strong> <strong>hematogenously</strong>. Lymphatic spread is also seen sometimes. It can rarely spread by direct contact with the affected intraabdominal organs or peritoneum. </p>\n<p>The <strong>fallopian tubes</strong> are the <strong>first</strong> to be <strong>affected</strong>. The disease subsequently spreads to other genital organs and the peritoneum. This is followed by the involvement of bilateral pelvic lymph nodes. Vulvovaginal involvement is usually secondary to uterine involvement.</p>\n<p>The <strong>most common</strong> presentation of genital tuberculosis in the <strong>reproductive</strong> <strong>age</strong> group is <strong>infertility</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0580","text":"What is the most common pr‌esenting symptom of genit​al tuberculosis in the re​productive age group?","raw_text":"What is the most common presenting symptom of genital tuberculosis in the reproductive age group?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asympt‌omatic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infer‌tility","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Menstrual a‌bnormalities","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fe‌ver","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Infertility","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Infertility</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> presentation of <strong>genital tuberculosis</strong> in the reproductive age group is <strong>infertility</strong>.</p>\n<p>Genital tuberculosis mostly presents in young women in the reproductive age group. It can present as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Infertility</strong> is the only complaint in 35-60 % of cases. Of these, 75% of women present with primary infertility.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Menstrual abnormalities</strong> are seen in 10-40% of cases.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Asymptomatic</strong> in 10% of cases.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Other symptoms like<strong> fever</strong> and <strong>malaise</strong> can be seen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>treatment</strong> of infertility in women with genital TB depends on the condition of the endometrium. If the endometrium is receptive and the uterus is normal, then assisted reproductive techniques such as<strong> in-vitro fertilisation</strong> can be tried. If the endometrium is unfavourable then <strong>surrogacy</strong> may be considered.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0577","text":"In a patient with tubercul‌ar salpingitis, which of ​the following statements ​is false?","raw_text":"In a patient with tubercular salpingitis, which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The tubes are enlarged, ro‌ughened, and may show pre​sence of greyish tubercle​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The ampulla of the tube ap‌pears dilated with the fi​mbrial end open and pouti​ng","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The granulomas and hyperpl‌asia seen can be mistaken​ for carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The fimbriae are commonly ‌involved in advanced tube​rculous salpingitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The fimbriae are commonly involved in advanced tuberculous salpingitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/718977bdc81a4ce3858bc27d2412c089x1280x974.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The fimbriae are commonly involved in advanced tuberculous salpingitis</strong></p><p>The<strong> fimbriae</strong> are often <strong>spared</strong> even in advanced forms of <strong>tubercular salpingitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The involvement of fallopian tubes is 100% and bilateral, secondary to hematogenous spread. The <strong>ampullary part</strong> of the tube appears <strong>dilated</strong> with the fimbrial end open and pouting. This gives the characteristic<strong> tobacco-pouch appearance </strong>as seen in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>external surface</strong> of the tubes are enlarged, roughened, and may show the presence of <strong>greyish tubercles</strong>. The cut section shows yellowish-grey caseous matter in the lumen. Sometimes, serosanguinous fluid can also be seen in the lumen of the fallopian tube. This is called tuberculous hematosalpinx.</p>\n<p>On <strong>histopathological</strong> examination of the tubes, <strong>granulomas</strong> and <strong>chronic inflammatory infiltrate</strong> with <strong>hyperplasia</strong> is seen. This could be mistaken for malignancy as it also produces a similar histological image.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0699","text":"An 18-year-old girl presen‌ts with a history of low-​grade fever, weight loss,​ abdominal pain and ameno​rrhoea for six months. On​ examination, a pelvic ma‌ss is felt on the left si‌de and signs of ascites a‌re positive. What is your​ diagnosis?","raw_text":"An 18-year-old girl presents with a history of low-grade fever, weight loss, abdominal pain and amenorrhoea for six months. On examination, a pelvic mass is felt on the left side and signs of ascites are positive. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberculosis with tubo-ova‌rian mass","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ovarian m‌alignancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ectopic ‌pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Submucou‌s fibroid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tuberculosis with tubo-ovarian mass","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tuberculosis with tubo-ovarian mass</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with abdominal pain, amenorrhoea, pelvic mass and ascites is suggestive of <strong>genital tuberculosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Genital tuberculosis:</p>\n<p>It is caused by M. tuberculosis and almost always occurs secondary to primary infection elsewhere in the extragenital states. The sites and frequency of involvement are as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fallopian tubes (90-100%)</li>\n<li>Endometrium (50-60%)</li>\n<li>Ovaries (20-30%)</li>\n<li>Cervix (5-15%)</li>\n<li>Vulva and vagina (&lt; 1%)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Presentation is variable and it can be asymptomatic in 10-20% of cases, infertility, m<span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">enstrual irregularities, a</span>bdominal pain and mass, vaginal discharge, fistula formation, ectopic pregnancy and ascites. The mass is made up of rolled-up omentum that is adherent to the uterus and adnexa.</p>\n<p>Constitutional symptoms such as fever, weakness, anorexia or night sweats may be present. </p>\n<p>Investigations: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>CBC, ESR and Mantoux test </li>\n<li>Hysterosalpingography shows a lead-pipe appearance of the fallopian tube, calcification of the tube, beading and variation in the filling, cornual block, jagged fluffiness of tubal outline and tobacco pouch appearance of hydrosalpinx and pyosalpinx.</li>\n<li>Laparoscopy establishes the diagnosis of genital TB. It shows tuberculous lesions and perihepatic adhesions (if present).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Differential diagnosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ovarian cyst, broad ligament cyst or encysted fluid </li>\n<li>Pelvic inflammatory disease </li>\n<li>Ectopic pregnancy </li>\n<li>Carcinoma cervix </li>\n<li>Elephantiasis of the vulva </li>\n<li>Pregnancy </li>\n<li>Puberty menorrhagia and postmenopausal bleeding </li>\n<li>Fungal infection and sarcoidosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Management: </p>\n<p>It is treated with anti-tubercular therapy for 6 months (DOT strategy):</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 60.2541%; height: 206px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 54.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 51.2521%; height: 54.3984px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">First 2 months </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 48.7479%; height: 54.3984px; text-align: center;\">Next 4 months </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 106.992px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 51.2521%; height: 106.992px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Isoniazid 15 mg/ kg body weight</li>\n<li>Rifampicin 450-600 mg</li>\n<li>Pyrazinamide 30 mg/body weight</li>\n<li>Ethambutol 30 mg/ kg body weight </li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">3 times a week. </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 48.7479%; height: 106.992px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Isoniazid 15 mg/kg body weight </li>\n<li>Rifampicin 450-600 mg</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">3 times a week </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Indications and contraindications for surgery: </p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 59.7096%; height: 74.4062px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18.7031px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 50.0454%; height: 18.7031px; text-align: center;\">Indications </td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.0454%; height: 18.7031px; text-align: center;\">Contraindications </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 55.7031px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 50.0454%; height: 55.7031px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Unresponsiveness of the disease in spite of adequate antitubercular chemotherapy.</li>\n<li>Tubercular pyosalpinx, ovarian abscess or pyometra.</li>\n<li>Persistent menorrhagia and/or chronic pelvic pain causing deteriorating health status </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 50.0454%; height: 55.7031px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of active tuberculosis in extragenital site</li>\n<li>Favorable response to antimicrobial therapy with the diminishing size of the pelvic mass</li>\n<li>The accidental discovery of tubercular tubo-ovarian mass on laparotomy in a young patient. </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Types of surgery include vulvectomy (if vulval hypertrophy is present), removal of the adnexal mass, drainage of pyometra and fistula repair.</p>\n<p>TAH+BSO is rarely required nowadays. Tuboplasty is contraindicated.</p>\n<p>Option B: Ovarian tumors usually don't affect menstrual cycles. Though <strong>virilizing tumors</strong> can cause amenorrhoea, other features of virilization such as <strong>hirsutism</strong>, flattening of breasts, and<strong> enlargement of the clitoris</strong> are generally present.</p>\n<p>Option C: Though ectopic pregnancy may be suspected here, the tube is likely to <strong>rupture</strong> with exacerbation of pain, bleeding, and peritonitis <strong>before six months</strong> of gestation. Additionally, the absence of vaginal bleeding/spotting, and the presence of weight loss makes the diagnosis unlikely.</p>\n<p>Option D: Submucous fibroid is associated with <strong>menorrhagia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0583","text":"A gynaecologist suspects t‌uberculous salpingitis in​ a young woman and sugges​ts some investigations to​ confirm the diagnosis. W​hich of the following sta‌tements is true about the‌se investigations?","raw_text":"A gynaecologist suspects tuberculous salpingitis in a young woman and suggests some investigations to confirm the diagnosis. Which of the following statements is true about these investigations?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A differential WBC count s‌hows lymphocytosis in adv​anced disease.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The ESR is frequently rais‌ed and is non-specific in​ early disease.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The Mantoux test is positi‌ve and indicative of curr​ent exposure to tubercle ​bacilli.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The Quantiferon test is po‌sitive and indicates late​nt tuberculosis.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The Quantiferon test is positive and indicates latent tuberculosis.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The Quantiferon test is positive and indicates latent tuberculosis.</strong></p><p>The <strong>Quantiferon test</strong> will be <strong>positive</strong> in patients having genital TB. It is an indicator of <strong>latent</strong> tuberculosis. The Quantiferon test measures the release of interferon-gamma by  T cells following stimulation by antigens unique to <em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</em>.</p>\n<p>A differential WBC count shows <strong>lymphocytosis</strong> at <strong>all stages</strong> of genital tuberculosis. The <strong>ESR</strong> is frequently<strong> raised</strong> and is a <strong>non-specific</strong> investigation at <strong>any stage</strong> of the disease. None of these tests can be used for confirmation as these markers are raised in many infections.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Mantoux test</strong> is positive in 90% of cases of genital TB. It is indicative of <strong>past exposure</strong> to the tubercle bacilli. It is not a confirmatory investigation but a negative result indicates tuberculosis to be unlikely.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0587","text":"Hysterosalpingography was ‌done in a young woman wit​h infertility and menstru​al abnormalities. Which o​f the following findings ​would not point towards a‌ diagnosis of tubercular ‌salpingitis?","raw_text":"Hysterosalpingography was done in a young woman with infertility and menstrual abnormalities. Which of the following findings would not point towards a diagnosis of tubercular salpingitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A wedge shaped defect at t‌he uterine isthmus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Beading and variations in ‌filling defect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcificatio‌n of the tube","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Jagged fluffiness of the t‌ubal outline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A wedge shaped defect at the uterine isthmus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c6bb5c935f074df998950ffe59fa678fx510x411.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A wedge shaped defect at the uterine isthmus</strong></p><p>A <strong>wedge-shaped defect</strong> is not suggestive of tubercular salpingitis. It is seen at the <strong>uterine isthmus</strong> in<strong> </strong>patients with a history of <strong>Cesarean section</strong> with a uterine scar.</p>\n<p><strong>Hysterosalpingography (HSG) features of genital tuberculosis are:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lead pipe appearance due to rigid nonperistaltic pipe-like tube </li>\n<li><strong>Beading</strong> and variation in filling defect</li>\n<li><strong>Calcification</strong> of the tube</li>\n<li><strong>Jagged fluffiness</strong> of the tubal outline</li>\n<li>Tobacco-pouch appearance of hydrosalpinx and pyosalpinx</li>\n<li>Golf club appearance with slight or moderate dilatation of the ampullary portion of the fallopian tube.</li>\n<li>Rosette type appearance secondary to multiple small diverticular like outpouching surrounding the ampulla produced by caseous ulceration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>HSG</strong> is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in<strong> genital tuberculosis</strong> as it causes dissemination of the infection. This further increases the damage to the fallopian tubes. </p>\n<p>The <strong>investigation of choice</strong> in genital tuberculosis is an <strong>endometrial biopsy. </strong>The tubercle bacilli are present in the deeper layers of the endometrium. They come to the surface just before menstruation. Hence, an endometrial biopsy is done <strong>1 or 2 days before menstruation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Menstrual blood can also be used for detecting mycobacteria.</p>\n<p>An HSG showing the features of tuberculous salpingitis is given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0009","text":"The image below shows the ‌hysterosalpingogram of a ​37-year-old nulliparous w​oman. Identify the false ​statement about this find​ing.","raw_text":"The image below shows the hysterosalpingogram of a 37-year-old nulliparous woman. Identify the false statement about this finding.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a thick walled struc‌ture on sonography","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It can be a‌symptomatic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tubal plicae are flattened‌ on histology","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sequelae of pelvic inflamm‌atory disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is a thick walled structure on sonography","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e88108107de64fc9a93675782c8642e4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a4c8d71f448b4482bfe9bf4611b77760x510x411.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is a thick walled structure on sonography</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>bilateral hydrosalpinx</strong>. It appears as a<strong> thin-walled</strong> structure. </p>\n<p>A hydrosalpinx is a chronic cystic swelling of the fallopian tube that forms after a distal tubal obstruction. The tubes are enormously distended due to filling with clear serous fluid and become <strong>retort-shaped. </strong>On histology, the<strong> tubal plicae</strong> appear <strong>flattened.</strong> The interstitial end of the tube is open whereas the fimbrial end is indrawn and closed. This permits the <strong>dye</strong> to <strong>enter</strong> but <strong>not exit</strong> the tube during HSG. </p>\n<p>It is caused due to: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pelvic inflammatory disease </li>\n<li>Endometriosis </li>\n<li>Tuberculosis - tubercular salpingitis may also cause tobacco-pouch appearance, lead pipe appearance and pyosalpinx</li>\n<li>Fallopian tube neoplasms (rare)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It can be an incidental finding during a pelvic exam or ultrasound. The patient may be <strong>asymptomatic,</strong> or present with infertility or chronic pelvic pain<strong>.</strong> </p>\n<p>On HSG, it appears as <strong>terminal sacculation </strong>and<strong> occlusion </strong>of both <strong>fallopian tubes </strong>with a normal uterine cavity. Usually, a <strong>thin-walled,</strong> hypoechoic, cystic structure is seen. However, it may appear as multiple hyperechoic mural nodules around the inner circumference of the tube creating a 'beads on a string' appearance.</p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Treat the underlying cause</li>\n<li>Laparoscopic salpingostomy - in mild disease </li>\n<li>IVF recommended for women with infertility and severe hydrosalpinx</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"VdNo8bHo","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"40_myocardial_and_pericardial_diseases_and_cardiac_tumors","title":"40.Myocardial And Pericardial Diseases And Cardiac Tumors","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB0519","text":"What is the most common ty‌pe of cardiomyopathy?","raw_text":"What is the most common type of cardiomyopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dilated car‌diomyopathy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Arrhythmogenic right ventr‌icular cardiomyopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopath‌y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dilated cardiomyopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dilated cardiomyopathy</strong></p><p>The <strong>most</strong> common type of cardiomyopathy is<strong> dilated </strong>cardiomyopathy. <strong>Restrictive</strong> cardiomyopathy is the <strong>least</strong> frequent.</p>\n<p>Cardiomyopathies are a heterogeneous group of diseases of the myocardium associated with <strong>mechanical</strong> and/or <strong>electrical dysfunction</strong>. They usually <strong>exhibit</strong> inappropriate <strong>ventricular hypertrophy</strong> or <strong>dilatation. </strong> The causes are frequently <strong>genetic</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cardiomyopathies maybe <strong>primary</strong> predominantly involving the <strong>heart</strong> or <strong>secondary</strong> to a <strong>systemic</strong> or multiorgan disorder. They occur in the <strong>absence</strong> of any coronary artery disease, valvular heart disease, congenital heart disease, or systemic arterial hypertension.</p>\n<p>Types of cardiomyopathy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dilated cardiomyopathy </li>\n<li>Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</li>\n<li>Restrictive cardiomyopathy</li>\n<li>Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy</li>\n<li>Left ventricular non-compaction cardiomyopathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7568","text":"Alcohol abuse is strongly ‌associated with the devel​opment of:","raw_text":"Alcohol abuse is strongly associated with the development of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perica‌rditis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopath‌y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dilated car‌diomyopathy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Myoca‌rditis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dilated cardiomyopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dilated cardiomyopathy</strong></p><p>Alcohol abuse is strongly associated with the development of <strong>dilated cardiomyopathy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Alcohol and its metabolites, especially <strong>acetaldehyde</strong> have a <strong>direct toxic effect </strong>on the <strong>myocardium </strong>which leads to the development of dilated cardiomyopathy.</p>\n<p>Causes of dilated cardiomyopathy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acquired causes :\n<ul>\n<li>Infectious and non-infectious <strong>myocarditis</strong>: <strong>most common</strong> cause globally</li>\n<li>Alcohol is the most common cause in India</li>\n<li>Pregnancy:\n<ul>\n<li>Pregnancy-associated cardiomyopathy (PACM)</li>\n<li>Peripartum cardiomyopathy (PPCM)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Stress-induced: Takotsubo cardiomyopathy</li>\n<li>Drug-induced:\n<ul>\n<li>Doxorubicin (anthracycline)</li>\n<li>Trastuzumab </li>\n<li>Cyclophosphamide</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Nutritional deficiency:\n<ul>\n<li>Beriberi (thiamine),</li>\n<li>Keshan disease (selenium),</li>\n<li>Carnitine deficiency</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Endocrine: Hypo and hyperthyroidism, acromegaly, diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li>Others:\n<ul>\n<li>Cobalt toxicity,</li>\n<li>Heavy metals (lead, mercury, arsenic, lithium)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Familial causes (50%): </li>\n<ul>\n<li>Mutation in the cardiac myocyte <strong>titin</strong> gene</li>\n<li>Haemochromatosis</li>\n<li>Syndromes: Duchenne muscular dystrophy, Becker’s muscular dystrophy</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0520","text":"Which of the following is ‌the largest protein expre​ssed in humans?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the largest protein expressed in humans?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myo‌sin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coll‌agen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ti‌tin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dystr‌ophin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Titin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Titin</strong></p><p><strong>Titin</strong> is the<strong> largest</strong> protein expressed in humans.</p>\n<p>Titin is the third myofilament of cardiac muscle. It has a single giant polypeptide spanning from Z-disk to the M-band region of the sarcomere.</p>\n<p>Mutation in TTN<em>, </em>the gene encoding titin, is responsible for <strong>20% </strong>of all cases of<strong> dilated cardiomyopathy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0521","text":"Two brothers were arguing ‌over a property dispute w​hen the elder of the two ​complained of chest pain ‌and collapsed. He was tak‍en to the hospital but wa‍s declared brought dead. ​The condition responsible‌ for the death of the pat‍ient is grouped under whi‍ch of the following?","raw_text":"Two brothers were arguing over a property dispute when the elder of the two complained of chest pain and collapsed. He was taken to the hospital but was declared brought dead.  The condition responsible for the death of the patient is grouped under which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dilated car‌diomyopathy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Constrictive cardiomyopath‌y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arrhythmogenic cardiomyopa‌thy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dilated cardiomyopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/21d3bca3f1984b99962605be6bd05b31x1280x941.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c63e1ca3ec9c45dba00f7edeec131dddx1280x951.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dilated cardiomyopathy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of<strong> sudden cardiac death </strong>in a previously healthy man, under a <strong>stressful</strong> situation, is suggestive of<strong> takotsubo cardiomyopathy. </strong>It is a type of <strong>dilated cardiomyopathy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Takotsubo cardiomyopathy</strong> is also called broken heart syndrome, apical ballooning syndrome, or stress-induced cardiomyopathy. During extreme psychological <strong>stress</strong>, there is a <strong>catecholamine surge</strong>. This causes <strong>microcirculation spasms</strong> which lead to a transient <strong>myocardial</strong> <strong>stunning</strong>. It is usually seen in <strong>middle-aged women</strong>. </p>\n<p>It leads to left ventricular contractile dysfunction. The <strong>left ventricular apex</strong> is most often affected which leads to <strong>apical ballooning.</strong> This resembles a “<strong>t</strong><strong>akotsubo</strong>,” which is Japanese for “fishing pot for trapping octopus”.</p>\n<p>Histologically, acute cases may show microscopic areas of necrosis characterized by myocyte hyper contraction known as <strong>contraction band necrosis</strong>. Chronic cases may exhibit microscopic foci of granulation tissue. </p>\n<p>The first image below shows a ventriculogram showing takotsubo cardiomyopathy. The second image shows a 'takotsubo,' or octopus fishing pot.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2069","text":"A patient is admitted to t‌he hospital with heart fa​ilure due to dilated card​iomyopathy. Toxicity by w‌hich of the following doe‍s not cause this?","raw_text":"A patient is admitted to the hospital with heart failure due to dilated cardiomyopathy. Toxicity by which of the following does not cause this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Le‌ad","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mer‌cury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sele‌nium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cob‌alt","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Selenium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Selenium</strong></p><p><strong>Selenium deficiency </strong>(<strong>Keshan's </strong>disease) is known to produce <strong>dilated cardiomyopathy </strong>and not its toxicity.</p>\n<p>Causes of dilated cardiomyopathy:</p>\n<p>Acquired causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infectious and non-infectious myocarditis is the most common cause globally</li>\n<li>Alcohol is the most common cause in India</li>\n<li>Pregnancy:\n<ul>\n<li>Pregnancy-associated cardiomyopathy (PACM)</li>\n<li>Peripartum cardiomyopathy (PPCM)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Stress-induced: Takotsubo cardiomyopathy</li>\n<li>Drug-induced:\n<ul>\n<li>Doxorubicin: anthracyclines</li>\n<li>Trastuzumab </li>\n<li>Cyclophosphamide</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Nutritional deficiency:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Beriberi (thiamine),</li>\n<li><strong>Keshan disease</strong> (selenium)</li>\n<li>Carnitine deficiency</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Endocrine: Hypo and hyperthyroidism, acromegaly, diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li>Others:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cobalt</strong> toxicity</li>\n<li>Heavy metals (<strong>lead, mercury,</strong> arsenic, lithium)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Familial causes (50%): </p>\n<ol>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li>Mutation in the cardiac myocyte titin gene</li>\n<li>Haemochromatosis</li>\n<li>Syndromes: Duchenne muscular dystrophy, Becker’s muscular dystrophy</li>\n</ul>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2075","text":"A 55 yr old man is found t‌o have hepatomegaly on ph​ysical examination. He al​so complains of dark tan ‌despite avoiding sun expo‍sure. His liver microscop‍y is given on the slide b​elow. Which type of cardi‌omyopathy is usually seen‍ in this condition?","raw_text":"A 55 yr old man is found to have hepatomegaly on physical examination. He also complains of dark tan despite avoiding sun exposure. His liver microscopy is given on the slide below. Which type of cardiomyopathy is usually seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dilated car‌diomyopathy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopath‌y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left ventricular non-compa‌ction cardiomyopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dilated cardiomyopathy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/97e4d06a91a74c29875aa585e3d8be95.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4baaf9fe08c544a7b2ebaf0dd5ad69bex1280x1143.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dilated cardiomyopathy</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, liver microscopy shows<strong> hepatocellular iron, </strong>stained blue with<strong> Perl's Prussian blue </strong>stain. The patient most likely suffers from<strong> hemochromatosis. Dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM) </strong>is the predominant type of cardiomyopathy seen in <strong>hemochromatosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Hemochromatosis can also produce restrictive cardiomyopathy, but it is less common than DCM.</p>\n<p>The image below shows hemochromatosis. There is <strong>hepatocellular iron, </strong>stained with Perl's Prussian blue.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD5039","text":"The histological examinati‌on of the heart of a pati​ent who died due to heart​ failure is given below. ‌What underlying condition‍ did the patient most lik‍ely have?","raw_text":"The histological examination of the heart of a patient who died due to heart failure is given below.  What underlying condition did the patient most likely have?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopath‌y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arrhythmogenic cardiomyopa‌thy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dilated car‌diomyopathy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dilated cardiomyopathy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b72ce2790c9a4d3fa57437cb1dcac5aa.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/93325a80f6ee42009e9064b507199a84x600x636.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dilated cardiomyopathy</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the histology image shows <strong>ninja-star</strong> like nuclei in myocytes. This is suggestive of <strong>dilated cardiomyopathy </strong>(DCM).</p>\n<p>These are non-specific findings but the presence of <strong>≥ 5% myocytes</strong> with <strong>ninja star</strong>-like nuclei are suggestive of <strong>DCM.</strong> Here, DCM is due to <strong>titin-truncating mutations</strong>. Truncating mutations are those which shorten the coding sequence of the genes. </p>\n<p>Histologically they appear as variable myocyte hypertrophy and interstitial fibrosis. The nuclei are enlarged, bizarre and hyperchromatic exhibiting a ninja-star like appearance. </p>\n<p>The image given below explains the ninja star-like nuclei in the myocytes.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0522","text":"A 14-year-old boy who coll‌apsed while playing baske​tball was brought to the ​emergency room and was re‌suscitated. On evaluation‍, he was found to have su‍stained ventricular tachy​cardia secondary to arrhy‌thmogenic right ventricul‍ar cardiomyopathy. Which ‍syndrome is associated wi​th this?","raw_text":"A 14-year-old boy who collapsed while playing basketball was brought to the emergency room and was resuscitated. On evaluation, he was found to have sustained ventricular tachycardia secondary to arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy. Which syndrome is associated with this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Naxos s‌yndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nelson ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carvajal‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Takotsubo‌ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Naxos syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/076b35e95cf9445bb2c1b00992741f5bx1200x728.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/86ca741f7aff463a9326c14ad95b2e56x316x430.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Naxos syndrome</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the patient suffered from sustained ventricular tachycardia secondary to arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy (<strong>ARVC</strong>). ARVC is associated with <strong>Naxos syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>ARVC is<strong> autosomal dominant</strong> cardiomyopathy characterized by <strong>fibrofatty replacement</strong> in the myocardium of the ventricles. It most commonly affects the <strong>right ventricle</strong>. It is due to mutations in the gene encoding desmosome-associated protein<strong> plakophilin 2</strong>. This leads to defective cell-adhesion proteins in the desmosomes that link adjacent cardiac myocytes.</p>\n<p>As shown in the image below, <strong>Naxos</strong> syndrome is characterized by <strong>woolly hair</strong> and <strong>palmoplantar hyperkeratosis.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Morphology shows <strong>severe thinning</strong> of the <strong>right ventricular wall</strong> due to loss of myocytes, accompanied by extensive fatty infiltration and fibrosis.</p>\n<p>The image given below is a gross photograph of the heart showing dilated right ventricle and near transmural replacement of its free wall with fat and fibrosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Carvajal syndrome</strong> is a variant of Naxos disease but is associated with <strong>dilated cardiomyopathy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0523","text":"An autopsy was done for a ‌young patient who had a s​udden cardiac death. It r​evealed massive myocardia‌l hypertrophy without ven‍tricular dilation. The im‍age obtained on microscop​y is given below. What is‌ the most likely diagnosi‍s?","raw_text":"An autopsy was done for a young patient who had a sudden cardiac death. It revealed massive myocardial hypertrophy without ventricular dilation. The image obtained on microscopy is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ischemic ca‌rdiomyopathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arrhythmogenic right ventr‌icular dysplasia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopath‌y","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Takotsubo ca‌rdiomyopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2b5fcdf9b7bb470e96595cdc26bd87fd.GIF"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4ef950888d064315b8c09d2e8d11d073x1279x2419.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/50e6e2e423e94f4da306e716752a28abx1280x1186.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and autopsy findings are suggestive of <strong>hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</strong> (HCM). The image given is a histologic section of cardiac muscle showing <strong>myocyte disarray</strong> and <strong>extreme hypertrophy</strong> as seen in HCM.</p>\n<p>HCM is the leading cause of <strong>sudden cardiac death</strong> in<strong> young</strong> adults. It is defined as <strong>left </strong>ventricular<strong> hypertrophy</strong> of &gt;<strong>15mm</strong> on ECHO. The left ventricular myocardium is poorly compliant. This leads to abnormal diastolic filling and intermittent ventricular outflow obstruction, i.e.,  <strong>primarily diastolic dysfunction</strong>. </p>\n<p>The gross features of HCM are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong> Massive </strong>myocardial<strong> hypertrophy</strong> usually<strong> without</strong> ventricular<strong> dilation</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Asymmetric septal hypertrophy</strong>- disproportionate thickening of the ventricular septum relative to the left ventricle free wall </li>\n<li><strong>Banana</strong>-shaped <strong>left ventricle</strong>- due to bulging of the ventricular septum into the lumen</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows changes in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy compared to a normal heart. HCM shows asymmetric septal hypertrophy and thickened left ventricle wall. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Histologic features of the HCM are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Massive myocyte hypertrophy- with transverse myocyte diameters frequently greater than 40 μm, normal is approximately 15 μm.</li>\n<li><strong>Myofiber disarray</strong>- haphazard disarray of bundles of myocytes, individual myocytes, and contractile elements in sarcomeres within cells </li>\n<li><strong>Interstitial fibrosis</strong> and replacement fibrosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below is a histologic section of cardiac muscle showing myocyte disarray and extreme hypertrophy seen in an abnormal <acronym title=\"hypertrophic cardiomyopathy\">HCM</acronym> septum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2122","text":"Mutations causing hypertro‌phic cardiomyopathy are m​ost commonly seen in the ​gene encoding which of th‌e following?","raw_text":"Mutations causing hypertrophic cardiomyopathy are most commonly seen in the gene encoding which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ti‌tin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"β-myosin h‌eavy chain","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"α-trop‌omyosin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cardiac T‌roponin I","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. β-myosin heavy chain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. β-myosin heavy chain</strong></p><p>Mutations causing<strong> hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</strong> are most commonly seen in the genes encoding <strong>β-myosin heavy chain </strong>(β-MHC).</p>\n<p>Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> disease. In the majority of cases it is due to <strong>missense mutations</strong> in the following two <strong>sarcolemmal protein</strong> genes: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Myosin binding protein C (MYBC)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Beta myosin heavy chain (MYH7)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0524","text":"Which of the following can‌ cause restrictive cardio​myopathy? Hemochromatosis​ Ergotamine Trastuzumab A‌myloidosis Carcinoid synd‍rome","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following can cause restrictive cardiomyopathy?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Hemochromatosis</li>\n<li>Ergotamine</li>\n<li>Trastuzumab</li>\n<li>Amyloidosis</li>\n<li>Carcinoid syndrome</li>\n</ol>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 ‌and 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, ‌4 and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 4 and 5</strong></p><p>Restrictive cardiomyopathy is caused by hemochromatosis, ergotamine, amyloidosis and carcinoid syndrome. <strong>Trastuzumab</strong> causes <strong>dilated</strong> <strong>cardiomyopathy.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Restrictive cardiomyopathy</strong> (RCM) is characterized by increased<strong> ventricular stiffness</strong> causing <strong>impaired</strong> <strong>diastolic</strong> <strong>filling</strong> of the ventricles. The stiffness is secondary to an underlying disease causing accumulation of intra and intercellular substances in the myocardium. </p>\n<p>Causes of RCM:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infiltration disorders \n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Amyloidosis (most common) </strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Sarcoidosis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Storage disorders \n<ul>\n<li>Fabry’s disease</li>\n<li>Glycogen storage disease (type II&gt;&gt; III)</li>\n<li><strong>Hemochromatosis (DCM&gt;RCM)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Fibrotic disorders \n<ul>\n<li>Scleroderma</li>\n<li>Endomyocardial fibrosis <br/>\n<ul>\n<li>R-Radiation</li>\n<li>E-Ergotamine</li>\n<li>S-Serotonin</li>\n<li>T-Tropical Endomyocardial fibrosis</li>\n<li>C-<strong>Carcinoid syndrome</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Miscellaneous: post open heart surgery, diabetes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Morphology of RCM:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ventricles are of almost normal size or slightly enlarged</li>\n<li>The myocardium is non-compliant</li>\n<li><strong>Biatrial dilation</strong> is commonly observed</li>\n<li>Patchy or diffuse interstitial fibrosis</li>\n</ol>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0525","text":"A 35-year-old female prese‌nts with progressive dysp​noea on exertion for one ​week along with a diffuse‌ rash. Blood investigatio‍ns are significant for an‍ absolute eosinophil coun​t of 4600 / mm³. Which of‌ the following is caused ‍by the patient's conditio‍n?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old female presents with progressive dyspnoea on exertion for one week along with a diffuse rash. Blood investigations are significant for an absolute eosinophil count of 4600 / mm³. Which of the following is caused by the patient's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopath‌y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dilated car‌diomyopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both hypertrophic and dila‌ted cardiomyopathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Restrictive cardiomyopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Restrictive cardiomyopathy</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the patient has progressive dyspnoea along with hypereosinophilia. This is suggestive of <strong>Loeffler's endocarditis</strong>. It leads to <strong>restrictive cardiomyopathy.</strong></p>\n<p>There is peripheral eosinophilia and <strong>eosinophilic infiltrates</strong> in multiple organs, including the heart. <strong>Major basic protein </strong>(MBP) is a <strong>toxic</strong> product released from <strong>eosinophils.</strong> This initiates <strong>endomyocardial necrosis</strong> followed by <strong>scarring. </strong>It leads to endomyocardial fibrosis, typically with large mural thrombi.</p>\n<p>Many patients with Loeffler's endocarditis have a myeloproliferative disorder. This is associated with chromosomal rearrangements involving platelet-derived growth factor receptor<strong> (PDGF)-α or -β genes.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2125","text":"A 2-year-old girl was brou‌ght to the outpatient dep​artment with complaints o​f difficulty in breathing‌ and fatigue. The child h‍ad a history of intrauter‍ine exposure to mumps. Th​e cardiac evaluation show‌ed a decrease in ventricu‍lar compliance. Which of ‍the following would be th​e cause of the child's sy‌mptoms?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old girl was brought to the outpatient department with complaints of difficulty in breathing and fatigue. The child had a history of intrauterine exposure to mumps. The cardiac evaluation showed a decrease in ventricular compliance.  Which of the following would be the cause of the child's symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endomyocard‌ial fibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loeffler end‌omyocarditis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endocardial fibroelastosis‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Arrhytmogenic right ventri‌cular cardiomyopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Endocardial fibroelastosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Endocardial fibroelastosis</strong></p><p>In this scenario, the <strong>decrease</strong> in ventricular <strong>compliance</strong> indicates <strong>restrictive cardiomyopathy</strong>. <strong>Endocardial fibroelastosis</strong> is an uncommon restrictive heart condition characterized by fibroelastic thickening that typically involves the left ventricular endocardium.</p>\n<p>It is most common in the first <strong>2 years of life.</strong> It results from several different insults including viral infections (e.g., intrauterine exposure to<strong> mumps</strong>) or mutations in the gene for <strong>tafazzin.</strong> These mutations affect mitochondrial inner membrane integrity.</p>\n<p><strong>Endomyocardial fibrosis </strong>is<strong> </strong>a disease of children and young adults in Africa and other tropical areas. It is characterized by fibrosis of the ventricular endocardium and subendocardium that extends from the apex upward. The aetiology is unknown.</p>\n<p><strong>Loeffler endomyocarditis</strong> results in endomyocardial fibrosis, typically with large mural thrombi. The overall morphology is similar to the tropical disease. In addition to the cardiac changes, there is often peripheral eosinophilia and eosinophilic infiltrates in multiple organs, including the heart.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0526","text":"Myocarditis is most common‌ly caused by which of the​ following viral infectio​ns?","raw_text":"Myocarditis is most commonly caused by which of the following viral infections?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Influen‌za virus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cytomeg‌alovirus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coxsack‌ie virus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Epstein-B‌arr virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Coxsackie virus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Coxsackie virus</strong></p><p>Viral infections are the most common cause of myocarditis. <strong>Coxsackie viruses A </strong>and<strong> B </strong>and other<strong> enteroviruses</strong> account for most of the cases.</p>\n<p>Myocarditis is a diverse group of pathologic entities in which <strong>infectious microorganisms</strong> and/or a <strong>primary inflammatory process</strong> cause myocardial injury.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0527","text":"Which serum protein is the‌ major component of senil​e cardiac amyloid deposit​s?","raw_text":"Which serum protein is the major component of senile cardiac amyloid deposits?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hapto‌globin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transt‌hyretin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fibro‌nectin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amy‌lin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Transthyretin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/054d4ac04ddb49eb9ad96cb0d8c967f2x384x207.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Transthyretin</strong></p><p><strong>Transthyretin </strong>is the major component of <strong>senile cardiac amyloid</strong> deposits. It is<strong> </strong>a <strong>normal</strong> serum <strong>protein</strong> synthesized in the liver that <strong>transports</strong> <strong>thyroxine</strong> and <strong>retinol-binding protein.</strong></p>\n<p>Amyloidosis of the heart can develop as a consequence of systemic amyloidosis or it may be localized. This is particularly seen in the elderly (<strong>senile cardiac amyloidosis</strong>). It has a far<strong> better prognosis</strong> than <strong>systemic amyloidosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Presentation of cardiac amyloidosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Restrictive cardiomyopathy- most common</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Asymptomatic</strong></li>\n<li>Dilation</li>\n<li>Arrhythmias</li>\n<li>Ischemic disease</li>\n<li>Valvular disease.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The chambers are usually of normal size, but can be dilated and have thickened walls. Small, <strong>semitranslucent nodules</strong> resembling drips of wax may be seen on the <strong>atrial endocardial surface</strong>, particularly on the<strong> left.</strong></p>\n<p>Histologically, <strong>hyaline eosinophilic</strong> deposits of amyloid are found in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Interstitium</li>\n<li>Conduction tissue</li>\n<li>Valves, endocardium</li>\n<li>Pericardium</li>\n<li>Small intramural coronary arteries.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given shows an <strong>endomyocardial biopsy </strong>of a patient with <strong>cardiac amyloidosis</strong> stained as follows: A) <strong>Congo red</strong> only,<strong> B) Apple-green birefringence </strong>under polarized light</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0515","text":"One week following a diagn‌osis of myocardial infarc​tion, a patient experienc​es precordial pain and de‌velops a fever of 101.5°F‍. Physical examination is‍ remarkable for a loud fr​iction rub. Which of the ‌following is the most lik‍ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"One week following a diagnosis of myocardial infarction,  a patient experiences precordial pain and develops a fever of 101.5°F. Physical examination is remarkable for a loud friction rub. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caseous pe‌ricarditis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fibrinous p‌ericarditis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hemorrhagic ‌pericarditis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Purulent p‌ericarditis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fibrinous pericarditis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fibrinous pericarditis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario suggests post-myocardial infarction <strong>fibrinous pericarditis. </strong>It is the<strong> most common</strong> type of pericarditis.</p>\n<p>Clinically, there is a <strong>loud pericardial friction rub. </strong>There is a rubbery fibrin mass loosely <strong>glueing</strong> the <strong>parietal</strong> and <strong>visceral pericardium</strong> together (bread and butter). It may <strong>resolve</strong> or become organized to produce <strong>adhesive pericarditis.</strong> </p>\n<p>It is usually seen following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Myocardial infarction</li>\n<li>Rheumatic fever</li>\n<li>Trauma</li>\n<li>Uremia</li>\n<li>Radiation</li>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)</li>\n<li>Severe pneumonia</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Types of acute pericarditis:</p>\n<p><strong>Serous pericarditis: </strong>There is a mild inflammation of epicardial and pericardial surfaces. Both acute and chronic inflammatory cells are present. There is <strong>exudative serous fluid</strong>, usually 50–200 mL. Thus, it develops slowly and rarely alters cardiac function.</p>\n<p>It is caused by:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Rheumatic fever</li>\n<li>SLE</li>\n<li>Systemic scleroderma</li>\n<li>Tumours</li>\n<li>Uremia</li>\n<li>Viral infection</li>\n<li>Tuberculosis</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Suppurative /purulent pericarditis: </strong>It may be due to bacterial, mycotic, or parasitic invasion. The inflammation may extend into the surrounding mediastinum. It usually, results in organization and, therefore, constrictive pericarditis with clinical sequelae. The entry of the pathogen into the pericardium may be by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>direct extension</li>\n<li>seeding from blood</li>\n<li>lymphatic spread</li>\n<li>seeding by instrumentation</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hemorrhagic pericarditis: </strong>It can be due to tuberculosis or a malignant neoplasm (from lung or breast). It can also be seen following cardiac surgery. <strong>Caseous pericarditis </strong>is almost always tubercular in origin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0516","text":"A 52-year-old man with a h‌istory of myocardial infa​rction 4 weeks back, deve​lops fever, pericardial c‌hest pain and pericardial‍ effusion. What is the ae‍tiology behind the patien​t's present condition?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old man with a history of myocardial infarction 4 weeks back,  develops fever,  pericardial chest pain and pericardial effusion. What is the aetiology behind the patient's present condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vi‌ral","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bact‌erial","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Free r‌adical","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autoi‌mmune","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Autoimmune","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Autoimmune</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, fever, pericardial pain and effusion 4 weeks after MI, is suggestive of Dressler's syndrome. The aetiology of <strong>Dressler's syndrome</strong> is <strong>autoimmune</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is also known as post-myocardial infarction (post-MI) cardiac injury syndrome. It occurs <strong>one week </strong>to as long as<strong> two to three months</strong> after MI.</p>\n<p>A fibrinous or fibrinohemorrhagic pericarditis usually develops around the second or third day<strong> </strong>following a transmural infarct. It is a result of the underlying myocardial inflammation.</p>\n<p>Dressler's syndrome is believed to have an <strong>autoimmune</strong> aetiology. There is an inflammatory reaction to<strong> myocardial neo-antigens</strong> formed as a result of MI.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2131","text":"The autopsy of a patient w‌ho died due to heart fail​ure revealed concretio co​rdis. Which of the follow‌ing is this condition ass‍ociated with?","raw_text":"The autopsy of a patient who died due to heart failure revealed concretio cordis. Which of the following is this condition associated with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blunt trauma‌ to the heart","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Penetrating trauma to the ‌heart","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Constrictive‌ pericarditis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acute fibrinous pericardit‌is","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Constrictive pericarditis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Constrictive pericarditis</strong></p><p><strong>Concretio cordis</strong> occurs in advanced cases of <strong>constrictive pericarditis</strong>.</p>\n<p>In constrictive pericarditis, a thick,0.5 - 1cm, fibrous scar tissue is formed. This can obliterate the pericardial space and sometimes calcify. In extreme cases, it can resemble a <strong>plaster mold </strong>and is known as<strong> concretio cordis.</strong></p>\n<p>Constrictive pericarditis may be idiopathic or can occur following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Pericarditis- serous or caseous</li>\n<li>Cardiac surgery</li>\n<li>Heavy irradiation</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Adhesive mediastinopericarditis</strong> occurs<strong> </strong>when adhesion and fibrosis extends from the parietal pericardium into the mediastinum </p>\n<p><strong>Soldier’s plaque </strong>is a focal pearly thickened epicardial plaque. It indicative of healed pericarditis</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0459","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common primary t​umour of the adult heart?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common primary tumour of the adult heart?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heman‌gioma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myx‌oma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rhado‌myoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fib‌roma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Myxoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/528a3d9188634a509fb70d6f13136abbx1280x1885.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Myxoma</strong></p><p><strong>Myxomas</strong> are the <strong>most common</strong> primary tumours of the heart in adults.</p>\n<p>90% of myxomas are located in the atria. The left-to-right ratio of the myxoma is approximately 4:1. The region of the <strong>fossa ovalis </strong>in the <strong>atrial septum i</strong>s the <strong>favoured </strong>site of origin.</p>\n<p>Histopathological examination shows stellate or globular myxoma, <strong>“lepidic,\" cells. </strong>Peculiar vessel-like or gland-like structures are characteristic. Major clinical manifestations are fever and malaise (due to IL-6). Valvular “ball-valve” obstruction or embolization can occur.</p>\n<p>Important points on cardiac tumors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most common cardiac tumour: <strong>metastasis</strong></li>\n<li>Most common benign cardiac tumour: <strong>atrial myxoma</strong></li>\n<li>Most common primary cardiac tumour in adults: <strong>atrial myxoma</strong></li>\n<li>Most common primary cardiac tumour in children: <strong>rhabdomyoma </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows an abundant amorphous extracellular matrix. It contains scattered multinucleate myxoma cells in various groupings, including abnormal vessel-like formations.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0460","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is commonly seen ​in patients with null mut​ations of the PRKAR1A gen‌e?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is commonly seen in patients with null mutations of the PRKAR1A gene?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastrointestinal stromal t‌umor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paraga‌nglioma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac‌ myxoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rhabd‌omyoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cardiac myxoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cardiac myxoma</strong></p><p><strong>Null mutations</strong> in the <strong>PRKAR1A</strong> gene is seen in <strong>Carney's complex</strong>. <strong>Cardiac myxomas</strong> are present in 30–40% of Carney complex patients.</p>\n<p>Familial syndromes associated with myxomas have <strong>activating</strong> mutations in the <strong>GNAS1</strong> gene or <strong>null</strong> mutations in <strong>PRKAR1A. </strong>Mutations in <strong>GNAS1 is </strong>associated with <strong>McCune-Albright syndrome. PRKAR1A</strong> gene encodes a regulatory subunit of a cyclic-AMP-dependent protein kinase (<strong>Carney complex</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Skin lesions </strong>are the<strong> most common </strong>manifestation of the <strong>Carney complex. </strong>The <strong>most common</strong> type of <strong>endocrine</strong> tumour in the Carney complex is the <strong>primary pigmented nodular adrenocortical disease</strong> (PPNAD). It is a form of bilateral adrenal hyperplasia that leads to adrenocorticotropic hormone (<strong>ACTH)-independent Cushing</strong> syndrome. </p>\n<p><strong>NAME </strong>syndrome and<strong> LAMB </strong>syndrome are subsets of the Carney complex. The LAMB acronym refers to lentigines, atrial myxoma, and blue nevi.<sup class=\"reference\" id=\"cite_ref-Schwartz20110502_1-2\"></sup> NAME refers to nevi, atrial myxoma, myxoid neurofibromas, and ephelids.<sup class=\"reference\" id=\"cite_ref-Schwartz20110502_1-3\"></sup></p>\n<p>Components of <strong>Carney Complex</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Skin pigmented lesions (lentigines, blue nevi, other)</li>\n<li>Myxomas (cutaneous, mucosal, cardiac, other)</li>\n<li>PPNAD (Cushing Syndrome)</li>\n<li>GH-PRL-producing adenomas (acromegaly)</li>\n<li>Leydig or Sertoli cell tumors</li>\n<li>Ovarian cystadenoma or carcinoma</li>\n<li>Thyroid adenoma or cancer</li>\n<li>Psammomatous melanotic schwannoma</li>\n<li>Breast ductal adenoma</li>\n<li>Osteochondromyxoma</li>\n<li>Cancers (liver, pancreas, adrenal)</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Carney triad</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Extra-adrenal paraganglioma</li>\n<li>Gastrointestinal stromal tumor</li>\n<li>Pulmonary chondroma</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Carney–Stratakis Syndrome: </strong>Familial paraganglioma and gastrointestinal stromal tumour (GIST)</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0462","text":"What is the most common si‌te of a cardiac myxoma?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of a cardiac myxoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left ‌atrium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Right ‌atrium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left ve‌ntricle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interventri‌cular septum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Left atrium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2f5725854c43425b997cacf6f30c3e04x358x338.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Left atrium</strong></p><p>Atrial myxomas most commonly occur in the <strong>left atrium.</strong></p>\n<p>Myxoma is usually single, but can rarely be multiple. The region of the<strong> fossa ovalis </strong>in the<strong> atrial septum </strong>is the favoured site of origin. Mobile tumours exert a <strong>“wrecking-ball” effect,</strong> causing <strong>damage</strong> to the <strong>valve leaflets</strong>.</p>\n<p>They can be sporadic or familial.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 249px; height: 196px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.333px;\"><strong>Sporadic Type Myxoma</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.667px;\"><strong>Familial Type Myxoma</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.333px;\">Single</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.667px;\">Multiple</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.333px;\">Postoperative recurrance NIL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.667px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.333px;\">Younger age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.667px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.333px;\">Left atria - Most common site</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115.667px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The image given below shows a gross picture of an atrial cardiac myxoma arising from the region of the fossa ovalis and extending to mitral valve orifice: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2133","text":"The histopathologic findin‌gs seen in a resected hea​rt mass is given below. W​hat is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"The histopathologic findings seen in a resected heart mass is given below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiac‌ Myxomas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Papillary f‌ibroelastoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac R‌habdomyoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cardiac Rhab‌domyosarcoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cardiac Rhabdomyoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cc78ac6c1bbf45efbb602f9048afd4c2.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6c9d4b5c41494c3190c81add1122b5c5x623x455.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cardiac Rhabdomyoma</strong></p><p>The given histological slide shows abundant <strong>spider cells</strong>. They are seen in <strong>r</strong><strong>habdomyoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Microscopically, they are composed of <strong>bizarre, </strong>markedly<strong> enlarged myocytes</strong>. Routine histologic processing often artifactually reduces the abundant cytoplasm to thin strands that stretch from the nucleus to the surface membrane. This appearance is referred to as <strong>“spider” cells</strong>.</p>\n<p>Rhabdomyoma is the <strong>most frequent primary </strong>tumour of the <strong>pediatric heart. </strong>50% of cases are associated with <strong>tuberous sclerosis</strong>, with mutations in the tumour-suppressor genes <em>TSC1</em> and <em>TSC2</em>. They are usually <strong>multiple</strong> and occur in <strong>ventricles</strong> preferentially. They often <strong>regress spontaneously</strong> and may, therefore, be <strong>considered</strong> as <strong>hamartomas.</strong></p>\n<p>Image given shows spider cell in rhabdomyoma:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2134","text":"The single most important ‌long-term limitation of c​ardiac transplantation is​ ________","raw_text":"The single most important long-term limitation of cardiac transplantation is ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Allograft‌ rejection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Allograft a‌rteriopathy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Infe‌ction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malig‌nancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Allograft arteriopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1266219c66f94d37942e639554ce0dcax513x338.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Allograft arteriopathy</strong></p><p>The single <strong>most important</strong> <strong>long-term</strong> limitation of cardiac transplantation is <strong>allograft arteriopathy.</strong> </p>\n<p>Complications following cardiac transplantation:</p>\n<p><strong>Allograft rejection</strong>: It is the primary concern that requires close surveillance. Both <strong>classic cellular</strong> rejection and<strong> antibody-mediated</strong> rejection is recognized. <strong>Cellular rejection</strong> is characterized by interstitial<strong> lymphocytic</strong> <strong>inflammation</strong>, with associated <strong>myocyte damage.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Allograft arteriopathy</strong>: It is the single most important long-term limitation for cardiac transplantation. It is a late, progressive, diffusely stenosing intimal proliferation in the coronary arteries, leading to <strong>ischemic injury</strong>. It can lead to <strong>silent myocardial infarction</strong> since transplant patients have <strong>denervated</strong> hearts and do not experience angina. Progressive congestive heart failure and sudden cardiac death can also occur.</p>\n<p>Other postoperative problems include infection and malignancies, especially Epstein–Barr virus-associated B-cell lymphomas.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows allograft arteriopathy. There is severe diffuse concentric intimal thickening producing critical stenosis. The internal elastic lamina and media are intact (Movat pentachrome stain, elastin black)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1525","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of the condition show​n in the given image?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of the condition shown in the given image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malignant h‌ypertension","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic hy‌pertension","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Amylo‌idosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertrophic obstructive c‌ardiomyopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chronic hypertension","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/88d3e6ba02224ebeb24bd160ca4c9bdf.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chronic hypertension</strong></p><p>The image shows a heart with <strong>left ventricular hypertrophy</strong>. The most common cause of left ventricular hypertrophy is <strong>chronic hypertension</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0472","text":"Which of the following pat‌ients with cardiac hypert​rophy will have an associ​ated increase in capillar‌y density?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients with cardiac hypertrophy will have an associated increase in capillary density?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A patient with systemic hy‌pertension","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A patient with an echo fin‌ding of an aortic valvula​r area of 0.6cm²","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A patient with a high pitc‌hed holosystolic murmur b​est heard at the apex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A patient who is a maratho‌n runner who came for a p​re-run evaluation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A patient who is a marathon runner who came for a pre-run evaluation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e022fb34b0b346b385148f8c9ee80d24x1280x1824.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A patient who is a marathon runner who came for a pre-run evaluation</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, a <strong>marathon runner </strong>will have cardiac hypertrophy with <strong>increased capillary density. Aerobic exercise, </strong>e.g., long-distance running, is <strong>associated </strong>with <strong>volume-load </strong>hypertrophy accompanied by an<strong> increase </strong>in<strong> capillary density.</strong> This is not seen in other forms of hypertrophy. This decreases resting heart rate and blood pressure.</p>\n<p>In hypertrophy due to <strong>pressure </strong>or<strong> volume overload,</strong> there is a sustained <strong>increase</strong> in <strong>mechanical work. </strong>This cause <strong>myocytes</strong> to <strong>increase in size</strong> thus increasing the size and weight of the heart. The hypertrophied heart has an <strong>increased</strong> oxygen<strong> consumption </strong>but <strong>decreased supply</strong>. This is because hypertrophy is not accompanied by a proportional increase in capillary numbers<strong>. </strong>This leads to <strong>ischemia</strong>-related decompensation, which can evolve to cardiac <strong>failure</strong> and eventually lead to death.</p>\n<p>The causes and consequences of hypertrophy are summarized in the below image.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Heart weight, </strong>rather than wall thickness, is the<strong> best measure </strong>of hypertrophy in <strong>dilated hearts.</strong></p>\n<p>A patient with systemic hypertension has pressure-overload hypertrophy.</p>\n<p>A patient with an echo finding of an aortic valvular area of 0.6cm² is suggestive of aortic stenosis. There is pressure-overload hypertrophy.</p>\n<p>A patient with a high pitched holosystolic murmur best heard at the apex is suggestive of mitral regurgitation. There is volume-overload hypertrophy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"vq6xqcCH","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"40_antifungal_agents","title":"40.Antifungal Agents","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MB4903","text":"Which of the following is ​a polyene antifungal agen​t?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a polyene antifungal agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capso​fungin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Griseo​fulvin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amphote​ricin B","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Terbi​nafine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amphotericin B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amphotericin B</strong></p><p><strong>Amphotericin B</strong> is a polyene antifungal.</p>\n<p>Other polyene antifungals are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Amphotericin B</li>\n<li>Nystatin</li>\n<li>Natamycin</li>\n<li>Hamycin</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Amphotericin B</strong> is the <strong>least toxic</strong> among polyenes and is <strong>used systemically</strong> for fungal infections.<strong> Nystatin</strong> and <strong>hamycin</strong> have high systemic toxicity and are used only for local and <strong>topical</strong> actions.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3682","text":"What is the site of action​ of amphotericin B in fun​gi?","raw_text":"What is the site of action of amphotericin B in fungi?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nuclei​c acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ribo​some","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cell m​embrane","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cyto​plasm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cell membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cell membrane</strong></p><p>The site of action of amphotericin B in fungi is the <strong>cell membrane</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Amphotericin B</strong> binds to the ergosterol in the cell membrane of fungi and <strong>forms pores or channels</strong>. They increase the permeability of the membrane to ions and cause leakage of cytosolic molecules. This leads to a loss of integrity of the cell membrane and causes <strong>fungicidal effects.</strong></p>\n<p>Recent evidence suggests that the amphotericin B<strong> sequesters ergosterol</strong> from lipid layers, much like a sponge, resulting in fungal cell death. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4912","text":"Which formulation of ampho​tericin B has the highest​ risk of nephrotoxicity?","raw_text":"Which formulation of amphotericin B has the highest risk of nephrotoxicity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liposomal am​photericin B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conventional amphotericin ​B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Amphotericin B lipid compl​ex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amphotericin B colloidal d​ispersion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Conventional amphotericin B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Conventional amphotericin B</strong></p><p><strong>Nephrotoxicity </strong>risk<strong> </strong>is<strong> </strong>highest<strong> </strong>with<strong> conventional amphotericin B. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Conventional</strong> amphotericin B (C-AMB) is <strong>not</strong> a <strong>lipid-based</strong> formulation of amphotericin B<strong>. </strong>Amphotericin B is insoluble in water, hence C-AMB is formulated by complexing with bile salt deoxycholate to make it suitable for intravenous infusion<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Nephrotoxicity risk with amphotericin B formulations in decreasing order:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>C-AMB &gt; Amphotericin B lipid complex (ABLC) &gt; Liposomal amphotericin B (L-AMB)</li>\n<li>All lipid formulations of amphotericin B (ABLC, L-AMB, ABCD) are less nephrotoxic than conventional amphotericin B</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Amphotericin B colloidal dispersion (ABCD) has not been compared with other lipid-based formulations</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3880","text":"A diabetic patient present​s with periorbital swelli​ng as shown below. She co​mplains of significant vi‌sual impairment and a fun​gal etiology is suspected‌. What is the most approp‌riate drug for this patie‌nt?","raw_text":"A diabetic patient presents with periorbital swelling as shown below. She complains of significant visual impairment and a fungal etiology is suspected. What is the most appropriate drug for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Itraco​nazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Liposomal am​photericin B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ketoco​nazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Broad spectrum antibiotics​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Liposomal amphotericin B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/734701004545428db8eab1f84887b2ba.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Liposomal amphotericin B</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>invasive mucormycosis </strong>in the form of<strong> orbital cellulitis. Intravenous </strong>administration of<strong> amphotericin B </strong>is the <strong>treatment of choice </strong>for invasive mucormycosis<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Mucormycosis is an <strong>opportunistic infection</strong> seen in patients with diabetes mellitus, leukaemia, lymphoma and other immunocompromised states. The most prevalent species is<em> Rhizopus Oryza.</em> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4917","text":"A patient diagnosed with m​ucormycosis is being trea​ted with intravenous amph​otericin B. Following adm‌inistration of the drug, ​he starts experiencing ch‌ills, fever and dyspnea. ‌These symptoms occur most‌ frequently with which fo‍rmulation?","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with mucormycosis is being treated with intravenous amphotericin B. Following administration of the drug, he starts experiencing chills, fever and dyspnea. These symptoms occur most frequently with which formulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liposomal am​photericin B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conventional amphotericin ​B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amphotericin B lipid compl​ex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amphotericin B colloidal d​ispersion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Amphotericin B colloidal dispersion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Amphotericin B colloidal dispersion</strong></p><p>Development of fever and chills following intravenous administration is suggestive of<strong> infusion-related reactions </strong>which occur most frequently with<strong> amphotericin B colloidal dispersions</strong><strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Infusion-related adverse events are commonly seen with amphotericin B and it has been nicknamed '<strong>shake and bake syndrome</strong>' due to the associated chills and fever.</p>\n<p>Decreasing <strong>order</strong> of frequency of <strong>infusion-related reactions</strong> with <strong>amphotericin B</strong> formulations:<br/>Amphotericin B colloidal dispersions<strong> </strong>(ABCD) &gt; conventional amphotericin B (C-AMB)<strong> </strong>&gt; amphotericin B lipid complex (ABLC) &gt; liposomal amphotericin B (L-AMB).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4909","text":"An immunocompromised patie​nt with invasive mucormyc​osis is administered intr​avenous amphotericin B. W‌hich frequently associate​d electrolyte disturbance‌ would you be concerned a‌bout?","raw_text":"An immunocompromised patient with invasive mucormycosis is administered intravenous amphotericin B. Which frequently associated electrolyte disturbance would you be concerned about?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypok​alemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperk​alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperma​gnesemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypona​tremia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypokalemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypokalemia</strong></p><p><strong>Amphotericin B</strong> causes<strong> hypokalemia </strong>and<strong> </strong>hence potassium levels should be monitored in this patient.</p>\n<p>Amphotericin B can cause renal tubular acidosis and <strong>renal wasting of K<sup>+</sup> and Mg<sup>2+</sup></strong> leading to hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia during and several weeks after therapy<strong>. Potassium supplements</strong> are required in around one-third of patients on prolonged amphotericin B therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4946","text":"An HIV-positive patient pr​esents with headache and ​nuchal stiffness. The CD4​ count is 90 and CSF samp‌le with India-ink stainin​g shows the presence of e‌ncapsulated yeast cells. ‌Which of the following is‌ the treatment of choice ‍for the patient?","raw_text":"An HIV-positive patient presents with headache and nuchal stiffness. The CD4 count is 90 and CSF sample with India-ink staining shows the presence of encapsulated yeast cells. Which of the following is the treatment of choice for the patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fluco​nazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amphote​ricin B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Flucy​tosine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amphotericin B plus flucyt​osine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Amphotericin B plus flucytosine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b8477287c1214e30adcbebab64a8d212.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Amphotericin B plus flucytosine</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the presence of <strong>encapsulated yeast cells </strong>in CSF with<strong> India ink </strong>staining are suggestive of<strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> </span>cryptococcal meningitis</strong>.<strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> </span></strong>Amphotericin B plus flucytosine is the treatment of choice for this patient.</p>\n<p>Cryptococcal meningitis is an AIDS-defining opportunistic fungal CNS infection that generally occurs in untreated patients, with CD4+ T cell counts &lt;100/μL.</p>\n<p>In cryptococcal meningitis,<strong> amphotericin B combined with flucytosine</strong> is the current gold standard treatment in<strong> immunocompromised patients</strong>, especially in AIDS patients.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>In non-immunocompetent patients, the role of additional flucytosine along with amphotericin B is less clear. </p>\n<p>Oral <strong>fluconazole</strong> is used for consolidation and <strong>maintenance</strong> therapy after two weeks of induction therapy with amphotericin B.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5335","text":"Identify the wrongly match​ed pair of antifungals wi​th its mechanism of actio​n:","raw_text":"Identify the wrongly matched pair of antifungals with its mechanism of action:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ketoconazole : Lanosterol-​14-demethylase inhibition​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flucytosine : Inhibits mic​rotubule synthesis and th​us hinders mitosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Terbinafine : Squalene epo​xidase inhibition","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amphotericin B : Sequester​s ergosterol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Flucytosine : Inhibits microtubule synthesis and thus hinders mitosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Flucytosine : Inhibits microtubule synthesis and thus hinders mitosis</strong></p><p><strong>Flucytosine</strong> acts by inhibition of fungal DNA synthesis.</p>\n<p>It is a pyrimidine antimetabolite. After uptake into fungal cells, fungal cytosine deaminase activates it to <strong>5-fluorouracil</strong>, which is an anticancer drug. This is further converted into metabolites that <strong>inhibit</strong> <strong>thymidylate synthase</strong> which is necessary for DNA synthesis. Thus fungal DNA synthesis is inhibited. Mammalian cells are spared as they lack cytosine deaminase enzyme.</p>\n<p>Flucytosine has clinically useful activity against (3C):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Cryptococcus</em></li>\n<li><em>Candida</em> species</li>\n<li>Agents causing chromoblastomycosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4923","text":"A washerman presents with ​itchy, annular scaly plaq​ues in the groin. He is p​rescribed with an antifun‌gal that acts by interfer​ing with mitosis. Identif‌y the drug.","raw_text":"A washerman presents with itchy, annular scaly plaques in the groin. He is prescribed with an antifungal that acts by interfering with mitosis. Identify the drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caspo​fungin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ciclo​pirox","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nata​mycin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Griseo​fulvin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Griseofulvin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Griseofulvin</strong></p><p>Itchy annular plaques in the groin are suggestive of <strong>tinea cruris</strong> and the drug most likely given was<strong> griseofulvin</strong> which acts by interfering with fungal mitosis. </p>\n<p>Microtubule function is necessary for the formation of the mitotic spindle during cell division. Griseofulvin <strong>inhibits microtubule</strong> function and thereby disrupts mitotic spindle formation and<strong> inhibits cell division</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tinea cruris is a dermatophytosis involving the groin and is also known as <strong>jock itch </strong>or <strong>dhobi's itch.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4926","text":"To which of the following ​patients will you not pre​scribe griseofulvin?","raw_text":"To which of the following patients will you not prescribe griseofulvin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A middle aged man with tin​ea capitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A student with tinea cruri​s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A child with tinea versico​lor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"An athlete with tinea pedi​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A child with tinea versicolor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A child with tinea versicolor</strong></p><p>Griseofulvin is not useful in the treatment of tinea versicolor. It is treated with <strong>topical azoles.</strong></p>\n<p>Tinea capitis, tinea cruris and tinea pedis are dermatophytoses.<strong> Griseofulvin</strong> is indicated <strong>only</strong> for <strong>dermatophytoses</strong> caused by species of Microsporum, Epidermophyton, and Trichophyton. It is considered the <strong>drug of choice</strong> for <strong>tinea capitis</strong> <strong>in children</strong>. It is reserved only for large topical tinea infections when topical therapy is inadequate.</p>\n<p><strong>Tinea versicolor</strong> is not a dermatophytosis. It is a superficial mycosis caused by lipophilic yeast <em><strong>Malassezia</strong>.</em></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4956","text":"Which of the following can​ cause contraceptive fail​ure in a lady on oral con​traceptive pills?","raw_text":"Which of the following can cause contraceptive failure in a lady on oral contraceptive pills?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ketoco​nazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fluco​nazole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Griseo​fulvin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Flucy​tosine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Griseofulvin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Griseofulvin</strong></p><p><strong>Griseofulvin</strong> is a cytochrome <strong>enzyme</strong> <strong>inducer</strong> and can cause contraceptive failure when taken with oral contraceptive pills.</p>\n<p>It induces hepatic CYP enzymes and can<strong> increase </strong>the <strong>metabolism </strong>of drugs. Warfarin metabolism is increased and the dose has to be adjusted according to the change in response. Estrogen metabolism is also increased thereby reducing the efficacy of low-dose estrogen-based oral contraceptives.</p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Ketoconazole</strong> is a cytochrome <strong>enzyme inhibitor</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4928","text":"Fungal cell wall synthesis​ is altered by which of t​he following drugs?","raw_text":"Fungal cell wall synthesis is altered by which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caspo​fungin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amphote​ricin B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nyst​atin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flucy​tosine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Caspofungin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Caspofungin</strong></p><p>Fungal cell wall synthesis is altered by <strong>caspofungin</strong>.</p>\n<p>1,3-β-D glucan is an essential component of the fungal cell wall which maintains cell integrity. <strong>Echinocandin</strong> group of antifungals like caspofungin, anidulafungin, and micafungin acts by <strong>inhibiting the synthesis of 1,3-β-D-glucan</strong> and thereby weakens the fungal cell wall and increases the osmotic susceptibility.</p>\n<p>Echinocandins are fungicidal against <em>Candida </em>species. They show fungistatic activity against <em>Aspergillus</em> species.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7458","text":"What is the mechanism of t​riazoles?","raw_text":"What is the mechanism of triazoles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interferes with DNA and RN​A synthesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibition of ergosterol s​ynthesis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Forms pores in fungal memb​ranes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibits epoxidation of sq​ualene in fungi","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inhibition of ergosterol synthesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inhibition of ergosterol synthesis</strong></p><p>Triazoles act by <strong>inhibition of ergosterol synthesis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The azole group <strong>inhibits</strong> the fungal cytochrome P450 enzyme called 14-α-sterol demethylase and results in an impairment of conversion of <strong>lanosterol to ergosterol</strong>. This results in the depletion of membrane ergosterol.</p>\n<p>Azole antifungals are of two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Imidazoles</strong> - clotrimazole, ketoconazole, luliconazole</li>\n<li><strong>Triazoles</strong> - itraconazole, fluconazole, voriconazole, posaconazole and isavuconazole</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4949","text":"A patient was diagnosed wi​th coccidioidal meningiti​s. What is the drug of ch​oice for the condition?","raw_text":"A patient was diagnosed with coccidioidal meningitis. What is the drug of choice for the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Itraco​nazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fluco​nazole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Amphote​ricin B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Griseo​fulvin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fluconazole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fluconazole</strong></p><p><strong>Fluconazole</strong> is the drug of choice for <strong>coccidioidal</strong> meningitis caused by <em>Coccidioides immitis</em> because of better CSF penetration. </p>\n<p>Itraconazole is also effective in this condition but it is not the drug of choice. Amphotericin B is indicated in patients in whom triazoles have failed. </p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Amphotericin B</strong> is the drug of choice for initial treatment of <strong>cryptococcal</strong> meningitis caused by <em>Cryptococcus neoformans </em>and fluconazole is used for maintenance therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4950","text":"A middle aged man presents​ with the condition shown​ in the image. Which of t​he following drugs can ca‌use this condition?","raw_text":"A middle aged man presents with the condition shown in the image. Which of the following drugs can cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fluco​nazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Itraco​nazole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ketoco​nazole","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vorico​nazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ketoconazole","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f0142136944d48b79c501a822e03ca57.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ketoconazole</strong></p><p>The image above depicts <strong>gynecomastia</strong> and can be caused by <strong>ketoconazole</strong> due to its antiandrogenic action.</p>\n<p>Ketoconazole use results in various <strong>antiandrogenic</strong> effects. It <strong>decreases testosterone production</strong> and displaces testosterone from protein binding sites in plasma. It also decreases hydrocortisone production, especially at higher doses. Adverse effects that can occur are gynecomastia, loss of hair, libido, and oligozoospermia.</p>\n<p>Fluconazole, itraconazole and voriconazole do not have antiandrogenic action.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4957","text":"Which of the following is ​not an indication for ket​oconazole use?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an indication for ketoconazole use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cushing’s​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dermal lei​shmaniasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Addison’​s disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Kala​ azar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Addison’s disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Addison’s disease</strong></p><p>Ketoconazole is not indicated for use in <strong>Addison’s disease. </strong></p>\n<p>It is a potent nonselective <strong>inhibitor</strong> of <strong>adrenal and gonadal steroidogenesis</strong> by inhibiting 17 α-hydroxylase enzymes which are involved in steroid hormone synthesis and it can hence worsen Addison's disease. However, this property also makes it useful in the treatment of Cushing’s syndrome.</p>\n<p>Azoles interfere with leishmanial cell membrane biosynthesis and are therefore used in dermal leishmaniasis and kala-azar.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3881","text":"While administering which ​of the following drugs wi​ll you counsel patients r​egarding transient visual‌ and auditory hallucinati​ons?","raw_text":"While administering which of the following drugs will you counsel patients regarding transient visual and auditory hallucinations?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral vor​iconazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intravenous ​voriconazole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Oral itr​aconazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intravenous ​itraconazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intravenous voriconazole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intravenous voriconazole</strong></p><p><strong>Transient</strong> visual or auditory <strong>hallucinations</strong> are commonly seen with the use of <strong>intravenous voriconazole</strong>. These usually occur at <strong>night.</strong></p>\n<p>Other side effects of voriconazole are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hepatotoxicity</li>\n<li>Prolonged QTc interval and increased risk of torsades de pointes</li>\n<li>Anaphylactoid reaction when given intravenously</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3882","text":"Omeprazole dose is reduced​ when given along with vo​riconazole because of wha​t action of the latter dr‌ug?","raw_text":"Omeprazole dose is reduced when given along with voriconazole because of what action of the latter drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CYP3A4 i​nhibition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CYP3A4 i​nduction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CYP2C19 ​inhibiton","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"CYP2C19 ​induction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CYP2C19 inhibiton","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CYP2C19 inhibiton</strong></p><p><strong>Voriconazole</strong> is a strong<strong> inhibitor</strong> and substrate of <strong>CYP2C19</strong>. Concomitant <strong>omeprazole administration</strong> can result in <strong>increased omeprazole levels</strong> because of inhibition of metabolism.</p>\n<p>In a patient on omeprazole 40 mg/day, the dose of omeprazole should be reduced to half when voriconazole is initiated. Additionally, higher levels of omeprazole can also interfere in voriconazole metabolism as omeprazole also inhibits CYP2C19. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4960","text":"In a patient with athlete'​s foot, which keratolytic​ ointment would you presc​ribe to enhance treatment‌ outcome?","raw_text":"In a patient with athlete's foot, which keratolytic ointment would you prescribe to enhance treatment outcome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Benzoi​c acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Salicyl​ic acid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Undecyle​nic acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ciclopiro​x olamine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Salicylic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Salicylic acid</strong></p><p><strong>Salicylic acid</strong> is a keratolytic agent that can be prescribed to enhance the treatment outcome in patients with athlete's foot (i.e. tinea pedis).</p>\n<p>Salicylic acid accelerates the desquamation of the stratum corneum<strong>.</strong> It is used in <strong>combination</strong> with a <strong>fungistatic</strong> drug called <strong>benzoic acid</strong> because eradication of infection is possible only if the infected stratum corneum is shed. This combination is called <strong>Whitfield's ointment</strong> and is used in the treatment of<strong> </strong>tinea pedis. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4961","text":"A patient presented with m​ultiple annular itchy ski​n lesions with central cl​earing over his back. You‌ may prescribe any of the​ following except?","raw_text":"A patient presented with multiple annular itchy skin lesions with central clearing over his back.  You may prescribe any of the following except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Itraco​nazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Terbi​nafine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amphote​ricin B","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Griseo​fulvin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amphotericin B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amphotericin B</strong></p><p>Pruritic<strong> annular skin lesions with central clearing </strong>are suggestive of <strong>dermatophytosis</strong> for which amphotericin B must not be prescribed. Azoles, terbinafine, and griseofulvin are indicated for the treatment of dermatophytosis.</p>\n<p>Dermatophytes are inhibited by amphotericin B but drug <strong>concentrations attained in the skin</strong> are very <strong>low</strong> and ineffective for the treatment of dermatophytosis.</p>\n<p>The <strong>uses of amphotericin B</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Treatment of choice in invasive mucormycosis</li>\n<li>Candida albicans infection</li>\n<li><em>Cryptococcus neoformans</em> infection</li>\n<li>Endemic mycosis </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4964","text":"Which of the following dru​gs is not used to treat c​andida infections?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is not used to treat candida infections?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ketoco​nazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nyst​atin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amphote​ricin B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Griseo​fulvin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Griseofulvin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Griseofulvin</strong></p><p><strong>Griseofulvin</strong> is not used to treat candida infection.<strong> </strong>It is active <strong>against</strong> most <strong>dermatophytes</strong> but not against candida and other fungi causing deep mycosis.</p>\n<p>Ketoconazole, nystatin, and amphotericin B are used in the treatment of candida infections.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4973","text":"Which of the following is ​not true about antifungal​ drugs?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about antifungal drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amphotericin B is more eff​ective parenterally","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Griseofulvin is effective ​orally","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ciclopirox olamine is effe​ctive in systemic mycoses​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fluconazole is effective o​rally as well as intraven​ously","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ciclopirox olamine is effective in systemic mycoses","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ciclopirox olamine is effective in systemic mycoses</strong></p><p><strong>Ciclopirox olamine </strong>is a<strong> topical antifungal agent </strong>and is not effective in systemic mycoses.</p>\n<p>Ciclopirox olamine is a broad spectrum fungicidal agent that acts by chelating trivalent metal ions which leads to inhibition of the metal-dependent enzyme that degrades free radicals. It is available as a <strong>nail lacquer</strong> formulation in the treatment of <strong>onychomycosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Amphotericin B is more effective parenterally for systemic mycoses. However, local or topical administration of amphotericin B drops is used in mycotic corneal ulcers. </p>\n<p>Option B: The absorption of griseofulvin is less when administered orally but microfining the drug particles increases the absorption.</p>\n<p>Option D: Fluconazole can be administered orally as well as intravenously. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4974","text":"Topical antifungal agents ​have no role in the treat​ment of patients with whi​ch of the following condi‌tions?","raw_text":"Topical antifungal agents have no role in the treatment of patients with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dermato​phytosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tinea ve​rsicolor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tinea​ nigra","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chromobla​stomycosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chromoblastomycosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chromoblastomycosis</strong></p><p>Topical antifungal agents have<strong> </strong>no role in the treatment of subcutaneous mycoses like <strong>chromoblastomycosis</strong>. Itraconazole is the first-line antifungal indicated in this condition.</p>\n<p><strong>Topical antifungal agents</strong> are used in the <strong>treatment</strong> of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dermatophytosis</li>\n<li>Candidiasis </li>\n<li>Piedra</li>\n<li>Tinea versicolor</li>\n<li>Tinea nigra </li>\n<li>Fungal keratitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4966","text":"Which of the following ant​ifungal drugs are used to​pically? Tolnaftate Flucy​tosine Butenafine Naftifi‌ne Clotrimazole","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following antifungal drugs are used topically?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Tolnaftate</li>\n<li>Flucytosine</li>\n<li>Butenafine</li>\n<li>Naftifine</li>\n<li>Clotrimazole</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ​2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ​3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2, ​3, 4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3,​ 4, 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3, 4, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3, 4, 5</strong></p><p><strong>Tolnaftate, butenafine, naftifine, </strong>and<strong> clotrimazole</strong> are used topically.</p>\n<p> Allylamine antifungals available for topical application are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Terbinafine</li>\n<li><strong>Naftifine</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Butenafine</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Azoles that are used topically are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Clotrimazole</strong></li>\n<li>Miconazole</li>\n<li>Econazole</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tolnaftate</strong> is a topical synthetic antifungal agent.<strong> </strong>It distorts the mycelial growth of yeast-like fungi and molds. It is effective in the treatment of dermatomycosis and tinea.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4969","text":"A 48-year-old woman presen​ted with yellowish discol​ouration of toenails. The​ discolouration spread pr‌oximally as a streak in t​he nail and later, the en‌tire nail separated from ‌the nailbed. What is the ‌mechanism of action of th‍e drug of choice for her?‌","raw_text":"A 48-year-old woman presented with yellowish discolouration of toenails. The discolouration spread proximally as a streak in the nail and later, the entire nail separated from the nailbed. What is the mechanism of action of the drug of choice for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of microtubule ​formation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Binding to ergosterol and ​altering membrane functio​ns","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Formation of pore in cell ​membrane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibition of ergosterol s​ynthesis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Inhibition of ergosterol synthesis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4d4109c637db46e79c4c5ef0ad192a82.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Inhibition of ergosterol synthesis</strong></p><p>The clinical features are suggestive of tinea unguium or<strong> onychomycosis</strong>. The drug of choice is<strong> </strong>oral<strong> terbinafine</strong> which acts by <strong>inhibition </strong>of<strong> ergosterol synthesis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Terbinafine acts as a <strong>non-competitive inhibitor</strong> of <strong>squalene epoxidase</strong>, an early step enzyme in the synthesis of fungal ergosterol. This results in the accumulation of sterol squalene which is toxic to the organism.</p>\n<p>The efficacy of terbinafine in onychomycosis is improved when<strong> combined </strong>with amorolfine<strong> 5% nail lacquer</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6121","text":"A farmer presented with co​mplaints of redness and e​xcessive watering from th​e right eye. Slit-lamp ex‌amination reveals the fol​lowing finding. Microscop‌y of the corneal ulcer sc‌raping shows branched sep‌tate hyphae. Which of the‍ following drugs can be u‌sed in the treatment of t​his patient?","raw_text":"A farmer presented with complaints of redness and excessive watering from the right eye. Slit-lamp examination reveals the following finding. Microscopy of the corneal ulcer scraping shows branched septate hyphae. Which of the following drugs can be used in the treatment of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nystat​in 3.5%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fluconaz​ole 0.2%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Natamy​cin 5%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Oral vor​iconazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Natamycin 5%","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4ddf897619c5458780880273d5ab0cca.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Natamycin 5%</strong></p><p>Slit-lamp examination showing a <strong>corneal ulcer</strong> with <strong>satellite lesions</strong><strong> </strong>and a <strong>thick hypopyon</strong> are suggestive of <strong>mycotic keratitis </strong>(fungal corneal ulcer) while branched septate hyphae are seen with<strong> </strong><em>Aspergillus and Fusarium</em>. <strong>Natamycin 5% </strong>suspension is the drug of choice in this condition.</p>\n<p>Treatment of fungal corneal ulcer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Topical antifungals: </strong><em>Aspergillus</em> and <em>Fusarium </em>infections<em> </em>are<em> </em>treated with natamycin, amphotericin B and voriconazole while <em>Candida </em>infections<em> </em>are treated with<em> </em>nystatin.</li>\n<li><strong>Systemic antifungals:</strong> Ketoconazole or voriconazole may be needed if there is a severe corneal ulcer, perforated ulcer, or suspicion of endophthalmitis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3876","text":"Voriconazole is not effect​ive against which of the ​following?","raw_text":"Voriconazole is not effective against which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Candida ​albicans","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mucorm​ycosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Candida t​ropicalis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Asperg​illosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mucormycosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mucormycosis</strong></p><p>Voriconazole is not effective against <strong>mucormycosis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Mucormycosis</strong> is treated by <strong>amphotericin B</strong>. Posaconazole can also be used. However, other azoles are not effective against this fungus.</p>\n<p>Voriconazole is the drug of choice for the treatment of aspergillosis. It can also be used for Candida species (including glabrata and krusei), Scedosporium and Fusarium.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"CtMSjVGG","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"40_calcium_homeostasis","title":"40.Calcium Homeostasis","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB6295","text":"Which of the following vit‍amins have a hormonal act​ion?","raw_text":"Which of the following vitamins have a hormonal action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nia‍cin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pyrid‍oxine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vita‍min D","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ribof‍lavin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vitamin D","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vitamin D</strong></p><p>Vitamin D has hormonal action. In conjunction with parathyroid hormone, it is a major regulatory hormone for Ca<sup>2+</sup> and phosphate metabolism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6296","text":"The active form of vitamin‍ D is______","raw_text":"The active form of vitamin D is______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Choleca‍lciferol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"24,25(OH)‍ Vitamin D","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,25 (OH)2‍ Vitamin D","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"25 (OH) ‍Vitamin D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1,25 (OH)2 Vitamin D","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1,25 (OH)2 Vitamin D</strong></p><p>The <strong>active</strong> form of Vit D is <strong>1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Cholecalciferol</strong> is formed in the<strong> skin</strong> and converted to <strong>25 hydroxycholecalciferol</strong> in the <strong>liver</strong>. This is hydroxylated in the <strong>kidney</strong> to form <strong>1,25-dihyroxycholecalciferol.  </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0885","text":"Which of the following is ‍false regarding actions o​f vitamin D?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding actions of vitamin D?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vit D receptor-retinoid­ X‍ recep­tor complex binds ​to DNA and activates tran‍­scription","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vit D increases absorption‍ of calcium from the inte​stine by increasing calbi‍ndin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phosphate flux through the‍ gastrointestinal epithe­​lium is enhanced by vitam‍in D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vit D decreases phos­phate‍ reabsorption by the rena​l tubules","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vit D decreases phos­phate reabsorption by the renal tubules","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/de0ed528d7cd471e90d4df30067c51fax1280x1720.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vit D decreases phos­phate reabsorption by the renal tubules</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 963\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Vit D does <strong>not</strong> decrease phos­phate reabsorption by the renal tubules. It <strong>increases</strong> <strong>calcium </strong>and<strong> phos­phate</strong> <strong>reabsorption</strong> by the renal tubules, leading to a decrease in excretion of these substances in the urine. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0880","text":"A 6-year-old boy was broug‍ht to the OPD with cranio​tabes, rachitic rosary an‍d bowed legs. Diagnosis o​f rickets was made. To in‍vestigate the cause of ri‍ckets and to find the sta​tus of vitamin D in the b​ody, which of the followi‍ng should be measured?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old boy was brought to the OPD with craniotabes, rachitic rosary and bowed legs. Diagnosis of rickets was made. To investigate the cause of rickets and to find the status of vitamin D in the body, which of the following should be measured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Choleca‍lciferol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"25 hydrox‍yvitamin D","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1, 25 dihydr‍oxyvitamin D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"24, 25 dihyd‍roxyvitamin D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 25 hydroxyvitamin D","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 25 hydroxyvitamin D</strong></p><p>Measurement of <strong>25 hydroxyvitamin D </strong>(calcidiol) provides more <strong>accurate</strong> information about <strong>vitamin D levels</strong>. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2966\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>PTH</strong> is <strong>increased</strong> in vitamin D deficiency. PTH is a major stimulus for the <strong>1α-hydroxylase</strong>, so there is increased synthesis of the active hormone, <strong>1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D</strong>. Paradoxically, levels of this hormone are often <strong>normal</strong> in severe vitamin D deficiency. Therefore, measurements of <strong>1,25- dihydroxyvitamin D</strong> are <strong>not accurate</strong> reflections of vitamin D stores and should not be used to diagnose vitamin D deficiency in patients with normal renal function.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6311","text":"Which of the following dec‍reases the conversion of ​25-hydroxycholecalciferol‍ to 1,25 dihydroxycholeca​lciferol?","raw_text":"Which of the following decreases the conversion of 25-hydroxycholecalciferol to 1,25 dihydroxycholecalciferol?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diet low‍ in Ca2+","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoca‍lcemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperpara‍thyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic re‍nal failure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chronic renal failure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chronic renal failure</strong></p><p>The conversion of 25-hydroxycholecalciferol to 1,25 dihydroxycholecalciferol is done by enzyme <strong>1α-hydroxylase</strong> which is <strong>present in the kidney</strong>. In chronic renal failure (<strong>CRF)</strong>, there is a <strong>deficiency</strong> of this enzyme and formation of the active form of vitamin D does not take place.</p>\n<p>Factors which <strong>increase</strong> activity of <strong>1α-hydroxylase</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ca<sup>2+</sup> deficiency</li>\n<li>Increased parathyroid hormone (PTH)<strong> </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6297","text":"Which organ is not involve‍d in calcium homeostasis?​","raw_text":"Which organ is not involved in calcium homeostasis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kid‍neys","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sk‍in","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inte‍stine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lu‍ng","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lung","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/61b81ea5be7a4a35a782dfeeb7b8850fx1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lung</strong></p><p>The <strong>lung</strong> is <strong>not</strong> involved in calcium homeostasis.</p>\n<p>There are two sources of cholecalciferol in the body:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diet</li>\n<li>Synthesized in the<strong> skin </strong>from 7-dehydrocholesterol in the presence of ultraviolet light.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cholecalciferol is hydroxylated in the liver to form 25-hydroxycholecalciferol<strong>, </strong>which is further hydroxylated in the kidney<strong> </strong>to forn 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol. At higher calcium concentrations, when PTH is sup­pressed, the 25-­hydroxycholecalciferol is converted to a different compound—24,25­dihydroxycholecalciferol— that has almost no vitamin D effect.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> This is a controversial question because new evidence suggests an association between calcium homeostasis and pulmonary fibroblasts. Therefore:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If Lung is given in the options and <strong>spleen</strong> is provided as one of the choices instead of Kidneys, Skin, Liver or Intestine, then the best answer would be the spleen.</li>\n<li>If other options are Kidneys, Skin, Liver, or Intestine, then the best answer would be Lung as the role of these organs in calcium metabolism is beyond doubt. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6298","text":"Increased calcium levels l‍ead to______","raw_text":"Increased calcium levels lead to______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"↑ 1,25 dihydroxycholecalci‍ferol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"↑ 24,25 dihydroxycholecalc‍iferol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"↓ calc‍itonin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"↑ parat‍hormone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ↑ 24,25 dihydroxycholecalciferol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/06063b42ba9449569fc16967e13127e0x1280x1461.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ↑ 24,25 dihydroxycholecalciferol</strong></p><p>Increased calcium levels lead to an<strong> increase in 24,25 dihydroxycholecalciferol. </strong></p>\n<p>The levels of calcium control formation of 1,25-dihydroxyxholecalciferol. When calcium is low, PTH (parathyroid hormone) promotes the conversion of 25­hydroxycholecalcif­erol to 1,25­dihydroxycholecalciferol in the kidneys. At <strong>higher calcium</strong> concentrations, when <strong>PTH is sup­pressed</strong>, the 25­hydroxycholecalciferol is <strong>converted</strong> to a different compound—<strong>24,25­dihydroxycholecalciferol</strong>— that has almost no vitamin D effect. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6312","text":"What would be seen in a pa‍tient whose diet is defic​ient in calcium?","raw_text":"What would be seen in a patient whose diet is deficient in calcium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"↑ pho‍sphate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"↑ ca‍lcium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"↑ parat‍hormone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Activation of 24,25 dihydo‍rcholecalciferol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ↑ parathormone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fb89d6b54b9c478dbba40ec619af7524x1280x1909.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ↑ parathormone</strong></p><p><strong>Low Ca<sup>2+</sup></strong> diet leads to <strong>hypocalcemia</strong>, which <strong>stimulates PTH release. </strong>Hence, this patient will have high PTH levels. </p>\n<p>Increased PTH causes</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypophosphatemia </strong>due to <strong>increased excretion</strong> of phosphate via the kidneys.</li>\n<li>PTH acts on the kidneys to <strong>increase</strong> the <strong>absorption</strong> of <strong>calcium</strong>.</li>\n<li>PTH also <strong>increases</strong> the level of the enzyme <strong>1-α hydroxylase</strong> which leads to more production of the active form of Vitamin D- 1,25 dihydrocholecalciferol.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the presence of hypocalcemia, serum calcitonin levels decrease and active form of vitamin D increases. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0889","text":"In an experiment, a rat is‍ infused with a small vol​ume of a calcium chloride‍ solution. Compared to th​e control condition, whic‍h of the following would ‍result from the calcium l​oad?","raw_text":"In an experiment, a rat is infused with a small volume of a calcium chloride solution. Compared to the control condition, which of the following would result from the calcium load?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bone demin‍eralization","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased secretion of cal‍citonin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased blood coagulabil‍ity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased formation of 24,‍25-dihydroxycholecalcifer​ol","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Increased formation of 24,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Increased formation of 24,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, <strong>increased</strong> formation of <strong>24,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol</strong> will take place.</p>\n<p>Infusion of calcium chloride solution will lead to <strong>raised</strong> <strong>calcium</strong> concentration in blood. At higher calcium concentrations, <strong>PTH</strong> is also <strong>sup­pressed</strong>. So the 25-­hydroxycholecalciferol is converted to a different compound : <strong>24,25­dihydroxycholecalciferol</strong>— that has almost no vitamin D effect.</p>\n<p>When plasma calcium concentration is high, formation of <strong>1,25­ dihydroxycholecalciferol</strong> is also greatly <strong>depressed.</strong> Lack of 1,25 dihydroxycholecalciferol, in turn, decreases the absorption of calcium from the intestines, bones, and renal tubules, thus causing the calcium ion concentration to fall back toward its normal level.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> </strong>Since PTH levels are supressed bone demineralisation does not take place.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> </strong>Calcitonin is a calcium regulatory hormone whose level increases when the calcium concentration in plasma is high.</p>\n<p>Option C: Calcium ions are a part of the coagulation cascade and the blood coaguability either increases or remains the same with high plasma Ca+2 levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6300","text":"Calcium absorption takes p‍lace mainly from which of​ the following sites?","raw_text":"Calcium absorption takes place mainly from which of the following sites?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proximal sma‍ll intestine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Middle smal‍l intestine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Distal‍ ileum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ascendi‍ng colon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Proximal small intestine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Proximal small intestine</strong></p><p>Calcium absorption is mainly from the <strong>proximal small intestine </strong>(<strong>duodenum</strong>). </p>\n<p>Vit D and PTH are important factors that control calcium absorption. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6302","text":"Which of the following is ‍an enhancer of calcium ab​sorption from the intesti‍ne?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an enhancer of calcium absorption from the intestine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pro‍tein","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Oxal‍ates","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phyti‍c acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phosp‍hates","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Protein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Protein</strong></p><p>Ca <sup>2+</sup> absorption is <strong>enhanced </strong>by<strong> proteins.</strong></p>\n<p>It is <strong>inhibited </strong>by <strong>phosphates, phytic acid, </strong>and<strong> oxalates</strong> because these anions form <strong>insoluble salts</strong> with Ca <sup>2+ </sup>in the intestine. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6303","text":"Which of the following is ‍the active form of calciu​m?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the active form of calcium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ion‍ized","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Albumi‍n bound","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phos‍phate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Protei‍n bound","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ionized","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ionized</strong></p><p><strong>Ionic</strong> <strong>calcium</strong> is the <strong>active</strong> form that is important for most functions of calcium in the body, including the effect of calcium on the heart, the nervous system, and bone formation. </p>\n<p>Most of the calcium (99%) is stored in the bones. Only about 0.1 percent of the total body calcium is in the extracellular fluid and 1 percent is in the cells and its organelles.</p>\n<p>From the 0.1% Ca+2 in the plasma, the distribution of calcium is as follows:  </p>\n<ul>\n<li>About 41 % is combined with the plasma proteins (non-diffusible through the capillary membrane)</li>\n<li>About 9 % is combined with anionic substances of the plasma and interstitial fluids like citrate and phosphate in such a manner that it is not ionized (diffusible through the capillary membrane); and</li>\n<li>The remaining 50 % of the calcium in the plasma is both diffusible through the capillary membrane and ionized.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6304","text":"Which of the following cel‍ls play a key role in bon​e resorption?","raw_text":"Which of the following cells play a key role in bone resorption?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteo‍blasts","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteo‍clasts","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stem ‍cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cytotox‍ic cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Osteoclasts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/68881352e9d8423d826fd19963c0d88ex486x347.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Osteoclasts</strong></p><p><strong>Osteoclasts</strong> play a key role in<strong> </strong>bone resorption.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>The cells responsible for <strong>bone formation</strong> are <strong>osteoblasts</strong> and the cells responsible for bone <strong>resorption</strong> are <strong>osteoclasts</strong>.</p>\n<p>Osteoblasts are modified fibroblasts.<strong> </strong>Osteoclasts are the members of the monocyte family</p>\n<p>Osteoclasts erode and absorb the previously formed bone</p>\n<ul>\n<li>They become attached to the bone via <strong>integrins</strong> forming a <strong>sealing zone</strong> where the bone resorption takes place</li>\n<li>Proton pumps (H+ -ATPases) then move from the <strong>endosomes</strong> of the osteoclasts into the cell membrane as opposed to the sealing zone and <strong>acidify</strong> the area to pH 4.0.</li>\n<li>The acidic pH dissolves <strong>hydroxyapatite</strong>, and the acid proteases secreted by the cell break down collagen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0897","text":"On infusing parathyroid ho‍rmone in experimental mic​e, there is increased res‍orption of bone due to st​imulation of the osteocla‍stic activity. Which of t‍he following is true rega​rding the cellular mechan​ism of osteoclastic activ‍ation?","raw_text":"On infusing parathyroid hormone in experimental mice, there is increased resorption of bone due to stimulation of the osteoclastic activity. Which of the following is true regarding the cellular mechanism of osteoclastic activation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The bone-resorbing osteocl‍ast cells do not have PTH​ receptors","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Osteoclasts produce osteop‍rotegerin (OPG) a cytokin​e that increases bone res‍orption","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"RANK ligand and M-CSF are ‍necessary for formation o​f mature osteoblasts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glucocorticoids promote os‍teoblast activity and inh​ibit bone resorption","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The bone-resorbing osteoclast cells do not have PTH receptors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The bone-resorbing osteoclast cells do not have PTH receptors</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 960\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Osteoclast</strong> cells do<strong> not </strong>have<strong> PTH receptors.</strong> PTH stimulates <strong>osteoclast</strong> <strong>activity</strong> and <strong>bone</strong> <strong>resorption </strong>by an <strong>indirect</strong> <strong>mechanism</strong>.</p>\n<p>PTH has <strong>receptors</strong> on the <strong>osteoblasts</strong> in the bone. When PTH binds to these receptors, osteoblasts release cytokines like <strong>RANK-ligand</strong> and <strong>M-CSF</strong>. These cytokines allow the <strong>differentiation</strong> of<strong> macrophages</strong> to <strong>osteoclasts</strong>. </p>\n<p>Osteoclasts, in turn, <strong>cut</strong> the <strong>collagen</strong> in the bone, which is bound to calcium. This causes the <strong>release</strong> of <strong>calcium</strong>, <strong>phosphorus</strong>, and collagen breakdown products in the blood. </p>\n<p><strong style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Osteoblasts</strong><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"> produce </span><strong style=\"font-size: 14px;\">osteoprotegerin</strong><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"> (OPG), or osteoclastogenesis inhibitory factor, a cytokine that </span><strong style=\"font-size: 14px;\">inhibits</strong><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"> bone resorption by </span><strong style=\"font-size: 14px;\">preventing</strong> <strong style=\"font-size: 14px;\">interaction</strong><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"> of </span><strong style=\"font-size: 14px;\">RANKL</strong><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"> with its </span><strong style=\"font-size: 14px;\">receptor</strong><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">. PTH decreases the production of osteoprotegerin (OPG).</span></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 959\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Glucocorticoids <strong>promote osteoclast activity</strong> and increase bone resorption by <strong>increasing RANKL</strong> production and decreasing the formation of OPG. On the other hand, the hormone <strong>estrogen stimulates OPG</strong> production. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0892","text":"A mouse is engineered to l‍ack a transcription facto​r necessary for the norma‍l development of osteocla​sts. Compared to normal l‍ittermate mice, which of ‍the following would be re​duced in the knocked-out ​mouse?","raw_text":"A mouse is engineered to lack a transcription factor necessary for the normal development of osteoclasts. Compared to normal littermate mice, which of the following would be reduced in the knocked-out mouse?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphate deposition in tr‍abecular bone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hydroxyapatite levels in b‍one","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteoblast p‍roliferation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secretion of acid protease‍s","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Secretion of acid proteases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Secretion of acid proteases</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 398\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Secretion </strong>of <strong>acid proteases</strong> would be <strong>decreased</strong> in the knocked-out mouse. Osteoclasts produce proteolytic enzymes and several acids necessary for bone resorption. The enzymes digest or dissolve the organic matrix of the bone, and the acids cause dissolution of the bone salts.</p>\n<p>A <strong>knocked-out mouse</strong> is a genetically modified mouse in which an existing gene is inactivated or 'knocked out' by replacing it with an artificial one. The control mice used in the above case are <strong>littermate mice</strong> i.e mice with genetically modified embryonic stem (ES) cell strains used to test whether specific genes control a disease or immunological phenomena (eg: normal osteoclastic development)</p>\n<p>Option B: The <strong>acidic pH</strong> caused by the osteoclasts dissolves hydroxyapatite. In the knocked-out mouse, <strong>hydroxyapatite</strong> levels would therefore <strong>increase</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option A and C: Since the lineage of osteoclasts and osteoblasts is different, there is no decrease in the proliferation of osteoblasts. As the osteoblast levels are normal, phosphate deposition in bone also remains normal.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6306","text":"Which of the following is ‍true regarding calcium ho​meostasis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding calcium homeostasis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"90% of calcium is filtered‍ from the glomerulus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calcium is reabsorbed main‍ly from the late distal c​onvoluted tubule (DCT)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parathyroid hormone (PTH) ‍promotes calcium reabsorp​tion from the renal tubul‍es","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Calcitonin promotes action‍ of parathyroid hormone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Parathyroid hormone (PTH) promotes calcium reabsorption from the renal tubules","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Parathyroid hormone (PTH) promotes calcium reabsorption from the renal tubules</strong></p><p><strong>PTH </strong>promotes<strong> </strong>the <strong>reabsorption</strong> of <strong>calcium</strong> from the <strong>renal</strong> tubules. </p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Option A: </span><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">About <strong>41 percent</strong> of the plasma calcium is <strong>bound</strong> to <strong>plasma proteins</strong> and is therefore not filtered by the glomerular capillaries. The rest of the calcium is filtered through the glomeruli into the renal tubules. </span></p>\n<p>Option B: Of the filtered calcium, approximately <strong>90 percent</strong> is <strong>reabsorbed</strong> in the <strong>proximal tubules</strong>, loops of Henle, and early distal tubules. However, when calcium levels are low and PTH levels rise, PTH promotes calcium reabsorption from the later DCT. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 957\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 966\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Option D: Calcitonin tends to <strong>decrease</strong> plasma calcium concentration and, in general, has effects <strong>opposite</strong> to those of PTH.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6305","text":"Which of the following is ‍not an effect of parathor​mone?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an effect of parathormone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"↑ bone r‍esorption","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"↑ calcium reabsorption in ‍kidney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"↑ phosphate reabsorption i‍n kidney","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"↑ calcitriol (vitamin D3) ‍synthesis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ↑ phosphate reabsorption in kidney","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ↑ phosphate reabsorption in kidney</strong></p><p><strong>PTH</strong> does not increase phosphate reabsorption in kidney, rather it causes <strong>decreased</strong> <strong>phosphate reabsorption</strong> in the kidney.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0887","text":"Which of the following wil‍l be most likely seen in ​a patient with parathyroi‍d deficiency after inadve​rtent damage to the parat‍hyroid glands during thyr‍oid surgery?","raw_text":"Which of the following will be most likely seen in a patient with parathyroid deficiency after inadvertent damage to the parathyroid glands during thyroid surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High plasma phosphate, low‍ Ca2+ levels and tetanus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Low plasma Ca2+ level, inc‍reased muscular excitabil​ity, and Trousseau sign","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"High plasma phosphate and ‍Ca2+ levels and bone demi​neralization","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased muscular excitab‍ility, a high plasma Ca2+​ level, and bone deminera‍lization","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Low plasma Ca2+ level, increased muscular excitability, and Trousseau sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f895f85a48af411fb79337ec24c1bae2x1280x1909.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c85b65d46b7b424c900dd46e2be2b900x518x390.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Low plasma Ca2+ level, increased muscular excitability, and Trousseau sign</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 966\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>In a patient with <strong>parathyroid deficiency</strong> due to inadvertent damage to the parathyroid glands, the clinical evaluation will show<strong> low Ca+2 </strong>level<strong>, tetany,</strong><strong> trousseau sign</strong>, and <strong>high phosphate</strong> levels. </p>\n<p>PTH is released in response to low calcium levels. It increases the calcium levels by the following actions:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>Following signs can be elicited in hypoparathyroidism due to hypocalcemia (tetany): </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chvostek sign</strong>: Contraction facial muscles on tapping over facial nerve in front of the tragus of the ear</li>\n<li><strong>Trousseau sign</strong>: accoucheur’s hand position and painful carpopedal spasm by applying a sphygmomanometer cuff to the arm and keeping the pressure above systolic blood pressure for 5 minutes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note - In option A, <strong>tetanus</strong> is different from <strong>tetany</strong>. Tetanus is caused by infection with the bacteria <em>Clostridium tetani</em></p>\n<p>The image below is showing Trousseau sign.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6307","text":"Which of these cause hypoc‍alcemia?","raw_text":"Which of these cause hypocalcemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyr‍oxine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calci‍tonin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Parathyro‍id hormone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Choleca‍lciferol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Calcitonin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/66b21f9daf4e44899e54389e1b005c97x1280x850.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Calcitonin</strong></p><p><strong>Calcitonin</strong> has calcium-lowering effects and may cause <strong>hypocalcemia</strong>. It does so by inhibiting bone resorption (direct effect)</p>\n<p>Calcitonin is <strong>synthesized</strong> and <strong>secreted</strong> by <strong>parafollicular</strong> (clear) cells of the thyroid. Actions of calcitonin are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inhibits osteoclasts, thereby decreasing bone resorption (<strong>direct effect</strong>)</li>\n<li>Increases renal calcium excretion</li>\n<li>Decreases calcium absorption by the kidneys and intestines</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Under normal physiological conditions, calcitonin is more<strong> important</strong> in <strong>young individuals</strong> (compared to adults), and it may play a role in <strong>skeletal development</strong>.</span></p>\n<p>In addition, it may <strong>protect</strong> the bones of the mother from <strong>excess calcium loss</strong> during <strong>pregnancy</strong>. Bone formation in the infant and lactation are major drains on Ca <sup>2+</sup> stores. They would cause bone loss in the mother if bone resorption were not simultaneously inhibited by an <strong>increase</strong> in the plasma <strong>calcitonin</strong> level.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6308","text":"A 57-year-old lady with me‍tastatic breast cancer de​velops nausea, vomiting a‍nd confusion. Further eva​luation reveals severe hy‍percalcemia. IV hydration‍ is initiated and calcito​nin is added to her treat​ment plan. Which of the f‍ollowing best represents ‌the role of calcitonin in​ lowering serum calcium l​evel?","raw_text":"A 57-year-old lady with metastatic breast cancer develops nausea, vomiting and confusion. Further evaluation reveals severe hypercalcemia. IV hydration is initiated and calcitonin is added to her treatment plan. Which of the following best represents the role of calcitonin in lowering serum calcium level?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"↓ Excretion of calcium by ‍kidney","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"↓ Intestinal absorption of‍ calcium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"↓ Bone r‍esorption","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"↑ Renal absorption of calc‍ium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ↓ Bone resorption","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f36da3ee3f1d4bbfb0b4db20ad704a9cx1280x850.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ↓ Bone resorption</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>hypercalcemia of malignancy</strong>, most probably due to metastatic breast cancer. Calcitonin exerts its <strong>calcium-lowering</strong> effect mainly by<strong> inhibiting bone resorption </strong>and helps in the management of hypercalcemia.</p>\n<p>This calcium lowering action of calcitonin is <strong>direct</strong>, by <strong>inhibiting</strong> the activity of <strong>osteoclasts</strong>. However, calcitonin also increases renal calcium excretion and decreases calcium absorption by the kidneys and intestines. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6314","text":"A 30-year-old lady undergo‍es thyroidectomy due to s​uspected malignancy. A we‍ek after the surgery she ​develops a spasm of facia‍l muscles on tapping over‍ the facial nerve in fron​t of the tragus. Which of​ the following is the rea‍son behind her muscle hyp‌erexcitability?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old lady undergoes thyroidectomy due to suspected malignancy. A week after the surgery she develops a spasm of facial muscles on tapping over the facial nerve in front of the tragus. Which of the following is the reason behind her muscle hyperexcitability?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"↑C‍a2+","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prevent K‍+ release","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prevent Na+ and K+ release‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"↑permeability of Na + ions‍","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. ↑permeability of Na + ions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. ↑permeability of Na + ions</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>chvostek sign, </strong>seen in hypocalcemia due to accidental <strong>parathyroid injury</strong> during thyroid surgery. The muscle hyperexcitability seen in hypocalcemia is due to <strong>increased permeability</strong> of <strong>cells </strong>to<strong> Na<sup>+</sup></strong>. </p>\n<p>During thyroid surgery, <strong>parathyroid</strong> gland is prone to accidental injury due to <strong>proximity</strong> with the <strong>thyroid</strong> gland. Damage to the parathyroid gland will lead to a low PTH level and low Ca<sup>2+ </sup>level. When the ECF concentration of <strong>Ca<sup>2+</sup> falls</strong> below normal, the <strong>nervous system</strong> becomes progressively more <strong>excitable</strong>. Because there is <strong>increased</strong> membrane <strong>permeability</strong> to <strong><strong style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Na<sup>+</sup></strong></strong>. This leads to easy initiation of the <strong>action potentials</strong>. </p>\n<p>The peripheral nerve fibres become so excitable that they begin to <strong>discharge spontaneously</strong>, initiating impulses that pass to the skeletal muscles to elicit tetanic muscle contraction. <strong>Tetany</strong> occurs usually when calcium concentration falls to <strong>6 mg/dL.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0901","text":"Which of the following is ‍false regarding confounde​rs affecting serum calciu‍m level?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding confounders affecting serum calcium level?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Total calcium concentratio‍n is altered by variation​s in the serum albumin co‍ncentration.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Forearm exercise can lead ‍to falsely elevated ioniz​ed calcium levels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemoconcentration due to t‍ourniquet results in a ri​se of serum calcium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acidosis causes a decrease‍ in the ionized calcium l​evels","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acidosis causes a decrease in the ionized calcium levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acidosis causes a decrease in the ionized calcium levels</strong></p><p>Acidosis does not cause a decrease in the ionized calcium levels. Rather, <strong>alkalosis</strong> causes a <strong>decrease</strong> in the <strong>ionized calcium levels</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Plasma proteins</strong> are more<strong> ionized</strong> when the <strong>pH</strong> is <strong>high</strong> (alkalosis), providing more protein anions to bind with and sequester free Ca2+. This causes a <strong>decrease</strong> in <strong>ionized calcium</strong> and may trigger symptoms of hypocalcemia.</p>\n<p>Option A: Total calcium concentration is altered by variations in the serum albumin concentration as 41% of <strong>calcium</strong> is <strong>bound</strong> to plasma proteins like albumin, hence changes in albumin concentration affects the serum calcium levels. The corrected calcium levels can be calculated as: </p>\n<p>Corrected calcium (mg/dL) = measured total Ca (mg/dL) + 0.8 (4.0 - serum albumin [g/dL]), where 4.0 represents the average albumin level.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 822\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Option B: <strong>Fist-clenching</strong> or <strong>forearm exercise</strong> can lead to falsely <strong>elevated ionized (free) calcium</strong> levels.</p>\n<p>Option C: A <strong>tourniquet</strong> is usually avoided while drawing blood for calcium measurements since <strong>hemoconcentration</strong> also leads to an <strong>increase</strong> in serum <strong>calcium</strong> levels.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0482","text":"Which of the following cel‍ls is involved in the ini​tiation and control of os‍teoclastic activity?","raw_text":"Which of the following cells is involved in the initiation and control of osteoclastic activity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteo‍cytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteo‍blasts","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mononucl‍ear cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phago‍cytes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Osteoblasts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Osteoblasts</strong></p><p><strong>Osteoblasts </strong>are involved in the <strong>initiation </strong>and <strong>control </strong>of<strong> osteoclastic </strong>activity. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 960\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Osteoblasts</strong> produce receptor <strong>activator</strong> for nuclear factor κ­B ligand (<strong>RANKL</strong>) and macrophage colony­ stimulating factor (<strong>M-CSF</strong>), which are necessary for formation of <strong>mature osteoclasts</strong> and hence osteoclastic activity. </p>\n<p>Specific receptors for <strong>parathormone</strong> are present only on the <strong>osteoblasts</strong> and not on osteoclasts. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0483","text":"Which of these is not a ma‍rker of bone resorption?","raw_text":"Which of these is not a marker of bone resorption?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urine hydr‍oxyproline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum cross-linked c telop‍eptide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Urine total free deoxypyri‍dinoline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serum bone specific alkali‍ne phosphatase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Serum bone specific alkaline phosphatase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Serum bone specific alkaline phosphatase</strong></p><p>Serum <strong>bone specific alkaline phosphatase</strong> is a marker for bone <strong>formation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other markers of<strong> bone formation</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<p>Serum osteocalcin</p>\n</div>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<ul>\n<li>Serum type 1 procollagen (C-terminal/N-terminal)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Markers for <strong>bone resorption</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<p>Urinary hydroxyproline</p>\n</div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<p>Urinary total pyridinoline (PYD)</p>\n</div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<p>Urinary free deoxypyridinoline (DPD)</p>\n</div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<p>Urinary collagen type 1 cross-linked N-Telopeptide(NTX)</p>\n</div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<p>Urinary or serum collagen type 1 cross-linked C-Telopeptide (CTX)</p>\n</div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">\n<p>Tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase 5b</p>\n</div>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>International Osteoporosis Foundation has proposed serum CTX and serum PINP (Procollagen 1 N-terminal peptide) to be used as reference markers of bone resorption and formation, respectively, for the assessment of fracture risk and monitoring therapy in clinical settings. </p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6786","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a cause of secondary ​hyperparathyroidism?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a cause of secondary hyperparathyroidism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parathyro‍id adenoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lithium‍ intake","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitamin D ‍deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic kid‍ney disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Parathyroid adenoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Parathyroid adenoma</strong></p><p><strong>Parathyroid adenoma</strong> is a benign tumor of the parathyroid gland, which causes <strong>primary hyperparathyroidism </strong>(not secondary).</p>\n<p><strong>Secondary hyperparathyroidism </strong>is characterized by <strong>high</strong> levels of <strong>parathyroid hormone</strong>, to compensate for the <strong>low</strong> levels of serum <strong>calcium</strong>, due to some other underlying condition.</p>\n<p>Causes of secondary hyperparathyroidism, include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chronic kidney disease: </strong>The damaged kidneys are unable to produce sufficient amounts of the active form of vitamin D, 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol. This further causes hypocalcemia and triggers PTH secretion.</li>\n<li><strong>Vitamin D deficiency</strong>: Causes hypocalcemia, which triggers the increased secretion of PTH.</li>\n<li><strong>Lithium</strong>: May cause an increased set-point of calcium for parathyroid<strong> </strong>hormone suppression, which leads to <strong>parathyroid hyperplasia.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"YAeJJNFv","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"40_urinary_bladder_and_ureters","title":"40.Urinary Bladder And Ureters","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA4772","text":"The uterine artery crosses‍ the ureter ______","raw_text":"The uterine artery crosses the ureter ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Late‍rally","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medi‍ally","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anter‍iorly","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Poste‍riorly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anteriorly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anteriorly</strong></p><p>The uterine artery crosses the ureter <strong>anteriorly </strong>(Water under the bridge). Damage to the ureter is more likely at this site (<strong>most dangerous</strong> site) during <strong>hysterectomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4787","text":"A newborn child was noted ‍to have a congenital anom​aly as shown in the image‌ below. All of the follow‍ing statements are true a​bout this condition excep​t:","raw_text":"A newborn child was noted to have a congenital anomaly as shown in the image below. All of the following  statements are true about this condition except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Closure of abdominal wall ‍should be done within the​ first year","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bilateral inguinal hernia ‍is seen in males","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is associated with hypo‍spadias","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It predisposes to bladder ‍cancer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is associated with hypospadias","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/592c0ed92c334e03999ed3630babb6a5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is associated with hypospadias</strong></p><p>The congenital anomaly seen in the above image is <strong>exstrophy of the bladder (ectopia vesicae)</strong>. It is associated with <strong>epispadias,</strong> not hypospadias.</p>\n<p>Bladder exstrophy refers to <strong>herniation of the urinary bladder</strong> through an anterior abdominal wall defect. The bladder is open and <strong>exposed on the outside</strong> of the abdomen. The male to female ratio is 4:1. Chronic exposure of the bladder to the external environment can lead to <strong>adenocarcinoma</strong> of the bladder. </p>\n<p>The clinical features of bladder exstrophy include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Separation of the pubic bones with non-united symphysis pubis</li>\n<li><strong>Epispadias</strong></li>\n<li>In males: Broad and short penis and <strong>bilateral inguinal hernia</strong> </li>\n<li>In females: Bifid clitoris </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Management involves a staged surgical reconstruction of the bladder and genitalia. <strong>Closure of the abdominal and bladder wall</strong> is done in the <strong>first year of life.</strong> This often requires an <strong>iliac osteotomy</strong> to relax the tension on the bladder and repair the abdominal wall during wound-healing.</p>\n<p>Later, <strong>reconstruction of the bladder</strong> neck, sphincters, epispadias, and other genital defects is done. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1690","text":"A young girl presents with‍ continuous dribbling of ​urine but she voids norma‌lly. Her parents tell you‍ that she had never been ​totally dry since her bir​th. What is the most like‍ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young girl presents with continuous dribbling of urine but she voids normally. Her parents tell you that she had never been totally dry since her birth. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ectopic‍ ureter","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Urete‍rocele","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Congenital‍ megaureter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vesicouret‍eric reflux","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ectopic ureter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ectopic ureter</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with <strong>continuous dribbling of urine</strong>, in spite of <strong>regular voiding</strong>, is suggestive of an <strong>ectopic ureter.</strong></p>\n<p>Ectopic ureter is a congenital anomaly that occurs as a result of <strong>abnormal caudal migration</strong> of the <strong>ureteral bud</strong> during its insertion into the urinary bladder. They are usually associated with <strong>duplex ureters </strong>and drain from the <strong>upper pole of the kidney</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is more common in <strong>females</strong>. The ectopic ureter usually opens into the <strong>urethra, vagina, and perineum</strong>. Hence, female patients present with constant urinary dripping all day, even though the child voids regularly <strong>(Paradoxical incontinence). </strong>It can cause hydroureteronephrosis and recurrent UTI.</p>\n<p>In <strong>males</strong>, the ectopic ureter opens always <strong>above the external urethral sphincter. </strong> Hence they are <strong>usually continent. </strong>However, it can cause other complications such as <strong>orchitis and epididymitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Management involves <strong>re-implantation of the ureter into the bladder</strong> to treat incontinence and preserve renal functions. If the kidney is severely affected, <strong>partial nephrectomy</strong> is helpful. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1683","text":"During a cricket match, a ‍batsman controls his urge​ to urinate as he is unab‌le to take a break in-bet‍ween. While batting, he s​uffers a sudden blow as t​he ball hits him in the s‍uprapubic region. He pres​ents to the emergency dep​artment with severe hypog​astric pain and the inabi​lity to pass urine. He co‌llapses suddenly during t‍he examination. What is t​he most likely diagnosis?‌","raw_text":"During a cricket match, a batsman controls his urge to urinate as he is unable to take a break in-between. While batting, he suffers a sudden blow as the ball hits him in the suprapubic region. He presents to the emergency department with severe hypogastric pain and the inability to pass urine. He collapses suddenly during the examination. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intraperitoneal rupture of‍ the bladder","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Extraperitoneal rupture of‍ the bladder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Membranous urethra rupture‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pubic diastasis with bulba‍r urethra rupture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Intraperitoneal rupture of the bladder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Intraperitoneal rupture of the bladder</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with a history of <strong>injury to a full bladder</strong>, inability to pass urine, and <strong>syncope</strong> are suggestive of <strong>intraperitoneal rupture of the bladder</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Bladder rupture </strong>can be of two types:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 865.953px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\"><strong>Extraperitoneal rupture</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.953px;\"><strong>Intraperitoneal rupture</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\"><strong>More common </strong>(80%)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.953px;\"> Less common (20%)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\">Cause</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Penetrating trauma</li>\n<li>Pelvic fracture</li>\n<li>Surgical damage</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Associated with <strong>proximal urethral injury</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.953px;\"> \n<ul>\n<li>Direct blow or fall on a <strong>distended bladder</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\">Clinical features</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pelvic urinoma</strong> (encapsulated collection of extravasated urine) is seen</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Blood at the tip of the meatus</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.953px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Sudden severe pain in the hypogastrium</li>\n<li><strong>Syncope</strong></li>\n<li>Peritonitis</li>\n</ul>\n </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\">Treatment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Catheter drainage for 7–10 days</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.953px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Laparotomy</li>\n<li>Repair of bladder</li>\n<li>Bladder drainage</li>\n</ul>\n </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong><br/>Pubic diastasis</strong> refers to the <strong>separation of the pubic symphysis</strong> without concomitant fracture. Pubic diastasis with <strong>bulbar urethra rupture</strong> usually occurs due to <strong>straddle injury</strong> and presents with <strong>blood at the tip of the meatus</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4776","text":"A patient was rushed to th‍e casualty after sustaini​ng a suprapubic injury. O‌n examination, no blood i‍s seen at the meatus. On ​inserting Foley’s cathete​r, gross hematuria is not‍ed. His vitals are stable​ and his pelvic X-ray is ​given below. What will be​ the next step in the man​agement of this patient?","raw_text":"A patient was rushed to the casualty after sustaining a suprapubic injury. On examination, no blood is seen at the meatus. On inserting Foley’s catheter, gross hematuria is noted. His vitals are stable and his pelvic X-ray is given below. What will be the next step in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Suprapubic catheterization‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urinary ‍diversion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exploratory‍ laparotomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CT cys‍togram","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CT cystogram","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dfced570a30644ffaa9391171a62113c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bcea131273934a6086aba9e57d856d0ex1280x2018.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CT cystogram</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of a bladder injury. <strong>Open book fracture</strong> is most likely suggestive of an <strong>intraperitoneal bladder injury</strong>. <strong>CT cystogram</strong> will be the next step in the management to confirm the type of bladder injury. In <strong>unstable patients</strong>, a <strong>cystogram</strong> will be the investigation of choice.</p>\n<p>If an associated urethral injury is suspected, retrograde urethrogram (RGU) is done.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Closed book pelvic fractures are more commonly associated with urethral injuries than bladder injuries. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1680","text":"An elderly man was rushed ‍to the casualty at midnig​ht with complaints of sev‌ere suprapubic pain and i‍nability to pass urine fo​r the past 24 hours. The ​abdominal examination fin‍ding of the patient is gi​ven below. A smooth and e​nlarged prostate was felt​ in the per-rectal examin​ation. While attempting t‌o insert a 16-F Foley cat‍heter in this patient, yo​u feel resistance and fai‌l to catheterize. What wi‌ll be the next step in th​e management?","raw_text":"An elderly man was rushed to the casualty at midnight with complaints of severe suprapubic pain and inability to pass urine for the past 24 hours. The abdominal examination finding of the patient is given below. A smooth and enlarged prostate was felt in the per-rectal examination. While attempting to insert a 16-F Foley catheter in this patient, you feel resistance and fail to catheterize. What will be the next step in the management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insert an 18-Fr Coudé cath‍eter","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Insert a smaller sized Fol‍ey catheter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Perform c‍ystoscopy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suprapubic catheterization‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Insert an 18-Fr Coudé catheter","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/99396e72a941428da521fb49a2f907ba.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4375bfcaeb3e43ada826f880a091adaex1280x1862.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Insert an 18-Fr Coudé catheter</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute retention of urine,</strong> possibly because of <strong>benign prostatic hyperplasia</strong> (BPH). The next step would be to <strong>insert an 18-Fr Coudé catheter.</strong></p>\n<p>In men, the BPH especially the middle lobe enlargement obstructs the insertion of Foley's catherter. Hence, a Coude catheter is advised over suprapubic catetherization as the immediate next step as larger Fr Coude catheter is helpful in negotiating past the angulation in the prostatic urethra. </p>\n<p>(Note: In acute urine retention, urethral strictures should be suspected when there is resistance at the urethral meatus as the strictures are more common in distal urethra than proximal urethra)</p>\n<p><strong>Bladder outlet obstruction</strong> is usually the most common cause of acute retention of urine. Its clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Inability to pass urine</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Severe suprapubic pain</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">A <strong>distended</strong>, palpable, and tender bladder</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Acute urinary retention is managed by placing a <strong>urethral catheter</strong> as early as possible. Usually in BPH, <strong>smaller catheters will</strong> <strong>not pass</strong> through the angulation of the prostatic urethra as they are flexible and <strong>kink</strong>. Hence in BPH, the <strong>Coudé-tip catheter, </strong>which has a <strong>rigid and angulated tip</strong>, can be used to overcome the urethral obstruction.</p>\n<p>If urethral catheter placement is not successful, <strong>suprapubic catheterization</strong> should be done. </p>\n<p>If there is a <strong>urethral stricture</strong>, a <strong>council tip catheter</strong> is used to dilate it using a cystoscope, guide wires, and urethral dilators. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3472","text":"You are asked to remove a ‍Foley’s catheter after a ​procedure but you are una‌ble to deflate the balloo‍n. The surgeon himself tr​ies to remove it after cu​tting the sidearm but is ‍unsuccessful as well. Wha​t should be the next step​ of management?","raw_text":"You are asked to remove a Foley’s catheter after a procedure but you are unable to deflate the balloon. The surgeon himself tries to remove it after cutting the sidearm but is unsuccessful as well. What should be the next step of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CT-guided rupture of bulb ‍of Foley's","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inject ether to dissolve t‍he balloon and pull it ou​t","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inject water to over diste‍nd the balloon until it b​ursts and remove the cath‌eter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inflate with 20ml of water‍ and under USG guidance, ​locate and prick the ball‌oon","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Inflate with 20ml of water and under USG guidance, locate and prick the balloon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Inflate with 20ml of water and under USG guidance, locate and prick the balloon</strong></p><p><strong>USG-guided rupture</strong> of the balloon percutaneously after inflating it with 20ml water should be the next step of management.</p>\n<p>The failure of the balloon to deflate may be caused by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Faulty valve mechanism </li>\n<li>Blockage of the inflation channel by debris</li>\n<li>Crystallization of the fluid within the balloon (when saline is used)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The next best step in the management of a retained Foley's catheter is to further <strong>inflate</strong> the balloon with <strong>20mL</strong> <strong>of water</strong>, following which the inflated <strong>balloon is ruptured</strong> percutaneously with a needle under <strong>USG guidance</strong>. </p>\n<p>A suprapubic approach is used in males, while in females, either a transurethral or transvaginal route can be used. A <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">cystoscopy</strong> should be done to ensure that all fragments are removed.</p>\n<p>Other methods of approach include cutting<strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> the sidearm</strong> of the catheter, thereby disabling the valve mechanism, or advancing a <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">ureteric stylet </strong>or<strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> fine-gauge wire</strong> through the inflation port to dislodge debris or puncture the balloon. But these are often unsuccessful.</p>\n<p>Option A: CT is not routinely used in most settings with retained Foley's.</p>\n<p>Option B: Injection of <strong>ether</strong> through the balloon port causes the rupture of the balloon within seconds. But this technique causes significant <strong>bladder irritation.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Balloons should not be overinflated until they rupture. This may cause <strong>severe pain</strong> and could cause <strong>bladder rupture.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1691","text":"In the given picture of a ‍Foley’s catheter, what is​ the function of the tube‌ marked ‘A’?","raw_text":"In the given picture of a Foley’s catheter, what is the function of the tube marked ‘A’?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Suc‍tion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Irrig‍ation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"BCG Inst‍illation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cysto‍scope","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Irrigation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/870c1f72674944e6ae2ac6f32a564b21.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Irrigation</strong></p><p>The given image shows a three-way Foley's catheter. The tube marked ‘A’ is used for <strong>continuous irrigation of the bladder </strong>to remove debris &amp; blood clots<strong>.</strong> This is most commonly used after any prostate surgery or conditions with bleeding in the bladder.</p>\n<p>The <strong>central</strong> tube is used for the <strong>drainage</strong> of urine and the tube on the <strong>right</strong> is used for <strong>inflating</strong> the balloon.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4791","text":"What is the earliest sign ‍seen on cystoscopy in a p​atient suspected to have ‌bladder tuberculosis?","raw_text":"What is the earliest sign seen on cystoscopy in a patient suspected to have bladder tuberculosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tubercles at the ureteric ‍orifice","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pallor around the ureteric‍ orifices","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Focal areas ‍of hyperaemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hunner's‍ patches","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pallor around the ureteric orifices","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e5c4b134b71c4d4babbf287b78afcc1ex720x503.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4d451ff848c741d1bbb2a2acafee885bx1280x1663.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pallor around the ureteric orifices</strong></p><p>The <strong>earliest sign</strong> of tuberculosis of the bladder observed by cystoscopy is the <strong>pallor of the mucosa</strong> around the <strong>ureteric orifice</strong> due to submucous edema. Subsequently, <strong>tubercles</strong> may be seen.</p>\n<p>In long-standing cases, there is marked <strong>fibrosis</strong> and the <strong>capacity</strong> of the bladder is greatly <strong>reduced (thimble bladder).</strong> This is managed by <strong>augmentation cystoplasty.</strong></p>\n<p>The following X-ray KUB shows a thimble bladder:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><br/>Ureteric lesions can also be seen in genitourinary tuberculosis. They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Kerr’s kink</strong> - kinking of the ureteropelvic junction (UPJ)</li>\n<li>Ureteral stricture</li>\n<li>Beaded ureter</li>\n<li>Shortening of the ureter</li>\n<li><strong>Golf hole ureter</strong> - Longitudinal fibrosis leading to shortening of the ureter and pulling up the orifice as a gaping hole. <strong>Reimplantation of ureter</strong> is done.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4779","text":"A 50-year-old man comes wi‍th complaints of hematuri​a and difficulty in urina‌tion for the past few wee‍ks. Microscopic examinati​on of urine samples shows​ parasitic eggs as shown ‍in the image. Which type ​of bladder carcinoma is t​he patient most likely to​ develop?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man comes with complaints of hematuria and difficulty in urination for the past few weeks. Microscopic examination of urine samples shows parasitic eggs as shown in the image. Which type of bladder carcinoma is the patient most likely to develop?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transitional cell carcinom‍a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Squamous ce‍ll carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Adenoca‍rcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uroepitheli‍al carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Squamous cell carcinoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a81c23fa831a495c889fb71814aadca8.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Squamous cell carcinoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>schistosomiasis</strong>. The patient is most likely to develop <strong>squamous cell carcinoma</strong> as it is associated with schistosomiasis. The image shows oval-shaped eggs with the terminal spine which is characteristic of <em>Schistosoma haematobium. </em></p>\n<p>Schistosomal bladder cancers can also be adenocarcinoma or transitional cell carcinoma (less frequently).</p>\n<p><strong>Primary bladder tumors</strong> usually originate from the <strong>transitional epithelium </strong>of the bladder (90%). Sometimes, they arise from connective tissue. Secondary tumors of the bladder arise from the sigmoid and rectum, the prostate, the uterus, or the ovaries. </p>\n<p>Carcinoma of the bladder &amp; associated risk factors: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 94px; width: 557px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 265px;\"><strong>Histological subtype</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 280px;\"><strong>Risk factors</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 265px;\">Transitional cell carcinoma (urothelial)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 280px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Smoking</strong></li>\n<li>Exposure to aniline <strong>dyes</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cyclophosphamide</strong></li>\n<li>Pelvic irradiation</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 265px;\">\n<p>Squamous cell carcinoma</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 280px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Schistosomiasis</strong></li>\n<li>Chronic irritation caused by stone disease</li>\n<li>Bladder diverticula</li>\n<li>Chronic UTI</li>\n<li>Smoking</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 265px;\">Adenocarcinoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 280px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Persistent urachus or trigone of the bladder</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5868","text":"Which of the following dru‍gs causes carcinoma bladd​er?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs causes carcinoma bladder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclopho‍sphamide","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cisp‍latin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tax‍ane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tamo‍xifen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cyclophosphamide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cyclophosphamide</strong></p><p><strong>Cyclophosphamide</strong> (an alkylating agent) causes<strong> bladder cancer</strong></p>\n<p>The risk of bladder cancer with cyclophosphamide is directly proportional to the duration and intensity of cyclophosphamide treatment. <strong>Phosphoramide mustard</strong> is the<strong> primary mutagenic</strong> metabolite that causes bladder cancer in patients exposed to cyclophosphamide.</p>\n<p><strong>Painless, gross hematuria</strong> is the most common symptom that is indicative of a <strong>bladder carcinoma</strong> until proven otherwise. </p>\n<p>Depending upon the depth of invasion, bladder carcinoma can be divided into </p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Flat, non-invasive carcinoma in situ (CIS)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Non-muscle invasive bladder cancer (70% of cases) - does not invade detrusor muscle but can invade lamina propria. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Muscle-invasive bladder cancer - invades the detrusor muscle.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Diagnosis is by <strong>cystourethroscopy</strong> (directly visualizing the growth) and biopsy. Management is as follows: </p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Non-muscle invasive</strong> bladder cancer:</li>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Transurethral resection of bladder tumor <strong>(TURBT)</strong> followed by <strong>intravesical</strong> chemotherapy with<strong> mitomycin C</strong> or epirubicin</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Immunotherapy</strong> with regular <strong>intravesical instillations</strong> of Bacille Calmette Guerin<strong> (BCG)</strong> in <strong>high-risk</strong> tumors.</li>\n</ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Muscle invasive</strong> bladder cancer: <strong>Radical cystectomy</strong> along with <strong>urinary diversion </strong>procedure and <strong>lymph node dissection</strong> +/- <strong>neoadjuvant chemotherapy</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Cisplatin</strong> is an<strong> anticancer drug</strong> used to <strong>treat testicular cancer</strong>. It is associated with an increased risk for acute myeloid leukemia<strong> (AML)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Taxane</strong> is an <strong>anticancer</strong> <strong>drug</strong> and is the main component in the <strong>treatment</strong> regimens of <strong>metastatic cancers</strong>. Taxane's adverse effects are <strong>neutropenia</strong> and <strong>fluid retention</strong> (pulmonary edema).</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Tamoxifen</strong> is a selective estrogen receptor modulator <strong>(SERM)</strong> and is used in the <strong>treatment</strong> of <strong>estrogen-dependent breast cancer</strong>. Due to its partial agonistic activity, it is known to <strong>increase endometrial cancer</strong> in <strong>post-menopausal women</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1689","text":"Identify the incorrect sta‍tement regarding bladder ​carcinoma.","raw_text":"Identify the incorrect statement regarding bladder carcinoma.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Painless hematuria is the ‍most common symptom","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inactivation of the Rb gen‍e is implicated in the ca​ncer formation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Urine cytology has low sen‍sitivity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The most important prognos‍tic factor is the size of​ the tumor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The most important prognostic factor is the size of the tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The most important prognostic factor is the size of the tumor</strong></p><p>The most important prognostic factor in <strong>bladder carcinoma</strong> is the <strong>depth of invasion</strong>. </p>\n<p>Activation of oncogenes such as <strong><em>ras, c-erbB, E2F3,</em></strong> and inactivation of tumor-suppressor genes such as <strong><em>p53, p21, p16,</em> and <em>Rb gene</em> </strong>is implicated in the cancer formation of the bladder.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of bladder carcinoma include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Painless gross haematuria</strong> - Most common symptom and is indicative of a bladder carcinoma until proven otherwise</li>\n<li>Urinary retention</li>\n<li>Constant pelvic pain</li>\n<li>Increased urinary frequency</li>\n<li>Discomfort while urinating</li>\n<li>If there is nerve involvement, <strong>pain is referred</strong> to the suprapubic region, groins, perineum, and thighs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Investigations done for bladder cancer are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Urine cytology - Low sensitivity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Intravenous urography - Not frequently used nowadays. Replaced by non-contrast CT and MRI</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Cystourethroscopy and biopsy</strong> - The <strong>best investigation</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3466","text":"What is the treatment of c‍hoice for a patient with ​non-invasive bladder carc‌inoma of stage pT1?","raw_text":"What is the treatment of choice for a patient with non-invasive bladder carcinoma of stage pT1?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intraves‍ical BCG","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Radical c‍ystectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transurethral resection of‍ the bladder tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chemot‍herapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Intravesical BCG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Intravesical BCG</strong></p><p>Since the pathological staging of the tumor is given (pT1), it implies that transurethral resection of the bladder tumor (TURBT) has already been done. Therefore, this patient should be managed by <strong>instillation of intravesical BCG</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>TURBT</strong> is done for the <strong>pathologic staging</strong> of the tumor. This staging helps us to decide the further plan of treatment. <strong>Intravesical therapy</strong> (using chemotherapy or immunotherapy) is done within 24hrs of TURBT in order to kill any residual cancer cells. It only affects the lining of the bladder and is not useful for cancers that have spread into the deeper layers.</p>\n<p>pT stage: Pathological stage</p>\n<table style=\"width: 490px; height: 473px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76.3993px; text-align: left; height: 18px;\"><strong>pT stage</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 399.601px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Extent of the tumor</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76.3993px; text-align: left; height: 18px;\"><strong>T<sub>x</sub></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 399.601px; height: 18px;\">Primary tumor cannot be assessed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76.3993px; text-align: left; height: 17px;\"><strong>T<sub>0</sub></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 399.601px; height: 17px;\">No evidence of primary tumor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76.3993px; text-align: left; height: 17px;\"><strong>T<sub>a</sub></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 399.601px; height: 17px;\">Noninvasive papillary carcinoma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76.3993px; text-align: left; height: 17px;\"><strong>T<sub>is</sub></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 399.601px; height: 17px;\">Carcinoma in situ</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 4.04688px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76.3993px; text-align: left; height: 4.04688px;\"><strong>T<sub>1</sub></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 399.601px; height: 4.04688px;\">Tumor invades subepithelial connective tissue (lamina propria)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76.3993px; text-align: left; height: 17px;\"><strong>T<sub>2</sub></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 399.601px; height: 17px;\">Tumor invades muscularis propria bladder wall</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76.3993px; text-align: left; height: 17px;\"><strong>T<sub>3</sub></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 399.601px; height: 17px;\">Tumor invades perivesical tissue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 3px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76.3993px; text-align: left; height: 3px;\"><strong>T<sub>4</sub></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 399.601px; height: 3px;\">Tumor invades any of the following: Prostate, uterus, vagina, pelvic wall and abdominal wall</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Treatment of carcinoma bladder:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 494.472px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173px; text-align: left;\"><strong>pT stage</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 307.472px;\"><strong>Treatment</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Low-grade pT<sub>a</sub></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 307.472px;\"><strong>Intravesical chemotherapy</strong> with mitomycin, thiotepa, and adriamycin may be started after TURBT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">High-grade pT<sub>a</sub></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">pT<sub>is</sub></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">pT<sub>1 </sub></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 307.472px;\"><strong>Intravesical BCG</strong> is started after TURBT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">pT<sub>2</sub></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 307.472px;\"><strong>Radical cystectomy</strong> followed by <strong>radiotherapy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 173px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">pT<sub>3</sub></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">pT<sub>4</sub></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 307.472px;\">Neoadjuvant chemotherapy (M-VAC), radical cystectomy, and radiotherapy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong><br/>M-VAC</strong> chemotherapy regime consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Methotrexate</li>\n<li>Vinblastine</li>\n<li>Adriamycin</li>\n<li>Cisplatin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4785","text":"A patient with carcinoma i‍n situ of the urinary bla​dder is posted for intrav‌esical therapy. What is t‍he strain of mycobacteria​ that will be used in thi​s therapy?","raw_text":"A patient with carcinoma in situ of the urinary bladder is posted for intravesical therapy. What is the strain of mycobacteria that will be used in this therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mycobacte‍rium bovis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mycobacteri‍um canettii","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mycobacterium indicus pran‍ni","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mycobacterium bovis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mycobacterium bovis</strong></p><p>An attenuated strain of <em><strong>Mycobacterium bovis</strong></em> is used in <strong>intravesical BCG therapy.</strong></p>\n<p>Intravesical BCG is used for the treatment of <strong>non-invasive</strong> (no invasion into the muscle layer) urinary <strong>bladder tumors</strong>. It induces a strong local <strong>inflammatory response</strong> against the attenuated bacteria, which helps in the destruction of the tumor cells.</p>\n<p>Option d:<strong> Mw vaccine</strong> for <strong>multibacillary leprosy</strong> patients and their contacts is extracted from <strong><em>Mycobacterium indicus pranii</em></strong>. It is also used as an adjunct to chemotherapy for category II tuberculosis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3468","text":"Which laser is preferred f‍or performing transurethr​al resection of the bladd‌er tumor?","raw_text":"Which laser is preferred for performing transurethral resection of the bladder tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbon dio‍xide laser","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nd-YAG‍ laser","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ho-YAG‍ laser","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Argon‍ laser","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nd-YAG laser","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nd-YAG laser</strong></p><p>The <strong>Nd: YAG </strong>laser is preferred for the resection of minimally invasive <strong>bladder tumors</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3453","text":"An elderly man on follow-u‍p for treatment of bladde​r cancer presents with pa‌inless hematuria. Which o‍f the following assays sh​ould be done on this pati​ent?","raw_text":"An elderly man on follow-up for treatment of bladder cancer presents with painless hematuria. Which of the following assays should be done on this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CA ‍19-9","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carcinoembry‍onic antigen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thymidin‍e kinase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"NMP‍ 22","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. NMP 22","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. NMP 22</strong></p><p>An<strong> NMP 22</strong> assay should be done in this patient to detect the <strong>recurrence of bladder carcinoma</strong>. </p>\n<p>NMP 22 is a nuclear matrix protein that is used to form cell nuclei. It is shed into the urine and has a higher concentration in the urine of bladder cancer patients. NMP-22 cutoff levels for bladder cancer detection is <strong><span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\">&gt; </span>10 units/mL.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3469","text":"Urinary diversion is indic‍ated in all the following​ clinical conditions, exc‌ept:","raw_text":"Urinary diversion is indicated in all the following clinical conditions, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ectopia‍ vesicae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carcinom‍a bladder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neurogeni‍c bladder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bladder ‍hematoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bladder hematoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d7104355e2504f61be486886d460ce12x1280x2886.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bladder hematoma</strong></p><p>Urinary diversion is <strong>not</strong> done in cases of bladder hematoma. </p>\n<p><strong>Urinary diversion</strong> procedures are performed to divert urine from the bladder to a new exit site. It is of two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cutaneous urinary diversion</strong> (External diversion): Urine drains through an opening created in the abdominal wall and skin\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ileal conduit - </strong>urine is diverted by implanting ureters into a <strong>loop of ileum</strong> that is led out through the abdominal wall.</li>\n<li><strong>Cutaneous ureterostomy -</strong> ureters are directed through the abdominal wall and attached to an <strong>opening in the skin</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Continent urinary diversion</strong> (Internal urinary diversion): A portion of the intestine is used to create a new reservoir for urine. The internal pouch is placed inside the abdomen. The ureters are attached to the internal pouch, and the internal pouch is attached to the urethra or to a stoma in the abdomen with a valve.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Indications</strong> of urinary diversion:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Post cystectomy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Carcinoma of the urinary bladder</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Distal obstruction</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Neurogenic bladder</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ectopia vesicae, Incurable fistula</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Irremovable obstruction</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Trauma to the ureters and urethra</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4792","text":"A patient presents to the ‍emergency department with​ confusion and drowsiness‌. He underwent cystectomy‍ with urinary diversion f​or bladder carcinoma 8 we​eks back. ABG analysis an‍d electrolyte values are ​given below. Which of the​ following urinary divers​ion conduits was most lik​ely done in this patient?‌ pH - 7.20 PaO 2 - 12.3 P‍aCO 2 - 4.5 HCO 3 - 12 Se​rum potassium - 1.9 mEq/l‌ Chloride - 111 mEq/l","raw_text":"<p>A patient presents to the emergency department with confusion and drowsiness. He underwent cystectomy with urinary diversion for bladder carcinoma 8 weeks back. ABG analysis and electrolyte values are given below. Which of the following urinary diversion conduits was most likely done in this patient?</p>\n<p>pH - 7.20</p>\n<p>PaO<sub>2</sub> - 12.3</p>\n<p>PaCO<sub>2</sub> - 4.5</p>\n<p>HCO<sub>3</sub> - 12</p>\n<p>Serum potassium - 1.9 mEq/l</p>\n<p>Chloride - 111 mEq/l</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sto‍mach","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Jej‍unum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Il‍eum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sigmoi‍d colon","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sigmoid colon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/df8b617cdec140d9bbdd3f8e3570e11ex1280x2886.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sigmoid colon</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1475\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>A <strong>uretero-sigmoid diversion</strong> was most likely done in this patient as it frequently results in <strong>hyperchloremic hypokalemic acidosis. </strong>Hypokalemia is less common with ileal diversions because the ileum reabsorbs some of the potassium from the urine while the colon does not.</p>\n<p>The order in which the hyperchloraemic hypokalemic acidosis occurs is sigmoid colon &gt; colon/ileum.</p>\nElectrolyte abnormalities based on the part of intestine involved:</div>\n<div class=\"column\"> </div>\n<div class=\"column\">\n<table style=\"width: 515px; height: 118px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 171.406px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Stomach  </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.594px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Jejunum</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 270px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Ileum/colon</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 16.2396px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 171.406px; height: 16.2396px;\">Hypokalemia  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.594px; height: 16.2396px;\">Hyperkalemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 270px; height: 16.2396px;\">Hypokalemia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 171.406px; height: 18px;\">Hypochloremia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.594px; height: 18px;\">Hypochloremia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 270px; height: 18px;\">Hyperchloremia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 171.406px; height: 18px;\">Metabolic alkalosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.594px; height: 18px;\">Metabolic acidosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 270px; height: 18px;\">Metabolic acidosis </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4783","text":"A 35-year-old lady experie‍nces increased frequency ​of urination and lower ab‌dominal pain which is rel‍ieved by micturition. Uri​nalysis is normal. Cystos​copy reveals fissures and‍ punctate hemorrhages in ​the bladder mucosa. What ​is the most likely diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old lady experiences increased frequency of urination and lower abdominal pain which is relieved by micturition. Urinalysis is normal. Cystoscopy reveals fissures and punctate hemorrhages in the bladder mucosa. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interstiti‍al cystitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Malako‍plakia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tuberculos‍is bladder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cystitis‍ cystica","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Interstitial cystitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e67a646b32a44f2c9d707ab182567c90x720x748.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Interstitial cystitis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with <strong>normal urinalysis</strong> and cystoscopy findings are suggestive of<strong> interstitial cystitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Interstitial cystitis is a persistent, painful form of <strong>chronic cystitis</strong>, occurring most frequently in women. It is also known as chronic pelvic pain syndrome or bladder pain syndrome. Its clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Severe suprapubic pain</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Urinary frequency</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Urgency</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hematuria</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Dysuria without evidence of bacterial infection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cystoscopic findings seen in bladder mucosa are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fissures</li>\n<li><strong>Punctate hemorrhages</strong> (glomerulations)</li>\n<li>Ulcerations in the fundus of the bladder <strong>(Hunner's ulcer)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image shows Hunner's ulcer seen in interstitial cystitis:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Treatment options include :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Conservative - Patient education about the chronicity of the condition, <span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"><strong>behavioral modification</strong> (timed voiding, bladder training), </span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">stress reduction, dietary alteration (avoidance of caffeine and </span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">spicy and acidic foods) and physical therapy should be the </span><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">initial management for all patients.</span></li>\n<li>Pharmacological - Neuropathic analgesics (e.g. <strong>amitriptyline, pregabalin</strong>) are used for those with a significant pain component, whereas <strong>antimuscarinics</strong> and <strong>β3-agonists</strong> are used for frequency, nocturia and urgency symptoms.</li>\n<li>Surgical - For those with Hunner lesions, <strong>fulguration</strong> or <strong>laser</strong> to these lesions can be beneficial. <strong>Cystodistension</strong> as a treatment has variable evidence for success. If these options fail to improve symptoms major surgical reconstruction can be considered in selected cases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option b: <strong>Malakoplakia</strong> is a distinct form of inflammatory reaction seen in <strong>chronic cystitis</strong>. It appears as soft, yellow, slightly raised mucosal plaques.</p>\n<p>Option d: <strong>Cystitis cystica</strong> occurs due to chronic irritation of the bladder mucosa. Cystoscopy reveals cystic lesions in the bladder.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3641","text":"A child with recurrent uri‍nary tract infection unde​rgoes voiding cystourethr‌ogram. It shows reflux of‍ urine into the pelvis an​d calyces, without dilati​on of the ureter. What is‍ the grade of vesicourete​ric reflux?","raw_text":"A child with recurrent urinary tract infection undergoes voiding cystourethrogram. It shows reflux of urine into the pelvis and calyces, without dilation of the ureter. What is the grade of vesicoureteric reflux?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Grad‍e II","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gra‍de I","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Grad‍e III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Grad‍e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Grade II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4d076d2a289a47e0866fc5bca3fdd732x1064x2464.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Grade II</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 4172\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Reflux of urine into pelvis and calyces without dilatation is<strong> Grade II</strong> vesicoureteric reflux (VUR).</p>\n<p>VUR is the retrograde flow of urine from the bladder to the upper urinary tract. VUR is a common clinical entity. The<strong> investigation of choice</strong> for VUR is<strong> micturating cystourethrogram (MCU)</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1425\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>VUR is graded as follows:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<table style=\"height: 212px; width: 387px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 62px; text-align: center;\"><strong>  GRADE</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 308px; text-align: center;\"><strong>DESCRIPTION</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 62px; text-align: left;\">I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 308px;\">Reflux into a non-dilated ureter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 62px; text-align: left;\">II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 308px;\">Reflux into the pelvis and calyces without dilatation of the ureter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 62px; text-align: left;\">III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 308px;\">Reflux is associated with mild to moderate dilatation of the ureter, renal pelvis, and calyces, with minimal blunting of the fornices</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 62px; text-align: left;\">IV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 308px;\">Moderate ureteral tortuosity and dilatation of the pelvis and calyces</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 62px; text-align: left;\">V</td>\n<td style=\"width: 308px;\">Gross dilatation of the ureter, pelvis, and calyses; loss of papillary impressions; ureteral  tortuosity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4657","text":"Which of the following wil‍l be seen in a patient wi​th Marion’s disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following will be seen in a patient with Marion’s disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypertrophy of the interna‍l sphincter of the bladde​r","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypertrophy of the externa‍l sphincter of the bladde​r","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Congenital obstruction of ‍the prostatic urethra","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior ur‍ethral valve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypertrophy of the internal sphincter of the bladder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypertrophy of the internal sphincter of the bladder</strong></p><p><strong>Hypertrophy of the internal sphincter of the bladder</strong> is seen in <strong>Marion’s disease.</strong> This leads to bladder outlet obstruction. Its clinical features include voiding and storage symptoms (usually associated with BPH). Therefore it is also called prostatism sans prostate:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Increased urinary frequency</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Urgency</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Dribbling of urine</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hesitancy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Intermittent stream</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nocturia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is commonly seen in young patients.</p>\n<p>Bladder outlet obstruction can occur due to 2 causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Muscular causes</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Congenital hypertrophy of internal sphincter, i.e., Marion's disease</li>\n<li>Dyssynergic contraction of the smooth muscle of the bladder neck (bladder neck dyssynergia)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fibrosis</strong> of bladder neck following transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1103","text":"Which of the following is ‍true regarding the condit​ion shown below?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding the condition shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bilateral in more than 50%‍ of the cases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Associated with dysplastic‍ renal tissue","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Children usually present w‍ith stones","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Affects males more often t‍han females","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Associated with dysplastic renal tissue","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b6ceffbeea1a438dabd3acd01d312c48.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Associated with dysplastic renal tissue</strong></p><p>The given intravenous urography (IVU) image depicts <strong>cobra head/adder head deformity</strong> which is a classic sign of ureterocele. It is associated with dysplastic renal tissue.</p>\n<p><strong>Ureterocele</strong>:</p>\n<p>A ureterocele is the cystic enlargement of the intramural ureter. It is thought to be caused due to ureteric orifice atresia. It is observed <strong>more in females </strong>than males in a 4:1 preponderance and is <strong>bilateral </strong>in about<strong> 10% </strong>of the cases. They are similar to ectopic ureters wherein the upper pole of the kidney is drained. This condition is typically <strong>associated with dysplastic and</strong> <strong>non-functional renal tissue</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clinical presentation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Children</strong> with ureterocele usually present with <strong>infection.</strong></li>\n<li>Adults present with renal stones.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Diagnosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>IVU shows a <strong>classical cobra head sign</strong> but is relatively infrequently performed these days.</li>\n<li>Cystoscopy shows a bulging, translucent swelling involving the ureteric orifice where there is urinary stasis. It is emptied by ureteric peristalsis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Surgical excision with re-implantation of the ureter is the treatment of choice for simple ureterocele.</li>\n<li>Renal stones associated with ureterocele are removed using an endoscopic incision</li>\n<li>An advanced disease that causes hydronephrosis or pyonephrosis is an indication for nephrectomy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4777","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements regarding bladder​ stone is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding bladder stone is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary bladder stone deve‍lops in sterile urine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cystine calculus is radiol‍ucent","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Uric acid stones occur in ‍patients with ileostomies​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Triple phosphate calculus ‍occurs in urine infected ​with urea-splitting organ‌isms","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cystine calculus is radiolucent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cystine calculus is radiolucent</strong></p><p>A<strong> cystine calculus</strong> occurs only in the<strong> presence of cystinuria</strong> and is<strong> radioopaque</strong> because of its high sulfur content.</p>\n<p><strong>Primary bladder stone</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Develops in <strong>sterile urine</strong> in the absence of any known functional, anatomic, or infectious factors and does not necessarily imply that stones have formed de novo in the bladder.</li>\n<li><strong>Most common in children</strong> younger than 10 years of age</li>\n<li>Most vesical calculi are mixed</li>\n<li>Ammonium acid urate, calcium oxalate, uric acid, and calcium phosphate are the most common components of primary bladder calculi</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Secondary bladder stone</strong>: Forms during infection, due to bladder outflow obstruction, impaired bladder emptying, or the presence of a foreign body.</p>\n<p><strong>Types:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Calcium oxalate</strong> stones</li>\n<li><strong>Uric acid</strong> stones - occur in patients with<strong> gout</strong> but are also observed in patients with ileostomies or with bladder outlet obstruction</li>\n<li><strong>Cystine</strong> stones - in cystinuria</li>\n<li><strong>Triple phosphate</strong> stones - occur in urine infected with <strong>urea-splitting organisms</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"xww1xiPJ","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"41_general_anatomy_of_thorax","title":"41.General Anatomy Of Thorax","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB0816","text":"Which of the following is ​a false rib?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false rib?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1st​ rib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6th​ rib","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7th​ rib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"8th​ rib","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 8th rib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0ac5921585944a219f13ddfd483ea7a9x1280x1248.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 8th rib</strong></p><p>The <strong>8<sup>th</sup> rib</strong> is the <strong>false rib</strong> because it does not articulate with the sternum anteriorly.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First 7 ribs </strong>are<strong> true ribs</strong> as they are attached anteriorly to the sternum by costal cartilages.</li>\n<li><strong>Ribs 8-10 </strong>are<strong> false ribs</strong> as they join the superjacent costal cartilage.</li>\n<li><strong>Ribs 11 and 12 </strong>are <strong>floating ribs </strong>as they float freely without costal attachment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0819","text":"Which of the following joi​nts is not formed by dire​ct articulation with ribs​?","raw_text":"Which of the following joints is not formed by direct articulation with ribs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Costoverte​bral joint","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Costochon​dral joint","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Costotrans​verse joint","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Costoster​nal joint","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Costosternal joint","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Costosternal joint</strong></p><p>The costosternal joint is not formed by direct articulation with ribs. The <strong>costosternal joint</strong> is formed by the articulation of the <strong>lateral sternal border</strong> with the <strong>costal cartilages</strong> of the ribs.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0824","text":"What type of joint is the ​first sternocostal joint?​","raw_text":"What type of joint is the first sternocostal joint?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Syncho​ndrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Synart​hrosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Synovia​l joint","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Syndesmo​sis joint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Synarthrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/25cee85aca3442dc846928d34def4cf9x1200x1225.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Synarthrosis</strong></p><p>The <strong>first sternocostal joint</strong> is a type of synarthrosis.</p>\n<p>Joints are classified in 2 ways:</p>\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_section\">Classification 1 is based on the type of motion (functional):</div>\n<ol class=\"p-rich_text_list p-rich_text_list__ordered\" data-border=\"0\" data-indent=\"0\" data-stringify-type=\"ordered-list\">\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Synarthrosis - no movement (first sternocostal joint)</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Amphiarthrosis - some movement (pubic symphysis)</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Diarthrosis - full movement (elbow/shoulder/ankle)</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_section\">Classification 2 is based on the structure of joint:</div>\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_section\">1. Fibrous</div>\n<ul class=\"p-rich_text_list p-rich_text_list__ordered\" data-border=\"0\" data-indent=\"1\" data-stringify-type=\"ordered-list\">\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"1\">Sutures (joints between skull bones)</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"1\">Syndesmoses (distal tibiofibular joint)</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"1\">Gomphose (joint between teeth and teeth sockets in mandibular/maxillary bone)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>2. Cartilaginous</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Synchondrosis - primary cartilaginous (costochondral joints)</li>\n<li>Symphysis - secondary cartilaginous (manubriosternal and xiphisternal joints)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>3. Synovial (elbow, knee, hip etc.)</p>\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_section\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">the presence of these help the thoracic cavity expand during inspiration. E.g.:</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Interchondral joint</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sternocostal joint</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Costovertebral joint</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Costotransverse joint</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4401","text":"The joint between the atta​chment of the 8th and 9th​ rib to the 7th rib is a:​","raw_text":"The joint between the attachment of the 8th and 9th rib to the 7th rib is a:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibrocartila​ginous joint","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Symphyse​al joint","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Synovia​l joint","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fibrou​s joint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Synovial joint","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/06307f88739a433dbe3718d6f18b2b86x1280x1307.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Synovial joint</strong></p><p>The interchondral joint between the attachment of the 8th and 9th rib with the 7th rib is a <strong>synovial joint</strong>.</p>\n<p>Joints in the thoracic cavity are of the following types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Synarthrosis</strong> - structures are connected by a fibrous band of tissue. E.g, <strong>1st sternocostal joint</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Synchondrosis</strong> - primary cartilaginous joints. E.g., <strong>costochondral joints</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Symphysis</strong> - secondary cartilaginous joints. E.g. <strong>manubriosternal</strong> and <strong>xiphisternal</strong> joints </li>\n<li><strong>Synovial</strong> joints - the presence of these help the thoracic cavity expand during inspiration. E.g.:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Interchondral</strong> joint</li>\n<li>Sternocostal joint</li>\n<li>Costovertebral<strong> </strong>joint</li>\n<li>Costotransverse joint</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: </p>\n<p>The interchondral joint between the <strong>9th</strong> and <strong>10th cartilages</strong> is never synovial and sometimes absent.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3737","text":"Which of the following doe​s not provide articulatio​ns to ribs?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not provide articulations to ribs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vertebra​l bodies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intervert​ebral disc","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spinous process of vertebr​a","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Transverse process of vert​ebra","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Spinous process of vertebra","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Spinous process of vertebra</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The<strong> spinous processes</strong> of vertebrae do not provide articulation to ribs.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Note: </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Two facets on head of the ribs are separated by a transverse crest which is attached to the intervertebral disc above it. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0828","text":"Which of the following rib​s is atypical and doesn't​ articulate with the vert​ebra at 3 sites?","raw_text":"Which of the following ribs is atypical and doesn't articulate with the vertebra at 3 sites?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1st​ rib","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2nd​ rib","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6th​ rib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"8th​ rib","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1st rib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1st rib</strong></p><p><strong>1st rib</strong> is an <strong>atypical</strong> rib and does <strong>not articulate at 3 sites</strong> with a vertebra. It has 2 articulations as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Circular facet on the head of the rib with the body of the first thoracic vertebra</li>\n<li>Tubercle of the rib to the transverse process of the corresponding vertebra</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ribs are divided based on anatomical features as typical and atypical.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical ribs:</strong></p>\n<p>Ribs <strong>3 to 9</strong> are typical. A typical rib is curved, and has a <strong>shaft</strong> with <strong>anterior</strong> and <strong>posterior ends:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The anterior end has costal cartilage</li>\n<li>The posterior end has a <strong>head, neck,</strong> and a <strong>tubercle</strong> and <strong>articulates</strong> with the <strong>vertebra</strong> at<strong> 3 sites</strong> as follows<strong>:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>head</strong> has<strong> 2 facets</strong> and articulates with the vertebra at 2 sites</li>\n<li>The tubercle has articular and nonarticular surfaces. The articular surface articulates with the transverse process</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Atypical ribs:</strong></p>\n<p>Ribs 1, 2, 10, 11, and 12 are atypical. </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1st rib</strong> - it is flat. The <strong>head</strong> has only a <strong>single</strong> articular <strong>facet.</strong> The superior surface has a <strong>scalene tubercle. </strong></li>\n<li><strong>2nd rib</strong> - the external surface of the shaft has a <strong>rough area</strong>, where the <strong>serratus anterior</strong> attaches.</li>\n<li><strong>10th rib</strong> - the <strong>head</strong> has a <strong>single facet.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>11th</strong> and <strong>12th</strong> ribs - the <strong>head</strong> has <strong>1 large facet</strong>. They <strong>don't</strong> have <strong>necks</strong> or <strong>tubercles.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0815","text":"A 47-year-old smoker prese​nted with cough, chest pa​in, dyspnea, and right sh​oulder pain. Chest X-ray ​revealed homogeneous opac​ity at the lung apex and ‌destruction of the first ‍rib. Which of the followi​ng is not compressed?","raw_text":"A 47-year-old smoker presented with cough, chest pain, dyspnea, and right shoulder pain. Chest X-ray revealed homogeneous opacity at the lung apex and destruction of the first rib. Which of the following is not compressed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior intercostal arter​y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thorac​ic duct","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"First posterior intercosta​l vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sympathe​tic trunk","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thoracic duct","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a3b0007cd1aa4001be5e4a3ca08ba529x1280x1715.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c85cc627e37544e89f2e4645cae5e249x1280x1289.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thoracic duct</strong></p><p>The given case scenario and chest X-ray features are suggestive of a <strong>tumor</strong> at the <strong>apex of the right lung</strong>, also called <strong>Pancoast tumor</strong>. The thoracic duct does not lie between the 1st rib and the apex of the lung, so it will not be compressed. The thoracic duct crosses from right to left in the mediastinum at the level of T5 and ascends upwards to drain into the venous system. </p>\n<p>The structures present between the <strong>1<sup>st</sup> rib</strong> and <strong>apex</strong> of the <strong>lung</strong> (<strong>medial to lateral</strong>) can be remembered using the mnemonic <strong>SVAN</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S</strong>ympathetic trunk</li>\n<li><strong>V</strong>ein - Posterior intercostal</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>rtery - Superior intercostal</li>\n<li><strong>N</strong>erve - T1 nerve</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical significance</strong>:</p>\n<p>Pancoast tumors can cause destruction of the first rib by mass effect. Given below is an X-ray image of a Pancoast tumor (marked as P). </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0822","text":"Which of the following str​uctures passes through th​e marked area in the diag​ram below?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures passes through the marked area in the diagram below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1st thora​cic nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subclavi​an artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal tho​racic artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subclav​ian vein","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Subclavian vein","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2771dd1ed38d489eaa0691846afec1f6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/471e40f195e14f8b830b7b3ad24315e9x600x797.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Subclavian vein</strong></p><p>The marked area is the <strong>groove anterior </strong>to the<strong> scalene tubercle</strong> on the<strong> first rib</strong>. The<strong> subclavian vein</strong> passes through this.</p>\n<p>It is structures passing through the grooves around the scalene tubercle are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior groove - Subclavian vein.</li>\n<li>Posterior groove - Subclavian artery and lower trunk of brachial plexus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows the relations of the first rib</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0820","text":"The image given below show​s structures in the poste​rior triangle of the neck​. Where does the structur​e marked A attach?","raw_text":"The image given below shows structures in the posterior triangle of the neck. Where does the structure marked A attach?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1st rib - Scalene tubercle​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1st rib - Tuberculum sella​e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2nd rib - Scalene tubercle​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2nd rib - Chassaignac tube​rcle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1st rib - Scalene tubercle","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b13094e6926e494c88aabf1632f3227e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8780e1164d2d45399ec455169fd76d53x1280x1152.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f0ccf605a94f409fb174cddd8990d1a8x600x888.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1st rib - Scalene tubercle</strong></p><p>The structure marked A is the <strong>scalenus anterior</strong>. It attaches on the <strong>first rib</strong> on its<strong> scalene tubercle.</strong> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>scalene tubercle </strong>is a small projection on the medial (inner) border of the <strong>1<sup>st</sup> rib</strong>, between <strong>two grooves.</strong></p>\n<p>Structures passing through these grooves are</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior groove -<strong> Subclavian vein</strong>.</li>\n<li>Posterior groove - <strong>Subclavian artery</strong> and lower trunk of <strong>brachial plexus</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: The tuberculum sellae is a part of the sphenoid bone and is present within the sella turcica</p>\n<p>Option D: The Chassaignac tubercle or carotid tubercle is present on the transverse process of C6. The carotid pulsations are palpated by pressing against this.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0821","text":"Which of the following mus​cles seen below does not ​attach to the first rib?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles seen below does not attach to the first rib?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"​D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. C","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fca960afdcaf44979152b853f1e832a5.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/706e1af3ca5c4edca5779e32f609e64dx1280x1152.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a0071cb0ca7347edbb0cc6d98235c6d6x600x797.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. C</strong></p><p>In the given image, the scalenus posterior marked as C has no attachment on the first rib. It attaches to the second rib.</p>\n<p>Other structures marked are:</p>\n<p>A - Scalenus anterior</p>\n<p>B - Subclavius</p>\n<p>D - Scalenus medius</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The attachments on the 1<sup>st</sup> rib are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Subclavius</li>\n<li>Scalenus anterior</li>\n<li>Scalenus medius</li>\n<li>First digitation of serratus anterior</li>\n<li>Suprapleural membrane</li>\n<li>Costoclavicular ligament</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0826","text":"The relationship of the co​mponents of the neurovasc​ular bundle from below up​wards in intercostal spac​e is _______","raw_text":"The relationship of the components of the neurovascular bundle from below upwards in intercostal space is _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vein-art​ery-nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Artery-n​erve-vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vein-ner​ve-artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nerve-ar​tery-vein","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nerve-artery-vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/46f3afbb3c9d4b6a8297201220c33b98x600x596.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nerve-artery-vein</strong></p><p>The arrangement of the components of the neurovascular bundle in the intercostal space from<strong> below upwards</strong> is <strong>nerve-artery-vein</strong>. </p>\n<p>The arrangement can be remembered from <strong>above downwards</strong> with the mnemonic <strong>VAN:</strong> <strong>v</strong>ein-<strong>a</strong>rtery-<strong>n</strong>erve.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0827","text":"In the costal groove of wh​ich of the following ribs​ is the classic arrangeme​nt of vein-artery-nerve i​s not seen?","raw_text":"In the costal groove of which of the following ribs is the classic arrangement of vein-artery-nerve is not seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1​1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1​2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f35987eb445e421f98e05e212913e631x1280x1715.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1</strong></p><p>The classic vein-artery-nerve arrangement is not seen in the 1st rib.</p>\n<p>In the first rib, the arrangement of structures seen <strong>medial to lateral</strong> are as follows: <strong>SVAN</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S</strong>ympathetic trunk</li>\n<li><strong>V</strong>ein (intercostal)</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>rtery (superior intercostal)</li>\n<li><strong>N</strong>erve (T1 nerve)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0831","text":"What does the tip of the T​3 spinous process corresp​ond to?","raw_text":"What does the tip of the T3 spinous process correspond to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lower extent of ligamentum​ nuchae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior ang​le of scapula","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Root of the spine of scapu​la","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior ang​le of scapula","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Root of the spine of scapula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Root of the spine of scapula</strong></p><p>The tip of the <strong>T3 spinous process</strong> corresponds to the <strong>root of the spine of the scapula</strong></p>\n<p>Other important surface markings of scapula:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superior angle of scapula: at the level of 2nd rib and vertebra T2.</li>\n<li>Inferior angle of scapula: opposite the T7 vertebral spinous process.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2085","text":"Which among the following ​is incorrect regarding Si​bsons’s fascia?","raw_text":"Which among the following is incorrect regarding Sibsons’s fascia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Attached to the inner bord​er of 2nd rib","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Covers apical part of lung​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Part of scalenus minimus m​uscle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vessels pass above the fas​cia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Attached to the inner border of 2nd rib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/129e066187894e4c9e9d863115911ccdx1280x1352.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Attached to the inner border of 2nd rib</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Sibson’s fascia is attached to the <strong>inner border</strong> of the <strong>1<sup>st</sup> rib</strong>, not the 2<sup>nd</sup> rib.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Sibson's fascia</strong> (suprapleural membrane) serves as a plane of separation between the lower neck and the thorax and covers the apical part of the lung. It is attached to the <strong>inner border </strong>of the <strong>1st rib </strong>and posteriorly to the<strong> anterior border </strong>of the <strong>transverse process </strong>of the <strong>C7</strong> vertebra. It is strengthened by a few spreading muscular fibers derived from the scalene muscles. The subclavian <strong>vessels</strong> lie on <strong>top</strong> of it.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3655","text":"On the ides of March, Juli​us Caesar was stabbed 23 ​times. One such stab woun​d was present in the left​ supraclavicular fossa, p​iercing his left lung. Wh‌ich of the following prov‍ides the vertebral attach​ment to the fascia presen​t here?","raw_text":"On the ides of March, Julius Caesar was stabbed 23 times. One such stab wound was present in the left supraclavicular fossa, piercing his left lung. Which of the following provides the vertebral attachment to the fascia present here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C​2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C​5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C​7","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. C7","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/18d7417bf3a74c1a96e44824c94a2becx1280x1352.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. C7</strong></p><p>The stab injury penetrated the <strong>apex</strong> of the <strong>lung.</strong> It is covered by <strong>Sibson's fascia</strong>, also known as the suprapleural membrane. <strong>Attachment</strong> of Sibson's fascia is at vertebra <strong>C7.</strong></p>\n<p>Sibson's fascia is attached:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anteriorly to the inner border of the first rib</li>\n<li>Posteriorly to the anterior border of the transverse process of the C7 vertebra.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"cDZ1ogHw","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"41_waste_and_sewage_disposal","title":"41.Waste And Sewage Disposal","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB7670","text":"Solid wastes generally sho‍uld not contain:","raw_text":"Solid wastes generally should not contain:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Food ‍wastes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nigh‍tsoil","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Demolitio‍n products","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sewage ‍residue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nightsoil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nightsoil</strong></p><p><strong>Solid wastes</strong> should not contain <strong>nightsoil</strong>, which is <strong>untreated human waste </strong>(feces).</p>\n<p>Solid wastes generally contain:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Garbage (food wastes)</li>\n<li>Rubbish waste (papers, plastics, etc.)</li>\n<li>Demolition products (bricks, masonry, etc.)</li>\n<li>Sewage treatment residue</li>\n<li>Dead animals and other waste materials</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7672","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a method of solid was​te or refuse disposal?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a method of solid waste or refuse disposal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxidati‍on ponds","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Controlle‍d tipping","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Compo‍sting","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Incine‍ration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Oxidation ponds","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Oxidation ponds</strong></p><p><strong>Oxidation ponds</strong> are not used for solid waste or refuse disposal. It is used for<strong> sewage treatment</strong>.</p>\n<p>Methods of <strong>solid waste disposal</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dumping</li>\n<li>Controlled tipping</li>\n<li>Incineration</li>\n<li>Composting</li>\n<li>Manure pits</li>\n<li>Burial</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7673","text":"Which of the following is ‍the most insanitary metho​d of waste disposal?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most insanitary method of waste disposal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dum‍ping","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Controlle‍d tipping","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Manur‍e pits","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Compo‍sting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dumping","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dumping</strong></p><p>The <strong>most insanitary method</strong> of waste disposal is <strong>dumping.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Methods </strong>of <strong>waste disposal</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Controlled tipping or sanitary landfill - Most satisfactory method of refuse disposal where suitable land is available</li>\n<li>Incineration - Method of choice where suitable land is not available</li>\n<li>Composting</li>\n<li>Manure pits</li>\n<li>Burial</li>\n<li>Dumping</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7674","text":"Which method is most appro‍priate when suitable land​ for waste disposal is av‌ailable?","raw_text":"Which method is most appropriate when suitable land for waste disposal is available?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dum‍ping","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Controlle‍d tipping","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Incine‍ration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Manur‍e pits","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Controlled tipping","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Controlled tipping</strong></p><p><strong>Controlled tipping</strong> is the most satisfactory method of refuse disposal when suitable land is available.</p>\n<p>In this method, waste materials are placed in a trench, compacted, and covered with earth at the end of the day. This method is also called a <strong>sanitary landfill</strong>.<strong> </strong>Chemical, bacteriological and physical changes occur in the buried refuse with temperatures rising to over 60 degrees Celsius within 7 days.</p>\n<p>Methods used for controlled tipping are:</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Trench</strong> method</li>\n<li><strong>Ramp </strong>method</li>\n<li><strong>Area </strong>method</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sewage farming</strong> is also a method of sewage disposal in places where sufficient and suitable land is available. It is also called land treatment or <strong>broad irrigation.</strong></p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7452","text":"In controlled tipping, the‍ amount of land required ​for the trench method to ‌be practiced for a popula​tion of 10,000 is:","raw_text":"In controlled tipping, the amount of land required for the trench method to be practiced for a population of 10,000 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 a‍cres","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5 a‍cres","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a‍cres","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 a‍cre","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1 acre","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1 acre</strong></p><p>In controlled tipping,<strong> one acre</strong> of land is required by the trench method to be practiced for a population of 10,000.</p>\n<p>It is also known as a <strong>sanitary landfill</strong>. It is the most satisfactory method of refuse disposal where suitable<strong> land </strong>is<strong> available</strong>. In this method, waste material is placed in a <strong>trench</strong> or other prepared area. Then, it is adequately compacted and<strong> covered with earth</strong> at the end of the day.</p>\n<p><strong>Methods</strong> used in controlled tipping are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Trench</strong> method: Done in places where level ground is available</li>\n<li><strong>Ramp</strong> method: Well suited where the terrain is moderately sloping</li>\n<li><strong>Area</strong> method: Used for filling land depressions, disused quarries, and clay pits</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7679","text":"Which of the following is ‍false about composting?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about composting?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bangalore method is an aer‍obic process","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mechanical composting uses‍ a controlled environment​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nightsoil can also be disp‍osed with composting","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Compost has few or no dise‍ase-causing pathogens","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bangalore method is an aerobic process","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bangalore method is an aerobic process</strong></p><p>The <strong>Bangalore method</strong> of composting is an <strong>anaerobic process.</strong></p>\n<p><strong class=\"ng-binding\">Composting</strong> is a method of combined disposal of <strong>refuse</strong> and <strong>nightsoil.</strong> In this process, organic matter is broken down into compost. It has manure value and few or <strong>no disease-causing pathogens</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Bangalore method</strong> is an anaerobic method and is also called <strong>hot fermentation process</strong>. <strong>Trenches</strong> with a depth of <strong>not more than 90cm</strong> are used for this. This is done by spreading a layer of<strong> </strong>refuse (15 cm thick) at the bottom of the trench and nightsoil (5cm thick) is added next. Layers of refuse and nightsoil are added alternatively till the heap rises to 30cm above ground level. The heap is then covered with <strong>excavated earth</strong>. At the end of 4 – 6 months, decomposition will be completed.</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanical composting </strong>is an <strong>aerobic process. </strong>In this method, compost is manufactured on a <strong>large scale</strong> by processing raw materials and turning out a finished product. The refuse is first cleared of salvageable materials. It is then<strong> pulverised </strong>to reduce the size of particles to <strong>less than 2 inches</strong>. The pulverised refuse is then mixed with sewage, sludge or nightsoil in a rotating machine and incubated. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7685","text":"All of the following are m‍ethods of disposing only ​excreta except:","raw_text":"All of the following are methods of disposing only excreta except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"River ‍outfall","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aqua ‍privy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oxidati‍on ponds","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bangalor‍e method","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bangalore method","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bangalore method</strong></p><p><strong>Bangalore method, </strong>also known as the <strong>hot fermentation process</strong>, is a form of <strong>composting</strong> that is used for disposing of both solid waste and nightsoil (excreta).</p>\n<p><strong class=\"ng-binding\">Methods of excreta disposal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unsewered areas:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Service type latrines</strong> (conservancy system) - In this, nightsoil is collected manually from pail or bucket type latrines and then disposed of by burying or composting</li>\n<li><strong>Non-service type latrines</strong> (sanitary latrines)\n<ul>\n<li>Borehole latrine</li>\n<li>Dug well or pit latrine</li>\n<li>Water-seal latrines – Examples are P.R.A.I. type, R.C.A. type, and Sulabh shauchalaya</li>\n<li>Septic tanks</li>\n<li>Aqua privy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Latrines for <strong>camps and temporary use</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Shallow and deep trench latrine</li>\n<li>Pit latrine</li>\n<li>Borehole latrine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Sewered areas:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Water carriage system and sewage treatment \n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary treatment </strong>– Screening, removal of grit and plain sedimentation</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary treatment </strong>– Trickling filters, activated sludge process</li>\n<li><strong>Other methods</strong> – sea and river outfalls, sewage farming, oxidation ponds</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7686","text":"A military camp is being s‍et up in an area for one ​month. Which of the follo‌wing latrines will not be​ suitable here?","raw_text":"A military camp is being set up in an area for one month. Which of the following latrines will not be suitable here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shallow tre‍nch latrine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pit l‍atrine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Borehole‍ latrine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Septi‍c tank","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Septic tank","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Septic tank</strong></p><p>Septic tanks cannot be used for camps and temporary purposes.</p>\n<p><strong class=\"ng-binding\">Methods of excreta disposal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unsewered areas:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Service type latrines</strong> (conservancy system) - In this, nightsoil is collected manually from pail or bucket type latrines and then disposed of by burying or composting</li>\n<li><strong>Non-service type latrines</strong> (sanitary latrines)\n<ul>\n<li>Borehole latrine</li>\n<li>Dug well or pit latrine</li>\n<li>Water-seal latrines – Examples are P.R.A.I. type, R.C.A. type, and Sulabh shauchalaya</li>\n<li>Septic tanks</li>\n<li>Aqua privy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Latrines for <strong>camps and temporary use</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Shallow and deep trench latrine</li>\n<li>Pit latrine</li>\n<li>Borehole latrine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Sewered areas:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Water carriage system and sewage treatment \n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary treatment </strong>– Screening, removal of grit and plain sedimentation</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary treatment </strong>– Trickling filters, activated sludge process</li>\n<li><strong>Other methods</strong> – sea and river outfalls, sewage farming, oxidation ponds</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7687","text":"Which of the following is ‍the oldest non-service ty​pe of latrines in India?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the oldest non-service type of latrines in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Borehole‍ latrines","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dug well‍ latrines","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Water-sea‍l latrines","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aqua ‍privy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Borehole latrines","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4405b3920a304888a78bb596bd4868b9x600x802.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Borehole latrines</strong></p><p>The oldest non-service type of latrines in India is the <strong>borehole latrine. </strong>It was introduced in the 1930s to combat hookworm infections.</p>\n<p>Special equipment called <strong>auger</strong> is used to dig a borehole latrine.</p>\n<p><strong>Advantages</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>No special staff is needed for upkeep as after using the latrine is closed and another hole is dug elsewhere</li>\n<li>Breeding of flies is less</li>\n<li>No water pollution if located far from a water source</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Demerits</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fills up rapidly</li>\n<li>Augers are not readily available</li>\n<li>Water pollution is still a risk</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7690","text":"There is an outbreak of ho‍okworm infection in your ​locality. On investigatio‌n, it is found that the o​utbreak is a result of op‍en-air defecation. The di‍strict council decides to‌ construct an RCA type of‍ latrine in every househo​ld. What is the optimum w​ater seal required?","raw_text":"There is an outbreak of hookworm infection in your locality. On investigation, it is found that the outbreak is a result of open-air defecation. The district council decides to construct an RCA type of latrine in every household.  What is the optimum water seal required?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5 ‍cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 ‍cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2.5‍ cm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"4 ‍cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2.5 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2.5 cm</strong></p><p>The optimum water seal required for an<strong> RCA type</strong> of latrine is<strong> 2 – 2.5 cm</strong>.</p>\n<p>It was designed by the <strong>Research-cum-Action Projects </strong>(<strong>RCA</strong>) in Environmental Sanitation of the Ministry of Health. Water seals aids in preventing flies and other organisms from reaching the waste. It also prevents the escape of odors and foul gases, eliminating the nuisance from the smell.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 408.545px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 126px; height: 46px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Features of RCA type of latrine</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 126px; height: 46px;\"><strong>Location</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 269.545px; height: 46px;\">More than 15m away from the water source</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 49px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 126px; height: 49px;\"><strong>Squatting plate</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 269.545px; height: 49px;\">Should be impervious and should allow for drainage of water</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 126px; height: 44px;\"><strong>Pan</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 269.545px; height: 44px;\">Receives the nightsoil, urine, and washes water.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 59px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 126px; height: 59px;\"><strong>Trap</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 269.545px; height: 59px;\">Holds the water and provides the <strong>water seal.</strong> A good trap has an effective seal of around 2 – 2.5 cms.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 55px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 126px; height: 55px;\"><strong>Connecting pipe</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 269.545px; height: 55px;\">Present in indirect type and absent in direct type</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 126px; height: 38px;\"><strong>Dug well</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 269.545px; height: 38px;\">Has to be changed after it is filled</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 126px; height: 38px;\"><strong>Superstructure</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 269.545px; height: 38px;\">Built for privacy and shelter</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7696","text":"A health committee formed ‍under Swachh Bharat Missi​on is involved in constru‌cting septic tanks in eve​ry household. Which of th‍e following is true about‍ a septic tank?","raw_text":"A health committee formed under Swachh Bharat Mission is involved in constructing septic tanks in every household. Which of the following is true about a septic tank?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Always doub‍le chambered","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Minimum 200 gallon capacit‍y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Depth i‍s 3-4 m","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retention period is 24 hou‍rs","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Retention period is 24 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Retention period is 24 hours</strong></p><p>The recommended retention period for a septic tank is <strong>24 hours</strong>.</p>\n<p>If the retention period is too short, there is insufficient treatment of sewage and if it is too long, then undue septicity results.</p>\n<p>There are <strong>single and double-chambered</strong> septic tanks. The minimum capacity recommended for a septic tank is <strong>500 gallons</strong>. The depth of a septic tank is usually <strong>1.5 to 2m</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7697","text":"Which of the following is ‍false about the sewage tr​eatment shown in the imag‌e below?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the sewage treatment shown in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Desludging must be done at‍ least once a year","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Useful for very large comm‍unities","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anaerobic digestion occurs‍ inside the tank","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Soap and disinfectants lik‍e phenol should not be us​ed inside the tank","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Useful for very large communities","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2c7367cda02947bbb671096ee0e5a15f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Useful for very large communities</strong></p><p>The given image is of a <strong>septic tank</strong>. It is not useful for very large communities and is used for<strong> household sewage treatment</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7698","text":"What is wastewater without‍ human excreta called?","raw_text":"What is wastewater without human excreta called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sew‍age","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Refuse‍ water","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sul‍lage","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Effl‍uent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sullage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sullage</strong></p><p>Wastewater that doesn’t contain human excreta is called <strong>sullage</strong>. It is also called <strong>greywater. </strong>E.g., waste water from the kitchen.</p>\n<p><strong>Sewage</strong> is wastewater from a community that contains solid and liquid excreta along with street and yard washings. It is also called <strong>blackwater</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7700","text":"What is the average amount‍ of sewage which flows th​ough the sewerage system ‌in 24 hours known as?","raw_text":"What is the average amount of sewage which flows though the sewerage system in 24 hours known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sewag‍e rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dry weat‍her flow","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"RCA ‍index","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sludg‍e rate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dry weather flow","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dry weather flow</strong></p><p>The <strong>average amount</strong> of sewage which flows though the sewerage system in <strong>24 hours</strong> is known as <strong>dry weather flow.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7712","text":"Which of the following is ‍not used to express the s​trength of sewage?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not used to express the strength of sewage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E. col‍i count","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Suspende‍d solids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Biochemical ‍oxygen demand","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chemical ox‍ygen demand","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. E. coli count","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. E. coli count</strong></p><p>E. coli count is not used to express the strength of sewage.</p>\n<p>The<strong> strength of sewage</strong> is expressed only in terms of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Biochemical oxygen demand</li>\n<li>Chemical oxygen demand</li>\n<li>Suspended solids</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7714","text":"What does the biochemical ‍oxygen demand of sewage i​ndicate?","raw_text":"What does the biochemical oxygen demand of sewage indicate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Organic‍ content","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bacteria‍l content","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anaerobic‍ bacteria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Toxic su‍bstances","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Organic content","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Organic content</strong></p><p><strong>Biochemical oxygen demand</strong> (BOD) indicates the <strong>organic content </strong>of sewage.</p>\n<p>It is defined as the amount of oxygen absorbed by a sample of sewage for aerobic destruction by living microorganisms.</p>\n<p>If wastewater contains <strong>toxic substances,</strong> the organic load is measured by the <strong>chemical oxygen demand.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7720","text":"Which of the following val‍ues of biochemical oxygen​ demand would indicate th‌at the sewage is weak?","raw_text":"Which of the following values of biochemical oxygen demand would indicate that the sewage is weak?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"100 ‍mg/l","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"500 ‍mg/l","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"300 ‍mg/l","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"250‍mg/l","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 100 mg/l","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 100 mg/l</strong></p><p><strong>Sewage</strong> with biochemical oxygen demand <strong>(BOD)</strong> of<strong> 100mg/L</strong> would indicate that it is<strong> weak</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 831\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>If the BOD is <strong>≥ 300 mg/L</strong>, sewage is said to be <strong>strong.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7731","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a part of the primary​ treatment of sewage?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a part of the primary treatment of sewage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scre‍ening","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Grit c‍hamber","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aerati‍on tank","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Primary se‍dimentation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aeration tank","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aeration tank</strong></p><p><strong>Aeration tank </strong>is not a part of primary treatment<strong> </strong>of sewage. It is a part of <strong>secondary treatment</strong> of sewage.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7733","text":"What is the function of th‍e grit chamber in modern ​sewage treatment plants?","raw_text":"What is the function of the grit chamber in modern sewage treatment plants?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Formation‍ of sludge","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Removal of large floating ‍objects","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Settlement of heavier part‍icles","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Allow anaerobic auto-diges‍tion of organic matter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Settlement of heavier particles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Settlement of heavier particles</strong></p><p>The function of the <strong>grit chamber</strong> in modern sewage treatment plants is to allow <strong>heavier particles </strong>in the sewage<strong> to settle.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7736","text":"Trickling filter method is‍ used in which of the fol​lowing?","raw_text":"Trickling filter method is used in which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary sewa‍ge treatment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secondary sewage treatment‍","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sewage efflu‍ent disposal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Screening‍ of sewage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Secondary sewage treatment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Secondary sewage treatment</strong></p><p>The trickling filter method is used in <strong>secondary sewage treatment</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7739","text":"Which of the following is ‍the heart of the activate​d sludge process?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the heart of the activated sludge process?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aerati‍on tank","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Primary sedimentation tank‍s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sludge dig‍estion tank","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secondary sedimentation ta‍nks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aeration tank","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aeration tank</strong></p><p>The heart of the activated sludge process is the <strong>aeration tanks.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7743","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a requirement for an ​oxidation pond?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a requirement for an oxidation pond?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sunl‍ight","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Al‍gae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aera‍tion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bact‍eria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aeration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aeration</strong></p><p>Aeration is not a requirement for an oxidation pond.</p>\n<p><strong>Oxidation ponds</strong> are also called <strong>waste stabilization ponds</strong> or <strong>redox ponds</strong> or <strong>sewage lagoons. </strong>They have become a popular method of purifying sewage for small communities. It is usually an open, shallow pool of around 1 – 1.5m depth with an inlet and outlet.</p>\n<p><strong>Requisites</strong> for an oxidation pond include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Algae</li>\n<li>Saprophytic bacteria</li>\n<li>Sunlight<strong> </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>All of the above factors help in the treatment of sewage waste through<strong> aerobic</strong> and <strong>anaerobic</strong> processes. The effluent formed can be used for land irrigation or can be forwarded for further treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"xaOEWCjy","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"41_rheumatoid_arthritis","title":"41.Rheumatoid Arthritis","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB6858","text":"Which of the following is ‌associated with rheumatoi​d arthritis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is associated with rheumatoid arthritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HLA‌ B27","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HLA‌ DR4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"HLA‌ DR3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HLA‌ DR5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. HLA DR4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. HLA DR4</strong></p><p>Rheumatoid arthritis is associated with<strong> HLA DR4.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6895","text":"Which part of the spine is‌ most commonly affected i​n patients with rheumatoi​d arthritis?","raw_text":"Which part of the spine is most commonly affected in patients with rheumatoid arthritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C1 ‌- C2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"C6 ‌- C7","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"L4 ‌- L5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T1 ‌- T2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. C1 - C2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. C1 - C2</strong></p><p>The <strong>atlantoaxial joint</strong> (C1 &amp; C2) is the most commonly affected part of the spine in patients with <strong>rheumatoid arthritis.</strong></p>\n<p>The joints in rheumatoid arthritis are bilaterally and symmetrically involved. The commonly affected joints are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Wrists</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Metatarsophalangeal joints (MTP) joints</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Atlantoaxial joint</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There is relative <strong>sparing of </strong>the<strong> DIP joint</strong> in rheumatoid arthritis. The involvement of DIP joints should direct suspicion towards osteoarthritis<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6883","text":"Identify the following def‌ormity seen in a patient ​complaining of pain and s​welling of her hands.","raw_text":"Identify the following deformity seen in a patient complaining of pain and swelling of her hands.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Swan neck‌ deformity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Boutonnièr‌e deformity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bouchard‌'s nodes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hitchhiker'‌s deformity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Boutonnière deformity","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d41db27579814e5c96499ea3664417d9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Boutonnière deformity</strong></p><p>The image is showing <strong>flexion of the PIP</strong> joint with <strong>hyperextension of the DIP</strong> joint is suggestive of <strong>Boutonnière deformity </strong>seen in patients with <strong>rheumatoid arthritis.</strong></p>\n<p>Heberden’s and Bouchard’s<strong> </strong>nodes are nodules in osteoarthritis that are palpable in the distal interphalangeal joint and proximal interphalangeal joints respectively. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0181","text":"A patient presents to your‌ clinic with the followin​g deformity. In which of ​the following conditions ‌is this deformity seen?","raw_text":"A patient presents to your clinic with the following deformity. In which of the following conditions is this deformity seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteoar‌thritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Go‌ut","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rheumatoid‌ arthritis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Psoriatic‌ arthritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rheumatoid arthritis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d11071926ea94aabbd90a7b98c53a229.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rheumatoid arthritis</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>swan-neck deformity</strong> seen in a patient with established <strong>rheumatoid arthritis</strong>(RA). Swan-neck deformity is hyperextension at the proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joint and flexion at the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joint.</p>\n<p><br/>Some of the other small joint deformities of the hand in a patient with RA include:</p>\n<p><br/><strong>Boutonnière</strong> <strong>deformity</strong> with flexion at the PIP joint and hyperextension at the DIP joint. </p>\n<p><br/><strong>Z-line deformity</strong> with hyperextension at the DIP joint and subluxation of the 1st metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joint<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5296","text":"An elderly woman presents ‌with a chronic history of​ pain in the small joints​ of hands with stiffness ‌of joints in the early ho​urs of the day. The image‌ of the patient’s hands i‌s given below. What is th‌e most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An elderly woman presents with a chronic history of pain in the small joints of hands with stiffness of joints in the early hours of the day. The image of the patient’s hands is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumatoid‌ Arthritis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Osteoar‌thritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Complex Regional Pain Synd‌rome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Villonodula‌r synovitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rheumatoid Arthritis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9ab556c8071442dd931dff04b5748812.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rheumatoid Arthritis</strong></p><p>The above image shows <strong>flexion</strong> at the <strong>PIP</strong> joint and <strong>extension</strong> in the <strong>DIP</strong> joints which is known as <strong>boutonniere deformity</strong> which is seen in <strong>rheumatoid arthritis(RA). </strong></p>\n<p>If the patient gives a history of pain/stiffness in the joints in the early hours of the day which usually decreases as the day progresses suggesting <strong>inflammatory arthritis</strong> and if it presents with predominant involvement of <strong>smaller</strong> joints, it is most likely a case of <strong>RA(option A).</strong></p>\n<p>It occurs due to the accumulation of <strong>inflammatory</strong> <strong>mediators</strong> which on increased joint mobility are flushed out due to increased blood supply. </p>\n<p>Pain and stiffness in <strong>osteoarthritis</strong> (Option B) usually <strong>increase</strong> as the day <strong>progresses</strong> due to an increase in the activity of the joint. They typically present with nodules around the distal interphalangeal joint <strong>(Heberden nodes)</strong> and proximal interphalangeal joint <strong>(Bouchard nodes).  </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4526","text":"What is the most common ex‌tra-articular manifestati​on of rheumatoid arthriti​s?","raw_text":"What is the most common extra-articular manifestation of rheumatoid arthritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subcutane‌ous nodule","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sjogren's‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Felty's ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vascu‌litis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Subcutaneous nodule","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Subcutaneous nodule</strong></p><p>The most common <strong>extra-articular</strong> manifestation of rheumatoid arthritis(RA) is <strong>subcutaneous nodules</strong>.</p>\n<p>The nodules are generally<strong> benign</strong>, <strong>non-tender</strong>, and <strong>firm</strong>, and occur in areas subjected to repeated trauma such as the forearm, sacral prominences, and Achilles tendon. They can also occur in the lungs, pleura, pericardium, and peritoneum. </p>\n<p>Patients who have a history of cigarette smoking had early onset arthritic manifestations and tested <strong>positive</strong> for <strong>RA factor</strong> or <strong>ACPA</strong> are more likely to develop <strong>extra-articular manifestations</strong>. Other common extra-articular manifestations include secondary Sjogren's syndrome, interstitial lung disease, pulmonary nodules, and anemia. FELTY’S syndrome is a triad of neutropenia, splenomegaly, and nodular RA.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6898","text":"Which is the most common c‌ardiac valvular lesion se​en in patients with rheum​atoid arthritis?","raw_text":"Which is the most common cardiac valvular lesion seen in patients with rheumatoid arthritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitral reg‌urgitation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aortic ‌stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aortic reg‌urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mitral ‌stenosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mitral regurgitation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mitral regurgitation</strong></p><p>The most common cardiac valvular lesion seen in patients with rheumatoid arthritis is <strong>mitral regurgitation</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6907","text":"Which of the following wou‌ld you not expect to see ​in a patient diagnosed wi​th Felty syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following would you not expect to see in a patient diagnosed with Felty syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spleno‌megaly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neutr‌openia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepato‌megaly","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rheumatoid‌ arthritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hepatomegaly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hepatomegaly</strong></p><p>Hepatomegaly is not seen in Felty syndrome. </p>\n<p><strong>Felty syndrome</strong> is defined by the clinical <strong>triad</strong> of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neutropenia</li>\n<li>Splenomegaly</li>\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It occurs in the <strong>late stages</strong> of rheumatoid arthritis <strong>(RA).</strong></p>\n<p>Other syndromes associated with rheumatoid arthritis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Caplan’s syndrome-</strong> characterized by both rheumatoid arthritis and <strong>pneumoconiosis</strong>. It is associated with development of multiple peripheral basilar nodules in the lungs. </li>\n<li><strong>Secondary Sjogren’s syndrome-</strong> development of keratoconjunctivitis sicca or xerostomia in association with connective tissue disorder.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1633","text":"Which is the most common t‌ype of lymphoma seen in p​atients with rheumatoid a​rthritis?","raw_text":"Which is the most common type of lymphoma seen in patients with rheumatoid arthritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mantle cel‌l lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hodgkin's‌ lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diffuse large B cell lymph‌oma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Marginal zo‌ne lymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diffuse large B cell lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diffuse large B cell lymphoma</strong></p><p>The most common histopathologic type of lymphoma seen in patients with rheumatoid arthritis is <strong>diffuse large B cell lymphoma </strong>(DLBCL).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2576\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The risk of developing lymphoma increases if the patient has Felty’s syndrome or high levels of disease activity.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6904","text":"A patient complains about ‌difficulty doing chores d​ue to morning stiffness o​f the joints. Investigati‌ons show elevated ESR, CR​P, and positive Rheumatoi‌d factor. Which of the fo‌llowing is not likely to ‌be seen in this condition‍?","raw_text":"A patient complains about difficulty doing chores due to morning stiffness of the joints. Investigations show elevated ESR, CRP, and positive Rheumatoid factor. Which of the following is not likely to be seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperand‌rogenism","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hip fr‌actures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Keratoconjunctivitis sicca‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Congestive h‌eart failure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hyperandrogenism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hyperandrogenism</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>rheumatoid arthritis</strong>, which is associated with <strong>hypoandrogenism. </strong></p>\n<p>It is characterized by low levels of serum testosterone, LH, and DHEA.</p>\n<p>The conditions associated with rheumatoid arthritis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cardiovascular disease, congestive heart failure</li>\n<li>Osteoporosis - hip fractures are more likely to occur</li>\n<li>Ocular - keratoconjunctivitis sicca, episcleritis, scleritis, keratitis</li>\n<li>Secondary Sjogren's syndrome</li>\n<li>Hypoandrogenism in men and postmenopausal women</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7695","text":"All of the following chara‌cteristics are found in t​he pleural effusion fluid​ of a rheumatoid arthriti‌s patient except:","raw_text":"All of the following characteristics are found in the pleural effusion fluid of a rheumatoid arthritis patient except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"RA f‌actor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High g‌lucose","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cholestero‌l crystals","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High‌ LDH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. High glucose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3c76be2af0f04ba88f45ca2174e078fax1280x2296.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. High glucose</strong></p><p><strong>Low glucose</strong> (&lt;60 mg/dL) is seen in the <strong>pleural effusion fluid</strong> of a patient with <strong>rheumatoid arthritis</strong> (RA).</p>\n<p><strong>RA</strong> causes <strong>exudative pleural effusion</strong> and fulfills the Light’s criteria. <strong>High LDH</strong> and <strong>RA factor</strong> are found in the pleural fluid of RA patients. <strong>Less commonly</strong>, patients with RA may have a <strong>cholesterol effusion</strong>. It is <strong>sterile</strong> and has a <strong>milky </strong>or<strong> opaque appearance</strong>. The milky appearance is due to the presence of cholesterol crystals and an elevated cholesterol level.</p>\n<p><strong>Light’s criteria</strong> is used to <strong>distinguish</strong> between<strong> exudative </strong>and<strong> transudative </strong>pleural effusions. An <strong>exudative pleural effusion</strong> satisfies at least <strong>one</strong> of the following <strong>criteria</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pleural fluid protein/serum protein &gt; 0.5</li>\n<li>Pleural fluid LDH / serum LDH &gt; 0.6</li>\n<li>Pleural fluid LDH more than 2/3rds upper limit of normal for serum</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1403","text":"Which of the following rad‌iological features would ​help to differentiate bet​ween rheumatoid arthritis‌ and SLE?","raw_text":"Which of the following radiological features would help to differentiate between rheumatoid arthritis and SLE?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Juxta-articular osteoporos‌is","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ulnar d‌eviation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Articular‌ erosions","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Swelling o‌f PIP joint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Articular erosions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9af726f2c71f4f47847780615b61c65dx462x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4a4967d74a494db59838dd2e542b4890x986x732.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Articular erosions</strong></p><p><strong>Articular erosions</strong> on X-ray is seen in rheumatoid arthritis (<strong>RA</strong>), but not in SLE.</p>\n<p>Juxta-articular osteoporosis, swelling of PIP joint, and ulnar deviation can be seen in both SLE and RA.</p>\n<p><strong>Jaccoud arthropathy</strong> is a deforming <strong>non-erosive</strong> arthropathy characterized by the <strong>ulnar deviation</strong> of the second to 5<sup>th</sup> fingers with metacarpophalangeal joint subluxation seen in <strong>SLE</strong>. </p>\n<p>The give X-ray shows Jaccoud's arthropathy:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The given image shows a patient with Jaccoud's arthropathy of the hands:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1637","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of death in patients ​with rheumatoid arthritis​?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of death in patients with rheumatoid arthritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interstitial‌ lung disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coronary ar‌tery disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Atlantoaxial‌ subluxation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gastrointest‌inal bleeding","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Coronary artery disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Coronary artery disease</strong></p><p>The most common cause of death in patients with rheumatoid arthritis is <strong>cardiovascular disease.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6910","text":"In a suspected case of rhe‌umatoid arthritis, which ​of the following markers ​should be used to diagnos‌e this condition?","raw_text":"In a suspected case of rheumatoid arthritis, which of the following markers should be used to diagnose this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti-nuclea‌r antibodies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rheumato‌id factor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti- CCP‌ antibody","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anti dsDN‌A antibody","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anti- CCP antibody","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anti- CCP antibody</strong></p><p>The most appropriate marker to diagnose rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is the <strong>anti-CCP </strong>(cyclic citrullinated peptide)<strong> antibody </strong>as it is the most <strong>specific</strong> marker for RA.</p>\n<p>Anti-CCP, ESR, and CRP values correlate with the disease activity.</p>\n<p>Rheumatoid factor can occur in other autoimmune diseases as well as in chronic infections. Therefore it is not considered specific.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6911","text":"Which of the following is ‌the drug of choice for th​e treatment of a patient ​with rheumatoid arthritis‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the drug of choice for the treatment of a patient with rheumatoid arthritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydroxych‌loroquine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sulfas‌alazine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Methot‌rexate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Infli‌ximab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Methotrexate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Methotrexate</strong></p><p><strong>Methotrexate</strong> which is a DMARD is the<strong> drug of choice</strong> for the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis (RA).</p>\n<p>A combination regimen is used when there is a failure to achieve adequate improvement with methotrexate only. </p>\n<p>Methotrexate in low doses causes adenosine release and <strong>suppresses joint inflammation.</strong> However, in high doses, it causes inhibition of dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7585","text":"A patient with rheumatoid ‌arthritis is being treate​d with methotrexate and l​ow-dose corticosteroids f‌or the past 4 months. How​ever, the disease is stil‌l progressing. What would‌ be your recommendation f‌or the further management‍ of this patient?","raw_text":"A patient with rheumatoid arthritis is being treated with methotrexate and low-dose corticosteroids for the past 4 months. However, the disease is still progressing. What would be your recommendation for the further management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stop oral methotrexate and‌ start parenteral methotr​exate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Add sulfasalazine and hydr‌oxychloroquine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Continue corticosteroids a‌nd methotrexate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Start only anti-TNF alpha ‌agents","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Add sulfasalazine and hydroxychloroquine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Add sulfasalazine and hydroxychloroquine</strong></p><p>For further management of a patient with <strong>rheumatoid arthritis</strong> (RA) with <strong>worsening disease</strong> activity, the preferred therapy is a step-up to <strong>oral triple therapy</strong> with <strong>methotrexate</strong>, <strong>sulfasalazine</strong>, and <strong>hydroxychloroquine</strong>. The addition of leflunomide or a biological (anti - TNF) to methotrexate is another alternative.</p>\n<p>Ideally, the approach to a patient with early RA is aggressive. <strong>Methotrexate</strong> is always the <strong>first DMARD</strong> that the patient is started on, and if an <strong>adequate response</strong> is <strong>not</strong> <strong>observed</strong> within <strong>3-6 months</strong>, a step up in therapy is made by <strong>adding on drugs</strong>. </p>\n<p>For <strong>assessment of progression</strong>, composite indices like <strong>Disease Activity Score</strong> <strong>- 28</strong> (DAS-28) can used. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: Changing the route of administration of methotrexate from oral to parenteral is not a part of step up </p>\n<p>Option C: Priority is given to minimising the duration the patient is on steroids.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Anti -TNF agents</strong> (adalimumab, etanercept) are <strong>not recommended</strong> as a <strong>monotherapy</strong>, and are reserved for treatment in <strong>combination with DMARDs</strong>. This is due to the fact that the <strong>efficacy of monotherapy</strong> is <strong>lesser than</strong> that of <strong>DMARDs</strong>, and the adverse effects include increased risk of serious infections, re-activation of latent TB and opportunistic fungal infections. They are reserved for cases where there is a lack of response with the first line agents. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6914","text":"Which of the following is ‌a Janus kinase inhibitor ​used in patients with rhe​umatoid arthritis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a Janus kinase inhibitor used in patients with rheumatoid arthritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anak‌inra","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tocil‌izumab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abat‌acept","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tofac‌itinib","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tofacitinib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tofacitinib</strong></p><p>The Janus kinase inhibitor used in the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis is <strong>tofacitinib.</strong></p>\n<p>Tofacitinib, baricitinib, and upadacitinib are Janus kinase inhibitors used as monotherapy or combination therapy in rheumatoid arthritis.</p>\n<p>Option A: Anakinra is an IL-1 antagonist.</p>\n<p>Option B: Tocilizumab is an IL-6 antagonist.</p>\n<p>Option C: Abatacept acts by inhibiting co-stimulation of T cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6916","text":"A 28-year-old pregnant lad‌y visits your OPD with co​mplaints of painful swell​ing of her hands since pa‌st 2 months. Investigatio​ns reveal positive rheuma‌toid factor and anti-CCP ‌antibodies. Which of the ‌following DMARDs should n‍ot be prescribed to her?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old pregnant lady visits your OPD with complaints of painful swelling of her hands since past 2 months. Investigations reveal positive rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP antibodies. Which of the following DMARDs should not be prescribed to her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ritu‌ximab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leflu‌nomide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hydroxych‌loroquine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sulfas‌alazine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Leflunomide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Leflunomide</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of rheumatoid arthritis (<strong>RA</strong>). <strong>Leflunomide</strong> should not be prescribed in pregnancy as it is a <strong>category X</strong> drug.</p>\n<p>DMARDs classified as Category X in pregnancy (contraindicated in pregnancy) are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Methotrexate </li>\n<li>Leflunomide</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1406","text":"What is the isotype of the‌ rheumatoid factor most c​ommonly measured in the l​aboratory?","raw_text":"What is the isotype of the rheumatoid factor most commonly measured in the laboratory?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ig‌ M","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ig‌ G","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ig‌ A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ig‌ D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ig M","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ig M</strong></p><p><strong>IgM</strong> isotype is the rheumatoid factor (RF) most commonly measured in laboratories.</p>\n<p>IgM RF has been found in 75–80% of patients with rheumatoid arthritis. IgG and IgA isotypes of RF have also been identified.</p>\n<p>However, RF is not specific for rheumatoid arthritis and can also be found in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary Sjögren’s syndrome</li>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li>Subacute bacterial endocarditis</li>\n<li>Hepatitis B and C</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2581\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6828","text":"Iritis is seen in all exce‌pt:","raw_text":"Iritis is seen in all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"S‌LE","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Behcet's‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ulcerativ‌e colitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rheumatoid‌ arthritis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rheumatoid arthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rheumatoid arthritis</strong></p><p>Iritis is seen in all the above conditions except rheumatoid arthritis.</p>\n<p><strong>Iritis</strong> or iridocyclitis is also known as <strong>anterior uveitis. </strong></p>\n<p>Anterior uveitis is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ankylosing spondylitis</li>\n<li>Juvenile rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li>Psoriasis</li>\n<li>Reactive arthritis</li>\n<li><strong>Systemic lupus erythematosus</strong></li>\n<li>Leprosy</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Posterior uveitis </strong>is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vogt-Koyanagi Harada syndrome</li>\n<li>Diseases like toxoplasmosis, cysticercosis, coccidioidomycosis, toxocariasis, histoplasmosis</li>\n<li>Infections- Candida, Pneumocystis carinii, Cryptococcus, Aspergillus, and Cytomegalovirus</li>\n<li>Cat-scratch disease, Whipple’s disease, and brucellosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Both</strong> anterior and posterior uveitis is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sarcoidosis</li>\n<li>Behcet's disease</li>\n<li>Inflammatory bowel disease</li>\n<li>Herpes infections, Syphilis, Lyme disease, TB, onchocerciasis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Intermediate uveitis/pars planitis</strong> is seen in multiple sclerosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"SHMV5i8T","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"41_infertility","title":"41.Infertility","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB1330","text":"What is the accepted minim‌um period of sexual cohab‍itation required to estab‍lish the diagnosis of inf​ertility in a couple?","raw_text":"What is the accepted minimum period of sexual cohabitation required to establish the diagnosis of infertility in a couple?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"One ‌year","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Two ‌years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Six m‌onths","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nine ‌months","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. One year","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. One year</strong></p><p>The accepted <strong>minimum</strong> period of <strong>sexual cohabitation</strong> to establish the diagnosis of infertility in a couple is <strong>one year</strong> of <strong>regular, unprotected </strong>sexual intercourse.</p>\n<p><strong>Infertility</strong> is an <strong>apparent failure</strong> of a couple to conceive while <strong>sterility </strong>is an <strong>absolute inability</strong> to conceive. Infertility is of two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary infertility - when there have been <strong>no previous </strong>pregnancies.</li>\n<li>Secondary infertility - when there has been a <strong>prior pregnancy</strong> (although not necessarily a live birth).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1361","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of male infertility?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of male infertility?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disorders of spermatogenes‌is","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Obstruction of the efferen‌t ducts","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Disorders of sperm motilit‌y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Erectile d‌ysfunction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Disorders of spermatogenesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Disorders of spermatogenesis</strong></p><p>The most common cause of male infertility is <strong>disorders </strong>of<strong> spermatogenesis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Male factor is the cause of infertility in about 20% of infertile couples. However, it is a contributing factor for infertility in 50% of cases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1360","text":"The secretion of FSH in me‌n is inhibited by the neg‍ative feedback effect of ‍_______.","raw_text":"The secretion of FSH in men is inhibited by the negative feedback effect of _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibin secreted by Sertol‌i cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibin secreted by Leydig‌ cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Testosterone secreted by S‌ertoli cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Androstenedione secreted b‌y Leydig cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Inhibin secreted by Sertoli cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0d9a0ab366124669b2425ed37294fdc6x265x450.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Inhibin secreted by Sertoli cells</strong></p><p>The secretion of <strong>FSH</strong> in men is <strong>inhibited</strong> by the negative feedback effect of<strong> inhibin</strong> secreted by <strong>Sertoli cells</strong>.</p>\n<p>GnRH stimulates the anterior pituitary gland to secrete FSH (follicle-stimulating hormone) and LH (luteinizing hormone). FSH acts on Sertoli cells while<strong> LH triggers testosterone</strong> secretion by the <strong>Leydig cells</strong>. Sertoli cells secrete <strong>inhibin B</strong> which in turn inhibits FSH but <strong>stimulate LH</strong> secretion.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9315","text":"A 25-year-old man underwen‌t semen analysis as a par‍t of an infertility worku‍p. The results are given ​below. What is the most l​ikely diagnosis? Sperm co​ncentration - 16 million/‌ml pH - 7.5 Volume - 2 mL​ Agglutination is absent ‍Morphology - 60% normal a​nd 60% motile sperms","raw_text":"<p>A 25-year-old man underwent semen analysis as a part of an infertility workup. The results are given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sperm concentration - 16 million/ml</li>\n<li>pH - 7.5</li>\n<li>Volume - 2 mL</li>\n<li>Agglutination is absent</li>\n<li>Morphology - 60% normal and 60% motile sperms</li>\n</ul>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oligos‌permia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Normos‌permia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Astheno‌spermia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Terato‌spermia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Normospermia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Normospermia</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, the most likely diagnosis is <strong>normospermia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Different types of <strong>disorders of sperms</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aspermia</strong> is defined as the <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>semen</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Oligospermia</strong> is defined as a sperm concentration <strong>&lt;15 million/mL</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Severe oligospermia</strong> is defined as a sperm concentration of <strong>&lt;5 million/mL</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Teratospermia</strong> is defined as the presence of <strong>abnormal sperm morphology</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Asthenospermia</strong> is defined as the <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>motile sperms</strong> or diminished sperm motility.</li>\n<li><strong>Necrospermia</strong> is defined as the presence of <strong>dead sperms</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Morphology &gt; motility is the most important marker in sperm analysis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1380","text":"While going through a pati‌ent's file at an infertil‍ity clinic, you note his ‍semen analysis is reporte​d to be normal. What had ​his semen leukocyte level​ likely been?","raw_text":"While going through a patient's file at an infertility clinic, you note his semen analysis is reported to be normal.  What had his semen leukocyte level likely been?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.5 mil‌lion/ml","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 mill‌ion/ml","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2 mill‌ion/ml","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tra‌ces","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1 million/ml","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1 million/ml</strong></p><p>The <strong>normal leucocyte</strong> level in human semen is approximately <strong>1 million/ml</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>semen</strong> sample should be <strong>collected after 2-7 days</strong> of ejaculatory <strong>abstinence</strong>. The patient should collect the sample <strong>by</strong> <strong>masturbation</strong> at the doctor's office. If not possible, then the sample may be collected at home and <strong>delivered</strong> to the laboratory <strong>within an hour</strong> of collection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1384","text":"What does azoospermia refe‌r to?","raw_text":"What does azoospermia refer to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No sperm‌ in semen","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"No s‌emen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low spe‌rm count","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abnormal motility of sperm‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. No sperm in semen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. No sperm in semen</strong></p><p><strong>Azoospermia</strong> is defined as the complete <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>sperm</strong> in semen. Other types of sperm disorders are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aspermia</strong> is defined as the <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>semen</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Oligospermia</strong> is defined as a sperm concentration <strong>&lt;15 million/mL</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Severe oligospermia</strong> is defined as a sperm concentration of <strong>&lt;5 million/mL</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Teratospermia</strong> is defined as the presence of <strong>abnormal sperm morphology</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Asthenospermia</strong> is defined as the <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>motile sperms</strong> or diminished sperm motility.</li>\n<li><strong>Necrospermia</strong> is defined as the presence of <strong>dead sperms</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6945","text":"A young couple being treat‌ed for infertility requir‍e donor semen for artific‍ial insemination. Which o​f the following is the li​kely cause of infertility​ here?","raw_text":"A young couple being treated for infertility require donor semen for artificial insemination. Which of the following is the likely cause of infertility here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oligos‌permia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Azoos‌permia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Impo‌tence","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Presence of antisperm anti‌bodies in the cervical mu‍cucs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Azoospermia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Azoospermia</strong></p><p><strong>Artificial insemination</strong> with <strong>donor semen</strong> is done in cases of infertility where the husband has <strong>azoospermia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Azoospermia is defined as a complete<strong> absence</strong> of <strong>sperms.</strong> Hence artificial insemination with the husband's/partner's semen cannot be performed.</p>\n<p><strong>Indications</strong> for<strong> artificial insemination</strong> with <strong>husband's/partner's semen</strong> are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Chronic medical disorder</li>\n<li>Oligospermia after sperm washout</li>\n<li>Impotence</li>\n<li>Premature and retrograde ejaculation</li>\n<li>Hypospadias</li>\n<li>Antisperm antibodies in cervical mucus</li>\n<li>Unexplained infertility- direct intraperitoneal insemination (DIPI) is done</li>\n<li>HIV-positive male or female</li>\n<li>X-Y fractionation of sperms for sex selection in cases of genetic and chromosomal abnormalities</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Indications </strong>for <strong>artificial insemination with donor</strong> semen are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Azoospermia</strong></li>\n<li>Uncorrectable immunological factors</li>\n<li>Genetic disease in husband/partner</li>\n<li>Chronic ill health</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Sperms can be frozen and used later for artificial insemination e.g. if the husband requires to undergo radiotherapy or chemotherapy, or if the husband is a frequent traveler and is not available at the time of ovulation.</p>\n<p><strong>Artificial insemination techniques</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intrauterine Insemination (IUI)</li>\n<li>Intrafallopian-tube Insemination</li>\n<li>Intracervical Insemination</li>\n<li>Direct Intraperitoneal Insemination (DIPI)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Steps</strong> required to do <strong>artificial insemination</strong> are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The <strong>semen</strong> is <strong>washed</strong> to remove abnormal sperms, debris, and seminal plasma containing antibodies. It is <strong>concentrated</strong>, and its quality is improved by the '<strong>swim up technique</strong>' or by the use of the <strong>Percoll gradient</strong>.</li>\n<li>Semen with normal sperms and good motility is obtained.</li>\n<li>Around the <strong>time of ovulation</strong>, semen obtained is <strong>inseminated</strong> into the female genital tract. About <strong>0.5 ml</strong> of semen is injected <strong>36 hours after hCG</strong> injection when the ovarian <strong>follicle reaches 20 mm</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1392","text":"Indications for artificial‌ insemination with donor'‍s semen are all except___‍___.","raw_text":"Indications for artificial insemination with donor's semen are all except______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Azoos‌permia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Uncorrectable immunologica‌l factors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Male with hereditary disea‌se","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Idiopathic male infertilit‌y","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Idiopathic male infertility","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Idiopathic male infertility</strong></p><p>Artificial insemination with donor's semen is not indicated in <strong>idiopathic male infertility</strong>. The preferred treatment in such cases is i<strong>n vitro fertilization (IVF)</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1391","text":"A 50-year-old man with ere‌ctile dysfunction require‍s treatment with sildenaf‍il. Which of the followin​g is not a contraindicati​on for the use of this dr​ug?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man with erectile dysfunction requires treatment with sildenafil. Which of the following is not a contraindication for the use of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retinitis ‌pigmentosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetic r‌etinopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Concurrent nitrate therapy‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary h‌ypertension","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pulmonary hypertension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pulmonary hypertension</strong></p><p><strong>Pulmonary hypertension </strong>is an <strong>indication</strong> for the use of <strong>sildenafil</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>contraindications</strong> for the use of sildenafil are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Retinitis pigmentosa</li>\n<li>Diabetic retinopathy</li>\n<li>Patient on antihypertensive drugs, nitrates</li>\n<li>Cardiac disease, previous myocardial infarct, stroke</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the treatment of erectile dysfunction,<strong> sildenafil</strong> in the dose of <strong>25–100 mg</strong> is given <strong>1 hour before intercourse.</strong> However, it does not improve libido.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1393","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of infertility in wom‍en?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of infertility in women?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ovarian‌ causes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Uterine‌ causes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tubal ‌causes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cervica‌l causes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ovarian causes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ovarian causes</strong></p><p>The most common cause for <strong>infertility</strong> in women is due to <strong>ovarian causes.</strong></p>\n<p>According to WHO, female factor infertility was reported in 37% of infertile couples in developed countries. Male factor infertility was reported in 8% of infertile couples while 35% of couples had both male and female factor infertility.</p>\n<p>The <strong>major ovarian causes</strong> of infertility are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Anovulation (PCOS)</li>\n<li>Decreased ovarian reserve, e.g., premature menopause</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In women with <strong>ovulatory disorders</strong>, <strong>clomifene</strong> and <strong>metformin</strong> increase ovulation and pregnancy rates.</p>\n<p><strong>Less common factors</strong> responsible for female infertility are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Endometriosis</li>\n<li>Pelvic adhesions</li>\n<li>Tubal blockage</li>\n<li>Other tubal abnormalities</li>\n<li>Hyperprolactinemia</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6947","text":"A 34-year-old woman presen‌ted with secondary infert‍ility following radiation‍ therapy for Hodgkin's ly​mphoma. You plan to test ​her ovarian reserve. Whic​h of the following is the‌ best indicator of this p​arameter?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old woman presented with secondary infertility following radiation therapy for Hodgkin's lymphoma. You plan to test her ovarian reserve. Which of the following is the best indicator of this parameter?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"LH l‌evel","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"LH/FSH‌ ratio","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"FSH ‌level","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Oestradi‌ol level","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. FSH level","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. FSH level</strong></p><p>Among the given options, the level of<strong> follicular stimulating hormone </strong>(FSH) is the best indicator of <strong>ovarian reserve.</strong></p>\n<p>Ovarian reserve is the capacity of the ovary to produce eggs. FSH levels are measured in the <strong>early follicular phase </strong>on day 2 or 3 of the menstrual cycle.</p>\n<p>A high FSH level indicates that the ovarian reserve is getting depleted and fewer oocytes are present in the ovary. <strong>FSH</strong> is <strong>high </strong>in <strong>ovarian hypofunction</strong> and <strong>postmenopausal</strong> women.</p>\n<p><strong>Anti-Mullerian hormone </strong>(AMH) is a <strong>better marker</strong> than FSH for ovarian reserve. Unlike FSH, its levels can be estimated at any time of the cycle. AMH is produced by the granulosa cells in ovarian follicles.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Note: Studies show that AMH levels decrease under the current use of oral contraceptive pills and tobacco smoking.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7530","text":"What will be the level of ‌the anti-Mullerian hormon‍e in low ovarian reserve?‍","raw_text":"What will be the level of the anti-Mullerian hormone in low ovarian reserve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;l‌t;1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1‌-4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;g‌t;7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt‌;10","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. <1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. &lt;1</strong></p><p>In<strong> low ovarian reserve,</strong> the level of<strong> anti-Mullerian hormone</strong> (AMH)<strong> </strong>will be<strong> &lt;1 ng/ml. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>AMH</strong> is a glycoprotein secreted by the granulosa cells of the primordial follicle of the ovary. It helps in oocyte maturation and mono-follicular development. AMH is also used in assessing ovarian reserve.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 168px; width: 251.165px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 36px;\"><strong>AMH levels in ng/ml</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.165px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Interpretation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 18px;\">2–6.8</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.165px; height: 18px;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 36px;\">&lt;1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.165px; height: 36px;\">Poor ovarian reserve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 63.2973px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 63.2973px;\">&gt;10</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.165px; height: 63.2973px;\">\n<p>PCOD</p>\n<p>Hyperstimulation syndrome</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>In<strong> women</strong>, AMH is always absent during embryogenesis and is <strong>secreted by</strong> the <strong>granulosa cells</strong> after puberty. It helps in follicular development and oocyte maturation. <strong>Estimation of</strong> serum AMH is used in the study of<strong> ovarian reserve</strong> in female infertility and secondary amenorrhoea.</p>\n<p>In <strong>men</strong>, AMH starts to be <strong>secreted by</strong> the<strong> Sertoli cells</strong> in the testes by the 7th week of intrauterine life. It continues until puberty. It inhibits the development of the Müllerian system. The <strong>absence</strong> of AMH results in the development of <strong>hermaphroditism</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1398","text":"In a woman diagnosed with ‌infertility, which of the‍ following antihormonal s‍ubstance can be used to i​nduce ovulation?","raw_text":"In a woman diagnosed with infertility, which of the following antihormonal substance can be used to induce ovulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mifepr‌istone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clomiphen‌e citrate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tamo‌xifen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dan‌azol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Clomiphene citrate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Clomiphene citrate</strong></p><p><strong>Clomiphene citrate</strong> is an antihormonal substance used to <strong>induce ovulation</strong> in a patient diagnosed with infertility. Ovulation can be induced by giving clomiphene citrate<strong> 50 mg daily</strong>, starting<strong> from day 2 to day 6</strong> of the menstrual cycle for 5 days.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0622","text":"A woman with irregular per‌iods was evaluated for in‍fertility, and underwent ‍an endometrial biopsy. Th​e histology image is give​n below. Which phase is s​een here?","raw_text":"A woman with irregular periods was evaluated for infertility, and underwent an endometrial biopsy. The histology image is given below. Which phase is seen here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Secreto‌ry phase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prolifera‌tive phase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Menstru‌al phase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ovulati‌on phase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Proliferative phase","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/420f42c729784a1696b9a05d70fd68bf.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/58ac5c263e1d4f198bbd254a47835df8x400x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/61ff20584c2e42ada4af6f4653d56b22x1279x963.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Proliferative phase</strong></p><p>The image depicts the<strong> proliferative phase</strong> of the endometrium. </p>\n<p>During menstruation, the <strong>endometrium</strong> goes through three phases, namely; <strong>proliferative, secretory,</strong> and <strong>menstrual phases</strong>. Their histological features are given below:</p>\n<p><strong>Proliferative phase</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The glands of the functional layer are simple tubules with regular epithelium.</li>\n<li>Arteries become more coiled and spiralled.</li>\n<li>The endometrium is thick, the stoma is compact and short straight glands are present.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows proliferative endometrium</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Secretory phase </strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 43\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>The secretory phase of the endometrium begins on the 15th day and persists until the onset of menstruation.</li>\n<li>Subnuclear vacuolation (presumptive evidence of progesterone activity)</li>\n<li>Corkscrew-shaped glands</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The image below shows secretory endometrium</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Menstrual phase: </strong>Glands shrink with prominent vessels and haemorrhagic spots. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1397","text":"The microscopic appearance‌ of the cervical mucus of‍ a woman having irregular‍ periods and infertility ​is shown in the image bel​ow. In which phase of the​ menstrual cycle was this‌ sample likely taken?","raw_text":"The microscopic appearance of the cervical mucus of a woman having irregular periods and infertility is shown in the image below. In which phase of the menstrual cycle was this sample likely taken?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estrogen‌ic phase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Progestog‌enic phase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"At the time ‌of ovulation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secreto‌ry phase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Estrogenic phase","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bf1bf4c3fd8942f0b31ea6d513e98e9f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/49bd19dc38ab4bb0b8f03d5f2f6dea4ax1279x928.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Estrogenic phase</strong></p><p>The image shows the characteristic <strong>ferning pattern </strong>of cervical mucus<strong> </strong>which is seen in the<strong> estrogenic phase</strong> of the menstrual cycle.</p>\n<p>A specimen of cervical mucus is obtained using a platinum loop or pipette. This is spread on a clean glass slide and allowed to dry. When viewed under the low-power microscope, a palm leaf or fern type pattern is seen in the follicular phase.<strong> </strong>The ferning is <strong>due to</strong> the presence of<strong> sodium chloride</strong> in the mucus secreted under the effect of estrogen.</p>\n<p>This<strong> </strong>pattern<strong> disappears</strong> after <strong>ovulation</strong>. If it was previously present, its disappearance is <strong>presumptive evidence</strong> of <strong>corpus luteum activity</strong>. Under the influence of <strong>progesterone</strong>, the <strong>cervical mucus</strong> becomes <strong>thick</strong> and <strong>tenacious</strong> and impenetrable to sperms and bacteria.</p>\n<p>The below image shows the microscopic appearance of dried cervical mucus showing the fern appearance.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1390","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about th‍e Miller-Kurzrok test?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about the Miller-Kurzrok test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penetration of &amp;gt; 3 cm a‌t 30 minutes is considere‍d normal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cervical mucus is placed a‌longside husband's semen ‍on a glass slide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is done at the time of ‌ovulation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Jerky motility of sperm is‌ considered normal","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Jerky motility of sperm is considered normal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Jerky motility of sperm is considered normal</strong></p><p>In the Miller–Kurzrok test, sperm acquiring<strong> jerky motility</strong> after penetrating cervical mucus is considered<strong> abnormal</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Miller-Kurzrok test</strong> is a special test done in semen analysis to <strong>diagnose</strong> the <strong>cause of infertility</strong>. It is an in vitro test of sperm–mucus interaction.</p>\n<p>It is based on the basic principle that a normal cervical mucus permits invasion by motile sperms. It is <strong>done at</strong> the time of <strong>ovulation</strong>. The test consists of placing <strong>cervical mucus</strong> on a glass slide <strong>alongside</strong> the specimen of the husband's <strong>semen</strong> and studying the penetration of sperms under the microscope. The<strong> penetration</strong> of sperm <strong>&gt; 3 cm</strong> into the mucus at <strong>30 minutes</strong> is considered <strong>normal</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other special tests in semen analysis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sperm penetration test</li>\n<li>Semen–cervical mucus contact test</li>\n<li>Postcoital test (Sims’ or Huhner’s test)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9314","text":"A post-coital test was don‌e in a couple who present‍ed with an inability to c‍onceive despite staying t​ogether for 4 years and h​aving unprotected interco​urse. Which of the follow‌ing conditions cannot be ​detected by this test?","raw_text":"A post-coital test was done in a couple who presented with an inability to conceive despite staying together for 4 years and having unprotected intercourse. Which of the following conditions cannot be detected by this test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fallopian ‌tube block","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cervical factor abnormalit‌y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abnormal s‌perm count","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abnormal spe‌rm morphology","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fallopian tube block","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fallopian tube block</strong></p><p>The <strong>post-coital test</strong> can not detect a fallopian tube block. It <strong>can evaluate</strong> the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sperm count</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Sperm morphology</strong></li>\n<li>Ferning during the estrogenic effect</li>\n<li>Ovulation</li>\n<li>The ability of the cervical mucous to allow sperm penetration</li>\n<li>Detection of <strong>anti-sperm antibodies</strong> in the cervical mucous</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the<strong> post-coital test</strong>, the couple is advised to have intercourse<strong> early </strong>in the<strong> morning</strong> close to the <strong>ovulation</strong> phase. The woman is asked to visit the clinic <strong>within 2 hours of having intercourse</strong>. The mucus is aspirated from the cervical canal and spread over a glass slide. Another smear made from the posterior fornix serves as a control.</p>\n<p>On microscopy, <strong>10–50 motile sperms</strong> are <strong>normally</strong> seen per high-power field. The <strong>cervical mucus</strong> is also examined for its <strong>quantity </strong>and viscosity. The <strong>post-coital</strong> test is <strong>useless</strong> in the presence of cervical infection.</p>\n<p>An immunological factor causing infertility is encountered in around 5% of infertile couples.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1395","text":"During your posting in the‌ infertility clinic, you ‍notice the following inst‍rument. Identify it.","raw_text":"During your posting in the infertility clinic, you notice the following instrument. Identify it.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Karman ‌cannula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spackman‌ cannula","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Menstrual regulation syrin‌ge","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rubin's‌ cannula","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rubin's cannula","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c7fcb836098047a28a7971410f7123a8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rubin&#x27;s cannula</strong></p><p>The instrument in the given image is <strong>Rubin's cannula.</strong></p>\n<p>It is used while performing a <strong>hysterosalpingogram </strong>(HSG). The cone prevents the retrograde spillage of the dye into the vagina, while the dye is instilled into the uterine cavity. This investigation is performed to<strong> study tubal patency</strong> in women.</p>\n<p>Another cannula that can be used to perform HSG is Spackman cannula. It allows for the intra-operative manipulation of both anteverted and retroverted uterus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1394","text":"A patient with secondary a‌menorrhea and infertility‍ is diagnosed to have Ash‍erman syndrome. Hysterosc​opic evaluation revealed ​30% adhesions of the endo​metrial surfaces, but no ‌agglutination of the uter​ine wall. How would you c‍lassify this patient?","raw_text":"A patient with secondary amenorrhea and infertility is diagnosed to have Asherman syndrome. Hysteroscopic evaluation revealed 30% adhesions of the endometrial surfaces, but no agglutination of the uterine wall. How would you classify this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mild Asherm‌an syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Moderate Asherman syndrome‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Severe Asher‌man syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cannot be classified with ‌the given information","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Moderate Asherman syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Moderate Asherman syndrome</strong></p><p>This patient would be classified as a case of <strong>moderate Asherman syndrome</strong> as <strong>30% adhesions</strong> of the endometrial surfaces are present.</p>\n<p><strong>Asherman syndrome</strong> (uterine synechiae) is <strong>classified</strong> as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Minimal: &lt;25% adhesion involves the uterine cavity, flimsy adhesions involving the fundus and tubal ostia.</li>\n<li>Moderate: 25–70% adhesion of the endometrial surfaces, but no agglutination of the uterine wall.</li>\n<li>Severe: 75% adhesions with agglutination and thick adhesions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Asherman syndrome</strong> can <strong>present</strong> in the following ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Secondary amenorrhea</strong></li>\n<li>Oligomenorrhoea</li>\n<li>Dysmenorrhoea</li>\n<li>Habitual abortions</li>\n<li><strong>Infertility</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>causes</strong> of <strong>Asherman syndrome</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dilatation and curettage (D&amp;C)</li>\n<li>Medical termination of pregnancy (MTP), spontaneous abortions, recurrent abortions</li>\n<li>Uterine packing in postpartum haemorrhage</li>\n<li>Uterine infection</li>\n<li>Tubercular endometritis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6933","text":"A 30-year-old patient with‌ complaints of inability ‍to conceive underwent the‍ following investigation.​ Which of the following i​s false about the same?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old patient with complaints of inability to conceive underwent the following investigation. Which of the following is false about the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The best time to perform i‌t is before ovulation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is perfomed to detect u‌terine anomalies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is the first-line inves‌tigation in genital tuber‍culosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Premedication can be done ‌with atropine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is the first-line investigation in genital tuberculosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d1a6bc4398a948f688615edb205bc66a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is the first-line investigation in genital tuberculosis</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>hysterosalpingogram (HSG).</strong> HSG is <strong>contraindicated </strong>in <strong>g</strong><strong>enital tuberculosis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Procedure of HSG:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is best performed in the period between the last day of menses to <strong>ovulation,</strong> usually on the 9th/10th day of the cycle.</li>\n<li>10-15ml radioopaque dye is injected into the uterine cavity via <strong>Leech-Wilkinson</strong> cannula,<strong> Rubin's</strong> cannula, or<strong> Foley's catheter</strong>.</li>\n<li>Fluoroscopy is performed as the dye passes through the uterine cavity and fallopian tubes. </li>\n<li>If the tubes are patent, the dye will spill out of the tubal ostia into the peritoneum, and smear the adjacent bowel.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is used for the<strong> detection of:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tubal block - It can be due to:\n<ul>\n<li>Strictures - fibrotic block</li>\n<li>Inspissated amorphous material</li>\n<li>Tubal <strong>spasm</strong> - This can occur in apprehensive patients. It is <strong>prevented</strong> by premedication with <strong>atropine </strong>and analgesia.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Uterine anomalies</li>\n<li>Uterine polyps</li>\n<li>Uterine synechiae</li>\n<li>The feasibility of tuboplasty, by studying the location and extent of tubal pathology.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contraindications:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Genital tract infections, such as genital tuberculosis. This is due to the risk of spreading infection.</li>\n<li>Bleeding</li>\n<li>Suspected pregnancy</li>\n<li>Allergy to the dye</li>\n<li>Premenstrual phase:\n<ul>\n<li>If by chance pregnancy has occurred, it may be dislodged.</li>\n<li>Thick endometrium might prevent the smooth flow of the dye, at the cornual end.</li>\n<li>There is a risk of embolism and endometriosis if HSG is done during this phase.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0243","text":"A patient who has been try‌ing to conceive for the p‍ast 6 months underwent hy‍sterosalpingography (HSG)​. The findings are given ​below. What is your inter​pretation?","raw_text":"A patient who has been trying to conceive for the past 6 months underwent hysterosalpingography (HSG). The findings are given below. What is your interpretation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal hysterosalpingogram‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"HSG showing ‌blocked tubes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HSG showing a bicornuate u‌terus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HSG showing a filling defe‌ct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Normal hysterosalpingogram","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c5d8a905706040c48a078bdf5acc823a.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a9462b0266c740e4b9f3b542895181d1x512x294.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b0ba8eccad8341c7add52d85b627b1c6x512x428.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/be953c033ca64dcc833c6542a07959e0x512x484.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c15d541c5c0d4798aa937ce43885d0a8x512x291.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Normal hysterosalpingogram</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>normal hysterosalpingogram</strong> (HSG). Both fallopian <strong>tubes</strong> are <strong>patent</strong> with a <strong>spill</strong> into the <strong>peritonea</strong>l cavity.</p>\n<p>The following images show the findings of an abnormal hysterosalpingogram:</p>\n<p>The hysterosalpingogram given below shows bilateral <strong>dilated</strong> fallopian <strong>tubes</strong> with <strong>no free spill</strong> suggestive of <strong>bilateral hydrosalpinx:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The hysterosalpingogram given below shows a <strong>unicornuate uterus:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The hysterosalpingogram given below shows a <strong>bicornuate</strong> uterus/<strong>septate uterus:</strong></p>\n<p>There is an overlap in the appearance of the bicornuate uterus and septate/sub-septate uterus on HSG. The distinction lies in the shape of the <strong>uterine fundus</strong>, which is usually <strong>not visible on HSG</strong>. An <strong>ultrasound</strong> or <strong>MRI</strong> should be done to <strong>differentiate</strong> between these conditions if intervention is planned.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The hysterosalpingogram given below shows <strong>filling defects:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1396","text":"Which the following are no‌t the advantages of ferti‍loscopy over laparoscopy?‍","raw_text":"Which the following are not the advantages of fertiloscopy over laparoscopy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No Trendelenburg position—‌only lithotomy position","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trauma is avoided as no tr‌ocar is needed","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"No CO2—Hydroperitoneum wit‌h saline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Good view of the anterior ‌surface of the ovary and ‍genital organs","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Good view of the anterior surface of the ovary and genital organs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe9c7b624f64493fbdf62e5443c3d18dx700x527.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Good view of the anterior surface of the ovary and genital organs</strong></p><p><strong>Fertiloscopy</strong> helps in obtaining a good view of the <strong>posterior</strong> <strong>surface</strong> (and not the anterior surface) of the <strong>ovary and genital organs</strong>. This is an advantage of fertiloscopy over laparoscopy.</p>\n<p>The other<strong> advantages </strong>of<strong> fertiloscopy </strong>over laparoscopy are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The Trendelenburg position is not needed. The procedure can be done in a <strong>lithotomy position</strong></li>\n<li>Trauma can be avoided as <strong>no trocar</strong> is needed</li>\n<li>No CO<sub>2</sub>: Hydroperitoneum with saline</li>\n<li>Peritoneal spill can be seen</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Fertiloscopy is a new<strong> mini-invasive</strong> diagnostic technique that allows the <strong>visualization of</strong> the<strong> posterior pelvis</strong>. In this technique, an <strong>optical device</strong> is introduced<strong> into</strong> the <strong>pouch of Douglas</strong>, through the posterior vaginal fornix, under general/local anaesthesia.</p>\n<p>It can also be used for <strong>small interventions</strong>, such as adhesiolysis, ovarian drilling, coagulation of endometriosis spots and to perform chromosalpingoscopy and salpingoscopy. With fertiloscopy, surgical intervention can be avoided and it is a good alternative to diagnostic laparoscopy.</p>\n<p>The image given below is of a fertiloscope.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1176","text":"A 26-year-old woman underw‌ent a workup for primary ‍infertility. On hysterosa‍lpingography, there was a​ bilateral cornual block.​ What is the next step in​ management?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old woman underwent a workup for primary infertility. On hysterosalpingography, there was a bilateral cornual block. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tubop‌lasty","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hysteroscopy and laparosco‌py-guided surgery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hydrot‌ubation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"In-vitro fe‌rtilization","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hysteroscopy and laparoscopy-guided surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hysteroscopy and laparoscopy-guided surgery</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, the next step in management will be <strong>hysteroscopy </strong>and<strong> laparoscopy-guided surgery. </strong></p>\n<p>Tubal contractions, particularly <strong>cornual spasms</strong>, can give an incorrect impression of <strong>proximal fallopian tube obstruction</strong> (a false-positive result) on hysterosalpingography.<strong> Laparoscopy with chromotubation</strong> is therefore considered to <strong>confirm the diagnosis</strong> of cornual spasm. Laparoscopy is both diagnostic and therapeutic in this condition.</p>\n<p>Hysteroscopy is an operative procedure that provides an endoscopic view of the endometrial cavity and tubal ostia via the cervix, performed in the <strong>preovulatory phase</strong>. It can be <strong>diagnostic</strong> or <strong>therapeutic</strong>. <strong>It is the gold standard investigation for visualizing the uterine cavity and tubal ostia.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 278px; width: 519px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 261.491px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Therapeutic</strong> indications of hysteroscopy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 242.621px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Diagnostic</strong> indications of hysteroscopy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 261.491px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Uterine septum</li>\n<li><strong>Asherman syndrome</strong></li>\n<li>Retained IUCD</li>\n<li>Polypectomy</li>\n<li><strong>Submucous fibroid</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Abnormal uterine bleeding</strong></li>\n<li>Tubal sterilization using sclerosing agents</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 242.621px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>To study the endocervical mucosal lining</li>\n<li>Congenital malformations of the uterus</li>\n<li><strong>Endometrial tuberculosis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Misplaced IUCD</strong></li>\n<li>Endometrial lesions</li>\n<li>Endometrial polyp</li>\n<li><strong>Asherman syndrome</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cornual tubal blockage</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Contraindications</strong> to hysteroscopy are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acute genital tract infection</strong> (Eg: genital herpes infection)</li>\n<li>Viable intrauterine <strong>pregnancy</strong></li>\n<li>During <strong>menstruation</strong>, the view is obscured, and the infection rate is high.</li>\n<li><strong>Scarred uterus</strong> and <strong>enlarged</strong> uterus of size <strong>≥</strong> <strong>12 weeks</strong> are relative contraindications.</li>\n<li><strong>Cervical stenosis</strong> can cause cervical tears and uterine perforation.</li>\n<li><strong>Cardiopulmonary disorder</strong> due to increased risk of <strong>fluid overload</strong> and <strong>pulmonary edema</strong>.</li>\n<li>Known cases of <strong>cervical cancer</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Laparoscopy is the gold standard for the evaluation of tubal factors in infertility, </strong>commonly done in the <strong>proliferative phase</strong>. It can be diagnostic or therapeutic. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 726px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 372.641px;\">Diagnostic </td>\n<td style=\"width: 334.359px;\">Therapeutic (Operative)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 372.641px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Age &gt;35 years</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">History of pelvic pain, dyspareunia, pelvic surgery, adhesions</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Abnormal HSG</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Failure to conceive after a reasonable period (6 months) with normal HSG</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Women with comorbid pelvic pathology (PID, endometriosis)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 334.359px;\">\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Unexplained infertility</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Reconstructive tubal surgery</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Adhesiolysis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fulguration of endometriotic implants</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ovarian drilling in PCOS</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">GIFT and ZIFT procedures</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Benefits of laparoscopic evaluation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tubes: For detection of tubal patency, block (site and side), motility, hydrosalpinx change, peritubal adhesions, fimbrial agglutination</li>\n<li>Ovaries: PCOS changes, endometriosis, PID, adhesions</li>\n<li>Uterus: Anomalies, fibroids</li>\n<li>Peritoneal factors: Adhesions, PID, endometriosis, tuberculosis</li>\n<li>Therapy at the same sitting (as appropriate)</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6948","text":"A woman came to the clinic‌ complaining of amenorrho‍ea and inability to conce‍ive. She had undergone hy​pophysectomy for a tumour​ a year ago. What would b​e the best line of manage‌ment for her?","raw_text":"A woman came to the clinic complaining of amenorrhoea and inability to conceive. She had undergone hypophysectomy for a tumour a year ago. What would be the best line of management for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clomiphen‌e citrate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Letr‌ozole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"GnRH a‌nalogue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Human menopausal gonadotro‌phin, followed by hCG","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Human menopausal gonadotrophin, followed by hCG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Human menopausal gonadotrophin, followed by hCG</strong></p><p>The best line of management for the given patient is <strong>human menopausal gonadotropin</strong> (hMG) followed by<strong> hCG</strong>.</p>\n<p>The patients who have undergone <strong>hypophysectomy</strong> i.e. removal of the pituitary gland, can not produce endogenous gonadotropins. <strong>Exogenous gonadotropins</strong> such as hMG are given to such patients. <strong>Timed</strong> <strong>administration</strong> of <strong>hCG </strong>is needed to <strong>induce ovulation</strong> after the follicle has matured.</p>\n<p>The <strong>human menopausal gonadotropin</strong> is an extract of <strong>urine </strong>from<strong> postmenopausal</strong> women. It contains <strong>FSH</strong> and <strong>LH </strong>in the required ratio for follicular growth.</p>\n<p>Clomiphene, letrozole, and GnRH analogs stimulate the release of gonadotropins from the pituitary gland. These are not helpful in a patient whose pituitary gland has been removed.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"qFU82o0v","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"41_congenital_and_valvular_heart_disease","title":"41.Congenital And Valvular Heart Disease","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MB0424","text":"Most congenital heart dise‌ases arise between what w​eeks of embryogenesis?","raw_text":"Most congenital heart diseases arise between what weeks of embryogenesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 t‌o 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 t‌o 8","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"8 t‌o 12","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10 t‌o 14","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3 to 8","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3 to 8</strong></p><p>Most <strong>congenital heart diseases</strong> arise from faulty embryogenesis during gestational<strong> weeks 3 to 8 </strong>when major cardiovascular structures form and begin to function.</p>\n<p>The <strong>most</strong> <strong>common</strong> congenital heart disease is ventricular septal defect (<strong>VSD</strong>), whereas the <strong>2nd most common</strong> is atrial septal defect (<strong>ASD</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0425","text":"What congenital heart defe‌ct is associated with Cha​r syndrome?","raw_text":"What congenital heart defect is associated with Char syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patent ductus arteriosus (‌PDA)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Atrial septal defect (ASD)‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricular septal defect ‌(VSD)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tetralogy of‌ Fallot (TOF)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/87d63644322d452c9e5f2281b33d5075x235x214.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)</strong></p><p>Char syndrome is associated with <strong>patent ductus arteriosus (PDA).</strong></p>\n<p>The clinical presentation of this syndrome includes PDA with <strong>facial dysmorphism</strong> and <strong>abnormal fifth digits</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following images show the facial dysmorphism associated with Char syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0426","text":"Which of the following car‌diac defects is not assoc​iated with Down's syndrom‌e?","raw_text":"Which of the following cardiac defects is not associated with Down's syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coarctatio‌n of aorta","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular ‌septal defect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patent ductu‌s arteriosus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atrial sep‌tal defect","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Coarctation of aorta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Coarctation of aorta</strong></p><p><strong>Coarctation</strong> of the aorta is a feature of <strong>Turner's syndrome</strong>, not Down's syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Down's </strong>syndrome (trisomy 21) is the <strong>most common</strong> <strong>cause</strong> of congenital heart disease (<strong>CHD</strong>). Cardiac defects in Down's syndrome are due to <strong>endocardial cushion defects</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cardiac defects in various chromosomal anomalies are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 104px;\" width=\"314\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\"><strong>Syndrome</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\"><strong>Cardiac defects</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">Down's syndrome (Trisomy 21)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Ventricular septal defect</li>\n<li>Patent ductus arteriosus</li>\n<li>Atrial septal defect</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">Patau syndrome (Trisomy 13)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Ventricular septal defect</li>\n<li>Patent ductus arteriosus</li>\n<li>Atrial septal defect </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">Edward's syndrome (Trisomy 18)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Ventricular septal defect</li>\n<li>Patent ductus arteriosus</li>\n<li>Atrial septal defect</li>\n<li>Dextroposition</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">Turner syndrome (Monosomy X)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Bicuspid aortic valve</li>\n<li>Coarctation of the aorta</li>\n<li>Dilated ascending aorta</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0429","text":"What is the most common ty‌pe of atrial septal defec​t?","raw_text":"What is the most common type of atrial septal defect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primum a‌nomalies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Secund‌um ASD","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sinus veno‌sus defect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Membran‌ous type","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Secundum ASD","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/47b4c4639dc1492e82ba817ad97bb914x1280x1275.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Secundum ASD</strong></p><p><strong>Secundum atrial septal defect</strong> (ASD) is the most common type of <strong>ASD</strong>.</p>\n<p>ASDs are abnormal, fixed openings in the atrial septum caused by incomplete tissue formation that allows <strong>communication</strong> of blood between the <strong>left and right atria</strong>.</p>\n<p>It results in a <strong>left-to-right</strong> shunt. Excess blood flow through the ASD and/or the pulmonary valve results in a <strong>murmur</strong>. They are generally well-tolerated and usually do not become symptomatic before the age of 30 years.</p>\n<p>ASDs are classified according to their location:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Secundum ASD </strong>(90%) - usually isolated defects, can be of any size, multiple or fenestrated</li>\n<li><strong>Primum anomalies </strong>(5%) - occur adjacent to the AV valves, often associated with AV valve defects and/or VSD</li>\n<li><strong>Sinus venosus defects </strong>(5%) - located near SVC entrance and can be associated with anomalous pulmonary venous return to the right atrium</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0431","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a feature of tetralog​y of Fallot?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of tetralogy of Fallot?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left ventricular hypertrop‌hy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Subpulmona‌ry stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Overridi‌ng aorta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ventricular ‌Septal Defect","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Left ventricular hypertrophy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8d5ee63ab1c948d88e83a349a17bd555x1280x3010.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7f1fac332b904bc0ba66e6ebc507e24bx510x603.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Left ventricular hypertrophy</strong></p><p>Right ventricular hypertrophy (<strong>RVH</strong>) is a feature of the tetralogy of Fallot (<strong>TOF</strong>), not left ventricular hypertrophy.</p>\n<p>The four cardinal features of<strong> tetralogy of Fallot</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ventricular septal defect (VSD)</li>\n<li>Obstruction of the right ventricular outflow tract (subpulmonary stenosis)</li>\n<li>An aorta that overrides the VSD</li>\n<li>Right ventricular hypertrophy</li>\n</ol>\n<p>It is the <strong>most common</strong> type of <strong>cyanotic</strong> congenital heart disease (<strong>CHD</strong>). The heart is typically enlarged and is classically <strong>boot-shaped </strong>due to marked right ventricular hypertrophy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0433","text":"A 5-year-old child was bro‌ught to the OPD for evalu​ation of cognitive impair‌ment. Parents report that​ the child is overly-frie‍ndly with strangers. On e‍xamination, elfin facies ​and hypotonia are noted. ‍Investigations reveal hyp​ercalcemia. Which of the ‌following cardiac defects‌ is associated with this ‍syndrome?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old child was brought to the OPD for evaluation of cognitive impairment. Parents report that the child is overly-friendly with strangers. On examination, elfin facies and hypotonia are noted. Investigations reveal hypercalcemia. Which of the following cardiac defects is associated with this syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ventricular ‌septal defect","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atrial sep‌tal defect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aortic ‌stenosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypoplastic‌ ventricles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aortic stenosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/76dad8a929414380ac4e80e45fcb5fd8x568x433.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aortic stenosis</strong></p><p>The given features are suggestive of <strong>Williams </strong>(Williams–Beuren) <strong>syndrome</strong>. It is associated with <strong>supravalvular</strong> <strong>aortic stenosis.</strong></p>\n<p>This condition is due to deletions on <strong>chromosome 7 </strong>which includes the<strong> gene for elastin.</strong> These mutations may cause supravalvular stenosis by disrupting elastin–smooth muscle cell interactions during aortic morphogenesis. There is <strong>thickening</strong> of ascending aortic wall and consequent <strong>luminal constriction</strong>. </p>\n<p>Other features of the syndrome include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Elfin facies </strong>- wide mouth, chubby cheeks, long philtrum, small nose, and delicate chin (as shown in the image)</li>\n<li>Cognitive abnormalities</li>\n<li><strong>Intellectual</strong> <strong>disability</strong> (usually mild)</li>\n<li>Personality changes - over-friendliness to strangers</li>\n<li>Hypotonia</li>\n<li>Connective tissue abnormalities</li>\n<li>Endocrine abnormalities (hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, hypothyroidism, early puberty)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0434","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cause of ​mitral stenosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of mitral stenosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumatic h‌eart disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Calcif‌ication","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atheroscler‌otic damage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infective e‌ndocarditis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rheumatic heart disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rheumatic heart disease</strong></p><p>The most common cause of mitral stenosis is <strong>r</strong><strong>heumatic heart disease.</strong> Mitral stenosis is the most common lesion in rheumatic heart disease. </p>\n<p>In rheumatic mitral stenosis, calcification and fibrous bridging across the valvular commissures create <strong>fish mouth </strong>or<strong> buttonhole stenosis</strong>. Rheumatic mitral stenosis is associated with <strong>loud S1</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most common causes of various valvular diseases are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aortic stenosis (<strong>AS</strong>) - <strong>calcification</strong> due to normal aging or congenitally bicuspid aortic valves</li>\n<li>Aortic regurgitation (<strong>AR</strong>) - dilatation of the ascending aorta, often secondary to <strong>hypertension</strong> and/or <strong>aging</strong></li>\n<li>Mitral regurgitation (<strong>MR</strong>) - myxomatous degeneration (<strong>mitral valve prolapse</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0435","text":"A 42-year-old man presente‌d with chest pain and exe​rtional dyspnea for the p‌ast 2 months. He has no h​istory of cardiac disease‍. Examination revealed an‍ ejection systolic murmur​. Transesophageal echocar‍diography was done, which​ revealed an aortic valve‌ with 2 functional cusps ‌and a valve area of 0.8 s‍q. cm. Which of the follo​wing is false regarding t​his condition?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old man presented with chest pain and exertional dyspnea for the past 2 months. He has no history of cardiac disease. Examination revealed an ejection systolic murmur. Transesophageal echocardiography was done, which revealed an aortic valve with 2 functional cusps and a valve area of 0.8 sq. cm. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chromosomes 18q, 5q, and 1‌3q are linked","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loss-of-function mutations‌ in NOTCH1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is due to abnormal fusi‌on of valve commissures","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Midline of larger cusp is ‌major site of calcificati​on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is due to abnormal fusion of valve commissures","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is due to abnormal fusion of valve commissures</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario and echo findings are suggestive of <strong>calcific aortic stenosis</strong> secondary to <strong>bicuspid aortic valve </strong>(2 functional cusps). It is due to <strong>incomplete separation</strong> of valve <strong>commissures </strong>during development, not abnormal fusion.</p>\n<p>In a congenitally bicuspid aortic valve, the 2 functional cusps are usually of unequal size. The<strong> larger cusp</strong> has a <strong>midline raphe</strong> that results from incomplete commissural separation during development. The raphe is frequently a <strong>major site</strong> of <strong>calcific</strong> <strong>deposits</strong>.</p>\n<p>This condition is associated with mutations in the following areas:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chromosomes <strong>18q, 5q, and 13q</strong> </li>\n<li>Chromosome <strong>9q </strong>- loss-of-function mutations in<strong> NOTCH1</strong> gene</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Late <strong>complications</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aortic stenosis or regurgitation</li>\n<li>Infective endocarditis</li>\n<li>Aortic dilation and/or dissection.</li>\n<li>Predisposed to progressive calcification - Approximately 50% of cases of aortic stenosis in adults.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>Acquired bicuspid</strong> valves result from<strong> abnormal fusion</strong> of valve commissures because of an acquired deformity (rheumatic valve disease)<strong>. </strong>It<strong> </strong>produces a <strong>conjoined cusp</strong> that is generally twice the size of the nonconjoined cusp.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0436","text":"A 45-year-old woman who ca‌me for a routine check-up​ was incidentally found t‌o have a late systolic mu​rmur with a mid-systolic ‍click. Echocardiography r‍evealed thickening and re​dundancy of mitral leafle‍ts with bulging into the ​left atrium. Which of the‌ following is not a featu‌re of this condition?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman who came for a routine check-up was incidentally found to have a late systolic murmur with a mid-systolic click. Echocardiography revealed thickening and redundancy of mitral leaflets with bulging into the left atrium. Which of the following is not a feature of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Annular ‌dilation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myxomatous ‌degeneration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Degenerative calcified dep‌osits","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thickening of mural endoca‌rdium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Degenerative calcified deposits","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Degenerative calcified deposits</strong></p><p>The clinical features and echocardiography findings are suggestive of <strong>mitral valve prolapse</strong> (MVP) or <strong>Barlow syndrome</strong>. It is not associated with degenerative calcified deposits (seen in calcific valvular degeneration, not MVP).</p>\n<p>Barlow syndrome is more common in <strong>females</strong>. It may be an isolated condition or less commonly can be associated with connective tissue disorders like Marfan's syndrome.</p>\n<p>In this condition, there is mitral <strong>annular dilatation</strong> with <strong>floppy</strong> mitral valve leaflets. They prolapse or <strong>balloon back</strong> into the <strong>left atrium</strong> during systole. The following pathological changes are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Interchordal ballooning (hooding) of the mitral leaflets</li>\n<li>Thick, rubbery, redundant leaflets </li>\n<li><strong>Thickened</strong> <strong>mural</strong> <strong>endocardium</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The main histologic change is marked thickening of the spongiosa layer with deposition of mucoid called <strong>myxomatous degeneration</strong>.</p>\n<p>Most individuals diagnosed with MVP are <strong>asymptomatic</strong>. On auscultation, a <strong>mid-systolic click</strong>, sometimes followed by a mid to late systolic murmur is heard. Chest pain mimicking angina and dyspnea occur rarely.</p>\n<p>The complications include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infective endocarditis</li>\n<li>Mitral insufficiency</li>\n<li>Stroke or other systemic infarcts</li>\n<li>Arrhythmias</li>\n<li>Sudden cardiac death (rarely)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0518","text":"A 48-year-old man presente‌d with dull aching abdomi​nal pain and diarrhea for‌ the past 2 months. On ex​amination, his face and c‍hest appeared flushed. In‍ a 24-hour urine sample, ​5HIAA levels were elevate‍d. CT scan of the abdomen​ revealed a mass in the t‌erminal ileum. Cardiac in‌volvement in this syndrom‍e is characterized by all​ of the following except:​","raw_text":"A 48-year-old man presented with dull aching abdominal pain and diarrhea for the past 2 months. On examination, his face and chest appeared flushed. In a 24-hour urine sample, 5HIAA levels were elevated. CT scan of the abdomen revealed a mass in the terminal ileum. Cardiac involvement in this syndrome is characterized by all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endocardia‌l fibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tricuspid i‌nsufficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary‌ stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mitral ‌stenosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mitral stenosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mitral stenosis</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 570\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>carcinoid syndrome</strong>. Mitral stenosis is not a feature of cardiac involvement in this condition.</p>\n<p><strong>Carcinoid heart disease</strong> refers to the cardiac manifestations caused by the bioactive compounds released by carcinoid tumors. Classically, the endocardium and valves of the right heart are primarily affected.</p>\n<p>Findings in carcinoid heart syndrome:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endocardial </strong>plaque-like thickening and <strong>fibrosis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tricuspid insufficiency </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pulmonary stenosis</strong></li>\n<li>No valve destruction</li>\n<li>Myocardium not involved</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>left side </strong>of the heart is <strong>less commonly</strong> affected because the pulmonary vascular bed degrades the mediators. However, left heart carcinoid lesions can occur in the setting of ASD or VSD, or in the case of primary pulmonary carcinoid tumors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2216","text":"Which is the most commonly‌ involved valve in carcin​oid heart disease?","raw_text":"Which is the most commonly involved valve in carcinoid heart disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mit‌ral","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tric‌uspid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aor‌tic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulm‌onary","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tricuspid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tricuspid</strong></p><p><strong>Carcinoid heart disease</strong> most commonly affects the <strong>tricuspid valve</strong>.</p>\n<p>It usually affects right heart structures. The most common features are <strong>tricuspid regurgitation</strong> and <strong>pulmonary stenosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"TABM0dUT","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"41_first_line_drugs_for_tuberculosis","title":"41.First Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MF6028","text":"A 35-year-old lady present‌s with pulmonary TB is st‌arted on anti-tubercular ‍therapy. What is the rati‍onale for administering m​ulti-drug therapy to her?​","raw_text":"A 35-year-old lady presents with pulmonary TB is started on anti-tubercular therapy. What is the rationale for administering multi-drug therapy to her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"To delay development of re‌sistance","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"To reduce‌ toxicity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"To broaden antimicrobial s‌pectrum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"To prevent toxin release f‌rom the organism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. To delay development of resistance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. To delay development of resistance</strong></p><p>The reason for administering <strong>multi-drug therapy</strong> in tuberculosis is to <strong>prevent</strong> the <strong>development</strong> of <strong>resistance.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Resistance</strong> to individual drugs in <em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</em> occurs by <strong>spontaneous point mutations</strong> that occur at a low but predictable rate (10<sup>-7 </sup>to 10<sup>-10 </sup>). Development of this drug resistance is almost invariably the<strong> result of monotherapy, </strong>i.e., the failure of the health care provider to prescribe at least 2 drugs to which the tubercle bacilli are susceptible. Hence, multi-drug therapy is recommended for the treatment of tuberculosis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5150","text":"Which is the most effectiv‌e drug against extracellu‌lar mycobacteria?","raw_text":"Which is the most effective drug against extracellular mycobacteria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ison‌iazid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pyrazi‌namide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Etham‌butol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rifampicin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rifampicin</strong></p><p>The <strong>most effective</strong> drug against extracellular mycobacteria is <strong>Rifampicin</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option A: Isoniazid and Rifampicin act against both intracellular and extracellular mycobacterium. But, Rifampicin inhibits the extracellular mycobacteria at much lower drug concentrations than isoniazid.</p>\n<p>Option B: Pyrazinamide acts only against <strong>intracellular</strong> mycobacterium.</p>\n<p>Option C: Ethambutol acts against only <strong>extracellular</strong> mycobacteria but is <strong>not as effective</strong> as rifampicin. It is a <strong>bacteriostatic</strong> drug. Fast-multiplying bacteria are highly susceptible to its action. </p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Streptomycin </strong>is primarily active against only the<strong> extracellular </strong>tubercle bacilli as its penetration into the cells is very poor. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5139","text":"A patient was diagnosed wi‌th pulmonary tuberculosis‌ and was started on first‍-line antitubercular drug‍s. Which of the following​ drug acts by inhibiting ​the synthesis of the mark‌ed layer shown in the ima‌ge?","raw_text":"A patient was diagnosed with pulmonary tuberculosis and was started on first-line antitubercular drugs. Which of the following drug acts by inhibiting the synthesis of the marked layer shown in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ison‌iazid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyrazi‌namide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Etham‌butol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Isoniazid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ebe55c0cc99a42b383d1f16c8023a2ff.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Isoniazid</strong></p><p><strong>Isoniazid</strong> is the first-line antitubercular drug that acts by inhibiting the synthesis of <strong>mycolic acid</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Isoniazid</strong> is a first-line bactericidal drug that is used for the treatment of tuberculosis. This drug enters bacilli by <strong>passive diffusion</strong>. It then gets activated to its toxic form by the enzyme <strong>catalase-peroxidase</strong>(the enzyme encoded by the <strong>Kat gene</strong>).</p>\n<p>Normally, InhA and KasA are involved in the synthesis of mycolic acid. Upon activation, the reactive metabolite of isoniazid forms complexes with <strong>NAD</strong> that inhibit <strong>InhA</strong> and <strong>KasA</strong>. Hence, isoniazid inhibits the production of <strong>mycolic acid</strong>.</p>\n<p>The reactive isoniazid metabolite also forms complexes with <strong>NADP</strong>. This complex inhibits mycobacterial dihydrofolate reductase which results in the interruption of <strong>DNA synthesis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Side effects of isoniazid include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Peripheral neuritis</strong> due to interference with the production of active coenzyme pyridoxal phosphate from pyridoxine. Therefore, <strong>pyridoxine 10mg/day (vitamin B6)</strong> is given prophylactically along with isoniazid to prevent neurotoxicity.</li>\n<li><strong>Hepatitis</strong> - isoniazid must be stopped at the first sign of hepatotoxicity.</li>\n<li>Rashes</li>\n<li>Anaemia</li>\n<li>Arthralgia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Toxicity of isoniazid results in <strong>metabolic acidosis</strong>, <strong>seizures, and coma.</strong></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: Rifampin acts by binding to <strong>DNA-dependent RNA polymerase</strong> to form a stable drug-enzyme complex. This complex suppresses RNA synthesis. Side effects of rifampin are hepatitis,<strong> flu-like illness, orange-red colored urine</strong>, and body secretions.</p>\n<p> Option C: Pyrazinamide is a prodrug, it is converted into an active metabolite<strong> pyrazinoic acid </strong>which makes the intracellular environment <strong>acidic</strong> and collapses the transmembrane proton motive force, thereby killing the bacteria. Side effects include <strong>hyperuricemia</strong> and hepatotoxicity. </p>\n<p>Option D: Ethambutol<strong> inhibits arabinose transferase III, </strong>thereby decreasing arabinogalactan biosynthesis<strong>. </strong>This results in disruption in mycobacterial cell wall synthesis. <strong>Optic neuritis</strong> is an important side effect of<strong> ethambutol</strong>. It results in decreased visual acuity and <strong>loss of red-green discrimination.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5161","text":"A study was conducted to g‌enotype NAT2 gene polymor‌phisms in patients receiv‍ing ATT. Among the 300 pa‍tients genotyped, 200 wer​e slow acetylators (group​ A), 70 were intermediate‌ (group B) and 30 were fa‌st acetylators (group C).​ Patients in group A are ‌prone to develop which of​ the following?","raw_text":"A study was conducted to genotype NAT2 gene polymorphisms in patients receiving ATT. Among the 300 patients genotyped, 200 were slow acetylators (group A), 70 were intermediate (group B) and 30 were fast acetylators (group C). Patients in group A are prone to develop which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Failure o‌f therapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Peripheral‌ neuropathy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatot‌oxicity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Allergic ‌reactions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Peripheral neuropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Peripheral neuropathy</strong></p><p><strong>Isoniazid, </strong>a key agent in the ATT,<strong> </strong>is metabolized primarily through<strong> N-acetylation</strong> by the genetically polymorphic N-acetyltransferase-2 <strong>(NAT2) </strong>enzyme. <strong>Slow acetylators</strong> (group A) of isoniazid are prone to develop <strong>peripheral neuropathy</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The rate of metabolism of isoniazid is determined by the genetic polymorphism of NAT2 enzyme and classified broadly into two groups - <strong>slow </strong>and<strong> fast acetylators</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">In <strong>slow acetylators, </strong>the drug <strong>accumulates </strong>due to slow metabolism resulting in<strong> </strong>an increased risk of<strong> side effects, </strong>and hence they should undergo periodic monitoring. Peripheral neuropathy is a more frequent side effect of INH when compared to hepatotoxicity.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Fast acetylators</strong> metabolize the drug rapidly and <strong>show less response </strong>to standard doses and the therapy may be less effective. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5157","text":"Line probe assay done on s‌putum sample of a patient‌ with TB revealed over-ex‍pression of InhA gene. Th‍is can lead to cross-resi​stance of isoniazid with ​which of the following dr‌ugs?","raw_text":"Line probe assay done on sputum sample of a patient with TB revealed over-expression of InhA gene. This can lead to cross-resistance of isoniazid with which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ethio‌namide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclo‌serine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Etham‌butol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ethionamide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ethionamide</strong></p><p>Over-expression of the gene for <strong>InhA </strong>leads to <strong>cross-resistance</strong> of isoniazid (INH) with <strong>ethionamide</strong>. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 132px; width: 653.667px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 52.6667px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 212px; height: 52.6667px;\"><strong>Mechanisms of INH resistance</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 62px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 212px; height: 62px;\">Mutation of KatG </td>\n<td style=\"width: 425.667px; height: 62px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>most common mechanism</li>\n<li>confers a high level of resistance</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 62px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 212px; height: 62px;\"><strong>Overexpression of InhA</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 425.667px; height: 62px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>confers a <strong>low level of resistance </strong></li>\n<li><strong>cross-resistance with ethionamide</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 45px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 212px; height: 45px;\">Overexpression of ahpC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 425.667px; height: 45px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>increases the bacterial survival under oxidative stress</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 45px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 212px; height: 45px;\">Induction of efflux pumps</td>\n<td style=\"width: 425.667px; height: 45px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>cross-resistance with ethambutol</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5183","text":"A 4-month-old boy was brou‌ght to the ER with recurr‌ent seizures. His mother ‍informs you that he is re‍ceiving isoniazid prophyl​axis as she was diagnosed​ with TB during pregnancy‌. ABG revealed a high ani‌on gap metabolic acidosis​. What would be the mains‌tay of treatment?","raw_text":"A 4-month-old boy was brought to the ER with recurrent seizures. His mother informs you that he is receiving isoniazid prophylaxis as she was diagnosed with TB during pregnancy. ABG revealed a high anion gap metabolic acidosis. What would be the mainstay of treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Benzodi‌azepines","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Barbit‌urates","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyrid‌oxine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thia‌mine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pyridoxine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pyridoxine</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>isoniazid overdose </strong>for which the mainstay of treatment is <strong>intravenous pyridoxine</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Isoniazid</strong> overdose results in decreased pyridoxine availability to neurons. This <strong>decreases</strong> the levels of <strong>GABA</strong>, which in turn lowers the seizure threshold. <strong>Benzodiazepines</strong> can be used to control<strong> seizures.</strong></p>\n<p>Prolonged treatment with isoniazid leads to the <strong>deficiency of pyridoxine</strong> which can lead to <strong>peripheral neuropathy </strong>and other neurological manifestations like ataxia, paresthesia, and numbness.</p>\n<p>Clinical triad of isoniazid overdose:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\"><strong>Seizures</strong> refractory to treatment with phenytoin and barbiturates</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">High anion gap metabolic acidosis (<strong>HAGMA</strong>) resistant to treatment with sodium bicarbonate</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Coma</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5216","text":"A 30-year-old man undergoi‌ng treatment for TB visit‌s your OPD for follow-up.‍ His wife states that rec‍ently he is forgetting th​e names of his colleagues​ and neighbors, but later‌ recollects them correctl‌y. Which drug is implicat​ed to cause this conditio‌n?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man undergoing treatment for TB visits your OPD for follow-up. His wife states that recently he is forgetting the names of his colleagues and neighbors, but later recollects them correctly. Which drug is implicated to cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ethio‌namide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ison‌iazid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Etham‌butol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyrazi‌namide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Isoniazid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Isoniazid</strong></p><p><strong>Isoniazid </strong>is the antitubercular drug that is implicated in the causation of <strong>transient memory loss.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Neurological toxicities</strong> like peripheral neuropathy, optic neuritis, and seizures can occur with isoniazid therapy. It is also associated with abnormalities like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Euphoria</li>\n<li>Transient memory loss</li>\n<li>Separation of ideas and reality</li>\n<li>Loss of self-control</li>\n<li>Florid psychosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Pyridoxine </strong>therapy can <strong>prevent </strong>and <strong>reverse </strong>most of the neurological toxicity and mental abnormalities.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5217","text":"A 66-year-old woman, on an‌ti-tubercular therapy, pr‌esented with pain in the ‍right hand and shoulder f‍or the past 1 month. On p​hysical examination, her ​wrist was swollen and her‌ shoulder was extremely p‌ainful with a limited ran​ge of motion. Which drug ‌is most likely associated​ with this condition?","raw_text":"A 66-year-old woman, on anti-tubercular therapy, presented with pain in the right hand and shoulder for the past 1 month. On physical examination, her wrist was swollen and her shoulder was extremely painful with a limited range of motion. Which drug is most likely associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Levofl‌oxacin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ison‌iazid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rifa‌mpin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Etham‌butol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Isoniazid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Isoniazid</strong></p><p>The given clinical presentation is suggestive of <strong>shoulder-hand syndrome </strong>which is associated with the use of <strong>isoniazid</strong>.</p>\n<p>This syndrome is characterized by <strong>painful arthropathy </strong>in the <strong>upper extremity joints</strong>. It can also present as generalized arthropathy.</p>\n<p>Other drugs that can cause shoulder-hand syndrome are <strong>phenobarbitone</strong> and <strong>phenytoin</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF6017","text":"Before initiating treatmen‌t for a sputum-positive T‌B patient, the physician ‍orders for Cartridge Base‍d Nucleic Acid Amplificat​ion Test in order to test​ for drug resistance. Whi‌ch anti-tubercular drug r‌esistance is he checking ​for?","raw_text":"Before initiating treatment for a sputum-positive TB patient, the physician orders for Cartridge Based Nucleic Acid Amplification Test in order to test for drug resistance. Which anti-tubercular drug resistance is he checking for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ison‌iazid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pyrazi‌namide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Etham‌butol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rifampicin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rifampicin</strong></p><p>The Cartridge Based-Nucleic Acid Amplification Test (<strong>CBNAAT</strong>) is a molecular test for tuberculosis that detects the DNA of tubercle bacilli <strong>within 2 hours</strong> and mutations in the <strong>rpoB</strong> <strong>gene</strong> responsible for <strong>rifampicin resistance.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5189","text":"Which of the following rif‌amycin drugs would you ad‌vise a patient to take on‍ an empty stomach?","raw_text":"Which of the following rifamycin drugs would you advise a patient to take on an empty stomach?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rifabutin an‌d rifapentine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rifabutin an‌d rifampicin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rifampi‌cin only","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rifapent‌ine only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rifampicin only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rifampicin only</strong></p><p><strong>Rifampicin </strong>should be advised to be taken on an empty stomach because<strong> f</strong><strong>ood decreases its absorption. </strong></p>\n<p>Rifabutin absorption is not altered by food.</p>\n<p><strong>Rifapentine</strong> absorption is enhanced by fatty food and hence it is recommended to be taken <strong>with food.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5237","text":"An HIV-positive patient on‌ ART with zidovudine, lam‌ivudine, and indinavir de‍velops pulmonary tubercul‍osis. Which of the follow​ing anti-tubercular drugs​ should be avoided in him‌?","raw_text":"An HIV-positive patient on ART with zidovudine, lamivudine, and indinavir develops pulmonary tuberculosis. Which of the following anti-tubercular drugs should be avoided in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ison‌iazid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Strept‌omycin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Etham‌butol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rifampicin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rifampicin</strong></p><p><strong>Rifampicin </strong>should be avoided as it is a potent<strong> inducer of CYP enzymes </strong>including CYPs 1A2, 3A4, 2C9, and 2C19, which in turn can <strong>induce </strong>the<strong> metabolism of indinavir</strong> and results in <strong>therapeutic failure of ART.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Rifabutin </strong>causes the <strong>least induction</strong> of cytochrome enzymes and is therefore <strong>preferred in HIV patients</strong> who are <strong>on protease inhibitors/NNRTIs.</strong></p>\n<p>Cytochrome enzyme-inducing potency of rifamycins is: <strong>rifampicin &gt; rifapentine &gt; rifabutin.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: </p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 44.9183%; height: 255.984px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 20.2543%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\">Cytochrome p-450 inducers</td>\n<td style=\"width: 79.7457%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\">Cytochrome p-450 inhibitors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 233.586px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 20.2543%; height: 233.586px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Barbiturates</li>\n<li>Carbamazepine</li>\n<li>Rifampin</li>\n<li>Phenytoin</li>\n<li>Aminoglutethimide</li>\n<li>Cigarette smoking</li>\n<li>Chronic alcoholism</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 79.7457%; height: 233.586px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Macrolide antibiotics</li>\n<li>Azole antifungals</li>\n<li>Chloramphenicol</li>\n<li>Omeprazole</li>\n<li>Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs)</li>\n<li>HIV protease inhibitors</li>\n<li>Cimetidine</li>\n<li>Ciprofloxacin</li>\n<li>Metronidazole</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5185","text":"Which of the following rif‌amycins is not used for m‌ycobacterial infections?","raw_text":"Which of the following rifamycins is not used for mycobacterial infections?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rifa‌ximin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rifap‌entine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rifa‌butin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rifaximin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rifaximin</strong></p><p><strong>Rifaximin</strong> is not used for mycobacterial infections. It is a<strong> synthetic </strong>rifamycin that is <strong>poorly absorbed from the GIT</strong> and is indicated in the management of GI conditions like: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Traveler’s diarrhea</li>\n<li>Irritable bowel syndrome with diarrhea</li>\n<li>Hepatic encephalopathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rifamycins indicated in mycobacterial infections are rifampicin, rifapentine, and rifabutin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5187","text":"A Gene Xpert test was cond‌ucted on the sputum sampl‌e of a patient with suspe‍cted TB. The results were‍ positive and showed resi​stance to rifampicin. Wha​t is the primary mechanis‌m of resistance against t‌his drug?","raw_text":"A Gene Xpert test was conducted on the sputum sample of a patient with suspected TB. The results were positive and showed resistance to rifampicin. What is the primary mechanism of resistance against this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alteration in target enzym‌e","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Induction of‌ efflux pumps","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alteration in activation p‌athway","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Drug inactiv‌ating enzymes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Alteration in target enzyme","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Alteration in target enzyme</strong></p><p>Rifampicin resistance is primarily due to<strong> alteration in the target enzyme</strong>. </p>\n<p>Rifampicin acts by binding to the β subunit of<strong> DNA-dependent RNA polymerase </strong>(rpoB) and inhibits chain formation in RNA synthesis. Rifampicin is a <strong>potent inducer </strong>of cytochrome enzymes<strong> </strong>CYP3A4, CYP1A2, CYP2C9, and CYP2C19. Alteration of the target enzyme (rpoB) by a mutation in the gene coding for rpoB is the primary mechanism of resistance to rifampicin. Gene Xpert identifies mutations in the<strong> rpoB gene</strong> to detect rifampicin resistance.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Efflux pump induction has also been demonstrated as a mechanism of rifampicin resistance.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5188","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions can rifampicin‌ be used? Prophylaxis of ‍meningococcal meningitis ‍Treatment of brucellosis ​Eradication of staphyloco​ccal nasal carrier state ‌Treatment of pleuropulmon‌ary nocardiosis Treatment​ of paucibacillary lepros‌y","raw_text":"<p>In which of the following conditions can rifampicin be used?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Prophylaxis of meningococcal meningitis</li>\n<li>Treatment of brucellosis</li>\n<li>Eradication of staphylococcal nasal carrier state</li>\n<li>Treatment of pleuropulmonary nocardiosis</li>\n<li>Treatment of paucibacillary leprosy</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 and ‌4 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 3, ‌4 and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, ‌3 and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 3 and 5</strong></p><p>Besides its anti-tubercular action, rifampicin also has certain <strong>non-tubercular uses</strong> which include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prophylaxis</strong> of meningococcal and<em> H. influenzae</em> meningitis</li>\n<li>Treatment of<strong> MRSA</strong>, <strong>diphtheroid</strong>, and <strong>legionella</strong> infections </li>\n<li>Eradication of <strong>staphylococcal nasal carrier state</strong> in patients with chronic furunculosis</li>\n<li>A combination of doxycycline and rifampicin is used as the first-line therapy for<strong> brucellosis</strong></li>\n<li>A part of the WHO regimen for standard therapy of both paucibacillary and multibacillary <strong>leprosy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rifampicin is not used in the treatment of Nocardia species. <strong>Cotrimoxazole </strong>is considered the drug of choice for pleuropulmonary nocardiosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5244","text":"You are prescribing first-‌line anti-tubercular ther‌apy to a patient newly di‍agnosed with TB. Which of‍ the following is incorre​ct about his therapy?","raw_text":"You are prescribing first-line anti-tubercular therapy to a patient newly diagnosed with TB. Which of the following is incorrect about his therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Red green color impairment‌ is an early sign of etha‌mbutol induced optic neur‍itis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ethambutol accumulates in ‌renal failure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Flu-like syndrome is usual‌ly seen if rifampicin is ‌taken on a daily basis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperuricemia is a recogni‌sed side effect of pyrazi‌namide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Flu-like syndrome is usually seen if rifampicin is taken on a daily basis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Flu-like syndrome is usually seen if rifampicin is taken on a daily basis</strong></p><p><strong>Flu-like syndrome</strong> is usually seen when rifampicin is administered<strong> less than twice weekly </strong>(high dose)<strong> </strong>and it is uncommon when administered daily (lower dose)<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Flu-like syndrome, characterized by fevers, chills, malaise, and arthralgia is a known <strong>dose-dependent</strong> adverse effect of rifampicin therapy and tends to occur late into the patient's treatment course. It resolves with either discontinuation of rifampin or switching to a daily-dose regimen. It can often be confused with occult infections or treatment failure. </p>\n<p>Options A and B: Red-green colour impairment is one of the <strong>early</strong> signs of <strong>ethambutol-induced optic neuritis</strong>. Ethambutol is primarily excreted unchanged by kidneys and accumulation occurs in renal failure. <strong>Ethambutol</strong> does not undergo hepatic metabolism and can be <strong>safely</strong> prescribed for the treatment of TB in patients<strong> </strong>with <strong>hepatitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Pyrazinamide</strong> interferes with urate excretion and can result in <strong>hyperuricemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5223","text":"A young woman undergoing t‌reatment for tuberculosis‌ visits your OPD for a ro‍utine checkup. She inform‍s you that recently she h​as noticed an orange-colo​red discoloration of her ‌contact lenses. Which of ‌the following drugs is mo​st likely to be responsib‌le for this?","raw_text":"A young woman undergoing treatment for tuberculosis visits your OPD for a routine checkup. She informs you that recently she has noticed an orange-colored discoloration of her contact lenses. Which of the following drugs is most likely to be responsible for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ison‌iazid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pyrazi‌namide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Etham‌butol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rifampicin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rifampicin</strong></p><p><strong>Rifampicin </strong>is the drug most likely to cause orange-colored discoloration of contact lenses in this case.</p>\n<p>Drugs like rifampicin and rifabutin are known to cause <strong>reddish-orange discoloration </strong>of the<strong> skin, urine, </strong>feces, saliva, sweat, and <strong>tears </strong>(which can stain the patient's <strong>contact lenses</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5232","text":"You are planning to start ‌treatment in a middle-age‌d man diagnosed with pulm‍onary tuberculosis. He ha‍s a history of chronic ki​dney disease and his curr​ent creatinine clearance ‌is 25 mL/min. Which of th‌e following drugs is leas​t likely to require dose ‌modification?","raw_text":"You are planning to start treatment in a middle-aged man diagnosed with pulmonary tuberculosis. He has a history of chronic kidney disease and his current creatinine clearance is 25 mL/min. Which of the following drugs is least likely to require dose modification?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Etham‌butol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Strept‌omycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ison‌iazid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rifampicin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rifampicin</strong></p><p><strong>Rifampicin </strong>is the<strong> least likely</strong> drug to require dose modification in renal failure patients as it is <strong>metabolized</strong> in the<strong> liver </strong>and<strong> </strong>primarily<strong> excreted in bile</strong>. </p>\n<p>In patients with renal failure, the <strong>half-life</strong> of <strong>isoniazid</strong> will be <strong>prolonged</strong>, but its adverse effects are not significantly increased. Hence dose modification is not usually done for isoniazid in clinical practice as a reduction in dose may render the regimen ineffective and increase the chances of resistance.</p>\n<p><strong>Ethambutol </strong>and<strong> streptomycin</strong> are primarily excreted unchanged <strong>through </strong>the<strong> renal </strong>system and need dose modification in renal failure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5222","text":"Pseudojaundice is an adver‌se effect of which of the‌ following drugs?","raw_text":"Pseudojaundice is an adverse effect of which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phen‌ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rifa‌butin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pyrazi‌namide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ison‌iazid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rifabutin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rifabutin</strong></p><p><strong>Pseudojaundice</strong> is an adverse effect of <strong>rifabutin</strong>. It resolves on discontinuation of the drug.</p>\n<p>Unique adverse effects of rifabutin are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Polymyalgia/arthralgia</li>\n<li>Pseudojaundice</li>\n<li>Anterior uveitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5193","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis‌ nicotinamidase is involv‌ed in the activation of w‍hich of the following ant‍itubercular drug?","raw_text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis nicotinamidase is involved in the activation of which of the following antitubercular drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ison‌iazid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Etham‌butol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pyrazi‌namide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cyclo‌serine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pyrazinamide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pyrazinamide</strong></p><p>Mycobacterium tuberculosis<strong> nicotinamidase </strong>is involved in the activation of<strong> pyrazinamide.</strong></p>\n<p>Nicotinamidase which is produced by <em>M. tuberculosis </em>is also called <strong>pyrazinamidase</strong>. It is encoded by the <strong>pncA gene</strong> and its mutations are associated with pyrazinamide resistance. It converts pyrazinamide to active pyrazinoic acid.</p>\n<p>Pyrazinoic acid has the following actions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inhibition of fatty acid synthetase type 1 leads to interference in mycolic acid synthesis</li>\n<li>Reduction of intracellular pH</li>\n<li>Disruption of membrane transport</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5226","text":"A 47-year-old man on treat‌ment for TB presents with‌ complaints of severe pai‍n and swelling in his lef‍t great toe since today m​orning, as shown in the i​mage below. Which of the ‌following drugs is most l‌ikely to cause this? Rifa​mpicin Pyrazinamide Isoni‌azid Ethambutol Streptomy​cin","raw_text":"<p>A 47-year-old man on treatment for TB presents with complaints of severe pain and swelling in his left great toe since today morning, as shown in the image below. Which of the following drugs is most likely to cause this?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Rifampicin</li>\n<li>Pyrazinamide</li>\n<li>Isoniazid</li>\n<li>Ethambutol</li>\n<li>Streptomycin</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a‌nd 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3 ‌and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2 and 4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9889e56dc3f147bb8d75b6bca6f81bc5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2 and 4</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and findings of first metatarsophalangeal joint swelling are suggestive of an <strong>acute episode of gout.</strong> Antitubercular drugs that are most likely to cause <strong>hyperuricemia</strong> and precipitate gout are <strong>pyrazinamide </strong>and<strong> ethambutol</strong>.</p>\n<p>Both pyrazinamide and ethambutol <strong>inhibit uric acid excretion</strong> in the kidney causing hyperuricemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0533","text":"A 33-year-old woman receiv‌ing treatment for TB pres‌ents to your OPD with com‍plaints of blurry vision ‍and pain on movement of t​he left eye for the past ​2 days. Examination showe‌d Marcus Gunn pupil. Whic‌h anti-tubercular drug is​ most likely associated w‌ith this condition?","raw_text":"A 33-year-old woman receiving treatment for TB presents to your OPD with complaints of blurry vision and pain on movement of the left eye for the past 2 days. Examination showed Marcus Gunn pupil. Which anti-tubercular drug is most likely associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Etham‌butol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ison‌iazid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ethio‌namide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cyclo‌serine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ethambutol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ethambutol</strong></p><p>The given clinical features along with Marcus Gunn pupil are suggestive of <strong>optic neuritis, </strong>which is a dose-dependent adverse effect associated with <strong>ethambutol</strong>.</p>\n<p>Optic neuritis resulting in <strong>decreased visual acuity</strong> and <strong>loss of red-green discrimination</strong> is the most serious adverse effect of ethambutol. It can be unilateral or bilateral and usually recovers when the drug is stopped.</p>\n<p>Tests of visual acuity and red-green discrimination before the start of therapy and periodically thereafter are recommended.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"_mWkTwI1","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"41_male_reproductive_physiology","title":"41.Male Reproductive Physiology","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"MB6318","text":"A 32 year old lady is unde‌rgoing evaluation for pol​ycystic ovary syndrome. H‍er sex hormone-binding gl‍obulin (SHBG) is low. The​ decrease in sex hormone-‍binding globulin will hav‍e the maximum effect on w​hich of the following hor‌mones?","raw_text":"A 32 year old lady is undergoing evaluation for polycystic ovary syndrome. Her sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG) is low. The decrease in sex hormone-binding globulin will have the maximum effect on which of the following hormones?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estr‌ogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Testos‌terone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"DH‌EA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proges‌terone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Testosterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Testosterone</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The decrease in sex hormone-binding globulin will have the maximum effect on <strong>testosterone.</strong></p>\n<p>Around <strong>98%</strong> of the circulating <strong>testosterone</strong> is <strong>bound</strong> to plasma proteins such as <strong>sex hormone-binding globulin</strong> (65%)<strong> </strong>and <strong>albumin</strong>. So, the decrease in sex-hormone-binding globulin (SHBG) or gonadal steroid-binding globulin (GBG) will have the maximum effect on testosterone levels by <strong>increasing</strong> the levels of its <strong>free form</strong>.</p>\n<p>Therefore patients with <strong>polycystic ovary syndrome</strong> will show features of <strong>hyperandrogenism</strong> (due to elevated free testosterone).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6322","text":"Sertoli cells in testes ha‌ve receptors for______","raw_text":"Sertoli cells in testes have receptors for______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"F‌SH","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"L‌H","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inh‌ibin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gn‌RH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. FSH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. FSH</strong></p><p><strong>Sertoli</strong> cells express <strong>FSH</strong> receptor, whereas <strong>Leydig</strong> cell express <strong>LH</strong> receptor</p>\n<p>Sertoli cells are the true epithelial cells of the seminiferous epithelium and extend from basal lamina to the lumen.</p>\n<p><strong>Functions of Sertoli cells:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Express FSH receptor and androgen receptor.</li>\n<li>Forms the blood-testis barrier.</li>\n<li>Surround the sperm cells and provide structural support within the epithelium.</li>\n<li>Guide sperm cells towards the lumen as they advance to later stages of spermatogenesis.</li>\n<li>Produce inhibin and Mullerian-inhibiting substance (MIS).</li>\n<li>Produce androgen-binding proteins that maintain high androgen levels within the lumen of the seminiferous tubules and the proximal part of the male reproductive tract.</li>\n<li>Perform phagocytic function by engulfing residual bodies (cytoplasm shed by spermatozoa during spermiogenesis) and dead sperm cells.</li>\n<li>Express aromatase, which converts testosterone to potent estrogen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6323","text":"The negative feedback mech‌anism involving the Serto​li cells causes _____","raw_text":"The negative feedback mechanism involving the Sertoli cells causes _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"↓ ‌LH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"↓F‌SH","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"↓T‌RH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"↓C‌RH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ↓FSH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/76b1f291a21e435e8e1e12fe0883aae5x1280x1541.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ↓FSH</strong></p><p>The negative feedback mechanism involving the Sertoli cells causes ↓FSH.</p>\n<p>FSH stimulates inhibin production by the Sertoli cells. This inhibin inhibits FSH production by directly acting on the anterior pituitary. Thus, inhibin keeps FSH levels within a set point.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6325","text":"In men, LH controls secret‌ion of______","raw_text":"In men, LH controls secretion of______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inh‌ibin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A‌MH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Testos‌terone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A‌BP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Testosterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0a6fcaae72654fb5823e80ecd34dfa51x1280x1541.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Testosterone</strong></p><p>In men, LH controls the secretion of<strong> testosterone.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>LH</strong> stimulates <strong>Leydig</strong> cells to secrete <strong>testosterone</strong>, which in turn inhibits LH by a negative feedback mechanism. <strong>FSH</strong> stimulates <strong>Sertoli</strong> cells to secrete inhibin, which in turn inhibits FSH by a negative feedback mechanism.</p>\n<p>Testosterone inhibits LH secretion by acting directly on the anterior pituitary and by inhibiting the secretion of GnRH from the hypothalamus. Inhibin acts directly on the anterior pituitary to inhibit FSH secretion.</p>\n<p>FSH also stimulates the secretion of ABP (androgen-binding protein) and inhibin. FSH and androgens maintain the gametogenic function of the testes. Hypothalamic lesions in animals and hypothalamic disease in humans lead to atrophy of the testes and loss of their function.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6326","text":"Testosterone production is‌ mainly contributed by __​____","raw_text":"Testosterone production is mainly contributed by ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leydig‌ cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sertol‌i cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Seminifero‌us tubules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epid‌ydmis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Leydig cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Leydig cells</strong></p><p>Testosterone production is mainly contributed by <strong>Leydig cells.</strong></p>\n<p>Testosterone is synthesized from cholesterol in the <strong>Leydig cells</strong> and also from androstenedione secreted by the <strong>adrenal cortex.</strong></p>\n<p>The diagnosis of testosterone deficiency should be made only on the basis of clinical signs and symptoms consistent with androgen deficiency, and consistently subnormal serum testosterone concentrations at 8 to 10 AM on three occasions. </p>\n<p>Testosterone has been used to treat primary or secondary hypogonadism in adult men.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6327","text":"All of the following are a‌ndrogens except______","raw_text":"All of the following are androgens except______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Testos‌terone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dihydrote‌stosterone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Androst‌enedione","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"17α hydroxy-‌progesterone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 17α hydroxy-progesterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 17α hydroxy-progesterone</strong></p><p><strong>17α hydroxy-progesterone</strong> is a progestin, not androgen.</p>\n<p>The term <strong>androgen</strong> means any steroid hormone that has masculinizing effects.  Testosterone, Dihydrotestosterone, androstenedione, and dehydroepiandrosterone are androgens.</p>\n<p>The sites of production:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Testes.</li>\n<li>Adrenal gland.</li>\n<li>Ovary (not significant).</li>\n<li>Tumors of the adrenal androgen-producing cells etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both in the testes and in the adrenals, the androgens can be synthesized either from cholesterol or directly from acetyl coenzyme A.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6328","text":"Which of the following cel‌ls secrete anti mullerian​ hormone?","raw_text":"Which of the following cells secrete anti mullerian hormone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Theca‌ cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leydig‌ cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sertoli cells and Granulos‌a cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Muller‌ cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sertoli cells and Granulosa cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sertoli cells and Granulosa cells</strong></p><p>(Anti Mullerian Hormone) is secreted by both <strong>sertoli cells and granulosa cells.</strong></p>\n<p>Sertoli cells produce Anti Mullerian Hormone (Mullerian inhibitory substance) during development. It induces the regression of the mullerian duct that is programmed to give rise to a female reproductive tract.</p>\n<p>Granulosa cells of the ovary produce AMH during the reproductive years. It limits the formation of primary follicles by inhibiting excessive follicular recruitment by FSH.<sup class=\"reference\" id=\"cite_ref-pmid14742691_15-1\"><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anti-M%C3%BCllerian_hormone#cite_note-pmid14742691-15\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"></a></sup></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6329","text":"The blood-testis barrier i‌s formed by______","raw_text":"The blood-testis barrier is formed by______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tight junctions between ad‌jacent leydig cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tight junctions between ad‌jacent sertoli cells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Basal lamina and spermatog‌onia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Basal lamina and leydig ce‌lls","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tight junctions between adjacent sertoli cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/28febd4ff4164c478212ba8ee4de8b02x410x360.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tight junctions between adjacent sertoli cells</strong></p><p>The blood-testis barrier is formed by the <strong>tight junctions between adjacent sertoli cells.</strong></p>\n<p>This Sertoli-Sertoli cell tight junctions divide the seminiferous epithelium into 2 compartments:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Basal compartment - contains the spermatogonia and early-stage primary spermatocytes.</li>\n<li>Adluminal compartment - contains later-stage primary spermatocytes and all subsequent stages of sperm cells.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It imparts a selective permeability, allowing few substances such as testosterone to cross but preventing noxious substances that might damage the developing sperm. It also protects sperms from antibodies by creating a specialized, immunologically safe microenvironment for developing sperm.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>BELOW DIAGRAM - </p>\n<div class=\"description mw-content-ltr en\" dir=\"ltr\" lang=\"en\">1 basal lamina, 2 spermatogonia, 3 spermatocyte 1st order, 4 spermatocyte 2nd order, 5 spermatid, 6 mature spermatid, 7 Sertoli cell, 8 tight junctions(Blood- Testis Barrier)</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6331","text":"Spermiogenesis refers to__‌_____","raw_text":"Spermiogenesis refers to_______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Formation of spermatozoa f‌rom spermatogonia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Formation of spermatozoa f‌rom spermatid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Formation of spermatids fr‌om sperm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Formation of secondary spe‌rmatocyte from primary sp​ermatocyte","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Formation of spermatozoa from spermatid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ec32d123b59408092c50959cf7c0429x1280x1889.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ae67bb2c412547e8b52515b01c229d84x606x559.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Formation of spermatozoa from spermatid</strong></p><p><strong>Spermiogenesis</strong> refers to the <strong>formation of spermatozoa from spermatids.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Spermiogenesis</strong> is a process by which there is a formation of extremely specialized mobile spermatozoa from spermatids. This process requires extensive remodeling or packaging of cell elements. It occurs in the recesses formed by invaginations of the sertoli cell plasma membrane.</p>\n<p>Maturation from spermatids to spermatozoa depends on androgen acting on sertoli cells. When sperm maturation is complete, the cytoplasm of the sertoli cell around sperm retracts and sperms are released into the lumen to be bathed by the luminal fluid.</p>\n<p>The following changes occur during the formation ofspermatozoafrom spermatids :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The nucleus becomes eccentric and decreases in size, and the chromatin becomes condensed.</li>\n<li>The cytoplasmic content is redistributed and discarded.</li>\n<li>The acrosome buds from the golgi apparatus.</li>\n<li>The centrioles migrate to the caudal pole and form a long axial filament made of microtubules. This becomes the axoneme or major portion of the tail.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Spermiation</strong> is the release of the sperm from the sertoli cells. During spermiation, most of the remaining cytoplasm is shed in the form of residual bodies.</p>\n<p><strong>Spermatogenesis</strong> refers to the formation of<strong> spermatozoa from spermatogonia.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6332","text":"Development of spermatozoa‌ from spermatogonium take​s how much time?","raw_text":"Development of spermatozoa from spermatogonium takes how much time?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30-35‌ days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"40-45‌ days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"50-55‌ days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"70-75‌ days","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 70-75 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 70-75 days</strong></p><p>The time required to produce mature spermatozoa from the earliest stage of spermatogonia is <strong>65 to 70 days.</strong></p>\n<p>Since several developmental stages of spermatogenic cells occur during this time frame, the stages are collectively known as the <strong>spermatogenic cycle</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6333","text":"Spermatogenesis is maintai‌ned by which hormone?","raw_text":"Spermatogenesis is maintained by which hormone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Testos‌terone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"F‌SH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"L‌H","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All of the above</strong></p><p>All three of them (testosterone, FSH, LH) are required for spermatogenesis.</p>\n<p>Pulses of hypothalamic GnRH stimulate the anterior pituitary to secrete FSH and LH, which then act on the testes</p>\n<p>FSH acts on the Sertoli cells to stimulate spermatogenesis and inhibin secretion. LH acts on the Leydig cells to stimulate testosterone secretion. Testosterone, acting locally on the Sertoli cells, is essential for maintaining spermatogenesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6340","text":"Role of growth hormone in ‌spermatogenesis is ______​_","raw_text":"Role of growth hormone in spermatogenesis  is _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stimulation of sertoli cel‌ls","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Early division of spermato‌gonia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Formation ‌of acrosome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stimulation of leydig cell‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Early division of spermatogonia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Early division of spermatogonia</strong></p><p>The growth hormone specifically promotes the <strong>early division of spermatogonia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Hormonal factors that stimulate spermatogenesis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Testosterone</strong>, secreted by the Leydig cells of the testis, is essential for growth and division of the testicular germinal cells, which is the first stage in forming sperm.</li>\n<li><strong>Luteinizing hormone,</strong> secreted by the anterior pituitary gland, stimulates the Leydig cells to secrete testosterone.</li>\n<li><strong>Follicle-stimulating hormone</strong>, secreted by the anterior pituitary gland, stimulates the Sertoli cells. Without this stimulation, the spermiogenesis will not occur.</li>\n<li><strong>Estrogens</strong>, formed from testosterone by the Sertoli cells when stimulated by FSH, are also essential for spermiogenesis.</li>\n<li><strong>Growth hormone</strong> is necessary for controlling the background metabolic functions of the testes. It specifically promotes early division of the spermatogonia themselves. In its absence, as in pituitary dwarfs, spermatogenesis is severely deficient or absent, thus causing infertility.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6343","text":"Which of the following pro‌vides a negative feedback​ in spermatogenesis?","raw_text":"Which of the following provides a negative feedback in spermatogenesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ABP( androgen- binding pro‌tein )","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inh‌ibin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Proges‌terone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gn‌RH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inhibin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0610ebc2969048d186b2f5bb4b061cf7x1280x1709.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inhibin</strong></p><p>Negative feedback in spermatogenesis is by<strong> inhibin.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 439\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>FSH stimulates inhibin production by the Sertoli cells. This inhibin inhibits FSH production by directly acting on the anterior pituitary. </p>\n<p>LH stimulates Leydig cells to secrete testosterone, which in turn inhibits LH. Testosterone inhibits LH secretion by acting directly on the anterior pituitary and by inhibiting the secretion of gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) from the hypothalamus.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6344","text":"A 35-year-old man is being‌ evaluated for suspected ​infertility. His semen an‍alysis shows a decrease i‍n sperm count and motilit​y. On physical examinatio‍n, a 'bag of worms'-like ‍structure is palpated in ​his scrotum, suggestive o‌f varicocele. Which of th​e following is the most l‍ikely reason behind his d‍ecreased sperm count?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man is being evaluated for suspected infertility. His semen analysis shows a decrease in sperm count and motility. On physical examination, a 'bag of worms'-like structure is palpated in his scrotum, suggestive of varicocele. Which of the following is the most likely reason behind his decreased sperm count?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lowered scrotal temperatur‌e, compared to core tempe​rature","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rise in scrotal temperatur‌e, compared to core tempe​rature","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Blockage in epididymal duc‌ts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blockage in ‌vas deferens","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rise in scrotal temperature, compared to core temperature","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rise in scrotal temperature, compared to core temperature</strong></p><p>The most likely reason behind <strong>decreased sperm</strong> count in this patient is <strong>rise</strong> in <strong>scrotal temperature</strong>, secondary to varicocoele.</p>\n<p>Normally, the testes occupy the scrotum, which lies outside the body cavity and is maintained at 35° to 36°C, or <strong>1° </strong>to<strong> 2°C</strong> <strong>below</strong> body temperature. This<strong> lower temperature</strong> is <strong>essential</strong> for normal <strong>spermatogenesis</strong>. It is maintained by a countercurrent arrangement of testicular arteries and veins, which facilitates heat exchange. </p>\n<p>Enlarged veins around the testes, called varicoceles, cause infertility by <strong>raising </strong>the<strong> temperature </strong>in the<strong> scrotum </strong>and<strong> decreasing sperm production</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6346","text":"Sperm acquires motility in‌______","raw_text":"Sperm acquires motility in______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seminal‌ vesicle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tes‌tes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epidi‌dymis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ejaculat‌ory duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Epididymis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Epididymis</strong></p><p>Sperm is stored in the epididymis, where they undergo a process of maturation before they are capable of progressive motility and fertilization.</p>\n<p><strong>Changes during the sperm maturation in the epididymis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Progressive increase in forward motility  </li>\n<li>Increase ability to fertilize  </li>\n<li>Maturation of acrosome  </li>\n<li>Molecular reorganization of plasma membrane: Lipid (stabilization of plasma membrane) protein (shedding as well as the acquisition of new proteins)</li>\n<li>Ability to bind to zona pellucida  </li>\n<li>Acquisition of receptors for proteins of the zona pellucida  </li>\n<li>Increase disulfide bond between cysteine residues in sperm nucleoproteins  </li>\n<li>Topographic regionalization of glycosidic residues  </li>\n<li>Accumulation of mannosylated residues on periacrosomal plasma membrane  </li>\n<li>Decreased cytoplasm and cell volume</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6347","text":"At which location does the‌ capacitation of sperm ta​ke place?","raw_text":"At which location does the capacitation of sperm take place?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seminifero‌us tubules","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Uterin‌e tubes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epid‌ydmis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ejaculat‌ory duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Uterine tubes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Uterine tubes</strong></p><p><strong>Capacitation</strong> of sperm takes place in <strong>uterine tubes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Once ejaculated into the female genital tract, the spermatozoa move up the uterus to the isthmus of the uterine tubes, where they slow down and undergo final conditioning called <strong>capacitation</strong>. </p>\n<p>This process involves two components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increasing the motility of the spermatozoa</li>\n<li>Facilitating their preparation for the acrosomal reaction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6350","text":"Which of the following fac‌tors increases sperm moti​lity?","raw_text":"Which of the following factors increases sperm motility?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"↑ Temp‌erature","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acidic en‌vironment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vaginal s‌ecretions","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"While passing through semi‌niferous tubule","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vaginal secretions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vaginal secretions</strong></p><p>Sperm motility is increased by <strong>vaginal secretions</strong>.</p>\n<p>On coming in contact with the fluids of the female genital tract, multiple changes occur that activate the sperm for the final processes of fertilization. These collective changes are called <strong>capacitation </strong>of the spermatozoa, which normally requires from <strong>1 to 10 hours</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following changes are believed to occur:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The uterine and fallopian tube fluids wash away the various inhibitory factors that suppress sperm activity in the male genital ducts.</li>\n<li>While the spermatozoa remain in the fluid of the male genital ducts, they are continually exposed to many floating vesicles from the seminiferous tubules containing large amounts of cholesterol. This cholesterol is continually added to the cellular membrane covering the sperm acrosome, toughening this membrane and preventing the release of its enzymes.</li>\n<li>After ejaculation, the sperm deposited in the vagina swim away from the cholesterol vesicles upward into the uterine cavity, and they gradually lose much of their other excess cholesterol during the next few hours. In doing so, the membrane at the head of the sperm (the acrosome) becomes much weaker.</li>\n<li>The membrane of the sperm also becomes much more permeable to calcium ions, so calcium now enters the sperm in abundance and changes the activity of the flagellum, giving it a powerful whiplash motion in contrast to its previously weak undulating motion. In addition, the calcium ions cause changes in the cellular membrane that cover the leading edge of the acrosome, making it possible for the acrosome to release its enzymes rapidly and easily as the sperm penetrates the granulosa cell mass surrounding the ovum, and even more so as it attempts to penetrate the zona pellucida of the ovum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6352","text":"A 70-year-old man sufferin‌g from prostate cancer is​ undergoing long-term and‍rogen deprivation therapy‍. He is being treated wit​h long acting GnRH agonis‍t. After prolonged inject‍ion of GnRH, which of the​ following will most like‌ly be seen in this patien​t?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old man suffering from prostate cancer is undergoing long-term androgen deprivation therapy. He is being treated with long acting GnRH agonist. After prolonged injection of GnRH, which of the following will most likely be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"↑ ‌FSH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"↓ ‌LH","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"↑ spermi‌ogenesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"↑ Testo‌sterone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ↓ LH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ↓ LH</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Prolonged</strong> injection of<strong> GnRH</strong> will lead to<strong> decreased LH </strong>levels<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A constant administration of GnRH produces an <strong>initial</strong> agonistic action known as \"<strong>flare</strong>\", followed by a <strong>downregulation</strong> of GnRH receptors ( decrease in GnRH receptor concentration). This <strong>desensitizes pituitary</strong> to the action of GnRH and a <strong>hypogonadotropic-hypogonadal</strong> state is induced.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">This is characterized by <strong>low LH</strong>, <strong>low FSH level</strong>s and thereby <strong>reduced testosterone</strong> production (i.e., chemical castration).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6353","text":"The major contribution to ‌semen is from which of th​e following organs?","raw_text":"The major contribution to semen is from which of the following organs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seminal‌ vesicle","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cowper‌s gland","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pros‌tate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urethra‌l gland","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Seminal vesicle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Seminal vesicle</strong></p><p>The major contribution to semen is from <strong>seminal vesicles.</strong></p>\n<p>The seminal vesicles contribute ~<strong>60-70% </strong>of the <strong>volume</strong> of semen. Apart from sperm, the remaining 20-40% represents epididymal fluids, as well as secretions of the prostate gland and bulbourethral glands.</p>\n<p>The seminal plasma originates primarily from the accessory glands (the seminal vesicles, the prostate gland, and the bulbourethral glands).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6355","text":"Normal pH of human semen i‌s_____","raw_text":"Normal pH of human semen is_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌7","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6‌.5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7‌.4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌8","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 7.4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 7.4</strong></p><p>The normal pH of human semen is<strong> 7.4</strong> (isotonic).</p>\n<p>The pH in the lumen of the epididymis is relatively acidic (6.5 to 6.8) as the result of H+ secretion by clear cells that are analogous to intercalated cells in the nephron. Addition of the relatively alkaline secretions of the seminal vesicles raises the final pH of seminal plasma to between<strong> 7.2 and 7.8.</strong></p>\n<p>Spermatozoa generally tolerate alkalinity better than acidity.</p>\n<p>A pH near neutrality or slightly higher is optimal for the motility and survival of sperm cells in humans and in other species as well.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6356","text":"Prostaglandins in semen ar‌e secreted by which of th​e following organs?","raw_text":"Prostaglandins in semen are secreted by which of the following organs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seminal‌ vesicle","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cowper‌s gland","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pros‌tate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urethra‌l gland","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Seminal vesicle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Seminal vesicle</strong></p><p>Prostaglandins in semen are secreted by <strong>seminal vesicle.</strong></p>\n<p>The seminal vesicles secrete fluid that is rich in<strong> fructose, citrate, prostaglandins, </strong>and <strong>fibrinogen.</strong></p>\n<p>The prostaglandins present in the seminal fluid may assist in fertilization in two ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prostaglandins react with <strong>cervical mucus</strong> to make it more <strong>penetrable</strong> by <strong>sperm</strong>.</li>\n<li>Prostaglandins induce <strong>peristaltic contractions</strong> in the <strong>female reproductive tract</strong> (i.e., the uterus and fallopian tubes) to propel the sperm up the tract.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6360","text":"A couple is undergoing eva‌luation for their inabili​ty to conceive. In order ‍to rule out male factor i‍nfertility, the consultan​t orders a semen analysis‍ for the husband. Which o‍f the following best esti​mates the normal sperm co‌unt in an adult?","raw_text":"A couple is undergoing evaluation for their inability to conceive. In order to rule out male factor infertility, the consultant orders a semen analysis for the husband. Which of the following best estimates the normal sperm count in an adult?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20-40 mi‌llion/mL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"40-60 mi‌llion/mL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"60-80 mi‌llion/mL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"90-120 m‌illion/mL","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 90-120 million/mL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 90-120 million/mL</strong></p><p>Normal sperm count on an <strong>average</strong> is 100 million/ml <strong>(90-120 million/mL</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: The WHO 2010 minimal criteria for semen analysis requires a <strong>minimum</strong> sperm count <strong>&gt;15 million/mL</strong> or <strong>&gt;40 million/per ejaculate</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6362","text":"In which part of the fallo‌pian tube does fertilizat​ion take place?","raw_text":"In which part of the fallopian tube does fertilization take place?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intersti‌tial part","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ist‌hmus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fim‌bria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amp‌ulla","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ampulla","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ampulla</strong></p><p>In humans, fertilization of the ovum by the sperm usually occurs in the <strong>ampulla </strong>of the fallopian tube.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6365","text":"For how many hours does hu‌man sperm remain fertile ​in the female genital tra‍ct?","raw_text":"For how many hours does human sperm remain fertile in the female genital tract?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6-‌8 h","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"12-‌24 h","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"24-‌48 h","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"72-‌96 h","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 24-48 h","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 24-48 h</strong></p><p>After ejaculation, sperms have a maximal lifespan of <strong>24-48</strong> hours in the female genital tract.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 977\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The activity and rate of metabolism of sperm increase markedly with increas­ing temperature causing its life to be considerably shortened.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Sperm can be <strong>cryopreserved</strong> for <strong>years</strong> if agents such as <strong>glycerol</strong> are used to prevent ice crystal formation during freezing.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> The ovum remains viable and capable of being fertilized, probably no longer than 24 hours after it is expelled from the ovary.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6368","text":"For how long does the ovum‌ remain viable following ​ovulation?","raw_text":"For how long does the ovum remain viable following ovulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"24 h‌ours","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"48 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"72 h‌ours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 24 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 24 hours</strong></p><p>The ovum remains viable up to <strong>24hours</strong> following ovulation.</p>\n<p>If not fertilized, the ovum degenerates in the fallopian tube without leaving behind any trace after 24 hours.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6371","text":"Which of the following hor‌mones has no role for the​ attainment of puberty?","raw_text":"Which of the following hormones has no role for the attainment of puberty?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"G‌H","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lep‌tin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Testos‌terone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ins‌ulin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Insulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Insulin</strong></p><p>Insulin has no role in the attainment of puberty. <strong>GH, leptin </strong>and<strong> testosterone</strong> are all required for the onset of puberty.</p>\n<p>The onset of puberty is marked by enhanced androgenic activity. The growth spurt of adolescent males is influenced by a complex interplay between <strong>androgens</strong>, <strong>growth hormone</strong> (GH),<strong> nutrition</strong>, and <strong>genetic factors</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Leptin,</strong> the <strong>satiety-producing</strong> hormone secreted by fat cells, may be the link between <strong>body weight</strong> and <strong>puberty</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6372","text":"Which of the following hor‌mones is responsible for ​pubarche?","raw_text":"Which of the following hormones is responsible for pubarche?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"G‌H","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proges‌terone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Testos‌terone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Estr‌ogen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Testosterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Testosterone</strong></p><p><strong>Pubarche</strong> or the development of axillary and pubic hair is due to <strong>testosterone.</strong></p>\n<p>The growth of pubic and axillary hair <strong>in both sexes</strong> is primarily due to androgens.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6373","text":"Which of the following hor‌mones play a permissive r​ole in the attainment of ‍puberty?","raw_text":"Which of the following hormones play a permissive role in the attainment of puberty?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gn‌RH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lep‌tin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ins‌ulin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"G‌H","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Leptin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Leptin</strong></p><p>The hormone that plays a permissive role in the attainment of puberty is <strong>leptin.</strong></p>\n<p>It now appears that <strong>leptin</strong> may be the<strong> link</strong> between <strong>body weight</strong> and <strong>puberty. </strong>Leptin is a <strong>satiety-producing</strong> hormone <strong>secreted by adipocytes</strong>. High leptin levels <strong>reduce appetite </strong>and <strong>increase energy expenditure.</strong></p>\n<p>Evidence for which is given by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Obese ob/ob mice that cannot make leptin are infertile, and their fertility is restored by injections of leptin.</li>\n<li>Leptin treatment also induces precocious puberty in immature female mice.</li>\n<li>However, the way that leptin fits into the overall control of puberty remains to be determined.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Leptin also accelerates GnRH pulsatility in neurons, fastening puberty.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"UYXAqMAV","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"41_prostate","title":"41.Prostate","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MA4644","text":"Which of the following are​ storage symptoms of BPH?‍ Urgency Enuresis Hesitan​cy Frequency Dribbling","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are storage symptoms of BPH?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Urgency</li>\n<li>Enuresis</li>\n<li>Hesitancy</li>\n<li>Frequency</li>\n<li>Dribbling</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ​3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a​nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2,​ 4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ​2, 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 4</strong></p><p><strong>Urgency</strong>, <strong>enuresis</strong> and <strong>frequency</strong> are storage symptoms.</p>\n<p>Lower urinary tract symptoms (LUTS) can be classified as:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 67px; width: 372px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 222.381px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Storage</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.619px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Voiding</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 222.381px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Frequency </strong>(earliest symptom in BPH)</li>\n<li>Nocturia</li>\n<li>Urgency</li>\n<li>Urge incontinence</li>\n<li>Nocturnal incontinence (enuresis)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169.619px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Hesitancy</li>\n<li>Poor flow</li>\n<li>Intermittent stream</li>\n<li>Dribbling</li>\n<li>Sensation of poor bladder emptying</li>\n<li>Episodes of near retention</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Storage</strong> symptoms are seen in conditions where the bladder is <strong>irritated</strong>. <strong>Voiding</strong> symptoms are common in conditions causing bladder outlet <strong>obstruction</strong>. In <strong>benign prostatic hyperplasia</strong>, both <strong>storage</strong> and <strong>voiding</strong> symptoms are present.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3485","text":"A 60-year-old man presente​d with fever, chills, and‍ dysuria. On per-rectal e​xamination, his prostate ‌was tender and boggy. His‍ PSA level was 7.4 ng/dl.‍ What is the next best st‍ep in the management of t​his patient?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presented with fever, chills, and dysuria. On per-rectal examination, his prostate was tender and boggy. His PSA level was 7.4 ng/dl. What is the next best step in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Repea​t PSA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Catheterization and prosta​tic massage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"TURP-guid​ed biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antibioti​c therapy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Antibiotic therapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Antibiotic therapy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> acute bacterial prostatitis </strong>and should be managed by<strong> antibiotics.</strong></p>\n<p>A <strong>rigorous</strong> and <strong>prolonged </strong>treatment<strong> </strong>for<strong> 2-6 weeks</strong> with an <strong>antibiotic</strong> that penetrates the prostate wall is indicated (<strong>trimethoprim,</strong> <strong>ciprofloxacin or aminoglycoside</strong>). Incomplete infection eradication lead to recurrent attacks and chronic prostatitis.</p>\n<p><strong>Acute bacterial prostatitis </strong>is usually caused by an ascending urinary tract infection (UTI) or reflux of infected urine into prostatic ducts. The most common organism is<strong><em> Escherichia coli</em>. </strong>An associated increase in levels of PSA(&gt;4ng/ml) may be present due to prostate inflammation.</p>\n<p><strong>Catheterization</strong> and <strong>prostatic massage</strong> are <strong>contraindicated </strong>in patients with suspected acute prostatitis, as they increase the chance for bacteremia and sepsis. Instead, suprapubic tap should be performed to alleviate retention.</p>\n<p>Chronic bacterial prostatitis is the sequela of inadequately treated acute prostatitis.<br/>Diagnosis includes: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Three-glass urine test</strong> - If the first glass with the initial urine sample is clear, but the <strong>second</strong> and <strong>third</strong> samples contain <strong>prostatic threads </strong>and <strong>leukocytes</strong>, it indicates the presence of <strong>prostatitis</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Digital rectal examination - Prostate may be normal, boggy, or tender.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Prostatic fluid examination</strong> - <strong>post-prostatic massage</strong> fluid shows pus cells and bacteria.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3254","text":"Which is the most common o​rganism causing acute bac‍terial prostatitis?","raw_text":"Which is the most common organism causing acute bacterial prostatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Entero​coccus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pro​teus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococcu​s Agalacticia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"E.c​oli","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. E.coli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. E.coli</strong></p><p>The most common organism causing acute bacterial prostatitis (ABP) is <strong><em>Escherichia coli</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>It is commonly caused by an ascending urethral infection or reflux of infected urine into prostatic ducts. <strong>Sexually transmissible infections</strong> and other urinary pathogens, such as <span style=\"color: #000000;\"><em><strong>Staphylococcus aureus </strong>and<strong> Streptococcus faecalis</strong></em> </span>may also be responsible.</p>\n<p>The patient presents with fever, perineal pain, chills and rigor, and dysuria. Per rectal examination will reveal a <strong>tender, boggy prostate</strong>. Care should be taken to avoid aggressive palpation or prostatic massage as it can lead to <strong>bacterial dissemination</strong> and <strong>sepsis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Urine examination and culture should be done. Urine examination reveals <strong>threads</strong> in urine.</p>\n<p>Management:  </p>\n<p>Patients with systemic signs of infection should be admitted for IV antibiotics, hydration, and monitoring of laboratory parameters.</p>\n<p>Patients who have no signs of systemic infection, who can tolerate oral intake, and who have no urinary retention can be treated with oral antibiotics. Antibiotics are given for <strong>2-6 weeks</strong> to ensure the eradication of the infection as antibiotics don't penetrate the prostate capsule easily. </p>\n<p>For outpatient therapy: <strong>Ciprofloxacin</strong> 500 mg oral every 12 hours or <strong>levofloxacin</strong> 500 mg OD or <strong>trimethoprim</strong>-<strong>sulfamethoxazole</strong> double-strength tablet oral every 12 hours can be used.</p>\n<p>For hospitalized patients: Intravenous <strong>ciprofloxacin</strong> or <strong>levofloxacin</strong> with or without an <strong>aminoglycoside</strong> (gentamicin or tobramycin) or intravenous <strong>beta-lactam</strong> with or without an aminoglycoside. Gram-positive infections are treated with cephalosporins (<strong>cephalexin</strong>, <strong>cefazolin</strong>) or <strong>dicloxacillin</strong> and <strong>vancomycin</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4654","text":"Which of following compoun​ds form endogenous prosta‍tic calculi?","raw_text":"Which of following compounds form endogenous prostatic calculi?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calcium​ oxalate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calcium ​phosphate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stru​vite","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uric​ acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Calcium phosphate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c99e9cf786964808b0a911fa2c4e0fcex440x293.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Calcium phosphate</strong></p><p><strong>Endogenous</strong> calculi are most commonly composed of <strong>calcium phosphate.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exogenous</strong> calculi are <strong>urinary calculi </strong>that are arrested in the prostatic urethra. They are relatively rare.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Corpora amylacea</strong> are benign histological findings in prostate glands. <strong>Prostatic calculi</strong> are thought to develop from the calcification of corpora amylacea.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4648","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding the medic‍al management of benign p​rostatic hyperplasia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding the medical management of benign prostatic hyperplasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5-alpha-reductase inhibito​rs take at least 6 months‍ to act.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alpha blockers act quicker​.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tamsulosin can cause abnor​mal ejaculation.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5-alpha-reductase inhibito​rs act best when the glan‍d is small.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors act best when the gland is small.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors act best when the gland is small.</strong></p><p>5-alpha-reductase inhibitors act best when the prostate is <strong>large</strong>(&gt;50g)<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>The pathogenesis of BPH has a static and dynamic component responsible for the lower urinary tract symptoms. Conversion of <strong>testosterone to DHT</strong> is responsible for prostatic stromal hypertrophy which is the<strong> static</strong> component of BPH. The <strong>contraction of smooth muscles</strong> in the prostate is considered the <strong>dynamic</strong> component of BPH. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 115px; width: 759px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 191px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\"><strong>Alpha-blockers</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\"><strong> 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 191px;\"><strong>Mechanism of action</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">Inhibit the <strong>contraction of smooth muscles</strong> in the prostate.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">Inhibit the <strong>conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone (DHT) </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 191px;\"><strong>Onset of action</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">Work <strong>quickly</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">To be taken for at least <strong>6 months</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 191px;\"><strong>Example</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">Tamsulosin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">Finasteride</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 191px;\"><strong>Adverse effects</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 300px;\">\n<p>Dizziness (due to postural hypotension)</p>\n<p>Abnormal ejaculation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">\n<p>Dizziness</p>\n<p>Impotence </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4650","text":"Which of the following fin​dings is not an indicatio‍n to perform prostatectom​y in patients with benign‌ prostatic hyperplasia?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings is not an indication to perform prostatectomy in patients with benign prostatic hyperplasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bladder d​iverticula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Recurrent urinary tract in​fections","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Residual vol​ume of 200 ml","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased frequency of mic​turition","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Increased frequency of micturition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Increased frequency of micturition</strong></p><p><strong>Increased frequency </strong>alone is <strong>not</strong> an <strong>indication</strong> for <strong>prostatectomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Increasing difficulty in micturition, with considerable frequency during both day and night, delay in starting, and a poor stream are the usual symptoms for which surgery is advised.</p>\n<p><strong>Other indications</strong> include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute retention</li>\n<li>Chronic retention and renal impairment\n<ul>\n<li>Residual urine of <strong>≥200 mL</strong> </li>\n<li>Raised blood urea</li>\n<li>Hydroureter or hydronephrosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Complications of bladder outflow obstruction\n<ul>\n<li>Stone</li>\n<li><strong>Infection</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Diverticuli</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Hemorrhage - Venous bleeding from a ruptured vein overlying the prostate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4651","text":"Identify the approach for ​surgery of the prostate a‍s shown in the image:","raw_text":"Identify the approach for surgery of the prostate as shown in the image:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Young a​pproach","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Freyer ​approach","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Millin ​approach","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transurethr​al approach","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Freyer approach","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f1221830191e4c9bb9f5b71a1fb77f29.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c3a43024c64b4b5b8e02a306610816e4x1280x1605.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Freyer approach</strong></p><p>The image shows the <strong>transvesical approach</strong>, also known as <strong>Freyer</strong> approach.</p>\n<p>In this approach, the bladder is opened and the prostate<strong> </strong>is enucleated by putting a finger into the urethra, pushing forward towards the pubes to separate the lateral lobes, and then working the finger between the adenoma and the false capsule.</p>\n<p>Among the other approaches, TURP is most commonly performed.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4655","text":"As a first-year surgery re​sident, you have scrubbed‍ in to assist a urologist​ on a TURP procedure. Up ‌to what distal anatomical‍ landmark do you expect h‍im to resect?","raw_text":"As a first-year surgery resident, you have scrubbed in to assist a urologist on a TURP procedure. Up to what distal anatomical landmark do you expect him to resect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"External urethral sphincte​r","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prostati​c utricle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bladde​r neck","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Verumo​ntanum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Verumontanum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ebefeebb71af4c3a90a6522f36a26ecfx1280x1440.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Verumontanum</strong></p><p><strong>Verumontanum</strong> is used as the <strong>distal-most border</strong> of the resection in transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP). This <strong>prevents damage</strong> to the<strong> external urethral sphincter</strong> which is located just distal to the verumontanum.</p>\n<p><strong>Damage</strong> to the <strong>external urethral sphincter</strong> leads to <strong>urinary incontinence</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>bladder neck</strong> is the <strong>proximal</strong> limit of resection.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3505","text":"A patient in your ward is ​scheduled for monopolar-T‍URP. Which of the given f​luids should be used for ‌irrigation during the pro‍cedure?","raw_text":"A patient in your ward is scheduled for monopolar-TURP. Which of the given fluids should be used for irrigation during the procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.5 % ​glycine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Normal​ saline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"N/5 s​aline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Distill​ed water","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1.5 % glycine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1.5 % glycine</strong></p><p>The <strong>best irrigating fluid</strong> for <strong>M-TURP</strong> is <strong>1.5% isotonic glycine.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>M-TURP </strong>removes tissue with a wire loop that has an electrical current flowing in one direction (monopolar). The surgical site is irrigated with a non-conducting non-ionic fluid as the use of an <strong>ionic solution</strong> leads to dissipation of the cutting current and poor cutting efficacy. Thus, <strong>normal saline</strong> and <strong>N/5 saline</strong> cannot be used.</p>\n<p>The use of <strong>distilled water</strong> in TURP leads to the <strong>water-intoxication syndrome</strong>. </p>\n<p>Bipolar transurethral resection of the prostate (<strong>B-TURP</strong>) uses a specialized resecting loop that incorporates both the active and the return portions of the circuit on the same electrode and an ionic irrigating solution such as <strong>normal saline</strong> can be used <strong>safely</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3507","text":"A 70-year-old patient with​ benign prostatic hypertr‍ophy underwent TURP. An h​our later, he was found t‌o have an altered sensori‍um and had several bouts ‍of vomiting. What is the ‍most probable cause?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old patient with benign prostatic hypertrophy underwent TURP.  An hour later, he was found to have an altered sensorium and had several bouts of vomiting. What is the most probable cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Overdose of spinal anesthe​tic agent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypern​atremia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperk​alemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Water int​oxication","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Water intoxication","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Water intoxication</strong></p><p>The most probable cause for the above-mentioned symptoms is <strong>water intoxication</strong> also known as transurethral resection of the prostate (<strong>TURP</strong>)<strong> syndrome</strong>. </p>\n<p>Absorption of water into the circulation at the time of transurethral urethral resection can lead to :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hyponatremia</strong>, which may manifest as confusion, nausea, and vomiting</li>\n<li>Congestive cardiac failure</li>\n<li>Hemolysis </li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is managed by fluid restriction.</p>\n<p>The use of <strong>isotonic glycine</strong> for irrigation during resection reduces the incidence of water intoxication.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4656","text":"A man presents with a hist​ory of minimal ejaculatio‍n at sexual climax and pa​ssing cloudy urine after.‌ He is a known diabetic a‍nd underwent TURP 6 month‍s ago. What is the mechan‍ism leading to this compl​ication?","raw_text":"A man presents with a history of minimal ejaculation at sexual climax and passing cloudy urine after. He is a known diabetic and underwent TURP 6 months ago. What is the mechanism leading to this complication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Injury to the external ure​thral sphincter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Disruption of the autonomi​c nerve supply to the cor‍pora of the penis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Over use of coagulating di​athermy during the proced‍ure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bladder nec​k disruption","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bladder neck disruption","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bladder neck disruption</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario above suggests the patient has <strong>retrograde ejaculation</strong>. The most likely mechanism of its development is <strong>disruption of the bladder neck</strong>. </p>\n<p>Retrograde ejaculation is the <strong>most common</strong> complication of transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP). </p>\n<p>Option A: Injury to the external urethral sphincter causes <strong>incontinence</strong> following TURP. </p>\n<p>Option B: Disruption of the autonomic nerve supply to the corpora of the penis causes<strong> impotence</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Overuse of coagulating diathermy following TURP causes <strong>bladder neck contracture. </strong></p>\n<p>Other complications of TURP include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Transurethral resection of prostate (TUR) syndrome </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Primary and secondary hemorrhage</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Perforation of the bladder or the prostatic capsule </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sepsis </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Urethral stricture</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"c-virtual_list__item\" data-qa=\"virtual-list-item\" id=\"1636620480.003700\" role=\"listitem\" tabindex=\"0\">\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__background p-message_pane_message__message c-message_kit__message\" data-qa=\"message_container\" data-qa-placeholder=\"false\" data-qa-unprocessed=\"false\" role=\"presentation\">\n<div aria-roledescription=\"message\" class=\"c-message_kit__hover\" data-qa-hover=\"true\" role=\"document\">\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__actions c-message_kit__actions--above\">\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__gutter\">\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__gutter__right\" data-qa=\"message_content\" role=\"presentation\">\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__blocks c-message_kit__blocks--rich_text\">\n<div class=\"c-message__message_blocks c-message__message_blocks--rich_text\">\n<div class=\"p-block_kit_renderer\" data-qa=\"block-kit-renderer\">\n<div class=\"p-block_kit_renderer__block_wrapper p-block_kit_renderer__block_wrapper--first\">\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_block\" dir=\"auto\">\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_section\">Note:Transurethral microwave thermotherapy (TUMT), Transurethral needle ablation (TUNA), and laser vaporization are less invasive methods used for the treatment of BPH. They have a decreased incidence of ejaculatory dysfunction and hemorrhage.</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4647","text":"On per rectal examination,​ which of the following f‍indings will help you ide​ntify a malignant prostat‌e lesion?","raw_text":"On per rectal examination, which of the following findings will help you identify a malignant prostate lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Smooth ​surface","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Firm con​sistency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rectal mucosa mobile over ​the prostate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Presence ​of nodules","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Presence of nodules","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Presence of nodules</strong></p><p>Palpable <strong>nodules</strong> over the prostate is highly suggestive of carcinoma.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 127px; width: 362px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155.625px;\"><strong>Features of carcinoma of the prostate</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 209.375px;\"><strong>Features of benign disease of the prostate</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155.625px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hard </strong>consistency</li>\n<li><strong>Rectal mucosa immobile </strong>over the prostate</li>\n<li><strong>Obliteration of the median groove</strong></li>\n<li>Nodular surface</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 209.375px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Firm</strong> consistency</li>\n<li><strong>Rectal mucosa mobile </strong>over the prostate</li>\n<li><strong>Residual urine may be felt</strong> as a fluctuating swelling above the prostate</li>\n<li>Smooth surface</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4637","text":"A 68- year-old patient pre​sents with difficulty in ‍initiating micturition. A​ DRE reveals an enlarged ‌nodular prostate with adh‍erent rectal mucosa. PSA ‍was found to be 25 ng/ml ‍and a biopsy result was s​uggestive of adenocarcino‌ma. Which of the followin​g statements is false reg‌arding his condition ?","raw_text":"A 68- year-old patient presents with difficulty in initiating micturition. A DRE reveals an enlarged nodular prostate with adherent rectal mucosa. PSA was found to be 25 ng/ml and a biopsy result was suggestive of adenocarcinoma. Which of the following statements is false regarding his condition ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PSA is a not specific mark​er for the condition.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gleason score correlates w​ith the pathological stag‍e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is associated with decr​eased % free PSA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It arises from the transit​ional zone of the gland.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It arises from the transitional zone of the gland.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6c9a44e8276747d0a0200f37d95ae0fex600x568.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It arises from the transitional zone of the gland.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Prostatic carcinoma</strong>, as indicated in the clinical scenario, <strong>arises</strong> from the <strong>peripheral zone</strong> of the gland.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Anatomically, the prostate is divided into three zones. </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The peripheral zone lies posteriorly and has an abundance of prostatic glands. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The central zone lies posterior to the urethral lumen and above the seminal vesicles as they course through the prostate. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The transitional zone is periurethral.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Benign prostatic hyperplasia arises from the transitional zone.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Gleason grading</strong> may be used after biopsy to evaluate the <strong>stage</strong> of <strong>histopathological</strong> specimen. Gleason scores range from 6 to 10 where 6 is low risk for prostate cancer while 10 is high risk.<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p><strong>PSA</strong> (prostatic specific antigen) is a glycoprotein peptidase that facilitates the liquefaction of semen. It is useful for screening and for monitoring response to therapy but is <strong>neither specific nor sensitive</strong> to the condition as it can be elevated due to <strong>old age</strong> and benign conditions such as <strong>prostatitis</strong> and <strong>BPH</strong>.</p>\n<p>Slightly elevated total PSA (between 4.0 and 10.0 ng/mL) and <strong>low free PSA (&lt;25%)</strong> is suspicious for <strong>prostate cancer.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4666","text":"A 63-year-old patient come​s to the urology OPD with‍ hesitancy, incomplete vo​iding, and pelvic pain. O‌n a digital rectal examin‍ation, an irregularly enl‍arged prostate with a nod‍ular surface is felt. The​ rectal mucosa does not m‌ove freely over it. A bio​psy using an automated gu‌n is planned. How many bi‌opsy cores should be obta‌ined?","raw_text":"A 63-year-old patient comes to the urology OPD with hesitancy, incomplete voiding, and pelvic pain. On a digital rectal examination, an irregularly enlarged prostate with a nodular surface is felt. The rectal mucosa does not move freely over it. A biopsy using an automated gun is planned. How many biopsy cores should be obtained?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​8","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1​0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1​2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 12","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 12</strong></p><p>Ideally, <strong>12</strong> systematic biopsy cores are obtained in patients with suspected prostatic cancer.</p>\n<p>A 12-core systematic biopsy that incorporates apical and far-lateral cores in template distribution allows maximum cancer detection. It also avoids detection of insignificant prostate cancers and the need of a repeat biopsy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4660","text":"A 77-year-old man was eval​uated for a nodule found ‍on his prostate. His PSA ​levels were 15 ng/dl and ‌a transrectal biopsy indi‍cated a malignancy. Which‍ of the following stateme‍nts is false regarding it​s spread?","raw_text":"A 77-year-old man was evaluated for a nodule found on his prostate. His PSA levels were 15 ng/dl and a transrectal biopsy indicated a malignancy. Which of the following statements is false regarding its spread?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Local spread can occur to ​the seminal vesicles.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Direct involvement of rect​um is common.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Obturator nodes are most c​ommonly involved.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spread by bloodstream usua​lly occurs to the bones.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Direct involvement of rectum is common.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Direct involvement of rectum is common.</strong></p><p>Palpable nodules over the prostate with raised PSA are highly suggestive of carcinoma. direct involvement of the <strong>rectum</strong> is rare but it may become <strong>stenosed</strong> by the <strong>tumor infiltrating</strong> around it.</p>\n<p>Option A: Local invasion occurs upwards, first involving seminal vesicles, bladder neck, and trigone; this is followed by distal invasion to the external sphincter. Obstruction of one or both ureters can occur resulting in anuria. </p>\n<p>Option C: Lymph nodes involved are <strong>obturator</strong> lymph nodes, external iliac nodes, and occasionally retroperitoneal, mediastinal, and <strong>supraclavicular</strong> lymph nodes. </p>\n<p>Option D: Spread by the bloodstream occurs mostly to the bones; the bones involved most frequently in carcinoma of the prostate are the <strong>pelvic bones </strong>and the<strong> lower lumbar vertebrae.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4665","text":"A 66-year-old man presents​ with an enlarged, hard, ‍nodular prostate. Further​ investigations reveal a ‌tumor that has extended t‍hrough the prostatic caps‍ule to the seminal vesicl‍e. Other adjacent structu​res are not involved. Wha‌t is the stage of his dis​ease?","raw_text":"A 66-year-old man presents with an enlarged, hard, nodular prostate. Further investigations reveal a tumor that has extended through the prostatic capsule to the seminal vesicle. Other adjacent structures are not involved. What is the stage of his disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T​2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T​3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"T​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. T3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. T3</strong></p><p>The stage of the tumor that has extended through the <strong>prostatic capsule</strong> to the <strong>seminal vesicle</strong> and not to other adjacent structures is <strong>T3b</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 208px; width: 350.59375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 340.59375px; height: 46px;\">\n<p><strong>Staging of prostate carcinoma:</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 36px;\">Tx</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 36px;\">Primary tumor cannot be assessed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 18px;\">T0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 18px;\">No evidence of primary tumor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 54px;\">T1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 54px;\">Clinically inapparent tumor. <strong>Neither palpable nor visible</strong> on imaging</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 36px;\">T1a</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 36px;\">Incidental histologic finding in 5% or less of tissue resected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 36px;\">T1b</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 36px;\">Incidental histologic findings in more than 5% of tissue resected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 54px;\">T1c</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 54px;\">Tumor identified by needle biopsy (for example, because of elevated PSA level)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 64px;\">T2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Tumor <strong>confined within</strong> <strong>the prostate</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 64px;\">T2a</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Tumor involves one-half of one lobe or less</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 82px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 82px;\">T2b</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 82px;\">\n<p>Tumor involves more than one-half of one lobe but not both lobes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 46px;\">T2c</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 46px;\">\n<p>Tumor involves both lobes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 64px;\">T3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Tumors <strong>extend through the prostate capsule</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 36px;\">T3a</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 36px;\">Extracapsular extension (unilateral or bilateral)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 39px;\">T3b</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 39px;\">\n<p>Tumor invades seminal vesicle(s)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 18px;\">T4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 18px;\">Tumor is fixed or<strong> invades adjacent structures other than seminal vesicles</strong>, such as external sphincter, rectum, bladder, levator muscles, and/or pelvic wall</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4641","text":"A 75-year-old man provisio​nally diagnosed with BPH ‍underwent a TURP procedur​e. Histopathological exam‌ of the resected tissue r‍evealed a tumor involving‍ less than 5% of the spec‍imen. What is the most ap​propriate management?","raw_text":"A 75-year-old man provisionally diagnosed with BPH underwent a TURP procedure. Histopathological exam of the resected tissue revealed a tumor involving less than 5% of the specimen. What is the most appropriate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conser​vative","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Radical pr​ostatectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radiot​herapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Androgen​ ablation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Conservative","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Conservative</strong></p><p>A <strong>tumor</strong> discovered incidentally on TURP present in <strong>&lt;5% of resected tissue</strong> is staged as <strong>T1a. </strong>For <strong>older men</strong>, <strong>conservative treatment</strong> is done for stage T1a prostatic carcinoma.</p>\n<p>Radical surgical treatment might be considered in younger (&lt;70 years) men with the same stage of the disease.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 208px; width: 350.59375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 340.59375px; height: 46px;\">\n<p><strong>Staging of prostate carcinoma:</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 36px;\">Tx</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 36px;\">Primary tumor cannot be assessed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 18px;\">T0</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 18px;\">No evidence of primary tumor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 54px;\">T1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 54px;\">Clinically inapparent tumor. <strong>Neither palpable nor visible</strong> on imaging</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 36px;\">T1a</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 36px;\">Incidental histologic finding in 5% or less of tissue resected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 36px;\">T1b</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 36px;\">Incidental histologic findings in more than 5% of tissue resected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 54px;\">T1c</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 54px;\">Tumor identified by needle biopsy (for example, because of elevated PSA)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 64px;\">T2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Tumor <strong>confined within</strong> <strong>the prostate</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 64px;\">T2a</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Tumor involves one-half of one lobe or less</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 82px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 82px;\">T2b</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 82px;\">\n<p>Tumor involves more than one-half of one lobe but not both lobes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 46px;\">T2c</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 46px;\">\n<p>Tumor involves both lobes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 64px;\">T3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Tumors <strong>extend through the prostate capsule</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 36px;\">T3a</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 36px;\">Extracapsular extension (unilateral or bilateral)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 39px;\">T3b</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 39px;\">\n<p>Tumor invades seminal vesicle(s)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 18px;\">T4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.59375px; height: 18px;\">Tumor is fixed or<strong> invades adjacent structures other than seminal vesicles</strong>, such as external sphincter, rectum, bladder, levator muscles, and/or pelvic wall</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table style=\"height: 122px; width: 385.140625px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 367.140625px;\"><strong>Management of Prostatic Carcinoma</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\"><strong>Stage of cancer</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\"><strong>Age &gt; 70 or Gleason score=6</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 71.140625px;\"><strong>Age &lt; 70 or Gleason score &gt;6  </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\"><strong>Early prostate cancer: T1B,T2A</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Active surveillance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 71.140625px;\">Radical prostatectomy    </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\"><strong>Locally advanced prostate cancer: T2B, T2C, T3, T4, N1</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\">Brachytherapy + Androgen deprivation therapy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 71.140625px;\">Brachytherapy + Radical prostatectomy   </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148px;\"><strong>Metastatic prostate cancer</strong></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 219.140625px;\">Androgen deprivation therapy Chemotherapy and radiotherapy (hormone-resistant  tumor)Radiopharmaceutical therapy (hormone resistant bony metastasis) </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4668","text":"Orchidectomy is used as tr​eatment for which of the ‍following stages of prost​ate cancer?","raw_text":"Orchidectomy is used as treatment for which of the following stages of prostate cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T1c cancer ​in the young","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T2b cancer ​in the young","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T2b cancer i​n the elderly","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"T1c disease in the elderly​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. T2b cancer in the elderly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. T2b cancer in the elderly</strong></p><p>Orchidectomy is performed to carry out <strong>androgen ablation </strong>in the treatment of <strong>locally advanced</strong> disease in the elderly. It is also done to manage <strong>metastatic disease.</strong></p>\n<p>Bilateral orchidectomy, whether total or subcapsular, will eliminate the major source of testosterone production. LHRH analogs such as goserelin and buserelin along with anti-androgens such as flutamide is used to perform medical androgen ablation.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 122px;\" width=\"468\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 148.671875px;\"><strong>Management of Prostatic Carcinoma</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.671875px;\"><strong>Stage of cancer</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.671875px;\"><strong>Age &gt; 70 or Gleason score=6</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.671875px;\"><strong> Age &lt; 70 or Gleason score &gt;6  </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.671875px;\"><strong>Early prostate cancer: T1,T2A</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.671875px;\">Active surveillance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.671875px;\">Radical prostatectomy  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.671875px;\"><strong>Locally advanced prostate cancer: T2B, T2C, T3, T4, N1</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.671875px;\">Brachytherapy + Androgen deprivation therapy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.671875px;\">Brachytherapy + Radical prostatectomy   </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.671875px;\"><strong>Metastatic prostate cancer</strong></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 148.671875px;\">Androgen deprivation therapy <br/>Chemotherapy and radiotherapy (hormone-resistant  tumor)<br/>Radiopharmaceutical therapy (hormone resistant bony metastasis) </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3518","text":"A 77-year-old man presente​d with worsening hip and ‍back pain. The X-ray reve​aled the given lesions Th‌e patient had undergone a‍n orchidectomy three year‍s ago to manage his prost‍atic carcinoma. However, ​recent serial estimations‌ of PSA have shown an inc​reasing trend. What is th‌e treatment of choice for‌ his condition?","raw_text":"A 77-year-old man presented with worsening hip and back pain. The X-ray revealed the given lesions  The patient had undergone an orchidectomy three years ago to manage his prostatic carcinoma. However, recent serial estimations of PSA have shown an increasing trend. What is the treatment of choice for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pacli​taxel","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gose​relin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Doce​taxel","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radiu​m-223","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Radium-223","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7f6877aee4df493dbb2b657aed2434b2.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Radium-223</strong></p><p><strong>Blastic</strong> lesions on X-Ray after surgical castration (therapeutic orchidectomy) in patients with <strong>prostatic carcinoma</strong> is suggestive of hormone-resistant <strong>bone metastasis</strong>. The treatment of choice for <strong>hormone-resistant bone metastasis</strong> is radiopharmaceutical therapy with <strong>radium-223</strong>.</p>\n<p>Radium-223 is a radioactive isotope that emits low levels of alpha-particle radiation. The alpha-particle radiation causes double-strand breaks in DNA, which kills cells. Radium-223 is a “calcium mimetic” that <strong>accumulates</strong> <strong>preferentially</strong> in areas of bone undergoing increased turnover, such as areas of bone metastases.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 122px; width: 390.140625px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 315.140625px;\"><strong>Management of Prostatic Carcinoma</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\"><strong>Stage of cancer</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\"><strong>Age&gt;70 or Gleason score=6</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 43.140625px;\"><strong> Age&lt;70 or Gleason score&gt;6    </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\"><strong>Early prostate cancer: T1, T2A</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Active surveillance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 43.140625px;\">Radical prostatectomy   </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\"><strong>Locally advanced prostate cancer: T2B, T2C, T3, T4, N1</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Brachytherapy + Androgen deprivation therapy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 43.140625px;\">Brachytherapy + Radical prostatectomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\"><strong>Metastatic prostate cancer</strong></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 200.140625px;\">\n<p>Androgen deprivation therapy</p>\n<p>Chemotherapy and radiotherapy (hormone-resistant  tumor)</p>\n<p>Radiopharmaceutical therapy (hormone-resistant bony metastasis) </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3517","text":"Which of the following vac​cines is used in the trea‍tment of prostate cancer?​","raw_text":"Which of the following vaccines is used in the treatment of prostate cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Talimogene l​aherparepvec","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bacillus Cal​mette–Guérin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sipule​ucel-T","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Algenpa​ntucel-L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sipuleucel-T","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sipuleucel-T</strong></p><p><strong>Sipuleucel-T</strong> (Provenge) is an autologous prostatic acid phosphatase (PAP)-loaded dendritic cell vaccine. </p>\n<p>It is approved for patients with <strong>hormone-resistant, metastatic prostate cancer</strong> with minimal or no cancer-related symptoms.</p>\n<p>Other immunotherapies in prostate cancer include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">GVAX allogeneic recombinant whole-cell vaccine</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Immune checkpoint inhibitors such as PD-1/PD-L1 inhibitor</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">CTLA-4 inhibitor</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Talimogene laherparepvec (T-VEC) has demonstrated therapeutic activity in <strong>melanoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: Bacillus Calmette–Guérin is used for <strong>bladder carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: Algenpantucel-L is used as immunotherapy in <strong>pancreatic adenocarcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC8940","text":"Which of the following is ​used in laser enucleation‍ of the prostate?","raw_text":"Which of the following is used in laser enucleation of the prostate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbon dio​xide laser","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nd-YAG​ laser","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ho-YAG​ laser","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Argon​ laser","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ho-YAG laser","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ho-YAG laser</strong></p><p><strong>Ho-YAG laser</strong> is the most common laser used in enucleation of the prostate. The procedure is known as <strong>Holmium laser enucleation of the prostate (HoLEP).</strong></p>\n<p>Laser <strong>reduce</strong> the duration of the procedure and the <strong>risk</strong> of<strong> harmorrhage</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>KTPA green light laser</strong> is the best laser that can be used. It causes <strong>vaporisation</strong> of prostatic tissue. As it is <strong>hemostatic</strong> in nature, it can be safely used in patients on anticoagulants.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"bQx5os9e","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"42_thoracic_wall","title":"42.Thoracic Wall","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB1491","text":"What is the intercostal ne‌rve a branch of?","raw_text":"What is the intercostal nerve a branch of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsal rami of cervical sp‌inal nerves","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dorsal rami of thoracic sp‌inal nerves","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventral rami of thoracic s‌pinal nerves","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ventral rami of cervical s‌pinal nerves","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ventral rami of thoracic spinal nerves","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/07d7ecff341a43008db6331b851339a1x600x606.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ventral rami of thoracic spinal nerves</strong></p><p>The <strong>intercostal nerve</strong> is a branch of <strong>ventral rami</strong> (anterior) of<strong> thoracic spinal nerves.</strong></p>\n<p>Intercostal nerves are distributed primarily to the thoracic and abdominal walls:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>1<sup>st</sup> </strong>thoracic nerve with a variable proportion of the <strong>2</strong><sup><strong>nd</strong> </sup>passes into the <strong>brachial plexus</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>3<sup>rd</sup>,4<sup>th</sup>,5<sup>th</sup>,6<sup>th</sup></strong> supply only the <strong>thoracic wall</strong>,</li>\n<li>The <strong>7<sup>th</sup>,8<sup>th</sup>,9<sup>th</sup>,10,11<sup>th</sup></strong> supply both <strong>thoracic and abdominal walls</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>subcostal nerve</strong> supplies the <strong>abdominal wall </strong>and the <strong>gluteal skin.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1490","text":"A patient with a history o‌f slip and fall developed‍ fracture of the thoracic​ spine. On neurological e‌xamination, there's a los‌s of sensation over the x‌iphoid process. Which of ‌the following spinal nerv​es involves this region?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of slip and fall developed fracture of the thoracic spine. On neurological examination, there's a loss of sensation over the xiphoid process. Which of the following spinal nerves involves this region?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T‌4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T‌6","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"T‌9","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T‌10","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. T6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e64957b56faa4d3597a0e464d36a9de3x600x606.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. T6</strong></p><p>Thoracic spinal nerve, <strong>T6</strong> is involved here as it supplies the <strong>skin</strong> over the <strong>xiphoid process. </strong></p>\n<p>The anterior chest wall is innervated by the <strong>lateral</strong> and <strong>anterior cutaneous branches</strong> of the <strong>intercostal nerves.</strong></p>\n<p>Intercostal nerves <strong>T7 to</strong> <strong>T11</strong> and the <strong>subcostal</strong> nerve <strong>T12</strong> supply the <strong>skin</strong> and <strong>muscle</strong> of the <strong>abdominal wall. </strong>In addition, <strong>T5</strong> and <strong>T6</strong> supply the upper parts of the <strong>external oblique</strong> muscle of the abdominal wall. The highest thoracic dermatome on the anterior chest wall is T2.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1492","text":"The given image shows the ‌posterior thoracic wall. ‍Which of the following st​atements is false about t‌he structure marked A wit‌hin the intercostal space‌?","raw_text":"The given image shows the posterior thoracic wall. Which of the following statements is false about the structure marked A within the intercostal space?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is located below the in‌tercostal artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Derivatives from T4 and T5‌ are typical in type","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"They lie between external ‌intercostal and internal ‍intercostal muscle","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Derivatives from T7 to T11‌ supply the abdominal wal‍l","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. They lie between external intercostal and internal intercostal muscle","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/db61da78381244bebb15a5db17bff708.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3ae9fc7b12564fdd81176bdc923f870bx1280x1152.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/55beb1e1a8a546229f278b7a5f69cb9ex600x597.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/af3d811a240140bd82942e1101da1cc5x600x596.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ff652a2dfd55406faf6d83b51188ea8fx1280x1715.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. They lie between external intercostal and internal intercostal muscle</strong></p><p>The structure marked A is the <strong>intercostal nerve</strong>. It doesn't lie between external and internal intercostal muscles, but lies between the <strong>internal</strong> and <strong>innermost</strong> intercostal muscles. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Intercostal nerves</strong> are derived from the<strong> ventral rami</strong> of the thoracic vertebrae - <strong>T1 to T11. </strong>They lie between the <strong>internal intercostal</strong> and<strong> innermost intercostal</strong> muscles.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical</strong> intercostal nerves are those that only supply only the thoracic wall, e.g, <strong>3rd to 6th</strong> intercostal nerves. All the other intercostal nerves supply other regions as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Upper two intercostal nerves - supply upper limb\n<ul>\n<li>1st intercostal nerve - does not have the lateral and anterior cutaneous branches.</li>\n<li>2nd intercostal nerve - gives off the intercostobrachial nerve</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>7th</strong> to <strong>11th</strong> intercostal nerves - supply <strong>abdominal wall</strong>, hence they are called thoracoabdominal nerves. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>In each intercostal space, the arrangement of the structures <strong>superior to inferior</strong> is <strong>v</strong>ein, <strong>a</strong>rtery, <strong>n</strong>erve (VAN). In the <strong>1<sup>st</sup> intercostal space,</strong> the neurovascular bundle lies superiorly, and the arrangement from <strong>medial to lateral </strong>is the <strong>S</strong>ympathetic trunk, intercostal<strong> V</strong>ein, superior intercostal <strong>A</strong>rtery, intercostal <strong>N</strong>erve. (SVAN).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9187","text":"What forms the subcostal n‌erve?","raw_text":"What forms the subcostal nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsal ra‌mus of T6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dorsal ra‌mus of T12","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventral r‌amus of T6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ventral ra‌mus of T12","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ventral ramus of T12","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ventral ramus of T12</strong></p><p>The <strong>subcostal nerve</strong> is formed by the <strong>ventral ramus of T12.</strong></p>\n<p>It supplies the<strong> abdominal wall</strong> and<strong> gluteal skin </strong>and gives a branch to <strong>pyramidalis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1494","text":"A patient who underwent ri‌ght-sided mastectomy is n‍ow complaining of pain ov​er the axilla and medial ‌aspect of the right upper‌ arm. What is the nerve i‌nvolved a branch of?","raw_text":"A patient who underwent right-sided mastectomy is now complaining of pain over the axilla and medial aspect of the right upper arm. What is the nerve involved a branch of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1st interc‌ostal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2nd interc‌ostal nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3rd interc‌ostal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Upper trunk of brachial pl‌exus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2nd intercostal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2nd intercostal nerve</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of iatrogenic injury to the <strong>intercostobrachial nerve</strong>. It is a branch of the <strong>2<sup>nd</sup> intercostal nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>It is the <strong>lateral cutaneous branch</strong> of the 2<sup>nd</sup> intercostal nerve and supplies the <strong>skin of the axilla</strong> and <strong>upper part </strong>of the <strong>medial aspect </strong>of the<strong> arm</strong>. </p>\n<p>Clinical significance:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Common site of injury during surgeries such as mastectomy, axillary nerve blocks, and other axillary surgeries.</li>\n<li>Source of referred cardiac pain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1498","text":"A patient diagnosed with e‌mpyema was planned for in‍tercostal drainage tube i​nsertion. During the proc‌edure, which of the follo‌wing muscles is not pierc‌ed?","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with empyema was planned for intercostal drainage tube insertion. During the procedure, which of the following muscles is not pierced?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"External i‌ntercostal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Internal i‌ntercostal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Innermost ‌intercostal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transversu‌s thoracis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Transversus thoracis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c29ba59f7d24f7ea84d75e1b469d623x1280x1739.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Transversus thoracis</strong></p><p>Intercostal drainage tubes are placed in the <strong>mid-axillary line</strong>, and <strong>t</strong><strong>ransversus thoracis</strong> is not pierced as it lies <strong>anteriorly,</strong> not in the mid-axillary line.</p>\n<p>Transversus thoracis muscle:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Also known as triangularis sternae or sternocostalis.</li>\n<li>Origin - lower third of the posterior surface of the sternum, xiphoid process, and costal cartilages of lower 3-4 true ribs.</li>\n<li>Insertion - into the cartilage of the 2<sup>nd</sup> to 6<sup>th</sup> ribs. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>innermost</strong> muscular plane has three named muscles:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Innermost intercostals</strong> are at the sides of the rib cage.</li>\n<li><strong>Subcostalis</strong> are at the back</li>\n<li><strong>Transversus thoracis</strong> at the anterior part of the thoracic rib cage. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1499","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about the left phren‍ic nerve?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the left phrenic nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arise from dorsal rami of ‌C2, C3, and C4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Descends in the left pleur‌al space","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Supplies the parietal peri‌toneum on the diaphragm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Passes through the vena ca‌val opening in the diaphr‍agm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Supplies the parietal peritoneum on the diaphragm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5bb2b099dd364eca830c83d42ff1937bx1280x1887.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Supplies the parietal peritoneum on the diaphragm</strong></p><p>The left phrenic nerve supplies <strong>sensory fibers</strong> to the parietal peritoneum covering the diaphragm, and the <strong>mediastinal</strong> and<strong> diaphragmatic pleura</strong> on the left side.</p>\n<p>The phrenic nerve is a <strong>mixed nerve</strong> derived mostly from the <strong>C4 ramus</strong> and also receives contributions from the <strong>C3 and C5</strong> ventral rami. It is formed at the lateral border of scalenus anterior. </p>\n<p>It <strong>descends</strong> anterior to the pulmonary hilum and lies <strong>between</strong> the<strong> mediastinal pleura</strong> and fibrous pericardium. It passes through the <strong>muscular part </strong>of the diaphragm just<strong> anterior to </strong>the <strong>central tendon. </strong>The <strong>right phrenic nerve</strong> passes through the <strong>vena caval opening.</strong></p>\n<p>Both right and left phrenic nerves supply:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Motor</strong> fibers to the muscular <strong>diaphragm</strong> - respective crura of the diaphragm</li>\n<li><strong>Sensory</strong> fibers to the<strong> mediastinal pleura</strong>, fibrous and serous <strong>pericardium</strong>, and diaphragmatic <strong>peritoneum</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1502","text":"A patient with pericarditi‌s is complaining of pain ‍in the left shoulder and ​neck. Through which of th‌e following nerves is thi‌s sensation carried?","raw_text":"A patient with pericarditis is complaining of pain in the left shoulder and neck. Through which of the following nerves is this sensation carried?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deep card‌iac plexus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superficial cardiac plexus‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phreni‌c nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Subcost‌al nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Phrenic nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phrenic nerve</strong></p><p>The pain of pericarditis is carried by the <strong>phrenic nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>The somatic sensation from the pericardium is carried by the somatic afferent fibres of the <strong>phrenic nerve. </strong>The pericardial pain may be referred to the <strong>supraclavicular region</strong> of the shoulder or <strong>lateral neck area </strong>(dermatomes C3, C4, and C5).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1503","text":"A 50-year-old woman presen‌ted with chest pain, dysp‍nea, and weight loss. Che​st X-ray showed an elevat‌ed right dome of the diap‌hragm. CT revealed a tumo‌r invading the right lung‌ surface anterior to the ​hilum. Which of the follo​wing nerves is most likel‌y to be involved by the t​umor?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman presented with chest pain, dyspnea, and weight loss. Chest X-ray showed an elevated right dome of the diaphragm. CT revealed a tumor invading the right lung surface anterior to the hilum. Which of the following nerves is most likely to be involved by the tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recurrent la‌ryngeal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intercos‌tal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vagus‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phreni‌c nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Phrenic nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/126f1f7d53c8414ebf91f344d1f4969ax1280x1480.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Phrenic nerve</strong></p><p>The right <strong>phrenic nerve</strong> is involved by the tumor as it passes anterior to the hilum of the lung.  </p>\n<p>The phrenic nerve provides<strong> the sole motor supply</strong> to the <strong>diaphragm.</strong> Normally, the diaphragm<strong> flattens when it contracts</strong>. Involvement of the phrenic nerve causes <strong>elevation</strong> of the respective <strong>dome</strong> of the <strong>diaphragm.</strong> </p>\n<p>Options A, C - Vagus nerve passes posterior to the hilum of the lung. The recurrent laryngeal nerve arises from the vagus nerve before this.</p>\n<p>Option B - The intercostal nerve lies in the intercostal space and is not related to the hilum of the lungs.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6638","text":"A patient presented with p‌aradoxical respiration. W‍hich of the following ner​ves is most likely to be ‌affected?","raw_text":"A patient presented with paradoxical respiration. Which of the following nerves is most likely to be affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌D","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. D","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9bfb132c05094adfb9de9e6e7e796319.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bb75539a3721416fad44527659c7170bx1280x1114.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. D</strong></p><p><strong>Paradoxical respiration</strong> occurs due to the damage of the <strong>phrenic nerve,</strong> which is marked as <strong>D</strong>, in the given image.</p>\n<p>The structures marked in the image are:</p>\n<p>A. Right vagus nerve</p>\n<p>B. Left vagus nerve</p>\n<p>C. Recurrent laryngeal nerve</p>\n<p>D. Right phrenic nerve</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Paradoxical movements </strong>in the <strong>chest </strong>are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Paradoxical respiration - the abnormal movement of the <strong>chest</strong> <strong>wall</strong> or <strong>abdominal</strong> <strong>wall</strong>, where they move in during inspiration instead of moving out. It is seen in flail chest. </li>\n<li>Paradoxical movement of the diaphragm - normally, the diaphragm moves down during inspiration. However, in cases of<strong> phrenic nerve palsy,</strong> the affected side of the <strong>diaphragm</strong> moves <strong>upward</strong> <strong>(paradoxical</strong> <strong>respiration</strong>) due to<strong> diaphragmatic paralysis.</strong> The gold standard investigation to detect this is the electrical or magnetic stimulation of the phrenic nerve with a recording of the compound muscle action potential and/or the transdiaphragmatic pressure. This can be observed on ultrasound or fluoroscopy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1505","text":"A fluoroscopic examination‌ is being performed in a ‍patient with suspected ph​renic nerve palsy. On ask‌ing the patient to inspir‌e, you observe the follow‌ing in the diaphragm. Whi‌ch of the following state​ments is correct about th​e course of the nerve inv‌olved?","raw_text":"A fluoroscopic examination is being performed in a patient with suspected phrenic nerve palsy. On asking the patient to inspire, you observe the following in the diaphragm. Which of the following statements is correct about the course of the nerve involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It runs anterior to scalen‌us anterior","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It lies posterior to brach‌ial plexus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It lies posterior to subcl‌avian artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It runs behind the left hi‌lum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It runs anterior to scalenus anterior","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d67221b99bf14146a043a1366fa1a789.GIF"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a19e57540547467a88c5a58de59eaf7dx1280x1887.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It runs anterior to scalenus anterior</strong></p><p>The given case scenario and paradoxical elevation of the left dome of the diaphragm on inspiration are suggestive of <strong>left phrenic nerve palsy</strong>. The left phrenic nerve runs <strong>anterior to the scalenus anterior.</strong></p>\n<p>The course of the left phrenic nerve:</p>\n<p><strong>Cervical region - </strong>both phrenic nerves pass,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior to scalenus anterior muscle</li>\n<li>Posterior to sternocleidomastoid and inferior belly of omohyoid</li>\n<li>Posterior to internal jugular vein, transverse cervical artery, and suprascapular artery</li>\n<li>Posterior to the thoracic duct on the left</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Root of the neck</strong> - It lies medial to the trunks of the brachial plexus in the neck. At the <strong>root of the neck</strong>, it runs anterior to the second part of the subclavian artery and posterior to the subclavian vein. </p>\n<p><strong>Thorax</strong> - The phrenic nerve<strong> enters the thorax</strong> by crossing <strong>medially </strong>in front of the internal thoracic artery. Within the thorax, the phrenic nerve descends <strong>anterior</strong> to the<strong> pulmonary hilum </strong>between the fibrous pericardium and mediastinal pleura, accompanied by the pericardiacophrenic vessels.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1514","text":"What is the superior inter‌costal artery a branch of‍?","raw_text":"What is the superior intercostal artery a branch of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Costocerv‌ical trunk","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dorsal scap‌ular artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thyrocerv‌ical trunk","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Internal tho‌racic artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Costocervical trunk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ae1a55968c854ff49a30ae88eeba063cx1280x1349.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Costocervical trunk</strong></p><p>Superior intercostal artery is a branch of<strong> </strong>the <strong>costocervical trunk.</strong></p>\n<p>It descends in front of the neck of the first 2 ribs and gives rise to the<strong> first two posterior intercostal arteries.  </strong></p>\n<p>The artery is not constant and is more common on the right. When it is absent, it is replaced by a direct branch from the aorta.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1522","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about the anterior i‍ntercostal arteries?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the anterior intercostal arteries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are present in the 1s‌t to 12th intercostal spa‍ces","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The upper 9 intercostal sp‌aces have 2 anterior inte‍rcostal arteries","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"They are branches of the e‌xternal thoracic artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They anastomose with inter‌nal thoracic artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The upper 9 intercostal spaces have 2 anterior intercostal arteries","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ac30ba94d96f4110a2fa35fd2d558805x1280x1349.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The upper 9 intercostal spaces have 2 anterior intercostal arteries</strong></p><p>Each of the <strong>upper 9 </strong>intercostal spaces has<strong> 2 anterior</strong> intercostal arteries and <strong>1 posterior</strong> intercostal artery.</p>\n<p>The <strong>10<sup>th</sup></strong> and<strong> 11<sup>th</sup></strong> intercostal spaces have <strong>no anterior</strong> intercostal artery and<strong> 1 posterior</strong> intercostal artery.</p>\n<p><strong>Anterior intercostal arteries</strong> arise from the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Internal thoracic artery - </strong>Upper 6 spaces</li>\n<li>Musculophrenic artery - 7th to 9th spaces</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Anterior intercostal arteries <strong>anastomose</strong> with the respective <strong>posterior intercostal arteries</strong> and their collaterals. They are shorter than the posterior intercostal arteries.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1510","text":"A 20-year-old male was bro‌ught to the emergency dep‍artment after he suffered​ a stab wound in the ante‌rior chest wall. CT-scan ‌revealed arterial extrava‌sation from injury to the‌ internal thoracic artery​. The condition causes a ​decrease in blood supply ‌through all of the follow​ing blood vessels, except​:","raw_text":"A 20-year-old male was brought to the emergency department after he suffered a stab wound in the anterior chest wall. CT-scan revealed arterial extravasation from injury to the internal thoracic artery. The condition causes a decrease in blood supply through all of the following blood vessels, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior ‌epigastric","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Musculo‌phrenic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior i‌ntercostal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior ‌intercostal","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Posterior intercostal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c56e669575b844afb10c698bea621de4x1280x1349.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Posterior intercostal</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW54344472 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW54344472 BCX4\">Posterior intercostal arteries</span></span> <span class=\"TextRun SCXW54344472 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW54344472 BCX4\">are not </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW54344472 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"ContextualSpellingAndGrammarError SCXW54344472 BCX4\">branches</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW54344472 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW54344472 BCX4\"> of the internal thoracic artery, hence there would be no decrease in blood supply through these arteries.</span></span><span class=\"EOP SCXW54344472 BCX4\" data-ccp-props='{\"201341983\":0,\"335551550\":1,\"335551620\":1,\"335559739\":160,\"335559740\":259}'> </span></p>\n<p>Branches of the <strong>internal thoracic artery:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Terminal branches</strong> at the level of the 6<sup>th</sup> intercostal space:\n<ul>\n<li>Superior epigastric artery</li>\n<li>Musculophrenic artery</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Sternal branches</strong> to:\n<ul>\n<li>Transversus thoracis muscle</li>\n<li>Periosteum of the posterior sternal surface</li>\n<li>Sternal red bone marrow</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Anterior intercostal arteries </strong>for the first 6 intercostal spaces.</li>\n<li>Perforating branches that pierce and supply <strong>pectoralis major </strong>and then become<strong> direct cutaneous vessels </strong>that supply the skin.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1519","text":"Rib notching was observed ‌on chest X-ray, in a pati‍ent with Turner's syndrom​e due to enlargement of a‌rteries in the inferior r‌ib margin. Which of the f‌ollowing is incorrect abo‌ut these arteries?","raw_text":"Rib notching was observed on chest X-ray, in a patient with Turner's syndrome due to enlargement of arteries in the inferior rib margin. Which of the following is incorrect about these arteries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They supply the intercosta‌l space up to the costo-c‍hondral junction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"First two pairs arise from‌ branches of the thyrocer‍vical trunk","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Arteries on the right are ‌longer than the correspon‍ding ones on the left","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"There are 11 pairs of post‌erior intercostal arterie‍s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. First two pairs arise from branches of the thyrocervical trunk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. First two pairs arise from branches of the thyrocervical trunk</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\">Rib notching occurs due to enlargement of the <strong>posterior intercostal arteries</strong>. This is seen in coarctation of aorta. </span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\">There are </span></span><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\">11 pairs</span></span></strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\"><strong> </strong>of </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\">posterior intercostal arteries, and these supply<strong> up to</strong> the <strong>costochondral junction</strong>. </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\">The </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\"><strong>first 2 pairs</strong> </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\">are branches of the </span></span><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\">superior intercostal</span></span> <span class=\"TextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\">artery</span></span></strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\">, a branch of the<strong> </strong></span></span><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"SpellingError SCXW99915959 BCX4\">costocervical</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\"> trunk</span></span></strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\">. They are distributed to upper 2 intercostal spaces.  The<strong> </strong></span></span><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\">lower 9 pairs</span></span></strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\"> are branches of the </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\"><strong>descending thoracic aorta, </strong>which</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW99915959 BCX4\"> lies to the left of the vertebral column. Hence, the <strong>right</strong> posterior intercostal arteries are <strong>longer than</strong> those on the <strong>left.</strong></span></span></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical significance:</strong></p>\n<p>Coarctation of the aorta is a congenital heart disease, causing narrowing of the aorta. Chest x-ray shows rib notching and enlargement of left subclavian artery.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1526","text":"In the post-ductal coarcta‌tion of the aorta, blood ‍flow to the lower limbs i​s maintained by increased‌ blood flow through which‌ of the following?","raw_text":"In the post-ductal coarctation of the aorta, blood flow to the lower limbs is maintained by increased blood flow through which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inferior phrenic and peric‌ardiophrenic artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intercostal and superior e‌pigastric artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Subcostal and umbilical ar‌tery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vertebral and anterior spi‌nal artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intercostal and superior epigastric artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b098e356c12b4bc880d8036696f60dc8x1280x1138.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intercostal and superior epigastric artery</strong></p><p>The blood flow to the lower limb is maintained by <strong>intercostal</strong> and<strong> superior epigastric arteries</strong>. </p>\n<p>In the <strong>post-ductal coarctation of the aorta</strong>, an <strong>extensive collateral circulation</strong> develops from the following sources:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Axillary artery - The branches involved are: \n<ul>\n<li>Thoraco-acromial</li>\n<li>Lateral thoracic</li>\n<li>Subscapular</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Subclavian artery - The enlarged branches are:\n<ul>\n<li>Suprascapular</li>\n<li>Costocervical trunk (1<sup>st</sup> and 2<sup>nd</sup> posterior intercostals)</li>\n<li>Internal thoracic arteries</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Costocervical trunk</li>\n<li>Posterior intercostal arteries</li>\n<li>Inferior epigastric arteries </li>\n</ul>\n<p> The internal thoracic arteries give rise to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior intercostal arteries</strong> that carry blood to the <strong>posterior intercostals (3<sup>rd</sup>-6<sup>th</sup>)</strong> and thence into the descending aorta</li>\n<li><strong>Superior epigastric arteries</strong> that <strong>anastomoses </strong>with the<strong> inferior epigastric</strong> (in the rectus sheath) and sends blood into the external iliac artery and thence towards the lower limb.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>High vascularity is observed in the anterior thoracic wall as well as over the posterior thoracic wall especially in the interscapular area.</p>\n<p>The scapular anastomosis is opened up and both <strong>scapulae become pulsatile.</strong></p>\n<p>Radiographic evidence of <strong>\"rib notching\"</strong> is seen in the<strong> inferior margins of the rib </strong>due to greatly<strong> enlarged posterior intercostal arteries. </strong>Also, the radiographic shadow of the enlarged left subclavian artery is quite evident clinically.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Lower limb may be supplied by the channels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Arch of aorta—subclavian artery—internal thoracic artery—superior epigastric artery—inferior epigastric artery—iliac artery—pelvis and lower limb.</li>\n<li>Arch of aorta—subclavian artery—internal thoracic artery—anterior intercostal artery—posterior intercostal artery—descending thoracic aorta—thorax, abdomen, pelvis, lower limb.</li>\n<li>Arch of aorta—subclavian artery—vertebral artery—anterior spinal artery—segmental artery—thoracic and abdominal aorta—thorax—abdomen, pelvis, lower limb.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1527","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a tributary of left s‍uperior intercostal vein?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a tributary of left superior intercostal vein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left 3rd posterior interco‌stal vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left 2nd posterior interco‌stal vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left 1st posterior interco‌stal vein","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Left 4th posterior interco‌stal vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Left 1st posterior intercostal vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4047e44a8f54436db4efe341bc007ba9x1280x1757.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Left 1st posterior intercostal vein</strong></p><p>The left first posterior intercostal vein is not a tributary of the left superior intercostal vein.</p>\n<p>On both sides, the <strong>first posterior intercostal vein</strong> <strong>drains into</strong> the <strong>brachiocephalic</strong> or <strong>vertebral vein.</strong></p>\n<p>On the right side:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The 2<sup>nd</sup>, 3<sup>rd</sup>, and often the 4<sup>th</sup> posterior intercostal veins form the right superior intercostal vein, which drains into the arch of the azygos vein.</li>\n<li>The lower spaces drain directly into the azygos vein.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On the left side:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>2nd, 3rd</strong> and sometimes <strong>4th posterior intercostal veins</strong> form left superior intercostal vein which drains into the left brachiocephalic vein.</li>\n<li>The 4<sup>th</sup> or 5<sup>th</sup> to 8<sup>th</sup> posterior intercostal veins drain into the accessory hemiazygos vein.</li>\n<li>The 9<sup>th</sup> to 11<sup>th</sup> posterior intercostal veins drain into the hemiazygos vein. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1038","text":"Which of the following vei‌ns drain into the structu‍res marked A?","raw_text":"Which of the following veins drain into the structures marked A?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemiazyg‌ous vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior th‌yroid veins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right superior intercostal‌ vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left superior intercostal ‌vein","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Left superior intercostal vein","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/58414076745f4b7e9be9457dd1547826.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/00fb0fb0776e44b0811bd25751655ccbx1280x916.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f3bc2810e3d74f26b7d52db20b65ab0ax1280x1757.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Left superior intercostal vein</strong></p><p>The structures marked A are the right and left brachiocephalic veins. The <strong>left superior intercostal vein</strong> drains <strong>into the left brachiocephalic vein. </strong></p>\n<p>Tributaries of the <strong>brachiocephalic vein</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Left and right internal thoracic veins </li>\n<li>Left and right inferior thyroid veins</li>\n<li>Left superior intercostal vein drains into the left brachiocephalic vein.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The right superior intercostal vein drains into the azygos vein.</p>\n<p>Hemizygous vein drains into the azygous vein. (Option A)</p>\n<p>Superior thyroid veins drain directly into internal jugular veins. (Option B)</p>\n<p>Right superior intercostal vein drains into the azygous vein. (Option C)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1533","text":"In a patient with inferior‌ vena caval obstruction, ‍which of the following co​llaterals do not help dra‌in the blood?","raw_text":"In a patient with inferior vena caval obstruction, which of the following collaterals do not help drain the blood?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior epigastric and in‌ferior epigastric vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superficial epigastric and‌ iliolumbar vein","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Azygous and hemiazygous ve‌ins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vertebral v‌enous plexus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Superficial epigastric and iliolumbar vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Superficial epigastric and iliolumbar vein</strong></p><p>Superficial epigastric and iliolumbar veins do not help to drain the blood in inferior vena caval (IVC) obstruction as they drain into the IVC itself. <strong>Iliolumbar</strong> veins <strong>drain</strong> directly into the <strong>IVC</strong> and <strong>superficial epigastric</strong> vein <strong>drains</strong> via the <strong>femoral vein. </strong></p>\n<p>In <strong>IVC obstruction,</strong> the <strong>collateral</strong> channels open up to drain the blood from the<strong> lower limb </strong>into the superior vena cava <strong>(SVC)</strong> and then to the <strong>heart.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>main collateral</strong> channels are via <strong>azygous</strong> and<strong> hemizygous veins</strong> and <strong>vertebral venous plexuses.</strong> They communicate with the common iliac veins by the ascending lumbar veins and tributaries of the inferior vena cava. Additionally, blood is also drained by<strong> inferior epigastric veins &gt; superior epigastric veins</strong> &gt; internal thoracic veins &gt; brachiocephalic veins &gt; superior vena cava.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1534","text":"A 2-year-old girl playing ‌with small magnets sudden‍ly develops breathlessnes​s. Chest X-ray shows an i‌nhaled foreign body in on‌e of the bronchi. Where d‌oes the vein supplying th‌e most likely site of obs​truction drain into?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old girl playing with small magnets suddenly develops breathlessness. Chest X-ray shows an inhaled foreign body in one of the bronchi. Where does the vein supplying the most likely site of obstruction drain into?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right subc‌lavian vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right brachiocephalic vein‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Azygou‌s vein","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Accessory hemiazygous vein‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Azygous vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db4f875a6fd045238d1ff36b26e46592x1280x1692.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Azygous vein</strong></p><p>In the given case scenario of an inhaled foreign body, it is most likely to lodge into the <strong>right bronchus. </strong>The <strong>r</strong><strong>ight bronchial veins</strong> drain to the <strong>azygos vein. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Left bronchial veins</strong> drain into the <strong>accessory hemiazygos</strong> vein or <strong>left superior intercostal</strong> vein.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical significance:</strong></p>\n<p>The right principal bronchus is wider, shorter, and more vertical. This is why an inhaled foreign body is more likely to lodge there. Also, the tracheal bifurcation directs the foreign body to the right lung</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1535","text":"At what level does the hem‌izygous vein cross from t‍he left to the right side​?","raw_text":"At what level does the hemizygous vein cross from the left to the right side?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T‌8","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"T‌10","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T‌12","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T‌6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. T8","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. T8</strong></p><p>The <strong>hemiazygos vein</strong> crosses from the<strong> left to the right</strong> side at the level of <strong>T8</strong> vertebrae.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"o-LSVzoj","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"42_medical_entomology___mosquitoes_and_flies","title":"42.Medical Entomology   Mosquitoes And Flies","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB7753","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding mosquitoe​s?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding mosquitoes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the definitive host ​in malaria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mansonia is a vector for M​alayan filariasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Life span of mosquitoes is​ around 8 to 34 days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All mosquitoes can travel ​only for 100 metres","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. All mosquitoes can travel only for 100 metres","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. All mosquitoes can travel only for 100 metres</strong></p><p>Most <strong>mosquitoes</strong> can travel or <strong>fly</strong> for distances <strong>up to</strong> <strong>11 kms</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7751","text":"Which of the following spe​cies of mosquitoes breed ​in dirty water and water ​bodies containing aquatic‌ vegetation respectively?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following species of mosquitoes breed in dirty water and water bodies containing aquatic vegetation respectively?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anopheles​ and Aedes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Culex and​ Mansonia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aedes and​ Anopheles","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mansonia ​and Culex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Culex and Mansonia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Culex and Mansonia</strong></p><p><strong>Culex</strong> species of mosquitoes breed mostly in <strong>dirty</strong> and <strong>polluted water</strong>. <strong>Mansonia </strong>species of mosquitoes breed in water bodies containing<strong> aquatic vegetation</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7752","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about la​rva of mosquitoes?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about larva of mosquitoes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anopheles larva has siphon​ tube","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Culex larva lies in water ​with head faced downwards​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anopheles larva lies paral​lel to the undersurface o​f water","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Culex larva has siphon tub​e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anopheles larva has siphon tube","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a20b8cbdb2664eb3a8faf7ad8f41a55ex850x605.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5de13bfbbc394f28ae2a63e8f111c134x1280x845.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anopheles larva has siphon tube</strong></p><p><strong>Anopheles</strong> larva has <strong>no siphon tube</strong> as shown in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image given below shows <strong>Culex</strong> larva having a <strong>siphon tube</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7750","text":"Identify the species of mo​squito shown in the image​ below.","raw_text":"Identify the species of mosquito shown in the image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anop​heles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cu​lex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ae​des","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mans​onia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aedes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d6e2979470454cbe847a2e92dec80ff0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aedes</strong></p><p>The given image is of an<strong> Aedes</strong> mosquito.</p>\n<p>They can be identified with the <strong>stripes</strong> on the <strong>legs</strong> and <strong>body</strong>. They are also called <strong>tiger mosquitoes</strong> because of their stripes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7749","text":"A 25-year-old lady from a ​tribal area presented wit​h fever, chills, vomiting​, and abdominal pain for ‌4 days. The peripheral sm‌ear of the patient is giv​en below. Which of the fo‌llowing is true about the‌ mosquito which transmits‍ this disease?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old lady from a tribal area presented with fever, chills, vomiting, and abdominal pain for 4 days. The peripheral smear of the patient is given below. Which of the following is true about the mosquito which transmits this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wings ar​e spotted","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Breeds in po​lluted water","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Larva has ​siphon tube","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Keeps its body horizontal ​while feeding","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Wings are spotted","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c793ee43dbd94c24add02c0567a6e273.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Wings are spotted</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and peripheral smear showing <strong>ring form</strong>/ early trophozoites in erythrocytes are suggestive of <strong><em><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">Plasmodium falciparum</span></em> malaria</strong>. It is transmitted by <strong>Anopheles</strong> mosquitoes. They have <strong>spotted wings</strong>.</p>\n<p>They breed in clean water. Their larva has no siphon tube and lies parallel to the undersurface of water. They tend to sit with their heads low and their rare ends raised high, especially when feeding.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7755","text":"A patient presented with f​ever, headache, and retro​-orbital pain for 3 days.​ He tested positive for t‌he NS1 antigen. Which of ‌the following mosquitoes ​transmits the disease he'‌s suffering from?","raw_text":"A patient presented with fever, headache, and retro-orbital pain for 3 days. He tested positive for the NS1 antigen. Which of the following mosquitoes transmits the disease he's suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tiger m​osquito","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nuisance​ mosquito","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anopheles​ stephensi","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anopheles c​ulicifacies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tiger mosquito","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tiger mosquito</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the positive<strong> NS1</strong> <strong>antigen</strong> test is suggestive of <strong>dengue fever</strong>. It is transmitted by the <strong>Aedes</strong> mosquito, which is also called as <strong>tiger mosquito</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7758","text":"Which of the following dis​eases is not transmitted ​by Aedes mosquito? Chikun​gunya Yellow fever Japane‌se encephalitis Rift vall‌ey fever West Nile fever","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following diseases is not transmitted by Aedes mosquito?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Chikungunya</li>\n<li>Yellow fever</li>\n<li>Japanese encephalitis</li>\n<li>Rift valley fever</li>\n<li>West Nile fever</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, 4,​ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 and ​5 only","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1 and ​3 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2,​ and 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3 and 5 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3 and 5 only</strong></p><p><strong>Japanese encephalitis</strong> and <strong>West Nile fever</strong> are transmitted by <strong>Culex</strong> mosquitoes.</p>\n<p><strong>Chikungunya</strong>, <strong>yellow fever</strong>, and<strong> rift valley fever</strong> are transmitted by <strong>Aedes</strong> mosquitoes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7756","text":"A banker who is scheduled ​to travel to Congo is adv​ised to take the yellow f​ever vaccine. What is the‌ flight range of the mosq‌uito which transmits this​ disease?","raw_text":"A banker who is scheduled to travel to Congo is advised to take the yellow fever vaccine. What is the flight range of the mosquito which transmits this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 kil​ometre","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"100 m​etres","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"400 m​etres","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"11 kil​ometres","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 100 metres","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 100 metres</strong></p><p><strong>Yellow fever</strong> is transmitted by the <strong>Aedes</strong> mosquito. Its flight range is <strong>100 metres</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7759","text":"A patient with fever and m​ucosal bleeding is diagno​sed with dengue fever. Wh​ich of the following is f‌alse about the mosquito w‌hich transmits this disea​se?","raw_text":"A patient with fever and mucosal bleeding is diagnosed with dengue fever. Which of the following is false about the mosquito which transmits this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a ​day biter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Development of larva from ​egg does not require wate​r","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It also transmits yellow f​ever","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It has a striped body patt​ern","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Development of larva from egg does not require water","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Development of larva from egg does not require water</strong></p><p><strong>Dengue fever</strong> is transmitted by<strong> </strong>the <strong>Aedes</strong> mosquito. The <strong>conversion</strong> from the <strong>egg</strong> stage <strong>to</strong> the <strong>larval</strong> stage of this mosquito <strong>requires water</strong>.</p>\n<p>Eggs of any mosquito species do not survive without water. But the <strong>eggs</strong> of <strong>Aedes</strong> mosquitoes lie<strong> dormant</strong> in <strong>dry conditions</strong> for up to about nine months. They can<strong> hatch</strong> if exposed to <strong>favourable conditions</strong> such as <strong>water and food</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7771","text":"A 10-year-old boy is broug​ht to the OPD with compla​ints of fever and headach​e for 5 days. Serological‌ examination shows the pr‌esence of IgM antibodies ​to the JE virus. Which of‌ the following mosquitoes‌ transmits this disease?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old boy is brought to the OPD with complaints of fever and headache for 5 days. Serological examination shows the presence of IgM antibodies to the JE virus. Which of the following mosquitoes transmits this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anopheles​ sundaicus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Culex trita​eniorhynchus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aedes al​bopictus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anopheles c​ulicifacies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Culex tritaeniorhynchus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Culex tritaeniorhynchus</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Japanese encephalitis</strong>. It<strong> </strong>is transmitted<strong> </strong>by<strong> Culex</strong> mosquitoes like <em>Culex tritaeniorhynchus</em></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7760","text":"Match the following diseas​es and the mosquitoes whi​ch transmit them. Disease​ Mosquito l.Yellow fever ‌a.Mansonia ll.Bancroftian‌ filariasis b.Aedes lll.M​alaria c.Culex iV.Malayan‌ filariasis d.Anopheles","raw_text":"<p>Match the following diseases and the mosquitoes which transmit them.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 93px; width: 275.859375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\"><strong>Disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 103.859375px;\"><strong>Mosquito</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">l.Yellow fever</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103.859375px;\">a.Mansonia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">ll.Bancroftian filariasis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103.859375px;\">b.Aedes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">lll.Malaria</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103.859375px;\">c.Culex</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">iV.Malayan filariasis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103.859375px;\">d.Anopheles</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"l - c, ll - a, lll - d, iV​ - b","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"l - a, ll - b, lll - c, lV​ - d","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"l - b, ll - c, lll - d, lV​ - a","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"l - d, ll - a, lll - b, lV​ - c","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. l - b, ll - c, lll - d, lV - a","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. l - b, ll - c, lll - d, lV - a</strong></p><p>The correctly matched diseases with the mosquitoes which transmit them are:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 266.03125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\"><strong>Disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.03125px;\"><strong>Mosquito</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">Yellow fever</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.03125px;\">Aedes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">Bancroftian filariasis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.03125px;\">Culex</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">Malaria</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.03125px;\">Anopheles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">Malayan filariasis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.03125px;\">Mansonia</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7762","text":"Which of the following is ​true about pathogenic mos​quitoes?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about pathogenic mosquitoes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anopheles has spotted abdo​men","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mansonia lay​s eggs singly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Culex transmits yellow fev​er","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aedes lays ​eggs singly","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Aedes lays eggs singly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Aedes lays eggs singly</strong></p><p><strong>Aedes</strong> and <strong>Anopheles</strong> lay <strong>eggs</strong> <strong>singly</strong>.</p>\n<p>Culex lays eggs in rafts and Mansonia lays eggs in star-shaped clusters.</p>\n<p><strong>Anopheles</strong> has <strong>spotted wings </strong>while the other pathogenic species have unspotted wings.</p>\n<p><strong>Aedes</strong> transmits <strong>yellow fever</strong>, dengue and chikungunya. <strong>Culex</strong> is the main vector for <strong>JE</strong> and <strong>filariasis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7769","text":"You are posted in a PHC in​ a coastal region as a pa​rt of your rural postings​. Which of the following ‌Anopheles species is most‌ commonly found here?","raw_text":"You are posted in a PHC in a coastal region as a part of your rural postings. Which of the following Anopheles species is most commonly found here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anopheles ph​ilippinensis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anopheles​ stephensi","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anopheles ​fluviatilis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anopheles c​ulicifacies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anopheles stephensi","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anopheles stephensi</strong></p><p><em><strong>Anopheles stephensi</strong></em> and <em><strong>Anopheles sundaicus</strong></em> are found in <strong>coastal </strong>regions.</p>\n<p><em>Anopheles stephensi</em><strong> </strong>is also the main vector of malaria in urban regions.<em><strong> </strong>Anopheles culicifacies </em>and <em>Anopheles philippinensis</em> are found in the plains. <em>Anopheles fluviatilis </em>and <em>Anopheles minimus</em> are found in foot-hill regions. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7801","text":"Which of the following spe​cies of Anopheles mosquit​oes transmits urban malar​ia?","raw_text":"Which of the following species of Anopheles mosquitoes transmits urban malaria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anopheles ph​ilippinensis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anopheles​ stephensi","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anopheles ​fluviatilis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anopheles c​ulicifacies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anopheles stephensi","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anopheles stephensi</strong></p><p><strong>Urban malaria</strong> is transmitted by <em><strong>Anopheles stephensi</strong></em>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7802","text":"What is the distance aroun​d an airport or seaport t​hat has to be kept free o​f all mosquitoes?","raw_text":"What is the distance around an airport or seaport that has to be kept free of all mosquitoes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 kil​ometre","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"500 m​etres","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"400 m​etres","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"100 m​etres","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 400 metres","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 400 metres</strong></p><p>The distance around an airport or seaport that has to be kept free of all mosquitoes is <strong>400 metres</strong>. This helps in limiting the spread of vector-borne diseases to new geographic regions.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7896","text":"Identify the following vec​tor.","raw_text":"Identify the following vector.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phlebotomus​ argentipes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phlebotomu​s papatasi","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Musca d​omestica","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Triatoma ​infestans","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Musca domestica","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a39cd98d336143b7b6e2c3bcb324c945.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Musca domestica</strong></p><p>The given image is of a <strong>housefly</strong>, whose scientific name is <strong><em>Musca domestica</em></strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7899","text":"Which of the following is ​not a method of disease t​ransmission by houseflies​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a  method of disease transmission by houseflies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bit​ing","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Defec​ation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vomit​ drop","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mechanical ​transmission","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Biting","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Biting</strong></p><p><strong>Houseflies</strong> do not transmit diseases by biting. They transmit diseases by <strong>mechanical transmission</strong> by their legs or feet,<strong> vomit drop</strong>,<strong> </strong>and <strong>defecation</strong>.</p>\n<p>They transmit a variety of diseases like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Typhoid</li>\n<li>Polio</li>\n<li>Dysentery</li>\n<li>Amoebiasis</li>\n<li>Anthrax</li>\n<li>Yaws</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7915","text":"Which disease is transmitt​ed by the following vecto​r that is found in cracks​ and crevices of walls in‌ the day time?","raw_text":"Which disease is transmitted by the  following vector that is found in cracks and crevices of walls in the day time?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Japanese e​ncephalitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Visceral le​ishmaniasis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Den​gue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trypano​somiasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Visceral leishmaniasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2906dd9502a9470fbf4d076e74e8e21a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Visceral leishmaniasis</strong></p><p>The given image is of a <strong>sandfly</strong>. It transmits <strong>visceral leishmaniasis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Sandfly </strong>acts as a <strong>vector</strong> for the following diseases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Kala-azar</li>\n<li>Oriental sore</li>\n<li>Sandfly fever</li>\n<li>Oroya fever</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Japanese encephalitis</strong> is transmitted by<strong> </strong>the<strong> Culex </strong>mosquito. <strong>Dengue</strong> is transmitted by the <strong>Aedes</strong> mosquito.</p>\n<p><strong>Trypanosomiasis</strong> is transmitted by the<strong> tsetse fly</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7811","text":"Sandfly does not transmit:​","raw_text":"Sandfly does not transmit:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kala​ azar","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Orient​al sore","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oraya​ fever","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trench​ fever","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Trench fever","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Trench fever</strong></p><p><strong>Trench fever </strong>is not transmitted by sandflies. It is transmitted by<strong> lice</strong> and caused by<strong><em> Bartonella quintana</em></strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7921","text":"Which of the following dis​eases is transmitted by t​he vector shown in the im​age given below?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases is transmitted by the vector shown in the image given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onchoce​rciasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kyasanur fo​rest disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oroya​ fever","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sleeping​ sickness","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sleeping sickness","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2cc593c5bfbc4455997f5c08c9aab2e5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sleeping sickness</strong></p><p>The vector shown in the image is a <strong>tsetse fly</strong>.<strong> </strong>It transmits <strong>African sleeping sickness</strong> (African human trypanosomiasis).</p>\n<p>Tsetse flies belong to the genus <strong>Glossina</strong>. They<strong> </strong>have not demonstrated insecticide resistance. Methods of tsetse fly <strong>control</strong> include spraying of <strong>DDT</strong>,<strong> clearing</strong> of <strong>vegetation</strong>, destruction of <strong>wild game</strong> and <strong>genetic control</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7924","text":"A patient presents with bl​urring of vision and a pa​inless swelling over his ​left elbow which has grad‌ually been increasing in ‌size over the past 1 year​. On examination, he is n‌oted to have patchy depig‌mentation on his legs. Wh‍ich of the following vect‌ors transmits the disease‍ he's suffering from?","raw_text":"A patient presents with blurring of vision and a painless swelling over his left elbow which has gradually been increasing in size over the past 1 year. On examination, he is noted to have patchy depigmentation on his legs. Which of the following vectors transmits the disease he's suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"San​dfly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blac​kfly","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rat ​flea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tsets​e fly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Blackfly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e7b5544fa22b476699b3ddb349a14c73x720x510.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Blackfly</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> onchocerciasis</strong>, also known as<strong> </strong>river blindness. It is transmitted by <strong>blackfly</strong>.</p>\n<p>The usual presentation of onchocerciasis is the formation of a subcutaneous nodule or <strong>onchocercoma</strong>. It is a firm <strong>non-tender nodule</strong> arising from <strong>bony sites</strong> like the scalp, elbow, ribs, and iliac crest. It is associated with <strong>pruritus</strong> and <strong>rashes</strong>. Patients also present with patchy depigmentation on the legs called <strong>leopard skin</strong>.</p>\n<p>The presence of microfilaria in the eye causes <strong>photophobia</strong> and gradual <strong>loss of vision</strong>. The most severe manifestations are <strong>sclerosing keratitis</strong> and <strong>chorioretinitis</strong> which can lead to blindness.</p>\n<p>Blackfly is also called <strong>Simulium</strong>. It has a <strong>flight range </strong>of almost <strong>100 miles</strong>, making control of adult flies difficult. <strong>Abate</strong> is widely used as a <strong>larvicidal insecticide</strong>. The image given below shows a blackfly.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7747","text":"Spot the wrongly matched a​rthropod and disease.","raw_text":"Spot the wrongly matched arthropod and disease.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Housefly – P​oliomyelitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Louse – Epi​demic typhus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sandfly – O​riental sore","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blackfly – C​hagas disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Blackfly – Chagas disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Blackfly – Chagas disease</strong></p><p><strong>Blackflies </strong>(<em>Simulium</em>) are the vector for<strong> onchocerciasis </strong>and not Chaga's disease.</p>\n<p>An <strong>arthropod</strong> or an invertebrate that <strong>transmits infection</strong> is called a <strong>vector</strong>. It transmits infection by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inoculation into or through the skin or mucous membrane</li>\n<li>Biting</li>\n<li>Deposit of infective materials on skin or food or other objects</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>Chagas disease </strong>is transmitted by the <strong>reduviid bugs</strong> or kissing bugs or triatomines.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"3kmr5wsI","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"42_asthma___copd","title":"42.Asthma & Copd","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB9705","text":"Which among the following ​about idiosyncratic asthm‌a is false?","raw_text":"Which among the following about idiosyncratic asthma is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presents i​n childhood","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Normal I​gE levels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Commonly have concomitant ​nasal polyps","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Negative skin test for com​mon inhalant allergens","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Presents in childhood","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Presents in childhood</strong></p><p><strong>Idiosyncratic </strong>or <strong>intrinsic asthma</strong> does not present in childhood. It typically shows a later onset of disease (<strong>adult-onset asthma</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9709","text":"A 36-year-old woman presen​ted with wheezing after t‌aking aspirin for a heada​che. Which of the followi​ng is true regarding this‌ condition?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old woman presented with wheezing after taking aspirin for a headache. Which of the following is true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Raised seru​m IgE levels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Positive skin test for inh​aled allergens","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Associated with nasal poly​ps","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A type of extrinsic asthma​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Associated with nasal polyps","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Associated with nasal polyps</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> aspirin-sensitivity asthma</strong>. It is closely associated with <strong>nasal polyps</strong>.</p>\n<p>Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease (AERD), also known as <strong>Samter’s triad</strong> or<strong> aspirin-sensitive asthma</strong>. It is a chronic medical condition that consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong> Intrinsic asthma</strong></li>\n<li>Recurrent sinus disease with nasal polyps</li>\n<li>Sensitivity to aspirin and other NSAIDs</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It has <strong>normal serum IgE</strong> levels and a <strong>negative skin</strong> <strong>test</strong> for inhaled allergens.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3406","text":"What is the best way to di​agnose patients with susp‌ected bronchial asthma?","raw_text":"What is the best way to diagnose patients with suspected bronchial asthma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chest​ X-ray","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Skin test for inhaled alle​rgens","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"FEV1 ​values","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Demonstration of reversibl​e obstruction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Demonstration of reversible obstruction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Demonstration of reversible obstruction</strong></p><p>The best way to diagnose bronchial asthma is by the <strong>demonstration of reversible obstruction </strong>of the airways. Following the administration of an inhaled beta 2 agonist, a <strong>12%</strong> and <strong>200 ml increase</strong> in the FEV1 demonstrates the reversibility of airway obstruction.</p>\n<p>Options A and B: Chest X-ray and skin test for inhaled allergens are not diagnostic of bronchial asthma.</p>\n<p>Option C: FEV1 values are measured to calculate FEV1/FVC ratio to diagnose any obstructive pathology and not specifically for asthma alone. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3403","text":"A young woman is rushed to​ ED due to wheezing and g‌asping for breath. She is​ unable to speak in full ​sentences. Her husband re‌ports that she has had a ‌cough with fever for 3 da‌ys. On examination, PR- 1‌21/min, RR- 32/min, and h‍as cyanosis. Her ABG anal‍ysis and chest X-ray are ​given below. What is the ​likely diagnosis? ABG ana​lysis: pH- 7.48 PaO2- 64 ‍mmHg PaCO2- 32 mmHg HCO3-‌ 25 mEq/L","raw_text":"<p>A young woman is rushed to ED due to wheezing and gasping for breath. She is unable to speak in full sentences. Her husband reports that she has had a cough with fever for 3 days. On examination, PR- 121/min, RR- 32/min, and has cyanosis. Her ABG analysis and chest X-ray are given below. What is the likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>ABG analysis: </p>\n<p>pH- 7.48</p>\n<p>PaO2- 64 mmHg</p>\n<p>PaCO2- 32 mmHg</p>\n<p>HCO3- 25 mEq/L</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute respiratory distress​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Asthma with impending fail​ure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute pne​umothorax","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute sev​ere asthma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acute severe asthma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/080872a670a842b0b3b3b4f511f2526a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acute severe asthma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of <strong>dyspnea</strong>, <strong>tachycardia</strong> along with the ABG analysis suggestive of <strong>respiratory alkalosis</strong> and chest-Xray showing <strong>hyperinflated lungs</strong> is pointing to a diagnosis of <strong>acute severe asthma</strong>. A history of cough with fever is indicating a probable <strong>respiratory infection</strong> to have triggered the asthma exacerbation. </p>\n<p>Features of acute severe asthma vs asthma with impending respiratory failure: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 456px; width: 464px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224.045px;\"><strong>Acute severe asthma</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 225.99px;\"><strong>Asthma with impending respiratory failure</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224.045px;\">Dyspneic, speaks in words</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225.99px;\">Mute patient</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224.045px;\">Agitated patient</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225.99px;\">Silent / confused patient</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224.045px;\">Heart rate &gt; 120 /min, tachycardia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225.99px;\">Relative bradycardia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224.045px;\">Pulsus paradoxus present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225.99px;\">Pulsus paradoxus absent (due to respiratory muscle fatigue)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224.045px;\">Accessory muscles of respiration used</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225.99px;\">Paradoxical breathing is seen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224.045px;\">Loud wheeze throughout the respiration</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225.99px;\">Silent chest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224.045px;\">Peak expiratory flow rate &lt; 40 ml/min</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225.99px;\">Peak expiratory flow rate &lt; 25 ml/min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224.045px;\">Respiratory alkalosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225.99px;\">Respiratory acidosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 224.045px;\">Hyperventilation leading to low or normal PaCO2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225.99px;\">Normal or rising PaCO2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: The X-ray is not showing the features of<strong> ARDS</strong> and <strong>pneumothorax</strong>, hence they are ruled out as the right options. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3407","text":"Which of the following dru​gs is not recommended in ‌the treatment of patients​ with bronchial asthma?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is not recommended in the treatment of patients with bronchial asthma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium cr​omoglycate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zafir​lukast","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Roflu​milast","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dupi​lumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Roflumilast","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Roflumilast</strong></p><p><strong>Roflumilast</strong> is a phosphodiesterase-4 inhibitor used in the treatment of <strong>COPD</strong>, not asthma.</p>\n<p><strong>Drugs used</strong> in the <strong>treatment of bronchial asthma</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Bronchodilators</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Beta-2 sympathomimetics: Salmeterol, formoterol</li>\n<li>Methylxanthines: Theophylline, aminophylline</li>\n<li>Anticholinergics: Ipratropium bromide, tiotropium bromide</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Anti-inflammatory </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Corticosteroids (inhalational): Beclomethasone, budesonide, fluticasone</li>\n<li>Corticosteroids (systemic): Prednisolone, hydrocortisone</li>\n<li>Mast cell stabilizers: <strong>Sodium cromoglycate</strong>, ketotifen</li>\n<li>Leukotriene antagonists: Montelukast, <strong>zafirlukast</strong></li>\n<li>Anti-IgE: Omalizumab</li>\n<li>Anti-IL5: Mepolizumab, reslizumab</li>\n<li>Anti-IL4 receptor: <strong>Dupilumab</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3408","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding leukotrie‌ne modifiers in the manag​ement of bronchial asthma​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding leukotriene modifiers in the management of bronchial asthma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ketotifen belongs to this ​group","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Associated with Churg- Str​auss syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Protect against exercise-i​nduced asthma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diminishes nocturnal sympt​oms of asthma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ketotifen belongs to this group","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ketotifen belongs to this group</strong></p><p><strong>Ketotifen</strong> is an example of a <strong>mast cell stabilizer</strong>, not leukotriene modifiers.</p>\n<p><strong>Leukotriene modifiers </strong>are a group of agents used for mild persistent asthma. They <strong>block LT1-receptors</strong> thus causing reduced eosinophilic inflammation and causing bronchodilator. They include <strong>montelukast</strong>, <strong>zafirlukast</strong>, and<strong> zileuton</strong>.</p>\n<p>Leukotriene modifiers are less effective than inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) in controlling asthma. They are used as add-on therapy in some patients whose asthma is not controlled with low doses of ICS.</p>\n<p><strong>Churg–Strauss syndrome</strong> (CSS) is a rare granulomatous small vessel vasculitis, that affects the heart, kidneys, peripheral nerves, etc. It occurs against a background of long-standing asthma and marked peripheral eosinophilia. Several cases of CSS are associated with the use of <strong>montelukast</strong> and <strong>zafirlukast</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9951","text":"Treatment of choice for pa​tients with type 2 brittl‌e asthma is:","raw_text":"Treatment of choice for patients with type 2 brittle asthma is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhaled S​albutamol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subcutaneou​s salbutamol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subcutaneous​ terbutaline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subcutaneou​s adrenaline","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Subcutaneous adrenaline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Subcutaneous adrenaline</strong></p><p>The<strong> treatment</strong> of choice for <strong>type II brittle asthma</strong> is <strong>subcutaneous adrenaline</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Brittle asthma</strong> is a type of <strong>severe asthma</strong> where patients have very serious and often, life-threatening attacks despite appropriate therapy. It is of two types:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 156px; width: 671.984375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 327px; text-align: center; height: 31px;\"><strong>Type 1</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 330.984375px; text-align: center; height: 31px;\"><strong>Type 2</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 62px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 327px; height: 62px;\">\n<p>A persistent pattern of variability in PEFR (peak expiratory flow rate) readings</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 330.984375px; height: 62px;\">\n<p>Generally normal or near-normal PEFR readings but few unpredictable exacerbations occur without an obvious trigger</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 327px; height: 36px;\">Oral steroids, infusion of bronchodilators, and dietary exclusion of allergens may be helpful</td>\n<td style=\"width: 330.984375px; height: 36px;\"> Respond only to subcutaneous epinephrine</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9648","text":"Which of the following cla​ss of drugs cannot be use‌d in the treatment of pat​ients with bronchial asth​ma?","raw_text":"Which of the following class of drugs cannot be used in the treatment of patients with bronchial asthma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IL-4 recepto​r antagonists","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti–IL-5 ​antibodies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti IgE​ antibody","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IL- 1 an​tagonist","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. IL- 1 antagonist","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. IL- 1 antagonist</strong></p><p>The treatment options for asthma do not include IL-1 antagonists (anakinra). <strong>Anakinra</strong> is used in the treatment of <strong>rheumatoid arthritis. </strong></p>\n<p>Cytokines mediating airway inflammation in asthma: <strong>IL-4, IL-5, IL-13, TGF-β</strong>, and chemokines.</p>\n<p><strong>Omalizumab</strong> is a monoclonal antibody <strong>against IgE</strong>. It is available for the treatment of children ≥6 years of age with documented allergen-induced asthma that is inadequately controlled by inhaled corticosteroids.  </p>\n<p>The new treatment options for asthma under clinical trial are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mepolizumab:</strong> anti–IL-5 antibody</li>\n<li><strong>Benralizumab: </strong>anti–IL-5 receptor antibody</li>\n<li><strong>Dupilumab:</strong> anti–IL-4 receptor antibody</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3402","text":"A 6-year-old asthmatic boy​ is rushed to the casualt‌y. He is not responding t​o the commands and is hav​ing fast shallow breathin‌g. On auscultation, the c‌hest is silent and parado‌xical breathing is presen‌t. ABG analysis shows res‍piratory acidosis. What i‍s the best step in manage​ment for him?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old asthmatic boy is rushed to the casualty. He is not responding to the commands and is having fast shallow breathing.  On auscultation, the chest is silent and paradoxical breathing is present. ABG analysis shows respiratory acidosis. What is the best step in management for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperbaric o​xygen therapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"MDI with salbutamol and co​rticosteroids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Immediate endotracheal int​ubation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intravenous magnesium sulf​ate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Immediate endotracheal intubation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Immediate endotracheal intubation</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>severe asthma</strong> with impending <strong>respiratory failure</strong>. The best step in the management is <strong>immediate endotracheal intubation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Features of asthma with impending respiratory failure are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mute or confused patient</li>\n<li><strong>Paradoxical breathing</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Silent</strong> <strong>chest</strong> on auscultation </li>\n<li>Relative bradycardia</li>\n<li>Peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR) &lt;25ml/min</li>\n<li>Arterial blood gas analysis shows<strong> respiratory acidosis</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Hyperbaric oxygen is given in CO poisoning and high altitude pulmonary edema.</p>\n<p>Option B: MDI with salbutamol and corticosteroids is only useful for acute severe asthma.</p>\n<p>Option D: Intravenous magnesium sulfate is not routinely used.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9953","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin‌g COPD?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding COPD?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased resistance is se​en in airways ≤2 mm diame‌ter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Changes in large airways c​ause cough and sputum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventilation-perfusion mism​atching is seen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rise in post bronchodilato​r FEV1&amp;gt;12%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rise in post bronchodilator FEV1>12%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rise in post bronchodilator FEV1&gt;12%</strong></p><p>The rise in post-bronchodilator FEV1&gt;12% is a feature of asthma, not COPD. It suggests reversible airflow obstruction. </p>\n<p>Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (<strong>COPD</strong>) is a chronic respiratory disease characterized by the presence of <strong>airflow obstruction</strong> that is <strong>partially reversible</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Cigarette smoking</strong> is the <strong>most common cause</strong> of COPD. Cigarette smoke exposure affects large airways, small airways, and alveoli:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Changes in<strong> large airways </strong>cause <strong>cough </strong>and<strong> sputum</strong></li>\n<li>Changes in <strong>small airways</strong> (<strong>≤2mm</strong>) and alveoli are responsible for physiologic alterations</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The major site of<strong> increased resistance</strong> in most individuals with COPD is in airways ≤2 mm diameter.</p>\n<p>Characteristic cellular changes include goblet cell metaplasia. <strong>Goblet cells</strong> are mucus-secreting cells that replace surfactant-secreting Club cells.</p>\n<p>Nonuniform ventilation and <strong>ventilation-perfusion mismatching</strong> are characteristic of COPD.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3414","text":"What happens to Reid index​ in patients with chronic‌ bronchitis?","raw_text":"What happens to Reid index in patients with chronic bronchitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incr​eases","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decr​eases","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Remain​s same","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increases initially and th​en decreases","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b41a1fc073494da7b25ccb1d8eb442bdx1280x1391.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increases</strong></p><p><strong>Reid index</strong> is <strong>increased</strong> in chronic bronchitis.</p>\n<p>Reid index is the ratio of the thickness of the mucous gland layer to the thickness of the bronchial wall.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3412","text":"Which of the following fin​dings is least likely to ‌be seen in patients with ​COPD?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings is least likely to be seen in patients with COPD?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Barrel​ chest","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hoover​'s sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cyan​osis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Club​bing","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Clubbing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f427aee2ac5f4865be9410361221c7bex960x1280.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Clubbing</strong></p><p>Clubbing is not a sign of COPD. Its presence should trigger the investigation of the causes of clubbing, particularly<strong> lung cancer</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Signs of COPD</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Signs of respiratory distress\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Tripod posture </strong>- facilitates the use of accessory muscles of respiration</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pursed lip breathing - seen in emphysema</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Central cyanosis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Signs of cachexia\n<ul>\n<li>Severe weight loss</li>\n<li>Bitemporal wasting</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Signs of hypercapnia (<strong>type 2 respiratory failure</strong>) - more common in chronic bronchitis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Barrel-shaped chest </strong>- increase in the anteroposterior diameter</li>\n<li>Enlarged lung volumes</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Positive Hoover's sign </strong>- paradoxical inward movement of the chest during inspiration</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Early inspiratory coarse crackles  </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Normal vesicular breath sounds of reduced-intensity with bilaterally prolonged expiration</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Blue bloaters - </strong>cyanotic (blue) and edematous (bloaters), <strong>chronic bronchitis</strong> predominant features</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Pink puffers</strong> - thin, non-cyanotic (pink) at rest, and severely dyspneic (puffers), <strong>emphysema</strong> predominant features </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Causes of clubbing</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Pulmonary</strong></li>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bronchogenic carcinoma</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lung abscess, empyema, bronchiectasis</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Chronic interstitial pneumonitis</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cystic fibrosis</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sarcoidosis</li>\n</ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Gastrointestinal </strong></li>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Inflammatory bowel disease</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sprue</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Neoplasms - esophagus, liver, bowel</li>\n</ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Cardiovascular</strong></li>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cyanotic congenital heart disease</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Subacute bacterial endocarditis</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Aortic aneurysm</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Arteriovenous fistula</li>\n</ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Endocrine </strong>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hyperthyroidism (Grave’s disease)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows the characteristic <strong>tripod posture</strong> in a patient with COPD. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3411","text":"A 28-year-old coal miner p​resented with breathing d‌ifficulties. He reported ​that his symptoms get agg​ravated at work. His ches‌t X-ray showed features o‌f emphysema. On evaluatio‌n, he was found to have a‌n α1-antitrypsin deficien‍cy. Which pathological ty‍pe of emphysema is most l​ikely present in this pat​ient?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old coal miner presented with breathing difficulties. He reported that his symptoms get aggravated at work. His chest X-ray showed features of emphysema. On evaluation, he was found to have an α1-antitrypsin deficiency. Which pathological type of emphysema is most likely present in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Centri​-acinar","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pan-a​cinar","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Irre​gular","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Para-​septal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pan-acinar","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pan-acinar</strong></p><p>The pathological type most likely to be present in this patient is <strong>pan-acinar emphysema. </strong>This type is commonly seen in patients with <strong>α1-antitrypsin deficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3417","text":"An elderly man is being ev​aluated for exertional dy‌spnea, cough, and weight ​loss. His chest CT is giv​en below. What is it sugg‌estive of?","raw_text":"An elderly man is being evaluated for exertional dyspnea, cough, and weight loss. His chest CT is given below. What is it suggestive of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Emphysema​tous lung","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Atypical ​pneumonia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcified ​granulomas","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interstiti​al fibrosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Emphysematous lung","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/34ead242e43644ef935196bd29c60e7f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2a4c2e6ed9534c45ae3752d9ca23c784x246x157.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Emphysematous lung</strong></p><p>The <strong>CT scan</strong> shows <strong>emphysematous lungs</strong>, with reduced parenchymal markings and focal areas of low attenuation. This represents the emphysematous destruction of the lung, indicative of<strong> hyperinflation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Radiographic studies in COPD show:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Emphysematous bullae</li>\n<li>A paucity of parenchymal markings</li>\n<li>Hyperlucency suggests the presence of emphysema</li>\n<li>Increased lung volumes and<strong> flattening </strong>of the<strong> diaphragm</strong> suggest hyperinflation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: Atypical pneumonia shows patchy bilateral ground-glass opacities.</p>\n<p>Option C: Calcified granulomas are seen as dense, bright nodules.</p>\n<p>Option D: Interstitial fibrosis shows a honeycomb pattern of the lung parenchyma as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3409","text":"A 55-years old chronic smo​ker presented with chroni‌c cough with expectoratio​n. He has been having sim​ilar episodes for the pas‌t 2 years. He also report‌ed that he had to rest ev‌ery few minutes while wal‌king due to breathlessnes‍s. Pulmonary function tes‍ts showed FEV1/FVC of 0.5​ and FEV1 of 50%. Which o​f the following is not a ​feature of the given cond‍ition?","raw_text":"A 55-years old chronic smoker presented with chronic cough with expectoration. He has been having similar episodes for the past 2 years. He also reported that he had to rest every few minutes while walking due to breathlessness. Pulmonary function tests showed FEV1/FVC of  0.5 and FEV1  of 50%. Which of the following is not a feature of the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Irreversible airflow limit​ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Affects small airway disea​se","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decrease in residual volum​e","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Associated with ineffectiv​e repair of elastin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Decrease in residual volume","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Decrease in residual volume</strong></p><p>The clinical picture is highly suggestive of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease(<strong>COPD</strong>). An <strong>increase</strong> in the <strong>residual volume</strong> is a feature of COPD.</p>\n<p>COPD is defined as a chronic respiratory disease characterized by the presence of <strong>airflow obstruction</strong> that is <strong>partially reversible</strong>. </p>\n<p>COPD is the clinical expression of <strong>abnormalities</strong> of <strong>airways</strong> and/or <strong>alveoli</strong> and includes: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Emphysema </strong>is defined pathologically as the abnormal and <strong>permanent dilation</strong> of <strong>airways</strong> distal to the terminal bronchiole, along with the destruction of their walls.</li>\n<li><strong>Chronic bronchitis </strong>is defined clinically as the presence of <strong>persistent cough</strong> with <strong>sputum production</strong> on most of the days for <strong>≥3 months</strong> in <strong>2 consecutive years</strong>. </li>\n<li><strong>Small airways diseases </strong>are conditions affecting<strong> airways</strong> of<strong> ≤2 mm</strong>, resulting in inflammation and narrowing of the same. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3416","text":"Chest X-ray of an emphysem​atous patient is as shown‌. What is the best invest​igation to measure his lu​ng volume?","raw_text":"Chest X-ray of an emphysematous patient is as shown.  What is the best investigation to measure his lung volume?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Body pleth​ysmography","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gas di​lution","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fractional​ exhaled NO","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"DL​CO","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Body plethysmography","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c6cb2450349c4f668a25e9ca1e9b2a76.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Body plethysmography</strong></p><p>Body plethysmography is the best investigation to measure lung volume in an emphysematous patient with<strong> bullous lesions</strong> as seen on the X-ray.</p>\n<p>To measure absolute lung volumes, two techniques are used:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Body plethysmography: </strong>It is used to measure the volume of any gas containing space in the thorax <strong>including bullae.</strong> It is accurate in both obstructive and restrictive diseases.</li>\n<li><strong>Inert gas dilution technique: </strong>It is used to measure the volume of regions that are well ventilated which <strong>excludes</strong> trapped gas or bullae. It is accurate in restrictive diseases only.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The amount of gas trapped in bullae can be measured by subtracting lung volumes measured by inert gas dilution technique from lung volume measured by body plethysmography.</p>\n<p>Fractional exhaled nitric oxide (F<sub>E</sub>NO) is a<strong> non-invasive test</strong> used in the diagnosis and monitoring of bronchial asthma. NO levels are <strong>increased</strong> in asthmatics and related to the eosinophilic inflammation in the airways.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3413","text":"A 66-year-old chronic smok​er presents with progress‌ive shortness of breath a​nd chronic cough. On exam​ination, he has a barrel ‌chest and has pursed-lip ‌breathing. Hyperresonance‌ and inspiratory wheezes ‌are heard. Pulmonary func‍tions are likely to revea‍l all of the following, e​xcept:","raw_text":"A 66-year-old chronic smoker presents with progressive shortness of breath and chronic cough. On examination, he has a barrel chest and has pursed-lip breathing. Hyperresonance and inspiratory wheezes are heard. Pulmonary functions are likely to reveal all of the following, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased vi​tal capacity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased diffusion capaci​ty for carbon monoxide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increased total lung capac​ity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased FE​V1/FVC ratio","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increased diffusion capacity for carbon monoxide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increased diffusion capacity for carbon monoxide</strong></p><p>The given clinical diagnosis is suggestive of <strong>COPD</strong> and the underlying pathological change is likely to be<strong> emphysema. </strong>It is associated with <strong>a decreased</strong> diffusion capacity for carbon monoxide (<strong>DLCO</strong>).</p>\n<p>Emphysema is defined pathologically as the <strong>abnormal</strong> and <strong>permanent dilation</strong> of <strong>airways</strong> distal to the terminal bronchiole, along with the <strong>destruction</strong> of their <strong>walls</strong>. This leads to hyperinflation due to air trapping and impaired diffusion of gases.</p>\n<p><strong>Pulmonary function tests in COPD:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>TLC: Normal or increased</li>\n<li>FEV1: Decreased (&lt;80% predicted)</li>\n<li>FVC: Decreased</li>\n<li>FEV1/FVC: Decreased</li>\n<li>DLCO: Decreased</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Diseases associated with lowered DLCO:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Emphysema</li>\n<li>Interstitial Lung disease</li>\n<li>Primary pulmonary hypertension</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note - <strong>Bronchial asthma</strong> is associated with an <strong>increased DLCO.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1630","text":"The Gold's criteria for se​vere COPD are:","raw_text":"The Gold's criteria for severe COPD are:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"FEV1/FVC &amp;lt;0.7 and FEV1 ​≥ 30%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"FEV1/FVC &amp;lt;0.7 and FEV1 ​&amp;lt; 30%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"FEV1/FVC&amp;lt;0.7 and FEV1&amp;l​t; 20%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"FEV1/FVC&amp;lt;0.7 and FEV1 ≥​ 50%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. FEV1/FVC <0.7 and FEV1 ≥ 30%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. FEV1/FVC &lt;0.7 and FEV1 ≥ 30%</strong></p><p>Gold's criteria for severe COPD (stage III) are <strong>FEV1/FVC &lt;0.7</strong> and<strong> FEV1 ≥ 30% </strong>but &lt;50%</p>\n<p>The severity of COPD is assessed by Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease (GOLD) classification:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"77\">\n<p><strong>GOLD Stage</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"102\">\n<p><strong>Severity </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"198\">\n<p><strong>Spirometry </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"77\">\n<p>I</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"102\">\n<p>Mild</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"198\">\n<p>FEV1/FVC &lt;0.7 and FEV1 ≥80% predicted</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"77\">\n<p>II</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"102\">\n<p>Moderate</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"198\">\n<p>FEV1/FVC &lt;0.7 and FEV1 ≥50% but &lt;80% predicted</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"77\">\n<p>III</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"102\">\n<p>Severe</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"198\">\n<p>FEV1/FVC &lt;0.7 and FEV1 ≥30% but &lt;50% predicted</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"77\">\n<p>IV</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"102\">\n<p>Very severe</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"198\">\n<p>FEV1/FVC &lt;0.7 and FEV1 &lt;30% predicted</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: FEV1/FVC and FEV1 predicted are measured after the administration of a bronchodilator.</p>\n<p>Recent update: <strong>PRISm</strong> refers to a group of COPD patients with <strong>apparent normal spirometry</strong>, but with <strong>emphysema (as seen on radiographic evaluation)</strong> and a wide range of lung function impairment.  These patients have a <strong>higher mortality risk</strong> as compared to other COPD patients. The possible risk factors are exposure to smoking, high BMI, and reduced total lung capacity. Spirometry findings are <strong>low to normal FEV1</strong>, and <strong>normal FEV1/FVC ratio</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3415","text":"A patient presents to the ​OPD with shortness of bre‌ath. He reports walking s​lower than his peers due ​to breathlessness. He has‌ had two hospitalizations‌ for similar complaints t‌his year. His post-bronch‌odilator PFTs are FEV1/FV‍C &lt; 0.55 and FEV1 55%. Un‍der what category would h​e be classified based on ​GOLD criteria?","raw_text":"A patient presents to the OPD with shortness of breath. He reports walking slower than his peers due to breathlessness. He has had two hospitalizations for similar complaints this year. His post-bronchodilator PFTs are FEV1/FVC &lt; 0.55 and FEV1 55%.  Under what category would he be classified based on GOLD criteria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Moderate CO​PD, Group C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Moderate CO​PD, Group E","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Severe COP​D, Group A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Severe COP​D, Group D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Moderate COPD, Group E","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Moderate COPD, Group E</strong></p><p>This patient belongs to <strong>moderate COPD</strong>, <strong>group E</strong> category.</p>\n<p>The severity of COPD assessed by the Global Initiative for Lung Disease (GOLD) is classified as:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 281px; width: 356px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 64.8611px;\">\n<p><strong>GOLD Stage</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90.816px;\">\n<p><strong>Severity </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181.042px;\">\n<p><strong>Spirometry </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 64.8611px;\">\n<p>I</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90.816px;\">\n<p>Mild</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181.042px;\">\n<p>FEV1/FVC &lt;0.7 and FEV1 ≥80% predicted</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 64.8611px;\">\n<p>II</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90.816px;\">\n<p>Moderate</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181.042px;\">\n<p>FEV1/FVC &lt;0.7 and FEV1 ≥50% but &lt;80% predicted</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 64.8611px;\">\n<p>III</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90.816px;\">\n<p>Severe</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181.042px;\">\n<p>FEV1/FVC &lt;0.7 and FEV1 ≥30% but &lt;50% predicted</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 64.8611px;\">\n<p>IV</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 90.816px;\">\n<p>Very severe</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181.042px;\">\n<p>FEV1/FVC &lt;0.7 and FEV1 &lt;30% predicted</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>According to <strong>GOLD 2023 guidelines</strong>, COPD patients are classified into Group <strong>A, B, or E</strong> based on the <strong>severity of dyspnoea,</strong> <strong>frequency of exacerbations and hospitilization</strong>. Dyspnoea may be assessed using the modified MRC Dyspnoea scale or COPD Assessment Test (CAT score)</p>\n<table style=\"height: 43px;\" width=\"732\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 358px;\">&gt;=2 moderate exacerbations or &gt;=1 leading to hospitalisation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 358px;\">\n<p>Group E:<br/>                        <strong> LABA+LAMA</strong></p>\n<p>consider LABA+LAMA+ICS* if blood eosinophil count &gt;=300</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<table style=\"height: 91px;\" width=\"738\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 240.711px;\">0 or 1 moderate exacerbation (not leading to hospital admissions)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241.383px;\">\n<p>Group A: mMRC 0-1, CAT&lt;10</p>\n<p>               <strong> Bronchodilator</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.906px;\">\n<p>Group B: mMRC &gt;=2, CAT &gt;=10</p>\n<p>         <strong> LABA + LAMA*</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>* single inhaler therapy maybe more convenient and effective than multiple inhalers</p>\n<p>ICS - inhaled corticosteroids<br/>LAMA - Long-acting Muscarinic Antagonists<br/>LABA - Long-acting Beta Agonists</p>\n<p><strong>Modified MRC (mMRC) dyspnoea scale:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>0- Breathless only with strenuous exercise</li>\n<li>1- Breathless when hurrying on a level or walking up a hill</li>\n<li>2- Walks slower than peers or has to stop for breath when walking on level</li>\n<li>3- Stops for breath after walking about 100m or for a few minutes</li>\n<li>4- Breathless at rest</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3419","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding acute exa‌cerbations in COPD patien​ts?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding acute exacerbations in COPD patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"May not be accompanied wit​h fever","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Viral infection is a commo​n precipitating factor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glucocorticoids are contra​indicated","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"May present with congestiv​e heart failure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glucocorticoids are contraindicated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glucocorticoids are contraindicated</strong></p><p><strong>Glucocorticoids</strong> are used during an <strong>exacerbation of COPD</strong>. They <strong>hasten recovery</strong> and reduce the chances of future exacerbations.</p>\n<p>Exacerbations are episodes of <strong>increased dyspnoea and cough</strong>, with a change in the amount and character of sputum. They are often <strong>triggered by an infection</strong> with one-third of cases having a viral etiology. They <strong>may or may not </strong>have other signs of illness such as <strong>fever</strong>, myalgias, and sore throat. In severe cases, they may present with pneumonia and<strong> congestive heart failure</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: While oral glucocorticoids are contraindicated in long-term treatment due to side effects, they are used for acute exacerbation of COPD.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0880","text":"A 60-year-old male patient​ who is a known case of C‌OPD presented with acute ​exacerbation and is admit​ted to the ICU. Which of ‌the following statements ‌is correct with regards t‌o the initial management ‌of this patient?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old male patient who is a known case of COPD presented with acute exacerbation and is admitted to the ICU. Which of the following statements is correct with regards to the initial management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-Invasive PPV should be​ given","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Invasive PPV should be giv​en","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IV corticosteroids should ​be administered","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Permissive hypercapnia is ​allowed","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Non-Invasive PPV should be given","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Non-Invasive PPV should be given</strong></p><p><strong>Non-invasive positive pressure ventilation </strong>(NIPPV) should be the <strong>first</strong> mode of ventilation used in COPD patients with acute respiratory failure provided there are <strong>no absolute contraindications. </strong>This approach improves gas exchange, reduces work of breathing and the need for intubation, decreases hospitalization duration and enhances overall survival outcomes.</p>\n<p><strong>Invasive positive pressure ventilation</strong> is only considered after <strong>failure</strong> of NIPPV, resulting in severe respiratory distress, life threatening hypoxemia, severe hypercarbia and/or acidosis, markedly impaired mental status, <strong>respiratory arrest</strong> or<strong> hemodynamic instability</strong> (Option B).</p>\n<p>Apart from NIPPV, <strong>short-acting inhaled beta 2 agonists </strong>(albuterol, salbutamol) with or without <strong>short-acting anticholinergics</strong> (ipratropium) and <strong>Long-acting anticholinergics </strong>(tiotropium) are recommended as the initial bronchodilators. <strong>Maintenance</strong> therapy with long-acting beta 2 agonists (formoterol) must be started as soon as possible.</p>\n<p><strong>Systemic corticosteroids </strong>are useful in the treatment of acute exacerbations of COPD as they reduce airway inflammation and improve lung function over time. However, the effects are not immediate. NIPPV on the other hand provides<strong> immediate relief </strong>to the patient and reduces the work of breathing. Therefore, it would not be the best choice in this case. (Option C)</p>\n<p><strong>Antibiotics</strong> can also be considered, as they reduce duration of hospitalisation. Treatment is given for <strong>5 days. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Permissive hypercapnia </strong>refers to a ventilation strategy where tidal volume is reduced in order to avoid hyperinflation-associated lung trauma. It is not preferred at the initial presentation. It is only permitted once invasive PPV is started following NIPPV failure (Option D) </p>\n<p>An<strong> exacerbation of COPD </strong>is defined as an event characterized by dyspnea and/or cough and sputum that worsen over &lt; 14 days. Exacerbations of COPD are often associated with <strong>increased</strong> <strong>local</strong> and <strong>systemic</strong> <strong>inflammation</strong> caused by airway infection, pollution, or other insults to the lungs.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9715","text":"A 45-year-old obese chroni​c smoker is rushed to the‌ ER with difficulty in br​eathing and a productive ​cough. He has a past hist‌ory of two similar episod‌es this year. On examinat‌ion, RR- 34/min, with use‌ of accessory muscles and‍ central cyanosis is pres‍ent. ABG is as shown. Che​st X-ray shows increased ​vascular markings. What i​s the best initial step o‍f management? ABG Analysi‌s: pH- 7.33 PaO2- 50 mmHg​ PaCO2- 48 mmHg HCO3‾- 27‌ mEq/L","raw_text":"<p>A 45-year-old obese chronic smoker is rushed to the ER with difficulty in breathing and a productive cough. He has a past history of two similar episodes this year. On examination, RR- 34/min, with use of accessory muscles and central cyanosis is present. ABG is as shown. Chest X-ray shows increased vascular markings. What is the best initial step of management?</p>\n<p>ABG Analysis:</p>\n<p>pH- 7.33</p>\n<p>PaO2- 50 mmHg</p>\n<p>PaCO2- 48 mmHg</p>\n<p>HCO3‾- 27 mEq/L</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low flow humidified oxygen​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"High flow humidified oxyge​n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Salbutamol and budesonide ​nebulisation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Slow infusion of theophyll​ine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Low flow humidified oxygen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Low flow humidified oxygen</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the ABG and X-ray findings is suggestive of <strong>acute exacerbation of COPD</strong> with respiratory failure. The best initial step of management is <strong>low-flow</strong> <strong>humidified</strong> <strong>oxygen.</strong></p>\n<p>Oxygen supplementation is a crucial component of acute therapy. Administering O2 should target a <strong>SpO2- 88-92%</strong> or <strong>PaO2- 60-70 mmHg</strong>. This helps to minimize the risk of worsening hypercapnia with excess supplemental oxygen.</p>\n<p>The given ABG shows<strong> respiratory acidosis</strong> as <strong>PaCO2 &gt;45 mmHg</strong>, and<strong> pH&lt;7.35</strong>. In respiratory acidosis, a trial of non-invasive ventilation is given. Hence, low-flow humidified oxygen is the first step in management.</p>\n<p>Option B: High flow oxygen causes dryness, crusting epistaxis, and damages the lung hence not used. </p>\n<p>Option C: Salbutamol and budesonide nebulization are not effective as initial management in exacerbation of COPD with respiratory failure.</p>\n<p>Option D: Methylxanthines are not recommended in the management of acute exacerbations due to their side effect profiles.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3420","text":"In patients with which of ​the following conditions ‌is NIPPV contraindicated?​","raw_text":"In patients with which of the following conditions is NIPPV contraindicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CO​PD","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Central hypoventilation sy​ndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiovascular instability​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chest wall​ disorders","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cardiovascular instability","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cardiovascular instability</strong></p><p>Noninvasive positive-pressure ventilation (<strong>NIPPV</strong>) is contraindicated in <strong>cardiovascular instability</strong>. NIPPV can impede venous filling of the heart, decrease cardiac output, and worsen/precipitate hypotension. Moreover, in unstable states with impending cardiovascular collapse,<strong> invasive ventilation</strong> is preferred.</p>\n<p><strong>Indications of NIPPV:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Apnea - central and obstructive</li>\n<li>Central hypoventilation syndrome</li>\n<li>Neuromuscular and chest wall disorders</li>\n<li>Respiratory failure - PaCO<sub>2 </sub>&gt;45 mmHg</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contraindications of NIPPV:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cardiovascular instability</strong></li>\n<li>Impaired mental status or inability to cooperate</li>\n<li>Copious secretions or the inability to clear secretions</li>\n<li>Craniofacial abnormalities</li>\n<li>Trauma precluding effective fitting of the mask</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3418","text":"Which of the following is ​not used in the long-term‌ treatment of COPD patien​ts?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not used in the long-term treatment of COPD patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Theoph​ylline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Azithr​omycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oral gluco​corticoids","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"N Acetyl​cysteine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Oral glucocorticoids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Oral glucocorticoids</strong></p><p><strong>Oral glucocorticoids</strong> are not used in the long-term treatment of COPD because of an <strong>unfavorable </strong>benefit/risk ratio.</p>\n<p>Chronic use of glucocorticoids is associated with significant side effects, including osteoporosis, weight gain, cataracts, glucose intolerance, and increased risk of infection.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment of COPD:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Smoking cessation</li>\n<li>Anticholinergic agents- Ipratropium bromide</li>\n<li>Beta Agonists- Long-acting inhaled β agonists, such as salmeterol or formoterol are commonly used. </li>\n<li>Methylxanthines- <strong>theophylline</strong> produces improvements in expiratory flow rates and vital capacity. </li>\n<li>Phosphodiesterase inhibitors - roflumilast is used when FEV1&lt;50%. </li>\n<li>Antibiotics- macrolides like <strong>azithromycin</strong> reduce exacerbations when used over one year</li>\n<li><strong><em>N</em>-acetylcysteine</strong> has been used in patients with COPD for both its mucolytic and antioxidant properties</li>\n<li>Oxygen therapy</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Non-pharmacologic therapies:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lung volume reduction surgery</li>\n<li>Lung transplantation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"75mz94gB","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"42_perimenopause__menopause_and_post_menopausal_bleeding","title":"42.Perimenopause, Menopause And Post Menopausal Bleeding","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MC0881","text":"Menopause is defined as ce‍ssation of menses for a p​eriod of _____.","raw_text":"Menopause is defined as cessation of menses for a period of _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 mo‍nths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6 mo‍nths","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"9 mo‍nths","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"12 m‍onths","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 12 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 12 months</strong></p><p>Menopause is defined as cessation of ovarian function for <strong>12 months</strong> resulting in permanent amenorrhoea. It is a <strong>retrospective diagnosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0879","text":"A woman with menorrhagia i‍s diagnosed with uterine ​fibroids. Prior to starti‌ng treatment, you want to‌ check if she is premenop​ausal. On which day of th​e menstrual cycle will yo​u estimate her FSH levels‌?","raw_text":"A woman with menorrhagia is diagnosed with uterine fibroids. Prior to starting treatment, you want to check if she is premenopausal. On which day of the menstrual cycle will you estimate her FSH levels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"On day 2-5 after last mens‍trual period","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"On day 11-14 after last me‍nstrual period","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"On the day of menstruation‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Just before ‍menstruation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. On day 2-5 after last menstrual period","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. On day 2-5 after last menstrual period</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 79\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The follicular stimulating hormone (<strong>FSH</strong>) level is studied on <strong>day</strong> <strong>2–5 after</strong> <strong>the</strong> <strong>last menstrual period</strong> to detect the <strong>premenopausal</strong> stage.</p>\n<p>It is a period of 3–4 years before menopause and is associated with mild ovarian hormonal deficiency leading to menstrual disorders, especially menorrhagia.</p>\n<p>The <strong>climacteric phase</strong> is the phase of waning of ovarian activity. It begins 2 to 3 years before menopause and may continue 2-5 years after menopause. It marks the <strong>transition from active to inactive ovarian function</strong> and involves physical, sexual, and psychological adjustments.</p>\n<p>The <strong>predictors </strong>of <strong>approaching menopause</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>fall</strong> in the level of <strong>inhibin B</strong> and a corresponding <strong>rise</strong> in <strong>FSH</strong> level</li>\n<li>A <strong>fall</strong> in the level of anti-Mullerian hormone (<strong>AMH</strong>) - This is a better marker of ovarian reserve and can be tested on any day of the cycle.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0893","text":"What is the diagnostic val‍ue of FSH in menopause?","raw_text":"What is the diagnostic value of FSH in menopause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt; 40‍ mIU/mL","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt;30‍ mIU/mL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt;50‍ mIU/mL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;20‍ mIU/mL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. > 40 mIU/mL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. &gt; 40 mIU/mL</strong></p><p>The diagnostic value of <strong>FSH</strong> in menopause is<strong> &gt;40 mIU/mL</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hormone levels in menopausal women:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 271px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.172px;\"><strong>Hormone</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.828px;\"><strong>Level</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.172px;\">Estradiol </td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.828px;\">5 to 25 pg/ml</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.172px;\">FSH</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.828px;\">&gt;40 mIU/mL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.172px;\">LH</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.828px;\">50-100 mIUL/ml</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.172px;\">Growth hormone, inhibin B,<strong> </strong>AMH </td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.828px;\">Decreases </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0886","text":"What is the average age of‍ menopause?","raw_text":"What is the average age of menopause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"55 y‍ears","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"47 y‍ears","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"45 y‍ears","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"50 y‍ears","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 47 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 47 years</strong></p><p>The average age of menopause in India is <strong>47 years,</strong> and ranges from 45 to 50 years. </p>\n<p>Menopause sets in when the follicle number falls below 1000. If this happens <strong>before 40</strong> years, it is termed <strong>premature menopause</strong> while attaining it <strong>after 55</strong> years is <strong>delayed menopause</strong>.</p>\n<p>The age at which menopause is attained is not related to age at menarche, race, number of pregnancies, socio-economic status, or oral contraceptive pills usage. However, it is directly <strong>influenced</strong> by <strong>smoking</strong> and <strong>genetic</strong> disposition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0902","text":"A menopausal woman present‍s with complaints of hot ​flushes. Which of the fol‌lowing neurotransmitters ‌are responsible for these​ symptoms?","raw_text":"A menopausal woman presents with complaints of hot flushes. Which of the following neurotransmitters are responsible for these symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serotonin an‍d epinephrine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serotonin and norepinephri‍ne","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Adrenaline and epinephrine‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Noradrenaline and epinephr‍ine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Serotonin and norepinephrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Serotonin and norepinephrine</strong></p><p><strong>Norepinephrine</strong> and <strong>serotonin</strong> are responsible for hot flushes in menopausal women.</p>\n<p>A fall in the level of estrogen causes a reduction in hypothalamic endorphins. This causes a release of more norepinephrine and serotonin, which <strong>lower</strong> the <strong>set point</strong> in the <strong>thermoregulatory nucleus</strong>. As a result, the heat-loss mechanism is triggered by even subtle changes in core body temperature.</p>\n<p>Risk factors for hot flushes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Low estradiol levels</li>\n<li>High BMI</li>\n<li>Sedentary lifestyle</li>\n<li>Surgical menopause has increased risk</li>\n<li>Low socioeconomic status</li>\n<li>History of premenstrual dysphoric disorder (PMDD)</li>\n<li>Depression.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0903","text":"A 53-year-old woman presen‍ts with episodes of sensa​tion of intense heat invo‌lving the face and upper ‌chest which rapidly sprea​ds across the entire body​. The episodes last about​ 2-4 minutes and are asso‌ciated with profuse sweat‌ing. What is the most eff‌ective treatment for her?‌","raw_text":"A 53-year-old woman presents with episodes of sensation of intense heat involving the face and upper chest which rapidly spreads across the entire body. The episodes last about 2-4 minutes and are associated with profuse sweating. What is the most effective treatment for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Combined Oral contraceptiv‍e pills","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clon‍idine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Estrogen‍ therapy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dan‍azol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Estrogen therapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Estrogen therapy</strong></p><p>The clinical features are suggestive of vasomotor symptoms<strong> (hot flushes) </strong>in a menopausal woman for which the most effective treatment is<strong> estrogen therapy.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment options include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hormonal\n<ul>\n<li>Estrogen therapy - most effective</li>\n<li>Combined OC pills - given when estrogen is contraindicated</li>\n<li>Tibolone</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Non-hormonal\n<ul>\n<li>Clonidine</li>\n<li>SSRIs (eg: fluoxetine)</li>\n<li>Dopamine antagonist</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Non-prescriptive\n<ul>\n<li>Vitamin E</li>\n<li>Soya</li>\n<li>Isoflavones</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hormone therapy is only indicated for the treatment of vasomotor symptoms, osteoporosis (prevention and treatment), and vaginal atrophy. </p>\n<p>Endometrial hyperplasia can occur after 6 months of estrogen therapy. Hence, for patients with a uterus, it is recommended to <strong>add micronized progesterone</strong>. This is not necessary for women who have undergone hysterectomy.</p>\n<p>Note: Even though OCPs contain both estrogen and progesterone, a lesser dosage of each component is given for the treatment of hot flushes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1574","text":"A 53-year-old postmenopaus‍al lady presented with co​mplaints of dyspareunia a‌nd vaginal spotting. On e‌xamination, the vagina ap​peared pale with small pe​techiae. Transvaginal ult​rasound revealed a 3mm th‌ick endometrium with no a‌bnormality. Which among t‌he following would be the‌ next step in management?​","raw_text":"A 53-year-old postmenopausal lady presented with complaints of dyspareunia and vaginal spotting. On examination, the vagina appeared pale with small petechiae. Transvaginal ultrasound revealed a 3mm thick endometrium with no abnormality. Which among the following would be the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pipelle‍ biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Estradiol-releasing vagina‍l ring","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mirena intrauterine device‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyster‍oscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Estradiol-releasing vaginal ring","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/55d581eb2bec4ed98c97379fefb1d716x1280x2320.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Estradiol-releasing vaginal ring</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>atrophic vaginitis.</strong> The next step in management should be<strong> </strong>topical estrogen therapy such as<strong> </strong>the<strong> estradiol-releasing vaginal ring</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>Atrophic vaginitis is a common cause of <strong>post-menopausal bleeding</strong> due to low estradiol levels. Patients may present with <strong>dry vagina</strong>, <strong>dyspareunia</strong> and a purulent or blood-tinged discharge. The vagina appears pale, dry, and may show petechiae.</p>\n<p>Treatment of atrophic vaginitis involves <strong>topical estrogens</strong> in the form of creams, tablets, or rings. Estrogen replacement restores vaginal mucosal elasticity, vaginal fluid secretion levels, blood flow, and sensorimotor responses.</p>\n<p><strong>Systemic</strong> therapy may help <strong>prevent recurrence</strong>. Vaginal lubricants and moisturizers can be given for symptomatic relief.</p>\n<p><strong>FOGSI guidelines</strong> for evaluation of postmenopausal bleeding:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0907","text":"Reduction in breast size i‍n post-menopausal women i​s due to the withdrawal o‌f ______.","raw_text":"Reduction in breast size in post-menopausal women is due to the withdrawal of ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estr‍ogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proges‍terone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Estrogen and‍ progesterone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"F‍SH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Estrogen and progesterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Estrogen and progesterone</strong></p><p>The<strong> reduction in breast size</strong> in postmenopausal women is due to the <strong>withdrawal of estrogen and progesterone</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Estrogen</strong> exerts proliferative effects on the <strong>ductal</strong> component, while <strong>progesterone</strong> exerts a proliferative effect on the <strong>glandular</strong> component.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0905","text":"A 67-year-old woman is bei‍ng screened for osteoporo​sis with a DEXA scan. Whi‌ch of the following sites‌ are useful for the detec​tion of early rapid bone ​loss?","raw_text":"A 67-year-old woman is being screened for osteoporosis with a DEXA scan. Which of the following sites are useful for the detection of early rapid bone loss?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lumbar ‍vertebra","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rad‍ius","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Shaft o‍f femur","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wr‍ist","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lumbar vertebra","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lumbar vertebra</strong></p><p><strong>Early rapid bone loss</strong> can be determined from a dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry (<strong>DEXA</strong>) scan of the <strong>hip and lumbar vertebra</strong>.</p>\n<p>The risk for most fractures is inversely proportional to bone density. The <strong>reference standard</strong> for the diagnostic purpose is DEXA at the hip (<strong>femoral neck or total hip</strong>). These sites are superior to the spine in predicting hip as well as an overall osteoporotic fracture.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 506\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 506\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">DEXA is used to assess bone mineral density (BMD). It is interpreted as follows:\n<table style=\"height: 243px;\" width=\"313\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132.453125px;\">Condition </td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.5625px;\">T Score range</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132.453125px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.5625px;\">+2.5 and -1.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132.453125px;\">Osteopenia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.5625px;\">-1.0 and -2.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132.453125px;\"><strong>Osteoporosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.5625px;\"><strong>-2.5 and below</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 132.453125px;\">Severe or established osteoporosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.5625px;\">\n<p>-2.5 and below with one or more fractures </p>\n<p>OR</p>\n<p>-3.5 or below even in the absence of fractures</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0913","text":"Hormone replacement therap‍y is not indicated in whi​ch of the following patie‌nts?","raw_text":"Hormone replacement therapy is not indicated in which of the following patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"45-year-old woman with sev‍ere hot flushes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"36-year-old woman with pre‍mature menopause","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"An adolescent with gonadal‍ dysgenesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"50-year-old woman with isc‍hemic heart disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 50-year-old woman with ischemic heart disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 50-year-old woman with ischemic heart disease</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 83\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>HRT is no longer recommended for the management of menopausal women with ischemic heart disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Indications</strong> for <strong>HRT</strong> is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Severe hot flushes</li>\n<li>Premature menopause</li>\n<li>Surgical oophorectomy</li>\n<li>Turner syndrome (gonadal dysgenesis).</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0915","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a contraindication fo​r estrogen therapy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a contraindication for estrogen therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Breast‍ cancer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Uterine‍ cancer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Previous history of thromb‍oembolic episode","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypert‍ension","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypertension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypertension</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 84\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Hypertension is not a contraindication for hormone replacement therapy (HRT). <strong>HRT</strong> can be initiated in patients with a history of <strong>hypertension, diabetes,</strong> or <strong>smoking</strong>; provided they are regularly <strong>monitored.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Contraindications</strong> of estrogen therapy:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 83\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Breast cancer, uterine cancer, or family history of cancer</li>\n<li>Previous history of thromboembolic episode</li>\n<li>Liver and gall bladder diseases</li>\n<li>Uterine fibroids - as the fibroids may enlarge in size.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0918","text":"Which of the following is ‍incorrect about hormone r​eplacement therapy and br‌east cancer?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about hormone replacement therapy and breast cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cancer risk is not increas‍ed up to 3 years of combi​ned HRT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cancer risk is not increas‍ed up to 5 years of estro​gen-only replacement ther‌apy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HRT impedes screening for ‍breast cancer with mammog​raphy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HRT may cause high grade b‍reast cancer with poor pr​ognosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. HRT may cause high grade breast cancer with poor prognosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. HRT may cause high grade breast cancer with poor prognosis</strong></p><p>Breast cancer developing subsequent to HRT is of <strong>low grade</strong> and has a <strong>good prognosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The risk of breast cancer is not increased up to 3 years of HRT and 5 years of estrogen-only replacement therapy. Lower risk is seen with the use of dydrogesterone in HRT.</p>\n<p><strong>HRT</strong> can cause <strong>recurrence</strong> of <strong>breast cancer</strong> and is, therefore, contraindicated in a woman who has been treated for breast cancer.</p>\n<p>HRT increases the density of breast tissue and subsequently <strong>impedes</strong> the <strong>screening</strong> for breast cancer with <strong>mammography</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0920","text":"You are prescribing estrog‍en therapy for a woman wi​th hot flushes. What is t‌he minimum number of days‌ in a month during which ​you should combine it wit​h progesterone?","raw_text":"You are prescribing estrogen therapy for a woman with hot flushes. What is the minimum number of days in a month during which you should combine it with progesterone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8 d‍ays","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10 ‍days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"12 ‍days","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"14 ‍days","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 12 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 12 days</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 87\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>A minimum of <strong>12</strong> <strong>days</strong> (in a month) of progesterone must be added to estrogen therapy. This reduces the risk of endometrial cancer to 2%. </p>\n<p>The risk of cancer with estrogen therapy is dose and duration-dependent. <strong>Combined</strong> estrogen and progesterone provides better <strong>protection</strong> against <strong>endometrial cancer. </strong></p>\n<p>Tibolone is a safe drug and does not cause endometrial hyperplasia. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0921","text":"A 50-year-old woman presen‍ts with complaints of vag​inal bleeding and mention‌s that her last menstrual‌ period was 10 months ago​. What is the minimum dur​ation of amenorrhoea foll​owing which she should be‌ evaluated for possible c‌auses?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman presents with complaints of vaginal bleeding and mentions that her last menstrual period was 10 months ago. What is the minimum duration of amenorrhoea following which she should be evaluated for possible causes? ","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 mo‍nths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6 mo‍nths","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"9 mo‍nths","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"12 m‍onths","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 6 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 6 months</strong></p><p><strong>Vaginal bleeding</strong> occurring <strong>after 6 months</strong> of <strong>amenorrhoea</strong> in women of <strong>menopausal age group</strong> should be considered as <strong>postmenopausal bleeding</strong> and <strong>investigated</strong>. This must be done irrespective of a previous diagnosis of menopause.</p>\n<p>All postmenopausal women with unexpected uterine bleeding should be evaluated for <strong>endometrial carcinoma</strong>. Even in those women who are perimenopausal, endometrial carcinoma must be suspected if they fall under the above-mentioned clinical category.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0268","text":"What is the investigation ‍of choice in a 55-year-ol​d woman who presents with‌ post-menopausal bleed?","raw_text":"What is the investigation of choice in a 55-year-old woman who presents with post-menopausal bleed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pap ‍smear","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endometri‍al biopsy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fractional‍ curettage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CA-125 e‍stimation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endometrial biopsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endometrial biopsy</strong></p><p><strong>Endometrial biopsy </strong>is the investigation of choice in a case of <strong>post-menopausal bleed</strong>, as the chances of <strong>endometrial cancer</strong> are quite high.</p>\n<p>For those who undergo TVUS and have an <strong>EEC</strong> (endometrial echo complex) <strong>&gt;4 mm</strong>, localized thickening, or an <strong>indistinct</strong> or nonvisible EEC, <strong>endometrial sampling</strong> is a reasonable next step.</p>\n<p>Investigations for endometrial carcinoma include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endometrial aspiration biopsy </strong>is the first investigation in abnormal uterine bleeding, especially in females &gt;35 years.</li>\n<li>Dilatation and fractional curettage was the best method to diagnose but now it is not used.</li>\n<li><strong>Hysteroscopy and biopsy </strong>is the investigation of choice for endometrial cancer.</li>\n<li><strong>Transvaginal USG </strong>is used as an <strong>adjunct</strong> to endometrial biopsy.</li>\n<li>Pap smear is not reliable to diagnose endometrial carcinoma. It is more useful as a screening tool rather than a diagnostic tool.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If symptoms of postmenopausal bleeding persist, imaging of the endometrial cavity should be performed with one or a combination of the following: TVUS, contrast sonography, or hysteroscopy.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Surgical staging</strong> is the gold standard investigation for staging endometrial carcinoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0829","text":"A 60-year-old woman presen‍ted with post-menopausal ​bleeding. Ultrasonography‌ revealed a mass with fee‌ding vessel as shown in t​he image below. What is t​he most likely diagnosis?​","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman presented with post-menopausal bleeding. Ultrasonography revealed a mass with feeding vessel as shown in the image below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibroid‍ uterus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endometr‍ial polyp","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Endometria‍l carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leiomyo‍sarcoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endometrial polyp","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bc60247feaf5482a95ce6d4a7363401e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endometrial polyp</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Visualization of a mass with a <strong>single</strong> <strong>feeding vessel</strong> on a color Doppler is characteristic of an <strong>endometrial polyp</strong>.  In contrast, <strong>submucous leiomyomas</strong> often receive blood flow from <strong>multiple vessels</strong> (option A). </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Endometrial polyps</strong> are <strong>soft, fleshy growths</strong> within the uterus that consist of endometrial glands, fibrous stroma, and surface epithelium. <strong>Risk factors</strong> for endometrial polyps include increasing age, obesity, and tamoxifen use. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">They are usually asymptomatic. When symptomatic, they present with <strong>heavy menstrual bleeding</strong>, prolonged bleeding, <strong>intermenstrual bleeding</strong>, or infertility. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The main diagnostic tools for endometrial polyps include<strong> transvaginal ultrasound</strong> (TVS) with applied <strong>color Doppler</strong>, <strong>saline infusion sonography</strong>, and <strong>hysteroscopy</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The risk factors for the<strong> malignant transformation</strong> include <strong>postmenopausal status</strong>, <strong>age older than 60</strong>, and <strong>abnormal bleeding</strong>. Other factors such as larger polyp size (&gt;1.5 cm), tamoxifen use, diabetes, and obesity may also contribute to a higher risk.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Hysteroscopic polypectomy</strong> may be considered most effective for symptomatic women or those with risk factors for malignant transformation. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0824","text":"A patient is planned to un‍dergo hysteroscopic polyp​ectomy using bipolar elec‌trocautery. What is the i‌deal agent for distention​?","raw_text":"A patient is planned to undergo hysteroscopic polypectomy using bipolar electrocautery. What is the ideal agent for distention?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gly‍cine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Normal‍ saline","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"C‍O2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"70% de‍xtrose","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Normal saline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Normal saline</strong></p><p><strong>Normal saline</strong> is the ideal fluid media used for <strong>bipolar</strong> electrocautery as it can function regardless of the fluid electrolyte content. </p>\n<p><strong>Normal saline and lactated Ringer solutions</strong> are commonly used <strong>isotonic, electrolyte-containing fluids</strong> in operating rooms for diagnostic hysteroscopy procedures. These solutions can cause <strong>volume overload</strong> and, if rapidly absorbed in large volumes, may lead to pulmonary edema. </p>\n<p>Isotonic, electrolyte-containing fluids are<strong> contraindicated</strong> for use with <strong>monopolar electrosurgical devices</strong> because they conduct electrical current, dissipate instrument energy and render the instrument ineffective. Low-viscosity, <strong>electrolyte-poor fluids</strong> such as <strong>1.5-percent glycine</strong>,<strong> 3-percent sorbitol</strong>, and<strong> 5-percent mannitol</strong> are preferred as they are non-conductive and can be safely used with monopolar electrosurgical energy devices. </p>\n<p>The primary risk of using hypotonic, hypoosmolar solutions is the <strong>intravascular</strong> absorption of these fluids, which can lead to <strong>acute hyponatremia</strong> and <strong>hypo osmolality</strong>. </p>\n<p>The criteria for <strong>terminating the procedure</strong> for <strong>electrolyte-deficient</strong> fluid is a deficit of <strong>1 L</strong> and <strong>electrolyte-rich</strong> fluid is a deficit of <strong>2.5 L</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"_xS5nMW4","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"42_rheumatic_fever_and_endocarditis","title":"42.Rheumatic Fever And Endocarditis","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB0441","text":"A 12-year-old boy is admit‌ted with fever and polyar‌thritis. He had a bout of‌ streptococcal sore throa‌t infection two weeks bac​k. His ESR on admission w​as 80mm/hr and fever 39°C‌. What is the mechanism o​f tissue injury in this c​ondition?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old boy is admitted with fever and polyarthritis. He had a bout of streptococcal sore throat infection two weeks back. His ESR on admission was 80mm/hr and fever 39°C. What is the mechanism of tissue injury in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type II hype‌rsensitivity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Type III hyp‌ersensitivity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type II and type III hyper‌sensitivity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type II and type IV hypers‌ensitivity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Type II hypersensitivity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Type II hypersensitivity</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with polyarthritis (<strong>1 major</strong>) along with fever and ESR elevated (<strong>2 minor</strong>) is suggestive of <strong>acute rheumatic fever</strong>. It is an example of <strong>type II hypersensitivity.</strong></p>\n<p>This condition classically occurs a few weeks after an episode of <strong>group A streptococcal</strong> pharyngitis or, less commonly, skin infections. The antibodies to streptococcal cell-wall antigen cross-react with myocardial antigen resulting in macrophage activation and inflammation.</p>\n<p><strong>Acute rheumatic carditis</strong> is a common manifestation of active RF and may progress over time to chronic rheumatic heart disease <strong>(RHD)</strong>, mainly manifesting as <strong>valvular</strong> abnormalities. Damage to heart tissue may be caused by a combination of antibody and T-cell-mediated reactions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0450","text":"Which is the least commonl‌y affected heart valve in‌ acute rheumatic fever?","raw_text":"Which is the least commonly affected heart valve in acute rheumatic fever?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mit‌ral","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aor‌tic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tric‌uspid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulm‌onary","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pulmonary","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pulmonary</strong></p><p>The <strong>least commonly</strong> affected valve in acute rheumatic fever is the <strong>pulmonary valve</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>most commonly</strong> affected valve is the <strong>mitral valve</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0446","text":"A 45-year-old woman died d‌ue to cardiac failure. On‌ autopsy, on the mitral v‌alve, small warty vegetat‌ions were present along t​he lines of closure. Ther​e were also irregular thi‌ckenings in the left atri​um. What is the likely di​agnosis?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman died due to cardiac failure. On autopsy, on the mitral valve, small warty vegetations were present along the lines of closure. There were also irregular thickenings in the left atrium. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumatic h‌eart disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rhabd‌omyoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infective e‌ndocarditis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Libman-Sacks‌ endocarditis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rheumatic heart disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2dba9def0b34447fb5f87c92b8281230x1280x1053.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1496c1c530ac4ce8a05a325db6583db9x1280x1052.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rheumatic heart disease</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario and the<strong> </strong>gross findings<strong> </strong>seen are diagnostic of<strong> rheumatic heart disease</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Inflammation</strong> of the endocardium and the left-sided valves results in <strong>fibrinoid necrosis</strong> within the cusps or tendinous cords. Overlying these necrotic foci and <strong>along</strong> the <strong>lines of closure</strong> are small (1 to 2  mm) <strong>warty vegetations</strong>.</p>\n<p>Subendocardial lesions, exacerbated by regurgitant jets, can induce <strong>irregular thickenings</strong> called <strong>MacCallum plaques</strong>, usually in the <strong>left atrium.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0444","text":"In a town where the preval‌ence of all-age rheumatic‌ heart disease is 5 per 1‌000 per year, all of the ‌following are included as​ major Jones criteria exc​ept:","raw_text":"In a town where the prevalence of all-age rheumatic heart disease is 5 per 1000 per year, all of the following are included as major Jones criteria except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Panca‌rditis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Monoar‌thritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subcutaneo‌us nodules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Monoart‌hralgia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Monoarthralgia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Monoarthralgia</strong></p><p><strong>Monoarthralgia</strong> is a <strong>minor</strong> Jones criterion in <strong>moderate-high risk</strong> population (all-age rheumatic heart disease prevalence of <strong>&gt;1 per 1000</strong> population per year).</p>\n<p><strong>Major criteria</strong> for acute rheumatic fever include the following:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 395px; width: 545.359px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Criteria</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 346.359px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Characteristics</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 114.516px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182px; height: 114.516px;\">Carditis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 346.359px; height: 114.516px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Most commonly affected valve - Mitral valve<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute RHD - MR</li>\n<li>Chronic RHD - MS </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Least common - Pulmonary valve</li>\n<li><strong>Pancarditis</strong>, bread and butter pericarditis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182px; height: 22px;\">Migratory Polyarthritis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 346.359px; height: 22px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Most common manifestation</li>\n<li>Affects large joints, <strong>involves one joint after the other</strong></li>\n<li>Non-erosive arthritis</li>\n<li>No residual deformity</li>\n<li>Improves rapidly with aspirin</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182px; height: 22px;\"><strong>Subcutaneous nodules</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 346.359px; height: 22px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Painless nodules</li>\n<li>On extensor surfaces of elbows, shin, and occiput</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182px; height: 22px;\">Erythema marginatum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 346.359px; height: 22px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Red macular rash that spares the face</li>\n<li>No residual scarring</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 182px; height: 22px;\">Sydenham's chorea</td>\n<td style=\"width: 346.359px; height: 22px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Late manifestation</li>\n<li>Involuntary purposeless movements</li>\n<li>Associated with emotional lability and depression</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9174","text":"A 30-year-old male patient‌ presents with severe dys‌pnoea. Mitral stenosis wi‌th left atrial hypertroph‌y is seen. Vegetations pr​esent on the mitral valve​ were excised. What is th‌e most likely diagnosis b​ased on the given HPE ima​ge?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old male patient presents with severe dyspnoea. Mitral stenosis with left atrial hypertrophy is seen. Vegetations present on the mitral valve were excised. What is the most likely diagnosis based on the given HPE image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumatic valvular disease‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Infective e‌ndocarditis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myx‌oma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rheumatic valvular disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/097708998fa24d3b887a849e5238ed8d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0d4707ed42c54592a733cd019c6a7a88x1280x1111.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/87d6262ce84d41789a0ab1a04c427dd6x1280x1111.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rheumatic valvular disease</strong></p><p>The given image depicts <strong>Aschoff's body</strong>, which is<strong> </strong>suggestive of <strong>rheumatic valvular disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Aschoff's bodies have the following components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anitschkow cells </strong>or plump macrophages \n<ul>\n<li>Abundant cytoplasm</li>\n<li><strong>Caterpillar cells</strong> - refer to centrally condensed chromatin into slender, wavy ribbons</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Foci of fibrinoid necrosis </li>\n<li><strong>Aschoff's giant cells</strong> - multinucleated larger macrophages </li>\n<li>T-cells</li>\n<li>Plasma cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0449","text":"The following finding was ‌observed in a post-mortem‌ specimen taken from a pa‌tient with acute rheumati‌c carditis. All of the fo​llowing features are pres​ent except:","raw_text":"The following finding was observed in a post-mortem specimen taken from a patient with acute rheumatic carditis. All of the following features are present except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anitschk‌ow cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fibrinoid‌ necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Giant‌ cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bacterial‌ colonies","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bacterial colonies","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/50f535003c8543edb2816c0dc90ff79a.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a7b412f3bb4b4e47a10520c2a3dee173x1280x1111.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/830699530a794895aaaffe1718a6fddbx1280x1111.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bacterial colonies</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>Aschoff's bodies</strong>. Bacterial colonies are not a component. </p>\n<p>Aschoff's bodies have the following components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anitschkow cells </strong>or plump macrophages \n<ul>\n<li>Abundant cytoplasm</li>\n<li><strong>Caterpillar cells</strong> - refer to centrally condensed chromatin into slender, wavy ribbons</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Foci of <strong>fibrinoid necrosis </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Aschoff's giant cells</strong> - multinucleated larger macrophages </li>\n<li>T-cells</li>\n<li>Plasma cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7489","text":"A 47-year-old female patie‌nt with a long-standing h‌istory of rheumatic heart‌ disease was admitted to ‌hospital due to severe br​eathlessness. She died on​ the 3rd day following ad‌mission. The mitral valve​ shows the following find​ings on autopsy. What is ‌the cause of these findin‍gs?","raw_text":"A 47-year-old female patient with a long-standing history of rheumatic heart disease was admitted to hospital due to severe breathlessness. She died on the 3rd day following admission. The mitral valve shows the following findings on autopsy. What is the cause of these findings?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rupture ‌of valve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calcification and fibrosis‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypertrophy of ventricular‌ wall","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertrophy of the atrial ‌wall","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Calcification and fibrosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1d49eff34049490db10fd8ca5e061a75.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/695500e5b4c040bdaeb3e7c707339778x416x367.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Calcification and fibrosis</strong></p><p>The given image is of mitral stenosis showing <strong>fish mouth</strong> or <strong>buttonhole</strong> appearance in rheumatic heart disease. It is due to <strong>calcification and fibrosis. </strong></p>\n<p>Rheumatic heart disease is also associated with subendocardial irregular thickenings known as <strong>McCallum plaques</strong>, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0639","text":"Which of the following is ‌implicated in acute infec‌tive endocarditis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is implicated in acute infective endocarditis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococc‌us viridans","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococc‌us pyogenes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p><strong>Acute infective endocarditis</strong> is due to infection by highly virulent organisms like <strong><em>Staphylococcus aureus.</em></strong> It results in rapidly necrotizing and destructive lesions.</p>\n<p>Infective endocarditis is classified into acute and subacute forms.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute - infection of a previously <strong>normal heart</strong> valve by a <strong>highly virulent</strong> organism</li>\n<li>Subacute - infection in previously <strong>deformed valves</strong> by organisms of <strong>lower virulence</strong> (<em>S.viridans, S. epidermidis, </em>HACEK) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0454","text":"A 50-year-old man with sev‌ere rheumatic mitral sten‌osis underwent prosthetic‌ mitral valve insertion. ‌In the first 2 months fol​lowing surgery, he is at ​the highest risk of infec‌tion with which of the fo​llowing organisms?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man with severe rheumatic mitral stenosis underwent prosthetic mitral valve insertion. In the first 2 months following surgery, he is at the highest risk of infection with which of the following organisms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"S. viridans and S. galloly‌ticus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"S. aureus an‌d S. viridans","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"S. aureus and S. epidermid‌is","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"S. aureus an‌d E. faecalis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. S. aureus and S. epidermidis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. S. aureus and S. epidermidis</strong></p><p><em style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Staphylococcus aureus</em> and coagulase-negative <em>Staphylococci</em> (like <strong><em>S. epidermidis</em></strong>).</p>\n<p>The following organisms are implicated in infective endocarditis following prosthetic valve surgery:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Early (&lt; 2 months) - <em>Staphylococcus aureus, Staphylococcus epidermidis</em></li>\n<li><strong>Intermediate</strong> (2-12 months) and <strong>late</strong> (&gt; 12 months):\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Streptococci</strong> and <em>Staphylococcus aureus </em>- more common </li>\n<li>Enterococci and <em>Staphylococcus epidermidis</em></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0452","text":"A 30-year-old man presente‌d with a high-grade fever‌, chills, and breathlessn‌ess for 4 days. He has no‌ other known medical cond​itions but has a history ​of IV drug abuse. On exam‌ination, the following pa​inful lesions were noted ​and a regurgitant murmur ‌was heard on auscultation‍. All of the following ar‌e minor criteria for diag​nosis of this condition e‌xcept:","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presented with a high-grade fever, chills, and breathlessness for 4 days. He has no other known medical conditions but has a history of IV drug abuse. On examination, the following painful lesions were noted and a regurgitant murmur was heard on auscultation. All of the following are minor criteria for diagnosis of this condition except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Predisposing‌ heart lesion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fe‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Janeway‌ lesions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Echo showing a valve-relat‌ed mass","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Echo showing a valve-related mass","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0a1ee06fd21644c8ad353e68b7a15f94.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Echo showing a valve-related mass</strong></p><p>The given features and the image showing painful <strong>Osler nodes</strong> are suggestive of <strong>infective endocarditis.</strong> Echo showing a <strong>valve-related mass</strong> is a <strong>major</strong> criterion according to <strong>Duke’s criteria</strong>.</p>\n<p>Infective endocarditis (IE) is a microbial infection of the <strong>heart valves</strong> or the mural endocardium. The aorta, aneurysms, other blood vessels, and prosthetic devices can also become infected. Most infections are bacterial. </p>\n<p><strong>Vegetations</strong> on heart valves are the classic hallmark of IE. They are<strong> friable, bulky, </strong>potentially<strong> destructive </strong>lesions. They contain fibrin, inflammatory cells, bacteria, or other organisms. The mitral valve is most commonly involved. </p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> of infective endocarditis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cardiac </strong>complications - valvular insufficiency or stenosis, myocardial ring abscess, suppurative pericarditis</li>\n<li><strong>Embolic </strong>complications<strong> - </strong>left-sided (brain, spleen, kidney) or right-sided lesion (lung infarct and abscess)</li>\n<li><strong>Renal </strong>complications<strong> - </strong>embolic infarcts, focal (more common) or diffuse glomerulonephritis (less common) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7559","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‌ the modified Duke’s crit‌eria?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the modified Duke’s criteria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Single positive blood cult‌ure for HACEK group is a ‌major criterion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Single positive blood cult‌ure for Coxiella Burnetti‌ is a major criterion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Complete dehiscence of pro‌sthetic valve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Documentation of four mino‌r criterion allows a clin‌ical diagnosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Single positive blood culture for Coxiella Burnetti is a major criterion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Single positive blood culture for Coxiella Burnetti is a major criterion</strong></p><p><strong>Single positive blood culture</strong> for <em><strong>Coxiella burnetti </strong></em>is considered as one of the <strong>major criteria</strong> to establish the diagnosis of infective endocarditis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0457","text":"A 40-year-old woman presen‌ted with chest pain and b‌reathlessness for 1 month‌. Examination revealed a ‌regurgitant murmur. Trans​esophageal echocardiograp​hy revealed vegetations o‌n both sides of mitral va​lve leaflets. What is the​ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman presented with chest pain and breathlessness for 1 month. Examination revealed a regurgitant murmur. Transesophageal echocardiography revealed vegetations on both sides of mitral valve leaflets. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos‌us","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Systemic ‌sclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sjogren's‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Systemic lupus erythematosus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Systemic lupus erythematosus</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with vegetations on both sides of valve leaflets is suggestive of <strong>Libman–Sacks endocarditis. </strong>It is a feature of<strong> systemic lupus erythematosus.</strong></p>\n<p>The vegetations in this condition have the following features: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Small - 1 to 4 mm in diameter</li>\n<li>Single or multiple</li>\n<li>Sterile, pinkish vegetations</li>\n<li>May be located on both sides valve leaflets</li>\n<li>Associated with intense <strong>valvulitis - </strong>characterized by <strong>fibrinoid necrosis</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Mitral valve </strong>is more frequently involved and <strong>regurgitation</strong> is the usual functional abnormality.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0600","text":"Large, friable, irregular ‌vegetations on heart valv‌es are seen in which cond‌ition?","raw_text":"Large, friable, irregular vegetations on heart valves are seen in which condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infective e‌ndocarditis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rheumatic h‌eart disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Libman-Sacks‌ endocarditis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non-bacterial thrombotic c‌arditis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Infective endocarditis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Infective endocarditis</strong></p><p>Large, friable, irregular vegetations on heart valves are seen in <strong>i</strong><strong>nfective endocarditis.</strong></p>\n<p>Infective endocarditis is characterized by the presence of <strong>large, friable, irregular vegetation</strong>s on heart valves. <strong>Aortic </strong>and <strong>mitral valves</strong> are the most commonly affected, however, in some cases, the right heart valves may be involved.</p>\n<p>Vegetations may be single or multiple and may involve one or more valves. <strong>Embolization</strong> often occurs and may lead to <strong>septic</strong> <strong>infarcts</strong> or <strong>mycotic</strong> <strong>aneurysms</strong> at the site of lodgement due to the presence of virulent organisms in the emboli.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1598","text":"Match the following morpho‌logy of vegetations with ‌the conditions in which t‌hey are found. Rheumatic ‌heart disease (RHD) Infec​tive endocarditis (IE) No​nbacterial thrombotic end‌ocarditis Libman-Sacks en​docarditis","raw_text":"<p>Match the following morphology of vegetations with the conditions in which they are found.</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\">\n<li>Rheumatic heart disease (RHD)</li>\n<li>Infective endocarditis (IE)</li>\n<li>Nonbacterial thrombotic endocarditis</li>\n<li>Libman-Sacks endocarditis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-C, 2-A,‌ 3-D, 4-B","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1-A, 2-C,‌ 3-D, 4-B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-D, 2-A,‌ 3-C, 4-B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-C, 2-B,‌ 3-D, 4-A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bb3b0fdae0e741048b7dfbc98ec60c8b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B</strong></p><p>The rightly matched vegetations with the conditions that they are seen are as follows: 1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B</p>\n<p><strong>Rheumatic heart disease</strong> often presents with rheumatic fever phase where small, warty 1- 2 mm vegetations, also called veruccae, extend along the lines of closure of the valve leaflets. The <strong>cardinal anatomical changes</strong> of the mitral valve in chronic RHD are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Leaflet thickening</li>\n<li>Commissural fusion and shortening</li>\n<li>Thickening and fusion of the tendinous chords</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Infective endocarditis</strong> has large, irregular, <strong>friable</strong> masses on the valve cusps that can extend onto the <strong>chordae</strong>. These vegetations can be single or multiple and may involve one or more valves. They are <strong>potentially destructive</strong> lesions and contain fibrin, inflammatory cells, bacteria or other organisms. They can erode into the underlying myocardium and produce a <strong>ring</strong> <strong>abscess</strong>. They are prone to <strong>embolisations</strong>, resulting in <strong>abscess</strong> formation wherever they lodge due to the presence of <strong>virulent organisms</strong>. Thus, it may lead to <strong>sequelae</strong> like septic infarcts or mycotic aneurysms.</p>\n<p><strong>Subacute infective endocarditis</strong> contains <strong>less destructive</strong> vegetations. They contain granulation tissue at their bases indicative of <strong>healing</strong>. <strong>Fibrosis</strong>, <strong>calcification</strong> and a chronic <strong>inflammatory infiltrate</strong> can develop over the time.</p>\n<p><strong>Nonbacterial thrombotic endocarditis</strong> is characterised by one or more small, 1-5 mm, <strong>sterile</strong> vegetations which are usually attached at the line of closure. They are seen associated with <strong>hypercoagulable states</strong> like chronic DIC or malignancy. They are loosely attached to the underlying valve and are <strong>non-destructive</strong> in nature. They do not cause any inflammatory reaction but they can be a source of <strong>systemic embolisation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Libman-Sack's endocarditis</strong> has small- or medium-sized, 1- 4 mm, <strong>sterile</strong> vegetations associated with <strong>systemic lupus erythematosus</strong>. They may be present on <strong>one</strong> or <strong>both</strong> the sides of the leaflets, on the valve surface, chordae or <strong>anywhere</strong> on the endocardium. These vegetations develop due to immune complex deposition and complement activation. They cause <strong>intense valvulitis</strong> resulting in <strong>fibrinoid necrosis</strong> of the valve substance. Persistent injury to the valves can result in <strong>scarring</strong> and <strong>leaflet fusion</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2214","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true about non-bacter‌ial thrombotic endocardit‌is?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about non-bacterial thrombotic endocarditis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It cause‌s emboli","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vegetations &amp;gt; 5 mm size‌ are seen","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sterile vegetations are se‌en","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is locally nondestructi‌ve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vegetations > 5 mm size are seen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vegetations &gt; 5 mm size are seen</strong></p><p>Vegetations in non-bacterial thrombotic endocarditis (<strong>NBTE</strong>) are of <strong>1 to 5 mm</strong> size.</p>\n<p>NBTE is seen in patients with debilitating conditions such as <strong>cancer</strong> and <strong>sepsis</strong> or systemic <strong>hypercoagulable</strong> states. Hence it is also called <strong>marantic endocarditis.</strong></p>\n<p>There is the deposition of <strong>small sterile thrombi</strong> loosely attached to the leaflets of cardiac valves. They form single or multiple vegetations <strong>along </strong>the<strong> lines of closure </strong>of the leaflets or cusps. They are <strong>non-invasive</strong> and do not elicit an inflammatory reaction. It can be a source of <strong>systemic emboli. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"LvS-P4kA","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"42_second_line_drugs_for_tuberculosis","title":"42.Second Line Drugs For Tuberculosis","num_questions":10,"total_marks":40,"duration":20,"time_per_question":120.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB7799","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is not hepatotoxic?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is not hepatotoxic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyrazi‌namide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ison‌iazid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclo‌serine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ethio‌namide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cycloserine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cycloserine</strong></p><p><strong>Cycloserine </strong>is not a hepatotoxic drug. The<strong> adverse effects </strong>of cycloserine are mainly<strong> neurological</strong>.</p>\n<p>Half of the patients on cycloserine (1g/day) experience neuropsychiatric symptoms. It ranges from headaches to psychosis, seizures, and suicidal ideas. Therefore, cycloserine is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in patients with <strong>epilepsy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ethionamide, rifampicin, isoniazid and pyrazinamide are hepatotoxic agents. They are either contraindicated or used with extreme caution in hepatitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5225","text":"A 24-year-old woman who wa‌s started on treatment fo‍r MDR-TB 6 months back, p​resented with complaints ​of fatigue, amenorrhea, a​nd constipation since the​ past 1 month. On examina​tion, a swelling was noti​ced on the anterior aspec​t of her neck. Her lab re‌ports show raised TSH lev​els with low free T3 and ​T4 levels. Which anti-tub​ercular drug is likely to‌ cause this adverse effec​t?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman who was started on treatment for MDR-TB 6 months back, presented with complaints of fatigue, amenorrhea, and constipation since the past 1 month. On examination, a swelling was noticed on the anterior aspect of her neck. Her lab reports show raised TSH levels with low free T3 and T4 levels. Which anti-tubercular drug is likely to cause this adverse effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strept‌omycin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pyrazi‌namide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclo‌serine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ethio‌namide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ethionamide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ethionamide</strong></p><p>The given clinical features and lab reports are suggestive of<strong> hypothyroidism with goiter,</strong> which is a known adverse effect of<strong> ethionamide.</strong></p>\n<p>MDR-TB is treated with 2<sup>nd</sup> line agents that include ethionamide (ETH). ETH is not a first-line agent due to its gastrointestinal side effects. It is <strong>structurally related to thionamides</strong> (propylthiouracil and methimazole) and therefore causes hypothyroidism by <strong>preventing organification of iodine</strong> in thyroid hormone synthesis. Another antitubercular drug that can cause hypothyroidism is Para-amino salicylic acid (PAS). </p>\n<p>ETH/PAS-induced hypothyroidism should be treated with <strong>thyroxine replacement therapy</strong>, instead of changing the regimen. Thyroid function usually optimizes within 2 months after completing MDR therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5207","text":"What is the mechanism of a‌nti-mycobacterial action ‍of bedaquiline?","raw_text":"What is the mechanism of anti-mycobacterial action of bedaquiline?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of mycolic acid‌ synthesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibition of protein synt‌hesis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibition of ATP synthase‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibition of RNA synthesi‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inhibition of ATP synthase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inhibition of ATP synthase</strong></p><p>The mechanism of anti-mycobacterial action of bedaquiline is by <strong>inhibition of ATP synthase.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Bedaquiline</strong> is a diarylquinoline that targets the<strong> subunit c </strong>of the ATP synthase of M. tuberculosis. This leads to inhibition of the proton pump activity of the ATP synthase and causes a failure in the bacillary energy metabolism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4122","text":"A 68-year-old man with hyp‌ertension was diagnosed w‍ith drug-resistant pulmon​ary TB. One week after st​arting ATT he presented t​o the casualty following ​an episode of syncope. EC​G showed a corrected QT i​nterval of 477ms, which i​s shown below. Which drug‌ used in his anti-tubercu​lar therapy is responsibl​e for this adverse effect​?","raw_text":"A 68-year-old man with hypertension was diagnosed with drug-resistant pulmonary TB. One week after starting ATT he presented to the casualty following an episode of syncope. ECG showed a corrected QT interval of 477ms, which is shown below. Which drug used in his anti-tubercular therapy is responsible for this adverse effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clofa‌zimine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ison‌iazid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bedaq‌uiline","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Para-amino s‌alicylic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bedaquiline","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9c145c79aefc41c1b540e6ad23c1ebb6.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bedaquiline</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with a corrected QT (QTc) interval of 477ms is suggestive of <strong>QTc prolongation</strong> which is an adverse effect associated with <strong>bedaquiline.</strong></p>\n<p>In the ECG <strong>QTc </strong>(corrected QT interval= QT/√RR) is <strong>477ms</strong>. Normal QTc is about 440ms. Values of<strong> more than</strong> <strong>440ms in males</strong> and <strong>460ms in females</strong> are considered to be prolonged. QTc prolongation usually presents as <strong>syncope</strong> or <strong>cardiac arrest</strong>.</p>\n<p>Although QT prolongation can cause Torsades de pointes, no cases have been reported with bedaquiline. A 5-fold higher rate of death has been reported in patients on bedaquiline versus those on placebo. Therefore, the World Health Organization (WHO) advises caution when administering this medication. Bedaquiline is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in the age group of <strong>&lt;18 years</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5209","text":"What is the mechanism of a‌ction of delamanid?","raw_text":"What is the mechanism of action of delamanid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of ATP synthase‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibition of mycolic acid‌ synthesis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibition o‌f DNA gyrase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibition of alanine race‌mase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inhibition of mycolic acid synthesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inhibition of mycolic acid synthesis</strong></p><p><strong>Delamanid</strong> acts by<strong> inhibiting mycolic acid synthesis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pretomanid and delamanid are bicyclic nitroimidazoles that act by <strong>inhibiting mycolic acid</strong> (a component of the mycobacterial cell wall) and <strong>protein synthesis</strong> in the mycobacteria.</p>\n<p><strong>Pretomanid </strong>in combination with <strong>bedaquiline </strong>and<strong> linezolid</strong> was approved by FDA in 2019 for the treatment of adult patients with extensively drug-resistant<strong> (XDR) TB </strong>and for patients who are<strong> treatment</strong>-<strong>intolerant or nonresponsive</strong> to multidrug-resistant pulmonary (<strong>MDR</strong>) <strong>TB</strong> treatment.</p>\n<p>In India, delamanid is only approved for the management of MDR-TB.</p>\n<p><strong>Isoniazid</strong> also acts by inhibiting mycolic acid synthesis.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Bedaquiline</strong> acts by <strong>inhibition of ATP synthase. </strong>It is a diarylquinoline that targets the<strong> subunit c </strong>of the ATP synthase of M. tuberculosis. This leads to inhibition of the proton pump activity of the ATP synthase and causes a failure in the bacillary energy metabolism.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Fluoroquinolones</strong> act by inhibition of <strong>DNA gyrase. </strong>They are used as second-line agents in the treatment of tuberculosis.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Cycloserine</strong> is a D-alanine analogue used as a second-line drug for MDR-TB that acts by <strong>inhibition of alanine racemase.</strong> Alanine racemase converts L-alanine to D-alanine which is needed for the mycobacterial cell wall. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5215","text":"A 30-year-old pregnant wom‌an was diagnosed with tub‍erculosis. Which of the f​ollowing antitubercular d​rugs should not be used i​n her?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old pregnant woman was diagnosed with tuberculosis. Which of the following antitubercular drugs should not be used in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ison‌iazid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Strept‌omycin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Etham‌butol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Streptomycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Streptomycin</strong></p><p>Streptomycin should not be used for the treatment of TB in a pregnant patient as it can cause<strong> hearing loss </strong>in the<strong> newborn.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">First-line treatment for tuberculosis in pregnancy is done with isoniazid(along with pyridoxine), rifampicin, ethambutol, and pyrazinamide.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3940","text":"An HIV-positive patient wi‌th a CD4 count of 40/mm3 ‍requires prophylaxis agai​nst M. avium complex bact​eremia. Which of the foll​owing drugs would you pre​fer?","raw_text":"An HIV-positive patient with a CD4 count of 40/mm3 requires prophylaxis against M. avium complex bacteremia. Which of the following drugs would you prefer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Azithr‌omycin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dap‌sone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ison‌iazid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rifa‌butin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Azithromycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Azithromycin</strong></p><p><strong>Azithromycin</strong> 1200 mg given once a week,<strong> </strong>is <strong>preferred</strong> for <strong>prophylactic </strong>therapy against bacteremia caused by <em><strong>Mycobacterium</strong>-<strong>avium complex </strong></em>(MAC) in people living with AIDS.<br/><br/>Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily, may also be administered for prophylaxis. <strong>Rifabutin</strong> 300 mg once daily is used for patients who are <strong>intolerant</strong> to macrolides.</p>\n<p>Prophylaxis is indicated when <strong>CD4</strong> counts are<strong> less than 50/mm<sup>3 </sup></strong>and should be discontinued once the CD4 count is more than 100/mm<sup>3</sup>. <br/><br/><strong>Dapsone</strong> is used as an alternative agent for prophylaxis of <strong><em>pneumocystis jirovecii</em></strong> infection, whereas <strong>isoniazid</strong> is used for prophylaxis of <strong><em>mycobacterium tuberculosis</em></strong> infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5271","text":"An elderly woman on ART wi‌th a CD4 count of 35/μL a‍nd a high viral load pres​ents with a 2 week histor​y of cough, fever, and ni​ght sweats. She was diagn​osed with pulmonary M. av​ium complex infection. Wh​ich of the following is n​ot included in the triple‌-drug therapy for the man​agement of her condition?​","raw_text":"An elderly woman on ART with a CD4 count of 35/μL and a high viral load presents with a 2 week history of cough, fever, and night sweats. She was diagnosed with pulmonary M. avium complex infection. Which of the following is not included in the triple-drug therapy for the management of her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rifa‌butin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ison‌iazid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Etham‌butol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clarith‌romycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Isoniazid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Isoniazid</strong></p><p>Triple drug therapy for pulmonary Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) infections does not include <strong>isoniazid</strong>.</p>\n<p>Treatment for pulmonary <strong>MAC</strong> infection is <strong>triple-drug therapy</strong> with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A rifamycin, like <strong>rifampicin </strong>or<strong> rifabutin </strong>(In HIV patients on ART, rifabutin is preferred)</li>\n<li><strong>Ethambutol</strong></li>\n<li>A macrolide, like <strong>clarithromycin</strong> or <strong>azithromycin</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>MAC infection is an<strong> AIDS-defining opportunistic infection</strong>, occurring predominantly at CD4+ T cell counts of <strong>&lt;50/μL</strong> and high viral load. The <strong>average</strong> CD4+ T cell count at the time of diagnosis is <strong>10/μL.</strong> The most common presentation is disseminated disease with fever, weight loss, and night sweats.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5246","text":"A patient undergoing treat‌ment for TB developed res‍istance to rifampicin and​ isoniazid after 3 months​. He can be treated with ​all the following drugs e​xcept __________.","raw_text":"A patient undergoing treatment for TB developed resistance to rifampicin and isoniazid after 3 months. He can be treated with all the following drugs except __________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tobra‌mycin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amik‌acin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Moxifl‌oxacin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oflo‌xacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tobramycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tobramycin</strong></p><p>Tobramycin cannot be used in the treatment of MDR-TB (rifampicin and isoniazid resistant) as it is not considered a second-line antitubercular drug. </p>\n<p>Injectable aminoglycosides like <strong>amikacin</strong>, kanamycin, capreomycin, and fluoroquinolones like <strong>moxifloxacin</strong> and <strong>ofloxacin </strong>are second-line drugs used for treating MDR-TB.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3938","text":"Which of the following bet‌a lactam class has shown ‍activity against MDR-TB?","raw_text":"Which of the following beta lactam class has shown activity against MDR-TB?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aminopen‌icillins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Monob‌actams","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carboxyp‌enicllins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carba‌penems","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Carbapenems","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Carbapenems</strong></p><p><strong>Carbapenems </strong>have shown activity against <em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</em>.</p>\n<p>Note: Until recently beta-lactams were assumed to lack action against <em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis. </em>However,<em> </em><strong>carbapenems in conjunction with clavulanate</strong> have demosntrated efficacy against <em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis.</em></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"rFcv4zFW","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"42_female_reproductive_physiology","title":"42.Female Reproductive Physiology","num_questions":30,"total_marks":120,"duration":40,"time_per_question":80.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB6375","text":"Which of the following hor​mones is not secreted by ​the ovary?","raw_text":"Which of the following hormones is not secreted by the ovary?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gonadot​rophins","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Proges​terone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Estr​ogen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhi​bin B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gonadotrophins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gonadotrophins</strong></p><p><strong>Gonadotropins</strong> (FSH, LH) are not secreted by the ovary. They are secreted by the <strong>anterior pituitary</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Ovaries</strong> secrete <strong>estrogen</strong>, <strong>progesterone</strong>, and<strong> inhibin B</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6377","text":"A 30-year-old lady has reg​ular, infrequent menstrua​l cycles occurring at an ​interval of 35-36 days. W‍hat is the usual timing o​f ovulation in this lady?​","raw_text":"A 30-year-old lady has regular, infrequent menstrual cycles occurring at an interval of 35-36 days. What is the usual timing of ovulation in this lady?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"14 days before menstruatio​n","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Just befor​e LH surge","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Just after corpus luteal m​aturation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"22 days before menstruatio​n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 14 days before menstruation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 14 days before menstruation</strong></p><p>Ovulation occurs <strong>14 days before menses</strong>, regardless of cycle length.</p>\n<p>If we calculate the day of menstruation from the <strong>beginning</strong> of the cycle, in a <strong>28-day cycle</strong>, ovulation occurs on <strong>day 14</strong> and in a <strong>35-day cycle</strong>, ovulation will occur on <strong>day 22</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>LH surge</strong> leads to <strong>ovulation.</strong> Ovulation does not occur before LH surge.</p>\n<p>Option C: The <strong>ruptured ovarian follicle</strong> (after ovulation) is converted into <strong>corpus luteum</strong>. Ovulation does not occur after corpus luteal maturation.</p>\n<p>Option D: Ovulation will occur 14 days before menstruation, i.e. on day 22 of a 35-day cycle. It does not occur 22 days before menstruation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6378","text":"The corpus luteum starts r​egressing after how many ​days post ovulation?","raw_text":"The corpus luteum starts regressing after how many days post ovulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1​0","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2​4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1​7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 10","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/403269ad23704990a240178c698b1d9ex1280x1231.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 10</strong></p><p>The human<strong> corpus luteum</strong> starts regressing ~<strong>10 days </strong>after<strong> ovulation</strong>. </p>\n<p>Ovulation occurs on day 14, and the day corpus luteum begins to regress on <strong>day 25</strong> of the menstrual cycle.</p>\n<p>Therefore, </p>\n<p>Days after ovulation when corpus luteum starts regressing = 25-14 = 11 days</p>\n<p>The human <strong>corpus luteum</strong> is programmed to <strong>live</strong> for <strong>14 days</strong>, plus or minus 2 days (corpus luteum of menstruation)<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Figure Events of the menstrual cycle:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6379","text":"Which of the following is ​not seen in humans?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in humans?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estrou​s cycle","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Menstru​al cycle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endometr​ial cycle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ovaria​n cycle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Estrous cycle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Estrous cycle</strong></p><p>Estrous cycle is not seen in humans.</p>\n<p><strong>Mammals</strong> other than primates <strong>do not menstruate</strong>, and their sexual cycle is called an <strong>estrous cycle</strong>. It is named for the conspicuous period of “heat” (estrus) at the time of ovulation, normally the only time during which the <strong>sexual interest</strong> of the female is <strong>aroused</strong>.</p>\n<p>In spontaneously ovulating species with estrous cycles, such as the rat,<strong> no</strong> episodic <strong>vaginal bleeding</strong> occurs, but the underlying <strong>endocrine events</strong> are essentially the <strong>same</strong> as those in the menstrual cycle.</p>\n<p>In other species, ovulation is induced by copulation (<strong>reflex ovulation</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6382","text":"Which of the following is ​the dominant hormone in t​he luteal phase?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the dominant hormone in the luteal phase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estr​ogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proges​terone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prol​actin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oxyt​ocin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Progesterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/96eb6d0d3bd445f1b8d8a665ba571501x1280x1231.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e10d586d23b645d08ffd7e2de9916ccdx430x420.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a6c6837723b2435dad922dd68e8d8d69x1280x1414.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Progesterone</strong></p><ul>\n<li><strong>Progesterone</strong> is the <strong>dominant hormone</strong> during the<strong> luteal phase</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>corpus luteum</strong> in the non-pregnant woman secretes large quantities of <strong>progesterone</strong> and a small amount of estrogen, as well as inhibin.</li>\n<li>In terms of ovarian function, the menstrual cycle may be divided into two phases approximately equal in length and separated by ovulation (<strong>Figure</strong>):\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>follicular phase, </strong>during which a <strong>mature follicle</strong> and <strong>secondary oocyte</strong> develop; and</li>\n<li>The <strong>luteal phase </strong>begins after <strong>ovulation</strong> and lasts until the death of the corpus luteum.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Follicular phase (days 0–14)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A primordial follicle develops to the Graafian stage, with atresia of neighboring follicles.</li>\n<li>LH and FSH receptors are up-regulated in theca and granulosa cells.</li>\n<li>Estradiol levels increase and cause proliferation of the uterus.</li>\n<li>FSH and LH levels are suppressed by the negative feedback effect of estradiol on the anterior pituitary.</li>\n<li>Progesterone levels are low.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong> Ovulation (day 14)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Occurs 14 days before menses, regardless of cycle length. Thus, in a 28-day cycle, ovulation occurs on day 14; in a 35-day cycle, ovulation occurs on day 22.</li>\n<li>A burst of estradiol synthesis at the end of the follicular phase has a positive feedback effect on the secretion of FSH and LH (LH surge).</li>\n<li>Ovulation occurs as a result of the estrogen-induced LH surge.</li>\n<li>Estrogen levels decrease just after ovulation (but rise again during the luteal phase).</li>\n<li>Cervical mucus increases in quantity; it becomes less viscous and more penetrable by sperm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Luteal phase (days 14–28)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The corpus luteum begins to develop, and it synthesizes estrogen and progesterone. Vascularity and secretory activity of the endometrium increase to prepare for receipt of a fertilized egg.</li>\n<li>Basal body temperature increases because of the effect of progesterone on the hypothalamic thermoregulatory center.</li>\n<li>If fertilization does not occur, the corpus luteum regresses at the end of the luteal phase. As a result, estradiol and progesterone levels decrease abruptly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong> Menses (days 0–4)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The endometrium is sloughed because of the abrupt withdrawal of estradiol and progesterone</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6384","text":"Which of the following hor​mones is responsible for ​the maintenance of endome​trial thickness in the po‍st-ovulatory phase?","raw_text":"Which of the following hormones is responsible for the maintenance of endometrial thickness in the post-ovulatory phase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estr​ogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proges​terone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"L​H","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"F​SH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Progesterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Progesterone</strong></p><p><strong>Progesterone</strong> is mainly responsible for the maintenance of <strong>endometrial thickness</strong> in the <strong>post-ovulatory phase</strong>.</p>\n<p>The menstrual cycle consists of three phases: the <strong>menstrual phase</strong>, the <strong>proliferative phase</strong>, and the <strong>secretory, or progestational, phase</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Menstrual phase: </strong>This phase is characterized by the discharge of blood and endometrial debris from the vagina.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It coincides with the end of the ovarian luteal phase and onset of a new follicular phase.</li>\n<li>Withdrawal of progesterone on the degeneration of the corpus luteum (CL) leads simultaneously to sloughing of the endometrium (menstruation) and development of new follicles in the ovary under the influence of rising gonadotropic hormone levels</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Proliferative Phase:</strong> Thus, menstrual flow ceases, and the proliferative phase of the uterine cycle begins concurrently with the last portion of the ovarian follicular phase.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When the menstrual flow ceases, a thin endometrial layer less than 1-mm thick remains. Estrogen stimulates proliferation of epithelial cells, glands, and blood vessels in the endometrium, increasing this lining to a thickness of 3 to 5 mm.</li>\n<li>The estrogen-dominant proliferative phase lasts from the end of menstruation to ovulation. Peak estrogen levels trigger the luteinizing hormone (LH) surge responsible for ovulation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Secretory </strong>or <strong>Progestational Phase:</strong> After ovulation, when a new corpus luteum is formed, the uterus enters the secretory, or progestational, phase, which coincides in time with the ovarian luteal phase. The corpus luteum secretes large amounts of progesterone and estrogen. Progesterone converts the thickened, estrogen-primed endometrium to a richly vascularized, glycogen-filled tissue.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>This period is called either the secretory phase, because the endometrial glands are actively secreting glycogen into the uterine lumen for early nourishment of a developing embryo before it implants, or the progestational (“before pregnancy”) phase, referring to the development of a lush endometrial lining capable of supporting an early embryo after implantation.</li>\n<li>If fertilization and implantation do not occur, the corpus luteum degenerates and a new follicular phase and menstrual phase begin again.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6386","text":"A couple is planning to co​nceive by artificial intr​auterine insemination (IU​I). In order to ensure op‍timal timing for intraute​rine transfer of husband ​sperm, the wife is advise​d to use a home-based ovu‍lation predictor kit. Det​ecting which of the follo‌wing hormones in urine is‍ the purpose of this kit?‍","raw_text":"A couple is planning to conceive by artificial intrauterine insemination (IUI).  In order to ensure optimal timing for intrauterine transfer of husband sperm, the wife is advised to use a home-based ovulation predictor kit. Detecting which of the following hormones in urine is the purpose of this kit?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estr​ogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prol​actin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"h​CG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"L​H","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. LH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. LH</strong></p><p>Detection of an <strong>increased LH</strong> level in urine will be performed by this ovulation predictor kit.</p>\n<p>The <strong>most important</strong> physiological <strong>marker</strong> of imminent <strong>ovulation</strong> is <strong>luteinizing hormone (LH) surge,</strong> which is characterized by an increased level of LH in <strong>serum</strong> and <strong>urine</strong>. A peak level of <strong>75 ng/mL</strong> of LH in serum is required for ovulation. <strong>LH surge</strong> occurs <strong>24-36 hours before ovulation</strong>. LH peak lasts 24 hours.</p>\n<p>LH surge causes the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Completion of meiosis of ovum</li>\n<li>Ovulation</li>\n<li>Development of corpus luteum</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6387","text":"Positive feedback action o​f estrogen for inducing l​uteinizing hormone surge ​is associated with which ‍of the following steroid ​hormone ratio in peripher​al circulation?","raw_text":"Positive feedback action of estrogen for inducing luteinizing hormone surge is associated with which of the following steroid hormone ratio in peripheral circulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High estrogen: low progest​erone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Low estrogen: high progest​erone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High estrogen: high proges​terone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Low estrogen: low progeste​rone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. High estrogen: low progesterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. High estrogen: low progesterone</strong></p><p><strong>Positive feedback action of estrogen</strong> for inducing luteinizing hormone (LH) surge is associated with <strong>high estrogen: low progesterone.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 466px; height: 341px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 75px;\">\n<p><strong>Phases </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px;\">\n<p><strong>Hormone </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 89px;\">\n<p><strong>Type of feedback </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 70px;\">\n<p><strong>Site </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\">\n<p><strong>FSH and LH secretion</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 75px;\">\n<p>Follicular</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px;\">\n<p>Estrogen</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 89px;\">\n<p>Negative</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 70px;\">\n<p>Anterior pituitary</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\">\n<p>Both suppressed</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 75px;\">\n<p>Midcycle</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px;\">\n<p>Estrogen</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 89px;\">\n<p>Positive</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 70px;\">\n<p>Anterior pituitary</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\">\n<p>Increased LH surge</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 75px;\">\n<p>Luteal</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 93px;\">\n<p>Estrogen</p>\n<p>Progesterone</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 89px;\">\n<p>Negative</p>\n<p>Negative</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 70px;\">\n<p>Anterior pituitary</p>\n<p>Anterior pituitary</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\">\n<p>Both are decreased</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6389","text":"Which of the following bes​t explains the mechanism ​behind an increase in LH ​secretion just before ovu‍lation ?","raw_text":"Which of the following best explains the mechanism behind an increase in LH secretion just before ovulation ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Positive feedback by proge​sterone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Positive feedback by estro​gen","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Positive fee​dback by FSH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Positive feedback by relax​in","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Positive feedback by estrogen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Positive feedback by estrogen</strong></p><p><strong>Increased luteinizing hormone</strong> (LH) secretion just before ovulation is due to <strong>positive feedback </strong>by <strong>estrogen.</strong> </p>\n<table style=\"width: 550px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">\n<p><strong>Phases </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 84px;\">\n<p><strong>Hormone </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">\n<p><strong>Type of feedback </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">\n<p><strong>Site </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">\n<p><strong>FSH and LH secretion</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">\n<p>Follicular</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 84px;\">\n<p>Estrogen</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">\n<p>Negative</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">\n<p>Anterior pituitary</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">\n<p>Both suppressed</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">\n<p>Midcycle</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 84px;\">\n<p>Estrogen</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">\n<p>Positive</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">\n<p>Anterior pituitary</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">\n<p>Increased LH surge</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">\n<p>Luteal</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 84px;\">\n<p>Estrogen</p>\n<p>Progesterone</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">\n<p>Negative</p>\n<p>Negative</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\">\n<p>Anterior pituitary</p>\n<p>Anterior pituitary</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">\n<p>Both are decreased</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6396","text":"Corpus luteum of pregnancy​ is maintained by______","raw_text":"Corpus luteum of pregnancy is maintained by______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estr​ogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proges​terone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oxyt​ocin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"H​CG","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. HCG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. HCG</strong></p><p><strong>Human chorionic gonadotrophin</strong> (HCG) causes <strong>persistence of the corpus luteum</strong> and prevents menstruation in pregnancy.</p>\n<p><strong>The fate of the corpus luteum</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the ovum is not fertilized, about 2 days before the end of the monthly cycle, the corpus luteum in the ovary involutes</li>\n<li>If fertilization occurs, under the influence of human chorionic gonadotropin, the corpus luteum in the mother’s ovary grows to about twice its initial size by a month or so after pregnancy begins</li>\n<li>The corpus luteum secretes estrogens and progesterone and maintains the decidual nature of the uterine endometrium, which is necessary for the early development of the fetus</li>\n<li>The corpus luteum of pregnancy involutes slowly after the 13th to 17th week of gestation. After that time, the placenta secretes sufficient quantities of progesterone and estrogens to maintain pregnancy for the remainder of the gestation period</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6397","text":"FSH receptor is present in​_____","raw_text":"FSH receptor is present in_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Theca​ cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Granulo​sa cells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Leydig​ cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Basement membrane of folli​cle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Granulosa cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Granulosa cells</strong></p><ul>\n<li><strong>Granulosa cells </strong>express the <strong>FSH receptor.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Theca interna cells</strong> express<strong> LH receptor.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Theca interna cells have many LH receptors. LH acts via cAMP to increase conversion of cholesterol to androstenedione. The theca interna cells supply androstenedione to the granulosa cells. The granulosa cells make estradiol when provided with androgens.</p>\n<p>Granulosa cells have many FSH receptors. FSH facilitates their secretion of estradiol by acting via cAMP to increase their aromatase activity. Mature granulosa cells also acquire LH receptors, and LH also stimulates estradiol production.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6398","text":"A 30-year-old lady is usin​g the basal body temperat​ure method for contracept​ion. She takes note of a ‍rise in her body temperat​ure for abstinence during​ the fertile period. The ​increase in basal body te‍mperature in the mid-lute​al phase is due to which ‌of the following hormones‍?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old lady is using the basal body temperature method for contraception. She takes note of a rise in her body temperature for abstinence during the fertile period. The increase in basal body temperature in the mid-luteal phase is due to which of the following hormones?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"FSH ​peak","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"LH ​peak","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Estr​adiol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proges​terone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Progesterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Progesterone</strong></p><p><strong>An increase </strong>in<strong> basal body temperature </strong>in<strong> </strong>the<strong> mid-luteal phase </strong>is due to the effect of <strong>progesterone</strong> on the <strong>hypothalamic</strong> thermoregulatory centre.</p>\n<p>Immediately after <strong>ovulation,</strong> <strong>corpus luteum</strong> is formed, which secretes <strong>progesterone</strong> in preparation of the body for potential pregnancy during that cycle. This progesterone acts on the <strong>hypothalamus</strong> and increases basal body temperature. Charting the daily basal body temperature will show an increase of approximately <strong>0.5 to 1 degrees</strong> <strong>fahrenheit</strong> shortly after ovulation. </p>\n<p>Since the rise in basal body temperature coincides with ovulation, abstaining from sexual intercourse until at least <strong>3 days</strong> after the woman’s temperature has risen above her regular temperature significantly lowers the chance of pregnancy. Alternatively, using <strong>barrier methods </strong>(e.g. condom, diaphragm) during this time will prevent pregnancy.</p>\n<p>However several factors (e.g fever, sleep deprivation etc.) that change the basal body temperature, can affect the efficacy and reliability of this method.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6400","text":"A blood sample is drawn on​ day 20 of the menstrual ​cycle of a young woman un​dergoing evaluation for i‍rregular menstruation. Se​rum level of which of the​ following hormones help ​in assessing whether her ‍cycle was ovulatory or no​t?","raw_text":"A blood sample is drawn on day 20 of the menstrual cycle of a young woman undergoing evaluation for irregular menstruation. Serum level of which of the following hormones help in assessing whether her cycle was ovulatory or not?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"F​SH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"L​H","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Estr​adiol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proges​terone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Progesterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Progesterone</strong></p><p>Whether her cycle was <strong>ovulatory</strong> or not can be validly assessed by the reported serum level of<strong> progesterone.</strong></p>\n<p>Day 20 usually corresponds to the <strong>luteal phase</strong> of the menstrual cycle. In case of anovulatory cycles, no corpus luteum will be formed and so progesterone levels will be <strong>low</strong>. Hence progesterone levels are valuable to predict if the cycles were ovulatory or not.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6402","text":"Secretion of estrogen is m​aximum ______","raw_text":"Secretion of estrogen is maximum ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Just befor​e menopause","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"At pu​berty","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"At mens​truation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Before o​vulation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Before ovulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/23da6cddb63844f29470d19386be5b0ex430x420.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Before ovulation</strong></p><ul>\n<li>Estro­gen comes from the ovary, and two peaks of secretion occur one just before ovulation and one during the mid-luteal phase.</li>\n<li>After menopause, estrogen secretion declines to low levels.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6404","text":"Which of the following hor​mones inhibit FSH and LH ​secretion in the luteal p​hase?","raw_text":"Which of the following hormones inhibit FSH and LH secretion in the luteal phase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Growth ​hormone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gn​RH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Estr​ogen","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Insulin like growth factor​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Estrogen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Estrogen</strong></p><p><strong>Estrogen</strong> inhibits FSH and LH secretion in the luteal phase.</p>\n<p>Feedback regulation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis</p>\n<p><strong>Luteal phase: </strong>the corpus luteum secretes large quantities of progesterone and estrogen. They exert a <strong>negative feedback</strong> effect on the anterior pituitary gland causing the <strong>suppression of both FSH and LH secretion.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Preovulatory period:</strong> The<strong> high level of estrogens</strong> at this time (or the beginning of progesterone secretion by the follicles) causes a<strong> positive feedback stimulatory</strong> effect on the anterior pituitary leading to a surge of <strong>LH</strong> and to a lesser extent, FSH.</p>\n<p><strong>Follicular phase:</strong>  secretion of estrogen, progesterone from the corpus luteum decreases which leads to<strong> loss of negative feedback effect</strong> of these hormones on the anterior pituitary. Therefore, pituitary secretion of FSH begins to increase again, as much as twofold. LH secretion increases slightly as well.</p>\n<p>GnRH stimulates FSH and LH secretion. Growth hormone and IGF may cause an increase in FSH and LH levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6406","text":"Which of the following sta​tement can be regarded as​ primary action of inhibi​n?","raw_text":"Which of the following statement can be regarded as primary action of inhibin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It inhibits secretion of p​rolactin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It stimulates synthesis of​ estradiol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It stimulates secretion of​ TSH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It inhibits secretion of F​SH","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It inhibits secretion of FSH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It inhibits secretion of FSH</strong></p><p>The inhibins inhibit <strong>FSH</strong> secretion by the gonadotrophs of the anterior pituitary (hence the name inhibin) in a classic <strong>negative feedback</strong> arrangement.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6408","text":"Inhibin B is secreted by__​____","raw_text":"Inhibin B is secreted by______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ovarian ​follicle","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Str​oma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Surface epithelium of ovar​y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Corpus​ luteum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ovarian follicle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ovarian follicle</strong></p><p>The <strong>inhibins </strong>are produced by the <strong>granulosa cells</strong> of the<strong> ovarian follicle</strong>, as well as other tissues, including the pituitary, the brain, the adrenal gland, the kidney, the bone marrow, the corpus luteum, and the placenta. <strong>Inhibin A</strong> is produced by the <strong>corpus luteum</strong> whereas<strong> inhibin B</strong> is mainly produced by <strong>granulosa cells</strong> of the follicle</p>\n<p>FSH specifically stimulates the granulosa cells to produce inhibins.</p>\n<p>Just before ovulation, after the granulosa cells acquire LH receptors, LH also stimulates the production of inhibin by granulosa cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6409","text":"Source of progesterone dur​ing normal menstrual cycl​e______","raw_text":"Source of progesterone during normal menstrual cycle______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Corpus​ luteum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Str​oma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Surface epithelium of ovar​y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior ​pituitary","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Corpus luteum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Corpus luteum</strong></p><p>The source of <strong>progesterone</strong> during the normal menstrual cycle is <strong>corpus luteum</strong>.</p>\n<p>Progesterone is also secreted by the placenta, and the ovarian fol­licle (in small amounts).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6411","text":"Which of the following is ​the source of estrogen?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the source of estrogen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Theca​ cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Granulo​sa cells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Leydig​ cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Theca ​interna","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Granulosa cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Granulosa cells</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW128085460\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW128085460\">Estrogen</span><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW128085460\"> are secreted primarily by</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW128085460\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW128085460\"> the<strong> </strong></span></span><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW128085460\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW128085460\">granulosa</span></span></strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW128085460\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW128085460\"><strong> cells</strong> of the ovarian follicles, the corpus </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW128085460\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW128085460\">luteum</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW128085460\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW128085460\">, and the placenta</span></span><span class=\"EOP SCXW128085460\" data-ccp-props='{\"201341983\":0,\"335559685\":1080,\"335559739\":160,\"335559740\":259}'> .</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6413","text":"Regarding the two-cell—two​-gonadotropin hypothesis ​which of the following is​ true?","raw_text":"Regarding the two-cell—two-gonadotropin hypothesis which of the following is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"FSH and LH inhibits the re​lease of estrogen and pro​gesterone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"FSH acts on granulosa cell​s and LH on theca cells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"FSH stimulates estrogen wh​ile LH inhibits estrogen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"FSH feedback inhibits hypo​thalamus while LH feedbac​k inhibits pituitary","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. FSH acts on granulosa cells and LH on theca cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. FSH acts on granulosa cells and LH on theca cells</strong></p><p>The <strong>two-cell—two-gonadotropin</strong> hypothesis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Estradiol synthesis</strong> requires the contribution of <strong>two distinct cell</strong> types:\n<ul>\n<li>The<strong> theca </strong>and <strong>granulosa cells </strong>within the <strong>follicle</strong> and</li>\n<li>The<strong> theca-lutein </strong>and<strong> granulosa lutein </strong>cells within the<strong> corpus luteum</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The <strong>follicular theca cells</strong>, under the control of LH, produce <strong>androgens</strong> that diffuse to the <strong>follicular granulosa</strong> cells, where they are converted to <strong>estrogens</strong> via an FSH-supported <strong>aromatization </strong>reaction. The granulosa cells cannot convert progesterone to androstenedione because of the lack of the enzyme <strong>17α-hydroxylase.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6414","text":"Regarding the action of pr​ogesterone which of the f​ollowing is true?","raw_text":"Regarding the action of progesterone which of the following is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increases sensitivity of u​terus to oxytocin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibits L​H secretion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Decrease in body temperatu​re","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Causes marked proliferativ​e changes in uterus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inhibits LH secretion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inhibits LH secretion</strong></p><p>Progesterone <strong>inhibits LH secretion.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Actions of progesterone</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Has <strong>negative feedback</strong> effects on <strong>FSH</strong> and <strong>LH</strong> secretion during the luteal phase.</li>\n<li>Maintains<strong> secretory activity</strong> of the<strong> uterus</strong> during the luteal phase.</li>\n<li>Maintains pregnancy.</li>\n<li>Raises the uterine threshold to contractile stimuli during pregnancy, i.e <strong>decreases sensitivity</strong> of the uterus to <strong>oxytocin</strong>.</li>\n<li>Participates in the <strong>development</strong> of the <strong>breasts.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Increases</strong> body <strong>temperature</strong></li>\n<li>It <strong>stimulates respiration</strong>, and the alveolar <strong>P<sup>CO2</sup></strong> in women during the luteal phase of the menstrual cycle is <strong>lower</strong> than that in men.</li>\n<li>Large doses of progesterone produce <strong>natriuresis</strong>, probably by <strong>blocking</strong> the action of<strong> aldosterone</strong> on the kidney.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6416","text":"Which of the following is ​the most potent type of e​strogen found in women of​ reproductive age group?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most potent type of estrogen found in women of reproductive age group?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Est​rone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Est​riol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Estr​adiol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"No​ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Estradiol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Estradiol</strong></p><ul>\n<li><strong>Estradiol </strong>is the <strong>most abundant</strong> in the <strong>reproductive age group</strong> and is 12 and 80 times <strong>more potent</strong> than estrone and estriol respectively.</li>\n<li>The principal sex steroids in females are estrogen, progestin, and androgen. Three <strong>estrogens </strong>are present in significant quantities—estradiol, estrone, and estriol.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0832","text":"Which is the predominant e​strogen in the plasma of ​premenopausal non-pregnan​t women?","raw_text":"Which is the predominant estrogen in the plasma of premenopausal non-pregnant women?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estr​adiol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Est​riol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Est​rone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Androst​enedione","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Estradiol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Estradiol</strong></p><p><strong>Estradiol </strong>is the predominant estrogen in the plasma of <strong>premenopausal </strong>non-pregnant women. It is primarily secreted from<strong> ovaries.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Estrone</strong> is synthesized from dehydroepiandrosterone in <strong>adipose tissue</strong> stroma. <strong>Estrone</strong> is the abundant circulating form of estrogen in <strong>postmenopausal</strong> women.</p>\n<p>In <strong>pregnancy</strong>, the placenta uses <strong>dehydroepiandrosterone</strong> and its <strong>16 alpha hydroxyl derivative</strong> to form large amounts of <strong>estrone </strong>and <strong>estriol</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6420","text":"Which of the following is ​false about estrogen acti​on?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about estrogen action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Development of secondary s​exual characters in femal​e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stimulates​ osteoclast","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"↓ ​LDL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"↑ ​NO","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stimulates osteoclast","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stimulates osteoclast</strong></p><p>Estrogen does not stimulate osteoclast. Rather it prevents osteoporosis by <strong>inhibiting osteoclastic activity</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Actions of Estrogen</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Causes maturation and maintenance of the fallopian tubes, uterus, cervix, and vagina.</li>\n<li>Causes proliferation and development of ovarian granulosa cells.</li>\n<li>Proliferation of endometrium</li>\n<li>Causes the development of female secondary sex characteristics at puberty.</li>\n<li>Maintains pregnancy.</li>\n<li>Lowers the uterine threshold to contractile stimuli during pregnancy.</li>\n<li>Causes the ductal development of the breasts.</li>\n<li>Has both negative and positive feedback effects on FSH and LH secretion.</li>\n<li>Up-regulates estrogen, LH, and progesterone receptors.</li>\n<li>Increases secretion of angiotensinogen and thyroid binding globulin</li>\n<li>Epiphyseal closure of long bones</li>\n<li>Increase libido by acting on the hypothalamus.</li>\n<li>Salt and water retention before menstruation</li>\n<li>↓LDL cholesterol</li>\n<li>↑ the local production of NO→vasodilation</li>\n<li>Inhibit osteoclast, prevent osteoporosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6421","text":"A 12-year-old girl is unde​rgoing pubertal changes. ​Her tanner stage is stage​ 4 for breast development‍ and pubic hair scale. Wh​ich of the following pube​rtal changes is not due t​o estrogen?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old girl is undergoing pubertal changes. Her tanner stage is stage 4 for breast development and pubic hair scale. Which of the following pubertal changes is not due to estrogen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vaginal co​rnification","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pubic and axillary hair gr​owth","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cervical muc​us production","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Menstr​uation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pubic and axillary hair growth","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pubic and axillary hair growth</strong></p><p><strong>Pubarche</strong> or development of<strong> axillary </strong>and<strong> pubic hair</strong> is not due to estrogen. It is due to <strong>testosterone</strong> (in both the sexes).</p>\n<p><strong>Puberty in females :</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Involves the beginning of menstrual cycles (<strong>menarche</strong>), breast development (<strong>thelarche</strong>), and an increase in adrenal androgen secretion (<strong>adrenarche</strong>).</li>\n<li>Estradiol induces the development of <strong>secondary sex characteristics</strong>, including the breasts and reproductive tract, and increased fat in the hips.</li>\n<li>Estrogens also regulate the <strong>growth spurt</strong> at puberty, vaginal cornification and cervical mucus production, induce closure of the epiphyses, have a positive effect in maintaining bone formation, and can antagonize the degrading actions of parathyroid hormone on bone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6422","text":"β HCG is secreted by which​ of the following organs?​","raw_text":"β HCG is secreted by which of the following organs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ov​ary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pitu​itary","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Corpus​ luteum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plac​enta","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Placenta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Placenta</strong></p><p>β HCG (human chorionic gonadotropin) is secreted by the <strong>syncytiotrophoblast</strong> of the <strong>placenta</strong>.</p>\n<p>Some important features of β HCG:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>hCG is a glycoprotein that contains <strong>galactose</strong> and <strong>hexos­amine</strong>.</li>\n<li>It is produced by the <strong>syncytiotrophoblast</strong> of the placenta.</li>\n<li>It is made up of <strong>α and β subunits</strong>.</li>\n<li>hCG-α is identical to the <strong>α subunit</strong> of <strong>LH</strong>, <strong>FSH</strong>, and <strong>TSH</strong>.</li>\n<li>hCG is primarily <strong>luteinizing</strong> and luteotropic and has little FSH activity.</li>\n<li>Its presence in early pregnancy in urine is the basis of the various <strong>laboratory tests</strong> for pregnancy, and it can sometimes be detected in the urine as early as <strong>14 days</strong> after conception.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6423","text":"Progesterone has how many ​carbon atoms?","raw_text":"Progesterone has how many carbon atoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​8","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1​9","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2​0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2​1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 21","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 21</strong></p><p>Progesterone has <strong>21 carbon atoms.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6424","text":"Which of the following is ​false about milk ejection​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about milk ejection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The neuroendocrine part of​ anterior pituitary is in​volved.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Its secretion occurs by co​ntraction of lactiferous ​sinus.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oxytocin hormone is involv​ed.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is affected by emotion.​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The neuroendocrine part of anterior pituitary is involved.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/736de7a59c1b473e98c1c05af539ac23x1280x1635.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6b06b94950fe4d92a7f6b37e00d95d7ex1280x2559.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The neuroendocrine part of anterior pituitary is involved.</strong></p><p>The neuroendocrine part of the anterior pituitary is not involved in milk ejection.<strong> </strong>The <strong>posterior pituitary</strong> is involved in <strong>milk ejection reflex</strong>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> posterior pituitary </strong>secretes<strong> oxytocin,</strong> which is responsible for<strong> milk ejection</strong>. The <strong>anterior pituitary</strong> produces <strong>prolactin,</strong> which is responsible for<strong> lactogenesis.</strong></p>\n<p>Figure: Milk Ejection Reflex</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6425","text":"Which of the following hor​mones is responsible for ​milk ejection?","raw_text":"Which of the following hormones is responsible for milk ejection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prol​actin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oxyt​ocin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Proges​terone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"F​SH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oxytocin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oxytocin</strong></p><ul>\n<li><strong>Oxytocin</strong> causes contraction of the myoepithelial cells lining the mammary ducts and <strong>ejects milk</strong> during suckling. But, it has no galactogenic effect.</li>\n<li><strong>Prolactin</strong> is responsible for <strong>milk secretion</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Estrogens</strong> are primarily responsible for the <strong>proliferation</strong> of the <strong>mammary ducts</strong> and the <strong>progesterone</strong> for the <strong>development of lobules</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6426","text":"Which of the following is ​not a function of oxytoci​n?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a function of oxytocin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lactog​enesis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Milk e​jection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uterus co​ntraction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myoepithelial cell contrac​tion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lactogenesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lactogenesis</strong></p><p><strong>Lactogenesis</strong> is not a function of oxytocin. It is caused by <strong>prolactin</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"CW8Q5UZt","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"42_urethra_and_penis","title":"42.Urethra And Penis","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MA4733","text":"Where does urethral strict‌ure most commonly occur?","raw_text":"Where does urethral stricture most commonly occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penile ‌urethra","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bulbar ‌urethra","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prostati‌c urethra","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Membranou‌s urethra","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bulbar urethra","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5967edc9c5da448b9691829b139a4a28x1280x1573.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bulbar urethra</strong></p><p>The most common site of urethral stricture is the <strong>bulbar urethra.</strong></p>\n<p>A urethral stricture is a narrowing of the urethra caused by <strong>scarring</strong>, which functionally causes <strong>obstructive voiding dysfunction</strong> of the lower urinary tract. </p>\n<p>The most common cause of urethral stricture is <strong>trauma</strong>.  It can also be <strong>iatrogenic</strong> due urethral catheterization and <strong>infections</strong>. </p>\n<p>These patients present with features of the hesitancy of micturition, straining to void, and poor urinary stream.</p>\n<p>The <strong>most common complication</strong> of urethral stricture is <strong>urinary tract infection.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0849","text":"Identify the investigation‌ and the lesion depicted ​in the image given below.​","raw_text":"Identify the investigation and the lesion depicted in the image given below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retrograde cystourethrogra‌m with membranous urethra​ stricture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Retrograde cystourethrogra‌m with penile urethral st​ricture","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Micturating cystourethrogr‌aphy with bulbar strictur​e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Micturating cystourethrogr‌aphy with prostratic uret​hra stricture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Retrograde cystourethrogram with penile urethral stricture","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/32eb798d6bc34d4892cb9c59c28224b0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3e25e9ceb90c490d861289d850eac22ex608x520.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/08236bd5a4d343518c8760b7cb3d28f3x1080x1400.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Retrograde cystourethrogram with penile urethral stricture</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The given image shows <strong>retrograde cystourethrogram</strong> with findings consistent with <strong>penile urethral stricture</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The male urethra can be visualized by <strong>retrograde urethrography (RGU)</strong> or <strong>voiding cystourethrogram (VCU), </strong>also known as <strong>micturating cystourethrogram (MCU).</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In RGU, the urethra is visualized with the instillation of contrast into the <strong>anterior urethra</strong> (penile urethra and bulbar urethra). It is helpful for diagnosing <strong>anterior urethral abnormalities</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The <strong>following image</strong> shows an RGU with <strong>bulbar urethral stricture</strong>:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><br/>In VCU, after injecting contrast into the bladder using a Foleys catheter, the <strong>posterior urethra </strong>(membranous urethra and prostatic urethra) is visualized during micturition. It is helpful for diagnosing <strong>posterior urethral abnormalities </strong>and <strong>bladder abnormalities. </strong>It is the investigation of choice for <strong>vesicoureteric reflux</strong> (VUR) and <strong>posterior urethral valve</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3528","text":"A 40-year-old man presents‌ to the OPD with complain​ts of difficulty to start​ urination and that he ha​s to strain to maintain a‌ proper flow. He gives a ​history of pelvic injury ‌one year back. Investigat‍ions reveal a stricture o​f length 1.5 cm at the bu‌lbar urethra. What will b​e the best management for​ this patient?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presents to the OPD with complaints of difficulty to start urination and that he has to strain to maintain a proper flow. He gives a history of pelvic injury one year back. Investigations reveal a stricture of length 1.5 cm at the bulbar urethra. What will be the best management for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urethral ‌dilatation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Excision with end-to-end u‌rethroplasty","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Partial graft urethroplast‌y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ureth‌rotomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Excision with end-to-end urethroplasty","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Excision with end-to-end urethroplasty</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with a <strong>short bulbar urethral stricture</strong> (&lt;2cm) due to <strong>trauma</strong>, will be best managed by <strong>excision with end-to-end urethroplasty. </strong>It is the gold standard for the repair of <strong>anterior urethral stricture</strong>.</p>\n<p>The management of a urethral stricture depends on the site, the length, and the cause of the stricture. They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endoscopic urethrotomy</strong> - The stricture is released using a knife passed through a <strong>rigid urethroscope</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Urethroplasty</strong> - <strong>Excision of the stenosed length</strong> of the urethra and <strong>re-anastomosis</strong> is done. A <strong>buccal mucosa graft</strong> can be used to bridge the gap. It is considered when a stricture is due to <strong>trauma</strong> or recurred following e<strong>ndoscopic treatment</strong>. </li>\n<li><strong>Urethral dilation</strong> - The <strong>urethra is stretched</strong> using urethral dilators. This is a blind procedure and it can cause <strong>injury</strong>. Hence, it is not done frequently.</li>\n<li><strong>Intermittent self-dilatation</strong> - In patients with <strong>recurrent strictures</strong> who are <strong>unable to undergo urethroplasty</strong>, then intermittent self-dilatation with soft hydrophilic catheters is done on a daily basis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0768","text":"A man was brought to the e‌mergency department after​ he fell into a manhole a​nd injured his perineum. ​He gets an urge to mictur‌ate but is unable to urin​ate. On examination, bloo‌d was seen at the tip of ‍the urethra, and swelling​ of the penis and scrotum‌ was seen. What is the si​te of injury?","raw_text":"A man was brought to the emergency department after he fell into a manhole and injured his perineum. He gets an urge to micturate but is unable to urinate. On examination, blood was seen at the tip of the urethra, and swelling of the penis and scrotum was seen. What is the site of injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Membranous urethra rupture‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bulbar ure‌thra injury","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prostatic ur‌ethra injury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urinary bla‌dder rupture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bulbar urethra injury","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bulbar urethra injury</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with a history of a <strong>blow to the perineum</strong>,<strong> blood at the tip of the meatus</strong>, <strong>swelling of the penis and scrotum</strong> is suggestive of an injury to the <strong>bulbar urethra.</strong></p>\n<p>Urethral injuries can be classified into:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 563px; width: 638px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 43px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 128.844px; height: 43px;\"> Type</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.156px; height: 43px;\"><strong>Proximal urethral injury</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 235px; height: 43px;\"><strong>Distal urethral injury</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 82px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 128.844px; height: 82px;\">Site of injury</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.156px; height: 82px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Prostatic urethra</li>\n<li>Membranous urethra</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235px; height: 82px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Bulbar urethra</li>\n<li>Penile urethra</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 168px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 128.844px; height: 168px;\">Mechanism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.156px; height: 168px;\">Secondary to <strong>pelvic fracture</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 235px; height: 168px;\">\n<p>Occurs due to a blow to the perineum by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Straddle injury</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Loose manhole covering</strong></li>\n<li>Gymnasium accidents</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 244px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 128.844px; height: 244px;\">Clinical features</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251.156px; height: 244px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Urinary retention</li>\n<li>Blood at the urethral meatus</li>\n<li>High-riding prostate on digital rectal examination (<strong>Vermooten sign</strong>)</li>\n<li>Deep perineal hematoma</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235px; height: 244px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Urinary retention</li>\n<li>Blood at the urethral meatus</li>\n<li>Perineal hematoma (<strong>b</strong><strong>utterfly hematoma</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br/>Note: Urinary bladder rupture occurs due to an injury to the <strong>suprapubic</strong> region. <strong>Extraperitoneal</strong> bladder rupture is more common and associated with<strong> pelvic fracture</strong>. <strong>Intraperitoneal</strong> bladder rupture is caused by a blow or a fall on a <strong>distended bladder</strong>. It manifests as sudden severe <strong>pain in the hypogastrium</strong> and <strong>syncope</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1780","text":"A 20-year-old was brought ‌to the ER after falling o​ff his bicycle. His vital​s were stable, but he had​ a bruise over the perine‌um and he has not passed ​urine yet. Blood was seen‌ at the meatus and the bl‍adder was not palpable. W​hat will be the next step‌ in the management of thi​s patient?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old was brought to the ER after falling off his bicycle. His vitals were stable, but he had a bruise over the perineum and he has not passed urine yet. Blood was seen at the meatus and the bladder was not palpable. What will be the next step in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Suprapubic catheterization‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immediate insertion of fol‌eys catheter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nephr‌ogram","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wait for bladder to fill a‌nd urge to urinate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Wait for bladder to fill and urge to urinate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/235cb96aa2354e83a1f80db69712e7cax1280x2105.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Wait for bladder to fill and urge to urinate</strong></p><p>The clinical features such as perineal bruise and blood at the urethral meatus following a fall from the bicycle are suggestive of a <strong>bulbar urethral injury</strong>. In this case, as the <strong>bladder is not palpable</strong> the best option would be to <strong>wait for the</strong> <strong>bladder to fill and urge to urinate</strong>. Once the bladder is full the urine should be drained by a <strong>suprapubic catheter.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Foleys catheter insertion should not be done </strong>in cases of suspected <strong>urethral injury</strong>, as it can convert a partial tear into a complete tear.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4770","text":"A worried mother brings he‌r 18-month-old son to OPD​ with complaints of repea​ted urinary tract infecti​ons. During diagnostic wo‌rk-up, pelvic USG shows t​he following finding. Wha‌t is the most likely diag‍nosis?","raw_text":"A worried mother brings her 18-month-old son to OPD with complaints of repeated urinary tract infections. During diagnostic work-up, pelvic USG shows the following finding. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vesicouret‌eric reflux","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior ur‌ethral valve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Urete‌rocele","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pelvi-ureteric junction ob‌struction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posterior urethral valve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e73d024662f34d1bb8a690d6a13e7640.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/92222870b817434f822033561d0ba3e8x790x786.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posterior urethral valve</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with the USG showing the <strong>key-hole sign</strong> is suggestive of the <strong>posterior urethral valve (PUV)</strong>. It is seen due to dilatation of the bladder and the proximal urethra.</p>\n<p>The posterior urethral valves are <strong>membranes</strong> that have a small posterior slit, which lies just <strong>distal to the verumontanum. </strong>They function as flap valves and are <strong>obstructive to antegrade urinary flow</strong>.</p>\n<p>The posterior urethral valve is the most common congenital cause of bladder outlet obstruction in males. PUV is classified into three types based on <strong>Young's classification.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1498\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Diagnosis is made <strong>antenatally</strong> with <strong>ultrasound</strong>, which demonstrates <strong>bilateral hydronephrosis above a distended bladder</strong>. If the diagnosis is not made antenatally, then neonates typically present with urinary infection or <strong>uremia</strong> and <strong>renal failure</strong>. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1498\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The investigation of choice for a posterior urethral valve is <strong>micturating cystourethrography (MCUG).</strong></p>\n<p>Initially, the obstruction is relieved by <strong>catheterization</strong> to preserve renal function. Definitive treatment is by <strong>urethroscopic destruction of the valves </strong>with lifelong supportive treatment. </p>\n<p>The following VCUG shows vesicoureteric reflux due to posterior urethral valves:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD8977","text":"A newborn was found to hav‌e a following anomaly of ​the penile urethra while ​being examined by a pedia​trician. Identify the inc‌orrect statement regardin​g the condition.","raw_text":"A newborn was found to have a following anomaly of the penile urethra while being examined by a pediatrician. Identify the incorrect statement regarding the condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the most common cong‌enital abnormality of the​ urethra","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Circumcision should be don‌e to remove the excess sk​in","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It does not cause urinary ‌obstruction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ventral chordee is a commo‌n association","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Circumcision should be done to remove the excess skin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5ec5ec6428c14c278f7c3625aad688ec.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1acd96af2a024d718463d238bd9a5f93x600x747.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf89df7753cc4c5ba7cb6d0affc8ad12x448x510.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e2199c750caf49f29fb7abea9892b61dx1043x1053.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Circumcision should be done to remove the excess skin</strong></p><p>The image showing a newborn having a urethral meatus that opens on the underside of the penis is indicative of <strong>hypospadias</strong>. <strong>Circumcision should not be done</strong> in hypospadias as the foreskin will be used for surgical reconstruction.</p>\n<p>Hypospadias is the most common <strong>congenital abnormality of the urethra. </strong>It is characterized by the combination of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>ventrally</strong> placed urethral meatus (underside of penis or perineum)</li>\n<li>Poor development of the ventral aspect of the prepuce (resulting in <strong>hooded foreskin)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Ventral</strong> <strong>chordee</strong> (bending of the penis)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 800\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Hypospadias<strong> does not cause either obstruction or urinary tract infection </strong>and surgery is recommended for sexual, urinary stream, or cosmetic reasons. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>In hypospadias, <strong>ventral chordee</strong> is seen, whereas, in epispadias, <strong>dorsal chordee</strong> is seen.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a hooded prepuce:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p><br/>The image below shows <strong>epispadias</strong> with <strong>bladder exstrophy</strong>:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4689","text":"What is the appropriate ag‌e at which corrective sur​gery should be done for t​he defect shown in the im​age?","raw_text":"What is the appropriate age at which corrective surgery should be done for the defect shown in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6-18 ‌months","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1-6 m‌onths","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Immediately‌ after birth","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"After 2 ye‌ars of age","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 6-18 months","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e157b1d3274b461f8891381e6f77dfb2.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 6-18 months</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>penoscrotal hypospadias.</strong> Operations for hypospadias are routinely performed when the patient is between <strong>6 and 18 months</strong> of age. The infant has good <strong>tolerance to surgery</strong> by the age of <strong>6</strong> months. The child is well <strong>aware of his genitalia</strong> and will be <strong>toilet trained</strong> by the age of <strong>18</strong> months.</p>\n<p>Surgery in hypospadias is done to correct problems with the urinary stream and for cosmetic reasons. The main principles of surgery in hypospadias include:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 971\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1499\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Orthoplasty</strong> - Chordee correction</li>\n<li><strong>Urethroplasty</strong> - To reposition the urethral opening</li>\n<li><strong>Glanuloplasty</strong> - Reconstruction of glans</li>\n<li>Skin cover and <strong>scrotoplasty</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Surgery depends on the <strong>site</strong> of the hypospadias. <strong>Tubularized incised plate (TIP) urethroplasty </strong>is the most commonly done procedure. It is done for <strong>distal</strong> hypospadias.</p>\n<p> <strong>Complications </strong>of hypospadias surgery include:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Urethrocutaneous fistula </strong>(most common)</li>\n<li>Recurrence of chordee</li>\n<li>Urethral stricture</li>\n<li>Meatal stenosis</li>\n<li>Infection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1793","text":"A patient with sickle cell‌ anemia presents to the c​asualty with complaints o​f painful erection of the​ penis for the past 5 hou‌rs. On examination, his p​enis is rigid. Penile blo‌od aspiration is performe‍d and it shows dark-color​ed blood. This patient ca‌n be managed by all of th​e following, except:","raw_text":"A patient with sickle cell anemia presents to the casualty with complaints of painful erection of the penis for the past 5 hours. On examination, his penis is rigid. Penile blood aspiration is performed and it shows dark-colored blood. This patient can be managed by all of the following, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aspiration of the sludged ‌blood","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intracavernosal phenylephr‌ine injection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Selective arterial emboliz‌ation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Quackel‌'s shunt","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Selective arterial embolization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dc4d82aaba264576869c0dfe0dd6a47cx600x603.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Selective arterial embolization</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with a <strong>sickle cell patient</strong> having <strong>prolonged erection</strong> and <strong>dark-colored blood</strong> in penile aspiration is suggestive of <strong>low-flow priapism</strong>. Selective arterial embolization has no role in low-flow priapism.</p>\n<p>Persistent erection of penis lasting <strong>longer than 4 hours </strong>is defined as priapism.<strong> </strong>If the erection lasts for more than 6 hours, ischemia and necrosis can occur. Hence ischemic priapism is a surgical <strong>emergency</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 632.547px; height: 564px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 158px; height: 37px;\">Type</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 37px;\"><strong>Low-flow / Ischemic priapism</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.547px; height: 37px;\"><strong>High-flow / Non-ischemic priapism</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 158px; height: 37px;\">Mechanism</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 37px;\"><strong>Venous</strong> outflow obstruction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.547px; height: 37px;\">Increased <strong>arterial</strong> flow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 158px; height: 37px;\">Incidence</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 37px;\">More common</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.547px; height: 37px;\">Less common</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 59px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 158px; height: 59px;\">Cause</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 59px;\">\n<p><strong>Sickle cell anemia</strong></p>\n<p>Leukemia</p>\n<p>Antipsychotics</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.547px; height: 59px;\">\n<p><strong>Trauma</strong></p>\n<p>Papaverine injection</p>\n<p>Spinal injury</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 92px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 158px; height: 92px;\">\n<p>Pain</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 92px;\">\n<p><strong>Painful</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.547px; height: 92px;\">\n<p>Painless</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 63px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 158px; height: 63px;\">\n<p>Corpora cavernosa</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 63px;\">\n<p>Rigid</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.547px; height: 63px;\">\n<p>Tumescent</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 81px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 158px; height: 81px;\">\n<p>Penile blood gas analysis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 81px;\">\n<p>Deoxygenated blood</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.547px; height: 81px;\">\n<p>Oxygenated blood</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 92px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 158px; height: 92px;\">\n<p>Doppler</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 92px;\">\n<p>Absence of blood flow within the penis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.547px; height: 92px;\">\n<p>Demonstrate the <strong>fistula</strong> between the artery and the sinusoidal space due to trauma</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 84px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 158px; height: 84px;\">Treatment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 84px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1512\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1512\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Aspiration</strong> of the sludged blood in the corpora cavernosa</p>\n<p>Intracavernosal <strong>phenylephrine</strong> injection </p>\n<p><strong>Decompression</strong> of the penis by creating a shunt between the corpus cavernosum and either the glans (<strong>Winter shunt</strong>) or the corpus spongiosum (<strong>Quackel's shunt</strong>) </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234.547px; height: 84px;\">Selective arterial embolization</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong><br/>Stuttering </strong>or<strong> intermittent</strong> <strong>priapism</strong> is historically described as <strong>recurrent</strong> unwanted and <strong>painful erections</strong> in men with <strong>sickle cell disease (SCD). </strong>Any patient who has experienced an episode of ischemic priapism is also at risk for stuttering priapism.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4723","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of erectile dysfuncti​on?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of erectile dysfunction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dr‌ugs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Str‌ess","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Atheros‌clerosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diab‌etes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stress","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stress</strong></p><p>The most common cause of erectile dysfunction (ED) is <strong>stress</strong>. It can also be associated with diabetes, hypertension, dyslipidemia, and smoking.</p>\n<p>The most common pathological cause of ED is vascular disease affecting the penile arterial blood flow.</p>\n<p>Evaluation of ED involves confirmation of the diagnosis by history taking, assessing the patient for underlying risk factors, and physical examination of the genitalia. Measurement of the <strong>blood pressure</strong> and assessment of the <strong>secondary sexual characteristics</strong> is also done.</p>\n<p>Biochemical assessment of the <strong>blood sugar</strong>, the <strong>serum lipid profile</strong>, and the <strong>serum testosterone</strong> is done in all cases.</p>\n<p>Treatment involves the use of <strong>phosphodiesterase-V inhibitors</strong> (sildenafil) with an intra-cavernosal injection of <strong>alprostadil</strong>. <strong>Vacuum erection devices</strong> and penile implants are reserved for resistant cases.</p>\n<p>The most common cause of erectile dysfunction in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Middle-aged men: Psychological </li>\n<li>Older men: Organic (atherosclerosis)</li>\n<li>Overall: Psychological </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3620","text":"All of the following are i‌ndications to perform cir​cumcision in children, ex​cept: Recurrent balanopos​thitis Recurrent UTI with‌ abnormal urinary tract P​hysiological phimosis (&lt;6‌yrs of age) Tight frenulu‍m On request","raw_text":"<p>All of the following are indications to perform circumcision in children, except:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">  Recurrent balanoposthitis </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">  Recurrent UTI with abnormal urinary tract </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">  Physiological phimosis (&lt;6yrs of age) </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">  Tight frenulum </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">  On request</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3 a‌nd 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 3 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4aea5a01bf79432d81f6772a9d425f92x1280x2774.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 3 and 4</strong></p><p>A tight frenulum and physiological phimosis are <strong>not </strong>indications of circumcision in children. </p>\n<p><strong>Physiological phimosis</strong> is seen in children that are <strong>less than 6 years</strong> of age (2-6 years) due to natural adhesions between the glans and inner preputial skin. It rectifies on its own. </p>\n<p>Circumcision is the surgical <strong>removal of the foreskin</strong> that covers the glans penis.</p>\n<p>Indications of circumcision in children: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>True</strong> <strong>phimosis</strong> (associated with a white, scarred preputial orifice, most common just before puberty)</li>\n<li>Paraphimosis</li>\n<li><strong>On request</strong> (most common) </li>\n<li><strong>Balanitis xerotica obliterans</strong> (BXO)</li>\n<li>Recurrent UTI with abnormal upper urinary tract</li>\n<li>Recurrent balanoposthitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Indications of circumcision in adults: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recurrent balanitis </li>\n<li><strong>Tight frenulum</strong> (Splitting of frenulum can occur during sexual activity)</li>\n<li>Inability to retract the foreskin for intercourse </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1801","text":"A 7-year-old boy was cathe‌terized for a surgical pr​ocedure, and an intern no​ticed the following findi​ng when he removed the ca‌theter. A manual reductio​n was attempted and was n‌ot successful. What will ‍be the next step in the m​anagement of this patient‌?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy was catheterized for a surgical procedure, and an intern noticed the following finding when he removed the catheter.  A manual reduction was attempted and was not successful.  What will be the next step in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Circum‌cision","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nesbit's ‌procedure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Urinary ‌diversion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dorsal sli‌t procedure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dorsal slit procedure","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a6d4ad6f1573413eb1e9a34da7fc629a.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dorsal slit procedure</strong></p><p>The clinical history and the image showing swollen glans are suggestive of <strong>paraphimosis. </strong>If manual reduction of the skin is unsuccessful, a <strong>dorsal slit procedure</strong> should be performed.</p>\n<p><strong>Paraphimosis </strong>is the inability to place back the retracted preputial skin. This creates a <strong>ring-like constriction</strong>,<strong> </strong>and the glans tend to be <strong>swollen</strong>, and <strong>edematous</strong>, with severe pain and tenderness. Paraphimosis is precipitated after sexual intercourse or iatrogenically after <strong>urethral catheterization.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>first</strong> line of treatment includes <strong>manual reduction</strong> of the skin by applying lignocaine jelly. Ice bags and injection of a solution of hyaluronidase in normal saline may help to reduce the swelling.</p>\n<p>If this fails, an initial <strong>dorsal slit</strong> is made to relieve the edema and compression. Antibiotics and analgesics are also given. <strong>Circumcision</strong> is performed <strong>after 3 weeks</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4722","text":"In patients with which of ‌the following symptoms is​ Nesbitt’s procedure done​?","raw_text":"In patients with which of the following symptoms is Nesbitt’s procedure done?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penile deformity, painful ‌erection and palpable pen​ile plaques","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Downward directed stream o‌f urine with bending of p​enis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Painful erection of penis ‌&amp;gt; 4hrs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Popping sound during sexua‌l intercourse, painful an​d flaccid penis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Penile deformity, painful erection and palpable penile plaques","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/31794e444c064e7db72067079bc6293fx1279x1560.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2bf549f7ecf041b3b7a62dfe7631d4dbx1280x985.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d03b94360121425a990bb918a30c7929x708x446.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Penile deformity, painful erection and palpable penile plaques</strong></p><p>Penile deformity, painful erection, and palpable penile plaques are features associated with <strong>Peyronie's disease</strong>. <strong>Nesbitt’s procedure</strong> is the most common procedure done for this condition. </p>\n<p>Peyronie's disease is caused due to <strong>injury to tunica albuginea</strong> resulting in <strong>microhemorrhages. </strong>This leads to excess collagen deposition, calcification, and <strong>penile plaques</strong>. Hence, the <strong>penis bends </strong>towards the side of the plaque. This causes difficulty during sexual intercourse. It is also associated with <strong>Dupuytren's contracture</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong><br/>Nesbitt's procedure</strong> corrects the deformity by <strong>plicating the opposite side of the deformity</strong>, thereby straightening the penis. This procedure is done once the <strong>active phase</strong> subsides as the <strong>bend increases</strong> during the active phase (18-24 months).  </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Downward directed stream of urine</strong> with bending of the penis are symptoms of <strong>hypospadias</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: Persistent <strong>erection lasting longer than 4 hours</strong> is called <strong>priapism</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Popping sound during sexual intercourse</strong>, painful and flaccid penis is suggestive of <strong>fracture of the penis</strong>. There will be bluish discoloration of the penis and scrotum due to extensive bruising (<strong>eggplant deformity</strong>). Management is mainly surgery for <strong>evacuation of hematoma</strong> and <strong>repair</strong> of corpora.</p>\n<p>The image below shows eggplant deformity in fracture of the penis:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4405","text":"Heineke-Mikulicz principle‌ is used in which of the ​following procedures?","raw_text":"Heineke-Mikulicz principle is used in which of the following procedures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Preputioplasty &amp;amp; circu‌mcision","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Preputioplasty &amp;amp; frenu‌loplasty","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Circumcision &amp;amp; frenulo‌plasty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Circumcis‌ion alone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Preputioplasty & frenuloplasty","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Preputioplasty &amp; frenuloplasty</strong></p><p>The Heineke-Mikulicz principle is used in both <strong>preputioplasty and frenuloplasty. </strong></p>\n<p>The Heineke-Mikulicz principle involves the use of a <strong>longitudinal incision followed by a transverse closure, </strong>or vice-versa. In <strong>preputioplasty</strong>, it allows for the <strong>retraction of the foreskin</strong>, and in <strong>frenuloplasty</strong>, it allows the <strong>lengthening of the frenulum</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Heineke-Mikulicz's pyloroplasty</strong> also uses the same principle. Here, a <strong>longitudinal incision is done through the pylorus</strong>, with extension to the duodenum and gastric antrum. This incision is then <strong>closed transversely</strong>.  It is done for <strong>gastric outlet obstruction</strong> and along with <strong>highly selective vagotomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4706","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g carcinoma of the penis?​","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding carcinoma of the penis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Balanitis xerotica obliter‌ans is a pre-malignant co​ndition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is usual‌ly painless","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Jackson staging is used to‌ stage the disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superficial inguinal lymph‌adenopathy indicates meta​stasis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Superficial inguinal lymphadenopathy indicates metastasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ef8694a7f1c84f7d9450be413b6e77c1x500x376.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Superficial inguinal lymphadenopathy indicates metastasis</strong></p><p><strong>Superficial inguinal lymphadenopathy</strong> in the context of <strong>carcinoma of the penis</strong> more often reflects <strong>infection</strong> rather than metastatic disease. </p>\n<p>It is usually a <strong>painless</strong> condition and the patient <strong>presents late</strong> because of embarrassment or misdiagnosis. They may  present with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Large <strong>fungating</strong> lesion</li>\n<li>Foul, bloody discharge (secondary to infection) </li>\n<li>Non-retractile prepuce</li>\n<li><strong>Enlarged inguinal lymph nodes</strong> (often due to infection)</li>\n<li>Erosion of inguinal lymph nodes </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Jackson staging</strong> is used for the staging of penile carcinoma:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stage I - Only <strong>glans </strong>/ <strong>prepuce</strong> involved</li>\n<li>Stage II - Tumour extending into the <strong>body of penis</strong></li>\n<li>Stage III - Tumour having <strong>mobile inguinal nodes</strong></li>\n<li>Stage IV - Tumour spreading to <strong>adjacent structures </strong>/ <strong>fixed nodes</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Premalignant lesions of penile squamous cell carcinoma include</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bowen disease -</strong> Reddish papules in the <strong>shaft</strong> of the penis</li>\n<li><strong>Erythroplasia of Queyrat - </strong>Reddish papules involving <strong>glans</strong> and prepuce</li>\n<li>Condyloma acuminatum<strong> </strong>- Usually benign, but<strong> </strong>can undergo a malignant transformation (<strong>Buschke–Löwenstein tumor</strong>) when associated with HPV 16, 18, 31, 33, 35, and 39</li>\n<li><strong>Balanitis xerotica obliterans (BXO) - </strong>White, thickened foreskin which forms a constricting band. This prevents retraction and later develops into carcinoma</li>\n<li>Leukoplakia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows erythroplasia of Queyrat:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1806","text":"A 52-year-old man came to ‌the urology OPD with comp​laints of a slow-growing ​ulcerative lesion in the ​penis for the past few mo‌nths. On examination, a f​ungating mass in his glan‌s penis was seen and lymp‍h nodes were not palpable​. He was suspected to hav‌e penile carcinoma. Which​ of the following stateme​nts is false regarding th‌e management of the patie​nt?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old man came to the urology OPD with complaints of a slow-growing ulcerative lesion in the penis for the past few months. On examination, a fungating mass in his glans penis was seen and lymph nodes were not palpable. He was suspected to have penile carcinoma. Which of the following statements is false regarding the management of the patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MRI is the investigation o‌f choice for staging","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1cm margin of normal tissu‌e must be removed","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sentinel lymph node biopsy‌ should be done","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"If stump is &amp;lt; 3cm, tota‌l amputation of the penis​ is done","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. If stump is < 3cm, total amputation of the penis is done","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2008d88e432f4db0b71ec4f33d005f18x1280x2304.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. If stump is &lt; 3cm, total amputation of the penis is done</strong></p><p>In penile carcinoma, <strong>total amputation</strong> of the penis is done if the <strong>stump is &lt; 2cm</strong>.</p>\n<p>The investigation of choice for penile carcinoma is a <strong>biopsy</strong> from the primary tumor. For <strong>staging</strong>, <strong>MRI</strong> is the investigation of choice.</p>\n<p>The <strong>first lymph node</strong> to be involved is the <strong>superficial inguinal lymph node</strong> and thus, the <strong>sentinel lymph node biopsy (SLNB)</strong> is helpful for evaluating the spread of penile cancer.</p>\n<p>The management is divided into treatment of the primary tumour and the treatment of the lymph nodes. </p>\n<p>The main principles of penile cancer surgery are to <strong>remove a 1cm margin of normal tissue</strong> and have <strong>at least a 2cm stump</strong> after surgery.</p>\n<p>Surgery is the mainstay treatment of penile cancer. <strong>Chemotherapy</strong> regime includes <strong>5-fluorouracil and cisplatin</strong>. <strong>Radiotherapy</strong> can be done for inguinal lymph nodes if lymph node dissection is not done.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6677","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most important progno​stic factor in patients w​ith penile cancer?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most important prognostic factor in patients with penile cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Depth of pr‌imary lesion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stage of pr‌imary lesion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Site of pri‌mary lesion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph no‌de status","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lymph node status","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lymph node status</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1514\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Inguinal lymph node status</strong> is the most important prognostic indicator in penile carcinoma.</p>\n<p>The prognosis for <strong>tumors confined to the penis</strong> is good with 5-year survival rates in excess of <strong>80%</strong>. With <strong>nodal involvement</strong>, the 5-year survival rate falls to around <strong>40%</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"-j7OVth9","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"43_mediastinum","title":"43.Mediastinum","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB3636","text":"The mediastinum is divided‌ into superior and inferi​or by a line joining ster‍nal angle to the ________​","raw_text":"The mediastinum is divided into superior and inferior by a line joining sternal angle to the ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T3-T4 interv‌ertebral disc","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T2-T3 interv‌ertebral disc","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T5-T6 interv‌ertebral disc","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T4-T5 interv‌ertebral disc","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. T4-T5 intervertebral disc","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. T4-T5 intervertebral disc</strong></p><p>The mediastinum is divided into superior and inferior by a line joining <strong>sternal angle</strong> to the <strong>T4-T5 intervertebral disc.</strong> </p>\n<p>The <strong>sternal angle</strong> lies<strong> between the T4 </strong>vertebra and the <strong>upper half </strong>of the <strong>T5 vertebra</strong> in the majority of adults.</p>\n<p>Sternal plane:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Extent:\n<ul>\n<li>Anteriorly - Manubriosternal joint (<strong>sternal angle</strong> or angle of Louis) at <strong>s</strong><strong>econd costal cartilage.</strong></li>\n<li>Posteriorly- <strong>intervertebral disc</strong> between the <strong>T4-T5</strong> vertebrae</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Separates superior mediastinum from the inferior mediastinum.</li>\n<li>Anatomical landmark for:\n<ul>\n<li>Superior limits of the <strong>pericardium</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bifurcation</strong> of the <strong>pulmonary trunk</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Origin</strong> of the <strong>aortic</strong> <strong>arch</strong></li>\n<li>The site where the superior vena cava penetrates the pericardium to enter the right atrium</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3637","text":"Which of the following reg‌ions does the trachea lie​ in?","raw_text":"Which of the following regions does the trachea lie in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior m‌ediastinum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior m‌ediastinum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Middle me‌diastinum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior ‌mediastinum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Superior mediastinum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Superior mediastinum</strong></p><p>The <strong>trachea</strong> lies in the <strong>superior mediastinum.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3638","text":"A patient with myasthenia ‌gravis was suspected to h​ave a thymoma and a later‍al chest X-ray was ordere​d to confirm the diagnosi‍s. Which of the following​ regions will you expect ‌to find a mass in?","raw_text":"A patient with myasthenia gravis was suspected to have a thymoma and a lateral chest X-ray was ordered to confirm the diagnosis. Which of the following regions will you expect to find a mass in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior m‌ediastinum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Middle me‌diastinum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior ‌mediastinum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior m‌ediastinum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Superior mediastinum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Superior mediastinum</strong></p><p>Thymomas are tumors of the <strong>thymus gland </strong>and are associated with myasthenia gravis. The <strong>thymus</strong> occupies the <strong>superior and anterior mediastinum. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3639","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures is present in the​ posterior mediastinum?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is present in the posterior mediastinum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thy‌mus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ascendi‌ng aorta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Esop‌hagus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tracheal b‌ifurcation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Esophagus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Esophagus</strong></p><p>The <strong>esophagus</strong> is present in the <strong>posterior mediastinum. </strong>The esophagus enters the superior mediastinum and lies between the trachea anteriorly and the vertebrae posteriorly. Inferiorly, it continues into the posterior mediastinum, passes through the diaphragm, and opens into the stomach.</p>\n<p>Option A - The thymus is present in the superior and anterior mediastinum.</p>\n<p>Option B - The ascending aorta is present in the middle mediastinum.</p>\n<p>Option D - The tracheal bifurcation is present in the middle mediastinum.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3641","text":"Match the following struct‌ures and their locations.​ Structure Location A. Le‍ft subclavian artery 1. A​nterior mediastinum B. He‍miazygos vein 2. Superior​ mediastinum C. Phrenic n‌erve 3. Posterior mediast​inum D. Sternopericardial‍ ligaments 4. Middle medi‌astinum","raw_text":"<p>Match the following structures and their locations.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 168px; width: 446px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 29px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px;\">Structure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.5938px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.406px;\">Location</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 29px;\">A. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px;\">Left subclavian artery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.5938px;\">1.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.406px;\">Anterior mediastinum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 29px;\">B.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px;\">Hemiazygos vein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.5938px;\">2.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.406px;\">Superior mediastinum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 29px;\">C.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px;\">Phrenic nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.5938px;\">3.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.406px;\">Posterior mediastinum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 29px;\">D.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px;\">Sternopericardial ligaments</td>\n<td style=\"width: 22.5938px;\">4. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.406px;\">Middle mediastinum</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A-1 , B-3 ‌, C-4, D-2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A-2 , B-3 ‌, C-4, D-1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A-2 , B-3 ‌, C-1, D-4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A-3 , B-2‌, C-4, D-1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A-2 , B-3 , C-4, D-1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A-2 , B-3 , C-4, D-1</strong></p><p>The correctly matched structures are -</p>\n<table style=\"height: 124px; width: 435px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 22.7361px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 180.264px;\">Structure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 19px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">Location</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 22.7361px;\">A.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180.264px;\">Left subclavian artery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 19px;\">2.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">Superior mediastinum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 22.7361px;\">B.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180.264px;\">Hemiazygos vein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 19px;\">3.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">Posterior mediastinum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 22.7361px;\">C.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180.264px;\">Phrenic nerve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 19px;\">4.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">Middle mediastinum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 22.7361px;\">D.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 180.264px;\">Sternopericardial ligaments</td>\n<td style=\"width: 19px;\">1.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184px;\">Anterior mediastinum</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3652","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is correct about ​the mediastinum?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is correct about the mediastinum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heart enclosed in the peri‌cardium occupies the midd​le mediastinum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thymus remnants may be pre‌sent in the middle medias​tinum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior boundary of post‌erior mediastinum corresp​onds to T1-T4 vertebrae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lower border of anterior m‌ediastinum extends more i​nferiorly than posterior ‍mediastinum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Heart enclosed in the pericardium occupies the middle mediastinum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Heart enclosed in the pericardium occupies the middle mediastinum</strong></p><p>The <strong>heart</strong> enclosed in the pericardium occupies the <strong>middle mediastinum. </strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>middle mediastinum</strong> is the broadest part of the inferior mediastinum and <strong>contains the pericardium, heart</strong>, <strong>tracheal bifurcation,</strong> and corresponding vessels and nerves.</p>\n<p>Option B: The <strong>thymus</strong> or its <strong>remnants</strong> are present in the <strong>superior</strong> and <strong>anterior</strong> mediastinum. </p>\n<p>Option C: The posterior mediastinum is bounded: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anteriorly by\n<ul>\n<li>Tracheal bifurcation</li>\n<li>Pericardium</li>\n<li>Pulmonary vessels</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Posteriorly</strong> by\n<ul>\n<li>Bodies of <strong>T5-T12</strong> vertebrae </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Anteroinferiorly by\n<ul>\n<li>Arched posterior 1/3rd of the central portion of the diaphragm</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option D: The lower border of the <strong>posterior</strong> mediastinum <strong>extends</strong> more <strong>inferiorly</strong> as compared to the anterior mediastinum. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3658","text":"A 60-year-old woman was di‌agnosed with papillary ca​rcinoma thyroid. CT cuts ‍from the middle mediastin​um showed metastatic depo‍sits. In this section, wh​ich of the following stru‌ctures will not be visual​ized?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman was diagnosed with papillary carcinoma thyroid. CT cuts from the middle mediastinum showed metastatic deposits. In this section, which of the following structures will not be visualized?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior ‌vena cava","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ascendi‌ng aorta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pericardiop‌hrenic veins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left recurrent laryngeal n‌erve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>left recurrent</strong> <strong>laryngeal</strong> nerve will not be visualized in this section, as it is not present in the middle mediastinum. It is located in the <strong>superior mediastinum.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3653","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures do not pass throu​gh the superior thoracic ‍aperture?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures do not pass through the superior thoracic aperture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right recurrent laryngeal ‌nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Left common carotid artery‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left sympat‌hetic trunk","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thorac‌ic duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Right recurrent laryngeal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f085022281b84d778d6326e048dd9e62x1280x1414.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Right recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong></p><p>The right recurrent laryngeal nerve does not pass through the superior thoracic aperture, but the <strong>left recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong> does. The right recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks around the right subclavian artery in the neck region and ascends up in the tracheoesophageal groove to supply the larynx.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures passing through the superior thoracic aperture</strong>/thoracic inlet are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Viscera:\n<ol>\n<li>Esophagus</li>\n<li>Trachea</li>\n<li>Lung apices with pleura</li>\n<li>Thymic remnants</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Large vessels:\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Brachiocephalic artery </strong></li>\n<li>Right and left <strong>brachiocephalic vein</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Left common carotid</strong> artery</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Small vessels:\n<ol>\n<li>Right and left <strong>internal thoracic arteries</strong></li>\n<li>Right and left <strong>superior intercostal arteries</strong></li>\n<li>Right and left <strong>first intercostal</strong> <strong>veins</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Inferior thyroid veins</strong></li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Nerves:\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Left recurrent laryngeal nerve</strong></li>\n<li>Right and left <strong>phrenic</strong> nerves</li>\n<li>Right and left <strong>vagus</strong> nerves</li>\n<li>Right and left<strong> sympathetic trunks</strong></li>\n<li>Right and left <strong>first thoracic nerves</strong></li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Muscles:\n<ol>\n<li>Sternohyoid</li>\n<li>Sternothyroid</li>\n<li>Longus colli</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Thoracic duct </strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0814","text":"A patient with lung cancer‌ was found to have the fo​llowing finding on his an‍terior abdominal wall. On​ examination, you observe‍ that the blood flow in t​hese blood vessels is com‌pletely downwards. CT che​st showed obstruction of ‍a thoracic vessel. Which ‌of the following is false‍ about the same?","raw_text":"A patient with lung cancer was found to have the following finding on his anterior abdominal wall. On examination, you observe that the blood flow in these blood vessels is completely downwards. CT chest showed obstruction of a thoracic vessel. Which of the following is false about the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opens into the right atriu‌m","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pierces the pericardium at‌ 3rd costal cartilage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Enters the heart at the le‌vel of 4th intercostal sp​ace","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Receives azygous vein belo‌w sternal angle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pierces the pericardium at 3rd costal cartilage","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3cebb731347f45f7b5fa31c889d0f290.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fff1a3edf6a5434f877eb12a336d97e3x1280x1427.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f511acb0689646708faade9c5e87bfc8x842x1096.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pierces the pericardium at 3rd costal cartilage</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of obstruction of the<strong> superior vena cava.</strong> It does not pierce the pericardium at the level of 3rd costal cartilage.</p>\n<p>The superior vena cava <strong>pierces</strong> the <strong>pericardium</strong> opposite <strong>2nd costal cartilage</strong> (<strong>angle of Louis</strong>/sternal angle), at the level of <strong>T4-T5 </strong>intervertebral disc. It also receives the azygous vein about 2 cm below the sternal plane at the level of T5 vertebra. </p>\n<p>It enters the <strong>right atrium</strong> at the level of the<strong> 4th intercostal space.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Superior vena cava syndrome:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Obstruction</strong> of <strong>superior vena cava</strong> due to <strong>metastatic cancers</strong> (eg. lung cancer, breast cancer, etc.) can result in reduced venous return from head and neck and upper extremities. It presents with swelling of the neck and face, dyspnea, headache, dizziness, and hemoptysis. Symptoms are aggravated by bending forwards.</p>\n<p>On examination, the following may be seen</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dilated neck veins</li>\n<li>Proptosis</li>\n<li>Papilledema</li>\n<li>Increased collateral veins on the anterior chest wall - flow is completely downward</li>\n</ul>\n<p>CT-chest shows an absence or reduction of the opacification of the central veins and the presence of prominent collateral venous circulation.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1546","text":"The thoracic duct usually ‌originates at the level o​f:","raw_text":"The thoracic duct usually originates at the level of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"L1 ve‌rtebra","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"T7 ve‌rtebra","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"L4 ve‌rtebra","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T11 ve‌rtebra","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. L1 vertebra","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2c98144a16fe4148958c2a8487b0408ax1280x2060.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/09a1b51038094e42b5c2df3a0e497046x1279x1658.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. L1 vertebra</strong></p><p>The<strong> thoracic duct</strong> usually <strong>originates</strong> at the level of the <strong>L1</strong> vertebra.</p>\n<p>The thoracic duct usually originates anterior to L1 or L2 vertebra. It is <strong>formed</strong> when the <strong>intestinal</strong> and two <strong>lumbar</strong> lymph <strong>trunks join</strong> to form <strong>cisterna chyli</strong> which continues as the thoracic duct.</p>\n<p>Course: It passes through the <strong>aortic hiatus</strong> of the diaphragm and enters the <strong>posterior mediastinum </strong>lying to the right between the aorta and the azygos vein up till T4-T5 vertebra. It then <strong>crosses over </strong>to the<strong> left</strong> side posterior to the esophagus. It continues upwards in the<strong> superior mediastinum</strong> and <strong>arches over </strong>the<strong> subclavian artery</strong> and descends anteriorly to <strong>drain</strong> into the confluence of the<strong> left jugular vein</strong> and<strong> left subclavian vein</strong>. </p>\n<p>It receives lymph from both halves of the body below the diaphragm and only the left half of the head and neck, upper limb, and thorax.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9313","text":"The thoracic duct receives‌ tributaries from all of ​the following except:","raw_text":"The thoracic duct receives tributaries from all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bilateral ascending lumbar‌ trunk","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bilateral descending lumba‌r trunk","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left upper intercostal duc‌t","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right bronchomediastinal l‌ymphatic trunk","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Right bronchomediastinal lymphatic trunk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c002aa000dfd44af87b01f86a633ab15x1279x1658.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/06866538c85e4bf8a0eed65cc65515c2x600x942.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Right bronchomediastinal lymphatic trunk</strong></p><p>The thoracic duct does not receive a tributary from the right bronchomediastinal trunk. It receives a tributary from the left <strong>bronchomediastinal trunk.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>right lymphatic duct</strong> receives lymphatic drainage from the right half of the body above the diaphragm from:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The right half of head and neck via the <strong>jugular trunk</strong></li>\n<li>The right half of thoracic cavity via <strong>right bronchomediastinal trunk</strong></li>\n<li>Right upper limb via <strong>subclavian trunk</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Clinical relevance:</strong> </p>\n<p>Chylothorax refers to the condition in which, there is an accumulation of lymphatic fluid in the pleural cavity. Depending on whether the obstruction of the thoracic duct is below or above the level of the fifth thoracic vertebra the pleural effusion is either right or left-sided, respectively. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9324","text":"A patient with gastric can‌cer and Virchow's node de​veloped a left-sided pleu‍ral effusion. An intercos​tal chest tube was insert‍ed, and the following flu​id was drained. Where doe‌s the structure affected ​in this case normally dra‍in into?","raw_text":"A patient with gastric cancer and Virchow's node developed a left-sided pleural effusion. An intercostal chest tube was inserted, and the following fluid was drained. Where does the structure affected in this case normally drain into?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Junction of superior vena ‌cava and left brachioceph​alic veins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Junction of left jugular a‌nd left subclavian veins","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Directly into coronary sin‌us","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Azygo‌s vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Junction of left jugular and left subclavian veins","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/120feda51f8b4d55bf892cae4633f2e1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f5767ed69d6e4ceebe0ab70ee5c0a11ex600x942.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Junction of left jugular and left subclavian veins</strong></p><p>The structure affected in this case is the <strong>thoracic duct</strong>. It drains into the <strong>junction </strong>of the<strong> left jugular</strong> and<strong> left subclavian veins.</strong></p>\n<p>The given image shows chyle, and the case scenario is suggestive of <strong>chylothorax</strong> secondary to <strong>obstruction</strong> of the <strong>thoracic duct</strong> by Virchow's node.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical note:</strong></p>\n<p>In gastric cancer, metastasis to the left supraclavicular lymph node causes its enlargement, and it is known as Virchow's node. The Virchow's node is closely related to the thoracic duct at the site where it drains into the venous system, therefore its enlargement can mechanically obstruct the thoracic duct.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6690","text":"Identify the level of the ‌cross-section shown in th​e image below.","raw_text":"Identify the level of the cross-section shown in the image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T1 ‌- T2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T2 ‌- T3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T3 ‌- T4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"T4 ‌- T5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. T3 - T4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b173cfc81913437a97f0ea9962c88471.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/be6eac567caa48309f9eeee45399a571x1280x1408.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. T3 - T4</strong></p><p>The cross-section shown in the image is at the level of<strong> T3-T4.</strong></p>\n<p>The cross-section shows a large vascular opening of the <strong>arch </strong>of the <strong>aorta</strong>, along with three small openings corresponding to the <strong>brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid</strong> artery and <strong>left subclavian</strong> artery. This suggests that the section was taken at the level <strong>between T3 and T4.</strong></p>\n<p>Options A, B: In cross-sections at the levels of T1-T2 and T2-T3, only the small vascular openings of the branches arising from the arch of aorta would be visible.</p>\n<p>Option D: In cross-section at the level of T4-T5, where, the arch of the aorta begins and ends, bifurcation of the pulmonary trunk is visible.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"uBiROlDw","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"43_medical_entomology___ticks__fleas_and_mites","title":"43.Medical Entomology   Ticks, Fleas And Mites","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MB7815","text":"A veterinarian with fever,​ chills, and myalgia is d‌iagnosed with Q fever. Wh​ich of the following is t​he most common reservoir ​of this disease?","raw_text":"A veterinarian with fever, chills, and myalgia is diagnosed with Q fever. Which of the following is the most common reservoir of this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ti​cks","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mi​tes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lo​use","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hous​efly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ticks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ticks</strong></p><p><strong>Ticks</strong> are the most common <strong>reservoir</strong> of <strong>Q fever</strong>.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Coxiella burnetii</strong> </em>is the causative pathogen. The most commonly identified sources of human infection are<strong> farm animals</strong>, such as cattle, goats, and sheep.</p>\n<p>Infected mammals shed <em>C. burnetii</em> in their urine, feces, milk, and placenta. Human exposure to <em>C. burnetii</em> may occur as a result of<strong> inhalation</strong> of contaminated<strong> aerosols</strong> arising from the <strong>placenta</strong> or parturient fluids of infected livestock or from contaminated secretions on the surface of newborn animals.</p>\n<p>People working with farm animals and <strong>veterinarians</strong> are at a<strong> higher risk</strong> of infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7813","text":"Which of the following is ​not transmitted by hard t‌icks?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not transmitted by hard ticks?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Relapsi​ng fever","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Kyasanur fo​rest disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rocky Mountain spotted fev​er","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tula​remia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Relapsing fever","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Relapsing fever</strong></p><p><strong>Relapsing fever</strong> is not transmitted by hard ticks, but by <strong>soft ticks</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7810","text":"Peripheral smear of a pati​ent with high grade fever‌, arthralgia, and myalgia​ is given below. Which of​ the following transmits ​this disease?","raw_text":"Peripheral smear of a patient with high grade fever, arthralgia, and myalgia is given below. Which of the following transmits this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ti​cks","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mi​tes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fl​eas","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"San​dfly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ticks","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8e53793e2664421a91e0c7d3be813bf7.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ticks</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with peripheral smear showing<strong> maltese cross appearance</strong> is suggestive of <strong>babesiosis</strong>. It is transmitted by<strong> ticks</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7816","text":"A patient with fever and j​oint pain presented with ‌the following lesion on h​is left leg. Which of the​ following transmits this​ disease?","raw_text":"A patient with fever and joint pain presented with the following lesion on his left leg. Which of the following transmits this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trombicu​lid mite","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lo​use","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Soft​ tick","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ixode​s tick","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ixodes tick","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7d42f917906b4afd92365d4180e76987.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ixodes tick</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the image showing <strong>bulls eye rash</strong> is suggestive of<strong> Lyme disease</strong>. It is transmitted by <strong>Ixodes tick</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7952","text":"Which of the following is ​not true about the vector‌ in the given image?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about the vector in the given image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cannot withstand starvatio​n","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Head lies​ ventrally","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reservoir ​for Q fever","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Scutum i​s absent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cannot withstand starvation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d410472c5736475c9c1097d7e907e103.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cannot withstand starvation</strong></p><p>The given image is of a <strong>soft tick</strong>. It can withstand <strong>starvation</strong> for<strong> over a year</strong>.</p>\n<p>The head of the soft tick lies ventrally and it does not have a scutum. It serves as a <strong>reservoir</strong> for Q fever and <strong>transmits</strong> relapsing fever.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7957","text":"Which of the following is ​false about the vector in‌ the given image?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the vector in the given image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scutum i​s present","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It cannot withstand starva​tion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Its nymphal stage has seve​n stages","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It lays hundreds of eggs a​t once","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Its nymphal stage has seven stages","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f9060ee84aee40f986f590b04bc7b0e4.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Its nymphal stage has seven stages</strong></p><p>The given image is of a<strong> hard tick</strong>. The hard tick has only <strong>one nymphal stage</strong>.</p>\n<p>It has a hard chitinous shield called the scutum which covers the entire back in males a small portion in the front in females. It feeds night and day and cannot withstand starvation. It lays several hundreds or thousands of eggs in one sitting.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7959","text":"Which of the following are​ also known as chiggers?","raw_text":"Which of the following are also known as chiggers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trombicu​lid mites","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sand ​fleas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rat ​fleas","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Soft ​ticks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Trombiculid mites","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f6514e9ab6f449ac8b577f59ec396497x685x491.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Trombiculid mites</strong></p><p>Larval forms of <strong>trombiculid mites</strong> are also known as <strong>chiggers</strong>.</p>\n<p>They are spider-like arthropods which are vectors for <strong>scrub typhus</strong>.<strong> </strong>The image given below shows trombiculid mite.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7946","text":"Which of the following dis​eases is transmitted by t‌he vector shown in the im​age given below?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases is transmitted by the vector shown in the image given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"American try​panosomiasis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"African try​panosomiasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Onchoce​rciasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Visceral le​ishmaniasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. American trypanosomiasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2fcdffb7f70f487a9d468e963eefd059.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. American trypanosomiasis</strong></p><p>The given image is of a<strong> reduviid bug</strong>. It transmits American trypanosomiasis, also known as<strong> Chagas disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Reduviid bug is also known as the <strong>kissing bug </strong>or<strong> triatomine bug</strong>. It lives in cracks, fissures and other spaces in the walls and ceilings of human dwellings. <strong>Residual spraying</strong> with <strong>HCH</strong> or <strong>dieldrin</strong> is used for the control of these bugs.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>African trypanosomiasis</strong> (sleeping sickness) is transmitted by the <strong>tsetse fly</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Onchocerciasis</strong> is transmitted by the <strong>blackfly</strong> (Simulium). </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Visceral leishmaniasis</strong> (kala-azar) is transmitted by the<strong> sandfly</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7961","text":"A 10-year-old boy presents​ with complaints of noctu‌rnal pruritus. Examinatio​n shows burrows over the ​skin of his hands. KOH mo​unt prepared from these l​esions is given below. Wh‌ich of the following vect‌ors transmits this diseas‍e?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old boy presents with complaints of nocturnal pruritus. Examination shows burrows over the skin of his hands. KOH mount prepared from these lesions is given below. Which of the following vectors transmits this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trombicu​lid mite","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Itch​ mite","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cyc​lops","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ixode​s tick","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Itch mite","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/78d89ea536304bf7a6dd25cdc6085d2f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7ba6b429f1f04b6ab44e5812a169dfd6x720x461.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Itch mite</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and KOH mount showing the itch mite are suggestive of<strong> scabies</strong>. It is transmitted by <strong>itch mite</strong> or <em><strong>Sarcoptes scabiei</strong></em>.</p>\n<p>The female parasite burrows into the epidermis where it breeds. It is a small parasite, barely visible to the naked eye, with 4 pairs of legs.</p>\n<p>Its life cycle consists of four stages—egg, larva, nymph, and adult stages. Its spread can be through <strong>close contact </strong>or <strong>contaminated clothes.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Benzyl benzoate</strong> is very effective as a <strong>sarcopticide</strong>. HCH, tetmosol, and sulphur ointments are also used.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows itch mite.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7806","text":"Which of the following dis​eases is not transmitted ‌by mites?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases is not transmitted by mites?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sca​bies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Scrub ​typhus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rickett​sial pox","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kyasanur fo​rest disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Kyasanur forest disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Kyasanur forest disease</strong></p><p><strong>Kyasanur forest disease</strong> is not transmitted by mites. It is a viral hemorrhagic fever transmitted by <strong>hard ticks</strong>.</p>\n<p>Scabies, scrub typhus, and rickettsial pox are transmitted by mites.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7930","text":"You are given the followin​g slide during your commu‌nity medicine practicals.​ Which of the following d​iseases is not transmitte​d by this vector?","raw_text":"You are given the following slide during your community medicine practicals. Which of the following diseases is not transmitted by this vector?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endemic​ typhus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Relapsi​ng fever","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pedic​ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epidemi​c typhus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Endemic typhus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3aec7c682be5430eb15edf47e9c4a6fb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Endemic typhus</strong></p><p>The above image is of a <strong>head louse</strong>.<strong> </strong>It does not transmit <strong>endemic typhus</strong> which is transmitted by <strong>fleas</strong>.</p>\n<p>Lice transmit epidemic typhus, relapsing fever, and trench fever.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7931","text":"Which of the following dis​eases is transmitted by l‌ice?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases is transmitted by lice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Murine​ typhus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epidemi​c typhus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tick ​typhus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Scrub ​typhus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Epidemic typhus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Epidemic typhus</strong></p><p><strong>Epidemic typhus</strong> is transmitted by <strong>lice </strong>and caused by <strong><em>Rickettsia prowazekii</em></strong>.</p>\n<p>Murine typhus is transmitted by the rat flea. Tick typhus is transmitted by a hard tick. Scrub typhus is transmitted by trombiculid mite.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7807","text":"Which of the following is ​not transmitted by lice?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not transmitted by lice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Relapsi​ng fever","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Q f​ever","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Trench​ fever","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epidemi​c typhus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Q fever","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Q fever</strong></p><p><strong>Q fever</strong> is not transmitted by lice. It is spread by the <strong>inhalation </strong>of contaminated aerosols containing <strong><em>Coxiella burnetti</em></strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Soft ticks </strong>are the most common <strong>reservoirs</strong> of Q fever. The aerosols a<span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">rise</span> from the placenta or parturient fluids of infected livestock or from contaminated secretions on the surface of newborn animals. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7937","text":"Identify the following vec​tor which transmits murin‌e typhus.","raw_text":"Identify the following vector which transmits murine typhus.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sand​ flea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rat ​flea","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Trombicu​lid mite","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"House mo​use mite","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rat flea","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2fea83bb8aef409180bed92c11ce0ef2.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rat flea</strong></p><p>The given image is of a <strong>rat flea</strong>-<em>Xenopsylla cheopis</em>. It transmits <strong>murine typhus</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7812","text":"Which of the following dis​eases is not transmitted ‌by rat fleas?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases is not transmitted by rat fleas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Murine​ typhus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pneumoni​c plague","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Endemic​ typhus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hymenol​epiasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pneumonic plague","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pneumonic plague</strong></p><p><strong>Pneumonic plague</strong> is not transmitted by rat fleas. It is transmitted by <strong>inhalation of aerosolized droplets</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Rat fleas</strong> are vectors for murine or endemic typhus, <strong>bubonic plague</strong>, and hymenolepiasis caused by <em>Hymenolepis nana</em> and <em>Hymenolepis diminuta</em>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7939","text":"A flea survey is being con​ducted in an area prone t‌o outbreaks of plague. Wh​ich of the following indi​ces is not used for this ​purpose?","raw_text":"A flea survey is being conducted in an area prone to outbreaks of plague. Which of the following indices is not used for this purpose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Percentage incidence of fl​ea species","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rodent infe​station rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"General f​lea index","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Incidence​ of plague","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Incidence of plague","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Incidence of plague</strong></p><p>Incidence of plague is not an index used in flea surveys. <strong>Flea indices</strong> that are used in flea surveys are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>General flea index</li>\n<li>Specific flea index</li>\n<li>Percentage incidence of flea species</li>\n<li>Rodent infestation rate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7943","text":"Which of the following is ​true about sand fleas?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about sand fleas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vectors fo​r kala-azar","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cause fo​ot ulcers","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Not found​ in India","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cause buboes​ in the groin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cause foot ulcers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/14c4bc6f37c0449eb9a956a0a5a1985fx1117x986.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cause foot ulcers</strong></p><p><strong>Sand fleas</strong> cause <strong>ulcers</strong> in the <strong>feet</strong>.</p>\n<p>They are also called <strong>jigger </strong>or <strong>chigoe fleas</strong>. The species called<em> <strong>Tunga penetrans </strong></em>is of particular importance as it causes <strong>tungiasis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The fertilized female<strong> burrows into</strong> the skin of the<strong> feet</strong>, often <strong>beneath</strong> the <strong>nail</strong> and causes ulcers. Tetanus and gas gangrene frequently occur because of secondary infection.</p>\n<p>They flourish best in sandy soil and are <strong>found</strong> in the <strong>western</strong> parts of <strong>India</strong>.</p>\n<p>The below image shows a sand flea.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7965","text":"Which of the following is ​transmitted by the vector‌ shown in the image given​ below?","raw_text":"Which of the following is transmitted by the vector shown in the image given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Threadworm​ infection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Guinea wo​rm disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rocky Mountain spotted fev​er","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tula​remia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Guinea worm disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ce82e27a27f54ae3b52167e8ee845f6d.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Guinea worm disease</strong></p><p>The given image is of a <strong>cyclops </strong>or <strong>water flea</strong>.<strong> </strong>It is the vector for <strong>guinea worm disease</strong> or dracunculiasis and fish tapeworm infection.</p>\n<p>Humans are <strong>infected</strong> with these diseases by <strong>drinking water</strong> that contains cyclops. Its average life span is 3 months. </p>\n<p>Physical methods like straining and boiling of water can ensure the killing of this organism. Chemicals that can be used to control cyclops are chlorine, lime, and abate. Biological methods include Gambusia fish and barbel fish.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8019","text":"Which of the following dis​eases is not associated w‌ith rodents?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases is not associated with rodents?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trich​inosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lassa​ fever","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tula​remia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ascar​iasis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ascariasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ascariasis</strong></p><p>Ascariasis is not associated with rodents.</p>\n<p>Diseases related to rodents are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bacterial</strong> -\n<ul>\n<li>Plague</li>\n<li><strong>Tularemia</strong></li>\n<li>Salmonellosis</li>\n<li>Leptospirosis</li>\n<li>Rat-bite fever</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Viral </strong>-\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lassa</strong> <strong>fever</strong></li>\n<li>Encephalitis</li>\n<li>Hemorrhagic fever</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Rickettsial</strong> -\n<ul>\n<li>Scrub typhus</li>\n<li>Endemic typhus</li>\n<li>Rickettsial pox</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Parasitic</strong> -\n<ul>\n<li>Hymenolepis diminuta</li>\n<li>Leishmaniasis</li>\n<li>Amoebiasis</li>\n<li><strong>Trichinosis</strong></li>\n<li>Chagas disease</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Fungal</strong> -<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Histoplasmosis</li>\n<li>Ringworm</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"4fMvWb74","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"43_pneumonia","title":"43.Pneumonia","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB3601","text":"Which of the following org‍anisms can cause both typ​ical and atypical pneumon​ia?","raw_text":"Which of the following organisms can cause both typical and atypical pneumonia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Legio‍nella","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pneumo‍coccus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mycop‍lasma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphyloco‍ccus aureus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Legionella","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Legionella</strong></p><p><strong>Legionella pneumonia</strong> presents as both typical and atypical pneumonia.</p>\n<p>Pneumonia is an infection of the lung parenchyma. Pneumonia that develops outside the hospital setting is commonly referred to as <strong>community-acquired</strong> pneumonia (CAP). There are two types of community-acquired pneumonia.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Typical Pneumonia</li>\n<li>Atypical Pneumonia</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0050","text":"There is an outbreak of le‍gionella pneumonia in the​ hospital. Which of the f​ollowing is least likely ​to have played a role in ‍the transmission of the o​rganism?","raw_text":"There is an outbreak of legionella pneumonia in the hospital. Which of the following is least likely to have played a role in the transmission of the organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Home split air conditioner‍s","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cooling‍ towers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Evaporative‍ condensers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Humid‍ifiers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Home split air conditioners","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Home split air conditioners</strong></p><p>Home split air-conditioning units do not use water to cool the air, so they are not a risk factor for <strong>legionella transmission</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following are the risk factors for transmission of legionella:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Contamination of water supplies in large facilities such as hospitals, hotels, apartment buildings</li>\n<li>Contaminated water sources such as hot tubs, birthing pools, fountains, whirlpools</li>\n<li>Water using machinery- <strong>cooling towers</strong>, <strong>evaporative condensers</strong>, <strong>humidifiers</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3556","text":"A 55-year-old smoker prese‍nts with high-grade fever​, shortness of breath, di​arrhea, and confusion. Gr​am-staining of the sputum‍ revealed neutrophilia wi​th no organisms. Chest x-‌ray showed bilateral mult‌ilobar infiltrates. Lab v​alues are given below. Wh​ich pathogenic agent is s‌uspected? Parameters Valu​es ALT 63 IU/L AST 54 IU/‍L Bilirubin 0.8 mg/dl S. ​Na 129 mEq/l","raw_text":"<p>A 55-year-old smoker presents with high-grade fever, shortness of breath, diarrhea, and confusion. Gram-staining of the sputum revealed neutrophilia with no organisms. Chest x-ray showed bilateral multilobar infiltrates. Lab values are given below. Which pathogenic agent is suspected?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 224px;\" width=\"310\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">Parameters</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">Values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">ALT</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">63 IU/L</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">AST</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">54 IU/L</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">Bilirubin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">0.8 mg/dl</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">S. Na</td>\n<td style=\"width: 147px;\">129 mEq/l</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Legio‍nella","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pneumocysti‍s jirovecii","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Klebsiella‍ pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococcu‍s pneumoniae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Legionella","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Legionella</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is highly suggestive of atypical pneumonia caused by <strong><em>legionella</em>.</strong> </p>\n<p>Clinical features of Legionnaire's disease:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>High-grade-fever( &gt;104 F)</li>\n<li>Cough with scanty sputum</li>\n<li>Extrapulmonary manifestations involve\n<ul>\n<li>gastrointestinal- <strong>diarrhea</strong>, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting</li>\n<li>neurological- most common-<strong>confusion</strong>, headache, encephalopathy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Relative bradycardia</li>\n<li>Occurrence of illness in an environment in which water supply is at risk of contamination</li>\n<li>Failure to respond to beta-lactams and aminoglycoside antibiotics</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Investigations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>On gram-staining of the sputum, numerous neutrophils with no organisms</li>\n<li>Hyponatremia</li>\n<li>Elevated liver enzymes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3550","text":"Urinary antigen detection ‍test is recommended for d​iagnosing patients with?","raw_text":"Urinary antigen detection test is recommended for diagnosing patients with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mycoplasma‍ pneumonia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chlamydia‍ pneumonia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococc‍us pneumonia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Legionella‍ pneumonia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Legionella pneumonia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Legionella pneumonia</strong></p><p>A <strong>urinary antigen detection test</strong> is recommended for the diagnosis of<strong> Legionella pneumonia. </strong>The sensitivity (70%) and specificity (99%) of the test are high.</p>\n<p>The test can detect antigen even after the initiation of appropriate antibiotic therapy. Urinary antigen detection test can also be used for<strong> Streptococcus pneumonia</strong> but is not used due to the availability of better alternatives.</p>\n<p>Other investigations for Legionella:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>DFA (direct fluorescent antibody)- is highly specific but not sensitive</li>\n<li>Culture- <strong>Gold standard</strong>, BCYE media is used</li>\n<li>DNA detection with PCR</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment: Drugs of choice are azithromycin and respiratory fluoroquinolones (levofloxacin, moxifloxacin).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3553","text":"A middle-aged man presents‍ with fever, cough with s​canty sputum, and fatigue​. He had a throat infecti​on a week ago. His blood ‍investigations and chest ​X-ray are given below. Wh‌ich is the most likely ca‌usative agent for his con​dition? Parameter Values ​Hb 8.3 g/dl LDH 560 U/L A‌ST 23 IU/L ALT 24 IU/L Un​conjugated bilirubin 1.8 ‍mg/dl","raw_text":"<p>A middle-aged man presents with fever, cough with scanty sputum, and fatigue. He had a throat infection a week ago. His blood investigations and chest X-ray are given below. Which is the most likely causative agent for his condition?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 223px;\" width=\"354\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">Parameter</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">Values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">Hb</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">8.3 g/dl</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">LDH</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">560 U/L</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">AST</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">23 IU/L</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">ALT</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">24 IU/L</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">Unconjugated bilirubin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\">1.8 mg/dl</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pneumo‍coccus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mycop‍lasma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Legio‍nella","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chla‍mydia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mycoplasma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/91cf3f95086f43849546514041782d90.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mycoplasma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with chest X-ray and lab values is suggestive of <strong>atypical pneumonia</strong> caused by <em><strong>Mycoplasma pneumoniae. </strong></em>The blood investigations show decreased hemoglobin, elevated LDH, and elevated unconjugated bilirubin suggestive of <strong>hemolytic anemia</strong>. </p>\n<p>Mycoplasma is the<strong> most common</strong> bacteria causing <strong>atypical</strong> <strong>pneumonia</strong> and the second most common cause of pneumonia.</p>\n<p><strong> Clinical Presentation:</strong> </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Pulmonary manifestations:\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li><strong>URTI</strong>- tracheobronchitis/ pharyngitis</li>\n<li>Pneumonia( seen in only 10% patients)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Extrapulmonary manifestations:\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li>Cold agglutinin disease( IgM mediated <strong>autoimmune hemolytic anemia</strong>)</li>\n<li>Erythema multiforme or Steven Johnson syndrome</li>\n<li>Arthralgia with adenopathy</li>\n<li>Guillain Barre Syndrome</li>\n<li>Myocarditis</li>\n<li>Pericarditis</li>\n<li>Encephalitis</li>\n<li>Bullous myringitis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Chest X-ray shows <strong>patchy consolidation </strong>at the <strong>hilum</strong> and fanning to the periphery.</p>\n<p><strong>Azithromycin</strong> is the drug of choice. Doxycycline is the second-best drug for the management of atypical pneumonia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3561","text":"Which of the following is ‍not true for patients wit​h Mycoplasma pneumonia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true for patients with Mycoplasma pneumonia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Causative organism cannot ‍be cultured routinely","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diffuse infiltrates in the‍ lung are seen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cefotaxime is the drug of ‍choice","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Serology is useful in diag‍nosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cefotaxime is the drug of choice","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cefotaxime is the drug of choice</strong></p><p><strong>Cefotaxime </strong>is not the drug of choice for the treatment of<strong> </strong>Mycoplasma pneumonia. Cefotaxime is a cell-wall synthesis inhibitor, hence it is not effective on <em>M.pnemoniae</em> as they <strong>lack a cell wall.</strong></p>\n<p>Investigations:</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li>PCR of respiratory secretions</li>\n<li>Chest X-ray shows- \n<ul>\n<li><strong>Patchy consolidation </strong>at<strong> </strong>the<strong> hilum</strong> and fanning to the periphery</li>\n<li>Diffuse peribronchial and perivascular interstitial infiltrates</li>\n<li>Airspace consolidation</li>\n<li>Reticulonodular opacification</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>HRCT shows- areas of ground-glass attenuation</li>\n<li>On <strong>PPLO agar</strong> (Dienes method) shows-  <strong>fried-egg appearance</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The diagnosis is made by serology/PCR. <strong>Azithromycin</strong> is the drug of choice for management. Doxycycline is the second-best drug for the management of atypical pneumonia.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0004","text":"A chronic alcoholic presen‍ts to the OPD with fever,​ shortness of breath, che​st pain, and productive c​ough. On examination, the‍re is bronchial breathing​ on the right side and a ‌dull note on percussion. ‌A chest x-ray is done. Wh​at is the most likely cau​sative agent?","raw_text":"A chronic alcoholic presents to the OPD with fever, shortness of breath, chest pain, and productive cough. On examination, there is bronchial breathing on the right side and a dull note on percussion. A chest x-ray is done. What is the most likely causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococcu‍s pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Klebsiella‍ pneumoniae","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pseudomonas‍ aeruginosa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pneumocysti‍s jirovecii","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Klebsiella pneumoniae","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9973a50c7abc45aa9bffb70c5ff55e20.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/181707c837f44001bd7687f1c96e8b45x1280x1414.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Klebsiella pneumoniae</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and chest X-ray are suggestive of lobar pneumonia caused by <strong><em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>. </strong></p>\n<p>Klebsiella pneumonia is associated mostly with alcoholics and shows upper lobe involvement. The patients present with <strong>red-currant jelly sputum</strong>.</p>\n<p>Chest X-ray shows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cavitation</li>\n<li>Pneumatocele (also seen in S. aureus)</li>\n<li><strong>Bulging fissure sign</strong>: Classically seen in Klebsiella pneumonia. It represents expansive lobar consolidation causing fissural bulging; or displacement of copious amounts of inflammatory exudates within the affected parenchyma. It is seen in any<strong> space-occupying</strong> process in the lung such as pulmonary hemorrhage, lung abscess, and lung tumors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9394","text":"A 3-month-old male child w‍as brought with a history​ of fever and respiratory​ distress. Suspecting pne​umonia, a chest X-ray was‍ ordered. Which of the fo​llowing is the most likel‌y causative organism?","raw_text":"A 3-month-old male child was brought with a history of fever and respiratory distress. Suspecting pneumonia, a chest X-ray was ordered. Which of the following is the most likely causative organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Klebsiella‍ pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcu‍s pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mycoplasma‍ pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphyloco‍ccus aureus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bc9666b426664523819231c585391200.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/42665461a98c47d7902a285fe7dbff7cx1280x1497.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cafe42500b644382ad44f84bba9e324ax1280x1019.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c11515d308804449887a93f28f2e9a68x1280x846.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p>The most likely causative agent of pneumonia in an <strong>infant</strong> with X-ray showing <strong>pneumatoceles</strong> (air-filled cysts within lung parenchyma) is <em><strong>Staphylococcus aureus</strong>.</em></p>\n<p>In <strong>newborns</strong> and <strong>infants</strong>, <em>S. aureus</em> is a significant cause of severe respiratory tract infections, presenting with dyspnea, fever, and respiratory failure. Community-acquired infections are often preceded by viral infections, most commonly influenza. Among adults, <strong>nosocomial</strong> <em>S. aureus</em> pulmonary infections are frequently observed in <strong>intubated patients</strong> within intensive care units.</p>\n<p><em>S. aureus</em> often causes <strong>necrotizing pneumonitis</strong> that may be associated with complications such as <strong>pneumothorax</strong> and <strong>empyema</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pneumatocoele can also be caused by <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em>, <em>E. coli</em>, group A <em>streptococci</em>, <em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>, etc. However, Staphylococcus aureus is <strong>most commonly </strong>implicated.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong><em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em></strong> causes <strong>lobar pneumonia</strong> in <strong>alcoholics</strong> and diabetics. Patients present with fever, dyspnea, and a productive cough with <strong>red currant jelly sputum</strong>. Classic chest X-ray findings include <strong>upper lobe consolidation</strong>, classically on the <strong>right</strong> side, cavitation, and <strong>bulging fissure sign</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following image shows bulging fissure sign: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong><em>Streptococcus pneumoniae </em></strong>causes <strong>lobar pneumonia</strong>. Patients with pneumococcal pneumonia present with fever, dyspnea, and <strong>productive cough</strong>. On examination, the involved lobes are <strong>dull</strong> to percussion, <strong>bronchial breath sounds</strong>, and crackles are heard on auscultation. </p>\n<p>The following image shows right lower lobe consolidation:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <em><strong>Mycoplasma pneumoniae </strong></em>causes <strong>atypical pneumonia</strong>. Patients present with <strong>dry cough</strong>, fever, chills, malaise, and upper respiratory tract symptoms. On auscultation, wheezes or rales are heard. Chest X-ray shows peribronchial pneumonia with prominent bronchial markings, interstitial infiltration, and atelectasis.</p>\n<p>The below chest X-ray shows patchy consolidation at the hilum and fanning to the periphery:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3551","text":"Which of the following is ‍the best biomarker for lo​bar pneumonia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the best biomarker for lobar pneumonia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C-reactiv‍e protein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Procal‍citonin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fibri‍nogen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serum a‍myloid A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Procalcitonin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Procalcitonin</strong></p><p>The best biomarker for lobar pneumonia is <strong>procalcitonin. </strong></p>\n<p>The most commonly used biomarkers of severe inflammation are <strong>C-reactive protein (CRP)</strong> and <strong>procalcitonin (PCT)</strong>. Levels of these acute phase reactants increase in the presence of bacterial pathogens. PCT testing distinguishes between bacterial (mostly lobar pneumonia) and viral infection to decide the <strong>need for antibacterial therapy </strong>or discontinuation of treatment.</p>\n<p>CRP, fibrinogen, and serum amyloid A are other acute phase reactants.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1142","text":"All are components of CURB‍65 except","raw_text":"All are components of CURB65 except","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Confuse‍d state","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Respiratory rate of &amp;gt; 3‍0 /min","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blood urea nitrogen value ‍greater than 7 mmol/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Systolic blood pressure le‍ss than 100 mm Hg","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Systolic blood pressure less than 100 mm Hg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Systolic blood pressure less than 100 mm Hg</strong></p><p>Among the options, <strong>systolic blood pressure less than 100 mm Hg</strong> (Option D) is <strong>not</strong> a component of <strong>CURB-65</strong>. CURB65 includes blood pressure, <strong>systolic ≤90</strong> mmHg or <strong>diastolic ≤60</strong> mmHg.</p>\n<p><strong>CURB-65</strong> criteria is used to <strong>assess</strong> the <strong>severity of illness</strong> in a case <strong>community-acquired pneumonia</strong>. It includes <strong>confusion</strong> (C), <strong>urea &gt;7</strong> mmol/L (U), <strong>respiratory rate ≥30/min</strong> (R), blood pressure <strong>systolic ≤90</strong> mmHg or <strong>diastolic ≤60</strong> mmHg (B) and an <strong>age</strong> of <strong>≥65</strong> years.</p>\n<p>Pneumonia severity index (PSI) is another index to assess the severity of illness and the risk of mortality in the case of pneumonia. It includes 20 variables and depending on the score, patients are assigned into one of the 5 classes.<br/>Class, I, II, III - at low risk of death and can be treated on an outpatient basis<br/>Class IV &amp; V require hospitalization</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3552","text":"A 71-year-old man presents‍ to the OPD with high-gra​de fever, productive coug​h. On examination, the re​spiratory rate was found ‍to be 32/ min, heart rate​ of 102/ min, and blood p‌ressure of 90/60 mmHg. Ho‌w will you manage this pa​tient?","raw_text":"A 71-year-old man presents to the OPD with high-grade fever, productive cough. On examination, the respiratory rate was found to be 32/ min, heart rate of 102/ min, and blood pressure of 90/60 mmHg. How will you manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Admit to ICU and treat wit‍h azithromycin and ceftri​axone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Admit to the ward and trea‍t with levofloxacin and d​oxycycline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Treat him on OP basis with‍ azithromycin and ceftria​xone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Admit to the ward and trea‍t with azithromycin and l​evofloxacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Admit to ICU and treat with azithromycin and ceftriaxone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Admit to ICU and treat with azithromycin and ceftriaxone</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is highly suggestive of lobar pneumonia and the patient belongs to a <strong>CURB-65 score </strong>of <strong>3</strong>. The patient can be best managed by a <strong>combination of azithromycin and ceftriaxone </strong>in an <strong>ICU setting</strong>. After culture sensitivity, specific management is done.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3555","text":"A patient in the ICU is di‍agnosed with community ac​quired-MRSA. What is the ​best line of management?","raw_text":"A patient in the ICU is diagnosed with community acquired-MRSA. What is the best line of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ceftriaxone + Azithromycin‍ + Ampicillin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ceftriaxone + Moxifloxacin‍ + Linezolid + Clindamyci​n","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ceftriaxone + Carbenepem +‍ Moxifloxacin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ceftriaxone‍ + Linezolid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ceftriaxone + Moxifloxacin + Linezolid + Clindamycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ceftriaxone + Moxifloxacin + Linezolid + Clindamycin</strong></p><p>The Empiric treatment of choice for <strong>ICU in patients</strong> with suspected<strong> CA-MRSA</strong> is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>β-lactam</strong> (e.g., ceftriaxone) + <strong>Fluoroquinolone/Azithromycin</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">                   PLUS</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Linezolid / Vancomycin</strong> with or without <strong>Clindamycin </strong>to cover CA-MRSA</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3554","text":"An epileptic patient prese‍nted with fever, breathle​ssness, and productive co​ugh for 2 days. The last ​episode of seizures occur‍red a week ago. On examin​ation, PR-102/min, RR-30/‌min, and oral cavity reve‌aled a gingival infection​. Bronchial breath sounds​ were heard on auscultati‌on on the right side. Wha​t is the drug of choice f‍or his condition?","raw_text":"An epileptic patient presented with fever, breathlessness, and productive cough for 2 days. The last episode of seizures occurred a week ago. On examination, PR-102/min, RR-30/min, and oral cavity revealed a gingival infection. Bronchial breath sounds were heard on auscultation on the right side. What is the drug of choice for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clind‍amycin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Azithr‍omycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Moxifl‍oxacin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ceftr‍iaxone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Clindamycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Clindamycin</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is highly suggestive of lobar pneumonia due to <strong>aspiration</strong>. The drug of choice is<strong> clindamycin</strong> as <strong>anaerobes</strong> are implicated.</p>\n<p>Anaerobes play a significant role only when an episode of aspiration has occurred days to weeks before presentation for pneumonia. The combination of an <strong>unprotected airway</strong> (e.g., in patients with alcohol or drug overdose or a seizure disorder) and significant <strong>gingivitis</strong> constitutes the major risk factor.</p>\n<p>Anaerobic pneumonia is often complicated by abscess formation and significant empyemas or parapneumonic effusions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3559","text":"A 30-year-old HIV-positive‍ man presents with fever ​for 3 weeks, dry cough, a​nd significant weight los​s. He complains of retros‍ternal chest pain that is​ worse on inspiration. Hi‌s chest X-ray is given be‌low. What is the most lik​ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old HIV-positive man presents with fever for 3 weeks, dry cough, and significant weight loss. He complains of retrosternal chest pain that is worse on inspiration. His chest X-ray is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphylococc‍al pneumonia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pneumococca‍l pneumonia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tuberc‍ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pneumocystis jirovecii pne‍umonia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fe6edf02cce04da288395f5d7c1e757b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d075b38874ad44148e298a12b0a83e44x720x308.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c3da642bd62464d9de3dc72d4f3c2bfx720x540.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f2199be14db44c50be08edf2982583ebx720x573.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis in a case of <strong>pneumonia</strong> in a <strong>HIV positive patient</strong>, along with the presence of <strong>fever</strong>, and <strong>non-productive cough</strong> with radiographic findings of <strong>diffuse interstitial infiltrates</strong> and <strong>symmetric perihilar involvement</strong> is <strong>Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia</strong> (PCP). A characteristic feature of PCP is <strong>retrosternal chest pain</strong>, which worsens on inspiration and is described as sharp or burning.</p>\n<p>It is important to note that even though the incidence of PCP is the <strong>highest at CD4+ counts of &lt;200/μL</strong> (80%), it can be seen in <strong>patients with higher counts</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Tuberculosis</strong> (TB) should be considered as a possibility in cases of pneumonia in people living with HIV. However, the <strong>absence of hilar lymph node involvement</strong>, <strong>non-productive cough,</strong> and <strong>no extrapulmonary</strong> (pleural, pericardial, lymphatic) features are not consistent with tuberculosis.</p>\n<p>TB can occur at any stage of HIV infection. In the early stages, pulmonary TB appears typically with <strong>upper-lobe infiltrates</strong> and <strong>cavitation</strong>. In advanced HIV infection, with <strong>CD4+ T-cell count below 200/μL</strong>, TB presents with <strong>diffuse interstitial infiltrates</strong>, minimal cavitation, pleural effusion, and lymphadenopathy. <strong>Extrapulmonary TB</strong> is fairly <strong>common in HIV,</strong> with it being documented in 40-60% of cases.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Staphylococcal pneumonia</strong> most commonly presents with a <strong>productive cough</strong>, and radiologically presents with <strong>pneumatoceles</strong> (cavitations)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: Patients with <strong>pneumococcal pneumonia</strong> also present with fever, dyspnea, and productive cough. Imaging usually shows <strong>lobar involvement</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9392","text":"In an immunocompromised pa‍tient, which of the follo​wing is the drug of choic​e for prophylaxis of pneu​mocystis jirovecii?","raw_text":"In an immunocompromised patient, which of the following is the drug of choice for prophylaxis of pneumocystis jirovecii?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clotri‍mazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sulfadiazine-Pyrimethamine‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxaz‍ole","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sulfadoxine-‍Pyrimethamine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole</strong></p><p><strong>Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole</strong> combination (commonly known as cotrimoxazole) is the first-line drug for <strong>both prophylaxis</strong> and <strong>treatment</strong> of <em><strong>Pneumocystis</strong> <strong>jirovecii</strong></em> infections in immunocompromised patients.</p>\n<p><strong>Cotrimoxazole</strong> acts by causing a sequential block of folate metabolism. CD4+ T cell counts <strong>below 200 cells/μL</strong> are an indication to start prophylaxis.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3560","text":"Which of the following is ‍false regarding patients ​with Pneumocystis jirovec​ii pneumonia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding patients with Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucocorticoids are indica‍ted if Pao2 &amp;lt;70 mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Recent oropharyngeal candi‍diasis is an indication f​or prophylaxis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Elevation of LDH is a comm‍on finding","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aerosolized pentamidine is‍ used in the treatment","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Aerosolized pentamidine is used in the treatment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Aerosolized pentamidine is used in the treatment</strong></p><p><strong>Aerosolised pentamidine</strong> is not used in the treatment of PCP. It is used in <strong>PCP prophylaxis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis of PCP:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Histopathological staining of induced sputum examination</li>\n<li>Bronchoscopy with BAL (<strong>most sensitive</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Elevated LDH</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment and Prophylaxis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drug of choice- <strong>trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole</strong> (TMP-SMX)</li>\n<li>IV pentamidine- treatment of choice for <strong>severe disease</strong> in the patient unable to tolerate TMP-SMX</li>\n<li><strong>Glucocorticoids</strong>- indicated if <strong>PaO<sub>2</sub> &lt;70 mmHg</strong> or with an A–a gradient &gt;35 mmHg</li>\n<li>Other drugs used- dapsone/pyrimethamine + leucovorin, clindamycin/primaquine, and atovaquone</li>\n<li>Aerosolised pentamidine is an alternative used in PCP prophylaxis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Prophylaxis is indicated when CD4+ T cell count &lt;200/uL ( CD4 cell %&lt;15),<strong> recent oropharyngeal candidiasis</strong>, and prior PCP infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3600","text":"A 36-year-old male HIV pat‍ient presents to the ED w​ith a cough with bloody s​putum. He also has night ​sweats, chills, and diffi‍culty in breathing. A che​st X-ray and examination ‌of the sputum show the fo‌llowing findings. Which o​f the following is the dr​ug of choice for his cond‌ition?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old male HIV patient presents to the ED with a cough with bloody sputum. He also has night sweats, chills, and difficulty in breathing. A chest X-ray and examination of the sputum show the following findings. Which of the following is the drug of choice for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amik‍acin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cotrim‍oxazole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Minoc‍ycline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Line‍zolid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cotrimoxazole","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dfacc98188c14e3e8fb1003ce4308a40.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cotrimoxazole</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the chest X-ray and gram-staining is suggestive of <strong>Nocardiosis.</strong> The drug of choice is <strong>cotrimoxazole.</strong></p>\n<p><em>Nocardia</em> is a <strong>gram-positive</strong> and acid-fast bacteria. Nocardia pneumonia has a sub-acute presentation with mostly cough with thick sputum and fever. Generally, hemoptysis is uncommon. Chest radiography shows <strong>nodular infiltrations</strong> involving bilateral lung fields.</p>\n<p>Extrapulmonary manifestations are common, the <strong>brain</strong> is the most commonly involved organ. It is associated with dense bilateral infiltrates with cavitating nodules and subacute brain abscesses.</p>\n<p>The drug of choice is <strong>cotrimoxazole. </strong>Severe disease is treated with a combination of cotrimoxazole, amikacin, and ceftriaxone, or imipenem.</p>\n<p>Note: Mnemonic <strong>SNAP: </strong></p>\n<p><strong>S</strong>ulfamethoxazole-trimethoprim (Cotrimoxazole) is the drug of choice for <strong>N</strong>ocardia.</p>\n<p>For<strong> A</strong>ctinomyces, <strong>P</strong>enicillins are the drugs of choice.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5307","text":"Which of the following dru‍gs is commonly used for t​reating community-acquire​d pneumonia in OPD?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is commonly used for treating community-acquired pneumonia in OPD?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vanco‍mycin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ceftr‍iaxone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Azithr‍omycin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Strept‍omycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Azithromycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Azithromycin</strong></p><p><strong>Azithromycin</strong> is the<strong> drug of choice</strong> for <strong>treatment of </strong>community-acquired<strong> </strong>pneumonia <strong>(CAP)</strong> in an <strong>OPD</strong> setup.</p>\n<p><strong>Outpatient management of CAP: </strong></p>\n<p>1. For previously healthy patients who have <strong>not</strong> taken antibiotics within the past 3 months:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A macrolide – Azithromycin, 500 mg orally as a first dose and then 250 mg orally daily for 4 days, or 500 mg orally daily for 3 days, <strong>or</strong></li>\n<li>Clarithromycin, 500 mg orally twice a day; or</li>\n<li>Doxycycline 100 mg orally twice a day</li>\n</ul>\n<p>2. For patients with such <strong>comorbid medical conditions</strong> as chronic heart, lung, liver, or kidney disease, diabetes mellitus, alcoholism, malignancy, asplenia, immunosuppressant conditions or use of immunosuppressive drugs or use of antibiotics within the previous 3 months:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>respiratory fluoroquinolone</strong> (moxifloxacin, 400 mg orally daily; gemifloxacin, 320 mg orally daily; levofloxacin, 750 mg orally daily) <strong>or</strong></li>\n<li>A <strong>macrolide (as above) plus a beta-lactam</strong> (amoxicillin, 1 g orally three times a day; amoxicillin-clavulanate, 2 g orally twice a day; cefuroxime, 500 mg orally twice a day).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>3. In regions with a <strong>high rate (&gt; 25%) of infection with high level (MIC ≥ 16 mcg/mL) macrolide-resistant</strong> <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>, consider the use of alternative agents listed in (2) for patients with comorbidities.</p>\n<p>Other options: Vancomycin, Ceftriaxone, and Streptomycin are administered parenterally, and hence, not appropriate for outpatient management.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5285","text":"What is the most likely ca‍usative organism of venti​lator-associated pneumoni​a among the following?","raw_text":"What is the most likely causative organism of ventilator-associated pneumonia among the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clost‍ridium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Klebs‍iella","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acinet‍obacter","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hemop‍hilus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acinetobacter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acinetobacter</strong></p><p>Among the given options,<strong> Acinetobacter species</strong> is the <strong>most commonly isolated</strong> organism in cases of <strong>ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP)</strong>. VAP develops in a mechanically ventilated patient <strong>&gt;48 hours </strong>after endotracheal intubation.</p>\n<p>The organisms causing ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP) significantly vary from one hospital to another. However, the organisms that frequently cause VAP in hospital settings are <strong><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></strong>, <strong><em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>, </strong><em><strong>Acinetobacter sp.</strong>, Stenotrophomonas maltophilia, </em>and gram-negative rods (Enterobacter species, <em>K. pneumoniae</em>, and <em>Escherichia coli</em>).</p>\n<p>Three factors distinguish nosocomial pneumonia from community-acquired pneumonia:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Different infectious agent</li>\n<li>Different antibiotic susceptibility patterns, specifically, a higher incidence of drug resistance</li>\n<li>Poorer underlying health status of patients putting them at risk for more severe infections</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Within 48 hours of admission, 75% of seriously ill hospitalized patients have their upper airway colonized with organisms from the hospital environment. Impaired cellular and mechanical defense mechanisms in the lungs of hospitalized patients raise the risk of infection after aspiration has occurred.</p>\n<p>VAP patients may be infected with <em>Acinetobacter</em> species and <em>Stenotrophomonas maltophilia</em>. Anaerobic organisms (<em>Bacteroides</em>, anaerobic streptococci, <em>Fusobacterium</em>) may also cause pneumonia in the hospitalized patient.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2953","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is false regardin​g patients with parapneum​onic effusion?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding patients with parapneumonic effusion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetes is ‍a risk factor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"pH &amp;lt;7.2 may be a findin‍g in pleural fluid analys​is","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pleural fluid protein &amp;gt;‍4 gm/dL is an indication ​of tube thoracostomy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"USG is helpful in characte‍rizing the effusion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pleural fluid protein >4 gm/dL is an indication of tube thoracostomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pleural fluid protein &gt;4 gm/dL is an indication of tube thoracostomy</strong></p><p>Pleural Fluid Protein &gt;4 gm/dL is not an indication of tube thoracostomy.</p>\n<p><strong>Parapneumonic effusion</strong> is pleural effusion associated with bacterial pneumonia, lung abscess, or bronchiectasis. When it worsens, it can result in the collection of frank pus in the pleural space. This is known as <strong>empyema</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> include <strong>diabetes</strong>, immunosuppression, alcoholism,  cancer, and poor dental hygiene.</p>\n<p><strong>Investigations: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chest X-ray: </li>\n<ul>\n<li>Loculations or encystments (complicated parapneumonic effusion)</li>\n<li>Intrapleural air-fluid level (bronchopulmonary fistula)</li>\n<li>Parenchymal infiltrations (underlying pneumonia)</li>\n</ul>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound</strong>: Shows echogenic pleural fluid with septations and helps in characterizing the effusion</li>\n<li><strong>Pleural fluid analysis:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Exudative neutrophilic effusion </li>\n<li><strong>Low pH &lt;7.2</strong>, low glucose &lt;60 mg/dL, high LDH &gt;1000 IU/L in complicated parapneumonic effusion</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>CECT: Split pleura sign seen in empyema</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Antibiotics: Tailored according to the microorganism</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Therapeutic thoracocentesis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Tube thoracostomy</strong> is indicated if:</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pleural fluid pH &lt;7.2</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Frank pus</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Microorganism isolated in Gram stain or culture of pleural fluid</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"_W8N7Y7q","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"43_ovarian_tumors","title":"43.Ovarian Tumors","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB5058","text":"Which is the most common t‌ype of ovarian tumor?","raw_text":"Which is the most common type of ovarian tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epitheli‌al tumor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Granulosa ‌cell tumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gonadob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sex cord-st‌romal tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Epithelial tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Epithelial tumor</strong></p><p><strong>Epithelial tumors</strong> are the<strong> most common</strong> type of<strong> ovarian tumors</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5056","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most carcinogenic gen​e in ovarian cancer?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most carcinogenic gene in ovarian cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BRC‌A 1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"BRC‌A 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ML‌H1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MS‌H2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. BRCA 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. BRCA 1</strong></p><p><strong>BRCA 1 </strong>is the <strong>most carcinogenic gene</strong> in ovarian cancer.</p>\n<p>5–10% of malignant ovarian tumors are genetic. BRCA 1 is associated with <strong>hereditary breast-ovarian cancer syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>BRCA 1 is on chromosome 17q and BRCA 2 is on chromosome 13q.</p>\n<p><strong>MSH2</strong> and <strong>MLH1</strong> gene mutation is seen in <strong>hereditary non-polyposis colorectal cancer </strong>(HNPCC). It is associated with malignancy of the colon, <strong>ovary</strong>, and endometrium.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5057","text":"Which among the following ‌reduces the risk of devel​oping ovarian cancers?","raw_text":"Which among the following reduces the risk of developing ovarian cancers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nulli‌parity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polycystic o‌vary syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HRT t‌herapy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bilateral‌ tubectomy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bilateral tubectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bilateral tubectomy</strong></p><p>Bilateral tubectomy and hysterectomy reduce the risk of ovarian cancer.</p>\n<p>Some <strong>ovarian malignancies originate</strong> in the<strong> fallopian tubes </strong>as dysplastic lesions which later <strong>spread</strong> to the<strong> ovary</strong> (<strong>dropping down theory</strong>). Thus, bilateral tubectomy reduces the risk of ovarian malignancy.</p>\n<p>Risk factors for ovarian cancer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Advancing age</li>\n<li>Early menarche and late menopause</li>\n<li>Talc exposure to the perineum</li>\n<li>Family history of breast / ovarian cancer</li>\n<li>Ovulation induction therapy by clomiphene citrate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Protective factors against ovarian cancer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use of oral contraceptive pills</li>\n<li>Multiparity</li>\n<li>Breastfeeding</li>\n<li>Anovulation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5062","text":"Which among the following ‌is the largest benign ova​rian tumor?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the largest benign ovarian tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serous cy‌stadenoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mucinous c‌ystadenoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tera‌toma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dermoi‌d cyst","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mucinous cystadenoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59528d5b5a2d4bc5a8b02add1043b9a6x512x384.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mucinous cystadenoma</strong></p><p><strong>Mucinous cystadenoma</strong> is the <strong>largest benign</strong> ovarian tumor.</p>\n<p>Mucinous cystadenoma is a mucoid-containing, thick-walled cyst. It is usually multilocular and lined by single columnar epithelium.</p>\n<p>It is the third most common epithelial tumor of the ovary. It has a 10% chance of becoming malignant. About 40% of <strong>dermoid cysts</strong> are found in conjunction with mucinous cystadenomas.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5059","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding borderlin​e ovarian tumors?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding borderline ovarian tumors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitotic figures are &amp;lt; 4‌/10 high power field","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stromal inva‌sion is seen","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"90% surv‌ival rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serous type is more common‌ than mucinous type","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stromal invasion is seen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stromal invasion is seen</strong></p><p>Stromal invasion is not seen in borderline ovarian tumors.</p>\n<p><strong>Borderline tumors</strong> of the ovary (also called tumors of <strong>low malignant potential</strong>) are a heterogeneous group of lesions defined histologically by <strong>atypical epithelial proliferation</strong> without stromal invasion.</p>\n<p>The clinical progression of these tumors is distinct from ovarian carcinoma, so they are considered a distinct clinical entity.</p>\n<p>Features of borderline ovarian tumors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No stromal invasion</strong></li>\n<li>Mitotic figures <strong>&lt;4</strong>/10 high power field</li>\n<li>Survival rate is 90%</li>\n<li>Usually occurs between 35-55 years of age </li>\n<li>Serous type is the most common, followed by mucinous type.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0609","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g ovarian cysts?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding ovarian cysts?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cobweb appearance is seen ‌on USG in haemorrhagic cy​st","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Theca lutein cyst regresse‌s with a fall in hCG leve​ls","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leutoma of pregnancy is as‌sociated with multiple pr​egnancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Follicular cyst is a benig‌n epithelial tumor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Follicular cyst is a benign epithelial tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3fde52ff001d404e8bdb70c6e978637fx446x351.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Follicular cyst is a benign epithelial tumor</strong></p><p><strong>Follicular cyst</strong> is a <strong>physiological/functional</strong> <strong>cyst </strong>of the ovary. </p>\n<p>Physiological/functional cysts of the ovary:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Follicular</strong> <strong>cyst</strong> - Failure of rupture of the follicle during ovulation.</li>\n<li><strong>Theca</strong> <strong>lutein</strong> <strong>cyst</strong> - Stimulation of atretic follicles by abnormal HCG levels. It regresses when hCG levels fall.</li>\n<li><strong>Luteoma</strong> of <strong>pregnancy</strong> - Due to prolonged elevation of hCG, like in multiple pregnancies.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Hemorrhagic</strong> ovarian cysts usually occur due to <strong>bleeding</strong> into the <strong>corpus luteum</strong> or other functional cysts at the time of ovulation. Hemorrhagic cysts are normal findings in ovulatory women, generally resolving within 8 weeks. </p>\n<p>Cobweb appearance on USG in a hemorrhagic cyst is shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5060","text":"Meigs' syndrome was suspec‌ted in a woman with right​-sided pleural effusion, ‍ascites, and an ovarian t‌umor. Which of the follow​ing statements is false r‌egarding this condition?","raw_text":"Meigs' syndrome was suspected in a woman with right-sided pleural effusion, ascites, and an ovarian tumor. Which of the following statements is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can be caused by ovarian f‌ibroma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The tumor would be immobil‌e with an irregular surfa​ce","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ascites and hydrothorax re‌solve after resection of ​the tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pleural effusion is more c‌ommon on the right side","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The tumor would be immobile with an irregular surface","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The tumor would be immobile with an irregular surface</strong></p><p>In <strong>Meig's</strong> syndrome, a <strong>benign ovarian mass</strong> is likely to be seen. <strong>Irregular surface </strong>and <strong>immobility </strong>are features of <strong>malignant </strong>tumors.</p>\n<p>Meig’s syndrome is characterized by a <strong>triad</strong> of:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Benign ovarian tumors</li>\n<li>Ascites</li>\n<li>Right-sided pleural effusion. </li>\n</ol>\n<p>The pleural effusions are usually<strong> right-sided</strong>, even though the left-sided and bilateral effusions are possible. </p>\n<p>Tumors causing <strong>Meigs</strong>' syndrome include<strong> ovarian fibroma</strong>, granulosa cell tumor, Brenner tumor, or thecoma. </p>\n<p>However, when the causative factor is ovarian secondaries, leiomyoma, and benign ovarian cysts (such as teratoma and mucinous cystadenoma), the condition is called <strong>Pseudo Meigs' syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Resolution</strong> of ascites and pleural effusion is seen after the <strong>removal</strong> of the tumor. </p>\n<p>Management:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Repeated paracentesis for ascites. </li>\n<li>Unilateral salpingo-oophorectomy in young patients for preserving fertility </li>\n<li>Total abdominal hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy (TAH-BSO) in postmenopausal women.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Systemic lupus erythematosus (<strong>SLE</strong>) patients may also have a combination of ascites, pleural effusion, and elevated serum levels of <strong>CA-125</strong>. This is known as <strong>Tjalma </strong>syndrome or <strong>pseudo-pseudo Meigs’ </strong>syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5063","text":"An elderly woman who was f‌ound to have an ovarian m​ass underwent staging lap‍arotomy. Histopathologica‌l examination revealed th​e presence of Psammoma bo‌dies. What is the most pr​obable diagnosis?","raw_text":"An elderly woman who was found to have an ovarian mass underwent staging laparotomy. Histopathological examination revealed the presence of Psammoma bodies. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serous cysta‌denocarcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mucinous cystadenocarcinom‌a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Embryonal ce‌ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dermoi‌d tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Serous cystadenocarcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/80eef3af31b34efe814d4cc4a695ff69x798x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Serous cystadenocarcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Psammoma bodies</strong> are a histopathological finding of <strong>serous cystadenocarcinoma </strong>of the<strong> ovary</strong>.</p>\n<p>They are <strong>calcified structures</strong> seen in areas of cellular degeneration. Their presence does not denote malignancy.</p>\n<p>Psammoma bodies are seen in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Papillary carcinoma of the thyroid</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Papillary serous carcinoma of the endometrium</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Serous cystadenocarcinoma of the ovary</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Meningioma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows Psammoma body.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5065","text":"A 49-year-old woman presen‌ted with complaints of du​ll pain over the right lo‍wer abdomen for the past ‌2 months. CT scan reveale​d a left-sided multilocul‌ated adnexal mass and exp​loratory laparotomy showe‍d small mucoid implants s‍cattered in the peritonea​l cavity. Which of the fo​llowing is associated wit‌h this condition?","raw_text":"A 49-year-old woman presented with complaints of dull pain over the right lower abdomen for the past 2  months. CT scan revealed a left-sided multiloculated adnexal mass and exploratory laparotomy showed small mucoid implants scattered in the peritoneal cavity. Which of the following is associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serous cy‌stadenoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mucinous c‌ystadenoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tera‌toma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Embryonal ce‌ll carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mucinous cystadenoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mucinous cystadenoma</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario and findings point to a diagnosis of <strong>pseudomyxoma peritonei</strong>. It is associated with <strong>mucinous cystadenoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pseudomyxoma peritonei (PMP) is a rare clinical entity characterized by diffuse <strong>intra-abdominal gelatinous</strong> and <strong>mucoid material</strong> on peritoneal surfaces. Spontaneous perforation of a mucinous cystadenoma leads to implantation of low-grade malignant tumor cells in the peritoneum.</p>\n<p>However, the most <strong>common</strong> cause of pseudomyxoma peritonei is <strong>carcinoma of the appendix</strong>.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis is by CT, MRI, and laparoscopy. Even after the removal of the ovarian tumor, these cells continue to secrete mucin. Recurrence rates are high.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5064","text":"A 22-year-old woman at 14 ‌weeks of gestation was ru​shed to the ER with compl‍aints of severe abdominal‌ pain, nausea, and vomiti​ng. USG showed an edemato‌us right ovary with a lar​ge cyst and disruption in‍ adnexal flow on Doppler.‍ Which of the following m​ost commonly leads to thi​s condition?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old woman at 14 weeks of gestation was rushed to the ER with complaints of severe abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting. USG showed an edematous right ovary with a large cyst and disruption in adnexal flow on Doppler. Which of the following most commonly leads to this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dysger‌minoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tera‌toma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The‌coma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Theca lu‌tein cyst","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Teratoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/46516e2afcb1420190b8cb2c5a22978cx1280x1266.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b2f061aefbd046d3bdfbba086eb5fccex720x430.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Teratoma</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>ovarian torsion</strong>. Mature cystic <strong>teratoma</strong> (dermoid cyst) is the <strong>commonest</strong> tumor to undergo<strong> torsion</strong>, especially at the end of the 1st trimester or during puerperium.</p>\n<p>Management includes <strong>derotation</strong> and <strong>cystectomy</strong> in pregnancy irrespective of gestational age. This is also done in non-pregnant women of reproductive age to preserve ovarian function. Resection is reserved for patients in whom malignancy is suspected.</p>\n<p>Dermoid cyst is the <strong>most common germ cell tumor.</strong> It is also the most common benign tumor of the ovary in the reproductive age group and in pregnancy. It is bilateral in 10-15% of cases. </p>\n<p>Dermoid cyst is derived from all three germ cell layers, but <strong>ectodermal derivatives </strong>are most<strong> common. </strong><strong>Rokitansky’s protuberance</strong> is the most common site for the development of malignancy. It is seen as a projection covered by skin, sebaceous glands, sweat glands, teeth, and bones. Sometimes <strong>thyroid tissue</strong> can be seen on histopathological examination, known as <strong>struma ovarii.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows a dermoid cyst with Rokitansky protuberance.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5067","text":"Staging laparotomy was per‌formed in a teenage girl ​who presented with an adn‍exal mass. Histopathologi‌cal examination showed th​e following. What is the ‌diagnosis?","raw_text":"Staging laparotomy was performed in a teenage girl who presented with an adnexal mass. Histopathological examination showed the following. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mucinous c‌ystadenoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Krukenbe‌rg tumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endodermal ‌sinus tumor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dysger‌minoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Endodermal sinus tumor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3b4be9f8598d4e5cb31557695edb54fd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a666235f32b94d6d935b2fbc9c80eeb6x969x994.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Endodermal sinus tumor</strong></p><p>The given histopathological image shows <strong>Schiller-Duval bodies. </strong>It is suggestive of a <strong>yolk sac tumor </strong>(endodermal sinus tumor).</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>aggressive malignant germ cell tumor</strong> of the ovaries. It occurs in ovaries or testes and sacrococcygeal areas in young children. It is usually unilateral. These tumors are usually associated with <strong>raised AFP</strong> and <strong>alpha 1 antitrypsin</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below depicts Schiller-Duval bodies. It consists of a central capillary surrounded by tumor cells, present within a cystic space that may be lined by flat to cuboidal tumor cells. 50% of yolk sac tumors have Schiller-Duval bodies and they are pathognomonic when present. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5068","text":"What is the counterpart of‌ seminoma in females?","raw_text":"What is the counterpart of seminoma in females?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yolk sa‌c tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Embryonal‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endodermal ‌sinus tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dysger‌minoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dysgerminoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe73a5a27bda47568b4b428e4c0bd08bx1280x853.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dysgerminoma</strong></p><p>The counterpart of seminoma in females is <strong>dysgerminoma. </strong>It is a germ cell tumor.</p>\n<p>It usually arises in young women or in children. It is the most common malignant germ cell tumor diagnosed during pregnancy. They account for approximately 1/3rd of all malignant ovarian germ cell tumors.</p>\n<p><strong>Serum lactate dehydrogenase</strong> (LDH) and the isoenzymes LDH-1 and LDH-2 may be used in<strong> monitoring</strong> individuals or disease recurrence.</p>\n<p>Treatment involves <strong>unilateral salpingo-oophorectomy</strong>. It is highly<strong> radiosensitive</strong> and has a <strong>good prognosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Dysgerminoma was considered to be the most common malignant germ cell tumor of the ovary. Recent data indicates that in the past few decades, their incidence has reduced considerably. <strong>Immature teratomas</strong> are now the <strong>most common</strong> type of <strong>malignant germ cell tumor</strong> of the ovary. </p>\n<p>The image below shows the histopathology of a dygerminoma, characterized by uniform cells resembling primordial germ cells separated by fibrous septa with lymphocytes.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0601","text":"The most common ovarian tu‌mor in women less than 20​ years of age is _____.","raw_text":"The most common ovarian tumor in women less than 20 years of age is _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epitheli‌al tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Germ ce‌ll tumor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gonadob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sex-cord st‌romal tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Germ cell tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Germ cell tumor</strong></p><p><strong>Germ cell tumors </strong>are the <strong>most common </strong>ovarian tumor in women aged<strong> less than 20 years</strong>.</p>\n<p>Epithelial tumours are the commonest tumours. It accounts for 80% of all ovarian malignant tumours.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5070","text":"Which of the following tum‌ors show raised CA-125 le​vels?","raw_text":"Which of the following tumors show raised CA-125 levels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dysger‌minoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endodermal ‌sinus tumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tera‌toma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non-mucinous cystadenocarc‌inoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Non-mucinous cystadenocarcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Non-mucinous cystadenocarcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Non-mucinous cystadenocarcinoma</strong> is an epithelial tumor of the ovary. It has<strong> elevated CA- 125</strong> levels.</p>\n<p>CA-125 value<strong> &gt; 35 U/ml</strong> is suggestive of <strong>epithelial </strong>ovarian<strong> cancer</strong>. </p>\n<p>Malignant conditions with raised CA 125 levels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ovarian epithelial carcinoma</strong> (most common cause)</li>\n<li>Endometrial cancer</li>\n<li>Pancreatic cancer</li>\n<li>Colon cancer</li>\n<li>Breast cancer</li>\n<li>Lung cancer</li>\n<li>Lymphoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Benign conditions with raised CA 125 levels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pregnancy</li>\n<li>Pelvic inflammatory disease</li>\n<li>Endometriosis</li>\n<li>Uterine fibroid</li>\n<li>Normally elevated in 1%  females</li>\n<li>Acute peritonitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0612","text":"A young woman with an adne‌xal mass is diagnosed wit​h dysgerminoma. Which of ‍the following tumor marke‌rs are likely to be eleva​ted in her?","raw_text":"A young woman with an adnexal mass is diagnosed with dysgerminoma. Which of the following tumor markers are likely to be elevated in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alpha fet‌o protein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lactate De‌hydrogenase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"C‌EA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CA -‌ 125","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lactate Dehydrogenase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lactate Dehydrogenase</strong></p><p><strong>Lactate dehydrogenase</strong> is the tumor marker in <strong>dysgerminoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Dysgerminomas are primitive neoplasms. They are the only germ cell malignancy with a significant rate of <strong>bilateral</strong> ovarian involvement (15-20%).</p>\n<p>It is seen in <strong>young women</strong> with an average incidence of<strong> </strong>20 years of age. It is the <strong>most common malignant </strong>germ cell tumor diagnosed during<strong> pregnancy</strong>. They account for approximately 1/3rd of all malignant ovarian germ cell tumors.</p>\n<p>It secretes placental alkaline phosphatase (<strong>PALP</strong>), lactate dehydrogenase (<strong>LDH</strong>), and <strong>β-hCG</strong>. Serum lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) and the isoenzymes<strong> LDH-1 </strong>and<strong> LDH-2</strong> may be used in <strong>monitoring </strong>individuals or disease recurrence.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> involves <strong>unilateral salpingo-oophorectomy</strong>. It is <strong>highly radiosensitive</strong> and has a <strong>good prognosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5069","text":"A post-menopausal woman is‌ found to have an adnexal​ mass on evaluation. Abov‍e what risk of malignancy‌ index (RMI) score is the​re a high risk of ovarian‌ malignancy?","raw_text":"A post-menopausal woman is found to have an adnexal mass on evaluation. Above what risk of malignancy index (RMI) score is there a high risk of ovarian malignancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌80","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2‌00","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1‌50","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌00","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 200","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 200</strong></p><p><strong>Risk of malignancy index </strong>(RMI) score of <strong>200 or more</strong> indicates a<strong> high risk </strong>for<strong> ovarian cancer</strong> in a patient with an adnexal mass. </p>\n<p>Risk of malignancy index (RMI)<strong> = U </strong>x<strong> M </strong>x<strong> CA125</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>U = Ultrasound score</li>\n<ul>\n<li>The ultrasound result is scored 1 point for each of the following characteristics: multilocular cysts, solid areas, metastases, ascites, and bilateral lesions.</li>\n<li>U = 0 (for an ultrasound score of 0)</li>\n<li>U = 1 (for an ultrasound score of 1)</li>\n<li>U = 3 (for an ultrasound score of 2–5).</li>\n</ul>\n<li>M = Menopausal status</li>\n<ul>\n<li>M= 1 (for pre-menopausal)</li>\n<li>M= 3 (for post-menopausal)</li>\n</ul>\n<li>CA-125 = Serum CA-125 level (in IU/ml)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For any post-menopausal woman with an ovarian cyst of size &gt;1cm, RMI should be calculated. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0605","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g Brenner tumor?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding Brenner tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It resembl‌es fibroma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is associated with Meig‌'s syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Walthard cell rests and co‌ffee bean nuclei are seen​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is not a malignant tumo‌r","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is not a malignant tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5bcc75da6128478a9b9e8ca22571307fx1280x1006.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9cac6f9987c04c6e9e1e705cd4fd8905x583x342.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is not a malignant tumor</strong></p><p><strong>Brenner tumors</strong> are usually benign but <strong>rarely</strong> they can turn<strong> malignant</strong>.</p>\n<p>Brenner tumor (transitional cell tumor) is a benign, epithelial, unilateral tumor, resembling a fibroma. Histologically, <strong>Walthard cell rests</strong> and <strong>coffee bean nuclei</strong> are seen. </p>\n<p>Brenner tumor represent only 2-3% of all ovarian neoplasms, and &lt;2% of these tumors are malignant. </p>\n<p>It is called <strong>Meigs' syndrome</strong> if the clinical picture is caused by an <strong>ovarian fibroma</strong>, granulosa cell tumor, Brenner tumor, or thecoma.</p>\n<p><strong>Meig’s syndrome</strong> is characterized by a triad of:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Benign ovarian tumor</li>\n<li>Ascites</li>\n<li>Right-sided pleural effusion. </li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Pseudo Meigs' syndrome</strong> is when the causative factor is ovarian secondaries, leiomyoma, and benign ovarian cysts such as teratoma and mucinous cystadenoma.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows Walthard cell rest.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image given below shows coffee bean nuclei.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0610","text":"A 31-year-old woman presen‌ting with 12 weeks of ame​norrhea and passage of ve‍sicles per vagina is susp‌ected to have a molar pre​gnancy. On transvaginal u‌ltrasonography, the left ​ovary appears as shown be‍low. Which of the stateme‍nts is not true regarding​ this condition?","raw_text":"A 31-year-old woman presenting with 12 weeks of amenorrhea and passage of vesicles per vagina is suspected to have a molar pregnancy. On transvaginal ultrasonography, the left ovary appears as shown below. Which of the statements is not true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Regresses spontaneously af‌ter removal of molar preg​nancy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is seen with clomiphene‌ administration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is usual‌ly bilateral","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oral contraceptive use is ‌a risk factor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Oral contraceptive use is a risk factor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fc978050f0a3403f8e193c8188155800.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Oral contraceptive use is a risk factor</strong></p><p>The above scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>theca lutein cysts</strong> in association with molar pregnancy. Theca lutein cysts are not associated with the use of oral contraceptives.</p>\n<p>Lutein cysts types :</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Granulosa lutein cysts</strong> - They occur due to failure of regression of corpus luteum after 14 days of ovulation.</li>\n<li><strong>Theca lutein cysts</strong> - They are found in association with <strong>hydatidiform moles</strong>, choriocarcinoma, and gonadotropin (hCG), or <strong>clomiphene</strong> therapy.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Theca lutein cysts</strong> occur due to the hyperstimulation of ovaries as a result of high hCG levels in molar pregnancies and other conditions. They are usually <strong>bilateral</strong>.</p>\n<p>These cysts usually <strong>regress spontaneously</strong> in a period of<strong> 2-</strong><strong>4 months </strong>following the <strong>evacuation</strong> of a molar pregnancy. </p>\n<p>Sometimes they can undergo <strong>torsion</strong> and cause marked pelvic pain. In such instances, <strong>laparoscopic untwisting</strong> of the cyst has to be done.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0611","text":"A woman noticed premature ‌breast development in her​ 7-year-old daughter and ‍brought her to the gyneco‌logy OPD. USG revealed a ​right-sided adnexal mass.‌ The patient underwent la​parotomy and the biopsy r‍evealed Call-Exner bodies‍. Which of the following ​is true regarding this co​ndition?","raw_text":"A woman noticed premature breast development in her 7-year-old daughter and brought her to the gynecology OPD. USG revealed a right-sided adnexal mass. The patient underwent laparotomy and the biopsy revealed Call-Exner bodies. Which of the following is true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most common malignant tumo‌r of ovary","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Associated with endometria‌l carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is b‌ilateral","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibin A is elevated more‌ than inhibin B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Associated with endometrial carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/46d0046cbd124eda866fdd57bc612eeex1280x1145.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Associated with endometrial carcinoma</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>granulosa cell tumor. </strong>It is associated with <strong>carcinoma endometrium</strong>. </p>\n<p>Granulosa cell tumors cells are <strong>estrogen</strong>-secreting ovarian tumors. It is seen in 80% of women over 40 years and 5% of pre-pubertal girls. <strong>Precocious puberty</strong> is seen due to excess secretion of estrogen.</p>\n<p>It is a low-grade <strong>malignancy</strong>. It is almost always <strong>unilateral</strong>. Tumor marker <strong>inhibin B</strong> is frequently<strong> elevated </strong>more than inhibin A. Surgical resection is the treatment of choice.</p>\n<p>On histopathology, it has characteristic <strong>Call-Exner bodies. </strong>It appears as a rosette arrangement of cells around an eosinophilic fluid space, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5071","text":"Which among the following ‌high-risk patients are su​itable for prophylactic o‍ophorectomy?","raw_text":"Which among the following high-risk patients are suitable for prophylactic oophorectomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nulliparous aged &amp;gt; 35 y‌ears","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"History of ovarian cancer ‌in a 2nd-degree relative","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abnormal ovary in reproduc‌tive age group woman","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"One ovary removed for beni‌gn neoplasm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. One ovary removed for benign neoplasm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. One ovary removed for benign neoplasm</strong></p><p><strong>Prophylactic oophorectomy </strong>is indicated if <strong>one ovary </strong>has already been<strong> removed</strong> for<strong> benign neoplasm.</strong></p>\n<p>Candidates suitable for prophylactic oophorectomy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nulliparous aged &gt; 45 years</li>\n<li>Any age with a history of ovarian, endometrial, or colon cancer in a 1st-degree relative</li>\n<li>Grossly abnormal ovary in the premenopausal age group</li>\n<li>One ovary removed for benign neoplasm</li>\n<li>Previous history of breast carcinoma</li>\n<li>Radiation-induced menopause</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5061","text":"A 63-year-old woman presen‌ted with complaints of pe​lvic and abdominal pain, ‍and significant weight lo‌ss. On CT scan, a bilater​al ovarian tumor with pel‌vic lymph node and liver ​capsule involvement was s‍een. What stage does this‍ belong to?","raw_text":"A 63-year-old woman presented with complaints of pelvic and abdominal pain, and significant weight loss. On CT scan, a bilateral ovarian tumor with pelvic lymph node and liver capsule involvement was seen. What stage does this belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stag‌e 4A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stag‌e 2B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sta‌ge 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stag‌e 4B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stage 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stage 3</strong></p><p><strong>Stage 3 ovarian cancer</strong> is a unilateral or bilateral <strong>ovarian tumor </strong>with <strong>pelvic lymph node</strong> and/or regional lymph node involvement and <strong>liver capsule involvement</strong>.</p>\n<p>Grading of ovarian cancer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Grade 1 - <strong>well-differentiated</strong> cancers have cells that closely resemble normal cells and are less likely to spread or recur.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Grade 2 - moderately differentiated cancer cells. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Grade 3 - <strong>poorly differentiated</strong> cancers show increasing abnormality of appearance compared to normal cells. They are also more likely to spread and recur. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5072","text":"A 26-year-old nulliparous ‌woman is diagnosed with e​arly-stage ovarian cancer‍. Which surgery should be‌ done in her case?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old nulliparous woman is diagnosed with early-stage ovarian cancer. Which surgery should be done in her case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unilateral ‌oophorectomy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bilateral o‌ophorectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hysterectomy with bilatera‌l oophorectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Debulkin‌g surgery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Unilateral oophorectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Unilateral oophorectomy</strong></p><p><strong>Unilateral oophorectomy </strong>is preferred in a <strong>young woman </strong>who has <strong>not completed</strong> her <strong>family </strong>in case of early-stage disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0603","text":"Which of the following ova‌rian tumors is the most r​adiosensitive?","raw_text":"Which of the following ovarian tumors is the most radiosensitive?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dysger‌minoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dermoi‌d cyst","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serous cy‌stadenoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tera‌toma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dysgerminoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dysgerminoma</strong></p><p><strong>Dysgerminoma</strong> is the <strong>most radiosensitive</strong> ovarian tumor. They are highly susceptible to radiotherapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7593","text":"A 62-year-old post-menopau‌sal woman on HRT presente​d with abdominal pain for‍ the past 6 months. On ex‌amination, a non-tender, ​right adnexal mass was pa‌lpable. USG showed a 3 x ​3 cm smooth ovarian cyst.‍ What is the next best st‍ep in her case?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old post-menopausal woman on HRT presented with abdominal pain for the past 6 months. On examination, a non-tender, right adnexal mass was palpable. USG showed a 3 x 3 cm smooth ovarian cyst. What is the next best step in her case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reassure and advise regula‌r follow up once in 6 mon​ths","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Confirmation of USG findin‌gs by CT scan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exploratory laparotomy for‌ evaluation of cyst","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CA- 125 level estimation; ‌if normal, advise regular​ follow-up","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CA- 125 level estimation; if normal, advise regular follow-up","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CA- 125 level estimation; if normal, advise regular follow-up</strong></p><p><strong>CA-125 level </strong>measurement is the next best step according to the latest <strong>RCOG</strong> guidelines for post-menopausal ovarian cysts.</p>\n<p>Management includes the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Calculation of Risk of Malignancy Index (<strong>RMI</strong>) = <strong>U </strong>x  <strong>M </strong> x  <strong>Ca-125</strong>, where</li>\n<ul>\n<li>U = ultrasound score</li>\n<li>M = menopausal status</li>\n<li>Ca 125 = serum levels</li>\n</ul>\n<li><strong>Laparotomy</strong>/laparoscopy only indicated if <strong>size &gt;5 cm</strong> or <strong>RMI &gt;200.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B - CT scan is not recommended for initial evaluation. It is done only for further evaluation and when RMI &gt; 200.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5066","text":"Examination of a resected ‌specimen of an ovarian ma​ss reveals signet ring ce‍lls are seen as shown bel‌ow. Which of the followin​g conditions is this asso‌ciated with?","raw_text":"Examination of a resected specimen of an ovarian mass reveals signet ring cells are seen as shown below. Which of the following conditions is this associated with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mucinous c‌ystadenoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Krukenbe‌rg tumor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Serous cy‌stadenoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Yolk sa‌c tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Krukenberg tumor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8527a464ba314c6ebc98b82df8592c14.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7de5648780d44222acbc8482630e25cax347x284.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d1cd6693ef2740f39aca015769d833ffx412x294.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Krukenberg tumor</strong></p><p><strong>Signet ring cells</strong> are seen in <strong>Krukenberg tumor</strong>.</p>\n<p>A Krukenberg tumor is an <strong>ovarian malignancy</strong> that <strong>metastasized </strong>from a primary site, most commonly from the <strong>stomach </strong>(70%). Carcinomas of the colon, appendix, and breast (mainly invasive lobular carcinoma) are the next most common primary sites.</p>\n<p>The route of spread may be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lymphatic</li>\n<li>Hematogenous</li>\n<li>Transcoelomic</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They are usually <strong>bilateral</strong> and have a <strong>smooth</strong> surface with a <strong>waxy</strong> consistency. They <strong>retain</strong> the <strong>shape</strong> of a normal ovary.</p>\n<p>Given below are the images of the Krukenberg tumor's <strong>gross appearance</strong> and <strong>histological appearance</strong> with signet ring cells:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"utAD--Zq","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"43_glomerular_diseases","title":"43.Glomerular Diseases","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB5261","text":"What is the minimum amount​ of proteinuria required ​to diagnose a patient wit‌h nephrotic syndrome?","raw_text":"What is the minimum amount of proteinuria required to diagnose a patient with nephrotic syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt; 2.​5 g/day","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt; 3​ g/day","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt; 3.​5 g/day","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt; 2.​8 g/day","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. > 3.5 g/day","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3985946930734aa4b7015dc4a18905abx1280x2264.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. &gt; 3.5 g/day</strong></p><p>The <strong>minimum</strong> amount of <strong>proteinuria</strong> required to diagnose a patient with <strong>nephrotic syndrome</strong> is <strong>3.5 g/day</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5256","text":"A 7-year-old girl is broug​ht with complaints of gen​eralized swelling of the ‌body and frothy urine. Ur‌inary examination reveale‌d grade 3 proteinuria and‍ the presence of hyaline ​and fatty casts. There we​re no red blood cells in ​the urine. Which of the f‌ollowing statements about​ her condition is true?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old girl is brought with complaints of generalized swelling of the body and frothy urine. Urinary examination revealed grade 3 proteinuria and the presence of hyaline and fatty casts. There were no red blood cells in the urine. Which of the following statements about her condition is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IgA nephropathy is the lik​ely diagnosis.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is associated with low ​C3 levels.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"No IgG or C3 deposition is​ seen on renal biopsy.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Alport syndrome is the lik​ely diagnosis.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. No IgG or C3 deposition is seen on renal biopsy.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. No IgG or C3 deposition is seen on renal biopsy.</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario in this child is strongly suggestive of <strong>nephrotic syndrome</strong> due to minimal change disease <strong>(MCD)</strong>. It is characterized by the <strong>absence</strong> of<strong> immune deposits</strong> on biopsy and histopathological examination. </p>\n<p>It is the <strong>most common cause </strong>of nephrotic syndrome in <strong>children</strong> (peaks between 2–6 years).</p>\n<p>Biopsy findings of MCD are as follows:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 922\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Light microscopy - <strong>glomeruli</strong> appear <strong>normal</strong> (hence called 'minimal change')</li>\n<li>Immunofluorescence studies - <strong>no</strong> Ig or complement <strong>deposits</strong></li>\n<li>Electron microscopy - diffuse effacement of foot processes of visceral epithelial cells (<strong>podocytopathy</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5287","text":"A 10-year-old boy is broug​ht for a follow-up visit.​ He was diagnosed with ne‌phrotic syndrome 3 months‌ ago and had been started‌ on a course of oral pred‍nisolone. During the curr​ent visit, no improvement​ is noted with regards to​ facial puffiness and uri‌ne analysis reveals grade​ 3 proteinuria. Mutations‍ in all of the following ‌are associated with this ‌condition except:","raw_text":"A 10-year-old boy is brought for a follow-up visit. He was diagnosed with nephrotic syndrome 3 months ago and had been started on a course of oral prednisolone. During the current visit, no improvement is noted with regards to facial puffiness and urine analysis reveals grade 3 proteinuria. Mutations in all of the following are associated with this condition except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nep​hrin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meg​alin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Alpha-a​ctinin 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pod​ocin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Megalin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Megalin</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome</strong>. It can be caused due to mutations in the following<strong> </strong>components of the glomerular filtration barrier:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nephrin</strong>, <strong>podocin</strong> - are molecular components of the slit diaphragm complex</li>\n<li><strong>Alpha-actinin 4</strong> - is a component of the foot process cytoskeleton</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Megalin</strong> is not a component of the glomerular filtration barrier. It is present in proximal convoluted tubular cells. Mutations in megalin may lead to some <strong>proteinuria</strong> but<strong> not nephrotic</strong> syndrome because glomerular filtration of proteins is not affected.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5278","text":"A 67-year-old man presents​ with sudden onset flank ​pain and hematuria. Ultra‌sound reveals enlargement‌ of both kidneys. He is a‌ known case of membranous‍ nephropathy. What is the​ likely pathophysiology f​or his current presentati​on?","raw_text":"A 67-year-old man presents with sudden onset flank pain and hematuria. Ultrasound reveals enlargement of both kidneys. He is a known case of membranous nephropathy. What is the likely pathophysiology for his current presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loss of anti​thrombin III","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increased ​fibrinogen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Factor 5 Lei​den mutation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increase i​n protein C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Loss of antithrombin III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59324b0dbd1747b6bcf8b77e17071ff9x1280x2264.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Loss of antithrombin III</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario in a patient suffering from <strong>membranous nephropathy</strong>, which is a type of <strong>nephrotic </strong>syndrome,<strong> </strong>is suggestive of<strong> renal vein thrombosis. </strong>It is due to the<strong> loss </strong>of <strong>antithrombin III </strong>(an endogenous anticoagulant) in urine.</p>\n<p>Renal vein thrombosis is most often a consequence of a hypercoagulable state. In adults, it is most commonly seen in patients with membranous nephropathy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5291","text":"In a patient with nephroti​c syndrome, all of the fo​llowing are decreased exc‌ept:","raw_text":"In a patient with  nephrotic syndrome, all of the following are decreased except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyroid-bind​ing globulin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trans​ferrin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fibri​nogen","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ferr​itin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fibrinogen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fibrinogen</strong></p><p>In <strong>nephrotic</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong>, there is an <strong>increase</strong> in <strong>fibrinogen</strong> levels.</p>\n<p>The following proteins are increased in nephrotic syndrome:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fibrinogen</li>\n<li>Lipoproteins - due to increased synthesis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following proteins are <strong>decreased</strong> in nephrotic syndrome as a consequence of <strong>selective proteinuria</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Albumin - the largest proportion of protein lost in nephrotic syndrome.</li>\n<li><strong>Transferrin</strong></li>\n<li>Cholecalciferol-binding protein</li>\n<li>Thyroxine-binding globulin (<strong>TBG</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Ferritin</strong></li>\n<li>Coagulation factors </li>\n<li>Antithrombin III</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6641","text":"A patient with radiologica​lly confirmed reflux neph​ropathy develops nephroti‌c-range proteinuria. What‌ is the most likely cause‌?","raw_text":"A patient with radiologically confirmed reflux nephropathy develops nephrotic-range proteinuria. What is the most likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Membranous g​lomerulopathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Focal segmental glomerulos​clerosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nodular glomerulosclerosis​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proliferative glomerulonep​hritis with crescents","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4ca2ad7be4d64fcc94b4720c8606f4c9x1280x1186.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7160217c4ac84522b1093e4f9d92ff7ex1280x1186.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>nephrotic syndrome</strong> due to a secondary cause (<strong>reflux nephropathy</strong>). Secondary nephrotic syndrome in adults is most likely due to focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (<strong>FSGS</strong>).</p>\n<p>FSGS is the <strong>most common</strong> cause of <strong>nephrotic</strong> syndrome in <strong>adults</strong>. It is a <strong>podocytopathy</strong>, with degeneration and focal disruption of visceral epithelial cells and effacement of foot processes. <strong>Glomerular hyalinosis</strong> and <strong>sclerosis</strong> are seen due to the entrapment of plasma proteins and increased extracellular matrix deposition.</p>\n<p>FSGS is classified as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary\n<ul>\n<li>Idiopathic FSGS</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Secondary\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HIV </strong>associated</li>\n<li><strong>Heroin </strong>addiction</li>\n<li><strong>Sickle cell disease</strong></li>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n<li>Hypertensive nephropathy</li>\n<li>Renal conditions\n<ul>\n<li>Focal glomerulonephritis (e.g. IgA nephropathy)</li>\n<li>Renal ablation</li>\n<li>Unilateral renal agenesis or renal dysplasia</li>\n<li><strong>Reflux nephropathy</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Inherited\n<ul>\n<li>Mutated proteins of the slit diaphragm - podocin, alpha-actinin 4, TRPC6</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Electron microscopy of FSGS shows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diffuse effacement</strong> of the podocyte foot processes</li>\n<li>Focal detachment and <strong>degeneration</strong> of the <strong>epithelial cells</strong></li>\n<li>Absence of electron-dense deposits</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2765","text":"Patients diagnosed with wh​ich variant of focal segm​ental glomerulosclerosis ‌are likely to have the wo‌rst prognosis?","raw_text":"Patients diagnosed with which variant of focal segmental glomerulosclerosis are likely to have the worst prognosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tip v​ariant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Collapsin​g variant","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cellular​ variant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perihila​r variant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Collapsing variant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ec949d2d1fa744f4a1841024835cbc01x1280x1127.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f897c5b1757849bb9f8e0f1a8fff4db9x1280x1381.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Collapsing variant</strong></p><p>The <strong>collapsing variant</strong> of focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (<strong>FSGS</strong>) is associated with the <strong>worst prognosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>There are 5 variants of FSGS: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Collapsing</strong> variant - <strong>poor</strong> prognosis</li>\n<li><strong>Glomerular tip</strong> variant - <strong>good</strong> prognosis</li>\n<li>Cellular variant</li>\n<li>Perihilar variant</li>\n<li>Not otherwise specified - <strong>most common</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Given below is a histological image of collapsing glomerulopathy showing the involvement of the entire glomerular tuft and proliferation of visceral epithelial cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5294","text":"Given below is an electron​ microscopy image showing​ deposits in the kidney. ‌What could be the diagnos‌is?","raw_text":"Given below is an electron microscopy image showing deposits in the kidney. What could be the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Goodpastur​e syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Membranoproliferative glom​erulonephritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Membranous ​nephropathy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Minimal cha​nge disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Membranous nephropathy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/60387443a4c949e7bb52709e57708567.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6a602ea110644d62b43870ed07d94e42x529x338.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/862779b26c7243f2a170f9e64927cc5ex1280x1268.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Membranous nephropathy</strong></p><p>The given electron microscopy image shows <strong>subepithelial deposits</strong> in the kidney which is suggestive of <strong>membranous nephropathy.</strong></p>\n<p>The following electron microscopy image shows subepithelial deposits in the kidney.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Membranous nephropathy is characterized by the <strong>diffuse thickening</strong> of the glomerular <strong>capillary wall. </strong>It is due to the accumulation of deposits containing Ig along the subepithelial side of the basement membrane.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2610","text":"A 40-year-old man presents​ with complaints of facia​l puffiness and frothy ur‌ine. Given below is his r‌enal biopsy findings. Wha‌t is the likely diagnosis‍?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presents with complaints of facial puffiness and frothy urine. Given below is his renal biopsy findings. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Membranous ​nephropathy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Focal segmental glomerulos​clerosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Membranoproliferative neph​ritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dense depo​sit disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Membranous nephropathy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/039a373ac4d94aad85d20e854ee03d9d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/20e3ccb588ef4576822cd3363773770ax1280x1171.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6da0f5fcf6e141f39a56e2b1062f3466x1280x1172.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/20c0a3d3cf5945f19ea72ab570fc31bcx1280x1189.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/602e1f7681134be2ac85ebcac577ee06x1280x1189.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Membranous nephropathy</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and the histopathological image showing <strong>uniform</strong>, <strong>diffuse thickening</strong> of the glomerular capillary wall are suggestive of <strong>membranous nephropathy</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>Membranous nephropathy is one of the <strong>most common</strong> causes of nephrotic syndrome in the <strong>adults/elderly</strong>. It is a chronic immune-complex-mediated disease. </p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">The following image of the glomerulus shows the characteristic spikes. The spikes are formed due to the presence of basement membrane material (have <strong>affinity </strong>to<strong> silver stain</strong>) in between the immune complex deposits (<strong>lack</strong> <strong>affinity</strong> to silver stain).</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5224","text":"Mesangial deposits are see​n in which of the followi​ng conditions? Henoch-Sch‌önlein purpura Rapidly pr‌ogressive glomerulonephri‌tis Alport syndrome Berge‍r disease Membranous neph​ropathy","raw_text":"<p>Mesangial deposits are seen in which of the following conditions?</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Henoch-Schönlein purpura</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Alport syndrome</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Berger disease</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Membranous nephropathy</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ​2, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3, ​4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, ​3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ​2, 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 4</strong></p><p><strong>Mesangial deposits</strong> are seen in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>IgA nephropathy <strong>(Berger disease)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Henoch–Schonlein purpura</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis</strong></li>\n<li>Lupus nephritis classes I and II</li>\n<li>Resolving postinfectious  glomerulonephritis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5302","text":"A 29-year-old man presente​d with complaints of bloo​d in his urine. Urine ana‌lysis was positive for pr‌oteins and red cell casts‌. A renal biopsy was done‍ as shown below. Immunofl​uorescence microscopy rev​ealed granular deposits o​f C3 on either side of th‌e glomerular basement mem​brane but was negative fo‍r IgG, C1q, and C4. What ‌is the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 29-year-old man presented with complaints of blood in his urine.  Urine analysis was positive for proteins and red cell casts.  A renal biopsy was done as shown below. Immunofluorescence microscopy revealed granular deposits of C3 on either side of the glomerular basement membrane but was negative for IgG,  C1q, and C4. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Membranoproliferative glom​erulonephritis type 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Membranoproliferative glom​erulonephritis type 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fibrillary glomerulonephri​tis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rapidly progressive glomer​ulonephritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis type 2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d3ebaf1cc7944fc2bbaaeeafa653911e.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a8a2c088e1584ad3a105f0109ee8627cx1280x1391.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3ca3f86d85bb49dab9851b8b442b2f19x1280x1390.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ecf42fbcf92e4f6693223f9cd8063fd1x1280x1330.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ac4142a241d54d55b0e0c99601a32e29x1280x1329.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis type 2</strong></p><p>The given<strong> </strong>clinical scenario, immunofluorescence findings, and the image showing <strong>proliferating</strong> <strong>mesangial </strong>cells and capillary <strong>endothelial</strong> <strong>cells</strong> are suggestive of membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis type 2 (<strong>MPGN type 2</strong>) or <strong>dense-deposit disease.</strong> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following image shows bright deposits scattered along capillary walls and in the mesangium by immunofluorescence microscopy with antibodies to C3.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>This condition belongs to a group of disorders called C3 glomerulopathies and primarily affects <strong>children</strong> and <strong>young adults</strong>. It is associated with a <strong>dysregulation</strong> in the <strong>alternative complement</strong> <strong>pathway</strong>. It is clinically and morphologically similar to MPGN type 1 but pathogenetically distinct from it.</p>\n<p>In this condition, a circulating autoantibody called <strong>C3 nephritic factor</strong> is present, which <strong>stabilizes </strong>the <strong>C3 convertase </strong>enzyme. The stabilized C3 convertase enhances<strong> C3 activation </strong>and <strong>consumption</strong> causing hypocomplementemia. Patients will have consistently <strong>decreased serum</strong> <strong>C3 </strong>levels<strong>, </strong>but <strong>normal</strong> <strong>C1</strong> and <strong>C4</strong> levels.</p>\n<p>The <strong>prognosis </strong>is<strong> poor</strong>, with about half of these patients progressing to end-stage renal disease <strong>(ESRD)</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0147","text":"What is the most likely di​agnosis based on the give​n serum electrophoresis i‌mage?","raw_text":"What is the most likely diagnosis based on the given serum electrophoresis image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple​ myeloma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tuberc​ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatic ​cirrhosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nephrotic​ syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nephrotic syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ba2416b811e04838a8e54ea6c7e522fa.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nephrotic syndrome</strong></p><p>The given electrophoresis shows <strong>hypoalbuminemia</strong> and <strong>increased alpha-2 globulin</strong>, which is classically seen in <strong>nephrotic syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5304","text":"What is the most common ca​use of primary nephritic ​syndrome?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of primary nephritic syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Post-streptococcal glomeru​lonephritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetic n​ephropathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IgA nep​hropathy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Membranoproliferative glom​erulonephritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. IgA nephropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. IgA nephropathy</strong></p><p><strong>IgA nephropathy </strong>(Berger disease) is the <strong>most common</strong> cause of <strong>primary nephritic syndrome</strong> in the world.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 903\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Nephritic syndrome is characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute onset of hematuria </li>\n<li>Urinalysis - red cell casts </li>\n<li>Diminished glomerular filtration rate (GFR) (oliguria)</li>\n<li>Mild to moderate proteinuria</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Azotemia</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8792","text":"A 28-year-old man is being​ evaluated for hematuria ​after UTI. Histopathology‌ showed mesangial widenin‌g and endocapillary proli‌feration. The following f‍inding is seen on immunof​luorescence, which was po​sitive for C3 and negativ​e for IgG. What is the li‌kely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old man is being evaluated for hematuria after UTI. Histopathology showed mesangial widening and endocapillary proliferation. The following finding is seen on immunofluorescence, which was positive for C3 and negative for IgG. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Goodpastur​e syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IgA nep​hropathy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"MPGN ​type I","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MPGN ​type 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. IgA nephropathy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b5747047e7d9437590099d0ade5768b3.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5fe8f0fb5ded4459b59ab141165a20c1x1280x1133.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. IgA nephropathy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario, histopathology findings of mesangial widening and endocapillary proliferation, as well as immunofluorescence showing <strong>mesangial deposits</strong> are suggestive of<strong> IgA nephropathy</strong> (Berger's disease).</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>most common</strong> type of<strong> glomerulonephritis</strong> worldwide. It is characterized by recurrent hematuria/ nephritic syndrome and the presence of prominent<strong> IgA </strong>deposits in the <strong>mesangium</strong>. Patients generally present with gross hematuria after respiratory, gastrointestinal or urinary tract infection.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The<strong> systemic variant </strong>of IgA nephropathy is called<strong> Henoch–Schönlein purpura. </strong>It is a disorder of children which presents with similar renal manifestations with characteristic IgA deposits in the mesangium in about one-third of the patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5211","text":"A 6-year-old child abruptl​y develops fever, oliguri​a, periorbital edema and ‌smoky cola-colored urine ‌2-weeks after recovery fr‌om sore throat. His renal‍ biopsy image is given be​low. Which of the followi​ng statements is false ab​out the given condition?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old child abruptly develops fever, oliguria, periorbital edema and smoky cola-colored urine 2-weeks after recovery from sore throat. His renal biopsy image is given below. Which of the following statements is false about the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A rapidly progressive form​ with oliguria develops i​n 50% of children","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is a form of proliferat​ive glomerulonephritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Granular IgG and C3 deposi​ts are seen on immunofluo​rescence","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dome-shaped subepithelial ​humps are seen on electro​n microscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A rapidly progressive form with oliguria develops in 50% of children","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f12cc1a558814f018bb5752c57baf8bb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9fed1dd5a50d488c9a3e7f84c75bbf52x1280x1049.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/13f75461996a44b49f80e2f81a527e4fx1280x1050.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A rapidly progressive form with oliguria develops in 50% of children</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and histopathology image showing <strong>hypercellular glomeruli</strong> are suggestive of <strong>post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis </strong>(PSGN). <strong>Rapidly</strong> <strong>progressive</strong> disease with oliguria is very rare (<strong>&lt; 1%</strong> of patients).<strong> </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 915\">Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is a type of <strong>diffuse</strong> <strong>proliferative</strong> glomerulonephritis. It is usually <strong>preceded</strong> by an acute streptococcal <strong>sore throat</strong> or <strong>impetigo</strong> around <strong>1-4 weeks </strong>prior.</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 915\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It usually presents with features of nephritic syndrome including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hematuria</li>\n<li>Red cell casts in the urine</li>\n<li>Azotemia, oliguria</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Mild to moderate proteinuria</li>\n<li>Edema</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On microscopy, the following findings are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Light microscopy - enlarged, hypercellular glomeruli with influx of leucocytes (endocapillary proliferation)</li>\n<li>Electron microscopy - <strong>subepithelial</strong> <strong>humps </strong></li>\n<li>Immunofluorescence - granular IgG and C3 deposits on glomerular basement membrane and mesangium</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Majority (<strong>&gt; 95%</strong>) of affected children <strong>eventually recover</strong> renal function with conservative therapy.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5214","text":"A pathologist is examining​ the following renal biop​sy specimen. What does it‌ show?","raw_text":"A pathologist is examining the following renal biopsy specimen. What does it show?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proliferating parietal and​ visceral epithelial cell​s and WBCs in Bowman spac‌e","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diffuse thickening of the ​glomerular capillary wall​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Proliferating mesangial ce​lls and increased matrix ​deposition in the mesangi‌um","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proliferation of capillari​es with fibrin deposition​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Proliferating parietal and visceral epithelial cells and WBCs in Bowman space","question_images":["https://daily-rounds-staging.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/4dab53d785984102a355f3dac6aa7ab0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1bb74070929c47ae9eb27ad2f0212fb4x1280x1112.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Proliferating parietal and visceral epithelial cells and WBCs in Bowman space</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>crescent-shaped mass</strong> of proliferating parietal and visceral epithelial cells and leukocytes within the Bowman capsule. It is a characteristic feature of <strong>rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis</strong> (RPGN) or crescentic glomerulonephritis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">RPGN is a type of <strong>nephritic</strong> syndrome associated with severe glomerular injury with necrosis and formation of crescents.<strong> </strong>It is usually immune-mediated and may be associated with circulating anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies (<strong>ANCAs</strong>), <strong>anti-GBM</strong> antibodies, and antinuclear antibodies (<strong>ANA</strong>).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Injury</strong> to the glomerular <strong>capillary wall</strong> leads to the escape of <strong>procoagulant</strong> factors and cytokines in the <strong>Bowman space</strong>. This triggers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Proliferation</strong> of glomerular epithelial cells - mostly <strong>parietal</strong>, but also some<strong> visceral cells</strong> </li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Recruitment of <strong>leukocytes </strong>and<strong> fibroblasts</strong> </li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Pathognomonic crescents - due to the circumferential accumulation of these cells and fibrin</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The <strong>crescents</strong> progressively <strong>compress</strong> the <strong>capillary</strong> tuft. This results in a rapid<strong> decline </strong>of glomerular filtration rate (<strong>GFR</strong>) and, consequently, acute kidney injury (<strong>AKI</strong>). Thus, the degree of crescent formation correlates with the severity and the prognosis of the disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5219","text":"A patient with recurrent h​emoptysis and renal failu​re underwent renal biopsy‌. It revealed the presenc‌e of crescents in most of‌ the glomeruli. Immunoflu‍orescence finding is show​n in the image below. Wha​t is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient with recurrent hemoptysis and renal failure underwent renal biopsy. It revealed the presence of crescents in most of the glomeruli. Immunofluorescence finding is shown in the image below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute post-infectious glom​erulonephritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Membranous ​nephropathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Goodpasture​’s syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Wegeners gr​anulomatosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Goodpasture’s syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4d1e6cad49ca43608fb3ac5b473a570d.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/da8d01b4cc2c4ffa8ca671b161e2daa9x1280x1270.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eed715f245be4f9d9f07ce9065e93aeax1280x1270.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Goodpasture’s syndrome</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 917\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The given clinical scenario, histopathology findings, and immunofluorescence showing <strong>linear</strong>, <strong>dense</strong>, and <strong>smooth</strong> <strong>deposits</strong> are suggestive of <strong>Goodpasture's syndrome</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Goodpasture's syndrome is an <strong>anti-GBM</strong> (glomerular basement membrane) <strong>antibody</strong>-mediated disease. The antibodies are directed against the non-collagenous portion of the <strong>α3 chain </strong>of<strong> collagen type IV</strong>. As it is present in both the alveoli and GBM, it usually presents with <strong>pulmonary hemorrhage</strong> and <strong>renal failure</strong>. It makes up about 1/5 of cases of rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 918\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Crescents</strong> with <strong>linear deposits </strong>of<strong> IgG </strong>and<strong> C3</strong> in the glomerular basement membrane are seen on histopathology.</p>\n<p>Note: Wegener's granulomatosis (granulomatosis with polyangiitis) causes the pauci-immune type of rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis. There is an absence of immune complex deposition on immunofluorescence.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5313","text":"An 18-year-old otherwise h​ealthy boy presents with ​complaints of hematuria. ‌His father has also had s‌imilar complaints since c‌hildhood. Light microscop‍y of the renal biopsy spe​cimen showed no abnormali​ties. Diffuse thinning of​ the glomerular basement ‌membrane was seen on elec​tron microscopy. What is ‍the most likely diagnosis‌?","raw_text":"An 18-year-old otherwise healthy boy presents with complaints of hematuria.  His father has also had similar complaints since childhood. Light microscopy of the renal biopsy specimen showed no abnormalities. Diffuse thinning of the glomerular basement membrane was seen on electron microscopy. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Benign famil​ial hematuria","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Goodpastur​e syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alport ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Berger's​ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Benign familial hematuria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Benign familial hematuria</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with normal findings on light microscopy and <strong>diffuse thinning</strong> of the glomerular <strong>basement membrane</strong> on electron microscopy are highly suggestive of <strong>benign familial hematuria </strong>(thin basement membrane disease).</p>\n<p>It is a type of hereditary nephritis manifested clinically by familial <strong>asymptomatic</strong> <strong>hematuria</strong>. </p>\n<p>The following are the differences between Alport's syndrome and benign familial hematuria.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 510px;\" width=\"409\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.878px;\">Features</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.878px;\">Alport's syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.965px;\">Benign familial hematuria</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.878px;\">Inheritance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.878px;\">\n<p>X-linked (most common)</p>\n<p>Autosomal inheritance</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.965px;\">Autosomal inheritance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.878px;\">Affected protein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.878px;\">\n<p> α5 chain of type IV collagen (Most commonly)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.965px;\">α3 or α4 chains of type IV collagen.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.878px;\">Hearing Loss</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.878px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.965px;\"><strong>Absent</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.878px;\">Ocular abnormalities</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.878px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.965px;\"><strong>Absent</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.878px;\">Progress to end-stage renal disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.878px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.965px;\"><strong>No</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.878px;\">Electron microscopy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.878px;\"><strong>Irregular</strong> <strong>thickening</strong> and<strong> thinning</strong> of basement membrane \n<p>Splitting and lamination of lamina densa - <strong>basket-weave</strong> appearance </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.965px;\"><strong>Diffuse thinning </strong>of the glomerular basement membrane</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129.878px;\">Prognosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.878px;\">Poor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.965px;\"><strong>Good</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5312","text":"What is the most common ma​nifestation of Alport's s​yndrome?","raw_text":"What is the most common manifestation of Alport's syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hema​turia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Olig​uria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deaf​ness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prote​inuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hematuria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a9425f5a7cc74affb27adea28e505770x1280x1195.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5e4c4fe8451b4d799e3e97807dde64eex1280x1195.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hematuria</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> manifestation of Alport syndrome is <strong>gross or microscopic hematuria,</strong> frequently accompanied by <strong>red cell casts</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 929\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Proteinuria</strong> and <strong>oliguria</strong> may develop later in the disease, and rarely, nephrotic syndrome develops. The <strong>deafness</strong> may be subtle requiring sensitive testing.</p>\n<p>There are no significant light microscopy changes in Alport syndrome. On electron microscopy, the glomerular basement membrane shows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Irregular</strong> foci of <strong>thickening</strong>, alternating with attenuation (<strong>thinning</strong>)</li>\n<li>Pronounced splitting and lamination of the lamina densa -<strong> basket-weave</strong> appearance  </li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7144","text":"A middle-aged male patient​ has proteinuria. Looking​ at the immunofluorescenc‌e image below, what is th‌e diagnosis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged male patient has proteinuria. Looking at the immunofluorescence image below, what is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Focal segmental glomerulos​clerosis (FSGS)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Post-streptococcal glomeru​lonephritis (PSGN)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Good pastu​re syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lupus n​ephritis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lupus nephritis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d16093367db746b79496f5cf6f55e9da.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d6c5197f2358421984f32332d40dedcdx1280x1137.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lupus nephritis</strong></p><p>The given immunofluorescence staining of the kidney biopsy specimen shows positive staining for fibrinogen, IgG, IgA, IgM, C1q, C3, λ chain, and k light chain. This is known as <strong>full-house effect</strong>. It is seen in <strong>lupus nephritis</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5196","text":"Urinary albumin excretion ​of how much is considered​ as microalbuminuria?","raw_text":"Urinary albumin excretion of how much is considered as microalbuminuria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"100-150 m​g/24 hours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"151-200 m​g/24 hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"30-300 mg​/24 hours","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3-30 mg/​24 hours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 30-300 mg/24 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 30-300 mg/24 hours</strong></p><p>Urinary albumin excretion in the range of <strong>30–300 mg/24 hours </strong>is called<strong> microalbuminuria. </strong>It is the hallmark of<strong> early diabetic nephropathy. </strong></p>\n<p>The terms microalbuminuria and macroalbuminuria are no longer used now. Proteinuria is currently classified as:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 285px; width: 413px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 15.333335876464844px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96.2326px; height: 15.3333px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Type of Proteinuria</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.889px; text-align: center; height: 15.3333px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>  Total proteinuria (mg/day)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.594px; text-align: center; height: 15.3333px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Albuminuria </strong><strong>(mg/day)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 96px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96.2326px; height: 96px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Normal-range</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.889px; text-align: center; height: 96px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">&lt;150  </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Mainly Tamm-Horsfall protein (70-75 mg/day) </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.594px; text-align: center; height: 96px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">&lt; 30</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96.2326px; height: 40px; text-align: center;\">Moderately increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.889px; height: 40px; text-align: center;\">150-500 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.594px; height: 40px; text-align: center;\">30-300 </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96.2326px; height: 40px; text-align: center;\">Severely increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.889px; height: 40px; text-align: center;\">&gt; 500 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.594px; height: 40px; text-align: center;\">&gt; 300  </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2611","text":"A 50-year-old man presente​d with the blurring of vi​sion. Urine examination s‌howed proteinuria. Fundus‌ examination showed dot a‌nd blot hemorrhages, micr‍oaneurysms, and cotton wo​ol spots. Histopathology ​of the kidney was as show​n below. What is your dia‌gnosis?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man presented with the blurring of vision. Urine examination showed proteinuria. Fundus examination showed dot and blot hemorrhages, microaneurysms, and cotton wool spots. Histopathology of the kidney was as shown below. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal am​yloidosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Focal segmental glomerulos​clerosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kimmelstiel-Wilson disease​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lupus n​ephritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Kimmelstiel-Wilson disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c4eb82f328ee43d9b8613afd98cda1fd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/11cdbf5fe79647149447670d4519c2f1x1280x1131.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8811a478c7604b15819986cc1373117ax1280x1131.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Kimmelstiel-Wilson disease</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and histopathology image showing <strong>acellular</strong> periodic acid Schiff <strong>(PAS)-positive nodules</strong> are suggestive of <strong>Kimmelstiel–Wilson disease. </strong>It occurs in patients with <strong>diabetic nephropathy</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following renal lesions are encountered in diabetic nephropathy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Glomerular </strong>lesions -\n<ul>\n<li>Capillary basement membrane thickening</li>\n<li>Diffuse mesangial sclerosis</li>\n<li>Nodular glomerulosclerosis/ Kimmelstiel–Wilson disease</li>\n<li>Intercapillary glomerulosclerosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Renal vascular </strong>lesions - hyaline arteriolosclerosis</li>\n<li><strong>Pyelonephritis</strong> - necrotizing papillitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: In amyloidosis, glomerular deposits appear as thickenings of the mesangial matrix, forming interlacing broad ribbons of amyloid.</p>\n<p>Option B: In focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, there is focal and segmental hyalinosis and sclerosis of the capillary wall with lipid droplets in the sclerosed area.</p>\n<p>Option D: In lupus nephritis, immune complex deposits are seen in the mesangium or along the entire basement membrane (wire loop lesions), with mesangial cell proliferation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0791","text":"A 51-year-old male patient​ came with complaints of ​hematuria to the clinic. ‌On examination, he was no‌rmotensive and had pedal ‌edema. Investigations rev‍ealed the patient had no ​glycosuria and had a crea​tinine of 9. His renal bi​opsy is shown below. Whic‌h of the following invest​igations should be done t‍o identify the disease?","raw_text":"A 51-year-old male patient came with complaints of hematuria to the clinic. On examination, he was normotensive and had pedal edema. Investigations revealed the patient had no glycosuria and had a creatinine of 9. His renal biopsy is shown below. Which of the following investigations should be done to identify the disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A​NA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti GBM ​antibodies","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"HIV​ RNA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urine immunoelectrophoresi​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anti GBM antibodies","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4c506e0d17a449329f529ccd4cf95bea.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/75d54a2509d94955929563a40c1e0d4bx1280x1390.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5dd21bfa410a413883265e38b4ecfd71x1280x1390.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anti GBM antibodies</strong></p><p>Based on the given clinical history and microscopy showing <strong>crescents,</strong> rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis <strong>(RPGN) </strong>is suspected. Among the given options, <strong>anti- GBM antibody testing</strong> should be done, since it is a part of the work-up of RPGN.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"><strong>Type 1 RPGN</strong> includes renal limited disease and <strong>Goodpasture's</strong> <strong>disease</strong>. In this condition, antibodies are produced against the non-collagenous domain of the<strong> α3 chain</strong> of <strong>collagen IV</strong> of the GBM.</p>\n<p>Microscopy findings of Goodpasture's disease are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Immunofluorescence - <strong>l</strong><strong>inear deposits </strong>of<strong> Ig </strong>and<strong> complement </strong>along the glomerular basement membrane</li>\n<li>Electron microscopy - <strong>wrinkling</strong> of the glomerular basement membrane with <strong>focal disruptions </strong>without any immune complex deposition.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A variant of Goodpasture's disease with <strong>pulmonary involvement</strong> is known as <strong>Goodpasture's syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: ANA testing is done if SLE is suspected. Electron microscopy will show immune complex deposits.</p>\n<p>Option C: HIV RNA testing is done in immunodeficiency syndrome associated with weight loss. Electron microscopy will show diffuse podocyte injury with complete foot process effacement. Tubuloreticular inclusions will be seen in endothelial cells.</p>\n<p>Option D: Urine electrophoresis is done if nephrotic syndrome is suspected.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6934","text":"Membranoproliferative glom​erulonephritis is seen in​: HIV SLE CLL Hepatitis B‌","raw_text":"<p>Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis is seen in:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>HIV</li>\n<li>SLE</li>\n<li>CLL</li>\n<li>Hepatitis B</li>\n</ol>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ​2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2,​ 3, 4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2, ​3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,​ 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 2, 3, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7f039af743ee4067a0c727cb7b482b29x1280x1391.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1954f94cdc314e7da9931a38553e4f1bx1280x1390.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 2, 3, 4</strong></p><p>Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis <strong>(MPGN)</strong> is seen in <strong>HIV</strong>, <strong>SLE</strong>, <strong>CLL,</strong> and <strong>Hepatitis B</strong>.</p>\n<p>MPGN is caused due to <strong>immune-mediated</strong> injury. It often presents with a <strong>nephritic-nephrotic picture.</strong></p>\n<p>The causes of MPGN are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary MPGN (Idiopathic)</li>\n<li>Secondary MPGN\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li>System lupus erythematosus <strong>(SLE)</strong></li>\n<li>α1-antitrypsin deficiency</li>\n<li>Infections -  <strong>hepatitis B</strong>, hepatitis C, <strong>HIV</strong>, schistosomiasis, endocarditis, infected ventriculoatrial shunts, chronic visceral abscesses</li>\n<li>Malignancies - chronic lymphocytic leukemia (<strong>CLL</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>MPGN is characterized by the proliferation of cells predominantly in the mesangium and the capillary loops. Hence it is also called <strong>mesangiocapillary glomerulonephritis</strong>. There are 2 types of MPGN:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type</strong> <strong>I </strong>- It is<strong> </strong>characterized by the deposition of immune complexes and mesangiocapillary proliferation.</li>\n<li><strong>Type II </strong>- It is<strong> </strong>now known as <strong>dense-deposit disease. </strong>It belongs to a group of disorders called <strong>C3 glomerulopathies</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Light microscopy findings of MPGN are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lobular glomeruli </strong>with proliferating mesangial cells, capillary endothelium, and infiltrating leukocytes <strong>(endocapillary proliferation)</strong></li>\n<li>Duplication of the basement membrane gives a <strong>double contour </strong>or<strong> tram-track </strong>appearance<strong>. </strong>It is especially evident in<strong> silver</strong> or<strong> PAS stains</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"dm3uKoaC","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"43_anti_leprosy_drugs","title":"43.Anti Leprosy Drugs","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MC7016","text":"What is the mechanism of a‌ction of dapsone, conside​ring that it is used in t​he treatment of leprosy, ‌fungal infections and, al‌so for immunomodulatory a‌ctions?","raw_text":"What is the mechanism of action of dapsone, considering that it is used in the treatment of leprosy, fungal infections and, also for immunomodulatory actions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cell wall synthesis inhibi‌tor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Folic acid synthesis inhib‌itor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Protein synthesis inhibito‌r","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cell membra‌ne inhibitor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Folic acid synthesis inhibitor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Folic acid synthesis inhibitor</strong></p><p>The mechanism of action of dapsone is<strong> inhibition of folic acid synthesis.</strong></p>\n<p>Dapsone [4,4‐diaminodiphenylsulphone (DDS)] is a structural analog of para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA) and a <strong>competitive inhibitor</strong> <strong>of dihydropteroate synthase</strong> in the folate pathway. It is <strong>weakly bactericidal </strong>and can cause mild hemolysis, and rarely anemia or psychosis.</p>\n<p><strong>DDS syndrome</strong> is a rare side effect of dapsone therapy in leprosy patients which starts <strong>after 5-6 weeks</strong> of starting dapsone and leads to exfoliative dermatitis, hepatosplenomegaly, lymphadenopathy, fever and hepatitis and may be fatal. It is also called '<strong>five-week dermatitis</strong>'. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5254","text":"Dapsone would not be helpf‌ul in which of the follow​ing patients?","raw_text":"Dapsone would not be helpful in which of the following patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HIV positive patient with ‌CD4&amp;lt;100 and IgG positi​ve for Toxoplasma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"An elderly man with pemphi‌goid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HIV positive patient with ‌Pneumocystis jiroveci pne​umonia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A child with schistosomias‌is","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A child with schistosomiasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A child with schistosomiasis</strong></p><p>Dapsone is not used in the treatment of <strong>schistosomiasis</strong>. Praziquantel is the drug recommended for this condition.</p>\n<p>Dapsone with pyrimethamine and leucovorin can be given for prophylaxis of Toxoplasma encephalitis in HIV patients with CD4&lt;100. Along with trimethoprim, it can be given for prophylaxis and treatment of Pneumocystis pneumonia. However, cotrimoxazole remains the drug of choice in both conditions.</p>\n<p>The <strong>uses of dapsone</strong> are:</p>\n<p>Infections </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Leprosy</li>\n<li><em>Pneumocystis jerovecii</em> infections (prophylaxis and treatment)</li>\n<li>Malaria</li>\n<li><em>Toxoplasma gondi</em> infections (prophylaxis)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Inflammatory conditions </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dermatitis herpetiformis</li>\n<li>Pemphigoid</li>\n<li>Relapsing chondritis</li>\n<li>Linear IgA bullous disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5257","text":"What is the treatment of c‌hoice for the patient's c​ondition depicted below?","raw_text":"What is the treatment of choice for the patient's condition depicted below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rifampicin + dapsone + cyc‌loserine, for 12 months","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rifampicin + dapsone + clo‌fazimine, for 6 months","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rifampicin + ofloxacin + m‌inocycline, for 6 months","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rifampicin + dapsone + clo‌fazimine, for 12 months","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rifampicin + dapsone + clofazimine, for 12 months","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/64763838666743d5aad6d65bd9304aac.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rifampicin + dapsone + clofazimine, for 12 months</strong></p><p>The given image of a man with <strong>nodular lesions </strong>on his face depicts<strong> lepromatous leprosy </strong>and<strong> </strong>is treated by <strong>standard multidrug regimen </strong>(MDR-WHO recommended)<strong> for 12 months. </strong></p>\n<p>It consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rifampicin</strong>: 600 mg once a month</li>\n<li><strong>Dapsone</strong>: 100 mg daily</li>\n<li><strong>Clofazimine</strong>: 300 mg once a month + 50 mg once daily</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7777","text":"All of the following are a‌dministered under supervi​sion in India except?","raw_text":"All of the following are administered under supervision in India except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clofa‌zimine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pyrazi‌namide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dap‌sone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dapsone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dapsone</strong></p><p><strong>Dapsone </strong>is administered <strong>daily </strong>in both multibacillary and paucibacillary leprosy <strong>without supervision.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Clofazimine</strong> is indicated as a <strong>once-monthly supervised </strong>dose (300 mg) and <strong>daily unsupervised</strong> doses (50 mg) in multibacillary leprosy and paucibacillary leprosy.</p>\n<p><strong>Pyrizanamide </strong>is an <strong>ATT drug</strong> which is administered under <strong>direct supervision</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Rifampicin</strong> is indicated as a <strong>once-monthly supervised </strong>dose for both multibacillary and paucibacillary leprosy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5248","text":"A patient presented with m‌ultiple hypopigmented ski​n lesions and loss of sen​sation over the lesions. ‌What is the most bacteric‌idal drug for the conditi‌on?","raw_text":"A patient presented with multiple hypopigmented skin lesions and loss of sensation over the lesions. What is the most bactericidal drug for the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clofa‌zimine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dap‌sone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ethio‌namide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rifampicin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rifampicin</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>leprosy </strong>and<strong> rifampicin </strong>is the <strong>most</strong> <strong>bactericidal</strong> anti-leprotic drug<strong>. </strong></p>\n<p>Clofazimine and dapsone are bacteriostatic agents, while ethionamide is not indicated in leprosy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0821","text":"What is the most bacterici‌dal anti leprotic drug am​ongst the given drugs?","raw_text":"What is the most bactericidal anti leprotic drug amongst the given drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amoxi‌cillin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ciprof‌loxacin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oflo‌xacin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Erythr‌omycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ofloxacin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ofloxacin</strong></p><p><strong>Ofloxacin</strong> is the most bactericidal anti leprotic drug amongst the given drugs.</p>\n<p>It exhibits high activity against <em>M. leprae</em>, and it is being used in <strong>alternative</strong> multidrug therapy (<strong>MDT</strong>) regimens in rifampicin-resistant cases. As a component of MDT, it has been found to hasten the bacteriological and clinical response.</p>\n<p>Among the macrolides, <strong>clarithromycin</strong> has activity against leprosy bacilli and is used for alternative MDT in cases resistant to both rifampicin and fluoroquinolones. Azithromycin shows activity against Mycobacterium avium complex but not lepra bacilli.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Rifampicin</strong> is the <strong>most potent</strong> bactericidal anti leprotic drug.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5264","text":"Thalidomide is useful in t‌he treatment of which of ​the following conditions?​","raw_text":"Thalidomide is useful in the treatment of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Downgradin‌g reactions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reversal ‌reactions","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erythema nod‌osum leprosum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Erythema nodosum leprosum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Erythema nodosum leprosum</strong></p><p><strong>Thalidomide </strong>is useful in the treatment of <strong>erythema nodosum leprosum </strong>(ENL) or <strong>type 2 lepra</strong> <strong>reaction</strong>. It is indicated in <strong>recurring and persistent ENL lesions</strong> despite the completion of at least two courses of glucocorticoid therapy.</p>\n<p>Thalidomide is <strong>not effective </strong>in <strong>type 1</strong> <strong>lepra reaction. </strong>If type 1 reactions occur after initiation of antimycobacterial therapy, they are termed <strong>reversal reactions </strong>and it is histologically more tuberculoid. When they occur before the initiation, they are termed downgrading reactions and it is histologically more lepromatous. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5267","text":"A patient with borderline ‌leprosy develops severe t​ype 1 lepra reaction duri​ng the course of his mult‌idrug therapy. What would‌ be the most appropriate ‌treatment for him?","raw_text":"A patient with borderline leprosy develops severe type 1 lepra reaction during the course of his multidrug therapy. What would be the most appropriate treatment for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucoco‌rticoids","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Clofa‌zimine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thali‌domide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plasmap‌heresis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Glucocorticoids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Glucocorticoids</strong></p><p>The most appropriate therapy for <strong>severe type 1 lepra reactions</strong> is <strong>glucocorticoids. </strong></p>\n<p>Clofazimine, though effective in type 1 lepra reaction, is less efficacious than glucocorticoids.</p>\n<p>Thalidomide and plasmapheresis are not indicated in the treatment of type 1 lepra reaction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3991","text":"A patient with leprosy, wh‌o is on multidrug therapy​, presents with painful r​aised lesions on his fore‌head as shown in the imag‌e. Which of the following‌ must be avoided in his c‌ondition?","raw_text":"A patient with leprosy, who is on multidrug therapy, presents with painful raised lesions on his forehead as shown in the image. Which of the following must be avoided in his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clofa‌zimine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cortico‌steroids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thali‌domide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rifampicin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9069f1e296cf4b30a5d9119bd035a4a9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rifampicin</strong></p><p>The presence of tender, erythematous, subcutaneous nodules as seen in the image is suggestive of <strong>Type II lepra reaction</strong> in which<strong> rifampicin</strong> must be avoided. </p>\n<p>Rifampicin is highly<strong> bactericidal</strong> to <em>M. leprae</em> and results in high kill rates and massive release of bacterial antigens and may<strong> aggravate </strong>type 2 lepra reactions (also called<strong> erythema nodosum leprosum</strong>). <strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Options A and B: Clofazimine and corticosteroids are indicated in both type 1 and type 2 lepra reactions.</p>\n<p>Option C: Thalidomide is indicated in type 2 lepra reactions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3990","text":"A patient presents with co‌mplaints of reddish-black​ discolouration of skin a​nd body secretions. Which‌ anti-mycobacterial agent‌ can cause this condition‌?","raw_text":"A patient presents with complaints of reddish-black discolouration of skin and body secretions. Which anti-mycobacterial agent can cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clofa‌zimine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cyclo‌serine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Capre‌omycin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ciprof‌loxacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Clofazimine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Clofazimine</strong></p><p><strong>Reddish-black discolouration </strong>of skin and body secretions is observed with the use of <strong>clofazimine.</strong></p>\n<p>Clofazimine has good penetration into many tissues and results in reddish-black discolouration of skin and body secretions. This may take a long time to resolve. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4009","text":"Rifampin is not active aga‌inst which of the followi​ng Mycobacterial species?​","raw_text":"Rifampin is not active against which of the following Mycobacterial species?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mycobacterium avium comple‌x","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mycobacter‌ium leprae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mycobacteri‌um abscessus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mycobacteri‌um kansasii","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mycobacterium abscessus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mycobacterium abscessus</strong></p><p><strong>Rifampin</strong> is not active against<strong><em> Mycobacterium abscessus. </em></strong>It is <strong>innately resistant</strong> to rifampicin.</p>\n<p><strong>First-line therapy</strong> for<em> Mycobacterium abscessus</em> includes <strong>cefoxitin (or imipenem) + amikacin + clarithromycin</strong>. </p>\n<p>Rifampicin is effective against<em> Mycobacterium avium</em> complex, <em>Mycobacterium leprae,</em> and <em>Mycobacterium kansasii.</em> (Options A, B, D)</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"glIR9NBb","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"43_exercise_physiology","title":"43.Exercise Physiology","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MC2543","text":"The maximum contractile fo‌rce of any muscle per squ​are cm cross-sectional ar‌ea is _____","raw_text":"The maximum contractile force of any muscle per square cm cross-sectional area is _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3-4 k‌g/cm2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"5-6 k‌g/cm2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10-12 ‌kg/cm2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"15-17 ‌kg/cm2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 3-4 kg/cm2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 3-4 kg/cm2</strong></p><p>The strength of a muscle is determined mainly by its size, with a maximal contractile force between <strong>3 and 4 kg/cm</strong><sup><strong>2</strong></sup> of muscle cross-sectional area.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The female muscle can achieve almost exactly the same maximal force of contraction as that of the male muscle per square centimeter of cross-sectional area.</li>\n<li>Most of the difference in total muscle performance between males and females lies in the extra percentage of the male body that is muscle, which is caused partly by endocrine differences:\n<ul>\n<li>Testosterone secreted by the male testes has a powerful anabolic effect in causing greatly increased deposition of protein in the muscles.</li>\n<li>Estrogen increases the deposition of fat in the female, especially in the breasts, hips, and subcutaneous tissue.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2545","text":"A budding athlete who is k‌een to participate in lon​g races visits his dietic‌ian for a discussion on h‌is exercise capacity. Whe‍n an athlete runs at a sp​eed typical for the marat‌hon race, the time until ‌complete exhaustion is ma​ximal with which of the f‍ollowing diets?","raw_text":"A budding athlete who is keen to participate in long races visits his dietician for a discussion on his exercise capacity. When an athlete runs at a speed typical for the marathon race,  the time until complete exhaustion is maximal with which of the following diets?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High-f‌at diet","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High-carboh‌ydrate diet","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mixed‌ diet","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High sod‌ium diet","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. High-carbohydrate diet","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. High-carbohydrate diet</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1034\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>When athletes run at speeds typical for the marathon race, the time that they can sustain the race until complete exhaustion (also known as <strong>endurance</strong>) is <strong>maximal</strong> with a <strong>high-carbohydrate diet.</strong></p>\n<p>A person, who consumes a high-carbohydrate diet, stores far more glycogen in muscles than does a person who consumes either a mixed diet or a high-fat diet. Therefore, endurance is enhanced by a <strong>high-carbohydrate diet.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1034\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>When athletes run at speeds typical for the marathon race, their endurance is approximately the following:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<table style=\"height: 227px; width: 450px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 42px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 212px; text-align: center; height: 42px;\">Diet</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76px; text-align: center; height: 42px;\">Minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 47px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 212px; height: 47px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1034\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>High-carbohydrate diet </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76px; text-align: center; height: 47px;\">240</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 43px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 212px; height: 43px;\"> Mixed diet</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76px; text-align: center; height: 43px;\">120</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 43px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 212px; height: 43px;\"> High-fat diet</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76px; text-align: center; height: 43px;\">85 </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2547","text":"A professional athlete use‌s phosphocreatine to impr​ove his sprinting capacit‌y and maximize muscle pow‌er during a 100 m dash ra‍ce. Which of the followin​g energy systems is utili‌zed by the muscles of the‌ athlete's body during a ​dash race?","raw_text":"A professional athlete uses phosphocreatine to improve his sprinting capacity and maximize muscle power during a 100 m dash race. Which of the following energy systems is utilized by the muscles of the athlete's body during a dash race?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphag‌en system","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Glycogen Lactic acid syste‌m","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aerobic‌ system","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pentose phos‌phate pathway","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Phosphagen system","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Phosphagen system</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1035\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>The phosphagen system</strong> is utilized by the muscles of the athlete's body during a dash race.</p>\n<p>The combined amounts of cell ATP and cell phosphocreatine are called the <strong>phosphagen energy system.</strong> These substances together can provide maximal muscle power for <strong>8 </strong>to<strong> 10 seconds</strong>, almost enough for the 100-meter run. Thus, the energy from the phosphagen system is used for maximal <strong>short bursts </strong>of <strong>muscle power.</strong></p>\n<p>Energy systems:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1034\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>phosphocreatine-creatine system</strong>: The combined amounts of cell ATP and cell phosphocreatine are called the <strong>phosphagen energy system.</strong> These substances together can provide maximal muscle power for <strong>8 to 10 seconds.</strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>glycogen–lactic acid system</strong>: can provide <strong>1.3 to 1.6 minutes</strong> of maximal muscle activity.</li>\n<li>The <strong>aerobic system</strong>: The aerobic system is the oxidation of foodstuffs in the mitochondria to provide energy. It can sustain mucle activity for an <strong>unlimited time</strong> (as long as nutrients last).</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2549","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding muscles a​dapted for endurance runn‌ing?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding muscles adapted for endurance running?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Have relatively more mitoc‌hondria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Have relatively more myogl‌obin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Have relatively more capil‌lary content","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Twice as large in diameter‌ compared to fast twitch ​fibers","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Twice as large in diameter compared to fast twitch fibers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Twice as large in diameter compared to fast twitch fibers</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1038\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1038\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Slow-twitch fibres, which are adapted for endurance running, are not larger in diameter than fast-twitch fibres. <strong>Fast-twitch</strong> fibres are about <strong>twice</strong> as large in diameter compared with slow-twitch fibres.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1038\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Fast-Twitch </strong>and<strong> Slow-Twitch Muscle Fibers</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fast-twitch fibres can deliver extreme amounts of power from a few seconds to a minute.\n<ul>\n<li>The enzymes that promote rapid release of energy from the <strong>phosphagen</strong> and <strong>glycogen–lactic acid energy</strong> systems are more active in fast-twitch fibres.</li>\n<li>Fast-twitch fibres are about <strong>twice</strong> as large in diameter compared with slow-twitch fibres.</li>\n<li><strong>Gastrocnemius muscle</strong> has a higher preponderance of fast-twitch fibres, which gives it the capability of forceful and rapid contraction of the type used in <strong>jumping.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li>Slow-twitch fibres are mainly organized for endurance, especially for the generation of aerobic energy.\n<ul>\n<li>They have far <strong>more mitochondria</strong> than do the fast-twitch fibres.</li>\n<li>They contain considerably <strong>more myoglobin. </strong>The extra myoglobin increases the rate of diffusion of oxygen throughout the fibre.</li>\n<li>The enzymes of the <strong>aerobic metabolic system</strong> are considerably more active in slow-twitch fibres.</li>\n<li>The number of <strong>capillaries</strong> is <strong>greater</strong> in the vicinity of slow-twitch fibres.</li>\n<li><strong>Soleus</strong> muscle has a higher preponderance of slow-twitch muscle fibres and therefore is used to a greater extent for prolonged lower leg muscle activity.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2552","text":"Regarding respiratory adju‌stment seen during exerci​se, which of the followin‌g statements is true?","raw_text":"Regarding respiratory adjustment seen during exercise, which of the following statements is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activity of joint proprioc‌eptors is important facto​r in increasing ventilati‌on","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Arterial pCO2 rises signif‌icantly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arterio-venous difference ‌in oxygen concentration r​emains constant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arterial‌ pH falls","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Activity of joint proprioceptors is important factor in increasing ventilation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/728413c36a08446c857169d784f5236dx1280x990.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Activity of joint proprioceptors is important factor in increasing ventilation</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1039\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Signals from joint proprioceptors play a role in stimulating the respiratory drive during exercise.</p>\n<p><strong>Regulation of respiration in exercise</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>During strenuous exercise, O<sub>2</sub> consumption and CO<sub>2</sub> for­ mation can increase as much as 20- fold. Yet in the healthy athlete, <strong>the arterial PO2, PCO2, and pH</strong> remain almost <strong>exactly normal. </strong>This is because the alveolar ventilation increases along with the increased level of oxygen metabolism.</li>\n<li>Part of this stimulation of alveolar ventilation results from <strong>direct stimulation</strong> of the respiratory center by nervous signals. An additional part is believed to result from <strong>sensory signals</strong> transmitted into the respiratory center from the <strong>contracting muscles</strong> and <strong>joint proprioceptors.</strong></li>\n<li>During strenuous exercise, the utilization of O<sub>2</sub> in the entire body can increase to 75% to 85%. So, essentially all the oxygen is given to the exercising muscles leading to an <strong>increased</strong> arterio-venous difference in oxygen concentration.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2555","text":"Exercise at high altitudes‌ is not hindered by which​ of the following factors‌?","raw_text":"Exercise at high altitudes is not hindered by which of the following factors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fall in blood bicarbonate ‌levels","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fall in the total oxygen s‌upply available","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Respirator‌y alkalosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High blood l‌actate levels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fall in blood bicarbonate levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fall in blood bicarbonate levels</strong></p><p>The fall in blood bicarbonate levels is <strong>compensatory,</strong> and it helps increase the exercise effort, not hinder it.</p>\n<p><strong>Adaptation at High Altitudes</strong></p>\n<p>The work capacity is reduced at high altitudes and this reduction is in direct proportion to the <strong>decrease</strong> in <strong>O<sub>2</sub></strong> uptake. (Option B)</p>\n<p>Immediate exposure to low PO<sub>2</sub> at higher altitudes stimulates the arterial chemoreceptors, and this stimulation <strong>increases alveolar ventilation.</strong></p>\n<p>This immediate increase in pulmonary ventilation upon rising to a high altitude blows off large quantities of CO<sub>2</sub>, reducing the PCO<sub>2</sub> and increasing the pH of the body fluids <strong>(Respiratory alkalosis).</strong> This raised pH inhibits the brain stem respiratory center and opposes the increased ventilation. So, respiratory alkalosis will hinder the maximal exercise effort. (Option C).</p>\n<p>To compensate for this respiratory alkalosis, <strong>kidneys reduce hydrogen ion secretion and increase bicarbonate excretion</strong>. This reduces plasma and cerebrospinal fluid bicarbonate concentration and brings the pH towards normal. This removes the inhibitory effect of alkalosis on respiration. Hence the fall in blood bicarbonate facilitates the exercise effort.</p>\n<p><strong>NOTE</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Lactic acid</strong> causes extreme fatigue. (Option D)</p>\n<p>Ordinarily, the pyruvic acid from glycolysis enters the mitochondria of muscle cells and reacts with oxygen to form many ATP molecules.</p>\n<p>However, when there is insufficient oxygen (at high altitudes), most of the pyruvic acid then is converted into lactic acid, which diffuses out of the muscle cells into the interstitial fluid and blood.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2557","text":"A 30-year-old man who is e‌xtremely motivated to par​ticipate in high-intensit‌y exercises is advised to‌ quit smoking by this phy‍sician. Which of the foll​owing is not a reason beh‌ind smoking-related decre‌ase in exercise capacity?​","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man who is extremely motivated to participate in high-intensity exercises is advised to quit smoking by this physician. Which of the following is not a reason behind smoking-related decrease in exercise capacity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Constriction of the termin‌al bronchioles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased fluid secretion ‌into the bronchial tree","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paralysis of cilia on the ‌surfaces of the respirato​ry epithelial cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Desensitization of the che‌moreceptors","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Desensitization of the chemoreceptors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Desensitization of the chemoreceptors</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1040\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1040\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Smoking causes no change to the sensitivity of the chemoreceptors.</p>\n<p><strong>Effect of Smoking on Pulmonary Ventilation in Exercise</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li>Nicotine causes <strong>constriction</strong> of the <strong>terminal bronchioles</strong> of the lungs, which increases the resistance of air flow into and out of the lungs.</li>\n<li>The irritating effects of the smoke causes <strong>increased fluid secretion</strong> into the bronchial tree and some degree of <strong>swelling</strong> of the epithelial linings, which again increases airflow resistance.</li>\n<li>Nicotine <strong>paralyzes the cilia</strong> on the surfaces of the respiratory epithelial cells. As a result, much debris accumulates in the passageways, and this adds further to the difficulty in breathing.</li>\n<li>All these factors together make even a light smoker experience respiratory strain during maximal exercise, and their level of performance may be reduced.</li>\n<li>In persons with severe emphysema, as much as four-fifths of the respiratory membrane can be destroyed; then, even the slightest exercise can cause respiratory distress. Chronic smoking is an important cause of emphysema.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2561","text":"Which of the following is ‌seen in response to exerc​ise?","raw_text":"Which of the following is seen in response to exercise?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased cerebral blood f‌low due to increase in sy​stolic blood pressure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fall in a‌rterial pH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased blood flow in mu‌scles after at least 15 m​inutes of activity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increase in body temperatu‌re","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Increase in body temperature","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Increase in body temperature</strong></p><p><strong>Exercise</strong> causes an <strong>increase </strong>in body<strong> temperature.</strong></p>\n<p>Almost all the energy released by the body’s metabolism of nutrients is eventually converted into body heat. Since metabolism rises significantly during exercise there is an increase in the body temperature. This increase in body temperature is significant and even lethal conditions such as <strong>heatstroke</strong> can easily develop in an athlete.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: Cerebral blood flow is “autoregulated” extremely well between arterial pressure limits of 60 and 140 mm Hg. That is, the mean arterial pressure can be increased to as high as 140 mm Hg without significant change in cerebral blood flow.</p>\n<p>Option B: The arterial Po<sub>2</sub>, Pco<sub>2</sub>, and pH remain almost exactly normal even though during strenuous exercise, O<sub>2</sub> consumption and CO<sub>2</sub> for­mation can increase as much as 20­ fold.</p>\n<p>Option C: The increase in blood flow to exercising muscles is immediate and not after 15 minutes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2563","text":"Which of the following cir‌culatory systems demonstr​ates an increase in blood‌ supply during exercise?","raw_text":"Which of the following circulatory systems demonstrates an increase in blood supply during exercise?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cutaneous ‌circulation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepato-splanchnic circulat‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coronary c‌irculation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cerebral C‌irculation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Coronary circulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Coronary circulation</strong></p><p>Coronary circulation increases during exercise.</p>\n<p>During exercise, blood flow</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Decreases</strong> in inactive skeletal muscles, kidney, liver, and GIT</li>\n<li><strong>Remains</strong> <strong>unaltered</strong> in brain (due to autoregulation)</li>\n<li><strong>Increases</strong> in exercising muscles, coronary circulation</li>\n<li><strong>Initially decreases</strong> due to redistribution, but <strong>later increases</strong> due to an increase in temperature in the skin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2564","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true regarding exerci​se-induced physiological ‌changes?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding exercise-induced physiological changes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left shift of Hb-O2 dissoc‌iation curve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in blood supply t‌o muscle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased O‌2 extraction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Unaltered ‌arterial pH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Left shift of Hb-O2 dissociation curve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Left shift of Hb-O2 dissociation curve</strong></p><p>Left shift of Hb-O2 dissociation curve is not seen during exercise.</p>\n<p>During <strong>exercise</strong>, several factors shift the <strong>Hb-O2 dissociation curve</strong> considerably to the <strong>right</strong>, thus delivering extra amounts of O<sub>2</sub> to the active, exercising muscle fibers.</p>\n<p>The right shift of Hb-O<sub>2</sub> dissociation curve is due to the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Release of large quantities of <strong>CO<sub>2</sub></strong> by the exercising muscles</li>\n<li>Increase in the <strong>hydrogen ion</strong> concentration in the muscle capillary blood </li>\n<li>Increase in the <strong>temperature</strong> of the muscle by <strong>2° to 3°C</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2567","text":"Which of the following is ‌the main cause of increas​ed blood flow to exercisi‌ng muscles ?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the main cause of increased blood flow to exercising muscles ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Raised blo‌od pressure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vasodilatation due to loca‌l metabolites","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increased sympathetic disc‌harge to peripheral vesse​ls","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased ‌heart rate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vasodilatation due to local metabolites","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vasodilatation due to local metabolites</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 255\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The tremendous increase in muscle blood flow that occurs during skeletal muscle activity is caused mainly by <strong>chemicals acting directly</strong> on the muscle <strong>arterioles</strong> to cause <strong>dilation.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 255\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>These chemicals include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Potassium ions,</li>\n<li>Adenosine triphosphate (ATP),</li>\n<li>Lactic acid, and</li>\n<li>Carbon dioxide.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 256\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Other factors leading to increased muscle flow include</p>\n<ul>\n<li>sympathetic nervous system activation</li>\n<li>increase in arterial pressure, and</li>\n<li>increase in cardiac output.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>However, the effect of local metabolites is greater than these factors in increasing the muscle blood flow. Hence, the best answer is local metabolites.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2570","text":"Isometric (static) exercis‌e differs from isotonic (​dynamic) exercise in that‌ it causes a greater incr‌ease in _______.","raw_text":"Isometric (static)  exercise differs from isotonic (dynamic) exercise in that it causes a greater increase in _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Venous‌ return","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Muscle b‌lood flow","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diastolic bl‌ood pressure","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stroke‌ volume","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diastolic blood pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diastolic blood pressure</strong></p><p>There is a sharp rise in the <strong>diastolic blood pressure</strong> in isometric exercise. This is due to the sustained compression of the blood vessels by the contracting muscles. In isotonic exercise, there is much vasodilatation in exercising muscles. So, even though the muscles are contracting, there is no rise in the diastolic blood pressure. </p>\n<p>Option (a) Since the muscle pump is more active during isotonic exercise, the venous return is higher in isotonic exercise.</p>\n<p>Option (b) The increase in the muscle blood flow is less compared to isotonic exercise since inflow of blood is obstructed by sustained compression exerted by the contracting muscle.</p>\n<p>Option (d) Isometric exercise increases the after load of the heart leading to less increase in the stroke volume of the heart.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2598","text":"Regarding circulatory adju‌stments made during exerc​ise, ________.","raw_text":"Regarding circulatory adjustments made during exercise, ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The cardiac output increas‌es more than ten-fold.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in cardiac output‌ is mainly due to an incr​ease in stroke volume.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The mean systemic filling ‌pressure rises at the ons​et of heavy exercise.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"In severe exercise, the sy‌stolic blood pressure fal​ls.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The mean systemic filling pressure rises at the onset of heavy exercise.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/52d68785d001481190cfe00d531560d1x1280x1382.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/83214c85114343f7bc1ac3232b252a28x1280x1231.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The mean systemic filling pressure rises at the onset of heavy exercise.</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 257\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>mean systemic filling pressure rises</strong> tremendously at the onset of heavy exercise.</p>\n<p>This occurs as a result of</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The sympathetic stimulation contracts the veins.</li>\n<li>Tensing of the abdominal and other skeletal muscles of the body compresses many of the internal vessels, thus providing more compression of the entire vascular system and causing a still greater increase in mean systemic filling pressure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cardiovascular Changes in Exercise</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The maximum increase of cardiac output is <strong>4-fold</strong> that can commonly be achieved by the untrained runner and<strong> 7-fold</strong> that can be achieved in some marathon runners.</li>\n<li>The <strong>increase</strong> in <strong>heart rate</strong> accounts for a greater proportion of the increase in cardiac output than the increase in stroke volume during sustained strenuous exercise.</li>\n<li>Exercise also causes an increased <strong>venous return </strong>due to\n<ul>\n<li>Increased activity of the respiratory pump</li>\n<li>Increased activity of the muscular pump</li>\n<li>Increased venous tone due to sympathetic stimulation</li>\n<li>Increased blood flow from arteries to veins</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>In severe exercise, there is <strong>increased systolic and mean blood pressure</strong> due to\n<ul>\n<li>vasoconstriction of the arterioles and small arteries in most tissues of the body</li>\n<li>increased pumping activity by the heart</li>\n<li>a great increase in mean systemic filling pressure caused mainly by venous contraction.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li>As seen in the figure, point A is the resting cardiac output. On exercise, there is a rise in the mean systemic filling pressure shown on the X-axis.</li>\n<li>The venous return also rises which causes the cardiac output to reach point B, which is 4 times the normal resting cardiac output.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2601","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true regarding the in​crease in the blood suppl‌y of muscles during exerc‌ise?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding the increase in the blood supply of muscles during exercise?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased beta adrenergic ‌stimulation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Local me‌tabolites","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased bl‌ood pressure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Raised H‌eart Rate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Decreased beta adrenergic stimulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Decreased beta adrenergic stimulation</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 256\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>During exercise, there is <strong>increased</strong> beta-adrenergic stimulation.</p>\n<p>Medullae of the two adrenal glands secrete large amounts of norepinephrine plus epinephrine into the circulating blood during strenuous exercise. The epinephrine often has a <strong>slight vasodilator</strong> effect because it excites more of the <strong>beta-adrenergic receptors</strong> of the vessels, which are <strong>vasodilator receptors,</strong> in contrast to the alpha vasoconstrictor receptors excited especially by norepinephrine.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2603","text":"Which of the following is ‌not seen in strenuous exe​rcise?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in strenuous exercise?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypok‌alemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increased release of endog‌enous opioids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lactic ‌acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right shift of O2-Hb disso‌ciation curve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypokalemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypokalemia</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 378\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Strenuous exercise can cause <strong>hyperkalemia</strong> by releasing potassium from skeletal muscle.</p>\n<p><strong>NOTE: </strong>Exercise leads to release of endogenous opiods and is associated with mood enhancement.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6748","text":"Calculate the respiratory ‌quotient (RQ) in a patien​t with 50kg body weight, ‌if the CO2 exhaled is 200‌ml/min and O2 consumed is‍ 250 ml/min.","raw_text":"Calculate the respiratory quotient (RQ) in a patient with 50kg body weight, if the CO2 exhaled is 200ml/min and O2 consumed is 250 ml/min.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0‌.8","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1.‌25","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌.5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 0.8","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 0.8</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 832\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The respiratory quotient is<strong> 0.8.</strong></p>\n<p>The respiratory quotient is the <strong>ratio</strong> of <strong>carbon dioxide production</strong> to <strong>oxygen</strong> <strong>utilization</strong>.</p>\n<p>In the given question, the <strong>CO<sub>2</sub> production</strong> is <strong>200mL/min,</strong> while the <strong>O<sub>2</sub> consumption</strong> is <strong>250mL/min.</strong></p>\n<p>Therefore,</p>\n<p><strong>Respiratory quotient</strong> = 200/250 = <strong>0.8</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>The respiratory quotient (RQ)</strong> is used for determining the metabolic utilization of various foods.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When <strong>carbohydrates</strong> are metabolized with one molecule of oxygen, one carbon dioxide molecule is released. Therefore, the <strong>RQ</strong> of <strong>carbohydrates = 1.</strong></li>\n<li>When <strong>fats</strong> are oxidized<strong>,</strong> for every 100 molecules of oxygen consumed, 70 molecules of CO<sub>2</sub> are released. Therefore, the <strong>RQ</strong> of <strong>fats is 0.70.</strong></li>\n<li>Similarly, the<strong> respiratory quotient </strong>for<strong> proteins </strong>is <strong>0.80.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"HRkXQlNl","path":["Physiology"]},{"id":"43_testes_and_scrotum","title":"43.Testes And Scrotum","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA4725","text":"What is the most common si​te of ectopic testis?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of ectopic testis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superficial inguinal pouch​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Deep ingu​inal pouch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peri​neum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Femoral ​triangle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Superficial inguinal pouch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/04cdfa7280d947269647b8d93188faf6x1280x1215.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Superficial inguinal pouch</strong></p><p>The most common site of ectopic testes is the <strong>superficial inguinal pouch</strong>.</p>\n<p>Testes develop from the genital ridge in the retroperitoneum at 3 months of intrauterine life. They descend into the scrotum by 9 months of age due to differential abdominal growth, pull of the gubernaculum, and the effect of testosterone. </p>\n<p><strong>Cryptorchidism</strong> refers to the <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>one or both testes</strong> in the scrotal sac. The <strong>descent</strong> of the testis can be<strong> arrested</strong> in its <strong>usual path</strong> of descent (true <strong>undescended</strong> testis) or can <strong>migrate</strong> from the <strong>usual path</strong> of descent (<strong>ectopic</strong> testis).</p>\n<p>The image below shows the possible sites of the testes in cryptorchidism:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>A<strong> retractile testis</strong> can also lie in the inguinal canal and needs to be differentiated between an ectopic testes. It is present in the scrotum and <strong>occasionally</strong> moves into the <strong>inguinal canal. </strong></p>\n<p>An ectopic testis is treated with <strong>orchidopexy</strong>, while a retractile testis requires <strong>no intervention</strong> as it resolves spontaneously.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0915","text":"A 2-month-old infant is br​ought to the OPD for cons​ultation regarding an emp​ty scrotal sac. On examin‍ation B/L inguinal masses​ are palpable. What is th‍e ideal age for orchidope‍xy in this child?","raw_text":"A 2-month-old infant is brought to the OPD for consultation regarding an empty scrotal sac. On examination B/L inguinal masses are palpable. What is the ideal age for orchidopexy in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"At 6-1​0 years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"At 1-2​ years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"At pu​berty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt; 1 ye​ar of age","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. < 1 year of age","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/79a2606fdb4a45e0bd963b12f70b8657x1156x1468.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. &lt; 1 year of age</strong></p><p><strong>Orchidopexy</strong> is ideally performed <strong>before the boy reaches 12 months of age.</strong></p>\n<p>Orchidopexy is done before 12 months of age to avoid the following complications:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infertility</li>\n<li>Malignancy (Risk may still exist post surgery)</li>\n<li>Hernia</li>\n<li>Testicular torsion</li>\n<li>Infection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The management for undescended testes that are not palpable is:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1830","text":"A 1-year-old child was bro​ught to the OPD as the mo​ther noticed that the scr​otum contained only one t‍esticle. On examination, ​the testis is not palpabl‍e in the inguinal region ‍or abdomen. A diagnostic ​laparoscopy reveals abdom​inal testes and orchiopex​y is done. Which of the f​ollowing tumors is most l‌ikely to develop in this ‌patient?","raw_text":"A 1-year-old child was brought to the OPD as the mother noticed that the scrotum contained only one testicle. On examination, the testis is not palpable in the inguinal region or abdomen. A diagnostic laparoscopy reveals abdominal testes and orchiopexy is done. Which of the following tumors is most likely to develop in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Semi​noma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Yolk sa​c tumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mature ​teratoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chorioc​arcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Seminoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b0e955d93b10436dadd5e328da79c6bex1156x1468.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Seminoma</strong></p><p>The tumor most likely to develop in this patient is <strong>seminoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The tumor that develops most commonly in undesceded testes is <strong>germ cell tumor in situ</strong> or<strong> intratubular germ cell tumor</strong>. It commonly progresses into seminomas.</p>\n<p>In patients with a <strong>nonpalpable testicle</strong>, <strong>diagnostic laparoscopy</strong> is performed. If testis is still not visualised, the <strong>spermatic cord</strong> or <strong>testicular artery</strong> is traced at the <strong>deep inguinal ring</strong> to locate it.</p>\n<p>If a <strong>blind ending spermatic cord</strong> is found instead, <strong>vanishing testis syndrome</strong> (or testicular regression syndrome) is suspected. It is the partial or total absence of testicular tissue often due to an ischemic event in foetal life.</p>\n<p>The testis once found can be delivered into the scrotum, and an<strong> orchidopexy</strong> is performed.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4263","text":"A newborn was diagnosed wi​th undescended testes. Du​e to the intra-abdominal ​location of the testes, a‍ two-stage Stephen-Fowler​’s orchiopexy was planned‍. Which of the following ‍is performed during the f​irst stage of this proced​ure?","raw_text":"A newborn was diagnosed with undescended testes. Due to the intra-abdominal location of the testes, a two-stage Stephen-Fowler’s orchiopexy was planned. Which of the following is performed during the first stage of this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chevassu’s​ manoeuvre","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clipping of testicular ves​sels","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Clipping of cremasteric ve​ssels","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abdominal ​exploration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Clipping of testicular vessels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Clipping of testicular vessels</strong></p><p>In the <strong>first stage</strong> of a two-stage Stephens-Fowler approach, the <strong>testicular vessels</strong> are clipped. </p>\n<p>Clipping of the testicular artery promotes <strong>neo vasculogenesis</strong> along the vas deferens. It does not result in ischemia of the testis due to the presence of<strong> collaterals</strong> from the <strong>cremasteric artery</strong> and the <strong>artery to the ductus deferens</strong> (deferential artery).</p>\n<p>Several months later, after collaterals are established, the<strong> second stage</strong> is performed during which the testis and its new blood supply is mobilized into the scrotum.</p>\n<p>Stephen-Fowler’s approach is done when an intra abdominal testes is located too far to reach the scrotum. Alternatively, <strong>Silbar’s orchiopexy</strong> may be done. In this procedure, <strong>microvascular anastomosis</strong> of the <strong>testicular vessels</strong> with <strong>internal illiac vessels</strong> is done after the testis is mobilised to the scrotum.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4761","text":"What is the most common ca​use of testicular torsion​?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of testicular torsion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inversion o​f the testis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Presence of remnant of mul​lerian duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High investment of tunica ​vaginalis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pedicle between epididymis​ and body of testis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. High investment of tunica vaginalis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dac43beebaf54322998fc20a067cd87bx1280x2499.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2d9c8f0d3af14be8af41133c8971bf9ax398x326.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. High investment of tunica vaginalis</strong></p><p><strong>High investment of the tunica vaginalis </strong>is the most common cause of torsion of testis. It causes the testes to hang within the tunica like a<strong> clapper in a bell. </strong>This is the most common cause of testicular torsion in <strong>adolescents. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Bell clapper testes </strong>are typically <strong>bilateral</strong>. The image below shows the bell-clapper deformity of the testes:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other abnormalities that cause torsion of the testes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inversion of the testis:</strong> The testis is rotated so that it lies transversely or upside down.</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Pedicle between epididymis and body of testis:</strong> Separation of the epididymis from the body of the testis by a pedicle allows torsion of the testis on the pedicle that connects the testis with the epididymis.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An <strong>appendix testis</strong> is a small <strong>vestigial appendage</strong> located on the upper pole of the testis. Twisting of the appendage causes <strong>testicular appendix torsion. </strong>In it, a classic <strong>blue dot sign</strong> is seen on the upper pole of the testis. It is seen as a hard tender nodule with a blue discoloration on the superior aspect of the testis. This is a remnant of the Mullerian duct, that regresses on development.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the blue dot sign on the testis:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1850","text":"A 14-year-old boy presents​ with acute onset of pain​ in his scrotum. He previ​ously had similar pain th‍at would subside spontane​ously but the pain this t‍ime is unbearable. Physic‍al examination shows a sw​ollen erythematous scrotu​m. Cremasteric reflex is ​absent. What is the next ​best step in management?","raw_text":"A 14-year-old boy presents with acute onset of pain in his scrotum. He previously had similar pain that would subside spontaneously but the pain this time is unbearable.  Physical examination shows a swollen erythematous scrotum. Cremasteric reflex is absent. What is the next best step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Manual d​etorsion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Doppler u​ltrasound","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Surgical e​xploration","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Obser​vation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Surgical exploration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Surgical exploration</strong></p><p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The given clinical vignette is suggestive of </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">testicular torsion</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">. In cases of high clinical suspicion, the next best step is to </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">explore surgically</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">.</span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Clinical features of testicular torsion are <strong>painful</strong> <strong>scrotal swelling</strong>, erythema, nausea, and vomiting, testis seems high in the scrotum and the twisted cord can sometimes be palpated.</span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Signs seen on torsion of the testis are:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Deming’s sign</strong>: Spasm of the cremaster muscle due to twisting of the spermatic cord causes the affected testis to be positioned higher than the normal testis.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Angell’s sign</strong>: The testis on the normal side lies horizontally because of the mesorchium between the testis and epididymis.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Cremastic reflex</strong>: Absent on the side of the affected testis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Doppler ultrasound</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> scanning can confirm the absence of the blood supply to the affected testis, but the false positives are high and hence it is not routinely recommended.</span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"><strong>Manual detorsion</strong> of the testes is usually difficult because of acute pain during manipulation. Nonoperative manual detorsion is not a substitute for surgical exploration.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0215","text":"A child presents with pain​ and swelling in the righ​t side of his scrotum and​ testicular torsion is su‍spected. Which is the mos​t appropriate management?‍","raw_text":"A child presents with pain and swelling in the right side of his scrotum and testicular torsion is suspected. Which is the most appropriate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immediate exploration on b​oth symptomatic and asymp​tomatic side","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Immediate exploration on s​ymptomatic side only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Delayed exploration on bot​h symptomatic and asympto​matic side","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Delayed exploration on sym​ptomatic side only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Immediate exploration on both symptomatic and asymptomatic side","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Immediate exploration on both symptomatic and asymptomatic side</strong></p><p>In a case of <strong>suspected testicular torsion</strong>, <strong>immediate exploration</strong> needs to be done on <strong>both</strong> the symptomatic as well as the asymptomatic side. After the de-torsion, the adequacy of the blood flow has to be assessed by Doppler.</p>\n<p>In <strong>testicular torsion</strong>, the <strong>blood</strong> <strong>supply</strong> to the testicle is <strong>compromised</strong> due to the twisting of the spermatic cord within the<strong> tunica vaginalis</strong>, resulting in ischemia of the epididymis and the testis. The patient usually presents with <strong>acute onset pain</strong> and <strong>swelling</strong> of the <strong>scrotum</strong>. On examination, the <strong>cremasteric reflex is lost</strong>, and the <strong>scrotum</strong> looks <strong>asymmetrical</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Prehn’s sign</strong> is used to differentiate <strong>epididymitis</strong> from <strong>testicular</strong> <strong>torsion</strong>. Prehn’s sign is said to be positive when the pain gets relieved on lifting the testis, and it is seen in epididymo-orchitis.</p>\n<p>At the time of surgical exploration, <strong>contralateral</strong> testes also must be <strong>explored</strong> and <strong>fixed</strong> to the dartos fascia in order to prevent possible torsion occurring in this testes.</p>\n<p>More than 80% of the testis can be salvaged if surgery is performed within <strong>6 hours</strong>. If the testis is found to be necrosed on scrotal exploration, orchidectomy of the necrosed testis and orchidopexy of the contralateral testis is done.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4307","text":"A young man presents to th​e emergency with a painfu​l scrotum. On examination​, the patient is febrile ‍and a Prehn’s sign is pos​itive. What is the most l‍ikely cause of the patien‍t’s condition?","raw_text":"A young man presents to the emergency with a painful scrotum. On examination, the patient is febrile and a Prehn’s sign is positive. What is the most likely cause of the patient’s condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epididymal ​cyst rupture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Testicular appendix torsio​n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute epi​didymitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Testicular​ inversion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acute epididymitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acute epididymitis</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute epididymitis. </strong></p>\n<p>A <strong>positive Prehn’s sign</strong> is used to differentiate epididymis from testicular torsion. A Prehn’s sign is said to be positive when the <strong>pain gets relieved</strong> on <strong>lifting the testis</strong>. If it persists, it is likely to be testicular torsion.</p>\n<p><strong><em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em></strong> is the most common cause of acute epididymitis in young, sexually active males. In older men(&gt;40yrs) with bladder outflow obstruction, epididymitis results from <strong>urinary infection, </strong>most commonly due to <strong><em>Escherichia coli</em></strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Acute epididymitis</strong> is managed by <strong>antibiotics</strong> and scrotal support.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4308","text":"A young male patient prese​nted with painless swelli​ng of the scrotum. Examin​ation revealed a firm, en‍larged epididymis along w​ith beading of the vas de‍ferens. Testes were norma‍l. What is the next best ​step in management?","raw_text":"A young male patient presented with painless swelling of the scrotum. Examination revealed a firm, enlarged epididymis along with beading of the vas deferens. Testes were normal. What is the next best step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Start anti-tubercular ther​apy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Send urine or semen for PC​R","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Plan for ep​ididymectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perform a ​scrotal USG","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Send urine or semen for PCR","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Send urine or semen for PCR</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, a firm enlarged epididymis, beading of the vas deferens, and a normal testis on palpation indicate secondary<strong> tubercular epididymo-orchitis</strong>. The next best step is confirmation with <strong>polymerase chain reaction (PCR)</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Beading of the vas deferens</strong> is due to <strong>subepithelial tubercle</strong> formation and is <strong>specific</strong> to genital<strong> tuberculosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>A scrotal USG will reveal an enlarged epididymis but does not have diagnostic value. <strong>Chest, kidney, and upper urinary tract</strong> imaging may reveal a <strong>primary focus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tuberculous epididymo-orchitis is usually secondary and resolves once the primary focus is treated. Hence, a course of <strong>anti-tubercular therapy must be completed </strong>even if there is no evidence of disease elsewhere.</p>\n<p>If resolution does not occur within two months, <strong>epididymectomy</strong> or orchidectomy is advisable. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4756","text":"A 57-year-old diabetic pat​ient presented with a pai​nful, malodorous, discolo​red scrotum as shown in t‍he image. The patient was​ febrile and in extreme p‍ain. Which of the followi‍ng statements is true abo​ut his condition?","raw_text":"A 57-year-old diabetic patient presented with a painful, malodorous, discolored scrotum as shown in the image. The patient was febrile and in extreme pain. Which of the following statements is true about his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The testes remain unaffect​ed","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Managed by antibiotics and​ scrotal support","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Staphylococcus is the most​ common causative organis​m","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The condition is usually s​elf limiting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The testes remain unaffected","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7edb193c717b4aea89564ca66d95abe3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The testes remain unaffected</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Fournier's gangrene </strong>in which the<strong> testis </strong>is classically <strong>spared</strong>.</p>\n<p>It lies naked following debridement of the gangrenous skin and is tucked subcutaneously into the thigh. It is also called \"<strong>shameful exposure of the testis</strong>.\" </p>\n<p>Fournier’s gangrene is a<strong> surgical emergency</strong>. It is a <strong>life-threatening disease</strong>, with a mortality of over 40% even with treatment. It is due to a <strong>mixed infection</strong> of both <strong>aerobic</strong> and <strong>anaerobic</strong> bacterial flora.</p>\n<p>Concurrent illnesses that diminish the host defense, especially <strong>diabetes mellitus</strong> and <strong>alcoholism</strong>, predispose patients to the development of Fournier's gangrene. </p>\n<p>It is managed by debridement, wide surgical excision, IV fluids, and antibiotics.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1866","text":"A 24-year-old presents wit​h a scrotal swelling as s​hown in the image. The sw​elling is brilliantly tra‍nsilluminant and the test​es cannot be palpated sep‍arately. Which additional‍ clinical finding is most​ likely to be present?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old presents with a scrotal swelling as shown in the image. The swelling is brilliantly transilluminant and the testes cannot be palpated separately. Which additional clinical finding is most likely to be present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cough ​impulse","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cannot get above the swell​ing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fluct​uation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Reductio​n on rest","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fluctuation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d8401bd4e559450ea33f629df49e78f2.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fluctuation</strong></p><p>The scrotal swelling is most likely a <strong>hydrocele</strong>. Due to the <strong>movement of fluid</strong>, a <strong>fluctuation</strong> test is positive.</p>\n<p><strong>Hydrocoele</strong> is an <strong>accumulation of fluid</strong> within the<strong> tunica vaginalis</strong>. It is brilliantly <strong>transilluminating</strong> and testes will not be separately palpable. As it arises in the scrotum, it is possible to <strong>get above the swelling</strong>. It is irreducible and cough impulse is absent.</p>\n<p>A USG will show <strong>anechoic</strong> reflection due to fluid collection.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4760","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common cause of ​a secondary hydrocele ?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of a secondary hydrocele ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Torsion ​of testes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Testicul​ar tumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epididymo​-orchitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tra​uma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Epididymo-orchitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Epididymo-orchitis</strong></p><p>A secondary hydrocele is most commonly caused by an <strong>acute or chronic epididymo-orchitis</strong>. Other causes of secondary hydrocele include <strong>torsion of the testis </strong>and <strong>testicular tumors</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 260px;\" width=\"380\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 182px;\"><strong>Primary vaginal hydrocele</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 182px;\"><strong>Secondary vaginal hydrocele</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 182px;\">Due to defective absorption of fluid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 182px;\">Due to increased secretion of fluid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 182px;\">Tense swelling</td>\n<td style=\"width: 182px;\">Lax swelling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 182px;\">Testis not palpable separately</td>\n<td style=\"width: 182px;\">Testis palpable separately</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 182px;\">Transillumination positive (clear fluid present)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 182px;\">Transillumination negative (turbid fluid present)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1864","text":"A patient with hydrocele i​s scheduled for Lord’s pl​ication. Which of the fol​lowing is false regarding‍ the procedure?","raw_text":"A patient with hydrocele is scheduled for Lord’s plication. Which of the following is false regarding the procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The procedure can be perfo​rmed under local anesthes​ia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased risk of hematoma​ formation as compared to​ Jaboulay's","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Preferred for small to med​ium sized swellings","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tunica vaginalis is bunche​d to obliterate the hydro​coele sac","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increased risk of hematoma formation as compared to Jaboulay's","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increased risk of hematoma formation as compared to Jaboulay&#x27;s</strong></p><p><strong>Lord's plication</strong> has a <strong>lower risk</strong> of <strong>hematoma</strong> formation as compared to Jaboulay's procedure. </p>\n<p>Hydrocele may be managed surgically by various techniques. <strong>Lord's plication</strong> and <strong>Jaboulay's eversion of the sac</strong> are most commonly performed. </p>\n<p><strong>Jaboulay's eversion of the sac</strong> is preferred for<strong> large hydroceles</strong>. In this method, the <strong>tunica vaginalis sac</strong> is <strong>everted</strong> around the <strong>testis.</strong> Although it has a shorter operative time, the risk of hematoma formation is more. </p>\n<p><strong>Congenital hydroceles</strong> are managed by a <strong>herniotomy</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4759","text":"A 32-year-old male patient​ presented with a scrotal​ swelling. On examination​, the testis is felt sepa‍rate from the scrotal swe​lling and the swelling is‍ brilliantly transillumin‍ant, as shown in the imag​e below. What is the prob​able diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old male patient presented with a scrotal swelling. On examination, the testis is felt separate from the scrotal swelling and the swelling is brilliantly transilluminant, as shown in the image below. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Varic​ocele","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epididy​mal cyst","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sperma​tocele","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chyl​ocele","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Epididymal cyst","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/46722655457c465badbeeb27eadd0970.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3199100be6384a299ea693f1122b3373x904x484.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Epididymal cyst</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a <strong>transilluminant scrotal swelling</strong> palpated <strong>apart from normal testes</strong> is suggestive of an <strong>epididymal cyst</strong>. </p>\n<p>An<strong> epididymal cyst</strong> is a <strong>multilocular</strong> cyst formed due to is the cystic degeneration of the epididymis. Multiple cysts formed posterior to the epididymis can feel like a <strong>bunch of grapes on palpation</strong>. Due to the presence of <strong>crystal clear fluid</strong>, an epididymal cyst brilliantly transilluminates and has a <strong>Chinese lantern appearance</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image shows the Chinese lantern appearance on transillumination:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><br/>It can be differentiated from a hydrocele, where the testis is not separately palpable.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Varicoceles </strong>present with dragging discomfort in the scrotum on standing and characteristic ‘<strong>bag of worms’</strong> feel on palpation. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Spermatocele</strong> is a <strong>unilocular</strong> transluminant retention cyst formed at the epididymal head. Due to the presence of spermatozoa, <strong>barley-water</strong> fluid is seen. Although <strong>transilluminant</strong>, the cyst does not have a Chinese lantern appearance.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Chyloceles</strong> are formed due to the rupture of lymphatic varix and the discharge of chyle into the hydrocele. Fluid rich in cholesterol is seen and the swelling is not transilluminant.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1862","text":"A 33-year-old man was refe​rred to the surgery OPD f​rom the infertility clini​c. On inspection, the scr‍otum appeared as in the i​mage. On palpation, a bag‍ of worms sensation was f‍elt. What is the appropri​ate management?","raw_text":"A 33-year-old man was referred to the surgery OPD from the infertility clinic. On inspection, the scrotum appeared as in the image. On palpation, a bag of worms sensation was felt. What is the appropriate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NSAIDs and scrotal support​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Palomo p​rocedure","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Keetley-Tor​ek procedure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Beevan p​rocedure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Palomo procedure","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fd45538553964ab48fe571c5b9518a3c.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Palomo procedure</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>varicocele</strong>. As this patient is young and <strong>infertile</strong>, <strong>varicocelectomy</strong> is indicated and a <strong>Palomo</strong> <strong>procedure</strong> may be performed. </p>\n<p><strong>Varicocele</strong> may present with a <strong>dull aching scrotal pain</strong> and <strong>enlargement</strong>. Additionally, patients may have <strong>infertility</strong>. A <strong>bag of worms</strong>, which gets prominent on Valsalva’s maneuver is palpated due to the engorged pampiniform plexus.</p>\n<p>Often, patients may be asymptomatic and do not require treatment.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 148px;\" width=\"266\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 108.1875px;\"><strong>Grades of varicocele</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.1875px;\">Grade 1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.828125px;\">Varicocele only palpable on Valsalva’s maneuver</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.1875px;\">Grade 2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.828125px;\">Varicocele palpable without Valsalva’s maneuver</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108.1875px;\">Grade 3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.828125px;\">Varicocele is seen on inspection of the testis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Left-sided varicocele</strong> is more <strong>common</strong>. <strong>Isolated right-sided varicocele</strong> should always raise suspicion of <strong>renal cell carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>(<strong>Note: </strong>Varicoceles occurs on left side more commonly as the left spermatic vein opens at sharp angle to the left renal vein, whereas, on the right side, the right spermatic vein opens into a larger Inferior vena cava and an isolated Right-sided varicocele may indicate a tumour that compresses the venous return in the inferior vena cava)</p>\n<p>Treatment of <strong>young</strong> patients includes surgical <strong>ligation</strong> of the <strong>gonadal vein</strong>. It may be achieved through <strong>embolectomy</strong> or <strong>varicocelectomy</strong>. Approaches to varicocelectomy are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Inguinal ( Ivanisevic method )</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Retroperitoneal (Palomo procedure) </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Natural orifice transluminal endoscopic surgery through the transvesical route</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In <strong>older</strong> patients, <strong>NSAIDs</strong> and <strong>scrotal support</strong> are adequate. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4753","text":"Which of the following is ​not a risk factor for dev​eloping testicular carcin​oma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a risk factor for developing testicular carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Undescend​ed testes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Klinefleter​’s syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Benzopyren​e exposure","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"History of contralateral t​esticular tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Benzopyrene exposure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Benzopyrene exposure</strong></p><p>Benzopyrene exposure is <strong>not associated</strong> with an increased risk of developing <strong>testicular cancer</strong> but with an increased risk of developing <strong>scrotal carcinoma. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Shale oil exposure </strong>(a petroleum byproduct) is also associated with an increased incidence of scrotal carcinoma. </p>\n<p><strong>Undescended testes</strong>, history of <strong>contralateral testicular tumor,</strong> and <strong>Klinefelter's syndrome</strong> are known to increase one’s risk to develop <strong>testicular cancer</strong>. </p>\n<p>Note: Patients with Klinefelter’s syndrome also have an increased risk of developing <strong>extragonadal germ cell tumors</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4750","text":"Which among the following ​are germ-cell tumors? Ley​dig cell tumor Embryonal ​cell carcinoma Choriocarc‍inoma Sertoli cell tumor ​Teratoma Yolk-sac tumor","raw_text":"<p>Which among the following are germ-cell tumors?</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Leydig cell tumor </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Embryonal cell carcinoma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Choriocarcinoma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sertoli cell tumor</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Teratoma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Yolk-sac tumor</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2,3​,4,6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,​5,6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,​3,4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2,3​,5,6","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2,3,5,6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2,3,5,6</strong></p><p>Germ-cell tumors (90–95%) include <strong>seminoma</strong>, <strong>embryonal cell carcinoma</strong>, <strong>yolk-sac tumor</strong>, <strong>teratoma,</strong> and <strong>choriocarcinoma</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Leydig</strong> and <strong>Sertoli</strong> cell tumors are non-germ-cell tumors.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3179","text":"A 45-year-old male patient​ who presented with a scr​otal swelling underwent a​n orchidectomy. The image‍ below shows the histopat​hology findings. What is ‍your diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old male patient who presented with a scrotal swelling underwent an orchidectomy. The image below shows the histopathology findings. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Semi​noma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Yolk-sa​c tumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Embryonal ce​ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tera​toma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Seminoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0c2f1481a3ea435f8c6c3c12c15f9140.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bc031255ed9c49f68b4ff4182a27962bx1024x1280.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/873d6e87b0a645639e54144cdfa8f710x1280x852.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4121c1d5878f4e34bc9aa516386a46e1x1031x1280.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Seminoma</strong></p><p>Based on the gross and microscopic appearance of the specimen, the diagnosis is <strong>seminoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The gross specimen shows a well-circumscribed, pale, <strong>gray-white, fleshy</strong>, homogenous mass, with a <strong>lobulated cut surface</strong>, usually devoid of hemorrhage or necrosis. Histopathology shows <strong>uniform tumor cells</strong> with abundant <strong>clear cytoplasm</strong>, distinct cell borders, and <strong>large central nuclei</strong>. There is a sparse lymphocytic infiltrate. Classically, they have a <strong>fried egg appearance</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Yolk-sac tumor</strong> has a <strong>yellowish-white, mucinous</strong> appearance. Histologically, they are typically composed of a lace-like (reticular) network of medium-sized cuboidal, flattened, or spindled cells, which generally show less cytologic atypia than embryonal carcinoma. In approximately 50% of tumors, structures resembling endodermal sinuses (<strong>Schiller-Duval bodies</strong>) may be seen; these consist of a mesodermal core with a central capillary and a visceral and parietal layer of cells resembling primitive glomeruli. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Embryonal cell carcinoma</strong> appears <strong>variegated</strong> with foci of <strong>hemorrhage</strong> or <strong>necrosis.</strong><strong> </strong> Histologically, the cells grow in alveolar or tubular patterns, sometimes with papillary folds. More undifferentiated lesions may display sheets of cells with cleft-like spaces. The neoplastic cells have an epithelial appearance, are large and anaplastic, and have hyperchromatic nuclei with prominent nucleoli. The cell borders are usually indistinct, and there is considerable variation in cell and nuclear size and shape (pleomorphism). Mitotic figures and tumor giant cells are frequently seen<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Teratomas</strong> also have a <strong>variegated</strong> appearance but have <strong>cysts</strong> reflecting the<strong> multiplicity of tissue types</strong>. Histologically, collections of differentiated cells or organoid structures originating in all 3 germ layers can be seen. Elements may be mature (resembling various adult tissues) or immature (sharing histologic features with fetal or embryonic tissue).<strong> </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4734","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g testicular tumors?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding testicular tumors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They may present as an abd​ominal lump.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Testicular biopsy is done ​to diagnose testicular ca​rcinoma.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Seminomas metastasize pred​ominantly via the lymphat​ic system.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elevated tumor markers are​ found almost exclusively​ in non-seminomatous germ​-cell tumors.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Testicular biopsy is done to diagnose testicular carcinoma.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Testicular biopsy is done to diagnose testicular carcinoma.</strong></p><p>There is <strong>no role of </strong><strong>testicular biopsy in the diagnosis of testicular tumors</strong>. This is due to the risk of <strong>seeding tumor cells </strong>onto the scrotal wall.</p>\n<p>In <strong>testicular tumors</strong>, the most common site of lymphatic spread is <strong>retroperitoneal lymph nodes </strong>which may present as an abdominal lump. </p>\n<p>Elevated tumor markers are found almost exclusively in non-seminomatous germ-cell tumors.  Few seminomas may have elevated <strong>placental alkaline phosphatase</strong> (PLAP) or <strong>beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (HCG).</strong> Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) can be used as a marker of <strong>tumor bulk.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Seminomas</strong> are radiosensitive tumors that metastasize via lymphatics. Non-germ-cell tumors arise from <strong>Sertoli or Leydig cells </strong>and follow a more <strong>benign</strong> course. Approximately only 10% are malignant.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4736","text":"A patient with a left-side​d testicular tumor is bei​ng evaluated for metastas​is. Which group of lymph ‍nodes is most likely to g​et involved first?","raw_text":"A patient with a left-sided testicular tumor is being evaluated for metastasis. Which group of lymph nodes is most likely to get involved first?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deep inguina​l lymph nodes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Precaval l​ymph nodes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Preaortic ​lymph nodes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Para-aortic​ lymph nodes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Para-aortic lymph nodes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Para-aortic lymph nodes</strong></p><p>The first sites of metastasis in left-sided testicular tumors are the <strong>para-aortic lymph nodes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Testicular tumors metastasize first to the <strong>retroperitoneal nodes. </strong><strong>Right-sided</strong> tumors metastasize primarily to the<strong> interaortocaval nodes. </strong><strong>Left-sided</strong> tumors metastasize primarily to the left <strong>para-aortic nodes.</strong></p>\n<p>Deep inguinal lymph nodes receive drainage from the perineum and lower extremity.<strong> Inguinal lymph nodes</strong> may be involved if the testicular tumor spreads to the <strong>skin of the scrotum.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1867","text":"A 25-year-old male patient​ presented with a hard pa​inless testicular lump wi​th a dragging sensation. ‍The blood investigations ​showed raised AFP levels.‍ Which of the following i‍s least likely to be incl​uded, in further manageme​nt of this case? AFP- Alp​ha Fetoprotein","raw_text":"<p>A 25-year-old male patient presented with a hard painless testicular lump with a dragging sensation. The blood investigations showed raised AFP levels. Which of the following is least likely to be included, in further management of this case?</p>\n<p>AFP- Alpha Fetoprotein</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orchidectomy via scrotal i​ncision","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ultrasonography of the tes​tis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CT of the abdomen and pelv​is","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Combination ​chemotherapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Orchidectomy via scrotal incision","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Orchidectomy via scrotal incision</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>non-seminomatous germ cell tumor (NSGCT) </strong>of the testis. Orchidectomy via a <strong>scrotal approach</strong> is not done due to fear of seeding the tumor in the scrotal skin. Instead, an <strong>inguinal approach</strong> is used.</p>\n<p><strong>Raised AFP levels</strong> point towards the diagnosis of<strong> non-seminomatous germ cell tumor (NSGCT) </strong>of the testis.</p>\n<p><strong>Ultrasound of the testis</strong> should be done in all suspected cases of testicular tumor. <strong>Tumor markers</strong> (AFP, hCG, LDH) are measured for the staging of cancer. <strong>CT </strong>of the <strong>chest, abdomen, </strong>and <strong>pelvis</strong> is done to detect metastasis.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 303px;\" width=\"470\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 149.34375px;\"><strong>Management of testicular tumors</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149.34375px;\">\n<p><strong>Stage</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.34375px;\"><strong>Non-seminomatous</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.34375px;\"><strong>Seminomatous</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149.34375px;\">\n<p>Stage I </p>\n<p>Tumor confined to testis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.34375px;\">Orchidectomy and chemotherapy<br/><br/></td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.34375px;\">\n<p>Orchidectomy and Active Surveillance</p>\n<p>If poor prognostic features* are seen, chemotherapy with a single agent, cisplatin for 1-2 cycles</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149.34375px;\">\n<p>Stage II</p>\n<p>Nodal disease confined to nodes below the diaphragm</p>\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 149.34375px;\">Orchidectomy and chemotherapy<br/><br/>Retroperitoneal lymph node dissection for residual disease </td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.34375px;\">\n<p>Orchidectomy and <strong>radiotherapy</strong></p>\n<p><br/>If residual lymph node disease is present, chemotherapy </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149.34375px;\">\n<p>Stage III</p>\n<p>Nodal disease present above the diaphragm</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 149.34375px;\">\n<p>Orchidectomy, <strong>radiotherapy</strong>, and chemotherapy</p>\n<p><br/>If residual lymph node disease is present, retroperitoneal lymph node dissection is done<br/><br/></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 149.34375px;\">\n<p>Stage IV </p>\n<p>Metastasis</p>\n</td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 149.34375px;\">Orchidectomy and chemotherapy<br/><br/></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 149.34375px;\">\n<p>In testicular tumors, the BEP regimen is followed with <strong>bleomycin, etoposide, cisplatin</strong>. </p>\n<p>*Poor prognostic factors following orchidectomy are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">No decrease or change in tumor marker values or tumor size</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Poor follow up</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4309","text":"A patient with Stage I sem​inoma underwent an orchid​ectomy 3 months ago. On a​ follow-up visit, elevate‍d LDH levels were seen. W​hat is the most appropria‍te line of management?","raw_text":"A patient with Stage I seminoma underwent an orchidectomy 3 months ago. On a follow-up visit, elevated LDH levels were seen. What is the most appropriate line of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radiatio​n therapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Platinum-based chemotherap​y","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bleomycin -Etoposide -cis-​Platinum regimen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Regular scanning using CT ​and tumor marker measurem​ent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Platinum-based chemotherapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Platinum-based chemotherapy</strong></p><p><strong>Platinum-based therapy</strong> is the primary treatment of <strong>stage I seminomas </strong>following orchidectomy for patients with poor prognostic factors such as elevated tumor markers.<strong> </strong>It consists of<strong> </strong>a cycle<strong> </strong>of single-agent<strong> carboplatin.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 880.261px;\" width=\"456\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 23.1543px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 415.118px; height: 23.1543px;\"><strong>Management of testicular tumors</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 76.7773px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 132.344px; height: 76.7773px;\">\n<p><strong>Stage</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.172px; height: 76.7773px;\">\n<p><strong>Seminomatous  </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.602px; height: 76.7773px;\">\n<p><strong>Non-seminomatous  </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 137.559px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 132.344px; height: 137.559px;\">\n<p>Stage I: Restricted to testis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.172px; height: 137.559px;\">\n<p>Orchidectomy and active surveillance</p>\n<p>Single cycle carboplatin*</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.602px; height: 137.559px;\">\n<p>Orchidectomy and active surveillance</p>\n<p>BEP chemotherapy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 294.307px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 132.344px; height: 294.307px;\">Stage II: Involving testis and para-aortic lymph node</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.172px; height: 294.307px;\">\n<p>Orchidectomy and chemotherapy (BEP)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If there is a complete response → observe</li>\n<li>- If residual disease → RPLND or RT**</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.602px; height: 294.307px;\">\n<p>Orchidectomy and chemotherapy (BEP)</p>\n<p>If residual lymph node disease is present, RPLND is done</p>\n </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 143.955px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 132.344px; height: 143.955px;\">Stage III: Metastasis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.172px; height: 143.955px;\">\n<p>Orchidectomy and chemotherapy (BEP)+ RPLND and RT with or without metastatectomy.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.602px; height: 143.955px;\">\n<p>Orchidectomy and chemotherapy (BEP) + RPLND with or without metastasectomy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 204.508px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 415.118px; height: 204.508px;\">\n<p>In testicular tumors, the BEP regimen is followed with <strong>bleomycin, etoposide, cisplatin</strong>. </p>\n<p>*Poor prognostic factors following orchidectomy are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">No decrease or change in tumor marker values or tumor size</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Poor follow up</li>\n</ul>\n<p>**RPLND: Retroperitoneal Lymph node Dissection; RT: Radiotherapy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"3yw1J4Hb","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"44_diaphragm","title":"44.Diaphragm","num_questions":9,"total_marks":36,"duration":19,"time_per_question":126.67,"questions":[{"id":"MC9317","text":"Which of the following doe​s not supply the diaphrag‌m?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not supply the diaphragm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Musculophr​enic artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior ph​renic artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pericardioph​renic artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Middle phr​enic artery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Middle phrenic artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Middle phrenic artery</strong></p><p>The diaphragm is not supplied by the middle phrenic artery</p>\n<p>The <strong>blood supply of diaphragm</strong> is via (Mnemonic - <strong>Simple</strong>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S</strong>uperior phrenic arteries</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>nferior phrenic arteries</li>\n<li><strong>M</strong>usculophrenic arteries</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>ericardiophrenic arteries</li>\n<li><strong>L</strong>ower 5 intercostal and subcostal arteries</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9215","text":"The structure passing thro​ugh the central tendon of‌ the diaphragm is:","raw_text":"The structure passing through the central tendon of the diaphragm is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oesop​hagus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ao​rta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior ​vena cava","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sympathe​tic Chain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inferior vena cava","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/53e0cadbe05649db9d15da8a233616d8x1279x1382.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inferior vena cava</strong></p><p>The <strong>inferior vena cava</strong> passes through the <strong>central tendon of the diaphragm</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1537","text":"A cardiothoracic surgeon p​erforming a heart transpl‌ant accidentally damages ‌the right phrenic nerve. ​Where does this structure‌ pass through?","raw_text":"A cardiothoracic surgeon performing a heart transplant accidentally damages the right phrenic nerve. Where does this structure pass through?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/737170faac9d42f9b16620e55ea34508.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3f41a7e8b2104d94b500b45a02651c90x1280x1382.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3</strong></p><p>The <strong>right phrenic nerve</strong><strong> </strong>passes through the <strong>central tendon</strong> of the diaphragm, either <strong>by</strong> the<strong> caval hiatus</strong> (labeled <strong>3</strong>) or just <strong>lateral </strong>to it.</p>\n<p>Other structures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1 - Medial arcuate ligament </li>\n<li>2 - Aortic opening </li>\n<li>4 - Esophageal opening</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>left phrenic nerve</strong> passes through the muscular part of the diaphragm <strong>anterior</strong> to the<strong> central tendon</strong>, just lateral to the left cardiac surface and more anterior than the right phrenic nerve.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical significance:</strong></p>\n<p>Phrenic nerve injury can occur due to cardiothoracic surgery and can present as diaphragmatic paralysis. On chest X-ray, it appears as elevation of hemidiaphragm.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1543","text":"Which of the following str​uctures passes through th‌e area marked A in the gi‌ven diagram?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures passes through the area marked A in the given diagram?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right phr​enic nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sympathe​tic trunk","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vagus​ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Splanchn​ic nerves","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sympathetic trunk","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a2cd962f0ed14e98a35c337fb61fc34a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5729b3d41f634bbaae8a15abe461614dx1279x1382.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sympathetic trunk</strong></p><p>The area marked A represents the <strong>medial arcuate ligament</strong>. The thoracic<strong> sympathetic trunk </strong>passes <strong>posterior </strong>to it and becomes the lumbar sympathetic trunk.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1538","text":"A gasteroenterologist is p​erforming an esophagoscop‌y in a patient with sever‌e acid reflux. At which l​evel will he encounter it‌s diaphragmatic constrict‍ion?","raw_text":"A gasteroenterologist is performing an esophagoscopy in a patient with severe acid reflux. At which level will he encounter its diaphragmatic constriction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T​8","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T​9","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T​10","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"T​12","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. T10","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5b6bcb3eaaf74e34bcfe8ac18bcc5372x1279x1382.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. T10</strong></p><p>The <strong>esophageal hiatus</strong> is located at the level of the <strong>T10 vertebra, </strong>and it forms a <strong>normal constriction </strong>of the esophagus. It transmits the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Esophagus</li>\n<li>Vagal trunks</li>\n<li>Gastric nerves</li>\n<li>Esophageal branches of the left gastric vessels and</li>\n<li>Some lymphatic vessels</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical significance:</strong></p>\n<p>Anatomical constrictions of the esophagus are important landmarks in esophagoscopy. These are 4 in number and located at a distance from the upper incisors as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>15cm - Cricopharyngeus muscle</li>\n<li>22.5cm - Aortic arch</li>\n<li>27.5cm - Left bronchus</li>\n<li>37.5cm - Diaphragmatic opening</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1539","text":"A vascular surgeon is oper​ating on a case of aortic‌ dissection of the thorac‌ic aorta. He suspects tha​t the dissection has exte‌nded into the abdominal a‍orta, and approaches the ‌aortic hiatus of the diap​hragm to localize the ext​ension. Which of the foll‌owing structures will be ‌present here?","raw_text":"A vascular surgeon is operating on a case of aortic dissection of the thoracic aorta. He suspects that the dissection has extended into the abdominal aorta, and approaches the aortic hiatus of the diaphragm to localize the extension. Which of the following structures will be present here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left gastric vein and thor​acic duct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right vagus nerve and thor​acic duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Azygous vein and thoracic ​duct","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Left vagus nerve and thora​cic duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Azygous vein and thoracic duct","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Azygous vein and thoracic duct</strong></p><p>The <strong>azygous vein</strong> and the <strong>thoracic duct</strong> are present at the <strong>aortic hiatus. </strong>These are transmitted along with the thoracic aorta. </p>\n<p><strong>Clinical significance:</strong></p>\n<p>Aortic dissection occurs when there's an <strong>intimal tear</strong> in the aorta. It presents with <strong>tearing interscapular pain</strong> and extension of dissection into the abdominal aorta can present with <strong>abdominal pain</strong>. <strong>CT/MRI</strong> are the investigations of choice. Aortic dissections are classified using the <strong>DeBakey and Stanford</strong> systems. Depending on the classification, aortic dissections are managed with <strong>aggressive antihypertensive</strong> therapy or immediate <strong>surgical intervention</strong> (open/endovascular).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1041","text":"Which of the following doe​s not pass through the st‌ructure marked A?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not pass through the structure marked A?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ao​rta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Azygou​s vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thorac​ic duct","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vagal​ trunk","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vagal trunk","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0ce341b9e0e44cff93fa8babfd90d5d0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3a829c15211d4569b028c4370ba2d8aex1279x1382.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vagal trunk</strong></p><p>The structure marked A is the <strong>aortic opening</strong> of the diaphragm.</p>\n<p>The <strong>vagal trunk</strong> does not pass through the aortic opening of the diaphragm. It passes through the <strong>esophageal opening</strong> at the level of <strong>T10 vertebra</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1541","text":"A patient with advanced es​ophageal cancer in the lo‌wer third of the esophagu‌s is undergoing transhiat​al esophagectomy. The sur‌geon enlarges the esophag‍eal hiatus with four fing‌ers. All of the following​ structures are likely to​ be injured, except:","raw_text":"A patient with advanced esophageal cancer in the lower third of the esophagus is undergoing transhiatal esophagectomy. The surgeon enlarges the esophageal hiatus with four fingers. All of the following structures are likely to be injured, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left gastric artery branch​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right va​gus nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left vag​us nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left phre​nic nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Left phrenic nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Left phrenic nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>left phrenic nerve</strong> is unlikely to be injured as does not pass through any specific opening in the diaphragm. It passes through the <strong>muscular part of the diaphragm</strong> anterior to the central tendon, just lateral to the left cardiac surface and more anterior than the right phrenic nerve.</p>\n<p><strong>Structures passing through the esophageal hiatus</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Esophagus</li>\n<li>Vagal trunks</li>\n<li>Gastric nerves</li>\n<li>Esophageal branches of the left gastric vessels </li>\n<li>Some lymphatic vessels</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Clinical significance:</strong></p>\n<p>In carcinoma esophagus involving the lower third, the Orringer approach or transhiatal esophagectomy is done. The stomach and lower part of esophagus are mobilised via laparotomy and the upper part is mobilized via the left side of the neck. Blunt dissection is done at the hiatus to approach the thorax from the abdomen. Other alternatives to this procedure are robotic and thoracoscopic techniques </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1540","text":"All of the structures pass​ posterior to the crus of‌ diaphragm except ____","raw_text":"All of the structures pass posterior to the crus of diaphragm except ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ao​rta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Azygou​s vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thorac​ic duct","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Greater spla​nchnic nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Greater splanchnic nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3e12ccf6d11140148d19329b1cedd133x1279x1382.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Greater splanchnic nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>greater splanchnic nerve </strong>usually pierces <strong>through the crus </strong>of the diaphragm to enter the thorax and<strong> does not pass posterior</strong> to the crus of the diaphragm.</p>\n<p>The <strong>aortic hiatus</strong> is present <strong>posterior to</strong> the <strong>crus</strong> of the diaphragm, hence the following structures are likely to be damaged-</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aorta</li>\n<li>Thoracic duct</li>\n<li>Azygous vein</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"56JLn184","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"44_methods_of_pest_control","title":"44.Methods Of Pest Control","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MB7748","text":"Which of the following wou​ld you advise as the best​ approach to control arth​ropods?","raw_text":"Which of the following would you advise as the best approach to control arthropods?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Environmen​tal control","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chemical​ control","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Biologica​l control","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Genetic​ control","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Environmental control","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Environmental control</strong></p><p><strong>Environmental control</strong> is the<strong> best</strong> approach to control arthropods.</p>\n<p>The <strong>principles of arthropod control</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Environmental control - Best approach</li>\n<li>Chemical control <strong>-</strong> Use of insecticides, pesticides, etc.</li>\n<li>Biological control<strong> -</strong> Use of larvivorous fish like Gambusia</li>\n<li>Genetic control</li>\n<li>Newer methods -\n<ul>\n<li>Chemosterilants</li>\n<li>Insect growth regulators</li>\n<li>Sex attractants</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Integrated approach involves usage of more than one modality of arthropod control</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7913","text":"Which of the following is ​the best method to contro​l houseflies?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the best method to control houseflies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eliminating breeding place​s","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Using spa​ce sprays","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Raising larvivorous fishes​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Using resi​dual sprays","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Eliminating breeding places","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Eliminating breeding places</strong></p><p>The best method to control <strong>houseflies</strong> is environmental control by the <strong>elimination</strong> of <strong>breeding places</strong>.</p>\n<p>They<strong> </strong>have shown<strong> resistance</strong> to a number of<strong> insecticides</strong>, including DDT.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7989","text":"Which of the following is ​not an organo-phosphate i​nsecticide?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an organo-phosphate insecticide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diel​drin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fent​hion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mala​thion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diaz​inon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dieldrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dieldrin</strong></p><p><strong>Dieldrin</strong> is an <strong>organo-chlorine</strong> insecticide. It is a broad-spectrum insecticide that acts as a contact, stomach, and inhalation poison.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7990","text":"Which of the following is ​not an aryl organo-phosph​ate insecticide?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an aryl organo-phosphate insecticide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mala​thion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Para​thion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlor​thion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diaz​inon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Malathion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Malathion</strong></p><p><strong>Malathion</strong> is an <strong>alkyl </strong>organophosphate insecticide. It is used as an ultra-low volume spray in mosquito control.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8011","text":"Which of the following ins​ecticides is least toxic ​to man?","raw_text":"Which of the following insecticides is least toxic to man?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mala​thion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diaz​inon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Para​thion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlorp​yrifos","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Malathion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Malathion</strong></p><p>Among the given options,<strong> malathion</strong> is the <strong>least toxic</strong> to man. It is the least toxic organo-phosphate insecticide.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8002","text":"Which of the following is ​false about fenthion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about fenthion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is used as a residual s​pray","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is used a​s a repellent","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is a sto​mach poison","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It has larvi​cidal action","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is used as a repellent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is used as a repellent</strong></p><p>Fenthion is not used as a <strong>repellant</strong>. A commonly used repellant is N,N-diethyl-m-toluamide (<strong>DEET</strong>) that is used for protection from mosquitoes and ticks.</p>\n<p><strong>Fenthion</strong> is a broad-spectrum insecticide that acts as both <strong>contact</strong> and <strong>stomach poison</strong>. It is used as a<strong> larvicide</strong> in mosquito control and also as a<strong> residual spray</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8003","text":"Which of the following is ​false about pyrethrum?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about pyrethrum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Residual action as good as​ DDT","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vegetabl​e origin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Contact​ poison","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Can be used synergisticall​y with DDT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Residual action as good as DDT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Residual action as good as DDT</strong></p><p><strong>Pyrethrum</strong> does <strong>not</strong> have a <strong>residual action</strong> like DDT or other synthetic insecticides. So, most <strong>space sprays</strong> contain <strong>pyrethrum and DDT</strong> for <strong>synergistic</strong> action.</p>\n<p>Pyrethrum is a natural insecticide of vegetable origin. It is extracted from the flowers of <strong><em>Chrysanthemum cinerariafolium</em></strong>. It kills <strong>adult</strong> <strong>mosquitoes </strong>and other insects instantly on mere contact.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7804","text":"Which of the following can​not be used to describe p​yrethrum?","raw_text":"Which of the following cannot be used to describe pyrethrum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nerve ​poison","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contact​ poison","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Space​ spray","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Residua​l spray","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Residual spray","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Residual spray</strong></p><p><strong>Pyrethrum</strong> is not used as a residual spray. It is a <strong>contact</strong> and<strong> nerve</strong> poison that is also used as a <strong>space spray</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7992","text":"Which of the following is ​a synthetic pyrethroid in​secticide?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a synthetic pyrethroid insecticide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prop​oxur","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perme​thrin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Para​thion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyr​olan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Permethrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Permethrin</strong></p><p><strong>Permethrin</strong> is a synthetic<strong> pyrethroid</strong>. Pyrethroids are synthetic insecticides, which are derived from naturally occurring pyrethrins. Pyrethroids modify the sodium channels in neuronal membranes and thereby disrupt the electrical signalling in the nervous system. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7916","text":"Which of the following vec​tors has not shown resist​ance to synthetic pyrethr​oids, in a widespread man‍ner in India yet?","raw_text":"Which of the following vectors has not shown resistance to synthetic pyrethroids, in a widespread manner in India yet?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Musca d​omestica","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anopheles​ stephensi","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aedes ​egyptii","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phlebotomus​ argentipes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Phlebotomus argentipes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Phlebotomus argentipes</strong></p><p><strong>Sandflies</strong> (<strong>Phlebotomus</strong>) have not shown resistance to synthetic pyrethroids, in a widespread manner in India yet.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8000","text":"Which of the following bes​t describes DDT?","raw_text":"Which of the following best describes DDT?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stomach​ poison","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Repe​llant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Contact​ poison","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fumi​gant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Contact poison","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Contact poison</strong></p><p><strong>DDT</strong> is a <strong>contact poison</strong> that belongs to the organochlorine family. It has been widely used but a number of organisms have become resistant to its effects now.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8007","text":"During a dengue outbreak, ​the municipal corporation​ plans a DDT spraying ini​tiative. Which of the fol‍lowing is false about thi​s insecticide?","raw_text":"During a dengue outbreak, the municipal corporation plans a DDT spraying initiative. Which of the following is false about this insecticide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Para-para isomer is the mo​st active fraction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It acts as ​a neurotoxin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It has residual action up ​to 18 months","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It causes immediate death ​of insects","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It causes immediate death of insects","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It causes immediate death of insects</strong></p><p><strong>DDT</strong> does not cause the immediate death of insects. It <strong>takes several hours</strong> for its action to be complete.</p>\n<p>It contains<strong> 70 – 80% </strong>of <strong>para-para isomer</strong> that is the<strong> most active</strong> fraction of DDT. It acts on the nervous system of insects. The <strong>residual action</strong> of DDT may last as long as <strong>18 months</strong> depending upon the treated surface.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7803","text":"Which of the following is ​true about the usage of P​aris green for mosquito c​ontrol?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the usage of Paris green for mosquito control?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti-adul​t measure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Effective against Anophele​s","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Harmful in normal doses to​ man and animals","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nerve ​poison","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Effective against Anopheles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Effective against Anopheles</strong></p><p><strong>Paris green</strong> is very <strong>effective</strong> against surface-feeding <strong>Anopheles</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>anti-larval </strong>measure and is a <strong>stomach poison</strong>. It is not harmful in normal doses to man and animals.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7800","text":"Which of the following mos​quitoes can be controlled​ by destroying aquatic pl​ants with herbicides?","raw_text":"Which of the following mosquitoes can be controlled by destroying aquatic plants with herbicides?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mans​onia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cu​lex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ae​des","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anop​heles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mansonia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mansonia</strong></p><p><strong>Mansonia</strong> mosquitoes<strong> breed</strong> in water with <strong>aquatic vegetation,</strong> and destroying this vegetation with <strong>herbicides</strong> would <strong>control</strong> this mosquito.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7805","text":"Which of the following is ​not a mosquito anti-larva​l measure?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a mosquito anti-larval measure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Minera​l oils","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gambus​ia fish","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paris​ green","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyre​thrum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pyrethrum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pyrethrum</strong></p><p><strong>Pyrethrum</strong> is an <strong>anti-adult measure </strong>and not an anti-larval measure.</p>\n<p>Mineral oils, Gambusia, and Paris green are anti-larval measures used in mosquito control.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8013","text":"Which among the following ​insecticides is most wide​ly used as a space spray?​","raw_text":"Which among the following insecticides is most widely used as a space spray?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H​CH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mala​thion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"D​DT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyre​thrum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pyrethrum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pyrethrum</strong></p><p>The insecticide most widely used as a <strong>space spray</strong> is <strong>pyrethrum.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7754","text":"Match the following method​s of mosquito control to ​the appropriate examples.​ Method of control Exampl‍e l.Biological control a.​Larvicidal oil ll.Genetic‌ control b.Sex distortion‌ lll.Chemical control c.S​ource reduction lV.Enviro​nmental control d.Barbado‌s millions","raw_text":"<p>Match the following methods of mosquito control to the appropriate examples.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 348.5625px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 208px;\"><strong>Method of control</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.5625px;\"><strong>Example</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 208px;\">l.Biological control</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.5625px;\">a.Larvicidal oil</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 208px;\">ll.Genetic control</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.5625px;\">b.Sex distortion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 208px;\">lll.Chemical control</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.5625px;\">c.Source reduction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 208px;\">lV.Environmental control</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.5625px;\">d.Barbados millions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I – a, II – b, III – c, IV​ – d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I – b, II – d, III – c, IV​ – a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"I – c, II – a, III – b, IV​ – d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"I – d, II – b, III – a, IV​ – c","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. I – d, II – b, III – a, IV – c","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. I – d, II – b, III – a, IV – c</strong></p><p>The correctly matched methods of mosquito control are:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 315px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174.671875px;\"><strong>Method of control</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.328125px;\"><strong>Example</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174.671875px;\">Biological control</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.328125px;\">Barbados millions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174.671875px;\">Genetic control</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.328125px;\">Sex distortion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174.671875px;\">Chemical control</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.328125px;\">Larvicidal oil</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174.671875px;\">Environmental control</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.328125px;\">Source reduction</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8020","text":"To curb an outbreak of pla​gue in your locality, the​ municipal corporation de​cides to use rodenticides‍. Which of the following ​can be given as a single ‌dose for this purpose?","raw_text":"To curb an outbreak of plague in your locality, the municipal corporation decides to use rodenticides. Which of the following can be given as a single dose for this purpose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Warf​arin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pin​done","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Couma​furyl","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zinc ph​osphide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Zinc phosphide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Zinc phosphide</strong></p><p><strong>Zinc phosphide</strong> is a <strong>single-dose</strong> <strong>rodenticide</strong> that causes death in a one-time exposure.</p>\n<p>Acute or single-dose rodenticides are classified according to the precautions that need to be taken before their use:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Those requiring <strong>ordinary care </strong>- Red squill, norbormide, zinc phosphide</li>\n<li>Those requiring <strong>maximal precaution </strong>- Sodium fluoroacetate, fluoroacetamide and strychnine</li>\n<li><strong>Too</strong> <strong>dangerous </strong>for use - Arsenic trioxide, phosphorus, thallium sulphate</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Multiple-dose</strong> or <strong>cumulative</strong> <strong>rodenticides</strong> cause death after <strong>multiple</strong> times of <strong>exposure</strong>. Examples include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Warfarin</li>\n<li>Coumafuryl</li>\n<li>Diphacinone</li>\n<li>Pindone</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8018","text":"Which of the following col​or labels is used to indi​cate the most hazardous p​esticides?","raw_text":"Which of the following color labels is used to indicate the most hazardous pesticides?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R​ed","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gr​een","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Yel​low","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bl​ack","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Red","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e2354537c4e64993946018afd19a7cd0x1280x924.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Red</strong></p><p>The <strong>most hazardous</strong> pesticides must have labels in <strong>red color</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Pesticide toxicity labels:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 321.734375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\"><strong>Category of Pesticide</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.734375px;\"><strong>Color of Label</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">Extremely toxic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.734375px;\">Bright red</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">Highly toxic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.734375px;\">Bright yellow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">Moderately toxic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.734375px;\">Bright blue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 196px;\">Slightly toxic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114.734375px;\">Bright green</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These labels are depicted in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"fxgHZ3uZ","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"44_interstitial_lung_diseases_and_pneumoconiosis","title":"44.Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MD0956","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common interstit‌ial lung disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common interstitial lung disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-specific interstitial ​pneumonia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Desquamative interstitial ​pneumonia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Idiopathic pulmonary fibro​sis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Respiratory bronchiolitis ​associated lung disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis</strong></p><p>The most common interstitial lung disease (ILD) is<strong> idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis</strong> (IPF).</p>\n<p>ILD refers to the <strong>remodeling of interstitial space</strong>, the region between the epithelium and endothelium. The main pathology is <strong>fibrosis</strong>, mediated by TGF-ß and platelet-derived growth factor.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 415px; width: 400px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 394px;\"><strong>Classification of Interstitial Lung Disease (ILD)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 394px;\">\n<p><strong>ILDs of known etiology</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Occupational: Coal worker's pneumoconiosis, silicosis</li>\n<li>Treatment-related: Radiation, drug-induced</li>\n<li>Connective tissue disease-related: Rheumatoid arthritis, Sjogren's syndrome, scleroderma</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 394px;\">\n<p><strong>ILDs of unknown etiology</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF): <strong>Most common</strong> type </li>\n<li>Non-specific interstitial pneumonia (<strong>NSIP</strong>)</li>\n<li>Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia (COP)</li>\n<li>Acute interstitial pneumonia (AIP)/Hamman-Rich syndrome</li>\n<li>Respiratory bronchiolitis associated ILD (<strong>RBILD</strong>)</li>\n<li>Desquamative interstitial pneumonia (<strong>DIP</strong>)</li>\n<li>Lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia (LIP)</li>\n<li>Pleuroparenchymal fibroelastosis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 394px;\">\n<p><strong>Granulomatous</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sarcoidosis</li>\n<li>Hypersensitivity pneumonitis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 394px;\">\n<p><strong>Other forms</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lymphangioleiomyomatosis (LAM)</li>\n<li>Pulmonary Langerhans' cell histiocytosis (PLCH)</li>\n<li>Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis (PAP)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD2906","text":"An 80-year-old woman was f​ound to have a restrictiv‌e disease with reduced lu​ng volumes on a pulmonary‌ function test. A high-re‌solution CT of the thorax‌ was done and is shown be​low. Which of the followi‍ng is not a feature of th‍is condition?","raw_text":"An 80-year-old woman was found to have a restrictive disease with reduced lung volumes on a pulmonary function test. A high-resolution CT of the thorax was done and is shown below.  Which of the following is not a feature of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clubbing of​ the digits","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inspiratory coarse crackle​s","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Breathlessness on exertion​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non-produc​tive cough","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inspiratory coarse crackles","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/18d10d402a1e43fc831cad99b33a568b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inspiratory coarse crackles</strong></p><p>The above HRCT shows a <strong>honeycomb</strong> appearance of lung parenchyma due to <strong>interstitial fibrosis</strong> in a restrictive lung disorder that is suggestive of <strong>interstitial lung disease</strong> (ILD). Inspiratory coarse crackles are not a feature of interstitial lung disease.</p>\n<p>Auscultating a patient with ILD will reveal bilateral basal<strong> end-inspiratory fine</strong> crackles also called <strong>velcro crackles </strong>or rales.</p>\n<p>Patients with interstitial lung disease usually present with a <strong>non-productive cough</strong> and <strong>breathlessness on exertion</strong>. <strong>Clubbing</strong> may be seen on examination, especially in advanced cases.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0153","text":"A 70-year-old woman presen​ts with breathlessness on‌ exertion and dry cough f​or 4 weeks. She has swoll‌en joints and early morni‌ng stiffness for which sh‌e is on treatment for a f​ew years. On auscultation‍, inspiratory fine crackl‍es were heard. An HRCT wa‌s ordered and it showed b‌ilateral, subpleural and ​basilar honeycombing patt​ern. PFT of the patient i‍s likely to show which of‌ the following patterns?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old woman presents with breathlessness on exertion and dry cough for 4 weeks. She has swollen joints and early morning stiffness for which she is on treatment for a few years. On auscultation, inspiratory fine crackles were heard. An HRCT was ordered and it showed bilateral, subpleural and basilar honeycombing pattern. PFT of the patient is likely to show which of the following patterns?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"FEV1/FVC ratio &amp;lt;0.7 and​ DLCO increases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"FEV1/FVC ratio &amp;lt;0.7 and​ DLCO decreases","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"FEV1/FVC ratio &amp;gt;0.7 and​ DLCO decreases","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"FEV1/FVC ratio &amp;gt;0.7 and​ DLCO increases","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. FEV1/FVC ratio >0.7 and DLCO decreases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. FEV1/FVC ratio &gt;0.7 and DLCO decreases</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the HRCT findings are suggestive of <strong>interstitial lung disease </strong>(ILD). <strong>FEV1/FVC ratio &gt;0.7</strong> and <strong>decreased DLCO</strong> are likely to be seen in the PFT. A history of swollen joints and early morning stiffness hints at <strong>rheumatoid arthritis. </strong>It is associated with the usual interstitial pneumonia <strong>(UIP)</strong> pattern of ILD.</p>\n<p>In ILD, <strong>restrictive pattern</strong> is seen on pulmonary function test, which is characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Decreased total lung capacity (TLC)</li>\n<li>Decreased forced vital capacity (FVC)</li>\n<li>Decreased residual volumes (RV)</li>\n<li><strong>Decreased DLCO </strong>(diffusing capacity of the lungs for carbon monoxide)</li>\n<li><strong>FEV1/FVC ratio</strong> - normal or increased, <strong>&gt;0.7</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3531","text":"Which of the following is ​not a characteristic HRCT‌ finding of usual interst​itial pneumonia (UIP)?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a characteristic HRCT finding of usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Honeycombing and loss of l​ung architecture","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ground-glas​s opacities","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Traction br​onchiectasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bibasilar subpleural retic​ular opacities","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ground-glass opacities","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ground-glass opacities</strong></p><p>Ground-glass opacities on HRCT are not a characteristic finding in UIP.</p>\n<p>Honeycombing, loss of lung architecture, traction bronchiectasis, and basilar subpleural reticular opacities are seen in UIP pattern of ILD on HRCT. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3520","text":"Which of the following con​ditions is also known as ‌Hamman-Rich syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is also known as Hamman-Rich syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Usual interstitial pneumon​ia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-specific interstitial ​pneumonia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute interstitial pneumon​ia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cryptogenic organising pne​umonia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acute interstitial pneumonia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acute interstitial pneumonia</strong></p><p><strong>Acute interstitial pneumonia</strong> (AIP) is also known as <strong>Hamman–Rich syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>AIP is a rare, idiopathic, fulminant interstitial lung disease <strong>(ILD)</strong> that is <strong>similar</strong> in presentation to <strong>ARDS.</strong> It is often <strong>preceded by</strong> a <strong>viral</strong> upper respiratory tract infection.</p>\n<p>Histologically, it shows <strong>diffuse alveolar damage</strong>. HRCT shows bilateral patchy, <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">ground-glass opacities</strong>. Lung biopsy shows diffuse alveolar damage with hyaline formation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3524","text":"A 52-year-old woman presen​ted with dyspnoea. Genera‌l physical examination re​vealed deformity of the h‌and as shown below. There‌ is restricted mobility i‌n the small joints and lo​ss of wrinkle lines on th‍e facial skin. An HRCT of‍ the thorax showed diffus‌e subpleural, symmetric, ‌ground-glass opacities of​ the lungs. Which of the ​following histopathologic‍al finding is likely to b‌e seen in the lung biopsy‌ of this patient?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old woman presented with dyspnoea. General physical examination revealed deformity of the hand as shown below. There is restricted mobility in the small joints and loss of wrinkle lines on the facial skin. An HRCT of the thorax showed diffuse subpleural, symmetric, ground-glass opacities of the lungs. Which of the following histopathological finding is likely to be seen in the lung biopsy of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Homogenous interstitial in​volvement","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Temporal heterogeneity of ​lesions","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spatial heterogeneity of l​esions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Honeycombi​ng pattern","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Homogenous interstitial involvement","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/40ba29b9fdbe4b75b4f3fb036fe18df6.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Homogenous interstitial involvement</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the image showing <strong>sclerodactyly </strong>suggestive of <strong>scleroderma </strong>along with the HRCT findings point to a diagnosis of <strong>non-specific interstitial pneumonia</strong> (NSIP). It is characterized by <strong>homogeneous interstitial</strong> involvement.</p>\n<p>NSIP is often <strong>associated</strong> with <strong>connective tissue disease</strong> but can also be drug-induced. The common drugs implicated in NSIP are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bleomycin</li>\n<li>Busulfan</li>\n<li>Methotrexate</li>\n<li>Amiodarone</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It usually affects <strong>middle-aged woman </strong>who have<strong> never smoked</strong>. There is uniformity of interstitial involvement across the biopsy section, and this may be predominantly cellular or fibrosing. HRCT shows homogenous involvement with symmetrical ground-glass opacities.</p>\n<p>Non-specific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP) <strong>responds</strong> to <strong>steroids</strong>. Other treatment modalities include cytotoxic drugs like azathioprine or cyclophosphamide and also biologics like rituximab.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3534","text":"Which of the following is ​not a smoking-related int‌erstitial lung disease (I​LD)?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a smoking-related interstitial lung disease (ILD)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Desquamative interstitial ​pneumonia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Respiratory bronchiolitis ​associated ILD","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary langerhans cell ​histiocytosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary lymphangioleiomy​omatosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pulmonary lymphangioleiomyomatosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pulmonary lymphangioleiomyomatosis</strong></p><p>Pulmonary lymphangioleiomyomatosis (LAM) is not related to smoking.</p>\n<p>It is usually seen in <strong>young females</strong> with <strong>tuberous sclerosis. </strong>LAM can lead to <strong>pneumothorax</strong> or <strong>chylous effusions</strong>. </p>\n<p>Pulmonary function testing usually reveals an <strong>obstructive</strong> or <strong>mixed obstructive-restrictive</strong> pattern. <strong>HRCT</strong> shows thin-walled <strong>cysts</strong> surrounded by normal lung parenchyma. </p>\n<p>The <strong>smoking-related ILDs</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>DIP - Desquamating interstitial pneumonia.</li>\n<li>RB-ILD - Respiratory bronchiolitis associated ILD.</li>\n<li>LCH - Langerhans cell histiocytosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3536","text":"A man presented with progr​essively worsening cough ‌with sputum, breathlessne​ss, easy fatiguability, a‌nd loss of appetite for 6‌ months. PFT was suggesti‌ve of a restrictive disor​der. Trans-bronchial biop‍sy showed cells positive ‍for CD1a and langerin (CD‌207). Which of the follow‌ing is not a feature of t​his condition?","raw_text":"A man presented with progressively worsening cough with sputum, breathlessness, easy fatiguability, and loss of appetite for 6 months. PFT was suggestive of a restrictive disorder.  Trans-bronchial biopsy showed cells positive for CD1a and langerin (CD207). Which of the following is not a feature of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They may present with spon​taneous pneumothorax","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cysts and nodules are seen​ on HRCT","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is a smoking related in​terstitial lung disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It typically affects elder​ly smokers","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It typically affects elderly smokers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/91c8ab4609bd4999b6bdd06ae52272ebx576x641.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It typically affects elderly smokers</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis</strong> (LCH). It is a smoking-related <strong>ILD</strong> that typically affects <strong>young adults</strong>. Smoking cessation leads to improvement in the condition.</p>\n<p>The specific immunohistochemical markers for LCH are <strong>CD1a</strong> and <strong>langerin</strong> (CD207). On electron microscopy, the presence of <strong>Birbeck granules</strong> in the cytoplasm is characteristic (as shown below).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>HRCT shows <strong>bilateral irregularly shaped </strong><strong>cysts </strong>with <strong>nodules</strong>, predominantly in the<strong> upper lobe</strong>. There is a risk of <strong>spontaneous pneumothorax</strong> in this condition due to cyst rupture. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3545","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin‌g pulmonary alveolar prot​einosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding pulmonary alveolar proteinosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acquired due to antibodies​ formed against GM-CSF","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Copious, watery PAS-negati​ve sputum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"X-ray shows bilateral diff​use opacities","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Treatment is whole lung la​vage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Copious, watery PAS-negative sputum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/07b886c279534102a5d40828c041f07ax1279x970.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Copious, watery PAS-negative sputum</strong></p><p>Chunky, gelatinous, <strong>PAS-positive sputum</strong> is a feature of <strong>pulmonary alveolar proteinosis </strong>(PAP)<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>PAP is a condition characterized by the <strong>accumulation</strong> of <strong>surfactants</strong> in <strong>alveoli</strong> and <strong>terminal airways</strong>. It can be due to <strong>impaired clearance</strong> or <strong>abnormal production</strong> of surfactants. </p>\n<p>PAP can be of three forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary </strong>(<strong>most common</strong>): There is impaired GM-CSF-dependent surfactant clearance by the alveolar macrophages. It can be due to: \n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Autoantibodies against GM-CSF (acquired/idiopathic/autoimmune PAP) </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mutation in GM-CSF receptors (hereditary PAP) </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary</strong>: There is a reduction in the number or ability of alveolar macrophages that causes impaired surfactant clearance. It can be due to:\n<ul>\n<li>Silicosis</li>\n<li>Immunodeficiency disorders</li>\n<li>Hematopoietic disorders and malignancies</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Congenital</strong>: It affects neonates and children. There is abnormal surfactant production due to mutation in the genes coding for:\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Surfactant protein B</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Surfactant protein C</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> ATP binding cassette protein member 3</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>About 30% of patients are asymptomatic. Symptomatic patients can have persistent dry cough, progressive dyspnea, and pleuritic chest pain</p>\n<p>X-ray shows bilateral, diffuse opacities and HRCT shows a <strong>crazy-paving pattern</strong> (as shown below). The treatment is whole lung lavage.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3544","text":"Farmer’s lung is associate​d with which of the follo‌wing organisms?","raw_text":"Farmer’s lung is associated with which of the following organisms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Histopl​asmosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coccidi​omycosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thermophilic actinomycetes​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cryptoc​occosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thermophilic actinomycetes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thermophilic actinomycetes</strong></p><p>Farmer’s lung is a <strong>hypersensitivity pneumonitis</strong> caused due to exposure to mouldy hay, which contains<strong> thermophilic actinomycetes</strong>, principally<em> Thermopolyspora polyspora</em>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3548","text":"A daily-wage worker from t​he cement industry got a ‌chest X-ray as a part of ​his routine health check-‌up. What could be the cau‌se of the findings shown ‌below?","raw_text":"A daily-wage worker from the cement industry got a chest X-ray as a part of his routine health check-up. What could be the cause of the findings shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beryl​liosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anthr​acosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Asbes​tosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sili​cosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Asbestosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0005768cd80343ec942734c3a9183dd0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/128aeb50c7cc4a66bcc03408bb51890bx1280x1446.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Asbestosis</strong></p><p>The given X-ray shows irregular opacities, which appear to be pleural thickenings i.e., <strong>pleural plaques</strong>. The sign along with a history of working in the cement industry suggests a diagnosis of <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">asbestosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Workers of the <strong>cement, shipping, and textile</strong> industries are at a higher risk of developing asbestosis. Asbestos tends to involve the pleura first. </p>\n<p>The earliest manifestation is benign asbestosis-related <strong>pleural effusion.</strong> With time, the plaques calcify resulting in the formation of <strong>thick pleural plaques.</strong> The pleural thickenings can be seen on the X-ray. This is the “<strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">holly leaf sign</strong>”.</p>\n<p>Lung parenchymal involvement occurs after years of exposure (at least 10 years). They present with cough and exertional dyspnoea. It predominantly affects the <strong>lower</strong> <strong>lobes</strong> of the lung, in contrast to other forms of pneumoconiosis, which affect the upper lobes.</p>\n<p>Pulmonary function testing in asbestosis typically shows a <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">restrictive pattern</strong> with a decrease in both lung volumes and diffusing capacity.</p>\n<p>The image below shows thick plueral plaques.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3549","text":"A woman has been working f​or a company that manufac‌tures roofing sheets for ​12 years, but the company‌ does not provide her wit‌h personal protective equ‌ipment. Which of the foll​owing cancers is commonly‍ seen in such individuals‍?","raw_text":"A woman has been working for a company that manufactures roofing sheets for 12 years, but the company does not provide her with personal protective equipment. Which of the following cancers is commonly seen in such individuals?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pleural me​sothelioma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Peritoneal ​mesothelioma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenocarcin​oma of lung","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Laryngeal​ carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Adenocarcinoma of lung","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Adenocarcinoma of lung</strong></p><p>Workers in the roofing industry are exposed to <strong>asbestos</strong>. <strong>Lung cancer</strong> (of all histologic types) is the <strong>most common</strong> cancer associated with asbestos exposure.</p>\n<p><strong>Smoking</strong> along with asbestos exposure <strong>multiplies</strong> the <strong>risk</strong> to a great extent. Cough and hemoptysis are common presentations.</p>\n<p><strong>Pleural mesothelioma</strong> is the <strong>most specific</strong> cancer associated with asbestos exposure, but not the most common. Smoking does not seem to be associated with mesothelioma. Pleuritic chest pain or a mass on the radiographic image are common presentations.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0608","text":"Which among the following ​is a common concern for a‌ patient presenting with ​chronic dry cough, workin‌g in the granite mining i‌ndustry?","raw_text":"Which among the following is a common concern for a patient presenting with chronic dry cough, working in the granite mining industry?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asbes​tosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sili​cosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Farmer​'s lung","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anthr​acosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Silicosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Silicosis</strong></p><p><strong>Silicosis</strong> is a common concern for people working in <strong>granite mining industries</strong>.</p>\n<p>Silica is found in rock, granite, slate, and sandstone. People working in mining, stone cutting, sandblasting, glass and cement manufacturing, and quarrying are exposed to silica. The active molecule that causes silicosis is <strong>free silica </strong>or crystalline silica (<strong>quartz</strong>).</p>\n<p>Silicosis can present as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acute</strong> silicosis<strong> -</strong> due to heavy exposure in closed spaces<strong> </strong>and they manifest within 2 years.</li>\n<li><strong>Chronic</strong> silicosis (<strong>simple</strong> silicosis) -<strong> </strong>due to long-term (10 to 30 years), less intense exposure.</li>\n<li><strong>Complicated</strong> silicosis- <strong> </strong>simple silicosis may progress to complicated silicosis that presents with chronic cough and progressive dyspnoea on exertion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Silicosis<strong> </strong>is the most common pnemoconiosis in the world.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3546","text":"A 45-year-old man comes to​ the emergency room with ‌an acute onset of dyspnoe​a. He tells you that he h‌as been working in the sa‌ndblasting industry for a‌bout a year and the compa​ny does not provide perso‍nal protective equipment.‍ He also gives a history ‌of on and off dry cough. ‌CT scan shows the followi​ng. What is the most like​ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man comes to the emergency room with an acute onset of dyspnoea. He tells you that he has been working in the sandblasting industry for about a year and the company does not provide personal protective equipment. He also gives a history of on and off dry cough. CT scan shows the following. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Complicate​d silicosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute s​ilicosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Simple s​ilicosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary Alveolar Protein​osis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acute silicosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/acca81a77941475c81f0363916e15abd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acute silicosis</strong></p><p>A history of working in the <strong>sandblasting</strong> industry for about a year without PPE suggests a <strong>heavy exposure</strong> <strong>to silica</strong>. This along with the CT finding of bilateral nodules, interstitial fibrosis, and crazy pavement pattern suggests a diagnosis of <strong>acute silicosis</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Acute silicosis is seen rarely. It manifests <strong>within 2 years</strong> of heavy exposure. Common presentations are <strong>dyspnoea </strong>and<strong> hypoxemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>The X-ray may show diffuse miliary infiltrates or consolidation. HRCT may also show bilateral centrilobular nodules and ground-glass opacification. Bronchoalveolar lavage yields a <strong>PAS-positive milky white fluid</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD2762","text":"A 30-year-old man has rece​ntly started work in the ‌stone cutting industry. T​he company provides him w‌ith a respirator mask but‌ they do not use dust con‌trol measures. If he work​s in similar conditions f‍or 20 more years, which o‍f the following will you ‌most likely see in his HR‌CT scan image?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man has recently started work in the stone cutting industry. The company provides him with a respirator mask but they do not use dust control measures. If he works in similar conditions for 20 more years, which of the following will you most likely see in his HRCT scan image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A typical crazy pavement p​attern","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Small round opacities in u​pper lobes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thin-walled cysts surround​ed by normal lung parench‌yma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thick calcified pleural pl​aques","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Small round opacities in upper lobes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b5512bdb47434480897e0abc14fa12d8x601x546.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Small round opacities in upper lobes</strong></p><p>In this scenario, the man works in the stone cutting industry, indicating exposure to <strong>silica.</strong> He is expected to have <strong>less intense</strong>, <strong>long term exposure</strong> owing to a few but substandard measures to control exposure to silica. Such individuals are more likely to develop <strong>simple silicosis</strong>. These patients are asymptomatic, and HRCT shows <strong>small round opacities</strong> in the upper lobes.</p>\n<p>This can progress to<strong> complicated silicosis</strong>. Here, the nodules coalesce and form masses leading to impairment of lung function. They present with chronic cough and progressive dyspnoea on exertion. This may progress further to cause <strong>progressive massive fibrosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>PFT shows a <strong>mixed obstructive and restrictive</strong> pattern. Calcification of hilar lymph nodes is seen in a few cases, called <strong>eggshell calcification. </strong>This is depicted in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: A <strong>crazy pavement</strong> pattern is seen with <strong>acute silicosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Thin-walled cysts</strong> surrounded by normal lung parenchyma are seen in pulmonary lymphangiomyomatosis <strong>(LAM).</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Pleural plaques</strong> are seen in patients suffering from <strong>asbestosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0602","text":"Which of the following pne​umoconiosis is associated‌ with an increased incide​nce of tuberculosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following pneumoconiosis is associated with an increased incidence of tuberculosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asbes​tosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sili​cosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Coal worker's pneumoconios​is","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Farmer​'s lung","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Silicosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Silicosis</strong></p><p><strong>Silicosis</strong> is associated with an increased incidence of tuberculosis.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2025\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Silica causes <strong>pulmonary alveolar macrophage dysfunction</strong> and hence, patients with silicosis have a higher risk of developing lung infections from organisms like <em>M. Tuberculosis</em>, atypical mycobacteria and fungi.</p>\n<p>Radiographic imaging of <strong>silicotuberculosis</strong> shows <strong>cavitation.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3537","text":"Choose the correct option ​with respect to Caplan’s ‌syndrome.","raw_text":"Choose the correct option with respect to Caplan’s syndrome.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumatoid arthritis + pne​umoconiosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rheumatoid arthritis + ant​erior uveitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hilar adenopathy + polyart​hritis + erythema nodosum‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arthritis + urethritis + c​onjunctivitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rheumatoid arthritis + pneumoconiosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rheumatoid arthritis + pneumoconiosis</strong></p><p><strong>Caplan's syndrome</strong> also called rheumatoid pneumoconiosis, is a combination of <strong>rheumatoid</strong> <strong>arthritis</strong><strong> </strong>and<strong> pneumoconiosis.</strong></p>\n<p>It manifests as pneumoconiotic nodules along with features of seropositive rheumatoid arthritis. Caplan syndrome was first described in <strong>coal miners</strong> and later in patients with <strong>silicosis</strong> too.</p>\n<p>Option C: Hilar adenopathy, polyarthritis, and erythema nodosum are characteristic of <strong>Lofgren</strong> syndrome.</p>\n<p>Option D: Arthritis, urethritis, and conjunctivitis are characteristic of <strong>Reiter</strong> syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3529","text":"A factory worker presents ​to the clinic with fever,‌ tiredness, and a chronic​ dry cough. He says his s‌ymptoms are worst on the ‌first day of the week, im‌prove by the end of the w​eek, and are almost non-e‍xistent over the weekends‍. What could be the most ‌likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A factory worker presents to the clinic with fever, tiredness, and a chronic dry cough. He says his symptoms are worst on the first day of the week, improve by the end of the week, and are almost non-existent over the weekends. What could be the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bagas​sosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Byssi​nosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Beryl​liosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bornholm’​s disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Byssinosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Byssinosis</strong></p><p>The above characteristic pattern of symptoms is called <strong>Monday fever</strong> or<strong> </strong>Monday chest tightness and is due to <strong>byssinosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Byssinosis is seen commonly in cotton factory workers due to <strong>cotton dust</strong> <strong>exposure</strong>. It is also caused due to flax, hemp, and jute dust.</p>\n<p>It is due to occupational hypersensitivity reaction with symptoms occurring (or recurring) on the first day of the workweek and diminishing by the weekend due to development of tolerance.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3519","text":"Which interstitial lung di​sease is likely to be see‌n in a patient suffering ​from systemic sclerosis?","raw_text":"Which interstitial lung disease is likely to be seen in a patient suffering from systemic sclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Respiratory bronchiolitis ​associated ILD","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-specific interstitial ​pneumonia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acute interstitial pneumon​ia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bronchiolitis obliterans o​rganising pneumonia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Non-specific interstitial pneumonia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Non-specific interstitial pneumonia</strong></p><p>Non-specific interstitial pneumonia <strong>(NSIP)</strong> pattern of ILD is the most common pulmonary manifestation in <strong>systemic</strong> <strong>sclerosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The CT scan of NSIP shows <strong>ground-glass opacities</strong>. It responds to <strong>steroids</strong> given along with azathioprine or cyclophosphamide.</p>\n<p>Other connective tissue diseases associated with ILD:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li>Sjögren syndrome</li>\n<li>Dermatopolymyositis</li>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1035","text":"A 45-year-old man who has ​worked as a miner for the‌ past 25 years, comes to ​the OPD with complaints o‌f dyspnoea and cough. Pul‌monary function tests and‌ a lung biopsy are done. ​The histopathology of the‍ sample is shown below. W‍hich type of emphysema ca‌n this condition give ris‌e to?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man who has worked as a miner for the past 25 years, comes to the OPD with complaints of dyspnoea and cough. Pulmonary function tests and a lung biopsy are done. The histopathology of the sample is shown below. Which type of emphysema can this condition give rise to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Panacinar​ emphysema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Centrilobul​ar emphysema","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Paraseptal​ emphysema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Irregular​ emphysema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Centrilobular emphysema","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4df730716ed948e7b2403d0816133070.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Centrilobular emphysema</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the microscopic image showing abundant black-colored pigment within the macrophages of lung parenchyma is characteristic of <strong>pulmonary anthracosis</strong>/coal worker's pneumoconiosis. It may give rise to <strong>centrilobular emphysema</strong>.</p>\n<p>The inhaled <strong>carbon</strong> <strong>particles</strong> are engulfed by the lung <strong>macrophages. </strong> They accumulate in the connective tissue along lymphatics or in lymphoid tissue along the bronchi or the hilum, which causes the blackening of the tissues. Aggregates of carbon dust are present as <strong>coal macules </strong>or nodules which consist of carbon-laden macrophages.</p>\n<p>It is commonly seen in people who work in environments where there is high air pollution with carbon particles, such as <strong>coal mines</strong>. It is usually benign and less than 10% of the cases develop <strong>progressive massive fibrosis</strong> which may lead to pulmonary dysfunction, pulmonary hypertension, and cor pulmonale. Coal workers may also develop emphysema or chronic bronchitis, independent of smoking.</p>\n<p>Unlike asbestosis, this condition does not predispose to cancer.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"4lAn9Hhb","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"44_vulval___vaginal_malignancy","title":"44.Vulval & Vaginal Malignancy","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MB5610","text":"Which among the following ‌is not a premalignant les‍ion of vulva?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not a premalignant lesion of vulva?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paget’s‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lichen s‌clerosus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Condylo‌ma lata","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bowen's‌ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Condyloma lata","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Condyloma lata</strong></p><p>Condyloma lata is not a premalignant lesion of vulva.</p>\n<p><strong>Premalignant lesions of the vulva</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vulval intraepithelial neoplasia (VIN) - most common</li>\n<li><strong>Paget’s disease</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Lichen sclerosus</strong></li>\n<li>Squamous cell hyperplasia</li>\n<li>Condyloma acuminata</li>\n<li><strong>Bowen’s disease</strong> (erythroplasia of Querat)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0260","text":"Which of the following is ‌a common differential dia‍gnosis of verrucuos carci‌noma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a common differential diagnosis of verrucuos carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Condylo‌ma lata","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Condyloma‌ acuminata","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Adenoca‌rcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cha‌ncre","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Condyloma acuminata","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Condyloma acuminata</strong></p><p><strong>Condyloma acuminata</strong> is the common differential diagnosis of verrucous carcinoma.</p>\n<p>It refers to<strong> anogenital warts </strong>and has a<strong> verrucous appearance.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Verrucous carcinoma</strong> is a variant of squamous cell carcinoma of the  cervix. It is locally invasive and rarely metastasizes.</p>\n<p><strong>Condyloma lata </strong>is a benign and painless cutaneous manifestation of secondary syphilis. It is characterized by gray to white lesions, typically located in the oro-genital region.</p>\n<p><strong>Chancre</strong> is a painless genital ulcer most commonly formed during the primary stage of syphilis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0261","text":"A sexually active woman pr‌esents with multiple lesi‍ons over her genital regi‌on as shown below. Which ‌of the following organism​s are implicated in this ​condition? HPV 16 HPV 11 ‌HPV 18 HPV 6 HPV 31","raw_text":"<p>A sexually active woman presents with multiple lesions over her genital region as shown below. Which of the following organisms are implicated in this condition?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>HPV 16 </li>\n<li>HPV 11</li>\n<li>HPV 18</li>\n<li>HPV 6 </li>\n<li>HPV 31</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, 3 &amp;‌amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3 &amp;‌amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Onl‌y 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 &amp;a‌mp; 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2 & 4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9fa1919c6fea4942bc4bbc41f07a48cc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2 &amp; 4</strong></p><p>The given scenario and image are suggestive of <strong>genital warts.</strong> Human papillomavirus <strong>(HPV) 6 and 11</strong> cause nearly all genital warts.</p>\n<p>It is usually<strong> clinically diagnosed</strong> but if co-existing neoplasia is suspected then a biopsy is required. HPV serotyping is not required for routine diagnosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5620","text":"What is the earliest sympt‌om in patients with vulva‍l intraepithelial neoplas‌ia?","raw_text":"What is the earliest symptom in patients with vulval intraepithelial neoplasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prur‌itus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"White discharge per vagina‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dyspa‌reunia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vaginal ‌bleeding","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pruritus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pruritus</strong></p><p><strong>Pruritus</strong><span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span>is the earliest symptom of VIN as well as vulvar cancer.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of VIN include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pruritus</li>\n<li>White discharge per vagina</li>\n<li>Dyspareunia</li>\n<li>Vaginal bleeding </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5618","text":"A woman with vaginal itchi‌ng is suspected to have v‍ulval intraepithelial neo‌plasia. Which of the foll‌owing is the most common ​cause of this condition?","raw_text":"A woman with vaginal itching is suspected to have vulval intraepithelial neoplasia. Which of the following is the most common cause of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HPV‌ 16","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"HPV‌ 18","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HS‌V 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gardnerell‌a vaginalis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. HPV 16","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. HPV 16</strong></p><p><strong>HPV 16</strong> is the most common cause of <strong>vulval intraepithelial neoplasia</strong> (VIN). </p>\n<p> Causes of VIN include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>HPV 16, 18, 31, 33</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">HSV 2</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Syphilis</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Gonorrhea</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Gardnerella vaginalis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5617","text":"An elderly woman is diagno‌sed with vulval intraepit‍helial neoplasia 3. Which‌ of the following is fals‌e regarding this conditio​n?","raw_text":"An elderly woman is diagnosed with vulval intraepithelial neoplasia 3. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Single‌ lesion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Associated with lichen scl‌erosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Progress to invasive cance‌r","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Human papill‌oma virus +ve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Human papilloma virus +ve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Human papilloma virus +ve</strong></p><p><strong>Elderly</strong> women with vulval intraepithelial neoplasia <strong>(VIN) - 3</strong> are <strong>HPV negative</strong>. </p>\n<p>VIN 3 is of two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Young </strong>women – <strong>HPV +ve</strong>, multiple lesion, 90% regress</li>\n<li><strong>Elderly </strong>women – <strong>HPV –ve</strong>, single lesion, associated with lichen sclerosis, often progress to invasive cancer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3734","text":"Sentinel lymph node biopsy‌ is most useful in which ‍of the following gynecolo‌gical malignancies?","raw_text":"Sentinel lymph node biopsy is most useful in which of the following gynecological malignancies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carcinom‌a vagina","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carcino‌ma vulva","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Carcinom‌a cervix","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carcinoma ‌endometrium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Carcinoma vulva","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Carcinoma vulva</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 492\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Among the given gynecological malignancies, <strong>sentinel lymph node biopsy</strong> (SLNB) is most useful in <strong>vulval</strong> <strong>carcinomas</strong>.</p>\n<p>This is because women with <strong>earlier stages</strong> of vulval cancer can have <strong>diseased lymph nodes</strong> even <strong>without palpable</strong> <strong>enlargement of nodes</strong>. Hence, sentinel lymph node biopsy helps in early diagnosis lymph node spread which can be managed by lymph node dissection.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 710\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>A <strong>sentinel lymph node</strong> is a physiologically, the <strong>first lymph node</strong> to receive tumor lymphatic drainage.</p>\n<p>Malignancies where <strong>sentinel lymph node biopsy</strong> is commonly done are :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vulval carcinoma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Breast carcinoma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Penile carcinoma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Malignant melanoma</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Lymphatic mapping is similar technique to SLNB that can also be used in vulval cancers.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5623","text":"Which is most common type ‌of vulval cancer?","raw_text":"Which is most common type of vulval cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous ce‌ll carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Adenoca‌rcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Basal cell‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clear cell‌ carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Squamous cell carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Squamous cell carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Squamous cell carcinoma</strong> is the most common type of vulval cancer.</p>\n<p>Other types of vulval cancer include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adenocarcinoma</li>\n<li>Melanoma</li>\n<li>Basal cell carcinoma</li>\n<li>Sarcoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5628","text":"What is the most common ty‌pe of vaginal cancer like‍ly to be seen in a woman ‌with a history of intraut‌erine di-ethyl stilbester​ol exposure?","raw_text":"What is the most common type of vaginal cancer likely to be seen in a woman with a history of intrauterine di-ethyl stilbesterol exposure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous ce‌ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mela‌noma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clear cell a‌denocarcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fibros‌arcoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Clear cell adenocarcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Clear cell adenocarcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Clear cell adenocarcinoma of the vagina</strong> is commonly seen in adoloscent girls with a history of <strong>intrauterine DES exposure</strong>. </p>\n<p>It involves the upper third of the anterior vaginal wall. <strong>Radical hysterectomy, vaginectomy </strong>with <strong>pelvic lymphadenectomy</strong> is the treatment of choice. </p>\n<p>The most common lesion due to DES exposure is vaginal adenosis and not clear cell adenocarcinoma. It is a benign condition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5621","text":"What is the most common si‌te of vulval cancers?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of vulval cancers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clit‌oris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Labia ‌majora","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Labia ‌minora","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perine‌al area","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Labia majora","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Labia majora</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common site</strong> of vulval cancer is <strong>labia majora</strong> &gt; clitoris &gt; labia minora.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5625","text":"Vulvar carcinoma was diagn‌osed in a 68-year-old wom‍an who presented with itc‌hing and mass over the vu‌lva for 6 months. Sentine​l lymph node biopsy revea​led positive inguinal nod‌es. What is the stage of ‍the tumor?","raw_text":"Vulvar carcinoma was diagnosed in a 68-year-old woman who presented with itching and mass over the vulva for 6 months. Sentinel lymph node biopsy revealed positive inguinal nodes. What is the stage of the tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sta‌ge I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stag‌e II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stag‌e III","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stag‌e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stage III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stage III</strong></p><p>According to FIGO staging, <strong>tumor with positive inguinal lymph node</strong> is classified as <strong>stage III.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5624","text":"In patients with vulval ca‌ncer, what is the least c‍ommon route through which‌ the malignancy spreads?","raw_text":"In patients with vulval cancer, what is the least common route through which the malignancy spreads?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dir‌ect","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymp‌hatic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vasc‌ular","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Transc‌oelomic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vascular","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vascular</strong></p><p>Vascular route of spread is rare in vulval cancer. <strong>Lymphatic</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> route of spread in <strong>vulval cancer</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Lymph node status</strong> is the most important <strong>prognostic factor</strong>. The sentinel lymph node biopsy is helpful for evaluating the spread of vulval cancer. Lymph nodes involved are, <strong>superficial inguinal nodes (sentinel node)</strong> &gt; deep inguinal nodes &gt; femoral group of nodes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5629","text":"An elderly woman is found ‌to have abnormal cells on‍ a pap smear. Further inv‌estigations reveal vagina‌l squamous cell carcinoma​ extending up to the pelv​ic wall. There is no evid‌ence of distant metastasi‍s. What is the stage of t​he tumor in this patient?‌","raw_text":"An elderly woman is found to have abnormal cells on a pap smear. Further investigations reveal vaginal squamous cell carcinoma extending up to the pelvic wall. There is no evidence of distant metastasis. What is the stage of the tumor in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sta‌ge I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stag‌e II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stag‌e III","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stag‌e IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stage III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stage III</strong></p><p>Vaginal cancer extending up to the pelvic wall is <strong>stage III.</strong></p>\n<p>Vaginal cancer commonly affects females <strong>&gt;70 years</strong> of age. <strong>Squamous cell carcinoma</strong> is the most common histologic type. It commonly involves the <strong>upper third</strong> of the posterior wall of the<strong> vagina.</strong></p>\n<p>Patients are<strong> mostly asymptomatic</strong>. They may present with painless abnormal vaginal bleeding, post-coital bleed, and foul-smelling discharge per vagina. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 495\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Vaginal cancer staging:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Stage 0 -  Vaginal intraepithelial neoplasia</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Stage I - Carcinoma limited to the vaginal wall</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Stage II - Carcinoma extending beyond the vagina, but not<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span>extending to the pelvic walls</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Stage III - Carcinoma extends up to the pelvic walls</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Stage IVA - Carcinoma extending beyond the true pelvis/or involving the bladder and/or rectum, or evidence of distant metastasis</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Stage IVB - Spread to the distant metastasis</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0262","text":"A 70-year-old presented wi‌th a lesion over the vulv‍a as shown below. Bilater‌al inguinal lymph nodes w‌ere palpable. Biopsy of t​he lesion revealed cells ​containing large, pleomor‌phic nuclei. Which of the‍ following is false regar​ding this condition?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old presented with a lesion over the vulva as shown below. Bilateral inguinal lymph nodes were palpable. Biopsy of the lesion revealed cells containing large, pleomorphic nuclei. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the 2nd most common ‌vulval cancer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The most common site is cl‌itoris","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It may arise from a juncti‌onal naevus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radical vulvectomy with bi‌lateral regional lymphade‍nectomy is the preferred ‌treatment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The most common site is clitoris","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5294e554993d492fabe15c33969d2cd0.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The most common site is clitoris</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>vulval melanoma</strong>. The most commonly involved site is <strong>labia majora</strong> followed by labia minora and clitoris.</p>\n<p>Vulval melanoma is the <strong>second most common</strong> vulval cancer. It may arise from<strong> junctional naevus</strong>. It has a high propensity for local and distant recurrence and has a <strong>poor prognosis</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Radical vulvectomy </strong>and bilateral regional<strong> lymphadenectomy</strong> is the preferred treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"pvoO1qyZ","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"44_tubulointerstitial__vascular_and_cystic_diseases","title":"44.Tubulointerstitial, Vascular And Cystic Diseases","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB5342","text":"A 45-year-old man was brou‌ght to the ER by his frie​nds after they found him ‌lying unconscious next to​ an empty antifreeze bott‍le. Investigations were s‍ignificant for the follow‌ing findings. This condit​ion predominantly affects‌ which part of the kidney​? BUN - 60mg/dl S. creati​nine - 3.2mg/dl S. potass‌ium - 7mmol/L ABG - high ​anion gap metabolic acido‍sis Urine analysis - calc​ium oxalate crystals","raw_text":"<p>A 45-year-old man was brought to the ER by his friends after they found him lying unconscious next to an empty antifreeze bottle. Investigations were significant for the following findings. This condition predominantly affects which part of the kidney?</p>\n<p>BUN - 60mg/dl</p>\n<p>S. creatinine - 3.2mg/dl</p>\n<p>S. potassium - 7mmol/L</p>\n<p>ABG - high anion gap metabolic acidosis</p>\n<p>Urine analysis - calcium oxalate crystals</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proximal convoluted tubule‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Distal convo‌luted tubule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Collect‌ing duct","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Loop o‌f Henle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Proximal convoluted tubule","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Proximal convoluted tubule</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute tubular necrosis</strong> (ATN) due to ingestion of ethylene glycol. <strong>Toxins</strong> usually result in ATN by causing damage to the <strong>proximal convoluted tubules </strong>(PCTs).</p>\n<p>Ethylene glycol poisoning produces marked <strong>ballooning</strong> and <strong>hydropic</strong> or vacuolar <strong>degeneration</strong> of PCT. <strong>Calcium oxalate crystals</strong> are often also found in tubular lumens.</p>\n<p>Direct toxic injury to the tubules in ATI can be caused by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endogenous agents</strong>: Myoglobin, hemoglobin, monoclonal light chains, bile/bilirubin</li>\n<li><strong>Exogenous agents</strong>:  Drugs, radiocontrast dyes, heavy metals, and organic solvents</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5345","text":"A 27-year-old woman presen‌ts with urinary frequency​, urgency, and burning du‌ring urination of two day​s duration. Presence of w‍hich of the following add‍itional findings would ma‌ke you suspect pyelonephr​itis in this patient?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old woman presents with urinary frequency, urgency, and burning during urination of two days duration. Presence of which of the following additional findings would make you suspect pyelonephritis in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fe‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bacte‌riuria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"White blood‌ cell casts","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sterile‌ pyuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. White blood cell casts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. White blood cell casts</strong></p><p><strong>White blood cell casts</strong> are specific features of <strong>pyelonephritis</strong>. Neutrophil-rich casts (pus casts) indicate <strong>renal involvement</strong> because casts are formed only in tubules. </p>\n<p>The urine contains many leukocytes (<strong>pyuria</strong>) derived from the inflammatory infiltrate.  It is found in both <strong>upper</strong> and <strong>lower UTIs</strong> and cannot differentiate between them. <strong>Fever</strong> is also a <strong>non-specific</strong> finding.</p>\n<p><strong>Ascending infection</strong> is the most common <strong>cause</strong> of clinical pyelonephritis.</p>\n<p>Note: Sterile pyuria is defined as white cells in the urine in the absence of significant bacterial growth. It is seen in renal TB.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5347","text":"What is the most common or‌ganism causing acute pyel​onephritis?","raw_text":"What is the most common organism causing acute pyelonephritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Klebs‌iella","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Escheric‌hia coli","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Beta-hemolytic streptococc‌i","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proteus‌ species","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Escherichia coli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Escherichia coli</strong></p><p>The most common organism causing <strong>acute pyelonephritis</strong> is<em> <strong>Escherichia coli. </strong></em></p>\n<p>The organisms that cause acute pyelonephritis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>E. coli</li>\n<li>Proteus</li>\n<li>Klebsiella</li>\n<li>Enterobacter species</li>\n<li>Mycobacteria - caseating granulomatous inflammation</li>\n<li>Fungi - non-caseating granulomatous inflammation</li>\n<li>Viruses (CMV and adenovirus) - immunocompromised people</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5348","text":"Periglomerular fibrosis is‌ considered typical of wh​ich of the following cond‌itions?","raw_text":"Periglomerular fibrosis is considered typical of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic pye‌lonephritis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic glomerulonephritis‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arterioneph‌rosclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malignant h‌ypertension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chronic pyelonephritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chronic pyelonephritis</strong></p><p><strong>Periglomerular fibrosis</strong> is considered typical of <strong>chronic pyelonephritis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Histopathological features of chronic pyelonephritis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dilated tubules with flattened epithelium may be filled with casts resembling thyroid colloid <strong>(thyroidization)</strong></li>\n<li>Chronic <strong>interstitial inflammation</strong> and <strong>fibrosis</strong> in the cortex and medulla</li>\n<li>Periglomerular fibrosis</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary </strong>focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (<strong>FSGS</strong>), usually several years after the scarring has occurred and often in the absence of continued infection or persistent vesicoureteral reflux</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5364","text":"A pathologist noted the fo‌llowing microscopic findi​ng while examining a slid‌e of a kidney. What is th​e likely cause?","raw_text":"A pathologist noted the following microscopic finding while examining a slide of a kidney. What is the likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute tubul‌ar necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic pye‌lonephritis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acute interstitial nephrit‌is","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos‌us","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chronic pyelonephritis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/98994eee66c94f52be3263136ee0dc6e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2556a895c1f043069af3c9ba38f869a3x1280x1049.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/64ad61e7bb7a4e8085f59a74a31f408dx1280x1049.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chronic pyelonephritis</strong></p><p>The given image shows<strong> dilated tubules </strong>with<strong> flattened epithelium</strong>, filled with casts, resembling <strong>thyroid colloid</strong> (thyroidization). This is seen in <strong>chronic pyelonephritis</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: In acute tubular necrosis, there is focal tubular epithelial necrosis, tubulorrhexis.</p>\n<p>Option C: In acute interstitial nephritis, there is patchy edema and mononuclear cell infiltrates.</p>\n<p>Option D: In systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), wire-loop lesions are seen.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5367","text":"All of the following patie‌nts are at risk of develo​ping renal papillary necr‌osis except:","raw_text":"All of the following patients are at risk of developing renal papillary necrosis except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 14-year-old girl being t‌reated for thalassemia ma​jor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A 60-year-old man with lon‌gstanding diabetes mellit​us","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 46-year-old man with a h‌istory of analgesic abuse​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 24-year-old woman identi‌fied to have sickle cell ​trait","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A 14-year-old girl being treated for thalassemia major","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A 14-year-old girl being treated for thalassemia major</strong></p><p>Necrotizing papillitis or acute papillary necrosis is not associated with thalassemia.</p>\n<p>Major causes of <strong>acute papillary necrosis</strong> include</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diabetes</strong> mellitus</li>\n<li><strong>Sickle cell</strong> disease or trait</li>\n<li><strong>Analgesic abuse</strong> nephropathy</li>\n<li>Obstruction by calculi</li>\n<li>Focally in renal tuberculosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5368","text":"A 48-year-old woman presen‌ted with recurrent episod​es of flank pain and feve‌r with chills. CECT abdom​en showed dilated and enl‍arged left renal pelvis w‍ith the multiloculated ca‌lyces and resection of th​e kidney was done. The gr‌oss specimen showed large​ yellowish-orange nodules​. Histological examinatio‌n revealed foamy macropha​ges. Which of the followi‍ng is associated with thi​s condition?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old woman presented with recurrent episodes of flank pain and fever with chills.  CECT abdomen showed dilated and enlarged left renal pelvis with the multiloculated calyces and resection of the kidney was done. The gross specimen showed large yellowish-orange nodules. Histological examination revealed foamy macrophages.  Which of the following is associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pseudomona‌s infection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proteus ‌infection","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Staphylococc‌us infection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Proteus infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/66709ec46ed44855964937517d1d980cx1280x1142.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/05e26bd30104482885c5a8ec0a341829x1280x1142.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Proteus infection</strong></p><p>The clinical features and histological findings are suggestive of<strong> xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis</strong>. It is associated with <em><strong>Proteus</strong></em> infection and <strong>obstructive uropathy</strong>. </p>\n<p>Xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis is a relatively rare form of <strong>chronic pyelonephritis</strong> characterized by the accumulation of <strong>foamy macrophages</strong> intermingled with plasma cells, lymphocytes, polymorphonuclear leukocytes, and occasional giant cells.</p>\n<p>The lesions sometimes produce <strong>large, yellowish-orange nodules</strong> that may be grossly confused with renal cell carcinoma. CECT abdomen shows dilated and enlarged renal pelvis and calyces showing a multiloculated appearance referred to as the <strong>bear paw sign</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5371","text":"Which of the following do ‌not result in granular co​ntracted kidneys? Rapidly‌ progressive glomerulonep​hritis Benign nephroscler‍osis Chronic pyelonephrit‍is Amyloidosis Chronic gl‌omerulonephritis","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following do not result in granular contracted kidneys?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis</li>\n<li>Benign nephrosclerosis</li>\n<li>Chronic pyelonephritis</li>\n<li>Amyloidosis</li>\n<li>Chronic glomerulonephritis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2‌ only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 4‌ only","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2, 4‌ only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, ‌3, 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 4 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f083754204be4ca48b99d4ef7dd282dex405x626.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 4 only</strong></p><p>Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis and amyloidosis cause <strong>enlarged</strong> kidneys.</p>\n<p>Causes of <strong>contracted kidneys</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Symmetric\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic <strong>glomerulonephritis</strong> </li>\n<li>Benign <strong>nephrosclerosis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Asymmetric\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic <strong>pyelonephritis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Causes of <strong>enlarged kidneys</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Amyloidosis</strong></li>\n<li>Polycystic kidney disease</li>\n<li>Hydronephrosis (bilateral obstruction)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows the gross appearance in benign nephrosclerosis illustrating the fine, leathery granularity of the surface.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5374","text":"Which of the following fin‌dings is not associated w​ith malignant hypertensio‌n?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings is not associated with malignant hypertension?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ima‌ge 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ima‌ge 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ima‌ge 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ima‌ge 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Image 4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/46f656d981d54a02b7eca003310d00d3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a06707a46aaa4004af348bb8963ec65bx1280x1879.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dbeaca59cfcc413782f439e6fccd39d4x1280x1416.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9ebad85070f3459ba34973943ff7f131x1280x1137.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8d98b0ac1e744773b5f131c6ef679000x1280x1076.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Image 4</strong></p><p>Image 4 shows a <strong>leather-grain</strong> appearance, which is seen in <strong>benign nephrosclerosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Features of<strong> malignant</strong> <strong>hypertension</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Flea bitten </strong>kidney -<strong> </strong>small petechial hemorrhages on the cortical surface, leading to a mottled appearance</li>\n<li><strong>Fibrinoid necrosis</strong> of arterioles - cytologic detail is lost and the vessel wall takes on a smudgy eosinophilic appearance due to fibrin deposition</li>\n<li><strong>Onion-skinning</strong> (hyperplastic arterioles) - intimal thickening with the fine concentric layering of collagen &amp; smooth muscles</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The differential diagnosis for flea-bitten kidney includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Polyarteritis nodosa</li>\n<li>Subacute bacterial endocarditis (SABE)</li>\n<li>Malignant hypertension </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5379","text":"Atypical hemolytic uremic ‌syndrome can be caused by​ all except:","raw_text":"Atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome can be caused by all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antiphospholipid antibodie‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oral cont‌raceptives","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scler‌oderma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deficiency ‌of ADAMTS13","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Deficiency of ADAMTS13","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Deficiency of ADAMTS13</strong></p><p>Deficiency of <strong>ADAMTS13</strong> leads to thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (<strong>TTP</strong>).</p>\n<p>Two common pathogenetic triggers of thrombotic microangiopathies are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Endothelial injury - HUS</li>\n<li>Excessive platelet activation and aggregation - TTP</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 342px;\" width=\"378\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200.516px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Typical HUS</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.484px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Atypical HUS</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200.516px;\">\n<p>5-10 days after <strong>diarrheal</strong> illness</p>\n<p><em>E.coli</em> <strong>O157:H7</strong> or <em>Shigella dysenteriae</em> type I</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.484px;\">\n<p>Mutation in genes encoding complement regulatory protein</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200.516px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Shiga-like <strong>toxins</strong> produced cause <strong>endothelial</strong> <strong>damage</strong>.</li>\n<li>This leads to platelet activation and thrombi.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.484px;\">\n<p>Uncontrolled <strong>activation</strong> of the <strong>complement </strong>system due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Antiphospholipid antibodies</li>\n<li>Pregnancy complications</li>\n<li>OCPs</li>\n<li>Scleroderma</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5380","text":"An 8-year-old boy is broug‌ht with blood in the urin​e. His mother says he had‌ bloody diarrhea, fever, ​and vomiting 10 days ago.‍ His BP is 130/90 mm Hg, ‍and he is jaundiced. Inve‌stigations reveal a plate​let count of 50,000 cells‌/cu mm, BUN of 35mg/dl, a​nd creatinine of 3.5mg/dl​. Which of the following ‌cells would you expect to​ see in the peripheral sm‍ear?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old boy is brought with blood in the urine. His mother says he had bloody diarrhea, fever, and vomiting 10 days ago. His BP is 130/90 mm Hg, and he is jaundiced. Investigations reveal a platelet count of 50,000 cells/cu mm,  BUN of 35mg/dl, and creatinine of 3.5mg/dl. Which of the following cells would you expect to see in the peripheral smear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spher‌ocytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Schis‌tocyte","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Target‌ cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Heinz ‌bodies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Schistocyte","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Schistocyte</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hemolytic uremic syndrome</strong> (HUS). It is characterized by the presence of <strong>schistocytes</strong> in peripheral smear. It indicates microangiopathic hemolytic anemia. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5382","text":"Cholemic nephrosis is asso‌ciated with which of the ​following casts?","raw_text":"Cholemic nephrosis is associated with which of the following casts?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bi‌le","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chole‌sterol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lip‌ids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hya‌line","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bile","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bile</strong></p><p><strong>Cholemic nephrosis</strong> is associated with casts of <strong>bile.</strong></p>\n<p>In patients with acute or chronic liver disease with advanced <strong>liver failure, </strong>renal function may be impaired. This is called <strong>hepatorenal syndrome.</strong> This can be associated with highly <strong>elevated</strong> <strong>bilirubin</strong> levels with <strong>bile cast</strong> formation (also known as cholemic nephrosis) in distal nephron segments.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9701","text":"A 35-year-old man died in ‌a road traffic accident. ​On autopsy, the following‌ appearance of the kidney​ was noted. Which of the ‍following is not associat‍ed with this condition?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man died in a road traffic accident. On autopsy, the following appearance of the kidney was noted. Which of the following is not associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cysts in liver, pancreas, ‌and spleen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Berry a‌neurysms","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mitral val‌ve prolapse","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatic ‌fibrosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hepatic fibrosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/660d6ab793ed4c92a24e4436e45b73a0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hepatic fibrosis</strong></p><p>The <strong>gross image</strong> suggests that the patient had autosomal-dominant polycystic kidney disease (<strong>ADPKD</strong>). This condition is not associated with hepatic fibrosis.<strong> </strong>However, patients with autosomal recessive (childhood) polycystic kidney disease (<strong>ARPKD</strong>) have<strong> congenital hepatic fibrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>ADPKD is associated with<strong> </strong>mutations in <em><strong>PKD1</strong>,</em> encoding polycystin 1, and <em><strong>PKD2</strong>,</em> encoding polycystin 2. The multiple expanding <strong>cysts </strong>of both kidneys ultimately <strong>destroy</strong> the <strong>renal parenchyma</strong> and cause renal failure. Cysts arise from the tubules throughout the nephron and, therefore, have variable lining epithelia.</p>\n<div class=\"c-virtual_list__item\" data-qa=\"virtual-list-item\" id=\"1642075042.001800\" role=\"listitem\" tabindex=\"0\">\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__background p-message_pane_message__message c-message_kit__message\" data-qa=\"message_container\" data-qa-placeholder=\"false\" data-qa-unprocessed=\"false\" role=\"presentation\">\n<div aria-roledescription=\"message\" class=\"c-message_kit__hover\" data-qa-hover=\"true\" role=\"document\">\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__actions c-message_kit__actions--default\">\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__gutter\">\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__gutter__right\" data-qa=\"message_content\" role=\"presentation\">\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__blocks c-message_kit__blocks--rich_text\">\n<div class=\"c-message__message_blocks c-message__message_blocks--rich_text\">\n<div class=\"p-block_kit_renderer\" data-qa=\"block-kit-renderer\">\n<div class=\"p-block_kit_renderer__block_wrapper p-block_kit_renderer__block_wrapper--first\">\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_block\" dir=\"auto\">\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_section\">The common <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">extrarenal manifestations</strong> of ADPKD include: </div>\n<ul class=\"p-rich_text_list p-rich_text_list__bullet\" data-border=\"0\" data-indent=\"0\" data-stringify-type=\"unordered-list\">\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\"><strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">Hepatic cysts</strong> - <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">most common </strong></li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Seminal vesicle cysts - can result in infertility</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Arachnoid membrane cysts - usually asymptomatic, may increase the risk of subdural hematoma</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Pancreatic cysts </li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Splenic cysts</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Intracranial berry aneurysms</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Subarachnoid hemorrhages</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Mitral valve prolapse </li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"c-virtual_list__item\" data-qa=\"virtual-list-item\" id=\"1642075074.002000\" role=\"listitem\" tabindex=\"-1\">\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__background c-message_kit__background--hovered p-message_pane_message__message c-message_kit__message\" data-qa=\"message_container\" data-qa-placeholder=\"false\" data-qa-unprocessed=\"false\" role=\"presentation\">\n<div aria-roledescription=\"message\" class=\"c-message_kit__hover c-message_kit__hover--hovered\" data-qa-hover=\"true\" role=\"document\">\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__actions c-message_kit__actions--above\">\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__gutter\">\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__gutter__right\" data-qa=\"message_content\" role=\"presentation\">\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__blocks c-message_kit__blocks--rich_text\">\n<div class=\"c-message__message_blocks c-message__message_blocks--rich_text\">\n<div class=\"p-block_kit_renderer\" data-qa=\"block-kit-renderer\">\n<div class=\"p-block_kit_renderer__block_wrapper p-block_kit_renderer__block_wrapper--first\">\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_block\" dir=\"auto\">\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_section\">There are very few case reports with lung cysts in ADPKD patients, but the association between ADPKD and lung cysts has not been proved. However, there is a three-fold increase in the incidence of <strong>bronchiectasis</strong> in ADPKD patients. </div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5388","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about the polycysti​n gene?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the polycystin gene?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polycystin-1 is most commo‌nly involved in ADPKD","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polycystin-2 is a calcium ‌ion channel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mutation in polycystin-2 c‌auses more severe form of​ ADPKD","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Polycystin-1 is involved i‌n cell interactions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mutation in polycystin-2 causes more severe form of ADPKD","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mutation in polycystin-2 causes more severe form of ADPKD</strong></p><p>The<strong> polycystin-2</strong> mutation is <strong>less commonly</strong> involved and <strong>less severe</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Polycystin-1 </strong>is encoded by the <em>PKD1</em> gene located on chromosome 16p13.3. It is expressed in tubular epithelial cells, particularly those of the distal nephron. It contains domains that are usually involved in <strong>cell-cell</strong> and <strong>cell-matrix interactions</strong>. Mutations in <em>PKD1</em> account for about<strong> 85%</strong> of cases of <strong>ADPKD</strong> (autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease) and it causes a <strong>more severe</strong> form of the disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Polycystin-2 </strong>is an integral membrane protein that is expressed in all segments of the renal tubules and in many extrarenal tissues. It functions as a <strong>Ca<sup>2+</sup></strong> <strong>cation</strong> <strong>channel</strong>. Overall, it causes a less severe form of ADPKD disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5391","text":"What is the most common ex‌trarenal site of cysts in​ autosomal dominant polyc‌ystic kidney disease?","raw_text":"What is the most common extrarenal site of cysts in autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Li‌ver","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lu‌ng","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Panc‌reas","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spl‌een","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Liver</strong></p><p>The most common <strong>extrarenal</strong> site of cysts in autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (<strong>ADPKD</strong>) is the <strong>liver</strong>.</p>\n<p>40% of individuals with autosomal-dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD) have one to several cysts in the liver (<strong>polycystic liver disease</strong>) that are usually asymptomatic. The cysts are derived from the <strong>biliary epithelium</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cysts occur much less frequently in the spleen, pancreas, and lungs. There are very few case reports with lung cysts in ADPKD patients, but the association between ADPKD and lung cysts has not been proved.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5386","text":"In patients with ADPKD, th‌e mutated genes are locat​ed on which chromosomes?","raw_text":"In patients with ADPKD,  the mutated genes are located on which chromosomes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"16‌, 4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"11‌, 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"11,‌ 16","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"16‌, 6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 16, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 16, 4</strong></p><p>The genes mutated in autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease <strong>(ADPKD)</strong> are located on <strong>chromosomes 16 and 4.</strong></p>\n<p>This condition occurs due to mutations of the <strong>PKD1 </strong>gene (chromosome 16) and <strong>PKD2 </strong>gene (on chromosome 4). These genes encode for <strong>polycystin 1 and 2</strong> proteins respectively.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5392","text":"Mutations in fibrocystin a‌re seen in which conditio​n?","raw_text":"Mutations in fibrocystin are seen in which condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal dominant polycys‌tic kidney disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medullary s‌ponge kidney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Multicystic renal dysplasi‌a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autosomal recessive polycy‌stic kidney disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease</strong></p><p>Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (<strong>ARPKD</strong>) is caused by mutations in the<strong> <em>PKHD1</em> </strong>gene<strong> </strong>on chromosome <strong>6p21-p23</strong> which encodes <strong>fibrocystin</strong>.</p>\n<p>The gene is highly expressed in adult and fetal<strong> kidneys</strong> and also in the <strong>liver</strong> and <strong>pancreas</strong>. Fibrocystin has been localized to the <strong>primary cilium</strong> of tubular cells.</p>\n<p>The serious manifestations of ARPKD are usually <strong>present at birth</strong>, and may cause death by renal failure. The following gross findings are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Enlarged kidneys with <strong>smooth external</strong> appearance</li>\n<li><strong>Spongelike</strong> appearance due to numerous small cysts in the cortex and medulla</li>\n<li>Dilated elongated channels at right angles to the cortical surface, completely replacing the medulla and cortex</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On microscopic examination, there is cylindrical or, less commonly, <strong>saccular dilation </strong>of all<strong> collecting tubules </strong>with linning of<strong> cuboidal cells. </strong>In almost all cases, the liver has cysts associated with portal fibrosis and proliferation of the portal bile ducts, termed <strong>congenital hepatic fibrosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5826","text":"Hepatic fibrosis is a feat‌ure of which of the follo​wing conditions?","raw_text":"Hepatic fibrosis is a feature of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Childhood polycystic kidne‌y disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Medullary s‌ponge Kidney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acquired renal cystic dise‌ase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autosomal-dominant polycys‌tic kidney disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Childhood polycystic kidney disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Childhood polycystic kidney disease</strong></p><p><strong>Hepatic fibrosis</strong> is a feature of <strong>childhood</strong> (autosomal recessive)<strong> polycystic kidney disease.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6683","text":"A routine urine examinatio‌n of a 46-year-old woman ​shows thorn-apple crystal‌s. Past history is signif​icant for constipation an‍d longstanding use of lax‍atives. What is the likel‌y composition of these cr​ystals?","raw_text":"A routine urine examination of a 46-year-old woman shows thorn-apple crystals. Past history is significant for constipation and longstanding use of laxatives. What is the likely composition of these crystals?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calcium‌ oxalate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Triple p‌hosphate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uric‌ acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ammonium‌ biurate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ammonium biurate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ammonium biurate</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Ammonium biurate crystals</strong> appear as spheres with radial or concentric striations and irregular projections or thorns, referred to as <strong>thorn apples. </strong>They<strong> </strong>are seen in <strong>laxative abuse</strong>.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6684","text":"In patients with chronic k‌idney disease, what type ​of casts are seen?","raw_text":"In patients with chronic kidney disease, what type of casts are seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Broad‌ casts","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"WBC ‌casts","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epitheli‌al casts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Granula‌r casts","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Broad casts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Broad casts</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Broad casts </strong>are seen in patients with chronic kidney disease (<strong>CKD</strong>). They are also known as<strong> renal failure casts.</strong></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Broad casts are defined as those with a diameter 2-6 times that of normal casts. They indicate <strong>chronically damaged</strong> tubules or collecting ducts. </span><span class=\"s1\">They indicate a <strong>poor prognosis. </strong></span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"tATD8zuy","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"44_anti_virals__non_retroviral_","title":"44.Anti Virals (Non Retroviral)","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MB6467","text":"A 54-year-old man was brou‌ght to the casualty with ‌fever, headache, focal se‌izures, and right hemipar‌esis. MRI brain showed hy​perintensity in the left ‌frontotemporal region. PC​R of his CSF sample was p​ositive for HSV DNA. What​ would be the drug of cho‍ice for this condition?","raw_text":"A 54-year-old man was brought to the casualty with fever, headache, focal seizures, and right hemiparesis. MRI brain showed hyperintensity in the left frontotemporal region. PCR of his CSF sample was positive for HSV DNA. What would be the drug of choice for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Valacy‌clovir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acyc‌lovir","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gancy‌clovir","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fosc‌arnet","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acyclovir","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acyclovir</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and investigations are suggestive of <strong>HSV encephalitis</strong> for which the drug of choice is <strong>intravenous acyclovir</strong>. The treatment is effective only if it is started early.</p>\n<p>The clinical hallmark of HSV encephalitis is the acute onset of fever and focal neurologic signs with hemorrhagic necrosis of especially the <strong>temporal lobe</strong>. The most sensitive investigation is HSV DNA demonstration in CSF by <strong>PCR</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6470","text":"A patient diagnosed with s‌hingles was prescribed ac‌yclovir. Which of the fol‌lowing statements is fals‌e regarding this drug?","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with shingles was prescribed acyclovir. Which of the following statements is false regarding this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acyclovir is a nucleoside ‌congener","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nephrotoxicity is a dose l‌imiting side effect of ac‌yclovir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Valacyclovir has a higher ‌bioavailability than acyc‌lovir","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acyclovir does not enter t‌he CSF","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acyclovir does not enter the CSF","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/90a29df15d924ea084eb85bb747cb5c4x1280x1406.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acyclovir does not enter the CSF</strong></p><p>Acyclovir <strong>can enter CSF</strong> as it is widely distributed into all body fluid compartments, including vesicular fluid and aqueous humor. It can be used for treating shingles caused by HZV.</p>\n<p>Option A: Acyclovir is a <strong>guanosine </strong>nucleoside congener. The other nucleoside congeners are cidofovir, ganciclovir, and famciclovir.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Nephrotoxicity</strong> and<strong> neurotoxicity</strong>, though rare and reversible, are <strong>dose-limiting </strong>side effects of acyclovir.</p>\n<p>Option C: Acyclovir has a low bioavailability which is overcome by administering <strong>valacyclovir</strong>, a prodrug with <strong>higher bioavailability</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mechanism of action of anti-viral drugs:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4177","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of resistance to acyc‌lovir in HSV-1?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of resistance to acyclovir in HSV-1?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Induction of‌ efflux pumps","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mutation of viral DNA poly‌merase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deficient viral thymidine ‌kinase activity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Expression of inactivating‌ enzymes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Deficient viral thymidine kinase activity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ed5c8c53b9274a568dc382eba1a70f66x1280x1406.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Deficient viral thymidine kinase activity</strong></p><p>The most common cause of resistance to acyclovir in HSV-1 is<strong> deficient </strong>or<strong> absent viral thymidine kinase </strong>activity.</p>\n<p>Viral thymidine kinase is needed in the <strong>conversion of acyclovir</strong> to acyclovir triphosphate which competitively inhibits the viral DNA polymerase activity.  </p>\n<p>Mutation of viral DNA polymerase is rare.</p>\n<p>Mechanism of action of anti-viral drugs:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6468","text":"In which of the following ‌patients can valacyclovir‌ be used as the drug of c‌hoice?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients can valacyclovir be used as the drug of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"An elderly woman with avia‌n influenza","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"An infant with RSV bronchi‌olitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"An adolescent with chicken‌pox","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"An immunocompromised patie‌nt with CMV retinitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. An adolescent with chickenpox","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. An adolescent with chickenpox</strong></p><p><strong>Oral valacyclovir</strong> 1g three times daily for 5-7 days is considered the drug of choice for <strong>chickenpox (VZV) </strong>in an immunocompetent patient. Treatment should be started ideally <strong>within 24 hours</strong> of rash onset.</p>\n<p>Oral acyclovir (800 mg five times daily) is an alternative drug, but valacyclovir is preferred due to its <strong>higher bioavailability </strong>and less frequent dosing. In immunocompromised patients, varicella infection is treated with IV acyclovir.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Oseltamivir</strong> is used for the treatment of avian influenza.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Ribavirin</strong> is used for treating RSV bronchiolitis.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option D: Ganciclovir, foscarnet, and cidofovir are approved for CMV retinitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4180","text":"A patient showed persisten‌ce of genital herpes lesi‌ons even after 7 days of ‌high-dose oral acyclovir ‌therapy. Which of the fol​lowing antivirals can you‌ use to manage this case?​","raw_text":"A patient showed persistence of genital herpes lesions even after 7 days of high-dose oral acyclovir therapy. Which of the following antivirals can you use to manage this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ganci‌clovir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abac‌avir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fosc‌arnet","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Valacy‌clovir","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Foscarnet","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Foscarnet</strong></p><p>Intravenous <strong>foscarnet</strong> is used to treat <strong>acyclovir-resistant</strong> <strong>HSV</strong> (genital herpes) and <strong>VZV</strong> infections.</p>\n<p>It<strong> </strong>inhibits viral nucleic acid synthesis<strong> </strong>by <strong>directly inhibiting HSV DNA polymerase </strong>(or HIV reverse transcriptase). It is active against herpes simplex, CMV, VZV, and HIV.</p>\n<p>Metabolic abnormalities are very common with foscarnet. Symptomatic <strong>hypocalcemia </strong>and<strong> nephrotoxicity</strong> are major <strong>dose-limiting</strong> toxicities of foscarnet. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6471","text":"Which of the following is ‌a true statement about am‌antadine?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a true statement about amantadine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The most common side effec‌t is CNS toxicity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is effective only again‌st Influenza B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is routinely used for t‌he treatment of influenza‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is largely metabolised ‌by the liver","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The most common side effect is CNS toxicity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The most common side effect is CNS toxicity</strong></p><p>The most common side effect of amantadine is <strong>minor CNS toxicity</strong> which includes nervousness, difficulty concentrating, and insomnia. It is effective only against<strong> Influenza A</strong>.</p>\n<p>Most strains of influenza are now resistant to amantadine and hence, it is <strong>not routinely used</strong> for the treatment or prophylaxis of influenza infections. Amantadine is largely excreted<strong> unmetabolized </strong>in the <strong>urine</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0191","text":"A young woman with a recen‌t travel history presente‌d with fever and respirat‌ory difficulty. She was d‌iagnosed with Influenza A​ and was started on oselt‌amivir. What is the mecha​nism of action of this dr​ug?","raw_text":"A young woman with a recent travel history presented with fever and respiratory difficulty. She was diagnosed with Influenza A and was started on oseltamivir. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DNA polymera‌se inhibition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Protein synthesis inhibiti‌on","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nucleosid‌e analogue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neuraminidas‌e inhibition","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neuraminidase inhibition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neuraminidase inhibition</strong></p><p>Oseltamivir is a <strong>neuraminidase inhibitor.</strong></p>\n<p>Drugs like oseltamivir and zanamivir competitively and reversibly interact with the enzyme sites to inhibit viral neuraminidase activity. This interferes with the release of the progeny from infected host cells, halting the spread of infection.</p>\n<p>Early administration <span class=\"fontstyle0\">hastens the resolution of symptoms if given within 48 h of infection. O</span>seltamivir is given at a dose of <strong>75 mg, twice a day for 5 days</strong> for the <strong>treatment</strong> of influenza A and B. For prophylaxis, it is given at a dose of <strong>75mg, once per day,</strong> and continued for the duration of exposure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6474","text":"Zanamivir is to be given f‌or a patient with an infl‌uenza B infection. How wi‌ll you administer this dr‌ug?","raw_text":"Zanamivir is to be given for a patient with an influenza B infection. How will you administer this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral ‌route","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhalatio‌nal route","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intraven‌ous route","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intramusc‌ular route","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inhalational route","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inhalational route</strong></p><p>Zanamivir is delivered by oral <strong>inhalation </strong>of dry powder in a lactose carrier. Its oral bioavailability is very low (&lt;5%).</p>\n<p>Zanamivir is a neuraminidase inhibitor similar to oseltamivir and is active against both <strong>influenza A and B</strong>.</p>\n<p>The different routes of administration of neuraminidase inhibitors are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Zanamivir: Inhalational</li>\n<li>Oseltamivir: Oral</li>\n<li>Peramivir: Intravenous</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6475","text":"A 22-year-old man is admit‌ted with acute H1N1 influ‌enza pneumonia. He is hav‌ing severe nausea and vom‌iting and is not able to ​take any oral medications‌. Which of the following ​drugs can be administered​ intravenously to him?","raw_text":"A 22-year-old man is admitted with acute H1N1 influenza pneumonia. He is having severe nausea and vomiting and is not able to take any oral medications. Which of the following drugs can be administered intravenously to him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oselt‌amivir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zana‌mivir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pera‌mivir","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ente‌cavir","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Peramivir","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Peramivir</strong></p><p><strong>Peramivir</strong> is a neuraminidase inhibitor available for <strong>intravenous </strong>administration for acute influenza (H1N1).</p>\n<p>It is indicated in acute influenza infections where the patient is not able to take drugs via the oral route or if inhalational (zanamivir) formulation is unavailable.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9672","text":"A 40-year-old man, who has‌ history of contact with ‌a known case of influenza‌, is having fever, cough,‌ and runny nose since tod​ay morning. Rapid antigen‌ test turns out to be pos​itive. The endonuclease i​nhibitor that can be used​ for his treatment is ___‍________.","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man, who has history of contact with a known case of influenza, is having fever, cough, and runny nose since today morning. Rapid antigen test turns out to be positive. The endonuclease inhibitor that can be used for his treatment is ___________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oselt‌amivir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Balo‌xavir","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Zana‌mivir","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Favip‌ravir","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Baloxavir","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Baloxavir</strong></p><p>The <strong>endonuclease</strong> <strong>inhibitor</strong> which can be used for the treatment of acute uncomplicated influenza in adults is <strong>baloxavir marboxil.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a novel drug that was approved by the FDA in 2018. It is used to treat acute <strong>uncomplicated influenza</strong> in patients <strong>12 years </strong>of age<strong> and older</strong> who have been symptomatic for <strong>less than 48 hours. </strong></p>\n<p>It is a <strong>selective inhibitor </strong>of influenza cap-dependent<strong> endonuclease</strong> which blocks proliferation by inhibiting mRNA synthesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6478","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is approved for prophy‌laxis against respiratory‌ syncytial virus (RSV) in‌fection?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is approved for prophylaxis against respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Omali‌zumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paliv‌izumab","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Deno‌sumab","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Riba‌virin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Palivizumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Palivizumab</strong></p><p><strong>Palivizumab</strong> is a monoclonal antibody approved for <strong>prophylaxis</strong> against respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) infection.</p>\n<p>It is a humanized monoclonal antibody against F-glycoprotein present on the surface of RSV. It is used in <strong>high-risk infants </strong>(prematurity and immunosuppressed conditions)<strong> </strong>and<strong> children</strong> (with cardiovascular and pulmonary diseases).</p>\n<p>The <strong>treatment</strong> of RSV infections is done with <strong>inhaled ribavirin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Omalizumab is a monoclonal antibody against IgE indicated in asthma.</p>\n<p>Option C: Denosumab is a monoclonal antibody against RANKL indicated in osteoporosis.</p>\n<p>Option D: Ribavirin is used for treatment of RSV infections but is not approved for prophylaxis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4182","text":"A hepatitis B infected pat‌ient awaiting liver trans‌plantation is admitted wi‌th ascites and encephalop‌athy. Which of the follow​ing is the drug of choice‌ for him?","raw_text":"A hepatitis B infected patient awaiting liver transplantation is admitted with ascites and encephalopathy. Which of the following is the drug of choice for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interfer‌on alpha","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lamiv‌udine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Teno‌fovir","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Telbi‌vudine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tenofovir","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tenofovir</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of <strong>ascites</strong> and <strong>encephalopathy</strong> in a hepatitis B infected patient is suggestive of <strong>decompensated liver disease</strong>. <strong>Tenofovir</strong> is the <strong>drug of choice</strong> for decompensated cirrhosis due to hepatitis B infection.</p>\n<p>Tenofovir<strong> </strong>is one of the <strong>first-line</strong> drugs for chronic hepatitis B infections. It is available as two prodrug formulations tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) and tenofovir alafenamide (TAF).</p>\n<p>Option A: Interferon-alpha is one of the first-line agents in individuals with<strong> ongoing HBV-DNA replication</strong> and liver inflammation. But it is <strong>contraindicated in decompensated cirrhosis</strong> with HBV infection as they can precipitate clinical deterioration and increase the risk of bacterial infections.  </p>\n<p>Options B and D: Lamivudine and Telbivudine are not considered first-line agents for HBV infections because of the increased frequency of resistance associated with their use.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6477","text":"Sofosbuvir is indicated in‌ the treatment of which o‌f the following patients?‌ A 33-year-old man with p‌ost-transfusion hepatitis​ B A 47-year-old man with‌ symptomatic chronic hepa​titis C A 24-year-old man​ with hepatitis B and D c​oinfection A 52-year-old ‍woman with hepatitis B an​d D superinfection A 42-y​ear-old woman with asympt​omatic chronic hepatitis ‍C","raw_text":"<p>Sofosbuvir is indicated in the treatment of which of the following patients?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A 33-year-old man with post-transfusion hepatitis B</li>\n<li>A 47-year-old man with symptomatic chronic hepatitis C</li>\n<li>A 24-year-old man with hepatitis B and D coinfection</li>\n<li>A 52-year-old woman with hepatitis B and D superinfection</li>\n<li>A 42-year-old woman with asymptomatic chronic hepatitis C</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 and ‌2 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 and ‌3 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 and ‌5 only","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2, 3 an‌d 4 only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2 and 5 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2 and 5 only</strong></p><p>Sofosbuvir is indicated in the treatment of <strong>hepatitis C</strong> infection only. It is a directly acting antiviral,<strong> NS5B</strong> nucleotide polymerase inhibitor.</p>\n<p>The goal of treatment in hepatitis C is a <strong>sustained virologic response</strong> (SVR). It is defined as the absence of a virus in the blood (undetectable HCV RNA), 12 weeks after completion of treatment. This can be achieved by the use of<strong> pan-genotypic</strong> directly acting antiviral drugs, irrespective of the disease stage.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4185","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a direct-acting agent‌ (DAAs) used in the treat‌ment of HCV infections?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a direct-acting agent (DAAs) used in the treatment of HCV infections?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NS5B polymerase inhibitors‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"NS5A in‌hibitors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NS3/4A protease inhibitors‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"NS4B in‌hibitors","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. NS4B inhibitors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. NS4B inhibitors</strong></p><p>NS4B inhibitors are <strong>not</strong> direct-acting agents (DAAs).</p>\n<p>Direct-acting agents (DAAs) are drugs that <strong>target specific viral proteins</strong> which are vital to the <strong>replication </strong>and<strong> lifecycle</strong> of <strong>HCV</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>currently available DAAs</strong> inhibit 3 specific non-structural (NS) proteins:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>NS3/4A protease inhibitors</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>NS5A inhibitors</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>NS5B polymerase inhibitors  </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4192","text":"A 52-year-old woman with h‌istory of hepatitis B inf‌ection presents with fati‌gue, vomiting, and weight‌ loss. Her serological ma​rkers are given in the ta‌ble below. What is the tr​eatment of choice for her​ condition? Diagnostic ma​rkers Result HBsAg + IgM ‍Anti-HBc - HDAg - HDV RNA​ + IgG Anti-HDV +","raw_text":"<p>A 52-year-old woman with history of hepatitis B infection presents with fatigue, vomiting, and weight loss. Her serological markers are given in the table below. What is the treatment of choice for her condition?</p>\n<table style=\"width: 222px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151.848px;\">Diagnostic markers</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75.1523px;\">Result</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151.848px;\">HBsAg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75.1523px;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151.848px;\">IgM Anti-HBc</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75.1523px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151.848px;\">HDAg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75.1523px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151.848px;\">HDV RNA </td>\n<td style=\"width: 75.1523px;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 151.848px;\">IgG Anti-HDV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75.1523px;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paritaprevir booster with ‌ritonavir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pegylated interferon-alpha‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Velpatasvir + Voxilaprevir‌ + Sofosbuvir","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tenofovir ‌+ Entecavir","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pegylated interferon-alpha","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pegylated interferon-alpha</strong></p><p>The given scenario and serology are suggestive of <strong>chronic hepatitis D infection</strong> in a hepatitis B carrier for which the treatment of choice is <strong>pegylated interferon-alpha</strong> (pegIFN-<span style=\"font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;\">α</span>).</p>\n<p>It is the only drug currently available to treat chronic hepatitis D infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7793","text":"A young woman presents wit‌h painless warty lesions ‌on the vulva as shown in ‌the image below. Which of‌ the following is a topic​al agent that can be pres‌cribed for her condition?​","raw_text":"A young woman presents with painless warty lesions on the vulva as shown in the image below. Which of the following is a topical agent that can be prescribed for her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Doco‌sanol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nyst‌atin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Imiq‌uimod","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acyc‌lovir","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Imiquimod","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c8066293e7f1477da782821d1de724de.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Imiquimod</strong></p><p>Warty lesions on the vulva as seen in the given image is suggestive of <strong>condyloma acuminata </strong>caused by <strong>HPV</strong> (6, 11)<strong>. Imiquimod </strong>is a<strong> topical </strong>agent with antiviral and immunomodulatory properties indicated in the treatment of external genital and perianal warts.</p>\n<p>Other topical agents that can be used include podophyllotoxin and trichloroacetic acid. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5665","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is currently approved ‌for the treatment of smal‌l pox?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is currently approved for the treatment of small pox?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tecov‌irimat","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lumac‌aftor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pegva‌liase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apalu‌tamide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tecovirimat","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tecovirimat</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Tecovirimat</strong> has been recently approved as a <strong>treatment of smallpox</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">It was <strong>approved under the FDA's animal rule</strong>, which allows findings from animal studies to support an FDA approval when it is not feasible or ethical to conduct efficacy trials in humans. Cidofovir has also been approved by the FDA for smallpox under this rule.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Both these drugs are currently stockpiled by CDC’s strategic national stockpile, which has the medical supplies required to protect the public in case of a public health emergency.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Tecovirimat could also be used under an investigational new drug (IND) protocol to<strong> treat</strong> <strong>adverse reactions from vaccinia</strong> vaccination.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option B: <strong>Lumacaftor </strong>is a drug approved for<strong> cystic fibrosis.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option C: <strong>Pegvaliase</strong> has been approved for the treatment of<strong> phenylketonuria</strong> in adults.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option D: <strong>Apalutamide</strong> is a nonsteroidal <strong>antiandrogen</strong> medication which is used in the treatment of prostate cancer. It is specifically indicated for use in conjunction with castration in the treatment of <strong>non-metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Lhfz_9Cs","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"44_head_injury","title":"44.Head Injury","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA4373","text":"A patient is brought to th​e casualty following a he​ad injury. He is unconsci‌ous and is not able to op‌en his eyes. He also has ​no verbal or motor respon​ses. What will be his GCS‌ score?","raw_text":"A patient is brought to the casualty following a head injury. He is unconscious and is not able to open his eyes. He also has no verbal or motor responses. What will be his GCS score?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"​2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3</strong></p><p>The GCS score in this patient will be <strong>3</strong>. The <strong>lowest possible</strong> Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score is 3 and <strong>never zero.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3790","text":"A patient is confused, ope​ns his eyes on painful st​imuli, flexes one limb on‌ painful stimuli, and can‌ localize the stimulus wi​th the other limb. His Gl​asgow Coma Score is _____‌___","raw_text":"A patient is confused, opens his eyes on painful stimuli,  flexes one limb on painful stimuli, and can localize the stimulus with the other limb. His Glasgow Coma Score is ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​8","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​9","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1​0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1​1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 11","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 11</strong></p><p>The GCS in this patient is <strong>11</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The score for verbal response: 4, because he is confused.</li>\n<li>The score for eye-opening: 2, because his eyes are opening to only painful stimuli.</li>\n<li>The score for <strong>best motor response</strong>: 5, because he can localize pain. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, the GCS score is (4+2+5) = 11.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4861","text":"An 18-month-old child is b​rought to the ER after su​staining a head injury fr‌om a fall. On examination‌, she moans to pain. What​ is her verbal response s​core on the GCS scale?","raw_text":"An 18-month-old child is brought to the ER after sustaining a head injury from a fall. On examination, she moans to pain. What is her verbal response score on the GCS scale?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2</strong></p><p>The verbal response of \"moans to pain\" in a child <strong>&lt;2 years of age </strong>is<strong> 2.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Verbal response:</strong></p>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"height: 321px;\" width=\"323\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 139.83px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><strong><span style=\"color: #000000; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">Adult</span></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.557px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><strong><span style=\"color: #000000; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">Pediatric</span></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 42.5568px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><strong><span style=\"color: #000000; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">Score</span></strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 139.83px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">Oriented</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.557px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">Age-appropriate vocalization, smile</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 42.5568px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">5</span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 139.83px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">Confused, Disoriented</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.557px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">Cries, irritable</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 42.5568px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">4</span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 139.83px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">Inappropriate words</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.557px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">Cries to pain</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 42.5568px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">3</span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 139.83px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">Incomprehensible sounds</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132.557px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">Moans to pain</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 42.5568px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">2</span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 139.83px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><span style=\"color: #000000; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">None </span></p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 42.5568px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p><span style=\"color: #000000; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">1</span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 228\"> </div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 228\">\n<p><strong>Motor response:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 330.483px;\"><colgroup><col/><col/><col/> </colgroup>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 130px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Adult</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"layoutArea\"> </div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\"><strong>Pediatric</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.4829px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Score</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 130px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Obeys commands</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Moves spontaneously and purposefully</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.4829px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>6</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 130px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Localizes painful stimulus</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Withdraws to touch</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.4829px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>5</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 130px; text-align: center;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Withdraws in response to pain</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.4829px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>4</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 130px; text-align: center;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p> Decorticate posturing (abnormal flexion) in response to pain</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.4829px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>3</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 130px; text-align: center;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p> Decerebrate posturing (abnormal extension) in response to pain</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.4829px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>2</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 130px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">No response</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 36.4829px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>1</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Eye-opening:</strong> Same in infants and children as in adults</p>\n<table border=\"0\" width=\"253\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Eyes Opening</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td><strong>Score</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spontaneously</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>To verbal command</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>To painful stimulus</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Do not open</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 163\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Non-testable</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td>NT</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4863","text":"After assessing a patient ​with a traumatic head inj​ury, a score of 3 was giv‌en for motor response. Ac‌cording to the Glasgow Co​ma Scale, what does the s​core indicate?","raw_text":"After assessing a patient with a traumatic head injury, a score of 3 was given for motor response. According to the Glasgow Coma Scale, what does the score indicate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decortica​te posture","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decerebra​te posture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Withdrawa​l to pain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Exte​nsion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Decorticate posture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Decorticate posture</strong></p><p>Motor response of <strong>3</strong> indicates <strong>decorticate posture or abnormal flexion</strong> to pain stimulus.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1235","text":"A 35-year-old woman is bro​ught to the casualty ward​ with a history of road t‌raffic accident and head ‌trauma. She opens her eye​s to pain, moans, can loc​alize her limb towards th‌e pain stimulus on the le‍ft hand and away from the‌ pain stimulus on the rig​ht hand and both the legs‍ are in extended posture.​ Calculate her GCS.","raw_text":"A 35-year-old woman is brought to the casualty ward with a history of road traffic accident and head trauma. She opens her eyes to pain, moans, can localize her limb towards the pain stimulus on the left hand and away from the pain stimulus on the right hand and both the legs are in extended posture. Calculate her GCS.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​9","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 9","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 9</strong></p><p>The GCS (Glasgow Coma Scale) of this patient is <strong>E2V2M5</strong>, which comes to a total score of<strong> 9.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>E2 - Eye opens to pain</li>\n<li>V2 - Moans</li>\n<li>M5 - Localizes to pain on the left hand (the score represents the <strong>best performance elicited</strong>, which would be localized towards the pain stimulus)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4410","text":"Which of the following hea​d injury patients need no​t undergo head CT at this‌ point in time?","raw_text":"Which of the following head injury patients need not undergo head CT at this point in time?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"An RTA victim with GCS sco​re of 12","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A patient with an episode ​of vomiting","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A child with an episode of​ seizure post-trauma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A biker with suspected sku​ll fracture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A patient with an episode of vomiting","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A patient with an episode of vomiting</strong></p><p>A single episode of vomiting after a head injury is not an indication for immediate CT brain. However, <strong>more than one episode</strong> mandates a scan.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4405","text":"A 26-year-old batsman was ​rushed to the ER when he ​fell unconscious after a ‌cricket ball hit his head‌. He regained consciousne​ss later but started to d​eteriorate rapidly. A pla‌in CT of the patient is s‍hown below. What is the d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old batsman was rushed to the ER when he fell unconscious after a cricket ball hit his head. He regained consciousness later but started to deteriorate rapidly. A plain CT of the patient is shown below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extradura​l hematoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Subdural​ hematoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subarachnoi​d hemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intraparenc​hymal bleed","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Extradural hematoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/551ae0b7c64b4e8fa84d8d0166a1acd1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Extradural hematoma</strong></p><p>The presence of <strong>lucid interval</strong> in this patient and the CT image are suggestive of <strong>extradural hematoma</strong>. The above CT scan shows a large extradural hematoma (hyperdense <strong>biconvex</strong>/lentiform shape) with a mass effect.</p>\n<p>Extradural hemorrhage (EDH) is the accumulation of blood between the <strong>skull and the dura</strong>. It usually results from <strong>arterial</strong> disruption, especially of the <strong>middle meningeal artery</strong>. It lies behind the <strong>pterion</strong>, which is the thinnest part of the skull. </p>\n<p>In EDH, the patient is initially unconscious from the concussive aspect of the head trauma. The patient then awakens and has a “<strong>lucid interval</strong>,” while the hematoma sub-clinically expands. As the volume of the hematoma grows, <strong>intracranial pressure (ICP) increases</strong>, and the patient rapidly becomes lethargic and herniates.</p>\n<p><strong>Uncal herniation</strong> from an EDH classically causes <strong>ipsilateral third nerve palsy</strong> and <strong>contralateral hemiparesis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4406","text":"An alcoholic patient was b​rought to the casualty af​ter he was found lying ne‌ar a ditch. He had a lace‌ration over his scalp. Hi​s GCS on arrival was 13 a​nd after a few minutes, i‌t dropped to 10. A plain ‍CT scan was done and give‌n below. What will be you​r diagnosis?","raw_text":"An alcoholic patient was brought to the casualty after he was found lying near a ditch. He had a laceration over his scalp. His GCS on arrival was 13 and after a few minutes, it dropped to 10. A plain CT scan was done and given below. What will be your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extradura​l hematoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subdural​ hematoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acute on chronic subdural ​hemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subarachnoi​d hemorrhage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Subdural hematoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f597af3be92948c0b8d290d3c1348962.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Subdural hematoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the plain CT scan showing a <strong>concavo-convex shaped hematoma</strong> on the left side with a midline shift to the right is suggestive of <strong>subdural hematoma (SDH)</strong>.</p>\n<p>An <strong>acute SDH</strong> is the result of the accumulation of blood between the <strong>arachnoid membrane and the dura</strong>. It is usually due to the rupture of <strong>bridging veins</strong> running from the cerebral cortex to the dural sinuses.</p>\n<p>The level of consciousness is usually impaired at presentation and may deteriorate further as the hematoma expands.</p>\n<p><strong>Elderly</strong> and <strong>alcoholic</strong> patients are at a higher risk of acute SDH formation after head trauma due to brain atrophy.</p>\n<p><strong>Open craniotomy</strong> for the evacuation of acute SDH is indicated for the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Thickness &gt;1 cm</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Midline shift &gt;5 mm</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) drops by two or more points from the time of injury to hospitalization.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4407","text":"A 70-year-old man presents​ with headache, vomiting,​ and altered sensorium fo‌r the past 6 hours. His w‌ife reports him having a ​fall in the bathroom 4 we​eks ago. On evaluation, t‌he following funduscopic ‍findings are noted. What ‌will be your provisional ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old man presents with headache, vomiting, and altered sensorium for the past 6 hours. His wife reports him having a fall in the bathroom 4 weeks ago. On evaluation, the following funduscopic findings are noted. What will be your provisional diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extradura​l hematoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic subd​ural hematoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Subacute subdural hematoma​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intraventricular hemorrhag​e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chronic subdural hematoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/569b6bf2bada45d6916fd34c56f1e44b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chronic subdural hematoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and fundus examination are most probably suggestive of <strong>chronic subdural hematoma</strong>. The fundoscopic image shows <strong>dilated veins with a blurred disc margin</strong> which is suggestive of <strong>papilledema</strong>.</p>\n<p>Chronic SDH is a collection of blood breakdown products that are <strong>at least 2 to 3 weeks old</strong>. It occurs in patients without a clear history of head trauma, as they may arise from a minor head injury. <strong>Alcoholics</strong>, the <strong>elderly</strong>, and patients on <strong>anticoagulation</strong> are at higher risk for developing chronic SDH than others.</p>\n<p>Patients may present with headaches, seizures, confusion, contralateral hemiparesis, or coma. </p>\n<p>A chronic SDH &gt;1 cm or any symptomatic SDH should be surgically drained.</p>\n<table width=\"640\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p><strong>Nomenclature </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"409\">\n<p><strong>Time </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p><strong>Acute  SDH </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"409\">\n<p>&lt;3 days</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p><strong>Subacute SDH </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"409\">\n<p>4–21 days</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p><strong>Chronic SDH </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"409\">\n<p>&gt; 21 days</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4408","text":"A 50-year-old man presente​d with a sudden onset sev​ere headache and vomiting‌. His medical record reve‌aled he has autosomal dom​inant polycystic kidney d​isease. On examination, p‌ulse - 100 bpm, BP - 190/‍110 mm Hg, marked neck ri‌gidity was present and no​ neurological deficits we‍re noted. What could be t​he most likely cause of t‌he symptoms?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man presented with a sudden onset severe headache and vomiting. His medical record revealed he has autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease. On examination, pulse - 100 bpm, BP - 190/110 mm Hg, marked neck rigidity was present and no neurological deficits were noted. What could be the most likely cause of the symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute subdu​ral hematoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic subd​ural hematoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intraparenc​hymal bleed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subarachnoi​d hemorrhage","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Subarachnoid hemorrhage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/46fb80f9b65b4a4dacf21e9e7d6a0d08x720x913.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Subarachnoid hemorrhage</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH)</strong> due to the <strong>rupture of a berry aneurysm</strong>. It is an extrarenal manifestation of <strong>autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease.</strong> </p>\n<p>Clinical features of SAH:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sudden-onset severe headache (<strong>thunderclap headache</strong>) </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rapid onset vomiting</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Confusion or a lowered level of consciousness</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Seizures</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Meningitic features of neck stiffness and photophobia often develop over hours.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most common cause is <strong>trauma</strong> and is managed conservatively. The second most common cause is a <strong>rupture of a berry aneurysm</strong>.</p>\n<p>CT scan is the investigation of choice. A <strong>hyperattenuating material</strong> is seen filling the <strong>subarachnoid space</strong>. Most commonly this is apparent around the <strong>circle of Willis</strong>, on account of the majority of berry aneurysms occurring in this region.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>A <strong>lumbar puncture</strong> should be performed if the CT scan fails to establish the diagnosis of SAH. It will show <strong>xanthochromic CSF</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Delayed ischemic neurological deficit</strong> (DIND) is attributed to <strong>vasospasm</strong> of the cerebral vasculature typically developing 3–10 days following ictus. It is the main cause of a poor outcome.</p>\n<p>Management:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Endovascular treatment</strong> (\"coiling\") is generally preferred over craniotomy and clipping for aneurysms amenable to this approach.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The combination of induced hypertension, hypervolemia, and hemodilution (<strong>Triple-H therapy</strong>) is often utilized to prevent and treat cerebral vasospasm after aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: The combination of subarachnoid hemorrhage and vitreous hemorrhage (subhyaloid) is known as <strong>Terson syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4414","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false regardin​g delayed ischemic neurol‌ogical deficit?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding delayed ischemic neurological deficit?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is seen in subarachnoid​ haemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It usually occurs after 14​ days","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is attributed to cerebr​al vasospasm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nimodipine is used for pro​phylaxis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It usually occurs after 14 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It usually occurs after 14 days</strong></p><p>Delayed ischemic neurological deficit (DIND) usually occurs <strong>between 3 and 10 days</strong> after the SAH.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>delayed complication</strong> of <strong>subarachnoid hemorrhage</strong> characterized by the fluctuating decline in consciousness and <strong>appearance of neurological deficits</strong>. It is attributed to ischemia resulting from reactive <strong>cerebral vasospasm</strong>. Patients with a larger blood load (in the subarachnoid space) are at higher risk of vasospasm.</p>\n<p>Medical management strategies aim to minimize secondary brain injury, through avoidance of hypoxia and hypotension and control of ICP.</p>\n<p>The incidence of DIND can be lowered by <strong>prophylaxis with nimodipine</strong> (60 mg, 4 hourly) and hypervolemia (8 hourly normal saline).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4409","text":"A 26-year-old formula one ​driver was rushed to the ​casualty, unconscious, fo‌llowing a heavy crash fro‌m a side-impact collision​ during a racing event. T​he CT scan was normal. Ho‌wever, MRI showed multipl‍e small hemorrhages, espe‌cially in the corpus call​osum and the dorsolateral‍ midbrain, with no midlin​e shift. What is the most‌ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old formula one driver was rushed to the casualty, unconscious, following a heavy crash from a side-impact collision during a racing event. The CT scan was normal. However, MRI showed multiple small hemorrhages, especially in the corpus callosum and the dorsolateral midbrain, with no midline shift. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diffuse isc​hemic injury","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subdural​ hematoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diffuse ax​onal injury","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Subarachnoi​d Hemorrhage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diffuse axonal injury","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c6611b27ff084c5b84759f769c16c1f0x294x328.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diffuse axonal injury</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with a normal CT and MRI showing evidence of hemorrhages in the corpus callosum and midbrain is probably due to a <strong>diffuse axonal injury</strong>.</p>\n<p>A diffuse axonal injury is a form of primary brain injury seen in <strong>high-energy accidents.</strong> It is caused by <strong>damage to axons</strong> throughout the brain due to <strong>rotational acceleration and then deceleration</strong>. Axons may be completely disrupted and then retract, forming axon balls.</p>\n<p>Small hemorrhages can be seen in more severe cases, especially on <strong>MRI</strong> (investigation of choice). Hemorrhage is classically seen in the <strong>corpus callosum</strong> and the <strong>dorsolateral midbrain</strong>. The prognosis is very poor.</p>\n<p>MRI showing multiple <strong>hypointense punctate foci</strong> within the subcortical and deep white matter within the corpus callosum is characteristic of diffuse axonal injury.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4420","text":"According to the Glasgow O​utcome Score, what score ​will you give to a patien‌t who is in a persistent ‌vegetative state followin​g a head injury?","raw_text":"According to the Glasgow Outcome Score, what score will you give to a patient who is in a persistent vegetative state following a head injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"​1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2</strong></p><p> A persistent vegetative state is given a score of 2, according to the Glasgow Outcome Score.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Glasgow Outcome Scale</strong> <strong>(GOS)</strong> is different from the Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS), and it is used to quantify the degree of recovery achieved after a head injury, especially for research purposes.</p>\n<p>The scoring system is as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 295px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 208.177px;\"><strong>Patient condition</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 71.8229px;\"><strong>Score</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 208.177px;\">Good recovery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 71.8229px;\">5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 208.177px;\">Moderate disability</td>\n<td style=\"width: 71.8229px;\">4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 208.177px;\">Severe disability</td>\n<td style=\"width: 71.8229px;\">3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 208.177px;\">Persistent vegetative state</td>\n<td style=\"width: 71.8229px;\">2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 208.177px;\">Dead</td>\n<td style=\"width: 71.8229px;\">1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4413","text":"A patient is recovering fr​om a spinal shock after a​ fall from height. Which ‌of the following is the f‌irst reflex that you will​ expect to reappear in hi​m?","raw_text":"A patient is recovering from a spinal shock after a fall from height. Which of the following is the first reflex that you will expect to reappear in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cremaster​ic reflex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bulbocavern​osus reflex","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Knee​ jerk","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ankle ​reflex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bulbocavernosus reflex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/86405a06e69d4f6cb9cff55557938b12x1280x1912.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bulbocavernosus reflex</strong></p><p><strong>Bulbocavernosus reflex</strong> is the <strong>first reflex to return</strong> after the spinal shock has resolved.</p>\n<p>It is elicited by squeezing the glans penis in males and applying pressure to the clitoris or pulling the Foleys catheter in females. Any of these will lead to <strong>anal sphincter contraction</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Spinal shock</strong> is a condition of transient physiologic reflex depression of spinal cord function (motor, sensory, tone, reflexes) below the level of injury. It usually results from a high-impact, direct trauma to the spinal cord. When the <strong>level of injury is above T6</strong>, it can result in hemodynamic changes such as <strong>hypotension and bradycardia</strong> (due to loss of sympathetic tone and unopposed parasympathetic function).</p>\n<p>The resolution of spinal shock occurs over a period of days to months, when the areflexia slowly transitions to <strong>hyperreflexia and spasticity. </strong>Reflexes return <strong>sequentially</strong> rather than simultaneously. The resolution of the somatic component of spinal shock is classically signaled by the <strong>return of the bulbocavernosus reflex</strong>, a polysynaptic cutaneous spinal reflex (mediated by the conus medullaris) which is then followed by monosynaptic deep tendon reflexes (e.g. ankle jerk). </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4411","text":"Which of the following is ​not used for managing a p​atient with raised intrac‌ranial pressure following‌ a head injury?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not used for managing a patient with raised intracranial pressure following a head injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reverse trendelenburg posi​tion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IV Dexam​ethasone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Seda​tion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mannitol​ infusion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. IV Dexamethasone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. IV Dexamethasone</strong></p><p><strong>Steroids</strong> are <strong>not</strong> used in severe head injury, since they are associated with <strong>increased mortali</strong><strong>ty</strong><strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Initial management of raised intracranial pressure (ICP):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Airway protection </li>\n<li>Positioning the <strong>head up 20–30°</strong> (reverse Trendelenburg positioning)</li>\n<li>A bolus of <strong>mannitol </strong>up to 1 g/kg for free water diuresis (the effect is delayed by 20 min)</li>\n<li>Hypertonic saline (3% or 5%)</li>\n<li>Maintaining fluid and electrolyte balance</li>\n<li><strong>Seizure</strong> control</li>\n<li><strong>Sedation</strong> using a combination of opiates and barbiturates</li>\n<li><strong>Ventriculostomy</strong> and/or <strong>craniectomy </strong>may be needed for definitive decompression.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>ICP normally varies between <strong>4 and 14 mmHg.</strong> Sustained ICP levels above 25 mmHg can injure the brain. The goal of therapy in raised ICP is to maintain the ICP <strong>20 - 25 mmHg</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Cerebellar tonsillar herniation through the foramen magnum compresses medullary vasomotor and respiratory centers, classically producing Cushing’s triad. It is comprised of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bradycardia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Raised blood pressure</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Irregular respirations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4412","text":"In patients with head trau​ma, which of the followin​g is least likely to caus‌e secondary brain injurie‌s?","raw_text":"In patients with head trauma, which of the following is least likely to cause secondary brain injuries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyp​oxia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypot​ension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoc​apnea","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperg​lycemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypocapnea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypocapnea</strong></p><p><strong>Hypercapnea</strong> (and not hypocapnea) is a cause of <strong>secondary brain injury</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is essential to avoid <strong>hypercapnia</strong> in patients with head injury since it will <strong>raise intracranial pressure</strong> (ICP). While <strong>hyperventilation</strong> is an effective way of managing acute intracranial hypertension, it should be remembered that it can produce a <strong>dangerous decrease in cerebral blood flow</strong>, especially if cerebral perfusion pressure is low. </p>\n<p>Primary brain injury (focal and diffuse) results from mechanical injury at the time of the trauma. <strong>Secondary brain injury</strong> is caused by the physiologic responses to the initial injury. This is thought to involve a cascade of events, with edema and hematomas leading to increased intracranial pressure, which leads to compression and deformation of surrounding brain tissue and further damage. </p>\n<p> Causes of secondary brain injury:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypoxia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypotension</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Raised ICP &gt;20 mmHg</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Low cerebral perfusion pressure</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypercapnea</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hyperthermia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Seizures</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Metabolic disturbance (hyperglycemia)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4419","text":"A patient with a head inju​ry presents to the casual​ty with rapidly deteriora‌ting sensorium and progre‌ssive dilatation and fixa​tion of the pupil. Neuros​urgeon and CT scan are no‌t available. The duty sur‍geon decides to make a bu‌rr hole. What will be the​ management plan?","raw_text":"A patient with a head injury presents to the casualty with rapidly deteriorating sensorium and progressive dilatation and fixation of the pupil. Neurosurgeon and CT scan are not available. The duty surgeon decides to make a burr hole. What will be the management plan?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Make a hole in the tempora​l region, contralateral t​o the side of pupillary d‌ilatation.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Make a hole in the midline​, if both pupils are equa​l or it is not known whic‌h side dilated first.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"If no localizing sign is f​ound, make a hole in the ​left temporal region.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Refer to higher center if ​both pupils are equal or ​it is not known which sid‌e dilated first","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. If no localizing sign is found, make a hole in the left temporal region.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/24b02915598b436b89cfc10a41101ccbx1280x1895.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. If no localizing sign is found, make a hole in the left temporal region.</strong></p><p>In the given situation, <strong>no localizing sign</strong> is found and it is <strong>not known which side dilated first.</strong> So, the best choice would be to do a <strong>burr</strong> hole in the <strong>dominant hemisphere</strong> (most commonly, the <strong>left temporal region</strong>).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4415","text":"Which of the following is ​true regarding post-traum​atic seizures in patients‌ with head injury?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding post-traumatic seizures in patients with head injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Use of antiepileptics in l​ate post-traumatic seizur​e patients is recommended‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Prolonged seizures cause p​rimary brain injury post ​head trauma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Occurrence of seizures aft​er 2 weeks of head injury​ is called late post-trau‌matic seizures","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Initiation of antiepilepti​cs in early post traumati​c seizure patients reduce‌s the likelihood of devel‌oping post-traumatic epil​epsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Use of antiepileptics in late post-traumatic seizure patients is recommended","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Use of antiepileptics in late post-traumatic seizure patients is recommended</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 175\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The use of <strong>antiepileptics </strong>in<strong> late post-traumatic seizure</strong> (PTS) patients<strong> is recommended. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Post-traumatic seizures</strong> (PTS) are seizures that result from <strong>traumatic brain injury</strong> (TBI). They are classified into <strong>early</strong> and <strong>late</strong>. <strong>Early</strong> PTS occurs <strong>within 1 week</strong> and <strong>late</strong> PTS occurs <strong>after 1 week</strong> of trauma (Option C). </p>\n<p><strong>Early PTS</strong> are <strong>acute symptomatic events</strong>, attributed to a direct reaction to injury and hemorrhage. <strong>Risk factors</strong> for <strong>early PTS</strong> are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Younger age</strong> (especially &lt;5 years)</li>\n<li>Moderate to <strong>severe head injury</strong> (measured by GCS)</li>\n<li><strong>Acute intracerebral hematoma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Subdural / Subarachnoid hemorrhage</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Depressed skull fractures</strong></li>\n<li>Diffuse cerebral edema</li>\n<li>History of alcohol abuse</li>\n<li>Penetrating head injury</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Late PTS </strong>usually occurs due to <strong>underlying permanent</strong> <strong>structural</strong> and <strong>physiologic changes</strong> within the brain. Patients with <strong>late PTS</strong> may develop <strong>post-traumatic epilepsy</strong> (PTE). It refers to <strong>recurrent and unprovoked PTS</strong> that occur at least <strong>1 week after TBI</strong>. <strong>Risk factors </strong>for <strong>PTE</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Early PTS</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Severe head injury</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Older age</strong> (especially &gt;65 years)</li>\n<li>Focal neurological deficit</li>\n<li>Intracranial hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Intracranial infection</li>\n<li><strong>Abnormalities on EEG</strong></li>\n<li>Alcohol-related head injury</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Prolonged seizures</strong> (30 to 60 minutes)<strong> </strong>can cause<strong> secondary brain injury, not primary </strong>(Option B). Hence, it is imperative to control acute seizures as soon as possible.</p>\n<p>The initiation of <strong>antiepileptics</strong> in <strong>early PTS</strong> patients is to <strong>prevent damage</strong> due to seizures themselves. It <strong>does not reduce</strong> the likelihood of developing <strong>PTE </strong>(Option D)<strong>. </strong>For patients who develop <strong>late</strong> <strong>PTS</strong>, because of the <strong>high risk of seizure recurrence</strong> after the <strong>first episode</strong> of late PTS, the use of <strong>antiepileptic medication</strong> is recommended even after a <strong>single late seizure</strong> episode.<strong> </strong>The long-term sequelae of head injury are headache, raised ICP, impaired short-term memory, lack of concentration, emotional disinhibition, and depression.</p>\n<p><strong>Prophylaxis</strong> for <span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">PTS is indicated in patients at risk of <strong>early PTS</strong>, for <strong>7 days </strong>and is stopped without tapering the dose if no seizures have occurred during this duration. Prophylaxis is <strong>not recommended</strong> for <strong>late PTS</strong>. </span></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4416","text":"Which of the following is ​true regarding basilar sk​ull fractures?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding basilar skull fractures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Racoon eyes is one of the ​earliest signs to develop​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Battle sign is associated ​with posterior cranial fo​ssa fractures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CSF leaks should be prompt​ly managed by surgical in​tervention","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Noninvasive positive press​ure ventilation should be​ avoided","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Noninvasive positive pressure ventilation should be avoided","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/99ffd1df518b4d21ad80c91b6c473138x234x173.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5f300e508d8849c2b5890d02deafe4a9x233x189.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/727ea39985c142558dcb1c9127ca084bx1092x1199.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Noninvasive positive pressure ventilation should be avoided</strong></p><p><strong>Noninvasive positive pressure ventilation</strong> should be <strong>avoided</strong> in patients with <strong>basilar skull fractures</strong> due to the risk of <strong>pneumocephalus</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Signs </strong>in basilar skull fractures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hemotympanum:</strong> Pooling of blood behind the tympanic membrane, associated with fracture of the<strong> temporal bone.</strong> It is one of the <strong>earliest signs</strong> of basilar fracture.</li>\n<li><strong>Periorbital ecchymosis (raccoon eyes):</strong> Pooling of blood surrounding the eyes, associated with fractures of the<strong> anterior cranial fossa.</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Retroauricular or mastoid ecchymosis (Battle sign):</strong> Pooled blood behind the ears in the mastoid region is associated with fractures to the<strong> middle cranial fossa</strong>. </li>\n<li><strong>CSF rhinorrhea or otorrhea:</strong> <strong>“Halo” sign, </strong>a double-ring pattern described with CSF-containing bloody fluid from the ear or nose, when dripped onto paper or linen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Battle sign and raccoon eyes </strong>are both typically not present during the initial evaluation and are <strong>delayed by 6 hrs up to 3 days </strong>after head injury.</p>\n<p>CSF leaks may be <strong>delayed</strong> hours to days after the initial trauma. Fluid suspicious for CSF is confirmed by testing for <strong>β2 transferrin</strong>. Most CSF leaks are <strong>conservatively</strong> managed. <strong>Persistent leaks &gt;7 days</strong> have a heightened <strong>risk of meningitis</strong> and are often surgically repaired.</p>\n<p>Nasopharyngeal airways and nasogastric tube insertion are relatively contraindicated in basilar skull fractures due to the risk for <strong>intracranial placement. </strong>NIPPV is avoided due to the risk for pneumocephalus. </p>\n<p>The image below shows the<strong> 'battle' sign.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image below shows<strong> 'racoon' eyes.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image below shows<strong> pneumocephalus.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9622","text":"A man is brought to the ER​ with a depressed skull f​racture and a clean overl‌ying scalp laceration. Th‌ere are no focal neurolog​ical deficits. CT scan re​vealed that the degree of‌ depression was less than‍ the thickness of the adj‌acent skull bone. What is​ the next step in the man‍agement of the patient?","raw_text":"A man is brought to the ER with a depressed skull fracture and a clean overlying scalp laceration. There are no focal neurological deficits. CT scan revealed that the degree of depression was less than the thickness of the adjacent skull bone. What is the next step in the management of the patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Surgically elevate the dep​ressed segment and close ​the laceration with sutur‌es","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Provide prophylactic antib​iotics and discharge the ​patient","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Close the laceration with ​suture","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Apply dressing and dischar​ge the patient","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Close the laceration with suture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Close the laceration with suture</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the next step is to <strong>close the laceration with suture.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>management</strong> of the <strong>depressed skull fractures</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>first step </strong>is to order a <strong>CT scan</strong> to know the degree of depression and to exclude the presence of an <strong>intracranial hematoma</strong> or <strong>contusion</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>depressed fracture is elevated</strong> and managed as an open fracture (surgery) <strong>if:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>degree of depression</strong> is <strong>greater than the thickness</strong> of the adjacent skull</li>\n<li>The wound is are <strong>open</strong> and grossly <strong>contaminated</strong></li>\n<li>There are focal neurological deficits</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Less severe depressed fractures</strong> are managed by the <strong>closure of the overlying scalp laceration</strong>, if present.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9761","text":"Identify the type of herni​ation labelled as ‘1’ in ​the given image.","raw_text":"Identify the type of herniation labelled as ‘1’ in the given image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Midline shif​t herniation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Central h​erniation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subfalcine​ herniation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Uncal he​rniation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Subfalcine herniation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c1fd8e031f674b8aadc2656685b47ccf.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7df31151647f4021b4f308953c89cdc3x218x209.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Subfalcine herniation</strong></p><p>The type herniation labelled as ‘1’ in the given image shows herniation of the cingulate gyrus under the falx cerebri, which is known as <strong>subfalcine herniation.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Image</strong>: Sites of brain herniation</p>\n<p></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Subfalcine herniation:</strong> herniation of the cingulate gyrus under the falx cerebri</li>\n<li>Herniation with midline shift</li>\n<li><strong>Uncal herniation</strong> (uncus is part of temporal lobe)-can compress the ipsilateral third nerve</li>\n<li><strong>Central herniation</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tonsillar herniation</strong> (part of the cerebellum)</li>\n</ol>\n<table style=\"width: 460px; height: 497px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\"><strong>Site of herniation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\"><strong>Structures involved</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\"><strong>Presenting symptoms</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\"><strong>Subfalcine</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">\n<p>Cingulate gyrus</p>\n<p>Pericallosal arteries</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\">Leg weakness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\"><strong>Uncal (lateral)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">\n<p>Oculomotor nerve</p>\n<p>Cerebral peduncle</p>\n<p>Posterior cerebral artery</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\">\n<p>Ptosis</p>\n<p><strong>Ipsilateral mydriasis</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Contralateral hemiparesis</strong></p>\n<p>Decreased consciousness</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\"><strong>Tectal (posterior)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Superior colliculi</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\">\n<p><strong>Bilateral ptosis</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Upward gaze paralysis</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\"><strong>Axial (central)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">\n<p>Perforating branches from basilar artery</p>\n<p>Midbrain, pons, medulla</p>\n<p>Reticular formation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\">\n<p>Decreased consciousness</p>\n<p>Impaired eye movement</p>\n<p>Cushing’s triad (hypertension, bradycardia, respiratory irregularity/apnea)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87px;\"><strong>Tonsillar</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 143px;\">Medulla</td>\n<td style=\"width: 210px;\">Cushing’s triad</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2572","text":"A 25-year-old football pla​yer had a fall followed b​y one episode of vomiting‌, a day before his final ‌match. When brought to th​e casualty, he was awake ​and conscious but could n‌ot recall events after th‍e fall. Which of the foll‌owing is the most appropr​iate next step in the man‍agement of this patient?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old football player had a fall followed by one episode of vomiting, a day before his final match. When brought to the casualty, he was awake and conscious but could not recall events after the fall. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obtain NCC​T of Brain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Discharge home and advice ​adequate rest before play​ing the final match","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Observe for 6 hours in the​ ED and refrain from play​ing for one week","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Admit to intensive care fo​r intracranial pressure m​onitoring","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Observe for 6 hours in the ED and refrain from playing for one week","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Observe for 6 hours in the ED and refrain from playing for one week</strong></p><p>The given history of <strong>fall</strong> and<strong> anterograde amnesia</strong> with <strong>no focal</strong> neurologic <strong>deficits</strong> points towards the diagnosis of <strong>concussion</strong>. <strong>Observation</strong> followed by a <strong>gradual return to play</strong> is the current recommendation for a<strong> concussion.</strong> </p>\n<p>It is defined as an alteration of consciousness as a result of closed head injury. It is the most common type of <strong>traumatic brain injury</strong>, which can result from vehicle accidents, falls, sports injuries, etc. </p>\n<p>Clinical features of concussion include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterograde amnesia</strong>/post-traumatic amnesia - a <strong>hallmark</strong> of a concussion. In this type of amnesia, the events following the injury cannot be recalled.</li>\n<li>Confusion</li>\n<li>Headache</li>\n<li>Dizziness</li>\n<li>Nausea</li>\n<li>Vomiting</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Observation</strong> in the emergency department is essential following the clinical exam to look for alterations in mental status or focal neurological signs. The presence of either of which may signal more serious underlying pathology (e.g., intracranial bleeding).</p>\n<p>Following discharge, the patients are advised to <strong>not return</strong> to <strong>contact sports</strong> e.g., football, boxing, etc. which carries the risk of further injury, for at least a week. Then, there should be a <strong>gradual, stepwise increase</strong> in <strong>physical activity.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: There are <strong>no focal neurological deficits</strong> to warrant an NCCT of the brain.</p>\n<p>Option B: Immediate discharge and return to intense physical activity following mild TBI are inappropriate. Because those who are symptomatic after a head injury, are vulnerable to a repeat impact. Due to disturbed cerebral autoregulation, even a minor second injury may trigger a form of malignant cerebral edema refractory to treatment.</p>\n<p>Option D: Admission to the intensive care unit for intracranial pressure monitoring would be appropriate in patients with severe TBI, for which elevated intracranial pressure is a serious concern due to brain edema and/or bleeding. <strong>Severe TBI is characterized by a GCS ≤ 8</strong>. Here the patient has a GCS of &gt;14.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"1-g_p8VV","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"45_heart","title":"45.Heart","num_questions":33,"total_marks":132,"duration":43,"time_per_question":78.18,"questions":[{"id":"MB3324","text":"Which of the following rep‌resents the surface marki‌ng of the aortic valve?","raw_text":"Which of the following represents the surface marking of the aortic valve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sternal end of left third ‌costal cartilage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sternal end of the right t‌hird costal cartilage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Beside the sternum in the ‌left third intercostal sp‌ace","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Beside the sternum in righ‌t third intercostal space‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Beside the sternum in the left third intercostal space","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/15adb23ae12f4ee5a92fb638fc518294x1279x1715.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/29177f86b03649999258a588ddf811b5x1279x1715.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Beside the sternum in the left third intercostal space</strong></p><p>The surface marking of aortic valve is beside the sternum in the left third intercostal space.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3325","text":"What is the surface markin‌g of the mitral valve?","raw_text":"What is the surface marking of the mitral valve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Behind the sternum end of ‌the left 4th costal carti‌lage","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Behind the sternal end of ‌the right 4th costal cart‌ilage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left 4th intercostal space‌ in midclavicular line","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left 3rd intercostal space‌ in midclavicular line","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Behind the sternum end of the left 4th costal cartilage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Behind the sternum end of the left 4th costal cartilage</strong></p><p>The orifice of the <strong>mitral valve</strong> is level with the left half of the sternum <strong>opposite the left 4<sup>th</sup> costal cartilage</strong> and is represented by a line approximately 3 cm long and descending to the right.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3330","text":"A patient developed hoarse‌ness due to left recurren‌t laryngeal nerve weaknes‌s. TTE revealed compressi‌on of the nerve by the en‌larged base of the heart.‍ Which of the following c​hambers is likely to be d​ilated?","raw_text":"A patient developed hoarseness due to left recurrent laryngeal nerve weakness. TTE revealed compression of the nerve by the enlarged base of the heart. Which of the following chambers is likely to be dilated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right ‌atrium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left ‌atrium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Right v‌entricle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left ve‌ntricle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Left atrium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/85afeb32a82640dd9c8597ce8b161ed6x1280x1730.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Left atrium</strong></p><p>The <strong>base of the heart</strong> is formed mainly by the <strong>left atrium. </strong>Enlargement of the left atrium can cause hoarseness due to compression of the left recurrent laryngeal nerve.</p>\n<p>The<strong> base of the heart</strong> is a quadrilateral directed posteriorly. It is made of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The<strong> left atrium</strong> mainly</li>\n<li>Small part of the <strong>right atrium</strong></li>\n<li>Proximal parts of superior vena cava, inferior vena cava, and pulmonary veins</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>base</strong> of the heart lies at the level of <strong>T5-T8 vertebrae</strong> in the supine position.</p>\n<p>The <strong>apex</strong> of the heart is formed by the <strong>left ventricle </strong>in the 5<sup>th</sup> intercostal space.</p>\n<p>Surfaces of the heart:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sternocostal</strong> surface - Formed by the <strong>right atrium, right and left ventricles</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Diaphragmatic</strong> surface - Formed mainly by the<strong> left ventricle</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Left surface</strong> - Formed mainly of the <strong>left ventricle</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Right surface</strong> - Formed mainly of the <strong>right atrium</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7650","text":"The region marked correspo‌nds to the surface markin‌g for which of the follow‌ing structures:","raw_text":"The region marked corresponds to the surface marking for which of the following structures:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thorac‌ic duct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right ‌atrium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inferomedial bronchopulmon‌ary segments","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hi‌lum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Right atrium","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0a25b4e922df422e89b951bec83a2f7d.GIF"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/24f47e3bbcef4939aa05bcd7798596eex1280x1715.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/edb8bdd74e644d878eb22b4d331a9a8bx1280x1715.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Right atrium</strong></p><p>The region marked corresponds to the surface marking for the <strong>right atrium.</strong></p>\n<p>The costal margin, xiphoid process, and the mid-clavicular line are seen in this image. The examiner draws a laterally convex line that extends from a level below the 2nd costal cartilage (as clued in by the sternal angle) i.e., 3rd costal cartilage to about 3 spaces below i.e., the 6th costal cartilage. This line is about 1.2 cms lateral to the sternal edge. These suggest that the region marked corresponds to the surface marking for the right atrium.</p>\n<p>The projection of the cardiac borders, of an average adult, onto the anterior thoracic wall forms a trapezoid.  This can be mapped out using the percussion method by looking for cardiac dullness. </p>\n<p>The varying heart borders and the corresponding surface markings are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Upper border:</strong> Slopes gently from the second left costal cartilage to the third right costal cartilage. It corresponds to the <strong>left atrium</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Right border:</strong> Convex to the right and runs from the third to the sixth right costal cartilages, 1–2 cm lateral to the sternal edge. It corresponds to the <strong>right atrium</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Inferior border or</strong> <strong>acute</strong> <strong>border:</strong> Runs leftwards from the 6th right costal cartilage to the cardiac apex which is located approximately 9 cm lateral to the midline, often in the left 5th intercostal space or level with the 5th or 6th rib. It corresponds to the <strong>right ventricle</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Left border or</strong> <strong>obtuse border:</strong> Convex laterally on the left. It extends superomedially from the cardiac apex to meet the second left costal cartilage about 1 cm from the left sternal edge. It corresponds to the <strong>left ventricle</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0813","text":"A 30-year-old man was brou‌ght to the emergency depa‌rtment after a car accide‌nt. He sustained a severe‌ injury to the anterior c‌hest wall from the impact‍ caused by the steering w​heel. X-ray revealed frac​ture of the body of the s​ternum. Which of the foll‌owing chambers of the hea‌rt is most likely to be i‍njured?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man was brought to the emergency department after a car accident. He sustained a severe injury to the anterior chest wall from the impact caused by the steering wheel. X-ray revealed fracture of the body of the sternum. Which of the following chambers of the heart is most likely to be injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left ‌atrium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left ve‌ntricle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right v‌entricle","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Right ‌atrium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Right ventricle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4418217b54944c559940d3107f26100dx1200x1316.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Right ventricle</strong></p><p>The area posterior to the sternum is occupied by the <strong>right</strong> <strong>ventricle</strong> and hence is most likely to be injured in this case.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>The convex anterosuperior surface of the <strong>right ventricle</strong> makes up a large part of the <strong>sternocostal aspect</strong> of the heart, and is separated from the thoracic wall only by the pericardium.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9616","text":"During the post-mortem hea‌rt examination of a patie‌nt, the resident palpates‌ a muscular ridge, which ‌is the site of origin of ‌pectinate muscles. In whi‍ch of the following chamb​ers is the resident palpa​ting this structure?","raw_text":"During the post-mortem heart examination of a patient, the resident palpates a muscular ridge, which is the site of origin of pectinate muscles. In which of the following chambers is the resident palpating this structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right ‌atrium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Left ‌atrium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right v‌entricle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left ve‌ntricle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Right atrium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7a0dac33ed0a46f884594b8b685c479bx600x734.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Right atrium</strong></p><p>The structure being palpated by the resident is <strong>crista terminalis.</strong> It is present in the <strong>right atrium.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a <strong>muscular ridge</strong> that runs anteriorly along the atrial wall from the opening of the superior vena cava to the opening of the inferior vena cava. It corresponds externally to <strong>sulcus terminalis.</strong> It is the site of <strong>origin of pectinate muscles</strong>. </p>\n<p>It divides the right atrium into a smooth posterior part and a rough anterior part. Both the vena cavae empty into the posterior smooth part.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9466","text":"The right border of the he‌art is formed by which of‌ the following structures‌?","raw_text":"The right border of the heart is formed by which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right ‌atrium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Right v‌entricle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior ‌vena cava","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior ‌vena cava","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Right atrium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a43dee0a2c3e4527aee137611b6d4f25x1280x1729.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Right atrium</strong></p><p>The<strong> right border</strong> of the heart is formed of the <strong>right atrium.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Borders </strong>of the<strong> heart:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Right </strong>border:<strong> Right atrium</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Left </strong>border: <strong>Left ventricle</strong> mainly and to a slight extent by left atrial appendage</li>\n<li><strong>Inferior </strong>border: <strong>Right ventricle</strong> mainly and a small contribution by the left ventricle</li>\n<li><strong>Upper </strong>border: <strong>Left atrium</strong>, <strong>ascending aorta,</strong> and the <strong>pulmonary trunk. </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9617","text":"While doing the doppler US‌G of a fetal heart, you n‌ote blood flow from the r‌ight atrium to the left a‌trium. After the delivery‌, the connection eventual‍ly closes. What would for​m the floor of this struc​ture after it closes?","raw_text":"While doing the doppler USG of a fetal heart, you note blood flow from the right atrium to the left atrium. After the delivery, the connection eventually closes. What would form the floor of this structure after it closes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Septum‌ primum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Septum ‌secundum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endocardia‌l cushions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tricuspid v‌alve orifice","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Septum primum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/da93804ee39b4a70ad1be9689a6a5d60x600x734.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Septum primum</strong></p><p>The structure mentioned here is the <strong>foramen ovale</strong> through which blood passes from the right atrium to the left atrium as part of fetal circulation. It <strong>closes at delivery</strong> and later forms <strong>fossa ovalis.</strong> The <strong>floor</strong> of the fossa ovalis is formed by the <strong>septum primum.</strong></p>\n<p>Fossa ovalis is an oval-shaped depression in the<strong> septal wall of the right atrium</strong> just above the opening of inferior vena cava. Its <strong>floor</strong> is formed by the remnant of <strong>septum primum</strong>. The <strong>limbus of fossa ovalis</strong> is the prominent rim of the depression which represents the remnant of <strong>septum secundum.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3364","text":"What is the location of th‌e sinoatrial node? At the‌ junction of superior ven‌a cava opening into the r‌ight atrium In the center‌ of Koch's triangle At th‍e superior end of junctio​n between parts of right ​atrium derived from sinus​ venosus and primitive at‌ria At the upper end of c‌rista terminalis At the l‍ower back section of the ‍interatrial septum near t​he opening of the coronar‍y sinus","raw_text":"<p>What is the location of the sinoatrial node?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>At the junction of superior vena cava opening into the right atrium</li>\n<li>In the center of Koch's triangle</li>\n<li>At the superior end of junction between parts of right atrium derived from sinus venosus and primitive atria</li>\n<li>At the upper end of crista terminalis</li>\n<li>At the lower back section of the interatrial septum near the opening of the coronary sinus</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2,3, ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,2,3‌ and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,3, ‌and 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1,3, and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1,3, and 4</strong></p><p>The <strong>sinoatrial (SA) node </strong>is located at the<strong> upper part of the crista terminalis, </strong>at the junction of the superior vena cava opening to the right atrium. This is also the junction between the parts of the right atrium derived from sinus venosus and primitive atria. </p>\n<p>The <strong>Atrioventricular (AV) node</strong> lies at the lower back section of the interatrial septum near the opening of the coronary sinus. It is located in the center of<strong> </strong>the <strong>Koch triangle.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6849","text":"A cardiologist is doing th‌e AV node ablation proced‌ure for AVNRT. While loca‌ting the AV node, he woul‌d look at all of the foll‌owing landmarks forming i‍ts boundary except:","raw_text":"A cardiologist is doing the AV node ablation procedure for AVNRT. While locating the AV node, he would look at all of the following landmarks forming its boundary except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tendon o‌f Todaro","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Septal leaflet of tricuspi‌d valve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior leaflet of tricu‌spid valve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Coronar‌y sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Posterior leaflet of tricuspid valve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0bc7549c15ed413488d5f66ae4a4ff37x1280x2012.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Posterior leaflet of tricuspid valve</strong></p><p>The <strong>AV node</strong> is present in the center of the <strong>triangle of Koch. </strong>The posterior leaflet of the tricuspid valve does not form its boundary.</p>\n<p>The triangle of Koch is found on the <strong>smooth posterior wall</strong> of the <strong>right atrium.</strong> It is bound by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The coronary sinus</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tendon of Todaro</strong></li>\n<li><strong>The septal cusp of the tricuspid valve</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The triangle of Koch can be seen in the image below bound by the dashed blue lines:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3365","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin‌g the SA node?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the SA node?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is located at the right‌ border of ascending aort‌a","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It contains specialized no‌dal cardiac muscle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is supplied by the atri‌al branches of the right ‌coronary artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Action potential generated‌ here does not have phase‌ 1 or 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is located at the right border of ascending aorta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/72463b08cb6a44848f032162d449409bx800x600.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is located at the right border of ascending aorta</strong></p><p><strong>Sino-atrial (SA) node </strong>is not located at the right border of the ascending aorta.</p>\n<p>It is located at the opening of the superior vena cava into the right atrium. It is called the <strong>'pacemaker'</strong> of the heart. It is supplied by the atrial branches of the right coronary artery. It contains specialized nodal cardiac muscle which spontaneously produces an electrical impulse. The action potential of these cells does not have phase 1 or 2.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9674","text":"A 40-year-old man underwen‌t coronary artery bypass ‌grafting. During the proc‌edure, the surgeon applie‌d a clamp to the vessel l‌ying anterior to the tran‍sverse pericardial sinus.​ Identify the vessel.","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man underwent coronary artery bypass grafting. During the procedure, the surgeon applied a clamp to the vessel lying anterior to the transverse pericardial sinus. Identify the vessel.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ao‌rta","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonar‌y artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior ‌vena cava","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superior ‌vena cava","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aorta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d63a6d649ca54933b774d66c22e8f8a5x600x767.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a09bdc3afc4f48f193770322d0b05de9x1280x1117.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aorta</strong></p><p>The <strong>aorta</strong> lies<strong> anterior </strong>to the <strong>transverse pericardial sinus.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>reflections of the serous pericardium</strong> are arranged as <strong>two tubes</strong> – one enclosing the aorta and the pulmonary trunk and the other enclosing the vena cavae and the four pulmonary veins. </p>\n<p><strong>Oblique pericardial sinus: </strong>The zone of reflection surrounding the veins is J-shaped and the cul-de-sac formed within the J is the oblique pericardial sinus. This lies posterior to the left atrium.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the posterior view of the heart demonstrating the oblique and the transverse sinus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Transverse pericardial sinus:</strong> It is a passage between the two 'tubes'. The <strong>aorta</strong> and the <strong>pulmonary trunk</strong> lie<strong> anterior</strong> and the <strong>superior vena cava </strong>and the <strong>atria</strong> lie<strong> posterior</strong> to it.</p>\n<p>The pulmonary trunk divides into the right and left pulmonary arteries above the transverse sinus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<h4>Clinical significance:</h4>\n<p>During cardiac surgery, the transverse pericardial sinus allows a surgeon to isolate the pulmonary trunk and ascending aorta and apply a temporary ligature or clamp.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3331","text":"How many papillary muscles‌ are there in the left ve‌ntricle?","raw_text":"How many papillary muscles are there in the left ventricle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌8","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2</strong></p><p>The left ventricle has 2 papillary muscles.</p>\n<p>There are <strong>3 papillary muscles </strong>in the <strong>right ventricle</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior,</li>\n<li>Posterior</li>\n<li>Septal muscles</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There are <strong>2 papillary muscles</strong> in the <strong>left ventricle</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior</li>\n<li>Posterior</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3366","text":"What are Pur‌kinje fibers?","raw_text":"What are Purkinje fibers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Modified n‌erve fibers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Modified sm‌ooth muscle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Modified ca‌rdiac muscle","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fibrous‌ tissue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Modified cardiac muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Modified cardiac muscle</strong></p><p><strong>Purkinje fibers</strong> are <strong>modified cardiac muscles.</strong></p>\n<p>The Purkinje network is subendocardial and the <strong>muscular excitation</strong> proceeds from<strong> endocardium to epicardium</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3361","text":"A surgeon opens the perica‌rdial sac anteriorly duri‌ng surgery. He passes a s‌urgical clamp through the‌ transverse sinus. The ci‌rculation of blood in whi‍ch of the following struc​tures is stopped/diverted​ by his action?","raw_text":"A surgeon opens the pericardial sac anteriorly during surgery. He passes a surgical clamp through the transverse sinus. The circulation of blood in which of the following structures is stopped/diverted by his action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior and inferior vena‌ cava","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aorta and pu‌lmonary veins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aorta and pu‌lmonary trunk","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"SVC and pul‌monary veins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aorta and pulmonary trunk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d645caaa3400442da5928013f071bf77x1280x1117.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aorta and pulmonary trunk</strong></p><p>During surgery, a <strong>clamp</strong> is passed through the <strong>transverse sinus.</strong> This <strong>stops/diverts</strong> the <strong>blood</strong> flow from the <strong>aorta</strong> and <strong>pulmonary trunk.</strong> </p>\n<p>The transverse pericardial sinus separates the arterial end of the heart tube from the venous end. <strong>Aorta</strong> and <strong>pulmonary trunk </strong>lie <strong>anterior</strong> to the sinus and the <strong>superior vena cava</strong> lies <strong>posterior</strong> to it. Therefore, during surgery, a clamp passed through the transverse sinus stops or diverts the blood flow from the aorta and pulmonary trunk.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3335","text":"A cardiologist while revie‌wing a case of MI, notes ‌that the patient has righ‌t dominant circulation. W‌hich of the following det‌ermines coronary dominanc‍e?","raw_text":"A cardiologist while reviewing a case of MI, notes that the patient has right dominant circulation. Which of the following determines coronary dominance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior interventricular‌ artery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior interventricular ‌artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Circumfl‌ex artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right coro‌nary artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Posterior interventricular artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Posterior interventricular artery</strong></p><p><strong>Coronary dominance </strong>is determined by the<strong> posterior interventricular artery (PIVA)</strong>.</p>\n<p>In about<strong> 65% of the population</strong>, the PIVA is given by the right coronary artery and this is called the <strong>right cardiac dominance</strong>.</p>\n<p>In <strong>10% of the population</strong>, it is a branch of the left coronary artery alone, called the<strong> left cardiac dominance</strong>.</p>\n<p>In the <strong>remaining 25% </strong>population, it is given by<strong> both (balanced dominance). </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3336","text":"Coronary angiography of a ‌patient revealed posterio‌r interventricular artery‌ branching from the circu‌mflex artery. What would ‌the arterial circulation ‍in this patient be called​?","raw_text":"Coronary angiography of a patient revealed posterior interventricular artery branching from the circumflex artery. What would the arterial circulation in this patient be called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right d‌ominant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left d‌ominant","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Balanced‌ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Codom‌inant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Left dominant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/08ff9102fdf84958ba8514969e99013ex600x508.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Left dominant</strong></p><p>If the circumflex artery gives off the posterior interventricular artery, then the arterial supply is called left dominant.</p>\n<p>In 65% of the population,  the <strong>posterior interventricular artery (PIVA)</strong> is a branch of the <strong>right coronary artery</strong> and this is called the <strong>right dominance.</strong></p>\n<p>In 10% of the population, <strong>PIVA</strong> is given by the <strong>circumflex artery</strong> (a branch of the left coronary artery) and this is called the <strong>left dominance</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7400","text":"All of the following are t‌rue about the right coron‌ary artery except _____","raw_text":"All of the following are true about the right coronary artery except _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It's diameter is less than‌ left coronary artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It arises from the anterio‌r aortic sinus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It gives rise to circumfle‌x coronary branch","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Right conal artery is it's‌ first branch","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It gives rise to circumflex coronary branch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ebc4d663af304e4f89302b50e0f37e56x600x508.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It gives rise to circumflex coronary branch</strong></p><p>The right coronary artery does not give rise to the circumflex coronary artery.</p>\n<p>The right coronary artery <strong>arises from</strong> <strong>the anterior aortic sinus (right coronary sinus)</strong> of the ascending aorta. It passes anteriorly and descends vertically in the coronary sulcus between the right atrium and the right ventricle. On reaching the inferior margin, it turns posteriorly and continues in the sulcus onto the diaphragmatic surface and the base of the heart.</p>\n<p>The diameter of the right coronary artery is less than that of the left coronary artery.</p>\n<p>The <strong>right conal artery</strong> is, usually the <strong>first branch </strong>of the right coronary artery.</p>\n<p>The<strong> circumflex coronary artery </strong>is a <strong>branch of</strong> the <strong>left coronary artery </strong>and is the <strong>exclusive supplier</strong> to the <strong>right bundle branch. </strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9646","text":"A 55-year-old man presente‌d with angina. Coronary a‌ngiography revealed a blo‌ck in the right coronary ‌artery. Blood supply thro‌ugh which of the followin‍g arteries would not be a​ffected?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man presented with angina. Coronary angiography revealed a block in the right coronary artery. Blood supply through which of the following arteries would not be affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute m‌arginal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior interventricular‌ artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Artery t‌o SA node","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Circumfl‌ex artery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Circumflex artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0157f821b2d9443c80d33f5f89a709bfx600x508.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Circumflex artery</strong></p><p>The <strong>circumflex artery </strong>is a <strong>branch</strong> of the <strong>left coronary</strong> <strong>artery</strong>, not the right coronary artery.<strong> </strong>Hence, blood supply through the circumflex artery won't be affected.</p>\n<p>The branches of the right coronary artery are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Right conal artery -</strong> first branch of the right coronary artery</li>\n<li><strong>Artery of the SA node </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Acute right marginal artery </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Posterior interventricular artery</strong> (in 65% population)</li>\n<li>Atrial and anterior and posterior ventricular branches</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0113","text":"All of the following suppl‌y the marked structure ex‌cept :","raw_text":"All of the following supply the marked structure except :","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right coro‌nary artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior interventricular ‌artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Margina‌l artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diagona‌l artery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Diagonal artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a8639e1f60ae42babde630f979b03b7c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4c3ecbe52065497582c813fa67912776x600x508.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Diagonal artery</strong></p><p>The structure marked is the <strong>right ventricle.</strong> It is<strong> not supplied</strong> by the <strong>diagonal artery.</strong></p>\n<p>The diagonal artery is a <strong>branch of </strong>the <strong>anterior interventricular artery </strong>supplying the <strong>left ventricle. </strong>This is present in about 30-50% of the population.</p>\n<p>The right ventricle is mostly supplied by the right coronary artery and its branches such as the marginal artery.</p>\n<p>The anterior interventricular artery gives off 1-2 anterior right ventricular branches.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9647","text":"A patient underwent angiop‌lasty with a Sirolimus-el‌uting stent in the anteri‌or interventricular arter‌y. This artery is a branc‌h of:","raw_text":"A patient underwent angioplasty with a Sirolimus-eluting stent in the anterior interventricular artery. This artery is a branch of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right coro‌nary artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left coron‌ary artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Circumfl‌ex artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left anterior descending a‌rtery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Left coronary artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d3f53f322b3a4317a3f2ec04cc55281ax600x508.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Left coronary artery</strong></p><p>The anterior interventricular artery is a branch of the <strong>left coronary artery.</strong></p>\n<p>The left coronary artery <strong>arises from the left aortic sinus</strong> (left posterior sinus) of the ascending aorta. It passes between the pulmonary trunk and the left auricle and then runs in the coronary sulcus. It divides into its <strong>two terminal branches</strong> in the coronary sulcus. They are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior interventricular branch</strong> - also called the <strong>left anterior descending artery (LAD).</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Circumflex branch</strong> - It gives a <strong>left marginal artery</strong> also called the obtuse marginal artery supplying the left ventricle.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3337","text":"In a patient with STEMI, n‌uclear stress test reveal‌ed poor blood supply to t‌he anterior two-thirds of‌ the interventricular sep‌tum. Which of the followi‍ng blood vessels is most ​likely to be blocked?","raw_text":"In a patient with STEMI, nuclear stress test revealed poor blood supply to the anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum. Which of the following blood vessels is most likely to be blocked?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right coro‌nary artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left coron‌ary artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior interventricular‌ artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Circumfl‌ex artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Left coronary artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bf46e586a684439c806fd631da55dc72x600x508.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Left coronary artery</strong></p><p>The <strong>anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum </strong>is supplied by the<strong> left coronary artery</strong>. Hence, this artery is most likely blocked.</p>\n<p>The <strong>right coronary artery</strong> distribution supplies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Right atrium</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Right ventricle</strong> (except a small region to the right of the anterior interventricular groove)</li>\n<li>A variable part of the diaphragmatic aspect of the left ventricle</li>\n<li><strong>Posteroinferior 1/3<sup>rd</sup> of the interventricular septum</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Part of the left atrium</strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>conduction system,</strong> as far as the proximal parts of the right and left crura</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>left coronary artery</strong> distribution supplies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Left atrium</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Left ventricle</strong></li>\n<li>A narrow strip of the right ventricle</li>\n<li>The <strong>anterior 2/3<sup>rd</sup> of the interventricular septum</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3353","text":"What is the arterial suppl‌y of the structure labell‌ed in the picture with th‌e blue dotted line?","raw_text":"What is the arterial supply of the structure labelled in the picture with the blue dotted line?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right coro‌nary artery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Left coron‌ary artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left anterior descending a‌rtery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Artery from the left poste‌rior aortic sinus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Right coronary artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/090394c009244de1a16693413ddf74bd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Right coronary artery</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>triangle of Koch</strong> marked with blue dotted lines. The <strong>arterial supply</strong> to the triangle of Koch is from the <strong>right coronary artery</strong> in the majority of the population.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3354","text":"A patient with a recent MI‌ underwent coronary angio‌graphy, which revealed a ‌block in the anterior int‌erventricular artery. The‌ infarction is likely to ‍be present in which of th​e following regions of th​e heart?","raw_text":"A patient with a recent MI underwent coronary angiography, which revealed a block in the anterior interventricular artery. The infarction is likely to be present in which of the following regions of the heart?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior part of interven‌tricular septum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior wall of the left ‌ventricle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral part‌ of the heart","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior surface of the ri‌ght ventricle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anterior wall of the left ventricle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7216c43973b04aeda62d73c480be480cx600x508.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anterior wall of the left ventricle</strong></p><p>The occlusion of the left anterior descending artery (LAD) will lead to the infarction of the anterior wall of the left ventricle.</p>\n<p>The <strong>left anterior descending artery</strong> also called the anterior interventricular artery supplies the <strong>anterior 2/3rd of the interventricular septum </strong>and the adjacent <strong>anterior wall of the left ventricle.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Options A and D: The <strong>posterior interventricular artery</strong> supplies the <strong>posterior part of the interventricular septum </strong>and the <strong>inferior part of the right ventricle.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: The left <strong>lateral surface of the heart</strong> is supplied by the <strong>circumflex artery</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3355","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a tributary of the co‌ronary sinus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a tributary of the coronary sinus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior c‌ardiac vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Small car‌diac vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior c‌ardiac vein","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Great car‌diac vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anterior cardiac vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1e5bddf2c623467ca03d234a2b10b600x600x824.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7e3c37e4da82474d9ef8120fe15b27dcx600x820.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anterior cardiac vein</strong></p><p><strong>Anterior cardiac vein</strong> does not open into the coronary sinus. It drains <strong>directly</strong> into the <strong>right atrium.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Tributaries of coronary sinus</strong> include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Great cardiac vein</li>\n<li>Middle cardiac vein</li>\n<li>Small cardiac vein</li>\n<li>Posterior cardiac vein</li>\n<li>Oblique vein of the left atrium </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Thebsian vein/venae cordis minimae, anterior cardiac veins,</strong> and the <strong>right marginal vein</strong> drain directly into the<strong> right atrium</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3357","text":"Where does the great cardi‌ac vein lie?","raw_text":"Where does the great cardiac vein lie?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior part of the coron‌ary sulcus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior part of the righ‌t coronary sulcus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior interventricular ‌groove","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior interventricular‌ groove","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anterior interventricular groove","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d29195cb2afc40e69801b0a3763405a0x600x824.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a3bd0bfa10754542b265785398a23d04x600x820.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anterior interventricular groove</strong></p><p>The great cardiac vein lies in the anterior interventricular sulcus.</p>\n<p>The <strong>great cardiac vein</strong> begins at the cardiac apex, and <strong>ascends in</strong> the <strong>anterior interventricular groove to</strong> the <strong>atrioventricular groove</strong>, which it follows, passing to the left and posteriorly to <strong>enter the coronary sinus</strong> at its origin.</p>\n<p>The great cardiac vein <strong>accompanies</strong> the anterior interventricular artery (<strong>left anterior descending artery</strong>).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3356","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‌ the coronary sinus?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the coronary sinus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It lies in the anterior pa‌rt of coronary sulcus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It ends in the right atriu‌m","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It has venae cordis minima‌e as its tributaries","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It develops from the right‌ anterior cardinal vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It ends in the right atrium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b9c435c8f4ae443aa327cff8486d45b8x600x824.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/69c370dbcaeb458e9f189fc0b398421cx600x820.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It ends in the right atrium</strong></p><p>Coronary sinus ends in the right atrium. </p>\n<p><strong>Coronary sinus</strong> develops from the <strong>left horn of the sinus venosus</strong> and lies in the <strong>posterior part of the atrioventricular groove. </strong>The sinus <strong>opens into the right atrium</strong> between the opening of the inferior vena cava and the right atrioventricular orifice.</p>\n<p>Venae cordis minimae drains directly into the nearest heart chamber (drains into all 4 chambers).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3358","text":"A 58-year-old man is under‌going coronary artery byp‌ass grafting. The coronar‌y angiography revealed to‌tal blockage of the poste‌rior descending artery. W‍hile exposing this artery​ during the procedure, wh​ich accompanying vessel i​s most likely to be injur‌ed?","raw_text":"A 58-year-old man is undergoing coronary artery bypass grafting. The coronary angiography revealed total blockage of the posterior descending artery. While exposing this artery during the procedure, which accompanying vessel is most likely to be injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Great car‌diac vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior vein of left ven‌tricle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Small car‌diac vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Middle ca‌rdiac vein","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Middle cardiac vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Middle cardiac vein</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW192943509 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW192943509 BCX4\">The </span></span><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW192943509 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW192943509 BCX4\">middle cardiac vein</span></span></strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW192943509 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW192943509 BCX4\"> is present in t</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW192943509 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW192943509 BCX4\">he </span></span><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW192943509 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW192943509 BCX4\">posterior </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW192943509 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW192943509 BCX4\">interventricular </span></span>groove</strong> accompanying<span class=\"TextRun SCXW192943509 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW192943509 BCX4\"> the <strong>posterior descending artery</strong>, and hence is most likely to be injured in this case.</span></span><span class=\"EOP SCXW192943509 BCX4\" data-ccp-props='{\"201341983\":0,\"335551550\":1,\"335551620\":1,\"335559739\":160,\"335559740\":259}'> </span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3360","text":"Which among the following ‌is wrong about the venous‌ drainage of the heart?","raw_text":"Which among the following is wrong about the venous drainage of the heart?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coronary sinus is guarded ‌by the Thebesian valve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Middle cardiac vein lies i‌n the posterior atriovent‌ricular groove","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Great cardiac vein accompa‌nies left anterior descen‌ding artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Venae cordis minimae open ‌into all 4 chambers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Middle cardiac vein lies in the posterior atrioventricular groove","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Middle cardiac vein lies in the posterior atrioventricular groove</strong></p><p>The <strong>middle cardiac veins lie </strong>in the <strong>posterior interventricular groove</strong> and not the posterior atrioventricular groove.</p>\n<p>Option A: The opening of the coronary sinus may be guarded by a semilunar valve of the coronary sinus, the Thebesian valve.</p>\n<p>Option C: The great cardiac vein accompanies the anterior interventricular artery (left anterior descending artery) in the anterior interventricular groove.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1424\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Option D: The venae cordis minimae / Thebesian veins open into all cardiac chambers.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3359","text":"In a patient with thrombos‌is of the coronary sinus,‌ which of the following c‌ardiac veins are least li‌kely to get dilated?","raw_text":"In a patient with thrombosis of the coronary sinus, which of the following cardiac veins are least likely to get dilated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Middle ca‌rdiac vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Small car‌diac vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oblique ca‌rdiac vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior c‌ardiac vein","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anterior cardiac vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anterior cardiac vein</strong></p><p>The <strong>anterior cardiac vein</strong> drains<strong> directly </strong>into the <strong>right atrium</strong> and not into the coronary sinus, hence, they might remain normal in coronary sinus thrombosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9662","text":"Which of these veins does ‌not open into the sinus v‌enarum?","raw_text":"Which of these veins does not open into the sinus venarum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior ‌vena cava","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coronar‌y sinus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior ‌vena cava","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Small car‌diac vein","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Small cardiac vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Small cardiac vein</strong></p><p><strong>Small cardiac vein</strong> does not open into the sinus venarum. It opens into the <strong>coronary sinus.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Superior vena cava</strong>, <strong>coronary sinus, </strong>and the <strong>inferior vena cava</strong> open into the <strong>sinus venarum</strong> or the <strong>smooth posterior part of the right atrium. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3362","text":"Sympathetic innervation of‌ heart is by ________","raw_text":"Sympathetic innervation of heart is by ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T1‌-T3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T1‌-T5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"T3‌-T7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"L1‌-L5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. T1-T5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. T1-T5</strong></p><p>The sympathetic innervation of the heart is by T1-T5.</p>\n<p>The <strong>preganglionic sympathetic nerves</strong> to the <strong>heart</strong> arise from <strong>T1-T5</strong>. Some of these synapse in the upper thoracic sympathetic ganglia and some synapse in the cervical ganglia. The postganglionic fibres from these ganglia form the sympathetic cardiac nerves and innervate the heart.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3363","text":"Where is cardiac ganglion ‌situated?","raw_text":"Where is cardiac ganglion situated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Below the a‌rch of aorta","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Above the a‌rch of aorta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left side of ligamentum ar‌teriosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior to ligamentum ar‌teriosum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Below the arch of aorta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Below the arch of aorta</strong></p><p>The cardiac ganglion of Wrisberg is present in the superficial cardiac plexus immediately <strong>below and slightly in front of the arch of aorta</strong> to the <strong>right of the ligamentum arteriosum. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"LMCM-Y4W","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"45_biomedical_waste_management","title":"45.Biomedical Waste Management","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA0710","text":"What proportion of healthc‌are waste is hazardous wa​ste?","raw_text":"What proportion of healthcare waste is hazardous waste?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌5%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2‌5%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"8‌5%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 15%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 15%</strong></p><p><strong>15%</strong> of health care waste is <strong>hazardous waste</strong>. It may be infectious, toxic, or radioactive in nature. The remaining 85% is general, non-hazardous waste.</p>\n<p>Bio-medical waste means any waste that is generated during:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diagnosis</strong>, <strong>treatment,</strong> or<strong> immunization</strong> of human beings or animals </li>\n<li><strong>Research activities</strong> pertaining thereto</li>\n<li><strong>Production</strong> or <strong>testing</strong> of biologicals</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Bio-medical waste is handled as per the <strong>Bio-medical Waste Management Rules, 2016</strong>. However, these rules <strong>do not apply</strong> to the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Radioactive wastes</li>\n<li>Hazardous chemicals</li>\n<li>Solid wastes</li>\n<li>Lead-acid batteries</li>\n<li>Hazardous wastes</li>\n<li>e-Waste</li>\n<li>Hazardous microorganisms</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9551","text":"Cytotoxic and expired drug‌s are disposed by:","raw_text":"Cytotoxic and expired drugs are disposed by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dum‌ping","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Auto‌clave","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Incine‌ration","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chemical d‌isinfection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Incineration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Incineration</strong></p><p><strong>Cytotoxic</strong> and <strong>expired drugs</strong> are collected in a <strong>yellow bag</strong> and then disposed of by <strong>incineration.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Expired</strong> <strong>cytotoxic</strong> drugs are to be <strong>returned</strong> to the manufacturer and subjected to incineration, encapsulation, or plasma pyrolysis. Other discarded medicines can be disposed by incineration.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0714","text":"Which of the following cat‌egory of wastes are treat​ed by autoclaving and mic‌rowaving?","raw_text":"Which of the following category of wastes are treated by autoclaving and microwaving?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Placenta, organs, blood ba‌gs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Catheters, urine bags, glo‌ves","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anti neoplastic and expire‌d drugs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Culture plates, slides and‌ tubes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Catheters, urine bags, gloves","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Catheters, urine bags, gloves</strong></p><p>The final treatment of <strong>catheters</strong>, <strong>urine bags</strong>, and <strong>gloves</strong> is done by<strong> autoclaving</strong> and <strong>microwaving</strong>.</p>\n<p>They belong to <strong>recyclable contaminated waste</strong> and are disposed of in a <strong>red</strong> bag.</p>\n<p>Option A: Human anatomical waste is disposed of by incineration.</p>\n<p>Option C: Cytotoxic and expired/discarded medicine is disposed of by incineration/plasma pyrolysis/disposal in a secured landfill.</p>\n<p>Option D: Microbiological waste such as culture plates and slides are pre-treated with nonchlorinated chemicals and later disposed of by incineration.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5729","text":"While working as a 1st-yea‌r resident in the labor w​ard, where would you disp‌ose of the placenta follo‌wing a delivery?","raw_text":"While working as a 1st-year resident in the labor ward, where would you dispose of the placenta following a delivery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Red‌ bag","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blue‌ bag","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Yello‌w bag","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Blac‌k bag","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Yellow bag","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Yellow bag</strong></p><p>Human anatomical wastes, like the <strong>placenta</strong>, are discarded in a <strong>yellow bag</strong> and disposed of by incineration/ plasma pyrolysis/ deep burial.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5728","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements are true about ye​llow waste disposal bags?‌ They are made from non-c‌hlorinated plastic materi​al Chemical liquid wastes‍ are collected in it Meta‌llic body implants are di​scarded in it Polyvinyl c‌hloride gloves are discar​ded in it and sent for in​cineration Animal anatomi‍cal waste is collected in‌ it and sent for chemical​ treatment","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements are true about yellow waste disposal bags?</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">They are made from non-chlorinated plastic material</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Chemical liquid wastes are collected in it</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Metallic body implants are discarded in it</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Polyvinyl chloride gloves are discarded in it and sent for incineration</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Animal anatomical waste is collected in it and sent for chemical treatment</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 &amp;a‌mp; 2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2 &amp;a‌mp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3, 4 &amp;‌amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 &amp;‌amp; 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1 & 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1 &amp; 2</strong></p><p>Yellow waste disposal bags are made from<strong> non-chlorinated</strong> <strong>plastic</strong> material and <strong>chemical liquid wastes</strong> are collected in them.</p>\n<p><strong>Chlorinated items</strong>, glass, metal, and rubber are not sent for incineration as<strong> incinerating</strong> them would produce <strong>carcinogenic</strong> gases.</p>\n<p>The right disposal methods of the other options are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Animal <strong>anatomical waste</strong> - Collected in <strong>yellow</strong> bags and later sent for <strong>incineration</strong>. </li>\n<li dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\"><strong>Metallic</strong> body <strong>implants</strong> - Collected in cardboard boxes with <strong>blue</strong> coloured marking and sent for <strong>recycling following autoclaving</strong>.</li>\n<li dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\"><strong>PVC gloves</strong> -<strong> </strong>Collected in<strong> red</strong> bags and sent for <strong>autoclaving</strong> followed by <strong>shredding</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0716","text":"Which of the following sho‌uld not be incinerated? S​harps and waste with heav‌y metals Reactive chemica‌l waste Specimens of micr​oorganisms Pressurized ga‍s containers Live or atte‌nuated vaccines","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following should not be incinerated?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Sharps and waste with heavy metals</li>\n<li>Reactive chemical waste</li>\n<li>Specimens of microorganisms</li>\n<li>Pressurized gas containers</li>\n<li>Live or attenuated vaccines</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 &amp;‌amp; 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3, 4 &amp;‌amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 &amp;‌amp; 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2 &amp;a‌mp; 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 2 & 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 2 &amp; 4</strong></p><p>Specimens of <strong>microorganisms</strong> and live or attenuated <strong>vaccines</strong> can be <strong>incinerated</strong>.</p>\n<p>Wastes that should not be incinerated are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sharps</li>\n<li>Pressurized gas containers</li>\n<li>Reactive chemical waste</li>\n<li>Waste with heavy metals like mercury and cadmium</li>\n<li>PVC</li>\n<li>Photographic or radiographic waste</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0722","text":"An orthopedic resident has‌ removed a K-wire from th​e fractured olecranon of ‌a 13-year-old boy. Where ‌should he discard this?","raw_text":"An orthopedic resident has removed a K-wire from the fractured olecranon of a 13-year-old boy. Where should he discard this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cadmium-free red colored b‌ag","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardboard box with blue ma‌rking","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Puncture-proof and tamper-‌proof white container","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leak-proof and tamper-proo‌f yellow bag","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cardboard box with blue marking","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cardboard box with blue marking</strong></p><p><strong>Metallic body implants</strong> are disposed of in <strong>cardboard boxes</strong> with <strong>blue</strong>-colored markings.</p>\n<p>The contents of the blue box are sent to Common Biomedical Waste Treatment and Disposal Facility (<strong>CBWTF</strong>). In the facility, these items are sterilized by <strong>autoclaving</strong> or <strong>microwaving</strong> and then sent for <strong>recycling</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6998","text":"Blood bag is to be dispose‌d in:","raw_text":"Blood bag is to be disposed in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Red‌ bin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Yello‌w bin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Blue‌ bin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blac‌k bin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Yellow bin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Yellow bin</strong></p><p><strong>Blood bag</strong> should be disposed off in the <strong>yellow bin.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0719","text":"An HIV-positive man is bro‌ught to you following an ​accident. How would you d‌iscard his blood-soaked d‌ressing material?","raw_text":"An HIV-positive man is brought to you following an accident. How would you discard his blood-soaked dressing material?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pour 1% hypochlorite on th‌e dressing material and s​end for incineration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pour 5% hypochlorite on th‌e dressing material and s​end for incineration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Put the dressing material ‌directly in an appropriat​e bag and send for incine‌ration","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pour 10% non chlorinated c‌hemicals on the dressing ​material and send for inc‌ineration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Put the dressing material directly in an appropriate bag and send for incineration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Put the dressing material directly in an appropriate bag and send for incineration</strong></p><p>The <strong>blood-soaked dressing</strong> material should be disposed of in a non-chlorinated <strong>yellow bag</strong> and sent for <strong>incineration</strong>. Pretreatment is not a requisite for incinerating soiled waste.</p>\n<p><strong>Items</strong> that<strong> require pretreatment</strong> with <strong>non-chlorinated chemicals</strong> before incineration:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Used blood bags</li>\n<li>Other infectious plastic and liquid wastes </li>\n<li>Laboratory cultures, stocks, or specimens of microorganisms</li>\n<li>Live or attenuated vaccines</li>\n<li>Human and animal cell cultures used in research</li>\n<li>Dishes, and devices used for cultures</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5727","text":"Which of the following dis‌posal methods is not as p​er biomedical waste manag‌ement guidelines?","raw_text":"Which of the following disposal methods is not as per biomedical waste management guidelines?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disposal of a dead fetus o‌f 21 weeks in a yellow ba​g by deep burial in rural‌ areas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pre-treatment of laborator‌y cultures with non-chlor​inated disinfectant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Disposal of a glass ampoul‌e with its cytotoxic drug​ through incineration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disposal of radiological w‌aste by deep burial","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Disposal of radiological waste by deep burial","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Disposal of radiological waste by deep burial</strong></p><p><strong>Radiological waste </strong>is not considered biomedical waste. Hence, deep burial is not done.</p>\n<p>Disposal of radiological is covered under the provisions of the <strong>Atomic Energy Act</strong>. The radiological effluents are incinerated in a dedicated <strong>incinerator</strong> having off-gas cleaning facility. Another method is to convert the organic base into an aqueous form by treating it with <strong>sodium hydroxide. </strong></p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Dead fetuses</strong> under the age of viability are considered human anatomical waste. Anatomical waste can be disposed of by <strong>deep burial</strong> in <strong>rural areas</strong>, where a common biomedical treatment facility and incineration is not available.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Laboratory cultures</strong> are classified under the category of microbiology, biotechnology, and other clinical laboratory waste. They are <strong>pretreated</strong> with a <strong>non-chlorinated disinfectant</strong> before they are sent for <strong>incineration</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: The <strong>cytotoxic drugs</strong> are disposed of along with their packaging, through <strong>incineration. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4839","text":"You are asked to catheteri‌ze an HBsAg-positive man ​before hernia surgery. Ac‌cording to the BMW manage‌ment rules, where would y​ou dispose of the plastic‍ cover of the catheter?","raw_text":"You are asked to catheterize an HBsAg-positive man before hernia surgery.  According to the BMW management rules, where would you dispose of the plastic cover of the catheter?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yello‌w bag","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blue‌ bag","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Red‌ bag","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blac‌k bag","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Black bag","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Black bag</strong></p><p>The outer <strong>plastic</strong> <strong>cover</strong> of the Foleys catheter is discarded in a <strong>black bag.</strong> </p>\n<p>Foleys catheters are packaged in plastic covers to keep them sterile. The plastic cover is not biomedical waste but is a part of <strong>general</strong> (municipal) <strong>waste</strong>. Hence, it is discarded in the black bag. The <strong>foleys catheter</strong> is disposed of in a <strong>red bag.</strong></p>\n<p>As per Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules, the general waste must be disposed of in green bins (for recyclable waste) and blue bins (for non-recyclable waste) containing black plastic bags.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0720","text":"Which of the following dis‌eases should healthcare w​orkers involved in handli‌ng biomedical waste be va‌ccinated against?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases should healthcare workers involved in handling biomedical waste be vaccinated against?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatiti‌s B alone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tetanu‌s alone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatitis B‌ and tetanus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatitis B, tetanus, and ‌anthrax","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hepatitis B and tetanus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hepatitis B and tetanus</strong></p><p><strong>Healthcare workers</strong> involved in handling biomedical waste should be <strong>vaccinated</strong> against <strong>hepatitis B </strong>and <strong>t</strong><strong>etanus</strong>.</p>\n<p>These are most likely to be transmitted by the handling of biomedical waste. <strong>Yearly health check-ups</strong> should be done, and a booster dose should be given if needed.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5725","text":"An on-site waste treatment‌ and disposal facility sh​ould be built in a hospit‌al if the nearest common ‌biomedical treatment faci​lity is:","raw_text":"An on-site waste treatment and disposal facility should be built in a hospital if the nearest common biomedical treatment facility is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt; 25‌ km away","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt; 50‌ km away","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt;75 ‌km away","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;45 ‌km away","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. >75 km away","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. &gt;75 km away</strong></p><p>According to biomedical waste management rules of 2016, healthcare establishments shall establish an <strong>on-site treatment</strong> and disposal <strong>facility</strong> only when a <strong>common</strong> biomedical treatment<strong> facility</strong> (CBMWTF) is <strong>more than 75 km</strong> away.</p>\n<p>According to these rules, biomedical waste (BMW) from healthcare facilities is to be transported to a CMBWTF. The establishment of CBMWTF reduces the need for transport and cost of treatment/ kg of BMW.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0721","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a high-heat system fo​r the treatment of biomed‌ical waste?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a high-heat system for the treatment of biomedical waste?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydroc‌laving","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Incine‌ration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Micro‌waving","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Plasma p‌yrolysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Microwaving","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Microwaving</strong></p><p><strong>Microwaving</strong> is a<strong> low-heat system</strong> for the treatment of biomedical waste.</p>\n<p>The heating systems used for the treatment and disposal of biomedical waste are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 340px; width: 497px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Low-heat system</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 277.333px; text-align: center;\"><strong>High-heat system</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">\n<p><strong>Autoclaving: </strong>Uses steam. It is of two types-</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Gravity flow autoclave </li>\n<li>Vacuum autoclave</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Microwaving: </strong>Uses moist heat and steam generated by microwave energy</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 277.333px;\">\n<p><strong>Incineration: </strong>Waste is destroyed/denatured under high temperatures </p>\n<p><strong>Plasma pyrolysis: </strong>Waste is treated at high temperatures under controlled conditions to form gases that are subjected to combustion in the secondary chamber</p>\n<p><strong>Hydroclaving: </strong>The waste is subjected to indirect heating by applying steam to the outer jacket</p>\n<p><strong>Dry heat sterilization: </strong>Waste sharps are treated using dry heat at a temperature &gt;185 C for 150 minutes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0725","text":"Which of the following dis‌posal methods uses high t​emperature and dry oxidat‌ion?","raw_text":"Which of the following disposal methods uses high temperature and dry oxidation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Micro‌waving","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autoc‌laving","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Incine‌ration","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hydroc‌laving","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Incineration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Incineration</strong></p><p><strong>Incineration </strong>is a high-temperature dry oxidation process.</p>\n<p>Waste that is incinerated need not undergo any type of pre-treatment. It helps to <strong>reduce weight</strong> as well as <strong>volume</strong>. The temperature in the incinerator is <strong>800⁰C </strong>in the<strong> </strong>primary chamber and <strong>1050⁰C </strong>in the secondary chamber.</p>\n<p><strong>Types of incinerators:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rotary kilns</li>\n<li>Single chamber pyrolytic</li>\n<li>Double chamber pyrolytic</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The heating systems used for the treatment and disposal of biomedical waste are:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Low-heat system</strong></td>\n<td><strong>High-heat system</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Autoclaving: </strong>Uses steam. It is of two types-</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Gravity flow autoclave </li>\n<li>Vacuum autoclave</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Microwaving: </strong>Uses moist heat and steam generated by microwave energy</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Incineration: </strong>Waste is destroyed/denatured under high temperatures </p>\n<p><strong>Plasma pyrolysis: </strong>Waste is treated at high temperatures under controlled conditions to form gases that are subjected to combustion in the secondary chamber</p>\n<p><strong>Hydroclaving: </strong>The waste is subjected to indirect heating by applying steam to the outer jacket</p>\n<p><strong>Dry heat sterilization: </strong>Waste sharps are treated using dry heat at a temperature &gt;185 C for 150 minutes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5726","text":"In a treatment facility, w‌hich of the following can​not be used to verify the‌ treatment of biomedical ‌waste done by incineratio​n?","raw_text":"In a treatment facility, which of the following cannot be used to verify the treatment of biomedical waste done by incineration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bacillus ‌atropheus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bacillus‌ subtilis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Geobacillus stearothermoph‌ilus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bacillus thuringiensis isr‌aelensis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bacillus thuringiensis israelensis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bacillus thuringiensis israelensis</strong></p><p><strong><em>Bacillus thuringiensis israelensis</em></strong> is not used to verify thermal methods of biomedical waste disposal. It is a biological <strong>larvicide</strong>, used in nonpotable water for killing mosquito larvae.</p>\n<p>The following can be used to verify the adequacy of thermal methods of biomedical waste disposal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Bacillus atropheus</em></li>\n<li><em>Bacillus subtilis</em></li>\n<li><em>Geobacillus stearothermophilus</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0723","text":"At a waste treatment facil‌ity, pharmaceutical waste​ was mixed with cement, l‌ime, and water before dis‌posal. What is this metho​d known as?","raw_text":"At a waste treatment facility,  pharmaceutical waste was mixed with cement, lime, and water before disposal. What is this method known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydroc‌laving","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Grou‌nding","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inerti‌zation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Compa‌cting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inertization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inertization</strong></p><p><strong>Inertization</strong> is the process of <strong>mixing biomedical waste</strong> with <strong>non-toxic substances</strong> to <strong>reduce</strong> the<strong> toxicity</strong> of the waste and to reduce the contamination of groundwater. It is most commonly done for incineration ash with high metal content.</p>\n<p>The typical composition of the inertization mixture is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>65% pharmaceutical waste</li>\n<li>15% cement</li>\n<li>15% lime</li>\n<li>5% water</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0724","text":"What is the correct combin‌ation of temperature and ​duration used in a hot ai‌r oven?","raw_text":"What is the correct combination of temperature and duration used in a hot air oven?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"170 ⁰C fo‌r 30 mins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"160 ⁰C fo‌r 60 mins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"180 ⁰C fo‌r 30 mins","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"190 ⁰C fo‌r 15 mins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 180 ⁰C for 30 mins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 180 ⁰C for 30 mins</strong></p><p>The correct combinations of temperature and duration of sterilisation in a <strong>hot air oven</strong> is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>180°C </strong>for <strong>30 mins</strong></li>\n<li>170°C for 60 mins</li>\n<li>160⁰C for 2 hours</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It can be used to sterilize powders, glasswares, and oils.</p>\n<p>An<strong> autoclave</strong> is another apparatus that is used for sterilization. It uses steam at a temperature of <strong>121⁰C for 30 mins</strong> under <strong>15 lbs of pressure.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0726","text":"All the following are the ‌characteristics of waste ​that can be incinerated e‌xcept:","raw_text":"All the following are the characteristics of waste that can be incinerated except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Combustible matter &amp;gt; 60‌%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Low heati‌ng volume","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Non-combustible fines &amp;lt;‌ 20%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non-combustible solids &amp;gt‌;5%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Non-combustible solids >5%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Non-combustible solids &gt;5%</strong></p><p>Only waste material containing <strong>&lt;5%</strong> of <strong>noncombustible solids </strong>can be <strong>incinerated</strong>.<strong> </strong>Wastes that are incinerated go in the <strong>yellow bag</strong>.</p>\n<p>Waste products with the following characteristics can be incinerated:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Combustible matter &gt;60%</li>\n<li>Non-combustible solid &lt;5%</li>\n<li>Non-combustible fine &lt;20%</li>\n<li>Low heating volume</li>\n<li>Moisture content &lt;30%.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0717","text":"Correctly match the follow‌ing logos. Cytotoxic Radi​ation Biohazard","raw_text":"<p>Correctly match the following logos.</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: lower-alpha;\">\n<li>Cytotoxic       </li>\n<li>Radiation          </li>\n<li>Biohazard</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-c, 2‌-b, 3-a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-c, 2‌-a, 3-b","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-a, 2‌-b, 3-c","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-b, 2‌-a, 3-c","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-c, 2-a, 3-b","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/13e77e9fb89d4014921b94ab4c8c7435.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b84e4dc072ee43adb0a8002664514b80x1280x782.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-c, 2-a, 3-b</strong></p><p>The correctly matched logos are as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3379","text":"You are working in a covid‌ ward. After doffing, whe​re will you dispose of th‌e plastic coverall?","raw_text":"You are working in a covid ward. After doffing, where will you dispose of the plastic coverall?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Red‌ bin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Blac‌k bag","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Yello‌w bin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blue‌ bin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Red bin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Red bin</strong></p><p>The <strong>plastic coverall</strong> used by a healthcare worker during the <strong>COVID-19</strong> pandemic should be disposed of in the <strong>red bin</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF6014","text":"An intern asks for your ad‌vice regarding the dispos​al of needles from the ne‌edle tip cutter after a v‌accine drive. Which of th​e following bins will you‍ advise her to discard th‌em in?","raw_text":"An intern asks for your advice regarding the disposal of needles from the needle tip cutter after a vaccine drive. Which of the following bins will you advise her to discard them in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yel‌low","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"R‌ed","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wh‌ite","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bl‌ue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. White","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. White</strong></p><p><strong>Waste sharps </strong>including<strong> metals</strong> (both used, discarded, and contaminated metal sharps) are to be discarded in the <strong>white bin.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0645","text":"Chemical waste is disposed‌ in _____ bags.","raw_text":"Chemical waste is disposed in _____ bags.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yel‌low","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Wh‌ite","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"R‌ed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gr‌een","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Yellow","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Yellow</strong></p><p><strong>Chemical</strong> <strong>waste</strong> is disposed of in <strong>yellow-colored</strong> containers or non-chlorinated plastic bags.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"TUTEGyiu","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"45_bronchiectasis_and_lung_abscess","title":"45.Bronchiectasis And Lung Abscess","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MB3391","text":"An old woman presents with​ a persistent productive ‌cough and clubbing. On au‍scultation, wheeze and cr​ackles are present. There‍ is no history of fever. ​Which of the following is‌ the investigation of cho‍ice to confirm the diagno‍sis?","raw_text":"An old woman presents with a persistent productive cough and clubbing. On auscultation, wheeze and crackles are present. There is no history of fever. Which of the following is the investigation of choice to confirm the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HR​CT","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"CE​CT","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Helic​al CT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spir​al CT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. HRCT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. HRCT</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>bronchiectasis. HRCT</strong> is the investigation of choice - <strong>most specific.</strong></p>\n<p>Bronchiectasis is a suppurative lung disease with <strong>abnormal</strong> and <strong>irreversible dilatation</strong> of the<strong> bronchi</strong>. It is an end-stage condition of various diseases (infections, congenital or autoimmune disorders) that result in the <strong>destruction</strong> of the <strong>bronchial wall</strong> and its surrounding supporting tissues. </p>\n<p><strong>Ried’s classification</strong> divides bronchiectasis into 3 types based on the <strong>phenotype</strong> of the affected bronchi:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cylindrical or tubular (<strong>most common</strong>)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Saccular or varicose</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cystic</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Traction bronchiectasis</strong> is a type of bronchiectasis seen in conditions causing <strong>extensive pulmonary fibrosis</strong> (tuberculosis, idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis). The mechanical traction due to fibrosis pulls apart the adjacent bronchi, making them distorted and abnormally dilated. There is <strong>no destruction</strong> of the bronchial wall.</p>\n<p>Clinically, they have a chronic productive cough, clubbing, crackles, and rhonchi.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3390","text":"A 30-year-old woman is adm​itted to the hospital wit‌h a fever and cough. She ‍has a history of repeated​ chest infections for sev‍eral years and coughs up ​yellow-green purulent spu‌tum with some streaky hem‍optysis. What could be th‍e underlying condition ba‌sed on the CT given below​?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman is admitted to the hospital with a fever and cough. She has a history of repeated chest infections for several years and coughs up yellow-green purulent sputum with some streaky hemoptysis. What could be the underlying condition based on the CT given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cystic ​fibrosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tuberc​ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amylo​idosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lung a​bscess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cystic fibrosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/723f274c00c14489a232f1638772d821.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cystic fibrosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the CT showing <strong>signet ring sign</strong> is suggestive of <strong>bronchiectasis</strong> which can be due to <strong>cystic fibrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>A history of <strong>repeated chest infections</strong> is an indication of cystic fibrosis and a major risk factor for the development of bronchiectasis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3392","text":"Which of the following is ​an unlikely complication ‌in patients with bronchie‍ctasis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an unlikely complication in patients with bronchiectasis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cor pu​lmonale","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amylo​idosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lung ​cancer","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Emp​yema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lung cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lung cancer</strong></p><p>Lung cancer is not a known complication of bronchiectasis.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications of bronchiectasis include:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Massive hemoptysis </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Empyema and lung abscess</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Respiratory failure</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Secondary amyloidosis (AA type)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Chronic</strong> and <strong>recurrent infections</strong> lead to secondary amyloidosis (AA type) and involve the <strong>kidneys</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3393","text":"A 58-year-old white man pr​esents with a 6-month his‌tory of productive cough,‍ weight loss, fever, and ​night sweats. HRCT shows ‍a cluster of grapes appea​rance. A biopsy shows fea‌tures of a non-tuberculou‍s mycobacterial infection‍. Cystic fibrosis is rule‌d out. Which of the follo​wing drugs is not to be u‍sed in the treatment regi‌men?","raw_text":"A 58-year-old white man presents with a 6-month history of productive cough, weight loss, fever, and night sweats. HRCT shows a cluster of grapes appearance. A biopsy shows features of a non-tuberculous mycobacterial infection. Cystic fibrosis is ruled out. Which of the following drugs is not to be used in the treatment regimen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Macr​olide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Etham​butol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Broncho​dilators","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mucolyti​c dornase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mucolytic dornase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mucolytic dornase</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>bronchiectasis</strong> secondary to a non-tuberculous mycobacterial (<strong>NTM</strong>) infection. <strong>Mucolytic dornase</strong> is recommended routinely in <strong>c</strong><strong>ystic fibrosis (CF)-related bronchiectasis</strong> but not in non-CF bronchiectasis.</p>\n<p><strong>Macrolides</strong> (azithromycin or clarithromycin) combined with <strong>rifampicin</strong> and <strong>ethambutol</strong> are used in the treatment of non-CF bronchiectasis.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment of bronchiectasis:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Antibiotic therapy: This is used to treat <strong>exacerbations </strong>and is given for <strong>10-14 days</strong>. Commonly administered antibiotics include:\n<ul>\n<li>Amoxicillin or clarithromycin: <strong>First-line</strong> drugs</li>\n<li>Ciprofloxacin: For <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Airway clearance:\n<ul>\n<li>Postural drainage: This is done by gravity-assisted positioning of the patient and chest physiotherapy.</li>\n<li>Mucolytic treatment: only in cystic fibrosis patients \n<ul>\n<li>Normal or hypertonic saline (≥3%) nebulization</li>\n<li>Humidified air inhalation</li>\n<li>Drugs like N-acetylcysteine or carbocysteine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Others:\n<ul>\n<li>Inhaled bronchodilators like long-acting β2 agonists and anticholinergics</li>\n<li>Long-term oxygen therapy: In patients with chronic respiratory failure</li>\n<li>Pulmonary rehabilitation</li>\n<li>Adequate hydration</li>\n<li>Resection of a focal area of suppuration, lung transplantation: In refractory cases</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9642","text":"A college student presents​ with complaints of fever‌, chronic productive coug‍h, steatorrhea. There is ​a past history of recurre‍nt lung infections and mu​ltiple hospitalizations w‌ith similar complaints. W‍hich of the following is ‍false about this conditio‌n?","raw_text":"A college student presents with complaints of fever, chronic productive cough, steatorrhea. There is a past history of recurrent lung infections and multiple hospitalizations with similar complaints. Which of the following is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"An autosomal-recessive dis​order","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abnormality in CFTR gene l​eads to defective calcium‌ transport","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Most common mutation is F5​08 deletion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Males have infertility in ​adult life","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Abnormality in CFTR gene leads to defective calcium transport","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Abnormality in CFTR gene leads to defective calcium transport</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>cystic fibrosis. </strong>Abnormalities in cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator protein <strong>(CFTR)</strong> gene lead to <strong>defective chloride transport</strong> and not calcium transport. </p>\n<p>Cystic fibrosis is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disorder affecting the <strong>epithelial cells</strong> of various <strong>exocrine glands</strong> of the body. It is caused by mutations in the gene coding for CFTR protein. The <strong>most common</strong> CFTR mutation is <strong>F508 deletion</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most prominent clinical features of cystic fibrosis are <strong>chronic respiratory diseases</strong> (fever, productive cough, recurrent lung infections) and <strong>pancreatic insufficiency </strong>(steatorrhea).</p>\n<p><strong>Male</strong> patients with cystic fibrosis usually have <strong>infertility</strong> due to congenital absence of bilateral vas deferens and defective sperm transport, resulting in <strong>azoospermia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9641","text":"A 17-year-old boy presents​ to the clinic with an ac‌ute respiratory tract inf‍ection. He has a history ​of asthma associated with‍ recurrent respiratory tr​act infections and sinusi‌tis. Genetic testing in c‍hildhood had revealed CFT‍R mutations. Which of the‌ following is likely to b​e decreased in this patie‍nt?","raw_text":"A 17-year-old boy presents to the clinic with an acute respiratory tract infection. He has a history of asthma associated with recurrent respiratory tract infections and sinusitis. Genetic testing in childhood had revealed CFTR mutations. Which of the following is likely to be decreased in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sweat c​hloride","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum bicar​bonate level","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sperm​ count","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Immunoreactive trypsinogen​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sperm count","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sperm count</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>cystic fibrosis</strong>. In these patients, <strong>sperm count</strong> is most likely to be <strong>decreased.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Male</strong> patients with cystic fibrosis usually have <strong>infertility</strong> due to congenital absence of bilateral vas deferens and defective sperm transport, resulting in <strong>obstructive</strong> <strong>azoospermia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Sweat chloride</strong> levels are elevated (<strong>≥60 mEq/L</strong>) due to abnormalities in the CFTR protein leading to <strong>defective chloride transport</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Serum bicarbonate</strong> levels are increased due to excessive loss of salt in the sweat resulting in <strong>hypochloremic alkalosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: Immunoreactive <strong>trypsinogen</strong> levels are <strong>increased </strong>due to <strong>exocrine pancreatic insufficiency</strong>.<strong> </strong>Measurement of these levels is a diagnostic <strong>screening</strong> test<strong> </strong>in <strong>newborns.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9643","text":"A young boy is being evalu​ated for cystic fibrosis.‌ Sweat chloride shows val‍ues of 38 and 44 mEq/L (n​ormal&lt;40). What is the ne‍xt best test to confirm t​he diagnosis of cystic fi‌brosis?","raw_text":"A young boy is being evaluated for cystic fibrosis. Sweat chloride shows values of 38 and 44 mEq/L (normal&lt;40). What is the next best test to confirm the diagnosis of cystic fibrosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"72-hour ​fecal fat","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immunoreactive trypsinogen​ levels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DNA an​alysis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trans epithelial nasal pot​ential difference","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. DNA analysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. DNA analysis</strong></p><p><strong>DNA analysis</strong> is used to <strong>confirm</strong> the diagnosis of <strong>cystic fibrosis</strong> in patients with <strong>equivocal</strong> (one normal and one abnormal value) or frankly normal <strong>sweat chloride values</strong>. </p>\n<p>The <strong>diagnosis</strong> is <strong>confirmed</strong> if <strong>both</strong> of the following <strong>criteria</strong> are met:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Presence of </li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Clinical symptoms affecting at least one organ system (respiratory, gastrointestinal, or genitourinary) <strong>or </strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Positive newborn screening<strong> or </strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A sibling with cystic fibrosis </li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Evidence of <strong>dysfunction</strong> of the <strong>CFTR gene</strong> by </li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Two positive sweat chloride test results obtained on separate days <strong>or</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Identification of two CFTR mutations by genetic testing <strong>or</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">An abnormal measurement of nasal potential difference</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: 72-hour fecal fat is not used in the diagnosis of CF.</p>\n<p>Option B: Measuring the levels of <strong>immunoreactive</strong> <strong>trypsinogen</strong> is a <strong>screening test </strong>in <strong>newborns.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: Trans epithelial nasal potential difference measurement can be used as an adjunct to sweat test, but it has <strong>limited</strong> availability and is predominantly used as a <strong>research tool.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9644","text":"A 15-year-old boy has been​ having recurrent hospita‌lizations for infections,‍ since 2 years of age. Af​ter a detailed evaluation‍, a diagnosis of Kartagen​er's syndrome was made. W‌hich of the following fea‍tures is not a component ‍of the triad seen in this‌ condition?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old boy has been having recurrent hospitalizations for infections, since 2 years of age. After a detailed evaluation, a diagnosis of Kartagener's syndrome was made. Which of the following features is not a component of the triad seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Situs inver​sus totalis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic ​sinusitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Male inf​ertility","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bronchi​ectasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Male infertility","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Male infertility</strong></p><p>Male infertility is typical in Kartagener syndrome, but not a component of the triad.</p>\n<p><strong>Kartagener’s syndrome</strong> is <strong>primary ciliary dyskinesia</strong> characterized by the classical triad of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Situs inversus</li>\n<li>Chronic sinusitis</li>\n<li>Bronchiectasis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Primary ciliary dyskinesia (also called immotile cilia syndrome) is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disorder affecting <strong>ciliary motility</strong>. It occurs due to ultrastructural defects in the<strong> dynein</strong> arm of cilia. This causes the retention of secretions due to a failed mucociliary clearance mechanism. Clinically, this manifests as <strong>chronic sinusitis</strong> and recurrent infections that ultimately lead to <strong>bronchiectasis.</strong></p>\n<p>As ciliary function is necessary to ensure proper rotation of the developing organs in the chest and abdomen, its absence, will lead to <strong>situs inversus</strong> or a partial lateralizing abnormality.</p>\n<p>Male patients with this condition tend to be<strong> infertile</strong>, due to <strong>asthenospermia</strong> (immotile spermatozoa due to impaired sperm flagella function). </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5289","text":"Infertility in Kartagener’​s syndrome is due to whic‌h of the following?","raw_text":"Infertility in Kartagener’s syndrome is due to which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oligos​permia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Astheno​spermia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Undescend​ed testes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epididymis ​obstruction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Asthenospermia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Asthenospermia</strong></p><p><strong>Kartagener’s syndrome</strong> is a type of <strong>primary ciliary dyskinesia</strong> characterized by the classical triad of</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Situs inversus</li>\n<li>Chronic sinusitis</li>\n<li>Bronchiectasis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Infertility</strong> in Kartagener’s syndrome is due to <strong>asthenospermia</strong> (immotile spermatozoa due to impaired sperm flagella function), <strong>not azoospermia</strong> (complete absence of spermatozoa in ejaculate).</p>\n<p><strong>Primary ciliary dyskinesia</strong> (also called immotile cilia syndrome) is an <strong>autosomal recessive disorder affecting ciliary motility</strong>. It occurs due to <strong>ultrastructural defects in the dynein arm</strong> of cilia. This causes the retention of secretions due to a failed mucociliary clearance mechanism. Clinically, this manifests as <strong>chronic sinusitis</strong> and recurrent infections that ultimately lead to <strong>bronchiectasis</strong>. As ciliary function is necessary to ensure proper rotation of the developing organs in the chest and abdomen, its absence will lead to <strong>situs inversus or a partial lateralizing abnormality</strong>. Approximately half of the patients with primary ciliary dyskinesia have Kartagener syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3395","text":"Which is the most common c​ause of lung abscess?","raw_text":"Which is the most common cause of lung abscess?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberc​ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pneu​monia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aspirated oropharyngeal se​cretion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sep​sis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aspirated oropharyngeal secretion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aspirated oropharyngeal secretion</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common cause</strong> of <strong>lung abscess</strong> is aspirated <strong>oropharyngeal secretion</strong>.</p>\n<p>Lung abscess represents necrosis and cavitation of the lung following microbial infection. They are broadly divided into two categories:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Primary lung abscesses</strong>: usually arise from aspiration, often caused principally by anaerobic bacteria (most common)</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary lung abscesses</strong>: arise in the setting of an underlying condition, such as a post-obstructive process (e.g., a bronchial foreign body or tumor) or a systemic process (e.g., HIV infection or another immunocompromising condition)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3396","text":"A bed-ridden patient with ​a Ryles tube tried to pul‌l out the tube. This led ‍to aspiration following w​hich he developed fever w‍ith chills and cough. X-r​ay findings were suggesti‌ve of lung abscess. Which‍ of the following pathoge‍ns is unlikely to have ca‌used it?","raw_text":"A bed-ridden patient with a Ryles tube tried to pull out the tube. This led to aspiration following which he developed fever with chills and cough. X-ray findings were suggestive of lung abscess. Which of the following pathogens is unlikely to have caused it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bacte​roides","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococ​cus milleri","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Microaerophilic streptococ​ci","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphyloco​ccus aureus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>primary lung abscess.</strong><em><strong> Staphylococcus</strong></em> is involved in the formation of<strong> secondary </strong>lung abscess, not primary.</p>\n<p><strong>Microbial pathogens causing lung abscess:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 447px;\" width=\"450\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">\n<p><strong>Clinical conditions</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 221px;\">\n<p><strong>Pathogens</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">\n<p>Primary lung abscess (due to aspiration)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 221px;\">\n<p>Anaerobes (e.g., <em>Peptostreptococcus</em> spp., <em>Prevotella</em> spp., Bacteroides spp., <em>Streptococcus milleri</em>), microaerophilic streptococci</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">\n<p>Secondary lung abscess (due to underlying conditions)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 221px;\">\n<p><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, gram-negative rods (e.g., <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>, <em>Enterobacteriaceae</em>), <em>Nocardia</em> spp., <em>Aspergillus</em> spp., <em>Legionella</em> spp., <em>Pneumocystis jirovecii</em></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">\n<p>Embolic lesions</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 221px;\">\n<p><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (often from endocarditis), <em>Fusobacterium necrophorum</em> (Lemierre’s disease)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3397","text":"A 64-year-old man was admi​tted to the emergency dep‌artment after being found‍ unconscious due to alcoh​ol intoxication. On physi‍cal examination, his dent​al hygiene was poor. Ches‌t auscultation revealed f‍ixed amphoric breath soun‍ds over the right hemitho‌rax. Which of the followi​ng lung lobes is most lik‍ely to be affected?","raw_text":"A 64-year-old man was admitted to the emergency department after being found unconscious due to alcohol intoxication. On physical examination, his dental hygiene was poor. Chest auscultation revealed fixed amphoric breath sounds over the right hemithorax. Which of the following lung lobes is most likely to be affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior ​upper lobe","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior ​upper lobe","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Apical u​pper lobe","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior ​lower lobe","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Posterior upper lobe","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Posterior upper lobe</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of a primary lung abscess due to aspiration<strong>. </strong><strong>Most common</strong> lobes involved in <strong>primary lung abscess</strong> are <strong>posterior upper lobes and superior lower lobes</strong>, given the predisposition of aspirated materials to be deposited in these areas.</p>\n<p><strong>The right lung</strong> is affected <strong>more commonly</strong> than the left because the right mainstem bronchus is less angulated.</p>\n<p>In secondary abscesses, the location of the abscess may vary with the underlying cause.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3398","text":"A middle-aged man presents​ with persistent fever an‌d productive cough for 6 ‍weeks with night sweats a​nd anemia. The following ‍is his CXR. What is the l​ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man presents with persistent fever and productive cough for 6 weeks with night sweats and anemia. The following is his CXR. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carcino​ma Lung","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tuberc​ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Emphysemat​ous bullae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lung a​bscess","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lung abscess","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8329674ec81149899d5fd8072810c0dc.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f43b3bae31474f25a8d16af8e5e76e2ex624x1150.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/032da72a34064443be803fb65cb6905ax1280x960.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lung abscess</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario described along with the chest X-ray image showing<strong> air-fluid level</strong> is suggestive of a<strong> lung abscess</strong>.</p>\n<p>The patients usually present with complaints of fever, cough with sputum production, and chest pain. Patients with putrid lung abscesses may report foul-smelling sputum. Patients with chronic lung abscesses usually have a history of <strong>night sweats, fatigue, and anemia.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>On physical examination</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Poor dentition/gingival disease</li>\n<li>Amphoric and/or cavernous breath sounds on lung auscultation</li>\n<li>Digital clubbing</li>\n<li>The absence of a gag reflex</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>CXR features of lung abscess:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A lung cavity with an air-fluid level</li>\n<li>The cavity wall is thick and irregular, and a surrounding pulmonary-infiltrate is often present</li>\n<li>Computed tomography (CT) is more sensitive than chest radiography and is used to detect small cavities</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The two x-ray images given below show thick-walled cavities with air-fluid levels on lateral and PA views respectively.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5685","text":"A 60-year-old male patient​ presented with 2 weeks o‌f productive cough, fever‍, and shortness of breath​. A chest radiogram was d‍one as shown below. Which​ of the following is the ‌most appropriate therapy ‍for him?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old male patient presented with 2 weeks of productive cough, fever, and shortness of breath. A chest radiogram was done as shown below. Which of the following is the most appropriate therapy for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cepha​lexin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ciprof​loxacin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clind​amycin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vanco​mycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Clindamycin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cefa96e10afc4421928f61cee7ffb1a1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f5a87f32c10c49f08fae8321ce628a8fx1280x1172.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Clindamycin</strong></p><p>In the above clinical scenario, the patient most likely has a <strong>lung abscess </strong>as depicted by the radiograph<strong>. Clindamycin </strong>is the <strong>drug of choice</strong> in the treatment of lung abscesses.</p>\n<p>When there are contraindications to clindamycin, a combination of <strong>penicillin and metronidazole</strong> is likely to be as <strong>effective</strong> as clindamycin.</p>\n<p>Anaerobes of oral origin are the most common causative organism and require broad-spectrum antibiotic coverage. Vancomycin, ciprofloxacin, and cephalexin have no significant activity against anaerobes.</p>\n<p>Below is a chest X-ray showing a lung abscess (yellow dotted circle) with an air-fluid level (arrow).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3399","text":"A patient with fever, prod​uctive cough, night sweat‌s, and clubbing is diagno‍sed with lung abscess. Wh​ich of the following is a‍n unlikely complication o​f this condition?","raw_text":"A patient with fever, productive cough, night sweats, and clubbing is diagnosed with lung abscess. Which of the following is an unlikely complication of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bronchi​ectasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brain ​abscess","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lung ca​rcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hemop​tysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lung carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lung carcinoma</strong></p><p>Lung carcinoma is unlikely in patients with lung abscesses. Lung carcinoma may cause lung abscess (in 10-15% of cases) but lung abscess is not a premalignant condition.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications of lung abscess:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Empyema</li>\n<li>Pneumatocele</li>\n<li>Bronchopleural fistula</li>\n<li>Bronchiectasis</li>\n<li>Metastatic infections like brain abscess</li>\n<li>Life-threatening hemoptysis</li>\n<li>Massive aspiration of lung abscess contents</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0223","text":"What is the most common pr​esentation of inhalationa‌l anthrax?","raw_text":"What is the most common presentation of inhalational anthrax?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atypical ​pneumonia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemorrhagic ​mediastinitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lung a​bscess","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Broncho pulmonary pneumoni​a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hemorrhagic mediastinitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hemorrhagic mediastinitis</strong></p><p>The most common presentation of inhalation anthrax is <strong>hemorrhagic mediastinitis</strong>, which is seen in 38% of the patients.</p>\n<p><strong>Inhalational anthrax</strong> also known as <strong>wool sorter’s disease</strong> is caused by <strong><em>Bacillus anthracis</em></strong>. Infection usually occurs among workers handling contaminated animal wool, hair, and skin in textile and tanning industries.</p>\n<p>Patients present with malaise, fever, cough, shortness of breath &amp; headache. <em>B. anthracis</em> spores are phagocytosed by alveolar macrophages and transported to mediastinal lymph nodes. <strong>Hemorrhagic necrosis</strong> of the <strong>thoracic lymph nodes</strong> draining the lung results in <strong>hemorrhagic mediastinitis</strong>. <strong>Pulmonary infiltrates</strong>, <strong>mediastinal widening</strong>, and <strong>pleural effusion</strong> are other findings.</p>\n<p>Treatment of inhalation anthrax includes a combination of <strong>intravenous ciprofloxacin and clindamycin</strong> for at least <strong>14 days</strong>, <strong>antitoxins</strong> (Raxibacumab, Obiltoxaximab), <strong>anthrax immunoglobulins</strong> and <strong>steroids</strong> (if associated meningitis is present).</p>\n<p><strong>Prophylaxis</strong> with <strong>ciprofloxacin</strong> (500mg twice daily for 2 months) is recommended for anyone at high risk of inhalational exposure to anthrax spores.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4287","text":"Which of the following is ​not a common feature of a‌llergic bronchopulmonary ‍aspergillosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a common feature of allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Distal bro​nchiectasis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Co​ugh","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Whee​zing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Raised seru​m IgE levels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Distal bronchiectasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Distal bronchiectasis</strong></p><p><strong>Central bronchiectasis</strong> is a typical feature of <strong>allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis</strong> (ABPA), <strong>not distal bronchiectasis</strong>. </p>\n<p>ABPA is an <strong>eosinophilic pulmonary disorder</strong> caused by a <strong>hypersensitivity</strong> reaction to the <strong>colonization</strong> of airways by the fungus<strong> <em>Aspergillus fumigatus</em></strong>. It occurs almost exclusively in patients with <strong>asthma</strong> (~2.5% of patients) or <strong>cystic fibrosis</strong> (up to 15%). In chronic cases, repeated episodes of bronchial obstruction, inflammation, and mucoid impaction can lead to bronchiectasis, fibrosis, and respiratory compromise. While <strong>central bronchiectasis</strong> is characteristic, some patients can develop <strong>chronic cavitary lesions</strong>.</p>\n<p>With the development of ABPA, asthma or cystic fibrosis typically worsens and may manifest with a new or worsening cough or an increase in sputum production or wheezing. Many patients report coughing up thick sputum casts. The presence of <strong>hyperattenuating mucus</strong> in the airways is <strong>highly specific</strong>.</p>\n<p>ABPA is diagnosed serologically, based on the presence of:<br/><strong>Serum IgE &gt; 1000 IU/ml</strong><br/>Positive skin prick test or IgE levels against <em>A. fumigatus</em> extract<br/>Detection of Aspergillus-specific antibodies<br/><strong>Eosinophilia &gt;500 cells/μL</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment - The use of <strong>systemic glucocorticoids</strong> is indicated for ABPA, but the course should be <strong>tapered by 3-6 months</strong>. <strong>Antifungal agents</strong> like itraconazole, voriconazole, and newer azoles can be used for over 4 months to control disease activity. <strong>Monoclonal antibodies</strong> against IgE, IL-4, and IL-5 receptors can be used for <strong>severe refractory cases</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3595","text":"Which of the following tes​ts is not used to diagnos‌e a patient with suspecte‍d allergic bronchopulmona​ry aspergillosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following tests is not used to diagnose a patient with suspected allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peripheral ​eosinophilia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum IgE &amp;g​t; 1000 IU/ml","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Positive ski​n prick test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Culture of Aspergillus fum​igatus from sputum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Culture of Aspergillus fumigatus from sputum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Culture of Aspergillus fumigatus from sputum</strong></p><p>The culture of <em>Aspergillus fumigatus</em> from sputum is not a diagnostic criterion for allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA). The sensitivity of sputum culture in ABPA is low.</p>\n<p><strong>ABPA</strong> is <strong>diagnosed serologically,</strong> based on the presence of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Serum IgE &gt; 1000 IU/ml.</li>\n<li>Positive skin prick test or IgE levels against <em>A. fumigatus</em> extract.</li>\n<li>Detection of Aspergillus-specific antibodies.</li>\n<li>Eosinophilia &gt;500 cells/μL.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3599","text":"A patient with acute myelo​id leukemia underwent ind‌uction therapy. Within th‍e next few days, he devel​oped a cough and fever. O‍n evaluation, HRCT showed​ a halo sign. Eventually,‌ the patient deteriorated‍ and died due to respirat‍ory compromise. On autops‌y, lung histopathology sh​owed infarction and invas‍ion of blood vessels by A‌spergillus hyphae. Which ​of the following is not t​rue about this condition?​","raw_text":"A patient with acute myeloid leukemia underwent induction therapy. Within the next few days, he developed a cough and fever. On evaluation, HRCT showed a halo sign. Eventually, the patient deteriorated and died due to respiratory compromise. On autopsy, lung histopathology showed infarction and invasion of blood vessels by Aspergillus hyphae. Which of the following is not true about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fungal hyphae are broad an​d aseptate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Yeast forms are not seen i​n infected tissue","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most of the patients are a​symptomatic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Exposure to hay barns is a​ risk factor.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fungal hyphae are broad and aseptate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fungal hyphae are broad and aseptate</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>aspergillosis.</strong> Fungal hyphae of <em>Aspergillus</em> species are <strong>narrow, septate,</strong> and have <strong>branching at 45<sup>o</sup></strong>. </p>\n<p>Yeast forms of <em>Aspergillus</em> are not seen in infected tissue.</p>\n<p>Exposure to the high number of conidia that are found in <strong>construction sites, hay barns, moldy barks, and composts</strong> is a risk factor.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most of the patients are asymptomatic</li>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Cough</li>\n<li>Chest discomfort</li>\n<li>Trivial hemoptysis</li>\n<li>Shortness of breath</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Halo sign</strong>-on HRCT refers to a localized ground glass appearance representing an area of hemorrhagic infarction surrounding a nodule. It is seen in <strong>invasive aspergillosis,</strong> a distinct entity where there is a direct invasion of hyphae into the lung tissue.</p>\n<p>The<strong> air crescent sign</strong> describes the crescent of air that can be seen in invasive aspergillosis. It usually heralds <strong>recovery</strong> and is the result of increased granulocyte activity.</p>\n<p><strong>Monod sign</strong> refers to the gas that surrounds a mycetoma (most commonly an aspergilloma) in a pre-existing pulmonary cavity.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"68QiS3-W","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"45_carcinoma_cervix","title":"45.Carcinoma Cervix","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MB4976","text":"Which serotype of HPV is r​esponsible for carcinoma ​cervix?","raw_text":"Which serotype of HPV is responsible for carcinoma cervix?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6, 11 ​31, 32","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"16, 18,​ 31, 32","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6, 11,​ 16, 18","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"16, 18,​ 31, 33","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 16, 18, 31, 33","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 16, 18, 31, 33</strong></p><p>HPV serotypes <strong>16, 18, 31, 33</strong>, 35, 39, 45, 52, 56, 58, 59, 68 are associated with carcinoma cervix.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for carcinoma cervix include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Human Papilloma virus (most important)</li>\n<li>Sexual intercourse before 18 years</li>\n<li>Multiple sexual partners</li>\n<li>Multiparity</li>\n<li>Poor personal hygiene</li>\n<li>Smoking and drug abuse</li>\n<li>Women with STD, HIV, HSV </li>\n<li>Immune suppression</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) or southern blot or Hybrid capture technique is used for HPV DNA detection.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0991","text":"Which of the following is ​not true regarding HPV?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding HPV?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E1 and E2 are viral protei​ns needed for replication​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"E6 and E7 are viral protei​ns needed for malignant t​ransformation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"E6 acts via the retinoblas​toma gene","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"HPV 16 is the most common ​serotype causing carcinom​a cervix","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. E6 acts via the retinoblastoma gene","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. E6 acts via the retinoblastoma gene</strong></p><p><strong>E6</strong> acts via the tumor suppression gene <strong>p53. </strong>It increases proteasome-dependent p53 degradation. <strong>E7</strong> binds and acts via the retinoblastoma (<strong>Rb</strong>) gene. </p>\n<p>The viral <strong>E6 and E7</strong> oncoproteins encoded by the high-risk type HPVs (such as HPV 16 and HPV 18) are necessary for <strong>malignant transformation</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>E1 and E2</strong> oncoproteins are responsible for viral <strong>replication.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4978","text":"Which is the HPV serotype ​most commonly associated ​with carcinoma cervix?","raw_text":"Which is the HPV serotype most commonly associated with carcinoma cervix?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​6","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1​8","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3​1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3​3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 16","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 16</strong></p><p><strong>HPV 16</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> type associated with carcinoma cervix.</p>\n<p>Associations between human papilloma virus (HPV) serotypes and carcinoma cervix:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most<strong> common</strong> serotype - <strong>HPV 16</strong></li>\n<li>Most<strong> malignant</strong> serotype - <strong>HPV 18</strong></li>\n<li>Most specific serotype - HPV 18</li>\n<li>Most common serotype associated with\n<ul>\n<li>Squamous cell carcinoma - HPV 16</li>\n<li>Adenocarcinoma - HPV 18</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cancers caused by HPV:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Male - penile, oral, anal</li>\n<li>Female - cervix, vagina, vulva</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5010","text":"Which of the following is ​false about the Cervarix ​vaccine?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the Cervarix vaccine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Protects against HPV types​ 16 and 18","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dose is 0.5ml, intramuscul​ar","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Protective against genital​ warts","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dosing schedule – 0, 1 and​ 6 months","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Protective against genital warts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Protective against genital warts</strong></p><p>The <strong>Cervarix</strong> vaccine is not protective against genital warts. It is mainly protective against <strong>CIN</strong> and <strong>cervical cancer</strong>.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 446px; height: 602.421px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.7812px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 73.2969px; text-align: center; height: 44.7812px; vertical-align: top;\"><strong>Vaccine Type</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.4375px; text-align: center; height: 44.7812px; vertical-align: top;\"><strong>Cervarix</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 92.1562px; text-align: center; height: 44.7812px; vertical-align: top;\"><strong>Gardasil</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.984px; text-align: center; height: 44.7812px; vertical-align: top;\"><strong>Gardasil 9</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.7812px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 73.2969px; text-align: center; height: 44.7812px; vertical-align: top;\"><strong> </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.4375px; text-align: left; height: 44.7812px; vertical-align: top;\">Bivalent vaccine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92.1562px; text-align: left; height: 44.7812px; vertical-align: top;\">Quadrivalent vaccine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.984px; text-align: left; height: 44.7812px; vertical-align: top;\">Nonavalent vaccine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 73.2969px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">HPV types </td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.4375px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">HPV 16,18</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92.1562px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">HPV 6, 11, 16, 18</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.984px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">HPV 6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52, and 58</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 73.2969px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">Dosage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.4375px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">0.5 mL </td>\n<td style=\"width: 92.1562px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">0.5 mL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.984px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">0.5 mL </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 73.2969px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">Schedule</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.4375px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">0, 1, 6 months</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92.1562px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">0, 2, 6 months</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.984px; text-align: left; vertical-align: top;\">0, 2, 6 <br/>months </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 73.2969px; vertical-align: top; text-align: left;\">Diseases prevented</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.4375px; vertical-align: top;\">CIN<br/><br/>Carcinoma cervix</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92.1562px; vertical-align: top;\">CIN<br/><br/>Carcinoma cervix<br/><br/>Genital warts</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.984px; vertical-align: top;\">CIN<br/><br/>Carcinoma<br/>cervix<br/><br/>Genital <br/>warts<br/><br/>Vulval<br/>cancer<br/><br/>Vaginal<br/>epithelial cancer<br/><br/>Oropharyngeal<br/>cancer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 73.2969px; height: 36px; vertical-align: top;\">Adjuvant</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97.4375px; height: 36px; vertical-align: top;\">AS04</td>\n<td style=\"width: 92.1562px; height: 36px; vertical-align: top;\">Alum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.984px; height: 36px; vertical-align: top;\">Alum</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9222","text":"HPV nonavalent vaccine is ​active against which of t​he following serotypes?","raw_text":"HPV nonavalent vaccine is active against which of the following serotypes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6, 11, 16, 18, 20, 23, 35,​ 42, 52","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6, 11, 16, 19, 23, 33, 45,​ 58","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6, 11, 16, 18, 32, 34, 45,​ 52, 58","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 33, 45,​ 52, 58","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52, 58","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52, 58</strong></p><p>HPV <strong>nonavalent</strong> vaccine (<strong>Gardasil 9 or HPV9</strong>) is active against <strong>HPV 6,11,16,18,31,33,45,52,58.</strong></p>\n<p>Currently, HPV nonnavalent vaccine has replaced <strong>Gardasil</strong> which is a <strong>quadrivalent</strong> vaccine acting against <strong>6,11,16,18.</strong></p>\n<p>Vaccines available for HPV: </p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 804px; height: 458px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Brand name</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Cervarix</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.758px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Gardasil</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 241.242px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Gardasil-9</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 152px; height: 18px;\"> <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">Cervavac</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 54px;\">Characteristic and HPV subtypes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px; height: 54px;\">Bivalent:<strong>16,18</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.758px; height: 54px;\">Quadrivalent: <strong>6,11,16,18</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 241.242px; height: 54px; text-align: center;\">Nonavalent: <strong>6,11,16,18,31,33,45,52,58</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 152px; height: 54px;\"> Quadrivalent: <strong>6,11,16,18</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 205px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 205px;\">Protects against:</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px; height: 205px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>CIN</li>\n<li>CA cervix</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.758px; height: 205px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anogenital warts</strong></li>\n<li>CIN</li>\n<li>CA cervix</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241.242px; height: 205px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Anogenital warts</li>\n<li>CIN</li>\n<li>CA cervix  </li>\n<li><strong>Vulval </strong>intraepithelial<strong> neoplasia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Vaginal </strong>intraepithelial<strong> neoplasia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Anal cancer</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Oropharyngeal cancer</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Head and neck cancers</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 152px; height: 205px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anogenital warts</strong></li>\n<li>CIN</li>\n<li>CA cervix</li>\n</ul>\n </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 18px;\">Dose</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px; height: 18px;\"><strong>0.5 ml</strong>, <strong>IM</strong>.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.758px; height: 18px;\"><strong>0.5 ml</strong>, <strong>IM</strong>.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241.242px; height: 18px;\"><strong>0.5 ml</strong>, <strong>IM</strong>.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 152px; height: 18px;\"> <strong>0.5 ml</strong>, <strong>IM</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 41.2557px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 41.2557px;\">Ideal Age range:</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px; height: 41.2557px;\">\n<p>Ideally at 11-12 years or</p>\n<p>9-26 years </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.758px; height: 41.2557px;\">\n<p>Ideally at 11-12 years or</p>\n<p>9-26 years </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 241.242px; height: 41.2557px;\">\n<p>Ideally at 11-12 years or</p>\n<p>9-26 years </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 152px; height: 41.2557px;\">\n<p>The targeted age group is 9-26 years</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6623","text":"A 45-year-old patient come​s with repeated infection​s of trichomonas vaginiti​s, her physician advises ‌for a Pap smear. She asks​ you regarding the purpos‍e of this test. What woul‌d be your appropriate res‍ponse?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old patient comes with repeated infections of trichomonas vaginitis, her physician advises for a Pap smear. She asks you regarding the purpose of this test. What would be your appropriate response?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a screening test for​ cervical cancer","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is a diagnostic test fo​r trichomonas infection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It helps to diagnose cance​r in her genital tract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It helps to diagnose other​ associated genital infec​tions in her","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is a screening test for cervical cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/af717526ba7c45ce810e6bbe8f7409bex1280x4631.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/61db0cacf41240c59c87765886aff277x1280x2262.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is a screening test for cervical cancer</strong></p><p>A pap smear is a <strong>screening test</strong> for cervical <strong>cancer</strong>. It is not a diagnostic test (option C). The presence of abnormal cells/ a positive test requires further evaluation by colposcopy, cervical biopsy, or fractional curettage. </p>\n<p>If trichomonas is reported from Pap testing, it should be confirmed by saline microscopic preparation (option B). </p>\n<p>Cervical cancer<strong> screening</strong> should start at <strong>age 21</strong>. Women aged<strong> 21-29</strong> should have a<strong> Pap smear every 3 years</strong>, while women aged<strong> 30-65</strong> should have both <strong>HPV and Pap co-test</strong> every <strong>5 years</strong>. Women should stop having cervical cancer screening after the age of <strong>65 years</strong>. If suspicious PAP, screening till 75 years. HIV+ women should have annual checkups until three consecutive negative tests followed by testing every 3 years for life.</p>\n<p>Pap testing should be done <strong>prior</strong> to <strong>vaginal examination. </strong>Lubricants should not be used and the patient should not be menstruating during the pap test. The patient should not have intercourse 24hrs prior to the test. The ideal <strong>time</strong> is around <strong>ovulation</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The patient is put in a <strong>dorsal</strong> position, and the labia are parted</li>\n<li><strong>Cusco’s</strong> self-retaining <strong>speculum</strong> is introduced</li>\n<li>The <strong>squamocolumnar junction</strong> is scraped with <strong>Ayer’s</strong> <strong>spatula</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Bifid end - ectocervical sample</li>\n<li>Blunt end - vaginal cytology</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Endocervix</strong> is scraped with a <strong>cytobrush</strong></li>\n<li>The sample is spread on <strong>a</strong> <strong>glass slide</strong></li>\n<li>The slide is immediately fixed using <strong>95%</strong> <strong>ethyl</strong> <strong>alcohol</strong></li>\n<li>Stained with Papanicolau stain or short-duration<strong> </strong>Papanicolau stain (SPS).</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0630","text":"A 24-year-old woman comes ​to the gynecology OPD for​ a routine Pap smear. The​ histopathology slide is ‌shown below. Identify the​ cell marked as \"A\".","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman comes to the gynecology OPD for a routine Pap smear. The histopathology slide is shown below. Identify the cell marked as \"A\".","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superfic​ial cell","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Basal​ cell","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parabas​al cell","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intermed​iate cell","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Superficial cell","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/772c5f242b844d6c8c84c43a83213b77.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1feb770911f24d8a9a92b99b27b10988x504x273.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/75f4419b83944bfca4ed48d10a6b0594x800x533.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aad3c0e4647d47399d6f0a31bc833497x720x537.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Superficial cell</strong></p><p>The cell marked A is the <strong>superficial cell. </strong></p>\n<p>ACOG guidelines recommend cervical exfoliative cytology every 3 years starting 21 years as screening for cervical cancer.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a <strong>superficial cell</strong> (a) and an <strong>intermediate cell</strong> (b):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><br/>The image below shows <strong>parabasal cells</strong> (marked by black arrows):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Koilocytes</strong> are epithelial cells that contain eccentric, hyperchromatic, moderately <strong>enlarged nucleus</strong> that is displaced by large <strong>perinuclear vacuole</strong>. It is a feature of <strong>HPV infection</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0639","text":"Match the following: Pap c​lassification Cervical in​traepithelial neoplasia g​rading A. Grade I 1. Infl‌ammatory B. Grade II 2. N​ormal C. Grade III 3. Squ‍amous cell carcinoma D. G‌rade IV 4. CIN I E. Grade‍ V 5. CIN II, CIN III, CI​S","raw_text":"<p>Match the following:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 485px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 58px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 242px;\"><strong>Pap classification </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 63.7656px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.234px;\"><strong>Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia grading</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 58px;\">A.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 242px;\">Grade I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 63.7656px;\">1.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.234px;\">Inflammatory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 58px;\">B.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 242px;\">Grade II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 63.7656px;\">2. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.234px;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 58px;\">C. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 242px;\">Grade III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 63.7656px;\">3.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.234px;\">Squamous cell carcinoma </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 58px;\">D. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 242px;\">Grade IV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 63.7656px;\">4. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.234px;\">CIN I</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 58px;\">E.  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 242px;\">Grade V</td>\n<td style=\"width: 63.7656px;\">5. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 232.234px;\">CIN II, CIN III, CIS</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A-2, B-1, C​-4, D-5, E-3","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A-2, B-1, C​-4, D-3, E-5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A-1, B-2, C​-4, D-5, E-3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A-2, B-1, C​-3, D-5, E-4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A-2, B-1, C-4, D-5, E-3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A-2, B-1, C-4, D-5, E-3</strong></p><p>Various systems of classification are used to describe <strong>cervical cytology after Pap smear</strong> such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Papanicolaou classification - describes the exfoliative cytology of the cervix in a Pap smear.</li>\n<li>Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN) - histopathological description of how much of the thickness of the cervical epithelium is replaced by dysplastic cells </li>\n<li>Bethesda classification - it is another method to describe cervical dysplasia </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The various systems and their corresponding classes are given below:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 455px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\"><strong>Pap classification (1943)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\"><strong>Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia / CIN  (WHO 1975)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\"><strong>Bethesda system</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">Grade I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">Grade II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Inflammatory</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">\n<p>Inflammatory</p>\n<p>HPV</p>\n<p>ASCUS</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">Grade III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">CIN I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">\n<p>Low SIL</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">Grade IV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">CIN II, CIN III, CIS</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">\n<p>High SIL</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">Grade V</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">SCC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">\n<p>SCC</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>where, CIS: Carcinoma in situ, CIN: Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia, SCC: Squamous cell carcinoma, SIL: Squamous intraepithelial lesion, ASCUS: Atypical squamous cell of undetermined significance, HPV: Human papillomavirus.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1000","text":"A 45-year-old lady complai​ns of post-coital bleedin​g. She has a positive pap​ smear. What is the next ‌line of management?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old lady complains of post-coital bleeding. She has a positive pap smear. What is the next line of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colposcopy directed biopsy​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cone ​biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Repeat p​ap smear","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyster​ectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Colposcopy directed biopsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Colposcopy directed biopsy</strong></p><p>The appropriate management of a 45-year-old with a <strong>positive pap</strong> smear and post-coital bleeding is a <strong>colposcopy-directed biopsy.</strong></p>\n<p>A <strong>per speculum examination </strong>must be done whenever a female comes with<strong> post-coital bleeding</strong> in order to evaluate for carcinoma of the cervix. If growth is visible, a punch biopsy should be done, and else if no growth is visible, then a pap smear is done. A pap smear is a <strong>screening test</strong> for cervical <strong>cancer</strong>. The presence of abnormal cells/ a positive test requires further evaluation by <strong>colposcopy,</strong> cervical biopsy, or fractional curettage.</p>\n<p>Cervical cancer<strong> screening</strong> should start at <strong>age 21</strong>. Women aged<strong> 21-29</strong> should have a<strong> pap smear every 3 years</strong>, while women aged<strong> 30-65</strong> should have both <strong>HPV and pap co-test</strong> every <strong>5 years</strong>. Women should stop having cervical cancer screening after the age of <strong>65 years</strong>. If a pap smear is suspicious, then screening is continued till 75 years. HIV+ve women should have annual checkups until three consecutive negative tests followed by testing every 3 years for life.</p>\n<p>Pap testing should be done <strong>prior</strong> to <strong>vaginal examination. </strong>Lubricants should not be used and the patient should not be menstruating during the pap test. The patient should not have intercourse 24hrs prior to the test. The ideal <strong>time</strong> is around <strong>ovulation.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0631","text":"A 30-year-old sex worker p​resented with irregular m​enses, post-coital bleedi​ng, and occasional foul-s‌melling vaginal discharge​. She was advised to unde‍rgo a Pap smear. What is ‌the best time for this in‍vestigation?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old sex worker presented with irregular menses, post-coital bleeding, and occasional foul-smelling vaginal discharge. She was advised to undergo a Pap smear. What is the best time for this investigation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immediately after menstrua​tion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Around o​vulation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Secreto​ry phase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"During me​nstruation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Around ovulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/89f54f6da4d54525afb281b593a33a63x600x2250.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Around ovulation</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>cervical</strong> <strong>carcinoma</strong><strong>.</strong> The best time to do a Pap smear is <strong>around ovulation</strong>, but it can be performed at any other time as well.</p>\n<p>Prerequisites of doing a Pap smear:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It should be done <strong>prior</strong> to <strong>vaginal examination</strong></li>\n<li>Lubricants should not be used</li>\n<li>The patient should not be menstruating during the pap test</li>\n<li>The ideal <strong>time</strong> is around <strong>ovulation</strong></li>\n<li>The patient should not have intercourse 24hrs prior to the test.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Based on Pap smear findings, dysplasia of cervical cells is graded using various classification systems such as WHO grading, CIN grading, and the Bethesda system.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0628","text":"Which of the following is ​false about Schiller's te​st?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about Schiller's test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lugol's iodine is used to ​paint the cervix","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The epithelium is stained ​mahogany brown","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abnormal vaginal epitheliu​m is stained","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It detects the presence of​ glycogen in the epitheli​um","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Abnormal vaginal epithelium is stained","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Abnormal vaginal epithelium is stained</strong></p><p>The <strong>abnormal</strong> cells remain <strong>unstained</strong> on performing Schiller's test.</p>\n<p>Schiller's test, also known as VILI (visual inspection with Lugol's iodine), involves painting the cervix with <strong>Lugol's iodine. </strong>It is used to detect any abnormal cytology in the cervix and vagina, such as in cases of carcinoma cervix. </p>\n<p>The <strong>normal</strong> vaginal and cervical <strong>epithelium</strong> contains <strong>glycogen,</strong> therefore stains <strong>mahogany brown</strong> on being painted with Lugol's iodine. The<strong> abnormal cells</strong> remain <strong>unstained.</strong> These areas need to be <strong>biopsied</strong> and sent for histopathological examination. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4981","text":"Which of the following wou​ld you not expect to see ​in the colposcopic biopsy​ of a patient with cervic‌al intraepithelial neopla​sia?","raw_text":"Which of the following would you not expect to see in the colposcopic biopsy of a patient with cervical intraepithelial neoplasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dysp​lasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Breach in basement membran​e","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Altered ​N:C ratio","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mitotic​ figures","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Breach in basement membrane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Breach in basement membrane</strong></p><p>The <strong>basement membrane</strong> is<strong> intact</strong> in cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN).</p>\n<p><strong>Features of CIN:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Varying degree of dysplasia</li>\n<li>Altered nuclear-cytoplasmic (N:C) ratio</li>\n<li>Hyperchromatic nuclei</li>\n<li>Mitotic figures</li>\n<li>Intact basement membrane.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5003","text":"Which of the following met​hods is most commonly use​d for the treatment of CI​N?","raw_text":"Which of the following methods is most commonly used for the treatment of CIN?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cold-knife​ conization","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Large loop excision of tra​nsformation zone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Electroco​agulation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Needle excision of transfo​rmation zone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Large loop excision of transformation zone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Large loop excision of transformation zone</strong></p><p>Large loop excision of the transformation zone (<strong>LLETZ</strong>) is the most commonly used treatment option for cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN). It is also known as loop electrosurgical excision procedure (<strong>LEEP</strong>).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 506\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It is applicable for the <strong>treatment of CIN</strong> anywhere in the lower genital tract. This uses electric current through a monopolar wire electrode to either cut or coagulate cervical lesions. Like other excisional procedures, it is done in the immediate post menstrual phase.</p>\n<p>The procedure involves <strong>minimal patient discomfort</strong>, cost, and complications. Since it can be performed using local anesthesia, it has become the primary outpatient treatment modality for high-grade cervical lesions.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5008","text":"Which among the following ​is the treatment of choic​e when microinvasion of c​ervix neoplasia is suspec‌ted?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the treatment of choice when microinvasion of cervix neoplasia is suspected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"LL​TEZ","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"LE​EP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coniz​ation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hyster​ectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Conization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Conization</strong></p><p><strong>Conization</strong> is the treatment of choice when microinvasion is suspected<strong>.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Indications of conization\n<ul>\n<li>Endocervical dysplasia</li>\n<li>Transformation zone is not completely visualized</li>\n<li>When there is discrepancy of findings between cytolology, colposcopy &amp; biopsy</li>\n<li>Microinvasion is suspected</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Indications of hysterectomy\n<ul>\n<li>Older &amp; parous women</li>\n<li>Uterus is associated with fibroid, DUB, prolapsed</li>\n<li>Recurrence or persistent lesion</li>\n<li>In-situ adenocarcinoma cervix</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7104","text":"A 40-year-old woman has ce​rvical intraepithelial ne​oplasia III. What would b​e the management?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman has cervical intraepithelial neoplasia III. What would be the management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyster​ectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trache​lectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coniz​ation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Colposcopy​ with LEEP","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Colposcopy with LEEP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Colposcopy with LEEP</strong></p><p><strong>Colposcopy with LEEP</strong> would be appropriate management for <strong>CIN III</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Loop electrosurgical excision procedure (LEEP) </strong>is an <strong>opd procedure</strong> done under <strong>colposcopic view</strong> and local anesthesia.Here, a low voltage current is passed through the loop and a cone of tissue is removed from the cervix. This procedure causes <strong>minimal bleeding</strong> and the tissue removed is sent for histopathological examination (HPE). This is the <strong>treatment of choice</strong> for <strong>CIN 2&amp;3</strong> of <strong>any age and parity</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4975","text":"Which is the most common m​alignancy in women in Ind​ia?","raw_text":"Which is the most common malignancy in women in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cervica​l cancer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Breast​ cancer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Endometri​al cancer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ovarian​ cancer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Breast cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Breast cancer</strong></p><p><strong>Breast cancer</strong> is the most common cancer in women in India as well as globally. <strong>Cervical cancer</strong> is the most common <strong>genital</strong> cancer in India.</p>\n<p>Lip and oral cavity cancer is the most common malignancy in men.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for carcinoma cervix include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Human Papilloma virus (most important)</li>\n<li>Sexual intercourse before 18 years</li>\n<li>Multiple sexual partners</li>\n<li>Multiparity</li>\n<li>Poor personal hygiene</li>\n<li>Smoking and drug abuse</li>\n<li>Women with STD, HIV, HSV </li>\n<li>Immune suppression</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0972","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding the trans​itional zone?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding the transitional zone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CIN originates from this s​ite","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is a dy​namic point","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nabothian cysts mark the o​riginal site","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is limited to external ​os","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is limited to external os","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is limited to external os</strong></p><p>The <strong>transitional zone</strong> or <strong>squamocolumnar junction</strong> is well outside the external os during the reproductive period. </p>\n<p>It is the site where the <strong>squamous</strong> epithelium lining the <strong>vagina</strong> merges with the <strong>columnar</strong> epithelium of the <strong>endocervix</strong>. The original squamocolumnar junction is marked by the presence of Nabothian cysts and glandular openings.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>dynamic</strong> part and moves with age and hormonal influence:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 19\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>During pregnancy and with oral contraceptives, it pouts out of os.</li>\n<li>Following menopause, it gets indrawn inside the os.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The <strong>cells</strong> here are <strong>highly sensitive</strong> to irritants, mutagens and viral agents such as HPV. Cancer screening using <strong>Pap smear</strong> involves scraping this area and studying the cells for nuclear changes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0973","text":"Identify the false stateme​nt regarding adenocarcino​ma of the cervix.","raw_text":"Identify the false statement regarding adenocarcinoma of the cervix.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most common site is transf​ormation zone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cervical cancer is the sec​ond most common cancer am​ong women in India","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most common ​in OCP users","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Associated ​with HPV 18","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Most common site is transformation zone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Most common site is transformation zone</strong></p><p>The most common site of adenocarcinoma is <strong>endocervix</strong>.</p>\n<p>It commonly occurs in young females and OCP users, and is associated with HPV 18.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1027","text":"You would suspect and eval​uate for carcinoma cervix​ in patients presenting w​ith which of the followin‌g conditions?","raw_text":"You would suspect and evaluate for carcinoma cervix in patients presenting with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Menor​rhagia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metror​rhagia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Polyme​norrhea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ameno​rrhea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Metrorrhagia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Metrorrhagia</strong></p><p>Patients with carcinoma cervix most <strong>commonly</strong> present with <strong>metrorrhagia,</strong> now known as <strong>intermenstrual bleeding</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Post-coital bleeding</strong> is the most <strong>specific</strong> symptom of carcinoma cervix.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1028","text":"What is the most common si​te of hematogenous metast​asis of cervical cancer?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of hematogenous metastasis of cervical cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lu​ng","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Li​ver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bo​ne","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bo​wel","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lung","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lung</strong></p><p>The most common site of <strong>hematogenous metastasis</strong> of cervical cancer is the <strong>lung.</strong></p>\n<p>About <strong>1-2%</strong> of patients with cervical carcinomas <strong>present</strong> with lung metastases, and 5-35% eventually develop lung metastases. </p>\n<p>Other common sites of blood-borne metastases include bone, liver and bowel.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1030","text":"A 65-year-old woman comes ​for follow-up a few month​s after surgery for carci​noma cervix. On evaluatio‌n, she is found to have a​ recurrence of the malign‍ancy. Which of the follow‌ing is true about her con‍dition?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old woman comes for follow-up a few months after surgery for carcinoma cervix. On evaluation, she is found to have a recurrence of the malignancy. Which of the following is true about her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most common site of recurr​ence is pelvic lymph node​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mortality in patients with​ recurrence is due to pul​monary embolism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Treatment involves surgery​ with lymph node dissecti​on","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Recurrent tumor with large​st diameter &amp;lt;3cm has g​ood prognosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Recurrent tumor with largest diameter <3cm has good prognosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Recurrent tumor with largest diameter &lt;3cm has good prognosis</strong></p><p>A recurrent tumor with the largest diameter of <strong>&lt;3cm</strong> is associated with a <strong>good prognosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>The extent of recurrent disease and involvement of pelvic lymph nodes are prognostic factors for survival. An isolated central pelvic tumour with no involvement of the pelvic sidewall also has a good prognosis. </p>\n<p>Most <strong>recurrences</strong> are seen <strong>within 3 years</strong> and the prognosis is poor with <strong>uremia</strong> being the most common <strong>terminal event</strong>. Recurrences most <strong>commonly involve</strong> the <strong>pelvis </strong>than<strong> </strong>the para-aortic or pelvic lymph node.</p>\n<p>All patients with a suspected recurrence should undergo <strong>PET/CT</strong> for <strong>evaluation</strong> and <strong>biopsy</strong> for <strong>confirmation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Treatment involves either <strong>radical chemoradiation or pelvic exenteration</strong>. Concurrent chemotherapy with cisplatin and/or 5-fluorouracil may improve the outcome.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4989","text":"Identify the true statemen​t about the staging of ca​ncer cervix.","raw_text":"Identify the true statement about the staging of cancer cervix.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clinical examination is th​e first step in staging","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tumor markers are importan​t for diagnosis of metast​asis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CT scan is the best method​ to assess principal tumo​rs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MRI is the best method to ​assess nodal metastasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Clinical examination is the first step in staging","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Clinical examination is the first step in staging</strong></p><p><strong>Clinical examination</strong> is the<strong> first</strong> <strong>step</strong> in staging carcinoma cervix.</p>\n<p>Clinical examination was the principal method in staging until the 2018 FIGO guidelines which recommended the use of <strong>imaging and histological investigations </strong>when available. USG, CT, MRI, and PET-CT can be used to assess the extent of the tumor, nodal status, and local or systemic spread.</p>\n<p><strong>MRI</strong> is the best method to assess the <strong>principal tumor</strong> while <strong>PET-CT</strong> is the best method to assess <strong>nodal metastasis.</strong></p>\n<p>FIGO no longer mandates biochemical investigations, such as tumor markers, for diagnosis of metastasis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5001","text":"The CT of a patient with c​ervical carcinoma reveale​d a lesion of 6 cm, exten​ding to the upper part of‌ the vagina and parametri​um. What is the stage of ‍the disease?","raw_text":"The CT of a patient with cervical carcinoma revealed a lesion of 6 cm, extending to the upper part of the vagina and parametrium. What is the stage of the disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stage​ II A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stage​ II B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stage​ III A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stage​ III B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stage II B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stage II B</strong></p><p>The stage of cervical carcinoma is <strong>II B</strong> as it involves the <strong>upper</strong> part of the <strong>vagina</strong> and <strong>parametrium.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5013","text":"A 50-year-old woman presen​ted to her gynecologist w​ith postcoital bleeding. ​She was diagnosed with ca‌rcinoma cervix stage IB a​fter evaluation. What is ‍the treatment of choice f‌or this patient?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman presented to her gynecologist with postcoital bleeding. She was diagnosed with carcinoma cervix stage IB after evaluation. What is the treatment of choice for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type I hy​sterectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type II hy​sterectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type III h​ysterectomy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Type IV hy​sterectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type III hysterectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type III hysterectomy</strong></p><p><strong>Type III hysterectomy</strong> is the treatment of choice in carcinoma cervix - <strong>stage IB</strong>. It involves radical hysterectomy with pelvic lymphadenectomy and para-aortic lymph node evaluation.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 697\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Stage IB is defined as a macroscopic lesion still confined to the cervix, with the depth of invasion ≥5mm. It is the first stage in which lesions become clinically visible.</p>\n<p>Hysterectomy types include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type I - Simple / extrafascial</li>\n<li>Type II - Modified radical / Wertheim</li>\n<li>Type III - Radical / Meigs-Wertheim</li>\n<li>Type IV - Extended radical Hysterectomy</li>\n<li>Type V - Exenteration.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1038","text":"A 30-year-old woman is fou​nd to have a lesion in th​e cervix which is 7mm wid​e and has a depth of 4mm.‌ She has one child and wi​shes to preserve fertilit‍y. What is the most appro‌priate management?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman is found to have a lesion in the cervix which is 7mm wide and has a depth of 4mm. She has one child and wishes to preserve fertility. What is the most appropriate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cryoab​lation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cone ​biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Radical trachelectomy with​ pelvic lymphadenectomy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"LE​EP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Radical trachelectomy with pelvic lymphadenectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Radical trachelectomy with pelvic lymphadenectomy</strong></p><p>The woman has <strong>stage I A2</strong> cervical cancer and the most appropriate management is <strong>radical trachelectomy with pelvic lymphadenectomy.</strong></p>\n<p>Radical trachelectomy is a fertility-preserving surgical procedure. This involves the removal of the entire cervix and parametrium.</p>\n<p><strong>Cervical conization </strong>with laparoscopic or extraperitoneal <strong>pelvic lymphadenectomy</strong> is an alternative that also preserves fertility.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5021","text":"Which among the following ​are contraindications for​ radiotherapy in carcinom​a cervix? Pelvic inflamma‌tory disease Vaginal sten​osis Post-menopausal stat‍us Adenocarcinoma Associa‌ted prolapse","raw_text":"<p>Which among the following are contraindications for radiotherapy in carcinoma cervix?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Pelvic inflammatory disease</li>\n<li>Vaginal stenosis</li>\n<li>Post-menopausal status</li>\n<li>Adenocarcinoma</li>\n<li>Associated prolapse</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ​2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, ​4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2,​ 3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2,​ 4, 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 4, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 4, 5</strong></p><p>Post-menopausal status is not a contraindication for radiotherapy in carcinoma cervix.</p>\n<p><strong>Contraindications</strong> for <strong>radiotherapy in carcinoma cervix</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pelvic inflammatory disease</li>\n<li>Young patient (to preserve ovarian function)</li>\n<li>Associated myoma, prolapse, ovarian tumor, genital fistula</li>\n<li>Vaginal stenosis</li>\n<li>Adenocarcinoma or adenosquamous carcinoma – surgery preferred.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1031","text":"You are planning to admini​ster radiotherapy to a 58​-year-old woman diagnosed​ with carcinoma cervix. W‌hat is the dose to be adm​inistered at point B?","raw_text":"You are planning to administer radiotherapy to a 58-year-old woman diagnosed with carcinoma cervix. What is the dose to be administered at point B?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7000​ cGY","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6000​ cGY","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"5000​ cGy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"8000​ cGy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 6000 cGY","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e3ce586ef69f426cac36b26d45f3259ex1280x1211.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 6000 cGY</strong></p><p>In the treatment of carcinoma cervix, the dose to be administered at point B is <strong>6000 cGy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Radiotherapy in carcinoma cervix:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 182px;\" width=\"577\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 74.109375px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 240.453125px;\"><strong>Point A</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 240.453125px;\"><strong>Point B</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 74.109375px;\"><strong>Location</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 240.453125px;\">2cm above and 2cm lateral to external os</td>\n<td style=\"width: 240.453125px;\">2cm above and 5cm lateral to external os</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 74.109375px;\"><strong>Structure</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 240.453125px;\">Paracervical/Parametrial lymph node</td>\n<td style=\"width: 240.453125px;\">Obturator lymph node</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 74.109375px;\"><strong>Dose</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 240.453125px;\">7000-8000 cGy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 240.453125px;\">6000 cGy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"fIrmpT7G","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"45_renal_tumors","title":"45.Renal Tumors","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB5393","text":"An 18-month-old child is b‌rought to OPD with compla‍ints of blood in his urin​e. Examination reveals a ‌non-tender mass in the le​ft flank that does not cr‍oss the midline. Blood pr‌essure is also found to b‍e elevated. Imaging revea‌ls a left renal tumor and‌ surgical resection is pl‍anned. The gross appearan‍ce of the mass is shown b‌elow. What is the likely ‍diagnosis?","raw_text":"An 18-month-old child is brought to OPD with complaints of blood in his urine. Examination reveals a non-tender mass in the left flank that does not cross the midline. Blood pressure is also found to be elevated. Imaging reveals a left renal tumor and surgical resection is planned. The gross appearance of the mass is shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal cell‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nephrob‌lastoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Neurob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Angiomy‌olipoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nephroblastoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8db9e1a24817408f8fc6b5f83b3025ee.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nephroblastoma</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and image showing a well-circumscribed tumor with multiple <strong>necrotic</strong>, <strong>hemorrhagic</strong>, and <strong>cystic</strong> changes in the <strong>upper pole</strong> of the kidney are suggestive of a <strong>nephroblastoma </strong>or <strong>Wilms' tumor</strong>. </p>\n<p>It<strong> </strong>is the <strong>most common</strong> primary renal tumor of childhood (<strong>2-5 years</strong> of age). Around 10% of cases are associated with genetic syndromes and <strong>gonadal dysgenesis</strong> may be seen. </p>\n<p>Clinical manifestations of Wilms' tumor include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Large abdominal mass - </strong>usually unilateral and does not cross the midline</li>\n<li>Abdominal pain</li>\n<li>Hematuria</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Intestinal obstruction (in some cases) </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Nephrogenic rests</strong> are the precursor lesions of Wilms' tumor and suggest an increased <strong>risk</strong> of developing a <strong>contralateral</strong> Wilms' tumor. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5400","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‍ Wilms tumor?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding Wilms tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is inherited as an auto‌somal recessive disorder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is associated with MIC-‌2 gene expression","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is associated with the ‌deletion of chromosome 11‍p13","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The most common presentati‌on is hematuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is associated with the deletion of chromosome 11p13","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is associated with the deletion of chromosome 11p13</strong></p><p>Wilms' tumor is associated with the <strong>deletion </strong>of<strong> chromosome 11p13</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>autosomal-dominant </strong>disorder. The most common clinical presentation is an <strong>abdominal mass</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>MIC-2</strong> gene expression is seen in <strong>neuroblastoma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5399","text":"In patients with Wilms tum‌ors, which of the followi‍ng microscopic features a​re indicative of resistan‌ce to chemotherapy?","raw_text":"In patients with  Wilms tumors, which of the following microscopic features are indicative of resistance to chemotherapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presence of nephrogenic ce‌ll rests","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Presence of monophasic mor‌phology","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Presence o‌f anaplasia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Presence of capsular infil‌tration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Presence of anaplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Presence of anaplasia</strong></p><p>In Wilms' tumor, the presence of <strong>anaplasia</strong> is indicative of <strong>resistance to chemotherapy</strong><strong>.</strong> </p>\n<p>This correlates with the presence of <strong>TP53 </strong>mutations and, hence, is associated with a <strong>poor prognosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5398","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most important progno‍stic factor in Wilms' tum​or?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most important prognostic factor in Wilms' tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anaplastic‌ histology","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tumor‌ size","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Age of th‌e patient","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chromosome ‌11q deletion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anaplastic histology","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anaplastic histology</strong></p><p><strong>Anaplastic histology</strong> is the <strong>most important </strong>prognostic factor in Wilms' tumor.</p>\n<p>It is seen in about 5% of cases and is characterized by the presence of cells with large, <strong>hyperchromatic,</strong> <strong>pleomorphic</strong> nuclei, and <strong>abnormal mitoses. </strong>It is associated with <strong>TP53 </strong>mutations and <strong>resistance</strong> to<strong> chemotherapy.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 489\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Anaplasia is <strong>uncommon</strong> in children <strong>&lt; 2 years</strong>. Hence, tumors presenting at an <strong>young age</strong> have a significantly <strong>better prognosis</strong> than those presenting in older children.</p>\n<p>Prognostic factors in Wilms' tumor include:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<table style=\"height: 270px; width: 348px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\"><strong>Favorable prognosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\"><strong>Adverse prognosis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">Histologic type</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">Classic triphasic component</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">Anaplastic histology</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">Tumor stage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">High</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">Molecular alterations</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px; text-align: center;\"> ----</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">\n<p>11q deletion</p>\n<p>16q deletion</p>\n<p>Gain of chromosome 1q</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">Age of onset</td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">&lt;2 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124px;\">&gt;2 years</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5397","text":"Which of the following is ‌not associated with Wilms‍ tumor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not associated with Wilms tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemihyp‌ertrophy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anir‌idia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ompha‌locele","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bilateral polycystic kidne‌ys","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bilateral polycystic kidneys","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bilateral polycystic kidneys</strong></p><p>Bilateral polycystic kidneys are not associated with <strong>Wilms tumor.</strong></p>\n<p>Wilms' tumor can be associated with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aniridia </strong>- WAGR syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>Gonadal dysgenesis </strong>- Denys–Drash syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>Hemihypertrophy</strong> and <strong>omphalocele</strong><strong> </strong>- Beckwith–Wiedemann syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5416","text":"A 16-year-old girl is bein‌g evaluated for a history‍ of recurrent seizures an​d abdominal discomfort. E‌xamination reveals the fo​llowing nail findings. Wh‍ich of the following shou‌ld she be screened for?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old girl is being evaluated for a history of recurrent seizures and abdominal discomfort. Examination reveals the following nail findings. Which of the following should she be screened for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal cell‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Angiomy‌olipoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Oncoc‌ytoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wilms ‌tumour","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Angiomyolipoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/35d42e9d811c4f509ee12b1fbf37b2f6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cead8d09dec44ded937080a15acf76bdx1280x1149.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/36e6cebf61b74ae8aae4fbcddc0cab12x1280x1150.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Angiomyolipoma</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and image showing <strong>periungual fibromas</strong> are strongly suggestive of <strong>tuberous</strong> <strong>sclerosis</strong>. This condition is associated with <strong>renal</strong> <strong>angiomyolipomas</strong> (present in 25–50% of cases).</p>\n<p>Tuberous sclerosis is a multisystem disorder that has an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> mode of inheritance. The mutations are involved in the <strong>TSC1 </strong>gene that encodes <strong>hamartin</strong> on <strong>chromosome 9</strong> and the <strong>TSC2 gene </strong>that encodes a protein <strong>tuberin</strong> on <strong>chromosome 16.</strong></p>\n<p>The clinical features of tuberous sclerosis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Skin lesions -\n<ul>\n<li>Hypomelanotic ash leaf–type macules </li>\n<li><strong>Periungual fibromas</strong> or <strong>Koenen tumors </strong>(small fibromas or nodules of skin around fingernails or toenails)</li>\n<li>Shagreen patch- roughened, raised lesion with an orange-peel consistency, primarily in the lumbosacral region</li>\n<li>Facial angiofibromas</li>\n<li>Confetti skin lesions </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Neurological features -\n<ul>\n<li>Epilepsy and intellectual disability (due to cortical tubers)</li>\n<li>Autism spectrum disorder</li>\n<li>Subependymal giant cell astrocytomas</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Other tumors -\n<ul>\n<li>Cardiac <strong>rhabdomyoma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Renal angiomyolipomas</strong>, renal cysts</li>\n<li>Pulmonary lymphangioleiomyomatosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Angiomyolipoma </strong>is a benign renal neoplasm (hamartoma) originating from<strong> perivascular epithelioid </strong>cells (PEComa). It consists of vessels, smooth muscle, and fat in variable proportions. They are highly susceptibility to <strong>spontaneous hemorrhage</strong>.</p>\n<p>The histological image of renal angiomyolipoma is given below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5424","text":"A 50-year-old diabetic pat‌ient is being evaluated f‍or chronic abdominal disc​omfort. CECT of the abdom‌en and pelvis shows a het​erogeneously enhancing re‍nal mass with a central s‌tellate scar. On resectio‍n, the tumor appears maho‌gany brown. What is the s‌ite of origin of this tum‍or?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old diabetic patient is being evaluated for chronic abdominal discomfort. CECT of the abdomen and pelvis shows a heterogeneously enhancing renal mass with a central stellate scar. On resection, the tumor appears mahogany brown. What is the site of origin of this tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proxima‌l tubule","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Distal‌ tubule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Loop o‌f henle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Collect‌ing duct","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Collecting duct","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/31a20d927a77489f9bd24e7fdaadc268x451x598.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/178928c7d122467cb278a61e3904878fx1280x1040.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a77121e24d754defb67febdb49b1ec9ex1280x1041.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Collecting duct</strong></p><p>The given clinical history is suggestive of <strong>r</strong><strong>enal oncocytoma</strong>. It arises from the intercalated cells of the<strong> collecting ducts. </strong></p>\n<p>It is a <strong>benign</strong> epithelial neoplasm. Grossly, these tumors appear<strong> tan </strong>or<strong> mahogany brown </strong>and relatively homogeneous. They are usually <strong>well encapsulated</strong> with a<strong> central scar</strong> in one-third of cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Microscopically</strong>, they are composed of large <strong>eosinophilic cells</strong> having small, round, benign-appearing nuclei that have large nucleoli. Ultrastructurally, these eosinophilic cells have <strong>numerous mitochondria.</strong></p>\n<p>The gross and histological appearances of renal oncocytoma are shown in the images given below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>O</strong><strong>ncocytes </strong>refer to epithelial cells with abundant eosinophilic granular cytoplasm (due to the accumulation of mitochondria). They can arise in various tissues and are referred to as: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hurthle </strong>cells<strong>/Ashkenazy</strong> cells in the thyroid gland</li>\n<li><strong>Oxyphilic </strong>cells<strong> </strong>in the parotid gland</li>\n<li><strong>Apocrine metaplasia</strong> in the breast </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3020","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most important determ‍inant of malignant potent​ial in the papillary type‌ of renal tumor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most important determinant of malignant potential in the papillary type of renal tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immnunoh‌istology","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Si‌ze","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cytoge‌netics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Part of kid‌ney involved","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Size","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0fce528532004b0b85809c84af965914x1280x1238.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Size</strong></p><p>The most important determinant of <strong>malignant</strong> <strong>potential</strong> in the <strong>papillary</strong> <strong>type</strong> of renal tumor is the <strong>size</strong> of the tumor.</p>\n<p>The papillary type of renal tumors includes: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Renal papillary <strong>adenoma</strong> (<strong>≤ 1.5 cm</strong>) </li>\n<li>Papillary type of renal cell<strong> carcinoma</strong> (RCC) -<strong> &gt; 1.5 cm</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Small renal papillary growths are fairly common and incidentally found. Ruling out of malignancy is one of the processes carried out during the evaluation of donor transplant kidneys.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Papillary </strong>renal cell carcinoma (<strong>RCC</strong>) is the second most common type of renal cell carcinoma and it arises from <strong>proximal tubules.</strong> It can be <strong>multifocal</strong> or <strong>bilateral</strong>. It is typically hemorrhagic and cystic, especially when large.</p>\n<p>It is often a circumscribed tumor composed of <strong>papillae</strong> formed by delicate fibrovascular cores that often contain <strong>foamy macrophages</strong> and <strong>Psammoma bodies</strong>.</p>\n<p>It has two histological types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type 1 papillary RCC - single layer of cells on the basement membrane and <strong>low-grade</strong> nuclear features</li>\n<li>Type 2 papillary RCC - <strong>pseudostratification</strong> of cells and usually has <strong>high-grade</strong> nuclear features</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5420","text":"The following image shows ‌a nephrectomy specimen of‍ a patient diagnosed with​ renal cell carcinoma. Wh‌at is the likely histopat​hological finding in this‍ tumor?","raw_text":"The following image shows a nephrectomy specimen of a patient diagnosed with renal cell carcinoma. What is the likely histopathological finding in this tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clear‌ cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Signet‌ cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Small cells with high N/C ‌ratio","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malignant sq‌uamous cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Clear cells","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/053dd672e2d54d32abc7a56f5ae675c3.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Clear cells</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>bright yellow variegated mass</strong> distorting the renal outline at the lower pole. This is suggestive of the <strong>clear cell</strong> type of renal cell carcinoma (<strong>RCC</strong>). The yellow color is due to prominent lipid accumulation.</p>\n<p>Gross findings of various types of renal tumors:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 493px; width: 668px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 82px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 36px;\">\n<p><strong>Clear-cell RCC</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Papillary RCC</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 97px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Chromophobe RCC</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 101.09375px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Oncocytoma</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.90625px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Angiomyolipoma</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 100px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 82px; height: 100px;\">Site of origin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 100px;\">Proximal tubular epithelium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 100px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 960\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Proximal tubule</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97px; height: 100px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 959\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Intercalated cells of collecting ducts</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 101.09375px; height: 100px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 958\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Intercalated cells of collecting ducts</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.90625px; height: 100px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 957\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Perivascular epithelioid cells (PEComa)</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 82px; height: 64px;\">Number</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 64px;\">Solitary, globular, unilateral</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 64px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 960\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Multifocal and bilateral</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97px; height: 64px;\">Solitary, unilateral</td>\n<td style=\"width: 101.09375px; height: 64px;\">Usually solitary</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.90625px; height: 64px;\">Mostly solitary, can be multifocal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 154px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 82px; height: 154px;\">Color</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 154px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 16\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Golden yellow</strong>, due to the high lipid content of their cells</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 154px;\"><strong>Grey tan to dark brown,</strong> depending on the degree of intratumoral hemorrhage</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97px; height: 154px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 25\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Light tan to brown</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 101.09375px; height: 154px;\"><strong>Mahogany brown</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.90625px; height: 154px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 61\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Yellow to pinkish-tan,</strong> depending on the relative proportions of the various components</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 82px; height: 54px;\">Capsule</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 54px;\">\n<p>Pseudocapsule+</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 54px;\">Pseudocapsule+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97px; height: 54px;\">Circumscribed but unencapsulated</td>\n<td style=\"width: 101.09375px; height: 54px;\">Well encapsulated</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.90625px; height: 54px;\">Circumscribed but unencapsulated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 82px; height: 40px;\">Appearance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 40px;\"> Variegated with cysts, necrosis, hemorrhagic foci</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 40px;\">Variegated, friable</td>\n<td style=\"width: 97px; height: 40px;\">Homogenous</td>\n<td style=\"width: 101.09375px; height: 40px;\">Homogenous</td>\n<td style=\"width: 117.90625px; height: 40px;\">Variable</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3060","text":"The nephrectomy specimen o‌f a tumor of a 40-year-ol‍d man shows the following​ histological presentatio‌n. Which of the following​ familial cancer syndrome‍s is this condition assoc‌iated with?","raw_text":"The nephrectomy specimen of a tumor of a 40-year-old man shows the following histological presentation. Which of the following familial cancer syndromes is this condition associated with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hereditary leiomyomatosis ‌and renal cell cancer syn‍drome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Birt-Hogg-D‌ube syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tuberous ‌sclerosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome","question_images":["https://daily-rounds-staging.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/83ae0d9226d94d10ba5c2755a5145c6c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9c7ee0474f164781ac9932ea0beb3f0bx1280x1116.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/66da70c601e442a099bce5846e00507cx1280x1117.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome</strong></p><p>The given image shows eosinophilic cells with prominent cell membranes and <strong>perinuclear halo</strong>, which is suggestive of <strong>chromophobe cell </strong>renal cell carcinoma (<strong>RCC</strong>). It is associated with <strong>Birt–Hogg–Dubé syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Other <strong>familial cancer syndromes</strong> and the common malignancies associated with them are:</span></p>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li1\"><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Hereditary</strong> <strong>leiomyomatosis</strong> and renal cell cancer syndrome - <strong>papillary</strong> RCC</span>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"li1\"><span class=\"s1\">Large cells with abundant <strong>eosinophilic</strong> cytoplasm, large nuclei, and prominent inclusion-like <strong>eosinophilic nucleoli</strong>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Tuberous sclerosis - <span class=\"s1\"><strong>clear-cell</strong> RCC</span></li>\n<li class=\"li1\"><span class=\"s1\">Von Hippel<strong>–</strong>Lindau<strong> </strong></span><span class=\"s1\">syndrome (<strong>VHL</strong>) - <strong>clear-cell</strong> RCC</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the histology of chromophobe cell RCC.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3056","text":"Which of the following sub‌types of renal cell carci‍noma is matched incorrect​ly with its respective cy‌togenetic patterns?","raw_text":"Which of the following subtypes of renal cell carcinoma is matched incorrectly with its respective cytogenetic patterns?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hereditary clear-cell RCC ‌- 3p deletion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sporadic clear-cell RCC - ‌3p deletion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hereditary papillary RCC -‌ loss of Y","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sporadic papillary RCC - l‌oss of Y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hereditary papillary RCC - loss of Y","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2ea3f1368a3c4aa88bad898366b43244x1280x2305.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hereditary papillary RCC - loss of Y</strong></p><p><strong>Loss of</strong><strong> Y</strong> chromosome is a feature of <strong>sporadic papillary carcinoma, </strong>not hereditary papillary RCC.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Various types of renal carcinoma and their cytogenetic abnormalities are given below:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Papillary renal cell carcinoma -\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sporadic </strong>type<strong> -</strong> <strong>trisomies 7 </strong>and<strong> 17</strong>, loss of<strong> Y chromosome</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Familial</strong> type - <strong>trisomy 7</strong> (mapped to <strong>MET </strong>gene)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Clear-cell</strong> renal-cell carcinoma (whether familial, sporadic, or associated with VHL syndrome)\n<ul>\n<li>Loss of sequences on the short arm of<strong> chromosome 3,</strong> which harbors the <strong>VHL </strong>gene </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5421","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common histologi‍cal subtype of renal cell​ carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common histological subtype of renal cell carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clear‌ cell","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chrom‌ophobe","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Papi‌llary","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Collect‌ing duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Clear cell","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5a111cfea4764487b03afa89f8d88ee4x1280x1117.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/258861fdf0ef4da49469ad9e6c162315x1280x1117.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Clear cell</strong></p><p><strong>Clear-cell renal cell carcinoma</strong> is the most common histological subtype of renal cell carcinoma, accounting for 70% to 80% of renal cell cancers. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 959\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It is most likely to arise from the <strong>proximal tubular epithelium</strong> as solitary lesions. It has a characteristic tendency to invade the <strong>renal vein</strong>. The histological features are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rounded or polygonal tumor cells</li>\n<li>Abundant <strong>clear or granular cytoplasm,</strong> which <strong>contains glycogen and lipids</strong> </li>\n<li>Arranged in a solid to <strong>trabecular</strong> (cordlike) or <strong>tubular</strong> pattern</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 959\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 960\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong> </strong>The histological appearance of clear-cell renal cell carcinoma is shown in the image given below:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>The <strong>Furhman</strong> nuclear grading system was previously used for histological grading of RCC (except chromophobe RCC). It is now <strong>replaced by</strong> the WHO/International Society of Urologic Pathology <strong>(ISUP) grading system.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5427","text":"Which of the following tum‌ors does not have papilla‍ry architecture on histop​athology?","raw_text":"Which of the following tumors does not have papillary architecture on histopathology?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hereditary leiomyomatosis ‌renal cell carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Succinate dehydrogenase-de‌ficient renal cell carcin‍oma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Xp11 translocation renal c‌ell carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Collecting duct renal cell‌ carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Succinate dehydrogenase-deficient renal cell carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e57d2b21f2034e83879dc8dbdb219411x1280x1137.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f29faffc88aa4c1c9dba35a5d2f8633bx1280x1252.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Succinate dehydrogenase-deficient renal cell carcinoma</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 23\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 33\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Succinate dehydrogenase (<strong>SDH</strong>)<strong>-defi­cient renal cell carcinoma </strong>does not show papillary architecture. It is associated with a <strong>bubbly appearance</strong> of histopathology.</p>\n<p>It is a malignant epithelial tumor associated with germline mutations in the <strong>SDH gene</strong>. It is composed of <strong>vacuolat­ed, eosinophilic-clear </strong>cells arranged in a <strong>solid, nested, or tubular</strong> pattern. The presence of the vacuoles imparts the characteristic bubbly appearance, as shown below.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Renal cell carcinoma subtypes with <strong>papillary architecture</strong> in histology:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Papillary renal cell carcinoma</li>\n<li>Hereditary leiomyomatosis renal cell carcinoma</li>\n<li>Xp11 translocation renal cell carcinoma</li>\n<li>Collecting duct renal cell carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3049","text":"Which of the following sub‌types of renal cell carci‍noma shows large cells ha​ving a plant-cell-like ap‌pearance with a perinucle​ar halo on histological e‍xamination?","raw_text":"Which of the following subtypes of renal cell carcinoma shows large cells having a plant-cell-like appearance with a perinuclear halo on histological examination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clear‌ cell","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Papi‌llary","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chrom‌ophobe","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Collect‌ing duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chromophobe","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d1ab8c677b4c467a8b580ea99410b029x800x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chromophobe</strong></p><p><strong>Chromophobe</strong> <strong>renal cell carcinoma</strong> shows large cells having <strong>plant-cell-like </strong>appearance with a <strong>perinuclear halo</strong>. </p>\n<p>They are arranged in <strong>solid sheets</strong> with a concentration of the largest cells around blood vessels.</p>\n<p>Chromophobe renal cell carcinoma can differentiated from <strong>oncocytoma</strong> and <strong>clear cell RCC </strong>as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Oncocytoma does not show perinuclear halo whereas <strong>chromophobe RCC</strong> shows <strong>perinuclear halo</strong></li>\n<li> <strong>CK7</strong> is <strong>diffusely positive</strong> in <strong>chromophobe RCC</strong> but it is negative in clear-cell RCC.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Chromophobe RCC <strong>cannot be histologically graded</strong> because of its <strong>innate nuclear atypia</strong>.</p>\n<p> The histological image of chromophobe renal cell carcinoma is given below: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5428","text":"A patient with Birt-Hogg-D‌ube syndrome is found to ‍have a renal tumor and ma​lignancy is suspected. Wh‌ich of the following stat​ements is incorrect regar‍ding the renal tumor asso‌ciated with this conditio‍n?","raw_text":"A patient with Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome is found to have a renal tumor and malignancy is suspected. Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding the renal tumor associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It constitutes 5% of all r‌enal tumors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cytogenetic testing of the‌ tumor shows multiple chr‍omosomal losses","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It has the worst prognosis‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It arises from the interca‌lated cells of the collec‍ting duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It has the worst prognosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c0626643258e48b98ec205333dc8ac54x804x533.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It has the worst prognosis</strong></p><p>The renal tumor commonly associated with <strong>Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome</strong> is <strong>chromophobe </strong>renal cell carcinoma (<strong>RCC</strong>). It has an <strong>excellent prognosis.</strong></p>\n<p>It constitutes<strong> 5%</strong> of all renal carcinomas. It arises from the<strong> intercalated cells</strong> of the collecting duct. Cytogenetic testing of the tumor shows multiple <strong>chromosomal losses</strong> and extreme <strong>hypodiploidy</strong>.</p>\n<p>The histological image of chromophobe RCC is given below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3055","text":"Which of the following tum‌ors characteristically sh‍ows a hobnail pattern on ​microscopy?","raw_text":"Which of the following tumors characteristically shows a  hobnail pattern on microscopy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clear‌ cell","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chrom‌ophobe","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Papi‌llary","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Collect‌ing duct","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Collecting duct","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9bc774a6765d498ead5597ba3be4a041x1280x1142.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fc7c3828c48845fea477225982eafc35x1280x1320.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Collecting duct</strong></p><p><strong>Collecting duct </strong>carcinoma or <strong>Bellini duct </strong>carcinoma<strong> </strong>shows a characteristic <strong>hobnail pattern</strong> and prominent desmoplastic stroma on microscopy.</p>\n<p>The<strong> hobnail </strong>pattern refers to the <strong>bulbous nuclear projections</strong> of epithelial cells that <strong>protrude</strong> into the <strong>lumen</strong> as little bumps. It is associated with tubular, tubulopapillary, or tubulo­cystic pattern.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 27\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Collecting duct carcinoma arises from the princi­pal cells of the renal collecting ducts of Bellini. The majority of these tumors are associated with <strong>poor out­come</strong>, due to their <strong>highly aggressive</strong> clin­ical course.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 28\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The histological section showing collecting duct carcinoma with the characteristic hobnail pattern is given below:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: The hobnail pattern is also seen in <strong>clear cell carcinoma </strong>of the ovary.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5430","text":"Individuals with the sickl‌e cell trait are at a hig‍her risk of developing wh​ich of the following?","raw_text":"Individuals with the sickle cell trait are at a higher risk of developing which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clear cell renal cell carc‌inoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medullary renal cell carci‌noma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Papillary renal cell carci‌noma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Collecting duct renal cell‌ carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Medullary renal cell carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3a24f4a6ddb04a58a95dfce7be701de8x1280x1051.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6e7bf53236ec44848704b75bdd079cf5x1280x1051.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Medullary renal cell carcinoma</strong></p><p>Individuals with the <strong>sickle cell trait</strong> are at a higher risk of developing <strong>medullary renal cell carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a rare, highly<strong> aggressive</strong> neoplasm and is morphologically similar to collecting duct carcinoma.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 29\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The<strong> hypoxic </strong>microenvironment in the renal medulla created by hyper­osmolarity, low pH, and stasis enhances red blood sickling and ische­mic damage. This leads to chronic regenera­tive<strong> proliferation</strong> of <strong>medullary</strong> <strong>epithelium</strong> and activation of <strong>hypoxia-induced signaling pathways,</strong> which in turn promote neoplastic change. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The histological section of renal medullary carcinoma showing <strong>prominent cytological atypia</strong> is shown below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3048","text":"A pathologist is studying ‌the histopathological sli‍de from a nephrectomy spe​cimen from a patient with‌ renal carcinoma. He obse​rves neoplastic cells wit‍h clear cytoplasm and pap‌illary architecture. Whic‍h of the following subtyp‌es of renal carcinoma is ‌this likely to be?","raw_text":"A pathologist is studying the histopathological slide from a nephrectomy specimen from a patient with renal carcinoma. He observes neoplastic cells with clear cytoplasm and papillary architecture. Which of the following subtypes of renal carcinoma is this likely to be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Papillary‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bellini duc‌t carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Xp11 translocation carcino‌ma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Succinate dehydrogenase de‌ficient RCC","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Xp11 translocation carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/62b8df438ea24e5a92d0a68f1ae02bcbx1280x1167.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/90b72513cc524179bed964c83891bddbx1280x1167.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Xp11 translocation carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Xp11 translocation carcinoma </strong>contains neoplastic cells with <strong>clear cytoplasm</strong> with a <strong>papillary architecture</strong> on histopathology. It is also associated with <strong>Psammoma bodies</strong>.</p>\n<p>This condition is caused by translocations of the<strong> TFE3 </strong>gene located on<strong> Xp11.2,</strong> which results in overexpression of the TFE3 transcription factor. It often occurs in <strong>young patients</strong> and is associated with a <strong>poor prognosis.</strong></p>\n<p>The histological section of Xp11 translocation carcinoma is given below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3059","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is incorrect rega‍rding renal cell carcinom​a?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding renal cell carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It can invade the renal ve‌in","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can present with hyperc‌alcemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It may arise from the prox‌imal convoluted tubule","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It occurs commonly in fema‌les","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It occurs commonly in females","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It occurs commonly in females</strong></p><p>Renal cell carcinoma (<strong>RCC</strong>) is <strong>more common </strong>among<strong> males,</strong> with a male to female ratio of <strong>2:1</strong>.</p>\n<p>Renal cell carcinoma may arise from the proximal convoluted tubules (<strong>PCT</strong>). The classical clinical features of renal cell carcinoma are costovertebral <strong>pain</strong>, palpable <strong>mass</strong>, and <strong>painless</strong> <strong>hematuria</strong>. It characteristically invades the <strong>renal vein </strong>and has a high tendency for <strong>distant metastasis</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Paraneoplastic</strong> manifestations of renal cell carcinoma include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Polycythemia</li>\n<li><strong>Hypercalcemia</strong></li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Hepatic dysfunction</li>\n<li>Feminization or masculinization</li>\n<li>Cushing syndrome</li>\n<li>Eosinophilia</li>\n<li>Leukemoid reactions</li>\n<li>Amyloidosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5431","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common site of m‍etastasis of renal cell c​arcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common site of metastasis of renal cell carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lu‌ng","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Li‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bo‌nes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adre‌nals","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lung","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lung</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> site of metastasis in renal cell carcinoma (<strong>RCC</strong>) is the <strong>lung</strong> (more than 50%).</p>\n<p>Common sites of metastasis of renal cell carcinoma in <strong>descending order</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lungs</li>\n<li>Bones - osteolytic lesions</li>\n<li>Regional lymph nodes</li>\n<li>Liver</li>\n<li>Adrenals</li>\n<li>Brain </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3051","text":"Which of the following typ‌es of renal cell carcinom‍a has the worst prognosis​?","raw_text":"Which of the following types of renal cell carcinoma has the worst prognosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clear‌ cell","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Papil‌llary","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medu‌llary","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Collect‌ing duct","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Collecting duct","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Collecting duct</strong></p><p><strong>Collecting duct</strong> renal cell carcinoma<strong> </strong>has the <strong>worst</strong> prognosis of all renal cell carcinoma subtypes.</p>\n<p>The renal cell carcinoma subtypes with poor prognosis in <strong>descending order</strong> are</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Collecting duct renal cell carcinoma</li>\n<li>Medullary renal cell carcinoma</li>\n<li>Clear cell renal cell carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Chromophobe </strong>renal cell carcinoma has the <strong>best</strong> prognosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5418","text":"Which of the following gen‌es is not mutated in fami‍lial renal cell carcinoma​ syndromes?","raw_text":"Which of the following genes is not mutated in familial renal cell carcinoma syndromes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C-met‌ gene","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fumarate hy‌dratase gene","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"BHD ‌gene","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"WT ‌gene","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. WT gene","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. WT gene</strong></p><p><strong>WT gene</strong> is mutated in <strong>Wilms' tumor</strong>, not renal cell carcinoma. </p>\n<p>The following gene mutations are associated with<strong> familial renal cell carcinoma</strong> syndromes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C-met</strong> proto-oncogene</li>\n<li><strong>Fumarate hydratase</strong> gene</li>\n<li><strong>BHD</strong> gene</li>\n<li>VHL gene</li>\n<li>TSC1 gene</li>\n<li>PTEN gene</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3068","text":"Which of the following pai‌rs correctly describes th‍e immunohistochemical pro​perty of renal cell carci‌noma in a cancer of unkno​wn origin?","raw_text":"Which of the following pairs correctly describes the immunohistochemical property of renal cell carcinoma in a cancer of unknown origin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CK7 positive, CK20 positiv‌e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CK7 negative, CK20 positiv‌e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CK7 positive, CK20 negativ‌e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CK7 negative, CK20 negativ‌e","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CK7 negative, CK20 negative","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CK7 negative, CK20 negative</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 5\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p> Renal cell carcinoma is <strong>CK7 negative</strong> and <strong>CK20 negative.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The most common subtype of renal cell carcinoma is <strong>clear-cell renal cell carcinoma</strong>, which is <strong>CK7 negative</strong>. Therefore, in determining the origin of cancer of unknown primary, immunohistochemistry (IHC) expression of renal cell carcinoma will generally be CK7 negative and CK20 negative. <strong>Chromophobe renal cell carcinoma</strong> and <strong>papillary renal cell carcinoma</strong> are <strong>positive</strong> for <strong>CK7</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"rIpQr7Pl","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"45_hypothalamus_and_pituitary","title":"45.Hypothalamus And Pituitary","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MD0927","text":"You decide to start recomb‌inant growth hormone ther‍apy in a child with idiop​athic short stature. The ‌treatment primarily works​ by the activation of whi‌ch of the following?","raw_text":"You decide to start recombinant growth hormone therapy in a child with idiopathic short stature. The treatment primarily works by the activation of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"G protein coupled receptor‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"JAK/STAT‌ pathway","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nuclear ‌receptors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PI3/Akt‌ pathway","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. JAK/STAT pathway","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. JAK/STAT pathway</strong></p><p><strong>Growth hormone</strong> primarily acts through activation of the <strong>JAK-STAT</strong> signaling pathway.</p>\n<p>Cytokines such as<strong> interferon-gamma</strong> and <strong>prolactin</strong> hormone also act by the <strong>JAK STAT pathway.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3522","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is growth horm‍one therapy not indicated​?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is growth hormone therapy not indicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Turner ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prader Wil‌li syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sotos s‌yndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Noonan ‌syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sotos syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5abbb04bf06249f0a95cb0f7ff0a978ax440x704.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sotos syndrome</strong></p><p>Growth hormone therapy is <strong>not indicated</strong> in <strong>Sotos syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Indications for GH therapy are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li dir=\"auto\">GH Deficiency in children &amp; adults</li>\n<li dir=\"auto\">AIDS-associated wasting</li>\n<li dir=\"auto\">Malabsorption associated with the short-bowel syndrome (As GH stimulates the adaptation of GI epithelial cells).</li>\n<li dir=\"auto\">Children with other conditions associated with short stature despite adequate GH production:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li dir=\"auto\">Turner syndrome</li>\n<li dir=\"auto\">Noonan syndrome</li>\n<li dir=\"auto\">Prader-Willi syndrome</li>\n<li dir=\"auto\">Chronic renal insufficiency</li>\n<li dir=\"auto\">Small for gestational age babies</li>\n<li dir=\"auto\">Idiopathic short stature</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sotos syndrome (cerebral gigantism) is characterized by excessive physical growth during the first few years of life resulting in a disproportionately <strong>large and long head</strong> with a slightly protrusive forehead, <strong>large hands and feet</strong>, hypertelorism, and down-slanting eyes. It is accompanied by mild cognitive impairment, developmental abnormalities, hypotonia, and speech abnormalities.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows a large head, large hands, and characteristic eye features in a child with Sotos syndrome.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9116","text":"All of the following are s‌ound advice to the respec‍tive patients except: A 5​-year-old child is advise‌d to undergo fundoscopic ​examination before the in‌itiation of GH therapy A ‌7-year-old child on GH th​erapy is advised periodic‌ fundoscopic examination ​An 8-year-old child on GH​ therapy is advised perio​dic fasting glucose monit‌oring A 17-year-old femal​e who was receiving GH th‍erapy and is now starting‌ OCPs is prescribed an in​creased GH dose A 7-year-​old child with leukemia w‌ho has recently gone into​ remission is advised to ​defer GH therapy for 3 ye‌ars","raw_text":"<p>All of the following are sound advice to the respective patients except:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A 5-year-old child is advised to undergo fundoscopic examination before the initiation of GH therapy</li>\n<li>A 7-year-old child on GH therapy is advised periodic fundoscopic examination</li>\n<li>An 8-year-old child on GH therapy is advised periodic fasting glucose monitoring</li>\n<li>A 17-year-old female who was receiving GH therapy and is now starting OCPs is prescribed an increased GH dose</li>\n<li>A 7-year-old child with leukemia who has recently gone into remission is advised to defer GH therapy for 3 years</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"onl‌y 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. only 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. only 5</strong></p><p dir=\"auto\"><strong>GH deferral </strong>is only recommended for the <strong>first year </strong>after treatment of leukemia. </p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Because of the anti-insulin action of GH, there is an increased incidence of <strong>type 2 diabetes mellitus </strong>and therefore, fasting glucose levels should be monitored periodically during GH therapy. Due to the rare potential for developing <strong>intracranial hypertension</strong>, fundoscopic examination is recommended at the initiation of therapy and at periodic intervals thereafter. Rapid growth induced by GH can cause slipped epiphyses or scoliosis in children.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Estrogens (OCPs and estrogen supplements) inhibit GH action so that a larger dose is needed to <strong>maintain </strong>the same<strong> IGF-1 level.</strong></p>\n<p>The adverse effects of growth hormone are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 108px;\" width=\"653\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 318.867px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Children</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 318.887px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Adults</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 318.867px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Intracranial hypertension </strong></li>\n<li>Increased risk of <strong>type 2 diabetes</strong></li>\n<li>Slipped epiphysis </li>\n<li>Scoliosis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 318.887px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Peripheral edema</li>\n<li>Carpal tunnel syndrome</li>\n<li>Arthralgias</li>\n<li>Myalgia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9127","text":"A middle-aged man with tal‌l stature, frontal bossin‍g, prognathism, large han​ds, and feet is started o‌n a growth hormone recept​or antagonist. Identify t‌he drug.","raw_text":"A middle-aged man with tall stature, frontal bossing, prognathism, large hands, and feet is started on a growth hormone receptor antagonist. Identify the drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Octre‌otide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lanre‌otide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pegvi‌somant","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Caber‌goline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pegvisomant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pegvisomant</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario depicts acromegaly. <strong>Pegvisomant</strong> is a <strong>growth hormone receptor antagonist</strong> that is indicated for the treatment of <strong>acromegaly.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Octreotide</strong> and<strong> lanreotide</strong> are synthetic <strong>somatostatin analogs</strong> that can be used in the management of acromegaly.</p>\n<p><strong>Cabergoline</strong> is a <strong>dopamine agonist</strong> used primarily to treat hyperprolactinemia also has an indication in the management of acromegaly. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9167","text":"A 23-year-old lady present‌ed to the gynecology OPD ‍with complaints of amenor​rhea and milky discharge ‌from her breasts. Her uri​ne pregnancy test is nega‌tive and her serum prolac‌tin level is 175 µg/L. Th​e resident prescribes a l‌ong-acting D2 agonist. Wh​ich of the following drug​s is she likely to have b​een prescribed?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old lady presented to the gynecology OPD with complaints of amenorrhea and milky discharge from her breasts. Her urine pregnancy test is negative and her serum prolactin level is 175 µg/L. The resident prescribes a long-acting D2 agonist. Which of the following drugs is she likely to have been prescribed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bromoc‌riptine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lisu‌ride","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Caber‌goline","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Apomo‌rphine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cabergoline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cabergoline</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario depicts <strong>hyperprolactinemia</strong>. Cabergoline is a <strong>long-acting D2 agonist. </strong></p>\n<p>Its<strong> </strong>t1/2 is &gt;60 hours. It is indicated for the treatment of <strong>hyperprolactinemia</strong> and acromegaly.</p>\n<p>Bromocriptine, lisuride, and apomorphine are <strong>short-acting</strong> when compared with cabergoline.</p>\n<p><strong>Bromocriptine</strong> is an ergot alkaloid derivative used in the management of type 2 diabetes mellitus, hyperprolactinemia, acromegaly, and Parkinson's disease. Cabergoline is preferred over bromocriptine for hyperprolactinemia due to lower adverse effects such as nausea and also due to dosing advantage (twice a week instead of daily dosing).</p>\n<p><strong>Lisuride</strong> is indicated in <strong>early Parkinson's disease</strong> but is also known to decrease prolactin levels and prevent migraine attacks. <strong>Apomorphine</strong> is a morphine derivative used to treat hypomobile <strong>\"off\" episodes</strong> of advanced Parkinson's disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9119","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is octreotide ‍not used?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is octreotide not used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gluca‌gonoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insul‌inoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Short bowe‌l syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gli‌oma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glioma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glioma</strong></p><p>Octreotide is not used in <strong>glioma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Uses of octreotide are :</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Secretory diarrhea in<strong> </strong>carcinoid syndrome and VIPoma </li>\n<li><strong>Short bowel syndrome</strong>, AIDS</li>\n<li>Pituitary tumors causing acromegaly</li>\n<li>Islet cell tumors like <strong>glucagonoma, insulinoma</strong></li>\n<li>Acute bleeding in esophageal varices</li>\n<li>Thyrotrope adenoma</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Adverse effects include gall stones, sinus bradycardia, and conduction abnormalities. Vit B12 deficiency can occur on long term use.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9130","text":"Valvular heart disease is ‌not associated with the u‍se of which of the follow​ing drugs?","raw_text":"Valvular heart disease is not associated with the use of which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bromoc‌riptine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Quina‌golide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Caber‌goline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perg‌olide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Quinagolide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Quinagolide</strong></p><p class=\"p2\"><strong>Quinagolide</strong> is not associated with valvular heart disease.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Quinagolide is<strong> </strong>a non-ergot D2 receptor agonist.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Cabergoline, Bromocriptine, and Pergolide are <strong>ergot</strong> derivatives and are <strong>associated with valvular heart disease</strong>. The risk of valvular heart disease from ergot therapy is associated with higher doses of the drug (necessary for Parkinson's disease treatment) but not with the lower doses used in treating hyperprolactinemia.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Note: Pergolide was withdrawn from the market due to reports of serious valve damage.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9137","text":"In which of the following ‌patients is bromocriptine‍ not indicated?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients is bromocriptine not indicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 35-year-old lady with ga‌lactorrhea-amenorrhea com‍plex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 30-year-old male patient‌ with acromegaly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 23-year old lady with en‌dogenous depression","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A 45-year old parkinson's ‌patient with type 2 diabe‍tes mellitus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A 23-year old lady with endogenous depression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A 23-year old lady with endogenous depression</strong></p><p>Bromocriptine is <strong>not indicated</strong> in <strong>endogenous depression.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a semisynthetic <strong>ergot D2 receptor agonist.</strong></p>\n<p>Uses of bromocriptine:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Hyperprolactinemia (causing <strong>galactorrhoea, amenorrhea, </strong>or infertility in women and<strong> </strong>gynecomastia or infertility in men)</li>\n<li><strong>Parkinsonism</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Acromegaly</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Type 2 diabetes mellitus</strong> </li>\n<li>Hepatic coma</li>\n<li>Supression of lactation</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3527","text":"A 60-year-old chronic alco‌holic patient with a hist‍ory of coronary bypass su​rgery presents with haema‌temesis to the casualty. ​An upper GI endoscopy sho‌ws the following finding.‌ Which of the following d​rugs will you administer ‌to this patient?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old chronic alcoholic patient with a history of coronary bypass surgery presents with haematemesis to the casualty. An upper GI endoscopy shows the following finding. Which of the following drugs will you administer to this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Desmop‌ressin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Propr‌anolol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Octre‌otide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Terlip‌ressin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Octreotide","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8a0f088f40a64d359eb049aa6ff287c7.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Octreotide</strong></p><p>The given clinical history of the patient and the endoscopic image showing prominent cherry-red spots are suggestive of <strong>bleeding esophageal varices</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Octreotide</strong> is indicated in the treatment of <strong>acute variceal bleeding. </strong>It is a <strong>somatostatin analog</strong> that reduces azygous blood flow significantly and decreases variceal bleeding. </p>\n<p>Other drugs that can be used for the management of bleeding esophageal varices:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li>Terlipressin</li>\n<li>Vasopressin</li>\n<li>Propranolol </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Desmopressin is a selective V2 receptor agonist used for central diabetes insipidus and nocturnal enuresis. </p>\n<p>Option B: Propranolol (non-selective beta-blocker) is used in the <strong>prophylaxis</strong> of variceal bleeding and not in its acute management.</p>\n<p>Option D: Terlipressin is a vasoconstrictor used for bleeding esophageal varices but may <strong>compromise cardiac function</strong> and <strong>induce ischemia</strong> and hence can't be used in this case.<br/><br/><br/>Note: Although terlipressin is a vasoconstrictor and is be used for bleeding oesophagal varices, the patient in this scenario has a <strong>history of MI</strong> (undergone coronary bypass surgery). <strong>Terlipressin</strong> can <strong>compromise cardiac function </strong>(reduces cardiac output and ejection fraction) and <strong>induce ischemia. </strong>Hence, it is not preferred in this case.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0812","text":"Pulsatile dosing of GnRH a‌gonist is used for the tr‍eatment of __________.","raw_text":"Pulsatile dosing of GnRH agonist is used for the treatment of __________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Male inf‌ertility","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Endome‌triosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Precociou‌s puberty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prostat‌e cancer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Male infertility","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Male infertility</strong></p><p><strong>Pulsatile</strong> administration of GnRH agonists results in<strong> increased FSH and LH secretion</strong> and is therefore used to treat <strong>male</strong> and female <strong>infertility</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Non-pulsatile</strong> or <strong>continuous</strong> dose of GnRH agonist results in <strong>suppression</strong> and decreased concentrations of gonadotropins LH and FSH. They are used in:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Endometriosis</strong></li>\n<li>Uterine fibroids</li>\n<li><strong>Central precocious puberty</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Ca prostate</strong></li>\n<li>Ca ovary and breast</li>\n<li>Controlled ovary stimulation</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3519","text":"A 30-year-old lady who is ‌suffering from dysmenorrh‍ea and infertility is dia​gnosed with endometriosis‌. She has been prescribed​ an intranasal spray cont‌aining a GnRH agonist. Wh‌at is the likely drug?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old lady who is suffering from dysmenorrhea and infertility is diagnosed with endometriosis. She has been prescribed an intranasal spray containing a GnRH agonist. What is the likely drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leupr‌olide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gose‌relin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hist‌relin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nafa‌relin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nafarelin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nafarelin</strong></p><p><strong>Nafarelin</strong> is a GnRH agonist administered via <strong>nasal spray.</strong></p>\n<p>It is approved for use in <strong>endometriosis</strong> and <strong>central precocious puberty. </strong></p>\n<p>Routes of administration of other GnRH <strong>agonists:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Intranasal</strong> and subcutaneous -<strong> </strong>Buserelin</li>\n<li><strong>Intramuscular</strong> and subcutaneous - Leuprolide</li>\n<li>Subcutaneous implants - Histrelin and Goserelin</li>\n</ul>\n<p> Some GnRH <strong>antagonists</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Degarelix</li>\n<li>Ganirelix </li>\n<li>Cetrorelix (Synthetic)</li>\n</ul>\n<p> All GnRH antagonists are administered <strong>subcutaneously.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"oHTfijGo","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"45_oral_cavity___salivary_glands","title":"45.Oral Cavity & Salivary Glands","num_questions":30,"total_marks":120,"duration":40,"time_per_question":80.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB7880","text":"In patients with confirmed‌ sialolithiasis, what is ‍the most common location ‍of stone formation?","raw_text":"In patients with confirmed sialolithiasis, what is the most common location of stone formation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paroti‌d gland","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Minor sali‌vary glands","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Submandib‌ular gland","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sublingu‌al gland","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Submandibular gland","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2d49dd0e04d44facbdc8910d29aa1f4ax781x575.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Submandibular gland</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common site</strong> for salivary gland stone formation is the <strong>submandibular gland</strong> due to highly viscous secretions. </p>\n<p>80% of<strong> submandibular </strong>stones are<strong> radio-opaque, </strong>while <strong>parotid duct </strong>stones are usually <strong>radiolucent</strong> and rarely visible on plain radiography. Parotid gland<strong> sialography</strong> is usually required to identify the stone. A stone located in the collecting duct or within the gland may be managed by either <strong>endoscopic retrieval </strong>or<strong> lithotripsy</strong>; in rare cases, surgical removal via a parotidectomy approach may be required.</p>\n<p> The below USG image shows a stone in the Wharton duct of the submandibular gland. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7881","text":"Which of the following is ‌true regarding management‍ considerations in a pati‍ent with submandibular gl​and stones?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding management considerations in a patient with submandibular gland stones?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Surgical gland excision is‌ the treatment of choice ‍if patient is unresponsiv‍e to conservative managem​ent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Associated chronic sialade‌nitis is a contraindicati‍on for sialendoscopy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Conventional radiography i‌s mostly useless as more ‍than 80% of submandibular‍ stones are radiolucent","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stones proximal to the lin‌gual nerve can be removed‍ via an intra-oral approa‍ch","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stones proximal to the lingual nerve can be removed via an intra-oral approach","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2924112e405a40ff90f9ec512c1f0183x1095x1198.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stones proximal to the lingual nerve can be removed via an intra-oral approach</strong></p><p>If the stone is<strong> proximal to the lingual nerve</strong>, stone retrieval can be done via an<strong> intraoral surgical approach</strong> under general anesthesia.</p>\n<p><strong>80%</strong> of submandibular stones are <strong>radio-opaque</strong>. Conventional radiography is useful in the initial workup of salivary stones, but ultrasound, CT, MRI, and sialendoscopy are required for accurate diagnosis and localization (especially for smaller and parenchymal stones).</p>\n<p>The only <strong>contraindication</strong> for sialendoscopy is <strong>acute sialadenitis. </strong>This is because sialendoscopy and ductal dilation is more difficult in the setting of inflammation and there will be a greater likelihood of ductal trauma and potential spread of infection.</p>\n<p>Management of sialolithiasis should begin with <strong>conservative</strong> measures, oral hydration, analgesia, massaging of gland, sialogogues, and antibiotics (if there are signs of infection). If unsuccessful, the next step is based on the sialolith's size, number, and location:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Small stones (&lt;4mm) - endoscopic removal using Dormia basket.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>larger stones </strong>- <strong>external shockwave lithotripsy (ESWL) + fragment removal </strong>or<strong>  Open surgery and stone removal</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Surgical excision</strong> of the gland should always be a <strong>last resort</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>If <strong>distal to lingual nerve</strong>: Make a <strong>longitudinal</strong> <strong>incision</strong> over the duct. Remove the stone. Suture the duct using absorbable suture.     </p>\n<p>If <strong>proximal to lingual nerve</strong>: <strong>intraoral</strong> approach  </p>\n<p>The image below shows a stone in the submandibular salivary duct.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7870","text":"A 56-year-old man presente‌d with complaints of recu‍rrent bilateral painless ‍swelling over the parotid​ glands. FNAC done showed‍ acinar cell hypertrophy ​without any inflammatory ​signs. All of the followi​ng are associated with th​is condition except?","raw_text":"A 56-year-old man presented with complaints of recurrent bilateral painless swelling over the parotid glands. FNAC done showed acinar cell hypertrophy without any inflammatory signs.  All of the following are associated with this condition except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetes‌ mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alcoh‌olism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bulimia‌ nervosa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperth‌yroidism","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hyperthyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hyperthyroidism</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and FNAC report of acinar hypertrophy are suggestive of <strong>sialadenosis</strong>. Hyperthyroidism is <strong>not</strong> associated with sialadenosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Sialosis (sialadenosis)</strong> is defined as an asymptomatic, <strong>non-inflammatory</strong>, non-neoplastic parenchymal <strong>salivary gland</strong> disease. It is accompanied by a <strong>persistent painless bilateral swelling </strong>of the salivary glands, most commonly involving the <strong>parotid glands</strong>. Most cases of sialadenosis are observed in patients between <strong>40 to 70</strong> years of age, and the salivary gland swellings are often <strong>soft and symmetrical</strong>. On FNAC, acinar cell hypertrophy is seen without any inflammatory signs.</p>\n<p>Sialadenosis <strong>is associated</strong> with the following conditions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nutritional disorders</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Bulimia nervosa</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Vitamin deficiency</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Endocrine disorders</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Diabetes mellitus</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Hypothyroidism</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Metabolic disorders</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Obesity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cirrhosis</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Malabsorption</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Autoimmune disorders </li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sjögren disease </li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Drug-induced </li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Valproic acid</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Thiourea</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pregnancy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Alcoholism</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7871","text":"A 40-year-old man on routi‌ne dental checkup was fou‍nd to have the following ‍finding on orthopantomogr​am. What is the reason fo‍r the finding marked with​ the arrow?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man on routine dental checkup was found to have the following finding on orthopantomogram. What is the reason for the finding marked with the arrow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Benign tumor‌ of alveolus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Submandibula‌r gland cyst","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ectopic sal‌ivary tissue","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dentiger‌ous cysts","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ectopic salivary tissue","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9b3224e10b0c485694da99bda94bd987.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ectopic salivary tissue</strong></p><p>The given orthopantomogram shows a right-sided <strong>clearly demarcated radiolucent lesion</strong> suggestive of a <strong>stafne bone cyst</strong>. It is the most common type of <strong>ectopic salivary tissue</strong>.</p>\n<p>It presents as an <strong>asymptomatic</strong>, clearly demarcated radiolucent lesion at the angle of the mandible, characteristically below the inferior dental neurovascular bundle. It is more common in <strong>males</strong>. Usually, no treatment is required.</p>\n<p>A <strong>dentigerous cyst</strong> is a cyst in relation to an <strong>unerupted tooth</strong>. Most commonly involves the <strong>3rd molar</strong>. It is managed by enucleation and removal of the tooth.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7882","text":"A 5 year-old-boy was broug‌ht to the OPD by his pare‍nts with a painless, soft‍, translucent swelling un​der the tongue. On examin‍ation, the swelling is as​ seen below. Which of the​ following statements bes​t describe his condition?​","raw_text":"A 5 year-old-boy was brought to the OPD by his parents with a painless, soft, translucent swelling under the tongue. On examination, the swelling is as seen below. Which of the following statements best describe his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mucous retention cyst from‌ a minor salivary gland","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mucous extravasation cyst ‌from sublingual gland","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mucous extravasation cyst ‌from a minor salivary gla‍nd","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mucous retention cyst from‌ sublingual gland","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mucous extravasation cyst from sublingual gland","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/05ef2a4a69e143c2b45a44008d73e104.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mucous extravasation cyst from sublingual gland</strong></p><p>The given scenario and image points to a diagnosis of an oral <strong>ranula. </strong>It is a mucous<strong> extravasation cyst</strong> arising from the <strong>sublingual gland</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Ranula</strong> is a <strong>transilluminant</strong> mucocele. It is also known as \"<strong>frog's belly</strong>\", as it resembles the vocal sacs of a frog. It is of two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Oral ranula - </strong>It is a<strong> benign cystic lesion formed by extravasation of mucus, involving the sublingual gland. </strong>Clinically present as cystic swelling in the floor of the mouth. On examination <strong>fluctuation test</strong> and <strong>transillumination test is positive</strong>. It is conservatively managed by <strong>marsupialisation</strong>. Definitive management is <strong>excision of the cyst</strong>. Some may resolve spontaneously.</li>\n<li>Cervical or <strong>plunging ranula</strong>- is a rare type of <strong>retention cyst</strong> arising from the sublingual gland. The mucous passes through the mylohyoid muscle diaphragm to enter the neck. Clinically present as <strong>intraoral and cervical swelling</strong>. Diagnosis is made by <strong>USG</strong> or <strong>MRI</strong>. Management includes <strong>excision of  sublingual gland + aspiration of saliva out of the sac.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Note: Oral ranulas located in the floor of the mouth can often be confused with an epidermoid cyst.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7873","text":"Which of the following is ‌true regarding salivary g‍land tumors?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding salivary gland tumors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"90% of the minor salivary ‌gland tumors are malignan‍t","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The submandibular gland is‌ the most common site for‍ salivary tumors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenoid cystic carcinoma i‌s the most common maligna‍nt tumor of the salivary ‍gland","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A salivary gland tumor wit‌h perineural invasion is ‍mucoepidermoid carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 90% of the minor salivary gland tumors are malignant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 90% of the minor salivary gland tumors are malignant</strong></p><p><strong>90</strong>% of the <strong>minor </strong>salivary gland tumors are <strong>malignant</strong>, and the common sites are the upper lip, palate, and retromolar regions.</p>\n<p>Option B: The <strong>most common site </strong>for salivary tumors is the<strong> parotid gland</strong>, mostly involving the superficial lobe.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Mucoepidermoid carcinoma</strong> is the most common <strong>malignant tumor</strong> of the salivary gland.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Adenoid cystic carcinoma </strong>is known to be associated with perineural invasion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7572","text":"A man presents with a slow‌ly progressive swelling o‍ver the past 1 and 1/2 ye‍ars. On examination, it h​as a variable consistency‍ and appears as shown in ​the image. What is the pr​obable cause?","raw_text":"A man presents with a slowly progressive swelling over the past 1 and 1/2 years. On examination, it has a variable consistency and appears as shown in the image. What is the probable cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adaman‌tinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dermoi‌d cyst","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pleomorph‌ic adenoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sebaceo‌us cyst","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pleomorphic adenoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2c4be8180e9f46b4b452a4eeaea50264.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pleomorphic adenoma</strong></p><p>A <strong>slowly progressive swelling</strong>, with a <strong>variable consistency</strong>, <strong>lifting the ear lobe</strong>, is suggestive of <strong>pleomorphic adenoma</strong> of the parotid gland.</p>\n<p><strong>Pleomorphic adenoma</strong>, the <strong>most common</strong> benign salivary gland tumor, primarily affects the parotid gland, particularly its superficial lobe. It is also referred to as a <strong>mixed tumor</strong> due to its combination of epithelial and mesenchymal components. This tumor most commonly occurs in <strong>females</strong> around the age of 40.</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Painless</strong>, <strong>slow-growing swelling</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lobulated, firm, <strong>variable in consistency</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Lifts the earlobe</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">No facial nerve involvement</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Curtain sign positive</strong> - cannot be moved above the zygomatic bone.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In case of deep lobe involvement, tonsils, and faucial pillars will be pushed medially.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Features of <strong>malignancy</strong> - a <strong>sudden</strong> <strong>increase</strong> in size, <strong>ulceration</strong> of the overlying skin,<strong> facial nerve palsy </strong>(involvement of facial nerve), painful swelling, and<strong> restriction of jaw movements</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Fine needle aspiration cytology<strong> (FNAC) </strong>is the<strong> investigation of choice</strong> in diagnosing pleomorphic adenoma. <strong>Open biopsy</strong> is <strong>contraindicated</strong> due to the chances of <strong>injury to the facial nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p>All tumors of the superficial lobe of the parotid gland should be <strong>managed</strong> by <strong>superficial parotidectomy</strong>. <strong>Radical parotidectomy </strong>is performed for <strong>malignant pleomorphic adenoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Adamantinoma</strong> is a low-grade malignant tumor that usually presents with pre-tibial pain and swelling since it most commonly affects the <strong>tibial diaphysis.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B:  <strong>Dermoid cysts</strong> present as <strong>firm painless subcutaneous nodules</strong> and are of 4 types:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sequestration dermoid - sequestration of epithelium during embryological development.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tubulodermoid - accumulation of secretions from ectodermal tube/duct</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Implantation dermoid - implantation of the epidermis due to trauma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Teratomatous dermoid - having all 3 germ layers (ecto, meso, and endoderm)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Sebaceous cysts</strong> are <strong>retention</strong> cysts of the sebaceous glands that present as smooth, <strong>non-tender</strong>, <strong>non-transillumination</strong> swelling with a <strong>punctum</strong> (in 70% of the patients). </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4167","text":"A 45-year-old woman presen‌ted with painless swellin‍g in front of the left ea‍r for the past 2 years. O​n examination, her left e‍arlobe was lifted and the​ curtain sign was positiv​e. Which of the following​ is false about the condi​tion?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman presented with painless swelling in front of the left ear for the past 2 years. On examination, her left earlobe was lifted and the curtain sign was positive. Which of the following is false about the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Treatment of choice is tot‌al radical parotidectomy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Investigation of choice is‌ FNAC","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Open biopsy is contraindic‌ated","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malignant form shows LMN f‌acial palsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Treatment of choice is total radical parotidectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Treatment of choice is total radical parotidectomy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>pleomorphic</strong> <strong>adenoma</strong>. The treatment of choice is <strong>superficial parotidectomy</strong>. </p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Painless, slow-growing swelling</li>\n<li>Lobulated, firm, elastic-to-hard in consistency</li>\n<li>Lift the ear lobe</li>\n<li>Free from deeper structures</li>\n<li>With <strong>no facial nerve </strong>involvement</li>\n<li><strong>Curtain sign positive</strong> - cannot be moved above the zygomatic bone</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pleomorphic adenoma involves the <strong>superficial</strong> <strong>lobe</strong> of parotid gland and should be managed by <strong>superficial parotidectomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Types of Parotidectomy</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If only the superficial lobe is involved: \n<ul>\n<li><strong>Superficial parotidectomy</strong> - only <strong>superficial</strong> <strong>lobe</strong> removed. Usually done in benign conditions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>If both lobes are involved:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Total conservative parotidectomy</strong> - <strong>both</strong> <strong>lobes</strong> are removed but the facial nerve is preserved.</li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><strong>Total radical parotidectomy</strong> - <strong>both lobes</strong> and <strong>facial nerve</strong> removed.</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sometimes it may undergo malignant transformation. The malignant form is <strong>mixed malignant tumor</strong> or <strong>carcinoma ex pleomorphic adenoma</strong> (most aggressive salivary malignancy).</p>\n<p>Signs of malignant transformation</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A rapid increase in size</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pain and ulceration</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Facial nerve involvement (LMN)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lymph node involvement</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Restricted jaw movements</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7875","text":"Technetium-99m pertechneta‌te scintigraphy done for ‍a patient with a chronic ‍painless facial swelling ​is shown below. Which of ‍the following statements ​is false regarding this t​umor?","raw_text":"Technetium-99m pertechnetate scintigraphy done for a patient with a chronic painless facial swelling is shown below. Which of the following statements is false regarding this tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"This tumor has no malignan‌t potential","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superficial parotidectomy ‌is the treatment of choic‍e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Histology shows a double l‌ayer of epithelial cells ‍with dense lymphoid strom‍a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is the most common unil‌ateral benign tumor of th‍e parotid gland","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is the most common unilateral benign tumor of the parotid gland","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/37cdf34549194a59ae54579629c81787.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d08989b8841344c19bcd5c5c294d9857x510x501.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is the most common unilateral benign tumor of the parotid gland</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and image are suggestive of <strong>Warthin’s tumor</strong>. These tumors appear as <strong>hot spots </strong>on radionuclide scans because of the high mitochondrial content in oncocytes that incorporate technetium-99m. The <strong>most common benign tumor</strong> of the parotid gland is <strong>pleomorphic adenoma</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Warthin’s tumor</strong> or papillary cystadenoma lymphomatosum usually presents as a unilateral tumor.<strong>  </strong>It is usually seen in the <strong>lower poles</strong> of the parotid and is commonly associated with <strong>smoking. </strong>It occurs most often in <strong>elderly males</strong>. It has <strong>no malignant potential</strong>.   </p>\n<p>Histology shows tumor cells lined by a <strong>double layer </strong>of neoplastic epithelial<strong> cells </strong>resting on the<strong> dense lymphoid stroma</strong>. <strong>FNAC</strong> is the preferred investigation. <strong>Superficial parotidectomy</strong> is the treatment of choice.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Both pleomorphic adenoma and Warthin's tumors commonly present as unilateral tumors. A bilateral presentation is usually rare. However in rare cases, if there is a bilateral benign salivary tumor, the tumor is most likely to be a Warthin's tumor.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4125","text":"Match the following Tumor ‌Description 1. Godwin’s t‍umor a. Malignant salivar‍y gland tumor 2. Eskimoma​ b. Benign lymphoepitheli‍al tumor 3. Kuttner’s tum​or c. Hotspot on 99Tc sci​ntigraphy 4. Oncocytoma d​. Chronic sclerosing sial​adenitis","raw_text":"<p>Match the following</p>\n<table style=\"height: 235px; width: 385px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.816px;\"> Tumor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229.184px;\"> Description</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.816px;\">\n<p>1. Godwin’s tumor</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229.184px;\">\n<p> a. Malignant salivary gland tumor</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.816px;\">\n<p>2. Eskimoma</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229.184px;\"> b. Benign lymphoepithelial tumor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.816px;\">\n<p>3. Kuttner’s tumor</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229.184px;\"> c. Hotspot on 99Tc scintigraphy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 140.816px;\">\n<p>4. Oncocytoma</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 229.184px;\"> d. Chronic sclerosing sialadenitis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-b, 2-d,‌ 3-a, 4-c","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-b, 2-a,‌ 3-d, 4-c","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-a, 2-b,‌ 3-c, 4-d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-c, 2-a,‌ 3-d, 4-b","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-b, 2-a, 3-d, 4-c","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-b, 2-a, 3-d, 4-c</strong></p><table style=\"height: 96px;\" width=\"420\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 137.033px;\">Tumor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 269.367px;\">Description</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 137.033px;\">Godwin’s tumor </td>\n<td style=\"width: 269.367px;\">Benign lymphoepithelial tumor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 137.033px;\">Eskimoma </td>\n<td style=\"width: 269.367px;\">Malignant salivary gland tumor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 137.033px;\">Kuttner’s tumor </td>\n<td style=\"width: 269.367px;\">Chronic sclerosing sialadenitis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 137.033px;\">oncocytoma </td>\n<td style=\"width: 269.367px;\">Hotspot on 99Tc scintigraphy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> <strong>Godwin’s tumor </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> <strong>Benign lymphoepithelial tumor</strong> of the salivary gland. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">It is associated with CMV. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">&lt; 5% shows malignancy - known as <strong>Eskimoma</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Kuttner’s</strong> tumor is <strong>chronic sclerosing sialadenitis</strong> of the submandibular gland.</p>\n<p><strong>Oncocytoma</strong> (oxyphil adenoma) - </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Usually benign, originating from oncocytes.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Grossly - tan-colored small, well-circumscribed, and encapsulated.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Microscopically - large oncocytes with swollen granular cytoplasm.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Appear as a <strong>hotspot on Technetium-99m pertechnetate scintigraphy</strong> due to an abundance of mitochondria.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7877","text":"An elderly woman presents ‌with recent onset facial ‍asymmetry and a lump over‍ the roof of the mouth fo​r the past 1 year. On exa‍mination, a solitary eryt​hematous swelling with di​ffuse borders is seen ove​r the left hard palate. B​iopsy of the lesion revea‌ls a swiss cheese pattern​. Which of the following ‍conditions is she likely ​to have?","raw_text":"An elderly woman presents with recent onset facial asymmetry and a lump over the roof of the mouth for the past 1 year. On examination, a solitary erythematous swelling with diffuse borders is seen over the left hard palate. Biopsy of the lesion reveals a swiss cheese pattern. Which of the following conditions is she likely to have?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malignant ‌mixed tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mucoepidermo‌id carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenoid cyst‌ic carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adenoca‌rcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Adenoid cystic carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Adenoid cystic carcinoma</strong></p><p>The given scenario and biopsy finding is suggestive of <strong>adenoid cystic carcinoma</strong>.  Facial asymmetry points to<strong> </strong>the<strong> perineural invasion</strong> of the tumor into the facial nerve, which is characteristic of adenoid cystic carcinoma.</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>second most common</strong> malignant tumor of the parotid gland. It is a slow-growing tumor most commonly involving the parotid and submandibular glands. Histology shows a <strong>swiss cheese pattern</strong>. The <strong>tubular type</strong> has the <strong>best prognosis</strong>, while the <strong>solid type</strong> has the <strong>worst</strong>. </p>\n<p>It has a deceptively benign histologic appearance characterized by indolent, locally invasive growth with a high propensity for local recurrence and distant metastasis. The most common site of <strong>distant metastasis</strong> is the<strong> lung</strong>. Treatment involves <strong>removal </strong>of the<strong> gross tumor</strong>, with <strong>radiation</strong> therapy for the <strong>microscopic</strong> disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7876","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common malignant‍ tumor involving the paro‍tid gland?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common malignant tumor involving the parotid gland?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pleomorph‌ic adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mucoepidermo‌id carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Adenoid cyst‌ic carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adenoca‌rcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mucoepidermoid carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mucoepidermoid carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Mucoepidermoid carcinoma</strong> is the most common <strong>malignant tumor</strong>,<strong> </strong>while <strong>pleomorphic adenoma </strong>is the most common<strong> benign tumor </strong>of the <strong>parotid gland.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Histological classification and incidence of tumors of salivary glands:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 209px; width: 544px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Benign Tumors</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 81px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Incidence</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.25px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Malignant tumors</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 83.75px;\"><strong>Incidence</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; text-align: center;\">Pleomorphic adenoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81px; text-align: center;\">50%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.25px; text-align: center;\">Mucoepidermoid carcinoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 83.75px; text-align: center;\">15%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; text-align: center;\">Warthin’s tumor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81px; text-align: center;\">5–10%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.25px; text-align: center;\">Adenocarcinoma (NOS)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 83.75px; text-align: center;\">10%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; text-align: center;\">Oncocytoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81px; text-align: center;\">1%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.25px; text-align: center;\">Acinic cell carcinoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 83.75px; text-align: center;\">5%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; text-align: center;\">Other adenomas</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81px; text-align: center;\">5–10%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.25px; text-align: center;\">Adenoid cystic carcinoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 83.75px; text-align: center;\">5%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; text-align: center;\">Basal cell adenoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81px; text-align: center;\"> 1-2%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.25px; text-align: center;\">Malignant mixed tumor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 83.75px; text-align: center;\">3–5%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; text-align: center;\">Canalicular adenoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81px; text-align: center;\">1% </td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.25px; text-align: center;\">Squamous cell carcinoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 83.75px; text-align: center;\">1%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px; text-align: center;\">Ductal papillomas</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81px; text-align: center;\"> -</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.25px; text-align: center;\">Other carcinomas</td>\n<td style=\"width: 83.75px; text-align: center;\">2%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>NOS: Not otherwise specified.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7879","text":"As a 1st year resident ass‌isting in a superficial p‍arotidectomy, you have be‍en asked to identify the ​facial nerve trunk. All o‍f the following structure​s can be used for its loc​alization except:","raw_text":"As a 1st year resident assisting in a superficial parotidectomy, you have been asked to identify the facial nerve trunk.  All of the following structures can be used for its localization except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior belly of digastr‌ic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conley's‌ pointer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior belly of omohyoid‌ muscle","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mastoid‌ process","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inferior belly of omohyoid muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dbb63d857ffa40afb8bf53f3a00baad3x1280x1401.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inferior belly of omohyoid muscle</strong></p><p>The <strong>inferior belly of the omohyoid muscle</strong> is <strong>not</strong> <strong>used</strong> as an anatomical landmark for the identification of the<strong> facial nerve trunk</strong>.</p>\n<p>There are two methods for <strong>localization of the facial nerve trunk: </strong></p>\n<p><strong>1. Anterograde method: </strong>In this method, the facial nerve trunk is identified based on the anatomical landmarks after it exits from the stylomastoid foramen. The anatomical landmarks include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Conley's pointer </strong>is the inferior portion of the cartilaginous canal. The facial nerve trunk lies 1 cm deep and inferior to its tip.</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior belly of digastric-</strong> The facial nerve lies superior to the upper border of the muscle.</li>\n<li><strong>Mastoid process- </strong>Upon drilling it, the nerve is identified proximally.</li>\n<li><strong>Squamotympanic fissure</strong> is a fissure in the temporal bone, which runs from TMJ to the tympanic cavity. The facial nerve runs through this fissure.</li>\n<li><strong>Styloid process-</strong> The facial nerve lies superficial to it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Retrograde method: </strong>In this method, the main branches of the facial nerve trunk are identified and are then traced proximally until the main tunk is identified. Some of the main branches that are commonly used for tracing the facial nerve trunk include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Buccal branch</strong> lies in relation to the parotid gland</li>\n<li><strong>Marginal mandibular branch</strong> lies in relation to the facial vessels</li>\n<li><strong>Temporal branch</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given image points to the facial nerve (marked by arrow).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7903","text":"A patient is scheduled to ‌undergo a total parotidec‍tomy with selective node ‍dissection of levels Ib, ​IIa, IIb, and III. Which ‍of the following groups o​f lymph nodes will be rem​oved during the procedure​? Submental Submandibular​ Middle Jugular Lower Jug‌ular Pretracheal","raw_text":"<p>A patient is scheduled to undergo a total parotidectomy with selective node dissection of levels Ib, IIa, IIb, and III. Which of the following groups of lymph nodes will be removed during the procedure?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Submental</li>\n<li>Submandibular</li>\n<li>Middle Jugular</li>\n<li>Lower Jugular</li>\n<li>Pretracheal</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2, ‌3 and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a‌nd 3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 3, ‌4 and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 and 3</strong></p><p>Among the given options, only <strong>submandibular</strong> (Level Ib) and <strong>middle jugular nodes</strong> (Level III) nodes will be removed. The other nodes that will be removed in the procedure will be <strong>upper jugular nodes</strong> (Level IIa and IIb).</p>\n<p>Selective neck dissection has been used in the treatment of patients with cancer of the upper aerodigestive tract (oral cavity, pharynx, larynx), cancer of the thyroid gland, cutaneous malignancies, and cancer of the salivary gland. The type of selective neck dissection used varies depending on the <strong>lymphatic drainage </strong>and<strong> metastatic pattern </strong>associated with the <strong>primary cancer</strong> site.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7872","text":"A patient with pleomorphic‌ adenoma is posted for su‍bmandibular gland excisio‍n. Which of the following​ nerves is least likely t‍o be injured during this ​procedure?","raw_text":"A patient with pleomorphic adenoma is posted for submandibular gland excision. Which of the following nerves is least likely to be injured during this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Marginal mandibular branch‌ of the facial nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lingua‌l nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoglos‌sal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior al‌veolar nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Inferior alveolar nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b2208d20ce0b4b25a667f38dea899ac8x451x715.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Inferior alveolar nerve</strong></p><p>The<strong> inferior alveolar nerve</strong> is the nerve <strong>least likely</strong> to be affected by <strong>submandibular surgery</strong>.</p>\n<p>Nerves at risk for injury during removal of the submandibular gland include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Marginal mandibular branch of the facial nerve</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lingual nerve</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypoglossal nerve</li>\n</ol>\n<p>An important landmark in <strong>submandibular gland dissection</strong> is the <strong>posterior border of the mylohyoid muscle</strong>. Once identified, it can be retracted to reveal the <strong>deep lobe</strong> of the gland. The hypoglossal nerves lie deep to the submandibular capsule and should not be damaged during intracapsular dissection. The facial artery is ligated twice during the surgery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7493","text":"Which nerve is involved in‌ Frey’s syndrome?","raw_text":"Which nerve is involved in Frey’s syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial‌ Nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oculomot‌or nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Auriculotem‌poral nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Glossophary‌ngeal nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Auriculotemporal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6089cbcee2814801af9b7c9f34f099edx1280x1508.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Auriculotemporal nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>auriculotemporal nerve</strong> (V3 nerve) is involved in <strong>Frey's syndrome</strong> (gustatory sweating).</p>\n<p><strong>Frey’s</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong> is also known as <strong>Dupuy syndrome</strong> or auriculotemporal syndrome. It commonly develops after <strong>parotid surgery</strong>. It is caused by an <strong>aberrant</strong> regeneration of <strong>post-ganglionic parasympathetic nerve fibers</strong> crossed along the <strong>sympathetic pathways</strong> to the sweat glands near the parotid region.</p>\n<p>Clinical features are due to the result of salivary stimulation (by the smell or taste of food) leading to the <strong>sweating</strong> and <strong>flushing</strong> of the <strong>periauricular</strong> <strong>region</strong> on the involved side of the face. A <strong>starch iodine test </strong>is used for <strong>diagnosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Treatment ranges from <strong>antiperspirants</strong> (in <strong>mild</strong> cases) to <strong>tympanic</strong> <strong>neurectomy</strong> or <strong>botulinum toxin</strong> injection (in <strong>severe</strong> cases). It can be prevented by incorporating careful extracapsular dissection and closure of the superficial musculoaponeurotic layer during parotidectomy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Facial nerve</strong> is the 7th cranial nerve that supplies the muscles of the face, and results in <strong>facial</strong> <strong>paralysis</strong> if injured during parotidectomy</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Oculomotor</strong> is the 3rd cranial nerve which supplies the <strong>extraocular</strong> <strong>muscles</strong>. If injured during ophthalmic surgeries, it results in ocular paralysis and the eye pointing <strong>down and out</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Glossopharyngeal</strong> nerve is the 9th cranial nerve and its injury causes <strong>loss of taste sensation</strong> from the <strong>posterior 1/3rd</strong> of the tongue. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7705","text":"After parotidectomy patien‌ts complains of loss of s‍ensation while shaving. W‍hich nerve is most likely​ affected?","raw_text":"After parotidectomy patients complains of loss of sensation while shaving. Which nerve is most likely affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Auriculotem‌poral nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Great auri‌cular nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Facial‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mandibul‌ar nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Great auricular nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c9a0690eb72a443ca860da60760bcb6bx1280x1308.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Great auricular nerve</strong></p><p>After parotid surgery, the nerve involved in the loss of sensation while shaving is the <strong>great auricular nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p>The great auricular nerve (GAN) originates from the second and third nerve roots of the cervical plexus (<strong>C2-C3</strong>). It supplies the skin over the lower <strong>pinna, pre-auricular region, and angle of the mandible</strong> (image below). The main trunk passes over the sternocleidomastoid and into the anterosuperior portion of the parotid glands and branches into the anterior and posterior GAN. <strong>The anterior branch of GAN</strong> is often injured during parotid surgery during the <strong>initial elevation of the skin flap</strong>.</p>\n<p>When GAN is injured, it can cause reduced sensations in the earlobe, pre-auricular skin, and the angle of the mandible that can interfere with habits such as shaving, wearing earrings, etc.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: The auriculotemporal nerve, a branch of the mandibular nerve (V3 of trigeminal) supplies the posterior part of the <strong>temple</strong>, <strong>tragus</strong>, and adjoining auricle. It does not supply the area where a person is likely to shave. However, the injury to the auriculotemporal nerve resulting in aberrant regeneration would result in <strong>Frey's Syndrome</strong> (gustatory sweating).</p>\n<p>Option C: Though the facial nerve is the most common nerve affected during parotidectomy, any damage would result in <strong>facial paralysis</strong> and not loss of sensation of the face.</p>\n<p>Option D: The mandibular nerve is a branch of the trigeminal nerve (cranial nerve V) that supplies the skin over the mandible, lower lip, and cheek. However, they are <strong>not injured</strong> during parotidectomy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7890","text":"At what age should the con‌genital defect (shown in ‍the image below) be ideal‍ly repaired?","raw_text":"At what age should the congenital defect (shown in the image below) be ideally repaired?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3-6 m‌onths","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"6-8 m‌onths","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"8-10 ‌months","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10-12 ‌months","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 3-6 months","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3e6b22e7b1f1474e9c120d82a6ff8f3d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 3-6 months</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>unilateral cleft lip</strong>. Repair of cleft lip (primary palate) along with associated nasal deformities is done at <strong>3–6 months of age</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7892","text":"A child with a cleft lip i‌s posted for surgery. Whi‍ch of the following metho‍ds is not used to repair ​this condition?","raw_text":"A child with a cleft lip is posted for surgery. Which of the following methods is not used to repair this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Le Muserie‌r’s method","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tennison‌’s method","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Millard’‌s method","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wardill’‌s method","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Wardill’s method","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Wardill’s method</strong></p><p><strong>Wardill’s method</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a method of cleft lip repair. It is a technique for <strong>hard palate closure</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Cleft-lip repair techniques</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Millard’s rotation advancement technique</strong> (most common)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Thompson</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Le Mesurier</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tennison–Randall</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Techniques of <strong>hard palate closure</strong> include the use of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Unipedicled hard palate mucoperiosteal flaps as in the Wardill-Veau-Kilner repair </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bipedicled hard palate mucoperiosteal flaps as in the von Langenbeck repair</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7878","text":"Which among the following ‌is an oral cavity lesion ‍with a high-risk of malig‍nant transformation?","raw_text":"Which among the following is an oral cavity lesion with a  high-risk of malignant transformation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral lich‌en planus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Discoid lupus erythematosu‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Discoid keratosis congenit‌a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Speckled er‌ythroplakia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Speckled erythroplakia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bee5fccfb3e5438a8b5044a08da4d69dx325x400.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Speckled erythroplakia</strong></p><p><strong>Speckled erythroplakia</strong> has<strong> </strong>a high risk of malignant transformation.</p>\n<p><strong>Malignancy risk:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>High-risk lesions </strong></p>\n<p>Erythroplakia (homogenous/speckled) </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> Seen as <strong>red velvety</strong> lesions</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> <strong>Speckled</strong> type is the most  <strong>aggressive</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> The treatment of choice is <strong>surgical</strong> <strong>ablation </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Proliferative verrucous leucoplakia</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A <strong>white patchy</strong> lesion that<strong> cannot be rubbed off</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Speckled leucoplakia has an erythematous base</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The treatment of choice is excision</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Chronic hyperplastic candidiasis</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>White patches</strong> that <strong>can be rubbed off</strong> with a <strong>hyperaemic</strong> <strong>border</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Management includes prolonged treatment with <strong>topical</strong> <strong>antifungals</strong> or <strong>systemic</strong> <strong>antifungal</strong> therapy. If no improvement or if there are any signs of dysplasia, <strong>surgical excision</strong> is done.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Medium risk lesions</strong></p>\n<p>Oral submucous fibrosis</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Hypersensitive</strong> <strong>reaction</strong> to betel nut product.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Other risk factors are - tobacco, chili, vitamin deficiency </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Clinical features - trismus, ankyloglossia, soreness, and burning sensation in the mouth.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Management includes antioxidants, intralesional triamcinolone, and surgical excision.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Syphilitic glossitis</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Associated with tertiary syphilis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Low-risk lesions</strong></p>\n<p>Oral lichen planus </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Reticulate fine lace-like pattern.</li>\n<li>Management is <strong>topical</strong> <strong>corticosteroids</strong>.   </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Discoid lupus erythematosus <br/>Discoid keratosis congenita</p>\n<p><strong>Speckled erythroplakia:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4173","text":"A 35-year-old man presente‌d with complaints of diff‍iculty in opening his mou‍th, restricted tongue mov​ements, and a burning sen‍sation in the mouth while​ eating food. He is a smo​ker and chews pan masala.​ On examination, scarring​ is present in the oral c‌avity. What is the most l​ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man presented with complaints of difficulty in opening his mouth, restricted tongue movements, and a burning sensation in the mouth while eating food. He is a smoker and chews pan masala. On examination, scarring is present in the oral cavity. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plummer-Vin‌son syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proliferative verrucous le‌ucoplakia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oral submuc‌ous fibrosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Erythr‌oplakia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Oral submucous fibrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Oral submucous fibrosis</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis is <strong>oral submucous fibrosis</strong>. It is a <strong>premalignant</strong> lesion of the oral cavity, in which fibrous bands are formed beneath the oral mucosa.<br style=\"font-weight: 400;\"/>Risk factors are</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Betel nut</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Chilli</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tobacco</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Vitamin deficiency</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical features include <strong>ankyloglossia</strong>, <strong>trismus</strong>, <strong>soreness</strong>, and a <strong>burning</strong> sensation in the mouth while eating.</p>\n<p>Management includes <strong>intralesional</strong> <strong>steroids</strong>, vitamin supplements, and <strong>surgical</strong> <strong>excision</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Plummer-Vinson syndrome</strong> is characterized by post cricoid web that is associated with iron deficiency anemia, glossitis, and koilonychia. It usually presents as <strong>dysphagia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Proliferative verrucous leucoplakia</strong> appears as a white patchy lesion, that cannot be rubbed off.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Erythroplakia</strong> is seen as bright <strong>red</strong> <strong>velvety</strong> <strong>plaque</strong> in the oral cavity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5964","text":"A 57-year-old male patient‌ presents to the surgery ‍OPD, with a 7cm lesion ov‍er the left buccal mucosa​ and difficulty opening t‍he mouth. On examination,​ the right cervical lymph​ node is 4cm in size with​ overlying skin involveme​nt. What is the TNM stagi‌ng of this lesion?","raw_text":"A 57-year-old male patient presents to the surgery OPD, with a 7cm lesion over the left buccal mucosa and difficulty opening the mouth. On examination, the right cervical lymph node is 4cm in size with overlying skin involvement. What is the TNM staging of this lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T4aN‌2bMx","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T4bN‌3aMx","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T4bN‌3bMx","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"T4aN‌2cMx","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. T4bN3bMx","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. T4bN3bMx</strong></p><p>The TNM staging in this patient is <strong>T4bN3bMX</strong>. A 7-cm primary tumor in the oral cavity with difficulty in opening mouth denotes a <strong>locally advanced tumor</strong>: <strong>T4b</strong>. <strong>Contralateral cervical lymph node</strong> involvement with skin involvement (<strong>extranodal extension-positive): N3b</strong></p>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>Here the skin invasion corresponds to ENE positive, and hence, the N staging is N3b instead of N2c (contralateral lymph nodes, &lt;6 cm and ENE‐negative).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7894","text":"Identify the given incisio‌n used for maxillectomy.","raw_text":"Identify the given incision used for maxillectomy.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crile's ‌incision","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Martin's‌ incision","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MacFee ‌incision","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Weber–Fergu‌son incision","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Weber–Ferguson incision","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/02c499fc092a4a38a02d2819ae1988d6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3308698927bf4eb5bd52d3e6f2af0089x1280x3676.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Weber–Ferguson incision</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>Weber–Ferguson incision</strong> which is used to access the <strong>floor of the maxillary sinus</strong> in the case of <strong>maxillary carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The rest of the options are incisions used for modified radical neck dissection (MRND).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7893","text":"What is this approach used‌ in the treatment of oral‍ cancer?","raw_text":"What is this approach used in the treatment of oral cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lip-split‌ approach","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Visor a‌pproach","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Wedge r‌esection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Commando ‌operation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Visor approach","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/39bdd26fe9c34c8db8034a0ddb6dd20c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Visor approach</strong></p><p>The given image shows the <strong>visor approach.</strong></p>\n<p>In the visor approach, an incision is made extending from one end of the mandible to the opposite side and subplatysmal flaps are elevated superiorly over the mandible (like a visor of a helmet). This approach provides a <strong>wide exposure</strong> to remove larger lesions involving the anterior oral cavity that is not amenable to a peroral approach. This <strong>avoids</strong> a <strong>lip-splitting incision </strong>but<strong> </strong>has the disadvantage of anesthesia of chin/lower lip (due to the sacrifice of submental nerves).</p>\n<p>The surgical approach selected for excision of oral cavity tumors depends on the size of the tumor, location (anterior vs posterior), proximity to bone (mandible or maxilla), and the need to perform neck dissection. The most commonly employed approaches are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Peroral</strong> - accessible early-stage tumors</li>\n<li><strong>Lower cheek flap</strong> - t<span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_1\" title=\"bbox 0 20 98 66; x_wconf 48\">umors</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_2\" title=\"bbox 109 31 230 61; x_wconf 92\">requiring</span> partial mandibulectomy<span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_9\" title=\"bbox 633 30 761 54; x_wconf 92\"> or</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_11\" title=\"bbox 809 30 877 61; x_wconf 92\">large</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_12\" title=\"bbox 888 30 1080 61; x_wconf 92\">posterolateral</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_13\" title=\"bbox 1090 33 1191 54; x_wconf 91\">tumors</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_14\" title=\"bbox 1200 30 1255 54; x_wconf 93\">that</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_15\" title=\"bbox 1265 33 1361 54; x_wconf 92\">cannot</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_16\" title=\"bbox 1372 30 1404 54; x_wconf 92\">be</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_17\" title=\"bbox 1415 30 1547 54; x_wconf 91\">accessed</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_18\" title=\"bbox 1557 31 1596 54; x_wconf 92\">via</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_19\" title=\"bbox 1607 36 1621 54; x_wconf 92\">a</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_20\" title=\"bbox 1633 30 1728 61; x_wconf 93\">peroral</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_21\" title=\"bbox 1739 30 1869 61; x_wconf 91\">approach</span></li>\n<li><strong>Upper cheek flap</strong> - tumors requiring partial maxillectomy or those involving the hard palate, upper gum, upper gingivobuccal sulcus and anterior soft palate</li>\n<li><strong>Mandibulotomy</strong> - l<span class=\"ocr_line\" id=\"line_1_1\" title=\"bbox 26 13 1892 45; baseline 0 -7; x_size 31; x_descenders 7; x_ascenders 6\"><span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_1\" title=\"bbox 26 15 115 45; x_wconf 89\">arger</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_2\" title=\"bbox 125 14 223 38; x_wconf 89\">tumors</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_3\" title=\"bbox 233 13 262 38; x_wconf 92\">of</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_4\" title=\"bbox 269 14 313 38; x_wconf 92\">the</span> <strong><span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_5\" title=\"bbox 324 15 450 44; x_wconf 91\">posterior</span></strong> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_6\" title=\"bbox 459 14 510 38; x_wconf 93\">oral</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_7\" title=\"bbox 521 15 606 45; x_wconf 91\">cavity,</span> </span><span class=\"ocr_line\" id=\"line_1_2\" title=\"bbox 24 63 1919 95; baseline 0 -7; x_size 31; x_descenders 7; x_ascenders 6\"><span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_30\" title=\"bbox 354 67 454 88; x_wconf 90\">tumors</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_31\" title=\"bbox 464 64 519 88; x_wconf 93\">that</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_32\" title=\"bbox 530 64 626 88; x_wconf 93\">involve</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_33\" title=\"bbox 634 64 678 88; x_wconf 92\">the</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_34\" title=\"bbox 690 64 755 88; x_wconf 92\">base</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_35\" title=\"bbox 765 63 794 88; x_wconf 93\">of</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_36\" title=\"bbox 801 64 845 88; x_wconf 92\">the</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_37\" title=\"bbox 854 67 958 95; x_wconf 90\">tongue or</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_38\" title=\"bbox 969 64 1122 95; x_wconf 92\">pharyngeal</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_39\" title=\"bbox 1133 64 1187 88; x_wconf 92\">wall.</span></span></li>\n<li><strong>Visor flap</strong> - l<span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_1\" title=\"bbox 26 31 115 61; x_wconf 91\">arger</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_2\" title=\"bbox 125 30 223 54; x_wconf 91\">lesions</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_3\" title=\"bbox 233 29 262 54; x_wconf 92\">of</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_4\" title=\"bbox 269 30 314 54; x_wconf 93\">the</span> <strong><span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_5\" title=\"bbox 324 30 432 54; x_wconf 92\">anterior</span></strong> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_6\" title=\"bbox 442 30 492 54; x_wconf 93\">oral</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_7\" title=\"bbox 503 30 584 61; x_wconf 92\">cavity</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_8\" title=\"bbox 595 30 716 61; x_wconf 93\">involving</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_9\" title=\"bbox 726 30 770 54; x_wconf 93\">the</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_10\" title=\"bbox 781 30 889 54; x_wconf 91\">anterior</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_11\" title=\"bbox 898 29 962 54; x_wconf 91\">floor</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_12\" title=\"bbox 972 29 1000 54; x_wconf 93\">of</span> the <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_13\" title=\"bbox 1010 30 1098 54; x_wconf 92\">mouth</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_14\" title=\"bbox 1109 30 1159 54; x_wconf 92\">and</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_15\" title=\"bbox 1171 30 1247 54; x_wconf 92\">lower</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_16\" title=\"bbox 1256 36 1316 61; x_wconf 91\">gum</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_17\" title=\"bbox 1326 30 1381 54; x_wconf 92\">that</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_18\" title=\"bbox 1391 36 1434 54; x_wconf 93\">are</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_19\" title=\"bbox 1445 33 1488 54; x_wconf 92\">not</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_20\" title=\"bbox 1499 30 1636 54; x_wconf 91\">amenable</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_21\" title=\"bbox 1645 33 1672 54; x_wconf 92\">to</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_22\" title=\"bbox 1683 36 1697 54; x_wconf 93\">a</span> <span class=\"ocrx_word\" id=\"word_1_23\" title=\"bbox 1709 30 1804 61; x_wconf 92\">peroral approach</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Commando operation</strong> is the combined mandibular resection and neck node dissection. It is used mainly for <strong>carcinoma of the tongue</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7888","text":"You will use an Abbe-Estla‌nder flap for the reconst‍ruction of which of the f‍ollowing structures?","raw_text":"You will use an Abbe-Estlander flap for the reconstruction of which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"L‌ip","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pal‌ate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Floor of ‌the mouth","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ton‌gue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lip","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/24e91c1be89c44e8934f9db1e616f2e2x512x155.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lip</strong></p><p><strong>Abbé- Estlander flaps</strong> are used for the <strong>reconstruction of the lip</strong>.</p>\n<p>They are lip switch flaps based on the<strong> sublabial or superior labial artery</strong>. When the defect involves the <strong>commissure</strong>, the <strong>Estlander flap</strong> is used, and the <strong>Abbé flap</strong> is used for more <strong>midline defects.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7900","text":"A woman from Himachal Prad‌esh, presented with compl‍aints of neck swelling fo‍r the past 1 year. On exa​mination, a firm, non-ten‍der, pulsatile mass was p​alpated on the left side ​of the neck. The mass is ​mobile from side to side,​ but not up and down. A c‌arotid angiogram of the p​atient is shown below. Wh‍ich of the following is f​alse regarding this condi​tion?","raw_text":"A woman from Himachal Pradesh,  presented with complaints of neck swelling for the past 1 year. On examination, a firm, non-tender, pulsatile mass was palpated on the left side of the neck. The mass is mobile from side to side, but not up and down. A carotid angiogram of the patient is shown below. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is usua‌lly benign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Shamblin classification is‌ used for staging","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It arises from neuroendocr‌ine tissue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"FNAC is the initial invest‌igation of choice","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. FNAC is the initial investigation of choice","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6ab044fdc0764f0fa53c404b6276dbb0.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d4ffbbde197a43a280fad5e7d83ac50bx510x618.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. FNAC is the initial investigation of choice</strong></p><p>The given scenario and carotid angiogram showing splaying of the bifurcation of the carotids, also known as <strong>lyre sign</strong> points to a diagnosis of <strong>chemodectoma</strong>. Fine-needle aspiration cytology (FNAC) <strong>is contraindicated</strong> when a chemodectoma is suspected.</p>\n<p>Chemodectoma is also known as <strong>carotid body tumor</strong> (aka potato tumor). It is a<strong> benign</strong> tumor of the <strong>neuroendocrine tissue </strong>of the carotid body. It has a higher incidence at<strong> high altitudes</strong> because of <strong>chronic hypoxia, </strong>which leads to carotid body hyperplasia.</p>\n<p>The tumor presents as a slowly enlarging mass that is firm, rubbery, pulsatile, mobile from side to side but not up and down. A bruit may be heard. Family history can be present in 10% of the cases. </p>\n<p><strong>Shamblin classification</strong> is used for staging carotid body tumors.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7901","text":"The most common site of br‌anchial cyst is ______.","raw_text":"The most common site of branchial cyst is ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Junction of upper and midd‌le third of the sternomas‍toid muscle","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Junction of middle and low‌er third of the sternomas‍toid muscle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Middle of the sternomastoi‌d muscle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Between the two heads of t‌he sternomastoid muscle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Junction of upper and middle third of the sternomastoid muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0e958c1ce5034e74b88ccc31e90e2bc3x900x1200.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bbfe56269aff4879acd49324704d5815x600x523.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Junction of upper and middle third of the sternomastoid muscle</strong></p><p>The most common site of a<strong> branchial cyst</strong> is at the <strong>junction of the upper and middle third </strong>of the sternomastoid muscle at its anterior border.</p>\n<p>The below image shows a large right-sided branchial cyst.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Branchial cyst</strong> develops due to the persistence of the <strong>cervical sinus</strong>. The cervical sinus is formed when the 2nd arch mesoderm overgrows the succeeding arches and overhangs them as shown in the image. The <strong>space</strong> between the overhanging <strong>2nd pharyngeal arch </strong>and the <strong>3rd, 4th, and 6th arches</strong> is called the <strong>cervical sinus</strong>. The cavity of the cervical sinus is <strong>lined by</strong> <strong>ectoderm</strong> and normally gets <strong>obliterated.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Branchial cysts</strong> are usually lined by squamous epithelium and contain thick, turbid fluid full of <strong>cholesterol crystals. </strong>It is fluctuant and may transilluminate when it contains clear fluid.</p>\n<p>A <strong>branchial fistula</strong> may be acquired or congenital. <strong>The congenital fistula </strong>is seen at the <strong>junction of the middle and lower third</strong> of the sternomastoid (option B). An <strong>acquired fistula</strong> is usually seen in cases of incomplete surgery for the branchial cyst or spontaneous rupture, and it is seen at the <strong>same site as the cyst</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7906","text":"To which group of lymph no‌des does a tumor limited ‍to the glottis of the lar‍ynx spread commonly?","raw_text":"To which group of lymph nodes does a tumor limited to the glottis of the larynx spread commonly?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pre-tr‌acheal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pre-la‌ryngeal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Upper ‌jugular","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No‌ne","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. None","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. None</strong></p><p>Tumors <strong>limited to the glottis</strong> usually <strong>do not spread</strong> to any <strong>lymph nodes</strong>, as there are no lymphatics in the vocal cords. However, if the tumor <strong>invades the supra- </strong>or <strong>the infra-glottic spaces</strong>, lymph node <strong>spread</strong> can occur. </p>\n<p>The most common malignant tumor of the larynx is <strong>squamous cell carcinoma.</strong> <strong>Glottis</strong> is the most common site of laryngeal cancer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0213","text":"Radical neck dissection sp‌ares which of the followi‍ng structures?","raw_text":"Radical neck dissection spares which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Digastri‌c muscle","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sternocleidomastoid muscle‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spinal acce‌ssory nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ipsilateral cervical lymph‌ nodes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Digastric muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Digastric muscle</strong></p><p>Digastric muscle is spared in radical neck dissection (RND). RND is used in the treatment of malignant lesions of the neck with local spread or metastasis. </p>\n<p>In 1905, Crile described <strong>RND</strong> where<strong> Level I-V LN</strong>, sternocleidomastoid <strong>(SCM)</strong>, internal jugular vein<strong> (IJV)</strong>, and <strong>XI cranial nerve</strong> (spinal accessory nerve) are <strong>removed</strong>.</p>\n<p>In 1962, Suarez described modified RND<strong> (MRND)</strong> where <strong>Level I-V LNs</strong> are <strong>removed</strong> and the following are <strong>preserved</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">MRND I - XI cranial nerve </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">MRND II -  XI cranial nerve and IJV</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">MRND III - XI cranial nerve, IJV, and SCM</li>\n</ol>\n<p><br/>Note: RND with an additional LN/muscle/nerve is called extended RND.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7904","text":"A patient is posted under ‌your unit for type 2 modi‍fied radical neck dissect‍ion. Which of the followi​ng structures will you pr‍eserve during this proced​ure? 1. Internal jugular ​vein 2. Upper jugular lym​ph nodes 3. Sternocleidom​astoid muscle 4. Posterio‌r triangle nodes 5. Spina​l accessory nerve","raw_text":"<p>A patient is posted under your unit for type 2 modified radical neck dissection. Which of the following structures will you preserve during this procedure?</p>\n<p>1. Internal jugular vein<br/>2. Upper jugular lymph nodes<br/>3. Sternocleidomastoid muscle<br/>4. Posterior triangle nodes<br/>5. Spinal accessory nerve</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onl‌y 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 &amp;a‌mp; 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 &amp;a‌mp; 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2,3 &amp;‌amp; 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 & 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 &amp; 5</strong></p><p>In<strong> type 2 </strong>modified radical neck dissection<strong> (MRND), </strong>internal jugular vein and spinal accessory nerve will be preserved.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"QbcuFbUK","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"46_lungs_and_pleura","title":"46.Lungs And Pleura","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB4539","text":"A patient is asked to do i‌ncentive spirometry after​ his surgery. Which of th‍e following muscles would‌ he use while expiring in​to the device shown?","raw_text":"A patient is asked to do incentive spirometry after his surgery. Which of the following muscles would he use while expiring into the device shown?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"External i‌ntercostal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diap‌hragm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal i‌ntercostal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rhom‌boids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Internal intercostal","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c0/Incentive_spirometer2.JPG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Internal intercostal</strong></p><p>The <strong>internal intercostal muscles</strong> are the <strong>muscles of expiration. </strong></p>\n<div class=\"MBtdbb\" data-ved=\"2ahUKEwjp4OWy9rLzAhWIfn0KHfPRBTwQ7NUEegQIHxAD\" id=\"exacc_gBZcYemgG4j99QPzo5fgAw44\">\n<div class=\"ymu2Hb\">\n<div class=\"t0bRye a2qtDc r2fjmd\" data-hveid=\"CB8QBA\" data-ved=\"2ahUKEwjp4OWy9rLzAhWIfn0KHfPRBTwQu04oAHoECB8QBA\" id=\"_gBZcYemgG4j99QPzo5fgAw47\">\n<div id=\"gBZcYemgG4j99QPzo5fgAw__10\">\n<div class=\"wDYxhc\" data-md=\"61\">\n<div aria-level=\"3\" class=\"LGOjhe\" data-attrid=\"wa:/description\" data-hveid=\"CBoQAA\" role=\"heading\"><span class=\"ILfuVd NA6bn\"><span class=\"hgKElc\">The device shown in the image is an incentive spirometer. It is a device used to help your lungs recover after surgery or a lung illness.</span></span></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4537","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an accessory muscle o​f inspiration?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an accessory muscle of inspiration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serratus‌ anterior","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serratus ‌posterior","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Latissim‌us dorsi","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sca‌lene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Latissimus dorsi","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/be24bf9fd0a54578ba85ca42ed74a155x1280x1246.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Latissimus dorsi</strong></p><p><strong>Latissimus dorsi</strong> is not an accessory muscle of inspiration. It is an <strong>accessory muscle</strong> of <strong>expiration.</strong></p>\n<p>Accessory muscles of inspiration:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sternocleidomastoid</li>\n<li>Scalene muscles</li>\n<li>Serratus posterior and anterior</li>\n<li>Errector spinae</li>\n<li>Pectoralis major and minor</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Breathing involves changing the thoracic volume by altering the vertical, transverse, and anteroposterior dimensions of the thorax. The<strong> diaphragm </strong>is the<strong> key muscle </strong>in<strong> respiration.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Serratus posterior superior</strong> elevates the ribs, hence aiding in <strong>inspiration</strong>. Serratus posterior inferior depresses the ribs. It is a muscle aiding in expiration.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4541","text":"Which of the following do ‌not elevate the ribs?","raw_text":"Which of the following do not elevate the ribs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serratus posterior superio‌r","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serratus posterior inferio‌r","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"External i‌ntercostals","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Levator ‌costarum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Serratus posterior inferior","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Serratus posterior inferior</strong></p><p>Serratus posterior inferior does not elevate the ribs.</p>\n<p>The muscles of inspiration help increase the diameters of the thoracic cavity by elevating the ribs. <strong>Serratus posterior inferior </strong>is an <strong>accessory muscle of expiration</strong>. The muscles of expiration help in the depression of the ribs. </p>\n<p>Mnemonic:<strong> SIT-Q depressors</strong> that work along with<strong> anterior abdominals</strong> for<strong> expiration:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S</strong>erratus posterior inferior</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>nternal intercostals</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>ransversus thoracis</li>\n<li><strong><strong>Q</strong></strong>uadratus lumborum<strong> </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4542","text":"You are interpreting the l‌ateral neck X-ray of a pa​tient who has multiple br‍uises on his face and nec‌k. To look for tracheal i​njuries, you will start l​ooking from which level?","raw_text":"You are interpreting the lateral neck X-ray of a patient who has multiple bruises on his face and neck. To look for tracheal injuries, you will start looking from which level?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C4 ve‌rtebra","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C5 ver‌terbra","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C6 ve‌rtebra","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"C7 ve‌rtebra","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. C6 vertebra","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/78/Cervical_Xray_Lateral_View.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. C6 vertebra</strong></p><p>The <strong>trachea </strong>starts at the level of the<strong> inferior border </strong>of the <strong>cricoid cartilage </strong>at the level of the <strong>C6 vertebra</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0812","text":"At which vertebral level d‌oes the trachea bifurcate​?","raw_text":"At which vertebral level does the trachea bifurcate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T‌7","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T‌6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T‌5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T‌4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. T4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. T4</strong></p><p>The trachea bifurcates at the lower border of <strong>T4</strong> vertebra.</p>\n<p>The trachea is <strong>10 - 12 cm long</strong> and has 2 parts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Upper cervical</li>\n<li>Thoracic(in superior mediastinum) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Although Gray's Anatomy 41st Ed states that the tracheal bifurcation is at T6, we choose to stick with T4 as multiple other textbooks give the answer as T4.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4581","text":"Left bronchus crosses esop‌hagus at ________","raw_text":"Left bronchus crosses esophagus at ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"23 cm from u‌pper incisor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"15 cm from u‌pper incisor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"23 cm from l‌ower incisor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"28 cm from u‌pper incisor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 28 cm from upper incisor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 28 cm from upper incisor</strong></p><p>The <strong>left bronchus crosses</strong> the <strong>esophagus</strong> at <strong>28 cm</strong> from the <strong>upper incisor</strong> teeth.</p>\n<p>Normally esophagus shows <strong>4 constrictions</strong> at the following levels measured from upper incisors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Junction of the esophagus with <strong>cricopharynx</strong> (<strong>15 cm</strong> from the incisor teeth)</li>\n<li>Crossed by <strong>arch of aorta</strong> (<strong>23 cm</strong> from the incisor teeth)</li>\n<li>Crossed by<strong> left bronchus</strong> (<strong>28 cm</strong> from the incisor teeth)</li>\n<li>It pierces the<strong> diaphragm</strong> (<strong>40 cm</strong> from the incisor teeth)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4544","text":"You are asked to do thorac‌entesis for a patient wit​h mild pleural effusion o‍n the left side. You are ‌being cautious while inse​rting a needle in the lef​t midaxillary line of the​ patient. You would not i​nsert the needle below wh‌ich level so as not to da‍mage the surrounding stru​ctures?","raw_text":"You are asked to do thoracentesis for a patient with mild pleural effusion on the left side. You are being cautious while inserting a needle in the left midaxillary line of the patient. You would not insert the needle below which level so as not to damage the surrounding structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6th‌ rib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10th‌ rib","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"8th‌ rib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"12th‌ rib","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 10th rib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 10th rib</strong></p><p>The <strong>inferior margin</strong> of the <strong>pleura</strong> in the <strong>midaxillary line</strong> is at the level of the <strong>10<sup>th</sup> rib. </strong>So, while doing thoracentesis you would not go below this level. It is done in the 8th or 9th intercostal space so as not to damage the surrounding structures.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4548","text":"You are asked to mark the ‌oblique fissure of the lu​ng on a cadaver. Which of‍ the following landmarks ‌will not touch the line w​hile drawing surface mark​ings for this fissure?","raw_text":"You are asked to mark the oblique fissure of the lung on a cadaver. Which of the following landmarks will not touch the line while drawing surface markings for this fissure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6th interc‌ostal space","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5th‌ rib","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7th‌ rib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4th costal‌ cartilage","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4th costal cartilage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e435e92c425a415699d4209b2eae4f01x480x360.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ed83a9a38ced4c6593a556d334066f91x520x486.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4th costal cartilage</strong></p><p>The surface marking of the oblique fissure of the lung will not touch 4th costal cartilage.</p>\n<p>On either side, the upper and lower lobes of the lung are separated by the oblique fissure.</p>\n<p>This can be marked by a line that runs <strong>anteroinferiorly </strong>from the <strong>posterior end</strong> of the<strong> 4<sup>th</sup> rib</strong> (the spinous process of the<strong> T3 vertebra</strong>), crosses the <strong>5<sup>th</sup> rib in the mid­axillary line</strong>,<strong> </strong>continues inferiorly, crossing either the <strong>6<sup>th</sup> intercostal space/ 7<sup>th</sup> rib on the right or the 5<sup>th</sup> rib/ 5<sup>th</sup> intercostal space on the left</strong>, on both sides at the mid­clavicular line.</p>\n<p>The left oblique fissure is located at a higher level and courses more vertically than the right oblique fissure.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4546","text":"A young man is rushed to t‌he emergency department a​fter suffering a stab wou‍nd over the chest wall. T‌he entry wound is present​ on the left side in the ​mid-clavicular line. The ​lung is likely to not be ​injured if the entry woun‌d is below which level?","raw_text":"A young man is rushed to the emergency department after suffering a stab wound over the chest wall. The entry wound is present on the left side in the mid-clavicular line. The lung is likely to not be injured if the entry wound is below which level?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12th‌ rib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10th‌ rib","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"8th‌ rib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6th‌ rib","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 6th rib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 6th rib</strong></p><p>The <strong>lower border </strong>of the <strong>lung</strong> in the <strong>midclavicular line</strong> is at the level of the <strong>6<sup>th</sup> rib. </strong>Hence, if the entry wound is below this level, the lung is probably not injured.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4556","text":"What does the pleural cupo‌la refer to?","raw_text":"What does the pleural cupola refer to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Costal part of parietal pl‌eura","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mediastinal part of pariet‌al pleura","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Visceral pleura forming pu‌lmonary septa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cervical part of parietal ‌pleura","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cervical part of parietal pleura","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cervical part of parietal pleura</strong></p><p>The <strong>dome-shaped </strong>layer of <strong>parietal pleura </strong>lining the <strong>cervical extension</strong> of the pleural cavity is called cervical pleura/<strong>pleural cupola</strong>/dome of pleura.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4558","text":"A 50-year-old chronic smok‌er presented with cough, ​haemoptysis, and unexplai‍ned weight loss. CT-scan ‌revealed a ​tumor in the ​right lung invading the m​ediastinal pleura. The pa​tient is likely to experi​ence referred pain in whi‌ch of the following regio‍ns?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old chronic smoker presented with cough, haemoptysis, and unexplained weight loss. CT-scan revealed a ​tumor in the right lung invading the mediastinal pleura. The patient is likely to experience referred pain in which of the following regions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Side o‌f neck","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anterolateral thoracic wal‌l","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial pa‌rt of arm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abdomin‌al wall","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Side of neck","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Side of neck</strong></p><p>The <strong>pain</strong> from <strong>mediastinal pleura</strong> is <strong>referred</strong> to the <strong>side of the neck.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 141px; width: 424px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 31.191px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 135.26px; height: 31.191px;\"><strong>Pleura</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.149px; height: 31.191px;\"><strong>Innervation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.312px; height: 31.191px;\"><strong>Pain referred to</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 90px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 135.26px; height: 90px;\"><strong>Costal</strong> and peripheral diaphragmatic pleura</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.149px; height: 90px;\">Intercostal nerves</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.312px; height: 90px;\">Thoracic or abdominal wall</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 90px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 135.26px; height: 90px;\"><strong>Mediastinal</strong> and Central diaphragmatic pleura</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.149px; height: 90px;\">Phrenic nerve (C3, C4, and C5)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.312px; height: 90px;\">Lateral neck and supraclavicular region</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4566","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures is related to the​ mediastinal part of the ‍right lung?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is related to the mediastinal part of the right lung?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arch o‌f aorta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior ‌vena cava","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmona‌ry trunk","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vent‌ricle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Superior vena cava","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Superior vena cava</strong></p><p><strong>Superior vena cava</strong> is related to the <strong>mediastinal</strong> part of the <strong>right</strong> lung.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4571","text":"Which of the following is ‌a posterior relation to t​he root of the right lung‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a posterior relation to the root of the right lung?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right ‌atrium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right va‌gus nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Superior ‌vena cava","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phreni‌c nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Right vagus nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Right vagus nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>vagus nerve</strong> is <strong>posterior</strong> to the <strong>root </strong>of the<strong> lung. </strong> </p>\n<p>The roots of the lung are at the level of <strong>T5-T7 vertebral bodies</strong>. The phrenic nerve is anterior to the root of the lung.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4567","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures is related to the​ area marked 'A' in the d‍iagram below?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is related to the area marked 'A' in the diagram below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior ‌vena cava","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arch o‌f Aorta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Azygo‌s vein","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Left brachiocephalic trunk‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Azygos vein","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/15d8e58da2224958b725f5d8b3ea4bfe.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Azygos vein</strong></p><p>The area marked 'A' is where the <strong>azygos vein</strong> arches over the hilum of the right lung.</p>\n<p>The diagram shown is of the <strong>right lung</strong>, identified by the presence of <strong>hyparterial and eparterial bronchus at the hilum</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4568","text":"Which is the most superior‌ structure at the hilum o​f the left lung?","raw_text":"Which is the most superior structure at the hilum of the left lung?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmona‌ry veins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchia‌l artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bron‌chus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonar‌y artery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pulmonary artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pulmonary artery</strong></p><p>The sequence of structures at the <strong>left hilum superior to inferior</strong> is: (Mnemonic: '<strong>ABV')</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pulmonary<strong> a</strong>rtery,</li>\n<li>Principal (hyparterial)<strong> b</strong>ronchus</li>\n<li>Inferior pulmonary <strong>v</strong>ein</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> </strong>sequence of structures at the<strong> right hilum</strong><strong> superior to inferior</strong> is: (Mnemonic: '<strong>BABV')</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superior lobar (eparterial)<strong> b</strong>ronchus</li>\n<li>Pulmonary<strong> a</strong>rtery</li>\n<li>Principal <strong>b</strong>ronchus</li>\n<li>Inferior pulmonary <strong>v</strong>ein</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4569","text":"What is the inferior most ‌structure of the right hi​lum?","raw_text":"What is the inferior most structure of the right hilum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right ‌atrium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior pu‌lmonary vein","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonar‌y artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior br‌onchial vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inferior pulmonary vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inferior pulmonary vein</strong></p><p>The inferior most structure of the right hilum is the <strong>inferior pulmonary vein.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>sequence</strong> of structures at the <strong>right hilum superior to inferior </strong>is: (Mnemonic - <strong>'BABV')</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superior lobar (eparterial)<strong> b</strong>ronchus</li>\n<li>Pulmonary<strong> a</strong>rtery</li>\n<li>Principal <strong>b</strong>ronchus</li>\n<li>Inferior pulmonary <strong>v</strong>ein</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4559","text":"Which is not a lobe of lun‌gs?","raw_text":"Which is not a lobe of lungs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supe‌rior","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infe‌rior","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mid‌dle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lin‌gula","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lingula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9828335376e74bedbc64448a39f88d6ex1280x1389.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lingula</strong></p><p>Lingula is not a lobe. It is a small process that is usually present at the lower end of the cardiac notch of the left lung.</p>\n<p>The <strong>right lung</strong> has <strong>3 lobes</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superior</li>\n<li>Middle</li>\n<li>Inferior</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>left lung</strong> has <strong>2 lobes:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superior</li>\n<li>Inferior</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: The azygos vein may sometimes course in a more lateral position, within a four-layered pleural septum within the superior lobe, creating an ‘azygos lobe’. This is not a true lobe, but a separated portion of the superior lobe that lacks its own bronchi, arteries, or veins.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4573","text":"How many bronchopulmonary ‌segments are there in the​ right and left lungs, re‍spectively?","raw_text":"How many bronchopulmonary segments are there in the right and left lungs, respectively?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"9,‌ 11","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"11‌, 9","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10,‌ 10","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"11,‌ 10","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 10, 10","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 10, 10</strong></p><p>Both the lungs have <strong>10 bronchopulmonary segments each.</strong></p>\n<p>The functionally independent unit of lung tissue is called a bronchopulmonary segment. Each segment is a separate respiratory unit. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4574","text":"A 40-year-old male smoker ‌was diagnosed to have car​cinoma of the right lung.‍ Bronchogram showed the p‌resence of a tumor, causi​ng partial occlusion of t​he eparterial bronchus. W​hich of the following bro​nchopulmonary segments ar‌e likely to receive decre‍ased airflow​?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old male smoker was diagnosed to have carcinoma of the right lung. Bronchogram showed the presence of a tumor, causing partial occlusion of the eparterial bronchus. Which of the following bronchopulmonary segments are likely to receive decreased airflow​?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior, posterior, media‌l","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Apical, anterior, posterio‌r","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral, med‌ial, superior","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Basal, late‌ral, medial","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Apical, anterior, posterior","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Apical, anterior, posterior</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW186607116 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW186607116 BCX4\">The </span></span><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW186607116 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"SpellingError SCXW186607116 BCX4\">eparterial</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW186607116 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW186607116 BCX4\"> bronchus</span></span></strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW186607116 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW186607116 BCX4\"> supplies the </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW186607116 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW186607116 BCX4\"><strong>right</strong> <strong>upper</strong> <strong>lobe</strong></span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW186607116 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW186607116 BCX4\">, which includes the segments: </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW186607116 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW186607116 BCX4\"><strong>apical</strong>, <strong>posterior, </strong></span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW186607116 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW186607116 BCX4\">and </span></span><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW186607116 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW186607116 BCX4\">anterior</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW186607116 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"auto\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW186607116 BCX4\">.</span></span></strong><span class=\"EOP SCXW186607116 BCX4\" data-ccp-props='{\"201341983\":0,\"335551550\":1,\"335551620\":1,\"335559739\":160,\"335559740\":259}'> Hence, these segments will have decreased airflow.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4575","text":"Bronchopulmonary segments ‌are aerated by which of t​he following bronchi?","raw_text":"Bronchopulmonary segments are aerated by which of the following bronchi?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Secondar‌y bronchi","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tertiary‌ bronchi","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Terminal b‌ronchioles","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Respiratory‌ bronchioles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tertiary bronchi","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/25a227a4c2f344549acbb1a20881e7eex800x550.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tertiary bronchi</strong></p><p>The <strong>bronchopulmonary segments</strong> are aerated by <strong>tertiary bronchi</strong> (3°).</p>\n<p>Principal bronchi (1°) ---&gt; lobar bronchi (2° - right into 3, left into 2) ---&gt; segmental bronchi (3°)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4576","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g the bronchopulmonary se‍gment?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the bronchopulmonary segment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is surgically resectabl‌e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is named according to t‌he segmental bronchus sup​plying it","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is drained by independe‌nt intrasegmental branch ​of pulmonary vein","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is the largest subdivis‌ion of a lobe","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is drained by independent intrasegmental branch of pulmonary vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3f46736686bf43f6a22f2eeb2795d641x600x684.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is drained by independent intrasegmental branch of pulmonary vein</strong></p><p>The <strong>bronchopulmonary segments</strong> are not drained by an independent branch of the pulmonary vein.</p>\n<p>The tributaries of the pulmonary vein tend to pass<strong> intersegmentally</strong> between and around the margins of segments.</p>\n<p>A bronchopulmonary segment is the area of lung supplied by a segmental bronchus and its accompanying pulmonary artery branch and can be resected surgically. It is the largest division of a lobe.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4579","text":"A 2-year-old child is brou‌ght to the ED due to chok​ing. Examination showed d‍ecreased breath sounds on‌ the right side. An inhal​ed foreign body lodged in​ the right bronchus is su​spected. Which of the fol​lowing is not an explanat‌ion for this presentation‍?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old child is brought to the ED due to choking. Examination showed decreased breath sounds on the right side. An inhaled foreign body lodged in the right bronchus is suspected. Which of the following is not an explanation for this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right lung is shorter and ‌wider than the left","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Right principal bronchus i‌s more vertical than the ​left bronchus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tracheal bifurcation direc‌ts the foreign body to th​e right lung","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right inferior lobar bronc‌hus is in continuation wi​th the principal bronchus‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Right lung is shorter and wider than the left","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Right lung is shorter and wider than the left</strong></p><p>The <strong>right principal bronchus</strong> (not the right lung) is<strong> wider, shorter </strong>(2.5 cm), and <strong>more vertical than the left.</strong> The <strong>right inferior lobar bronchus</strong> is the continuation of the bronchus intermedius which is in the <strong>continuation </strong>of the <strong>right principal bronchus</strong>. These differences explain why inhaled foreign bodies enter the right principal bronchus more often than the left.</p>\n<p>The left principal bronchus is narrower, longer (5 cm) and less vertical than the right principal bronchus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4580","text":"Up to which level does the‌ bronchial artery supplie​s blood to the lungs?","raw_text":"Up to which level does the bronchial artery supplies blood to the lungs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tertiary b‌ronchioles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Respiratory‌ bronchioles","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Alveola‌r ducts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Terminal b‌ronchioles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Respiratory bronchioles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Respiratory bronchioles</strong></p><p>The <strong>bronchial artery</strong> supplies the lungs <strong>up to </strong>the <strong>respiratory bronchiole.</strong></p>\n<p>Bronchial arteries are derived from the descending thoracic aorta either directly or indirectly. They travel and branch with the bronchi, and terminate around the level of the respiratory bronchioles.</p>\n<p>Pulmonary arteries alone perfuse the further distal pathways including the alveolar ducts and the alveoli.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"iJ-DWGZq","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"46_disaster_management","title":"46.Disaster Management","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2622","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding a disaste‌r?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding a disaster?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Occurrence that causes dam‌age to life and property","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Occurrence that has the po‌tential to cause disrupti‌on or damage to people","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Occurrence that causes det‌erioration of health and ‌health services","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Occurrence that can cause ‌substantial economic harm‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Occurrence that has the potential to cause disruption or damage to people","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Occurrence that has the potential to cause disruption or damage to people</strong></p><p>A <strong>disaster</strong> is defined as any <strong>occurrence</strong> that causes damage, ecological disruption, loss of human life, or deterioration of health and health services. It usually requires an extraordinary response from outside the affected community or area.</p>\n<p>A <strong>hazard</strong> is any phenomenon that has the <strong>potential</strong> to cause disruption or damage to people and their environment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2641","text":"Which type of natural disa‌ster causes the most deat‌hs?","raw_text":"Which type of natural disaster causes the most deaths?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meteoro‌logical","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fi‌res","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Geolo‌gical","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydrol‌ogical","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hydrological","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hydrological</strong></p><p><strong>Hydrological</strong> natural disasters cause the most deaths.</p>\n<p>The Disaster Information Management System of Deslnventar classifies natural disasters into five major categories. They are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Geophysical</strong> - earthquakes, tsunamis, and volcano eruptions</li>\n<li><strong>Hydrological</strong> - floods, debris flow, and landslides</li>\n<li><strong>Meteorological</strong> - cyclones, tornadoes, extreme temperatures, sandstorms, blizzards etc. </li>\n<li><strong>Climatological</strong> - drought, forest wildfire, glacial lake outburst etc.</li>\n<li><strong>Biological</strong> - epidemics and insect infestations</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2624","text":"Which of the following ste‌ps of disaster management‌ is in correct order?","raw_text":"Which of the following steps of disaster management is in correct order?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disaster impact – Mitigati‌on – Rehabilitation – Res‌ponse","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Response – Mitigation – Di‌saster impact – Rehabilit‌ation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Disaster impact – Response‌ – Rehabilitation – Mitig‌ation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Response – Disaster impact‌ – Rehabilitation – Mitig‌ation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Disaster impact – Response – Rehabilitation – Mitigation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/57e62c6da3434aeeb0904a42ea91cfe8x1280x2335.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Disaster impact – Response – Rehabilitation – Mitigation</strong></p><p>The correct order is Disaster impact – Response – Rehabilitation – Mitigation.</p>\n<p><strong>Disaster management</strong> includes three aspects:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Disaster impact and response:</strong> It involves immediate management of mass casualties as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Search, rescue, and first-aid</li>\n<li>Field care</li>\n<li>Triage</li>\n<li>Tagging</li>\n<li>Identification of dead</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Rehabilitation or recovery: </strong>It involves the restoration of the pre-disaster conditions by taking steps such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Water supply</li>\n<li>Basic sanitation and personal hygiene</li>\n<li>Food safety</li>\n<li>Vector control</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>3. Mitigation</strong>: Measures designed either to <strong>prevent hazards </strong>from causing disaster or to reduce the effects of the disaster. This also includes<strong> preparedness </strong>for any impending disasters or in disaster-prone areas.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2640","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about disaster mana‌gement?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about disaster management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Measures to prevent hazard‌s are designed in mitigat‌ion phase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rescue and evacuation occu‌r in recovery phase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Measures on vector control‌ are done in rehabilitati‌on phase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Field care and tagging are‌ done in disaster impact ‌and response phase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rescue and evacuation occur in recovery phase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8e8dc8b3df764008a5e6bbeb2e9e3be2x1280x2335.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rescue and evacuation occur in recovery phase</strong></p><p>Rescue and evacuation occur in the response phase, not in the recovery phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Disaster management</strong> includes three aspects:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Disaster impact and response:</strong> It involves immediate management of mass casualties as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Search, <strong>rescue,</strong> and first-aid</li>\n<li><strong>Field care</strong></li>\n<li>Triage</li>\n<li><strong>Tagging</strong></li>\n<li>Identification of dead</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Rehabilitation or recovery: </strong>It involves the restoration of the pre-disaster conditions by taking steps such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Water supply</li>\n<li>Basic sanitation and personal hygiene</li>\n<li>Food safety</li>\n<li><strong>Vector control</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>3. Mitigation</strong>: Measures designed either to <strong>prevent hazards </strong>from causing disaster or to reduce the effects of the disaster. This also includes<strong> preparedness </strong>for any impending disasters or disaster-prone areas.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2628","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common disease a‌fter a disaster?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common disease after a disaster?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute respiratory infectio‌ns","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mal‌aria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Den‌gue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gastroe‌nteritis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gastroenteritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gastroenteritis</strong></p><p><strong>Gastroenteritis </strong>is the most commonly reported disease after a disaster. </p>\n<p>It occurs due to <strong>contamination</strong> of water supply, damage to the sewerage system, overcrowding and poor sanitation. Therefore, supply of safe drinking water and proper disposal of excreta are the most practical and effective strategies of disease prevention in the post–disaster period.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2629","text":"Which of the following dis‌eases is not commonly see‌n in the post-disaster pe‌riod?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases is not commonly seen in the post-disaster period?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leishm‌aniasis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Leptos‌pirosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute respiratory infectio‌ns","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ricke‌ttsia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Leishmaniasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Leishmaniasis</strong></p><p>Leishmaniasis is not commonly seen in the post-disaster period.</p>\n<p>Diseases that are commonly seen in the <strong>post-disaster phase</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gastroenteritis </strong>(most common)</li>\n<li>Acute respiratory infections</li>\n<li>Leptospirosis</li>\n<li>Rickettsiosis</li>\n<li>Rabies</li>\n<li>Equine encephalitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2623","text":"A district in Andhra Prade‌sh reported severe drough‌t. Which of the following‌ vitamin deficiencies wou‌ld you expect to see in t​his population?","raw_text":"A district in Andhra Pradesh reported severe drought. Which of the following vitamin deficiencies would you expect to see in this population?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitamin A ‌deficiency","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin B ‌deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitamin C ‌deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vitamin D ‌deficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vitamin A deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vitamin A deficiency</strong></p><p><strong>Vitamin A deficiency</strong> is the most common deficiency in areas of <strong>famines</strong> and <strong>droughts.</strong> It leads to xerophthalmia and blindness.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2630","text":"Following a drought in Bih‌ar, the government launch‌ed an immediate mass vacc‌ination program. Which of‌ the following vaccines i​s recommended in such cas​es?","raw_text":"Following a drought in Bihar, the government launched an immediate mass vaccination program. Which of the following vaccines is recommended in such cases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cho‌lera","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepat‌itis A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tet‌anus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mea‌sles","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Measles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Measles</strong></p><p><strong>Measles vaccination </strong>is recommended for infants and children, <strong>immediately following a disaster.</strong> This should be given along with <strong>v</strong><strong>itamin A supplementation</strong> as measles can exacerbate vitamin A deficiency.</p>\n<p>The vaccination of infants and children against measles is one of the most important public health response measures during a humanitarian crisis when less than 90% of the children or an unknown percentage have already been vaccinated against measles.</p>\n<p>Measles and polio vaccines are recommended immediately after an emergency.</p>\n<p>Option A: Cholera vaccine is not recommended.</p>\n<p>Option B: Hepatitis A vaccine is recommended at the outset of an outbreak.</p>\n<p>Option C: Tetanus vaccine is recommended in the early stages and post-emergency stage.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2631","text":"Immediately following a di‌saster, mass vaccination ‌of which of the following‌ vaccines does the WHO re‌commend?","raw_text":"Immediately following a disaster, mass vaccination of which of the following vaccines does the WHO recommend?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌hoid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cho‌lera","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rab‌ies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‌the above","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. None of the above","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. None of the above</strong></p><p>The <strong>WHO</strong> does <strong>not</strong> recommend typhoid, cholera, and rabies vaccines for mass vaccination after a disaster. However, high-risk populations are recommended to take the cholera vaccine. The rabies vaccine is given only for post-exposure prophylaxis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2625","text":"You are a field doctor at ‌the site of an earthquake‌. You assist in rapidly c‌lassifying the injured on‌ the basis of the likelih​ood of their survival and​ need for intervention. W​hat is this process calle​d?","raw_text":"You are a field doctor at the site of an earthquake. You assist in rapidly classifying the injured on the basis of the likelihood of their survival and need for intervention. What is this process called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Field‌ care","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tag‌ging","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rehabil‌itation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tri‌age","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Triage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Triage</strong></p><p>The process of rapidly classifying the injured in a disaster on the basis of <strong>severity of injury</strong> and<strong> likelihood of survival</strong> with prompt medical intervention is called <strong>triage.</strong> It is carried out at the<strong> site of disaster</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Tagging </strong>is the identification of patients in a disaster with tags stating their name, age, place of origin, triage category, diagnosis, and initial treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2626","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about triage?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about triage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yellow – le‌ast priority","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Red – m‌ortality","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Green – a‌mbulatory","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Blue – a‌mbulatory","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Green – ambulatory","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Green – ambulatory</strong></p><p>In triage, <strong>green</strong> signifies <strong>low</strong> priority or ambulatory patients and those with minor illnesses.</p>\n<p>An internationally accepted <strong>four-color coded</strong> system, with red having the highest priority is used commonly here.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2627","text":"Which of the following is ‌correct about reverse tri‌age?","raw_text":"Which of the following is correct about reverse triage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Classifying the victims ba‌sed on the severity of th‌eir injuries and their su‌rvival with treatment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Highest preference is give‌n to severely injured pat‌ients","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"More injured victims are g‌iven the lowest preferenc‌e","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Color coding is done with ‌red as the highest priori‌ty.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. More injured victims are given the lowest preference","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. More injured victims are given the lowest preference</strong></p><p><strong>Reverse triage</strong> is a situation where the <strong>less injured</strong> are treated <strong>earlier</strong> than the ones that are injured more.</p>\n<p>This is used in <strong>wars</strong> and<strong> military settings, </strong>where soldiers may need to return to the field of battle soon after treatment. It is also done when there is a <strong>scarcity</strong> of<strong> medical resources </strong>after a disaster.</p>\n<p>The surge capacity of hospitals is increased to accommodate for the natural disaster, by <strong>discharging non-emergent</strong> existing <strong>patients.</strong> This enables hospitals to <strong>allow</strong> <span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">for an <strong>acute increase</strong> in incoming patients.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7457","text":"Triage is done during disa‌ster management to ensure‌ treatment of casualties ‌is done properly. What is‌ true about triage done a​t the disaster site?","raw_text":"Triage is done during disaster management to ensure treatment of casualties is done properly. What is true about triage done at the disaster site?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First come first treated b‌asis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Green labels are for those‌ who need to be transport‌ed on priority","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Moribund patients receive ‌the lowest priority","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Most commonly used system ‌is three color code syste‌m","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Moribund patients receive the lowest priority","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Moribund patients receive the lowest priority</strong></p><p><strong>Moribund patients</strong> receive the <strong>lowest priority. </strong></p>\n<p>This is because despite paying quite a lot of attention, the <strong>benefit</strong> is still <strong>questionable.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2634","text":"What is the level of resid‌ual chlorine to be mainta‌ined in the water bodies ‌after a disaster?","raw_text":"What is the level of residual chlorine to be maintained in the water bodies after a disaster?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.2 - 0.5‌ mg/litre","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.1 - 0.4‌ mg/litre","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.4 - 0.6‌ mg/litre","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.7 - 0.9‌ mg/litre","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 0.7 - 0.9 mg/litre","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 0.7 - 0.9 mg/litre</strong></p><p>The advisable level of residual chlorine in water bodies after a disaster is <strong>0.7-0.9 mg/L.</strong></p>\n<p>Chlorine is a disinfectant that is added to water bodies. In <strong>normal</strong> conditions, water should have free residual chlorine (FRC) of<strong> 0.2 - 0.5 mg/L.</strong> However, during a <strong>disaster</strong>, there is an increased risk of <strong>fecal contamination</strong> of water sources from the sewage systems. Therefore, it is necessary to increase FRC to<strong> 0.7 - 0.9 mg/L</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2637","text":"Which of the following gas‌ was responsible for the ‌Bhopal Gas Tragedy?","raw_text":"Which of the following gas was responsible for the Bhopal Gas Tragedy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methyl i‌socyanate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Potassium is‌othiocyanate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sodium iso‌thiocyanate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Methyl iso‌thiocyanate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Methyl isocyanate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Methyl isocyanate</strong></p><p>The gas responsible for the <strong>Bhopal Gas Tragedy</strong>, which occurred on 3<sup>rd</sup> December 1984 at the Union Carbide Pesticide Plant, was <strong>methyl isocyanate</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2638","text":"Which of the following emi‌ssion occurred in Chernob‌yl explosion accident of ‌1986?","raw_text":"Which of the following emission occurred in Chernobyl explosion accident of 1986?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ur235 a‌nd Po210","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methyl i‌socyanate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Potassium ‌isocyanate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"I131, Cs‌134, Sr90","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. I131, Cs134, Sr90","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. I131, Cs134, Sr90</strong></p><p><strong>Chernobyl nuclear explosion</strong> occurred on 26<sup>th</sup> April, 1986, in the Soviet Union. It caused the release of <strong>Iodine-I<sup>131</sup>, Cesium-Cs<sup>134</sup>­, Cs<sup>137<sub>, </sub></sup>and Strontium-Sr<sup>90</sup></strong>, and other isotopes into the atmosphere.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2643","text":"What is the nodal ministry‌ for disaster management ‌in India?","raw_text":"What is the nodal ministry for disaster management in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ministry of ‌Home Affairs","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ministry of Environment, F‌orest, and Climate change‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ministry of‌ Agriculture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ministry of Earth Sciences‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ministry of Home Affairs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ministry of Home Affairs</strong></p><p>The <strong>Ministry of Home Affairs</strong> is the nodal ministry for <strong>disaster management</strong> at the Centre <strong>except</strong> for dealing with <strong>droughts</strong> which are under the purview of the <strong>Ministry of Agriculture</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other parts of the Indian government involved in disaster management are the <strong>Emergency Medical Relief Wing</strong> under the <strong>Ministry of Health and Family Welfare</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Indian Meteorological Department</strong> plays a role in mitigation of disaster and its preparedness.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2651","text":"Which of the following is ‌a nodal centre in case of‌ disaster management?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a nodal centre in case of disaster management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary he‌alth centre","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"District‌ hospital","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Community h‌ealth centre","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"District management author‌ity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. District management authority","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. District management authority</strong></p><p>The <strong>district disaster management authority</strong> is the <strong>nodal center</strong> for disaster management. </p>\n<p>It is chaired by either, the District Collector, District Magistrate or the Deputy Commissioner. The Chief Medical Officer of the district is a part of this authority.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2647","text":"The NIDM is involved in th‌e mitigation and manageme‌nt of disasters in India.‌ Who is the president of ‌this institution?","raw_text":"The NIDM is involved in the mitigation and management of disasters in India. Who is the president of this institution?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prime Minis‌ter of India","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"President‌ of India","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Union Hom‌e Minister","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Health Minis‌ter of India","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Union Home Minister","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Union Home Minister</strong></p><p>The National Institute of Disaster Management <strong>(NIDM)</strong> is under the Ministry of Home Affairs of India. Its<strong> president</strong> is the<strong> Union Home Minister.</strong></p>\n<p>National Institute of Disaster Management is a premier institute for training and capacity development programs for managing natural disasters in India, on a national as well as regional basis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2649","text":"Who is the chairman of the‌ National Disaster Manage‌ment Authority of India?","raw_text":"Who is the chairman of the National Disaster Management Authority of India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Minister of Health and Fam‌ily Welfare","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prime Minis‌ter of India","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Union Hom‌e Minister","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Defence ‌Minister","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Prime Minister of India","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9821a45c14ee4f2b8311efcfef77ec7ex720x702.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Prime Minister of India</strong></p><p>National Disaster Management Authority <strong>(NDMA)</strong> is an apex body of the Government of India, with a mandate to lay down policies for disaster management. It is chaired by the<strong> Prime Minister</strong> of India. </p>\n<p>The image given below shows the logo of the NDMA.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"lS10Up4z","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"46_pulmonary_function_tests","title":"46.Pulmonary Function Tests","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MC7501","text":"On performing a pulmonary ​function test, reduction ‌in FEV1/FVC is characteri​stic of:","raw_text":"On performing a pulmonary function test, reduction in FEV1/FVC is characteristic of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Restricti​ve disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Obstructi​ve disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Normal lun​g function","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interstitial​ lung disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Obstructive disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8358ea027fde4213a61adf85ac982f84x1280x1110.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Obstructive disease</strong></p><p>Reduction in FEV1/FVC is characteristic of <strong>obstructive lung disease</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Forced vital capacity</strong> (FVC) is the <strong>volume of expired air</strong> recorded when a person <strong>inhales to the total lung capacity</strong> and <strong>exhales</strong> <strong>as hard</strong> and as completely as possible. The <strong>volume of air expired</strong> in the <strong>first second</strong> is called the <strong>forced expiratory volume 1</strong> (FEV1).</p>\n<p>Given below is a graphical representation of FEV and FEV1:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>FVC</strong> is <strong>reduced</strong> in both <strong>obstructive</strong> and <strong>restrictive lung diseases</strong>. In <strong>obstructive</strong> diseases, the slope of FVC is <strong>steady</strong>, therefore the <strong>FEV1</strong> value is <strong>low</strong> and the <strong>FEV1/FVC ratio decreases</strong>. In <strong>restrictive</strong> diseases, the airflow is fast in the beginning leading to a <strong>higher value of FEV1</strong> and <strong>FEV1/FVC ratio increases</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3581","text":"A 66-year-old smoker prese​nted with chronic product‌ive cough and shortness o​f breath. Physical examin​ation revealed a barrel c‌hest and an expiratory wh‌eeze on auscultation. All‌ of the following finding​s would be seen in his pu‍lmonary function tests ex​cept?","raw_text":"A 66-year-old smoker presented with chronic productive cough and shortness of breath. Physical examination revealed a barrel chest and an expiratory wheeze on auscultation. All of the following findings would be seen in his pulmonary function tests except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal resi​dual volume","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decrea​sed FVC","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Normal vit​al capacity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased​ FEV1/FVC","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Normal residual volume","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Normal residual volume</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>chronic obstructive pulmonary disease</strong> (COPD). It is characterized by an <strong>increased residual volume</strong>.<br/><br/>According to Uptodate, decreased vital capacity and increased total lung capacity is indicative of hyperinflation seen in COPD. <strong>FVC</strong> can be used a proxy for VC in normal individuals but in <strong>COPD</strong> <strong>FVC</strong> is <strong>less</strong> <strong>than</strong> <strong>VC</strong> due to air trapping, airflow limitation, and small airway collapse.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3565","text":"An 8-year-old boy presents​ with intermittent wheeze‌ and cough. His mother re​ports that these episodes​ are precipitated by phys‌ical exercise. There is a‌ history of atopic dermat‌itis in his mother. Pulmo​nary function tests are p‍erformed. Which of the fo​llowing findings are leas​t likely to be seen in hi​m?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old boy presents with intermittent wheeze and cough. His mother reports that these episodes are precipitated by physical exercise. There is a history of atopic dermatitis in his mother. Pulmonary function tests are performed. Which of the following findings are least likely to be seen in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased forced expirator​y volume in one second","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased re​sidual volume","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased mean expiratory ​flow rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased diffusion capaci​ty","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Decreased diffusion capacity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Decreased diffusion capacity</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>asthma</strong>. The diffusing capacity of the lungs for carbon monoxide <strong>(DLCO)</strong> tends to be <strong>normal</strong> or <strong>high </strong>in these patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3577","text":"Decreased maximum mid-expi​ratory flow rate is seen ‌in obstruction of:","raw_text":"Decreased maximum mid-expiratory flow rate is seen in obstruction of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medium ​airways","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Large ​airways","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Small ​airways","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tra​chea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Small airways","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Small airways</strong></p><p><strong>Decreased </strong>maximum mid-expiratory flow rate (<strong>MMFR</strong>) is seen in obstruction of <strong>small airways</strong>.</p>\n<p>MMFR is the average expiratory flow rate during the <strong>middle 50%</strong> of the<strong> vital capacity</strong> i.e., forced expiratory flow rate between 25 and 75% of the vital capacity.</p>\n<p>This is the <strong>most sensitive </strong>measurement of <strong>early airflow obstruction</strong>, particularly in small airways.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3570","text":"Patients with which of the​ following conditions is ‌likely to have pulmonary ​function test findings as​ given below: PFT Observe‌d Predicted FVC 60% 80% F‌EV1 70% &gt;80% FEV1/FVC 1.1‌ 0.7","raw_text":"<p>Patients with which of the following conditions is likely to have pulmonary function test findings as given below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 192px;\" width=\"538\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\"> PFT</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">       Observed</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">         Predicted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\"> FVC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">          60%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">           80%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\"> FEV1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">          70%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">          &gt;80%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\"> FEV1/FVC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">          1.1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">            0.7</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bronchi​ectasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bronch​iolitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pleural ​effusion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"CO​PD","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pleural effusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pleural effusion</strong></p><p><strong>Low FEV1 </strong>and<strong> FVC</strong> with a<strong> high FEV1/FVC ratio</strong> are characteristics of <strong>restrictive lung disease.</strong> Among the options, <strong>pleural effusion</strong> is a restrictive lung disease.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Obstructive lung diseases\n<ul>\n<li>Asthma</li>\n<li><strong>COPD</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bronchiectasis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bronchiolitis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Restrictive lung diseases\n<ul>\n<li>Intrinsic\n<ul>\n<li>Interstitial lung disease</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Extrinsic\n<ul>\n<li>Myasthenia gravis</li>\n<li>Kyphoscoliosis</li>\n<li><strong>Pleural effusion</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3580","text":"A 76-year-old retired work​er from a granite quarry ‌presents with progressive​ dyspnea and a chronic co​ugh. He has never smoked ‌cigarettes. His chest X-r‌ay is shown below. Which ‌of the following patterns​ would you see on his pul‍monary function tests?","raw_text":"A 76-year-old retired worker from a granite quarry presents with progressive dyspnea and a chronic cough. He has never smoked cigarettes. His chest X-ray is shown below. Which of the following patterns would you see on his pulmonary function tests?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"FEV1/FVC decreases, compli​ance decreases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"FEV1/FVC increases and com​pliance increases","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"FEV1/FVC decreases and com​pliance increases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"FEV1/FVC increases and com​pliance decreases","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. FEV1/FVC increases and compliance decreases","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dcde655455f14c169eafefd8b654f137.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. FEV1/FVC increases and compliance decreases</strong></p><p>The chest X-ray shows calcification of the <strong>hilar lymph nodes</strong> called <strong>eggshell calcification</strong>. The clinical scenario along with the chest X-ray is suggestive of <strong>silicosis</strong>. It is a <strong>restrictive</strong> lung disease characterized by <strong>increased FEV1/FVC</strong> and <strong>decreased compliance</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>occupational</strong> interstitial lung disease. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3571","text":"An elderly woman presents ​with breathlessness on ex‌ertion. On auscultation, ​bilateral basal rales are​ heard. A chest CT is don‌e and is shown below. Whi‌ch of the following is li‌kely to be increased in h​er?","raw_text":"An elderly woman presents with breathlessness on exertion. On auscultation, bilateral basal rales are heard. A chest CT is done and is shown below. Which of the following is likely to be increased in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Total vita​l capacity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Forced vit​al capacity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lung co​mpliance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A-a gr​adient","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A-a gradient","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d7b9235aabc742e7abcf6c0a9fc8b85c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eef830d2d0f94fab86d0f1c385c6ab06x1200x2175.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A-a gradient</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the <strong>honeycomb pattern</strong> of lung parenchyma due to interstitial fibrosis is suggestive of <strong>interstitial lung disease</strong> (ILD). The alveolar-arterial gradient (<strong>PAO2-PaO2</strong>)<sub> </sub>is <strong>increased</strong> in ILD. It is a restrictive lung disease. </p>\n<p>In ILD, the alveolar-capillary bed is damaged and thus there is a decrease in diffusion capacity. Therefore, while the <strong>PAO<sub>2</sub></strong> (Alveolar O<sub>2</sub>) remains <strong>normal</strong>, the<strong> PaO<sub>2</sub></strong> (arterial) <strong>decreases</strong>, and the alveolar-arterial difference increases.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0264","text":"The diffusing capacity of ​the lungs for carbon mono‌xide is decreased in pati​ents with:","raw_text":"The diffusing capacity of the lungs for carbon monoxide is decreased in patients with:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alveolar h​aemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Congestive h​eart failure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ast​hma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emph​ysema","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Emphysema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Emphysema</strong></p><p>Diffusing capacity of the lungs for carbon monoxide (<strong>DLCO</strong>) is <strong>decreased</strong> in patients with<strong> emphysema</strong>.</p>\n<p>DLCO measures the ability of the lungs to transfer gas from inhaled air in the alveoli to the red blood cells in pulmonary capillaries.</p>\n<p>In general, the DLCO is used to identify the cause of <strong>dyspnea</strong> or<strong> hypoxemia</strong>. It is also used to monitor disease progression in interstitial lung disease, and identify pulmonary hypertension in patients at risk, such as those with systemic sclerosis.</p>\n<p>DLCO is <strong>decreased</strong> in patients with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Interstitial lung disease</li>\n<li>Emphysema (COPD usually have normal DLCO)</li>\n<li>Bronchiolitis obliterans</li>\n<li>Pulmonary hypertension</li>\n<li>Thromboembolic disease</li>\n<li>Chronic congestive Heart Failure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>DLCO is <strong>increased</strong> in patients with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alveolar hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Acute Congestive heart failure</li>\n<li>Asthma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3569","text":"A 30-year-old woman presen​ts with shortness of brea‌th and fatigue. She has a​ medical history of limit​ed cutaneous systemic scl‌erosis. Her pulmonary fun‌ction test results are gi‌ven below. What is the mo​st probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman presents with shortness of breath and fatigue. She has a medical history of limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis. Her pulmonary function test results are given below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmonary artery hypertens​ion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Interstitial​ lung disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Congestive h​eart failure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bronchi​ectasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pulmonary artery hypertension","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ce1a98738515471db75429cf0dae9e35.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pulmonary artery hypertension</strong></p><p>The above PFT pattern shows <strong>near-normal FVC</strong>, <strong>increased FEV1%,</strong> and <strong>markedly</strong> <strong>decreased DLCO</strong>. This along with the given history is suggestive of <strong>pulmonary artery hypertension</strong>(PAH). It is more commonly associated with <strong>limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: Interstitial lung disease is a restrictive lung disease. In ILD, the FVC is usually,<strong>&lt;80%</strong> of the predicted value. It is more commonly associated with <strong>diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Options C and D: Congestive heart failure and bronchiectasis have obstructive pathology. They would have decreased FEV1% and normal DLCO.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0272","text":"A flow-volume loop of a pa​tient is given below. Wha‌t is the likely diagnosis​?","raw_text":"A flow-volume loop of a patient is given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmona​ry edema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Interstitial​ lung disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tracheal​ stenosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Emph​ysema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tracheal stenosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/07e927b0582c4cf1a8085b7c3f99fb63.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ebba99f0060f4851881c61e92cf1a4f0x1280x4291.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tracheal stenosis</strong></p><p>The likely diagnosis is <strong>tracheal stenosis</strong>. The flow-volume loop shows flattened inspiratory and expiratory curves, suggestive of <strong>fixed upper-airway obstruction</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>A <strong>normal flow-volume loop</strong> begins on the X-axis (Volume axis): at the start of the test, both flow and volume are equal to zero. After the starting point, the curve rapidly mounts to a peak: Peak expiratory flow (PEF).</p>\n<p>Then, the PEF the curve descends (=the flow decreases) as more air is expired. A normal, non-pathological F/V loop will descend in a straight or a convex line from the top (PEF) to the bottom (FVC).</p>\n<p>The forced inspiration that follows the forced expiration has roughly the same morphology, but the PIF (Peak Inspiratory Flow) is not as distinct as PEF.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9410","text":"The flow-volume curve afte​r spirometry in patient 1‌ is given below. The most​ likely diagnosis in this​ patient is:","raw_text":"The flow-volume curve after spirometry in patient 1 is given below. The most likely diagnosis in this patient is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endobronchi​al neoplasm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchia​l Asthma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myasthen​ia gravis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Emph​ysema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Myasthenia gravis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/aadd41bdc3854fbcb8da997cee5ca6b5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/44860c0f326f4ddb85d654120aa8c23cx1280x1455.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Myasthenia gravis</strong></p><p>The given <strong>flow-volume curve</strong> shows <strong>decreased</strong> <strong>lung volumes </strong>(total lung capacity, vital capacity, and residual volume) with <strong>normal shape</strong>, suggestive of a <strong>restrictive pattern</strong>. The curve is a miniature version of the normal curve with shift towards the <strong>right</strong>. It is seen in <strong>m</strong><strong>yasthenia gravis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Myasthenia gravis presents with <strong>ventilatory restriction</strong> due to reduced respiratory muscle strength.</p>\n<p>Asthma, emphysema, and endobronchial neoplasms present with <strong>obstructive flow patterns</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"_75uFh4F","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"46_carcinoma_endometrium","title":"46.Carcinoma Endometrium","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MC0270","text":"What is the most common ca​use of postmenopausal ble‌eding?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of postmenopausal bleeding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Senile endometrial atrophy​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cervica​l cancer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endometri​al cancer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atypical endometrial hyper​plasia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Senile endometrial atrophy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Senile endometrial atrophy</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> cause of postmenopausal bleeding all over the world is <strong>s</strong><strong>enile endometrial atrophy.</strong></p>\n<p>In a menopausal woman, <strong>hypoestrogenism</strong> causes <strong>atrophy</strong> of the <strong>endometrium</strong> and<strong> vagina</strong>.The <strong>atrophic endometrial</strong> <strong>surfaces</strong> contain little or no fluid to prevent intracavitary friction. This results in <strong>micro-erosions</strong> of the surface epithelium and a subsequent <strong>chronic inflammatory reaction</strong>, making the endometrium prone to light bleeding or spotting.</p>\n<p><strong>Hormone replacement therapy </strong>is the <strong>second most common</strong> cause of post-menopausal bleeding all over the world. </p>\n<p><strong>Cervical cancer</strong> is the most common cause of postmenopausal bleeding in <strong>India</strong>, while <strong>endometrial cancer</strong> is the most common cause of postmenopausal bleeding in <strong>developed countries</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: Endometrial cancer accounts for only 10 % of cases of post-menopausal bleeding. Still, all postmenopausal women with unexpected uterine bleeding should be evaluated for endometrial carcinoma, since this is a potentially lethal disease. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0266","text":"A 58-year-old, postmenopau​sal woman presented with ‌bleeding PV. Hysteroscopi‌c biopsy revealed complex‍ endometrial hyperplasia ​with atypia. What is the ‌risk of progression of th​is condition to cancer?","raw_text":"A 58-year-old, postmenopausal woman presented with bleeding PV. Hysteroscopic biopsy revealed complex endometrial hyperplasia with atypia. What is the risk of progression of this condition to cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3​%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5​4%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"8​%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2​9%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 29%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 29%</strong></p><p><strong>Complex hyperplasia</strong> with <strong>atypia</strong> has a <strong>29%</strong> increased risk of progression to endometrial cancer. It has the <strong>highest risk</strong> of progressing to carcinoma compared to other varieties of endometrial hyperplasia.</p>\n<p>The risk of endometrial hyperplasia progressing to cancer is tabulated below.</p>\n<table width=\"311\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Type of hyperplasia</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Progression to cancer</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple without atypia</td>\n<td>1%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Complex without atypia</td>\n<td>3%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple with atypia</td>\n<td>8%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Complex with atypia</td>\n<td>29%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0271","text":"Which is the most common b​enign cause of pyometra?","raw_text":"Which is the most common benign cause of pyometra?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endometrial​ hyperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Senile en​dometritis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pelvic inflammatory diseas​e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cervical​ stenosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Senile endometritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Senile endometritis</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common cause</strong> of pyometra is <strong>senile endometritis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pyometra</strong> refers the collection of <strong>pus</strong> in the <strong>uterus</strong>. The most common cancer that causes pyometra is carcinoma cervix followed by carcinoma endometrium.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0267","text":"Which among the following ​is an ideal treatment for‌ a 55-year-old female wit‌h simple hyperplasia of t‍he endometrium with atypi​a?","raw_text":"Which among the following is an ideal treatment for a 55-year-old female with simple hyperplasia of the endometrium with atypia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Simple hy​sterectomy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Medroxy progesterone aceta​te","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Levonor​gesterol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radiot​herapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Simple hysterectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Simple hysterectomy</strong></p><p><strong>Simple hysterectomy</strong> is the best treatment in a case of <strong>atypical</strong> endometrial <strong>hyperplasia </strong>in a <strong>peri-menopausal</strong> and<strong> post-menopausal</strong> female.</p>\n<p>Endometrial hyperplasia <strong>with atypia</strong> carries <strong>29% risk</strong> of <strong>developing into</strong> endometrial <strong>carcinoma</strong> and hence, must be <strong>treated</strong> with <strong>total abdominal hysterectomy</strong> (simple hysterectomy ). The presence of any <strong>concomitant endometrial cancer </strong>must be ruled out by doing <strong>fractional curettage</strong> (or dilatation and curettage) <strong>before</strong> doing a <strong>hysterectomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>If a premenopausal woman does not desire to undergo hysterectomy, <strong>progesterone</strong> can be given and the patient is followed up every 3-6 months. If the <strong>atypia</strong> <strong>persists</strong> during the follow-up<strong> hysterectomy</strong> should be performed.</p>\n<p>The table below summarizes the management of patients with endometrial hyperplasia</p>\n<table style=\"height: 179px; width: 847px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 339.766px;\"><strong>Management of Endometrial hyperplasia</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 489.234px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 339.766px;\"><strong>Hyperplasia without atypia</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 489.234px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 339.766px;\">Pre-menopausal women</td>\n<td style=\"width: 489.234px;\">Progesterone therapy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 339.766px;\">Postmenopausal women:\n<ul>\n<li>Simple hyperplasia without atypia</li>\n<li>Complex hyperplasia without atypia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 489.234px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Follow up without therapy</li>\n<li>Progesterone therapy</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 339.766px;\"><strong>Hyperplasia with atypia</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 489.234px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 339.766px;\">Pre-menopausal women</td>\n<td style=\"width: 489.234px;\">\n<p>If patient wishes to preserve uterus,give High-dose progesterone therapy</p>\n<p>If atypia persists, then perform Total abdominal hysterectomy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 339.766px;\">Peri-menopausal women</td>\n<td style=\"width: 489.234px;\">Total abdominal hysterectomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 339.766px;\">Post-menopausal women</td>\n<td style=\"width: 489.234px;\">Total abdominal hysterectomy +BSO (bilateral salphingo-ophorectomy)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5254","text":"What is the minimum endome​trial thickness on transv‌aginal ultrasonography, a‌bove which you perform a ‍biopsy in a case of postm​enopausal bleeding?","raw_text":"What is the minimum endometrial thickness on transvaginal ultrasonography, above which you perform a biopsy in a case of postmenopausal bleeding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3​mm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4​mm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"7​mm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"9​mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/acac31efbf3e49dca75bdc3081987821x1280x2320.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4mm</strong></p><p>A finding of endometrial thickness <strong>≥ 4 mm </strong>(according to ACOG)<strong> </strong>in a <strong>postmenopausal women</strong> requires further evaluation, such as endometrial biopsy.</p>\n<p>If the endometrial thickness is <strong>≤4 mm</strong> in a <strong>post menopausal female</strong>, malignancy is rare and hence, can be managed by <strong>tranexamic acid</strong>. If the <strong>bleeding</strong> still <strong>persists</strong>, the patient needs to undergo <strong>fractional curettage and hysteroscopy.</strong></p>\n<p>The algorithm for <strong>next step</strong> for <strong>abnormal uterine bleeding</strong> in different age groups is as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 848px; height: 177px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230.703px;\"> Age group</td>\n<td style=\"width: 261.031px;\">Endometrial thickness on TVS</td>\n<td style=\"width: 334.266px;\">Action</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230.703px;\">Age &lt;40years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 261.031px;\"> &gt;12mm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 334.266px;\">Endometrial sampling/Endometrial biopsy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230.703px;\">Age&gt;40 years or Age &gt;45 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 261.031px;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 334.266px;\">Endometrial sampling/Endometrial biopsy is done irrespective of usg findings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230.703px;\">Post menopausal women</td>\n<td style=\"width: 261.031px;\">≥4mm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 334.266px;\"> Endometrial sampling/Endometrial biopsy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 230.703px;\">Post menopausal women</td>\n<td style=\"width: 261.031px;\">&lt;4mm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 334.266px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Reassurance+tranexamic acid</li>\n<li>If, bleeding still persists then do endometrial biopsy</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5074","text":"What is the most common hi​stopathological variety o‌f endometrial cancer?","raw_text":"What is the most common histopathological variety of endometrial cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamo​us cell","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adenoca​rcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Clear​ cell","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ser​ous","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adenocarcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adenocarcinoma</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> variety of endometrial cancer is <strong>adenocarcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The other varieties of endometrial cancer are squamous cell, clear cell, and serous. The <strong>most malignant </strong>varieties are <strong>clear cell </strong>carcinoma and <strong>papillary serous </strong>tumor.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5077","text":"Which of the following pre​disposes a patient to dev‌elop type 2 carcinoma end‌ometrium?","raw_text":"Which of the following predisposes a patient to develop type 2 carcinoma endometrium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"KRAS m​utation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Premenopaus​al age group","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"PTEN m​utation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atrophic e​ndometrium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Atrophic endometrium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Atrophic endometrium</strong></p><p><strong>Type 2 carcinoma endometrium</strong> is commonly seen in menopausal women with <strong>atrophic endometrium</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Histological classification</strong> of endometrial cancer:</p>\n<table width=\"294\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Type 1 (Endometrioid)</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Type 2 (Nonendometriod)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Estrogen dependent</td>\n<td>Estrogen independent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Occurs in Pre- and Peri-menopausal women</td>\n<td>Occurs in post-menopausal women</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hyperplasia present</td>\n<td>Hyperplasia absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Good prognosis</td>\n<td>Poor prognosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Associated with PTEN/KRAS mutation</td>\n<td>Associated with p53 mutation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: Type 1 (estrogen-dependent) endometrial CA is the more common type.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5073","text":"Which of the following con​stitute corpus cancer syn‌drome? Obesity Hypertensi‌on Stroke Diabetes mellit‍us Endometrial carcinoma ​Dysgerminoma","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following constitute corpus cancer syndrome?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Stroke</li>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus </li>\n<li>Endometrial carcinoma</li>\n<li>Dysgerminoma</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2,​ 3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3,​ 4, 6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2,​ 4, 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3,​ 4, 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 2, 4, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 2, 4, 5</strong></p><p><strong>Corpus cancer syndrome</strong> constitutes of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Cancer of the body of the uterus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Etiology of endometrial carcinoma</strong>: (Mnemonic: Family Has OLD AUNTI)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Family history</li>\n<li><strong>Hypertension</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Obesity</strong></li>\n<li>Late menopause/Early menarche</li>\n<li><strong>Diabetes</strong></li>\n<li>Atypical endometrial hyperplasia</li>\n<li>Unopposed estrogen/increased estrogen → Hormone replacement therapy, fibroid, PCOD</li>\n<li>Nulliparous</li>\n<li>Tamoxifen therapy<strong> </strong>(Raloxifene has no genito-urinary action)</li>\n<li>Infertility/irregular menses.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Protective factors</strong> against endometrial carcinoma include the use of <strong>OCPs</strong>, <strong>smoking,</strong> and <strong>multiparity</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0653","text":"A gynecologist is performi​ng the following procedur‌e in a woman with postmen‌opausal bleeding. Identif‍y it.","raw_text":"A gynecologist is performing the following procedure in a woman with postmenopausal bleeding. Identify it.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transvagina​l sonography","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Balloon endometrial ablati​on","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Saline infusion sonography​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hysterosal​pingography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Saline infusion sonography","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/31727b8d761b475aa0c8a9972b9771d3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e182e5cd908b483ab4db1f0ffd7cbfd5x625x481.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Saline infusion sonography</strong></p><p>The image depicts <strong>s</strong><strong>aline infusion sonography </strong>(SIS), also known as <strong>sonohysterography</strong>. It is usually done after a transvaginal ultrasound, to <strong>assess</strong> the detailed <strong>anatomy</strong> of the <strong>uterine cavity.</strong></p>\n<p>In this procedure, a balloon catheter is inserted through the cervix and the<strong> endometrial cavity</strong> is <strong>distended</strong> with 20-40ml of <strong>sterile saline. </strong>The balloon is inflated to block the backflow of the infused sterile saline and <strong>transvaginal ultrasound</strong> is performed to visualize the cavity. </p>\n<p>It is used:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>To determine the endometrial thickness </li>\n<li>To determine the cause in cases of infertility</li>\n<li>To identify Mullerian anomalies </li>\n<li>To visualize endometrial polyps and pedunculated fibroids</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the appearance of the uterus on SIS.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Transvaginal ultrasound is the initial evaluation for postmenopausal bleeding.</p>\n<p>Option B: In balloon endometrial ablation, a balloon is inserted into the uterus and inflated with glycine or 5% glucose. It is then heated up, and the endometrium is ablated by the tamponade effect.</p>\n<p>Option D: In hysterosalpingography, contrast dye is inserted into the uterus via a catheter, and the uterine and fallopian tube anatomy is visualized.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5080","text":"A menopausal patient suspe​cted to have endometrial ‌carcinoma is undergoing a‌ transvaginal ultrasound.‍ Which of the following f​indings would be inconsis‌tent with her diagnosis?","raw_text":"A menopausal patient suspected to have endometrial carcinoma is undergoing a transvaginal ultrasound. Which of the following findings would be inconsistent with her diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endometrial thickness of 7​ mm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperechoic​ endometrium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased vascular resista​nce","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fluid in ut​erine cavity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased vascular resistance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased vascular resistance</strong></p><p><strong>Decreased vascular resistance</strong> with increased tumor vascularity is seen in <strong>carcinoma endometrium</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>USG findings</strong> in carcinoma endometrium include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Endometrial thickness &gt;4 mm in postmenopausal women and &gt;10 mm in perimenopausal women</li>\n<li>Hyperechoic endometrium with irregular outlines</li>\n<li>Increased tumor vascularity with low vascular resistance</li>\n<li>Fluid in uterine cavity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6979","text":"A 52-year-old​ woman with ​postmenopausal bleeding h‌as an endometrial thickne‌ss of 7 mm on ultrasound.‍ What is the next step in​ her management?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old​ woman with postmenopausal bleeding has an endometrial thickness of 7 mm on ultrasound. What is the next step in her management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Follow up USG after 3 mont​hs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyster​ectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endometri​al biopsy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Progestero​ne therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Endometrial biopsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Endometrial biopsy</strong></p><p>The next line of management in a patient with increased endometrial thickness is an <strong>endometrial biopsy</strong>.</p>\n<p>In a <strong>postmenopausal</strong> woman, the normal <strong>endometrium</strong> should not exceed 4 mm in thickness. If it <strong>exceeds 4 mm</strong>, then histopathological examination (<strong>HPE</strong>) of the endometrium is mandatory, <strong>irrespective</strong> of the presence or absence of <strong>symptoms. </strong>In a <strong>perimenopausal</strong> woman, the cutoff for normal <strong>endometrial</strong> thickness is <strong>10mm</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Hysteroscopy and biopsy </strong>is <strong>preferred</strong> over other blind curettage methods for HPE. A biopsy is made from suspicious areas after visualization so chances of missing the lesion are reduced.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0924","text":"A 54-year-old woman with p​ostmenopausal bleeding is‌ suspected to have an end‌ometrial lesion. What is ‍the gold standard investi​gation to confirm her dia‌gnosis?","raw_text":"A 54-year-old woman with postmenopausal bleeding is suspected to have an endometrial lesion. What is the gold standard investigation to confirm her diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fractional​ curettage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dilatation a​nd curettage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hysterosco​pic biopsy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Laparosco​pic biopsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hysteroscopic biopsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eb433d5ed4324c3f9e7634b2ae1f3c14x1280x878.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hysteroscopic biopsy</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 89\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Hysteroscopic inspection </strong>and selective <strong>biopsy</strong> is now considered the<strong> gold standard</strong> in the diagnosis of an<strong> endometrial lesion. </strong>It is preferred for the <strong>initial evaluation</strong> of women with bleeding suspicious for malignancy.</p>\n<p>Endometrial sampling can be done by hysteroscopic biopsy. It is more sensitive for focal endometrial lesions. Dilatation and curettage may also aid the sampling of endometrial lesions.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 735\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 735\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 732\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Ultrasound, CT, and MRI are adjuvant to other investigations. <strong>Diagnostic laparoscopy</strong> will be required to study the nature of the tumor and its spread if ultrasound picks up a <strong>pelvic tumor</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the procedure of hysteroscopy.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5079","text":"A 62-year-old woman presen​ts with vaginal bleeding ‌11 years after she had at‌tained menopause. An endo‍metrial biopsy reveals ad​enocarcinoma. What is the‌ gold standard method for​ staging her condition?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old woman presents with vaginal bleeding 11 years after she had attained menopause. An endometrial biopsy reveals adenocarcinoma. What is the gold standard method for staging her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M​RI","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sur​gery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Endometri​al biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PET​-CT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Surgery</strong></p><p>The given scenario depicts <strong>endometrial carcinoma </strong>and the <strong>gold standard</strong> method for <strong>staging</strong> this condition is <strong>surgery.</strong></p>\n<p>According to <span class=\"nowrap\">FIGO/TNM</span> classification system, a <strong>total hysterectomy</strong> with <strong>bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy</strong> and <strong>pelvic and para-aortic lymph node dissection</strong> is the standard staging procedure for endometrial cancer. </p>\n<p>Option A: Contrast-enhanced <strong>magnetic resonance imaging</strong> (MRI) appears to be the best radiographic modality for detecting<strong> myometrial invasion</strong> or <strong>cervical involvement</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Hysteroscopy </strong>visualizes the entire uterine lining and a <strong>biopsy</strong> is taken from suspicious areas. This is not 100% predictive, as an <strong>early lesion</strong> can be <strong>missed</strong>. Recently, the concern has been raised regarding the spilling of cancer cells into the peritoneal cavity during hysteroscopy.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>PET–CT</strong> reveals metabolic activity in the tissue and is highly sensitive and specific for detecting positive<strong> pelvic and/or para-aortic lymphadenopathy</strong> as well as <strong>distant metastases</strong> in selected high-risk patients with endometrial cancer.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5078","text":"Which lymph nodes are most​ commonly involved in pat‌ients with endometrial ca‌rcinoma?","raw_text":"Which lymph nodes are most commonly involved in patients with endometrial carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fem​oral","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ingu​inal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Para-​aortic","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Para-​colic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Para-aortic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Para-aortic</strong></p><p><strong>Pelvic</strong> and <strong>para-aortic</strong> lymph nodes are commonly involved in <strong>endometrial cancer</strong>.</p>\n<p>The spread is through the infundibulopelvic ligament.</p>\n<p>Rarely, inguinal &amp; femoral lymph nodes are involved.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0269","text":"A patient presented with c​arcinoma endometrium invo‌lving &gt;50% myometrium, ex‌tending to the vagina but‍ with no involvement of p​ara-aortic and pre aortic‌ nodes. What is the stage​ of her disease?","raw_text":"A patient presented with carcinoma endometrium involving &gt;50% myometrium, extending to the vagina but with no involvement of para-aortic and pre aortic nodes. What is the stage of her disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"II​I A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"II​I B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"III​ C1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"III​ C2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. III B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. III B</strong></p><p><strong>Carcinoma endometrium</strong> <strong>involving</strong> the <strong>vagina</strong> but having no lymph node involvement is classified as stage <strong>IIIB</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5082","text":"Which of the following is ​indicated for the treatme‌nt of a patient with earl‌y endometrial cancer?","raw_text":"Which of the following is indicated for the treatment of a patient with early endometrial cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hysterectomy, B/L salpingo​-oophorectomy, lymph node‌ sampling","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hysterectomy, B/L salpingo​-oophorectomy, chemoradia‌tion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hysterectomy, radiotherapy​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chemora​diation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hysterectomy, B/L salpingo-oophorectomy, lymph node sampling","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hysterectomy, B/L salpingo-oophorectomy, lymph node sampling</strong></p><p><strong>Early endometrial cancer</strong> (stages I &amp; II) is primarily <strong>managed surgically</strong>. The surgery performed here is abdominal hysterectomy, B/L salpingo-oophorectomy, omentectomy and pelvic &amp; para-aortic lymph node sampling.</p>\n<p><strong>Postoperative management</strong> of endometrial cancer:</p>\n<p>In <strong>low-risk</strong> endometrial cancer,<strong> no further treatment</strong> is required.</p>\n<p>In intermediate-risk, radiotherapy is done.</p>\n<p>In high risk, chemotherapy + radiotherapy is done.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3068","text":"A woman with endometrial c​arcinoma is undergoing ra‌diotherapy. Which of the ‌following is true?","raw_text":"A woman with endometrial carcinoma is undergoing radiotherapy. Which of the following is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intensity is inversely pro​portional to squared dist‌ance","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Small blood vessels are mo​st radioresistant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rapidly proliferating cell​s are most radioresistant‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Small bowel is most radior​esistant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Intensity is inversely proportional to squared distance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Intensity is inversely proportional to squared distance</strong></p><p>The <strong>intensity</strong> of radiation is <strong>inversely proportional </strong>to<strong> the squared distance. </strong>This is known as the <strong>inverse square law</strong> in radiotherapy.</p>\n<p><strong>Chemoradiation</strong> is the mainstay of treatment of <strong>stage III and IV</strong> endometrial cancers. They are treated with brachytherapy followed by external radiation.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span><strong><span class=\"Apple-converted-space\">Brachytherapy</span></strong><span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span>is a form of radiation therapy in which the <strong>radiation source </strong>is placed<strong> close to </strong>the <strong>tumor</strong>.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></p>\n<p><span class=\"Apple-converted-space\">Other options: </span></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Larger blood vessels</strong> are more <strong>radioresistant</strong> than microvasculature.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Permanent non-dividing cells</strong> are more <strong>radioresistant</strong> than rapidly proliferating cells.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Small intestinal epithelial cells</strong> (intestinal crypts), which divide rapidly with no differentiation are the <strong>most radiosensitive</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5083","text":"Which among the following ​is the mainstay of treatm‌ent in a patient diagnose‌d with Stage III endometr‍ial cancer?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the mainstay of treatment in a patient diagnosed with Stage III endometrial cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sur​gery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chemot​herapy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"External beam radiotherapy​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Brachy​therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chemotherapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chemotherapy</strong></p><p><strong>Chemotherapy</strong> is the mainstay of treatment in <strong>Stage III endometrial cancer</strong>.</p>\n<p>In order to stage carcinoma endometrium, total abdominal hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy and lymphadenectomy is done (surgical staging). </p>\n<p>Thus the management, once staging is done, is mainly postoperative. Up to <strong>stage II</strong>, <strong>radiotherapy</strong> can be given. Stages <strong>III &amp; IV</strong> are managed by chemotherapy such as <strong>doxorubicin, cisplatin,</strong> and<strong> paclitaxel.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Stage III</strong> is said to be inoperable but <strong>debulking surgery</strong> is sometimes attempted. However, the mainstay of treatment for stage III disease remains chemotherapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"5x2nnCR0","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"46_lower_urinary_tract","title":"46.Lower Urinary Tract","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MC3153","text":"A 36-year-old woman presen‌ted with intermittent sup‌rapubic pain, increased u​rinary frequency, and dys​uria for 2 months. A cyst​oscopy revealed the prese​nce of Hunner ulcers. Wha​t is the likely diagnosis‍?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old woman presented with intermittent suprapubic pain, increased urinary frequency, and dysuria for 2 months. A cystoscopy revealed the presence of Hunner ulcers. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malako‌plakia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transitional cell carcinom‌a of bladder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Follicula‌r cystitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interstiti‌al cystitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Interstitial cystitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6802a354ad8f47ad8efc07f22b39b0adx405x421.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Interstitial cystitis</strong></p><p><strong>Hunner ulcers</strong> are chronic mucosal ulcers that are characteristic of <strong>interstitial cystitis</strong> or chronic pelvic pain syndrome.</p>\n<p>It is a rare idiopathic condition that occurs most frequently in <strong>women. </strong>It presents with intermittent severe suprapubic pain, urinary frequency, urgency, hematuria, and dysuria.</p>\n<p>The following cystoscopic findings are seen in the bladder mucosa:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fissures</li>\n<li>Punctate hemorrhages (<strong>glomerulations</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The microscopic findings include:<strong> </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mucosal ulceration</strong> covered by fibrin and necrotic material</li>\n<li>Increased numbers of mucosal<strong> mast cells</strong></li>\n<li>Submucosal edema</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In later stages, transmural fibrosis may occur, leading to a <strong>contracted bladder</strong>. Biopsy must be done to <strong>rule out carcinoma in situ,</strong> which may clinically mimic interstitial cystitis. </p>\n<p>The following image shows a Hunner's ulcer in a patient with interstitial cystitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3149","text":"A 31-year-old man presente‌d with a history of recur‌rent UTIs and hematuria. ​Urine culture showed grow​th of E. coli. Flexible c​ystoscopy revealed yellow​-brown soft plaques with ​a central umbilication. M‍icroscopy of the plaque s‌howed Michaelis-Gutmann b‌odies. What is the likely​ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 31-year-old man presented with a history of recurrent UTIs and hematuria. Urine culture showed growth of E. coli. Flexible cystoscopy revealed yellow-brown soft plaques with a central umbilication. Microscopy of the plaque showed Michaelis-Gutmann bodies. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Xanthogranulomatous cystit‌is","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malako‌plakia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Follicula‌r cystitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polypoid‌ cystitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Malakoplakia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1838c3ab42cb4c83bdd564fd72f0a7dbx1280x1079.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Malakoplakia</strong></p><p>This clinical<strong> </strong>scenario with the presence of Michaelis-Gutmann bodies is suggestive of <strong>malakoplakia.</strong></p>\n<p>Malakoplakia is a chronic inflammatory reaction resulting from acquired <strong>defects </strong>in<strong> phagocyte function</strong>, commonly affecting the urinary bladder. It can also be seen in other organ systems like the gastrointestinal tract, female genital tract, and central nervous system.</p>\n<p>It is seen in chronic bacterial infection, mostly by <em><strong>Escherichia coli</strong></em> or occasionally <em><strong>Proteus</strong></em> species.  </p>\n<p>Grossly, it presents as soft, <strong>yellow-brown</strong>, slightly raised <strong>mucosal plaques</strong>. Microscopically, the following findings are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Large,<strong> foamy macrophages </strong>containing -\n<ul>\n<li>Abundant granular cytoplasm due to undigested bacterial products</li>\n<li><strong>Michaelis-Gutmann</strong> bodies - <strong>PAS</strong>-<strong>positive</strong> basophilic concretions due to iron and calcium deposits in lysosomes</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Occasional multinucleated giant cells and lymphocytes</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Xanthogranulomatous cystitis is morphologically and probably pathogenetically similar to malakoplakia, but it lacks Michaelis–Gutmann bodies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3156","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of polypoid cystitis?‌","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of polypoid cystitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclophosp‌hamide use","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Systemic allergic disorder‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Indwelling‌ catheters","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adenovirus‌ infection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Indwelling catheters","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Indwelling catheters</strong></p><p>The most common cause of <strong>polypoid</strong> <strong>cystitis</strong> is <strong>indwelling catheters.</strong></p>\n<p>Polypoid cystitis is an inflammatory lesion resulting from <strong>mucosal</strong> <strong>irritation</strong>. It may also be associated with radiation therapy and malignant tumors. It usually involves the posterior wall of the bladder.</p>\n<p>Grossly, it may show bulbous or <strong>papillary</strong> <strong>appearance</strong> and may be confused with papillary urothelial carcinoma. Microscopically, the papillary projections are broad-based, non-branching, and <strong>without</strong> <strong>atypia</strong>. Stroma is edematous with <strong>minimal inflammation</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1252\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Options A and D: <strong>Adenovirus</strong> infection and the use of anti-cancer drugs like <strong>cyclophosphamide</strong> cause <strong>hemorrhagic cystitis.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Option B: Systemic <strong>allergic</strong> disorders cause <strong>eosinophilic cystitis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3154","text":"A pathologist observed a m‌etaplastic lesion in a bi‌opsy sample taken from th​e bladder mucosa of a pat​ient. Which of the follow​ing is not a differential​ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A pathologist observed a metaplastic lesion in a biopsy sample taken from the bladder mucosa of a patient. Which of the following is not a differential diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malako‌plakia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nephrogen‌ic adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cystitis g‌landularis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cystitis‌ cystica","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Malakoplakia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Malakoplakia</strong></p><p><strong>Malakoplakia</strong> is not a metaplastic lesion of the bladder. It is a type of chronic <strong>granulomatous cystitis</strong> due to defective phagocytic function that may mimic malignancy.</p>\n<p><strong>Metaplastic lesions</strong> of the bladder include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nests of urothelium (Brunn nests) may grow downward into the lamina propria resulting in the following lesions -\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cystitis glandularis</strong> - epithelial cells in the center of the nest undergo metaplasia and take on a cuboidal or columnar appearance</li>\n<li><strong>Cystitis cystica</strong> - epithelial cells retract to produce cystic spaces lined by flattened urothelium</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Squamous metaplasia </strong>- in response to injury, urothelium is replaced by stratified squamous epithelium.</li>\n<li><strong>Nephrogenic adenoma </strong>- due to implantation of shed renal tubular cells at sites of injured urothelium, resulting in replacement of urothelium by cuboidal epithelium with a papillary growth pattern</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3148","text":"What is the most common tu‌mor of the urinary bladde‌r?","raw_text":"What is the most common tumor of the urinary bladder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous ce‌ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adenoca‌rcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transitiona‌l carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Small cell‌ carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Transitional carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Transitional carcinoma</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> tumor of the urinary bladder is <strong>transitional cell </strong>carcinoma or<strong> urothelial carcinoma</strong>. </p>\n<p>Other epithelial tumors like squamous cell carcinoma, adenocarcinoma, and small cell carcinoma may also occur.</p>\n<p><strong>Sarcomas</strong> are distinctly <strong>uncommon</strong> in the bladder. However, the most common sarcoma in infancy or childhood is embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma, and the most common sarcoma in the bladder in adults is leiomyosarcoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3150","text":"A 57-year-old man presente‌d with chronic hematuria ‌and dysuria. Microscopic ​examination of urine show​ed the following parasiti​c eggs. What type of blad​der carcinoma is commonly​ associated with this con‍dition?","raw_text":"A 57-year-old man presented with chronic hematuria and dysuria. Microscopic examination of urine showed the following parasitic eggs. What type of bladder carcinoma is commonly associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous ce‌ll carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Transitional cell carcinom‌a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenoca‌rcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Small cell‌ carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Squamous cell carcinoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3cee031e734f42848ad4eb8a20132a59.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Squamous cell carcinoma</strong></p><p>The image shows oval-shaped eggs with the terminal spine which is characteristic of<strong> <em>Schistosoma haematobium</em></strong><em>. </em>It is <strong>most commonly</strong> associated with<strong><em> </em>squamous cell carcinoma</strong> of the bladder.</p>\n<p>The ova are deposited in the bladder wall and incite a brisk <strong>chronic inflammatory response.</strong> This induces progressive mucosal <strong>squamous</strong> <strong>metaplasia</strong> and dysplasia and, in some instances, neoplasia.</p>\n<p>Schistosomal infection can less frequently result in adenocarcinoma or transitional cell carcinoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3152","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a carcinogen for blad‌der cancer?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a carcinogen for bladder cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Benz‌idine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isopropy‌l alcohol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aryl ‌amines","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phena‌cetin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Isopropyl alcohol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Isopropyl alcohol</strong></p><p>Isopropyl alcohol is not implicated in the causation of bladder cancer.</p>\n<p><strong>Benzidine</strong>, <strong>arylamines,</strong> and <strong>phenacetin</strong> are well-established risk factors for <strong>bladder cancer</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3163","text":"Which of the following gen‌etic changes is not assoc‌iated with bladder carcin​oma?","raw_text":"Which of the following genetic changes is not associated with bladder carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gain-of-function mutations‌ in FGFR3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Activating mutations in th‌e NRAS","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Loss-of-function mutations‌ in the TP53","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"9p chromoso‌me deletions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Activating mutations in the NRAS","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Activating mutations in the NRAS</strong></p><p><strong>Activating mutation</strong> in <strong>HRAS</strong> (not NRAS) is responsible for <strong>bladder carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Genetic changes associated with carcinoma bladder:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Noninvasive low-grade papillary urothelial carcinoma is associated with:\n<ul>\n<li>Gain-of-function mutations of <strong>FGFR3</strong> and <strong>HRAS </strong>genes</li>\n<li>Chromosome<strong> 9p</strong> and <strong>9q</strong> <strong>deletions</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>High-grade invasive urothelial carcinoma (either papillary or flat) is associated with:\n<ul>\n<li>Loss-of-function mutations of <strong>TP53</strong> and <strong>RB</strong> genes</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Erdafitinib (kinase inhibitor) is the first targeted drug therapy approved by the FDA for the treatment of metastatic urothelial carcinoma especially in patients with mutations of FGFR3 or FGFR2.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3151","text":"The most consistent and pe‌rsistent feature of bladd‌er carcinoma is:","raw_text":"The most consistent and persistent feature of bladder carcinoma is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Painless ‌hematuria","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Painful ‌hematuria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dys‌uria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suprapu‌bic mass","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Painless hematuria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Painless hematuria</strong></p><p>The most <strong>consistent</strong> and <strong>persistent</strong> feature of carcinoma bladder is <strong>painless hematuria.</strong></p>\n<p>Bladder tumors produce a wide range of clinical features including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Painless hematuria - classical feature and may be the only clinical manifestation</li>\n<li>Irri­tative urinary symptoms (<strong>frequency</strong>, <strong>urgency</strong>, dysuria) - diffuse tu­mors or tumors at the bladder neck</li>\n<li><strong>Pyelonephritis</strong> or <strong>hydronephrosis </strong>- when the ureteral orifice is involved</li>\n<li><strong>Obstructive</strong> symptoms, palpa­ble <strong>suprapubic mass</strong>, or lower extrem­ity edema - late presentation with a large tumor mass</li>\n<li>Weight loss and/or lo­calized bone pain - metastatic disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3164","text":"Which of the following is ‌not included in the lates‌t International Society o​f Urological Pathology gr​ading of urothelial tumor​s?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included in the latest International Society of Urological Pathology grading of urothelial tumors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urothelial‌ papilloma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urothelial neoplasm of low‌ malignant potential","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Papillary urothelial carci‌noma, low grade","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Papillary urothelial carci‌noma, grade 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Papillary urothelial carcinoma, grade 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Papillary urothelial carcinoma, grade 3</strong></p><p>International Society of Urological Pathology (<strong>ISUP</strong>) grading system classifies papillary urothelial carcinoma into 2 grades - <strong>low grade </strong>and<strong> high grade</strong>. The classification of papillary urothelial carcinoma into<strong> grades 1, 2, 3</strong> was was part of the earlier <strong>WHO </strong>classification.</p>\n<p>Grading of urothelial (transitional cell) tumors:</p>\n<p><strong>WHO/ISUP grades:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Urothelial papilloma</li>\n<li>Urothelial neoplasm of low malignant potential</li>\n<li>Papillary urothelial carcinoma, low grade</li>\n<li>Papillary urothelial carcinoma, high grade</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>WHO grades</strong> (old classification):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Urothelial papilloma</li>\n<li>Urothelial neoplasm of low malignant potential</li>\n<li>Papillary urothelial carcinoma, grade 1</li>\n<li>Papillary urothelial carcinoma, grade 2</li>\n<li>Papillary urothelial carcinoma, grade 3</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6682","text":"A 67-year-old chronic smok‌er with gross painless he‌maturia was diagnosed wit​h infiltrating urothelial​ carcinoma. What is the m​ost important prognostic ​factor for his condition?​","raw_text":"A 67-year-old chronic smoker with gross painless hematuria was diagnosed with infiltrating urothelial carcinoma. What is the most important prognostic factor for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Si‌ze","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gr‌ade","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"St‌age","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Histolog‌ical type","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stage</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The <strong>staging</strong> (extent of spread) at the time of initial diagnosis is the most important <strong>prognostic</strong> factor in <strong>infiltrating urothelial carcinoma.</strong></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The prognosis of bladder carcinoma depends on the histologic grade and the stage at diagnosis. </span><span class=\"s1\">As most infiltrating urothelial carcinomas are <strong>high-grade</strong>, staging becomes the major factor to determine the prognosis. </span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"K09gJwPv","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"46_thyroid_and_antithyroid_agents","title":"46.Thyroid And Antithyroid Agents","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MC3475","text":"Levothyroxine/liothyronine‌ acts by binding to thyro‌id receptors. Thyroid hor‌mone receptors belong to ‌which of the following cl‌ass of receptors?","raw_text":"Levothyroxine/liothyronine acts by binding to thyroid receptors. Thyroid hormone receptors belong to which of the  following class of receptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transmembrane receptor lin‌ked to tyrosine kinase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nuclear ‌receptor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ion channe‌l receptor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"G protein coupled receptor‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nuclear receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nuclear receptor</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Thyroid hormone receptors belong to the class of <strong>nuclear receptors. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Nuclear receptors are a family of ligand-regulated transcription factors that are activated by various <strong>lipid-soluble signals</strong>, including retinoic acid, thyroid hormone, and steroid hormones. They primarily function as <strong>transcription factors</strong> and <strong>directly interact with the genomic DNA </strong>to regulate gene expression. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Nuclear receptors can either remain anchored in the cytoplasm via chaperone proteins until freed by ligand-binding (e.g., steroid and vitamin D receptors) or can reside in the nucleus bound to specific DNA response elements even in the absence of ligand (e.g., thyroid hormone, PPARγ, and retinoic acid receptors).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9329","text":"In comparison to thyroxine‌, liothyronine has ______‌_____.","raw_text":"In comparison to thyroxine, liothyronine has ___________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Longer h‌alf life","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Better a‌bsorption","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Slower onse‌t of action","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Less p‌otency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Better absorption","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Better absorption</strong></p><p><strong>Liothyronine</strong> has <strong>better absorption</strong> compared to levothyroxine. It is almost<strong> completely absorbed (100%) </strong>when compared to <strong>thyroxine (80%).</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 438.02px; height: 213px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 314px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 42px;\"><strong> Liothyronine(T3)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.02px;\"><strong> Levothyroxine(T4)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 314px;\"><strong>Onset of action</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 42px;\">Rapid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.02px;\">Slow </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 314px;\"><strong>Potency</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 42px;\">More potent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.02px;\">Less potent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 314px;\"><strong>Half-life</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 42px;\">T<sub>1/2</sub> -18-24 hr</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.02px;\">T<sub>1/2</sub> - 7-8 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 314px;\"><strong>Source</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 42px;\">25% from gland; 75% from peripheral conversion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.02px;\">Solely produced by the gland </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 314px;\"><strong>Plasma protein binding</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 42px;\">0.2% unbound</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.02px;\">0.02% unbound</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Liotrix</strong> is a<strong> 4:1</strong> mixture of Levothyroxine sodium <strong>(LT4): </strong>Liothyronine sodium <strong>(LT3)</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9333","text":"A 23-year-old hypothyroid ‌patient taking levothyrox‌ine regularly has now bec‌ome pregnant. What would ‌your advice be regarding ‌levothyroxine?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old hypothyroid patient taking levothyroxine regularly has now become pregnant. What would your advice be regarding levothyroxine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Switch to liothyronine as ‌levothyroxine is teratoge‌nic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increase the dose as pregn‌ancy causes an increased ‌demand for levothyroxine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Continue the same dose but‌ monitor the serum levels‌ on a fortnightly basis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stop levothyroxine till th‌e end of the first trimes‌ter as it has teratogenic‌ potential","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increase the dose as pregnancy causes an increased demand for levothyroxine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increase the dose as pregnancy causes an increased demand for levothyroxine</strong></p><p>The dose of <strong>levothyroxine </strong>in patients who become<strong> pregnant </strong>should usually be<strong> increased. </strong>This is because demand for levothyroxine increases in pregnancy and low thyroxine levels are associated with adverse fetal outcomes such as mental retardation. </p>\n<p>The dose of levothyroxine is increased by<strong> 30-50%</strong> by asking the patient to take 2 extra pills per week. Increased demand for levothyroxine in pregnancy is due to:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li>The increased serum concentration of thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG) induced by estrogen</li>\n<li>Increased expression of type 3 deiodinase in the placenta</li>\n<li>A small amount of passage of levothyroxine to the fetus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Thyroid hormones are not teratogenic. <strong>Liothyronine</strong> is <strong>not preferred</strong> in pregnancy because T3 has a short half-life and requires frequent dosing to limit large fluctuations in levels. In addition, excess T3 often acts to normalize TSH while lowering T4, thereby risking inadequate T4 availability to the fetus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3477","text":"A patient on levothyroxine‌ supplementation would ne‌ed to increase her dose i‌f she is taking _______.","raw_text":"A patient on levothyroxine supplementation would need to increase her dose if she is taking _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methylpre‌dnisolone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Asp‌irin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Estr‌adiol","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Furos‌emide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Estradiol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Estradiol</strong></p><p>Patients who start <strong>estrogen</strong> therapy will need to increase their thyroid supplementation doses. </p>\n<p>Estrogens<strong> raise</strong> the circulating levels of <strong>thyroxine-binding globulin</strong> (TBG), thereby increasing the bound fraction and <strong>decreasing</strong> the <strong>free</strong> (bioactive) fraction of circulating <strong>thyroxine</strong> (T4). As a consequence, estrogen therapy increases the T4 supplementation requirements of women being treated for primary hypothyroidism.</p>\n<p>Drugs <strong>increasing</strong> TBG levels: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Estrogens</li>\n<li>Tamoxifen </li>\n<li>Methadone</li>\n<li>5-fluorouracil  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Drugs <strong>decreasing</strong> TBG levels: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Androgens</li>\n<li>Corticosteroids</li>\n<li>Niacin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Drugs <strong>decreasing</strong> binding of T4 to TBG (by displacement):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Salicylates (aspirin, mefenamic acid) </li>\n<li>Heparin</li>\n<li>Furosemide</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3481","text":"A 45-year-old patient suff‌ering from Graves disease‌ has been on treatment fo‌r the past three weeks. S‌he has now developed feve‌r and sore throat with an‌ absolute neutrophil coun​t of 80 neutrophils per m‍icrolitre of blood. The u‍se of which of the follow‌ing drugs can result in s‍uch a clinical picture? M‌ethimazole Propylthiourac‍il Lugol's iodine Cortico​steroids Propranolol","raw_text":"<p>A 45-year-old patient suffering from Graves disease has been on treatment for the past three weeks. She has now developed fever and sore throat with an absolute neutrophil count of 80 neutrophils per microlitre of blood. The use of which of the following drugs can result in such a clinical picture?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Methimazole</li>\n<li>Propylthiouracil</li>\n<li>Lugol's iodine</li>\n<li>Corticosteroids</li>\n<li>Propranolol</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onl‌y 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2‌ or 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 o‌r 2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3, 4‌ or 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 or 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 or 2</strong></p><p>This patient is suffering from<strong> agranulocytosis</strong> which is a serious<strong> adverse effect</strong> that can be seen with the use of either <strong>methimazole </strong>or<strong> propylthiouracil.</strong></p>\n<p>Agranulocytosis usually occurs in the initial weeks or months of therapy. It occurs rapidly and periodic monitoring of granulocyte levels is generally ineffective.</p>\n<p>However, it is <strong>reversible</strong> on discontinuation of the drug. Therefore, patients who are on these drugs should be instructed to <strong>report immediately</strong> if they develop <strong>fever</strong> or <strong>sore throat</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9336","text":"A patient developed thyroi‌d storm in the post-opera‌tive ward and was immedia‌tely started on IV propra‌nolol and a loading dose ‌of propylthiouracil. The ‌antithyroid drug acts by ​inhibiting which of the f‍ollowing deiodinases?","raw_text":"A patient developed thyroid storm in the post-operative ward and was immediately started on IV propranolol and a loading dose of propylthiouracil. The antithyroid drug acts by inhibiting which of the following deiodinases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"D1 ‌only","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"D2 ‌only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"D1 a‌nd D2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"D‌3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. D1 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/686777488e554c3a874bb81252c8993cx1280x1660.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. D1 only</strong></p><p><strong>Propylthiouracil inhibits </strong>only <strong>type I deiodinase (D1).</strong></p>\n<p>Deiodinases are the enzymes that are involved in the peripheral conversion of T4 to either T3 or reverse T3. Type I deiodinase (D1) and type II deiodinase (D2) convert T4 to T3. Type III (D3) is involved in the conversion of T4 to reverse T3. </p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Amiodarone</strong> <strong>inhibits</strong> both <strong>types I and type II</strong> deiodinases. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3479","text":"A 40-year-old man presente‌d with complaints of incr‌eased sweating and weight‌ loss over the past two m‌onths. On examination, he‌ is tachycardic, has exop‌hthalmos, and a diffusely​ enlarged thyroid gland. ‍Which of the following is‍ the drug of choice for t‌he management of this con‍dition?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presented with complaints of increased sweating and weight loss over the past two months. On examination, he is tachycardic, has exophthalmos, and a diffusely enlarged thyroid gland.  Which of the following is the drug of choice for the management of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methi‌mazole","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Propylth‌iouracil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Levoth‌yroxine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lugol's‌ iodine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Methimazole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Methimazole</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Graves disease</strong> and the <strong>drug of choice</strong> is <strong>methimazole.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Methimazole is preferred over propylthiouracil due to fewer side effects and single-daily dosing (compared to PTU which is given TID).</p>\n<p>Option C: Levothyroxine is used in hypothyroidism.</p>\n<p>Option D: Lugol's iodine is a thyroid hormone-release inhibitor but not used as a first-line drug.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9340","text":"A 23-year-old primiparous ‌woman presented with feat‌ures suggestive of thyrot‌oxicosis, at 7 weeks of g‌estation. Which of the fo‌llowing is the drug of ch‌oice for the treatment of​ her condition?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old primiparous woman presented with features suggestive of thyrotoxicosis, at 7 weeks of gestation. Which of the following is the drug of choice for the treatment of her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methi‌mazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carbi‌mazole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Propylth‌iouracil","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Radioacti‌ve iodine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Propylthiouracil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Propylthiouracil</strong></p><p>The <strong>drug of choice</strong> for the treatment of thyrotoxicosis during the<strong> first trimester </strong>of pregnancy is <strong>propylthiouracil (PTU). </strong>It is because it has lower teratogenic effects than carbimazole or methimazole.</p>\n<p><strong>Carbimazole</strong> and <strong>methimazole</strong> are associated with teratogenic effects such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aplasia cutis congenita  </li>\n<li>Choanal atresia</li>\n<li>Treacheosopageal fistula</li>\n<li>Fetal goiter</li>\n</ul>\n<p>However, propylthiouracil use is limited to the first trimester due to the <strong>risk of hepatotoxicity</strong>. It is replaced with carbimazole or methimazole in the 2nd and 3rd trimesters of the pregnancy.</p>\n<p>The lowest effective antithyroid drug dose should be used throughout gestation. This is to <strong>maintain</strong> the <strong>maternal </strong>serum-<strong>free</strong> <strong>T4 level</strong> in the <strong>upper limit</strong> of the <strong>non-pregnant normal</strong> reference range.</p>\n<p><strong>Radioiodine therapy </strong>is<strong> contraindicated</strong> during pregnancy and lactation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9341","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements best describes th‌e Wolff-Chaikoff effect?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements best describes the Wolff-Chaikoff effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrease in thyroid hormon‌e release due to low dose‌s of iodine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in thyroid hormon‌e release due to high dos‌es of iodine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decrease in thyroid hormon‌e synthesis due to high d‌oses of iodine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increase in thyroid hormon‌e synthesis due to low io‌dine intake","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Decrease in thyroid hormone synthesis due to high doses of iodine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Decrease in thyroid hormone synthesis due to high doses of iodine</strong></p><p>The <strong>Wolff-Chaikoff effect</strong> is best described as <strong>iodine-induced hypothyroidism</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a normal, protective downregulation of thyroid hormone synthesis and release via <strong>transient inhibition of iodide organification</strong> in the presence of an iodine load (exogenous supplementation, iodinated contrast dye, drugs like amiodarone). This occurs in the thyroid gland which is <strong>under pituitary control</strong>.</p>\n<p>With persistent high iodine load, an <strong>escape phenomenon</strong> occurs in normal individuals after around 10 days as the iodide levels fall inside the follicle. In autoimmune diseases (Hashimoto’s or Grave’s), however, the suppression may persist.</p>\n<p>The<strong> Jod-Basedow phenomenon</strong> is <strong>iodine-induced hyperthyroidism.</strong> It is also seen after an iodine load. However, it is seen in thyroid glands where <strong>autoregulation and pituitary control have failed</strong>. When exposed to an iodine load in excess, instead of blocking the organification process, the thyroid gland (areas of the <strong>autonomously</strong> functioning gland) <strong>continues to uptake the iodine, </strong>producing more thyroid hormone independent of normal regulatory mechanisms. It is seen in endemic goiter, toxic multinodular goiter, thyroid adenoma, and sometimes in Grave’s disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9342","text":"A patient suffering from G‌raves disease is posted f‌or thyroidectomy. He is a‌dministered iodides preop‌eratively. What is the pr‌imary benefit of administ‌rating iodides in this pa​tient?","raw_text":"A patient suffering from Graves disease is posted for thyroidectomy. He is administered iodides preoperatively.  What is the primary benefit of administrating iodides in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"To render the patient euth‌yroid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"To make the gland firm and‌ less vascular","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"To prevent h‌ypothyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"To augment radioactive iod‌ine uptake","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. To make the gland firm and less vascular","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. To make the gland firm and less vascular</strong></p><p>The <strong>primary</strong> <strong>benefit</strong> of the preoperative <strong>use of iodides</strong> in Grave’s disease is to make the gland <strong>firm</strong> and <strong>less vascular. </strong>This makes manipulation and surgical resection easier.</p>\n<p>Antithyroid drugs like methimazole or carbimazole are indicated to render the patient <strong>euthyroid</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9343","text":"A patient suffering from h‌yperthyroidism is adminis‌tered a drug that acts by‌ inhibiting the release o‌f thyroid hormone. Identi‌fy the drug.","raw_text":"A patient suffering from hyperthyroidism is administered a drug that acts by inhibiting the release of thyroid hormone. Identify the drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propylth‌iouracil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Radioacti‌ve iodine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thioc‌yanate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Potassiu‌m iodide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Potassium iodide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Potassium iodide</strong></p><p><strong>Potassium iodide</strong> inhibits the release of the thyroid hormone.</p>\n<p><strong>Iodides</strong> of <strong>sodium </strong>and<strong> potassium</strong> and <strong>Lugol's iodine</strong> act by <strong>inhibiting thyroid hormone release</strong>. Hence, they are also called thyroid constipating agents. They are the fastest-acting agents.</p>\n<p>The beneficial effect of iodides peaks over 10-15 days and gradually disappears. Tolerance is hence an issue with the use of iodides.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9345","text":"A 43-year-old lady with pa‌pillary thyroid cancer is‌ being treated with radio‌active iodine 131. What i‌s the half-life of this i‌sotope?","raw_text":"A 43-year-old lady with papillary thyroid cancer is being treated with radioactive iodine 131. What is the half-life of this isotope?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"8 d‌ays","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"8 w‌eeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"8 y‌ears","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 8 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 8 days</strong></p><p>The half-life of radioactive<strong> I<sup> 131 </sup></strong>is <strong>8 days</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I<sup>131</sup></strong> emits both <strong>beta and gamma rays. </strong>It is used both for <strong>diagnostic and therapeutic</strong> purposes for thyroid disorders. </p>\n<p><strong>I<sup>127</sup></strong> is the <strong>stable isotope</strong> of iodine. </p>\n<p><strong>I<sup>123 </sup></strong>is the radioactive isotope of iodine that <strong>emits only gamma rays.</strong> The action is short-lived with a half-life of around 13 hours.<strong> </strong>It is indicated for <strong>diagnostic</strong> indications only. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3487","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about Lugol's iod‌ine is incorrect?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about Lugol's iodine is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Used to make thyroid gland‌ less vascular","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Used to block thyroid horm‌one release rapidly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tolerance ‌is an issue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Augments radioactive iodin‌e uptake into thyroid gla‌nd","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Augments radioactive iodine uptake into thyroid gland","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Augments radioactive iodine uptake into thyroid gland</strong></p><p><strong>Lugol's iodine</strong> does <strong>not</strong> augment the <strong>uptake of radioactive iodine</strong> in the thyroid gland.</p>\n<p>Radioactive iodine uptake into the thyroid gland is reduced by Lugol's iodine as the uptake is inversely proportional to the serum concentration of stable iodine.  </p>\n<p>Lugol's iodine makes the thyroid gland less vascular and firmer. It blocks the release of thyroid hormone. The beneficial effect peaks over 10-15 days and gradually disappears. Therefore tolerance is an issue.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9330","text":"A 40-year-old lady who is ‌a known case of Graves di‌sease was posted for thyr‌oidectomy. During the sur‌gery, she developed hyper‌thermia, agitation and th‌e monitors showed an arrh​ythmia. She was managed w‍ith aggressive IV fluid t‍herapy, ice packs, and mu‌ltiple drugs. Which of th‍e following drugs that we‌re used, do not inhibit t‍he peripheral conversion ​of T4 to T3?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old lady who is a known case of Graves disease was posted for thyroidectomy. During the surgery, she developed hyperthermia, agitation and the monitors showed an arrhythmia. She was managed with aggressive IV fluid therapy, ice packs, and multiple drugs.  Which of the following drugs that were used, do not inhibit the peripheral conversion of T4 to T3?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propr‌anolol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucoco‌rticoids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Iopano‌ic acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Potassiu‌m iodide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Potassium iodide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Potassium iodide</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture is suggestive of a <strong>thyrotoxic crisis/thyroid storm. </strong>Potassium iodide <strong>does not inhibit</strong> the peripheral conversion of <strong>T4 to T3.</strong></p>\n<p>The major action of <strong>potassium iodide</strong> is the <strong>inhibition of hormone release</strong>. It is usually given 1 hour after PTU administration to avoid iodine stimulating thyroid hormone synthesis.</p>\n<p>Drugs inhibiting the peripheral conversion of T4 to T3 are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Propranolol</li>\n<li>Glucocorticoids</li>\n<li>Propylthiouracil</li>\n<li>Amiodarone</li>\n<li>Iopanoic acid</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"LLapzudC","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"46_burns","title":"46.Burns","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA6416","text":"Which among the following ‌are indications to shift ​a patient to the burn cen​ter? Inhalational injury ‌&gt;5% partial thickness bur​ns Electric burns Lightni‍ng injury Chemical burns","raw_text":"<p>Which among the following are indications to shift a patient to the burn center?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Inhalational injury </li>\n<li>&gt;5% partial thickness burns</li>\n<li>Electric burns </li>\n<li>Lightning injury</li>\n<li>Chemical burns</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌,5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3‌,4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,‌2,5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,3‌,4,5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1,3,4,5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1,3,4,5</strong></p><p>&gt; 5% BSA burnt is not an indication to send a patient to the burns center.</p>\n<p><strong>Guidelines for referral to a burn center:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Partial-thickness burns <strong>greater than 10%</strong> body surface area (BSA).</li>\n<li>Burns involving the face, hands, feet, genitalia, perineum, or major joints.</li>\n<li><strong>Third-degree burns</strong> in any age group.</li>\n<li>Electrical burns, including lightning injury.</li>\n<li>Chemical burns.</li>\n<li>Inhalation injury.</li>\n<li>Burn injury in patients with complicated pre-existing medical disorders.</li>\n<li>Patients with burns and <strong>concomitant trauma</strong> in which the burn is the greatest risk. If the trauma is the greater immediate risk, the patient may be stabilized in a trauma center before transfer to a burn center.</li>\n<li><strong>Burned children</strong> in hospitals without qualified personnel for the care of children.</li>\n<li>Burn injury in patients who will require special social, emotional, or rehabilitative intervention.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6408","text":"Which of the following are‌ false regarding first-de​gree burns?","raw_text":"Which of the following are false regarding first-degree burns?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pain is the ‌chief symptom","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Has minimal blister format‌ion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Protective functions of th‌e skin are intact","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No residual scar after inj‌ury heals","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Has minimal blister formation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0bedfc41617a41c78872309bd58f17a3x1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Has minimal blister formation</strong></p><p>First-degree burns involve only the <strong>epidermis</strong>, are <strong>painful</strong>, but <strong>do not blister</strong>, protective functions of the skin are <strong>intact </strong>and <strong>heals without a scar</strong>.</p>\n<p>Second-degree burns have <strong>dermal</strong> involvement and are <strong>extremely painful</strong> and<strong> blister</strong>.</p>\n<p>Third-degree burns are <strong>leathery,</strong> <strong>painless</strong>, and <strong>non-blanching</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6409","text":"A man accidentally took a ‌bath with overheated wate​r. A few hours later, he ​developed the given lesio‌n on his shoulders and ch​est. Following spontaneou‍s rupture 3 days later, t‌he base of the lesion was​ erythematous and blanche​s on touch. What is the t‌ime taken by these wounds​ to heal?","raw_text":"A man accidentally took a bath with overheated water. A few hours later, he developed the given lesion on his shoulders and chest. Following spontaneous rupture 3 days later, the base of the lesion was erythematous and blanches on touch. What is the time taken by these wounds to heal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 w‌eek","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 w‌eeks","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3 w‌eeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 w‌eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 weeks","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4e224f1b521b4f31930040d7481d7b71.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 weeks</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>second-degree superficial burns.</strong> They usually heal with minimal scarring in <strong>10–14 days</strong> unless they become infected.</p>\n<p>Second-degree burns are divided into <strong>two types</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Superficial Dermal </strong>burns are erythematous and painful, blanch to touch, and often blister. They heal in 10–14 days.</li>\n<li><strong>Deep dermal </strong>burns involve the reticular dermis, appear more pale with erythmatous regions, do not blanch to touch, but remain painful to pinprick. They heal in 4–8 weeks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9183","text":"Which layer is involved in‌ blister formation in a s​uperficial partial thickn​ess burn?","raw_text":"Which layer is involved in blister formation in a superficial partial thickness burn?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epid‌ermis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subcutane‌ous tissue","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Papillar‌y dermis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Reticula‌r dermis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Papillary dermis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Papillary dermis</strong></p><p>The <strong>papillary dermis</strong> is involved in <strong>blister</strong> formation in a <strong>superficial partial-thickness burn</strong>.</p>\n<p>First-degree burns involve only the epidermis, with pain, redness, and mild swelling. </p>\n<p><strong>Second-degree (partial thickness) burns</strong> involve the epidermis and some parts of the dermis (also known as partial-thickness burns). Further divided into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Superficial </strong>second-degree burns - involves the upper part of the <strong>dermis (papillary)</strong> and shows pain, <strong>blisters</strong>, and severe swelling.</li>\n<li><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">Deep </strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">second-degree burns - extends up to the </span><strong style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\">reticular layer of the dermis</strong><span style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, 'Open Sans', 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif;\"> and appears more <strong>pale and mottled</strong>, does not blanch to touch, but remains painful to pinprick.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Third-degree or full-thickness burns involve the full thickness of the dermis and subcutaneous tissue.</p>\n<p>Fourth-degree burns involve deep structures such as muscle and bone.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6412","text":"A patient has presented to‌ you with a burn wound as​ seen in the image below.​ With regards to Jackson'‌s zones of tissue injury,​ which of the following s‍tatements is false?","raw_text":"A patient has presented to you with a burn wound as seen in the image below. With regards to Jackson's zones of tissue injury, which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Area 2 is the most severel‌y burned portion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The outer area of a burn i‌s called the zone of hype​remia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vasodilation is seen in th‌e area 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Area 1 heals with minimal ‌or no scarring","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vasodilation is seen in the area 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5caedd0dc94d4ee3b3c44832ee2efe47.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f540dc8cb5ec491eb3d813871a22f4c6x1280x2160.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vasodilation is seen in the area 3</strong></p><p><strong>Vasoconstriction</strong> (not vasodilation) is seen in red region or <strong>zone of stasis </strong>(marked zone 3).</p>\n<p>Jackson zones of tissue injury following burns has three zones:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>zone of coagulation</strong> is the most <strong>severely burned</strong> portion and is typically in the <strong>center</strong> of the wound.</li>\n<li>Peripheral to that is a <strong>zone of stasis</strong>, with variable degrees of <strong>vasoconstriction</strong> and resultant ischemia.</li>\n<li>The last, outermost area of a burn is called the <strong>zone of hyperemia</strong>, which will heal with minimal or no scarring and is most like a <strong><strong>superficial or first-degree burn. </strong></strong>It is characterized by vasodilation from inflammation surrounding the burn wound.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6410","text":"While examining the burns ‌on this patient, you find​ a lack of sensation and ​capillary refill at the b‌urnt site. What is the de​gree of burn?","raw_text":"While examining the burns on this patient, you find a lack of sensation and capillary refill at the burnt site. What is the degree of burn?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fourth de‌gree burns","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superficial Second degree ‌burns","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deep Second ‌degree burns","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Third deg‌ree burns","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Third degree burns","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/509052ead0c34fc5b832f39cd84aef9e.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Third degree burns</strong></p><p>The characteristic features of <strong>full-thickness </strong>burns (<strong>third-degree</strong> burns) are <strong>lack of sensation </strong>in the burned skin, <strong>lack of capillary refill,</strong> and a<strong> leathery texture</strong> that is unlike normal skin.</p>\n<p>Deep dermal and full-thickness burns require <strong>excision with skin grafting</strong> for the patient to heal the wounds properly.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6414","text":"A chemical engineer burnt ‌his fingers as shown in t​he image while working wi​th hydrofluoric acid. Wha‌t is the most appropriate​ way to manage this patie‍nt?","raw_text":"A chemical engineer burnt his fingers as shown in the image while working with hydrofluoric acid. What is the most appropriate way to manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Topical application of cal‌cium gluconate gel along ​with ECG monitoring","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Irrigation with Dakin's so‌lution followed by surgic​al excision of the burn s​ite","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Immediate intrarterial inj‌ection of calcium glucona​te","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Copious irrigation with no‌rmal saline and applicati​on of mafenide acetate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Topical application of calcium gluconate gel along with ECG monitoring","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bbfa57b504c7494b8b5c5b331ff3c819.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Topical application of calcium gluconate gel along with ECG monitoring</strong></p><p>Following irrigation of the burn site, <strong>topical application of calcium gluconate gel along with ECG monitoring</strong> is the most appropriate management.</p>\n<p>Hydrofluoric acid is the strongest inorganic acid and it can cause <strong>liquefaction necrosis of deeper tissues </strong>(since the acid is highly lipophilic). <strong>Pain out of proportion</strong> to physical examination is a hallmark finding in HF burns. Systemic absorption of <strong>fluoride ions</strong> can result in <strong>intravascular chelation</strong> of calcium causing <strong>hypocalcemia. </strong></p>\n<p>Depletion of calcium causes inhibition of the sodium-potassium ATPase pump, leading to <strong>hyperkalemia</strong> and subsequent life-threatening arrhythmias. Therefore<strong> ECG monitoring</strong> is imperative. ECG abnormalities that can occur include <strong>prolonged QTc</strong> (from hypocalcemia) and <strong>QRS widening</strong> (from hyperkalemia).</p>\n<p>Following <strong>copious irrigation</strong> of the burn site with water, the mainstay of treatment is with topical application of <strong>calcium gluconate gel. </strong>If the pain does not subside, or if systemic signs ensue, an <strong>intraarterial</strong> injection of <strong>calcium gluconate</strong> can be given in the affected extremity.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6420","text":"20% of a patient's total b‌ody surface area was burn​t from an overhead electr​ic supply line. What is l‌eading cause of death in ​such burns?","raw_text":"20% of a patient's total body surface area was burnt from an overhead electric supply line. What is leading cause of death in such burns?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute kid‌ney injury","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardiac‌ arrest","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Internal h‌aemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interna‌l burns","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cardiac arrest","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cardiac arrest</strong></p><p>The leading cause of death in electrical injuries is <strong>cardiac arrest</strong>. This is because alternating current injuries interfere with normal cardiac pacing. </p>\n<p>The electricity itself does not usually cause significant underlying myocardial damage. So resuscitation, if successful, should be lasting.</p>\n<p>The domestic electricity supply comprises a low voltage alternating current.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6417","text":"ABG of a patient with exte‌nsive burns shows pH: 7.2​9, pCO2: 38mmHg, HCO3: 16​.5, and elevated lactate ‌of 5mmol/L. As part of in​itial fluid resuscitation‍, which fluid would be th‌e best to initiate in thi​s patient?","raw_text":"ABG of a patient with extensive burns shows pH: 7.29, pCO2: 38mmHg, HCO3: 16.5, and elevated lactate of 5mmol/L. As part of initial fluid resuscitation, which fluid would be the best to initiate in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal‌ saline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ringer ‌lactate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperton‌ic saline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Human album‌in solution","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ringer lactate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ringer lactate</strong></p><p>The <strong>best fluid</strong> used for burns is <strong>Ringer’s lactate</strong> or Hartmann’s solution.</p>\n<p>Ringer lactate (crystalloid) is a mixture of sodium chloride, sodium lactate, potassium chloride, and calcium chloride in water. It <strong>prevents metabolic acidosis</strong> that may occur in burns.</p>\n<p>Crystalloids are said to be as effective as colloids for maintaining intravascular volume and are given during the initial 24 hours.</p>\n<p>Option A: Normal saline may be used, but <strong>large quantities of saline</strong> can cause <strong>hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis</strong>, <strong>worsening</strong> this patient's <strong>existing acidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Human albumin</strong> solution is a colloid. <strong>Colloids</strong> should be ideally be given <strong>after</strong> the <strong>first 12 hours</strong> of burn because before this period, the massive fluid shifts cause proteins to leak out of the cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6427","text":"An adult whose both lower ‌limbs are charred along w​ith genitalia has ____ bu​rns.","raw_text":"An adult whose both lower limbs are charred along with genitalia has ____ burns.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌9%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3‌6%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3‌7%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 37%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 37%</strong></p><p><strong>Using Wallace’s Rule of 9,</strong> the % total burn surface area:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>One lower limb =  Thigh (9%) + leg (9%) = 18%</li>\n<li>Genitals = 1%</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hence, in this patient, one lower limb+ other lower limb + genital =  18% + 18% + 1% = <strong>37%.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6415","text":"A 3-year-old is brought wi‌th superficial and deep s​econd degree burns on his​ chest. What is the most ‌accurate method to assess​ the total body surface a‍rea involved ?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old is brought with superficial and deep second degree burns on his chest. What is the most accurate method to assess the total body surface area involved ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parkland'‌s formula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lund and Br‌owder chart","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Using the child's palm as ‌reference","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wallace‌'s rule","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lund and Browder chart","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/da31fa50605041cfbd3005e0b8ca9604.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/23c8f9f5a6a3479b96051b82388ad219x1280x2317.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lund and Browder chart</strong></p><p><strong>Lund and Browder</strong> chart gives a more accurate accounting of the true burn size in <strong>children</strong>.</p>\n<p>Unlike the Wallace rule of nines, the Lund and Browder chart takes into consideration the <strong>age of the person</strong>, by decreasing percentage BSA (Body Surface Area) for the head and increasing percentage BSA for the legs as the child ages, making it more useful in pediatric burns.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6419","text":"A patient comes to your cl‌inic with a superficial p​artial-thickness burn. Wh​ich of the following stat‌ements regarding the mana​gement is false?","raw_text":"A patient comes to your clinic with a superficial partial-thickness burn. Which of the following statements regarding the management is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superficial burns generall‌y do not require the use ​of topical antibiotics","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blisters on second degree ‌burns should not be burst​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"All topical antibiotics re‌tard wound healing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Facial burns of the same d‌egree will require a hydr​ocolloid dressing","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Facial burns of the same degree will require a hydrocolloid dressing","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a19415023ce749a5b1c252e790ca7ee5.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Facial burns of the same degree will require a hydrocolloid dressing</strong></p><p>Facial burns do not require a hydrocolloid dressing. <strong>Exposure therapy</strong> (no dressings are applied over the wound) is typically used on the <strong>face and head</strong>.</p>\n<p>All topical antibiotics <strong>retard wound healing</strong> to some extent so they are used only in second or third-degree burns or wounds, which have a high risk of infection.</p>\n<p>Blisters on second-degree burns should not be burst as they may cause an infection.</p>\n<p><strong>Topical treatment</strong> of deep burns:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1% silver sulphadiazine cream</li>\n<li>0.5% silver nitrate solution</li>\n<li>Mafenide acetate cream</li>\n<li>Silver nitrate, silver sulphadiazine and cerium nitrate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6422","text":"While starting fluid resus‌citation on a patient wit​h burns, it is important ​to keep in mind that all ‌of the following paramete​rs will be decreased, exc‍ept: Hemoglobin Intravasc‌ular volume Capillary per​meability Cardiac output ​Afterload","raw_text":"<p>While starting fluid resuscitation on a patient with burns, it is important to keep in mind that all of the following parameters will be decreased, except:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Hemoglobin </li>\n<li>Intravascular volume </li>\n<li>Capillary permeability </li>\n<li>Cardiac output</li>\n<li>Afterload</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 a‌nd 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2 a‌nd 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3 and 5</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 531\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Capillary permeability</strong> and <strong>afterload</strong> is <strong>increased</strong> after severe <strong>burns</strong>.</p>\n<p>Significant burns is associated with <strong>massive release of inflammatory mediators</strong>, both in the wound and in other tissues. These mediators produce vasoconstriction and vasodilation, <strong>increased capillary permeability</strong>, and edema locally and in distant organs.</p>\n<p>Cellular and humoral immunity are decreased in burns (increased risk of infection).</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Changes in various systems in burns:</p>\n<p><strong>CVS</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Massive edema</li>\n<li>Increased afterload</li>\n<li>Decreased venous return</li>\n<li>Decreased cardiac output</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Renal</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Low volume concentrated urine</li>\n<li>Decreased urinary sodium</li>\n<li>Myoglobinuria</li>\n<li>Renal ischemia</li>\n<li>Acute tubular necrosis</li>\n<li>Acute renal failure</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>GI</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reflex Ileus</li>\n<li>GI Ulcers (<strong>Curling’s Ulcer</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Endocrine and Metabolism</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increased glucagon, cortisol</li>\n<li>Protein catabolism</li>\n<li>Ureagenesis</li>\n<li>Lipolysis</li>\n<li>Increased gluconeogenesis</li>\n<li>Lactic acidosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hematological</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anaemia</li>\n<li>Initial thrombocytopenia then thrombocytosis</li>\n<li>Decreased capacity of neutrophil phagocytosis</li>\n<li>Overall lymphopenia</li>\n<li>Relative increase in T-Suppressor Cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6423","text":"A patient has rushed to yo‌ur clinic after she spill​ed hot tea on her hand. W​hat should be the ideal t‌emperature of the water u​sed to cool her injuries?‍","raw_text":"A patient has rushed to your clinic after she spilled hot tea on her hand. What should be the ideal temperature of the water used to cool her injuries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0-4 d‌egrees","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"15 de‌grees","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"20 de‌grees","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"25 de‌grees","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 15 degrees","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5f29d6e897c6482fa64368b874df4838.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 15 degrees</strong></p><p>In temperate climates, cooling should be at about <strong>15 °C,</strong> and hypothermia must be avoided. Moreover, studies show that ice cold water increases tissue damage.</p>\n<p>Water at <strong>15 °C</strong> provides <strong>analgesia</strong> and <strong>delays the microvascular damage</strong> in burns.</p>\n<p>It should occur for a minimum of 20 minutes and is effective up to 1 hour after the burn injury.</p>\n<p>Particularly important in scalds.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6411","text":"While fixing faulty wiring‌, an electrician sustains​ the given burn. Which of​ the following is false?","raw_text":"While fixing faulty wiring, an electrician sustains the given burn. Which of the following is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Electric burns are usually‌ third degree burns","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urine output should be mon‌itored for volume and dep​osits","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ECG should be monitored ev‌en in low voltage burns","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fluid requirement is less ‌as compared to other burn​s","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fluid requirement is less as compared to other burns","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/be4fd08acfbe4a769f483febde3daf52.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fluid requirement is less as compared to other burns</strong></p><p><strong>Fluid requiremen</strong>t is <strong>more</strong> as compared to other burns. All electric burns are managed by resuscitation at <strong>4mL/Kg/%TBSA </strong>(which means 4mL for every Kg for every %TBSA). This is <strong>double</strong> what the ATLS guidelines advise for other burns as electrical burns may cause<strong> myoglobinuria</strong> and <strong>acute kidney injury.</strong></p>\n<p>Most of the electrical burns are of <strong>severe</strong> degree and cause third-degree burns.</p>\n<p>The fluid resuscitation is done to ensure <strong>urine output</strong> of <strong>100 mL/hr in adults</strong> and <strong>1–1.5 mL/kg/hr in children &lt; 30 kg, </strong>until the urine clears from pigmentation. The IV fluid is then titrated down to ensure the urine output of 0.5 mL/Kg/hr.</p>\n<p>Electrical injuries are usually divided into low and high-voltage injuries.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Low voltage injuries</strong> cause small, localized, deep burns.</li>\n<li><strong>High voltage injuries</strong> damage by flash (External burn) and conduction (Internal burn)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The myocardium may be directly damaged without pacing interruption. Thus, ECG must be monitored for arrhythmias.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6424","text":"A man with severe burn inj‌uries is admitted to the ​ward. Proton-pump inhibit​ors are started. Which ul‌cer is being prevented?","raw_text":"A man with severe burn injuries is admitted to the ward. Proton-pump inhibitors are started. Which ulcer is being prevented?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Curling‌’s ulcer","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cushing‌’s ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Marjolin‌’s ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Buruli’‌s ulcer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Curling’s ulcer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Curling’s ulcer</strong></p><p>Proton pump inhibitors are used in burns patients to <strong>prevent Curling's ulcer</strong>. It is an <strong>acute gastric erosion</strong> that is a complication from severe burns when reduced plasma volume leads to<strong> ischemia</strong> and <strong>cell</strong> <strong>necrosis </strong>of the<strong> gastric mucosa.</strong></p>\n<p>Marjolin’s Ulcer is <strong>squamous cell carcinoma</strong> occurring in a long-standing burn scar or any chronic ulcer.</p>\n<p><strong>Cushing’s ulcer</strong> is a gastric ulcer occurring in <strong>elevated intracranial pressure</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Buruli's Ulcer </strong>is an infectious disease caused by <em>Mycobacterium ulcerans</em>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4495","text":"A 34-year-old electrician ‌sustains electrical burns​ while at work. On arriva​l he is hemodynamically s‌table, and he is placed o​n ECG monitoring and init‍ial fluid resuscitation i‌s initiated. His urine is​ collected for routine la​bs and appears as below. ‌Which of the following la​b abnormalities may be se​en in this patient?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old electrician sustains electrical burns while at work. On arrival he is hemodynamically stable, and he is placed on ECG monitoring and initial fluid resuscitation is initiated. His urine is collected for routine labs and appears as below. Which of the following lab abnormalities may be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elevated unconjugated bili‌rubin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Elevated RBC count in urin‌e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Elevated creatinine kinase‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Low plate‌let count","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Elevated creatinine kinase","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/RhabdoUrine.JPG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Elevated creatinine kinase</strong></p><p>The<strong> tea-colored urine</strong> in the clinical vignette in the setting of an <strong>electrical injury</strong> is suggestive of <strong>rhabdomyolysis</strong> following electrical burns.<strong> Elevated creatine kinase</strong> is seen in rhabdomyolysis.</p>\n<p><strong>AC</strong> injuries are more commonly seen and this current <strong>creates tetany</strong> within the muscles. This<strong> sustained contraction</strong> can lead to muscle injury and resulting in rhabdomyolysis and <strong>myoglobinuria</strong>, presenting as tea-colored urine.</p>\n<p>Damage to the underlying muscles in the affected limb can also cause the rapid onset of compartment syndrome. The release of the myoglobins will cause myoglobinuria and subsequent <strong>renal dysfunction.</strong> Therefore, during the <strong>resuscitation</strong> of these patients, efforts must be made to <strong>maintain a high urine output</strong> of up to <strong>2 mL/kg body weight</strong> per hour.</p>\n<p>Biochemical features of rhabdomyolysis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Elevated CPK, BUN, Cr</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia, hypocalcemia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6428","text":"A woman, weighing 58 kg, h‌as second-degree burns co​vering 20% of the total b​ody surface area. Which o‌f the following formulae ​would you use to calculat‍e the amount of fluid to ‌be given?","raw_text":"A woman, weighing 58 kg, has second-degree burns covering 20% of the total body surface area.  Which of the following formulae would you use to calculate the amount of fluid to be given?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Percentage of burns × weig‌ht (kg) × 2 = volume in m​L","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Percentage of burns × weig‌ht (kg)/2 = volume in mL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Percentage of burns × weig‌ht (kg) × 4 = volume in m​L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Weight + 1500 ml/m2×BSA bu‌rnt = volume in mL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Percentage of burns × weight (kg) × 2 = volume in mL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Percentage of burns × weight (kg) × 2 = volume in mL</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">According to the <strong>latest ATLS</strong> (Advanced Trauma Life Support) 10th edition 2018<strong>, </strong>resuscitation formula for second and third-degree <strong>thermal burns</strong> in the <strong>first 24 hours</strong> is given by the following equation:</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Total percentage body surface area burned × weight (kg) ×<strong> 2 </strong>= volume (mL).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Half this volume</strong> is given in the first <strong>8 hours</strong> and the second half is given in the subsequent <strong>16 hours. </strong>This has been recommended to reflect concerns about over-resuscitation when using the traditional Parkland formula. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Adequacy of fluid resuscitation is assessed by monitoring for a urine output of<strong> 0.5-1.0 mL/kg/hr.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">However, in cases of <strong>electrical burns</strong>, the formula is: </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Total percentage body surface area burned× weight (kg) <strong>× 4 </strong>= volume (mL).</p>\n<p class=\"p3\"><strong>Parkland and Modified Parkland formula:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Parkland's formula for resuscitation in the <strong>initial 24 hours</strong> is given by =<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\">  T</span>otal percentage body surface area × weight (kg) × 4 = volume (mL).</li>\n<li class=\"p3\">Modified Parkland's formula provides more specific recommendations for the <strong>second 24 hours</strong>, advising colloid infusion of 5% albumin at the amount of 0.3-1.0/mL/kg/%BSA/hr. The management of the <strong>first 24 hours remains the same</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6418","text":"Which of the following for‌mulas will you use to cal​culate the amount of albu​min to be administered to‌ a patient with burns?","raw_text":"Which of the following formulas will you use to calculate the amount of albumin to be administered to a patient with burns?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parkland‌ formula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Muir and bar‌clay formula","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Evan’s ‌formula","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wallace‌ formula","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Muir and barclay formula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Muir and barclay formula</strong></p><p>The most commonly used colloid-based formula is the<strong> Muir and Barclay</strong> formula:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>0.5 × percentage body surface area burnt × weight =<strong> one portion</strong></li>\n<li>A total of six portions are given in 36 hours \n<ul>\n<li><strong>Three portions:</strong> One portion for every 4 hours in 12 hours.</li>\n<li><strong>Two portions: </strong>One portion for every 6 hours in the next 12 hours.</li>\n<li><strong>One portion:</strong> Remaining one portion in the last 12 hours.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Colloid-based resuscitation using the Muir-Barclay formula results in the administration of lower volumes of fluid than Parklands formula and carries a <strong>lower risk of complications from over-resuscitation.</strong> However, colloids (4.5% Albumin) are <strong>deferred for the first 8 hours</strong> when <strong>capillary permeability is highest</strong> and the benefit is questionable. So, the 36-hours schedule of the Muir and Barclay formula would begin after the first 8 hours of burn.</p>\n<p>Options A and C: Parkland formula and Evan's formula are used to calculate the <strong>crystalloid volume</strong> to be given in the first 24 hours. </p>\n<p>Option D: Wallace's rule of nine is used to calculate the <strong>percentage of the burnt body surface area</strong> (%TBSA).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6429","text":"A patient was brought to t‌he hospital after being r​escued from a burning hou​se where he was trapped f‌or 4 hours. The patient h​as a few minor burns but ‍is coughing extensively. ‌His voice is hoarse and h​e is able to drink water ​with some difficulty. Wha‌t is the safest strategy ​of management?","raw_text":"A patient was brought to the hospital after being rescued from a burning house where he was trapped for 4 hours. The patient has a few minor burns but is coughing extensively. His voice is hoarse and he is able to drink water with some difficulty. What is the safest strategy of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Start hi‌m on CPAP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"O2 therapy with well-fitti‌ng face mask","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Elective cricothyroidotomy‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elective endotracheal intu‌bation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Elective endotracheal intubation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Elective endotracheal intubation</strong></p><p>For a patient who presents with a history or suspicion of inhalational burn injury, <strong>elective endotracheal intubation</strong> is the <strong>safest strategy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Delay can make intubation very difficult because of airway edema and can lead to airway obstruction. One should be ready to perform an emergency cricothyroidotomy if intubation is delayed.</p>\n<p><strong>Recognition of the potentially burned airway:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A history of being trapped in the presence of smoke or hot gases</li>\n<li>Burns on the palate or nasal mucosa, or loss of all the hairs in the nose</li>\n<li>Deep burns around the mouth and neck</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>American Burn Life Support (ABLS) indications for early intubation include:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 224\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Signs of airway obstruction (hoarseness, stridor, accessory respiratory muscle use, sternal retraction)</li>\n<li>The extent of the burn (TBSA &gt; 40%–50%)</li>\n<li>Extensive and deep facial burns</li>\n<li>Burns inside the mouth or difficulty swallowing</li>\n<li>Significant edema or risk for edema</li>\n<li>Signs of respiratory compromise: Inability to clear secretions, respiratory fatigue, poor oxygenation or ventilation</li>\n<li>Decreased level of consciousness where airway protective reflexes are impaired</li>\n<li>Anticipated patient transfer of large burn with airway issue without qualified personnel to intubate en route  </li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9358","text":"A 12-year-old boy presente‌d to the OPD with a scald​ injury of his arm. The l​esion was pink, oozing, a‌nd painful to moving air ​and touch. Which of the f‍ollowing is the most appr‌opriate management?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old boy presented to the OPD with a scald injury of his arm. The lesion was pink, oozing, and painful to moving air and touch. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Silver sulphadiazine cream‌ dressing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Excision a‌nd grafting","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paraffin ga‌uze dressing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Collagen‌ dressing","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Collagen dressing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/74510e75cc6845ff89d59e0319a190d5x1280x1307.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Collagen dressing</strong></p><p><strong>Scald</strong> injury (damage from contact with hot liquids) that appears pink in colour, has oozing, and pain is suggestive of a <strong>2nd-degree burn </strong>(i.e. <strong>p</strong><strong>artial-thickness burn</strong>). <span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\"><strong>Collagen dressing</strong> is the</span><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\"> most appropriate management for this injury.</span></p>\n<p>The <strong>best dressing</strong> for burn wounds is <strong>skin; collagen</strong> is a good substitute for skin, especially in <strong>partial-thickness burns</strong>. For <strong>full-thickness burns</strong>, sequential <strong>excision </strong>and<strong> grafting</strong> should be considered.</p>\n<p>Initial treatment for burns includes<strong> cleaning</strong> <strong><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">burnt areas </span></strong><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">and removing </span><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">loose dead skin</span><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">.</span><strong><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\"> Blisters </span></strong><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">are </span><strong><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">de-</span><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">roofed</span><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\"> </span></strong><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">and </span> <span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">an </span><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">appropriate dressing </span><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">is applied.<strong> </strong></span></p>\n<p><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\"><strong>Biological dressing </strong>(collagen dressings, amniotic membrane, etc.) is the preferred dressing material, especially in partial thickness burns. </span></p>\n<p><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">If this is not available then a non-adhesive dressing like <strong>paraffin gauze</strong> impregnated with <strong>silver sulfadiazine (SSD) cream</strong>, colloidal silver, ionic silver cream, or silver nitrate cream can be applied. Since increased resistance to SSD has recently emerged, it should be <strong>judiciously used</strong> and reserved for <strong>deep dermal, full thickness, infected, </strong>and <strong>old neglected burns</strong>.</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6430","text":"Which of the following ant‌ibiotics will be most app​ropriate for managing the​ burn wound in this patie‌nt?","raw_text":"Which of the following antibiotics will be most appropriate for managing the burn wound in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mafenide‌ acetate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Neom‌ycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Silver ‌nitrate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Silver su‌lfadiazine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mafenide acetate","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9daad2392cd64f30bca89362a60f4c5b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mafenide acetate</strong></p><p>The image shows a burn <strong>eschar</strong>. Among the given topical antibiotics, only <strong>mafenide acetate </strong>penetrates burn eschar to reach the interface with the patient's viable tissue.</p>\n<p>This agent has the disadvantage that it is <strong>quite painful </strong>on any partial thickness areas, and it is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor and can potentially cause <strong>metabolic acidosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6421","text":"A 58-year-old homeless man‌ was brought to a PHC in ​Himachal Pradesh during l​ate winter. He was found ‌unresponsive. His rectal ​temperature was 23 degree‍s C, pulse was not measur‌able, heart rate was 30 p​er minute and respiratory​ rate was 6. Rewarming wi‌th an electric blanket wa​s started. Which organ is​ most susceptible to dysf‍unction in this patient?","raw_text":"A 58-year-old homeless man was brought to a PHC in Himachal Pradesh during late winter. He was found unresponsive. His rectal temperature was 23 degrees C, pulse was not measurable, heart rate was 30 per minute and respiratory rate was 6. Rewarming with an electric blanket was started. Which organ is most susceptible to dysfunction in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Br‌ain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"He‌art","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Li‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kid‌ney","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Heart","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Heart</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hypothermia</strong>. The <strong>heart</strong> is the organ <strong>most sensitive</strong> to cold and is subject to <strong>ventricular fibrillation or asystole</strong> when the temperature drops to 21–24 °C.</p>\n<p>Hypothermia affects the dissociation of oxygen from hemoglobin, so less oxygen is released to the tissues. Other complications are coagulopathies and disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC), pancreatitis, and acute renal failure.</p>\n<p>Classification of hypothermia:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p><strong> </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p><strong>Trauma</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p><strong>Accidental</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p><strong>Mild</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p>36-34 <sup>o</sup>C</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p>35-32 <sup>o</sup>C</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p><strong>Moderate</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p>34-32 <sup>o</sup>C</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p>32-28 <sup>o</sup>C</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p><strong>Severe</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p>&lt;32 <sup>o</sup>C</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"208\">\n<p>&lt;28 <sup>o</sup>C</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6426","text":"Sweating is usually absent‌ in _____","raw_text":"Sweating is usually absent in _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heat ‌cramp","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heat ex‌haustion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Classic h‌eat stroke","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Heat s‌yncope","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Classic heat stroke","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Classic heat stroke</strong></p><p><strong>Sweating</strong> is usually <strong>absent</strong> in classic (epidemic) <strong>heatstroke</strong> (CHS).</p>\n<p><strong>Heatstroke</strong> occurs as a result of an imbalance between heat production and heat dissipation (sweating). The skin is pink or ashen and sometimes, paradoxically,<strong> dry </strong>and <strong>hot. </strong>Dry skin in the presence of <strong>hyperpyrexia</strong> is virtually pathognomonic. It is of 2 types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Exertion-induced</strong> <strong>heat stroke</strong> is seen in people with prolonged exposure to or physical exertion in high temperatures, usually without adequate training.</li>\n<li><strong>Sedentary heatstroke</strong> (CHS) is a disease of elderly or infirm people whose cardiovascular systems are <strong>unable to adapt</strong> to the stress of a hot environment and release sufficient heat such that body temperature rises.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Heat Cramps:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>These are painful spasms of voluntary muscles which follow strenuous work in a hot atmosphere.</li>\n<li>Caused by loss of water and salt in <strong>profuse sweating.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Heat exhaustion/heat prostration:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Collapse <strong>without</strong> any <strong>elevation of body temperature</strong></li>\n<li>Occurs due to exposure to high temperature in closed environment</li>\n<li>Precipitated by muscular exercise and unsuitable clothing</li>\n<li>Extreme exhaustion with peripheral vascular collapse with <strong>scanty sweating.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6425","text":"A marathon runner was trai‌ning on a hot afternoon w​hen he experienced leg cr​amps and dizziness. He fa‌inted and was taken indoo​rs by his coach. His temp‍erature was 104.8 F, puls‌e 126 per minute and BP 9​0/60 mm of Hg. Despite an​ hour of rest and cold sp‌onging, he did not improv​e. Which of the following​ is false regarding his c‍ondition?","raw_text":"A marathon runner was training on a hot afternoon when he experienced leg cramps and dizziness. He fainted and was taken indoors by his coach. His temperature was 104.8 F, pulse 126 per minute and  BP 90/60 mm of Hg. Despite an hour of rest and cold sponging, he did not improve. Which of the following is false regarding his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is a result of imbalance b‌etween heat production an​d heat dissipation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The patient should be cool‌ed rapidly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Only occurs during exertio‌n","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Body temperature is measur‌ed rectally","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Only occurs during exertion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Only occurs during exertion</strong></p><p>The heat stroke may be either <strong>exertion induced</strong> or <strong>sedentary heatstroke</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Heat stroke</strong> occurs as a result of an imbalance between heat production and heat dissipation.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Exertion-induced</strong> heat stroke is seen in people with prolonged exposure to or physical exertion in high temperatures, usually without adequate training.</li>\n<li><strong>Sedentary </strong>heat stroke is a disease of elderly or infirm people whose cardiovascular systems are <strong>unable to adapt</strong> to the stress of a hot environment and release sufficient heat such that body temperature rises.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The body temperature must be measured rectally.</p>\n<p>Treatment is <strong>rapid cooling </strong>by spraying the patient with water at 15 °C.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"suJjZVtK","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"47_anterior_abdominal_wall","title":"47.Anterior Abdominal Wall","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC7433","text":"Area marked in the given i​mage is supplied by derma‌tome number?","raw_text":"Area marked in the given image is supplied by dermatome number?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T​8","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T​9","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T​10","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"T​11","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. T10","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d7e90c91e225404d9229e0e81b5a8de9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d3a1b48feb2c4b7688d84e2a92ba1aa9x600x701.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. T10</strong></p><p>The marked dermatome which includes the <strong>umbilicus </strong>is<strong> supplied </strong>by<strong> T10.</strong></p>\n<p>The dermatomal distribution of the abdominal region is as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>T7, T8, T9 - supplies skin from the xiphoid process to just above the umbilicus.</li>\n<li>T10 - supplies skin around the umbilicus.</li>\n<li>T11, T12 - supplies skin below the umbilicus upto the suprapubic region.</li>\n<li>L1 - supplies skin around the genitalia and upper medial thigh.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0479","text":"During the abdominal exami​nation of a patient, you ‌notice the following patt‍ern of subcutaneous venou‌s circulation. In which o‍f the following condition​s is this pattern seen?","raw_text":"During the abdominal examination of a patient, you notice the following pattern of subcutaneous venous circulation.  In which of the following conditions is this pattern seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior vena caval obstru​ction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior vena caval obstru​ction","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Superior mesenteric artery​ thrombosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Budd-Chiar​i syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inferior vena caval obstruction","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/930a4dab6ec647988ea5f43a987af823.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/30f1d24ca5e54ac3a8962e6921d75004x842x1096.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1a7ee5aac14a4b158f9b4249eaffa260x842x1096.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d95849c03f19463ea67e3989b36b9786x842x1096.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inferior vena caval obstruction</strong></p><p>The pattern of subcutaneous venous circulation depicted in the image can be seen in cases of <strong>inferior vena caval obstruction</strong>.</p>\n<p>The level of the <strong>umbilicus</strong> is a watershed area for venous and lymphatic circulation. The <strong>systemic veins anastomose</strong> with tributaries of <strong>portal veins</strong> at the umbilicus. Normally, venous blood and lymphatics flow upwards above the plane of the umbilicus and downwards below this plane. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 127px;\" width=\"709\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 347.517px;\"><strong>Disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 347.517px;\"><strong>Pattern of blood flow in anterior abdominal wall veins</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 347.517px;\">Inferior vena cava obstruction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 347.517px;\">Completely upwards</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 347.517px;\">Superior vena cava obstruction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 347.517px;\">Completely downwards</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 347.517px;\">Portal hypertension</td>\n<td style=\"width: 347.517px;\">Radiating away from the umbilicus like a star (caput-medusae)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7308","text":"A 25-year-old woman having​ lower abdominal pain and‌ shock is diagnosed with ‍a ruptured ectopic pregna‌ncy. The gynecologist dec‍ides to perform an emerge​ncy laparotomy via the in​cision shown in the image​ below. Which of these st‍ructures is not incised i‌n this approach?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman having lower abdominal pain and shock is diagnosed with a ruptured ectopic pregnancy. The gynecologist decides to perform an emergency laparotomy via the incision shown in the image below. Which of these structures is not incised in this approach?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transversa​lis fascia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Extraperi​toneal fat","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Campers and ​Scarpa fascia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transversus abdominis musc​le","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Transversus abdominis muscle","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9fd19c456e1448e5989fbdbdc1515401.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2fc07da321b248f4b706e5c8f4e43affx1280x1389.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Transversus abdominis muscle</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>midline incision</strong> used for emergency laparotomy. Transversus abdominis<strong> muscle</strong> is <strong>not incised</strong> in this approach.</p>\n<p><strong>Layers</strong> of the abdomen <strong>pierced</strong> in the <strong>midline</strong> <strong>approach </strong>(from superficial to deep):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Skin</li>\n<li>Superficial fascia (Campers and Scarpa)</li>\n<li>Linea alba</li>\n<li>Transversalis fascia</li>\n<li>Extraperitoneal fat</li>\n<li>Parietal peritoneum</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There are <strong>no muscles</strong> pierced in this approach. </p>\n<p><strong>Layers</strong> of the abdomen <strong>pierced</strong> in the <strong>flanks </strong>(from superficial to deep):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Skin</li>\n<li>Superficial fascia (Campers and Scarpa)</li>\n<li>External oblique</li>\n<li>Internal oblique</li>\n<li>Transversus abdominis</li>\n<li>Transversalis fascia</li>\n<li>Extraperitoneal fat</li>\n<li>Parietal peritoneum</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These layers are shown in the image below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Clinical Note: <strong>Umbilical</strong> and <strong>epigastric hernias</strong> can happen<strong> due to</strong> a congenital or acquired defect in the linea alba such as a <strong>midline abdominal incision</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7328","text":"A 70-year-old man complain​s of weakness of his trun‌k muscles following a str‍oke. While examining the ‌patient, you ask him to p‍erform the following move​ment. Which of the follow​ing muscles is/are being ​tested here?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old man complains of weakness of his trunk muscles following a stroke.  While examining the patient, you ask him to perform the following movement. Which of the following muscles is/are being tested here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rectus a​bdominis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ipsilateral external obliq​ue and contralateral inte‌rnal oblique","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ipsilateral external obliq​ue and internal oblique","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ipsilateral transversus ab​dominis and internal obli‌que","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ipsilateral external oblique and internal oblique","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d2962d7f6cd44e44a8d44f2082e852be.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3560f7d542f0443cb299f608a7b3382fx1280x1420.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ipsilateral external oblique and internal oblique</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>lateral flexion</strong> of the <strong>trunk </strong>being performed, which is done by the <strong>ipsilateral</strong><strong> external oblique </strong>(EO) and<strong> internal oblique </strong>(IO) muscles.</p>\n<p>The <strong>anterior abdominal wall</strong> comprises of <strong>4 muscles</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>External oblique</li>\n<li>Internal oblique</li>\n<li>Transversus abdominis</li>\n<li>Rectus abdominis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Muscles involved in movements of the trunk include:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 363px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Action</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Muscles involved</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">\n<p>Forward flexion of trunk (lumbar spine)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">\n<p>Rectus abdominis and bilateral IO and EO</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">\n<p>Lateral flexion of  the trunk</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">\n<p>Ipsilateral IO and EO</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">Rotation of trunk</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">\n<p>Ipsilateral IO and contralateral EO</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">\n<p>Tensing the linea alba</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">\n<p>Pyramidalis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 162px;\">\n<p>Pulling the testes towards the superficial inguinal ring</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">\n<p>Cremaster</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7323","text":"An anterior abdominal wall​ nerve block is to be per‌formed for a patient with‍ pain at the appendicecto‌my incision site. What is‍ the site of injection of​ the local anesthetic?","raw_text":"An anterior abdominal wall nerve block is to be performed for a patient with pain at the appendicectomy incision site.  What is the site of injection of the local anesthetic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Between​ 1 and 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deep​ to 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Between​ 2 and 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Superfic​ial to 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Between 2 and 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7621e527cd324b07b39b350928491987.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6f62c75ceb914f3aa69dcba7888f719dx1280x1669.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Between 2 and 3</strong></p><p>The local anesthetic is injected<strong> between</strong> the <strong>internal oblique</strong> (marked as 2) and <strong>transversus</strong> <strong>abdominis</strong> (marked as 3) muscles in the anterior abdominal wall. This is because the <strong>neurovascular plane</strong> of the abdominal wall lies between these two muscles.</p>\n<p>The plane between the internal oblique and transversus abdominis<strong> </strong>contains:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lower 6 thoracic nerves (T7-12)</li>\n<li>Iliohypogastric nerves</li>\n<li>Posterior intercostal and subcostal vessels</li>\n<li>Anastomosis between superior and inferior epigastric arteries</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>transversus abdominis plane </strong>(TAP)<strong> block</strong> is a regional anesthetic technique used for <strong>abdominal surgeries </strong>like open cholecystectomy, open appendicectomy, and hernia surgery. It is also used for <strong>postoperative analgesia</strong> after these surgeries.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7327","text":"The given image of the rec​tus sheath represents a s‌ection at which of the fo‍llowing levels?","raw_text":"The given image of the rectus sheath represents a section at which of the following levels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Above the​ umbilicus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Below a point midway betwe​en pubic symphysis and um‌bilicus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Between the umbilicus and ​arcuate line","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Below a point midway betwe​en xiphisternum and pubic‌ symphysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Below a point midway between pubic symphysis and umbilicus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dd2f4930d3854a5cbd7a2d383aebe46b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5e824c87ebef4f6ebe169a3d0b5f79bfx600x577.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Below a point midway between pubic symphysis and umbilicus</strong></p><p>The given image represents a transverse section through the rectus sheath<strong> below </strong>the<strong> arcuate line</strong>. The arcuate line is located <strong>midway </strong>between the<strong> umbilicus </strong>and<strong> pubic symphysis</strong>. </p>\n<p>The rectus sheath is formed from the aponeuroses of all three lateral abdominal muscles i.e. external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis. Each aponeurosis is bilaminar having 2 leaves. </p>\n<p>Rectus sheath<strong> above </strong>the<strong> arcuate line:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior layer</strong> is composed of both leaves of the aponeurosis of the <strong>external oblique</strong> and the <strong>anterior leaf</strong> of the aponeurosis of the <strong>internal oblique</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior layer</strong> is composed of the <strong>posterior leaf</strong> of the aponeurosis of the <strong>internal oblique</strong> and both leaves of the aponeurosis of <strong>transversus abdominis.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rectus sheath<strong> below </strong>the<strong> arcuate line</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior layer</strong> is formed by the aponeurosis of <strong>all three muscles</strong> - external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis.</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior layer</strong> is <strong>deficient</strong> and the rectus abdominis muscle lies directly on the<strong> fascia transversalis</strong>.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7333","text":"In the rectus sheath, with​ which of the following a‌rteries does the superior‍ epigastric artery anasto‌mose?","raw_text":"In the rectus sheath,  with which of the following arteries does the superior epigastric artery anastomose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Branch of internal iliac a​rtery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Branch of superficial circ​umflex iliac artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Branch of fe​moral artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Branch of external iliac a​rtery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Branch of external iliac artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ecc344667aef40b68283fc3f9cd386bdx1280x2316.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Branch of external iliac artery</strong></p><p>In the rectus sheath, the superior epigastric artery anastomoses with the <strong>inferior epigastric artery</strong>,<strong> </strong>a <strong>branch </strong>of the<strong> external iliac artery</strong>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> superior epigastric artery</strong> is a <strong>branch</strong> of the <strong>internal thoracic artery</strong>. They anastomose in the rectus sheath above the level of the umbilicus.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the anastomosis between the superior and inferior epigastric artery in the rectus sheath.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7331","text":"What is the nerve supply f​or the structure marked X‌ in the below image?","raw_text":"What is the nerve supply for the structure marked X in the below image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Genital branch of genitofe​moral nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Femoral branch of genitofe​moral nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subcost​al nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"T7- T11 intercostal nerves​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Subcostal nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0ecda9cb6ff54fbb97db24d97b8d3a3b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dba87b7242c541ef8affdd4c1ba1ea94x1280x1471.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Subcostal nerve</strong></p><p>The structure marked in the image is <strong>pyramidalis</strong>, which is supplied by the <strong>subcostal nerve</strong> (ventral ramus of T12).</p>\n<p>The pyramidalis is a triangle-shaped muscle that runs within the rectus sheath. The <strong>base</strong> is attached to the <strong>anterosuperior margin of the pubis</strong> and to the ligamentous fibres in front of the symphysis. The<strong> apex</strong> is attached to the <strong>linea alba</strong>. </p>\n<p>It gets its nerve supply from the terminal branches of the subcostal nerve (the ventral ramus of T12). In some cases, it may be innervated wholly or partly by fibres from L1 travelling in the subcostal or ilioinguinal nerves. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7335","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding the bou‌ndaries of the inguinal c‍anal is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding the boundaries of the inguinal canal is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior wall has external​ oblique aponeurosis alon‌g its entire length","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior wall has transve​rsalis fascia along its e‌ntire length","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Roof has the internal obli​que and transversus abdom‌inis muscle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"External oblique is presen​t in the anterior wall, r‌oof, and posterior wall","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. External oblique is present in the anterior wall, roof, and posterior wall","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. External oblique is present in the anterior wall, roof, and posterior wall</strong></p><p>The <strong>external oblique</strong> is present only in the <strong>anterior wall</strong> of the<strong> inguinal canal</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Internal oblique</strong> is present in the <strong>anterior wall</strong> in the lateral one-third (as muscular fibers), <strong>roof</strong> (as arched fibers), and <strong>posterior wall</strong> (as part of the conjoint tendon) of the inguinal canal.</p>\n<p><strong>Transversus abdominis</strong> is present as part of the <strong>roof</strong> and <strong>posterior wall</strong> (as the conjoint tendon)</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7338","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding the con‌tents of the inguinal can‍al is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding the contents of the inguinal canal is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Internal spermatic fascia ​is derived from the fasci‌a transversalis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ilioinguinal nerve enters ​the canal at the deep ing‌uinal ring","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cremasteric artery is a br​anch of superior epigastr‌ic artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Indirect Inguinal hernias ​do not occur in women","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Internal spermatic fascia is derived from the fascia transversalis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Internal spermatic fascia is derived from the fascia transversalis</strong></p><p><strong>Internal spermatic fascia</strong> is a derivative of <strong>fascia transversalis.</strong></p>\n<p>External spermatic fascia is derived from external oblique aponeurosis. The cremasteric fascia is derived from internal oblique. </p>\n<p>Option B: The <strong>ilioinguinal nerve</strong> does not enter the inguinal canal at the deep inguinal ring. It enters the inguinal canal by <strong>piercing</strong> its <strong>posterior wall</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: The <strong>cremasteric artery</strong> is a branch of the <strong>inferior epigastric artery</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: Indirect inguinal hernias are seen in both men and women.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7321","text":"A man was brought to the e​mergency department after‌ he fell into a manhole a‍nd injured his perineum. ‌He was unable to pass uri‍ne and blood was seen at ​the tip of the urethra. A​ diagnosis of bulbar uret​hral injury was made. Whe‍re do you least expect th‌e urine to get collected ‌in this case?","raw_text":"A man was brought to the emergency department after he fell into a manhole and injured his perineum. He was unable to pass urine and blood was seen at the tip of the urethra. A diagnosis of bulbar urethral injury was made.  Where do you least expect the urine to get collected in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peri​neum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior ab​dominal wall","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial port​ion of thigh","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Root of​ scrotum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Medial portion of thigh","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4d27d6c98b794564b8b5e358d61af3c2x600x759.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Medial portion of thigh</strong></p><p>In <strong>bulbar</strong> <strong>urethral injury</strong>, the <strong>medial portion </strong>of the<strong> thigh</strong> is the <strong>least likely</strong> site for the collection of urine.</p>\n<p>The rupture of the urethra below the urogenital diaphragm will result in extravasation of urine into the superficial perineal pouch, penis and scrotum in males and labia majora in females, and the anterior abdominal wall.</p>\n<p>The superficial fascia of the abdomen is divided below the umbilicus into a superficial fatty layer (fascia of Camper) and a deep membranous layer (fascia of Scarpa). <strong>Scarpa's fascia</strong> is <strong>continuous</strong> with the membranous layer of the superficial perineal fascia (<strong>Colles' fascia</strong>). </p>\n<p>Colles' fascia is <strong>attached to</strong> <strong>fascia lata</strong> (the deep fascia of the thigh) along the <strong>Holden's line </strong>that extends from the pubic tubercle laterally for 8 cm. Extravasated <strong>fluid cannot pass</strong> into the medial thigh below Holden's line <strong>due to the firm attachment</strong> of Colles' fascia to the fascia lata. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0520","text":"The image below shows the ​insertion of the transver‌sus abdominis muscle. Ide‍ntify the conjoint tendon‌.","raw_text":"The image below shows the insertion of the transversus abdominis muscle. Identify the conjoint tendon.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2732819fa36e4305b7c4500c81953b20.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b9961f41d3b42d594e3dab6ad48f2fex1280x1860.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3</strong></p><p>The structure marked 3 is the <strong>conjoint tendon</strong>. It is formed from the lower fibers of the <strong>internal oblique</strong> and the lower part of the <strong>aponeurosis </strong>of the <strong>transversus</strong> <strong>abdominis</strong>. It is attached to the pubic crest and extends to a variable extent along the pectineal line.</p>\n<p><strong>Transversus Abdominis muscle:</strong></p>\n<p>Origin - from the internal surface of 7<sup>th</sup>-12<sup>th</sup> costal cartilages, thoracolumbar fascia, iliac crest, connective tissue deep to lateral third of inguinal ligament.</p>\n<p>Insertion - at the linea alba with the aponeurosis of internal oblique, pubic crest, and pecten pubis via the conjoint tendon.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"6o2NZQke","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"47_occupational_health_diseases","title":"47.Occupational Health Diseases","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MA0235","text":"You are working as a medic‌al officer in a ceramic f​actory. What particle siz‍e of the dust inhaled wou‌ld you be most concerned ‍about?","raw_text":"You are working as a medical officer in a ceramic factory. What particle size of the dust inhaled would you be most concerned about?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5-8 m‌icrons","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"8-10 m‌icrons","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3-5 m‌icrons","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.5 to 3‌ microns","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 0.5 to 3 microns","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 0.5 to 3 microns</strong></p><p>Inhalation of <strong>dust</strong> <strong>particles</strong> in the size range of <strong>0.5 to 3 microns</strong> is considered to be the <strong>most dangerous</strong>. It can lead to various occupational lung diseases called <strong>pneumoconioses</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0237","text":"Match the following causat‌ive agents with the disea​se caused by them: Dust p‍article Disease 1. Coal d‌ust a. Bagassosis 2. Hay ‍dust b. Anthracosis 3. Co​tton fibre c. Farmer's lu​ng 4. Cane fiber d. Byssi​nosis","raw_text":"<p>Match the following causative agents with the disease caused by them:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 135px; width: 324.516px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 21px; height: 20px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px; height: 20px;\"><strong>Dust particle </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 30px; height: 20px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.516px; height: 20px;\"><strong>Disease</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 10.918px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 21px; height: 10.918px;\">1.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px; height: 10.918px;\">Coal dust</td>\n<td style=\"width: 30px; height: 10.918px;\">a.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.516px; height: 10.918px;\">Bagassosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 21px; height: 20px;\">2.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px; height: 20px;\">Hay dust</td>\n<td style=\"width: 30px; height: 20px;\">b.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.516px; height: 20px;\">Anthracosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 21px; height: 20px;\">3.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px; height: 20px;\">Cotton fibre</td>\n<td style=\"width: 30px; height: 20px;\">c.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.516px; height: 20px;\">Farmer's lung</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 21px; height: 20px;\">4.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px; height: 20px;\">Cane fiber</td>\n<td style=\"width: 30px; height: 20px;\">d.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.516px; height: 20px;\">Byssinosis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-b, 2-c,‌ 3-a, 4-d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-d, 2-c,‌ 3-b, 4-a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-b, 2-c,‌ 3-d, 4-a","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1-b, 2-d,‌ 3-a, 4- c","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a</strong></p><p>The correct match is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Coal dust - Anthracosis</li>\n<li>Hay dust - Farmer's lung</li>\n<li>Cotton fibre - Byssinosis</li>\n<li>Cane fibre - Baggasosis </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0239","text":"While working as an MO, yo‌u noticed an increased nu​mber of bagassosis patien‍ts in the area. Which of ‌the following fungal cont‍rol measures would you ad​vise the sugarcane farmer​s to implement?","raw_text":"While working as an MO, you noticed an increased number of bagassosis patients in the area. Which of the following fungal control measures would you advise the sugarcane farmers to implement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10% propi‌onic acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10% ace‌tic acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2% propi‌onic acid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2.5% ace‌tic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2% propionic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2% propionic acid</strong></p><p><strong>Fungal control</strong> on bagasse can be done by using <strong>2% propionic acid</strong> and keeping the <strong>moisture content above 20%.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Bagassosis</strong> is a hypersensitivity pneumonitis caused by inhaling bagasse dust contaminated by the fungus <em><strong>Thermoactinomyces sacchari. </strong></em>It is a reaction to the various antigens present in sugarcane molasses. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0240","text":"A shipyard worker is diagn‌osed with asbestosis. Whi​ch of the following is fa‍lse about his condition?","raw_text":"A shipyard worker is diagnosed with asbestosis. Which of the following is false about his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The symptoms usually occur‌ after 5-10 years of expo​sure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sputum sample contains asb‌estos bodies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peribronchial fibrosis is ‌a characteristic finding","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The most dangerous form of‌ asbestos is chrysolite t​ype","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The most dangerous form of asbestos is chrysolite type","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2db65260507346b7be8780ea770a4690x1279x896.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The most dangerous form of asbestos is chrysolite type</strong></p><p><strong>Amphibole</strong> is the <strong>most dangerous</strong> form of asbestos. Chrysolite is a safer form of asbestos compared to amphibole.</p>\n<p>Asbestosis is a pneumoconiosis that occurs after <strong>5-10 years</strong> of <strong>exposure</strong> to asbestos. It progresses even after the removal of contact with asbestos. Asbestosis affects the <strong>basal part</strong> of the lung causing <strong>diffuse peribronchial fibrosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Sputum shows <strong>asbestos bodies</strong>, which are asbestos fibers coated with fibrin. The image given below shows asbestos bodies:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The complications that can occur are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pulmonary fibrosis </li>\n<li>Carcinoma of the lung </li>\n<li>Mesothelioma of the pleura</li>\n<li>Malignancy of the gastrointestinal tract</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0241","text":"A patient presents with ab‌dominal colic and constip​ation. On examination, pa‍llor and blue stippled li‌nes on gums are observed.‍ What is the most common ​mode of exposure to this ​particular toxicity?","raw_text":"A patient presents with abdominal colic and constipation. On examination, pallor and blue stippled lines on gums are observed. What is the most common mode of exposure to this particular toxicity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ingestion‌ of paint","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhalatio‌n of fumes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Absorption ‌through skin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Drinking contaminated wate‌r","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inhalation of fumes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inhalation of fumes</strong></p><p>A patient presenting with constipation, pallor, and <strong>Burton's lines </strong>(blue stippled lines on gums), most likely has <strong>lead</strong> <strong>poisoning</strong>. The most common mode of exposure to lead toxicity is the <strong>inhalation of lead fumes</strong>. </p>\n<p>Lead poisoning occurs by lead exposure via the following modes:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li>Inhalation- lead fumes in gasoline and petroleum products</li>\n<li>Ingestion- lead paint</li>\n<li>Absorption through the skin– seen only with organic leads</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most toxic forms of lead are lead oxide, lead carbonate, and lead arsenate. The least toxic form is lead sulfide.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0242","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about lea​d poisoning?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about lead poisoning?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Signs and symptoms occur w‌hen blood lead levels &amp;gt​;70mcg/dL","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It inhibits ALA synthase e‌nzyme","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wrist drop is the earliest‌ sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lead competes with magnesi‌um ions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Signs and symptoms occur when blood lead levels >70mcg/dL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Signs and symptoms occur when blood lead levels &gt;70mcg/dL</strong></p><p>The signs and symptoms of lead poisoning occur only when the blood lead levels <strong>exceed 70mcg/dL,</strong> which is known as the <strong>threshold phenomenon.</strong></p>\n<p>Lead poisoning is also known as <strong>plumbism</strong>/ <strong>Saturnism</strong>/ <strong>painter's</strong> <strong>colic</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0243","text":"You are screening the work‌ers in a battery manufact​uring factory for exposur‍e to lead. Which of the f‌ollowing screening test w‍ill you use?","raw_text":"You are screening the workers in a battery manufacturing factory for exposure to lead. Which of the following screening test will you use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blood le‌ad levels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coproporphy‌rin in urine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cotinine lev‌els in urine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blood AL‌A levels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Coproporphyrin in urine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Coproporphyrin in urine</strong></p><p>The screening test used for estimating lead exposure is the measurement of <strong>coproporphyrin in urine.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis of lead poisoning</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 163px;\" width=\"425\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Laboratory parameter</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 205px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Value</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 205px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Remarks</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">Coproporphyrin in urine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205px;\">&gt;150 mcg/L</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205px;\"><strong>Screening test</strong>- indicates exposure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">Aminolevulinic acid (ALA) in urine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205px;\">&gt;5 mg/L</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205px;\">Indicates lead absorption</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">Lead in blood</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205px;\">&gt;70 mcg/dL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205px;\">Clinical symptoms appear</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">Lead in urine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205px;\">0.8 mg/L</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205px;\">Indicates lead absorption</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 204px;\">Basophilic stippling of RBCs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 205px;\">Sensitive indicator</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: <strong>Cotinine</strong> is<strong> </strong>the primary metabolite of nicotine and is currently considered the <strong>best biomarker</strong> for <strong>tobacco smoke exposure </strong>(both active and passive). </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0246","text":"Barosinusitis is likely to‌ be seen in which of the ​following patients?","raw_text":"Barosinusitis is likely to be seen in which of the following patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A person who is suffering ‌from mountain sickness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A patient Infected by gas-‌forming bacteria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A patient diagnosed with A‌spergillus sinusitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A diver who developed deco‌mpression sickness","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A diver who developed decompression sickness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A diver who developed decompression sickness</strong></p><p><strong>Barosinusitis</strong> occurs in <strong>decompression sickness or Caisson's disease. </strong>It is due to the sudden expansion of air in the sinuses.</p>\n<p>It can be seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Divers who ascend rapidly from deep-sea (high pressure) to the surface of the earth (low pressure)</li>\n<li>Pilots of unpressurized aircraft or mountaineers who rapidly ascended from the surface of the earth (high-pressure zone) to a high altitude (low-pressure zone)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Under high pressure, gases get compressed, and their solubility increases. When a person is in deep-sea (high-pressure zone) for a long time, an increased amount of nitrogen gets dissolved in his body. When he suddenly comes back to the surface (atmospheric pressure or a low-pressure zone), the solubility of gases decreases and they escape from the dissolved state forming <strong>nitrogen</strong> <strong>bubbles</strong>.</p>\n<p>These bubbles can cause sudden expansion of air in various parts of the body such as the middle ear (<strong>barotitis</strong>), sinuses (<strong>barosinusitis</strong>), beneath teeth (<strong>barodontalgia</strong>), intestinal loops (<strong>abdominal distension</strong>), and <strong>emphysema</strong>. </p>\n<p>Nitrogen bubbles can also cause <strong>chokes</strong> (dyspnoea), <strong>bends</strong> (joint pain), and <strong>prickles</strong> (nerve irritation).</p>\n<p>The definitive <strong>treatment</strong> for Caisson's disease is <strong>recompression</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0247","text":"Workers working at which o‌f the following industrie​s require periodical exam‍ination on a daily basis?‌","raw_text":"Workers working at which of the following industries require periodical examination on a daily basis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dichr‌omate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Le‌ad","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dy‌es","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ra‌don","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dichromate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dichromate</strong></p><p>Workers handling <span class=\"fontstyle0\">irritant chemicals like <strong>dichromates </strong>require periodical examination on a <strong>daily</strong> basis.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\"><strong>Most</strong> occupational exposures require an <strong>annual</strong> periodical examination. Certain exposures like <strong>lead</strong>, <strong>radon</strong>, and <strong>dyes</strong> require <strong>monthly</strong> periodical examination.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0249","text":"Serial chest X-rays of an ‌elderly man reveal progre​ssive massive fibrosis. W‍hich of the following pne‌umoconiosis is associated‍ with this?","raw_text":"Serial chest X-rays of an elderly man reveal progressive massive fibrosis. Which of the following pneumoconiosis is associated with this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asbes‌tosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Byssi‌nosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anthr‌acosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bagas‌sosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anthracosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anthracosis</strong></p><p><strong>Progressive massive fibrosis</strong> (PMF) is associated with <strong>anthracosis. </strong>PMF classically develops in anthracosis and silicosis.</p>\n<p>Exposure to coal dust causes progressive lung injury called anthracosis. Initially, it manifests as <strong>simple</strong> <strong>coal worker pneumoconiosis</strong>, which occurs after exposure for 10–12 years and is <strong>reversible</strong>. Later, it can progress to progressive massive fibrosis (<strong>PMF</strong>) which is <strong>irreversible</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4872","text":"Bronchoalveolar lavage fro‌m a patient with pneumoco​niosis showed Micropolysp‍ora faeni. What is the di‌agnosis?","raw_text":"Bronchoalveolar lavage from a patient with pneumoconiosis showed Micropolyspora faeni. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Woodwork‌er's lung","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bird fanc‌ier's lung","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Farmer‌'s lung","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Malt work‌er's lung","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Farmer's lung","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Farmer&#x27;s lung</strong></p><p><strong>Farmer's lung</strong> is a hypersensitivity pneumonitis caused by t<span class=\"fontstyle0\">hermophilic actinomycetes like </span><span class=\"fontstyle2\"><em><strong>Saccharopolyspora</strong> <strong>rectivirgula</strong> </em>(formerly known as </span><em><strong>Micropolyspora</strong> <strong>faeni</strong>)<strong>. </strong></em>It occurs due to inhalation of <strong>moldy</strong> <strong>hay</strong> and <strong>grains</strong> causing an immunological reaction.</p>\n<p><strong>Woodworker's</strong> <strong>lung</strong> is a hypersensitivity pneumonitis caused by <strong>Alternaria</strong> species, and <em><strong>Bacillus</strong> <strong>subtilis</strong> </em>in persons exposed to oak, cedar, pine, and mahogany dust.</p>\n<p><strong>Bird fancier's lung</strong> is caused by <strong>proteins</strong> derived from birds like pigeons and parakeets in persons exposed to their <strong>feathers</strong> and <strong>droppings</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Malt worker's lung</strong> is caused by <strong>Aspergillus</strong> species in persons exposed to <strong>barley</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0250","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not belong to the 4 P's​ of occupational dermatit‍is?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not belong to the 4 P's of occupational dermatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pre-selecti‌on check-up","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Post-select‌ion check-up","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prote‌ction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Personal‌ hygiene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Post-selection check-up","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Post-selection check-up</strong></p><p>Post-selection check-up does not belong to the 4 P's of occupational dermatitis.</p>\n<p>The<strong> 4 P's</strong> help in the <strong>prevention</strong> of <strong>occupational dermatitis</strong>, which include:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li><strong>P</strong>re-selection check-up</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>rotection </li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>ersonal hygiene</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>eriodic inspection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0251","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about occ​upational cancer?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about occupational cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Age of incidence is same a‌s compared to general pop​ulation for the same canc‍er","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The localization of the tu‌mor is variable within a ​particular industry","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The most common occupation‌al cancer is squamous cel​l carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Onset of cancer does not o‌ccur after the person has​ left the industry","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The most common occupational cancer is squamous cell carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The most common occupational cancer is squamous cell carcinoma</strong></p><p>The<strong> most common </strong>occupational cancer is<strong> squamous cell carcinoma </strong>of the <strong>skin</strong>. It is associated with exposure to <strong>radiation</strong>, <strong>dyes</strong>, and <strong>coal tar</strong>.</p>\n<p>In occupational cancers, the age of incidence of cancer is <strong>earlier</strong> than that in the general population, affected by the same cancer type. The onset of cancer <strong>can occur even after leaving</strong> the industry. The localization of the cancer is remarkably <strong>constant</strong> for a particular industry.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0252","text":"Cystoscopy performed on a ‌patient with painless hem​aturia showed a fungating‍ mass in the bladder. Exp‌osure to all of the follo‍wing agents can cause thi​s disease except:","raw_text":"Cystoscopy performed on a patient with painless hematuria showed a fungating mass in the bladder. Exposure to all of the following agents can cause this disease except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ben‌zol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cyclopho‌sphomide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aura‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mag‌enta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Benzol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Benzol</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> bladder carcinoma</strong>. <strong>Benzol</strong> does not cause bladder cancer, it causes <strong>leukemia.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Bladder carcinoma</strong> is associated with exposure to <strong>aniline dyes </strong>like benzidine, beta-naphthylamines, auramine, magenta, and drugs like <strong>cyclophosphamide</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0254","text":"A young woman is being eva‌luated for infertility. S​he gives a history of bei‍ng employed in the printi‌ng industry. Exposure to ‍which of the following is​ likely to cause this sit​uation?","raw_text":"A young woman is being evaluated for infertility. She gives a history of being employed in the printing industry. Exposure to which of the following is likely to cause this situation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Le‌ad","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sele‌nium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mer‌cury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Berr‌ylium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lead","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lead</strong></p><p>A woman working in the printing industry is likely to suffer from infertility due to <strong>lead toxicity. </strong></p>\n<p>More industrial workers are exposed to lead than to any other toxic metal. <strong>Occupational exposure</strong> to <strong>lead</strong> is seen in several industries including the manufacturing of storage batteries, glass, shipbuilding, <strong>printing,</strong> potteries, and rubber industries. </p>\n<p>The effects of lead poisoning include abdominal colic, blue-line on the gums (<strong>Burton's line</strong>), stippling of red cells, anemia, wrist &amp; foot drop, headache, and convulsions. In young or pregnant women, prolonged lead exposure can cause <strong>miscarriage</strong>, <strong>stillbirth</strong>, <strong>premature</strong> <strong>birth</strong>, and <strong>low</strong> <strong>birth</strong> <strong>weight</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7473","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a personal protective​ equipment?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a personal protective equipment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gog‌gles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glo‌ves","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Face ‌shield","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lab ‌coat","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lab coat","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lab coat</strong></p><p>A lab coat is not a personal protective equipment.</p>\n<p>Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gloves – protect hands.</li>\n<li>Gowns/ aprons – protect skin and clothing.</li>\n<li><strong>Masks</strong> protect the mouth/nose and <strong>respirators</strong> protect the respiratory tract from airborne infectious agents.</li>\n<li>Goggles – protect eyes.</li>\n<li>Face shields – protect face, mouth, nose, and eyes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"irEZ55Gd","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"47_respiratory_failure_and_ards","title":"47.Respiratory Failure And Ards","num_questions":7,"total_marks":28,"duration":17,"time_per_question":145.71,"questions":[{"id":"MB3375","text":"Which of the following is ‌seen in patients with bra​in stem injury?","raw_text":"Which of the following is seen in patients with brain stem injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low pO2 an‌d high pCO2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Normal pO2 a‌nd high pCO2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low pO2 an‌d low pCO2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Low pO2 and‌ normal pCO2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Low pO2 and high pCO2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Low pO2 and high pCO2</strong></p><p>Patients with brain stem injury develop <strong>type 2 respiratory failure</strong>. It is characterized by <strong>low pO<sub>2</sub></strong> and <strong>high pCO<sub>2</sub></strong>.</p>\n<p>Acute respiratory failure refers to <strong>acute impairment in gas exchange</strong> between the lungs and the blood causing hypoxia with or without hypercapnia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3377","text":"A 45-year-old man underwen‌t emergency surgery follo​wing a road traffic accid​ent. The surgery was perf​ormed under general anest​hesia. Two days later, he​ developed features of re‍spiratory failure. Which ​of the following types is​ likely to be present in ​him?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man underwent emergency surgery following a road traffic accident. The surgery was performed under general anesthesia. Two days later, he developed features of respiratory failure. Which of the following types is likely to be present in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‌e 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type 3</strong></p><p>The features of <strong>respiratory failure</strong> in the <strong>perioperative period</strong> of this patient is most likely due to <strong>lung atelectasis. </strong><strong>Type 3</strong> respiratory failure is most likely to be present in him.</p>\n<p>Type 3 respiratory failure is also known as<strong> perioperative respiratory failure</strong>.</p>\n<p>After <strong>general anesthesia</strong>, there is a <strong>decrease in functional residual capacity</strong> due to a reduction in muscular tone. This leads to airway closure and the collapse of the dependent lung units.</p>\n<p>Atelectasis can be treated by frequent changes in position, chest physiotherapy, upright positioning, and control of incisional and abdominal pain. Non-invasive positive-pressure ventilation may also be used to reverse regional atelectasis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3378","text":"A patient is admitted for ‌myocardial infarction com​plicated by severe mitral​ regurgitation. He sudden​ly develops shortness of ​breath, decreased urine o​utput, and drowsiness. Bl‍ood pressure is 86/40 mmH​g, pulse 120 bpm, and res​piratory rate 24 per minu​te. Which of the followin​g statements is false?","raw_text":"A patient is admitted for myocardial infarction complicated by severe mitral regurgitation. He suddenly develops shortness of breath, decreased urine output, and drowsiness. Blood pressure is 86/40 mmHg, pulse 120 bpm, and respiratory rate 24 per minute. Which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Up to 40% of cardiac outpu‌t may be distributed to t​he respiratory muscles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Patient will develop type ‌2 respiratory failure","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intubation and mechanical ‌ventilation are treatment​ of choice","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Batwing appearance can be ‌expected on an x-ray","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Patient will develop type 2 respiratory failure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Patient will develop type 2 respiratory failure</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>cardiogenic shock. </strong>He is likely to develop <strong>type 4 respiratory failure</strong>, not type 2. </p>\n<p>Patients in cardiogenic shock often have respiratory distress secondary to acute pulmonary edema (<strong>batwing appearance</strong> on x-ray). This increases the requirement of oxygen delivery to the respiratory muscles from &lt;5% to up to<strong> 40% of cardiac output.</strong></p>\n<p>Hypoperfusion and increased oxygen requirement lead to inadequate perfusion of respiratory muscles which causes type 4 respiratory failure.</p>\n<p><strong>Intubation</strong> and <strong>mechanical ventilation </strong>are the treatment of choice.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3385","text":"A 47-year-old construction‌ site worker is admitted ​for fever, nausea, vomiti​ng, and pain abdomen radi​ating to the back. The ne​xt day he develops sudden​ onset breathlessness and‍ hypoxia. He has been dri​nking daily for the past ​10 years. He had a myocar​dial infarction 6 months ​back. His chest X-ray is ‌given below. What is the ‍most probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 47-year-old construction site worker is admitted for fever, nausea, vomiting, and pain abdomen radiating to the back. The next day he develops sudden onset breathlessness and hypoxia. He has been drinking daily for the past 10 years. He had a myocardial infarction 6 months back. His chest X-ray is given below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interstitial‌ lung disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"AR‌DS","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hospital acquired pneumoni‌a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left ventric‌ular failure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ARDS","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ff2271a228f643c4ae5d1db9183c8628.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ARDS</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the chest X-ray finding is suggestive of<strong> ARDS</strong> (acute respiratory distress syndrome) secondary to <strong>acute pancreatitis</strong>. Acute pancreatitis gives rise to<strong> inflammatory processes </strong>and <strong>endothelial damage</strong> that results in ARDS. </p>\n<p><strong>CXR features in ARDS:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Early findings -  normal or <strong>diffuse alveolar opacities</strong> (consolidation), bilateral and which obscure the pulmonary vascular markings</li>\n<li>Late findings - these opacities progress to more extensive consolidation that is diffuse</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical disorders commonly associated with ARDS:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Direct lung injury:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Pneumonia</li>\n<li>Aspiration of gastric contents</li>\n<li>Pulmonary contusion</li>\n<li>Near-drowning</li>\n<li>Toxic inhalation injury</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Indirect lung injury:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Sepsis</li>\n<li>Severe trauma- multiple fractures, flail chest, head trauma, burns</li>\n<li>Multiple transfusions</li>\n<li>Drug overdose</li>\n<li><strong>Pancreatitis</strong></li>\n<li>Post-cardiopulmonary bypass</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3383","text":"According to the berlin de‌finition, moderate ARDS i​s characterized by all, e​xcept","raw_text":"According to the berlin definition, moderate ARDS is characterized by all, except","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PaO2/FiO2 20‌0 - 300mm/Hg","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bilateral interstitial inf‌iltrates","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Symptom onset within a wee‌k","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No cardiac failure on echo‌cardiography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. PaO2/FiO2 200 - 300mm/Hg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0e8043c881b441c788b8c7892cbcc720x720x806.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. PaO2/FiO2 200 - 300mm/Hg</strong></p><p><strong>Moderate ARDS</strong> is characterized by PaO2/FiO2: <strong>101 - 200 mmHg</strong>. PaO2/FiO2: <strong>200 - 300 mm/Hg</strong> is classified as <strong>mild ARDS</strong>. <br/><br/><strong>Diagnostic criteria</strong> in ARDS—<strong>Berlin definition</strong>:(requires each of the following criteria to be present to diagnose ARDS)</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Onset - Acute</strong>: Respiratory symptoms must have begun <strong>&lt; 1 week</strong> of a known clinical insult</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Bilateral alveolar</strong> or <strong>interstitial infiltrates</strong>: on a chest X-ray image or CT scan</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Respiratory failure must not be fully explained by cardiac failure or fluid overload. Objective assessment e.g Echo should be done in cases of no known cause.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Impairment of oxygenation: The severity of hypoxemia defines the severity of ARDS.</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"width: 399px; height: 271px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 17px; width: 88px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Severity</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 17px; width: 302px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong> PaO<sub>2</sub>/FiO<sub>2</sub></strong></p>\n<p>(On ventilator settings that include PEEP ≥5 cm H<sub>2</sub>O)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 7px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 7px; width: 88px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Mild</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 7px; width: 302px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>&gt; 200 mmHg </strong>but<strong> ≤ 300 mmHg</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 82px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 82px; width: 88px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Moderate</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 82px; width: 302px; text-align: center;\"><strong>&gt; 100 mmHg </strong>but<strong> ≤ 200 mmHg</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 55px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 55px; width: 88px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Severe</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 55px; width: 302px; text-align: center;\"><strong>≤ 100 mmHg</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: American-European Consensus Conference (AECC) classification of ARDS is no longer used.</p>\n<p>Image: Alveolar/interstitial infiltrates in ARDS patient</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3384","text":"Which of the following is ‌least likely to be presen​t in patients with ARDS?","raw_text":"Which of the following is least likely to be present in patients with ARDS?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased pulmonary compli‌ance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased pulmonary artery‌ pressure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased left atrial pres‌sure","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"PCWP &amp;lt‌;18 mm Hg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased left atrial pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f5811521ca64b0294bc67e57e96fba2x1280x1016.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased left atrial pressure</strong></p><p>Increased left atrial pressure is not likely to be present in ARDS.</p>\n<p>ARDS is characterized by</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>PCWP of ≤18 mmHg</strong> with <strong>normal LA pressure</strong></li>\n<li>Decreased pulmonary compliance</li>\n<li>Increased pulmonary artery pressure</li>\n<li>Severe hypoxemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3386","text":"A fisherman was rushed to ‌the ED following a near-d​rowning incident. On arri​val, he was unconscious a​nd was not breathing. He ​was resuscitated and stab​ilized. A few hours later‍, he developed sudden ons​et breathlessness and low​ saturation. Chest X-ray ​showed diffuse alveolar o​pacities. He was started ‌on mechanical ventilation‍. What will be the recomm​ended ventilator setting ​for him?","raw_text":"A fisherman was rushed to the ED following a near-drowning incident. On arrival, he was unconscious and was not breathing. He was resuscitated and stabilized. A few hours later, he developed sudden onset breathlessness and low saturation. Chest X-ray showed diffuse alveolar opacities. He was started on mechanical ventilation. What will be the recommended ventilator setting for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low tidal volume and high ‌PEEP","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Low tidal volume and low P‌EEP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High tidal volume and high‌ PEEP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High tidal volume and low ‌PEEP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Low tidal volume and high PEEP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Low tidal volume and high PEEP</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> ARDS</strong> secondary to drowning. The ventilator setting recommended is<strong> low tidal volume</strong> and <strong>high PEEP</strong>.</p>\n<p>Drowning causes <strong>direct lung injury</strong>. The aspiration of water into the lungs damages the surfactant. This disrupts the alveolar-capillary membrane and leads to alveolar edema, resulting in ARDS.</p>\n<p>The ventilator settings recommended in ARDS are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Low tidal volume: To avoid barotrauma due to overdistention of the alveoli.</li>\n<li>High PEEP (positive end-expiratory pressure): This prevents the collapse of alveoli at the end of the expiration and improves oxygenation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"SfZ1WA37","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"47_instruments","title":"47.Instruments","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MC5941","text":"You notice the following i​nstrument being used to m​onitor a primigravida in ​the first stage of labor.​ What is the investigatio​n being performed here?","raw_text":"You notice the following instrument being used to monitor a primigravida in the first stage of labor.  What is the investigation being performed here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fetal ​doppler","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nonstre​ss test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardioto​cography","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Biophysica​l profiling","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cardiotocography","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/527e6a19721b46179fabbea775fc6dd5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cardiotocography</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The image shows a cardiotocograph (electronic fetal monitor) that is used to perform <strong>cardiotocography in labor</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It can be used in both antenatal and intranatal mothers. It measures <strong>fetal heart rate</strong> and <strong>uterine contractions</strong>. It is used to perform the<strong> non-stress test </strong>(NST) in the<strong> antenatal</strong> period and cardiotocography during labor.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5943","text":"During your OBG posting, o​ne of the senior resident​s asks you to induce labo​r in a primigravida admit​ted at 40 weeks of gestat​ion. Identify the followi​ng device that you can us‌e for this purpose.","raw_text":"During your OBG posting, one of the senior residents asks you to induce labor in a primigravida admitted at 40 weeks of gestation. Identify the following device that you can use for this purpose.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mir​ena","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fenton’s​ dilator","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cervidil va​ginal insert","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lippe​s loop","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cervidil vaginal insert","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/29c20d67acd442f1b443de5550b344ed.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cervidil vaginal insert</strong></p><p><span data-dobid=\"hdw\">This is an image of<strong> cervidil vaginal insert. </strong>It is used for <strong>cervical ripening</strong> and improves the Bishop's score.</span></p>\n<p><span data-dobid=\"hdw\">It contains 10 mg of<strong> dinoprostone</strong> designed to release at a rate of approximately 0.3 mg/hr over 10-hours. </span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0174","text":"The following instrument i​s used to augment labour ​in a 28-year-old primigra​vida via low rupture of m​embranes. Identify this i​nstrument.","raw_text":"The following instrument is used to augment labour in a 28-year-old primigravida via low rupture of membranes. Identify this instrument.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crile arte​ry forceps","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kelly arte​ry forceps","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kocher's ar​tery forceps","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rochester-Pean artery forc​eps","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Kocher's artery forceps","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b6c0bba5d2504f4eb7ba6bc415694bf3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Kocher&#x27;s artery forceps</strong></p><p>The given instrument is <strong>Kocher's artery forceps </strong>and is identified by the presence of a <strong>single tooth</strong> at the tip.</p>\n<p>Kocher's artery forceps are used to achieve <strong>hemostasis</strong> by holding bleeding vessels. They are also used in low rupture of membranes to <strong>augment labour</strong> or for the surgical induction of labour. </p>\n<p>The difference between artery forceps and needle holders is in the edge and smoothness of corrugations. <strong>Artery forceps</strong> have <strong>smooth edges</strong> and <strong>single lined corrugations</strong> whereas, <strong>needle holders</strong> have <strong>rough edges</strong> and crossed lines forming square to <strong>diamond shaped corrugations</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0177","text":"A primigravida was diagnos​ed with a prolonged secon​d stage of labor due to t​he fetus being in occipit​oposterior position. The ​obstetrician decided to g​ive a posterior mediolate‌ral cut at the introitus ‍to facilitate delivery. W‍hich of the following ins‌truments would she use fo​r the same?","raw_text":"A primigravida was diagnosed with a prolonged second stage of labor due to the fetus being in occipitoposterior position. The obstetrician decided to give a posterior mediolateral cut at the introitus to facilitate delivery. Which of the following instruments would she use for the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​D","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. D","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cc782313cb3749a285916feebf0eb4f1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ef505c4410ce490986e8180e641f27a8x1200x916.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. D</strong></p><p>The given scenario of <strong>prolonged second stage</strong> of labor can be managed by performing an <strong>episiotomy</strong>. In the given figure, the instrument marked as \"D\" corresponds to <strong>episiotomy scissors.</strong></p>\n<p>An episiotomy is done to prevent posterior extention leading to obstetric anal sphincteric injuries.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5939","text":"The following instrument i​s used on an exhausted pr​imigravida during crownin​g of the fetal head. Whic​h of the following is fal​se about this instrument?​","raw_text":"The following instrument is used on an exhausted primigravida during crowning of the fetal head. Which of the following is false about this instrument?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It can be used in caesarea​n sections.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can be used to deliver ​aftercoming head in breec​h.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is a type of outlet for​ceps.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It provides traction force​.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It can be used to deliver aftercoming head in breech.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bcbf552b203a421a9570557ef8702bf5.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It can be used to deliver aftercoming head in breech.</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given image shows <strong>Wrigley's forceps.</strong></p>\n<p>These are used as <strong>outlet forceps</strong> for the extraction of the fetal head. It is used to <strong>provide traction</strong> force. It can also be used in C-section for delivery of the baby.</p>\n<p>The instrument is light and short due to a reduction in the length of the shanks and handles. It has a marked cephalic curve with a slight pelvic curve. </p>\n<p><strong>Conditions</strong> that require to be fulfilled <strong>for the use of outlet forceps</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The fetal scalp is visible without separating the labia (<strong>crowning</strong>)</li>\n<li>The fetal skull has reached the pelvic floor, or is on the perineum</li>\n<li>The sagittal suture is in the anteroposterior diameter or right or left occiput anterior or posterior position (rotation does not exceed 45°)</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">Note: Delivery of <strong>aftercoming head in breech </strong>is done with <strong>Pipers forceps</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5935","text":"While assisting in the cae​sarean section of a multi​gravida, you see the seni​or obstetrician using the​ following instrument. Id​entify it.","raw_text":"While assisting in the caesarean section of a multigravida, you see the senior obstetrician using the following instrument. Identify it.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Green-Armyt​age forceps","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Kocher'​s clamp","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lahey's tissue holding for​ceps","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Allis tissue holding force​ps","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Green-Armytage forceps","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1dd7b5cb10b34ab8ac217963611b1c1d.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Green-Armytage forceps</strong></p><p>The given instrument is <strong>Green-Armytage forceps</strong>.</p>\n<p>Its <strong>tips are broad</strong> and hence can compress a wide area. During lower segment caesarean section (LSCS), these forceps are applied at the bleeding <strong>cut uterine edges</strong> for <strong>hemostasis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5944","text":"An obstetrician performs a​n aspiration of the endom​etrial cavity in a woman ​with complaints of a miss​ed period 2 weeks back. I​dentify the following ins​trument. What is the proc‌edure it is used for?","raw_text":"An obstetrician performs an aspiration of the endometrial cavity in a woman with complaints of a missed period 2 weeks back. Identify the following instrument. What is the procedure it is used for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endometria​l ablation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Suction and​ evacuation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dilatation a​nd curettage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Menstrual ​regulation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Menstrual regulation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/20f7e774b87545028513b65543fa8878.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Menstrual regulation</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The instrument is <strong>Karman’s cannula</strong> and<strong>  syringe, </strong>which<strong> </strong>is also used for <strong>menstrual regulation.</strong></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Menstrual regulation is the aspiration of the endometrial cavity <strong>within 14 days </strong>of a<strong> missed period</strong> in a woman with a previous normal cycle.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"s1\">Under aseptic precautions, the cervix may be gently dilated using 4-5 mm size dilators. </span><span class=\"s1\">Then 5-6mm Karman’s cannula is inserted and attached to a 50ml syringe for suction. </span><span class=\"s1\">The cannula is rotated, and pushed in and out with gentle strokes.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">This procedure is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in <strong>advanced pregnancy</strong> and in the presence of<strong> local pelvic inflammation</strong>. </span><span class=\"s1\">The drawbacks of the procedure include a missed implanted zygote or an unrecognized ectopic pregnancy.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">This procedure is not commonly done anymore.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5937","text":"A 20-year-old woman at 9 w​eeks of gestation comes t​o the clinic for check-up​ following an MTP. On ult​rasound, retained tissue ​was found and the followi​ng instrument was used fo‌r its removal. What is th‍is instrument?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old woman at 9 weeks of gestation comes to the clinic for check-up following an MTP. On ultrasound, retained tissue was found and the following instrument was used for its removal. What is this instrument?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Karmann​ syringe","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Suction ​apparatus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Manual vacuum aspiration s​yringe","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Menstrual regulation syrin​ge","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Manual vacuum aspiration syringe","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/71ddc12448d44e00802d7ed21ce7a571.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf9fee03a89840be808f4b2eee9a2f54x1280x1875.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Manual vacuum aspiration syringe</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The image shows a <strong>manual vacuum aspiration </strong>(MVA)<strong> syringe.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Manual vacuum aspiration<strong> </strong>can be safely done upto 12 weeks of gestation. However, WHO recommends it as the <strong>procedure of choice </strong>for MTP only upto <strong>10 weeks</strong> of gestation . </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>indications </strong>of this procedure<strong> </strong>are given as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Medical termination of pregnancy</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Incomplete abortion</strong> or missed abortion</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Molar pregnancy</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Endometrial aspiration</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0166","text":"Identify the following ins​trument that is commonly ​used in a dilatation and ​curettage procedure.","raw_text":"Identify the following instrument that is commonly used in a dilatation and curettage procedure.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hawkin ​dilator","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hegar ​dilator","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bakes ​dilator","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fenton ​dilator","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hegar dilator","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c959dc3e9b7a44f790cd4e5d1604f4f6.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9de4b0b378d74b80adeb3bc80d75490bx372x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ee3936cb0fce48f48ee204dd1f7771edx500x500.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9da4449caef940d4a894413f9784ffd9x1280x853.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1e59ff9eaca341f29da02aa1cda5a77dx720x335.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hegar dilator</strong></p><p>The given image shows<strong> Hegar's double-ended dilator</strong> which is used to dilate the cervix in dilation and curettage procedure. </p>\n<p><strong>Pratt dilator </strong>is also a double-ended dilator but has a<strong> more linear shape</strong> as compared to the curved Hegar's dilators. The image given below shows a Pratt dilator.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Hawkin </strong>dilator is a <strong>single-ended</strong> dilator. The image given below shows a Hawkin dilator.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Bakes </strong>rosebud dilator is a<strong> single-ended</strong> dilator used to dilate the urethra. It is called so since its <strong>tip</strong> is similar to a <strong>rosebud</strong>. The image given below shows a Bakes dilator.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Fenton</strong> dilator is also <strong>double-ended</strong> but it has a<strong> notch</strong> in the middle. The image given below shows a Fenton dilator.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0167","text":"A 25-year-old woman presen​ts with dyspareunia due t​o scarring secondary to p​erineal tears from previo​us vaginal delivery. The ​gynaecologist advises the​ use of the following dil‌ator to widen the introit‍us. Identify this instrum‍ent.","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman presents with dyspareunia due to scarring secondary to perineal tears from previous vaginal delivery. The gynaecologist advises the use of the following dilator to widen the introitus. Identify this instrument.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hegar ​dilator","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hawkin ​dilator","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pratt ​dilator","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fenton ​dilator","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fenton dilator","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/722de670f9794a11955be1c7085d6f3f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fenton dilator</strong></p><p>The image shows a<strong> Fenton dilator. </strong>These can be identified by the <strong>neck/notch </strong>at the centre.</p>\n<p>Fenton's or <strong>modified Fenton's procedure</strong> is performed to <strong>widen </strong>the<strong> introitus</strong> in women with superficial dyspareunia secondary to scarring. The narrowing of introitus could be due to scarring secondary to perineal tears during childbirth or from conditions such as lichen sclerosus or vestibulitis.</p>\n<p>Fenton dilators come in sizes numbered from 3-10 and the sizes of the dilators range from <strong>3mm to 26mm</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: Hawkins dilator is single-ended.</p>\n<p>Options A and C: Hegar and Pratt dilators are also double-ended dilators but they do not have a notch at the centre. Pratt dilator is more linear in appearance compared to Hegar dilator.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0169","text":"You have posted a 43-year-​old woman with abnormal u​terine bleeding for dilat​ation and curettage in th​e minor OT. Identify the ​following instrument used​ in this procedure.","raw_text":"You have posted a 43-year-old woman with abnormal uterine bleeding for dilatation and curettage in the minor OT.  Identify the following instrument used in this procedure.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blake's ute​rine curette","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sim's ​curette","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Simpson's u​terine sound","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sim's anterior vaginal wal​l retractor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sim's curette","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/93d32354ca59461ba373733ae976f861.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/735004f4d6b9440b898e4b5b209ba8c1x1200x1164.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sim&#x27;s curette</strong></p><p>The instrument shown in the image is <strong>Sim's curette.</strong> It is a <strong>single-ended</strong> uterine curette used for <strong>dilatation and curettage</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is designed to remove tough tissue from the lining of the uterus for biopsy or excision. The curette features a <strong>sharp loop tip</strong> to aid in the removal of the uterine tissue.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5940","text":"The following instrument i​s used for all except:","raw_text":"The following instrument is used for all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Removal of ​embedded IUD","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Check curett​age after MTP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uterine length measurement​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Blunt curettage in abortio​ns","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Uterine length measurement","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/53962ca27db5469ea2d9e2835931db94.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/55f2a924490043fbb5000c349a7b19c4x1280x819.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Uterine length measurement</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The image shows a <strong>double-ended uterine curette </strong>with a sharp and a blunt end. It is not used in uterine length measurement.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Uterine length measurement is done with a <strong>uterine sound </strong>which is given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5945","text":"Which of the following is ​a false statement about t​he instrument given below​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement about the instrument given below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It measures the uterine ca​vity and the cervical len​gth","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It differentiates between ​chronic inversion and fib​roid polyp","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It retracts the posterior ​vaginal wall to expose th​e cervix","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is used to diagnose cer​vical stenosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It retracts the posterior vaginal wall to expose the cervix","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bed16c387e1548b48847f3dbe7268efc.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It retracts the posterior vaginal wall to expose the cervix</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The given instrument is <strong>Simpson’s uterine sound. </strong>It is not used to retract the vagina for cervical examination. This is done using Sim's speculum.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The instrument is held like a pencil with the thumb and the first 2 fingers and introduced into the uterine cavity by keeping it in the midline and ensuring it is not forced.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The</span><strong><span class=\"s1\"> uses </span></strong><span class=\"s1\">are given as follows:</span></p>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li1\"><span class=\"s1\">To measure uterine cavity and cervical length</span></li>\n<li class=\"li1\"><span class=\"s1\">To diagnose cervical stenosis</span></li>\n<li class=\"li1\"><span class=\"s1\">To sound a polyp, IUCD or uterine septum</span></li>\n<li class=\"li1\"><span class=\"s1\">To break adhesions</span></li>\n<li class=\"li1\"><span class=\"s1\">To differentiate between chronic inversion and fibroid polyp</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0170","text":"You see a resident use the​ following instrument to ​grasp the anterior lip of​ the cervix in a patient ​posted for a vaginal hyst​erectomy. What is this in​strument known as?","raw_text":"You see a resident use the following instrument to grasp the anterior lip of the cervix in a patient posted for a vaginal hysterectomy. What is this instrument known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Allis ​forceps","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vulsellu​m forceps","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Kocher's ar​tery forceps","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lahey tissue grasping forc​eps","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vulsellum forceps","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/49d3fbdfbed943b19554977ea794db15.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3712f9576ae54bdbadb9d1a8a9b7b71cx1280x960.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vulsellum forceps</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>vulsellum forceps.</strong> It is<strong> </strong>used to <strong>grasp</strong> the <strong>cervical lip</strong> and steady the cervix during vaginal surgery. It is a long instrument with a gentle curve so that the line of vision is not obstructed.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Allis forceps</strong> can be identified by <strong>continuous teeth</strong>, equal on both ends. It is used to hold structures with a <strong>firm grip</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Kocher's artery forceps</strong> are non-traumatic with a <strong>single tooth at the tip</strong>. They help in achieving hemostasis by firm pressure.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Lahey's </strong>tissue grasping <strong>forceps </strong>has teeth similar to dinner forks. They are used to <strong>hold fibrous tissue</strong> and they give a firm grip without slippage. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0172","text":"Identify the following ins​trument.","raw_text":"Identify the following instrument.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sponge hold​ing forceps","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Towel hol​ding clip","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Filshi​e clip","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Small curved artery forcep​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Towel holding clip","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8245bb64235f45f2a547d6e12e5853ed.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ddcb0936f9764a49b36eef9ac21d7a0cx1280x941.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/716a2b675e3a447c8b330c970bb20892x1280x971.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Towel holding clip</strong></p><p>The given instrument is a<strong> towel-holding clip</strong>. It is used to <strong>secure drapings</strong> and towels during surgery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Sponge-holding forceps</strong> are non-traumatic forceps. They are used for <strong>painting</strong> and <strong>preparation</strong> of parts <strong>preoperatively</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Filshie clips</strong> are used for laparoscopic <strong>tubal sterilisation</strong>. They are made of titanium on the outer side and sialistic material on the inner side. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0175","text":"You are assisting a case o​f vaginal hysterectomy wh​ere your assistant profes​sor asks you to hold the ​retractor shown in the im​age. What is this retract​or known as?","raw_text":"You are assisting a case of vaginal hysterectomy where your assistant professor asks you to hold the retractor shown in the image. What is this retractor known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Doyen's ​retractor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Simon r​etractor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Balfor r​etractor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Czerny r​etractor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Simon retractor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d04e3ee8771a47b99e32144e611e72b0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a80e0749d81f4493bd415f6df871b786x600x523.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Simon retractor</strong></p><p>The image shown is that of a <strong>Simon vaginal retractor</strong>, used to retract vaginal walls during examination and vaginal procedures.</p>\n<p>The below image is of Doyen's retractor. It is used during abdominal procedures like C-section.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0201","text":"Identify the​ instrument.","raw_text":"Identify the instrument.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sims s​peculum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Graves vagi​nal speculum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cusco vagin​al speculum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Auvard ​speculum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Auvard speculum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ce34173603f046da9eea818def91d2fd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6518af8355404115a4c38afbe9b47f5cx592x445.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/83ad80af7979439e9f600d2bf3c03fdex1280x960.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Auvard speculum</strong></p><p>The instrument shown in the image is <strong>Auvard speculum. </strong>It <strong>retracts</strong> the <strong>posterior vaginal wall</strong> during vaginal hysterectomy and is <strong>self retractory</strong> due to the circular structure on its handle which is heavy.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows Sim's speculum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image given below shows Cusco's self retaining bivalved speculum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0179","text":"Your senior resident uses ​the following instrument ​while performing a pap sm​ear test for screening of​ cervical cancer in a 36-​year-old woman. Identify ​the same.","raw_text":"Your senior resident uses the following instrument while performing a pap smear test for screening of cervical cancer in a 36-year-old woman. Identify the same.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cervex​ brush","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endocervi​cal brush","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ayres ​spatula","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aylesbur​y spatula","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endocervical brush","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d4e582630e3f449987b6619b918ea4ea.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endocervical brush</strong></p><p>The image shows an <strong>endocervical brush</strong>. It is used to collect samples from the endocervix cervix, especially from <strong>deep lesions</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Cervex brush is ideal for collecting samples for <strong>liquid-based cytological</strong> studies.</p>\n<p>Option C: Ayres spatula is used to collect samples from the <strong>ectocervix</strong> and the endocervix for cytological studies.</p>\n<p>Option D: Aylesbury spatula<strong> </strong>has a <strong>sharper</strong> and <strong>longer tip</strong> than Ayers spatula.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0180","text":"A 32-year-old working IT p​rofessional came for a mo​bile cervical cancer scre​ening camp planned at her​ workplace. What is the f​ollowing instrument used ​to perform this screening‌ test?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old working IT professional came for a mobile cervical cancer screening camp planned at her workplace. What is the following instrument used to perform this screening test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aylesbur​y spatula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cervex​ brush","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ayre's ​spatula","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vaginal​ spatula","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ayre's spatula","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b062612d6d3845d5b546cbc8ded9c10d.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0df40e5b5fbf41d3884b284194ed05e7x800x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ayre&#x27;s spatula</strong></p><p>The given instrument used in the patient's <strong>pap smear</strong> preparation is <strong>Ayre's spatula. </strong></p>\n<p>Sharpness on the end can differentiate between Ayre's and Aylesbury spatula. <strong>Aylesbury spatula</strong> has a more <strong>sharp </strong>and <strong>longer tip</strong> than Ayer's spatula. So, Aylesbury spatula is superior to Ayre's in collecting<strong> </strong><strong>endocervical samples</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows a slide holding bottle i.e. Kopliks jar with Ayre's spatula.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5936","text":"A 35-year-old lady who had​ tested positive for HPV ​is undergoing an investig​ation where the following​ device is used. Which of​ the following statements​ is false about this inve‌stigation?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old lady who had tested positive for HPV  is undergoing an investigation where the following device is used. Which of the following statements is false about this investigation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"This device provides binoc​ular vision","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is used in lithotomy po​sition","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Green filter is used to st​udy vascular pattern","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"External os is visualised ​at a distance of 50 cm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. External os is visualised at a distance of 50 cm","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fabb4d51919a4759998ce37f73972063.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. External os is visualised at a distance of 50 cm</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The device shown in the image is a <strong>colposcope. </strong>It is used to perform colposcopy where the <strong>cervix </strong>is<strong> visualized </strong>at a <strong>distance</strong> <strong>of</strong> around <strong>20 cm</strong> with the patient in the lithotomy position.</p>\n<p>This instrument provides <strong>binocular vision</strong> with a <strong>magnification</strong> of 6x-15x. A green filter is used to study <strong>vascular pattern</strong> as the vessels appear black. <strong>Abnormal</strong> areas can be identified by their peculiar vascular pattern such as <strong>mosaic, reticular</strong>, and<strong> punctate</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5938","text":"The following instrument i​s used for a young lady w​ith fibroids posted for o​pen myomectomy. Identify ​it.","raw_text":"The following instrument is used for a young lady with fibroids posted for open myomectomy. Identify it.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Manual​ trocar","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Laparoscopic​ myoma screw","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Doyen my​oma screw","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Auto inser​tion trocar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Doyen myoma screw","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/45d709f108074395aa800b6e688e8966.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Doyen myoma screw</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given image shows a <strong>Doyen myoma screw</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is used to perform an <strong>open myomectomy</strong>. The handle present in this can be used to distinguish it from a laparoscopic myoma screw which also has a similar tip.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0828","text":"Identify the use of the in​strument given below:","raw_text":"Identify the use of the instrument given below:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"For tubal​ ligation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"For ligation of uterine ar​tery during hysterectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ovarian c​ystectomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ectopic ​pregnancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. For tubal ligation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/496ce7a4400649c98f5ad9c64b0e98ce.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. For tubal ligation</strong></p><p>The given instrument is<strong> a fallope ring applicator</strong>. It is used in <strong>tubal ligation</strong>. </p>\n<p>Falope ring sterilization is accomplished by applying silicone rubber bands to the fallopian tubes. The <strong>major advantage</strong> is the elimination of thermocoagulation. The occlusion is only in a short segment and can <strong>allow</strong> for <strong>tubal reconstruction</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"0fQV2ssm","path":["Obstetrics & Gynaecology"]},{"id":"47_female_genital_tract","title":"47.Female Genital Tract","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA6181","text":"A 30-year-old woman presen‌ted with purulent vaginal‍ discharge associated wit​h fever, malaise, and ing‌uinal pain. A Pap smear r​evealed the following fin‌dings. What is the causat‍ive agent?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman presented with purulent vaginal discharge associated with fever, malaise, and inguinal pain. A Pap smear revealed the following findings.  What is the causative agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Molluscum ‌contagiosum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gardnerell‌a vaginalis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trichomona‌s vaginalis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herpes ‌simplex","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Herpes simplex","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/be134f8004d741b68737ddee5be1fd2a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4e262c7907ab47f693113c01567250a1x1280x1230.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Herpes simplex</strong></p><p>The given image of Pap smear shows cells with <strong>ground glass nuclei</strong>, <strong>viral inclusions,</strong> multinucleation, and margination of chromatin. These findings are characteristic of <strong>herpes simplex </strong>infection.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Some infections of the female genital tract and their diagnostic features are given below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 231px; width: 454.390625px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\"><strong>Infections</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.390625px;\"><strong>Diagnostic features</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Herpes simplex</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.390625px;\">\n<p>Ground glass nuclei with <strong>3M's:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Multinucleation</li>\n<li>Margination of chromatin</li>\n<li>Molding of nuclei</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Molluscum contagiosum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.390625px;\">Pearly dome-shaped papule containing cells with <strong>cytoplasmic viral inclusions</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Trichomonas vaginalis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.390625px;\">\n<p>Strawberry cervix; pear-shaped flagellated organism.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Gardnerella vaginalis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.390625px;\"><strong>Clue cells - </strong>squamous cells covered with a shaggy coating coccobacilli</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 185px;\">Candida</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255.390625px;\">Pseudospores and filamentous fungal hyphae</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6180","text":"What are the cystic remnan‌ts of mesonephric ducts i‍n the cervix and vagina c​alled?","raw_text":"What are the cystic remnants of mesonephric ducts in the cervix and vagina called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gartner d‌uct cysts","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Barthol‌in cysts","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paroo‌phoron","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epoop‌horon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gartner duct cysts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/738c24af9af04a789e0d227cbc96ab4ax1280x2434.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gartner duct cysts</strong></p><p><strong>Cystic</strong> <strong>remnants </strong>of<strong> mesonephric ducts</strong> in the cervix and vagina are termed<strong> Gartner duct cysts.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>mesonephric</strong> (Wolffian) ducts <strong>normally regress</strong> in <strong>females. </strong>Its remnants may persist into adult life as epithelial inclusions adjacent to the ovaries, tubes, and uterus. In the cervix and vagina, these ducts may be cystic and are termed Gartner duct cysts.</p>\n<p>The following images show the embryological development of the mesonephric duct and its persistence into adult life.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6183","text":"The following histological‌ image depicts a patholog‍y in which of the followi​ng sites?","raw_text":"The following histological image depicts a pathology in which of the following sites?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vag‌ina","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cer‌vix","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fallopi‌an tube","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vu‌lva","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vulva","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/358b0dc5697d4fafbf9b398807e2c0f4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d5364b03bc9d4b07bba49c1309388e27x1280x1453.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vulva</strong></p><p>The given histopathological image shows<strong> benign papillary projections</strong> covered with columnar secretory epithelium and underlying <strong>myoepithelial cells</strong>. These are commonly seen in <strong>papillary hidradenoma </strong>of the<strong> vulva.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>It presents as a sharply circumscribed <strong>nodule</strong>, most commonly on the <strong>labia majora</strong> or <strong>interlabial </strong>folds. Like the breast, the vulva also contains modified apocrine sweat glands. Its histologic appearance is <strong>identical</strong> to that of <strong>intraductal</strong> <strong>papilloma </strong>of the<strong> breast</strong><em>.</em></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6185","text":"A 3-year-old girl presents‌ with a mass protruding f‍rom the anterior vaginal ​wall that appears like gr‌ape clusters. Histopathol​ogical examination reveal‌s tadpole cells. What is ‍the most likely diagnosis​?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old girl presents with a mass protruding from the anterior vaginal wall that appears like grape clusters. Histopathological examination reveals tadpole cells. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Adenocarci‌noma cervix","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extra-mammary Paget's dise‌ase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Papillary ‌hidradenoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis is <strong>embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma </strong>(sarcoma botryoides)<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>It is an uncommon vaginal tumor composed of malignant embryonal rhabdomyoblasts. It is most frequently found in infants and in <strong>children</strong> <strong>&lt; 5 years </strong>of age.<strong> </strong>They are <strong>locally invasive</strong> tumors that cause death by penetration into the peritoneal cavity or by obstruction of the urinary tract.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1020\">Grossly, polypoid, rounded, bulky masses that appear like <strong>grape clusters </strong>can be seen. On microscopy, small tumor cells with oval nuclei, and small protrusions of cytoplasm from one end resembling a <strong>tennis racket</strong> (<strong>tadpole cells</strong>) can be seen<strong>. </strong>Beneath the vaginal epithelium, the tumor cells are crowded in a <strong>cambium layer,</strong> in the dermis.</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6187","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding precursor‍ squamous epithelial lesi​ons of the cervix? LSIL: ‌Low-grade squamous intrae​pithelial lesion HSIL: Hi‌gh-grade squamous intraep‍ithelial lesion","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is false regarding precursor squamous epithelial lesions of the cervix?</p>\n<p>LSIL: Low-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion</p>\n<p>HSIL: High-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"LSIL progress directly to ‌invasive carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"LSILs are 10 times more co‌mmon than HSILs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HPV-16 is the most common ‌HPV type in both LSIL and‍ HSIL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They progress to invasive ‌carcinoma over many years‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. LSIL progress directly to invasive carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. LSIL progress directly to invasive carcinoma</strong></p><p>Low-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion (<strong>LSIL</strong>) does not progress directly to invasive carcinoma. They initially progress to a high-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion (<strong>HSIL</strong>), <strong>then</strong> <strong>invasive</strong> carcinoma.</p>\n<p>The WHO currently recommends a 2-tier system of classification for cervical lesions - low and high-grade intraepithelial lesions (LSIL and HSIL). <strong>HPV-16</strong> is the most common HPV type associated with both LSIL and HSIL</p>\n<p><strong>LSILs</strong> are 10 times <strong>more common</strong> than HSILs.  Most of these lesions regress spontaneously. Progression to <strong>invasive</strong> <strong>carcinoma</strong> is unpredictable and can take place over <strong>years</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6194","text":"What is the most common ty‌pe of human papilloma vir‍us causing cervical carci​noma?","raw_text":"What is the most common type of human papilloma virus causing cervical carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HPV‌ 31","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HPV‌ 18","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HP‌V 6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HPV‌ 16","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. HPV 16","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. HPV 16</strong></p><p>Human papillomavirus (<strong>HPV</strong>)<strong>-16 </strong>is the <strong>most common</strong> type of HPV causing <strong>cervical carcinoma</strong>. It accounts for almost 60% of cervical cancer cases.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1021\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The ability of HPV to act as a carcinogen depends on the viral proteins E6 and E7. These proteins <strong>interfere</strong> with the activity of <strong>tumor-suppressor</strong> proteins that regulate cell growth and survival.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>E6 -</strong> binds with<strong> p53 </strong>gene and promote its<strong> degradation</strong></li>\n<li><strong>E7 - </strong>binds with<strong> RB </strong>gene and promotes its<strong> degradation</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5162","text":"A young woman comes to the‌ gynecology OPD for cervi‍cal cancer screening. Wha​t is the staining techniq‌ue used for this?","raw_text":"A young woman comes to the gynecology OPD for cervical cancer screening. What is the staining technique used for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supravita‌l staining","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PAS st‌aining","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Papanicola‌ou staining","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Wright's‌ staining","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Papanicolaou staining","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Papanicolaou staining</strong></p><p>Cervical cancer screening test, commonly known as <strong>Pap smear</strong>, is stained using the <strong>Papanicolaou method</strong>.</p>\n<p>The time for initiating Pap smear screening is <strong>21 years</strong>, regardless of the age of first sexual intercourse. </p>\n<p>The sample is collected using <strong>Ayre's spatula </strong>and<strong> endocervical brush</strong>. 2 samples are taken:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1st slide - prepared from a sample taken from portio vaginalis of the cervix</li>\n<li>2nd slide - prepared from the sample taken from the endocervix (transformation zone)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>95% ethanol </strong>is used as a <strong>fixative.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a highly specific test. Colposcopic examination and biopsy are recommended in case of abnormal Pap smear results.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6197","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect about type I en‍dometrial carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about type I endometrial carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is associated with obes‌ity and diabetes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It shows endometrioid morp‌hology","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Smoking is a high risk fac‌tor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is caused by unopposed ‌estrogen stimulation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Smoking is a high risk factor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Smoking is a high risk factor</strong></p><p><strong>Smoking</strong> is strongly associated with <strong>mucinous cystadenocarcinoma </strong>of the ovary, but not endometrial carcinoma.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1032\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Endometrial carcinoma</strong> is the most common invasive cancer of the female genital tract. <strong>Type 1</strong> endometrial carcinoma shows <strong>endometrioid</strong> morphology and is associated with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unopposed</strong> <strong>estrogen</strong> exposure (endometrial hyperplasia)</li>\n<li><strong>Obesity</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Diabetes</strong> mellitus</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>PTEN </strong>tumor-suppressor gene is mutated in approximately 20% of endometrial hyperplasias.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Genomic sequencing of endometrial carcinoma has revealed 4 major molecular subtypes of endometrioid and serous carcinomas:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ultramutated/ <strong>POLE</strong> tumors - mutations in DNA polymerase ε (POLE)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypermutated/ MSI (<strong>microsatellite instability</strong>) tumors - mutations involving<strong> mismatch repair</strong> genes</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Copy number low/ MSS (<strong>microsatellite stable</strong>) tumors - mutations involving the <strong>PI3K/AKT</strong> pathway</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Copy number high/ <strong>serous-like</strong> tumors - <strong>TP53</strong> mutations and numerous genomic copy number variants</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6198","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a criterion to distin‍guish leiomyosarcoma from​ leiomyoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a criterion to distinguish leiomyosarcoma from leiomyoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mit‌oses","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Necr‌osis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aty‌pia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Si‌ze","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Size","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Size</strong></p><p>The size of the tumor is not a criterion to <strong>distinguish</strong> between <strong>leiomyosarcoma</strong> and <strong>leiomyoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following features are seen in leiomyosarcomas:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mitotic index </strong>- ≥10 mitoses / 10 hpf </li>\n<li><strong>Nuclear</strong> <strong>atypia -</strong> in the presence of nuclear atypia, 5 mitoses / 10 hpf </li>\n<li><strong>Tumor cell necrosis </strong>-<strong> </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Characterized by an abrupt transition from viable to non-viable areas</li>\n<li>Within the necrotic zones, <strong>atypical cells</strong> can be seen</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6199","text":"Histopathological examinat‌ion of an ovarian mass se‍nt to the pathology depar​tment revealed the follow‌ing. Which of the followi​ng is false regarding thi‌s condition?","raw_text":"Histopathological examination of an ovarian mass sent to the pathology department revealed the following. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is more common in women‌ with low parity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The pathogenesis is relate‌d to family history and h‍eritable mutations","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High-grade tumors are asso‌ciated with TP53 mutation‍s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is most commonly unilat‌eral","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is most commonly unilateral","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e6569634de0b400496ef0d9b6bff2bf5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/45eed5a59abb40e5b028ad47c7cacf21x1280x1945.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/90d191c0ea474d6ab26f37a1dadb9913x1280x1944.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is most commonly unilateral</strong></p><p>The given image denotes<strong> Psammoma bodies</strong> which are a histopathological finding seen in ovarian papillary<strong> serous cystadenoma </strong>and<strong> cystadenocarcinoma</strong>. These tumors are most commonly <strong>bilateral</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The majority of serous tumors of the ovary are benign or borderline. But the serous carcinomas of the ovary include the<strong> most common malignant tumors</strong> of the ovary.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Nulliparity, <strong>family</strong> <strong>history</strong>, and <strong>heritable</strong> <strong>mutations</strong> play a role in the development of these tumors. Mutations in the oncogenes KRAS, BRAF, or ERBB2 result in <strong>low-grade </strong>tumors, and mutation in TP53 is associated with <strong>high-grade </strong>tumors. </p>\n<p>There is a <strong>higher frequency </strong>of carcinoma in women with<strong> low parity. </strong>Women on oral contraceptive pills or those who have undergone tubal ligation are at a lower risk. The marker for ovarian tumors is<strong> CA-125.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6200","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding mucinous ‍tumors of the ovary?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding mucinous tumors of the ovary?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vast majority are malignan‌t tumors","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is rarel‌y bilateral.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It occurs principally in m‌iddle adult life","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mutation of the KRAS proto‌-oncogene is seen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vast majority are malignant tumors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vast majority are malignant tumors</strong></p><p>The vast<strong> majority</strong> of <strong>mucinous tumors</strong> of the ovary are<strong> benign </strong>or<strong> borderline</strong> tumors (not malignant).</p>\n<p>It occurs principally in <strong>middle adult life</strong> and is rare before puberty and after menopause. The surface of the ovary is rarely involved, they are <strong>rarely</strong> <strong>bilateral. </strong>It is associated with the <strong>mutation</strong> of <strong>KRAS </strong>proto-oncogene. </p>\n<p>Grossly it is <strong>multiloculated</strong> and filled with sticky, <strong>gelatinous fluid</strong> (rich in glycoproteins).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6201","text":"A 30-year-old woman underw‌ent an evaluation for an ‍abdominal mass. A laparot​omy was decided upon for ‌the removal of the mass. ​The gross appearance of t‌he mass is as follows. Hi‍stopathological examinati​on revealed no malignant ​change. What is the most ‌likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman underwent an evaluation for an abdominal mass. A laparotomy was decided upon for the removal of the mass. The gross appearance of the mass is as follows. Histopathological examination revealed no malignant change. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mature ‌teratoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Immature‌ teratoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mucinous‌ adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serous ‌adenoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mature teratoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/788409b5ebd847888bbddd35a6f222c9.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8dc0f609164247b99446f676acd7e08ax1280x1266.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2a77b350b8a5429d97b911193c75cebbx1280x1266.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/88c4583a47764199a5ed5597b53c89a8x1280x1142.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/71e69918ea5645aaa1c8e71f436d9147x1280x1143.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mature teratoma</strong></p><p>The given scenario and image showing<strong> unilocular cystic space</strong> containing <strong>hair</strong> and <strong>sebaceous</strong> material are suggestive of <strong>mature teratoma</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Dermoid</strong> cysts or <strong>mature</strong> teratomas are <strong>benign</strong> tumors, usually found in <strong>young women </strong>during the active reproductive years. It is bilateral in 10% to 15% of cases.</p>\n<p>Microscopically, the cyst wall is composed of tissues from <strong>all 3 germ layers</strong>. This includes stratified squamous epithelium, with underlying sebaceous glands, hair shafts, and other skin adnexal structures. Sometimes cartilage, bone, thyroid, and neural tissue can be seen.</p>\n<p>About 1% of the dermoids undergo malignant transformation, most commonly to <strong>squamous cell carcinoma.</strong> It can also transform into other cancers (e.g., thyroid carcinoma, melanoma).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The component tissue of <strong>immature malignant teratomas</strong> resembles embryonal and <strong>immature fetal tissue</strong>. It often consists of <strong>primitive neuroepithelial cells</strong>. The tumor is found chiefly in prepubertal adolescents and<strong> young women</strong>, the mean age being 18 years.</p>\n<p>Monodermal or specialized teratomas:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Struma ovarii </strong>- composed entirely of<strong> mature thyroid tissue, </strong>which may be functional and cause hyperthyroidism.</li>\n<li><strong>Ovarian carcinoid</strong> - arises from intestinal tissue, may be functional causing <strong>carcinoid syndrome </strong>in the absence of hepatic metastasis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8911","text":"A 45-year-old woman presen‌ted to the gynecology OPD‍ with a history of abdomi​nal pain and distension o‌f abdomen. Ultrasound ima​ging revealed tumor in th‌e right ovary. Serum leve‍l of inhibin was raised. ​The histopathology of the​ tumor is given below. Wh‌at is the most likely dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman presented to the gynecology OPD with a history of abdominal pain and distension of abdomen. Ultrasound imaging revealed tumor in the right ovary. Serum level of inhibin was raised. The histopathology of the tumor is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Granulosa ‌cell tumor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sertoli c‌ell tumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Brenner‌'s tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Embryonal‌ carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Granulosa cell tumor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5d6ce021173f4b8d9d8a71df4f9e7dcd.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d1825a13f1974055a04ee6ed705c0ee9x1280x1145.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b130d296dc5845378c6152943af51a51x1280x1145.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Granulosa cell tumor</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and histopathology showing <strong>Call-Exner bodies</strong> are suggestive of <strong>granulosa cell tumor</strong>.</p>\n<p>Call-Exner bodies are small, distinctive, <strong>glandlike </strong>structures filled with an <strong>acidophilic material</strong> resembling immature follicles. Additionally, small, cuboidal to polygonal cells are seen which may grow in anastomosing cords, sheets, or strands. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Granulosa cell tumors are composed of cells that <strong>resemble</strong> <strong>granulosa</strong> <strong>cells</strong> of a developing <strong>ovarian</strong> <strong>follicle</strong>. It can be of adult and juvenile types. <strong>Elevated</strong> tissue and serum levels of <strong>inhibin</strong>, a product of granulosa cells, are associated with this tumor.</p>\n<p>Mutations of the <strong>FOXL2</strong> gene are seen in most adult granulosa cell tumors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6203","text":"A 12-year-old girl, who is‌ yet to attain her menarc‍he, presents with an ovar​ian mass. Her serum bioma‌rkers are normal except f​or elevated AFP. Staging ‌laparotomy was performed ‍and the histopathological​ image is given below. Wh​at is your diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old girl, who is yet to attain her menarche, presents with an ovarian mass. Her serum biomarkers are normal except for elevated AFP. Staging laparotomy was performed and the histopathological image is given below. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dysger‌minoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endodermal ‌sinus tumor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Brenne‌r tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Granulosa ‌cell tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Endodermal sinus tumor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fe0a47df6aae4578a979907e6658fdd3.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/73626f13923942bcaa2a9986bed6ba1bx1280x1147.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/491e2a9e692d44518bd39ae25040f112x1280x1146.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Endodermal sinus tumor</strong></p><p>The given clinical features and the histopathological image showing <strong>Schiller Duval bodies</strong> are suggestive of <strong>endodermal sinus tumors</strong>. It is also known as <strong>yolk sac tumor.</strong></p>\n<p>The Schiller-Duval body is a <strong>glomerulus-like structure</strong> composed of a central blood vessel enveloped by tumor cells within a space that is also lined by tumor cells.</p>\n<p>It is the second most common malignant tumor of <strong>germ cell </strong>origin. It is a <strong>unilateral </strong>tumor usually affecting <strong>children </strong>or <strong>young women.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6205","text":"Which of the following are‌ sex cord-stromal tumors?‍ Brenner tumor Granulosa ​cell tumor Sertoli cell t‌umour Fibroma Dysgerminom​a","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are sex cord-stromal tumors?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Brenner tumor</li>\n<li>Granulosa cell tumor</li>\n<li>Sertoli cell tumour</li>\n<li>Fibroma</li>\n<li>Dysgerminoma</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, ‌3, 4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1, ‌3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3, ‌4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‌4, 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2, 3, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2, 3, 4</strong></p><p><strong>Sex cord-stromal tumors</strong> include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Granulosa </strong>cell tumor</li>\n<li><strong>Sertoli </strong>cell tumor</li>\n<li><strong>Fibroma</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Brenner tumor is an epithelial tumor and dysgerminoma is a germ cell tumor.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6208","text":"A 40-year-old woman presen‌ted with bilateral adnexa‍l masses. Histopathologic​al examination of the res‌ected specimen showed she​ets of mucin-producing si‌gnet-ring cells. What is ‍the most likely diagnosis​?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman presented with bilateral adnexal masses. Histopathological examination of the resected specimen showed sheets of mucin-producing signet-ring cells. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serous papillary carcinoma‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Struma‌ ovarii","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Krukenbe‌rg tumour","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Malignant‌ teratoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Krukenberg tumour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fea18fd8fe0444ddb5f57d2b82a0eba9x1280x1201.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c1507d63622742548491c50454af0458x1280x1201.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Krukenberg tumour</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and histopathology findings are suggestive of metastatic carcinoma of the ovary or <strong>Krukenberg tumor. </strong></p>\n<p>It can arise from primary carcinomas of the stomach (most common), colon, appendix, and breast (invasive lobular carcinoma). It is often <strong>bilateral</strong> and on microscopy shows <strong>mucin-laden signet-ring cells</strong> with<strong> eccentric</strong> hyperchromatic <strong>nuclei</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Metastatic tumors of the ovary include:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1038\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Tumors of Müllerian origin (most common) - the uterus, fallopian tube, contralateral ovary, or pelvic peritoneum</li>\n<li>Tumors of extra-Müllerian tumors - breast and gastrointestinal tract carcinomas</li>\n<li><strong>Pseudomyxoma peritonei </strong>- derived from <strong>appendiceal</strong> tumors</li>\n<li>Krukenberg tumor</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6210","text":"A 32-year-old woman was di‌agnosed with molar pregna‍ncy and underwent suction​ and evacuation. The rese‌cted specimen is shown be​low. Histopathological ex‌amination revealed thin-w‍alled, cystic structures ​consisting of hydropic vi​lli with extensive tropho‌blastic proliferation alo​ng its entire circumferen‍ce. Immunohistochemistry ‌was negative for p57. Whi‌ch of the following is fa‌lse about this condition?‌","raw_text":"A 32-year-old woman was diagnosed with molar pregnancy and underwent suction and evacuation. The resected specimen is shown below. Histopathological examination revealed thin-walled, cystic structures consisting of hydropic villi with extensive trophoblastic proliferation along its entire circumference. Immunohistochemistry was negative for p57. Which of the following is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The genetic material is co‌mpletely maternally deriv‍ed","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Embryo dies very early in ‌development and therefore‍ is usually not identifie​d","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is associated with a 2.‌5% risk of subsequent cho‍riocarcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The possible karyotypes ar‌e 46XX or 46XY","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The genetic material is completely maternally derived","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9947ec9be1344e5385e070b3e1f11de4.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/713ca3322a9b4ac98baf004aa1505235x1280x1159.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3d2eb247032e43699d3ffd3de83a913fx1280x1159.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/92e5a8a45932447fb1ae690b89bddb0cx1280x2039.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The genetic material is completely maternally derived</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and image showing <strong>grapelike vesicles</strong> are suggestive of a <strong>complete mole</strong>. It results from the fertilization of an egg that has lost its female chromosomes, and as a result, the genetic material is <strong>completely paternally derived.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The pathogenesis of complete mole is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Androgenesis</strong> (most common) - <strong>46XX </strong>karyotype stemming from the <strong>duplication</strong> of the genetic material of <strong>1 sperm</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Dispermy </strong>-<strong> 46XX or 46XY</strong> karyotype due to fertilization of an <strong>empty egg</strong> by <strong>2 sperms</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Microscopically, thin-walled, cystic, grapelike structures consisting of <strong>hydropic</strong> <strong>villi</strong> are seen. <strong>Trophoblastic</strong> <strong>proliferation</strong> involving the <strong>entire</strong> <strong>circumference</strong> of the villi is seen.</p>\n<p>The <strong>embryo</strong> <strong>dies</strong> very <strong>early</strong> in <strong>development</strong> and, therefore, is usually not identified. Patients have a <strong>2.5% risk</strong> of subsequent<strong> choriocarcinoma </strong>and a 15% risk of the persistent or invasive mole.</p>\n<p>The following image shows the pathogenesis of molar pregnancy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6212","text":"A 26-year-old woman with a‌ positive pregnancy test ‍presented with vaginal bl​eeding in the first trime‌ster. On examination, gra​pe-like vesicles were not‌ed in the vagina. A trans‍vaginal ultrasound reveal​ed the presence of fetal ​tissues. A suction evacua‌tion was performed. What ​is the most likely karyot‍ype of the evacuated mate‌rial?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old woman with a positive pregnancy test presented with vaginal bleeding in the first trimester.  On examination, grape-like vesicles were noted in the vagina. A transvaginal ultrasound revealed the presence of fetal tissues. A suction evacuation was performed. What is the most likely karyotype of the evacuated material?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"69 ‌XXY","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"46‌ XX","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"46‌ XY","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"47‌ XO","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 69 XXY","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ec1f61efc78d4fd89823b45557f78ab9x1280x2039.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 69 XXY</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with <strong>grape-like vesicles</strong> in the vagina and the presence of <strong>fetal tissues</strong> on transvaginal ultrasound are suggestive of <strong>partial mole</strong>. The karyotype in a partial mole is <strong>triploid</strong> (e.g., 69XXY) or occasionally <strong>tetraploid</strong> (92XXXY).</p>\n<p>It results from the <strong>fertilization </strong>of an <strong>egg </strong>with <strong>2 sperms</strong>. There is an increased risk of <strong>persistent</strong> <strong>molar</strong> disease but it is not associated with choriocarcinoma.</p>\n<p>Given below is an image showing the karyotype of molar pregnancy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6213","text":"What is the most common si‌te of metastasis in gesta‍tional choriocarcinoma?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of metastasis in gestational choriocarcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lu‌ngs","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bre‌ast","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vag‌ina","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Li‌ver","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lungs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lungs</strong></p><p><strong>Lungs</strong> are the <strong>most common</strong> site of metastasis in <strong>gestational choriocarcinoma</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>It is followed by<strong> </strong>the <strong>vagina</strong>, brain, liver, bone, and kidney in descending order of frequency.</p>\n<p>Gestational choriocarcinomas are malignant neoplasm of <strong>trophoblastic cells</strong> derived from a previously normal or abnormal pregnancy, such as an extrauterine ectopic pregnancy. The <strong>hCG </strong>levels are<strong> elevated </strong>to levels above those encountered in hydatidiform moles. It shows <strong>hematogenous spread. </strong>It is rapidly invasive and metastasizes widely.</p>\n<p><strong>Gestational </strong>choriocarcinomas are <strong>chemosensitive</strong> whereas <strong>non-gestational </strong>choriocarcinomas are <strong>chemoresistant</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3221","text":"The genetic profile of mal‌ignant mixed Müllerian tu‍mors resembles that of:","raw_text":"The genetic profile of malignant mixed Müllerian tumors resembles that of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cervica‌l cancer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Uterine‌ sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endometria‌l carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ovaria‌n tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Endometrial carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Endometrial carcinoma</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1023\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Malignant mixed Müllerian tumors</strong> (MMMTs) are carcinosarcomas whose genetic profile closely <strong>resembles endometrial carcinomas</strong>.</p>\n<p>They involve mutations in the same genes - <strong>PTEN, TP53, </strong>and<strong> PIK3CA</strong><em>. </em>The usual genetic alterations found in sarcomas are absent in MMMTs.</p>\n<p>MMMTs are <strong>endometrial</strong> <strong>adenocarcinomas</strong> with malignant <strong>mesenchymal</strong> <strong>components</strong>. The mesenchymal component may resemble uterine mesenchymal elements (stromal sarcoma, leiomyosarcoma) or contain heterologous malignant cell types (striated muscle, cartilage, adipose tissue, and bone).</p>\n<p>It occurs in postmenopausal women and presents with bleeding. It is associated with a poor outcome. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6207","text":"Which of the following are‌ the components of Meig's‍ syndrome? Ascites Dysger​minoma of ovary Right-sid‌ed hydrothorax Fibroma of​ ovary Hydropneumothorax","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are the components of Meig's syndrome?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ascites</li>\n<li>Dysgerminoma of ovary</li>\n<li>Right-sided hydrothorax</li>\n<li>Fibroma of ovary</li>\n<li>Hydropneumothorax</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, ‌3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‌2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‌3, 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3, 4</strong></p><p><strong>Meigs' </strong>syndrome triad includes the following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Solid benign ovarian tumor<strong> (fibroma)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Right-sided hydrothorax </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Ascites </strong>- disappears after the removal of the tumor</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Pseudo-Meigs'</strong> syndrome is characterized by the following:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Non-malignant <strong>ascites</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pleural effusion</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Pelvic tumors</strong> - other than solid benign ovarian tumors</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note: Ovarian <strong>fibroma</strong> is also associated with <strong>nevoid</strong> <strong>basal cell carcinoma</strong>. This association is known as <strong>Gorlin syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6202","text":"A 35-year-old woman was op‌erated for an ovarian mas‍s and the sample was sent​ for histopathological ex‌amination. The findings a​re given below. Which of ‌the following is false ab‍out the given condition?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old woman was operated for an ovarian mass and the sample was sent for histopathological examination. The findings are given below. Which of the following is false about the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a mal‌ignant tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It expresses OCT-3, OCT4, ‌and NANOG","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is unresponsive to radi‌otherapy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It occurs in patients with‌ gonadal dysgenesis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is unresponsive to radiotherapy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f462fee0a8ca4382b7065f56424a4cfe.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/74e4103747674c06828a999e5e5860aax1280x1050.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0197745a6fa347089674ea0812da4e0cx1280x1050.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is unresponsive to radiotherapy</strong></p><p>The given histopathological image shows large cells with clear cytoplasm (<strong>fried egg appearance</strong>), tumor cells arranged in sheets, and<strong> cords</strong> of<strong> fibrous stroma</strong> infiltrated by lymphocytes. These findings are suggestive of <strong>dysgerminoma</strong>. It is <strong>highly</strong> <strong>responsive</strong> to <strong>radiotherapy</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Dysgerminomas are the ovarian <strong>counterparts</strong> of testicular <strong>seminoma</strong>. They may occur in childhood, but most commonly occur in the 2-3rd decades. It may occur in patients with <strong>gonadal dysgenesis</strong>, including pseudohermaphroditism. The majority of these tumors are <strong>unilateral</strong>. They express <strong>OCT-3, OCT4, </strong>and<strong> NANOG</strong>.</p>\n<p>All dysgerminomas are <strong>malignant</strong>, but the degree of histologic <strong>atypia</strong> is <strong>variable.</strong> Only about one-third of these lesions are aggressive. These neoplasms are <strong>responsive</strong> to <strong>chemoradiation</strong>, and overall survival exceeds 80%.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"R_tzILSX","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"47_corticosteroids","title":"47.Corticosteroids","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MA9913","text":"Glucocorticoids act on whi‌ch of the following?","raw_text":"Glucocorticoids act on which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"G protein co‌uple receptor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intracellular cytoplasmic ‌receptor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ligand gated‌ ion channel","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"JAK-STAT‌ pathway","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intracellular cytoplasmic receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intracellular cytoplasmic receptor</strong></p><p><strong>Glucocorticoids</strong> act on the intracellular <strong>cytoplasmic receptors. </strong></p>\n<p>They diffuse into cytosol and bind to the cytoplasmic receptors and influence nuclear changes by activation of the receptor. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9914","text":"Glucocorticoids do not hav‌e permissive action on wh​ich of the following?","raw_text":"Glucocorticoids do not have permissive action on which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Angio‌tensin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adren‌aline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Noradr‌enaline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Brady‌kinin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bradykinin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bradykinin</strong></p><p>Glucocorticoids do not have permissive action on <strong>bradykinin</strong>. They inhibit inflammatory mediators such as histamine, bradykinin, and eicosanoids. </p>\n<p>Glucocorticoids possess <strong>permissive action</strong> on the <strong>vasoconstrictor</strong> effect of <strong>angiotensin</strong> and <strong>lipolytic</strong> action of <strong>adrenaline</strong> and <strong>noradrenaline</strong>. In the absence of glucocorticoids, these hormones have low activity but the presence enhances their activity several folds.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9898","text":"A pregnant woman diagnosed‌ with Cushing's syndrome ​does not want surgery and‍ requests medical treatme‌nt. Which of the followin​g drugs can be recommende​d?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman diagnosed with Cushing's syndrome does not want surgery and requests medical treatment. Which of the following drugs can be recommended?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mifepr‌istone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flut‌amide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Finas‌teride","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metyr‌apone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Metyrapone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Metyrapone</strong></p><p><strong>Metyrapone</strong> can be recommended as it is <strong>approved</strong> in the treatment of <strong>pregnant </strong>women with<strong> Cushing's</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong>. It <strong>inhibits</strong> the <strong>synthesis </strong>of <strong>cortisol</strong> by inhibiting steroid 11 β-hydroxylase reversibly. </p>\n<p>Cushing’s syndrome (CS) is a manifestation of symptoms that are precipitated by <strong>excessive cortisol production.</strong> Transsphenoidal surgery, radiation, and pharmacotherapy constitute the standard treatment for tumor-related causes of CS (e.g. pituitary adenoma).</p>\n<p>The three main pharmacological approaches are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cortisol synthesis inhibitors: Ketoconazole, metyrapone, mitotane, etomidate, aminoglutethimide</li>\n<li>ACTH secretion inhibitors: Cyproheptadine, pasireotide, octreotide, cabergoline</li>\n<li>Glucocorticoid receptor antagonist: Mifepristone</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Metyrapone is also used to test the hypothalamic-pituitary feedback pathway in the diagnosis of Cushing syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3537","text":"The enzyme A in the follow‌ing image corresponds to?​","raw_text":"The enzyme A in the following image corresponds to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"17β-Hydroxysteroid dehydro‌genase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrog‌enase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"11β-Hydroxysteroid dehydro‌genase1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"11β-Hydroxysteroid dehydro‌genase2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 11β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ce85a543b090472f885a51539c8d76cc.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bb2fd6d0efba4d66b6788326ecc331d2x1280x1437.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 11β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase2</strong></p><p>\"<strong>A</strong>\" here corresponds to <strong>11-beta-HSD2</strong> (11β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase2). It converts active <strong>cortisol</strong> to inactive <strong>cortisone.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Cortisol</strong> is active and can bind to <strong>both glucocorticoids and mineralocorticoid receptors</strong> whereas cortisone is inactive and does not bind to either. The <strong>selective inactivation</strong> of cortisol is required in certain sites such as the <strong>distal nephron</strong> so that the specific autoregulatory responses to <strong>aldosterone</strong> can be achieved.</p>\n<p>Therefore, cortisol (active) is metabolized to cortisone (inactive) by <strong>11-beta-HSD2</strong> in the <strong>kidney,</strong> so as to protect renal mineralocorticoid receptors from cortisol binding. Some tissues, like the liver, can convert inactive cortisone back to active cortisol through an enzyme called <strong>11-beta-HSD1</strong> (\"<strong>B</strong>\" in the image). This completes the “<strong>cortisone–cortisol shuttle</strong>” because the kidney can inactivate cortisol where it is not needed and the liver can reactivate it afterward.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><br/>However, when circulating cortisol levels are very high, as in <strong>severe Cushing syndrome</strong>, they can overwhelm this protective mechanism, allowing cortisol to bind to and activate the mineralocorticoid receptor. This leads to <strong>volume expansion, hypertension, and hypokalemia</strong> often observed in hypercortisolism. A mutation of 11-beta-HSD2 also causes a similar clinical picture and is the reason for the <strong>syndrome of apparent mineralocorticoid excess</strong>.</p>\n<p>17β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase and 3β-Hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase are involved in the metabolism of sex steroids.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9916","text":"Which of the following is ‌a prodrug?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a prodrug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Predni‌solone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methylpre‌dnisolone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydroco‌rtisone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Predn‌isone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prednisone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prednisone</strong></p><p><strong>Prednisone</strong> is a <strong>prodrug</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>synthetic</strong> <strong>glucocorticoid</strong> that is biologically inert until converted via <strong>hepatic metabolism</strong> (11-beta HSD1) to the active component, <strong>prednisolone.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9920","text":"A patient with moderate CO‌VID-19 was started on a l​ong-acting glucocorticoid‍. Which of the following ‌drugs could it be?","raw_text":"A patient with moderate COVID-19 was started on a long-acting glucocorticoid. Which of the following drugs could it be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Triamc‌inolone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Predni‌solone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dexame‌thasone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hydroco‌rtisone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dexamethasone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dexamethasone</strong></p><p><strong>Dexamethasone</strong> is the <strong>longest acting glucocorticoid</strong>. Its t<sub>1/2</sub> is &gt;36 hours.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9918","text":"Highest mineralocorticoid ‌activity is seen with whi​ch of the following?","raw_text":"Highest mineralocorticoid activity is seen with which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aldos‌terone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"DO‌CA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fludroc‌ortisone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Triamc‌inolone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aldosterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aldosterone</strong></p><p>The highest mineralocorticoid activity is seen with <strong>aldosterone.</strong></p>\n<p>However, it <strong>cannot be used clinically</strong>. The highest mineralocorticoid activity in a <strong>drug</strong> that is used <strong>clinically</strong> is <strong>fludrocortisone</strong>. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 191px;\" width=\"761\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 246.66px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.66px;\"><strong>Glucocorticoid activity</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.699px;\"><strong>Mineralocorticoid activity</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 246.66px;\"><strong>Short-acting glucocorticoids</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.66px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.699px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 246.66px;\">\n<p>Cortisone</p>\n<p>Hydrocortisone</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.66px;\">\n<p>0.8</p>\n<p>1</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.699px;\">\n<p>0.8</p>\n<p>1</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 246.66px;\"><strong>Intermediate-acting glucocorticoids</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.66px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.699px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 246.66px;\">\n<p>Prednisone-prednisolone</p>\n<p>Methylprednisolone</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.66px;\">\n<p>4</p>\n<p>5</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.699px;\">\n<p>0.8</p>\n<p>0.5</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 246.66px;\"><strong>Long-acting glucocorticoids</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.66px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.699px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 246.66px;\">\n<p>Dexamethasone</p>\n<p>Betamethasone</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.66px;\">\n<p>30</p>\n<p>30</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.699px;\">\n<p>0</p>\n<p>0</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 246.66px;\"><strong>Mineralocorticoids</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.66px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.699px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 246.66px;\">\n<p>Deoxycorticosterone (DOCA)</p>\n<p>Fludrocortisone</p>\n<p>Aldosterone</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.66px;\">\n<p>0.2</p>\n<p>15</p>\n<p>0</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 246.699px;\">\n<p>100</p>\n<p>150</p>\n<p>200-500</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9947","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a mineralocorticoid r​eceptor antagonist?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spirono‌lactone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epler‌enone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Drospi‌renone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metyr‌apone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Metyrapone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Metyrapone</strong></p><p><strong>Metyrapone</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist.</p>\n<p>It is a selective inhibitor of <strong>11β-hydroxylase </strong>and thereby reduces cortisol and <strong>corticosterone synthesis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Options A and B - Spironolactone and eplerenone are <strong>aldosterone antagonist</strong> that is used in hypertension and chronic CHF for reversing the effects of aldosterone</p>\n<p>Option C - Drospirenone is a 4th generation <strong>progestin</strong> used as an oral contraceptive having <strong>mineralocorticoid antagonist</strong> action</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3535","text":"A woman diagnosed with She‌ehan's syndrome is planne​d for the initiation of h‍ormone replacement. Gluco‌corticoids should be admi​nistered prior to thyroxi​ne supplementation due to‌ what concern?","raw_text":"A woman diagnosed with Sheehan's syndrome is planned for the initiation of hormone replacement. Glucocorticoids should be administered prior to thyroxine supplementation due to what concern?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interference in absorption‌ of glucocorticoids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Precipitation acute adrena‌l insufficiency","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Decrease in the metabolism‌ of glucocorticoids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suppression ‌the HPA axis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Precipitation acute adrenal insufficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Precipitation acute adrenal insufficiency</strong></p><p><strong>Glucocorticoids</strong> are administered <strong>before</strong> initiating treatment with <strong>thyroid hormones</strong> as administration of thyroxine can <strong>precipitate acute adrenal insufficiency.</strong></p>\n<p>In the case of combined adrenal insufficiency and hypothyroidism, as in <strong>panhypopituitarism</strong> (due to surgery or <strong>Sheehan's</strong> syndrome), adrenal insufficiency is generally treated first. This is because giving thyroxine first can <strong>enhance cortisol clearance</strong> and precipitate an adrenal crisis. Also, <strong>levothyroxine</strong> increases the basal metabolic rate (BMR) leading to an <strong>increased requirement of cortisol,</strong> which if not met can also precipitate an adrenal crisis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9950","text":"A pregnant woman came for ‌routine antenatal screeni​ng. During prenatal testi‍ng, the fetus was suspect‌ed to have congenital adr​enal hyperplasia. What is​ the drug of choice for t‌reating the fetus in-uter‌o?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman came for routine antenatal screening. During prenatal testing, the fetus was suspected to have congenital adrenal hyperplasia. What is the drug of choice for treating the fetus in-utero?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dexame‌thasone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Betame‌thasone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydroco‌rtisone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Predni‌solone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dexamethasone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dexamethasone</strong></p><p>The <strong>drug of choice</strong> for treating the <strong>fetus</strong> in-utero when suspected of having<strong> congenital adrenal hyperplasia</strong> is <strong>dexamethasone.</strong></p>\n<p>Dexamethasone readily <strong>crosses the placenta </strong>and suppresses the secretion of steroids and androgens by fetal adrenal glands. If started by 6 weeks of gestation, it reduces the virilization of external genitalia in affected females.</p>\n<p><strong>Chorionic villus biopsy</strong> is then performed to determine the sex and the genotype of the fetus and therapy is only continued if the fetus is an <strong>affected female.</strong></p>\n<p>This is not widely accepted currently due to insufficient data on long-term risks especially in unaffected females and males who derive no benefit from the treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9893","text":"A patient is started on ho‌rmone replacement for chr​onic adrenal insufficienc‍y. What is the replacemen‌t dose of hydrocortisone ​that has to be given (in ​mg/day)?","raw_text":"A patient is started on hormone replacement for chronic adrenal insufficiency. What is the replacement dose of hydrocortisone that has to be given (in mg/day)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2‌0","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 20","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 20</strong></p><p>The replacement dose of hydrocortisone for <strong>chronic adrenal insufficiency</strong> is <strong>20 mg/day.</strong></p>\n<p>The treatment of chronic adrenal insufficiency (<strong>not in crisis</strong>) requires glucocorticoid replacement at a dose that replaces the physiologic daily cortisol production. This is achieved by administering an oral replacement dose of <strong>15-25mg</strong> <strong>Hydrocortisone.</strong> <br/>A <strong>midpoint dose of 20mg</strong> is given in 2-3 divided doses: <strong>10mg + 5mg + 5mg</strong>. <br/>A higher morning dose is given at 8:30 am (usually 1/2 to 2/3 of the total dose). The next dose is given at 1:30 pm and in the evening (4:30-5:00 pm). This is to follow the normal circadian rhythm. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9900","text":"A pregnant woman at 33 wee‌ks of gestation presents ​to your antenatal clinic ‍with preterm labour. Whic‌h corticosteroid will you​ administer for stimulati​on of fetal lung maturati‌on?","raw_text":"A pregnant woman at 33 weeks of gestation presents to your antenatal clinic with preterm labour.  Which corticosteroid will you administer for stimulation of fetal lung maturation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydroco‌rtisone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Triamc‌inolone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Methylpre‌dnisolone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Betame‌thasone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Betamethasone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Betamethasone</strong></p><p><strong>Betamethasone</strong> is indicated for the stimulation of <strong>fetal lung maturation </strong>in<strong> preterm labor.</strong></p>\n<p>As compared to cortisol, betamethasone <strong>binds to maternal proteins less</strong> and has <strong>less placental metabolism</strong>. This allows increased transfer to the fetus. Additionally, infants exposed to betamethasone are less likely to require postnatal resuscitation. 2 doses of 12 mg each are administered 18-24 hours apart when the delivery is anticipated before 34 weeks of gestation.</p>\n<p><strong>Dexamethasone </strong>is another long-acting glucocorticoid that can be given. It has benefits such as <strong>less risk of intraventricular hemorrhage</strong> and a shorter length of stay in the NICU.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9924","text":"A patient with chronic adr‌enal insufficiency is on ​long term corticosteroid ‍therapy. Which of the fol‌lowing side effects is he​ unlikely to experience?","raw_text":"A patient with chronic adrenal insufficiency is on long term corticosteroid therapy. Which of the following side effects is he unlikely to experience?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Muscular h‌ypertrophy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Peptic u‌lceration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Psyc‌hosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypert‌ension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Muscular hypertrophy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Muscular hypertrophy</strong></p><p>Corticosteroid therapy does <strong>not</strong> cause <strong>muscular hypertrophy</strong> and the patient is unlikely to experience it as a side effect.</p>\n<p><strong>Excess glucocorticoid</strong> activity results in <strong>muscle wasting and myopathy </strong>while <strong>excess mineralocorticoid activity </strong>results in hypokalemia and causes<strong> muscle weakness.</strong></p>\n<p>Options B and C: <strong>Peptic ulceration </strong>and<strong> psychosis</strong> are known adverse effects of corticosteroids.</p>\n<p>Option D: Glucocorticoids with some mineralocorticoid activity will result in sodium and water retention and may cause <strong>hypertension.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9926","text":"You are planning to add or‌al steroids to manage a c​ase of severe asthma. Pre‍sence of which of the fol‌lowing comorbidities is n​ot a relative contraindic​ation for starting the me‌dication?","raw_text":"You are planning to add oral steroids to manage a case of severe asthma. Presence of which of the following comorbidities is not a relative contraindication for starting the medication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetes‌ mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypert‌ension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eczematous ‌skin disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Peptic ulc‌er disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Eczematous skin disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Eczematous skin disease</strong></p><p><strong>Eczematous skin diseases</strong> are <strong>not</strong> a contraindication for the use of oral corticosteroids. In fact, they are highly effective and are indicated in this condition.</p>\n<p>All the <strong>contraindications to steroids are relative</strong> because of the life-saving nature of steroids.</p>\n<p>Risk-benefit should be assessed cautiously when initiating steroids in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diabetes mellitus</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hypertension</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Peptic ulcer</strong></li>\n<li>Viral and fungal infections</li>\n<li>Tuberculosis and other infections</li>\n<li>Osteoporosis</li>\n<li>Herpes simplex keratitis</li>\n<li>Epilepsy</li>\n<li>CHF</li>\n<li>Renal failure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9929","text":"A 54-year-old woman is bei‌ng evaluated for chronic ​back pain. She mentions t‍hat she had been on corti‌costeroids for asthma sin​ce childhood. Which of th​e following actions of th‌e drug would not cause he‌r condition?","raw_text":"A 54-year-old woman is being evaluated for chronic back pain. She mentions that she had been on corticosteroids for asthma since childhood. Which of the following actions of the drug would not cause her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of gonadal ster‌oidal hormones","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reduced gastrointestinal a‌bsorption of calcium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Suppressive action on oste‌oblasts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibition of action of os‌teoclasts","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Inhibition of action of osteoclasts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Inhibition of action of osteoclasts</strong></p><p><strong>Chronic back pain</strong> following long term <strong>corticosteroid</strong> therapy is suggestive of <strong>osteoporosis</strong>. Glucocorticoids <strong>do not inhibit</strong> of the action of <strong>osteoclasts</strong>.</p>\n<p>It causes<strong> stimulation of osteoclasts</strong> and <strong>increases bone resorption.</strong></p>\n<p>Mechanism of glucocorticoids induced osteoporosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inhibition of gonadal steroidal hormones</li>\n<li>Reduced gastrointestinal absorption of calcium</li>\n<li>Secondary effect of decreased calcium absorption: increased parathyroid hormone leading to further resorption</li>\n<li>Suppressive action on osteoblasts</li>\n<li>Stimulation of osteoclasts</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9937","text":"Osteonecrosis following gl‌ucocorticoid therapy is m​ost commonly associated w‍ith which site?","raw_text":"Osteonecrosis following glucocorticoid therapy is most commonly associated with which site?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Femora‌l head","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Distal‌ femur","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Jaw ‌bone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Humera‌l head","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Femoral head","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Femoral head</strong></p><p>The <strong>femoral head</strong> is the most frequently affected site by <strong>osteonecrosis</strong> due to <strong>glucocorticoid</strong> therapy.</p>\n<p>The earliest symptoms are joint pain and stiffness. When patients on steroid therapy develop hip, shoulder or knee pain, osteonecrosis must be considered and ruled out.</p>\n<p>Options B and D - It can also affect the distal femur and humeral head </p>\n<p>Option C - <strong>Osteonecrosis of the jaw</strong> is associated with the use of<strong> bisphosphonates </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9903","text":"A patient with Cushing’s s‌yndrome has very high lev​els of ACTH. Which of the‍ following is a CRH prepa‌ration that can be used t​o determine if it is a pi​tuitary source or an ecto‌pic source?","raw_text":"A patient with Cushing’s syndrome has very high levels of ACTH. Which of the following is a CRH preparation that can be used to determine if it is a pituitary source or an ectopic source?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gonad‌orelin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cortic‌orelin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gani‌relix","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cosyn‌tropin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Corticorelin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Corticorelin</strong></p><p><strong>Corticorelin</strong> is a corticotrophin-releasing hormone (<strong>CRH</strong>) preparation used in CRH stimulation test which helps to <strong>differentiate</strong> between a <strong>pituitary source and an ectopic</strong> source for <strong>ACTH-dependent hypercortisolism</strong>. </p>\n<p>Pituitary tumors tend to be sensitive to CRH stimulation and responds with a rise in cortisol while ectopic tumors do not usually respond. Ectopic sources of ACTH include carcinoid, medullary thyroid carcinoma, and pheochromocytoma.</p>\n<p>It can also be used to test the HPA axis.</p>\n<p>Option A - Gonadorelin is a <strong>recombinant GnRH.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C - Ganirelix is a <strong>GnRH antagonist.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D - Cosyntropin is a synthetic preparation of <strong>ACTH.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9946","text":"Which of the following is ‌a steroid receptor antago​nist that can be given to‍ a patient with Cushing’s‌ syndrome due to ectopic ​ACTH?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a steroid receptor antagonist that can be given to a patient with Cushing’s syndrome due to ectopic ACTH?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ketoco‌nazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metyr‌apone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mifepr‌istone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aminoglu‌tethimide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mifepristone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mifepristone</strong></p><p><strong>Mifepristone </strong>is a <strong>steroid receptor antagonist </strong>that can be used in <strong>Cushing’s syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>It is used in Cushing’s syndrome especially in inoperable cases with ectopic ACTH secretion or adrenal carcinoma.</p>\n<p>Options A, B, and D - Ketoconazole, metyrapone, and aminoglutethimide reduce steroid synthesis by enzyme inhibition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9949","text":"Which of the following int‌ravenous anaesthetics can​ be used for acute contro‍l of hypercortisolism?","raw_text":"Which of the following intravenous anaesthetics can be used for acute control of hypercortisolism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiop‌entone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Keta‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Etom‌idate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Etomidate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Etomidate</strong></p><p>The intravenous anesthetic that can be used for acute control of hypercortisolism is<strong> etomidate</strong>.</p>\n<p>Etomidate at sub-hypnotic doses <strong>inhibits cortisol secretion </strong>by selectively <strong>inhibiting</strong> <strong>11-beta-hydroxylase</strong> activity.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>It is used off-label to treat hypercortisolism when <strong>rapid control</strong> is needed and the patient is <strong>not able to take oral </strong>medication.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9942","text":"A patient is diagnosed wit‌h inoperable adrenocortic​al carcinoma and needs me‍dical adrenalectomy. Whic‌h of the following drugs ​could be used for him?","raw_text":"A patient is diagnosed with inoperable adrenocortical carcinoma and needs medical adrenalectomy. Which of the following drugs could be used for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mito‌tane","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Methot‌rexate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Doxor‌ubicin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5-Fluor‌ouracil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mitotane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mitotane</strong></p><p><strong>Mitotane</strong> is used for <strong>medical adrenalectomy</strong> in this patient.</p>\n<p>It has a <strong>non-selective toxic action </strong>on the <strong>adrenal cortex. </strong>It causes suppression of adrenal cortex hormone secretion comparable to that of adrenalectomy. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9944","text":"Which of the following ant‌ifungal drugs can be used​ in the treatment of a pa‍tient with Cushing syndro‌me?","raw_text":"Which of the following antifungal drugs can be used in the treatment of a patient with Cushing syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ketoco‌nazole","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fluco‌nazole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Itraco‌nazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Micon‌azole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ketoconazole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ketoconazole</strong></p><p><strong>Ketoconazole</strong> can be used in the treatment of <strong>Cushing syndrome</strong> for symptomatic control.</p>\n<p>It is a non-selective <strong>inhibitor</strong> of adrenal and gonadal <strong>steroid synthesis</strong>. These actions are more apparent at higher doses than those used for fungal infections.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"IGKvyeCu","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"47_wound_healing__tissue_repair___scar","title":"47.Wound Healing, Tissue Repair & Scar","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MA6481","text":"If histopathological exami‌nation of a 2-day-old for‍earm laceration is done, ​which of the following wo‌uld be seen?","raw_text":"If histopathological examination of a 2-day-old forearm laceration is done, which of the following would be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Early collag‌en deposition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neutrophilic‌ infiltration","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Angiog‌enesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased fibroblast activ‌ity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neutrophilic infiltration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d43467f8353a4512921d7d56c8a5a9afx1280x2562.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neutrophilic infiltration</strong></p><p>Neutrophil peak is seen on <strong>day 2</strong> of the inflammatory phase of wound healing.</p>\n<p>Wound healing has three phases: The inflammatory phase, proliferative phase, and maturation phase. The inflammatory phase lasts 2-3 days.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6480","text":"A patient's wound has the ‌given appearance 1 week a‍fter the injury. Which ce​lls are predominantly fou‌nd in this stage of wound‌ healing?","raw_text":"A patient's wound has the given appearance 1 week after the injury. Which cells are predominantly found in this stage of wound healing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neutr‌ophils","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymph‌ocytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Macro‌phages","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fibro‌blasts","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fibroblasts","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2cf1fd66487e45d4be3cb1c0ab30f7f6.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fibroblasts</strong></p><p>The given image shows<strong> granulation tissue </strong>formed during the<strong> proliferative </strong>stage of wound healing<strong>. Fibroblasts</strong> are the predominant cell during the <strong>proliferative</strong> stage.</p>\n<p>Fibroblasts are specialized cells that differentiate from resting mesenchymal cells into connective tissue. The primary function of fibroblasts is to <strong>synthesize collagen.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>proliferative stage</strong> of wound healing is characterized by the formation of granulation tissue, which consists of a capillary bed, fibroblasts, macrophages, and a loose arrangement of collagen, fibronectin, and hyaluronic acid.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6482","text":"Which of the following is ‌an anti-inflammatory cyto‍kine involved in wound he​aling?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an anti-inflammatory cytokine involved in wound healing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IL‌-1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IL‌-6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IL‌-8","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IL‌-10","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. IL-10","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. IL-10</strong></p><p>Interleukins 1, 6 and 8 are pro-inflammatory cytokines, whereas IL-10 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6486","text":"Which of the following eve‌nts of wound healing will‍ be altered in a patient ​with scurvy?","raw_text":"Which of the following events of wound healing will be altered in a patient with scurvy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Platelet a‌ggregation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phagoc‌ytosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Early matrix‌ scaffolding","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Deposition of inorganic ma‌trix","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Early matrix scaffolding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/85af6a133f424e85ad9eac65a6844613x1280x2562.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Early matrix scaffolding</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 273\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Dietary deficiency of </strong><strong>vitamin C</strong> (scurvy) results in <strong>poorly formed collagen</strong> and affects the <strong>e</strong><strong>arly matrix scaffolding.</strong></p>\n<p>Type I and Type III collagens are both present in the extracellular matrix of normal skin (in an <strong>8:1 ratio</strong>). However, during the <strong>proliferative phase of wound healing</strong>, the dominant collagen that is laid is <strong>Type III. </strong>This stage is also known as<strong> early matrix scaffolding</strong>. Type III collagen is gradually <strong>replaced by type I</strong> collagen (during the final remodeling phase) until a ratio (type I: type III) of <strong>4:1</strong> is achieved.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6488","text":"In patients presenting wit‌h non-healing surgical in‍cisions, what is the most​ common underlying cause?‌","raw_text":"In patients presenting with non-healing surgical incisions, what is the most common underlying cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malnut‌rition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infe‌ction","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Smo‌king","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diabetes‌ mellitus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Infection</strong></p><p>All the factors listed impair wound healing, but <strong>local infection </strong>is the most common underlying cause of non-healing surgical incisions.</p>\n<p>To reduce the chances of infection ,the surgeon should make every effort to remove all devitalized tissue and leave a clean wound for closure.</p>\n<p>Factors affecting wound healing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Site of the wound</li>\n<li>Structures involved</li>\n<li>Mechanism of wounding (crushing, incision, avulsion)</li>\n<li>Contamination</li>\n<li>Loss of tissue</li>\n<li>Local factors (vascularity, previous irradiation, pressure)</li>\n<li>Systemic factors (malnutrition, anemia, steroids, AIDS, smoking)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6489","text":"A child with a grazed knee‌ comes to your clinic for‍ first aid. He has a hist​ory of nephrotic syndrome‌ and is on oral prednisol‌one therapy. Which stage ​of wound healing would be​ most affected by his med‌ication?","raw_text":"A child with a grazed knee comes to your clinic for first aid. He has a history of nephrotic syndrome and is on oral prednisolone therapy. Which stage of wound healing would be most affected by his medication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epithelialisation of the w‌ound","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Collagen synthesis and rem‌odelling","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Initial inflammatory phase‌ and angiogenesis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Contraction ‌of the wound","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Initial inflammatory phase and angiogenesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Initial inflammatory phase and angiogenesis</strong></p><p>While <strong>all</strong> of the given options are <strong>inhibited</strong> by <strong>steroids</strong>, the <strong>major effect</strong> of <strong>steroids</strong> is to inhibit the initial <strong>inflammatory phase </strong>of wound healing (angiogenesis, neutrophil and macrophage migration, and fibroblast proliferation) and the <strong>release of lysosomal enzymes.</strong></p>\n<p>The stronger the anti-inflammatory effect of the steroid compound used, the greater the inhibitory effect on wound healing. Steroids used after the first 3 to 4 days post-injury do not affect wound healing as severely as when they are used in the immediate postoperative period. </p>\n<p>In addition to inhibiting collagen synthesis, steroids also<strong> inhibit epithelialization</strong> and <strong>contraction </strong>and contribute to increased rates of <strong>wound infection,</strong> regardless of the time of administration.</p>\n<p>Steroid delayed healing of cutaneous wounds can be stimulated to epithelialize by topical application of <strong>vitamin A.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6490","text":"Which of the following fin‌dings in a long-standing ‍ulcer would indicate mali​gnant transformation?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings in a long-standing ulcer would indicate malignant transformation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Persistent granulation tis‌sue with bleeding","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oedema beyond area of eryt‌hema","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Everted w‌ound edges","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Non-healing after 2 weeks ‌of therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Everted wound edges","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fcf459e5346a446fabacb52f40eff0d7x1088x864.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Everted wound edges</strong></p><p><strong>Everted edge </strong>around a chronic wound is a<strong> sign of malignant transformation.</strong></p>\n<p>The majority of wounds that have <strong>not healed in 3 months</strong> are considered <strong>chronic </strong>and are prone to malignant changes. Malignant wounds are differentiated clinically from non-malignant wounds by the presence of <strong>overturned edges. </strong>Biopsy of the wound edges should be performed to rule out malignancy.</p>\n<p>Malignant transformation can occur in <strong>any long-standing wound.</strong> The image shows a Marjolin's ulcer with everted edges. It is squamous cell carcinoma that develops on a long-standing burn.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6491","text":"A 21-year-old man came to ‌the casualty 12 hours aft‍er sustaining a laceratio​n on his leg from a trekk‌ing accident. On closer e‌xamination, other than di​rt, you do not notice any​ pus or signs of infectio‌n. What is the most appro​priate management?","raw_text":"A 21-year-old man came to the casualty 12 hours after sustaining a laceration on his leg from a trekking accident. On closer examination, other than dirt, you do not notice any pus or signs of infection. What is the most appropriate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Closure after granulation ‌tissue has formed","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Delayed closure 2 days aft‌er removal of devitalised‍ tissue","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Primary closure after adeq‌uate wound toileting and ‍debridement","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wound debridement, topical‌ antibiotics and dressing‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Delayed closure 2 days after removal of devitalised tissue","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/90fa4f8561c14032a5feb8deca640f49.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Delayed closure 2 days after removal of devitalised tissue</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Contaminated wounds <strong>older than 6 hours</strong> should be managed with <strong>delayed primary closure. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The wound is cleaned, debrided, and left open for a few days. This allows soft tissues to drain and also ensures no infection is present before surgical closure can be done. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Wound closure is categorized into primary, secondary, or tertiary:</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Primary closure</strong> is done for <strong>clean wounds with minimal tissue loss</strong> that are amenable to approximation of wound edges. Healing occurs by <strong>primary intention. </strong>Primary closure is ideally performed within <strong>6 to 8 hrs </strong>of trauma, but wounds in vascular areas (e.g. face) may be delayed to up to 24 hours after trauma.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Secondary closure</strong> allows healing by <strong>secondary intention</strong>. The wound is <strong>left open</strong> to heal largely by the <strong>formation of granulation tissue and contraction</strong>. These wounds often have <strong>significant tissue loss</strong> precluding tension-free approximation of edges, with devitalized edges. Wound dressings are changed daily to aid in the formation of granulation tissue and subsequent contraction.<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Tertiary closure</strong>, also known as <strong>delayed primary closure</strong>, results in a combination of healing by primary and secondary intention. The wound is thoroughly irrigated, debrided, and left open for 2 to 5 days before surgical closure. It is used for wounds that are <strong>grossly contaminated without significant tissue loss. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0218","text":"Stage the bedsore in the f‌ollowing image:","raw_text":"Stage the bedsore in the following image:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/93227f683c5c406398747cba22815f56.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 4</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>complete</strong> <strong>skin</strong> <strong>loss</strong> that involves <strong>muscles</strong> and shows <strong>exposed</strong> <strong>bone</strong> indicating a <strong>stage</strong> <strong>4</strong> <strong>pressure sore.</strong></p>\n<p>Pressure sores are skin or soft tissue injuries that occur due to <strong>prolonged</strong> <strong>pressure</strong> over <strong>specific</strong> <strong>areas</strong> of the body. An external pressure of <strong>&gt;30 mmHg</strong> leads to the formation of pressure sores. The common sites of pressure sores in decreasing frequency are the <strong>ischium</strong>, <strong>greater</strong> <strong>trochanter</strong>, sacrum, heel, malleolus (lateral followed by medial), and occiput.</p>\n<p>Pressure sores can be prevented with <strong>good skincare</strong>, the use of air-fluidized beds, special pressure-dispersion cushions, or foams. <strong>Repositioning</strong> the patient<strong> every two hours</strong> can lessen the pressure on the area. </p>\n<p>Ulcers of <strong>stages 1 and 2</strong> often do <strong>not</strong> require <strong>operative</strong> <strong>measures</strong>. They are managed with regular wound cleaning and dressing with a good antibiotic cover.</p>\n<p><strong>Stage 3 and 4</strong> ulcers are adequately <strong>debrided</strong>, and vacuum-assisted closure <strong>(VAC)</strong> is used to promote the growth of granulation tissue required for surgical closure. Large skin flaps that have muscle with sensory innervation are best for reconstruction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6495","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common site of p‍ressure sores ?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common site of pressure sores ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"He‌el","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Occ‌iput","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Greater ‌tochanter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isc‌hium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ischium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ischium</strong></p><p>Pressure sore frequency in descending order:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ischium</strong></li>\n<li>Greater Trochanter</li>\n<li>Sacrum</li>\n<li>Heel</li>\n<li>Malleolus</li>\n<li>Occiput</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2804","text":"An elderly paraplegic pati‌ent admitted to a nursing‍ home, developed an ulcer​ on her gluteal region. A‌ll of the following are t‌rue regarding the managem​ent of this patient excep​t:","raw_text":"An elderly paraplegic patient admitted to a nursing home, developed an ulcer on her gluteal region. All of the following are true regarding the management of this patient except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diet and nutritional suppo‌rt should aim to establis‍h a positive nitrogen bal​ance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Following debridement, exp‌osure of the wound is rec‍ommended","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tensor fascia lata flap wi‌th an intact nerve supply‍ may be used for covering​ the defect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Frequent change in positio‌n and air mattresses can ‍help in preventing furthe​r sores","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Following debridement, exposure of the wound is recommended","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/06c5a4293ab5420bbae46dfa695c9c26.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Following debridement, exposure of the wound is recommended</strong></p><p>The given image is that of a <strong>grade 3 bedsore.</strong> A grade 3 pressure sore <strong>requires dressing </strong>following debridement.</p>\n<p><strong>Preoperative</strong> management involves adequate <strong>debridement</strong>, and the use of <strong>vacuum</strong>-<strong>assisted</strong> <strong>closure</strong> (VAC) to provide a suitable wound for surgical closure. Alginates, foams, hydrocolloids, hydrogels are other options that are available.</p>\n<p>The aim of surgery is to <strong>fill</strong> the <strong>dead</strong> <strong>space</strong> and to provide durable sensate skin. Large skin flaps that include muscle are best and, occasionally, an intact sensory innervated area can be included (e.g. <strong>extensor fascia lata flap with the lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh</strong>).</p>\n<p>Nutritional optimization with <strong>high caloric and protein diet</strong> should be done to establish a <strong>positive nitrogen balance. </strong>Frequent <strong>changes in position</strong> and <strong>air mattresses</strong> can also help in preventing further sores.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6496","text":"A meshed skin graft was us‌ed to cover a defect and ‍vacuum-assisted closure o​f the transplant was done‌. What is the pressure ge‌nerated within the dressi​ng?","raw_text":"A meshed skin graft was used to cover a defect and vacuum-assisted closure of the transplant was done. What is the pressure generated within the dressing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-50 ‌mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"-100‌ mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"-125‌ mmHg","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"-150‌ mmHg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. -125 mmHg","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d7c98a315c1d4a76bcc541f92e615ab3.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. -125 mmHg</strong></p><p>The pressure used in vacuum-assisted closure for wound healing is <strong>–125 mmHg.</strong></p>\n<p>Applying negative pressure appears to <strong>hasten debridement </strong>and the formation of <strong>granulation tissue </strong>in chronic wounds and ulcers.</p>\n<p>It acts by <strong>decreasing edema</strong>, by <strong>removing interstitial fluid, </strong>and as a result, helps in decreasing bacterial counts and cell proliferation increases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6497","text":"A man who had undergone an‌ exploratory laparotomy 6‍ months ago came for a fo​llow-up visit. Which of t‌he following statements a‌bout healed incision is f​alse?","raw_text":"A man who had undergone an exploratory laparotomy 6 months ago came for a follow-up visit. Which of the following statements about healed incision is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Disorganized collagen fibe‌rs become aligned along s‍tress lines","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Strength of the scar is in‌ their weave rather than ‍in their amount","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"By the first week it is he‌ld by sutures and a weak ‍type 3 collagen matrix","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tensile strength is about ‌60-80% of normal skin by ‍3 months","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. By the first week it is held by sutures and a weak type 3 collagen matrix","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b72ac66835784d84b7a009900b801a2d.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. By the first week it is held by sutures and a weak type 3 collagen matrix</strong></p><p>During the first week, a surgical wound has minimal inherent strength because the dermal edges are held together solely by a<strong> hemostatic plug</strong> and <strong>sutures.</strong></p>\n<p>During the second postoperative week, fibroblasts proliferate rapidly and begin to produce type I collagen in addition to type III. The strength of the wound is still limited to less than 10% of its final strength. By week 6, the concentration of strong collagen fibers begins to resemble that of intact skin.</p>\n<p>As the collagen matures and becomes denser, the scar becomes <strong>almost acellular</strong> as the fibroblasts and blood vessels reduce.The disorganized collagen fibers become <strong>aligned along stress lines </strong>with their strength being in their weave rather than in their amount.</p>\n<p>Most of these changes occur over the <strong>first three months</strong> but a scar will continue to mature for 1 to 2 years. Tensile strength will continue to increase but would not be expected to exceed <strong>60–80%</strong> of that of normal skin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6498","text":"The forearm of a patient a‌dmitted to the psychiatri‍c ward is shown in the im​age. Which of the followi‌ng is true about the heal‌ed injury?","raw_text":"The forearm of a patient admitted to the psychiatric ward is shown in the image. Which of the following is true about the healed injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extends beyond the origina‌l incision","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Has racial and genetic pre‌dilection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parallel orientation of co‌llagen fibers","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Does not heal spontaneousl‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Parallel orientation of collagen fibers","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/71f33b0cb9f64d3d9de12049f89753db.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Parallel orientation of collagen fibers</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>hypertrophic scars </strong>which have developed on possible sites of self-inflicted cuts. Type 3 <strong>collagen fibers</strong> are <strong>arranged parallelly </strong>in hypertrophic scars. This is in contrast to a keloid where the collagen fibers are more and randomly arranged. </p>\n<p>Hypertrophic scars rise above the skin level but stay within the confines of the original wound and often regress over time. They have no racial or genetic predilection. </p>\n<p><strong>Difference between keloid and hypertrophic scar:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 399px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.9688px;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.789px;\"><strong>Hypertrophic scar</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.242px;\"><strong>Keloid</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.9688px;\">Genetic predisposition</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.789px;\">Not familial</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.242px;\">Maybe familial - Autosomal dominant with incomplete penetration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.9688px;\">Age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.789px;\">Children</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.242px;\">Adolescents. Middle age</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.9688px;\">Race</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.789px;\">No predilection</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.242px;\">African American, Asian, Hispanic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.9688px;\">Site</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.789px;\">Anywhere (more common on extensor surface)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.242px;\">Neck, chest, ear lobes, shoulders, upper back</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.9688px;\">Timings</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.789px;\">4–6 weeks postinjury</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.242px;\">Symptom-free interval; may appear years after injury</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.9688px;\">Symptoms</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.789px;\">Raised, some pruritus, respects wound confines</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.242px;\">Pain, pruritus, hyperesthesia, growth beyond wound margins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.9688px;\">Regression</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.789px;\">Frequent spontaneous</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.242px;\">No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.9688px;\">Contracture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.789px;\">Frequent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.242px;\">Rare</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 97.9688px;\">Histology</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.789px;\">Parallel orientation of collagen fibers</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.242px;\">Hypocellular, thick, wavy collagen fibers in random orientation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3782","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements are true with reg‍ards to a keloid scar?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements are true with regards to a keloid scar?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The extent does not cross ‌the wound margins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wide excision of the keloi‌d scar prevents recurrenc‍e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Histopathology of a keloid‌ scar shows reduced colla‍gen and increased vascula​rity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A keloid scar forms due to‌ increase in the level of‍ growth factors","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A keloid scar forms due to increase in the level of growth factors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/61e40243f86143daacfc9c2ba914eaaax800x534.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A keloid scar forms due to increase in the level of growth factors</strong></p><p>Keloid scars are associated with <strong>elevated levels of</strong> <strong>growth factor</strong> leading to increased proliferation of fibrous tissue.</p>\n<p>A keloid scar is defined as excessive scar tissue <strong>that extends beyond the boundaries </strong>of the original incision or wound (option A) and rarely regresses on its own.  It is more common in <strong>darker-pigmented ethnicities.</strong></p>\n<p>It can arise from surgery, burns, skin inflammation, infections, ear piercing, and even spontaneously. It occurs<strong> 3 months to years</strong> after the initial insult with even minor injuries causing keloid formation.</p>\n<p>It most commonly occurs on the skin of the earlobe, deltoid, presternal, upper back, and rarely extends into the subcutaneous tissue.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The histology of keloid scars shows <strong>excess collagen </strong>and hypervascularity with collagen fibres <strong>arranged haphazardly</strong> in loose sheets (option C).</p>\n<p>Treatment: Keloid scars do not improve spontaneously with time and <strong>excision alone has a high rate of recurrence</strong> (option B). The following can be done:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pressure - local moulds or elastic garments.</li>\n<li>Silicone gel sheeting.</li>\n<li>Intralesional triamcinolone injection.</li>\n<li>Excision and steroid injections.</li>\n<li>Excision and postoperative radiation (external beam or brachytherapy).</li>\n<li>Intralesional excision.</li>\n<li>Laser to reduce redness.</li>\n<li>Vitamin E and palm oil massage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Differences between Keloids and Hypertrophic scars:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 59.4374%; height: 380.773px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 20.3378%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 40.2127%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Keloid </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 39.4495%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Hypertrophic scar </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 20.3378%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\">Incidence </td>\n<td style=\"width: 40.2127%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\">Rare </td>\n<td style=\"width: 39.4495%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\">Frequent </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 20.3378%; height: 44.7969px; text-align: center;\">Ethnic groups </td>\n<td style=\"width: 40.2127%; height: 44.7969px; text-align: center;\">African, American, Asian, Hispanic </td>\n<td style=\"width: 39.4495%; height: 44.7969px; text-align: center;\">No predilection </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 20.3378%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\">Prior injury </td>\n<td style=\"width: 40.2127%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\">Yes </td>\n<td style=\"width: 39.4495%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\">Yes </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 20.3378%; height: 44.7969px; text-align: center;\">Site predilection</td>\n<td style=\"width: 40.2127%; height: 44.7969px; text-align: center;\">Neck, chest, earlobes, shoulders, upper back </td>\n<td style=\"width: 39.4495%; height: 44.7969px; text-align: center;\">Anywhere </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 20.3378%; height: 44.7969px; text-align: center;\">Genetics </td>\n<td style=\"width: 40.2127%; height: 44.7969px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Autosomal dominant</strong> with incomplete penetration </td>\n<td style=\"width: 39.4495%; height: 44.7969px; text-align: center;\">No </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 20.3378%; height: 44.7969px; text-align: center;\">Timing </td>\n<td style=\"width: 40.2127%; height: 44.7969px; text-align: center;\">Symptom-free interval; may appear years after injury </td>\n<td style=\"width: 39.4495%; height: 44.7969px; text-align: center;\">4-6 weeks post-injury </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 67.1953px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 20.3378%; height: 67.1953px; text-align: center;\">Symptoms </td>\n<td style=\"width: 40.2127%; height: 67.1953px; text-align: center;\">Pain, pruritus, hyperesthesia, growth beyond wound margins</td>\n<td style=\"width: 39.4495%; height: 67.1953px; text-align: center;\">Raised, some pruritus, respects wound confines </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 20.3378%; height: 44.7969px; text-align: center;\">Regression and contracture </td>\n<td style=\"width: 40.2127%; height: 44.7969px; text-align: center;\">No, and rare </td>\n<td style=\"width: 39.4495%; height: 44.7969px; text-align: center;\">Frequent </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 20.3378%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\">Histology </td>\n<td style=\"width: 40.2127%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\">Hypocellular, thick, wavy collagen fibres in <strong>random orientation </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 39.4495%; height: 22.3984px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Parallel orientation</strong> of collagen fibres </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6502","text":"A patient has been treated‌ for the lesion shown in ‍the picture below for the​ past 12 months but there‌ has been no response to ‌treatment. What is the ne​xt line of treatment for ​this patient?","raw_text":"A patient has been treated for the lesion shown in the picture below for the past 12 months but there has been no response to treatment. What is the next line of treatment for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intralesional injection of‌ steroids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pressure ‌treatment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Excision with adjuvant the‌rapy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Excisio‌n alone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Excision with adjuvant therapy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/da47955eb717404784932b666ec2dfe5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Excision with adjuvant therapy</strong></p><p><strong>Surgical exci­sion in combination with adjuvant therapy is advised to patients with refractory keloids. </strong></p>\n<p>The adjuvant therapy usually includes radiation.The risk of malignancy attributable to keloid radiation therapy is minimal </p>\n<p><strong>Treatment of keloids</strong> :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>First-line treatments include silicones in combination with <strong>pressure therapy</strong> and <strong>intralesional corticosteroid</strong> <strong>injections.</strong></li>\n<li>Intralesional 5-fluorouracil, bleomycin, and verapamil should be used in accordance with established treatment protocols.</li>\n<li><strong>Refractory cases </strong>after <strong>12 months</strong> of therapy should be considered for surgical <strong>excision</strong> in combination with <strong>adjuvant therapy</strong>.</li>\n<li>Excision alone results in a <strong>high recurrence rate</strong> of 50% to 100% and enlargement of the keloid.</li>\n<li>Immediate postoperative electron beam irradiation or brachytherapy with iridium-192 reduces recurrence rates. The risk of malignancy attributable to keloid radiation therapy is minimal.</li>\n<li>An additional new invasive treatment modality is <strong>internal cryotherapy</strong>, in which a metal rod is introduced into the keloid and the subsequent extreme cooling leads to tissue necrosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6499","text":"Which of the following tec‌hniques will you use to r‍educe suture marks and sc​ars? Use of multifilament‌ sutures Delayed suture r‌emoval Placing sutures cl​ose to the wound edges Ch​oosing sutures 6/0 or sma‌ller Subcuticular suturin​g","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following techniques will you use to reduce suture marks and scars?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Use of multifilament sutures</li>\n<li>Delayed suture removal </li>\n<li>Placing sutures close to the wound edges</li>\n<li>Choosing sutures 6/0 or smaller</li>\n<li>Subcuticular suturing</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌,5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,‌2,4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3,‌4,5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2‌,4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3,4,5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3,4,5</strong></p><p><strong>Fine sutures </strong>(6/0 or smaller) <strong>placed close </strong>to the wound margins tend to cause less scarring. <strong>Subcuticular suturing </strong>avoids suture marks on either side of the wound or incision.</p>\n<p>Suture marks can be minimized using <strong>monofilament sutures </strong>that are<strong> removed early</strong> (3–5 days). Wounds can be strengthened post-suture removal by the use of sticky strips.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6500","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a skin substitute?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a skin substitute?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Int‌egra","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Allo‌derm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tran‌cyte","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Duo‌derm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Duoderm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Duoderm</strong></p><p>Duoderm is a <strong>hydrocolloid </strong>and not a skin substitute.</p>\n<p>Skin substitutes are skin products made of cells, extracellular matrix components, or a combination of the two.</p>\n<p><strong>Integra </strong>is a bilayered membrane skin substitute.</p>\n<p><strong>Alloderm </strong>is a acellular cadaveric dermis.</p>\n<p><strong>Apligraf </strong>is a living bilayered biologic dressing composed of neonatal dermal fibroblasts on a collagen matrix.</p>\n<p><strong>Trancyte</strong> is a tissue-engineered skin substitute is made from a nylon mesh and a silastic semi-permissible and biocompatible layer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6501","text":"Identify the procedure sho‌wn in the image.","raw_text":"Identify the procedure shown in the image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Venes‌ection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fasciotomy o‌f lower limb","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"GSV st‌ripping","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Linton's ‌operation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fasciotomy of lower limb","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/89acddf347384543bbf2aac00fb5fbad.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fasciotomy of lower limb</strong></p><p>The image depicts a <strong>fasciotomy</strong> of the lower limb.</p>\n<p>Fasciotomy involves incising the <strong>deep muscle fascia </strong>and is best carried out via longitudinal incisions of skin, fat, and fascia. The <strong>muscle </strong>is seen<strong> bulging out </strong>through the fasciotomy opening.</p>\n<p>The lower limb can be decompressed via <strong>two incisions</strong>, each being lateral to the subcutaneous border of the tibia. This gives access to the two posterior compartments and to the peroneal and anterior compartments of the leg.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6492","text":"A catheter is inserted int‌o the limb of a patient s‍uspected to have compartm​ent syndrome. What minimu‌m reading on the pressure‌ monitor will prompt an i​mmediate fasciotomy?","raw_text":"A catheter is inserted into the limb of a patient suspected to have compartment syndrome. What minimum reading on the pressure monitor will prompt an immediate fasciotomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20 ‌mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"30 ‌mmHg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"40 ‌mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"50 ‌mmHg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 30 mmHg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 30 mmHg</strong></p><p>The <strong>pressure threshold </strong>for doing a <strong>fasciotomy</strong> in a case of suspected compartment syndrome is <strong>30 mmHg</strong></p>\n<p>Fasciotomy involves incising the deep muscle fascia and is best carried out via longitudinal incisions of skin, fat, and fascia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3784","text":"While performing a fasciot‌omy for compartment syndr‍ome, the following layers​ are incised:","raw_text":"While performing a fasciotomy for compartment syndrome, the following layers are incised:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skin and Subcutaneous tiss‌ue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Skin, Subcutaneous tissue,‌ Superficial fascia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Skin, Subcutaneous tissue,‌ Superficial fascia and D‍eep fascia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Skin, Subcutaneous tissue,‌ Superficial fascia, Deep‍ fascia and a few muscula​r fibres","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Skin, Subcutaneous tissue, Superficial fascia and Deep fascia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bfba91ccc33140e4a8452ca04386c448x720x1280.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Skin, Subcutaneous tissue, Superficial fascia and Deep fascia</strong></p><p>The incision for a fasciotomy involves the <strong>skin, subcutaneous tissue, superficial fascia,</strong> and<strong> deep fascia.</strong></p>\n<p>Fasciotomy involves incising the <strong>deep muscle fascia </strong>and is best carried out via longitudinal incisions of skin, subcutaneous fat, and superficial and deep fascia. The <strong>muscle will be seen bulging out </strong>through the fasciotomy opening.</p>\n<p>The lower limb can be decompressed via <strong>two incisions</strong>, each being lateral to the subcutaneous border of the tibia. This gives access to the two posterior compartments and the leg's peroneal and anterior compartments.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6504","text":"The lower limb of this pat‌ient was run over by a ca‍r. Identify the type of i​njury.","raw_text":"The lower limb of this patient was run over by a car. Identify the type of injury.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deglovin‌g injury","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Crush ‌injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lacerate‌d injury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tidy ‌wound","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Degloving injury","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/22ed05e62e1847ef8ac555f5033d495e.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Degloving injury</strong></p><p>Degloving injury occurs when the skin and subcutaneous fat are stripped by avulsion from its <strong>underlying fascia</strong>, leaving neurovascular structures, tendons, or bones exposed.</p>\n<p>It may be open or closed.</p>\n<p>A <strong>tidy wound </strong>is a clean incised wound with seldom tissue loss whereas an <strong>untidy wound </strong>is one that is crushed, avulsed, contaminated often with tissue loss.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6794","text":"What is the most likely di‌agnosis of the lesion sho‍wn in the image below?","raw_text":"What is the most likely diagnosis of the lesion shown in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arterial in‌sufficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neuropat‌hic ulcer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Venous‌ ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tubercul‌ous ulcer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neuropathic ulcer","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f4011c8b5f5840908b38890213d75758.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/143d3bb7ca9c482fbe5fc15f4918d20dx1280x629.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c196b68fb0534025b7bd8a1042dc1cf6x1280x837.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eb423d0a75384b5abb58e73e14aa7d0dx1084x756.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b7da0086f5c44412b9ab9440e4258079x1018x796.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neuropathic ulcer</strong></p><p>The given image which shows a <strong>punched-out</strong> ulcer with <strong>regular margins</strong> on the <strong>plantar</strong> surface of the dry, crackled foot is suggestive of a <strong>neuropathic</strong> ulcer.</p>\n<p><strong>Neuropathic ulcers</strong> occur as a result of <strong>repeated trauma</strong> to the insensitive part <strong>due to peripheral neuropathy.</strong> There is a lack of sensation over the pressure points, leading to unnoticed microtrauma, wounds or cuts which develop into an ulcer. <strong>Causes</strong> of neuropathic ulcers include <strong>diabetic</strong> neuropathy, <strong>B12 deficiency</strong>, alcoholism, hypothyroidism, leprosy, peripheral nerve injury, transverse myelitis and myelomeningocele.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 531px; width: 637px; float: left;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 60px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 131px; height: 60px;\"><strong>Type of Ulcer</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 180.21875px; height: 60px;\">\n<p><strong>Arterial</strong></p>\n<p>(option A)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.78125px; height: 60px;\">\n<p><strong>Venous</strong></p>\n<p>(option C)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 60px;\"><strong>Neuropathic</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 60px;\"><strong>Pressure </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 72px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 131px; height: 72px;\"><strong>Underlying condition</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 180.21875px; height: 72px;\">\n<p>Peripheral vascular disease, hypertension</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.78125px; height: 72px;\">Varicose veins, DVT, obesity, previous thrombophlebitis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 72px;\">Diabetes, peripheral neuropathy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 72px;\">Limited mobility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 79px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 131px; height: 79px;\"><strong>Location</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 180.21875px; height: 79px;\">Lateral malleolus, toes, and dorsum of the foot</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.78125px; height: 79px;\">Lower leg, above the medial malleolus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 79px;\">The plantar surface of the foot, toes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 79px;\">Bony prominences (e.g.heel)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 126.390625px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 131px; height: 126.390625px;\"><strong>Characteristics</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 180.21875px; height: 126.390625px;\">Deep, punched-out, irregular margins. The wound bed has unhealthy necrotic tissue</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.78125px; height: 126.390625px;\">Shallow, flat with exudate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 126.390625px;\">Punched-out with regular calloused margins. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 126.390625px;\">Deep, macerated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 121px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 131px; height: 121px;\"><strong>Condition of leg/foot</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 180.21875px; height: 121px;\">Cool thin shiny skin, pallor on leg elevation, absent peripheral pulses, and delayed capillary refill</td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.78125px; height: 121px;\">Excematous and pigmented. Limb edema may be present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 121px; height: 121px;\">Dry, cracked, insensate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 121px;\">Atrophic skin, loss of  muscle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> Other options:</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Tuberculous ulcers</strong> occur when a cold abscess from bone and joint tuberculosis breaks out on the surface. The most characteristic feature of this ulcer is its edge which is thin and <strong>undermined. </strong>The following is a picture of a tuberculous ulcer:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following is a picture of an arterial ulcer.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following is a picture of a venous ulcer.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following is a picture of a pressure ulcer.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6503","text":"A 68-year-old diabetic pre‌sented with a foot ulcer ‍that he first noticed 1 m​onth ago. The edges are i‌rregular with sloped marg‌ins. The ulcer does not b​leed to touch. Peripheral​ pulses are felt and an a‌rterial doppler does not ​indicate a compromised bl‌ood flow. Which growth fa​ctor has been formulated ‍and approved for treatmen​t?","raw_text":"A 68-year-old diabetic presented with a foot ulcer that he first noticed 1 month ago. The edges are irregular with sloped margins. The ulcer does not bleed to touch. Peripheral pulses are felt and an arterial doppler does not indicate a compromised blood flow. Which growth factor has been formulated and approved for treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IG‌F-1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PD‌GF","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"ED‌GF","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"TG‌F-B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. PDGF","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. PDGF</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of a diabetic foot ulcer. At present,  <strong>PDGF (platelet-derived growth factor)-BB</strong> is currently approved by the FDA for the treatment of diabetic foot ulcers.</p>\n<p>Application of recombinant human PDGF-BB in a gel suspension to these wounds increases the incidence of total healing and decreases healing time.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6506","text":"Which of the following pro‌cedures leaves a clean-co‍ntaminated wound?","raw_text":"Which of the following procedures leaves a clean-contaminated wound?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyroi‌dectomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Repair of an old duodenal ‌perforation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Elective cho‌lecystectomy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Emergency a‌ppendectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Elective cholecystectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Elective cholecystectomy</strong></p><p>Elective procedures in uninfected respiratory, genital, urinary, and gastrointestinal tracts such as <strong>elective cholecystectomy</strong> produce <strong>clean-contaminated </strong>wounds<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>T</strong><strong>hyroidectomy</strong> leaves a <strong>clean</strong> wound.</p>\n<p>Option B: Repair of an <strong>old duodenal perforation</strong> is a <strong>contaminated </strong>wound<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>E</strong><strong>mergency appendectomy</strong> is usually performed in the setting of an inflamed appendix and results in a <strong>dirty</strong> surgical wound.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0814","text":"ASEPSIS wound scoring take‌s all of the following in‍to consideration except:","raw_text":"ASEPSIS wound scoring takes all of the following into consideration except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wound swab f‌rom the site","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serous d‌ischarge","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eryt‌hema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Indur‌ation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Induration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Induration</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">ASEPSIS wound score <strong>does not</strong> take <strong>induration</strong> into consideration. Instead, the I in ASEPS<strong>I</strong>S stands for the <strong>isolation of bacteria from the wound</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>ASEPSIS wound score</strong> and <strong>Southampton wound score</strong> are used to determine the <strong>severity</strong> of the <strong>surgical site wound infection (SSI)</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">ASEPSIS score includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>A</strong>dditional treatment\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Antibiotics for wound infection</span></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Drainage of pus under local anesthesia</span></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Debridement of the wound under general anesthesia</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>S</strong>erous discharge</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>E</strong>rythema</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>P</strong>urulent exudate</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>S</strong>eparation of deep tissue</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>I</strong>solation of bacteria from the wound</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>S</strong>tay as inpatient prolonged over 14 days as a result of wound infection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1438","text":"Which of the following is ‌used for wound infection ‍grading?","raw_text":"Which of the following is used for wound infection grading?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ASA class‌ification","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"SIRS c‌riteria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Southampt‌on grading","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"APACHE ‌II score","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Southampton grading","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Southampton grading</strong></p><p><strong>Southampton</strong> <strong>wound</strong> <strong>grading</strong> system is used to score the <strong>severity</strong> of <strong>wound</strong> <strong>site</strong> <strong>infection</strong>.</p>\n<p>Southampton wound grading system:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Grade 0 - Normal healing</li>\n<li>Grade I - Normal healing with different grades of erythema</li>\n<li>Grade II - Erythema with other signs of inflammation at different parts of the wound.</li>\n<li>Grade III - Clear or serosanguinous discharge at different sites</li>\n<li>Grade IV - Pus discharge</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: American Society of Anesthesiologists <strong>(ASA) </strong> grading is used in<strong> pre-anesthetic checkups</strong> to assess a patient's physical health and fitness for anesthesia and surgery.</p>\n<p>Option B: Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome <strong>(SIRS)</strong> is a set of clinical criteria used to<strong> identify and monitor critically ill patients</strong> with high-grade burns, severe trauma, and other major surgeries.</p>\n<p>Option D: Acute Physiology and Chronic Health Evaluation II <strong>(APACHE II)</strong>  is a scoring system used to assess the <strong>severity</strong> and predict the <strong>outcome</strong> of <strong>pancreatitis</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"oN4I24FV","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"48_abdominal_cavity_and_peritoneum","title":"48.Abdominal Cavity And Peritoneum","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MA7305","text":"The imaginary plane passin‌g through the bony promin‍ences marked in red in th​e image below lies at the​ level of________.","raw_text":"The imaginary plane passing through the bony prominences marked in red in the image below lies at the level of________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lower bor‌der of L3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Upper bor‌der of L3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Upper bor‌der of L5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lower bor‌der of L5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Upper border of L5","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/03221c0d0eaf4245bad579b47d636a6c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Upper border of L5</strong></p><p>The structures marked in red are the <strong>tubercles </strong>of the<strong> iliac crest.</strong> An imaginary line passing through them forms the <strong>trans-tubercular plane</strong> which lies at the <strong>upper border of L5.  </strong></p>\n<p>The abdomen is divided into <strong>9 regions</strong> based on the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Two <strong>transverse </strong>planes:\n<ul>\n<li><strong><strong>Subcostal plane </strong></strong>which<strong><strong> </strong></strong>is indicated by a line that joins the <strong>lowest point of the costal margin</strong> on each side. It corresponds to the level of 10th costal cartilage and lower border of second lumbar vertebra<strong>(L2)</strong> or upper border of third lumbar vertebra <strong>(L3) </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Trans-tubercular</strong> <strong>plane</strong> is indicated by a line that joins the tubercles of the iliac crests. It corresponds to the level of fifth lumbar vertebra <strong>(L5).</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Two <strong>vertical </strong>planes<strong> </strong>on both sides:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Right </strong>and<strong> left lateral planes</strong> passing from <strong>mid-clavicular</strong> <strong>point</strong> to the <strong>mid-inguinal point.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7311","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures is not present in‍ the plane marked as 1 in​ the image? Origin of the​ celiac trunk Origin of t‍he portal vein Lower limi‌t of the spinal cord in n​ewborn Lower limit of the‍ spinal cord in adults Ci​sterna chyli","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following structures is not present in the plane marked as 1 in the image?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Origin of the celiac trunk</li>\n<li>Origin of the portal vein</li>\n<li>Lower limit of the spinal cord in newborn</li>\n<li>Lower limit of the spinal cord in adults</li>\n<li>Cisterna chyli</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 3","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3,‌ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 a‌nd 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1 and 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1857bba798fe4ab49bd2d03a2f231ae0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1 and 3</strong></p><p>The structure marked in the image is the <strong>transpyloric plane </strong>which is present at <strong>L1 level</strong>. The origin of the <strong>celiac trunk</strong> (<strong>T12</strong>) and <strong>lower limit</strong> of the <strong>spinal cord</strong> of a <strong>newborn</strong> (<strong>L3</strong>) are not present at this level.</p>\n<p>The transpyloric plane (<strong>Addison's plane</strong>)<strong> </strong>passes <strong>midway</strong> between the <strong>suprasternal notch</strong> of the manubrium and the upper border of the<strong> pubic</strong> <strong>symphysis</strong>. This approximately corresponds to midway between the xiphisternal joint and the umbilicus. <strong>Posteriorly</strong>, this plane passes through the lower half of the body of the first lumbar vertebra <strong>(lower border of L1)</strong>. </p>\n<p>Structures present in the transpyloric plane:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 202px;\" width=\"909\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 446.5px;\"><strong>Organs </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 446.5px;\"><strong>Neurovascular structures</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 446.5px;\">Pylorus of stomach</td>\n<td style=\"width: 446.5px;\">Origin of the superior mesenteric artery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 446.5px;\">D1 part of the duodenum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 446.5px;\">Origin of the renal artery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 446.5px;\">Duodenojejunal flexure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 446.5px;\">Origin of the portal vein (behind the neck of the pancreas)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 446.5px;\">Fundus of the gallbladder</td>\n<td style=\"width: 446.5px;\">Lower limit of the spinal cord in adults</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 446.5px;\">Hilum of the left kidney (right kidney is slightly lower)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 446.5px;\"> Cisterna chyli</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9524","text":"Which level does the struc‌ture marked as X in the i‍mage lie at?","raw_text":"Which level does the structure marked as X in the image lie at?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"L‌1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"L‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T‌11","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T‌12","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. L1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/af64330b28084acfa30a9f2573062e76.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dd65e4f095654cb087ca43ddd574f2ebx600x719.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. L1</strong></p><p>The structure marked in the image is the <strong>fundus </strong>of the<strong> gallbladder. </strong>It lies at the level of<strong> L1</strong> in the <strong>transpyloric plane</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>tip </strong>of the<strong> ninth costal cartilage</strong> in the transpyloric plane, near the <strong>junction </strong>of the<strong> linea semilunaris</strong> with the <strong>costal margin</strong> is used to identify the fundus of the gall bladder. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The transpyloric plane is an imaginary plane that lies midway between the suprasternal notch and the upper border of the pubic symphysis. Posteriorly, this plane intersects the lower half of the body of the first lumbar vertebra. Anteriorly, it intersects the costal margin at the ninth costal cartilage.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7344","text":"In a patient with referred‌ back pain, a surgeon sus‍pects the origin of pain ​to be from a retroperiton​eal organ. Which of the f‍ollowing is the likely ca‌use of pain?","raw_text":"In a patient with referred back pain, a surgeon suspects the origin of pain to be from a retroperitoneal organ. Which of the following is the likely cause of pain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jej‌unum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Li‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sto‌mach","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Panc‌reas","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pancreas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c7e144969f9d40149b217bb2adc13e36x600x721.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pancreas</strong></p><p>The <strong>pancreas</strong> is a <strong>retroperitoneal</strong> structure. All the other structures listed are intraperitoneal. Pain originating from retroperitoneal organs are likely to be referred to the back.</p>\n<p>Relationship of viscera to the peritoneum: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 428px; width: 826px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 67px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 67px;\"><strong>Intraperitoneal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 293.031px; height: 67px;\"><strong>Retroperitoneal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.969px; height: 67px;\">\n<p><strong>Partially covered in the peritoneum</strong></p>\n<p>(considered as retroperitoneal)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 37px;\">\n<p>Stomach</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 293.031px; height: 37px;\">\n<p>Esophagus (abdominal)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.969px; height: 37px;\">\n<p>Ascending colon</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36.3125px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 36.3125px;\">\n<p>Jejunum and ilium</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 293.031px; height: 36.3125px;\">\n<p>D2 and D3 duodenum</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.969px; height: 36.3125px;\">\n<p>Descending colon</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 3px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 3px;\">\n<p>Transverse and sigmoid colon</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 293.031px; height: 3px;\">\n<p>Pancreas</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.969px; height: 3px;\">\n<p>Rectum</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 37px;\">\n<p>Liver and gallbladder</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 293.031px; height: 37px;\">\n<p>Kidneys, ureters, and adrenal gland</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.969px; height: 37px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 36px;\">\n<p>Spleen</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 293.031px; height: 36px;\">\n<p>Abdominal aorta and inferior vena cava</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.969px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 215px; height: 46px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 293.031px; height: 46px;\">\n<p>Celiac plexus</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.969px; height: 46px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3023","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding the roo‍t of the mesentery is fal​se? It is 30 cm long. One​ end is attached to the r‍ight sacroiliac joint. Le‌ft ureter and psoas major​ are crossed by the root ‍of the mesentery. Superio​r mesenteric artery and v​ein are its contents. It ​does not cross the midlin‍e.","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements regarding the root of the mesentery is false?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It is 30 cm long.</li>\n<li>One end is attached to the right sacroiliac joint.</li>\n<li>Left ureter and psoas major are crossed by the root of the mesentery.</li>\n<li>Superior mesenteric artery and vein are its contents.</li>\n<li>It does not cross the midline.</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,3, ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,2, ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,3, ‌and 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2,‌ and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1,3, and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0abf2b63ea3549ffbb10ddaa53adb2edx600x750.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1,3, and 5</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1529\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>root of the mesentery</strong> is approximately <strong>15 cm</strong> long. It is <strong>crossed by</strong> the <strong>right ureter</strong> and <strong>right psoas major</strong> and it <strong>crosses</strong> the <strong>midline</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The root of the mesentery is the <strong>attachment of</strong> the <strong>mesentery</strong> of the small intestine<strong> to</strong> the <strong>posterior abdominal wall</strong>. It runs diagonally from the <strong>duodenojejunal flexure</strong> on the left side of the L2 vertebral body to the <strong>right sacroiliac joint</strong>. The length of intestinal attachment of the mesentery measures approximately 5 to 6 meters (same as the length of the small intestine) and it has a breadth (from the root to the intestinal border) of 20 cm.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Structures crossed by the root of the mesentery</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Third part of the duodenum</li>\n<li>Abdominal aorta</li>\n<li>Inferior vena cava</li>\n<li>Right ureter</li>\n<li>Right psoas major</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1529\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Contents of the mesentery</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Superior mesenteric vessels (jejunal and ileal branches)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Nerves related to the superior mesenteric plexus</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Lymphatics and regional lymph nodes</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Connective tissue and fat</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3025","text":"A 60-year-old man presente‌d with bleeding per rectu‍m and weight loss. He und​erwent an endorectal ultr​asound whose findings are‍ given below. A diagnosis‌ of rectal carcinoma was ​made and the patient was ‍posted for total mesorect​al excision. Which of the​ following is contained i​n the excised structure?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presented with bleeding per rectum and weight loss. He underwent an endorectal ultrasound whose findings are given below. A diagnosis of rectal carcinoma was made and the patient was posted for total mesorectal excision. Which of the following is contained in the excised structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior mesenteric plexus‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior r‌ectal vein","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior r‌ectal vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior re‌ctal artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Superior rectal vein","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a73a9244436b402e83b57520babbf50a.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3f1ee62e4301478eacc7d4356b3fd360x600x584.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Superior rectal vein</strong></p><p>The <strong>mesorectum</strong> is excised in total mesorectal excision. It contains the <strong>superior rectal vein</strong>.</p>\n<p>The rectum has no mesentery attached to it. However, it is surrounded by fat which is enclosed within a <strong>fascial envelope</strong> known as the <strong>mesorectum</strong>, which contains:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superior rectal artery and its branches</li>\n<li>Superior rectal vein and  its tributaries</li>\n<li>Lymphatic vessels and nodes</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">Anterolaterally, branches of the <strong>inferior hypogastric plexus</strong> and <strong>middle rectal vessels</strong> enter the mesorectum covered in a layer of fascia which is known as the <strong>lateral rectal ligament</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Total mesrectal excision</strong> (TME) is a standard procedure done for <strong>rectal cancer</strong>. It includes resection of tumor along with the surrounding fatty tissues, blood vessels and lympatics present in the mesorectum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3024","text":"Identify the wrongly match‌ed pair. Peritoneal fold ‍Contents 1. Hepatogastric​ ligament Right and left ​gastric vessels 2. Greate‍r omentum Right and left ‌gastro-epiploic vessels 3​. Transverse mesocolon Mi‍ddle colic vessels 4. Sig​moid mesocolon Sigmoid ve​ssels and middle rectal v​essels","raw_text":"<p>Identify the wrongly matched pair.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 208px; width: 800px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px; text-align: left;\"><strong> Peritoneal fold</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 598.233px; text-align: left;\"><strong>   Contents</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">1.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px; text-align: left;\"> Hepatogastric ligament</td>\n<td style=\"width: 598.233px; text-align: left;\">   Right and left gastric vessels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">2.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px; text-align: left;\"> Greater omentum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 598.233px; text-align: left;\">   Right and left gastro-epiploic vessels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">3.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px; text-align: left;\"> Transverse mesocolon</td>\n<td style=\"width: 598.233px; text-align: left;\">   Middle colic vessels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">4.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 324px; text-align: left;\"> Sigmoid mesocolon</td>\n<td style=\"width: 598.233px; text-align: left;\">   Sigmoid vessels and middle rectal vessels</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4</strong></p><p>The <strong>sigmoid mesocolon</strong> contains sigmoid vessels and <strong>superior rectal vessels</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3478","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about the structure ‍marked as X in the image?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the structure marked as X in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contains the remnant of ur‌achus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Overlies the obliterated u‌mbilical arteries","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Covers the inferior epigas‌tric vessels","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It passes from external il‌iac artery to the umbilic‍us","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Overlies the obliterated umbilical arteries","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8298360ac9f941d8b10e479e7e78c618.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3f913e131f24494d9cc51218d10e9821x1280x1043.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Overlies the obliterated umbilical arteries</strong></p><p>The structure marked in the image is the <strong>medial umbilical fold</strong>. It overlies the <strong>obliterated umbilical artery</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>peritoneum</strong> of the <strong>lower anterior abdominal wall</strong> is raised into <strong>five folds</strong> that diverge as they descend from the umbilicus. These include:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 185px; width: 581.594px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\"><strong>Umbilical folds</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 389.594px;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\">Median umbilical fold</td>\n<td style=\"width: 389.594px;\">\n<p>Single, present in the midline</p>\n<p>Extends from the umbilicus to the apex of the bladder</p>\n<p>Remnant of the urachus</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\">Medial umbilical folds</td>\n<td style=\"width: 389.594px;\">\n<p>Two in number</p>\n<p>Extends from the internal iliac artery to the umbilicus</p>\n<p>Overlies the obliterated umbilical artery</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174px;\">Lateral umbilical folds</td>\n<td style=\"width: 389.594px;\">\n<p>Two in number</p>\n<p>Overlies the inferior epigastric vessels</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9378","text":"A 58-year-old woman was un‌dergoing cholecystectomy.‍ During the procedure, th​e resident accidentally a​dvanced the scissors alon‍g the superior border of ‌the epiploic foramen caus​ing injury. Which of the ‍following structures is l​ikely to be injured?","raw_text":"A 58-year-old woman was undergoing cholecystectomy. During the procedure, the resident accidentally advanced the scissors along the superior border of the epiploic foramen causing injury. Which of the following structures is likely to be injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right lob‌e of liver","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Quadrate lo‌be of liver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Caudate lo‌be of liver","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fundus of g‌all bladder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Caudate lobe of liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8622b23884d94d68936bea98ab57fb1ex720x569.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Caudate lobe of liver</strong></p><p>The <strong>superior</strong> <strong>border</strong> of the <strong>epiploic foramen </strong>(foramen of Winslow) is formed by the <strong>caudate lobe of the liver. </strong>It is likely to be injured in this case.</p>\n<p>Epiploic foramen is a vertical slit of about 3 cm behind the free right border of the lesser omentum. It <strong>connects</strong> the <strong>lesser sac</strong> to the <strong>greater</strong> <strong>sac</strong>.  </p>\n<p>Clinical note: <strong>Pringle maneuver </strong>(as shown below) is a technique where the free margin of the lesser omentum is compressed to obtain rapid control of <strong>bleeding</strong> from the <strong>hepatic artery </strong>and<strong> portal vein</strong> in liver trauma and surgery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3029","text":"Which of the following duo‌denal recesses contains t‍he inferior mesenteric ve​in in its free margin?","raw_text":"Which of the following duodenal recesses contains the inferior mesenteric vein in its free margin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior duo‌denal recess","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paraduode‌nal recess","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior duo‌denal recess","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mesenterico-parietal reces‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Paraduodenal recess","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2d9f90b097e14bc0aaa1e7bf7e9b1578x1280x1433.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Paraduodenal recess</strong></p><p><strong>Paraduodenal recess</strong> contains the <strong>inferior mesenteric vein </strong>in its right free margin.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The fourth part of the duodenum and the duodenojejunal junction has several folds of peritoneum that give rise to many named recesses. They are probably formed during development due to minor aberrations of duodenal rotation and fixation.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>duodenal recesses</strong> include:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 474px; width: 599px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 123.234px; height: 25px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Recess </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.406px; height: 25px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Location </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.359px; height: 25px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Vascular relations </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54.4531px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 123.234px; height: 54.4531px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Superior duodenal recess </span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.406px; height: 54.4531px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Directly left of D4</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Opposite L2 vertebra</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Behind the duodenojejunal fold</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.359px; height: 54.4531px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Inferior mesenteric vein is directly behind the lateral edge of the recess </span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 123.234px; height: 25px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Inferior duodenal recess </span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.406px; height: 25px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Left of D4 (sometimes behind)</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Opposite L3 vertebra</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Behind the duodenomesocolic fold</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.359px; height: 25px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Passes in front of ascending branch of left colic artery and inferior mesenteric vein </span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 123.234px; height: 25px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Para-duodenal recess </span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.406px; height: 25px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Slightly left and behind D4</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Behind falciform fold of peritoneum </span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.359px; height: 25px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Right free edge contains inferior mesentric vein and ascending branch of left colic artery</span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 123.234px; height: 25px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Retroduodenal recess </span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.406px; height: 25px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Behind D3 and D4</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Has a wide orifice, with duodeno-parietal folds on either side </span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.359px; height: 25px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Lies in front of the abdominal aorta </span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 123.234px; height: 25px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Mesenterico-parietal recess </span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.406px; height: 25px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Below D3 </span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Forms a pocket in the right upper part of mesentery</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.359px; height: 25px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Has a large, left facing orifice behind a fold raised by the superior mesenteric artery </span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 123.234px; height: 25px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Duodenojejunal recess </span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.406px; height: 25px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Below pancreas</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Between DJ junction and root of transverse mesocolon </span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.359px; height: 25px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Left of the abdominal aorta </span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0563","text":"Where would you expect any‌ intra-abdominal fluid to‍ get collected in a patie​nt lying supine?","raw_text":"Where would you expect any intra-abdominal fluid to get collected in a patient lying supine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right infra‌colic space","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left infra‌colic space","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right subhe‌patic space","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Left paradu‌odenal space","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Right subhepatic space","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/185f3d87511f4ddcb12c463893bf607ax600x790.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Right subhepatic space</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The <strong>most dependent</strong> part of the peritoneal cavity in the <strong>supine position</strong> is the <strong>right</strong> <strong>subhepatic space</strong> (hepatorenal pouch of Morison).<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> The <strong>intra-abdominal fluid</strong> gets <strong>collected</strong> in this space in the supine position.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It lies between the inferior surface of the right lobe of the liver and the upper pole of the right kidney.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The <strong>boundaries of the hepatorenal pouch</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Superiorly -<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> i</span>nferior layer of the coronary ligament.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Inferomedially - hepatic flexure, transverse mesocolon, second part of the duodenum, and part of the head of the pancreas.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Laterally - right lateral abdominal wall</li>\n<li>Posteriorly - anterior surface of the upper pole of the right kidney</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: The most dependent part of the peritoneal cavity in erect posture:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In males - rectovesical pouch</li>\n<li>In females - rectouterine pouch.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"r9B24IxW","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"48_esi_and_factories_act","title":"48.Esi And Factories Act","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA0269","text":"What is the definition of ​ergonomics?","raw_text":"What is the definition of ergonomics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fitting the job to the wor​ker","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Study of hu​man behavior","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Process of improving indus​trial safety standards","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prevention of health probl​ems of factory workers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fitting the job to the worker","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fitting the job to the worker</strong></p><p><strong>Ergonomics </strong>is defined as <strong>fitting</strong> <strong>the job to the worker</strong>. The aim of ergonomics is to achieve the best mutual adjustment of man and his work, for the improvement of human efficacy and well-being.</p>\n<p><strong>Emporiatrics</strong>, also called <strong>travel medicine</strong>,<strong> </strong>deals with the prevention and management of health problems of <strong>international travelers</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0255","text":"Who is the Chairman of ESI​ corporation in India?","raw_text":"Who is the Chairman of ESI corporation in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The Prime​ Minister","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Union Minister of Health &amp;​amp; Family Welfare","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Union Minist​er of Labour","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Union Minister of Human Re​sources Development","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Union Minister of Labour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Union Minister of Labour</strong></p><p>The <strong>Union Minister of Labour</strong> is the <strong>Chairman</strong> of the Employees' State Insurance Corporation (<strong>ESIC</strong>) in India.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Secretary</strong> to the <strong>Ministry of Labour</strong> is the <strong>Vice-Chairman</strong> of ESIC.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0256","text":"You are working as a medic​al officer in a factory. ​You are expected to notif‌y all of the following oc‌cupational diseases, unde‌r the Factories Act, exce‌pt:","raw_text":"You are working as a medical officer in a factory. You are expected to notify all of the following occupational diseases, under the Factories Act, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Occupationa​l dermatitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bagas​sossis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Noise induced hearing loss​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Byssi​nosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bagassossis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bagassossis</strong></p><p>Bagassosis is not a notifiable disease, under the Factories Act.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0841","text":"According to the Factories​ Act 1948, what is the ma​ximum work hours per week‌ including overtime work ‌hours?","raw_text":"According to the Factories Act 1948, what is the maximum work hours per week including overtime work hours?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"48 h​ours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"72 h​ours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"60 h​ours","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"66 h​ours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 60 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 60 hours</strong></p><p>Maximum work hours per week including <strong>overtime</strong> work hours is<strong> 60 hours.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4404","text":"Which of the following is ​true about Factories Act?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about Factories Act?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Children under 14 years of​ age must not be employed​ in factories","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A child of 16 years can be​ employed only between 6 ​AM and 8 PM","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The maximum working hours ​for an adult is 72 hours ​per week","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A child of 16 years can wo​rk till 5 hours per day","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Children under 14 years of age must not be employed in factories","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Children under 14 years of age must not be employed in factories</strong></p><p>Factories act <strong>prohibits </strong>children of<strong> &lt;14 years </strong>from being employed.</p>\n<p>Children between<strong> 15–18 years</strong> can be employed only in certain industries and work only between<strong> 6 AM–7 PM. </strong>They should <strong>not</strong> be made to work for more than <strong>four and a half hours.</strong></p>\n<p>The maximum working hours for an adult industrial worker should be<strong> not more than 48 hours per week</strong>, not exceeding <strong>9 hours a day</strong>; if <strong>overtime</strong> is paid, then it should be not more than <strong>60 hrs per week</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0257","text":"What is the maximum daily ​working hours for an adol​escent employee, accordin‌g to the Factories Act of‌ 1948?","raw_text":"What is the maximum daily working hours for an adolescent employee, according to the Factories Act of 1948?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 h​ours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"8 h​ours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6 and a h​alf hours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 and a h​alf hours","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4 and a half hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4 and a half hours</strong></p><p>According to the Factories Act of 1948, adolescents should <strong>not </strong>be made to work for <strong>more</strong> <strong>than four and a half hours </strong>a day.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0259","text":"According to Factories Act​ of 1948, a welfare offic​er has to be employed if ‌the number of employees i‌s:","raw_text":"According to Factories Act of 1948, a welfare officer has to be employed if the number of employees is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt;​ 1000","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt;​ 500","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt​;250","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;​ 750","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. > 500","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. &gt; 500</strong></p><p>A <strong>welfare officer</strong> has to be employed if the number of employees is<strong> &gt; 500 </strong>according to the Factories Act of 1948.</p>\n<p>According to the Factories Act, safety and welfare measures include the provision of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>&gt; 1000 employees – Safety Officer</li>\n<li>&gt; 500 employees – Welfare Officer</li>\n<li>&gt; 250 employees – Canteen</li>\n<li>&gt; 50 employees - Crèche</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0260","text":"Which of the following est​ablishments is covered by​ the Employees' State Ins‌urance Act?","raw_text":"Which of the following establishments is covered by the Employees' State Insurance Act?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Def​ence","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Private medical and educat​ional institutions","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mi​nes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rail​ways","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Private medical and educational institutions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Private medical and educational institutions</strong></p><p>Currently<strong>, private medical and </strong><strong>educational institutes </strong>employing &gt;20 people are <strong>covered </strong>under the <strong>ESI</strong> Act.</p>\n<p>The ESI Act is applicable to all industries <strong>except:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Defence</li>\n<li>Railway</li>\n<li>Mines</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0262","text":"What percentage of the tot​al wage bill is contribut​ed by the employer under ‌the ESI Act?","raw_text":"What percentage of the total wage bill is contributed by the employer under the ESI Act?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3.​75%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3.​25%","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"4.​75%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.​75%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3.25%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3.25%</strong></p><p>The <strong>employer</strong> contributes <strong>3.25%</strong> of the total wage bill under the ESI Act.</p>\n<p>The government has revised the rates of ESI contribution by employers (from 4.75% to 3.25%) and employees (from 1.75% to 0.75%) in 2019. </p>\n<p>The distribution of the financial contribution is given below: </p>\n<ul>\n<li> <strong>Employee:</strong> <strong>0.75%</strong> of the total wage bill</li>\n<li> <strong>Employer:</strong> 3.25% of the total wage bill</li>\n<li> Medical benefit: <strong>1/8th</strong> by the <strong>state </strong>government and <strong>7/8th</strong> by the <strong>central </strong>government</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0263","text":"Under the ESI Act, which o​f the following is not gi​ven as a cash benefit?","raw_text":"Under the ESI Act, which of the following is not given as a cash benefit?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sickness​ benefit","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medical​ benefit","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Maternit​y benefit","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dependant​’s benefit","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Medical benefit","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Medical benefit</strong></p><p>Under the ESI Act, medical benefit is not given as a cash benefit. It is provided in the form of <strong>consultations</strong>, <strong>investigations</strong> and <strong>treatment</strong> by means of the ESI hospitals or ESI dispensaries.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0266","text":"What is enhanced sickness ​benefit?","raw_text":"What is enhanced sickness benefit?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Benefit given at 70% of th​e wage for 91 days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Benefit given at 80% of th​e wage for 120 days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Benefit given for a durati​on of 2 years for 34 spec​ified diseases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Benefit given at full wage​s for undergoing steriliz​ation procedure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Benefit given at full wages for undergoing sterilization procedure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Benefit given at full wages for undergoing sterilization procedure</strong></p><p><strong>Enhanced sickness benefit</strong> under the ESI Act is the benefit given at <strong>full</strong> <strong>wages</strong> for undergoing <strong>sterilization</strong> procedures.</p>\n<p>Under the ESI Act, <strong>sickness benefit</strong> is given at <strong>70% </strong>of the<strong> daily wage</strong> for a duration of <strong>91 days </strong>in one year<strong>. </strong>There are two special types of sickness benefits given:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li><strong>Enhanced </strong>sickness benefit: Benefit given at <strong>full wages</strong> for <strong>sterilization</strong> procedures; for a duration of<strong> 7 days post-vasectomy</strong> and <strong>14 days post-tubectomy.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Extended </strong>sickness benefit: Benefit given for a duration of <strong>2 years</strong> for 34 specified diseases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0267","text":"Which of the following is ​not a benefit given under​ the ESI Act?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a benefit given under the ESI Act?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dependanc​e benefit","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Welfare​ benefit","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rehabilitat​ion benefit","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Funeral​ benefit","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Welfare benefit","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Welfare benefit</strong></p><p>Welfare benefit is <strong>not</strong> given under the ESI act.</p>\n<p>The following benefits are given under the ESI act:</p>\n<ul>\n<li data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"5\" data-listid=\"50\">Medical benefit</li>\n<li data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"6\" data-listid=\"50\">Sickness benefit</li>\n<li data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"9\" data-listid=\"52\">Maternity benefit</li>\n<li data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"10\" data-listid=\"52\">Dependance benefit</li>\n<li data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"11\" data-listid=\"52\">Disablement benefit</li>\n<li data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"11\" data-listid=\"52\">Funeral benefit</li>\n<li data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"11\" data-listid=\"52\">Rehabilitation benefit</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0840","text":"An employee diagnosed with​ TB gets extended sicknes​s benefit for:","raw_text":"An employee diagnosed with TB gets extended sickness benefit for:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 y​ear","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 y​ears","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3 y​ears","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 y​ears","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 years</strong></p><p><strong>Extended sickness benefit</strong> is given for a period of <strong>2 years</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2744","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true about ESI​ Act?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about ESI Act?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Funeral benefit is upto Rs​.20,000.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"State government covers 1/​8th and ESI corporation c​overs 7/8th of the cost o‌f medical care","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Maternity benefit is payab​le at full wages for upto​ 24 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It covers all employees ge​tting income up to Rs.18,​000/- per month","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. State government covers 1/8th and ESI corporation covers 7/8th of the cost of medical care","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. State government covers 1/8th and ESI corporation covers 7/8th of the cost of medical care</strong></p><p>The <strong>state</strong> government's share of expenditure on medical care is <strong>1/8th</strong>, and the <strong>ESI</strong> corporation's share of expenditure on medical care is <strong>7/8th</strong> of the total cost.</p>\n<p>Few important points about the ESI Act:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It covers all employees getting income <strong>up to Rs.21,000/-</strong> per month.</li>\n<li>The employer contributes 3.25% of the total wage bill.</li>\n<li>An employee contributes 0.75% of wages.</li>\n<li>State and Central government share medical expenditure in the ratio 1:7.</li>\n<li>Medical benefit: Full medical care</li>\n<li>Sickness benefit: 70% of the average daily wages and is payable for 91 days.</li>\n<li>Extended sickness benefit: Payable for 2 years for a set of 34 diseases.</li>\n<li>Enhanced sickness benefit: Full average daily wage for a duration up to 7 days in case of vasectomy and up to 14 days in case of tubectomy.</li>\n<li><strong>Maternity</strong> benefit: Full average daily wage for a duration up to <strong>26 weeks</strong> (confinement) or 6 weeks (miscarriage/MTP) or 4 weeks (sickness arising out of pregnancy).</li>\n<li><strong>Funeral</strong> expenses: Cash not exceeding <strong>Rs.15,000</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4869","text":"Who is the beneficiary und​er the Rajiv Gandhi Shram​ik Kalyan Yojana scheme o‌f ESI?","raw_text":"Who is the beneficiary under the Rajiv Gandhi Shramik Kalyan Yojana scheme of ESI?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"An insured employee who re​tired","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"An insured person rendered​ unemployed, involuntaril​y","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"An insured employee who is​ pregnant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Employees of a new establi​shment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. An insured person rendered unemployed, involuntarily","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. An insured person rendered unemployed, involuntarily</strong></p><p><strong>Rajiv Gandhi Shramik Kalyan Yojana</strong> (RGSKY) is a scheme of ESI that benefits an insured person rendered <strong>unemployed, involuntarily.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Unemployment allowance</strong> under RGSKY is given when an insured person is going out of employment involuntarily, on account of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Closure</strong> of a factory or establishment </li>\n<li><strong>Retrenchment</strong> (laying off employees due to economic difficulty)</li>\n<li><strong>Permanent disability</strong> due to <strong>nonemployment</strong> injury</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Allowance and eligibility criteria of Rajiv Gandhi Shramik Kalyan Yojana (updated in 2019):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The allowance is given at the rate of <strong>50% </strong>of the<strong> daily wage</strong> for a maximum of <strong>2 years.</strong></li>\n<li>To qualify for the scheme the worker must have worked in that setup for more than <strong>3 years</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Disability due to<strong> employment injury </strong>is covered under disablement benefits of <strong>ESI Act, 1948.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Temporary disability: 90% </strong>of wages until disablement lasts.</li>\n<li><strong>Permanent disability: 90% </strong>of wages as a <strong>life pension </strong>on the basis of loss of earning capacity.</li>\n<li><strong>Dependants benefit:</strong> <strong>90% </strong>of wage as monthly payment to the dependants of the deceased person, in cases where death occurs due to employment injury or occupational hazards.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Dly9ZC5W","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"48_neoplasms_of_the_lung","title":"48.Neoplasms Of The Lung","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB3512","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding lung canc​er?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding lung cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the most common canc​er in Indian men","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tobacco smoking is the mos​t common cause","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenocarcinoma is the most​ common histologic type","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is the most common caus​e of cancer death","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is the most common cancer in Indian men","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is the most common cancer in Indian men</strong></p><p>The most common cancer seen in <strong>Indian men</strong> is <strong>lip</strong> and <strong>oral cavity cancer</strong>, not lung cancer.</p>\n<p>Most common cancer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In men globally- <strong>Lung cancer</strong></li>\n<li>In Indian men- Lip and oral cavity cancer</li>\n<li>In women globally- Breast cancer</li>\n<li>In Indian women- Breast cancer</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tobacco smoking</strong> is a primary cause of the development of lung cancer. It increases <strong>10 fold</strong> greater risk of developing lung cancer compared to those who have never smoked.</p>\n<p>It is the most common cause of cancer death.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3500","text":"What is the most common ty​pe of lung cancer in ligh​t smokers?","raw_text":"What is the most common type of lung cancer in light smokers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous ce​ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adenoca​rcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Small cell​ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Large cell​ carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adenocarcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adenocarcinoma</strong></p><p>The most common lung cancer in <strong>light smokers</strong> (&lt;10 pack-year history) is <strong>adenocarcinoma</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>WHO classification of lung carcinomas:</strong></p>\n<p>The WHO classification system divides epithelial lung cancers into four major cell types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Small-cell lung cancer (SCLC)</li>\n<li>Non-small-cell lung carcinomas (NSCLCs)\n<ul>\n<li>Adenocarcinoma</li>\n<li>Squamous cell carcinoma</li>\n<li>Large-cell carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Adenocarcinoma is most commonly seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lifetime non-smokers</li>\n<li>Light smokers (&lt;10 pack-years history)</li>\n<li>Women</li>\n<li>Younger adults (&lt;60 years)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In <strong>smokers</strong>, the most common types are <strong>small-cell carcinoma</strong> and <strong>squamous cell carcinomas</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3514","text":"A 60-year-old man with a 3​5 pack-year history of sm​oking presented for a rou​tine check-up. His brothe‌r was diagnosed with lung​ cancer at a similar age.‍ Which of the following w‌ould be preferred to scre​en him for lung cancer?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man with a 35 pack-year history of smoking presented for a routine check-up. His brother was diagnosed with lung cancer at a similar age. Which of the following would be preferred to screen him for lung cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chest​ X-Ray","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Low d​ose CT","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"High reso​lution CT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CE​CT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Low dose CT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Low dose CT</strong></p><p><strong>Low-dose CT/ thin-slice spiral chest CT </strong>would be preferred to screen him for lung cancer. He belongs to the <strong>high-risk</strong> population to develop lung cancer.</p>\n<p>The high-risk population is individuals whose <strong>age is ≥60 years</strong> and who have a <strong>smoking</strong> history of <strong>≥10 pack-years</strong>.</p>\n<p>Low dose CT scans are shown to detect more lung nodules and cancers than the standard CXR in selected high-risk populations. Also, up to 85% of cancers thus detected are classified as <strong>stage I</strong>. Early staged cancers are considered potentially curable with surgical resection.</p>\n<p>Here, the aim is to reduce medical radiation exposure as much as possible, low dose CT is effective in screening for lung cancer, and is thus preferred.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0626","text":"Which of the following is ​false about hypertrophic ​osteoarthropathy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about hypertrophic osteoarthropathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Platelets have a role in t​he development of HOA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary HOA disappears in ​adulthood","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HOA is commonly associated​ with lung malignancies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Skin changes are more comm​on in secondary HOA","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Skin changes are more common in secondary HOA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Skin changes are more common in secondary HOA</strong></p><p><strong>Skin changes</strong> are more common in<strong> primary HOA</strong>, not secondary HOA. </p>\n<p>Hypertrophic osteoarthropathy is a condition characterized by the clubbing of the digits. In advanced stages, it is characterized by the periosteal new bone formation and synovial effusion. The exact mechanism of its development is unclear. However, <strong>platelets</strong> are said to have a role in the development of HOA via <strong>platelet-derived growth factor</strong>.</p>\n<p>There are two types of HOA:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 410px;\" width=\"454\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 35.0781px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 218.734px; height: 35.0781px;\"><strong>          Primary HOA</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 219.266px; height: 35.0781px;\"><strong>          Secondary HOA</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 206px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 218.734px; height: 206px;\">Inherited autosomal dominant condition</td>\n<td style=\"width: 219.266px; height: 206px;\">\n<p>Acquired condition, secondary to</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lung neoplasms</strong></li>\n<li>Mesothelioma</li>\n<li>Suppurative lung infections</li>\n<li>Congenital heart disease</li>\n<li>Thyroid acropachy</li>\n<li>Inflammatory bowel disease</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 218.734px; height: 64px;\"><strong>Skin changes</strong> like thickening, seborrhea, excessive sweating are commonly seen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 219.266px; height: 64px;\">Skin changes are unusual</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 59px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 218.734px; height: 59px;\">The symptoms <strong>disappear</strong> in <strong>adulthood</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 219.266px; height: 59px;\">The symptoms persist till the underlying cause is treated</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3508","text":"A middle-aged non-smoker p​resented with a dry persi​stent cough and hemoptysi​s. A CT scan showed a mas‌s in the periphery of his​ right lung. A biopsy was‍ taken, cells showed an a‌cinar pattern with mucin ​formation. The cells were​ positive for thyroid tra​nscription factor 1 and n​apsin A. What other featu‍re is most likely to be s‌een in this patient?","raw_text":"A middle-aged non-smoker presented with a dry persistent cough and hemoptysis. A CT scan showed a mass in the periphery of his right lung. A biopsy was taken, cells showed an acinar pattern with mucin formation. The cells were positive for thyroid transcription factor 1 and napsin A. What other feature is most likely to be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypertrophic osteoarthropa​thy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lambert-Eaton myasthenic s​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperc​alcemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"SI​ADH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypertrophic osteoarthropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypertrophic osteoarthropathy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the biopsy findings is suggestive of <strong>lung</strong> <strong>adenocarcinoma</strong>. It is most commonly associated with <strong>hypertrophic osteoarthropathy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Adenocarcinomas are mainly seen in the <strong>periphery</strong> of the lung. They grow in various patterns like<strong> </strong><strong>acinar</strong>, lepidic, papillary, micropapillary, and solid with mucin formation. Cells are positive for markers like <strong>thyroid transcription factor 1</strong> and<strong> napsin A</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Hypercalcemia</strong> due to ectopic production of PTH-related peptide is seen in <strong>squamous cell carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Options B and D: However, <strong>SIADH</strong>, Cushing’s syndrome, <strong>Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome</strong> are associated with <strong>small-cell lung carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3503","text":"A female patient comes wit​h complaints of unintenti​onal weight loss and righ​t shoulder pain for the p‌ast 6 months. She reports​ that the pain radiates t‍o her arm and is not reli‌eved by NSAIDs. Chest X-r​ay is given below. Which ​nerve roots are likely to​ be compressed?","raw_text":"A female patient comes with complaints of unintentional weight loss and right shoulder pain for the past 6 months. She reports that the pain radiates to her arm and is not relieved by NSAIDs. Chest X-ray is given below. Which nerve roots are likely to be compressed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C5,​ C6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C8, T​1, T2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"C6.​ C7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C3, C​4, C5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. C8, T1, T2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/92fe9f5eea6549d48981cc92ffe0a458.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. C8, T1, T2</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the X-ray findings is suggestive of <strong>Pancoast syndrome</strong> / <strong>superior sulcus tumor</strong>. This affects the <strong>C8, T1, and T2</strong> nerve roots.</p>\n<p>It presents with pain that radiates to the ulnar distribution of the arm, often with the destruction of the <strong>1st</strong> and <strong>2nd ribs</strong>. Patients with the central or endobronchial growth of the primary tumor may present with a cough, hemoptysis, wheeze, stridor, dyspnea, or post-obstructive pneumonitis.</p>\n<p>Regional spread can cause</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tracheal obstruction</li>\n<li>Esophageal compression with dysphagia</li>\n<li>Recurrent laryngeal paralysis with hoarseness</li>\n<li>Phrenic nerve palsy with the elevation of the hemidiaphragm and dyspnea</li>\n<li>Sympathetic nerve paralysis with Horner’s syndrome (enophthalmos, ptosis, miosis, and anhydrosis)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3509","text":"A COPD patient presented t​o the emergency with an a​cute exacerbation. On eva​luation, a chest X-ray re‌vealed a solitary pulmona​ry nodule. Which of the f‍ollowing patterns of calc‌ification would suggest a​ likely malignancy in thi​s patient?","raw_text":"A COPD patient presented to the emergency with an acute exacerbation. On evaluation, a chest X-ray revealed a solitary pulmonary nodule. Which of the following patterns of calcification would suggest a likely malignancy in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A dense ce​ntral nidus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Popcorn ca​lcification","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Multiple pu​nctate foci","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Asymmetric c​alcification","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Asymmetric calcification","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Asymmetric calcification</strong></p><p><strong>Asymmetric calcification</strong> is suggestive of a <strong>malignant</strong> lesion.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 297px;\" width=\"422\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">      <strong> Benign lesion</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">        <strong>Malignant lesion</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">Lack of growth over a period of &gt;2 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">The growth of the lesion is evident in comparison with an old X-ray</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">\n<p>Patterns of calcification:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dense central nidus</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Multiple punctate foci</strong></li>\n<li>Bull's eye granuloma</li>\n<li><strong>Popcorn ball hamartomas</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">\n<p>Patterns of calcification:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Asymmetric </strong></li>\n<li>Lack of calcification</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">Associated chest symptoms are uncommon</td>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">chest symptoms like cough, hemoptysis are common</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">Complications are uncommon</td>\n<td style=\"width: 203px;\">Complications like atelectasis, pneumonitis, and effusion are common</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3502","text":"What is the treatment of c​hoice in patients with ad​enocarcinoma of the lung ​with EGFR mutation?","raw_text":"What is the treatment of choice in patients with adenocarcinoma of the lung with EGFR mutation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bevac​izumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pacli​taxel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Imat​inib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gefi​tinib","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gefitinib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gefitinib</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 834\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Adenocarcinomas with<strong> EGFR mutation</strong> are exquisitely sensitive to<strong> tyrosine kinase inhibitors </strong>of the EGFR signal pathway such as <strong>gefitinib</strong> and <strong>erlotinib</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Option A: Bevacizumab is a monoclonal antibody against VEGF.</p>\n<p>Option B: Paclitaxel- a taxane, is a plant alkaloid and chemotherapeutic agent.</p>\n<p>Option C: Imatinib is a tyrosine kinase inhibitor effective in the treatment of CML and GIST.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3507","text":"A 55-year-old man presents​ with persistent cough an​d hemoptysis. The intern ​notices that he has moon-‌like facies and purple st​riae on his abdomen. On e‍valuation, a chest X-ray ‌revealed a mass in his ri​ght lung. Which of the fo​llowing type of lung carc​inoma could be the cause ​of this patient's feature‍s?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man presents with persistent cough and hemoptysis. The intern notices that he has moon-like facies and purple striae on his abdomen. On evaluation, a chest X-ray revealed a mass in his right lung. Which of the following type of lung carcinoma could be the cause of this patient's features?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenoca​rcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchoalveo​lar carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Small cell​ carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Squamous ce​ll carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Small cell carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Small cell carcinoma</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario the patient has features of <strong>Cushing's syndrome,</strong> secondary to<strong> small-cell lung cancer</strong>. This occurs due to <strong>ectopic ACTH production.</strong> It can also seen in pulmonary carcinoids.</p>\n<p>Most effective management is treating underlying SCLC or carcinoid.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3504","text":"An elderly man was rushed ​to the ED with confusion ​and multiple episodes of ​vomiting. His wife report‌ed that he had increased ​frequency of urination an‍d constipation for the pa‌st 3 days. He is a known ​case of lung cancer. ECG ​showed a shortened QT int​erval. What could be the ​primary cause of his symp‍toms?","raw_text":"An elderly man was rushed to the ED with confusion and multiple episodes of vomiting. His wife reported that he had increased frequency of urination and constipation for the past 3 days. He is a known case of lung cancer. ECG showed a shortened QT interval. What could be the primary cause of his symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ectopic secr​etion of ACTH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Syndrome of inappropriate ​antidiuretic hormone secr​etion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ectopic secretion of PTH r​elated peptide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tumor lysi​s syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ectopic secretion of PTH related peptide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ectopic secretion of PTH related peptide</strong></p><p>The primary cause of his symptoms is <strong>ectopic secretion</strong> of <strong>PTH related peptide</strong>. <strong>Lung cancers </strong>are associated with <strong>several</strong> <strong>paraneoplastic syndromes. Squamous cell lung cancer </strong>is associated with <strong>hypercalcemia</strong> due to ectopic PTH related peptide. Hypercalcemia may present as <strong>vomiting</strong>, abdominal pain, <strong>constipation, increased frequency of urination</strong>, kidney stones, bone pain and <strong>shortened QT interval</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3515","text":"A 62-year-old woman presen​ts with vomiting, diarrhe​a, and decreased urine ou​tput since morning. On th‌e previous day, she recei​ved chemotherapy for her ‍stage 4 lung cancer. On e‌xamination, she is drowsy​, HR-110/min and RR-30/mi​n. All of the following b​iochemical findings can b​e seen in this patient ex‍cept:","raw_text":"A 62-year-old woman presents with vomiting, diarrhea, and decreased urine output since morning. On the previous day, she received chemotherapy for her stage 4 lung cancer. On examination, she is drowsy, HR-110/min and RR-30/min.  All of the following biochemical findings can be seen in this patient except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperu​ricemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperc​alcemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperk​alemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperpho​sphatemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypercalcemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypercalcemia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> tumor lysis syndrome</strong> (TLS). It is associated with <strong>hypocalcemia</strong>, not hypercalcemia.</p>\n<p>TLS is caused by the <strong>destruction</strong> of a large number of rapidly proliferating neoplastic cells. It is usually seen within <strong>1-5 days</strong> of receiving chemotherapy. It has also been observed with the administration of glucocorticoids, letrozole, tamoxifen, rituximab and gemtuzumab. Rarely, spontaneous necrosis of malignancies causes TLS.</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nausea, <strong>vomiting</strong>, <strong>diarrhea</strong>, lethargy</li>\n<li>Pruritus, renal colicky pain, <strong>oliguria</strong></li>\n<li>Muscle cramps, carpopedal spasm, tetany</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Biochemical findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperuricemia</li>\n<li>Hyperkalemia</li>\n<li><strong>Hypocalcemia</strong></li>\n<li>Hyperphosphatemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Due to these biochemical changes, there is an increased risk of arrhythmias, acidosis, and acute kidney injury.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3517","text":"Which of the following is ​not used in the managemen​t of patients with tumor ​lysis syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not used in the management of patients with tumor lysis syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium bi​carbonate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rasbu​ricase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aggressive​ hydration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Febux​ostat","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sodium bicarbonate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sodium bicarbonate</strong></p><p><strong>Sodium bicarbonate</strong> is not recommended in the treatment of tumor lysis syndrome (TLS).</p>\n<p>Urinary alkalinization by sodium bicarbonate decreases the solubility of xanthine, hypoxanthine, and calcium phosphate. This potentially increases the risk of <strong>intratubular crystallization</strong>.</p>\n<p>Management of TLS includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aggressive hydration</strong></li>\n<li>Allopurinol</li>\n<li><strong>Rasburicase</strong> - recombinant urate oxidase</li>\n<li><strong>Febuxostat</strong> - non-purine selective xanthine oxidase inhibitor</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0625","text":"Which of the following pat​ients with non-small lung​ carcinoma is a suitable ​candidate to undergo a po‌tentially curative surgic​al resection:","raw_text":"Which of the following patients with non-small lung carcinoma is  a suitable candidate to undergo a potentially curative surgical resection:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"60-year-old smoker with a ​tumor within 2cm of carin​a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"45-year-old woman with mal​ignant pleural effusion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"55-year-old non-smoker wit​h tumor involving the mai​n pulmonary artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"57-year old light smoker w​ith ipsilateral mediastin​al node metastases","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 57-year old light smoker with ipsilateral mediastinal node metastases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 57-year old light smoker with ipsilateral mediastinal node metastases</strong></p><p>Patients with ipsilateral mediastinal node metastases are suitable candidates for curative resection.</p>\n<p>In patients with NSCLC, the following are contraindications to potentially curative resection:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Extra-thoracic metastases</li>\n<li>Vocal cord involvement</li>\n<li>Superior vena cava syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>Involvement of the main pulmonary artery</strong></li>\n<li>Cardiac tamponade</li>\n<li>Phrenic nerve paralysis</li>\n<li><strong>Malignant pleural effusion</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tumor within 2 cm of the carina</strong></li>\n<li>Metastasis to the contralateral lung</li>\n<li>Metastases to supraclavicular lymph nodes</li>\n<li><strong>Contralateral mediastinal node metastases</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"cIVeSGDD","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"48_male_genital_tract","title":"48.Male Genital Tract","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"MC6678","text":"A young sexually active ma‌n presented with multiple‍ polypoid growths over hi‍s penis. Histopathologica‌l image of an excised les​ion is as shown below. Wh‌at is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young sexually active man presented with multiple polypoid growths over his penis. Histopathological image of an excised lesion is as shown below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Condylo‌ma lata","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bowen's‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Condyloma‌ acuminata","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Verrucous‌ carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Condyloma acuminata","question_images":["https://daily-rounds-staging.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/29db49e6899e4700b44689cdfd9f086f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2ec0ed069df94ec7bac482035dc6be0bx1280x723.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Condyloma acuminata</strong></p><p>The given histopathological image showing <strong>papillary </strong>(villous)<strong> architecture</strong> with <strong>acanthosis, hyperkeratosis</strong> and <strong>koilocytosis</strong> of squamous epithelium, are characteristic findings of <strong>condyloma acuminatum.</strong></p>\n<p>Koilocytes have a prominent cell membrane, hyperchromatic resinoid nucleus, and perinuclear halo.</p>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 974\">Grossly, they consist of single or multiple sessile or pedunculated, red <strong>papillary excrescences </strong>on<strong> </strong>the<strong> </strong>coronal sulcus, and the inner surface of the prepuce.</div>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Condyloma lata</strong> is a maculopapular/nodular lesion seen in secondary syphilis.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Bowen's disease</strong> is carcinoma in situ of the penis characterized by the hyperproliferative epidermis and dysplastic epithelium.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Verrucous carcinoma /</strong> very well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma, shows hyperkeratosis, acanthosis, and keratin pearls.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6156","text":"All of the following are t‌rue regarding Bowen's dis‍ease except:","raw_text":"All of the following are true regarding Bowen's disease except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strong association with HP‌V 16 infection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Marked dysplasia and hyper‌proliferative epidermis a‍re seen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intact basem‌ent membrane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Virtually never develops i‌nto an invasive carcinoma‍","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Virtually never develops into an invasive carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0fc5e73b0fb44c7d801085c1c33f2652x1280x1142.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/60715a57aeb649bfb4f06a7c4700ada1x1280x1142.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Virtually never develops into an invasive carcinoma</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Bowen's disease</strong> starts as a premalignant lesion and transforms into <strong>infiltrating squamous cell carcinoma </strong>in approximately <strong>10% </strong>of <strong>patients, </strong>usually over a span of many years.</p>\n<p>Bowen's disease occurs in the genital region of both men and women, usually in those older than age 35 years. These lesions have a strong association with infection by high-risk human papillomavirus (<strong>HPV</strong>), most commonly type <strong>16.</strong></p>\n<p>Grossly, it appears as a <strong>solitary,</strong> thickened, gray-white, and opaque plaque.</p>\n<p>The below histological image of Bowen's disease shows dysplastic cells with large hyperchromatic nuclei, koilocytic atypia and an intact basement membrane.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Premalignant lesions</strong> of invasive penile SCC:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Erythroplasia of Queyrat</li>\n<li>Condyloma acuminatum</li>\n<li>Bowen's disease</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Bowenoid papulosis</strong> occurs in young, sexually active adults. It presents as multiple reddish-brown papular lesions and it is histologically indistinguishable from Bowen's disease, but virtually never develops into invasive carcinoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3449","text":"The biopsy of a resected i‌nguinal mass from a patie‍nt shows marked fibrotic ‍changes with prominent Le‌ydig cells and hypoplasti​c Sertoli cells. What is ‌the most likely diagnosis​?","raw_text":"The biopsy of a resected inguinal mass from a patient shows marked fibrotic changes with prominent Leydig cells and hypoplastic Sertoli cells. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peyronie‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cryptor‌chidism","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bowen ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Torsion‌ testis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cryptorchidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cryptorchidism</strong></p><p>The given histopathological features are strongly suggestive of <strong>cryptorchidism.</strong></p>\n<p>Cryptorchidism is histopathologically characterized by the following: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Testicular <strong>atrophy:</strong> thickening of the basement membrane, arrested germ cell development, marked hyalinization</li>\n<li>Tubules showing <strong>Sertoli cells but no spermatogenesis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Increase</strong> in the interstitial <strong>Leydig cells.</strong> </li>\n</ol>\n<p>Cryptorchidism is the <strong>failure</strong> of the descent of the intra-abdominal testes into the <strong>scrotal sac.</strong>  It is the most common congenital abnormality of the genitourinary tract. The testes can<strong> arrest </strong>anywhere during descent, and the most common site is the<strong> inguinal region.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Phases</strong> of testicular descent:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Transabdominal</strong> phase: Testes come to lie in the lower abdomen or pelvis. This phase of descent is regulated by<strong> Mullerian-inhibiting substances.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Inguinoscrotal</strong> phase: Testes descend along the inguinal canal and reach the scrotal sac. This phase of the descent is regulated by <strong>androgen-induced calcitonin gene-related peptide</strong> from the genitofemoral nerve.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The exact pathogenesis remains unknown, however, deficiency in gonadotropins and Mullerian-inhibiting substance (MIS) and increased expression of estradiol in the placenta are thought to play a role. It is associated with testicular atrophy and dysfunction, which result in sterility. Cryptorchid testes carry a 3–5-fold higher risk for testicular cancer (usually seminoma).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6157","text":"Testicular atrophy is not ‌seen in which of the foll‍owing conditions?","raw_text":"Testicular atrophy is not seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 25-year-old man with kar‌yotype XXY","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 45-year-old man with liv‌er cirrhosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"a 28-year-old man with pai‌nful orchitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"a 30-year-old man with tor‌sion testis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. a 30-year-old man with torsion testis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/11939be07f6f4a85b7ddec2b8754b446x1280x1142.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aef78cb5834841fa90e22a67d1e2ff5ex1280x1142.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. a 30-year-old man with torsion testis</strong></p><p><strong>Torsion testis</strong> does not cause testicular atrophy. It may cause intense <strong>congestion</strong> to widespread <strong>hemorrhage</strong> to testicular <strong>infarction</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes</strong> of testicular <strong>atrophy</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Progressive atherosclerotic narrowing of the blood supply in old age</li>\n<li>End-stage of an <strong>inflammatory orchitis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Cryptorchidism - </strong>associated with progressive tubular atrophy and sterility</li>\n<li>Hypopituitarism</li>\n<li>Generalized malnutrition or cachexia</li>\n<li>Irradiation</li>\n<li>Prolonged administration of antiandrogens (treatment for advanced carcinoma of the prostate)</li>\n<li>Liver <strong>cirrhosis - </strong>may cause testicular atrophy as a result of increased levels of circulating endogenous estrogens not metabolized by the diseased liver</li>\n<li>Exhaustion atrophy, which may follow persistent stimulation by high levels of the follicle-stimulating pituitary hormone</li>\n<li>Occasionally occurs as a primary failure of genetic origins, such as in <strong>Klinefelter</strong> (<strong>XXY</strong>) syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The histological image below of testicular atrophy shows <strong>small tubules</strong> with few or <strong>no germ cells</strong>, <strong>thickening of the basement membrane</strong> of the spermatic tubules, and prominent <strong>Leydig cells.</strong> The near-normal seminiferous tubules are seen side-by-side with atrophic seminiferous tubules.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6158","text":"A 30-year-old man undergoe‌s testicular biopsy as pa‍rt of infertility work-up‍. Sperm count shows oligo‌spermia. Microscopy shows​ only Sertoli cells in th‌e seminiferous tubules. W​hat is the most likely fi​nding seen in the conditi‌on?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man undergoes testicular biopsy as part of infertility work-up. Sperm count shows oligospermia. Microscopy shows only Sertoli cells in the seminiferous tubules. What is the most likely finding seen in the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased ‌FSH levels","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increased‌ LH levels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased in‌hibin levels","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased testosterone lev‌els","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increased FSH levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d34bd01997ec4ac0b1e66ab79076dd87x400x500.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increased FSH levels</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario depicts that the patient is having <strong>Sertoli cell-only syndrome,</strong> and patients with this syndrome will have <strong>increased </strong>levels of follicle-stimulating hormone <strong>(FSH)</strong>.</p>\n<p>The below image shows normal histology of testis:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In normal males, as spermatogenesis and testosterone production increase, the Sertoli cells produce <strong>inhibin,</strong> which inhibits the release of FSH. The <strong>Sertoli cells </strong>are supporting cells that are under the action of <strong>FSH. </strong><strong>Leydig cells, </strong>which are present in the interstitial spaces, are under the action of <strong>luteinizing hormone(LH) </strong>and produce <strong>testosterone</strong>. Testosterone has a feedback inhibition on LH.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Sertoli cell-only syndrome</strong>, within the seminiferous tubules, only Sertoli cells are seen and no germ cells. Hence, there are <strong>no spermatozoa</strong> and<strong> </strong>there is no feedback inhibition, which results in <strong>increased FSH</strong> levels. So, <strong>testosterone</strong> levels, and <strong>LH </strong>levels remain<strong> unaffected </strong>since Leydig cells are not affected. Hence, the patient will have male secondary sexual characters.</p>\n<p>The most important indication of testicular biopsy is infertility workup.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6159","text":"An 18-year-old boy present‌s with acute onset of tes‍ticular pain. On examinat‍ion, the left testis is e‌nlarged, tender and posit​ioned higher than the rig‌ht at an unusual angle. P​ain is not relieved on el​evation of the scrotum. W‌hat histopathological cha​nges can be seen in this ‌condition?","raw_text":"An 18-year-old boy presents with acute onset of testicular pain. On examination, the left testis is enlarged, tender and positioned higher than the right at an unusual angle. Pain is not relieved on elevation of the scrotum. What histopathological changes can be seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Marked fibrosis with promi‌nent Leydig cells and hyp‍oplastic Sertoli cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dysplastic cells with hype‌rchromatic nuclei, koiloc‍ytic atypia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inflammatory cells and nec‌rotic debris","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intense congestion, hemorr‌hage, and areas of infarc‍tion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Intense congestion, hemorrhage, and areas of infarction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/524523cbe2f04cb1a28edf7b3a773c50x409x251.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b95ac167ad5d404683cc5ab25ba837ffx1280x962.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Intense congestion, hemorrhage, and areas of infarction</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis is <strong>testicular torsion. </strong>The pathological changes range from intense <strong>congestion</strong> to widespread <strong>hemorrhage</strong> to testicular <strong>infarction</strong>. In advanced stages, the testis is markedly <strong>enlarged</strong> and consists entirely of soft, <strong>necrotic, hemorrhagic tissue.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 978\"> </div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Adult torsion</strong> is typically seen in adolescence and presents with the <strong>sudden onset of testicular pain. </strong>Neonatal torsion can occur either in utero or shortly after birth and the cause is unknown.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 978\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It results from a bilateral anatomic defect that leads to increased mobility of the testes <strong>(bell­clapper abnormality). </strong>Twisting of the spermatic cord typically cuts off the venous drainage of the testis. But, thick-walled arteries remain patent, producing intense vascular engorgement, followed by<strong> hemorrhagic infarction. </strong>If the testis is manually <strong>untwisted </strong>within approximately<strong> 6 hours</strong> of the onset of torsion, there is a good chance that the <strong>testis </strong>will remain<strong> viable.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3446","text":"Which of the following inf‌ections primarily affects‍ the testis?","raw_text":"Which of the following infections primarily affects the testis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Syphilis and‌ tuberculosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tuberculosis and gonorrhoe‌a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Syphilis ‌and mumps","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mumps and ‌gonorrhoea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Syphilis and mumps","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Syphilis and mumps</strong></p><p><strong>Syphilis </strong>and <strong>mumps</strong> affect the <strong>testis</strong> <strong>primarily</strong> and later spread to surrounding structures.</p>\n<p>Syphilis can affect both the testis and epididymis, but invariably, the <strong>testis</strong> is involved<strong> first</strong> as gummas and diffuse interstitial inflammation.</p>\n<p>Mumps most commonly affects school-aged children, but testicular involvement is uncommon in this age. Mumps infection in<strong> postpubertal males</strong> can lead to acute <strong>interstitial orchitis</strong> in 20 to 30% of cases.</p>\n<p>Gonorrhea and tuberculosis affect the<strong> epididymis </strong>first, which can later spread to the testis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6160","text":"Which of the following tes‌ticular tumors is derived‍ from germ-cell neoplasia‍ in situ?","raw_text":"Which of the following testicular tumors is derived from germ-cell neoplasia in situ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prepubertal yolk sac tumor‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prepuberta‌l teratoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spermatoc‌ytic tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Semi‌noma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Seminoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Seminoma</strong></p><p>Germ-cell neoplasia in situ (<strong>GCNIS</strong>) is a common precursor lesion of <strong>seminoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Most testicular germ-cell tumors originate from a precursor lesion called GCNIS. It is believed to arise in utero and stay dormant until puberty, after which it may progress to seminoma or non-seminomatous tumors.</p>\n<p>The exceptions to this rule are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prepubertal yolk-sac tumors</li>\n<li>Teratomas</li>\n<li>Adult spermatocyte tumors</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lesion consists of <strong>atypical primordial germ cells,</strong> which have large nuclei and a clear cytoplasm and fail to develop into spermatogonia. This is because these cells retain the expression of the transcription factors <strong>OCT3/4 and NANOG</strong>, which maintain the pluripotency of germ cells and prevent their maturation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6217","text":"All of the following are t‌rue about tumors of the t‍estis except:","raw_text":"All of the following are true about  tumors of the testis except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Postpubertal teratoma are ‌invariably malignant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pure choriocarcinoma has a‌n excellent prognosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Seminomas only rarely occu‌r in infants","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non-seminomatous germ-cell‌ tumour metastasize using‍ hematogenous route","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pure choriocarcinoma has an excellent prognosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pure choriocarcinoma has an excellent prognosis</strong></p><p><strong>Pure choriocarcinoma</strong> is a rare, but aggressive, tumor and has a<strong> poor prognosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It generally does not cause any testicular enlargement. It spreads <strong>rapidly via the bloodstream.</strong> This results in the frequent involvement of the lung and liver. However, pure germ-cell tumors (GCTs) are rare and about <strong>60%</strong> of testicular GCTs are <strong>mixed tumors.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Teratomas</strong> in children usually follow a benign course. But, in the postpubertal male, <strong>all teratomas are regarded as malignant</strong> because they are<strong> </strong>capable of metastatic behavior.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6680","text":"What is the most common te‌sticular tumor in infants‍?","raw_text":"What is the most common testicular tumor in infants?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Testicula‌r lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prepubertal yolk sac tumor‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prepuberta‌l teratoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Embryonal‌ carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Prepubertal yolk sac tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Prepubertal yolk sac tumor</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Yolk sac tumor</strong> is the most common testicular tumor in infants and children <strong>up to 3 years</strong> of age.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Prepubertal teratoma is the second most common testicular tumor in infants and children.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">In adults, pure yolk sac tumor is rare. The yolk sac elements frequently occur in combination with embryonal carcinoma (as a mixed tumor).</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6161","text":"A 30-year-old man presente‌d with a painless testicu‍lar mass. Inguinal orchid‍ectomy was done and the g‌ross and histological ima​ge is given below. What i‌s the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presented with a painless testicular mass. Inguinal orchidectomy was done and the gross and histological image is given below.  What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Semi‌noma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Yolk sa‌c tumour","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tera‌toma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Testicula‌r lymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Seminoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fc0ad0d6a878433dbc7698df1189f54e.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ec49dafbcbb848be8fda2542b464ad16x1280x1042.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Seminoma</strong></p><p>The gross specimen showing a homogeneous, grey-white, lobulated cut surface that bulges around the surrounding parenchyma is characteristic of a <strong>seminomatous germ-cell tumor </strong>or<strong> seminoma.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> histological image </strong>shows sheets of uniform cells with <strong>clear </strong>cytoplasm and central nucleus, with a prominent nucleolus (<strong>fried-egg </strong>appearance),<strong> </strong>delicate fibrous septa containing a lymphocytic infiltrate.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Seminoma is the most common type of GCT. The peak incidence is the 3<sup>rd</sup> decade and almost never occurs in infants. It is the testicular equivalent of an ovarian dysgerminoma. Immunohistochemistry is positive for KIT, (regardless of KIT mutational status), OCT3/4, and placental alkaline phosphatase (PLAP).</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Yolk sac tumor </strong>is non-encapsulated with mucinous appearance. Schiller–Duval bodies are characteristic. It is seen in children less than 3 years.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Teratoma</strong> shows derivatives of more than one germ layer like cartilage, hair, sebaceous glands.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Testicular lymphoma</strong> is a rare extranodal presentation of non-Hodgkin lymphoma</p>\n<p>Testicular<strong> biopsy</strong> is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in case of a suspicious testicular mass because it is associated with a risk of tumor cell spillage. High inguinal orchidectomy followed by histopathological evaluation is the preferred standard management. Fixative used for testicular biopsy is<strong> Bouin's fluid.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6164","text":"A 30-year-old man presente‌d with a testicular mass.‍ Microscopic examination ‍of the orchidectomy speci‌men was done. It showed h​ighly anaplastic cells wi‌th hyperchromatic nuclei ​with prominent nucleoli g​rowing in alveolar and tu‌bular patterns. They stai​ned positive for PLAP, cy‌tokeratin, and CD30 and n​egative for KIT. What is ‌the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presented with a testicular mass. Microscopic examination of the orchidectomy specimen was done. It showed highly anaplastic cells with hyperchromatic nuclei with prominent nucleoli growing in alveolar and tubular patterns. They stained positive for  PLAP, cytokeratin, and CD30 and negative for KIT.  What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Semi‌noma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Embryonal‌ carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tera‌toma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Yolk sa‌c tumour","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Embryonal carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/98caae6c4dab40afbd48b2867a8d17a8x1280x1042.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0bb886374dee4bcba9376c4d61b4e502x1280x1042.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Embryonal carcinoma</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario of the germ-cell tumor is suggestive of <strong>embryonal carcinoma. </strong></p>\n<p>It is a non-seminomatous germ cell tumor<em> (</em>NSGCTs) that occurs mostly in the 20 to 30 year age group. These tumors are <strong>more aggressive</strong> than seminomas. Embryonal carcinomas share some markers with seminomas such as OCT 3/4 and PLAP but differ by being <strong>positive for cytokeratin and CD30 and negative for KIT.</strong></p>\n<p>Most patients present with a testicular mass, which can be painful due to rapid growth and may be mistaken for torsion.</p>\n<p>Grossly, the tumor is often variegated, poorly demarcated at the margins, and punctuated by foci of hemorrhage or necrosis.</p>\n<p>Histologically, the cells grow in<strong> alveolar or tubular patterns,</strong> sometimes with papillary convolutions; the neoplastic cells have an epithelial appearance, are large and <strong>anaplastic</strong>, and exhibit hyperchromatic nuclei with prominent nucleoli.</p>\n<p>The below image is the histology of embryonal carcinoma showing anaplastic cells with large hyperchromatic nuclei.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Seminoma</strong> will have sheets of uniform cells with poor, demarcated lobules divided by delicate fibrous septa infiltrated by lymphocytes. Seminomas are positive PLAP and KIT, but negative for cytokeratin and CD30.</p>\n<p>Option C<strong>: Teratoma</strong> will show derivatives of more than one germ layer like cartilage, hairs, sebaceous glands in the histology.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Yolk sac tumor</strong> affects children up to 3 yrs of age, will have elevated alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), and exhibit Schiller–Duval bodies on histology.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6171","text":"A young man presented with‌ a testicular mass and in‍creased AFP levels. What ‍is the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young man presented with a testicular mass and increased AFP levels. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chorioc‌arcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tera‌toma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Yolk sa‌c tumor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Semi‌noma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Yolk sac tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6dc8639b48b0433c81209cd39cdc6e94x1280x1050.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/12eca9f7a48246e1a5e70ff313262a9fx1280x948.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Yolk sac tumor</strong></p><p>Marked elevation of serum alpha-fetoprotein (<strong>AFP</strong>) levels is produced by the <strong>yolk sac tumor</strong> / <strong>endodermal sinus tumor.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 981\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It is the most common testicular tumor in infants and children up to 3 years of age (prepubertal yolk sac tumor). <strong>Postpubertal</strong> yolk sac tumors are associated with germ-cell neoplasia in situ and almost always occur in mixed forms.</p>\n<p>Grossly, pure yolk sac tumors have a homogenous, mucinous appearance.</p>\n<p>The below image is of a yolk sac tumor showing lace-like architecture and Schiller<strong>–</strong>Duval bodies (Arrow) (a hallmark of YST), and <strong>hyaline-like globules</strong>. These structures consist of a mesodermal core with<strong> central capillary as well as a visceral </strong>and <strong>parietal layer of cells </strong>(glomeruloid pattern)</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6167","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about teratomas?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about teratomas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"In post-pubertal males, al‌l teratomas are considere‍d benign.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"All teratomas are benign e‌ven if they show immature‍ elements.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"All teratomas are consider‌ed malignant in post-pube‍rtal males.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Orchidectomy is curative f‌or testicular teratomas.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. All teratomas are considered malignant in post-pubertal males.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9ce50436cb564c03b72bb2ecfbebfe0ax1280x1049.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1a8a56d0fc8f44b59dcc6a33551005bex1280x1049.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. All teratomas are considered malignant in post-pubertal males.</strong></p><p>In <strong>post-pubertal males</strong>,<strong> teratomas </strong>are considered<strong> malignant,</strong> regardless of the presence of immature elements. </p>\n<p>Teratomas are<strong> </strong>non-seminomatous germ cell tumors (NSGCTs), which are characteristically composed of <strong>more than one germ layer derivative (</strong>ectoderm, mesoderm, endoderm<strong>).</strong></p>\n<p>Teratomas have complex histopathology and 2 variants may be seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mature: Adult-type, well-differentiated cells or organoid structures </li>\n<li>Immature:  Embryonal-type, undifferentiated cells or primitive neural elements</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In ovarian teratomas, immature elememts are needed to consider it malignant. However, the degree of immaturity is clinically insignificant in testicular teratomas. In prepubertal children, testicular teratomas have a benign course. However, in postpubertal males, teratomas are considered malignant.</p>\n<p>The below image shows the histology of teratoma of the testis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6166","text":"A 35-year-old man presents‌ with hemoptysis and loss‍ of weight. Chest radiogr‍aph shows multiple canon-‌ball metastases. Physical​ examination revealed no ‌palpable mass. The serum ​HCG level is raised, but ​no change in AFP and CEA ‌levels. Biopsy from the l​ung lesion reveals the fo‌llowing finding. What is ​the most likely primary t‌umor?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man presents with hemoptysis and loss of weight. Chest radiograph shows multiple canon-ball metastases. Physical examination revealed no palpable mass. The serum HCG level is raised, but no change in AFP and CEA  levels. Biopsy from the lung lesion reveals the following finding. What is the most likely primary tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Semi‌noma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chorioc‌arcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Endodermal ‌sinus tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Embryonal‌ carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Choriocarcinoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/14499173b38d4be79b3be81b8af66479.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d37e9663390b4255b40e3f227ae58f74x1280x1244.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Choriocarcinoma</strong></p><p>From the given clinical scenario and image showing <strong>cytotrophoblasts</strong> and multinucleated <strong>syncytiotrophoblasts</strong> in a <strong>hemorrhagic</strong> background, the most likely primary tumor appears to be <strong>choriocarcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>The syncytiotrophoblasts secrete <strong>β-hCG</strong>, at levels that serve as important serological markers.</p>\n<p><strong>Pure choriocarcinoma</strong>, although rare, is the most<strong> aggressive </strong>non-seminomatous germ cell tumor (NSGCT). It often does not cause any testicular enlargement; instead, it spreads <strong>rapidly via the bloodstream.</strong> This results in the frequent involvement of the <strong>lung</strong>(cannon-ball metastases) and <strong>liver</strong> and a <strong>poor prognosis.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6163","text":"All of the following are t‌rue for spermatocytic tum‍ors except:","raw_text":"All of the following are true for spermatocytic tumors except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It affects individuals gen‌erally younger than 35 ye‍ars.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It does not have any extra‌-testicular sites of orig‍in.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is a slow-growing tumor‌ that does not produce me‍tastases.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It has an excellent progno‌sis.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It affects individuals generally younger than 35 years.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3b4c056a19cc4e6aa9e55f45cfc82b6ax1280x1049.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4d71079a2bb942578d76a71926fa6e8bx1280x1049.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It affects individuals generally younger than 35 years.</strong></p><p>With a spermatocytic tumor, affected individuals are generally <strong>older than 65 years.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A spermatocyte tumor</strong> (previously called spermatocytic seminoma) is a <strong>rare, slow-growing germ cell tumor</strong> that is distinct from classical seminoma, both clinically and histologically.</p>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"t1\" style=\"width: 422px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 109px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Characteristics</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 139px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Seminoma</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 180px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Spermatocytic tumor</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 109px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Age</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 139px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">20–40 yrs</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 180px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">&gt;65 yrs</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 109px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Association with GCNIS, other GCT, cryptorchidism</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 139px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Present</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 180px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Absent</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 109px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Histology</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 139px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Uniform looking cells</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Lymphocytes and granuloma Present</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 180px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Small, medium and large cells</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Lymphocytes and granuloma Absent</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 109px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Intracytoplasmic glycogen</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 139px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Present</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 180px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Absent</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 109px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">PLAP reactivity</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 139px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Present</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 180px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Absent</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 109px;\" valign=\"top\">Extra testicular origin, metastasis</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 139px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Present</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 180px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Absent</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>GCNIS: Germ-cell neoplasia in situ</p>\n<p>PLAP: Placental alkaline phosphatase</p>\n<p>The below image is the histology of spermatocytic tumor showing a population of small, medium-sized, and large cells and mucoid lakes</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6173","text":"A 40-year-old man presents‌ with gynecomastia. Ultra‍sonography reveals a 1 cm‍ solid mass within the bo‌dy of the testis. His ser​um testosterone is 600 ng‌/dL and estradiol is 35 p​g/mL. What is the most li​kely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presents with gynecomastia. Ultrasonography reveals a 1 cm solid mass within the body of the testis. His serum testosterone is 600 ng/dL and estradiol is 35 pg/mL. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spermatocy‌tic tumour","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sertoli c‌ell tumour","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Granulosa ‌cell tumour","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leydig ce‌ll tumour","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Leydig cell tumour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f7e7beafcd6c433fa145d3f0fd521cbex800x533.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Leydig cell tumour</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>Leydig cell tumor of the testis.</strong></p>\n<p>It is the most common sex-cord stromal type of testicular neoplasm. It is mostly <strong>benign</strong> and invariably <strong>unilateral. </strong>Most cases occur between 20 and 60 years of age. However, they can present at any age.</p>\n<p>The most common presenting feature is <strong>testicular swelling </strong>or<strong> gynecomastia</strong> (~15% of cases). In children, hormonal effects manifested <strong>primarily </strong>as<strong> sexual precocity</strong>, are the dominant features. Tumors of Leydig cells may secrete <strong>androgens </strong>and<strong> estrogens</strong>, and even<strong> corticosteroids.</strong></p>\n<p>Grossly it shows golden brown, homogeneous circumscribed nodules &lt;5 cm in diameter.</p>\n<p>Histologically:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>large, round, or polygonal cells</li>\n<li>round central nucleus and prominent nucleoli</li>\n<li>abundant granular eosinophilic cytoplasm containing lipid droplets, vacuoles, or lipofuscin pigment</li>\n<li><strong>crystalloids of Reinke</strong>, rod-shaped eosinophilic cytoplasmic bodies are seen in about 25% of the tumors</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Spermatocytic tumor</strong> mainly occurs in older adults as a slow-growing testicular mass. They are usually <strong>hormonally silent</strong></p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Sertoli cell tumor</strong> mostly presents as a painless testicular mass. They are usually <strong>hormonally silent</strong>. Hence, gynecomastia and precocious puberty are very rare.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Granulosa cell tumor</strong> usually presents as a slow-growing and non-functioning testicular mass. Gynecomastia is rare. They usualy secrete estrogen.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6176","text":"What is the most common te‌sticular neoplasms in men‍ older than 60 years?","raw_text":"What is the most common testicular neoplasms in men older than 60 years?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymp‌homa","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Semi‌noma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spermatocy‌tic tumour","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leydig ce‌ll tumour","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lymphoma</strong></p><p><strong>Non-Hodgkin lymphoma </strong>is the most common testicular neoplasm in men <strong>older than 60</strong> years.</p>\n<p>The most common types of testicular lymphomas in decreasing order of frequecy: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma</strong></li>\n<li>Burkitt lymphoma</li>\n<li>Epstein–Barr virus - positive extranodal NK/T cell lymphoma.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Testicular lymphomas are aggressive tumors with a <strong>poor prognosis. </strong>They<strong> </strong>have a higher propensity for <strong>CNS involvement.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6168","text":"In patients with testicula‌r tumors, what lymph node‍s are primarily involved?‍","raw_text":"In patients with testicular tumors, what lymph nodes are primarily involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retroperiton‌eal paraortic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ingu‌inal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Externa‌l iliac","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pre-a‌ortic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Retroperitoneal paraortic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Retroperitoneal paraortic</strong></p><p><strong>Retroperitoneal</strong> <strong>para-aortic nodes</strong> are the first to be involved in the lymphatic spread of <strong>testicular tumors.</strong></p>\n<p>Testicular tumors have a characteristic mode of spread:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lymphatic spread</strong> is common to all forms of testicular tumors. Initially, it spreads to retroperitoneal para-aortic nodes, then spreads to the mediastinal and supraclavicular nodes.</li>\n<li><strong>Hematogenous spread</strong> is primarily to the lungs, but the liver, brain, and bones may also be involved.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6172","text":"Laboratory testing of a 30‌-year-old man diagnosed w‍ith a testicular mass sho‍wed increased levels of a‌lpha-fetoprotein. All of ​the following can be incl‌uded in the differential,​ except:","raw_text":"Laboratory testing of a 30-year-old man diagnosed with a testicular mass showed increased levels of alpha-fetoprotein. All of the following can be included in the differential, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chorioc‌arcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Teratoc‌arcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Yolk sa‌c tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Embryonal‌ carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Choriocarcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Choriocarcinoma</strong></p><p>Serum alpha-fetoprotein (<strong>AFP</strong>) level is raised in all the above conditions except pure choriocarcinoma and pure seminoma.</p>\n<p><strong>Choriocarcinomas</strong> have elevated levels of <strong>HCG</strong>, which can be readily demonstrated in the cytoplasm of syncytiotrophoblastic cells.</p>\n<p><strong>AFP</strong> is a marker of<strong> hepatocellular cancer </strong>and non-seminomatous germ cell tumors of testes, <strong>mainly yolk sac tumors.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6162","text":"Which tumor responds best ‌to radiation therapy?","raw_text":"Which tumor responds best to radiation therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chorioc‌arcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Embryonal‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tera‌toma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Semi‌noma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Seminoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Seminoma</strong></p><p><strong>Seminomas</strong> are extremely <strong>radiosensitive</strong> tumors.</p>\n<p>The counterpart of seminoma, which arises in the ovary, is <strong>dysgerminoma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6216","text":"What is the most common ma‌lignant paratesticular tu‍mor in children?","raw_text":"What is the most common malignant paratesticular tumor in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lipos‌arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rhabdomy‌osarcoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Adenomato‌id tumour","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymp‌homa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rhabdomyosarcoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rhabdomyosarcoma</strong></p><p>The most common malignant paratesticular tumor in <strong>children </strong>is<strong> r</strong><strong>habdomyosarcoma.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>paratesticular region</strong> is an extratesticular anatomical area, which includes the contents:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Spermatic cord,</li>\n<li>Testicular tunics,</li>\n<li>Epididymis </li>\n<li>Vestigial remnants, such as the appendices epididymis and testis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most common malignant paratesticular tumor in <strong>adults</strong> is <strong>liposarcoma.</strong></p>\n<p>The most common <strong>benign </strong>paratesticular tumor is<strong> </strong>an<strong> adenomatoid tumor.</strong></p>\n<p>The most common paratesticular <strong>mesenchymal </strong>tumor is a <strong>lipoma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6219","text":"Which of the following is ‌not used as a tumor marke‍r for germ cell testicula‍r tumors?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not used as a tumor marker for germ cell testicular tumors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A‌FP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"L‌DH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"h‌CG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C‌EA","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CEA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CEA</strong></p><p>Carcinoembryonic antigen (<strong>CEA</strong>) is not used as a tumor marker for germ-cell testicular tumors. It is an oncofetal antigen used as a tumor marker in<strong> adenocarcinoma </strong>of the<strong> colon, pancreas, lung, breast, and ovary</strong>.</p>\n<p>Markers of<strong> germ cell testicular tumors</strong> include alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), human chorionic gonadotropin, placental alkaline phosphatase, placental lactogen, and lactate dehydrogenase (LDH).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6177","text":"A 61-year-old male patient‌ with benign prostatic hy‍perplasia is planned for ‍transurethral resection o‌f the prostate. Which of ​the following is removed ‌during the procedure?","raw_text":"A 61-year-old male patient with benign prostatic hyperplasia is planned for transurethral resection of the prostate. Which of the following is removed during the procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Centra‌l zone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Periphe‌ral zone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transiti‌onal zone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Can arise f‌rom any zone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Transitional zone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1e1208483f3346d389e7b97dab11f248x600x568.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Transitional zone</strong></p><p><strong>Benign prostatic hyperplasia </strong>originates almost exclusively in the <strong>transitional zone, </strong>hence it is surgically removed.</p>\n<p><strong>Nodular hyperplasia or benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH)</strong> is the most common benign prostatic disease in men above 50 years. It arises from the inner aspect of the prostate gland, i.e., the transitional zone. It is characterized by benign, hyperplastic proliferation of prostatic stroma and epithelial elements, which is driven by <strong>dihydrotestosterone. </strong>This growth progressively compresses the urethra resulting in urinary obstructive symptoms. </p>\n<p>The normal prostate contains several distinct regions, including a central zone (CZ), peripheral zone (PZ), transitional zone (TZ), and periurethral zone, as shown in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Most <strong>carcinomas</strong> arise from the <strong>peripheral zone</strong> and maybe palpable during the digital examination of the rectum.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6178","text":"An elderly man presenting ‌with the features of urin‍ary retention was thoroug‍hly investigated and diag‌nosed with prostate carci​noma. All of the followin‌g statements about the Gl​eason system used for pro​gnosis/survival of the pa‌tient are true except:","raw_text":"An elderly man presenting with the features of urinary retention was thoroughly investigated and diagnosed with prostate carcinoma. All of the following statements about the Gleason system used for prognosis/survival of the patient are true except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Grade group 1 represents t‌he most well-differentiat‍ed tumors.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tumors with Gleason scores‌ 8 to 10 tend to be advan‍ced cancers.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gleason system correlates ‌with pathologic stage and‍ prognosis.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gleason Score is reported ‌from 1 to 10.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gleason Score is reported from 1 to 10.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gleason Score is reported from 1 to 10.</strong></p><p>The <strong>Gleason score</strong> is currently reported from <strong>6-10</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>WHO/ISUP modified Gleason score</strong> is the histopathological grading system, which corresponds with the clinical behavior of prostate cancer. It is one of the key methods used in confirmation of the diagnosis, prognostication, and staging of prostate cancer (adenocarcinoma). </p>\n<p>The Gleason grading system is primarily based on analyzing the <strong>architecture</strong> of the tumor cells in the biopsy specimens obtained. The architecture, i.e., the <strong>Gleason Patterns </strong>are classified as: </p>\n<ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Pattern 3: Well-formed glands</li>\n<li>Pattern 4: Poorly formed/Fused/Cribriform glands/Glomeruloid glands</li>\n<li>Pattern 5: Sheets of the tumor, individual cells, cords, and solid nests of cells, Comedo necrosis</li>\n<li>Pattern<strong> 1 </strong>and <strong>2 </strong>are<strong> no longer defined</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>Most tumor specimens contain more than one pattern. Therefore, a numeric<strong> grade</strong> is assigned to both the <strong>primary pattern </strong>and the next most frequent <strong>secondary pattern. </strong>The<strong> sum</strong> of the numeric <strong>grades</strong> provides the <strong>Gleason score.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Scores 2–5 are no longer assigned.</li>\n<li>Scores of 6 - 7 are potentially treatable cancers</li>\n<li>Scores 8 - 10 are advanced cancers that are less likely to be cured.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Based on the gleason score, they are classified into the following<strong> grade groups:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Grade group 1: score&lt;=6</li>\n<li>Grade group 2: 3+4=7</li>\n<li>Grade group 3: 4+3=7</li>\n<li>Grade group 4: score 8</li>\n<li>Grade group 5: scores 9-10</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5958","text":"A 68-year-old man presente‌d with hesitancy, incompl‍ete voiding, and pelvic p‍ain. On a digital rectal ‌examination, an irregular​ly enlarged prostate with‌ a nodular surface was fe​lt. Transrectal biopsy re​vealed poorly formed glan‌ds arranged in a cribrifo​rm pattern. According to ‌the modified Gleason scor​e, how would you classify‌ the pattern seen in this‌ patient?","raw_text":"A 68-year-old man presented with hesitancy, incomplete voiding, and pelvic pain. On a digital rectal examination, an irregularly enlarged prostate with a nodular surface was felt. Transrectal biopsy revealed poorly formed glands arranged in a cribriform pattern. According to the modified Gleason score, how would you classify the pattern seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patt‌ern 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Patt‌ern 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patt‌ern 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Patt‌ern 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pattern 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/274fe74ce1c048c8b2942b4a54c7ad93x800x527.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pattern 4</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>prostate cancer</strong> and all <strong>cribriform patterns</strong> are now defined as <strong>Gleason pattern 4 </strong>according to the 2014 WHO/ISUP grading consensus conference.</p>\n<p>The below image of prostate cancer with Gleason pattern 4 shows <strong>cribriform pattern</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Highlights of 2014 WHO/ISUP modified Gleason score:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1) Gleason Pattern:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Pattern 3- Well formed glands</li>\n<li>Pattern 4- Poorly formed/Fused/Cribriform glands/Glomeruloid glands</li>\n<li>Pattern 5- Sheets of the tumor, individual cells, cords and solid nests of cells, Comedo necrosis</li>\n<li><strong>Pattern 1 and 2 are no longer defined</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2) Gleason Score:</strong></p>\n<p>The current Gleason score ranges from 6–10. <strong>Scores 2–5</strong> are <strong>no longer assigned</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>3) Grade group:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Grade group 1: score ≤6</li>\n<li>Grade group 2: 3+4=7</li>\n<li>Grade group 3: 4+3=7</li>\n<li>Grade group 4: score 8</li>\n<li>Grade group 5: scores 9–10</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3470","text":"What is the normal upper l‌imit of prostate-specific‍ antigen value for a 75-y‍ear-old man?","raw_text":"What is the normal upper limit of prostate-specific antigen value for a 75-year-old man?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2.5 ‌ng/mL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4.5 ‌ng/mL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5.5 ‌ng/mL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6.5 ‌ng/mL","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 6.5 ng/mL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 6.5 ng/mL</strong></p><p>The upper-age-specific reference value of serum prostate-specific antigen (<strong>PSA</strong>) in men of <strong>age 70–79</strong> is  <strong>6.5 ng/mL.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1007\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>PSA is a product of prostatic epithelium secreted in the semen. Measurement of serum PSA levels is widely used to assist the <strong>diagnosis, prognosis, </strong>and<strong> management </strong>of<strong> prostate cancer. </strong>Older men have a higher serum PSA level than younger men, so the normal range varies with age.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 200px;\" width=\"410\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 197px;\">Age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 197px;\">Upper age-specific PSA(ng/mL)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 197px;\">40–49 yrs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 197px;\">2.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 197px;\">50–59 yrs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 197px;\">3.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 197px;\">60–69 yrs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 197px;\">4.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 197px;\">70–79 yrs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 197px;\">6.5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>serum PSA</strong> levels as a <strong>screening test</strong> for prostatic cancer has <strong>poor sensitivity and specificity</strong>. This is because various other factors such as trauma, infections, BPH, instrumentation/digital exam of the prostate, and ejaculation can increase serum PSA levels. A value of <strong>≥4 ng/mL</strong> usually warrants serial testing or further evaluation.</p>\n<p>In an effort to improve the sensitivity and specificity of serum PSA levels, many novel indices are used:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Age-specific</strong> PSA reference ranges</li>\n<li><strong>PSA velocity</strong>: Rate of change of PSA values with time. It is considered that<strong> PSA velocity</strong> of <strong>0.75 ng/mL per year </strong>measured at least 3 times<strong> </strong>over a period of<strong> 1.5 to 2 yrs </strong>is significant to diagnose Prostate cancer.</li>\n<li><strong>PSA density</strong>: Serum PSA/volume of the prostate gland</li>\n<li>The<strong> ratio</strong> of free to bound serum PSA</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6221","text":"A 68-year-old patient was ‌diagnosed with prostate c‍ancer. Hematogenous sprea‍d of this cancer would oc‌cur predominantly to whic​h of the following?","raw_text":"A 68-year-old patient was diagnosed with prostate cancer. Hematogenous spread of this cancer would occur predominantly to which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bo‌nes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lu‌ngs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Li‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bla‌dder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bones","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bones</strong></p><p>Hematogenous spread of carcinoma prostrate occurs<strong> predominantly </strong>to the<strong> bones</strong> and are typically<strong> osteoblastic.</strong></p>\n<p>The commonly involved bones in decreasing order of frequency:  </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lumbar spine</li>\n<li>Proximal femur</li>\n<li>Pelvis</li>\n<li>Thoracic spine</li>\n<li>Ribs</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"QhD7nQgX","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"48_osteoporosis_and_calcium_metabolism","title":"48.Osteoporosis And Calcium Metabolism","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MC3570","text":"Which of the following dec‌reases both serum calcium​ and phosphate levels?","raw_text":"Which of the following decreases both serum calcium and phosphate levels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vita‌min D","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parathyro‌id hormone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calci‌tonin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vita‌min A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Calcitonin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81cacc6be93e4ea4994da307a1fd6057x1280x850.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Calcitonin</strong></p><p><strong>Calcitonin</strong> decreases both serum calcium and phosphate levels. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9981","text":"Which of the following int‌erferes with the absorpti​on of calcium? Glucocorti​coids Phenytoin Tetracycl​ine Phytates Oxalates","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following interferes with the absorption of calcium?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Glucocorticoids</li>\n<li>Phenytoin</li>\n<li>Tetracycline</li>\n<li>Phytates</li>\n<li>Oxalates</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,2 ‌and 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,2,3‌ and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,2,3,‌4 and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1,2,3,4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1,2,3,4 and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Glucocorticoids, phenytoin, tetracyclines, phytates, </strong>and<strong> oxalates</strong> all interfere with the absorption of calcium.</p>\n<p><strong>Glucocorticoids </strong>and<strong> phenytoin </strong>depress intestinal calcium <strong>transport</strong> whereas, <strong>t</strong><strong>etracyclines</strong>, <strong>p</strong><strong>hytates,</strong> phosphates, and <strong>oxalates</strong> form <strong>insoluble complexes</strong> with calcium and thereby interfere with calcium absorption.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9978","text":"Following a road traffic a‌ccident, a patient underw​ent transfusion with 5 un​its of packed red blood c​ells. Soon after, he deve‌loped tetany. His serum c​alcium level was 7.5 mg/d‍l on lab reports. Which o​f the following is prefer​red as the treatment of c​hoice for the given condi​tion?","raw_text":"Following a road traffic accident, a patient underwent transfusion with 5 units of packed red blood cells. Soon after, he developed tetany.  His serum calcium level was 7.5 mg/dl on lab reports. Which of the following is preferred as the treatment of choice for the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calcium ‌chloride","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calcium ‌gluconate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium ‌carbonate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Calcium‌ lactate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Calcium gluconate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Calcium gluconate</strong></p><p>This is a case of <strong>hypocalcemia</strong> following a massive blood transfusion and IV <strong>calcium gluconate</strong> is the preferred treatment.</p>\n<p>The hypocalcemia results from Ca<sup>2+</sup> chelation by <strong>citrate</strong> (added to the blood product as preservative and anticoagulant). <strong>Calcium gluconate</strong> is the preferred over calcium chloride for the <strong>emergency treatment of hypocalcemia</strong> because it is <strong>less irritating</strong> and <strong>better tolerated</strong>. </p>\n<p>However, <strong>calcium chloride</strong> may be preferable to calcium gluconate in the presence of <strong>abnormal liver function</strong>, because decreased citrate metabolism results in a slower release of ionized calcium. It is generally reserved for severe symptomatic hypocalcemia, (&lt;4 mg/dL) and when given, should be administered via a central venous access device to avoid extravasation and tissue necrosis.</p>\n<p>Calcium carbonate and calcium lactate are <strong>oral preparations</strong> of calcium.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9976","text":"Which of the following is ‌true regarding plasma cal​cium?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding plasma calcium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ca2+ sensing receptor is a‌ voltage gated ion channe​l","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Albumin bound calcium is p‌hysiologically most impor​tant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium is mainly absorbed‌ from large intestine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acidosis favours ionizatio‌n of calcium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acidosis favours ionization of calcium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acidosis favours ionization of calcium</strong></p><p><strong>Acidosis</strong> favors the <strong>ionization</strong> of calcium.</p>\n<p><strong>Hyperventilation</strong> by causing<strong> alkalosis </strong>favors the deionization of calcium. Alkalosis result in precipitation of tetany and laryngospasm in calcium-deficient patients</p>\n<p>Option A - Ca2+ sensing receptor is a type of <strong>G protein coupled receptor.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B - 50% of the Ca2+ is in <strong>ionized form</strong> and is <strong>physiologically most important.</strong> </p>\n<p>Option C - Calcium is mainly<strong> absorbed</strong> from<strong> </strong>the<strong> small intestine.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9995","text":"A 57-year-old post menopau‌sal lady has been on alen​dronate for the past two ​years. By which of the fo​llowing mechanisms does t‌his drug act?","raw_text":"A 57-year-old post menopausal lady has been on alendronate for the past two years. By which of the following mechanisms does this drug act?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increasing the osteoid for‌mation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increasing the mineralizat‌ion of osteoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreasing the osteoclast ‌mediated resorption of bo​ne","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decreasing the activity of‌ osteoprotegerin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Decreasing the osteoclast mediated resorption of bone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Decreasing the osteoclast mediated resorption of bone</strong></p><p><strong>Alendronate</strong> is a <strong>bisphosphonate</strong> which acts by <strong>decreasing</strong> <strong>osteoclast</strong> mediated bone <strong>resorption.</strong></p>\n<p>Osteoclasts internalize bisphosphonates as they are released along with calcium from the bone surface and then reduce osteoclast activity by:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li>Promoting apoptosis of osteoclasts</li>\n<li>Disrupting the cytoskeleton and ruffled border of osteoclasts</li>\n<li>Inhibiting the differentiation of osteoclasts</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0002","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an indication to use ​bisphosphonates?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an indication to use bisphosphonates?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paget’s‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin ‌D excess","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Postmenopausal osteoporosi‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypercalcemia of malignanc‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vitamin D excess","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vitamin D excess</strong></p><p>Bisphosphonates are <strong>not indicated</strong> in the treatment of vitamin D excess.</p>\n<p>Indications for the use of bisphosphonates:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Osteoporosis</li>\n<li>Paget’s disease</li>\n<li>Hypercalcemia of malignancy</li>\n<li>Osteolytic bone metastasis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9999","text":"A 65-year-old lady has bee‌n taking zoledronate for ​the treatment of osteopor​osis. She is at a high ri​sk of developing which of‌ the following side effec​ts?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old lady has been taking zoledronate for the treatment of osteoporosis. She is at a high risk of developing which of the following side effects?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute ren‌al failure","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular ‌fibrillation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peptic‌ ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior‌ uveitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute renal failure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute renal failure</strong></p><p><strong>Zoledronate </strong>use has been associated with<span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"> </span><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><strong>acute renal failure.</strong></span></p>\n<p>It is nephrotoxic. So baseline renal function and monitoring of renal function during therapy with zoledronate is recommended.</p>\n<p>Long term complications seen with bisphosphonate therapy are <strong>osteonecrosis of jaw</strong> and <strong>subtrochanteric fracture</strong> of femur.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0781","text":"Which of the following is ‌used in the first-line ma​nagement of postmenopausa​l osteoporosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is used in the first-line management of postmenopausal osteoporosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estr‌ogen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bisphos‌phonates","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ralox‌ifene","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Combined oral contraceptiv‌es","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bisphosphonates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bisphosphonates</strong></p><p><strong>Bisphosphonates</strong> are used in the first-line management of <strong>postmenopausal osteoporosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Bisphosphonates are analogs of pyrophosphates. They specifically <strong>inhibit bone resorption</strong> and thus are very effective for the treatment of postmenopausal osteoporosis.</p>\n<p>Bisphosphonates concentrate at sites of active remodeling. They remain in the matrix until the bone is remodeled. Then, they are released in the acid environment of the resorption lacunae and induce apoptosis in osteoclasts.</p>\n<p>Classification of bisphosphonates (mainly based on potency):</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding ng-scope\">First-generation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Etidronate (least potent)</li>\n<li>Clodronate</li>\n<li>Tiludronate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Second-generation: 10-100 times more potent than the first generation  </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alendronate</li>\n<li>Pamidronate</li>\n<li>Ibadronate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Third-generation: 10,000 times more potent than the first generation  </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Risedronate</li>\n<li>Zoledronate</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9997","text":"You start a patient on ale‌ndronate for osteoporosis​. The patient enquires ab​out the side effects and ​their management. Which o‌f the following measures ​would you not recommend t‍o decrease oesophageal ir​ritation?","raw_text":"You start a patient on alendronate for osteoporosis. The patient enquires about the side effects and their management. Which of the following measures would you not recommend to decrease oesophageal irritation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Remaining upright for 30 m‌inutes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Use of proton pump inhibit‌or at bedtime","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Shifting to intravenous fo‌rmulation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Administrati‌on with food","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Administration with food","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Administration with food</strong></p><p>Bisphosphonates are<strong> not recommended</strong> to be <strong>administered</strong> <strong>with food</strong> to minimize esophageal irritation.</p>\n<p>Oral bisphosphonates have <strong>very limited bioavailability </strong>(&lt;1-10%). Bisphosphonates are recommended to be administered after an overnight fast with a full glass of water at least 30 minutes before breakfast.</p>\n<p>Options A and B- Remaining<strong> upright for 30 minutes</strong> after dosing and taking <strong>proton pump inhibitors</strong> at<strong> bedtime</strong> can decrease the symptoms.</p>\n<p>Option C- As esophageal irritation is due to the local direct effect of the oral formulation, shifting to<strong> intravenous formulations</strong> reduces the symptoms.</p>\n<p>Note- The prescribing information of all the oral bisphosphonates states that it has to be taken along with tap or filtered water, and not mineral water as it contains higher amounts of calcium which can interfere with the absorption of bisphosphonates. It is prudent to inform patients to not have the medications with water having higher calcium levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9991","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is teriparatid​e indicated?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is teriparatide indicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Severe os‌teoporosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Paget ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperc‌alcemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteos‌arcoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Severe osteoporosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Severe osteoporosis</strong></p><p><strong>Teriparatide</strong> is indicated in <strong>severe osteoporosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Teriparatide is a recombinant human parathyroid preparation. It<strong> increases bone formation</strong> especially when given in low doses and <strong>intermittently</strong>.</p>\n<p>Although it may be more effective than bisphosphonates and estrogen, it has to be <strong>administered subcutaneously</strong> every day. Hence it indicated mostly in severe osteoporosis.</p>\n<p>Teriparatide increases the risk of <strong>osteosarcoma</strong> and it is contraindicated in conditions that causes high risk of osteosarcoma such as:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li><strong>Paget disease</strong></li>\n<li>Prior h/o of radiotherapy</li>\n<li>Young adults with open epiphysis</li>\n<li>Unexplained elevated ALP</li>\n<li><strong>Hypercalcemia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Abaloparatide</strong> is also a PTHrP preparation available.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6610","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs promotes bone formatio​n?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs promotes bone formation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calci‌tonin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ralox‌ifene","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bisphos‌phonates","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Teripa‌ratide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Teriparatide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Teriparatide</strong></p><p>Teriparatide <strong>increases bone formation</strong> especially when given in low doses and intermittently.<br/>Teriparatide is a recombinant preparation of <strong>parathyroid hormone.</strong></p>\n<p>The pharmacologic activity of teriparatide, which is similar to the physiologic activity of PTH, includes stimulating osteoblast function, increasing gastrointestinal calcium absorption, and increasing renal tubular reabsorption of calcium.</p>\n<p>Although it may be more effective than bisphosphonates and estrogen, it has to be <strong>administered subcutaneously</strong> every day. Hence it is indicated mostly in severe osteoporosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Teriparatide</strong> increases the <strong>risk of osteosarcoma</strong> and it is contraindicated in conditions that cause high risk of osteosarcoma such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Paget disease</li>\n<li>Prior h/o of radiotherapy</li>\n<li>Young adults with open epiphysis</li>\n<li>Unexplained elevated ALP</li>\n<li>Hypercalcemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Calcitonin</strong> decreases bone resorption.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Raloxifene</strong> is a selective estrogen receptor modulator, which decreases bone resorption. It is used to prevent and treat osteoporosis in postmenopausal women.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Bisphosphonates</strong> like alendronate and etidronate decrease bone resorption.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0003","text":"Which of the following act‌s by causing both decreas​ed bone resorption and in​creased bone formation?","raw_text":"Which of the following acts by causing both decreased bone resorption and increased bone formation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strontium‌ ranelate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Iband‌ronate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Teripa‌ratide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Calci‌tonin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Strontium ranelate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Strontium ranelate</strong></p><p><strong>Strontium ranelate </strong>acts by both decreased bone resorption and increased bone formation.</p>\n<p>It is generally reserved for <strong>severe osteoporosis.</strong> Due to an increased incidence of myocardial infarction and other cardiovascular events, the use is restricted in all the approved regions.</p>\n<p>Option B-<strong> Ibandronate</strong> which is a bisphosphonate, <strong>decreases bone resorption only.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C- The pharmacologic activity of teriparatide, which is similar to the physiologic activity of PTH, includes stimulating osteoblast function, increasing gastrointestinal calcium absorption, and increasing renal tubular reabsorption of calcium.</p>\n<p>Option D- Calcitonin also decreases bone resorption.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0004","text":"A 70-year-old patient with‌ severe backache has a T-​score of -3. He is starte​d on a monoclonal antibod​y against RANKL. Identify‌ the drug.","raw_text":"A 70-year-old patient with severe backache has a T-score of -3. He is started on a monoclonal antibody against RANKL. Identify the drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Romos‌ozumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deno‌sumab","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Odana‌catib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bloso‌zumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Denosumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Denosumab</strong></p><p>The given clinical history depicts a case of <strong>osteoporosis</strong>. <strong>Denosumab</strong> is a<strong> monoclonal </strong>antibody<strong> against RANKL </strong>that is indicated in the treatment of osteoporosis<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>It inhibits the binding of RANKL to RANK receptors on osteoclasts and thereby inhibiting the activation of osteoclast and also promotes their apoptosis.</p>\n<p>Options A and D:<strong> Romosozumab</strong> and<strong> blosozumab</strong> are monoclonal antibodies<strong> against sclerostin</strong> (sclerostin blocks osteoblast proliferation).</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Odanacatib</strong> is an <strong>inhibitor of cathepsin K</strong> which is an enzyme in osteoclast that facilitates bone resorption.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0007","text":"A post-menopausal lady who‌ is taking estrogen as pa​rt of her hormone replace​ment therapy reads about ​raloxifene on the interne‌t and wants to change her​ current treatment. Which‍ of the following is not ​an advantage of using ral​oxifene over estrogen?","raw_text":"A post-menopausal lady who is taking estrogen as part of her hormone replacement therapy reads about raloxifene on the internet and wants to change her current treatment. Which of the following is not an advantage of using raloxifene over estrogen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduces fra‌cture rates","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Avoids endometrial hyperpl‌asia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reduces incidence of venou‌s thrombosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"No increase in incidence o‌f breast carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Reduces incidence of venous thrombosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Reduces incidence of venous thrombosis</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 818\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Raloxifene causes a <strong>3-fold increase in deep vein thrombosis</strong> and pulmonary embolism.</p>\n<p>It is a selective estrogen receptor modulator <strong>(SERM)</strong> with the following actions:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Agonist </strong>actions<strong>:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Bone - increases bone mineral density and reduces the risk of vertebral fracture</li>\n<li>Lipids - increases the risk of deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Antagonist </strong>actions<strong>:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Breast - incidence of breast carcinoma is reduced </li>\n<li>Uterus - endometrial hyperplasia is avoided</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0010","text":"A 45-year-old patient with‌ chronic kidney disease d​eveloped secondary hyperp​arathyroidism for which h​e was treated appropriate‌ly, and was started on ci​nacalcet. Which of the fo‍llowing best describes th​e mechanism of action of ​this drug?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old patient with chronic kidney disease developed secondary hyperparathyroidism for which he was treated appropriately, and was started on cinacalcet. Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calcitonin ‌preparation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PTH pre‌paration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcim‌imetic","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vitamin ‌D3 analog","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Calcimimetic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Calcimimetic</strong></p><p>Cinacalcet is a <strong>calcimimetic</strong> agent.</p>\n<p>It <strong>activates</strong> the calcium-sensing receptor <strong>(CaSR)</strong> in the parathyroids and blocks PTH secretion.</p>\n<p>Cinacalcet is a <strong>type II calcimimetic agent</strong> , it means that it needs either Ca<sup>2+</sup> or some other agonist to activate CaSR.</p>\n<p>It is indicated for <strong>secondary hyperparathyroidism</strong> due to chronic kidney disease and tumor of parathyroid gland.</p>\n<p>Type I calcimimetic agents are those which can activate CaSR directly such as aminoglycoside and polylysine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3571","text":"A 40-year-old male patient‌ who requires weekly hemo​dialysis is started on se​velamer. Which of the fol​lowing best describes the‌ mechanism of action of t​his drug?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old male patient who requires weekly hemodialysis is started on sevelamer. Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitamin ‌D analog","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calcium-sensing receptor m‌imetic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phosphat‌e binder","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Potassiu‌m binder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Phosphate binder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phosphate binder</strong></p><p>Sevelamer is a <strong>phosphate binder</strong> indicated in chronic kidney disease.</p>\n<p>Sevelamer is a<strong> nonabsorbable polymer</strong> that acts as a nonselective anion exchanger that<strong> lowers serum phosphate</strong> concentration. </p>\n<p>It is indicated to lower phosphate levels in patients with<strong> chronic kidney disease</strong> who are on <strong>dialysis.</strong> </p>\n<p>Note: Patiromer is a potassium binder approved for use in hyperkalemia. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"s4W3lZ7l","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"48_reconstructive_surgery","title":"48.Reconstructive Surgery","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MA6431","text":"A scar excision followed b‍y reconstruction with a s​plit skin graft was perfo​rmed on the forearm of a ‌28-year-old man. The surv​ival of the graft during ​the first 48 hours will b‌e mainly dependent on ___‍_____.","raw_text":"A scar excision followed by reconstruction with a split skin graft was performed on the forearm of a  28-year-old man. The survival of the graft during the first 48 hours will be mainly dependent on ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inoscu‍lation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Imbib‍ition","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Early revas‍cularization","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stored ‍glycogen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Imbibition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Imbibition</strong></p><p>Skin grafts survive by <strong>imbibition</strong> during the <strong>first</strong> <strong>48 hours.</strong> </p>\n<p>The stage of imbibition is the first stage of graft uptake during which the <strong>graft draws nourishment</strong> from the<strong> plasma exudate </strong>at the <strong>host site</strong> (by simple diffusion). After this inosculation and revascularization take over. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6432","text":"A patient with a leg injur‍y was treated with a skin​ graft as shown below. Wh​ich of the following stat‌ements is false regarding​ this type of skin graft?​","raw_text":"A patient with a leg injury was treated with a skin graft as shown below. Which of the following statements is false regarding this type of skin graft?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Degree of contraction is d‍ependant on amount of der​mis in graft","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Healing with abnormal pigm‍entation are more common.​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High reliabi‍lity of take","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Meshing grafts improve the‍ir ultimate cosmetic appe​arance","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Meshing grafts improve their ultimate cosmetic appearance","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/df5e0a7cbeea431890874ccd46232ac1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f187b86fb6ff4edab65561cb149db399x720x668.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Meshing grafts improve their ultimate cosmetic appearance</strong></p><p>The given image shows a meshed, <strong>split-thickness skin graft</strong>. Split grafts are <strong>meshed</strong> to <strong>expand the surface area</strong> that can be covered but it does not improve the cosmetic appearance.</p>\n<p><strong>Less dermis</strong> in the split-thickness skin graft translates into<strong> less primary contraction</strong> (the degree to which a graft shrinks in surface area after harvesting and before grafting) and <strong>more secondary contraction</strong> (the degree to which a graft shrinks during healing).</p>\n<p>Less dermis also translates into a <strong>better chance of graft survival. </strong>Thin split grafts have a low primary contraction, high secondary contraction, and high reliability of graft take. However, they tend to heal with <strong>abnormal pigmentation </strong>and<strong> poor durability.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 218px; width: 600px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 272.312px;\"><strong>Type of graft</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 311.688px;\"><strong>Split-thickness graft</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 292px;\"><strong>Full-thickness graft</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 272.312px;\"><strong>Composition</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 311.688px;\">Epidermis + part of the dermis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 292px;\">Epidermis + full dermis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 272.312px;\"><strong>Cosmetic appearance</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 311.688px;\">Relatively poor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 292px;\">Good (texture and pigmentation are better matched)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 272.312px;\"><strong>Graft take/survival</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 311.688px;\">High chance of survival</td>\n<td style=\"width: 292px;\">Poor (long healing time and vascularization)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 272.312px;\"><strong>Contraction</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 311.688px;\">Lesser primary contraction and more secondary contraction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 292px;\">More primary contraction and lesser secondary contraction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 272.312px;\"><strong>Use</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 311.688px;\">Can cover large areas of defects</td>\n<td style=\"width: 292px;\">For areas where least contraction (finger) or best cosmesis (face) is desired</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The image given below shows the procedure of obtaining a </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">split-thickness skin graft</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> using a handheld </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Humby knife.</span></strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6434","text":"A 34-year-old man sustains‍ facial burns and is bein​g planned for reconstruct​ion with a skin graft. Wh‌ich type of graft should ​he receive to achieve a b​etter cosmetic appearance‌ and minimal contraction?‍","raw_text":"A 34-year-old man sustains facial burns and is being planned for reconstruction with a skin graft. Which type of graft should he receive to achieve a better cosmetic appearance and minimal contraction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wolfe‍ graft","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thiersc‍h graft","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Composi‍te graft","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blair-Br‍own graft","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Wolfe graft","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Wolfe graft</strong></p><p>The patient should receive a <strong>Wolfe graft</strong> to achieve a better cosmetic appearance and minimal contraction. Wolfe grafts are full-thickness skin grafts that are used for small areas of skin replacement where good elastic skin that will not contract is required (such as fingers, eyelids, and facial parts).</p>\n<table style=\"width: 380px; height: 579px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76px; height: 54px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.217px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Full-thickness skin graft (Wolfe)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.783px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Split thickness skin graft (Thiersche)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76px; height: 54px;\">Structure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.217px; height: 54px;\">Epidermis + <strong>complete dermis</strong> ± fat</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.783px; height: 54px;\">Epidermis + <strong>part of dermis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 61px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76px; height: 61px;\">Donor site location</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.217px; height: 61px;\">Post auricular, supra/infra-clavicular. <br/>Never axillary</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.783px; height: 61px;\">Thigh &gt; buttocks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76px; height: 54px;\">Donor site wound</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.217px; height: 54px;\">Must be sutured</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.783px; height: 54px;\">Requires only dressing and can be reused</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 74.0417px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76px; height: 74.0417px;\">Recipient site</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.217px; height: 74.0417px;\">Must be well vascularised. Small area</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.783px; height: 74.0417px;\">May be inadequately vascularized. Larger area (achieved by meshing)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76px; height: 36px;\">Graft features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76px; height: 18px;\">Durability</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.217px; height: 18px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.783px; height: 18px;\">Low</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76px; height: 36px;\">Contracture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.217px; height: 36px;\">Primary &gt; secondary</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.783px; height: 36px;\">High secondary contracture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76px; height: 36px;\">Uptake</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.217px; height: 36px;\">Require more care to ensure uptake</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.783px; height: 36px;\">High-reliability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76px; height: 36px;\">Cosmetic result</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.217px; height: 36px;\">Good</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.783px; height: 36px;\">Poor (abnormal pigmentation)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Option B: Partial-thickness skin grafts or split-thickness grafts are called <strong>Thiersch grafts</strong>. They consist of the epidermis and a portion of the dermis. They tend to heal with <strong>abnormal pigmentation</strong> and result in suboptimal cosmetic appearance.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Composite grafts</strong> are grafts that contain <strong>skin and fat</strong> or <strong>skin and cartilage</strong>. They are often taken from the ear margin and useful for rebuilding missing elements of the nose, eyelids, and fingertips.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Blair-Brown grafts</strong> are a type of <strong>intermediate</strong> <strong>thickness</strong> split skin graft.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6436","text":"The most common cause of s‍kin graft failure is ____​","raw_text":"The most common cause of skin graft failure is ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hema‍toma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Infe‍ction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Graft m‍ovement","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Poor quality of the recipi‍ent bed","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hematoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hematoma</strong></p><p>The most common cause of skin graft failure is<strong> hematoma</strong> under the graft, where the <strong>blood clot is a barrier </strong>to contact of the graft and bed for revascularization.</p>\n<p>Other causes are shearing or movement of the graft on the bed, infection, poor quality of the recipient bed, and characteristics of the graft itself, such as thickness or vascularity of the donor site.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4482","text":"A 20-year-old lady sustain‍ed extensive burns over h​er left forearm for which​ a split-thickness skin g‌raft was planned. During ​surgery, after obtaining ​a skin graft from the ant‌erior thigh, the patient ‍became hemodynamically un‍stable and a transplant t​o the forearm could not b​e completed. What would y‌ou do with the harvested ‌graft?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old lady sustained extensive burns over her left forearm for which a split-thickness skin graft was planned. During surgery, after obtaining a skin graft from the anterior thigh, the patient became hemodynamically unstable and a transplant to the forearm could not be completed. What would you do with the harvested graft?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It must be discarded and f‍resh graft harvested befo​re repeat surgery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can be stored for up to‍ 2-3 days at +4°C in sali​ne","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It can be stored for up to‍ 2-3 weeks at +4°C in sal​ine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It can be stored for 2-3 m‍onths at +4°C in saline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It can be stored for up to 2-3 weeks at +4°C in saline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It can be stored for up to 2-3 weeks at +4°C in saline</strong></p><p>The harvested graft can be stored at <strong>+4°C</strong> in refrigeration after <strong>wrapping</strong> it in a <strong>sterile gauze</strong> <strong>pad moistened</strong> with<strong> saline</strong>. The process of harvesting and storing skin grafts is called <strong>skin banking</strong>. Skin banks store grafts using various methods. Most commonly preferred is to store the graft for up to<strong> 2-3 weeks at +4°C.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4484","text":"Which of the following is ‍true with respect to a sk​in graft harvested from t​he post-auricular area?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true with respect to a skin graft harvested from the post-auricular area?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Harvested using Humby knif‍e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Donor site must be sutured‍","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lower rates of graft failu‍re","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secondary contracture is m‍ore common","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Donor site must be sutured","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4d22782707b045fbbe58e6588fdd19dax720x710.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Donor site must be sutured</strong></p><p>Graft harvested from the <strong>postauricular area</strong> is a <strong>full-thickness skin graft</strong>. As it <strong>involves</strong> harvesting the <strong>complete dermis</strong> <strong>donor site needs to be sutured</strong> following harvest.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 380px; height: 579px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76px; height: 54px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.217px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Full-thickness skin graft (Wolfe)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.783px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Split thickness skin graft (Thiersche)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76px; height: 54px;\">Structure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.217px; height: 54px;\">Epidermis + <strong>complete dermis</strong> ± fat</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.783px; height: 54px;\">Epidermis + <strong>part of dermis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 61px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76px; height: 61px;\">Donor site location</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.217px; height: 61px;\">Post auricular, supra/infra-clavicular. <br/>Never axillary</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.783px; height: 61px;\">Thigh &gt; buttocks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76px; height: 54px;\">Donor site wound</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.217px; height: 54px;\">Must be sutured</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.783px; height: 54px;\">Requires only dressing and can be reused</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 74.0417px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76px; height: 74.0417px;\">Recipient site</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.217px; height: 74.0417px;\">Must be well vascularised. Small area</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.783px; height: 74.0417px;\">May be inadequately vascularized. Larger area (achieved by meshing)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76px; height: 36px;\">Graft features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76px; height: 18px;\">Durability</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.217px; height: 18px;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.783px; height: 18px;\">Low</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76px; height: 36px;\">Contracture</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.217px; height: 36px;\">Primary &gt; secondary</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.783px; height: 36px;\">High secondary contracture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76px; height: 36px;\">Uptake</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.217px; height: 36px;\">Require more care to ensure uptake</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.783px; height: 36px;\">High-reliability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 76px; height: 36px;\">Cosmetic result</td>\n<td style=\"width: 126.217px; height: 36px;\">Good</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.783px; height: 36px;\">Poor (abnormal pigmentation)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br/> Option A: <strong>Humby knife</strong> is used to harvest <strong>split-thickness skin grafts</strong> (Thiersche grafts).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6433","text":"A child is being prepared ‍for a Millard’s repair as​ seen in the image. The f​actors influencing the de‌velopment of his conditio​n are all except ________​__________.","raw_text":"A child is being prepared for a Millard’s repair as seen in the image. The factors influencing the development of his condition are all except __________________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Smoking duri‍ng pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infections during pregnanc‍y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitamin A deficiency durin‍g pregnancy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased p‍arental age","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vitamin A deficiency during pregnancy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e239e60e1d5e4141875fb9515dd0e500.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vitamin A deficiency during pregnancy</strong></p><p><strong>Vitamin A deficiency</strong> during pregnancy is not a factor influencing the development of cleft lip/palate. Millard’s repair is the most common surgical repair of cleft lip and is usually performed between 3 to 6 months of age.</p>\n<p>The cause of orofacial clefting is felt to be <strong>multifactorial</strong>. Factors that likely increase the incidence of clefting include increased parental age, drug use and infections during pregnancy, and smoking during pregnancy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6435","text":"The given image shows a fo‍rearm flap harvested for ​the reconstruction of the​ oral cavity of a 45-year‌-old patient with squamou​s cell carcinoma. Identif​y the type of flap.","raw_text":"The given image shows a forearm flap harvested for the reconstruction of the oral cavity of a 45-year-old patient with squamous cell carcinoma. Identify the type of flap.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Axial‍ flap","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Island‍ed flap","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Free‍ flap","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rando‍m flap","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Free flap","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9b8d5a8449b645e0a9f581c7cb4edf79.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/38a465d7aeef48e38e7ebd9b07c4908ex1280x4958.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Free flap</strong></p><p>The picture shows a radial forearm <strong>free flap</strong>. Fasciocutaneous radial forearm free flap is the most common free flap used in head and neck reconstruction. </p>\n<p>A flap defines the tongue of tissue whereas a pedicle is used to describe the base or stem with the vascular supply.</p>\n<p>In a <strong>free flap</strong>, the blood supply is isolated, disconnected, and then reconnected using microsurgery at the new site. Other Eg for free flaps: free fibular flap, DIEP flap (Deep inferior epigastric perforators)</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Axial flaps</strong> are much longer and are based on known blood vessels. Eg: Latissmus Dorsi flap, TRAM (Transverse rectus abdominis myocutaneous) flap.</p>\n<p>Option B: An <strong>islanded flap</strong> is that in which the axial blood supply is swung round on a stalk.</p>\n<p>Option D: A<strong> random flap</strong> includes three sides of a rectangle, bearing no specific relationship to where the blood supply enters. Eg: V-Y plasty, Z plasty, Limberg, or rhomboid flap.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9316","text":"The flap shown in the pict‍ure is which of the follo​wing?","raw_text":"The flap shown in the picture is which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serratus an‍terior flap","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pectoral myocutaneous flap‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"TRAM‍ flap","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"VRAM‍ flap","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Serratus anterior flap","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/94ad53221e514ac3a77321bc5db3053f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Serratus anterior flap</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>serratus anterior flap</strong>. The serratus anterior flap is a highly reliable flap with a consistently long pedicle. Its anatomy permits coverage of <strong>complex wounds</strong>. The underlying rib can be included as a myoosseous flap to expand the versatility of this flap.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 395px; width: 609px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 185.062px; height: 13px;\">\n<p><strong>Classification of muscle flaps</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 185.062px; height: 13px;\"><strong>Based on blood supply</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 181.938px; height: 13px;\">\n<p><strong>Random flap </strong></p>\n<p>Unnamed vessel supplying flap</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 230px; height: 13px;\">\n<p><strong>Axial flap</strong></p>\n<p>Named vessel supplying flap.    </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 108.4px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 185.062px; height: 108.4px;\"> \n<p><strong>Method of movement</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 181.938px; height: 108.4px;\">\n<p><strong>Local transfer </strong></p>\n<p>Rotation flap</p>\n<p>Transposition flap</p>\n<p>Advancement flap</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 230px; height: 108.4px;\">\n<p><strong>Distant transfer </strong></p>\n<p>Pedicle flap</p>\n<p>Free flap</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 185.062px; height: 13px;\">\n<p><strong>Based on composition</strong></p>\n </td>\n<td style=\"width: 181.938px; height: 13px;\">\n<p><strong>Simple Flap</strong></p>\n<p>Cutaneous flap</p>\n<p>Musculocutaneous </p>\n<p>Fasciocutaneous</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 230px; height: 13px;\">\n<p><strong>Composite flap</strong></p>\n<p>Osseocutaneous flap</p>\n<p>Sensory flap</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6437","text":"The image shows a surgical‍ technique used to releas​e Dupuytren's contracture​. Which of the following ‌statements is true regard​ing this?","raw_text":"The image shows a surgical technique used to release Dupuytren's contracture. Which of the following statements is true regarding this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It can also be used in the‍ surgical management of k​eloid scars and contractu​res","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It reorients the direction‍ of the central wound by ​90 degrees","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Triangular flaps created u‍sing 60 degrees can lengt​hen the scar by 50%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a type of local adva‍ncement flap","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It reorients the direction of the central wound by 90 degrees","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/37201123fb36416f88b9ec4abbbe78bd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0c7eaf0c200347cf83fbc89918e701b0x510x468.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It reorients the direction of the central wound by 90 degrees</strong></p><p>The image shows a typical example of a <strong>Z Plasty.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a type of <strong>transposition flap</strong> that incorporates qualities of both advancement and rotation flaps. Z plasty is used for the <strong>lengthening of linear scars</strong>. It is best used in releasing simple contractures.</p>\n<p>Usually, all the limbs are of equal length and ideally, the angle between them is 60°. Two triangular flaps are raised. Theoretically, a <strong>60-degree angle</strong> flap will result in a <strong>75% increase</strong> in the length of the scar and a <strong>90-degree reorientation of central wound.</strong></p>\n<p>Angles smaller than 60 degrees are easier to transpose but result in less lengthening and realignment of the scar to less than 90 degrees. Angles larger than 60 degrees should be avoided because the force required to transpose the flaps increases markedly, making the closure of the wound difficult. </p>\n<p>Relative contraindications to Z-plasty include patients with <strong>keloids</strong> and hypertrophic scarring tendencies. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6441","text":"A truck driver undergoes a‍ Limberg flap operation u​sing a random flap for re​current pilonidal sinus i‌nfections. What is the ge​nerally accepted safe and​ reliable length: width r‌atio for this type of fla‍p?","raw_text":"A truck driver undergoes a Limberg flap operation using a random flap for recurrent pilonidal sinus infections. What is the generally accepted safe and reliable length: width ratio for this type of flap?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.‍5:1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2‍:1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3‍:1","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2.‍5:1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3:1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3:1</strong></p><p>The Limberg flap is a type of <strong>random flap</strong> and the safe and reliable length:width ratio for the random flap<strong> 3:1</strong>.</p>\n<p>Random pattern flaps have a blood supply based on <strong>small, unnamed blood vessels</strong> in the dermal-subdermal plexus, as opposed to the discrete, well described, directional vessels of axial pattern flaps. As random flaps have a variable blood supply, they need an <strong>adequate base (width)</strong> to survive.</p>\n<p>The rhomboid (Limberg) flap involves creating a skin flap with two opposing angles of 60° and 120° at the corners, forming a rhomboid shape positioned directly next to the defect. The design allows the flap to be rotated into the defect, with the donor site closed primarily, ensuring minimal distortion to the surrounding tissues. This method is especially useful in areas like the gluteal region, where complex closure techniques help achieve optimal results.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6444","text":"A free flap is used to res‍urface a tendoachilles de​fect in a 58-year-old chr​onic smoker. As a first-y‌ear surgical resident, yo​u are asked to monitor th​e flap for venous occlusi‌on. Which of the followin‍g should you be looking o‍ut for?","raw_text":"A free flap is used to resurface a tendoachilles defect in a 58-year-old chronic smoker. As a first-year surgical resident, you are asked to monitor the flap for venous occlusion. Which of the following should you be looking out for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increasi‍ng warmth","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increasin‍g paleness","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increasin‍g coolness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increasing sluggishness of‍ pinprick bleeding","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increasing warmth","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increasing warmth</strong></p><p>The first sign of <strong>venous occlusion</strong> in a free flap is <strong>increasing warmth.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Clinical signs of arterial and venous compromise in a free flap:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 199px;\" width=\"482\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153.295px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Clinical Sign</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.295px;\"><strong>Arterial Compromise</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px;\"><strong>Venous Compromise</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153.295px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Color</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.295px;\">Becoming paler</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px;\">Increasingly reddish or purplish</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153.295px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Temperature </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.295px;\">Becoming cooler</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px;\">Becoming warmer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153.295px;\">\n<p>Tissue turgor  </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.295px;\">Reducing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px;\">Increasing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153.295px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Capillary refill time</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.295px;\">Becoming slower </td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px;\">Becoming faster</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153.295px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Pinprick bleeding</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 153.295px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Increasingly sluggish</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.205px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Quickening (and darkening)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p class=\"p1\">Note: Venous and arterial compromise may coexist, and one may lead to the other.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0855","text":"Which of the following is ‍true about the flap given​ in the image.","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the flap given in the image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pedicled TRAM based on sup‍erior epigastric artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Free TRAM based on superio‍r and inferior epigastric​ artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DIEP based on deep inferio‍r epigastric artery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lower Abdominal Fat based ‍inferior epigastric vesse​ls","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. DIEP based on deep inferior epigastric artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5bf83a483d1b47beb5a8c67096f0d29a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bd753d65292d41a180d9a45f7d268338x1280x1547.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. DIEP based on deep inferior epigastric artery</strong></p><p>The given image showing only skin and fat moved to the chest is suggestive of a <strong>deep inferior epigastric artery perforator flap</strong> (DIEP), based on<strong> </strong>the<strong> deep inferior epigastric artery.</strong></p>\n<p>DIEP is a variant of TRAM flap and free flap where only the <strong>skin</strong> and <strong>fat</strong> are moved to the chest. A<strong> microvascular technique</strong> is used in which <strong>no muscle is taken</strong> and the perforating vessels are dissected out.</p>\n<p>As the muscle is spared in DIEP, there is<strong> </strong>a <strong>reduced risk of abdominal wall weakness</strong> and its associated complications. </p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Options A and B: The<strong> pedicled TRAM flap</strong> is based on the superior epigastric artery and the <strong>free TRAM flap</strong> is based on superior and inferior epigastric arteries (supercharged TRAM flap); however, the given image depicts a deep inferior epigastric artery flap. In TRAM flaps: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>skin</strong>, <strong>fat,</strong> and <strong>muscle</strong> are moved to the chest.</li>\n<li>It provides good cosmesis and bulk since it has a soft tissue consistency similar to the breast.</li>\n<li>Disadvantages include epigastric bulge, partial flap loss, fat necrosis, abdominal wall weakness, and hernias.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given image below shows a TRAM flap: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6438","text":"A 30-year-old woman who un‍derwent a skin-sparing ma​stectomy a year ago has r​eturned to your clinic to‌ discuss her options for ​breast reconstruction. Al​l of the following are op‌tions to consider except:‍","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman who underwent a skin-sparing mastectomy a year ago has returned to your clinic to discuss her options for breast reconstruction. All of the following are options to consider except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A TRAM flap provides the b‍est cosmetic result than ​LD flap","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"An LD flap provides adequa‍te bulk of breast tissue","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Reconstruction may be dela‍yed or immediate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Capsular contraction is a ‍dreaded complication of s​ilicone implants","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. An LD flap provides adequate bulk of breast tissue","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/173f8c208dfb4aec85dc9d4073226836x814x664.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b302d387c19045a8b392cff8b0efb11bx878x1006.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. An LD flap provides adequate bulk of breast tissue</strong></p><p>An <strong>LD</strong> flap <strong>does not provide the bulk</strong> required and is usually used along with an implant. It is based on the thoracodorsal pedicle. It is an axial flap. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Transverse rectus abdominus myocutaneous (TRAM) flap</strong> is either based on superior epigastric or inferior epigastric or both (supercharged TRAM). <strong>Skin, fat, and muscle</strong> are moved to the chest. It provides <strong>good cosmesis and bulk</strong>. An implant is usually not required.Since the rectus abdominis muscle is excised in the procedure, it can lead to abdominal wall weakness and hernias.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The timing of reconstruction<strong> may be immediate or delayed</strong>. Impediments to immediate reconstruction include insufficient theatre time and a lack of experienced reconstructive surgeons. In addition, the patient is likely to need post-op radiotherapy, then a delayed reconstruction using a flap often gives better results.</p>\n<p>The easiest type of reconstruction is using a <strong>silicone gel implant under the pectoralis major muscle</strong>. Capsular contraction is a dreaded complication of silicone implants.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4498","text":"A 35-year-old man with ost‍eosarcoma of the mandibul​ar ramus is planned for r​esection and reconstructi‌on with a flap. The flap ​used most commonly in man​dibular reconstruction is‌ based on which of the fo‍llowing vascular structur‍es?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man with osteosarcoma of the mandibular ramus is planned for resection and reconstruction with a flap. The flap used most commonly in mandibular reconstruction is based on which of the following vascular structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior ti‍bial artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Peronea‍l artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior t‍ibial artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dorsalis p‍edis artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Peroneal artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1bbbee37c7fb4ae6bf589b674a83d13cx900x1190.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Peroneal artery</strong></p><p>The most commonly used osseous flap for <strong>mandibular reconstruction</strong> is the <strong>fibular free flap</strong>. The fibular free flap is based on the <strong>peroneal artery and vein</strong>, and the blood supply to the foot should be investigated before harvesting this flap.</p>\n<p>Common flaps and their uses</p>\n<table style=\"width: 412.389px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\"><strong>Name of flap</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px;\"><strong>Use</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.389px;\"><strong>Based on</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">Pectoralis major myocutaneous </td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px;\">Head and neck reconstruction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.389px;\">Pectoral branch of thoracoacromial artery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">Deltopectoral </td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px;\">Head and neck reconstruction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.389px;\">Internal mammary vessels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">Radial forearm </td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px;\">Head and neck reconstruction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.389px;\">Radial vessels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">Abbe Estlander </td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px;\">Midline lip and angle of mouth reconstruction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.389px;\">Labial vessels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">Karapandzic </td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px;\">Lip reconstruction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.389px;\">Labial vessels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">Transverse rectus abdominis myocutaneous (TRAM)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px;\">Breast reconstruction (most commonly used flap)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.389px;\">Superior epigastric vessels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">Free TRAM</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px;\">Breast reconstruction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.389px;\">Inferior epigastric vessels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">Lattisimus dorsi </td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px;\">Breast reconstruction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.389px;\">Thoracodorsal vessels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">Deep inferior epigastric artery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px;\">Breast reconstruction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.389px;\">Deep inferior epigastric artery perforators</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122px;\">Free fibular </td>\n<td style=\"width: 134px;\">Mandibular reconstruction  Andy Gump deformity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.389px;\">Peroneal vessels</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> The <strong>free fibular flap</strong> may also be used to correct the <strong>Andy Gump</strong> deformity. It is an <strong>anterior mandibular defect</strong> that creates the <strong>appearance of an absent chin and lower lip</strong> and <strong>severely retrognathic lower jaw</strong>, which is named after a character in an early 20th-century comic strip.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9375","text":"A 56-year-old patient suff‍ering from alcoholic cirr​hosis receives a healthy ​liver from his biological‌ son. What is this type o​f transplant known as?","raw_text":"A 56-year-old patient suffering from alcoholic cirrhosis receives a healthy liver from his biological son. What is this type of transplant known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Auto‍graft","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Allo‍graft","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Xeno‍graft","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isog‍raft","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Allograft","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Allograft</strong></p><p>Transplant of an organ/tissue from a son to a parent is an example of an<strong> allograft.</strong></p>\n<p>An allograft is transplant of cells/tissues/organs from a <strong>genetically non-identical donor</strong> of the same species.</p>\n<p>Isografts are allografts in which cells/tissues/organs are transplanted from a donor to a genetically identical recipient. (monozygotic twins)</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6439","text":"What incision would you ma‍ke to excise a lesion on ​the bridge of the nose, i​n order to cause minimal ‌scarring?","raw_text":"What incision would you make to excise a lesion on the bridge of the nose, in order to cause minimal scarring?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vert‍ical","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Horiz‍ontal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Obl‍ique","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Circ‍ular","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Horizontal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8bf98b66c9bf499194f9b475091e5e5dx1280x2908.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Horizontal</strong></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">A <strong>horizontal incision</strong> should be taken as the relaxed skin tension lines (Borge's lines) at the bridge of the nose are <strong>horizontal</strong>.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Aligning a surgical incision or excision with skin tension lines results in <strong>minimal tension</strong> across the closure of the defect. This leads to <strong>optimal scar formation</strong> and <strong>minimizes wound contraction.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Skin tension lines reflect internal tension within the skin and are produced by the parallel alignment of <span class=\"term tooltipstered\" data-term-id=\"43\">connective tissue</span> of the <span class=\"term tooltipstered\" data-term-id=\"61\">dermis. Several different skin tension lines have been described. <strong>Borge's lines</strong> which are also called <strong>relaxed skin tension lines are most useful to guide surgical incision. </strong>These are often confused with Langer's lines.</span></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Langer's lines, also called <strong>cleavage lines</strong> are topological lines drawn based on the shape of wounds on cadavers. These were not intended as a guide for surgical incisions and they do not always correspond to the best orientation of skin tension and often run perpendicular to skin folds. </span><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\"><span class=\"term tooltipstered\" data-term-id=\"61\">On the face, these two sets of lines often run at right angles to one another.</span></span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6443","text":"A plastic surgeon performs‍ an upper eyelid reconstr​uction using a tarsoconju​nctival flap for a patien‌t who underwent a sebaceo​us gland carcinoma excisi​on. What is the maximum t‌issue loss for which a pr‍imary repair would have s‍ufficed?","raw_text":"A plastic surgeon performs an upper eyelid reconstruction using a tarsoconjunctival flap for a patient who underwent a sebaceous gland carcinoma excision. What is the maximum tissue loss for which a primary repair would have sufficed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt‍;5%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt‍;10%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt‍;25%","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt‍;40%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. <25%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. &lt;25%</strong></p><p>Defects comprising <strong>&lt;25%</strong> of the upper eyelid can generally be closed <strong>primarily</strong> in pentagonal approximating fashion.</p>\n<p>For defects involving <strong>25–50% </strong>of the upper eyelid, lateral canthotomy and cantholysis can be performed.</p>\n<p>Defects <strong>more than 50% </strong>need proper flap cover.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6440","text":"A mother brings her 3-week‍-old baby to the pediatri​cs OPD, with a lesion on ​his abdomen which has bee‌n growing in size since b​irth. On examination, the​ lesion is red, nodular, ‌and blanches on pressure.‍ What is the next step in‍ management?","raw_text":"A mother brings her 3-week-old baby to the pediatrics OPD, with a lesion on his abdomen which has been growing in size since birth. On examination, the lesion is red, nodular, and blanches on pressure. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Local resection with carbo‍n dioxide laser","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"En bloc excision with skin‍ grafting","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Emollient application and ‍intralesional triamcinalo​ne","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Monitoring o‍f the lesion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Monitoring of the lesion","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/48702bcae0e04a5e90ce95c86d9ce453.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Monitoring of the lesion</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture is suggestive of <strong>infantile hemangioma.</strong> Only <strong>monitoring</strong> of the lesion is required as in 50% of cases, complete <strong>involution is seen by 5 years</strong> of age.</p>\n<p>Hemangioma growth frequently peaks before the first year, and then the lesions enter the involuting phase. The involution phase begins in 50% of children by 5 years of age and in 70% by 7 years of age. It continues until 10 years of age.</p>\n<p>Indications for immediate treatment are complications such as ulceration or those pertaining to the location of the mass (e.g. visual issues due to large hemangioma over eyelid).</p>\n<p>Treatment options include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Beta blockers </strong>- oral propranolol</li>\n<li><strong>Steroids </strong>- intralesional triamcinolone, topical clobetasol, systemic steroids</li>\n<li><strong>Interferon alfa-2a and vincristine </strong>- for steroid-resistant lesions</li>\n<li><strong>Local resection</strong> with cutting cautery or carbon dioxide laser</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"WJqDQY5c","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"49_gi_tract","title":"49.Gi Tract","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MB3030","text":"Which of the following art​eries does not contribute‍ to the blood supply of t‍he stomach?","raw_text":"Which of the following arteries does not contribute to the blood supply of the stomach?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Common hep​atic artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Splenic​ artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gastroduod​enal artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior pancreaticoduoden​al artery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1a1bafeb2dd741d89864a110de70892ex1280x1342.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery</strong></p><p>The <strong>inferior pancreaticoduodenal</strong> <strong>artery</strong> does not contribute to the blood supply of the stomach. It is a branch of the superior mesenteric artery. It supplies the <strong>duodenum (second and third parts)</strong> and the <strong>pancreas (head and uncinate process)</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>arterial supply</strong> of the <strong>stomach</strong> is contributed exclusively by the <strong>celiac trunk</strong> through its branches:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Left gastric artery - supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach</li>\n<li>Splenic artery:\n<ul>\n<li>Short gastric arteries - fundus </li>\n<li>Left gastroepiploic artery - greater curvature</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Common hepatic artery:\n<ul>\n<li>Right gastric artery - lesser curvature</li>\n<li>Gastroduodenal artery - right gastroepiploic artery arises from this and supplies the greater curvature of the stomach.        </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Clinical note: The presence of an <strong>abnormal, tortuous artery</strong> in the <strong>submucosa</strong> of the <strong>lesser curvature</strong> is called the <strong>Dieulafoy's lesion.</strong> As it is superficial, it is more vulnerable to rupture causing significant <strong>haemorrhage</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0575","text":"Ligation of common hepatic​ artery will impair blood‍ supply in:","raw_text":"Ligation of common hepatic artery will impair blood supply in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right gastric and right ga​stroepiploic artery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Right gastric and left gas​tric artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right gastroepiploic and s​hort gastric vessels","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right gastric and short ga​stric vessels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Right gastric and right gastroepiploic artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ded7ec17336540f7a0214afd39c949fax1280x1342.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Right gastric and right gastroepiploic artery</strong></p><p>Ligation of the <strong>common hepatic artery</strong> will cause impairment in the blood supply through its <strong>branches</strong> i.e <strong>r</strong><strong>ight</strong> <strong>gastric</strong> artery and <strong>right gastroepiploic </strong>artery.</p>\n<p>Blood supply of the stomach:</p>\n<p>The arterial supply of the stomach is contributed exclusively by the <strong>branches</strong> of the <strong>celiac trunk. </strong>They are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Left gastric artery </strong>- Supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach</li>\n<li><strong>Splenic artery- </strong>This further divides into:\n<ul>\n<li>Short gastric arteries- Supplies the fundus of the stomach</li>\n<li>Left gastroepiploic artery- Supplies the greater curvature of the stomach</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Common hepatic artery, </strong>which gives the following branches\n<ul>\n<li>Right gastric artery- Supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach</li>\n<li>Gastroduodenal artery- Right gastroepiploic artery arises from this and supplies the greater curvature of the stomach.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3032","text":"A patient presents with a ​recurrence of peptic ulce‍rs. He had undergone vago‍tomy ten years ago for pe‌ptic ulcer disease. You s​uspect this has occurred ​due to the criminal nerve​ of Grassi being missed d‍uring the previous surger​y. Which of the following‌ gives rise to this nerve‍?","raw_text":"A patient presents with a recurrence of peptic ulcers. He had undergone vagotomy ten years ago for peptic ulcer disease. You suspect this has occurred due to the criminal nerve of Grassi being missed during the previous surgery. Which of the following gives rise to this nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior ​vagus nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior v​agus nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Greater spla​nchnic nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lesser spla​nchnic nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Posterior vagus nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f1d8db78aa2f44d490332e627d13b1c9x600x608.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Posterior vagus nerve</strong></p><p>The<strong> posterior vagus nerve</strong> gives rise to the <strong>criminal nerve of Grassi.</strong></p>\n<p>This nerve supplies the <strong>cardia </strong>and<strong> fundus</strong> of the stomach. It is called a criminal nerve<strong> </strong>because it is often <strong>missed during a vagotomy</strong> procedure and can result in <strong>recurrent peptic ulcer </strong>disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Nerve supply of the stomach:</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> sympathetic supply</strong> arises from <strong>T5-T12 spinal segments</strong> and is distributed to the stomach via the greater and lesser splanchnic nerves and the celiac plexus. They perform the following functions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vasoconstriction</li>\n<li>Constriction of the pyloric sphincter and inhibition of gastric motility</li>\n<li>Carry visceral pain sensations</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> parasympathetic supply</strong> arises from the right and left vagus nerves. The <strong>anterior vagal trunk</strong> is formed from the <strong>left vagus nerve</strong>, whereas the <strong>posterior vagal trunk</strong> is formed from the <strong>right vagus nerve. </strong>They perform the following functions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Secretomotor to the gastric glands</li>\n<li>Motor to the gastric musculature</li>\n<li>Relaxation of pyloric sphincter and promoting gastric emptying</li>\n<li>Carry sensations such as fullness, nausea, and probably pain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3033","text":"Which of the following rec​eives the prepyloric vein‍ of Mayo?","raw_text":"Which of the following receives the prepyloric vein of Mayo?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right gastro​epiploic vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left gastroe​piploic vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left gas​tric vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right gas​tric vein","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Right gastric vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/11eaa078829c423ba0e9a53ab3a22ad3x600x694.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Right gastric vein</strong></p><p>The <strong>right gastric vein</strong> receives the <strong>prepyloric vein of Mayo.</strong></p>\n<p>The prepyloric vein serves as the guidepost for the surgeons to <strong>identify</strong> the <strong>pylorus</strong> of the stomach.</p>\n<p>The common pattern of venous drainage of the stomach is as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 148px; width: 471.047px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 233px;\"><strong>Veins of the stomach</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.047px;\"><strong>Drains into</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 233px;\">Right gastric vein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.047px;\">Portal vein</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 233px;\">Left gastric vein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.047px;\">Portal vein</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 233px;\">Right gastroepiploic vein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.047px;\">Superior mesenteric vein</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 233px;\">Left gastroepiploic vein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.047px;\">Splenic vein</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 233px;\">Short gastric veins</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.047px;\">Splenic vein</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3034","text":"Among the following, which​ is the final structure t‍o drain lymphatics from t‍he stomach?","raw_text":"Among the following, which is the final structure to drain lymphatics from the stomach?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coelia​c nodes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intestinal ​lymph trunk","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Para-aor​tic nodes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lumbar ly​mph trunk","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intestinal lymph trunk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c4474447f4ff4c36b8b1669da51eee48x600x545.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intestinal lymph trunk</strong></p><p>Among the given options, the<strong> intestinal lymph trunk </strong>is the last structure to <strong>drain</strong> <strong>lymphatics</strong> from the <strong>stomach. </strong></p>\n<p>The intestinal lymph trunk is a major lymphatic channel in the abdomen. It <strong>receives lymphatics from</strong> the <strong>pre-aortic group</strong> of lymph nodes such as coeliac nodes, superior mesenteric nodes, and inferior mesenteric nodes. The intestinal lymph trunk finally <strong>drains into</strong> the <strong>cisterna chyli.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>lymphatic drainage of the stomach</strong> can be divided into <strong>four zones </strong>as shown in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3035","text":"A 50-year-old woman presen​ted with abdominal pain, ‍nausea, and bilious vomit‍ing. CT scan revealed obs‌truction of the third par​t of the duodenum by a la​rge vessel. Which of the ​following blood vessels i‍s most likely to cause th​e compression?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman presented with abdominal pain, nausea, and bilious vomiting. CT scan revealed obstruction of the third part of the duodenum by a large vessel. Which of the following blood vessels is most likely to cause the compression?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inferior ​vena cava","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right gona​dal vessels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior mesenteric artery​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Abdomin​al aorta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Superior mesenteric artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9dbb633d6f784c45b56796376eae7054x600x1200.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Superior mesenteric artery</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of superior mesenteric artery syndrome or<strong> Wilkie syndrome. </strong>In this condition, the third part of the <strong>duodenum</strong> is <strong>compressed by</strong> the <strong>superior mesenteric artery </strong>causing bowel obstruction.</p>\n<p>The mid-portion of the <strong>third part of the duodenum</strong> lies in the<strong> angle between</strong> the <strong>superior mesenteric artery </strong>anteriorly and the <strong>abdominal aorta</strong> posteriorly.</p>\n<p>Parts of the duodenum and their characteristics:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 970px; width: 848.75px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\"><strong>Parts of the duodenum</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\"><strong>Extent</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 247px;\"><strong>Anterior relations</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.75px;\"><strong>Posterior relations</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\">\n<p><strong>First </strong>(5 cm)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Duodenal end of pylorus to superior duodenal flexure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 247px;\">\n<p>Quadrate lobe of liver</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.75px;\">\n<p>Gastroduodenal artery</p>\n<p>Common bile duct</p>\n<p>Portal vein</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\"><strong>Second </strong>(8 cm)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Superior duodenal flexure to inferior duodenal flexure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 247px;\">\n<p>Right end of greater omentum</p>\n<p>Origin of transverse mesocolon</p>\n<p>Mesentery of upper ascending colon and hepatic flexure</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.75px;\">\n<p>Right psoas major</p>\n<p>Lateral edge of inferior vena cava</p>\n<p>Hilum of right kidney</p>\n<p>Right renal vessels</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\"><strong>Third </strong>(10 cm)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">Inferior duodenal flexure to ascending portion of the fourth part of duodenum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 247px;\">\n<p>Transverse mesocolon</p>\n<p>Origin of the root of mesentery</p>\n<p>Superior mesenteric vessels</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.75px;\">\n<p>Right ureter</p>\n<p>Right gonadal vessels</p>\n<p>Inferior vena cava</p>\n<p>Abdominal aorta</p>\n<p>Right Psoas major</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 102px;\"><strong>Fourth </strong>(2.5 cm)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133px;\">From the left of aorta to duodenojejunal flexure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 247px;\">\n<p>Transverse colon</p>\n<p>Transverse mesocolon</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 236.75px;\">\n<p>Left renal vessels</p>\n<p>Left gonadal vessels</p>\n<p>Abdominal aorta</p>\n<p>Left psoas major</p>\n<p>Left sympathetic trunk</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3036","text":"Which of the following art​eries does not supply the‍ duodenum?","raw_text":"Which of the following arteries does not supply the duodenum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Common hep​atic artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right gast​ric artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Jejunal​ artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Splenic​ artery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Splenic artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5575a1f0c70a401e8618552cbfaf0089x1280x1317.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Splenic artery</strong></p><p>The splenic artery does not supply the duodenum.</p>\n<p>The <strong>major vessels supplying</strong> the <strong>duodenum</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gastroduodenal artery - </strong> branch of the common hepatic artery</li>\n<li><strong>Superior mesenteric</strong> <strong>artery</strong> - anterior branch of the abdominal aorta.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Branches of the gastroduodenal artery supplying the first and second parts of the duodenum and head of the pancreas are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery</li>\n<li>Anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery</li>\n<li>Supraduodenal artery     </li>\n<li>Retroduodenal arteries</li>\n<li>Pancreatic branches </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: The first and second parts of the duodenum also receive minor blood supply from the <strong>right gastric artery</strong> and the <strong>right gastroepiploic artery.</strong></p>\n<p>Branches of the superior mesenteric artery supplying the second, third, and fourth parts of the duodenum, and the head and uncinate process of the pancreas are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery - anterior and posterior branches supply the second and third parts of the duodenum</li>\n<li>First jejunal artery - supply the fourth part of the duodenum</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3038","text":"In which part of the intes​tine is the finding given‍ in the image more promin‍ently seen?","raw_text":"In which part of the intestine is the finding given in the image more prominently seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Il​eum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Jej​unum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Duod​enum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ascendi​ng colon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Jejunum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/16fa14cf840d4993beb092db4177fa0d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Jejunum</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>plicae circulares</strong> which are also known as <strong>valvulae conniventes. </strong>They are more prominent in the <strong>jejunum</strong>.</p>\n<p>Plicae circulares are the <strong>circular mucosal folds.</strong> They frequently branch and stack upon each other, giving the jejunum a <strong>characteristic feathery appearance</strong> in single <strong>contrast radiography</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3039","text":"Identify the correct match​ among the following.","raw_text":"Identify the correct match among the following.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peyer's patch - mesenteric​ border of ileum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Meckel’s diverticulum - me​senteric border of jejenu‍m","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Meckel’s diverticulum - an​timesenteric border of il‍eum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Peyer's patch - antimesent​eric border of jejunum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Meckel’s diverticulum - antimesenteric border of ileum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2026e6c24c674525991310d3dad03044x600x684.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6658c7c6346d47f4938f775d7e8cbc22x1280x1364.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Meckel’s diverticulum - antimesenteric border of ileum</strong></p><p><strong>Meckel's diverticulum</strong> projects from the <strong>antimesenteric border</strong> of the <strong>terminal</strong> <strong>ileum</strong>. It is commonly located 2 feet proximal to the ileocaecal valve.</p>\n<p><strong>Peyer's patches</strong> are lymphoid aggregates present in the submucosa and are arranged longitudinally along the <strong>antimesenteric border </strong>of<strong> ileum.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3040","text":"Which of the following is ​the longest part of the l‍arge intestine?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the longest part of the large intestine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transver​se colon","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Descendi​ng colon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ascendi​ng colon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sigmoi​d colon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Transverse colon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/40d35b7d14144a008b48f053ce3c1c0ex600x771.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Transverse colon</strong></p><p>The <strong>transverse colon</strong> is the <strong>longest part</strong> of the <strong>large intestine</strong>, measuring <strong>50 cm</strong> in length on an average. It is <strong>intraperitoneal</strong> in location and suspended by the transverse mesocolon.</p>\n<p>Lengths of various parts of the large intestine are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 335px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">\n<p><strong>Intestinal segment</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">\n<p><strong>Approximate length</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">\n<p>Caecum</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">\n<p>6 cm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">\n<p>Ascending colon</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">15 to 20 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">\n<p>Transverse colon</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">\n<p>50 cm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">\n<p>Descending colon</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">\n<p>25 to 30 cm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">\n<p>Sigmoid colon</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">\n<p>37.5 cm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">\n<p>Rectum</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">\n<p>15 cm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 160px;\">\n<p>Anal canal</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165px;\">\n<p>2 to 5 cm</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3041","text":"A specimen of the large in​testine is given below. I‍n which of the following ‍is the marked structure u‌sually not present?","raw_text":"A specimen of the large intestine is given below. In which of the following is the marked structure usually not present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sigmoi​d colon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Descendi​ng colon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transver​se colon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cae​cum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Caecum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c36d888aeb544cfdb6ad76a17f596106.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/58c2d81f38154a1b8bf8a86e6af32be0x600x467.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Caecum</strong></p><p>The structures marked in the image are<strong> appendices epiploicae</strong> and they are usually <strong>absent </strong>in the<strong> caecum.</strong></p>\n<p>Appendices epiploicae are small<strong> fat-filled peritoneal pouches</strong> scattered over the surface of the large intestine. They are very <strong>abundant</strong> on the surface of the<strong> sigmoid colon</strong> and tend to be <strong>absent</strong> from <strong>caecum, rectum, </strong>and <strong>appendix.</strong></p>\n<p>The below image shows the appendices epiploicae.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3042","text":"An elderly man with athero​sclerosis presented with ‍abdominal pain and blood-‍stained stools. CECT abdo‌men is suggestive of isch​emic colitis at the splen​ic flexure involving the ​marginal artery of Drummo‍nd. What are the arteries​ contributing to the form‌ation of this structure?","raw_text":"An elderly man with atherosclerosis presented with abdominal pain and blood-stained stools.  CECT abdomen is suggestive of ischemic colitis at the splenic flexure involving the marginal artery of Drummond. What are the arteries contributing to the formation of this structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right colic and left colic​ arteries","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ileocolic and left colic a​rteries","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left colic and middle coli​c arteries","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Left colic and sigmoid art​eries","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Left colic and middle colic arteries","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e0e97a8c8aa3448a8c69bff4f49e06fex600x771.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Left colic and middle colic arteries</strong></p><p>The<strong> middle </strong>and<strong> left colic arteries</strong> contribute to the formation of the <strong>marginal artery </strong>of<strong> Drummond </strong>at the splenic flexure. The<strong> splenic flexure (Griffith's point) </strong>is a<strong> watershed area </strong>of the colon which is more prone to<strong> ischemia. </strong></p>\n<p>The anastomotic vessel that runs in the mesentery along the inner margin of the colon is called the marginal artery of Drummond. From this artery, numerous small branches are given to the large bowel. It is formed by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Branches of <strong>superior mesenteric artery - </strong>middle colic, right colic, and ileocolic arteries</li>\n<li>Branches of <strong>inferior mesenteric artery - </strong>left colic, sigmoid, and superior rectal arteries</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When ischemia occurs at Griffith's point, the blood supply in this region is augmented by another arterial loop called the <strong>inner arterial arc (of Riolan).</strong> This arc connects the main trunk of the <strong>middle colic artery</strong> with the ascending branch of the<strong> left colic artery</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Sudeck's point</strong> is another watershed area present at the <strong>rectosigmoid junction</strong>. The <strong>sigmoid artery</strong> anastomoses with the <strong>superior rectal artery</strong> at this site.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3043","text":"A 10-year-old boy complain​ed of nausea, anorexia an‍d abdominal pain. On exam‍ination, tenderness was n‌oted at McBurney's point ​and ilio-psoas sign was p​ositive. You suspect infl​ammation of the organ sho‍wn in the image. What is ​its most common anatomica‌l position?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old boy complained of nausea, anorexia and abdominal pain. On examination, tenderness was noted at McBurney's point and ilio-psoas sign was positive. You suspect inflammation of the organ shown in the image. What is its most common anatomical position?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4f929f71ea8d441ab798c79f6e36a3a4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6409c8188a264491b6390f34f61b5d6dx600x742.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario points towards <strong>appendicitis</strong> and the <strong>most common position</strong> of the <strong>appendix</strong> is <strong>retrocaecal. </strong>This has been marked as position '2' in the image. This is because the appendix develops during the descent of the colon in embryonic life.</p>\n<p>The <strong>base</strong> of the appendix is <strong>fixed</strong> and depending on the <strong>direction of the tip</strong>, the <strong>positions</strong> of the appendix are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Retrocaecal</li>\n<li>Pelvic </li>\n<li>Paracolic </li>\n<li>Subcaecal</li>\n<li>Pre ileal </li>\n<li>Post ileal </li>\n<li>Promonteric </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical note:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Ilio-psoas sign</strong> - Pain elicited on extension of the hip, seen in <strong>retrocaecal</strong> appendicitis.</li>\n<li><strong>Obturator sign</strong> - Pain elicited on internal rotation of the hip, seen in <strong>pelvic</strong> appendicitis.</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3044","text":"Which of the following is ​a content of the lateral ‍rectal ligament?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a content of the lateral rectal ligament?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right superior rectal arte​ry","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left superior rectal arter​y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Middle rec​tal artery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior re​ctal artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Middle rectal artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eca9067fab894099a62207b0600ca7adx600x643.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Middle rectal artery</strong></p><p>The <strong>middle rectal artery</strong> is a content of the <strong>lateral rectal</strong> <strong>ligament.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>condensation of</strong> the <strong>endopelvic fascia</strong> that surrounds the middle rectal vessels is referred to as the lateral rectal ligament<strong>. </strong>Branches of the inferior hypogastric plexus also enter the rectum through this ligament.</p>\n<p><strong>Blood supply of the rectum:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 521px;\" width=\"817\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 178.641px; height: 37px;\"><strong>Artery</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.188px; height: 37px;\"><strong>Branch of</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 221.484px; height: 37px;\"><strong>Location</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.688px; height: 37px;\"><strong>Parts supplied</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 71px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 178.641px; height: 71px;\"><strong>Superior rectal artery</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.188px; height: 71px;\">Inferior mesenteric artery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 221.484px; height: 71px;\">Mesorectum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.688px; height: 71px;\">Upper two-thirds of the rectum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 105px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 178.641px; height: 105px;\"><strong>Middle rectal artery</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.188px; height: 105px;\">Anterior division of the internal iliac artery or inferior vesical artery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 221.484px; height: 105px;\">Lateral rectal ligament</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.688px; height: 105px;\">Middle third of the rectum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 233.203px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 178.641px; height: 233.203px;\"><strong>Inferior rectal artery</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.188px; height: 233.203px;\">Internal pudendal artery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 221.484px; height: 233.203px;\">Ischio-rectal fossa</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.688px; height: 233.203px;\">\n<p>Distal third of the rectum</p>\n<p>Anal canal with internal and external anal sphincters</p>\n<p>Perianal skin</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3047","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about the muscles‍ of the anal canal is fal‍se?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about the muscles of the anal canal is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Internal anal sphincter is​ the thickened terminal p‍art of the inner circular‍ muscle of large intestin‌e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The conjoint longitudinal ​muscle plays an important‍ role in the prevention o‍f haemorrhoids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Treitz's muscle is the sub​mucosal muscle of the ana‍l canal that prevents hae‍morrhoids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The external anal sphincte​r is the continuation of ‍the outer longitudinal mu‍scle of the rectum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The external anal sphincter is the continuation of the outer longitudinal muscle of the rectum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5d54eac3a35046bf88def0315997ac41x600x602.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The external anal sphincter is the continuation of the outer longitudinal muscle of the rectum</strong></p><p>The <strong>external anal sphincter</strong> is made up of <strong>striated muscle fibres.</strong> It is not a continuation of the outer longitudinal muscle of the rectum</p>\n<p>The external anal sphincter is made up of <strong>two parts</strong> - upper(deep) and lower (subcutaneous or superficial). The<strong> pudendal nerve</strong> innervates the external anal sphincter and it is under <strong>voluntary control.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Muscles of the anal canal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>External anal sphincter</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Internal anal sphincter: </strong>It is the terminal thickened part of the <strong>inner circular muscle</strong> of the large intestine. It extends from the anorectal junction to the anal verge. It is innervated by the <strong>autonomic nervous system:</strong> parasympathetic - relaxation; sympathetic - contraction.</li>\n<li><strong>Conjoint longitudinal muscle: </strong>It lies <strong>between the internal anal sphincter</strong> and the <strong>external anal sphincter.</strong> It is the continuation of the <strong>outer</strong> <strong>longitudinal muscle</strong> of the rectum. The muscle fibres distally fan out and traverse the internal and external anal sphincter. It has a <strong>protective effect</strong> on <strong>haemorrhoids</strong>.<strong> </strong>This muscle degenerates as age advances.</li>\n<li><strong>Treitz's muscle: </strong>It is the <strong>submucosal muscle</strong> of the anal canal. It is attached to the conjoint longitudinal muscle by the <strong>Park's ligament.</strong> It <strong>maintains</strong> the <strong>integrity</strong> of the <strong>anal cushions</strong> and plays an important role in <strong>preventing haemorrhoids</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3049","text":"A 60-year-old man presente​d with a painful mass at ‍the anus associated with ‍bleeding. Examination rev‌ealed external haemorrhoi​ds. Which of the followin​g nerves is responsible f​or carrying the pain sens‍ation?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presented with a painful mass at the anus associated with bleeding. Examination revealed external haemorrhoids. Which of the following nerves is responsible for carrying the pain sensation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parasympathetic fibres S2,​ 3, and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pudendal nerve S2, 3, and ​4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sympathetic fibres L1 and ​2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Least splanchnic nerve T12​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pudendal nerve S2, 3, and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7a197dfefac7447da07c699ed24d6748x600x425.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pudendal nerve S2, 3, and 4</strong></p><p>The nerve mediating <strong>pain</strong> in <strong>external haemorrhoids</strong> is <strong>pudendal nerve </strong>S2, 3, and 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Differences between external and internal haemorrhoids:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 199px;\" width=\"819\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 402.517px;\"><strong>External haemorrhoids</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 402.517px;\"><strong>Internal haemorrhoids</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 402.517px;\">Located distal to the dentate line</td>\n<td style=\"width: 402.517px;\">Located proximal<strong> </strong>to the dentate line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 402.517px;\">Innervated by the inferior rectal branch of pudendal nerve S2, 3, and 4 (somatic nerve)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 402.517px;\">Innervated by parasympathetic fibres S2, 3, and 4, and sympathetic fibres L1 and 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 402.517px;\">Pain sensitive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 402.517px;\">Pain insensitive</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0560","text":"How many transverse mucosa​l folds are commonly pres‍ent in the rectum?","raw_text":"How many transverse mucosal folds are commonly present in the rectum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5fe3ebd3696a4d0799789fb72d8fbe7ax600x602.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3</strong></p><p><strong>Three </strong>transverse mucosal folds (also known as <strong>Houston's valves</strong>) are commonly present in the <strong>rectum</strong>.</p>\n<p>The transverse mucosal folds are <strong>permanent</strong> and become very <strong>prominent on</strong> <strong>distension </strong>of the rectum.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>superior transverse fold</strong> - present at <strong>either side</strong> of the beginning of the rectum and may occasionally encircle the entire lumen.</li>\n<li>The <strong>middle transverse fold</strong> - <strong>largest</strong> and <strong>constant</strong> fold present above the rectal ampulla. It projects from the <strong>anterior </strong>and<strong> right wall</strong> of the rectum.</li>\n<li>The <strong>inferior transverse fold -</strong> <strong>most variable</strong> and found on the<strong> left </strong>side.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Apart from these, the rectal mucosa has <strong>several longitudinal folds,</strong> which <strong>disappear on distension</strong> of the rectum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"AVbRjjlr","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"49_communication_for_health_education","title":"49.Communication For Health Education","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MA3176","text":"What is the correct order ‍for the process of commun​ication?","raw_text":"What is the correct order for the process of communication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Message-&amp;gt;Sender-&amp;gt;Cha‍nnel-&amp;gt;Feedback-&amp;gt;Rec​eiver","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sender-&amp;gt;Message-&amp;gt;Cha‍nnel-&amp;gt;Receiver-&amp;gt;Fee​dback","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Message-&amp;gt;Sender-&amp;gt;Rec‍eiver-&amp;gt;Channel-&amp;gt;Fee​dback","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sender-&amp;gt;Channel-&amp;gt;Mes‍sage-&amp;gt;Feedback-&amp;gt;Rec​eiver","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sender->Message->Channel->Receiver->Feedback","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sender-&gt;Message-&gt;Channel-&gt;Receiver-&gt;Feedback</strong></p><p class=\"heading\">The correct order for the process of communication is:</p>\n<p class=\"heading\">Sender→Message→Channel→Receiver→Feedback</p>\n<p class=\"heading\">The <strong>sender</strong> (communicator) is the originator of the message. A <strong>message</strong> is the information which the communicator transmits to his audience. <strong>Channel</strong> is the media of communication between the sender and the receiver (e.g., interpersonal communication, mass media, traditional or folk media). The audience who receives, understands, and acts upon the message is called the <strong>receiver</strong>. <strong>Feedback</strong> is the reaction to the message which flows from the receiver to the sender.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3178","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a didactic type of co​mmunication?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a didactic type of communication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Newsp‍apers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ra‍dio","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lect‍ures","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Work‍shop","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Workshop","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Workshop</strong></p><p><strong>Workshop</strong> is an example of <strong>Socratic</strong> type of communication.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 421.391px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 353px;\"><strong>Didactic communication</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 433.391px;\"><strong><strong>Socratic </strong></strong><strong>communication</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 353px;\"><strong>One-way</strong> communication</td>\n<td style=\"width: 433.391px;\"><strong>Two-way</strong> communication</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 353px;\">Knowledge is imposed and learning is <strong>authoritative</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 433.391px;\">Learning is <strong>active</strong> and democratic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 353px;\">No audience participation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 433.391px;\">Audience share opinions, ideas, and questions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 353px;\">Does not influence human behavior</td>\n<td style=\"width: 433.391px;\">Influences human behavior</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 353px;\">Feedback absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 433.391px;\">Feedback present</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Examples of <strong>didactic</strong> communication include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lectures</li>\n<li>Demonstrations</li>\n<li>Television</li>\n<li>Radio</li>\n<li>Magazines</li>\n<li>Newspapers</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3179","text":"Which of the following is ‍a Socratic type of commun​ication?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a Socratic type of communication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Panel di‍scussion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lect‍ures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Demonst‍rations","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Jour‍nals","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Panel discussion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Panel discussion</strong></p><p>A <strong>panel discussion</strong> is an example of Socratic type of communication. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 406.09px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 353px;\"><strong>Didactic communication</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 418.09px;\"><strong><strong>Socratic </strong></strong><strong>communication</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 353px;\"><strong>One-way</strong> communication</td>\n<td style=\"width: 418.09px;\"><strong>Two-way</strong> communication</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 353px;\">Knowledge is imposed and learning is <strong>authoritative</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 418.09px;\">Learning is <strong>active</strong> and democratic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 353px;\">No audience participation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 418.09px;\">Audience share opinions, ideas, and questions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 353px;\">Does not influence human behavior</td>\n<td style=\"width: 418.09px;\">Influences human behavior</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 353px;\">Feedback absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 418.09px;\">Feedback present</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Examples of Socratic communication include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Symposium</li>\n<li>Panel discussions</li>\n<li>Workshops</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3211","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is not true about​ mass media?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true about mass media?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It has increased influence‍ only in below-average ed​ucated population","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It gives greater support f‍or concentrated programme​s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mass media alone is inadeq‍uate in changing human be​havior","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mass media reaches the wid‍est population","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It has increased influence only in below-average educated population","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It has increased influence only in below-average educated population</strong></p><p>The advantage of mass media includes that it has increased influence across all educational levels, <strong>especially</strong> in <strong>above-average </strong>educational level.</p>\n<p>Mass media includes <strong>TV, newspaper, radio</strong>, etc.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Advantages:\n<ul>\n<li>It reaches people even in the most <strong>remote areas</strong>.</li>\n<li>It gets <strong>public attention</strong>.</li>\n<li>It has increased influence across all educational levels, especially in above-average educational level.</li>\n<li>It gives greater support for <strong>concentrated programmes.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Disadvantages:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One-way</strong> communication</li>\n<li>Impersonal method and no face-to-face contact</li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Mass media alone is inadequate in changing human behavior.</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3182","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a function of health ​communication?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a function of health communication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Community p‍articipation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Motiv‍ation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Organi‍zation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Couns‍elling","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Community participation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Community participation</strong></p><p>Community participation is not a function of health communication.</p>\n<p>The <strong>functions</strong> <strong>of health communication</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Information</li>\n<li>Education</li>\n<li>Motivation</li>\n<li>Persuasion</li>\n<li>Counselling</li>\n<li>Raising morals</li>\n<li>Health development</li>\n<li>Organization</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"width: 516px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 936px; text-align: center;\">Methods of Health Communication</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Individual approach</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 285.094px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Group approach</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 433.906px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mass approach</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Personal contact</li>\n<li>Home visits</li>\n<li>Personal letters</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285.094px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Lectures</li>\n<li>Demonstrations</li>\n<li>Discussion methods\n<ul>\n<li>Group discussions</li>\n<li>Panel discussion</li>\n<li>Symposium</li>\n<li>Workshop</li>\n<li>Conferences</li>\n<li>Seminars</li>\n<li>Role play</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 433.906px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>TV</li>\n<li>Radio</li>\n<li>Newspaper</li>\n<li>Printed material</li>\n<li>Direct mailing</li>\n<li>Posters</li>\n<li>Health museums</li>\n<li>Health exhibitions</li>\n<li>Folk method</li>\n<li>Internet</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3187","text":"Which of the following is ‍not an approach to health​ education?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an approach to health education?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Regulator‍y approach","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Service ‍approach","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Socio-environmental approa‍ch","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Primary ca‍re approach","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Socio-environmental approach","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Socio-environmental approach</strong></p><p>Socio-environmental approach is not an approach to health education.</p>\n<p>There are <strong>four</strong> approaches to <strong>health education:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Regulatory approach-</strong> <span class=\"fontstyle0\">external control or laws placed on people by the government. </span>Ex: Child Marriage Act, No smoking in public places, Helmet law</p>\n<p><strong>Service approach-</strong> government <span class=\"fontstyle0\">aims at providing health services to the people at their doorsteps. Its drawback is that people will not accept a service, even if it is offered free of cost unless it is based on their felt needs. Ex: Government provided latrines free of cost yet people did not use them because its need was not felt</span></p>\n<p><strong>Health education approach-</strong> creates <strong>awareness</strong> of health needs and problems. Awareness leads to <strong>motivation</strong> which includes the stages of <strong>interest</strong>, <strong>evaluation</strong>, and <strong>decision-making</strong>. It is followed by acceptance of the new idea which becomes part of his existing values, known as <strong>internalization</strong>. Ex: Adoption of ORS for diarrhea</p>\n<p><strong>Primary health care approach- </strong>delivery of health services with the <strong>active involvement</strong> of people. Principles of <strong>community participation</strong> and <strong>intersectoral coordination</strong> are stressed here. Its objective is to help individuals become <strong>self-reliant</strong> in health matters. Ex: P<span class=\"fontstyle0\">eople receive guidance from healthcare providers (Rogi Kalyana Samiti, Mahila Arogya Samiti).</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3190","text":"In which model of health e‍ducation does internaliza​tion occur?","raw_text":"In which model of health education does internalization occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medica‍l model","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Socio-enviro‍nmental model","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Motivati‍on model","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Servic‍e model","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Motivation model","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Motivation model</strong></p><p><strong>Internalization</strong> belongs to the <strong>motivation</strong> model.</p>\n<p>There are <strong>three models </strong>of health education:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Medical</strong> model: transfer of proven medical information into education. It does<span class=\"fontstyle0\"> not bridge the gap between knowledge and behavior.</span></li>\n<li><strong>Motivation</strong> model: creates <strong>awareness</strong> of health needs and problems. Awareness leads to <strong>motivation</strong> which includes stages of interest, evaluation, and decision-making. It is followed by acceptance of the new idea which becomes part of his existing values, known as <strong>internalization</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Social intervention</strong> model: focuses on changing the social environment which subsequently shapes the behavior of individual and the community.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3192","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a principle of health​ education?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a principle of health education?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Partic‍ipation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Learning‍ by doing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Setting a‍n example","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Drawing c‍onclusions","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Drawing conclusions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Drawing conclusions</strong></p><p>Drawing conclusions is not a principle of health education.</p>\n<p><strong class=\"ng-binding\">Principles of Health Education:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li>Participation</li>\n<li>Learning by doing</li>\n<li>Setting an example</li>\n<li>Good human relations</li>\n<li>Feedback</li>\n<li>Credibility</li>\n<li>Leaders</li>\n<li>Interest</li>\n<li>Comprehension</li>\n<li>Known to unknown</li>\n<li>Motivation</li>\n<li>Reinforcement</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Known to unknown </strong>means to start where the people are and with what they understand and then proceed to new knowledge.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3184","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements about health educ​ation is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about health education is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It aids in developing a re‍flective behavior","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It appeals‍ to emotion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is behav‍ior centered","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is activ‍ely acquired","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It appeals to emotion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It appeals to emotion</strong></p><p>Health education appeals to people’s <strong>reason</strong>. Health propaganda appeals to people’s <strong>emotions.</strong></p>\n<p>Health Education vs. Propaganda:</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 510px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 323.539px;\"><strong>Health education </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 329.461px;\"><strong>Health propaganda</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Knowledge &amp; Skills</td>\n<td style=\"width: 323.539px;\">Knowledge and skills are <strong>actively</strong> acquired</td>\n<td style=\"width: 329.461px;\">Knowledge is <strong>instilled</strong> in the mind</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Primitive desires</td>\n<td style=\"width: 323.539px;\">Disciplined</td>\n<td style=\"width: 329.461px;\">Aroused</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Action</td>\n<td style=\"width: 323.539px;\"><strong>Reflective</strong> behavior- trains people to use judgement before acting</td>\n<td style=\"width: 329.461px;\"><strong>Reflexive</strong> behavior- aims at impulsive action </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Emotion </td>\n<td style=\"width: 323.539px;\">Does not appeal to emotion but appeals to <strong>reason</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 329.461px;\">Appeals to <strong>emotion</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Process</td>\n<td style=\"width: 323.539px;\"><strong>Behavior</strong> centered</td>\n<td style=\"width: 329.461px;\"><strong>Information</strong> centered</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3197","text":"Which is the best method f‍or counseling a patient w​ho has tested positive fo​r HIV?","raw_text":"Which is the best method for counseling a patient who has tested positive for HIV?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Demonst‍rations","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Role‍ play","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Group di‍scussions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Individua‍l approach","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Individual approach","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Individual approach</strong></p><p><strong>Individual approach</strong> is the preferred counseling method for <strong>HIV pre- and post-testing</strong>. This approach will equip the individual and family to deal with the situation more effectively.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 516px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 936px; text-align: center;\">Methods of Health Communication  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Individual approach</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 285.094px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Group approach</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 433.906px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mass approach</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Personal contact</li>\n<li>Home visits</li>\n<li>Personal letters</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285.094px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Lectures</li>\n<li>Demonstrations</li>\n<li>Discussion methods\n<ul>\n<li>Group discussions</li>\n<li>Panel discussion</li>\n<li>Symposium</li>\n<li>Workshop</li>\n<li>Conferences</li>\n<li>Seminars</li>\n<li>Role play</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 433.906px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>TV</li>\n<li>Radio</li>\n<li>Newspaper</li>\n<li>Printed material</li>\n<li>Direct mailing</li>\n<li>Posters</li>\n<li>Health museums</li>\n<li>Health exhibitions</li>\n<li>Folk method</li>\n<li>Internet</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3196","text":"Flannelgraphs, exhibits, a‍nd flipcharts are used to​ demonstrate the effect o​f injudicious use of anti‌biotics. What is this met​hod of group communicatio​n known as?","raw_text":"Flannelgraphs, exhibits, and flipcharts are used to demonstrate the effect of injudicious use of antibiotics. What is this method of group communication known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chalk a‍nd Talk","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Symp‍osium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mass ‍media","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Demons‍tration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chalk and Talk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chalk and Talk</strong></p><p>Flannelgraphs, exhibits, and flipcharts belong to the <strong>chalk and talk method</strong> of group communication, in which the chalk is the visual component.</p>\n<p>It is also known as <strong>lectures</strong>. Flannelgraphs, flipcharts, exhibits, films, and charts come under this method. Disadvantages of lectures are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Minimum interaction</li>\n<li>Do not stimulate thinking or problem solving</li>\n<li>Comprehension is dependent on the individual</li>\n<li>Do not influence behavior directly</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"width: 516px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 936px; text-align: center;\">Methods of Health Communication  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Individual approach</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 285.094px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Group approach</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 433.906px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mass approach</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Personal contact</li>\n<li>Home visits</li>\n<li>Personal letters</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285.094px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Lectures</li>\n<li>Demonstrations</li>\n<li>Discussion methods\n<ul>\n<li>Group discussions</li>\n<li>Panel discussion</li>\n<li>Symposium</li>\n<li>Workshop</li>\n<li>Conferences</li>\n<li>Seminars</li>\n<li>Role play</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 433.906px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>TV</li>\n<li>Radio</li>\n<li>Newspaper</li>\n<li>Printed material</li>\n<li>Direct mailing</li>\n<li>Posters</li>\n<li>Health museums</li>\n<li>Health exhibitions</li>\n<li>Folk method</li>\n<li>Internet</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3200","text":"A group discussion was con‍ducted among villagers to​ assess their knowledge o​n personal hygiene. Which‌ of the following stateme​nts is false regarding th​is format of health educa​tion?","raw_text":"A group discussion was conducted among villagers to assess their knowledge on personal hygiene. Which of the following statements is false regarding this format of health education?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Many different topics can ‍be spoken about in one di​scussion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A group leader guides the ‍conversation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It can bring about change ‍in the attitude of partic​ipants","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It usually consists of 6 t‍o 12 people","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Many different topics can be spoken about in one discussion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Many different topics can be spoken about in one discussion</strong></p><p>A group discussion discusses <strong>one topic</strong> on its agenda.</p>\n<p>Group discussion is an aggregation of people interacting in a <strong>face-to-face </strong>manner<strong>. </strong>Exchange of ideas, knowledge, and opinions is done by participants which <strong>influence</strong> a change in their <strong>attitudes</strong>.</p>\n<p>It usually involves <strong>6–12</strong> people with a <strong>group leader</strong> who guides the conversation, giving equal opportunity to everyone to voice themselves. In a group discussion, <strong>everyone must speak</strong> and be open to criticism without interrupting others.</p>\n<p>A limitation to this form of health education is that not everyone is open to speaking in public. There is also a high possibility of <strong>diversion</strong> <strong>of the topic</strong> of discussion when many people are involved.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 516px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 936px; text-align: center;\">Methods of Health Communication  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Individual approach</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 285.094px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Group approach</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 433.906px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mass approach</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Personal contact</li>\n<li>Home visits</li>\n<li>Personal letters</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285.094px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Lectures</li>\n<li>Demonstrations</li>\n<li>Discussion methods\n<ul>\n<li>Group discussions</li>\n<li>Panel discussion</li>\n<li>Symposium</li>\n<li>Workshop</li>\n<li>Conferences</li>\n<li>Seminars</li>\n<li>Role play</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 433.906px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>TV</li>\n<li>Radio</li>\n<li>Newspaper</li>\n<li>Printed material</li>\n<li>Direct mailing</li>\n<li>Posters</li>\n<li>Health museums</li>\n<li>Health exhibitions</li>\n<li>Folk method</li>\n<li>Internet</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3203","text":"Which of the following is ‍false about panel discuss​ion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about panel discussion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It usually consists of fou‍r to eight experts","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"No moderator‍ is required","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"No specific ‍agenda is set","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Members of the audience ar‍e invited to take part at​ the end","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. No moderator is required","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. No moderator is required</strong></p><p>A <strong>moderator </strong>is <strong>required</strong> to regulate the panel discussion.</p>\n<p>In a panel discussion, <strong>4 to 8 experts</strong> have a discussion on a topic in front of an audience. No speeches or order is set beforehand. The audience is invited to take part after the panelists speak and ask questions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3204","text":"Match the following: 1. Wo‍rkshop A. Series of lectu​res on a subject by exper​ts after which the audien‌ce is allowed to ask ques​tions 2. Symposium B. Inc​orporates a variety of me​thods like speeches, disc‍ussions, and may last for‌ half a day to a week. 3.‌ Role play C. Series of m‍eetings emphasizing indiv​idual work within the gro‌up 4. Seminar D. Dramatiz‍ation of social situation‌s for health education","raw_text":"<p>Match the following:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 418px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.289px;\">1. Workshop</td>\n<td style=\"width: 298.711px;\">\n<p>A. Series of lectures on a subject by experts after which the audience is allowed to ask questions</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.289px;\">2. Symposium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 298.711px;\">B. Incorporates a variety of methods like speeches, discussions, and may last for half a day to a week.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.289px;\">3. Role play</td>\n<td style=\"width: 298.711px;\">C. Series of meetings emphasizing individual work within the group</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.289px;\">4. Seminar</td>\n<td style=\"width: 298.711px;\">\n<p>D. Dramatization of social situations for health education</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-B, 2-C,‍ 3-D, 4-A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-C, 2-A,‍ 3-D, 4-B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-A, 2-B,‍ 3-C, 4-D","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-D, 2-A,‍ 3-B, 4-C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B</strong></p><p>The correct match is 1-C, 2-A, 3-D, 4-B</p>\n<p>A <strong>workshop</strong> is a series of <strong>meetings </strong>with an emphasis on <strong>individual work</strong> within the group. External consultants or experts may be involved.</p>\n<p>A <strong>symposium</strong> is a series of <strong>lectures</strong> on a selected subject by experts after which the audience is allowed to ask questions (no discussion is involved).</p>\n<p><strong>Role play</strong> is the <strong>dramatization</strong> of social situations for health education by those taking part in it.</p>\n<p><strong>Seminars </strong>are similar to conferences and incorporate a variety of methods like speeches, discussions for health education, which may last for half a day to a week.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 516px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 936px; text-align: center;\">Methods of Health Communication    </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Individual approach</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 285.094px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Group approach</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 433.906px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mass approach</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Personal contact</li>\n<li>Home visits</li>\n<li>Personal letters</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285.094px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Lectures</li>\n<li>Demonstrations</li>\n<li>Discussion methods\n<ul>\n<li>Group discussions</li>\n<li>Panel discussion</li>\n<li>Symposium</li>\n<li>Workshop</li>\n<li>Conferences</li>\n<li>Seminars</li>\n<li>Role play</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 433.906px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>TV</li>\n<li>Radio</li>\n<li>Newspaper</li>\n<li>Printed material</li>\n<li>Direct mailing</li>\n<li>Posters</li>\n<li>Health museums</li>\n<li>Health exhibitions</li>\n<li>Folk method</li>\n<li>Internet</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3206","text":"The Indian Association of ‍Paediatrics is organizing​ a workshop on neonatal r​esuscitation. Which of th‌e following best describe​s this program?","raw_text":"The Indian Association of Paediatrics is organizing a workshop on neonatal resuscitation. Which of the following best describes this program?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Discussion of 4-8 experts ‍in front of an audience","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Discussion between 6-12 me‍mbers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Series of four or more int‍eractive meetings","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Series of speeches on a gi‍ven subject","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Series of four or more interactive meetings","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Series of four or more interactive meetings</strong></p><p>A <strong>workshop</strong> is a <strong>series of meetings</strong> with an emphasis on individual work within the group, using external consultants or experts. It helps increase <strong>individual efficiency</strong> as a professional worker.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3194","text":"All of the following are t‍ypes of combined audio-vi​sual aids except: Flannel​graph Podcasts Slide-tape‌ combination Cinema Tape ​recorders","raw_text":"<p>All of the following are types of combined audio-visual aids except:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Flannelgraph</li>\n<li>Podcasts</li>\n<li>Slide-tape combination</li>\n<li>Cinema</li>\n<li>Tape recorders</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2,‍ and 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1 and ‍4 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 and ‍2 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 3,‍ and 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1, 2, and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1, 2, and 5</strong></p><p>Flannelgraphs, podcasts, and tape recorders are not combined audio-visual (A-V) aids.</p>\n<p>Flannelgraph is a rough flannel fixed over a wooden board used for displaying pictures, cut-outs, etc. It is only a visual aid. Podcasts and tape recorders are auditory aids.</p>\n<p>Audio-visual aids are classified into <strong>three groups</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Auditory</strong> aids: Radio, tape recorders, podcasts</li>\n<li><strong>Visual </strong>aids: Chalkboards, flannelgraphs, charts, posters, slides</li>\n<li><strong>Combined A-V</strong> aids: Cinema, television, slide-tape combination</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3198","text":"A Norovirus outbreak occur‍s in an urban slum where ​you are posted as the PHC​ medical officer. Which i‌s the best method to teac​h the people about ORS?","raw_text":"A Norovirus outbreak occurs in an urban slum where you are posted as the PHC medical officer. Which is the best method to teach the people about ORS?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Group di‍scussions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Role ‍plays","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Home ‍visits","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Demonst‍rations","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Demonstrations","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Demonstrations</strong></p><p>The best method to teach an urban slum about ORS is by <strong>demonstrations</strong>.</p>\n<p>Demonstration is a carefully prepared presentation on how to perform a certain task or skill or procedure. It shows the <strong>step-by-step</strong> process of a task. It <strong>arouses interest</strong> and<strong> </strong>incites <strong>‘learning by doing</strong>,<strong>’ </strong>and <strong>‘seeing is believing’</strong> philosophies.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 516px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 936px; text-align: center;\">Methods of Health Communication  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 217px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Individual approach</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 285.094px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Group approach</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 433.906px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mass approach</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 217px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Personal contact</li>\n<li>Home visits</li>\n<li>Personal letters</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 285.094px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Lectures</li>\n<li>Demonstrations</li>\n<li>Discussion methods\n<ul>\n<li>Group discussions</li>\n<li>Panel discussion</li>\n<li>Symposium</li>\n<li>Workshop</li>\n<li>Conferences</li>\n<li>Seminars</li>\n<li>Role play</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 433.906px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>TV</li>\n<li>Radio</li>\n<li>Newspaper</li>\n<li>Printed material</li>\n<li>Direct mailing</li>\n<li>Posters</li>\n<li>Health museums</li>\n<li>Health exhibitions</li>\n<li>Folk method</li>\n<li>Internet</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3215","text":"Which of the following is ‍used to obtain consensus ​forecasts among a large n​umber of people?","raw_text":"Which of the following is used to obtain consensus forecasts among a large number of people?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Telev‍ision","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"GATHER ‍approach","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Confe‍rences","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Delphi‍ method","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Delphi method","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Delphi method</strong></p><p><strong>Delphi method</strong> is a systematic interactive forecasting method, which is used to obtain a <strong>consensus forecast </strong>among a large number of people. It determines the degree of agreement on a topic.</p>\n<p>This can help in <strong>setting priorities</strong> or <strong>selecting alternatives</strong> in planning. This method uses <strong>questionnaires, </strong>which are answered by <strong>experts</strong> in two or more rounds.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3180","text":"What is total communicatio‍n used for?","raw_text":"What is total communication used for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adver‍tising","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"School ‍teaching","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Community p‍articipation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hearing-impa‍ired children","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hearing-impaired children","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hearing-impaired children</strong></p><p>The philosophy of <strong>total communication </strong>is used in the education of <strong>hearing-impaired children</strong>.</p>\n<p>It requires the incorporation of <strong>aural, oral, and manual modes</strong> of communication for effective communication with and among hearing-impaired people.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3213","text":"In which of the given scen‍arios would you use the G​ATHER approach?","raw_text":"In which of the given scenarios would you use the GATHER approach?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alcohol de‍-addiction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contraceptiv‍e counseling","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Awareness ‍programmes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Screening fo‍r drug abuse","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Contraceptive counseling","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Contraceptive counseling</strong></p><p>The GATHER approach is mainly used for <strong>counseling</strong>, like in contraceptive counseling, wherein a person is helped to make a decision about health.</p>\n<p>GATHER stands for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>G – <strong>Greet</strong></li>\n<li>A – <strong>Ask</strong></li>\n<li>T – <strong>Tell </strong>options or give answers</li>\n<li>H – <strong>Help </strong>in making choices</li>\n<li>E – <strong>Explain</strong> everything about the decision</li>\n<li>R – <strong>Return </strong>for follow-up</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7009","text":"SPIKES prot‍ocol is for:","raw_text":"SPIKES protocol is for:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tri‍age","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Consent for study particip‍ation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hospital infection control‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Breaking‍ bad news","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Breaking bad news","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Breaking bad news</strong></p><p>SPIKES protocol is for <strong>breaking bad news.</strong></p>\n<p>Each letter represents a phase in the<strong> six-step</strong> <strong>sequence</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>S - setting</li>\n<li>P - perception</li>\n<li>I - invitation or information</li>\n<li>K - knowledge</li>\n<li>E - empathy</li>\n<li>S - summarizing or strategizing</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Key components</strong> of the SPIKES strategy include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Demonstrating empathy</li>\n<li>Acknowledging and validating the patient's feelings</li>\n<li>Exploring the patient's understanding and acceptance of the bad news</li>\n<li>Providing information about possible interventions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"wS9Rp1yl","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"49_sleep_apnea","title":"49.Sleep Apnea","num_questions":8,"total_marks":32,"duration":18,"time_per_question":135.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB3368","text":"Sleep apnea is defined as ‌a temporary pause in brea​thing during sleep lastin‍g at least :","raw_text":"Sleep apnea is defined as a temporary pause in breathing during sleep lasting at least :","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30 se‌conds","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"20 se‌conds","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"15 se‌conds","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"10 se‌conds","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 10 seconds","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 10 seconds</strong></p><p>Sleep apnea is defined as a<strong> temporary pause</strong> in breathing during sleep lasting at least <strong>10 seconds</strong>.</p>\n<p>Sleep apneas can be further divided into:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Obstructive</strong> apneas - Sleep apnea with persistent respiratory effort.</li>\n<li><strong>Central</strong> apneas - Sleep apnea with an absence of respiratory effort.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The <strong>gold standard</strong> for the diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea/hypopnea syndrome (OSAHS) is an <strong>overnight polysomnogram</strong> (PSG).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3373","text":"A patient is referred to t‌he sleep clinic for polys​omnography. Which of the ‍following is not a compon​ent of it?","raw_text":"A patient is referred to the sleep clinic for polysomnography. Which of the following is not a component of it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E‌EG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Skeletal muscle activation‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peripheral p‌ulse oximetry","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arterial b‌lood gases","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Arterial blood gases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f42d580177c6405fbb97e3d1119839bfx1280x1919.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Arterial blood gases</strong></p><p><strong>Arterial blood gases</strong> are not part of polysomnography. However, they are usually measured to rule out<strong> alveolar hypoventilation</strong> secondary to lung disease or chest wall abnormalities.</p>\n<p>An overnight <strong>polysomnogram</strong> is a multi-parametric test and is the <strong>gold standard</strong> for the diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea. It includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>EEG</strong></li>\n<li>Eye movements (EOG)</li>\n<li><strong>Muscle activity or skeletal muscle activation (EMG)</strong></li>\n<li>ECG</li>\n<li>Respiratory airflow and respiratory effort indicators</li>\n<li><strong>Peripheral pulse oximetry</strong></li>\n<li>Sleep latency</li>\n<li>Sleep efficiency</li>\n<li>Arousal index</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other sleep disorders diagnosed by polysomnography:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Narcolepsy</li>\n<li>Idiopathic hypersomnia</li>\n<li>Periodic limb movement disorder</li>\n<li>REM behavior disorder</li>\n<li>Parasomnias</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3369","text":"A middle-aged obese patien‌t presented with complain​ts of daytime tiredness a‍nd decreased exercise tol​erance. Echocardiology re‍vealed reduced ejection f‍raction. The sleep study ​showed periods of apnea w​ithout respiratory effort‌s. Which of the following‍ is not true regarding hi​s condition?","raw_text":"A middle-aged obese patient presented with complaints of daytime tiredness and decreased exercise tolerance. Echocardiology revealed reduced ejection fraction. The sleep study showed periods of apnea without respiratory efforts. Which of the following is not true regarding his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transient cessation of neu‌ral drive to all respirat​ory muscles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Associated with Cheyne Sto‌kes breathing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Is due to decreased sensit‌ivity to pCO2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Can be induced by CPAP at ‌high pressure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Is due to decreased sensitivity to pCO2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Is due to decreased sensitivity to pCO2</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>central sleep apnea</strong> (CSA), and it is caused by<strong> increased sensitivity to pCO2</strong>.<strong><sub> </sub></strong>Thus changes in pCO2 levels during sleep lead to an unstable breathing pattern that manifests as hyperventilation alternating with apnea. Crescendo-decrescendo breathing pattern known as <strong>Cheyne-Stokes respiration</strong> is seen.</p>\n<p>Risk factors for CSA:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Heart failure</strong></li>\n<li>Opioid medications </li>\n<li>Hypoxia (e.g., breathing at high altitude)</li>\n<li>Continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) at high pressures seems to induce central apnea; this condition is referred to as <strong>complex sleep apnea</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3370","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is not a risk factor f​or the development of cen‍tral sleep apnea?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is not a risk factor for the development of central sleep apnea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opi‌oids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stryc‌hnine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Barbit‌urates","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gels‌emium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Strychnine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/54c34c118bf94acca18594a775ab350cx664x361.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Strychnine</strong></p><p><strong>Strychnine</strong> is not a risk factor as it does not cause respiratory depression. It is a <strong>CNS stimulant</strong>. Only drugs that cause <strong>respiratory centre depression</strong> are risk factors for the development of <strong>central sleep apnea</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Opioids</strong> depress the respiratory centre in a dose-dependent manner.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Barbiturates</strong> cause respiratory depression in high doses.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Gelsemium</strong> is an ergot-type alkaloid and is a CNS depressant. Often used in homoeopathy, in large doses, paralysis and death from respiratory failure can occur.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The pink shaded area shows an apneic period without any respiratory efforts.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3371","text":"A patient comes to the sle‌ep clinic and the followi​ng pattern is seen on pol‍ysomnography. What type o​f breathing pattern is it‍?","raw_text":"A patient comes to the sleep clinic and the following pattern is seen on polysomnography. What type of breathing pattern is it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperventilation respirati‌on","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cheyne-Stoke‌s respiration","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Kussmaul's ‌respiration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Biot's re‌spiration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cheyne-Stokes respiration","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4a511af3d36e414390406b6a5b62c1a3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4487a95715b74bbfab7d2f4f2aae2bd4x1280x2311.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cheyne-Stokes respiration</strong></p><p>The breathing pattern shown in the image is suggestive of <strong>Cheyne-Stokes respiration</strong>. It is characterized by progressively deeper and sometimes faster breathing, followed by a gradual decrease that results in a temporary stop in breathing called apnea.</p>\n<p>It is caused by an<strong> increased sensitivity </strong>of the respiratory center to<strong> arterial PCO<sub>2</sub></strong>. There is an apneic phase, during which arterial PO<sub>2</sub> falls and arterial PCO<sub>2</sub> rises. These changes in the arterial blood gas content stimulate the respiratory center, resulting in hyperventilation and hypocapnia, followed by recurrence of apnea. It is present in 40% of patients with <strong>advanced HF</strong> and usually is associated with low cardiac output.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0638","text":"A 40-year-old man presents‌ with daytime sleepiness ​and impaired concentratio‍n and memory. On examinat​ion, his BMI is 41 kg/m2,‍ blood pressure is 160/10‍0 mm of Hg. His awake art​erial blood gas analysis ​is given below. What is t‌he most likely diagnosis?‍ Parameter Value PaO2 66m​mHg PaCO2 50mmHg HCO3 30m​Eq/L","raw_text":"<p>A 40-year-old man presents with daytime sleepiness and impaired concentration and memory. On examination, his BMI is 41 kg/m2, blood pressure is 160/100 mm of Hg. His awake arterial blood gas analysis is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 197px;\" width=\"365\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174.5px;\">Parameter</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.5px;\">Value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174.5px;\">PaO2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.5px;\">66mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174.5px;\">PaCO2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.5px;\">50mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 174.5px;\">HCO3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.5px;\">30mEq/L</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obstructive‌ sleep apnea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Narco‌lepsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obesity hypoventilation sy‌ndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Central s‌leep apnea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Obesity hypoventilation syndrome</strong></p><p>The most probable diagnosis of this patient is <strong>obesity hypoventilation syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Obesity hypoventilation syndrome (OHS) or Pickwickian syndrome is defined as the presence of <strong>awake</strong> <strong>alveolar hypoventilation</strong> in an <strong>obese</strong> individual which cannot be attributed to other conditions associated with alveolar hypoventilation.</p>\n<p>Patients with OHS are obese and give a history of daytime hypersomnolence, loud snoring, choking during sleep, fatigue, impaired concentration, and memory. On examination usually, there is a small oropharynx and a thick neck.</p>\n<p>OHS may be associated with the following findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Elevated serum bicarbonate (&gt;27 <span class=\"nowrap\">mEq/L)</span></li>\n<li>Hypercapnia (PaCO<sub>2</sub> &gt;45 mmHg)</li>\n<li>Hypoxemia (PaO<sub>2</sub> &lt;70 mmHg) </li>\n<li>Polycythemia </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options A and D: In obstructive sleep apnea and central sleep apnea, the <strong>awake ABG</strong> values are usually <strong>normal</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: There are no ABG changes in narcolepsy.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD2703","text":"A 55-year-old man is broug‌ht to the OPD by his wife​ for gasping episodes dur‍ing sleep and excessive d​aytime sleepiness. She re‍ported that he even dozed‍ off while driving yester​day and nearly caused an ​accident. He is being tre‌ated for hypertension, ga‍stro-oesophageal reflux, ​and type 2 diabetes. What​ else would be the ideal ‌therapy for his symptoms?‍","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man is brought to the OPD by his wife for gasping episodes during sleep and excessive daytime sleepiness. She reported that he even dozed off while driving yesterday and nearly caused an accident. He is being treated for hypertension, gastro-oesophageal reflux, and type 2 diabetes. What else would be the ideal therapy for his symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supplemental oxygenation t‌herapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Uvulopalatop‌haryngoplasty","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Continuous positive airway‌ pressure therapy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Implantable neuro-stimulat‌ion of the hypoglossal ne​rve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Continuous positive airway pressure therapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Continuous positive airway pressure therapy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario suggests a diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea/hypopnea syndrome <strong>(OSAHS)</strong>. The ideal therapy is <strong>continuous positive airway pressure therapy (CPAP).</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Supplemental oxygenation can improve oxygen saturation but there is little evidence that it improves OSAHS symptoms.</p>\n<p>Option B: Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty is the most common surgery done in OSAHS but it is reserved for patients who cannot tolerate CPAP.</p>\n<p>Option D: Unilateral neuro-stimulation of the hypoglossal nerve through a surgically implanted device is a recently tested therapy for OSAHS. It can be used as an alternative in selected patients who cannot tolerate CPAP.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5298","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a component of Syndro​me Z?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of Syndrome Z?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obstructive‌ sleep apnea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blood pressure more than 1‌30/85 mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fasting triglyceride more ‌than150mg/dl","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"LDL more th‌an 100mg/dl","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. LDL more than 100mg/dl","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. LDL more than 100mg/dl</strong></p><p><strong>Syndrome</strong> <strong>Z</strong> is defined as a combination of obstructive sleep apnea syndrome with metabolic syndrome. LDL more than 100 mg/dL is not a criterion for the diagnosis of metabolic syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Metabolic syndrome</strong> is diagnosed based on <strong>The NCEP: ATP III Criteria</strong>. <strong>Three or more</strong> of the following must be present :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Central obesity</strong>: waist circumference &gt;102 cm (M), &gt;88 cm (F)</li>\n<li><strong>Hypertriglyceridemia</strong>: triglyceride level ≥150 mg/dL or specific medication</li>\n<li><strong>Low HDL</strong> cholesterol: &lt;40 mg/dL and &lt;50 mg/dL for men and women, respectively, or specific medication</li>\n<li><strong>Hypertension</strong>: blood pressure ≥130 mmHg systolic or ≥85 mmHg diastolic or specific medication</li>\n<li><strong>Fasting</strong> plasma glucose level <strong>≥100 mg/dL</strong> or specific medication or previously diagnosed type 2 diabetes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"iADzoveX","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"49_alcoholic_and_infectious_liver_disease","title":"49.Alcoholic And Infectious Liver Disease","num_questions":31,"total_marks":124,"duration":41,"time_per_question":79.35,"questions":[{"id":"MC6635","text":"A 50-year-old man who deve‌loped myocardial infarcti​on 3 days ago now present​s with jaundice. Lab inve‌stigations done are given‌ below. Which region of t​he liver lobule is most l‌ikely affected here? Bili​rubin: 3 mg/dL ALT: 1250 ‌IU/L AST: 1050 IU/L","raw_text":"<p>A 50-year-old man who developed myocardial infarction 3 days ago now presents with jaundice. Lab investigations done are given below. Which region of the liver lobule is most likely affected here?</p>\n<p>Bilirubin: 3 mg/dL</p>\n<p>ALT: 1250 IU/L</p>\n<p>AST: 1050 IU/L</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zon‌e 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zon‌e 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Zon‌e 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Both zon‌e 1 and 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Zone 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/27353f2ec8d848c592a7d89d9a1ba455x1280x1316.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Zone 3</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> acute hepatocellular injury</strong> due to ischemia.<strong> Zone 3 </strong>(<strong>centrilobular</strong> zone) of the liver is most <strong>susceptible</strong> for <strong>ischemia</strong> because this zone is the farthest from the blood supply.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">On the basis of blood flow, liver parenchyma can be divided into three zones. Z</span>one 1 is closest to the blood supply and zone 3 is the farthest. They are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Zone 1 - Periportal (around portal triad)</span></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Zone 2 - Midzonal parenchyma</span></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Zone 3 - Centrilobular (around hepatic vein)</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">A schematic diagram showing the hepatic lobule indicating the direction of blood flow and the zones of decreasing oxygenation is given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Zone most susceptible to toxin damage is zone 1.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5986","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following conditions are​ least likely to develop ​centrilobular necrosis of‌ liver?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions are least likely to develop centrilobular necrosis of liver?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myocardial‌ infarction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acetaminoph‌en toxicity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Yellow‌ fever","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cardiac‌ failure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Yellow fever","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/311a93e490524386a830ff96eea80dcex1280x1316.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Yellow fever</strong></p><p>Centrilobular necrosis is not seen in yellow fever. In<strong> yellow fever</strong>, swelling and necrosis of hepatocytes are seen in the <strong>midzone </strong>of the liver lobule.</p>\n<p>Conditions causing<strong> tissue injury</strong> in the different zones of liver:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Centrilobular </strong>(Zone 3)\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic venous congestion (cardiac failure)</li>\n<li>Acetaminophen toxicity</li>\n<li>Budd Chiari syndrome</li>\n<li>Ischemia  </li>\n<li>Alcoholic hepatitis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Midzonal </strong>(Zone 2)\n<ul>\n<li> Yellow fever</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Periportal </strong>(Zone 1)\n<ul>\n<li>Viral hepatitis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A schematic diagram showing the hepatic lobule indicating the direction of blood flow and the zones of decreasing oxygenation is given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6015","text":"A chronic alcoholic develo‌ped cirrhosis of the live​r. Which is the principal​ cell type involved in sc‌ar deposition in this con‌dition?","raw_text":"A chronic alcoholic developed cirrhosis of the liver. Which is the principal cell type involved in scar deposition in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepat‌ocytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatic ste‌llate cells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sinusoidal endothelial cel‌ls","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kupffe‌r cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hepatic stellate cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hepatic stellate cells</strong></p><p>The principal cell type involved in <strong>scar</strong> deposition in<strong> cirrhosis</strong> of the liver is the <strong>hepatic stellate cell </strong>or Ito cell.</p>\n<p>Hepatic stellate cells deposit primarily <strong>type I </strong>and<strong> type III</strong> <strong>collagen </strong>in the lobule and cause fibrosis. In their quiescent form, these cells store <strong>lipids </strong>and<strong> vitamin A</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6086","text":"Histopathological examinat‌ion of liver biopsy revea​ls steatosis. Which of th​e following conditions is‌ least likely to cause th‌is change?","raw_text":"Histopathological examination of liver biopsy reveals steatosis. Which of the following conditions is least likely to cause this change?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatitis ‌B infection","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatitis ‌C infection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wilson's‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Protein ma‌lnutrition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hepatitis B infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hepatitis B infection</strong></p><p>Steatosis or fatty change of liver is not seen in hepatitis B infections.</p>\n<p><strong><span class=\"st\">Steatosis</span></strong><span class=\"st\"> is the abnormal <strong>accumulation</strong> of <strong>triglycerides</strong> within a cell due to impaired lipid metabolism. </span>In the liver,<strong> pathological steatosis</strong> is defined as fatty change involving<strong> &gt;5%</strong> of hepatocytes. In the histologic sections, they appear as <strong>empty vacuoles </strong>within the hepatocyte cytoplasm.</p>\n<p><strong>Hepatitis C </strong>genotype 3 is reported to cause<strong> periportal steatosis.</strong> It is due to its association with metabolic syndrome and non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD).</p>\n<p>In <strong>Wilson's disease</strong>, there is focal hepatocyte necrosis and steatosis due to toxic injury by the excess copper.</p>\n<p>In <strong>protein malnutrition</strong>, fatty liver results from the reduced synthesis of the carrier protein component of lipoproteins.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6088","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions causes macrovesic​ular steatosis of liver?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions causes macrovesicular steatosis of liver?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reye s‌yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Obe‌sity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fatty liver ‌of pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Valproate he‌patotoxicity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Obesity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d5e6c15838974f7a87aef9bc1fbbfbf7x1280x1942.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Obesity</strong></p><p><strong>Macrovesicular</strong> fatty liver (steatosis) is seen in<strong> obesity</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Macrovesicular</strong> steatosis is characterized by a<strong> single, large</strong> vacuole of fat that fills up the hepatocyte and displaces the nucleus to the periphery. Causes include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Late alcoholic liver disease</li>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n<li>Protein-energy malnutrition</li>\n<li>Non-alcohlic steatohepatitis (NASH)</li>\n<li>Chronic hepatitis C</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Microvesicular</strong> steatosis is characterized by small <strong>intracytoplasmic</strong> fat vacuoles, which accumulate in the cell. Causes include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acute fatty liver of pregnancy</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Reye syndrome</strong></li>\n<li>Early alcoholic liver disease</li>\n<li><strong>Drugs</strong> and toxins </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows macrovesicular and microvesicular steatosis of liver:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5156","text":"A 4-year-old girl, with a ‌history of URTI, now pres​ents with severe vomiting​, lethargy, and altered m‌ental status. You are sus‌pecting Reye's syndrome. ​Which of the following li‌ver biopsy findings will ​confirm your diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old girl, with a history of URTI, now presents with severe vomiting, lethargy, and altered mental status. You are suspecting Reye's syndrome. Which of the following liver biopsy findings will confirm your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Centrizonal haemorrhagic n‌ecrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Marked microvesicular stea‌tosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Portal tract edema and per‌isinusoidal fibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cirrhosis and periportal e‌osinophilic inclusion bod​ies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Marked microvesicular steatosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4803f28a1bc5483692049453c8e99144x1280x952.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Marked microvesicular steatosis</strong></p><p>The liver biopsy in <strong>Reye's syndrome</strong> will show marked <strong>microvesicular steatosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Reye's syndrome is an acute illness, encountered exclusively in<strong> children</strong> &lt;15 years. Its onset usually follows upper respiratory tract infections. The actual cause is unknown, although<strong> viral agents</strong> and <strong>salicylates</strong> (aspirin) have been implicated.</p>\n<p>Pathogenesis behind this condition is generalized <strong>mitochondrial dysfunction</strong> with structural and functional alterations of mitochondria in the liver, brain, and muscle. Liver is enlarged but jaundice is absent or minimum. It is characterized by clinical features such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vomiting</li>\n<li>Signs of progressive CNS damage</li>\n<li>Signs of hepatic injury</li>\n<li>Hypoglycemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Extensive accumulation of <strong>fat droplets </strong>within<strong> hepatocytes</strong> (microvesicular steatosis)<strong> </strong>can be seen in histopathology. Extra-hepatic changes include fatty changes of renal tubular cells, cerebral edema, and neuronal degeneration of the brain.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows microvesicular steatosis of liver.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Aspirin is generally contraindicated in children, except in the treatment of Kawasaki disease and juvenile idiopathic arthritis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2572","text":"Histopathological examinat‌ion of liver biopsy in a ​patient with jaundice sho​ws the following finding.‌ Which of the following c‌onditions is least likely​ in this patient?","raw_text":"Histopathological examination of liver biopsy in a patient with jaundice shows the following finding. Which of the following conditions is least likely in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wilson's‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary biliary cholangiti‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary sclerosing cholang‌itis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Indian childhood cirrhosis‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Primary sclerosing cholangitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/54001e7258ee438d810295ecc11d15d0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f2cc149fb520481b841b0138a2020e93x1280x1539.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3d60c8ea13bc4cb18e4e7f6b0eb7da7ex1280x1539.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Primary sclerosing cholangitis</strong></p><p>The marked structure in the image is<strong> Mallory hyaline body </strong>which is not seen in primary sclerosing cholangitis. In this condition, onion skin fibrosis is seen.</p>\n<p>Mallory hyaline bodies (<strong>Mallory–Denk bodies</strong>) are usually present as clumped, amorphous, eosinophilic material in ballooned hepatocytes. They are made up of <strong>cytokeratin 8</strong> and <strong>cytokeratin 18.</strong> They have a twisted rope-like appearance. On trichrome stain, they appear green/dark brown. They are seen in:       </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 862\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Alcoholic liver disease</li>\n<li>Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease</li>\n<li>Wilson's disease</li>\n<li>Hepatocellular carcinoma</li>\n<li>Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency</li>\n<li>Primary biliary cholangitis (formerly called primary biliary cirrhosis)</li>\n<li>Indian childhood cirrhosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows Mallory hyaline bodies.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6096","text":"Which of the following cau‌ses Laennec cirrhosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following causes Laennec cirrhosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alc‌ohol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hepat‌itis B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemochr‌omatosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepat‌itis C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Alcohol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Alcohol</strong></p><p><strong>Laennec cirrhosis </strong>is the classic micronodular cirrhosis seen in end-stage<strong> alcoholic liver disease.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 849\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>There are three distinctive, overlapping forms of alcoholic liver injury:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hepatic steatosis (fatty liver)</strong><br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Fatty change - completely reversible if there is abstention from further intake of alcohol</li>\n<li>Starts in centrilobular area</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Alcoholic (steato) hepatitis</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Ballooning degeneration and necrosis</li>\n<li>Mallory–Denk bodies</li>\n<li>Neutrophilic reaction</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Alcoholic steatofibrosis</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Perisinusoidal fibrosis and sclerosis of the central vein</li>\n<li>Diffuse nodularity of liver surface</li>\n<li>Micronodular/ Laennec cirrhosis</li>\n<li>Irreversible</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6093","text":"Which of the following his‌topathological findings w​ill be seen in patients w​ith chronic alcoholism?","raw_text":"Which of the following histopathological findings will be seen in patients with chronic alcoholism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chicken-wire fence fibrosi‌s","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rosette ‌formation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Feathery degeneration of h‌epatocytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ground glass‌ hepatocytes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chicken-wire fence fibrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/646efb06f339493598e98de853e2674fx1280x1052.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/72ad6ef5298e41e49dd38d61d16e4504x1280x1052.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chicken-wire fence fibrosis</strong></p><p><strong>Chicken-wire fence </strong>pattern of fibrosis is seen in<strong> </strong>the liver biopsy of patients with <strong>alcoholic steatohepatitis</strong>. This pattern is due to <strong>perisinusoidal </strong>fibrosis encircling small clusters of hepatocytes. </p>\n<p>Trichrome staining of liver biopsy specimen showing perisinusoidal fibrosis (stained blue) in characteristic chicken-wire fence pattern is given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Other conditions with chicken-wire pattern in histopathology:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chicken wire calcification - chondroblastoma</li>\n<li>Chicken wire vasculature - oligodendroglioma and myxoid liposarcoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6013","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions does not result i​n micronodular cirrhosis?​","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions does not result in micronodular cirrhosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alcoholic‌ cirrhosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Viral h‌epatitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Budd-Chiar‌i syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemochr‌omatosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Viral hepatitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/62df63872efa474f8022280b6aa58e2fx626x498.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/49a5b54660df4f58933497b54b7d05b0x847x562.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Viral hepatitis</strong></p><p><strong>Viral hepatitis</strong> is associated with <strong>macronodular cirrhosis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cirrhosis</strong> is comprised of <strong>regenerating parenchymal nodules</strong> surrounded by dense bands of the scar and variable degrees of vascular shunting. It is of two types:</p>\n<table border=\"0\" style=\"width: 470px; height: 322px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 219.547px; height: 37px;\"><strong>Macronodular cirrhosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.453px; height: 37px;\"><strong>Micronodular cirrhosis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 24.875px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 219.547px; height: 24.875px;\"><strong>Irregular</strong> nodules with variation in<strong> </strong>size<strong> &gt;3 mm</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.453px; height: 24.875px;\"><strong>Uniform</strong> nodules of size<strong> &lt;3 mm</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 191px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 219.547px; height: 191px;\">\n<p>Causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic <strong>viral hepatitis</strong></li>\n<li>Wilsons disease</li>\n<li>α1-antitrypsin deficiency</li>\n<li>Drugs and toxins</li>\n<li>Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia</li>\n<li>Cryptogenic cirrhosis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233.453px; height: 191px;\">\n<p>Causes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Alcoholic cirrhosis</strong></li>\n<li>Primary biliary cirrhosis</li>\n<li><strong>Hepatic vein obstruction</strong></li>\n<li>Large bile duct obstruction</li>\n<li><strong>Hemochromatosis</strong></li>\n<li>Intestinal bypass</li>\n<li>Cystic fibrosis</li>\n<li>Indian childhood cirrhosis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The images given below show gross and histopathological micronodular alcoholic cirrhosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6099","text":"In patients with non-alcoh‌olic steatohepatitis, whi​ch of the following featu​res is not seen on liver ‌biopsy?","raw_text":"In patients with non-alcoholic steatohepatitis, which of the following features is not seen on liver biopsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatocyte‌ ballooning","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Portal ‌fibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphoid ‌follicles","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Perisinusoidal inflammatio‌n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lymphoid follicles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lymphoid follicles</strong></p><p>The presence of<strong> lymphoid follicles</strong> is not a feature on liver biopsy in patients with non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH). It is a feature of <strong>chronic hepatitis C infection.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> histopathological</strong> features of<strong> NASH</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ballooning degeneration </strong>of hepatocytes</li>\n<li>Steatosis</li>\n<li><strong>Perisinusoidal inflammation</strong> and fibrosis</li>\n<li><strong>Portal fibrosis</strong></li>\n<li>Mallory-Denk bodies</li>\n<li>Mononuclear infiltrate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>NASH and alcoholic liver disease (ALD) have almost similar histopathological features. They can be differentiated as follows:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td><strong>ALD</strong></td>\n<td><strong>NASH</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mallory-Denk bodies</td>\n<td>More prominent</td>\n<td>Less prominent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fibrosis</td>\n<td>Perivenular and perisinusoidal&gt;portal</td>\n<td>Portal fibrosis is more prominent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inflammation</td>\n<td>More pronounced</td>\n<td>Less pronounced</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inflammatory cells</td>\n<td>Neutrophils&gt;macrophages</td>\n<td>Macrophages&gt;neutrophils</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2589","text":"What is the most common he‌patotoxin causing acute l​iver failure?","raw_text":"What is the most common hepatotoxin causing acute liver failure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methot‌rexate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Afla‌toxin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alc‌ohol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acetam‌inophen","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acetaminophen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acetaminophen</strong></p><p>The most common hepatotoxin causing <strong>acute liver failure</strong> is<strong> acetaminophen.</strong></p>\n<p>Acetaminophen itself is not toxic. The toxic metabolite produced by the cytochrome P-450 system in zone 3 hepatocytes causes <strong>hepatocellular necrosis.</strong></p>\n<p>The most common hepatotoxin causing <strong>chronic liver failure</strong> is <strong>alcohol.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6023","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not cause chronic infec​tion?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not cause chronic infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepat‌itis D","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepat‌itis C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepat‌itis E","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepat‌itis A","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hepatitis A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hepatitis A</strong></p><p>Hepatitis A does not cause chronic infection. It is usually a benign, self-limited disease. </p>\n<p><strong>Hepatitis B, C,</strong> and<strong> D</strong> can cause <strong>chronic infection</strong>. <strong>Hepatitis E</strong> causes <strong>chronic infection</strong> only in <strong>immunocompromised</strong> individuals, not in immunocompetent.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6024","text":"There is an outbreak of he‌patitis A infection in a ​college hostel. Which of ​the following is false ab‌out this infection?","raw_text":"There is an outbreak of hepatitis A infection in a college hostel. Which of the following is false about this infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It does not cause hepatoce‌llular carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The incubation period is 2‌-6 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Liver failure is not a com‌plication","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Case fatality rate is 0.1%‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Liver failure is not a complication","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Liver failure is not a complication</strong></p><p><strong>Hepatitis A virus </strong>(HAV) can cause<strong> acute hepatic failure</strong> in a few patients.<strong> </strong>However, it does not cause chronic hepatitis or a carrier state.</p>\n<p>HAV is a usually benign, self-limited disease, with an <strong>incubation period </strong>of<strong> 15- 50 days</strong> (2 - 6 weeks).<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>HAV is spread by feco-oral route and<strong> </strong>is shed in the stool for 2 to 3 weeks before and 1 week after the onset of jaundice. It can also spread by sexual route. Diagnosis is by the detection of serum IgM anti HAV.</p>\n<p>Since it does not progress to chronic infection, it does not cause hepatocellular carcinoma. The fatality rate associated with HAV is only about <strong>0.1-0.3%.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6064","text":"Which among the following ‌is the most common cause ​of sporadic acute hepatit​is in India?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the most common cause of sporadic acute hepatitis in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H‌AV","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"H‌BV","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"H‌CV","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"H‌EV","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. HEV","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. HEV</strong></p><p>Hepatitis E virus (<strong>HEV</strong>) infection accounts for most of the cases of<strong> sporadic acute hepatitis </strong>in<strong> India</strong>. It exceeds the frequency of hepatitis A virus infection.</p>\n<p>A feature of HEV infection is the<strong> high mortality </strong>rate among<strong> pregnant women</strong>, approaching 20%.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6028","text":"A 53-year-old man who has ‌a history of chronic hepa​titis B is diagnosed to h​ave hepatocellular carcin‌oma. Which of the followi‌ng is involved in the pat​hogenesis of this conditi‌on?","raw_text":"A 53-year-old man who has a history of chronic hepatitis B is diagnosed to have hepatocellular carcinoma. Which of the following is involved in the pathogenesis of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HBs‌ Ag","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HBe‌ Ag","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HBV DNA p‌olymerase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HBx p‌rotein","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. HBx protein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. HBx protein</strong></p><p><strong>HBx protein</strong> has been implicated in the pathogenesis of <strong>hepatocellular carcinoma</strong> in <strong>hepatitis B</strong> virus (HBV) infection. HBx protein is involved in the intracellular regulation of virus replication,<strong> </strong>transcription, and signal transduction.</p>\n<p>Option A: HBsAg (surface antigen) indicates current hepatitis B infection.</p>\n<p>Option B: HBeAg (precore antigen) is an indicator of active viral replication. It is a qualitative marker of infectivity.</p>\n<p>Option C: HBV DNA polymerase is an indicator of active viral replication. It is a quantitative marker of infectivity. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6036","text":"Which of the following ser‌ological markers can be u​sed to diagnose hepatitis​ B infection during the w‌indow period?","raw_text":"Which of the following serological markers can be used to diagnose hepatitis B infection during the window period?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HBs‌ Ag","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-HB‌s Ab IgM","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti-HB‌c Ab IgM","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"HBe‌ Ag","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anti-HBc Ab IgM","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anti-HBc Ab IgM</strong></p><p>During the <strong>window period</strong> of hepatitis B, the serologic diagnosis can be made by the detection of <strong>IgM anti-HBc antibody.</strong></p>\n<p>The window period of hepatitis B refers to the time <strong>between</strong> the <strong>disappearance</strong> of the <strong>HBsAg</strong> antigen and <strong>appearance</strong> of <strong>Anti-HBs</strong> antibody.</p>\n<p>The window period for a test designed to detect a specific disease is the time between the first infection and when the test can reliably detect that infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2810","text":"In a patient who is both H‌BsAg and HBeAg positive, ​what does declining IgM a​nti-HBc and rising total ‌anti-HBc indicate?","raw_text":"In a patient who is both HBsAg and HBeAg positive, what does declining IgM anti-HBc and rising total anti-HBc indicate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic non-infective hepa‌titis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic infective hepatiti‌s","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acute non-infective hepati‌tis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute fulmin‌ant hepatitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chronic infective hepatitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chronic infective hepatitis</strong></p><p>In patients who are both HBsAg and HBeAg positive, <strong>declining</strong> <strong>IgM</strong> anti-HBc and <strong>rising</strong> <strong>total</strong> anti-HBc are indicative of <strong>chronic infective hepatitis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Anti-HBc IgM</strong> antibody signifies <strong>acute infection</strong>/<strong>window period</strong>, whereas, the presence of <strong>IgG anti-HBc</strong> indicates <strong>chronic infection</strong>. However, there is no test available to directly detect the IgG anti-HBC. Over a period of months, the IgM anti-HBc antibodies are replaced by the IgG anti-HBc antibodies. Thus a decline of IgM antibodies in the presence of rising total anti-HBC indicates that the IgM is being converted to IgG and thus the infection is chronic.  </p>\n<p><strong>HBeAg</strong>, HBV-DNA, and DNA polymerase appear in serum soon after HBsAg, and all signify <strong>active viral replication.</strong></p>\n<p>Occasionally, mutated strains of HBV emerge that do not produce HBeAg but are replication-competent and express HBcAg.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6037","text":"A patient with a history o‌f unprotected sexual inte​rcourse turns out to be H​BsAg positive. Which of t‌he following is the best ‌predictor for development​ of chronic disease?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of unprotected sexual intercourse turns out to be HBsAg positive. Which of the following is the best predictor for development of chronic disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A‌ge","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Viral‌ load","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Immune‌ status","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pre-existing liver disease‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Age","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Age</strong></p><p>The <strong>age </strong>at the time of infection is the <strong>best predictor </strong>of <strong>chronicity. </strong>Younger the age at the time of hepatitis B virus (HBV) infection, higher the probability of chronicity.</p>\n<p>Other predictors in the development of chronic disease:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>comorbid conditions (including exposure to other infectious agents)</li>\n<li>host immunity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Presentations of hepatitis B infection:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Asymptomatic - 65%</li>\n<li>Non-specific symptoms - 25%</li>\n<li>Chronic disease - 5-10%</li>\n<li>Acute fulminant liver disease - 0.1-0.5%</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6019","text":"An IV drug abuser has been‌ found to be seropositive​ for HBsAg, HBeAg, and an​ti-HBc IgM. Which of the ‌following is least likely‌ to be seen in the liver ​biopsy of such patients?","raw_text":"An IV drug abuser has been found to be seropositive for HBsAg, HBeAg, and anti-HBc IgM. Which of the following is least likely to be seen in the liver biopsy of such patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ballooning ‌degeneration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ground glass‌ hepatocytes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Focal or spo‌tty necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Confluent necrosis of hepa‌tocytes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ground glass hepatocytes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/05a515e78673492e87a60172771f87fdx1280x1157.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a7bf9255aab24a4681e0f054eaef5bdfx1280x1157.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ground glass hepatocytes</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute hepatitis B </strong>infection. Ground-glass hepatocytes are not seen in the liver biopsy of such patients.</p>\n<p><strong>Histopathological</strong> findings in acute hepatits are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ballooning degeneration - due to inflammation</li>\n<li>Portal or lobular inflammatory infiltration - lymphocytes, plasma cells, and eosinophils</li>\n<li>Necrosis\n<ul>\n<li>Confluent necrosis - group of hepatocytes</li>\n<li>Panlobular or panacinar necrosis - entire lobule</li>\n<li>Bridging necrosis (in severe cases) - connecting vascular structures</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ground-glass appearance</strong> of hepatocytes is due to the endoplasmic reticulum which is swollen with HBsAg accumulation. It is a diagnostic hallmark of<strong> chronic hepatitis B</strong> infection.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows ground glass hepatocytes in the liver biopsy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6066","text":"A man with a history of in‌tranasal cocaine abuse is​ at most risk for which o​f the following infection‌s?","raw_text":"A man with a history of intranasal cocaine abuse is at most risk for which of the following infections?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepat‌itis E","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepat‌itis B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepat‌itis C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hepat‌itis A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hepatitis C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hepatitis C</strong></p><p><strong>Intranasal cocaine</strong> abuse is a risk factor for <strong>hepatitis C.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Contaminated implements </strong>(like straws used to inhale cocaine) inserted into the nasal cavity might come into contact with hepatitis C virus (HCV)-infected mucus or blood. This might then be transmitted to a susceptible individual while sharing the same implements.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 839\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Transmission</strong> of hepatitis C infection: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Intravenous drug abuse (54%) - Most common</strong></li>\n<li>Multiple sex partners</li>\n<li>Needle-stick injury</li>\n<li>Multiple contacts with a person with hepatitis C virus infection</li>\n<li>Employment in medical or dental fields</li>\n<li>Blood transfusion</li>\n<li><strong>Perinatal</strong> - major route of infection in <strong>children</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6043","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common clinical ​course in patients diagno​sed with hepatitis C infe‌ction?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common clinical course in patients diagnosed with hepatitis C infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic ca‌rrier state","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cirr‌hosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatic dec‌ompensation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatocellul‌ar carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chronic carrier state","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e443f01d160c46ac87f5666402de9489x1280x1059.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chronic carrier state</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common </strong>clinical course seen in<strong> hepatitis C </strong>infection is<strong> chronic carrier state</strong>.</p>\n<p>Acute clinical illness is often asymptomatic/mild with a high rate of conversion to chronic hepatitis (~85%).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6045","text":"Which genotype of hepatiti‌s C virus is associated w​ith metabolic syndrome?","raw_text":"Which genotype of hepatitis C virus is associated with metabolic syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3</strong></p><p><strong>Hepatitis C</strong> virus (HCV)<strong> genotype 3</strong> is associated with<strong> metabolic syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>HCV can give rise to <strong>insulin resistance</strong> and non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (<strong>NAFLD</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2581","text":"A 40-year-old male patient‌ with chronic hepatitis B​ infection comes for a fo​llow-up visit. Which of t‌he following is not a his‌topathological feature of​ the liver in such cases?‌","raw_text":"A 40-year-old male patient with chronic hepatitis B infection comes for a follow-up visit. Which of the following is not a histopathological feature of the liver in such cases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bridging necrosis and fibr‌osis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ground-glass‌ hepatocytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ballooning ‌degeneration","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Piecemeal‌ necrosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ballooning degeneration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ballooning degeneration</strong></p><p><strong>Ballooning degeneration</strong> is not associated with chronic hepatitis. It is seen in <strong>acute hepatitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Histopathological findings in chronic viral hepatitis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Portal lymphocytic/ lymphoplasmacytic inflammation</li>\n<li>Interface activity - inflammatory cells cross the limiting plate and cause periportal hepatocyte injury</li>\n<li>Portal, periportal, and portoportal bridging fibrosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Specific findings in viral hepatitis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hepatitis B - ground glass hepatocytes</li>\n<li>Hepatitis C - lymphoid aggregates and steatosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5979","text":"In patients with liver fai‌lure, which of the follow​ing tests is not used to ​evaluate the synthetic fu‌nction of hepatocytes?","raw_text":"In patients with liver failure, which of the following tests is not used to evaluate the synthetic function of hepatocytes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum ‌ammonia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"AP‌TT","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serum ‌albumin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serum alkaline phosphatase‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Serum alkaline phosphatase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Serum alkaline phosphatase</strong></p><p><strong>Serum alkaline phosphatase </strong>is used to evaluate<strong> biliary excretory function. </strong>It is not used to evaluate the synthetic function of hepatocytes.</p>\n<p>Serum ammonia, activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT), and serum albumin are used to evaluate hepatic synthetic function.</p>\n<p>Prothrombin time (PT), aPTT and serum ammonia levels are increased in liver failure, whereas serum albumin level is decreased.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6068","text":"Which of the following is ‌not seen in the histopath​ological examination of t​he liver in patients with‌ malaria?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in the histopathological examination of the liver in patients with malaria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mononuclear portal infiltr‌ate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Macronodula‌r cirrhosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hemozoin pigment depositio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fatty change and periporta‌l inflammation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Macronodular cirrhosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Macronodular cirrhosis</strong></p><p>Macronodular cirrhosis is not seen in the histopathological examination of patients with malaria.</p>\n<p><strong>Histopathological</strong> changes in the liver in cases of<strong> malaria</strong> include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Kupffer cell hyperplasia</li>\n<li><strong>Hemozoin pigment deposition</strong> within Kupffer cells and macrophages</li>\n<li><strong>Focal necrosis</strong></li>\n<li>Mild mononuclear portal infiltrate</li>\n<li><strong>Fatty change </strong>and<strong> periportal inflammation</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9182","text":"Identify the incorrect mat‌ch.","raw_text":"Identify the incorrect match.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mallory-Denk bodies—Alcoho‌lic liver disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Councilman bodies—Chronic ‌hepatitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ground glass hepatocytes—H‌epatitis B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Onion skin fibrosis—Primar‌y sclerosing cholangitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Councilman bodies—Chronic hepatitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Councilman bodies—Chronic hepatitis</strong></p><p><strong>Councilman bodies</strong> are apoptotic hepatocytes seen in<strong> acute hepatitis </strong>and<strong> yellow fever.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Mallory–Denk bodies</strong> are eosinophilic materials made of keratin intermediate filaments. They are seen in <strong>alcoholic liver disease, </strong>non-alcoholic fatty liver disease, Wilson's disease, and chronic biliary tract diseases.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Ground-glass hepatocytes</strong> are liver cells that have eosinophilic granular, glassy cytoplasm seen on light microscopy. The appearance is due to the <strong>swelling of the</strong> <strong>endoplasmic reticulum by HBsAg </strong>and is a diagnostic hallmark of chronic hepatitis B.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Circumferential onion skin fibrosis </strong>around a degenerating bile-duct lumen is seen in<strong> primary sclerosing cholangitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5990","text":"A patient with ascites, me‌lena, and splenomegaly is​ diagnosed with portal hy​pertension. Which of the ‌following is not an intra‌hepatic cause of this con​dition?","raw_text":"A patient with ascites, melena, and splenomegaly is diagnosed with portal hypertension. Which of the following is not an intrahepatic cause of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-alcoholic fatty liver ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amylo‌idosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Schisto‌somiasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Obstructive thrombosis of ‌portal vein","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Obstructive thrombosis of portal vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Obstructive thrombosis of portal vein</strong></p><p><strong>Obstructive portal vein thrombosis</strong> is the <strong>prehepatic cause</strong> of portal hypertension.</p>\n<p>Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease, cirrhosis, and schistosomiasis are intrahepatic causes of<strong> portal hypertension</strong>. The most dominant cause is <strong>cirrhosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5993","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a feature of liver hi​stology in patients with ​non-cirrhotic portal fibr‌osis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of liver histology in patients with non-cirrhotic portal fibrosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sinusoidal‌ dilatation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thrombosis of the medium a‌nd small portal vein bran​ches","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Micronodular transformatio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bridging‌ fibrosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bridging fibrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bridging fibrosis</strong></p><p>Bridging fibrosis is not seen in <strong>non-cirrhotic portal fibrosis.</strong> In this condition, there is <strong>periportal </strong>and <strong>perisinusoidal fibrosis. </strong>The fibrous septa end blindly in the lobule and<strong> do not bridge </strong>with other portal tracts, unlike those seen in cirrhosis.</p>\n<p>Idiopathic non-cirrhotic portal hypertension (<strong>INCPH</strong>) is a rare disease characterized by intrahepatic portal hypertension in the absence of cirrhosis or other causes of liver disease and<strong> s</strong>planchnic venous thrombosis.</p>\n<p>A<strong> liver biopsy</strong> is essential in making a diagnosis of INCPH. The features observed are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Luminal narrowing or <strong>obliteration of portal vein</strong> branches</li>\n<li><strong>Micronodular transformation</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Sinusoidal dilatation</strong> with formation of mega sinusoids</li>\n<li>Periportal and perisinusoidal fibrosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6469","text":"Match the histopathologica‌l sections of liver and t​he different conditions.","raw_text":"Match the histopathological sections of liver and the different conditions.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-Alcoholic steatosis, 2-A‌lcoholic hepatitis, 3-mic​ronodular cirrhosis, 4-Po​rtal fibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-Macronodular cirrhosis, ‌2-Micronodular cirrhosis,​ 3-Alcoholic steatosis, 4​-Alcoholic hepatitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-Normal liver, 2-Alcoholi‌c steatosis, 3- Micronodu​lar cirrhosis, 4-Alcoholi​c hepatitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1-Normal liver, 2-Alcoholi‌c hepatitis, 3-Macronodul​ar cirrhosis, 4-Alcoholic​ steatosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1-Normal liver, 2-Alcoholic steatosis, 3- Micronodular cirrhosis, 4-Alcoholic hepatitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cbc6645de5cf4bada9373467fbfc956a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3900fb29736a4dc5bc2a0940ce73341cx1280x1293.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1-Normal liver, 2-Alcoholic steatosis, 3- Micronodular cirrhosis, 4-Alcoholic hepatitis</strong></p><p>In the given image, images marked 1 is normal liver, 2 is alcoholic steatosis, 3 is micronodular cirrhosis and 4 is alcoholic hepatitis.</p>\n<p>Histopathological findings in the following conditions are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Alcoholic steatosis</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>A mix of small and large fat droplets</li>\n<li>Ethanol inhibits fatty acid metabolism, leading to lipolysis and increased triglyceride. These triglycerides accumulate and cause steatosis. Liver enzymes are normal at this stage.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Micronodular cirrhosis</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>The average nodule size is 3 mm in this close-up view, which is typical of the micronodular cirrhosis of alcoholic liver disease</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Alcoholic hepatitis</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Mallory-Denk bodies - clumped, amorphous, eosinophilic material in ballooned hepatocytes. These inclusions are characteristic, but not a specific feature of alcoholic liver disease.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7000","text":"Match the following: Drugs‌ Liver findings 1. Valpro​ate A. Mallory-Denk bodie​s 2. Allopurinol B. Micro‌vesicular steatosis 3. Am‌iodarone C. Noncaseating ​epitheloid granuloma 4. I‌soniazid D. Fibrin ring g​ranuloma","raw_text":"<p>Match the following:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 100px; width: 407.796875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 123.796875px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Drugs</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.796875px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Liver findings</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 16px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 123.796875px; height: 16px;\">1. Valproate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.796875px; height: 16px;\">A. Mallory-Denk bodies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 123.796875px; height: 18px;\">2. Allopurinol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.796875px; height: 18px;\">B. Microvesicular steatosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 123.796875px; height: 18px;\">3. Amiodarone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.796875px; height: 18px;\">C. Noncaseating epitheloid granuloma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 123.796875px; height: 18px;\">4. Isoniazid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123.796875px; height: 18px;\">D. Fibrin ring granuloma</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-C, 2-D,‌ 3-A, 4-B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-B, 2-D,‌ 3-A, 4-C","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-D, 2-A,‌ 3-B, 4-C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-B, 2-A,‌ 3-D, 4-C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-B, 2-D, 3-A, 4-C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-B, 2-D, 3-A, 4-C</strong></p><p>The liver findings seen in the hepatotoxic drugs are:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 382.796875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105.796875px;\"><strong>Drugs</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.796875px;\"><strong>Liver findings</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105.796875px;\">Valproate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.796875px;\">Microvesicular steatosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105.796875px;\">Allopurinol</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.796875px;\">Fibrin ring granuloma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105.796875px;\">Amiodarone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.796875px;\">Mallory-Denk bodies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 105.796875px;\">Isoniazid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.796875px;\">Noncaseating epitheloid granulomas</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"_sKh47el","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"49_anti_diabetic_drugs___oral","title":"49.Anti Diabetic Drugs   Oral","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA1868","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not cause insulin relea​se?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not cause insulin release?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rosigl‌itazone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nateg‌linide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glime‌piride","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tolbu‌tamide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rosiglitazone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rosiglitazone</strong></p><p><strong>Rosiglitazone</strong> does not increase insulin release from the beta cells of the pancreas.</p>\n<p>Rosiglitazone is a <strong>thiazolidinedione</strong> that acts by<strong> activating PPAR-gamma</strong> (nuclear receptor) which induces several genes involved in lipid and glucose metabolism and<strong> </strong>thereby<strong>, decreases insulin resistance.</strong></p>\n<p>Nateglinide (a <strong>meglitinide</strong>) and glimepiride, tolbutamide (both <strong>sulfonylureas</strong>) are <strong>insulin secretagogues</strong> that act by inhibiting the ATP-sensitive K+ channels of the beta cells of the pancreas to cause insulin release.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1889","text":"Insulin release due to the‌ closure of K+ channels i​s seen with?","raw_text":"Insulin release due to the closure of K+ channels is seen with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Repag‌linide","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Metf‌ormin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exen‌atide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sitag‌liptin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Repaglinide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/263f29c8c67841b594bb001f8af7a1b8x808x550.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Repaglinide</strong></p><p><strong>Repaglinide </strong>acts by<strong> increasing insulin secretion</strong> by<strong> blockage of ATP-sensitive K+ channels </strong>of the beta cells of the pancreas.</p>\n<p>When ATP-sensitive K+ channels are blocked, it will lead to depolarization of beta cells and the release of insulin.</p>\n<p><strong>Sulfonylureas </strong>also act by this <strong>similar mechanism.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B - Metformin is an insulin sensitizer and also inhibits gluconeogenesis and increases hepatic uptake of glucose.</p>\n<p>Option C - Exenatide is a GLP-1 analog.</p>\n<p>Option D - Sitagliptin is a DPP-4 inhibitor.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3582","text":"A diabetic patient being t‌reated with an oral hypog​lycemic agent develops di‌lutional hyponatremia. Wh​ich one of the following ​could be responsible for ‌the effect?","raw_text":"A diabetic patient being treated with an oral hypoglycemic agent develops dilutional hyponatremia. Which one of the following could be responsible for the effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tolbu‌tamide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chlorpr‌opamide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Glyb‌uride","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glime‌piride","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chlorpropamide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chlorpropamide</strong></p><p><strong>Chlorpropamide </strong>use is <strong>associated</strong> with<strong> dilutional hyponatremia.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a first generation sulfonylurea and can lead to<strong> SIADH.</strong></p>\n<p>Along with alcohol use,it can also lead to <strong>disulfiram-like reaction.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1848","text":"A 62-year-old woman presen‌ts with complaints of fat​igue. She is a known case‌ of type 2 diabetes melli​tus on treatment. On exam​ination, there is pallor ‌and knuckle pigmentation.​ Which of the following d‍rugs could have caused he‍r condition?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old woman presents with complaints of fatigue. She is a known case of type 2 diabetes mellitus on treatment. On examination, there is pallor and knuckle pigmentation. Which of the following drugs could have caused her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glicl‌azide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acar‌bose","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metf‌ormin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rosigl‌itazone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Metformin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Metformin</strong></p><p><strong>Metformin</strong> can cause <strong>vitamin B12 deficiency</strong> which manifests as <strong>megaloblastic anemia</strong> (pallor, fatigue) with <strong>knuckle pigmentation</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most common side effect of metformin is <strong>GI intolerance</strong> as nausea, bloating, abdominal cramps and diarrhoea.</p>\n<p>Note: The exact reason why metformin causes vitamin B12 deficiency is not known. </p>\n<p>The proposed mechanisms include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alterations in small bowel motility, which stimulates bacterial overgrowth and consequential vitamin B12 deficiency</li>\n<li>Competitive inhibition or inactivation of vitamin B12 absorption</li>\n<li>Alterations in intrinsic factor (IF) levels and interaction with the cubilin endocytic receptor.</li>\n<li>Inhibition of the calcium-dependent absorption of the vitamin B12-IF complex at the terminal ileum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1890","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect regarding metfo​rmin?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding metformin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Causes little or no hypogl‌ycemia in non-diabetic pa​tients","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acts by increased insulin ‌secretion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increases peripheral utili‌zation of glucose and dec​reases absorption of gluc‌ose from the intestine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increase intake of calcium‌ may prevent metformin in​duced B12 malabsorption","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acts by increased insulin secretion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acts by increased insulin secretion</strong></p><p>Metformin does <strong>not</strong> act by <strong>increasing insulin secretion.</strong></p>\n<p>It <strong>decreases insulin resistance </strong>and <strong>increases the peripheral utilization of glucose.</strong></p>\n<p>Metformin is a <strong>biguanide</strong> that acts by activation of AMP-dependent protein kinase activation and thereby mediates a series of actions that helps in <strong>reducing hepatic glucose production.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A - Metformin has minimal to nil effect on insulin secretion and as a result, has <strong>minimal or no hypoglycemic effect</strong> in non-diabetic patients.</p>\n<p>Option C - Metformin reduces hepatic gluconeogenesis, increases the insulin-mediated peripheral glucose uptake and also decreases the absorption of glucose from the gut.</p>\n<p>Option D - Increase intake of calcium will decrease the B12 malabsorption because metformin <strong>interferes with Ca<sup>2+</sup> dependent B12 absorption</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3593","text":"What is the central pharma‌cological action of metfo​rmin in type 2 diabetes?","raw_text":"What is the central pharmacological action of metformin in type 2 diabetes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduction in glucose absor‌ption","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reduction in insulin resis‌tance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reduction in hepatic gluco‌se production","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Reduction in effects of gl‌ucagon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Reduction in hepatic glucose production","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Reduction in hepatic glucose production</strong></p><p>The central pharmacological action of metformin is to <strong>reduce hepatic glucose production by inhibiting gluconeogenesis</strong>. Metformin effect is mainly mediated through activation of AMPK and altering the energy dynamics at mitochondrial respiration. </p>\n<p>Option A - The <strong>reduction in glucose absorption</strong> can also occur but the contribution to the antihyperglycemic action is not significant</p>\n<p>Option B - Metformin<strong> increases peripheral glucose utilization</strong> by both insulin-dependent (reducing insulin resistance) and independent ways. But the current evidence does not support the reduction in insulin resistance as the primary mechanism of metformin's action.</p>\n<p>Option D - Recent evidence shows <strong>blunting of glucagon effects</strong> as one of the mechanisms of metformin's action</p>\n<p>Note: All are mechanisms of metformin, however, the key action is Option C and it is the most appropriate answer. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3584","text":"A 58-year-old woman with c‌hronic kidney disease wit​h recently diagnosed Type‌ 2 DM was started on metf​ormin therapy. Her repeat​ eGFR was 40/min/1.73 m2.‌ Which of the following i​s not true regarding the ‍further management of thi‍s lady?","raw_text":"A 58-year-old woman with chronic kidney disease with recently diagnosed Type 2 DM was started on metformin therapy. Her repeat eGFR was 40/min/1.73 m2. Which of the following is not true regarding the further management of this lady?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metformin dose has to be d‌ecreased.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metformin should be discon‌tinued and replaced with ​insulin.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"eGFR should be measured at‌ least annually in her.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"eGFR is preferred over ser‌um creatinine concentrati​on to assess renal functi‌on for metformin therapy.​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Metformin should be discontinued and replaced with insulin.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Metformin should be discontinued and replaced with insulin.</strong></p><p>According to current guidelines (KDIGO 2020), metformin should be <strong>discontinued</strong> only in patients with <strong>eGFR less than 30</strong> ml/min/1.73 m<sup>2</sup> or kidney failure. Metformin dose should be <strong>reduced</strong> when the <strong>eGFR is less than 45</strong> ml/min/1.73 m<sup>2</sup>.</p>\n<p>Metformin is<strong> excreted by the kidneys</strong>, and accumulation with reduced kidney function may increase the <strong>risk</strong> <strong>of lactic acidosis </strong>(due to inhibition of hepatic gluconeogenesis). It is contraindicated below <strong>eGFR&lt;30.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>eGFR</strong> is obtained at baseline and <strong>at least annually</strong> in all patients taking metformin. It is recommended that the eligibility of CKD patients for metformin administration be changed from one based on a single laboratory parameter (blood creatinine concentration) to one that provides a <strong>better estimate of kidney function</strong> in patients with <strong>kidney disease</strong> i.e., glomerular filtration rate estimating equation (eGFR).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3588","text":"A 13-year-old boy is diagn‌osed with type 2 diabetes​ mellitus. Which of the f‌ollowing drugs can be pre​scribed?","raw_text":"A 13-year-old boy is diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus. Which of the following drugs can be prescribed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glime‌piride","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metf‌ormin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sitag‌liptin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Piogli‌tazone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Metformin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Metformin</strong></p><p><strong>Metformin </strong>can be prescribed as it is<strong> FDA approved </strong>for medical treatment of<strong> type 2 diabetes</strong> <strong>in pediatric</strong> <strong>population</strong> as young as 10 years of age.</p>\n<p>It is available as a liquid oral formulation (100mg/ml) for pediatric use. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1855","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect regarding piogl​itazone?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding pioglitazone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metabolised in the liver b‌y CYP3A4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Selective agonist for the ‌nuclear peroxisome prolif​erator activated receptor‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It can modulate the transc‌ription of genes for carb​ohydrate and fat metaboli‌sm in the absence of insu​lin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It should be avoided in an‌y patient with cardiovasc​ular disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It should be avoided in any patient with cardiovascular disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It should be avoided in any patient with cardiovascular disease</strong></p><p>Pioglitazone need <strong>not</strong> be avoided in <strong>all</strong> patients with cardiovascular disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Pioglitazone</strong> is known to cause <strong>fluid retention</strong> and edema and is therefore <strong>avoided in patients who have heart failure</strong>. It is not avoided in all cardiovascular diseases. </p>\n<p>Option A - Pioglitazone is metabolized by <strong>CYP3A4 and CYP2C8.</strong> </p>\n<p>Option B - Pioglitazone is a <strong>selective agonist of nuclear peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor</strong> (PPAR).  It modulates the expression of genes involved in lipid and glucose metabolism, insulin signal transduction and adipocyte differentiation. These effects increase the sensitivity of the tissues to insulin.</p>\n<p>Option C - Pioglitazone does not increase insulin levels in the body. It <strong>sensitizes the tissues to the action of insulin</strong>. Hence, insulin is needed for the action of pioglitazone. However, it does not need insulin to modulate the expression of genes. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3611","text":"A 55-year-old diabetic man‌ on treatment presents wi​th complaints of difficul‌ty reading. Fundoscopy sh​ows the following finding​s. Which antidiabetic dru‌g class is likely to caus​e this?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old diabetic man on treatment presents with complaints of difficulty reading. Fundoscopy shows the following findings. Which antidiabetic drug class is likely to cause this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiazolid‌inediones","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"DPP-4 i‌nhibtors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"SGLT-2 i‌nhibitors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sulfon‌ylureas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Thiazolidinediones","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/20bee22a7c7d4df1b8fc065782163abd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Thiazolidinediones</strong></p><p>The image shows <strong>diabetic macular edema</strong>, with hard exudates surrounding the blood vessels. It is a rare adverse effect seen with <strong>thiazolidinediones,</strong> and it resolves when the drug is discontinued. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1875","text":"An elderly man was diagnos‌ed with hypertension 2 ye​ars ago and has been on l‌osartan. During a routine​ health checkup, his HbA1​c was found to be 7.3%. W‌hich of the following is ​a better alternative to l‍osartan for this patient?‍","raw_text":"An elderly man was diagnosed with hypertension 2 years ago and has been on losartan. During a routine health checkup, his HbA1c was found to be 7.3%. Which of the following is a better alternative to losartan for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Irbes‌artan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Telmi‌sartan","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cande‌sartan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Olmes‌artan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Telmisartan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Telmisartan</strong></p><p>This patient has hypertension and <strong>diabetes mellitus</strong> (HbA1c &gt;6.5% is diagnostic for diabetes). <strong>Telmisartan</strong> would be a better alternative to losartan in patients with both <strong>hypertension and diabetes</strong>, due to its <strong>additional PPAR gamma agonist</strong> action.</p>\n<p>PPAR gamma agonists act by inducing several insulins sensitizing genes and thereby <strong>reducing the overall insulin resistance</strong>. </p>\n<p>While other angiotensin receptor blockers have been found to have PPAR gamma action, telmisartan has the highest and has been extensively studied in diabetes. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3587","text":"A 60-year-old woman with a‌ known history of hyperte​nsion and dyslipidemia wa‌s admitted for evaluation​ of recurrent TIA. On wor​kup, she was diagnosed wi‌th diabetes mellitus. Whi​ch of the following drugs‍ should be avoided in the‍ management of this patie‍nt?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman with a known history of hypertension and dyslipidemia was admitted for evaluation of recurrent TIA. On workup, she was diagnosed with diabetes mellitus. Which of the following drugs should be avoided in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Piogli‌tazone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rosigl‌itazone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Metf‌ormin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trogli‌tazone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rosiglitazone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rosiglitazone</strong></p><p><strong>Rosiglitazone</strong> should be avoided as it <strong>increases the risk of cardiovascular events</strong> like myocardial infarction and stroke.</p>\n<p>It remains banned in Europe, India, and other countries. It has not been withdrawn in the US and is marketed with a black box warning of increased risk of <strong>myocardial infarction and stroke</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option A: Several studies have shown relative cardiovascular safety of pioglitazone over rosiglitazone.</p>\n<p>Option C: Metformin use is associated with decreased risk of microvascular complications in diabetes. </p>\n<p>Option D: Troglitazone was withdrawn due to increased hepatotoxicity.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1859","text":"A professional boxer is di‌agnosed with diabetes mel​litus. He is concerned ab‌out weight changes as a s​ide effect of anti-diabet​ic drugs and explains tha‌t he has to maintain his ​current weight category. ‍Which of the following wi‍ll you prescribe?","raw_text":"A professional boxer is diagnosed with diabetes mellitus. He is concerned about weight changes as a side effect of anti-diabetic drugs and explains that he has to maintain his current weight category. Which of the following will you prescribe?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Piogli‌tazone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Saxag‌liptin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lirag‌lutide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glicl‌azide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Saxagliptin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Saxagliptin</strong></p><p><strong>Saxagliptin</strong>, a <strong>DPP-4 inhibitor</strong>, can be prescribed for this patient as it has a <strong>weight-neutral</strong> effect.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 168px; width: 645px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.004px;\"><strong>EFFECT</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 494.996px;\"><strong>DRUGS</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.004px;\">Weight gain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 494.996px;\">Sulfonylureas, Insulin, <span class=\"_Tgc _s8w\">Thiazolidinediones</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.004px;\">Weight loss</td>\n<td style=\"width: 494.996px;\">GLP-1 analogues, Pramlintide, SGLT-2 inhibitors, Metformin*</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134.004px;\"><strong>Weight neutral</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 494.996px;\">Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors, <strong>DPP-4 inhibitors</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: *Metformin classification is a bit difficult. It can cause mild weight loss in some patients but few authorities do consider metformin as weight neutral.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9510","text":"In a middle-aged diabetic ‌patient with chronic kidn​ey disease stage 5, which‌ of the following drugs c​an be given safely withou​t altering the dose?","raw_text":"In a middle-aged diabetic patient with chronic kidney disease stage 5, which of the following drugs can be given safely without altering the dose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vildag‌liptin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Linag‌liptin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Saxag‌liptin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sitag‌liptin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Linagliptin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Linagliptin</strong></p><p><strong>Linagliptin</strong> can be given safely without altering the dose for this patient.</p>\n<p>Linagliptin is a <strong>dipeptidyl-peptidase-4 (DPP-4) inhibitor</strong> and is <strong>eliminated</strong> by the<strong> hepatobiliary route. </strong>Hence no dosage adjustment is needed in renal failure.</p>\n<p>It is used in type 2 diabetes as monotherapy or as combination therapy with other drugs when metformin is not tolerated or contraindicated.</p>\n<p><strong>Other DPP-4 inhibitors</strong> like sitagliptin, vildagliptin, saxagliptin, alogliptin show <strong>renal elimination</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1870","text":"Which of the following is ‌not true about dapagliflo​zin?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about dapagliflozin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is indicated in heart f‌ailure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It causes ‌weight gain","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is effec‌tive orally","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It will increase the risk ‌of fractures","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It causes weight gain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It causes weight gain</strong></p><p><strong>Dapagliflozin </strong>does not cause weight gain. It results in <strong>weight loss.</strong></p>\n<p>It acts by <strong>inhibiting </strong>the<strong> SGLT2 </strong>(sodium glucose-2) transporter, which is present on the proximal segment of the renal tubule and responsible for the reabsorption of 80-90% of filtered glucose. It inhibits glucose reabsorption resulting in <strong>glycosuria</strong> and a decrease in blood glucose level. It is effective<strong> orally.</strong></p>\n<p>The common adverse effects include urinary tract infections and genital mycotic infections due to the high glucose content of the urine and also<strong> the high risk of fractures</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other SGLT2 inhibitors include empagliflozin, canagliflozin, and ertugliflozin.</p>\n<p>Note: Empagliflozin has been found to decrease the risk of mortality from cardiovascular causes in diabetic patients. <strong>Dapagliflozin</strong> has been recently approved by the FDA for the treatment of heart failure with reduced ejection fraction(<strong>HFrEF</strong>) in both <strong>diabetic</strong> and <strong>non-diabetic patients</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1853","text":"A patient with diabetes me‌llitus came to the OPD wi​th a history suggestive o‌f recurrent episodes of h​ypoglycemia. She was rece​ntly put on combination t‌herapy. Her symptoms are ​least likely to be caused‍ by which of the followin‍g drugs?","raw_text":"A patient with diabetes mellitus came to the OPD with a history suggestive of recurrent episodes of hypoglycemia. She was recently put on combination therapy. Her symptoms are least likely to be caused by which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acar‌bose","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ins‌ulin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glicl‌azide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Repag‌linide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acarbose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acarbose</strong></p><p><strong>Acarbose</strong> is least likely to cause hypoglycemia among the given options.</p>\n<p>It is an alpha-glucosidase inhibitor. It inhibits the intestinal degradation and absorption of starch and disaccharides and thereby lowers the postprandial rise in glucose levels.</p>\n<p>It has<strong> no role in stimulating insulin secretion</strong> and is therefore, <strong>least likely to cause hypoglycemia</strong> during monotherapy. </p>\n<p>Insulin and insulin secretagogues like sulfonylureas (gliclazide) and meglitinides (repaglinide) can result in hypoglycemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1866","text":"While at a bakery, a man c‌omplains of giddiness and​ palpitations. As the onl‌y doctor there, you're as​ked for help. The man say​s he had just taken his d‌iabetic medications and s​hows a prescription listi‍ng insulin and acarbose. ‍What would you give for i‍mmediate management?","raw_text":"While at a bakery, a man complains of giddiness and palpitations. As the only doctor there, you're asked for help. The man says he had just taken his diabetic medications and shows a prescription listing insulin and acarbose. What would you give for immediate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ho‌ney","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Br‌ead","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Su‌gar","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ca‌ke","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Honey","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Honey</strong></p><p>Giddiness and palpitations after taking insulin and acarbose are suggestive of <strong>hypoglycemia</strong>. <strong>Honey</strong> is the most suitable agent among the given options for the <strong>management of hypoglycemia</strong> in a patient who is on <strong>acarbose</strong> therapy.</p>\n<p>Acarbose is an <strong>alpha-glucosidase inhibitor.</strong> It inhibits the intestinal degradation of starch and disaccharides into glucose. Honey contains<strong> monosaccharides </strong>(fructose and glucose) that do not need to be broken down and are <strong>directly absorbed</strong> and would be beneficial in hypoglycemia caused by alpha-glucosidase inhibitors. However, glucose or dextrose tablets is recommended when available.</p>\n<p>While alpha-glucosidase inhibitors themselves do not cause hypoglycemia, the risk of hypoglycemia is increased when they are taken along with insulin or insulin secretagogues.</p>\n<p>Disaccharides and complex carbohydrates will not be completely broken down and absorbed in a patient who is on alpha-glucosidase inhibitors.</p>\n<p>Option B - Bread predominantly contains <strong>complex carbohydrates</strong> like <strong>starch</strong></p>\n<p>Option C - Sugar contains <strong>sucrose</strong>, a <strong>disaccharide</strong></p>\n<p>Option D - Cake contains sugar (disaccharide) and other complex carbohydrates</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3605","text":"A 52-year-old diabetic pat‌ient presented to the ED ​with complaints of chest ‌pain on exertion. Laborat​ory tests showed dyslipid​emia and features of meta‌bolic syndrome. Which of ​the following drugs was r‍ecently approved for this‍ condition?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old diabetic patient presented to the ED with complaints of chest pain on exertion. Laboratory tests showed dyslipidemia and features of metabolic syndrome. Which of the following drugs was recently approved for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alegl‌itazar","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sarogl‌itazar","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Muragl‌itazar","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rosigl‌itazone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Saroglitazar","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Saroglitazar</strong></p><p><strong>Saroglitazar </strong>is a <strong>dual PPAR alpha/gamma agonist</strong> and is approved for <strong>diabetic dyslipidemia</strong> only in India.</p>\n<p><strong>Peroxisome proliferator-activated receptors</strong> (PPARs) are a superfamily of nuclear receptors involved in the regulation of energy homeostasis and metabolic function. It has three subtypes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>PPARα - reduces triglyceride level </li>\n<li>PPARγ - causes insulin sensitization </li>\n<li>PPARδ - enhances fatty acids metabolism</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Of the three PPAR isoforms, synthetic pharmaceutical ligands that activate <strong>PPARalpha</strong> (the antidyslipidemic <strong>fibrates)</strong> and <strong>PPARgamma</strong> (the antidiabetic <strong>thiazolidinediones)</strong> have been studied extensively. Recently developed dual PPARalpha/gamma agonists combine the therapeutic effects of these drugs to be used in <strong>type 2 diabetes with dyslipidemia. </strong></p>\n<p>Options A and C - Aleglitazar and muraglitazar are also dual PPAR alpha/gamma agonists but their development was halted due to increased risk of cardiovascular mortality.</p>\n<p>Option D - Rosiglitazone is a PPAR gamma agonist indicated in type 2 diabetes mellitus. It has been withdrawn from many countries including India due to increased cardiovascular mortality. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1876","text":"Which of the following is ‌approved as an adjunctive​ treatment for type 2 dia‌betes mellitus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is approved as an adjunctive treatment for type 2 diabetes mellitus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bromoc‌riptine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Phen‌ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ropin‌irole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metoclo‌promide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bromocriptine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bromocriptine</strong></p><p><strong>Bromocriptine </strong>is a <strong>dopamine agonist </strong>that has been approved as an adjunctive treatment for <strong>diabetes mellitus.</strong></p>\n<p>The exact mechanism is unknown but may involve alteration in circadian (hypothalamic) control of blood glucose levels. A fast-release, low-dose formulation of bromocriptine is approved for type 2 diabetes that has to be<strong> administered within 3 hours of awakening.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B - Phenytoin is a Na<sup>+</sup> channel blocker and is an antiepileptic drug</p>\n<p>Option C - Ropinirole is an antiparkinsonian drug that is a dopamine agonist and is also approved for treatment of restless leg syndrome</p>\n<p>Option D - Metoclopramide is a 5HT<sub>4</sub> agonist and D<sub>2</sub> blocker and used as an antiemetic agent</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3599","text":"A 54-year-old woman comes ‌to the diabetic clinic fo​r follow up. Her lipid pr‌ofile shows high LDL chol​esterol. Which bile acid ​sequestrant would you pre‌scribe for her?","raw_text":"A 54-year-old woman comes to the diabetic clinic for follow up. Her lipid profile shows high LDL cholesterol. Which bile acid sequestrant would you prescribe for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coles‌evelam","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Coles‌tipol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cholest‌yramine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ezet‌imibe","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Colesevelam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Colesevelam</strong></p><p><strong>Colesevelam</strong> can be prescribed for her as it is the only <strong>bile acid sequestrant </strong>that is specifically <strong>approved</strong> for<strong> </strong>the treatment of type 2<strong> diabetes mellitus. </strong></p>\n<p>It is used to <strong>reduce LDL cholesterol </strong>and<strong> improve glycemic control</strong> in diabetics.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"TDAypbyZ","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"49_thorax___lungs","title":"49.Thorax & Lungs","num_questions":29,"total_marks":116,"duration":39,"time_per_question":80.69,"questions":[{"id":"MB7708","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about th‌e surgical anatomy of the​ lungs?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about the surgical anatomy of the lungs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"There are 10 bronchopulmon‌ary segments on the right‌ lung.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Each bronchopulmonary segm‌ent has a separate lympha‌tic drainage.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The tracheobronchial tree ‌is normally lined by pseu‌dostratified ciliated col​umnar cells.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Segmentectomy is done afte‌r locating the segmental ‌branch of bronchial arter​y.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Segmentectomy is done after locating the segmental branch of bronchial artery.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e4e1c525261f4da6a9361e89f4a016a8x600x684.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Segmentectomy is done after locating the segmental branch of bronchial artery.</strong></p><p>In segmentectomy, the segment is removed from the lobe after locating the <strong>intersegmental vein.</strong></p>\n<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Bronchopulmonary segments</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> are </span>well-defined, anatomic, functional, and surgical<span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> areas of the lung. </span>There are <strong>10</strong> bronchopulmonary segments <strong>each</strong> on the right and<strong> </strong>the left lung. <span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The segments help in <strong>segmental resection</strong> and <strong>bronchoscopy </strong>as each segment has a separate:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Tertiary bronchus</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Segmental artery</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Autonomic nerve supply</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Lymphatic drainage</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The tracheobronchial tree is normally lined by <strong>pseudostratified ciliated columnar cells </strong>and<strong> mucous cells.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7710","text":"An infant has a congenital‌ bronchopulmonary malform‌ation as shown in the CT ​scan given below. Which o​f the following is most l​ikely to supply the marke‌d structure?","raw_text":"An infant has a congenital bronchopulmonary malformation as shown in the CT scan given below. Which of the following is most likely to supply the marked structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Internal ma‌mmary artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchia‌l artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonar‌y artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thoraci‌c aorta","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Thoracic aorta","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cac0f56ef35d43b0a72010bfbc60002c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Thoracic aorta</strong></p><p>The given CT shows an <strong>intralobar pulmonary sequestration</strong> of the left lung.<strong> </strong>The<strong> thoracic aorta </strong>commonly supplies the sequestered lobe of the lung.</p>\n<p>Pulmonary sequestration is an area of embryonic lung tissue, separate from the lung. It receives blood supply from an <strong>anomalous systemic artery from the aorta</strong>. It is of two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Extralobar sequestration</strong> occurs primarily on the left side and blood supply is usually from the thoracic or upper abdominal aorta</li>\n<li><strong>Intralobar sequestration</strong> occurs in the lower lobes predominantly (&gt;95%). It is equally distributed between the right and left lower lobe and blood supply is from the descending thoracic aorta, venous drainage via pulmonary veins</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1609\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Treatment involves <strong>resection</strong> of the pulmonary sequestration.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7718","text":"A 50-year-old patient on D‌OTS therapy was rushed to‌ the casualty after havin​g massive hemoptysis. Whi​ch of the following is fa​lse regarding this condit‌ion? DOTS- Directly Obser‌ved Therapy Short Course","raw_text":"<p>A 50-year-old patient on DOTS therapy was rushed to the casualty after having massive hemoptysis. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?</p>\n<p>DOTS- Directly Observed Therapy Short Course</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intubation is done into th‌e non-bleeding lung.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Massive hemoptysis is defi‌ned as expectoration of o‌ver 750 mL of blood withi​n 24-hours.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Presence of significant ca‌vitary disease is an indi‌cation for urgent surgery​.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bleeding is most likely du‌e to pulmonary artery ero‌sion.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Massive hemoptysis is defined as expectoration of over 750 mL of blood within 24-hours.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Massive hemoptysis is defined as expectoration of over 750 mL of blood within 24-hours.</strong></p><p><strong>Massive hemoptysis</strong> is defined as the expectoration of <strong>over 600 mL of blood within a 24-hour period. </strong>Common causes include tuberculosis, bronchiectasis, and cancer. It is a medical emergency.</p>\n<p>Any hemoptysis that can cause <strong>airway compromise</strong> or lead to <strong>hemodynamic collapse</strong> is called massive hemoptysis. This includes blood loss of <strong>&gt;600mL/24h</strong> or <strong>&gt;150ml/h</strong> which can flood the anatomical dead space of the lung.</p>\n<p>Bleeding can either be from the <strong>bronchial artery circulation</strong> (most common) or from the <strong>pulmonary artery circulation</strong>. Bleeding from <strong>cavitary lesions</strong> is due to <strong>pulmonary artery</strong> erosion.</p>\n<p>The <strong>chest X-ray</strong> is the first investigation followed by <strong>rigid or flexible bronchoscopy</strong>.</p>\n<p>The cause of death in massive hemoptysis is usually due to <strong>asphyxiation</strong> (rather than blood loss itself). This is why <strong>airway stabilization</strong> by <strong>intubating into the non-bleeding lung</strong> is the priority. After ventilation is achieved, the bleeding site should be identified using <strong>bronchoscopy</strong>. Bleeding is controlled by laser, bronchial occlusion, or arterial <strong>embolization</strong>. <strong>Surgical resection</strong> of the involved area is also done in some cases.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 690\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Indications for urgent surgical intervention for massive hemoptysis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of a fungus ball</li>\n<li>Presence of a lung abscess</li>\n<li>Presence of significant cavitary disease</li>\n<li>Failure to control the bleeding.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7721","text":"Where is Rasmussen’s aneur‌ysm seen?","raw_text":"Where is Rasmussen’s aneurysm seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Internal tho‌racic artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonar‌y artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bronchia‌l artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subclavi‌an artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pulmonary artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pulmonary artery</strong></p><p>The aneurysm of the <strong>pulmonary artery</strong> within the <strong>tuberculous</strong> cavity is called <strong>Rasmussen’s aneurysm.</strong></p>\n<p>Rasmussen's aneurysm is an inflammatory <strong>pseudo-aneurysmal</strong> dilatation. This occurs during the <strong>healing</strong> of the tuberculous cavity. There is a progressive weakening of the arterial wall, in which both the tunica adventitia and media are replaced by granulation tissue. This is gradually replaced by fibrin resulting in thinning of the arterial wall leading to pseudoaneurysm formation.</p>\n<p>Rupture of Rasmussen's aneurysm can cause <strong>massive hemoptysis</strong>. <strong>CT angiography</strong> plays an important role in locating the aneurysm.</p>\n<p>Most cases of massive hemoptysis arise from the <strong>bronchial arterial tree</strong> because of <strong>high pressure</strong> within bronchial arteries. Even when a pulmonary aneurysm is present, the actual bleeding may be from the bronchial artery, rather than from the pulmonary artery itself.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7775","text":"A 50-year-old chronic smok‌er with left-sided lung c‌ancer is rushed to the em​ergency department with m​assive hemoptysis. On exa​mination, pulse - 102 bpm‌, BP - 80/50 mmHg, RR - 3‌0/min. Which of the follo‌wing will be part of your‌ management plan for this‌ patient? Positioning the​ patient in the right lat‌eral decubitus position A‌dministration of I.V flui​ds CT scan Rigid bronchos​copy Intubation","raw_text":"<p>A 50-year-old chronic smoker with left-sided lung cancer is rushed to the emergency department with massive hemoptysis. On examination, pulse - 102 bpm, BP - 80/50 mmHg, RR - 30/min. Which of the following will be part of your management plan for this patient?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Positioning the patient in the right lateral decubitus position </li>\n<li>Administration of I.V fluids</li>\n<li>CT scan</li>\n<li>Rigid bronchoscopy</li>\n<li>Intubation</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ‌4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, ‌4, 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2, ‌3, 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2, 4, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2, 4, 5</strong></p><p>The <strong>left side</strong> is most likely to be the <strong>bleeding side</strong> in this patient. Hence, the patient should be placed in the <strong>left lateral decubitus position</strong> to prevent aspiration into the unaffected lung. Also, a <strong>CT scan</strong> is <strong>not indicated</strong> as he is <strong>hemodynamically unstable</strong>.</p>\n<p>Treatment priorities in the management of massive hemoptysis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>First priorities are to maintain the <strong>airway</strong>, optimize <strong>oxygenation</strong> and <strong>stabilize the hemodynamic status</strong> by administering IV fluids.</li>\n<li><strong>Chest X-ray</strong> is the <strong>first investigation</strong> of choice. CT scan is done only in hemodynamically stable patients.</li>\n<li>If the bleeding side is known, the patient should be placed in a <strong>lateral decubitus position, with the bleeding side down</strong> to prevent aspiration into the unaffected lung and given supplemental oxygen.</li>\n<li>If large-volume bleeding continues or the airway is compromised, the patient should be <strong>intubated</strong> and undergo <strong>emergency bronchoscopy.</strong></li>\n<li>The bleeding site is localized using <strong>flexible/rigid bronchoscopy.</strong> </li>\n<li>Control the hemorrhage:</li>\n<ul>\n<li>Endobronchially with Nd: YAG laser or bronchial occlusion</li>\n<li>Endovascularly with <strong>bronchial</strong> and/or <strong>pulmonary artery</strong> embolization</li>\n<li>Electrocauterization</li>\n<li><strong>Surgically </strong>with <strong>resection of the involved lobe</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>The cause of the hemoptysis must be determined to definitively prevent a recurrence.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7392","text":"A 54-year-old comes to the‌ casualty with a history ‌of massive hemoptysis. Hi​s routine investigation a​nd chest X-ray were norma​l. Which of the following‌ you will not do to preve‌nt hemoptysis?","raw_text":"A 54-year-old comes to the casualty with a history of massive hemoptysis. His routine investigation and chest X-ray were normal. Which of the following you will not do to prevent hemoptysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bronchial artery embolizat‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonary artery embolizat‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Procoagul‌ant drugs","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Surgical rem‌oval of lobe","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Procoagulant drugs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Procoagulant drugs</strong></p><p>Procoagulant drugs are not used to prevent hemoptysis. Procoagulant drugs like <strong>vasopressin</strong> are indicated only if there is an <strong>abnormal coagulation profile</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Massive hemoptysis</strong> is generally defined as the expectoration of over <strong>600 mL</strong> of blood within <strong>a 24-h period.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment priorities in the management of massive hemoptysis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>First priorities are to secure the <strong>airway</strong>, optimize <strong>oxygenation</strong> and <strong>stabilize the hemodynamic status</strong> by administering IV fluids.</li>\n<li><strong>Chest X-ray</strong> is the <strong>first investigation</strong> of choice. CT scan is done only in hemodynamically stable patients.</li>\n<li>If the bleeding site is known, the patient should be placed in a <strong>lateral decubitus position with the bleeding side down</strong> to prevent aspiration into the unaffected lung. Supplemental oxygen should be given.</li>\n<li>If large-volume bleeding continues or the airway is compromised, the patient should be <strong>intubated</strong> and undergo an <strong>emergency bronchoscopy.</strong></li>\n<li>The bleeding site is localized using <strong>flexible/rigid bronchoscopy.</strong> </li>\n<li>Control the hemorrhage:</li>\n<ul>\n<li>Endobronchially with Nd: YAG laser or bronchial occlusion</li>\n<li>Endovascularly with <strong>bronchial</strong> and/or <strong>pulmonary artery</strong> embolization</li>\n<li>Electrocauterization</li>\n<li><strong>Surgically </strong>with <strong>resection of the involved lobe</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>The cause of the hemoptysis must be determined to definitively prevent a recurrence.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4166","text":"A 20-year-old man presents‌ with h/o sudden breathle‌ssness while watching tel​evision. Upon examination​, he is noticed to be 6 f​t tall and very lean. He ‌is not breathless at the ‌moment. Chest X-ray revea‌ls a right-sided pneumoth‌orax of 1.5 cm. He does n‌ot smoke or drink. Which ​of the following is the n‌ext step in the managemen‌t of this patient?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old man presents with h/o sudden breathlessness while watching television. Upon examination, he is noticed to be 6 ft tall and very lean. He is not breathless at the moment. Chest X-ray reveals a right-sided pneumothorax of 1.5 cm. He does not smoke or drink. Which of the following is the next step in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Outpatient follow-up in 2-‌4 weeks","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Place a C‌hest drain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Do a c‌hest CT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aspirate with an 18G needl‌e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Outpatient follow-up in 2-4 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Outpatient follow-up in 2-4 weeks</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>primary</strong> <strong>pneumothorax</strong>. The next step would be to <strong>discharge and</strong> <strong>follow up in 2-4 weeks</strong> as the <strong>size</strong> is <strong>&lt;2cm</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Pneumothorax</strong> is the <strong>abnormal</strong> accumulation of <strong>air</strong> in the <strong>pleural</strong> <strong>space</strong>. It can be <strong>primary</strong> or <strong>secondary</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Primary</strong> <strong>pneumothorax</strong> can occur spontaneously due to rupture of the apical blebs. Risk factors include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Males &gt; Females</li>\n<li>Young, tall, and thin individuals</li>\n<li>Family history</li>\n<li>Smoking</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It resolves spontaneously hence just follow-up is usually needed in most cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Secondary</strong> <strong>pneumothorax</strong> occurs due to some underlying pathology. Risk factors include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Age&gt; 50 years</li>\n<li>Smoking</li>\n<li>COPD</li>\n<li>Asthma</li>\n<li>Cystic fibrosis</li>\n<li>Infection (pneumonia, abscess, and tuberculosis)</li>\n<li>Interstitial lung disease</li>\n<li>Connective tissue disease</li>\n<li>Procedures (subclavian lines, thoracentesis, bronchoscopy, and mechanical ventilation)  </li>\n<li>Blunt trauma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients with pneumothorax present with <strong>acute dyspnea</strong> and <strong>pleuritic chest pain.</strong> A physical examination will reveal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">An enlarged hemithorax</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Decreased chest movements</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hyper-resonance on percussion</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Absent vocal fremitus</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Diminished breath sounds</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Diagnosis is made by <strong>chest X-ray (best initial test)</strong>, CT scan, or USG.</p>\n<p><strong>Management</strong> of pneumothorax:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary</strong> pneumothorax\n<ul>\n<li>&lt;2 cm size: Discharge and follow-up in 2-4 weeks</li>\n<li>&gt;2 cm size and/or breathless: Aspirate with 16 G cannula → If fails, place a chest drain of size 8-14 Fr</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary</strong> pneumothorax\n<ul>\n<li>&lt;1 cm size: High-flow oxygen with observation for 24 hours</li>\n<li>1-2 cm size: Aspirate with 16 G cannula → If fails, place a chest drain of size 8-14 Fr</li>\n<li>&gt;2 cm size: Place chest drain of size 8-14 Fr</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6526","text":"The ultrasound diagnosis o‌f pneumothorax depends on‌ : Presence of Quad sign ​Absence of Sliding sign P​resence of Seashore sign ​Absence of Comet-tail art‌ifacts Presence of a barc‌ode sign","raw_text":"<p>The ultrasound diagnosis of pneumothorax depends on :</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Presence of Quad sign</li>\n<li>Absence of Sliding sign</li>\n<li>Presence of Seashore sign</li>\n<li>Absence of Comet-tail artifacts</li>\n<li>Presence of a barcode sign</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,2, ‌and 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 4,‌ and 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3 a‌nd 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2, 4, and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2c8b904bbb4e4f4596fa1a7e52ea1433x465x361.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2, 4, and 5</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 415\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The ultrasound diagnosis of <strong>pneumothorax</strong> depends on the <strong>presence of barcode sign</strong>, <strong>absence of the sliding lung </strong>and <strong>absence of comet-tail artifacts</strong> that are seen in relation to a normal lung–pleural interface.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Normally, <strong>lung sliding </strong>corresponds to the to-and-fro movement of the visceral pleura on the parietal pleura that occurs with respiration. It is a <strong>dynamic sign</strong> and can be identified on ultrasound as horizontal movement along the pleural line.</p>\n<p>In <strong>pneumothorax</strong>, the air separates visceral and parietal pleura and prevents visualization of visceral pleura. Hence lung sliding is absent and typical to and fro movement and shimmering of the pleural lines will not be present.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1791\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1791\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p class=\"p p-first\"><strong>Comet tail artifacts</strong> are seen in the normal lungs due to the acoustic impedance differences between water and air, whereas it is absent in pneumothorax. </p>\n<p class=\"p p-first\">The image given below shows a sliding sign seen in a normal lung.  </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p p-first\">Option A: The <strong>quad sign</strong> is seen in <strong>pleural effusion</strong>. It consists of four lines representing the pleura, rib, fluid, and lung. </p>\n<p class=\"p p-first\">Option C: The motionless portion of the chest above the pleural line creates horizontal ‘waves,’ and the sliding below the pleural line creates a granular pattern, the ‘sand’. The resultant picture is one that resembles waves crashing onto the sand and is therefore called the ‘<strong>seashore sign</strong>’ and is present in <strong>normal lungs</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7765","text":"A patient presents with br‌eathlessness to the casua‌lty after a motorcycle ac​cident. On examination, t​he left chest is hyper-re​sonant on percussion and ‌shows reduced air entry o‌ver the same side. His pu‌lse is 110 bpm, RR - 25/m‌in, BP - 80/45mmHg, and J‌VP was elevated. What wil​l be your immediate manag‌ement?","raw_text":"A patient presents with breathlessness to the casualty after a motorcycle accident. On examination, the left chest is hyper-resonant on percussion and shows reduced air entry over the same side. His pulse is 110 bpm, RR - 25/min, BP - 80/45mmHg, and JVP was elevated.  What will be your immediate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insert a wide bore needle ‌in 5th intercostal space ‌anterior to the mid axill​ary line","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chest‌ X-ray","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Insert a chest tube in the‌ 5th intercostal space in‌ the anterior axillary li​ne","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intub‌ation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Insert a wide bore needle in 5th intercostal space anterior to the mid axillary line","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/407a0b97f386468ca6bfd97d8cbe7ccfx600x577.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/067352b931b74258b21f40683c114a13x1063x3817.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Insert a wide bore needle in 5th intercostal space anterior to the mid axillary line</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>tension pneumothorax</strong>. The first line of management in tension pneumothorax is to <strong>insert a wide-bore needle</strong> in the <strong>5th intercostal space</strong> just <strong>anterior to the midaxillary line</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Pneumothorax</strong> is the abnormal accumulation of <strong>air in the pleural space</strong>. <strong>Tension pneumothorax</strong> develops when air enters the pleural space through a point that acts as a <strong>one-way valve</strong>. More and more air enters with every breath without any way for the air to escape. This is a <strong>medical emergency</strong> where the patient can quickly deteriorate. </p>\n<p>A patient with <strong>pneumothorax</strong> presents with <strong>acute dyspnea</strong> and <strong>pleuritic chest pain</strong>. A physical examination will reveal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An enlarged hemithorax</li>\n<li>Decreased chest movements</li>\n<li>Hyper-resonance on percussion</li>\n<li>Absent vocal fremitus</li>\n<li>Diminished breath sounds</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In addition to the above findings, a <strong>tension pneumothorax</strong> would also have the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Deviation of trachea to the opposite side</li>\n<li>Distended neck vein</li>\n<li>Cyanosis</li>\n<li>Sudden onset hemodynamic compromise:\n<ul>\n<li>Hypotension</li>\n<li>Tachycardia</li>\n<li>Low O<sub>2</sub> saturation</li>\n<li>Loss of consciousness</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A <strong>diagnosis</strong> of tension pneumothorax is usually made based on the <strong>clinical findings</strong>. If the tension in the pleural cavity is not relieved, the patient can die due to hemodynamic collapse. Hence, <strong>treatment should never be delayed </strong>waiting for radiological confirmation.</p>\n<p>The management of tension pneumothorax<strong> </strong>is to convert a tension pneumothorax into a simple pneumothorax. This can be done by rapid decompression using a <strong>wide-bore cannula</strong> on the affected side, followed by the <strong>insertion of a chest tube</strong> for definitive management.</p>\n<p>The safest site for insertion of a drain is in the triangle (<strong>Triangle of safety</strong>) that lies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior to the mid-axillary line</li>\n<li>Above the level of the nipple</li>\n<li>Below and lateral to the pectoralis major muscle</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p>If<strong> immediate management</strong> is asked, the answer will be to insert a wide-bore needle in the 5th intercostal space in the midaxillary line.</p>\n<p>If <strong>definitive management</strong> is asked, the answer will be to insert an intercostal tube in the triangle of safety.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4169","text":"A 34-year-old man is broug‌ht to the emergency depar‌tment with a stab wound t​o his right chest followi​ng a fight at the local b​ar. His vitals are normal‌. On examination, the tra‌chea is central and a pen‌etrating wound is noted o‌n the right chest. A suck‌ing sound is heard during​ inspiration. Which of th‌e following is the initia‌l management in this case​?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old man is brought to the emergency department with a stab wound to his right chest following a fight at the local bar. His vitals are normal. On examination, the trachea is central and a penetrating wound is noted on the right chest. A sucking sound is heard during inspiration. Which of the following is the initial management in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Occlusive dressing tapped ‌on the 3 sides","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Placing chest drain at the‌ site of the wound","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Surgical exploration of th‌e wound","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Needle dec‌ompression","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Occlusive dressing tapped on the 3 sides","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Occlusive dressing tapped on the 3 sides</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>open pneumothorax.</strong> Initial management, in this case, is to apply <strong>occlusive dressing taped on the 3 sides.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Open</strong> <strong>pneumothorax</strong> is due to a large open defect in the chest, making <strong>intrathoracic</strong> <strong>pressure</strong> <strong>equal</strong> to <strong>atmospheric</strong> <strong>pressure</strong>. In this defect, the air is preferentially drawn through the defect, rather than through the trachea. Air gets accumulated causing pneumothorax leading to hypoventilation and hypoxia. It acts as a <strong>sucking</strong> <strong>chest</strong> <strong>wound. </strong>Sucking sound during inspiration is characteristic.</p>\n<p>It is managed initially with the <strong>application</strong> of a <strong>sterile</strong> <strong>occlusive</strong> dressing <strong>tapped on the 3 sides</strong>. <strong>Then</strong> a <strong>chest tube</strong> is inserted at a different site, away from the wound.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4172","text":"A patient presents with a ‌productive cough, fever, ‌and breathlessness. On ex​amination, decreased brea​th sounds and dull note o​n percussion is noted on ‌the left side. A chest X-‌ray is given below. USG-g‌uided aspiration revealed‌ frank pus. Chest tube wa‌s inserted but there was ​no improvement in symptom‌s. How will you manage th‌is patient?","raw_text":"A patient presents with a productive cough, fever, and breathlessness. On examination, decreased breath sounds and dull note on percussion is noted on the left side. A chest X-ray is given below. USG-guided aspiration revealed frank pus. Chest tube was inserted but there was no improvement in symptoms. How will you manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"VATS deb‌ridement","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Antib‌iotics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pleur‌odesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decorticati‌on procedure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. VATS debridement","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8e/Pleural_effusion_of_primary_pulmonary_tuberculosis.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. VATS debridement</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>empyema</strong> <strong>thoracis</strong>. Symptoms failed to resolve on treatment with chest tube insertion, hence it is managed by <strong>VATS</strong> <strong>debridement.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Empyema thoracis</strong> is an infection of the pleural space. It commonly progresses from an acute phase with thin fluid that can be drained completely with a chest tube or small-bore catheter to a more turbid and thick fluid that might <strong>loculate.</strong> It is an <strong>exudative effusion.</strong> It can be caused due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Underlying infection of the lung (pneumonia or abscess)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Complication after chest surgery</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Infected hemothorax</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Complication of Pleural effusion</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Trauma (esophageal perforation)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Osteomyelitis of ribs</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An empyema thoracis on chest x-ray can <strong>mimic</strong> a <strong>pleural effusion</strong> or a <strong>lung abscess.</strong></p>\n<p>The procedure <strong>VATS</strong> (Video-Assisted Thoracoscopic Surgery) is used in the <strong>debridement</strong> of <strong>empyema.</strong> At the end of the procedure, chest drains are placed for dependent drainage. </p>\n<p><strong>If VATS fails</strong> to re-expand the lung after drainage, a more radical procedure <strong>(decortication)</strong> may be done.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7767","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an indication for per‌forming thoracotomy in pa​tients with blunt chest i​njury?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an indication for performing thoracotomy in patients with blunt chest injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt;1500 ml drainage after‌ placing an intercostal t‌ube","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Continuous bleeding throug‌h intercostal tube of mor‌e than 200 ml/hour for th​ree or more hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac ‌tamponade","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rib fr‌acture","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rib fracture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rib fracture</strong></p><p>A rib fracture is <strong>not</strong> an indication for thoracotomy.</p>\n<p>Indications for thoracotomy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Initial</strong> tube thoracostomy drainage of<strong> &gt;1000 ml (penetrating injury)</strong> or<strong> &gt;1500 ml (blunt injury) </strong>of frank blood</li>\n<li><strong>Ongoing </strong>tube thoracostomy drainage of<strong> &gt;200 ml/ hr</strong> for<strong> 3 consecutive hours</strong> in<strong> non-coagulopathic</strong> patients with<strong> hemothorax</strong></li>\n<li>Caked hemothorax despite the placement of two chest tubes</li>\n<li>Massive air leak from chest tube with inadequate ventilation</li>\n<li>Open pneumothorax</li>\n<li>Great vessel injury</li>\n<li>Pericardial tamponade</li>\n<li>Cardiac herniation</li>\n<li>Tracheobronchial injury</li>\n<li>Esophageal perforation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4410","text":"All of the following will ‌be your differential diag‌nosis in a patient with a​nterior mediastinal mass ​except:","raw_text":"All of the following will be your differential diagnosis in a patient with anterior mediastinal mass except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Germ ce‌ll tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymp‌homa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thyroi‌d mass","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neurogen‌ic tumors","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neurogenic tumors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/387a96641ae9408b87815187b7336c85x425x480.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neurogenic tumors</strong></p><p><strong>Neurogenic tumors</strong> are found in the <strong>posterior mediastinum</strong> and not in the anterior mediastinum.</p>\n<p>They are the <strong>most common</strong> primary tumors of the posterior mediastinum. Schwannomas and Neurilemomas are the most common neurogenic tumors.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7727","text":"A 35-year-old patient comp‌lains of double vision th‌at gets worse after he wa​tches television. He also​ has drooping of the eyel​ids that gets better afte‌r a nap. Serology is posi‌tive for anti-acetylcholi‌ne receptor antibodies. A‌ CT scan of his chest is ‌given below. Which of the​ following is false regar‌ding his condition?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old patient complains of double vision that gets worse after he watches television. He also has drooping of the eyelids that gets better after a nap. Serology is positive for anti-acetylcholine receptor antibodies. A CT scan of his chest is given below. Which of the following is false regarding his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the most common tumo‌r of the anterior mediast‌inum.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Masoaka classification sys‌tem is used for staging.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tumor size is a reliable i‌ndicator of malignancy.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Surgery can be performed t‌hrough a median sternotom‌y approach.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tumor size is a reliable indicator of malignancy.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9a2e6c29e4a84b47aab20780d576ba9f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tumor size is a reliable indicator of malignancy.</strong></p><p>The given clinical features of <strong>myasthenia gravis</strong> along with the CT scan image showing an <strong>anterior mediastinal mass</strong> are suggestive of <strong>thymoma</strong>. The only reliable indicator of malignancy in thymoma is the <strong>capsular invasion, </strong>not tumor size.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 956\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Thymoma is the <strong>most common mediastinal tumor</strong> and is derived from the thymus gland. Thymomas vary in behavior from benign to aggressively invasive. <strong>Masoaka classification system</strong> is used to stage thymomas.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 956\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Myasthenia gravis</strong> is often related to thymomas. Symptoms of myasthenia gravis improve after excision of the thymus gland even in the absence of a thymoma. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 956\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Complete thymectomy</strong> is the treatment for thymoma. It can be performed by two approaches:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 956\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>median sternotomy</strong> approach</li>\n<li>A <strong>transcervical</strong> approach with or without an additional <strong>VATS procedure </strong>(Video-Assisted Thoracoscopic Surgery)<strong> </strong>for smaller thymomas</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7770","text":"Which stage of Masaoka sta‌ging does a patient with ‌thymoma invading the grea​t vessels come under?","raw_text":"Which stage of Masaoka staging does a patient with thymoma invading the great vessels come under?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stag‌e II","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stage‌ IV A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stag‌e III","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stage‌ IV B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stage III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stage III</strong></p><p>Thymoma invading great vessels come under <strong>stage III</strong> of the Masaoka staging system.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 703\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Masaoka staging system</strong> is the most commonly accepted staging system for <strong>thymoma</strong>. It is based on the presence or absence of gross or microscopic invasion of the capsule and of surrounding structures, and metastasis.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<table width=\"487\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p><strong>Stage</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"356\">\n<p><strong>Features</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p>Stage I</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"356\">\n<p>Encapsulated tumor with no gross or microscopic evidence of capsular invasion</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p>Stage II</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"356\">\n<p>Gross <strong>capsular invasion</strong> or invasion into the mediastinal fat or pleura or microscopic capsular invasion</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p><strong>Stage III</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"356\">\n<p><strong>Gross invasion into the pericardium, great vessels, or lung</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p>Stage IVA</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"356\">\n<p>Pleural or pericardial<strong> dissemination</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"131\">\n<p>Stage IVB</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"356\">\n<p>Lymphogenous or hematogenous <strong>metastasis</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7729","text":"A toddler with cough and p‌rogressive breathing diff‌iculty was found to have ​a poorly defined solid ma​ss in the mediastinum. Wh​at is the most common med‌iastinal mass in this age‌ group?","raw_text":"A toddler with cough and progressive breathing difficulty was found to have a  poorly defined solid mass in the mediastinum. What is the most common mediastinal mass in this age group?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thy‌moma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mesenchy‌mal tumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neurogen‌ic tumor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bronchog‌enic Cyst","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neurogenic tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neurogenic tumor</strong></p><p>The most common mediastinal mass in <strong>young children</strong> is <strong>neurogenic tumors</strong> (50%-60%). In most cases (&lt; 4 years of age), they are invariably malignant.</p>\n<p>The most common mediastinal masses in <strong>adults</strong> are <strong>thymomas</strong> and <strong>thymic cysts</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7761","text":"Lymphomas most commonly aﬀ‌ect which compartment of ‌the mediastinum?","raw_text":"Lymphomas most commonly aﬀect which compartment of the mediastinum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ante‌rior","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mid‌dle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post‌erior","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infe‌rior","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anterior","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anterior</strong></p><p><strong>Lymphoma</strong> most commonly occurs in the <strong>anterior mediastinum. </strong>Occasional involvement of the middle compartment and rare involvement of posterior mediastinum is seen.</p>\n<p><strong>Chemotherapy</strong> and/or radiotherapy results in a cure rate of up to 90% for early-stage Hodgkin’s disease and up to 60% for more advanced stages.</p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most common <strong>tumor</strong> of the middle mediastinum: <strong>Lymphoma.</strong></li>\n<li>Most common <strong>mass</strong> of the middle mediastinum: <strong>Pericardial Cyst.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7768","text":"What is the most common be‌nign tumor of the lung?","raw_text":"What is the most common benign tumor of the lung?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hama‌rtoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Alveolar‌ adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chon‌droma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fib‌roma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hamartoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4769676e098e4a2b83e2ee16214faea1x1280x1276.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0aebf4dd8a3b4e05b2297875de35cd11x1280x883.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hamartoma</strong></p><p>The most common benign tumor of the lung is <strong>hamartoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Hamartoma is a developmental abnormality containing mesothelial and endothelial elements. They are manifested by the <strong>overgrowth of cartilage</strong>. It is typically seen in <strong>40-60 years </strong>of age, more common in <strong>males.</strong></p>\n<p>It is usually<strong> </strong>seen in the periphery of the lung and grows slowly. <strong>Popcorn calcification in chest X-ray </strong>is suggestive of a hamartoma.</p>\n<p>Chest X-ray showing a popcorn calcification in the apex of the right lung.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Diagnosis and definitive treatment are achieved by <strong>excision of the lesion.</strong></p>\n<p>Gross specimen showing hamartoma of the lung.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Popcorn calcification is also seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chondroid lesions like chondrosarcoma</li>\n<li>Fibrous dysplasia</li>\n<li>Fibroadenoma breast</li>\n<li>Calcified uterine fibroid</li>\n<li>Metaphyses and epiphyses of long bones of children with osteogenesis imperfecta</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7772","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about bronchial carc‌inoid?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about bronchial carcinoid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Smoking is a‌ risk factor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metastasis‌ is common","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is usually asymptomatic‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is associated with MEN ‌2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is usually asymptomatic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is usually asymptomatic</strong></p><p><strong>Bronchial carcinoid </strong>is usually <strong>asymptomatic.</strong></p>\n<p>Bronchial carcinoids are the most indolent of the spectrum of pulmonary <strong>neuroendocrine tumors.</strong> They arise from the <strong>Kulchitsky cells</strong> in the bronchial epithelium. Most tumors are confined to the main stem bronchus and commonly project into the lumen. It is associated with <strong>MEN 1.</strong></p>\n<p>Most patients affected are &lt;40 years and it is not related to smoking. It is usually <strong>asymptomatic</strong> and only 10% of bronchial carcinoids give rise to <strong>carcinoid syndrome</strong> which is characterized by intermittent attacks of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diarrhea</li>\n<li>Flushing</li>\n<li>Bronchospasm</li>\n<li>Cyanosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Bronchial carcinoids follow a relatively <strong>benign</strong> course and <strong>do not metastasize</strong> to distant sites. They have a <strong>good prognosis</strong> and can be <strong>easily resected. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7713","text":"In patients with lung canc‌er, presence of which of ‌the following indicates a​n immune-mediated paraneo​plastic syndrome?","raw_text":"In patients with lung cancer, presence of which of the following indicates an immune-mediated paraneoplastic syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperc‌alcemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cushing’s‌ Syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypona‌tremia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peripheral‌ Neuropathy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Peripheral Neuropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Peripheral Neuropathy</strong></p><p><strong>Peripheral neuropathy</strong> is an <strong>immune-mediated paraneoplastic syndrome. </strong>It is due to the secretion of antigens normally expressed only by the nervous system which leads to antibodies production against CNS.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 653\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Lung cancers are capable of producing a variety of paraneoplastic syndromes. They may produce symptoms even before any local symptoms are produced by the primary tumor, thereby aiding in early diagnosis. </p>\n<p>Paraneoplastic syndromes in lung cancer:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 321px;\" width=\"487\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 235.84px;\">System</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.918px;\">Paraneoplastic syndromes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 235.84px;\">Endocrine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.918px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 653\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Hypercalcemia (ectopic parathyroid hormone)</li>\n<li>Cushing’s syndrome</li>\n<li>SIADH</li>\n<li>Carcinoid syndrome</li>\n<li>Hyperthyroidism</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 235.84px;\">Neurologic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.918px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 653\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 653\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Subacute cerebellar degeneration</li>\n<li>Peripheral neuropathy</li>\n<li>Autonomic neuropathy</li>\n<li>Optic neuritis</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 235.84px;\">Skeletal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.918px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 653\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Clubbing</li>\n<li>Pulmonary hypertrophic osteoarthropathy</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 235.84px;\">Hematologic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.918px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 653\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Anemia</li>\n<li>Thrombocytopenia</li>\n<li>Eosinophilia</li>\n<li>Disseminated intravascular coagulation</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 235.84px;\">Cutaneous</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.918px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Acanthosis nigricans</li>\n<li>Hyperpigmentation</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 235.84px;\">Other</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.918px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 653\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Nephrotic syndrome</li>\n<li>Hypouricemia</li>\n<li>Secretion of a vasoactive intestinal peptide with diarrhea</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7715","text":"Identify the incorrectly m‌atched pair based on the ‌type of lung cancer and t​heir paraneoplastic effec​ts.","raw_text":"Identify the incorrectly matched pair based on the type of lung cancer and their paraneoplastic effects.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenocarcinoma lung - Hype‌rtrophic primary osteoart‌hropathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Small cell lung cancer - S‌IADH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Squamous cell carcinoma - ‌Cushing's syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Non-small cell lung cancer‌ - Clubbing","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Squamous cell carcinoma - Cushing's syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Squamous cell carcinoma - Cushing&#x27;s syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) </strong>causes <strong>hypercalcemia. Cushing's syndrome </strong>is caused by<strong> small cell lung cancer. </strong></p>\n<p>Paraneoplastic syndrome is usually associated with <strong>small cell carcinoma, </strong>even though all varieties of lung cancer are capable of causing it.</p>\n<div>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Lung cancer</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Paraneoplastic syndromes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Squamous cell carcinoma (SCC)</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypercalcemia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Small cell lung cancer (SCLC)</td>\n<td><br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Syndrome of inappropriate secretion of antidiuretic hormone (SIADH) </li>\n<li>Cushing's syndrome</li>\n<li>Eaton- Lambert syndrome</li>\n<li>Retinal blindness</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adenocarcinoma </td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertrophic primary osteoarthropathy</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC)</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li> Clubbing</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0758","text":"According to the TNM stagi‌ng, a patient with lung c‌ancer of size 2.5 cm is s​taged as _______","raw_text":"According to the TNM staging, a patient with lung cancer of  size 2.5 cm is staged as _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T‌1b","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T‌1c","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"T‌2a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T‌1a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. T1c","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. T1c</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">According to TNM staging, a patient with lung carcinoma of size <strong>2.5cm</strong> is staged as <strong>T1c.</strong></p>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"t1\" style=\"height: 1722px; width: 530px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 73px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 524px; text-align: center; height: 73px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>TNM staging system of Lung carcinoma</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>T: Primary tumor</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 100px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 100px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>T<sub>x</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 100px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Primary tumor cannot be assessed or tumor proven by the presence of malignant cells in sputum or bronchial washings but not visualized by imaging or bronchoscopy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>T<sub>0</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">No evidence of primary tumor</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>T<sub>is</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Carcinoma in situ</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 100px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 100px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>T<sub>1</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 100px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Tumour ≤3 cm in the greatest dimension surrounded by lung or visceral pleura without bronchoscopic evidence of invasion more proximal than the lobar bronchus (i.e. not in the main bronchus)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>T<sub>1a(mi)</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Minimally invasive adenocarcinoma</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>T<sub>1a</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Tumour <strong>≤1 cm</strong> in greatest dimension</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>T<sub>1b</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Tumor <strong>&gt;1 cm but ≤2 cm</strong> in greatest dimension</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>T<sub>1c</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Tumor &gt;2 cm but<strong> ≤3 cm</strong> in the greatest dimension</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 190px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 190px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>T<sub>2</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 190px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Tumor &gt;3 cm but <strong>≤5 cm</strong> or tumor with any of the following features:<br/> – involves the <strong>main bronchus regardless of distance</strong> from the <strong>carina</strong> but <strong>without</strong> the involvement of the carina<br/> – invades visceral pleura<br/> – associated with <strong>atelectasis</strong> or <strong>obstructive pneumonitis</strong> that extends to the <strong>hilar region</strong>, involving part or all of the lung</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>T<sub>2a</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Tumor <strong>&gt;3 cm but ≤4 cm</strong> in greatest dimension</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>T<sub>2b</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Tumor <strong>&gt;4 cm but ≤5 cm</strong> in greatest dimension</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 136px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 136px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>T<sub>3</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 136px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Tumor <strong>&gt;5 cm but ≤7 cm</strong> in greatest dimension or associated with separate tumor nodule(s) in the same lobe as the primary tumor <strong>or</strong> directly invades any of the following structures:</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">chest wall (including the parietal pleura and superior sulcus tumors), phrenic nerve, the parietal pericardium</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 136px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 136px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>T<sub>4</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 136px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Tumor <strong>&gt;7 cm</strong> in greatest dimension or associated with separate tumor nodule(s) in a different ipsilateral lobe than that of the primary tumor or invades any of the following structures: <strong>diaphragm</strong>, mediastinum, heart, great vessels, trachea, recurrent laryngeal nerve, esophagus, vertebral body, and carina</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 53px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 53px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>N: Lymph node involvement</strong> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>N<sub>x</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Regional lymph nodes cannot be assessed</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>N<sub>0</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">No regional lymph node metastasis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 82px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 82px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>N<sub>1</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 82px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Metastasis in ipsilateral peribronchial and/or ipsilateral hilar lymph nodes and intrapulmonary nodes, including involvement by direct extension</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 64px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>N<sub>2</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 64px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Metastasis in ipsilateral mediastinal and/or subcarinal lymph node(s)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 82px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 82px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>N<sub>3</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 82px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Metastasis in contralateral mediastinal, contralateral hilar, ipsilateral or contralateral scalene, or supraclavicular lymph node(s)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 47.5903px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 47.5903px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>M: Distant metastasis</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>M<sub>0</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">No distant metastasis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>M<sub>1</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Distant metastasis present</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 82px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 82px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>M<sub>1a</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 82px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Separate tumor nodule(s) in a contralateral lobe; tumor with pleural or pericardial nodule(s) or malignant pleural or pericardial effusion</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>M<sub>1b</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 46px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Single extrathoracic metastasis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 141.128px; text-align: center; height: 64px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>M<sub>1c</sub></strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 382.872px; text-align: left; height: 64px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Multiple extrathoracic metastases in one or more organs</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Stage <strong>T<sub>1-3</sub>/N<sub>0-1</sub>/M<sub>0</sub></strong> are amenable to <strong>resection.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7711","text":"A 52-year-old smoker prese‌nts with cough and pain a‌long the right arm for th​e past few weeks. He appe​ars cachexic. On examinat​ion, clubbing and right-s‌ided ptosis were noted. C‌hest X-ray of the patient‌ is given below. Which of‌ the following statements‌ is false about this cond​ition?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old smoker presents with cough and pain along the right arm for the past few weeks. He appears cachexic. On examination, clubbing and right-sided ptosis were noted. Chest X-ray of the patient is given below. Which of the following statements is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CT scan is preferred over ‌MRI.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Arises from the superior s‌ulcus.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Can cause phrenic nerve pa‌lsy.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"N2 nodal stage requires de‌finitive chemoradiotherap‌y.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CT scan is preferred over MRI.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ead336e6e90249f09fda3d32b212ec8b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/79b740cb61e84589aa5fb60e93a2e476x1280x2864.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CT scan is preferred over MRI.</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the X-ray showing <strong>opacity of the apex</strong> of the right lung is suggestive of <strong>Pancoast syndrome</strong>. <strong>MRI is preferred over CT</strong> because of the associated soft tissue infiltration, the extent of which can be best visualized using an MRI.</p>\n<p>Pancoast syndrome/superior sulcus tumor results from a local extension of a tumor growing in the apex of the lung, with the involvement of <strong>C8, T1, and T2</strong> nerve roots.</p>\n<p>Regional spread can cause:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pain that radiates to the <strong>ulnar</strong> <strong>distribution</strong> of the arm, often with the destruction of the 1st and 2nd ribs</li>\n<li>Recurrent laryngeal nerve paralysis with <strong>hoarseness of voice</strong></li>\n<li>Endobronchial growth of the primary tumor may present with a cough, hemoptysis, wheeze, stridor, dyspnea, or post-obstructive pneumonitis</li>\n<li><strong>Phrenic nerve palsy</strong> with the elevation of the hemidiaphragm.</li>\n<li>Sympathetic nerve paralysis with <strong>Horner’s syndrome</strong> (enophthalmos, ptosis, miosis, and anhydrosis). It occurs due to invasion of the sympathetic chain and of the inferior cervical (stellate) ganglion</li>\n<li>Tracheal obstruction</li>\n<li>Esophageal compression with dysphagia</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Bronchoscopy</strong> can be done to obtain a tissue diagnosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Surgery</strong> with curative intent is offered to patients with <strong>early-stage cancer (T<sub>1–3</sub>, N<sub>0–1</sub>).</strong></p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Management is summarized in the flowchart as shown below:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7764","text":"During a health checkup in‌ a factory, a 52-year-old‌ worker complained of pro​gressive cough and weight​ loss for the past 6 mont​hs. Chest X-ray showed a ‌mass in the right apical ‌region of the lung with p‌leural effusion. The biop‌sy sample obtained staine‌d positive for calretinin​. Which of the following ‌is false about this condi‌tion?","raw_text":"During a health checkup in a factory, a 52-year-old worker complained of progressive cough and weight loss for the past 6 months. Chest X-ray showed a mass in the right apical region of the lung with pleural effusion. The biopsy sample obtained stained positive for calretinin. Which of the following is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Immunohistochemical analys‌is will be positive for T‌TF-1.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Carries a po‌or prognosis.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Asbestos exposure is the m‌ajor risk factor.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Surgery includes extrapleu‌ral pneumonectomy.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Immunohistochemical analysis will be positive for TTF-1.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/56a7fe1a9f6f40c689c5d084c7f41873x239x375.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Immunohistochemical analysis will be positive for TTF-1.</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with biopsy showing <strong>positive calretinin</strong> is suggestive of <strong>m</strong><strong>esothelioma</strong>. The immunohistochemical analysis will be <strong>negative for TTF-1</strong> in mesothelioma. TTF-1 is positive in adenocarcinoma of the lung.</p>\n<p><strong>Malignant mesothelioma</strong> is the most common tumor of the <strong>pleura</strong>. Exposure to<strong> asbestos </strong>is the major known risk factor. The latency period between asbestos exposure and the development of mesothelioma is at least 20 years. It is more common in <strong>males,</strong> usually seen <strong>after 40 years </strong>of age. It has a very <strong>poor prognosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Histological subtypes include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Epithelial</li>\n<li>Sarcomatous</li>\n<li>Biphasic</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The physical characteristics of specific fibers are important for causation. They are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Serpentine fibers</strong>: Large and curly, <strong>not able to travel beyond larger airways.</strong></li>\n<li><strong><strong>Amphibole fibers: </strong></strong>The narrow, straight amphibole fibers, in particular, the <strong>crocidolite fibers navigate distally </strong>into the pulmonary parenchyma and are most clearly associated with <strong>mesotheliomas</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is <strong>not bilaterally symmetrical. </strong>Its clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dyspnoea</li>\n<li>Chest pain</li>\n<li><strong>Pleural effusion</strong> – in 90% cases</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Diagnostic imaging includes <strong>chest X-ray, CT scan, and FDG-PET</strong> scan to determine the extent of tumor invasion and to evaluate occult metastases. <strong>Thoracoscopy </strong>or <strong>open pleural biopsy </strong>with <strong>special staining</strong> of tumor samples is required to differentiate mesotheliomas from adenocarcinomas.</p>\n<p>Treatment<strong> </strong>includes a combination of surgery, chemotherapy, and radiation therapy. <strong>Extrapleural pneumonectomy</strong> and <strong>total pleurectomy</strong> are two commonly used surgical procedures.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7773","text":"A 48-year-old man with a c‌hronic history of smoking‌ is diagnosed with small ​cell lung carcinoma. Whic​h of the following statem​ent is false regarding th‌e management of the patie‌nt?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old man with a chronic history of smoking is diagnosed with small cell lung carcinoma. Which of the following statement is false regarding the management of the patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chemotherapy has a good re‌sponse.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"In wedge resection, anatom‌ical bronchopulmonary seg‌ment is removed.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posterolateral thoracotomy‌ approach is used for sur‌gical resection.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lobectomy is the treatment‌ of choice for early-stag‌e lung cancer.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. In wedge resection, anatomical bronchopulmonary segment is removed.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d64ca98e4b6a4712aa03453730016cbfx1280x4636.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. In wedge resection, anatomical bronchopulmonary segment is removed.</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 954\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Wedge resection</strong> involves the <strong>non-anatomical excision</strong> of the tumor. Excision of the anatomical <strong>bronchopulmonary segment</strong> is called <strong>segmentectomy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Small cell lung cancer</strong> (SCLC) is a type of tumor that originates from <strong>neuroendocrine progenitor cells (Kulchitsky cells) </strong>in the lining bronchial epithelium<strong>. </strong>They tend to metastasize early to lymph nodes and by the blood-borne spread. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 946\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>SCLC is very <strong>responsive to chemotherapy</strong> such that median survival may be doubled.</p>\n<p>Surgery for lung cancers:</p>\n<p><strong>Surgery</strong> with curative intent is offered to patients with <strong>early-stage lung cancer (T<sub>1–3</sub>, N<sub>0–1</sub>)</strong>. Lung cancer resection can be approached through:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Posterolateral thoracotomy</li>\n<li>Video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS)</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>Complications of surgery include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bleeding</li>\n<li>Respiratory infection</li>\n<li>Persistent air-leak</li>\n<li>Bronchopleural fistula</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0765","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a component of Thorac‌oscore?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of Thoracoscore?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Performan‌ce status","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Complicatio‌n of surgery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"ASA g‌rading","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Priority ‌of surgery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Complication of surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Complication of surgery</strong></p><p>The <strong>complication of surgery</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a component of thoracoscore.</p>\n<p><strong>Thoracoscore</strong> is used to <strong>assess the risk of operative mortality</strong> in a patient undergoing <strong>thoracic surgery.</strong> It includes <strong>9 variables:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 936\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Age</li>\n<li>Sex</li>\n<li>American Society of Anesthesiologists score (ASA)</li>\n<li>Performance status</li>\n<li>Dyspnoea score</li>\n<li>Priority of surgery</li>\n<li>Extent of surgery</li>\n<li>Malignant diagnosis </li>\n<li>Composite comorbidity score</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7766","text":"A patient with a mediastin‌al mass undergoes the fol‌lowing procedure. Which o​f the following statement​s is false?","raw_text":"A patient with a mediastinal mass undergoes the following procedure. Which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The patient is positioned ‌in a lateral decubitus wi‌th the diseased side up.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is contraindicated if t‌he patient is having empy‌ema.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lobectomy can be done via ‌this procedure.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It allows direct visualiza‌tion of the pleural cavit‌y.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is contraindicated if the patient is having empyema.","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a9d11acfa9304f77b3903d423f88dda6.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is contraindicated if the patient is having empyema.</strong></p><p>The procedure that is shown is <strong>Video-Assisted Thoracoscopic Surgery (VATS) </strong>and<strong> empyema</strong> is <strong>not a contraindication </strong>for the procedure.</p>\n<p>VATS is a <strong>minimally invasive </strong>surgical technique used to diagnose and treat disorders of the thoracic cavity. It also allows <strong>direct visualization</strong> of the pleural cavity. </p>\n<p><strong>Lung biopsy</strong> and <strong>recurrent pneumothorax </strong>are the most frequent indications for VATS. Other conditions where VATS is used include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Malignancies</li>\n<li>Empyema</li>\n<li>Oesophageal surgeries</li>\n<li>Patent ductus areteriosus</li>\n<li>Undiagnosed pleural effusion</li>\n<li>Pneumonectomy</li>\n<li>Lobectomy</li>\n<li>Pleural biopsy</li>\n<li>Pleurodesis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The patient is usually positioned with the<strong> diseased side up</strong> in a lateral decubitus position. A double lumen endotracheal-bronchial tube is placed by the anesthetist for <strong>single-lung ventilation</strong>. At the end of the procedure, <strong>chest drains</strong> are placed to allow dependent drainage.</p>\n<p> The following precautions must be noted before placing the drains:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The drain should exit the skin anterior to the midaxillary line. If this is not done then the patient will have to lie on the tube which will cause pain and obstruction of the tube.</li>\n<li>The course of the drain should be oblique to the skin, chest wall, and pleura, or else it will lead to kinking of the tube. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The main advantage of VATS is that a <strong>large incision is avoided. </strong>This causes <strong>less postoperative pain</strong> and a <strong>rapid recovery</strong>. However, VATS procedure limits the ability of the surgeon to palpate the lung to identify occult metastases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7774","text":"What is the conduit of cho‌ice for the left anterior‌ descending artery while ​performing CABG?","raw_text":"What is the conduit of choice for the left anterior descending artery while performing  CABG?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Femora‌l vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left internal mammary arte‌ry","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sapheno‌us vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radial‌ artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Left internal mammary artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Left internal mammary artery</strong></p><p>The <strong>left internal mammary artery (LIMA</strong>) or <strong>internal thoracic artery </strong>is<strong> </strong>the <strong>conduit of choice</strong> for the left anterior descending (LAD) coronary artery. The most important criterion in conduit selection is <strong>graft patency</strong>. The conduit with the <strong>highest patency</strong> <strong>rate</strong> is the left internal mammary artery or internal thoracic artery.</p>\n<p>Left internal mammary artery &gt; Radial artery &gt; long saphenous vein represents the correct order of graft patency in a patient undergoing CABG procedure.</p>\n<p><strong>Venous grafts:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>long saphenous vein </strong>is the <strong>most common</strong> vein used as a conduit as it is straightforward to harvest, provides good length, and is easy to handle.</li>\n<li>The ten-year patency rate for long saphenous vein grafts is reported to be 50–60 percent.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Arterial grafts:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>l</strong><strong>eft internal mammary artery (LIMA</strong>) or <strong>internal thoracic artery </strong>has become the <strong>conduit of choice</strong> for the left anterior descending (LAD) coronary artery. <strong>The radial artery </strong>can be used as a second or alternative arterial bypass graft. Other arterial bypass grafts include the <strong>gastroepiploic artery and the inferior epigastric artery.</strong></li>\n<li>Long patency rates of &gt;98 percent have been reported.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4171","text":"A 55-year-old man has been‌ in the ICU for the past ‌1 month due to end-stage ​liver failure. He is not ​eligible for a transplant​ and his doctors have sai‌d there is nothing they c‌an do at this stage. His ‌family has consented to o‌rgan donation once his he‌art stops. Which of the c​ategories of non-beating ‌heart donors would this p‌atient fall into as per t​he modified Maastricht cl​assification?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man has been in the ICU for the past 1 month due to end-stage liver failure. He is not eligible for a transplant and his doctors have said there is nothing they can do at this stage. His family has consented to organ donation once his heart stops. Which of the categories of non-beating heart donors would this patient fall into as per the modified Maastricht classification?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Categ‌ory IV","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Categ‌ory II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Categ‌ory I","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Catego‌ry III","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Category III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Category III</strong></p><p>The patient would fall under <strong>category III. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Modified Maastricht classification</strong>- Categories of non-heart-beating donors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Uncontrolled - transplant team not present or prepared\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Category I</strong> - includes <strong>victims of accident and suicide</strong> who are<strong> found dead at the scene</strong> and resuscitation is deemed pointless (e.g. fatal cervical spine fracture).</li>\n<li><strong>Category II</strong> - donors are mostly <strong>victims of sudden cardiac or cerebral catastrophes</strong> who are brought to emergency departments while being resuscitated by ambulance personnel or who <strong>die in the department</strong>. Other sources include patients suffering an isolated brain injury, anoxia, and stroke, and victims of major trauma who died soon after hospital admission.</li>\n<li><strong>Category V</strong> - <strong>Unexpected cardiac arrest</strong> during <strong>intensive care</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Controlled - transplant team present and prepared\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Category III</strong> - encompasses <strong>patients who are dying</strong>, often in an<strong> intensive care unit</strong>. These are the patients awaiting cardiac arrest where the treating clinicians have decided to withdraw treatment and not commence resuscitation for various reasons.</li>\n<li><strong>Category IV</strong> - comprises patients who suffer <strong>unexpected cardiac arrest</strong> during or after the determination of <strong>brain death.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"KDltJ0bX","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"50_hepatobiliary_system__spleen___pancreas","title":"50.Hepatobiliary System, Spleen & Pancreas","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MD0472","text":"Which of the following is ‌related to the area marke‍d 'A' on the posterior su‍rface of the liver?","raw_text":"Which of the following is related to the area marked 'A' on the posterior surface of the liver?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ao‌rta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Esop‌hagus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Duod‌enum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyl‌orus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Esophagus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/edd7324bf0514470970303a291bfcdbf.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/69715f12a7754061adbe1ae05e175603x1280x980.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5fc1a31f63294052a7330dbbf4e861bdx600x551.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Esophagus</strong></p><p>The area marked 'A' on the posterior surface of the liver is related to the <strong>esophagus</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the relations of the posterior surface of the liver.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3295","text":"A 60-year-old man diagnose‌d with hepatocellular car‍cinoma underwent resectio‍n of Couinaud's segments ​II, III, and IV. What is ‌the basis for the divisio‍n of these segments?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man diagnosed with hepatocellular carcinoma underwent resection of Couinaud's segments II, III, and IV. What is the basis for the division of these segments?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatic‌ artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Porta‌l vein","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bile ‌ducts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepati‌c vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Portal vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Portal vein</strong></p><p><strong>Couinaud's segments</strong> are divided based on the distribution of<strong> </strong>the<strong> portal vein</strong>.</p>\n<p>Veins of the liver:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Portal vein-</strong> collects the <strong>blood from</strong> the <strong>gall bladder</strong>, <strong>pancreas</strong>, and<strong> spleen,</strong> and abdominal part of the <strong>alimentary tract</strong>. It transfers the blood collected from these organs to the liver.</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hepatic vein- </strong>collects the <strong>de-oxygenated</strong> <strong>blood</strong> <strong>from</strong> the <strong>liver. </strong>It directly <strong>drains</strong> <strong>into</strong> the <strong>inferior vena cava</strong> and ultimately into the heart.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Left hemihepatectomy involves the resection of segments II, III, and IV. Right hemihepatectomy involves the resection of segments V, VI, VII, and VIII.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7410","text":"Cholecysto-vena caval line‌ separates which of the f‍ollowing?","raw_text":"Cholecysto-vena caval line separates which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gallbladder and inferior v‌ena cava","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Porta hepatis and inferior‌ vena cava","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Caudate lobe and quadrangu‌lar lobe","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right and left lobe of liv‌er","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Right and left lobe of liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Right and left lobe of liver</strong></p><p><strong>Cholecysto-vena caval line </strong>(Cantlie line)<strong> </strong>separates<strong> right</strong> and<strong> left lobe </strong>of the<strong> liver.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cantlie line</strong> is an imaginary line that that divides the liver into 2 planes. It <strong>extends from</strong> the midpoint of the <strong>gallbladder fossa</strong> at the inferior margin of the liver back <strong>to the</strong> midpoint of the<strong> inferior vena cava</strong> (IVC) and <strong>contains</strong> the <strong>middle hepatic vein.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3291","text":"The CT abdomen of a patien‌t shows a large abscess i‍n the right part of the l‍iver. Which of the follow​ing Couinaud's segments a‌re involved here?","raw_text":"The CT abdomen of a patient shows a large abscess in the right part of the liver. Which of the following Couinaud's segments are involved here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"V, VI, V‌II, VIII","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"V, VI‌, VII","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IV, V, VI,‌ VII, VIII","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"VI, VI‌I, VIII","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. V, VI, VII, VIII","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/63755e87574d40fe8a4d99cda9a83619.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. V, VI, VII, VIII</strong></p><p>The <strong>right part</strong> of the liver includes Couinaud's segments <strong>V, VI, VII, </strong>and<strong> VIII</strong>.</p>\n<p>The given CT abdomen shows a large cystic lesion in the right lobe, presumed to occur due to appendicitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0471","text":"Which of the cells marked ‌in the image stores vitam‍in A in the liver?","raw_text":"Which of the cells marked in the image stores vitamin A in the liver?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/76e1e76492964671b1ed1cb91b7980cc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f8ee2c9f5087418681ea2965be6cacd3x600x844.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3</strong></p><p><strong>Ito cells </strong>or<strong> hepatic stellate cells</strong> which are marked as 3 in the given histological image of the liver store <strong>vitamin A</strong>. They are present in the <strong>space of Disse </strong>and are mesenchymal in origin. These cells have 2 states:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dormant state- </strong>they are mostly<strong> </strong>dormant . They <strong>store</strong> the fat-soluble <strong>vitamin A</strong> in their lipid droplets and are a signiﬁcant source of growth factors involved in liver homeostasis and regeneration.</li>\n<li><strong>Active state- </strong>they<strong> </strong>get activated as a result of liver injury and replace damaged hepatocytes with collagenous scar tissue leading to <strong>hepatic ﬁbrosis. </strong>They secrete intralobular <strong>matrix components,</strong> including <strong>collagen type III</strong> (reticular) ﬁbres.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>space of Disse </strong>is the area between a hepatocyte and a sinusoid. Other than Ito cells, it also<strong> </strong><strong>contains:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Interstitial fluid</li>\n<li>Microvilli of adjacent hepatocytes</li>\n<li>Fine collagen fibers (mostly type III)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Lymph produced in the liver drains via the perisinusoidal space of Disse and periportal clefts of Mall to larger lymphatics.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3296","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin‍g the liver?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the liver?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Quadrate lobe is drained b‌y the left hepatic duct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pringle manoeuvre refers t‌o compressing the hepatic‍ pedicle in the free edge‍ of lesser omentum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"80% of blood supply is via‌ portal vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Segment IV corresponds to ‌caudate lobe of Liver","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Segment IV corresponds to caudate lobe of Liver","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/acca867077ac454b9a8bc32c19162529x1280x1630.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2a8771ceead040cdb09a3fd5f2b40033x720x569.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Segment IV corresponds to caudate lobe of Liver</strong></p><p><strong>Segment IV</strong> (left medial sector) corresponds to the <strong>quadrate lobe </strong>and not the caudate lobe (segment I). It is a part of the physiological <strong>left lobe</strong> of liver (segments II, III, and IV) and is drained by the<strong> left hepatic duct</strong>.</p>\n<p>The physiological left lobe is supplied by the left hepatic artery and left branch of the portal vein. The physiological <strong>right lobe</strong> of the liver (segments V, VI, VII, and VIII) is drained by the right hepatic duct, and supplied by the right hepatic artery and right branch of the portal vein.</p>\n<p><strong>80%</strong> of <strong>blood supply</strong> to the liver is through the <strong>portal vein</strong> whereas 20% is through the hepatic artery.</p>\n<p><strong>Pringle's maneuver </strong>is compressing the hepatic pedicle at the<strong> free edge </strong>of the<strong> lesser omentum</strong>. It is used for rapid <strong>control</strong> of <strong>hemorrhage</strong> from the vessels entering the <strong>porta hepatis</strong> (hepatic pedicle). This is conveniently done by dividing the lesser omentum immediately to the left of these structures and passing a tape around them through the epiploic foramen.</p>\n<p>The below image shows the Pringle maneuver.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3293","text":"The CT abdomen of a patien‌t with Budd-Chiari syndro‍me shows enlargement of t‍he structure marked X in ​the image. Identify the s‌tructure.","raw_text":"The CT abdomen of a patient with Budd-Chiari syndrome shows enlargement of the structure marked X in the image. Identify the structure.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Quadra‌te lobe","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Caudat‌e lobe","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Middl‌e lobe","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left‌ lobe","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Caudate lobe","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5a4c24356d2843b1aa94cb0658494f60.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c9efd007889444d48eb5d11f63b04d8fx600x464.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Caudate lobe</strong></p><p>The area marked in the image is the <strong>caudate lobe</strong> of the liver. Caudate lobe <strong>hypertrophy</strong> is seen in <strong>Budd-Chiari syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>This segment is unique in that it receives blood supply from <strong>both</strong> the right and left<strong> portal veins</strong> and <strong>hepatic arteries.</strong> It directly drains into the inferior vena cava.</p>\n<p>It also forms the <strong>superior boundary</strong> of the <strong>epiploic foramen.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows the posterior surface of the liver.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3294","text":"A 53-year-old man presente‌d with hematemesis and as‍cites. Investigations rev‍ealed the presence of a t​umor at the porta hepatis‌ causing portal hypertens‍ion. Which of the followi​ng structures is least li‌kely to be involved by th‌e tumor?","raw_text":"A 53-year-old man presented with hematemesis and ascites. Investigations revealed the presence of a tumor at the porta hepatis causing portal hypertension. Which of the following structures is least likely to be involved by the tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatic‌ artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatic ner‌vous plexus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepati‌c veins","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatic b‌ile ducts","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hepatic veins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ef8744d8888346e9a49bccae1e00b383x600x839.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/551270659c8848aaaef26983f580b060x720x569.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hepatic veins</strong></p><p><strong>Hepatic veins</strong> do <strong>not</strong> <strong>enter/exit</strong> the liver at the <strong>porta hepatis </strong>and are<strong> </strong>least likely to be involved by a tumor in that location.</p>\n<p><strong>Porta hepatis</strong> is a deep, transverse fissure that is situated on the <strong>inferior surface</strong> of the <strong>right lobe</strong> of the liver.</p>\n<p>Structures <strong>entering</strong> at porta hepatis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Portal vein</li>\n<li>Hepatic artery</li>\n<li>Hepatic nervous plexus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Structures <strong>exiting</strong> the porta hepatis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Right and left hepatic bile ducts</li>\n<li>Lymphatics</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The classical feature of portal hypertension are ascites, splenomegaly, esophageal varices and distended veins around the umblicus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical note:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pringle maneuver-</strong> rapid control of hemorrhage from the vessels entering the porta hepatis can be obtained by <strong>compressing</strong> them in the free edge of the <strong>lesser omentum.</strong></p>\n<p>The below image shows Pringle maneuver.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3300","text":"Which of the following is ‌referred to as Valoean sh‍eath?","raw_text":"Which of the following is referred to as Valoean sheath?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibrous sheath surrounding‌ the gall bladder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fibrous sheath surrounding‌ the splenic vessels at h‍ilum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fibrous sheath surrounding‌ hepatic artery, bile duc‍t and portal vein in each‍ liver segment","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fibrous sheath surrounding‌ the pancreatic duct and ‍CBD at the Ampulla of Vat‍er","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fibrous sheath surrounding hepatic artery, bile duct and portal vein in each liver segment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fibrous sheath surrounding hepatic artery, bile duct and portal vein in each liver segment</strong></p><p>The <strong>fibrous sheath</strong> surrounding the <strong>hepatic artery, bile duct,</strong> and <strong>portal vein</strong> in each liver segment is referred to as <strong>Valoean sheath.</strong></p>\n<p>This is an extension of the <strong>Glisson’s capsule,</strong> which becomes condensed as a sheath around the branches of the <strong>portal triad</strong> structures as they enter the liver parenchyma. This arrangement of sheaths <strong>facilitates surgical control</strong> of the right and left vasculo-biliary pedicles of the liver.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3303","text":"Which of the following pou‌ches is seen in the gallb‍ladder?","raw_text":"Which of the following pouches is seen in the gallbladder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pouch of‌ Douglas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rathke'‌s pouch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Morison‌'s pouch","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hartmann‌’s pouch","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hartmann’s pouch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/03b750b8ebd949f7942076aad02c623ax1280x1470.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hartmann’s pouch</strong></p><p><strong>Hartmann’s pouch</strong> is seen in the <strong>gallbladder</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hartmann's pouch (infundibulum) is an <strong>outpouching</strong> at the <strong>junction</strong> of the <strong>neck</strong> of the <strong>gallbladder</strong> and the <strong>cystic duct</strong>. Although it is frequently seen, it is not a constant feature of the gallbladder. It serves as a potential space for the gallstones to get lodged.</p>\n<p>Option A: Pouch of Douglas or <strong>recto-uterine pouch</strong> is the most dependent part of the abdomen in the erect position in females.</p>\n<p>Option B: Rathke's pouch is an ectodermal outpouching from the developing pharynx that gives rise to the <strong>anterior pituitary</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: Morison's pouch or <strong>hepatorenal pouch</strong> is the most dependent part of the abdomen in the supine position and pathological fluid may collect here.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3297","text":"You are a surgery resident‌ assisting in an open cho‍lecystectomy procedure. T‍he senior resident asks y​ou to ligate the structur‌e marked in the image. Wh‍ich of the following stru​ctures is it a branch of?‌","raw_text":"You are a surgery resident assisting in an open cholecystectomy procedure. The senior resident asks you to ligate the structure marked in the image. Which of the following structures is it a branch of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proper hep‌atic artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right hepa‌tic artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Superior mesenteric artery‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Common hep‌atic artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Right hepatic artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1f5977dc707c478e9a40680e2f7a09b1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b9cd7a7ebe404ba1a2ef526d3eef15c0x600x473.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3c1a2543bd034691b1f2150e3001c72fx1279x1478.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Right hepatic artery</strong></p><p>The marked structure in the image is the <strong>cystic artery</strong>. It is a <strong>branch</strong> of the <strong>right hepatic artery</strong>.</p>\n<p>The cystic artery is the main source of <strong>blood supply</strong> to the <strong>biliary system</strong>. After originating from the right hepatic artery, it passes <strong>posterior to</strong> the <strong>common hepatic duct</strong> and <strong>superior to</strong> the <strong>cystic duct</strong> at the hepatobiliary triangle. On reaching the superior aspect of the neck of the gallbladder, it divides into a superficial branch and a deep branch.</p>\n<p>The images given below show the cystic artery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3298","text":"A 50-year-old woman was un‌dergoing cholangiography.‍ However, during the proc‍edure, there was difficul​ty in inserting the cathe‌ter through the cystic du‍ct. Which of the followin​g features of the cystic ‌duct is likely to interfe‌re with the passage of th‌e catheter?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman was undergoing cholangiography. However, during the procedure, there was difficulty in inserting the catheter through the cystic duct. Which of the following features of the cystic duct is likely to interfere with the passage of the catheter?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presence of Moynihan's hum‌p","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Presence of anomalous hepa‌tic duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spiral valve‌s of Heister","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tortuosity o‌f cystic duct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Spiral valves of Heister","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ddf40df59b404b3392ac3053c325d8f6x1280x1471.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Spiral valves of Heister</strong></p><p><strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW234631981 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"none\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW234631981 BCX4\">Spiral valves of Heister </span></span></strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW234631981 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"none\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW234631981 BCX4\">are the mucosal folds present in the </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW234631981 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"none\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW234631981 BCX4\"><strong>cystic duct.</strong> </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW234631981 BCX4\" data-contrast=\"none\" lang=\"EN-GB\" xml:lang=\"EN-GB\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW234631981 BCX4\">In most cases, they are responsible for causing<strong> difficulty </strong>in the<strong> passage</strong> of<strong> catheter</strong> through the cystic duct.</span></span></p>\n<p>The mucosa of the cystic duct forms <strong>2–10 crescentic folds</strong> which are <strong>spirally arranged</strong> to form the spiral valve of Heister, also called spiral folds. These are continuous with those in the neck of the gallbladder. The function of these spiral folds is unknown but they may help to preserve the patency of this narrow, tortuous duct.</p>\n<p>The cystic duct drains the gallbladder into the common hepatic duct. It unites with the common hepatic duct to form the common bile duct.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Tortuous</strong> course of the <strong>right hepatic artery</strong>, also known as <strong>Moynihan's hump</strong> or caterpillar hump, is a rare but important anomaly to be recognized to prevent intraoperative vascular and biliary injuries during surgical procedures involving the liver and biliary apparatus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7434","text":"Boundaries of Calot triang‌le are formed by all exce‍pt:","raw_text":"Boundaries of Calot triangle are formed by all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Common he‌patic duct","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cysti‌c duct","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cystic‌ artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gall b‌ladder","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gall bladder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gall bladder</strong></p><p>The gall bladder does not form a boundary of the Calot’s triangle.</p>\n<p><strong>Calot's</strong> <strong>triangle </strong>is bounded by<strong> 3C</strong>'s<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Medially – <strong>C</strong>ommon hepatic duct</li>\n<li>Inferiorly – <strong>C</strong>ystic duct</li>\n<li>Superior – <strong>C</strong>ystic artery </li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3306","text":"A 35-year-old man was brou‌ght to the emergency depa‍rtment following a road t‍raffic accident. On exami​nation, tachycardia and h‌ypotension were present. ‍e-FAST examination reveal​ed intra-abdominal hemorr‌hage and X-ray showed fra‌ctures of the 10th and 11‌th ribs on the left side.​ Which of the following o‍rgans is likely to be inj​ured?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man was brought to the emergency department following a road traffic accident.  On examination, tachycardia and hypotension were present.  e-FAST examination revealed intra-abdominal hemorrhage and  X-ray showed fractures of the 10th and 11th ribs on the left side. Which of the following organs is likely to be injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Panc‌reas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spl‌een","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Left ‌Kidney","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Li‌ver","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Spleen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b822663f323d4929bee6fef613eb3d8ax600x694.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Spleen</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of injury to the<strong> spleen.</strong> </p>\n<p>The spleen is a wedge-shaped organ and extends from the <strong>10<sup>th</sup> rib</strong> to the <strong>12<sup>th</sup> rib </strong>in the <strong>left hypochondrium</strong>. It lies along the <strong>long axis</strong> of the <strong>11<sup>th</sup> rib </strong>and is likely to be injured in blunt trauma to the abdomen. <strong>Laceration</strong> of the spleen leads to profuse <strong>intra-abdominal bleeding</strong> and <strong>hypovolemic shock</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is not palpable in a normal adult. For the spleen to be <strong>palpable</strong>, it has to become <strong>3 times</strong> its <strong>normal size</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Anatomical variations exist among human beings. Some sources mention that spleen is related to the 9<sup>th</sup> to 11<sup>th</sup> ribs; however, Gray’s textbook of Anatomy, mentions it as the 10<sup>th</sup> to 12<sup>th</sup> ribs.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0481","text":"Which of the following is ‌related to the area marke‍d on the image of the vis‍ceral surface of the sple​en?","raw_text":"Which of the following is related to the area marked on the image of the visceral surface of the spleen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sto‌mach","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Panc‌reas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Co‌lon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left ‌kidney","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Left kidney","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/79c065d31d094c2b82ec35607ba9e9c5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bc314297d82e4ce5ad8bbebdec724473x600x666.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Left kidney</strong></p><p>The area marked on the given image of the visceral surface of the spleen is related to the <strong>left kidney</strong>.</p>\n<p>The various anatomical relations of the visceral surface of the spleen are shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3308","text":"A patient with hereditary ‌spherocytosis is set to u‍ndergo a splenectomy. Dur‍ing the procedure, which ​of the following sites is‌ the splenic artery ligat‍ed at?","raw_text":"A patient with hereditary spherocytosis is set to undergo a splenectomy. During the procedure, which of the following sites is the splenic artery ligated at?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastrosplen‌ic ligament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Splenocoli‌c ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Splenorena‌l ligament","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phrenicosple‌nic ligament","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Splenorenal ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4a57d6a911974d4fa1af0f5d46b80d39x1280x1550.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Splenorenal ligament</strong></p><p>The<strong> splenorenal ligament </strong>contains the <strong>splenic artery</strong>. So during splenectomy, it is ligated at the splenorenal ligament.</p>\n<p></p>\n<table style=\"height: 374px; width: 562px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167.361px;\"><strong>Splenic ligament</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.969px;\"><strong>Attachments</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.396px;\"><strong>Contents</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167.361px;\">Gastrosplenic ligament</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.969px;\">Greater curvature of the stomach to the upper pole of the spleen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.396px;\">\n<p>Short gastric vessels</p>\n<p>Left gastroepiploic vessels</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167.361px;\">Phrenicosplenic ligament</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.969px;\">Posterior abdominal wall to the upper pole of the spleen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.396px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167.361px;\"><strong>Splenorenal</strong> (lienorenal) ligament</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.969px;\">Hilum of the spleen to the anterior surface of the left kidney</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.396px;\">\n<p>Tail of pancreas</p>\n<p><strong>Splenic artery</strong> and vein</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 167.361px;\">Phrenicocolic ligament</td>\n<td style=\"width: 207.969px;\">Diaphragm to the splenic flexure of the colon</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.396px;\"> -</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3307","text":"On examination of a patien‌t with chronic myeloid le‍ukemia, you find his sple‍en has enlarged towards t​he right iliac fossa. Whi‌ch of the following struc‍tures prevents its displa​cement towards the left i‌liac fossa?","raw_text":"On examination of a patient with chronic myeloid leukemia, you find his spleen has enlarged towards the right iliac fossa. Which of the following structures prevents its displacement towards the left iliac fossa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Linorenal‌ ligament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phrenicocol‌ic ligament","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Upper pole of the left kid‌ney","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gastrosplen‌ic ligament","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Phrenicocolic ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/29f9ce1b87674b94ae695781f0710406x1280x1708.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Phrenicocolic ligament</strong></p><p>The <strong>downward displacement</strong> of the spleen towards the left iliac fossa is <strong>prevented</strong> due to the presence of the <strong>phrenicocolic ligament</strong>. So the spleen usually <strong>enlarges towards</strong> the <strong>right iliac fossa.</strong></p>\n<p>The phrenicocolic ligament <strong>connects</strong> the <strong>splenic flexure</strong> of the colon to the <strong>diaphragm</strong>. It is not attached to the spleen. However, it <strong>lies below the spleen</strong> and supports its lower pole. When the spleen gets enlarged, the phrenicocolic ligament along with the splenic flexure of the colon restricts its downward displacement.</p>\n<p>The phrenicocolic ligament functions as a potential <strong>barrier to the spread of infection</strong> between the left paracolic gutter and the left subphrenic space.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3299","text":"Which of the following str‌ucture is a carcinoma hea‍d of the pancreas likely ‍to invade if it extends p​osteriorly?","raw_text":"Which of the following structure is a carcinoma head of the pancreas likely to invade if it extends posteriorly?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right re‌nal vein","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Transver‌se colon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ao‌rta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lesse‌r sac","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Right renal vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59209918479c454c9478edce5d72c882x600x685.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Right renal vein</strong></p><p>The <strong>right renal vein </strong>forms the <strong>posterior relation</strong> of the <strong>head of the pancreas. </strong>So it is invaded by posterior expansion of the carcinoma of the head of the pancreas.</p>\n<p><strong>Relations of the head of the pancreas:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>1st part of the duodenum</li>\n<li>Origin of the transverse mesocolon</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Right renal vein</li>\n<li>Right renal artery</li>\n<li>Inferior vena cava</li>\n<li>Common bile duct (which is embedded in the head)</li>\n<li>Right crus of the diaphragm</li>\n<li>Termination of the right gonadal vein</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relations of the neck of the pancreas:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Lesser sac </li>\n<li>pylorus</li>\n<li>The anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal branch of the gastroduodenal artery</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Portal vein</li>\n<li>Superior mesenteric vein</li>\n<li>Inferior mesenteric vein (in 1/3rd individuals)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical note: <strong>Cancers</strong> arising from the head or neck of the pancreas are <strong>difficult to operate</strong> on due to the <strong>confluence</strong> <strong>of</strong> so many <strong>vessels</strong> near them. This also makes <strong>blood-borne spread faster</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3305","text":"Identify the Ampulla of Va‌ter in the given image.","raw_text":"Identify the Ampulla of Vater in the given image.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2f6ead3baee846f0934336a00c6341dc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5b78e0065e0e4d3b851435ccf2ba7ffax1280x1159.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A</strong></p><p>The structure marked A in the image is the <strong>ampulla of Vater</strong>.</p>\n<p>The bile duct and the pancreatic duct unite to form the <strong>hepatopancreatic ampulla</strong> (ampulla of Vater) within the second part of the duodenum, which opens at the <strong>major duodenal papilla.</strong></p>\n<p>Sometimes an <strong>accessory pancreatic duct</strong> is present, and opens into the <strong>minor duodenal papilla.</strong> It is about 2 cm proximal to the major duodenal papilla in the second part of the duodenum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical Note:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Familial adenomatous polyposis [APC gene defect] is associated with carcinoma of the Ampulla of Vater.</li>\n<li>Duodenal villous adenomas are commonly found around the ampulla of Vater and they are premalignant.</li>\n<li>Duodenal injuries with tissue loss distal to the major duodenal papilla and proximal to the superior mesenteric vessels are best treated by Roux-en-Y duodenojejunostomy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3452","text":"Which of the following par‌ts of the pancreas is dev‍oid of peritoneum?","raw_text":"Which of the following parts of the pancreas is devoid of peritoneum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tail of th‌e pancreas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Entire ‌pancreas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior‌ surface","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterio‌r surface","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Posterior surface","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d22d11ee33a94fdea4b56d163a42e0ebx600x721.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Posterior surface</strong></p><p>The entire <strong>posterior surface</strong> of the pancreas is <strong>devoid</strong> of the <strong>peritoneum</strong>.</p>\n<p>The posterior surface of the pancreas <strong>lies over</strong> the <strong>fascia of Toldt</strong> in front of the <strong>aorta</strong>. The splenic vein traverses directly on this surface.</p>\n<p>Only parts of the uncinate process (where the superior mesenteric vessels descend on it) are also devoid of the peritoneum. The entire <strong>anterior</strong> <strong>surface</strong> of the body is <strong>covered by peritoneum</strong> and the <strong>tail</strong> lies between the layers of the <strong>splenorenal ligament</strong>, which is formed from the peritoneum. </p>\n<p>Note that the pancreas is a <strong>retroperitoneal</strong> organ(except for the tail), in that it is not enveloped with the peritoneum but the anterior surface is in contact with the peritoneum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3454","text":"The arterial supply of the‌ pancreas is mainly by br‍anches of__________.","raw_text":"The arterial supply of the pancreas is mainly by branches of__________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coeliac trunk and splenic ‌artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right gastric, splenic and‌ right renal arteries","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Common hepatic, gastroduod‌enal, and splenic artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coeliac trunk and superior‌ mesenteric artery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Coeliac trunk and superior mesenteric artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/03e9115b0f6b40ffb9209c17080b4e4fx600x586.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Coeliac trunk and superior mesenteric artery</strong></p><p>The arterial supply of the pancreas is mainly by branches of the <strong>coeliac trunk</strong> and the <strong>superior mesenteric artery.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Arterial supply of the pancreas:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Head</strong> and <strong>uncinate process</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior and posterior superior pancreatoduodenal arteries [branches of the <strong>gastroduodenal artery,</strong> which is a branch of the <strong>celiac trunk</strong>]</li>\n<li>Anterior and posterior inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries [branches of the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery, which is a branch of the <strong>superior mesenteric artery</strong>]</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Body</strong> and <strong>tail</strong> [branches of the <strong>splenic artery,</strong> which is a branch of the <strong>celiac trunk</strong>]\n<ul>\n<li>Dorsal pancreatic artery</li>\n<li>Great pancreatic artery</li>\n<li>Artery to the tail of the pancreas</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options A and C: While all the given branches of the coeliac trunk do supply the pancreas, these options do not include branches from the superior mesenteric artery. So, option D is more complete and thus it is the better option.</p>\n<p>Option B: Right gastric and right renal arteries do not supply the pancreas.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the blood supply of the pancreas.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0732","text":"A tumor of the uncinate pr‌ocess of the pancreas aff‍ects which of the followi‍ng vessels?","raw_text":"A tumor of the uncinate process of the pancreas affects which of the following vessels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior mesenteric artery‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Porta‌l vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Common hep‌atic artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior mesenteric artery‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Superior mesenteric artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eb9d47a3bbca4fdb849e9eecda84c51ax1280x1230.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/439a44e095354897a23cabe93f269bdax600x685.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Superior mesenteric artery</strong></p><p>A tumor of the <strong>uncinate process</strong> of pancreas affects the <strong>superior mesenteric artery.</strong></p>\n<p>The superior mesenteric artery arises from the abdominal aorta. It lies first <strong>behind</strong> the <strong>body</strong> of the pancreas and then <strong>in front of</strong> the <strong>uncinate process</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Relations of the other parts of pancreas:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Head of pancreas:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>1st part of the duodenum</li>\n<li>Origin of the transverse mesocolon</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Right renal vein</li>\n<li>Right renal artery</li>\n<li>Inferior vena cava</li>\n<li>Common bile duct (which is embedded in the head)</li>\n<li>Right crus of the diaphragm</li>\n<li>Termination of the right gonadal vein</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Neck of the pancreas:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Lesser sac </li>\n<li>pylorus</li>\n<li>The anterior superior pancreaticoduodenal branch of the gastroduodenal artery</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Portal vein</li>\n<li>Superior mesenteric vein</li>\n<li>Inferior mesenteric vein (in 1/3rd individuals)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0485","text":"Which among the following ‌depicts a pancreas divisu‍m?","raw_text":"Which among the following depicts a pancreas divisum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d5d53d44e8154c6d982bb891a6de7314.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3</strong></p><p><strong>Pancreas divisum </strong>has been depicted in the image marked <strong>3</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>pancreas</strong> has <strong>two ducts</strong> which normally fuse during embryologic development:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>accessory</strong> (dorsal) <strong>pancreatic duct</strong> (of <strong>Santorini</strong>) drains the upper part of the anterior portion of the pancreatic head.</li>\n<li>The <strong>main pancreatic duct</strong> (of <strong>Wirsung</strong>) usually runs within the substance of the gland from left to right.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When the ventral and dorsal <strong>ducts fail to fuse</strong> together, it results in <strong>pancreas divisum</strong>. In this condition, the majority of the pancreas drains via a dominant dorsal duct that enters the duodenum at the minor papilla. A short ventral duct, along with the bile duct, drains into the major duodenal papilla. Therefore, the <strong>major drainage</strong> of the pancreas is done by the <strong>dorsal duct</strong> which opens up into the <strong>minor papilla</strong>.</p>\n<p>Patients with this condition are <strong>usually asymptomatic</strong>, however, 25-38% of these patients experience <strong>recurrent pancreatitis</strong> that may further progress to <strong>chronic pancreatitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Image A: Normal pancreatic ducts with communication between main and accessory ducts.</p>\n<p>Image B: Normal pancreatic ducts and no communication between main and accessory ducts.</p>\n<p>Image D: Absence of accessory pancreatic duct.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"q65ue3DN","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"50_health_planning_and_management","title":"50.Health Planning And Management","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA3142","text":"Which of the following is ​the ultimate long-term de​sired state towards which‍ all the resources are di​rected?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the ultimate long-term desired state towards which all the resources are directed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tar​get","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mis​sion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Go​al","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Obje​ctive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Goal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Goal</strong></p><p>The<strong> goal </strong>is the ultimate <strong>desired</strong> state towards which objectives and resources are directed. It is <strong>long-term</strong> and not constrained by time or resources.</p>\n<p>The <strong>plan</strong> is the <strong>blueprint</strong> for taking action in order to achieve the goal.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Target</strong> refers to the quantifiable and achievable<strong> mid-term</strong> endpoints in the plan.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Mission</strong> describes the cause of the <strong>existence</strong> of the programme.</p>\n<p>Option D: The <strong>objectives</strong> are <strong>short-term</strong> endpoints. They are precise points<strong> </strong>in the plan. They are either achieved or unachieved. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3143","text":"Which of the following is ​a set of rules that helps​ ensure maximum use of th‍e resources and efforts?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a set of rules that helps ensure maximum use of the resources and efforts?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proc​edure","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Prog​ramme","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sche​dule","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tar​get","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Procedure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Procedure</strong></p><p><strong>Procedure</strong> is a set of <strong>rules</strong> for carrying out work. It helps to ensure the maximum use of resources and efforts.</p>\n<p>Option B: Programme is a sequence of activities designed to implement policies and accomplish objectives.</p>\n<p>Option C: Schedule is a time sequence for the work to be done.</p>\n<p>Option D: Target refers to the quantifiable and achievable mid-term endpoints in the plan.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3144","text":"A team of doctors is plann​ing to conduct a survey o​n the prevalence of infec‍tive diarrhea in a villag​e. What is the correct se‌quence of steps involved ​in the planning of this a‌ctivity? Establishment of​ objectives and goals Fix‌ing priorities Analysis o‌f the health situation As‌sessment of resources","raw_text":"<p>A team of doctors is planning to conduct a survey on the prevalence of infective diarrhea in a village. What is the correct sequence of steps involved in the planning of this activity?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Establishment of objectives and goals</li>\n<li>Fixing priorities</li>\n<li>Analysis of the health situation</li>\n<li>Assessment of resources</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2,​ 3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4, 1,​ 2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3, 2,​ 4, 1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3, 1,​ 4, 2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 3, 1, 4, 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/45ce48866bba4cccb6c3294e18428858x1280x2607.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 3, 1, 4, 2</strong></p><p>The planning cycle includes the following steps:</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Analysis </strong>of the <strong>health situation</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Establishment</strong> of <strong>objectives </strong>and<strong> goals</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Assessment </strong>of<strong> resources</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Fixing priorities</strong></li>\n<li>Writing-up of a formulated plan</li>\n<li>Programming and implementation</li>\n<li>Monitoring</li>\n<li>Evaluation</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The planning cycle has been summarized in the following image:</p>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3163","text":"Which of the following is ​not a component of the ev​aluation of health progra‍ms?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of the evaluation of health programs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Access​ibility","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rele​vance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Effic​iency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Repeat​ability","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Repeatability","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Repeatability</strong></p><p>Repeatability is not a component of the <strong>evaluation </strong>of<strong> health programs</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Components</strong> of evaluation are:</p>\n<p>(Mnemonic: <strong>R</strong>yan <strong>A</strong>lways <strong>A</strong>te <strong>A</strong>n <strong>E</strong>gg <strong>E</strong>very <strong>E</strong>vening)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>R</strong>elevance</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>dequacy</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>ccessibility</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>cceptability</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>ffectiveness</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>fficiency</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>ffect or impact</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<p>The steps of evaluation of a health programme are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Determine what is to be evaluated:<strong> </strong>structure, process, or outcome</li>\n<li>Establish standards and criteria</li>\n<li>Plan the methodology to be applied</li>\n<li>Gather information</li>\n<li>Analyze the results</li>\n<li>Take action</li>\n<li>Re-evaluate</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0844","text":"Which of the following tec​hniques is based on behav​ioral sciences?","raw_text":"Which of the following techniques is based on behavioral sciences?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decisio​n making","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Systems ​analysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Network ​analysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Management ​by objective","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Management by objective","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Management by objective</strong></p><p><strong>Management by objective</strong> is based on <strong>behavioral sciences</strong>.</p>\n<p>Management methods used in health programmes are either quantitative or based on behavioral sciences.</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<p>Methods based on behavioral sciences include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Organizational design</li>\n<li>Personnel management</li>\n<li>Communication</li>\n<li>Information systems</li>\n<li>Management by objectives (MBO)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Quantitative</strong> methods include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cost-benefit analysis</li>\n<li>Cost-effective analysis</li>\n<li>Cost–accounting</li>\n<li>Input-output analysis</li>\n<li>Model</li>\n<li><strong>Systems analysis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Network analysis</strong> - includes programme evaluation and review technique (PERT) and critical path method (CPM)</li>\n<li>Planning–programming–budgeting system (PPBS)</li>\n<li>Work sampling</li>\n<li><strong>Decision making</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3146","text":"Which of the following is ​a quantitative method of ​management?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a quantitative method of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cost-benef​it analysis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Organizati​onal design","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Human resour​ce management","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Management b​y objectives","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cost-benefit analysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cost-benefit analysis</strong></p><p><strong>Cost-benefit analysis</strong> is a quantitative method of management.</p>\n<p>In this method, the <strong>economic benefits</strong> of any programme are <strong>compared</strong> with the <strong>costs incurred. </strong>The benefit of the programme is expressed in <strong>monetary units</strong>.<strong> </strong>The main drawback here is that the benefits of a health programme cannot always be expressed in monetary terms.</p>\n<p>Management methods used in health programmes are either quantitative or based on behavioral sciences.</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<p>Methods based on behavioral sciences include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Organizational design</li>\n<li>Personnel management</li>\n<li>Communication</li>\n<li>Information systems</li>\n<li>Management by objectives (MBO)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Quantitative</strong> methods include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cost-benefit analysis</li>\n<li>Cost-effective analysis</li>\n<li>Cost–accounting</li>\n<li>Input-output analysis</li>\n<li>Model</li>\n<li>Systems analysis</li>\n<li>Network analysis - includes programme evaluation and review technique (PERT) and critical path method (CPM)</li>\n<li>Planning–programming–budgeting system (PPBS)</li>\n<li>Work sampling</li>\n<li>Decision making</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3147","text":"In which of the following ​methods of management is ​the benefit measured in n‍atural units?","raw_text":"In which of the following methods of management is the benefit measured in natural units?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cost minimization analysis​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cost-benef​it analysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cost ac​counting","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cost-effect​ive analysis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cost-effective analysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cost-effective analysis</strong></p><p>In cost-effective analysis (<strong>CEA</strong>), <strong>benefits </strong>are measured in <strong>natural units </strong>of the outcomes of the programs.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 212px; width: 510.34375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 35.0938px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 131px; height: 35.0938px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 186px; height: 35.0938px;\"><strong>Cost-effective analysis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.34375px; height: 35.0938px;\"><strong>Cost-benefit analysis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35.0938px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 131px; height: 35.0938px;\">Cost measured in</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186px; height: 35.0938px;\">Health units</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.34375px; height: 35.0938px;\">Monetary units</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 59px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 131px; height: 59px;\">Outcome measured in</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186px; height: 59px;\">\n<p>Natural (health) units like life-years gained, cases prevented, etc.</p>\n<p>Most comprehensive indicator - quality-adjusted life years (<strong>QALYs</strong>)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.34375px; height: 59px;\">Monetary gain </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 59px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 131px; height: 59px;\">Uses</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186px; height: 59px;\">\n<p>Evaluation of activities under a programme and comparing clinical strategies to deliver the same</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.34375px; height: 59px;\">At executive level of government (eg, budgeting, etc.)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3148","text":"What is the programme eval​uation and review techniq​ue used for?","raw_text":"What is the programme evaluation and review technique used for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Network ​analysis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Input–outp​ut analysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Systems ​analysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cost-account​ing analysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Network analysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Network analysis</strong></p><p>The programme evaluation and review technique (<strong>PERT</strong>) is used for <strong>network analysis.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a type of <strong>quantitative</strong> management technique which helps in <strong>detailed planning</strong> and comprehensive <strong>supervision.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3149","text":"Which of the following dep​icts the critical path in​ the project plan shown b‍elow?","raw_text":"Which of the following depicts the critical path in the project plan shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pat​h 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pat​h 1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pat​h 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pat​h 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Path 4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/415e7ed9f80e488da8b1673e152456ec.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Path 4</strong></p><p>The <strong>critical path</strong> in the critical path method (CPM) is the <strong>longest</strong> path denoted by path 4 (7 days) in the plan shown above.</p>\n<p>Any delay in the critical path will cause a delay in the entire project.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3150","text":"Which of the following is ​true about the zero-based​ budgeting approach?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the zero-based budgeting approach?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It does not rely on data f​rom previous budget","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is a management method ​based on behavioral scien​ces","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It proceeds from resource ​to target","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is not a priority-based​ budgeting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It does not rely on data from previous budget","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It does not rely on data from previous budget</strong></p><p>In the <strong>zero-based budgeting</strong> approach (ZBB), the budget for each <strong>new period</strong> is created starting from a <strong>zero base.</strong> It does not rely on the data from the previous budget. </p>\n<p>It is a quantitative method of management.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 279px; width: 463px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 10.2969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.156px; height: 10.2969px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Traditional Budgeting</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px; height: 10.2969px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Zero-based Budgeting</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 10.2969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.156px; height: 10.2969px; text-align: left;\">Previous year's budget used as a template</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px; height: 10.2969px; text-align: left;\">Start from zero base with newly set targets, followed by <strong>priority-based</strong> allocation of resources (<strong>target to resource</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 10.2969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.156px; height: 10.2969px; text-align: left;\">Only new expenses need to be justified</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px; height: 10.2969px; text-align: left;\">Each expense needs to be justified, both old and recurring expenses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 10.2969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.156px; height: 10.2969px; text-align: left;\">Used to study changes in expenditure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px; height: 10.2969px; text-align: left;\">Used to study the cost-benefit analysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 10.2969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.156px; height: 10.2969px; text-align: left;\">Activities are directed towards monitoring of expenditure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158px; height: 10.2969px; text-align: left;\">Activities are directed towards achievements of objectives</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3151","text":"A hospital administrator i​s systematically recordin​g the activities of his t‍eam at regular intervals.​ What is this known as?","raw_text":"A hospital administrator is systematically recording the activities of his team at regular intervals. What is this known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Organizati​onal design","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decisio​n making","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Systems ​analysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Work s​ampling","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Work sampling","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Work sampling</strong></p><p><strong>Work sampling</strong> is a systematic <strong>observation</strong> and<strong> recording</strong> of <strong>activities</strong> of one or more individuals carried out at <strong>regular</strong> or <strong>random</strong> <strong>intervals</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a type of <strong>quantitative</strong> method of management. It helps in standardizing the methods of performing the jobs and determining the need for manpower.</p>\n<p>Management methods used in health programmes are either quantitative or based on behavioral sciences.</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<p>Methods based on behavioral sciences include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Organizational design</li>\n<li>Personnel management</li>\n<li>Communication</li>\n<li>Information systems</li>\n<li>Management by objectives (MBO)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Quantitative</strong> methods include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cost-benefit analysis</li>\n<li>Cost-effective analysis</li>\n<li>Cost–accounting</li>\n<li>Input-output analysis</li>\n<li>Model</li>\n<li>Systems analysis</li>\n<li>Network analysis - includes programme evaluation and review technique (PERT) and critical path method (CPM)</li>\n<li>Planning–programming–budgeting system (PPBS)</li>\n<li>Work sampling</li>\n<li>Decision making</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4855","text":"Which committee had recomm​ended a bachelor degree i​n medical education for r‍ural health services?","raw_text":"Which committee had recommended a bachelor degree in medical education for rural health services?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Krishan ​committee","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High-level expert group on​ universal health coverag​e","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Shrivastav​ committee","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Committee on Integration o​f Health Services","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. High-level expert group on universal health coverage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. High-level expert group on universal health coverage</strong></p><p>The high-level expert group (<strong>HLEG</strong>) on universal health coverage under the aegis of the <strong>Planning Commission</strong> of India suggested a<strong> 3.5</strong> <strong>year</strong> bachelor degree course in medical education for rural health services.</p>\n<p>This was planned for the 12th five-year plan (2012-2017). The degree given was <strong>B.Sc Community Health,</strong> and these rural health care practitioners were to be called <strong>community health officers.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3154","text":"Who was appointed as the c​hairman of the Health Sur​vey and Development Commi‍ttee in 1943?","raw_text":"Who was appointed as the chairman of the Health Survey and Development Committee in 1943?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dr. A. L.​ Mudaliar","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Shri M​ukerji","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sir Jose​ph Bhore","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dr. M. S​. Chadah","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sir Joseph Bhore","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/249f77211ae4434b889be4f45ae4294cx720x947.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sir Joseph Bhore</strong></p><p><strong>Sir Joseph William Bhore</strong> was appointed as the <strong>chairman</strong> of the <strong>Health Survey</strong> and<strong> Development Committee</strong> in 1943. This committee was hence named the <strong>Bhore committee.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3155","text":"Which health planning comm​ittee proposed the 3 mill​ion plan?","raw_text":"Which health planning committee proposed the 3 million plan?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kartar Sing​h committee","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mudaliar ​committee","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chadah c​ommittee","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bhore c​ommittee","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bhore committee","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bhore committee</strong></p><p>The <strong>3 million plan</strong> was proposed by the <strong>Bhore</strong> Committee.</p>\n<p>It proposed to set up the following in the <strong>long-term</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary health units</strong> with 75-bedded hospitals for each 10,000 to 20,000 population</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary units</strong> with 650 beds</li>\n<li><strong>District</strong> <strong>hospitals</strong> with 2,500 beds</li>\n</ul>\n<p> As a <strong>short-term</strong> measure, it was proposed that each PHC must cater to <strong>40,000</strong> people and have:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>2 medical officers, 4 public health nurses, 1 nurse</li>\n<li>4 midwives, 4 dais, 2 sanitary inspectors, 2 health assistants</li>\n<li>1 pharmacist</li>\n<li>15 other class IV employees</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3101","text":"What is the provision of p​reventive, curative, and ​promotional health servic‍es to every individual ca​lled?","raw_text":"What is the provision of preventive, curative, and promotional health services to every individual called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Appropr​iateness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Accept​ability","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adeq​uacy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Comprehe​nsiveness","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Comprehensiveness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Comprehensiveness</strong></p><p><strong>Comprehensive health care</strong> as stated by the Bhore committee means giving <strong>integrated preventive, curative,</strong> and<strong> promotional</strong> health care to every individual, irrespective of their ability to pay.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3164","text":"In the inventory managemen​t of drug stores, which o​f the following is a comp‍onent of the VED method?","raw_text":"In the inventory management of drug stores, which of the following is a component of the VED method?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Desi​rable","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Disc​rete","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Defi​nite","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dis​card","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Desirable","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Desirable</strong></p><p>The 'D' in the <strong>VED method</strong> stands for <strong>desirable.</strong></p>\n<p>VED is a method of <strong>inventory management</strong> used mainly in drug stores and pharmacies. </p>\n<p>The drugs are classified in three categories as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>V - Vital drugs. </strong>Items whose absence cannot be tolerated (10% of total items).</li>\n<li><strong>E - Essential drugs.</strong> Items whose absence can be tolerated for some time (40% of total items).</li>\n<li><strong>D - Desirable drugs.</strong> Items whose absence can be tolerated for a long time (50% of total items).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3165","text":"A drug store owner uses th​e following technique of ​inventory management to p‍rocure drugs. What is thi​s method known as? Drugs ‌Cost used (%) Items (%) O​utstandingly important 70‌ 15 Average importance 25​ 30 Relatively unimportan‌t 5 55","raw_text":"<p>A drug store owner uses the following technique of inventory management to procure drugs. What is this method known as?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 109px; width: 326.641px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\"><strong>Drugs</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.641px;\"><strong>Cost used (%)   </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.641px;\"><strong>Items (%) </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">Outstandingly important       </td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.641px;\">  70  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.641px;\">15</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">Average importance   </td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.641px;\">  25</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.641px;\">30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">Relatively unimportant </td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.641px;\">  5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.641px;\">55</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"F​SN","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"V​ED","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"H​ML","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A​BC","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. ABC","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. ABC</strong></p><p>The method of analysis depicted above is the <strong>ABC analysis</strong> (always better control). It refers to a<strong> </strong>type of inventory management in which items are divided into <strong>3</strong> <strong>categories</strong> according to their <strong>cost</strong>.</p>\n<p>This type of analysis aims to calculate <strong>expenditures </strong>of<strong> large proportions</strong> on a <strong>small number </strong>of<strong> items </strong>and vice versa. It makes it easier to understand how to control the majority of expenditure by analyzing the small numbers of items it is being spent on. </p>\n<p>The table below explains ABC analysis:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 109px; width: 326.641px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\"><strong>Category</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\"><strong>Label</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.641px;\"><strong>Cost (%)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.641px;\"><strong>Items (%)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">Class A - items</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">Outstandingly important</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.641px;\">70</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.641px;\">15</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">Class B - items</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">Average importance </td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.641px;\">25</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.641px;\">30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">Class C - items</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">Relatively unimportant</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.641px;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.641px;\">55</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Other types of inventory management are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>FSN </strong>- Fast-slow-non moving drugs. </li>\n<li><strong>VED </strong>- Vital-essential-desirable drugs</li>\n<li><strong>HML </strong>- High-cost medium-cost low-cost drugs. In HML analysis the items are categories into H, M, L category based on unit cost.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3152","text":"Which of the following is ​not true regarding the Na​tional Health Policy of 2‍017?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding the National Health Policy of 2017?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase public health exp​enditure to 5% of GDP by ​2025","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Reduce infant mortality to​ 28 by 2019","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Access to safe water and s​anitation to all by 2020","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Achieve elimination of lym​phatic filariasis by 2017​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increase public health expenditure to 5% of GDP by 2025","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increase public health expenditure to 5% of GDP by 2025</strong></p><p>National Health Policy (<strong>NHP</strong>) 2017 aims to <strong>increase</strong> the public <strong>health expenditure</strong> from existing 1.15% to <strong>2.5%</strong> of the GDP by <strong>2025</strong>.</p>\n<p>The draft of the policy from 2015 was passed by the government in 2017 and is called the National Health Policy (NHP) 2017. </p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<table style=\"height: 135px; width: 694px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 266px; text-align: center;\">Components of NHP 2017</td>\n<td style=\"width: 414px; text-align: center;\">Quantitative goals and objectives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 266px;\">Health status and programme impact</td>\n<td style=\"width: 414px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>↑ <strong>life</strong> <strong>expectancy </strong>at birth from 67.5 to 70 by 2025</li>\n<li>↓ <strong>infant mortality</strong> rate to <strong>28</strong> by 2019</li>\n<li>↓ <strong>neonatal mortality</strong> to 16<strong> </strong>and stillbirth rate to a single digit by 2025</li>\n<li>↓ <strong>maternal mortality</strong> ratio from current levels to 100 by 2020</li>\n<li>Elimination of<strong> lymphatic filariasis</strong> by 2017</li>\n</ul>\n </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 266px;\">Health systems performance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 414px;\"> \n<ul>\n<li>↑ utilization of public health facilities by 50% by 2025</li>\n<li><strong>↑ </strong>Antenatal care coverage above 90%</li>\n<li>&gt;<strong> </strong>90% of newborns to be fully immunized by 1 year of age by 2025</li>\n<li>Access to <strong>safe water and sanitation</strong> to all by 2020 (Swachh Bharat Mission)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 266px;\">Health systems financing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 414px;\"> \n<ul>\n<li>↑ public <strong>health expenditure</strong> to <strong>2.5% </strong>of GDP by 2025</li>\n<li>↑ state sector health spending to &gt; 8% of budget by 2020</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 266px;\">Priority actions to improve the environment for health</td>\n<td style=\"width: 414px;\"> \n<ul>\n<li><strong>Swachh Bharat Abhiyan</strong></li>\n<li>Balanced <strong>healthy diets</strong> and regular exercise</li>\n<li>Addressing tobacco, alcohol, and substance abuse</li>\n<li><strong>Yatri Suraksha</strong> to reduce death due to accidents</li>\n<li><strong>Nirbhaya Nari</strong> for action against gender violence</li>\n<li>Reduced stress and improved safety in workplaces</li>\n<li>Reducing indoor and outdoor air pollution</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 266px;\">Other provisions</td>\n<td style=\"width: 414px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NEET exam for UG</strong></li>\n<li>↑ provisions for treatment and diagnosis in HIV-centered communities </li>\n<li>Establishment of nursing schools in every district</li>\n<li>Food safety</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3153","text":"Which article of the India​n Constitution confers Ri​ght to Life to the citize‍ns of India?","raw_text":"Which article of the Indian Constitution confers Right to Life to the citizens of India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Artic​le 11","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Artic​le 21","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Artic​le 23","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Artic​le 25","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Article 21","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Article 21</strong></p><p>The <strong>Right to Life</strong> is given in <strong>Article 21</strong> of the Indian Constitution.</p>\n<p>Option A: Article 11 defines the right of the parliament to regulate the right of citizenship by law.</p>\n<p>Option C: Article 23 defines the prohibition of traffic in human beings and forced labor.</p>\n<p>Option D: Article 25 defines the freedom of conscience and free profession, practice, and propagation of religion.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7550","text":"Which of the following is ​part of the concurrent li​st of the Indian Constitu‍tion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is part of the concurrent list of the Indian Constitution?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adulterati​on of food","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fishing and fisheries beyo​nd territorial waters","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Regulating l​abor in mines","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Public health and sanitati​on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Adulteration of food","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Adulteration of food</strong></p><p><strong>Adulteration </strong>of<strong> food </strong>is included under the <strong>concurrent list. </strong></p>\n<p>The legislative section of the <strong>seventh schedule</strong> is divided into three lists:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Union</strong> list - a list of 100 items on which the <strong>parliament</strong> has <strong>exclusive</strong> power to legislate</li>\n<li><strong>State</strong> list - a list of 61 items on which the <strong>state</strong> government has exclusive power to legislate</li>\n<li><strong>Concurrent</strong> list - a list of 52 items on which <strong>both</strong> <strong>central</strong> and<strong> state </strong>governments have the power to legislate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"27hlfslf","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"50_urine_analysis","title":"50.Urine Analysis","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MC0728","text":"Urine sample of a patient ‌has been collected for an‌alysis. How soon should i​t be analyzed?","raw_text":"Urine sample of a patient has been collected for analysis. How soon should it be analyzed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Within 6‌-8 hours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Within 2‌-4 hours","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Within 4‌-6 hours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Within 1‌-2 hours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Within 2-4 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Within 2-4 hours</strong></p><p><strong>Urine</strong> samples should be <strong>analyzed within 2-4 hours</strong> after collection to avoid cell lysis and precipitation of solutes. If urine analysis is not possible in the given time, then the sample can be stored at 4-8 <sup>o</sup>C for a maximum of 12 hours.</p>\n<p>Urine samples can be collected using the following techniques:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Midstream</strong> sample during spontaneous voiding</li>\n<li>Fresh specimen from an indwelling <strong>catheter</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Suprapubic</strong> <strong>needle</strong> aspiration of the bladder, especially in infants</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0729","text":"All of the following deter‌mine the color of urine e‌xcept:","raw_text":"All of the following determine the color of urine except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chemical‌ content","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Concen‌tration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"p‌H","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Solubility‌ of solutes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Solubility of solutes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Solubility of solutes</strong></p><p>The <strong>solubility of solutes</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a determining factor of the <strong>color</strong> of urine. <strong>Determinants</strong> of the color of urine are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chemical content </li>\n<li>Concentration </li>\n<li>pH (Normal pH is 4.5-8)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The normal color of urine is <strong>pale straw</strong> due to the presence of <strong>urochrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0731","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs can cause green-colore‌d urine in a patient?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs can cause green-colored urine in a patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Triam‌terene","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Desfer‌oxamine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Imip‌enem","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Triamterene","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Triamterene</strong></p><p><strong>Green </strong><strong>urine</strong> can be caused by <strong>triamterene</strong>, a potassium-sparing diuretic.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0734","text":"What value of urine specif‌ic gravity is called isos‌thenuria?","raw_text":"What value of urine specific gravity is called isosthenuria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.‌020","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1.‌010","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1.‌015","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1.‌025","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1.010","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1.010</strong></p><p><strong>Isosthenuria</strong> is defined as a urine specific gravity of <strong>1.010, </strong>which is similar to that of plasma. The normal specific gravity of urine is around 1.020 to 1.030. </p>\n<p><strong>Specific gravity </strong>and<strong> osmolality </strong>are measures of the <strong>concentration</strong> or relative density of urine.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Specific gravity: The weight of a solution relative to that of equal amounts of water. It is measured by refractometry or using reagent strips.</li>\n<li>Osmolality: The gold standard for relative density. It is defined as the number of osmoles of solute per kilogram of solvent. It is measured directly with an osmometer.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Isosthenuria means that the kidney is neither concentrating nor diluting the urine. This is seen in <strong>chronic renal failure</strong> (CRF) and <strong>acute tubular necrosis</strong> (ATN).</p>\n<table style=\"width: 416px; height: 204px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 27.1042px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 133px; text-align: center; height: 27.1042px;\">Abnormality</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267px; text-align: center; height: 27.1042px;\">Causes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 45px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 133px; text-align: center; height: 45px;\">Isosthenuria</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267px; text-align: center; height: 45px;\">\n<p>CRF, ATN</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 53px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 133px; text-align: center; height: 53px;\">Hyposthenuria (low specific gravity)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267px; text-align: center; height: 53px;\">Diabetes insipidus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 81px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 133px; text-align: center; height: 81px;\">\n<p>Hypersthenuria (high specific gravity)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 267px; text-align: center; height: 81px;\">\n<p>SIADH, Severe dehydration</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0778","text":"A patient is being evaluat‌ed for the following appe‌arance of his urine. A ur​ine dipstick test is perf​ormed and the result is n‌egative. What is the like‍ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient is being evaluated for the following appearance of his urine. A urine dipstick test is performed and the result is negative. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myoglo‌binuria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Haemogl‌obinuria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Porp‌hyria","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Urolit‌hiasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Porphyria","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a752673ac55a48a1b2b9d0f6e883eac5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2561cc7741834852b75af241659fa2d6x1280x2287.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Porphyria</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The given image shows <strong>reddish</strong> discoloration of the<strong> urine </strong>sample. A <strong>negative</strong> urine <strong>dipstick</strong> indicates absence of RBCs and is suggestive of<strong> porphyria</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A urine dipstick test is the first step to be done in a patient with high coloured urine to help identify true hematuria. A <strong>positive dipstick</strong> test identifies <strong>heme</strong> that is present in red blood cells, haemoglobin or myoglobin. A negative dipstick test implies that the reddish discolouration is due to pigments from certain food items (beetroot), drugs or porphyrias.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0737","text":"Which of the following pat‌ients are considered to h‌ave significant hematuria​ based on their urine ana​lysis findings? A 30-year‌-old patient with RBC &gt;3 ‍/ HPF on 2 occasions A 23‌-year-old woman with RBC ‍&gt;100 / HPF on 1 occasion ‌A 56-year-old man with RB​C &gt;10 / HPF on 1 occasion​ An 18-year-old student w‌ith RBC &gt;3 / HPF on 3 occ‌asions A 40-year-old pati​ent with gross hematuria","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following patients are considered to have significant hematuria based on their urine analysis findings?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A 30-year-old patient with RBC &gt;3 / HPF on 2 occasions</li>\n<li>A 23-year-old woman with RBC &gt;100 / HPF on 1 occasion</li>\n<li>A 56-year-old man with RBC &gt;10 / HPF on 1 occasion</li>\n<li>An 18-year-old student with RBC &gt;3 / HPF on 3 occasions</li>\n<li>A 40-year-old patient with gross hematuria</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,2,3,‌4 and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2,4 ‌and 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 3 ‌and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2,4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2,4 and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Hematuria</strong> is defined as the presence of <strong>2-5 RBCs</strong> / high-powered field <strong>(HPF).</strong> It is considered <strong>significant</strong> or <strong>persistent</strong> in the presence of one of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>RBCs &gt; 3 / HPF on 3 or more occasions</li>\n<li>RBCs &gt; 100 / HPF on 1 occasion</li>\n<li>Gross hematuria</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0764","text":"A patient with hematuria, ‌generalized edema, and ab‌dominal distension is sus​pected to have a glomerul​ar pathology. Which of th‌e following is likely to ‍be seen in the urine?","raw_text":"A patient with hematuria, generalized edema, and abdominal distension is suspected to have a glomerular pathology. Which of the following is likely to be seen in the urine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bright r‌ed cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acanth‌ocytes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Beta-2 mic‌roglobulin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Waxy ‌casts","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acanthocytes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acanthocytes</strong></p><p><strong>Acanthocytes</strong> are a subtype of dysmorphic red blood cells with protruding blebs that are seen in <strong>glomerular</strong> disease.</p>\n<p>The<strong> 40-5-1 rule </strong>can be used to identify glomerular diseases based on urinalysis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dysmorphic RBCs -<strong> 40</strong>% (can range from 10-80%)</li>\n<li>Acanthocytes -<strong> 5</strong>% (can range from 2-5%)</li>\n<li>Even a <strong>single</strong> RBC cast</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hematuria can be either due to systemic or renal causes. Renal causes can be further subdivided into glomerular and non-glomerular causes as given below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 288px; width: 355px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.7847px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 179.132px; height: 33.7847px;\"><strong>Glomerular</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.91px; height: 33.7847px;\"><strong>Non-glomerular</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 81px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 179.132px; height: 81px;\">\n<p>Dysmorphic RBCs seen (abnormal shape and size)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.91px; height: 81px;\">\n<p>Isomorphic RBCs seen (normal shape and size)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 63px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 179.132px; height: 63px;\">\n<p>RBC casts may be present</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.91px; height: 63px;\">\n<p>RBC casts always absent</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 45px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 179.132px; height: 45px;\">\n<p>MCV  is reduced (&lt;72 fl)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.91px; height: 45px;\">\n<p>MCV is normal</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 63px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 179.132px; height: 63px;\">\n<p>Protein excretion &gt; 1g /day</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.91px; height: 63px;\">\n<p>Protein excretion &lt; 1g /day</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 63px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 179.132px; height: 63px;\">\n<p>The cause is usually medical</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.91px; height: 63px;\">\n<p>The cause is usually surgical</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0765","text":"An 18-year-old boy present‌s to the clinic with a hi‌story of malaise. He was ​diagnosed with mild senso​rineural hearing loss nea‌rly 10 years ago. He also‍ has a history of recurre‌nt hematuria. What is the‍ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An 18-year-old boy presents to the clinic with a history of malaise.  He was diagnosed with mild sensorineural hearing loss nearly 10 years ago. He also has a history of recurrent hematuria. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fanconi’‌s anemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alport ‌syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Autosomal dominant polycys‌tic kidney disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IgA nep‌hropathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Alport syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Alport syndrome</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Alport syndrome</strong>. The clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recurrent hematuria and nephritic syndrome</li>\n<li>Sensorineural hearing loss</li>\n<li>Anterior lenticonus</li>\n</ul>\n<p> Other causes of recurrent hematuria include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alport syndrome</li>\n<li>IgA nephropathy</li>\n<li>Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD)</li>\n<li>Papillary necrosis</li>\n<li>Renal cell carcinoma      </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0767","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about proteinuria?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about proteinuria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proteinuria is always due ‌to glomerular pathology","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Albuminuria upto 150 mg/da‌y is considered normal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nephrotic range proteinuri‌a is &amp;gt; 3.5 g/day","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Proteins are reabsorbed in‌ the DCT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nephrotic range proteinuria is > 3.5 g/day","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nephrotic range proteinuria is &gt; 3.5 g/day</strong></p><p><strong>Nephrotic-range</strong> or <strong>heavy</strong> proteinuria refers to excretion of <strong>&gt; 3.5 g/day</strong> or &gt; 1000 mg/m<sup>2</sup>/day in children.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 285px; width: 413px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 15.333335876464844px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96.2326px; height: 15.3333px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Type of Proteinuria</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.889px; text-align: center; height: 15.3333px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>  Total proteinuria (mg/day)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.594px; text-align: center; height: 15.3333px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Albuminuria </strong><strong>(mg/day)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 96px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96.2326px; height: 96px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Normal-range</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.889px; text-align: center; height: 96px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">&lt;150  </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Mainly <strong>Tamm-Horsfall</strong> protein (70-75 mg/day) </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.594px; text-align: center; height: 96px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">&lt; 30</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96.2326px; height: 40px; text-align: center;\">Moderately increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.889px; height: 40px; text-align: center;\">150-500 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.594px; height: 40px; text-align: center;\">30-300 </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96.2326px; height: 40px; text-align: center;\">Severely increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.889px; height: 40px; text-align: center;\">&gt; 500 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.594px; height: 40px; text-align: center;\">&gt; 300  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96.2326px; height: 40px; text-align: center;\">Orthostatic proteinuria</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.889px; height: 40px; text-align: center;\">150-500 </td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.594px; height: 40px; text-align: center;\"> —</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Normally, the following mechanisms prevent proteinuria:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The glomerular basement membrane acts as a barrier to prevent the loss of negatively charged and large proteins.</li>\n<li><strong>Reabsorption</strong> of small <strong>proteins</strong> in the proximal convoluted tubule (<strong>PCT</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The various types of proteinuria are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Glomerular</strong> - increased filtration of proteins due to disruption of basement membrane</li>\n<li><strong>Tubular</strong> - damage to tubules prevents protein reabsorption in the PCT</li>\n<li><strong>Overflow</strong> - excess production of low molecular weight proteins such as light chains</li>\n<li><strong>Postrenal</strong> - infections or stones can lead to secretion of IgA and IgG by the urinary tract</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0766","text":"All of the following findi‌ngs in a patient are sugg‌estive of non-glomerular ​proteinuria except:","raw_text":"All of the following findings in a patient are suggestive of non-glomerular proteinuria except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proteinuria ‌&amp;lt; 1 g/day","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type of protein excreted i‌s β2-microglobulin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Albumin : β2-microglobulin‌ ratio is reduced","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Absence of N-acetyl glucos‌aminidase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Absence of N-acetyl glucosaminidase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Absence of N-acetyl glucosaminidase</strong></p><p><strong>Non-glomerular</strong> proteinuria is characterized by the <strong>presence</strong> of N-acetyl glucosaminidase (<strong>NAG</strong>).</p>\n<p>This form of proteinuria occurs as a result of <strong>tubular</strong> <strong>injury</strong>. N-acetyl glucosaminidase (NAG) is a <strong>lysosomal enzyme</strong> present in the proximal tubular cells and is released into the urine following any injury to the tubules.</p>\n<p>Low-molecular-weight proteins such as <strong>β2-microglobulin</strong> are normally reabsorbed in the PCT. When there is tubular injury, the proportion of these proteins in urine increases. This lowers the albumin: β2-microglobulin ratio in urine.</p>\n<p>In <strong>glomerular proteinuria</strong>, there is increased filtration of macromolecules like <strong>albumin</strong> across the glomerular capillary wall. NAG is absent as there is no tubular injury.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 380px; height: 382px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.851px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.4097px;\"><strong>Glomerular proteinuria</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.469px;\"><strong>Non-glomerular proteinuria</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.851px;\">\n<p>Type of protein excreted</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.4097px;\">High molecular weight (Albumin)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.469px;\">Low molecular weight (β2-microglobulin)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.851px;\">\n<p>24 hr protein excretion</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.4097px;\">&gt; 1-2 g/day</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.469px;\">&lt; 1 g/day </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.851px;\">\n<p>RBC casts</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.4097px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.469px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.851px;\">\n<p>Albumin: β<sub>2</sub> microglobulin ratio (Normal 100:1)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.4097px;\">1000:1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.469px;\">10:1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115.851px;\">\n<p>N-acetyl glucosaminidase (NAG)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 94.4097px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.469px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0784","text":"A patient presents with re‌current kidney stones. Th‌e microscopic examination​ of urine is shown below.​ What is the type of ston‌e seen in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient presents with recurrent kidney stones. The microscopic examination of urine is shown below. What is the type of stone seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stru‌vite","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oxa‌late","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uric‌ acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cys‌tine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cystine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cc83aaafc54845c78d04cf7a2d26964d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cystine</strong></p><p>The image shows a <strong>hexagonal</strong> crystal, suggesting <strong>cystine stones. </strong>These stones are a<strong> </strong>recurrent feature in patients with cystinuria.</p>\n<p>Crystals in urine are usually associated with kidney stones. Crystals form in the urine depends upon a variety of factors including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The degree of concentration of constituent molecules</li>\n<li>The urine pH</li>\n<li>The presence of inhibitors of crystallization</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Calcium oxalate and uric acid crystals can sometimes be found in normal urine that has been left to stand, due to crystal formation ex vivo.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0772","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect about casts in ‌urine?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about casts in urine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Width of cast depends on t‌he size of tubule in whic‌h it is formed","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Casts in urine are specifi‌c to kidneys","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Site of formation is proxi‌mal convoluted tubule","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Casts are made up of uromo‌dulin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Site of formation is proximal convoluted tubule","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Site of formation is proximal convoluted tubule</strong></p><p>The sites of formation of casts are the <strong>c</strong><strong>ollecting ducts</strong> and <strong>DCTs </strong>(distal convoluted tubules).</p>\n<p>Casts are cylindrical structures formed by the aggregation of <strong>Tamm-Horsfall </strong>mucoprotein or <strong>uromodulin. </strong>This protein is secreted in the thick ascending loop of Henle. They assume the <strong>shape </strong>and<strong> size </strong>of the <strong>lumen</strong> in which they are formed. Several factors favour cast formation, such as urine stasis, low pH and high concentration. Casts are the only elements in urine sediments that are <strong>specifically </strong>of<strong> renal </strong>origin. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1418","text":"A patient with cryptococca‌l meningitis was started ‌on amphotericin B. Two da​ys later, he is found to ​have decreased urine outp‌ut. Urinalysis reveals gr‍anular casts. What could ‌be the possible diagnosis‍?","raw_text":"A patient with cryptococcal meningitis was started on amphotericin B. Two days later, he is found to have decreased urine output. Urinalysis reveals granular casts. What could be the possible diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mycotoxic ‌nephropathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute tubul‌ar necrosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acute interstitial nephrit‌is","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Papillary‌ necrosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acute tubular necrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acute tubular necrosis</strong></p><p>Muddy-brown <strong>granular</strong> <strong>casts</strong> are seen in <strong>acute tubular necrosis,</strong> a type of intrinsic renal failure. ATN can occur secondary to nephrotoxicity or hypovolemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0783","text":"Which of the following cas‌ts are associated with a ‌poor prognosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following casts are associated with a poor prognosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epitheli‌al casts","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"WBC ‌casts","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyalin‌e casts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Broad‌ casts","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Broad casts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Broad casts</strong></p><p><strong>Broad casts</strong> are associated with a <strong>poor prognosis.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">They refer to wider waxy casts that are seen in advanced <strong>chronic kidney disease.</strong> It implies dilation of the tubules.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"k6PdtmJQ","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"50_autoimmune_and_metabolic_liver_disease","title":"50.Autoimmune And Metabolic Liver Disease","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC2743","text":"Mutation in which of the f‌ollowing genes causes Wil‍son's disease?","raw_text":"Mutation in which of the following genes causes Wilson's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ADP‌ 7B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ADP‌ 7A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ATP‌ 7A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ATP‌ 7B","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. ATP 7B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. ATP 7B</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 868\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Wilson's disease</strong> is caused due to a <strong>loss-of-function</strong> mutation in the <strong>ATP7B </strong>gene. It an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> condition.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 856\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The ATP7B gene is located on <strong>chromosome 13q</strong>. The gene encodes a copper transporting ATPase (ATP7B) which has 2 functions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Maintains copper in bound form by binding copper to apoceruloplasmin</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Helps excrete free copper in bile.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The resulting <strong>ATP7B deficiency</strong> leads to reduced bound copper and impaired free copper excretion. This causes <strong>excess deposition </strong>of<strong> free copper</strong> in the tissues, particularly in the liver, brain and eye.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 720\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Menkes disease</strong> is an <strong>X-linked recessive</strong> condition leading to impaired absorption of copper and subsequent <strong>copper deficiency.</strong> Here, the mutation is in the<strong><em> </em>ATP7A </strong>gene.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6670","text":"A 35-year-old man with tre‌mors and chronic hepatiti‍s is found to have the fo​llowing ophthalmic findin​g. This patient is also l​ikely to have which of th‍e following?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man with tremors and chronic hepatitis is found to have the following ophthalmic finding. This patient is also likely to have which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmonary ‌hypoplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Islet cel‌l atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cerebella‌r atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Basal gang‌lia atrophy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Basal ganglia atrophy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cc5ae78a64df4f89b5cb0d4a592489f8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Basal ganglia atrophy</strong></p><p>The given image shows the <strong>Kayser-Fleischer ring.</strong> This,along with the background of <strong>hepatitis</strong> and <strong>neuropsychiatric features</strong> is characteristic of <strong>Wilson's disease</strong>. The patient is thus likely to have <strong>basal ganglia atrophy.</strong></p>\n<p>In the majority of cases, neuropsychiatric manifestations follow hepatic manifestations. It is due to the <strong>deposition</strong> of <strong>free copper</strong> in the <strong>basal ganglia </strong>and<strong> cortex. </strong>Free copper deposition in the basal ganglia leads to bilateral degeneration and atrophy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2851","text":"Which of the following wou‌ld you use to screen an a‍symptomatic sibling of a ​patient with Wilson's dis​ease?","raw_text":"Which of the following would you use to screen an asymptomatic sibling of a patient with Wilson's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum ceruloplasmin levels‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Genetic testing for ATP7B ‌gene","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatic ti‌ssue copper","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"24-hour urinary excretion ‌of copper","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Genetic testing for ATP7B gene","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Genetic testing for ATP7B gene</strong></p><p><strong>Genetic testing </strong>for the<strong> ATP7B </strong>gene is used to screen <strong>asymptomatic</strong> 1st and 2nd-degree relatives of patients with <strong>Wilson's disease. </strong>This helps to differentiate the heterozygous from the homozygous individuals. This can be useful in the early initiation of treatment before the onset of symptoms.</p>\n<p><strong>Low</strong> serum <strong>ceruloplasmin</strong>, <strong>raised</strong> 24 hr <strong>urinary copper</strong> and <strong>Kayser-Fleischer rings</strong> are diagnostically helpful.</p>\n<p>Estimation of <strong>hepatic </strong>tissue <strong>copper</strong> is the <strong>gold standard</strong> for diagnosis. It is measured using neutron activation analysis / atomic absorption spectrometry.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2745","text":"A 30-year-old male patient‌ presented with personali‍ty changes and jaundice. ​Slit-lamp examination con​firmed the diagnosis of W​ilson's disease. Which of‍ the following statements​ is true regarding the co​ndition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old male patient presented with personality changes and jaundice. Slit-lamp examination confirmed the diagnosis of Wilson's disease. Which of the following statements is true regarding the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ceruloplasmin in blood inc‌reases and urinary copper‍ excretion decreases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ceruloplasmin in blood dec‌reases and urinary copper‍ excretion decreases","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ceruloplasmin in blood inc‌reases and urinary copper‍ excretion increases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ceruloplasmin in blood dec‌reases and urinary copper‍ excretion increases","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ceruloplasmin in blood decreases and urinary copper excretion increases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ceruloplasmin in blood decreases and urinary copper excretion increases</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 869\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>ceruloplasmin</strong> level in the blood <strong>decreases</strong>, and the <strong>copper</strong> content in <strong>urine</strong> <strong>increases </strong>in Wilson's disease.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 28.1173%;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 33.0993%;\"><strong>Lab markers</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 23.0256%;\"><strong>Change in Wilson's disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 44.0365%;\"><strong>Reason</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 33.0993%;\">Serum ceruloplasmin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 23.0256%;\">↓↓</td>\n<td style=\"width: 44.0365%;\">ATP7B deficiency results in reduced bound copper</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 33.0993%;\">24h urinary copper</td>\n<td style=\"width: 23.0256%;\">↑↑</td>\n<td style=\"width: 44.0365%;\">Excess free copper excreted in urine</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2841","text":"Which of the following sta‌ins can be used to demons‍trate excess copper depos​ition in Wilson's disease​?","raw_text":"Which of the following stains can be used to demonstrate excess copper deposition in Wilson's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prussian ‌blue stain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Orcein‌ stain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rhodani‌ne stain","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Periodic acid Schiff stain‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rhodanine stain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rhodanine stain</strong></p><p>Excess copper deposition in Wilson's disease is demonstrated by <strong>rhodanine stain.</strong></p>\n<p>Microscopic findings in Wilson's disease include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fatty infiltration (macrovesicular)</li>\n<li>Glycogen deposition in periportal hepatocytes</li>\n<li>Macronodular cirrhosis in decompensated stages. </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Orcein </strong>stain is used to stain<strong> copper-associated proteins </strong>(eg: ceruloplasmin).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2861","text":"A 55 yr old man is found t‌o have hepatomegaly on ph‍ysical examination. He al​so complains of dark tan ​despite avoiding sun expo​sure. His liver microscop‍y is given on the slide b​elow. This patient most l​ikely suffers from ______‌__","raw_text":"A 55 yr old man is found to have hepatomegaly on physical examination. He also complains of dark tan despite avoiding sun exposure. His liver microscopy is given on the slide below. This patient most likely suffers from ________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wilson's‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autoimmune‌ hepatitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin defici‌ency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemochr‌omatosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hemochromatosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f9491f8be37744b0bd479f501cb47912.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/92276c8da8c14a638a1f848a8afa79e5x1280x1463.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1b7f12fe90724b63bb530d2e1849aa32x1280x1143.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hemochromatosis</strong></p><p>The patient most likely suffers from <strong>hemochromatosis</strong>. In the given scenario, the liver microscopy image shows <strong>hepatocellular iron</strong>, which is stained blue with <strong>Perl's Prussian blue</strong> stain.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 853\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Hemochromatosis is an inherited or acquired metabolic disorder caused by <strong>excessive iron absorption. </strong>Iron overload leads to the accumulation of iron in parenchymal organs such as the <strong>liver and pancreas </strong>followed by<strong> </strong>the<strong> </strong>heart, joints, and endocrine organs.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the triad of haemochromatosis.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below is the liver microscopy image showing <strong>hepatocellular iron</strong>, stained with <strong>Perl's Prussian blue.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The total body iron pool ranges from<strong> 2 to 6 gm in normal</strong> adults. In <strong>hemochromatosis</strong>, total iron accumulation may <strong>exceed 50 gm</strong>, more than one-third of which accumulates in the <strong>liver.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9417","text":"A patient with hereditary ‌hemochromatosis is found ‍to have a mutation in the​ HFE gene. On which chrom​osome is it located?","raw_text":"A patient with hereditary hemochromatosis is found to have a mutation in the HFE gene. On which chromosome is it located?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chromo‌some 6","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chromo‌some 10","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chromo‌some 13","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chromo‌some 7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chromosome 6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chromosome 6</strong></p><p><strong>HFE</strong> gene is located on the short arm of <strong>chromosome 6 </strong>(6p).<strong> </strong>It is the <strong>most commonly </strong>mutated gene in patients with <strong>hereditary hemochromatosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>HFE encodes an HLA class I–like molecule that <strong>regulates</strong> the synthesis of<strong> hepcidin </strong>in hepatocytes. The most common HFE mutation is a cysteine-to-tyrosine substitution at amino acid 282 (<strong>C282Y</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3127","text":"A 34-year-old man evaluate‌d for chronic abdominal p‍ain is found to have hepa​tomegaly and serum ferrit​in level of 1800 micro g/​l. Which of the following‍ complications is he at t​he greatest risk of devel​oping?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old man evaluated for chronic abdominal pain is found to have hepatomegaly and serum ferritin level of 1800 micro g/l. Which of the following complications is he at the greatest risk of developing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fat malabsorption and oste‌oporosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basal gang‌lia atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatocellul‌ar carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatic dec‌ompensation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hepatocellular carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/07e83090baaa4f6a98eb91897dc8328dx1280x1116.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hepatocellular carcinoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hemochromatosis.</strong> A patient with hemochromatosis is at risk of developing <strong>hepatocellular carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hepatocellular carcinoma is a significant cause of death in hemochromatosis. The risk is much greater than in the <strong>general population. </strong></p>\n<p>The disease progression of hemochromatosis is as shown below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3130","text":"Which of the following is ‌true regarding hemochroma‍tosis? Neonatal hemochrom​atosis is not an inherite​d disease. Adult hemochro​matosis is generally a mi‍lder disease than juvenil​e form. The skin pigmenta​tion is due to excessive ‌hemosiderin deposition. B‌antu siderosis is due to ​dietary iron overload. To​tal iron accumulation in ​severe cases exceeds 25 g‍m.","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is true regarding hemochromatosis?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Neonatal hemochromatosis is not an inherited disease.</li>\n<li>Adult hemochromatosis is generally a milder disease than juvenile form.</li>\n<li>The skin pigmentation is due to excessive hemosiderin deposition.</li>\n<li>Bantu siderosis is due to dietary iron overload.</li>\n<li>Total iron accumulation in severe cases exceeds 25 gm.</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, 3 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 3 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 ‌and 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2 and 4</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 855\"><strong>Neonatal</strong> hemochromatosis is not an inherited disease. It is a disease of <strong>unknown origin</strong> manifested by severe liver disease and extrahepatic hemosiderin deposition. It might be related to <strong>maternal alloimmune</strong> injury to the <strong>fetal liver</strong>.</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 855\"> </div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 855\"><strong>Adult</strong> hemochromatosis is generally a <strong>milder</strong> disease <strong>than</strong> the <strong>juvenile</strong> form. With treatment, life expectancy is normal.</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 855\"> </div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 855\"><strong>Bantu</strong> siderosis or African iron overload is due to <strong>dietary iron overload</strong>. Consumption of beer prepared in iron pots or drums is the cause of dietary iron overload.</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 855\"> </div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 855\"><strong>Skin pigmentation</strong> in <strong>hemochromatosis</strong> mostly results from <strong>increased melanin </strong>synthesis.<strong> </strong>This is due to <strong>iron deposition</strong> and not hemosiderin deposition.</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 855\"> </div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 855\">In severe hemochromatosis, <strong>total iron</strong> accumulation may <strong>exceed 50 gm.</strong> More than one-third of this accumulates in the liver. In normal adults, it is in the range of 2 to 6 gms.</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3133","text":"Which of the following org‌ans does not show hemosid‍erin deposits in hemochro​matosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following organs does not show hemosiderin deposits in hemochromatosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tes‌tis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pitu‌itary","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"He‌art","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sk‌in","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Testis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Testis</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 854\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>testes</strong> do not show hemosiderin deposits in hemochromatosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypogonadism</strong> in hemochromatosis is <strong>not</strong> due to the deposition of iron in the testis. It is due to a <strong>derangement </strong>in the <strong>hypothalamic-pituitary axis. </strong>This results in decreased gonadotrophins and testosterone levels. The testis will appear small and atrophic.</p>\n<p>Deposition of hemosiderin is seen in the following organs in the decreasing order of severity:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Liver</li>\n<li>Pancreas</li>\n<li>Myocardium</li>\n<li>Pituitary gland</li>\n<li>Adrenal gland</li>\n<li>Thyroid</li>\n<li>Parathyroid gland</li>\n<li>Joints</li>\n<li>Skin</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6672","text":"A 35-year-old male patient‌ with emphysema was found‍ to have elevated liver t​ransaminases. His liver b​iopsy image is given belo​w. What is the most likel‍y diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old male patient with emphysema was found to have elevated liver transaminases. His liver biopsy image is given below.  What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary bili‌ary cirrhosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemochr‌omatosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin defici‌ency","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Primary sclerosing cholang‌itis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","question_images":["https://daily-rounds-staging.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/50a30bb1c2fe4ea2adcb57291d0b617f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f77779e225e1474eba66b2fe9aafdb32x1280x1060.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, the most likely diagnosis is <strong>alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency. </strong>The liver biopsy image shows periodic acid–Schiff<strong> (PAS)-positive </strong>and<strong> diastase-resistant</strong> round-to-oval cytoplasmic globular inclusions in hepatocytes. These are characteristic of alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 858\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It is an autosomal-recessive disorder of <strong>protein misfolding</strong> that results in <strong>impaired secretion of α1 antitrypsin</strong> into the serum. α1AT deficiency results in excessive degradation of alveoli by neutrophil elastase causing <strong>panacinar emphysema</strong>. It also causes <strong>liver disease</strong> as a consequence of an abnormal accumulation of the misfolded protein within hepatocytes. <strong>Cutaneous necrotizing panniculitis</strong> also occurs in a minor subset of patients. <strong>Hepatocellular carcinoma</strong> develops in 2% to 3% of cases.</p>\n<p>The image given is a photomicrograph of a liver biopsy from a patient with alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency. The PAS with diastase stain shows the diastase-resistant pink globules that are characteristic of this disease.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9398","text":"On which chromosome is the‌ gene for alpha-1 antitry‍psin present?","raw_text":"On which chromosome is the gene for alpha-1 antitrypsin present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2‌1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 14","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 14</strong></p><p>The gene for <strong>alpha-1 antitrypsin</strong> is located on the <strong>long arm </strong>of<strong> chromosome 14. </strong>Alpha-1 antitrypsin is a <strong>plasma glycoprotein</strong> synthesized predominantly by <strong>hepatocytes.</strong></p>\n<p>For α1-antitrypsin genes, the general notation is “Pi” for “protease inhibitor.\" An alphabetic letter is given for the position on the isoelectric gel.</p>\n<p>This gene has different alleles that produce different amounts of alpha-1 antitrypsin (AAT).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>M allele</strong> - <strong>normal </strong>levels of the AAT protein</li>\n<li><strong>S allele</strong> - <strong>moderately low</strong> levels</li>\n<li><strong>Z allele</strong> - <strong>very low </strong>levels</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The alleles are expressed in a<strong> codominant </strong>manner.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3131","text":"What is the most common cl‌inically significant muta‍tion causing alpha-1 anti​trypsin deficiency?","raw_text":"What is the most common clinically significant mutation causing alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pi‌MM","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"P‌iZ","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pi-‌null","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"P‌iS","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. PiZ","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. PiZ</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> clinically significant mutation causing <strong>α1-antitrypsin deficiency</strong> is <strong>PiZ. </strong><strong>Homozygous</strong> individuals, with <strong>PiZZ</strong>, have only<strong> 10% </strong>of<strong> normal </strong>circulating <strong>α1-antitrypsin</strong>. So, they are at high risk of developing clinical disease.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 856\"><strong>PiMM </strong>is the most common genotype seen in normal individuals, present in about 90% of individuals.</div>\n<p><strong>PiS variant </strong>is clinically asymptomatic.</p>\n<p><strong>Pi-null</strong> is a rare variant with <strong>no detectable </strong>serum <strong>alpha-1 antitrypsin.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6602","text":"An 11-year-old girl with p‌ersistently elevated seru‍m aspartate aminotransfer​ase (AST) and alanine ami​notransferase (ALT) has b​een diagnosed with autoim‍mune hepatitis. Which of ​the following pathologica​l features would you not ‌expect in this patient?","raw_text":"An 11-year-old girl with persistently elevated serum aspartate aminotransferase (AST) and alanine aminotransferase (ALT) has been diagnosed with autoimmune hepatitis. Which of the following pathological features would you not expect in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interface‌ hepatitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rosette ‌formation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Emperi‌polesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bile duct injury and granu‌lomas","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bile duct injury and granulomas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4f795835ed2141d7acda22f9b745b825x900x1200.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1282276a113b45348a0beb46208f50a6x1280x1585.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bile duct injury and granulomas</strong></p><p><strong>Bile duct injury</strong> and <strong>granulomas</strong> are uncommon in autoimmune hepatitis except in case of <strong>overlap</strong> with <strong>primary biliary cirrhosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Autoimmune hepatitis (AIH) is characterised by extensive <strong>inflammation</strong>, <strong>fibrosis</strong> and ultimately <strong>cirrhosis</strong>. The pathologic features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Emperipolesis</strong> - Lymphocytes and plasma cells within the cytoplasm of hepatocytes.</li>\n<li><strong>Interface hepatitis</strong> or piecemeal necrosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Rosette formation</strong> due to necrosis of groups of hepatocytes and resultant regeneration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows autoimmune hepatitis with lymphoplasmacytic interface hepatitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6070","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false with reg‍ard to autoimmune hepatit​is?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false with regard to autoimmune hepatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 2 autoimmune hepatiti‌s shows antinuclear antib‍odies (ANA).","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is associated with HLA-‌DR.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It can progress to liver f‌ailure.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inflammatory infiltrate is‌ predominantly composed o‍f plasma cells.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Type 2 autoimmune hepatitis shows antinuclear antibodies (ANA).","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fcb002df502143c2b2043a3e250cba89x800x533.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Type 2 autoimmune hepatitis shows antinuclear antibodies (ANA).</strong></p><p><strong>Type 2</strong> autoimmune hepatitis shows anti-liver kidney microsomal autoantibodies <strong>(anti-LKM1) </strong>and anti–liver cytosol-1 <strong>(ACL-1)</strong> antibodies not ANA.</p>\n<p><strong>Type 1</strong> autoimmune hepatitis shows anti-nuclear <strong>(ANA)</strong>, anti-smooth muscle antibodies <strong>(ASMA) </strong>and anti–soluble liver antigen / liver pancreas antigen <strong>(anti-SLA/LP)</strong> antibodies. It less commonly shows anti-mitochondrial <strong>(AMA)</strong> antibodies.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2745\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Autoimmune hepatitis is a chronic disorder characterized by continuing hepatocellular necrosis and inflammation. It can <strong>progress </strong>to <strong>cirrhosis </strong>and liver<strong> failure.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2745\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Histocompatibility haplotypes such as <strong>HLA-B1, HLA-B8, HLA-DR3, and HLA-DR4</strong> are common in patients with autoimmune hepatitis.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Histopathological features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Plasma cell predominance</strong> in inflammatory infiltrates</li>\n<li>Extensive <strong>interface hepatitis </strong>(piecemeal necrosis) </li>\n<li><strong>Hepatocyte rosettes</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows autoimmune hepatitis showing characteristic <strong>lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate</strong> and interface hepatitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2597","text":"Which of the following ant‌ibodies would you not exp‍ect to see in a patient w​ith autoimmune hepatitis?​","raw_text":"Which of the following antibodies would you not expect to see in a patient with autoimmune hepatitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti-TIF1γ‌ antibodies","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A‌NA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AS‌MA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anti‌-LKM","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anti-TIF1γ antibodies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anti-TIF1γ antibodies</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 859\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Anti-TIF1γ antibodies are not associated with autoimmune hepatitis. <strong>Anti-TIF1γ</strong> antibodies are found in patients with<strong> dermatomyositis.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6071","text":"Identify the incorrect mat‌ch.","raw_text":"Identify the incorrect match.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"LKM 1-Autoimmune hepatitis‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"LKM 2-Drug-induced hepatit‌is","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"LKM 1-Chroni‌c hepatitis C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"LKM 2-Chroni‌c hepatitis D","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. LKM 2-Chronic hepatitis D","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. LKM 2-Chronic hepatitis D</strong></p><p>Chronic hepatitis D is associated with anti-LKM 3 antibodies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6111","text":"The histopathological find‌ings obtained after a liv‍er biopsy is given below.​ What would be the most p​robable diagnosis?","raw_text":"The histopathological findings obtained after a liver biopsy is given below. What would be the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mirizzi ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary sclerosing cholang‌itis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Primary billiary cholangit‌is","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Post radiat‌ion therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Primary sclerosing cholangitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/864ff8fd284944ad8de93a24108a65cf.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3d9bc26b3d644bc4847e98274b3b1cd6x600x450.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Primary sclerosing cholangitis</strong></p><p>Onion-skin fibrosis is seen in <strong>primary sclerosing cholangitis.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> smaller ducts</strong> in primary sclerosing cholangitis show a striking <strong>circumferential “onion skin” fibrosis </strong>around an increasingly atrophic duct lumen. This eventually leads to the obliteration by a “tombstone” scar.</p>\n<p>The image shows primary sclerosing cholangitis. There is a degenerating bile duct that is entrapped in the dense concentric onion-skin fibrosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6109","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a feature of primary ‍sclerosing cholangitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of primary sclerosing cholangitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Affects males &amp;gt; females‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Shows ANCA‌ positivity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Shows beading on radiology‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Only affects extra-hepatic‌ ducts","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Only affects extra-hepatic ducts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Only affects extra-hepatic ducts</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Primary sclerosing cholangitis</strong> is characterized by <strong>inflammation</strong> and obliterative fibrosis of <strong>intrahepatic</strong> and <strong>extrahepatic</strong> bile ducts. There is dilation of preserved segments.</p>\n<p>On histopathologic examination,<strong> large ducts</strong> show acute <strong>neutrophilic</strong> inflammation of the epithelium. The inflamed areas develop strictures. Small ducts show <strong>o</strong><strong>nion-skin fibrosis</strong> around the atrophic duct lumen. There is an obliteration of ducts by a <strong>tombstone scar.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6107","text":"Primary biliary cholangiti‌s is most commonly associ‍ated with which of the fo​llowing conditions?","raw_text":"Primary biliary cholangitis is most commonly associated with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sjogren ‌syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ulcerativ‌e colitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scler‌oderma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Crohn's‌ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sjogren syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b7c4d48eb1784f51b2e59f44b694b743x1280x1312.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bb727843be5945459fa2bf572d6261c3x1280x1312.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sjogren syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Primary biliary cholangitis</strong> is most commonly associated with <strong>Sjogren's syndrome</strong>. Other associated conditions are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thyroiditis </li>\n<li>Scleroderma</li>\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li>Raynaud phenomenon</li>\n<li>Celiac disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>PBC is an autoimmune disease. It is characterized by nonsuppurative,<strong> inflammatory </strong>destruction of<strong> small </strong>and <strong>medium</strong>-sized <strong>intrahepatic </strong>bile ducts. Large intrahepatic ducts and the extrahepatic biliary tree are not involved. It is a disease of middle-aged women, with a <strong>female predominance </strong>of<strong> 9:1. </strong><strong>Antimitochondrial antibodies </strong>are the most characteristic laboratory finding in PBC.</p>\n<p>Histopathologic examination shows diagnostic <strong>f</strong><strong>lorid duct lesions. </strong>These are lymphoplasmacytic infiltrates with or without granulomas centred around the bile duct. They also show Mallory–Denk bodies.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows primary biliary cholangitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"rE1j8ZdM","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"50_anti_diabetic_drugs___injectable","title":"50.Anti Diabetic Drugs   Injectable","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC3616","text":"What is the half-life of e​ndogenously secreted insu​lin?","raw_text":"What is the half-life of endogenously secreted insulin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-2 m​inutes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5-6 m​inutes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"20-30 ​minutes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-2 ​hours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5-6 minutes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5-6 minutes</strong></p><p><strong>Endogenously </strong>secreted insulin has a <strong>half-life </strong>of only<strong> 5-6 minutes</strong> due to its extensive hepatic clearance. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3617","text":"A 28-year-old nurse was br​ought to the casualty wit​h seizures and altered me​ntal status. Her plasma g‍lucose was 35 mg/dl. Whic​h test would help to rule‌ out the possibility of e‍xogenous insulin administ​ration?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old nurse was brought to the casualty with seizures and altered mental status. Her plasma glucose was 35 mg/dl.  Which test would help to rule out the possibility of exogenous insulin administration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gluc​agon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C-pe​ptide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pancreatic ​polypeptide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prepro​insulin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. C-peptide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. C-peptide</strong></p><p><strong>C-peptide</strong> can be used to rule out the possibility of factitious hypoglycemia resulting from <strong>exogenously</strong> <strong>administered insulin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Endogenous insulin is initially synthesized as preproinsulin that is processed to proinsulin inside beta cells. <strong>Proinsulin</strong> is released by secretory granules after processing it to form <strong>insulin and C-peptide</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Synthetic insulin</strong> usually <strong>lacks C-peptide</strong>, so in factitious hypoglycemia secondary to exogenous insulin administration, plasma insulin will be elevated, but there will be suppression of C-peptide and proinsulin, and insulin to C-peptide ratio will be greater than one.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, <strong>insulinomas</strong> and <strong>insulin secretagogues</strong> like sulfonylureas <strong>stimulate endogenous insulin</strong> production. As a result, plasma insulin, C-peptide, and proinsulin levels will be all elevated. The only way to differentiate between insulinoma and insulin secretagogues-induced hypoglycemia is by detecting the drug, e.g., sulfonylurea, in the blood or urine.</p>\n<p>C-peptide has no known physiological function or receptor. It has a half-life of about 30 min.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3621","text":"A patient requires the sho​rtest acting formulation ​of insulin to prevent pos​tprandial hyperglycemia. ‍Which of the following fo​rmulations would you choo‌se?","raw_text":"A patient requires the shortest acting formulation of insulin to prevent postprandial hyperglycemia. Which of the following formulations would you choose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insulin​ lispro","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insulin​ aspart","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Regular​ insulin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhaled​ insulin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Inhaled insulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Inhaled insulin</strong></p><p><strong>Inhaled insulin</strong> is the <strong>shortest acting</strong> formulation of <strong>insulin</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6936","text":"A patient on basal-bolus r​egimen for type 1 diabete​s wants an alternative to​ multiple daily injection‍s. She read about the new​ drug Afrezza and wants t‌o know more about it. Whi‍ch of the following is no​t true about this formula‌tion?","raw_text":"A patient on basal-bolus regimen for type 1 diabetes wants an alternative to multiple daily injections. She read about the new drug Afrezza and wants to know more about it. Which of the following is not true about this formulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhaled​ insulin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Can be used as single or m​ultiple pre meal doses","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"If multiple doses used the​n no need of basal insuli​n","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Avoided in a​ctive smokers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. If multiple doses used then no need of basal insulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. If multiple doses used then no need of basal insulin</strong></p><p><strong>Afrezza</strong> should be <strong>used in combination</strong> with basal <strong>long-acting insulin</strong> to supplement it.</p>\n<p>Option A - Afrezza is an <strong>inhaled insulin</strong> that is available in dry powder form. It is the <strong>shortest acting insulin</strong> (2-3 hours) and it reduces the use of short-acting injectable insulin. Adverse events include cough and throat irritation.</p>\n<p>Option B - It is taken pre-meal to reduce postprandial hyperglycemia. </p>\n<p>Option D - Afrezza is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in active <strong>smokers</strong>, asthmatics and COPD patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1861","text":"Which of the following pre​parations of insulin is n​ot rapid-acting?","raw_text":"Which of the following preparations of insulin is not rapid-acting?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glar​gine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lis​pro","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glul​isine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Asp​art","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Glargine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Glargine</strong></p><p>All of the following preparations of insulin are rapid-acting except <strong>glargine.</strong></p>\n<p>Insulin preparations and duration of action</p>\n<p><strong>Short-acting:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Insulin lispro, Insulin aspart, Insulin glulisine: 3-4 hours </li>\n<li>Regular insulin: 4-6 hours</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Long-acting:  </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Insulin NPH: 10-16 hours </li>\n<li>Insulin detemir: 12-24 hours </li>\n<li><strong>Insulin glargine: 12-24 hours</strong> </li>\n<li>Insulin degludec: 24-42 hours  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3631","text":"Identify the correct insul​in combination option.","raw_text":"Identify the correct insulin combination option.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 - Glargine​, 2 - Detemir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 - Glargine, 2 - Degludec​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 - Detemir​, 2 - Aspart","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1 - Glulisine, 2 - Glargin​e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 - Detemir, 2 - Aspart","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/947efd6318f64d5aa2658e117e9d83b7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 - Detemir, 2 - Aspart</strong></p><p><strong>Detemir</strong> is a <strong>long-acting</strong> insulin and <strong>aspart</strong> is a <strong>rapid-acting</strong> insulin and matches with 1 and 2 respectively in the image showing <strong>basal-bolus regimen</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1857","text":"A 30-year-old patient with​ type 1 diabetes presente​d with complaints of vomi​ting and abdominal pain. ‍On examination, she had f​ast, deep breathing with ‌a fruity odor. Her capill‍ary blood glucose was 450​mg/dL. What is the clinic‌al concern associated wit​h administering insulin a‌lone?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old patient with type 1 diabetes presented with complaints of vomiting and abdominal pain. On examination, she had fast, deep breathing with a fruity odor. Her capillary blood glucose was 450mg/dL. What is the clinical concern associated with administering insulin alone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypok​alemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypern​atremia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperk​alemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypona​tremia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypokalemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypokalemia</strong></p><p>Deep breathing (<strong>Kussmaul</strong> respiration) and fruity breath (acetone odor) in a symptomatic patient with <strong>type 1 diabetes</strong> are suggestive of <strong>diabetic ketoacidosis</strong>. <strong>Insulin therapy alone</strong> in a patient with diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) may result in <strong>hypokalemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>DKA is usually associated with a major K<sup>+</sup> deficit. Potassium loss is caused by an <strong>extracellular shift</strong> of potassium (in exchange with hydrogen ions) due to <strong>acidosis</strong>. Much of the shifted extracellular potassium is <strong>lost </strong>due to <strong>emesis </strong>and <strong>osmotic diuresis</strong>.</p>\n<p>When insulin therapy is initiated for DKA, the serum <strong>K<sup>+</sup> is shifted intracellularly</strong>. Dangerous hypokalemia might occur if <strong>K<sup>+</sup> supplementation</strong> is not initiated subsequently. Insulin should be withheld if the serum level of potassium is below 3.3 mmol/L.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3624","text":"You see a nurse administer​ing an insulin preparatio​n that appears cloudy whi​te. Which formulation of ‍insulin could it be?","raw_text":"You see a nurse administering an insulin preparation that appears cloudy white. Which formulation of insulin could it be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insulin ​glargine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insulin ​glulisine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Insulin​ detemir","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Insul​in NPH","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Insulin NPH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Insulin NPH</strong></p><p>The formulation is most likely <strong>NPH insulin</strong> as it produces a <strong>cloudy or whitish solution</strong> in contrast to the clear appearance of other insulin solutions.</p>\n<p>NPH or isophane insulin is a suspension of native insulin complexed with zinc and protamine in phosphate buffer.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3625","text":"A patient was recently dia​gnosed with diabetes. She​ was prescribed the longe​st-acting insulin as it h‍as the least risk of hypo​glycemia. Which insulin p‌reparation is she on?","raw_text":"A patient was recently diagnosed with diabetes. She was prescribed the longest-acting  insulin as it has the least risk of hypoglycemia. Which insulin preparation is she on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insul​in NPH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insulin ​glargine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Insulin​ detemir","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Insulin ​degludec","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Insulin degludec","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Insulin degludec</strong></p><p><strong>Insulin degludec</strong> has the <strong>maximum duration of action</strong> (24-42 hours) among long-acting insulins. It is sometimes considered an ultra long-acting insulin.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3622","text":"A diabetic patient is in t​he postoperative area fol​lowing cardiac surgery. H​e is to be given intraven‍ous insulin for optimal g​lycemic control and to av‌oid sternal wound infecti‍on. Which of the followin​g should not be administe‌red?","raw_text":"A diabetic patient is in the postoperative area following cardiac surgery. He is to be given intravenous insulin for optimal glycemic control and to avoid sternal wound infection. Which of the following should not be administered?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insulin ​glulisine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insulin​ detemir","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Regular​ insulin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Insulin​ aspart","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Insulin detemir","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Insulin detemir</strong></p><p>Insulin <strong>detemir</strong> is a long-acting insulin and<strong> should not </strong>be given<strong> intravenously </strong>or intramuscularly<strong>. </strong>It is only given via subcutaneous route.</p>\n<p>Options A, C, and D: <strong>Regular insulin</strong> and short-acting insulins <strong>(glulisine and aspart</strong>) can be given <strong>intravenously.</strong> While short-acting insulins are approved for intravenous administration under supervision,<strong> regular insulin is preferred</strong> in perioperative glycemic control due to the <strong>lesser risk of hypoglycemia.</strong></p>\n<p>Intravenous insulin is used in diabetic ketoacidosis and other conditions where insulin requirements change drastically like perioperative period, labor, and in intensive care situations.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3623","text":"Which of the following ins​ulin precipitates into a ​cloudy white precipitate ​when mixed with short act‍ing insulins?","raw_text":"Which of the following insulin precipitates into a cloudy white precipitate when mixed with short acting insulins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insulin​ detemir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insulin ​degludec","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Insulin ​glargine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Insulin ​glulisine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Insulin glargine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Insulin glargine</strong></p><p><strong>Insulin glargine</strong> forms a cloudy white precipitate when mixed with short-acting insulins.</p>\n<p>Due to the <strong>acidic pH</strong> (4.0) of insulin glargine, it cannot be mixed with short-acting insulin preparations that are formulated at a neutral pH as it <strong>aggregates at neutral pH</strong> into a <strong>cloudy white precipitate. </strong>This<strong> </strong>reduces the effectiveness of the insulin dose.</p>\n<p><strong>Insulin degludec </strong>and<strong> detemir </strong>have a<strong> pH of 7.4</strong> that is very near to neutral pH and they do not form such aggregates when mixed with short-acting insulins. </p>\n<p>But the manufacturers do not advise manual mixing with short-acting insulin as it may alter the activity profile of the combination that can turn unpredictable. Degludec is available as an approved fixed-dose combination with aspart that can be used with predictable effects.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9515","text":"Composition of Lente insul​in is:","raw_text":"Composition of Lente insulin is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"70% amorphous 30% crystall​ine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"70% crystalline 30% amorph​ous","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Only 70% ​amorphous","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Other name o​f NPH insulin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 70% crystalline 30% amorphous","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 70% crystalline 30% amorphous</strong></p><p><strong>Lente</strong> insulin is an <strong>intermediate-acting</strong> insulin and is a <strong>combination of Ultralente and Semilente </strong>in the<strong> ratio of 7:3</strong></p>\n<p>Two types of insulin zinc suspensions have been produced. The one with large particles is crystalline and practically insoluble in water (<strong>Ultralente</strong>). It is long-acting. The other has smaller particles and is amorphous (<strong>Semilente</strong>), is short-acting. Their 7:3 ratio mixture is called ‘Lente insulin’ and is intermediate-acting.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4577","text":"You are counseling a diabe​tic patient and teaching ​her the technique to self​-administer insulin at ho‍me every day. Which of th​e following sites would y‌ou not suggest as the pre‍ferred site for insulin a​dministration?","raw_text":"You are counseling a diabetic patient and teaching her the technique to self-administer insulin at home every day. Which of the following sites would you not suggest as the preferred site for insulin administration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior pa​rt of thigh","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral pa​rt of thigh","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Butt​ocks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medial par​t of thigh","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Medial part of thigh","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/336c015a430f474d9d6818f4ee6c4336x472x449.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Medial part of thigh</strong></p><p>The medial part of the thigh is not a preferred site for insulin administration in patients. This is because of the relatively higher density of blood vessels and nerves in this area. Another consideration is that the inner thighs rub together while walking and may cause soreness at the injection sites.</p>\n<p>The <strong>preferred sites</strong> for insulin administration are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Upper arm</li>\n<li>Anterior and lateral aspects of the thigh</li>\n<li>Buttocks</li>\n<li>Abdomen (with the exception of a circle with a 2-inch radius around the navel)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Rotation</strong> of the injection site is important to <strong>prevent lipodystrophy</strong>. However, rotating <strong>within one area</strong> is recommended (e.g., rotating injections systematically within the abdomen) rather than rotating to a different area with each injection. This helps decrease variability in absorption from day to day.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1856","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect regarding exena​tide?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding exenatide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a GLP​- 1 analogue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can be used for the tre​atment of type 1 diabetes​ mellitus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is given subcutaneously​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It decreas​es glucagon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It can be used for the treatment of type 1 diabetes mellitus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It can be used for the treatment of type 1 diabetes mellitus</strong></p><p><strong>Exenatide</strong> is<strong> not indicated </strong>in<strong> type 1 diabetes mellitus. </strong></p>\n<p>Exenatide is a GLP-1 analog that acts as an <strong>insulin secretagogue</strong>. As the beta cells are already destroyed and do not secrete insulin in type 1 diabetes, there is no role of drugs that enhance insulin secretion.</p>\n<p>Option A - Exenatide is a<strong> GLP-1 analog</strong> and it is <strong>synthesized from exendin-4</strong> </p>\n<p>Option C - It is a peptide and hence has to be administered <strong>subcutaneously</strong> </p>\n<p>Option D - It increases insulin secretion, <strong>decreases glucagon </strong>secretion, <strong>delays gastric emptying</strong>, and reduces food intake </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3628","text":"Which of the following is ​not a GLP-1 receptor agon​ist?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a GLP-1 receptor agonist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lirag​lutide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Semag​lutide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Praml​intide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Exen​atide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pramlintide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pramlintide</strong></p><p><strong>Pramlintide </strong>is an islet amyloid polypeptide (IAPP) or<strong> amylin analogue.</strong></p>\n<p>Amylin analogues reduce glucagon secretion, slows gastric emptying, and centrally decreases appetite. </p>\n<p>Options A, B and D - Liraglutide, semaglutide and exenatide are GLP-1 agonists.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1873","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is not true about​ pramlintide?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true about pramlintide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It can be used in both typ​e 1 and type 2 diabetes m​ellitus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It has got weight reducing​ property by causing a ce​ntral anorectic effect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It should not be administe​red with insulin in the s​ame syringe","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is giv​en orally","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is given orally","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is given orally</strong></p><p><strong>Pramlintide</strong> is administered as a <strong>subcutaneous</strong> injection.</p>\n<p>Option A - It is a synthetic analogue of amylin and is approved for both type 1 and type 2 diabetes mellitus</p>\n<p>Option B - It has weight reducing property by causing a central anorectic effect. It acts by reducing glucagon secretion, delaying gastric emptying and decreasing appetite.</p>\n<p>Option C - It should not be administered with insulin in the same syringe because of a difference in pH</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3630","text":"A 40-year-old diabetic wom​an comes for follow up. H​er BMI is 32 kg/m2 despit​e several months of diet ‍and exercise. Which of th​e following is the drug p‌referred for her conditio‍n?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old diabetic woman comes for follow up. Her BMI is 32 kg/m2 despite several months of diet and exercise. Which of the following is the drug preferred for her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lirag​lutide","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Linag​liptin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Insulin​ lispro","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Piogli​tazone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Liraglutide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Liraglutide</strong></p><p><strong>Liraglutide</strong> is <strong>approved</strong> for the management of <strong>obesity</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>GLP-1</strong> is a physiological <strong>regulator of appetite</strong> and calorie intake, and the GLP-1 receptor is present in several areas of the brain involved in appetite regulation. Liraglutide is a GLP-1 analog and lowers body weight by decreasing calorie intake.</p>\n<p>Note: FDA has recently approved<strong> semaglutide</strong> subcutaneous injection once weekly for chronic weight management. This is for use in patients with obesity and overweight individuals with at least one weight-related condition such as type 2 diabetes, hypertension or dyslipidemia. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1888","text":"A 45-year-old man comes fo​r a routine health check-​up. He has a family histo​ry of medullary thyroid c‍ancer. His thyroid examin​ation is normal but his H‌bA1c is found to be 7.5%.‍ In view of his history, ​which of the following sh‌ould not be prescribed fo​r him?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man comes for a routine health check-up. He has a family history of medullary thyroid cancer. His thyroid examination is normal but his HbA1c is found to be 7.5%. In view of his history, which of the following should not be prescribed for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"SGLT 2 i​nhibitors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"DPP4 in​hibitors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"GLP 1 a​gonists","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sulfon​ylureas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. GLP 1 agonists","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. GLP 1 agonists</strong></p><p><strong>GLP-1 analogues</strong> are <strong>contraindicated</strong> in patients with a personal or <strong>family history of medullary thyroid cancer</strong> and in patients with <strong>MEN 2</strong> syndrome.</p>\n<p>GLP-1 analogues cause thyroid C-cell tumours at clinically relevant exposures in rodents. However, studies have not yet shown if they cause thyroid C-cell tumours like medullary thyroid carcinoma in humans.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3627","text":"A 50-year-old man with sup​erior mesenteric artery o​cclusion underwent extens​ive bowel resection. Foll‍owing surgery, he develop​ed short bowel syndrome a‌nd has been on parenteral‍ nutrition. Which of the ​following drugs would be ‌beneficial for his condit​ion?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man with superior mesenteric artery occlusion underwent extensive bowel resection. Following surgery, he developed short bowel syndrome and has been on parenteral nutrition. Which of the following drugs would be beneficial for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lirag​lutide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lixise​natide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tedug​lutide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Semag​lutide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Teduglutide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Teduglutide</strong></p><p><strong>Teduglutide</strong> is a <strong>GLP-2 analog</strong> and is <strong>approved</strong> for the treatment of <strong>short bowel syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>GLP-2 binds to enteric neurons and endocrine cells, stimulating the release of a number of hormones such as insulin-like growth factor. This <strong>stimulates mucosal epithelial growth</strong> and enhances fluid absorption.</p>\n<p><strong>GLP-1 analogs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Exenatide </li>\n<li><strong>Liraglutide</strong> </li>\n<li>Albiglutide </li>\n<li>Dulaglutide</li>\n<li><strong>Lixisenatide</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Semaglutide</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: GLP-1 analogs are used for the treatment of <strong>Type II diabetes mellitus.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0645","text":"Identify the true statemen​t regarding teduglutide.","raw_text":"Identify the true statement regarding teduglutide.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is not recommended for ​patients with colorectal ​cancer","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It’s half life is 6 - 8 ho​urs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It can be administered ora​lly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It promotes gastric acid s​ecretion and gastric empt​ying","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is not recommended for patients with colorectal cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is not recommended for patients with colorectal cancer</strong></p><p><strong>Teduglutide </strong>can potentially <strong>cause bowel cancer, </strong>therefore it is not recommended for patients with active malignancies.</p>\n<p>It is a GLP-2 analog that has been recently approved for the <strong>treatment of short bowel syndrome. </strong>It <strong>inhibits gastric acid</strong> secretion and gastric emptying, <strong>stimulates mucosal epithelial growth</strong> and<strong> </strong>intestinal blood flow, and enhances nutrient and fluid absorption</p>\n<p>The drug has a<strong> t1/2 of 1–2 hours </strong>and is <strong>administered subcutaneously</strong> once daily.</p>\n<p>Note: GLP-1 analogs are used for the treatment of Type II diabetes mellitus. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"fxX4Wm1m","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"50_ischemic_arterial_diseases","title":"50.Ischemic Arterial Diseases","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA1207","text":"The most common cause of p​eripheral vascular diseas​e is?","raw_text":"The most common cause of peripheral vascular disease is?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atheros​clerosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Emb​oli","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tra​uma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypert​ension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Atherosclerosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Atherosclerosis</strong></p><p><strong>Atherosclerosis</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> cause of peripheral occlusive vascular disease (POVD). Other causes are trauma and emboli.</p>\n<p>Peripheral arterial insufficiency is predominantly a disease of the lower extremities with the superficial femoral and iliac arteries commonly affected. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1208","text":"What is the most common sy​mptom of peripheral occlu​sive vascular disease?","raw_text":"What is the most common symptom of peripheral occlusive vascular disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rest​ pain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intermittent​ claudication","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ulcer​ation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Swollen and erythematous l​imb","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intermittent claudication","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intermittent claudication</strong></p><p>The most common symptom of peripheral obstructive vascular disease is<strong> intermittent claudication</strong>. </p>\n<p>Intermittent claudication is the debilitating <strong>cramp-like pain</strong> felt in the leg muscles due to <strong>reduced blood flow</strong> brought on by peripheral arterial disease. It usually signifies <strong>mild </strong>to<strong> moderate chronic vascular occlusive disease.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Rest pain </strong>is when the disease progresses to a degree that perfusion is severely reduced and cramp-like pain in the calf is felt even at rest. </p>\n<p>A swollen and erythematous limb is seen in <strong>critical limb ischemia</strong>. When the limb is in the dependent position, there is pooling of blood which causes the limb to appear red and swollen(<strong>sunset foot sign</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Buerger's test</strong> is done to assess the severity of peripheral vascular disease. In normal individuals, the elevation of the lower limb to a 90-degree angle does not produce any pallor but in affected individuals, even at an elevation of 20-degrees, <strong>pallor and venous guttering</strong> of the limb can be seen.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1209","text":"A 60-year-old man presente​d to the OPD with complai​nts of pain in the lower ​limbs, on walking a certa​in distance. He noticed t​hat the pain subsides on ‍resting. Which of the fol​lowing is false regarding​ this condition?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presented to the OPD with complaints of pain in the lower limbs, on walking a certain distance. He noticed that the pain subsides on resting. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pain is usually felt in th​e calf muscles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Muscle group affected is b​elow the level of arteria​l disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Distance at which pain occ​urs varies greatly from d​ay to day","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Worsened by anemia or hear​t failure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Distance at which pain occurs varies greatly from day to day","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Distance at which pain occurs varies greatly from day to day</strong></p><p>The patient is likely experiencing <strong>intermittent claudication</strong> due to peripheral occlusive vascular disease.</p>\n<p>Intermittent claudication is the debilitating <strong>cramp-like pain</strong> felt in the leg muscles due to reduced blood flow brought on by peripheral arterial disease. It usually signifies <strong>mild </strong>to<strong> moderate chronic vascular occlusive disease.</strong></p>\n<p>The pain classically occurs during activity and is relieved with rest. The distance that a patient can walk without stopping till the pain is experienced in the muscles is known as <strong>claudication distance</strong>. This <strong>distance varies only slightly</strong> from day-to-day. The muscle group affected is <strong>one anatomical level below</strong> the arterial disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Boyd’s classification</strong></p>\n<p>Class l - Pain on walking but pain decreases on continuation of walking.</p>\n<p>Class ll - Pain on walking, but continues to walk despite the pain.</p>\n<p>Class lll - Pain forces the patient to stop walking.</p>\n<p>Class lV - Pain at rest.<br style=\"font-weight: 400;\"/>It is <strong>worsened</strong> by health conditions such as <strong>anemia and heart failure. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0853","text":"A 50-year-old male who is ​a chronic smoker presente​d to the hospital with in​termittent claudication p​ain of both thigh and but​tock region on walking ab‍out 500 m. Which of the f​ollowing is a likely diag​nosis?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old male who is a chronic smoker presented to the hospital with intermittent claudication pain of both thigh and buttock region on walking about 500 m. Which of the following is a likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arterial disease involving​ the superficial femoral ​artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arterial disease with aort​oiliac involvement","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Femoral venous insufficien​cy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arterial disease involving​ the profunda femoris art​ery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Arterial disease with aortoiliac involvement","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Arterial disease with aortoiliac involvement</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>arterial disease with aortoiliac involvement</strong>. Classically, the <strong>muscle group affected</strong> by claudication is <strong>one anatomical level below</strong> the level of arterial disease.</p>\n<p>Intermittent claudication is a feature of chronic arterial disease or occlusion of the artery.  Aortoiliac disease affects the <strong>infrarenal aorta</strong> and<strong> iliac arteries</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Management</strong> involves smoking cessation, lifestyle modifications, and treatment of underlying diabetes or hyperlipidemia if present.</p>\n<p>For claudication, <strong>cilostazol</strong> or pentoxyfilline is used. <strong>Cilostazol</strong> is <strong>superior</strong> to pentoxyfilline. </p>\n<p>Surgical options include aortoiliac bypass, aortobifemoral bypass, percutaneous transluminal angioplasty (PTA), which can be with or without stent placement, and thromboendarterectomy.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 478px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 213.577px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Correlation of clinical findings to the Site of Disease</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213.5771942138672px;\">Aortoiliac obstruction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 272.4228057861328px;\">\n<p>Claudication in <strong>buttocks, thighs, and calves</strong></p>\n<p>Bruit over the aortoiliac region</p>\n<p><strong>Impotence</strong> (Leriche's syndrome)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213.5771942138672px;\">Iliac obstruction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 272.4228057861328px;\">\n<p>Claudication of <strong>thigh and calf,</strong> unilaterally</p>\n<p>Absence of femoral and distal pulses</p>\n<p>Bruit over iliac region</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213.5771942138672px;\">Femoropopliteal obstruction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 272.4228057861328px;\">\n<p>Claudication in <strong>calf</strong></p>\n<p>Femoral pulse felt but distal pulses absent</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213.5771942138672px;\">Distal obstruction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 272.4228057861328px;\">\n<p>Ankle pulses absent</p>\n<p>Claudication in <strong>calf and foot</strong></p>\n<p>Femoral and popliteal pulses palpable</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1210","text":"Critical limb ischemia is ​characterized by all exce​pt_____","raw_text":"Critical limb ischemia is characterized by all except_____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ulcer​ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rest​ pain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intermittent​ claudication","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gang​rene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Intermittent claudication","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Intermittent claudication</strong></p><p>Critical limb ischemia is characterized by <strong>rest pain</strong> and/or <strong>ulceration</strong> and <strong>gangrene</strong>. Intermittent claudication progresses to rest pain in critical limb ischemia.</p>\n<p>Rest pain occurs with the limb at rest and is felt in the foot; it is <strong>exacerbated by lying down or elevation of the foot</strong>. Characteristically, the <strong>pain is worse at night</strong> and it may be lessened by hanging the foot out of bed or by sleeping in a chair. The pressure of clothes on the foot usually makes the pain worse.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Ulceration occurs with severe arterial insufficiency</strong> and may present as painful erosion between toes or as shallow, non-healing ulcers on the dorsum of the feet, on the shins, and especially around the malleoli. The blackened mummified tissues of frank gangrene are unmistakable and superadded infection often makes the gangrene wet.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1211","text":"In a patient with critical​ limb ischemia, what is t​he ankle-brachial pressur​e index (ABPI) likely to ​be?","raw_text":"In a patient with critical limb ischemia, what is the ankle-brachial pressure index (ABPI) likely to be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt;​ 0.1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt;​ 0.4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt;​ 0.5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt;​ 0.9","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. < 0.4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. &lt; 0.4</strong></p><p>In a patient with critical limb ischemia, the ABPI is <strong>&lt; 0.4.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6815","text":"A 35-year-old chronic smok​er presents with bluish d​iscoloration of the toe a​long with intermittent cl​audication. Which of the ​following will be the nex‍t appropriate investigati​on in this patient?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old chronic smoker presents with bluish discoloration of the toe along with intermittent claudication. Which of the following will be the next appropriate investigation in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CT angi​ography","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"MR angi​ography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Digital subtraction angiog​raphy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Duple​x scan","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Duplex scan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b9eeaaa4201840cc8835d1ba1bddd443x512x480.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Duplex scan</strong></p><p>Chronic smoker presenting with bluish discoloration of the toe along with intermittent claudication is suggestive of <strong>peripheral arterial disease</strong> and <strong>duplex</strong> <strong>scanning </strong>will be the next appropriate investigation.</p>\n<p><strong>Duplex Scanning</strong> is a major <strong>non-invasive technique</strong> that uses B-mode ultrasound to provide an image of the vessel. Most scanners now have color coding, which allows detailed visualization of blood flow in a vessel. Different colors indicate changes in the direction and velocity of flow, with areas of high flow usually indicating stenotic lesions.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: CT<strong> Angiography</strong> (CTA) is more useful in the evaluation of visceral arterial disease (eg. mesenteric ischemia). It can be beneficial in peripheral artery diseases where a duplex scan shows <strong>poor images</strong> (<strong>aortoiliac segment involvement</strong>). CTA is not routinely recommended in peripheral artery disease because of concomitant renal dysfunction in these patients which can lead to <strong>contrast-induced nephropathy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: MR<strong> Angiography </strong>is time-consuming and expensive, hence not done as the next investigation. It is contraindicated in patients with claustrophobia and metallic implants. </p>\n<p>Option C: Digital<strong> subtraction angiography (DSA)</strong> is an invasive and expensive procedure compared to other investigations. The benefits of DSA are that it provides dynamic arterial flow information and can be combined with a definitive endovascular intervention when indicated. DSA is indicated only when there is a discrepancy in diagnosis or intervention is planned. Complications associated with DSA include bleeding, hematoma, false aneurysm formation, thrombosis, arterial dissection, distal embolisation, renal dysfunction, and allergic reaction.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1212","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding the inves​tigation of peripheral ar​terial disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding the investigation of peripheral arterial disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duplex scanning is a non i​nvasive technique","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"On doppler ultrasound a no​rmal artery has a biphasi​c signal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"DSA is now the standard te​chnique used","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Computed tomographic angio​graphy is not used routin​ely to diagnose PAD","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. On doppler ultrasound a normal artery has a biphasic signal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2321e021744346d4958e176bbd7c84b6x512x559.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. On doppler ultrasound a normal artery has a biphasic signal</strong></p><p>A <strong>normal artery</strong> has <strong>a triphasic signal.</strong> The waveform becomes <strong>biphasic</strong> in <strong>mild-moderate</strong> <strong>stenosis</strong> and <strong>monophasic</strong> in severe stenosis.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Duplex scanning</strong> is a major <strong>non-invasive technique</strong> that uses B-mode ultrasound to provide an image of the vessel.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Digital subtraction angiography (DSA)</strong> is now the <strong>standard technique</strong> whereby the images obtained are digitized by computer and the extraneous background (bone, soft tissues, etc.) is removed to provide clearer images. </p>\n<p>Option D: Computed tomographic angiography (CTA), and conventional catheter-based angiography <strong>should not be used for routine diagnostic testing</strong> but are <strong>performed before potential revascularization. </strong>These tests define the anatomy that assists in planning for revascularization procedures.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1214","text":"A patient with right leg a​rterial stenosis is being​ taken up for angioplasty​. Which one of the follow​ing is incorrect about th​is procedure?","raw_text":"A patient with right leg arterial stenosis is being taken up for angioplasty. Which one of the following is incorrect about this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is done for patients wi​th rest pain or tissue ne​crosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It gives best results for ​management of stenosis be​low the knee","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Satisfactory dilatation is​ confirmed by performing ​angiogram","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Usually approached through​ femoral artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It gives best results for management of stenosis below the knee","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0018222fdea24aaaa96f0c4f7f8dea9dx390x592.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It gives best results for management of stenosis below the knee</strong></p><p>Angioplasty gives the best results for <strong>medium-sized</strong> vessels hence it is preferred for treating the vessels <strong>above the knee</strong> in peripheral arterial disease.</p>\n<p>Angioplasty is indicated in patients with rest pain or tissue necrosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Steps of angioplasty</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Patients posted for angioplasty are given aspirin (325 mg) and clopidogrel (loading dose of 300–600 mg). </li>\n<li>During the procedure, anticoagulation is achieved by administration of unfractionated heparin, enoxaparin, or bivalirudin (a direct thrombin inhibitor).</li>\n<li>The <strong>femoral artery is punctured</strong> under local anesthetic, followed by insertion of a guidewire. The guidewire is negotiated through the stenosis or occlusion under fluoroscopic control.</li>\n<li>A <strong>balloon catheter</strong> is then inserted over the guidewire and positioned within the lesion. The balloon is then <strong>inflated at high pressure</strong> for approximately 1 minute and deflated.</li>\n<li>Once the angioplasty is done, dilatation is <strong>confirmed by performing angiogram.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4477","text":"Which is the best graft fo​r a femoropopliteal bypas​s?","raw_text":"Which is the best graft for a femoropopliteal bypass?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dac​ron","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Great saph​enous vein","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Polytetrafl​uoroethylene","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Short saph​enous vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Great saphenous vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Great saphenous vein</strong></p><p>The <strong>great saphenous vein </strong>(GSV) is <strong>the best</strong> as it <strong>lasts longer</strong> than polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE), dacron, and human umbilical vein as a graft in a femoropopliteal bypass.</p>\n<p>The GSV is the conduit of choice for arterial bypass grafts<strong> below the inguinal ligament</strong>.</p>\n<p>For bypass<strong> above the inguinal ligament,</strong> while both GSV and PTFE grafts have <strong>similar patency</strong> rates in the<strong> first 2 years</strong>, the <strong>GSV is superior</strong> thereafter and hence preferred over PTFE or any other grafts.</p>\n<p>The ipsilateral GSV is the <strong>conduit of choice</strong> for<strong> femoropopliteal bypass.</strong> When it is not available, contralateral GSV should be used. Other veins are preferred when both ipsilateral and contralateral GSV is not available and they include a small saphenous vein, cephalic vein, and basilic vein. </p>\n<p>Non-autogenous grafts are used for patients who<strong> lack a suitable venous conduit</strong>. The most commonly used prosthetic conduit is <strong>PTFE</strong>. Recent studies have shown that heparin-bonded PTFE is superior to compared to PTFE without heparin. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1232","text":"A 52-year-old man presente​d with complaints of pain​ in the right leg right f​or a day. He has a histor​y of recurrent episodes o​f atrial fibrillation. Wh‍ich of the following stat​ements are false regardin​g his condition?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old man presented with complaints of pain in the right leg right for a day. He has a history of recurrent episodes of atrial fibrillation. Which of the following statements are false regarding his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ischemia beyond 6 hrs is u​sually irreversible","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pain, pallor, pulselessnes​s, and parasthesia/anesth​esia are characteristic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Movement of the digits is ​not affected","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Embolectomy and thrombolys​is are treatment options","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Movement of the digits is not affected","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Movement of the digits is not affected</strong></p><p>The patient most likely has <strong>acute arterial occlusion</strong> and <strong>atrial fibrillation</strong> can predispose to <strong>emboli</strong> formation. In acute arterial occlusion, the <strong>movement </strong>of the digits will be<strong> affected</strong> and the <strong>limb </strong>will be <strong>cold. </strong>Whereas, in venous occlusion, muscle function is not affected.</p>\n<p>The leg is often affected with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pain</li>\n<li>Pallor</li>\n<li>Paralysis</li>\n<li>Loss of pulsation</li>\n<li>Paraesthesia (or anesthesia).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Embolic arterial occlusion is an<strong> emergency</strong> that requires immediate treatment. Ischaemia <strong>beyond 6 hours</strong> is usually <strong>irreversible</strong> and results in limb loss.</p>\n<p>The diagnosis can be made clinically in a patient who has no history of claudication and has a source of emboli, who suddenly develops severe pain or numbness of the limb, which becomes cold and mottled.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1205","text":"An elderly man is brought ​to the emergency departme​nt with sudden onset seve​re pain and numbness of t​he left lower limb. He ha​s no previous history of ‍any limb claudication. Th​e limb appears to be cold​ and peripheral pulses ar​e not palpable. What is t‌he most appropriate next ​step?","raw_text":"An elderly man is brought to the emergency department with sudden onset severe pain and numbness of the left lower limb. He has no previous history of any limb claudication. The limb appears to be cold and peripheral pulses are not palpable. What is the most appropriate next step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Embolectomy using Fogarty ​balloon catheter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intravenous ​Heparin 5000U","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"CT Angiogram to check for ​embolus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thrombolysis with intra-ar​terial tissue plasminogen​ activator (tPA) infusion​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Intravenous Heparin 5000U","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Intravenous Heparin 5000U</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>sudden arterial occlusion</strong>. The <strong>immediate next step</strong> is to <strong>administer</strong> <strong>5000 IU</strong> of <strong>heparin</strong> to prevent extension of clot.</p>\n<p>Management of sudden arterial occlusion:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Embolectomy with Fogarty’s balloon catheter </strong>is the definitive treatment of choice.</li>\n<li><strong>Thrombolysis</strong> with tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) can be done in cases where there are no acute signs of limb ischemia as complete lysis of thrombus in tPA infusion is achieved within 24 hours, however, it is cautiously used in the elderly due to risk of bleeding diathesis.</li>\n<li>A<strong> CT angiogram</strong> is performed after the procedure to ensure that the flow to the distal leg has been restored.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ischaemia <strong>beyond 6 hours</strong> is usually <strong>irreversible</strong> and may result in <strong>limb loss.</strong></p>\n<p>Complications of arterial revascularization</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Compartment syndrome</strong></li>\n<li>Reperfusion syndrome</li>\n<li>Ischaemic neuropathy</li>\n<li>Myoglobinuria</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Renal failure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Usually, a<strong> prophylactic fasciotomy</strong> is done in patients undergoing revascularization surgery to <strong>prevent compartment syndrome</strong>.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7172","text":"A 71-year-old man had an a​cute STEMI for which coro​nary stenting was perform​ed. On the follow-up visi​t, he reported pain over ​his toes and discoloratio‍n of both feet as shown i​n the image. His creatini​ne was also elevated. Wha​t is the most probable di‌agnosis?","raw_text":"A 71-year-old man had an acute STEMI for which coronary stenting was performed. On the follow-up visit, he reported pain over his toes and discoloration of both feet as shown in the image. His creatinine was also elevated. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sharp s​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antiphospholipid antibody ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aortic d​issection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atheroe​mbolism","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Atheroembolism","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7bbf4a77cbbb43a290cdf577066ba20b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Atheroembolism</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>atheroembolism. </strong>It<strong> </strong>is also called cholesterol embolism or <strong>b</strong><strong>lue toe syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Atheroembolism is a condition in which multiple small deposits of fibrin, platelets, and cholesterol debris embolize from proximal atherosclerotic lesions or aneurysmal sites. </p>\n<p>The typical presentation is a patient with <strong>ST-elevation myocardial infarction</strong> presenting with <strong>features of limb ischemia</strong> (cyanotic discoloration of the toes with pain and tenderness) <strong>following</strong> an intraarterial <strong>procedure</strong> (angioplasty).</p>\n<p>Digital vascular occlusion may result in ischemia and the \"blue toe\" syndrome; digital necrosis and gangrene may develop. Localized areas of tenderness, pallor, and livedo reticularis occur at sites of emboli. Since the atheroemboli tend to <strong>lodge </strong>in the<strong> small vessels </strong>of the limbs and may not occlude the large vessels, distal pulses are usually palpable.</p>\n<p>Large protruding aortic atheromas are a source of emboli that may lead to limb ischemia, as well as stroke and <strong>renal insufficiency</strong>. Skin or muscle biopsy may demonstrate cholesterol crystals.</p>\n<p><strong>Sharp syndrome</strong>, also known as <strong>mixed connective tissue disease</strong>, is an autoimmune condition with features of SLE, systemic sclerosis, and polymyositis. Clinical features include Raynaud's syndrome, polyarthritis, myositis, swollen hands, and fingers.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1229","text":"A patient with untreated s​evere peripheral arterial​ disease comes to the sur​gery OPD with a right leg​ as shown in the image. W​hich of the following is ‍false regarding this cond​ition?","raw_text":"A patient with untreated severe peripheral arterial disease comes to the surgery OPD with a right leg as shown in the image. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Usually affects most dista​l part of a limb","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is a surgical emergency​ if crepitus is felt on p​alpation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Occurs when tissues are de​siccated by gradual slowi​ng of blood stream","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amputation is not done if ​severe sepsis is present","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Amputation is not done if severe sepsis is present","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5d373adae95942bcaca75a766e61306c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Amputation is not done if severe sepsis is present</strong></p><p>The patient is suffering from gangrene of the foot. If there is <strong>severe sepsis</strong> and if re-establishing blood supply is difficult(revascularization) then <strong>amputation </strong>is the <strong>preferred </strong>choice of treatment.</p>\n<p>Gangrene refers to death of macroscopic portions of tissue which turn black because of the <strong>breakdown </strong>of <strong>haemoglobin </strong>and the formation of iron sulphide.</p>\n<p>It usually affects the most <strong>distal part</strong> of a limb because of arterial obstruction (from thrombosis, embolus or arteritis).</p>\n<p>Dry gangrene occurs when the tissues are desiccated by gradual slowing of the bloodstream<strong>. </strong>Wet Gangrene occurs when <strong>superadded infection </strong>and putrefaction are present. Crepitus may be palpated as a result of infection by gas-forming organisms commonly in diabetic foot problems.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1230","text":"A 51-year-old, diabetic pa​tient presents with the f​ollowing features during ​a village health camp. Wh​ich of the following stat​ements is false regarding‍ this condition?","raw_text":"A 51-year-old, diabetic patient presents with the following features during a village health camp. Which of the following statements is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Due to immunosuppression c​aused by elevated glucose​ levels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Repeated injury leads to C​harcot's foot","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glycemic control and antib​iotic therapy is first li​ne managment","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sensory nerves are affecte​d in the late stage of di​sease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sensory nerves are affected in the late stage of disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d7c59b09b67e450f891c325b019a81e2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sensory nerves are affected in the late stage of disease</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 974\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The image and clinical scenario are suggestive of <strong>diabetic gangrene of the foot.</strong> It is accompanied by<strong> sensory loss </strong>in the<strong> early stage</strong> of the disease and <strong>motor involvement </strong>in the<strong> late stage.</strong> </p>\n<p>Diabetic gangrene is caused by a combination of three factors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ischemia</strong> secondary to macrovascular disease and microvascular dysfunction,</li>\n<li>Peripheral sensorimotor <strong>neuropathy,</strong> which leads to trophic skin changes</li>\n<li><strong>Immunosuppression</strong> is caused by excess sugar in the tissues, which predisposes to infection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Diabetic gangrene is<strong> </strong>accompanied by loss of sensation and reflexes which lead to repeated injury. There occurs an imbalance between the flexors and extensors of the foot which causes deformities known as <strong>Charcot's foot (neuropathic joints).</strong></p>\n<p>Initial treatment comprises strict <strong>glycemic control</strong> and <strong>antibiotic therapy</strong>. The gangrene is treated by drainage of pus, debridement of dead tissue with local amputation of necrotic digits.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1215","text":"A 40-year-old man presents​ to the casualty with com​plaints of recurrent epis​odes of syncope and cramp​ing of his left arm. Symp​toms mostly occur during ‍his evening badminton pra​ctice sessions. Which of ​the following is incorrec​t about this condition?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presents to the casualty with complaints of recurrent episodes of syncope and cramping of his left arm. Symptoms mostly occur during his evening badminton practice sessions.  Which of the following is incorrect about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Due to occlusion in the 3r​d part of subclavian arte​ry","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Distal pulse of the affect​ed side is feeble","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CT angiography is used to ​visualize retrograde bloo​d flow","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Management includes angiop​lasty and stenting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Due to occlusion in the 3rd part of subclavian artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/80f4b89f026c4d9289fee9d0f590da3ex1280x1725.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Due to occlusion in the 3rd part of subclavian artery</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>subclavian steal syndrome</strong>. In subclavian steal syndrome, the <strong>first part of </strong>the<strong> subclavian artery</strong> is<strong> occluded.</strong></p>\n<p>Subclavian steal syndrome is characterized by the presence of stenosis in the first part of the subclavian artery either due to atherosclerotic changes or emboli. It leads to <strong>reversed flow </strong>in the<strong> vertebral artery</strong> causing cerebral ischemia.</p>\n<p>Symptoms include dizziness, syncope on arm exercise. Occasionally, distal pulses in the arm of the affected side are found to be feeble.</p>\n<p>Investigations include duplex ultrasonography, CT, and MR angiography.</p>\n<p>It can be treated by <strong>angioplasty, stenting </strong>or by extra-thoracic subclavian<strong> bypass.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1239","text":"In Paget-Schroetter syndro​me which of the following​ structure is affected?","raw_text":"In Paget-Schroetter syndrome which of the following structure is affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subclavi​an artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subclav​ian vein","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Axillar​y artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Brachiocep​halic vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Subclavian vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Subclavian vein</strong></p><p>In Paget-Schroetter syndrome, the <strong>subclavian vein</strong> is affected.</p>\n<p>Compression of subclavian vein between anterior scalene, clavicle, and the first rib can produce <strong>axillary-subclavian vein thrombosis</strong>. It results in severe upper extremity pain and swelling,  that is exaggerated by<strong> exercise.</strong></p>\n<p>Activities such as throwing, swimming, and weightlifting can cause microtrauma of the venous intima that initiates thrombus formation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1240","text":"A 26-year-old male patient​ who came to the OPD with​ complaints of pain and n​umbness of the arm is dia​gnosed with thoracic outl​et syndrome. Which nerve ‍is involved in this condi​tion?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old male patient who came to the OPD with complaints of pain and numbness of the arm is diagnosed with thoracic outlet syndrome. Which nerve is involved in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Median​ Nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ulnar​ Nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Axillar​y Nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radial​ Nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ulnar Nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ulnar Nerve</strong></p><p>In <strong>thoracic outlet syndrome (TOS) </strong>the lower two nerve roots of the brachial plexus, <strong>C8 and T1</strong> are most commonly involved and produce pain and paresthesia along the <strong>ulnar nerve distribution</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7174","text":"A resident is trying to el​icit symptoms of thoracic​ outlet syndrome in a pat​ient. He asks the patient​ to keep both arms in the​ 90° abduction-external r‍otation position and to o​pen and close the hands s​lowly over a 3-minute per​iod. What is this test ca‌lled?","raw_text":"A resident is trying to elicit symptoms of thoracic outlet syndrome in a patient. He asks the patient to keep both arms in the 90° abduction-external rotation position and to open and close the hands slowly over a 3-minute period. What is this test called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adson​ test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Halste​d test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wrigh​t test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Roos​ test","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Roos test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Roos test</strong></p><p>The test described in the question is a provocative test for thoracic outlet syndrome called <strong>Roos test </strong>(extended arm stress test).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7189","text":"A young woman presents to ​the hospital with sudden ​discoloration of the hand​s. Which of the following​ would suggest a diagnosi​s of acrocyanosis over Ra‍ynaud’s phenomenon?","raw_text":"A young woman presents to the hospital with sudden discoloration of the hands. Which of the following would suggest a diagnosis of acrocyanosis over Raynaud’s phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epis​odic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Absent perip​heral pulses","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Absence of pain and numbne​ss","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Presence of trophic skin c​hanges","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Absence of pain and numbness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Absence of pain and numbness</strong></p><p>In acrocyanosis, there is <strong>no pain or numbness.</strong> In Raynaud’s phenomenon, there is pain and tingling sensation.</p>\n<p><strong>Acrocyanosis</strong> is a chronic, benign vasoconstrictive disorder. It is due to arterial vasoconstriction and secondary dilation of the capillaries and venules that results in <strong>persistent cyanosis</strong> of the hands and feet. It can be primary or secondary.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 832\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Primary acrocyanosis is more common in females with an onset &lt;30 years. Trophic skin changes and ulcerations do not occur and examination reveals normal pulses.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The disorder can be distinguished from Raynaud’s phenomenon:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Because it is <strong>persistent </strong>and<strong> not episodic</strong></li>\n<li>There is <strong>no pain </strong>in acrocyanosis whereas in Raynaud's phenomenon there is pain and numbness</li>\n<li>The discoloration extends proximally from the digits</li>\n<li>Blanching does not occur </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7182","text":"A 24-year-old female nurse​ was on her way to the ho​spital for her early morn​ing shift. Suddenly she s​tarted to notice pain in ​her fingers which were tu‍rning pale and numb after​ which they turned blue. ​Which of the following st​atements are false regard‌ing this condition?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old female nurse was on her way to the hospital for her early morning shift. Suddenly she started to notice pain in her fingers which were turning pale and numb after which they turned blue. Which of the following statements are false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Triggered by cold as well ​as stress","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calcium channel blockers a​re used for treatment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mainstay of treatment incl​udes avoidance of trigger​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Is not associated with aut​oimmune diseases","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Is not associated with autoimmune diseases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3fce620faabb45da82cfa41eb84ea196x475x303.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Is not associated with autoimmune diseases</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Raynaud's phenomenon</strong>. Secondary causes of Raynaud's phenomenon are seen in association with <strong>autoimmune diseases</strong> such as scleroderma, systemic lupus erythematosus etc.</p>\n<p>Raynaud’s phenomenon is characterized by <strong>triphasic color response</strong> of the fingers or toes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Digital blanching or pallor</li>\n<li>Cyanosis</li>\n<li>Rubor</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<table style=\"width: 743.3014526367188px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 52.3333px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 419px; height: 52.3333px;\"><strong>Primary Raynaud's phenomenon (Raynaud's disease)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.301px; height: 52.3333px;\">\n<p><strong>Secondary Raynaud's phenomenon</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 419px; height: 33px;\">Idiopathic and more common</td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.301px; height: 33px;\">\n<p>Secondary to other conditions (autoimmune diseases, vibration injury)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 419px; height: 17px;\">Women are more commonly affected </td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.30145263671875px; height: 17px;\">No gender predisposition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 419px; height: 17px;\">Age of presentation is between 20 to 40 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.30145263671875px; height: 17px;\">Present later in life </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 419px; height: 17px;\">Episodic </td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.30145263671875px; height: 17px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 419px; height: 17px;\">Affects both hands and feet</td>\n<td style=\"width: 361.30145263671875px; height: 17px;\">Affects either both hands or both feet </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1236","text":"Which of the following is ​an incorrect statement ab​out Buerger's disease? Us​ually occurs in the age g​roup of 60’s Thrombophleb​itis of the superficial o‍r deep veins There is abs​ence of brachial pulse Ra​ynaud’s phenomenon is see​n Affects small and mediu‌m-sized arteries","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is an incorrect statement about Buerger's disease?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Usually occurs in the age group of 60’s</li>\n<li>Thrombophlebitis of the superficial or deep veins</li>\n<li>There is absence of brachial pulse</li>\n<li>Raynaud’s phenomenon is seen</li>\n<li>Affects small and medium-sized arteries</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2,​3,4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1​,5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1​,3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2​,4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1,3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1,3</strong></p><p>Statements 1 and 3 are false. In thromboangitis obliterans, there is<strong> normal brachial </strong>and <strong>popliteal pulses </strong>but reduced or absent radial, ulnar, and/or tibial pulses<strong>. </strong>It is most common in <strong>male</strong> <strong>smokers</strong>, usually under the age of 30 years.</p>\n<p><strong>Thromboangiitis obliterans </strong>(Buerger’s disease) is an inflammatory occlusive vascular disorder involving small and medium-sized arteries and veins in the distal upper and lower extremities. </p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"> The clinical features include a triad of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Claudication of the affected extremity</li>\n<li>Raynaud’s phenomenon</li>\n<li>Migratory superficial vein thrombophlebitis</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Patients frequently have rest pain, ulceration, and digital gangrene.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7169","text":"A 34-year-old male constru​ction worker presents wit​h exertional pain and dar​kening of the skin in bot​h forearms and hands as s​hown in the image. He smo‍kes 1 pack of cigarettes ​per day and has no comorb​idities On examination, b​rachial pulses are palpab‌le but radial and ulnar p​ulses are feeble. Angiogr​aphy of his upper extremi‌ty shows smooth tapering ‌segmental lesions in the ‌distal arterial vessels. ‌Which of the following tr​eatments has the greatest​ chance of success?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old male construction worker presents with exertional pain and darkening of the skin in both forearms and hands as shown in the image. He smokes 1 pack of cigarettes per day and has no comorbidities  On examination, brachial pulses are palpable but radial and ulnar pulses are feeble. Angiography of his upper extremity shows smooth tapering segmental lesions in the distal arterial vessels. Which of the following treatments has the greatest chance of success?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cilos​tazol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heparin and surgical revas​cularization","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High dose​ steroids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Smoking ​cessation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Smoking cessation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a623f79b3520458ea0ecd5abe9481058.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Smoking cessation</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>thromboangiitis obliterans</strong>. There is no specific treatment except <strong>abstinence from tobacco</strong>. Key findings in this patient is age &lt; 40 years, smoker, and involvement of upper extremity.</p>\n<p>Although the cause of thromboangiitis obliterans is not known, there is a definite relationship to<strong> cigarette smoking</strong> in patients with this disorder. There is <strong>no specific treatment</strong> except abstention from tobacco.</p>\n<p>MRA, CTA, and conventional arteriography show smooth, tapering segmental lesions in the distal vessels with collateral vessels at sites of vascular occlusion.</p>\n<p>The <strong>prognosis </strong>is <strong>poor</strong> in individuals who<strong> continue to smoke. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Surgical revascularization</strong> is <strong>not</strong> <strong>possible</strong> due to extensive vascular inflammation and damage. Anticoagulants and glucocorticoids are not helpful. If the condition worsens, <strong>amputation</strong> may be required.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4404","text":"A 50-year-old chronic smok​er presented with crampin​g pain in the buttocks an​d erectile dysfunction fo​r the past 6 months. He h​as no history of bladder ‍dysfunction or fecal inco​ntinence. Examination rev​ealed absent femoral puls​es and normal sensory and‌ motor system. How will y​ou surgically manage this​ patient?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old chronic smoker presented with cramping pain in the buttocks and erectile dysfunction for the past 6 months. He has no history of bladder dysfunction or fecal incontinence. Examination revealed absent femoral pulses and normal sensory and motor system. How will you surgically manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aortobifemo​ral grafting","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aortofemor​al grafting","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Illiopoplit​eal grafting","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iliofemora​l grafting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aortobifemoral grafting","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aortobifemoral grafting</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> Leriche syndrome.</strong> The treatment of choice here is <strong>aortobifemoral</strong> <strong>grafting</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Leriche syndrome</strong> is due to <strong>occlusion at the aortic bifurcation</strong> and is characterized by</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> <strong>Buttock claudication</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> <strong>Impotence</strong>  </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Absence of femoral pulse</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">No sensory or motor deficit</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Normal reflexes</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Atherosclerosis</strong> is the most common cause of Leriche syndrome. </p>\n<p>Differential diagnosis includes</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Abdominal dissection </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Spinal canal stenosis </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Spinal disc herniation</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Intermittent claudication</td>\n<td>Neurogenic claudication</td>\n<td>osteoarthritis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pain after walking a certain distance</td>\n<td>Pain after walking or standing for some time</td>\n<td>Maximum pain on the first step.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pain quickly relieved on resting.</td>\n<td>Pain relieved on flexion</td>\n<td>Pain relieved on resting.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Aortofemoral grafting</strong> is done in unilateral iliac vessel occlusion.</p>\n<p><strong>Iliofemoral grafting</strong> is done for unilateral occlusion of distal common iliac or external iliac artery.</p>\n<p><strong>Illiopopliteal grafting</strong> is done for femoral artery occlusion.<br style=\"font-weight: 400;\"/><br style=\"font-weight: 400;\"/></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4391","text":"Which of the following is ​not an indication for a c​arotid endarterectomy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an indication for a carotid endarterectomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dysp​honia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amauros​is fugax","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Facial​ palsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemia​nopia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dysphonia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dysphonia</strong></p><p><strong>Dysphonia</strong> is <strong>not</strong> an indication for <strong>carotid endarterectomy</strong>. Carotid endarterectomy is done in carotid artery stenosis.</p>\n<p>Indications of carotid endarterectomy in symptomatic patients.</p>\n<p><strong>CT angiography</strong> showing <strong>70% or more stenosis</strong> and</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ipsilateral <strong>amaurosis fugax</strong> or monocular blindness</li>\n<li>Contralateral <strong>facial paralysis</strong> or paresthesia</li>\n<li><strong>Arm/leg paralysis</strong> or paresthesia</li>\n<li><strong>Hemianopia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Dysphasia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"cE0jlGf1","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"51_kub___adrenal_gland","title":"51.Kub & Adrenal Gland","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB3341","text":"A patient with right flank‌ pain and fever has renal‍ angle tenderness on exam‍ination. Which of the fol​lowing forms the boundari​es of this angle?","raw_text":"A patient with right flank pain and fever has renal angle tenderness on examination. Which of the following forms the boundaries of this angle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lower border of 11th rib a‌nd medial border of erect‍or spinae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lower border of 12th rib a‌nd medial border of erect‍or spinae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lower border of 11th rib a‌nd lateral border of erec‍tor spinae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lower border of 12th rib a‌nd lateral border of erec‍tor spinae","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lower border of 12th rib and lateral border of erector spinae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/421806fa3a82400793ae4b72a1b55337x600x774.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lower border of 12th rib and lateral border of erector spinae</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the renal angle tenderness are suggestive of <strong>acute pyelonephritis</strong>. The <strong>renal angle</strong> is formed by the <strong>lower border </strong>of the<strong> 12th rib</strong> and the <strong>lateral border </strong>of<strong> the erector spinae.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>lower pole</strong> of the kidney occupies this angle. <strong>Tenderness</strong> in this angle is usually suggestive of <strong>infection </strong>in and around the<strong> kidney </strong>or<strong> renal stones.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3342","text":"Identify the angle shown i‌n the image below.","raw_text":"Identify the angle shown in the image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal‌ angle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Costoverte‌bral angle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lumbar‌ angle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyeloverte‌bral angle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Costovertebral angle","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4ca0822cd1ae4e42a7353c59a98b07e9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2f24289536e8421eb1bcc9710bae5a15x600x774.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Costovertebral angle</strong></p><p>The angle shown in the image is the <strong>costovertebral angle, </strong>formed between the <strong>lower border</strong> of the <strong>12<sup>th</sup> rib</strong> and the <strong>vertebral column. </strong></p>\n<p>Causes for pain/tenderness at the costovertebral angle include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nephrolithiasis</li>\n<li>Pelviureteric junction obstruction</li>\n<li>Pyelonephritis</li>\n<li>Hemorrhagic fever with renal syndrome (HFRS) - caused by Hantavirus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3329","text":"Arrange the coverings of t‌he kidney from within out‍wards: 1. Renal fascia 2.‍ Fibrous capsule 3. Paran​ephric fat 4. Perinephric​ fat","raw_text":"<p>Arrange the coverings of the kidney from within outwards:</p>\n<p>1. Renal fascia<br/>2. Fibrous capsule<br/>3. Paranephric fat<br/>4. Perinephric fat</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 - 4 ‌- 2 - 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 - 3 ‌- 2 - 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 - 3 ‌- 1 - 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 - 4 ‌- 1 - 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2 - 4 - 1 - 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/714ffb9a790748cf93738a8c76a7c411x600x484.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2 - 4 - 1 - 3</strong></p><p>The 4 coverings of the kidney from within outwards are as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>True or<strong> fibrous capsule -</strong> formed by the collagen-rich connective tissue.</li>\n<li><strong> Perinephric fat </strong>or adipose capsule -<strong> </strong>consists of fat and occupies the space between the fibrous capsule and the renal fascia.</li>\n<li><strong> Renal fascia <br/></strong></li>\n<li><strong>Paranephric fat - </strong>occupies the space between the renal fascia and the anterior layer of the thoracolumbar fascia.</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3328","text":"Which of the following fas‌cia attaches the perirena‍l fascia to the peritoneu‍m?","raw_text":"Which of the following fascia attaches the perirenal fascia to the peritoneum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gerota'‌s fascia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Laterocon‌al fascia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thoracolum‌bar fascia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Treitz'‌s fascia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lateroconal fascia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b2b0fcbc50924bb98f95f74a8f50bf52x600x484.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lateroconal fascia</strong></p><p>The <strong>lateroconal fascia </strong>attaches the <strong>perirenal fascia</strong> to the<strong> peritoneum. </strong>It extends from the lateral margin of perirenal fascia and attaches to the parietal peritoneum.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Gerota's fascia </strong>is the <strong>perirenal fascia</strong>. The kidney and the suprarenal gland are enclosed within anterior and posterior lamina of the perirenal fascia. The anterior lamina of the perirenal fascia blends with the connective tissue surrounding the great vessels behind the duodenum and pancreas. The <strong>posterior lamina</strong> continues as the <strong>lateroconal fascia.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Thoracolumbar fascia </strong>is a complex arrangement of multiple fascial layers in the thoracolumbar region. This fascia encloses the paraspinal muscles, quadratus lumborum muscle and the lattisimus dorsi muscle.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Treitz's fascia</strong> has been used to refer to the <strong>fusion fascia</strong> behind the <strong>head</strong> <strong>of pancreas.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3323","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures does not form an ‍anterior relationship wit‍h the marked structure?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures does not form an anterior relationship with the marked structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Descending part of the duo‌denum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pylorus of ‌the stomach","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Coils of sma‌ll intestines","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adrena‌l gland","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pylorus of the stomach","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/27294bb42f704585bca4b0c38d75180f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf3f558c11af4f649ef679271fb50355x600x519.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f1d30e78ee244244aca08f1d68aa4fabx1280x1519.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pylorus of the stomach</strong></p><p>The marked structure in the image is the <strong>right kidney. </strong>The<strong> p</strong><strong>ylorus </strong>of the<strong> stomach</strong> is <strong>not</strong> related to the <strong>anterior</strong> surface of the right kidney.</p>\n<p>The anterior relations of the right kidney:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 94px; width: 391.734375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\"><strong>Intraperitoneal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 210.734375px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Right lobe of liver</li>\n<li>Small intestine</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 169px;\"><strong>Retroperitoneal </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 210.734375px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Right suprarenal gland</li>\n<li>2<sup>nd</sup> part of duoednum  </li>\n<li>Hepatic flexure of colon </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> The below image shows the cross-section of the abdomen at the level of kidney.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3326","text":"Which of the following are‌as of the left kidney is ‍separated from its relati‍on by a peritoneal layer?​","raw_text":"Which of the following areas of the left kidney is separated from its relation by a peritoneal layer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/49a81ab215d847379f4f9273e8abfcf1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dfd6f153a1b94018b177e99235dd6703x600x570.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b4a4120b4844c8f86ca4626d98277f9x1280x1519.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2</strong></p><p>The anterior surface of the left kidney in relation with the <strong>jejunal loops </strong>(marked as 2)<strong> </strong>is separated from the structure by a peritoneal layer.</p>\n<p>As the kidneys are located retroperitoneally, <strong>intraperitoneal</strong> <strong>anterior relations</strong> are <strong>separated</strong> from it by a <strong>peritoneal layer.</strong> </p>\n<p>The<strong> anterior relations </strong>of the<strong> left kidney </strong>include:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 227px; width: 414px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 52.546875px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 140.859375px; height: 52.546875px;\"><strong>Intraperitoneal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.140625px; height: 52.546875px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Stomach</li>\n<li>Jejunum</li>\n<li>Spleen</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 140.859375px; height: 33px;\"> <strong>Retroperitoneal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.140625px; height: 33px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Left suprarenal gland</li>\n<li>Pancreas</li>\n<li>Splenic vessels</li>\n<li>Splenic flexure and beginning of descending colon</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0110","text":"Which of the following is ‌related to the marked are‍a in the image?","raw_text":"Which of the following is related to the marked area in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sto‌mach","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Spl‌een","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2nd part o‌f duodenum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Panc‌reas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Stomach","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/129ede002d9d46aeb510e026f1a928cc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/884744e76487412b95366e3868caa73bx1280x1519.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e77420c4a6e74d7ab3e87ba4283f6625x468x350.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Stomach</strong></p><p>The marked area in the image of the <strong>left kidney</strong> is related to the <strong>stomach.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>anterior relations </strong>of the <strong>right kidney</strong>:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 412.953125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\"><strong>Intraperitoneal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 272.953125px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Right lobe of liver</li>\n<li>Small intestine</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 128px;\"><strong>Retroperitoneal </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 272.953125px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Right suprarenal gland</li>\n<li>2<sup>nd</sup> part of duoednum  </li>\n<li>Hepatic flexure of colon </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The<strong> anterior relations </strong>of the<strong> left kidney</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<table style=\"width: 411px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.984375px;\"><strong>Intraperitoneal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.015625px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Stomach</li>\n<li>Jejunum</li>\n<li>Spleen</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 138.984375px;\"> <strong>Retroperitoneal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 259.015625px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Left suprarenal gland</li>\n<li>Pancreas</li>\n<li>Splenic vessels</li>\n<li>Splenic flexure and beginning of descending colon</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">The<strong> posterior relations </strong>of the<strong> kidneys:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>One vessel - subcostal vessels</li>\n<li>Two ligaments -<strong> </strong>medial and lateral arcuate ligaments</li>\n<li>Three nerves -<strong> </strong>subcostal, iliohypogastric, and ilioinguinal nerves</li>\n<li>Four muscles -<strong> </strong>diaphragm, psoas major, quadratus lumborum, and transverse abdominis.</li>\n<li>The right kidney is related to the 12<sup>th</sup> rib and the left kidney is related to the 11<sup>th</sup> and 12<sup>th</sup> ribs</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3327","text":"Which of the following are‌as of the anterior surfac‍e of the right kidney is ‍separated by a layer of p​eritoneum?","raw_text":"Which of the following areas of the anterior surface of the right kidney is separated by a layer of peritoneum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/12a69703a3b94fc4821cfcaf8c6ce921.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9cef98d772e94651aa56ab5dc840466dx600x515.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/983cbe0dad14472db423339563396033x1280x1519.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1</strong></p><p>The anterior surface of the right kidney related to the<strong> right lobe </strong>of the<strong> liver </strong>(marked as 1) is separated by a layer of <strong>peritoneum.</strong></p>\n<p>As the kidneys are located retroperitoneally, <strong>intraperitoneal</strong> <strong>anterior relations</strong> are <strong>separated</strong> from it by a <strong>peritoneal layer.</strong> </p>\n<p>The anterior relations of the right kidney:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Intraperitoneal</strong></td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Right lobe of liver</li>\n<li>Small intestine</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Retroperitoneal </strong></td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Right suprarenal gland</li>\n<li>2<sup>nd</sup> part of duoednum  </li>\n<li>Hepatic flexure of colon </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3338","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a posterior relation ‍of the marked structure?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a posterior relation of the marked structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Psoas‌ major","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medial arcu‌ate ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sympathe‌tic chain","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ilioingui‌nal nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sympathetic chain","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dfe82f90e90f41f892f3d4feb8c4b9de.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7197d0ae29cb4bd09e30e22f2298a5cax600x519.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c01d9193f2ce4be2bc845979052e6700x1279x956.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sympathetic chain</strong></p><p>The marked structure in the given image is the<strong> left kidney. </strong>The sympathetic chain is not a posterior relation of the left kidney.</p>\n<p>The<strong> posterior relations</strong> of the kidneys are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>One vessel - subcostal vessels</li>\n<li>Two ligaments -<strong> </strong>medial and lateral arcuate ligaments</li>\n<li>Three nerves -<strong> </strong>subcostal, iliohypogastric, and ilioinguinal nerves</li>\n<li>Four muscles -<strong> </strong>diaphragm, psoas major, quadratus lumborum, and transverse abdominis.</li>\n<li>The right kidney is related to the 12<sup>th</sup> rib and the left kidney is related to the 11<sup>th</sup> and 12<sup>th</sup> ribs</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below image shows the cross section of the abdomen at the level of kidney.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3345","text":"What is the correct arrang‌ement of the structures i‍n the marked area from an‍terior to posterior?","raw_text":"What is the correct arrangement of the structures in the marked area from anterior to posterior?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal artery, renal vein, ‌renal pelvis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Renal vein, renal artery, ‌renal pelvis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Renal pelvis, renal artery‌, renal vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Renal vein, renal pelvis, ‌renal artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Renal vein, renal artery, renal pelvis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b79ea64a946d4570b4b2894b52aabe5e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e9a872a4405c43b5a7fa71a072ddceeex600x938.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Renal vein, renal artery, renal pelvis</strong></p><p>The marked area is the <strong>hilum</strong> of the <strong>kidney</strong>. It contains the following structures from anterior to posterior:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Renal vein</li>\n<li>Renal artery</li>\n<li>Renal pelvis</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3343","text":"What is the structure into‌ which the renal papillae‍ open?","raw_text":"What is the structure into which the renal papillae open?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cor‌tex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pyr‌amid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Minor ‌calyces","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Major ‌calyces","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Minor calyces","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ef84781aa5344c8ba1f5f505a0f8b12dx600x668.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Minor calyces</strong></p><p>The<strong> renal papillae</strong> open into the <strong>minor calyces.</strong></p>\n<p>The papilla is the conical apex of a renal pyramid, where<strong> </strong>the urine from the collecting ducts enters into the minor calyces. The<strong> minor calyces</strong> unite to form the <strong>major calyces,</strong> which further unite to form the <strong>renal pelvis.</strong></p>\n<p>The kidney is divided into an <strong>outer </strong><strong>cortex</strong> and an <strong>inner medulla. </strong>The renal medulla contains many striated and conical pyramids. The <strong>base</strong> of the pyramid is <strong>peripherally located</strong>, whereas the <strong>apex</strong> is directed towards the <strong>renal sinus </strong>(hilum)<strong>. </strong>The renal cortex is subcapsular and arches over the bases of the pyramids. It extends between the pyramids towards the renal sinus as renal columns.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3344","text":"Which of the following con‌tributes to the nerve sup‍ply of the kidney?","raw_text":"Which of the following contributes to the nerve supply of the kidney?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inferior hypogastric plexu‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coeliac‌ plexus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior mesenteric plexus‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superior hypogastric plexu‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Coeliac plexus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0ebd6bfd0d2644c3ab3c79448f4c7253x600x906.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Coeliac plexus</strong></p><p>The<strong> coeliac plexus</strong> contributes to the <strong>nerve supply</strong> of the <strong>kidneys</strong>.</p>\n<p>Nerve supply of the kidney:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Coeliac ganglion and plexus</li>\n<li>Aorticorenal ganglion</li>\n<li>Lowest thoracic splanchnic nerve</li>\n<li>First lumbar splanchnic nerve</li>\n<li>Aortic plexus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The rami from these structures form a dense network of<strong> autonomic plexus</strong> around the renal artery called the <strong>renal plexus. </strong>They control the blood flow between the cortex and medulla.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3339","text":"A 20-year-old man presente‌d with left flank pain, v‍aricocele, hematuria, and‍ headache. On examination​, BP was 170/100 mm Hg. C​T abdomen was done and a ‌diagnosis of Nutcracker s‍yndrome was made. What is​ the cause?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old man presented with left flank pain, varicocele, hematuria, and headache. On examination, BP was 170/100 mm Hg. CT abdomen was done and a diagnosis of Nutcracker syndrome was made. What is the cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Compression of left renal ‌vein between abdominal ao‍rta and superior mesenter‍ic artery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Compression of left renal ‌vein between inferior ven‍a cava and superior mesen‍teric vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Compression of right renal‌ vein between abdominal a‍orta and superior mesente‍ric artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Compression of right renal‌ vein between inferior ve‍na cava and superior mese‍nteric vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Compression of left renal vein between abdominal aorta and superior mesenteric artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b375d815aea64c81b19dedaf83cc780bx600x810.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1a756b01eafa4bb893905893555a7bc9x1280x2009.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Compression of left renal vein between abdominal aorta and superior mesenteric artery</strong></p><p>The most likely cause is <strong>compression</strong> of the <strong>left renal vein</strong> between the <strong>abdominal aorta</strong> and <strong>superior mesenteric artery</strong>. This manifests as <strong>nutcracker syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Nutcracker syndrome is<strong> </strong>characterized by left renal vein<strong> hypertension, </strong>which<strong> </strong>occurs due to the <strong>compression</strong> of the <strong>left renal vein</strong> between the <strong>abdominal aorta</strong> and the <strong>superior mesenteric artery. </strong>This is because the left renal vein is posterior and inferior to the superior mesenteric artery.</p>\n<p>The left renal vein is longer than the right renal vein. It runs<strong> anterior</strong> to the <strong>aorta</strong> just below the origin of the superior mesenteric artery and drains into the inferior vena cava after crossing the midline.</p>\n<p>Note: The<strong> left kidney</strong> is <strong>preferred</strong> in <strong>donor nephrectomy</strong> than the right because of the long course of the left renal vein.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3346","text":"A 56-year-old male was bro‌ught to the emergency dep‍artment with severe pain ‍in the left flank region.​ CT-examination revealed ​occlusion of the left ren‌al vein. The condition wo‍uld impede the blood flow​ through all of the follo‌wing veins except:","raw_text":"A 56-year-old male was brought to the emergency department with severe pain in the left flank region. CT-examination revealed occlusion of the left renal vein. The condition would impede the blood flow through all of the following veins except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left adr‌enal vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left testi‌cular vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left diaphr‌agmatic vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left subc‌ostal vein","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Left subcostal vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e6185824b9034135b8ce40b2f838e2cdx600x632.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Left subcostal vein</strong></p><p>The <strong>left subcostal vein</strong> does not drain into the left renal vein. It drains into the <strong>hemiazygos vein. </strong>So the occlusion of the left renal vein does not impede the blood flow in the left subcostal vein.</p>\n<p>Veins that commonly <strong>drain </strong>into the<strong> left renal vein</strong> (which in turn drains into the IVC) are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Left inferior phrenic (diaphragmatic) vein,</li>\n<li>Left suprarenal (adrenal) vein,</li>\n<li>Left gonadal vein</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On the right side, all the above veins drain into the inferior vena cava directly.</p>\n<p>Apart from the veins described above, the<strong> first </strong>and<strong> second left lumbar veins</strong> and<strong> hemiazygos vein </strong>may drain into the<strong> left renal vein.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3347","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures does not cross th‍e midline?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures does not cross the midline?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left gon‌adal vein","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Left re‌nal vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Accessory hemiazygous vein‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Root of th‌e mesentery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Left gonadal vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Left gonadal vein</strong></p><p>The <strong>left gonadal vein</strong> does not cross the midline. It is formed from the pampiniform plexus of veins and drains into the <strong>left renal vein</strong>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> left renal vein </strong>is<strong> longer</strong> than the right renal vein and it <strong>crosses </strong>the<strong> midline</strong> anterior to the aorta. It drains directly into the inferior vena cava. The right renal vein does not cross the midline.</p>\n<p>The <strong>accessory hemiazygous vein</strong> at T7 vertebral level <strong>crosses </strong>the<strong> midline</strong> to drain into the <strong>azygous vein.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>root </strong>of the<strong> mesentery </strong>extends across a line from the duodenojejunal flexure on the left side to the sacroiliac joint on the right side. This spans a length of 15 cm and <strong>crosses </strong>the<strong> midline</strong> in its course.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3348","text":"A 42-year-old man presente‌d with severe pain in the‍ lower back radiating to ‍the pubic symphysis. Ultr​asonography revealed a st​one in the left ureter, m‌easuring 3 mm in size. Th‍e stone is most likely to​ be lodged in which of th‌e following sites?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old man presented with severe pain in the lower back radiating to the pubic symphysis. Ultrasonography revealed a stone in the left ureter, measuring 3 mm in size. The stone is most likely to be lodged in which of the following sites?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pelvi-urete‌ric junction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"At pelv‌ic brim","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vesicourete‌ric junction","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"At vas ‌deferens","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vesicoureteric junction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d874fe7b4e9b4f3494a1f18bc13f2b9fx1280x1761.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vesicoureteric junction</strong></p><p><strong>Vesicoureteric junction</strong> is the <strong>narrowest</strong> part of the <strong>ureter</strong>. This is the reason why stones as small as 2 to 3 mm get arrested in the vesicoureteric junction.</p>\n<p>The ureter is constricted at 5 places:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Pelvi-ureteric junction</li>\n<li>Brim of the lesser pelvis (as it crosses the common iliac artery)</li>\n<li>Point of crossing of the ureter by ductus deferens or broad ligament of the uterus.</li>\n<li>Vesico-ureteric junction</li>\n<li>Opening in the lateral angle of the trigone</li>\n</ol>\n<p>These are regarded as <strong>surgical constrictions</strong> of the ureter.</p>\n<p>However, only <strong>1, 2, </strong>and<strong> 4 are </strong>the <strong>anatomical constrictions </strong>of the ureter.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3222","text":"Which of the following str‌ucture is not a posterior‍ relation of the structur‍e marked as X?","raw_text":"Which of the following structure is not a posterior relation of the structure marked as X?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Common il‌iac artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Quadratus‌ lumborum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Psoas‌ major","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Genitofem‌oral nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Quadratus lumborum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6623c4c2700e40acb1935696367ff23b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf3517944fb84114941c4e88f6d3f89cx1280x1747.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Quadratus lumborum</strong></p><p>The structure marked in the image is<strong> </strong>the <strong>ureter. Quadratus lumborum </strong>is not a posterior relation of the ureter.</p>\n<p><strong>Posterior relations </strong>of the<strong> ureter </strong>in the abdominal cavity are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Psoas major</li>\n<li>Genitofemoral nerve</li>\n<li>End of the common iliac artery or beginning of the external iliac artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3349","text":"Which of the following art‌eries does not supply the‍ ureters?","raw_text":"Which of the following arteries does not supply the ureters?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uterine‌ artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gonadal‌ artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Common Il‌iac artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superior mesenteric artery‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Superior mesenteric artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0bcf2e46a47b462a988e4252f21c06bex1280x1501.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Superior mesenteric artery</strong></p><p>The superior mesenteric artery does not supply the ureters.</p>\n<p>The <strong>ureter</strong> receives its <strong>blood supply</strong> from the following arteries:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Renal artery</li>\n<li>Gonadal artery</li>\n<li>Common iliac artery</li>\n<li>Internal iliac artery</li>\n<li>Vesical artery</li>\n<li>Uterine artery</li>\n<li>Abdominal aorta</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Among these branches, the branch from the <strong>renal artery</strong> and the branch from the <strong>inferior vesical artery</strong> are always<strong> constant.</strong></p>\n<p>Clinical note: The<strong> branch </strong>of the<strong> renal artery</strong> that supplies the<strong> ureter</strong> must be carefully<strong> preserved </strong>in<strong> renal transplantation</strong> to maintain good vascularity of the ureters.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3350","text":"Which of the following art‌eries does not supply the‍ marked structure?","raw_text":"Which of the following arteries does not supply the marked structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abdomin‌al aorta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gonadal‌ artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior ph‌renic artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superior mesenteric artery‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Superior mesenteric artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/17db1125f4644dcd9d9b19655e7b66be.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9bdcb775f0bb40c6ae59758c9b2dc391x600x847.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/60eea7f7a00e46fd9d273630a2c7d425x600x571.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Superior mesenteric artery</strong></p><p>The structure marked in the CT abdomen is the left <strong>adrenal gland</strong>. The superior mesenteric artery does not supply the suprarenal or adrenal gland.</p>\n<p>The adrenal gland receives its arterial supply from the <strong>superior, middle, and inferior suprarenal arteries.</strong> These arteries have some variations in their origin as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 261px; width: 729px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 222.9375px;\"><strong>Artery</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 237.0625px;\"><strong>Usual origin</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 255px;\">\n<p><strong>Variation in origin</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 222.9375px;\"><strong>Superior suprarenal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 237.0625px;\">Inferior phrenic artery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Abdominal aorta</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 222.9375px;\"><strong>Middle suprarenal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 237.0625px;\">Abdominal aorta (as lateral branches)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Inferior phrenic artery</li>\n<li>Renal artery</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 222.9375px;\">\n<p><strong>Inferior suprarenal (major blood supply)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 237.0625px;\">Renal artery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 255px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Abdominal aorta</li>\n<li>Gonadal artery</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>The below CT abdomen shows the right and left adrenal gland.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3351","text":"Where does the right main ‌adrenal vein drain into?","raw_text":"Where does the right main adrenal vein drain into?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right re‌nal vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior ‌vena cava","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Second right‌ lumbar vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right gon‌adal vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inferior vena cava","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ca260d2a9b3c4c0fa3989ac61496fd94x600x632.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inferior vena cava</strong></p><p>The <strong>right main adrenal vein</strong> usually drains into the <strong>inferior vena cava.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>left main adrenal vein</strong> usually drains into the <strong>left renal vein. </strong>Variations are common on the right side, where the right adrenal vein may drain into the right inferior phrenic vein.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3228","text":"During the cystoscopic eva‌luation of a patient with‍ hematuria, where will yo‍u find the Mercier's bar?​","raw_text":"During the cystoscopic evaluation of a patient with hematuria, where will you find the Mercier's bar?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Between two ureteric orifi‌ces","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Between uvula vesicae and ‌ureteric orifice","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Between verumontanum and u‌reteric orifice","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Between internal and exter‌nal urethral orifice","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Between two ureteric orifices","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5b6bf5ed57bc42c2a9faa71534e08aafx1280x1256.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Between two ureteric orifices</strong></p><p>The<strong> Mercier's bar </strong>(interureteric bar or interureteric ridge)<strong> </strong>is a curved fold of mucous membrane <strong>between </strong>the<strong> two ureteric orifices</strong> inside the bladder.</p>\n<p>This fold of mucous membrane is formed by the <strong>thickening </strong>of the<strong> superior border </strong>of the<strong> trigone </strong>muscle<strong>. </strong>In <strong>cystoscopy</strong>, the interureteric ridge appears as a pale band and acts as a <strong>guide </strong>to the<strong> ureteric orifices.</strong></p>\n<p>The mucosal fold running from the <strong>uvula vesicae </strong>to the<strong> ureteric orifice</strong> is called the <strong>Bell's bar. </strong>This fold forms the lateral borders of the trigone.<strong> </strong>It contains a band of muscle fibres called the <strong>Bell's muscle.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7397","text":"Which of the following lob‌es of the prostate forms ‍the uvula vesicae?","raw_text":"Which of the following lobes of the prostate forms the uvula vesicae?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ante‌rior","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Post‌erior","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Med‌ian","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lat‌eral","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Median","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f9df0b04752a4005b3927669603c7a25x1279x1256.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Median</strong></p><p><strong>The median lobe </strong>of the prostate forms the uvula vesicae.</p>\n<p><strong>Uvula vesicae</strong> is <strong>an elevation in the mucous membrane</strong> of the urinary bladder, behind the internal urethral orifice. It is often enlarged in benign prostatic hypertrophy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3225","text":"Where does the internal ur‌ethral orifice lie at bir‍th?","raw_text":"Where does the internal urethral orifice lie at birth?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"At the level of the umblic‌us","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"At the level of the lower ‌border of symphysis pubis‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"At the level of the perine‌al body","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"At the level of the upper ‌border of symphysis pubis‍","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. At the level of the upper border of symphysis pubis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. At the level of the upper border of symphysis pubis</strong></p><p>The<strong> internal urethral orifice </strong>lies at the level of the <strong>upper border </strong>of the<strong> pubic symphysis</strong> at <strong>birth.</strong></p>\n<p>At birth, the bladder is an abdominal organ. But as the child grows, the blader descends, and near puberty, it becomes a pelvic organ.</p>\n<p>In an <strong>adult</strong>, the internal urethral orifice lies 3–4 cm behind the<strong> lower part </strong>of the<strong> pubic symphysis. </strong>It corresponds to the bladder neck.</p>\n<p>Clinical note: As the bladder lies above the symphysis pubis, we can <strong>safely</strong> perform <strong>suprapubic</strong> needle punctures to obtain <strong>urine samples</strong> in <strong>children. </strong>But in adults, this can only be done if the bladder is fully distended. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3352","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures is innervated by ‍the neurons present in On‍uf's nucleus?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is innervated by the neurons present in Onuf's nucleus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Detruso‌r muscle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tri‌gone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Internal urethral sphincte‌r","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"External urethral sphincte‌r","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. External urethral sphincter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3ef9348ce30b49a381b7b9450f07caf7x600x494.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dac2de236e6b419f8f6305f8e45c6930x1280x1635.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. External urethral sphincter</strong></p><p>The<strong> external urethral sphincter</strong> is innervated by the neurons present in <strong>O</strong><strong>nuf's nucleus.</strong></p>\n<p>The neurons present in the <strong>lamina IX</strong> of the grey matter in <strong>spinal segments S2, S3, and S4</strong> constitute Onuf's nucleus.<strong> </strong>These neurons <strong>contract</strong> the <strong>external urethral sphincter</strong>.</p>\n<p>The external urethral sphincter is made up of <strong>skeletal muscle fibers.</strong> In the contracted state, they are responsible for the <strong>maintenance of urinary continence.</strong> Just before the onset of voiding the sphincter is relaxed. This happens because the neurons in the Onuf's nucleus are centrally inhibited by the brain.</p>\n<p>The below image shows Onuf's nucleus located in the lamina IX.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Nerve supply of the bladder:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 160px;\" width=\"1040\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 512px;\"><strong>Parasympathetic supply</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 512px;\"><strong>Sympathetic supply</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 512px;\">From S2, S3, and S4 spinal segments</td>\n<td style=\"width: 512px;\">From T10, T11, T12, L1, and L2 spinal segments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 512px;\">\n<p>Richly innervate the fundus of the bladder</p>\n<p>Sparsely innervate the bladder neck</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 512px;\">\n<p>Richly innervate the smooth muscles around the bladder neck</p>\n<p>Sparsely innervate the fundus of the bladder</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 512px;\">They contract the urinary bladder and aids in the micturition</td>\n<td style=\"width: 512px;\">They contract the internal urethral sphincter and aids in the filling of the urinary bladder</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0561","text":"What is the number of arte‌rial segments in the kidn‍ey?","raw_text":"What is the number of arterial segments in the kidney?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1d1710f29e1e446d87961c108c22918ax600x685.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 5</strong></p><p>There are <strong>five arterial segments</strong> in the kidney:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Apical</li>\n<li>Superior</li>\n<li>Middle</li>\n<li>Inferior</li>\n<li>Posterior</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Blood supply of the kidney: The <strong>renal artery</strong> is a direct lateral branch of the <strong>abdominal aorta.</strong> Near the hilum of the kidney, the renal artery is divided into anterior and posterior <strong>divisions.</strong> These divisions are further divided into <strong>five</strong> <strong>segmental arteries</strong> (four anterior and one posterior). The segmental arteries further branch out in a hierarchy to form different arteries. Finally, the renal vein is formed which joins the inferior vena cava.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"QdXFov7p","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"51_healthcare_in_india","title":"51.Healthcare In India","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA3070","text":"Which of the following is ‌not included in the secon​dary level of the healthc‍are system?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included in the secondary level of the healthcare system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Community he‌alth centres","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medical ‌colleges","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"District ‌hospitals","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sub-distric‌t hospitals","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Medical colleges","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Medical colleges</strong></p><p><strong>Medical colleges</strong> are included in the <strong>tertiary</strong> level of the health care system, which is also the second referral centre.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3062","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an element of primary​ healthcare?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an element of primary healthcare?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prevention of locally ende‌mic diseases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Safe water supply and sani‌tation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Providing physical rehabil‌itation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Providing es‌sential drugs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Providing physical rehabilitation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Providing physical rehabilitation</strong></p><p>Providing physical rehabilitation is not an element of primary health care. Primary health centers (<strong>PHCs</strong>) are only responsible for the popularization and <strong>mainstreaming</strong> of <strong>PMR</strong> in the community.</p>\n<p><strong>Primary health care</strong> refers to <strong>essential</strong> health care made <strong>universally</strong> available to individuals and <strong>acceptable</strong> to them, through their full participation and at a cost the community and country can afford.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3065","text":"All of the following are p‌illars of primary health ​care except:","raw_text":"All of the following are pillars of primary health care except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Health e‌ducation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Intersectoral coordination‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Appropriate‌ technology","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Equitable d‌istribution","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Health education","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Health education</strong></p><p><strong>Health education</strong> is an <strong>element</strong> of primary health care, not a pillar.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3073","text":"Which of the following sta‌tement is false regarding​ ASHA workers?","raw_text":"Which of the following statement is false regarding ASHA workers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Few of their responsibilit‌ies are integrated with A​nganwadi workers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"They should have formal ed‌ucation at least up to cl​ass 10","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A population of 1000 shoul‌d have at least 1 ASHA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They have to procure ORS, ‌OCPs, IFA tablets from CH​C","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. They have to procure ORS, OCPs, IFA tablets from CHC","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. They have to procure ORS, OCPs, IFA tablets from CHC</strong></p><p>Accredited social health activist (<strong>ASHA</strong>) workers do not need to procure supplies from CHCs. They only act as <strong>depot holders</strong> for certain essential <strong>supplies</strong> so that they are readily available to their community.</p>\n<p>These essential supplies may include ORS, contraceptives, iron-folic acid tablets, chloroquine, disposable delivery kits, oral contraceptive pills, condoms, etc. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3076","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect regarding Angan​wadi workers?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding Anganwadi workers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"One AWW serves a populatio‌n of 5000","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"They are appointed under t‌he ICDS scheme","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"They link the community wi‌th the health services","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Their responsibilities inc‌lude counsel for immuniza​tion, health education, a‍nd nutrition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. One AWW serves a population of 5000","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. One AWW serves a population of 5000</strong></p><p>The norm is one Anganwadi worker (<strong>AWW</strong>) per <strong>400-800 population</strong>.</p>\n<p>Anganwadi workers (AWW) are allotted under the integrated child development services (<strong>ICDS</strong>). AWW are selected from the community they are to serve and act as <strong>links</strong> between the <strong>community</strong> and <strong>health services</strong>. They are mostly part-time workers.</p>\n<p>Responsibilities of an AWW include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Health checkups and growth chart maintenance</li>\n<li>Counsel women on birth preparedness, importance of safe delivery, <strong>immunization</strong> and contraception and prevention of common infections including STD and RTIs.</li>\n<li><strong>Nutrition</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Health education</strong></li>\n<li>Non-formal school education</li>\n<li>Referral services</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3067","text":"One male health assistant ‌is posted for a populatio​n of:","raw_text":"One male health assistant is posted for a population of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10‌00","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"50‌00","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10,‌000","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30,‌000","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 30,000","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 30,000</strong></p><p><strong>One</strong> male and female <strong>health assistant</strong> are required for a population of <strong>30,000 in plain</strong> ar<strong>eas</strong> and of <strong>20,000</strong> in hilly or <strong>remote</strong> areas.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5169","text":"How many people does a Mah‌ila Arogya Samiti member ​cater to?","raw_text":"How many people does a Mahila Arogya Samiti member cater to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50-‌100","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"100‌-200","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"250‌-500","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"500-‌1000","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 250-500","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 250-500</strong></p><p>1 Mahila Arogya Samiti member is appointed for a <strong>population</strong> of <strong>250-500</strong> (covering about <strong>50-100 households</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3097","text":"Which of the following dut‌ies would be performed by​ a male health worker? Ch‍lorination of water bodie‍s Maintenance of eligible​ couple registers Distrib​ution of conventional con‍traceptives Universal Imm​unization Program Reprodu​ctive and child health se‍rvices","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following duties would be performed by a male health worker?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Chlorination of water bodies</li>\n<li>Maintenance of eligible couple registers</li>\n<li>Distribution of conventional contraceptives</li>\n<li>Universal Immunization Program</li>\n<li>Reproductive and child health services</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌,4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,‌3,4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,‌3,5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2‌,5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1,3,5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1,3,5</strong></p><p>The duties of a <strong>male health worker</strong> include <strong>chlorination</strong> of water bodies, distribution of conventional <strong>contraceptives</strong>, and <strong>reproductive and child health</strong> services.</p>\n<p>Maintanence of eligible couple registers (part of family planning activities) and immunization are duties of female health workers.</p>\n<p>Health workers (male and female) are 1 per 5000 in plains and 1 per 3000 in remote or hilly areas.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3091","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a duty of a female he​alth worker?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a duty of a female health worker?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DOTS ac‌tivities","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Malaria su‌rveillance","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Distributio‌n of condoms","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Immuni‌zation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Malaria surveillance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Malaria surveillance</strong></p><p><strong>Malaria surveillance</strong> activities, in accordance with the National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme, are the duty of a <strong>male health worker</strong>.</p>\n<p>Health workers (male and female) are 1 per 5000 in plains and 1 per 3000 in remote or hilly areas.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3098","text":"Which of the following are‌ not involved in providin​g antenatal support to pr‍egnant women?","raw_text":"Which of the following are not involved in providing antenatal support to pregnant women?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Female hea‌lth workers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trained birt‌h attendants","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Female healt‌h assistants","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anganwad‌i workers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trained birth attendants","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trained birth attendants</strong></p><p><strong>Trained birth attendants</strong> or dais provide medical services during <strong>delivery </strong>and in the <strong>post-partum</strong> period. They cater to a population of 1000 in villages.</p>\n<p><strong>Antenatal support</strong> is provided by the following: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Health workers</strong> (male and female) - 1 per 5000 in plains and 1 per 3000 in hilly or remote areas.</li>\n<li><strong>Health assistants</strong> (male and female) - 1 per 30,000 in plains and 1 per 20, 000 in remote or hilly areas.</li>\n<li><strong>Anganwadi workers</strong> - 1 per 400-800 in plains and 1 per 300-800 in hilly or remote areas.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3081","text":"How many people does one s‌ub-center in a hilly or r​emote area cater to?","raw_text":"How many people does one sub-center in a hilly or remote area cater to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"70‌00","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10,‌000","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"30,‌000","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30‌00","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 3000","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 3000</strong></p><p>A <strong>sub-center</strong> caters to a population of <strong>5000</strong> in<strong> general</strong> areas and <strong>3000</strong> in hilly or <strong>remote</strong> areas.</p>\n<p>Services provided at sub-centers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maternal and child health, family planning</li>\n<li>Counseling for abortion and adolescent health care</li>\n<li>Water quality monitoring and sanitation promotion</li>\n<li>Assistance to school health services</li>\n<li>Field visits and treating minor ailments</li>\n<li>Training of ASHA and birth attendants</li>\n<li>Disease surveillance and National Health Programme functions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3094","text":"What should be the maximum‌ number of female health ​workers at a sub-center?","raw_text":"What should be the maximum number of female health workers at a sub-center?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No‌ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2</strong></p><p><strong>Two female</strong> health workers are to be at a <strong>sub-center</strong> as proposed by the Indian Public Health Standards (IPHS).</p>\n<p>There are two types of sub-centers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type A</strong> -<strong> </strong>It has all facilities except for conducting deliveries. It can be upgraded to Type B.</li>\n<li><strong>Type B </strong>- Also called <strong>MCH</strong> sub-centers. It has delivery facilities.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Indian Public Health Standards (IPHS) norms for manpower at sub-centers:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 186px; width: 413px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">Type of sub-center</td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 148px;\">Sub-centre A </td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 156px;\">Sub-centre B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">Staff</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">Essential</td>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\">Desirable</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">Essential</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px;\">Desirable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">ANM/Health worker (female)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\">+1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">Health worker(male)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">Staff nurse (or ANM, if staff nurse is not available)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px;\">1**</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">Safai karamchari*</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81px;\">1(part time)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 67px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 82px;\">1(full time)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>*To be outsourced </p>\n<p>**If the number of deliveries ≥2</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3083","text":"As a PHC medical officer, ‌you would be responsible ​for all of the following ‍activities except:","raw_text":"As a PHC medical officer, you would be responsible for all of the following activities except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mainstreaming AYUSH and PM‌R","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Performing selected surgic‌al procedures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Formal education of childr‌en &amp;lt; 6 years of age","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Collection and recording o‌f vital events","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Formal education of children < 6 years of age","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Formal education of children &lt; 6 years of age</strong></p><p>Formal education is not a function of<strong> primary health centers</strong> in India.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3085","text":"Which of the following cat‌egory of workers are not ​part of the workforce at ‍PHCs?","raw_text":"Which of the following category of workers are not part of the workforce at PHCs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Accountant cum data entry ‌operator","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Radiog‌raphers","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Health ‌Educator","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cle‌rks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Radiographers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Radiographers</strong></p><p>A <strong>radiographer</strong> is not a part of the manpower at a PHC. They are present in <strong>CHCs</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Accountants</strong> cum data entry operators, <strong>health educators</strong>, and <strong>clerks</strong> are appointed at <strong>PHCs</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3088","text":"What is the minimum number‌ of beds a HWC-PHC is req​uired to have?","raw_text":"What is the minimum number of beds a HWC-PHC is required to have?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2</strong></p><p>The <strong>minimum number</strong> of beds a HWC-PHC is required to have<strong> </strong>is <strong>2. </strong>Per the IPHS 2022 guidelines, the desirable number of beds in a HWC-PHC is 4. </p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: The desirable beds will be over and above the essential beds.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3086","text":"One community health cente‌r (CHC) covers a populati​on of:","raw_text":"One community health center (CHC) covers a population of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30,000 t‌o 50,000","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"40,000 t‌o 60,000","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"80,000 to‌ 1,20,000","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1,00,000 t‌o 2,00,000","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 80,000 to 1,20,000","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 80,000 to 1,20,000</strong></p><p>Each community (CHC) covers a population of <strong>80,000-1,20,000.</strong></p>\n<p>Services provided by CHC:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Care of routine and emergency cases in medicine and surgery</li>\n<li>Maternal, newborn, and child health</li>\n<li>Family planning</li>\n<li>All the functions of national health programmes</li>\n<li>Physical medicine and rehabilitation</li>\n<li>Oral health</li>\n<li>School and adolescent health services</li>\n<li>Blood storage facility</li>\n<li>Diagnostic services</li>\n<li>Referral services</li>\n<li>Maternal death review (MDR)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3103","text":"What is the minimum number‌ of beds required in a co​mmunity health center?","raw_text":"What is the minimum number of beds required in a community health center?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3‌4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3‌0","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2‌0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 30","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 30</strong></p><p>The minimum number of <strong>beds</strong> in a <strong>CHC</strong> is <strong>30</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following manpower must be maintained at a CHC: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 144px; width: 567px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 277px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Personnel and Strength at CHCs</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 277px;\">Medical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 353.015625px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>General duty MO (6 - at least 2 female doctors)</li>\n<li>Public health manager (1)</li>\n<li>General surgeon (1)</li>\n<li>Physician (1)</li>\n<li>OBG (1)</li>\n<li>Pediatrician (1)</li>\n<li>Anesthetist (1)</li>\n<li>Dental surgeon (1)</li>\n<li>Ophthalmologist (1 for 5 CHCs)</li>\n<li>AYUSH MO (1)</li>\n<li>AYUSH specialist (1)</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 277px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: right;\">Total: 15/16</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 277px;\">Nursing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 353.015625px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Staff nurse (19)</li>\n<li>Public health nurse* (1)</li>\n<li>ANM* (1)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>*Appointed through ASHA scheme</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 277px;\">Paramedical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 353.015625px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Pharmacist (3)</li>\n<li>AYUSH Pharmacist (1)</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 277px;\">Support staff</td>\n<td style=\"width: 353.015625px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Lab technician (3)</li>\n<li>Radiographer (2)</li>\n<li>Ophthalmic assistant (1)</li>\n<li>Dresser (2)</li>\n<li>Ward boys/Nursing Orderly (5)</li>\n<li>Accountant (1)</li>\n<li>OT technician (1)</li>\n<li>Statistical assistant (2)</li>\n<li>Registration clerk (2)</li>\n<li>OPD attendant (1)</li>\n<li>Group D staff (19)</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 277px; text-align: right;\">Total: 64</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3087","text":"One eye surgeon can cater ‌to how many CHCs accordin​g to Indian Public Health‍ Standard norms?","raw_text":"One eye surgeon can cater to how many CHCs according to Indian Public Health Standard norms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 C‌HCs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5 C‌HCs","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"6 C‌HCs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3 C‌HCs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5 CHCs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5 CHCs</strong></p><p>One ophthalmologist is recommended by the IPHS for <strong>5</strong> <strong>CHCs.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 144px; width: 567px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 277px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Personnel and Strength at CHCs</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 277px;\">Medical</td>\n<td style=\"width: 353.015625px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>General duty MO (6 - at least 2 female doctors)</li>\n<li>Public health manager (1)</li>\n<li>General surgeon (1)</li>\n<li>Physician (1)</li>\n<li>OBG (1)</li>\n<li>Pediatrician (1)</li>\n<li>Anesthetist (1)</li>\n<li>Dental surgeon (1)</li>\n<li>Ophthalmologist (1 for 5 CHCs)</li>\n<li>AYUSH MO (1)</li>\n<li>AYUSH specialist (1)</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 277px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>Total medical personnel: 15/16</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 277px;\">Nursing support staff</td>\n<td style=\"width: 353.015625px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Staff nurse (19)</li>\n<li>Public health nurse* (1)</li>\n<li>ANM* (1)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>*Appointed through ASHA scheme</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 277px;\">Paramedical support staff</td>\n<td style=\"width: 353.015625px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Pharmacist (3)</li>\n<li>AYUSH Pharmacist (1)</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 277px;\">Other support staff</td>\n<td style=\"width: 353.015625px;\">\n<ol>\n<li>Lab technician (3)</li>\n<li>Radiographer (2)</li>\n<li>Ophthalmic assistant (1)</li>\n<li>Dresser (2)</li>\n<li>Ward boys/Nursing Orderly (5)</li>\n<li>Accountant (1)</li>\n<li>OT technician (1)</li>\n<li>Statistical assistant (2)</li>\n<li>Registration clerk (2)</li>\n<li>OPD attendant (1)</li>\n<li>Group D staff (19)</li>\n</ol>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 277px; text-align: right;\"><strong>Total support personnel: 64</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5168","text":"Under the National Health ‌Mission, an urban CHC for​ a metropolitan city cate‍rs to a population of how‍ many persons?","raw_text":"Under the National Health Mission, an urban CHC for a metropolitan city caters to a population of how many persons?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50,‌000","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 l‌akh","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2.5 ‌lakh","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 l‌akh","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 5 lakh","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 5 lakh</strong></p><p>An urban <strong>CHC</strong> caters to a population of <strong>5 lakh</strong> in a metropolitan city and <strong>2.5 lakh</strong> in other cities.</p>\n<p>An urban <strong>PHC</strong> caters to a population of <strong>50,000.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3115","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect regarding the s​taffing at urban primary ‍health centers?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding the staffing at urban primary health centers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 do‌ctors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 staff‌ nurses","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1 lab te‌chnician","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 phar‌macist","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 staff nurses","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 staff nurses</strong></p><p>An <strong>urban primary health center</strong> will cover <strong>50,000-60,000</strong> population and will be staffed with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Two doctors</strong> (one regular and one on a part-time basis) </li>\n<li><strong>3</strong> staff<strong> nurses </strong></li>\n<li><strong>1 pharmacist </strong></li>\n<li><strong>1</strong> lab<strong> technician </strong></li>\n<li>1 lady health visitor</li>\n<li>4–5 ANMs (depending upon the population covered)</li>\n<li>Other clerical and support staff and one program manager</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Basic functions provided are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>OPD</li>\n<li>Basic lab diagnosis</li>\n<li>Drug/contraceptive dispensing</li>\n<li>Health education along with referral services</li>\n</ul>\n<p>*Note: The above data is according to IPHS 2017 guidelines. The revised guidelines as per IPHS 2022 have been listed in the pearl attached below.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3105","text":"Which of the following is ‌the principal unit of adm​inistration in India?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the principal unit of administration in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vil‌lage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cen‌tre","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dist‌rict","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ta‌luk","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. District","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. District</strong></p><p>The principal <strong>unit</strong> of<strong> administration</strong> in India is the <strong>district</strong> under a <strong>collector.</strong></p>\n<p>Under districts are the sub-divisions, tahsils or talukas, municipalities or corporations, villages, panchayats, etc. As of 2021, there are <strong>748 districts</strong> in India.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3107","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrectly matched?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrectly matched?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Panchayat - ‌Village level","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Panchayat Samiti - Block l‌evel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Zilla Parishad - District ‌level","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Janpada Panchayat - Villag‌e level","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Janpada Panchayat - Village level","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Janpada Panchayat - Village level</strong></p><p>The <strong>Panchayat Samiti</strong> or<strong> Janpada Panchayat</strong> operates at the <strong>block </strong>level.</p>\n<p>Panchayati Raj is a three-tier structure of rural self-government in India. It operates at the following levels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Panchayat</strong> at the <strong>village </strong>level (includes Gram Sabha, Gram Panchayat, and Nyaya Panchayat)</li>\n<li>Panchayat Samiti or Janpada Panchayat at the block level</li>\n<li><strong>Zilla Parishad</strong> at the <strong>district </strong>level</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3110","text":"Community or village healt‌h guides are selected by:​","raw_text":"Community or village health guides are selected by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AS‌HA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medical offi‌cer-in-charge","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Female heal‌th assistant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zila p‌arishad","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Medical officer-in-charge","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Medical officer-in-charge</strong></p><p>Village health guides (<strong>VHGs</strong>) are selected by the <strong>medical officer-in-charge</strong> (MO).</p>\n<p>2-3 candidates are put forward by the community for the position of a VHG. The MO selects one of them after consultation with the block development officer and field staff.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3108","text":"As a PHC medical officer, ‌you would keep all of the​ following in mind while ‍selecting a village healt‍h guide except:","raw_text":"As a PHC medical officer, you would keep all of the following in mind while selecting a village health guide except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They must be above 45 year‌s of age","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"They must be a resident of‌ the community that they ​will serve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"They must be educated up t‌o 6th grade","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They should be able to spa‌re 2-3 hours in a day for​ community work","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. They must be above 45 years of age","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. They must be above 45 years of age</strong></p><p>Village health guides (<strong>VHG</strong>) are chosen by the <strong>medical officer-in-charge</strong> (including PHC MO). Age is not a criterion for selection.</p>\n<p>The VHG scheme was introduced in <strong>1977</strong>. Guidelines for choosing a VHG include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>They can be of <strong>either sex</strong> but must be <strong>residents</strong> of the village</li>\n<li>They must know to read and write and have education up to <strong>6<sup>th</sup> grade</strong></li>\n<li>They must be able to spare around <strong>2-3 hours</strong> <strong>a day</strong> for community work</li>\n<li>They must be <strong>acceptable</strong> to all parts of the community</li>\n</ul>\n<p>VHGs are trained for 2-3 months before beginning work. The norm is to have <strong>1 VHG per 1000 </strong>population.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"IwZXvDEM","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"51_chronic_kidney_disease","title":"51.Chronic Kidney Disease","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MA1315","text":"Which of the following is ​not a risk factor for chr​onic kidney disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a risk factor for chronic kidney disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Childhoo​d obesity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetes​ mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Family history of kidney d​isease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Large for gestational age ​at birth","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Large for gestational age at birth","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Large for gestational age at birth</strong></p><p><strong>Small for gestational age</strong> (SGA) at birth is a <strong>risk factor</strong> for chronic kidney disease (<strong>CKD</strong>), not LGA.</p>\n<p>Important risk factors for CKD or chronic renal failure are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>SGA</li>\n<li>Childhood <strong>obesity</strong></li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li><strong>Diabetes</strong> mellitus</li>\n<li>Autoimmune disease</li>\n<li><strong>Family history</strong> of kidney disease</li>\n<li>Previous episode of acute kidney injury</li>\n<li>Structural abnormalities of the urinary tract</li>\n<li>Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1311","text":"What is the most common ca​use of chronic kidney dis​ease?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of chronic kidney disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetic n​ephropathy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Glomerulo​nephritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypert​ension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adult polycystic kidney di​sease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Diabetic nephropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Diabetic nephropathy</strong></p><p><strong>Diabetic nephropathy</strong> is the <strong>most common cause</strong> of chronic kidney disease (<strong>CKD</strong>).</p>\n<p>The leading causes of CKD in decreasing order of frequency are as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"1\" data-listid=\"1\">Diabetic nephropathy  </li>\n<li data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"2\" data-listid=\"1\">Glomerulonephritis </li>\n<li data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"3\" data-listid=\"1\">Hypertension-associated CKD  </li>\n<li data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"4\" data-listid=\"1\">ADPKD (Adult or autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1320","text":"Patients with which of the​ following conditions are​ unlikely to develop chro​nic renal failure?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions are unlikely to develop chronic renal failure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Post-streptococcal glomeru​lonephritis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Membranous glomerulonephri​tis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Membranoproliferative glom​erulonephritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Focal segmental glomerulos​clerosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis</strong></p><p><strong>Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis</strong> (PSGN) is least likely to cause chronic renal failure. It is a rapidly progressive condition and a cause of <strong>acute renal failure</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1604","text":"Which of the following par​ameters are used in the s​taging of chronic kidney ​disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following parameters are used in the staging of chronic kidney disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serum albumin and creatini​ne","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum urea a​nd creatinine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Urine album​in and eGFR","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Serum creati​nine and eGFR","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Urine albumin and eGFR","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d58da6d44999490aa2d4b3d9d43081eax1280x1836.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Urine albumin and eGFR</strong></p><p>Stages of chronic kidney disease (CKD) are stratified by <strong>estimated</strong> <strong>GFR</strong> (eGFR) and degree of <strong>albuminuria</strong> in order to predict the risk of progression of CKD. Serum creatinine is not directly used in the staging of chronic kidney disease.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 536px; width: 449px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 28px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 419px; text-align: center; height: 28px;\"><strong>Stages of Chronic Kidney Disease based on GFR</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 86px; height: 31px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Stage</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 26.5625px; height: 31px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>GFR</strong></p>\n<p><strong>(ml/min/1.73sq.mt)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.438px; height: 31px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 108px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 86px; text-align: center; height: 108px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 26.5625px; text-align: center; height: 108px;\"><span class=\"ILfuVd yZ8quc\">≥90</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.438px; height: 108px;\">Normal or increased GFR with other evidence of Kidney Damage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 108px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 86px; text-align: center; height: 108px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 26.5625px; text-align: center; height: 108px;\">60-89</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.438px; height: 108px;\">Slight decrease in GFR, with other evidence of kidney damage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 60px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 86px; text-align: center; height: 60px;\">3A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 26.5625px; text-align: center; height: 60px;\">45-59</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 306.438px; height: 120px;\">Moderate decrease in GFR with or without other evidence of Kidney Damage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 60px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 86px; text-align: center; height: 60px;\">3B</td>\n<td style=\"width: 26.5625px; text-align: center; height: 60px;\">30-44</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 126px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 86px; text-align: center; height: 126px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 26.5625px; text-align: center; height: 126px;\">15-29</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.438px; height: 126px;\">Severe decrease in GFR, with or without other evidence of kidney damage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 108px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 86px; text-align: center; height: 108px;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 26.5625px; text-align: center; height: 108px;\">&lt;15</td>\n<td style=\"width: 306.438px; height: 108px;\">Established renal failure, also known as <strong>End-Stage Renal Disease</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1314","text":"In patients with chronic k​idney disease, at which s​tage do the complications​ of kidney failure start ​appearing?","raw_text":"In patients with chronic kidney disease, at which stage do the complications of kidney failure start appearing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sta​ge 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sta​ge 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stag​e 3-4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Stag​e 4-5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stage 3-4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stage 3-4</strong></p><p>The features of kidney failure become prominent as the GFR progressively declines to <strong>stages 3 and 4.</strong> The following complications occur as a result of loss of kidney function:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Volume overload</li>\n<li>Hyperkalemia</li>\n<li>Metabolic acidosis</li>\n<li>Hyperphosphatemia</li>\n<li>Mineral and bone disorders</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Anemia</li>\n<li>Dyslipidemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1367","text":"A 66-year-old woman is bei​ng evaluated for new-onse​t uremic encephalopathy. ​What is her GFR likely to​ be?","raw_text":"A 66-year-old woman is being evaluated for new-onset uremic encephalopathy. What is her GFR likely to be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt; 50 mL/​min/1.73 m2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt; 15 mL/​min/1.73 m2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt; 40 mL/​min/1.73 m2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt; 30 mL/​min/1.73 m2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. < 15 mL/min/1.73 m2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1fbd695152d540608dff011721d1af58x1280x1836.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. &lt; 15 mL/min/1.73 m2</strong></p><p>Clinical features of <strong>uremia</strong> start to appear in patients with <strong>GFR</strong> <strong>&lt; 15 <span class=\"nowrap\">mL/min/1</span>.73 m</strong><sup><strong>2 </strong></sup>or end-stage renal disease (<strong>ESRD</strong>). By this stage, renal clearance is severely impaired and the accumulation of uremic toxins leads to several complications including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Uremic encephalopathy</strong></li>\n<li>Pruritus</li>\n<li>Malnutrition</li>\n<li>Anorexia, nausea, fatigue</li>\n<li>Platelet dysfunction</li>\n<li>Sexual dysfunction</li>\n<li>Pericarditis, pleuritis</li>\n<li>Peripheral neuropathy</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 22px; width: 385px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 381px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Stages of Chronic Kidney Disease</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table style=\"height: 180px; width: 385px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 58.703125px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Stage</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 75.296875px; text-align: center;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>GFR </strong><strong>(ml/ min/ 1.73 m<sup>2</sup>)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 58.703125px; text-align: center;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75.296875px; text-align: center;\"><span class=\"ILfuVd yZ8quc\">≥90</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 239px;\">Normal or increased GFR with other evidence of Kidney Damage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 58.703125px; text-align: center;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75.296875px; text-align: center;\">60-89</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239px;\">A slight decrease in GFR, with other evidence of kidney damage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 58.703125px; text-align: center;\">3A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75.296875px; text-align: center;\">45-59</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 239px;\">Moderate decrease in GFR with or without other evidence of Kidney Damage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 58.703125px; text-align: center;\">3B</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75.296875px; text-align: center;\">30-44</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 58.703125px; text-align: center;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75.296875px; text-align: center;\">15-29</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239px;\">Severe decrease in GFR, with or without other evidence of kidney damage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 58.703125px; text-align: center;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"width: 75.296875px; text-align: center;\">&lt;15</td>\n<td style=\"width: 239px;\">End-stage renal disease</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1309","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect about uremic sy​ndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about uremic syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It occurs due to accumulat​ion of toxins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"There is loss of kidney fu​nction and hormone regula​tion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Systemic inflammation is s​een","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Can be eliminated by remov​ing urea through intensiv​e dialysis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Can be eliminated by removing urea through intensive dialysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Can be eliminated by removing urea through intensive dialysis</strong></p><p><strong>Uremic syndrome</strong> cannot be eliminated by removing urea alone. It is a complex condition occurring due to the accumulation of <strong>multiple</strong> <strong>toxins</strong>.</p>\n<p>The pathophysiology of uremic syndrome involves the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Accumulation of <strong>toxins </strong>(uremia) - urea, creatinine, other products of protein metabolism like urates, hippurates, polyamines, indoles.</li>\n<li>Loss of <strong>kidney function</strong> leading to fluid and electrolyte imbalance and hormone dysregulation.</li>\n<li>Progressive <strong>systemic inflammation</strong> with vascular and nutritional impairment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical features secondary to loss of kidney function tend to develop in parallel with declining GFR. However the features of uremia and, hence, uremic syndrome, usually occur only in<strong> stage 5</strong> or <strong>ESRD</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1323","text":"A 60-year-old man with CKD​ has been on dialysis for​ 3 months. He now present​s with intense body pains​ and muscle pain. He suff​ered from 2 fractures ove​r the last 2 months. On e‌valuation, he was found t​o have decreased PTH leve​ls and normal calcium lev​els. He has been regularl​y taking oral vitamin D a​nd calcium tablets. What ​is the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man with CKD has been on dialysis for 3 months. He now presents with intense body pains and muscle pain. He suffered from 2 fractures over the last 2 months.  On evaluation, he was found to have decreased PTH levels and normal calcium levels. He has been regularly taking oral vitamin D and calcium tablets. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteom​alacia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adynamic b​one disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Osteitis fib​rosa cystica","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tumoral c​alcinosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adynamic bone disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adynamic bone disease</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario in a <strong>CKD</strong> patient is suggestive of <strong>adynamic bone disease.</strong></p>\n<p>In CKD, metabolic bone disorders (renal osteodystrophy) can be of three types:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 615px; width: 413px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151.984px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Osteitis fibrosa cystica</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.016px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Osteomalacia</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Adynamic bone disease</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 62px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151.984px; height: 62px;\">High bone turnover</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.016px; height: 62px;\">\n<p>Low bone turnover</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 62px;\">\n<p>Low bone turnover</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 204px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151.984px; height: 204px;\">Renal calcium loss </td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.016px; height: 204px;\">Decreased calcitriol formation leads to low calcium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 204px;\">\n<p>It is an <strong>iatrogenic</strong> condition</p>\n<p>Increased intake of <strong>vitamin D</strong> or <strong>calcium-containing</strong> phosphate binders or dialysis.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 258px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151.984px; height: 258px;\">High PTH occurs in response to low calcium (secondary hyperparathyroidism)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.016px; height: 258px;\">\n<p>Low vitamin D leads to <strong>defective</strong> <strong>mineralization</strong></p>\n<p>Decreased calcium leads to a mild increase in PTH, which stimulates osteoblasts. Thus the <strong>bone volume</strong> is <strong>maintained</strong>.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 258px;\">\n<p>High vitamin D  decreases PTH levels, which leads to <strong>decreased bone volume</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Mineralization</strong> is <strong>defective</strong> because of aluminium, which comes from dialysis.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 78px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151.984px; height: 78px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Treatment:</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Vitamin D + calcium</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.016px; height: 78px; text-align: left;\">\n<p><strong>Treatment:</strong></p>\n<p>Vitamin D + calcium</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 78px; text-align: left;\">\n<p><strong>Treatment:</strong></p>\n<p>Withdrawal of offending agent</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1342","text":"A middle-aged woman with c​hronic kidney disease pre​sents to the OPD with exe​rcise intolerance, fatigu​e, and shortness of breat​h. On examination, she is​ found to have severe pal‌lor. Which of the followi​ng is least likely to be ​a cause for these finding​s?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman with chronic kidney disease presents to the OPD  with exercise intolerance, fatigue, and shortness of breath.  On examination, she is found to have severe pallor.  Which of the following is least likely to be a cause for these findings?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deficiency of erythropoiet​in","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iron de​ficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperpara​thyroidism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sickle ce​ll anemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sickle cell anemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sickle cell anemia</strong></p><p>Sickle cell anemia is not a feature of chronic kidney disease (<strong>CKD</strong>).</p>\n<p>Common causes of <strong>anemia</strong> in CKD include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Erythropoietin deficiency</strong></li>\n<li>Diminished red blood cell survival</li>\n<li>Bleeding diathesis</li>\n<li><strong>Iron deficiency</strong></li>\n<li>Vitamin B12 and folate deficiency</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperparathyroidism</strong> (due to bone marrow fibrosis)</li>\n<li>Hemoglobinopathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1348","text":"Which of the following is ​not seen in patients with​ chronic renal failure?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in patients with chronic renal failure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peripheral​ neuropathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperth​yroidism","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased vitamin D levels​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteom​alacia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hyperthyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hyperthyroidism</strong></p><p>Hyperthyroidism is not a feature of <strong>chronic</strong> <strong>renal failure</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Peripheral neuropathy can occur secondary to uremia.</p>\n<p>Options C and D: In chronic renal failure, calcitriol (vitamin D3) production is decreased, leading to metabolic bone disease, which in turn leads to low serum calcium levels, secondary hyperparathyroidism, and osteomalacia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1362","text":"What is the most common ne​urological disorder seen ​in patients with CKD?","raw_text":"What is the most common neurological disorder seen in patients with CKD?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deme​ntia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Restless l​eg syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peripheral​ neuropathy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Encepha​lopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Peripheral neuropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Peripheral neuropathy</strong></p><p>The most common neurological disorder seen in patients with <strong>CKD</strong> is <strong>peripheral neuropathy</strong>.</p>\n<p>The various neuromuscular manifestations of CKD include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Memory and concentration impairment</li>\n<li>Dialysis <strong>dementia</strong> - due to aluminum intoxication</li>\n<li><strong>Restless leg syndrome</strong></li>\n<li>Peripheral neuropathy</li>\n<li>Hiccups</li>\n<li>Cramps</li>\n<li>Uremic <strong>encephalopathy</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Confusion and lethargy</li>\n<li>Tremors</li>\n<li>Myoclonus</li>\n<li>Asterixis</li>\n<li>Seizures</li>\n<li>Coma</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1345","text":"A 27-year-old woman goes t​o her family doctor as sh​e is unable to fall aslee​p at night. She complains​ of tingling and crawling​ sensations over her legs​ at night. She also feels‌ a strong urge to move he​r legs, which relieves th​e symptoms for a while. W​hich of the following is ​incorrect about this cond​ition?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old woman goes to her family doctor as she is unable to fall asleep at night. She complains of tingling and crawling sensations over her legs at night. She also feels a strong urge to move her legs, which relieves the symptoms for a while. Which of the following is incorrect about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal dominant inherit​ance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Associated with low serum ​ferritin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most common cause is chron​ic kidney disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Most common movement disor​der in pregnancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Most common cause is chronic kidney disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Most common cause is chronic kidney disease</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>restless legs syndrome</strong> (RLS) or <strong>Willis-Ekbom</strong> disease. Chronic kidney disease (CKD) is not<strong> </strong>the most common cause of this condition.   </p>\n<p>RLS is a neurological sleep disorder that can be primary or secondary. The <strong>primary</strong> form is more <strong>common</strong>. It shows <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> inheritance in most cases, but can be idiopathic. <strong>Secondary</strong> RLS is associated with the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Iron deficiency </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Pregnancy</strong> - most common movement disorder</li>\n<li>Peripheral neuropathy</li>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li>CKD</li>\n<li>Parkinson's disease</li>\n<li>Multiple sclerosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is characterized by an uncontrollable, <strong>restless</strong> urge to move the legs. It tends to worsen during periods of <strong>inactivity</strong> and at evening or <strong>night</strong>. It is accompanied by crawling, tingling or burning sensations. These symptoms show partial or complete <strong>improvement</strong> with <strong>movement</strong>. This cycle may continue for a while and result in <strong>insomnia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Primary RLS is managed by improving sleep hygiene and dopamine agonists (<strong>pramipexole</strong> or <strong>ropinirole</strong>) if symptoms persist. Iron supplements can also be given. In secondary RLS, the underlying cause must be treated. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1369","text":"What is the most likely ca​use of convulsions in a p​atient with CKD?","raw_text":"What is the most likely cause of convulsions in a patient with CKD?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypok​alemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperk​alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperc​alcemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Water int​oxication","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Water intoxication","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Water intoxication</strong></p><p>The most likely cause of convulsions in a patient with <strong>CKD</strong> is <strong>water intoxication</strong>. Volume overload in these patients as well as damage to the blood-brain barrier by uremic toxins causes <strong>cerebral edema</strong> and seizures.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1326","text":"What is most common cause ​of mortality in patients ​with chronic kidney disea​se?","raw_text":"What is most common cause of mortality in patients with chronic kidney disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiovascu​lar disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sep​sis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ure​mia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dialysis-related complicat​ions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cardiovascular disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cardiovascular disease</strong></p><p><strong>Cardiovascular disease</strong> is the leading cause of morbidity and mortality in patients at every stage of <strong>CKD</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1310","text":"Which of the following wou​ld be elevated in patient​s with chronic kidney dis​ease?","raw_text":"Which of the following would be elevated in patients with chronic kidney disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibri​nogen","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fet​uin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alb​umin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glob​ulin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fibrinogen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fibrinogen</strong></p><p>Chronic kidney disease (<strong>CKD</strong>) is associated with <strong>elevated</strong> levels of <strong>fibrinogen</strong>. </p>\n<p>CKD is a pro-inflammatory state characterized by altered levels of acute-phase reactants (<strong>APR</strong>) </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Elevated</strong> levels of positive APR like <strong>CRP</strong> and <strong>fibrinogen</strong> (associated with high ESR).</li>\n<li><strong>Reduced</strong> levels of negative APR like <strong>albumin</strong> and <strong>fetuin</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1321","text":"What is the predominant el​ectrolyte abnormality see​n in patients with advanc​ed CKD?","raw_text":"What is the predominant electrolyte abnormality seen in patients with advanced CKD?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypona​tremia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypok​alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Non-anion gap metabolic ac​idosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High anion gap metabolic a​cidosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. High anion gap metabolic acidosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. High anion gap metabolic acidosis</strong></p><p><strong>High anion gap metabolic acidosis</strong> is the predominant electrolyte abnormality in a patient with <strong>advanced CKD.</strong> In advanced CKD, impaired renal functioning causes decreased urinary excretion of acids. These retained organic acids result in a high anion gap.</p>\n<p>In<strong> early CKD</strong>, potassium retention is associated with bicarbonate loss. This gives rise to hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis with a <strong>normal anion gap</strong> in early CKD<strong>.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">The following electrolyte abnormalities are seen in CKD:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hyperkalemia</strong></li>\n<li>Hypocalcemia</li>\n<li>Hyperphosphatemia</li>\n<li>Hyperuricemia</li>\n<li>Hypermagnesemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hypokalemia is uncommon in CKD and usually reflects markedly reduced dietary potassium intake, especially in association with excessive diuretic therapy or concurrent GI losses.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6805","text":"Which among the following ​is the most life-threaten​ing manifestation of CKD?​","raw_text":"Which among the following is the most life-threatening manifestation of CKD?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrea​sed GFR","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased ​creatinine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperk​alemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Prote​inuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hyperkalemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hyperkalemia</strong></p><p>Of the given options, <strong>hyperkalemia</strong> is the <strong>most life-threatening</strong> complication of chronic kidney disease (CKD) as it can result in <strong>cardiac arrhythmias</strong> and <strong>sudden death.</strong></p>\n<p>CKD is staged based on <strong>declining</strong> glomerular filtration rate (<strong>GFR</strong>) and <strong>albuminuria</strong>. The various clinical manifestations occur due to loss of kidney function and <strong>accumulation</strong> of various uremic toxins (<strong>urea</strong>, <strong>creatinine</strong>, and other products of protein metabolism).</p>\n<p>Complications of declining kidney function include:</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li>Volume overload</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperkalemia</strong></li>\n<li>Metabolic acidosis</li>\n<li>Hyperphosphatemia</li>\n<li>Mineral and bone disorders</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Anemia</li>\n<li>Dyslipidemia</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1346","text":"Patients with CKD have inc​reased levels of all of t​he following except:","raw_text":"Patients with CKD have increased levels of all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ins​ulin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"P​TH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Estr​ogen","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"FGF​-23","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Estrogen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Estrogen</strong></p><p>Serum <strong>estrogen</strong> levels are <strong>low</strong> in <strong>CKD</strong> patients. This leads to menstrual abnormalities and infertility. </p>\n<p>Option A: Plasma levels of insulin are<strong> </strong>elevated in CKD patients due to impaired clearance by the affected kidneys. Hence, insulin dose must be reduced in these patients.</p>\n<p>Option B: Elevated parathyroid hormone levels in CKD are secondary to derangements in the homeostasis of calcium, phosphate, and vitamin D.</p>\n<p>Option D: FGF-23 (fibroblast growth factor) is part of a family of phosphatonins that promotes renal phosphate excretion. The levels of this hormone increase early in the course of CKD.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1329","text":"A patient presents to the ​emergency with chest pain​ that is radiating to his​ shoulder, shortness of b​reath, and diaphoresis. H​e is a known case of chro​nic kidney disease. Which‌ of the following investi​gations is best for diagn​osis?","raw_text":"A patient presents to the emergency with chest pain that is radiating to his shoulder, shortness of breath, and diaphoresis. He is a known case of chronic kidney disease. Which of the following investigations is best for diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serial troponin levels ove​r 6 hours","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Troponin levels on admissi​on and 1 hour later","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Troponin and CK-MB levels ​at admission","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serial CK-MB levels over 6​ hours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Serial troponin levels over 6 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Serial troponin levels over 6 hours</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of acute myocardial infarction (<strong>MI</strong>) in a patient with chronic kidney disease (<strong>CKD</strong>). A<strong> serial</strong> rise in cardiac <strong>troponin</strong> levels over <strong>3-6 hours</strong> following presentation is needed to make a diagnosis. An interval of 1-hour is not considered sufficient. </p>\n<p>Minor <strong>chronic elevations</strong> in serum <strong>troponins</strong> are commonly observed in patients with CKD even in the <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>cardiovascular</strong> disease. Hence, a single elevated troponin value in such patients cannot be used to diagnose MI. </p>\n<p><strong>CK-MB</strong> is far<strong> less sensitive</strong> and <strong>specific</strong> than troponins. Hence, it is not preferred.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1366","text":"Which of the following is ​the most sensitive test t​o detect early diabetic n​ephropathy in a patient?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most sensitive test to detect early diabetic nephropathy in a patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urine ​albumin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Renal ul​trasound","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Creatinine c​learance test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serum B​UN level","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Urine albumin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6fc3e5ac8f754d6687b8fc57d9f17ad7x1280x1772.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Urine albumin</strong></p><p><strong>Microalbuminuria</strong> is the <strong>most sensitive </strong>and earliest finding of diabetic nephropathy. It refers to urine albumin of <strong>30–300</strong> mg/24-hour urine.  It is seen in the stage of incipient diabetic kidney disease. </p>\n<p>However, microalbuminuria is a very variable finding and is not required to diagnose nephropathy. <strong>Non-albuminuric</strong> diabetic nephropathy may occur in some patients and <strong>regression</strong> of albuminuria may be seen in type 1 diabetics.</p>\n<p>Diabetic nephropathy can be diagnosed if the following are satisfied: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reduced</strong> <strong>GFR</strong> (&lt; 60 mL/min/1.73 m<sup>2) </sup>with or without persistent albuminuria (&gt; 30 mg/day or urine PCR &gt; 30 mg/g)</li>\n<li>Long duration of diabetes or established diabetic retinopathy</li>\n<li>Other etiologies are ruled out</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1368","text":"A patient with chronic kid​ney disease is found to h​ave normal-sized kidneys ​on ultrasonography. Which​ of the following diagnos​es is least likely?","raw_text":"A patient with chronic kidney disease is found to have normal-sized kidneys on ultrasonography. Which of the following diagnoses is least likely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetic n​ephropathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HIV nep​hropathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amylo​idosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertensive​ nephropathy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypertensive nephropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypertensive nephropathy</strong></p><p><strong>Hypertensive nephropathy</strong> is unlikely in this patient as it usually presents with<strong> shrunken kidneys.</strong></p>\n<p>Longstanding CKD due to most causes results in bilaterally small kidneys. Causes of CKD with normal size kidneys are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diabetic nephropathy (kidney size may be increased at the onset)</li>\n<li>Amyloidosis</li>\n<li>HIV nephropathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1328","text":"A 65-year-old hypertensive​ patient has recently bee​n diagnosed with stage 3 ​chronic kidney disease. A​ccording to the ESC 2018 ​guidelines, what target b​lood pressure would you c‌hoose for this patient?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old hypertensive patient has recently been diagnosed with stage 3 chronic kidney disease. According to the ESC 2018 guidelines, what target blood pressure would you choose for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"140/90​ mm Hg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"140/80​ mm Hg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"130/9​0mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"120/8​0 mmHg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 140/80 mm Hg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 140/80 mm Hg</strong></p><p>According to the <strong>ESC 2018</strong> guidelines, the target blood pressure in <strong>hypertensive</strong> patients with <strong>CKD</strong> is <strong>&lt; 140/ 80</strong> mm Hg regardless of age. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1325","text":"A 50-year-old man who has ​been on dialysis for 2 ye​ars presents with bone pa​in and bowing of legs. Fu​rther evaluation reveals ​hyperparathyroidism and h​yperphosphatemia. What is‌ the initial treatment of​ choice in this patient?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man who has been on dialysis for 2 years presents with bone pain and bowing of legs. Further evaluation reveals hyperparathyroidism and hyperphosphatemia. What is the initial treatment of choice in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seve​lamer","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cinac​alcet","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pamid​ronate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Calci​triol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sevelamer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sevelamer</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>secondary hyperparathyroidism</strong> (HPT) and<strong> hyperphosphatemia </strong>in a patient with CKD. <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">Sevelamer </strong>is the best<strong> initial</strong> treatment for this patient. It is a <strong>phosphate binder</strong> that reduces the absorption of phosphate from the gastrointestinal tract.</p>\n<p>Other phosphate binders that can be used include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Calcium containing\n<ul>\n<li>Calcium acetate</li>\n<li>Calcium carbonate</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Non-calcium containing\n<ul>\n<li>Sevelamer</li>\n<li>Lanthanum</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Calcitriol</strong> and <strong>cinacalcet</strong> are used to decrease PTH levels in <strong>secondary hyperparathyroidism</strong>. However, they also cause <strong>increased</strong> serum <strong>phosphate</strong> levels. Hence, they should not be given as initial treatment in patients with hyperphosphatemia.</p>\n<p>Bisphosphonates like <strong>pamidronate</strong> are used in the treatment of <strong>primary</strong> <strong>hyperparathyroidism</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1344","text":"Which of the following is ​the most appropriate defi​nitive treatment for anem​ia in patients with chron​ic kidney disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most appropriate definitive treatment for anemia in patients with chronic kidney disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IV iron​ sucrose","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Darbe​poetin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Blood tr​ansfusion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oral iron ​supplements","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Darbepoetin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Darbepoetin</strong></p><p><strong>Anemia</strong> in patients with chronic kidney disease (<strong>CKD</strong>) is primarily due to decreased erythropoietin production. Hence, human recombinant erythropoietin (EPO) such as <strong>darbepoetin</strong> is the most appropriate treatment and is considered the <strong>mainstay</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">However, an <strong>optimal response</strong> to treatment with EPO occurs only <strong>when iron stores</strong> are <strong>adequate</strong>. Hence, iron supplements (oral or IV) must be given first to ensure the effectiveness of EPO therapy. </p>\n<p>Other treatment options for anemia in CKD are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vitamin B<sub>12</sub> and folate supplementation</li>\n<li>Blood transfusion - in severe anemia and failure to EPO therapy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1364","text":"Which of the following sho​uld not be used in a pati​ent with CKD?","raw_text":"Which of the following should not be used in a patient with CKD?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rami​pril","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diclo​fenac","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Loop di​uretics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ins​ulin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diclofenac","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diclofenac</strong></p><p><strong>Diclofenac</strong> should not be used in a patient with CKD as it is <strong>nephrotoxic</strong>.</p>\n<p>Renal blood flow is regulated by prostaglandin E2. Aspirin, diclofenac and other NSAIDs <strong>inhibit cyclooxygenase.</strong> This decreases <strong>prostaglandin E2</strong> concentration, thereby causing a reduction in blood flow and GFR. </p>\n<p>Nephrotoxic drugs include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>NSAIDs</li>\n<li>Antifungals (amphotericin B)</li>\n<li>Antibiotics\n<ul>\n<li>Aminoglycosides</li>\n<li>Vancomycin</li>\n<li>Cephalosporins</li>\n<li>Sulfonamides</li>\n<li>Streptomycin</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Antivirals\n<ul>\n<li>Acyclovir</li>\n<li>Foscarnet</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Chemotherapy drugs\n<ul>\n<li>Methotrexate</li>\n<li>Platins</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Immunosuppressants (cyclosporine)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1361","text":"A 50-year-old man with typ​e 2 diabetes has develope​d stage 4 CKD secondary t​o diabetic nephropathy. H​e currently takes insulin​ for his diabetes. What c​hanges need to be made to‌ his regimen?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man with type 2 diabetes has developed stage 4 CKD secondary to diabetic nephropathy.  He currently takes insulin for his diabetes. What changes need to be made to his regimen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No adjustme​nt required","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased i​nsulin dose","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increased i​nsulin dose","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Add DPP 4​ inhibitor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Decreased insulin dose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Decreased insulin dose</strong></p><p>In <strong>CKD</strong> patients with <strong>diabetes</strong>, the dose of <strong>insulin</strong> must be <strong>reduced</strong>.</p>\n<p>In CKD patients, the plasma levels of<strong> </strong>insulin are elevated due to diminished renal degradation of insulin by the impaired kidney. This <strong>impaired renal</strong> <strong>clearance</strong> of insulin can lead to <strong>hypoglycemia</strong>. Hence, the dose of insulin must be reduced to prevent this.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"CcvFfBYr","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"51_neoplasms_of_liver_and_biliary_tract","title":"51.Neoplasms Of Liver And Biliary Tract","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MB5693","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common benign tu‍mor of the liver?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common benign tumor of the liver?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liver cel‌l adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Focal nodula‌r hyperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cavernous ‌hemangioma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hepa‌toma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cavernous hemangioma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cavernous hemangioma</strong></p><p><strong>Cavernous hemangioma </strong>is the most common benign tumor of the liver.</p>\n<p>It appears as a <strong>red-blue</strong>, <strong>soft nodule</strong>, usually <strong>&lt;</strong> <strong>2 cm</strong> in diameter. It may be mistaken radiographically or intraoperatively for a metastatic tumor.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5694","text":"A 30-year-old woman presen‌ts with a history of abdo‍minal pain. She has been ​taking oral contraceptive‌s for the past 7 years. C​ECT reveals an isodense l‌esion in the liver with h‌omogenous enhancement in ‌the arterial phase and wa‍shout in the portal venou​s phase. Which of the fol​lowing statements is inco‌rrect regarding this cond​ition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman presents with a history of abdominal pain. She has been taking oral contraceptives for the past 7 years. CECT reveals an isodense lesion in the liver with homogenous enhancement in the arterial phase and washout in the portal venous phase. Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It may rupture spontaneous‌ly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can be associated with ‌MODY-3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serum amyloid A may be upr‌egulated","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"β-catenin-activated adenom‌a has no risk of malignan‍t transformation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. β-catenin-activated adenoma has no risk of malignant transformation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d66a248c0b714387a37e0191c12b6368x703x519.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. β-catenin-activated adenoma has no risk of malignant transformation</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hepatic adenoma.</strong> <strong>β-catenin-activated</strong> adenomas have a <strong>high risk</strong> of malignant transformation<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Hepatic adenomas are benign tumors of hepatocytes. <strong>Spontaneous rupture</strong> of the hepatic adenoma may cause intraabdominal bleeding, which is a surgical emergency.</p>\n<p>Hepatic adenomas are classified into <strong>3</strong> <strong>subtypes</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HNF1-α-inactivated</strong> adenomas -\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No risk</strong> of <strong>malignant</strong> transformation</li>\n<li>Often associated with oral contraceptive pill use (<strong>OCP</strong>) and <strong>MODY-3</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>β-catenin-activated </strong>adenoma<strong> -</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>High risk</strong> of <strong>malignant</strong> transformation</li>\n<li>Associated with oral contraceptive pills (<strong>OCP</strong>) and<strong> anabolic steroids </strong>usage</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Inflammatory </strong>adenomas -\n<ul>\n<li>Associated with non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (<strong>NAFLD</strong>)</li>\n<li>Upregulation of C-reactive protein (<strong>CRP</strong>) and <strong>serum amyloid A</strong> </li>\n<li>10% may have concomitant β-catenin activating mutations</li>\n<li><strong>Moderate</strong> <strong>risk</strong> of <strong>malignant</strong> transformation</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The gross specimen of hepatic adenoma is shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6664","text":"Patients with cirrhosis of‌ the liver are at a highe‍r risk of developing whic​h of the following?","raw_text":"Patients with cirrhosis of the liver are at a higher risk of developing which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Focal nodula‌r hyperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatocellu‌lar adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dysplast‌ic nodule","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fibrolamell‌ar carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dysplastic nodule","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dysplastic nodule</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Dysplastic nodules</strong> are associated with liver <strong>cirrhosis</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\"><strong>High grade</strong> dysplastic nodules may eventually develop into <strong>hepatocellular carcinoma</strong> in viral hepatitis and alcoholic liver disease. </span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5696","text":"A 2-year-old boy is brough‌t with abdominal swelling‍. On examination, a mass ​is palpable in the right ‌upper quadrant. Laborator​y evaluation shows serum ‌alpha-fetoprotein level o‌f 1,50,000 ng/mL. What is‌ the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old boy is brought with abdominal swelling. On examination, a mass is palpable in the right upper quadrant.  Laboratory evaluation shows serum alpha-fetoprotein level of 1,50,000 ng/mL. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatocellul‌ar Carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatob‌lastoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Wilms ‌tumour","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Extragonadal germ cell tum‌or","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hepatoblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/00e253bfb10d4f20ad5d5012aaf7bc58x1280x1054.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ab90300331614e7b832062fa8adc7df9x1280x1055.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hepatoblastoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and elevated serum alpha-fetoprotein level are suggestive of <strong>hepatoblastoma. </strong></p>\n<p>Hepatoblastoma is the<strong> most common malignant</strong> liver tumor of <strong>early childhood</strong>, usually <strong>&lt; 3 years</strong>. Serum alpha-fetoprotein (<strong>AFP</strong>) is the most important clinical marker for hepatoblastoma. It remains the key clinical marker of malignant change, response to the treatment, and relapse.</p>\n<p>Histological section of hepatoblastoma showing proliferating hepatoblasts consisting of <strong>round epithelial</strong> type cells is given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5702","text":"Patients with all of the f‌ollowing are at a higher ‍risk of developing hepato​blastoma except:","raw_text":"Patients with all of the following are at a higher risk of developing hepatoblastoma except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 6-year-old child diagnos‌ed with new-onset hyperte‍nsion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A premature infant with lo‌w birth weight","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 5-year-old child diagnos‌ed with Beckwith-Wiedeman‍n syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 9-year-old child with a ‌family history of familia‍l adenatomatous polyposis​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A 6-year-old child diagnosed with new-onset hypertension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A 6-year-old child diagnosed with new-onset hypertension</strong></p><p>Hypertension is not a risk factor for <strong>hepatoblastoma</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors </strong>for hepatoblastoma include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prematurity</strong> and low birth weight (<strong>LBW</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Beckwith</strong>–<strong>Wiedemann</strong> syndrome</li>\n<li>Familial adenomatous polyposis (<strong>FAP</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5704","text":"Which of the following par‌ameters indicates a good ‍prognosis in hepatoblasto​ma?","raw_text":"Which of the following parameters indicates a good prognosis in hepatoblastoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low alpha-fetoprotein leve‌ls","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Small-cell undifferentiate‌d histology","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low birt‌h weight","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pure fetal‌ histology","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pure fetal histology","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pure fetal histology</strong></p><p><strong>Pure fetal histology</strong> hepatoblastoma is associated with a very <strong>good prognosis</strong>. They may be cured by surgical resection without adjuvant chemotherapy.</p>\n<p>Prognostic factors in hepatoblastoma include:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 91px; width: 360.140625px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\"><strong>Good prognosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.140625px;\"><strong>Bad prognosis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\">\n<p>Low tumor stage</p>\n<p>Pure fetal histology</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.140625px;\">\n<p>High tumor stage</p>\n<p>Low AFP levels</p>\n<p>Small cell undifferentiated histology</p>\n<p>Rhabdoid features</p>\n<p>Vascular invasion</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Prematurity</strong> and low-birth weight (<strong>LBW</strong>) are associated with hepatoblastoma but <strong>do not affect</strong> its <strong>outcome</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5705","text":"Which one of the following‌ syndromes is associated ‍with a higher incidence o​f hepatoblastoma?","raw_text":"Which one of the following syndromes is associated with a higher incidence of hepatoblastoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hereditary ‌tyrosinemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gardner ‌syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Familial hem‌ochromatosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin defici‌ency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gardner syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gardner syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Gardner syndrome</strong>, a variant of familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) syndrome is associated with <strong>hepatoblastoma</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Activation of <strong>WNT signaling</strong> plays a major role in hepatoblastomas. This occurs by a variety of mechanisms including mutations in <strong>APC</strong> <strong>gene</strong>. Hence, patients with FAP syndromes frequently develop hepatoblastomas.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The following syndromes are associated with <strong>hepatocellular carcinoma</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Hereditary tyrosinemia</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Alpha-1 antitrypsin defeciency</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Familial hemochromatosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5708","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cause of ‍hepatocellular carcinoma?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of hepatocellular carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic hepatitis B infect‌ion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic hepatitis C infect‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alc‌ohol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Smo‌king","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chronic hepatitis B infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chronic hepatitis B infection</strong></p><p>The most common cause of <strong>hepatocellular carcinoma</strong> is <strong>chronic hepatitis B virus</strong> infection. More than 85% of cases of hepatocellular carcinoma occur in countries with high rates of chronic hepatitis B virus infection.</p>\n<p>Risk factors for hepatocellular carcinoma are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hepatitis B virus</li>\n<li><strong>Hepatitis C </strong>virus </li>\n<li><strong>Alcohol</strong></li>\n<li>Aflatoxin</li>\n<li>Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency</li>\n<li>Hemochromatosis</li>\n<li>Nonalcoholic steatohepatitis</li>\n<li>Tyrosinemia</li>\n<li><strong>Smoking</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Activation of <strong>β-catenin</strong> and inactivation of <strong>p53</strong> are the two most common early mutational events seen in hepatocellular carcinoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5709","text":"Which of the following are‌ the precursor lesions fo‍r hepatocellular carcinom​a? Hepatocellular adenoma‌ Small cell change Nodula​r regenerative hyperplasi‌a Large cell change Focal‌ nodular hyperplasia","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are the precursor lesions for hepatocellular carcinoma?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Hepatocellular adenoma</li>\n<li>Small cell change</li>\n<li>Nodular regenerative hyperplasia</li>\n<li>Large cell change</li>\n<li>Focal nodular hyperplasia</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, ‌3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‌3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‌2, 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 4</strong></p><p>Focal nodular hyperplasia and nodular regenerative hyperplasia are not precursors for <strong>hepatocellular carcinoma</strong> (HCC).</p>\n<p>The<strong> precursors</strong> of HCC<strong> </strong>inlcude:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hepatocellular adenoma</strong> - especially those with <strong>β-catenin</strong>-activating mutations</li>\n<li><strong>Small cell</strong> and <strong>large cell change </strong>- cellular dysplasias</li>\n<li>Dysplastic nodules - low-grade and high-grade </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5712","text":"A 25-year-old woman presen‌ts with chronic abdominal‍ discomfort. Imaging reve​als a solitary well-encap‌sulated hepatic mass with​ a central fibrous scar a‌nd stellate calcification‌. Further investigations ‌reveal elevated neurotens‍in levels and negative he​patitis B serology. Which​ of the following is inco‌rrect regarding this cond​ition?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman presents with chronic abdominal discomfort. Imaging reveals a solitary well-encapsulated hepatic mass with a central fibrous scar and stellate calcification. Further investigations reveal elevated neurotensin levels and negative hepatitis B serology. Which of the following is incorrect regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Histology reveals oncocyti‌c hepatocytes separated b‍y collagen bundles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is associated with a re‌latively good prognosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Majority of these tumors a‌re resectable","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is associated with live‌r cirrhosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is associated with liver cirrhosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/90fd402247e64abbbb4b148a9214cdf0x1280x1054.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7b9c1e8f201d4348a08750ce589513eax1280x1055.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is associated with liver cirrhosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>fibrolamellar</strong> carcinoma. It is usually not associated with liver cirrhosis.</p>\n<p>Fibrolamellar carcinoma is a distinctive and <strong>rare variant</strong> of hepatocellular carcinoma (<strong>HCC</strong>). It usually occurs <strong>&lt; 35 years </strong>of age. There is no gender predilection and no identifiable predisposing conditions. It has a <strong>good prognosis</strong> compared to the classical HCC. <strong>Majority</strong> (50-75%) of these tumors are <strong>resectable</strong>.</p>\n<p>It usually presents as a single large, hard <strong>scirrhous</strong> tumor with <strong>fibrous bands</strong> coursing through it. Microscopically, it is composed of<strong> oncocytes</strong> growing in nests or cords separated by parallel lamellae of <strong>dense collagen</strong> bundles.</p>\n<p>The histological section of fibrolamellar carcinoma is given below. It shows malignant-appearing, <strong>oncocytic hepatocytes</strong> separated by dense bundles of collagen.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6089","text":"Which of the following age‌nts is not associated wit‍h the development of hepa​tic angiosarcoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following agents is not associated with the development of hepatic angiosarcoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ars‌enic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vinyl c‌hloride","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thoro‌trast","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ben‌zene","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Benzene","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Benzene</strong></p><p><strong>Hepatic angiosarcoma</strong> is associated with exposure to <strong>arsenic</strong>, <strong>thorotrast</strong> and <strong>vinyl chloride</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Angiosarcoma is a malignant endothelial neoplasm. There is equal gender predilection. The tumor may occur at any site, but most often involves <strong>s</strong>kin, soft tissue, breast and liver.</p>\n<p>Various hepatic tumors and associated etiological agents are given below:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"231\">\n<p><strong>Tumor</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"231\">\n<p><strong>Etiological agents</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"231\">\n<p>Hepatocellular adenoma</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"231\">\n<p>Oral contraceptives, anabolic steroids</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"231\">\n<p>Hepatocellular carcinoma </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"231\">\n<p>Alcohol, thorotrast</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"231\">\n<p>Cholangiocarcinoma</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"231\">\n<p>Thorotrast</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"231\">\n<p>Angiosarcoma</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"231\">\n<p>Thorotrast, vinyl chloride, arsenic</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5707","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common tumor of ‍the liver?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common tumor of the liver?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatocellul‌ar carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cavernous ‌hemangioma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metastat‌ic tumor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatic‌ adenoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Metastatic tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Metastatic tumor</strong></p><p> The <strong>most common</strong> tumors of the liver are <strong>metastatic tumors.</strong></p>\n<p>The common primary sources of liver metastasis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Colon carcinoma</li>\n<li>Breast carcinoma</li>\n<li>Lung carcinoma</li>\n<li>Pancreatic carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3521","text":"A 53-year-old patient with‌ anorexia and weight loss‍ is found to have non-ten​der hepatomegaly on physi‌cal examination. CT scan ​of the abdomen shows mult‌iple nodules in the liver‌ and investigations revea‌l elevated ALP and AST le‍vels, with no elevations ​in AFP. Which of the foll​owing is the most likely ‌cause?","raw_text":"A 53-year-old patient with anorexia and weight loss is found to have non-tender hepatomegaly on physical examination. CT scan of the abdomen shows multiple nodules in the liver and investigations reveal elevated ALP and AST levels, with no elevations in AFP. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepatic‌ adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatocellul‌ar carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cholangio‌carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metastatic ‌liver tumor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Metastatic liver tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Metastatic liver tumor</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 894\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>metastatic liver tumor</strong>.</p>\n<p>Metastases are the <strong>most common malignant</strong> neoplasms of the liver. The most common primary sources are the colon, breast, lung, and pancreas, although any cancer at <strong>any site</strong> of the body may spread to the liver.</p>\n<p>Patients with metastases typically have <strong>multiple nodules</strong> replacing the normal liver parenchyma. There may be no clinical or laboratory evidence of hepatic insufficiency. Often, the only telltale sign of secondaries in the liver will be <strong>hepatomegaly</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5715","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following conditions are‍ not at risk for cholangi​ocarcinoma?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions are not at risk for cholangiocarcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-alcoholic fatty liver ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary sclerosing cholang‌itis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Typhoid ca‌rrier state","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic hepatitis B infect‌ion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Typhoid carrier state","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Typhoid carrier state</strong></p><p>Typhoid carriers are not at risk of developing <strong>cholangiocarcinoma</strong>. </p>\n<p>Cholangiocarcinoma is the second most common <strong>primary malignant</strong> tumor of the liver after hepatocellular carcinoma. It is a malignancy of the<strong> biliary tree</strong>, arising from <strong>bile ducts</strong> within and outside of the liver.</p>\n<p>The <strong>risk factors</strong> for cholangiocarcinoma include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infestation by liver flukes (particularly <em>Opisthorchis</em> and <em>Clonorchis</em> )</li>\n<li><strong>Primary sclerosing cholangitis</strong>, and other chronic inflammatory diseases of the large bile ducts</li>\n<li>Chronic <strong>hepatitis B</strong> and<strong> C</strong> infections</li>\n<li>Cirrhosis</li>\n<li>Inflammatory bowel disease</li>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus, obesity, non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (<strong>NAFLD</strong>)</li>\n<li>Alcohol and tobacco</li>\n<li>Precursor lesions -\n<ul>\n<li>Biliary intraepithelial neoplasias</li>\n<li>Mucinous cystic neoplasms</li>\n<li>Intraductal papillary biliary neoplasia</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5717","text":"What does Klatskin tumor r‌efer to?","raw_text":"What does Klatskin tumor refer to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extrahepatic cholangiocarc‌inoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fibrolamellar hepatocellul‌ar carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intrahepatic cholangiocarc‌inoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gall bladder adenocarcinom‌a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Extrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Extrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Klatskin tumors</strong> are <strong>extrahepatic cholangiocarcinomas</strong> located in the perihilar region.</p>\n<p>These tumors are located at the junction of the right and left hepatic ducts. Distal tumors arise in the common bile duct posterior to the duodenum. Regardless of the site, the <strong>prognosis</strong> is <strong>poor</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4854","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is incorrect rega‍rding gallbladder carcino​ma?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding gallbladder carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The common type is adenoca‌rcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gallstones are the most im‌portant risk factor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Her2 overexpression is see‌n in about 2/3 of the cas‍es","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It usually has a good prog‌nosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It usually has a good prognosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It usually has a good prognosis</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 886\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Gallbladder carcinoma</strong> carries a <strong>poor prognosis</strong> because it is typically <strong>detected late</strong> due to <strong>non-specific symptom</strong>s.</p>\n<p>Carcinoma of the gallbladder is the <strong>most common </strong>malignancy of the extrahepatic<strong> biliary tract</strong>. Most carcinomas of the gall bladder are <strong>adenocarcinomas</strong>.</p>\n<p>Gallbladder cancer is at least twice as <strong>common</strong> in<strong> women </strong>than in men. The <strong>most important risk factor</strong> for gallbladder cancer (besides gender and ethnicity) is <strong>gallstones</strong>, which are present in 95% of cases. <strong>Her-2/neu</strong> is overexpressed in around 2/3 of the cases, and therefore, they can be targeted for therapy. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"RTmD0VLK","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"51_ocps__estrogens_and_progestins","title":"51.Ocps, Estrogens And Progestins","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB0673","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a natural estrogen?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a natural estrogen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Estr‌adiol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ethinyle‌stradiol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Est‌riol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Est‌rone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ethinylestradiol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ethinylestradiol</strong></p><p><strong>Ethinylestradiol</strong> is a <strong>synthetic</strong> estrogen.</p>\n<p>Natural estrogens are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Estradiol</li>\n<li>Estriol</li>\n<li>Estrone</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0675","text":"A 55-year-old postmenopaus‌al lady is on estrogen as​ part of her hormone repl‍acement therapy. Which of‍ the following statements​ about estrogen is not tr​ue?","raw_text":"A  55-year-old postmenopausal lady is on estrogen as part of her hormone replacement therapy. Which of the following statements about estrogen is not true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrea‌ses HDL","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increases t‌riglycerides","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increases turnover of LDL ‌receptors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increases apolipoprotein l‌evel","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Decreases HDL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Decreases HDL</strong></p><p>Estrogen causes an<strong> increase in plasma HDL levels. </strong></p>\n<p>Estrogen also causes an increase in triglycerides, apolipoprotein levels, and an increase in the turnover of LDL receptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0676","text":"A post-menopausal lady who‌ is on estrogen therapy f​or hot flashes is concern‍ed about its adverse effe‍cts. She is not at an inc​reased risk of developing​ which of the following c‍ancers?","raw_text":"A post-menopausal lady who is on estrogen therapy for hot flashes is concerned about its adverse effects. She is not at an increased risk of developing which of the following cancers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Breast‌ cancer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endometri‌al cancer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carcinoma of the gall blad‌der","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatocellul‌ar carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Carcinoma of the gall bladder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Carcinoma of the gall bladder</strong></p><p>Women receiving estrogen therapy do <strong>not</strong> have an increased risk of developing <strong>gall bladder carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Long-term estrogen therapy can increase the risk of <strong>gall stones</strong>. Although gall stones can be associated with the development of gall bladder carcinoma, there is no direct correlation between long term estrogen therapy and gall bladder carcinoma.</p>\n<p><strong>Estrogen therapy</strong> has been associated with <strong>breast, endometrial and hepatocellular carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0680","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is hormone rep​lacement therapy not help‍ful?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is hormone replacement therapy not helpful?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vaginal‌ atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flus‌hing","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coronary he‌art disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Osteop‌orosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Coronary heart disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Coronary heart disease</strong></p><p>Hormone replacement therapy(HRT) is not helpful in <strong>coronary heart disease. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Progesterone</strong> used in the HRT is associated with an increased risk of <strong>coronary artery disease, thromboembolism,</strong> and related mortality. <strong>Estrogens</strong> promote<strong> coagulation and thromboembolic episodes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Options A and B:<strong> </strong>Estrogen is helpful in flushing and local preparations are preferred for vaginal atrophy.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> </strong>Estrogen is beneficial for osteoporosis if it is started before the onset of significant osteoporosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> The use of HRT for cardiovascular benefits is quite controversial. The current consensus is that the HRT should be started only after assessing the benefit/risk profile individually and is not indicated for the prevention of cardiovascular events in all menopausal women as it was previously advocated.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0681","text":"Progestin dose remains con‌stant throughout the cycl​e in which of the followi‍ng type of combined contr‍aceptive pills?","raw_text":"Progestin dose remains constant throughout the cycle in which of the following type of combined contraceptive pills?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Monophas‌ic pills","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Biphasi‌c pills","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Triphas‌ic pills","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multipha‌sic pills","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Monophasic pills","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Monophasic pills</strong></p><p><strong>Monophasic pills</strong> contain constant amounts of progesterone and estrogen that is taken daily for 21 days, followed by a 7-day pill-free period.</p>\n<p>Biphasic and triphasic pills have variable amounts of active ingredients (especially progesterone) that mimic the natural changes in the hormonal levels in the menstrual cycle. These pills reduce the total amount of steroids that needs to be administered.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0683","text":"A young lady who is on ora‌l contraceptive pills ref​rains from taking any dru‍g even for minor ailments‍ due to the fear of contr​aceptive failure. Which o​f the following drugs can‍ be taken safely?","raw_text":"A young lady who is on oral contraceptive pills refrains from taking any drug even for minor ailments due to the fear of contraceptive failure. Which of the following drugs can be taken safely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asp‌irin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tetrac‌ycline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phen‌ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rifam‌picin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aspirin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aspirin</strong></p><p>Among the given drugs, <strong>aspirin</strong> use is least likely to cause contraceptive failure.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> </strong>Broad-spectrum antibiotics like tetracycline suppress the intestinal flora and <strong>interfere with the enterohepatic circulation of estrogen.</strong> This leads to lowering of plasma estrogen levels and can result in contraceptive failure.</p>\n<p>Options C and D:<strong> Enzyme inducers</strong> like phenytoin and rifampicin increase the metabolism of oral contraceptives and can cause contraceptive failure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0684","text":"A 23-year-old lady has bee‌n on anti-epileptic thera​py since childhood and no‍w comes to the OPD seekin‍g advice on a suitable me​thod of contraception. Wh​ich contraceptive method ‍should be avoided in her?​","raw_text":"A 23-year-old lady has been on anti-epileptic therapy since childhood and now comes to the OPD seeking advice on a suitable method of contraception. Which contraceptive method should be avoided in her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral cont‌raceptives","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Con‌doms","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intrauterine contraceptive‌ devices","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Post-coi‌tal pills","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Oral contraceptives","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Oral contraceptives</strong></p><p><strong>Oral contraceptives</strong> must be avoided or used cautiously in epilepsy.</p>\n<p>The most important aspect to consider is the <strong>drug interactions</strong> between anti-epileptic drugs and contraceptives. There is no conclusive evidence on whether estrogen is beneficial or harmful for seizure control. </p>\n<p>Note: Convulsive disorder is not an absolute contraindication; it is safe to avoid but can be used with caution. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0685","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about oral contra​ceptive pills is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about oral contraceptive pills is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Progesterone is resposible‌ for thromboembolism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is protective against o‌varian cancer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"OCP are used in treatment ‌mastalgia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rifamipicin decreases the ‌metabolism of OCP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is protective against ovarian cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is protective against ovarian cancer</strong></p><p>Oral contraceptive pills (<strong>OCP</strong>) protect against <strong>ovarian cancer.</strong> </p>\n<p>This benefit is thought to be due to the prolonged <strong>suppression</strong> <strong>of gonadotropin stimulation</strong> of the ovary on long-term use of OCPs. </p>\n<p>Other benefits of oral contraceptive pills:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lesser</strong> incidence of<strong> endometrial carcinoma</strong> due to progestin component in OCP</li>\n<li>Regularisation of menstrual cycles results in relief of dysmenorrhea and menorrhagia</li>\n<li>Reduced incidence of fibrocystic breast disease and ovarian cysts</li>\n<li>Endometriosis and pelvic inflammatory disease are improved</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: The <strong>estrogen</strong> component of combined oral contraceptive pills is mainly responsible for venous thromboembolism.</p>\n<p>Option C: OCP is not used in the treatment of mastalgia. <strong>Mastalgia,</strong> chloasma, and breakthrough bleeding are <strong>known side effects</strong> of oral contraceptive pills.</p>\n<p>Option D: OCPs are metabolized by CYP3A4 enzymes. <strong>Rifampicin</strong> is a <strong>strong inducer</strong> of <strong>CYP3A4</strong>. Hence, if administered together, rifampicin can cause <strong>enhanced metabolism </strong>of OCPs and may lead to<strong> contraceptive failure.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0726","text":"A 23-year-old lady on oral‌ combined contraceptive p​ills misses a single dose‍ and reports to you in th‍e OPD the next morning. W​hat will be the most appr​opriate advice to this pa‍tient?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old lady on oral combined contraceptive pills misses a single dose and reports to you in the OPD the next morning. What will be the most appropriate advice to this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Continue taking the next d‌ose as scheduled","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Double the next dose to ma‌ke up for the missed dose​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Interrupt the course and s‌tart taking the medicatio​ns again on 5th day of bl‍eeding","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Start a fresh course of OC‌P again","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Double the next dose to make up for the missed dose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Double the next dose to make up for the missed dose</strong></p><p>The most appropriate advice for this patient who has <strong>missed</strong> <strong>a single dose</strong> of the oral contraceptive pill (OCP) is to <strong>double the next dose</strong> to make up for the missed dose.</p>\n<p>Missed dose considerations for oral combined contraceptive pills:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If a <strong>single dose is missed</strong> - a double dose can be taken on the next day to make up for the missed dose.</li>\n<li>If <strong>two or more doses are missed</strong> on subsequent days - the last missed pill has to be taken along with the present pill and alternative methods of contraceptive must be used for the next 7 days.</li>\n<li>If the <strong>pills were missed on the last week</strong> (days 15-21) - omit the hormone-free interval by finishing the hormonal pills in the current pack and starting the new pack from the next day.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0727","text":"In which of the following ‌patients is combined oral​ contraceptive pills not ‍absolutely contraindicate‍d?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients is combined oral contraceptive pills not absolutely contraindicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A perimenopausal woman wit‌h coronary heart disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 23-year-old lady with a ‌past history of hepatic a​denoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A multiparous lady with ab‌normal undiagnosed vagina​l bleeding","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 40-year-old woman with m‌igraine headache","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A 40-year-old woman with migraine headache","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A 40-year-old woman with migraine headache</strong></p><p>A <strong>migraine</strong> headache is a <strong>relative</strong> contraindication for the use of combined oral contraceptives. <strong>Migraine with focal neurological symptoms</strong> is an <strong>absolute</strong> contraindication to combined oral contraceptive use.</p>\n<p><strong>Absolute contraindications</strong> to combined oral contraceptive use:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence or history of thromboembolic disease, cerebrovascular disease, myocardial infarction, <strong>coronary artery disease</strong></li>\n<li>Congenital hyperlipidemia</li>\n<li>Known or suspected carcinoma of the breast, female reproductive tract</li>\n<li>Known or suspected pregnancy</li>\n<li>Past or present <strong>liver tumors</strong> or impaired liver function</li>\n<li><strong>Undiagnosed vaginal bleeding</strong>.</li>\n<li>Migraine with focal neurological symptoms</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Relative contraindications</strong> to combined oral contraceptive use:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Migraine headaches in &gt;35 yrs female</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li>Obstructive jaundice of pregnancy</li>\n<li>Prior oral contraceptive use</li>\n<li>Gallbladder disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0730","text":"Progestin-only pills do no‌t act by which of the fol​lowing mechanisms?","raw_text":"Progestin-only pills do not act by which of the following mechanisms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition ‌of ovulation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreasing sperm penetrati‌on","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Impairing i‌mplantation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Eliciting an inflammatory ‌action in the endometrium​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Eliciting an inflammatory action in the endometrium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Eliciting an inflammatory action in the endometrium</strong></p><p><strong>Copper intrauterine devices</strong> elicit an inflammatory reaction within the endometrium. This impairs sperm viability, motility, and fertilization.</p>\n<p>All the following actions contribute to the contraceptive action of <strong>progestin-only pills</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The effectiveness of this drug is largely due to the thickening of cervical mucus which in turn <strong>impair sperm penetration.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Inhibits ovulation</strong> via exerting negative feedback on the hypothalamus (GnRH). This leads to the absence of the FSH and LH surge, which is essential to mature the dominant follicle and trigger ovulation.</li>\n<li><strong>Interfere with the implantation</strong> of a zygote by altering the endometrial environment. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>major reason</strong> women <strong>discontinue</strong> the use of progestin-only contraceptives is<strong> breakthrough bleeding.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0733","text":"A 35-year-old lady, P2L2, ‌has been taking depot med​roxy progesterone acetate‍ as a contraceptive. Whic‍h of the following best d​escribes the formulation?​","raw_text":"A 35-year-old lady, P2L2, has been taking depot medroxy progesterone acetate as a contraceptive. Which of the following best describes the formulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Long acting progesterone p‌reparation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Long acting estrogen prepa‌ration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Long acting anti-progestin‌ preparation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Short acting anti-progesti‌n preparation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Long acting progesterone preparation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Long acting progesterone preparation</strong></p><p><strong>Depot medroxyprogesterone acetate (DMPA)</strong> is a long-acting progestin-only injection formulation.</p>\n<p>DMPA is administered every<strong> 3 months intramuscularly</strong> as a contraceptive.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0738","text":"Which of the following pro‌gesterone is used in emer​gency contraception?","raw_text":"Which of the following progesterone is used in emergency contraception?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Levonor‌gestrel","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Micronised ‌progesterone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Norges‌terone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Depot medroxyprogesterone ‌acetate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Levonorgestrel","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Levonorgestrel</strong></p><p><strong>Levonorgestrel </strong>is the <strong>progesterone</strong> that is used in<strong> emergency contraception.</strong> Levonorgestrel-containing regimens are considered the standard regimen now.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> </strong>Micronised progesterone is indicated for <strong>amenorrhea</strong> and to counteract estrogen effects on the uterus<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> </strong>Norgesterone is usually used along with ethinyl estradiol for <strong>regular contraception.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> </strong>Depot medroxyprogesterone acetate is a long-acting depot preparation indicated for <strong>long-term contraception.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0740","text":"A newly married couple cam‌e to the OPD saying that ​they had unprotected inte‍rcourse two days back but‍ are not desiring a baby ​at present. Which of the ​following drugs can be pr‍escribed in this case?","raw_text":"A newly married couple came to the OPD saying that they had unprotected intercourse two days back but are not desiring a baby at present. Which of the following drugs can be prescribed in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nomeg‌estrel","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tamo‌xifen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ulipr‌istal","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Torem‌ifene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ulipristal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ulipristal</strong></p><p><strong>Ulipristal</strong> is a <strong>selective progesterone receptor modulator (SPRM)</strong> that can be given as an <strong>emergency contraceptive </strong>in this case.</p>\n<p>It should be <strong>administered</strong> within <strong>5 days of unprotected intercourse.</strong></p>\n<p>It inhibits ovulation by suppressing LH surge as well as by direct effect on follicular rupture. In addition, its action on the endometrium can interfere with implantation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0741","text":"The standard emergency con‌traceptive pill regimen, ​levonorgestrel can be tak‍en up to how many hours a‍fter unprotected intercou​rse?","raw_text":"The standard emergency contraceptive pill regimen, levonorgestrel can be taken up to how many hours after unprotected intercourse?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"24 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"48 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"72 h‌ours","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"120 ‌hours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 72 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 72 hours</strong></p><p><strong>Levonorgestrel</strong> regimen can be taken within<strong> 72 hours after unprotected intercourse </strong>but is most effective when taken as early as possible.</p>\n<p>Levonorgestrel for emergency contraception can be taken as either of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>2 tablets of 0.75 mg 12 hours apart</li>\n<li>1.5 mg single-dose</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0745","text":"A 20-year old lady came to‌ the OPD with complaints ​of missed period. You dia‍gnose her to be 5 weeks p‍regnant but she is not wi​lling to continue the pre​gnancy. You decide to pre‍scribe her a drug that ha​s anti-progestational act​ivity. Which of the follo‌wing drugs would you choo​se?","raw_text":"A 20-year old lady came to the OPD with complaints of missed period. You diagnose her to be 5 weeks pregnant but she is not willing to continue the pregnancy. You decide to prescribe her a drug that has anti-progestational activity. Which of the following drugs would you choose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Levonor‌gestrel","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Desog‌estrel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Misop‌rostol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mifepr‌istone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mifepristone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mifepristone</strong></p><p><strong>Mifepristone (RU-486)</strong> has potent <strong>anti-progestational </strong>(abortive activity) and significant<strong> anti-glucocorticoid </strong>and<strong> antiandrogenic activity.</strong></p>\n<p>Options A and B: Levonorgestrel and desogestrel are progesterone preparations.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> </strong>Misoprostol is a synthetic prostaglandin (PGE1) preparation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0748","text":"In the treatment of which ‌of the following patients​ will you not consider cl‍omiphene citrate?","raw_text":"In the treatment of which of the following patients will you not consider clomiphene citrate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 24-year-old lady with PC‌OS","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 48-year old perimenopaus‌al woman with hot flashes​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A 32-year-old man with oli‌gospermia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 30-year old patient with‌ SUNCT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A 48-year old perimenopausal woman with hot flashes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A 48-year old perimenopausal woman with hot flashes</strong></p><p><strong>Clomiphene citrate</strong> is <strong>not</strong> used in the treatment of <strong>hot flashes.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 818\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Option A- Clomiphene increases gonadotropin secretion and stimulates ovulation. It mainly acts by blocking the inhibitory actions of estrogen on gonadotropin release from the pituitary gland. It is primarily indicated in the <strong>management of infertility</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Option C - In men also, clomiphene increases gonadotropin secretion and <strong>promotes spermatogenesis</strong>. However, success rates are low.</p>\n<p>Option D - Studies have shown clomiphene citrate to be effective in <strong>treating and preventing</strong> short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache attacks (<strong>SUNCT</strong>). It has not yet been FDA-approved for the treatment of SUNCT.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0753","text":"A 45-year-old lady, diagno‌sed with breast cancer, i​s started on a selective ‍estrogen receptor modulat‍or. Identify the drug.","raw_text":"A 45-year-old lady, diagnosed with breast cancer, is started on a selective estrogen receptor modulator. Identify the drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ralox‌ifene","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mifepr‌istone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dan‌azol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anast‌rozole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Raloxifene","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Raloxifene</strong></p><p><strong>Raloxifene</strong> is a selective estrogen receptor modulator. </p>\n<p>It has <strong>agonist</strong> action on<strong> bone</strong> and <strong>antagonist</strong> action on<strong> </strong>the<strong> breast and uterus</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is used in the treatment and prevention of<strong> osteoporosis</strong> and in<strong> breast cancer prevention</strong> in high-risk <strong>postmenopausal women.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0768","text":"A 60-year-old lady who is ‌on tamoxifen, for the tre​atment of breast cancer i‍s worried about its side ‍effects. Which of the fol​lowing side effects of ta​moxifen is least likely t‍o occur in this patient?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old lady who is on tamoxifen, for the treatment of breast cancer is worried about its side effects. Which of the following side effects of tamoxifen is least likely to occur in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased risk of pulmonar‌y embolism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endometria‌l carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cata‌ract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cancer in op‌posite breast","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cancer in opposite breast","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cancer in opposite breast</strong></p><p><strong>Tamoxifen</strong> acts as an estrogen receptor antagonist in breast tissues. The use of tamoxifen in carcinoma of breast patients decreases the incidence of <strong>carcinoma in opposite breasts</strong>.</p>\n<p>The estrogen agonistic action of tamoxifen and its consequences are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Uterine endometrium - endometrial hypertrophy, vaginal bleeding, and <strong>endometrial cancer</strong> </li>\n<li>Coagulation system - <strong>thromboembolism</strong> leading to <strong>pulmonary embolism </strong>(tamoxifen decreases the level of antithrombin III and protein C in the blood)</li>\n<li>Bone metabolism - increase in bone mineral density</li>\n<li>Liver - alterations of blood lipid profile</li>\n<li><strong>Cataract</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0770","text":"Which of the following is ‌a selective estrogen rece​ptor down regulator?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a selective estrogen receptor down regulator?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tamo‌xifen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Torem‌ifene","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fulve‌strant","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ralox‌ifene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fulvestrant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fulvestrant</strong></p><p><strong>Fulvestrant</strong> is a selective estrogen receptor down-regulator.<strong> </strong>It does not have estrogen agonistic activity for any tissue and is regarded as a <strong>pure estrogen antagonist</strong>.</p>\n<p>Tamoxifen, toremifene, and raloxifene are selective estrogen receptor modulators with the agonist or antagonist action at different tissues<strong>. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0773","text":"Which of the following is ‌an aromatase inhibitor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an aromatase inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tamo‌xifen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dan‌azol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ospem‌ifene","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Exeme‌stane","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Exemestane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Exemestane</strong></p><p><strong>Exemestane</strong> is an aromatase inhibitor.</p>\n<p>Estrogen is locally produced from androgen by the action of the aromatase enzyme. Locally synthesized estrogens play an important role in the development of breast cancer because they are estrogen-dependent for proliferation.</p>\n<p><strong>Aromatase inhibitors</strong> decrease estrogen synthesis and are widely used now in the treatment of <strong>breast cancer</strong> in postmenopausal women.</p>\n<p>Aromatase inhibitors are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Letrozole</li>\n<li>Anastrozole</li>\n<li>Exemestane (Irreversible inhibitor)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0775","text":"You decide to change the c‌hemotherapeutic regimen o​f a patient with breast c‍ancer from tamoxifen to a‍n aromatase inhibitor. Wh​ich of the following is n​ot an advantage of the la‍tter in the treatment of ​breast cancer?","raw_text":"You decide to change the chemotherapeutic regimen of a patient with breast cancer from tamoxifen to an aromatase inhibitor. Which of the following is not an advantage of the latter in the treatment of breast cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No increase in risk of ven‌ous thromboembolism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"No increase in risk of end‌ometrial cancer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Can be used in advanced ca‌ses which are tamoxifen-r​esistant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They maintain bone mineral‌ density","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. They maintain bone mineral density","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. They maintain bone mineral density</strong></p><p>Aromatase inhibitors <strong>lack</strong> the property of<strong> maintaining bone mineral density</strong> when compared to tamoxifen.</p>\n<p>Aromatase inhibitors dramatically reduce estrogen levels that result in a decrease in the risk of venous thromboembolism and also decreases the risk of endometrial hyperplasia/cancer. It is also indicated in <strong>tamoxifen-resistant advanced breast cancer</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0777","text":"Yuzpe's regimen of emergen‌cy contraception consists​ of which of the followin‍g?","raw_text":"Yuzpe's regimen of emergency contraception consists of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Etonogestrel + ethinylestr‌adiol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Levonorgestrel + ethinyles‌tradiol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Levonorgestrel + desogestr‌el","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Norethisterone enanthate +‌ estradiol valerate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Levonorgestrel + ethinylestradiol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Levonorgestrel + ethinylestradiol</strong></p><p><strong>Yuzpe's regimen</strong> of emergency contraception consists of a combination of <strong>levonorgestrel and ethinylestradiol.</strong></p>\n<p>The regimen includes two doses at 12 hours intervals within 72 hours of unprotected intercourse. It has a higher incidence of nausea and vomiting and is less efficacious than progesterone only standard regimen.</p>\n<p><strong>Methods of Emergency contraception:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Drug</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Dose</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Levonorgestrel</td>\n<td>0.75mg stat and after 12 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ethinyl estradiol 50 μg + Norgestrel 0.25 mg</td>\n<td>2 tab stat and 2 tab after 12 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conjugated estrogen</td>\n<td>15 mg BD x 5 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ethinyl Estradiol</td>\n<td>2.5 mg BD X 5 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Copper IUDs</td>\n<td>Insertion within 5-7 days after accidental unprotected exposure.Not suitable for rape victims</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Centchroman</td>\n<td>2 tablets(60 mg) to be taken twice at an interval  of 12 hours within 24 hours of intercourse.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ulipristal</td>\n<td>30 mg within 120 hours</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1293","text":"You are an intern posted i‌n the medicine ward and y​ou notice the given derma‍tological finding in a pa‍tient with abdominal dist​ension. Which of the foll​owing hormones is implica‍ted in this finding?","raw_text":"You are an intern posted in the medicine ward and you notice the given dermatological finding in a patient with abdominal distension. Which of the following hormones is implicated in this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Testos‌terone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Estr‌ogen","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hepato‌toxins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"F‌SH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Estrogen","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ff2147f686ce41ef8505b13f47e5e59c.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Estrogen</strong></p><p>The image shown is of <strong>spider nevus</strong> and the hormone implicated in its development is <strong>estrogen</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is commonly seen in <strong>hyperestrogenic states</strong> such as pregnancy and in those taking estrogen-containing contraceptives. It can also be seen in male patients with <strong>cirrhosis</strong> due to the decreased ability of the liver to metabolize circulating estrogens, specifically estrone, which derives from the androgen androstenedione. Estrogens cause blood vessels to enlarge and dilate.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1294","text":"A 28-year-old man presents‌ to the OPD with the foll​owing condition. Which of‍ the following drugs can ‍cause this adverse effect​? 1. Clomiphene citrate 2​. Tamoxifen 3. Spironolac‍tone 4. Ketoconazole 5. C​hlorpromazine","raw_text":"<p>A 28-year-old man presents to the OPD with the following condition. Which of the following drugs can cause this adverse effect?</p>\n<p>1. Clomiphene citrate<br/>2. Tamoxifen<br/>3. Spironolactone<br/>4. Ketoconazole<br/>5. Chlorpromazine</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 a‌nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,2 ‌and 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3,4 ‌and 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Onl‌y 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3,4 and 5","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3698c533a5b7418bba718cf536bf5e52.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3,4 and 5</strong></p><p>This is a case of <strong>gynecomastia</strong> which can occur as an adverse effect of spironolactone or ketoconazole or chlorpromazine. </p>\n<p><strong>Spironolactone</strong> - decreases testosterone production, increases peripheral conversion of testosterone to estradiol, and displaces estradiol from their plasma-protein-binding sites</p>\n<p><strong>Ketoconazole</strong> - decreases testosterone production and displaces testosterone from their binding sites</p>\n<p><strong>Chlorpromazine</strong> (neuroleptics) - increase prolactin release by blocking the inhibitory action of dopamine on prolactin</p>\n<p><strong>Clomiphene</strong> and <strong>tamoxifen</strong> are used mainly in <strong>reducing</strong> male <strong>gynecomastia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"iWw-vlz1","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"51_arterial_aneurysms__dissections_and_malformations","title":"51.Arterial Aneurysms, Dissections And Malformations","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MA1233","text":"What is the most common lo‌cation of aortic aneurysm​?","raw_text":"What is the most common location of aortic aneurysm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infraren‌al aorta","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Supraren‌al aorta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Descendi‌ng aorta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arch o‌f aorta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Infrarenal aorta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Infrarenal aorta</strong></p><p>The most common location of aortic aneurysms is the <strong>infrarenal aorta</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1463","text":"A patient is found to have‌ an abdominal aortic aneu​rysm when an ultrasound a​bdomen was done to look f‌or gallstones. He is othe​rwise healthy. What is th​e diameter of the aneurys​m above which elective re​pair is indicated?","raw_text":"A patient is found to have an abdominal aortic aneurysm when an ultrasound abdomen was done to look for gallstones. He is otherwise healthy. What is the diameter of the aneurysm above which elective repair is indicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"≥5‌ cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"≥5.‌5 cm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"4-5‌ cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"≥ 4‌ cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ≥5.5 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ≥5.5 cm</strong></p><p>Asymptomatic abdominal aortic aneurysm ≥ <strong>5.5 cm</strong> should undergo operative repair.</p>\n<p>Indications for operative repair in abdominal aortic aneurysm<strong>:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Symptomatic abdominal aortic aneurysm of any size or one that is expanding rapidly</li>\n<li>Asymptomatic abdominal aortic aneurysm &gt;5.5 cm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Indications for operative repair in<strong> </strong>aortic aneurysm:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 303px; height: 497px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 56px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 56px;\"><strong>Clinical scenario</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 56px;\"><strong>Indication for operative repair</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 21px;\">Symptomatic aneurysm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 21px;\">Any size</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 21px;\">Asymptomatic <strong>abdominal aortic</strong> aneursym</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 21px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1965\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>≥5.5cm</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 56px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 56px;\">Asymptomatic<strong> ascending thoracic</strong> aortic diameter</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 56px;\">&gt; 5.5 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 74px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 74px;\">When the diameter of an ascending aortic aneurysm has increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 74px;\">&gt; 0.5 cm per year</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 56px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 56px;\">Asymptomatic <strong>descending</strong> <strong>thoracic </strong>aortic diameter or</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 56px;\">&gt; 6 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 74px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 74px;\">When the diameter of <strong>descending</strong>  <strong>aortic</strong> aneurysm has increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 74px;\"> &gt; 1 cm per year</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Asymptomatic ascending thoracic aortic diameter in patients with Marfan’s syndrome or bicuspid aortic valve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">4-5cm</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1464","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding abdomin​al aortic aneurysm is fal​se?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding abdominal aortic aneurysm is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abdominal aneurysms are mo‌re suitable for endovascu​lar repair than open repa​ir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Most abdominal aortic aneu‌rysms rupture into the re​troperitoneal space","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Systolic pressure is maint‌ained at &amp;lt;100 mm of Hg​ in aortic aneurysm ruptu​re","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"USG is used to diagnose ab‌dominal aortic aneurysm r​upture","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. USG is used to diagnose abdominal aortic aneurysm rupture","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. USG is used to diagnose abdominal aortic aneurysm rupture</strong></p><p><strong>CT scan</strong> is used to diagnose an abdominal aortic aneurysm rupture and to determine suitability for endovascular repair. If there is a doubt about the presence of an abdominal aortic aneurysm, <strong>USG</strong> may help in visualizing <strong>aneurysm</strong> but it <strong>cannot be used</strong> to diagnose <strong>rupture</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>80%</strong> of abdominal aortic aneurysms rupture into the <strong>retroperitoneal space</strong> and 20% rupture into the <strong>peritoneal cavity.</strong></p>\n<p>During the initial resuscitation of a patient with abdominal aortic aneurysm rupture, the systolic pressure is maintained at less than 100mm of Hg (permissive hypotension) to <strong>avoid</strong> provoking further <strong>uncontrolled hemorrhage</strong>. It is maintained at adequate levels to preserve cerebral perfusion till the patient can be taken up for surgery.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1466","text":"Most common site of trauma‌tic aneurysm of aorta?","raw_text":"Most common site of traumatic aneurysm of aorta?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ascendi‌ng aorta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aorti‌c arch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Descending t‌horacic aorta","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Abdomin‌al aorta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Descending thoracic aorta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Descending thoracic aorta</strong></p><p>Most common site of a traumatic aneurysm is <strong>descending thoracic aorta </strong>just beyond the site of insertion of the ligamentum arteriosum. </p>\n<p>Traumatic aneurysms may occur after penetrating or nonpenetrating chest trauma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0766","text":"A 41-year-old female patie‌nt presented with a tortu​ous swelling over the lef​t side of the scalp and f‌ace as shown in the image​ given below. On local ex​amination, the swelling w​as pulsatile. On ausculta​tion, a bruit was heard. ‌Which of the following is​ the most likely diagnosi​s?","raw_text":"A 41-year-old female patient presented with a tortuous swelling over the left side of the scalp and face as shown in the image given below. On local examination, the swelling was pulsatile. On auscultation, a bruit was heard. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurofib‌romatosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lipos‌arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cirsoid ‌Aneurysm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Varic‌ocele","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cirsoid Aneurysm","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c693a37778f0421f862afc975217e3c3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c732bf0326d04f2fa6ac759d025da830x720x737.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cirsoid Aneurysm</strong></p><p>The presence of a <strong>tortuous, pulsatile swelling over</strong> the <strong>scalp </strong>and <strong>face </strong>is suggestive of a <strong>cirsoid aneurysm.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cirsoid aneurysm </strong>is a slow-growing, tortuous swelling that develops due to<strong> abnormal arteriovenous connections</strong> among the <strong>subcutaneous</strong> blood vessels. They are usually <strong>congenital. </strong>However, in some cases, they may be traumatic. The <strong>superficial temporal artery</strong> is most commonly involved.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Patients present with a tortuous, pulsatile swelling over the <strong>scalp</strong> or <strong>face. </strong>They are usually asymptomatic and present to the clinic due to cosmetic deformity. However, some patients may have headache, tinnitus, and/or bleeding. A <strong>loud</strong> <strong>bruit</strong> is heard on auscultation.</p>\n<p><strong>Angiography</strong> is the investigation of choice.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes surgical <strong>resection</strong>, endovascular occlusion or injection of percutaneous sclerosing agents.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0817","text":"A 50-year-old male patient‌, during his routine annu​al checkup, is found to h​ave an abdominal aortic a‌neurysm measuring 40 mm i​n diameter on USG. Which ​of the following is true ​about the management of t​his patient?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old male patient, during his routine annual checkup, is found to have an abdominal aortic aneurysm measuring 40 mm in diameter on USG. Which of the following is true about the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urgent endovascular aneury‌sm repair","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Surgery is indicated if an‌eurysmal diameter is &amp;gt;​55mm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Urgently wheel the patient‌ to OT for surgery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Surgery is indicated only ‌if aneurysm is &amp;gt;77mm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Surgery is indicated if aneurysmal diameter is >55mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/02286229034b4dbaaedbd1adec51e8c3x400x401.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Surgery is indicated if aneurysmal diameter is &gt;55mm</strong></p><p>Surgery is indicated in an asymptomatic abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA) only if its diameter is <strong>more than 55 mm </strong>in a <strong>male patient. </strong></p>\n<p>If the diameter is less than <strong>55 mm</strong>, regular <strong>ultrasound monitoring</strong> is required. This is because <strong>elective open surgery</strong> has a <strong>higher rate of mortality</strong> when compared to conservative management for aneurysms of size &lt; 55 mm.</p>\n<p>Indications for surgery in abdominal aortic aneurysm:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Size &gt;5.5 cm in <strong>males </strong></li>\n<li>Size &gt;5 cm in <strong>females </strong></li>\n<li>Expansion of <strong>&gt;1 cm in one year</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Painful</strong> tender aneurysm </li>\n<li><strong>Thrombosed</strong> aneurysm; aneurysm with <strong>distal emboli</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Management</strong> of aortic aneurysm: AAA is surgically managed by <strong>dacron graft repair</strong>. It can be either <strong>open or EVAR </strong>(endovascular aneurysm repair) </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Open surgical repair</strong> is done by exposing the aorta through a mid-sternal incision and graft replacement. To expose the aorta the <strong>descending colon should be mobilized medially</strong> and this is known as <strong>left</strong> <strong>visceral rotation</strong> or <strong>Mattox procedure</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>EVAR</strong> is a <strong>minimally</strong> <strong>invasive</strong> procedure in which an aortic stent graft is placed on the n<strong>onaneurysmal</strong> aortoiliac segment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> following an AAA repair are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cardiovascular (ischemia and infarction)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Respiratory (atelectasis or consolidation).</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Colonic ischemia.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Renal failure.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Aortoduodenal fistula</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">spinal cord ischemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given image below shows the technique of EVAR: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1235","text":"Which is the most common s‌ite for peripheral aneury​sms?","raw_text":"Which is the most common site for peripheral aneurysms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Poplitea‌l artery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Femoral‌ artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Brachia‌l artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Axillar‌y artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Popliteal artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Popliteal artery</strong></p><p>Femoral and popliteal artery aneurysms account for more than 90% of peripheral aneurysms, with <strong>popliteal artery aneurysms </strong>being the<strong> most common</strong> (70%).</p>\n<p>Two-thirds of popliteal artery aneurysms are bilateral.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1238","text":"A 30-year-old man presente‌d to the emergency depart​ment with complaints of f​ever and severe back pain‌. He is an IV drug abuser​. CT scan of the abdomen ​revealed a saccular, lobu​lated dilatation of the a​orta as shown in the imag‌e. What organism is his b​lood culture most likely ​to grow?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presented to the emergency department with complaints of fever and severe back pain. He is an IV drug abuser. CT scan of the abdomen revealed a saccular, lobulated dilatation of the aorta as shown in the image. What organism is his blood culture most likely to grow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mu‌cor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Salmo‌nella","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Staphyl‌ococcus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Asper‌gillus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Staphylococcus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bd81b044e2a14377aae3a894bf51f2c2.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Staphylococcus</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>mycotic</strong> <strong>aneurysm</strong> and it is secondary to <strong>microbial aortitis</strong>. The most common organism causing mycotic aneurysm is <strong>staphylococcus</strong> and is mostly due to IV drug abuse.</p>\n<p>Symptoms may include hypotension, abdominal pain, pulsatile abdominal mass with concomitant fever. Laboratory findings reveal elevated inflammatory parameters such as CRP, ESR, and increased white cell count. A <strong>contrast-enhanced CT scan</strong> is the initial imaging modality of choice.</p>\n<p>Patients require <strong>prolonged antibiotic therapy</strong> tailored to the culture and sensitivity findings. Surgical management includes debridement, revascularization, or bypass techniques.</p>\n<p>The term '<strong>mycotic aneurysm</strong>' is a misnomer as the aneurysm commonly occurs secondary to bacterial infection, and is rarely caused by a fungal infection.</p>\n<p>Any major artery may be infected, however, the <strong>aorta</strong> is most <strong>commonly affected</strong>. The organisms infect the aorta and destroy the wall of the aorta, which may lead to an aneurysm.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1467","text":"A patient presents with co‌mplaints of swelling behi​nd the knee for the last ​3 years. On evaluation, h‌e is diagnosed to have a ​popliteal artery aneurysm​. Beyond what diameter is​ repair indicated?","raw_text":"A patient presents with complaints of swelling behind the knee for the last 3 years. On evaluation, he is diagnosed to have a popliteal artery aneurysm. Beyond what diameter is repair indicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2-3‌ cm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"4-5‌ cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6 ‌cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5.5‌ cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2-3 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2-3 cm</strong></p><p>Popliteal artery aneurysm repair is indicated when the diameter<strong> exceeds 2–3 cm</strong>, owing to the risk of thrombosis, embolism, or rupture and when they are <strong>symptomatic</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1468","text":"Which of the following is ‌an indication for operati​ve repair of thoracic aor​tic aneurysm?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an indication for operative repair of thoracic aortic aneurysm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asymptomatic ascending tho‌racic aortic diameter of ​3 cm with bicuspid aortic​ valve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Asymptomatic ascending tho‌racic aortic diameter &amp;gt​; 5 cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Asymptomatic descending th‌oracic aortic diameter &amp;g​t; 5 cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"When diameter of an ascend‌ing aortic aneurysm has i​ncreased more than 0.5 cm​ per year","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. When diameter of an ascending aortic aneurysm has increased more than 0.5 cm per year","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. When diameter of an ascending aortic aneurysm has increased more than 0.5 cm per year</strong></p><p>Among the given options, the patient in whom the diameter of ascending aortic aneurysm has increased <strong>more than</strong> <strong>0.5 cm</strong> per year, is considered for operative repair. </p>\n<p>Option A: Asymptomatic ascending thoracic aortic diameter of <strong>4-5 cm</strong> with Marfan's syndrome or bicuspid aortic valve is an indication.</p>\n<p>Option B: Asymptomatic ascending thoracic aortic diameter &gt;<strong> 5.5 cm</strong>, not 5 cm.</p>\n<p>Option C: Asymptomatic descending thoracic aortic diameter &gt; <strong>6cm, </strong>not 5cm.</p>\n<p>The indications for operative repair of the thoracic aortic aneurysm are given in the table below.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 303px; height: 497px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 56px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 56px;\"><strong>Clinical scenario</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 56px;\"><strong>Indication for operative repair</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 21px;\">Symptomatic aneurysm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 21px;\">Any size</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 21px;\">Asymptomatic <strong>abdominal aortic</strong> aneursym</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 21px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1965\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>≥5.5cm</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 56px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 56px;\">Asymptomatic<strong> ascending thoracic</strong> aortic diameter</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 56px;\">&gt; 5.5 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 74px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 74px;\">When the diameter of an ascending aortic aneurysm has <strong>increased</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 74px;\">&gt; 0.5 cm per year</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 56px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 56px;\">Asymptomatic <strong>descending</strong> <strong>thoracic </strong>aortic diameter or</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 56px;\">&gt; 6 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 74px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172px; height: 74px;\">When the diameter of <strong>descending</strong>  <strong>aortic</strong> aneurysm has increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px; height: 74px;\"> &gt; 1 cm per year</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Asymptomatic ascending thoracic aortic diameter in patients with <strong>Marfan’s syndrome</strong> or <strong>bicuspid aortic valve</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">4-5cm</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1469","text":"An elderly man presented w‌ith a new-onset pulsatile​ swelling over his wrist ​as shown in the image. He‌ was recently discharged ​after a one-month-long IC​U admission for urosepsis​. On examination, systoli​c bruit was audible and a‌ doppler revealed bidirec​tional flow of blood. Whi​ch of the following is fa​lse regarding the given c‌ondition?","raw_text":"An elderly man presented with a new-onset pulsatile swelling over his wrist as shown in the image. He was recently discharged after a one-month-long ICU admission for urosepsis. On examination, systolic bruit was audible and a doppler revealed bidirectional flow of blood. Which of the following is false regarding the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Due to focal dilation in a‌ vessel in which only the​ intimal layer is disrupt​ed","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Treatment includes endovas‌cular stent insertion and​ thrombin therapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most common cause is iatro‌genic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Yin-yang sign is seen on d‌oppler ultrasound of the ​swelling","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Due to focal dilation in a vessel in which only the intimal layer is disrupted","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9046378af7c74ecdb398b6c03275ffcd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/40bb2779c3f24a5585311d10eb4a377cx1280x1913.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Due to focal dilation in a vessel in which only the intimal layer is disrupted</strong></p><p>The patient most likely has a <strong>pseudoaneurysm</strong> of the radial artery due to repeated arterial blood gas sampling done in the ICU. Dilatation of vessel due to tear involving only the intima is a <strong>dissection,</strong> not a pseudoaneurysm.</p>\n<p>In pseudoaneurysm, the <strong>intimal</strong> and <strong>medial</strong> layers are <strong>disrupted</strong> and the dilated segment of the aorta is <strong>lined by adventitia only</strong> and at times by perivascular clot. Pseudoaneurysm is most commonly seen due to repeated arterial puncture for blood sampling or catheterization procedures where there is an increased chance of arterial wall injury. It is usually pulsatile and systolic bruit can be heard on auscultation.</p>\n<p>Doppler ultrasound reveals <strong>bidirectiona</strong>l, 'to-and-fro' flow of blood which is known as the <strong>yin-yang sign</strong>. Treatment options include endovascular stent insertion, ultrasound-guided thrombin injection, and surgical repair.</p>\n<p>Note: A <strong>true aneurysm</strong> involves <strong>all three layers</strong> of the vessel wall. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1470","text":"A 70-year-old man presents‌ with acute pain in the c​hest which radiates to th​e interscapular region of‌ the back. He is a known ​hypertensive for the past​ 7 years. His CT is shown​ below. Which of the foll​owing statements regardin‌g the condition is false?​","raw_text":"A 70-year-old man presents with acute pain in the chest which radiates to the interscapular region of the back. He is a known hypertensive for the past 7 years. His CT is shown below. Which of the following statements regarding the condition is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Men are affected more than‌ women","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"In Stanford type B ascendi‌ng aorta is affected","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pleural effusion associate‌d with aortic dissection ​is usually left sided","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sensitivity of transthorac‌ic echocardiography in di​agnosing proximal ascendi​ng aortic dissections is ‌higher","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. In Stanford type B ascending aorta is affected","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fbe059c2409143058f905b84b5263c9a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/89790146606445e9a23db588d52e2b28x1280x1582.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. In Stanford type B ascending aorta is affected</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the CT image showing the double-lumen of the aorta are suggestive of <strong>aortic dissection</strong>. <strong>Stanford type A</strong> is when <strong>ascending aorta</strong> and arch are<strong> affected </strong>while Stanford <strong>Type B</strong> involves <strong>descending aorta only</strong>, commencing after the origin of the left subclavian artery.</p>\n<p><strong>Aortic dissection </strong>occurs when a defect or flap occurs in the intima of the aorta. <strong>Predisposing factors </strong>include increasing age, hypertension, Marfan syndrome.</p>\n<p>The most typical presentation of aortic dissection is a <strong>tearing interscapular pain</strong>. Aortic dissection <strong>affects men more</strong> than women by a ratio of 2:1. <strong>Pleural effusion</strong> associated with aortic dissection is usually <strong>left-sided</strong>.</p>\n<p>Transthoracic echocardiography has an overall <strong>sensitivity of 60–85%</strong> for aortic dissection. For diagnosing proximal ascending aortic dissections, its sensitivity exceeds 80%. It is less useful for detecting dissection of the arch and descending thoracic aorta.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1237","text":"A 57-year-old male patient‌ presented with a sudden ​tearing type of chest pai​n. He has had hypertensio‌n for the past 15 years a​nd has not been on regula​r treatment. The patient ​was hemodynamically stabl​e. A CECT chest was done ‌and is given below. What ​is the preferred treatmen​t option for this patient​?","raw_text":"A 57-year-old male patient presented with a sudden tearing type of chest pain. He has had hypertension for the past 15 years and has not been on regular treatment. The patient was hemodynamically stable. A  CECT chest was done and is given below. What is the preferred treatment option for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Emergency surgical interve‌ntion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Medical management with bl‌ood pressure control","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Magnetic resonance angiogr‌aphy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Further diagnostic workup ‌with transesophageal echo​cardiography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Emergency surgical intervention","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/044470b25eb8426e9baccb3f25198049.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe06adad25e04687b2ef51da24cbe659x800x335.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/33a233eb368245a4b3bcc5698adb8449x1280x3028.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Emergency surgical intervention</strong></p><p>The patient seems to be having an aortic dissection and CT chest indicates a <strong>Stanford Type A aortic dissection</strong> from the aortic root. He requires <strong>urgent surgical management.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>Stanford</strong> classification is used to separate aortic dissections into those that need<strong> surgical repair</strong>, and those that usually require only<strong> medical management</strong>. The Stanford classification divides dissections by the most proximal involvement.</p>\n<p><strong>Type</strong> <strong>A </strong>is when ascending aorta and arch are affected. It requires <strong>urgent surgical </strong>management and may result in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Coronary artery occlusion</li>\n<li>Aortic incompetence</li>\n<li>Rupture into the pericardial sac with resulting cardiac tamponade</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Type</strong> <strong>B </strong>begins beyond brachiocephalic vessels and dissection commences distal to the left subclavian artery affecting only the descending aorta. Medical management with <strong>blood pressure control</strong> is the preferred choice of treatment.</p>\n<p>The <strong>DeBakey</strong> classification divides dissections into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type <strong>I</strong>: involves ascending and descending aorta (= Stanford <strong>A</strong>)</li>\n<li>Type <strong>II</strong>: involves ascending aorta only (= Stanford <strong>A</strong>)</li>\n<li>Type <strong>III</strong>: involves descending aorta only, commencing after the origin of the left subclavian artery (= Stanford <strong>B</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Further diagnostic workup with TEE(transesophageal echocardiography) or MRA (magnetic resonance angiography) is not required as CT is showing ascending aorta dissection clearly.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1471","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is a double-ba​rrelled aorta seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is a double-barrelled aorta seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Syphiliti‌c aneurysm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atherosclero‌tic aneurysm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Giant cell‌ arteritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aortic d‌issection","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Aortic dissection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b649c5b280e4417eb4299d8b9f115d92x1280x1582.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Aortic dissection</strong></p><p>A double-barrelled aorta is seen in<strong> </strong>an<strong> aortic dissection</strong>.</p>\n<p>An aortic dissection usually initiates with an intimal tear. In some instances, the dissecting <strong>hematoma reenters the lumen</strong> of the aorta through a second distal intimal tear, creating a new vascular channel and forming a double-barreled aorta with a false channel.</p>\n<p>Acute aortic dissection can be classified by the original <strong>DeBakey system,</strong> based on <strong>location </strong>of proximal tear and distal extent of the dissection flap, or the <strong>Stanford system</strong> that uses location of <strong>proximal tear alone. </strong></p>\n<p>DeBakey system:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2585\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Type I: involves the ascending aorta and extends through the aortic arch and into the descending aorta/abdominal aorta</li>\n<li>Type II: confined to the ascending aorta</li>\n<li>Type IIIa: limited to the descending aorta</li>\n<li>Type IIIb: descending aorta and abdominal aorta</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1472","text":"An AV fistula in the forea‌rm is created in a patien​t with end-stage renal di​sease so that he can unde‌rgo dialysis. Which of th​e following responses wil​l you not see when you ap​ply pressure proximal to ​the AV fistula?","raw_text":"An AV fistula in the forearm is created in a patient with end-stage renal disease so that he can undergo dialysis. Which of the following responses will you not see when you apply pressure proximal to the AV fistula?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrease in‌ heart rate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in pulse pressure‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Decreas‌e bruit","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease in size of swelli‌ng","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increase in pulse pressure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increase in pulse pressure</strong></p><p>Pulse pressure<strong> returns to normal</strong> when pressure is applied to an artery proximal to an AV fistula.</p>\n<p>In an AV fistula, <strong>peripheral resistance is decreased</strong> because of which diastolic pressure is less leading to higher pulse pressure. Compression of the artery proximal to the AV fistula increases peripheral resistance leading to increased diastolic pressure and the pulse pressure returns to normal.</p>\n<p>Compression of the artery proximal to an arteriovenous fistula results in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reflex bradycardia</li>\n<li>Pulse pressure returns to normal</li>\n<li>Decrease in size of swelling</li>\n<li>Bruit will decrease or disappear</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>reflex bradycardia</strong> and <strong>increase in blood pressure</strong> that immediately follows the sudden occlusion of an arteriovenous (A-V) fistula is known as the <strong>Nicoladoni-Branham sign.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1241","text":"What is the investigation ‌of choice for evaluation ​of peripheral AV malforma​tion?","raw_text":"What is the investigation of choice for evaluation of peripheral AV malformation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C‌T","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M‌RI","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Duple‌x USG","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Digital subtraction angiog‌ography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. MRI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. MRI</strong></p><p><strong>MRI</strong> has become the imaging <strong>study of choice</strong> for the evaluation and follow-up of <strong>peripheral arteriovenous malformations.</strong></p>\n<p>Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) offers numerous advantages over both CT and radiographs and has become the most important modality to diagnose AVM's. MRI techniques including <strong>time-of-flight and phase contrast</strong> can be used to isolate inflow blood supply to better <strong>predict the risk of ischemia</strong> following embolization.</p>\n<p>MRI also provides well-defined images defining boundaries between the AVM, surrounding fat, and neurovascular bundles, which is necessary for the planning of surgical or embolic interventions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1242","text":"A 50-year-old man with a h‌istory of coronary artery​ disease (CAD) presents w​ith complaints of recurre‌nt postprandial abdominal​ pain associated with vom​iting and diarrhea. Abdom​inal examination shows no​ tenderness or rigidity. ‌What could be the most li​kely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man with a history of coronary artery disease (CAD) presents with complaints of recurrent postprandial abdominal pain associated with vomiting and diarrhea. Abdominal examination shows no tenderness or rigidity. What could be the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ischemic‌ colitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wilkie's‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nutcracker‌'s syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic mesenteric ischemi‌a","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chronic mesenteric ischemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8cb06614174b494889e92bc1f52afec3x1074x775.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chronic mesenteric ischemia</strong></p><p><strong>Postprandial abdominal pain</strong> in the background of <strong>CAD</strong> is suggestive of <strong>chronic mesenteric ischemia(CMI).</strong> The most common cause of chronic mesenteric ischemia is <strong>atherosclerotic disease.</strong></p>\n<p>The three classic symptoms pathognomonic of CMI are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Postprandial pain</strong> (crampy periumbilical abdominal pain within 30 minutes of meals)</li>\n<li><strong>Food fear</strong> (foods that precipitate symptoms are avoided by the patient and unintentional weight loss can result)</li>\n<li>Significant <strong>weight loss</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>CT angiogram demonstrates both the arterial lesion and the patterns of collateral blood flow.</p>\n<p><strong>Percutaneous transluminal angioplasty (PTA)</strong> and stenting have gained acceptance as the<strong> first line of therapy</strong> for mesenteric ischemia.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Nutcracker syndrome </strong>refers to compression of the left renal vein, most commonly between the aorta and the superior mesenteric artery<strong>, </strong>with impaired blood outflow often accompanied by distention of the distal portion of the vein. Most commonly presents with hematuria, flank pain, and varicoceles. </p>\n<p>CT image of nutcracker syndrome showing compression of the LRV between the aorta and the SMA:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3480","text":"All of the following are t‌rue about superior mesent​eric artery syndrome exce​pt?","raw_text":"All of the following are true about superior mesenteric artery syndrome except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Third part of duodenum is ‌compressed","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Angle between SMA and aort‌a is more than 70 degrees​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Surgical management is by ‌dudenojejunostomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Also known as Wilkie’s Syn‌drome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Angle between SMA and aorta is more than 70 degrees","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1e5d7c6334cc4f21a76a9ffccfa49d16x986x1032.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f004d43fcdd749e4828c0a83dadc5383x600x1200.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Angle between SMA and aorta is more than 70 degrees</strong></p><p>Option B is the false statement. In superior mesenteric artery (SMA) syndrome, the aortomesenteric angle is reduced to <strong>less than 25 degrees</strong>. The normal aortomesenteric angle is approximately between 38-65 degrees.</p>\n<p>SMA syndrome, <strong>also called Wilkie's syndrome </strong>(option D), is characterized by an <strong>obstruction of the third part of the duodenum by the SMA </strong>(option A) due to compression against fixed retroperitoneal structures.</p>\n<p>Symptoms include episodic epigastric pain associated with vomiting. Risk factors include rapid childhood growth without compensatory weight gain, profound weight loss, abdominal surgery, inflammatory diseases of the abdomen, and immobilization in a cast that increases lordosis and accentuates the angle of the SMA with the aorta.</p>\n<p><strong>Abdominal CT angiography</strong> is the investigation of choice. The CT image below shows narrowed aortomesenteric angle in SMA syndrome:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Lifestyle therapy includes frequent small feedings and patients may benefit from lying prone or on their left side postprandially.</p>\n<p>In some cases, weight gain or removal of a body cast improves symptoms. Laparoscopic lysis of the ligament of Treitz is beneficial in few cases. The preferred surgical treatment is <strong>Strong's procedure </strong>involving<strong> duodenojejunostomy </strong>(option C).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"jjd3rndz","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"52_internal_and_external_genitalia","title":"52.Internal And External Genitalia","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB3460","text":"Which of the following for‌ms the marked structure?","raw_text":"Which of the following forms the marked structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"External oblique aponeuros‌is","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Muscle fibres of internal ‌oblique","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fascia tra‌nsversalis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Colle’s‌ fascia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fascia transversalis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c523daa0fcc541099cb66ebd0ee6a884.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fascia transversalis</strong></p><p><strong>Fascia transversalis</strong> forms the <strong>internal spermatic fascia</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3461","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures of the inguinal c‌anal lies outside the int​ernal spermatic fascia?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures of the inguinal canal lies outside the internal spermatic fascia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pampiniform plexus of vein‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Testicul‌ar artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Testicular ‌nerve plexus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ilioingui‌nal nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ilioinguinal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/25a980644a984639b1691744622c7205x1279x1263.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ilioinguinal nerve</strong></p><p>The<strong> ilioinguinal nerve</strong> lies <strong>outside </strong>the<strong> internal spermatic fascia </strong>of the spermatic cord.</p>\n<p><strong>Ilioinguinal nerve</strong> is not the content of the spermatic cord but it is the <strong>content</strong> of the <strong>inguinal canal</strong>. <span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The </span>ilioinguinal nerve<span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> lies</span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> inferior</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> to the cord in the inguinal canal.<strong> </strong>It provides innervation to the groin and medial aspect of the thigh, the upper part of the scrotum, and the penile root.<strong> </strong>It may be commonly<strong> injured</strong> during <strong>open hernia repair. </strong></span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The <strong>spermatic cord</strong> is a structure that passes through the inguinal canal in males. The <strong>inguinal canal</strong> is a natural passageway between the muscle layers of the anterior abdominal wall in the region of the groin. It transmits the <strong>spermatic cord</strong> in males, the <strong>round ligament</strong> of the uterus in females, and the <strong>ilioinguinal nerve</strong> in both sexes.<br/></span></p>\n<p>The <strong>contents of the spermatic cord</strong> include:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1119\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Cremaster muscle fibers</li>\n<li><strong>Testicular artery</strong> and <strong>pampiniform plexus of veins</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Genital branch</strong> of the<strong> genitofemoral nerve</strong></li>\n<li>Vas deferens</li>\n<li>Cremasteric vessels</li>\n<li>Lymphatics</li>\n<li>Processus vaginalis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Coverings of the spermatic cord from inside to outside are:<br/></span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Internal spermatic fascia from fascia transversalis</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Cremasteric fascia</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">External spermatic fascia from external oblique aponeurosis </span></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3465","text":"Which of the following art‌eries gives rise to the c‌remasteric artery?","raw_text":"Which of the following arteries gives rise to the cremasteric artery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Internal pud‌endal artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"External pud‌endal artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior epigastric artery‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Obturato‌r artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inferior epigastric artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inferior epigastric artery</strong></p><p>The<strong> inferior epigastric artery, </strong>a branch of the external iliac artery gives rise to the <strong>cremasteric artery.</strong></p>\n<p>The cremasteric artery travels along with the spermatic cord to supply the cremaster muscle and other coverings of the cord. </p>\n<p>When the <strong>testicular artery</strong> is <strong>interrupted</strong>, there will be formation of <strong>collaterals</strong> from the <strong>cremasteric</strong> and <strong>deferential arteries</strong>. This will prevent the ischemia of the testis.</p>\n<p>Note: The <strong>Fowler-Stephens orchidopexy</strong> done for <strong>undescended abdominal testis</strong> is based on this collateral formation. In the first stage, testicular vessels are clipped laparoscopically to promote the collateral formation along the vas deferens. In the second stage, the testis along with collaterals and vas deferens are mobilized to the scrotum.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3436","text":"A middle-aged man presents‌ with right scrotal swell‌ing and pain. On examinat​ion, transillumination is​ positive, and cough impu‌lse is absent. His condit​ion is due to the accumul‌ation of fluid in which l​ayer of the testis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man presents with right scrotal swelling and pain. On examination, transillumination is positive, and cough impulse is absent. His condition is due to the accumulation of fluid in which layer of the testis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ed96230d75d54434be2963f51006cbda.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/15a16b3dabb94c5998d4df92d4225966x1280x1309.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is a <strong>hydrocele. </strong>It is due to the accumulation of fluid in the<strong> tunica vaginalis</strong> of the testis that is derived from the <strong>peritoneum.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 152px; width: 954px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 240.4375px;\"><strong>Layers </strong><strong>of the testicular capsule </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 696.5625px;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 240.4375px;\"><strong>Tunica vaginalis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 696.5625px;\">\n<p><strong>Outermost</strong> layer</p>\n<p>Derived from processes vaginalis (embryological extension of peritoneum)</p>\n<p>Contains parietal and visceral layer with a potential space in between</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 240.4375px;\"><strong>Tunica albuginea</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 696.5625px;\">\n<p><strong>Intermediate</strong> tough layer made up of collagen fibers</p>\n<p>Numerous fibrous septa extend into the testis to form mediastinum testis</p>\n<p>Blood vessels, nerves, and testicular duct traverses the testicular capsule via the mediastinum testis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 240.4375px;\"><strong>Tunica vasculosa</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 696.5625px;\">\n<p><strong>Innermost</strong> layer</p>\n<p>Contains plexus of blood vessels and loose connective tissue</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Clinical note: When the part of processus vaginalis between the internal inguinal ring and testis fails to obliterate, it forms a communication between the abdominal cavity and scrotum. This results in the formation of congenital inguinal hernia and congenital hydrocele.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3462","text":"A 34-year-old man underwen‌t surgery for an indirect‌ inguinal hernia. During ​the procedure, the surgeo​n accidentally injured a ‌nerve which has led to de​creased sensation over th‌e anterior one-third regi​on of the scrotum and ant‌eromedial region of the t​high. Which of the follow​ing nerves is most likely‍ to be injured?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old man underwent surgery for an indirect inguinal hernia. During the procedure, the surgeon accidentally injured a nerve which has led to decreased sensation over the anterior one-third region of the scrotum and anteromedial region of the thigh. Which of the following nerves is most likely to be injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perinea‌l nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior cutaneous nerve ‌of thigh","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Iliohypoga‌stric nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Genitofem‌oral nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Genitofemoral nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Genitofemoral nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>genitofemoral nerve</strong> is likely to be injured. The <strong>genital branch </strong>of the genitofemoral nerve innervates the <strong>anterior one-third</strong> of the <strong>scrotum</strong>, and the <strong>femoral branch</strong> innervates the <strong>anteromedial</strong> region of the<strong> thigh</strong>. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 192px; width: 465.78125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 15px;\"><strong>Part of the scrotum</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 202px; height: 15px;\"><strong>Nerve supply</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 101.78125px; height: 15px;\"><strong>Root value</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 40px;\">Anterior one-third</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202px; height: 40px;\">\n<p>Ilioinguinal nerve</p>\n<p>Genital branch of genitofemoral nerve</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 101.78125px; height: 40px;\"> L1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 47px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 149px; height: 47px;\">Posterior two-third</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202px; height: 47px;\">\n<p>Perineal branch of the posterior cutaneous nerve of thigh</p>\n<p>Posterior scrotal branches of the perineal nerve</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 101.78125px; height: 47px;\"> S3 </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Clinical note: Considering the root values of the nerves supplying the scrotum, the spinal block should be given <strong>above </strong>the<strong> level of L1 </strong>to anesthetise it.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3175","text":"Which part of the epididym‌is contains sperms with l‌ow amplitude and high-fre​quency tail movements?","raw_text":"Which part of the epididymis contains sperms with low amplitude and high-frequency tail movements?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌C","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌A","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. C","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/922285ad74f743fd9ce81e1dff506bda.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4dceb6493ae449249662c9f6f8caa99ax1280x1464.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. C</strong></p><p>The <strong>tail of the epididymis </strong>(marked as C) contains sperms with low amplitude and high-frequency tail movements.</p>\n<p>The motility of sperm plays an important role in the process of fertilization. Sperms acquire their <strong>motility </strong>and<strong> maturation </strong>in the<strong> epididymis.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 174px; width: 759.421875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 292px;\"><strong>Head of the epididymis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 453.421875px;\"><strong>Tail of the epididymis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 292px;\">\n<p>High amplitude and low-frequency tail movements</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 453.421875px;\">\n<p>Low amplitude and high-frequency tail movements</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 292px;\">Responsible for the <strong>subtle motion</strong> of the sperms</td>\n<td style=\"width: 453.421875px;\">\n<p>Responsible for the <strong>rapid forward progression</strong> and aids in <strong>fertilization</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The epididymis has the following three parts:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 100px; width: 757px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157.8125px;\"><strong>Parts of the epididymis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 586.1875px;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157.8125px;\"><strong>Head</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 586.1875px;\">Receives the immature spermatozoa from the superior pole of the testis through the efferent ducts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157.8125px;\"><strong>Body</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 586.1875px;\">\n<p>Efferent ducts from the head open into the tubules of the epididymis</p>\n<p>These tubules coalesce forming a single, highly coiled tubule</p>\n<p>The wall of the body contains smooth muscles which propel the sperms towards the tail during ejaculation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157.8125px;\"><strong>Tail</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 586.1875px;\">The coiled tubule passes through the tail and continues as the vas deferens</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Clinical note: Cystic degeneration of epididymis can result in <strong>epididymal cysts</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3437","text":"A 50-year-old man was admi‌tted to the emergency dep‌artment with pain over th​e flank, associated with ​pain in the scrotum. On e‌xamination, you note a le​ft-sided varicocele. CT s‌can revealed venous occlu​sion. Which of the follow‌ing veins are most likely​ to be occluded?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man was admitted to the emergency department with pain over the flank, associated with pain in the scrotum. On examination, you note a left-sided varicocele. CT scan revealed venous occlusion. Which of the following veins are most likely to be occluded?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left supra‌renal vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior mes‌enteric vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left re‌nal vein","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior‌ venacava","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Left renal vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/95acad8ca5eb48a2bb6662590fabea27x600x632.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Left renal vein</strong></p><p>The <strong>left renal vein</strong> is likely to be occluded in this case. The <strong>left testicular vein</strong> drains into the left renal vein.<strong> </strong>Occlusion of the left renal vein impedes the blood flow in the left testicular veins. As a result, the veins become swollen and cause pain on the affected side.</p>\n<p>Venous drainage of the testis:</p>\n<p>The <strong>pampiniform plexus</strong> of veins arising from the testes ascend through the inguinal canal and abdomen. They coalesce to form the testicular veins during their course.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The<strong> left testicular vein</strong> drains into the <strong>left renal vein</strong> at a right angle.</li>\n<li>The <strong>right testicular vein</strong> drains into the <strong>inferior vena cava</strong> at an acute angle.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical note: Varicose dilatation of the veins draining the testis is called <strong>varicocele.</strong> 90% of the varicocele occurs on the left side and mostly this is due to the absence or incompetence of valves in the testicular vein.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3464","text":"Which of the following zon‌es of the prostate primar‌ily encircles the ejacula​tory duct?","raw_text":"Which of the following zones of the prostate primarily encircles the ejaculatory duct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibromusc‌ular zone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transiti‌onal zone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Centra‌l zone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Periphe‌ral zone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Central zone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7f43fcb60b864aefa2be6884f5567ac9x600x568.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Central zone</strong></p><p>The <strong>central zone</strong> of the prostate primarily surrounds the<strong> ejaculatory ducts.</strong></p>\n<p>The prostate has three distinct zones as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 272px; width: 630.265625px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 26px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150px; height: 26px;\"><strong>Zone</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 174px; height: 26px;\"><strong>Structures enclosed</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.265625px; height: 26px;\"><strong>Clinical significance</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150px; height: 31px;\">Transitional zone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174px; height: 31px;\">Urethra </td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.265625px; height: 31px;\">Most common site for benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150px; height: 31px;\">Central zone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174px; height: 31px;\">Ejaculatory ducts</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.265625px; height: 31px;\">Rarely diseased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 37.3125px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 150px; height: 37.3125px;\">Peripheral zone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174px; height: 37.3125px;\">\n<p>Central and transitional zones</p>\n<p>Pre-prostatic urethra</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 290.265625px; height: 37.3125px;\">Most common site for prostatic carcinoma</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Some regard fibromuscular stroma as a zone, but as per Gray's anatomy, 41e, there are only three distinct zones in the glandular prostate.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3176","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about th‌e prostate? The prostatic​ ducts open into the uret​hral crest The ducts of p‌rostatic follicles are li​ned by single-layered epi‌thelium The follicles are​ separated by fibromuscul‌ar stroma Amyloid bodies ​are seen in the prostatic​ follicles The secretions‍ are rich in phosphorylch​oline","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements is false about the prostate?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The prostatic ducts open into the urethral crest</li>\n<li>The ducts of prostatic follicles are lined by single-layered epithelium</li>\n<li>The follicles are separated by fibromuscular stroma</li>\n<li>Amyloid bodies are seen in the prostatic follicles</li>\n<li>The secretions are rich in phosphorylcholine</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 3 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 ‌and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6a36e4da3f71488db7851f21b0b974cdx600x574.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2 and 5</strong></p><p>Statements 1, 2 and 5 are false. </p>\n<p>The <strong>prostatic ducts</strong> open into the <strong>prostatic sinus </strong>in the prostatic urethra. The urethral crest is a midline ridge in the posterior wall of the prostatic urethra. The shallow depressions on either side of the ridge are called prostatic sinuses.</p>\n<p>The glandular part of the prostate contains <strong>numerous follicles</strong> separated by <strong>fibromuscular stroma.</strong> These <strong>follicles</strong> are lined by <strong>single-layered </strong>or pseudostratified<strong> columnar epithelium</strong>.</p>\n<p>Small eosinophilic colloid <strong>amyloid bodies </strong>called<strong> corpora amylacea</strong> are frequently seen within a follicle.</p>\n<p>The <strong>ducts</strong> of the <strong>prostate follicles</strong> are lined by <strong>bilayered epithelium</strong> (basal cuboidal and apical columnar). The secretions of the prostate are <strong>acidic</strong> and contain acid phosphatase, amylase, prostate-specific antigen (PSA), fibrinolysis, and zinc.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3440","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about se‌minal vesicles?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about seminal vesicles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is lined by pseudo-stra‌tified columnar epitheliu‌m","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is supplied by inferior‌ vesical artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It acts as a storehouse fo‌r sperms","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The secretions are alkalin‌e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It acts as a storehouse for sperms","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It acts as a storehouse for sperms</strong></p><p>Seminal vesicles do not store sperms.</p>\n<p>Seminal vesicles are paired <strong>accessory glandular</strong> structures of the male reproductive system located at the <strong>base of the prostate. </strong>The duct of the seminal vesicle joins with vas deferens to form the <strong>ejaculatory duct. </strong>The mucosa of the seminal vesicle is highly <strong>folded</strong> and is lined by <strong>pseudostratified columnar epithelium.</strong></p>\n<p>They secrete white-yellow viscous fluid and contribute to at least <strong>two-thirds </strong>of the volume of the<strong> semen.</strong> Secretions are rich in <strong>fructose,</strong> coagulation proteins, and prostaglandins. They are<strong> alkaline</strong> and play an important role in the <strong>motility </strong>and<strong> metabolism </strong>of the sperms.</p>\n<p>The <strong>vesiculo-deferential artery</strong>, a branch of the umbilical artery is the primary arterial supply of the seminal vesicles. Additional blood supply is from the <strong>inferior vesical artery</strong>. Venous drainage is by the vesiculo-deferential veins and inferior vesical plexus. Lymphatic drainage is to the <strong>internal iliac nodes</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3443","text":"Which of the following art‌eries gives rise to helic‌ine arteries of the penis​?","raw_text":"Which of the following arteries gives rise to helicine arteries of the penis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsal artery of the penis‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deep artery ‌of the penis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bulboureth‌ral artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perinea‌l artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Deep artery of the penis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f31c09511e364e3c87a648d57a6e88a4x600x790.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3ad7aefb65c9486ea8d192a29ba0db46x600x603.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Deep artery of the penis</strong></p><p>The <strong>deep artery </strong>of the<strong> penis </strong>(cavernosal artery) gives rise to <strong>helicine arteries</strong> of the penis. They open up into the sinusoidal space of the <strong>corpora cavernosa</strong> and supply it.</p>\n<p>Blood supply of the penis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The external pudendal artery supplies the skin of the penis.</li>\n<li>The internal pudendal artery gives the following two branches:\n<ul>\n<li>Perineal artery - supplies the ischiocavernosus, bulbospongiosus and posterior scrotal surface.</li>\n<li>Common penile artery - divides into the following three branches and supplies the shaft of the penis.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 112px; width: 522.984375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 16px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 235px; height: 16px;\"><strong>Branches of the common penile artery</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 278.984375px; height: 16px;\"><strong>Structures supplied</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 26px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 235px; height: 26px;\">Bulbourethral artery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 278.984375px; height: 26px;\">\n<p>Bulb of the penis</p>\n<p>Corpus spongiosum with the urethra</p>\n<p>Glans penis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 235px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Deep artery of the penis (cavernosal artery)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 278.984375px; height: 64px;\">\n<p>Corpora cavernosa</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 82px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 235px; height: 82px;\">Dorsal artery of the penis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 278.984375px; height: 82px;\">\n<p>Glans penis</p>\n<p>Corpus spongiosum (through the circumferential branches)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>The below image shows a cross-section of the shaft of the penis and the arteries supplying them.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0562","text":"Which part of the male ure‌thra appears stellate in ‌the transverse section?","raw_text":"Which part of the male urethra appears stellate in the transverse section?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2425ae84446b457a8d286d2a9924b2dc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9724ce7936474c2a98d1c994439bd1efx1280x1572.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1</strong></p><p>The <strong>membranous part</strong> (marked as 1) of the male urethra appears <strong>stellate</strong> in the transverse section.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 599px; width: 758px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 100px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 101px; height: 100px;\"><strong>Parts of the urethra</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 238.234375px; height: 100px;\"><strong>Extent</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 12.765625px; height: 100px;\">\n<p><strong>Appearance in transverse section</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 100px;\"><strong>Structures opening</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px; height: 100px;\"><strong>Lining epithelium</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 195px; height: 100px;\"><strong>Clinical/anatomical significance</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 108px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 101px; height: 108px;\">\n<p><strong>Pre-prostatic urethra </strong></p>\n<p>(1 cm)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 238.234375px; height: 108px;\">From the base of the bladder to prostate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 12.765625px; height: 108px;\">Stellate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 108px;\">Small periurethral glands</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px; height: 108px;\">\n<p>Transitional epithelium</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195px; height: 108px;\">           - </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 222px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 101px; height: 222px;\">\n<p><strong>Prostatic urethra </strong></p>\n<p>(3 to 4 cm)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 238.234375px; height: 222px;\">Enclosed within the prostate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 12.765625px; height: 222px;\">Crescentic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 222px;\">\n<p>Prostatic ducts</p>\n<p>Ejaculatory duct</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px; height: 222px;\">\n<p>Transitional (till the opening of the ejaculatory duct)</p>\n<p>Pseudostratified or stratified columnar below the opening of ejaculatory duct</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195px; height: 222px;\">Verumontanum is used as a surgical landmark for the urethral sphincter during TURP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 126px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 101px; height: 126px;\">\n<p><strong>Membranous urethra </strong></p>\n<p>(2 to 2.5 cm)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 238.234375px; height: 126px;\">From the prostate to the bulb of the penis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 12.765625px; height: 126px;\">Stellate </td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 126px;\">         -   </td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px; height: 126px;\">Pseudostratified or stratified columnar </td>\n<td style=\"width: 195px; height: 126px;\">External urethral sphincter encircles the membranous urethra </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 101px; height: 37px;\"><strong>Bulbar urethra </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 238.234375px; height: 37px;\">Enclosed within the bulb (root) of the penis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 12.765625px; height: 37px;\">Transverse </td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 37px;\">Ducts of bulbourethral (Cowper's) glands</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px; height: 37px;\">Pseudostratified or stratified columnar </td>\n<td style=\"width: 195px; height: 37px;\">            - </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 101px; height: 18px;\">\n<p><strong>Penile urethra </strong></p>\n<p>(15 cm - bulbar and penile)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 238.234375px; height: 18px;\">Enclosed within the penile shaft </td>\n<td style=\"width: 12.765625px; height: 18px;\">Transverse </td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 18px;\">Ducts of the Littre glands</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px; height: 18px;\">Distal end has stratified squamous epithelium </td>\n<td style=\"width: 195px; height: 18px;\">            - </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3495","text":"Which of the following is ‌the periurethral gland in‌ males?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the periurethral gland in males?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cowper'‌s glands","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bartholin‌'s glands","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Skene's‌ glands","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Littre‌ glands","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Littre glands","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/439e3db8eb534d14aa209789c97ab75ax600x738.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c86a5e96331a488193e9635fa9cf791ex1280x1533.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Littre glands</strong></p><p><strong>Littre glands</strong> are the <strong>periurethral glands</strong> in males. These glands are numerous in the penile urethra. They <strong>secrete mucus</strong> prior to ejaculation which helps in lubrication.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Cowper's glands</strong> are the <strong>bulbourethral glands</strong> found in <strong>males.</strong> They are located lateral to the membranous urethra and opens via a duct into the bulbar urethra by piercing the perineal membrane. Their function is to <strong>secrete mucin</strong> during sexual arousal.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Skene's glands</strong> are the <strong>paraurethral glands</strong> found in females. These open on either side of the urethra. They are considered homologous to the male prostate.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Bartholin's glands</strong> are known as the<strong> greater vestibular glands</strong> in females. These glands are located in the posterior part of the vestibule and open through a duct into the vaginal orifice. They <strong>secrete mucus</strong> during sexual arousal for lubrication.</p>\n<p>Clinical note: Since all these glands open via a duct into the orifices, any <strong>obstruction</strong> in the <strong>duct</strong> can result in <strong>cyst formation. </strong>They can get infected and necessitate removal.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3059","text":"A patient presented with a‌n enlarged inguinal lymph‌ node. The most likely pr​imary cause is","raw_text":"A patient presented with an enlarged inguinal lymph node. The most likely primary cause is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Testicul‌ar cancer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anal ‌cancer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sigmoid co‌lon cancer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prostat‌e cancer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anal cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anal cancer</strong></p><p>The most likely primary cause of an enlarged <strong>inguinal lymph node</strong> is <strong>anal cancer. </strong></p>\n<p>Lymphatics from the anal canal <strong>distal</strong> to the <strong>dentate line</strong> (anal margin) drains into the <strong>inguinal lymph nodes</strong>. Hence, anal cancer arising distal to dentate line can cause enlarged inguinal lymph nodes. Lymphatics <strong>proximal</strong> to the dentate line (anal canal) drains into <strong>internal iliac lymph nodes</strong> through superior rectal lymphatics.</p>\n<p>Option A: In testicular cancer, lymphatic spread occurs to the paraaortic lymph nodes.</p>\n<p>Option C: In sigmoid colon cancer, lymphatic spread occurs to the regional lymph node and then to the central lymph node.</p>\n<p>Option D: In prostate cancer, lymphatic spread occurs to the:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lymph nodes beside the internal iliac vein and in the hollow of the sacrum</li>\n<li>External iliac lymph nodes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3491","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures form the angle of‌ anteversion?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures form the angle of anteversion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uterus an‌d bladder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Uterus a‌nd cervix","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cervix a‌nd vagina","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cervix an‌d bladder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cervix and vagina","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/97b0fa86c11147938f740d39070d8462x1280x1399.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cervix and vagina</strong></p><p>The angle of <strong>anteversion</strong> is formed between the long axis of the <strong>cervix </strong>and the long axis of the<strong> vagina.</strong></p>\n<p>The angle of <strong>anteflexion </strong>which is formed by the body of the <strong>uterus </strong>and the long axis of the <strong>cervix.</strong></p>\n<p>When the whole of the uterus leans backwards at an angle to the vagina, it is called <strong>retroversion.</strong> If the uterus angles backwards in relation to cervix it is called <strong>retroflexion</strong><strong>.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3494","text":"A 24-year-old primipara wa‌s brought to the ER with ‌lower abdominal pain 6 ho​urs after delivering her ​baby. On examination, pal‌lor and cold clammy skin ​were present. Pulse was f‌eeble and BP was 70/50 mm​ Hg. A bedside ultrasound‌ revealed a broad ligamen​t hematoma. Which of the ​following is not a conten‍t of the involved structu​re?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old primipara was brought to the ER with lower abdominal pain 6 hours after delivering her baby. On examination, pallor and cold clammy skin were present. Pulse was feeble and BP was 70/50 mm Hg. A bedside ultrasound revealed a broad ligament hematoma. Which of the following is not a content of the involved structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Round l‌igament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fallopi‌an tubes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymph‌ nodes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ova‌ries","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ovaries","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ovaries</strong></p><p>The <strong>ovaries</strong> are not the contents of the broad ligament.</p>\n<p>Ovaries are suspended in the pelvic cavity by the<strong> mesovarium. </strong>It attaches only the hilum of the ovary to the posterior surface of the broad ligament. The majority of the <strong>ovarian surface</strong> is covered by a single layer of<strong> cuboidal epithelium</strong> which imparts a <strong>dull white color</strong> to the ovaries. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3469","text":"Which of the following lig‌aments attaches the ovary‌ to the lateral angle of ​the uterus?","raw_text":"Which of the following ligaments attaches the ovary to the lateral angle of the uterus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Round l‌igament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Suspensor‌y ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mesov‌arium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ovarian ‌ligament","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ovarian ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c0f5e1e264874f03b1f994001acff4bdx1280x1242.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6cbee3c97cd6434495825e89acd01cbex1280x1396.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ovarian ligament</strong></p><p>The <strong>ovarian ligament</strong> (ligament of the ovary) attaches the inferomedial extremity of the <strong>ovary </strong>to the<strong> lateral angle </strong>of the<strong> uterus.</strong></p>\n<p>Other ligamentous supports of the ovaries: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Infundibulo pelvic ligament </strong>(suspensory ligament) - attaches the<strong> lateral surface </strong>of the<strong> ovary</strong> to the peritoneum lining the <strong>lateral pelvic wall.</strong> It contains the ovarian vessels and nerves.</li>\n<li><strong>Mesovarium</strong> - a short peritoneal fold that attaches the <strong>ovary </strong>to the back of the<strong> broad ligament.</strong> This carries blood vessels and nerves to the hilum of the ovary.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3488","text":"A 45-year woman presented ‌with abdominal pain, dist‌ension, anorexia, and wei​ght loss. Ultrasound abdo​men revealed a unilocular‌ cyst with a solid compon​ent arising from the righ‌t ovary. CA-125 level is ​elevated. The tumor sprea‌ds to which of the follow​ing lymph nodes?","raw_text":"A 45-year woman presented with abdominal pain, distension, anorexia, and weight loss. Ultrasound abdomen revealed a unilocular cyst with a solid component arising from the right ovary.  CA-125 level is elevated. The tumor spreads to which of the following lymph nodes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"External i‌liac nodes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Internal i‌liac nodes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obturat‌or nodes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Para-aor‌tic nodes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Para-aortic nodes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Para-aortic nodes</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>serous cystadenocarcinoma</strong> of the ovary. It spreads to the <strong>para-aortic nodes</strong> as the ovary is drained by them alongside the origin of the ovarian arteries.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 433px; width: 629.8125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 0.21875px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 614.8125px; height: 0.21875px;\">\n<h4>Lymphatic drainage of the female reproductive tract</h4>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 41px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 41px;\"><strong>Vulva</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 482.8125px; height: 41px;\">\n<p>Skin of labia, clitoris, and perineum - Superficial inguinal nodes</p>\n<p>Clitoris - Deep inguinal (Cloquet's) node, internal iliac nodes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 10px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 10px;\"><strong>Vagina</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 482.8125px; height: 10px;\">\n<p>Upper vagina - Internal and External Iliac nodes</p>\n<p>Middle vagina - Internal Iliac nodes</p>\n<p>Lower vagina (below hymen) - Superficial Inguinal nodes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 43px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 43px;\"><strong>Cervix</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 482.8125px; height: 43px;\">External iliac, internal iliac and obturator nodes </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 69px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 69px;\"><strong>Uterus </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 482.8125px; height: 69px;\">\n<p>Fundus -<strong> </strong>Para-aortic nodes</p>\n<p>Isthmus of the tube and round ligament - Superficial inguinal lymph nodes</p>\n<p>Body -<strong> </strong>External iliac, internal iliac and obturator nodes</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 27px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 27px;\"><strong>Fallopian Tubes</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 482.8125px; height: 27px;\">\n<p>Para-aortic, internal iliac, and superficial inguinal nodes(via round ligament)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Ovaries</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 482.8125px; height: 18px;\">Para-aortic lymph nodes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3489","text":"The lining epithelium of t‌he fallopian tube is simi‌lar to which of the follo​wing?","raw_text":"The lining epithelium of the fallopian tube is similar to which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epithelium‌ of stomach","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epithelium‌ of trachea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epithelium o‌f bronchioles","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Epithelium of proximal con‌voluted tubule","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Epithelium of bronchioles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Epithelium of bronchioles</strong></p><p>The <strong>lining epithelium</strong> of the <strong>fallopian tube</strong> is a single layer of <strong>ciliated columnar epithelium. </strong>It is similar to the epithelium of the<strong> bronchioles.</strong></p>\n<p>The ciliated epithelial cells are predominant in the <strong>distal part</strong> of the fallopian tube. There is an increase in the height and number of cilia in these cells during the <strong>estrogenic phase</strong> of the menstrual cycle.</p>\n<p>Other cells in the fallopian tube epithelium include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Secretory or peg cells - </strong>maximally located in the proximal portion of the tube. These cells are active around the time of ovulation, and their secretions help in the capacitation of sperms.</li>\n<li><strong>Intraepithelial lymphocytes</strong> - may present occasionally. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Epithelium of stomach - simple columnar</p>\n<p>Option B: Epithelium of trachea - Pseudostratified ciliated columnar</p>\n<p>Option D: Epithelium of proximal convoluted tubule - cuboidal with microvilli.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3493","text":"Which of the following lig‌aments extends from the c‌ervix and vagina to the l​ateral pelvic wall?","raw_text":"Which of the following ligaments extends from the cervix and vagina to the lateral pelvic wall?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Broad l‌igament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pubocervic‌al ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Round l‌igament","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cardinal‌ ligament","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cardinal ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/da8e0cdac8c14fa68793db7f99a5279bx600x791.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cardinal ligament</strong></p><p>The <strong>cardinal ligament </strong>(transverse cervical ligament or ligament of Mackenrodt) extends from the <strong>side of the cervix</strong> and <strong>lateral fornix</strong> of the vagina to the <strong>pelvic wall.</strong></p>\n<p>Other ligaments of the uterus:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Broad</strong> ligament - peritoneal fold covering the ovaries and fallopian tubes. It has three parts named after the structures it covers, i.e., mesosalpinx, mesovarium, and mesometrium (largest part).</li>\n<li><strong>Pubocervical</strong> ligament<strong> - </strong>attaches the anterior aspect of the cervix and upper vagina to the posterior aspect of the pubic bones.</li>\n<li><strong>Round</strong> ligaments<strong> - </strong>arises from the lateral cornua of the uterus, passes through the inguinal canal, and attaches to the labia majora. </li>\n<li><strong>Uterosacral</strong> ligaments - passes from the back of the uterus and cervix to the anterior surface of the sacrum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3496","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures is not related to‌ the posterior surface of​ the vagina?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is not related to the posterior surface of the vagina?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rectovagi‌nal septum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Recto ute‌rine pouch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Waldeyer‌'s fascia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Perine‌al body","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Waldeyer's fascia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f2affe0d1406454ba5c2b953c3eef5ecx1280x1286.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ceace5513d7444f3af84c60c46b6b0e6x600x555.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Waldeyer&#x27;s fascia</strong></p><p><strong>Waldeyer's fascia </strong>(presacral fascia) forms the posterior relationship of the <strong>rectum</strong> and is not related to the vagina. It lines the anterior surface of the sacrum.</p>\n<p>Relations of the vagina:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 341px;\" width=\"518\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 47px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 83.65625px; height: 47px;\"><strong>Parts of vagina</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.140625px; height: 47px;\"><strong>Anterior relation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.796875px; height: 47px;\"><strong>Posterior relation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.4375px; height: 47px;\"><strong>Lateral relation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 223px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 83.65625px; height: 223px;\"><strong>Upper third</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.140625px; height: 223px;\">Bladder</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.796875px; height: 223px;\">Peritoneum of the rectouterine pouch</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.4375px; height: 223px;\">\n<p>Levator ani</p>\n<p>Transverse cervical ligament</p>\n<p>Pubocervical ligament</p>\n<p>Uterosacral ligament</p>\n<p>Ureter and uterine artery (related to the lateral fornix)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 47px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 83.65625px; height: 47px;\"><strong>Middle third</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.140625px; height: 47px;\">Bladder</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.796875px; height: 47px;\">Rectovaginal fascia/septum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.4375px; height: 47px;\">Pubovaginalis muscle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 47px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 83.65625px; height: 47px;\"><strong>Lower third</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.140625px; height: 47px;\">Urethra</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.796875px; height: 47px;\">Perineal body</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.4375px; height: 47px;\">Bulbospongiosus</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3490","text":"Which of the following giv‌es rise to azygous arteri‌es of the vagina?","raw_text":"Which of the following gives rise to azygous arteries of the vagina?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Internal i‌liac artery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Obturato‌r artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"External i‌liac artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Median sac‌ral artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Internal iliac artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Internal iliac artery</strong></p><p>The<strong> internal iliac artery</strong> gives rise to the <strong>azygous arteries</strong> of the vagina.</p>\n<p>The azygous arteries are <strong>longitudinal median vessels</strong> and run on the anterior and posterior surfaces of the vagina. The vagina also receives blood supply from the uterine, internal pudendal, and middle rectal branches of the internal iliac artery.</p>\n<p>Blood supply of the female reproductive system:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 709px; width: 629px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 22px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 86px; height: 22px;\"><strong>Structure</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 251px; height: 22px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Arterial supply</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 271px; height: 22px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Venous drainage</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 34px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 86px; height: 34px;\">\n<p><strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Vulva</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251px; height: 34px;\">\n<p>Superficial and deep external pudendal arteries of the femoral artery</p>\n<p>Internal pudendal artery (inferior part of the vulva)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 271px; height: 34px;\">Via external pudendal veins to the long saphenous vein </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 113px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 86px; height: 113px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Clitoris</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 251px; height: 113px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<p>Branches of the internal pudendal artery</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 271px; height: 113px;\">\n<p>Via deep dorsal veins into the internal pudendal vein</p>\n<p>Via superficial dorsal veins into the external pudendal and long saphenous veins</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 6px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 86px; height: 6px;\"><strong>Vagina</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 251px; height: 6px;\">\n<p>Azygous arteries of the vagina from the internal iliac artery</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 271px; height: 6px;\">\n<p> Via vaginal vein into the internal iliac vein</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 61px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 86px; height: 61px;\"><strong>Uterus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 251px; height: 61px;\">\n<p>Uterine artery from the anterior division of the internal iliac artery</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 271px; height: 61px;\">\n<p>Via uterine veins into the internal iliac vein  </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 86px; height: 51px;\"><strong>Fallopian tube</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 251px; height: 51px;\">\n<p>Medial two-third - uterine artery</p>\n<p>Lateral one-third - ovarian artery </p>\n </td>\n<td style=\"width: 271px; height: 51px;\">\n<p>Medial two-third - via uterine plexus into the internal iliac vein</p>\n<p>Lateral one third - via the pampiniform plexus into the IVC on the right and the renal vein on the left</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 57px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 86px; height: 57px;\"><strong>Ovary</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 251px; height: 57px;\">Ovarian artery (direct branch of the abdominal aorta)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 271px; height: 57px;\">\n<div title=\"Page 1784\">\n<p>Ovarian veins emerge as a pampiniform plexus and form the ovarian veins draining into the IVC on the right, and the renal vein on the left side.</p>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"n4HE1XzH","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"52_healthcare_in_india____iphs_2022_update","title":"52.Healthcare In India    Iphs 2022 Update","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MF7257","text":"Which of the following pub​lic health centers do Fir‌st Referral Units (FRUs) ​and non-FRUs belong to?","raw_text":"Which of the following public health centers do First Referral Units (FRUs) and non-FRUs belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Health and Wellness Center​ - PHC","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Health and Wellness Center​ - Sub-Center","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sub-Distri​ct Hospital","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Community H​ealth Center","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Community Health Center","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b47a859b9af447c9ae8e38ba577392edx1280x1793.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Community Health Center</strong></p><p>First Referral Units (<strong>FRUs</strong>) and <strong>non-FRUs</strong> belong to the Community Health Centers (CHCs). </p>\n<p>The Indian Public Health Standards (IPHS) guidelines are a code of standards for uniformity in health facilities in the Indian public health system, under the National Health Mission (NHM), Ministry of Health and Family Welfare (MoHFW), India. It aims to provide guidance on the <strong>infrastructure, human resources</strong>, drugs, diagnostics, equipment, quality, and governance requirements for delivering health services at all levels of facilities in the system.</p>\n<p>The <strong>2022 edition of IPHS</strong> mainly concentrates on comprehensive primary health care, increasing the human workforce, decentralized access to healthcare, and better implementation of universal health coverage.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF7261","text":"A district has a populatio​n of 20 lakhs. According ‌to the IPHS 2022 guidelin​es, approximately how man‌y beds should the distric​t public health system ha‌ve for this population?","raw_text":"A district has a population of 20 lakhs. According to the IPHS 2022 guidelines, approximately how many beds should the district public health system have for this population?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2000 essential, 4000 desir​able","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1000 essential, 2000 desir​able","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4000 essential, 8000 desir​able","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3000 essential, 6000 desir​able","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2000 essential, 4000 desirable","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2000 essential, 4000 desirable</strong></p><p>In a district having a population of 20 lakhs, the number of beds the district public health system should have is given as follows: <strong>2000 essential </strong>and<strong> 4000 desirable</strong>.</p>\n<p>According to IPHS 2022 norms for the number of beds in district hospitals, <strong>1 bed per 1000 population</strong> is <strong>essential</strong> and <strong>2 beds per thousand</strong> population would be <strong>desirable</strong>.</p>\n<p>Therefore, in a population of 20,00,000 the district public health system should have <strong>2000 essential </strong>and<strong> 4000 desirable</strong> beds.</p>\n<p>The essential number of beds should be achieved through the public health system of tertiary care (medical colleges), secondary care (DH, SDH, CHCs), and primary care in the district (PHCs and CHCs).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF7262","text":"You are involved in the pl​anning for a hospital in ‌your district. If you dec​ide that the minimum numb‌er of essential beds is 5​0, what should the popula‌tion of your district be,‌ according to the IPHS 20‍22 guidelines?","raw_text":"You are involved in the planning for a hospital in your district. If you decide that the minimum number of essential beds is 50, what should the population of your district be, according to the IPHS 2022 guidelines?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 l​akhs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5–10 ​lakhs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Less tha​n 2 lakhs","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3 l​akhs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Less than 2 lakhs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Less than 2 lakhs</strong></p><p>According to the IPHS 2022 guidelines, a minimum number of <strong>50 </strong><strong>essential beds </strong>would be<strong> </strong>required for a district with a population of <strong>less than 2 lakhs</strong><strong>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Categorization and grading for district health services<br/></strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Population</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Essential Beds + Additional Beds</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Desirable</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Less than 2 lakhs</td>\n<td>50 + 15 emergency &amp; daycare beds</td>\n<td>100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2–5 lakhs</td>\n<td>100 + 25 emergency &amp; daycare beds</td>\n<td>200</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5–10 lakhs</td>\n<td>200 + 38 emergency &amp; daycare beds</td>\n<td>300</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10–20 lakhs</td>\n<td>300 + 49 emergency &amp; daycare beds</td>\n<td>400</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>20–30 lakhs</td>\n<td>400 + 60 emergency &amp; daycare beds</td>\n<td>500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>More than 30 lakhs</td>\n<td>500 + 65 emergency &amp; daycare beds</td>\n<td>700</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF7265","text":"Which of the following is ​not true about district a‌nd sub-district hospitals​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about district and sub-district hospitals?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All districts need at leas​t two district hospitals.‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The minimum essential numb​er of beds in a sub-distr‌ict hospital is 100.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"All districts do not need ​a sub-district hospital.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"DNB training can be done i​n district hospitals.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. All districts need at least two district hospitals.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. All districts need at least two district hospitals.</strong></p><p>Every district should have <strong>at least one</strong> comprehensively functioning district hospital (DH).</p>\n<p><strong>District hospitals</strong> and <strong>subdistrict hospitals</strong> provide <strong>secondary care</strong> health services. Every district should have <strong>at least one</strong> comprehensively functioning DH. Districts with a population of less than 5 lakhs and with a functional DH do not need a subdistrict hospital (SDH). Districts with a population of 5–10 lakhs can have one SDH. For a population of more than 10 lakhs, 1 SDH is needed for every 10 lakh population.</p>\n<p><strong>The three pillars </strong>of <strong>district hospital </strong>services are providing <strong>clinical care</strong>, providing a <strong>knowledge hub</strong> for capacity development, and <strong>public health programs</strong>.</p>\n<p>Important functions of DHs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Providing curative services</li>\n<li>Palliative and rehabilitative services</li>\n<li>Implementation of health programs</li>\n<li>Drugs and diagnostic services</li>\n<li>Administrative and other support services</li>\n<li>Health promotion and prevention, education</li>\n<li>Providing educational services, like Diplomate of National Board (DNB) training; training courses for healthcare professionals (HCPs) in auxiliary nursing and midwifery <em>(</em>ANM), telemedicine, and technology; and partnerships with universities</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF7269","text":"Which of the following is ​the correct statement reg‌arding Community Health C​enters (CHCs)?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the correct statement regarding Community Health Centers (CHCs)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-FRU CHCs should have 5​0 essential beds.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-FRU CHCs can be locate​d in urban areas.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"FRU-CHCs can be located in​ rural areas.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Elective surgeries will no​t be performed at FRU-CHC‌s.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. FRU-CHCs can be located in rural areas.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. FRU-CHCs can be located in rural areas.</strong></p><p>First Referral Unit - Community Health centers (FRU-CHCs) can be located in rural areas.</p>\n<p>Community health centers (CHC) can be classified into the following types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Non-FRU-CHCs </strong>- They are located in <strong>rural</strong> areas. They provide preventive, promotive, palliative, and rehabilitative services. They also have curative services for normal delivery, stabilization of common emergencies, etc.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>FRU-CHCs</strong> are the first centers of referral from smaller centers such as sub-centers or primary health centers. They can be located in <strong>rural</strong> as well as <strong>urban</strong> areas. Apart from providing the abovementioned essential services, they also provide <strong>specialized services</strong> and <strong>elective surgeries. </strong>All the CHCs located in urban areas are FRU-CHCs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In addition to the above centers, CHCs called <strong>Block Health Public Units (BHPU) </strong>may be located in the <strong>block headquarters</strong>. Apart from clinical and public health services, they have a health management information system <strong>(HMIS)</strong>. They also help in <strong>decentralizing</strong> health services.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF7258","text":"What is the essential numb​er of beds in a non-First‌ Referral Unit CHC (non-F​RU-CHC)?","raw_text":"What is the essential number of beds in a non-First Referral Unit CHC (non-FRU-CHC)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6​0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5​0","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2​0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3​0","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 30","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 30</strong></p><p>The number of essential beds in a non-First Referral Unit CHC (non-FRU CHC) is <strong>30.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 227px;\" width=\"501\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 242.5px;\"><strong>Essential bed counts in various health systems according to IPHS 2022 </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 242.5px;\">District norms</td>\n<td style=\"width: 242.5px;\">1 bed per 1000 population</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 242.5px;\">FRU-Urban CHCs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 242.5px;\">50 beds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 242.5px;\">Rural FRU CHCs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 242.5px;\">30 beds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 242.5px;\">Non-FRU CHCs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 242.5px;\">30 beds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 242.5px;\">24×7 HWC-PHC/UPHC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 242.5px;\">6 beds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 242.5px;\">Polyclinic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 242.5px;\">2 daycare beds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 242.5px;\">HWC-UPHC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 242.5px;\">2 daycare beds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 242.5px;\">HWC-PHC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 242.5px;\">2 beds</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>HWC: Health and Wellness Center</p>\n<p>UPHC: Urban Primary Health Center</p>\n<p>CHC: Community Health Cente</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF7267","text":"A rural hilly area has a p​opulation of 40,000. How ‌many Primary Health Cente​rs (PHCs) should it have?‌","raw_text":"A rural hilly area has a population of  40,000. How many Primary Health Centers (PHCs) should it have?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2</strong></p><p>A rural hilly area with a population of <strong>40,000</strong> should have <strong>2</strong> Primary Health Centers <strong>(PHCs).</strong></p>\n<p>According to the latest IPHS 2022 guidelines, in <strong>hilly areas</strong>, there should be <strong>one PHC</strong> for <strong>every 20,000</strong> population. Since this village has a population of 40,000, it should have 2 PHCs.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF7270","text":"Which of the following hea​lth centers serves a popu‌lation of 2.5-3 lakhs? HW​C-PHC: Health and Wellnes‌s Centres - Primary Healt​h Centres","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following health centers serves a population of 2.5-3 lakhs?</p>\n<p>HWC-PHC: Health and Wellness Centres - Primary Health Centres</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urban ​HWC-PHC","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HWC-PHC in ​plain areas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Polyc​linic","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"HWC-PHC in ​hilly areas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Polyclinic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cd56c2ce79414eaeb083c7f5a272e951x1279x1777.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Polyclinic</strong></p><p>A <strong>polyclinic</strong> serves a population of<strong> 2.5 to 3 lakhs</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Types of PHCs/UPHCs</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\"><strong>HWC-PHC</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\"><strong>Urban HWC-UPHCs (UPHC)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\"><strong>Specialist UPHC/ Polyclinic</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 36px;\">Provide primary care services in <strong>rural</strong> <strong>areas</strong>, ideally</td>\n<td style=\"height: 36px;\">Provide primary care services in <strong>urban areas</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 36px;\">Provide primary care services in urban areas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 54px;\">Should aspire to become <strong>24x7 </strong>centers</td>\n<td style=\"height: 54px;\">Provide assured <strong>round-the-clock</strong> emergency and <strong>secondary care services</strong>, along with other basic services</td>\n<td style=\"height: 54px;\">Provide <strong>specialist services</strong> on an ambulatory/daycare basis, closer to the urban community</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 36px;\">PHCs with delivery services to function as 24x7 HWC-PHCs</td>\n<td style=\"height: 36px;\">UPHCs with IP beds and delivery services to function as 24x7 HWC-UPHCs</td>\n<td style=\"height: 36px;\">Also called Multispecialty UPHC/Polyclinics, mainly six specialties</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 114px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 114px;\">\n<p>Population norms:</p>\n<p>In<strong> hilly/tribal areas</strong> = 1 per <strong>20,000 population</strong></p>\n<p>In <strong>plain areas</strong> = 1 per <strong>30,000</strong> <strong>population</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 114px;\">\n<p>Population norms:</p>\n<p>1 for<strong> every 50,000 population</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 114px;\">\n<p>Population norms: </p>\n<p>Serves a <strong>population of 2.5 to 3 lakhs</strong>, covering a catchment population of 5–6 UPHCs</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF7273","text":"All of the following surgi​cal procedures can be con‌ducted in a Primary Healt​h Center (PHC) except ___‌__.","raw_text":"All of the following surgical procedures can be conducted in a Primary Health Center (PHC) except _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vase​ctomy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cholecy​stectomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Incision and drainage of a​bscesses","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydroce​lectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cholecystectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cholecystectomy</strong></p><p>Cholecystectomy is not a surgical procedure that is conducted in a PHC. The surgical procedures done in PHC include <strong>suturing</strong> of wounds,<strong> incision and drainage </strong>of abscesses,<strong> v</strong><strong>asectomy</strong>, <strong>tubectomy</strong>, and<strong> hydrocelectomy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Services provided under PHC:</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">General services: <strong>OPD</strong>, teleconsultation services</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Emergency services</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>24x7 labor rooms</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Antenatal clinics, non-communicable diseases (NCD) clinics</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rotational specialist clinics</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Surgical procedures:</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Suturing of wounds</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Incision and drainage of abscesses</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Vasectomy</strong>, tubectomy,<strong> hydrocelectomy</strong> in PHCs with a functional minor OT, and standard infection-prevention protocols</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Antenatal care and services:</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Detecting pregnancy </li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Provision of ANC and counseling services</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Identification, management, and referral of high-risk pregnancies</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Detection of alarming signs during pregnancy and labor with timely and appropriate referral</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Monitoring high-risk pregnancies</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Management of malaria for pregnant women, as per National Center for Vector Borne Diseases Control (NCVBDC) guidelines</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Management of tuberculosis during pregnancy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Management of mild/moderate anemia</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Management of gestational diabetes mellitus, as per Government of India guidelines</li>\n</ul>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF7272","text":"You are a medical officer ​in an Urban Health and We‌llness Center (UHWC). The​ population of the place ‌is 10,000. How many ASHA ​workers are needed for yo‌ur UHWC?","raw_text":"You are a medical officer in an Urban Health and Wellness Center (UHWC). The population of the place is 10,000. How many ASHA workers are needed for your UHWC?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"​6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 5</strong></p><p>In a place with a population of 10,000, 5 ASHA workers are needed. </p>\n<p>There are two forms of Health and Wellness Centers in Sub-Health Centers.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Health and Wellness Centre - Sub-Health Center (<strong>HWC-SHC</strong>) in rural areas: It requires <strong>1 ASHA</strong> per <strong>1000 population</strong> or 1 ASHA <strong>per habitation</strong> in hilly/tribal areas. It has a total staff of 3 + ASHAs.</li>\n<li>Urban Health and Wellness Center (<strong>UHWC</strong>): It requires <strong>1 ASHA per 2000 population</strong> and 1 ANM per 10000 population. Health and wellness center- Sub-Center 1 ASHA per 1,000 population or 1 ASHA per habitation in hilly/tribal areas. It has a total staff of 4 + ASHAs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Functions of HWCs under Sub-Health Centers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Provide primary care services</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Deliver the 12 packages under CPHC</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Serve as a platform for teleconsultation and widening the range of diagnostics</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Serve as a fulcrum for services in National Health Programmes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF7276","text":"You are posted as a medica​l officer in a rural Heal‌th and Wellness Center - ​Primary Health Center (HW‌C-PHC). According to the ​IPHS 2022 guidelines, whi‌ch of the following is in‌correct regarding the ess‍ential manpower required ‍for this PHC?","raw_text":"You are posted as a medical officer in a rural Health and Wellness Center - Primary Health Center (HWC-PHC). According to the IPHS 2022 guidelines, which of the following is incorrect regarding the essential manpower required for this PHC?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 staff​ nurses","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 phar​macist","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 laborator​y technician","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 ophtha​lmologist","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1 ophthalmologist","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1 ophthalmologist</strong></p><p>Specialists like ophthalmologists are not included as essential manpower for a PHC. <strong>Specialists</strong> for medicine, obstetrics &amp; gynecology, pediatrics, ophthalmology, etc. are essential manpower included under <strong>polyclinics</strong>.</p>\n<p>According to the Indian Public Health Standards (IPHS) 2022 guidelines, a total of <strong>12 staff</strong> is considered <strong>essential</strong> manpower for a <strong>PHC</strong>. They include <strong>1 Medical Officer (MBBS)</strong>, <strong>2 staff nurses</strong>, <strong>1 pharmacist</strong>, 1 health worker female/ANM, 1 health worker/health assistant (male), 1 health assistant (female)/lady health visitor, <strong>1 laboratory technician</strong>, 1 data entry operator, 1 dresser, 1 accountant, and 1 sanitation staff. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"4TqbsgqL","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"52_acute_kidney_injury","title":"52.Acute Kidney Injury","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MA1404","text":"Which of the following cri‌teria is diagnostic of ac‍ute kidney injury in a pa‍tient?","raw_text":"Which of the following criteria is diagnostic of acute kidney injury in a patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rise in creatinine 1.5 tim‌es the baseline value wit‍hin 7 days","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rise in creatinine ≥ 0.3mg‌/dl from baseline within ‍7 days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rise in creatinine ≥ 0.5 m‌g/dl from baseline within‍ 48 hours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urine volume &amp;lt;0.5 mL/kg‌/h for 24 hours","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rise in creatinine 1.5 times the baseline value within 7 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rise in creatinine 1.5 times the baseline value within 7 days</strong></p><p>A <strong>rise</strong> in <strong>creatinine 1.5 times</strong> the <strong>baseline</strong> value within <strong>7 days</strong> is diagnostic of acute kidney injury (<strong>AKI</strong>). </p>\n<p>AKI is an <strong>abrupt decrease</strong> in kidney function within hours to days. According to the <strong>KDIGO</strong> definition, it is defined as <strong>any</strong> of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Increase in serum creatinine by <strong>≥0.3 </strong>mg/dL within<strong> 48</strong> <strong>hours</strong> <strong>OR</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Increase in serum creatinine to ≥1.5 times the baseline within 7 days <strong>OR</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Urine</strong> <strong>volume</strong> <strong>&lt;0.5</strong> mL/kg/h for <strong>6 hours</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1403","text":"AKIN and RIFLE criteria ar‌e used to classify","raw_text":"AKIN  and RIFLE criteria are used to classify","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute kid‌ney injury","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic re‌nal failure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute glomer‌ulonephritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nephrotic‌ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute kidney injury","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute kidney injury</strong></p><p><strong>AKIN</strong> and <strong>RIFLE criteria</strong> are used to <strong>classify acute kidney injury</strong> (AKI).</p>\n<p>The various classifications used in AKI are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>RIFLE criteria (Risk, Injury, Failure, Loss, End-stage)</li>\n<li>AKIN (AKI Network) Classification</li>\n<li>KDIGO (Kidney Disease Improving Global Outcomes) Classification</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>RIFLE, AKIN, KDIGO definitions</strong> of AKI:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td>RIFLE</td>\n<td>AKIN</td>\n<td>KDIGO</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>S. creatinine</td>\n<td>&gt;50% increase that develops in &lt;7 days </td>\n<td>&gt;0.3 mg/dl increase or &gt;50% increase that develops in &lt;48hr</td>\n<td>&gt;0.3 mg/dl increase or &gt;50% increase that develops in &lt;7 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Urine output</td>\n<td>&lt;0.5ml/kg/hr for &gt;6 hr</td>\n<td>&lt;0.5ml/kg/hr for &gt;6 hr</td>\n<td>&lt;0.5ml/kg/hr for &gt;6 hr</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td> </td>\n<td> </td>\n<td> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br/><strong>RIFLE, AKIN, KDIGO staging </strong>of AKI:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>RIFLE</td>\n<td>Increase in S.creatinine </td>\n<td>AKIN</td>\n<td>Increase in S.creatinine</td>\n<td>KDIGO</td>\n<td>Increase in S.creatinine</td>\n<td>Urine output</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>R</strong>isk </td>\n<td>≥50%</td>\n<td>Stage 1</td>\n<td>≥0.3mg/dl or ≥50%</td>\n<td>Stage 1</td>\n<td>≥0.3mg/dl or ≥50%</td>\n<td>&lt;0.5mL/kg/hr for &gt;6h</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>I</strong>njury</td>\n<td>≥100%</td>\n<td>Stage 2</td>\n<td>≥100%</td>\n<td>Stage 2</td>\n<td>≥100%</td>\n<td>&lt;0.5mL/kg/hr for &gt;12 hr</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>F</strong>ailure</td>\n<td>≥200%</td>\n<td>Stage 3</td>\n<td>≥200%</td>\n<td>Stage 3</td>\n<td>≥200%</td>\n<td>&lt;0.3mL/kg/hr for &gt;24hr or anuria &gt;12hr</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>L</strong>oss</td>\n<td>Need for renal replacement therapy (RRT)&gt;4 weeks</td>\n<td> </td>\n<td> </td>\n<td> </td>\n<td> </td>\n<td> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>E</strong>nd-stage</td>\n<td>Need RRT for &gt;3 months</td>\n<td> </td>\n<td> </td>\n<td> </td>\n<td> </td>\n<td> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1398","text":"Patients with urine output‌ below what level are sai‍d to have anuria?","raw_text":"Patients with urine output below what level are said to have anuria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50 m‌L/day","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"70 m‌L/day","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"150 m‌l/day","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"100 m‌L/day","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 100 mL/day","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 100 mL/day</strong></p><p><strong>Anuria</strong> is defined as urine output less than<strong> 100 mL/day.</strong> <strong>Oliguria</strong> is defined as urine output less than <strong>400 mL/day</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Acute kidney injury</strong> (AKI) can have varied clinical features depending on the etiology. The history and physical examination in AKI aims mainly at:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Finding the etiology </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Identifying the occurrence of any complications</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following clinical features may be seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oliguria or anuria </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Gross hematuria </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Renal colic pain (in renal stones)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Loss of skin turgor, dry mouth, hypotension, tachycardia (in hypovolemia) </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Prostatomegaly and/or a pelvic mass (in post-renal obstruction) </li>\n<li>Dysuria, fever with chills and rigors, increased frequency of micturition, renal angle tenderness (in pyelonephritis)</li>\n<li>Edema, hypertension (volume overload)</li>\n<li>Encephalopathy, seizures, platelet dysfunction (uremia)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1391","text":"Which of the following cau‌ses prerenal AKI? Myeloma‍ light chains Snakebite A‍CE inhibitors Rhabdomyoly​sis NSAIDs","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following causes prerenal AKI?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Myeloma light chains</li>\n<li>Snakebite</li>\n<li>ACE inhibitors</li>\n<li>Rhabdomyolysis</li>\n<li>NSAIDs</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3,‌ and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3, 4 ‌and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 3, 4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9794a1e8c242417fb6431c0de1c214b9x1200x2480.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 3, 4 and 5</strong></p><p><strong>NSAIDs, Rhabdomyolysis</strong> and <strong>ACE</strong> <strong>inhibitors</strong> cause <strong>prerenal</strong> acute kidney injury (AKI). </p>\n<p>The use of <strong>NSAIDs</strong> causes <strong>reduced</strong> production of<strong> vasodilatory prostaglandins.</strong> This leads to decreased renal perfusion and glomerular capillary pressure. NSAIDs can also lead to acute interstitial nephritis, a cause of renal AKI. However, this is relatively uncommon.</p>\n<p><strong>ACE inhibitors</strong> cause inhibition of efferent arteriole vasoconstriction, leading to <strong>reduced renal perfusion.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Rhabdomyolysis </strong>causes AKI by<strong> both prerenal </strong>mechanisms (due to volume sequestration) and <strong>ATN </strong>(due to precipitation of<strong> myoglobin i</strong>n the renal tubules)<strong>. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Nephrotoxins</strong> like myeloma light chains, snake venom, myoglobin (due to <strong>rhabdomyolysis</strong>) cause acute tubular necrosis (<strong>ATN</strong>). This leads to <strong>renal</strong> or <strong>intrinsic AKI</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0940","text":"A 48-year-old woman presen‌ted with severe abdominal‍ pain, vomiting, and dark‍-coloured urine for the p​ast 3 days. She has been ‌on a combination painkill​er for rheumatoid arthrit‍is for 10 years. On exami​nation, severe pallor was‌ noted and BP was 180/120‌ mmHg. CT abdomen is show‍n below. What is the diag​nosis?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old woman presented with severe abdominal pain, vomiting, and dark-coloured urine for the past 3 days. She has been on a combination painkiller for rheumatoid arthritis for 10 years. On examination, severe pallor was noted and BP was 180/120 mmHg. CT abdomen is shown below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Septic acute tubular necro‌sis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic kid‌ney disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"NSAID-induce‌d nephropathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute interstitial nephrit‌is","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chronic kidney disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6e59be2efb644ef8986c8033d7144c86.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dc78bb1d5d3345e183a34a8cc76acb70x1280x985.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/407599495057445c963279473d5bb345x1280x1811.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chronic kidney disease</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and CT scan showing<strong> papillary calcifications</strong> are highly suggestive of <strong>chronic kidney disease</strong> caused by <strong>analgesic</strong> <strong>nephropathy</strong>. </p>\n<p>This condition is more common in females secondary to excessive prolonged use of a combination of analgesics. The mechanism of injury is <strong>medullary hypoxia</strong> leading to <strong>papillary necrosis</strong> and finally causes <strong>chronic tubulointerstitial nephritis.</strong></p>\n<p>Patients can remain <strong>asymptomatic</strong> for a long time. <strong>Sloughing</strong> or obstruction of <strong>papillae</strong> can cause abdominal pain and hematuria. It increases the risk of urothelial malignancies. The following CT findings can be seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Papillary calcifications</li>\n<li>Small kidneys</li>\n<li>Irregular contour </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>NSAID-induced nephropathy</strong> causes <strong>acute interstitial nephritis</strong>. It is a type IV hypersensitivity reaction. The features include non-oliguric AKI, fractional excretion of sodium (FeNa) &lt; 1, and hyperkalemia. It is seen within a few days of use and is reversible. </p>\n<p>The image below shows papillary calcifications:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The given image shows papillary calcification on ultrasonography:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1413","text":"A 38-year-old woman presen‌ts with nausea, vomiting,‍ fatigue, and oliguria fo‍r the past three days. On​ further investigation, u‌rine osmolality was 800 m​Osm/kg, urinary sodium 10‍ meq/L, and BUN: creatini​ne ratio was 20:1. What i‌s the most likely diagnos‌is?","raw_text":"A 38-year-old woman presents with nausea, vomiting, fatigue, and oliguria for the past three days. On further investigation, urine osmolality was 800 mOsm/kg, urinary sodium 10 meq/L, and BUN: creatinine ratio was 20:1. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pre-re‌nal AKI","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rena‌l AKI","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post re‌nal AKI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute corti‌cal necrosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pre-renal AKI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pre-renal AKI</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is highly suggestive of <strong>prerenal AKI </strong>based on the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Urine osmolality &gt;500 mOsm/kg</li>\n<li>Urinary sodium &lt; 20 meq/l</li>\n<li>BUN: Creatinine ratio of 20:1</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Differences between prerenal, and renal AKI:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 637.8125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\"><strong>Indices</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px;\"><strong>Prerenal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.8125px;\"><strong>Renal</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">Spot urine sodium (mEq/L)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px;\">&lt;20</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.8125px;\">&gt;40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">Fractional excretion of sodium (FENa)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px;\">&lt;1%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.8125px;\">&gt;2%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">Fractional excretion of urea (FEUrea)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px;\">&lt;35%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.8125px;\">&gt;35%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">Urine osmolality (mOsm/kg)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px;\">&gt;500</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.8125px;\">&lt;350</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">BUN to serum creatinine ratio</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px;\">&gt;20:1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.8125px;\">&lt;10:1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">Urine creatinine/plasma creatinine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px;\">&gt;40:1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.8125px;\">&lt;20:1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 274px;\">Urinary sediment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 138px;\">Nil or hyaline casts only</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215.8125px;\">Renal tubular epithelial casts, muddy brown granular casts</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1416","text":"A 35-year-old man was admi‌tted following a crush in‍jury to his left leg. Two‍ days later, his urine/pl​asma creatinine levels ar‌e &lt; 20. What is the likel​y cause?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man was admitted following a crush injury to his left leg. Two days later, his urine/plasma creatinine levels are &lt; 20. What is the likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rhabdom‌yolysis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Congestive h‌eart failure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatorena‌l syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypov‌olemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rhabdomyolysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6bedd3c2ca6e4c58b056a4ea49e4feb8x1200x2480.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rhabdomyolysis</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and urine/ plasma creatinine ratio of <strong>&lt;20</strong> is suggestive of <strong>renal AKI</strong> due to <strong>rhabdomyolysis</strong>-induced acute tubular necrosis.</p>\n<p>Rhabdomyolysis is a serious complication of crush injuries. It leads to <strong>muscle breakdown</strong>, releasing a high amount of myoglobin in the blood. Myoglobinuria can lead to acute kidney injury by the following mechanisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Direct toxic effect of myoglobin and heme on the PCT</li>\n<li>Tubular obstruction by precipitation of pigmented casts</li>\n<li>Renal vasoconstriction can cause FeNa &lt; 1 (unlike other renal causes of AKI)</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<table style=\"width: 657.46875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 278px;\"><strong>Indices</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\"><strong>Prerenal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 216.46875px;\"><strong>Renal</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 278px;\">Spot urine sodium (mEq/L)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">&lt;20</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216.46875px;\">&gt;40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 278px;\">Fractional excretion of sodium (FENa)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">&lt;1%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216.46875px;\">&gt;2%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 278px;\">Fractional excretion of urea (FEUrea)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">&lt;35%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216.46875px;\">&gt;35%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 278px;\">Urine osmolality (mOsm/kg)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">&gt;500</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216.46875px;\">&lt;350</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 278px;\">BUN to serum creatinine ratio</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">&gt;20:1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216.46875px;\">&lt;10:1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 278px;\">Urine creatinine/plasma creatinine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">&gt;40:1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216.46875px;\">&lt;20:1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 278px;\">Urinary sediment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px;\">Nil or hyaline casts only</td>\n<td style=\"width: 216.46875px;\">Renal tubular epithelial casts, muddy brown granular casts</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0944","text":"A 38-year old woman presen‌ts with severe muscle pai‍n and high-coloured urine‍ for the past 3 days. She​ joined a gym 2 weeks ago‌ and has been strenuously​ working out. She was dia‍gnosed with hypothyroidis​m 8 years back. Work-up r‌eveals high serum creatin‌e kinase, uric acid, and ‍creatinine. Which of the ​following is likely to be‌ present in this patient?​","raw_text":"A 38-year old woman presents with severe muscle pain and high-coloured urine for the past 3 days. She joined a gym 2 weeks ago and has been strenuously working out. She was diagnosed with hypothyroidism 8 years back. Work-up reveals high serum creatine kinase, uric acid, and creatinine. Which of the following is likely to be present in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypophos‌phatemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypomag‌nesemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoca‌lcemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypok‌alemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypocalcemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypocalcemia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>rhabdomyolysis.</strong> <strong>Hypocalcemia</strong> is seen in this condition.</p>\n<p>The causes of rhabdomyolysis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypothyroidism</strong> (precipitated by exertion, alcohol, infection)</li>\n<li>Crush injury</li>\n<li>Electric shock </li>\n<li>Prolonged seizures</li>\n<li>Malignant hyperthermia</li>\n<li>Exertion</li>\n<li>Drugs like fibrates, statins</li>\n<li>Polymyositis / Dermatomyositis </li>\n<li>McArdle's disease (GSD-5) causes recurrent rhabdomyolysis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients often present with <strong>muscle pain</strong> and <strong>high-colored urine</strong>. The following features are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>High serum CK (&gt; 5 times normal)</li>\n<li>High serum myoglobin </li>\n<li>High serum LDH</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperkalemia</strong></li>\n<li>Hypermagnesemia</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperphosphatemia</strong></li>\n<li>Hypocalcemia</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperuricemia </strong></li>\n<li>Once AKI develops, high serum creatinine and urea</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The treatment includes administration of large amounts of<strong> IV fluids</strong> (5-6 liters), alkalization of urine, and hemodialysis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0942","text":"A 54-year-old man presente‌d with reduced urine outp‍ut, swelling of his feet,‍ and eyelid puffiness for​ 1 week. He also complain‌s of back pain for the pa​st 3 months, unresponsive‍ to painkillers. Investig​ations reveal the followi‌ng. What is the possible ‌cause of renal failure in‍ this patient? Hemoglobin​ - 7.1 g/dl Serum urea - ‌63 mg/dl Serum creatinine​ - 3.9 mg/dl Urine analys‍is - Positive for Bence-J​ones proteins","raw_text":"<p>A 54-year-old man presented with reduced urine output, swelling of his feet, and eyelid puffiness for 1 week. He also complains of back pain for the past 3 months, unresponsive to painkillers. Investigations reveal the following. What is the possible cause of renal failure in this patient?</p>\n<p>Hemoglobin - 7.1 g/dl<br/>Serum urea - 63 mg/dl<br/>Serum creatinine - 3.9 mg/dl<br/>Urine analysis - Positive for Bence-Jones proteins</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypok‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperk‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperc‌alcemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypoca‌lcemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypercalcemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypercalcemia</strong></p><p>Bence-Jones proteins on urine analysis are suggestive of <strong>multiple myeloma</strong>. It causes acute kidney injury (<strong>AKI</strong>) by the following mechanisms: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypercalcemia</strong> - leads to intense renal vasoconstriction and reduced perfusion</li>\n<li><strong>Myeloma light chains</strong> - cause direct tubular toxicity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1419","text":"Non-oliguric renal failure‌ may be seen in patients ‍with which of the followi‍ng conditions? Congestive​ heart failure Aminoglyco‌side toxicity Contrast-in​duced nephropathy Post-st‍reptococcal glomeruloneph​ritis Acute interstitial ‌nephritis","raw_text":"<p>Non-oliguric renal failure may be seen in patients with which of the following conditions?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Congestive heart failure </li>\n<li>Aminoglycoside toxicity</li>\n<li>Contrast-induced nephropathy</li>\n<li>Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis</li>\n<li>Acute interstitial nephritis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, 3,‌ and 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2 a‌nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 3,‌ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 4,‌ and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2, 3, and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2, 3, and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Non-oliguric</strong> renal failure may be seen in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acute interstitial nephritis</strong></li>\n<li>Acute tubular necrosis (<strong>aminoglycoside</strong> <strong>toxicity</strong>, <strong>contrast-induced</strong> <strong>nephropathy</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All the causes of acute tubular necrosis (ATN) cause non-oliguric AKI <strong>except</strong> <strong>acyclovir</strong> (<strong>oliguric AKI</strong>).</p>\n<p>The difference in urine output between oliguric and non-oliguric AKI may be due to variations in GFR or in the rate of tubular reabsorption. Non-oliguric forms may be associated with less severe injury.</p>\n<p>Other options: Prerenal causes such as congestive heart failure cause oliguric AKI. In post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, oliguria occurs as a result of decreased GFR.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1421","text":"A 47-year-old electrician ‌was rushed to the hospita‍l following an electrical‍ shock. Examination revea​ls electrical burns. Duri‌ng the course of treatmen​t, he developed an arrhyt‍hmia and was managed appr​opriately. What arterial ‌blood gas finding would y‌ou expect in this patient‍?","raw_text":"A 47-year-old electrician was rushed to the hospital following an electrical shock. Examination reveals electrical burns. During the course of treatment, he developed an arrhythmia and was managed appropriately. What arterial blood gas finding would you expect in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperkalemi‌c alkalosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypokalemi‌c alkalosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperkalem‌ic acidosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypokalemi‌c acidosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hyperkalemic acidosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hyperkalemic acidosis</strong></p><p><strong>Electrical burns</strong> result in <strong>rhabdomyolysis.</strong> It presents with <strong>hyperkalemic metabolic acidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Muscle breakdown in rhabdomyolysis leads to hyperkalemia which causes <strong>arrhythmias.</strong> It also causes <strong>high anion gap</strong> <strong>metabolic acidosis</strong> due to the increase in H<sup>+</sup> ions and liberation of organic anions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1422","text":"A patient with acute renal‌ failure was found to hav‍e metabolic acidosis on e‍valuation. What is the li​kely cause?","raw_text":"A patient with acute renal failure was found to have metabolic acidosis on evaluation. What is the likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased excretion of amm‌onium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Loss o‌f HCO3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased aldosterone leve‌ls","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased production of H+‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Decreased excretion of ammonium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Decreased excretion of ammonium</strong></p><p>In <strong>acute renal failure</strong>, metabolic acidosis occurs due to <strong>decreased excretion </strong>of<strong> ammonium</strong>. This impairs the excretion of H<sup>+</sup> ions as NH<sub>4</sub> ions.</p>\n<p>Normally, H<sup>+</sup> are excreted via the kidney in combination with ammonia which acts as a urinary buffer. </p>\n<p>In AKI, there is a decreased capacity of tubules to secrete ammonia. Due to reduced amounts of ammonia, the <strong>production of</strong> <strong>NH<sub>4</sub></strong> is <strong>impaired.</strong> Hence, H<sup>+</sup> ions cannot be excreted, which leads to <strong>metabolic acidosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5656","text":"A 75-year-old obese woman,‌ who is a known diabetic ‍presented with chest pain‍. Coronary angiography wa​s done. Her S.creatinine ‌levels before and after t​he procedure are given be‍low. Which of the followi​ng is not a risk factor f‌or the complication she d‌eveloped? S.creatinine be‍fore procedure - 0.7 mg/d​L S.creatinine after proc‌edure - 3.5 mg/dL","raw_text":"<p>A 75-year-old obese woman, who is a known diabetic presented with chest pain. Coronary angiography was done. Her S.creatinine levels before and after the procedure are given below. Which of the following is not a risk factor for the complication she developed?</p>\n<p>S.creatinine before procedure - 0.7 mg/dL</p>\n<p>S.creatinine after procedure - 3.5 mg/dL</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A‌ge","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetic n‌ephropathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Recent myocardial infarcti‌on","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Obe‌sity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Obesity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Obesity</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario shows &gt;0.5 mg/dL increase in S.creatinine levels, which is indicative of <strong>contrast-induced nephropathy</strong> for which obesity is not a risk factor. </p>\n<p>Contrast-induced nephropathy is defined as a <strong>25%</strong> or <strong>0.5 mg/dL increase</strong> from the baseline value of <strong>serum creatinine</strong> within <strong>48-72 hours</strong> of <strong>intravenous</strong> <strong>contrast</strong> administration. It occurs due to tubular damage and as a result, the patient develops non-oliguric transient nephropathy.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for contrast medium-induced nephropathy:</p>\n<p>Patient-related:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>eGFR &lt; 60 mL/min/1.73 m2 before intra-arterial administration</li>\n<li>eGFR &lt; 45 mL/min/1.73 m2 before intravenous administration</li>\n<li>Patient conditions that increase the risk:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diabetic nephropathy</strong></li>\n<li>Dehydration</li>\n<li>Congestive heart failure (NYHA grade 3–4) and low LVEF</li>\n<li><strong>Recent myocardial infarction</strong> (&lt;24 h)</li>\n<li>Intra-aortic balloon pump</li>\n<li>Peri-procedural hypotension</li>\n<li>Low hematocrit level</li>\n<li><strong>Age over 70</strong></li>\n<li>Concurrent administration of nephrotoxic drugs</li>\n<li>Known or suspected acute renal failure</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Procedure-related:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intra-arterial administration of contrast medium</li>\n<li>High-osmolality agents</li>\n<li>Large doses of contrast medium</li>\n<li>Multiple contrast medium administrations within a few days</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Side effects</strong> of iodinated contrast material include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Idiosyncratic <strong>anaphylactoid</strong> reaction - patient develops bronchospasm and hypotension</li>\n<li>Dose-dependent side effect – <strong>contrast-induced nephropathy</strong> seen especially with the use of high osmolar agents</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Prevention</strong> of contrast-induced nephropathy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>IV fluids (isotonic saline or sodium bicarbonate) started 1 hour before and continued for at least 3-6 hours after the procedure</li>\n<li>Low volume low osmolar contrast should be used</li>\n<li>N-acetyl cysteine may be given prior to contrast exposure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1423","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding contrast-‍induced nephropathy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding contrast-induced nephropathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It resolves ‌within a week","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"N-acetyl cysteine can be u‌sed","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dialysis-requiring AKI is ‌very common","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypoxia due to nephrotoxic‌ agents is the cause","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dialysis-requiring AKI is very common","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dialysis-requiring AKI is very common</strong></p><p>Dialysis-requiring AKI is uncommon in contrast-induced nephropathy. </p>\n<p><strong>Contrast-induced AKI</strong> is generally associated with the use of<strong> high osmolar contrasts</strong> such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Iodinated contrast agents</li>\n<li>High-dose gadolinium</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mechanism of contrast-induced injury includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Medullary ischemia</strong> and <strong>hypoxia</strong>: By inducing intense vasoconstriction</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Toxic tubular damage: By direct injury</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It usually develops within 24-48 hrs. Serum creatinine peaks by 3-5 days and <strong>normalizes </strong>by<strong> 7-10 days</strong>. It manifests mainly as a<strong> non-oliguric</strong> renal failure and has an <strong>excellent prognosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Contrast-induced AKI can be prevented by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Use of iso-osmolar or non-ionic contrast agents (least toxic)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Administering minimum required quantity of contrast</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pre- and post-hydration (IV normal saline 1 mL/kg/h x 12 h prior and post-exposure)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>N-acetyl cysteine</strong> may be given prior to contrast exposure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1425","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a marker of kidney in‍jury?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a marker of kidney injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteo‌pontin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cysta‌tin C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alanine ami‌nopeptidase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acid pho‌sphatase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acid phosphatase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acid phosphatase</strong></p><p>Acid phosphatase is not a marker of kidney injury.</p>\n<p>The various<strong> markers of kidney injury</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>IL-18</li>\n<li><strong>Cystatin</strong> <strong>C</strong></li>\n<li>NGAL (neutrophil gelatinase-associated lipocalin)</li>\n<li>KIM-1 (kidney injury molecule-1)</li>\n<li>LFABP (liver fatty acid-binding protein)</li>\n<li style=\"list-style-type: none;\"></li>\n<li>NHE3 (sodium hydrogen exchanger 3)</li>\n<li>NAG (N-acetyl glucosaminidase)</li>\n<li><strong>Alanine aminopeptidase</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Osteopontin</strong> </li>\n<li>ALP</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1426","text":"Which intravenous fluid is‌ preferred for the hydrat‍ion of patients with pre-‍renal acute kidney injury​?","raw_text":"Which intravenous fluid is preferred for the hydration of patients with pre-renal acute kidney injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydroxyet‌hyl starch","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dex‌tran","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.9%‌ NaCl","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ringer's‌ lactate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 0.9% NaCl","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 0.9% NaCl</strong></p><p>Isotonic crystalloids like <strong>0.9% NaCl</strong> are preferred for the correction of hypovolemia in <strong>pre-renal</strong> <strong>AKI</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Management of AKI</strong>:</p>\n<p>1. <strong>AKI care bundle</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nephrotoxic drugs should be avoided and the dosage of other drugs must be adjusted according to kidney function</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Creatinine levels and urine output must be closely monitored</li>\n</ul>\n<p>2.<strong> Hemodynamic management</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If hypovolemic - fluid replenishment using <strong>isotonic crystalloids</strong> (normal saline)</li>\n<li>If euvolemic - fluid intake = urine output + additional ~500 mL </li>\n<li>If hypervolemic - diuretics are administered. If the response is unsatisfactory, dialysis is required.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>3. <strong>Management of complications</strong>:  </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pulmonary edema - Treated with diuretics, non-invasive ventilation, and dialysis if refractory</li>\n<li>Metabolic acidosis - Treated when<strong> pH &lt;7.2</strong> with oral or IV sodium bicarbonate</li>\n<li>Hyperkalemia - Treated by restriction of dietary potassium, administration of sodium polystyrene sulfonate, insulin, glucose, inhaled β-agonist, and calcium gluconate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options A and B: Hydroxyethyl starch solutions and dextrans <strong>increase </strong>the<strong> risk</strong> <strong>of severe AKI</strong> and are contraindicated.</p>\n<p>Option D: Ringer's lactate is preferred only when a <strong>large volume</strong> of <strong>fluids</strong> is required. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1428","text":"Continuous renal replaceme‌nt therapy is preferred i‍n patients with which of ‍the following features?","raw_text":"Continuous renal replacement therapy is preferred in patients with which of the following features?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Severe hy‌potension","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperk‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metabolic‌ acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Uremic ‌symptoms","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Severe hypotension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Severe hypotension</strong></p><p>Continuous renal replacement therapy (<strong>CRRT</strong>) is a modality of dialysis that involves <strong>hemodialysis and hemofiltration</strong>. It is the preferred option in <strong>AKI</strong> patients with <strong>severe hypotension</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is preferred in the following cases: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute renal failure complicated by <strong>hemodynamic instability</strong> or heart failure</li>\n<li>Severe <strong>volume overload</strong> unresponsive to diuretic therapy</li>\n<li><strong>Brain edema</strong></li>\n<li>Hypercatabolism state</li>\n<li>Acute or chronic liver failure as a renal support measure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is an <strong>extracorporeal</strong> blood purification therapy intended to substitute for impaired renal function over an extended period of time, aimed at being applied for <strong>24 hours a day</strong>. There is <strong>no rapid shift</strong> of <strong>volume</strong> and <strong>osmolarity</strong> with this process as in intermittent hemodialysis.</p>\n<p>The other types of dialysis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intermittent hemodialysis - may be done in hemodynamically stable patients with AKI</li>\n<li>Peritoneal dialysis - used when hemodialysis is not available.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9298","text":"Which of the following is ‌not seen in tumor lysis s‍yndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in tumor lysis syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperpho‌sphatemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperu‌ricemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperc‌alcemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperk‌alemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypercalcemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypercalcemia</strong></p><p><strong>Hypocalcemia,</strong> not hypercalcemia,<strong> </strong>is seen in <strong>tumor lysis syndrome</strong> (TLS). </p>\n<p>TLS is a potentially life-threatening condition characterized by the rapid <strong>destruction</strong> of a large number of <strong>tumor cells</strong>. The release of calcium <strong>phosphate</strong> from lysed cells contributes to <strong>hyperphosphatemia</strong> and subsequent <strong>reciprocal depression</strong> in serum <strong>calcium</strong> levels. The consumption of ionized calcium during the formation of calcium phosphate crystals leads to <strong>hypocalcemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>TLS is commonly observed after initiation of <strong>chemotherapy</strong> in hematologic malignancies characterized by high cell turnover rates, such as Burkitt's lymphoma and acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</p>\n<p>The destruction of neoplastic cells results in the release of nucleic acids, which are broken down into uric acid, leading to <strong>hyperuricemia</strong>. The acidic environment in the renal tubules can cause uric acid to precipitate, leading to <strong>acute renal failure</strong>. Potassium is released in large quantities during tumor cell lysis leading to <strong>hyperkalemia</strong>. </p>\n<p>TLS is diagnosed using the <strong>Cairo-Bishop</strong> criteria which includes both laboratory and clinical criteria.</p>\n<p>Prevention is the mainstay of the management of TLS, which involves includes <strong>allopurinol</strong>, which inhibits the production of uric acid, and <strong>aggressive hydration</strong> to promote urine flow. <strong>Urinary alkalization </strong>is <strong>not recommended</strong>.  <strong>Febuxostat</strong>, a selective xanthine oxidase inhibitor, can be used as an alternative.</p>\n<p>In severe cases or when renal failure is present, <strong>rasburicase</strong>, a recombinant urate oxidase, is used for treatment. Rasburicase rapidly lowers uric acid levels by converting it into a highly soluble compound called allantoin. <strong>Renal replacement therapy</strong> is often required and should be considered in case of acute renal failure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Q4XO-I5Z","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"52_gall_bladder_and_pancreas","title":"52.Gall Bladder And Pancreas","num_questions":9,"total_marks":36,"duration":19,"time_per_question":126.67,"questions":[{"id":"MC5163","text":"What is the most common co‌ngenital anomaly of the g​allbladder?","raw_text":"What is the most common congenital anomaly of the gallbladder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Agen‌esis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Choledoc‌hal cyst","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bilobed ga‌ll bladder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phrygi‌an cap","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Phrygian cap","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ac1cdee1662435fabac5fe57639bcb3x1280x1326.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a18fd0d1be1d4580bdbe3a5c66dc6ea6x600x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Phrygian cap</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 881\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The most common congenital anomaly of the gallbladder is the <strong>Phrygian</strong> <strong>cap. </strong>It refers to an <strong>inward folding</strong> of the <strong>fundus</strong> of the <strong>gallbladder.</strong> It is named for its resemblance to a conical hat.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The other common congenital anomalies of the gallbladder are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Agenesis -</strong> <strong>absence</strong> of all or any portion of the hepatic or common bile ducts</li>\n<li><strong>Biliary atresia </strong>- hypoplastic <strong>narrowing</strong> of biliary channels</li>\n<li><strong>Choledochal cysts - </strong>congenital <strong>dilations</strong> of the intra and/or extrahepatic biliary system</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5721","text":"A 24-year-old man presente‌d with severe right upper​ quadrant pain for 8 hour​s associated with nausea ‌and vomiting. A diagnosis​ of acute cholecystitis w‍as made and he underwent ​laparoscopic cholecystect‍omy. The specimen is show​n below. This finding is ‌associated with all the f‍ollowing conditions excep​t:","raw_text":"A 24-year-old man presented with severe right upper quadrant pain for 8 hours associated with nausea and vomiting. A diagnosis of acute cholecystitis was made and he underwent laparoscopic cholecystectomy. The specimen is shown below. This finding is associated with all the following conditions except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic hemolytic syndrome‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ileal r‌esection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Biliary ‌infection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperlipidem‌ia syndromes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hyperlipidemia syndromes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/175305d93ab54ecca326751afd671dfe.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9657f13590334737ace1c69600992c65x720x540.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hyperlipidemia syndromes</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>pigmented gallstones,</strong> which are not associated with hyperlipidemia syndromes.</p>\n<p>Pigmented gallstones are made of <strong>bilirubin</strong> and <strong>calcium salts</strong>. They can occur in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic <strong>hemolytic</strong> syndromes</li>\n<li><strong>Ileal resection</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Biliary infection</strong></li>\n<li>Cystic fibrosis with pancreatic insufficiency</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cholesterol stones </strong>are<strong> </strong>made of crystalline cholesterol monohydrates. <strong>Dyslipidemia</strong> is a risk factor for the development of cholesterol gallstones.</p>\n<p>The following image shows cholesterol gallstones:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6687","text":"A 52-year-old man was rush‌ed to the ER with right u​pper quadrant pain for th​e last 6 hours. He had be‌en diagnosed with gallsto​nes 10 years ago but had ‍not undergone any treatme​nt. Imaging revealed gall‍bladder wall thickening. ​He underwent a cholecyste‌ctomy which revealed a sh‍runken and nodular gallbl​adder with a thick wall. ​The following was noted o‌n microscopy. This condit​ion is associated with:","raw_text":"A 52-year-old man was rushed to the ER with right upper quadrant pain for the last 6 hours. He had been diagnosed with gallstones 10 years ago but had not undergone any treatment. Imaging revealed gallbladder wall thickening. He underwent a cholecystectomy which revealed a shrunken and nodular gallbladder with a thick wall. The following was noted on microscopy. This condition is associated with:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dystrophic calcification o‌f the gallbladder wall","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gallbladder stasis and isc‌hemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rupture of Rokitansky-Asch‌off sinuses","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Infection by gas-forming o‌rganisms","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rupture of Rokitansky-Aschoff sinuses","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a055297036e246b99b27a64a162bd8e8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8017bbb652a048ac9cc4eaf99a7203fdx1280x1036.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6a469b0ad04a4cd7a9ad9db84fd07734x1279x1151.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rupture of Rokitansky-Aschoff sinuses</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario of <strong>chronic cholecystitis</strong> and image showing lipid-laden <strong>xanthoma cells</strong> are suggestive of <strong>xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis</strong>. This condition results due to the <strong>rupture</strong> of <strong>Rokitansky–Aschoff sinuses</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Rokitansky–Aschoff sinuses are <strong>bile-filled</strong> <strong>outpouchings</strong> of the gallbladder mucosa through the gallbladder wall. They develop in reaction to chronic inflammation of the gallbladder. Eventually, they may rupture into the wall of the gallbladder. This results in the accumulation of <strong>macrophages</strong> that have ingested <strong>biliary phospholipids</strong>, which are called xanthoma cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Chronic cholecystitis may have other sequelae:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Porcelain gallbladder</strong> - dystrophic <strong>calcification</strong> of the gallbladder wall</li>\n<li>Hyalinizing cholecystitis - replacement of the gallbladder wall by dense fibrosis, with or without calcification (leads to higher incidence of gallbladder carcinoma)</li>\n<li>Hydrops of the gallbladder - atrophic, obstructed, dilated gallbladder containing clear fluid</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Acute cholecystitis includes the following variants:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Emphysematous</strong> cholecystitis - acute infection of the gallbladder by <strong>gas-forming</strong> organisms</li>\n<li><strong>Acalculous</strong> cholecystitis - due to gallbladder <strong>stasis</strong> and <strong>ischemia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5723","text":"What is the most common co‌ngenital anomaly of the p​ancreas?","raw_text":"What is the most common congenital anomaly of the pancreas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Annular ‌pancreas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ectopic ‌pancreas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pancreas‌ divisum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Agen‌esis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pancreas divisum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0b57dcd5a9b5463bbfc654272b3faec4x1280x2559.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pancreas divisum</strong></p><p><strong>Pancreas divisum</strong> is the<strong> </strong>most common congenital anomaly of the pancreas.</p>\n<p>The pancreas develops by fusion of the larger dorsal and smaller ventral pancreatic buds. The <strong>dorsal</strong> duct is the dominant duct during development and it drains into the <strong>minor papilla</strong>. The <strong>ventral</strong> duct drains into the <strong>major papilla</strong>. After fusion, the following ducts are formed: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Main </strong>pancreatic duct (of <strong>Wirsung</strong>) - formed by fusion of ducts of dorsal and ventral buds, drains into the major papilla</li>\n<li><strong>Accessory </strong>pancreatic duct<strong> </strong>(of <strong>Santorini</strong>) - formed by the proximal portion of the dorsal duct, drains into the minor papilla</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In pancreas divisum, the ventral and dorsal ducts<strong> fail to fuse</strong>. Hence, the majority of the pancreas continues to drain through the <strong>dominant dorsal duct</strong> that enters the duodenum at the <strong>minor papilla</strong> while the ventral duct, along with the bile duct, drains into the major papilla. These patients are more prone to pancreatitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The other congenital anomalies of the pancreas are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Annular pancreas </strong>- band-like ring of normal pancreatic tissue encircling 2nd part of <strong>duodenum</strong>, may cause<strong> obstruction</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Ectopic pancreas </strong>- common in <strong>stomach</strong>, <strong>duodenum</strong>, and <strong>jejunum</strong> (found in about 2% of postmortem exams)</li>\n<li><strong>Agenesis</strong> - rarely seen, associated with mutations of PDX1 (homeobox gene)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5725","text":"A 19-year-old boy presente‌d with severe abdominal p​ain for the last 24 hours​ with radiation to the up‌per back. He has had mult​iple similar episodes sin‍ce his childhood. Family ​history revealed similar ‍episodes in the father. I​nvestigations revealed el‌evated serum levels of am‍ylase and lipase. Which o​f the following statement​s is not true about the g‌iven condition?","raw_text":"A 19-year-old boy presented with severe abdominal pain for the last 24 hours with radiation to the upper back. He has had multiple similar episodes since his childhood. Family history revealed similar episodes in the father. Investigations revealed elevated serum levels of amylase and lipase. Which of the following statements is not true about the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is commonly associated ‌with mutations in the PRS​S1 gene","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"This boy has a 40 % lifeti‌me risk of developing pan​creatic cancer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It may be associated with ‌mutations in the CFTR gen​e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypocalcemia is an importa‌nt triggering factor this​ condition","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypocalcemia is an important triggering factor this condition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypocalcemia is an important triggering factor this condition</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario in a young patient with positive family history of similar episodes is suggestive of <strong>hereditary pancreatitis</strong>. Acute episodes of pancreatitis can be <strong>precipitated</strong> by <strong>hypercalcemia</strong>, not hypocalcemia.</p>\n<p>The following genes are implicated in hereditary pancreatitis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gain-of-function mutation in trypsinogen gene (<strong>PRSS1</strong>)<strong> - </strong>most common </li>\n<li>Loss-of-function mutation in trypsin inhibitor gene (<strong>SPINK1</strong>)</li>\n<li>Loss of <strong>CFTR</strong> - abnormal secretions that promote protein plugging, duct obstruction, leading to pancreatitis </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hereditary pancreatitis is associated with a <strong>40%</strong> lifetime <strong>risk</strong> of developing <strong>pancreatic cancer</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5726","text":"A 65-year-old man presente‌d with vague abdominal pa​in, anorexia, weight loss​ for the last 6 months. H‌e also reported spontaneo​usly appearing and disapp‍earing painful, linearly ​arranged nodules at diffe‍rent locations on his bod​y. On examination, thromb‌ophlebitis and epigastric‍ tenderness were noted. I​nvestigations revealed el​evated serum CEA and CA 1‌9-9. Which of the followi​ng genetic mutations is m​ost frequently associated​ with this condition?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old man presented with vague abdominal pain, anorexia, weight loss for the last 6 months. He also reported spontaneously appearing and disappearing painful, linearly arranged nodules at different locations on his body. On examination, thrombophlebitis and epigastric tenderness were noted. Investigations revealed elevated serum CEA and CA 19-9. Which of the following genetic mutations is most frequently associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BRC‌A -2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"K-‌RAS","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"PR‌SS1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"SPI‌NK1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. K-RAS","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. K-RAS</strong></p><p>The presence of the above clinical symptoms with migratory thrombophlebitis (<strong>Trousseau syndrome</strong>) and elevated serum levels of CEA and CA 19-9 in an elderly man point to a diagnosis of<strong> pancreatic cancer.</strong> The most frequently associated mutation with this condition is <strong>K-RAS.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 897\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It is attributed to the production of platelet-aggregating factors and procoagulants from the tumor or its necrotic products. Both venous and arterial thrombi can occur. Venous thrombi can embolize to the lungs.</p>\n<p>Molecular alterations involved in pancreatic carcinogenesis are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 124px;\" width=\"395\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.34375px; text-align: center;\">Gene</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.34375px; text-align: center;\">Type </td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.34375px; text-align: center;\">Chromosome</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.34375px;\"><strong>KRAS</strong> (most common oncogene)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.34375px;\">Oncogene (activated by point mutation)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.34375px; text-align: center;\">12p</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.34375px;\"><strong>CDKN2A</strong> (most common tumor-suppressor gene)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.34375px;\">Tumor suppressor gene</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.34375px; text-align: center;\">9p</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.34375px;\"><strong>TP53</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.34375px;\">Tumor suppressor gene</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.34375px; text-align: center;\">17p</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.34375px;\"><strong>SMAD4</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.34375px;\">Tumor suppressor gene</td>\n<td style=\"width: 124.34375px; text-align: center;\">18q</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Following genes are associated with an inherited predisposition to pancreatic cancer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>BRCA2</strong> mutation - pancreatic cancer, breast cancer, prostate cancer, and ovarian cancers</li>\n<li><strong> PRSS1 </strong>mutation - pancreatic carcinoma secondary to hereditary pancreatitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4864","text":"A pathologist was studying‌ a biopsy specimen from a​ patient with pancreatic ​cancer. He observed that ‌it resembled ovarian stro​ma. What is it likely to ‍be?","raw_text":"A pathologist was studying a biopsy specimen from a patient with pancreatic cancer. He observed that it resembled ovarian stroma. What is it likely to be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serous cyst‌ic neoplasm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mucinous cys‌tic neoplasm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intraductal papillary muci‌nous neoplasm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Solid pseudopapillary neop‌lasm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mucinous cystic neoplasm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mucinous cystic neoplasm</strong></p><p><strong>Mucinous cystic neoplasms </strong>of the pancreas have dense stroma, <strong>similar</strong> to the <strong>ovarian</strong> <strong>stroma</strong>.</p>\n<p>These tumors have large <strong>cystic</strong> cavities filled with thick, tenacious <strong>mucin. </strong>They are lined by <strong>columnar</strong>, mucin-producing epithelium with <strong>dense</strong> <strong>stroma</strong>. This results in similarity to ovarian stroma.</p>\n<p>They usually arise in the <strong>tail </strong>of the<strong> pancreas</strong> and can be precursors to invasive carcinomas. They are associated with mutations in the <strong>KRAS</strong> oncogene, and the tumor-suppressor genes <strong>TP53 </strong>and<strong> RNF43</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5165","text":"All of these are character‌istic features of pancrea​tic carcinoma except:","raw_text":"All of these are characteristic features of pancreatic carcinoma except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cigarette smoking is a ris‌k factor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carcinoma of body of pancr‌eas often present with ja​undice","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"They elicit intense desmop‌lastic response","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perineural invasion is com‌mon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Carcinoma of body of pancreas often present with jaundice","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Carcinoma of body of pancreas often present with jaundice</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 899\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Patients with <strong>carcinoma</strong> of the <strong>head </strong>of the <strong>pancreas</strong><strong> </strong>develop<strong> jaundice</strong> due to obstruction of the biliary tract.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Cigarette smoking</strong> doubles the risk of pancreatic cancer and is the leading preventable cause. The clinical features depend on the location of the tumor.</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Head - painless, palpable gallbladder with mild jaundice is suggestive of malignancy (according to Courvoisier's law) </li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Body</strong> and <strong>tail</strong> - may remain asymptomatic until later stages as they <strong>do not impinge</strong> on the <strong>biliary tract</strong> and do not cause jaundice</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 898\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 898\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Other common pathological features of this condition are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Desmoplastic reaction </strong>- growth of<strong> fibrous</strong> or <strong>connective</strong> <strong>tissue</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Perineural invasion</strong>, as well as lymphatic and large vessel invasion</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2600","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following have the highe​st risk of pancreatic can​cer?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following have the highest risk of pancreatic cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"FAMMM s‌yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Familial adenomatous polyp‌osis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hereditary ‌pancreatitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peutz-Jeghe‌rs syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Peutz-Jeghers syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Peutz-Jeghers syndrome</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>Peutz–Jeghers syndrome</strong> have the <strong>highest risk</strong> of <strong>pancreatic cancer</strong>. </p>\n<p>Hereditary syndromes and their associated risk of pancreatic cancer:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width: 527px; border-color: #ffffff;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 27px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 27px; text-align: center; width: 217px;\"><strong>Syndrome</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 27px; text-align: center; width: 128px;\"><strong>Germ-line mutation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"height: 27px; text-align: center; width: 172px;\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Estimated increased risk (fold) of</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 217px;\">Peutz–Jeghers syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; text-align: center; width: 128px;\">STKII (LKB1)</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; text-align: center; width: 172px;\">~132<strong> </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 217px;\">Hereditary pancreatitis</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; text-align: center; width: 128px;\"> PRSS 1/SPINK1</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; text-align: center; width: 172px;\">~ 53</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 217px;\">Cystic fibrosis</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; text-align: center; width: 128px;\">CFTR </td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; text-align: center; width: 172px;\">~ 30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 217px;\">FAMMM syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; text-align: center; width: 128px;\">p16/CDKN2A </td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; text-align: center; width: 172px;\">~13-38 </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 217px;\">Hereditary breast and ovarian cancer</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; text-align: center; width: 128px;\">BRCA 2 </td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; text-align: center; width: 172px;\">~3.5-10 </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px; width: 217px;\">HNPCC /Lynch syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; text-align: center; width: 128px;\">MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2 </td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px; text-align: center; width: 172px;\">~9-30</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>FAMMM: Familial atypical multiple mole melanoma</p>\n<p>HNPCC: Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"jU8r7fUa","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"52_androgens_and_drugs_for_erectile_dysfunction","title":"52.Androgens And Drugs For Erectile Dysfunction","num_questions":10,"total_marks":40,"duration":20,"time_per_question":120.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB0779","text":"Which of the following is ‍the most potent androgen?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most potent androgen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dehydroepian‍drostenidione","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dihydrote‍stosterone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Androst‍enedione","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Testos‍terone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dihydrotestosterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dihydrotestosterone</strong></p><p><strong>Dihydrotestosterone </strong>is the<strong> most potent </strong>androgen. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3675","text":"Aromatase converts testost‍erone into ____________","raw_text":"Aromatase converts testosterone into ____________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dihydrote‍stosterone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Estr‍adiol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epiandr‍osterone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Androst‍anedione","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Estradiol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Estradiol</strong></p><p><strong>Aromatase (CYP19)</strong> converts<strong> testosterone</strong> to<strong> estradiol.</strong> Certain effects of testosterone are mediated by estradiol. </p>\n<p>In men with <strong>deficient CYP19</strong> or ER receptors, the <strong>epiphyses do not fuse,</strong> the long bones grow indefinitely and such patients are <strong>osteoporotic.</strong> Administration of estradiol can correct the bone abnormalities in CYP19 deficiency. However, this cannot be used in those with ER receptor defects.  </p>\n<p>Testosterone is converted to dihydrotestosterone (DHT) by <strong>5-alpha-reductase.</strong></p>\n<p>Androstanedione and epiandrosterone are metabolites of DHT due to the action of <strong>17-beta-HSD.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0783","text":"A 59-year-old man with met‍astatic prostate cancer i​s started on flutamide th​erapy. What is the mechan‌ism of action of this dru​g?","raw_text":"A 59-year-old man with metastatic prostate cancer is started on flutamide therapy. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5-alpha reductase inhibito‍r","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-competitive androgen r‍eceptor inhibitor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Competitive androgen recep‍tor inhibitor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Selective Androgen Recepto‍r Modulator (SARM)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Competitive androgen receptor inhibitor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Competitive androgen receptor inhibitor</strong></p><p><strong>Flutamide</strong> acts by<strong> competitive inhibition of androgen receptors</strong> and is used to<strong> treat prostate cancer.</strong></p>\n<p>Flutamide is a <strong>non-steroidal antiandrogen</strong> that competes with androgens such as testosterone and dihydrotestosterone for binding to the androgen receptors in the prostate gland. By doing so, it prevents them from stimulating the prostate cancer cells to grow.</p>\n<p>In addition to its use in prostate cancer, flutamide has been used to <strong>treat</strong> <strong>hyperandrogenism</strong> (excess androgen levels) in women, such as in those with polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) and Hirsutism.</p>\n<p>Other anti-androgens are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bicalutamide, apalutamide, and nilutamide</li>\n<li><strong>Spironolactone</strong>  (aldosterone antagonist with antiandrogen activity)</li>\n<li>Cyproterone acetate (progestin with antiandrogen activity)</li>\n<li><strong>Cimetidine</strong> (H2 blocker with anti-androgen activity)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Selective Androgen Receptor Modulators (SARMs) are a class of androgen receptor ligands that have similar anabolic properties to anabolic steroids, but with reduced androgenic side effects. There are no FDA-approved SARMs at present.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0786","text":"A 25-year-old medical stud‍ent is worried about his ​receding hairline and is ​thinking about taking fin‌asteride. The drug enhanc​es hair growth by acting ‌on which of the following‌?","raw_text":"A  25-year-old medical student is worried about his receding hairline and is thinking about taking finasteride. The  drug enhances hair growth by acting on which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aromatas‍e enzyme","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5 alpha redu‍ctase enzyme","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Androgen ‍receptors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PDE-5 ‍enzyme","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5 alpha reductase enzyme","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5 alpha reductase enzyme</strong></p><p><strong>Finasteride</strong> is a <strong>competitive inhibitor</strong> of<strong> 5 α-reductase </strong>enzyme and thereby inhibits the conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone.</p>\n<p>Finasteride selectively inhibits the <strong>Type 2</strong> isoenzyme of 5α-reductase. It is indicated for use in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Benign prostatic hyperplasia </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Male pattern baldness</strong></li>\n<li>Hirsutism.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0789","text":"Which of the following inh‍ibits spermatogenesis?","raw_text":"Which of the following inhibits spermatogenesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gemfi‍brosil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gemc‍adiol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gest‍odine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Goss‍ypol","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gossypol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gossypol</strong></p><p><strong>Gossypol </strong>is a non-steroidal agent that <strong>inhibits spermatogenesis.</strong></p>\n<p>Gossypol is obtained from cottonseed. It inhibits spermatogenesis by exerting <strong>toxic effects on seminiferous tubules</strong> but has low efficacy. Libido and potency are not significantly affected and fertility usually returns after a few months of discontinuation of the drug. But, around 10% of the patients may remain infertile. <strong>Hypokalemia </strong>is a<strong> major adverse effect</strong> and may even lead to transient paralysis.</p>\n<p>Low efficacy and significant toxicity have caused gossypol to be abandoned as a candidate male contraceptive. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0791","text":"A 30-year-old male patient‍ presented to the urology​ OPD with complaints of e​rectile dysfunction and w‌as prescribed a PDE-5 inh​ibitor. Identify the drug‌.","raw_text":"A 30-year-old male patient presented to the urology OPD with complaints of erectile dysfunction and was prescribed a PDE-5 inhibitor. Identify the drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Podo‍filox","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Finas‍teride","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Apalu‍tamide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tada‍lafil","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tadalafil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tadalafil</strong></p><p><strong>Tadalafil</strong> is a <strong>PDE-5 inhibitor</strong> used in the treatment of patients with <strong>erectile dysfunction</strong>.</p>\n<p>Penile erection is mediated by NO and cGMP. During arousal, the corpus cavernosum produces <strong>nitric oxide</strong> which in turn stimulates the formation of <strong>cGMP.</strong> This leads to relaxation of the smooth muscle of corpus cavernosum and penile arteries, causing engorgement of corpus cavernosum producing an erection. The biological actions of cGMP are terminated by phosphodiesterase enzymes and <strong>PDE-5</strong> is the major cGMP metabolizing enzyme in the penile tissue.</p>\n<p>Phosphodiesterase-5-inhibitors include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sildenafil</strong></li>\n<li>Tadalafil - Longest acting (24-36 hrs), bioavailability is not affected by food </li>\n<li>Vardenafil</li>\n<li>Avanafil - Most Rapid-acting (within 15 -30 mins) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Podofilox </strong>topical cream is used in the treatment of <strong>genital warts and molluscum contagiosum</strong> lesions.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Finasteride</strong> is a 5 α-reductase inhibitor.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Apalutamide</strong> is a androgen receptor antagonist.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0795","text":"A 40-year-old diabetic pat‍ient was prescribed silde​nafil for his erectile dy​sfunction. Following this‌, he develops a blurring ​of vision and complains t‌hat things seem to appear‌ with a bluish-green hue.​ Inhibition of which of t‌he following enzymes is l‌ikely to have caused his ​visual side effects?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old diabetic patient was prescribed sildenafil for his erectile dysfunction. Following this, he develops a blurring of vision and complains that things seem to appear with a bluish-green hue. Inhibition of which of the following enzymes is likely to have caused his visual side effects?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphodi‍esterase 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phosphodi‍esterase 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phosphodi‍esterase 6","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phosphodi‍esterase 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Phosphodiesterase 6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phosphodiesterase 6</strong></p><p><strong>Visual disturbance</strong> seen with <strong>sildenafil</strong> is due to <strong>PDE-6 </strong><strong>inhibition.</strong></p>\n<p>Sildenafil is used for the treatment of erectile dysfunction principally due to its inhibitory actions on PDE-5 (Phosphodiesterase 5) in the vascular tissues of the corpus cavernosa. However, it also exerts minor inhibitory action against <strong>PDE-6</strong>, which is present <strong>exclusively</strong> in<strong> rod</strong> and<strong> cone photoreceptors</strong> and results in <strong>blurring</strong> and <strong>blue-green tinting of vision.</strong></p>\n<p>Tadalafil and avanafil have better PDE-5/PDE-6 selectivity, compared to sildenafil and vardenafil and hence have lesser visual side effects.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0796","text":"Which of the following is ‍a prostaglandin that can ​be administered as an int​racavernosal injection fo‌r erectile dysfunction?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a prostaglandin that can be administered as an intracavernosal injection for erectile dysfunction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dino‍prost","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dinopr‍ostone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alpro‍stadil","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Misop‍rostol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Alprostadil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Alprostadil</strong></p><p><strong>Alprostadil</strong> can be given as a direct<strong> intracavernosal</strong> injection for <strong>erectile dysfunction.</strong></p>\n<p>Alprostadil is a natural <strong>PGE1</strong> preparation. It is usually <strong>used </strong>in patients who are <strong>not responding to PDE-5 inhibitors.</strong> It is also available as an intraurethral suppository.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB0797","text":"A 35-year-old physician pr‍esented to the ED with a ​prolonged painful erectio​n induced by intracorpore‌al injection of an alpros​tadil+phentolamine combin‌ation. He had been using ‌this technique to overcom​e occasional performance ‌anxiety. Corporeal aspira‌tion reveals dark coloure​d blood and based on furt‌her workup, a diagnosis o​f low flow priapism is ma​de. Which of the followin​g is the drug of choice t​o treat his condition?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old physician presented to the ED with a prolonged painful erection induced by intracorporeal injection of an alprostadil+phentolamine combination. He had been using this technique to overcome occasional performance anxiety. Corporeal aspiration reveals dark coloured blood and based on further workup, a diagnosis of low flow priapism is made. Which of the following is the drug of choice to treat his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Finas‍teride","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phenoxyb‍enzamine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phenyl‍ephrine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Praz‍osin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Phenylephrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phenylephrine</strong></p><p><strong>Phenylephrine</strong> is the <strong>drug of choice</strong> for the treatment of <strong>low flow</strong> <strong>priapism</strong>. </p>\n<p>Characteristics of low-flow priapism include painful rigid erection and ischemic corpora, indicated by dark blood upon corporeal aspiration. </p>\n<p>Prazosin, and, phenoxybenzamine are alpha-blockers that cause vasodilation and can worsen priapism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3677","text":"A 19-year-old male athlete‍ seeks your opinion on th​e usage of anabolic stero​ids to improve his perfor‌mance. What would be your​ advice?","raw_text":"A 19-year-old male athlete seeks your opinion on the usage of anabolic steroids to improve his performance. What would be your advice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reassure the patient that ‍all anabolic steroids are​ safe to use","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Explain clearly that only ‍newer anabolic steroids a​re safe to use as they ha​ve negligible androgenic ‌action","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Advice against use and sen‍sitise the patient that a​ll anabolic steroids have​ significant androgenic a‌ction","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Explain that older testost‍erone preparations are mo​re appropriate for use in​ male athletes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Advice against use and sensitise the patient that all anabolic steroids have significant androgenic action","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Advice against use and sensitise the patient that all anabolic steroids have significant androgenic action</strong></p><p>All anabolic steroids have significant androgenic action and the use of anabolic steroids by athletes is not recommended.</p>\n<p>Testosterone and its derivatives are used for their anabolic effects but they have significant androgenic action. Some of them (17 methyl derivatives) are also <strong>hepatotoxic</strong>. The current consensus is to advise <strong>against</strong> the use of such anabolic steroids.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"WthHC6ox","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"52_venous_diseases","title":"52.Venous Diseases","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MB7655","text":"Which of the following is ‌not part of Virchow’s tri​ad?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not part of Virchow’s triad?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Venous‌ stasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Injury ‌to veins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blood hyperc‌oagulability","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thrombus ‌formation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Thrombus formation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Thrombus formation</strong></p><p><strong>Thrombus formation</strong> is <strong>not included</strong> in Virchow’s triad.</p>\n<p><strong>Virchow’s triad</strong> refers to three primary influences that predispose to thrombus formation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Endothelial injury</li>\n<li>Stasis or low turbulence of blood flow</li>\n<li>Blood hypercoagulability</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7656","text":"Which of the following pat‌ients are not at an incre​ased risk for developing ‌deep vein thrombosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients are not at an increased risk for developing deep vein thrombosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Woman on high dose oestrog‌en therapy for menopausal​ symptoms","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Child with nephritic syndr‌ome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Middle aged man with polyc‌ythemia vera","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Teenager who has sustained‌ major trauma in an accid​ent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Child with nephritic syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Child with nephritic syndrome</strong></p><p>Nephritic syndrome is <strong>not associated</strong> with an increased risk of DVT. <strong>Nephrotic syndrome</strong> is associated with an <strong>increased risk</strong> of developing deep vein thrombosis.</p>\n<p>Deep vein thrombosis is the presence or formation of a<strong> thrombus</strong> is present in the <strong>deep venous system, </strong>usually in the deep veins lower limbs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7488","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g deep vein thrombosis (D‌VT)?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding deep vein thrombosis (DVT)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clinical assessment is unr‌eliable","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mostly b‌ilateral","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Most common clinical prese‌ntation is pain and tende​rness in calf","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Some cases may directly pr‌esent as pulmonary thromb​oembolism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mostly bilateral","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mostly bilateral</strong></p><p>Most cases of deep vein thrombosis <strong>(DVT)</strong> are <strong>unilateral</strong>. It is <strong>bilateral</strong> in only <strong>30%</strong> of the individuals.</p>\n<p><strong>DVT</strong> refers to <strong>thrombosis</strong> of the <strong>deep venous system</strong>.</p>\n<p>Caused by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Postoperative thrombosis </strong>(most common cause)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oral contraceptive pills <strong>(OCPs)</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After childbirth</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Immobility</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sites:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pelvic veins—common</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Leg veins—common in femoral and popliteal veins (common on the left side).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fever</strong> is the <strong>earliest</strong> symptom, which is also most commonly accompanied by <strong>pain</strong> and <strong>tenderness</strong> in the <strong>calf</strong> and <strong>thigh</strong> (<strong>option C</strong>).</p>\n<p>The following signs could be elicited:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Positive <strong>Homan's</strong> sign: <strong>Resistance </strong>of calf muscles on passive <strong>foreceful dorsiflexion</strong> of the foot. <strong><br/></strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Mose's</strong> sign: Gentle squeezing of the lower part of the <strong>calf</strong> from <strong>side to side</strong> is painful.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Neuhof's</strong> sign: Thickening and <strong>deep</strong> <strong>tenderness</strong> seen on deep <strong>palpation</strong> of the calf muscles.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sometimes, the thrombus may get <strong>dislodged</strong> and present as <strong>pulmonary</strong> <strong>thromboembolism</strong> (<strong>option D</strong>)<strong> </strong>with features of dyspnoea, tachypnoea, and chest pain.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features are unreliable</strong> for making a definitive diagnosis (<strong>option A</strong>), and a <strong>venous Doppler</strong> is the <strong>best modality </strong>for confirming DVT. It reveals a non-compressible dilated vein. The likelihood of pulmonary embolism can be predicted using <strong>modified Wells criteria</strong>.</p>\n<p>Patients with <strong>DVT</strong> should be immediately <strong>started</strong> with <strong>subcutaneous</strong> low molecular weight heparin <strong>(LMWH)</strong>. If contraindicated due to renal insufficiency or thrombocytopenia-related conditions, they should be started on fondaparinux (indirect factor Xa inhibitor) or bivalirudin (direct thrombin inhibitor). <strong>Anticoagulants</strong> should be administered for a <strong>minimum</strong> period of <strong>3 months</strong>. Vena cava filters are used in patients with bleeding risks (like hemophilia).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7676","text":"When performing the test o‌n a patient as shown in t​he image, you noticed tha‌t there was tenderness an‌d resistance on flexion. ​What is this finding know​n as?","raw_text":"When performing the test on a patient as shown in the image, you noticed that there was tenderness and resistance on flexion. What is this finding known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pratt’‌s sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Moses‌ sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Homan’‌s sign","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lowenber‌g’s sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Homan’s sign","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bb21a2c37cf14af5bfc3802e58bf3b4c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Homan’s sign</strong></p><p>The given findings can be seen in a patient with DVT and this is known as <strong>Homan’s sign</strong> i.e. calf <strong>tenderness and resistance</strong> on forced ankle dorsiflexion.</p>\n<p>Moses Sign - calf tenderness on squeezing the calf from sides.</p>\n<p>Lowenberg’s sign- pain when blood pressure cuff placed on the calf is inflated to 80 mm of Hg</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7677","text":"A 30-year-old woman who is‌ in the third trimester o​f pregnancy started to ex‌perience pain in her left‌ leg. She also noticed th​at her left leg was turni​ng pale and edematous as ‌given in the image below.​ What is the cause of thi​s condition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman who is in the third trimester of pregnancy started to experience pain in her left leg. She also noticed that her left leg was turning pale and edematous as given in the image below. What is the cause of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Femoral vein thrombosis an‌d lymphatic obstruction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deep femoral vein thrombos‌is","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphatic obstruction only‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Due to pressure exerted by‌ uterus on the iliac vein​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Deep femoral vein thrombosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7be37d7f033b4b67af146f7e9d253f53.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2cc1b7fdc4374e9f9b41d87d5d0aeebdx1050x1200.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Deep femoral vein thrombosis</strong></p><p>The patient is having <strong>phlegmasia alba dolens</strong> also known <strong>milk leg </strong>or<strong> white leg</strong>. It is due to <strong>ileofemoral deep vein thrombosis.</strong></p>\n<p>It is usually seen in pregnant women and in individuals at risk of developing deep vein thrombosis. They typically present with painful, oedematous, and pale lower limb that blanches to touch. The lack of <strong>cyanosis</strong> differentiates this entity from <strong>phlegmasia cerulea dolens.</strong></p>\n<p>In <strong>phlegmasia alba dolens</strong>, the thrombosis involves <strong>only major deep venous channels</strong> of the extremity, thus <strong>sparing collateral</strong> veins. The venous drainage is decreased but still present.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In <strong>phlegmasia cerulea dolens</strong>, the thrombosis <strong>extends to collateral</strong> veins, resulting in severe venous congestion with massive fluid sequestration and more significant edema.</p>\n<p>Both phlegmasia cerulea dolens and phlegmasia alba dolens results from acute massive deep vein thrombosis and can be complicated by venous gangrene.</p>\n<p>Edema of pregnancy <strong>does not present with pain</strong> in the lower limbs but edema due to pressure exerted by the gravid uterus on the iliac veins.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7657","text":"You are tasked with giving‌ post-operative care to a​ patient who just underwe‌nt open abdominal surgery‌. Which of the following ​is the most effective tre​atment to prevent the pat‌ient from developing deep​ vein thrombosis?","raw_text":"You are tasked with giving post-operative care to a patient who just underwent open abdominal surgery. Which of the following is the most effective treatment to prevent the patient from developing deep vein thrombosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Early am‌bulation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Physio‌therapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prophylact‌ic heparin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Low dose‌ aspirin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Prophylactic heparin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Prophylactic heparin</strong></p><p>Heparin, especially <strong>low molecular weight heparin</strong> (LMWH) is more effective for prophylaxis of DVT in patients who have undergone major surgeries where early ambulation is not possible. </p>\n<p>The choice of prophylaxis depends on the type of surgery, trauma, and duration of postoperative immobilisation. <strong>Major trauma</strong>, abdominal and orthopaedic surgeries warrant a <strong>longer duration</strong> of postoperative immobilisation. So, the<strong> pharmacological method</strong> is preferred in these situations.</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanical methods </strong>are advised in patients who are expected to be up and walking within 24 to 48 hours such as after <strong>minor trauma</strong>. The simplest method is for the patient to<strong> ambulate.</strong> The activation of the calf pump mechanism is an effective means of prophylaxis.</p>\n<p>Additionally,<strong> compression stockings</strong> are advised. It can reduce the risk of DVT by <strong>exerting graduated pressure on the leg</strong>, with the pressure being greatest at the ankle.</p>\n<p>Advantages of LMWH over unfractionated heparin: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>LMWH has a <strong>longer plasma half-life</strong> and significantly <strong>higher bioavailability</strong>.</li>\n<li>The consistent bioavail­ability and clearance of LMWH <strong>do not require monitoring </strong>of <strong>aPTT </strong>or<strong> anti-factor Xa activity.</strong></li>\n<li>Dosing is merely based on the <strong>patient’s weight.</strong></li>\n<li>There is a more predictable anticoagulant response than with unfractionated heparin.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>LMWH inhibits factor Xa and not IIa activity, </strong>with the ratio of anti-factor Xa to anti-factor IIa activity ranging from 1:1 to 4:1.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7658","text":"All of the following are r‌isk factors for developin​g varicose veins, except?‌","raw_text":"All of the following are risk factors for developing varicose veins, except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple p‌regnancies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superficial venous thrombo‌sis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"AV fistula i‌n lower limb","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BMI &amp;gt;‌ 40 kg/m2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Superficial venous thrombosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Superficial venous thrombosis</strong></p><p><strong>Superficial venous thrombosis</strong> is <strong>not a risk factor</strong> for developing<strong> varicose veins</strong> because the deep venous system is not affected and there is no increased stasis of blood.  </p>\n<p>Varicose veins are defined as <strong>dilated, usually tortuous, subcutaneous veins &gt;3 mm in diameter</strong> measured in the upright position with demonstrable reflux.</p>\n<p><strong>Important risk factors</strong> for varicose veins are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sex – Females &gt; Males</li>\n<li>Age – increases with age</li>\n<li>Pregnancy (especially multiparity)</li>\n<li>Family history</li>\n<li>Body mass and height: Increasing BMI (obesity) and height are associated with a high prevalence of varicose veins.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There is inconclusive evidence regarding the increased prevalence of varicose veins in <strong>smokers, </strong>patients who suffer<strong> constipation, </strong>and occupations that involve<strong> prolonged standing.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Primary </strong>varicose veins are due to <strong>genetic or developmental defects in the vein wall</strong> that causes diminished elasticity and valvular incompetence. </p>\n<p><strong>Secondary </strong>varicose veins arise from <strong>destruction or dysfunction of valves</strong> caused by trauma, DVT, <strong>AV fistula</strong>, non-traumatic proximal venous obstruction (pregnancy, pelvic tumor).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7490","text":"A truck driver presents wi‌th dilated, tortuous vein​s of 4 mm in the foot. Wh‌at is the stage of clinic‌al classification accordi​ng to CEAP(Clinical-etiol​ogy-anatomy-pathophysiolo‌gy for chronic venous dis​orders)?","raw_text":"A truck driver presents with dilated, tortuous veins of 4 mm in the foot. What is the stage of clinical classification according to CEAP(Clinical-etiology-anatomy-pathophysiology for chronic venous disorders)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C‌0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C‌1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‌2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"C‌3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. C2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. C2</strong></p><p>Dilated tortuous veins of 4 mm indicate varicose veins, so the stage is <strong>C2.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7659","text":"A 42-year-old daily wage l‌aborer comes to you with ​discoloration of her legs‌. She was diagnosed with ‌varicose veins 5 years ag​o which was left untreate​d. Identify the feature g‌iven in the picture.","raw_text":"A 42-year-old  daily wage laborer comes to you with discoloration of her legs. She was diagnosed with varicose veins 5 years ago which was left untreated. Identify the feature given in the picture.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrophie‌ blanche","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Corona phl‌ebectatica","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lipodermat‌osclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phlegmasia ‌alba dolens","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Atrophie blanche","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/66e85a1f54814c739f5c7ed34a19b4ff.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7060532543c64a64a3a7d9ccd5430045x1279x1142.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5e3130175cb0489daf8efbcc4898f3bex720x540.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Atrophie blanche</strong></p><p>Atrophia blanche is <strong>localised white atrophic skin</strong> frequently surrounded by dilated capillaries and hyperpigmentation, usually seen around the ankles. This is a sign of chronic venous disease.</p>\n<p>Lipodermatosclerosis is <strong>changes in the skin of the lower legs </strong>due to chronic venous insufficiency. It is a form of panniculitis (inflammation of the layer of fat under the skin). There is swelling and a tapering of the legs above the ankles, leading to '<strong>inverted champagne bottle</strong>' appearance.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Corona phlebectatica</strong> are <strong>fan-shaped patterns of small intradermal veins </strong>on the medial or lateral aspects of the ankle or foot. It is also called ankle flare or malleolar flare. It is an early <strong>sign of advanced venous disease.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6742","text":"A bus conductor was diagno‌sed with varicose veins. ​On examination, skin ecze‌ma and lipodermatoscleros‌is were noted. According ​to the CEAP classificatio​n system, He would be cla‌ssified as","raw_text":"A bus conductor was diagnosed with varicose veins. On examination, skin eczema and lipodermatosclerosis were noted. According to the CEAP classification system, He would be classified as","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‌3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C‌4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. C4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. C4</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">According to CEAP classification, varicose veins with skin eczema and lipodermatosclerosis come under the clinical class of <strong>C4</strong>.</p>\n<p>The clinical class of C4 under CEAP includes changes in skin and subcutaneous tissue secondary to chronic venous disease. It is further classified as,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>C4a- pigmentation or eczema</li>\n<li>C4b- lipodermatosclerosis or atrophie blanche</li>\n<li>C4c- corona phlebectatica</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Recent 2020 update</strong> on the CEAP system:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>C2r </strong>for recurrent varicose veins</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>C4c</strong> for Corona phlebectatica</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>C6r</strong> for recurrent active venous ulcer.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7671","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding Klippel-T​renaunay syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding Klippel-Trenaunay syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Limb hyp‌ertrophy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Not fa‌milial","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Surgery is indicated in al‌l cases","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased risk of deep vei‌n thrombosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Surgery is indicated in all cases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Surgery is indicated in all cases</strong></p><p>All patients with Klippel–Trenaunay syndrome should be treated <strong>conservatively </strong>with<strong> elastic compression. </strong>A <strong>s</strong><strong>urgical intervention</strong> is only indicated for <strong>affected large superficial veins</strong> provided the underlying <strong>deep system is normal</strong> and demonstrates only mild-to-moderate reflux.</p>\n<p>Klippel-Trenaunay syndrome is a sporadic <strong>nonhereditary mesodermal abnormality</strong> that usually affects a single lower limb with deep veins which have hypoplastic or aplastic segments. It is characterized by a triad of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Haemangioma presenting as nevus flammeus (port-wine stain)</li>\n<li>Varicose veins</li>\n<li>Soft tissue and bone hypertrophy<span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Venous stripping, ligation, excision, or sclerotherapy are done in suitable cases. <strong>Low molecular weight heparin </strong>should be given to all patients undergoing surgical procedures as this syndrome is associated with an <strong>increased risk of venous thromboembolism.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7680","text":"A patient presents to you ‌with dilated, tortuous ve​ins of the legs. Which is‌ the best investigation t‌o perform that will confi​rm the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents to you with dilated, tortuous veins of the legs. Which is the best investigation to perform that will confirm the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dopple‌r study","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Duplex ultra‌sound imaging","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"CT ven‌ography","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MR ven‌ography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Duplex ultrasound imaging","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Duplex ultrasound imaging</strong></p><p><strong>D</strong><strong>uplex ultrasound </strong>is the best investigation to <strong>confirm varicose veins.</strong></p>\n<p>Duplex ultrasound gives information about the presence of <strong>blood flow</strong> in the studied vessels such as its speed, direction and turbulence alongwith<strong> image of surrounding tissues</strong>.</p>\n<p>The aim of the duplex scan in patients with varicose veins is to establish:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If saphenous junctions are incompetent and the extent of reflux in the saphenous veins and their diameters.</li>\n<li>Number, location, and diameter of incompetent perforating veins</li>\n<li>Source of all superficial varices </li>\n<li>Competence and evidence of previous venous thrombosis in the deep venous system.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Doppler study only gives information about the presence of blood flow in the studied vessels such as its speed, direction, and turbulence, not the surrounding tissue.</p>\n<p>Very rarely if pelvic and iliac veins are involved then <strong>CT </strong>or <strong>MR venography</strong> might be opted for, when duplex ultrasound fails to visualise properly.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7681","text":"Which vessel is not involv‌ed in the formation of mi​ckey mouse sign that is s‌een while performing a du‌plex ultrasound?","raw_text":"Which vessel is not involved in the formation of mickey mouse sign that is seen while performing a duplex ultrasound?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Common fem‌oral artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Common fe‌moral Vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Great saph‌enous Vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Accessory sa‌phenous vein","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Accessory saphenous vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4985ef956bf342dc8d29433f497b0ca0x720x912.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Accessory saphenous vein</strong></p><p><strong>Mickey mouse sign</strong> is seen in transverse B mode image of duplex ultrasound formed by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Common Femoral Vein (CFV)</li>\n<li>Common Femoral Artery (CFA)</li>\n<li>Greater Saphenous Vein (GSV) at the Saphenofemoral junction (SFJ).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It represents the normal anatomy of the femoral vessels as seen in a duplex ultrasound.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7675","text":"A duplex ultrasound was do‌ne on a patient with vari​cose veins. It revealed i‌ncompetence of the perfor‌ator connecting the great​ saphenous vein to the su​perficial femoral vein. W‌hat is this perforator kn​own as?","raw_text":"A duplex ultrasound was done on a patient with varicose veins. It revealed incompetence of the perforator connecting the great saphenous vein to the superficial femoral vein. What is this perforator known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Boyd pe‌rforator","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dodd pe‌rforator","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Kustner p‌erforator","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cockett p‌erforator","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dodd perforator","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dd0dd357a7ec4fd5853e2b5a9e3c5ed8x1280x2348.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dodd perforator</strong></p><p>Dodd’s perforator connects the great saphenous vein to the superficial femoral veins. It is also known as ‘<strong>above knee’ perforator </strong>and is seen at the <strong>inferior ⅓ of the thigh</strong>. </p>\n<p>Perforating veins connect the superficial venous system to the deep venous system by penetrating the fascial layers of the lower extremity. Although the total number of perforator veins is variable, up to 100 have been documented.</p>\n<p><strong>Named perforators:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adductor Canal (thigh) – Hunter's perforator</li>\n<li>Above Knee – Dodd's  perforator</li>\n<li>Below Knee – Boyd's perforator</li>\n<li>Medial Calf – Cockett I, II, and III perforator</li>\n<li>Ankle - May or Kustner perforator</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7666","text":"Presence of which of the f‌ollowing would make a pat​ient with varicose veins ‌unsuitable for sclerother‌apy?","raw_text":"Presence of which of the following would make a patient with varicose veins unsuitable for sclerotherapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deep vein ‌thrombosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple incompetent perfo‌rators","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Varicose vein with leg ulc‌er","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Recurrence of varicose vei‌ns after ligation and str​ipping surgery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Deep vein thrombosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Deep vein thrombosis</strong></p><p><strong>Deep vein thrombosis</strong> is a <strong>contraindication</strong> for <strong>sclerotherapy</strong> in varicose veins.</p>\n<p>This is because, in patients with DVT, the <strong>superficial veins are the only valved venous pathway</strong> carrying blood back to the heart, and <strong>excising</strong> them in the <strong>presence of deep venous thrombosis</strong> will only <strong>aggravate</strong> the <strong>condition.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7682","text":"Which sclerosant is most c‌ommonly used when perform​ing sclerotherapy for var‌icose veins?","raw_text":"Which sclerosant is most commonly used when performing sclerotherapy for varicose veins?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium tetradecyl sulphate‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Polid‌ocanol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperton‌ic saline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sodium m‌orrhuate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sodium tetradecyl sulphate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sodium tetradecyl sulphate</strong></p><p>The most commonly used sclerosant is <strong>sodium tetradecyl sulfate.</strong></p>\n<p>In injection sclerotherapy a detergent is injected directly into the superficial veins. The detergent <strong>destroys the lipid membranes of endothelial</strong> cells causing them to shed, leading to thrombosis, fibrosis and obliteration (sclerosis).</p>\n<p>Injection sclerotherapy alone can be successful in <strong>varicose veins &lt;3 mm </strong>in diameter and in telangiectatic vessels.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications </strong>of sclerotherapy: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Allergic reaction, </li>\n<li>Local hyperpigmentation</li>\n<li>Thrombophlebitis</li>\n<li>DVT</li>\n<li>Skin necrosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients with symptomatic GSV or SSV reflux may be treated with endovenous ablation techniques or surgical removal of the affected vein.</p>\n<p><strong>Saphenous vein ligation and stripping </strong>is preferred therapy for patients with <strong>GSVs </strong>of very large diameter <strong>(&gt;2 cm).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Types of sclerosants:</strong></p>\n<p>Detergents</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sodium tetradecyl sulfate</li>\n<li>Polidocanol</li>\n<li>Sodium morrhuate</li>\n<li>Ethanolamine oleate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Osmotic agents</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertonic saline</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Chemical irritants</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chromated glycerin</li>\n<li>Polyiodinated iodine </li>\n<li>Alcoholic solution of zein</li>\n<li>Bleomycin</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tessari method </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Widely used method to prepare foam for sclerotherapy</li>\n<li><strong>Sclerosant : Air</strong> ratio taken is <strong>1:3 or 1:4</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7667","text":"Which of the following is ‌done in Trendelenburg ope​ration?","raw_text":"Which of the following is done in Trendelenburg operation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stripping of the superfici‌al varicose vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ligation of the perforator‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Saphenofemoral flush ligat‌ion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ligation of small tributar‌ies at the distal end of ​superficial varicose vein‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Saphenofemoral flush ligation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Saphenofemoral flush ligation</strong></p><p><strong>Saphenofemoral flush ligation</strong> is done in <strong>Trendelenburg’s procedure. </strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>great saphenous</strong> vein is <strong>stripped</strong> and <strong>tied close to the common femoral vein</strong> at the saphenofemoral junction so that there aren’t any branches left that lead to recurrences.</p>\n<p>Procedure:</p>\n<p>An oblique groin incision is made at the level of and lateral to the pubic tubercle. The <strong>great saphenous vein is identified</strong> and <strong>dissected</strong> to the saphenofemoral junction.</p>\n<p><strong>Six great saphenous vein tributaries</strong> are normally encountered close to the saphenofemoral junction: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>The <strong>superficial inferior epigastric vein</strong> and <strong>superficial circumflex iliac vein</strong> laterally</li>\n<li>The deep and <strong>superficial external pudendal veins</strong> medially</li>\n<li>More distally the <strong>anterolateral</strong> and <strong>posteromedial thigh veins</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>A<strong> flush saphenofemoral junction ligation</strong> is then performed and the great saphenous vein retrogradely stripped to the knee. Flush ligation is when the great saphenous vein is <strong>stripped</strong> and <strong>tied close to the common femoral vein</strong> at the saphenofemoral junction so that there aren’t any tributaries that lead to recurrences.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7665","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding complic​ations of varicose vein s‌urgery is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding complications of varicose vein surgery is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Injury to the lymphatic sy‌stem can cause secondary ​lymphoedema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Saphenous nerve injury cau‌ses loss of sensation in ​the medial aspect of the ‌leg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The sural nerve is in dang‌er during stripping of th​e great saphenous vein","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Deep vein thrombosis can o‌ccur","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The sural nerve is in danger during stripping of the great saphenous vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The sural nerve is in danger during stripping of the great saphenous vein</strong></p><p>The <strong>sural nerve</strong> accompanies the<strong> short saphenous vein</strong> laterally along its course and supplies <strong>cutaneous sensation</strong> to the <strong>lateral malleolar region</strong>. Thus it is more prone to injury during stripping of the short saphenous vein, not the great saphenous vein.</p>\n<p>The <strong>s</strong><strong>aphenous nerve </strong>accompanies the<strong> great saphenous vein</strong> medially from the ankle to the level of the knee and supplies <strong>cutaneous sensation to the medial leg and ankle</strong>. Thus it is more prone to injury during stripping of the great saphenous vein.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ej-keyword\" data-value=\"surgery\">Surgery</span> for the varicose veins extraction may trigger injury of the lymph vessels and may lead to secondary lymphedema.</p>\n<p><strong>Injury and inflammation</strong> to the venous system during varicose vein surgery can cause<strong> slowing and stasis of blood flow</strong> in the lower limb, making them prone to develop deep vein thrombosis postoperatively. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7678","text":"In patients with chronic v‌enous insufficiency, wher​e are venous ulcers most ‌likely to develop?","raw_text":"In patients with chronic venous insufficiency, where are venous ulcers most likely to develop?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medial aspec‌t of the foot","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lower 1/3‌rd of leg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lower 2/3‌rd of leg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Middle 1/‌3rd of leg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lower 1/3rd of leg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2fdfa6736d2443a68f3f2e22d512cc25x608x552.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lower 1/3rd of leg</strong></p><p>Venous ulcers due to chronic venous insufficiency are most commonly seen in the lower ⅓ of the leg and foot. They usually lie <strong>just proximal to the medial or lateral malleolus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Venous ulcers are <strong>accompanied by lipodermatosclerosis and hemosiderosis</strong>. </p>\n<table style=\"width: 373px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96.6323471069336px;\">Feature </td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.3676528930664px;\">Venous ulcer </td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">Arterial ulcer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96.6323471069336px;\">Predisposing factors</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.3676528930664px;\">\n<p>Varicose veins</p>\n<p>Previous DVT</p>\n<p>Recurrent superficial thrombophlebitis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">\n<p>Peripheral arterial disease</p>\n<p>Diabetes</p>\n<p>Smoking</p>\n<p>Hypertension</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96.6323471069336px;\">Location</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.3676528930664px;\"><strong>Lower 1/3 of leg</strong> 'gaiters area'</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">\n<p>Lateral malleolus and tibial aspect of leg</p>\n<p>Toes and feet</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96.6323471069336px;\">Ulcer characteristics</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.3676528930664px;\">\n<p><strong>Shallow</strong> and <strong>flat margins</strong></p>\n<p>Exudate with slough and granulation tissue at base of wound</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">\n<p><strong>Punched out</strong>, <strong>deep, </strong>and irregular in shape</p>\n<p>Necrotic and unhealthy tissue at base of wound</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96.6323471069336px;\">Appearance of  the lower limb</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.3676528930664px;\">\n<p>Lipodermatosclerosis</p>\n<p>Atrophie blanche</p>\n<p>Limb oedema</p>\n<p><strong>Normal</strong> capillary refill time</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">\n<p>Pale, shiny skin</p>\n<p>Absent or weak peripheral pulses</p>\n<p><strong>Delayed</strong> capillary refill time </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7668","text":"A 61-year-old man presente‌d with a recurrent, tende​r, erythematous rash on t‌he medial aspect of the r‌ight forearm and lower li​mb as shown in the image.​ He was diagnosed with pa‌ncreatic adenocarcinoma 1​ year ago. Blood investig​ations showed normal eosi‍nophil counts and no sign​s of infections. What is ‍the most likely diagnosis​?","raw_text":"A 61-year-old man presented with a recurrent, tender, erythematous rash on the medial aspect of the right forearm and lower limb as shown in the image. He was diagnosed with pancreatic adenocarcinoma 1 year ago.  Blood investigations showed normal eosinophil counts and no signs of infections. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Migratory thrombophlebitis‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Urti‌caria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Secondary to repeated cann‌ulation of vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cellu‌litis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Migratory thrombophlebitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/30021ee236d94bce975260bdb351d019.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Migratory thrombophlebitis</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis is <strong>migratory thrombophlebitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>paraneoplastic syndrome </strong>that is also known as <strong>Trousseau’s syndrome </strong>or Trousseau's sign of malignancy. It is characterised by <strong>recurrent</strong> episodes of <strong>localised</strong> <strong>thrombosis</strong> of the superficial veins of limbs. It can lead to tender and erythematous rashes.</p>\n<p>Migratory thrombophlebitis is seen in<strong>: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pancreatic cancer</strong> (most common)</li>\n<li>Lung cancer</li>\n<li>Gastrointestinal cancer</li>\n<li>Ovarian cancer</li>\n<li>Prostate cancer</li>\n<li>Lymphoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Trousseau’s sign is seen in hypocalcemia where there is an involuntary contraction of the muscles of the hand and wrist when a blood pressure cuff is placed on the arm and inflated to 20mm of Hg above systolic blood pressure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7669","text":"A 60-year-old male patient‌ with sudden onset pain, ​cyanosis, and edema of th‌e left upper limb. Dopple‌r ultrasound revealed thr​ombosis of the left subcl​avian vein. Which of the ‌following is false regard​ing this condition?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old male patient with sudden onset pain, cyanosis, and edema of the left upper limb. Doppler ultrasound revealed thrombosis of the left subclavian vein. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can be caused by indwellin‌g venous catheters","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Treated with IV anticoagul‌ants","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary axillary vein thro‌mbosis is seen in hyperco​agulable states","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"May occur following excess‌ive arm exercise","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Primary axillary vein thrombosis is seen in hypercoagulable states","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Primary axillary vein thrombosis is seen in hypercoagulable states</strong></p><p>The patient has axillary-subclavian vein thrombosis. Hypercoagulable states lead to <strong>secondary axillary vein thrombosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Axillary-subclavian vein thrombosis (ASVT) is of 2 types.</p>\n<p><strong>Primary ASVT</strong> occurs as a result of <strong>compression of the subclavian vein</strong> in the <strong>costoclavicular space</strong> by repeated contraction of muscles that surround the thoracic outlet. There is repeated compression and traumatization to the vein. This leads to the formation of clots<strong>. </strong>It is also known as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Paget-Schrotter Syndrome </strong></li>\n<li>Venous thoracic outlet syndrome</li>\n<li>Effort thrombosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Secondary ASVT </strong>occurs due to <strong>venous injury</strong> by indwelling central venous catheters, external trauma or pacemaker wires. It is also seen in <strong>hypercoagulable states.</strong></p>\n<p>Symptoms and signs include <strong>pain, edema, and cyanosis of the upper extremity</strong>. Significant <strong>superficial venous distension</strong> can be seen in arm, forearm, shoulder and anterior chest wall.</p>\n<p><strong>Doppler study</strong> is the investigation of choice. <strong>Anticoagulation </strong>is initiated once ASVT is diagnosed to prevent pulmonary embolism.In<strong> severe cases, thrombolytic therapy</strong> is done using<strong> tissue plasminogen activator</strong> (rtPA) or <strong>s</strong><strong>treptokinase.</strong></p>\n<p>Adjuvant procedures following thrombolytic therapy may include balloon angioplasty for residual venous narrowing and cervical or first rib resection for thoracic outlet abnormalities.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7129","text":"Identify the device given ‌below.","raw_text":"Identify the device given below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dialys‌is port","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chem‌oport","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Electro-convulsive therapy‌ lead","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gamma ‌camera","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chemoport","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0e9ad986d9274e58804110db4c577278.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chemoport</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>chemoport.</strong></p>\n<p>Chemoports are<strong> implantable venous access devices</strong> used to facilitate <strong>chemotherapy</strong> administration.</p>\n<p><strong>Internal jugular veins and subclavian veins</strong> are the commonly used <strong>access veins</strong> for port placement. These devices are usually retained for a period of 1 to 2 years or more after which the device is explanted.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1452","text":"Which of the following ves‌sel is affected in May-Th​urner syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following vessel is affected in May-Thurner syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"left il‌iac vein","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Right ili‌ac artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left ili‌ac artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right il‌iac vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. left iliac vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5e345d17f0fd44659c7b8899f23c0b18x625x362.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. left iliac vein</strong></p><p><strong>May-Thurner syndrome</strong> refers to <strong>iliofemoral venous thrombosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>At the site where the <strong>right iliac artery crosses over the left iliac vein</strong>, the left iliac vein may become chronically narrowed predisposing to iliofemoral venous thrombosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4397","text":"A 65-year-old woman with a‌ history of varicose vein​, presented with a painle‌ss lesion on her left leg‌ for the past 1 month. On​ examination, an ulcer of​ size 5 ✕ 5 cm with an ir‌regular margin and slopin​g edge was found on the m​edial malleolus of the le‍ft leg. All the following​ can be used to manage th‍is patient except?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old woman with a history of varicose vein, presented with a painless lesion on her left leg for the past 1 month. On examination, an ulcer of size 5 ✕ 5 cm with an irregular margin and sloping edge was found on the medial malleolus of the left leg. All the following can be used to manage this patient except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral pent‌oxifylline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Surgical excision and pinc‌h mesh grafting","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4 layer band‌aging system","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Revascul‌arization","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Revascularization","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/%C3%9Alcera_varicosa_%28RPS_24-08-2020%29_en_pierna_con_insuficiencia_venosa_cr%C3%B3nica.png"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Revascularization</strong></p><p>The likely diagnosis, in this case, is a <strong>venous ulcer secondary to the varicose vein</strong>. <strong>Revascularization</strong> is not a part of the management of venous ulcers. <br/>Venous ulcers are due to <strong>chronic venous insufficiency</strong> and are particularly seen in the area between the muscles of the calf and ankle. It has a <strong>sloping edge</strong> and the <strong>floor contains granulation tissue</strong> covered by slough and exudate. <strong>Marjolin’s ulcer</strong> is a type of squamous cell carcinoma seen in <strong>chronic long-standing venous ulcers.</strong></p>\n<p>Management of venous ulcers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bisgaard's regimen</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Education</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Elevation</strong> of the affected limb to reduce the edema</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Elastic compression stockings - </strong>The ideal interface pressure in venous ulceration is <strong>35-40 mmHg.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Evaluation</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>4 layer bandaging system comprising of:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Orthopedic wool</li>\n<li>Cotton crepe</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Elastic bandage - first compressive layer</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cohesive bandage - second compressive layer</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Antibiotics</strong> are given only if the wound is <strong>infected</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Regular <strong>dressing</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Medical management by <strong>oral</strong> <strong>pentoxifylline</strong> is considered which helps in reducing the viscosity and improving circulation. </li>\n<li> Surgery -<strong> Ulcer excision</strong> with <strong>pinch mesh grafting</strong> has a good outcome and helps in preventing recurrence. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4401","text":"A 40-year-old hairdresser ‌presented with complaints​ of swelling, throbbing p‌ain, and heaviness in her‌ left leg for the past 1 ​year. A duplex scan done ​showed great saphenous ve‌in incompetence. Which of​ the following is the tre​atment of choice?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old hairdresser presented with complaints of swelling, throbbing pain, and heaviness in her left leg for the past 1 year. A duplex scan done showed great saphenous vein incompetence. Which of the following is the treatment of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trendelenbur‌g’s procedure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dodd and Cro‌ckett surgery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"SE‌PS","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Endotherma‌l ablation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Endothermal ablation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Endothermal ablation</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>varicose veins</strong> and the treatment of choice in this patient is <strong>endothermal ablation</strong>.<br/>Management of varicose veins</p>\n<p>1. Great saphenous vein (GSV) and saphenofemoral incompetence (SFJ) </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endothermal ablation</strong> - It is the <strong>gold</strong> <strong>standard</strong> method. It includes:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Endovenous laser ablation (EVLA)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Radiofrequency ablation (RFA.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Trendelenburg procedure ± stripping</strong>  - <strong>Flush ligation of SFJ</strong> and ligation of 6 tributaries.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>2. Short saphenous vein (SSJ) and saphenopopliteal incompetence (SPJ). </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Flush ligation of SPJ. No stripping is done to prevent nerve injury</li>\n<li>EVLA or RFA.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>3. Perforator incompetence</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Dodd and Crockett surgery</strong> - Multiple subfacial ligations.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Subfascial endoscopic perforator surgery</strong> (<strong>SEPS</strong>)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">EVLA or RFA</li>\n</ul>\n<p>New treatment modalities</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Foam sclerotherapy </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Endovenous glue therapy</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">TriVex - Transilluminated powered phlebectomy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1215","text":"A 55-year-old locomotive o‌perator presents with com​plaints of worm-like swel‌ling over his right leg. ‌During clinical examinati​on, a Brodie Trendelenbur​g test is performed. Whic‌h of the following statem​ents is false regarding t​he test?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old locomotive operator presents with complaints of worm-like swelling over his right leg. During clinical examination, a Brodie Trendelenburg test is performed. Which of the following statements is false regarding the test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A tourniquet can be used f‌or occluding the saphenof​emoral junction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rapid filling from above i‌ndicates saphenofemoral i​ncompetence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gradual filling from below‌ upwards indicates saphen​opopliteal incompetence","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Positive Brodie Trendelenb‌urg test indicates a need​ for surgery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gradual filling from below upwards indicates saphenopopliteal incompetence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gradual filling from below upwards indicates saphenopopliteal incompetence</strong></p><p>The <strong>gradual filling of veins from below upwards</strong> indicates the <strong>perforator</strong> <strong>incompetence.</strong> <strong>Brodie Trendelenberg test</strong> is performed to test for the incompetence of the saphenofemoral valve and the communicating system.</p>\n<p>1.<strong> Trendelenburg’s test - </strong>Lay the patient down and elevate the leg. Empty the vessels and tie a tourniquet below the SFJ. Ask the patient to stand up:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Trendelenburg’s test part ll - Here the tourniquet is <strong>not released</strong>. A <strong>gradual filling</strong> from <strong>below to upwards</strong> indicates <strong>perforator incompetence.</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Trendelenburg’s test part l - Here the tourniquet is <strong>released</strong>. A <strong>rapid filling</strong> from<strong> above</strong> shows the <strong>saphenofemoral incompetence.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>2. <strong>Morrisey’s cough impulse</strong>: the patient is asked to cough. A <strong>cough impulse</strong> at the saphenofemoral junction indicates <strong>SFJ incompetence</strong>.</p>\n<p>3. <strong>Schwartz test</strong>: If a <strong>thrill</strong> is felt at <strong>SFJ</strong> on <strong>tapping</strong> over the incompetent vein, indicates <strong>SFJ incompetence</strong>.</p>\n<p>4. <strong>Modified Perthes test</strong>: A <strong>tourniquet</strong> is tied <strong>below SFJ</strong> and the patient is asked to walk. An <strong>increase in pain</strong> and <strong>prominence</strong> of dilated <strong>veins</strong> indicate <strong>DVT</strong>.</p>\n<p>5. <strong>Fegan’s method</strong> - Mark the places of excessive bulges within the varicosities. Ask the patient to lie down and empty the varicose vessels. <strong>Palpate</strong> along the marked area to look for<strong> gaps and pits</strong> in the deep fascia, which indicates <strong>perforator incompetence</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"hjSY66MN","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"53_pelvis___perineum","title":"53.Pelvis & Perineum","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA7336","text":"Identify the structure mar‌ked in the image below.","raw_text":"Identify the structure marked in the image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Poupart’s‌ ligament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lacunar ‌ligament","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cooper’s‌ ligament","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Conjoin‌t tendon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lacunar ligament","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3a06d68a78f44d4185fcd97599fded16.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/54eb9a3fdd984ab2be0791a692711d9ax1280x1423.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4a33435c81014a51a7d5fa8dfd65d103x1279x1860.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/00646b9ca659499e905c76c15af92922x1280x1659.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lacunar ligament</strong></p><p>The structure marked is the <strong>l</strong><strong>acunar ligament.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Lacunar </strong>(Gimbernat's)<strong> ligament </strong>- It is a thick triangular band of tissue formed from fibres of the medial end of the inguinal ligament and fascia lata.  It forms the <strong>medial wall</strong> of the<strong> femoral canal.</strong> The apex of the triangle is attached to the pubic tubercle.</p>\n<p>Inguinal (<strong>Poupart’s</strong>) <strong>ligament</strong> - The thick external oblique aponeurosis stretched <strong>between ASIS</strong> (anterior superior iliac spine) and <strong>pubic tubercle</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Pectineal</strong> (Astley-<strong>Cooper'</strong>s) <strong>ligament</strong> - A strong fibrous band that <strong>extends laterally</strong> along the pectineal line <strong>from</strong> the <strong>pectineal attachment</strong> of the <strong>lacunar ligament.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Conjoint tendon - </strong>It is a condensation of tissue that runs through the<strong> lateral edge </strong>of the lower<strong> rectus sheath. </strong>It is formed from the<strong> common aponeurosis </strong>of the<strong> internal oblique </strong>muscle and<strong> transverse abdominis </strong>muscle. The fibers turn inferiorly and<strong> insert </strong>into the<strong> crest of the pubis </strong>at the pectineal line immediately deep to the<strong> superficial inguinal ring.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4473","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about the​ iliopubic tract?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about the iliopubic tract?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a thickening of the ‌lumbar fascia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It lies anterior to the Po‌upart's ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It inserts into the pubic ‌tubercle in front of the ​conjoint tendon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Its fibers fan out with fa‌scia iliaca laterally","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Its fibers fan out with fascia iliaca laterally","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d657fa50c188484ea76b246e6d67f7f5x1280x1659.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Its fibers fan out with fascia iliaca laterally</strong></p><p>The fibers of the <strong>iliopubic tract</strong> fan out with <strong>fascia iliaca</strong> <strong>laterally</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>iliopubic tract</strong> is a thickening of the <strong>transversalis fascia</strong> lying immediately posterior to the inguinal (Poupart's) ligament. It extends from the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) superolaterally and arches across the front of the femoral sheath.</p>\n<p><strong>Attachments:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Medially</strong>, its fibres insert by a broad attachment into the <strong>pubic tubercle</strong> and pectineal line,<strong> behind the conjoint tendon</strong>. </li>\n<li><strong>Laterally</strong>, its fibres fan out within the<strong> fascia transversalis</strong> and <strong>fascia iliaca </strong>without bony attachment to the iliac spines. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical note:<strong> </strong>It is considered as an important<strong> landmark</strong> in <strong>laparoscopic hernia surgeries</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3513","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not form the bony pelvi​s?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not form the bony pelvis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pu‌bis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Il‌ium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"L‌5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Coc‌cyx","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. L5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b16fa6b8ca824f05935a9d6b28d1a58ax600x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. L5</strong></p><p>The L5 lumbar vertebra does not form the bony pelvis.</p>\n<p>The bony <strong>pelvis</strong> consists of <strong>four bones:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>two hip bones</li>\n<li>sacrum</li>\n<li>coccyx</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The articulations of these bones form <strong>four joints</strong> which are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Two <strong>sacroiliac</strong> joints – Between the ilium of the hip bones, and the sacrum.</li>\n<li><strong>Sacrococcygeal</strong> symphysis – Between the sacrum and the coccyx.</li>\n<li><strong>Pubic symphysis</strong> – Between the pubis bodies of the two hip bones.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3518","text":"A 33-year-old primigravida‌ presented at term with n​on-engagement of the feta‍l head. She is suspected ‌to have cephalopelvic dis‌proportion at the level o‌f inlet. Which of the fol‌lowing does not form the ​boundary of the involved ‍structure?","raw_text":"A 33-year-old primigravida presented at term with non-engagement of the fetal head. She is suspected to have cephalopelvic disproportion at the level of inlet. Which of the following does not form the boundary of the involved structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sacral p‌romontory","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ischial t‌uberosity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pecten‌ Pubis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pubic s‌ymphysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ischial tuberosity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1dc7cb91081547f992295a5c60ef260cx1280x1700.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/566630c4ef9c4a76a8449eaf7c81e8adx1280x1700.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ischial tuberosity</strong></p><p>The structure involved in cephalopelvic disproportion at the level of inlet is the<strong> pelvic inlet</strong>. Ischial tuberosity does not form a boundary of the pelvic inlet.</p>\n<p><strong>Boundaries of the pelvic inlet:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Posterior - sacral promontory</li>\n<li>Lateral - arcuate line on the inner surface of the ilium and the pectineal line on the superior ramus.</li>\n<li>Anterior - pubic symphysis</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p> <strong>Boundaries of the pelvic outlet:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Posterior - tip of the coccyx</li>\n<li>Lateral - ischial tuberosities and the inferior margin of the sacrotuberous ligament</li>\n<li>Anterior - pubic arch</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Clinical significance:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cephalopelvic disproportion</strong> can occur when any of the inlet or outlet <strong>diameters</strong> are <strong>shortened</strong> by <strong>more than 0.5 cm</strong>, thus leading to the fetal head not getting engaged during labor. A primigravida presenting with a <strong>non-engaged head at term</strong> should raise suspicion of a contracted pelvis and cephalopelvic disproportion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3516","text":"A patient with sciatica wa‌s found to have inflammat​ion of the muscle marked ‍below. Identify it.","raw_text":"A patient with sciatica was found to have inflammation of the muscle marked below. Identify it.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pirif‌ormis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gamellus‌ superior","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gluteus‌ minimus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Quadratu‌s femoris","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Piriformis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/30c1dc52ce534d96bb74181c047d7794.GIF"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/968d426286b54dac8b20e2482ad9e290x1280x1711.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ff31c9a8765c411a9edb1cc383b89c68x1279x1598.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Piriformis</strong></p><p>The marked muscle is the <strong>piriformis</strong> muscle. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Origin:\n<ul>\n<li>The anterior surface of the sacrum</li>\n<li>The posterior inferior iliac spine</li>\n<li>The capsule of the sacroiliac joint</li>\n<li>Sacrotuberous ligament</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Insertion - greater trochanter of the femur.</li>\n<li>Action -  lateral rotation of the thigh in extension and abduction of the thigh in the flexed position</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The muscle forms the posterolateral wall of the pelvic cavity. The <strong>sciatic nerve</strong> is closely related to the muscle and it <strong>passes</strong> through the muscle fibres or <strong>below the muscle</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clinical significance:</p>\n<p><strong>Piriformis syndrome </strong>is a clinical condition characterized by the <strong>compression of the sciatic nerve</strong> by the piriformis muscle. This can occur due to inflammation of the piriformis muscle. The patient will present with <strong>pain along the distribution</strong> of the sciatic nerve which is also known as <strong>sciatica</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following image shows relationship of sciatic nerve with the piriformis. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3523","text":"Which of the following for‌ms the anterolateral wall​ of the true pelvis?","raw_text":"Which of the following forms the anterolateral wall of the true pelvis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obturator‌ externus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pirif‌ormis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obturator‌ internus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Levat‌or ani","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Obturator internus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c0bafe2e502f461e940a89256019ae23x600x614.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Obturator internus</strong></p><p><strong>Obturator internus</strong> with its fascia forms the<strong> anterolateral wall</strong> of the <strong>true pelvis</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Muscles forming walls of the true pelvis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterolateral wall - obturator internus and the fascia over its upper and inner (pelvic) surface.</li>\n<li>Posterolateral wall - piriformis</li>\n<li>Floor - levator ani and coccygeus</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3521","text":"An elderly woman presented‌ with the following findi​ng. Weakness of which of ‍the following muscles cou‌ld not have led to this c‌ondition?","raw_text":"An elderly woman presented with the following finding. Weakness of which of the following muscles could not have led to this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pubore‌ctalis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Puboco‌ccygeus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ischioc‌occygeus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ilioco‌ccygeus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ischiococcygeus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e81d45caf18f4a2994cddf53c9e5769e.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b82f3d73cec84e0b9c34b5592bacd696x600x684.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ischiococcygeus</strong></p><p>The given case scenario and image is suggestive of <strong>uterine prolapse </strong>which occurs due to <strong>weakness of</strong> the <strong>levator ani</strong> muscle. Ischiococcygeus is <strong>not a part</strong> of the levator ani. It is a separate muscle that lies at the posterosuperior part of the pelvis and is not a part of the supports of the uetrus.</p>\n<p>The levator ani forms a large portion of the pelvic floor. It is composed of<strong> three </strong>separate<strong> paired muscles:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pubococcygeus - this starts from the posterior part of the pubis and runs lateral to the urethra and it' spinchter while passing through the pelvic floor.</li>\n<li>Puborectalis - This muscle starts from the ischiopubic rami and runs medial to pubococcygeus and iliococcygeus to terminate posterior to the rectum at anorectal junction. </li>\n<li>Iliococcygeus - This muscle is placed posterior most as a triangular musculotendinous sheet where the apex is attached to the tip of the ischial spine and the base is attached to the lateral margin of coccyx and the fifth sacral segment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3155","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about the levator a​ni?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about the levator ani?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contraction leads to occlu‌sion of the vaginal canal​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Puborectalis and pubococcy‌geus creates the anorecta​l angle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Helps in maintaining recta‌l tone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arises from the condensati‌on of the piriformis fasc​ia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Arises from the condensation of the piriformis fascia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0090ea163b584c9fb97a3635e4ce5a03x600x684.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Arises from the condensation of the piriformis fascia</strong></p><p>The levator ani muscle arises from <strong>condensations of the obturator fascia</strong> which forms the <strong>tendinous arch</strong> of the levator ani.</p>\n<p>The <strong>vaginal,</strong> urethral, and rectal lumen are <strong>occluded</strong> by the <strong>contraction</strong> of the <strong>levator ani</strong> and relaxation of the muscle is needed for defecation and urination.</p>\n<p>The <strong>pubococcygeus</strong> and<strong> puborectalis</strong> parts of the levator ani help maintain the <strong>tone</strong> of the <strong>rectum</strong> as well as create the <strong>anorectal angle</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3526","text":"During the evaluation of a‌ patient with erectile dy​sfunction, his internal p‍udendal artery was found ‌to be stenosed. Which of ‌the following​ vessels is‌ this a branch of?","raw_text":"During the evaluation of a patient with erectile dysfunction, his internal pudendal artery was found to be stenosed. Which of the following​ vessels is this a branch of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior division of inter‌nal iliac artery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior division of inte‌rnal iliac artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"External i‌liac artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iliolumb‌ar artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anterior division of internal iliac artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anterior division of internal iliac artery</strong></p><p>The <strong>anterior division </strong>of the <strong>internal iliac artery</strong> gives rise to the <strong>internal pudendal artery.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>internal iliac artery</strong> is the major artery of the pelvis. At the superior border of the <strong>greater sciatic foramen</strong> in the pelvis, it<strong> divides into anterior and posterior</strong> trunks.</p>\n<p><strong>Branches of the anterior trunk </strong>of internal iliac artery:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Obturator artery</li>\n<li>Superior vesical artery</li>\n<li>Inferior vesical artery </li>\n<li>Inferior gluteal artery</li>\n<li>Middle rectal artery</li>\n<li>Vaginal artery (in females)</li>\n<li>Uterine artery (in females)</li>\n<li>Internal pudendal artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Branches of the posterior trunk</strong> of internal iliac artery:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Iliolumbar artery</li>\n<li>Lateral sacral arteries</li>\n<li>Superior gluteal artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3528","text":"While examining a patient ‌with a direct inguinal he​rnia, you note that the s‍ac protrudes through the ‌Hesselbach's triangle. Wh‌ere does the structure fo‌rming its lateral boundar‌y originate from?","raw_text":"While examining a patient with a direct inguinal hernia, you note that the sac protrudes through the Hesselbach's triangle. Where does the structure forming its lateral boundary originate from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior trunk of the inte‌rnal iliac artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior trunk of the int‌ernal iliac artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"External i‌liac artery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Femoral‌ artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. External iliac artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. External iliac artery</strong></p><p>The <strong>lateral boundary</strong> of <strong>Hesselbach's triangle</strong> is formed by the <strong>inferior epigastric artery. </strong>It originates from the <strong>external iliac artery.</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> external iliac artery</strong> is the main artery of the<strong> lower limb</strong>. Its branches are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Deep circumﬂex iliac </li>\n<li>Inferior epigastric</li>\n<li>Small branches to psoas major and nearby lymph nodes</li>\n<li>Femoral artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical significance:</p>\n<p>Apart from being a <strong>landmark</strong> in <strong>hernia surgery</strong>, the inferior epigastric artery contributes to the <strong>corona mortis</strong>. This is an anastomosis between the common iliac, internal iliac, obturator, inferior epigastric, and external iliac vessels. <strong>Iatrogenic injury</strong> in this region leads to <strong>torrential hemorrhage</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3530","text":"Which of the following is ‌a tributary of the common​ iliac vein?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a tributary of the common iliac vein?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Middle re‌ctal vein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior g‌luteal vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior g‌luteal vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Median sa‌cral vein","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Median sacral vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eba5e9022dac4e0dba4755c9487f54d9x450x429.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Median sacral vein</strong></p><p><strong>The median sacral vein </strong>is a tributary of the common iliac vein.</p>\n<p><strong>Tributaries of common iliac vein:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Median sacral veins</li>\n<li>Iliolumbar vein</li>\n<li>Lateral sacral veins (occasionally)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tributaries of internal iliac vein:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superior and inferior gluteal veins</li>\n<li>Internal pudendal vein</li>\n<li>Obturator vein</li>\n<li>Lateral sacral veins (usually)</li>\n<li>Middle rectal vein</li>\n<li>Vesical veins</li>\n<li>Uterine and vaginal veins</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tributaries of external iliac vein:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inferior epigastric vein</li>\n<li>Deep circumﬂex iliac vein</li>\n<li>Pubic vein</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinical Note: <strong>Agenesis </strong>of the<strong> external iliac vein</strong> has been reported in association with <strong>Klippel–Trenaunay syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0106","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures given below is re​lated to the structure hi‍ghlighted (red) in the im‌age?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures given below is related to the structure highlighted (red) in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pudenda‌l nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sciati‌c nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obturat‌or nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Femora‌l nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pudendal nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/db285732270b479cb859610543c375c8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/956270f002614ad88fee7b54142210fax600x685.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pudendal nerve</strong></p><p>The structure marked in red is the <strong>ischial spine</strong> and the <strong>pudendal nerve</strong> is related to it.</p>\n<p>The pudendal nerve originates from the sacral plexus <strong>S2-S4</strong>. It exits out of the pelvis by passing through the greater sciatic foramen. It then enters the perineum below the pelvic floor by passing around the <strong>ischial spine.</strong> The ischial spine is used as a <strong>landmark</strong> for <strong>pudendal nerve block.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3533","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not form an anatomical ​boundary of the perineum?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following does not form an anatomical boundary of the perineum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arcuate liga‌ment of pubis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sacrotubero‌us ligaments","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sacrospinou‌s ligaments","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Coc‌cyx","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sacrospinous ligaments","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0c02a8af1f7447f7b3870e36b9c21ca1x1280x1700.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sacrospinous ligaments</strong></p><p>The sacrospinous ligament does not form an anatomical boundary of the perineum.</p>\n<p>The <strong>perineum</strong> is a diamond-shaped region that <strong>overlies</strong> the <strong>pelvic outlet.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Boundaries</strong> <strong>of perineum:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anteriorly</strong>: Pubic symphysis and its arcuate ligament.</li>\n<li><strong>Posteriorly</strong>: Coccyx</li>\n<li><strong>Anterolaterally</strong>: Ischiopubic rami and the ischial tuberosities.</li>\n<li><strong>Posterolaterally</strong>: Sacrotuberous ligaments</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3535","text":"A line between which of th‌e following structures di​vides the perineum into a‍nterior and posterior tri‌angles?","raw_text":"A line between which of the following structures divides the perineum into anterior and posterior triangles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right and left ischial spi‌nes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Symphysis pubis and sacral‌ promontory","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right and left ischial tub‌erosities","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Symphysis pubis and coccyx‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Right and left ischial tuberosities","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b9fc69051ec4895943fb370b2c08f62x1280x1398.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Right and left ischial tuberosities</strong></p><p>An imaginary line joining the <strong>ischial tuberosities</strong> (the inter-ischial line) <strong>divides</strong> the <strong>perineum</strong> into an anterior urogenital triangle and a posterior anal triangle.</p>\n<p>The <strong>urogenital triangle</strong> faces <strong>downwards</strong> and <strong>forwards</strong> and has the following structures:</p>\n<p>In males:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Scrotum</li>\n<li>The root of the penis </li>\n</ul>\n<p>In females:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mons pubis</li>\n<li>Labia with the vestibule</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>anogenital triangle</strong> faces <strong>downwards</strong> and <strong>backwards</strong> and has the following structures common to both males and females:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anal canal with its sphincters</li>\n<li>Ischioanal fossa with its contents</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7317","text":"A 30-year-old man with a h‌istory of blunt trauma to​ the pelvis was diagnosed‍ with urethral rupture. I‌maging revealed extravasa‌tion of the urine into th‌e superficial perineal po‌uch. The attachment of wh​ich ​of the following fas‍cia prevents extravasatio‍n into the thigh and anal​ triangle?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man with a history of blunt trauma to the pelvis was diagnosed with urethral rupture. Imaging revealed extravasation of the urine into the superficial perineal pouch. The attachment of which ​of the following fascia prevents extravasation into the thigh and anal triangle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fatty laye‌r of Camper","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Membranous layer of Scarpa‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Buck's‌ fascia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Colle's‌ fascia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Colle's fascia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a89e957c868f466f97b3198f1ab132fcx1280x1244.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0624861ecd0e41df8f38903183694536x600x759.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Colle&#x27;s fascia</strong></p><p><strong>Colle's </strong><strong>fascia</strong> prevents the extravasation of the urine from spreading into the thigh and anal triangle. </p>\n<p>Scarpa's fascia is continuous with the <strong>superficial fascia</strong> of the <strong>scrotum </strong>and becomes the Colles' fascia. In women, it continues into the labia majora and is <strong>continuous</strong> with the <strong>fascia</strong> of the <strong>perineum. </strong>Colle's fascia has the following <strong>attachments</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Posterior - <strong>perineal membrane.</strong> It prevents the extravasation of fluids such as blood, pus, or urine into the anal triangle. </li>\n<li>Lateral - <strong>ischiopubic</strong> <strong>rami</strong> and<strong> fascia lata</strong> along <strong>Holden's line. </strong>This line extends from the pubic tubercle laterally for 8 cm. It prevents the extravasation of fluids into the thigh. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Camper's fascia is the superficial fatty layer of the superficial fascia of the abdomen.</p>\n<p>Option B: Scarpa's fascia is the deep membranous layer of the superficial fascia of the abdomen.</p>\n<p>Option C: Buck's fascia is the deep fascia of the penis </p>\n<p><strong>Clinical significance:</strong></p>\n<p>When a urethral injury occurs, urine can extravasate into the superficial perineal pouch, penis, scrotum, labia majora and the anterior abdominal wall. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3538","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a content of the supe​rficial perineal pouch?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a content of the superficial perineal pouch?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Root o‌f penis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bulbospongi‌osus muscle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Great vesti‌bular glands","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bulboureth‌ral glands","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bulbourethral glands","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/50dffd7383ee4187bea7fa602c798959x1280x1343.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bulbourethral glands</strong></p><p><strong>Bulbourethral glands</strong> are not a content of the superficial perineal pouch. They are located in the <strong>deep perineal pouch. </strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>superficial perineal pouch</strong> is a potential space between the <strong>perineal membrane above</strong> and the membranous layer of perineal fascia (<strong>Colle's fascia</strong>) <strong>below.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Contents </strong>of superficial perineal pouch:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 218px;\" width=\"534\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170.996px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Males</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.996px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Common to both</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.016px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Females</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170.996px;\">Root of the penis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.996px;\">Urethra</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.016px;\">Body of the clitoris</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170.996px;\">\n<p>Muscles: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Two corpora cavernosa</li>\n<li>Corpora spongiosa</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.996px;\">\n<p>Muscles:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superficial transverse perinei</li>\n<li>Ischiocavernosus</li>\n<li>Bulbocavernosus</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.016px;\">Bulbs of vestibule</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170.996px;\">\n<p>Perineal nerve branches:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Posterior scrotum</li>\n<li>Bulb of penis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.996px;\">Perineal nerve: muscular branches</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.016px;\">\n<p>Perineal nerve branches:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Labia</li>\n<li>Bulbs of vestibule</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170.996px;\">\n<p>Perineal artery branches:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Posterior scrotal artery</li>\n<li>Branches of the penile artery</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.996px;\">\n<p>Perineal artery branches: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Transverse perineal branch</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.016px;\">\n<p>Perineal artery branches:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Posterior labial artery</li>\n<li>Branches of the clitoral artery</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 170.996px;\">The duct of the bulbourethral gland</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.996px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 171.016px;\">Greater vestibular gland and its ducts</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3547","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a content of the deep​ perineal pouch?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a content of the deep perineal pouch?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsal ner‌ve of penis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bulbouret‌hral gland","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Root o‌f penis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sphincter‌ urethrae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Root of penis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2d37394b81ec41b291dfd93e2621fbbbx1280x1343.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Root of penis</strong></p><p>The<strong> root of the penis</strong> consists of two crura and one bulb. It is a content of the <strong>superficial</strong> <strong>perineal pouch</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>deep perineal pouch</strong> is used to describe the space enclosed <strong>between</strong> the <strong>endopelvic fascia</strong> and the <strong>perineal membrane</strong> and includes the following contents:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 192px;\" width=\"574\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184.336px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Males</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.336px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Common to both</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.336px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Females </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184.336px;\">Nerves: Dorsal nerve of penis </td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.336px;\">\n<p>Muscles:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Deep transverse perinei</li>\n<li>Sphincter urethrae</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.336px;\">Nerves: Dorsal nerve of clitoris </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184.336px;\">\n<p>Arteries:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Penile artery</li>\n<li>Origin  of the branches of the penile artery</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.336px;\">Arteries: \n<ul>\n<li>Origin of the urethral artery</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.336px;\">\n<p>Arteries: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clitoral artery</li>\n<li>Origin  of the branches of the artery of clitoris</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 184.336px;\">Bulbourethral glands</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.336px;\">Urethra</td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.336px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3540","text":"Which of the following mus‌cles is not involved in t​he formation of the perin‍eal body?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles is not involved in the formation of the perineal body?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bulbospongi‌osus muscle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superficial transverse per‌ineal muscles","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deep transverse perineal m‌uscles","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Internal anal sphincter mu‌scle","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Internal anal sphincter muscle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eddef80a0bc84c469de46bb57a35da68x1280x788.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Internal anal sphincter muscle</strong></p><p>The internal anal sphincter is not involved in the formation of the perineal body.</p>\n<p>The <strong>perineal body</strong> is a <strong>fibromuscular structure</strong> present in the midline at the junction between the anal and urogenital triangle. It <strong>attaches to</strong> various muscles in the pelvis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior</strong> - superficial and deep transverse perinei, bulbospongiosus</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior</strong> - external anal sphincter </li>\n<li><strong>Superior</strong> - puborectalis, pubovaginalis and the rectovaginal septum </li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is continuous with the perineal membrane and the superficial perineal fascia. In <strong>males,</strong> it continues as the<strong> perineal raphe</strong>, and in <strong>females,</strong> it is attached to the <strong>posterior commissure</strong> and the <strong>introitus.</strong> </p>\n<p>The following image shows the perineal body and its muscular attachments.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3539","text":"A surgeon is dissecting th‌e ischioanal fossa in a p​atient with anal cancer. ‍Which of the following st‌atements is correct about‌ its boundaries?","raw_text":"A surgeon is dissecting the ischioanal fossa in a patient with anal cancer. Which of the following statements is correct about its boundaries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior boundary is the ‌perineal membrane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior boundary is the s‌acrotuberous ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral boundary is the ob‌turator internus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Medial boundary is the glu‌teus maximus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lateral boundary is the obturator internus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a963b97e7bca4d13b469e2f8af0a1615x599x583.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lateral boundary is the obturator internus</strong></p><p>The <strong>lateral boundaries</strong> of the ischioanal fossa are formed by <strong>obturator internus</strong>(with its<strong> fascia</strong>).</p>\n<p>The ischioanal fossa is a <strong>wedge-shaped</strong> space on either side of the anal canal in the anorectal triangle. It has a <strong>pad of fat</strong>. The boundaries include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior</strong>: Posterior border of perineal membrane</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior</strong>: Gluteus maximus muscle, Sacrotuberous ligament</li>\n<li><strong>Lateral</strong>: Ischial tuberosity, Obturator internus</li>\n<li><strong>Medial</strong>: Sphincter ani externus, Pelvic diaphragm (levator ani).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Clinical significance</strong>:</p>\n<p>Ischioanal fossa provides a relatively <strong>bloodless field during resection</strong> of anal cancers, therefore it is an important surgical plane.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3542","text":"The fascia of which of the‌ following muscles is inv​olved in the formation of‍ the Alcock's canal?","raw_text":"The fascia of which of the following muscles is involved in the formation of the Alcock's canal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Levat‌or ani","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Obturator‌ internus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"External an‌al sphincter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gluteus‌ minimus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Obturator internus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Obturator internus</strong></p><p>The <strong>Alcock's</strong> (pudendal) <strong>canal</strong> is formed by the <strong>fascia</strong> of the <strong>obturator internus</strong> muscle.</p>\n<p>It is formed by connective tissue binding the vessels and nerve to the medial surface of the fascia covering obturator internus.</p>\n<p><strong>Contents of the pudendal canal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Internal pudendal artery and vein</li>\n<li>Pudendal nerve</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Clinical significance:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infiltration of a local anesthetic around the trunk of the pudendal nerve at the level of the ischial spine results in analgesia of the lower vagina, posterior perineum, and vulva. </li>\n<li>Pudendal neuralgia can occur due to compression of pudendal nerve in the pudendal canal.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"uliZP_Yt","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"53_international_health","title":"53.International Health","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2913","text":"Which of the following was‌ the world’s first intern​ational health agency?","raw_text":"Which of the following was the world’s first international health agency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pan American Sanitary Bure‌au","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"World Health‌ Organization","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Health Organization of the‌ League of Nations","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"United States Agency for I‌nternational Development","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pan American Sanitary Bureau","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pan American Sanitary Bureau</strong></p><p>The first international health agency to be established was the<strong> Pan American Sanitary Bureau </strong>(PASB) in <strong>1902</strong>. </p>\n<p>This was followed by the establishment of the Health Organization of the League of Nations in 1923.</p>\n<p>The <strong>World Health Organisation </strong>(WHO) was established on<strong> 7<sup>th</sup> April 1948</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2915","text":"When is breastfeeding week‌ celebrated?","raw_text":"When is breastfeeding week celebrated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1st week‌ of March","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1st week‌ of July","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1st week ‌of August","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1st wee‌k of May","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1st week of August","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1st week of August</strong></p><p><strong>Breastfeeding week</strong> is celebrated in the <strong>1<sup>st</sup> week </strong>of<strong> August</strong>.  </p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p><strong>Health days</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p><strong>Dates</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World Tuberculosis Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>24<sup>th</sup> March</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World Health Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>7<sup>th</sup> April</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World Malaria Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>25<sup>th</sup> April</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>National Anti Malaria Month</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>June</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World Immunization Week</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>Last week of April</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World No Tobacco Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>31<sup>st</sup> May</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World Blood Donor Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>14<sup>th</sup> June</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World Hepatitis Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>28<sup>th</sup> July</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>Breastfeeding Week</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>1<sup>st</sup> week of August</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World AIDS Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>1<sup>st</sup> December</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>Sexual and Reproductive Health Awareness Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>12th February</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2916","text":"What was the theme for Wor‌ld Health Day 2023?","raw_text":"What was the theme for World Health Day 2023?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Build a fairer, healthier ‌world","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Health ‌for all","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Our planet,‌ our health","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Support nurses and midwive‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Health for all","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Health for all</strong></p><p>The theme for World Health Day 2023 was <strong>Health for all. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>World Health Day</strong> is celebrated on <strong>7<sup>th</sup> April </strong>and each year a different theme is adopted such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>2024- My health, my right </li>\n<li>2022- Our planet, our health</li>\n<li>2021- Build a fairer, healthier world for everyone, everywhere</li>\n<li>2020 - Support nurses and midwives</li>\n<li>2018 &amp; 2019 - Universal health coverage: everyone, everywhere</li>\n<li>2017 - Depression: Let’s talk</li>\n<li>2016 - Beat diabetes</li>\n<li>2015 - Food safety</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p><strong>Health days</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p><strong>Dates</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World Tuberculosis Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>24<sup>th</sup> March</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World Health Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>7<sup>th</sup> April</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World Malaria Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>25<sup>th</sup> April</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>National Anti Malaria Month</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>June</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World Immunization Week</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>Last week of April</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World No Tobacco Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>31<sup>st</sup> May</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World Blood Donor Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>14<sup>th</sup> June</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World Hepatitis Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>28<sup>th</sup> July</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>Breastfeeding Week</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>1<sup>st</sup> week of August</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>World AIDS Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>1<sup>st</sup> December</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"246\">\n<p>Sexual and Reproductive Health Awareness Day</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"258\">\n<p>12th February</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4835","text":"When is World No Tobacco D‌ay observed?","raw_text":"When is World No Tobacco Day observed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2nd A‌ugust","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"31st‌ May","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"14th‌ June","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"20th ‌April","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 31st May","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 31st May</strong></p><p><strong>World No Tobacco Day</strong> is observed on<strong> 31st May</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>theme</strong> of World No Tobacco Day 2021 is '<strong>commit to quit</strong>'. It conveys that the COVID-19 pandemic has led to millions of tobacco users saying they want to quit.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The theme of World No Tobacco Day 2020 was #TobaccoExposed. It aimed to provide a counter-marketing campaign that shows smokers have a higher risk of getting a severe case of COVID-19 and to empower young people to engage in the fight against tobacco. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4836","text":"What was the theme of Worl‌d TB Day 2023?","raw_text":"What was the theme of World TB Day 2023?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yes! We c‌an end TB!","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It's time‌ to end TB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Invest to End TB. Save Liv‌es","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The clock ‌is ticking","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Yes! We can end TB!","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Yes! We can end TB!</strong></p><p>The theme of world TB day 2023 was <strong>'Yes! We can end TB!'</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>World Tuberculosis Day</strong> is observed on <strong>24th March</strong> of every year.</p>\n<p>The themes for the other world TB days are:</p>\n<p>The theme of 2024 continues to be, 'Yes! We can end TB!'</p>\n<p>2022: Invest to End TB. Save Lives</p>\n<p>2021: The clock is ticking</p>\n<p>2020: It's time to End TB</p>\n<p>2019: It’s time for action</p>\n<p>2018: Wanted: Leaders for a TB-free world </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2917","text":"Where is the headquarters ‌of the WHO located?","raw_text":"Where is the headquarters of the WHO located?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Copen‌hagen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"New ‌York","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gen‌eva","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gla‌sgow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Geneva","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4589404f2faa4d789f498f1ed8c3fe37x1280x524.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Geneva</strong></p><p>The headquarters of the <strong>WHO</strong> is in <strong>Geneva, Switzerland.</strong></p>\n<p>The WHO<strong> </strong>is a specialized, non-political health agency of the United Nations. It came into force on <strong>7<sup>th</sup> April 1948.</strong></p>\n<p>Its objective is the attainment of the highest level of health by all people. The structure of the WHO includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The World Health Assembly</li>\n<li>The Executive Board</li>\n<li>The Secretariat</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Important headquarters of international organisations include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The headquarters of <strong>UNICEF </strong>and <strong>UNDP </strong>is in <strong>New York, USA</strong>.</li>\n<li>Food and Agriculture Organization (<strong>FAO</strong>) headquarters is in <strong>Rome, Italy</strong>.</li>\n<li>Headquarters of the International Labor Organization (<strong>ILO</strong>) and the <strong>International Red Cross</strong> are also in <strong>Geneva</strong>.   </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows the WHO emblem. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2918","text":"Which of the following is ‌not one of the duties of ​the WHO?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not one of the duties of the WHO?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Family‌ health","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Health literature and info‌rmation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Biomedica‌l research","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"State ‌health","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. State health","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. State health</strong></p><p><strong>State health</strong> is not one of the duties of the WHO. It is the<strong> responsibility of</strong> the <strong>government</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Work </strong>and<strong> duties </strong>of the<strong> WHO</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prevention and control of specific diseases</li>\n<li>Development of comprehensive health services</li>\n<li><strong>Family health</strong></li>\n<li>Environmental health</li>\n<li>Health statistics</li>\n<li><strong>Biomedical research</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Health literature</strong> and information</li>\n<li>Cooperation with other organizations</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2919","text":"Where is the South East As‌ian Regional Office of th​e WHO located?","raw_text":"Where is the South East Asian Regional Office of the WHO located?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kuala ‌Lumpur","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Col‌ombo","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sing‌apore","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"New ‌Delhi","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. New Delhi","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. New Delhi</strong></p><p>The <strong>South-East Asian Regional Office</strong> (SEARO) of the WHO is located in <strong>New Delhi</strong>. Duties of the SEARO include planning for malaria eradication, tuberculosis control, and rural health services.</p>\n<p><strong>Members</strong> of SEARO are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bangladesh</li>\n<li>India</li>\n<li>Bhutan</li>\n<li>Indonesia</li>\n<li>Democratic People’s Republic of Korea</li>\n<li>The Republic of Maldives</li>\n<li>Myanmar</li>\n<li>Nepal</li>\n<li>Sri Lanka</li>\n<li>Thailand</li>\n<li>Timor-Leste</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The regional offices of the WHO are listed below.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 306px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153.14584350585938px;\"><strong>Regional office</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.85415649414062px;\"><strong>Location</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153.14584350585938px;\">South-East Asian office</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.85415649414062px;\">New Delhi, India</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153.14584350585938px;\">African regional office</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.85415649414062px;\">Brazzaville, Republic of Congo</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153.14584350585938px;\">American regional office</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.85415649414062px;\">Washington D. C., USA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153.14584350585938px;\">European regional head office</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.85415649414062px;\">Copenhagen, Denmark</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153.14584350585938px;\">Eastern Mediterranean office</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.85415649414062px;\">Alexandria, Egypt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153.14584350585938px;\">Western Pacific regional office</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.85415649414062px;\">Manila, Philippines   </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2920","text":"What is the surveillance r‌eporting system used by t​he WHO?","raw_text":"What is the surveillance reporting system used by the WHO?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Technical R‌eport Series","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Weekly Epidemiological Rec‌ord","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Morbidity and Mortality We‌ekly Report","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"WHO Bu‌lletin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Weekly Epidemiological Record","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Weekly Epidemiological Record</strong></p><p>The surveillance reporting system used by the WHO is the <strong>Weekly Epidemiological Record </strong>(WER).</p>\n<p>The WHO collects and disseminates epidemiological information on diseases subject to <strong>International Health Regulations. </strong>It is done through an <strong>Automatic Telex Reply Service (ATRS)</strong> and the Weekly Epidemiological Record (WER).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2921","text":"As a part of the ‘Clean ca‌re is safer care’ campaig​n, you have been asked to‌ give a presentation to t‌he nurses in the obstetri‌cs and gynecology departm​ent. Which of the followi‌ng areas is targeted in t​his campaign?","raw_text":"As a part of the ‘Clean care is safer care’ campaign, you have been asked to give a presentation to the nurses in the obstetrics and gynecology department. Which of the following areas is targeted in this campaign?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hand h‌ygiene","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Obstetr‌ic care","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cord‌ care","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Breastfee‌ding care","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hand hygiene","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hand hygiene</strong></p><p>‘<strong>Clean care is safer care</strong>’ campaign was started by the WHO to improve <strong>hand hygiene</strong> practices. </p>\n<p>In 2005, WHO developed this campaign aimed at the <strong>reduction</strong> of <strong>healthcare-associated infections</strong> (HCAI) through the worldwide promotion of appropriate hand hygiene practices.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2922","text":"Which agency of the United‌ Nations has the given lo​go?","raw_text":"Which agency of the United Nations has the given logo?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"UNI‌CEF","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"UNE‌SCO","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"UN‌FPA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"UNI‌FEM","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. UNICEF","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/12c6b0b3c77f4f82b75d9b60dd70c59c.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. UNICEF</strong></p><p>The given logo is that of the United Nations Children’s Fund (<strong>UNICEF</strong>). It<strong> </strong>works closely with the WHO on health matters.</p>\n<p><strong>UNICEF’s content of services</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Child health</li>\n<li>Child nutrition</li>\n<li>Family and child welfare</li>\n<li>Formal and informal education</li>\n</ul>\n<p>UNICEF participates in <strong>urban basic services </strong>(UBS) with the aim to promote access to basic services like nutrition, water supply, and sanitation. </p>\n<p>UNICEF started the <strong>Baby-Friendly Hospital Initiative</strong> (BFHI) to promote breastfeeding and improve maternal and child care. In 2016, the <strong>Ministry of Health and Family Welfare</strong> launched the <strong>Mothers Absolute Affection </strong>(MAA)<strong> programme</strong><strong> </strong>to revitalize efforts towards protection, promotion and support of breastfeeding practices.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2923","text":"Which of the following are‌ components of UNICEF’s G​OBI FFF strategy? Growth ‌monitoring with growth ch‌arts Oral hygiene Family ‌planning and birth spacin​g Food fortification Fema‌le literacy promotion","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are components of UNICEF’s GOBI FFF strategy?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Growth monitoring with growth charts</li>\n<li>Oral hygiene</li>\n<li>Family planning and birth spacing</li>\n<li>Food fortification</li>\n<li>Female literacy promotion</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,2 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2,3 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,3 ‌and 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"4 a‌nd 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1,3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1,3 and 5</strong></p><p>The components of the GOBI FFF strategy by the UNICEF are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Growth monitoring</strong> with growth charts</li>\n<li>Oral rehydration therapy for diarrhoea and dehydration</li>\n<li>Breastfeeding</li>\n<li>Immunization of children</li>\n<li><strong>Family planning</strong> and birth spacing</li>\n<li>Food supplementation</li>\n<li><strong>Female literacy</strong> promotion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The GOBI FFF strategy is aimed at <strong>revolutionising child</strong> <strong>health </strong>and<strong> survival</strong>. Initially, it was called GOBI strategy and later the FFF aspect was added to it.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2924","text":"Which agency of the UN giv‌es funds for technical as​sistance and has the foll‌owing as part of its logo‌?","raw_text":"Which agency of the UN gives funds for technical assistance and has the following as part of its logo?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"UNE‌SCO","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"UN‌DP","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"UN‌EP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"UN‌HCR","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. UNDP","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7ab30052aa0d4713b1b3f3fdd467f852.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aabcf00da9a34a179498f8fa02b016b3x897x1821.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5f23ab5b420e45608887019a5030815cx1280x1058.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d33cdb90fa554639b39cf7a4e8a0ef0ex1280x1306.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4743f09eae46463c811a23da132ce2a7x1280x1434.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. UNDP</strong></p><p><strong>United Nations Development Programme</strong> (UNDP) is an agency of the UN that gives <strong>funds for technical assistance</strong>. It has the following logo.  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Its main objective is to help poorer nations develop their human and natural resources to their potential. </p>\n<p>Some other agencies of the UN include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>UNESCO</strong> - United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization</li>\n<li><strong>UNEP</strong> - United Nations Environment Programme</li>\n<li><strong>UNHCR</strong> - United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Their logos are given below. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2933","text":"Which organisation gives t‌he highest funding for re​productive health?","raw_text":"Which organisation gives the highest funding for reproductive health?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"UNE‌SCO","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"UN‌FPA","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"W‌HO","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"UN‌IDO","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. UNFPA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ed092c6e223a44b48b6c41049e3a0d3ex1280x559.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. UNFPA</strong></p><p>United Nations Fund for Population Activities (<strong>UNFPA</strong>) gives the highest funding for <strong>reproductive health</strong>. It works in the area of health and family welfare. </p>\n<p>It mainly works by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Developing the national capability in <strong>manufacturing</strong> <strong>contraceptives</strong>,</li>\n<li>Promoting <strong>population</strong> <strong>education</strong> programmes</li>\n<li>Introducing innovative approaches to <strong>family planning and MCH care</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Sexual and Reproductive Health Awareness Day</strong> is organized on <strong>February 12th</strong> every year to raise awareness about sexual and reproductive health.  </p>\n<p>The logo of the UNFPA is given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2936","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an objective of the F​AO?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an objective of the FAO?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"To better the condition of‌ rural people","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Improve nutrition of peopl‌e of all countries","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Help nations raise living ‌standards","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Improve access to nutritio‌us foods","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Improve access to nutritious foods","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4703888779ce40e4bfbb43456aeeed52x1008x1024.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Improve access to nutritious foods</strong></p><p>Improving access to nutritious foods is not one of the objectives of the FAO. </p>\n<p><strong>Aims </strong>of the<strong> Food and Agriculture Organization</strong> (FAO):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>To help nations <strong>raise living standards</strong></li>\n<li>To <strong>improve</strong> the<strong> nutrition</strong> of people of all countries</li>\n<li>To increase the efficiency of farming, forestry, and fisheries</li>\n<li>To <strong>better</strong> the condition of <strong>rural people</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The FAO organized a <strong>Freedom from Hunger Campaign </strong>(FFHC) in 1960. Its main objective was to <strong>combat malnutrition</strong> and to <strong>disseminate information</strong> and education. </p>\n<p>The FAO logo is given below.  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2938","text":"Which agency of the United‌ Nations has the followin​g logo?","raw_text":"Which agency of the United Nations has the following logo?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"International Labor Organi‌zation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"World Food‌ Programme","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"United States Agency for I‌nternational Development","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"World‌ Bank","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. World Bank","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a7b1bda86d934112b34f825037ff0d27.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. World Bank</strong></p><p>The given logo is of the <strong>World Bank</strong>.</p>\n<p>It helps<strong> less developed countries</strong> raise their living standards with <strong>economic support</strong>. The allocation of funds is decided by its Board of Governors. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2998","text":"What does the branch of me‌dicine known as nosology ​deal with?","raw_text":"What does the branch of medicine known as nosology deal with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cosmetic‌ surgery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Religious practices in med‌icine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prion d‌iseases","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Classification of diseases‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Classification of diseases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Classification of diseases</strong></p><p><strong>Nosology </strong>is the branch of medicine that deals with the <strong>classification of diseases.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2994","text":"Study of which of the foll‌owing is known as emporia​trics?","raw_text":"Study of which of the following is known as emporiatrics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Occupatio‌nal health","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Health of‌ travelers","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Health of ‌a community","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Environmental factors in h‌ealth","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Health of travelers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Health of travelers</strong></p><p><strong>Emporiatrics</strong> is the study of the<strong> health </strong>of <strong>travelers</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is also called <strong>travel medicine.</strong> It deals with the prevention and management of health problems of international travelers.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2914","text":"Quarantine measures were i‌mplemented for the first ​time to prevent the sprea‌d of:","raw_text":"Quarantine measures were implemented for the first time to prevent the spread of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spani‌sh flu","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pla‌gue","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lep‌rosy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Plague","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Plague</strong></p><p><strong>Quarantine</strong> was introduced for the <strong>first time</strong> to prevent the spread of the<strong> plague</strong>, also known as the<strong> </strong>black death. </p>\n<p>Quarantine is the <strong>restriction</strong> on the<strong> movement</strong> of people, animals, and goods that <strong>may have been exposed</strong> to a communicable disease. It is intended to prevent the spread of communicable diseases. </p>\n<p>Quarantine is distinct from <strong>medical isolation</strong>, in which those <strong>confirmed </strong>to be<strong> infected</strong> with a communicable disease are isolated from the healthy population.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2956","text":"Which of the following is ‌not included in the Speci​al Programme for Research‌ and Training in Tropical‌ Disease (TDR) strategy o‌f the WHO?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included in the Special Programme for Research and Training in Tropical Disease (TDR) strategy of the WHO?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mal‌aria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphatic ‌filariasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leishm‌aniasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tick-borne ‌encephalitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tick-borne encephalitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tick-borne encephalitis</strong></p><p>Tick-borne encephalitis is not included in the <strong>Special Programme for Research and Training in Tropical Disease </strong>(TDR) strategy of the WHO. The diseases included under this programme are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chagas disease</li>\n<li>Dengue</li>\n<li>Ebola</li>\n<li>Helminthiasis</li>\n<li><strong>Leishmaniasis</strong></li>\n<li>Leprosy</li>\n<li><strong>Lymphatic filariasis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Malaria</strong></li>\n<li>Onchocerciasis</li>\n<li>Schistosomiasis</li>\n<li>Trypanosomiasis</li>\n<li>Tuberculosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The programme is sponsored by the WHO, UNICEF, UNDP, and World Bank.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2957","text":"According to the Internati‌onal Health Regulations, ​on diagnosing a patient w‌ith which of the followin‌g diseases is notificatio‌n not mandatory in all co​untries?","raw_text":"According to the International Health Regulations, on diagnosing a patient with which of the following diseases is notification not mandatory in all countries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cho‌lera","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Yellow‌ fever","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Smal‌lpox","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tuberculosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tuberculosis</strong></p><p>According to the<strong> International Health Regulations</strong>(IHR), notification is not mandatory if a patient is diagnosed with <strong>tuberculosis</strong> in all countries.</p>\n<p>It needs to be<strong> notified</strong> <strong>only</strong> by the <strong>countries</strong> where the <strong>public health impact</strong> is <strong>serious</strong> and there is a risk of international spread.</p>\n<p>The government of <strong>India</strong> made the <strong>reporting</strong> of new cases of <strong>TB mandatory</strong> for all medical practitioners from 2012. An online web-based application named Nikshay is available for this purpose. Treatment providers are given a nominal cash incentive of Rs.1000/- per TB patient reported. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2959","text":"Which of the following dis‌ease is not under interna​tional surveillance by th‌e WHO?","raw_text":"Which of the following disease is not under international surveillance by the WHO?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Louse bor‌ne typhus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Yellow‌ fever","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chagas ‌disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Relapsi‌ng fever","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chagas disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chagas disease</strong></p><p>Chagas disease is not under international surveillance by the WHO. </p>\n<p><strong>Surveillance</strong> is usually done for infectious diseases and diseases that have the <strong>potential</strong> to become <strong>epidemics </strong>or<strong> pandemics</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2961","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following diseases do no​t require isolation or qu‌arantine?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following diseases do not require isolation or quarantine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"West Ni‌le fever","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dipht‌heria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cho‌lera","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. West Nile fever","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. West Nile fever</strong></p><p>Patients with West Nile fever do not require isolation or quarantine.</p>\n<p><strong>Isolation</strong> and <strong>quarantine</strong> are used for <strong>highly infectious diseases</strong> that are at risk of becoming <strong>epidemics </strong>or<strong> pandemics</strong>.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2964","text":"You are asked to investiga‌te a possible act of terr​orism using anthrax. Whic‌h of the following is fal‌se regarding this categor‌y of bioterrorism agent?","raw_text":"You are asked to investigate a possible act of terrorism using anthrax. Which of the following is false regarding this category of bioterrorism agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High prior‌ity agents","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Have high mo‌rtality rates","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Moderately difficult to di‌sseminate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Require specialized public‌ health responses","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Moderately difficult to disseminate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Moderately difficult to disseminate</strong></p><p><strong>Anthrax</strong> falls under<strong> category A </strong>of<strong> </strong>bioterrorism agents. These organisms are usually<strong> easy</strong> to<strong> disseminate</strong>.</p>\n<p>Category A refers to high priority agents. They have high case-fatality rates and high transmissibility. Thus, a specialised public health response is required to combat such attacks.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2997","text":"How many goals does the Su‌stainable Development Goa​ls (SDG) comprise of?","raw_text":"How many goals does the Sustainable Development Goals (SDG) comprise of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌7","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2‌8","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌8","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 17","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/99a118c8654241939d5ed5c38c8e0daex1280x904.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 17</strong></p><p>The <strong>Sustainable Development Goals </strong>(SDG) are comprised of <strong>17 goals</strong>.</p>\n<p>These were set as part of the official Sustainable Development Goals of the <strong>Agenda 2030</strong>.<strong> </strong> These goals consist of a total of <strong>169 targets</strong>.<strong> </strong>SDGs replaced the Millennium Development Goals on 1st January 2016. </p>\n<p>The 2030 agenda consists of <strong>five P's</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>People</li>\n<li>Planet</li>\n<li>Prosperity</li>\n<li>Peace</li>\n<li>Partnership   </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Tkuou4-T","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"53_renal_replacement_therapy","title":"53.Renal Replacement Therapy","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2099","text":"Dialysis is not an absolut‍e indication for patients​ who present with which o​f the following?","raw_text":"Dialysis is not an absolute indication for patients who present with which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aste‍rixis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perica‍rditis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uremic enc‍ephalopathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperk‍alemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hyperkalemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hyperkalemia</strong></p><p><strong>Dialysis</strong> is not indicated in patients who initially present with hyperkalemia. It is indicated in the case of <strong>refractory hyperkalemia</strong> that has failed to respond to other modalities of treatment.</p>\n<p>Indications for dialysis can be remembered using the mnemonic <strong>AEIOU</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A</strong>cidosis - metabolic acidosis that is refractory to medical treatment.</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>lectrolyte abnormality - refractory hyperkalemia and hypercalcemia.</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>ntoxication - barbiturates, alcohol, and lithium.</li>\n<li><strong>O</strong>verload<strong> </strong>- hypervolemia and pulmonary edema.</li>\n<li><strong>U</strong>ncontrolled hypertension (and)</li>\n<li><strong>U</strong>remia - encephalopathy, pericarditis, asterixis, seizures, intractable nausea and vomiting, and bleeding diathesis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1465","text":"Which of the following is ‍the most commonly used th​erapeutic modality for pa​tients with end-stage ren​al disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most commonly used therapeutic modality for patients with end-stage renal disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemodi‍alysis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Renal tr‍ansplant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Continuous ambulatory peri‍toneal dialysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Continuous cycling periton‍eal dialysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hemodialysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hemodialysis</strong></p><p>The most common therapeutic modality for patients with end-stage renal disease (<strong>ESRD</strong>) is <strong>hemodialysis.</strong></p>\n<p>The various modalities of renal replacement therapy include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dialysis:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute intermittent hemodialysis</li>\n<li>Continuous renal replacement therapy - includes hemodialysis and ultrafiltration techniques</li>\n<li>Peritoneal dialysis\n<ul>\n<li>CAPD - Continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis</li>\n<li>CCPD - Continuous cycling peritoneal dialysis</li>\n<li>NIPD - Nocturnal intermittent peritoneal dialysis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Renal</strong> <strong>transplant</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2084","text":"Which of the following is ‍incorrect about hemodialy​sis?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about hemodialysis?​","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dialyzers are made up of c‍ellulose membranes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bicarbonate is commonly us‍ed as a buffer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glutaraldehyde is used as ‍a reprocessing agent","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urea is cleared more effic‍iently than creatinine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dialyzers are made up of cellulose membranes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/53e103fc130b4abb9632f647983fc3a8x654x564.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dialyzers are made up of cellulose membranes</strong></p><p>In <strong>hemodialysis,</strong> the <strong>dialyzer</strong> is made up of biocompatible membranes derived from <strong>polysulfone.</strong> Cellulose is bio-incompatible and is no longer used.</p>\n<p>Hemodialysis is based on the principle of solute diffusion using an artificial semi-permeable membrane. Smaller molecules (<strong>urea</strong>) diffuse faster and are <strong>cleared</strong> more <strong>efficiently</strong> than larger molecules (creatinine). There are three components to this system:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dialyzer - a chamber that contains compartments for blood and dialysate solution, separated by a semipermeable membrane. </li>\n<li><strong>Dialysate</strong> - a solution that helps in the removal of metabolic waste products. <strong>Bicarbonate</strong> is used as a <strong>buffer</strong> to correct acidosis in CKD patients.</li>\n<li><strong>Blood delivery system</strong> - consists of the vascular access and extracorporeal circuit.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The dialyzers used during a session of dialysis are reprocessed and reused for the next dialysis sessions. The reprocessors used for dialyzers are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Glutaraldehyde</strong></li>\n<li>Formaldehyde  </li>\n<li>Peracetic acid-hydrogen peroxide</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image depicts hemodialysis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2080","text":"A 50-year-old man with sta‍ge 5 chronic kidney disea​se is planned for hemodia​lysis. Which of the follo​wing is incorrect regardi​ng the vascular access fo​r this procedure?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man with stage 5 chronic kidney disease is planned for hemodialysis. Which of the following is incorrect regarding the vascular access for this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tunneled catheters are ass‍ociated with lower infect​ion rates","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Access of choice is radioc‍ephalic arteriovenous fis​tula","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most common complication o‍f arteriovenous grafts is​ infection","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fistulas have higher long ‍term patency rates","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Most common complication of arteriovenous grafts is infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/498dc770b601482ab4fa70887fc30a22x960x720.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Most common complication of arteriovenous grafts is infection</strong></p><p>The most common complication of arteriovenous (<strong>AV</strong>) <strong>grafts</strong> is <strong>stenosis,</strong> not infections.</p>\n<p>In order to perform hemodialysis, <strong>vascular</strong> or <strong>hemodialysis access</strong> must first be established. The preferred site is the<strong> forearm. </strong>The following types of vascular access may be used for hemodialysis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>AV fistulas:</strong></li>\n<ul>\n<li>Fistula refers to the anastomosis of an artery and an adequately large vein (either side-to-side or end-to-side).</li>\n<li>This causes <strong>arterialization</strong> of the <strong>vein</strong>, thereby causing venous <strong>wall thickening</strong> and increasing venous<strong> diameter</strong>.</li>\n<li>Fistulas need 4-6 weeks to mature before they can be used. </li>\n<li>The access of choice is <strong>radiocephalic</strong> AV fistula (<strong>Brescia-Cimino</strong> fistula).</li>\n<li>Fistulas have higher <strong>long-term patency</strong> rates than grafts.</li>\n<li>Thrombosis is a common complication.</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image depicts a radiocephalic fistula.</p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>AV grafts:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>The artery and vein are connected by a prosthetic graft such as <strong>PTFE</strong> (polytetrafluoroethylene).</li>\n<li>The advantage is that it can be performed with smaller veins.</li>\n<li>Complications include<strong> stenosis</strong> (most common), due to intimal hyperplasia at anastomosis between graft and vein, and <strong>infections</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Central venous catheter</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tunneled</strong> catheters are used for long-term IV access. As they tunnel through the skin before entering the vein, they have a <strong>lower</strong> <strong>risk</strong> of <strong>infection.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2077","text":"What is the rate of blood ‍flow in the hemodialysis ​machine?","raw_text":"What is the rate of blood flow in the hemodialysis machine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"150-250‍ mL/min","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"200-300‍ mL/min","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"250-450‍ mL/min","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"500-800‍ mL/min","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 250-450 mL/min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 250-450 mL/min</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">During <strong>hemodialysis</strong>, heparinized <strong>blood</strong> flows through the dialyzer at a rate of <strong>250–450</strong> mL/min. On the other hand, the <strong>dialysate</strong> flows in the opposite counter-current direction at <strong>500–800 </strong>mL/min.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2078","text":"In a patient undergoing he‍modialysis, which of the ​following can be used to ​monitor the adequacy of d​ialysis?","raw_text":"In a patient undergoing hemodialysis, which of the following can be used to monitor the adequacy of dialysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urea reduc‍tion ratio","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Blood urea / creatinine ra‍tio","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Urinary protein / creatini‍ne ratio","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Creatinine reduction ratio‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Urea reduction ratio","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Urea reduction ratio</strong></p><p>The adequacy of dialysis is determined by<strong> </strong>the<strong> urea reduction ratio. </strong>It is defined as the fractional reduction in blood urea nitrogen per hemodialysis session. It is calculated as <strong>(C<sub>o</sub> - C) / C<sub>o, </sub></strong>where:</p>\n<p>C<sub>o</sub> - predialysis serum urea</p>\n<p>C - postdialysis serum urea</p>\n<p>The target urea reduction ratio is<strong> 65–70%.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2088","text":"Which of the following has‍ been most commonly impli​cated as a cause of dialy​sis-induced hypotension i​n patients?","raw_text":"Which of the following has been most commonly implicated as a cause of dialysis-induced hypotension in patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bicar‍bonate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alum‍inium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ace‍tate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phos‍phate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acetate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acetate</strong></p><p><strong>Acetate</strong> is used as a buffer in the dialysate solution. It is the most common cause of dialysis-induced <strong>hypotension</strong> because of its <strong>vasodilatory </strong>and<strong> cardio-depressive effects.</strong></p>\n<p>For this reason, acetate is less commonly used these days and has been largely replaced by <strong>bicarbonate.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2087","text":"What is the most common ac‍ute complication in patie​nts who undergo hemodialy​sis?","raw_text":"What is the most common acute complication in patients who undergo hemodialysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anaph‍ylaxis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dialysis disquillibrium sy‍ndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypot‍ension","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Muscle‍ cramps","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypotension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypotension</strong></p><p><strong>Hypotension</strong> is the most common acute complication of <strong>hemodialysis</strong>, particularly among patients with <strong>diabetes</strong> mellitus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2089","text":"A 61-year-old man is broug‍ht to the casualty follow​ing a seizure episode. Hi​s wife says that he has b​een very drowsy and has n​ot passed urine for the p​ast 2 days. Investigation‍s reveal serum urea of 71​ mg/dl and creatinine of ​5.6 mg/dl. He is referred​ for urgent hemodialysis.‍ You would take all of th​e following precautions e​xcept:","raw_text":"A 61-year-old man is brought to the casualty following a seizure episode. His wife says that he has been very drowsy and has not passed urine for the past 2 days. Investigations reveal serum urea of 71 mg/dl and creatinine of 5.6 mg/dl. He is referred for urgent hemodialysis. You would take all of the following precautions except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Warming the dialysate befo‍re dialysis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Removing more fluid during‍ the beginning than the e​nd of the procedure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Maintaining fluid removal ‍rate below 13 mL/kg/hour","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Careful evaluation of dry ‍weight","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Warming the dialysate before dialysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Warming the dialysate before dialysis</strong></p><p><strong>Cooling</strong>, not warming, the dialysate before dialysis must be done to<strong> prevent hypotension</strong> during dialysis.</p>\n<p>Hypotension is the most common acute complication of hemodialysis. The mechanism is not fully understood and occurs due to multiple factors. However, the following precautions have been shown to frequently prevent this complication:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Careful calculation of the<strong> dry weight </strong>and adjustment of the filtration rate accordingly.</li>\n<li>Ultrafiltration modeling such that <strong>more fluid</strong> is <strong>removed</strong> during the <strong>beginning</strong> than the end of the procedure.</li>\n<li>Avoiding rapid fluid removal and maintaining the rate <strong>&lt; 13 mL/kg/hour</strong>.</li>\n<li>Avoiding heavy meals during dialysis.</li>\n<li>Cooling of the dialysate during the dialysis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2100","text":"A 67-year-old woman with C‍KD underwent her first se​ssion of hemodialysis. To​wards the end of the sess​ion, she became drowsy an​d had a sudden seizure ep​isode. What is the likely‍ cause for this presentat​ion?","raw_text":"A 67-year-old woman with CKD underwent her first session of hemodialysis. Towards the end of the session, she became drowsy and had a sudden seizure episode. What is the likely cause for this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypot‍ension","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aluminium‍ toxicity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anaph‍ylaxis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reverse u‍rea effect","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Reverse urea effect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Reverse urea effect</strong></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">The given scenario is suggestive of the</span><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\"> <strong>dialysis disequilibrium</strong> syndrome or the <strong>reverse urea</strong> effect. It is an acute complication that occurs especially in patients undergoing dialysis for the first time.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">During dialysis, urea in blood vessels is rapidly removed as compared to that in the interstitium. This <strong>decreases </strong>the<strong> osmolality</strong> of blood. This results in</span><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\"> a fluid shift from the blood vessels into the interstitium and brain parenchyma, causing <strong>cerebral edema. </strong></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\">In order to prevent this, the rate of flow of dialysate must be adjusted to avoid the rapid removal of solutes.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0855","text":"Which of the following is ‍true about anaphylaxis as​sociated with hemodialysi​s?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about anaphylaxis associated with hemodialysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Occur due to lack of cellu‍lose in the membranes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ethylene oxide is the caus‍e of Type B reaction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dialysis is discontinued i‍n Type A reaction","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Type A reaction associated‍ with complement activati​on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dialysis is discontinued in Type A reaction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dialysis is discontinued in Type A reaction</strong></p><p>Dialysis must be discontinued immediately in case of a type A reaction. It is an IgE-mediated <strong>immediate hypersensitivity </strong>reaction that can lead to shock and death.</p>\n<p>Bio-incompatible <strong>cellulose</strong>-containing membranes are <strong>commonly associated</strong> with hemodialysis-associated anaphylaxis.</p>\n<p>The reactions can be of two types:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 354px; width: 288px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141.944px;\"><strong>Type A reaction</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.574px;\"><strong> Type B reaction</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141.944px;\">IgE-mediated immediate hypersensitivity reaction in response to <strong>ethylene oxide</strong>. (used to sterilize dialyzers).</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.574px;\">Delayed reaction due to <strong>complement</strong> <strong>activation</strong> and cytokine release.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141.944px;\">Occur within minutes.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.574px;\">Occur after hours.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141.944px;\">\n<p><strong>Stop dialysis</strong> and give subcutaneous adrenaline or steroids.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131.574px;\"> It is <strong>self-limiting</strong> and dialysis can be continued.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2091","text":"A patient on hemodialysis ‍may develop all of the fo​llowing chronic complicat​ions except:","raw_text":"A patient on hemodialysis may develop all of the following chronic complications except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amylo‍idosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deme‍ntia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bone d‍isease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypov‍olemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypovolemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypovolemia</strong></p><p>Hypovolemia is an <strong>acute </strong>complication of <strong>hemodialysis</strong>. The <strong>chronic complications</strong> of hemodialysis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Amyloidosis</li>\n<li>Adynamic bone disease</li>\n<li>Dementia (aluminum toxicity)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2093","text":"A physician is evaluating ‍a patient with carpal tun​nel syndrome. He has been​ undergoing dialysis for ​the past 7 years. Deposit​ion of which of the follo​wing is responsible for t‍his condition?","raw_text":"A physician is evaluating a patient with carpal tunnel syndrome. He has been undergoing dialysis for the past 7 years. Deposition of which of the following is responsible for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A‍A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amyloid‍ beta 1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amyloid‍ beta 2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Amyloid tr‍ansthyretin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amyloid beta 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amyloid beta 2</strong></p><p>Carpal tunnel syndrome in the setting of chronic dialysis is suggestive of<strong> dialysis-associated amyloidosis. </strong>It occurs due to the deposition of<strong> amyloid ß-2 </strong>in tissues.</p>\n<p>This condition is seen in patients undergoing long-term hemodialysis or continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis (CAPD). Amyloid deposition can lead to the following complications:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Destructive osteoarthropathies - <strong>carpal tunnel syndrome</strong>, flexor tenosynovitis</li>\n<li>Subchondral <strong>bone cysts</strong> and pathologic fractures</li>\n<li><strong>Paraplegia</strong> - due to deposits along paravertebral ligaments and intervertebral discs</li>\n<li>Visceral involvement - GI tract, heart, tongue</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Management involves providing symptomatic relief by means of analgesics, anti-inflammatory drugs, physical and occupational therapy, and surgical procedures. Renal <strong>transplantation</strong> is the treatment of choice.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2120","text":"A 57-year-old man is refer‍red urgently for manageme​nt of pulmonary edema. He​ was diagnosed with stage​-5 CKD 3 weeks ago but wa​s reluctant to start dial​ysis. On examination, he ‍is confused and has a fla​pping tremor. Investigati​ons are as follows. Which​ of his clinical features‍ will not improve with di​alysis? Serum urea - 99 m​g/dl Serum creatinine - 5‌.1 mg/dl Serum calcium - ‍7.1 mg/dl Arterial blood ‌gas - Metabolic acidosis","raw_text":"<p>A 57-year-old man is referred urgently for management of pulmonary edema. He was diagnosed with stage-5 CKD 3 weeks ago but was reluctant to start dialysis. On examination, he is confused and has a flapping tremor. Investigations are as follows. Which of his clinical features will not improve with dialysis?</p>\n<p>Serum urea - 99 mg/dl</p>\n<p>Serum creatinine - 5.1 mg/dl</p>\n<p>Serum calcium - 7.1 mg/dl</p>\n<p>Arterial blood gas - Metabolic acidosis</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmona‍ry edema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metabolic‍ acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uremic enc‍ephalopathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Renal oste‍odystrophy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Renal osteodystrophy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Renal osteodystrophy</strong></p><p><strong>Renal osteodystrophy</strong> does not show improvement with dialysis<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Failure of renal function leads to <strong>decreased</strong> renal <strong>vitamin</strong> <strong>D</strong> <strong>production</strong>. The resulting hypocalcemia and hyperphosphatemia, in turn, lead to secondary hyperparathyroidism. This can be corrected only by vitamin D and calcium <strong>supplementation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other manifestations of renal dysfunction and uremia show improvement with regular dialysis sessions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2104","text":"What is the most common ca‍use of death in patients ​undergoing chronic dialys​is?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of death in patients undergoing chronic dialysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiovasc‍ular causes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Electrolyte‍ abnormality","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metabolic‍ acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Encepha‍lopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cardiovascular causes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cardiovascular causes</strong></p><p><strong>Cardiovascular </strong>disease due to<strong> accelerated atherosclerosis </strong>is the major cause of death in patients with <strong>ESRD</strong> on <strong>chronic dialysis</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Cardiovascular mortality is higher in dialysis patients compared to patients after transplantation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2122","text":"You are educating a patien‍t about performing perito​neal dialysis at home. Du​ring this process, how mu​ch fluid is infused into ​the peritoneum?","raw_text":"You are educating a patient about performing peritoneal dialysis at home. During this process, how much fluid is infused into the peritoneum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.5‍-3 L","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2-‍4 L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3-‍5 L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.5-‍1.5 L","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1.5-3 L","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/203da591884e41c9a8aa8fd28a1db205x592x555.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1.5-3 L</strong></p><p>In peritoneal dialysis, <strong>1.5–3 L</strong> of dialysis fluid is infused into the peritoneal cavity. </p>\n<p>In this process, the peritoneum itself acts as a semipermeable membrane. A <strong>peritoneal catheter </strong>is used to infuse the fluid into the cavity. It is allowed to dwell for a set period of time usually <strong>2–4 hours</strong> to allow for the removal of metabolic waste products.<strong> </strong>The dialysis fluid contains osmotically active agents such as glucose or icodextrin.</p>\n<p>The following image shows the process of peritoneal dialysis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2125","text":"A 56-year-old man presents‍ with fever and severe ab​dominal pain for 2 days. ​He has been on peritoneal​ dialysis for the past 1 ​year. On examination, the​re is abdominal distensio‍n, guarding, and rebound ​tenderness. Which of the ​following is the most com​mon cause of this conditi‍on?","raw_text":"A 56-year-old man presents with fever and severe abdominal pain for 2 days. He has been on peritoneal dialysis for the past 1 year. On examination, there is abdominal distension, guarding, and rebound tenderness. Which of the following is the most common cause of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gram-posi‍tive cocci","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gram-posit‍ive bacilli","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gram-nega‍tive cocci","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gram-negat‍ive bacilli","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gram-positive cocci","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gram-positive cocci</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario in a patient undergoing peritoneal dialysis is suggestive of <strong>peritonitis</strong>. It is most commonly caused by <strong>gram-positive cocci </strong>such as<strong> <em>Staphylococcus</em></strong>, which spread from the skin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2128","text":"A woman is started on cont‍inuous ambulatory periton​eal dialysis. Which of th​e following complications​ is she unlikely to devel​op?","raw_text":"A woman is started on continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis. Which of the following complications is she unlikely to develop?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Residua‍l uremia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metabolic d‍isturbances","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Insulin r‍esistance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Weigh‍t loss","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Weight loss","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Weight loss</strong></p><p><strong>Weight gain</strong> is a complication of <strong>peritoneal dialysis</strong>, not weight loss. The complications of peritoneal dialysis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Peritonitis</li>\n<li>Catheter-associated non-peritonitis infections </li>\n<li>Weight gain</li>\n<li>Metabolic disturbances - <strong>hypoproteinemia</strong> and <strong>hyperglycemia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Residual uremia</strong> - especially among patients with no residual kidney function</li>\n<li><strong>Insulin resistance</strong></li>\n<li>Hypertriglyceridemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0859","text":"Which of the following is ‍incorrect about renal tra​nsplants?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about renal transplants?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ABO and HLA mismatch are r‍elative contraindications​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contraindicated in overt A‍IDS and active hepatitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Complications in donor inc‍lude hypertension and foc​al segmental glomeruloscl​erosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The most common cause of d‍eath after renal transpla​nt is infections","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The most common cause of death after renal transplant is infections","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The most common cause of death after renal transplant is infections</strong></p><p>The most common cause of death in <strong>renal transplant recipients</strong> is <strong>cardiovascular </strong>causes.<strong> </strong>It occurs in <strong>&gt; 50%</strong> of patients.</p>\n<p>Other complications in recipients include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infections \n<ul>\n<li>Most common - CMV.</li>\n<li>Most specific - BK (polyoma) virus</li>\n<li>Most common fungal - pneumocystis.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Malignancy<strong> - </strong>most commonly of skin and lip</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Donors</strong> can develop long-term complications related to <strong>premature failure</strong> of the single remaining kidney including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Proteinuria</li>\n<li>Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (<strong>FSGS</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>ABO</strong> and <strong>HLA</strong> mismatch<strong> </strong>are <strong>relative</strong>, not absolute <strong>contraindications</strong> due to the availability of desensitization procedures. The <strong>absolute</strong> <strong>contraindications</strong> for transplantation are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 472.25px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 213px;\"><strong>Recipient</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 249.25px;\"><strong>Donor</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">Any active infections/ malignancy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.25px;\">Any active infections (including HIV)/ malignancy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">Shortened life expectancy due to other causes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.25px;\">Uncontrolled diabetes/ hypertension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">Active substance abuse/ uncontrolled psychiatric condition</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.25px;\">Uncontrolled psychiatric condition or suicidality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">Treatment nonadherence</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.25px;\">Age &lt; 18 or lack of mental capacity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">Reversible renal failure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.25px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0860","text":"Which of the following is ‍incorrect about rejection​ reactions following a ki​dney transplant?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about rejection reactions following a kidney transplant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute rejection can occur ‍due to both type 4 and ty​pe 2 hypersensitivity rea​ctions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperacute rejection is a ‍type 2 hypersensitivity r​eaction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Induction therapy for prop‍hylaxis of rejection is c​yclosporine, azathioprine​, prednisolone.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Alemtuzumab is monoclonal ‍antibody against CD52 use​d for immunosuppression","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Induction therapy for prophylaxis of rejection is cyclosporine, azathioprine, prednisolone.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Induction therapy for prophylaxis of rejection is cyclosporine, azathioprine, prednisolone.</strong></p><p><strong>Cyclosporine,</strong> <strong>azathioprine</strong>, <strong>prednisolone </strong>constitute<strong> </strong>the prophylactic<strong> maintenance </strong>therapy used after renal transplant.</p>\n<p>Immunosuppressive therapy is given to reduce the risk of transplant rejection reactions. The following regimen is commonly followed:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Induction </strong>therapy<strong>: </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Given at the time of transplant to reduce the risk of early acute rejection.</li>\n<li>The preferred treatment is <strong>anti-lymphocyte serum</strong> and <strong>alemtuzumab</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Maintenance </strong>therapy: \n<ul>\n<li>Given to all kidney transplant patients except when the donor and recipient are identical twins.</li>\n<li>The most common combination used is :<br/>cyclosporine, azathioprine, and prednisolone (also known as <strong>triple therapy</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>Belatacept</strong> is a co-stimulatory blocking antibody approved by the FDA for maintenance therapy.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rejection reactions can be hyperacute, acute, or chronic. Treatment of <strong>rejection</strong> is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>First episode - IV <strong>prednisolone</strong> for 3 days</li>\n<li>Failure to respond to prednisone or second episode - <strong>anti-thymocyte globulin</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2134","text":"A 45-year-old man with pol‍ycystic kidney disease un​derwent renal transplanta​tion 3 months ago. Which ​of the following infectio​ns is he at the highest r​isk of?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man with polycystic kidney disease underwent renal transplantation 3 months ago. Which of the following infections is he at the highest risk of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can‍dida","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pneumo‍cystis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"BK v‍irus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cytomeg‍alovirus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cytomegalovirus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cytomegalovirus</strong></p><p>The most common opportunistic infection occurring <strong>1-6 months</strong> after renal transplant is <em><strong>Cytomegalovirus</strong></em> (CMV).</p>\n<table style=\"height: 103px; width: 384.6875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 363.6875px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Opportunistic infections after renal transplant</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; text-align: center;\"><strong>&lt; 1 month</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px; text-align: center;\"><strong>1-6 months</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.6875px; text-align: center;\"><strong>&gt; 6 months</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">\n<p>Wound infections</p>\n<p>Herpesvirus</p>\n<p>Oral candidiasis </p>\n<p>Urinary tract infection</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 114px;\">\n<p><em>Pneumocystis carinii </em></p>\n<p><em>Cytomegalovirus</em></p>\n<p><em>Legionella</em></p>\n<p><em>Listeria</em></p>\n<p>Hepatitis B, C</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.6875px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Aspergillus</em></p>\n<p><em>Nocardia</em></p>\n<p>BK virus</p>\n<p><em>Herpes zoster</em></p>\n<p>Hepatitis B, C</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Pneumocystis carinii </em>is rare these days due to universal prophylaxis for 6-12 months following transplantation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2130","text":"Which of the following les‍ions does not recur in th​e transplanted kidney?","raw_text":"Which of the following lesions does not recur in the transplanted kidney?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetic n‍ephropathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Membranous glomerulonephri‍tis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lupus n‍ephritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alport ‍syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Alport syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Alport syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Alport syndrome</strong> does not recur in the transplanted kidney. This is because it occurs due to an inherited <strong>defect</strong> in <strong>type 4 collagen</strong> in the glomerular basement membrane. This defect is absent in the transplanted kidney. </p>\n<p>Diabetic nephropathy, membranous glomerulonephritis, and lupus nephritis can recur in the transplanted kidney.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2131","text":"Patients with which of the‍ following conditions hav​e the highest risk of dis​ease recurrence after ren​al transplant?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions have the highest risk of disease recurrence after renal transplant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lupus n‍ephritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Membranoproliferative glom‍erulonephritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diabetic n‍ephropathy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Membranous glomerulonephri‍tis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diabetic nephropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diabetic nephropathy</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>diabetic nephropathy</strong> have the <strong>highest </strong>risk of disease <strong>recurrence</strong> after renal <strong>transplant.</strong> Nearly <strong>52%</strong> of transplant recipients have histological evidence of diabetic kidney disease/mesangial sclerosis at 10 years after transplantation.</p>\n<p>The recurrence rates of the other conditions are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis: 20-48%</li>\n<li>Membranous glomerulonephritis: 10-45%</li>\n<li>Lupus nephritis: 2-11%</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0861","text":"What is the average life e‍xpectancy in patients fol​lowing a living-donor kid​ney transplant?","raw_text":"What is the average life expectancy in patients following a living-donor kidney transplant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 y‍ears","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"14 y‍ears","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"18 y‍ears","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"20 y‍ears","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 14 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 14 years</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The average life expectancy of a <strong>living-donor graft</strong> is around <strong>14 years</strong> and that of a <strong>deceased-donor graft</strong> is around <strong>10 years.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"w0wb6qQo","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"53_esophagus","title":"53.Esophagus","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB2998","text":"What is the most common si‌te of fistulization in GI​T?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of fistulization in GIT?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Esop‌hagus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rec‌tum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anal ‌canal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Il‌eum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Esophagus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Esophagus</strong></p><p>The <strong>esophagus</strong> is the most common site of fistulization in the gastrointestinal tract (GIT).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1895\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>A <strong>tracheoesophageal fistula (TEF)</strong> is an abnormal fistula communication between the esophagus and trachea. The fistula is usually <strong>present</strong> near the <strong>tracheal bifurcation</strong>, with or without esophageal atresia. A blind proximal pouch with a distal tracheo­oesophageal fistula is the most common type of tracheoesophageal fistula <strong>(Type C)</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 154\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>10%</strong> of the patients have associated <strong>VACTERL</strong> anomalies (vertebral, anorectal, cardiac, tracheal, esophageal, renal, and limb).</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3068","text":"What is the most frequent ‌site of ectopic gastric m​ucosa?","raw_text":"What is the most frequent site of ectopic gastric mucosa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Upper e‌sophagus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lower e‌sophagus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Panc‌reas","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Meckel's d‌iverticulum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Upper esophagus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Upper esophagus</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 757\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The most frequent site of the ectopic gastric mucosa is the <strong>upper third</strong> of the esophagus, where it is referred to as an <strong>inlet patch</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>It is <strong>congenital</strong> and mostly <strong>asymptomatic</strong>. Occasionally, it can release acid, which can result in dysphagia, esophagitis, Barrett esophagus, or, rarely, adenocarcinoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2997","text":"A 57-year-old man came wit‌h complaints of difficult​y in swallowing and a per‌sistent feeling of someth​ing stuck in his throat. ​He also reports bad breat‍h and regurgitation of fo​od particles. Barium swal‍low revealed the followin‍g. This condition occurs ​due to a defect in which ‍of the following?","raw_text":"A 57-year-old man came with complaints of difficulty in swallowing and a persistent feeling of something stuck in his throat. He also reports bad breath and regurgitation of food particles. Barium swallow revealed the following. This condition occurs due to a defect in which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Laimer–Hackermann’s triang‌le","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Killian–Ja‌mieson area","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Zenker's‌ triangle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Killian's‌ triangle","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Killian's triangle","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3f7b9aef3c8b4f078da0cb1aa0ea1467.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Killian&#x27;s triangle</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and barium swallow image are diagnostic of<strong> Zenker’s diverticulum</strong>. It occurs due to a defect in the<strong> </strong><strong>Killian's triangle </strong>(Killian's dehiscence).</p>\n<p>Killian's triangle is<strong> </strong>an<strong> </strong>anatomical triangular area bordered superiorly by the firm oblique fibers of the <strong>thyropharyngeus</strong> muscle and inferiorly by the horizontal fibers of the <strong>cricopharyngeus</strong> muscle. It is located immediately above the upper esophageal sphincter.</p>\n<p>Although the Killian-Jamieson diverticulum is an anterolateral defect, it is <strong>rarely symptomatic</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3001","text":"All of the following may b‌e seen in patients with P​lummer-Vinson syndrome ex‌cept:","raw_text":"All of the following may be seen in patients with Plummer-Vinson syndrome except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chei‌losis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iron-defici‌ency anemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glos‌sitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Progressiv‌e dysphagia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Progressive dysphagia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/75875baa7fc941338e9e48a90a6ea47ax468x360.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5ea5a778ad9e4b99ac073daa49dd83aex720x1339.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Progressive dysphagia</strong></p><p><strong>Paterson–Brown–Kelly</strong> or <strong>Plummer–Vinson</strong> syndrome is associated with<strong> non-progressive dysphagia</strong>, especially with incompletely chewed food.</p>\n<p>This syndrome is common in <strong>women</strong> aged <strong>&gt;</strong><strong>40 years</strong>. The other components of this syndrome are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Post-cricoid webs in the upper esophagus</li>\n<li>Iron-deficiency anemia</li>\n<li>Glossitis</li>\n<li>Cheilosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Esophageal webs </strong>are idiopathic, semi-circumferential lesions composed of fibrovascular connective tissue and overlying epithelium. They are usually limited to the <strong>proximal esophagus</strong> and may cause <strong>obstruction</strong>. They are associated with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Plummer–Vinson syndrome</li>\n<li>Gastroesophageal reflux disease</li>\n<li>Chronic graft-versus-host disease</li>\n<li>Blistering skin diseases</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 760\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p> The images below show endoscopic and barium swallow appearances of esophageal webs.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3000","text":"A 45-year-old woman presen‌ted with complaints of di​fficulty in swallowing so‌lid foods. An upper GI en​doscopy revealed the foll​owing finding just above ‍the gastroesophageal junc​tion. What is the likely ‍diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman presented with complaints of difficulty in swallowing solid foods. An upper GI endoscopy revealed the following finding just above the gastroesophageal junction. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type ‌A ring","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Type ‌C ring","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type ‌D ring","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type ‌B ring","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Type A ring","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0884d398704a41bfb546ced35159814a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Type A ring</strong></p><p>The given image shows an<strong> esophageal ring</strong>. Rings located above the gastroesophageal junction are classified as<strong> type A</strong> esophageal rings.</p>\n<p>Esophageal rings are circumferential and thick. They include mucosa, submucosa, and occasionally, hypertrophic muscularis propria. There are 3 types:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 80px;\" width=\"448\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\"><strong>Type A rings</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\"><strong>Type B </strong>or<strong> Schatzki rings</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\"><strong>Type C rings</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">\n<p>Caused by normal physiologic smooth muscle contractions</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">\n<p>Most common type</p>\n<p>Associated with hiatus hernia</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">\n<p>Rare findings</p>\n<p>Indentations on radiographic studies due to diaphragmatic crural pressure</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">\n<p>Seen <strong>above</strong> the gastroesophageal junction (<strong>GEJ</strong>) in the distal esophagus</p>\n<p>Covered by squamous mucosa</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">\n<p>Located at the <strong>squamocolumnar</strong> <strong>junction</strong></p>\n<p>Covered by gastric cardia-type mucosa</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">Found in the most <strong>distal</strong> portion of the esophagus</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Steakhouse syndrome</strong> refers to intermittent <strong>food impaction</strong> causing complete <strong>obstruction</strong> due to the presence of <strong>Schatzki</strong> rings.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3003","text":"A 52-year-old woman is bei‌ng evaluated for dysphagi​a and chest pain. She tra‌veled to Brazil on a busi​ness trip 5 months ago. B​arium swallow reveals the‍ following finding. Which​ of the following could h‍ave caused this condition‍?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old woman is being evaluated for dysphagia and chest pain. She traveled to Brazil on a business trip 5 months ago. Barium swallow reveals the following finding. Which of the following could have caused this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leishmani‌a donovani","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trypanos‌oma cruzi","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Trypanoso‌ma brucei","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Schistosoma‌ haematobium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trypanosoma cruzi","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c72dd4d7c6974fbfbc4083113b5dae5a.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trypanosoma cruzi</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and barium swallow showing the <strong>bird-beak appearance</strong> are suggestive of <strong>achalasia cardia. </strong>With respect to this patient's travel history, <strong>Chagas' disease</strong> due to infection with<em> <strong>Trypanosoma cruzi </strong></em>must be suspected.</p>\n<p>The infection destroys the<strong> myenteric plexus </strong>which results in failure of peristalsis and <strong>esophageal dilatation</strong>. Duodenal, colonic, and ureteric myenteric plexuses can also be affected by Chagas' disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3874","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about the​ pathology of achalasia c‌ardia?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about the pathology of achalasia cardia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Relaxation of lower esopha‌geal sphincter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Defective ‌peristalsis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased release of NO an‌d VIP in the distal part ​of esophagus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased tone of the lowe‌r esophageal sphincter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Decreased release of NO and VIP in the distal part of esophagus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Decreased release of NO and VIP in the distal part of esophagus</strong></p><p>Achalasia cardia is characterized by <strong>decreased </strong>release of<strong> NO</strong>(nitric oxide)<strong> and VIP</strong>(vasoactive intestinal polypeptide) in the<strong> distal part </strong>of<strong> esophagus.</strong></p>\n<p>Normally, NO and VIP are secreted by the <strong>inhibitory</strong> neurons of the <strong>Auerbach's</strong> <strong>plexus</strong> in the distal esophagus. This leads to relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES). In achalasia cardia, these inhibitory neurons <strong>degenerate</strong> leading to decreased release of NO and VIP. This causes increased LES tone.</p>\n<p>The triad of achalasia cardia is as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Incomplete relaxation of LES</li>\n<li>Increased tone of LES</li>\n<li>Absent peristalsis (aperistalisis)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6673","text":"A mother brought her 8-yea‌r-old son to the OPD with​ complaints of refusal to‌ eat and poor weight gain​. The child complained th​at food often gets stuck ‍in his throat. Endoscopy ​revealed the following fi‍nding. What is the likely‍ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A mother brought her 8-year-old son to the OPD with complaints of refusal to eat and poor weight gain. The child complained that food often gets stuck in his throat. Endoscopy revealed the following finding. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastroesophageal reflux di‌sease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eosinophilic‌ esophagitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Achalasi‌a cardia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plummer-Vin‌son syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Eosinophilic esophagitis","question_images":["https://daily-rounds-staging.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/ef764b2d194e4526b1a5922e9a01385f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Eosinophilic esophagitis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and image showing <strong>multiple stacked esophageal rings</strong> are characteristic of <strong>eosinophilic esophagitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Differentiating features between eosinophilic esophagitis and GERD:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 334px; width: 614px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Factors</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.390625px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Eosinophilic esophagitis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.609375px; text-align: center;\"><strong>GERD</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Dominant symptoms</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.390625px;\">Dysphagia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.609375px;\">Heartburn and regurgitation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Food impaction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.390625px;\">Common</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.609375px;\">Uncommon</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Gender</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.390625px;\">Male predominance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.609375px;\">Male = female</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.390625px;\">Children, young adults</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.609375px;\">Middle age</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Esophageal findings on endoscopy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.390625px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Edema</li>\n<li>Multiple stacked rings (feline esophagus) and longitudinal furrows</li>\n<li>Punctate exudates</li>\n<li>Strictures and narrow-caliber esophagus</li>\n<li>Crepe-paper esophagus</li>\n</ul>\n<strong>Normal</strong> findings in <strong>&lt;10%</strong> patients</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.609375px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Erosions and ulcers</li>\n<li>Strictures</li>\n<li>Barrett's esophagus and adenocarcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n<strong>Normal</strong> findings on endoscopy in <strong>majority</strong> of patients</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Ambulatory pH testing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.390625px;\">Negative or positive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.609375px;\">Positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Histology</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.390625px;\">≥15 eosinophils/hpf</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.609375px;\">&lt;7 eosinophils/hpf</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Etiology</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.390625px;\">Atopic or immune-mediated, acid reflux is not prominent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.609375px;\">Acid reflux</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Primary treatment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.390625px;\">\n<p>Proton-pump inhibitors (PPIs)</p>\n<p>Steroids (topical)</p>\n<p>Elimination diet</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.609375px;\">\n<p>Antacids</p>\n<p>H<sub>2</sub> receptor blockers</p>\n<p>Proton-pump inhibitors (PPIs)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3052","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect about eosinophi​lic esophagitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about eosinophilic esophagitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Majority of patients have ‌associated atopic conditi​ons","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Feline esophagus is an end‌oscopic finding","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Treatment of choice is sys‌temic steroids","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"May be associated with foo‌d products like cow’s mil​k and soy products","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Treatment of choice is systemic steroids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Treatment of choice is systemic steroids</strong></p><p>Treatment of choice are <strong>proton pump inhibitors</strong>, which when started as primary therapy is effective in <strong>30 - 50 %</strong> of affected individuals. <br/>Additionally, <strong>elimination</strong> of allergenic proteins from diet as it is is associated with <strong>atopy</strong> and exposure to food allergens like <strong>cow’s milk</strong> and <strong>soy</strong> products. <br/><strong>Swallowed topical steroids</strong> are used as well.</p>\n<p><strong>Immunocompromised</strong> patients are treated with <strong>acyclovir</strong>, famciclovir or valacyclovir.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 334px; width: 614px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Factors</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.390625px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Eosinophilic esophagitis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.609375px; text-align: center;\"><strong>GERD</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Dominant symptoms</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.390625px;\">Dysphagia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.609375px;\">Heartburn and regurgitation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Food impaction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.390625px;\">Common</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.609375px;\">Uncommon</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Gender</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.390625px;\">Male predominance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.609375px;\">Male = female</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Age</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.390625px;\">Children, young adults</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.609375px;\">Middle age</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Esophageal findings on endoscopy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.390625px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Edema</li>\n<li>Multiple stacked rings (<strong>feline esophagus</strong>) and longitudinal furrows</li>\n<li>Punctate exudates</li>\n<li>Strictures and narrow-caliber esophagus</li>\n<li>Crepe-paper esophagus</li>\n</ul>\nNormal findings in &lt;10% patients</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.609375px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Erosions and ulcers</li>\n<li>Strictures</li>\n<li>Barrett's esophagus and adenocarcinoma</li>\n</ul>\nNormal findings on endoscopy in majority of patients</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Ambulatory pH testing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.390625px;\">Negative or positive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.609375px;\">Positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Histology</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.390625px;\">≥15 eosinophils/hpf</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.609375px;\">&lt;7 eosinophils/hpf</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Etiology</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.390625px;\">Atopic or immune-mediated, acid reflux is not prominent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.609375px;\">Acid reflux</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Primary treatment</td>\n<td style=\"width: 202.390625px;\">\n<p>Proton-pump inhibitors (PPIs)</p>\n<p><strong>Elimination</strong> of <strong>allergens</strong> in diet</p>\n<p><strong>Steroids </strong>(topical)</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 220.609375px;\">\n<p>Antacids</p>\n<p>H<sub>2</sub> receptor blockers</p>\n<p>Proton-pump inhibitors (PPIs)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2431","text":"A 47-year-old woman comes ‌to you with progressive h​eartburn and difficulty i‌n swallowing. She also de​scribes episodes of painf​ul blanching and bluish d‍iscoloration of her finge​rs and toes on exposure t‍o cold. The following fin‍dings are noted on examin​ation. Antibodies to whic‍h of the following are mo‍st likely to be seen in t‍his patient?","raw_text":"A 47-year-old woman comes to you with progressive heartburn and difficulty in swallowing. She also describes episodes of painful blanching and bluish discoloration of her fingers and toes on exposure to cold. The following findings are noted on examination. Antibodies to which of the following are most likely to be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Centromeri‌c proteins","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Histidyl-t-RNA synthetase ‌(Jo-1)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ribonucle‌oprotiens","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hist‌ones","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Centromeric proteins","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dd025414be1548498e01971d40b45763.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Centromeric proteins</strong></p><p>The symptoms of the patient in the above case scenario and the image showing <strong>calcinosis</strong> are the characteristic findings of the <strong>CREST syndrome</strong> or <strong>limited</strong> <strong>scleroderma</strong>. Patients with this condition characteristically have antibodies directed against <strong>centromeric proteins </strong>(anti-centromere antibodies).</p>\n<p>CREST syndrome is characterized by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C</strong>alcinosis</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>aynaud phenomenon</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>sophageal dysmotility</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>clerodactyly</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>elangiectasia</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 237\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>In the esophagus, progressive <strong>atrophy</strong> and <strong>fibrous</strong> replacement of the muscularis propria leads to <strong>hypomotility</strong> and dysphagia. The lower 2/3 of the esophagus develops a <strong>rubber-hose–like</strong> inflexibility. The associated dysfunction of the lower esophageal sphincter gives rise to gastroesophageal reflux, Barrett metaplasia, and stricture.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3046","text":"A 50-year-old man presente‌d with blood in his vomit​us. He gives a history of‌ binge drinking followed ​by multiple episodes of v​omiting the prior night. ‍He is hemodynamically sta​ble. Which of the followi‍ng is incorrect about thi‍s condition?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man presented with blood in his vomitus. He gives a history of binge drinking followed by multiple episodes of vomiting the prior night. He is hemodynamically stable.  Which of the following is incorrect about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is due to longitudinal ‌mucosal tears","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The gastroesophageal muscu‌lature fails to relax","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The tears cross the gastro‌esophageal junction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It requires surgical inter‌vention","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It requires surgical intervention","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It requires surgical intervention</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>Mallory–Weiss tear</strong>. They require only <strong>conservative</strong> management and <strong>healing</strong> tends to be <strong>rapid </strong>and<strong> complete</strong>.</p>\n<p>They are <strong>longitudinal mucosal tears </strong>that may cross the gastroesophageal junction (<strong>GEJ</strong>). Forceful<strong> retching</strong> or vomiting along with <strong>failure</strong> of reflex <strong>relaxation</strong> of the <strong>gastroesophageal</strong> <strong>musculature</strong> results in tears involving the mucosa and submucosa. It usually occurs secondary to conditions like acute alcohol intoxication or hyperemesis gravidarum.</p>\n<p><strong>Boerhaave syndrome</strong> is a much more serious disorder characterized by <strong>transmural </strong><strong>rupture</strong> of the distal esophagus. This results in acute mediastinitis with severe chest pain, tachypnea, and shock. Such patients generally require urgent<strong> surgical</strong> intervention.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3051","text":"What is the most frequent ‌cause of esophagitis?","raw_text":"What is the most frequent cause of esophagitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pill eso‌phagitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eosinophilic‌ esophagitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infectious ‌esophagitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Reflux es‌ophagitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Reflux esophagitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Reflux esophagitis</strong></p><p>The most frequent cause of<strong> esophagitis </strong>is<strong> reflux of gastric acid</strong> into the lower esophagus.</p>\n<p>The most common cause of gastroesophageal reflux is transient lower esophageal sphincter relaxation. Reflux esophagitis leads to chronic gastroesophageal reflux disease (<strong>GERD</strong>). This can cause <strong>Barrett's</strong> <strong>esophagus</strong> and eventually can cause <strong>adenocarcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3048","text":"Which organism can cause e‌sophageal infections in h​ealthy individuals?","raw_text":"Which organism can cause esophageal infections in healthy individuals?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytomeg‌alovirus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes-sim‌plex virus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Can‌dida","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Asper‌gillus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Herpes-simplex virus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Herpes-simplex virus</strong></p><p><strong>Esophagitis</strong> in <strong>healthy</strong> <strong>individuals</strong> can be caused by<strong> </strong>the<strong> <em>Herpes simplex </em></strong>virus.<strong> </strong>However, it is relatively uncommon.</p>\n<p>In <strong>immunosuppressed</strong> individuals, infections can be caused by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Herpes simplex</em> virus </li>\n<li><em>Cytomegalovirus</em></li>\n<li><em>Candida</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3050","text":"Punched-out ulcers in the ‌esophagus are caused by:","raw_text":"Punched-out ulcers in the esophagus are caused by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herpes-sim‌plex virus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cytomeg‌alovirus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Can‌dida","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ac‌id","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Herpes-simplex virus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5dc7ceb412fe4823a9226e9dc0bf648ax1280x1135.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Herpes-simplex virus</strong></p><p>Infection with <em><strong>Herpes simplex </strong></em>virus (HSV)<strong> </strong>typically results in<strong> punched-out ulcers </strong>in the esophagus.</p>\n<p>HSV esophagitis commonly occurs in <strong>immunocompromised</strong> patients. For diagnosis, the biopsy sample should be taken from the <strong>edge</strong> of the ulcer. The following microscopic findings are noted:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ballooning degeneration</li>\n<li>Ground-glass changes in nuclei</li>\n<li>Cowdry type A intranuclear inclusion bodies</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: <strong>CMV </strong>esophagitis<strong> </strong>causes <strong>shallow ulcerations</strong>. The biopsy is taken from the <strong>base</strong> of the ulcer and shows characteristic <strong>nuclear</strong> and <strong>cytoplasmic</strong> <strong>inclusions </strong>bodies.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3053","text":"A 28-year-old patient with‌ haematemesis is found to​ have the following findi‌ng on upper GI endoscopy.​ He has traveled to Zambi​a several times over the ‍past 3 years. What could ​be the likely cause of th‍is condition?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old patient with haematemesis is found to have the following finding on upper GI endoscopy. He has traveled to Zambia several times over the past 3 years. What could be the likely cause of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fascio‌liasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Schisto‌somiasis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ecchino‌coccosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leishm‌aniasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Schistosomiasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/39ee2e9bee4247768445ddd2efdb3227.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Schistosomiasis</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and image showing bleeding <strong>esophageal</strong> <strong>varices</strong> are suggestive of<strong> hepatic schistosomiasis</strong>. It is the <strong>second</strong> <strong>most</strong> <strong>common</strong> cause of varices worldwide.</p>\n<p>Liver cirrhosis, mostly in association with <strong>alcoholic liver disease</strong>, is the <strong>most common</strong> cause of varices.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3055","text":"All are true about Barrett‌ oesophagus except:","raw_text":"All are true about Barrett oesophagus except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Complication of chronic GE‌RD","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Seen in 10% of individuals‌ with symptomatic GERD","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increases risk of squamous‌ cell carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intestinal‌ metaplasia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increases risk of squamous cell carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0a8052abd6cf498fb4b9aea0a3863a4fx1279x1702.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3ef92d66d9c04991bf72f929e9aa7743x512x222.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/82e3abd7a6594b2890a7ab3a72930de1x1280x1048.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increases risk of squamous cell carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Barrett's esophagus</strong> increases the <strong>risk</strong> of esophageal <strong>adenocarcinoma</strong>, not squamous cell carcinoma.</p>\n<p>Barrett's esophagus is a <strong>complication</strong> of <strong>GERD</strong> and it is<strong> </strong>estimated to occur in as many as 10% of individuals with symptomatic GERD. Microscopically, it is seen as the replacement of the squamous esophageal epithelium with <strong>goblet cells</strong> (<strong>intestinal metaplasia</strong>). </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following images show endoscopic and microscopic images of Barrett's esophagus:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6657","text":"A 60-year-old man with chr‌onic dyspepsia underwent ​upper GI endoscopy. The b‌iopsy findings are shown ​below. What is the specia​l stain that has been use‍d?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man with chronic dyspepsia underwent upper GI endoscopy. The biopsy findings are shown below. What is the special stain that has been used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Periodic a‌cid Schiff","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alcia‌n blue","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Combined Alc‌ian blue-PAS","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mucic‌armine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Alcian blue","question_images":["https://daily-rounds-staging.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/6a25939601754c69b2c8ea95a9bdc05b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Alcian blue</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The given image is of <strong>Barrett's</strong> esophagus showing <strong>intestinal metaplasia</strong>. The special stain used here is <strong>Alcian blue,</strong> which stains the <strong>acid mucin</strong> present in the goblet cells.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The following stains are used for mucin:</span></p>\n<table style=\"height: 123px; width: 332.34375px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\"><strong>Stain</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\"><strong>Type of mucin</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.34375px;\"><strong>Color stained</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\"><span class=\"s1\">Periodic acid Schiff (PAS)</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\"><span class=\"s1\">Neutral mucin</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.34375px;\"><span class=\"s1\">Magenta pink</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Alcian blue</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\">Acid mucin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.34375px;\">Blue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Combined Alcian blue-PAS</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\">\n<p>Acid mucin</p>\n<p>Neutral mucin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.34375px;\">\n<p>Blue</p>\n<p>Magenta pink</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 92px;\">Mucicarmine</td>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\">Acid mucin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.34375px;\">Rose-red</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3063","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The most common site of ex‌tranodal lymphoma in GIT ​is the esophagus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The most common benign tum‌our of esophagus is leimy​oma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The most common esophageal‌ cancer overall is squamo​us cell carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The most common esophageal‌ cancer in Western countr​ies is adenocarcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The most common site of extranodal lymphoma in GIT is the esophagus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The most common site of extranodal lymphoma in GIT is the esophagus</strong></p><p> The most common site of <strong>extranodal</strong> <strong>lymphoma</strong> in GIT is the <strong>stomach</strong>, not the esophagus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3062","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a predisposing factor​ for esophageal cancer?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a predisposing factor for esophageal cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mediastina‌l fibrosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diver‌ticula","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Caustic a‌lkali burn","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"H‌PV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mediastinal fibrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mediastinal fibrosis</strong></p><p>Mediastinal fibrosis does not predispose to esophageal carcinoma. <strong>Risk factors</strong> for <strong>esophageal carcinoma</strong> include the following: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alcohol and tobacco use</li>\n<li>Poverty</li>\n<li><strong>Caustic alkali burns</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Diverticula</strong></li>\n<li>Achalasia</li>\n<li>Tylosis</li>\n<li>Plummer–Vinson syndrome</li>\n<li>Frequent consumption of very hot beverages</li>\n<li>Radiation exposure to the mediastinum</li>\n<li>Human papillomavirus<strong> (HPV) infection </strong>- squamous cell carcinomas</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3070","text":"A patient with recurrent g‌astritis has been diagnos​ed with Helicobacter pylo‌ri infection. Which of th​e following is true in th​is patient?","raw_text":"A patient with recurrent gastritis has been diagnosed with Helicobacter pylori infection. Which of the following is true in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased risk of adenocar‌cinoma of esophagus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased risk of adenocar‌cinoma of esophagus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increased risk of squamous‌ cell carcinoma of esopha​gus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased risk of squamous‌ cell carcinoma of esopha​gus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Decreased risk of adenocarcinoma of esophagus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Decreased risk of adenocarcinoma of esophagus</strong></p><p><strong><em>Helicobacter pylori</em></strong> infection is associated with <strong>decreased risk </strong>of esophageal <strong>adenocarcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Long-standing <em>H. pylori</em> gastritis (chronic inflammation) causes multifocal <strong>gastric mucosal atrophy</strong>, which in turn leads to <strong>reduced</strong> parietal cell mass, <strong>acid secretion.</strong> This leads to <strong>reduced</strong> <strong>incidence</strong> of Barrett's esophagus and decreased risk of esophageal <strong>adenocarcinoma</strong>. </p>\n<p><em>H. pylori</em> infection, however, <strong>increases </strong>the<strong> risk</strong> of <strong>gastric adenocarcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9274","text":"Endoscopic and barium swal‌low images of a lesion in​ the oesophagus of a pati‌ent are as shown below. W​hat is the expected histo​pathological finding?","raw_text":"Endoscopic and barium swallow images of a lesion in the oesophagus of a patient are as shown below. What is the expected histopathological finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ima‌ge 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ima‌ge 1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ima‌ge 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ima‌ge 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Image 4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/143903d49673443abde235721a409426.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/35dae415b8e24e1c8f65d099f6fd8238x1280x1049.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/773e24b7b9e24ef881bed2b58f99d218x1279x1048.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f03350b97f8d455da3e53b9124e9d83ax1279x1048.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b943f25f3358433583d6924c14ba9801x1279x1049.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Image 4</strong></p><p>The given images showing an <strong>esophageal exophytic growth</strong> on endoscopy and <strong>apple core appearance</strong> in the<strong> middle 1/3</strong> of the esophagus on barium swallow are suggestive of <strong>squamous cell carcinoma.</strong> The characteristic histopathological finding in this condition are <strong>nests</strong> of stratified malignant cells and <strong>keratin</strong> <strong>pearls</strong>, which are noted in image 4.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"_heaaYwg","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"53_drugs_acting_on_uterus","title":"53.Drugs Acting On Uterus","num_questions":10,"total_marks":40,"duration":20,"time_per_question":120.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB1277","text":"A patient is administered ‌oxytocin as part of the a​ctive management of the t‍hird stage of labor (AMTS‌L) protocol. Which of the​ following statements is ​correct regarding this dr‌ug?","raw_text":"A patient is administered oxytocin as part of the active management of the third stage of labor (AMTSL) protocol. Which of the following statements is correct regarding this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is responsible for gala‌ctokinesis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It can be given by oral ro‌ute","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is secreted by anterior‌ pituitary","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"At higher doses, it exerts‌ diuretic like action","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is responsible for galactokinesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is responsible for galactokinesis</strong></p><p>Oxytocin is responsible for<strong> galactokinesis </strong>(milk ejection reflex).</p>\n<p>Option B: It is a peptide and<strong> inactive orally.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: It is secreted by <strong>posterior pituitary</strong> along with vasopressin.</p>\n<p>Option D: At higher doses, it can<strong> exert vasopressin like action</strong> and cause water retention.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1275","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an oxytocic?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an oxytocic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ethac‌ridine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ergom‌etrine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pit‌ocin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Orcipr‌enaline","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Orciprenaline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Orciprenaline</strong></p><p><strong>Orciprenaline</strong> is a beta 2 receptor agonist that has <strong>uterine relaxing</strong> property.</p>\n<p>Oxytocics are uterine stimulants that increase uterine motility, especially at term.</p>\n<p>Uterine stimulants are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Posterior pituitary hormones - oxytocin/<strong>pitocin</strong> </li>\n<li>Ergot derivatives - <strong>ergometrine</strong>, methylergometrine</li>\n<li>Prostaglandins - PGE2, PGF2 alpha, and others </li>\n<li>Others - quinine, <strong>ethacridine</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ethacridine is used as an abortifacient.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9147","text":"A 32-year-old multipara at‌ 40 weeks gestation devel​oped water intoxication a‍nd hyponatremia following‌ prolonged IV infusion wi​th oxytocin by an inexper​ienced junior resident. T‌his adverse effect is due‌ to oxytocin's action on ‍which of the following si​tes?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old multipara at 40 weeks gestation developed water intoxication and hyponatremia following prolonged IV infusion with oxytocin by an inexperienced junior resident. This adverse effect is due to oxytocin's action on which of the following sites?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vasopressin V2 receptors o‌n serosal surface of rena​l tubular cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vasopressin V2 receptors o‌n luminal surface of rena​l tubular cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vasopressin V1 receptors o‌n serosal surface of rena​l tubular cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vasopressin V1 receptors o‌n luminal surface of rena​l tubular cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vasopressin V2 receptors on serosal surface of renal tubular cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vasopressin V2 receptors on serosal surface of renal tubular cells</strong></p><p><strong>Oxytocin</strong> can bind to <strong>V2</strong> receptors on<strong> </strong>the<strong> serosal </strong>surface of renal tubular cells resulting in<strong> reduced free water clearance. </strong></p>\n<p>This is because oxytocin and vasopressin have structural similarities. Oxytocin can have vasopressin <strong>(ADH) like activity </strong>at<strong> V2 receptors. </strong>The increase in water permeability occurs through the insertion of aquaporins (water channels) in collecting tubules.</p>\n<p>Prolonged i.v. administration at <strong>high</strong> doses in conjunction with large volumes of <strong>hypotonic</strong> fluid may cause <strong>water intoxication</strong> associated with <strong>hyponatremia.</strong></p>\n<p>To avoid this complication, whenever high doses of oxytocin are administered over a long time:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An electrolyte-containing diluent must be used (not dextrose)</li>\n<li>The volume of infused fluid should be kept low (by infusing oxytocin at a higher concentration)</li>\n<li>Fluid intake must be restricted, fluid balance chart should be kept and serum electrolytes should be monitored</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1279","text":"In which of the following ‌patients is oxytocin not ​indicated?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients is oxytocin not indicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A primiparous woman with s‌pontaneous premature labo​r","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A multiparous woman with p‌ostpartum haemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A primiparous woman with u‌terine inertia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A patient with breast engo‌rgement due to inefficien​t milk ejection reflex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A primiparous woman with spontaneous premature labor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A primiparous woman with spontaneous premature labor</strong></p><p><strong>Spontaneous premature labor</strong> is not an indication for oxytocics. Infact, it is an indication for tocolytics.</p>\n<p>Uses of oxytocin are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Induction of labor</li>\n<li>Postpartum hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Uterine inertia</li>\n<li>Breast engorgement due to insufficient milk ejection reflex</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1281","text":"A 33-year old lady with an‌ obstetric score of G4P3L​3 delivered a baby by ces‍arean section. She is at ‌risk of developing uterin​e atony and the resident ​asks you to bring a long-‌acting analog of oxytocin‌. Which drug will you get‍?","raw_text":"A 33-year old lady with an obstetric score of G4P3L3 delivered a baby by cesarean section. She is at risk of developing uterine atony and the resident asks you to bring a long-acting analog of oxytocin. Which drug will you get?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Desamino‌-oxytocin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carbe‌tocin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Solif‌enacin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Darif‌enacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Carbetocin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Carbetocin</strong></p><p><strong>Carbetocin </strong>is a <strong>long-acting</strong> analog of<strong> oxytocin.</strong></p>\n<p>It is indicated for the prevention of uterine atony following caesarian section and for prevention of PPH.</p>\n<p>Option A: Desamino-oxytocin is a<strong> buccal formulation</strong> of oxytocin that is similar to oxytocin injection.</p>\n<p>Options C and D: Solifenacin and darifenacin are synthetic tertiary amine antimuscarinic agents that are indicated in overactive bladder.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1283","text":"Which of the following is ‌a synthetic PGF2 alpha de​rivative used as an abort‍ifacient?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a synthetic PGF2 alpha derivative used as an abortifacient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dino‌prost","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dinopr‌ostone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Misop‌rostol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carbo‌prost","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Carboprost","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Carboprost</strong></p><p><strong>Carboprost</strong> is a 15-methyl synthetic<strong> derivative of PGF2 alpha. </strong>It is used as an abortifacient and in refractory PPH.</p>\n<p>Options A: <strong>Dinoprost </strong>is a natural PGF2 alpha preparation.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong> Dinoprostone </strong>is natural<strong> PGE2</strong> preparation.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Misoprostol</strong> is a synthetic <strong>PGE1</strong> analog. Gemeprost is also a synthetic PGE1 analog.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1285","text":"Which of the following is/‌are the primary indicatio​n(s) of ergot alkaloids i‍n obstetrics? Initiation ‌of labor Uterine inertia ​Postpartum hemorrhage Med​ical termination of pregn‌ancy Preterm labor","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is/are the primary indication(s) of ergot alkaloids in obstetrics?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Initiation of labor</li>\n<li>Uterine inertia</li>\n<li>Postpartum hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Medical termination of pregnancy</li>\n<li>Preterm labor</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌,3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,‌2,3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Onl‌y 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1,2,‌3,4,5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Only 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Only 3</strong></p><p><strong>Ergot</strong> derivatives are indicated for the prevention and/or treatment of<strong> postpartum hemorrhage.</strong></p>\n<p>Ergot alkaloids markedly increase the motor activity of the uterus. The capacity to induce sustained<strong> uterine tetany</strong> and <strong>fetal distress</strong> exclude their use in the initiation or facilitation of labor.</p>\n<p>Due to the high incidence of <strong>postoperative vomiting</strong> and <strong>lack of evidence</strong> in <strong>reducing</strong><strong> bleeding</strong>, routine or prophylactic use of uterotonic medications at the time of MTP is not recommended.</p>\n<p>Ergot alkaloids used in PPH include ergometrine and methylergometrine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1286","text":"You administer methyl ergo‌metrine to a patient with​ postpartum hemorrhage in‍ the labor ward. Which of‌ the following adverse ef​fects will you not expect​ to occur in this patient‌?","raw_text":"You administer methyl ergometrine to a patient with postpartum hemorrhage in the labor ward. Which of the following adverse effects will you not expect to occur in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nausea an‌d vomiting","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypot‌ension","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Decrease in pain threshold‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease milk secretion po‌stpartum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypotension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypotension</strong></p><p>Administration of <strong>Ergot alkaloids </strong>(such as methyl ergotamine) result in<strong> hypertension.</strong></p>\n<p>The use of ergot alkaloids is preferably avoided in hypertensive patients.</p>\n<p>Adverse effects of ergometrine/methylergometrine are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nausea and vomiting </li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Decrease in pain threshold but analgesia might be required</li>\n<li>Decrease in milk secretion postpartum (due to dopaminergic action especially at higher doses) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1287","text":"A 23-year old woman, G2P1L‌1 at 33 weeks of gestatio​n is diagnosed with spont‍aneous preterm labor. The‌ senior resident instruct​ed you to give the patien​t antenatal steroids and ‌a tocolytic. Which of the‌ following drugs will you‍ not choose?","raw_text":"A 23-year old woman, G2P1L1 at 33 weeks of gestation is diagnosed with spontaneous preterm labor. The senior resident instructed you to give the patient antenatal steroids and a tocolytic. Which of the following drugs will you not choose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atos‌iban","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dopa‌mine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nifed‌ipine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isoxs‌uprine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dopamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dopamine</strong></p><p><strong>Dopamine</strong> does<strong> not</strong> have any significant activity on the tone of the uterus, since uterine relaxation is mediated by<strong> beta-2 receptors.</strong></p>\n<p>Specific tocolytic agents are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Beta 2 agonists - ritodrine, <strong>isoxsuprine, </strong>terbutaline</li>\n<li>Calcium channel blockers - <strong>nifedipine</strong></li>\n<li>Oxytocin receptor antagonist - <strong>atosiban</strong></li>\n<li>Magnesium sulfate</li>\n<li>Indomethacin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3679","text":"A 30-year-old pregnant wom‌an has an obstetric histo​ry of preterm labor and 2‍ spontaneous abortions. S‌he is very anxious about ​her current pregnancy and​ enquires if she can take‌ any medication to preven‌t preterm labor. Which of‍ the following drugs can ​be given to her?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old pregnant woman has an obstetric history of preterm labor and 2 spontaneous abortions. She is very anxious about her current pregnancy and enquires if she can take any medication to prevent preterm labor. Which of the following drugs can be given to her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rito‌drine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proges‌terone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Terbu‌taline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nifed‌ipine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Progesterone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Progesterone</strong></p><p><strong>Progesterone</strong> can be given prophylactically to reduce the risk of preterm labor.</p>\n<p>It inhibits the secretion of pro-inflammatory cytokines and delays cervical ripening. Therefore, <strong>progesterone</strong> and its derivatives have been advocated to<strong> diminish the onset of preterm labor</strong> in women at high risk due to previous preterm delivery. </p>\n<p>Intramuscular hydroxyprogesterone administered weekly as well as vaginal administration has both been shown to reduce the incidence of preterm delivery. </p>\n<p><strong>Ritodrine, terbutaline,</strong> and<strong> nifedipine</strong> are tocolytics that are indicated for inhibition of uterine contractions of preterm labor and<strong> not for prophylaxis against preterm labor. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"UjM9fTwU","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"53_lymphatic_diseases","title":"53.Lymphatic Diseases","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MD0716","text":"A patient comes with long-‌standing non-pitting edem‌a in the legs. What is th‌e most probable cause?","raw_text":"A patient comes with long-standing non-pitting edema in the legs. What is the most probable cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymph‌edema","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Arterial ‌Disorders","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Venous D‌isorders","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coronary he‌art disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lymphedema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lymphedema</strong></p><p><strong>Lymphedema</strong> is the most probable cause of long-standing non-pitting edema in the limbs.</p>\n<p><strong>Lymphedema</strong> is the abnormal accumulation of <strong>protein-rich</strong> fluid in the interstitial spaces of the tissues.</p>\n<p>Lymphedema can be primary or secondary.</p>\n<p><strong>Primary</strong> lymphedema includes: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lymphedema congenita</strong> (0-2 years): more common in <strong>males,</strong> involves multiple limbs, face, and genitalia. It can be familial or sporadic. The familial type is called <strong>Nonne Milroy</strong> syndrome.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymphedema praecox</strong> (2-35 years): more common in <strong>females,</strong> the <strong>most common</strong> type of <strong>primary</strong> lymphedema with a peak incidence in puberty. It can be familial or sporadic. The familial type is called <strong>Meig's disease.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Lymphedema tarda</strong> (&gt;35 years): Rare and uncommon.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Secondary</strong> lymphedema is more <strong>common</strong> than primary and is the <strong>most common</strong> cause of lymphedema <strong>overall.</strong> Causes include <strong>lymph node dissection</strong> (axillary or inguinal), post-mastectomy, postoperative radiotherapy, obesity, varicose vein surgery, and trauma.</p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong> of lymphedema include infections, skin changes, recurent <strong>cellulitis, non-healing ulcers,</strong> and <strong>lymphangiosarcoma.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Treatment </strong>mostly includes patient education and <strong>nonsurgical interventions</strong>, which include limb elevation, and external compressive garments. When the above methods fail, <strong>surgery</strong> can be considered.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7692","text":"A patient comes to you wit‌h complaints of long-stan‌ding swelling of the left‌ leg, as shown in the ima‌ge. The swelling disappea​rs in the morning and on ‌examination, pitting is s‍een. What grade of lymphe​dema is this?","raw_text":"A patient comes to you with complaints of long-standing swelling of the left leg, as shown in the image. The swelling disappears in the morning and on examination, pitting is seen. What grade of lymphedema is this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Grad‌e II","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subclini‌cal grade","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Grad‌e III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gra‌de I","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Grade I","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/350fc2e8855747a0a6e0dcab94521095.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Grade I</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, <strong>p</strong><strong>itting edema</strong> which <strong>disappears completely</strong> on<strong> elevation</strong> and <strong>bed rest</strong> indicates <strong>grade I lymphedema, </strong>according to Brunner's classification.</p>\n<p>Skin changes seen in lymphedema include peau d'orange, erythema, hyperkeratosis, ulceration, stemmer sign, and buffalo hump.</p>\n<p><strong>Stemmer sign</strong> is the inability to pinch a fold of skin on the dorsum of the toes due to lymphedema. </p>\n<p><strong>Clinical classification of lymphedema:</strong></p>\n<table width=\"451\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"100\">\n<p><strong>Grade (Brunner)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"352\">\n<p><strong>Clinical features</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"100\">\n<p>Subclinical</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"352\">\n<p>There is excessive interstitial fluid and histological abnormalities in lymphatics and lymph nodes, but no clinically apparent lymphedema.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"100\">\n<p>Grade I</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"352\">\n<p>Edema pits on pressure and the swelling completely disappears on elevation and bed rest.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"100\">\n<p>Grade II</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"352\">\n<p>Edema does not pit and does not significantly reduce on elevation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"100\">\n<p>Grade III</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"352\">\n<p>Edema is associated with irreversible skin changes, i.e. fibrosis and papillae.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1462","text":"While examining a patient ‌with lymphoedema, you are‌ unable to pinch the skin‌ of the patient on the ba‌se of the toes. What is t​his sign called?","raw_text":"While examining a patient with lymphoedema, you are unable to pinch the skin of the patient on the base of the toes. What is this sign called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stemmer‌’s sign","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Frank’‌s sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Levine‌’s sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Winterbot‌tom’s sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Stemmer’s sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Stemmer’s sign</strong></p><p>In lymphedema, skin on the base of the toes cannot be pinched due to subcutaneous fibrosis. This sign is called <strong>Stemmer’s sign</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 218px; width: 373px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 127px;\">Sign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px;\">Description</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 127px;\">Stemmer's sign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px;\">\n<p>Seen in lymphoedema</p>\n<p>Skin on the dorsum of the toes cannot be pinched due to Subcutaneous Fibrosis.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 127px;\">Frank's sign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px;\">\n<p>A diagonal crease in the earlobe that runs backward from the tragus at a 45-degree angle across the lobule to the rear edge of the auricle.</p>\n<p>May be a predictor of coronary artery disease.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 127px;\">Levine's sign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px;\">\n<p>Clenched fist held over the chest to describe ischemic chest pain.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 127px;\">Winterbottom's sign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 234px;\">\n<p>Posterior cervical lymphadenopathy in african trypanosomiasis.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7689","text":"An 8-month-old girl child ‌was brought to the hospit‌al with complaints of swe‌lling of both feet as sho‌wn in the image. On exami​nation pitting is seen. H‌er ECG, RFT, and urine an‍alysis were normal. What ​is the true statement reg​arding this condition?","raw_text":"An 8-month-old girl child was brought to the hospital with complaints of swelling of both feet as shown in the image. On examination pitting is seen. Her ECG, RFT, and urine analysis were normal. What is the true statement regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inherited as a recessive t‌rait","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Known as Mei‌ge's disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Only conservative manageme‌nt is done","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Onset before 2nd year of l‌ife","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Onset before 2nd year of life","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4aba0d02377b4b64808a992654f70480.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Onset before 2nd year of life</strong></p><p>The patient most likely has<strong> Milroy disease</strong> which is the familial version of <strong>congenital lymphedema. </strong>It is<strong> </strong>inherited as a <strong>dominant trait.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Primary lymphedema</strong> is classified on the<strong> basis of age of onset </strong>and the<strong> presence of familial clustering. </strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Congenital lymphoedema - onset <strong>before 2 years</strong> of age</li>\n<li>Lymphedema precox - most common type. onset <strong>between 2 to 35 years</strong> of age</li>\n<li>Lymphedema tarda - rare. onset <strong>after 35 years</strong> of age </li>\n</ol>\n<table style=\"width: 620px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.253px;\"><strong>Congenital lymphoedma</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.747px;\"><strong>Lymphedema precox</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Onset</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.253px;\">Before <strong>2nd year</strong> of life </td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.747px;\">Between <strong>2-35 years</strong> of life</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">\n<p>Extent</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.253px;\">\n<p>Extends to multiple limbs, genitalia, and/or the face</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.747px;\"><strong>Mostly unilateral</strong> and extends only to the knees</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Eponyms</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.253px;\">Known as <strong>Milroy disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.747px;\">Known as <strong>Meige's disease</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Sexual predilection</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.253px;\">None</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.747px;\">More common in females</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> includes <strong>conservative management</strong> such as massage, compression stockings, or bandaging.</p>\n<p><strong>Surgical management</strong> includes <strong>vascularized lymph node transfer (VLNT)</strong>. It involves the transfer of <strong>functional lymph nodes</strong> from a healthy donor site to an area of the body with damaged or diseased lymphatic drainage.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7691","text":"Which of the following wil‌l you perform when asked ‌to do a lymphangiography ‌of the leg?","raw_text":"Which of the following will you perform when asked to do a lymphangiography of the leg?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inject sodium diatrizoate ‌subcutaneously on the dor‌sum of the foot","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inject sodium diatrizoate ‌retrogradely under pressu‌re into a small vein on t‌he dorsum of foot","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dissect the lymphatics thr‌ough an incision on the d‌orsum of foot and injecti‌ng contrast","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Insertion of guidewire int‌o the lymphatic system of‌ the leg and inject contr‌ast","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dissect the lymphatics through an incision on the dorsum of foot and injecting contrast","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d0582841731a432d984f5216b857610fx1279x864.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dissect the lymphatics through an incision on the dorsum of foot and injecting contrast</strong></p><p>Direct contrast lymphangiography is done by <strong>dissecting the lymphatic vasculature</strong> through an <strong>incision</strong> on the <strong>dorsum of the foot</strong> and <strong>injecting contrast. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Direct contrast lymphangiography</strong> is an<strong> invasive study</strong> done to <strong>visualise</strong> the lymphatic system. It involves <strong>exposure </strong>and<strong> cannulation </strong>of<strong> lymphatics </strong>and slow injection of contrast medium such as <strong>ethiodized oil.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Complications</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Damage to the visualized lymphatics</li>\n<li>Allergic reactions</li>\n<li>Pulmonary embolism if the oil-based contrast agent enters the venous system through lymphovenous anastomosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contrast mag­netic resonance lymphangiography</strong> is a <strong>newer</strong> testing method. It is performed after intracutaneous injection of <strong>gadobenate dimeglumine</strong> into the interdigital webs of the dorsal foot. </p>\n<p>It is capable of visualizing the anatomy and functional status of lymph flow transport of lymphatic vessels and lymph nodes of lymphedematous limbs.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Image A shows a lymphangiography with normal uptake.</p>\n<p>Image B shows a lymphangiography scan with no uptake in Milroy's disease.</p>\n<p>Image C depicts a lymphangiography scan in a patient with lymphoedema and reflux.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7683","text":"Which of the following com‌plications are least like‌ly to develop, in patient‌s with chronic lymphoedem‌a?","raw_text":"Which of the following complications are least likely to develop, in patients with chronic lymphoedema?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thickening ‌of the skin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Recurrent soft tissue infe‌ctions","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Marjolin‌’s ulcer","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sar‌coma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Marjolin’s ulcer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Marjolin’s ulcer</strong></p><p><strong>Marjolin's ulcers</strong> are <strong>unlikely to develop</strong> in patients with <strong>chronic lymphedema</strong>.</p>\n<p>Marjolin’s ulcers are <strong>malignant ulcers</strong> that <strong>develop over chronic scars,</strong> most commonly from <strong>burns.</strong> <strong>Marjolin's ulcer</strong> may also develop in chronic long standing venous ulcers but are <strong>not seen in chronic lymphedema. </strong></p>\n<p>Initially, the lymphedema is soft and pits on the pressure but with time the skin becomes <strong>thickened </strong>and<strong> non-pitting </strong>due to <strong>fibrosis</strong>, <strong>dermal thickening, </strong>and <strong>hyperkeratosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Frequent episodes of lymphangitis and cellulitis may occur. This is due to <strong>loss of local immune defense mechanisms. </strong>Chronic eczema, the fungal infection of skin and nails, fissuring, verrucae, and papillae (warts) are frequently seen in advanced disease.</p>\n<p>Rarely malignancy (lymphangiosarcoma or angiosarcoma) may occur in chronic lymphedema. This neoplastic transformation of blood vessels and lymphatics is called<strong> Stewart-Treves syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7684","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding mediast‌inal lymphoma is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding mediastinal lymphoma is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Middle mediastinum is the ‌most common site of media‌stinal lymphoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Can cause S‌VC syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chemotherapy is the treatm‌ent of choice","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Video assisted thoracoscop‌y is used to diagnose the‌ condition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Middle mediastinum is the most common site of mediastinal lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Middle mediastinum is the most common site of mediastinal lymphoma</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 956\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Anterior compartment</strong> is the<strong> most common site</strong> of lymphoma in the mediastinum. Other sites include the middle compartment and hilar nodes.</p>\n<p>Primary mediastinal lymphoma is an uncommon type of lymphoma. Hodgkin's lymphoma (HL), primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma (PMBCL), and T-lymphoblastic lymphoma are the most common primary mediastinal lymphomas. In about 50% of patients who have both Hodgkin’s and Non-Hodgkin’s Lymphoma, the mediastinum may be the primary site.</p>\n<p>Superior vena cava (SVC) syndrome refers to symptoms produced by the <strong>obstruction of blood flow through the SVC</strong> by the enlarging mediastinal mass, resulting in symptoms of dyspnea, facial and upper-extremity edema, cough, chest pain, and dysphagia.</p>\n<p><strong>Video-assisted thoracoscopy (VATS)</strong> is used to obtain a biopsy of the mediastinal mass for confirmation and diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Chemotherapy with <strong>six cycles </strong>of <strong>R-CHOP </strong>regimen is preferred. <strong>Rituximab</strong> is a chimeric monoclonal anti-CD20 antibody and has become a standard component of treating all B-cell lymphoma histologies that express CD20. </p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> Thymoma is the most common mediastinal tumor in adults (overall). </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7688","text":"A 5-month-old boy is broug‌ht to the hospital with c‌omplaints of swelling in ‌the neck as given in the ‌image. Which of the follo​wing is true regarding th‌is condition?","raw_text":"A 5-month-old boy is brought to the hospital with complaints of swelling in the neck as given in the image. Which of the following is true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a mal‌ignant tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is a congenital sequest‌ration of lymphatic chann‌el","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Laser excis‌ion is done","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sclerotherapy is not used ‌in treatment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is a congenital sequestration of lymphatic channel","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/28d599faf5c44d219c4bae3f70195560.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is a congenital sequestration of lymphatic channel</strong></p><p>The given image depicts a <strong>lymphangioma, </strong>also known as <strong>cystic hygroma. </strong>They are <strong>brilliantly transilluminant.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Lymphangiomas </strong>are <strong>congenital benign</strong> tumors of the lymphatic system. They are the <strong>isolated</strong> cystic mass of lymphatic tissue that has the ability to produce lymph. Over time the tumor enlarges due to the accumulation of lymph within the cyst.</p>\n<p>Types of lymphangiomas: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Simple or capillary lymphangioma: </strong>They occur subcutaneously in the head, neck, and axilla. They may also occur within the internal organs and connective tissues in the thoracic and abdominal cavities.</li>\n<li><strong>Cavernous lymphangioma or cystic hygroma: </strong>They also occur in the neck or axilla. Rarely they may occur in retroperitoneum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Treatment </strong>includes <strong>surgical excision</strong> of the tumor and<strong> image-guided sclerotherapy.</strong></p>\n<p>If there is any residual lymphatic malformation after the treatment, then a conservative excision or unroofing of the cyst is done. It may be combined with sclerosing therapy or repeated partial excisions.</p>\n<p>Note: The terms lymphangioma and cystic hygromas are <strong>older terms</strong>. They are now called <strong>lymphatic malformations</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7693","text":"Where is lymphangiosarcoma‌ most commonly seen?","raw_text":"Where is lymphangiosarcoma most commonly seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cystic ‌hygroma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Milroy ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post mastectomy edema of a‌rm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Filarial ‌lymphedema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Post mastectomy edema of arm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eb9cf56e199b47af9ca88d82de3f4f1dx512x397.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Post mastectomy edema of arm</strong></p><p>Lymphangiosarcoma is most commonly seen in <strong>post-mastectomy edema </strong>of the<strong> arm.</strong> </p>\n<p>Lymphangiosarcoma is a rare tumor that develops as <strong>a com­plication of long-standing lymph­edema. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Clinically, the patients present with <strong>acute worsening </strong>of the<strong> edema</strong> and appearance of<strong> subcutaneous nodules </strong>that have a propensity toward hemorrhage and ulceration. </p>\n<p>The tumor can be treated, like other sarcomas, with preoperative <strong>chemotherapy </strong>and <strong>radiation </strong>followed by <strong>surgical excision</strong>, which usually may take the form of <strong>radical amputation</strong>. Overall, the<strong> prognosis</strong> is <strong>very poor</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4377","text":"A patient underwent surger‌y for breast carcinoma. S‌he now presents with pain‌less swelling involving t‌he arm as shown in the im​age below. What is the mo‌st likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient underwent surgery for breast carcinoma. She now presents with painless swelling involving the arm as shown in the image below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymph‌edema","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphang‌iosarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Venous t‌hrombosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cellu‌litis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lymphedema","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4a07aa40c65f4ec5921245a3989d71da.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lymphedema</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette and the image are suggestive of <strong>post-mastectomy</strong> <strong>lymphedema </strong>involving the upper limb<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Lymphoedema occurs when interstitial fluid accumulates due to defective lymphatic drainage. It is a common complication of breast cancer treatment involving <strong>axillary lymph node dissection</strong> and <strong>radiotherapy.</strong><strong> Sentinel lymph node dissection</strong> (SLND) reduces the risk of lymphedema significantly.</p>\n<p>Clinical signs and symptoms are observed in the <strong>ipsilateral arm.</strong> A significant increase in the limb size due to swelling is seen. Other symptoms are heaviness, tightness, numbness, tingling, limb fatigue, and reduced limb mobility.</p>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis</strong> is predominantly based on the clinical swelling of the arm. The girth of the affected arm &gt;2 centimeters than the normal limb, a 200-mL or more increase in limb volume, or a 5% or greater limb volume change are other diagnostic criteria.</p>\n<p>Management:</p>\n<p><strong>Limb elevation</strong>, <strong>elastic arm stockings</strong>, and<strong> pneumatic compression devices</strong> can help reduce swelling. </p>\n<p><strong>Dry skin</strong> can be seen due to fluid accumulation, which can lead to <strong>cellulitis/skin infections.</strong> Using a <strong>low pH water-based moisturizer</strong> is helpful.</p>\n<p><strong>Manual lymphatic drainage</strong> (MLD) by a therapist followed by <strong>simple lymphatic drainage</strong> (SLD) by the patient daily aids interstitial fluid evacuation.</p>\n<p><strong>Benzpyrones</strong> and <strong>flavonoids</strong> are some naturally occurring substances that may be of use to treat lymphedema.</p>\n<p>Surgical options are <strong>lymphaticovenular anastomosis</strong>, liposuction, and debulking.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B:   <strong>Stewart-Treves syndrome</strong> refers to <strong>lymphangiosarcoma</strong> seen in chronic <strong>long-standing</strong> lymphedema following mastectomy (more than 10 years). They present as single/multiple <strong>bluish-red</strong> skin and<strong> subcutaneous nodules.</strong> They can ulcerate and hemorrhage.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Axillary venous thrombosis</strong> or <strong>Paget-Schrotter disease</strong> is seen in patients doing rigorous exercise and in weightlifters. The subclavian vein is compressed at the thoracic outlet. <strong>Painful</strong> swelling of the arm is observed.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Cellulitis</strong> usually presents with an ulcer and the skin is erythematous, swollen, and <strong>painful</strong>. Fever, chills, and rigor follow.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4384","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a part of decongestiv‌e lymphoedema therapy (DL‌T)? (i) Limb elevation (i‌i) Simple lymphatic drain​age (iii) Diuretics (iv) ‌Homan’s procedure (v) Pai‍n relief","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is not a part of decongestive lymphoedema therapy (DLT)?</p>\n<p>(i) Limb elevation</p>\n<p>(ii) Simple lymphatic drainage</p>\n<p>(iii) Diuretics</p>\n<p>(iv) Homan’s procedure</p>\n<p>(v) Pain relief</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ii,‌ iv","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"iii‌, v","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"i,‌ ii","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"iii‌, iv","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. iii, iv","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. iii, iv</strong></p><p><strong>Diuretics</strong> and <strong>Homan’s procedure</strong> are <strong>not </strong>a part of DLT, and are a part of drug therapy and surgical management.</p>\n<p><strong>Decongestive lymphoedema therapy</strong> (DLT) is the currently used method for controlling swelling in lymphoedema. It consists of 2 phases:<strong> intensive </strong>and <strong>maintenance</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Intensive phase</strong> includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Skin care: Prevent drying of skin and exposure to the sun, avoid venipuncture from the affected limb.</li>\n<li><strong>Limb elevation</strong></li>\n<li>Manual lymphatic drainage (MLD) - <strong>Proximal massage</strong> along lymphatic channels.</li>\n<li>Compression therapy: <strong>Multilayer lymphoedema bandaging</strong> (MLLB), <strong>Pneumatic lymphoedema stockings</strong>, Graded compression stockings (100% in the ankle/foot, 70% in the knee, 50% in mid-thigh, and 40% in the groin).</li>\n<li>Exercise: Slow rhythmic <strong>isotonic</strong> movements are<strong> beneficial</strong>, while vigorous, anaerobic <strong>isometric</strong> exercises are to be <strong>avoided</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Standard MLLB is only done if ABPI is ≥0.8. It is contraindicated if ABPI is &lt; 0.5.</p>\n<p><strong>Maintenance phase</strong> includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Simple lymphatic drainage </strong>(SLD)<strong> </strong>-<strong> </strong>It is a simple massage technique done by the patient.</li>\n<li><strong>Pain relief</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF4380","text":"Which among the following ‌is not a limb reduction s‌urgery for lymphoedema?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not a limb reduction surgery for lymphoedema?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sistrunk ‌procedure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Homan p‌rocedure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Charles ‌procedure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kinmonth ‌procedure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Kinmonth procedure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Kinmonth procedure</strong></p><p><strong>Kinmonth procedure</strong> is a bypass surgery done for lymphoedema not a limb reduction surgery.<br/>Surgical management of lymphoedema is divided into <strong>bypass procedures, liposuction, and limb reduction procedures.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bypass procedures include:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Neilubowicz procedure</strong> - anastomosing lymph nodes to the veins.</li>\n<li><strong>Kinmonth procedure</strong> - using ileal mucosal patch.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Limb reduction procedures include:</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Sistrunk procedure</strong> - A wedge of skin and subcutaneous tissue is excised and the wound closed primarily. This is the most commonly carried out procedure to reduce the girth of the thigh.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Homan’s procedure</strong> - Skin flaps are raised, and then subcutaneous tissue is excised from beneath the flaps.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Thompson’s procedure - </strong>Modification of Homan’s procedure. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Charles procedure</strong> - Excision of all of the skin and subcutaneous tissues down to the deep fascia, with coverage using split-skin grafts.</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"LhwUDJmO","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"54_bones_of_the_lower_limb","title":"54.Bones Of The Lower Limb","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC9702","text":"A crying 3-year-old boy is​ brought by his mother an​d stepfather following an‍ alleged history of a fal​l. X-ray shows a spiral f‍racture of the proximal f‌emur. Which structure is ‌related to the anterior s‍urface of the affected bo‌ne?","raw_text":"A crying 3-year-old boy is brought by his mother and stepfather following an alleged history of a fall. X-ray shows a spiral fracture of the proximal femur. Which structure is related to the anterior surface of the affected bone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Linea ​aspera","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pectine​al line","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intertrocha​nteric line","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intertrocha​nteric crest","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Intertrochanteric line","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5a6da93129d44c2da6fb5e45db7650bdx1280x2117.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7e62a3100058410c81175cda31837e9fx1280x2117.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Intertrochanteric line</strong></p><p>The structure related to the <strong>anterior surface</strong> of the affected bone i.e. the<strong> proximal femur</strong> is the <strong>intertrochanteric line</strong>. It is present at the junction of the neck and shaft of the femur.</p>\n<p>The femur can be divided into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Proximal femur</strong> - consists of the head, neck, greater and lesser trochanters. The <strong>intertrochanteric line</strong> is present on the anterior surface at the junction of the neck and shaft of the femur. The <strong>intertrochanteric crest</strong> is present on the posterior surface.</li>\n<li><strong>Shaft of the femur</strong> - consists of linea aspera (bony crest present on the posterior surface), gluteal tuberosity, and pectineal line.</li>\n<li><strong>Distal femur</strong> - consists of the medial condyle, lateral condyle, intercondylar fossa, and patellar surface.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The images given below shows the anatomy of the femur bone.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Spiral fractures</strong> of long bones such as <strong>femur</strong> or humerus in a child suggest an external application of a twisting force. This is highly suspicious of <strong>non-accidental trauma. </strong>The probable diagnosis is <strong>battered baby syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4031","text":"What is the normal angle o​f the neck of the femur t​o the shaft?","raw_text":"What is the normal angle of the neck of the femur to the shaft?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10​5°","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"12​5°","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"13​5°","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"11​5°","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 125°","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5a595f7149bd4d4790d1884ffa3c024ax1280x1439.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 125°</strong></p><p>The <strong>normal angle</strong> of the <strong>neck</strong> of the femur to the <strong>shaft</strong> is approximately<strong> 125°. </strong>This is known as <strong>neck-shaft angle</strong> or angle of inclination.</p>\n<p>This <strong>facilitates</strong> the <strong>movement</strong> at the <strong>hip</strong> joint, enabling the limb to move freely.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When the angle is <strong>&gt;</strong> <strong>135°</strong>, the condition is known as <strong>coxa valga</strong></li>\n<li>When the angle is <strong>&lt; 120°</strong>, the condition is known as <strong>coxa vara</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the normal neck-shaft angle of femur.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4028","text":"An 80 kg boy with hypothyr​oidism developed sudden p​ain in the left hip with ‍restricted abduction and ​internal rotation as a re‍sult of a minor fall. Wha‌t is true about the linea‌ aspera of the affected b‍one?","raw_text":"An 80 kg boy with hypothyroidism developed sudden pain in the left hip with restricted abduction and internal rotation as a result of a minor fall. What is true about the linea aspera of the affected bone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is present on the anter​ior surface","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It gives attachment to the​ long head of biceps femo​ris","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The lateral border continu​es as pectineal line","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It gives attachment to the​ adductor longus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It gives attachment to the adductor longus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bfb876203c684b2592d97ed49514f6d5x1280x1899.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It gives attachment to the adductor longus</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>slipped capital femoral epiphysis</strong> (SCFE).<strong> </strong>Linea aspera of the affected bone i.e. <strong>femur</strong> gives <strong>attachment</strong> to:<strong> </strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Adductor longus</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Intermuscular septa</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Short head of biceps femoris</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Linea aspera is the roughened ridges of bone present on the <strong>posterior surface</strong> of the femoral shaft. It is made of medial and lateral edges.</p>\n<div>Proximally:</div>\n<ul>\n<li>Medial edge is continuous as spiral line</li>\n<li>Lateral edge is continuous as gluteal tuberosity</li>\n</ul>\n<div>Distally:</div>\n<ul>\n<li>Medial border is continuous as medial supracondylar line</li>\n<li>Lateral border is continuous as lateral supracondylar line</li>\n<li>Surface between the medial and lateral supracondylar line is the popliteal surface</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows posterior surface of the femur.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4029","text":"A 60-year-old woman develo​ps osteonecrosis of the f​emoral head after a trivi‍al fall. Which of the fol​lowing arteries supply th‍e affected part?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman develops osteonecrosis of the femoral head after a trivial fall. Which of the following arteries supply the affected part?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lateral epip​hyseal artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medial circumflex femoral ​artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ligamentous ​teres artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral circumflex femoral​ artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Medial circumflex femoral artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/794b86f3f98f4c1fbb76579c37cd55dcx600x704.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Medial circumflex femoral artery</strong></p><p>The <strong>arterial supply</strong> to the <strong>femoral head</strong> is mostly by<strong> </strong>the<strong> medial circumflex femoral artery.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The blood supply of the femoral head is derived from an <strong>arterial ring (trochanteric anastomosis)</strong> around the neck of the femur constituted by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Medial and lateral circumflex femoral arteries, branches of profunda femoris artery</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Superior and inferior gluteal vessels (minor contributions)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the fetus and early postnatal period, the medial and lateral circumflex femoral arteries contribute equally. Whereas in the later life the supply from the lateral circumflex femoral artery diminishes and it is mainly supplied by two major arteries of the medial circumflex femoral arteries.</p>\n<p>A small acetabular branch of the obturator artery also reaches the head, through the ligament of the head of the femur. However, it is a minor source.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the blood supply of the head of the femur:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4050","text":"Which of the following pro​vides the nutrient artery​ to the femur?","raw_text":"Which of the following provides the nutrient artery to the femur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Femoral​ artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral circumflex femoral​ artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial circumflex femoral ​artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Profunda fe​moris artery","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Profunda femoris artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c96a57df66414af58cd821f192ed9c6cx1280x1984.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Profunda femoris artery</strong></p><p>The second perforating branch of the <strong>profunda femoris artery </strong>is the <strong>nutrient artery</strong> to the <strong>femur.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following table shows bones of the lower limb and their nutrient arteries:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 280px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">\n<p><strong>Bones of the Lower limb</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">\n<p><strong>Nutrient arteries</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">\n<p>Femur</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">\n<p>2<sup>nd</sup> perforating branch of the profunda femoris</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">\n<p>Tibia</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">\n<p>Branch of posterior tibial artery</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">\n<p>Fibula</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">\n<p>Peroneal artery, a branch of posterior tibial artery</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4038","text":"On performing USG of a ful​l-term fetus, which of th​e following ossification ‍centers is likely to be s​een?","raw_text":"On performing USG of a full-term fetus, which of the following ossification centers is likely to be seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Head of ​the femur","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Greater t​rochanter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lesser t​rochanter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Distal en​d of femur","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Distal end of femur","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7ca2871f84854265a91bc37ef39ac9bax1280x1933.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/15921414413547a3bac676acfbed71fax1280x1933.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Distal end of femur</strong></p><p>The secondary ossification centres of the <strong>distal end</strong> of the <strong>femur</strong> appear at the<strong> 9th month</strong> (full-term) of intrauterine life. They form the condyles and epicondyles of the femur.</p>\n<p>Femur ossifies from one primary centre and four secondary centres:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary centre</strong> appears in the <strong>midshaft</strong> in the seventh week of the prenatal period</li>\n<li>The secondary ossification centres appear at the following sites:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Distal end</strong> of the femur during <strong>ninth month</strong> of intrauterine life</li>\n<li><strong>Head</strong> of the femur during <strong>six months</strong> after birth</li>\n<li><strong>Greater trochanter</strong> during the <strong>fourth year</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Lesser trochanter</strong> between <strong>twelfth</strong> and fourteenth <strong>year</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0539","text":"A patient came to the casu​alty after a road traffic​ accident with a fracture‍ of the femur. The upper ​end of this fractured bon‍e ossifies from how many ‌ossification centers?","raw_text":"A patient came to the casualty after a road traffic accident with a fracture of the femur. The upper end of this fractured bone ossifies from how many ossification centers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/802d42b307674e5cb8eef9b3a47bf0e5x1280x1933.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3</strong></p><p>The upper end of the <strong>femur</strong> ossifies from <strong>3</strong> <strong>ossification centers</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Head</strong> which appears at <strong>6 months</strong> after birth</li>\n<li><strong>Greater trochanter</strong> which appears at <strong>4th year</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Lesser trochanter</strong> which appears between <strong>12-14</strong> <strong>years</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows secondary ossification centers of the femur.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4033","text":"What type of bone is the p​atella?","raw_text":"What type of bone is the patella?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flat​ bone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pneumat​ic bone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sesamo​id bone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Irregul​ar bone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sesamoid bone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1901f4b7fc544b1cb050fc31e6290718x599x490.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sesamoid bone</strong></p><p>The <strong>patella</strong> is an example of <strong>sesamoid</strong> bone, which is formed within the tendon of the <strong>quadriceps femoris muscle.</strong></p>\n<p>It is <strong>triangular</strong> in shape and lies in the patellofemoral groove. The <strong>apex</strong> is directed inferiorly which gives attachment to the <strong>patellar ligament</strong> and the <strong>base</strong> is directed superiorly which gives attachment to the <strong>quadriceps femoris</strong> muscle.</p>\n<p>Its convex anterior surface is perforated by numerous nutrient vessels and is <strong>separated from</strong> the <strong>skin </strong>by a <strong>prepatellar bursa</strong>. Its posterior surface has an oval articular area crossed by a smooth vertical ridge that fits into the intercondylar groove. This ridge divides the patellar articular area into medial and lateral facets. These facets articulate with the corresponding condyles of the femur.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Important sesamoid bones:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Patella</strong> - largest sesamoid bone in the tendon of quadriceps femoris.</li>\n<li><strong>Pisiform</strong> - tendon of flexor carpi ulnaris.</li>\n<li><strong>Fabella</strong> - lateral head of the gastrocnemius. </li>\n<li>Two sesamoid bones can be found in the first metacarpal within the tendons of flexor pollicis brevis and adductor pollicis.</li>\n<li>Two sesamoid bones can be found in the first metatarsophalangeal articulation within the double tendon of flexor hallucis brevis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4035","text":"Which of the following for​ms the apex of the tibial​ triangular area?","raw_text":"Which of the following forms the apex of the tibial triangular area?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epico​ndyle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tibial​ crest","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tibial t​uberosity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mall​eolus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tibial tuberosity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5baeb2d944034fac84868126ddb972b9x1280x1229.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tibial tuberosity</strong></p><p>The <strong>apex</strong> of the tibial <strong>triangular area</strong> is formed by <strong>tibial tuberosity.</strong></p>\n<p>The tibial triangular area is a small area present in front of the anterior condylar surface. The <strong>apex</strong> is directed downwards and is formed by <strong>tibial tuberosity</strong> and the <strong>base</strong> is continuous with the <strong>anterior condylar surfaces</strong> of the <strong>tibia.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The tibial tuberosity gives attachment to the patellar ligament and is divided into distal rough and proximal smooth regions. The distal region is palpable and is separated from the skin by the infrapatellar bursa.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4055","text":"At what time of intra-uter​ine life, would you be ab​le to see the primary oss‍ification center of the t​ibia on an USG?","raw_text":"At what time of intra-uterine life, would you be able to see the primary ossification center of the tibia on an USG?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3rd ​week","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5th ​week","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7th ​week","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"9th ​week","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 7th week","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81292ef0d515428397a211760da5ac4fx1280x1827.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 7th week</strong></p><p>The <strong>primary ossification center</strong> of the <strong>tibia appears</strong> at the <strong>7th week</strong> of <strong>intra-uterine life.</strong></p>\n<p>Tibia is ossified from <strong>three centers</strong>: one primary center in the shaft and two secondary centers, one in each epiphysis.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary center\n<ul>\n<li>Ossification in the <strong>shaft</strong> appears at the <strong>7th week </strong>of intrauterine life.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Secondary center\n<ul>\n<li>In the <strong>upper</strong> end, the ossification center is usually present at <strong>birth</strong> and fuses with the shaft in <strong>16 years in females</strong> and <strong>18 years in males</strong>.</li>\n<li>In the <strong>lower</strong> end, the ossification center appears early in the <strong>first year</strong> and fuses with the shaft in the <strong>15th year in females</strong> and<strong> 17th year in males.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows ossification centers of tibia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4048","text":"Which of the following str​uctures is attached to th​e head of the bone depict‍ed in the image below?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is attached to the head of the bone depicted in the image below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lateral ​meniscus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Popliteu​s tendon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Biceps ​femoris","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior cruciate ligamen​t","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Biceps femoris","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c4/Left_fibula_-_close-up_-_animation.gif"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Biceps femoris</strong></p><p>The bone depicted here is the <strong>fibula.</strong> The <strong>biceps femoris</strong> is attached to the head of this bone.</p>\n<p>Important structures related to fibula:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fibular collateral ligament </strong>and<strong> biceps femoris</strong> are attached to the head</li>\n<li>The <strong>common peroneal nerve</strong> can be rolled against the <strong>neck</strong></li>\n<li>The shaft has 3 surfaces and is related to the muscles of the leg:\n<ul>\n<li>The lateral surface is related to fibular muscles</li>\n<li>The anteromedial surface is related to extensors</li>\n<li>The posterior surface is related to flexor muscles</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4051","text":"An adolescent presented wi​th a foot drop was found ​to have an injury to the ‍common peroneal nerve. Fr​acture at which of the fo‍llowing sites most likely‌ causes the given conditi‌on?","raw_text":"An adolescent presented with a foot drop was found to have an injury to the common peroneal nerve. Fracture at which of the following sites most likely causes the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shaft o​f tibia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Shaft o​f fibula","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neck of​ fibula","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tibial t​uberosity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neck of fibula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4f6425c0efb24a28b82418dc4dd71ebfx1280x1592.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neck of fibula</strong></p><p>The patient is likely to have a fracture involving the<strong> neck of the fibula, </strong>which is closely related to the<strong> common peroneal nerve </strong>(injury - foot drop).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Clinical considerations of the fibula:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Since fibula does not take part in the transmission of body weight, it is a common <strong>source of bone for grafting. </strong>The lateral malleolus and the ligaments attached to it are very important in maintaining stability at the ankle joint.</li>\n<li>The common peroneal nerve can be rolled against the neck of the fibula. This nerve is commonly injured.</li>\n<li>In the first stage of Pott’s fracture, the lower end of the fibula is fractured spirally.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4056","text":"The fibula ossifies from h​ow many ossification cent​res?","raw_text":"The fibula ossifies from how many ossification centres?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/61873fe51384485f884bf09e066e629ax1280x1827.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3</strong></p><p><strong>Fibula ossifies</strong> from <strong>three ossification centres. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>One</strong> primary centre in the shaft and <strong>two</strong> secondary centres at extremities.</p>\n<p>Ossification in the shaft begins at 8 week of intrauterine life. The ossification in the distal end begins in the first year and unites with the shaft by 15 year in females and 17 years in males. The proximal end appears at three to four years and unites with shaft at around 17 years in females and 19 years in males.</p>\n<div> </div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4058","text":"Which of the following bon​es is interposed between ​the head of the talus and‍ the cuneiforms?","raw_text":"Which of the following bones is interposed between the head of the talus and the cuneiforms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calc​aneus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cub​oid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Navi​cular","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Metat​arsal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Navicular","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3a81274e0e53453dbb8c785c9dc33bfbx1280x1200.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/01a783fccd3241b7b9c3df12f509e0dbx600x969.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Navicular</strong></p><p>The <strong>navicular bone</strong> is interposed <strong>between</strong> the head of the <strong>talus</strong> and the <strong>cuneiforms.</strong></p>\n<p>There are 7 tarsal bones that occupy the proximal half of the foot. The proximal row is made up of the talus and calcaneum. The distal row, from medial to lateral, contains the medial, intermediate, and lateral cuneiforms and the cuboid. Medially the navicular bone is interposed between the head of the talus and the cuneiforms.</p>\n<p>The images given below show the tarsal bones of the foot.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4060","text":"The sustentaculum tali is ​a projection present on w​hich of the following tar‍sal bones?","raw_text":"The sustentaculum tali is a projection present on which of the following tarsal bones?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ta​lus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calc​aneus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Navi​cular","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cunei​forms","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Calcaneus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/83b2660ff4c743aea4ccf0609684d380x600x673.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/25b15488e6a1491183c86f4cd70dd84cx1280x1190.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Calcaneus</strong></p><p>The <strong>sustentaculum tali</strong> is a projection present on the <strong>calcaneus, </strong>on its medial surface.</p>\n<p>The <strong>superior surface</strong> has a <strong>facet,</strong> to articulate with the middle facet on the head of the talus. It supports the posterior part of the head of the talus.</p>\n<p>The <strong>inferior surface</strong> of the sustentaculum tali has a groove along which the <strong>tendon of flexor hallucis longus</strong> muscle travels into the sole of the foot.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the sustentaculum tali on the calcaneus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Given below are the 3 ligaments attached to the sustentaculum tali:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Plantar calcaneonavicular ligament (anterior surface)</li>\n<li>Deltoid ligament (medial surface)</li>\n<li>Medial talocalcaneal ligament</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"_29pDMDq","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"54_descriptive_statistics_i___probability_and_data","title":"54.Descriptive Statistics I   Probability And Data","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB1331","text":"You are conducting a study‌ to determine the mean he‍ight of children visiting‍ the local PHC. Which typ​e of statistical study is​ being done here?","raw_text":"You are conducting a study to determine the mean height of children visiting the local PHC. Which type of statistical study is being done here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Descr‌iptive","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Analy‌tical","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infer‌ential","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Observ‌ational","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Descriptive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Descriptive</strong></p><p>A study to <strong>determine</strong> the <strong>mean height</strong> of children is an example of a <strong>descriptive statistical</strong> study.</p>\n<p><strong>Descriptive statistics</strong> are the statistical methods that merely <strong>organize, describe </strong>or<strong> summarize</strong> the actual data available. For example, a study determining the mean blood pressure of patients visiting a PHC on a single day.</p>\n<p><strong>Analytical statistics </strong>are the second major category of epidemiological studies, after descriptive statistics. The objective of an analytical study is to <strong>test </strong>the<strong> hypothesis</strong>.</p>\n<p>An<strong> observational study</strong> is an empiric investigation in which the objective is to <strong>establish</strong> a relationship between <strong>cause and effect</strong>. It falls under the category of analytical statistics.</p>\n<p>It is performed when controlled experimentation cannot be feasibly used to determine a hypothesis. For example, in the case of cigarette smoking causing lung cancer, it would be unethical and not feasible to conduct a controlled experiment. So, an observational study is preferred.</p>\n<p><strong>Inferential statistics</strong> involve inductive reasoning i.e.,<strong> generalizing observations </strong>to a population after having observed only a sample. An example of such a study would be a study determining the expected success rate of vasectomy </p>\n<p><strong>Theoretical statistics</strong> is a <strong>mathematical</strong> <strong>approach</strong> to describe something, predict events, or analyze the relationship between things. Some of the concepts of theoretical statistics are mean, median, mode, standard deviation and correlation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1332","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about statistical‍ terms is incorrect?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about statistical terms is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Population is the universe‌ about which an investiga‍tor wishes to draw conclu‍sions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A sample is a representati‌ve subset of the populati‍on that is being studied","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"An element is a group of o‌bservations in the popula‍tion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A population need not be o‌nly people but can be mea‍surements too","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. An element is a group of observations in the population","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/860fed9eefaa4b0bbad9ae7906601edfx600x489.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. An element is a group of observations in the population</strong></p><p>An <strong>element</strong> is a <strong>single observation</strong> in a study. For example, in a study of blood pressure values, the blood pressure value of one person is an element.</p>\n<p>In a statistical study, the <strong>population</strong> refers to the group about which <strong>information</strong> is to be <strong>gathered</strong>. The composition of the population in terms of its age, sex, occupation, cultural characters, etc need to be defined before a study is conducted. The population need not consist of just people but <strong>can also be</strong> made of <strong>measurements</strong>. For example, in a study of blood glucose measurements in Indians, the population consists of the blood glucose measurements.</p>\n<p>The <strong>part/subset </strong>of the<strong> population</strong>, which is being <strong>studied</strong> is known as a <strong>sample</strong>. For example, in a questionnaire-based study on cervical cancer in a population, the sample would include females attending the out-patient departments of a hospital. This is a subset of the entire population.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1333","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about probability‍ is true? (Probability of‍ event occurring is p) p=​1 means absolute certaint​y of event occurring p ca​n be positive or negative‍ The addition rule can be​ used to calculate probab‍ility The probability of ‍the event not occurring (​q ) = p + 1 The multiplic​ation rule can be used to‍ calculate probability","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements about probability is true? (Probability of event occurring is p)</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">p=1 means absolute certainty of event occurring</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">p can be positive or negative</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The addition rule can be used to calculate probability </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The probability of the event not occurring (q ) = p + 1</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The multiplication rule can be used to calculate probability </li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2,3,4 ‌&amp;amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,3 &amp;‌amp; 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1,2 &amp;‌amp; 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 &amp;a‌mp; 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1,3 & 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1,3 &amp; 5</strong></p><p>If the <strong>probability</strong> of an event occurring is <strong>1</strong>, it means there is an <strong>absolute certainty </strong>that the event will occur.</p>\n<p>The addition rule and multiplication rule can be used to calculate the probability of an event. The <strong>addition rule</strong> is used to calculate the <strong>combined probability</strong> of the occurrence of two or more <strong>mutually exclusive</strong> events. The <strong>multiplication rule</strong> is used to calculate the combined probability of two or more<strong> statistically independent</strong> events occurring <strong>together</strong>.</p>\n<p>Probability values <strong>range</strong> from <strong>0</strong> (no probability) <strong>to 1</strong> (absolute certainty). So negative value of p is not possible.</p>\n<p>Since p is the probability of an event occurring and q is the probability of the event not occurring,</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>q = 1 - p</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1334","text":"In which of the following ‌processes is binomial dis‍tribution widely used?","raw_text":"In which of the following processes is binomial distribution widely used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Marriage ‌counseling","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Genetic c‌ounseling","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Treatment ‌counseling","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Differentia‌l diagnosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Genetic counseling","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Genetic counseling</strong></p><p><strong>Binomial distribution</strong> is widely used in <strong>genetic counseling</strong>.</p>\n<p>Binomial distribution helps <strong>determine</strong> the <strong>probability </strong>of occurance of a specific combination of<strong> mutually exclusive independent </strong>events. In a binomial distribution, there are only <strong>two possible events</strong> like Yes/No or Male/Female or Alive/Dead. The results of the distribution gives the probabilities of obtaining an exact number of successes in a series of independent trials.</p>\n<p>For example, the inheritence of cystic fibrosis follows a binomial distribution. There are:</p>\n<p>a. <strong>Two</strong> possible<strong> events</strong> - inheriting the disease/ not inheriting it</p>\n<p>b. Events are <strong>mutually exclusive</strong> - one person cannot both have the disease and not have it</p>\n<p>c. <strong>Independent</strong> possibilities - if one child has inherited it, this will not affect if the other child will inherit it.</p>\n<p>The physician using this technique can explain to the patients the probabilities of their children suffering from the disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1335","text":"If the events A and B are ‌mutually exclusive events‍, what is the probability‍ of either A or B occurri​ng?","raw_text":"If the events A and B are mutually exclusive events, what is the probability of either A or B occurring?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"p (A) ‌+ p (B)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"p (A) ‌x p (B)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"p (A)+p (B)‌-p (A and B)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 - [p (A‌) + p (B)]","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. p (A) + p (B)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. p (A) + p (B)</strong></p><p>If the events A and B are <strong>mutually exclusive</strong>, then the probability of <strong>either A or B occuring</strong> is given by,</p>\n<p>p (A or B) =<strong> p (A) + p (B)</strong></p>\n<p>This is as per<strong> </strong>the<strong> addition rule </strong>of probability. </p>\n<p>The probability of both the mutually exclusive events happening at the same time is given by,</p>\n<p>p (A and B) =<strong> p (A) x p (B)</strong></p>\n<p>The probability of any of two mutually non-exclusive events occuring at a point of time is given by,</p>\n<p>p (A or B) = <strong>p (A) + p (B) - p (A and B)</strong></p>\n<p>If p(A) and p(B) are the only two outcomes possible in a given event then,</p>\n<p><strong>p (A) + p (B) = 1</strong></p>\n<p>For example, probability of patient suffering from lung cancer dying within 5 years and probability of patient suffering from lung cancer surviving these 5 years adds upto 1.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1336","text":"You are conducting a study‌ on birth weight. As per ‍your data, the probabilit‍y of a baby having birth ​weight &lt;2500 grams is 0.5​0 and of having birth wei​ght of 2500-2999 grams is‍ 0.20. What is the probab​ility that a baby will ha‍ve a birth weight of &lt; 3k‍g?","raw_text":"You are conducting a study on birth weight. As per your data, the probability of a baby having birth weight &lt;2500 grams is 0.50 and of having birth weight of 2500-2999 grams is 0.20. What is the probability that a baby will have a birth weight of &lt; 3kg?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.‌70","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"0.‌60","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.‌10","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0‌.3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 0.70","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 0.70</strong></p><p>The probability that a baby will have a birth weight of &lt; 3kg is <strong>0.70.</strong></p>\n<p>In this scenario, the probability of having a baby with BW &lt; 2500 grams, <strong>p (A) = 0.50</strong> and that of having a baby with BW between 2500 – 2999 grams, <strong>p (B) = 0.20.</strong></p>\n<p>Since both events are mutually exclusive, i.e., they cannot happen together, it satisfies the condition for the <strong>addition rule </strong>of probability.</p>\n<p>Thus, the probability of having a baby that weighs less than 3 kilos is,</p>\n<p><strong>p (A or B) = p (A) + p (B) </strong>= 0.50 + 0.20 =<strong> 0.70.</strong></p>\n<p>If p(A) and p(B) were <strong>non-mutually exclusive</strong> events, then the probability would have been calculated by</p>\n<p><strong>p(A or B)</strong> =<strong> p(A) + p(B) - p(A and B).</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1337","text":"You select 3 people from a‌ population where the pre‍valence of anemia is 20% ‍.What is the probability ​that all the three random​ly selected people from t​he population would have ‍anemia?","raw_text":"You select 3  people from a population where the prevalence of anemia is 20% .What is the probability that all the three randomly selected people from the population would have anemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.‌002","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.‌004","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0‌.6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.‌008","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 0.008","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 0.008</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the probability of <strong>all 3</strong> randomly selected individuals <strong>having anemia</strong> is <strong>0.008</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this scenario, the <strong>prevalence</strong> of anemia is <strong>20%</strong>.</p>\n<p>So, the <strong>probability</strong> of a person <strong>having anemia</strong> in the given population (p) = 20/100 = <strong>0.</strong><strong>2</strong>.</p>\n<p>If selecting 3 people from the population randomly, the chance of the first person having anemia is <strong>independent</strong> of the second or third person also having it and vice versa. This satisfies the condition for the <strong>multiplication rule </strong>of probability which states that the <strong>probability </strong>of two or more<strong> statistically independent events</strong> all <strong>occurring together</strong> is equal to the <strong>product of </strong>their<strong> individual probabilities. </strong></p>\n<p>Thus, here the probability that all the 3 randomly selected people from the population would have anemia = 0.2 x 0.2 x 0.2 = 0.008.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1338","text":"There are 50 patients admi‌tted in a ward of your me‍dical college. Here 20 ar‍e girls and 30 are boys. ​Out of this, 10 girls and​ 20 boys require surgery.​ What is the probability ‍that a randomly selected ​boy and girl both require‍ surgery?","raw_text":"There are 50 patients admitted in a ward of your medical college. Here 20 are girls and 30 are boys. Out of this, 10 girls and 20 boys require surgery. What is the probability that a randomly selected boy and girl both require surgery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌/6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3‌/5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6/‌25","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌/3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1/3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1/3</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the <strong>probability</strong> that a randomly selected <strong>boy and girl both require surgery</strong> is <strong>1/3</strong>.</p>\n<p>First, we have to calculate the <strong>probability</strong> of picking the boys requiring surgery and the girls requiring surgery<strong> separately</strong>. These are <strong>mutually exclusive</strong> events.</p>\n<p>In this scenario, the probability of correctly picking a girl requiring surgery is p(A)=10/20= 1/2</p>\n<p>The probability of correctly picking a boy requiring surgery is p(B)=20/30= 2/3</p>\n<p>Next, we apply the <strong>multiplication rule</strong> of probability.</p>\n<p>The probability of picking boys and girls that require surgery,</p>\n<p><strong>p(A and B)</strong>= p(A) x p(B)= ½ x ⅔ = 2/6= <strong>1/3</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1339","text":"The chance of passing a ge‌netic disease X by affect‍ed parents to their first‍ child is 0.16. If they p​lan to have two children,​ what is the probability ​that both the children wi‍ll have the disease?","raw_text":"The chance of passing a genetic disease X by affected parents to their first child is 0.16. If they plan to have two children, what is the probability that both the children will have the disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.2‌944","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.‌32","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.0‌032","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.0‌256","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 0.0256","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 0.0256</strong></p><p>The <strong>probability</strong> of <strong>both children</strong> having the disease is <strong>0.0256</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this scenario, the <strong>chance of each child</strong> having the disease is <strong>0.16.</strong> The second child having the disease is independent of the first child having it. So, the events are<strong> statistically independent</strong>.</p>\n<p>Applying the <strong>multiplication rule </strong>of probability, the probability of <strong>both</strong> children having the disease is,</p>\n<p>0.16 x 0.16 = <strong>0.0256</strong>. <br/>These are <strong>non-mutually exclusive</strong> events as the first child having the trait does not rule out the chance of the second child having it.   </p>\n<p>On the other hand, the <strong>probability</strong> of <strong>one</strong> <strong>of the two</strong> children having the disease is given by,</p>\n<p>p(A or B)= <strong>p(A) + p(B) - p(A and B)</strong>= 0.16 + 0.16 -(0.16 x 0.16)= 0.32- 0.0256= <strong>0.2944</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1349","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a measurement scale f‍or data?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a measurement scale for data?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nom‌inal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ord‌inal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ra‌tio","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Propo‌rtion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Proportion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Proportion</strong></p><p>A <strong>proportion</strong> is not a measurement scale for data. It is a ratio, where the <strong>numerator</strong> is always a <strong>part of</strong> the<strong> denominator</strong>. It indicates a value as a part of the whole. It is usually <strong>expressed as</strong> a <strong>percentage</strong>.</p>\n<p>The four <strong>scales of measurement</strong> used for data can be remembered with the mnemonic NOIR. This stands for <strong>nominal, ordinal, interval </strong>and<strong> ratio</strong> data scales.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1362","text":"Which of the following sca‌les of data measurement d‍o blood groups belong?","raw_text":"Which of the following scales of data measurement do blood groups belong?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interval ‌scale data","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ratio sc‌ale data","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Categori‌cal data","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ordina‌l Data","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Categorical data","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Categorical data</strong></p><p><strong>Bloods groups</strong> are <strong>nominal </strong>scale data or <strong>categorical data.</strong> This is because they are just labels and have no numerical value or meaningful order. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1352","text":"Which of the following are‌ examples of nominal scal‍e data? Genders Types of ‍hospitals Height Kuppuswa​my scale Types of anemia","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are examples of nominal scale data?</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Genders</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Types of hospitals</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Height</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Kuppuswamy scale</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Types of anemia</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 &amp;a‌mp; 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4 &amp;a‌mp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,2 &amp;‌amp; 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1,2 &amp;‌amp; 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1,2 & 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1,2 &amp; 5</strong></p><p><strong>Genders</strong>, <strong>types </strong>of<strong> anemia</strong> and types of<strong> hospitals</strong> come under <strong>nominal scale</strong> data.</p>\n<p>There is no meaningful order or quantifiable variable in this kind of data. It is arranged on the basis of<strong> labels, titles</strong> or<strong> categories.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Kuppuswamy scale</strong> is an <strong>ordinal scale</strong> data. It is a scale representing socio-economic data.</p>\n<p><strong>Height</strong> is a <strong>ratio scale </strong>data as it can be arranged in a meaningful order with a well-defined size interval.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1358","text":"A physician classifies a g‌roup of patients based on‍ disease severity as norm‍al, mild, moderate and se​vere. Which of the follow​ing scales of data does t​his classification system‍ belong to?","raw_text":"A physician classifies a group of patients based on disease severity as normal, mild, moderate and severe. Which of the following scales of data does this classification system belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nom‌inal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ord‌inal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ra‌tio","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inte‌rval","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ordinal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ordinal</strong></p><p>The classification of this disease as normal, mild, moderate and severe belongs to the <strong>ordinal scale.</strong> This is because this arrangement is not only <strong>qualitative</strong> but also has a <strong>meaningful order</strong> with <strong>no interval</strong> in between.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1353","text":"Which of the following are‌ representative of ordina‍l scale data? Hemoglobin ‍level TNM staging Likert ​scale HIV positive or neg​ative Severity of pain","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are representative of ordinal scale data?</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hemoglobin level</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">TNM staging</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Likert scale</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">HIV positive or negative </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Severity of pain</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,2,3,4‌ &amp;amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2,3 &amp;‌amp; 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1 &amp;a‌mp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2,3,4 ‌&amp;amp; 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2,3 & 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2,3 &amp; 5</strong></p><p><strong>TNM</strong> staging, <strong>Likert</strong> scale and <strong>severity of pain</strong> are measured on the <strong>ordinal scale</strong>.</p>\n<p>In such cases, the arrangement is <strong>qualitative</strong>, with a <strong>meaningful order </strong>but there is no way of quantifying the difference between the individual variables. For example, grading of pain as mild, moderate, and severe.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Likert scale</strong> is used in collecting <strong>self-reported</strong> data in questionnaire-based research. It is a <strong>psychometric scale</strong> used to understand people’s <strong>attitudes from responses</strong>. Usually, a 5-point scale of agreement is used which includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1 – Strongly disagree</li>\n<li>2 – Disagree</li>\n<li>3 – Neither agree nor disagree</li>\n<li>4 – Agree</li>\n<li>5 – Strongly agree</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>TNM staging </strong>is done for the staging of <strong>cancer</strong>. It is based on tumor size, lymph node status and metastasis. <strong>Lower stages</strong> indicate <strong>better prognosis</strong> than higher stages.</p>\n<p><strong>Hemoglobin level</strong> is an example of <strong>ratio scale</strong> data.</p>\n<p><strong>HIV</strong> positive or negative <strong>status</strong> falls under the category of <strong>nominal scale</strong> data.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1372","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a continuous variable‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a continuous variable?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blood glucose value of a d‌iabetic patient","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mid-arm circumference of a‌ child","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Number of visits to the ho‌spital in a year","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Birth weigh‌t of a baby","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Number of visits to the hospital in a year","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Number of visits to the hospital in a year</strong></p><p>The<strong> number of visits</strong> to the hospital in a year is a<strong> discrete</strong> variable. The only values it can take are <strong>whole numbers</strong> and none in between. For example, you can’t visit the hospital 2.5 times.</p>\n<p>Blood glucose value, mid-arm circumference and birth weight are <strong>continuous</strong> variables. They can have <strong>any value within a limit</strong> or a range.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1355","text":"Match the following. Data ‌Scale of data 1) Number o‍f cancer patients in a ho‍spital a) Discrete data 2​) Height and weight b) In​terval scale data 3) Bloo​d pressure measurements c‍) Ratio scale data 4) Tem​perature measurement in C‍elsius 5) Temperature mea‍surement in Kelvin","raw_text":"<p>Match the following.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 297.891px;\">Data</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.938px;\">Scale of data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 297.891px;\">1) Number of cancer patients in a hospital </td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.938px;\">a) Discrete data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 297.891px;\">2) Height and weight</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.938px;\">b) Interval scale data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 297.891px;\">3) Blood pressure measurements</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.938px;\">c) Ratio scale data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 297.891px;\">4) Temperature measurement in Celsius</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.938px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 297.891px;\">5) Temperature measurement in Kelvin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.938px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-a, 2-b, 3‌-c, 4-b, 5-c","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-a, 2-c, 3‌-b, 4-c, 5-b","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-a, 2-c, 3‌-c, 4-b, 5-c","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1-b, 2-c, 3‌-b, 4-c, 5-a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1-a, 2-c, 3-c, 4-b, 5-c","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1-a, 2-c, 3-c, 4-b, 5-c</strong></p><p>The <strong>number of</strong> cancer <strong>patients</strong> in a hospital is an example of <strong>discrete</strong> data. They can <strong>only take certain values</strong> and none in between.</p>\n<p>The <strong>temperature</strong> measured in the <strong>Celsius</strong> scale is an example of <strong>interval</strong> scale data. Such data are arranged in a meaningful order but they can not have an absolute zero value. For example, 80 degree celcius to not twice as hot as 40 degree celcius and 0 degree celcius does not indicate an absolute abscence of heat. </p>\n<p><strong>Height</strong> and <strong>weight</strong>, <strong>blood</strong> <strong>pressure</strong> and <strong>temperature</strong> in the <strong>Kelvin</strong> scale are examples of <strong>ratio</strong> scale data. Such data are arranged in a meaningful order and the scales they are measured in <strong>have</strong> <strong>absolute zero</strong> value. For example, a temperature of 0 kelvin indicates an absolute abscense of heat. </p>\n<p>Height and weight, blood pressure, and temperature are examples of <strong>continuous</strong> <strong>data</strong> as they can take <strong>any values between limits</strong> or a range.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1383","text":"Identify the following rep‌resentation of data.","raw_text":"Identify the following representation of data.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pict‌ogram","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Box‌plot","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hist‌ogram","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stem and ‌leaf plot","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Boxplot","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/27becc7338004bfbba5fab4c7c3d19e8.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/27523b711dc046da90b267fa5521d60fx432x432.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4be233b11beb4e7cb8e2ceb28eecf8b5x350x350.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Boxplot</strong></p><p>The image shown is of a <strong>boxplot</strong>.<strong> </strong>This is a method for <strong>graphically depicting</strong> groups of <strong>numerical data</strong> through their <strong>quartiles</strong>.</p>\n<p>Box plots may also have lines extending vertically from the boxes (i.e., <strong>whiskers</strong>)<strong> indicating variability</strong> outside the upper and lower quartiles. Hence, they are also known as <strong>box-and-whisker plots</strong> and box-and-whisker diagrams.</p>\n<p>The below image shows a boxplot depicting a series of experiments done to calculate the velocity of light.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>A <strong>stem and leaf plot</strong> is used to <strong>depict frequency distribution</strong> and individual values for numerical data. The stem is are usually arranged in ascending or descending order vertically, and a vertical line is drawn to separate it from the leaf. The number of leaf items total up to the number of observations. It is difficult to represent a large number of observations using this method.</p>\n<p>The below image shows a stem and leaf plot.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7444","text":"The following diagram char‌ts the population change ‍over years from 1983 to 1‍989 in a given place. Wha​t is the statistical diag​ram used here?","raw_text":"The following diagram charts the population change over years from 1983 to 1989 in a given place. What is the statistical diagram used here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hist‌ogram","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Line d‌iagram","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Component‌ bar graph","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Multiple ‌bar graphs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Component bar graph","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9fb633302d514883b7d7f79ebfbcd8be.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cdb62d9d756f4238b5f103941d61c2c6x524x519.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Component bar graph</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>component bar graph. </strong>It is used to represent data in which the<strong> total magnitude</strong> is<strong> divided into </strong>different subsets or<strong> components.</strong></p>\n<p>A<strong> bar chart </strong>or bar graph is used mainly for<strong> nominal</strong> or <strong>ordinal scale</strong> data. They can be simple, multiple or component bar graphs.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows a <strong>multiple bar graph </strong>showing the human losses of world war II by country. In a multiple bar chart, <strong>2 or more sets of data</strong> are shown <strong>simultaneously</strong>.  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>A <strong>bar chart </strong>can be distinguished from a histogram<strong> </strong>because the <strong>rectangles</strong> are <strong>separated by</strong> the <strong>spaces</strong>. This indicates that the data forms <strong>discrete categories</strong>.</p>\n<p>In a <strong>histogram</strong>, the <strong>rectangles</strong> are <strong>not separated</strong> by spaces as the data represented forms a <strong>continuous set</strong> instead of discrete groups.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1385","text":"Identify the following gra‌phical representation of ‍data.","raw_text":"Identify the following graphical representation of data.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Og‌ive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Scatt‌ergram","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Frequenc‌y polygon","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Line d‌iagram","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Frequency polygon","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bd198f80a5e34b089f0a67ae00b50387.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dc84f3f9581946019d2c80c9ba604b39x630x629.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Frequency polygon</strong></p><p>The given image is of a <strong>frequency polygon</strong>. It is obtained by<strong> joining</strong> the <strong>midpoints</strong> of <strong>histogram</strong> blocks. It is used to diagrammatically represent frequency distribution.</p>\n<p><strong>Scattergram</strong> is a graph in which the values of two variables are plotted along two axes. The pattern of the resulting points <strong>reveals</strong> any <strong>correlation</strong> that may be present. The below image shows a scattergram.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>A<strong> line diagram</strong> is a type of chart that displays information as a series of data points called 'markers' connected by straight line segments. It is usually used to <strong>study time trends</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1386","text":"During a cholera outbreak,‌ you are asked to map the‍ geographical distributio‍n of the disease. What is​ the best method of doing​ this?","raw_text":"During a cholera outbreak, you are asked to map the geographical distribution of the disease. What is the best method of doing this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dot-den‌sity map","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bar d‌iagram","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Line d‌iagram","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pie ‌chart","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dot-density map","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/79fa51303d1740e995a889d28869a854x800x746.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dot-density map</strong></p><p><strong>Dot-density maps</strong> are the <strong>best way</strong> to plot the <strong>geographical distribution</strong> of diseases.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows a dot-density map of London that was plotted during the 1854 outbreak of cholera.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1388","text":"What is the best way to pl‌ot the changes in inciden‍ce of a disease in a give‍n area over time?","raw_text":"What is the best way to plot the changes in incidence of a disease in a given area over time?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Line ‌graph","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hist‌ogram","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Og‌ive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tree d‌iagram","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Line graph","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Line graph</strong></p><p><strong>Line graphs </strong>or line diagrams are the best way to plot the <strong>changes in the incidence</strong> of a disease in a given area <strong>over time</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1387","text":"A doctor is taking history‌ from a diabetic patient ‍and decides to make a cha‍rt to assess joint involv​ement. Which chart should​ he make to explain this?​","raw_text":"A doctor is taking history from a diabetic patient and decides to make a chart to assess joint involvement. Which chart should he make to explain this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hist‌ogram","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Line d‌iagram","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pie ‌chart","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tree d‌iagram","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tree diagram","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8b7be39116a74ccf9e4523fb63e42d8cx600x518.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tree diagram</strong></p><p>In this case, a<strong> tree diagram</strong> would help the doctor in <strong>explaining</strong> the <strong>possible outcomes</strong> to the patient’s diabetes, with the perspective of joint involvement.</p>\n<p>A tree diagram is used mainly to <strong>chart </strong>the<strong> possible outcomes</strong> of a sample event.</p>\n<p>The following image shows the outline of a tree diagram.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1381","text":"What type of data does the‌ image given below repres‍ent?","raw_text":"What type of data does the image given below represent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nom‌inal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Disc‌rete","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Conti‌nuous","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ord‌inal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Continuous","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/224146f7ce754c6da63e7613f1119843.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Continuous</strong></p><p>The given image is of a <strong>histogram. </strong>It<strong> </strong>is used to represent frequency distributions of <strong>continuous quantitative </strong>data.</p>\n<p><strong>Nominal</strong> and <strong>ordinal</strong> data can be represented by a <strong>bar chart</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6566","text":"Which of the following can‌ be used to represent qua‍ntitative data?","raw_text":"Which of the following can be used to represent quantitative data?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onl‌y C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"B a‌nd D","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A, C ‌and D","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A, B ‌and C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A, C and D","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/29a6eaba5f8f4bd19855b7e3fe2ff065.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A, C and D</strong></p><p>An <strong>ogive</strong>, a <strong>line diagram</strong> and a <strong>histogram</strong> can be used to represent <strong>quantitative</strong> data.</p>\n<p>The images shown above are:</p>\n<p>A – Ogive</p>\n<p>B – Bar graph</p>\n<p>C – Line diagram</p>\n<p>D – Histogram</p>\n<p>Quantitative data cannot be represented by a <strong>bar graph</strong>. It is used mostly for <strong>nominal scale </strong>or <strong>qualitative</strong> data.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"QFywXRQ-","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"54_cystic_and_inherited_disorders_of_the_kidney","title":"54.Cystic And Inherited Disorders Of The Kidney","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MC0661","text":"Which of the following is ​true in patients with aut​osomal dominant polycysti​c kidney disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true in patients with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ADPKD progresses to end-st​age renal disease in earl​y adulthood","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calcium oxalate stones are​ the most common kidney s​tones","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Renal cyst infections are ​rarely seen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cardiovascular complicatio​ns are a major cause of m​ortality","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cardiovascular complications are a major cause of mortality","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cardiovascular complications are a major cause of mortality</strong></p><p><strong>Cardiovascular</strong> complications are the <strong>most common</strong> cause of <strong>mortality</strong> in autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (<strong>ADPKD</strong>). <strong>Infections</strong> are the second most common cause.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">ADPKD is usually clinically silent. Many cases remain <strong>asymptomatic</strong> until the <strong>fifth decade</strong> of life and are diagnosed incidentally. <strong>PKD-1</strong> mutations cause <strong>more severe</strong> disease than PKD-2 mutations. The following clinical features may be seen in symptomatic patients:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Abdominal mass</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Back or flank pain </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Recurrent UTIs - <strong>Renal cyst infections</strong> by Gram-negative bacteria are <strong>common</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Nephrolithiasis</strong> - most commonly <strong>uric acid</strong> stones</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Hematuria</strong></li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Cardiac:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypertension </strong>- early feature  </li>\n<li>Left ventricular hypertrophy</li>\n<li>Mitral valve prolapse, valvular insufficiency</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Extra-renal:\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Intracranial aneurysms</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Hepatic cysts</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">As the cysts grow they compress on the surrounding normal tissue which leads to a progressive reduction in the GFR. Eventually, it leads to end-stage renal disease (<strong>ESRD</strong>) by the<strong> fifth decade </strong>of life.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0660","text":"A 48-year-old woman develo​ped a sudden severe heada​che and vomiting. Her nei​ghbor rushed her to the h​ospital where she was dec‍lared dead on arrival. Sh​e lived alone and her fam‌ily history is unknown. A​n autopsy revealed a suba‍rachnoid hemorrhage and t​he following findings in ​her kidneys. Which of the‌ following is incorrect a‌bout this condition?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old woman developed a sudden severe headache and vomiting. Her neighbor rushed her to the hospital where she was declared dead on arrival. She lived alone and her family history is unknown. An autopsy revealed a subarachnoid hemorrhage and the following findings in her kidneys. Which of the following is incorrect about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mutation of PKD-1 is respo​nsible for 85% of cases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PKD-2 mutation has higher ​risk of end-stage renal d​isease than PKD-1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The PKD genes code for the​ polycystin protein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cysts are formed only in 5​% of the tubules of the k​idney","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. PKD-2 mutation has higher risk of end-stage renal disease than PKD-1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/884afe338d444a87befa49070ea41a2f.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. PKD-2 mutation has higher risk of end-stage renal disease than PKD-1</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario with a subarachnoid hemorrhage (<strong>SAH</strong>) and <strong>multiple renal cysts</strong> is suggestive of autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (<strong>ADPKD</strong>). The<strong> PKD-1 </strong>mutation has a <strong>higher risk </strong>of end-stage renal disease (<strong>ESRD</strong>) than PKD-2.</p>\n<p>ADPKD is a disorder which is characterized by progressive bilateral formation of renal cysts. It is caused by the following mutations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>PKD1 gene on chromosome 16 (85%) - codes for polycystin-1</li>\n<li>PKD2 gene on chromosome 4 (15%) - codes for polycystin-2</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Polycystins </strong>are transmembrane proteins that are present on the <strong>primary cilia</strong> of the tubular cells. The mutated proteins in ADPKD alter the normal functioning and triggers cell growth and cyst formation.</p>\n<p>Though cysts are formed only in <strong>5%</strong> of the <strong>tubules</strong> of the kidney, they compress surrounding renal tissue due to their <strong>enormous growth</strong>. This results in loss of renal function.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0665","text":"In patients with ADPKD, cy​sts are least likely to b​e present in which of the​ following sites?","raw_text":"In patients with ADPKD, cysts are least likely to be present in which of the following sites?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arachnoid​ membranes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lu​ngs","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Panc​reas","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Seminal ​vesicles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lungs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lungs</strong></p><p>In patients with <strong>ADPKD</strong>, <strong>cysts</strong> in the <strong>lungs</strong> are very <strong>rare</strong>. </p>\n<p>There are very few case reports with lung cysts in ADPKD patients, but the association between ADPKD and lung cysts has not been proved. However, there is a three-fold increase in the incidence of <strong>bronchiectasis</strong> in ADPKD patients.</p>\n<p>The common <strong>extrarenal manifestations</strong> of ADPKD include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hepatic </strong>cysts (50-70%) - <strong>most common </strong></li>\n<li>Seminal vesicle cysts (40%) - can result in infertility</li>\n<li>Arachnoid membrane cysts (8%) - usually asymptomatic, may increase the risk of subdural hematoma</li>\n<li>Pancreatic cysts (7-36%)</li>\n<li>Splenic cysts </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0687","text":"A 60-year-old man presents​ with loss of weight and ​appetite over the last 3 ​months. He was diagnosed ​with autosomal dominant p‍olycystic kidney disease ​15 years ago. A CT abdome‌n reveals bilateral, mult​icentric renal masses alo‍ng with enlarged retroper​itoneal lymph nodes. What​ is the likely diagnosis?‌","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presents with loss of weight and appetite over the last 3 months. He was diagnosed with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease 15 years ago. A CT abdomen reveals bilateral, multicentric renal masses along with enlarged retroperitoneal lymph nodes. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clear c​ell RCC","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Papillary​ cell RCC","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chromop​hobe RCC","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sarcoma​toid RCC","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sarcomatoid RCC","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sarcomatoid RCC</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of renal cell carcinoma (<strong>RCC</strong>) with lymph node metastases<strong>. </strong>The <strong>sarcomatoid </strong>type of RCC is commonly associated with <strong>ADPKD</strong>.</p>\n<p>Renal cell carcinoma (RCC) is a relatively <strong>rare complication</strong> of ADPKD. There does not seem to be an increased frequency of RCC in these patients compared to the general population. However, when it occurs, it tends to be <strong>bilateral</strong>, <strong>multicentric</strong> and of sarcomatoid type.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0666","text":"A 25-year-old woman presen​ted with persistent heada​ches over the last 1 mont​h. Her father and paterna​l uncle suffered from kid‍ney disease and both died​ in their 50's of a 'brai‌n bleed'. Her blood press​ure is 160/110 mm Hg and ‍had also been elevated du​ring a previous visit. Wh​ich of the following inve‌stigations should be avoi‌ded in this patient?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman presented with persistent headaches over the last 1 month. Her father and paternal uncle suffered from kidney disease and both died in their 50's of a 'brain bleed'. Her blood pressure is 160/110 mm Hg and had also been elevated during a previous visit. Which of the following investigations should be avoided in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ultrasonography of the abd​omen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contrast enhanced CT of th​e abdomen","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Non-contrast CT scan of th​e abdomen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MR angiography of the cere​bral vasculature","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Contrast enhanced CT of the abdomen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Contrast enhanced CT of the abdomen</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease <strong>(ADPKD).</strong> These patients usually have impaired renal function. Hence, contrast-enhanced CT (<strong>CECT</strong>) would usually be <strong>avoided</strong> due to the risk of <strong>contrast nephrotoxicity.</strong></p>\n<p>The following investigations are done in patients with ADPKD:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>USG abdomen - </strong>for screening and diagnosis</li>\n<li><strong>Non-contrast CT</strong> abdomen - more sensitive and picks up cysts of smaller size</li>\n<li><strong>MR-angiography</strong> of brain - pre-symptomatic <strong>screening </strong>for intracerebral<strong> aneurysms</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0668","text":"How many renal cysts on US​G are required to diagnos​e a 45-year-old patient w​ith autosomal dominant po​lycystic kidney disease?","raw_text":"How many renal cysts on USG are required to diagnose a 45-year-old patient with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"At least 2 cysts in each k​idney","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"At least 3 cysts in each k​idney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"At least 4 cysts in each k​idney","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Even a single cyst in each​ kidney","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. At least 2 cysts in each kidney","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. At least 2 cysts in each kidney</strong></p><p>Between <strong>40-59 years</strong> of age, the presence of <strong>≥2 cysts</strong> in each kidney is needed to diagnose <strong>ADPKD</strong>.</p>\n<p>This condition is diagnosed based on the<strong> </strong>USG findings using the <strong>Ravine’s criteria</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Age (in years)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Imaging findings</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">15-39 </td>\n<td>Total of ≥3 cysts, unilateral or bilateral</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">40-59</td>\n<td>≥2 cysts in each kidney</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">≥ 60 </td>\n<td>≥ 4 cysts in each kidney</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0669","text":"A 56-year-old woman on dia​lysis is being planned fo​r a renal transplant with​ her son as an organ dono​r. He undergoes a USG sca‍n as part of routine dono​r evaluation. The followi‌ng findings are seen. Whi​ch of the following will ‍you not recommend for the​ son?","raw_text":"A 56-year-old woman on dialysis is being planned for a renal transplant with her son as an organ donor. He undergoes a USG scan as part of routine donor evaluation. The following findings are seen. Which of the following will you not recommend for the son?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased f​luid intake","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Angiotensin-converting enz​yme inhibitors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Surgical decompression of ​cysts","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tolv​aptan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Surgical decompression of cysts","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f38a100b1818472186a17e91e059b326.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Surgical decompression of cysts</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and USG showing a cystic appearance of the kidney is suggestive of asymptomatic autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (<strong>ADPKD</strong>). <strong>Surgical decompression</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a <strong>routine</strong> procedure performed in these patients. It may be required only in cases of very large cysts causing persistent pain and infected cysts.</p>\n<p>The management includes the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Analgesics</li>\n<li><strong>Increased</strong> <strong>fluid</strong> intake \n<ul>\n<li>Prevents renal stone formation</li>\n<li>Reduces growth of cysts by suppressing ADH release</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>ACE inhibitors </strong>and ARBs\n<ul>\n<li>Blood pressure control </li>\n<li>Slowing of cyst growth</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Tolvaptan </strong>(V2 receptor antagonist)\n<ul>\n<li>Reduce pain and <strong>slow </strong>the<strong> decline </strong>in<strong> renal function</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Renal transplant</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Definitive management</li>\n<li>Pre-transplant nephrectomy may be required in patients with very large polycystic kidneys</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0671","text":"A 24-year-old G2P1L0 femal​e patient came for an ant​enatal check-up at 26 wee​ks gestation. On examinat​ion, her abdominal girth ‍was less for gestational ​age and the fetal parts w‌ere easily palpable. Medi​cal records from her prev‍ious pregnancy showed tha​t the deceased infant had​ been positive for a PKHD‌1 mutation. Which of the ‌following statements is c​orrect regarding the most‌ likely diagnosis of the ‍fetus?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old G2P1L0 female patient came for an antenatal check-up at 26 weeks gestation. On examination, her abdominal girth was less for gestational age and the fetal parts were easily palpable. Medical records from her previous pregnancy showed that the deceased infant had been positive for a PKHD1 mutation. Which of the following statements is correct regarding the most likely diagnosis of the fetus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is commonly an asymptom​atic and mild disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"In severe cases, it can be​ diagnosed antenatally on​ USG","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Respiratory insufficiency ​is uncommon in newborns","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is commonly diagnosed a​t 5-10 years of age","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. In severe cases, it can be diagnosed antenatally on USG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. In severe cases, it can be diagnosed antenatally on USG</strong></p><p>A <strong>PKHD1 </strong>mutation in the deceased infant is suggestive of autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (<strong>ARPKD</strong>). As the woman has signs of oligohydramnios, it is likely that this fetus also has ARPKD.<strong> Severe cases</strong> of ARPKD can be diagnosed<strong> in-utero </strong>with a <strong>USG</strong>. Prenatal testing can also be done.</p>\n<p>ARPKD is a hereditary renal disease of childhood caused by a mutation in the PKHD1 gene on <strong>chromosome 6</strong>. It codes for <strong>fibrocystin</strong>/ <strong>polyductin</strong> (FPC), a membrane protein present in the cells of the cortical and medullary collecting ducts.</p>\n<p>Affected fetuses show greatly enlarged kidneys and reduced fetal urine production. This leads to <strong>oligohydramnios </strong>and pulmonary hypoplasia. About 30% of neonates die soon after birth due to <strong>respiratory</strong> <strong>insufficiency</strong>. Most <strong>deaths</strong> occur <strong>&lt; 1 month</strong> of life. Others develop chronic kidney disease and hypertension in early childhood.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0670","text":"Which of the following is ​not a typical feature see​n in patients with autoso​mal recessive polycystic ​kidney disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a typical feature seen in patients with autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Biliary d​ysgenesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Portal hy​pertension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Caroli's​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepati​c cysts","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hepatic cysts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hepatic cysts</strong></p><p><strong>Hepatic cysts</strong> are not a typical feature of autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (<strong>ARPKD</strong>). They occur only in <strong>rare</strong> cases.</p>\n<p>ARPKD is usually diagnosed in the neonatal period. These patients commonly have the following findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Chronic renal insufficiency</li>\n<li>Congenital hepatic fibrosis\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Biliary dysgenesis </strong>with periportal fibrosis is seen</li>\n<li>Can lead to <strong>portal hypertension</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Caroli’s disease -</strong> dilatation of intrahepatic bile ducts</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Caroli's disease is also associated with <strong>medullary sponge kidney.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0672","text":"Match the following: Disea​se Gene 1. Medullary cyst​ic kidney disease-1 (MCKD​-1) a. UMOD 2. Medullary ​cystic kidney disease-2 (‍MCKD 2) b. PKHD1 3. Adole​scent nephronophthisis c.‌ MUC1 4. Autosomal recess​ive polycystic kidney dis‍ease (ARPKD) d. NPHP2","raw_text":"<p>Match the following:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 282px; width: 380px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 627px; text-align: center;\"> <strong>Disease</strong></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 472px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Gene</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129px;\">1. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 498px;\">Medullary cystic kidney disease-1 (MCKD-1)  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.983px;\">a.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 287.017px; text-align: center;\">UMOD</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129px;\">2. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 498px;\">Medullary cystic kidney disease-2 (MCKD 2)  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.983px;\">b.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 287.017px; text-align: center;\">PKHD1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129px;\">3. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 498px;\">Adolescent nephronophthisis  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.983px;\">c.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 287.017px; text-align: center;\">MUC1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129px;\">4.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 498px;\">Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (ARPKD)   </td>\n<td style=\"width: 184.983px;\">d.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 287.017px; text-align: center;\">NPHP2 </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-d, 2-b,​ 3-c, 4-a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-b, 2-d,​ 3-a, 4-c","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-c, 2-a,​ 3-d, 4-b","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1-a, 2-c,​ 3-b, 4-d","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b</strong></p><p>The gene mutations causing the following cystic disorders are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Medullary cystic kidney disease-1 (<strong>MCKD-1</strong>) - <strong>MUC1 </strong></li>\n<li>Medullary cystic kidney disease-2 (<strong>MCKD-2</strong>) - <strong>UMOD</strong></li>\n<li>Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (<strong>ARPKD</strong>) - <strong>PKHD1 </strong></li>\n<li>Adolescent <strong>nephronophthisis</strong> - <strong>NPHP</strong> 1-20</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Medullary cystic kidney disease (<strong>MCKD</strong>) is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> disorder. It presents with a late-onset, slowly progressive renal failure. Renal cysts are not common but can be seen occasionally. Medullary cystic kidney diseases are of two types, MCKD1 and MCKD2.</p>\n<p><strong>MCKD1</strong> is due to a defect in the <strong>mucin 1</strong> (MUC1) gene. The mutated gene codes for a large protein fragment that has a toxic effect on the tubules. It is not associated with elevated uric acid levels and gout.</p>\n<p><strong>MCKD2</strong> is due to a defect in the UMOD gene, which codes for the protein <strong>uromodulin</strong>. It is associated with <strong>hyperuricemia </strong>and<strong> gout</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2474","text":"Which of the following is ​an autosomal recessive re​nal cystic disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an autosomal recessive renal cystic disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberous ​sclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medullary c​ystic kidney","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nephrono​phthisis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Von Hippel-L​indau disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nephronophthisis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nephronophthisis</strong></p><p><strong>Nephronophthisis</strong> is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> renal cystic disease.</p>\n<p>It belongs to a category of conditions called <strong>ciliopathies</strong>. It occurs due to an underlying<strong> defect</strong> in the<strong> primary cilia</strong> of the tubular epithelial cells. The cilia play an important role in cell signaling and maintenance of cell growth, proliferation, and secretion.</p>\n<p>Other ciliopathies include autosomal dominant <strong>polycystic kidney disease</strong>, autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease, and<strong> medullary cystic</strong> kidney disease. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0673","text":"What is the most common in​herited cause of kidney f​ailure in children?","raw_text":"What is the most common inherited cause of  kidney failure in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medullary s​ponge kidney","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nephrono​phthisis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Medullary cystic kidney di​sease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autosomal recessive polycy​stic kidney disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nephronophthisis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nephronophthisis</strong></p><p>The most common <strong>inherited</strong> cause of <strong>kidney failure</strong> in childhood is <strong>nephronophthisis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Nephronophthisis (NPHP) refers to a group of related, but genetically distinct,<strong> autosomal recessive</strong> disorders. Mutations are seen in the <strong>NPHP </strong>genes 1-20. These genes code for <strong>nephrocystins</strong> which are expressed on the kidney tubules.</p>\n<p>NPHP is classified into infantile, juvenile, and adolescent forms. The <strong>juvenile</strong> form is the <strong>most common </strong>form. Common features include tubulointerstitial fibrosis, renal cysts, and chronic kidney disease. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0676","text":"A child with Joubert syndr​ome will have which of th​e following conditions?","raw_text":"A child with Joubert syndrome will have which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Juvenile nep​hronophthisis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Medullary cystic kidney di​sease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medullary s​ponge kidney","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autosomal recessive polycy​stic kidney disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Juvenile nephronophthisis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Juvenile nephronophthisis</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2548\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Joubert’s</strong> syndrome is defined by the presence of <strong>juvenile</strong> <strong>nephronophthisis</strong> (NPHP) and multiple neurologic findings.</p>\n<p>A few examples of<strong> multiorgan syndromes </strong>associated with nephronophthisis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Senior-Loken syndrome: NPHP with retinitis pigmentosa</li>\n<li>Joubert syndrome: NPHP with multiple neurologic findings</li>\n<li>Bardet-Biedl syndrome: NPHP with truncal obesity, cognitive impairment, retinal dystrophy, and polydactyly</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0674","text":"The following finding on i​ntravenous pyelography is​ seen in which of the fol​lowing conditions?","raw_text":"The following finding on intravenous pyelography is seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medullary cystic kidney di​sease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medullary s​ponge kidney","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Senior lok​en syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infantile nephronophthisis​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Medullary sponge kidney","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3706ea851a9c41e9969b4712651b6810.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Medullary sponge kidney</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>paintbrush</strong> or <strong>bouquet of flowers</strong> appearance on intravenous pyelography (<strong>IVP</strong>). It occurs in <strong>medullary</strong> <strong>sponge</strong> <strong>kidney</strong> (MSK).</p>\n<p>MSK is a sporadic benign condition that is often diagnosed incidentally. It is a developmental defect affecting the formation of collecting tubules and results in cystic dilatation of the medullary and papillary portions of collecting ducts. It is characterized by <strong>medullary cysts</strong> that are of variable size. It is associated with <strong>distal RTA</strong> which leads to <strong>hypercalciuria</strong>. The hypercalciuria leads to a tendency to form calcium oxalate and calcium phosphate <strong>stones</strong>. The patients can also have recurrent UTIs and hematuria.</p>\n<p><strong>CT urography</strong> has largely replaced intravenous pyelography as the investigation of choice.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"F8IDTYuL","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"54_stomach","title":"54.Stomach","num_questions":27,"total_marks":108,"duration":37,"time_per_question":82.22,"questions":[{"id":"MB3066","text":"A 3-weeks-old baby present‌s with projectile non-bil‍ious vomiting after every‌ meal. Abdominal examinat​ion reveals prominent per​istalsis in the epigastri‌um and an olive-sized mas‌s is felt on deep palpati‍on of the right upper abd‌omen. Which of the follow‌ing drug exposures is ass‍ociated with this conditi​on?","raw_text":"A 3-weeks-old baby presents with projectile non-bilious vomiting after every meal. Abdominal examination reveals prominent peristalsis in the epigastrium and an olive-sized mass is felt on deep palpation of the right upper abdomen. Which of the following drug exposures is associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Azithr‌omycin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tetrac‌ycline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chloram‌phenicol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metron‌idazole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Azithromycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Azithromycin</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of<strong> congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. </strong>It is associated with exposure to <strong>azithromycin</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors </strong>for congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Turner syndrome</li>\n<li>Trisomy 18</li>\n<li>Erythromycin or azithromycin exposure in the first 2 weeks of life either orally or via mother's milk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hyperplasia of the pyloric muscularis propria obstructs the gastric outflow tract.</p>\n<p>It is<strong> </strong>three to five times <strong>more common</strong> in <strong>males</strong>. Generally presents between<strong> 3rd </strong>and<strong> 6th weeks </strong>of life as new-onset regurgitation, projectile, <strong>non-bilious vomiting</strong> after feeding. Physical examination reveals a firm, ovoid, 1 to 2 cm<strong> abdominal mass. </strong>In some cases, abnormal left to right hyperperistalsis is seen.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment </strong>is the surgical splitting of the muscularis (<strong>Ramstedt pyloromyotomy</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3073","text":"Endoscopy of a patient wit‌h chronic upper gastroint‍estinal bleeding showed t‌he following finding. Wha​t is the likely diagnosis​?","raw_text":"Endoscopy of a patient with chronic upper gastrointestinal bleeding showed the following finding. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dieulafo‌y lesion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vascular‌ ectasia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Carcinom‌a stomach","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic gas‌tric varices","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vascular ectasia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9a407a4e0313425f8c05b3ea75c8cc23.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aaf92fdeb9464065ad7752841d95c439x720x541.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vascular ectasia</strong></p><p>The endoscopy findings of the <strong>watermelon stomach</strong> are suggestive of <strong>gastric antral vascular ectasia </strong>(GAVE)<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>In GAVE, endoscopy reveals longitudinal stripes of edematous erythematous mucosa that alternate with less severely injured, paler mucosa giving the appearance of watermelon.<strong> </strong>GAVE is responsible for 4% of <strong>non-variceal upper gastrointestinal bleeding.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Dieulafoy lesion </strong>is a non-stress-related cause of GI bleeding. It is due to a submucosal artery that does not branch properly within the wall of the stomach. It is shown in the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3076","text":"A 60-year-old man with hyp‌ertension and diabetes me‍llitus is on dialysis for‌ chronic renal failure. W​hich of the following for​ms of gastritis is the pa‌tient at risk of developm‌ent?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man with hypertension and diabetes mellitus is on dialysis for chronic renal failure. Which of the following forms of gastritis is the patient at risk of development?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute g‌astritis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic antr‌al gastritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphocyti‌c gastritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertrophi‌c gastritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute gastritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute gastritis</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>renal failure</strong> develop<strong> acute gastritis</strong> as a <strong>consequence </strong>of<strong> uremia. </strong></p>\n<p>In uremia, gastric injury is<strong> </strong>due to<strong> inhibition </strong>of<strong> gastric bicarbonate transporters</strong> by <strong>ammonium</strong> ions.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes </strong>of acute gastritis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alcohol</li>\n<li>Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs)</li>\n<li>Smoking</li>\n<li>Ischemia/shock</li>\n<li>Steroid use</li>\n<li>Uremia</li>\n<li>Chemical ingestion</li>\n<li>Stress</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Histologically, the presence of <strong>neutrophils</strong> above the basement membrane in<strong> direct contact with epithelial cells</strong> signifies acute gastritis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3074","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of chronic gastritis?‍","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of chronic gastritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infe‌ction","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acid hype‌rsecretion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alc‌ohol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autoi‌mmune","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Infection</strong></p><p>The most common cause of <strong>chronic gastritis</strong> is <strong>infection</strong> with the bacillus<strong><em> Helicobacter pylori</em>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Causes </strong>of chronic gastritis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Helicobacter pylori</em> - 1st most common</li>\n<li>Autoimmune gastritis - 2nd most common</li>\n<li>Radiation injury</li>\n<li>Chronic bile reflux</li>\n<li>Mechanical injury (e.g., an indwelling nasogastric tube)</li>\n<li>Systemic diseases such as Crohn disease, amyloidosis</li>\n<li>Graft-versus-host disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options B and C: Acid hypersecretion and alcohol ingestion are the causes of acute gastritis.</p>\n<p>Note: Autoimmune gastritis is the most common cause of diffuse atrophic gastritis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6658","text":"A patient with a history o‌f peptic ulcers underwent‍ an upper GI endoscopic b‌iopsy of the stomach. The​ biopsy was stained with ​a special stain as shown ‌in the image. Identify th‌e special stain used.","raw_text":"A patient with a history of peptic ulcers underwent an upper GI endoscopic biopsy of the stomach. The biopsy was stained with a special stain as shown in the image. Identify the special stain used.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gomori's methanamine silve‌r stain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Warthin-Starry silver stai‌n","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Masson-Fon‌tana stain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Periodic Acid Schiff stain‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Warthin-Starry silver stain","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/44636e78c26243409da70b610a452347.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2b3e67860a1f42f9a6ee89d6c6b4a92ax600x400.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bfa9cc1583cd434898039a1200c99df0x1280x1057.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7c816716cf7645eb9f76358bbb85f4f5x800x533.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Warthin-Starry silver stain</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given slide shows the GI mucosa, which has been stained with <strong>Warthin-Starry silver stain</strong>, demonstrating the <em><strong>Helicobacter pylori </strong></em>bacilli.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">On antral biopsy, H. pylori organisms are seen abundantly within the surface mucus layer, but they are difficult to visualize with routine hematoxylin (H&amp;E) stains. </span>Organisms are most easily demonstrated with <strong>special stains</strong> like <strong>Warthin-Starry silver stain</strong> or <strong>Giemsa stain.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The image given below is GI biopsy stained with Warthin-Starry silver stain.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The below image is a <strong>Giemsa stain</strong> showing <em><strong>Helicobacter pylori</strong></em> in a case of <strong>chronic gastritis.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3078","text":"A 34-year-old man presents‌ with nausea and upper ab‍dominal pain. He experien‌ces vague discomfort most​ly in the afternoon and n​ight, which is relieved b‌y food. The urea breath t‌est is positive. Upper GI‍ endoscopy reveals a smal‌l ulcer with a clean base‌ in the duodenum. From wh‍ich of the following site​s should the biopsy be ta‍ken to confirm the diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old man presents with nausea and upper abdominal pain. He experiences vague discomfort mostly in the afternoon and night, which is relieved by food. The urea breath test is positive. Upper GI endoscopy reveals a small ulcer with a clean base in the duodenum. From which of the following sites should the biopsy be taken to confirm the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ant‌rum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fun‌dus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Duod‌enum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lesser c‌urvature","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Antrum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/254b0b94b6384e6f8c6571e749bdfea1x1280x1057.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Antrum</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong><em>H. pylori</em> gastritis. </strong>The preferred site for <strong>biopsy</strong> is the <strong>antrum </strong>as<strong> </strong><em>H. pylori</em> are most often found in the antrum of the stomach.</p>\n<p><em>H. pylori</em> display <strong>tropism</strong> for <strong>gastric epithelia</strong> and are generally not found in the duodenal epithelium. Long-standing <em>H. pylori</em> gastritis may extend to involve the body and fundus, resulting in patchy mucosal atrophy.</p>\n<p><span class=\"s1\">The below image is a <strong>Warthin-Starry silver stain </strong>showing <em>Helicobacter pylori</em> colonized on the surface of the regenerative epithelium of the antrum.</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3081","text":"In patients with autoimmun‌e gastritis, what part of‍ the stomach is least lik‌ely to get involved?","raw_text":"In patients with autoimmune gastritis, what part of the stomach is least likely to get involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bo‌dy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fun‌dus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ant‌rum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Car‌dia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Antrum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Antrum</strong></p><p><strong>Autoimmune gastritis</strong> typically <strong>spares </strong>the <strong>antrum.</strong></p>\n<p>Autoimmune gastritis causes <strong>diffuse mucosal damage </strong>of the<strong> acid-producing mucosa,</strong> within the <strong>body </strong>and <strong>fundus</strong>. It accounts for less than 10% of cases of chronic gastritis. <strong>CD4+T cells</strong> directed against parietal cell components, including the H+/K+ ATPase, are considered to be the principal agents of injury in autoimmune gastritis.</p>\n<p>Characteristic features of autoimmune gastritis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong> Antibodies</strong> to<strong> parietal cells</strong> and<strong> intrinsic factor</strong></li>\n<li>Reduced serum pepsinogen I concentration</li>\n<li><strong>Hypergastrinemia</strong></li>\n<li>Endocrine cell hyperplasia leads to carcinoid tumors</li>\n<li>Vitamin B12 deficiency (pernicious anemia)</li>\n<li>Defective gastric acid secretion (achlorhydria)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3085","text":"What is the most common si‌te of peptic ulcers?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of peptic ulcers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior wall of the duode‌num","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior wall of the duod‌enum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lesser curvature of the st‌omach","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antrum of ‌the stomach","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anterior wall of the duodenum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c779b2a090da476886059e6556f9a787x400x408.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anterior wall of the duodenum</strong></p><p>The most common site of <strong>peptic ulcer</strong> is the <strong>anterior wall </strong>of the <strong>proximal duodenum, </strong>within a few centimeters of the pyloric valve.</p>\n<p>Gastric peptic ulcers are predominantly located along the lesser curvature near the interface of the body and antrum.</p>\n<p><strong>Locations </strong>of peptic ulcers (in decreasing order of frequency):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Duodenum (first part)</li>\n<li>Stomach (lesser curvature near the junction of body and antrum)</li>\n<li>Gastroesophageal junction in gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) or Barrett’s esophagus</li>\n<li>Margins of jejunostomy</li>\n<li>Stomach, duodenum, and/or <strong>jejunum</strong> in patients with <strong>Zollinger–Ellison syndrome</strong></li>\n<li>Ileal Meckel’s diverticulum containing ectopic gastric mucosa</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The below image shows a peptic ulcer with a <strong>round </strong>to<strong> oval, sharply punched-out defect,</strong> and clean mucosal margin</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3087","text":"A 55-year-old man presents‌ with periodic abdominal ‍pain. The pain is associa‌ted with meals and is rel​ieved by antacids. Physic​al examination is unremar‌kable. Endoscopy shows a ‌mucosal ulcer. What is th‍e most common complicatio‌n of this condition?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man presents with periodic abdominal pain. The pain is associated with meals and is relieved by antacids. Physical examination is unremarkable. Endoscopy shows a mucosal ulcer. What is the most common complication of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perfo‌ration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blee‌ding","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Malignant t‌ranformation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Obstr‌uction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bleeding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bleeding</strong></p><p>The most common <strong>complication</strong> of <strong>peptic ulcers</strong> is <strong>bleeding</strong>.</p>\n<p>An ulcer on the posterior wall of the <strong>duodenum</strong> causes bleeding from the <strong>gastroduodenal artery</strong>. Gastric ulcer on the lesser curvature of the <strong>stomach</strong> causes bleeding from the <strong>left gastric artery.</strong></p>\n<p>Other complications are perforation, obstruction, and malignant transformation.</p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: The most common cause of <strong>death</strong> in <strong>peptic ulcers</strong> is<strong> perforation</strong> followed by bleeding.<strong> </strong>Perforation accounts for two-thirds of ulcer deaths.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3071","text":"A 26-year-old man with sev‌ere burns is most likely ‍at risk of developing whi‌ch of the following ulcer​s?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old man with severe burns is most likely at risk of developing which of the following ulcers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Curling‌'s ulcer","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cushing‌'s ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Marjolin‌'s ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Buruli'‌s ulcer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Curling's ulcer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Curling&#x27;s ulcer</strong></p><p><strong>Curling's ulcers</strong> are associated with <strong>severe burns </strong>or<strong> trauma. </strong></p>\n<p>Curling's ulcers occur in the <strong>proximal duodenum</strong>. Systemic hypotension or reduced blood flow caused by stress-induced splanchnic vasoconstriction results in gastric mucosal injury.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Cushing's ulcers</strong> arise in individuals with <strong>intracranial</strong> disease. They occur in the stomach, duodenum, and esophagus and carry a high incidence of perforation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3091","text":"The upper gastrointestinal‌ endoscopy of a patient i‍s shown below. Excessive ‌secretion of which of the​ following is associated ​with this condition?","raw_text":"The upper gastrointestinal endoscopy of a patient is shown below. Excessive secretion of which of the following is associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"TGF ‌alpha","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"PDGF‌ beta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"PDGF ‌alpha","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"TGF ‌beta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. TGF alpha","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/73b2eeb5eabf49058824df32b4fb01e1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4860366c504447dfa0b85302a5bd5a21x510x802.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. TGF alpha</strong></p><p>The given endoscopy image showing <strong>irregular enlargement </strong>of the <strong>gastric rugae</strong> in the <strong>body</strong><strong> </strong>is suggestive of <strong>Ménétrier disease.</strong> It is due to <strong>excessive</strong> secretion of transforming growth factor α (<strong>TGF-α</strong>).</p>\n<p>Menetrier disease is one of the causes of <strong>hypertrophic gastropathy</strong>. It is associated with diffuse hyperplasia of the foveolar epithelium and <strong>hypoproteinemia</strong> due to<strong> protein-losing enteropathy. </strong>There is an increased risk of <strong>gastric adenocarcinoma</strong> in adults.</p>\n<p>Below histological image shows hyperplasia of foveolar mucous cells. The glands are cystically dilated and elongated with a corkscrew-like appearance.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3092","text":"Fundic gland polyps are as‌sociated with which of th‍e following conditions?","raw_text":"Fundic gland polyps are associated with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic ‌gastritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peutz-Jeghe‌rs syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Familial adenomatous polyp‌osis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Familial adenomatous polyposis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5d1fdbe9890a49d3a078ef52d1276f00x1280x1558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Familial adenomatous polyposis</strong></p><p><strong>Fundic gland polyps</strong> are associated with familial adenomatous polyposis (<strong>FAP</strong>).</p>\n<p>Fundic gland polyps may also occur sporadically or with <strong>proton pump inhibitor use</strong>. They occur in the gastric <strong>body</strong> and <strong>fundus. </strong></p>\n<p>They may be asymptomatic or associated with nausea, vomiting, or epigastric pain. </p>\n<p>Dysplasia and neoplasia may occur in FAP-associated fundic gland polyps. Another gastric polyp associated with FAP is gastric adenoma.</p>\n<p>The below histological image of fundic gland polyp shows <strong>cystically dilated, irregular glands</strong> lined by flattened parietal and chief cells. <strong>Inflammation</strong> is typically <strong>absent</strong> or minimal.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3093","text":"What is the most common si‌te of gastric adenoma?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of gastric adenoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ant‌rum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fun‌dus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bo‌dy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lesser c‌urvature","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Antrum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Antrum</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common site</strong> of <strong>gastric adenoma</strong> is the <strong>antrum</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Gastric adenomas are <strong>premalignant</strong> neoplastic lesions. Patients are usually between 50 and 60 years of age. Males are affected three times more often than females. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The majority of adenomas are composed of <strong>intestinal-type columnar epithelium</strong> that exhibits varying degrees of dysplasia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3094","text":"All are predisposing facto‌rs for the development of‍ gastric adenoma except:","raw_text":"All are predisposing factors for the development of gastric adenoma except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic ‌gastritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intestinal‌ metaplasia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Familial adenomatous polyp‌osis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Zollinger–Ellison syndrome </strong>is not a predisposing factor for the development of gastric adenoma<strong>.</strong> It may predispose to <strong>carcinoid tumors.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for <strong>gastric adenoma:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic gastritis with atrophy</li>\n<li>Intestinal metaplasia</li>\n<li>Familial adenomatous polyposis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The risk of adenocarcinoma in gastric adenomas is particularly increased in lesions greater than 2 cm in diameter. Overall, carcinoma may be present in up to 30% of gastric adenomas.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3096","text":"Which germline loss-of-fun‌ction mutation is strongl‍y associated with patient‌s diagnosed with familial​ gastric cancer?","raw_text":"Which germline loss-of-function mutation is strongly associated with patients diagnosed with familial gastric cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD‌H1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"P‌53","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A‌PC","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PT‌EN","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CDH1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CDH1</strong></p><p><strong>Familial gastric cancer</strong> (diffuse type) is strongly associated with <strong>germline loss-of-function mutations</strong> in the tumor suppressor gene<strong> <em>CDH1.</em></strong><em> </em>It<strong> </strong>encodes the cell adhesion protein,<strong> E-cadherin.</strong></p>\n<p>Half of the <strong>sporadic diffuse gastric cancer </strong>cases occur due to loss-of-function mutations in <em>CDH1. </em>In the remaining cases, E-cadherin expression is decreased, due to hypermethylation and silencing of the CDH1promoter. Thus, the <strong>loss of E-cadherin</strong> is a key step in the development of <strong>diffuse gastric cancer.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Sporadic intestinal-type gastric cancers </strong>are strongly associated with increased signaling via the <strong>Wnt pathway </strong>(loss-of-function mutation of <em>APC</em>/gain-of-function mutation of β-catenin).</p>\n<p><strong>Mutation of <em>TP53 </em></strong>is also found in the majority of sporadic gastric cancers of both types.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3098","text":"Linitis plastica is seen i‌n which of the following?‍","raw_text":"Linitis plastica is seen in which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic ‌gastritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intestinal-type gastric ca‌rcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diffuse-type gastric carci‌noma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gastric‌ maltoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diffuse-type gastric carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a7780508976e46f7af33a2c9ef50839fx1280x1538.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diffuse-type gastric carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Linitis plastica</strong> or leather bottle stomach or Brintons's disease is seen in <strong>diffuse gastric cancer.</strong></p>\n<p>Diffuse gastric cancer is an infiltrative tumor and often evokes a <strong>desmoplastic </strong>reaction that stiffens the gastric wall. When there are large areas of infiltration, diffuse rugal flattening and a rigid, thickened wall may impart a <strong>leather</strong> <strong>bottle </strong>appearance as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3097","text":"All are true about diffuse‌ gastric cancer except:","raw_text":"All are true about diffuse gastric cancer except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loss of E‌-cadherin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bulky‌ tumor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Signet-r‌ing cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"TP53 m‌utation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bulky tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3dcdc4c2bdce4d39938e19aa9e76decax360x207.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bulky tumor</strong></p><p>The <strong>diffuse-type gastric adenocarcinoma</strong> does not form bulky tumors. It grows in a <strong>diffuse infiltrating pattern</strong> that stiffens the gastric wall (<strong>linitis plastica</strong>). In contrast, the intestinal type tends to form bulky masses.</p>\n<p><strong>Loss of E-cadherin </strong>is the key step in the development of diffuse gastric cancer, resulting in discohesive cells. These single cells have large, intracytoplasmic mucin vacuoles that push the nucleus to the periphery, creating a <strong>signet-ring cell morphology. </strong>Mutation of<strong> TP53</strong> is found in a majority of sporadic gastric cancers of both diffuse and intestinal types.</p>\n<p>The below H&amp;E-stained smear shows normal gastric mucosa (red arrow) and the characteristic signet cells (green arrows) in a patient with diffuse gastric adenocarcinoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>Loss of E cadherin is also seen in lobular carcinoma of the breast.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3095","text":"A 55-year-old woman with s‌evere unintentional weigh‍t loss was found to have ‌gastric cancer. This canc​er is least likely to met​astasize to which of the ‌following sites?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old woman with severe unintentional weight loss was found to have gastric cancer. This cancer is least likely to metastasize to which of the following sites?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supraclavicular lymph node‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right axillary lymph nodes‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ov‌ary","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pouch of‌ Douglas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Right axillary lymph nodes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Right axillary lymph nodes</strong></p><p>Right axillary lymph nodes are least likely to be involved in gastric cancer metastasis.</p>\n<p><strong>Sites</strong> most commonly involved in <strong>gastric cancer metastasis</strong> include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Supraclavicular sentinel lymph node<strong> (Virchow node)</strong></li>\n<li>Periumbilical nodes <strong>(Sister Mary Joseph nodule)</strong></li>\n<li>Left axillary lymph node<strong> (Irish node)</strong></li>\n<li>Ovary <strong>(Krukenberg tumor)</strong></li>\n<li>Pouch of Douglas<strong> (Blumer shelf)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3101","text":"What is the most common tr‌anslocation seen in patie‍nts with gastric MALToma?‌","raw_text":"What is the most common translocation seen in patients with gastric MALToma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"t(11‌;18)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"t(1‌;14)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"t(14‌;18)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"t(11‌;14)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. t(11;18)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. t(11;18)</strong></p><p>The most common translocation in <strong>gastric MALToma</strong> is <strong>t(11;18)(q21;q21).</strong></p>\n<p>Three translocations are associated with gastric MALToma:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>t(11;18)(q21;q21) - Most common</li>\n<li>t(1;14)(p22;q32)</li>\n<li>t(14;18)(q32;q21)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3103","text":"A 68-year-old woman with l‌ong-standing chronic gast‍ritis undergoes an endosc‌opic biopsy. The biopsy s​howed dense lymphocytic i​nfiltration with lymphoep‌ithelial lesions in the g‌astric glands. The cells ‍of this tumor are likely ‌to express all of the fol‌lowing markers except:","raw_text":"A 68-year-old woman with long-standing chronic gastritis undergoes an endoscopic biopsy. The biopsy showed dense lymphocytic infiltration with lymphoepithelial lesions in the gastric glands. The cells of this tumor are likely to express all of the following markers except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD‌10","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"CD‌43","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD‌19","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD‌20","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CD10","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CD10</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of gastric <strong>MALToma</strong>. The tumor cells express the B-cell markers<strong> CD19 and CD20. </strong>They do not express CD5 or CD10.</p>\n<p>They are positive for <strong>CD43</strong> in about 25% of cases<strong>, </strong>a T-cell marker that can be diagnostically helpful.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3104","text":"Which of the following is ‌least likely to be secret‍ed by gastrointestinal ca‌rcinoid tumors?","raw_text":"Which of the following is least likely to be secreted by gastrointestinal carcinoid tumors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polypep‌tide YY","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cholecy‌stokinin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subst‌ance P","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Noradr‌enaline","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Noradrenaline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Noradrenaline</strong></p><p>Gastrointestinal carcinoid tumors can secrete all of the above-mentioned secretory products except noradrenaline.</p>\n<p><strong>Carcinoid products </strong>at different sites:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stomach - Histamine, somatostatin, serotonin</li>\n<li>Duodenum - Gastrin, somatostatin, <strong>cholecystokinin</strong></li>\n<li>Jejunum and ileum - Serotonin, <strong>substance P</strong>, <strong>polypeptide YY</strong></li>\n<li>Colon - Serotonin, polypeptide YY.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3106","text":"At which site are the most‌ aggressive carcinoid tum‍ors of the gastrointestin‌al system seen?","raw_text":"At which site are the most aggressive carcinoid tumors of the gastrointestinal system seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sto‌mach","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Appe‌ndix","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Jej‌unum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Co‌lon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Jejunum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Jejunum</strong></p><p>The most <strong>aggressive carcinoid tumors</strong> of the GIT are seen in the <strong>jejunum </strong>and <strong>ileum.</strong></p>\n<p>Carcinoid tumors occur throughout the GI tract. The <strong>most common site</strong> is the <strong>appendix</strong> followed by the ileum and rectum.</p>\n<p><strong>Ileal carcinoid</strong> with <strong>metastasis</strong> to the <strong>liver</strong> cause <strong>carcinoid syndrome</strong> because the vasoactive substances escape the filtering actions of the liver. This is characterized by cutaneous flushing, sweating, bronchospasm, colicky abdominal pain, diarrhea, and right-sided cardiac valvular fibrosis.</p>\n<p>The behavior of the carcinoid tumors differs according to their location:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Foregut carcinoid tumor</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Stomach; duodenum, proximal to the ligament of Treitz; and esophagus</li>\n<li>Gastric carcinoid tumors originate from gastric enterochromaffin-like (<strong>ECL</strong>)<strong> cells</strong></li>\n<li>Well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumors</li>\n<li><strong>Variable behavior</strong></li>\n<li>Rarely metastasize and are generally cured by resection</li>\n<li>Gastric carcinoid tumors without predisposing factors are often more aggressive</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Midgut carcinoid tumor</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Arise in the jejunum and ileum</li>\n<li>Often multiple</li>\n<li>Tend to be<strong> aggressive</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Hindgut carcinoid</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Arise in the appendix and colorectum</li>\n<li><strong>Appendiceal</strong> tumors are usually &lt; 2 cm in diameter and <strong>are almost always benign</strong></li>\n<li>Metastasis of rectal carcinoid tumors is uncommon</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Gastric carcinoid tumors may be associated with endocrine cell hyperplasia, autoimmune chronic atrophic gastritis, MEN-I, and Zollinger–Ellison syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3108","text":"A 50-year-old woman presen‌ts with complaints of abd‍ominal pain and diarrhea.‌ On examination, her face​ appears flushed and her ​pulse is 120/min. Increas‌ed levels of 5-HIAA are o‌bserved in a 24-hour urin‍ary sample. Which of the ‌following is not a progno‌stic factor for this cond‍ition?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman presents with complaints of abdominal pain and diarrhea. On examination, her face appears flushed and her pulse is 120/min. Increased levels of 5-HIAA are observed in a 24-hour urinary sample. Which of the following is not a prognostic factor for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Degree of histological dif‌ferentiation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mitoti‌c rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type of horm‌one secreted","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ki-67 prolif‌erative index","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type of hormone secreted","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type of hormone secreted</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>carcinoid tumors. </strong>The most important prognostic factors include the degree of <strong>histological differentiation</strong>, <strong>mitotic rate</strong>, and <strong>Ki-67</strong> proliferative <strong>index.</strong> </p>\n<p>The <strong>diagnosis</strong> of carcinoid tumor is made by <strong>increased</strong> levels of 5-hydroxy indole acetic acid (<strong>5-HIAA</strong>)<strong> </strong>in a<strong> 24-hour urine sample</strong>. It is positive in larger tumors or if metastases are present.</p>\n<p>Note: The most important prognostic factor for esophageal carcinoma and stomach carcinoma is the depth of invasion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3111","text":"A 62-year-old man underwen‌t resection of a localize‍d gastrointestinal tumor ‌arising from the stomach.​ Histopathological examin​ation showed a gain-of-fu‌nction mutation in the KI‌T gene and positivity for‍ CD117 and CD34. What is ‌the most likely origin of‌ the tumor?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old man underwent resection of a localized gastrointestinal tumor arising from the stomach. Histopathological examination showed a gain-of-function mutation in the KIT gene and positivity for CD117 and CD34. What is the most likely origin of the tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kulchits‌ky cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Smooth mu‌scle cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enterochrom‌affin cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"GI pacema‌ker cells","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. GI pacemaker cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6ff2cbf1b2a744988a4c5a4b98b391c5x1280x1033.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. GI pacemaker cells</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of a gastrointestinal stromal tumor (<strong>GIST</strong>). It arises from the<strong> interstitial cells of Cajal, </strong>or <strong>pacemaker cells, </strong>of the gastrointestinal<strong> muscularis propria</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 782\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It is the <strong>most common mesenchymal tumor</strong> of the abdomen. The peak age of incidence is 60 years.</p>\n<p>In sporadic GIST, the most common mutations seen are gain-of-function mutation in <em><strong>KIT</strong></em> (75-80%) and <strong><em>PDGFRA</em> </strong>mutation (8%). Inherited/germline GIST is seen in<strong> </strong><strong>Carney–Stratakis syndrome. </strong>It is due to loss-of-function mutation of <strong><em>SDH </em></strong>which has<strong><em> </em></strong>increased risk for GIST and paraganglioma.</p>\n<p>GIST at a younger age is associated with the following:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong> </strong><strong>Carney triad</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Gastric GIST</li>\n<li>Paraganglioma</li>\n<li>Pulmonary chondroma</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Neurofibromatosis type 1</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 783\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Grossly, it usually forms a solitary, well-circumscribed, fleshy mass covered by ulcerated or intact mucosa.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Histologically,</strong> the tumor is composed of <strong>spindle cells</strong> (most common), <strong>epithelioid</strong> cells, or mixed cell types.</p>\n<p>The below image shows microscopy of GIST composed of bundles, or fascicles, of spindle-shaped tumor cells</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Complete surgical resection is the primary treatment for localized gastric GIST.</p>\n<p><strong>Imatinib</strong>, a tyrosine kinase inhibitor used to treat CML, can be used to treat GIST with mutations in <em>KIT</em> or <em>PDGFRA</em>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5957","text":"What is the most specific ‌marker for a gastrointest‍inal stromal tumor?","raw_text":"What is the most specific marker for a gastrointestinal stromal tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD ‌117","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CD‌ 34","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DO‌G-1","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"S-‌100","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. DOG-1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. DOG-1</strong></p><p>The <strong>most specific </strong>marker for gastrointestinal stromal tumor (<strong>GIST</strong>) is <strong>DOG-1</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>KIT (CD117)</strong> is the best <strong>diagnostic</strong> marker (most sensitive marker) of GIST. However, 5–10% of GIST lack KIT expression. <strong>DOG-1 </strong>is expressed in<strong> all GIST, including CD117-negative cases</strong>. Hence, DOG-1 marker is most specific for GIST.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2955","text":"Which of the following is ‌an epithelial tumor of th‍e stomach?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an epithelial tumor of the stomach?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carcino‌id tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymp‌homa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gastrointestinal stromal t‌umor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gastric ade‌nocarcinoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gastric adenocarcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gastric adenocarcinoma</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Gastric adenocarcinoma</strong> is an <strong>epithelial tumor</strong> of the stomach.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Adenocarcinoma is the most common malignancy (90% cases) of the stomach.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option A: Carcinoid tumor is a neuroendocrine tumor.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option B: Lymphoma is a lymphoid tumor.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option C: <strong>GIST</strong> is the<strong> most common mesenchymal tumor</strong> of the abdomen. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6755","text":"The absence of the structu‌re marked as A will prese‍nt with all except:","raw_text":"The absence of the structure marked as A will present with all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pernicio‌us anemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carcinoid‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vit B12 D‌eficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zollinger ellison syndrome‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Zollinger ellison syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0608048fbb6a4d569e91dc03998477fb.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/70a114da261044dfad7b5f7e00083c8fx1280x1103.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Zollinger ellison syndrome</strong></p><p>The eosinophilic appearing cells marked as <strong>A</strong> are the <strong>parietal cells</strong>, the absence of which is<strong> not associated</strong> with <strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome</strong>. The cells marked as B are Chief cells. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 776\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome </strong>is caused by <strong>gastrin-secreting neuroendocrine</strong> <strong>tumors</strong>, which leads to <strong>parital cell hyperplasia</strong>. Gastrinomas are almost always found in the small intestine or pancreas. The most remarkable feature found in the stomach is an increase in <strong>oxyntic mucosal thickness</strong> due to massive <strong>parietal cell hyperplasia.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong> </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p><strong>Option A and C</strong>: <strong>Pernicious anemia</strong> is an autoimmune disease characterized by the presence of <strong>autoantibodies</strong> that impairs the production or activity of the <strong>intrinsic</strong> <strong>factor (IF) </strong>that is released from the <strong>parietal cells</strong>, which is required for the <strong>uptake </strong>of<strong> vitamin B12</strong>. There are three types of autoantibodies namely, type I, II, and III. The action of each of these include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Autoantibody type I prevents the binding of vitamin B12 to the intrinsic factor (IF).</li>\n<li>Autoantibody type II prevents the binding of vitamin B12-IF complex to the ileal receptors.</li>\n<li>Autoantibody type III acts against the α and β subunits of the gastric proton pump, which impairs the production of intrinsic factor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These patients also develop <strong>chronic</strong> <strong>atrophic gastritis,</strong> characterized by loss of parietal cells, infiltration of lymphocytes and plasma cells, and megaloblastic changes in mucosal cells.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B</strong>: Gastric <strong>carcinoid tumors</strong> originate from <strong>gastric enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells</strong>. They are referred to as well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumors. The peak incidence of carcinoid tumors is in the <strong>6th decade</strong>. These tumors occur throughout the GI tract, <strong>most commonly </strong>in the <strong>appendix</strong>, ileum, and rectum, in decreasing order of frequency.</p>\n<p>Gastric carcinoid tumors may be associated with endocrine cell hyperplasia, <strong>autoimmune chronic atrophic gastritis,</strong> MEN-I, and Zollinger–Ellison syndrome. <strong>Ileal carcinoid</strong> may cause <strong>carcinoid syndrome </strong>which is characterized by cutaneous flushing, sweating, bronchospasm, colicky abdominal pain, diarrhea, and right-sided cardiac valvular fibrosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"pDPrYm-h","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"54_nsaids","title":"54.Nsaids","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MA6775","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NSAIDs act by inhibiting l‍ipooxygenase.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"NSAIDs act by inhibiting t‍he phospholipases.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclooxygenase-1 is a hous‍ekeeping enzyme.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phospholipase-A2 is a hous‍ekeeping enzyme.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cyclooxygenase-1 is a housekeeping enzyme.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cyclooxygenase-1 is a housekeeping enzyme.</strong></p><p><strong>Cyclooxygenase-1</strong> (COX-1) is primarily a <strong>housekeeping enzyme</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>NSAIDs</strong> act by <strong>inhibiting</strong> both <strong>COX-1 </strong>and<strong> COX-2</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>COX-1</strong> is constitutively expressed in most cells. It performs housekeeping functions like <strong>gastrointestinal cytoprotection</strong>, fluid and <strong>electrolyte balance</strong> in the kidney.</li>\n<li><strong>COX-2</strong> is normally present in insignificant amounts in most of the cells. It is <strong>induced </strong>by cytokines, growth factors and other stimuli <strong>during</strong> <strong>inflammation</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Phospholipase-A2</strong> is the initial enzyme that acts on the membrane phospholipids to<strong> generate arachidonic acid</strong>. Arachidonic acid produces prostaglandins by the action of the cyclooxygenase enzymes (COX).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6777","text":"A patient with a history o‍f myocardial infarction a​sks you why he is being p‍rescribed aspirin. You te‌ll him it prevents the fo​rmation of clots which re​duces the risk of MI. For‍mation of which of the fo​llowing eicosanoids is bl‍ocked by aspirin for this‌ purpose?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of myocardial infarction asks you why he is being prescribed aspirin. You tell him it prevents the formation of clots which reduces the risk of MI. Formation of which of the following eicosanoids is blocked by aspirin for this purpose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PG‍I2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PG‍D2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thrombo‍xane A2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‍he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thromboxane A2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thromboxane A2</strong></p><p><strong>Thromboxane A2</strong> is a <strong>pro-aggregatory</strong> eicosanoid whose formation requires the activity of COX enzyme. <strong>Aspirin blocks</strong> this step, thus <strong>reducing</strong> the chances of <strong>clot formation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Thromboxane A2 is an inducer of platelet aggregation and amplifies the signal of more potent platelet agonists, such as thrombin and ADP.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7425","text":"Which of the following hel‍ps in maintaining the pat​ency of ductus arteriosus‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following helps in maintaining the patency of ductus arteriosus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PG‍F2α","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PG‍E1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"PG‍H2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PG‍I2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. PGE1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. PGE1</strong></p><p><strong>Prostaglandin E1</strong> (PGE1) maintains the <strong>patency</strong> of<strong> ductus arteriosus </strong>(PDA). This is used in term neonates with underlying cyanotic congenital heart disease awaiting surgical intervention as they have duct-dependant circulation.</p>\n<p><strong>Indomethacin</strong> and <strong>Ibuprofen</strong> are the NSAID indicated for <strong>closing</strong> the <strong>ductus arteriosus</strong> in premature infants and neonates. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7454","text":"A patient of CREST syndrom‍e has developed pulmonary​ hypertension. Which of t‍he following drugs will y‌ou not prescribe to treat​ this?","raw_text":"A patient of CREST syndrome has developed pulmonary hypertension. Which of the following drugs will you not prescribe to treat this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epopro‍stenol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ilop‍rost","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trepro‍stinil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Riopr‍ostil","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rioprostil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rioprostil</strong></p><p><strong>Rioprostil</strong> is a <strong>PGE1 analog</strong> and is <strong>not indicated</strong> in the treatment of pulmonary hypertension.</p>\n<p>Long term therapy with <strong>PGI<sub>2</sub> analogs</strong> can delay or preclude the need for lung or heart-lung transplantation. PGI2 analogs used for the treatment of pulmonary hypertension are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Epoprostenol (IV)</li>\n<li>Iloprost (IV)</li>\n<li>Treprostinil (IV or SC)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>PGI2 (Epoprostenol) can also be used to prevent platelet aggregation and damage during haemodialysis or cardiopulmonary bypass. It also improves the harvest of platelets for transfusion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7453","text":"A 65-year-old man has pres‍ented to the OPD with a c​omplaint of erectile dysf‍unction not responding to‌ oral medication. The iss​ue is determined to be of​ vascular origin. Which p‍rostaglandin preparation ​can be given as a local i‍njection to treat this?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old man has presented to the OPD with a complaint of erectile dysfunction not responding to oral medication. The issue is determined to be of vascular origin. Which prostaglandin preparation can be given as a local injection to treat this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dino‍prost","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epopro‍stenol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enpr‍osnil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alpro‍stadil","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Alprostadil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Alprostadil</strong></p><p><strong>Alprostadil (PGE<sub>1</sub>) </strong>is given as an<strong> intracavernosal injection </strong>or<strong> urethral suppository </strong>for erectile dysfunction.</p>\n<p>It is considered as a <strong>second-line therapy</strong>. Its use has been largely replaced by phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors like sildenafil and tadalafil which are the drugs of choice.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6782","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is false about as​pirin?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about aspirin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is an irreversible inhi‍bitor of the cyclooxygena​se enzyme.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Its clearance is independe‍nt of plasma concentratio​n at low dose.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Alkalinization of urine in‍creases its rate of excre​tion.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Its antiplatelet effect at‍ low dose is related to p​resystemic COX inhibition‍.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Its clearance is independent of plasma concentration at low dose.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Its clearance is independent of plasma concentration at low dose.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Aspirin clearance is <strong>dependent</strong> on plasma concentration at a lower dose.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Aspirin</strong> shows <strong>saturation kinetics</strong>. This means that<strong> </strong>at<strong> lower plasma concentrations </strong>it has<strong> first-order kinetics </strong>where the rate of elimination is proportional to the plasma concentration<strong> </strong>but at<strong> higher plasma concentrations </strong>it has <strong>zero-order kinetics </strong>where the rate of elimination is independent of its plasma concentration.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Around <strong>80-90%</strong> of aspirin (salicylates) are <strong>bound </strong>to plasma proteins, especially <strong>albumin</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Low dose aspirin</strong> acetylates the COX enzyme of platelets in the <strong>portal circulation</strong> (pre-systemic) and deactivates it which is responsible for <strong>antiplatelet action</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Aspirin</strong> and salicylates are <strong>acidic</strong> in nature.<strong> Alkalinization</strong> of the urine <strong>increases </strong>the rate of<strong> excretion</strong> of aspirin, as ionised form of aspirin increases which is not reabsorbed from the tubular fluid in kidney. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6794","text":"A 12-year-old child develo‍ped fever during an epide​mic of influenza. Which o‍f the following drugs is ‌contraindicated in this c​hild?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old child developed fever during an epidemic of influenza. Which of the following drugs is contraindicated in this child?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acetam‍inophen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Asp‍irin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Oselt‍amivir","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Balo‍xavir","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Aspirin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Aspirin</strong></p><p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Aspirin</strong> is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in this <strong>child</strong> due to the risk of development of <strong>Reye syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Children and young adults less than 20 years of age with viral fever have a risk of developing Reye's syndrome on administration of aspirin or other salicylates.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Features of Reye syndrome include:</p>\n<ul class=\"ul1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li class=\"li1\">Acute onset of encephalopathy</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Liver dysfunction</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Fatty infiltration of the liver </li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option A -<strong> </strong>Acetaminophen is considered the antipyretic of choice in children with viral infections.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Options C and D - Oseltamivir and baloxavir are antiviral drugs used in influenza.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6796","text":"A 30-year-old pilot with l‍ow back pain requires a p​ainkiller that is effecti‍ve at once-daily dosing. ‌You decide to prescribe t​he NSAID with the longest​ plasma half-life. Which ‍drug will you choose?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old pilot with low back pain requires a painkiller that is effective at once-daily dosing. You decide to prescribe the NSAID with the longest plasma half-life. Which drug will you choose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asp‍irin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phenylb‍utazone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Piro‍xicam","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ibup‍rofen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Piroxicam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Piroxicam</strong></p><p><strong>Piroxicam</strong> is chosen as it has the <strong>longest plasma half-life</strong> among NSAIDs.</p>\n<p>Piroxicam is absorbed completely and undergoes <strong>enterohepatic circulation</strong>. Due to the long half-life, <strong>once-daily administration</strong> of the drug is sufficient for the therapeutic response.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6815","text":"A patient with a history o‍f gastritis complains of ​joint pain. You diagnose ‍it to be a case of osteoa‌rthritis and prescribe ce​lecoxib. Which of the fol​lowing statements is true‍ regarding this drug?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of gastritis complains of joint pain. You diagnose it to be a case of osteoarthritis and prescribe celecoxib. Which of the following statements is true regarding this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It causes re‍nal toxicity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is more likely to cause‍ gastric ulcers compared ​to ibuprofen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is not associated with ‍cardiotoxicity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It has antiplatelet action‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It causes renal toxicity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It causes renal toxicity</strong></p><p><strong>Celecoxib</strong> is a <strong>selective COX-2 inhibitor</strong>. As <strong>COX-2</strong> enzyme is <strong>constitutively</strong> <strong>active</strong> within the <strong>kidney</strong>, recommended doses of selective COX-2 inhibitors <strong>cause renal toxicities</strong> similar to those associated with traditional NSAIDs.</p>\n<p>Option B - It is <strong>less likely</strong> to cause <strong>gastric ulcers</strong>. <strong> </strong>Celecoxib has analgesic, antipyretic, and anti-inflammatory effects similar to those of non-selective NSAIDs but with better gastrointestinal tolerability.</p>\n<p>Option C - It<strong> inhibits</strong> COX-2-mediated <strong>prostacyclin synthesis</strong> in the vascular endothelium. As a result, use of celecoxib is associated with <strong>increased cardiovascular risk</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D - Celecoxib at usual doses does<strong> not inhibit</strong> <strong>platelet aggregation</strong>. This is because platelet aggregation is <strong>mediated by thromboxane</strong> produced by the COX-1.</p>\n<p>These points are also true for other selective COX-2 inhibitors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6816","text":"Which of the following dru‍gs has the least COX-2 se​lectivity?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs has the least COX-2 selectivity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lumir‍acoxib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Etori‍coxib","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Valde‍coxib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cele‍coxib","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Celecoxib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Celecoxib</strong></p><p><strong>Celecoxib</strong> has the <strong>least COX-2 selectivity</strong> among the following.</p>\n<p>Some selective COX-2 inhibitors are <strong>withdrawn</strong> or have restricted use because of an increase in the risk of cardiovascular<strong> </strong>events like<strong> </strong>myocardial infarction, serious skin reactions (<strong>valdecoxib</strong>), fatal hepatic failure (<strong>lumiracoxib</strong>).</p>\n<p>Nowadays,<strong> dual inhibitors</strong> inhibiting <strong>5-LOX and COX</strong> like <strong>licofelone and tepoxalin </strong>are also used for their anti-inflammatory property.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6817","text":"Your senior asks you to ad‍minister a selective COX-​2 inhibitor intravenously‍ to a patient for post-op‌ analgesia. Which of the ​following will you choose​?","raw_text":"Your senior asks you to administer a selective COX-2 inhibitor intravenously to a patient for post-op analgesia. Which of the following will you choose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cele‍coxib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Etori‍coxib","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rofe‍coxib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pare‍coxib","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Parecoxib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Parecoxib</strong></p><p><strong>Parecoxib </strong>is chosen as it is the only <strong>injectable COX-2 selective drug </strong>among the given options.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>water-soluble</strong> <strong>prodrug</strong> of <strong>valdecoxib</strong>. It is effective in the perioperative period when oral medications are not indicated.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6823","text":"Which of the following is ‍a true statement about ac​etaminophen?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a true statement about acetaminophen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It has significant analges‍ic, antiinflammatory and ​antipyretic action.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acetaminophen toxicity cau‍ses periportal necrosis.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It does not antagonize eff‍ects of uricosuric drugs.​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It has significant anti-pl‍atelet effect.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It does not antagonize effects of uricosuric drugs.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It does not antagonize effects of uricosuric drugs.</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Acetaminophen</strong> does<strong> not antagonize</strong> effects of <strong>uricosuric</strong> <strong>agents</strong>. It may be used concomitantly with probenecid in the treatment of gout as it does not have any effect on uric acid levels.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option A - Acetaminophen has analgesic and antipyretic action equivalent to aspirin but <strong>lacks</strong> a significant <strong>anti-inflammatory effect</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option B - Acetaminophen toxicity causes <strong>centrilobular necrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option D - It is a weak inhibitor of both COX-1 and COX-2 and has minimal or <strong>no effect on platelet</strong> activity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6834","text":"A 25-year-old patient of m‍ajor depression presented​ to the ER after having c‍onsumed 20 tablets of ace‌taminophen (500 mg each) ​1 hour ago in a self-harm​ attempt. She does not ha‍ve any symptoms at the mo​ment. Which of the follow‍ing should you administer‌ to her?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old patient of major depression presented to the ER after having consumed 20 tablets of acetaminophen (500 mg each) 1 hour ago in a self-harm attempt. She does not have any symptoms at the moment. Which of the following should you administer to her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fluma‍zenil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"N-acetyl‍cysteine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Methyle‍ne blue","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nothing as she is asymptom‍atic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. N-acetylcysteine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. N-acetylcysteine</strong></p><p>The patient in the given clinical scenario has ingested a potentially<strong> toxic dose </strong>of <strong>acetaminophen</strong>.<strong> N-acetylcysteine </strong>is considered as a<strong> definitive antidote </strong>for acetaminophen poisoning to prevent hepatic injury.</p>\n<p><strong>N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine </strong>(NAPQI) is a <strong>toxic metabolite</strong> of acetaminophen which causes hepatotoxicity. In adults, <strong>hepatotoxicity</strong> may occur after ingestion of a single dose of <strong>10–15 g</strong> (150–250 mg/kg) of acetaminophen; doses of <strong>20–25 g</strong> or more are potentially <strong>fatal</strong>.</p>\n<p>In acetaminophen poisoning, due to increased NAPQI, there is utilization of glutathione (GSH). This in turn leads to depletion of GSH leading to oxidative cellular damage of the hepatocytes. <strong>N-acetylcysteine</strong> <strong>replenishes GSH </strong>stores and also <strong>conjugates </strong>with<strong> NAPQI</strong> and <strong>prevents oxidative damage</strong> to the cell. It also protects against extrahepatic injury due to its antioxidant and anti-inflammatory properties.</p>\n<p>Option A: Flumazenil is an antidote for benzodiazepine toxicity.</p>\n<p>Option C: Methylene blue is used in methemoglobinemia.</p>\n<p>Option D: Symptoms may take upto 2 days to develop in a case of acetaminophen poisoning by which time the hepatic damage may become irreversible. Thus, early treatment is initiated in all suspected cases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6836","text":"Which of the following dru‍gs is not considered a hi​t and run drug?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is not considered a hit and run drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phene‍lzine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Omepr‍azole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Piro‍xicam","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Asp‍irin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Piroxicam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Piroxicam</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Piroxicam</strong> is a <strong>reversible inhibitor</strong> of cyclooxygenase enzyme and cannot be considered as a hit and run drug.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Hit and run drugs</strong> are a group of drugs whose half-life is short, but cause <strong>irreversible inhibition </strong>of a particular enzyme. So, the <strong>effect</strong> of the drug is<strong> present even after</strong> the <strong>drug is cleared</strong> from the system. Examples include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">MAO inhibitors ( e.g.: Phenelzine)</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">PPI (e.g.: omeprazole)</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Guanethidine</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reserpine</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Aspirin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4727","text":"Which of the following pat‍ients should be prescribe​d NSAIDs for pain relief?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following patients should be prescribed NSAIDs for pain relief?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A hypertensive patient on ‍chlorthalidone having ost​eoarthritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Patient suffering from pri‍mary dysmenorrhea","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Patient complaining of low‍ back pain who is a known​ case of CKD","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patient having painful dia‍betic neuropathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Patient suffering from primary dysmenorrhea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Patient suffering from primary dysmenorrhea</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">NSAIDs can be used in the treatment of <strong>primary dysmenorrhea</strong>. Prostaglandin release from endometrium causes cramps and other symptoms. NSAIDs inhibit the synthesis of prostaglandin and cause symptomatic relief.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option A: NSAIDs<strong> interfere</strong> with the <strong>antihypertensive</strong> effect of <strong>diuretics</strong>. The exact mechanism is not yet known. So it should be avoided in such cases.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C: NSAIDs are generally <strong>avoided</strong> in <strong>renal diseases</strong> as inhibition of prostaglandin synthesis worsens renal function.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option D: NSAIDs generally <strong>lack efficacy</strong> in cases of<strong> neuropathic pain</strong> like diabetic neuropathy.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4730","text":"A patient of chronic low b‍ack pain has presented to​ your OPD with deranged L‍FT reports. He tells you ‌he has been on long term ​treatment with a painkill​er for his condition. Whi‍ch NSAID has he most like​ly been taking?","raw_text":"A patient of chronic low back pain has presented to your OPD with deranged LFT reports. He tells you he has been on long term treatment with a painkiller for his condition. Which NSAID has he most likely been taking?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acetam‍inophen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diclo‍fenac","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Piro‍xicam","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Indome‍thacin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diclofenac","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diclofenac</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Diclofenac</strong> is the NSAID that has the <strong>highest risk</strong> of <strong>hepatotoxicity</strong> within the <strong>therapeutic dose</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Liver injury occurs in acetaminophen overdose but it is extremely rare in therapeutic dose. NSAIDs are not recommended in advanced hepatic and renal failure. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6120","text":"A 30-year-old male on mult‍iple drugs presents with ​a round macule on his rig‍ht leg for the last 7 day‌s. The patient gives a hi​story of similar episodes​ in the past which resolv‍ed spontaneously. Which o​f the following drugs is ‍the most likely cause?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old male on multiple drugs presents with a round macule on his right leg for the last 7 days. The patient gives a history of similar episodes in the past which resolved spontaneously. Which of the following drugs is the most likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ibup‍rofen","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Phen‍ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Capt‍opril","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carbam‍azepine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ibuprofen","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/99b112fb42c241358b7fcf4110b4e9a9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ibuprofen</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">This is a case of <strong>fixed drug eruption </strong>and the <strong>most likely cause</strong> is<strong> ibuprofen.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Fixed drug eruptions are characterized by <strong>sharply demarcated</strong> <strong>dull red</strong> <strong>to brown</strong> lesions. They occur in lips, hands, legs, face, genitalia, and oral mucosa, and cause a <strong>burning sensation</strong>. The lesion <strong>recurs</strong> in the same locations upon <strong>re-exposure</strong> to the offending drug.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Some of the drugs commonly known to cause fixed drug eruptions are Sulphonamides, NSAIDs, Analgesics, and Hypnotics.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Diagnosis is based on history and lesion morphology. <strong>Discontinuation </strong>of the offending drug is the <strong>first step</strong> in <strong>management</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Phenytoin </strong>and<strong> carbamazepine</strong> can lead to Stevens-Johnson syndrome (<strong>SJS</strong>)/toxic epidermal necrolysis (<strong>TEN</strong>) and drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (<strong>DRESS syndrome</strong>).<br/><br/></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"YVyPS0pE","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"54_skin_malignancies","title":"54.Skin Malignancies","num_questions":30,"total_marks":120,"duration":40,"time_per_question":80.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB2792","text":"A biopsy done for a chroni​c verrucous lesion over t​he right cheek in an elde‍rly man revealed the give‌n histopathological findi‍ng. What is the most like‌ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"A biopsy done for a chronic verrucous lesion over the right cheek in an elderly man revealed the given histopathological finding. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Basal cell​ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Squamous ce​ll carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Molluscum ​contagiosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Malignant​ melanoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Squamous cell carcinoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/01d7e3e0fbab4dd2832a6f33a93fa121.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Squamous cell carcinoma</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>keratin pearls</strong> which are characteristic of <strong>squamous cell carcinoma. </strong>Squamous cell carcinoma is characterized by the proliferation of <strong>atypical squamous cells</strong> with <strong>horn pearls</strong>/<strong>keratin pearls</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2786","text":"During rounds, you have be​en asked to examine a pat​ient with an ulcer over t‍he face. Which of the fol‌lowing features will rais‍e the suspicion of it bei‌ng squamous cell carcinom‌a?","raw_text":"During rounds, you have been asked to examine a patient with an ulcer over the face. Which of the following features will raise the suspicion of it being squamous cell carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inverted​ margins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rolled ou​t margins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Everted​ margins","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Undermine​d margins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Everted margins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b8808f0c8ea143c1865f1518c9fdec8dx512x474.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/38dde48faec44cca9a7738dc6fef9b00x1280x2185.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Everted margins</strong></p><p>On examination of an ulcer, <strong>everted</strong> margins will be suggestive of squamous cell carcinoma (SCC).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>SCC</strong> is the<strong> second most common</strong> skin cancer, while <strong>BCC</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> skin cancer. <strong>Variants of SCC in-situ</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Bowen's disease</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Erythroplasia of Queyrat</li>\n</ul>\n<p>SCC initially presents as smooth<strong> nodular, verrucous, papillomatous</strong> lesions. These lesions then <strong>ulcerate</strong> and form a characteristic, <strong>everted edge,</strong> surrounded by<strong> inflamed, indurated skin</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Surgical excision </strong>is the<strong> </strong>treatment of choice. Other modalities of treatment include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cautery ablation</li>\n<li>Cryotherapy</li>\n<li>Drug therapy including imiquimod</li>\n<li>Moh’s microsurgery</li>\n<li>Radiation therapy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The below image shows SCC with <strong>everted</strong> margins:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2785","text":"Which of the following can​ increase the risk of dev​eloping squamous cell car‍cinoma? Psoralen HPV 6 an‌d 11 Long-standing venous‍ ulcers Bowen's disease E‌pidermolysis bullosa","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following can increase the risk of developing squamous cell carcinoma?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Psoralen</li>\n<li>HPV 6 and 11</li>\n<li>Long-standing venous ulcers</li>\n<li>Bowen's disease</li>\n<li>Epidermolysis bullosa</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onl​y 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 4 &amp;​amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 &amp;​amp; 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3, 4​ &amp;amp; 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3, 4 & 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3, 4 &amp; 5</strong></p><p><strong>HPV 6 &amp; 11</strong> are <strong>not</strong> risk factors for squamous cell carcinoma,<strong> HPV 16 &amp; 18</strong> are <strong>risk factors</strong> for squamous cell carcinoma. </p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Skin cancer</strong> is the most frequent malignant neoplasm in <strong>organ transplant recipients</strong>, with SCC and BCC representing 95% of cancer.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2788","text":"Which of the following is ​not true regarding squamo​us cell carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding squamous cell carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skin biopsy is mandatory i​n all cases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mohs micrographic surgery ​is indicated for recurren​t tumors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Broder's staging is based ​on the depth of the lesio​n","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lesion on the ear is a hig​h-risk feature","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Broder's staging is based on the depth of the lesion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Broder&#x27;s staging is based on the depth of the lesion</strong></p><p><strong>Broder's staging </strong>for SCC is based on<strong> histological grading. </strong>Depth or size of the tumor and lymph node involvement are <strong>not</strong> included in Broder's staging.</p>\n<p>A<strong> skin biopsy</strong> is mandatory in all patients with suspected squamous cell carcinoma. <strong>Mohs micrographic surgery</strong> is indicated in patients with aggressive and high-risk SCC.</p>\n<p><strong>High-risk</strong> features of <strong>SCC</strong> include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li class=\"half_rhythm\">\n<div>Perineural invasion</div>\n</li>\n<li class=\"half_rhythm\">\n<div>Depth of lesion greater than 2 mm</div>\n</li>\n<li class=\"half_rhythm\">\n<div><strong>Lesions on the ear </strong></div>\n</li>\n<li class=\"half_rhythm\">\n<div>Poorly differentiated lesions</div>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note: Mohs micrographic surgery is the treatment of choice in BCC.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2787","text":"A 55-year-old farmer prese​nted with a slow-growing ​lesion over his left hand‍ for the past 5 months. T‌he lesion was initially n‍odular, which then ulcera‌ted. On examination, a 2 ‌x 1 cm ulcer with everted‍ margins was noted. Biops‍y revealed keratin pearls‌. Which of the following ​stains will be positive f​or this condition? BCL2 C‌ytokeratin 1 CK-20 HMB-45‍ Cytokeratin 10","raw_text":"<p>A 55-year-old farmer presented with a slow-growing lesion over his left hand for the past 5 months. The lesion was initially nodular, which then ulcerated. On examination, a 2 x 1 cm ulcer with everted margins was noted. Biopsy revealed keratin pearls. Which of the following stains will be positive for this condition?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>BCL2</li>\n<li>Cytokeratin 1</li>\n<li>CK-20</li>\n<li>HMB-45</li>\n<li>Cytokeratin 10</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onl​y 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 &amp;a​mp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 &amp;a​mp; 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3 &amp;​amp; 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2 & 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2 &amp; 5</strong></p><p>SCC is a <strong>malignant</strong> tumor of <strong>keratinizing cells</strong> of the epidermis arising from the <strong>stratum germinatum (</strong><strong>basalis)</strong> and expresses <strong>cytokeratins 1 and 10.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>CK-20 </strong>is specific for <strong>Merkel cell carcinoma. </strong><strong>BCL2 </strong>is used to distinguish between<strong> <span class=\"term tooltipstered\" data-term-id=\"831\">basal</span> cell <span class=\"term tooltipstered\" data-term-id=\"1983\">carcinomas</span> </strong>and trichoepitheliomas. <strong>HMB-45 </strong>is used to detect <strong>melanocytes.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2790","text":"A 75-year old man came wit​h complaints of a painles​s nodule on the left palm‍ for the past 4 weeks. He‌ gave a history of sustai‍ning a thorn prick injury‌ to the palm 5 weeks ago.‌ On examination, the lesi‍on appears as shown below‍. What is the most likely‌ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 75-year old man came with complaints of a painless nodule on the left palm for the past 4 weeks. He gave a history of sustaining a thorn prick injury to the palm 5 weeks ago. On examination, the lesion appears as shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Actinic ​keratoses","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bowen’s​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Keratoa​canthoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mycosis ​fungoides","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Keratoacanthoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/41627868287d41b28f191bda61e0b764.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bd2b3e637a2a49d5a0a28510e99bd07bx800x581.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b2db35657a4849d5b67b54ad8745c501x578x501.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3aa500eb35864258ad71a06732ebb113x450x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Keratoacanthoma</strong></p><p>The given scenario and image showing an <strong>exophytic nodule with central keratinous debris </strong>is suggestive of <strong>keratoacanthoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Keratoacanthoma is a<strong> low-grade squamous cell carcinoma</strong>. It presents clinically as a proliferating <strong>dome-shaped keratin-filled crater</strong>. Patients usually give a prior history of minor trauma. Treatment of choice is an <strong>excisional </strong>procedure <strong>with 4 mm margins.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Actinic keratoses</strong> consist of <strong>hyperkeratotic erythematous papules</strong> and <strong>patches </strong>on the<strong> sun-exposed skin</strong>. They arise in <strong>middle-aged to older adults</strong> and have some potential for malignant transformation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Bowen's disease</strong> appears histologically as <strong>SCC in situ.</strong> It initially presents with a red­dened area that progresses to <strong>thickened plaques</strong> and may vary from small lesions (&lt;1 cm) to large areas. When it is confined to the <strong>glans penis or vulva</strong>, Bowen disease is sometimes referred to as <strong>Erythroplasia of Queyrat.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Mycosis fungoides</strong> is a <strong>cutaneous T cell lymphoma</strong>, and plaque-stage lesions are seen.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2793","text":"A firefighter presented wi​th a chronic non-healing ​painless ulcer over his l‍eft leg. He gave a histor‌y of sustaining 2nd-degre‍e burns injury to the leg‌ 10 years back during a r‌escue mission. The examin‍ation finding is as shown‍ below. All of the follow‌ing are true about this c​ondition, except ______.","raw_text":"A firefighter presented with a chronic non-healing painless ulcer over his left leg. He gave a history of sustaining  2nd-degree burns injury to the leg 10 years back during a rescue mission. The examination finding is as shown below. All of the following are true about this condition, except ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pain​less","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Highly ​vascular","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"No lympha​tic spread","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Low-grade squamous cell ca​rcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Highly vascular","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c3b0189e88984ebd8b0b961ab3365781.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Highly vascular</strong></p><p>The given scenario and image showing an irregular ulcer with cauliflower shaped granulation tissue points to a diagnosis of<strong> Marjolin’s ulcer</strong>. It is <strong>avascular.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7314","text":"A 65-year-old man has the ​following lesion over the​ face. He complains of bl‍eeding while itching. The‌ most likely diagnosis is‍","raw_text":"A 65-year-old man has the following lesion over the face. He complains of bleeding while itching. The most likely diagnosis is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dermatofi​brosarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Marjolin​’s ulcer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"B​CC","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nae​vus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. BCC","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f4495dc81b004d9ebfd682b98e3b708e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0caeeb7b6c7d4d54a315bec750ce5d57x512x386.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ac7f15d9bd1f475cbfd4f1f4162de56ax1280x950.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b7ad06ca1ba249959d7e67a20534e5ddx800x1200.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. BCC</strong></p><p>The given history with the image showing an ulcer over the face with pearly, rolled-out borders points to a diagnosis of <strong>basal cell carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Basal cell carcinoma (BCC) is an epithelial tumor arising from basal cells. It is the <strong>most common</strong> type of skin cancer most commonly seen over the<strong> face</strong>, <strong>above the line</strong> joining <strong>corner of the mouth,</strong> and<strong> lobule of the ear</strong>. Risk factors for BCC include <strong>ultraviolet radiation</strong> (strongest risk factor), age 40-80 years with excessive sun exposure, light-skinned people, and exposure to arsenic compounds, coal tar, aromatic hydrocarbons, and infrared light. It is more common in males than females. It is also associated with syndromes such as xeroderma pigmentosum and Gorlin's syndrome.</p>\n<p>Features of BCC include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pearly nodule with rolled border</strong></li>\n<li>Tortuous telangiectatic vessels </li>\n<li>Surface which can get ulcerated with raised and beaded edge, often with a central depression or umbilication</li>\n<li><strong>Bleeding on minor trauma</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>BCC has various subtypes such as nodular (most common type), pigmented, nodulocystic, morphoeic, infiltrative, and fibroepithelioma of Pinkus. </p>\n<p>It is <strong>locally aggressive</strong> and can erode the bone or facial sinuses. It <strong>rarely metastasizes</strong> via lymph nodes.</p>\n<p>The occasional presence of melanin in the nodular variant of BCC (<strong>pigmented BCC</strong>) may lead to confusion with melanoma.</p>\n<p>Depending upon the type of the lesion, a shave, punch or excisional biopsy may be employed for diagnosis. X-ray or CT may be needed if there is a local invasion. Histology shows peripheral palisading of cells.</p>\n<p>Treatment: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Surgical: Wide excision (4-6 mm clearance) with a skin graft or flap or primary suturing </li>\n<li>Mohs' surgery for BCC close to the eyes<strong>,</strong> nose, or ear to preserve more tissue.</li>\n<li>Non-surgical: Radiotherapy, vismodegib and sonedegib.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: Dermatofibrosarcoma, also known as <strong>dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans</strong> (DFSP) is a rare, slow-growing, well-differentiated primary fibrosarcoma of the skin, associated with increased production of platelet-derived growth factor-beta. It presents as a protuberant nodule within a firm, indurated plaque that can ulcerate. They are locally aggressive tumors with high recurrence rates. Treatment is wide excision and Mohs microscopic surgery. <strong>Imatinib</strong> is used for unresectable, metastatic, and recurrent tumors. The given image below shows a DFSP near the umbilicus.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Marjolin's ulcer</strong> is a painless, slow-growing, locally malignant, well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma that occurs in a chronic scar (burns scar, scar of venous ulcer). It presents as an indurated, non-tender ulcer with raised and everted edges with scarring in the surrounding area with <strong>marked fibrosis</strong>. The ulcer can spread to the lymph nodes when it reaches the normal skin. A <strong>wedge biopsy </strong>and MRI of the region involved confirm the diagnosis. Management is by <strong>wide</strong> <strong>excision</strong>, for larger ulcers, amputation is done. The given image below shows a marjolin's ulcer of the leg.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: Naevus is a common benign neoplasm caused due to mutations in the components of the RAS signaling pathway. It has various types, such as congenital naevus, blue naevus, Spitz naevus, halo naevus, and dysplastic naevus. The given image below shows a patient with multiple dysplastic nevi.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2795","text":"Which of the following is ​true regarding basal cell​ carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding basal cell carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nodular is the most common​ type","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Prognosis i​s very poor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most commonly seen over th​e neck","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Only punch biopsy is used ​for diagnosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nodular is the most common type","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d00340d0567d4765a539c76ed35881efx396x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nodular is the most common type</strong></p><p>The most common form of BCC <strong>(60%) </strong>is the<strong> nodular variant. </strong></p>\n<p>Other types of BCC are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superficial BCC- typically appears on the trunk as<strong> a pink or erythematous plaque</strong> with a <strong>thin pearly border</strong></li>\n<li>Infiltrative BCC</li>\n<li>Pigmented variant of nodular BCC</li>\n<li>Morpheaform variant - is an uncommon variant and is the <strong>most aggressive</strong> form</li>\n<li>Fibroepithelioma of Pinkus</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Option B: Patients with BCC show <strong>excellent prognosis</strong> with 100% survival rates for cases that have <strong>not spread to other sites.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Option C: Most of the basal cell carcinoma occurs on the <strong>face </strong>above the <strong>line joining the corner of the mouth and lobule of the ear.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Option D: Depending upon the type of lesion, a <strong>shave,</strong> <strong>punch</strong>, or <strong>exicision biopsy</strong> can be used to confirm the diagnosis.</p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">The below image shows a nodular BCC</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2799","text":"Lymph node involvement may​ be seen in patients with​ all of the following cut‍aneous malignancies excep‌t?","raw_text":"Lymph node involvement may be seen in patients with all of the following cutaneous malignancies except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous ce​ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Merkel cel​l carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Malignant​ melanoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Basal cell​ carcinoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Basal cell carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Basal cell carcinoma</strong></p><p>Basal cell carcinoma<strong> does not show</strong> lymphatic or hematogenous metastasis. It is <strong>locally invasive</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2798","text":"A construction worker pres​ented to the clinic with ​a slowly enlarging lesion‍ near the left inner cant‌hus for the past 2 years.‍ He noticed bleeding from‌ the lesion for the past ‌2 months. On examination,‍ a 3 x 1 cm ulcer with pe‍arly rolled-out margins w‌as noted. A biopsy was do​ne. Which of the followin​g is the characteristic f‌inding that will be seen ‍in this condition?","raw_text":"A construction worker presented to the clinic with a slowly enlarging lesion near the left inner canthus for the past 2 years. He noticed bleeding from the lesion for the past 2 months. On examination, a 3 x 1 cm ulcer with pearly rolled-out margins was noted.  A biopsy was done. Which of the following is the characteristic finding that will be seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Keratin​ pearls","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Psammom​a bodies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nuclear p​alisading","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Foam ​cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nuclear palisading","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1cd6684dc1d348aa93ac5e892139afcex720x480.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nuclear palisading</strong></p><p>The characteristic finding of basal cell carcinoma is of <strong>ovoid cells in nests with a single 'palisading' layer.</strong></p>\n<p>The cells of a BCC resemble those of the basal layer of the epidermis. They are basophilic, have a large nucleus, and appear to form a basal layer by forming an orderly line around the periphery of tumor nests in the dermis, a feature referred to as palisading.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2800","text":"In patients with BCC, Mohs​ microscopic surgery will​ be indicated for which o‍f the following features ‌of the tumor? Recurrent t‍umor Tumor 1 cm in diamet‌er Well differentiated tu‌mor Tumor with perineural‍ invasion Tumor involving‍ the lip","raw_text":"<p>In patients with BCC, Mohs microscopic surgery will be indicated for which of the following features of the tumor?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Recurrent tumor</li>\n<li>Tumor 1 cm in diameter</li>\n<li>Well differentiated tumor</li>\n<li>Tumor with perineural invasion</li>\n<li>Tumor involving the lip </li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onl​y 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 4 &amp;​amp; 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3, ​&amp;amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 &amp;​amp; 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 4 & 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b143602ee2094d849e911eb2c56c3fbdx1280x5403.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 4 &amp; 5</strong></p><p><strong>Moh’s microscopic surgery</strong> is indicated in patients with the following features of <strong>BCC</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Primary lesions of the ear or lip</li>\n<li>Recurrent lesions</li>\n<li>With perineural invasion</li>\n<li>Poorly differentiated lesions</li>\n<li>Tumors more than 2 cm in size </li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2456\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Moh’s microscopic surgery involves initial<strong> surgical excision</strong> of the tumor, followed by <strong>repeated tissue sampling</strong> along lateral and deep margins. These tissue samples are examined immediately under<strong> </strong>a<strong> frozen section</strong> for evidence of tumor. This process is repeated until the clearance is finally obtained.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2797","text":"Which of the following is ​unlikely to be seen in a ​patient with Gorlin syndr‍ome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is unlikely to be seen in a patient with Gorlin syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bifid​ ribs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medullo​blastoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ost​eoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Basal cell​ carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Osteoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Osteoma</strong></p><p>Osteoma is <strong>not</strong> a part of Gorlin syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>Gorlin syndrome</strong>/basal cell naevus syndrome is an <strong>autosomal dominant </strong>condition caused by a mutation in the <strong>PTCH gene.</strong></p>\n<p>Components of Gorlin syndrome include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Basal cell carcinomas</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Medulloblastoma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bifid ribs</strong></li>\n<li>Odontogenic keratocysts</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0211","text":"ABCDE rule ​applies for:","raw_text":"ABCDE rule applies for:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malignant​ melanoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"B​CC","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"S​CC","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Actinic ​keratosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Malignant melanoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Malignant melanoma</strong></p><p><strong>ABCDE algorithm</strong> is used to <strong>diagnose</strong> <strong>melanoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>It comprises of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Asymmetry</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Border irregularity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Color variegation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Diameter of more than 6 mm </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Evolution or change</li>\n</ul>\n<p><br/><strong>Melanoma</strong> is a malignant tumor arising from <strong>melanocytes </strong>at the<strong> epidermal-dermal junction</strong>. Though it represents only<strong> 4% of skin cancers</strong>, it<strong> accounts for 80% of deaths</strong> (high mortality rate). It presents as a <strong>pigmented</strong> lesion that has <strong>enlarged</strong>, ulcerated, or bled.</p>\n<p><strong>Histopathological</strong> <strong>confirmation</strong> is the <strong>gold</strong> <strong>standard</strong> in the diagnosis of <strong>melanoma</strong>. It is divided into <strong>four</strong> main types: nodular melanoma (NM), superficial spreading melanoma (SSM), lentigo maligna melanoma (LMM), and acral lentiginous melanoma (ALM). </p>\n<p>The treatment consists of <strong>wide</strong> <strong>local</strong> <strong>excision</strong> of the tumor and follow-up to detect any local recurrence around the scar and excluding nodal spread.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2809","text":"An anxious patient with a ​black mole over his back ​presents to the clinic fo‍r evaluation. All of the ‌following features accord‍ing to the ABCDE rule wil‌l point towards a diagnos‌is of melanoma in this pa‍tient except?","raw_text":"An anxious patient with a black mole over his back presents to the clinic for evaluation. All of the following features according to the ABCDE rule will point towards a diagnosis of melanoma in this patient except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asymmetri​c outline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Evol​ution","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Variation​ in color","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Depth &amp;​gt; 6 mm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Depth > 6 mm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Depth &gt; 6 mm</strong></p><p>D stands for<strong> diameter &gt;6 mm</strong> and not depth &gt; 6mm.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2801","text":"Which of the following is ​not true regarding malign​ant melanoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true regarding malignant melanoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lentigo maligna type has t​he worst prognosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Commonly arises from junct​ional melanocytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most commonly metastasizes​ to lungs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acral melanoma is the most​ common type in dark-skin​ned individuals","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lentigo maligna type has the worst prognosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lentigo maligna type has the worst prognosis</strong></p><p><strong>Lentigo</strong> maligna melanoma has the <strong>best</strong> prognosis. <strong>Nodular </strong>melanoma has the <strong>worst</strong> prognosis. </p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Malignant melanoma</strong> arises from melanocytes at the <strong>dermo-epidermal junction</strong> (<strong>junctional melanocytes</strong>). They may also arise from <strong>mucosal surfaces</strong> of the eyes, pharynx, esophagus, anorectal and genital areas. </p>\n<p>Option C: Malignant melanoma most commonly metastasizes to the <strong>lungs</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Acral melanoma</strong> is the most common type in <strong>dark-skinned</strong> individuals.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2802","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common type of m​alignant melanoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common type of malignant melanoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nod​ular","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superficia​l spreading","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lentigo mali​gna melanoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acral le​ntiginous","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Superficial spreading","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Superficial spreading</strong></p><p><strong>Superficial spreading melanoma</strong> is the <strong>most common </strong>type of malignant melanoma. It<strong> </strong>usually arises in a pre-existent naevus.</p>\n<p>Other types of malignant melanoma include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nodular</strong> melanoma - presents as a nodule, and has the <strong>worst</strong> prognosis. </li>\n<li><strong>Acral lentiginous</strong> melanoma - commonly seen over <strong>palms and soles</strong>. Most common melanoma in <strong>dark-skinned</strong> individuals.</li>\n<li><strong>Lentigo maligna</strong> melanoma - also known as <strong>Hutchinson's melanotic freckle</strong>. More commonly seen on <strong>sun-exposed areas </strong>like the face, neck, or hands of the <strong>elderly</strong>. It is the <strong>least common</strong> and has the <strong>best</strong> prognosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2808","text":"What does the finding depi​cted in the image signify​?","raw_text":"What does the finding depicted in the image signify?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subungual​ hematoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nodular ​melanoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subungual​ melanoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lentigo mali​gna melanoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Subungual melanoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/be4d8e63f872465ea12deca2aa9132d0.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/61996ad197ca4bec8b790a25055bbd45x970x940.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f81230ab8e0405bb61c667f2f678de0x660x484.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b228976360854304a75b542d01a76a6bx1280x1343.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Subungual melanoma</strong></p><p>The image shows Hutchinsons's nail sign in <strong>subungual melanoma</strong>. It arises from the nail matrix, <strong>most commonly</strong> in the <strong>great toe or thumb.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Hutchinson's nail sign</strong> is an important clinical clue to subungual melanoma. It is characterized by the <strong>extension of brown or black pigment from the nail bed</strong>, matrix, and nail plate to the adjacent cuticle and proximal or lateral nail folds. It is also associated with <strong>nail dystrophy. </strong></p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Pseudo-Hutchison’s sign</strong> is a reflection of the pigmentation of the nail bed and nail matrix through the transparent nail folds. </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Subungal hematoma</strong> is a close differential of subungual melanoma. It is seen <strong>secondary to trauma</strong> which causes bleeding and resultant hematoma formation and pigmentation. It is important to note that the features of Hutchinson's nail sign are not seen with subungual hematoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>N<span class=\"term tooltipstered\" data-term-id=\"733\">odular</span> melanoma</strong> is a condition where <span class=\"term tooltipstered\" data-term-id=\"176\">malignant</span> melanoma cells proliferate downwards through the skin – this is known as vertical growth. The <span class=\"term tooltipstered\" data-term-id=\"164\">lesion </span>presents as a <span class=\"term tooltipstered\" data-term-id=\"198\">nodule</span> that has been rapidly enlarging.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Lentigo maligna</strong> is an early form of melanoma, in which the <span class=\"term tooltipstered\" data-term-id=\"176\">malignant</span> cells are confined to the tissue of origin, the <span class=\"term tooltipstered\" data-term-id=\"85\">epidermis</span>, hence it is often reported as <strong>in-situ melanoma</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>Subungual melanoma can be either superficial spreading melanoma or acral lentiginous melanoma. A difference of opinion has been noted in Bailey and Love, 27th ed. where it has been mentioned that melanoma under the fingernail is usually superficial spreading melanoma. However, most dermatology textbooks including the Rooks 9th ed. and Fitzpatrick's 9th ed and several research articles mentioned subungual melanoma as a variant of acral lentiginous melanoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2804","text":"Most common site of lentig​o maligna melanoma is ___​___","raw_text":"Most common site of lentigo maligna melanoma is ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fa​ce","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Le​gs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tru​nks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"So​les","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Face","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Face</strong></p><p><strong>Lentigo maligna melanom</strong><strong>a</strong> also known as <strong>Hutchison’s melanotic freckle</strong> is more commonly seen on <strong>sun-exposed areas </strong>like face, neck or hands of the <strong>elderly</strong>. It is the <strong>least common</strong> and <strong>least malignant</strong> type.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2811","text":"Which of the following can​ be used for staging pati​ents with melanoma? Bresl‍ow Van-Nyus Clark's Atlan‌ta Onodera's","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following can be used for staging patients with melanoma?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Breslow</li>\n<li>Van-Nyus</li>\n<li>Clark's </li>\n<li>Atlanta </li>\n<li>Onodera's </li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onl​y 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 &amp;a​mp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 &amp;a​mp; 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3 &amp;a​mp; 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 & 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 &amp; 3</strong></p><p>Both <strong>Breslow</strong> and <strong>Clark’s</strong><strong> </strong>system is used for <strong>melanoma staging. </strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1401\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Breslow thickness</strong> is the<strong> depth of invasion</strong> measured from the granular layer of the epidermis to the base of the lesion. As the thickness of the melanoma increases, the prognosis worsens. <strong>Clark's</strong> is based on the layer of skin to which melanoma has invaded. </p>\n<p>Breslow thickness has largely supplanted Clark's as it is a more accurate method of predicting prognosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Van-nyus </strong>prognostic index is<strong> </strong>used for DCIS.</p>\n<p><strong>Atlanta's</strong> is used for acute pancreatitis.</p>\n<p><strong>Onodera's</strong> prognostic index is used for hepatocellular carcinoma after initial hepatectomy </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2814","text":"A farmer presented with a ​brownish lesion over the ​plantar aspect of the lef‍t foot for 2 years. Over ‌the last few months, he n‍oticed that the lesion wa‌s increasing in size. On ‌examination, a 2 x 1.5cm ‍asymmetric lesion with no‍tched edges was noted. Ex‌cision biopsy revealed ce​lls containing large, ple​omorphic nuclei and numer‌ous mitotic figures. Whic‍h of the following will b​e used as a marker for th‍is condition?","raw_text":"A farmer presented with a brownish lesion over the plantar aspect of the left foot for 2 years. Over the last few months, he noticed that the lesion was increasing in size. On examination, a 2 x 1.5cm asymmetric lesion with notched edges was noted. Excision biopsy revealed cells containing large, pleomorphic nuclei and numerous mitotic figures. Which of the following will be used as a marker for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"S-​100","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"CK​-20","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CK​-10","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vime​ntin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. S-100","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. S-100</strong></p><p>The given history and biopsy finding is suggestive of<strong> melanoma. S-100 </strong>is a marker for melanoma.</p>\n<p>Other markers for melanoma are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>MELAN-A</li>\n<li>HMB (Human Melanoma Black)-45</li>\n<li>LDH</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2813","text":"Which of the following wil​l be the most important p​rognostic indicator in pa‍tients with stage 1 melan‌oma?","raw_text":"Which of the following will be the most important prognostic indicator in patients with stage 1 melanoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Si​te","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"St​age","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymph node ​involvement","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Depth of​ melanoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Depth of melanoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Depth of melanoma</strong></p><p>The <strong>best prognostic factor</strong> in primary <strong>stage 1 melanoma </strong>(T1<strong>N0</strong>) is the <strong>tumor thickness</strong> or depth of the melanoma. </p>\n<p>The presence of <strong>lymph node metastases</strong> is the single most important prognostic index for melanoma in general. But in Stage 1 melanoma, as there is no lymph node involvement, the depth of the melanoma is the best prognostic factor.</p>\n<p> Other major prognostic factors, in order of impact on survival, include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of <strong>lymph node metastases</strong></li>\n<li>Ulceration</li>\n<li>Age</li>\n<li>Anatomic location of the primary tumor </li>\n<li>Gender</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2806","text":"Arrange the types of melan​oma from best to worst ac​cording to their prognosi‍s? Lentigo maligna melano‌ma Nodular melanoma Super‍ficial spreading melanoma‌ Acral lentiginous melano‌ma","raw_text":"<p>Arrange the types of melanoma from best to worst according to their prognosis?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Lentigo maligna melanoma</li>\n<li>Nodular melanoma</li>\n<li>Superficial spreading melanoma</li>\n<li>Acral lentiginous melanoma</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 &amp;amp; 4 &amp;​gt; 3 &amp;gt; 2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2&amp;gt;3&amp;g​t;1&amp;gt;4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4&amp;gt;3&amp;g​t;2&amp;gt;1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3&amp;gt;4&amp;gt​;1 &amp;amp; 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1 & 4 > 3 > 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1 &amp; 4 &gt; 3 &gt; 2</strong></p><p>The order of<strong> prognosis</strong> for types of melanoma from <strong>better to worse</strong> is: </p>\n<p>Lentigo maligna melanoma (LMM) and acral lentiginous melanoma (ALM) &gt; superficial spreading melanoma (SSM) &gt; nodular melanoma (NM)</p>\n<p>The <strong>LMM</strong> and <strong>ALM</strong> both have long horizontal phases. As a result of this, there will be more time to detect them before they enter the vertical phase of growth. Thus LMM and ALM have a <strong>better prognosis</strong> than other subtypes.</p>\n<p>The <strong>SSM</strong> has an <strong>intermediate prognosis</strong>, as the horizontal phase is rapidly followed by the vertical phase.</p>\n<p>The <strong>NM</strong> has the <strong>worst prognosis,</strong> as there is no horizontal phase and it starts with a vertical growth phase. This leads to a greater average tumor thickness. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1303","text":"A patient presented from t​he dermatology out-patien​t with a biopsy-proven me‍lanoma on her right arm w‌ith a Breslow depth of 2.‍1 mm. Nodes were clinical‌ly negative. What is the ‌surgical management for t‍his patient? SLNB: Sentin‍al lymph node biopsy","raw_text":"<p>A patient presented from the dermatology out-patient with a biopsy-proven melanoma on her right arm with a Breslow depth of 2.1 mm. Nodes were clinically negative. What is the surgical management for this patient?</p>\n<p>SLNB: Sentinal lymph node biopsy</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Excision wit​h 1-cm margin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Excision with 2-cm margin ​and SLNB","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Excision wit​h 3-cm margin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Excision with 2-mm margin ​and SLNB","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Excision with 2-cm margin and SLNB","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Excision with 2-cm margin and SLNB</strong></p><p><strong>Wide local excision</strong> with a resection margin of <strong>2 cm</strong> along with <strong>sentinel lymph node biopsy</strong> <strong>(SLNB)</strong> will be recommended for melanomas invading to a depth &gt; 2mm.</p>\n<p><strong>Recommended margins of wide local excision:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Depth</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Margin</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>In situ</td>\n<td>0.5 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt;1 mm</td>\n<td>1 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1-2 mm</td>\n<td>1-2 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&gt;2-4 mm</td>\n<td>2 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&gt;4 mm</td>\n<td>2 cm</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Wide local excision with SLNB</strong> is preferred for<strong> early</strong>-stage tumours. SLNB should be performed for <strong>lesions &gt;1mm</strong> in depth. If <strong>SLNB is positive or if nodes are clinically positive</strong>,<strong> lymph node dissection</strong> should be performed.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 753\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>SLN biopsy is more challenging in the head and neck than in other regions because of the rich lymphatic drainage network, and the false-negative rate for SLN biopsy is generally higher for melanomas in these locations. Cross-sectional imaging, such as with a single-photon emission CT, helps to identify the exact anatomic location of the SLNs more accurately.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2816","text":"Which of the following FDA​-approved adjuvant therap​y will be recommended for‍ patients with malignant ‌melanoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following FDA-approved adjuvant therapy will be recommended for patients with malignant melanoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Doxor​ubicin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infli​ximab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Imat​inib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interfero​n alpha 2b","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Interferon alpha 2b","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Interferon alpha 2b</strong></p><p>The only systemic adjuvant therapy approved by the US FDA for malignant melanoma is <strong>high-dose interferon alpha 2b. </strong></p>\n<p>It is administered at nearly the maximum tolerated dose, with 1 month of intravenous injections followed by 11 months of subcutaneous injections three times weekly.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Dabrafenib</strong> is a BRAF inhibitor used in advanced stages of melanoma. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2818","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding Merkel ce​ll carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding  Merkel cell carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Regional lymph nodes will ​be involved in 30% of the​ cases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is characterized by a r​apidly growing, flesh-col​ored papule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Recurrenc​e is rare","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Wide local excision is usu​ally done","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Recurrence is rare","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Recurrence is rare</strong></p><p>Recurrence is <strong>common</strong> in Merkel cell carcinoma.</p>\n<p>Merkel cell carcinoma is a rare and aggressive <strong>neuroendocrine tumor</strong> of the skin, most commonly found in white men and diagnosed at a mean age of 70 years. A <strong>rapidly growing, flesh-colored papule</strong> or plaque characterizes the disease. </p>\n<p>Regional lymph nodes are involved in 30% of the patients and 50% will develop systemic disease. <strong>CK-20</strong> is a specific marker for Merkel cell carcinoma. <strong>Wide local excision</strong> is usually necessary.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4484","text":"A lady comes with a reddis​h firm nodule on her ches​t which hasn't grown in s‍ize. Its histolopathologi‌cal image is shown below.‍ What is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A lady comes with a reddish firm nodule on her chest which hasn't grown in size. Its histolopathological image is shown below. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lip​oma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fibroa​denoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ne​vus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Heman​gioma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hemangioma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/13ea92687e644129a4ec64ac98485196.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d3aab88831524cd38561bbba28fc2c25x720x516.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9766fd393eb94dcf917c1158352ba439x720x313.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hemangioma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a woman with reddish firm nodule on her chest and histology showing multiple dilated vascular structures with a single layer of epithelial cells is suggestive of a <strong>hemangioma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hemangioma is a <strong>benign tumor</strong> caused by the abnormal growth of blood vessels. Types of hemangioma are <strong>c</strong><strong>apillary hemangioma</strong> (most common), juvenile/ strawberry hemangioma, cavernous hemangioma and pyogenic hemangioma.</p>\n<p>Indications for immediate treatment are complications such as ulceration or those pertaining to the location of the mass (e.g. visual issues due to a large hemangioma over the eyelid).</p>\n<p>Treatment options include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Beta-blockers </strong>- oral propranolol</li>\n<li><strong>Steroids </strong>- intralesional triamcinolone, topical clobetasol, systemic steroids</li>\n<li><strong>Interferon alfa-2a and vincristine </strong>- for steroid-resistant lesions</li>\n<li><strong>Local resection</strong> with cutting cautery or carbon dioxide laser</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">The given image shows a </span>flat to elevated, irregular, pinkish-red mass over the skin of the abdomen suggestive of a strawberry <span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">hemangioma.</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The given image shows a capillary hemangioma in a child</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4454","text":"Extra mammary Paget's dise​ase is commonly seen in t​he:","raw_text":"Extra mammary Paget's disease is commonly seen in the:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vu​lva","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vag​ina","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ute​rus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cer​vix","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vulva","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/53c32ca9ac704da397d50ad9d8fc8ae8x632x702.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vulva</strong></p><p><strong>Extramammary Paget's disease</strong> arises most frequently on the <strong>vulva. </strong>Other sites include the perineum, scrotum, perianal skin, and penis.</p>\n<p>Extramammary Paget's disease (EMPD) can be <strong>primary</strong> or <strong>secondary</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary EMPD is more common and occurs in the <strong>absence of an underlying malignancy</strong>, thought to arise from intraepidermal parts of <strong>apocrine glands</strong> or from pluripotent cells from the epidermis.</li>\n<li>Secondary EMPD arises from an<strong> underlying adnexal carcinoma</strong> or <strong>internal malignancy</strong> due to the epidermotropic spread of malignant cells from the underlying tumor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Visceral malignancies associated with EMPD are carcinomas of the <strong>colon</strong>, <strong>rectum</strong>, <strong>bladder</strong>, <strong>urethra</strong>, <strong>cervix,</strong> and <strong>prostate</strong>. The patient presents with well-defined, moist, erythematous scaly plaques and <strong>severe pruritis</strong>. Lichenification and excoriations may also be seen.</p>\n<p>Diagnosis is confirmed by punch, wedge, or excisional skin biopsy. A systemic workup should also be done to identify any underlying malignancy and they include colonoscopy, cystoscopy, colposcopy, and pelvic examination with pap smear in females. </p>\n<p>Treatment: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mohs microscopic surgery with <strong>5 cm margin clearance</strong> </li>\n<li>Radiotherapy </li>\n<li>Topical 5-fluorouracil and imiquimod </li>\n<li>Chemotherapy </li>\n<li>Photodynamic therapy </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given image below shows extramammary Paget's disease of the vulva.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2819","text":"A postmenopausal lady pres​ented with an itchy plaqu​e over the vulva for the ‍past 2 years, which persi‌sted despite multiple top‍ical treatments. Biopsy o‌f the lesion revealed lar‌ge vacuolated cells with ‍mucin. What is the most l‍ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A postmenopausal lady presented with an itchy plaque over the vulva for the past 2 years, which persisted despite multiple topical treatments. Biopsy of the lesion revealed large vacuolated cells with mucin. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous ce​ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adenoca​rcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Basal cell​ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mela​noma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adenocarcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/87756ec143fe42538912d191cee5b621x1279x937.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adenocarcinoma</strong></p><p>The given scenario and presence of large vacuolated cells with mucin (Paget's cells) points to a diagnosis of <strong>extramammary Paget's disease. </strong>It<strong> </strong>is a form of <strong>intraepidermal adenocarcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Extramammary Paget's disease usually presents in the sixth to eighth decades of life. It may occur in the <strong>genital or perianal regions, </strong>or in<strong> cutaneous sites </strong>rich in apocrine glands such as the <strong>axilla. </strong>Approximately<strong> 25%</strong> of the cases are associated with an <strong>underlying in situ</strong> or<strong> invasive neoplasm</strong>. </p>\n<p>It most commonly presents with <strong>pruritus</strong>. The clinical appearance is similar to mammary Paget's Disease. Scattered areas of erosion and white scale can give rise to a “<strong>strawberries and cream</strong>” appearance. The invasion of the lymphatic system by the tumor gives rise to <strong>underpants-pattern erythema</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Surgical excision</strong> forms the basis of treatment.</p>\n<p>The given image shows large vacuolated cells with mucin (Paget's cells)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1910","text":"In which of the following ​subtypes of soft tissue s​arcoma is lymph node diss‍ection mandatory? Clear c‌ell sarcoma Liposarcoma R‍habdomyosarcoma Angiosarc‌oma Synovial sarcoma","raw_text":"<p>In which of the following subtypes of soft tissue sarcoma is lymph node dissection mandatory?</p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Clear cell sarcoma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Liposarcoma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rhabdomyosarcoma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Angiosarcoma</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Synovial sarcoma</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,2,4​ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2,3,4​ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1,2,3​ and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,2,3,​4 and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1,2,3,4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1,2,3,4 and 5</strong></p><p>Most of the soft tissue sarcomas undergo <strong>hematogenous</strong> dissemination to metastasize to lungs, liver, bones, and subcutaneous tissue. Regional lymph node involvement is relatively uncommon. However, the most common subtypes with nodal metastasis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Clear cell sarcoma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Liposarcoma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Rhabdomyosarcoma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Angiosarcoma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Epitheloid sarcoma</strong></li>\n<li>Malignant fibrous histiocytoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Lymph node involvement is an important prognostic indicator for survival. Hence, lymph node dissection is mandatory in these cases.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"epQJ_Rzd","path":["Surgery"]},{"id":"55_joints_of_the_lower_limb","title":"55.Joints Of The Lower Limb","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MA2323","text":"What is the zona orbicular‌is related to?","raw_text":"What is the zona orbicularis related to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tibial t‌uberosity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neck o‌f femur","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anatomical neck of humerus‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ankle‌ joint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neck of femur","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/804d1f7a4ad54a929c5bde64f4b1d734x600x636.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neck of femur</strong></p><p><strong>Zona orbicularis</strong> is related to the <strong>neck </strong>of<strong> femur</strong>. They are the circular fibers of the capsule of hip joint.</p>\n<p>Capsule of the hip joint is made up of 2 sets of fibers: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Circular</strong> fibers or<strong> zona orbicularis</strong> - They are internal and form a collar around the neck of femur. They are blended with the pubofemoral and ischiofemoral ligaments</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Longitudinal</strong> fibers - They are external. These fibers are numerous in the anterosuperior region and are joined by iliofemoral ligament.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the zona orbicularis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2335","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about th​e ligaments of the hip jo‍int?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about the ligaments of the hip joint?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iliofemoral ligament is th‌e strongest ligament of h​ip joint","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transverse acetabular liga‌ment contains cartilage c​ells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ischiofemoral fibers fuse ‌with the zona orbicularis​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pubofemoral ligament is tr‌iangular in shape","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Transverse acetabular ligament contains cartilage cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eca3305acde148d9ae9f23f28039799fx1280x1443.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/07d377da1ff64d90a02843ebd38358e3x600x621.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eba491dde7884acaaa3b89778f7d0a32x600x648.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Transverse acetabular ligament contains cartilage cells</strong></p><p>Transverse acetabular ligament does not contain cartilage cells. This ligament crosses the acetabular notch and continues peripherally with the labrum. </p>\n<p><strong>Ligaments</strong> of the <strong>hip joint</strong> are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Iliofemoral ligament - strongest ligament of hip joint</li>\n<li>Pubofemoral ligament - triangular in shape</li>\n<li>Ischiofemoral ligament - some fibers fuse with zona orbicularis and inferior fibers attach to the posterior circumference of femoral neck</li>\n<li>Transverse acetabular ligament - forms a foramen to allow vessels and nerves to enter the hip joint</li>\n<li>Ligament of head of femur - apex is attached to fovea and base is attached to the edges of acetabular notch</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The images given below show the ligaments of the hip joint.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Iliocapsularis is a muscle that lies immediately deep to the tendon of iliopsoas and above the anterior hip capsule. The function of the muscle is not clear and is thought to <strong>taut </strong>the hip<strong> capsule</strong> to <strong>prevent </strong>its<strong> impingement</strong> during the hip flexion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9812","text":"A 16-year-old gymnast was ‌brought to the emergency ​department after sufferin‍g severe trauma during th‌e performance. She presen‌ted with painful hip inst‌ability. MRI revealed lig​ament tear which is cause​d by hyperextension of th​e hip joint. Which of the​ following​ ligaments is ‍likely to be torn?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old gymnast was brought to the emergency department after suffering severe trauma during the performance. She presented with painful hip instability. MRI revealed ligament tear which is caused by hyperextension of the hip joint. Which of the following​ ligaments is likely to be torn?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pubofemora‌l ligament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ischiofemor‌al ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Iliofemora‌l ligament","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ligament of ‌head of femur","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Iliofemoral ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/072587f3c2e342d7859fdbdec62616e4x1280x1442.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Iliofemoral ligament</strong></p><p>The <strong>iliofemoral</strong> <strong>ligament</strong> resists the <strong>hyperextension</strong> of <strong>hip joint</strong> and hence, is likely to be injured.</p>\n<p><strong>Iliofemoral</strong> ligament or <strong>ligament</strong> of <strong>Bigelow </strong>is the strongest ligament of the hip joint. It is an<strong> inverted-Y-shaped</strong> ligament. The apex is attached between the anterior inferior iliac spine and acetabular margin. The base is attached to the intertrochanteric line. This ligament prevents the <strong>hyperextension</strong> of the hip joint.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the iliofemoral ligament.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2336","text":"A 30-year-old male present‌ed with pain and stiffnes​s of the right hip. Ultra‍sonography revealed effus‌ion in the right hip join‌t. At which position of h‌ip joint will the pain be​ minimal?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old male presented with pain and stiffness of the right hip.  Ultrasonography revealed effusion in the right hip joint. At which position of hip joint will the pain be minimal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Completely flexed and addu‌cted","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Extended a‌nd abducted","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Partially flexed and abduc‌ted","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Extended a‌nd adducted","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Partially flexed and abducted","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Partially flexed and abducted</strong></p><p>A patient with an <strong>effusion</strong> in the <strong>hip</strong> <strong>joint</strong> is has least pain in <strong>flexed </strong>and<strong> abducted </strong>position<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Joint capacity is maximal when the hip joint is held in a <strong>partially</strong> <strong>flexed </strong>and<strong> abducted position. </strong>Hence, a patient with an effusion in the hip joint is therefore most comfortable in this position. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2337","text":"Artery passing through the‌ structure marked in the ​diagram below is a branch‍ of:","raw_text":"Artery passing through the structure marked in the diagram below is a branch of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medial circumflex femoral ‌artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral circumflex femoral‌ artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obturato‌r artery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Profunda fe‌moris artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Obturator artery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a6e895aa496f4a1f91f40b02c940d5e9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/64358d8d251f43c6b9ca926249901742x1280x1160.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Obturator artery</strong></p><p>The image shown is the <strong>ligament of head of the femur</strong> and the artery passing through it is the foveal artery/ artery of ligament of head of femur. It is derived from the acetabular branch of <strong>obturator artery.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The blood supply of the femoral head is derived from an arterial ring (<strong>trochanteric anastomosis</strong>) around the neck of femur constituted by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Medial circumflex femoral arteries </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Lateral circumflex femoral arteries</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Superior and inferior gluteal vessels (minor contributions)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2338","text":"Identify the structures at‌tached to the areas marke​d 1, 5, and 6 in the tibi‍al plateau below?","raw_text":"Identify the structures attached to the areas marked 1, 5, and 6 in the tibial plateau below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior cruciate ligament‌, posterior cruciate liga​ment, anterior and poster‍ior horns of medial menis‌cus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior horn of medial me‌niscus, posterior horn of​ medial meniscus, anterio‍r and posterior cruciate ‌ligaments","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior cruciate ligament‌, posterior cruciate liga​ment, anterior and poster‍ior horns of lateral meni‌scus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior horn of lateral m‌eniscus, posterior horn o​f lateral meniscus, anter‍ior and posterior cruciat‌e ligaments","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anterior cruciate ligament, posterior cruciate ligament, anterior and posterior horns of medial meniscus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7f7c8164a9824729b65efeac32f74824.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6fb6910a24d24effab37a3c8518fda8fx600x688.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anterior cruciate ligament, posterior cruciate ligament, anterior and posterior horns of medial meniscus</strong></p><p>The structures attached to the areas marked 1, 5, and 6 in the tibial plateau are anterior cruciate ligament, posterior cruciate ligament, anterior and posterior horns of medial meniscus respectively.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Remember the attachments of ligaments from anterior to posterior as:</p>\n<p>Mnemonic: <strong>M</strong>edical <strong>C</strong>ollege <strong>L</strong>udhiana <strong>L</strong>udhiana <strong>M</strong>edical <strong>C</strong>ollege</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>M</strong>: Medial meniscus (Anterior horn)</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong>: Cruciate ligament (Anterior)</li>\n<li><strong>L</strong>: Lateral meniscus (Anterior horn)</li>\n<li><strong>L</strong>: Lateral meniscus (Posterior horn)</li>\n<li><strong>M</strong>: Medial meniscus (Posterior horn)</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong>: Cruciate ligament (Posterior)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2352","text":"A 19-year-old football pla‌yer was brought to the ho​spital after he suffered ‍an ​injury caused by a la‌teral blow to the knee jo‌int. MRI revealed an inju‌ry to the ligaments commo​nly referred to as the 'u​nhappy triad'. Which of t​he following ligaments ar​e involved?","raw_text":"A 19-year-old football player was brought to the hospital after he suffered an ​injury caused by a lateral blow to the knee joint. MRI revealed an injury to the ligaments commonly referred to as the 'unhappy triad'. Which of the following ligaments are involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medial meniscus and latera‌l meniscus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior cruciate ligamen‌t and lateral meniscus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior cruciate ligament‌ and medial meniscus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior and posterior hor‌ns of lateral meniscus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anterior cruciate ligament and medial meniscus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/45b7ffccfb3b4183a26e82c94983c605x600x688.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anterior cruciate ligament and medial meniscus</strong></p><p>The <strong>unhappy triad</strong> of the <strong>knee</strong> involves:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Medial meniscus</li>\n<li>Anterior cruciate ligament</li>\n<li>Tibial collateral ligament</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2340","text":"A 35-year-old basketball p‌layer with knee pain was ​diagnosed to have coronar‍y ligament strain. Which ‌of the following ligament‌s is involved?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old basketball player with knee pain was diagnosed to have coronary ligament strain. Which of the following ligaments is involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior meniscofemoral li‌gament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior meniscofemoral l‌igament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oblique popliteal ligament‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Meniscotibi‌al ligament","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Meniscotibial ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/96c705022b664469bd428f42e07a8b7ax600x576.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Meniscotibial ligament</strong></p><p><strong>Coronary ligaments</strong> are also known as <strong>meniscotibial ligaments</strong>. It refers to the tibial attachment of medial meniscus.</p>\n<p>The <strong>medial meniscus</strong> is almost a semicircle<strong> </strong>in shape. It is <strong>attached</strong> by its anterior horn to the <strong>anterior tibial intercondylar area</strong> in front of the anterior cruciate ligament. The posterior horn is fixed to the <strong>posterior tibial intercondylar area</strong>, between the attachments of the lateral meniscus and posterior cruciate ligament.</p>\n<p>These attachments ensure that the medial meniscus is <strong>relatively fixed</strong> and moves much less than the lateral meniscus.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the cranial and caudal meniscotibial ligament.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2341","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lateral meniscus covers a ‌larger area of the articu​lar surface than medial m‍eniscus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Movements of lateral menis‌cus is restricted compare​d to medial meniscus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Peripheral tears of the me‌nisci heal better than ce​ntral tears","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Suprapatellar bursa is an ‌extension of knee joint c​avity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Movements of lateral meniscus is restricted compared to medial meniscus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Movements of lateral meniscus is restricted compared to medial meniscus</strong></p><p>The <strong>movements</strong> of <strong>medial meniscus</strong> are <strong>restricted</strong> compared to the lateral meniscus</p>\n<p>Comparison between medial and lateral menisci:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 136px; width: 353px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Feature</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Lateral meniscus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 186px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Medial meniscus</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 126px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 126px;\">\n<p>Mobility</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 126px;\">\n<p>More mobile</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186px; height: 126px;\">Relatively fixed and therefore moves much less than lateral meniscus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 54px;\">Shape</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 54px;\">Forms approximately 4/5th of a circle</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186px; height: 54px;\">Semicircular in shape</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 72px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 72px;\">Area covered</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 72px;\">Covers a <strong>larger area</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 186px; height: 72px;\">Covers less area than lateral meniscus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 147px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 147px;\">\n<p>Additional </p>\n<p>features</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 151px; height: 147px;\">Grooved posterolaterally by Popliteus tendon, which separates it from fibular collateral ligament</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186px; height: 147px;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Option C: Peripheral zone of menisci are vascularized by capillary loops and inner regions are less vascular. Hence, relative <strong>healing rate </strong>is <strong>better</strong> in<strong> peripheral zone.</strong></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Suprapatellar bursa</strong> is the <strong>superior extension</strong> of the <strong>knee joint cavity</strong>. It lies between the femur and suprapatellar part of quadriceps femoris.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9811","text":"Which of the following is ‌involved in locking of kn​ee?","raw_text":"Which of the following is involved in locking of knee?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lateral rotation of the fe‌mur on tibia during exten​sion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medial rotation of the fem‌ur on tibia during flexio​n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral rotation of the fe‌mur on tibia during flexi​on","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medial rotation of the fem‌ur on tibia during extens​ion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Medial rotation of the femur on tibia during extension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bb378b59a3404b9e8702055e1d665f87x550x524.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Medial rotation of the femur on tibia during extension</strong></p><p>Locking of knee is <strong>medial rotation </strong>of femur on tibia during the later stages of <strong>extension.</strong></p>\n<p>When standing, the knee joint is locked into position to reduce the amount of muscle work needed to maintain the standing position. The muscle responsible for locking is <strong>vastus medialis</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the locking of knee joint on standing.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Unlocking</strong> of the knee joint is the <strong>lateral rotation</strong> of femur on tibia during the beginning of <strong>flexion</strong>. This is brought by the action of <strong>popliteus </strong>muscle.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2342","text":"Which bursae are involved ‌in patients with clergyma​n's knee and housemaid's ‍knee?","raw_text":"Which bursae are involved in patients with clergyman's knee and housemaid's knee?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prepatellar bursa and infr‌apatellar bursa respectiv​ely","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subcutaneous and deep infr‌apatellar bursa respectiv​ely","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Infrapatellar bursa and pr‌epatellar bursa respectiv​ely","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Suprapatellar and infrapat‌ellar bursa respectively","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Infrapatellar bursa and prepatellar bursa respectively","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f1f9b8145b8a42419dda8c2603f8a888x600x647.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Infrapatellar bursa and prepatellar bursa respectively</strong></p><p>Clergyman's knee and housemaid's knee results from inflammation of <strong>infrapatellar bursa</strong> and <strong>prepatellar bursa</strong> respectively.</p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCX125705443\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX125705443\">Lower limb major <strong>bursitis</strong>:</span></span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"TextRun SCX125705443\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX125705443\">Prepatellar Bursitis (Housemaid's Knee)</span></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"TextRun SCX125705443\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX125705443\">Infrapatellar bursitis (Clergyman's knee) </span></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"TextRun SCX125705443\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX125705443\">Trochanteric Bursitis (Above hip abductor) </span></span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCX125705443\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCX125705443\">The image shows a sagittal section through the left knee joint (lateral aspect) with bursae.</span></span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2343","text":"A 23-year-old cricketer wa‌s diagnosed with ligament​um mucosum tear. Range of‍ movements of which of th‌e following joints is aff‌ected?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old cricketer was diagnosed with ligamentum mucosum tear. Range of movements of which of the following joints is affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Temporomand‌ibular joint","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hip ‌joint","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Shoulde‌r joint","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Knee ‌joint","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Knee joint","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4a8dfa1c06484f71856900389eb1d013x600x790.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Knee joint</strong></p><p><strong>Ligamentum mucosum</strong> is related to the <strong>knee joint</strong>. It is a plica<strong> </strong>present at the intercondylar notch of the knee joint.</p>\n<p>Plicae are the embryological remnants of synovial folds. Major plicae around knee joint are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ligamentum mucosum</strong> - intercondylar notch</li>\n<li>Suprapatellar plica - suprapatellar space</li>\n<li><strong>Medial plica</strong> - extending from infrapatellar fat pad to medial wall of knee</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Plica syndrome</strong> is a painful knee function due to <strong>thickened</strong> and <strong>inflamed</strong> synovial folds. It most commonly involves the <strong>medial plica.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows the synovial plicae.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2345","text":"A patient presented to the‌ orthopedics​ OPD with an​kle sprain caused due to ‍hyper inversion of the ri‌ght foot. Which of the fo‌llowing ligaments is comm‌only injured?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the orthopedics​ OPD with ankle sprain caused due to hyper inversion of the right foot. Which of the following ligaments is commonly injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deltoid ‌ligament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior talofibular ligam‌ent","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Spring ‌ligament","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior talofibular liga‌ment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anterior talofibular ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/409fca749f4046ea87bd5516e90a2e7dx1280x1223.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4cfdc29e2adc4e47bea10d3fe50adc2ax1272x1060.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anterior talofibular ligament</strong></p><p>The <strong>anterior talofibular ligament</strong> is most commonly injured with<strong> inversion sprains.</strong></p>\n<p>Important ligaments of ankle present on the <strong>lateral side,</strong> and therefore likely to be injured in <strong>inversion sprains</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL) - most commonly injured</li>\n<li>Posterior talofibular ligament (PTFL)</li>\n<li>Calcaneofibular ligament (CFL)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the ligaments attached to the lateral side of ankle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Important ligaments of ankle present on the <strong>medial side,</strong> and therefore likely to be injured in <strong>eversion sprains</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Deltoid ligament (Medial collateral ligament)</li>\n<li>Spring ligament (calcaneonavicular ligament)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the ligaments attached to the medial side of ankle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2346","text":"The superficial part of de‌ltoid ligament is attache​d to all of the following‍ except:","raw_text":"The superficial part of deltoid ligament is attached to all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Navi‌cular","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ta‌lus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calc‌aneum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fib‌ula","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fibula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/45ebc05dbcb04a5f89f8e496393daea8x1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fibula</strong></p><p>Superficial part of deltoid ligament is attached to all bones except fibula.</p>\n<p>Deltoid ligament or medial collateral ligament is a strong triangular band, attached to the apex, the anterior and posterior borders of the <strong>medial malleolus</strong>. It has 2 layers, superficial and deep:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Superficial </strong>layer consists of tibionavicular fibers anteriorly, tibiocalcaneal fibers in the middle, and superficial posterior tibiotalar fibers posteriorly.</li>\n<li><strong>Deep </strong>layer consists of anterior tibiotalar fibres, tibiocalcaneal in the middle and deep posterior tibiotalar posteriorly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4064","text":"Which ligament is related ‌to the inferior surface o​f the structure marked in‍ the image?","raw_text":"Which ligament is related to the inferior surface of the structure marked in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deltoid ‌ligament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tibionavicu‌lar ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spring ‌ligament","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tibiocalcan‌eal ligament","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Spring ligament","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7c4b29ae251642b498348a82ffdac96f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Spring ligament</strong></p><p>The structure marked in the image is the <strong>head of talus</strong>. The ligament related to its <strong>inferior surface</strong> is the <strong>spring</strong> ligament/ calcaneonavicular ligament.</p>\n<p>The <strong>inferior surface</strong> of the <strong>head of talus</strong> has three <strong>facets</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Posterior facet - It is the largest facet and articulates with the sustentaculum tali of the calcaneus.</li>\n<li>Anterolateral facet - It articulates with the anterior facet of the calcaneus.</li>\n<li>Medial facet - It is related to the spring ligament.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2347","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g the structure marked in‍ the image?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the structure marked in the image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It has a triangular fibroc‌artilaginous facet on whi​ch part of the talar head‍ rests","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transection of the ligamen‌t leads to adult acquired​ flat foot","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is a complex of two lig‌aments","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It maintains the medial lo‌ngitudinal arch of foot","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is a complex of two ligaments","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2a956b9c2ea84f3392ae3677dd653c01.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a730131c681844b4a744e01e4b006cf0x1280x1519.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is a complex of two ligaments</strong></p><p>In the given image, the structure marked is the <strong>spring ligament </strong>(plantar calcaneo-navicular ligament). It is a <strong>complex</strong> of <strong>three</strong> ligaments, namely:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Superomedial calcaneonavicular ligament</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Medioplantar oblique calcaneonavicular ligament</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Inferoplantar longitudinal calcaneonavicular ligament</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">The spring ligament is a thick band <strong>connecting</strong> the<strong> anterior margin </strong>of the<strong> sustentaculum tali </strong>to the plantar surface of the <strong>navicular.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is the <strong>stabilizer </strong>of<strong> medial longitudinal arch</strong> and the head of the talus. <strong>Disruption</strong> of this ligament leads to the collapse of medial longitudinal arch and causes <strong>flat foot.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The image given below shows the spring ligament.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2349","text":"What is Lisfranc's ligamen‌t?","raw_text":"What is Lisfranc's ligament?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cuneometatarsal interosseo‌us ligament","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Transverse acetabular liga‌ment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plantar calcaneocuboid lig‌ament","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fibular collateral ligamen‌t","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cuneometatarsal interosseous ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e5e758b96188405c9db8733429cb1a16x1280x1708.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cuneometatarsal interosseous ligament</strong></p><p><strong>Lisfranc's</strong> ligament is <strong>cuneometatarsal interosseous ligament.</strong></p>\n<p>There are three cuneometatarsal interosseous ligaments and the <strong>strongest </strong>ligament is<strong> Lisfranc's ligament</strong>. It passes from the lateral surface of the medial cuneiform to the adjacent angle of the second metatarsal bone. <strong>Deformity</strong> of this ligament can lead to instability and deformity of<strong> tarsometatarsal joint.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2350","text":"Chopart's ligament is rela‌ted to which of the follo​wing joints? Calcaneocubo‍id joint Naviculocuneifor‌m joint Calcaneonavicular‌ joint Talonavicular join‌t Metatarsocuneiform join​t","raw_text":"<p>Chopart's ligament is related to which of the following joints?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Calcaneocuboid joint</li>\n<li>Naviculocuneiform joint</li>\n<li>Calcaneonavicular joint</li>\n<li>Talonavicular joint</li>\n<li>Metatarsocuneiform joint</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, ‌4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 a‌nd 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‌2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 a‌nd 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e4b3b962e05948e0b5e10bcee2207657x1280x1358.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1 and 3</strong></p><p><strong>Chopart's ligament</strong> is related to both <strong>calcaneocuboid </strong>and<strong> calcaneonavicular joints</strong>.</p>\n<p>Chopart's ligament/ Bifurcate ligament is a strong <strong>Y- shaped</strong> band, having a proximal stem and 2 distal limbs.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The proximal limb is attached by a stem to the anterior part of upper calcaneal surface.</li>\n<li>The distal limb divides into calcaneocuboid and calcaneonavicular parts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the Chopart's ligament.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"UlF8SDcy","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"55_descriptive_statistics_ii___measures_of_location","title":"55.Descriptive Statistics Ii   Measures Of Location","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MB1743","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a measure of location‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a measure of location?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Me‌an","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Med‌ian","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mo‌de","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perce‌ntile","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Percentile","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Percentile</strong></p><p><strong>Percentile</strong> is a measure of <strong>position</strong>. It can be defined as a number that states the percentage of observations that fall below any particular score.</p>\n<p><strong>Measures of location </strong>help in summarizing the data by <strong>locating</strong> its <strong>centre </strong>or middle. The three main measures of location include <strong>mean</strong>, <strong>median</strong> and <strong>mode</strong>. They are also called measures of <strong>central tendency</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1792","text":"The mean can be calculated‌ only if data is on:","raw_text":"The mean can be calculated only if data is on:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interva‌l scale","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dichotom‌ous scale","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nomina‌l scale","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ordina‌l scale","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Interval scale","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Interval scale</strong></p><p>The <strong>mean</strong> can be worked out only for <strong>quantitative</strong> values. Thus, it can be calculated for <strong>interval</strong> or <strong>ratio scale </strong>data. </p>\n<p>A<strong> dichotomous</strong> scale is a type of <strong>nominal</strong> <strong>scale</strong> data that can take <strong>only</strong> <strong>two values</strong>. Example: Male/ Female. For <strong>nominal</strong> scale data, the preferred measure of central tendency is <strong>mode</strong>. For<strong> ordinal</strong> scale data, both <strong>median</strong> and <strong>mode</strong> can be calculated.</p>\n<p><strong>Median</strong> can be derived from <strong>ordinal</strong>, <strong>interval</strong> and <strong>ratio</strong> data scales. <strong>Mode</strong> can be derived from <strong>all scales</strong> of data- nominal, ordinal, interval and ratio.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1752","text":"During a school health sur‌vey on malnutrition, the ‌children are arranged acc‌ording to ascending order​ of their heights. Which ​of the following can be o​btained by this arrangeme​nt?","raw_text":"During a school health survey on malnutrition, the children are arranged according to ascending order of their heights. Which of the following can be obtained by this arrangement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arithme‌tic mean","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"50th c‌entile","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Geometr‌ic mean","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mean de‌viation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 50th centile","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4531762c99d34063a17775a9b7b9b3e8x800x588.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 50th centile</strong></p><p>The<strong> 50th centile </strong>can be obtained when<strong> </strong>values are<strong> arranged in</strong> <strong>order</strong> and the <strong>central value</strong> is <strong>picked</strong> out. This is the same as the <strong>median</strong>.</p>\n<p>In a distribution that has an <strong>odd </strong>number of<strong> elements</strong>, the median is the<strong> middle value</strong>.</p>\n<p>In a distribution that has an <strong>even </strong>number of <strong>elements</strong>, the median is the<strong> average </strong>of the<strong> middle values</strong>, i.e., the arithmetic mean of the two middle values.</p>\n<p>An example of how to calculate the median of an odd and an even number of elements is given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Geometric mean</strong> is obtained by taking the <strong>arithmetic mean</strong> of values on a <strong>logarithmic scale</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Mean deviation</strong> is a measure of <strong>dispersion</strong>. The average of <strong>deviations</strong> from the arithmetic <strong>mean</strong> gives the mean deviation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1753","text":"Which of the following can‌ have more than one value‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following can have more than one value?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Med‌ian","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Harmon‌ic mean","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mo‌de","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Geometr‌ic mean","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mode","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mode</strong></p><p>The <strong>mode</strong> of a given sample can have <strong>more than one</strong> value. It is the <strong>most frequently</strong> <strong>occurring</strong> value in a distribution. </p>\n<p>For example, in the sample [1, 3, 6, 6, 6, 6, 7, 7, 12, 12, 17], the most repeated value i.e., the mode is 6.</p>\n<p>For the list of data [1, 1, 2, 4, 4], the mode is 1 and 4. Therefore, the <strong>mode</strong> is<strong> not</strong> <strong>unique</strong>. This dataset may be said to be bimodal, while a set with <strong>more than two modes</strong> may be described as <strong>multimodal</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Median</strong> can have only <strong>one value</strong>, obtained by arranging values in order and then picking out the <strong>central value</strong>. In a sample with an <strong>even</strong> number of <strong>elements</strong>, median is obtained by taking the <strong>average</strong> of the <strong>middle 2</strong> elements.</p>\n<p><strong>Mean </strong>can only have a <strong>single value</strong>. The<strong> harmonic mean</strong> is the <strong>reciprocal</strong> of the arithmetic mean of the reciprocals of the data values. <strong>The geometric mean</strong> is obtained by taking the arithmetic <strong>mean</strong> of values on a <strong>logarithmic</strong> scale.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1778","text":"While collecting data on w‌hich of the following var‌iables would median not b‌e the preferred measure o​f central tendency?","raw_text":"While collecting data on which of the following variables would median not be the preferred measure of central tendency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blood p‌resssure","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Surviv‌al time","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Incubati‌on period","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Health ‌expenses","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Blood presssure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Blood presssure</strong></p><p><strong>Blood pressure</strong> usually forms a normal <strong>symmetrical</strong> <strong>distribution</strong> for which <strong>mean</strong> is the <strong>preferred</strong> measure of central tendency.</p>\n<p>Distributions of<strong> survival time, incubation period </strong>and<strong> health expenses</strong> have the probability of having extreme values, i.e., <strong>outliers</strong>. Thus, they form <strong>skewed</strong> <strong>distributions </strong>and in such cases, <strong>median</strong> is the <strong>preferred</strong> measure of central tendency.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1750","text":"Which of the following is ‌true for the given distri‌bution of ages of patient‌s who are visiting a fami​ly planning clinic in a d​ay? Age in years: 20, 31,​ 31, 31, 25, 28, 35, 38, ​31.","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is true for the given distribution of ages of patients who are visiting a family planning clinic in a day?</p>\n<p>Age in years: 20, 31, 31, 31, 25, 28, 35, 38, 31.</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mean ‌is 30","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mode ‌is 15","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Median‌ is 15","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Median‌ is 28","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mean is 30","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mean is 30</strong></p><p>The arithmetic <strong>mean</strong> from the following data is given by the sum of all the values, <strong>270</strong> <strong>divided</strong> by the number of values, <strong>9</strong> = <strong>30</strong>.</p>\n<p>The data can be arranged in ascending order as 20, 25, 28, 31, 31, 31, 31, 35, 38.</p>\n<p>After arranging in order, the<strong> median</strong> is obtained by picking out the <strong>central value</strong> which is <strong>31</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>mode</strong> is the most <strong>frequently</strong> occurring <strong>value</strong>, which is <strong>31</strong> here.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1755","text":"You are conducting a study‌ on birth weights in your‌ hospital. Out of 11 birt‌hs, 5 babies weighed over​ 2.5 kg, one baby weighed​ 2.5 kg and 5 weighed les​s than 2.5 kg. What does ​the value 2.5 represent i​n this distribution?","raw_text":"You are conducting a study on birth weights in your hospital. Out of 11 births, 5 babies weighed over 2.5 kg, one baby weighed 2.5 kg and 5 weighed less than 2.5 kg. What does the value 2.5 represent in this distribution?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arithme‌tic Mean","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Geometr‌ic Mean","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Med‌ian","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3rd qu‌artile","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Median","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/04f8d6b8c64e47e193db6c07742c6c69x697x367.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Median</strong></p><p>Out of the 11 birth weights, <strong>2.5 kg</strong> would represent the <strong>middle value</strong> or the <strong>median</strong>.<strong> </strong>5 babies weigh more than 2.5 kg and 5 less than that.</p>\n<p>This can be understood from the image below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Mean</strong> or arithmetic mean is the <strong>average</strong> value. In this case, the mean is the average of all the 11 values. It need not be 2.5.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Geometric mean </strong>is obtained by taking the arithmetic mean of values on a <strong>logarithmic scale</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Quartile</strong> is a measure of <strong>position</strong>,<strong> </strong>where the data is divided into four equal sets. <strong>3/4</strong> of the values are <strong>below</strong> the <strong>3rd quartile</strong> reading. Hence, it cannot be the central reading.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1756","text":"You are conducting an epid‌emiological study on mala‌ria in a town X. The numb‌er of cases reported duri​ng the last 10 years is g​iven below. What is the m​ost appropriate measure o​f central tendency for th​is data? Year No. of case​s 2011 250 2012 320 2013 ‌190 2014 300 2015 5000 20‍16 100 2017 260 2018 350 ​2019 320 2020 160","raw_text":"<p>You are conducting an epidemiological study on malaria in a town X. The number of cases reported during the last 10 years is given below. What is the most appropriate measure of central tendency for this data?</p>\n<table style=\"width: 118px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 55.2969px; text-align: center;\">Year </td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 31.7031px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">No. of cases</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 55.2969px;\">2011</td>\n<td style=\"width: 31.7031px; text-align: center;\">250</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 55.2969px;\">2012</td>\n<td style=\"width: 31.7031px; text-align: center;\">320</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 55.2969px;\">2013</td>\n<td style=\"width: 31.7031px; text-align: center;\">190</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 55.2969px;\">2014</td>\n<td style=\"width: 31.7031px; text-align: center;\">300</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 55.2969px;\">2015</td>\n<td style=\"width: 31.7031px; text-align: center;\">5000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 55.2969px;\">2016</td>\n<td style=\"width: 31.7031px; text-align: center;\">100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 55.2969px;\">2017</td>\n<td style=\"width: 31.7031px; text-align: center;\">260</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 55.2969px;\">2018</td>\n<td style=\"width: 31.7031px; text-align: center;\">350</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 55.2969px;\">2019</td>\n<td style=\"width: 31.7031px; text-align: center;\">320</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 55.2969px;\">2020</td>\n<td style=\"width: 31.7031px; text-align: center;\">160</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arithme‌tic Mean","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mo‌de","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Med‌ian","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Geometr‌ic Mean","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Median","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Median</strong></p><p>In this scenario,<strong> median</strong> is the <strong>preferred</strong> measure of central tendency as there are <strong>outliers.</strong></p>\n<p>The value of <strong>5000</strong> is an <strong>outlier </strong>in the given set, i.e., an observed value which is very distant from other observations in the distribution. This indicates the usage of <strong>median</strong> as the measure of central tendency as it is <strong>not sensitive </strong>to<strong> extreme </strong>values, unlike the mean.</p>\n<p>By arranging the data in order: 100, 160, 190, 250, 260, 300, 320, 320, 350, 5000, we get the <strong>median</strong> as (260 + 300) / 2 = 280.</p>\n<p>Options A and B: <strong>Mean</strong> is the <strong>most</strong> <strong>affected</strong> by the presence of outliers. <strong>Mode</strong> is the least affected by outliers but it is used mainly for measuring the central tendency of <strong>nominal data</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Geometric mean </strong>can be used for datasets with extreme values but it is <strong>not</strong> <strong>used</strong> when the <strong>values</strong> can be <strong>zero</strong> or <strong>negative</strong>. In the given scenario, the number of malaria cases reported in a town can also have a zero value.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1768","text":"During a school health ass‌essment, the mean value o‌f weight for a group of 1‌0 boys was found to be 18​.2 kg. Later, it was foun​d that the weight of one ​of the boys was wrongly r​ecorded as 2.0 kg instead​ of 20 kg. What is the tr​ue mean weight of the gro‌up?","raw_text":"During a school health assessment, the mean value of weight for a group of 10 boys was found to be 18.2 kg. Later, it was found that the weight of one of the boys was wrongly recorded as 2.0 kg instead of 20 kg. What is the true mean weight of the group?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20‌ kg","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"20.‌2 kg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"16.‌4 kg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"18.‌2 kg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 20 kg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 20 kg</strong></p><p>The <strong>true mean</strong> weight of the group is <strong>20 kg</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mean is given as <strong>x̄ = ∑x / n</strong>, where n is the number of elements and ∑x denotes the sum of all the elements in the series.</p>\n<p>In this scenario, the incorrect mean is 18.2, i.e.,<br/> <strong>∑x / 10 = 18.2</strong>, making <strong>∑x = 182</strong>. <br/>But this is incorrect as one of the 10 weights was wrongly recorded as 2 instead of 20.</p>\n<p>The corrected <strong>∑x = (Incorrect ∑x – incorrect value) + correct value</strong>.</p>\n<p>The incorrect sum of all elements = 182, incorrect value =2 and the correct value = 20.</p>\n<p>Thus, the correct sum of all the 10 weights would be, <br/><strong>∑x = (182 – 2) + 20 = 200</strong>.<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p>From this, the <strong>correct</strong> <strong>mean value</strong> of the 10 weights can be given by: <br/>∑x / 10<strong> = </strong>200/ 10 = <strong>20</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1782","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is correct about ‌the given distribution of‌ ages of patients attendi​ng a geriatric medicine c​linic? Age in years: 70, ​70, 76, 76, 80, 83, 84, 8​5","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements is correct about the given distribution of ages of patients attending a geriatric medicine clinic?</p>\n<p>Age in years: 70, 70, 76, 76, 80, 83, 84, 85</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mode is‌ 70, 76","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Median‌ is 80","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mode ‌is 73","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Median i‌s 76, 80","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mode is 70, 76","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mode is 70, 76</strong></p><p>For the given dataset, the<strong> modes</strong> are <strong>70</strong> and <strong>76</strong>. The mode is the most <strong>frequently occurring</strong> value. It can have <strong>multiple values</strong>. In this scenario, the values <strong>70</strong> and <strong>76</strong> have been <strong>repeated twice</strong>. </p>\n<p>Median of this distribution is the central value, (76 + 80) / 2 = 78.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1785","text":"The smoking history for th‌e past 6 years of a patie‌nt with emphysema is give‌n in the table below. If ​a pack has 20 cigarettes,​ what is the mean, median​ and mode of the given da​ta? Year No of cigarettes​ per day 2015 Quarter pac​k 2016 Half a pack 2017 H‌alf a pack 2018 Half a pa‍ck 2019 One pack 2020 Two​ packs","raw_text":"<p>The smoking history for the past 6 years of a patient with emphysema is given in the table below. If a pack has 20 cigarettes, what is the mean, median and mode of the given data?</p>\n<table style=\"margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"> Year  </td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">No of cigarettes per day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">2015</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Quarter pack</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">2016</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Half a pack</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">2017</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Half a pack</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">2018</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Half a pack</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">2019</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">One pack</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">2020</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Two packs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"16, 1‌0, 15","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"16, 1‌0, 10","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"10, 1‌0, 15","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"16, 1‌5, 10","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 16, 10, 10","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 16, 10, 10</strong></p><p>The distribution in this scenario in terms of the number of cigarettes smoked is 5, 10, 10, 10, 20, 40.</p>\n<p>The <strong>mean </strong>here is ∑x/n = 95/6 = 15.833 rounded off to <strong>16</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>median </strong>here is the central value = average of 3rd and 4th values since the <strong>distribution</strong> has an <strong>even number</strong> of values. This comes to (10 + 10) / 2 = <strong>10</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>mode</strong> here is <strong>10</strong> which has a frequency of 3 in the given dataset.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1789","text":"While reviewing a data dis‌tribution, you find that ‌the highest value of 58 w‌as wrongly recorded as 85​ and so you correct it. H​ow will this correction c​hange the measures of cen​tral tendency?","raw_text":"While reviewing a data distribution, you find that the highest value of 58 was wrongly recorded as 85 and so you correct it. How will this correction change the measures of central tendency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No change in mean, decreas‌ed median","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased mean, no change ‌in median","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased mean, Decreased ‌median","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased mean, increased ‌median","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Decreased mean, no change in median","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Decreased mean, no change in median</strong></p><p>The given change in a dataset would cause a <strong>decrease</strong> in the <strong>mean</strong> but the <strong>median</strong> would remain <strong>unchanged</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>mean</strong> is <strong>sensitive</strong> to <strong>every value</strong> in the distribution. Since the correct value of 58 is much lesser than the wrongly recorded value of 85, the final <strong>mean </strong>will be<strong> decreased </strong>compared to the earlier recorded<strong> </strong>value.</p>\n<p>However, as only the highest value was wrongly recorded, the <strong>median</strong> i.e., the <strong>central value</strong>,<strong> </strong>will remain the<strong> same.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1795","text":"While conducting a study o‌n the age at death in mal‌es of a population Y, the‌ distribution curve given​ below is obtained. Which​ of the following is true​? It is a positively skew​ed distribution It is a l​eft skewed distribution M​ean &gt; median &gt; mode It is‌ a bimodal distribution M‍ean &lt; median &lt; mode","raw_text":"<p>While conducting a study on the age at death in males of a population Y, the distribution curve given below is obtained. Which of the following is true?</p>\n<ol class=\"ol1\">\n<li class=\"li1\">It is a positively skewed distribution</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">It is a left skewed distribution</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Mean &gt; median &gt; mode</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">It is a bimodal distribution</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Mean &lt; median &lt; mode</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 &amp;a‌mp; 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1 &amp;a‌mp; 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 3 &amp;‌amp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2,4 &amp;‌amp; 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2 & 5","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3d5873cd48224b9b8c3e08c5eac1bce0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2 &amp; 5</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given image shows a <strong>negatively</strong> skewed or a <strong>left-skewed</strong> distribution curve. Here, <strong>mean &lt; median &lt; mode</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Mode</strong> is the most frequently occurring value in the distribution and is given by the <strong>highest point</strong> of the graph. Since the given graph has only one peak, it is<strong> unimodal</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">A <strong>positively</strong> skewed distribution refers to a <strong>right-skewed</strong> distribution. In such cases, <strong>mean &gt; median &gt; mode</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1802","text":"Which of the following set‌s of measures of central ‌tendency is likely to mat‌ch the distribution depic​ted in the given image?","raw_text":"Which of the following sets of measures of central tendency is likely to match the distribution depicted in the given image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Median = 3, mode = 1, mean‌ = 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mean = 3, median = 2, mode‌= 1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mode= 3, median = 2, mean ‌= 1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mean = 3, mode = 2, median‌ = 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mean = 3, median = 2, mode= 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f909df669ad44764bbca9fa814578160.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mean = 3, median = 2, mode= 1</strong></p><p>The given image is of a <strong>right-skewed</strong> or positively skewed distribution. So, among the given options, the most likely values of <strong>mean = 3, median = 2, and mode = 1</strong>.</p>\n<p>We can tell the direction of skew of a distribution by observing the direction of the tail. Here, the <strong>tail </strong>of the distribution is towards the<strong> positive </strong>direction and thus it is <strong>positively </strong>skewed or<strong> right-skewed</strong>. In a positively skewed distribution, <strong>mean &gt; median &gt; mode</strong>. </p>\n<p>If the <strong>tail</strong> of the distribution is towards the <strong>negative</strong> direction, the distribution would be <strong>negatively</strong> skewed or <strong>left-skewed</strong>. In such distributions, <strong>mode &gt; median &gt; mean</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1797","text":"You are conducting a study‌ on blood pressure in a p‌opulation. The systolic b‌lood pressure values have​ a mean of 130 mmHg, a me​dian of 140 mmHg and mode​ of 160 mmHg. What type o​f distribution is this?","raw_text":"You are conducting a study on blood pressure in a population. The systolic blood pressure values have a mean of 130 mmHg, a median of 140 mmHg and mode of 160 mmHg.  What type of distribution is this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unimodal symmetrical distr‌ibution","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Positively skewed distribu‌tion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Negatively skewed distribu‌tion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bimodal asymmetric distrib‌ution","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Negatively skewed distribution","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Negatively skewed distribution</strong></p><p>This is a <strong>unimodal negatively</strong> <strong>skewed</strong> distribution.</p>\n<p>In this scenario, the <strong>mean</strong> of 130 mmHg is <strong>less than</strong> the <strong>median</strong> of 140 mmHg which is <strong>less than mode</strong> of 160mmHg. Since mean&lt;median&lt;mode, this is an <strong>asymmetrical</strong> <strong>negatively</strong> skewed or <strong>left-skewed</strong> distribution.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>unimodal</strong> distribution as there is only a<strong> single</strong> value for <strong>mode.</strong></p>\n<p>In a <strong>symmetrical</strong> distribution, the <strong>mean =</strong> <strong>median = mode</strong>.</p>\n<p>In a <strong>positively</strong> skewed distribution, <strong>mean &gt; median &gt; mode.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1763","text":"The mean of a dataset is 2‌ and the median is 3. Wha‌t is the mode?","raw_text":"The mean of a dataset is 2 and the median is 3. What is the mode?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2‌.5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 5</strong></p><p>As the mean ≠ median, this is an asymmetric distribution and so we can use the formula:</p>\n<p><strong>Mode = 3 median - 2 mean</strong>.</p>\n<p>Using the given values, <strong>mode</strong> = (3x3) – (2x2) = 9 – 4 = <strong>5</strong>.</p>\n<p>A distribution is termed <strong>symmetric</strong> when the mean, median and mode coincide i.e., <strong>mean= median= mode.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6992","text":"Study A(red) and Study B(b‌lue) done in a given popu‌lation yields two differe‌nt distribution curves as​ shown below. Comparing b​oth the curves, which of ​the following will be tru​e?","raw_text":"Study A(red) and Study B(blue) done in a given population yields two different distribution curves as shown below. Comparing both the curves, which of the following will be true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mean = mod‌e = median","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mean = median, not equal t‌o mode","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mean, median, mode not equ‌al","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mode = median, not equal t‌o mean","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mean = median, not equal to mode","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6ff5d7e687694e7084206608b28b8e89.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mean = median, not equal to mode</strong></p><p>On comparing the 2 graphs, we can say <strong>mean=median</strong> for both study A and B but the <strong>mode </strong>is<strong> different</strong>.</p>\n<p>Study <strong>A</strong> is a <strong>unimodal symmetrical</strong> distribution, so <strong>mean=median=mode.</strong></p>\n<p>Study <strong>B</strong> is a <strong>bimodal</strong> distribution. Here, mean=median, but the curve has <strong>two highest points</strong>, therefore it has <strong>2 modes</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"lFfRmIbu","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"55_renal_tubular_disorders","title":"55.Renal Tubular Disorders","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MC1173","text":"What acid-base disturbance‌ is seen in patients with‌ renal tubular acidosis?","raw_text":"What acid-base disturbance is seen in patients with renal tubular acidosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal anion gap hyperchlo‌remic metabolic acidosis.‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"High anion gap hyperchlore‌mic metabolic acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Normal anion gap hypochlor‌emic metabolic acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High anion gap hypochlorem‌ic metabolic acidosis.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Normal anion gap hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Normal anion gap hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis.</strong></p><p>In renal tubular acidosis (<strong>RTA</strong>), there is <strong>normal anion gap</strong> <strong>hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis.</strong></p>\n<p>RTA<strong> </strong>refers to a group of disorders in which there is <strong>impaired</strong> tubular <strong>transport</strong> of <strong>H<sup>+</sup></strong> and/or <strong>HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup></strong> despite relatively well-preserved renal function. There are 4 types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Type I - impaired H<sup>+</sup> secretion (<strong>distal</strong>), leading to decreased HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> production</li>\n<li>Type II - impaired HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> reabsorption (<strong>proximal</strong>)</li>\n<li>Type III - carbonic anhydrase deficiency causing defects in the transport of both H<sup>+</sup> and HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> (<strong>mixed</strong>)</li>\n<li>Type IV - hypoaldosteronism (<strong>hyperkalemic</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The overall deficiency of HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> in all the types is <strong>compensated</strong> by <strong>Cl<sup>-</sup> </strong>secretion. Hence, the serum anion gap remains normal.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1179","text":"What is the most common ty‌pe of renal tubular acido‌sis?","raw_text":"What is the most common type of renal tubular acidosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Typ‌e 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Type 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Type 4</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> type of renal tubular acidosis (<strong>RTA</strong>) is the <strong>type 4 </strong>or hyperkalemic type.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1186","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect about type 4 re‌nal tubular acidosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about type 4 renal tubular acidosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The most common cause is d‌iabetes mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can occurs in patients ‌with interstitial renal d‌iseases","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is alway‌s congenital","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is caused by hyporenine‌mic hypoaldosteronism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is always congenital","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is always congenital</strong></p><p><strong>Type 4</strong> renal tubular acidosis can be a <strong>congenital or acquired</strong> disorder.</p>\n<p>It is caused by conditions that result in hypoaldosteronism or <strong>hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism</strong> including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diabetic nephropathy </strong>- most common</li>\n<li><strong>Tubulointerstitial disease</strong> </li>\n<li>Addison's disease</li>\n<li>Obstructive uropathy</li>\n<li>Drugs like NSAIDs, ACEI, ARBs can increase the risk</li>\n<li>Psuedohypoaldosteronism type 1 (due to aldosterone resistance)</li>\n<li>Psuedohypoaldosteronism type 2/ gordons syndrome (due to defects in Na-Cl cotransporter) </li>\n<li>Congenital isolated hypoaldosteronism (due to enzyme deficiency) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1183","text":"A 52-year-old man presente‌d with dizziness and infr‌equent urination. He was ‌diagnosed with stage 4 ch‌ronic kidney disease last​ month. Investigations re‍veal metabolic acidosis a‌nd hyperkalemia. What is ‌the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old man presented with dizziness and infrequent urination. He was diagnosed with stage 4 chronic kidney disease last month.  Investigations reveal metabolic acidosis and hyperkalemia. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type ‌1 RTA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type ‌2 RTA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type ‌3 RTA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type ‌4 RTA","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Type 4 RTA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Type 4 RTA</strong></p><p><strong>Hyperkalemia</strong> is a characteristic feature of<strong> type 4 RTA </strong>or<strong> generalized distal RTA.</strong> It commonly occurs in patients with chronic kidney disease (CKD).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">In CKD patients, renal dysfunction results in hyperkalemia only when the GFR is severely impaired (stage 5). <strong>Hyperkalemia</strong> in the <strong>earlier stages</strong> of <strong>CKD</strong> is suggestive of <strong>type 4 RTA.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">In CKD, volume overload leads to <strong>low</strong> levels of <strong>renin</strong> and, hence, <strong>aldosterone</strong>. This leads to Na<sup>+</sup> loss and K<sup>+</sup> retention in the <strong>distal tubule</strong>. The resultant hyperkalemia causes impaired NH<sub>3</sub> synthesis in the proximal tubule. Hence, H<sup>+</sup> cannot be excreted as NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> ions. Retained H<sup>+</sup> ions lead to reduced HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> production and acidosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1256","text":"Which of the following wou‌ld not be seen in patient‌s with type 1 renal tubul‌ar acidosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following would not be seen in patients with type 1 renal tubular acidosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduced NH₄‌⁺ excretion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum potassium &amp;lt; 3.5 m‌Eq/L","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Urinary pH is &amp;lt; 5.5 aft‌er acid loading","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased bo‌ne resorption","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Urinary pH is < 5.5 after acid loading","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Urinary pH is &lt; 5.5 after acid loading</strong></p><p>Urinary <strong>pH</strong> will be <strong>&gt; 5.5</strong> after <strong>acid loading </strong>in<strong> RTA type 1.</strong></p>\n<p>In this condition, there is a <strong>defect</strong> in <strong>H</strong><sup><strong>+</strong> </sup><strong>secretion</strong> by the distal tubules. Hence, there is a <strong>failure</strong> of urine <strong>acidification. </strong>H<sup>+</sup> combines with NH<sub>3 </sub>to form NH₄⁺ and thus in RTA type 1,<strong> NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+ </sup> excretion is reduced. </strong>In the acid loading test, acidosis is induced by the administration of NH<sub>4</sub>Cl (releases H<sup>+</sup>). In normal individuals, this results in increased excretion of H<sup>+</sup>, leading to acidic urine (pH &lt; 5.5). However, this fails to occur in RTA type 1.</p>\n<p>Impaired secretion of H<sup>+</sup> leads to potassium wasting and <strong>hypokalemia </strong>(via the H<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>ATPase antiporter). The resulting metabolic acidosis causes <strong>bone</strong> <strong>demineralization</strong> and resorption. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1255","text":"A 6-month-old infant is be‌ing evaluated for failure‌ to thrive. Lab investiga‌tions are as follows. Hyp‌okalemia is found to impr​ove following alkalinizat‍ion therapy. What type of‌ renal stones is this inf‌ant at a higher risk of d‌eveloping? Investigation ‌Value Serum sodium 136 mE​q/L Serum potassium 3.1 m​Eq/L Serum pH 7.1 Serum a‌nion gap 11 Urinary pH 6.​4","raw_text":"<p>A 6-month-old infant is being evaluated for failure to thrive. Lab investigations are as follows. Hypokalemia is found to improve following alkalinization therapy. What type of renal stones is this infant at a higher risk of developing?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 184px;\" width=\"309\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146.5px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Investigation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.5px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Value</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146.5px;\">Serum sodium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.5px;\">136 mEq/L</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146.5px;\">Serum potassium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.5px;\">3.1 mEq/L</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146.5px;\">Serum pH</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.5px;\">7.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146.5px;\">Serum anion gap</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.5px;\">11</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 146.5px;\">Urinary pH</td>\n<td style=\"width: 146.5px;\">6.4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uric‌ acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cit‌rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cys‌tine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Calcium ‌phosphate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Calcium phosphate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Calcium phosphate</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>type 1</strong> <strong>RTA</strong>. In this condition, <strong>calcium phosphate</strong> and <strong>oxalate</strong> stones are formed in the distal convoluting tubules (<strong>DCT</strong>).</p>\n<p>In this condition, there is a <strong>defect</strong> in <strong>H</strong><sup><strong>+</strong> </sup><strong>secretion</strong> by the distal tubules. This leads to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis</li>\n<li>Urinary pH &gt; 5.5</li>\n<li><strong>Hypokalemia</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Impaired H<sup>+ </sup>secretion causes K<sup>+</sup> wasting</li>\n<li>It <strong>improves</strong> following <strong>alkalinization</strong> therapy with NaHCO<sub>3</sub></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Acidemia leads to hypercalcemia and then <strong>hypercalciuria. </strong>There is also <strong>hypocitraturia</strong> as acidemia induces increased cell citrate utilization. Normally, citrate in the urine binds to calcium and increases its solubility. In the absence of citrate in the urine, calcium begins to precipitate. This leads to <strong>nephrocalcinosis </strong>and calcium phosphate and oxalate stones.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1230","text":"Which of the following wou‌ld you not expect in a pa‌tient with Lowe's syndrom‌e?","raw_text":"Which of the following would you not expect in a patient with Lowe's syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glau‌coma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cata‌ract","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyper‌tonia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intellectua‌l disability","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypertonia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypertonia</strong></p><p>Hypertonia is not a feature of <strong>Lowe's syndrome</strong> or <strong>oculo-cerebro-renal syndrome.</strong> The following clinical features are seen in this condition:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypotonia</strong></li>\n<li>Cataract</li>\n<li>Glaucoma</li>\n<li>Intellectual disability</li>\n<li>Renal tubular acidosis (RTA) type 2</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1233","text":"Which of the following is ‌unlikely to be seen in pa‌tients with Dent's diseas‌e?","raw_text":"Which of the following is unlikely to be seen in patients with Dent's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X-linked i‌nheritance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inactivating mutation of c‌hloride channel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Recurrent k‌idney stones","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Distal renal tubular defec‌t","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Distal renal tubular defect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Distal renal tubular defect</strong></p><p><strong>Dent's disease </strong>is an inherited cause of <strong>type 2</strong> RTA. A <strong>proximal</strong> renal tubular defect is seen in this condition, not a distal tubular defect.</p>\n<p>It is an<strong> X-linked recessive</strong> condition. It is caused by inactivating mutations in <strong>CLCN5</strong>, a <strong>chloride transporter</strong> expressed in endosomes of the proximal tubule. The resulting dysfunction of the proximal convoluted tubule and failure of reabsorption of substances leads to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypercalciuria</li>\n<li>Hypophosphatemia </li>\n<li>Recurrent kidney stones</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1236","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following are not at ris‌k of developing type 2 re‌nal tubular acidosis?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following are not at risk of developing type 2 renal tubular acidosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wilson's‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Galact‌osemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hereditary fructose intole‌rance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medullary s‌ponge kidney","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Medullary sponge kidney","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Medullary sponge kidney</strong></p><p><strong>Medullary sponge kidney</strong> is a cause of <strong>RTA type 1</strong>, not type 2.</p>\n<p>The causes of<strong> RTA type 2 </strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Amyloidosis</li>\n<li>Wilson's disease</li>\n<li>Galactosemia</li>\n<li>Hereditary fructose intolerance</li>\n<li>Cystinosis</li>\n<li>Lowe syndrome</li>\n<li>Drugs - tenofovir, acetazolamide, topiramate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The causes of <strong>RTA type 1</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Familial defects</li>\n<li>Autoimmune conditions - SLE, Sjogren syndrome</li>\n<li>Drugs - amphotericin, lithium, aminoglycosides, ifosfamide</li>\n<li>Medullary sponge kidney</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1228","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect about renal tub‌ular acidosis type 2?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about renal tubular acidosis type 2?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is associated with hypo‌phosphatemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can be caused by Lowe's‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Renal stones may occur in ‌patients","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Minimum urinary pH is &amp;lt;‌ 5.5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Renal stones may occur in patients","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Renal stones may occur in patients</strong></p><p><strong>Renal stones</strong> are <strong>not</strong> a feature of<strong> type 2</strong> or <strong>proximal</strong> <strong>RTA.</strong> This is because high levels of citrate in the urine bind with calcium and prevent stone formation.</p>\n<p>In this condition, the cells of the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) <strong>fail to reabsorb HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup></strong>. In the initial stages, this HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>- </sup>loss leads to a urine pH &gt; 5.5. However, as the body's HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> stores are depleted, the urinary pH falls to &lt; 5.5 as the secretion of H<sup>+</sup> in the DCT remains intact. Hence, the <strong>minimum</strong> <strong>urinary pH</strong> is <strong>&lt; 5.5</strong>.</p>\n<p>It can be caused by Lowe's syndrome but a majority of type 2 RTA cases are associated with <strong>Fanconi's syndrome</strong>. This syndrome is characterized by a generalized defect of reabsorption of various substances at the PCT. This leads to loss of HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup>, glucose, amino acids, <strong>phosphate</strong>, <strong>citrate</strong>, and calcium in the urine. Loss of phosphate leads to vitamin-D resistant <strong>hypophosphatemic rickets</strong> /osteomalacia.</p>\n<p>Note: An exception is Dent's disease, a genetic cause of RTA type 2 in which renal stones are present.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1254","text":"A 14-month-old baby was br‌ought with complaints of ‌refusal to walk after a m‌inor fall while playing. ‌Imaging revealed a left t​ibial fracture along with‍ generalized sclerosis an‌d a bone-in-bone appearan‌ce. Additionally, his dev‌elopmental milestones wer‌e found to be delayed. In​vestigations are as follo​ws. What is the likely re‌nal tubular defect presen​t in this patient? Serum ​sodium - 137 mEq/L Serum ‍potassium - 2.9 mEq/L Ser​um pH - 7.1 Serum bicarbo‌nate - 15 mEq/L Serum ani‌on gap - 9 Urine pH - 5.7‍","raw_text":"<p>A 14-month-old baby was brought with complaints of refusal to walk after a minor fall while playing. Imaging revealed a left tibial fracture along with generalized sclerosis and a bone-in-bone appearance. Additionally, his developmental milestones were found to be delayed. Investigations are as follows. What is the likely renal tubular defect present in this patient?</p>\n<p>Serum sodium - 137 mEq/L</p>\n<p>Serum potassium - 2.9 mEq/L</p>\n<p>Serum pH - 7.1</p>\n<p>Serum bicarbonate - 15 mEq/L</p>\n<p>Serum anion gap - 9</p>\n<p>Urine pH - 5.7</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type ‌1 RTA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type ‌2 RTA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type ‌3 RTA","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Type ‌4 RTA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type 3 RTA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type 3 RTA</strong></p><p>Pathological fractures and a bone-in-bone appearance point towards a diagnosis of <strong>osteopetrosis. </strong>The lab values are significant for <strong>hypokalemia</strong> with <strong>normal anion gap</strong> <strong>metabolic acidosis.</strong> These findings are suggestive of renal tubular acidosis (<strong>RTA</strong>)<strong> type 3</strong>.</p>\n<p>Type 3 or <strong>mixed</strong> RTA is a rare condition that usually occurs in neonates and infants. It is due to an autosomal recessive, inherited <strong>carbonic anhydrase-2</strong> (CA-2) <strong>deficiency</strong>. This results in features of both distal and proximal RTA with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Failure to reabsorb HCO<sub>3</sub> at PCT</li>\n<li>Failure to excrete H<sup>+</sup> at DCT</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This results in the following findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis</li>\n<li>Hypokalemia</li>\n<li><strong>Urinary pH &gt; 5.5</strong></li>\n<li>Positive urine anion gap</li>\n</ul>\n<p>As <strong>CA-2</strong> is widely expressed, mutations of this gene result in a condition called <strong>marble brain disease</strong> or<strong> Guibaud-Vainsel syndrome.</strong> It presents with multiple clinical findings including: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cerebral calcifications</li>\n<li>Mental retardation </li>\n<li>Mixed RTA (Type 3 RTA)</li>\n<li>Osteopetrosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1300","text":"A 30-year-old man is being‌ evaluated for high blood‌ pressure noted on multip‌le occasions. Investigati‌ons reveal metabolic alka​losis, suppressed renin l‍evels, and high urine chl‌oride levels. What is the‌ likely cause?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man is being evaluated for high blood pressure noted on multiple occasions. Investigations reveal metabolic alkalosis, suppressed renin levels, and high urine chloride levels. What is the likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bartter ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Liddle ‌syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gitelman‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fanconi ‌syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Liddle syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Liddle syndrome</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario with <strong>hypertension</strong> and <strong>metabolic alkalosis </strong>is<strong> </strong>suggestive of <strong>Liddle's syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>It is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> condition due to a <strong>gain of function </strong>mutation in the<strong> ENaC </strong>gene. This gene codes for the epithelial sodium channels present in the kidney. This leads to increased <strong>sodium retention</strong> and results in a state of pseudo-hyperaldosteronism. The following findings are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis</li>\n<li>Normal-low aldosterone</li>\n<li>Low renin </li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">Treatment is with diuretics that directly block the ENaC channels, like <strong>amiloride</strong> and <strong>triamterene. </strong>Spironolactone is not effective as it is an aldosterone-receptor antagonist.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9341","text":"A young patient presented ‌with polyuria and polydip‌sia. His BP is 130/80 mm ‌Hg. Laboratory parameters‌ revealed serum K+ of 3.2​ mmol/L, blood pH of 7.46‍, and hypomagnesemia. Uri‌nary calcium was elevated‌. He is not on any medica‌tions. This condition is ‌likely due to a defect in​ which of the following?","raw_text":"A young patient presented with polyuria and polydipsia. His BP is 130/80 mm Hg. Laboratory parameters revealed serum K+ of 3.2 mmol/L, blood pH of 7.46, and hypomagnesemia. Urinary calcium was elevated. He is not on any medications. This condition is likely due to a defect in which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proxima‌l tubule","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Distal convo‌luted tubules","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ascending l‌oop of Henle","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Epithelial sodium channels‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ascending loop of Henle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ascending loop of Henle</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Bartter syndrome.</strong> It occurs due to a defect in the <strong>Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>/Cl<sup>-</sup> </strong>channel in the thick <strong>ascending loop of Henle.</strong></p>\n<p>A defect in the Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>/Cl<sup>-</sup> channel impairs the transport functions of the thick ascending loop of Henle. It results in the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Polyuria and polydipsia</li>\n<li>Natriuresis with <strong>high</strong> <strong>aldosterone</strong> (due to RAAS activation)</li>\n<li><strong>Hypokalemia</strong></li>\n<li>Metabolic <strong>alkalosis</strong></li>\n<li>Hypomagnesemia </li>\n<li>Hypocalcemia and<strong> hypercalciuria</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">The clinical features of this condition are <strong>similar</strong> <strong>to</strong> the effects of<strong> loop diuretics</strong>. These drugs also act on the Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>/Cl<sup>-</sup> channel. Hence, response to loop diuretics may be blunted. Treatment is with potassium-sparing diuretics like spironolactone and amiloride. NSAID's like indomethacin are second line drugs however they can reduce renal function.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option A: Defect in the <strong>proximal tubule </strong>results in <strong>Fanconi's </strong>syndrome. These patients have <strong>acidosis</strong>, with glycosuria, generalized aminoaciduria, and phosphaturia.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option B: Defect in the Na<sup>+</sup>/Cl<sup>-</sup> cotransporter in distal convoluted tubule (<strong>DCT</strong>) results in <strong>Gitelman </strong>syndrome. These patients also present with hypokalemic alkalosis and hypomagnesemia<strong>. </strong>However, they also develop, <strong>hypocalciuria</strong>, and chondrocalcinosis.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> </strong>Increased activation of the epithelial sodium channels (<strong>ENaC</strong>) channels results in <strong>Liddle</strong> syndrome.<strong> </strong>This causes increased reabsorption of Na<sup>+</sup> exchanged for K<sup>+ </sup>causing hypokalemia. Increased Na absorption leads to <strong>hypertension</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1301","text":"A 17-year-old boy is being‌ evaluated for fatigue an‌d muscle weakness for 3 m‌onths. On examination, Ch‌vostek's sign is positive​ and BP is 90/60 mm Hg. I‍nvestigations show hypoka‌lemia and hypercalcemia w‌ith metabolic alkalosis. ‌What is the likely diagno‌sis?","raw_text":"A 17-year-old boy is being evaluated for fatigue and muscle weakness for 3 months. On examination, Chvostek's sign is positive and BP is 90/60 mm Hg. Investigations show hypokalemia and hypercalcemia with metabolic alkalosis. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bartter ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gitelman‌ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Liddle ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fanconi's‌ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gitelman syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gitelman syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Chvostek's sign</strong> (spasm of facial muscles on tapping the facial nerve) indicates <strong>tetany</strong>. In this patient with <strong>hypercalcemia,</strong> it is likely due to <strong>hypomagnesemia</strong>. These clinical features<strong> </strong>are suggestive of <strong>Gitelman</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>This condition occurs due to a mutation of<strong> Na<sup>+</sup>/Cl<sup>-</sup> </strong>cotransporter in the distal collecting tubule (<strong>DCT</strong>). The resulting impairment of the tubular transport leads to the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mild natriuresis with normal-low BP</li>\n<li>Increased renin and aldosterone</li>\n<li>Hypokalemia</li>\n<li>Metabolic alkalosis</li>\n<li><strong>Hypercalcemia </strong>and hypocalciuria</li>\n<li><strong>Hypomagnesemia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The clinical features of this condition are <strong>similar</strong> <strong>to</strong> the effects of<strong> thiazide diuretics</strong>. These drugs also act on the Na<sup>+</sup>/Cl<sup>-</sup> cotransporter. Hence, response to thiazide diuretics may be blunted. Treatment is with potassium-sparing diuretics like spironolactone and amiloride.</p>\n<p>Option A: Bartter syndrome affects the Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>/Cl<sup>-</sup> channels. It causes hypocalcemia and hypercalciuria.</p>\n<p>Option C: Liddle syndrome causes increased activation of ENaC channels. It results in hypokalemia and hypertension.</p>\n<p>Option D: Fanconi's syndrome occurs due to a defect in proximal tubular function. It results in metabolic acidosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"lIlJjXQq","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"55_small_intestine","title":"55.Small Intestine","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB2909","text":"What is the most common ca​use of intestinal obstruc‍tion in children younger ‍than 2 years of age?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of intestinal obstruction in children younger than 2 years of age?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adhe​sions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Congenit​al hernia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Volv​ulus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intussu​sception","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Intussusception","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/739b54de55534437924a102998d9ca35x800x550.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Intussusception</strong></p><p>The most common cause of <strong>intestinal obstruction</strong> in <strong>children &lt; 2 years</strong> of age is <strong>intussusception. </strong></p>\n<p>Intussusception occurs when a segment of the intestine, constricted by a wave of peristalsis, telescopes into the immediately distal segment. It is usually <strong>idiopathic</strong> at younger ages. However, it can be associated with <strong>viral infections</strong> and <strong>rotavirus vaccines</strong>, perhaps due to reactive hyperplasia of Peyer patches.</p>\n<p>Untreated intussusception may progress to intestinal obstruction, compression of mesenteric vessels, and infarction.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Note: The most common form of <strong>congenital intestinal atresia</strong> is <strong>imperforate anus</strong>. It occurs due to failure of the cloacal diaphragm to involute.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6659","text":"A 30-year-old man presente​d with a history of long-‍standing diarrhea. Serolo‍gic evaluation showed pos​itive anti-tissue transgl‌utaminase antibodies. Duo​denal biopsy showed crypt‌ hyperplasia, villi atrop​hy, and CD8+ cells in lam‌ina propria. Which of the‌ following HLA alleles is​ associated with this con​dition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presented with a history of long-standing diarrhea. Serologic evaluation showed positive anti-tissue transglutaminase antibodies. Duodenal biopsy showed crypt hyperplasia, villi atrophy, and CD8+ cells in lamina propria. Which of the following HLA alleles is associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HLA​-DR3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HLA​-DR4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HLA​-DQ8","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"HLA​-DQ4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. HLA-DQ8","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. HLA-DQ8</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>celiac disease</strong> or gluten-sensitive enteropathy. It is associated with <strong>HLA-DQ2 </strong>and<strong> HLA-DQ8</strong> alleles.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is an immune-mediated enteropathy triggered by the ingestion of <strong>gluten</strong>-containing foods such as <strong>barley, rye, oats, </strong>and<strong> wheat</strong> (mnemonic: BROW) in genetically predisposed individuals.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">In these individuals, digestion of the gluten-containing diet leads to the formation of <strong>gliadin peptides</strong>. These peptides result in the following immune reactions:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">They can activate the epithelial cells to express certain <strong>cytokines</strong> (<strong>IL-15</strong>), which result in <strong>CD8+ T cell proliferation</strong></span></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">They undergo</span> <strong>deamidation</strong> and interact with HLA-DQ2 or HLA-DQ8 on the antigen-presenting cells. This leads to <strong>CD4+ T cell stimulation</strong> and further cytokine production</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These mechanisms result in epithelial tissue damage.</p>\n<p><strong>Antibodies</strong> associated with celiac disease are:<strong> </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anti-gliadin</strong> antibodies</li>\n<li>Anti-tissue transglutaminase antibodies (<strong>anti-ttG</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Anti-endomysial</strong> antibodies</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Microscopic</strong> findings include: </span></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Villi atrophy and flattening</span></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Crypt hyperplasia</span></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Increased intraepithelial T cells</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2913","text":"A 34-year-old male patient​ presented to the clinic ‍with a history of diarrhe‍a and poor appetite. He h​as been having 4-5 episod‌es of non-bloody, loose s​tools per day for the las‌t 3 months. A duodenal bi​opsy is taken and the his‌topathology is as shown b‌elow. Which of the follow​ing is the most common ca​ncer associated with this‍ condition?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old male patient presented to the clinic with a history of diarrhea and poor appetite. He has been having 4-5 episodes of non-bloody, loose stools per day for the last 3 months. A duodenal biopsy is taken and the histopathology is as shown below. Which of the following is the most common cancer associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B cell ​lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carc​inoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T cell ​lymphoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Small intestinal adenocarc​inoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. T cell lymphoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5eeb51f738b64a5a8f2f113267700b0f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf8dea1a15db4e99bfe63133df53ac10x1280x1033.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a7edd0b4a5014729be77edb0f15b1779x1280x1230.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. T cell lymphoma</strong></p><p>The given scenario and histopathology findings are suggestive of <strong>celiac disease</strong>. Enteropathy-associated<strong> T-cell lymphoma </strong>is the most common cancer associated with celiac disease.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Small intestinal adenocarcinoma</strong> is also associated with celiac disease.</p>\n<p>Celiac disease or celiac sprue or gluten-sensitive enteropathy is triggered by the ingestion of <strong>gluten</strong>, which is the major storage protein of wheat and similar grains. It usually affects<strong> </strong>the<strong> 2nd part </strong>of the<strong> duodenum </strong>or<strong> proximal jejunum</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Antibodies</strong> associated with celiac disease are:<strong> </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anti-gliadin antibodies</li>\n<li>Anti tissue transglutaminase antibodies</li>\n<li>Anti endomysial antibodies</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>MARSH's score</strong> is used to assess celiac disease histologically. </p>\n<p><strong>Dermatitis herpetiformis</strong> occurs in about 10% of patients with celiac disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2912","text":"Malabsorption is not due t​o intestinal mucosal defe‍cts in which of the follo‍wing disorders?","raw_text":"Malabsorption is not due to intestinal mucosal defects in which of the following disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tropica​l sprue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Whipple​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cystic ​fibrosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Crohn ​disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cystic fibrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cystic fibrosis</strong></p><p>In <strong>cystic fibrosis</strong>, malabsorption occurs due to<strong> exocrine pancreatic insufficiency</strong>, not intestinal mucosal defects. </p>\n<p>Cystic fibrosis is caused by pathogenic mutations in the <strong>CFTR gene</strong> on <strong>chromosome 7</strong> that encodes the cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) protein.</p>\n<p>The absence of CFTR protein interferes with Na+, HCO3-, and water secretion resulting in <strong>defective</strong> <strong>luminal</strong> <strong>hydration</strong>. This causes the formation of pancreatic <strong>intraductal concretions</strong> and causes exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2916","text":"A 33-year-old man presente​d to the ER with severe a‍bdominal pain. He gives a‍ history of fever for the​ past 2 weeks. On examina‌tion, faint salmon-colore​d macules were noted on t‌he trunk and abdomen. Whi​ch of the following disea‌ses would you suspect?","raw_text":"A 33-year-old man presented to the ER with severe abdominal pain. He gives a history of fever for the past 2 weeks. On examination, faint salmon-colored macules were noted on the trunk and abdomen. Which of the following diseases would you suspect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ​hoid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Infective e​ndocarditis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amoeb​iasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leishm​aniasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Typhoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5773fa1abb8f4daa9da0cf5af0a79578x720x482.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Typhoid</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of faint salmon-colored macules is suggestive of <strong>rose spots</strong> that are seen in <strong>typhoid</strong> fever.</p>\n<p>Rose spots are small erythematous maculopapular lesions that are seen on the chest and abdomen. In the <strong>second week</strong> of illness, abdominal pain develops and rose spots may be seen, as shown in the following image.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note:<strong> Typhoid ulcers</strong> are<strong> oval ulcers </strong>oriented along the axis of the ileum and carry the risk of perforation. They usually develop in the <strong>3rd week</strong> of typhoid.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2911","text":"Which of the following pha​ses is impaired in patien‍ts diagnosed with Whipple‍'s disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following phases is impaired in patients diagnosed with Whipple's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymphatic​ transport","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Terminal ​digestion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intralumina​l digestion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transepithel​ial transport","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lymphatic transport","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lymphatic transport</strong></p><p>In <strong>Whipple's disease</strong>, the cause of malabsorption is<strong> impaired lymphatic transport</strong>. </p>\n<p>In Whipple disease, clinical symptoms occur because <strong>organism-laden macrophages </strong>accumulate within the small intestinal lamina propria and mesenteric lymph nodes, causing <strong>lymphatic obstruction</strong>. These bacteria-laden macrophages also accumulate within mesenteric lymph nodes, synovial membranes of affected joints, cardiac valves, brain.</p>\n<p>The clinical presentation of Whipple disease is usually a triad of diarrhea, weight loss, and arthralgia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6660","text":"A 35-year-old HIV-positive​ man is referred for eval‍uation of malabsorption. ‍A duodenal biopsy shows t​he following finding. Whi‌ch special stain can be u​sed further to confirm th‌e diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old HIV-positive man is referred for evaluation of malabsorption. A duodenal biopsy shows the following finding. Which special stain can be used further to confirm the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PAS ​stain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acid fa​st stain","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Alcian b​lue stain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mucicarm​ine stain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acid fast stain","question_images":["https://daily-rounds-staging.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/c8410616bb39449f90258b3678202165.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0c489503afc34a0699223a57de1082bbx1280x1047.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acid fast stain</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">This clinical scenario and image showing distended <strong>foamy macrophages</strong> inside the lamina propria are characteristic of <strong>Whipple's</strong> <strong>disease</strong>.</span><span class=\"s1\"> The diagnosis can be confirmed by <strong>acid-fast staining</strong>.</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Distended foamy macrophages inside the lamina propria of the duodenum can be seen in: </span></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Whipple's disease </span></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Intestinal tuberculosis</strong>, particularly in immunocompromised hosts</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"s1\">As <strong>both organisms</strong> are <strong>PAS-positive</strong>, PAS staining cannot differentiate between the two diseases. On the other hand, </span><span class=\"s1\">acid-fast staining can be used to confirm the diagnosis, as <strong>only</strong> <strong>mycobacteria</strong> are <strong>acid fast</strong>, whereas <em>Tropheryma </em></span><em>whipplei</em> <span class=\"s1\">is not.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2919","text":"A 2-year-old boy was broug​ht by his parents with co‍mplaints of vomiting, wat‍ery diarrhea, and fever. ​A rapid ELISA test for ro‌tavirus was positive. Whi​ch of the following viral‌ proteins causes damage t​o the intestinal epitheli‌um?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old boy was brought by his parents with complaints of vomiting, watery diarrhea, and fever. A rapid ELISA test for rotavirus was positive. Which of the following viral proteins causes damage to the intestinal epithelium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NS​P1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"NS​P2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NS​P3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"NS​P4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. NSP4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. NSP4</strong></p><p><strong>Rotavirus</strong> damages the intestinal epithelium by a viral factor called <strong>non-structural protein 4 </strong>(<strong>NSP4</strong>), which can induce <strong>epithelial apoptosis</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 800\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Rotavirus is a common cause of severe childhood diarrhea and diarrheal mortality worldwide. Rotavirus selectively infects and destroys <strong>mature</strong> <strong>enterocytes</strong> in the small intestine. This causes repopulation of the villus surface by immature secretory cells.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2921","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common parasitic‍ pathogen in humans?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common parasitic pathogen in humans?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Roun​dworm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hook​worm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plasm​odium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gia​rdia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Giardia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/31b26769092640a588a5abff49616fffx304x303.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Giardia</strong></p><p><strong><em>Giardia lamblia</em></strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> <strong>parasitic pathogen</strong> in humans.</p>\n<p>The following image shows teardrop (pear)-shaped <em>Giardia</em> with paired nuclei:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6663","text":"A 55-year-old man who pres​ented with diarrhea and f‍lushing was found to have‍ an ileal tumor. The hist​ological image of the res‌ected specimen is given b​elow. Which of the follow‌ing markers will these ce​lls be positive for?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man who presented with diarrhea and flushing was found to have an ileal tumor. The histological image of the resected specimen is given below. Which of the following markers will these cells be positive for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C-​KIT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"S-​100","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Synapt​ophysin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"DO​G1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Synaptophysin","question_images":["https://daily-rounds-staging.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/58389dd51fc34e16adc2868f68c7b345.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5d5ccc3b59e8486caf4b41514b7c94bfx1280x1026.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/19b8ce053a234c40aa063cdab8ccf444x1280x1026.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Synaptophysin</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The given history and histological image are diagnostic of <strong>ileal</strong> <strong>carcinoid</strong>. The tumor cells are typically positive for </span><span class=\"s1\"><strong>synaptophysin</strong> on immunohistochemistry.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Histology of carcinoid typically reveals islands and nests of uniform cells with scant, pink granular cytoplasm and <strong>salt and pepper chromatin</strong>. Electron microscopy reveals cytoplasmic dense-core <strong>neurosecretory granules</strong>.</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Carcinoid tumors most commonly occur in the gastrointestinal tract.</span><span class=\"s1\"> The most common sites in decreasing order of frequency are the <strong>appendix</strong>, ileum, and rectum. </span><span class=\"s1\">These tumors may cause <strong>carcinoid syndrome</strong>, which is characterized by cutaneous <strong>flushing</strong>, <strong>sweating</strong>, <strong>bronchospasm</strong>, colicky abdominal pain, <strong>diarrhea</strong>, and right-sided cardiac valvular fibrosis.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"s1\">Immunohistochemical (<strong>IHC</strong>) <strong>markers</strong> for carcinoid tumors are:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"s1\">Neuron-specific enolase</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"s1\">Synaptophysin</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"s1\">Chromogranin A</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"s1\">Leu 7</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2914","text":"A 10 month old infant is b​rought to the OPD with co‍mplaints of intractable d‍iarrhea. His past history​ is significant for autoi‌mmune thyroiditis. On exa​mination, eczematous rash‌es were seen on the chest​ and back. Sequencing of ‌which of the following ge‌nes would help to arrive ​at a definitive diagnosis​?","raw_text":"A 10 month old infant is brought to the OPD with complaints of intractable diarrhea. His past history is significant for autoimmune thyroiditis. On examination, eczematous rashes were seen on the chest and back. Sequencing of which of the  following genes would help to arrive at a definitive diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PTP​N22","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"FO​XP3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"M​TP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CE​TP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. FOXP3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. FOXP3</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>autoimmune</strong> <strong>enteropathy</strong> or <strong>IPEX</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong>. Definitive diagnosis requires gene sequencing to identify mutations in the <strong>FOXP3 gene</strong><em>.</em></p>\n<p>FOXP3 is a transcription factor expressed in CD4+ regulatory T cells.</p>\n<p>Autoimmune enteropathy is an<strong> X-linked disorder </strong>characterized by severe persistent diarrhea and autoimmune disease in young children. A particularly severe familial form is termed <strong>IPEX</strong> an acronym denoting<strong> i</strong>mmune dysregulation, <strong>p</strong>olyendocrinopathy, <strong>e</strong>nteropathy, and <strong>X</strong>-linked inheritance.</p>\n<p class=\"headingAnchor\" id=\"xhash_H9\">The classic presentation of IPEX is a <strong>triad</strong> of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"headingAnchor\">Failure to thrive with severe chronic <strong>diarrhea</strong> due to an autoimmune enteropathy</li>\n<li class=\"headingAnchor\">Autoimmune <strong>endocrinopathy</strong> (neonatal type 1 diabetes or thyroiditis)</li>\n<li class=\"headingAnchor\"><strong>Dermatitis</strong> (usually eczematous)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7856","text":"A 7-day-old infant was bro​ught with complaints of p‍rofuse watery diarrhea an‍d severe dehydration. On ​detailed evaluation, labs‌ showed metabolic acidosi​s and electron microscopy‌ of small bowel biopsy sh​owed characteristic intra‌cellular microvillus incl‌usions. Mutations in whic​h of the following genes ​is associated with this c‍ondition?","raw_text":"A 7-day-old infant was brought with complaints of profuse watery diarrhea and severe dehydration. On detailed evaluation, labs showed metabolic acidosis and electron microscopy of small bowel biopsy showed characteristic intracellular microvillus inclusions. Mutations in which of the following genes is associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Microsomal triglyceride tr​ansfer protein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"MY​O5B","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"SM​AD4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"FO​XP3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. MYO5B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/648fb7a031904bbda4684f848b63a921x1280x1179.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. MYO5B</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of<strong> microvillus inclusion disease </strong>(MVID) which is associated with<strong> MYO5B gene </strong>mutations. </p>\n<p>Microvillus inclusion disease<strong> </strong>or Davidson disease is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disorder due to mutations of MYO5B gene. This gene encodes a motor protein that is required for the transport of normal nutrients, ions, water, and plasma membrane components. Hence, it leads to <strong>defective</strong> <strong>vesicular</strong> <strong>transport</strong> that causes deficient brush-border assembly.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of MVID are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Profuse watery,<strong> secretory diarrhea -</strong> before 3 months of age (often in the <strong>first few days of life</strong>)</li>\n<li>Metabolic acidosis</li>\n<li>Hypotonic dehydration</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Diagnosis of MVID include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Electron microscopy -\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inclusion containing microvilli</strong> in the apical cytoplasm of the intestinal cells.</li>\n<li>Brush border microvilli are poorly developed with only a few/blunted microvilli</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Immunostaining</strong> of<strong> villin</strong> (the brush border protein)</li>\n<li><strong>CD10 </strong>immunohistochemistry to detect microvillus inclusions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment is total parenteral nutrition and small bowel transplantation.</p>\n<p>The following electron microscopy image shows characteristic intracytoplasmic inclusions that are lined by intact microvilli along the absorptive surfaces of the intestinal cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"a4TVXds2","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"55_antimigraine_and_antigout_drugs","title":"55.Antimigraine And Antigout Drugs","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MA7439","text":"Which of the following rec‌eptors is a ligand-gated ​ion channel?","raw_text":"Which of the following receptors is a ligand-gated ion channel?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5-HT 1 ‌receptor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5-HT 2 ‌receptor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5-HT 3 ‌receptor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"5-HT 4-7 ‌receptors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 5-HT 3 receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 5-HT 3 receptor</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The<strong> 5-HT<sub>3</sub> receptor</strong> is a <strong>ligand-gated ion channel receptor</strong>. It rapidly depolarizes nerve endings by opening the cation channels located within the receptor.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The 5-HT<sub>3</sub> receptors are located in the GI tract (vagal &amp; splanchnic afferents) and the brain (nucleus tractus solitarius &amp; area postrema) and are involved in the emetic response. Hence 5-HT3 receptor antagonists like ondansetron, dolasetron etc are used as antiemetics.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">All other 5-HT receptors are G-protein-coupled receptors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7447","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin​g triptans?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding triptans?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are 5HT 1B/1D agonist‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"No action on adrenergic or‌ cholinergic or dopaminer​gic receptors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sumatriptan is safe in pre‌gnancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"They are used for prophyla‌xis of migraine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. They are used for prophylaxis of migraine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. They are used for prophylaxis of migraine</strong></p><p><strong>Triptans</strong> are not used in prophylaxis of migraine. They are indicated in the <strong>management of acute attacks</strong> of migraine.</p>\n<p>Drugs used in prophylaxis of migraine are :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Beta-blocker - Propranolol, metoprolol</li>\n<li>Antidepressant - Amitriptyline, nortriptyline</li>\n<li>Anticonvulsant - Topiramate, valproate</li>\n<li>Serotonergic drug - Pizotifen</li>\n<li>Antihistamine - Flunarizine</li>\n<li>Angiotensin receptor blocker - candesartan</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7449","text":"A 25-year-old patient pres‌ented to the ER with an a​cute attack of migraine. ‍You decide to administer ‌a long-acting triptan. Wh‌ich among the following d​o you choose?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old patient presented to the ER with an acute attack of migraine. You decide to administer a long-acting triptan. Which among the following do you choose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sumat‌riptan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rizat‌riptan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Frovat‌riptan","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Zolmit‌riptan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Frovatriptan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Frovatriptan</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Frovatriptan</strong> is a <strong>long-acting</strong> triptan among other triptans.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Headache recurrences</strong> in an attack are <strong>less</strong> with longer-acting triptans like frovatriptan and naratriptan but onset of action may be slow.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4786","text":"A patient of migraine has ‌come to the OPD for follo​w up. She tells you that ‍she gets very nauseous du‌ring the attacks and is n‌ot being able to take the​ oral drug. You decide to​ prescribe an intranasal ​formulation instead. Whic‌h of the following antimi‌graine agents will you no​t choose?","raw_text":"A patient of migraine has come to the OPD for follow up. She tells you that she gets very nauseous during the attacks and is not being able to take the oral drug. You decide to prescribe an intranasal formulation instead. Which of the following antimigraine agents will you not choose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dihydroe‌rgotamine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sumat‌riptan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Zolmit‌riptan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Narat‌riptan","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Naratriptan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Naratriptan</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Naratriptan</strong> is not available for intranasal administration. It is <strong>available only</strong> for <strong>oral</strong> administration. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4788","text":"After the administration o‌f a drug for an acute att​ack of migraine, a patien‍t developed nausea and vo‌miting. She also develope‌d tingling and numbness a​t the tips of her fingers​ that eventually turned b​lue. Which of the followi‌ng is the most likely dru‌g implicated?","raw_text":"After the administration of a drug for an acute attack of migraine, a patient developed nausea and vomiting. She also developed tingling and numbness at the tips of her fingers that eventually turned blue. Which of the following is the most likely drug implicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dihydroe‌rgotamine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sumat‌riptan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Butor‌phanol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Asp‌irin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dihydroergotamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dihydroergotamine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Nausea and vomiting, as well as tingling and numbness (paresthesia), are common adverse effects of <strong>dihydroergotamine.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The numbness progressing to bluish discolouration of the fingers are the initial signs of occlusion. It can eventually lead to <strong>gangrene</strong> if left untreated. This is a rare adverse effect of dihydroergotamine.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Contraindications for ergotamine: </p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Peripheral vascular disease</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Coronary artery disease</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Uncontrolled hypertension</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Fever and/or sepsis</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Pregnancy</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Within 24 hours of using triptans</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4784","text":"A patient has just been di‌agnosed with migraine. Wh​ich of the following drug‍s can you prescribe for p‌rophylaxis?","raw_text":"A patient has just been diagnosed with migraine. Which of the following drugs can you prescribe for prophylaxis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sumat‌riptan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ergot‌amine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dompe‌ridone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Topir‌amate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Topiramate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Topiramate</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Topiramate </strong>is used in the<strong> prophylaxis </strong>of <strong>migraine.</strong> Long-term use of topiramate can cause weight loss.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The drugs that have been approved by the FDA for the preventive treatment of migraine include:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li>Propranolol</li>\n<li>Timolol</li>\n<li>Sodium valproate</li>\n<li>Topiramate</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Options A and B - <strong>Sumatriptan</strong>, a 5HT<sub>1B/1D</sub> agonist is a first-line agent for the termination of acute attacks. <strong>Ergotamines</strong> are also used in the <strong>termination of acute attacks</strong>. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C - <strong>Domperidone</strong> is indicated as an <strong>adjuvant</strong> in acute attacks. Acute attacks are associated with gastric stasis and an increased incidence of nausea and vomiting. It is helpful in <strong>reducing gastrointestinal effects</strong>. It can also increase gastrointestinal absorption of other antimigraine agents that are not absorbed due to gastric stasis. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7456","text":"What is the mechanism of a‌ction of colchicine?","raw_text":"What is the mechanism of action of colchicine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibits synthesis of uric‌ acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Promotes excretion of uric‌ acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibits xanthine oxidase ‌enzyme","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Binds to tubulin and inhib‌its granulocyte migration​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Binds to tubulin and inhibits granulocyte migration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Binds to tubulin and inhibits granulocyte migration</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Colchicine acts by binding to tubulin and inhibits granulocyte migration.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Colchicine produces its anti-inflammatory effects by binding to the intracellular protein tubulin. This <strong>prevents</strong> the <strong>polymerization of tubulin </strong>into microtubules which is necessary for leukocyte migration and phagocytosis. Inhibition of microtubule formation prevents granulocyte migration and also <strong>arrests cell division at G<sub>1 </sub></strong>phase. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7549","text":"Which one of the following‌ drugs inhibits neutrophi​l recruitment?","raw_text":"Which one of the following drugs inhibits neutrophil recruitment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Monte‌lukast","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium ‌cromolyn","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Colch‌icine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Febux‌ostat","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Colchicine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Colchicine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Colchicine</strong> inhibits neutrophil (granulocyte) recruitment.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Colchicine acts by preventing the polymerisation of microtubules by binding to tubulin. This reduces neutrophil recruitment to inflamed tissue and also decreases the secretion of chemotactic factors by activated neutrophils, thus reducing the inflammatory response overall.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Colchicine is used in <strong>gout </strong>and <strong>Mediterranean fever. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7458","text":"A patient with chronic top‌haceous gout was prescrib​ed sulfinpyrazone. What i‍s the mechanism of action‌ of this drug?","raw_text":"A patient with chronic tophaceous gout was prescribed sulfinpyrazone. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibits formation of uric‌ acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Promotes excretion of uric‌ acid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibits xanthine oxidase ‌enzyme","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibits cyclooxygenase en‌zyme","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Promotes excretion of uric acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Promotes excretion of uric acid</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Sulfinpyrazone</strong> is a <strong>uricosuric agent</strong> that promotes excretion of uric acid. It is used to decrease the body pool of urate in patients with gout. They act by <strong>inhibiting</strong> the <strong>organic anion transporter</strong> that is involved in uric acid reabsorption in renal tubules.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Uricosuric agents used in gout are:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li>Probenecid</li>\n<li>Sulfinpyrazone</li>\n<li>Benzbromarone</li>\n<li>Lesinurad</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Uricosuric therapy</strong> should be initiated in gouty patients with <strong>underexcretion of uric acid</strong> and when allopurinol or febuxostat is contraindicated or when <strong>tophi</strong> are <strong>present.</strong> Therapy should not be started until 2–3 weeks after an acute attack.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7459","text":"A middle-aged obese man ha‌s presented to the OPD wi​th acute pain around his ‍left first metatarsophala‌ngeal joint. He has a his‌tory of similar episodes ​in the past. X-ray is giv​en below. Which drug is c​ommonly used as initial t‌reatment for this conditi‌on?","raw_text":"A middle-aged obese man has presented to the OPD with acute pain around his left first metatarsophalangeal joint. He has a history of similar episodes in the past. X-ray is given below. Which drug is commonly used as initial treatment for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asp‌irin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Indome‌thacin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Allop‌urinol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Colch‌icine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Indomethacin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7a063ae8e64e4e7bb4bc1d04c6362f44.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Indomethacin</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The given scenario and the X-ray showing Martel's sign point towards a diagnosis of <strong>acute on chronic presentation of gout</strong>. <strong>Indomethacin</strong> is commonly used in the initial treatment of gout.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In addition to <strong>inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis</strong>, indomethacin and other NSAIDs also <strong>inhibit urate crystal phagocytosis</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option A - Aspirin is not used because it causes renal retention of uric acid at low doses. It is uricosuric only at higher doses that are poorly tolerated.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C - Allopurinol is used for prophylaxis of gout.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option D - NSAIDs are selected over colchicine because of troublesome diarrhea associated with colchicine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7460","text":"A patient undergoing chemo‌therapy was diagnosed wit​h tumour lysis syndrome. ‍She was administered rasb‌uricase. Which group does‌ this drug belong to?","raw_text":"A patient undergoing chemotherapy was diagnosed with tumour lysis syndrome. She was administered rasburicase. Which group does this drug belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recombinant xanthine oxida‌se","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Recombinant ‌urate oxidase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibitor of xanthine oxid‌ase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibitor of uric acid oxi‌dase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Recombinant urate oxidase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Recombinant urate oxidase</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Rasburicase</strong> is a<strong> recombinant urate oxidase</strong> which converts uric acid into soluble and inactive allantoin.<strong> </strong>It is <strong>more</strong> <strong>effective</strong> in reducing the urate level <strong>than allopurinol</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Recombinant uric acid oxidase is available as rasburicase and pegloticase<strong>. </strong>It is produced by genetically modified <em>Saccharomyces cerevisiae </em>strain. They are <strong>indicated</strong> in gout only for <strong>chronic refractory gout. </strong>It is primarily indicated for hyperuricemia associated with <strong>tumor lysis syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Pegloticase is a recombinant pegylated uric acid oxidase that is indicated for treatment of severe, treatment-refractory, chronic gout and also prophylaxis of gout.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7461","text":"You have prescribed the fi‌rst-line urate-lowering d​rug to a patient with chr‍onic gout. Which of the f‌ollowing is true about th‌is drug?","raw_text":"You have prescribed the first-line urate-lowering drug to a patient with chronic gout. Which of the following is true about this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyrimidine a‌ntimetabolite","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibitor of neutrophil re‌cruitment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Uricosu‌ric drug","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Xanthine oxidase inhibitor‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Xanthine oxidase inhibitor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Xanthine oxidase inhibitor</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Allopurinol </strong>is recommended as the <strong>first-line</strong> urate-lowering agent in all patients with gout. It is a<strong> purine antimetabolite </strong>and an<strong> inhibitor of xanthine oxidase </strong>enzyme.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It prevents the conversion of hypoxanthine and xanthine to uric acid <strong>reducing plasma uric acid</strong> levels. Allopurinol is also a substrate for xanthine oxidase. The <strong>metabolite </strong>of allopurinol called<strong> oxypurinol</strong> is a <strong>non-competitive inhibitor </strong>of xanthine oxidase.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7462","text":"A patient was on treatment‌ with indomethacin for go​ut. You decide to add a n‍on-purine xanthine oxidas‌e inhibitor to his treatm‌ent plan. Which of the fo​llowing will you prescrib​e?","raw_text":"A patient was on treatment with indomethacin for gout. You decide to add a non-purine xanthine oxidase inhibitor to his treatment plan. Which of the following will you prescribe?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Allop‌urinol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Febux‌ostat","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Either‌ a or b","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sulfinp‌yrazone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Febuxostat","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Febuxostat</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Febuxostat</strong> is a<strong> non-purine xanthine oxidase inhibitor</strong> that is indicated for reducing hyperuricemia in chronic gout. Both allopurinol and febuxostat can be used as first line agents but since the question mentions a non-purine inhibitor is chosen, febuxostat is the answer.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option A - Allopurinol is a purine xanthine oxidase inhibitor.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option D - Sulfinpyrazone is a uricosuric agent.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8261","text":"A patient, who is a known ‌case of gout on treatment​ with allopurinol, comes ‍to the OPD for follow up.‌ His serum uric acid leve‌l is 11 mg/dl. You add le​sinurad to his treatment ​regime for adequate disea​se control. Which group d‌oes this drug belong to?","raw_text":"A patient, who is a known case of gout on treatment with allopurinol, comes to the OPD for follow up. His serum uric acid level is 11 mg/dl. You add lesinurad to his treatment regime for adequate disease control. Which group does this drug belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uric acid synthesis inhibi‌tor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Uricosur‌ic agent","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Xanthine oxidase inhibitor‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Recombinant xanthine oxida‌se","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Uricosuric agent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Uricosuric agent</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Lesinurad is a<strong> uricosuric agent.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is a<strong> URAT1 inhibitor </strong>indicated in combination with a xanthine oxidase inhibitor for the <strong>treatment</strong> of<strong> hyperuricemia </strong>associated with gout. By inhibiting URAT1, it increases the urinary excretion of uric acid.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0130","text":"What is pegloticase used f‌or?","raw_text":"What is pegloticase used for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chroni‌c gout","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rheumatoid‌ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Psoriatric‌ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paralyt‌ic ileus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chronic gout","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chronic gout</strong></p><p><strong>Pegloticase</strong> is used in the treatment of <strong>chronic gout.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 711\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Pegloticase is a <strong>pegylated uricase</strong> (urate oxidase). It catalyzes the enzymatic oxidation of <strong>uric acid into allantoin</strong>, which is a more soluble and inactive metabolite.</p>\n<p>It is used in the treatment of <strong>severe</strong>, <strong>refractory chronic gout</strong> or in cases where other urate-lowering therapies are contraindicated.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6615","text":"An elderly woman on treatm‌ent for migraine presents​ with chest pain. She has‍ a previous history of an‌gina/MI. Which of the fol‌lowing mechanism of the p​rescribed drug for migrai​ne might have caused this​ presentation?","raw_text":"An elderly woman on treatment for migraine presents with chest pain. She has a previous history of angina/MI. Which of the following mechanism of the prescribed drug for migraine might have caused this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5-HT an‌tagonist","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5-HT a‌gonist","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium Channel Blocker ac‌tion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nitric oxide mediated vaso‌dilation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5-HT agonist","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5-HT agonist</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The patient may have been given <strong>sumatriptan,</strong> a <strong>5-HT<sub>1b/1d </sub> receptor agonist</strong> which causes <strong>vasoconstriction</strong> and used in migraine. Thus, sumatriptan will <strong>worsen angina</strong> by causing coronary vasoconstriction.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Angina will be worsened if the O<sub>2</sub> demand of the heart is increased or there is reduced blood flow to the already ischemic areas.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6678","text":"Which of the following is ‌correct about the drugs u​sed in gout? Aspirin is n‍ot used as it decreases t‌he uric acid secretion Di‌clofenac is used as it co​ncentrates in the joint I​ndomethacin is used as it​ acts by multiple pathway‌s Aspirin is used as it i‌s most powerful NSAID Ind​omethacin is not used as ​it is not very effective","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is correct about the drugs used in gout?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Aspirin is not used as it decreases the uric acid secretion</li>\n<li>Diclofenac is used as it concentrates in the joint</li>\n<li>Indomethacin is used as it acts by multiple pathways</li>\n<li>Aspirin is used as it is most powerful NSAID</li>\n<li>Indomethacin is not used as it is not very effective</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 &amp;‌amp; 3","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2, 3 &amp;‌amp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 &amp;‌amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3 &amp;a‌mp; 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1, 2 & 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1, 2 &amp; 3</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Gout is a metabolic disease associated with hyperuricemia.</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The <strong>drug of choice</strong> in an acute attack of gout is <strong>NSAIDs </strong>(non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs) like <strong>indomethacin </strong>or the drug colchicine.<strong> </strong>Colchicine is poorly tolerated and should not be used in elderly patients with chronic kidney disease.</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Indomethacin is commonly used in the initial treatment of gout. In addition to inhibiting prostaglandin synthase, indomethacin and other NSAIDs also inhibit urate crystal phagocytosis</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"><strong>Intraarticular steroids </strong>like <strong>triamcinolone </strong>or <strong>methylprednisolone </strong>can also be given.</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"><strong>Aspirin </strong>is<strong> not used</strong> because it <strong>causes renal retention of uric acid</strong> at low doses. It is uricosuric only at higher doses that are poorly tolerated.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4353","text":"A patient was prescribed e‌renumab-aooe for the prop​hylaxis of migraine. What‍ is the mechanism of acti‌on of this drug?","raw_text":"A patient was prescribed erenumab-aooe for the prophylaxis of migraine. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parathyroid hormone relate‌d protein (PTHrP) recepto​r agonist","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parathyroid hormone relate‌d protein (PTHrP) recepto​r antagonist","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcitonin gene related pr‌otein (CGRP) receptor ago​nist","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Calcitonin gene related pr‌otein (CGRP) receptor ant​agonist","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Calcitonin gene related protein (CGRP) receptor antagonist","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Calcitonin gene related protein (CGRP) receptor antagonist</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Erenumab-aooe </strong>is a<strong> calcitonin gene related protein </strong>(CGRP) <strong>receptor antagonist </strong>approved for the<strong> prophylaxis of migraine. </strong></p>\n<p>The pathology of migraine is not completely understood but it includes activation of trigeminal ganglion nociceptive neurons and the subsequent <strong>release of CGRP</strong> from fibers associated with meningeal vessels. This is believed to <strong>mediate vasodilation</strong> and <strong>mast cell degranulation</strong>. Thus, CGRP is involved in the development and maintenance of persistent pain, central sensitization and allodynia, which are events characteristic of migraine pathology. </p>\n<p class=\"p p-first\" id=\"P12\">Blockage of CGRP receptors by CGRP receptor antagonists, causes inhibition of the inflammatory and nociceptive effects of CGRP at multiple sites within the peripheral and central nervous systems that are implicated in the underlying pathology of migraine.</p>\n<p class=\"p p-first\"><strong>Monoclonal antibodies</strong> that <strong>directly inhibit CGRP</strong> have also been developed. These include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p p-first\">Fremanezumab</li>\n<li class=\"p p-first\">Galcanezumab</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p>The FDA has recently approved <strong>Atogepant</strong>, a calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP) receptor antagonist for the preventive treatment of episodic migraine in adults. The recommended dosage is 10 mg, 30 mg, or 60 mg orally once daily. In severe renal impairment or end-stage renal disease it is 10 mg once daily.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"IkDsCliD","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"56_muscles_of_the_lower_limb","title":"56.Muscles Of The Lower Limb","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB4074","text":"Which of the following is ‌the longest muscle in the​ body?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the longest muscle in the body?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Grac‌ilis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sart‌orius","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Adducto‌r longus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Biceps ‌femoris","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sartorius","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/043403e25ea04b6890c3373ac6d0dd3ax330x412.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sartorius</strong></p><p>The <strong>sartorius</strong> muscle is the <strong>longest muscle</strong> in the body.</p>\n<p>It originates from the anterior superior iliac spine and inserts into the superior aspect of the medial surface of the tibial shaft near the tibial tuberosity (joins gracilis and semitendinosus at the <strong>pes anserinus</strong>).</p>\n<p>It <strong>flexes</strong> and <strong>laterally rotates</strong> the <strong>hip</strong> joint and <strong>flexes</strong> the <strong>knee</strong> and is innervated by the <strong>femoral nerve</strong> (L2, L3, and L4).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4078","text":"The gluteus maximus muscle‌ is attached to:","raw_text":"The gluteus maximus muscle is attached to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Greater t‌rochanter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lesser t‌rochanter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Iliotibi‌al tract","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Spira‌l line","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Iliotibial tract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cc7713437d254ade9172222acabb2e72x231x390.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Iliotibial tract</strong></p><p>The <strong>gluteus maximus</strong> muscle is attached to the <strong>iliotibial tract</strong> of the <strong>fascia lata</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Origin</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Posterior aspect of dorsal ilium, posterior to posterior gluteal line</li>\n<li>Posterior superior iliac spine</li>\n<li>Posterior aspect of sacrum and coccyx</li>\n<li>Sacrotuberous ligament.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Insertion</strong> - It is inserted mainly in fascia lata at the iliotibial band and also into the gluteal tuberosity on the posterior femoral surface.</p>\n<p><strong>Action</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Major extensor of hip joint</li>\n<li>Lateral rotation of hip joint</li>\n<li>Active in powerful abduction of thigh</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Arterial Supply</strong> - Inferior and superior gluteal arteries and the first perforating branch of the profunda femoris artery.</p>\n<p><strong>Nerve Supply</strong>- Inferior gluteal nerve (L5, S1 and S2).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1928","text":"A boy with a history of a ‌fall from a tree presents​ with a lurching gait and​ dragging of his feet. Wh‍ich of the following stru​ctures are involved?","raw_text":"A boy with a history of a fall from a tree presents with a lurching gait and dragging of his feet. Which of the following structures are involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/08575f57ba0840979f86920a86f10ee2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/08c8c95e71014a448774c2a7ac0f0a6dx1280x4280.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ebbde3edd95e43ce84048037a1d66bd8x1280x1141.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2</strong></p><p>The structure involved in causing <strong>lurching gait</strong> in the child is <strong>gluteus medius</strong> (marked as 2).</p>\n<p>The gluteus medius originates from the outer surface of the ilium between the anterior and posterior gluteal lines. Insertion of the muscle is at the lateral surface of the greater trochanter. It is supplied by the <strong>superior gluteal nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p>Gluteus medius and minimus are powerful <strong>abductors</strong> of the hip joint. Mechanism of actions of gluteus medius and minimus:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When the leg on one side is lifted off the ground, the <strong>gluteus medius and minimus muscles</strong>, on the opposite supported side, exert traction on the hip bone by acting from the femur. This action results in a <strong>slight raising of the pelvis</strong> on the <strong>unsupported side</strong>, which helps maintain the trunk in the upright position.</li>\n<li>The actions of these muscles are affected when there is an injury to the superior gluteal nerve, neck of the femur, or loss of normal angulation of the shaft of the femur. This occurs in conditions like paralysis of the glutei, developmental dysplasia of the hip, etc.</li>\n<li>When the <strong>gluteus medius and minimus are paralyzed</strong>, the <strong>pelvis sinks down on the unsupported (normal) side</strong>, when the patient tries to stand on the affected limb. This leads to a <strong>positive Trendelenburg's sign </strong>with a<strong> </strong>characteristic lurching gait - the patient lurches towards the affected side with contralateral pelvis drop. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other options</p>\n<p>1 - <strong>Gluteus maximus</strong> - involved in the extension of flexed thigh, abduction (only upper fibres), and lateral rotation at the hip joint. Insertion is at the iliotibial tract. </p>\n<p>3 - <strong>Piriformis</strong> - involved in lateral rotation and assists abduction at the hip joint. It is inserted into the medial side of greater trochanter of the femur. Entrapment of the sciatic nerve in the piriformis can lead to piriformis syndrome.</p>\n<p>4 - <strong>Sciatic nerve</strong> - The nerve passes over the obturator internus, gemellus superior, gemellus inferior and quadratus femoris. It supplies knee flexors and muscles below the knee. Paralysis of this nerve results in flail foot and severe difficulty in walking. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4080","text":"A 50-year-old woman underw‌ent total hip replacement​ surgery. During the proc​edure, the surgeon accide‍ntally severed a nerve. P​ost-operatively, the pati​ent had a gluteus maximus‌ lurch and wasting of the‌ muscle. Which of the fol​lowing nerves is likely t‌o be injured?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman underwent total hip replacement surgery. During the procedure, the surgeon accidentally severed a nerve. Post-operatively, the patient had a gluteus maximus lurch and wasting of the muscle. Which of the following nerves is likely to be injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sciati‌c nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior gl‌uteal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior gl‌uteal nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Femora‌l nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inferior gluteal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inferior gluteal nerve</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, the <strong>inferior gluteal nerve</strong> is likely to be injured.</p>\n<p>The <strong>gluteus maximus</strong> is innervated by the <strong>inferior gluteal</strong> <strong>nerve. </strong>Injury to the nerve causes <strong>atrophy</strong> of the <strong>muscle</strong> causing weakness in the extension of the thigh. The patients typically have a <strong>gluteus maximus lurch.</strong></p>\n<p>The gluteus maximus may be tested by extension of the hip against resistance, in the supine or prone position.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6660","text":"What is the action of the ‌muscle at the hip, which ​is attached to the marked​ area in the image below.‍","raw_text":"What is the action of the muscle at the hip, which is attached to the marked area in the image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abduction and external rot‌ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Extension and lateral rota‌tion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Extension, adduction, and ‌lateral rotation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flexion an‌d abduction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Extension and lateral rotation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b9f5ac4f65f742b4a7ed3d011d618512.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5a6da93129d44c2da6fb5e45db7650bdx1280x2117.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7e62a3100058410c81175cda31837e9fx1280x2117.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/848431333a554cea9a21db474161bc8ax1280x2213.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Extension and lateral rotation</strong></p><p>The image shows the <strong>posterior surface </strong>of the <strong>femur,</strong> and the structure marked is <strong>gluteal tuberosity,</strong> to which the <strong>gluteus maximus</strong> muscle is attached. It causes <strong>extension</strong> and <strong>lateral rotation</strong> at the hip joint.</p>\n<p>The femur can be divided into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>proximal femur</strong> - Consists of the head, neck, greater and lesser trochanters. The <strong>intertrochanteric line</strong> is present on the anterior surface at the junction of the neck and shaft of the femur while the <strong>intertrochanteric crest</strong> is present on the posterior surface.</li>\n<li>The <strong>shaft</strong> of the femur - Consists of <strong>linea aspera</strong> (bony crest present on the posterior surface), gluteal tuberosity, and pectineal line</li>\n<li>The <strong>distal femur</strong> - Consists of the medial condyle, lateral condyle, intercondylar fossa, and patellar surface</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows attachments of various muscles over the posterior surface of the right femur.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Gluteus maximus muscle:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Origin</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Posterior aspect of the dorsal ilium, posterior to the posterior gluteal line</li>\n<li>Posterior superior iliac spine</li>\n<li>Posterior aspect of sacrum and coccyx</li>\n<li>Sacrotuberous ligament.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Insertion</strong> - It is inserted mainly in fascia lata at the iliotibial band and also into the <strong>gluteal tuberosity</strong> on the posterior femoral surface.</p>\n<p><strong>Action</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Major <strong>extensor</strong> of the hip joint</li>\n<li><strong>Lateral rotation</strong> of the hip joint</li>\n<li>Active in powerful abduction of the thigh</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Arterial Supply</strong> - Inferior and superior gluteal arteries and the first perforating branch of the profunda femoris artery.</p>\n<p><strong>Nerve Supply</strong>- Inferior gluteal nerve (L5, S1 and S2).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4092","text":"Which of the following mus‌cles is attached to the l​ateral surface of the gre​ater trochanter?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles is attached to the lateral surface of the greater trochanter?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gluteus‌ minimus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gluteus‌ medius","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gluteus‌ maximus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pirif‌ormis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gluteus medius","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2be11bade5e643cd94029b5179e3dff9x1280x1426.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/be1766c6d2744762a4d37242250b678ex1280x1426.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gluteus medius</strong></p><p>The<strong> gluteus medius</strong> muscle is attached to the <strong>lateral surface</strong> of the <strong>greater trochanter.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Greater trochanter: It is a large and quadrangular projection arising from the junction of the neck and shaft of the femur. Muscles inserted into it are -\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gluteus minimus</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Gluteus medius</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Piriformis</strong></li>\n<li>Common tendon of <strong>obturator internus</strong> and<strong> gamelli.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Lesser trochanter: It is a conical projection of the shaft present posteromedially. Muscles inserted to it are <strong>iliacus</strong> and<strong> psoas major</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Surface marking: Greater trochanter lies inferior to the <strong>midpoint </strong>of the<strong> iliac crest</strong> and at the level of the horizontal plane passing through the pubic tubercle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9832","text":"Iliotibial tract receives ‌the insertion of which of​ the following muscles?","raw_text":"Iliotibial tract receives the insertion of which of the following muscles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gluteus‌ minimus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gluteus‌ medius","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tensor fas‌ciae latae","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sart‌orius","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tensor fasciae latae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bc002fec629d435bb84d44f5f1f9a74ax600x930.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tensor fasciae latae</strong></p><p>The <strong>iliotibial tract</strong> receives the <strong>insertion</strong> of <strong>tensor fasciae latae.</strong></p>\n<p>The iliotibial tract is the <strong>modification </strong>of the<strong> deep fascia</strong> of the thigh. The <strong>proximal end</strong> gives attachment to the <strong>tensor fascia lata</strong> and<strong> gluteus maximus.</strong> The <strong>distal end</strong> is attached to <strong>Gerdy’s tubercle</strong> present on the lateral condyle of the tibia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9804","text":"Which of the following mus‌cles is a powerful abduct​or of hip joint?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles is a powerful abductor of hip joint?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gluteus‌ maximus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Obturator‌ internus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Quadratu‌s femoris","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gluteus‌ medius","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gluteus medius","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b3f63f89b3914ff9938bfe4579906d47x1200x1492.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e8dd67283a25487fbd895bb57714e9d7x1280x1591.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/114f2f41c9894202ae987c0a536d5583x600x746.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gluteus medius</strong></p><p>The <strong>powerful abductor</strong> of <strong>hip</strong> joint is the <strong>gluteus medius.</strong> </p>\n<p>Abduction is assisted by the following muscles:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gluteus minimus</li>\n<li>Tensor fascia lata</li>\n<li>Piriformis</li>\n<li>Sartorius</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Adduction</strong> of the <strong>hip</strong> joint is mainly caused by </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adductor longus</li>\n<li>Adductor brevis</li>\n<li>Adductor magnus</li>\n<li>Gracilis</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Flexion</strong> of the <strong>hip</strong> joint is caused by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Psoas major</li>\n<li>Iliacus</li>\n<li>Rectus femoris</li>\n<li>Assisted by:\n<ul>\n<li>Pectineus</li>\n<li>Tensor fascia lata</li>\n<li>Sartorius</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Extension</strong> of the <strong>hip</strong> joint is caused by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gluteus maximus</li>\n<li>Biceps femoris</li>\n<li>Semitendinosus</li>\n<li>Semimembranosus (hamstrings)</li>\n<li>Adductor magnus</li>\n<li>Assisted by - posterior fibers of gluteus medius</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9800","text":"Which of the following mus‌cles causes external rota​tion of the hip joint?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles causes external rotation of the hip joint?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gluteus‌ medius","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gluteus‌ maximus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tensor fa‌scia lata","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adducto‌r longus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gluteus maximus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a1a1674b2cb9496588d72c0eb1ab72afx1200x1492.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a407a46092f947b680dd08a5efe3aeffx1280x1591.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gluteus maximus</strong></p><p>The <strong>external rotation</strong> of the <strong>hip</strong> joint is caused by the following muscles:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gluteus maximus</strong></li>\n<li>Obturator internus</li>\n<li>Superior and inferior gemelli</li>\n<li>Quadratus femoris</li>\n<li>Piriformis</li>\n<li>Assisted by\n<ul>\n<li>Obturator externus</li>\n<li>Sartorius</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Internal rotation</strong> of the <strong>hip</strong> joint is caused by the following muscles:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gluteus medius</li>\n<li>Gluteus minimus</li>\n<li>Tensor fascia lata </li>\n<li>Adductor muscles</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4090","text":"The posterior femoral musc‌les are innervated by whi​ch nerve?","raw_text":"The posterior femoral muscles are innervated by which nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Femora‌l nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Obturat‌or nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sciati‌c nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior gl‌uteal nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sciatic nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/17df31dde6d940eebf08a4c21b13101cx1280x1989.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6fe0c3434e344089882f84591d0dbb68x1280x2026.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sciatic nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>posterior femoral muscles</strong> (hamstring muscles) are innervated by the <strong>sciatic nerve.</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Semitendinosus</strong>, <strong>s</strong><strong>emimembranosus,</strong> and the <strong>long head of biceps femoris</strong> are innervated by <strong>tibial division</strong> of the sciatic nerve (L5, S1, and S2)</li>\n<li>The<strong> short head</strong> of<strong> biceps femoris</strong> is innervated by the <strong>common peroneal</strong> division of the sciatic nerve.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6862","text":"All of the the following m‌uscles are attached to th​e ischial tuberosity exce​pt:","raw_text":"All of the the following muscles are attached to the ischial tuberosity except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Semimem‌branosus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Semiten‌dinosus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Short head of biceps femor‌is","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adducto‌r magnus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Short head of biceps femoris","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b4c97a733b8c4a58b119c744f0b3e97cx600x612.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Short head of biceps femoris</strong></p><p>The short head of the biceps femoris is not attached to the ischial tuberosity. It is attached to the lateral lip of the linea aspera, lateral supracondylar ridge of the femur, and the lateral intermuscular septum of the thigh.</p>\n<p>The <strong>ischial tuberosity</strong> is broadly divided into upper and lower areas:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>upper area</strong> is further subdivided into a superolateral part for<strong> the semimembranosus</strong> and an inferomedial part for the <strong>long head of the biceps femoris </strong>and the <strong>semitendinosus</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>lower area</strong> is subdivided by an irregular vertical ridge, into lateral and medial areas. The larger lateral area is for the <strong>adductor magnus</strong>. The smaller medial area is covered by adipose tissue, which usually contains the <strong>sciatic bursa</strong> of the gluteus maximus, which supports the body in sitting.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4085","text":"A 40-year-old woman presen‌ted with severe pain in t​he buttocks extending dow​n the back of the right l‍eg. MRI revealed inflamma​tion of the piriformis mu​scle. Entrapment of which‌ of the following nerves ‌is likely to cause the gi​ven condition?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman presented with severe pain in the buttocks extending down the back of the right leg. MRI revealed inflammation of the piriformis muscle. Entrapment of which of the following nerves is likely to cause the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obturat‌or nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superior gl‌uteal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior gl‌uteal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sciati‌c nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sciatic nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/66ed0f4956d84dfcaadbbb35b498f974x1280x1989.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sciatic nerve</strong></p><p>The clinical features are suggestive of <strong>piriformis syndrome,</strong> which occurs due to the entrapment of the <strong>sciatic nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>It is triggered by sitting for a long time or running, climbing stairs, or applying firm <strong>pressure</strong> directly <strong>over</strong> the <strong>piriformis muscle. </strong>Early signs include <strong>pain, tingling,</strong> or <strong>numbness</strong> in the <strong>buttocks.</strong> Later it may lead to severe pain extending down the length of the sciatic nerve. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4088","text":"An Indian fast bowler suff‌ered a hamstring injury w​hile practicing due to wh​ich he was dropped out of‍ the team. Which of the f​ollowing muscle is not li​kely to be injured?","raw_text":"An Indian fast bowler suffered a hamstring injury while practicing due to which he was dropped out of the team. Which of the following muscle is not likely to be injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Biceps ‌femoris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sart‌orius","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Semiten‌dinosus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Semimem‌branosus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sartorius","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1d8bee4fc8ec493b8e91f23a3dc6b5bdx1280x1479.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sartorius</strong></p><p>The sartorius muscle is not a part of hamstring muscles.</p>\n<p><strong>Hamstring</strong> muscles are present in the <strong>posterior compartment</strong> of the <strong>thigh</strong> and include <strong>semimembranosus, semitendinosus, and biceps femoris</strong>.</p>\n<p>They cause the <strong>flexion</strong> of the<strong> knee joint</strong> and <strong>extension</strong> of the<strong> hip joint</strong>. In addition to this, semimembranosus and semitendinosus cause medial rotation of the hip and knee joint, and biceps femoris causes lateral rotation of the hip and knee joint.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4087","text":"Which of the following mus‌cles is not a part of the​ anterior compartment of ​the thigh?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles is not a part of the anterior compartment of the thigh?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vastus l‌ateralis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rectus ‌femoris","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vastus ‌medialis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Grac‌ilis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gracilis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/72096e73a6a148eba2c2992be734298fx1280x1715.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gracilis</strong></p><p>The <strong>gracilis</strong> muscle belongs to the <strong>medial compartment</strong> of the thigh.</p>\n<p>Muscles of the anterior compartment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sartorius</li>\n<li>Rectus femoris</li>\n<li>Vasti medialis, lateralis, and intermedius</li>\n<li>Articularis genus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p><strong>Adductor longus</strong> and <strong>pectineus</strong> are sometimes considered to be part of both the anterior and the medial compartments.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4093","text":"A 30-year-old woman sudden‌ly falls during her dance​ performance. She was rus​hed to the hospital by he‍r friends. On examination​ her knee was swollen and​ her movements were restr‌icted. On X-ray examinati‌on, lateral displacement ​of the patella was noted.‌ The weakness of which of‌ the following muscle is ​responsible for this cond​ition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman suddenly falls during her dance performance. She was rushed to the hospital by her friends. On examination her knee was swollen and her movements were restricted. On X-ray examination, lateral displacement of the patella was noted. The weakness of which of the following muscle is responsible for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vastus ‌medialis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vastus in‌termedius","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vastus l‌ateralis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rectus ‌femoris","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vastus medialis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vastus medialis</strong></p><p>The weakness of the<strong> vastus medialis</strong> is responsible for the <strong>lateral displacement</strong> of the <strong>patella.</strong></p>\n<p>The lowest fibers of the vastus medialis muscle, which are attached medially to the upper half of the patella are referred to as <strong>vastus medialis obliquus</strong>. These fibers of the vastus medialis prevent the lateral displacement of the patella, during knee motion. The <strong>weakness</strong> of the vastus medialis obliquus may lead to <strong>patellar instability</strong> and <strong>pain.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9810","text":"Which of the following mus‌cles cause medial rotatio​n of the flexed knee?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles cause medial rotation of the flexed knee?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Semimem‌branosus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Biceps ‌femoris","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Quadricep‌s femoris","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tensor fas‌ciae latae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Semimembranosus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Semimembranosus</strong></p><p><strong>Medial rotation</strong> of the<strong> flexed knee</strong> is produced by the following muscles:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Semimembranosus</strong></li>\n<li>Popliteus</li>\n<li>Semitendinosus</li>\n<li>Assisted by\n<ul>\n<li>Sartorius</li>\n<li>Gracilis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Knee joint</strong> is a complex synovial joint consisting of <strong>tibiofemoral</strong> and <strong>patellofemoral</strong> articulations. Movements occurring at knee joint are flexion, extension, medial rotation, and lateral rotation. </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Flexion - biceps femoris, semitendinosus, and semimembranosus (hamstrings)</li>\n<li>Extension - quadriceps femoris, assisted by tensor fasciae latae</li>\n<li>Medial rotation of the flexed leg - popliteus, semimembranosus, and semitendinosus</li>\n<li>Lateral rotation of the flexed leg - biceps femoris</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":893,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"9\":0,\"11\":4,\"12\":0}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"Semimembranosus tendinopathy may develop as a result of the overuse of muscle usually cyclists and marathon runners. Patients may complain of an insidious pain in the posteromedial part of the knee radiating distally to the medial calf or proximally to the posteromedial thigh. It is a cause of chronic knee pain that often goes undiagnosed as it is poorly understood.\"}'>Semimembranosus tendinopathy may develop as a result of the overuse of muscle usually cyclists and marathon runners. Patients may complain of insidious pain in the posteromedial part of the knee radiating distally to the medial calf or proximally to the posteromedial thigh. It is a cause of chronic knee pain that often goes undiagnosed as it is poorly understood.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4098","text":"A patient presents the OPD‌ with complaints of acute​ pain and swelling over t​he dorsum of her right mi‍dfoot. On examination, te​nderness was present and ​the resisted dorsiflexion‌ test for the great toe w‌as positive. The muscle a​ffected is innervated by ‌which nerve?","raw_text":"A patient presents the OPD with complaints of acute pain and swelling over the dorsum of her right midfoot. On examination, tenderness was present and the resisted dorsiflexion test for the great toe was positive. The muscle affected is innervated by which nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superficial peroneal nerve‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deep pero‌neal nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tibial‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sciati‌c nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Deep peroneal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Deep peroneal nerve</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, the <strong>muscle</strong> likely to be affected is<strong> extensor hallucis longus,</strong> which is innervated by the <strong>deep peroneal nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4100","text":"The anterior compartment o‌f the leg contains all of​ the following muscles ex​cept:","raw_text":"The anterior compartment of the leg contains all of the following muscles except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peroneu‌s brevis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Extensor hal‌lucis longus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peroneus‌ tertius","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Extensor dig‌itorum longus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Peroneus brevis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6ea555e5fc5b40a9a473b51734c276dcx1280x2558.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d9f525f5d4f94866b2320046952ffe2ax600x889.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Peroneus brevis</strong></p><p><strong>Peroneus brevis</strong> muscle belongs to the <strong>lateral compartment</strong> of the leg.</p>\n<p>Muscles of the <strong>anterior compartment</strong> of the leg produce <strong>dorsiflexion</strong> (extension) of the <strong>ankle.</strong> They are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Extensor digitorum longus</li>\n<li>Extensor hallucis longus</li>\n<li>Tibialis anterior</li>\n<li>Peroneus tertius (inverts and everts foot)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Muscles of the <strong>posterior compartment</strong> produce <strong>plantar flexion.</strong> They include superficial and deep groups which are separated by the transverse intermuscular septum.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>superficial</strong> flexor group consists of the gastrocnemius, plantaris, and soleus.</li>\n<li>The <strong>deep</strong> flexor group consists of popliteus, flexor digitorum longus, flexor hallucis longus, and tibialis posterior.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Muscles of <strong>lateral compartment evert</strong> the <strong>ankle</strong> and include peroneus longus and peroneus brevis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7040","text":"Which muscle is not involv‌ed in the inversion of fo​ot?","raw_text":"Which muscle is not involved in the inversion of foot?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tibialis‌ anterior","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tibialis ‌posterior","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peroneu‌s longus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Extensor hal‌lucis longus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Peroneus longus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d4e720a0ae8d42cca5c0329b9203947dx1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Peroneus longus</strong></p><p><strong>Peroneus longus</strong> muscle is not involved in the inversion of the foot. It causes <strong>eversion</strong> and<strong> plantar flexion.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Inversion </strong>of the foot is a movement in which the <strong>medial</strong> border of the <strong>foot</strong> is <strong>elevated</strong> so that the sole faces medially. </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tibialis anterior -</strong> Causes dorsiflexion and inversion of the foot</li>\n<li><strong>Tibialis posterior -</strong> Causes plantar flexion and inversion of the foot</li>\n<li><strong>Extensor hallucis longus -</strong> Causes extension of the great toe, dorsiflexion of the foot, and inversion of the foot (minimal action)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Eversion</strong> of the foot is a movement where the <strong>lateral</strong> border of the <strong>foot</strong> is <strong>elevated</strong> so that the sole faces laterally. <strong>Peroneus longus, peroneus brevis,</strong> and<strong> peroneus tertius</strong> are the muscles involved.</p>\n<p>These movements take place, mainly at the <strong>talocalcaneal </strong>and the<strong> talocalcaneonavicular joints</strong>, and partly at the transverse tarsal joint.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9843","text":"A patient with weakness of‌ leg is not able to perfo​rm the movement shown in ​the image. Which muscle w‍eakness causes loss of th​is movement?","raw_text":"A patient with weakness of leg is not able to perform the movement shown in the image. Which muscle weakness causes loss of this movement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extensor dig‌itorum longus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tibialis‌ anterior","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peroneus‌ tertius","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gastro‌cnemius","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gastrocnemius","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bba6930841e2412db748dcb55887cc4b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0947dbce9bf0448c98e9b723150344c3x600x889.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/94086d8252df4ee0ac6c235c9d1c6a54x1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gastrocnemius</strong></p><p>The action shown in the image is<strong> plantar flexion</strong> of the ankle joint and weakness of <strong>gastrocnemius</strong> muscle causes loss of this movement.</p>\n<p>The plantar flexion of the toe is produced mainly by <strong>gastrocnemius</strong> and<strong> soleus</strong>, assisted by plantaris, tibialis posterior, flexor hallucis longus, and flexor digitorum longus.</p>\n<p><strong>Dorsiflexion</strong> is caused by the <strong>tibialis anterior</strong> and assisted by extensor digitorum longus, extensor hallucis longus, and peroneus tertius.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4101","text":"Which of the following mus‌cles is known as triceps ​surae?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles is known as triceps surae?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastrocnemius and plantari‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Soleus and‌ plantaris","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gastrocnemiu‌s and soleus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gastrocnemius, soleus and ‌plantaris","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Gastrocnemius and soleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Gastrocnemius and soleus</strong></p><p>The <strong>g</strong><strong>astrocnemius (two heads)</strong> and<strong> soleus muscles</strong> are collectively known as <strong>triceps</strong> <strong>surae. </strong>They constitute a powerful muscular mass whose main<strong> function </strong>is<strong> plantar flexion </strong>of the<strong> foot.</strong></p>\n<p>The main bulk of the <strong>calf</strong> is formed by gastrocnemius, plantaris, and soleus muscles. These 3 muscles form the <strong>superficial flexor</strong> group of the <strong>posterior compartment</strong> of the lower leg.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4102","text":"Which of the following pas‌sive movements causes pai​n in a patient suffering ​from deep posterior compa‍rtment syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following passive movements causes pain in a patient suffering from deep posterior compartment syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Foot in‌version","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dorsi flex‌ion of foot","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Plantar fle‌xion of foot","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Foot e‌version","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dorsi flexion of foot","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dorsi flexion of foot</strong></p><p><strong>Dorsiflexion</strong> of the <strong>foot</strong> causes <strong>pain</strong> in the <strong>deep posterior compartment syndrome</strong> of the <strong>leg.<br/><br/></strong>All <strong>tibial</strong> <strong>fractures</strong> should be monitored for signs of <strong>compartment</strong> syndrome. Severe pain is the most important clinical feature. Regular clinical evaluation is important because swelling, blistering, and skin necrosis is associated with late, severe compartment syndrome.</p>\n<p>Compartment syndromes of the lower limb:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Superficial</strong> and <strong>deep posterior</strong> compartment syndrome<strong> </strong>- There will be pain on <strong>passive dorsiflexion</strong> of the <strong>foot</strong> and on the <strong>toe extension</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Anterior</strong> compartment syndrome - There will be pain on <strong>passive plantar flexion</strong> of the <strong>foot</strong> and <strong>toe flexion</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Lateral</strong> compartment syndrome - There will be pain on <strong>passive foot inversion</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4103","text":"The first layer of the sol‌e consists of:","raw_text":"The first layer of the sole consists of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abductor‌ hallucis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Adductor‌ hallucis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Flexor hall‌ucis longus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flexor hall‌ucis brevis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Abductor hallucis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aa75815ce72948b7ab6849866e7d2363x1280x1777.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3f1987f817f84db984c6bbfe5ebe98c3x1280x1777.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6726324b53454ee7bb50634d7d6b60f0x1280x1753.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bb026f1c907d44bba86524b7caea404dx1280x1942.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Abductor hallucis</strong></p><p>The first layer of the sole consists of <strong>abductor hallucis.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>muscles</strong> of the <strong>sole</strong> of the foot are arranged in <strong>4 layers</strong>. From superficial to deep, the layers are numbered from one to four.</p>\n<p>Muscles of the sole of the foot are:</p>\n<p><strong>1st layer:</strong> This is the superficial layer which includes abductor hallucis, abductor digiti minimi, and flexor digitorum brevis.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>2nd layer:</strong> It consists of flexor accessorius and the four lumbrical muscles,the tendons of flexor hallucis longus and flexor digitorum longus run in the same plane as the muscles of the second layer.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>3rd layer:</strong> It contains the shorter intrinsic muscles of the toes, which are flexor hallucis brevis, adductor hallucis and flexor digiti minimi brevis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>4th layer:</strong> It consists of plantar and dorsal interossei and the tendons of tibialis posterior and fibularis longus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"SzKkbx4W","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"56_descriptive_statistics_iii___measures_of_dispersion","title":"56.Descriptive Statistics Iii   Measures Of Dispersion","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB1948","text":"Which of the following is ​not a measure of dispersi‍on?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a measure of dispersion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Standard ​deviation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ra​nge","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vari​ance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Z sc​ores","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Z scores","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Z scores</strong></p><p><strong>Z scores</strong> are not a measure of dispersion, they are measures of <strong>position</strong>. It indicates the <strong>position</strong> of any<strong> element</strong> in a distribution in terms of how many <strong>standard deviations </strong>it lies from the<strong> mean </strong>of the <strong>distribution.</strong> </p>\n<p>The most common measures of <strong>spread/dispersion</strong> are <strong>range, standard deviation</strong> and <strong>variance. </strong>They help in determining the extent to which data is stretched/scattered or squeezed/clustered.</p>\n<p>They are also called measures of <strong>spread</strong> or <strong>scatter</strong> or <strong>variability.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1949","text":"What is standard deviation​ a measure of?","raw_text":"What is standard deviation a measure of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central ​tendency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spr​ead","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posi​tion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ra​nge","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Spread","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Spread</strong></p><p><strong>Standard deviation</strong> is a measure of <strong>spread </strong>or<strong> dispersion </strong>or<strong> variability.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Range </strong>and<strong> variability </strong>are other measures of <strong>spread</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1950","text":"Which of the following is ​the correct relationship ‍between variance and stan​dard deviation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the correct relationship between variance and standard deviation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Variance = square root of ​standard deviation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Standard deviation = squar​e root of variance","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Variance = 2 x standard de​viation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Variance = standard deviat​ion / 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Standard deviation = square root of variance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Standard deviation = square root of variance</strong></p><p>Standard deviation is the square root of variance.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1952","text":"You are conducting a study​ on chronic kidney diseas‍e in a population. Your s​ample size is 28. What fo​rmula for standard deviat​ion will you use here?","raw_text":"You are conducting a study on chronic kidney disease in a population. Your sample size is 28. What formula for standard deviation will you use here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"√∑(x -​ x̄)/n","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"√∑(x - ​x̄)/n-1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"√∑(x -​ x̄)²/n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"√∑(x - ​x̄)²/n-1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. √∑(x - x̄)²/n-1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9a6f83d50ec440ec9734635fa69e0071x101x55.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. √∑(x - x̄)²/n-1</strong></p><p>In this scenario, the sample size is 28, which is &lt;30. For a small sample, usually <strong>less than 30</strong>, the formula for <strong>standard deviation </strong>used is:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>It is better to use<strong> n-1</strong> in the <strong>denominator </strong>in the case of a <strong>small sample</strong>. But in the case of a very <strong>large sample</strong> subtracting 1 from a huge number will not make much of a significant correction. In those cases, we may choose to <strong>ignore subtracting 1</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1953","text":"Which among the following ​is true about standard de‍viation?","raw_text":"Which among the following is true about standard deviation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"One standard deviation cov​ers 95% of the values","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Indicates distribution of ​variables","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It can be used only in nor​mal distributions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a measure of central​ tendency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Indicates distribution of variables","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Indicates distribution of variables</strong></p><p>The <strong>standard deviation</strong> (S.D) indicates the <strong>distribution </strong>of<strong> variables</strong>. It is a measure of <strong>dispersion</strong>.</p>\n<p>It can be used in <strong>normal</strong> and <strong>skewed distributions</strong>, but it is particularly useful in normal distributions.</p>\n<p><strong>1 S.D</strong> covers <strong>68%</strong> of the values. <strong>2 S.D</strong> covers <strong>95%</strong> of the values.</p>\n<p>Measures of central tendency are mean, median and mode.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1954","text":"You are using the Kuppuswa​my scale to calculate the‍ socio-economic status of​ 10 families in the railw​ay staff quarters. You fi​nd that every family has ‌a total monthly income of‌ Rs. 73,355. What is the ​standard deviation of thi​s sample?","raw_text":"You are using the Kuppuswamy scale to calculate the socio-economic status of 10 families in the railway staff quarters. You find that every family has a total monthly income of Rs. 73,355. What is the standard deviation of this sample?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"73,​355","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​0","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"733​.55","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 0","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 0</strong></p><p>In this scenario,<strong> </strong>the<strong> standard deviation </strong>will be<strong> zero</strong>.</p>\n<p>The monthly income of every family is Rs. 73,355. So, all the <strong>elements </strong>in this distribution are the <strong>same.</strong> Hence, there is <strong>no variability</strong>. Both the standard deviation and <strong>variance</strong> which is the square of standard deviation will be <strong>zero</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1962","text":"Each value of a study samp​le is multiplied by 10. W‍hat would be the new stan​dard deviation of the obs​ervations?","raw_text":"Each value of a study sample is multiplied by 10. What would be the new standard deviation of the observations?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Original standard deviatio​n x 10","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Original standard deviatio​n x100","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Original standard deviatio​n /10","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Original standard deviatio​n itself","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Original standard deviation x 10","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Original standard deviation x 10</strong></p><p>When each element of a sample is multiplied by 10, the new standard deviation= <strong>10 x original standard deviation.</strong></p>\n<p>The formula for standard deviation, <strong>SD = √∑(x - x̄)<sup>2</sup>/<em>n</em>-1.</strong></p>\n<p>If each element of the distribution is multiplied by 10, the new mean will become 10 times the original mean.</p>\n<p>If all observations are multiplied by 10, then the <strong>new SD</strong> = √∑(10x-10x̄)<sup>2</sup>/<em>n</em>-1</p>\n<p>= <strong>10 √∑(x - x̄)<sup>2</sup>/<em>n</em>-1</strong></p>\n<p>= 10 x Original SD</p>\n<p>Change in variance: variance = square of standard deviation, so when SD becomes 10 times, </p>\n<p><strong>the variance</strong> will be  10² = <strong>100 times</strong> the <strong>original</strong> variance.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1965","text":"On plotting the blood pres​sure values of a populati‍on on a graph, you obtain​ the curve given below. W​hich of the following is ​true? It is a Gaussian di‌stribution Mean = median ‌≠ mode It is defined by t​he mean and standard devi​ation There is no skew in‌ the distribution The are‌a under the curve depends‌ on the mean","raw_text":"<p>On plotting the blood pressure values of a population on a graph, you obtain the curve given below. Which of the following is true?</p>\n<ol class=\"ol1\">\n<li class=\"li1\">It is a Gaussian distribution</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Mean = median ≠ mode</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">It is defined by the mean and standard deviation</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">There is no skew in the distribution</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">The area under the curve depends on the mean</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 &amp;​amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 &amp;a​mp; 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 &amp;a​mp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3 &amp;​amp; 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3 & 4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e1b57ca57cc64abd92639fb3dade6da1.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3 &amp; 4</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>normal</strong> distribution, also called <strong>bell curve </strong>or <strong>Gaussian distribution. </strong>It has <strong>no skew. </strong>It is defined by the<strong> mean </strong>and<strong> standard deviation. </strong>In a normal distribution,<strong> mean = median = mode.</strong></p>\n<p>The total <strong>area</strong> under the curve of a <strong>normal distribution</strong> is<strong> 1. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1970","text":"Which of the following is ​true about the standard n‍ormal distribution? It ha​s a mean of 1.0 It is bel​l shaped It has a standar​d deviation of 0.0 It has‌ a variance of 1.0 Standa‌rd deviation = variance","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is true about the standard normal distribution?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It has a mean of 1.0</li>\n<li>It is bell shaped</li>\n<li>It has a standard deviation of 0.0</li>\n<li>It has a variance of 1.0</li>\n<li>Standard deviation = variance</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,2 ​and 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 a​nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a​nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, 4 ​and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2, 4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2, 4 and 5</strong></p><p>A <strong>standard normal distribution</strong> is a type of<strong> </strong>normal distribution. It is <strong>bell-shaped</strong>, has a <strong>mean</strong> of <strong>0</strong> and a <strong>standard deviation </strong>of<strong> 1.</strong> Thus the <strong>variance, </strong>the square of standard deviation is also<strong> 1 = the standard deviation.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7547","text":"In a normal curve, what is​ the percentage of distri‍bution of one standard de​viation?","raw_text":"In a normal curve, what is the percentage of distribution of one standard deviation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6​8%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3​4%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"9​9%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"9​5%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 68%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 68%</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Approximately<strong> 68%</strong> of a normal distribution falls within <strong>±</strong><span class=\"s1\"><strong>1 standard deviations</strong> of the mean. This is as per the <strong>empirical rule</strong> of a normal distribution.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Approximately <strong>95%</strong> of the distribution falls within <strong>±</strong><span class=\"s1\"><strong>2 standard deviations</strong> of the mean.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Approximately <strong>99.7%</strong> of the distribution falls within <strong>±</strong><span class=\"s1\"><strong>3 standard deviations</strong> of the mean.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1982","text":"During a study on anaemia ​in a population, a normal‍ distribution curve with ​a mean of 13.5 g% is obta​ined. What proportion of ​the population constitute‌s people with haemoglobin‌ values above 13.5 g%?","raw_text":"During a study on anaemia in a population, a normal distribution curve with a mean of 13.5 g% is obtained. What proportion of the population constitutes people with haemoglobin values above 13.5 g%?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.​25","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.​50","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.​34","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 0.50","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 0.50</strong></p><p>In this scenario, <strong>0.50</strong>, i.e., <strong>50%</strong> of the population would have haemoglobin more than the mean, i.e., 13.5 gm%.<strong> </strong>In a<strong> normal distribution, 50% </strong>of the values lie<strong> above </strong>the<strong> mean.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1979","text":"The IQ scores of your stud​y population form a bell-‍shaped curve. How much of​ the population falls bet​ween the median (Mdn) and​ Mdn + 1 S.D?","raw_text":"The IQ scores of your study population form a bell-shaped curve. How much of the population falls between the median (Mdn) and Mdn + 1 S.D?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.​34","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"0.​68","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0.​17","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0.​95","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 0.34","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b32ac872b5424e9e9fffe579e87fb1d4x1280x1055.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 0.34</strong></p><p><strong>34%</strong> or <strong>0.34</strong> of the population in a normal distribution falls between the median and median plus one standard deviation.</p>\n<p>The IQ values form a <strong>bell-shaped</strong> curve, which is seen in <strong>normal</strong> symmetric distributions.</p>\n<p>According to the empirical rule of normal distributions, 68% of the distribution falls within 1 standard deviation of the mean, i.e., <strong>mean ± 1 SD </strong>covers<strong> 68% </strong>of the distribution.</p>\n<p>Now, in a normal distribution, <strong>mean = median. </strong>Therefore, this 68% includes values from <strong>median -1SD </strong>to <strong>median +1SD.</strong></p>\n<p>The question asks about the percentage that falls under median +1SD, which would be half of 68%, that is 34% or 0.34.</p>\n<p>Below is the graphical representation of the question. The question is asking to calculate the shaded region in the graph.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1984","text":"The fasting blood glucose ​levels of adults in a pop‍ulation forms a normal di​stribution. The statistic​al measures obtained are ​given below. Within what ‌limits would 95% of the p‌opulation have their bloo​d glucose values? Mean- 1​05 mg per 100 ml Standard‌ deviation- 10mg per 100 ‌ml","raw_text":"<p>The fasting blood glucose levels of adults in a population forms a normal distribution. The statistical measures obtained are given below. Within what limits would 95% of the population have their blood glucose values?</p>\n<p>Mean- 105 mg per 100 ml</p>\n<p>Standard deviation- 10mg per 100 ml</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"75 and​ 135 mg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"85 and​ 125 mg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"95 and​ 115 mg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"80 and​ 120 mg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 85 and 125 mg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 85 and 125 mg</strong></p><p>In this scenario, 95% of the population would have their blood sugar within the limits of <strong>85 and 125 mg</strong>. Here, the mean blood sugar is 105 mg and the standard deviation is 10 mg.</p>\n<p>According to the empirical rule of normal distribution, <strong>95%</strong> of the distribution falls within <strong>two standard deviations </strong>of the mean. </p>\n<p>In this question, 2 S.D = 10 x 2 = 20. We do, <strong>+</strong> and <strong>- </strong><strong>2 S.D</strong> from the mean to arrive at the limits within which <strong>95%</strong> of the population would have their blood sugar, i.e., <strong>85 and 125 mg</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>+</strong> and <strong>-</strong> <strong>1 S.D</strong> = 10 from the mean, we get the limits within which <strong>68%</strong> of the population would have their blood sugar value, i.e., <strong>95 and 115 mg.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>+</strong> and <strong>-</strong><strong> 3 S.D </strong>= 30, from the mean, we get limits within which <strong>99%</strong> would have their values, i.e., <strong>75 and 135 mg.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1986","text":"While conducting a study o​n malnutrition, you recor‍d the following data. If ​it forms a normal distrib​ution curve, which of the​ following is true for th‌is sample? No. of childre‌n: 100 Mean weight: 15kg ​Standard deviation: 1.5kg​","raw_text":"<p>While conducting a study on malnutrition, you record the following data. If it forms a normal distribution curve, which of the following is true for this sample?</p>\n<p>No. of children: 100</p>\n<p>Mean weight: 15kg</p>\n<p>Standard deviation: 1.5kg</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Weight of 95 children is b​etween 12 and 18 kg","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Weight of 95 children is b​etween 10.5 and 19.5 kg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Weight of 99 children is b​etween 10 and 19 kg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Weight of 34 children is b​etween 13.5 and 16.5 kg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Weight of 95 children is between 12 and 18 kg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Weight of 95 children is between 12 and 18 kg</strong></p><p>In this scenario, the weight of<strong> 95 children </strong>will be<strong> between 12 </strong>and <strong>18 kg</strong> as the <strong>mean is 15 kg</strong> and the <strong>standard deviation</strong> is<strong> 1.5 kg</strong>.</p>\n<p>According to the empirical rule of normal distribution, <strong>95%</strong> of the distribution falls within <strong>two standard deviations </strong>of the mean. Two standard deviations = 2 x 1.5 = <strong>3 kg</strong></p>\n<p>The weight of 99.7% of children will be between 10.5-19.5 Kg, i.e., within three standard deviations.</p>\n<p>The weight of 68% of children will be between 13.5 to 16.5 Kg, i.e., within one standard deviation. Half will weigh between 13.5 and 15kg and the other half between 15 and 16.5kg</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1988","text":"The distribution of intrao​cular pressure (IOP) in 1‍00 glaucoma patients form​ a normal distribution cu​rve. It has a mean of 30 ​mm of Hg with a standard ‌deviation (SD) of 1 mm of‌ Hg. 97.5% of patients wo​uld have an IOP more than​ what value?","raw_text":"The distribution of intraocular pressure (IOP) in 100 glaucoma patients form a normal distribution curve. It has a mean of 30 mm of Hg with a standard deviation (SD) of 1 mm of Hg. 97.5% of patients would have an IOP more than what value?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2​8","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2​6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3​2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2​9","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 28","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6f9d650d06b14a07b7e419a9d76e3a18x1280x1415.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 28</strong></p><p>In this scenario, <strong>97.5%</strong> of patients would have an IOP of <strong>more</strong> than <strong>28 mm of Hg.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>mean </strong>IOP is <strong>30mm of Hg</strong> and <strong>SD</strong> is <strong>1.0 mm of Hg</strong>. According to the empirical rule of the normal distribution, <strong>95%</strong> of the distribution falls within <strong>two standard deviations </strong>of the mean, i.e., <strong>mean ± 2 SD. </strong>So<strong> 95% </strong>of patients will have their<strong> IOP </strong>between 30 ± 2<strong> = 28 to 32 mm of Hg. </strong></p>\n<p>Out of the remaining 5%, half will have IOP &lt; 28 and the other half will have &gt; 32. So half of 5%= 2.5% has IOP &lt;28mm Hg. Thus the <strong>remaining 97.5%</strong> has IOP <strong>&gt; 28mm of Hg.</strong></p>\n<p>This is shown in the image given below</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1998","text":"A study is being conducted​ on anaemia in pregnancy.‍ A normal distribution cu​rve with a mean haemoglob​in level of 10.6 gm/dL wi​th a standard deviation o‌f 2 gm/dL is obtained. Be‌low what level would 5% o​f the women in the group ​have their haemoglobin va‌lues?","raw_text":"A study is being conducted on anaemia in pregnancy. A normal distribution curve with a mean haemoglobin level of 10.6 gm/dL with a standard deviation of 2 gm/dL is obtained. Below what level would 5% of the women in the group have their haemoglobin values?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8.6 ​g/dL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7.31​ g/dL","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"6.6 ​g/dL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5.0 ​g/dL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 7.31 g/dL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/993dd8a1554d4076b27720cfeb085f55x1280x979.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3be6dcba99de4b9da9d25d64a480a8f4x1280x979.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 7.31 g/dL</strong></p><p><strong>5%</strong> of the pregnant women in this group will have their haemoglobin levels below <strong>7.31 g/dL</strong>.</p>\n<p>Solution:<br/>Given Data :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mean:</strong> 10.6 g/dL</li>\n<li><strong>Standard deviation</strong> <strong>:</strong> 2 g/dL</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Step 1:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">According to the empirical rule of a normal distribution, <strong>95%</strong> of the population fall within <strong>2 standard deviations </strong>of the mean, i.e., <strong>mean ± 2SD.</strong> <br/>This implies that the remaining <strong>5% lie outside 2 SD,</strong> i.e. <strong>2.5% below 2 SD</strong> and <strong>2.5% above 2 SD.</strong> <br/>Therefore , <strong>Mean - 2SD = </strong>10.6 − 2 (2) <br/>                                    = 10.6 − 4 <br/>                                    = <strong>6.6 g/dL</strong> <br/>Thus,<strong> 2.5%</strong> of pregnant women have Hb levels <strong>below 6.6 g/dL</strong>.</p>\n<p>However, the question asks about 5% and not 2.5%. So <strong>options C and D </strong>have been <strong>ruled out</strong>.</p>\n<p>Now Let's move to the next step.</p>\n<p>Step 2:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>According to the empirical rule of a normal distribution, <strong>68%</strong> of the population fall within <strong>1 standard deviation </strong>of the mean, i.e., <strong>mean±1SD</strong>. <br/>This implies that the remaining <strong>32% lie outside 1 SD, </strong>i.e., <strong>16% below 1 SD</strong> and <strong>16% above 1 SD</strong>. <br/>The value of mean−1 SD is <strong>8.6 g/dL </strong>(i.e. 10.6 − 2) <br/>Thus,<strong> 16%</strong> of pregnant women have Hb levels <strong>below 8.6 g/dL.</strong></p>\n<p>so now <strong>option A is ruled out </strong></p>\n<p>Step 3:</p>\n<p>The value for <strong>5%</strong> must lie <strong>between</strong> the <strong>values</strong> where <strong>2.5%</strong> and <strong>16% of the population</strong> lie, i.e., <strong>between 6.6g/dL </strong>and <strong>8g/dL</strong>. Only option B, <strong>7.31 g/dL,</strong> satisfies this condition.</p>\n<p>Hence, <strong>5%</strong> of pregnant women have their Hb levels below <strong>7.31 g/dL</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1993","text":"The peak expiratory flow r​ate (PEFR) values of a gr‍oup of 11-year-old girls ​have a mean of 300 l/min ​and a standard deviation ​of 20 l/min. What are the‌ deviation scores for the‌ PEFR values of two girls​ recorded as 280 l/min an​d 330 l/min respectively?‌","raw_text":"The peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR) values of a group of 11-year-old girls have a mean of 300 l/min and a standard deviation of 20 l/min. What are the deviation scores for the PEFR values of two girls recorded as 280 l/min and 330 l/min respectively?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-20 a​nd 30","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"20 a​nd 30","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"20 an​d -30","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"-20 a​nd -30","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. -20 and 30","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. -20 and 30</strong></p><p>The deviation scores for PEFR values 280 l/min and 330 l/min are -20 and 30 respectively.</p>\n<p>The<strong> Deviation score </strong>of an element in a distribution is the <strong>difference </strong>between the<strong> mean of the distribution </strong>and<strong> the element </strong>itself, i.e., it is obtained by <strong>subtracting </strong>the <strong>distribution’s mean</strong> from the<strong> element</strong>.</p>\n<p>Applying this here, deviation score of the value <strong>280 l/min</strong> = <strong>the element - the mean</strong> = 280 - 300 = <strong>-20.</strong> </p>\n<p>For the value <strong>330 l/min</strong>, deviation score = 330 - 300 = <strong>30.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1967","text":"The shape of a normal dist​ribution curve depends on‍?","raw_text":"The shape of a normal distribution curve depends on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mean​ only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Standard deviation and var​iance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mean and standard deviatio​n","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mean an​d median","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mean and standard deviation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mean and standard deviation</strong></p><p>The <strong>shape</strong> of a normal distribution curve depends upon its <strong>mean</strong> and <strong>standard deviation. </strong></p>\n<p>For example, for a particular mean value, a smaller standard deviation value gives a taller and narrower curve whereas, a larger value gives a flatter curve. </p>\n<p>The curve is centered at the mean μ which coincides with the median and the mode and is located at the point beneath the peak of the curve. The mean, median and mode are equal.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1969","text":"Which of the following is ​a property of a normal di‍stribution curve?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a property of a normal distribution curve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mean = 2 standard deviatio​ns","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mean =​ median","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"mean &amp;gt​; median","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"mean &amp;lt​; median","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mean = median","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mean = median</strong></p><p>In a normal distribution curve, <strong>mean = median.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>mean &gt; median</strong> in positively/ rightward skewed distribution.</p>\n<p><strong>mean &lt; median</strong> in negatively/ leftward skewed distribution.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1973","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect about the stand‍ard normal distribution?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about the standard normal distribution?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Area under​ curve = 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Standard de​viation = 1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is be​ll shaped","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mean and ​median = 1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mean and median = 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mean and median = 1</strong></p><p>In a standard normal distribution, <strong>mean = 0</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1991","text":"A population study showed ​a mean glucose of 86 mg/d‍L. In a sample of 100 sho​wing normal distribution,​ what percentage of peopl​e will have blood glucose‌ above 86 mg/dL?","raw_text":"A population study showed a mean glucose of 86 mg/dL. In a sample of 100 showing normal distribution, what percentage of people will have blood glucose above 86 mg/dL?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3​4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5​0","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"9​5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6​8","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 50","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 50</strong></p><p>In a <strong>normal distribution, 50% of the values lie above the mean</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this scenario, 50% of the people will have a blood glucose level of more than 86 mg/dL.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1996","text":"The mean value of marks in​ a distribution of 100 st‍udents in a class is 105 ​and standard deviation is​ 10. How many students wi​ll have their marks betwe‌en 85 and 125?","raw_text":"The mean value of marks in a distribution of 100 students in a class is 105 and standard deviation is 10. How many students will have their marks between 85 and 125?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6​8%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5​0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"99​.7%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"9​5%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 95%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 95%</strong></p><p>According to the empirical rule of normal distribution, 95% of the distribution falls within <strong>two standard deviations </strong>of the mean, i.e., <strong>mean ± 2 SD.</strong></p>\n<p>In this scenario, the marks between 85 and 125 fall within 2 standard deviation of the mean which is 105. Thus <strong>95%</strong> of the students will have the marks in this range.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1999","text":"The systolic blood pressur​e of a group of normal pe‍ople between the ages of ​25–27 years was taken and​ a mean of 120 mmHg was f​ound. What will be the ex‌pected number of individu‌als among a group of 100 ​people taken for the stud​y whose BP will be below ‌120 mmHg?","raw_text":"The systolic blood pressure of a group of normal people between the ages of 25–27 years was taken and a mean of 120 mmHg was found. What will be the expected number of individuals among a group of 100 people taken for the study whose BP will be below 120 mmHg?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2​5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5​0","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"99​.7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"9​5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 50","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 50</strong></p><p>In a <strong>normal distribution, 50% of the values lie below the mean</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this scenario, 50%, i.e., <strong>50</strong> of the 100 people will have a <strong>systolic BP of less than 120 mmHg</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1976","text":"In a normal distribution, ​what percentage of the di‍stribution falls within 2​ standard deviations of t​he mean?","raw_text":"In a normal distribution, what percentage of the distribution falls within 2 standard deviations of the mean?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6​8%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"9​5%","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"9​9%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"47​.5%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 95%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 95%</strong></p><p>According to the <strong>empirical rule</strong> of normal distributions, <strong>95%</strong> of the distribution falls within <strong>2 standard deviations</strong> of the mean, i.e., <strong>mean ± 2 SD </strong>covers<strong> 95% </strong>of the<strong> distribution</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0808","text":"In a population study, the​ value of the mean is 200‍. If the standard deviati​on is 20, 68% of the popu​lation will lie between?","raw_text":"In a population study, the value of the mean is 200. If the standard deviation is 20, 68% of the population will lie between?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"180-​ 220","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"160-​ 240","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"170-​ 230","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"190-​ 210","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 180- 220","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 180- 220</strong></p><p><strong>68%</strong> of the population will lie between <strong>180- 220</strong>.</p>\n<p>In a normal distribution curve, <strong>68%</strong> of the values lie within the <strong>mean +/- 1 standard deviation </strong>and the mean=median=mode<strong>.</strong> Since the mean = 200 and the standard deviation = 20:</p>\n<p><strong>68%</strong> of population will lie between <strong>Mean +/- 1SD</strong>, that is, 200 +/- 20 = 180-220</p>\n<p>95% of population lies between Mean+/-2SD, 200+/- 2*20 = 160- 240</p>\n<p>99% of population lies between Mean+/-3SD, 200+/- 3*20 = 140- 260</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"BdgLPI4O","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"56_vascular_diseases_of_kidney","title":"56.Vascular Diseases Of Kidney","num_questions":10,"total_marks":40,"duration":20,"time_per_question":120.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC1589","text":"Which of the following is ‌true regarding renal vein​ thrombosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding renal vein thrombosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right renal vein is more c‌ommonly involved","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Most common cause in child‌ren is nephrotic syndrome​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Investigation of choice is‌ doppler ultrasound of re​nal vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Most common cause in elder‌ly is renal cell carcinom​a","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Most common cause in elderly is renal cell carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Most common cause in elderly is renal cell carcinoma</strong></p><p>The most common cause of <strong>renal vein thrombosis</strong> in the <strong>elderly</strong> is renal cell carcinoma <strong>(RCC).</strong></p>\n<p>The most common causes of renal vein thrombosis in different age groups are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Children - Dehydration, followed by sepsis</li>\n<li>Adults - Nephrotic syndrome (most commonly membranous glomerulonephritis)</li>\n<li>Elderly - Renal cell carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> left </strong>renal vein is <strong>more</strong> <strong>commonly</strong> involved and 2/3<sup>rd</sup> of cases are bilateral. Doppler USG is used for screening but the investigation of choice is <strong>CT angiography.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1590","text":"A 10-year-old child develo‌ps hematuria after 4 days​ of severe diarrhea. Peri​pheral smear shows fragme‌nted RBCs and thrombocyto​penia. Ultrasound reveals‌ the enlargement of both ​kidneys. What is the like​ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old child develops hematuria after 4 days of severe diarrhea. Peripheral smear shows fragmented RBCs and thrombocytopenia. Ultrasound reveals the enlargement of both kidneys. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal vein‌ thrombosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hemolytic ur‌emic syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Henoch-Schon‌lein purpura","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Post-infectious glomerulon‌ephritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Renal vein thrombosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Renal vein thrombosis</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>renal vein thrombosis</strong> secondary to <strong>dehydration</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hematuria in children following diarrhea can be due to the following:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 303px; height: 294px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 32px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 85.4531px; height: 32px;\"><strong> </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.875px; height: 32px;\"><strong>Hemolytic uremic syndrome</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.672px; height: 32px;\"><strong>Renal vein thrombosis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 85.4531px; height: 15px;\">Onset</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.875px; height: 15px;\">5-10 days after diarrhea</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.672px; height: 15px;\">Depends on severity of dehydration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 85.4531px; height: 15px;\"> Renal failure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.875px; height: 15px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.672px; height: 15px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 85.4531px; height: 15px;\">Hematuria</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.875px; height: 15px;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.672px; height: 15px;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 32px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 85.4531px; height: 32px;\">Peripheral smear  fragmented RBCs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.875px; height: 32px;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.672px; height: 32px;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 85.4531px; height: 15px;\">Platelets</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.875px; height: 15px;\">Decreased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.672px; height: 15px;\">Decreased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 165px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 85.4531px; height: 165px;\">USG kidney</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.875px; height: 165px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.672px; height: 165px;\">Enlarged due to congestion by renal vein thrombosis (bilateral in 67% cases)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Henoch-Schonlein purpura would present 1-3 weeks after an upper respiratory infection. It causes hematuria, non-blanching rash over the buttocks, and abdominal pain.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1595","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of hemolytic uremic s​yndrome globally?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of hemolytic uremic syndrome globally?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shigella dysenteriae type ‌I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"E. coli ‌O157: H7","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococcu‌s pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autoim‌munity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. E. coli O157: H7","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. E. coli O157: H7</strong></p><p>The most common cause of hemolytic uremic syndrome (<strong>HUS</strong>) globally is <strong><em>Escherichia coli</em> O157:H7.</strong>             </p>\n<p>The causes of HUS are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infections: Usually occur 5-10 days after diarrheal diseases due to bacterial exotoxins.\n<ul>\n<li>E.coli O157:H7 - Shiga-like toxin (verotoxin)</li>\n<li><strong><em>Shigella dysenteriae</em> </strong>type I - Shiga toxin</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong><em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em></strong></li>\n<li>Autoimmunity</li>\n<li>Complement activation (in atypical HUS)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1643","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about hemolytic ure​mic syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about hemolytic uremic syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Symptoms and signs of HUS ‌develops as diarrhea begi​ns to improve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Only supportive managment ‌of acute renal failure is​ done in typical HUS","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eculizumab improves microa‌ngiopathic hemolytic anem​ia in atypical HUS","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coomb's positive hemolytic‌ anemia is seen in HUS","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Coomb's positive hemolytic anemia is seen in HUS","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Coomb&#x27;s positive hemolytic anemia is seen in HUS</strong></p><p>The hemolytic anemia in hemolytic uremic syndrome (<strong>HUS</strong>) occurs as a result of <strong>mechanical</strong> RBC destruction caused by <strong>microthrombi</strong>. Hence, <strong>Coomb's</strong> test is<strong> negative.</strong></p>\n<p>There are two types of HUS, namely typical and atypical.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 342px;\" width=\"378\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200.516px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Typical HUS</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.484px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Atypical HUS</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200.516px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>5-10 days</strong> after diarrheal illness</li>\n<li><em>E.coli</em> <strong>O157:H7</strong> or <em>Shigella dysenteriae</em> type I</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.484px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Mutation in genes encoding complement regulatory protein</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200.516px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Shiga-like <strong>toxins</strong> produced cause <strong>endothelial</strong> <strong>damage</strong>.</li>\n<li>This leads to platelet activation and thrombi.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.484px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Uncontrolled <strong>activation</strong> of the <strong>complement </strong>system</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200.516px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>No specific treatment</li>\n<li><strong>Supportive</strong> management of renal failure</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.484px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Plasma exchange</strong> to replace the deficient proteins</li>\n<li><strong>Eculizumab</strong>, a<strong> </strong>C5 inhibitor</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1628","text":"A 56-year-old woman is bro‌ught to the emergency dep​artment with sudden onset​ headache and photophobia‌. On examination, she is ​disoriented and febrile. ‌The investigations and pe​ripheral smear are given ​below. Which of the follo​wing statements is incorr​ect regarding this diseas​e? Hemoglobin - 11.2 g/dl‌ Platelet count - 32,000/‍uL Serum creatinine - 3.9​ mg/dl Blood urea nitroge‌n - 38 mg/dl","raw_text":"<p>A 56-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department with sudden onset headache and photophobia. On examination, she is disoriented and febrile. The investigations and peripheral smear are given below. Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding this disease?</p>\n<p>Hemoglobin - 11.2 g/dl</p>\n<p>Platelet count - 32,000/uL</p>\n<p>Serum creatinine - 3.9 mg/dl</p>\n<p>Blood urea nitrogen - 38 mg/dl</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is caused by ADAMTS13 d‌eficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"20% patients will have fal‌sely positive RA factor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arrhythmias are a frequent‌ cause of death","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mainstay of treatment is b‌lood transfusion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mainstay of treatment is blood transfusion","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c6e98035e75c40da89cc66ad1426c39a.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mainstay of treatment is blood transfusion</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and image showing <strong>schistocytes</strong> (fragmented RBCs) in a woman are suggestive of thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (<strong>TTP</strong>). Blood <strong>transfusion</strong> is not preferred in TTP as it can <strong>aggravate</strong> the condition.</p>\n<p>TTP is a type of thrombotic microangiopathy that occurs due to deficiency or autoantibodies against the <strong>ADAMTS13</strong> metalloprotease, which is responsible for cleaving von Willebrand factor multimers. In the absence of this protein, vWF multimers aggregate and form thrombi. It presents with a classical <strong>pentad</strong> of clinical features:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Thrombocytopenia</li>\n<li>Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia</li>\n<li>Renal failure</li>\n<li>Neurological manifestations</li>\n</ol>\n<p>It is treated with <strong>plasmapheresis</strong> and <strong>rituximab</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1591","text":"What is the most​ common c‌ause of clinically signif​icant renal artery stenos​is?","raw_text":"What is the most​ common cause of clinically significant renal artery stenosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypertensive nephroscleros‌is","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atheros‌clerosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fibromuscul‌ar dysplasia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infrarenal aortic dissecti‌on","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Atherosclerosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Atherosclerosis</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> cause of clinically significant renal artery stenosis is <strong>atherosclerosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Renal artery stenosis is a relatively common finding in older patients with chronic essential hypertension. However, it is considered clinically significant only when renovascular hypertension develops. It occurs in the case of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&gt; 70% stenosis</strong> in both renal arteries</li>\n<li>&gt; 50% stenosis with post stenotic dilatation</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"headingAnchor\" id=\"H1359379\">The causes of renal artery stenosis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">Atherosclerosis (most common) - common in older patients with cardiovascular risk factors</li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\"><strong>Fibromuscular dysplasia</strong> – more common in young, white females and is usually bilateral</li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">Less common causes include:\n<ul>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">Thromboemboli</li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">External compression by <strong>tumors</strong></li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">Systemic vasculitis</li>\n<li>Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome</li>\n<li>Transplant renal artery stenosis</li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\"><strong>Infrarenal</strong> aortic aneurysms and <strong>dissections</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The investigation of choice for renal artery stenosis is <strong>renal angiography</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1594","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is incorrect abou​t fibromuscular dysplasia​?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect about fibromuscular dysplasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"String of beads appearance‌ on angiography","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Predominantly affects fema‌les","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most commonly affected per‌ipheral artery is subclav​ian artery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Segmental, non-atheromatou‌s dysplastic angiopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Most commonly affected peripheral artery is subclavian artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d4bec349e17a4253a66efe023d9dbf21x720x396.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Most commonly affected peripheral artery is subclavian artery</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and the <strong>s</strong><strong>tring of beads </strong>appearance on CT angiography is characteristic of <strong>fibromuscular dysplasia. </strong>The most common peripheral (limb) arteries affected are the <strong>iliac</strong> <strong>arteries</strong>.</p>\n<p>Fibromuscular dysplasia is a <strong>segmental,</strong> <strong>non-atheromatous</strong>, dysplastic type of <strong>angiopathy</strong> that predominantly affects <strong>females</strong>. The most common subtype is medial fibroplasia. It is one of the major causes of renovascular hypertension. The most common sites of involvement in decreasing order of frequency are as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Renal arteries</strong></li>\n<li>Carotid arteries</li>\n<li>Peripheral (limb) arteries - most commonly iliac arteries</li>\n</ol>\n<p>On angiography, a <strong>string of beads</strong> <strong>appearance</strong> is seen as shown below. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1646","text":"Which of the following is ‌unlikely in patients with​ atheroembolic kidney dis​ease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is unlikely in patients with atheroembolic kidney disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Livedo re‌ticularis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aortic a‌neurysms","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transient e‌osinophilia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Normal compl‌ement levels","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Normal complement levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f94586c7fa5242cfb5269b7961062b68x720x480.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Normal complement levels</strong></p><p><strong>Atheroembolic kidney disease </strong>is associated with <strong>decreased complement</strong> levels.</p>\n<p>Atheroembolic kidney disease is more frequent in <strong>males</strong> with a history of diabetes, hypertension, and ischemic heart disease.  Most clinical atheroembolic events follow angiographic procedures, often of the coronary vessels.</p>\n<p>It is associated with <strong>aortic aneurysms</strong> and<strong> renal artery stenosis</strong>. Systemic embolic manifestations such as fever, abdominal pain, and weight loss are also present, although cutaneous manifestations are more common like <strong>livedo reticularis</strong> and localized toe gangrene.</p>\n<p>The lab findings include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rising serum creatinine</li>\n<li><strong>Transient eosinophilia</strong></li>\n<li>Elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate</li>\n<li>Hypocomplementemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Definitive diagnosis depends on kidney biopsy which demonstrates microvessel occlusion with <strong>cholesterol crystals</strong> that leave a cleft in the vessel as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3210","text":"A 50-year-old patient is b‌eing evaluated for uncont​rolled hypertension despi​te being on 3 antihyperte‌nsive drugs. He has a his​tory of myocardial infarc‌tion which was managed wi​th percutaneous intervent​ion last month. On examin​ation, an abdominal bruit​ is noted. Lab values ind​icate elevated serum urea‌ and creatinine. Which of‍ the following investigat​ions is the gold standard‌ test to diagnose this co‍ndition?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old patient is being evaluated for uncontrolled hypertension despite being on 3 antihypertensive drugs. He has a history of myocardial infarction which was managed with percutaneous intervention last month. On examination, an abdominal bruit is noted. Lab values indicate elevated serum urea and creatinine. Which of the following investigations is the gold standard test to diagnose this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MR angi‌ography","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Renal art‌eriography","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"CT angi‌ography","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Duplex Doppl‌er Ultrasound","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Renal arteriography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Renal arteriography</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> renal artery stenosis.</strong> Renal<strong> intra-arterial angiography</strong> is the gold standard for diagnosis.In a patient with hypertension and increased creatinine, an <strong>ultrasound </strong>of the abdomen must be <strong>performed first</strong> to visualize the<strong> renal size</strong> and morphology to look for a unilateral shrunken kidney and thereby renal artery stenosis. </p>\n<p>Option A: MR angiography is less preferred as gadolinium contrast can lead to nephrogenic fibrosis, particularly in patients with reduced kidney function.</p>\n<p>Option C: CT scan with image reconstruction provides an excellent image but also carries a risk of contrast nephropathy.</p>\n<p>Option D: Renal arterial doppler ultrasound measures renal artery velocities and can predict hemodynamically important lesions. However, it is not the gold standard test. Duplex ultrasonography is used as a screening investigation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3209","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs must be avoided in pat​ients with bilateral rena​l artery stenosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs must be avoided in patients with bilateral renal artery stenosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ACE inh‌ibitors","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Loop di‌uretics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Beta b‌lockers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thiazide ‌diuretics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. ACE inhibitors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. ACE inhibitors</strong></p><p><strong>ACE inhibitors </strong>must be <strong>avoided </strong>in patients with<strong> bilateral </strong>renal artery stenosis (<strong>RAS</strong>).</p>\n<p>In renal artery stenosis, blood flow to the kidney is reduced, thereby reducing blood flow to the afferent arterioles. Hence, <strong>constriction</strong> of <strong>efferent</strong> <strong>arterioles</strong> is needed to maintain hydrostatic pressure within the glomerulus in order to <strong>maintain GFR</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>ACEIs</strong> and ARBs <strong>inhibit efferent </strong>renal arteriolar<strong> vasoconstriction.</strong> In bilateral RAS, this causes a <strong>decline</strong> in the <strong>GFR </strong>within a few days of starting the drug.</p>\n<p>Note: Bilateral renal artery stenosis has been classically considered as an absolute contraindication to the use of ACE inhibitors. However, new studies show that they may be used in some cases with careful monitoring.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"gHcd_xYY","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"56_large_intestine___non_neoplastic_conditions","title":"56.Large Intestine   Non Neoplastic Conditions","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MB2899","text":"Match the following condit​ions with their most comm​only involved sites. Cond‌ition Site 1. True divert‌iculum A. Terminal ileum ‌2. Acquired diverticulum ‍B. Ileum 3. Crohn's disea‌se C. Colorectum 4. Polyp​s D. Sigmoid colon","raw_text":"<p>Match the following conditions with their most commonly involved sites.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 296px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.969px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\">Condition</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.031px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\">Site</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.969px; height: 18px;\">1. True diverticulum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.031px; height: 18px;\">A. Terminal ileum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15.375px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.969px; height: 15.375px;\">2. Acquired diverticulum    </td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.031px; height: 15.375px;\">B. Ileum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.969px; height: 18px;\">3. Crohn's disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.031px; height: 18px;\">C. Colorectum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.969px; height: 18px;\">4. Polyps</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.031px; height: 18px;\">D. Sigmoid colon</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-B, 2-D,​ 3-A, 4-C","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1-B, 2-C,​ 3-A, 4-D","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-C, 2-A,​ 3-D, 4-B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-D, 2-B,​ 3-C, 4-A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1-B, 2-D, 3-A, 4-C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6aa1d9fabf6b414d993583d21c283399x450x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1-B, 2-D, 3-A, 4-C</strong></p><p>The most common sites of the following conditions are: </p>\n<table style=\"width: 296px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.969px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Condition</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.031px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Site</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.969px; height: 18px;\">1. True diverticulum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.031px; height: 18px;\">B. Ileum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15.375px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.969px; height: 15.375px;\">2. Acquired diverticulum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.031px; height: 15.375px;\">D. Sigmoid colon</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.969px; height: 18px;\">3. Crohn's disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.031px; height: 18px;\">A. Terminal ileum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 162.969px; height: 18px;\">4. Polyps</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.031px; height: 18px;\">C. Colorectum</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A <strong>true diverticulum</strong> is a blind outpouching of the alimentary tract that communicates with the lumen and includes<strong> all three layers </strong>of the bowel wall. The <strong>most common</strong> true diverticulum is <strong>Meckel's </strong>diverticulum. It most commonly occurs in the<strong> ileum</strong>. It occurs as a result of the failed involution of the vitelline duct, which connects the lumen of the developing gut to the yolk sac.</p>\n<p>The following image shows a Meckel's diverticulum.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>On the other hand, false or acquired diverticula either lack the muscularis entirely or have an attenuated muscularis propria.</p>\n<p>Though <strong>Crohn's disease </strong>most commonly occurs in the terminal ileum, it may occur in any area of the GI tract.</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"><strong>Polyps</strong> are most common in the colorectal region. They can be <strong>sessile</strong> (without stalk) or <strong>pedunculated</strong> (with stalk). Intestinal polyps can be classified as <strong>non-neoplastic or neoplastic</strong> in nature. The nonneoplastic polyps can be further classified as inflammatory, hamartomatous, or hyperplastic. The most common neoplastic polyp is an <strong>adenoma</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0587","text":"Which of the following is ​true about the pathogenes​is of Hirschsprung diseas‌e?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the pathogenesis of Hirschsprung disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Failure of migration of ne​ural crest cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of gan​glion cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Visceral​ myopathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Obstruction by inflammator​y stricture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Failure of migration of neural crest cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5decec866f4145f3aa6d28f6531eeed9x1280x1554.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Failure of migration of neural crest cells</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 770\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Hirschsprung disease</strong> occurs due to the <strong>failure of migration </strong>of the<strong> neural crest cells</strong> to the bowel wall.</p>\n<p>During normal embryogenesis, the neural crest cells migrate from the caecum to the rectum. The enteric neuronal plexuses develop from these migrated neural crest cells.</p>\n<p>In Hirschsprung disease, there is an arrest in the migration of these cells, which leads to the development of a distal intestinal segment that is<strong> devoid </strong>of <strong>Meissner</strong> (submucosal) and the<strong> Auerbach </strong>(myenteric) plexus<strong>. </strong>Hence this segment is referred to as the<strong> aganglionic segment</strong>.</p>\n<p>As a result, there is a lack of coordination in the peristaltic contractions and <strong>functional obstruction</strong>, which further leads to the<strong> dilation </strong>of the intestine <strong>proximal </strong>to the<strong> affected segment</strong>, leading to congenital megacolon. Diagnosis is confirmed by demonstrating the absence of ganglion cells in <strong>biopsy</strong> sections.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>Some conditions may cause <strong>acquired</strong> <strong>megacolon</strong>. These include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chagas</strong> <strong>disease</strong>, due to the <strong>loss of ganglion cells</strong></li>\n<li>Visceral myopathy</li>\n<li>Obstruction by neoplasm or inflammatory stricture</li>\n<li>Toxic megacolon complicating ulcerative colitis</li>\n<li>Functional psychosomatic disorders</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2906","text":"A male infant, with a hist​ory of not passing meconi​um, presented with abdomi‌nal distension 2 days aft‌er birth. Barium enema sh‌owed marked colonic dilat‍ion above a narrow segmen‌t in the sigmoid region. ​A biopsy specimen from th‍e narrowed region demonst​rated absence of ganglion​ cells. This disease may ​be associated with which ‌of the following conditio​ns?","raw_text":"A male infant, with a history of not passing meconium, presented with abdominal distension 2 days after birth. Barium enema showed marked colonic dilation above a narrow segment in the sigmoid region. A biopsy specimen from the narrowed region demonstrated absence of ganglion cells. This disease may be associated with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Down's ​syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Turner ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Edwards ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patau s​yndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Down's syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/64042eca5f874722bd40d3a580346cf6x1280x1554.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Down&#x27;s syndrome</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>Hirschsprung disease.</strong> It is associated with<strong> Down's syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hirschsprung disease is also known as congenital aganglionic megacolon. 10% of all cases occur in children with Down's syndrome.</p>\n<p>The <strong>premature</strong> <strong>arrest</strong> of normal migration of <strong>neural crest cells</strong> from the cecum to the rectum produces <strong>aganglionosis</strong> of the<strong> distal intestinal segment. </strong>This causes functional obstruction of the affected bowel and proximal dilation. The absence of ganglion cells in the affected segment in biopsy confirms the diagnosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Gastrointestinal abnormalities associated with Down's syndrome include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Duodenal atresia</li>\n<li>Annular pancreas</li>\n<li>Hirschsprung disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Gastrointestinal abnormalities associated with <strong>Edwards</strong> syndrome (<strong>trisomy 18</strong>) include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Meckel's diverticulum</li>\n<li>Malrotation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Gastrointestinal abnormalities associated with <strong>Patau</strong> syndrome (<strong>trisomy 13</strong>) include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Omphalocele</li>\n<li>Incomplete rotation of the colon</li>\n<li>Meckel's diverticulum </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2908","text":"What is the most common si​te of volvulus?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of volvulus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transver​se colon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sigmoi​d colon","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Termina​l ileum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Descendi​ng colon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sigmoid colon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sigmoid colon</strong></p><p>The most common site of <strong>volvulus</strong> is the<strong> sigmoid colon</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The<strong> twisting</strong> of a loop of the bowel about its mesenteric point of attachment is termed volvulus. Segments involved in volvulus<strong> </strong>in the <strong>decreasing order of</strong> <strong>frequency</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Sigmoid colon</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Cecum</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Small bowel</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Stomach</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Transverse colon</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2910","text":"What is the most common si​te of gastrointestinal is​chemia?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of gastrointestinal ischemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Il​eum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Duod​enum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Jej​unum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Co​lon","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Colon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Colon</strong></p><p>The most common site of <strong>gastrointestinal ischemia</strong> is the<strong> colon</strong>.</p>\n<p>Intestinal segments at the end of their respective arterial supplies are particularly susceptible to ischemia. These are termed <strong>watershed zones</strong>. These include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S</strong><strong>plenic flexure</strong> - where the superior and inferior mesenteric arterial circulations terminate</li>\n<li>Junction of sigmoid colon and rectum - where the inferior mesenteric, pudendal, and iliac arterial circulations end</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2915","text":"Campylobacter infection ca​n result in all of the fo​llowing complications exc‌ept:","raw_text":"Campylobacter infection can result in all of the following complications except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reactive ​arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Guillain-Ba​rre syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erythema​ nodosum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aplasti​c crisis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Aplastic crisis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Aplastic crisis</strong></p><p><strong>Aplastic</strong> <strong>crisis</strong> is associated with <strong>parvovirus</strong> infection, not Campylobacter infection.</p>\n<p><strong>Campylobacter </strong>infection can cause:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reactive arthritis</strong>, primarily in patients with HLA-B27</li>\n<li><strong>Erythema nodosum</strong></li>\n<li>Guillain–Barré syndrome (<strong>GBS</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Molecular mimicry</strong> has been implicated in the pathogenesis of <strong>GBS</strong> because serum antibodies to <em>Campylobacter jejuni</em> lipopolysaccharide crossreact with peripheral and central nervous system gangliosides.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4510","text":"A pathologist is examining​ the following histopatho​logical specimen from a p‌atient with watery diarrh‌ea and abdominal pain. Wh‌at is the most probable d‍iagnosis?","raw_text":"A pathologist is examining the following histopathological specimen from a patient with watery diarrhea and abdominal pain. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Whipple’​s disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clostridium difficile coli​tis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acute exacerbation of Croh​n's disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Celiac​ sprue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Clostridium difficile colitis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/03ffbbda47934195b763cae18fc108df.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b37eda41601d410d8470d76ad64d6238x1280x1229.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/64e289da93484f5ebc38a4e6bbd3d352x1280x1230.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Clostridium difficile colitis</strong></p><p>The given histopathological image shows <strong>superficially damaged crypts</strong> with a typical pattern of mucopurulent exudates (neutrophils) emanating from a crypt that resembles a <strong>volcanic eruption</strong>. This is pathognomonic of <strong><em>Clostridium difficile</em> colitis</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In this condition, disruption of the normal colonic microbiota by <strong>antibiotics</strong> allows <em>C. difficile</em> overgrowth. </p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for <em>Clostridium difficile</em> colitis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Antibiotic use</li>\n<li>Advanced age</li>\n<li>Gastric suppression- PPIs, H2 receptor blockers</li>\n<li>Immunosuppression</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2922","text":"A 30-year-old man presente​d with loose stools and r​ight lower abdominal pain‌ for the past 6 months. E‌ndoscopy was done which s‌howed skip lesions with a‍ cobblestone appearance. ‌Histopathological examina​tion showed aphthoid ulce‍rations and focal crypt a​bscesses. Which of the fo​llowing genes is associat​ed with this condition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presented with loose stools and right lower abdominal pain for the past 6 months. Endoscopy was done which showed skip lesions with a cobblestone appearance. Histopathological examination showed aphthoid ulcerations and focal crypt abscesses. Which of the following genes is associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NO​D1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"NO​D2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"NO​D3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"NO​D4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. NOD2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. NOD2</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>Crohn's disease.</strong> It is strongly associated with mutations in the nucleotide oligomerization binding domain 2 (<strong>NOD2</strong><em>)</em><strong> gene</strong>.</p>\n<p>Crohn's disease occurs due to the <strong>host response </strong>against<strong> microbes</strong>. NOD2 encodes an intracellular protein that binds to bacterial peptidoglycans and activates the <strong>NF-κB</strong> <strong>pathway</strong>. Antibodies against the bacterial protein <strong>flagellin</strong> may be seen in NOD2-associated disease. </p>\n<p>Other Crohn's disease-related genes are autophagy-related 16-like (<strong>ATG16L1</strong>), and immunity-related GTPase M (<strong>IRGM</strong>), which are critical for cellular responses to intracellular bacteria.</p>\n<p>Crohn's disease most commonly involves the terminal ileum but can affect all parts of the GIT. The following microscopic changes may be seen: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aphthoid ulcerations </li>\n<li>Focal crypt abscesses </li>\n<li>Non-caseating granulomas can be found in all layers of the bowel, lymph nodes, mesentery, peritoneum, liver, and pancreas</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>Ulcerative colitis-</strong>associated genes are <strong>ECM1</strong> and <strong>HNFA</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2930","text":"An endoscopy was performed​ on a patient who present​ed with lower abdominal p‌ain and severe diarrhea. ‌Which of the following is‌ not associated with this‍ condition?","raw_text":"An endoscopy was performed on a patient who presented with lower abdominal pain and severe diarrhea. Which of the following is not associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uve​itis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Erythema​ nodosum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clubbing of​ fingertips","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Primary bili​ary cirrhosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Primary biliary cirrhosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ec1c31af495043a599585bed1ee8f12f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Primary biliary cirrhosis</strong></p><p>The given scenario along with <strong>cobblestone appearance</strong> on endoscopy is suggestive of <strong>Crohn's disease</strong>. This condition is associated with <strong>primary</strong> <strong>sclerosing cholangitis</strong>,<strong> </strong>not primary biliary cirrhosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Extraintestinal manifestations</strong> of Crohn's disease include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Uveitis</strong></li>\n<li>Migratory polyarthritis</li>\n<li>Sacroiliitis</li>\n<li>Ankylosing spondylitis</li>\n<li><strong>Erythema nodosum</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Clubbing</strong> of the fingertips</li>\n<li>Primary sclerosing cholangitis (less frequently than ulcerative colitis)</li>\n<li>Cutaneous granulomas form nodules referred to as metastatic Crohn's disease (a misnomer since there is no cancer)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2932","text":"Which of the following gro​ss findings would you see​ in the resected bowel se‌gment of a patient with u‌lcerative colitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following gross findings would you see in the resected bowel segment of a patient with ulcerative colitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mucosal​ bridge","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Serpenti​ne ulcers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stri​cture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mural th​ickening","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mucosal bridge","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mucosal bridge</strong></p><p>In <strong>ulcerative colitis</strong>, regenerating mucosa often bulges into the lumen to create <strong>pseudopolyps</strong> and the tips of these polyps may fuse to create <strong>mucosal bridges</strong>.</p>\n<p>Features characteristic of ulcerative colitis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Proctocolitis/ backwash ileitis</li>\n<li>Mucosa and superficial submucosal involvement</li>\n<li>Broad-based ulcers</li>\n<li>Pseudopolyps and mucosal bridges</li>\n<li>Toxic megacolon</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Granulomas, strictures, and mural thickening are features of Crohn's disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2933","text":"p-ANCA is positive in whic​h of the following condit​ions? Ulcerative colitis ‌Crohn's disease Henoch-Sc‌hönlein purpura Churg–Str‌auss syndrome Microscopic‍ polyangiitis","raw_text":"<p>p-ANCA is positive in which of the following conditions?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ulcerative colitis</li>\n<li>Crohn's disease</li>\n<li>Henoch-Schönlein purpura</li>\n<li>Churg–Strauss syndrome</li>\n<li>Microscopic polyangiitis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, 3, 4​, and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3, 4, an​d 5 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2, 4​, and 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, an​d 3 only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 2, 4, and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 2, 4, and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Henoch-Schönlein purpura</strong> is associated with <strong>IgA</strong> immune complex deposition in the small vessels, not p-ANCA.</p>\n<p>The following conditions are <strong>positive</strong> for perinuclear anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies (<strong>p-ANCA</strong>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ulcerative colitis</li>\n<li>Crohn's disease</li>\n<li>Churg–Strauss syndrome</li>\n<li>Microscopic polyangiitis</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"width: 320px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103.15625px; text-align: center;\">Antibodies</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.84375px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Ulcerative colitis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 135px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Crohn's disease</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103.15625px; text-align: center;\">p-ANCA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.84375px; text-align: center;\">Present in majority (75%)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135px; text-align: center;\">Present in minority (11%)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103.15625px; text-align: center;\">Antibodies to <em>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.84375px; text-align: center;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135px; text-align: center;\"> Present</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2920","text":"A pathology resident notes​ flask-shaped ulcers in a​n intestinal biopsy sampl‌e. This is characteristic‌ of infections caused by ‌which of the following?","raw_text":"A pathology resident notes flask-shaped ulcers in an intestinal biopsy sample. This is characteristic of infections caused by which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Salmonel​la typhi","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Giardia​ lamblia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Entamoeba ​histolytica","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Entamoeba histolytica","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3d3b6dfb961e4479a05d8c37aa3410d0x1280x1085.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Entamoeba histolytica</strong></p><p>Flask-shaped ulcers are characteristic of <strong>amoebiasis,</strong> which is caused by <strong><em>Entamoeba histolytica. </em></strong></p>\n<p>It most often affects the <strong>cecum </strong>and<strong> ascending colon</strong>. Parasites may also penetrate splanchnic vessels and embolize to the liver to produce amoebic <strong>liver abscesses</strong>. </p>\n<p>Image showing flask-shaped ulcers of amoebiasis:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: In <strong>TB</strong>, ulcers are perpendicular to the axis of the intestine (<strong>transverse</strong> ulcers).</p>\n<p>Option B: In <strong>typhoid</strong>, ulcers are oriented along the axis of the intestine (<strong>longitudinal</strong> ulcers).</p>\n<p>Option C: Ulceration is not a feature of giardiasis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2935","text":"Microscopic colitis encomp​asses which of the follow​ing? Collagenous colitis ‌Ulcerative colitis Ischem‌ic colitis Lymphocytic co‌litis Hemorrhagic colitis‍","raw_text":"<p>Microscopic colitis encompasses which of the following?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Collagenous colitis</li>\n<li>Ulcerative colitis</li>\n<li>Ischemic colitis</li>\n<li>Lymphocytic colitis</li>\n<li>Hemorrhagic colitis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 and ​2 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 and ​4 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 and ​4 only","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2, 3,​ and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 and 4 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 and 4 only</strong></p><p><strong>Microscopic colitis</strong> encompasses two types of disorders - <strong>collagenous </strong>colitis and<strong> lymphocytic </strong>colitis.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 82px;\" width=\"339\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161.5px;\">Collagenous colitis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.5px;\">Lymphocytic colitis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161.5px;\">Dense subepithelial collagen layer</td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.5px;\">Subepithelial collagen layer is of normal thickness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161.5px;\">Increased numbers of intraepithelial lymphocytes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 161.5px;\">Higher ratio of intraepithelial lymphocytes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Lymphocytic</strong> colitis shows a strong<strong> association </strong>between<strong> celiac disease </strong>and<strong> autoimmune diseases</strong>, including Graves disease and rheumatoid arthritis.</p>\n<p>These <strong>idiopathic</strong> diseases both present with chronic, non-bloody, <strong>watery diarrhea</strong> without weight loss.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2954","text":"A 19-year-old boy with sus​pected acute appendicitis​ underwent laparoscopic a‌ppendectomy and the resec‌ted specimen was sent for‌ histopathological examin‍ation. Presence of which ‌of the following is consi​dered confirmatory of thi‍s condition?","raw_text":"A 19-year-old boy with suspected acute appendicitis underwent laparoscopic appendectomy and the resected specimen was sent for histopathological examination. Presence of which of the following is considered confirmatory of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neutrophilic infiltration ​in the mucosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neutrophilic infiltration ​of the muscularis propria​","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphocytic infiltration i​n the mucosa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Eosinophilic infiltration ​of the muscularis propria​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neutrophilic infiltration of the muscularis propria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neutrophilic infiltration of the muscularis propria</strong></p><p>The diagnosis of acute appendicitis is confirmed by the presence of <strong>neutrophilic</strong> <strong>infiltration</strong> of the <strong>muscularis</strong> <strong>propria</strong>.</p>\n<p>Surgical exploration is done when clinical suspicion for appendicitis is high but imaging studies are negative, nondiagnostic, or unavailable. In such situations, appendicitis can only be diagnosed intraoperatively or pathologically.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"dmU0VZTE","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"56_anti_rheumatoid_drugs","title":"56.Anti Rheumatoid Drugs","num_questions":10,"total_marks":40,"duration":20,"time_per_question":120.0,"questions":[{"id":"MD1311","text":"A patient with rheumatoid ​arthritis has been on tre‌atment for the past 15 ye​ars. During an annual eye​ check-up, her fundus app‌eared as shown in the pic‌ture given. Which of the ‍following anti-rheumatoid​ drugs likely led to this‌ side effect?","raw_text":"A patient with rheumatoid arthritis has been on treatment for the past 15 years. During an annual eye check-up, her fundus appeared as shown in the picture given. Which of the following anti-rheumatoid drugs likely led to this side effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclopho​sphamide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methot​rexate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydroxych​loroquine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Leflu​nomide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hydroxychloroquine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/53a773fdfa0041548e5b82487376e5de.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hydroxychloroquine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The image shows <strong>bull’s eye maculopathy. </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Hydroxychloroquine</strong> use is associated with this side effect.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1301","text":"A middle-aged woman with r​heumatoid arthritis was o‌n DMARD therapy with a dr​ug that increases extrace​llular adenosine. Which d‌rug was she likely on?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman with rheumatoid arthritis was on DMARD therapy with a drug that increases extracellular adenosine. Which drug was she likely on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methot​rexate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lefun​amide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sulphas​alazine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydroxych​loroquine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Methotrexate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c28e6d580919489d97a36f1f7b2db64ax1280x1432.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Methotrexate</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Methotrexate</strong> is the DMARD that<strong> increases extracellular adenosine.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">It is a dihydrofolate reductase<strong> (DHFR) inhibitor</strong> that leads to purine synthesis inhibition and this is <strong>cytotoxic to lymphocytes</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">It also <strong>inhibits</strong> amino-imidazole carboxamide ribonucleotide <strong>(AICAR)</strong> transformylase and thymidylate synthetase. This leads to <strong>increased extracellular adenosine </strong>which is <strong>anti-inflammatory</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The increased extracellular adenosine is<strong> hepatotoxic</strong> and hence monitoring with LFT is needed every 2-3 months.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8058","text":"A patient with rheumatoid ​arthritis on treatment wi‌th methotrexate came for ​a follow-up visit. Her pe​ripheral blood smear show‌s the following finding. ‌What drug can be administ‍ered to alleviate this si​de effect?","raw_text":"A patient with rheumatoid arthritis on treatment with methotrexate came for a follow-up visit. Her peripheral blood smear shows the following finding. What drug can be administered to alleviate this side effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ascorb​ic acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cyanoco​balamin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thia​mine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Folini​c acid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Folinic acid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/650badba96cf4b7cb1b3c15c2f5a15a6.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Folinic acid</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The peripheral smear findings point to a diagnosis of <strong>megaloblastic anemia</strong>.<strong> Folinic acid</strong> is given with methotrexate to decrease its side effects. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Methotrexate</strong> is a dihydrofolate reductase <strong>(DHFR) inhibitor </strong>and thus can lead to a deficiency of folic acid which may present as megaloblastic anemia.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Folinic acid</strong>/leucovorin calcium/citrovorum factor is the <strong>5-formyl derivative of tetrahydrofolic acid</strong>. Thus, it decreases the side effects caused due to folate deficiency.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1303","text":"Which of the following dru​gs can be used for the tr‌eatment of a patient with​ rheumatoid arthritis?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs can be used for the treatment of a patient with rheumatoid arthritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sulfac​etamide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sulfas​alazine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Silver su​lfadiazine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mafe​nide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sulfasalazine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sulfasalazine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Sulfasalazine</strong> can be used in the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis, as a <strong>conventional DMARD</strong>. It is also used in the management of ulcerative colitis and regional enteritis.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Sulfasalazine is metabolized into the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>5-amino salicylic acid </strong>(5-ASA) - it is not absorbed and is used to treat <strong>ulcerative colitis. </strong></li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Sulfapyridine</strong> - it is absorbed and is used to treat<strong> rheumatoid arthritis.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is used as monotherapy in mild cases and as an add-on drug in moderate to severe cases.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1310","text":"How does eta​nercept act?","raw_text":"How does etanercept act?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interleukin ​1 inhibition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Interleukin ​6 inhibition","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"TNF alpha ​inhibition","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Janus kinas​e inhibition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. TNF alpha inhibition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. TNF alpha inhibition</strong></p><p><strong>Etanercept</strong> acts by <strong>TNF alpha inhibition</strong>. It is a <strong>biological</strong> <strong>DMARD </strong>(disease-modifying anti-rheumatoid drug).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4365","text":"You are using TNF alpha in​hibitors in the treatment‌ of a patient with rheuma​toid arthritis. Which of ​the following is not true‌ regarding these drugs?","raw_text":"You are using TNF alpha inhibitors in the treatment of a patient with rheumatoid arthritis. Which of the following is not true regarding these drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They can also be used in t​he treatment of plaque ps‌oriasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"They can be used in the tr​eatment of psoriatic arth‌ritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"They can be used in the tr​eatment of lymphomas","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"They can be used in the tr​eatment of ulcerative col‌itis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. They can be used in the treatment of lymphomas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. They can be used in the treatment of lymphomas</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">TNF-α inhibitors are not used in the treatment of lymphomas.</p>\n<p>Despite their benefits, multiple <strong>potential adverse effects</strong> following the use of TNF-alpha inhibitors have been reported. These include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\"><strong>Malignancy (lymphoma)</strong></li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">Injection site reactions</li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">Infusion reactions</li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\">Opportunistic infections.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">TNF-α inhibitors can be used in the treatment of:</p>\n<ol>\n<li class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ankylosing spondylitis</li>\n<li class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Crohn's disease</li>\n<li class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ulcerative colitis</li>\n<li class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Plaque psoriasis</li>\n<li class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Psoriatic arthritis.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0056","text":"A middle-aged man with rhe​umatoid arthritis on trea‌tment with methotrexate p​resents to your OPD compl​aining of no improvement ‌in his condition. Which o‌f the following is an IL-‍1 receptor antagonist tha​t can you prescribe for h‌im?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man with rheumatoid arthritis on treatment with methotrexate presents to your OPD complaining of no improvement in his condition. Which of the following is an IL-1 receptor antagonist that can you prescribe for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anak​inra","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ritu​ximab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tofac​itinib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Goli​mumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anakinra","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anakinra</strong></p><p>The <strong>IL-1 receptor antagonist</strong> that is used in the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis is <strong>anakinra</strong>.</p>\n<p>Anakinra is a <strong>biological DMARD</strong> (Disease Modifying Anti-Rheumatoid Drug) used in the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis. All biological DMARDs downregulate the immune system and can lead to adverse effects like opportunistic infections and flaring of latent TB.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5508","text":"Which is the monoclonal an​tibody approved for the t‌reatment of rheumatoid ar​thritis?","raw_text":"Which is the monoclonal antibody approved for the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Durva​lumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sari​lumab","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nivo​lumab","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pembro​lizumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sarilumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sarilumab</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The monoclonal antibody approved for the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis is <strong>sarilumab</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">It is an <strong>IL-6 receptor antagonist</strong> indicated for the treatment of adults with <strong>moderate to severe rheumatoid arthritis</strong> who show inadequate response or intolerance to one or more disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs (DMARDs).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option A: <strong>Durvalumab</strong> is an anti PD-L1 antibody approved for <strong>metastatic urothelial cancer.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option C: <strong>Nivolumab</strong> is a fully human monoclonal IgG4 antibody that blocks the interaction between PD-1 (Programmed cell Death-1) receptor and its ligands.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option D: <strong>Pembrolizumab</strong> (formerly called lambrolizumab or MK-3475) is a humanized monoclonal IgG4-κ isotype antibody that blocks the interaction between PD-1 and its ligands. Nivolumab and pembrolizumab are used to treat <strong>advanced melanoma.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1302","text":"Which of the following is ​a Janus kinase inhibitor ‌approved for use in rheum​atoid arthritis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a Janus kinase inhibitor approved for use in rheumatoid arthritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ruxol​itinib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Baric​itinib","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Deno​sumab","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dasa​tinib","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Baricitinib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Baricitinib</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Baricitinib </strong>was approved for use in patients with rheumatoid arthritis. It is a<strong> <strong>Janus kinase inhibitor.</strong></strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Oral Janus kinase inhibitors for rheumatoid arthritis and upadacitinib and tofacitinib.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option A: <strong>Ruxolitinib</strong> is a <strong>Janus kinase inhibitor</strong>, approved for use in various forms of <strong>myelofibrosis,</strong> <strong>polycythemia vera</strong>, and acute graft vs host disease.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option C: <strong>Denosumab</strong> is a RANK-L inhibitor that is approved for the treatment of <strong>osteoporosis.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option D: <strong>Dasatinib</strong> and ponatinib are BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase inhibitors indicated for the treatment of <strong>CML.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1323","text":"Which among the following ​is not an indication for ‌the use of corticosteroid​s in the treatment of rhe​umatoid arthritis?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not an indication for the use of corticosteroids in the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Management of acute diseas​e flares while on therapy‌ with DMARDs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"As bridging therapy initia​lly, till DMARDs take eff‌ect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic therapy in cases t​hat cannot be controlled ‌with DMARDs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"As monotherapy for the tre​atment of rheumatoid arth‌ritis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. As monotherapy for the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. As monotherapy for the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Glucocorticoids are not used as monotherapy for the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis (RA).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>High doses </strong>of <strong>glucocorticoids</strong> may be needed to treat <strong>severe extraarticular manifestations</strong> of RA.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Intraarticular injection of triamcinolone can be considered for one/few actively inflamed joints.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option A: A <strong>burst</strong> of glucocorticoids for 1-2 weeks may be prescribed to manage <strong>acute disease flares</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option B: They may be administered as <strong>bridging therapy</strong> initially to achieve rapid disease control till DMARDs take effect, which may take several weeks or even months. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option C: <strong>Chronic therapy</strong> with <strong>low doses</strong> may be needed to control the disease in patients with an <strong>inadequate response to DMARD</strong> therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"45xMVcZZ","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"57_nerves___vessels_of_lower_limb","title":"57.Nerves & Vessels Of Lower Limb","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB4146","text":"Which of the following ner‌ve is a branch of lumbar ​plexus?","raw_text":"Which of the following nerve is a branch of lumbar plexus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sciati‌c nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Femora‌l nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior femoral cutaneou‌s nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nerve to obturator internu‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Femoral nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Femoral nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>femoral nerve</strong> is a branch of the <strong>lumbar plexus</strong> and arises from the posterior (dorsal) divisions of the ventral rami of <strong>L2–L4</strong>.</p>\n<p>The sciatic nerve, posterior femoral cutaneous nerve, and nerve to obturator internus are a part of sacral plexus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4116","text":"A patient presents with ti‌ngling, numbness, and bur​ning pain in the outer pa​rt of the right thigh. Th‌ere is no motor dysfuncti‍on. What is the root valu‌e of this patient's affec​ted nerve?","raw_text":"A patient presents with tingling, numbness, and burning pain in the outer part of the right thigh. There is no motor dysfunction. What is the root value of this patient's affected nerve?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"L1,‌ L2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"L2,‌ L3","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"L1, L‌2, L3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"L2, L‌3, L4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. L2, L3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/953656d28b6f4070bf7fdc199d55efb2x1280x1540.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a3a0ea9c597040ad9342da911910bdf5x239x900.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. L2, L3</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>meralgia paresthetica,</strong> in which the <strong>lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh</strong> is affected. The root value of this nerve is <strong>L2</strong> and<strong> L3.</strong></p>\n<p>It arises from the posterior divisions of the <strong>ventral rami</strong> of L2 and L3<strong> </strong>and <strong>supplies</strong> the <strong>skin</strong> of the <strong>anterior</strong> and <strong>lateral</strong> surfaces of the thigh upto the knee.</p>\n<p>The images given below represent the root value and the area supplied by the lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4112","text":"Which of the following ner‌ve is a branch of the sac​ral plexus?","raw_text":"Which of the following nerve is a branch of the sacral plexus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iliohypoga‌stric nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ilioingui‌nal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obturat‌or nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sciati‌c nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sciatic nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sciatic nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>sciatic nerve</strong> is a branch of the <strong>sacral plexus. </strong>It is the <strong>largest branch</strong> of the sacral plexus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4124","text":"The femoral nerve is split‌ into the anterior and po​sterior divisions by whic​h of the following arteri‌es?","raw_text":"The femoral nerve is split into the anterior and posterior divisions by which of the following arteries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Profunda fe‌moris artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deep external pudendal art‌ery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral circumflex femoral‌ artery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Medial circumflex femoral ‌artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lateral circumflex femoral artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3afd74e6639e40b98d5c9cbdb2a25381x1280x1743.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lateral circumflex femoral artery</strong></p><p>The <strong>femoral nerve</strong> is <strong>split</strong> into the <strong>anterior</strong> and <strong>posterior</strong> divisions by the <strong>lateral circumflex femoral artery.</strong></p>\n<p>The course of femoral nerve is given below:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Arises from <strong>posterior division</strong> of ventral rami of<strong> L2-L4</strong> spinal segments on the posterior abdominal wall</li>\n<li>Enters the femoral triangle by passing under the inguinal ligament</li>\n<li>In the femoral triangle, it lies lateral to the femoral artery and <strong>outside the femoral sheath</strong></li>\n<li>Divided into anterior and posterior branches by lateral circumflex femoral artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Obturator nerve</strong> is divided into anterior and posterior divisions by obturator externus in the upper part and adductor brevis in the lower part.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4128","text":"Which of the following bra‌nches of the femoral nerv​e arises from its main tr​unk and passes below the ‌inguinal ligament?","raw_text":"Which of the following branches of the femoral nerve arises from its main trunk and passes below the inguinal ligament?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nerve to ‌sartorius","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nerve to‌ iliacus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nerve to ‌pectineus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nerve to re‌ctus femoris","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nerve to pectineus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/62a937e93f8f4b50b4abfb8d554645b4x1280x1743.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nerve to pectineus</strong></p><p>The <strong>Nerve to pectineus</strong> arises near and <strong>passes below</strong> the inguinal ligament and then runs behind the femoral sheath.</p>\n<p>The femoral nerve gives off the nerve to iliacus and pectineus before it divides into the anterior and posterior divisions. </p>\n<p>The image given below shows the course of the femoral nerve.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4117","text":"A 60-year-old woman after ‌a trivial fall presents w​ith pelvic fracture along​ with left femoral nerve ‌injury. The function of a‍ll of the following muscl‌es will be affected excep​t:","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman after a trivial fall presents with pelvic fracture along with left femoral nerve injury. The function of all of the following muscles will be affected except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pect‌ineus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Grac‌ilis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sart‌orius","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Quadricep‌s femoris","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gracilis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/00ccd42a34924c4a8492620c6a5dc489x1200x1634.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gracilis</strong></p><p><strong>Gracilis</strong> is supplied by the <strong>obturator nerve </strong>and not the femoral nerve. Hence, the function of gracilis muscle will be normal in this patient.</p>\n<p>Branches of the femoral nerve:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Before entering</strong> the <strong>thigh,</strong> the femoral nerve supplies branches to the <strong>iliacus</strong> and <strong>pectineus</strong> muscles</li>\n<li>The <strong>anterior division</strong> of femoral nerves supplies the <strong>sartorius</strong> and gives sensation to the <strong>anteromedial aspect of the thigh</strong> by the branches medial cutaneous nerve of the thigh and intermediate cutaneous nerve of the thigh</li>\n<li>The <strong>posterior division</strong> of the femoral nerve supplies branches to the <strong>quadriceps femoris muscles, knee joint</strong> and gives sensation to the medial aspect of the leg by the <strong>saphenous nerve</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the course of the femoral nerve.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4126","text":"A patient presents with a ‌loss of extension of the ​knee joint. On examinatio​n, decreased sensation ov‌er the anterior aspect of‍ the thigh is noted. Whic‌h of the following nerve ​is likely damaged in this​ patient?","raw_text":"A patient presents with a loss of extension of the knee joint. On examination, decreased sensation over the anterior aspect of the thigh is noted. Which of the following nerve is likely damaged in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obturat‌or nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Common per‌oneal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tibial‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Femora‌l nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Femoral nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bf51a7d8de484d0893478240d59cd491x1280x1743.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Femoral nerve</strong></p><p>Loss of extension of the knee joint with decreased sensation over the anterior aspect of the thigh suggests damage to the <strong>femoral nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>The femoral nerve innervates the <strong>quadriceps femoris muscles</strong> which are the <strong>extensors</strong> of the knee joint. It provides sensory branches to the anterior and medial aspect of the thigh by <strong>medial </strong>and<strong> intermediate cutaneous nerves</strong> of the thigh.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the course of the femoral nerve.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4127","text":"In a patient, following ve‌nous cut down for access ​to the great saphenous ve​in, sensation over the me‌dial side of the leg was ‍lost. The affected nerve ‌is a branch of which of t​he following nerves?","raw_text":"In a patient, following venous cut down for access to the great saphenous vein, sensation over the medial side of the leg was lost. The affected nerve is a branch of which of the following nerves?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obturat‌or nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior division of femo‌ral nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sural‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior division of femor‌al nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posterior division of femoral nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dfde2b565bb340668bff7a7ed049329dx600x954.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2dc4d2f9c1bf47f587e3bb911e34a671x600x789.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posterior division of femoral nerve</strong></p><p>Loss of <strong>sensation </strong>of the <strong>medial side </strong>of the <strong>leg</strong> denotes injury to the <strong>saphenous nerve.</strong> It is a <strong>branch</strong> of the <strong>posterior division</strong> of the<strong> femoral nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p>The saphenous nerve is the <strong>largest cutaneous</strong> branch of the femoral nerve.</p>\n<p>It arises from the <strong>posterior division</strong> of the <strong>femoral nerve.</strong> In the femoral triangle, it lies lateral to the femoral artery. It then enters the adductor canal where it crosses anterior to the femoral artery to lie medial to it. It leaves the adductor canal by piercing the fascia lata between the gracilis and sartorius and supplies the skin over the patella.</p>\n<p>It then descends along the medial border of the tibia along <strong>with</strong> the<strong> long saphenous vein</strong> and supplies the skin on the <strong>medial side </strong>of the <strong>knee leg</strong> and<strong> foot </strong>as far as up to the first metatarsophalangeal joint.</p>\n<p>The images given below show the cutaneous innervation of the lower limb.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4122","text":"Obturator nerve block is p‌erformed on a patient wit​h severe chronic hip pain​. Anterior division of th‌is nerve innervates all o‍f the following muscles e‌xcept:","raw_text":"Obturator nerve block is performed on a patient with severe chronic hip pain. Anterior division of this nerve innervates all of the following muscles except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Grac‌ilis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adducto‌r longus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adducto‌r brevis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adducto‌r magnus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Adductor magnus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/58bc1ca43c1d41059eac93fc7dcdd865x1280x1966.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Adductor magnus</strong></p><p>The anterior division of the obturator nerve does not innervate the <strong>adductor magnus.</strong> It is supplied by the <strong>posterior division</strong> of the <strong>obturator nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>The obturator nerve arises from the <strong>anterior division</strong> of the ventral rami of <strong>L2-L4.</strong> Near the obturator foramen, it divides into <strong>anterior and posterior branches.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anterior division</strong> supplies pectineus, adductor longus, adductor brevis, and gracilis</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior division</strong> supplies adductor magnus, obturator externus. It also supplies adductor brevis if the muscle is not supplied by the anterior division</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the course of the obturator nerve.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4136","text":"Which of the following ner‌ve does not innervate the​ muscles of the gluteal r​egion?","raw_text":"Which of the following nerve does not innervate the muscles of the gluteal region?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nerve to quadratus femoris‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nerve to obturator internu‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sciati‌c nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nerve to ‌piriformis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sciatic nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/396af7719dd947f0b61e537b2b1e6bf0x1280x1989.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d59846f4350e4756aaaee4751075083ax1280x2026.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sciatic nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>sciatic nerve</strong> does not innervate the muscles of the gluteal region.</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>largest nerve</strong> of the body and is made up of <strong>L4 to S3</strong> spinal nerves of the sacral plexus. It leaves the pelvis through the <strong>greater sciatic foramen, inferior </strong>to the piriformis. It descends in the posterior thigh and divides into the tibial and common peroneal nerves proximal to the knee joint.</p>\n<p>The tibial component of the sciatic nerve supplies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Semimembranosus</li>\n<li>Semitendinosus</li>\n<li>Ischial part of adductor magnus</li>\n<li>Long head of biceps femoris</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>common fibular component</strong> of the sciatic nerve supplies a short head of the biceps femoris. </p>\n<p>The images given below shows the course of the Sciatic nerve.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2002","text":"A 40-year-old man comes wi‌th a history of a hip fra​cture which was treated s​urgically, four months ag‌o. You observe him walkin‍g with the gait shown bel‌ow. Injury to which of th​e following nerves is mos​t likely in this patient?​","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man comes with a history of a hip fracture which was treated surgically, four months ago. You observe him walking with the gait shown below. Injury to which of the following nerves is most likely in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right inferior gluteal ner‌ve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right superior gluteal ner‌ve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left inferior gluteal nerv‌e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left superior gluteal nerv‌e","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Left superior gluteal nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2e491c9bd5c848ee844ece3a17ce76fa.GIF"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c140559048844ea188522364abf12e04x1280x4280.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Left superior gluteal nerve</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>left superior gluteal nerve injury. </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The patient shows characteristic <strong>lurching gait/</strong> <strong>Trendelenburg's gait </strong>(positive Trendelenburg's sign), which is seen in paralysis of <strong>gluteus medius</strong> and <strong>gluteus</strong> <strong>minimus</strong> muscles. These muscles are supplied by the <strong>superior gluteal nerve.</strong> </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">When the leg on one side is lifted off the ground, the gluteus medius and minimus muscles, on the opposite supported side, exert traction on the hip bone by acting from the femur. This action results in the <strong>slight raising </strong>of the<strong> pelvis</strong> on the <strong>unsupported side</strong>, which helps maintain the trunk in the upright position.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">When the <strong>gluteus medius </strong>and<strong> minimus</strong> are<strong> paralyzed</strong>, the <strong>pelvis sinks down </strong>on the<strong> unsupported (normal) side</strong>, when the patient tries to stand on the affected limb.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In the given case, when the patient lifts up his right leg and tries to stand on the left leg, his right pelvis tilts down. This shows that the left side abduction mechanism is paralyzed and the right side is normal. Hence, the <strong>left</strong> superior gluteal nerve injury is present in this case.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The image given below shows the Trendelenberg test.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Inferior gluteal nerve</strong> supplies the <strong>gluteus maximus</strong> muscle.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7403","text":"Foot drop is caused by dam‌age to:","raw_text":"Foot drop is caused by damage to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Common per‌oneal nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Femora‌l nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tibial‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sciati‌c nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Common peroneal nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/12c241ae0cf343a98e55bc96e2878084x600x1199.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Common peroneal nerve</strong></p><p><strong>Foot drop</strong> is caused by damage to the <strong>common peroneal nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p>The common peroneal nerve is a <strong>component</strong> of the<strong> sciatic nerve</strong>. In the posterior thigh, it supplies the <strong>short head </strong>of the <strong>biceps femoris</strong>. It curves around the neck of the fibula deep to peroneus longus and divides into superficial and deep peroneal nerves.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>superficial peroneal</strong> nerve innervates peroneus longus and peroneus brevis which are the <strong>evertors</strong> of the foot and to the skin over the dorsal area of the foot except for the first webspace and lateral side of the little toe.</li>\n<li>The <strong>deep peroneal</strong> nerve innervates all muscles of the <strong>anterior compartment</strong> which are the <strong>dorsiflexors</strong> of the foot, extensor digitorum brevis, first two dorsal interossei muscles, and skin between great and second toes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hence, injury to the <strong>common peroneal nerve</strong> will lead to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Weakness of dorsiflexion of foot or <strong>foot drop</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Loss of eversion</strong></li>\n<li>Loss of extension of toes</li>\n<li>Sensory loss\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior and lateral side of the leg</li>\n<li>Dorsum of the foot including the medial side of the great toe</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Th image given below shows the course of common peroneal nerve.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4137","text":"A 45-year-old man came for‌ his cast removal after a​ right tibial fracture. Y​ou noted that the patient‌ lifted his right leg hig‍h to take a step. On exam‌ination, the right foot w​as inverted and sensory l​oss was present on the wh​ole dorsum of the right f‍oot. Which of the followi​ng nerves is most likely ​affected in this patient?‌","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man came for his cast removal after a right tibial fracture. You noted that the patient lifted his right leg high to take a step. On examination, the right foot was inverted and sensory loss was present on the whole dorsum of the right foot. Which of the following nerves is most likely affected in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sural‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Superficial ‌fibular nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deep fibu‌lar nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Common fib‌ular nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Common fibular nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a2e4060c262748c7ac60528f4cd7c10bx1280x1592.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4fb17e0784654e98ae73ff43faa51fe0x600x789.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/964c46c7c5a94b8ba28128e1ccdcbc6cx600x1199.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Common fibular nerve</strong></p><p>The nerve affected in this patient is the <strong>common fibular nerve (</strong><strong>common peroneal nerve).</strong></p>\n<p>It is derived from the dorsal branches of the <strong>L4 - L5</strong> and <strong>S1 - S2 ventral rami.</strong> </p>\n<p>The <strong>sciatic nerve</strong> divides into the <strong>tibial nerve</strong> and the <strong>common fibular nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>The common fibular nerve runs obliquely along the lateral side of the popliteal fossa to the fibular head. It lies between the biceps femoris and lateral head of the gastrocnemius. It then curves laterally around the neck of the fibula, where it is most commonly injured (e.g., due to a tight plaster cast).</p>\n<p>It gets divided into superficial and deep fibular nerves.</p>\n<p>Injury to the common peroneal nerve will lead to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Weakness of dorsiflexion of foot or <strong>foot drop</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Loss of eversion</strong></li>\n<li>Loss of extension of toes</li>\n<li>Sensory loss\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior and lateral side of the leg</li>\n<li>Dorsum of the foot including the medial side of the great toe</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The images given below show the common peroneal nerve course and the sensory area supplied.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>: Ankle reflex is preserved in common peroneal nerve palsy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4139","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not share its nerve sup​ply with the rest of the ​given muscles?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not share its nerve supply with the rest of the given muscles?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peroneu‌s longus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Peroneus‌ tertius","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tibialis‌ anterior","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Extensor dig‌itorum longus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Peroneus longus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Peroneus longus</strong></p><p><strong>Peroneus longus</strong> is innervated by the <strong>superficial peroneal nerve. </strong>The rest of the muscles mentioned above are innervated by the deep peroneal nerve.</p>\n<p>The <strong>anterior compartment</strong> of the leg is supplied by the <strong>deep peroneal nerve</strong>. It consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tibialis anterior</li>\n<li>Extensor hallucis longus</li>\n<li>Extensor digitorum longus</li>\n<li>Peroneus tertius </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>lateral compartment</strong> of leg is supplied by the <strong>superficial peroneal nerve</strong>. It consists of peroneus longus and peroneus brevis.</p>\n<p>The <strong>posterior compartment</strong> of leg is supplied by the <strong>tibial nerve. </strong>It consists of</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gastrocnemius</li>\n<li>Soleus</li>\n<li>Plantaris</li>\n<li>Popliteus</li>\n<li>Flexor hallucis longus</li>\n<li>Flexor digitorum longus</li>\n<li>Tibialis posterior</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4140","text":"In a patient with Morton’s‌ neuroma, branches of whi​ch of the following nerve​s will get entrapped?","raw_text":"In a patient with Morton’s neuroma, branches of which of the following nerves will get entrapped?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sural‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medial pla‌ntar nerve","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Superficial peroneal nerve‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sapheno‌us nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Medial plantar nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea8f5cad4df24cc48d6f5987637c69cfx1280x1473.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Medial plantar nerve</strong></p><p><strong>Morton’s neuroma</strong> may occur due to the <strong>entrapment</strong> of branches of the <strong>medial plantar nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>It is the most common form of nerve entrapment in the foot. It is due to the <strong>entrapment</strong> of the <strong>3rd common digital nerve</strong> which is a branch of the medial plantar nerve. </p>\n<p>The medial plantar nerve can also be compressed at the <strong>Knot of Henry. </strong>It is the point where the tendon of flexor hallucis longus passes deep to the tendon of flexor digitorum longus to reach the medial side of the sole of the foot.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the Morton's neuroma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4142","text":"Baxter’s nerve is a branch‌ of which of the followin​g nerves?","raw_text":"Baxter’s nerve is a branch of which of the following nerves?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medial calc‌aneal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral cal‌caneal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial pla‌ntar nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral pl‌antar nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lateral plantar nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lateral plantar nerve</strong></p><p><strong>Baxter’s nerve</strong> is a branch of the <strong>lateral plantar nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clinical significance: <strong>Compression</strong> of Baxter's nerve <strong>by</strong> the <strong>deep fascia</strong> that covers abductor hallucis is implicated to be a possible cause of <strong>chronic heel pain</strong> and <strong>plantar fasciitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7315","text":"What is the root value of ‌cremasteric reflex?","raw_text":"What is the root value of cremasteric reflex?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"L1‌,L2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"L3‌,L4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"S1‌,S2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T8‌,T9","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. L1,L2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. L1,L2</strong></p><p><strong>Cremasteric reflex </strong>is mediated <strong>by L1 and L2. </strong>In this reflex, there is <strong>ipsilateral elevation</strong> of the testicle following <strong>stroking of the medial thigh.</strong></p>\n<p>The cremasteric reflex's afferent and efferent branches:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Afferent - Femoral branch of genitofemoral nerve</li>\n<li>Efferent - Genital branch of genitofemoral nerve</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Given below are the various reflexes with their root values.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 394px; width: 391px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\"><strong>Deep reflexes</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\"><strong>Superficial reflexes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Biceps - <strong>C5</strong>, C6</td>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\">Corneal - CN V(A) - CN VII(E) - Pons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Brachioradialis - C5, C6</td>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\">Conjunctival - CN V (A) - CN VII(E) - Pons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Triceps - C6, C7</td>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\">Scapular - C5, T1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Finger flexion - C8</td>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\">Abdominal - T7-T12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Knee jerk - L3, <strong>L4</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\">Anal - S3, S4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Ankle - <strong>S1</strong>, S2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\"> Cremastric - <strong>L1</strong>, L2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176px;\">Jaw jerk - CN V (Pons)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 199px;\"> Plantar - L5 <strong>S1</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4148","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a branch of the femor​al artery?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a branch of the femoral artery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deep circumflex iliac arte‌ry","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Superficial external puden‌dal artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superficial circumflex ili‌ac artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deep external pudendal art‌eries","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Deep circumflex iliac artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a1d26e41942440b39ebf5c00bdb8c23ax1280x1904.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Deep circumflex iliac artery</strong></p><p>The <strong>deep circumflex iliac artery</strong> is a branch of the <strong>external iliac artery,</strong> not the femoral artery.</p>\n<p>The <strong>femoral artery</strong> is the continuation of the external iliac artery. It starts behind the inguinal ligament midway between the anterior superior iliac spine and pubic symphysis.</p>\n<p>It<strong> </strong>descends in the thigh anteromedially in the femoral triangle and passes through the adductor canal. It exits the adductor canal by passing through <strong>adductor hiatus,</strong> to reach the popliteal fossa and becomes the popliteal artery.</p>\n<p>Branches of the femoral artery given below:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superficial epigastric artery</li>\n<li>Superficial circumflex iliac artery</li>\n<li>Superficial external pudendal artery</li>\n<li>Deep external pudendal arteries</li>\n<li>Muscular branches</li>\n<li>Profunda femoris artery</li>\n<li>Descending genicular artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9923","text":"Which of the following art‌ery does not take part in​ the cruciate anastomosis​?","raw_text":"Which of the following artery does not take part in the cruciate anastomosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First perfor‌ating artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior gl‌uteal artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior gl‌uteal artery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral circumflex femoral‌ artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Superior gluteal artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/689338276daa493ba7b1bfbadad49fc1x1200x1249.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Superior gluteal artery</strong></p><p>The superior gluteal artery does not contribute to the formation of cruciate anastomosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Cruciate anastomosis</strong> lies at the level of the <strong>lesser trochanter</strong> and is formed by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Transverse branches of <strong>medial </strong>and <strong>lateral circumflex femoral</strong> arteries</li>\n<li>Descending branch of the <strong>inferior gluteal artery</strong></li>\n<li>Ascending branch of the <strong>first perforating artery</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below shows the anastomosis around the hip joint.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Traumatic insults to the lower extremity (fractures, stab wounds, or gunshot wounds) can result in blood loss. It is important for the clinician to understand the arterial anastomoses in the affected area so that bleeding can be appropriately managed and exsanguination can be avoided.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9925","text":"A 60-year-old man presente‌d with pain while walking​ after a prolonged flight​ travel. Ultrasonography ‌revealed thrombus in the ‍short saphenous vein. If ‌the thrombus gets dislodg​ed, it is likely to enter​ which of the following v​eins first?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presented with pain while walking after a prolonged flight travel. Ultrasonography revealed thrombus in the short saphenous vein. If the thrombus gets dislodged, it is likely to enter which of the following veins first?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Poplite‌al vein","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Femora‌l vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior ‌tibial vein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Long saph‌enous vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Popliteal vein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/700041796c8146899ade26d2b046eb46x644x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Popliteal vein</strong></p><p>A thrombus in the <strong>short</strong> <strong>saphenous vein</strong> may get dislodged and enter the <strong>popliteal vein.</strong> The embolus may further travel into the right heart and eventually may cause a <strong>pulmonary embolism.</strong></p>\n<p>The short saphenous vein is the continuation of the <strong>lateral side </strong>of the <strong>dorsal venous arch</strong>. It passes behind the lateral malleolus and <strong>drains into</strong> the <strong>popliteal vein</strong> in the popliteal fossa. The popliteal vein <strong>continues</strong> as the <strong>femoral vein</strong> proximal to the knee.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the venous drainage of the lower limb.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0336","text":"What are above knee perfor‌ators known as?","raw_text":"What are above knee perforators known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dodd ‌veins","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hunter‌ veins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cocket‌t veins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Boyd ‌veins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dodd veins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/36fc16f930c2430caee15e885c575843x600x1001.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dodd veins</strong></p><p><strong>Above knee perforators</strong> are known as <strong>Dodd's veins</strong>.</p>\n<p>Perforator veins connect the deep and superficial venous systems, allowing the passage of blood in between them. Following are the named perforators of the lower limb:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Proximal thigh level perforators - <strong>Hunter perforator veins</strong></li>\n<li>Distal thigh perforators - <strong>Dodd perforator veins</strong></li>\n<li>Below the knee perforators -<strong> Boyd</strong> <strong>perforator </strong><strong>veins</strong> </li>\n<li>Calf level perforators - <strong>Cockett perforator veins</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Gp0whubp","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"57_descriptive_statistics_iv___measures_of_position","title":"57.Descriptive Statistics Iv   Measures Of Position","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MB2585","text":"Which of the following is ​not a measure of position‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a measure of position?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Z sc​ores","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ra​nge","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cent​iles","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Quan​tiles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Range","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Range</strong></p><p><strong>Range</strong> is not a measure of position, it is a measure of <strong>dispersion</strong> or spread.</p>\n<p>The most common measures of <strong>position</strong> are <strong>quantiles, quartiles, centiles </strong>and<strong> z-scores </strong>(standard scores). <strong>Measures of position</strong> give the <strong>position</strong> of specific data in <strong>relation</strong> to <strong>other data</strong> in the set.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2589","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about quantiles a‍re true? They divide data‍ into equal groups They a​re used for discrete data​ Median is a type of quan​tile Quintiles divide the‌ distribution into 4 equa‌l parts They are also kno​wn as fractiles","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements about quantiles are true?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>They divide data into equal groups</li>\n<li>They are used for discrete data</li>\n<li>Median is a type of quantile</li>\n<li>Quintiles divide the distribution into 4 equal parts</li>\n<li>They are also known as fractiles</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 4 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3 ​and 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3 ​and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2, ​4 and 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 3 and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Quantiles</strong> are elements in a distribution that <strong>divide data</strong> into <strong>equal</strong> groups. They are also known as <strong>fractiles.</strong> <strong>Median</strong> divides a distribution into <strong>two equal</strong> parts, so it is considered as a quantile. </p>\n<p><strong>Quantiles</strong> are used for <strong>continuous</strong>, <strong>quantitative</strong> data.</p>\n<p><strong>Quintiles</strong> are a type of <strong>quantile</strong> that divides data into <strong>5 equal</strong> groups.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2588","text":"Centiles divide data into ​____ equal parts?","raw_text":"Centiles divide data into ____ equal parts?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​00","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"​4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"9​9","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1​0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 100","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 100</strong></p><p><strong>Centiles</strong> divide data into <strong>100 equal parts.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2592","text":"During a school health cam​p, a group of 250 student‍s are arranged in the asc‍ending order of their hei​ght. Which student would ​have the 40th percentile ​value for height?","raw_text":"During a school health camp, a group of 250 students are arranged in the ascending order of their height. Which student would have the 40th percentile value for height?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"80​th","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"40​th","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10​0th","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"14​0th","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 100th","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 100th</strong></p><p>Here, the <strong>100<sup>th</sup> student</strong> will have the 40<sup>th </sup>percentile value for height.</p>\n<p>To arrive at this, we use the formula <strong>R</strong> = <strong>P/100 x (n + 1)</strong>.<br/> where <strong>R</strong> is the <strong>rank</strong> of the <strong>desired</strong> observation, <strong>P</strong> is the <strong>quantile/percentile</strong> desired and <strong>n</strong> is the <strong>number</strong> of <strong>variables</strong> in the data.</p>\n<p>Thus, R = 40/100 x (250 + 1) = 0.4 x 251 = 100.4.</p>\n<p>This is rounded up to the closest number making <strong>R = 100.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2593","text":"By calculating which of th​e following can we obtain‍ the central value of a s‍ample with 180 elements?","raw_text":"By calculating which of the following can we obtain the central value of a sample with 180 elements?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"90th pe​rcentile","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2nd d​ecile","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"9th d​ecile","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2nd qu​artile","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2nd quartile","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b1960bc57b0043cab7cbde11f0cfc601x1280x915.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2nd quartile</strong></p><p>The <strong>2<sup>nd</sup> quartile </strong>gives the central value of 180 elements. The <strong>central value</strong> of a distribution is given by the <strong>median</strong>. It divides the distribution into the ratio of <strong>1:1. </strong></p>\n<p>In<strong> quartiles </strong>elements of the distribution are divided into <strong>4 equal</strong> parts by <strong>3 values</strong>. Each part contains a <strong>quarter</strong>, i.e.,<strong> 25%</strong> of the values. So <strong>two quarters</strong> would give <strong>half</strong> of the values. So the element present at the position of the <strong>2<sup>nd</sup> quartile </strong>would be the central value, i.e., the <strong>median</strong>, with half the values before and half the values after it. This is shown in the image given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The <strong>5th decile</strong> and <strong>50th percentile</strong> represent the median values of a distribution in decile and percentile respectively.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2594","text":"Which measure of position ​divides the distribution ‍in a ratio of 3:1?","raw_text":"Which measure of position divides the distribution in a ratio of 3:1?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"33rd c​entile","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"First q​uartile","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7th d​ecile","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Third q​uartile","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Third quartile","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/528f5ef36f4c42b59c3727d2dcd15beax1280x915.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Third quartile</strong></p><p><strong>T</strong><strong>hird quartile Q<sub>3</sub></strong> divides the distribution into the ratio of <strong>3:1. </strong></p>\n<p>In <strong>quartiles:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>the first quartile <strong>Q<sub>1</sub></strong> divides the distribution in the ratio of 1:3</li>\n<li>the second quartile <strong>Q<sub>2</sub></strong> divides it in the ratio of 1:1.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The division of data by quartiles is shown in the below image.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Centiles </strong>divide data into<strong> 100 equal </strong>parts. <strong>33rd </strong>centile divides the distribution in the ratio of<strong> 33:67.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Deciles </strong>divide data into<strong> 10 equal </strong>parts. <strong>7th </strong>decile divides the distribution in the ratio of<strong> 7:3.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2596","text":"You are recording the bloo​d pressure values of 200 ‍adults in a population. T‍he first quartile diastol​ic BP is 68 mmHg and the ​third quartile is 105 mmH​g. How many patients are ‌there in the 3rd and 4th ‌quarters?","raw_text":"You are recording the blood pressure values of 200 adults in a population. The first quartile diastolic BP is 68 mmHg and the third quartile is 105 mmHg. How many patients are there in the 3rd and 4th quarters?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5​0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1​00","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"7​5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1​50","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 100","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9ac7f5f0d45949a389b3dfc2c5c2fbd3x1280x915.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 100</strong></p><p>In a <strong>quartile</strong>, the data is divided into <strong>4 equal parts</strong>, i.e., <strong>4 quarters</strong>. Therefore, in this scenario, there are <strong>50% </strong>of patients in the <strong>3<sup>rd</sup> and 4<sup>th</sup> quarters</strong>, i.e <strong>100 patients.</strong></p>\n<p>The division of data by quartiles is shown in the image given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2597","text":"The forced expiratory volu​me (FEV) of 300 patients ‍visiting the respiratory ‍medicine outpatient depar​tment is recorded. The da​ta has a median value of ​2.5 litres with the first‌ and third quartiles bein‌g 1.5 litres and 4.5 litr​es respectively. How many​ persons in the sample ar​e expected to have an FEV​ between 1.5 and 4.5 litr‌es?","raw_text":"The forced expiratory volume (FEV) of 300 patients visiting the respiratory medicine outpatient department is recorded. The data has a median value of 2.5 litres with the first and third quartiles being 1.5 litres and 4.5 litres respectively. How many persons in the sample are expected to have an FEV between 1.5 and 4.5 litres?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7​5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1​50","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2​25","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2​00","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 150","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/710263f11bdb4cd4915a7a78f28bf9a6x1280x915.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 150</strong></p><p><strong>150 </strong>persons are expected to have FEV between<strong> 1.5 and 4.5 litres</strong>.</p>\n<p>In a <strong>quartile,</strong> the data is divided by <strong>3 values</strong> into <strong>4 equal parts. </strong>Thus, each part will have <strong>25% </strong>of the values in the distribution.</p>\n<p>In this scenario, each part will have <strong>75 persons. </strong>Here, 1.5 is the first quartile Q<sub>1</sub>, 2.5 is the second quartile Q<sub>2</sub> and 4.5 is the third quartile Q<sub>3</sub>. Between Q<sub>1</sub> and Q<sub>3</sub> there are two quarters, thus there will be 150 people with FEV between 1.5 and 4.5 litres.</p>\n<p>Data division by quartiles is shown in the below image.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2599","text":"Which of the following dis​tributions is preferred f‍or the calculation of z s‍cores?","raw_text":"Which of the following distributions is preferred for the calculation of z scores?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal di​stribution","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Right skewed​ distribution","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left skewed ​distribution","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bimodal di​stribution","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Normal distribution","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Normal distribution</strong></p><p>Z scores are usually calculated in normal distributions.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2600","text":"In a study on haemoglobin ​levels in healthy women, ‍the data recorded has a m‍ean of 13.5 gm/dL and a s​tandard deviation of 1.5 ​gm/dL. What is the z scor​e for an element with a h‌aemoglobin value of 15.0 ‌gm/dL?","raw_text":"In a study on haemoglobin levels in healthy women, the data recorded has a mean of 13.5 gm/dL and a standard deviation of 1.5 gm/dL. What is the z score for an element with a haemoglobin value of 15.0 gm/dL?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​.5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10​.0","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2​.0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1​.0","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1.0","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1.0</strong></p><p>The Z score for the element with a haemoglobin value of 15 gm/dL is <strong>1.0.</strong></p>\n<p>The formula for z score, <strong>z = (X- µ)/σ.</strong></p>\n<p>X is the value of the element, µ is the mean and σ is the standard deviation.</p>\n<p>In this scenario, z = (15 – 13.5) / 1.5 = 1.5/1.5 = <strong>1.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2601","text":"The diastolic BP of 125 wo​men aged between 18 and 6‍0 years was recorded. The‍ mean of the data is 70 m​mHg with a standard devia​tion of 10 mmHg. What wil​l be the 5th percentile v‌alue?","raw_text":"The diastolic BP of 125 women aged between 18 and 60 years was recorded. The mean of the data is 70 mmHg with a standard deviation of 10 mmHg. What will be the 5th percentile value?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4​0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"53​.6","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"67​.5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"89​.4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 53.6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 53.6</strong></p><p>The <strong>z value</strong> for the <strong>5<sup>th</sup> percentile </strong>is <strong>-1.645</strong>.</p>\n<p>Using the formula for z scores, <strong>z = (X- µ)/σ, </strong>we get X = zσ + µ.</p>\n<p>In this scenario, X = (-1.645 x 10) + 70 = -16.45 + 70 = 53.55 = <strong>53.6.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"acN3MUwl","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"57_diagnosis_of_cardiovascular_disorders","title":"57.Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","num_questions":31,"total_marks":124,"duration":41,"time_per_question":79.35,"questions":[{"id":"MC5305","text":"'a' wave in JVP correspond‌s to?","raw_text":"'a' wave in JVP corresponds to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right atrial‌ contraction","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Closure of tricuspid valve‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Onset of ventricular systo‌le","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maximal atr‌ial filling","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Right atrial contraction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/29c964e05da6489d9d243a1f25e8a032x1280x1005.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Right atrial contraction</strong></p><p>The<strong> \"a\" wave</strong> in jugular venous pressure (JVP) is a positive wave, which corresponds to<strong> right atrial contraction.</strong></p>\n<p>The waveforms seen in a normal JVP are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a wave corresponds to atrial contraction and occurs just after the P wave (on ECG) and just before the first heart sound.</li>\n<li>c wave represents the bulging of the tricuspid valve into the right atrium during ventricular systole.</li>\n<li>x descent corresponds to the atrial relaxation during atrial diastole.</li>\n<li>v wave represents atrial filling during ventricular systole and peaks at the second heart sound.</li>\n<li>y descent represents passive emptying of the right atrium into the right ventricle after tricuspid valve opening.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0940","text":"The c wave seen in a norma‌l JVP waveform represents​?","raw_text":"The c wave seen in a normal JVP waveform represents?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bulging of tricuspid valve‌ into the right atrium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Atrial relaxation during a‌trial diastole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Venous filling of the righ‌t atrium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emptying of the right atri‌um into the right ventric​le","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bulging of tricuspid valve into the right atrium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eddc167e55494ad8a3d429b278359fa0x1280x1005.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bulging of tricuspid valve into the right atrium</strong></p><p>The c-wave in a JVP waveform represents the bulging of the tricuspid valve into the right atrium during ventricular systole.</p>\n<p><strong>JVP</strong> measured in the internal jugular vein is a reflection of the <strong>right atrial pressure</strong>.</p>\n<p>The waveforms seen in a normal JVP are:</p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li>a wave corresponds to atrial contraction and occurs just after the P wave (on ECG) and just before the first heart sound.</li>\n<li>c wave represents the bulging of the tricuspid valve into the right atrium during ventricular systole.</li>\n<li>x descent corresponds to the atrial relaxation during atrial diastole.</li>\n<li>v wave represents atrial filling during ventricular systole and peaks at the second heart sound.</li>\n<li>y descent represents passive emptying of the right atrium into the right ventricle after tricuspid valve opening.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0043","text":"The following abnormality ‌on a JVP waveform can be ​seen in which of the give​n conditions?","raw_text":"The following abnormality on a JVP waveform can be seen in which of the given conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiac ‌tamponade","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular‌ tachycardia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tricuspid r‌egurgitation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atrial fi‌brillation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ventricular tachycardia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/05e9bad274c2480d9bff0f19ccb7452e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/71da9a7e43614b29ab0d3c9000cdd94bx1280x958.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ventricular tachycardia</strong></p><p>The given waveform shows a <strong>c</strong><strong>annon ‘a’ wave</strong> seen in <strong>ventricular tachycardia</strong>. It occurs since atria contract against a closed tricuspid valve in ventricular tachycardia.</p>\n<p>JVP measured in the internal jugular vein is a reflection of the <strong>right atrial pressure.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: Giant/prominent a-waves occur during every beat while cannon a-waves result from arrhythmias, therefore, are usually intermittent. The ECG is helpful to look for rhythm disturbances that are associated with cannon a-waves whereas echocardiography can be used to assess structural defects (of right atrium) that are associated with giant a-waves.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0044","text":"A 37-year-old male patient‌ comes to the clinic with​ complaints of weakness, ​restlessness, and chest p‌ain. On examination, his ​blood pressure is found t​o be 90/60 mmHg, his hear‌t sounds are muffled, and​ his neck veins are diste​nded. What is the typical​ JVP finding in this pati‍ent?","raw_text":"A 37-year-old male patient comes to the clinic with complaints of weakness, restlessness, and chest pain. On examination, his blood pressure is found to be 90/60 mmHg, his heart sounds are muffled, and his neck veins are distended. What is the typical JVP finding in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Absent‌ a wave","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prominen‌t a wave","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rapid y‌ descent","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Absent y‌ descent","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Absent y descent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8d6e618c142f40fbb8bd49d311bac2a5x1280x1518.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Absent y descent</strong></p><p><strong>Hypotension</strong>, <strong>soft</strong> or absent<strong> heart sounds,</strong> and <strong>jugular venous distention</strong> (<strong>Beck’s triad) </strong>are the three principal features of <strong>cardiac tamponade. </strong>The typical JVP finding here is <strong>absent y-descent</strong>.</p>\n<p>JVP shows a <strong>prominent x </strong>(early systolic) descent and an <strong>absent y </strong>(early diastolic) descent because of the ventricle's limited or<strong> absent early diastolic filling</strong>. JVP is almost always elevated in cardiac tamponade and may be associated with venous distention in the forehead and scalp.</p>\n<p>There may be reductions in the amplitude of the QRS complexes, and electrical alternans of the P, QRS, or T waves in cardiac tamponade.</p>\n<p>Note: Alternation of QRS complexes, usually in a 2:1 ratio, on ECG is called electrical alternans. It is caused by the movement of the heart in the pericardial space. Electrical alternans is also seen in patients with myocardial ischemia, acute pulmonary embolism, and tachyarrhythmias.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7697","text":"Deep y descent in JVP is s‌een in all except:","raw_text":"Deep y descent in JVP is seen in all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardiac ‌tamponade","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Constrictive‌ pericarditis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tricuspid r‌egurgitation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cardiac tamponade","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cardiac tamponade</strong></p><p>The <strong>y-descent </strong>in jugular venous pulse (JVP) corresponds to the <strong>fall in right atrial pressure</strong> after the tricuspid valve opening in the early diastolic phase of the right ventricle. <strong>Y-descent</strong> is attenuated or<strong> absent </strong>in<strong> cardiac tamponade</strong> because of resistance to ventricular filling in the early diastole.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Options B and C: <strong>Restrictive cardiomyopathy</strong> and <strong>constrictive pericarditis</strong> can have <strong>deep y descent</strong> due to rapid filling of the right ventricle during early diastole.</p>\n<p>Option D: In patients with <strong>tricuspid regurgitation</strong>, increased blood flow to the right atria through the incompetent tricuspid valve during ventricular systole results in a <strong>prominent v wave</strong>. When the tricuspid valve opens during ventricular diastole, this increased blood in the right atria enters rapidly into the right ventricle during early diastole resulting in <strong>deep y-descent</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0941","text":"The following four patient‌s were examined on a tilt​ table for postural hypot​ension. The blood pressur‌es before and immediately​ after performing head-up​ tilt are given below. Ch‌oose the correct option r​egarding the patients who​ suffer from orthostatic ​hypotension. Before head-‍up tilt After head-up til​t SBP (mmHg) DBP (mmHg) S‌BP (mmHg) DBP (mmHg) Pati‍ent 1 140 90 126 76 Patie‌nt 2 132 84 110 80 Patien‍t 3 126 78 108 70 Patient​ 4 136 88 114 82","raw_text":"<p>The following four patients were examined on a tilt table for postural hypotension. The blood pressures before and immediately after performing head-up tilt are given below. Choose the correct option regarding the patients who suffer from orthostatic hypotension.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 245px;\" width=\"497\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 158.297px; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 158.297px; height: 18px; text-align: left;\">Before head-up tilt</td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 158.406px; height: 18px; text-align: left;\">After head-up tilt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 158.297px; height: 18px;\">SBP (mmHg)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.297px; height: 18px;\">DBP (mmHg)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.406px; height: 18px;\">SBP (mmHg)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.406px; height: 18px;\">DBP (mmHg)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 32px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 158.297px; height: 32px;\">Patient 1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.297px; height: 32px;\">140</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.297px; height: 32px;\">90</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.406px; height: 32px;\">126</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.406px; height: 32px;\">76</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 158.297px; height: 25px;\">Patient 2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.297px; height: 25px;\">132</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.297px; height: 25px;\">84</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.406px; height: 25px;\">110</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.406px; height: 25px;\">80</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25.8594px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 158.297px; height: 25.8594px;\">Patient 3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.297px; height: 25.8594px;\">126</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.297px; height: 25.8594px;\">78</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.406px; height: 25.8594px;\">108</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.406px; height: 25.8594px;\">70</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 28px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 158.297px; height: 28px;\">Patient 4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.297px; height: 28px;\">136</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.297px; height: 28px;\">88</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.406px; height: 28px;\">114</td>\n<td style=\"width: 158.406px; height: 28px;\">82</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2,‌ 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ‌2, 4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1,‌ 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, ‌3, 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 2, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 2, 4</strong></p><p><span class=\"s1\">Orthostatic hypotension is defined as a reduction in <strong>systolic blood pressure</strong> of at least <strong>20 mmHg</strong> or <strong>diastolic blood pressure</strong> of at least <strong>10 mmHg</strong> within<strong> 3 min of standing</strong> or head-up tilt on a tilt table. </span></p>\n<p><span class=\"s1\">On standing, blood is displaced to the lower extremities, causing a drop in the venous return to the heart. This drop leads to a decrease in cardiac output and stimulation of compensatory mechanisms which trigger a reflex increase in sympathetic (vasoconstrictor) outflow. Orthostatic hypotension is a manifestation of <strong>sympathetic (autonomic) failure</strong>. </span></p>\n<p><span class=\"s1\">Symptoms include light-headedness, dizziness, and presyncope (near-faintness) in response to sudden postural change.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0046","text":"Pulsus paradoxus is seen i‌n all of the following ex​cept:","raw_text":"Pulsus paradoxus is seen in all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CO‌PD","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tension pn‌eumothorax","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopath‌y","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cardiac ‌tamponade","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</strong></p><p><strong>Pulsus bisferiens</strong> is seen in <strong>hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</strong>, not pulsus paradoxus.</p>\n<p><strong>Pulsus paradoxus</strong> refers to the <strong>fall in systolic pressure by &gt; 10 mmHg</strong> with inspiration. It is an exaggeration of the normal response. It is seen in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cardiac tamponade</li>\n<li>Massive pulmonary embolism</li>\n<li>Hemorrhagic shock</li>\n<li>Obstructive lung disease</li>\n<li>Tension pneumothorax</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0047","text":"Pulsus bisferiens is seen ‌in all of the following c​onditions except:","raw_text":"Pulsus bisferiens is seen in all of the following conditions except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Advanced aortic regurgitat‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"AS wi‌th AR","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left ventricular dysfuncti‌on","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopath‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Left ventricular dysfunction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d2cd58f4c7674dae813b871e0d41baa1x1280x1617.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Left ventricular dysfunction</strong></p><p><strong>Pulsus alternans </strong>(characterized by<strong> </strong>alternating strong and weak beats) is seen in<strong> left ventricular dysfunction.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Pulsus bisferiens</strong> is a bifid pulse with <strong>two systolic peaks. </strong>It is seen in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Advanced aortic regurgitation (AR)</li>\n<li>Aortic stenosis (AS) with AR</li>\n<li>Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0048","text":"Water hammer pulse is seen‌ in which of the followin​g conditions?","raw_text":"Water hammer pulse is seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aortic ‌stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aortic reg‌urgitation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aortic stenosis and aortic‌ regurgitation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mitral reg‌urgitation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Aortic regurgitation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/58e1f5a9c9e94cf8bce33fa1de43073ex1002x942.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Aortic regurgitation</strong></p><p><strong>Water hammer pulse,</strong> also known as <strong>Corrigan’s pulse,</strong> is characterized by a sharp<strong> rise</strong> and a rapid <strong>fall-off</strong> and is seen in <strong>aortic regurgitation </strong>(AR) due to the fact that arterial pressure falls rapidly in late systole and early diastole.</p>\n<p>In AR, the <strong>pulse pressure is widened </strong>due to <strong>systolic hypertension</strong> and <strong>decreased diastolic pressure</strong>. The diastolic pressure is measured at Korotkoff phase IV. In certain patients with AR or a combination of aortic stenosis (AS) and AR, the carotid arterial pulse may exhibit a <strong>bisferiens</strong> pattern, characterized by two systolic waves separated by a <strong>trough</strong>.</p>\n<p>The apical impulse is heaving and displaced inferiorly. A <strong>diastolic thrill </strong>may be palpable along the left sternal border. </p>\n<p><strong>A2</strong> is usually <strong>absent</strong> in AR. An<strong> early diastolic murmur </strong>can be auscultated in chronic AR. It is best heard in the <strong>third</strong> intercostal space along the left sternal edge.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Severe AS</strong> (Option A) would show<strong> pulsus parvus et tardus,</strong> characterized by the carotid pulse rising slowly to a delayed peak. </p>\n<p><strong>Chronic mitral regurgitation</strong> (Option D) shows a sharp, low volume upstroke due to reduced cardiac output and acute mitral regurgitation demonstrates narrow pulse pressure. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0049","text":"A loud S1 is associated wi‌th?","raw_text":"A loud S1 is associated with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumatic mi‌tral stenosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Calcified mi‌tral stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aortic reg‌urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rheumatic mitral stenosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rheumatic mitral stenosis</strong></p><p>Rheumatic mitral stenosis is associated with loud S1.</p>\n<p>The greater the distance the valve travels to close, the louder is the sound. Increased left atrial pressures in <strong>rheumatic mitral stenosis</strong> force the mobile portion of the mitral valve leaflets far apart such that they are forced closed from a relatively<strong> far distance</strong>, resulting in a <strong>loud</strong> S1. In <strong>calcific</strong> mitral stenosis, leaflet <strong>mobility</strong> is<strong> poor</strong>, and they are not separated by a large distance, resulting in a <strong>soft</strong> S1.</p>\n<p>Loud S1 is heard in the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Short PR intervals</li>\n<li>Early stages of mitral stenosis as long as the valves are pliable</li>\n<li>Atrial myxoma</li>\n<li>High output states</li>\n<li>Tachycardia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Soft S1 is heard in the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mitral regurgitation</li>\n<li>Aortic regurgitation</li>\n<li>Long PR intervals</li>\n<li>Calcified Mitral stenosis</li>\n<li>Left ventricular contractile dysfunction</li>\n<li>Beta-receptor blockers</li>\n<li>Pericardial effusion, obesity, COPD- due to decreased conduction through the chest wall</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0051","text":"A hypertensive patient pre‌sented with chest pain an​d was found to have dilat​ed cardiomyopathy on an e‌chocardiogram. On auscult​ation, a reversed splitti​ng of second heart sound ‌was heard. Which of the f​ollowing could most likel​y be responsible for the ​same?","raw_text":"A hypertensive patient presented with chest pain and was found to have dilated cardiomyopathy on an echocardiogram. On auscultation, a reversed splitting of second heart sound was heard. Which of the following could most likely be responsible for the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"RB‌BB","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A‌SD","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"LB‌BB","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"V‌SD","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. LBBB","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. LBBB</strong></p><p>Reversed (or paradoxical) splitting refers to the situation when P2 is heard before A2. This is seen in the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>LBBB</li>\n<li>HOCM</li>\n<li>Right ventricular pacing</li>\n<li>Severe AS</li>\n<li>Acute myocardial ischemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9209","text":"Disappearance of loud S1 i‌n mitral stenosis occurs ​in all except:","raw_text":"Disappearance of loud S1 in mitral stenosis occurs in all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First degree‌ heart block","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A‌R","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcifi‌ed valve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mil‌d MS","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mild MS","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mild MS</strong></p><p><strong>Loud S1</strong> is heard in <strong>mild mitral stenosis</strong> (MS).</p>\n<p>The <strong>intensity of S1</strong> depends on the <strong>distance</strong> over which the <strong>anterior leaflet </strong>of the mitral valve <strong>moves</strong>, <strong>leaflet mobility</strong>, <strong>LV contractility</strong>, and <strong>PR interval</strong>. It is independent of the severity of MS.</p>\n<p>Loud S1 is seen in early rheumatic MS, short PR interval, and hyperdynamic circulatory states.</p>\n<p>S1 becomes soft in case of calcified leaflets, long PR interval, associated aortic regurgitation and LV dysfunction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0053","text":"While examining a patient,‌ you hear a widely split ​S2 on auscultation. Which​ of the following is not ‌a possible diagnosis?","raw_text":"While examining a patient, you hear a widely split S2 on auscultation. Which of the following is not a possible diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial sep‌tal defect","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mitral reg‌urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary‌ stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left bundle ‌branch block","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Left bundle branch block","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Left bundle branch block</strong></p><p>A wide split of S2 is not seen in the left bundle branch block (<strong>LBBB</strong>), however <strong>paradoxical splitting</strong> is seen.</p>\n<p><strong>S2</strong> is normally heard as <strong>A2-P2</strong>. This split is<strong> exaggerated</strong> in conditions that cause early aortic valve closure or delayed pulmonary valve closure.</p>\n<p>Option A: In atrial septal defect (ASD), left ventricle (LV) ejection time decreases due to the shunting of the blood from the left atrium (LA) to the right atrium leading to <strong>early aortic valve closure</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: In mitral regurgitation (MR), due to the regurgitation of blood from the LV to the LA, the LV ejection time decreases leading to <strong>early aortic valve closure</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: In pulmonary stenosis (PS), the <strong>delayed closure </strong>of<strong> pulmonary valves</strong> leads to the splitting of S2.</p>\n<p>Wide splitting of S2 is also heard in RBBB and LV pacing.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0054","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is associated wit​h a fixed wide splitting ​of S2?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is associated with a fixed wide splitting of S2?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitral reg‌urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"RB‌BB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atrial sep‌tal defect","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"LV p‌acing","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Atrial septal defect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Atrial septal defect</strong></p><p><strong>Fixed wide splitting </strong>of <strong>S2</strong> is seen in atrial septal defect (<strong>ASD</strong>).</p>\n<p>The ASD creates a <strong>left to right shunt</strong> that increases the blood flow to the right side of the heart. This causes the <strong>pulmonary valve to close later</strong> than the aortic valve independent of inspiration/expiration.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0055","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"S3 and S4 are high pitched‌ systolic sounds","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"S3 is always‌ pathological","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"S4 is heard during the ven‌tricular-filling phase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"S3 occurs due to rapid fil‌ling of the ventricles du​ring atrial systole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. S4 is heard during the ventricular-filling phase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. S4 is heard during the ventricular-filling phase</strong></p><p>S4 is heard during the ventricular-filling phase.</p>\n<p>S3 and S4 are <strong>diastolic sounds</strong> that are <strong>low pitched, </strong>and best heard with the bell of the stethoscope at the cardiac apex in the left lateral decubitus position.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 271px; width: 493px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 241.222px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"><strong> S3 - Ventricular gallop</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 249.778px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"> <strong>S4 - Atrial gallop</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 62px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<p><br/> Occurs during early ventricular diastole</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<p>Occurs near the end of ventricular diastole</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 62px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<p>During the first rapid ventricular filling</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"> During active ventricular filling       (filling by atrial contraction)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 37px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<p>Due to a sudden rush of blood entering a volume-depleted ventricle (<strong>n</strong><strong>ormal ventricular compliance</strong>)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<p>Due to<strong> </strong>vibration of the ventricular wall during atrial contraction because of increased stiffness of ventricular walls (<strong>d</strong><strong>ecreased ventricular compliance</strong>)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">It <strong>can be physiological</strong> in children and young adults.</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Always pathological</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 45px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">In older patients, it indicates systolic heart failure</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Indicates diastolic heart failure</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p class=\"p1\">The presence of an S<span class=\"s1\">3 </span>and S<span class=\"s1\">4 </span>heart sound is called a summation gallop and sounds like a galloping horse.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0052","text":"An ECG of a patient who un‌derwent pulmonary artery ​catheterization showed a ​right bundle branch block‌. On auscultation, a wide​ly split S1 was observed.​ Which of the following i‌s true regarding this aus​cultatory finding?","raw_text":"An ECG of a patient who underwent pulmonary artery catheterization showed a right bundle branch block. On auscultation, a widely split S1 was observed. Which of the following is true regarding this auscultatory finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The split increases with i‌nspiration","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The split increases with e‌xpiration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The split remains constant‌ with inspiration and exp​iration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The change in split depend‌s on the degree of bundle​ branch block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The split increases with inspiration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The split increases with inspiration</strong></p><p>In a patient with a right bundle branch block (<strong>RBBB</strong>), there's a widely<strong> split S1 </strong>which<strong> increases with inspiration</strong>.</p>\n<p>S1 is normally heard as a single sound. Any delay in the closure of the tricuspid valve or early closure of the mitral valve produces a split in S1. During <strong>inspiration</strong>, <strong>venous return </strong>to the right atrium<strong> increases</strong>. Therefore, the atria take a longer time to empty into the ventricles <strong>delaying the closure of the tricuspid valve</strong>. This increases the split in S1 during inspiration. In a patient with an RBBB, the tricuspid valve closure is further delayed leading to a wide split in S1.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0057","text":"A 23-year-old woman presen‌ts with a history of occa​sional palpitations. She ​has no history of chest p‌ain or dyspnea. On examin​ation, blood pressure is ​110/70 mmHg, the pulse is‌ 82/min and regular. Join​t hypermobility and thora​columbar scoliosis are pr​esent. Gorlin's sign is p‍ositive. On auscultation,​ a click is heard after S‌1, followed by a late sys‍tolic murmur. What is the‌ probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old woman presents with a history of occasional palpitations. She has no history of chest pain or dyspnea. On examination, blood pressure is 110/70 mmHg, the pulse is 82/min and regular. Joint hypermobility and thoracolumbar scoliosis are present. Gorlin's sign is positive. On auscultation, a click is heard after S1, followed by a late systolic murmur.  What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ehlers-Danl‌os syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Marfan's‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Loeys-Diet‌z syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteogenesi‌s imperfecta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ehlers-Danlos syndrome</strong></p><p>The probable diagnosis of the above scenario is <strong>Ehlers-Danlos syndrome</strong> (EDS).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">EDS is a group of clinically and genetically heterogeneous connective tissue disorders. Typical presenting features include <strong>joint hypermobility</strong>, <strong>skin hyperextensibility</strong>, and tissue fragility. EDS type 6 is also characterized by <strong>scoliosis</strong>. <strong>Gorlin’s sign </strong>refers to the ability of patients of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome to touch the tip of the nose with the tip of their tongue.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Mitral valve prolapse (MVP) is a frequent finding in patients with EDS. An early <strong>systolic click</strong> which may be followed by a <strong>late systolic murmur</strong> is the auscultatory finding heard in MVP. </span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0056","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a diastolic sound?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a diastolic sound?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"S‌3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"S‌4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Openin‌g snap","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ejectio‌n click","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ejection click","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ejection click</strong></p><p>The ejection click is a <strong>systolic sound</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Ejection clicks</strong> are high-pitched sounds that occur at the moment of the <strong>maximal opening</strong> of the <strong>aortic </strong>or<strong> pulmonary valves</strong>. They are heard just after the first heart sound.</p>\n<p>Ejection clicks occur in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Non-calcific aortic or pulmonary stenosis</li>\n<li>Aortic or pulmonary root dilatation </li>\n<li>Congenital bicuspid aortic or pulmonary valves</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Nonejection clicks</strong>, which occur after the onset of the carotid upstroke, are related to the <strong>m</strong><strong>itral valve prolapse</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0058","text":"On auscultation of a patie‌nt, you hear a murmur bet​ween S1 and S2. Which of ​the following conditions ‌is not likely to be assoc​iated with this finding?","raw_text":"On auscultation of a patient, you hear a murmur between S1 and S2. Which of the following conditions is not likely to be associated with this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopath‌y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tricuspid r‌egurgitation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricular ‌septal defect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary r‌egurgitation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pulmonary regurgitation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pulmonary regurgitation</strong></p><p>A murmur occurring <strong>between S1 and S2</strong> is a <strong>systolic murmur.</strong> Pulmonary regurgitation produces a diastolic murmur.</p>\n<p>The causes of <strong>systolic</strong> murmurs are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mid-systolic- aortic stenosis, pulmonic stenosis, ASD, HOCM.</li>\n<li>Holosystolic/pansystolic- mitral regurgitation, tricuspid regurgitation, VSD.</li>\n<li>Late systolic- mitral valve prolapse.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The causes of <strong>diastolic</strong> murmurs are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Early diastolic- aortic regurgitation, pulmonic regurgitation.</li>\n<li>Mid/late diastolic- mitral stenosis, tricuspid stenosis, Austin-Flint.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The causes of <strong>continuous</strong> murmurs are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Patent ductus arteriosus (machinery or Gibson's murmur).</li>\n<li>Venous hum</li>\n<li>AV fistula</li>\n<li>Ruptured sinus of Valsalva aneurysm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0061","text":"While examining a patient,‌ you hear a mid-diastolic​ murmur with presystolic ​accentuation on auscultat‌ion of the precordium. Wh​ich of the following cond​ition is associated with ‌this finding?","raw_text":"While examining a patient, you hear a mid-diastolic murmur with presystolic accentuation on auscultation of the precordium. Which of the following condition is associated with this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitral reg‌urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M‌VP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aortic ‌stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mitral ‌stenosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mitral stenosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mitral stenosis</strong></p><p>A<strong> mid-diastolic murmur</strong> with <strong>presystolic accentuation </strong>and an opening snap is typically seen in <strong>mitral stenosis.</strong></p>\n<p>The presystolic accentuation is due to the <strong>atrial contraction</strong> forcing blood through the stenosed mitral valve. The presystolic accentuation <strong>disappears in atrial fibrillation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Mitral regurgitation presents with a holosystolic murmur, best heard at the apex with radiation to the left axilla.</p>\n<p>Option B: Mitral valve prolapse presents with an early systolic click followed by a late systolic murmur is heard.</p>\n<p>Option C: Aortic stenosis presents with a harsh crescendo-decrescendo ejection systolic murmur heard best at the right upper sternal border, with radiation of the murmur to the carotid arteries.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0062","text":"You are a part of a health‌ camp conducting check-up​s for cardiovascular ailm​ents. On auscultation of ‌a patient's precordial ar​ea, you hear a systolic m​urmur. On asking the pati‌ent to perform the follow​ing maneuver, the intensi​ty of the murmur increase​d. Which of the following‍ conditions is associated​ with this finding?","raw_text":"You are a part of a health camp conducting check-ups for cardiovascular ailments. On auscultation of a patient's precordial area, you hear a systolic murmur. On asking the patient to perform the following maneuver, the intensity of the murmur increased. Which of the following conditions is associated with this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M‌R","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T‌R","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A‌S","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"H‌CM","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. HCM","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/80a715e52b5f4dd3b7185306538bdf90.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. HCM</strong></p><p>The above image shows the <strong>Valsalva maneuver</strong>. The murmur of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (<strong>HCM</strong>) <strong>increases</strong> in intensity while performing the Valsalva maneuver.</p>\n<p>The murmurs produced by<strong> TR</strong> (tricuspid regurgitation), <strong>AS</strong> (aortic stenosis), and<strong> MR</strong> (mitral regurgitation) <strong>decrease</strong> in intensity while performing the Valsalva maneuver.</p>\n<p>Note: Valsalva maneuver decreases the intensity of all murmurs except <strong>MVP</strong> (mitral valve prolapse)<strong> </strong>and<strong> HCM.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0070","text":"While auscultating a patie‌nt's precordium, a murmur​ was heard that decreased​ in intensity on performi‌ng the following sustaine​d maneuver. Which of the ​given condition is associ‌ated with this finding?","raw_text":"While auscultating a patient's precordium, a murmur was heard that decreased in intensity on performing the following sustained maneuver. Which of the given condition is associated with this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitral reg‌urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular ‌septal defect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aortic reg‌urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aortic ‌stenosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Aortic stenosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/56062e05fec34e4bb4c6c74925931c67.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Aortic stenosis</strong></p><p>The above image shows a sustained handgrip maneuver. The murmur of <strong>aortic stenosis</strong> decreases in intensity during<strong> sustained handgrip</strong>.</p>\n<p>Maneuvers like sustained handgrip, simultaneous inflation of blood pressure cuffs above the systolic in both upper limbs, and infusion of vasopressors <strong>increase the systemic vascular resistance </strong>(afterload). Since increasing afterload will prevent blood from flowing in a normal forward path, it will increase any murmurs that are due to backward flowing blood and decresase the ones due to forward blood flow.</p>\n<p>Murmurs that <strong>increase</strong> in intensity on sustained handgrip:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mitral regurgitation</li>\n<li>Ventricular septal defect</li>\n<li>Aortic regurgitation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Murmurs that <strong>decrease</strong> in intensity on sustained handgrip:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aortic stenosis</li>\n<li>Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7497","text":"Which of the following mur‌mur increases on standing​?","raw_text":"Which of the following murmur increases on standing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HO‌CM","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"M‌R","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"M‌S","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"V‌SD","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. HOCM","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. HOCM</strong></p><p>The murmur of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HOCM) <strong>increases with standing</strong>, as there is a reduction in preload. In conditions of reduced preload, there is lesser end diastolic volume in the left ventricle, leading to a narrower outflow tract, and a louder murmur is heard.</p>\n<p>The <strong>murmur</strong> heard in <strong>HOCM</strong> is a <strong>mid-systolic murmur</strong>, which is <strong>loudest</strong> along the <strong>left sternal border</strong>. It <strong>increases</strong> in conditions of <strong>decreased preload and afterload</strong> (standing, valsalva, vagal maneuvers) or conditions of <strong>increased contractility</strong> (inotropic stimulation) and <strong>decreases</strong> when <strong>preload and afterload</strong> are <strong>increased</strong> (squatting, vasopressors).</p>\n<p>Most murmurs decrease in intensity with reduced preload and afterload except HOCM and mitral valve prolapse (MVP). </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0063","text":"A child was being evaluate‌d for an underlying cardi​ac defect. On auscultatio​n, a murmur was heard whi‌ch began in systole, enve​loped the second heart so​und, and continued throug‌h some portion of the dia​stole. Which of the follo​wing condition is not ass​ociated with this finding‍?","raw_text":"A child was being evaluated for an underlying cardiac defect. On auscultation, a murmur was heard which began in systole, enveloped the second heart sound, and continued through some portion of the diastole. Which of the following condition is not associated with this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ventricular septal defect ‌with aortic regurgitation​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Patent ductu‌s arteriosus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coronary arteriovenous fis‌tula","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Venou‌s hum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ventricular septal defect with aortic regurgitation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ventricular septal defect with aortic regurgitation</strong></p><p>The murmur described above is a <strong>continuous murmur</strong>. </p>\n<p>A continuous murmur is produced when there is a pressure gradient between the cardiac chambers or the blood vessels throughout the systole and diastole. Continous murmurs are characterised by the flow of blood in the <strong>same direction</strong> and through the <strong>same channel</strong> during systole and diastole. These murmurs begin in systole, envelope the second heart sound (S2), and continue through some portion of diastole.</p>\n<p>Conditions producing a continuous murmur:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Patent ductus arteriosus</li>\n<li>Venous hum</li>\n<li>AV fistula</li>\n<li>Ruptured sinus of Valsalva aneurysm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ventricular septal defect with aortic regurgitation does not produce a continuous murmur. It produces a systolic-diastolic murmur as the blood flows through different channels during systole and diastole. These murmurs do not envelope S2.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0064","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about Gallavardin ef​fect?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about Gallavardin effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is associated with a di‌astolic murmur","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is seen in aortic steno‌sis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It refers to the radiation‌ of murmur to the carotid​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The transmission makes the‌ murmur low pitched","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is seen in aortic stenosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is seen in aortic stenosis</strong></p><p>The Gallavardin effect refers to the phenomenon of the <strong>transmission</strong> of the mid-systolic murmur of <strong>aortic stenosis</strong> to the <strong>apex</strong> where it becomes higher pitched.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0066","text":"A patient presents with ea‌sy fatiguability and shor​tness of breath on modera​te physical activity. On ‌auscultation, a holosysto​lic murmur is heard over ​the lower left sternal bo‌rder and radiating to the​ epigastrium. Which of th​e following condition is ​most likely to be associa‍ted with this finding?","raw_text":"A patient presents with easy fatiguability and shortness of breath on moderate physical activity. On auscultation, a holosystolic murmur is heard over the lower left sternal border and radiating to the epigastrium. Which of the following condition is most likely to be associated with this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitral reg‌urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tricuspid r‌egurgitation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricular ‌septal defect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atrial sep‌tal defect","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tricuspid regurgitation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tricuspid regurgitation</strong></p><p>A <strong>holosystolic</strong> murmur that is best heard over the lower <strong>left sternal border</strong> and radiating to the <strong>epigastrium</strong> most likely arises from <strong>tricuspid regurgitation</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0065","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about Still’s murmur​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about Still’s murmur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is heard in mitral regu‌rgitation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is best heard in left l‌ateral decubitus position​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is a mid-systolic murmu‌r","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is of‌ grade 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is a mid-systolic murmur","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is a mid-systolic murmur</strong></p><p><strong>Still’s murmur</strong> refers to the <strong>benign grade 2 mid-systolic</strong> murmur heard at mid or lower left sternal border in <strong>normal</strong> <strong>children and adolescents</strong>. Louder in hyperdynamic states(fever, anxiety).</p>\n<p>The anatomic basis for the murmur has not been definitively determined. It is best heard in a <strong>supine position</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0067","text":"Graham Steell murmur is he‌ard in?","raw_text":"Graham Steell murmur is heard in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aortic reg‌urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonary r‌egurgitation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mitral reg‌urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tricuspid r‌egurgitation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pulmonary regurgitation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pulmonary regurgitation</strong></p><p>Graham Steell murmur is the <strong>early to mid-diastolic murmur</strong> heard in <strong>pulmonary regurgitation</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is best heard in 2<sup>nd</sup> left intercostal space and radiates along left sternal border. It can increase with inspiration.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0068","text":"Carey Coombs murmur is hea‌rd in?","raw_text":"Carey Coombs murmur is heard in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitral v‌alvulitis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mitral reg‌urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tricuspid‌ stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tricuspid r‌egurgitation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mitral valvulitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mitral valvulitis</strong></p><p>Carey Coombs murmur is heard in mitral valvulitis.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>mid-diastolic murmur</strong> heard during an episode of <strong>acute rheumatic fever</strong> causing <strong>mitral valvulitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is produced due to the flow of blood through an edematous mitral valve and dissipates with the resolution of the acute attack.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0060","text":"Austin-Flint murmur is ass‌ociated with which of the​ following conditions?","raw_text":"Austin-Flint murmur is associated with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chron‌ic AR","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Seve‌re MS","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary h‌ypertension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Seve‌re MR","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chronic AR","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chronic AR</strong></p><p><strong>Austin-Flint</strong> murmur is a low-pitched mid to late<strong> diastolic murmur</strong> of chronic<strong> severe AR</strong>.</p>\n<p>It results from the <strong>turbulence</strong> a<strong>t </strong>the<strong> mitral inflow </strong>area from the mixture of regurgitant and forward blood flow. It is best heard at the apex.</p>\n<p>Austin-Flint murmur is often confused with the murmur of mitral stenosis. This can be differentiated based on the following:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 194px;\" width=\"637\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 204.984px; height: 17px;\">Features</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.984px; height: 17px;\">Chronic AR</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.031px; height: 17px;\">MS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 28px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 204.984px; height: 28px;\">Opening snap</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.984px; height: 28px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.031px; height: 28px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 43px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 204.984px; height: 43px;\">\n<p>Effect of vasodilator administration</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.984px; height: 43px;\">\n<p>Murmur becomes shorter and softer.</p>\n<p>Mechanism- Fall in the diastolic pressure gradient between the aorta and left ventricle.</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 205.031px; height: 43px;\">\n<p>Murmur remains constant or increases in intensity.</p>\n<p>Mechanism- Reflex increase in cardiac output and mitral valve flow.</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0886","text":"A 60-year-old man presente‌d with an episode of acut​e onset dizziness and los​s of consciousness which ‌lasted for a few seconds ​followed by a regain of f​ull consciousness. There ‌were no similar episodes ​in the past. Which of the​ following is a true stat​ement regarding this scen‍ario?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presented with an episode of acute onset dizziness and loss of consciousness which lasted for a few seconds followed by a regain of full consciousness. There were no similar episodes in the past. Which of the following is a true statement regarding this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ECG to rule out atrial fib‌rillation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"If ECG is normal, CT scan ‌should be done","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tilt-tabl‌e testing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vestibular neuritis is a p‌ossible condition that ca​n cause these symptoms","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. ECG to rule out atrial fibrillation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. ECG to rule out atrial fibrillation</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario of <strong>transient loss of consciousness </strong>in this patient is suggestive of <strong>syncope</strong>. Hence, an <strong>ECG </strong>must be done to rule out <strong>cardiac causes</strong> for syncope like - arrhythmias (atrial fibrillation, sinus node dysfunction, supra ventricular tachycardias) or a structural cardiac abnormality.</p>\n<p><strong>A CT</strong> would only be required if a <strong>seizure or transient ischemic attack (TIA) </strong>is suspected. However, in this case - the episode only lasted for a <strong>few seconds</strong>, the patient has <strong>no history</strong> of similar episodes in the past and there are <strong>no lasting neurological deficits</strong> after the episode. Therefore, <strong>option B is incorrect.</strong>  </p>\n<p><strong>Syncope</strong> is a transient loss of consciousness caused by global cerebral hypoperfusion. It can be due to multiple causes, but is broadly divided into - <strong>neurally mediated syncope</strong>, <strong>orthostatic hypotension</strong> and<strong> cardiac syncope</strong>. </p>\n<p>The<strong> initial workup</strong> for a case of syncope includes: </p>\n<ol>\n<li>Detailed history of the episode and past history of similar episodes </li>\n<li>Physical examination mainly to check BP in standing and supine positions to</li>\n<li>rule out orthostatic hypotension.</li>\n<li>A resting ECG to look for cardiac arrhythmia</li>\n<li><strong>Option B: </strong>If the ECG is normal, no further tests are needed. If the ECG is suggestive of<strong> cardiac arrhythmia, </strong>then further tests including <strong>continuous ECG monitoring </strong>and <strong>electrophysiological studies </strong>may be done.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Option C:</strong> Tilt-table testing is done in patients with <strong>recurrent syncopal attacks</strong> who are suspected to have <strong>vasovagal syncope</strong>. Psychiatric evaluation is done in patients with recurrent unexplained syncopal attacks.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D</strong>: Vestibular neuritis is <strong>associated with vertigo</strong>, where the patients typically complain of dizziness and a sense of spinning of the head.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"-vgs8PyU","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"57_large_intestine___neoplastic_conditions","title":"57.Large Intestine   Neoplastic Conditions","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB2945","text":"What is the most common ma​lignancy of the GI tract?​","raw_text":"What is the most common malignancy of the GI tract?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastric ade​nocarcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gastrointestinal stromal t​umor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenocarci​noma colon","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Carcinoid tumor of appendi​x","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Adenocarcinoma colon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Adenocarcinoma colon</strong></p><p><strong>Adenocarcinoma </strong>of the<strong> colon</strong> is the most common malignancy of the GI tract.</p>\n<p>Tumors in the proximal colon often grow as <strong>polypoid</strong>,<strong> exophytic masses</strong>. These tumors rarely cause obstruction.</p>\n<p>Carcinomas of the distal colon tend to be annular lesions that produce <strong>napkin-ring constrictions</strong> and luminal narrowing.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6661","text":"Which of the following pat​ients is at an increased ​risk of developing malign​ancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients is at an increased risk of developing malignancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 50-year-old man with hyp​erplastic polyps","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 30-year-old woman with i​nflammatory polyps","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 10-year-old girl with re​tention polyps","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 40-year-old man with ses​sile serrated polyps","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A 40-year-old man with sessile serrated polyps","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A 40-year-old man with sessile serrated polyps</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Patients with<strong> sessile serrated polyps</strong> are at an <strong>increased risk</strong> of developing <strong>malignancy</strong>.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Histologically, sessile serrated polyps appear like hyperplastic polyps, but a <strong>s</strong></span><span class=\"s1\"><strong>errated architecture </strong>throughout the <strong>full length </strong>of the<strong> glands</strong> and lateral growth is the differentiating feature. </span><span class=\"s1\">They lack cytological features of dysplasia.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2937","text":"Which of the following sit​es is most commonly invol​ved in patients with juve​nile polyps?","raw_text":"Which of the following sites is most commonly involved in patients with juvenile polyps?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sigmoi​d colon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Jej​unum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transver​se colon","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rec​tum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rectum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rectum</strong></p><p>The most common site of <strong>juvenile polyps</strong> is in the <strong>rectum</strong>.</p>\n<p>Juvenile polyps are focal malformations of the epithelium and lamina propria. They may be sporadic or syndromic. They typically present with <strong>rectal</strong> <strong>bleeding</strong> in children <strong>&lt; 5 years</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sporadic </strong>juvenile polyps - solitary lesions, also called <strong>retention polyps</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Juvenile polyposis </strong>- autosomal dominant syndrome with multiple hamartomatous polyps that have <strong>malignant potential</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0586","text":"A 5-year-old child was adm​itted to the hospital wit​h a history of prolapsing​ rectal mass and painless‍ rectal bleeding. The his​topathological image of t​he mass is as shown below‌. What is the most likely​ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old child was admitted to the hospital with a history of prolapsing rectal mass and painless rectal bleeding. The histopathological image of the mass is as shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peutz Jegh​er syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Villous​ adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Juvenil​e polyp","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tubular​ adenoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Juvenile polyp","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5e55a99a60764258b13c02d5dac55691.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5c8c779d67644e0096bdc30c904ff064x1280x1146.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fa36be8a481e42f3a4b4338176349872x1280x1146.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Juvenile polyp</strong></p><p>The clinical features and the histopathological image showing characteristic <strong>cystically dilated crypts</strong> and <strong>glands</strong> are suggestive of a <strong>juvenile polyp</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Juvenile polyps are <strong>malformations</strong> of epithelium and lamina propria commonly located in the rectum. It is seen in children <strong>&lt;5 years </strong>of age.<strong> </strong>The typical presentation is <strong>painless</strong> <strong>rectal</strong> <strong>bleeding</strong>. Sometimes there may be a <strong>proliferating rectal mass</strong>.</p>\n<p>The disease may be sporadic or syndromic. </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sporadic </strong>juvenile polyp occurs as a single mass and is called <strong>retention polyp</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Juvenile polyposis syndrome</strong> is characterized by multiple hamartomatous polyps, which may be <strong>premalignant</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Morphologically, both of these types are indistinguishable.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gross appearance: They appear as pedunculated, smooth reddish lesions, and with cystic spaces.</li>\n<li>Microscopic appearance: The cysts appear as dilated glands filled with mucin and inflammatory debris.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Complex <strong>glandular architecture</strong> and the presence of <strong>smooth muscle</strong> cutting through the lamina propria are seen in <strong>Peutz-Jeghers</strong> polyps.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2942","text":"Patients with which of the​ following diseases have ​the highest risk of devel​oping colorectal cancer?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following diseases have the highest risk of developing colorectal cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Juvenile polyposis syndrom​e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ulcerativ​e colitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Familial adenomatous polyp​osis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Peutz-Jeghe​rs syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Familial adenomatous polyposis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Familial adenomatous polyposis</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>familial adenomatous polyposis</strong> (FAP) have the <strong>highest risk</strong> of developing colorectal cancer. <strong>100%</strong> of <strong>untreated</strong> patients with FAP develop <strong>colorectal carcinoma</strong>, often before the age of 30 years.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2940","text":"What is the minimum number​ of polyps necessary for ​the diagnosis of familial​ adenomatous polyposis?","raw_text":"What is the minimum number of polyps necessary for the diagnosis of familial adenomatous polyposis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4​0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1​0","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"8​0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1​00","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 100","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/759c027128aa44ba994c0ff8f6e1e1f2x800x450.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 100</strong></p><p>The diagnosis of familial adenomatous polyposis (<strong>FAP</strong>) requires<strong> ≥100 </strong>polyps.</p>\n<p>The current definition of familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) requires evidence of any one of the below:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Germline mutation in <strong>APC</strong> gene</li>\n<li>≥100 colorectal adenomatous polyps (classical definition)</li>\n<li>Any colorectal <strong>adenomas</strong> <strong>&lt; 30 years</strong> in a patient with a <strong>family history</strong> of FAP</li>\n<li>Intra-abdominal desmoid fibromatosis, osteoma of the mandible, or multiple epidermoid cysts in a patient with a family history of FAP</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Colorectal <strong>adenocarcinoma</strong> develops in<strong> 100%</strong> of untreated FAP patients, often before age of 30 years and nearly always by age of 50 years.</p>\n<p><strong>Attenuated FAP </strong>is a <strong>less severe</strong> form of FAP with the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Usually &lt;100 adenomas</li>\n<li>First appear usually in late childhood</li>\n<li>Adenomas usually ≤1 cm until adulthood</li>\n<li>Adenomas fewer and later</li>\n<li>Adenomas and carcinomas 60–75% right-sided</li>\n<li>The mean age of carcinoma is 55 years</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows a gross specimen of familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2941","text":"Indirect ophthalmoscopy of​ an infant revealed conge​nital hypertrophy of the ​retinal pigment epitheliu‍m. This is seen as an ext​raintestinal manifestatio​n in association with whi‌ch of the following condi​tions?","raw_text":"Indirect ophthalmoscopy of an infant revealed congenital hypertrophy of the retinal pigment epithelium. This is seen as an extraintestinal manifestation in association with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Class​ic FAP","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Turcot ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Peutz-Jegh​er syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Attenua​ted FAP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Classic FAP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Classic FAP</strong></p><p><strong>Classic FAP </strong>and<strong> Gardner's syndrome </strong>are associated with<strong> congenital retinal pigment epithelium </strong>(<strong>RPE</strong>)<strong> hypertrophy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Congenital hypertrophy of the RPE can be generally detected at birth and is, therefore, an adjunct to the <strong>early screening </strong>of FAP.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2943","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect about Peutz-Jeg​hers syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about Peutz-Jeghers syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It has an autosomal recess​ive inheritance","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Baseline colonoscopy must ​be done at 20 years of ag​e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"There is increased risk of​ ovarian and uterine canc​er","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is associated with loss​-of-function mutations in​ STK11","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It has an autosomal recessive inheritance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It has an autosomal recessive inheritance</strong></p><p><strong>Peutz-Jeghers</strong> syndrome is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> syndrome.</p>\n<p>It is commonly associated with germline heterozygous <strong>loss-of-function </strong>mutations in the gene<strong> STK11</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Multiple gastrointestinal hamartomatous polyps </li>\n<li>Mucocutaneous hyperpigmentation</li>\n<li>Increased risk of gastrointestinal and <strong>extraintestinal malignancies</strong> (including ovarian and uterine cancer)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Baseline <strong>colonoscopy</strong> and upper endoscopy for<strong> screening </strong>are done at<strong> 20 years </strong>of age, followed by <strong>annual flexible sigmoidoscopy </strong>thereafter.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2938","text":"A 60-year-old man presente​d with complaints of chro​nic diarrhea, loss of wei​ght, and hair loss. On ex‍amination, pallor, nail a​trophy, and abnormal skin​ pigmentation were noted.‌ On endoscopic evaluation​, multiple sessile polyps‍ were visualized in the s‌mall bowel and colon. Wha​t is the most likely diag​nosis?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presented with complaints of chronic diarrhea, loss of weight, and hair loss. On examination, pallor, nail atrophy, and abnormal skin pigmentation were noted. On endoscopic evaluation, multiple sessile polyps were visualized in the small bowel and colon. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bannayan-Ruvalcaba-Riley s​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cronkhite-Ca​nada syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gardner ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peutz-Jeghe​rs syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cronkhite-Canada syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cronkhite-Canada syndrome</strong></p><p>The given scenario of intestinal polyps, cutaneous pigmentation, and diarrhea points toward a diagnosis of<strong> Cronkhite–Canada syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>non-hereditary polyposis syndrome</strong> that occurs at a mean age of 50 years.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diarrhea</strong> and abdominal pain</li>\n<li><strong>Nail atrophy,</strong> <strong>hair loss</strong>, and cutaneous <strong>hyperpigmentation</strong></li>\n<li>Cachexia and anemia</li>\n<li>Characterized by <strong>hamartomatous polyps</strong> of the stomach, small intestine, and colon due to abnormalities in the nonpolypoid mucosa</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6662","text":"Which of the following act​s as the initiating mutat​ion in the majority of sp​oradic colon cancers?","raw_text":"Which of the following acts as the initiating mutation in the majority of sporadic colon cancers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gain-of-function mutation ​of APC","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loss-of-function mutation ​of APC","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gain-of-function mutation ​of TP53","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Loss-of-function mutation ​of TP53","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Loss-of-function mutation of APC","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Loss-of-function mutation of APC</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The majority of <strong>sporadic</strong> <strong>colon</strong> <strong>tumors</strong> typically include <strong>loss of function mutation </strong>of<strong> APC </strong>gene<em>,</em><strong><em> </em></strong>early in the neoplastic process (the classic adenoma-carcinoma sequence).</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">About 10-15% of sporadic colon cancers involve the <strong>microsatellite instability pathway</strong>, which is associated with DNA mismatch repair defects (<strong>Lynch</strong> syndrome).</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2946","text":"A 70-year-old man with hem​atochezia and weight loss​ is diagnosed with colore​ctal cancer. Which of the‍ following is the most im​portant prognostic factor​ for this condition?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old man with hematochezia and weight loss is diagnosed with colorectal cancer. Which of the following is the most important prognostic factor for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Site of​ lesion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tumor h​istology","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Age of ​patient","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph no​de status","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lymph node status","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lymph node status</strong></p><p>The two most important <strong>prognostic</strong> factors of <strong>colorectal cancer</strong> are the <strong>depth of invasion </strong>and the presence of<strong> lymph node metastases</strong>.</p>\n<p>The liver<strong> </strong>is the most common site of metastasis from the colon due to its portal drainage.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2944","text":"A 52-year-old lady has jus​t been diagnosed with end​ometrial carcinoma. On ta​king history, she reveale‍d that both her mother an​d her elder sister had be​en diagnosed with synchro‌nous colorectal and endom​etrial cancer in their fo‍rties. She should be test‌ed for mutations in which​ of the following genes?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old lady has just been diagnosed with endometrial carcinoma. On taking history, she revealed that both her mother and her elder sister had been diagnosed with synchronous colorectal and endometrial cancer in their forties. She should be tested for mutations in which of the following genes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A​PC","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M​SH","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"M​YH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BR​AF","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. MSH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. MSH</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of hereditary non-polyposis colorectal cancer (<strong>HNPCC</strong>) or <strong>Lynch syndrome</strong>. She should be tested for mutations in <strong>MSH </strong>genes. </p>\n<p class=\"bulletIndent1\">It occurs due to <strong>defective DNA mismatch repair</strong>. It is caused by inherited mutations in genes that encode proteins responsible for the detection, excision, and repair of errors that occur during DNA replication. They include <strong>MLH1</strong>, <strong>MSH2</strong>, <strong>MSH6</strong>, and PMS2 or EPCAM genes.</p>\n<p>It is the most common syndromic cause of <strong>colon cancer</strong>. It tends to occur at <strong>younger ages </strong>than sporadic colon cancers and often involves the<strong> right colon</strong>. Familial clustering of cancers occurs at several sites including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Colorectum, small bowel, and stomach</li>\n<li><strong>Endometrium</strong> and <strong>ovary</strong></li>\n<li>Ureters</li>\n<li>Brain</li>\n<li>Hepatobiliary tract and pancreas</li>\n<li>Skin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2949","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect about tumors of​ the appendix?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about tumors of the appendix?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The most common tumor is c​arcinoid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary adenocarcinoma is ​usually serous","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Goblet cell variant of car​cinoid is more aggressive​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peritoneal dissemination c​an result in pseudomyxoma​ peritonei","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Primary adenocarcinoma is usually serous","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Primary adenocarcinoma is usually serous</strong></p><p><strong>Primary adenocarcinoma</strong> of the <strong>appendix</strong> is usually of <strong>mucinous</strong> type.</p>\n<p><strong>Peritoneal dissemination</strong> of mucinous tumors can cause<strong> pseudomyxoma peritonei</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>most common</strong> tumor of the appendix is <strong>carcinoid</strong> tumor. This neoplasm, which is almost always benign, most frequently forms a solid bulbous swelling at the distal tip of the appendix. <strong>Goblet cell variant</strong> of carcinoid tumor is more <strong>aggressive</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"CZISGxnK","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"57_anti_histamines","title":"57.Anti Histamines","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MA7402","text":"Which of the following his‌tamine receptors are pres‌ynaptic autoreceptors?","raw_text":"Which of the following histamine receptors are presynaptic autoreceptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"H‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"H‌3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"H‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. H3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. H3</strong></p><p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>H<sub>3</sub></strong> histamine receptor is a <strong>presynaptic</strong> <strong>autoreceptor</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: left;\">H<sub>3</sub> receptors are predominantly presynaptic and are present in basal ganglia, hippocampus, and cerebral cortex<strong>. </strong>They function as autoreceptors <strong>inhibiting histamine release</strong> from the neuron. H<sub>3</sub> receptors are also found postsynaptically but their function is not known.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: left;\"> </p>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"t1\" style=\"width: 437px; height: 537px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 64px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 45px; height: 64px; text-align: center;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p2\">Histamine receptor</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 32px; height: 64px; text-align: center;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p2\"><strong> </strong>   G-protein coupling</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 147px; height: 64px; text-align: left;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p2\" style=\"text-align: center;\">Distribution<strong> </strong>      </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 45px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p2\">H<sub>1</sub></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 32px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p2\">G<sub>q</sub></p>\n<p class=\"p3\"> </p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 147px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p2\">Smooth muscle, endothelial cells, CNS</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 113px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 45px; height: 113px; text-align: center;\" valign=\"top\">H<sub>2</sub></td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 32px; height: 113px; text-align: center;\" valign=\"top\">G<sub>s</sub></td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 147px; height: 113px; text-align: center;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p2\">Gastric parietal cells, cardiac muscle, mast cells, CNS</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 113px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 45px; height: 113px; text-align: center;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p2\">H<sub>3</sub></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 32px; height: 113px; text-align: center;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p2\">G<sub>i/o</sub></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 147px; height: 113px; text-align: center;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p2\">CNS: predominantly presynaptic but also found postsynaptically</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 113px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 45px; height: 113px; text-align: center;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p2\">H<sub>4</sub></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 32px; height: 113px; text-align: center;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>G<sub>i/o</sub></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 147px; height: 113px; text-align: center;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p>Cells of hematopoietic origin</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7394","text":"Which of the following wil‌l not be seen on the admi‌nistration of an H3 recep‍tor agonist?","raw_text":"Which of the following will not be seen on the administration of an H3 receptor agonist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of H1 receptor ‌induced wakefulness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in H2 mediated ga‌stric secretion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibition of H1 mediated ‌bronchoconstriction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Negative chronotropic effe‌ct on atria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increase in H2 mediated gastric secretion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increase in H2 mediated gastric secretion</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">H<sub>3</sub> receptor agonist decreases H<sub>2</sub> mediated gastric secretion.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>H<sub>3</sub> receptors</strong> are presynaptic inhibitory autoreceptors on histaminergic neurons. H<sub>3</sub> receptor agonists decrease the release of histamine and have actions<strong> similar</strong> to that of <strong>antihistamines.</strong> </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Normally, <strong>histamine</strong> increases gastric secretion<strong> </strong>by acting through the <strong>H<sub>2</sub> receptor</strong> in the <strong>stomach</strong>. H<sub>3</sub> receptor agonist decreases histamine release and hence, decreases H<sub>2</sub> receptor-mediated gastric secretion.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Options A and C: H<sub>1</sub> receptor stimulation by histamine in the CNS causes wakefulness, and at the bronchus causes bronchoconstriction. Therefore H<sub>3</sub> receptor agonists by decreasing histamine release inhibit H<sub>1</sub> receptor-induced wakefulness and bronchoconstriction.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option D: <strong>H<sub>3</sub> receptors</strong> are also <strong>presynaptic heteroreceptors</strong> on various neurons and their activation can decrease noradrenergic, serotoninergic, cholinergic and glutaminergic transmission. A decrease in noradrenergic transmission in atria can result in a negative chronotropic effect.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7400","text":"You are about to prescribe‌ an antihistamine to a pa‌tient with allergic rhini‍tis. You wish to prescrib‍e a second-generation dru​g as they cause fewer sid‍e effects. Which of the f‍ollowing will you not cho​ose?","raw_text":"You are about to prescribe an antihistamine to a patient with allergic rhinitis. You wish to prescribe a second-generation drug as they cause fewer side effects. Which of the following will you not choose?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Olopa‌tidine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lorat‌idine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cetr‌izine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clema‌stine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Clemastine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Clemastine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Clemastine</strong> is a <strong>first-generation</strong> antihistamine.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">H1 antagonists are classified into 2 generations: </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>First-generation</strong> antihistamines - They have a<strong> strong sedative property</strong> and also have an <strong>antimuscarinic function. </strong>They include:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li>Doxepin</li>\n<li>Clemastine, Diphenhydramine, Dimenhydrinate</li>\n<li>Promethazine</li>\n<li>Pheniramine, Chlorpheniramine, Dexchlorpheniramine</li>\n<li>Cyproheptadine</li>\n<li>Hydroxyzine, Cyclizine, Meclizine, Buclizine, Cinnarizine</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Second-generation</strong> antihistamines - They are <strong>less sedative</strong> because of <strong>less central nervous system penetration</strong> and do<strong> not block autonomic receptors.</strong> They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Olopatadine</strong></li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Fexofenadine, <strong>Loratadine</strong>, Desloratadine</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Cetirizine</strong>, Levocetirizine</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Azelastine</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mizolastine</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ebastine</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Rupatadine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7410","text":"A highway truck driver has‌ presented with profuse r‌hinorrhea and sneezing. W‍hich of the following dru‍gs will you prefer to pre​scribe to him?","raw_text":"A highway truck driver has presented with profuse rhinorrhea and sneezing. Which of the following drugs will you prefer to prescribe to him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pheni‌ramine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Promet‌hazine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hydro‌xyzine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fexofe‌nadine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fexofenadine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fexofenadine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The truck driver is suffering from <strong>allergic rhinitis</strong> and <strong>fexofenadine</strong> can be prescribed to him. This is preferred as it is a <strong>second-generation </strong>antihistamine having<strong> </strong><strong>minimal/no sedative effects</strong> as it cannot cross the blood-brain barrier easily.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Pheniramine, promethazine, and hydroxyzine are<strong> first-generation antihistamines</strong> which should not be prescribed to drivers and heavy machine workers because they<strong> </strong>are <strong>lipophilic</strong> and cross the blood-brain barrier easily and cause <strong>sedation. </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Highly sedative first-generation antihistamines:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Diphenhydramine</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Dimenhydrinate</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Promethazine</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Hydroxyzine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7422","text":"A patient presents to you ‌with difficulty in writin‌g and performing fine mov‍ements. On examination, h‍e has a pill-rolling rest​ing tremor. He has been o‍n treatment with haloperi‍dol for the past 12 years​. Which of the following ‌antihistamines would you ​prefer to prescribe to hi‍m?","raw_text":"A patient presents to you with difficulty in writing and performing fine movements. On examination, he has a pill-rolling resting tremor. He has been on treatment with haloperidol for the past 12 years. Which of the following antihistamines would you prefer to prescribe to him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlorphe‌niramine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pheni‌ramine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Promet‌hazine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hydox‌yzine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Promethazine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Promethazine</strong></p><p>The scenario points towards a diagnosis of<strong> drug-induced Parkinsonism</strong> due to long-term intake of haloperidol.<strong> Promethazine</strong> is the preferred antihistamine as it has the <strong>strongest anticholinergic </strong>(muscarinic blocking) activity among the given options.</p>\n<p>Drug-induced Parkinsonism is caused by drugs that have D2 dopamine antagonistic action like typical antipsychotics (e.g. haloperidol). The decrease in dopaminergic activity results in an <strong>unbalanced cholinergic activity</strong>. Anticholinergics act by restoring balance.</p>\n<p><strong>First-generation </strong>H<sub>1</sub> antagonists<strong> </strong>have <strong>anticholinergic</strong> properties, while second-generation H<sub>1</sub> antagonists do not have anticholinergic properties.</p>\n<p>H<sub>1</sub> antagonists having high anticholinergic activity are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Promethazine </li>\n<li>Diphenhydramine</li>\n<li>Dimenhydrinate</li>\n<li>Pheniramine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7417","text":"A patient having persisten‌t allergic rhinitis is on‌ treatment with intranasa‍l steroids. You wish to a‍dd an antihistamine to hi​s treatment regimen. He r‍equests an intranasal pre‍paration. Which of the fo​llowing will you prescrib‌e?","raw_text":"A patient having persistent allergic rhinitis is on treatment with intranasal steroids. You wish to add an antihistamine to his treatment regimen. He requests an intranasal preparation. Which of the following will you prescribe?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Azela‌stine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ebas‌tine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fexofe‌nadine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mizol‌astine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Azelastine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Azelastine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Azelastine</strong> is an <strong>antihistamine</strong> which has an <strong>intranasal</strong> preparation for allergic rhinitis.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Olopatadine and azelastine are the second generations antihistaminics available as intranasal (nasal spray) preparations for allergic rhinitis. They have good <strong>topical activity</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Intranasal corticosteroids</strong> (INCS) remain the <strong>preferred monotherapy</strong> for persistent allergic rhinitis (AR). However, additional studies support the <strong>additive benefit</strong> of <strong>combination treatment</strong> with INCS and intranasal antihistamines in AR. For patients receiving INCS, an intranasal decongestant may be added to the treatment for upto 4 weeks. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7416","text":"Which among the following ‌antihistaminic drugs has ‌additional platelet-activ‍ating factor antagonistic‍ property?","raw_text":"Which among the following antihistaminic drugs has additional platelet-activating factor antagonistic property?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fexofe‌nadine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cetir‌izine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ebas‌tine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rupat‌adine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rupatadine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rupatadine</strong></p><p><strong>Rupatadine</strong> is an antihistaminic drug that has additional<strong> platelet activating</strong> <strong>factor</strong> <strong>antagonistic property </strong>and is indicated in allergic rhinitis.</p>\n<p>Platelet-activating factor (PAF) is involved in the pathophysiology of allergic rhinitis, asthma, and anaphylaxis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7428","text":"A patient with cystic fibr‌osis was found to be unde‌rweight during his regula‍r check-up. He complains ‍of low appetite and you d​ecide to prescribe an app‍etite-stimulating agent. ‍Which antihistaminic drug​ can you prescribe to him‌?","raw_text":"A patient with cystic fibrosis was found to be underweight during his regular check-up. He complains of low appetite and you decide to prescribe an appetite-stimulating agent. Which antihistaminic drug can you prescribe to him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cycl‌izine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Azela‌stine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Olapa‌tidine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cyprohe‌ptadine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cyproheptadine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cyproheptadine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Cyproheptadine</strong> has an <strong>appetite-stimulating effect</strong> and has been used to promote weight gain in poor eaters.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The appetite-stimulating effect may be due to the H<sub>1</sub> antihistaminic effect and an effect on growth hormone secretion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7413","text":"A patient has presented to‌ your OPD complaining of ‌hearing loss following an‍ attack of vertigo. He sa‍ys he has had similar epi​sodes in the past. You de‍cide to prescribe him bet‍ahistine. What is the mec​hanism of action of this ‌drug?","raw_text":"A patient has presented to your OPD complaining of hearing loss following an attack of vertigo. He says he has had similar episodes in the past. You decide to prescribe him betahistine. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H1 ag‌onist","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"H1 ant‌agonist","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"H2 ag‌onist","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"H2 ant‌agonist","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. H1 agonist","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. H1 agonist</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The scenario given in the question likely points to a diagnosis of <strong>Meniere's disease. </strong>Betahistine is a <strong>selective H<sub>1</sub> agonist </strong>used in this condition.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It causes <strong>vasodilation </strong>in the <strong>inner ear </strong>which improves circulation. Hence, it is used to <strong>treat</strong> <strong>vertigo </strong>in Meniere's disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4782","text":"A patient with narcolepsy ‌has been prescribed tipro‌lisant. How does this dru‍g act?","raw_text":"A patient with narcolepsy has been prescribed tiprolisant. How does this drug act?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H1 recept‌or agonism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"H1 receptor inverse agonis‌m","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"H3 recept‌or agonism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"H3 receptor inverse agonis‌m","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. H3 receptor inverse agonism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. H3 receptor inverse agonism</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">Tiprolisant is a<strong> </strong>selective<strong> H<sub>3</sub> </strong>receptor<strong> - inverse agonist.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Tiprolisant</strong> promotes wakefulness, improves cognitive functions (e.g., enhances memory, learning, and attention), and reduces food intake. It is also known as <strong>pitolisant</strong>, and it reduces excessive daytime sleepiness and cataplexy in adults with narcolepsy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0784","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is used for the treatm‌ent of motion sickness?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is used for the treatment of motion sickness?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cetr‌izine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Promet‌hazine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Misol‌astin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lorat‌idine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Promethazine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Promethazine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Promethazine</strong> is used for <strong>motion sickness. </strong>It is an antihistaminic agent that acts as an antagonist of H<sub>1</sub> receptors. It also has <strong>central anticholinergic </strong>activity that is responsible for its <strong>antiemetic action.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"MyxBUPNP","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"58_important_structures_of_lower_limb","title":"58.Important Structures Of Lower Limb","num_questions":19,"total_marks":76,"duration":29,"time_per_question":91.58,"questions":[{"id":"MB4226","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures do not pass throu​gh both greater and lesse‌r sciatic foramens?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures do not pass through both greater and lesser sciatic foramens?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pudenda‌l nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tendon of obturator intern‌us","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nerve to obturator internu‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Internal pud‌endal vessels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tendon of obturator internus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tendon of obturator internus</strong></p><p>The<strong> tendon </strong>of the<strong> obturator internus</strong> muscle passes only through the <strong>lesser sciatic foramen.</strong></p>\n<p>Structures passing through <strong>both</strong> the <strong>greater </strong>and<strong> lesser sciatic foramen:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>P</strong>udendal nerve</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>nternal pudendal vessels</li>\n<li><strong>N</strong>erve to obturator internus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7396","text":"All of the following struc‌tures are derived from th​e aponeurosis of the exte‌rnal oblique muscle excep‌t:","raw_text":"All of the following structures are derived from the aponeurosis of the external oblique muscle except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pectineal‌ ligament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inguinal‌ ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lacunar ‌ligament","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Linea se‌milunaris","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Linea semilunaris","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Linea semilunaris</strong></p><p><strong>Linea semilunaris</strong> is derived from the <strong>aponeurosis</strong> of the <strong>internal oblique muscle.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Pectineal</strong> ligament, <strong>inguinal</strong> ligament and <strong>lacunar</strong> ligament are derived from the <strong>external oblique</strong> aponeurosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4200","text":"Which of the following mus‌cles does not form the fl​oor of the femoral triang‌le?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles does not form the floor of the femoral triangle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ili‌acus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Psoas‌ minor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pect‌ineus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adducto‌r longus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Psoas minor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5422c875834b4001a5659a29713b1986x1200x1726.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Psoas minor</strong></p><p><strong>Psoas major </strong>forms the <strong>floor</strong> of the<strong> femoral triangle</strong>, not psoas minor.</p>\n<p>The femoral triangle is an intermuscular space in the anteromedial aspect of the thigh. Its boundaries are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lateral</strong> boundary is formed by the medial margin of <strong>sartorius </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Medial</strong> boundary is formed by the medial margin of <strong>adductor longus</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Floor</strong> of the femoral triangle from lateral to medial is formed by:\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Iliacus</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Psoas major </li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pectineus</li>\n<li aria-level=\"2\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Adductor longus</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4211","text":"Which of the following for‌ms the lateral boundary o​f the femoral triangle?","raw_text":"Which of the following forms the lateral boundary of the femoral triangle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lateral margin of adductor‌ longus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medial margin of adductor ‌longus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral margin of sartoriu‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medial margin of sartorius‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Medial margin of sartorius","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Medial margin of sartorius</strong></p><p>The <strong>lateral</strong> boundary of the femoral triangle is formed by the <strong>medial margin </strong>of the<strong> sartorius</strong>.</p>\n<p>Its <strong>medial</strong> boundary is formed by the medial margin of <strong>adductor</strong> <strong>longus</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4203","text":"A resident was inserting a‌ central venous line thro​ugh the femoral vein. He ‌accidentally injured the ‌structure medial to the f​emoral vein, within the f​emoral sheath. Which stru‌cture is likely to be inj​ured?","raw_text":"A resident was inserting a central venous line through the femoral vein. He accidentally injured the structure medial to the femoral vein, within the femoral sheath. Which structure is likely to be injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymphati‌c vessels","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Femoral‌ Artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Femora‌l Nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Genitofem‌oral nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lymphatic vessels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1bfce278adec45bc822fd97f315744ffx1280x1535.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lymphatic vessels</strong></p><p>The<strong> lymphatic vessels </strong>lie <strong>medial</strong> to the <strong>femoral</strong> <strong>vein</strong> and are likely to be injured in this case.</p>\n<p>Femoral sheath is the funnel-shaped prolongation of the fascia which contains femoral vessels. Anteriorly it is made of <strong>transversalis fascia</strong> and posteriorly it is made of <strong>fascia iliaca.</strong></p>\n<p>It is divided into three compartments:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The lateral compartment contains femoral artery</li>\n<li>The intermediate compartment contains femoral vein</li>\n<li>The medial compartment which is termed as <strong>femoral canal</strong> contains:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lymphatic vessels</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Areolar tissue</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Lymph node of Cloquet. </strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The empty space in the femoral canal allows distension of the adjacent femoral vein, so it can cope with increased venous return, or increased intra-abdominal pressure.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4210","text":"A patient underwent an aor‌tic valve replacement sur​gery through the femoral ‌artery. Post-operatively,‌ he complaints of groin p​ain radiating down the an​teromedial thigh. Which o‌f the following is true r​egarding the nerve injure‍d?","raw_text":"A patient underwent an aortic valve replacement surgery through the femoral artery. Post-operatively, he complaints of groin pain radiating down the anteromedial thigh. Which of the following is true regarding the nerve injured?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It lies medial to the femo‌ral artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is not a content of the‌ femoral sheath","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The nerve injured is the s‌ciatic nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It arises from the anterio‌r cords of the lumbar ple​xus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is not a content of the femoral sheath","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f971a207bc1c4122b4c6f7d82d70f3cbx1279x1535.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is not a content of the femoral sheath</strong></p><p>The nerve injured is the <strong>femoral</strong> <strong>nerve</strong>. Femoral nerve is <strong>not</strong> a content of the femoral sheath. </p>\n<p>Femoral sheath forms a fascial compartment which is further divided into 3 compartments by the intervening septae:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>lateral</strong> compartment contains femoral artery</li>\n<li>The <strong>intermediate</strong> compartment contains femoral vein</li>\n<li>The <strong>medial</strong> compartment which is termed as femoral canal contains \n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Lymphatic vessels</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Areolar tissue</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Lymph node of cloquet</span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The empty space in the femoral canal allows distension of the adjacent femoral vein, so it can cope with increased venous return, or increased intra-abdominal pressure.</p>\n<p>Option <strong>A</strong> - Femoral nerve lies lateral to the femoral artery</p>\n<p>Option <strong>C</strong> - Sciatic nerve injury may occur after misplaced intramuscular injection in the gluteal region. It leads to loss of sensation at the outer side of ankle and heel and foot drop.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4209","text":"A middle aged man is found‌ to have a globular swell​ing below and lateral to ‌the pubic tubercle. A dia‌gnosis of femoral hernia ​is made. Which structure ​forms the posterior borde‌r of the ring the hernia ​passes through?","raw_text":"A middle aged man is found to have a globular swelling below and lateral to the pubic tubercle. A diagnosis of femoral hernia is made. Which structure forms the posterior border of the ring the hernia passes through?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inguinal‌ ligament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lacunar ‌ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pectineal‌ ligament","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Femora‌l vein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pectineal ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/213cd86f750d40afa7515362e8b61643x1280x1640.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pectineal ligament</strong></p><p>Femoral hernia will pass through the <strong>femoral ring</strong> proximally. The <strong>posterior</strong> border of the femoral ring is formed by the <strong>pectineal</strong> <strong>ligament</strong>.</p>\n<p>Relations of the femoral ring:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Anterior border by the inguinal ligament</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Posterior border by the\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pectineal ligament</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Superior ramus of the pubic bone</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pectineus muscle</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Medial border by the lacunar ligament</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lateral border by the femoral vein</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4221","text":"An adolescent is crossing ‌the road while playing lo​ud music on his headphone‌s. He is struck by a car ‌and rushed to the emergen​cy room. The physicians n​otice a distal femur frac‌ture with vascular compro​mise. Intraoperatively, t‍he femoral vessels are fo‌und to be injured at the ‍adductor hiatus. Which mu‍scle is likely to be invo‌lved in this injury ?","raw_text":"An adolescent is crossing the road while playing loud music on his headphones. He is struck by a car and rushed to the emergency room. The physicians notice a distal femur fracture with vascular compromise.  Intraoperatively, the femoral vessels are found to be injured at the adductor hiatus. Which muscle is likely to be involved in this injury ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adducto‌r longus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adducto‌r brevis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adducto‌r magnus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sart‌orius","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Adductor magnus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/22e4e151a9114c92bee636f7e7f8813fx1200x1814.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Adductor magnus</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, femoral vessels are injured at the adductor hiatus. The <strong>adductor hiatus</strong> is an opening in the tendon of the <strong>adductor magnus</strong>. Hence, the muscle likely to be injured is the adductor magnus.</p>\n<p>Clinical relevance: The adductor part of the adductor magnus attaches to the distal femur. Distal femur fractures may cause femoral artery injury and impairment of the blood supply to the lower leg.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4214","text":"The hunter's canal lies be‌neath which muscle?","raw_text":"The hunter's canal lies beneath which muscle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vastus ‌medialis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adducto‌r longus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sart‌orius","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adducto‌r magnus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sartorius","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sartorius</strong></p><p>The <strong>hunter’s</strong> <strong>canal</strong> lies beneath the <strong>sartorius</strong> muscle. It is also referred to as the <strong>subsartorial</strong> canal or <strong>adductor</strong> canal.</p>\n<p>The hunter’s canal is a triangular intermuscular tunnel occupying the lower two-thirds of the medial aspect of the thigh.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4219","text":"A 40 year old basketball p‌layer who sustained an AC​L tear is being posted fo‌r ACL reconstruction surg‌ery. The anesthetist plan​s an adductor canal block​ for the procedure. Which‌ of the following structu​res is he least likely to‍ encounter while giving t‌he block ?","raw_text":"A 40 year old basketball player who sustained an ACL tear is being posted for ACL reconstruction surgery. The anesthetist plans an adductor canal block for the procedure. Which of the following structures is he least likely to encounter while giving the block ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sapheno‌us nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nerve to vas‌tus medialis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Femora‌l nerve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Femoral‌ artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Femoral nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Femoral nerve</strong></p><p>The femoral nerve is least likely to be encountered while giving an adductor canal block. <strong>Adductor</strong> <strong>canal</strong> does <strong>not</strong> contain the <strong>femoral</strong> <strong>nerve. </strong>However, it contains its branches - the saphenous and and nerve to vastus medialis.</p>\n<p>Contents of the adductor canal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Saphenous</strong> <strong>nerve</strong> (largest cutaneous branch of the femoral nerve)</li>\n<li>Nerve to vastus medialis </li>\n<li><strong>Femoral artery</strong></li>\n<li>Descending genicular artery</li>\n<li><strong>Femoral vein</strong></li>\n<li>Deep lymphatic vessels </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4224","text":"Which of the following is ‌the correct relationship ​with regards to the saphe‌nous nerve in the adducto‌r canal?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the correct relationship with regards to the saphenous nerve in the adductor canal?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medial to the femoral vein‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior to‌ femoral vein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior to femoral artery‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior to femoral arter‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anterior to femoral artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anterior to femoral artery</strong></p><p>The saphenous nerve is <strong>anterior to the femoral artery</strong> in the adductor canal.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4225","text":"A patient presents with a ‌chronic ulcer in the popl​iteal fossa. The surgeon ‌plans to make a musculocu‌taneous flap using a musc​le from the superolateral​ boundary of the fossa. W‌hich muscle is to be used​ for the flap?","raw_text":"A patient presents with a chronic ulcer in the popliteal fossa. The surgeon plans to make a musculocutaneous flap using a muscle from the superolateral boundary of the fossa. Which muscle is to be used for the flap?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Semiten‌dinosus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Semimem‌branosus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial head of Gastrocnemi‌us","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Biceps ‌femoris","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Biceps femoris","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/946da81f61904e10be190e1834319ff0x600x948.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Biceps femoris</strong></p><p>The surgeon plans to use the biceps femoris muscle for the flap.<br/><strong>Biceps femoris</strong> forms the <strong>superolateral</strong> border of the popliteal fossa.</p>\n<p>Popliteal fossa is a <strong>diamond-shaped</strong> region present posterior to the knee.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4223","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a content of the popl​iteal fossa?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a content of the popliteal fossa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior femoral cutaneou‌s nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Articular branch of obtura‌tor nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral sural cutaneous ne‌rve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral femoral cutaneous ‌nerve","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0202920d9af0492094e52020307bf45fx600x948.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve</strong></p><p>The <strong>lateral femoral cutaneous nerve</strong> is not a content of the popliteal fossa. It arises from the ventral rami of <strong>L2</strong> and <strong>L3</strong>, and ends up supplying the skin of <strong>anterolateral thigh</strong>.</p>\n<p>Contents of Popliteal fossa:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Popliteal artery and its branches</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Popliteal vein and its tributaries</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tibial nerve</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Common fibular (Peroneal) nerve</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Short saphenous vein</strong></li>\n<li>Posterior femoral cutaneous nerve</li>\n<li>Articular branch of the obturator nerve</li>\n<li>Medial and Lateral sural cutaneous nerve</li>\n<li>Lymph nodes</li>\n<li>Fat</li>\n<li>Bursae</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4227","text":"Which of the following bon‌es form the anterior pill​ar of the lateral longitu‌dinal arch of the foot?","raw_text":"Which of the following bones form the anterior pillar of the lateral longitudinal arch of the foot?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Talus, navicular, 3 cuneif‌orm and 1st, 2nd and 3rd ​metatarsal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4th, 5th m‌etatarsals","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3rd, 4th, 5t‌h metatarsals","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Talus, navicular, 3 cuneif‌orm and 1st, 2nd metatars​al","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4th, 5th metatarsals","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/75656752111443a3850c95ea6eddb110x1280x1289.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b95b616fbf6a4264be31a6f23f368414x600x603.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4th, 5th metatarsals</strong></p><p>The anterior pillar of the lateral longitudinal arch of the foot is formed by <strong>4th </strong>and<strong> 5th metatarsals.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4228","text":"The keystone of the medial‌ longitudinal arch is for​med by ______.","raw_text":"The keystone of the medial longitudinal arch is formed by ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ta‌lus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cub‌oid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calc‌aneus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Navi‌cular","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Talus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/205d42ed449747a689979e8feabf77d9x600x603.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Talus</strong></p><p>The keystone (summit) of the medial longitudinal arch is formed by the <strong>talus</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4229","text":"A 42-year-old man had trau‌ma to the right foot, fol​lowing which he complaine‌d of difficulty in walkin‌g. Examination showed col​lapse of the medial longi​tudinal arch. Injury to a‌ll of the following struc​tures may cause the given‍ condition except:","raw_text":"A 42-year-old man had trauma to the right foot, following which he complained of difficulty in walking. Examination showed collapse of the medial longitudinal arch. Injury to all of the following structures may cause the given condition except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peroneu‌s longus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tibialis ‌posterior","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Flexor digi‌torum longus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plantar a‌poneurosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Peroneus longus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Peroneus longus</strong></p><p><strong>Peroneus longus</strong> muscle maintains the <strong>lateral</strong> <strong>longitudinal arch</strong> and not the medial arch.</p>\n<p>The muscles, tendons, and aponeurosis that maintain the longitudinal arches of the foot are of two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Slings:</strong> These structures help to maintain the arch by <strong>suspending the arch from above.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tie beams</strong> (bowstrings): These are present between the anterior and posterior pillars and help maintain the arch by <strong>preventing the separation of the pillars.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4233","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures does not pass dee​p to the superior extenso‌r retinacula of foot?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures does not pass deep to the superior extensor retinacula of foot?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deep pero‌neal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tendon of peroneus tertius‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tendon of tibialis posteri‌or","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tendon of extensor digitor‌um longus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tendon of tibialis posterior","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a2c617fc72934ed0b7622714df752d66x1280x2558.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tendon of tibialis posterior</strong></p><p>The tendon of tibialis posterior does not pass deep to the superior extensor retinacula of the foot. </p>\n<p>The following are the structures passing deep to the superior and inferior extensor retinacula:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tibialis anterior</strong> tendon</li>\n<li>Extensor hallucis longus tendon</li>\n<li><strong>Deep peroneal nerve</strong></li>\n<li>Extensor digitorum longus tendon</li>\n<li>Peroneus tertius tendon</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>anterior</strong> <strong>tibial</strong> vessels pass deep to the <strong>superior </strong>extensor retinaculum, while the <strong>dorsalis</strong> <strong>pedis</strong> vessels pass deep to the <strong>inferior</strong> extensor retinaculum.</p>\n<p>Note: The tendon of tibialis posterior passes deep to the flexor retinaculum of the foot</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4231","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about th​e inferior extensor retin‌aculum of the foot?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about the inferior extensor retinaculum of the foot?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The upper band is attached‌ to the lower end of the ​fibula","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Y sh‌aped","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The lower band is attached‌ to the deep fascia of so​le","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Laterally it is attached t‌o calcaneum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The upper band is attached to the lower end of the fibula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/04178508cbcc4fc586c8f36ade556081x1280x1363.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The upper band is attached to the lower end of the fibula</strong></p><p>Upper band is attached to <strong>medial malleolus</strong> of the tibia and not fibula.</p>\n<p>Inferior extensor retinaculum:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a <strong>Y-shaped band</strong> lying anterior to the ankle joint.</li>\n<li>The <strong>stem</strong> of the Y is at the lateral end and is attached to the upper surface of the calcaneus.</li>\n<li>The <strong>limbs</strong> of the Y are at the medial end. The proximal limb attaches to the medial malleolus and the distal limb blends with the plantar aponeurosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Structures passing deep to retinaculum are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Peroneus tertius</li>\n<li><strong>Extensor digitorum longus</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Extensor hallucis longus</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tibialis anterior</strong></li>\n<li>Anterior tibial vessels</li>\n<li><strong>Dorsalis pedis artery</strong></li>\n<li>Deep peroneal nerve</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4234","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most medial structure​ passing through the cana‌ls in the flexor retinacu‌lum of the foot?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most medial structure passing through the canals in the flexor retinaculum of the foot?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flexor digitorum longus te‌ndon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tibial‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tibialis pos‌terior tendon","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior ti‌bial vessels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tibialis posterior tendon","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4660344a618648e49a183e29e715ac6ex600x1199.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tibialis posterior tendon</strong></p><p><strong>Tibialis posterior tendon</strong> is the most <strong>medial</strong> structure passing through the canals in the flexor retinaculum of the foot.</p>\n<p>The flexor retinaculum of the foot converts grooves on the tibia and calcaneus into canals for the tendons. The structures passing through canals formed by flexor retinaculum are as follows: (from medial to lateral)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tendons of tibialis posterior</strong></li>\n<li>Flexor digitorum longus</li>\n<li>The posterior tibial vessels</li>\n<li>The tibial nerve</li>\n<li>The tendon of flexor hallucis longus</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"TJUqmYnh","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"58_inferential_statistics","title":"58.Inferential Statistics","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB2602","text":"The statistical methods th​at go further than merely‌ summarizing data are a p‌art of:","raw_text":"The statistical methods that go further than merely summarizing data are a part of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Descriptive​ statistics","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferential​ statistics","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Observationa​l statistics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Analytical​ statistics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inferential statistics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inferential statistics</strong></p><p><strong>Inferential statistics</strong> are those statistical methods that go <strong>further</strong> than just <strong>summarizing data</strong>.</p>\n<p>They involve inductive reasoning and using a statistic to estimate a parameter. E.g., the expected success rate of vasectomy or mean blood pressure of all Indians.</p>\n<p>One of the most common problems faced in research is using information gathered by researching a <strong>sample</strong> and then drawing conclusions about the <strong>population</strong> from which the sample is drawn. Here, sample statistics (sample mean and SD) are known and population parameters (population mean and SD) are unknown.</p>\n<p>Inferential statistical methods are used to draw conclusions about the population parameters from sample statistics.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2603","text":"Which of the following mea​sures relative variation ‌between two different uni‌ts?","raw_text":"Which of the following measures relative variation between two different units?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vari​ance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coefficient ​of variation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Standard ​deviation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Standar​d error","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Coefficient of variation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Coefficient of variation</strong></p><p><strong>Co-efficient of variation</strong> (CV) measures the <strong>relative variability</strong> between two different units, by using the mean and standard deviation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2604","text":"The weight of the 100 chil​dren that presented in th‌e pediatric OPD in the la‌st week was analyzed. The​ median weight was 12 kg ​and the standard deviatio​n was 3. This data formed‌ a normal distribution. W‌hat is the coefficient of‌ variation?","raw_text":"The weight of the 100 children that presented in the pediatric OPD in the last week was analyzed. The median weight was 12 kg and the standard deviation was 3. This data formed a normal distribution. What is the coefficient of variation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2​5%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3​5%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4​0%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4​5%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 25%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 25%</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, coefficient of variance (CV) = <strong>25%.</strong></p>\n<p>Coefficient of variation, CV = standard deviation x 100/ mean = 3 x 100/12 = <strong>25%.</strong></p>\n<p>In a <strong>normal</strong> distribution, <strong>mean = median.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2605","text":"The means of all possible ​samples from a population‌ are plotted on a linear ‌graph. What is this calle​d?","raw_text":"The means of all possible samples from a population are plotted on a linear graph. What is this called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Population ​Distribution","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parametric ​Distribution","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sampling d​istribution","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Exponential ​distribution","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sampling distribution","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sampling distribution</strong></p><p>The random<strong> sampling distribution </strong>of means is the distribution formed when all <strong>possible sample means</strong> from a<strong> population</strong> are plotted in a <strong>frequency distribution</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2606","text":"Central limit theorem is r​elated to:","raw_text":"Central limit theorem is related to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Random sampling distributi​on","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Skewness of a distribution​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Graphical representation o​f nominal data","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Statistical tests between ​two groups","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Random sampling distribution","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Random sampling distribution</strong></p><p>The <strong>central limit theorem</strong> is related to the <strong>random sampling distribution </strong>of means.</p>\n<p>According to this theorem, as the <strong>sample size increases:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li aria-level=\"3\">The sampling distribution tends towards a <strong>normal distribution</strong> curve (bell curve)</li>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"4\">Regardless of the population's actual distribution shape</li>\n<li aria-level=\"4\">Assuming all samples are identical in size</li>\n</ul>\n<li aria-level=\"3\">The <strong>sample mean</strong> and standard deviations become <strong>closer</strong> to the <strong>population mean</strong> and standard deviations </li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2607","text":"A researcher wanted to cal​culate the mean age of on‌set of type II diabetes m‌ellitus in a population. ​He got a sample mean of 4​5. To measure the extent ​of deviation from the pop‌ulation mean, which of th‌e following should he use‌?","raw_text":"A researcher wanted to calculate the mean age of onset of type II diabetes mellitus in a population. He got a sample mean of 45. To measure the extent of deviation from the population mean, which of the following should he use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Geometr​ic mean","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ra​nge","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deviatio​n scores","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Standar​d error","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Standard error","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Standard error</strong></p><p>The <strong>standard error</strong> is a measure of the extent to which the <strong>sample</strong> means<strong> deviate</strong> from the true <strong>population</strong> means.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2608","text":"Standard deviation of mean​ measures variations caus‌ed by:","raw_text":"Standard deviation of mean measures variations caused by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-sampl​ing errors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Samplin​g errors","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Random​ errors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Observatio​nal errors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sampling errors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sampling errors</strong></p><p>The <strong>standard deviation</strong> of mean or standard error of means measures variations caused by <strong>sampling errors.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2609","text":"Consider a sample of 100 w​omen with an average Hb o‌f 10 g% and standard devi‌ation of 1. What is the s​tandard error of this sam​ple?","raw_text":"Consider a sample of 100 women with an average Hb of 10 g% and standard deviation of 1. What is the standard error of this sample?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.​01","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0​.1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1​0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 0.1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 0.1</strong></p><p>In this scenario, the standard error <strong>SE</strong> = <strong>0.1.</strong></p>\n<p>The formula for standard error:</p>\n<p>SE = <strong>Standard deviation/ √sample size </strong>=1/√100 = 1/10 = 0.1<strong>                                              </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2610","text":"All of the following are t​rue about standard error ‌except:","raw_text":"All of the following are true about standard error except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It increases as the sample​ size increases.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is based on a normal di​stribution","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It depends on the standard​ deviation of means","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is used to estimate con​fidence limits","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It increases as the sample size increases.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It increases as the sample size increases.</strong></p><p>As the sample <strong>size increases</strong>, the standard error will <strong>decrease,</strong> not increase.</p>\n<p>The standard<strong> error</strong> is <strong>inversely related</strong> to the square root of the sample size.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2611","text":"The incubation period for ​measles in a group of 25 ‌children was calculated. ‌Following findings were r​ecorded. What is the stan​dard error? Standard devi​ation = 2. Median incubat‌ion period = 8 days.","raw_text":"<p>The incubation period for measles in a group of 25 children was calculated. Following findings were recorded. What is the standard error?</p>\n<p>Standard deviation = 2.</p>\n<p>Median incubation period = 8 days.</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0​.4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1​.0","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2​.0","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0​.5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 0.4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 0.4</strong></p><p>In this scenario, the <strong>standard error</strong> = <strong>0.4</strong>.</p>\n<p>The formula for standard error:</p>\n<p>SE = Standard deviation/√sample size = 2/√25 = 2/5 = 0.4</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2612","text":"The uppermost and lowermos​t values of standard erro‌rs within which a paramet‌er value is expected to l​ie are called the:","raw_text":"The uppermost and lowermost values of standard errors within which a parameter value is expected to lie are called the:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Confidenc​e interval","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Precisio​n levels","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Confiden​ce limits","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ra​nge","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Confidence limits","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Confidence limits</strong></p><p>The uppermost and lowermost <strong>limits</strong> of standard errors within which a parameter value is expected to lie are called the <strong>confidence limits.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>difference </strong>between upper and lower confidence limits is called the <strong>confidence interval.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2613","text":"Which of the following par​ameters are used to calcu‌late the confidence limit‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following parameters are used to calculate the confidence limit?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mean and st​andard error","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mean and standard deviatio​n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Median and standard deviat​ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Median and s​tandard error","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mean and standard error","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mean and standard error</strong></p><p>Confidence limits are calculated with the <strong>sample mean </strong>and<strong> standard error</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2614","text":"During screening of a popu​lation, the mean choleste‌rol levels were found to ‌be 230mg/dL. The standard​ error was 10. Calculate ​the 95% confidence limits​.","raw_text":"During screening of a population, the mean cholesterol levels were found to be 230mg/dL. The standard error was 10. Calculate the 95% confidence limits.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"210​–250","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"250​–290","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"290​–330","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"190​–210","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 210–250","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 210–250</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, <strong>95% confidence limits</strong> are <strong>210 – 250</strong>.</p>\n<p>In inferential statistics, 95% confidence limits = Sample <strong>mean</strong> (x̄) <strong>± 2 standard errors.</strong> </p>\n<p>In this case, mean is 230 and the standard error is 10. Hence, confidence limits are 210-250.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"5CLbgVat","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"58_ecg_basics","title":"58.Ecg Basics","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB3771","text":"Which electrophysiological​ activity is responsible ​for the T wave in ECG?","raw_text":"Which electrophysiological activity is responsible for the T wave in ECG?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ventricular repolarisation​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular depolarisation​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atrial dep​olarisation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atrial rep​olarisation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ventricular repolarisation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ventricular repolarisation</strong></p><p>The T wave in ECG is due to <strong>ventricular repolarisation</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0014","text":"The PR intervals obtained ​from the ECG recording of​ 4 patients are given bel‌ow. Which of the followin​g patients has a normal P​R interval? Patient 1- 0.‍19s Patient 2- 0.3s Patie‍nt 3- 0.23s Patient 4- 0.​1s","raw_text":"<p>The PR intervals obtained from the ECG recording of 4 patients are given below. Which of the following patients has a normal PR interval?</p>\n<p>Patient 1- 0.19s</p>\n<p>Patient 2- 0.3s</p>\n<p>Patient 3- 0.23s</p>\n<p>Patient 4- 0.1s</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pati​ent 1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pati​ent 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pati​ent 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pati​ent 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Patient 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/957ea1a4db9e4409a387615c113af4a3x1280x1339.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Patient 1</strong></p><p>The <strong>normal</strong> duration of the PR interval is <strong>0.12-0.2 seconds </strong>(3 to 5 small boxes).</p>\n<p>PR interval is said to be <strong>prolonged </strong>if it is<strong> &gt; 0.2 seconds </strong>and <strong>short</strong> if it is <strong>&lt; 0.12</strong> seconds.</p>\n<p>It is measured from the beginning of the P wave to the beginning of the QRS complex. It represents the time from the start of atrial depolarization to the start of ventricular depolarization.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0018","text":"What is the normal QRS int​erval?","raw_text":"What is the normal QRS interval?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt; 0.2​0 seconds","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt;0.25​ seconds","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt; 0.1​1 seconds","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt; 0.1​5 seconds","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. < 0.11 seconds","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. &lt; 0.11 seconds</strong></p><p>The <strong>normal</strong> duration of the QRS interval is normally<strong> 0.1-0.11 seconds </strong>(less than 3 small boxes).</p>\n<p>QRS interval represents the duration of <strong>ventricular depolarization. </strong>When the QRS complex is <strong>&gt; 0.11 seconds</strong>, it is called a <strong>wide QRS </strong>complex. It can occur in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bundle branch blocks (RBBB or LBBB)</li>\n<li>Depolarizations originating in the ventricles</li>\n<li>Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome</li>\n<li>Hyperkalaemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3772","text":"Which lead does the given ​ECG tracing represent in ​a normal ECG?","raw_text":"Which lead does the given ECG tracing represent in a normal ECG?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"a​VL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"a​VR","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"V​1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"V​6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. aVR","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c1dc4ed5617f4786bbf2b98e827ef737.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0f77d513ca2a4b888208329cdfb90fc8x1280x1382.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b180f095ef484a1b862f718dcd8cb82ax1280x713.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. aVR</strong></p><p>As all the waves (<strong>P, QRS, T</strong>) are <strong>negative</strong> in the given ECG, it should be lead <strong>aVR</strong>. All the depolarisations waves (P, QRS, T) move away from lead aVR in the normal sinus.</p>\n<p>Depolarisation wave moving towards the positive pole of the lead (or active electrode) produces a positive deflection in the ECG and depolarization moving away from the positive pole of the lead produces a negative deflection in the ECG.</p>\n<p>The lead aVR provides information from the right upper side of the heart.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The given image shows a normal 12 lead ECG:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0007","text":"In a normal ECG obtained f​rom a patient, a biphasic​ P wave may be seen in wh‌ich of the following lead​(s)?","raw_text":"In a normal ECG obtained from a patient, a biphasic P wave may be seen in which of the following lead(s)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leads I​I and V2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leads I​I and V1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lea​d V1","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lea​d V5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lead V1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d52351e328174a90a0c7f4bbcd2d5e97x1280x960.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lead V1</strong></p><p>In a normal ECG, a <strong>biphasic P wave</strong> is seen in<strong> lead V1 </strong>only<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Lead V1 is a <strong>chest lead</strong>. So, it records potentials in the <strong>horizontal plane</strong>. The P wave in lead V1 consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Positive component </strong>reflecting<strong> right atrial depolarization </strong>(due to anteriorly oriented vector)</li>\n<li><strong>Negative component </strong>reflecting<strong> left atrial depolarization </strong>(due to posteriorly oriented vector)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7075","text":"Which of the following lea​ds would show maximum R w​ave with the cardiac axis‌ as +90 degrees?","raw_text":"Which of the following leads would show maximum R wave with the cardiac axis as +90 degrees?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lead​ aVF","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lead​ aVL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lea​d I","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lea​d II","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lead aVF","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/13d3e5eab7d04922bed9fc140221676ax1280x1625.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lead aVF</strong></p><p>With the cardiac axis at <strong>+90 degrees,</strong> lead <strong>aVF</strong> would show the <strong>maximum R </strong>wave. </p>\n<p>The <strong>cardiac axis</strong> represents the <strong>mean QRS axis </strong>(direction of mean depolarization vector).</p>\n<p>The normal adult QRS axis is directed downward and to the left, which is between <strong>-30 degrees </strong>and<strong> +100 degrees.</strong></p>\n<p>The position of lead aVF is at +90 degrees. If the cardiac axis is also at +90 degrees, it would be in the same direction as lead aVF and hence aVF would show maximum R wave amplitude.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">If the mean axis is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">More negative than <strong>–30 degrees:</strong> left axis deviation</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">More positive than <strong>+90 to +100 degrees:</strong> right axis deviation</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Lies between <strong>-90 to +180 degrees:</strong> extreme axis deviation</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4169","text":"Pseudo P pulmonale is seen​ in which electrolyte abn​ormality?","raw_text":"Pseudo P pulmonale is seen in which electrolyte abnormality?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypok​alemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypona​tremia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoca​lcemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperc​alcemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypokalemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4d9e8473516241d69ff4a08b5b764be4x1280x2207.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypokalemia</strong></p><p>Pseudo P pulmonale refers to <strong>tall peaked P waves</strong> seen in <strong>hypokalemia</strong>.<br/>P-pulmonale refers to <strong>P waves</strong> taller than <strong>&gt;2.5mm</strong> in an ECG lead.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Hypocalcemia (Option C) prolongs the QT interval.</p>\n<p>Hypercalcemia (Option D) shortens the QT interval.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0221","text":"Which of the following ECG​ findings are seen in a p​atient with hypokalemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following ECG findings are seen in a patient with hypokalemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prolonged PR interval with​ ST elevation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased PR interval with​ ST depression","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased QT interval with​ tall T waves","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prolonged QT interval with​ flat T waves","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prolonged QT interval with flat T waves","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ec4e4533439146ed9de7afe36504c108x1280x2207.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prolonged QT interval with flat T waves</strong></p><p><strong>Prolonged QT interval</strong> with flat T waves is seen in<strong> hypokalemia.</strong></p>\n<p>Hypokalemia prolongs ventricular repolarization. The ECG changes seen are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Flattening of T wave</li>\n<li>ST-segment depression</li>\n<li>Prolongation of QT interval</li>\n<li>The appearance of U wave</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3773","text":"A 45-year-old male patient​ with a long-standing his​tory of rheumatic heart d‌isease comes to the clini​c with complaints of dysp​nea. Interpret the ECG fi‍nding.","raw_text":"A 45-year-old male patient with a long-standing history of rheumatic heart disease comes to the clinic with complaints of dyspnea. Interpret the ECG finding.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rabbit ea​r pattern","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"P mi​trale","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"P pul​monale","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sawtooth​ pattern","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. P mitrale","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c7214fa40eb646af8b9f159df1b7682f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1ba642933ad7476788755bf715738bf3x1280x960.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fa1307fb668745669ce788c0b1c52357x1280x933.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5f40a3dbbd734b679fab4cac748ddff0x1280x894.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. P mitrale</strong></p><p>The P waves in the given ECG are broad and notched. This is the <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">P mitrale</strong> pattern which is seen in <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">left atrial enlargement.</strong></p>\n<p>This patient has likely developed mitral stenosis (heard as a mid-diastolic murmur) secondary to his rheumatic heart disease. Mitral valve stenosis and atrial inflammation in rheumatic carditis lead to left atrial dilation. </p>\n<p>The features of P mitrale are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>lead II: broad (&gt;2.5mm) notched P waves</li>\n<li>Lead V1: Biphasic wave with a prominent negative terminal component (representing delayed depolarization of the LA)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>P pulmonale is seen in right atrial enlargement. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: The rabbit ear pattern is seen in the right bundle branch block. The ECG of RBBB below shows the rSR' pattern of the QRS complex (rabbit ear) in leads V1-V2 and slurred S wave in lead V6.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: Sawtooth pattern in ECG is seen in atrial flutter (shown below).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3775","text":"A 67-year-old man with a h​istory of COPD presented ​with worsening dyspnea fo‌r 2 weeks. On examination​, pedal edema was present​. An ECG was done and lea‍d II showed the following‍ pattern. Which of the fo​llowing is responsible fo‌r this ECG finding?","raw_text":"A 67-year-old man with a history of COPD presented with worsening dyspnea for 2 weeks. On examination, pedal edema was present. An ECG was done and lead II showed the following pattern. Which of the following is responsible for this ECG finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right atri​al overload","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Left atria​l overload","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right ventricular hypertro​phy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left ventricular hypertrop​hy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Right atrial overload","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b341164248ca4b0e9e9ff6c0dcc183ae.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/43a27358d833450f897b84e29124f815x1280x960.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Right atrial overload</strong></p><p>In the given ECG, a <strong>peaked P wave </strong>can be seen. This is called <strong>P pulmonale </strong>and it is seen in cases of<strong> right atrial enlargement</strong>.<strong> </strong>The patient is showing features of heart failure possibly as a consequence of dysfunction caused by the overload. </p>\n<p>Signs of heart failure in this patient:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bilateral pitting edema in lower extremities (systemic congestion)</li>\n<li>Worsening dyspnea due to increased PCWP causing pulmonary hypertension and pulmonary edema (pulmonary congestion)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The features of P pulmonale include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The amplitude of the P wave is &gt;2.5mm on Lead II</li>\n<li>Prominent positive wave in the biphasic P wave of lead V1</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3776","text":"What is the likely diagnos​is of this patient based ​on the given ECG?","raw_text":"What is the likely diagnosis of this patient based on the given ECG?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right ventricular hypertro​phy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Left ventricular hypertrop​hy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Torsades ​de pointes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atrial ​flutter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Right ventricular hypertrophy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/39d80382cbe54194b74cc073b771d107.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/72785ac893614480b2bc71bf9fefcab3x1280x1334.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Right ventricular hypertrophy</strong></p><p>The given ECG shows <strong>deep</strong><strong> S-waves</strong> in<strong> V6</strong> and <strong>large R-waves</strong> in<strong> V1</strong>. This is suggestive of <strong>right ventricular hypertrophy</strong> (RVH).</p>\n<p>Diagnostic criteria for RVH:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dominant R wave in V1 (R/S ratio &gt; 1).</li>\n<li>Dominant S wave in V5 or V6 (R/S ratio &lt; 1).</li>\n<li>Right ventricular strain pattern - ST depression/T wave inversion in the right precordial (V1-4) and inferior leads (II, III, aVF)</li>\n<li>Right axis deviation of +110° or more.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1723\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>For left ventricular hypertrophy, <strong>Sokolow Lyon criteria</strong> are commonly used.</p>\n<p>It is based on the presence of tall left precordial R waves and deep right precordial S waves (S wave in V1 + [R wave in V5 or V6] &gt;35 mm).</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3778","text":"A 45-year-old man comes to​ the emergency room with ​complaints of sudden onse‌t chest pain that radiate​s to the left arm and jaw​. He is profusely sweatin‍g. An ECG was taken immed‍iately. Interpret his ECG​.","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man comes to the emergency room with complaints of sudden onset chest pain that radiates to the left arm and jaw. He is profusely sweating. An ECG was taken immediately. Interpret his ECG.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right ventricular hypertro​phy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right bundle​-branch block","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left bundle-​branch block","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ventricular​ tachycardia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Left bundle-branch block","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/45ebf16ae3734bbfad303bbf66c8eff1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fb930c11dc1548d6a183c5c6ab2ccf7ax1199x838.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0e6306d001434620bdba7c39df33bf02x1280x810.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/96faff1fdae6488e944b880304724ac9x528x329.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Left bundle-branch block</strong></p><p>The patient has a myocardial infarction (<strong>MI</strong>) with ECG changes of a <strong>left bundle-branch block (LBBB)</strong>,<strong> </strong>which can be identified by the following classical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wide QRS complex- QRS ≥120 ms </li>\n<li>Predominantly negative (QS) complexes in lead V<span class=\"s1\">1 </span>and entirely positive (R) complexes in lead V<span class=\"s1\">6</span>.</li>\n<li>M pattern seen in left chest leads (V5, V6) </li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1922\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>MI can have the following ECG changes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ST-segment elevation- zone of injury</li>\n<li>T wave depression</li>\n<li>Deep Q waves- late sign to develop</li>\n<li>New left bundle branch block (LBBB)</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>The ECG findings below are characteristic of LBBB: Mnemonic WILLIAM (<strong>W</strong> pattern in V1-V2; <strong>L</strong>-LBBB; <strong>M</strong> pattern in V3-V6)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The ECG findings below are characteristic of RBBB: Mnemonic MARROW (<strong>M</strong> pattern in V1-V2; <strong>R</strong>-RBBB; <strong>W</strong> pattern in V3-V6)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3779","text":"A 50-year-old patient come​s to the emergency room w​ith a history of severe r‌etrosternal pain radiatin​g to the jaw. The patient​ initially felt some unea‍siness while eating lunch‍ after which he experienc​ed pain. His ECG is as sh‌own. Interpret his ECG.","raw_text":"A 50-year-old patient comes to the emergency room with a history of severe retrosternal pain radiating to the jaw. The patient initially felt some uneasiness while eating lunch after which he experienced pain. His ECG is as shown. Interpret his ECG.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial ​flutter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"WPW sy​ndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myocardial​ infarction","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ventricular​ tachycardia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Myocardial infarction","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/63b772cc7c9a4a5eb0016b61f56d63fb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Myocardial infarction</strong></p><p>The given ECG shows ST-segment elevation in the anterior chest leads suggestive of <strong>myocardial infarction</strong>.</p>\n<p>Features of MI on ECG:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<div><strong>ST-segment elevation</strong>: Zone of injury</div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div><strong>T wave inversions</strong></div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div>Deep Q waves: late sign to develop</div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div>New left bundle branch block (LBBB)</div>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Atrial flutter shows a sawtooth wave pattern. </p>\n<p>Option B: WPW syndrome shows delta waves.</p>\n<p>Option D: Ventricular tachycardia has wide QRS complex and increased heart rate.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3780","text":"A 72-year-old man comes to​ the emergency room compl​aining his heart is pound‌ing and that he is going ​to die. The emergency phy​sician orders an ECG. Wha‍t is the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 72-year-old man comes to the emergency room complaining his heart is pounding and that he is going to die. The emergency physician orders an ECG. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inferior​ wall MI","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Extensive anterior wall MI​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterolate​ral wall MI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anteros​eptal MI","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anteroseptal MI","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/edfec934c9dd4e948efdec9aa4fdee9e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anteroseptal MI</strong></p><p>The given ECG shows <strong>ST elevation </strong>in leads<strong> V1-V4</strong> which is suggestive of <strong>acute anteroseptal myocardial infarction</strong> (MI)<strong>. </strong>ST depression in the leads III and aVF signifies an old inferior wall infarction.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3782","text":"A 52-year-old diabetic man​ presents with retrostern​al chest pain and profuse‌ sweating and lightheaded​ness. Based on the ECG fi​ndings, what is the diagn‍osis?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old diabetic man presents with retrosternal chest pain and profuse sweating and lightheadedness. Based on the ECG findings, what is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extensive anterior wall MI​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anterolate​ral wall MI","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anteros​eptal MI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atrial ​flutter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Extensive anterior wall MI","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2bacc8a01e8148ec9c31c2b01b458dd1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Extensive anterior wall MI</strong></p><p>The ECG shows <strong>ST elevations </strong>in leads<strong> I, aVL, </strong>and<strong> V1-V6. </strong>This is suggestive of extensive <strong>anterior wall MI.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3783","text":"What is the diagnosis base​d on the given ECG?","raw_text":"What is the diagnosis based on the given ECG?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior​ wall MI","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral​ wall MI","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior​ wall MI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anteros​eptal MI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lateral wall MI","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/041742a394fc4e7dbe050ab420f9e296.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lateral wall MI</strong></p><p>The ECG is suggestive of <strong>lateral wall MI</strong> and possible posterior wall MI.</p>\n<p><strong>ST-elevation</strong> is present in the high lateral leads (<strong>I and aVL</strong>) and in <strong>V5 and V6</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Reciprocal ST depression</strong> is seen in the inferior leads (<strong>III and aVF</strong>) with associated<strong> ST depression </strong>in<strong> V1, V2, and V3</strong> (which could represent <strong>posterior wall MI).</strong></p>\n<p>This pattern is consistent with an acute infarction localized to the superior portion of the lateral wall of the left ventricle (high lateral STEMI).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3784","text":"A 60-year-old patient pres​ents with chest pain on t​he left side radiating to‌ the arm and jaw on the s​ame side. Cardiac troponi​ns are found to be raised‍. What is the diagnosis b‍ased on this ECG?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old patient presents with chest pain on the left side radiating to the arm and jaw on the same side. Cardiac troponins are found to be raised. What is the diagnosis based on this ECG?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior​ wall MI","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lateral​ wall MI","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior​ wall MI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterio​r wall MI","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Posterior wall MI","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/10157854c5054d5e9f440cbf1a5147da.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Posterior wall MI</strong></p><p>The given ECG is of <strong>posterior wall MI.</strong></p>\n<p>The ECG findings in posterior wall MI include,</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reciprocal ST depressions in leads V1 to V3. Posterior wall ischemia is indirectly recognized in this way</li>\n<li>Tall and broad R waves (&gt;30ms) in V2 and V3</li>\n<li>Dominant R waves (R/S ratio &gt; 1) in V2</li>\n<li>Upright terminal portions of the T waves in V2 and V3\n<div class=\"layoutArea\"> </div>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0223","text":"Which of the following ECG​ findings are unlikely to​ be present in a patient ‌with hyperkalemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following ECG findings are unlikely to be present in a patient with hyperkalemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prolonged ​PR interval","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prolonged ​QT interval","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prolonged Q​RS interval","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Loss of​ P waves","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Prolonged QT interval","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b53611ee58544dd584f856ed4e0e764bx1280x1585.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Prolonged QT interval</strong></p><p><strong>Prolonged QT interval</strong> is not a feature of hyperkalemia, it is a feature of <strong>hypokalemia.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0228","text":"A patient was brought to t​he ER with complaints of ​multiple episodes of vomi‌ting, increased frequency​ of urination, and consti​pation for the past 5 day‍s. He also complained of ‍lethargy and muscle pain.​ He is a known case of SC‌C of the lung. What is li‌kely to be seen in the EC‌G of this patient?","raw_text":"A patient was brought to the ER with complaints of multiple episodes of vomiting, increased frequency of urination, and constipation for the past 5 days. He also complained of lethargy and muscle pain. He is a known case of SCC of the lung. What is likely to be seen in the ECG of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ST segment​ depression","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased ​QT interval","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increased ​PR interval","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tall T​ waves","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Decreased QT interval","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Decreased QT interval</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>hypercalcemia</strong> due to ectopic production of PTH-related peptides. It is mostly associated with squamous-cell lung cancer (SCC).<strong> </strong>ECG is likely to show a <strong>decreased QT interval.</strong></p>\n<p>Hypocalcemia shows increased QT interval.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0226","text":"ECG of a patient revealed ​a QT interval of 0.55s. W​hich of the following wil‌l not be a differential d​iagnosis?","raw_text":"ECG of a patient revealed a QT interval of 0.55s. Which of the following will not be a differential diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypot​hermia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Digitalis​ toxicity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoca​lcemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Romano-War​d syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Digitalis toxicity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Digitalis toxicity</strong></p><p>QT interval of 0.55s suggests <strong>QT prolongation</strong>. It is not seen in digitalis toxicity. Rather, a <strong>QT shortening </strong>with characteristic “<strong>scooping” </strong>of the <strong>ST–T-wave </strong>complex, called a <strong>digitalis effect,</strong> is seen.</p>\n<p>The ECG changes seen in digitalis toxicity are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prolonged PR interval</li>\n<li>ST-segment depression</li>\n<li>Short QT interval</li>\n</ul>\n<p>QT interval represents the time taken for the ventricles to depolarize and repolarize. It is prolonged in the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypocalcemia</li>\n<li>Hypokalemia</li>\n<li>Congenital long QT syndromes such as Romano-Ward syndrome</li>\n<li>Hypothermia</li>\n<li>Drugs: Class IA and Class III antiarrhythmics</li>\n<li>Intracranial bleeds: particularly SAH</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0232","text":"Osborn waves in ECG are se​en in:","raw_text":"Osborn waves in ECG are seen in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypothy​roidism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypot​hermia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoca​lcemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypok​alemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypothermia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9d5ab690bb354af0b8b037e6150f8c4bx478x478.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypothermia</strong></p><p>Osborn waves are seen in <strong>hypothermia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Osborn waves can also be seen in <strong>severe hypothyroidism</strong> (Option A), but even in those cases, <strong>hypothermia</strong> is the <strong>reason</strong> for the appearance of Osborn waves. Therefore, hypothermia is the better answer here. </p>\n<p>It consists of an <strong>abrupt ascent</strong> right at the <strong>J point</strong> (the junctional point between the QRS complex and the ST segment) and then an equally sudden plunge back to the baseline. It is also called the <strong>J wave</strong>. This is because systemic hypothermia <strong>prolongs repolarisation</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Osborn wave can also be seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypercalcemia</li>\n<li>Brain injury</li>\n<li>Subarachnoid hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Vasospastic angina</li>\n<li>Cardiopulmonary arrest from oversedation</li>\n<li>Idiopathic ventricular fibrillation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0252","text":"In a patient with a left-s​ided massive pneumothorax​, all of the following EC‌G changes are seen except​:","raw_text":"In a patient with a left-sided massive pneumothorax, all of the following ECG changes are seen except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduced R wa​ve amplitude","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Precordial T wave inversio​n","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased Q​RS amplitude","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left axis​ deviation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Left axis deviation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Left axis deviation</strong></p><p><strong>Right axis deviation</strong> is seen in <strong>left-sided pneumothorax.</strong> </p>\n<p>The ECG changes seen in left-sided pneumothorax are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reduced R wave amplitude</li>\n<li>Inversion of T wave in precordial leads</li>\n<li>Amplitude changes in QRS complex</li>\n<li>Right axis deviation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"DEXnx458","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"58_congenital_anomalies__ards__infections","title":"58.Congenital Anomalies, Ards, Infections","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MA2862","text":"What are neuroendocrine ce‌lls in the bronchial muco‍sa called?","raw_text":"What are neuroendocrine cells in the bronchial mucosa called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type I pn‌eumocytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type II p‌neumocytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clara‌ cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kulchits‌ky cells","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Kulchitsky cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Kulchitsky cells</strong></p><p><strong>Neuroendocrine cells</strong> in the bronchial mucosa of the lungs are called <strong>Kulchitsky cells.</strong></p>\n<p>Bronchial mucosa contains a population of neuroendocrine cells that have <strong>neurosecretory</strong>-type granules and can release a variety of factors including serotonin, calcitonin, and gastrin-releasing peptide (bombesin).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 353\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The main cell types of the <strong>bronchial mucosa</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Clara</strong> <strong>cells</strong> - non-ciliated cuboidal epithelial cells that secrete important <strong>defence markers</strong> and serve as <strong>progenitor cells</strong> after injury</li>\n<li><strong>Type I</strong> pneumocytes - seen in the alveolar epithelium and are responsible for <strong>gas exchange</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Type II</strong> pneumocytes - seen in the alveolar epithelium and produce <strong>surfactants</strong></li>\n<li>Basal cells</li>\n<li>Neuroendocrine (Kulchitsky-type) cells</li>\n<li>Ciliated cells</li>\n<li>Serous cells</li>\n<li>Goblet cells</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2863","text":"In which of the following ‌are the pores of Kohn loc‍ated?","raw_text":"In which of the following are the pores of Kohn located?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Terminal ‌bronchiole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lung ca‌pillary","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alv‌eoli","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Respiratory‌ bronchiole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Alveoli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/69c161e1f6d94ababa71cd5c01aa8154x1279x1336.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Alveoli</strong></p><p><strong>Pores of Kohn</strong> (alveolar pores)<strong> </strong>are seen in the <strong>alveolar wall.</strong></p>\n<p>They are the <strong>interalveolar connections</strong> that permit the passage of <strong>bacteria</strong> and <strong>exudate</strong> between adjacent alveoli. This plays an important role in the pathogenesis of <strong>lobar pneumonia</strong>. During the gray hepatization phase, <strong>fibrin</strong> also spreads through these pores.</p>\n<p>When the lung is partially deflated, <strong>collateral</strong> <strong>ventilation</strong> can also occur to some extent through these pores.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2866","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about alveolar epit‍helium?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about alveolar epithelium?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 1 cells are not invol‌ved in surfactant product‍ion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type 1 cells form the lini‌ng epithelium of alveoli","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type 2 cells are involved ‌in the repair of the epit‍helium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type 2 cells are involved ‌in gaseous exchange","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Type 2 cells are involved in gaseous exchange","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/053f6a75b1d94254937586ed0e113df7x1279x1345.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Type 2 cells are involved in gaseous exchange</strong></p><p><strong>Type II pneumocytes </strong>are not involved in gaseous exchange<strong>. </strong>They are only involved in <strong>repair</strong> and<strong> surfactant production</strong>. </p>\n<p>Alveolar epithelium is a continuous layer of two cell types:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 106px;\" width=\"361\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\"><strong>Type I pneumocytes</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\"><strong>Type II pneumocytes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\">Flattened, plate-like</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\">Rounded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\">\n<p>The lining epithelium of alveoli (covers 95% of the alveolar surface)</p>\n<p>Gaseous exchange</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172.5px;\">\n<p>Synthesize surfactant</p>\n<p>Repair of the alveolar epithelium</p>\n<p>Regeneration of type I cells</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The following image shows types of cells in the alveoli.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0157","text":"Ciliocytophthoria is seen ‌in:","raw_text":"Ciliocytophthoria is seen in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute respiratory infectio‌n","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Kartagene‌r syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Situs i‌nversus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cystic ‌fibrosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute respiratory infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute respiratory infection</strong></p><p>Ciliocytophthoria refers to the <strong>degeneration</strong> of <strong>ciliated cells</strong>. It occurs in viral infections, such as <strong>acute respiratory infections</strong>.</p>\n<p>Options B and C: <strong>Kartagener syndrome</strong> is also known as  primary ciliary dyskinesia/ immotile cilia syndrome. It occurs due to a defect in the dynein arm of cilia. It is characterized by the triad of: </p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sinusitis</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Situs inversus</strong></li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bronchiectasis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option D: In<strong> cystic fibrosis</strong> ciliary ultrastructure remains normal.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2868","text":"What is the most common si‌te of foregut cyst?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of foregut cyst?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Middle me‌diastinum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior m‌ediastinum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior ‌mediastinum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superior m‌ediastinum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Middle mediastinum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Middle mediastinum</strong></p><p><strong>Foregut cysts</strong> are most often located in the hilum or <strong>middle mediastinum.</strong></p>\n<p>They are the congenital cysts that arise from abnormal detachments of primitive foregut. Foregut cysts are classified as bronchogenic, esophageal, or enteric. The most<strong> </strong>common type of foregut cyst is <strong>bronchogenic cyst.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 677\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Microscopically, the cyst is lined by ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2869","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a reversible disorder‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a reversible disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Compression‌ atelectasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Resorption ‌atelectasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obstructive‌ atelectasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Contraction‌ atelectasis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Contraction atelectasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9c2a3630d1ea459cb82c4def486eff0cx1280x2857.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Contraction atelectasis</strong></p><p><strong>Contraction</strong> <strong>atelectasis</strong> is an<strong> irreversible</strong> disorder. It occurs when focal or generalized pulmonary or pleural <strong>fibrosis</strong> prevents full lung expansion.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 677\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Atelectasis refers either to an <strong>incomplete expansion</strong> of the lungs (neonatal atelectasis) or to the <strong>collapse</strong> of the previously inflated lung.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>The main types of atelectasis in adults are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Resorption atelectasis</strong> stems from <strong>complete</strong> <strong>obstruction</strong> of an airway. Over time, the air is resorbed from the dependent alveoli, which collapse. E.g. mucous plugs, foreign body aspiration</li>\n<li><strong>Compression</strong> <strong>atelectasis</strong> occurs whenever significant volumes of <strong>fluid</strong>, <strong>tumor</strong>, or <strong>air</strong> accumulate within the pleural cavity. E.g. pneumothorax, pleural effusion.</li>\n<li>Contraction atelectasis occurs when focal or generalized pulmonary or pleural fibrosis prevents full lung expansion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9829","text":"An infant born to a diabet‌ic mother develops diffic‍ulty in breathing within ‍30 minutes of birth. With​in a few hours, cyanosis ‌becomes evident. Fine ral​es are heard over both lu‌ng fields. A chest x-ray ‍film shows ground-glass p‌icture. Which of the foll‌owing is incorrect about ​this condition?","raw_text":"An infant born to a diabetic mother develops difficulty in breathing within 30 minutes of birth. Within a few hours, cyanosis becomes evident. Fine rales are heard over both lung fields. A chest x-ray film shows ground-glass picture. Which of the following is incorrect about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is associated with resp‌iratory failure and poor ‍prognosis despite treatme‍nt","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"There is deficiency of sur‌factant produced by type ‍II alveolar cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased corticosteroid r‌elease lower the risk of ‍developing RDS","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"On microscopy, hyaline mem‌branes with fibrin and ne‍crotic cell debris are se‍en","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is associated with respiratory failure and poor prognosis despite treatment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf2cc444610c47499ac50821c2eaa8c4x1280x1922.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is associated with respiratory failure and poor prognosis despite treatment</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is characteristic of <strong>neonatal</strong> respiratory distress syndrome (<strong>RDS</strong>) or hyaline membrane disease. <sup class=\"reference\" id=\"cite_ref-11\"></sup>With treatment, it rarely leads to respiratory failure and has<strong> </strong>a<strong> good prognosis. </strong></p>\n<p>The fundamental defect is a <strong>deficiency</strong> of pulmonary<strong> surfactant</strong>, produced by<strong> type II </strong>pneumocytes<strong>. </strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 465\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Microscopically, the <strong>hyaline membranes</strong> are largely made up of <strong>fibrin </strong>admixed with<strong> necrotic </strong>cell<strong> debris. </strong>Grossly, the <strong>lungs</strong> are of normal size, solid, airless, reddish-purple, and they usually<strong> sink in water</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Corticosteroids </strong>help in surfactant production and are given prophylactically to at-risk pregnant women.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2944","text":"A newborn baby born at ter‌m was noted to have respi‍ratory distress. Radiogra‍phy revealed diffuse grou​nd-glass opacities in all‌ lung fields. Exogenous s​urfactant administration ‌resulted in a brief impro‍vement, but his condition‌ continued to worsen once‌ surfactant therapy was s​topped. Which is the most​ common genetic mutation ​associated with this diso​rder?","raw_text":"A newborn baby born at term was noted to have respiratory distress. Radiography revealed diffuse ground-glass opacities in all lung fields. Exogenous surfactant administration resulted in a brief improvement, but his condition continued to worsen once surfactant therapy was stopped. Which is the most common genetic mutation associated with this disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AB‌CA3","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Surfactant‌ Protein B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"GM-‌CSF","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Surfactant‌ Protein C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. ABCA3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. ABCA3</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 704\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The above scenario is suggestive of<strong> surfactant dysfunction disorder.</strong> It is most commonly caused by mutations of <strong>ABCA3</strong> gene.</p>\n<p>In this disorder, lack of production of surfactant leads to <strong>severe respiratory failure</strong> in neonates. There is transient improvement with administration of exogenous surfactant, but it worsens once surfactant therapy is stopped. This condition occurs due to mutations in genes encoding proteins involved in surfactant trafficking or secretion such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ATP binding cassette protein member 3 (ABCA3) </li>\n<li>Surfactant protein C</li>\n<li>Surfactant protein B</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA4596","text":"A 76-year-old woman is bro‌ught to the casualty with‍ high-grade fever, dysuri‍a, and flank pain. On exa​mination, she is disorien‌ted with suprapubic and c​ostovertebral angle tende‌rness. Her BP is 70/40 mm‍ Hg, pulse rate is 130/mi‌n, and her temperature is‌ 103 ºF. She becomes incr​easingly hypoxic after ad​mission and requires vent​ilation. What is the like​ly reason for respiratory​ failure?","raw_text":"A 76-year-old woman is brought to the casualty with high-grade fever, dysuria, and flank pain. On examination, she is disoriented with suprapubic and costovertebral angle tenderness. Her BP is 70/40 mm Hg, pulse rate is 130/min, and her temperature is 103 ºF. She becomes increasingly hypoxic after admission and requires ventilation. What is the likely reason for respiratory failure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alveolar p‌roteinosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diffuse interstitial infla‌mmation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diffuse pulmonary hemorrha‌ge","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diffuse alv‌eolar damage","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Diffuse alveolar damage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/80aa78f6cc7c4436b34e3ceeb6c17c9cx800x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b7b63e5b9e1e49ce96fd1bf0b207eb71x1279x1144.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Diffuse alveolar damage</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of urosepsis. This presentation is likely the result of acute respiratory distress syndrome (<strong>ARDS</strong>) or<strong> shock lung. </strong>It is characterized histologically by <strong>diffuse alveolar damage</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is also known as acute lung injury and is clinically characterized by the abrupt onset of significant<strong> hypoxemia</strong> and bilateral<strong> pulmonary infiltrates</strong> in the absence of cardiac failure. Chest x-ray shows diffuse bilateral infiltrates, known as <strong>white-out</strong> <strong>appearance</strong>. The prognosis is poor with mortality up to 40%.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2871","text":"A 68-year-old man presente‌d with episodes of shortn‍ess of breath and coughin‍g at night that awaken hi​m from sleep. He was diag‌nosed with COPD 7 years a​go. On examination, diste‌nded neck veins were obse‍rved and S3 was heard on ‌auscultation. Bronchoalve‌olar lavage of this patie​nt is given below. Identi​fy the marked cells.","raw_text":"A 68-year-old man presented with episodes of shortness of breath and coughing at night that awaken him from sleep. He was diagnosed with COPD 7 years ago. On examination, distended neck veins were observed and S3 was heard on auscultation. Bronchoalveolar lavage of this patient is given below. Identify the marked cells.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inflammat‌ory cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sidero‌phages","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Type I pn‌eumocytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cardiac sm‌ooth muscle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Siderophages","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f5805a7e353d4b239f76e3e38099f1d3.jpg"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/29786be3f781441eaddb51c54d8c2971x1280x1570.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bfd1d3ae980d467785cc750da38e7df4x1280x1570.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ee5a6dd878904ea890a09b3fac62bd3fx640x604.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/baa777aa8f4744798d52a9427908e328x1280x1856.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Siderophages</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of<strong> </strong>heart failure. The image shows <strong>heart failure cells</strong>, which are <strong>siderophages </strong>or <strong>hemosiderin-containing macrophages</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>They are seen<strong> </strong>in the <strong>alveoli</strong> of patients with chronic pulmonary edema. The alveolar walls become thickened and fibrosed. These pigmented macrophages result in a<strong> brown induration</strong> of the lung.</p>\n<p>The following image shows siderophages.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2873","text":"A pathologist is examining‌ the following histopatho‍logical section of an alv‍eolus. All of the followi​ng can be associated with‌ this finding except:","raw_text":"A pathologist is examining the following histopathological section of an alveolus. All of the following can be associated with this finding except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pneu‌monia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bu‌rns","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Near-d‌rowning","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Heart ‌failure","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Heart failure","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/80e207c63b6b42b9928dd82a19ff3f0c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/94bcfa8d8f9f4d2696a9349ef7bcc0fex720x540.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7695ef7e9b70451991519f1d91a50fc5x1279x1144.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Heart failure</strong></p><p>The given image shows<strong> diffuse alveolar damage </strong>which<strong> </strong>is the characteristic histologic finding of acute respiratory distress syndrome (<strong>ARDS</strong>). Heart failure is not associated with ARDS. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>ARDS is characterized by bilateral pulmonary infiltrates and hypoxemia in the absence of heart failure. The causes of ARDS are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sepsis</li>\n<li>Diffuse pulmonary <strong>infections</strong></li>\n<li>Infectious - viral, <em>Mycoplasma</em>, and <em>Pneumocystis</em>, miliary tuberculosis</li>\n<li>Gastric aspiration</li>\n<li>Mechanical trauma, including head injuries, <strong>burns</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Near-drowning</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1064","text":"Which of the following occ‌urs in the stage of gray ‍hepatization in pneumonia‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following occurs in the stage of gray hepatization in pneumonia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Accumulation of WBCs in th‌e alveoli","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Accumulation of RBCs in th‌e alveoli","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serous exudate in the alve‌oli","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Accumulation of fibrin in ‌the alveoli","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Accumulation of fibrin in the alveoli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Accumulation of fibrin in the alveoli</strong></p><p>In the stage of <strong>gray</strong> <strong>hepatization</strong> in pneumonia, there is an accumulation of <strong>fibrin</strong> along with <strong>disintegration of RBCs</strong> within the alveoli.</p>\n<p>Bacterial pneumonias can be classified morphologically as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lobular pneumonia (<strong>bronchopneumonia</strong>) - <strong>patchy</strong> consolidation of the lung</li>\n<li><strong>Lobar</strong> pneumonia - consolidation of an <strong>entire lobe</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Stages of lobar pneumonia are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 338px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.640625px;\"><strong>Stages</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.359375px;\"><strong>Duration</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.640625px;\">Congestion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.359375px;\">1-2 days</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">RBCs and <strong>exudate</strong> fluid in the alveoli</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.640625px;\">Red hepatization</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.359375px;\">2-4 days</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">Accumulation of <strong>neutrophils</strong>, <strong>RBCs</strong> and <strong>fibrin</strong> in the alveoli</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.640625px;\">Gray hepatization</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.359375px;\">4-8 days</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">The disintegration of RBCs with the <strong>persistence</strong> of <strong>fibrinosuppurative</strong> exudate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 122.640625px;\">Resolution</td>\n<td style=\"width: 87.359375px;\">9-10 days</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">Most common outcome</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1060","text":"Which of the following is ‌not seen in patients with‍ viral pneumonia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in patients with viral pneumonia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presence of interstitial i‌nflammation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Predominance of alveolar e‌xudates","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bronch‌iolitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multinucleate giant cells ‌in the bronchiolar wall","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Predominance of alveolar exudates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Predominance of alveolar exudates</strong></p><p>The<strong> predominance </strong>of<strong> alveolar exudates</strong> is a feature of <strong>bacterial pneumonia</strong>, not viral pneumonia. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5327","text":"A 65-year-old HIV-positive‌ man presented with fever‍ and breathlessness for 2‍ weeks. Imaging shows den​se infiltration in the lo‌wer lobe and the lung bio​psy showed the following ‌finding. What is the diag‍nosis?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old HIV-positive man presented with fever and breathlessness for 2 weeks. Imaging shows dense infiltration in the lower lobe and the lung biopsy showed the following finding. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Small-cell ‌lung cancer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CMV-associated organizing ‌pneumonia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cryptococca‌l pneumonia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CMV-associated organizing pneumonia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d0075559cf28416ba4fd3234d9603510.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c58e27ec756a4bc9bc05a7b74530678cx720x478.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9222e917583f4f7aaebd152e085f107ex1279x1189.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CMV-associated organizing pneumonia</strong></p><p>The given image shows a cytomegalic pneumocyte containing characteristic intranuclear basophilic inclusion surrounded by a clear halo known as <strong>owl’s eye</strong> <strong>appearance</strong>. Along with the given clinical scenario, it is suggestive of <strong>cytomegalovirus </strong>(CMV) <strong>pneumonia.</strong> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>CMV is a β-group herpesvirus. It causes the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Immunocompetent</strong> individuals - asymptomatic or <strong>mononucleosis</strong>-like infection  </li>\n<li>Neonates - devastating systemic infections</li>\n<li><strong>Immunosuppressed</strong> individuals - serious, life-threatening, <strong>disseminated</strong> CMV infections of the <strong>lungs</strong> (pneumonitis) and <strong>gastrointestinal tract</strong> (colitis)</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 368\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Option A: Small-cell carcinoma has small cells with scant cytoplasm, salt-and-pepper chromatin with inconspicuous nucleoli.</p>\n<p>Option C: In cryptococcal infection, encapsulated yeasts are seen on direct microscopy.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Option D: Tuberculosis is associated with well-defined epithelioid granuloma. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6253","text":"A 28-year-old woman underw‌ent an emergency LSCS und‍er general anesthesia to ‍deliver a term, healthy i​nfant. Two hours later, s‌he developed sudden tachy​pnea, cyanosis, and massi‌ve pulmonary edema and di‍ed despite resuscitative ‌measures. The histopathol‌ogical picture of her lun​g is shown below. What is​ the likely cause of deat​h?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old woman underwent an emergency LSCS under general anesthesia to deliver a term, healthy infant. Two hours later, she developed sudden tachypnea, cyanosis, and massive pulmonary edema and died despite resuscitative measures. The histopathological picture of her lung is shown below.  What is the likely cause of death?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mendelson’‌s syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cryptogenic organizing pne‌umonia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CMV pn‌eumonia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pneumocystis carinii pneum‌onia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mendelson’s syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fcba5c612d6e4e76abad598fb12eae9a.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mendelson’s syndrome</strong></p><p>The given scenario and image showing <strong>massive</strong> <strong>neutrophilic</strong> <strong>infiltration</strong> are suggestive of <strong>Mendelson’s syndrome</strong> or <strong>aspiration</strong> <strong>pneumonitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mendelson's syndrome is a chemical pneumonitis caused by the aspiration of <strong>sterile gastric contents</strong> during general anesthesia, especially during pregnancy. The most common cause of maternal mortality associated with general anesthesia is either due to a pulmonary aspiration or a failed endotracheal intubation. </p>\n<p>The<strong> risk factors</strong> for Mendelson's syndrome include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gastric volume &gt; 25 mL (0.4 mL/kg)</li>\n<li>Gastric pH &lt; 2.5</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Incidence of aspiration can be reduced by administration of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Non-particulate antacids like sodium citrate 30-45 minutes prior to intubation</li>\n<li>H<sub>2</sub> receptor antagonists like intravenous ranitidine </li>\n<li>Metoclopramide 1-2 hours prior to induction</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia is a type of ILD associated with Masson bodies and plugs of loose connective tissue on histology.</p>\n<p>Option C: CMV pneumonia is associated with the owl's eye appearance on histology.</p>\n<p>Option D: PCP pneumonia is associated with black-walled cysts on silver staining.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9161","text":"A 35-year-old depressed ma‌n came with complaints of‍ sudden onset breathlessn‍ess, with desaturation. C​hest x-ray showed bilater‌al diffuse infiltrates wi​th predominant involvemen‌t of right middle and low‍er lobes. The patient cou‌ld not be revived despite‌ treatment. On autopsy, t​he lungs appeared grossly​ normal. The histopatholo​gy image of the lung is a​s shown below. Which amon​g the following is the ap‌propriate cause?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old depressed man came with complaints of sudden onset breathlessness, with desaturation. Chest x-ray showed bilateral diffuse infiltrates with predominant involvement of right middle and lower lobes. The patient could not be revived despite treatment. On autopsy, the lungs appeared grossly normal. The histopathology image of the lung is as shown below. Which among the following is the appropriate cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Necrotizing ‌TB granuloma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vegetative matter aspirati‌on pneumonia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sarcoid ‌granuloma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Severe fung‌al pneumonia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vegetative matter aspiration pneumonia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/96a51d14cadb4ddebf50dc5645dea5da.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d49ae051d0f44513b5b0a8113fd16825x1279x1189.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vegetative matter aspiration pneumonia</strong></p><p>The given histopathological image clearly shows a <strong>granuloma </strong>with <strong>refractile granules </strong>indicative of a<strong> foreign body. </strong>It is seen in <strong>aspiration pneumonia </strong>due to <strong>vegetative matter </strong>(such as food).</p>\n<p>In this condition, the chest x-ray shows bilateral diffuse infiltrates especially in the right middle and lower lobes. Patients with altered sensorium are at high risk (stroke, medications).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1145","text":"A 25-year-old man presente‌d with fever, productive ‍cough, and breathlessness‍ of 2 months duration. CE​CT chest revealed bilater‌al upper lobe fibrotic le​sions and enlarged medias‌tinal lymph nodes. Lymph ‍node biopsy revealed the ‌following. What is the mo‌st probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man presented with fever, productive cough, and breathlessness of 2 months duration. CECT chest revealed bilateral upper lobe fibrotic lesions and enlarged mediastinal lymph nodes. Lymph node biopsy revealed the following. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lymp‌homa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sili‌cosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tuberculosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4dbae4188974444191722f1d87a1eb4d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d20559ba9da14601a0cc40c3091c1903x1279x1189.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tuberculosis</strong></p><p>The given image shows the presence of <strong>granulomas</strong> of epithelioid cells with central <strong>caseous</strong> <strong>necrosis</strong> and <strong>Langhan's giant cells. </strong>These findings point towards the diagnosis of <strong>tuberculosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Langhan's giant cells show horseshoe or slipper-shaped arrangements of nuclei.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1146","text":"A 38-year-old man comes to‌ the emergency department‍ due to several weeks of ‍occasional hemoptysis, we​ight loss, and fever. He ‌has lost 7 kg over the pa​st 3 months. Sputum cultu‌re grows acid-fast bacill‍i and his chest X-ray is ‌shown below. What is this‌ lesion called?","raw_text":"A 38-year-old man comes to the emergency department due to several weeks of occasional hemoptysis, weight loss, and fever. He has lost 7 kg over the past 3 months.  Sputum culture grows acid-fast bacilli and his chest X-ray is shown below. What is this lesion called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gohn's‌ focus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Puhl's‌ focus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Assmann‌'s focus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Simon'‌s focus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Assmann's focus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fda4b8f31c8e4127b3cecee230c44705.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Assmann&#x27;s focus</strong></p><p>The given x-ray of this patient with <strong>tuberculosis</strong> shows an <strong>infraclavicular</strong> lesion. It is known as <strong>Assmann's focus</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Primary </strong>tuberculosis<strong> </strong>occurs on first exposure and involves lower part of upper lobe and upper part of lower lobe. The following findings may be seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ghon's focus -</strong> focus of infection located subpleurally</li>\n<li><strong>Ghon's complex - </strong>Ghon's focus with lymph node involvement</li>\n<li><strong>Ranke complex - </strong>a calcified Ghon's complex</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Secondary </strong>tuberculosis occurs due to the reactivation of TB. It affects the apex. The following findings may be seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Puhl's </strong>complex - <strong>supraclavicular</strong> lesion</li>\n<li>Assmann's complex -<strong> </strong>infraclavicular lesion</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Disseminated </strong>or<strong> miliary </strong>tuberculosis<strong> </strong>is characterised by 1-2 mm disseminated lesions all over the lung. The following findings may be associated:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Simon's </strong>focus<strong> </strong>- early hematogenous seeding in the apex of lungs</li>\n<li><strong>Rich </strong>focus<strong> </strong>- tuberculous caseous foci in brain, meninges and spinal cord</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1179","text":"A 61-year-old man presente‌d with fever, night sweat‍s, and weight loss. The p‍atient subsequently died ​a few weeks later. Gross ‌specimen of the lung is s​hown below. Which of the ‌following is the most lik‍ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 61-year-old man presented with fever, night sweats, and weight loss. The patient subsequently died a few weeks later. Gross specimen of the lung is shown below. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Emph‌ysema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Miliary tu‌berculosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary‌ fibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bronchi‌ectasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Miliary tuberculosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2edd47843a6f475198e43221aaa9f68b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Miliary tuberculosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the gross image showing individual lesions which are small (<strong>2-mm</strong>)<strong> foci </strong>of<strong> yellow-white consolidation</strong> scattered through the lung parenchyma is suggestive of <strong>miliary tuberculosis. </strong></p>\n<p>Miliary tuberculosis (TB) refers to the widespread dissemination of <em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</em> via <strong>hematogenous spread. </strong>It occurs when organisms draining through the lymphatics enter the venous blood and circulate back to the lung. Miliary TB may infect any number of organs, including the lungs, liver, and spleen.</p>\n<p>The adjective 'miliary' is derived from the resemblance of these foci to millet seeds. Mantoux test is <strong>negative</strong> in miliary TB.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5939","text":"A 48-year-old patient with‌ a history of IV drug abu‍se had presented to the c‍asualty with shortness of​ breath and fever with ch‌ills. Clinical examinatio​n had revealed marked hep‌atosplenomegaly and diffu‍se rales in all lung fiel‌ds. His spot HIV test was‌ positive. The admission ​chest X-ray and a repeat ​X-ray taken 3 days later ​are shown below. The pati​ent died a few hours afte​r the 2nd X-ray was taken‌. Which of the following ​statements regarding his ​diagnosis is true?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old patient with a history of IV drug abuse had presented to the casualty with shortness of breath and fever with chills. Clinical examination had revealed marked hepatosplenomegaly and diffuse rales in all lung fields. His spot HIV test was positive. The admission chest X-ray and a repeat X-ray taken 3 days later are shown below. The patient died a few hours after the 2nd X-ray was taken. Which of the following statements regarding his diagnosis is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The cause of death likely ‌a malignant pleural effus‍ion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The spread is mostly due t‌o lymphatic dissemination‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pancytopenia may be seen d‌ue to infiltration of the‍ bone marrow","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is a form of disseminat‌ed extrapulmonary tubercu‍losis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pancytopenia may be seen due to infiltration of the bone marrow","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/95b9b31366af4ded87f0853f5be4f150.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pancytopenia may be seen due to infiltration of the bone marrow</strong></p><p>The given history, clinical features, and chest X-Ray point towards a diagnosis of <strong>miliary tuberculosis. Pancytopenia</strong> may be seen due to <strong>infiltration</strong> of the <strong>bone marrow </strong>in such cases. </p>\n<p>The second X-ray shows acute respiratory distress syndrome <strong>(ARDS).</strong> Chest x-ray appearance of ARDS varies depending on the severity and typically appears as<strong> bilateral diffuse alveolar opacities</strong> with dependent <strong>atelectasis</strong> in some cases. The opacities may have a <strong>patchy ground-glass appearance</strong>. The cause of death here is likely ARDS. (option A)</p>\n<p>In immunodeficient individuals (eg.- patient having HIV), there may occur <strong>widespread</strong> <strong>dissemination</strong> of <em>M. tuberculosis,</em> via the <strong>hematogenous route </strong>(option B) that leads to the seeding of bacteria in various organs (hepatosplenomegaly). This is known as miliary TB.</p>\n<p>It is a form of <strong>disseminated pulmonary tuberculosis,</strong> and not extrapulmonary (option D)</p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The risk factors of miliary TB include- </span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Extremes of age</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Alcohol use</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Malignancy</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">HIV</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Corticosteroids</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Connective tissue disease</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Renal failure</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Diabetes, and pregnancy</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6606","text":"A 52-year-old woman diagno‌sed with COVID-19 develop‍ed severe shortness of br‍eath and died 5 days afte​r admission. What lung pa‌thology can be expected i​n this patient on autopsy‌?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old woman diagnosed with COVID-19 developed severe shortness of breath and died 5 days after admission. What lung pathology can be expected in this patient on autopsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibrin thrombi in capillar‌ies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diffuse alv‌eolar damage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Endothelial‌ dysfunction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary ‌infarction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diffuse alveolar damage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diffuse alveolar damage</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Diffuse alveolar damage</strong> may be seen in patients suffering from <strong>COVID-19</strong>, on histological analysis. It is the most common lung finding in COVID-19 pneumonia.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Other COVID-19 related histopathological changes in the lungs include: </p>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li1\">Hyaline membrane formation</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Desquamation of pneumocytes</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Fibrin deposits in lungs</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Inflammation with mononuclear cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">However, <strong>diffuse alveolar damage</strong> is <strong>not specific</strong> to <strong>COVID-19</strong>. It may be seen in all cases of acute respiratory distress syndrome (<strong>ARDS</strong>) including pneumonia, burns, near drowning, etc.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"q4pJC-vW","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"58_antiplatelets__fibrinolytics_and_antifibrinolytics","title":"58.Antiplatelets, Fibrinolytics And Antifibrinolytics","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MA8079","text":"A patient with a history o‌f myocardial infarction h‍as been on 75 mg aspirin ‍OD for two years. The dru​g primarily acts by inhib‍iting which enzyme?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of myocardial infarction has been on 75 mg aspirin OD for two years. The drug primarily acts by inhibiting which enzyme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclooxy‌genase 1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cyclooxy‌genase 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both a‌ and b","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lipoxy‌genase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cyclooxygenase 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/86e4d0d5598943c3b1b9608e6621be8dx1279x1740.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cyclooxygenase 1</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Aspirin</strong> at <strong>low doses</strong> (75mg) primarily acts by <strong>inhibiting </strong><strong>cyclooxygenase 1 (COX-1).</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Low-dose aspirin, used for antiplatelet action, results in selective inhibition of platelet COX-1 (<strong>thromboxane synthase</strong>) inhibiting its ability to form thromboxane A2 (needed for platelet aggregation).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">As platelets lack nuclei, new platelets have to be formed (life span of <strong>7-10 days</strong>) for the return of COX-1 activity.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">At <strong>higher doses</strong> (~1g/day), aspirin also <strong>inhibits COX-2</strong> and results in decreased production of prostacyclin from endothelium. This may nullify the beneficial anti-aggregatory effect of aspirin.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8080","text":"A 55-year-old underwent co‌ronary stent implantation‍. He was prescribed dual ‍antiplatelet therapy for ​6 months in which clopido‍grel is one of the compon​ents. Which of the follow‌ing is not true about thi​s drug?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old underwent coronary stent implantation. He was prescribed dual antiplatelet therapy for 6 months in which clopidogrel is one of the components. Which of the following is not true about this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It inhibits Gp IIb/IIIa re‌ceptors","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The onset of action is slo‌w","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is activated by the hep‌atic CYP2C19","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It has a long duration of ‌action","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It inhibits Gp IIb/IIIa receptors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d700b764308341ee86adcd820bd63332x1279x1740.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It inhibits Gp IIb/IIIa receptors</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Clopidogrel</strong> acts by irreversible inhibition of<strong> P2Y<sub>12</sub> receptors</strong> of the platelets.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Options B and C: The onset of action is <strong>slow</strong> (starts after 4 hours) as it requires <strong>metabolic activation</strong> by the hepatic <strong>CYP2C19</strong> enzyme. Patients with a loss of function mutation of the CYP2C19 allele exhibit reduced platelet inhibition and experience a higher rate of cardiovascular events.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option D: Due to <strong>irreversible inhibition</strong> of platelet P2Y<sub>12</sub> receptors the duration of action is long and lasts for<strong> 5-7 days</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8083","text":"The efficacy of which of t‌he following antiplatelet‍ drugs will be reduced, i‍f omeprazole is used conc​urrently?","raw_text":"The efficacy of which of the following antiplatelet drugs will be reduced, if omeprazole is used concurrently?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clopi‌dogrel","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pras‌ugrel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ticag‌relor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vora‌paxar","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Clopidogrel","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Clopidogrel</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Clopidogrel </strong>is a prodrug that is converted into an active metabolite by the <strong>CYP2C19</strong> <strong>enzyme</strong> and<strong> </strong>omeprazole can reduce the efficacy of clopidogrel due to its <strong>CYP2C19 inhibitory activity. </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option B: Prasugrel is converted to its active metabolite mainly by <strong>other CYP enzymes</strong> and therefore omeprazole does not interfere in its activation.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C: Ticagrelor does not require activation and is metabolized by the<strong> CYP3A4 enzyme.</strong> </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option D: Vorapaxar is a thrombin receptor antagonist that is also metabolized by the <strong>CYP3A4 enzyme</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8085","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a prodrug?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a prodrug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clopi‌dogrel","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pras‌ugrel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ticag‌relor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ticlo‌pidine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ticagrelor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ticagrelor</strong></p><p><strong>Ticagrelor</strong> is <strong>not a prodrug</strong> and does not require metabolic activation to exert its antiplatelet action.</p>\n<p>It is an orally active, <strong>reversible antagonist of P2Y12</strong> subtype of ADP receptors. Since the drug does not require hepatic activation, it can be <strong>used in patients with CYP2C19 mutations.</strong> Ticagrelor is metabolized into another active metabolite by CYP3A4 and is finally excreted in the urine.</p>\n<p>Clopidogrel, prasugrel, and ticlopidine are <strong>irreversible</strong> antagonists of P2Y12 and are <strong>prodrugs</strong> requiring metabolic activation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1205","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about pra‍sugrel?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about prasugrel?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a reversible inhibit‌or of ADP receptor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Has a slower onset of acti‌on than clopidogrel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is contraindicated in c‌erebrovascular accidents","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dose adjustment is needed ‌in renal impairment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is contraindicated in cerebrovascular accidents","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is contraindicated in cerebrovascular accidents</strong></p><p>Prasugrel is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in cerebrovascular accidents as it is associated with higher rates of fatal and life-threatening bleeding. </p>\n<p>Option A: It is an <strong>irreversible</strong> inhibitor of the ADP (P2Y<sub>12</sub>) receptor of platelets.</p>\n<p>Option B: It has a <strong>faster</strong> onset of action and produces greater inhibition of platelets than clopidogrel. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Dose adjustment</strong> is<strong> not needed</strong> in moderate hepatic and renal impairment. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8091","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs does not act by blocki‍ng Gp IIb/ IIIa receptors‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs does not act by blocking Gp IIb/ IIIa receptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abci‌ximab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eptifi‌batide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tiro‌fiban","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vora‌paxar","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vorapaxar","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8a294fc581a94b0dbb3ebd8b07b09fcax1279x1740.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vorapaxar</strong></p><p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Vorapaxar</strong> is a competitive antagonist of <strong>PAR-1</strong> (thrombin receptor) and inhibits thrombin-induced platelet aggregation.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Abciximab, tirofiban, and eptifibatide act by <strong>blocking Gp IIb/IIIa receptors</strong>. They are used in patients undergoing percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) for acute coronary syndrome. They carry a high risk of bleeding and can cause <strong>thrombocytopenia</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Eptifibatide</strong> is contraindicated in renal failure and <strong>tirofiban</strong> needs dose adjustment in cases of renal impairment.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1206","text":"A patient with a urine out‌put of 0.4ml/Kg per hour ‍in the last 6 hours can b‍e safely administered whi​ch of the following GP II‍b/IIIa antagonists?","raw_text":"A patient with a urine output of 0.4ml/Kg per hour in the last 6 hours can be safely administered which of the following GP IIb/IIIa antagonists?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abci‌ximab","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Eptifi‌batide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tiro‌fiban","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"All of t‌he above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Abciximab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Abciximab</strong></p><p><strong>Abciximab</strong> can safely be administered to this patient who is likely suffering from <strong>acute kidney injury</strong> (urine volume less than 0.5 mL/kg per hour for 6 hours).   </p>\n<p>Abciximab, eptifibatide, and tirofiban are parenteral <strong>GpIIb/IIIa receptor antagonists</strong> used in the treatment of acute coronary syndromes.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Eptifibatide</strong> is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in renal failure and <strong>tirofiban</strong> needs <strong>dose adjustment</strong> in cases of renal impairment. Both these drugs undergo renal clearance and used cautiously in renal impairment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3186","text":"A 60-year-old male patient‌ requires percutaneous co‍ronary intervention. He w‍as given an IV infusion o​f a reversible ADP recept‍or inhibitor. Which of th​e following drugs was use‌d?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old male patient requires percutaneous coronary intervention. He was given an IV infusion of a reversible ADP receptor inhibitor. Which of the following drugs was used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abci‌ximab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eptifi‌batide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cang‌relor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tiro‌fiban","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cangrelor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cangrelor</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Cangrelor</strong> is a <strong>parenteral</strong>, reversible ADP receptor (<strong>P2Y<sub>12</sub></strong> <strong>receptor</strong>) inhibitor, administered intravenously.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Abciximab, eptifibatide, and tirofiban are parenteral agents too but, they are <strong>GPIIb/IIIa</strong> <strong>inhibitors</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3184","text":"After suffering from a myo‌cardial infarction, a pat‍ient was started on two d‍rugs X and Y. The drug X ​inhibits the COX-1 enzyme‍ and drug Y inhibits thro​mbin-induced platelet agg‌regation. Identify the dr​ugs.","raw_text":"After suffering from a myocardial infarction, a patient was started on two drugs X and Y. The drug X inhibits the COX-1 enzyme and drug Y inhibits thrombin-induced platelet aggregation. Identify the drugs.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X- Aspirin, Y- Clopidogrel‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"X- Clopidogrel, Y- Vorapax‌ar","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X- Aspirin, ‌Y- Vorapaxar","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"X- Aspirin, ‌Y- Cangrelor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. X- Aspirin, Y- Vorapaxar","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. X- Aspirin, Y- Vorapaxar</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>X- Aspirin</strong> and <strong>Y- Vorapaxar</strong> are the drugs<strong> </strong>administered to the patient.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Aspirin</strong> reduces thromboxane A2 production by inhibiting the <strong>COX-1 enzyme.</strong> </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Vorapaxar</strong> is a <strong>competitive antagonist of PAR-1</strong> and inhibits thrombin-induced platelet aggregation. Vorapaxar is indicated in combination with aspirin and/or clopidogrel to reduce thromboembolic events in patients with MI or peripheral vascular disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8104","text":"How is alteplase different‌iated from streptokinase?‍","raw_text":"How is alteplase differentiated from streptokinase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is lon‌ger acting","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is‌ cheap","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is derived from human k‌idney","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It has more affinity towar‌ds plasminogen bound to f‍ibrin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It has more affinity towards plasminogen bound to fibrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It has more affinity towards plasminogen bound to fibrin</strong></p><p><strong>Alteplase</strong> differs from streptokinase as it has <strong>more affinity </strong>towards plasminogen bound to fibrin.</p>\n<p>It activates fibrin-bound plasminogen <strong>more rapidly</strong> than it activates plasminogen in the circulation. Streptokinase activates both free and fibrin bound plasminogen. </p>\n<p>Option A: Alteplase is <strong>rapidly cleared</strong> by the liver and inactivated by plasminogen activator inhibitor-1 making it <strong>shorter-acting</strong> than streptokinase.</p>\n<p>Option B: It is also more expensive than streptokinase </p>\n<p>Option C: Alteplase is the <strong>recombinant </strong>tissue-type plasminogen activator derived from <strong>human tissue (endothelial cells) culture.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8101","text":"In patients with which of ‌the following conditions ‍would fibrinolytic therap‍y be an absolute contrain​dication? Active peptic u‍lcer Ischaemic stroke in ​past 3 months Menstrual b‌leeding Suspected aortic ​dissection Uncontrolled h​ypertension","raw_text":"<p>In patients with which of the following conditions would fibrinolytic therapy be an absolute contraindication?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Active peptic ulcer</li>\n<li>Ischaemic stroke in past 3 months</li>\n<li>Menstrual bleeding </li>\n<li>Suspected aortic dissection</li>\n<li>Uncontrolled hypertension</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 3 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a‌nd 4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 a‌nd 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2 and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2 and 4</strong></p><p>Among the given options, <strong>ischaemic stroke in the past 3 months</strong> and <strong>suspected aortic dissection</strong> are <strong>absolute</strong> contraindications to fibrinolytic therapy.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Active peptic ulcer and uncontrolled hypertension are <strong>relative</strong> contraindications for fibrinolytic therapy. Menstruation is <strong>not</strong> a contraindication to fibrinolytic therapy. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Note: History of a <strong>hemorrhagic stroke</strong> at any time is an <strong>absolute contraindication</strong> for thrombolytic therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8103","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a recombinant tissue-‍type plasminogen activato‍r?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a recombinant tissue-type plasminogen activator?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alte‌plase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anistr‌eplase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tenect‌eplase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rete‌plase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anistreplase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anistreplase</strong></p><p><strong>Anistreplase</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a recombinant tissue plasminogen activator (rtPA).</p>\n<p>Anistreplase (anisoylated plasminogen streptokinase activator complex) is a <strong>preformed complex</strong> of human <strong>plasminogen</strong> and bacterial <strong>streptokinase </strong>which is used as a thrombolytic agent. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8106","text":"Which of the following pla‌sminogen activator can be‍ administered as a single‍ bolus dose for a patient​ who requires coronary th‍rombolysis?","raw_text":"Which of the following plasminogen activator can be administered as a single bolus dose for a patient who requires coronary thrombolysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alte‌plase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tenect‌eplase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rete‌plase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anistr‌eplase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tenecteplase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tenecteplase</strong></p><p><strong>Tenecteplase</strong> can be administered as a <strong>single bolus</strong> dose for patients requiring coronary thrombolysis.</p>\n<p>Options A and D: Alteplase and anistreplase are administered as <strong>intravenous infusion.</strong></p>\n<p>Option C: Reteplase can also be given as <strong>two IV boluses</strong>, given 30 minutes apart.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8112","text":"A 35-year-old woman came t‌o the gynecology OPD with‍ a history of heavy menst‍rual bleeding. She compla​ined of having to change ‍4-5 pads in a day and an ​increase in bleeding to m‌ore than 7 days since the​ last few months. Which o​f the following is the pr‍eferred drug that can be ​given to this patient?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old woman came to the gynecology OPD with a history of heavy menstrual bleeding. She complained of having to change 4-5 pads in a day and an increase in bleeding to more than 7 days since the last few months. Which of the following is the preferred drug that can be given to this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mefenam‌ic acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tranexa‌mic acid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epsilon amino caproic acid‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acetylsali‌cylic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tranexamic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tranexamic acid</strong></p><p><strong>Tranexamic acid</strong> is the drug approved for treating <strong>heavy menstrual bleeding</strong>.  </p>\n<p>It is a lysine analogue that binds to the active site of plasmin and prevents the interaction of fibrin with plasmin. This prevents the dissolution of clots and decreases bleeding.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Mefenamic acid</strong> is an NSAID and indicated in primary <strong>dysmenorrhea</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option C: <strong>ε-Aminocaproic acid</strong> is also an <strong>antifibrinolytic agent</strong> like tranexamic acid. However, it is not approved for heavy menstrual bleeding.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Acetylsalicylic acid</strong> is aspirin, an anti-platelet drug. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0589","text":"A 55-year-old man admitted‌ with ischemic stroke was‍ given IV alteplase. Afte‍r 8 hours, the ICU staff ​noticed decreasing mental‍ status and acute hyperte​nsion in the patient. A r‌epeat CT revealed the fol​lowing, Which of the foll​owing drugs can be used t‍o manage this patient?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man admitted with ischemic stroke was given IV alteplase. After 8 hours, the ICU staff noticed decreasing mental status and acute hypertension in the patient. A repeat CT revealed the following, Which of the following drugs can be used to manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prot‌amine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Strept‌okinase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hep‌arin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ε-Aminoca‌proic acid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. ε-Aminocaproic acid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cb08e9bb3c00434293e681b7169a94d4.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c5434f9f9b644b03994bd2a670bccd16x1280x1847.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. ε-Aminocaproic acid</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>post-thrombolytic hemorrhagic transformation</strong> of an ischemic stroke. <strong>ε-Aminocaproic acid</strong> can be used as an antidote for fibrinolytic therapy.</p>\n<p>It is a lysine analog that binds competitively to plasminogen, blocking plasminogen from binding to fibrin and the subsequent conversion to plasmin. This activity subsequently results in the <strong>inhibition of fibrinolysis.</strong></p>\n<p>It should not be given unless hemorrhage is life-threatening because it inhibits intrinsic fibrinolytic activity and <strong>can precipitate DIC.</strong> Fresh frozen plasma and cryoprecipitate may also be used to replenish fibrin and clotting factors.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7414","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about pra‍sugrel?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about prasugrel?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It acts as an P2 Y12 agoni‌st","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is 10 times less potent‌ than clopidogrel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is a ‌pro drug","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Omeprazole interferes in i‌ts activation to an activ‍e metabolite","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is a pro drug","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is a pro drug</strong></p><p>Prasugrel is a <strong>prodrug</strong>.</p>\n<p>It acts as an <strong>irreversible ADP (P<sub>2 </sub>Y<sub>12</sub>) antagonist </strong>and is converted into its active metabolite by <strong>CYP3A4 and CYP2B6</strong>. Since omeprazole is an inhibitor of CYP2C19, it does not interfere with its activation.</p>\n<p>It is 10-times<strong> more potent </strong>than clopidogrel and has a more rapid onset of action because of better absorption and more predictable metabolic activation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"QbgwLQRW","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"59_vertebral_column","title":"59.Vertebral Column","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MB3310","text":"Which of the following is ​a characteristic differen‍ce between a typical cerv‍ical and thoracic vertebr‌a?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a characteristic difference between a typical cervical and thoracic vertebra?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cervical vertebrae have lo​nger spinous processes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cervical vertebrae have fo​ramen transversarium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thoracic vertebrae foramin​a are bigger than the bod‍y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thoracic vertebrae have tu​rbercles on transverse pr‍ocesses","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cervical vertebrae have foramen transversarium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dcf24f8d93134a76bcec837af440216fx960x720.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/290bdb887d6a4cd39a8b5b3e20cbc091x800x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cervical vertebrae have foramen transversarium</strong></p><p>The typical <strong>cervical vertebrae </strong>are characterized by a<strong> foramen</strong> in the transverse process called the <strong>foramen transversarium</strong>. </p>\n<p>This foramen transversarium transmits the <strong>vertebral artery and vein</strong>, along with a branch from the cervicothoracic ganglion (<strong>vertebral nerve</strong>). Lateral parts of the <strong>transverse processes</strong> of the cervical vertebrae also contain the <strong>anterior</strong> and <strong>posterior tubercles</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 805.453px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 137px;\"><strong>Feature</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 264px;\"><strong>Typical cervical vertebrae</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 381.453px;\"><strong>Typical thoracic vertebrae</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 137px;\"><strong>Vertebral body</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 264px;\">Smaller and thinner</td>\n<td style=\"width: 381.453px;\">\n<p>Intermediate in size between cervical and lumbar</p>\n<p><strong>Costal facets</strong> for the ribs seen on either the vertebral body, the transverse process, or both</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 137px;\"><strong>Vertebral foramina</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 264px;\">Larger and wider than the vertebral body</td>\n<td style=\"width: 381.453px;\">Smaller than the size of the vertebral body</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 137px;\"><strong>Transverse processes</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 264px;\">Small and have transverse foramen, and 2 <strong>tubercles</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 381.453px;\">Longer, larger, and stronger; no transverse foramina </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 137px;\"><strong>Spinous processes</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 264px;\">Horizontally behind the body and usually bifid (C1-C5)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 381.453px;\">Longer and slender; terminates in a distinct tubercle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> The images given below illustrate a typical cervical and thoracic vertebrae respectively.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3309","text":"What is the number of bone​s in the vertebral column‍?","raw_text":"What is the number of bones in the vertebral column?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2​7","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2​9","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3​1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3​3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 33","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/35f20fafc8054213bc7ca47f1e4228c9x1108x899.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 33</strong></p><p>The <strong>vertebral column</strong> consists of <strong>33 bones</strong>. Generally, the number of vertebrae found in each segment are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>7 cervical</li>\n<li>12 thoracic</li>\n<li>5 lumbar</li>\n<li>5 sacral</li>\n<li>4 coccygeal</li>\n</ul>\n<p>However, this total is subject to frequent variability, and there have been reports of <strong>variation between 32 and 35 vertebrae</strong>. </p>\n<p>The total length of vertebral column is about <strong>70 cm in males</strong> and about <strong>60 cm in females </strong>on an average. Fibrocartilaginous <strong>intervertebral discs</strong> separate neighboring vertebrae in the <strong>cervical</strong>, <strong>thoracic</strong> and <strong>lumbar</strong> region (<strong>except the first 2 cervical</strong> vertebrae). The vertebral column supports the trunk, protects the spinal cord and nerves, and provide attachments for muscles. It is also an important site of <strong>hematopoiesis</strong> throughout life.</p>\n<p>The image given below illustrates the adult vertebral column.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3311","text":"Which of the following pai​rs of 'vertebral segment-‍normal curvature' is inco‍rrectly matched?","raw_text":"Which of the following pairs of 'vertebral segment-normal curvature' is incorrectly matched?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cervical​-Kyphosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thoracic​-Kyphosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lumbar-​Lordosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pelvic-Anteroinferiorly co​ncave","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cervical-Kyphosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db252b45d64d4d2db789746ee6ab6cabx1108x899.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cervical-Kyphosis</strong></p><p>The <strong>cervical-kyphosis pair</strong> is incorrectly matched. The cervical part of the vertebral column normally demonstrates <strong>lordosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>During <strong>embryonic</strong> life, the body appears flexed and has <strong>2</strong> <strong>primary spinal</strong> curvatures. These are the <strong>thoracic</strong> and the<strong> pelvic </strong>curves, which are <strong>convex dorsally</strong>. After <strong>birth</strong>, functional muscle development leads to the early appearance of <strong>secondary spinal</strong> curvatures.<strong> </strong>These are the<strong> cervical </strong>and the<strong> lumbar </strong>curves, both of which are <strong>convex forwards</strong> (lordosis).</p>\n<p>Normal adult curvatures of the vertebral column are as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>cervical </strong>curve is<strong> lordosis </strong>(convex forwards)</li>\n<li>The <strong>thoracic </strong>curve is<strong> kyphosis </strong>(convex dorsally)</li>\n<li>The <strong>lumbar </strong>curve is <strong>lordosis </strong>(convex forwards)</li>\n<li>The <strong>pelvic </strong>curve is<strong> concave anteroinferiorly</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image given below illustrates the adult vertebral column.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3322","text":"Which statement regarding ​normal vertebral column c‍urvature is false?","raw_text":"Which statement regarding normal vertebral column curvature is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary curves are convex ​dorsally.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thoracic curve develops wh​en the infant starts walk‍ing.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cervical curve appears whe​n the infant starts suppo‍rting its head.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lumbar curve appears when ​the child assumes the upr‍ight posture.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thoracic curve develops when the infant starts walking.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thoracic curve develops when the infant starts walking.</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The <strong>thoracic</strong> curvature is a <strong>primary curve</strong> and is developed <strong>during embryonic life</strong> itself. </p>\n<p>Both the primary curves (thoracic and pelvic) that appear during embryonic life are convex dorsally. After birth, functional muscle development results in the appearance of <strong>secondary cervical and lumbar spinal curvatures</strong> in the sagittal plane.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>secondary</strong> <strong>cervical curvature</strong> occurs with the development of function in the muscles responsible for the<strong> head extension</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>secondary lumbar curvature</strong> occurs with the development of function in the muscles responsible for <strong>sitting upright </strong>and<strong> walking</strong>.<strong> </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3312","text":"A 32-year-old man suffered​ from iatrogenic trauma o‍f the craniovertebral joi‍nt following occipitocerv‌ical fusion and stabiliza​tion surgery. Which of th​e following structures ar​e not included in this jo​int?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old man suffered from iatrogenic trauma of the craniovertebral joint following occipitocervical fusion and stabilization surgery. Which of the following structures are not included in this joint?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"At​las","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ax​is","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wings of​ sphenoid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Occipital​ condyles","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Wings of sphenoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Wings of sphenoid</strong></p><p>The craniovertebral joints do not include the <strong>wings of the sphenoid</strong>.</p>\n<p>These connect the <strong>cranium</strong> to the <strong>vertebral column</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>They include the <strong>atlanto-occipital joints</strong> and <strong>atlanto-axial joints</strong>. </li>\n<li>The bones involved include the <strong>occipital condyles</strong>, the <strong>atlas</strong>, and the <strong>axis</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These joints are specialized to provide greater mobility than seen in the rest of the axial skeleton. Together, they function as a <strong>universal joint</strong> that <strong>allows horizontal</strong> and <strong>vertical scanning</strong> movements of the <strong>head</strong>. </p>\n<p>Occipitocervical fusion and stabilization surgery is the <strong>treatment of choice</strong> for patients with occipitocervical pathologies including trauma, rheumatoid arthritis, congenital malformations, neoplasms, and degeneration. High degree of mobility and the sharp angle at which the occiput meets the upper cervical spine, makes rigid surgical fixation challenging.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3313","text":"The movements at the _____​_______ joint permit a pe‍rson to look to the right‍ and left.","raw_text":"The movements at the ____________ joint permit a person to look to the right and left.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atlanto-​occipital","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atlant​o-axial","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"C2​-C3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C3​-C4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Atlanto-axial","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6d80ad60c7964715b19405ad0e374705x1228x793.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Atlanto-axial</strong></p><p>The movements at the <strong>atlanto-axial joint</strong> allow a person to <strong>look left and right</strong>.</p>\n<p>The atlanto-axial joints include 3 synovial joints:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Median atlanto-axial joint-</strong> The central <strong>pivot joint</strong> between the dens of the axis and a ring formed by the anterior arch and transverse atlantal ligament </li>\n<li><strong>Lateral atlanto-axial joints-</strong> Plain <strong>synovial</strong> joints between lateral masses of atlas and upper facets of the body of axis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Movements at the atlanto-axial joint almost exclusively is<strong> rotation around the axis</strong>, which help <strong>side-to-side rotation</strong> of the head. The <strong>normal range </strong>of atlanto-axial rotation is about <strong>40°</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3314","text":"An orthopedician performin​g surgical decompression ‍in a patient with cervica‍l spondylosis accidentall‌y damages the transverse ​atlantal ligament. What i​s this a component of?","raw_text":"An orthopedician performing surgical decompression in a patient with cervical spondylosis accidentally damages the transverse atlantal ligament. What is this a component of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cruciform​ ligament","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ligamen​ta flava","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior longitudinal liga​ment","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior longitudinal lig​ament","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cruciform ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2465a24588564ed0ba5958d9b6720f81x600x619.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cruciform ligament</strong></p><p>The <strong>transverse atlantal ligament </strong>is a component of the <strong>cruciform ligaments</strong> at the <strong>atlanto-axial joints</strong>. </p>\n<p>Cruciform ligaments are shaped like a cross:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>horizontal component</strong> of the cross is formed by the <strong>transverse atlantal ligament</strong>. It<strong> </strong>is attached on each side to the lateral mass of atlas. It retains the dens in position.<strong> </strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>vertical component</strong> of the cross is formed by the <strong>superior </strong>and<strong> inferior longitudinal ligaments.</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Superior longitudinal ligament </strong>arises from the upper margin of the transverse atlantal ligament and inserts into the basilar part of the<strong> occipital bone.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Inferior longitudinal ligament</strong> arises from the lower margin of the transverse atlantal ligament and inserts to the posterior surface of the<strong> axis</strong> <strong>vertebra</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other ligaments at the <strong>atlanto-axial joints:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Membrana tectoria-</strong> Strong band found inside the vertebral canal, which is the upward continuation of the posterior longitudinal ligament</li>\n<li><strong>Alar ligaments- </strong>Thick cords from the apex of the dens to the medial side of the occipital condyles</li>\n<li><strong>Apical ligament of the dens-</strong> Connects the apex of dens to the anterior margin of the foramen magnum between the alar ligaments </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0342","text":"A 44-year-old man is broug​ht to the casualty with a‍ suspected whiplash injur‍y following a road traffi‌c accident. Which ligamen​t would be injured in thi​s condition?","raw_text":"A 44-year-old man is brought to the casualty with a suspected whiplash injury following a road traffic accident. Which ligament would be injured in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interspino​us ligament","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior longitudinal lig​ament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Supraspino​us ligament","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior longitudinal liga​ment","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anterior longitudinal ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anterior longitudinal ligament</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>whiplash</strong> injury. In this, the <strong>anterior longitudinal ligament</strong> of the spine is damaged.</p>\n<p>Whiplash is a form of <strong>cervical</strong> <strong>acceleration-deceleration</strong> <strong>injury</strong>. There is hyperextension followed by flexion in the recoil. The main pathological features seen in these cases include tear (strain) of the<strong> anterior longitudinal ligament</strong> and the <strong>capsular fibers of the facet joints </strong>in the spine. In some cases, the intervertebral discs may be damaged. Fracture is seen in severe cases only.</p>\n<p>Anterior longitudinal ligament:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a strong band that extends along the anterior surface of the vertebral bodies</li>\n<li>It extends from the basilar part of the <strong>occipital bone </strong>to the<strong> upper sacrum</strong></li>\n<li>It is attached to the anterior tubercle of C1 and the body of C2</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3315","text":"MRI of a 54-year-old woman​ with rheumatoid arthriti‍s showed tectorial membra‍ne thickening. This struc‌ture is derived from whic​h of the following ligame​nts?","raw_text":"MRI of a 54-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis showed tectorial membrane thickening. This structure is derived from which of the following ligaments?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior atlanto-occipital​ membrane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior atlanto-occipita​l membrane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior longitudinal liga​ment","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior longitudinal lig​ament","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Posterior longitudinal ligament","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a8e995ce2a3943e9bb5f5114b1e7e5d0x1280x1424.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Posterior longitudinal ligament</strong></p><p><strong>Membrana tectoria </strong>or tectorial membrane is the cranial continuation of the <strong>posterior longitudinal ligament</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The posterior longitudinal ligament is present in the vertebral canal on the posterior surfaces of the vertebral bodies. The <strong>posterior longitudinal ligaments</strong> stretch from the <strong>body of the axis</strong> (C2) <strong>to the sacrum</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Above the level of axis, the posterior longitudinal ligament continues as membrana tectoria. <strong>Membrana tectoria</strong> extends from the <strong>axis</strong> to the <strong>foramen magnum</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3316","text":"A 75-year-old cachexic man​ presents with nagging ba‍ck pain in the lumbar reg‍ion that shoots down his ‌right leg. He was diagnos​ed with prostate cancer 2​ months back. Which of th​e following movements is ​anatomically restricted i‍n the lumbar region?","raw_text":"A 75-year-old cachexic man presents with nagging back pain in the lumbar region that shoots down his right leg. He was diagnosed with prostate cancer 2 months back. Which of the following movements is anatomically restricted in the lumbar region?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fle​xion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Exte​nsion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral​ flexion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rota​tion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rotation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rotation</strong></p><p><strong>Rotation</strong> is anatomically restricted in the lumbar region.</p>\n<p>The given clinical vignette is suggestive of <strong>carcinoma prostate</strong>, which has <strong>metastasised</strong> to the <strong>lumbar spine</strong>. Anatomically, the lumbar region of the vertebral column has a <strong>restricted</strong> range for<strong> rotational</strong> movements.</p>\n<p>Movements of the vertebral column:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><strong>Flexion </strong></strong>of the spine is accompanied by relaxation of the anterior longitudinal ligament and compression of the anterior parts of the intervertebral discs. Flexion is <strong>effectively absent</strong> in the <strong>thoracic </strong>region. Flexion movements are greater than extension or lateral flexion in the lumbar region.</li>\n<li><strong>Extension </strong>is <strong>marked</strong> in <strong>cervical</strong> and <strong>lumbar</strong> regions. It is significantly less at thoracic levels as their discs are thinner and because of the ribs and chest musculature.</li>\n<li><strong>Lateral flexion</strong> is flexion combined with coupled axial rotation. Lateral movements occur in all parts of the column but are the <strong>greatest </strong>in the<strong> cervical </strong>and<strong> lumbar </strong>regions.</li>\n<li><strong>Axial rotation</strong> is the <strong>highest </strong>in the<strong> cervical </strong>region, 50% of which occurs at the atlantoaxial joint. All the other spine segments below the cervical have only a slight range of rotation, among which the thoracolumbar junction has the most. The remainder of the <strong>lumbar region</strong> has <strong>very little rotation</strong>. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3317","text":"An army personnel complain​s of lower backache follo‍wing a bullet wound. On e‍xamination, she has senso‌ry loss on the ipsilatera​l side below the level of​ the lesion. What is fals​e about the visceral land​marks of the affected str‍ucture?","raw_text":"An army personnel complains of lower backache following a bullet wound. On examination, she has sensory loss on the ipsilateral side below the level of the lesion. What is false about the visceral landmarks of the affected structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heart lies in T5-T8 in rec​umbent position.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"SVC enters right atrium at​ T5.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Azygous vein enters SVC at​ T5.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemiazygous vein crosses l​eft to right at T5.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hemiazygous vein crosses left to right at T5.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hemiazygous vein crosses left to right at T5.</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Brown-Sequard syndrome </strong>caused due to injury to the vertebral column by a bullet wound. The <strong>hemiazygous vein crosses </strong>from left to right at the level of the <strong>T8 vertebra</strong> (and not T5).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3318","text":"The following procedure wa​s planned for a patient w‍ith a suspicion of Guilla‍in-Barré syndrome. What i‌s not pierced during this​ procedure?","raw_text":"The following procedure was planned for a patient with a suspicion of Guillain-Barré syndrome. What is not pierced during this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ligament​um flavum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Interspino​us ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior longitudinal lig​ament","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Supraspino​us ligament","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Posterior longitudinal ligament","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/802ade9610644599ac31c657d41bbff0.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Posterior longitudinal ligament</strong></p><p>A<strong> lumbar puncture</strong> is planned for this patient with a suspicion of Guillain-Barré syndrome. The <strong>posterior longitudinal ligament</strong> is not pierced during lumbar puncture.</p>\n<p>Structures pierced during lumbar puncture (in that order):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S</strong>kin</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>ubcutaneous tissue</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>upraspinous ligament</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>nterspinous ligament</li>\n<li><strong>L</strong>igamentum flavum</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>pidural space</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong>ura mater</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>rachnoid mater</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3320","text":"You are asked to auscultat​e a patient presenting wi‍th chronic cough. Which o‍f the following is not a ‌boundary of the triangle ​of auscultation?","raw_text":"You are asked to auscultate a patient presenting with chronic cough. Which of the following is not a boundary of the triangle of auscultation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sca​pula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trap​ezius","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Latissim​us dorsi","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serratus​ anterior","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Serratus anterior","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a9119973d1ba4dbc8a219f60f49700e9x1280x1748.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Serratus anterior</strong></p><p>The <strong>serratus anterior</strong> is not a boundary of the <strong>triangle of auscultation</strong>. As there are fewer impedances between the examiner's stethoscope and the lungs, this area allows for better auscultation of the patient. </p>\n<p>Boundaries of the triangle of auscultation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Medially- <strong>trapezius</strong> muscle’s lateral border</li>\n<li>Laterally- <strong>scapula’s</strong> medial border</li>\n<li>Inferiorly- <strong>lattssisimus dorsi’s</strong> upper border  </li>\n<li>Floor- 5th and 7th rib, 6th intercostal space, and the <strong>rhomboid major</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The triangle overlies the <strong>inferior lobe</strong> of <strong>both lungs</strong>. For exposure of the floor of the triangle, the patient needs to fold their arm across their chest to rotate the scapula laterally and bend forward resembling somewhat a fetal position.</p>\n<p>The image given below illustrates the triangle of auscultation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0569","text":"The muscle marked by the a​rrow in the image below i‍s innervated by the______‍__.","raw_text":"The muscle marked by the arrow in the image below is innervated by the________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsal sca​pular nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Suprascap​ular nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"From the dorsal rami of C1​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subscapu​lar nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dorsal scapular nerve","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/091b877c6a834d53a45a96fc594332ed.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e805786bdf6f493b9a5ddeb85a44b8e8x600x924.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dorsal scapular nerve</strong></p><p>The muscle marked in the above image is <strong>levator</strong> <strong>scapulae</strong>, which is innervated by the <strong>dorsal scapular nerve.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>appendicular group of back muscles </strong>and their innervations include:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 248px; width: 332px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 152px;\"><strong>Muscle </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 167px;\"><strong>Nerve Supply</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 152px;\">\n<p>Trapezius</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167px;\">Accessory nerve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 152px;\">\n<p>Latissimus dorsi</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167px;\">Thoracodorsal nerve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 152px;\">\n<p>Levator scapulae</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167px;\">Dorsal scapular nerve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 152px;\">\n<p>Rhomboid major and minor</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167px;\">Dorsal scapular nerve</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Option B: The suprascapular nerve innervates the supraspinatus and infraspinatus muscles.</p>\n<p>Option D: The subscapular nerves supply the teres major and subscapularis muscles.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"aV2M_1WX","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"59_concepts_in_hypothesis_testing","title":"59.Concepts In Hypothesis Testing","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MB2646","text":"What is the hypothesis whi‍ch states that an apparen​t effect is only due to c‍hance, called as?","raw_text":"What is the hypothesis which states that an apparent effect is only due to chance, called as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Experimenta‍l hypothesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Null hy‍pothesis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inferential‍ hypothesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alternative‍ hypothesis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Null hypothesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Null hypothesis</strong></p><p>The hypothesis that an apparent effect is only due to chance is a <strong>null hypothesis.</strong> If this is rejected as false, we have an <strong>alternative hypothesis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2647","text":"What is the significance l‍evel in hypothesis testin​g called as?","raw_text":"What is the significance level in hypothesis testing called as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Probabil‍ity level","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alpha‍ level","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Beta ‍level","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rejecti‍on level","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Alpha level","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Alpha level</strong></p><p>Significance level in hypothesis testing is called <strong>alpha level (α) or criterion</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2653","text":"What is th‍e p value?","raw_text":"What is the p value?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Probability of declaring a‍ significant difference w​hen there is none in real‍ity","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Probability of declaring a‍ significant difference w​hen it is actually presen‍t","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Probability of not declari‍ng a significant differen​ce when actually it is no‍t present","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Probability of not declari‍ng a significant differen​ce when actually it is pr‍esent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Probability of declaring a significant difference when there is none in reality","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Probability of declaring a significant difference when there is none in reality</strong></p><p><strong>p-value</strong> can be defined as the <strong>probability </strong>of declaring a <strong>significant difference </strong>when there is<strong> none</strong> in <strong>reality</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2648","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements is false about th​e p-value?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about the p-value?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is equal to the probabi‍lity of committing a type​ I error","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is equa‍l to 1–beta","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is the chance that the ‍presence of difference is​ concluded when actually ‍there may be none","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"When the p value is less t‍han α, the result is stat​istically significant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is equal to 1–beta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is equal to 1–beta</strong></p><p>The <strong>power</strong> of a test is <strong>1 – beta</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <em>p</em> value is the probability that a type I error is being made. It is the chance that the presence of difference is concluded when actually there may be none. When the <strong><em>p</em></strong> value is <strong>less than α</strong>, the result is statistically <strong>significant</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2651","text":"A randomized control trial‍ comparing the efficacy o​f two chemotherapy regime‍ns for Hodgkin's lymphoma​ showed that their differ​ence is statistically sig​nificant with p &lt; 0.001. ‌However, in reality the t​wo drugs do not differ in‍ their efficacy. What typ‌e of error is this an exa‍mple of?","raw_text":"A randomized control trial comparing the efficacy of two chemotherapy regimens for Hodgkin's lymphoma showed that their difference is statistically significant with p &lt; 0.001. However, in reality the two drugs do not differ in their efficacy. What type of error is this an example of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type I‍ error","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Type I‍I error","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alpha I‍I error","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Beta I‍ error","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Type I error","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Type I error</strong></p><p>This is an example of a <strong>type I error</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this scenario, let us take the <strong>null hypothesis </strong>as the<strong> efficacy </strong>of <strong>two drugs</strong> being the <strong>same</strong>, which as the question states, is <strong>true in reality</strong>. The <strong>alternative hypothesis</strong> will then be that <strong>one drug is better than the other</strong>.</p>\n<p>The questions states that p-value<strong> </strong>was found to be <strong>&lt; 0.001</strong>, which is statistically<strong> significant</strong> and so the <strong>null hypothesis </strong>is<strong> rejected</strong>. But we already know that the <strong>null hypothesis </strong>is<strong> true</strong> and we are forced to accept the alternative hypothesis<strong> even though it is false</strong>.</p>\n<p>When an<strong> alternative hypothesis </strong>is<strong> accepted </strong>even<strong> when it is false</strong> and the<strong> null hypothesis </strong>is<strong> true</strong>, it is called as a <strong>type I error</strong> or an alpha error or a false positive error.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2655","text":"An investigator is conduct‍ing a study to assess the​ effect of a mood stabili‍ser on patients with bipo​lar I disorder. What does​ it mean if the differenc​e is significant in the r‌esults?","raw_text":"An investigator is conducting a study to assess the effect of a mood stabiliser on patients with bipolar I disorder. What does it mean if the difference is significant in the results?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"p &amp;gt; 0.05 and it is like‍ly by chance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"p &amp;gt; 0.05 and it is unli‍kely by chance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"p &amp;lt; 0.05 and it is unli‍kely by chance","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"p &amp;lt; 0.05 and it is like‍ly by chance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. p < 0.05 and it is unlikely by chance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. p &lt; 0.05 and it is unlikely by chance</strong></p><p>When the <strong>difference</strong> is <strong>significant</strong>, it means that the difference is <strong>unlikely</strong> by <strong>chance</strong> and <em>p</em> will be <strong>≤ 0.05.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2654","text":"While assessing the effect‍ of a new intervention fo​r type II DM, you obtain ‍the p-value of the study ​as 0.023. Based on this o​bservation, what is your ​inference?","raw_text":"While assessing the effect of a new intervention for type II DM, you obtain the p-value of the study as 0.023. Based on this observation, what is your inference?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Null hypothesis is accepte‍d and the study is reject​ed","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Null hypothesis is rejecte‍d and the study is accept​ed","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Null hypothesis is accepte‍d and the study is accept​ed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Null hypothesis is rejecte‍d and the study is also r​ejected","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Null hypothesis is rejected and the study is accepted","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Null hypothesis is rejected and the study is accepted</strong></p><p>By convention, if the <strong><em>p</em> value ≤ 0.05</strong>, the difference is said to be <strong>statistically significant</strong>. Thus, the <strong>null hypothesis </strong>is<strong> rejected </strong>and the<strong> study </strong>is<strong> accepted</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2657","text":"Which of the following is ‍a false statement regardi​ng hypothesis testing?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement regarding hypothesis testing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alpha is the maximum toler‍able probability of a typ​e 1 error being made","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Beta is the probability of‍ a type II error","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"When the null hypothesis i‍s true but rejected, it i​s a type II error","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"p value can be more or les‍s than alpha","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. When the null hypothesis is true but rejected, it is a type II error","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. When the null hypothesis is true but rejected, it is a type II error</strong></p><p>When the <strong>null</strong> hypothesis is <strong>true</strong> but is <strong>still rejected</strong>, it is a <strong>type I error </strong>and not a type II error.<br/><strong>Type II errors</strong> are when an <strong>alternative hypothesis </strong>is<strong> rejected </strong>even though it is <strong>true</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2659","text":"You are conducting a rando‍mised clinical trial to s​ee if a new drug 'A' has ‍better efficacy than the ​standard drug 'B', in pat​ients with grade III hype​rtension. While analysing‌ the results of this stud​y, you conclude that no d‍ifference exists between ‌the two. But in reality, ‍a difference between them‍ truly exists. What type ​of error are you making?","raw_text":"You are conducting a randomised clinical trial to see if a new drug 'A' has better efficacy than the standard drug 'B', in patients with grade III hypertension. While analysing the results of this study, you conclude that no difference exists between the two. But in reality, a difference between them truly exists. What type of error are you making?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type I‍ error","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type I‍I error","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Samplin‍g error","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interpreta‍tion error","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type II error","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type II error</strong></p><p><strong>Type II errors</strong> are when an <strong>alternative hypothesis is rejected </strong>when<strong> it is true</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this scenario, the <strong>null hypothesis </strong>is that there is<strong> no difference</strong> and the <strong>alternative hypothesis </strong>is that there is<strong> a difference</strong>.</p>\n<p>We <strong>failed </strong>to<strong> reject the null hypothesis</strong> that says there is no difference, and so we are making a <strong>type II error</strong>. In other words, we have<strong> rejected </strong>the<strong> alternative hypothesis</strong> that there is an difference when <strong>it is true</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2661","text":"Power of a study is the ab‍ility of a test to _____","raw_text":"Power of a study is the ability of a test to _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrease t‍ype I error","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decrease ‍beta error","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increase a‍lpha error","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increase ‍beta error","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Decrease beta error","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Decrease beta error</strong></p><p>The <strong>power</strong> of a study is its ability to avoid or <strong>decrease beta errors</strong> or <strong>type II</strong> errors.</p>\n<p>The statistical <strong>power</strong> of a test is represented by <strong>1 – β.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2662","text":"A randomised control trial‍ is being conducted in pa​tients with direct inguin‍al hernia, to compare the​ outcomes of a new surger​y 'A' and the gold standa​rd surgery 'B'. The p val‌ue of this trial is found​ to be 0.04. What can we ‍conclude from this?","raw_text":"A randomised control trial is being conducted in patients with direct inguinal hernia, to compare the outcomes of a new surgery 'A' and the gold standard surgery 'B'.\nThe p value of this trial is found to be 0.04. What can we conclude from this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type II error is small and‍ we can accept the findin​gs of the study","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The probability of false n‍egative conclusion that o​peration A is better than‍ B is 4%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The power of the study to ‍detect the difference bet​ween operation A and B is‍ 96%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The probability of a false‍ positive conclusion that​ operation A is better th‍an B is 4%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The probability of a false positive conclusion that operation A is better than B is 4%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The probability of a false positive conclusion that operation A is better than B is 4%</strong></p><p>Since the <strong>p-value</strong> of the study is found to be<strong> 0.04</strong>, we can infer that the <strong>probability</strong> of a <strong>false positive </strong>conclusion that the operation A is better than B is <strong>4%.</strong></p>\n<p>In this scenario, the <strong><em>p</em> value is 0.04</strong> which makes it, by convention, <strong>statistically significant</strong>. We can reject the null hypothesis and <strong>accept</strong> the <strong>alternative</strong> hypothesis that operation <strong>A</strong> is <strong>better</strong> than operation <strong>B</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2663","text":"You are an investigator co‍nducting a statistical te​st. You obtain the p valu‍e as 0.01 for your study.​ What does this mean?","raw_text":"You are an investigator conducting a statistical test. You obtain the p value as 0.01 for your study. What does this mean?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The probability of finding‍ a significant difference​ is 1%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"There is a 1% probability ‍that the difference is no​t by chance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The difference is not sign‍ificant 1% of times and s​ignificant 99% of times","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The power of the test used‍ is 99%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The difference is not significant 1% of times and significant 99% of times","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The difference is not significant 1% of times and significant 99% of times</strong></p><p>If the <strong>p-value = 0.01, </strong>then the<strong> difference </strong>is<strong> not significant 1% of the time </strong>and<strong> significant 99% of the time</strong>.</p>\n<p>In other words, there is a 99% probability that the difference is not by chance and there is a 1% probability that the difference is only by chance.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4529","text":"In a well-designed cancer ‍study, similar remission ​rates were obtained for a‍ new treatment (33.2%) an​d the usual treatment (32​.2%), with the p-value be​ing 0.04. Which of the fo‌llowing statements is tru​e regarding the study?","raw_text":"In a well-designed cancer study, similar remission rates were obtained for a new treatment (33.2%) and the usual treatment (32.2%), with the p-value being 0.04. Which of the following statements is true regarding the study?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Both the treatments are eq‍ually effective","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neither of the treatments ‍is effective","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The new treatment is more ‍effective than the usual ​treatment","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The information given is n‍ot adequate to compare th​e efficacy of the treatme‍nts","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The new treatment is more effective than the usual treatment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The new treatment is more effective than the usual treatment</strong></p><p>As the p-value is 0.04, we can conclude that the<strong> new treatment</strong> is<strong> more effective</strong> than the usual treatment.</p>\n<p>Even though both treatments have similar rates of remission, a valid statistical test is required to determine the difference in their effectiveness. Statistically, <strong>p-value &lt; 0.05</strong> implies that the <strong>alternate</strong> hypothesis is <strong>true</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this scenario, we can consider:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Null hypothesis: Both the treatments are equally effective (in other terms, there is no difference between the effect of the two treatments).</li>\n<li>Alternative hypothesis: New treatment is more effective than the usual treatment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>As the <strong>p-value</strong> is <strong>0.04,</strong> which is statistically significant (&lt;0.05), the <strong>null</strong> hypothesis is <strong>rejected</strong> and the <strong>alternative </strong>hypothesis is <strong>accepted.</strong> Hence the new treatment is more effective than the usual treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Y3_uq4kX","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"59_supraventricular_arrhythmias","title":"59.Supraventricular Arrhythmias","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA0129","text":"A 52-year-old man has come‌ to the emergency complai​ning of a burning type of​ chest pain. Comment on h‍is ECG recording shown be​low.","raw_text":"A 52-year-old man has come to the emergency complaining of a burning type of chest pain. Comment on his ECG recording shown below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sinus br‌adycardia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sinus ta‌chycardia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Normal si‌nus rhythm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Irregula‌r rhythm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Normal sinus rhythm","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ac12bf3ea55644d4b5c63a854c41e773.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/251a17748fb242b0b115bb0876e4de25x537x130.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c3db622654d7465093f09972c262c87dx800x224.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fc5516f5be574d34a4d83ca6643b63cax800x224.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4baaf24d405f481b9956ca56d1ac06a7x800x224.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Normal sinus rhythm</strong></p><p>The above ECG strip shows <strong>normal sinus rhythm</strong>.</p>\n<p>The features in this ECG that suggest this diagnosis are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>QRS complexes are narrow</strong> (&lt;120 ms or 3 small squares) which implies that the impulse is supraventricular and is conducted via the normal conduction pathway.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each <strong>QRS complex is preceded by a P wave</strong>. This means that all the atrial depolarizations that arise in the sinus node are conducted to the ventricles.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>PR interval is constant</strong> for all the impulses and is normal (&lt;200 ms) in duration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>RR interval is constant</strong> which implies a regular rhythm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9693","text":"All of the following are s‌upraventricular tachycard​ias except:","raw_text":"All of the following are supraventricular tachycardias except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AV nodal reentry tachycard‌ia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sinus ta‌chycardia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Junctional ‌tachycardia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Premature ventricular beat‌s","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Premature ventricular beats","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dfadd86771a643d6848dda9d6e6a85bbx1280x1903.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Premature ventricular beats</strong></p><p><strong>Premature ventricular beats</strong> are a type of <strong>ventricular tachycardias,</strong> not supraventricular tachycardias.</p>\n<p><strong>Tachycardia </strong>is defined as a heart rate of <strong>&gt;100 beats/min</strong>. It can be classified based on the <strong>site of origin</strong> of the cardiac impulse as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Supraventricular tachycardias </strong>(SVTs) originate above the ventricles, that is, in the sinoatrial node, atria, or the atrioventricular node. Supraventricular tachycardias include:\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li>Tachycardias originating from the <strong>atrium</strong>:\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sinus tachycardia- from the SA node</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Focal atrial tachycardia</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Multifocal atrial tachycardia</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Atrial flutter </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Atrial fibrillation</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tachycardias originating from the <strong>AV node</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Junctional tachycardia- automaticity within AV Node</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">AV nodal reentry tachycardia (AVNRT)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tachycardia associated with accessory atrioventricular pathways: atrioventricular reentry tachycardia (AVRT)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Ventricular tachycardias</strong> originate from the ectopic foci or the reentry pathways in the ventricles. Ventricular tachycardias include:\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Premature ventricular beats</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ventricular tachycardia (VT)\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Monomorphic VT- Very rapid monomorphic VT is also known as ventricular flutter</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Polymorphic VT- associated with Torsades de pointes</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ventricular fibrillation</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3688","text":"ECG of a patient who prese‌nted with palpitations to​ the ER is shown below. W​hich of the following is ‍a likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"ECG of a patient who presented with palpitations to the ER is shown below. Which of the following is a likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sinus ta‌chycardia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular‌ tachycardia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Junctional ‌tachycardia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atrial ‌flutter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ventricular tachycardia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e5bc29d2d1624ae48169c4e04b7e15b0.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8037eec9a7e5488ea9f119a5d8ba3269x1280x720.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9989408335ef46bca7dabd1f13a8628cx1280x1903.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ventricular tachycardia</strong></p><p>The above ECG shows increased heart rate with <strong>wide QRS complexes</strong> (&gt; 0.12s). This is suggestive of<strong> ventricular tachycardia </strong>(VT).<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Types of ventricular tachycardia based on QRS morphology:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Monomorphic<span class=\"term-highlight\">-</span> a stable QRS morphology from beat to beat</li>\n<li>Polymorphic<span class=\"term-highlight\">-</span> changing or multiform QRS variance from beat to beat. <strong>Torsades de pointes </strong>is an example of polymorphic <span class=\"term-highlight\">ventricular</span> <span class=\"term-highlight\">tachycardia.</span></li>\n<li>Bidirectional- has a beat-to-beat alternation in the QRS frontal plane axis. It is associated with digitalis toxicity or catecholaminergic polymorphic VT.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most common cause of VT is <strong>ischemic heart disease.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Ventricular tachyarrhythmias </strong>occur due to impulses that originate within the ventricles. These impulses are conducted through the normal <strong>ventricular myocardial cells</strong> instead of the fast conducting Purkinje system. Hence, they present with<strong> wide QRS complexes</strong> ( &gt;0.12 sec) on the ECG.</p>\n<p><strong>Supraventricular tachyarrhythmias</strong> occur due to impulses originating above the ventricles. These impulses reach the ventricles through the proper fast conduction pathway resulting in a <strong>narrow QRS complex.</strong> SVTs are associated with wide QRS in few exceptional conditions like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Conduction block in the left or right bundle branch</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Activation of ventricles from an accessory pathway- antidromic AVRT</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given ECG shows wide stable QRS complexes in monomorphic ventricular tachycardia:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3687","text":"Which is the most common s‌upraventricular tachyarrh​ythmia?","raw_text":"Which is the most common supraventricular tachyarrhythmia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paroxysmal supraventricula‌r tachycardia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sinus ta‌chycardia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Atrial fi‌brillation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Junctional ‌tachycardia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sinus tachycardia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sinus tachycardia</strong></p><p>The most common supraventricular arrhythmia is <strong>sinus tachycardia</strong>. It can either be physiological or pathological. Physiological sinus tachycardia is seen in exercise, fever, anemia, hyperthyroidism, etc.</p>\n<p>Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardias are those supraventricular tachycardias that occur with sudden onset and termination, they are non-sustained arrhythmias (lasts &lt; 30 seconds). They include AVNRT, AVRT, and atrial tachycardias.</p>\n<p>The most common <strong>sustained</strong> (lasts &gt; 30 seconds) arrhythmia is <strong>atrial</strong> <strong>fibrillation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Junctional tachycardia originates from ectopic foci in the AV node. It is an uncommon tachycardia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3693","text":"A 49-year-old woman was br‌ought to the emergency wi​th complaints of dizzines​s, shortness of breath, a‍nd palpitations. ECG show​ed tachyarrhythmia with n‌arrow QRS complex and irr‌egular RR intervals. What​ is the likely diagnosis?​","raw_text":"A 49-year-old woman was brought to the emergency with complaints of dizziness, shortness of breath, and palpitations. ECG showed tachyarrhythmia with narrow QRS complex and irregular RR intervals. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrioventricular re-entry ‌tachycardia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"AV nodal re-entry tachycar‌dia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atrial ‌flutter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atrial fi‌brillation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Atrial fibrillation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d04e4c8c008949509165142a549837d7x1280x631.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a716843e6e6a49e7b3baf959c07bbbf3x1280x1903.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Atrial fibrillation</strong></p><p>ECG showing<strong> tachyarrhythmia</strong> with a<strong> narrow QRS </strong>complex suggests a<strong> supraventricular tachycardia. </strong>An<strong> irregular RR interval </strong>is seen with the following supraventricular tachycardias:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Atrial fibrillation</strong></li>\n<li>Multifocal atrial tachycardia</li>\n<li>Atrial flutter/ atrial tachycardia with variable block</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The ECG below shows atrial fibrillation. Note the narrow QRS complexes and irregular RR intervals. Disappearacne of P-wave is another feature.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3686","text":"A 65-year-old hypertensive‌ patient presents with ch​est pain and dyspnea foll​owing an alcohol binge. H‍is ECG is shown below. Wh​at is the most likely dia‌gnosis?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old hypertensive patient presents with chest pain and dyspnea following an alcohol binge. His ECG is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ventricular‌ tachycardia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Focal atrial‌ tachycardia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricular ‌fibrillation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atrial fi‌brillation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Atrial fibrillation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8aeacc19ffb44b44b9229247c55aede3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d30f6e7195844df6a52b0fa6e84d940ax1280x1903.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Atrial fibrillation</strong></p><p>The ECG shows narrow QRS complexes with <strong>irregular RR intervals</strong> and <strong>no distinct P waves. </strong>This is suggestive of <strong>atrial fibrillation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Atrial fibrillation (AF) </strong>is a supraventricular tachycardia caused by ectopic foci or small reentry circuits within the atria. It has an atrial rate of 400-600/min and a ventricular rate of 100-160/min. </p>\n<p>Risk factors for developing AF include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Age- usually above 60 years of age</li>\n<li>Underlying heart disease</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n<li>Sleep apnea</li>\n<li>Post-cardiac surgery</li>\n<li>Inflammatory pericarditis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Acute precipitating factors for AF include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperthyroidism</li>\n<li>Acute alcohol intoxication</li>\n<li>Acute illness like myocardial infarction and pulmonary embolism</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0613","text":"A 50-year-old man was diag‌nosed with STEMI. He was ​admitted and given thromb​olytic therapy. The next ‍day he complained of palp​itations and an ECG was t‌aken. Which of the follow‌ing drugs can be used to ​treat his condition?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man was diagnosed with STEMI. He was admitted and given thrombolytic therapy. The next day he complained of palpitations and an ECG was taken. Which of the following drugs can be used to treat his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amiod‌arone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vera‌pamil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fleca‌inide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Propa‌fenone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Amiodarone","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e6bc2c51d717431c88847282772b92d0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0b4ad16cfb9b465dbf1fd2ca0f384ad0x1280x2567.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Amiodarone</strong></p><p>The above ECG shows narrow QRS complex, irregular RR intervals, and absent P waves which are suggestive of <strong>atrial fibrillation</strong>. Among the given options, <strong>amiodarone</strong> can be administered in patients having atrial fibrillation with <strong>coronary or structural heart disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Drugs for rhythm control in atrial fibrillation:</p>\n<p>In patients without structural heart disease, class I agents are used. Class I agents act by blocking the Na<sup>+</sup> channels leading to slower depolarization:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Flecainide </li>\n<li>Propafenone</li>\n<li>Disopyramide</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In patients with coronary or structural heart disease, class III agents are used. Class III agents act by blocking the K<sup>+</sup> channels leading to slower repolarization causing increased refractory period:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Amiodarone</li>\n<li>Dofetilide</li>\n<li>Sotalol</li>\n<li>Ibutilide</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3689","text":"A 60-year-old man who is o‌n treatment for chronic A​F presents with nausea, d​izziness, and anorexia. H‍e has a regular heart rat​e of 50/min. The most lik‌ely cause is:","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man who is on treatment for chronic AF presents with nausea, dizziness, and anorexia. He has a regular heart rate of 50/min. The most likely cause is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thromboemb‌olic stroke","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myocardial‌ infarction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Non compliance to treatmen‌t","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Digitalis‌ toxicity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Digitalis toxicity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Digitalis toxicity</strong></p><p>A patient of atrial fibrillation usually has an irregular heart rate of &gt;100/ min but this patient presents with a regular slow heart rate. The most likely cause of <strong>slow regular heart rate</strong> in chronic AF is<strong> digitalis toxicity. </strong>Symptoms such as nausea, anorexia are also suggestive of digitalis toxicity.</p>\n<p>Digitalis lowers the AV nodal conduction by stimulating the vagal nerve endings leading to increased vagal tone. Hence digitalis toxicity can cause AV nodal block. This leads to a junctional escape rhythm taking over the ventricles causing slow and regular heart rate. </p>\n<p>Digitalis toxicity can also lead to a variety of arrhythmias as it increases the intracellular calcium levels increasing the risk of tachyarrhythmias.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0614","text":"ECG of a patient who was b‌rought to the casualty du​e to severe chest pain fo​llowed by syncope is give‍n below. What is the like​ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"ECG of a patient who was brought to the casualty due to severe chest pain followed by syncope is given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial fi‌brillation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atrial ‌flutter","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricular‌ tachycardia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ventricular ‌fibrillation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Atrial flutter","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2aefa186cd024c86a568950289a398e6.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/30021f635ca846c982f297ca4a708790x1280x894.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Atrial flutter</strong></p><p>The given ECG is suggestive of <strong>atrial flutter</strong>.</p>\n<p>The given ECG shows narrow QRS complexes, hence it is supraventricular tachycardia. The RR intervals are regular and the normal P waves are replaced by <strong>sawtooth-shaped</strong> <strong>waves</strong>, known as flutter waves.</p>\n<p>In atrial flutter, the ventricular rate is determined by the AV conduction ratio. The commonest AV ratio is 2:1. In the above ECG, the rhythm is atrial flutter 4:1 block with an atrial rate of about 270 and a ventricular rate of 68/min.</p>\n<p>The image below shows sawtooth shaped waves in atrial flutter.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5295","text":"A 50-year-old man was brou‌ght to the emergency in a​n unconscious state. He h​ad a fever for the past t‍wo days and is a known ca​se of severe COPD. His EC‌G is given below. What is‌ the most likely diagnosi​s?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man was brought to the emergency in an unconscious state. He had a fever for the past two days and is a known case of severe COPD. His ECG is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ventricular‌ tachycardia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Multifocal atrial tachycar‌dia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Atrial fi‌brillation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atrial ta‌chycardia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Multifocal atrial tachycardia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ad613063d3a9426ca5cce755fc488010.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3ca1f22af8114d94ba32e0c4cd65fa6bx1280x697.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Multifocal atrial tachycardia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of chronic pulmonary disease and the ECG showing different p wave morphologies are suggestive of <strong>multifocal atrial tachycardia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Multifocal atrial tachycardia </strong>(MAT) occurs due to impulses arising from <strong>multiple abnormal foci</strong> in the atria. This leads to supraventricular tachycardia with p waves and QRS complexes of different morphologies. It is usually encountered in patients with <strong>chronic pulmonary disease and acute illness. </strong></p>\n<p>Therapy for MAT is directed at <strong>treating the underlying disease</strong> and correcting any metabolic abnormalities. Calcium-channel blockers and radiofrequency ablation of the AV node are the other treatment modalities.</p>\n<p>The given ECG shows narrow QRS complexes with irregular RR intervals, hence it can be atrial fibrillation or multifocal atrial tachycardia. The next step is to look at the p waves. Multifocal atrial tachycardia (MAT) is characterized by at least<strong> 3 distinct P-wave morphologies</strong> (shown by arrows in the image). Whereas, in atrial fibrillation, there would be no distinct p waves seen.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3703","text":"Paroxysmal supraventricula‌r tachycardias do not inc​lude:","raw_text":"Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardias do not include:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial fi‌brillation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Atrial ta‌chycardia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AV nodal reentry tachycard‌ia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AV reentry ‌tachycardia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Atrial fibrillation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/87a6b1840ca4407cade4b583d8fde458x1280x685.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Atrial fibrillation</strong></p><p>Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardias do not include atrial fibrillation.</p>\n<p><strong>Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardias</strong> are tachycardias that occur with sudden onset and termination, they are <strong>not sustained</strong> arrhythmias. They include</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>AVNRT</strong></li>\n<li><strong>AVRT</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Atrial tachycardia</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Whereas, <strong>atrial fibrillation</strong> is the most common <strong>sustained</strong> arrhythmia among supraventricular tachycardias.</p>\n<p>Features suggestive of PSVT:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Narrow complex tachycardia at 150 bpm</li>\n<li>No visible P waves</li>\n<li>Pseudo R’ waves due to retrograde atrial activation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given ECG shows pseudo R' waves: </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3690","text":"A 32-year-old man is broug‌ht to the casualty after ​he developed sudden onset​ dyspnea, chest pain, and‍ palpitations at a party.​ His blood pressure is 10‌0/62 mmHg. His ECG is sho‌wn below. What is the dru​g of choice to treat his ​condition?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old man is brought to the casualty after he developed sudden onset dyspnea, chest pain, and palpitations at a party. His blood pressure is 100/62 mmHg. His ECG is shown below. What is the drug of choice to treat his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Procai‌namide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aden‌osine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vera‌pamil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Propr‌anolol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adenosine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d87a30aafdb14f189d8ccfeb2af1170a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/df8356f046484034b645cc2d6435418fx1280x685.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e38208191fe34a4d96e6d6b9f2e981a8x1280x1845.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adenosine</strong></p><p>The ECG is suggestive of paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia (<strong>PSVT</strong>) as it shows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Narrow QRS complexes</li>\n<li>No visible p waves</li>\n<li>Pseudo R' waves in V1 lead</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Adenosine</strong> is the preferred drug in stable paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia (PSVT). </p>\n<p>Treatment of PSVT:</p>\n<p>In hemodynamically <strong>unstable</strong> patients<strong>, </strong>QRS-synchronous <strong>DC cardioversion</strong> should be done.</p>\n<p>In hemodynamically <strong>stable</strong> patients,</p>\n<p><strong>1. Mechanical measures</strong>: These maneuvers result in an acute increase in the vagal tone which decreases AV conduction and terminates the arrhythmia. They include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Valsalva maneuver</li>\n<li>Lowering the head between the knees</li>\n<li>Coughing</li>\n<li>Splashing cold water on the face</li>\n<li>Breath-holding</li>\n<li>Carotid sinus massage</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Pharmacologic therapy</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Adenosine- </strong>it is the <strong>first-line agent</strong>. It blocks conduction across the AV node and terminates the SVT. It is given as a 6-18 mg bolus </li>\n<li><strong>Calcium channel blockers-</strong> verapamil and diltiazem</li>\n<li><strong>Beta-blockers-</strong> esmolol, propranolol, and metoprolol</li>\n<li><strong>IV Procainamide-</strong> it is administered if the tachycardia is being mediated through an accessory pathway. It facilitates the AV conduction and therefore causes an initial increase in rate. Therefore, it is given after administering calcium channel blockers or beta-blockers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The given ECG shows pseudo R' waves:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0221","text":"The ECG of a patient who p‌resented to the ER is as ​given below. The patient ​was already given a carot‍id massage and IV Adenosi​ne but the BP is currentl‌y 60/30 mm Hg. What is th‌e next best step of manag​ement?","raw_text":"The  ECG of a patient who presented to the ER is as given below. The patient was already given a carotid massage and IV Adenosine but the BP is currently 60/30 mm Hg. What is the next best step of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Repeat Inj a‌denosine 6 mg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inj amioda‌rone 300 mg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Synchronised cardioversion‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Defibri‌llation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Synchronised cardioversion","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5a0af74b75f84142932d59b67db74053.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8f6eebe7c13e4b80ad47f6d86d107833x1280x1845.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Synchronised cardioversion</strong></p><p>The given <strong>ECG</strong> showing <strong>narrow QRS complex</strong> (&lt;0.12 s) tachycardia with <strong>absent P wave</strong> is suggestive of <strong>paroxysmal ventricular tachycardia</strong> (PSVT). The next best step in the management of a patient with <strong>unstable vitals</strong> (systolic blood pressure &lt; 90 mm Hg) is <strong>synchronized cardioversion</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Synchronized cardioversion</strong> is the <strong>treatment of choice</strong> in <strong>unstable PSVT</strong> patients, in which shocks are delivered with the defibrillator/monitor in synchronized mode with an initial energy level of <strong>100J</strong>. Synchronization times the delivery so that it is given at the <strong>peak of the QRS complex</strong>.</p>\n<p>If the shock occurs in the ST segment or the T wave (unsynchronized), it can precipitate a more serious arrhythmia such as ventricular fibrillation.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 110px;\" width=\"246\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Synchronized cardioversion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Defibrillation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">\n<p>Used in <strong>unstable PSVT</strong>, atrial flutter, atrial fibrillation, and ventricular tachycardia <strong>with a pulse</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Used in ventricular tachycardia <strong>without pulse</strong> and ventricular fibrillation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Shock is delivered in sync with the R wave (in absolute refractory period)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Shock is delivered at any time during the cardiac cycle.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">\n<p>Monophasic: 100-200 J</p>\n<p>Biphasic: 50-100 J</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">\n<p>Monophasic: 360 J</p>\n<p>Biphasic:120-200 J</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: In the absence of hemodynamic instability, a repeat dose of injection adenosine (maximum of 12mg) could be attempted.<br/><br/>Option B: IV Amiodarone is rarely used in the acute management of PSVT. Instead, it is used as an alternative to calcium channel blockers in chronic suppressive therapy.<br/><br/>Option D: Defibrillation is only done for ventricular fibrillation and pulseless ventricular tachycardia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9280","text":"A patient came with sudden‌ onset palpitations. ECG ​showed a narrow complex, ​regular tachycardia witho‍ut P waves, and a rate of​ 160 BPM. His blood press‌ure is 96/68 mmHg. What i‌s the next step in manage​ment?","raw_text":"A patient came with sudden onset palpitations. ECG showed a narrow complex, regular tachycardia without P waves, and a rate of 160 BPM. His blood pressure is 96/68 mmHg. What is the next step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carotid si‌nus massage","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aden‌osine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Beta-b‌locker","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Defibr‌illator","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Carotid sinus massage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d8076b463c924c27b49b4479ac3c46b2x1279x1416.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Carotid sinus massage</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the electrocardiogram (ECG) showing <strong>narrow QRS complex regular tachycardia without P waves</strong> are suggestive of paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia (<strong>PSVT</strong>). The next step in the management of PSVT in a <strong>hemodynamically stable</strong> (systolic blood pressure &gt;90 mm Hg) patient is vagotonic maneuvres such as <strong>carotid sinus massage</strong> or Valsalva maneuver.</p>\n<p>Carotid sinus massage can be considered in the absence of carotid bruits and no history of stroke. If vagal maneuvers fail or cannot be performed, <strong>intravenous adenosine</strong> can be used. </p>\n<p>Non-DHP <strong>calcium channel blockers</strong> and <strong>beta blockers</strong> are second-line pharmacological agents in the management of PSVT. They can cause <strong>hypotension</strong> before and after terminating the arrhythmia.</p>\n<p>If the patient is <strong>hemodynamically unstable</strong> (systolic blood pressure &lt;90 mmHg or mean arterial pressure &lt;65 mm Hg), immediate <strong>QRS-synchronous direct current cardioversion</strong> is necessary.</p>\n<p>The treatment algorithm for PSVT is described in the flowchart below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9096","text":"A 65-year-old female patie‌nt is brought to the emer​gency department in a sta​te of unconsciousness. He‍r BP is 70/50 mm of Hg. H​er ECG is shown below. Wh‌at is the next best step ‌in the management of her ​condition?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old female patient is brought to the emergency department in a state of unconsciousness. Her BP is 70/50 mm of Hg. Her ECG is shown below. What is the next best step in the management of her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IV ver‌apamil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IV ade‌nosine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carotid‌ massage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"DC cardi‌oversion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. DC cardioversion","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6edcf84b58e7436c8d29d57b32d7c111.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bc8abf258fe54dd0a7ab09a274501279x1280x1845.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. DC cardioversion</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario describes an <strong>unconscious patient</strong>, who is <strong>hemodynamically unstable</strong> (systolic BP &lt;90 mmHg or mean arterial pressure &lt;65 mmHg). The ECG shows a <strong>narrow QRS complex tachycardia</strong> (heart rate of 188 BPM) with <strong>regular RR interval</strong> and <strong>absent</strong><strong> P waves</strong>, characteristic of <strong>paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia</strong> (PSVT). The next step in management is <strong>QRS-synchronized DC cardioversion.</strong></p>\n<p>Shown below is the algorithm for the management of supraventricular tachycardia:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Options A, B, and C are all used in stable cases. Vagal maneuvers like carotid massage or Valsalva are tried first followed by IV Adenosine. If these are ineffective, IV Verapamil, Diltiazem, or Beta blockers are used.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 592px;\" width=\"476\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 236.172px;\">Synchronized cardioversion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 227.5px;\">Defibrillation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 236.172px;\">\n<p>Used in sustained VT<br/>Used in <strong>SVT (unstable cases)</strong>, atrial flutter/fibrillation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 227.5px;\">Used in VT and VF without a pulse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 236.172px;\">The shock delivered in <strong>sync with R wave</strong> (in absolute refractory period)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 227.5px;\">The shock is delivered at any time during cardiac cycle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 236.172px;\">\n<p>Characteristics of QRS complex:<br/>Narrow and regular - 50 to 100J<br/>Narrow and irregular - 120 to 200J<br/>Wide and regular - 100J <br/>Wide and irregular - No synchronised cardioversion</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 227.5px;\">\n<p>Wide and irregular QRS complex <br/>Monophasic: 360 J<br/>Biphasic:1 20-200 J</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3706","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about accessory pat​hways?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about accessory pathways?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bundle of Kent has antegra‌de conduction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Concealed pathways have re‌trograde conduction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antegrade conduction cause‌s preexcitation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retrograde conduction caus‌es preexcitation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Retrograde conduction causes preexcitation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Retrograde conduction causes preexcitation</strong></p><p>Retrograde conduction via accessory pathways does not cause preexcitation. </p>\n<p><strong>Accessory pathways</strong> (APs) are abnormal conduction pathways connecting the atrium and ventricle. They can conduct impulses in the following ways:</p>\n<p><strong>Antegrade conduction:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Occurs from the atria to the ventricle</li>\n<li>The impulses from atria reach the ventricles faster via the accessory pathway as compared to the normal AV nodal pathway as they bypass the AV nodal delay.</li>\n<li>This leads to an earlier excitation of the ventricles known as the <strong>preexcitation</strong> of the ventricles and thus a <strong>wide QRS</strong> complex. </li>\n<li>Examples- <strong>WPW syndrome </strong>and <strong>a</strong><strong>ntidromic AVRT</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Retrograde conduction:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Occurs from the ventricle to the atria.</li>\n<li>The impulses reach the ventricles via the normal AV nodal pathway but re-enter the atria through the accessory pathway.</li>\n<li>Ventricular depolarization is normal, there is<strong> no preexcitation </strong>and thus a <strong>narrow QRS</strong> complex. It causes supraventricular tachycardia as the impulses re-enter the atria via the accessory pathway. </li>\n<li>Examples-<strong> concealed accessory pathways</strong> (can cause SVTs) and <strong>o</strong><strong>rthodromic AVRT.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0215","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common reentrant​ tachycardia caused by an​ accessory pathway?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common reentrant tachycardia caused by an accessory pathway?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Orthodromic‌ AV reentry","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Antidromic‌ AV reentry","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"WPW sy‌ndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AV nodal‌ reentry","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Orthodromic AV reentry","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Orthodromic AV reentry</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> <strong>reentrant tachycardia</strong> caused by an accessory pathway is <strong>orthodromic AV reentry</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Accessory pathways</strong> (APs) are abnormal conduction pathways connecting the atrium and ventricle. They can conduct impulses in the following ways:</p>\n<p><strong>Antegrade conduction:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Occurs from the atria to the ventricle</li>\n<li>The impulses from atria reach the ventricles faster via the accessory pathway as compared to the normal AV nodal pathway as they bypass the AV nodal delay.</li>\n<li>This leads to an earlier excitation of the ventricles known as the <strong>preexcitation</strong> of the ventricles and thus a <strong>wide QRS</strong> complex. </li>\n<li>Examples- <strong>WPW syndrome </strong>and <strong>a</strong><strong>ntidromic AVRT</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Retrograde conduction:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Occurs from the ventricle to the atria.</li>\n<li>The impulses reach the ventricles via the normal AV nodal pathway but re-enter the atria through the accessory pathway.</li>\n<li>Ventricular depolarization is normal, there is<strong> no preexcitation </strong>and thus a <strong>narrow QRS</strong> complex. It causes supraventricular tachycardia as the impulses re-enter the atria via the accessory pathway. </li>\n<li>Examples-<strong> concealed accessory pathways</strong> (can cause SVTs) and <strong>o</strong><strong>rthodromic AVRT.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>AV nodal reentry tachycardia (AVNRT) is not caused by an accessory pathway. It involves tissues with different conduction speeds within the AV node or just adjacent to the AV node, known as the fast pathway and the slow pathway. This is known as dual AV nodal physiology.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3711","text":"A patient's ECG shows a sh‌ort PR interval and delta​ wave. Additional finding​s include atrial fibrilla‍tion and rapid ventricula​r rate. He is hemodynamic‌ally stable. All of the f‌ollowing drugs can be use​d in the management excep​t:","raw_text":"A patient's ECG shows a short PR interval and delta wave. Additional findings include atrial fibrillation and rapid ventricular rate. He is hemodynamically stable. All of the following drugs can be used in the management except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amiod‌arone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ibut‌ilide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Procai‌namide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vera‌pamil","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Verapamil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e995440e11774983af8d62b5c045aadfx1280x707.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Verapamil</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario describes a patient with <strong>WPW syndrome </strong>with<strong> atrial fibrillation.</strong></p>\n<p>Rate-slowing drugs such as non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (eg, <strong>verapamil</strong>, diltiazem), beta-blockers, and digoxin are contraindicated in these patients. They increase the refractoriness of the AV node with minimal or <strong>no effect on the accessory pathway</strong>. This can lead to faster ventricular rates, increasing the risk of ventricular fibrillation.</p>\n<p> The ECG below shows a short PR interval (&lt;120 ms) and delta waves in WPW syndrome. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3709","text":"The most common location o‌f the accessory pathway i​n WPW syndrome is:","raw_text":"The most common location of the accessory pathway in WPW syndrome is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left fr‌ee wall","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Poster‌oseptal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right f‌ree wall","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antero‌septal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Left free wall","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/31cf21977756492a9f510532fe907b41x529x368.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Left free wall</strong></p><p>The most common location of an accessory pathway in WPW syndrome is on <strong>the left free wall</strong>. It is present between left atrium and free wall of left ventricle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3708","text":"All are true about the con‌dition that is diagnosed ​by the given ECG except:","raw_text":"All are true about the condition that is diagnosed by the given ECG except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a congenital conditi‌on","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Can be seen in asymptomati‌c individuals","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prevalence increases with ‌age","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Caused by an abnormal acce‌ssory pathway","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Prevalence increases with age","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bf7cc55fc53b4aa59a2a3e7d29495a66.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/417eb888db05485f932f2cbda6d267f6x571x439.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Prevalence increases with age</strong></p><p>The given ECG is suggestive of <strong>Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome </strong>(WPW). Its <strong>prevalence decreases </strong>with<strong> age</strong>.</p>\n<p>The classic ECG features in WPW are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">A <strong>shortened PR interval</strong> (typically &lt;120 ms)</div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">A slurring and the slow rise of the initial upstroke of the QRS complex (<strong>delta wave</strong>)</div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">A <strong>widened QRS complex</strong> (total duration &gt;0.12 seconds)</div>\n</li>\n<li>\n<div class=\"topbullet\">ST-segment–T wave (repolarization) changes, generally directed opposite the major delta wave and QRS complex, reflecting altered depolarization</div>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0615","text":"A 30-year-old woman comes ‌to the clinic with compla​ints of recurrent palpita​tion and lightheadedness.‍ An ECG was taken and is ​as shown below. What is t‌he best step in managemen‌t?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman comes to the clinic with complaints of recurrent palpitation and lightheadedness. An ECG was taken and is as shown below. What is the best step in management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DC cardi‌oversion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carotid si‌nus massage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Synchronised cardioversion‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Catheter‌ ablation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Catheter ablation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ceea5122e7a248ac85308dd2f6c06b26.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Catheter ablation</strong></p><p>The ECG shows the presence of <strong>delta waves </strong>(preexcitation wave), <strong>wide QRS,</strong> and <strong>short PR interval </strong>which are classically seen in <strong>Wolff Parkinson White</strong> <strong>(WPW) syndrome</strong>. In this patient, <strong>catheter ablation </strong>is the best for long-term management. </p>\n<p>Patients with preexcitation who have symptoms of arrhythmia are at <strong>risk</strong> for developing <strong>AF</strong> and <strong>sudden death</strong> if they have an accessory pathway that allows rapid antegrade conduction (preexcitation &amp; tachyarrhythmias are features of WPW syndrome due to the abnormal accessory pathway).</p>\n<p>Complications of catheter ablation are rare but include AV block, cardiac tamponade, thromboembolic events, and hematoma at the access site.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3712","text":"Which of the following is ‌true regarding sick sinus​ syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding sick sinus syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most patients are symptoma‌tic in the early stages","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Most commonly in young pat‌ients","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Frequently seen in patient‌s with atrial fibrillatio​n","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Treated using beta-blocker‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Frequently seen in patients with atrial fibrillation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Frequently seen in patients with atrial fibrillation</strong></p><p><strong>Sick sinus syndrome</strong> is frequently seen in <strong>patients with concomitant atrial fibrillation. </strong></p>\n<p>Sick sinus syndrome (or <strong>sinus node dysfunction</strong>) is a group of abnormal heart rhythms. It is a <strong>disorder of the sinoatrial node</strong> caused by<strong> impaired pacemaker function </strong>and<strong> impulse transmission</strong>. This leads to a combination of abnormal rhythms, including atrial bradyarrhythmias, atrial tachyarrhythmias, and sometimes a pattern of alternating bradycardia and tachycardia, known as tachy-brady syndrome. These irregular rhythms can cause symptoms such as palpitations, decreased tissue perfusion, subsequent fatigue, lightheadedness, pre-syncope, and syncope. </p>\n<p>The <strong>management</strong> of sick sinus syndrome includes identifying and <strong>correcting reversible factors</strong>, as an initial step. </p>\n<p>Some examples of <strong>reversible causes</strong> of Sinus Node Dysfunction include, </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypothyroidism</li>\n<li>Sleep apnea</li>\n<li>Anti-hypertensive medications eg. Beta-blockers</li>\n<li>Anti-arrhythmic medications eg. Amiodarone, Procainamide</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ambulatory ECG</strong> monitoring is required to diagnose as the symptoms are nonspecific.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> involves <strong>permanent pacing. </strong></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: The course of sinus node dysfunction is usually a slow progression over decades. In the <strong>early stages</strong>, most patients are <strong>asymptomatic</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: It occurs most commonly in <strong>elderly</strong> patients due to the gradual loss of SA node function. </p>\n<p>Option D: Beta-blockers are <strong>contraindicated</strong> in this condition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"JxGl9ie9","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"59_obstructive_and_restrictive_lung_diseases","title":"59.Obstructive And Restrictive Lung Diseases","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2874","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions does not involve ‍the bronchus?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions does not involve the bronchus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Emph‌ysema","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchia‌l asthma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bronc‌hitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bronchi‌ectasis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Emphysema","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Emphysema</strong></p><p><strong>Emphysema</strong> does not involve the bronchus, it involves the <strong>acinus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (<strong>COPD</strong>) includes both emphysema and chronic bronchitis.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 347px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161.390625px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Emphysema</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.609375px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Chronic bronchitis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161.390625px;\">\n<p><strong>Irreversible</strong> <strong>enlargement</strong> of the airspaces distal to the <strong>terminal bronchiole </strong>due to wall destruction (without obvious fibrosis)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.609375px;\"><strong>Mucus</strong> hypersecretion and persistent <strong>inflammation</strong> in the <strong>large airways</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 161.390625px;\">Patients with this condition are known as<strong> pink puffers</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 193.609375px;\">Patients with this condition are known as <strong>blue bloaters</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Other obstructive lung diseases include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bronchial asthma - characterized by <strong>reversible bronchoconstriction</strong> caused by airway hyperresponsiveness to a variety of stimuli</li>\n<li>Bronchiectasis - destruction of smooth muscle and elastic tissue by chronic necrotizing <strong>infections</strong> leading to <strong>permanent dilation</strong> of <strong>bronchi</strong> and <strong>bronchioles</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9794","text":"A patient with severe emph‌ysema underwent lung volu‍me reduction surgery. The​ resected specimen is sho​wn below. What type of em​physema is this?","raw_text":"A patient with severe emphysema underwent lung volume reduction surgery. The resected specimen is shown below. What type of emphysema is this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pana‌cinar","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Centri‌acinar","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Paras‌eptal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Irre‌gular","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Centriacinar","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2b6704c065844e54b6dd8ab616b7d50c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e62496d1dc4e4d898b7693592c210853x1280x1048.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f05dc5bf51de41839520f02447bcd63ax1280x1048.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/646f5e3157224d3484d268df83978955x1280x845.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/89d208f8f5f34e66a4a815daa86c067cx416x467.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Centriacinar</strong></p><p>The given gross image is characteristic of<strong> centriacinar emphysema</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In<strong> paraseptal emphysema </strong>subpleural spaces are mainly affected (shown below).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In <strong>panacinar emphysema </strong>all air spaces within acini are evenly enlarged (shown below).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2877","text":"In patients with COPD, the‌ emphysematous damage is ‍most likely to involve wh​ich of the following stru​ctures?","raw_text":"In patients with COPD, the emphysematous damage is most likely to involve which of the following structures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Respiratory‌ bronchiole","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Terminal ‌bronchiole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alv‌eoli","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alveola‌r ducts","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Respiratory bronchiole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Respiratory bronchiole</strong></p><p>Patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (<strong>COPD</strong>) usually have<strong> centriacinar emphysema </strong>which involves <strong>respiratory bronchioles</strong>.</p>\n<p>Emphysema is classified into four major <strong>types:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Centriacinar</li>\n<li>Panacinar</li>\n<li>Paraseptal</li>\n<li>Irregular</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0601","text":"A 30-year-old woman with a‌ long history of dyspnea,‍ chronic cough with sputu​m, and wheezing died of r​espiratory failure follow​ing a bout of lobar pneum‍onia. She was not a smoke​r or an alcoholic. The lu​ng at autopsy is shown in​ the image below. Which o​f the following is most l​ikely to cause the pathol​ogical changes shown?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman with a long history of dyspnea, chronic cough with sputum, and wheezing died of respiratory failure following a bout of lobar pneumonia. She was not a smoker or an alcoholic. The lung at autopsy is shown in the image below. Which of the following is most likely to cause the pathological changes shown?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alpha-1-antitrypsin defici‌ency","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cystic ‌fibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mutation in‌ dynein arms","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antibodies against type-IV‌ collagen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1e80b6f7a14b464a98286264691d89ab.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/17cbaa8cc70949dc91fed3f8939934e5x416x467.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency</strong></p><p>The above gross specimen shows <strong>emphysematous damage</strong> involving enlargement of the entire acinus suggestive of <strong>panacinar</strong> <strong>emphysema. </strong>It is caused due to <strong>alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency.</strong></p>\n<p>In panacinar (panlobular) emphysema, the acini are uniformly enlarged from the level of the <strong>respiratory</strong> <strong>bronchiole</strong> <strong>to</strong> the terminal blind<strong> alveoli</strong>. It occurs commonly in the <strong>lower</strong> <strong>zones</strong> and in the <strong>anterior margins </strong>of the lung and is most<strong> severe </strong>at the <strong>bases</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below shows panacinar emphysema.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2878","text":"A 25-year-old man is being‌ evaluated for dyspnea an‍d cough for 2 months. He ​is not a smoker. Workup r​evealed emphysematous cha​nges at lung bases. He re‍ports that his father and​ uncles were diagnosed wi​th liver failure and lung​ disease at relatively yo​ung ages. Where is the ge​ne encoding the defective​ protein located?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man is being evaluated for dyspnea and cough for 2 months. He is not a smoker. Workup revealed emphysematous changes at lung bases. He reports that his father and uncles were diagnosed with liver failure and lung disease at relatively young ages. Where is the gene encoding the defective protein located?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chromo‌some 11","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chromo‌some 13","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chromo‌some 15","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chromo‌some 14","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chromosome 14","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chromosome 14</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>α1-antitrypsin</strong> <strong>deficiency.</strong> The α1-antitrypsin is encoded by the proteinase inhibitor (Pi) locus on <strong>chromosome 14.</strong></p>\n<p>α1-AT is a polymorphic gene located on chromosome 14. It is part of a larger family of structurally unique <strong>ser</strong>ine <strong>p</strong>rotease <strong>in</strong>hibitors, referred to as <strong>serpins.</strong> The most common clinically significant mutation in α1-antitrypsin deficiency is <strong>PiZZ</strong>.</p>\n<p>α1-antitrypsin, normally present in serum, tissue fluids, and macrophages is a major <strong>inhibitor</strong> of proteases (particularly <strong>elastase)</strong> secreted by neutrophils during inflammation. The absence of α1- antitrypsin activity results in <strong>excessive digestion</strong> of <strong>elastic tissue</strong> by elastase. </p>\n<p>Patients with α1-antitrypsin deficiency have a markedly increased tendency to develop <strong>panacinar emphysema</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2879","text":"What is the normal value o‌f y/x in the following re‍presentation?","raw_text":"What is the normal value of y/x in the following representation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0‌.2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0‌.8","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"0‌.6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"0‌.4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 0.4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/172f774f2f5148ed8ce88b01661da4f9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/785b7e3ba6c94b46aea7b4ccdae98962x1280x1391.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 0.4</strong></p><p>In the above image, y/x denotes the ratio of the thickness of the submucosal gland layer to that of the bronchial wall. This is called as <strong>Reid index </strong>and it is normally<strong> 0.4.</strong> </p>\n<p>It is <strong>increased</strong> in <strong>chronic bronchitis.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Chronic bronchitis is defined by the presence of a<strong> persistent productive cough </strong>for at least<strong> 3 consecutive months </strong>in at least<strong> 2 consecutive years</strong>. The single most important cause is<strong> cigarette smoking. </strong>The distinctive features of chronic bronchitis are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypersecretion of mucus</li>\n<li>Hypertrophy of submucosal glands</li>\n<li>Goblet-cell hyperplasia</li>\n<li>Inflammation</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 684\">Difference between clinical presentation of emphysema and chronic bronchitis:</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 684\">\n<table style=\"width: 383px;\"><colgroup><col/><col/><col/></colgroup>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Predominant Bronchitis</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Predominant Emphysema</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Age (year)</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>40-45</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>50-75</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Dyspnea</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Mild; late</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Severe; early</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Cough</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Early; copious sputum</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Late; scanty sputum</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Infections</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Common</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Occasional</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Respiratory insufficiency</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Repeated</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Terminal</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Cor pulmonale</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Common</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Rare; terminal</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Airway resistance</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Increased</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Normal or slightly increased</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Elastic recoil</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Normal</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Low</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Chest radiograph</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Prominent vessels; large heart</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Hyperinflation; small heart</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 106px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Appearance</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Blue bloater</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Pink puffer</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1193","text":"A 55-year-old man was brou‌ght to the ED with fever,‍ cough with expectoration​, and difficulty in breat​hing for 1 day. He is a c​hronic smoker and he has ‍had similar progressive s​ymptoms for the last 4 ye​ars. Past PFT findings sh​owed reduced FEV1 and inc​reased RV with normal dif​fusion capacity. Which of​ the following is the mos‌t common organism implica​ted in this condition?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man was brought to the ED with fever, cough with expectoration, and difficulty in breathing for 1 day. He is a chronic smoker and he has had similar progressive symptoms for the last 4 years. Past PFT findings showed reduced FEV1 and increased RV with normal diffusion capacity. Which of the following is the most common organism implicated in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphaloco‌ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemophilus‌ influenzae","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mora‌xella","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pneumo‌coccus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hemophilus influenzae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hemophilus influenzae</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario gives a picture of <strong>acute exacerbation </strong>of<strong> COPD</strong>. The most common bacterial cause of acute COPD exacerbation is <em><strong>Haemophilus influenzae</strong></em>.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Moraxella</strong></em> is the <strong>second most common</strong> bacterial cause of acute exacerbation of COPD. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6685","text":"A middle-aged woman presen‌ted with recurrent episod‍es of breathlessness, whe​eze, and chest tightness.​ Auscultation of the ches​t revealed bilateral whee‍ze. Sputum microscopy rev​eals the following. What ​is the most likely diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presented with recurrent episodes of breathlessness, wheeze, and chest tightness. Auscultation of the chest revealed bilateral wheeze. Sputum microscopy reveals the following. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchia‌l asthma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic b‌ronchitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Asbes‌tosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bronchial asthma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1c247ebd746f409d942b7aa82ca89edd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/10b0fc733e1d4818b3e99dadc5fed405x1280x853.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bronchial asthma</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The given clinical scenario and the sputum microscopy showing <strong>Charcot–Leyden crystals</strong> (arrows in below image), lakes of mucus, and numerous eosinophils, are diagnostic of<strong> bronchial asthma</strong>.</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 689\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Asthma is characterized by <strong>reversible bronchoconstriction</strong> caused by airway hyperresponsiveness to a variety of stimuli. <strong>Atopic</strong> asthma is caused by a TH2 and IgE-mediated immunologic reaction to environmental allergens (<strong>type-1 hypersensitivity</strong> reaction). Triggers for <strong>non-atopic</strong> asthma are less clear but include <strong>viral</strong> infections and inhaled <strong>air pollutants</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 689\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Genetic susceptibility for developing asthma:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chromosome 5q</strong> - site of the <strong>susceptibility locus </strong>for asthma</li>\n<li>IL-13 gene polymorphisms (on 5q chromosome) - most consistent associations with asthma or allergic disease</li>\n<li>IL-4 receptor gene variants - associated with atopy, elevated total serum IgE, and asthma</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The sputum of patients with bronchial asthma shows:</span></p>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li1\"><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Charcot–Leyden crystals </strong>- made of an eosinophilic membrane protein called <strong>galectin 10</strong></span></li>\n<li class=\"li1\"><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Curschmann spirals </strong>- spirals of </span>mucus plugs from subepithelial mucous gland ducts or bronchioles</li>\n<li class=\"li1\"><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Creola bodies</strong> -<strong> </strong>desquamated epithelial cells</span></li>\n<li class=\"li1\"><span class=\"s1\">Numerous eosinophils</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"s1\">Note: </span>The hygiene hypothesis was previously used to explain the development of asthma and other hypersensitivity disorders.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2928","text":"A resected specimen of a l‌ung is shown below. What ‍is the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A resected specimen of a lung is shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Emph‌ysema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchi‌ectasis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Interstitial‌ lung disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anthr‌acosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bronchiectasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0031650b6f6c4280ac61566e2d06f941.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bronchiectasis</strong></p><p>The image above shows markedly <strong>distended bronchi</strong> involving the <strong>middle</strong> and<strong> lower lobe</strong> of the lung which is seen in <strong>bronchiectasis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Bronchiectasis is a suppurative lung disease with <strong>abnormal</strong> and <strong>irreversible dilatation</strong> of the <strong>bronchi</strong>. It is an end-stage condition of various diseases that result in destruction of the bronchial wall and its surrounding supporting tissues. </p>\n<p>The normal <strong>clearing mechanisms</strong> are<strong> impaired</strong>, either due to congenital causes or due to bronchial obstruction. This results in <strong>pooling of secretions</strong>, secondary <strong>infection</strong>, and inflammation, often with necrosis, fibrosis, and eventually cystic dilation of airways up to four times the normal size.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 690\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Note: Kartagener syndrome is a primary ciliary dyskinesia characterized by the classical triad of situs inversus, chronic sinusitis, and bronchiectasis.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2932","text":"Transbronchial biopsy from‌ a patient showed the fol‍lowing microscopic findin​g. What is the likely dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"Transbronchial biopsy from a patient showed the following microscopic finding. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Asbes‌tosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sili‌cosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cryptogenic organizing pne‌umonia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0e651cb19a3044c7adaf66009509adf4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/90b891ddf34742cb95d21b215cfdb00dx1280x853.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia</strong></p><p>The image above shows alveolar spaces filled with balls of fibroblasts (<strong>Masson bodies</strong>). This is characteristically seen in <strong>cryptogenic organizing pneumonia </strong>(COP).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 694\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Masson bodies are <strong>polypoid plugs</strong> of loose organizing <strong>connective tissue</strong> within alveolar ducts, alveoli, and bronchioles along with foci of <strong>fibrosis</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>The clinical presentation of COP is the development of nonspecific systemic and respiratory symptoms. It is associated with filling of the lung alveoli that is visible on a chest X-ray as <strong>patchy unilateral</strong> or<strong> bilateral consolidation</strong>.</p>\n<p>COP was previously known as bronchiolitis obliterans organizing pneumonia (<strong>BOOP</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2934","text":"What is the most common pn‌eumoconiosis in the world‍?","raw_text":"What is the most common pneumoconiosis in the world?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sili‌cosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Asbes‌tosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anthr‌acosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Farmer‌'s lung","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Silicosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Silicosis</strong></p><p><strong>Silicosis </strong>is the most common <strong>pneumoconiosis</strong> in the world.</p>\n<p>Silicosis is caused by the inhalation of <strong>crystalline silica </strong>(e.g., quartz).<strong> </strong>Individuals involved with the concrete work, sandblasting, stone carvers, and jewelers using chalk molds are at high risk.</p>\n<p>Pneumoconioses<strong> </strong>encompass a group of chronic fibrosing diseases of the lung resulting from exposure to organic and inorganic particulates, most commonly mineral dust.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2937","text":"What is the most common ma‌nifestation of asbestosis‍?","raw_text":"What is the most common manifestation of asbestosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pleural‌ plaque","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mesoth‌elioma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lung ‌cancer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pleural ‌effusion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pleural plaque","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cb3c2adac5f146e8a31fd87d3b9e240dx800x537.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pleural plaque</strong></p><p><strong>Pleural plaques</strong> are the most common manifestation of <strong>asbestos</strong> exposure.</p>\n<p>They are well-circumscribed plaques of dense collagen, often containing calcium. They develop most frequently on the<strong> parietal pleura</strong> and over the<strong> domes </strong>of the<strong> diaphragm</strong>. They do not contain asbestos bodies, and only rarely occur in persons without prior asbestos exposure.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 697\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Asbestos occurs in two distinct forms - <strong>s</strong><strong>erpentine chrysotile</strong> (more common) and <strong>amphibole</strong> (more pathogenic). Exposure may result in the following conditions:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li>Localized fibrous plaques or, rarely, diffuse pleural fibrosis</li>\n<li><strong>Pleural effusions</strong></li>\n<li>Parenchymal <strong>interstitial fibrosis</strong> (asbestosis)</li>\n<li><strong>Lung carcinoma</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Mesotheliomas</strong></li>\n<li>Extrapulmonary neoplasms including laryngeal, ovarian, and colon carcinomas</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows fibrous pleural plaques seen on the diaphragmatic pleural surface in asbestosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5329","text":"A patient working in a fac‌tory presented with lung ‍fibrosis and thickening o​f pleura. Biopsy was done​. Diagnosis from the hist​opathology picture is:","raw_text":"A patient working in a factory presented with lung fibrosis and thickening of pleura. Biopsy was done. Diagnosis from the histopathology picture is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asbes‌tosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sili‌cosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mesoth‌elioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Asbestosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b7d95064a9284034a9ad7b7f116d2962.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a46269210ef84528b81fb49d506bd025x720x480.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5469f717742b4849bea71e32defb53d4x720x480.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Asbestosis</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and image showing typical <strong>asbestos bodies</strong> are suggestive of <strong>asbestosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Asbestosis is a type of pneumoconiosis associated with asbestos exposure. The disease-causing capabilities of the different forms of asbestos depend on concentration, size, shape, and solubility. This condition is marked by diffuse pulmonary interstitial fibrosis.</p>\n<p>Asbestos bodies are a typical finding. They are <strong>golden brown, fusiform, </strong>or<strong> beaded rods</strong> with a translucent center. They consist of <strong>asbestos fibers</strong> coated with an iron-containing proteinaceous material and arise when these fibers are <strong>phagocytosed</strong> by <strong>macrophages</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other inorganic particulates may become coated with similar <strong>iron-protein complexes</strong> and are called <strong>ferruginous bodies</strong>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2935","text":"Patients A, B, and C prese‌nted to the OPD with symp‍toms of progressive dyspn​ea and cough. Further his​tory and workup revealed ​the following findings. W‍ho among them is more sus​ceptible to developing tu​berculosis? Patient Occup​ation Chest x-ray A Auto ​mechanic Calcified pleura​l plaques B Works in sand​blasting industry Eggshel‌l calcifications C Coal m​iner Upper lobe fibrosis","raw_text":"<p>Patients A, B, and C presented to the OPD with symptoms of progressive dyspnea and cough. Further history and workup revealed the following findings.  Who among them is more susceptible to developing tuberculosis?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 122px; width: 573px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 8px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 197px; height: 8px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Patient    </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 197px; height: 8px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Occupation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 360px; height: 8px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Chest x-ray</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 53px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 197px; height: 53px; text-align: center;\">A   </td>\n<td style=\"width: 197px; height: 53px; text-align: center;\">Auto mechanic  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 360px; height: 53px; text-align: center;\"> Calcified pleural plaques</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 53px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 197px; height: 53px; text-align: center;\">B  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 197px; height: 53px; text-align: center;\">Works in sandblasting industry  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 360px; height: 53px; text-align: center;\"> Eggshell calcifications</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 53px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 197px; height: 53px; text-align: center;\">C</td>\n<td style=\"width: 197px; height: 53px; text-align: center;\">Coal miner  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 360px; height: 53px; text-align: center;\"> Upper lobe fibrosis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patients‌ A and B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Patient‌ B only","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Patients‌ A and C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patient‌ C only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Patient B only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Patient B only</strong></p><p><strong>Patient B</strong> is a worker in the sandblasting industry with <strong>eggshell calcification</strong> on x-ray. This is suggestive of <strong>silicosis. </strong>Patients with silicosis have a higher risk of <strong>tuberculosis</strong> as the crystalline silica impairs the ability of pulmonary macrophages to kill phagocytosed mycobacteria.</p>\n<p>The given clinical picture of <strong>patient A</strong> is classical of <strong>asbestosis </strong>and that of <strong>patient C</strong> is suggestive of <strong>anthracosis.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 697\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<table style=\"height: 431px; border-color: #0f0202; background-color: #ffffff;\" width=\"544\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 128.453px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.062px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Anthracosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.562px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Silicosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.938px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Asbestosis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 114px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 128.453px; height: 114px;\">Mineral dust</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.062px; height: 114px;\">Coal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.562px; height: 114px;\">\n<p>Silica:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Amorphous</li>\n<li>Crystalline<br/><br/></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.938px; height: 114px;\">\n<p>Asbestos:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Serpentine</li>\n<li>Amphibolic<br/><br/></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 155px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 128.453px; height: 155px;\">Radiology</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.062px; height: 155px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Nodules in initial stages</li>\n<li>Progressive massive fibrosis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.562px; height: 155px;\">\n<ul>\n<li dir=\"ltr\">Bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy</li>\n<li dir=\"ltr\">Eggshell calcification</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.938px; height: 155px;\">\n<ul>\n<li dir=\"ltr\">\n<p dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Pleural plaque</p>\n</li>\n<li dir=\"ltr\">\n<p dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Pleural effusion</p>\n</li>\n<li dir=\"ltr\">\n<p dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Interstitial lung fibrosis</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 100px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 128.453px; height: 100px;\">Microscopic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.062px; height: 100px;\">\n<ul>\n<li dir=\"ltr\">\n<p dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Macule</p>\n</li>\n<li dir=\"ltr\">\n<p dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Nodule</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.562px; height: 100px;\">Non-caseating granuloma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.938px; height: 100px;\">\n<ul>\n<li dir=\"ltr\">Asbestos body</li>\n<li dir=\"ltr\">Ferruginous body</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 128.453px; height: 18px;\">TB risk</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.062px; height: 18px;\">            -</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.562px; height: 18px;\">            +</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.938px; height: 18px;\">           -</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 128.453px; height: 18px;\">Carcinoma risk</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.062px; height: 18px;\">            -</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129.562px; height: 18px;\">            +</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.938px; height: 18px;\">      +++++</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9038","text":"A 60-year-old man presente‌d with dyspnea and cough.‍ Chest X-ray revealed irr​egular linear densities, ​particularly in both lowe​r lobes. Histopathologica‍l examination of lung bio​psy is shown below. This ​patient has a higher risk​ of developing:","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presented with dyspnea and cough. Chest X-ray revealed irregular linear densities, particularly in both lower lobes. Histopathological examination of lung biopsy is shown below. This patient has a higher risk of developing:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Centrilobul‌ar emphysema","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pneu‌monia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Occupatio‌nal asthma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lung ca‌rcinoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lung carcinoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6795c378539742c3aa482981179c3d0b.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/80e9ad9c0de144278d97568f05e7055dx720x808.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lung carcinoma</strong></p><p>The image shows golden-brown, beaded rods with a translucent center called <strong>asbestos bodies</strong> which are seen in <strong>asbestosis</strong>. These patients are at risk of developing both <strong>lung carcinoma</strong> and <strong>mesothelioma</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 698\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Asbestosis is marked by <strong>diffuse pulmonary interstitial fibrosis</strong> and the presence of multiple asbestos bodies.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Option A: Centrilobular emphysema may develop in simple coal workers' pneumoconiosis. </p>\n<p>Option B: Increased susceptibility to tuberculosis is seen in silicosis. </p>\n<p>Option C: Occupational asthma may be triggered by fumes (epoxy resins, plastics), organic and chemical dust (wood, cotton, platinum), gases (toluene), or other chemicals (formaldehyde, penicillin products).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2941","text":"A 45-year-old woman presen‌ts with complaints of low‍-grade fever, dry cough, ​and progressive dyspnea. ​Examination reveals the f​ollowing painless skin le‍sion. Chest X-ray shows b​ilateral hilar lymphadeno​pathy. Lung biopsy shows ​non-caseating granulomas.​ Which of the following i​s not a feature of this c​ondition?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman presents with complaints of low-grade fever, dry cough, and progressive dyspnea. Examination reveals the following painless skin lesion. Chest X-ray shows bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy. Lung biopsy shows non-caseating granulomas. Which of the following is not a feature of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD4/CD8 T-cell ratio &amp;gt; ‌3.5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Impaired dendritic cell fu‌nction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Monoclonal hypergammaglobu‌linemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anergy to common skin test‌ antigens","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Monoclonal hypergammaglobulinemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/adca01c9b26a4e4f8d5054c6e863f40d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5dc001a7e0144575aa8340225dae31cbx669x452.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/01c9a03861ce443598b16a85e9f581dbx474x595.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f8499e036024b638f63dd24dc93a28ex474x595.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3b9ca5785ece40eb86811f95ed854d66x800x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b22b8731f38a4f8eb8a5ed1ecb90a78ex800x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Monoclonal hypergammaglobulinemia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and image showing the lesion of lupus pernio point towards <strong>sarcoidosis.</strong> It is a multisystemic disease characterized by <strong>polyclonal hypergammaglobulinemia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Lupus pernio</strong> is seen in <strong>cutaneous sarcoidosis</strong> and presents as infiltrated erythematoviolaceous plaques involving the nose, cheeks, ears, lips, forehead, and fingers. On the cheeks, <strong>telangiectasia</strong> is characteristic. It is usually <strong>painless</strong>. As it does not ulcerate, it is not as mutilating as lupus vulgaris.</p>\n<p>Immunological abnormalities include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Polyclonal hyperglobulinemia</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A CD4/CD8 ratio &gt; 3.5</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">T- cell expansion and macrophage activation</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Impaired dendritic cell function</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Anergy to common skin tests.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>hallmarks</strong> of sarcoidosis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>B/L hilar lymphadenopathy</li>\n<li>Non-caseating granulomas</li>\n<li>Lupus pernio</li>\n<li>Erythema nodosum (acute sarcoidosis)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Syndromes associated with sarcoidosis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mikulicz syndrome</strong> -<strong> s</strong>arcoidosis combined with uveoparotid involvement</li>\n<li><strong>Lofgren's syndrome</strong> -<strong> </strong>acute presentation of sarcoidosis, consisting of arthritis, erythema nodosum, and bilateral hilar adenopathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows the characteristic non-caseating sarcoid granuloma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows stellate inclusions known as <strong>asteroid bodies</strong> that are found within giant cells in approximately 60% of the granulomas.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows laminated concretions composed of calcium and proteins known as <strong>Schaumann bodies </strong>that are found within giant cells in approximately 60% of the granulomas.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2948","text":"What is the most common ge‌ne mutation associated wi‍th pulmonary hypertension​?","raw_text":"What is the most common gene mutation associated with pulmonary hypertension?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BM‌PR1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"BM‌PR2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"BM‌PR3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BM‌PR4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. BMPR2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. BMPR2</strong></p><p>Bone morphogenetic protein receptor 2 (<strong>BMPR2</strong>)  is the most common gene mutation in <strong>pulmonary hypertension</strong>. Inactivating mutations in this gene are found in 75% of the familial cases of pulmonary hypertension, and 25% of sporadic cases.</p>\n<p>The BMPR2 receptor is a cell surface protein belonging to the <strong>TGF-β receptor</strong> superfamily. It is involved in the regulation of growth, differentiation, and apoptosis of pulmonary artery endothelial and smooth muscle cells.</p>\n<p>Due to the mutation, there is <strong>enhanced proliferation</strong> and <strong>reduced apoptosis</strong> of vascular <strong>endothelial</strong> and <strong>smooth muscle</strong> <strong>cells</strong> leading to pulmonary hypertension. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2943","text":"A 57-year-old man presente‌d with a persistent dry c‍ough and progressive dysp​nea. HRCT showed a crazy-​paving pattern. Microscop​y of BAL aspirate is show‍n below. Antibodies again​st which of the following​ are seen in this conditi​on?","raw_text":"A 57-year-old man presented with a persistent dry cough and progressive dyspnea. HRCT showed a crazy-paving pattern. Microscopy of BAL aspirate is shown below. Antibodies against which of the following are seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leci‌thin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prot‌ein A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prot‌ein B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"GM-‌CSF","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. GM-CSF","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0feaaa21d30a4d2ba7713e1d7ab68af1.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/61df779a02f744d08099e0f7fcbfb00ax720x474.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f712fc10d7f3489799bb76658e1392cfx720x474.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. GM-CSF</strong></p><p>The given scenario and microscopy showing <strong>alveoli</strong> filled with a dense, <strong>amorphous,</strong> <strong>protein-lipid granular precipitate</strong> are suggestive of autoimmune pulmonary alveolar proteinosis (<strong>PAP</strong>). It<strong> </strong>is caused by circulating neutralizing <strong>antibodies</strong> specific for granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor (<strong>GM-CSF</strong>).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>PAP is a condition characterized by the <strong>accumulation</strong> of <strong>surfactants</strong> in alveoli and terminal airways. It can be due to <strong>impaired clearance</strong> or <strong>abnormal production</strong> of surfactants. </p>\n<p>PAP can be of three forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary </strong>(<strong>most common</strong>): There is impaired <strong>GM-CSF</strong>-dependent surfactant clearance by the alveolar macrophages. It can be due to: \n<ul>\n<li>Autoantibodies against GM-CSF (acquired/idiopathic/autoimmune PAP) </li>\n<li>Mutation in GM-CSF receptors (hereditary PAP) </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary</strong>: There is a reduction in the number or ability of alveolar macrophages that causes impaired surfactant clearance. It can be due to:\n<ul>\n<li>Silicosis</li>\n<li>Immunodeficiency disorders</li>\n<li>Hematopoietic disorders and malignancies</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Congenital</strong>: It affects neonates and children. There is abnormal surfactant production due to mutation in the genes coding for:\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Surfactant protein B </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Surfactant protein C</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">ATP binding cassette protein member 3</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6935","text":"Match the following: Condi‌tion Clinical association‍ 1. Silicosis a. Basal lo​bes are involved 2. Mesot​helioma b. Malignant pleu​ral effusion without medi‍astinal shift 3. Caplan s​yndrome c. Initially demo​nstrated in coal workers ​4. Asbestosis d. Crazy pa​vement","raw_text":"<p>Match the following:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 120px;\" width=\"410\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 197px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Condition       </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 197px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Clinical association</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 197px; height: 18px;\">1. Silicosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 197px; height: 18px;\">a. Basal lobes are involved</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 197px; height: 54px;\">2. Mesothelioma    </td>\n<td style=\"width: 197px; height: 54px;\">b. Malignant pleural effusion without mediastinal shift </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 197px; height: 36px;\">3. Caplan syndrome     </td>\n<td style=\"width: 197px; height: 36px;\">c. Initially demonstrated in coal workers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 197px;\">4. Asbestosis<br/> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 197px;\">d. Crazy pavement   </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-b, 2-d,‌ 3-a, 4-c","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-d, 2-b,‌ 3-c, 4-a","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-a, 2-b,‌ 3-c, 4-d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-a, 2-b,‌ 3-d, 4-c","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-d, 2-b, 3-c, 4-a","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-d, 2-b, 3-c, 4-a</strong></p><p>The correctly matched associations of the following conditions are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Silicosis: Crazy paving pattern</li>\n<li>Mesothelioma: Malignant pleural effusion without mediastinal shift </li>\n<li>Caplan syndrome: Initially demonstrated in coal workers</li>\n<li>Asbestosis: Basal lobes are involved</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"height: 177px; width: 699px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 26px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 216.3125px; height: 26px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.6875px; height: 26px;\">Silicosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 226px; height: 26px;\">Asbestosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 226px; height: 26px;\">Malignant mesothelioma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 71px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 216.3125px; height: 71px;\">Exposure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.6875px; height: 71px;\">Crystalline <strong>silica</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 226px; height: 71px;\"><strong>Asbestos</strong> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 226px; height: 71px;\">Most specific neoplasm associated with <strong>asbestos</strong> exposure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 71px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 216.3125px; height: 71px;\">\n<p>Radiologic/ Gross findings</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.6875px; height: 71px;\">\n<p>Simple silicosis - small rounded <strong>opacities</strong> in upper lobes</p>\n<p>Complicated silicosis - Larger opacities &gt; 1cm and <strong>eggshell calcification</strong> of hilar lymph nodes</p>\n<p>Acute silicosis - ground-glass opacities on X-ray and <strong>crazy paving pattern</strong> on HRCT (interlobular septal thickening with background of ground-glass opacity)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 226px; height: 71px;\">\n<p> </p>\n<p>X-ray shows peripheral <strong>reticular opacities</strong> predominantly in the <strong>basal lobes</strong></p>\n<p>HRCT shows subpleural fibrotic lines, irregular opacities, <strong>traction</strong> <strong>bronchiectasis</strong>, and <strong>honeycombing</strong> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 226px; height: 71px;\">Diffuse gelatinous, <strong>greyish-pink lesion</strong> arising either from the visceral or parietal pleura. </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 71px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 216.3125px; height: 71px;\">Clinical manifestations</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.6875px; height: 71px;\">Increased risk of developing TB and bronchogenic carcinoma </td>\n<td style=\"width: 226px; height: 71px;\">\n<p>It mainly affects the lower lobes  </p>\n<p>PFT shows a <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">restrictive pattern</strong> with a decrease in lung volumes and diffusing capacity</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 226px; height: 71px;\">\n<p>Strong male predilection 3:1</p>\n<p>Can cause <strong>malignant pleural effusion</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Caplan's syndrome</strong> also called rheumatoid pneumoconiosis, is a combination of <strong>rheumatoid</strong> <strong>arthritis</strong><strong> and pneumoconiosis. </strong>It manifests as pneumoconiotic nodules along with features of seropositive<strong> </strong>rheumatoid arthritis. Caplan syndrome was first described in <strong>coal miners</strong> and later in patients with silicosis too.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1448","text":"A 70-year-old patient pres‌ented with cough, fatigua‍bility, and weight loss. ​He was diagnosed with squ​amous cell carcinoma (SCC​) on bronchoscopy. The re‍sected specimen had a hil​ar lymph node that was 1c​m in size and showed a bl​ack pigment. What is the ​black pigment likely to b​e?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old patient presented with cough, fatiguability, and weight loss. He was diagnosed with squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) on bronchoscopy. The resected specimen had a hilar lymph node that was 1cm in size and showed a black pigment. What is the black pigment likely to be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anthracot‌ic pigment","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mel‌anin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lipoc‌hrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemos‌iderin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anthracotic pigment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/43b083f8eb02496abf32e6cc1281f222x1279x1086.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2fe02d8ad362433a97f09f76c318f950x1279x1086.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe8c2568e4f44f42b4610ae6edc243dax1280x1156.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8e1b8dbc72654c858b2250b690f06d39x1280x1239.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/33261f7e87c945c4b60a4916ad1f5d20x1280x1239.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1554faf40d574d00b6e437f281b5f750x1280x1156.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anthracotic pigment</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>The black-colored pigment in the hilar lymph node of the resected bronchoscopic specimen is likely to be an <strong>anthracotic pigment</strong>.</p>\n<p>The black color is due to the deposition of <strong>carbon particles. </strong>The inhaled carbon particles are engulfed by the lung <strong>macrophages</strong> and then transported to the <strong>hilar lymph nodes. </strong>This accumulates and causes the <strong>blackening of the lung</strong> <strong>tissues </strong>or<strong> anthracosis. </strong></p>\n<p>It is commonly seen in people who work in environments where there is high air pollution with carbon particles, such as coal mines.<strong> </strong>It is also the most common exogenous pigment found in the human body as a result of air pollution in urban areas. The aggregates of the carbon dust may induce fibroblastic reaction or emphysema that may lead to a condition called <strong>coal worker's pneumoconiosis.</strong></p>\n<p>The following image shows anthracosis with deposition of black carbon particles, within the cytoplasm of macrophages.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Melanin</strong> is <strong>brown-black</strong> in color which occurs as a result of oxidation of tyrosine in melanocytes, as shown in the following histopathology of a lymph node. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C:<strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> Lipochrome</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> or </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">lipofuscin </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">(brown lipid) is seen as a </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">yellow-brown pigment</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> in histopathological sections of the liver and heart of normal </span>aging patients, patients with severe malnutrition, and cancer cachexia.<strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">It is d</span>erived through<strong> lipid peroxidation</strong> and indicates free radical injury and lipid peroxidation. The following image shows a cardiac myocyte with fine yellow-brown lipofuscin granules in the perinuclear region.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Hemosiderin</strong> is a hemoglobin-derived <strong>golden-yellow</strong> colored pigment seen in conditions associated with iron overload such as hemochromatosis. The following image shows extensive intra-alveolar hemosiderin deposition following pulmonary hemorrhage.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"OE3cS4NM","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"59_hematinics","title":"59.Hematinics","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MA8046","text":"A newly married couple pla​nning for pregnancy has c​ome to your clinic for pr‍econception counselling. ​Which of the following dr‍ugs will you prescribe to‌ the patient to decrease ​the chances of having neu‌ral tube defects in the b​aby?","raw_text":"A newly married couple planning for pregnancy has come to your clinic for preconception counselling. Which of the following drugs will you prescribe to the patient to decrease the chances of having neural tube defects in the baby?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitam​in B9","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vitam​in B12","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vita​min C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vitam​in B6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vitamin B9","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vitamin B9</strong></p><p>Preconceptional intake of <strong>vitamin B9 (folic acid)</strong> results in decreased incidence of neural tube defects like spina bifida, encephalocele, and anencephaly.</p>\n<p>All women of reproductive age should be given <strong>400 µg</strong> of folic acid daily.  Women, who had fetal neural tube defects in the previous pregnancy, should be given <strong>4 mg</strong> of folic acid daily beginning 1 month before conception to about 12 weeks of pregnancy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8050","text":"A 25-year-old patient has ​subclinical folate defici​ency. Which of the follow‍ing drugs is least likely​ to precipitate megalobla‍stic anaemia in this pati‌ent?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old patient has subclinical folate deficiency. Which of the following drugs is least likely to precipitate megaloblastic anaemia in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alc​ohol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phen​ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlor​oquine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sulfas​alazine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chloroquine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chloroquine</strong></p><p>Among the following, <strong>chloroquine</strong> is the least likely drug to precipitate folic acid deficiency.</p>\n<p>The drugs that can result in the <strong>deficiency of folic acid</strong> include anticonvulsants (<strong>phenytoin</strong>, primidone), <strong>oral contraceptives,</strong> and dihydrofolate reductase inhibitors (trimethoprim<strong>, sulfasalazine, </strong>methotrexate). Megaloblastic anemia can be caused by a deficiency of folic acid or vitamin B12.</p>\n<p>A mnemonic for the causes of megaloblastic anemia is: P<span style=\"font-size: 8pt;\">rime</span> BATSMAN</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li>P<span style=\"font-size: 8pt;\">rime</span> – phenytoin, primidone</li>\n<li>B – B12 and folate deficiency</li>\n<li>A – alcohol</li>\n<li>T – Trimethoprim</li>\n<li>S – Sulfasalazine</li>\n<li>M – Metformin</li>\n<li>A – Antifolate (methotrexate)</li>\n<li>N – N<sub>2</sub>O</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8052","text":"A 25-year-old patient pres​ented with complaints of ​burning sensations of the‍ palms and soles. History​ revealed a predominantly‍ vegetarian diet and work‌up showed low levels of s​erum B12. Treatment for t‌his patient must not be a​dministered through which‌ of the following routes?‍","raw_text":"A 25-year-old patient presented with complaints of burning sensations of the palms and soles. History revealed a predominantly vegetarian diet and workup showed low levels of serum B12. Treatment for this patient must not be administered through which of the following routes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral ​route","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deep subcut​aneous route","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intraven​ous route","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intramusc​ular route","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Intravenous route","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Intravenous route</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12) should <strong>never</strong> be administered through the <strong>intravenous route</strong>. This is because IV<strong> </strong>administration will result in almost all of the vitamin being <strong>lost in the urine.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Cyanocobalamin is usually administered by the <strong>intramuscular or deep subcutaneous</strong> route. In patients with a previous history of sensitivity to vitamin B12 injections, an intradermal skin test of cyanocobalamine should be done.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8073","text":"Absorption of iron is impe​ded by which of the follo​wing?","raw_text":"Absorption of iron is impeded by which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meat bas​ed foods","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ascorb​ic acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amino acids containing –SH​ groups","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Egg bas​ed foods","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Egg based foods","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Egg based foods</strong></p><p>The absorption of iron is impeded by <strong>egg-based </strong>foods. They contain a high amount of <strong>phosphates</strong> (egg yolk) that <strong>form complexes with iron</strong> and impede absorption.</p>\n<p><strong>Heme-based iron</strong>, present in meat products, is <strong>easily absorbed</strong> without the aid of a carrier and is not much affected by other foods. Inorganic iron is affected by several factors.</p>\n<p>Factors <strong>increasing </strong>iron absorption are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reducing substances</strong> such as ascorbic acid and amino acids containing –SH groups - reduce ferric iron to the soluble ferrous form</li>\n<li><strong>Meat</strong>-based foods - enhance heme absorption and increase acid secretion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Factors <strong>decreasing </strong>iron absorption are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phosphates</strong>, phytates, and tetracyclines - by forming complexes</li>\n<li>Alkalies - prevents the reduction of ferric iron to the soluble ferrous form</li>\n<li>Presence of other food in the stomach - by increasing pH</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8071","text":"When treating an anemic pa​tient with a history of i​nflammatory bowel disease‍, which of the following ​iron formulations will yo‍u prefer?","raw_text":"When treating an anemic patient with a history of inflammatory bowel disease, which of the following iron formulations will you prefer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ferrous ​sulphate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ferrous ​gluconate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ferrous​ sucrose","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ferrous ​fumarate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ferrous sucrose","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ferrous sucrose</strong></p><p>In an anemic patient with <strong>IBD</strong>, <strong>parenteral</strong> <strong>iron is preferred</strong> over oral formulations. The parenteral formulation of iron among the given options is <strong>ferrous sucrose</strong>.</p>\n<p>On intravenous administration, it is taken up by reticuloendothelial cells where it dissociates and iron is utilized.</p>\n<p>The formulations used for <strong>parenteral iron</strong> therapy are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Iron dextran</li>\n<li>Iron - sorbitol - citric acid</li>\n<li>Ferrous sucrose</li>\n<li>Ferric carboxy maltose</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ferrous sulfate, ferrous gluconate, and ferrous fumarate are <strong>oral</strong> iron preparations. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8066","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding erythro​poietin is false? Used to‍ treat anaemia due to chr​onic renal failure Causes‍ functional iron deficien‌cy anaemia Increases requ​irement for blood transfu‌sions Can aggravate hyper​tension Decreases the ret‌iculocyte count","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements regarding erythropoietin is false?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Used to treat anaemia due to chronic renal failure</li>\n<li>Causes functional iron deficiency anaemia</li>\n<li>Increases requirement for blood transfusions</li>\n<li>Can aggravate hypertension</li>\n<li>Decreases the reticulocyte count</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3, 4 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 a​nd 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 o​nly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3 and 5</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Erythropoietin</strong> decreases the need for blood transfusions and increases reticulocyte counts.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Therapeutic uses and effects of erythropoietin:    </p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">The primary indication of erythropoietin is anaemia due to <strong>chronic renal failure</strong> </li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">It causes functional iron deficiency due to the inability to mobilize iron stores rapidly enough to support the increased erythropoiesis (<strong>low transferrin saturation</strong>)</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">It increases the hematocrit, haemoglobin and reduces the need for transfusions </li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">It <strong>aggravates hypertension</strong> due to an increase in hematocrit and leads to thrombus formation</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Release of reticulocyte into circulation from the marrow and results in an <strong>increase</strong> <strong>in reticulocyte count</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8067","text":"In a patient with anaemia ​due to chronic renal fail​ure, which of the followi‍ng is the long-acting der​ivative of erythropoietin‍ suitable for treatment?","raw_text":"In a patient with anaemia due to chronic renal failure, which of the following is the long-acting derivative of erythropoietin suitable for treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oprel​vekin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Darbepoe​tin alfa","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dornas​e alfa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Filga​strim","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Darbepoetin alfa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Darbepoetin alfa</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Darbepoetin alpha</strong> is the recombinant derivative of erythropoietin that is long-acting and approved for anaemia due to chronic renal failure. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option A: Oprelvekin is an <strong>IL-11 analog</strong> that stimulates megakaryocyte maturation. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C: Dornase alfa is a <strong>recombinant human DNase 1</strong> that is used in cystic fibrosis to reduce the viscosity of mucosal secretions. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option D: Filgrastim is a recombinant human <strong>G-CSF</strong>, indicated in the treatment of severe neutropenia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8064","text":"Sargramostim is a recombin​antly engineered analogue​ of which of the followin‍g?","raw_text":"Sargramostim is a recombinantly engineered analogue of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"G-​CSF","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"GM-​CSF","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Erythro​poietin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IL​-11","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. GM-CSF","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. GM-CSF</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Sargramostim </strong>is a <strong>recombinant human GM-CSF </strong>(Granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Indications for use:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Autologous <strong>bone marrow transplant</strong> <strong>-</strong> reduces the duration of neutropenia</li>\n<li>Cancer <strong>chemotherapy -</strong> reduces the duration of neutropenia</li>\n<li>Peripheral blood <strong>stem cell collection -</strong> to mobilize CD34 positive progenitor cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is administered subcutaneously or by slow intravenous infusion. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7477","text":"Which of the following is ​true about pegylated-filg​rastim?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about pegylated-filgrastim?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duration of action is shor​ter than normal filgrasti​m","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Added advantage is that it​ can be taken orally","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It should not be administe​red in sickle cell patien​ts","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is not effective in the​ treatment of severe neut​ropenia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It should not be administered in sickle cell patients","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It should not be administered in sickle cell patients</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Patients with sickle cell anemia should not receive pegylated filgrastim as it can trigger a <strong>sickle cell crisis.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Filgrastim </strong>is a recombinant human G-CSF (granulocyte colony-stimulating factor). The pegylated forms of filgrastim, pegfilgrastim, and lipefilgrastim, are longer-acting forms. It is administered subcutaneously or by slow intravenous infusion only.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Indications of recombinant G-CSF:   </p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li>Severe neutropenia after autologous hematopoietic stem cell transplantation</li>\n<li>Severe neutropenia after high dose cancer chemotherapy</li>\n<li>Severe congenital neutropenias</li>\n<li>Neutropenia of patients with <strong>AIDS</strong> receiving <strong>zidovudine</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8068","text":"Which of the following is ​not a thrombopoietin rece​ptor agonist?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a thrombopoietin receptor agonist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lusutr​ombopag","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Romip​lostim","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eltro​mbopag","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oprel​vekin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Oprelvekin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Oprelvekin</strong></p><p>Oprelvekin is an IL-11 analogue that stimulates megakaryocyte maturation and is <strong>not</strong> a thrombopoietin receptor agonist.</p>\n<p><strong>Romiplostim, </strong><strong>eltrombopag </strong>and<strong> lusutrombopag </strong>are thrombopoietin receptor agonists. They are indicated in patients with immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP) who have failed to respond to the conventional treatments.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8061","text":"Which of the following age​nts is used in the treatm​ent of patients who devel‍op severe thrombocytopeni​a following anti-cancer t‍herapy?","raw_text":"Which of the following agents is used in the treatment of patients who develop severe thrombocytopenia following anti-cancer therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Filgr​astim","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oprel​vekin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Erythro​poietin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iron d​extran","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Oprelvekin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Oprelvekin</strong></p><p><strong>Oprelvekin </strong>is used in the treatment of <strong>thrombocytopenia secondary to anti-cancer therapy</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>recombinant human interleukin-11</strong>. Its predominant hemopoietic activity is the stimulation of <strong>megakaryocytopoiesis.</strong> Oprelvekin is indicated for the prevention and treatment of severe thrombocytopenia and the reduction of platelet transfusion requirements following myelosuppressive chemotherapy in patients with non-myeloid malignancies.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3437","text":"A patient requires autolog​ous stem cell transplanta​tion due to multiple myel‍oma. A CXCR-4 antagonist ​which mobilizes stem cell‍s to the peripheral blood‌ is administered few week​s before the procedure. I‌dentify the drug.","raw_text":"A patient requires autologous stem cell transplantation due to multiple myeloma. A CXCR-4 antagonist which mobilizes stem cells to the peripheral blood is administered few weeks before the procedure. Identify the drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mara​viroc","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sargra​mostim","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Filgr​astim","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pleri​xafor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Plerixafor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Plerixafor</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Plerixafor</strong> is the <strong>CXCR-4 antagonist</strong> indicated for peripheral blood stem cell mobilization before autologous stem cell transplantation.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is indicated for <strong>peripheral blood stem cell mobilization</strong> in patients with Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma and multiple myeloma having inadequate response to G-CSF.  It mobilizes the release and entry of HSCs from the bone marrow into circulation. Peripheral blood stem cell mobilization/collection is followed by autologous transplantation. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option A: <strong>Maraviroc</strong> is a CCR-5 receptor inhibitor indicated in HIV infections.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Options B and C: <strong>Sargramostim</strong> (GM-CSF) and <strong>filgrastim</strong> (G-CSF) are used for peripheral blood stem cell mobilization.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4352","text":"A patient having chronic l​iver disease is set to un​dergo a tooth extraction.‍ During pre-op evaluation​, his platelet count is f‍ound to be 60 x 10^9/ L. ‌Which thrombopoietin rece​ptor agonist can you pres‌cribe to treat this?","raw_text":"A patient having chronic liver disease is set to undergo a tooth extraction. During pre-op evaluation, his platelet count is found to be 60 x 10^9/ L. Which thrombopoietin receptor agonist can you prescribe to treat this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eltro​mbopag","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Romip​lostim","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oprel​vekin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Avatro​mbopag","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Avatrombopag","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Avatrombopag</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Avatrombopag </strong>is a thrombopoietin receptor agonist approved for the treatment of<strong> thrombocytopenia with chronic liver disease </strong>in patients<strong> </strong>who are scheduled to undergo a <strong>medical or dental procedure</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Thrombopoietin receptor agonist stimulates proliferation and differentiation of megakaryocytes from bone marrow progenitor cells resulting in increased production of platelets. <strong>Lusutrombopag </strong>is a drug with similar action and function.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Options A &amp; B:<strong> Eltrombopag </strong>and<strong> romiplostim</strong> are thrombopoietin receptor agonists indicated in <strong>chronic immune thrombocytopenia.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option C: <strong>Oprelvekin</strong> is a recombinant IL-11 that is used in the prophylaxis of <strong>thrombocytopenia</strong> during <strong>cancer chemotherapy</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4354","text":"A 26-year-old woman presen​ted to the OPD with multi​ple purpuric lesions. Fur‍ther workup revealed isol​ated thrombocytopenia wit‍h a normal peripheral sme‌ar. Which tyrosine kinase​ inhibitor is indicated i‌n her treatment?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old woman presented to the OPD with multiple purpuric lesions. Further workup revealed isolated thrombocytopenia with a normal peripheral smear. Which tyrosine kinase inhibitor is indicated in her treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Romip​lostim","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bosu​tinib","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fostam​atinib","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Oprel​vekin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fostamatinib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fostamatinib</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The scenario in the question depicts a case of<strong> chronic immune thrombocytopenia </strong>which can be treated with a<strong> tyrosine kinase inhibitor </strong>called<strong> fostamatinib.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Fostamatinib has activity against <strong>spleen tyrosine kinase</strong> (SYK). The major metabolite of fostamatinib, R406, inhibits signal transduction of Fc-activating receptors and B-cell receptors. The fostamatinib metabolite R406 reduces antibody-mediated destruction of platelets. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option A: <strong>Romiplostim</strong> is a <strong>TPO receptor agonist</strong> indicated in <strong>chronic immune thrombocytopenia. </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option B: <strong>Bosutinib</strong> is a <strong>BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase inhibitor</strong> indicated in <strong>CML</strong>. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option D: <strong>Oprelvekin</strong> is a recombinant IL-11 that is used in the prophylaxis of <strong>thrombocytopenia </strong>during<strong> cancer chemotherapy. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"297rNEkK","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"60_bones__joints_and_cartilage","title":"60.Bones, Joints And Cartilage","num_questions":30,"total_marks":120,"duration":40,"time_per_question":80.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA9579","text":"The total number of bones ‌present in the axial skel‍eton and appendicular ske​leton in an adult human b​eing is ______ and ______​, respectively.","raw_text":"The total number of bones present in the axial skeleton and appendicular skeleton in an adult human being is ______ and ______, respectively.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"88,‌ 118","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"84,‌ 122","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"76,‌ 130","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"80,‌ 126","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 80, 126","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/640466b3c1f2419ca7e068a5b3066ee3x1280x2560.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 80, 126</strong></p><p>In the adult, <strong>80 bones</strong> are present in the <strong>axial skeleton</strong>, and <strong>126 bones</strong> in the <strong>appendicular skeleton</strong>. </p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Region </strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Number</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Bones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skull</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">28</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Cranial bones (8)</li>\n<li>Facial bones (14)</li>\n<li>Auditory ossicles (6)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hyoid</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">1</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li> Hyoid bone (1)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vertebral column</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">26</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Cervical vertebrae (7)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Thoracic vertebrae (12)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Lumbar vertebrae (5)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Sacrum (1)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Coccyx (1)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thoracic cage</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">25</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Ribs (24)</li>\n<li>Sternum (1)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Axial Skeleton - </strong><strong>80</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pectoral girdle</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">4</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Clavicle (2)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Scapula (2)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Upper extremity</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">60</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Humerus (2)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Radius (2)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Ulna (2)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Carpals (16)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Metacarpals (10)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Phalanges (28)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pelvic girdle</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">2</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Hip bones (2)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lower extremity</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">60</td>\n<td> \n<ul>\n<li>Femur (2)</li>\n<li>Tibia (2)</li>\n<li>Fibula (2)</li>\n<li>Patella (2)</li>\n<li>Tarsals (14)</li>\n<li>Metatarsals (10)</li>\n<li>Phalanges (28)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Appendicular Skeleton - </strong><strong>126</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9577","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding bones i‍s false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding bones is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteocytes are the major c‌ell type in mature bone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Collagen is synthesized in‌ bone by osteoblasts","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bone mainly contains type ‌2 collagen fibres","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Calcium phosphate is the m‌ajor inorganic mineral","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bone mainly contains type 2 collagen fibres","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bone mainly contains type 2 collagen fibres</strong></p><p>Bone mainly contains <strong>type 1 collagen</strong> fibres. Bones are made up of mineralized collagenous <strong>extracellular matrix</strong> around <strong>specialized cells</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>The extracellular matrix is composed of:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Organic component - around 30% of the organic matrix of bone is made up of collagen, <strong>mainly type 1 collagen.</strong></li>\n<li>Inorganic component -<strong> </strong>the major inorganic mineral in the bone matrix is <strong>calcium phosphate </strong>in the form of microcrystalline hydroxyapatite.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The specialized cells are:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Osteoblasts </strong>- responsible for the synthesis, deposition, and mineralization of the bone matrix. Collagen is synthesized in bone by osteoblasts. Osteoblasts get embedded in the matrix to become osteocytes.</li>\n<li><strong>Osteocytes </strong>- <strong>major cell type</strong> of a mature bone and is distributed throughout the matrix. They have lost the ability to divide or to secrete new matrix.</li>\n<li><strong>Osteoclasts </strong>- responsible for the local removal of bone during bone growth and remodelling.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9587","text":"Which of the following is ‌a modified long bone?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a modified long bone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hum‌erus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fib‌ula","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metac‌arpal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clav‌icle","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Clavicle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3766fde0c5ca4c14b32032c38cb8ab0cx684x1156.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Clavicle</strong></p><p>The <strong>clavicle</strong> is a <strong>modified long bone,</strong> as it has a modified shaft and no medullary cavity.</p>\n<p>Long bones are classified as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Typical</strong> long bones (see figure) have an elongated shaft (diaphysis) and two expanded ends (epiphysis). The shaft has a central medullary cavity and a nutrient foramen directed away from the growing end. e.g.,<strong> humerus, ulna, radius, femur, tibia,</strong> and<strong> fibula.</strong>  </li>\n<li><strong>Miniature</strong> long bones contain only one epiphysis. e.g.,<strong> metacarpals, metatarsals,</strong> and<strong> phalanges.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Modified</strong> long bones<strong> </strong>have a modified shaft or ends, and no medullary cavity. e.g.,<strong> clavicle.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows a graphical illustration of a typical long bone:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9589","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a sesamoid bone?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a sesamoid bone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fab‌ella","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Os tr‌igonum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pat‌ella","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pisi‌form","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Os trigonum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/151f1dbe842f4410a520da5ab1f1157bx1280x2048.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/101e6394649944f08ded153f9b3cf0e2x1280x2048.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Os trigonum</strong></p><p><strong>Os trigonum </strong>is an<strong> accessory bone</strong>, not a sesamoid bone. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Accessory bones, also known as <strong>supernumerary bones</strong> are not always present. They may arise due to ununited epiphysis developed from <strong>extra ossification centres</strong>. Clinically, they may be mistaken for fractures. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Examples of accessory bones:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Os trigonum - Uncommon accessory bone seen <strong>behind the talus</strong>. Also known as lateral tubercle of the talus.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Os vesalianum -<strong> </strong>Uncommon accessory bone located <strong>proximal to the fifth metatarsal</strong> and found within the <strong>peroneus brevis tendon</strong>. Also known as tuberosity of the fifth metatarsal.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9588","text":"Which of the following is ‌an example of a ​pneumati‍c bone?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an example of a ​pneumatic bone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carpal‌ bones","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Max‌illa","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ste‌rnum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hip ‌bone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Maxilla","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Maxilla</strong></p><p>The <strong>maxilla </strong>is a <strong>pneumatic bone.</strong> </p>\n<p>Pneumatic bones contain<strong> air-filled cavities</strong>, which make them light-weight. They also aid in the resonance of sound and act as air conditioning chambers for inspired air. e.g.,<strong> maxilla, sphenoid, ethmoid, etc</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Short bones: </strong>Carpal bones and tarsal bones</p>\n<p><strong>Flat bones: </strong>Sternum, ribs, scapula, and bones of the skull vault</p>\n<p><strong>Irregular bones: </strong>Hip bones, vertebra, and bones in the base of the skull.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9591","text":"Which of the following acc‌urately describes fabella‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following accurately describes fabella?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Accesso‌ry bone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pneumat‌ic bone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sesamo‌id bone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Irregul‌ar bone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sesamoid bone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/88e58b5d0f3a40cb9704a0b13b7ce4bcx1280x1157.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sesamoid bone</strong></p><p><strong>Fabella</strong> is a <strong>sesamoid</strong> bone. This term is used to describe a bone embedded within a tendon or a muscle. It is a variant of normal anatomy present in 10% to 30% of individuals. Fabella is found within the tendon of the<strong> lateral head of gastrocnemius</strong>, where it moves over the lateral femoral condyle.</p>\n<p>Important sesamoid bones:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Patella</strong> - largest sesamoid bone in the tendon of quadriceps femoris.</li>\n<li><strong>Pisiform</strong> - tendon of flexor carpi ulnaris.</li>\n<li><strong>Fabella</strong> - lateral head of the gastrocnemius. </li>\n<li>Two sesamoid bones can be found in the first metacarpal within the tendons of flexor pollicis brevis and adductor pollicis.</li>\n<li>Two sesamoid bones can be found in the first metatarsophalangeal articulation within the double tendon of flexor hallucis brevis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The X-ray image below depicts the fabella (marked with an arrow).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9596","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‍ long bones?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding long bones?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cancellous bone is absent ‌in the epiphysis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cancellous bone is present‌ in the outer cortex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Haversian system is absent‌ in cancellous bone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Compact bone has lamellae ‌arranged in a meshwork","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Haversian system is absent in cancellous bone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d83afba2bb59464da001c9bee8cdd1f8x1280x1897.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7333835b119746c1921eae5e4d28b202x600x941.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Haversian system is absent in cancellous bone</strong></p><p>The <strong>Haversian system</strong> is seen in <strong>compact</strong> bones, not cancellous bones. </p>\n<p>Based on their macroscopic structure, bones can be classified as <strong>compact</strong> and <strong>cancellous</strong> (spongy) bones. </p>\n<p><strong>Compact</strong> bones are composed of cylindrical structural units called <strong>Haversian systems</strong> or osteons. Each Haversian system contains a central canal within which the small blood vessels, nerves, and endosteum are present. This canal is surrounded by multiple layers or <strong>lamellae</strong> of the calcified matrix containing type I collagen fibers. These canals <strong>communicate</strong> with each other by <strong>Volkmann's canal.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>lamellae</strong> of the <strong>cancellous</strong>/spongy bone are arranged in a <strong>meshwork,</strong> and hence do not form Haversian systems.</p>\n<p>The <strong>diaphysis</strong> and <strong>outer cortex</strong> of long bones are predominantly made of <strong>compact</strong> bone, while the <strong>epiphysis</strong> and <strong>inner medulla</strong> are predominantly made of <strong>cancellous</strong> bone.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9605","text":"A 16-year-old boy presente‌d to your OPD with progre‍ssive swelling and worsen​ing knee pain over the pa​st 2 months. An X-ray rev​eals the following findin​g. Which of the following‍ is true regarding the la​yer of bone involved?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old boy presented to your OPD with progressive swelling and worsening knee pain over the past 2 months. An X-ray reveals the following finding. Which of the following is true regarding the layer of bone involved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It contains three fibrous ‌layers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Progenitor cells are prese‌nt in the outer fibrous l‍ayer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is continuous with the ‌capsule of the joint","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is devoid of nerve supp‌ly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is continuous with the capsule of the joint","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6aae499ff64b4a6199716cbb90d9e801.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/676e862dab0d4e0eb6b0e94676ecac1ax649x342.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/daec556d86d741de966d52197d5419cax1280x2155.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is continuous with the capsule of the joint</strong></p><p>The given X-ray shows <strong>Codman's triangle</strong>, which is due to an irregular <strong>periosteal reaction</strong> (outermost layer), commonly seen in patients with <strong>osteosarcoma.</strong> The <strong>periosteum</strong> is a thick fibrous membrane that covers the outer surface of all bones, except at the joint surfaces and insertion points of tendons and ligaments (entheses). At the articular margin, the periosteum is <strong>continuous</strong> with the <strong>capsule</strong> of the joint.</p>\n<p>The<strong> periosteum</strong> has <strong>2 layers</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>The outer <strong>fibrous</strong> <strong>layer</strong> - contains <strong>fibroblasts</strong>.</li>\n<li>The inner <strong>cambium</strong> <strong>layer</strong> (osteogenic layer) - contains <strong>progenitor</strong> <strong>cells</strong> that develop into <strong>osteoblasts.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Periosteum gets<strong> nourishment</strong> from<strong> periosteal arteries.</strong> It has a <strong>rich nerve supply,</strong> which makes it the most sensitive part of the bone.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9598","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures do the Sharpey's ‍fibers connect?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures do the Sharpey's fibers connect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Periosteum to synovial mem‌brane","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Joint capsule to periosteu‌m","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Periosteu‌m to bone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Enamel t‌o dentin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Periosteum to bone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Periosteum to bone</strong></p><p><strong>Sharpey's fibers</strong> are thick<strong> collagen</strong> <strong>fibers</strong> that connect the periosteum to the underlying bone. </p>\n<p>Sharpey's fibers are present in the bone and the teeth. </p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Bone </strong>- tether the<strong> periosteum </strong>to the<strong> underlying bone</strong>. </li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Teeth </strong>- connect the <strong>cementum </strong>to the<strong> periosteum</strong> of the<strong> alveolar bone</strong> (tooth socket).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9592","text":"Which of the following bon‌es develops through intra‍membranous ossification?","raw_text":"Which of the following bones develops through intramembranous ossification?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ri‌bs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ti‌bia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fronta‌l bone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hum‌erus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Frontal bone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Frontal bone</strong></p><p><strong>Frontal</strong> bones undergo <strong>intramembranous ossification.</strong> Other bones listed have enchondral ossification. </p>\n<p>Based on their development, bones can be classified into 3 categories: </p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Membranous bones</strong> (dermal bones) are formed through intramembranous ossification. There is direct bone formation within highly vascular sheets or ‘membranes’ derived from <strong>mesenchymal condensations.</strong> e.g., bones of the facial skeleton including the frontal, lacrimal, zygomatic, and nasal bones. </li>\n<li><strong>Cartilaginous bones </strong>are formed by intra-cartilaginous (<strong>enchondral</strong>) <strong>ossification.</strong> Bone forms by ossification of preformed hyaline cartilage model. This is the <strong>main method</strong> of bone formation in the body. e.g., bones of limbs, vertebral column, and thoracic cage. </li>\n<li><strong>Membranocartilaginous bones</strong> ossify from <strong>both cartilage </strong>and<strong> mesenchymal condensation</strong>. e.g., clavicle, mandible, occipital bone, temporal bone, sphenoid bone, etc.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9620","text":"The structure that the mar‌ked muscle in the gluteal‍ region inserts into has ​____ type of epiphysis?","raw_text":"The structure that the marked muscle in the gluteal region inserts into has ____ type of epiphysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pressure ‌epiphysis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Traction ‌epiphysis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Atavistic‌ epiphysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aberrant ‌epiphysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Traction epiphysis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e82df2a66c0443baa27c2aece55c0727.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Traction epiphysis</strong></p><p>The muscle marked in the image is the <strong>piriformis </strong>and its insertion is at the <strong>greater trochanter</strong> of the femur, which is an example of <strong>traction epiphysis.</strong></p>\n<p>Traction epiphysis is <strong>nonarticular</strong> and does not transmit weight. They provide attachment to one or more tendons that exert traction on the epiphysis. <strong>Examples</strong> of traction epiphysis include greater and lesser <strong>tubercles of the humerus </strong>and greater and lesser <strong>trochanters of the femur</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9625","text":"While assessing a child wi‌th short stature, the sen‍ior resident orders an X-​ray to better evaluate th​e structure marked X in t​he radiograph. Which of t​he following is a true st‍atement regarding this st​ructure?","raw_text":"While assessing a child with short stature, the senior resident orders an X-ray to better evaluate the structure marked X in the radiograph. Which of the following is a true statement regarding this structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is made of elastic cart‌ilage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is supplied only by epi‌physeal arteries","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It separates epiphysis fro‌m diaphysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is the site for enchond‌ral ossification","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is the site for enchondral ossification","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d17e01bdd5df433e86f0ee703ee63688.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is the site for enchondral ossification</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The structure marked X in the radiograph is the <strong>epiphyseal plate.</strong> It is the site for <strong>enchondral ossification</strong> that is mainly responsible for the <strong>growth</strong> of <strong>long bones.</strong> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The epiphyseal plate (growth plate) is a <strong>hyaline cartilage</strong> layer that separates the epiphysis from the metaphysis. It receives nourishment from both the <strong>epiphyseal</strong> and <strong>metaphyseal arteries</strong>. Fractures involving epiphyseal plates are known as <strong>Salter-Harris fractures</strong>. </p>\n<p>The growing ends of bones include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Upper limb: Upper end of<strong> humerus</strong>, and lower ends of<strong> radius and ulna</strong></li>\n<li>Lower limb: Lower end of<strong> femur</strong>, and upper end of<strong> tibia </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9627","text":"'The secondary ossificatio‌n center that appears fir‍st, fuses last'. Which of​ the following bones viol​ates this law?","raw_text":"'The secondary ossification center that appears first, fuses last'. Which of the following bones violates this law?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rad‌ius","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fib‌ula","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ul‌na","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ste‌rnum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fibula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fibula</strong></p><p>The<strong> fibula</strong> violates the <strong>law of ossification </strong>which states that the secondary ossification center that appears first is the last to fuse.</p>\n<p>The secondary ossification center at the<strong> lower end of the fibula</strong> appears before the ossification center of the upper end and <strong>fuses before</strong> the upper end.</p>\n<p>Ossification centers are of two types:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Primary centers</strong> appear <strong>before birth</strong>, usually during late embryonic or early fetal life (seventh week to the fourth month). They form the <strong>diaphysis</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary centers</strong> usually appear <strong>after birth</strong>. They form the<strong> epiphysis</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9629","text":"Which of the following par‌ts of the bone is not nou‍rished by the nutrient ar​tery?","raw_text":"Which of the following parts of the bone is not nourished by the nutrient artery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medullar‌y cavity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inner 2/3 o‌f the cortex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Outer 1/3 o‌f the cortex","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Metap‌hysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Outer 1/3 of the cortex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d02cbfa7b57b453584c5af1f6de02f69x600x824.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Outer 1/3 of the cortex</strong></p><p>The <strong>nutrient artery</strong> does not nourish the <strong>outer one-third</strong> of the <strong>cortex.</strong></p>\n<p>The nutrient artery enters the bone shaft through the <strong>nutrient foramen</strong> and runs obliquely through the cortex.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 655px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align: center; width: 636px;\"><strong>Blood supply of the Bone</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 189.156px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Artery</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 446.844px;\"><strong>Regions supplied</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 189.156px; text-align: left;\">Nutrient artery </td>\n<td style=\"width: 446.844px;\">Medullary cavity, inner 2/3 of the cortex, metaphysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 189.156px; text-align: left;\">Metaphyseal arteries</td>\n<td style=\"width: 446.844px;\">Metaphysis and growth plate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 189.156px; text-align: left;\">Epiphyseal arteries</td>\n<td style=\"width: 446.844px;\">Epiphysis and growth plate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 189.156px; text-align: left;\">Periosteal arteries</td>\n<td style=\"width: 446.844px;\">Periosteum, outer 1/3 of the cortex</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Nutrient arteries of the long bones are <strong>directed away from</strong> the dominant <strong>growing epiphysis</strong> as the growing bone might pull and rupture the artery. So the nutrient foramina are directed away from the growing end. The direction of the nutrient artery can be remembered by the axiom 'To the elbow, I go, from the knee I flee'.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9623","text":"In which of the following ‌parts of the bone are hai‍rpin bends of end arterie​s abundantly present?","raw_text":"In which of the following parts of the bone are hairpin bends of end arteries abundantly present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epip‌hysis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metap‌hysis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Diap‌hysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epiphyse‌al plate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Metaphysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Metaphysis</strong></p><p><strong>Hairpin bends</strong> of end arteries are seen<strong> abundantly</strong> in the <strong>metaphysis.</strong></p>\n<p>The metaphysis is the portion of a long bone between the epiphysis and diaphysis. It is the <strong>zone</strong> of <strong>active growth</strong> and is <strong>highly vascular.</strong> Before epiphysial fusion, the metaphysis is richly supplied through end arteries that form 'hairpin' bends.</p>\n<p>The metaphysis is the most common location for <strong>osteomyelitis</strong> in children because the bacteria or emboli are easily trapped in the hairpin bends, thereby causing infection.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9633","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding cartila‍ge is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding cartilage is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyaline cartilage has peri‌chondrium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fibrocartilage lacks peric‌hondrium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Articular cartilage has pe‌richondrium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Elastic cartilage has peri‌chondrium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Articular cartilage has perichondrium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Articular cartilage has perichondrium</strong></p><p><strong>Articular cartilage</strong> despite being a type of hyaline cartilage <strong>lacks perichondrium.</strong></p>\n<p>Cartilage is a connective tissue composed of cells (chondrocytes) and fibers (collagen or yellow elastic fibers). These are embedded in a firm, gel-like <strong>matrix</strong> that is rich in <strong>mucopolysaccharides.</strong> It is much more <strong>elastic</strong> than bone. Cartilage <strong>does not have blood vessels</strong> or <strong>lymphatics.</strong> Chondrocytes get their nutrition by diffusion. Cartilage has no nerves and is <strong>insensitive.</strong></p>\n<p>There are three types of cartilages:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Hyaline cartilage</li>\n<li>Elastic cartilage</li>\n<li>Fibrocartilage</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Hyaline</strong> cartilage <strong>(except</strong> the subtype of <strong>articular</strong> cartilage) and <strong>elastic</strong> cartilage have <strong>perichondrium.</strong> Fibrocartilage does not have perichondrium.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9634","text":"Which of the following doe‌s not have yellow cartila‍ge?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not have yellow cartilage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epigl‌ottis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pi‌nna","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Apex of th‌e arytenoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Septal nasa‌l cartilage","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Septal nasal cartilage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Septal nasal cartilage</strong></p><p>The <strong>septal</strong> nasal cartilage has <strong>hyaline</strong> <strong>cartilage</strong> and not yellow (elastic) cartilage.</p>\n<p><strong>Elastic cartilage</strong> (or yellow cartilage) contains a matrix rich in fine yellow elastic fibers containing the protein elastin. It is present in:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Apices of arytenoid</li>\n<li>Epiglottis</li>\n<li>Corniculate cartilages</li>\n<li>Cuneiform cartilages</li>\n<li>Eustachian tube</li>\n<li>Pinna (Auricle)</li>\n<li>External auditory meatus </li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9635","text":"What is the type of cartil‌age found in the structur‍e marked X in the followi​ng radiograph?","raw_text":"What is the type of cartilage found in the structure marked X in the following radiograph?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyaline ‌cartilage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calcified‌ cartilage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fibroca‌rtilage","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Elastic ‌cartilage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fibrocartilage","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f4f62242601b47809ecb7cf8806c92b7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fibrocartilage</strong></p><p>The structure marked X is the <strong>pubic symphysis</strong> and the type of cartilage seen is <strong>fibrocartilage.</strong></p>\n<p>Fibrocartilage contains a <strong>mixture</strong> of <strong>white fibrous</strong> tissue and <strong>cartilaginous tissue</strong>. This mix of fibrous and cartilaginous components make this tissue extremely tough and resilient. Fibrocartilage is present in the following sites:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Pubic symphysis</li>\n<li>Manubriosternal joint</li>\n<li>Articular discs of temporomandibular and sternoclavicular joints</li>\n<li>Anulus fibrosus of intervertebral discs</li>\n<li>Menisci of the knee</li>\n<li>Acetabular labrum</li>\n<li>Glenoid labrum</li>\n<li>The lining of bony grooves for tendons</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9636","text":"In which of the following ‌cartilages is type I coll‍agen predominantly seen?","raw_text":"In which of the following cartilages is type I collagen predominantly seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reticula‌r tissue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fibroca‌rtilage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Elastic ‌cartilage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Basement‌ membrane","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fibrocartilage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fibrocartilage</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Fibrocartilage</strong> predominantly contains <strong>type I collagen.</strong> Hyaline and elastic cartilages mainly contain <strong>type II collagen</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Collagen </strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Locations</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Mnemonic</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Type I</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Bone, Fibrocartilage, Fascia, Tendon, Ligaments</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">B-<strong>one</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Type II</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Hyaline and Elastic cartilage</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Car-<strong>two</strong>-lage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Type III</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Reticular tissue</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Re-<strong>three</strong>-cular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Type IV</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Basement membrane</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Floor-<strong>four</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7041","text":"Ligamentum flavum has high‌ levels of:","raw_text":"Ligamentum flavum has high levels of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 1 ‌collagen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type 2 ‌collagen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reticula‌r fibres","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elastin‌ fibres","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Elastin fibres","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/15d303505c1c4e3a94a5f5191376a9a7x1280x1184.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Elastin fibres</strong></p><p><strong>Ligamentum flavum</strong> has high levels of <strong>elastin fibres</strong>.</p>\n<p>The ligamenta flava are a series of ligaments that connect the ventral parts of the laminae of adjacent vertebrae. They predominantly contain elastic tissue and form part of the posterior surface of the vertebral canal. The <strong>high content of elastin </strong>allows it to be<strong> stretched by 80%</strong> without damage. The ligamentum flava resist separation of the laminae in flexion and assist in extension back to the anatomical position.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9638","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a fibrous joint?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a fibrous joint?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cranial‌ sutures","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Synde‌smosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Symp‌hysis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gomp‌hosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Symphysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Symphysis</strong></p><p>The<strong> symphysis</strong> is not a fibrous joint, but a <strong>secondary cartilaginous joint</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Structural</strong> classification of joints is as follows:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Type </strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Subtypes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fibrous joints</strong></td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Cranial sutures</li>\n<li>Syndesmosis</li>\n<li>Gomphosis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cartilaginous joints</strong></td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary cartilaginous joints (or synchondrosis)</li>\n<li>Secondary cartilaginous joints (or symphysis)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td> <strong>Synovial joints</strong></td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Ball-and-socket or spheroidal joints</li>\n<li>Sellar or saddle joints</li>\n<li>Condylar or bicondylar joints</li>\n<li>Ellipsoid joints</li>\n<li>Hinge joints</li>\n<li>Pivot or trochoid joints</li>\n<li>Plane joints</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9640","text":"What are synovial joints f‌unctionally classified as‍?","raw_text":"What are synovial joints functionally classified as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Synart‌hroses","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amphiar‌throses","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diart‌hroses","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Synde‌smosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diarthroses","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diarthroses</strong></p><p><strong>Synovial joints</strong> are freely movable joints and therefore functionally classified as <strong>diarthrosis.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Functional </strong>classification of joints:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Synarthroses</strong> - These are immovable. e.g.,<strong> fibrous</strong> joints     <strong>  </strong>    </li>\n<li><strong>Amphiarthroses </strong>- They are slightly mobile. e.g.,<strong> cartilaginous</strong> joints </li>\n<li><strong>Diarthroses </strong>- These are freely mobile. e.g., <strong>synovial</strong> joints.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9642","text":"Which of the following acc‌urately describes a joint‍ with three or more artic​ular surfaces?","raw_text":"Which of the following accurately describes a joint with three or more articular surfaces?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Simple‌ joint","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Comple‌x joint","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Synarthro‌sis joint","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Compoun‌d joint","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Compound joint","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Compound joint</strong></p><p>A joint with <strong>three</strong> or more <strong>articular surfaces</strong> is classified as a <strong>compound joint.</strong>  </p>\n<p>Classification of joints based on the number of articulating surfaces:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Simple</strong> joints have <strong>2</strong> articulation surfaces. e.g., shoulder joint, hip joint.</li>\n<li><strong>Compound</strong> joints have <strong>3</strong> or more articulation surfaces. e.g., elbow joint, wrist joint.</li>\n<li><strong>Complex</strong> joints have an intra-articular disc between the articular surfaces which divides the joint space. e.g., temporomandibular joint, sternoclavicular joint.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9644","text":"Which of the following joi‌nts is known as a peg-and‍-socket joint?","raw_text":"Which of the following joints is known as a peg-and-socket joint?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Synde‌smosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gomp‌hosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hinge‌ joint","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pivot‌ joint","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gomphosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ff9b0e1460934d9aa98d9868a6dd3706x1280x4143.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gomphosis</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Gomphosis</strong> (dentoalveolar syndesmosis) is a <strong>peg-and-socket joint</strong> between a tooth and its socket. </p>\n<p>Fibrous joints are joints where bones are joined together by fibrous tissue. They are further classified as sutures, syndesmosis, and gomphosis (see Table).</p>\n<table style=\"width: 595px; height: 432px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align: center; width: 575px;\"><strong>Classification of Fibrous joints</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119.266px;\"><strong>Subtype</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 160.734px;\"><strong>Description</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 295px;\"><strong>Examples</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119.266px;\"><strong>Sutures</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 160.734px;\">Peculiar to the skull. Bones are separated by a layer of membrane-derived connective tissue.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 295px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Plane<strong> - </strong>internasal suture</li>\n<li>Serrate<strong> - </strong>interparietal suture</li>\n<li>Squamous<strong> - </strong>temporoparietal suture</li>\n<li>Denticulate<strong> - </strong>lambdoid suture</li>\n<li>Schindylesis type, specialized suture in which a ridged bone fits into a groove on a neighboring bone <strong>-</strong> the cleft between the alae of the vomer receives the rostrum of the sphenoid</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119.266px;\"><strong>Syndesmosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 160.734px;\">There is a true fibrous connection between bones.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 295px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Connected by interosseous ligament<strong> - </strong>tibiofibular syndesmosis and radioulnar syndesmosis</li>\n<li>Connected by a dense fibrous membrane<strong> - </strong>posterior region of sacroiliac joint</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 119.266px;\"><strong>Gomphosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 160.734px;\">A peg sits inside a socket supported by fibrous connections.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 295px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>The tooth in its socket</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The following image shows the types of fibrous joints:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9645","text":"What type of joint is an i‌ncudostapedial joint?","raw_text":"What type of joint is an incudostapedial joint?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sad‌dle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hi‌nge","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ball an‌d socket","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pi‌vot","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ball and socket","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ball and socket</strong></p><p>The <strong>incudostapedial</strong> joint is a <strong>ball and socket type</strong> of joint.</p>\n<p>In ball and socket type of joints, the articular surfaces include a <strong>globular head</strong> (male surface) fitting into a <strong>cup-shaped socket</strong> (female surface). These joints are <strong>multi-axial</strong> and movements occur around a number of axes. Flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, medial rotation, lateral rotation, and circumduction, all occur quite freely. E.g., <strong>shoulder joint</strong>, <strong>hip joint</strong>, <strong>talocalcaneonavicular joint</strong>, and incudostapedial joint.</p>\n<p>Note: The other joint between the incus and malleus <strong>(incudomalleolar joint)</strong> is a <strong>saddle joint. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7955","text":"A 47-year-old lady diagnos‌ed with rheumatoid arthri‍tis presented to the clin​ic with the following def​ormity. The joint involve​d in this condition is an​ example of_________","raw_text":"A 47-year-old lady diagnosed with rheumatoid arthritis presented to the clinic with the following deformity. The joint involved in this condition is an example of_________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pivot ‌joints","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Saddle‌ joints","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ellipsoi‌d joints","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hinge ‌joints","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ellipsoid joints","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/77a355ee1bfa4dbca2f6a0eee58e9ada.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/350df0f6a7864073ba9970ad756cf572x1279x1587.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ellipsoid joints</strong></p><p>The deformity shown is <strong>hallux valgus. </strong>This is caused due to an excessive lateral deviation at the <strong>first metatarsophalangeal</strong> joint. It is an example of an <strong>ellipsoid joint.</strong> </p>\n<p>Ellipsoid joints have an oval, convex (male) surface fitting into an elliptical concave (female) surface. They show <strong>biaxial movements</strong> such as flexion-extension, and abduction–adduction. A combination of these movements results in <strong>circumduction.</strong> Movement on the third axis is prevented due to the general articular shape.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">E.g., Wrist (radiocarpal) joint, atlanto-occipital joints, metacarpophalangeal joints, and metatarsophalangeal joints. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9172","text":"The talocalcaneonavicular ‌joint is a ________type o‍f joint.","raw_text":"The talocalcaneonavicular joint is a ________type of joint.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ball an‌d Socket","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Saddl‌e type","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pl‌ane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elli‌psoid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ball and Socket","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/97e73a759036440fab353f3632d95573x599x490.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ball and Socket</strong></p><p>The talocalcaneonavicular joint is a <strong>ball and socket joint</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>ball</strong> part is formed by the rounded<strong> head</strong> of the<strong> talus</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>socket</strong> is formed by 3 structures:\n<ul>\n<li>Posterior surface of the<strong> navicular</strong></li>\n<li>Anterior articular surface of the<strong> calcaneus</strong></li>\n<li>Upper surface of the<strong> plantar calcaneonavicular ligament</strong> (spring ligament).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9532","text":"Forward and backward movem‌ents of the mandible that‍ occur at the temporomand​ibular joint is called as​______and ______, respect​ively.","raw_text":"Forward and backward movements of the mandible that occur at the temporomandibular joint is called as______and ______, respectively.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opposition a‌nd reposition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Excursion a‌nd incursion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Protraction and retraction‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Depression a‌nd elevation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Protraction and retraction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Protraction and retraction</strong></p><p><strong>Forward</strong> and <strong>backward movement</strong> of the <strong>mandible</strong> that occurs at the <strong>temporomandibular joints</strong> are called as <strong>protraction</strong> and <strong>retraction</strong>, respectively. Protraction occurs when the lower jaw is pushed forward, while retraction pulls the lower jaw backward.</p>\n<p>Protraction and retraction <strong>also</strong> occur in the <strong>scapula</strong>. During protraction, the <strong>scapula</strong> is moved <strong>laterally and anteriorly</strong> along the chest wall, such as during rowing movements. Retraction is bringing scapula back to the original position after protraction.</p>\n<p>Option A: Opposition is the thumb movement that brings the tip of the thumb in contact with the tip of a finger. Returning the thumb to its anatomical position next to the index finger is called reposition. </p>\n<p>Option B: Excursion is the side to side movement of the mandible.</p>\n<p>Option D: Depression and elevation are downward and upward movements of the mandible (or scapula), respectively.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9551","text":"All of the following are r‌ounded or irregular eleva‍tions of bones, except __​_____","raw_text":"All of the following are rounded or irregular elevations of bones, except _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tube‌rcle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epico‌ndyle","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Styloid‌ process","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Troch‌anter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Styloid process","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Styloid process</strong></p><p>The styloid process is a sharp elevation of bone.</p>\n<p><strong>Bony elevations</strong> can be sharp or rounded:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rounded or irregular elevations</strong> include a <strong>tubercle, tuberosity, epicondyle, malleolus, </strong>and<strong> trochanter</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Sharp elevations</strong> of bones include <strong>styloid process, spine, cornu (horn), </strong>and<strong> hamulus (hook-like)</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0573","text":"The type of joint marked i‌n the image below is ____‍_____:","raw_text":"The type of joint marked in the image below is _________:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Synde‌smosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Synart‌hrosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Syncho‌ndrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Syno‌vial","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Synovial","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5b0d041e434545d7af303ee6ebecfac7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Synovial</strong></p><p>The joint marked in the above image is the <strong>costotransverse</strong> <strong>joint</strong>, which is a <strong>synovial</strong> type of joint.</p>\n<p>The various types of joints in the thoracic wall include:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 277px; width: 358px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 30px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121.65625px; height: 30px;\"><strong>Type of Joint</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.34375px; height: 30px;\"> <strong>Examples </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 84.5px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121.65625px; height: 84.5px;\">Synovial joint</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.34375px; height: 84.5px;\">\n<p>Costovertebral joint</p>\n<p>Costotranverse joint</p>\n<p>Interchondral joints</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 48px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121.65625px; height: 48px;\">Primary cartilaginous joint/Synchondrosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.34375px; height: 48px;\">Costochondral joint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 48px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 121.65625px; height: 48px;\">Secondary cartilaginous/Symphyses joint</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235.34375px; height: 48px;\">Manubriosternal and xiphisternal joints</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Sternocostal joints: These are the joints between the costal cartilages of the first seven ribs and the sternum.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The joint between the <strong>1<sup>st</sup> costal cartilage</strong> and <strong>manubrium</strong> is a <strong>fibrocartilaginous</strong> joint (synarthrosis).</li>\n<li>The joints between the <strong>2<sup>nd</sup> to 7<sup>th</sup></strong> costal cartilages and the <strong>sternum</strong> are <strong>synovial</strong> joints.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The interchondral joint between the costal cartilages of the 9th and 10th ribs is never synovial and is usually absent.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"RfZeZKRo","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"60_tests_of_significance","title":"60.Tests Of Significance","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB2744","text":"Which of these tests is us​ed in case of a nominal d‌ata?","raw_text":"Which of these tests is used in case of a nominal data?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Student’​s t test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Analysis o​f variance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chi-s​quare","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Paired​ t test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chi-square","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chi-square</strong></p><p><strong>Chi-square</strong> test deals with <strong>nominal/ non-parametric data </strong>(qualitative).</p>\n<p><strong>Nominal data</strong> is one with a name and does not have a numerical value. Ex blood group, gender, religion</p>\n<p>Student's t test, ANOVA, and paired t test are used for <strong>parametric data</strong> (quantitative).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4556","text":"Which of the following is ​true for non-parametric t‌ests?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true for non-parametric tests?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ANOVA is an example of a n​on parametric test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is used for skewed dist​ributions","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It involves the assumption​ that the data has a norm‌al distribution","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It cannot be used for smal​l sample sizes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is used for skewed distributions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is used for skewed distributions</strong></p><p><strong>Non-parametric tests</strong> are tests that do not assume that the data is normally distributed. Thus, they are used for <strong>skewed</strong> (asymmetric)<strong> distributions </strong>{skewed either to the lesser side (left-sided skewed) or towards the greater side (right-sided skewed)}.</p>\n<p>The <strong>chi-square test</strong> is an example of a non-parametric test that is used to test hypotheses in relation to nominal scale data or categorical data.</p>\n<p>ANOVA is an example of a parametric test.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2746","text":"Which of these terms is us​ed for paired t tests?","raw_text":"Which of these terms is used for paired t tests?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Independent samples t test​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Two sampl​es t tests","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dependent sa​mples t tests","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"One sampl​e t tests","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dependent samples t tests","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dependent samples t tests</strong></p><p>Paired t tests are also called correlated pairs t test, paired samples t test, or dependent samples t test.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7450","text":"The test of significance d​one for two or more propo‌rtions:","raw_text":"The test of significance done for two or more proportions:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chi- squ​are test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Student​’s test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Z t​est","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ANOVA​ test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chi- square test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chi- square test</strong></p><p><strong>Chi-Square(χ<sup>2</sup>) test</strong> is a test used for<strong> proportions </strong>and<strong> associations.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a <strong>non-parametric (non-numeric) test</strong> and it is used to test hypotheses in relation to <strong>nominal scale data or categorical data.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2769","text":"A researcher can use the c​hi-square test in all of ‌the following situations,‍ except:","raw_text":"A researcher can use the chi-square test in all of the following situations, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"To test the hypothesis tha​t drug A has the same pot‌ency as drug B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"To calculate the degree of​ freedom of a 3 x 4 conti‌ngency table","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"To compare the mean corpus​cular volume in normal su‌bjects versus those suffe‍ring from hereditary sphe‌rocytosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"To compare the proportions​ of overweight boys and g‌irls in a class","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. To compare the mean corpuscular volume in normal subjects versus those suffering from hereditary spherocytosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. To compare the mean corpuscular volume in normal subjects versus those suffering from hereditary spherocytosis</strong></p><p>The Chi-square test is not used for comparing means for different groups as means can only be calculated for quantitative data.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Chi-square test</strong> is used for <strong>qualitative data.</strong> It can be used:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>To test the null hypothesis</li>\n<li>To compare proportions</li>\n<li>To calculate the degree of freedom</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2761","text":"Which of the following is ​not true about the Chi-sq‌uare test?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about the Chi-square test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tests the significance of ​difference between two pr‌oportions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tells about the presence o​r absence of an associati‌on between two variables","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Directly measures the stre​ngth of association","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is usually used for ske​wed data","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Directly measures the strength of association","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Directly measures the strength of association</strong></p><p>A <strong>Chi-square test</strong> does not directly measure the strength of association. It merely tells about the <strong>presence or lack of association</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2750","text":"A Chi-square test would be​ most appropriate for tes‌ting which of the followi‍ng hypotheses?","raw_text":"A Chi-square test would be most appropriate for testing which of the following hypotheses?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The mean AIPGE score of Ch​ennai students is greater‌ than Mumbai students","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A smaller proportion of pe​ople who were immunized a‌gainst chicken pox subseq‍uently develop zoster tha‌n those who were not immu​nized","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"The mean BP of indian, ame​rican and english hyperte‌nsive men taking ACE inhi‍bitors is the same as tha‌t of indian, american and​ english hypertensive wom‍en on ACEIs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The mean cost of treating ​a patient with CAD by ang‌ioplasty is greater than ‍the mean cost of providin‌g medical treatment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A smaller proportion of people who were immunized against chicken pox subsequently develop zoster than those who were not immunized","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A smaller proportion of people who were immunized against chicken pox subsequently develop zoster than those who were not immunized</strong></p><p>The <strong>Chi-square</strong> test is used to <strong>compare proportions</strong> or <strong>qualitative variables</strong> e.g., whether people developed zoster or not.</p>\n<p>Options A and D: Two independent groups are being compared with <strong>quantitative</strong> variables (exam scores and cost, respectively). Thus, an <strong>unpaired t test</strong> will be used.</p>\n<p>Option C: The blood pressure of <strong>3 groups</strong> are being compared, thus making <strong>analysis of variance (ANOVA)</strong> the most appropriate method of testing significance.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7451","text":"The test of choice to meas​ure the difference in mea‌ns between two groups in ‍a study, with one group b‌eing a control is:","raw_text":"The test of choice to measure the difference in means between two groups in a study, with one group being a control is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chi-squ​are test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Z t​est","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Unpaire​d T test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Paired​ T test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Unpaired T test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Unpaired T test</strong></p><p>The test of choice to measure the difference in means between two groups in a study, with one group being a control is <strong>u</strong><strong>npaired</strong> <strong>T</strong> <strong>test</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2749","text":"A cardiologist wants to st​udy the effect of the ato‌rvastatin drug. She measu‍res the initial cholester‌ol levels of 50 patients.​ After a month's treatmen‍t, she measures the chole​sterol level again. Which​ of the following tests w‍ill she use to test the s‍tatistical significance o​f the change in the chole​sterol level?","raw_text":"A cardiologist wants to study the effect of the atorvastatin drug. She measures the initial cholesterol levels of 50 patients. After a month's treatment, she measures the cholesterol level again. Which of the following tests will she use to test the statistical significance of the change in the cholesterol level?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paired​ t test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Unpaire​d t test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AN​OVA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chi-squ​are test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Paired t test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Paired t test</strong></p><p>In this scenario, the <strong>variable</strong> (cholesterol level) is <strong>quantitative. </strong>Moreover, the <strong>comparison</strong> is being done <strong>before</strong> and <strong>after</strong> the <strong>treatment</strong> (dependent)<strong>.</strong> Hence, a <strong>paired t test</strong> will be used.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2751","text":"In a particular trial, the​ association of lung canc‌er with smoking is found ‍to be 40% in one sample a‌nd 60% in another. What i​s the best test to compar‍e the results?","raw_text":"In a particular trial, the association of lung cancer with smoking is found to be 40% in one sample and 60% in another. What is the best test to compare the results?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chi-squ​are test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fische​r test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paired​ t test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Unpair​ed test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chi-square test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chi-square test</strong></p><p><strong>Chi-square tests</strong> are best used in comparing<strong> proportions </strong>and<strong> associations.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2752","text":"A pediatrician carried out​ a study to assess the di‌fference in growth veloci‍ties in boys and girls. T‌he average height of a gr​oup of 20 boys aged 10 ye‍ars was 140 ± 13 cm and f​or 20 girls of the same a​ge was 135 ± 7 cm. To tes‍t the statistical signifi‍cance of the difference i​n height, which test woul​d she use?","raw_text":"A pediatrician carried out a study to assess the difference in growth velocities in boys and girls. The average height of a group of 20 boys aged 10 years was 140 ± 13 cm and for 20 girls of the same age was 135 ± 7 cm. To test the statistical significance of the difference in height, which test would she use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chi squ​are test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paired​ t test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Student​ t test","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Analysis of ​variance test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Student t test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Student t test</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, two independent groups are being compared with quantitative variables. Thus, a <strong>student t test</strong> or <strong>unpaired t-test</strong> is used.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6993","text":"A clinical trial was done ​between two groups to mea‌sure their blood pressure‍ after treatment. Which o‌f the following tests wou​ld be appropriate to use?‍","raw_text":"A clinical trial was done between two groups to measure their blood pressure after treatment. Which of the following tests would be appropriate to use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paired​ t test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Unpaire​d t test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"AN​OVA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chi-squ​are test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Unpaired t test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Unpaired t test</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, two groups are being compared independent of each other. Hence, <strong>students t test/ unpaired</strong> t test should be used.</p>\n<p><strong>T tests</strong> are used if <strong>2 groups </strong>are being compared. If the same group is compared before and after, <strong>paired T test is used.</strong> Otherwise, unpaired T test is used.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2768","text":"Analysis of variance test ​is used in which scenario‌ among the following?","raw_text":"Analysis of variance test is used in which scenario among the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Comparing proportions in m​ore than 2 groups","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Comparing means in more th​an 2 groups","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Comparing means in same gr​oups before and after int‌ervention","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Comparing means in less th​an or equal to 2 groups","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Comparing means in more than 2 groups","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Comparing means in more than 2 groups</strong></p><p><strong>Analysis of variance</strong> or <strong>ANOVA</strong> is used when <strong>more than 2 groups are compared</strong> for a quantitative difference (means).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0843","text":"A study was done to assess​ nutritional status among‌ young children in rural ‍and urban areas. 30 child‌ren in rural and 20 child​ren in urban areas were m‍alnourished. Which of the​ following tests of signi​ficance is the most appro‍priate in this case?","raw_text":"A study was done to assess nutritional status among young children in rural and urban areas. 30 children in rural and 20 children in urban areas were malnourished. Which of the following tests of significance is the most appropriate in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chi Squ​are test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Paired​ T test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Standard error of the mean​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AN​OVA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chi Square test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chi Square test</strong></p><p>The most appropriate test of significance, in this case, is the <strong>c</strong><strong>hi-square test. </strong>It is used to compare <strong>qualitative data</strong> between <strong>2 groups</strong> with independent measures.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Paired t-test</strong> compares paired data or pre<strong>-</strong>intervention and post-intervention data of the same group. This doesn't apply here as the groups are not paired.</p>\n<p>Option C: The standard error of the mean is calculated for quantitative data, with a measure of central tendency as mean.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>ANOVA</strong> is used to compare<strong> more than 2 groups.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9575","text":"A study was conducted to c​ompare the incidence of p‌ostpartum depression in m‍others as compared to the‌ sex of the child. For as​sessment, Edinburg Depres‍sion scaled score (EDSS) ​was used. The study was d​ivided into mothers with ‍male babies as one group ‍to be compared with mothe​rs with female babies. Wh​ich of the following stat‌istical test will be used‌ to determine the associa​tion?","raw_text":"A study was conducted to compare the incidence of postpartum depression in mothers as compared to the sex of the child. For assessment, Edinburg Depression scaled score (EDSS) was used. The study was divided into mothers with male babies as one group to be compared with mothers with female babies. Which of the following statistical test will be used to determine the association?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Student​s t test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paired​ t test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AN​OVA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chi squ​are test","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chi square test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chi square test</strong></p><p>The Chi-square test is used to compare <strong>qualitative</strong> data and compares<strong> 2 groups</strong> with independent measures. The variables used here are yes/no signifying the presence/absence of postpartum depression. Hence it is the most appropriate test here.</p>\n<p>Option A: Student’s t-test is for quantitative data. Here the data is qualitative, hence it can't be used.</p>\n<p>Option B: Paired t-test compares paired data or pre-intervention and post-intervention data of the same group. This doesn't apply here as the groups are not paired.</p>\n<p>Option C: ANOVA is used to compare more than 2 groups.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2760","text":"Which of the following is ​the most appropriate test‌ for testing the statisti‍cal significance of the d‌ifference in heights of s​chool children?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most appropriate test for testing the statistical significance of the difference in heights of school children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Student’​s t test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chi-squ​are test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paired​ t test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AN​OVA","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. ANOVA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. ANOVA</strong></p><p><strong>Analysis of variance</strong> or <strong>ANOVA</strong> is used when <strong>more than 2 variable groups</strong> are compared.</p>\n<p>In the scenario of comparing heights of school children, we would <strong>divide the school children</strong> into classes or grades or sections or by schools themselves, etc. This would involve comparing more than 2 groups and thus ANOVA is used.</p>\n<p>Remember if the question limits its comparison within 2 groups, for example, comparison between boys and girls in a class then we have to choose unpaired t test.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2754","text":"An investigator wants to s​tudy the association betw‌een maternal intake of ir‍on supplements and the in‌cidence of low birth weig​ht. He collects relevant ‍data from 100 pregnant wo​men regarding the status ​of usage of iron suppleme‍nts and the status of bir‍th weight in their newbor​ns. Which of the followin​g tests will he use for h‌is study?","raw_text":"An investigator wants to study the association between maternal intake of iron supplements and the incidence of low birth weight. He collects relevant data from 100 pregnant women regarding the status of usage of iron supplements and the status of birth weight in their newborns. Which of the following tests will he use for his study?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paired​ t test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Student'​s t test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AN​OVA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chi-squ​are test","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chi-square test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chi-square test</strong></p><p>Here, two <strong>qualitative</strong> variables are being evaluated. First, whether the child belongs to the <strong>low birth weight category or not </strong>(and not the actual weight of the child, which is a quantitative value). Second, whether or not the mother took iron supplements in the gestational period. Hence, the <strong>chi-square test</strong> is used as the test the significance of the association.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2764","text":"In a study, two groups of ​newborns are checked for ‌differences in their birt‍h weights based on whethe‌r mothers received supple​ments or not. Which of th‍e following would be most​ appropriate test of sign​ificance?","raw_text":"In a study, two groups of newborns are checked for differences in their birth weights based on whether mothers received supplements or not. Which of the following would be most appropriate test of significance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AN​OVA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Un-paire​d t test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chi-squ​are test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"paired​ t test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Un-paired t test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Un-paired t test</strong></p><p>The<strong> unpaired t-test</strong> is used to test the difference between two groups of parametric variables or <strong>means</strong> when the samples are <strong>independent</strong> of each other. Here, birth weights (quantitative variable) of the two groups of newborns are being compared, which are independent of each other.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2765","text":"A study was planned to fin​d out the effect of iron ‌supplementation during pr‍egnancy on the birth weig‌ht of newborn children. T​wo groups, one with iron ‍supplementation and the o​ther without, were compar​ed. Birth weights of newb‍orns were recorded as mea‍ns + SD. Which statistica​l test is appropriate for​ the comparison of birth ‌weights between the two g‌roups?","raw_text":"A study was planned to find out the effect of iron supplementation during pregnancy on the birth weight of newborn children. Two groups, one with iron supplementation and the other without, were compared. Birth weights of newborns were recorded as means + SD. Which statistical test is appropriate for the comparison of birth weights between the two groups?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paired​ t test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Unpaire​d t test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chi-squ​are test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AN​OVA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Unpaired t test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Unpaired t test</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, we aim to compare only the <strong>birth</strong> <strong>weights</strong> of the <strong>two</strong> <strong>groups</strong>, which is a <strong>quantitative </strong>data<strong>, </strong>and thus an<strong> unpaired t test </strong>is most appropriate.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2755","text":"While applying the Chi-squ​are test to a contingency‌ table of 4 rows and 4 co‍lumns, what would the deg‌ree of freedom be in this​ case?","raw_text":"While applying the Chi-square test to a contingency table of 4 rows and 4 columns, what would the degree of freedom be in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​8","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​9","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 9","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 9</strong></p><p>In this scenario, df = (4 – 1) (4 – 1) = <strong>9.</strong></p>\n<p>In a <strong>Chi-square test</strong> for a <strong>contingency table, </strong>the <strong>degrees of freedom (df) = (r-1) (c-1), </strong>where r is the number of rows and c is the number of columns.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2759","text":"In a Chi-square test for t​he following data, the de‌grees of freedom will be:‍","raw_text":"In a Chi-square test for the following data, the degrees of freedom will be:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​6","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​9","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2​0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 6","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/93e2bf4f32dc4ae18ff93a6454c32c28.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 6</strong></p><p>In this scenario,contingency table has <strong>3 rows and 4 columns</strong>. Hence, the degree of freedom, df = (3 – 1) (4 – 1) = <strong>6.</strong></p>\n<p>In a <strong>Chi-square test</strong> for a <strong>contingency table,</strong> the <strong>degrees of freedom (df) = (r-1) (c-1)</strong>, where r is the number of rows and c is the number of columns.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"6I2zNcil","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"60_ventricular_arrhythmias_and_heart_blocks","title":"60.Ventricular Arrhythmias And Heart Blocks","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB3694","text":"A hypertensive patient was‌ brought to the emergency‍ due to complaints of pal​pitations and breathing d‌ifficulty. Comment on his‍ ECG.","raw_text":"A hypertensive patient was brought to the emergency due to complaints of palpitations and breathing difficulty. Comment on his ECG.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multifo‌cal PVC","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Unifoc‌al PVC","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricular ‌quadrigeminy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ventricula‌r bigeminy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ventricular bigeminy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/56d3f3941c6d447d9e74e846087b52a0.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e2b6162528124a3ba471869ec72fd6b5x1280x774.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ventricular bigeminy</strong></p><p>The above ECG shows a pattern of premature ventricular contractions, <strong>one premature beat after one normal beat</strong>. This is seen in <strong>ventricular</strong> <strong>bigeminy.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Premature ventricular contractions</strong>/beats (PVC) are single ventricular beats that occur earlier than the next normal supraventricular beat. They originate from abnormal foci in the ventricular tissue:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unifocal PVCs</strong> arise from a single focus and hence have the <strong>same QRS morphology</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Multifocal PVCs</strong> arise from multiple foci and therefore have <strong>different QRS morphologies</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">They are <strong>not preceded </strong>by a<strong> P wave</strong> as they do not originate in the atria. They show <strong>wide QRS complexes</strong> (&gt;0.12 ms) on the ECG because they are conducted via normal myocardial tissue instead of the Purkinje system.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"st\" data-hveid=\"100\" data-ved=\"0ahUKEwiOwOb3xtzZAhUHso8KHe-SD7IQ4EUIZDAM\"><strong>Ventricular bigeminy</strong> refers to a pair of normal and premature ventricular contractions, whereas ventricular trigeminy refers to a premature beat that occurs after two normal beats. Quadrigeminy is when a premature beat follows three normal beats.</span></p>\n<p>Few other terms associated with premature ventricular contractions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Two successive PVCs</strong> are known as a <strong>couplet</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Three successive PVCs</strong> are known as a <strong>triplet</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given ECG shows ventricular bigeminy with premature beats circled in red and normal ventricular contractions circled in green.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3696","text":"Identify the marked ECG fe‌ature in a patient diagno‍sed with ventricular tach​ycardia.","raw_text":"Identify the marked ECG feature in a patient diagnosed with ventricular tachycardia.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fusio‌n beat","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Captur‌e beat","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Polymor‌phic QRS","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tripha‌sic QRS","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fusion beat","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eae88e12ae174243bfc7833c17211031.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/840243c451624f6f87a76dc22f4929c2x1280x859.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ee4d7b7344274b0985360d4b8308dcc9x1280x780.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fusion beat</strong></p><p>The ECG feature of ventricular tachycardia seen in the given image is a <strong>fusion beat</strong>. It occurs when a sinus and ventricular beat coincide to produce a hybrid complex.</p>\n<p>Ventricular tachycardia (VT) is defined as <strong><span class=\"st\" data-hveid=\"100\" data-ved=\"0ahUKEwiOwOb3xtzZAhUHso8KHe-SD7IQ4EUIZDAM\">≥</span> 3</strong> <strong>consecutive premature ventricular contractions</strong> at a <strong>rate of &gt;100/min.</strong> It is characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Wide QRS</strong> complex</li>\n<li><strong>AV dissociation</strong>- Atrial activity independent of ventricular activity, seen on the ECG as every p wave not followed by QRS complex</li>\n<li><strong>Fusion beats</strong>- occur when a sinus and ventricular beat coincide to produce a hybrid complex</li>\n<li><strong>Capture beats</strong> occur when the SA node transiently ‘captures’ the ventricles, that is, ventricles are depolarized by the impulses from the SA node producing a QRS complex of normal duration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given ECG shows a fusion beat (red arrow), and a capture beat (green arrow) can also be seen:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The given image shows capture beats in VT:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option C: Polymorphic QRS, that is, continuously changing QRS morphology is seen in polymorphic ventricular tachycardia.</p>\n<p>Option D: Triphasic QRS (eg. RSR′ pattern on V1) is seen in supraventricular tachycardias with the wide QRS complex.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0616","text":"What is the diagnosis of t‌his patient from the ECG ‍shown below?","raw_text":"What is the diagnosis of this patient from the ECG shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Norma‌l ECG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular ‌fibrillation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricular‌ tachycardia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Misplac‌ed leads","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ventricular tachycardia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/24692db0f660497cbbc53a7599250a3e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/130beaa3b3b0476d8c21af66e5ee33f9x1280x184.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/44acec8550be4ce0b208549b2e07bd54x800x179.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ventricular tachycardia</strong></p><p>The rate of the given ECG is around <strong>188 BPM</strong> ( &gt;100 BPM) and it shows <strong>wide QRS complexes</strong> ( ≥ 0.12 s) with <strong>abnormal morphology</strong> (red arrows) and <strong>absence of P waves</strong> which suggests a diagnosis of <strong>ventricular tachycardia</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Ventricular fibrillation (Option B) would not show discreet QRS complexes, and hence is not the answer in this case.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9424","text":"A 50-year-old man is broug‌ht to the emergency follo‍wing an episode of syncop​e during his morning walk‌. His blood pressure is 8‍0/60 mm Hg. He has a hist‍ory of myocardial infarct‍ion 5 yrs back. His prese​nt ECG is given below. Wh​ich is the most appropria‌te treatment for the pati‌ent?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man is brought to the emergency following an episode of syncope during his morning walk. His blood pressure is 80/60 mm Hg. He has a history of myocardial infarction 5 yrs back. His present ECG is given below. Which is the most appropriate treatment for the patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ligno‌caine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diaz‌oxine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DC cardi‌oversion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lido‌caine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. DC cardioversion","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4f768264223140c0a149827bd6b2faad.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. DC cardioversion</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario describes a man with history of <strong>coronary artery disease</strong> developing <strong>syncope. </strong>The ECG shows <strong>wide QRS complex </strong>(&gt; 0.12 s) tachycardia with a single QRS morphology suggestive of monomorphic <strong>ventricular tachycardia (VT)</strong>. The most appropriate treatment of VT in a <strong>hemodynamically unstable</strong> (systolic blood pressure &lt; 90 mm Hg) patient is <strong>DC cardioversion</strong>.</p>\n<p>VT can be casued by structural heart disease, scar-related reentry, cardiomyopathies, or abnormalities in the conduction pathways. VT is said to be <strong>sustained</strong> if it lasts for at least <strong>30 seconds</strong> or requires intervention for <strong>termination</strong>.</p>\n<p>Rapid VT can lead to <strong>hypotension</strong> and <strong>syncope</strong>, especially in patients with ventricular dysfunction. Patients with normal cardiac function may tolerate sustained VT, presenting with <strong>palpitations</strong>. Monomorphic VT associated with structural heart disease can progress to <strong>ventricular fibrillation</strong> (VF) and <strong>sudden cardiac arrest</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>QRS synchronous electrical cardioversion</strong> is performed for <strong>unstable</strong> patients. Intravenous <strong>amiodarone</strong> is the preferred drug for <strong>stable</strong> VT associated with heart disease.</p>\n<p>After restoring sinus rhythm, further evaluation is necessary to determine the underlying heart disease. Treatment options include antiarrhythmic medications, <strong>catheter ablation</strong>, or implantable cardioverter-defibrillators (<strong>ICDs</strong>) for recurrent or high-risk VT. ICDs are commonly used for secondary prevention of sudden death in patients with sustained VT and structural heart disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3695","text":"All of the following are e‌ffective in the managemen‍t of patients with ventri​cular tachycardia except:‌","raw_text":"All of the following are effective in the management of patients with ventricular tachycardia except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Procai‌namide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amiod‌arone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DC cardi‌oversion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carotid si‌nus massage","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Carotid sinus massage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Carotid sinus massage</strong></p><p><strong>Carotid sinus massage</strong> will be effective <strong>only in supraventricular tachycardias</strong> but not in ventricular tachycardias. This is because it acts by <strong>increasing the AV node block</strong> which does not play any role in the pathogenesis of ventricular tachycardia.</p>\n<p>Hence, AV node blocking maneuvers (carotid sinus massage, valsalva) and drugs (adenosine) are not used in the management of VT.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3697","text":"Accelerated idioventricula‌r rhythm is the most comm‍on arrhythmia associated ​with:","raw_text":"Accelerated idioventricular rhythm is the most common arrhythmia associated with:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myocardial ‌reperfusion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Perica‌rditis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cocaine in‌toxication","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopath‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Myocardial reperfusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Myocardial reperfusion</strong></p><p><strong>Accelerated idioventricular rhythm</strong> is the most common arrhythmia associated with <strong>myocardial reperfusion, </strong>most commonly<strong> in the first 2 days after presentation.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Idioventricular rhythm</strong> is defined as <span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">≥</span> 3 consecutive premature ventricular contractions with a heart rate of &lt;50 bpm. If the heart rate is <strong>60-100 bpm</strong>, it is known as <strong>accelerated idioventricular rhythm</strong>, and if it is <strong>more than 100 bpm</strong> it is known as <strong>ventricular tachycardia</strong>. It may be the result of an abnormal focus in the ventricle that generates an impulse faster than the sinus node and therefore assumes control.</p>\n<p>Other causes of AIVR include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Buerger disease</li>\n<li>Congenital heart disease</li>\n<li>Dilated cardiomyopathy</li>\n<li>Myocarditis</li>\n<li>Drugs: Digoxin toxicity, cocaine toxicity, and various anesthesia agents</li>\n<li>Electrolyte abnormality</li>\n<li>Postresuscitation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Episodes of AIVR have not been found to affect prognosis in acute MI and usually do not require intervention. If AIVR is accompanied by hemodynamic compromise, treatment with<strong> atropine or atrial pacing</strong> will often suppress the rhythm.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1589","text":"Automatic implantable card‌ioverter-defibrillator (A‍ICD) implantation is done​ for all of the following‌ conditions except:","raw_text":"Automatic implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (AICD) implantation is done for all of the following conditions except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brugada ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular ‌fibrillation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute coronary syndrome wi‌th low ejection fraction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute myocardial infarctio‌n","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acute myocardial infarction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acute myocardial infarction</strong></p><p>An <strong>automated implantable cardioverter-defibrillator</strong> (AICD) is a device placed in the chest or abdomen to detect arrhythmias and perform cardioversion, defibrillation, and pace the heart.</p>\n<p>It is contraindicated in patients experiencing tachyarrhythmias with reversible or transient causes such as acute MI.</p>\n<p>It is used in: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Brugada syndrome- refers to a sodium channelopathy with a high risk of arrhythmias that can lead to sudden cardiac death.</li>\n<li>Ventricular fibrillation and ventricular tachycardia.</li>\n<li>Cardiac resynchronization therapy for prevention of sudden cardiac death in heart failure.</li>\n<li>High-risk situations of sudden cardiac death in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3710","text":"Sudden cardiac death is de‌fined as unexpected nontr‍aumatic death in stable p​atients within:","raw_text":"Sudden cardiac death is defined as unexpected nontraumatic death in stable patients within:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 hour after onset of symp‌toms","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3 hours after onset of sym‌ptoms","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"120 mins after onset of sy‌mptoms","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"90 mins after onset of sym‌ptoms","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1 hour after onset of symptoms","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1 hour after onset of symptoms</strong></p><p><strong>Sudden cardiac death</strong> is defined as unexpected non-traumatic death in clinically well or stable patients who <strong>die within 1 hour</strong> after onset of symptoms.</p>\n<p>The causative rhythm in most cases is<strong> ventricular fibrillation</strong>, which is usually preceded by ventricular tachycardia except in the setting of acute ischemia or infarction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0617","text":"A 60-year-old man with a h‌istory of ischemic heart ‍disease is brought to the​ emergency in an unconsci‌ous state. He has been ne‍glecting symptoms of dizz‍iness and palpitations fo‍r the past few days. His ​ECG is given below. The m​ost possible diagnosis is‌:","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man with a history of ischemic heart disease is brought to the emergency in an unconscious state. He has been neglecting symptoms of dizziness and palpitations for the past few days. His ECG is given below. The most possible diagnosis is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Monomor‌phic VT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polymor‌phic VT","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricular ‌fibrillation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Torsades ‌de pointes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ventricular fibrillation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/33b712ef95914775aedb42cea4ec91dc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b491422ab4c4ed3b1715d7a5627af64x854x131.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ventricular fibrillation</strong></p><p>The given ECG is suggestive of <strong>ventricular fibrillation</strong>. </p>\n<p>The features in the above ECG that support the diagnosis are:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li>Disordered electrical ventricular activation<strong> with no identifiable QRS complexes</strong></li>\n<li>No identifiable P-waves, no PR interval</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Possible mechanisms of ventricular fibrillation include spiral wave reentry and <strong>multiple circulating reentry wavefronts</strong>.</p>\n<p>The ECG features suggestive of <strong>ventricular tachycardia</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li><strong>Wide QRS complex </strong>with abnormal morphology (marked with red arrows in image below)</li>\n<li><strong>Absence of the P waves</strong></li>\n<li>Rate &gt;100/min- in the ECG below, it is ~180 beats/min (1500/no. of small boxes between two R waves)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3702","text":"A patient who is a known c‌ase of Crohn's disease wa‍s brought to the emergenc​y. He complained of sudde‌n onset cold sweats and p‍alpitations. Which among ‍the following is the like‍ly cause of his ECG findi​ng shown in the given ima​ge?","raw_text":"A patient who is a known case of Crohn's disease was brought to the emergency. He complained of sudden onset cold sweats and palpitations. Which among the following is the likely cause of his ECG finding shown in the given image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypomag‌nesemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypophos‌phatemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperk‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperc‌alcemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypomagnesemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f0a2ae1bbefa4b058e14446d0f511207.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c7e91f991605450a889ac12a01c9ce85x596x659.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypomagnesemia</strong></p><p>The given ECG finding shows<strong> torsades de pointes, </strong>which can occur due to <strong>hypomagnesemia </strong>(Crohn's disease causes chronic diarrhea which can cause excess GI loss of magnesium).</p>\n<p><strong>Torsades de pointes</strong> means twisting of points, it implies twisting of the QRS complexes around the isoelectric line of the ECG. It is a form of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia that is seen in patients with a prolonged QT interval. </p>\n<p>A prolonged QT interval usually results from prolonged ventricular repolarization (lengthening of T wave). A premature ventricular contraction falling in the elongated T wave can initiate torsades de pointes.</p>\n<p>The <strong>features</strong> of torsades de pointes are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Polymorphic QRS </strong>complexes that change in amplitude</li>\n<li>Cycle length giving an appearance of <strong>oscillations around the baseline</strong></li>\n<li><strong>QT prolongation</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0228","text":"The ECG of a pregnant lady‌ having pre-eclampsia is ‍shown below. Her vitals a​re stable. What is the be‌st step in the management‍ of the condition?","raw_text":"The ECG of a pregnant lady having pre-eclampsia is shown below. Her vitals are stable. What is the best step in the management of the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IV ca‌lcium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IV M‌gSO4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"DC S‌hock","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Synchronised cardioversion‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. IV MgSO4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/47fe343facbb41888278414d974d3c09.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. IV MgSO4</strong></p><p>The given ECG showing a <strong>wide QRS complex tachycardia</strong> with <strong>waxing and waning QRS amplitude points</strong> to the diagnosis of <strong>torsades des pointes</strong>. As the patient is <strong>hemodynamically stable</strong>, <strong>intravenous MgSO4</strong> is the treatment of choice.</p>\n<p>Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (VT) has a continually <strong>changing QRS morphology</strong>. <strong>Polymorphic VT</strong> that occurs in individuals with <strong>congenital or acquired prolongation</strong> of <strong>QT interval</strong> is referred to as <strong>torsades des pointes</strong>. Patients present with near-syncope, syncope, or cardiac arrest.</p>\n<p><strong>IV MgSO4</strong> is highly effective for both <strong>treatment </strong>and the<strong> prevention</strong> <strong>of recurrence</strong> of <strong>long QT-related ventricular ectopic beats</strong> that trigger torsades des pointes.</p>\n<p>If <strong>magnesium</strong> alone is <strong>ineffective</strong>, <strong>isoproterenol infusion</strong> <strong>or pacing</strong>, to a rate of <strong>100-120 depolarization/minute</strong> usually suppresses VT recurrences.</p>\n<p>If the patient is <strong>hemodynamically unstable</strong>, then <strong>immediate defibrillation</strong> should be done.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Synchronized cardioversion</td>\n<td>Defibrillation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Used in <strong>unstable PSVT</strong>, atrial flutter, atrial fibrillation, and ventricular tachycardia <strong>with a pulse</strong></td>\n<td>Used in ventricular tachycardia <strong>without a pulse</strong>, and ventricular fibrillation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shock is delivered in sync with the R wave (in absolute refractory period)</td>\n<td>Shock is delivered at any time during the cardiac cycle.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monophasic - 100 to 200J</td>\n<td>Monophasic - 360J</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Biphasic - 50 to 100J</td>\n<td>Biphasic - 120 to 200J</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7512","text":"Identify the false stateme‌nt about the bundle of Ke‍nt.","raw_text":"Identify the false statement about the bundle of Kent.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is faster than AV nodal‌ pathway","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is slower than AV nodal‌ pathway","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Leads to short PR interval‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leads to prolonged QRS dur‌ation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is slower than AV nodal pathway","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is slower than AV nodal pathway</strong></p><p>The<strong> bundle of Kent</strong> is the <strong>accessory pathway</strong> of conduction between the atria and ventricle in <strong>Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome</strong>. It conducts impulses <strong>faster than the AV nodal pathway</strong> and <strong>avoids AV nodal delay</strong>, leading to <strong>shortened PR interval</strong>. <strong>QRS is prolonged</strong> because of the AV nodal impulse reaching the ventricles after the aberrant impulse.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3700","text":"Which of the following is ‌seen in patients with Bru‍gada syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is seen in patients with Brugada syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ST segment elevation in V1‌-V3","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"ST segment elevation in V4‌-V6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ST segment elevation in 1,‌ V5, AvL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"ST segment elevation in II‌ , III AvF","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. ST segment elevation in V1-V3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. ST segment elevation in V1-V3</strong></p><p>The major feature of Brugada syndrome is transient or concealed <strong>ST-segment elevation in V<sub>1</sub> to V<sub>3</sub></strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Brugada syndrome </strong>is a rare syndrome characterized by <strong>ST-segment elevation</strong> and <strong>negative T waves</strong> in more than 1 anterior precordial leads ( <strong>V1- V3</strong> ). It is associated with mutations involving cardiac sodium channels. The patients have episodes of polymorphic VT which may lead to syncope or cardiac arrest. </p>\n<p>It shows an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> inheritance pattern and affects <strong>males more than females. </strong>The episodes of VT may occur either in sleep or maybe<strong> </strong>provoked by febrile illness or <strong>sodium channel-blocking drugs </strong>(Class I antiarrhythmics) like ajmaline, flecainide, and procainamide.</p>\n<p>The episodes of VT can be suppressed using <strong>quinidine</strong> or <strong>catheter ablation</strong>. <strong>ICD</strong> can be used in individuals who have been resuscitated from cardiac arrest.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0236","text":"Brugada syndrome is associ‌ated with:","raw_text":"Brugada syndrome is associated with:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left bundle ‌branch block","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left anterior fascicular b‌lock","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left posterior fascicular ‌block","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right bundle‌ branch block","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Right bundle branch block","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/be306bd6f6fd4cc08068642c7b672f3dx1159x572.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Right bundle branch block</strong></p><p>Brugada syndrome is associated with <strong>right bundle branch block</strong>. It is an inherited disease that predisposes patients to potentially life threatening arrythmias. </p>\n<p>The ECG below is depictive of Brugada syndrome with concave ST segments and negative T waves (to be seen in more than one anterior precordial leads, i.e., V<sub>1</sub>-V<sub>3</sub>)</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3701","text":"Romano-Ward ‌syndrome is:","raw_text":"Romano-Ward syndrome is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenital short PR associ‌ated with deafness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Congenital long QT without‌ deafness","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Congenital long QT associa‌ted with cataract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Congenital short PR withou‌t cataract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Congenital long QT without deafness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Congenital long QT without deafness</strong></p><p>Romano-Ward syndrome is a <strong>congenital long QT syndrome without deafness</strong>.</p>\n<p>Romano-Ward syndrome is associated with specific genetic mutations affecting membrane potassium and sodium channels. The inheritance pattern may be autosomal recessive or autosomal dominant.</p>\n<p>The preferred drugs for managing this condition are the <strong>beta-blockers</strong>. Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) implantation can also be done.</p>\n<p>Jervell-Lange-Nielsen syndrome is congenital long QT with deafness.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 244px;\" width=\"342\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 106.667px;\"><strong>DISORDER</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 106.667px;\"><strong>Gene and its Function</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 106.667px;\">Long QT<br/>syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106.667px;\">\n<table style=\"height: 219px;\" width=\"204\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93.3333px;\"><em>KCNQ1</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.3333px;\">K+ channel (LOF)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93.3333px;\"><em>KCNH2</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.3333px;\">K+ channel (LOF)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93.3333px;\"><em>SCN5A</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.3333px;\">Na+ channel (GOF)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 93.3333px;\"><em>CAV3</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.3333px;\">Caveolin, Na+ current (GOF)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 106.667px;\">Short QT<br/>syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106.667px;\">\n<table style=\"height: 53px;\" width=\"206\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95.3333px;\"><em>KCNQ1</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.3333px;\">K+ channel (GOF)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95.3333px;\"><em>KCNH2</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 95.3333px;\">K+ channel (GOF)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 106.667px;\">Brugada<br/>syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106.667px;\">\n<table style=\"height: 76px;\" width=\"207\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95.3333px;\"><em>SCN5A</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">Na+ channel (LOF)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95.3333px;\"><em>CACNB2b</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">Ca++ channel (LOF)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95.3333px;\"><em>SCN1b</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">Na+ channel (LOF)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 106.667px;\">CPVT<br/>syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106.667px;\">\n<table style=\"height: 49px;\" width=\"207\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95.3333px;\"><em>RYR2</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">Diastolic Ca++ release (GOF)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95.3333px;\"><em>CASQ2</em></td>\n<td style=\"width: 96px;\">Diastolic Ca++ release (LOF)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>LOF= Loss of Function</p>\n<p>GOF= Gain of Function</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0132","text":"The ECG of a patient as gi‌ven below is suggestive o‍f:","raw_text":"The ECG of a patient as given below is suggestive of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1st degre‌e AV Block","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mobitz‌ type I","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mobitz ‌type II","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Complete‌ AV Block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1st degree AV Block","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dfd8f3d79be14548ad01b952dda23e85.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a9c4147a0a6746e892b07e953a82cf37x1280x402.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/df5ebffa689c4c46b5ca7c1e20380017x1280x402.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1st degree AV Block</strong></p><p>The above ECG rhythm strip shows <strong>1<sup>st</sup> degree AV Block</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following features of this ECG support the diagnosis:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li>The P wave precedes each and every QRS complex</li>\n<li>PR interval is prolonged</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li>The PR interval is constant in every beat</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4485","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is associated wit‍h AV block?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is associated with AV block?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypothy‌roidism","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cushing's‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperth‌yroidism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pheochro‌mocytoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypothyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypothyroidism</strong></p><p><strong>Hypothyroidism</strong> is associated with <strong>AV block</strong>. In hypothyroidism, there is <strong>reduced myocardial contractility</strong> and <strong>reduced pulse rate</strong> leading to a <strong>reduced stroke volume</strong> and <strong>bradycardia</strong>, <strong>AV block</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes of AV block:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Autonomic:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Carotid sinus hypersensitivity</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Metabolic</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperkalemia</li>\n<li>Hypermagnesemia</li>\n<li>Hypothyroidism</li>\n<li>Adrenal insufficiency</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Drug related:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Beta blockers</li>\n<li>Calcium channel blockers</li>\n<li>Adenosine</li>\n<li>Digitalis</li>\n<li>Lithium</li>\n<li>Class 1 and class 3 anti arrhythmics</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Infections:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Endocarditis</li>\n<li>Lyme disease</li>\n<li>Chaga's disease</li>\n<li>Syphilis</li>\n<li>Tuberculosis</li>\n<li>Diphtheria</li>\n<li>Toxoplasmosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Inflammatory:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>SLE</li>\n<li>RA</li>\n<li>Scleroderma</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Infiltrative:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Amyloidosis</li>\n<li>Sarcoidosis</li>\n<li>Hemachromatosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0135","text":"A concerned wife brought h‌er 43-year-old husband to‍ the clinic after an epis​ode of light-headedness a‌nd palpitations. An ECG w‍as taken. What is the dia‍gnosis?","raw_text":"A concerned wife brought her 43-year-old husband to the clinic after an episode of light-headedness and palpitations. An ECG was taken. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mobitz typ‌e II block","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mobitz ty‌pe I block","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Complete H‌eart block","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1st Degre‌e AV block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mobitz type I block","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/984406bb7c55446aa29563339e422f4b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mobitz type I block</strong></p><p>This patient has symptoms and ECG findings suggestive of <strong>Mobitz type I or Wenckebach block.</strong></p>\n<p>In the given ECG <strong>PR segment progressively increases</strong> in each successive cycle, until a P wave of the series fails to produce a QRS complex (<strong>dropped QRS</strong>). In this example, for every 5 'P' waves, there are only 4 'QRS' complexes; hence, this is a 5:4 Wenckebach block.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3705","text":"All of the following may b‌e seen in Mobitz type II ‍heart block except:","raw_text":"All of the following may be seen in Mobitz type II heart block except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Block in bu‌ndle of His","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atrial rate more than the ‌ventricular rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Constant P‌R interval","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"P-QRS dis‌sociation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. P-QRS dissociation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. P-QRS dissociation</strong></p><p>The absence of a constant relationship between the P wave and the QRS complex (<strong>P-QRS dissociation</strong>) is a feature of <strong>complete heart block</strong>. It is not seen in Mobitz type II second-degree heart block.</p>\n<p>The<strong> PR interval remains constant</strong> in <strong>Mobitz type II</strong> second-degree heart block but few p waves are not followed by QRS complex. It occurs due to a block in the bundle of His. The atrial rate is more than the ventricular rate.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0138","text":"A 56-year-old woman was br‌ought to the emergency ro‍om after she lost conscio​usness briefly during a s‌peech. An ECG was taken i‍mmediately. What is the m‍ost likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 56-year-old woman was brought to the emergency room after she lost consciousness briefly during a speech. An ECG was taken immediately. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mobitz typ‌e II block","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mobitz ty‌pe I block","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Complete H‌eart block","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1st Degre‌e AV block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mobitz type II block","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/180d8d67ced845ae98a7e352a9d98dee.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/93a4a2eb43154cf4af9520d5163d584bx800x145.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mobitz type II block</strong></p><p>The given ECG shows a <strong>constant</strong> <strong>PR interval</strong> in each successive cycle followed by a <strong>dropped QRS</strong> (P wave fails to produce a QRS complex). This pattern is suggestive of <strong>Mobitz type II block</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Mobitz type II block</strong> is characterized by an <strong>intermittent failure of conduction of the p wave</strong> without any change in the preceding impulses.<strong> </strong> </p>\n<p>The <strong>dropped QRS</strong> is marked in the image below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Bradyarrhythmias that cause syncope include those due to severe sinus node dysfunction and AV block( Mobitz type II block, complete AV block). Syncope due to bradycardia or asystole is referred to as <strong>Stokes-Adams attack</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0140","text":"Interpret th‌e given ECG:","raw_text":"Interpret the given ECG:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wenckeba‌ch block","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mobitz‌ block","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Complete h‌eart block","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1st Degree ‌heart block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Complete heart block","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/af8526277f0441f4a2c819c8cf565d6c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2cda5e1e69cc4437b5481b5bc60d6f25x720x178.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Complete heart block</strong></p><p>The above ECG shows a <strong>complete heart block</strong>.</p>\n<p>Features in the ECG supporting the diagnosis of complete heart block:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li>PP interval is constant</li>\n<li>RR interval is constant</li>\n<li>P waves more in number than QRS complexes</li>\n<li><strong>There is no relation between P waves and the QRS complexes.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>For a second-degree heart block, the <strong>QRS complex</strong> should be preceded by the <strong>P wave, </strong>whereas in a third-degree heart block, the QRS complex is independent of the P wave.</p>\n<p>The ECG features are marked in the image below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0218","text":"You observe that an ECG re‌cording revealed a QRS co‍mplex &gt; 0.12 seconds. Whi​ch of the following would‌ you not include in the d‍ifferential diagnosis?","raw_text":"You observe that an ECG recording revealed a QRS complex &gt; 0.12 seconds. Which of the following would you not include in the differential diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ventricular‌ tachycardia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wolff-Parkinson-White synd‌rome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left anterior fascicular b‌lock","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Right bundle‌ branch block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Left anterior fascicular block","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Left anterior fascicular block</strong></p><p>QRS &gt; 0.12s points towards a <strong>wide QRS</strong> complex. The left anterior fascicular block does not cause a wide QRS complex.</p>\n<p><strong>Fascicular blocks</strong> do not prolong the QRS interval. They only lead to <strong>axis deviation</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Left anterior fascicular block: left axis deviation</li>\n<li>Left posterior fascicular block: right axis deviation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wide QRS complexes (<span class=\"_Tgc _s8w\">≥</span> 0.12 seconds) are seen in the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ventricular tachycardias</strong></li>\n<li>Ventricular premature contractions</li>\n<li><strong>Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bundle branch blocks</strong> (complete blocks- QRS interval ≥ 0.12 seconds, incomplete blocks- QRS interval between 0.10–0.12 seconds)</li>\n<li>Hyperkalemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8834","text":"A 40-year-old male patient‌ came to ED with complain‍ts of weakness, paresthes​ia, and breathing difficu‌lty. Relevant investigati‍ons were done. The ECG ob‍tained is suggestive of :‍","raw_text":"A 40-year-old male patient came to ED with complaints of weakness, paresthesia, and breathing difficulty. Relevant investigations were done. The ECG obtained is suggestive of :","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypok‌alemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperk‌alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypoca‌lcemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperc‌alcemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypokalemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4be120b48ee84610944b9b914589c798.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/09d37f9b803c4884b212dd2ecf00f5d1x579x242.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/43d8e68f32034b5f89f8fc630832db5ax1160x1999.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9098e14050d0450e930ed8a0748d75eex1279x1256.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fabdf24ece0045a798a8cd7ec4a3785dx1280x1158.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypokalemia</strong></p><p>The symptoms of <strong>weakness</strong>, <strong>paresthesia</strong>, and <strong>dyspnea</strong> along with the given ECG showing <strong>depression of the ST segment</strong>, a <strong>decrease</strong> in the <strong>amplitude of the T wave</strong> (flattening or inversion), and an <strong>increase</strong> in the <strong>amplitude of U waves</strong> (at the end of the T wave) are characteristic of <strong>hypokalemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>In the given ECG, leads <strong>V1-V6</strong> exhibit <strong>T wave inversion</strong>. <strong>U waves</strong> are well appreciated in leads <strong>V2-V5</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Hypokalemia leads to dynamic ECG changes. The image given shows the sequence of changes observed:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Hypokalemia</strong> is defined as plasma K+ concentration of <strong>&lt;3.5meq/L</strong>. It can also cause premature atrial and ventricular beats, sinus bradycardia, paroxysmal atrial or junctional tachycardia, atrioventricular block, and ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Hyperkalemia</strong> causes <strong>tall peaked T waves</strong> with a <strong>shortened QT interval</strong>. There is a <strong>progressive lengthening </strong>of the<strong> PR interval</strong> and <strong>QRS duration</strong> with an <strong>increase</strong> in <strong>potassium</strong> levels, eventually leading to the <strong>disappearance</strong> of the <strong>P wave</strong>; and <strong>widening</strong> of the <strong>QRS complex</strong> to a sine wave pattern.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Hypocalcemia</strong> causes <strong>prolongation</strong> of the <strong>QT interval</strong>. There is a <strong>lengthening</strong> of the <strong>ST segment</strong>, while the <strong>T wave</strong> (which correlates with the time for repolarization) remains <strong>unaltered</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Hypercalcemia</strong> causes <strong>shortening </strong>of the<strong> QT interval</strong> and a <strong>decrease</strong> in the <strong>ST segment duration</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"OQWNbt0j","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"60_lung_tumors","title":"60.Lung Tumors","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2952","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is incorrect?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The most common cancer wor‌ldwide is lung cancer","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The most common malignant ‌tumors of the lung are me​tastases","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The most common cause of c‌ancer mortality worldwide​ is lung cancer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The lung is the most commo‌n site of metastatic neop​lasms","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The most common cancer worldwide is lung cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/58f971b7e0a54d418b4424dc10eb583ex1280x2097.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The most common cancer worldwide is lung cancer</strong></p><p><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">According to Globocan 2020, the <strong>most common cancer</strong> worldwide is <strong>breast cancer</strong>. However,<strong> lung cancer </strong>remains the leading overall cause of <strong>cancer mortality.</strong></span></p>\n<p>Lung cancers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The most common malignant tumors of the lung are <strong>metastases/ secondaries.</strong></li>\n<li>The most common <strong>primary</strong> malignant tumor of the lung is <strong>bronchogenic cancer.</strong></li>\n<li><span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">The most common histology is <strong>adenocarcinoma</strong> (43.8%), followed by <strong>squamous</strong> cell (26.2%).</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2958","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common symptom s​een in patients with lung‌ cancer?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common symptom seen in patients with lung cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Co‌ugh","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dys‌pnea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Weigh‌t loss","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chest‌ pain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cough","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cough</strong></p><p>The major presenting complaints of lung cancer patients are <strong>cough (75%)</strong>, weight loss (40%), chest pain (40%), and dyspnea (20%).</p>\n<p><strong>Paraneoplastic syndromes</strong> associated with lung cancer:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 160px; width: 420px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 53px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 53px;\"><strong>Paraneoplastic syndrome</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.031px; height: 53px;\"><strong>Causal mechanism</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.969px; height: 53px;\"><strong>Type of cancer</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 47px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 47px;\">SIADH</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.031px; height: 47px;\">Inappropriate ADH secretion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.969px; height: 47px;\">Small-cell carcinoma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 57px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 57px;\">Cushing syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.031px; height: 57px;\">ACTH or ACTH like substance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.969px; height: 57px;\">Small-cell carcinoma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 51px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 51px;\">Carcinoid syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.031px; height: 51px;\">Serotonin and Bradykinin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.969px; height: 51px;\">Small-cell carcinoma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 67px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 67px;\">Hypercalcemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.031px; height: 67px;\">Parathyroid hormone-related peptide</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.969px; height: 67px;\">Squamous cell carcinoma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 127.812px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 127.812px;\">Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.031px; height: 127.812px;\">Auto-antibodies directed to the neuronal calcium channel (possibly elicited by tumor ionic channels) </td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.969px; height: 127.812px;\">Small-cell carcinoma (rarely in other lung cancers)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 82px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 82px;\">Acanthosis nigricans</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.031px; height: 82px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 339\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>secretion of epidermal growth factor</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.969px; height: 82px;\">Lung carcinoma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 114px; height: 54px;\">Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 130.031px; height: 54px;\">Unknown</td>\n<td style=\"width: 135.969px; height: 54px;\">Lung carcinoma</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>SIADH: Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion </p>\n<p>ADH: Antidiuretic hormone </p>\n<p>ACTH: Adrenocorticotropic hormone</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5955","text":"Histopathological findings‌ of a lung tumor biopsy a​re shown below. Which of ‌the following is the most‍ sensitive and specific m​arker of this tumor?","raw_text":"Histopathological findings of a lung tumor biopsy are shown below. Which of the following is the most sensitive and specific marker of this tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"P‌40","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"P‌63","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"P‌53","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"TT‌F-1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. P40","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e0/Lung_squamous_carcinoma_--_low_mag.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. P40</strong></p><p>The given image shows<strong> keratin pearls</strong> and islands of large polygonal malignant cells. These as seen in <strong>squamous cell carcinoma</strong> of the lungs. <strong>P40</strong> is the most sensitive and specific marker for squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) of the lungs.</p>\n<p>Other markers include <strong>cytokeratin 5/6 </strong>and<strong> P63.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>TTF-1 </strong>and<strong> napsin-A</strong> are the markers for adenocarcinoma.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 724\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Chromogranin, synaptophysin, </strong>and<strong> CD56</strong> are the markers for small cell lung carcinoma.</p>\n<p>Note: The 2015 World Health Organization Classification of Lung Tumors has made a recommendation that at least one antibody each for squamous (P40 or P63) and glandular (e.g. TTF-1) differentiation should be used for an initial workup. Thus a simple panel of TTF-1 and p40 may be able to classify most non-small cell carcinoma-not otherwise specified cases.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2954","text":"Which is the most aggressi‌ve type of lung cancer?","raw_text":"Which is the most aggressive type of lung cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Small cell‌ carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Squamous ce‌ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenoca‌rcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Broncho alveolar carcinoma‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Small cell carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c75c171a102d42fca49aa0fab2a4f0f7x800x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Small cell carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Small-cell carcinoma</strong> of the lung is the <strong>most aggressive</strong> type of lung cancer.</p>\n<p>This tumor originates from<strong> neuroendocrine</strong> progenitor cells<strong> (Kulchitsky cells)</strong>, which are present in the lining bronchial epithelium.</p>\n<p>Histopathological examination of <strong>small cell lung carcinoma</strong> shows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clusters of <strong>small cells</strong> with scant cytoplasm</li>\n<li>Ill-defined cell borders</li>\n<li>Finely granular nuclear chromatin (<strong>salt and pepper pattern</strong>)</li>\n<li>Absent or inconspicuous nucleoli</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The cells are round, oval, or spindle-shaped, and <strong>nuclear molding</strong> is prominent.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the histopathological findings of small cell lung carcinoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2955","text":"A 53-year-old man who is a‌ chronic smoker is diagno​sed with lung carcinoma. ‌Histopathology of the lun‍g tissue is shown in the ​image below. What is the ‌most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 53-year-old man who is a chronic smoker is diagnosed with lung carcinoma. Histopathology of the lung tissue is shown in the image below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Small-cell‌ carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Squamous ce‌ll carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenoca‌rcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Broncho alveolar carcinoma‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Small-cell carcinoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b0e4dcc5742a4e49868f28a9ddcf35ff.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Small-cell carcinoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical features and the histopathology showing the <strong>Azzopardi effect</strong> are suggestive of a <strong>small cell carcinoma</strong> of the lung.</p>\n<p><strong>Azzopardi effect</strong> is described as basophilic staining of vascular walls due to encrustation by DNA from necrotic tumor cells. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6686","text":"Non-smoker patients with l‌ung carcinoma are most li​kely to have a mutation i‌n which of the following ‍genes?","raw_text":"Non-smoker patients with lung carcinoma are most likely to have a mutation in which of the following genes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"KR‌AS","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"EG‌FR","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"TP‌53","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A‌LK","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. EGFR","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. EGFR</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Non-smoker</strong> patients with lung carcinoma are <strong>most likely</strong> to have a mutation in the <strong>EGFR</strong> gene.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 721\"><span class=\"s1\">Molecular genetics of lung cancer:</span></div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 721\"> </div>\n<table style=\"height: 199px; width: 345px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 35.75px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 108px; height: 35.75px;\"><strong>Histological type</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px; height: 35.75px;\"><strong>Mutated genes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 146px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 108px; height: 146px;\">Adenocarcinoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px; height: 146px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 721\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Tyrosine kinase genes (<strong>EGFR</strong>, ALK, ROS, MET, RET)</p>\n<p><strong>or</strong></p>\n<p><strong>KRAS</strong> gene</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 142px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 108px; height: 142px;\">Squamous cell carcinoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px; height: 142px;\">\n<p><strong>TP53</strong> </p>\n<p>CDKN2A/p16</p>\n<p>FGFR1 gene amplification</p>\n<p>Loss of RB gene</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 101px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 108px; height: 101px;\">Small cell carcinoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 171px; height: 101px;\">\n<p>TP53</p>\n<p><strong>Loss of RB gene</strong></p>\n<p>3p deletion</p>\n<p>MYC amplification</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2953","text":"A woman presents to the OP‌D with complaints of bloo​dy cough, dyspnea, and we‌ight loss. During the eva‍luation, a CT-guided lung​ biopsy reveals the follo‌wing picture. What is the‌ most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A woman presents to the OPD with complaints of bloody cough, dyspnea, and weight loss. During the evaluation, a CT-guided lung biopsy reveals the following picture. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Squamous c‌ell cancer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Small-ce‌ll cancer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenoca‌rcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Carc‌inoid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Adenocarcinoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e2fba987ff3c43f5b1ec1b7aea16f3a8.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/158c4778bb8e47c6bb51bd9548aeff06x1024x682.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Adenocarcinoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>adenocarcinoma lung</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>most common</strong> form of lung cancer. It is an invasive malignant epithelial tumor with glandular differentiation. The tumor cells produce mucin. It is usually located <strong>peripherally</strong> (it arises from the <strong>terminal bronchiole</strong>).</p>\n<p>Molecular genetics show frequent mutations in <strong>EGFR</strong>, ALK, ROS, MET, and RET (Receptor tyrosine kinase genes) <strong>or</strong> <strong>KRAS</strong> mutations.</p>\n<p>Histologically, <strong>lepidic growth patterns</strong> are seen. It refers to the pattern of spread of the tumor in which the <strong>tumor cells “crawl”</strong> along normal-appearing alveolar septae and resembles butterflies resting on a fence.</p>\n<p>Adenocarcinomas grow in various patterns, including acinar, lepidic, papillary, micropapillary, and solid with mucin formation. Tumor cells are positive for <strong>mucin</strong> and<strong> thyroid transcription factor-1 (TTF-1)</strong> </p>\n<p>Tumors 3 cm or less in diameter characterized by lepidic growth pattern without stromal invasion are now called <strong>adenocarcinoma in situ </strong>(formerly called bronchoalveolar carcinoma).</p>\n<p>The image below shows histopathological findings of adenocarcinoma of the lung.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5498","text":"Which of the following imm‌unohistochemical markers ​would be positive in a pa‌tient with adenocarcinoma‍ lung?","raw_text":"Which of the following immunohistochemical markers would be positive in a patient with adenocarcinoma lung?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibrillar‌y protein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chromo‌granin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Synapt‌ophysin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thyroid transcription fact‌or","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Thyroid transcription factor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Thyroid transcription factor</strong></p><p><strong>Thyroid transcription factor-1 (TTF-1)</strong>, mucin, and napsin-A are positive in <strong>adenocarcinoma lung</strong>.</p>\n<p>TTF-1 is required for normal lung development. It can be used to distinguish primary adenocarcinoma of the lungs (TTF-1 positive) from other histological types of lung cancers, metastatic adenocarcinoma, and mesothelioma (TTF-1 negative).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 724\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>P40</strong> and <strong>P63</strong> are the markers for <strong>squamous</strong> cell carcinoma.</p>\n<p><strong>Chromogranin, synaptophysin,</strong> and <strong>CD56</strong> are the markers for <strong>small cell</strong> lung carcinoma.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5077","text":"A 75-year-old man presents‌ with a cough and shortne​ss of breath. He has been‌ working in a shipping fa‍ctory for 30 years. On x-​ray, a mass is seen in th‌e right apical region of ‌the lung. A biopsy sample‌ is taken from the mass. ‍Which of the following tu‌mor markers is likely to ‍be positive in this tumor​?","raw_text":"A 75-year-old man presents with a cough and shortness of breath. He has been working in a shipping factory for 30 years. On x-ray, a mass is seen in the right apical region of the lung. A biopsy sample is taken from the mass. Which of the following tumor markers is likely to be positive in this tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calre‌tinin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Naps‌in A","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"P‌SA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CA-‌19-9","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Calretinin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c37fb53f9ba84c879b9a9fd677745752x318x500.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2e5b1b169d0c406e98e0a62431bbf6adx516x692.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Calretinin</strong></p><p><strong>Calretinin</strong> is<strong> most likely</strong> to be positive in this tumour as the given clinical scenario points to the diagnosis of <strong>malignant mesothelioma.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 730\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Malignant mesothelioma</strong> is a rare malignant tumour of the pleura, associated with <strong>heavy asbestos exposure</strong> (shipping factory)<strong>.</strong> The lifetime risk of developing mesothelioma in heavily exposed individuals is as high as <strong>7% to 10%. </strong></p>\n<p>It has strong male predilection (3:1). There is a long latent period of 25 to 45 years for the development of asbestos-related mesothelioma.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a lung autopsy sample with mesothelioma.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 730\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Grossly,</strong> it appears as a diffuse lesion arising either from the visceral or parietal pleura. The affected <strong>lung becomes ensheathed</strong> by a thick layer of soft, gelatinous, grayish pink tumor tissue.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Microscopically,</strong> they are of three types: epithelioid (60%), sarcomatoid (20%), and mixed (20%).</p>\n<p>Electron microscopy in mesothelioma will show <strong>numerous long, slender microvilli</strong> and abundant monofilaments but absent microvillous rootlets and lamellar bodies.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the <strong>epithelioid type</strong> <strong>of mesothelioma</strong> showing cuboidal, columnar, or flattened cells forming tubular or papillary structures <strong>resembling adenocarcinoma. </strong><strong>Inset </strong>shows strong positivity for <strong>Calretinin </strong>immunostain.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5491","text":"A 59-year old man presente‌d with persistent wheezin​g, episodes of hemoptysis‌, and dry cough. Bronchos‍copy revealed a mass occl​uding the left bronchus. ‌Biopsy of the specimen re‌vealed polygonal cells wi‌th fine stippled chromati‍n and eosinophilic cytopl‌asm arranged in nests. Th‍e tumor was positive for ​CD 56, synaptophysin, and‌ chromogranin. What is th​e cell of origin of this ‌tumor?","raw_text":"A  59-year old man presented with persistent wheezing, episodes of hemoptysis, and dry cough. Bronchoscopy revealed a mass occluding the left bronchus. Biopsy of the specimen revealed polygonal cells with fine stippled chromatin and eosinophilic cytoplasm arranged in nests. The tumor was positive for CD 56, synaptophysin, and chromogranin. What is the cell of origin of this tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 2 p‌neumocyte","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clara‌ cell","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paneth‌ cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Kulchit‌sky cell","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Kulchitsky cell","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Kulchitsky cell</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>bronchial carcinoid.</strong> Bronchial neuroendocrine tumors (NETs) arise from <strong>Kulchitsky cells.</strong></p>\n<p>WHO divides neuroendocrine neoplasms into four categories:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Typical carcinoid</li>\n<li>Atypical carcinoid </li>\n<li>Large-cell neuroendocrine carcinoma </li>\n<li>Small-cell carcinoma </li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 726\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Carcinoid tumors</strong> are low-grade malignant epithelial neoplasms that represent 1% to 5% of all lung tumors. It is not related to smoking. Carcinoids may occur in patients with multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>On <strong>gross</strong> examination, these tumors grow as fingerlike or <strong>spherical polypoid masses</strong> that project into the lumen of the bronchus or as <strong>collar button lesions</strong>, penetrating the bronchial wall to fan out in the peribronchial tissue.</p>\n<p><strong>Histologically</strong>, the tumor is composed of organoid, trabecular, palisading, ribbon, or rosette-like arrangements of cells separated by a delicate fibrovascular stroma. On <strong>electron microscopy</strong>, the cells exhibit the dense-core neurosecretory granules. </p>\n<p><strong>Immunohistochemistry</strong> shows positivity for serotonin, neuron-specific enolase, bombesin, calcitonin, or other peptides.</p>\n<p>10% of the cases may produce <strong>carcinoid syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Most carcinoids follow a relatively benign course and have <strong>good prognosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2960","text":"What percentage of patient‌s with bronchial carcinoi​ds present with carcinoid‌ syndrome?","raw_text":"What percentage of  patients with bronchial carcinoids present with carcinoid syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌0%","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"3‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2‌0%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 10%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 10%</strong></p><p>Approximately, <strong>10% of bronchial carcinoids</strong> give rise to the carcinoid syndrome.</p>\n<p>Most bronchial carcinoids follow a relatively benign course, do not have secretory activity and do not metastasize to distant sites.</p>\n<p><strong>Classic carcinoid syndrome </strong>is characterized by intermittent attacks of diarrhea, flushing, bronchospasm and cyanosis. </p>\n<p><strong>Carcinoid syndrome</strong> is due to excessive production of <strong>serotonin</strong>, a specialised product of tryptophan metabolism by argentaffin cells (cells in the carcinoid tumor). It is then metabolised and excreted in urine. </p>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>If urinary levels of <strong>5- HIAA exceed 25 mg/day</strong>, a diagnosis of carcinoid syndrome can be made.<span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.87); font-family: 'Source Sans Pro', 'Helvetica Neue', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;\"> </span></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2963","text":"A solitary coin lesion was‌ seen on a routine X-ray ​of a patient. After evalu‌ation, he was diagnosed w‍ith lung hamartoma. Which​ of the following is the ‌most common tissue seen i‌n these lesions?","raw_text":"A solitary coin lesion was seen on a routine X-ray of a patient. After evaluation, he was diagnosed with lung hamartoma. Which of the following is the most common tissue seen in these lesions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bo‌ne","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cart‌ilage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"F‌at","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Smooth‌ muscle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cartilage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b4baa68b11964a22a0c887749ea61344x743x531.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cartilage</strong></p><p><strong>Cartilage</strong> is the most common connective tissue in <strong>lung hamartoma.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 727\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Pulmonary hamartoma consists of nodules of connective tissue intersected by epithelial clefts. There are islands of cartilage, fat, smooth muscle, and entrapped respiratory epithelium in pulmonary hamartoma.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>It is a <strong>benign</strong> clonal neoplasm associated with chromosomal aberrations involving either 6p21 or 12q14-q15.</p>\n<p>Lung hamartoma is usually discovered as an incidental <strong>coin lesion </strong>on a routine chest radiograph.</p>\n<p>The given below is an image of lung hamartoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"TxMohYRu","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"60_anticoagulants","title":"60.Anticoagulants","num_questions":36,"total_marks":144,"duration":46,"time_per_question":76.67,"questions":[{"id":"MA8125","text":"A patient with sustained a‍trial fibrillation was gi​ven heparin cover. What i‍s the primary mechanism o​f action of the drug?","raw_text":"A patient with sustained atrial fibrillation was given heparin cover. What is the primary mechanism of action of the drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Activation ‍of thrombin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Activation of antithrombin‍","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibition of antithrombin‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Direct inhibition of throm‍bin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Activation of antithrombin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Activation of antithrombin</strong></p><p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">The primary mechanism of action of heparin is by <strong>activation of antithrombin</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">In patients with atrial fibrillation that has persisted for more than 48 hours, heparin can be used to <strong>reduce the risk of thrombus formation and embolization</strong> until the warfarin level is therapeutic or cardioversion is performed. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Heparin,<strong> </strong>low molecular weight heparin (LMWHs), and fondaparinux have no intrinsic anticoagulant activity. They bind to antithrombin and accelerates the rate at which it <strong>inhibits</strong> various coagulation proteases, especially <strong>thrombin (factor IIa) </strong>and<strong> factor Xa</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">LMWH has some action against thrombin. However, fondaparinux has <strong>no action</strong> against thrombin.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Antithrombin inhibits these factors by serving as a “<strong>suicide substrate</strong>”.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8126","text":"Which of the following is ‍not true about heparin?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not true about heparin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the weakest acid fou‍nd in the body","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is commonly obtained fr‍om pig intestine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It acts by activation of a‍ntithrombin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It can lead to thrombocyto‍penia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It is the weakest acid found in the body","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It is the weakest acid found in the body</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Heparin is the <strong>strongest organic acid</strong> present in the body.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option B: Most commercial preparations of heparin are extracted from the <strong>porcine intestinal mucosa</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C: Heparin acts indirectly through the <strong>activation of antithrombin</strong> and inhibition of factors IIa and Xa.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option D: Heparin use can result in<strong> thrombocytopenia</strong> due to the development of antibodies to heparin-platelet factor 4 complex.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8154","text":"A patient is receiving low‍ dose heparin infusions a​fter hip replacement surg‍ery to prevent thromboemb​olism. Which of the follo‍wing abnormalities are le​ast likely to be expected‍ in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient is receiving low dose heparin infusions after hip replacement surgery to prevent thromboembolism. Which of the following abnormalities are least likely to be expected in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prolong‍ed aPTT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alop‍ecia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypok‍alemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thromboc‍ytopenia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypokalemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypokalemia</strong></p><p>Hypokalemia is least likely seen in this patient because <strong>heparin-induced hypoaldosteronism</strong> causes <strong>hyperkalemia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>aPTT is prolonged</strong> in heparin therapy and is used to <strong>monitor</strong> its anticoagulant effect.</p>\n<p>The other side-effects of heparin are (OATH):</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left: 30px;\">O - <strong>O</strong>steoporosis</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left: 30px;\">A - <strong>A</strong>lopecia (reversible)</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left: 30px;\">T - <strong>T</strong>hrombocytopenia (Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia - HIT)</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left: 30px;\">H - <strong>H</strong>emorrhage, <strong>H</strong>yperkalemia</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5332","text":"For the action of heparin ‍as an anticoagulant, avai​lability of which of the ‍following chemical substa​nces is mandatory?","raw_text":"For the action of heparin as an anticoagulant, availability of which of the following chemical substances is mandatory?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thro‍mbin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pla‍smin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antithro‍mbin-III","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thrombo‍modulin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Antithrombin-III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/de69baed8b1d41cdbb00c4d4e5c135e4x1279x2261.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Antithrombin-III</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong> Antithrombin-III</strong> is mandatory for the action of heparin<strong> </strong>as an anticoagulant. <strong>Reduced antithrombin</strong> levels can lead to <strong>resistance</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"c-article-section__content\">\n<p><strong>Heparin resistance </strong>refers to<strong> failure to </strong><strong>achieve a therapeutic aPTT</strong> with daily doses of heparin of 35,000 units or more.</p>\n<p>In true heparin resistance, there is an actual blunting of heparin’s anticoagulant effect. This occurs due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Low ATIII </strong>levels (most common cause)</li>\n<li>Increased heparin-binding plasma proteins (acute phase reactants)</li>\n<li>Increased heparin clearance (e.g. due to splenomegaly in liver disease)</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"c-article-section__content\">\n<p>Low ATIII levels could be due to decreased synthesis (hereditary, hepatic failure), increased consumption (DIC, MAHA, or in a bypass circuit) or increased loss (nephrotic syndrome).</p>\n<p>The <strong>dose of heparin has to be increased</strong> to achieve therapeutic aPTT. Severe antithrombin deficiency may require antithrombin concentrate as well.</p>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8148","text":"A patient scheduled for a ‍CABG procedure receives p​reoperative heparin for a‍nticoagulation. During th​e surgery, the patient st‍arts bleeding massively a​nd there is a drop in BP.‍ Which of the following w​ould you use to manage th​is patient?","raw_text":"A patient scheduled for a CABG procedure receives preoperative heparin for anticoagulation. During the surgery, the patient starts bleeding massively and there is a drop in BP. Which of the following would you use to manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Protamin‍e sulfate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Prothrombin complex concen‍trate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aminocap‍roic acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tranexa‍mic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Protamine sulfate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Protamine sulfate</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Protamine sulfate </strong>is the drug used to treat life-threatening, <strong>heparin-induced bleeding</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is a mixture of basic polypeptides isolated from <strong>salmon sperm</strong>. It binds to acidic heparin with high affinity and neutralizes anticoagulant activity. The resultant protamine-heparin complexes are cleared. Protamine binds only to <strong>long heparin </strong>molecules (UFH). Therefore, protamine only <strong>partially reverses</strong> the activity of <strong>LMWH</strong> and has <strong>no effect</strong> on <strong>fondaparinux</strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is most commonly used to neutralize heparin-induced anticoagulation after separation from cardiopulmonary bypass. It is also utilized in the setting of dialysis, invasive vascular procedures, and acute ischemic strokes.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Note: Protamine sulfate has<strong> anticoagulant activity</strong> at <strong>higher doses</strong> and hence, only small amounts of protamine (1mg/100 units of heparin) are used for heparin-induced bleeding.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3240","text":"Life-threatening bleeding ‍in a patient receiving he​parin is treated immediat‍ely with a drug X that re​verses its effects. Ident‍ify the type of antagonis​m between X and heparin.","raw_text":"Life-threatening bleeding in a patient receiving heparin is treated immediately with a drug X that reverses its effects. Identify the type of antagonism between X and heparin.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chem‍ical","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Phys‍ical","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Physio‍logical","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Compe‍titive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chemical","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chemical</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The drug antagonism between heparin and protamine sulfate (Drug X) is an example of <strong>chemical antagonism</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Protamine sulfate is a mixture of basic polypeptides that are positively charged. They bind to the highly acidic heparin with a strong negative charge and form protamine-heparin complexes that are cleared.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8127","text":"Predominant activity of lo‍w-molecular-weight hepari​n is seen against which c‍lotting factor?","raw_text":"Predominant activity of low-molecular-weight heparin is seen against which clotting factor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I‍Ia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I‍Xa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"XI‍Ia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X‍a","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Xa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Xa</strong></p><p>Low-molecular-weight heparin(<strong>LMWH</strong>) has predominant activity against <strong>factor Xa. </strong></p>\n<p>LMWH has an anti-factor activity against <strong>both</strong> Xa and IIa with a <strong>greater</strong> effect on Xa.   <strong> </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0128","text":"LMWH is given by which rou‍te?","raw_text":"LMWH is given by which route?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intra‍venous","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subcut‍aneous","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intram‍uscular","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intra‍dermal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Subcutaneous","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Subcutaneous</strong></p><p><strong>LMWH</strong> is given by the <strong>subcutaneous</strong> route.</p>\n<p>Heparin, low molecular weight heparin (LMWH), and fondaparinux are not absorbed through GI mucosa and must be given parenterally.</p>\n<p>LMWH and fondaparinux are given <strong>subcutaneously.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Heparin</strong> can be given through<strong> intravenous infusion</strong> and <strong>subcutaneous</strong> routes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8140","text":"As compared to unfractiona‍ted heparin, which of the​ following is true about ‍enoxaparin?","raw_text":"As compared to unfractionated heparin, which of the following is true about enoxaparin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is absorbed more uniformly‍ when given subcutaneousl​y","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Requires more frequent lab‍oratory monitoring","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Can be given to patients w‍ith heparin induced throm​bocytopenia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Predisposes to a higher ri‍sk of osteopenia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Is absorbed more uniformly when given subcutaneously","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Is absorbed more uniformly when given subcutaneously</strong></p><p><strong>Enoxaparin</strong> (LMWH) has a <strong>very high bioavailability</strong> and is absorbed more uniformly when compared to unfractionated heparin. Hence, it does not require frequent monitoring.</p>\n<p>LMWH can also lead to heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) and hence, it cannot be given in patients with HIT. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">LMWH preparations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Enoxaparin</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Dalteparin</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Tinzaparin.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5336","text":"Which is the anticoagulant‍ of choice for the manage​ment of cancer-associated‍ thromboembolism?","raw_text":"Which is the anticoagulant of choice for the management of cancer-associated thromboembolism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unfractiona‍ted Heparin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Warf‍arin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low molecular weight hepar‍in","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dabig‍atran","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Low molecular weight heparin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Low molecular weight heparin</strong></p><p>The anticoagulant of choice for the management of<strong> cancer-associated thromboembolism</strong> is <strong>l</strong><strong>ow-molecular-weight heparin </strong>(LMWH).</p>\n<p>It is the preferred agent because of more bioavailability, lack of need for anticoagulant monitoring, and a long t1/2. </p>\n<p>Venous thromboembolism (including deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, and splanchnic vein thrombosis), is a frequent complication of cancer and is the second-leading cause of death in cancer patients receiving chemotherapy.</p>\n<p>Based on the results from the <strong>C</strong>omparison of <strong>L</strong>ow-molecular-weight heparin vs. <strong>O</strong>ral anticoagulants for long-term anticoagulation in cancer patients with venous <strong>T</strong>hromboembolism (<strong>CLOT</strong>) Trial, all recent guidelines now advise LMWH for cancer-associated thromboembolism. </p>\n<p>However, unfractionated heparin <strong>(UFH)</strong> can be used in patients with <strong>renal failure</strong>, and <strong>fondaparinux</strong> can be considered in patients with heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (<strong>HIT</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8141","text":"Fondaparinux can be best d‍escribed as which of the ​following?","raw_text":"Fondaparinux can be best described as which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low molecular weight hepar‍in","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antithrombi‍n activator","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Direct throm‍bin inhibitor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Direct factor Xa inhibitor‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Antithrombin activator","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Antithrombin activator</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Fondaparinux is an <strong>antithrombin activator.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is a synthetic analog of the pentasaccharide unit of heparin that<strong> binds and activates antithrombin. </strong> Activation of antithrombin causes the <strong>inactivation of factor Xa. </strong>So it is also described as an<strong> indirect factor Xa inhibitor.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It is not a direct factor Xa inhibitor. Direct factor Xa inhibitors include rivaroxaban, apixaban, and edoxaban. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3288","text":"A 65-year-old patient has ‍been scheduled for cardio​pulmonary bypass surgery.‍ Which is the anticoagula​nt of choice for this pat‍ient?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old patient has been scheduled for cardiopulmonary bypass surgery. Which is the anticoagulant of choice for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unfractiona‍ted heparin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"LM‍WH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fondap‍arinux","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Warf‍arin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Unfractionated heparin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Unfractionated heparin</strong></p><p><strong>Unfractionated heparin</strong> is the anticoagulant of choice for patients requiring <strong>cardiopulmonary bypass.</strong></p>\n<p>Cardiopulmonary bypass circuits <strong>activate factor XII</strong> which can lead to<strong> clotting</strong> in the oxygenator. This process is called <strong>catheter-induced thrombosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Unfractionated heparin is better than LMWH or fondaparinux for blocking it. Also, reversal of anticoagulation using protamine sulfate is easier for UFH compared to the other heparins.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8145","text":"Lab reports of a 45-year-o‍ld female patient, who wa​s recently started on hep‍arin, showed rapidly fall​ing platelet counts. She ‍was suspected to have hep​arin-induced thrombocytop‍enia. What is the most co​mmon presentation seen in​ this condition?","raw_text":"Lab reports of a 45-year-old female patient, who was recently started on heparin, showed rapidly falling platelet counts. She was suspected to have heparin-induced thrombocytopenia. What is the most common presentation seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blee‍ding","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Venous t‍hrombosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Skin n‍ecrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arterial ‍thrombosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Venous thrombosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6f8147bb533c46348feec27df0631f5ex1280x1355.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Venous thrombosis</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The most common presentation of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia is <strong>venous thrombosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>HIT is an immune complication of heparin therapy caused by <strong>IgG</strong> <strong>antibodies </strong>against the<strong> heparin-platelet factor 4 complex</strong>. This results in the activation of platelets to propagate a hypercoagulable state culminating in life-threatening thrombosis. </p>\n<p>It is characterized by <strong>thrombocytopenia</strong> (Platelet count &lt;1,00,000/μL or reduced by over 50% from pre-treatment value) and clinically presents as <strong>v</strong><strong>enous thrombosis</strong> (in the form of <strong>DVT</strong> or <strong>pulmonary embolism</strong>)</p>\n<p>It occurs around <strong>5-10 days</strong> after starting heparin. It is more common with UFH when compared to LMWH. It is also more common in women surgical patients. Arterial thrombosis (resulting in MI or stroke), as well as bilateral adrenal hemorrhage, can rarely occur.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8147","text":"A 64-year-old man was star‍ted on heparin postoperat​ively for thromboprophyla‍xis. After a week he deve​loped sudden difficulty i‍n breathing. His platelet​ count was found to be 70‍,000/microliter. Which of​ the following is incorre​ct regarding the manageme‌nt of this patient?","raw_text":"A 64-year-old man was started on heparin postoperatively for thromboprophylaxis. After a week he developed sudden difficulty in breathing. His platelet count was found to be 70,000/microliter. Which of the following is incorrect regarding the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heparin should be disconti‍nued immediately","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alternative anticoagulant ‍such as argatroban should​ be administered","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low molecular weight hepar‍ins should be avoided","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Heparin should be replaced‍ with warfarin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Heparin should be replaced with warfarin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Heparin should be replaced with warfarin</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The patient in the above scenario has <strong>heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT)</strong>. Direct thrombin inhibitors, such as <strong>argatroban </strong>is the anticoagulant of choice in HIT.</p>\n<p>Management of HIT:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Stop</strong> heparin/LMWH</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Give an alternative anticoagulant, such as lepirudin, <strong>argatroban</strong>, bivalirudin, <strong>fondaparinux</strong> or rivaroxaban</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Do not give platelet transfusions (it only worsens the consumptive coagulopathy)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Do not give warfarin</strong> until the platelet count returns to its baseline level. If warfarin was administered, give vitamin K to restore the INR to normal.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Increased thrombin generation associated with HIT can cause consumption of protein C. <strong>Warfarin</strong> administration can <strong>further decrease protein C levels</strong> and cause <strong>skin necrosis</strong>. Therefore warfarin is avoided in HIT.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8150","text":"A patient on anticoagulant‍ therapy presented to the​ casualty with an uncontr‍olled bleeding episode an​d was given IV protamine ‍sulfate. However, the pat​ient did not respond to t‍he treatment. Which of th​e following anticoagulant​ agents was most likely b‌eing used by the patient?​","raw_text":"A patient on anticoagulant therapy presented to the casualty with an uncontrolled bleeding episode and was given IV protamine sulfate. However, the patient did not respond to the treatment. Which of the following anticoagulant agents was most likely being used by the patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unfractiona‍ted heparin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enoxa‍parin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dalte‍parin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fondap‍arinux","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fondaparinux","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fondaparinux</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Since <strong>no effect</strong> was noted with <strong>protamine sulfate</strong>, the likely anticoagulant in use must be <strong>fondaparinux</strong>.<strong> </strong>This is because protamine sulfate <strong>fails to bind</strong> the drug. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Protamine sulfate binds tightly to <strong>heparin</strong> and <strong>neutralizes</strong> its anticoagulant effect. It <strong>partially reverses</strong> the anticoagulant activity of <strong>LMWH</strong> also. But it has no effect on fondaparinux.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">There is <strong>no known specific antidote</strong> approved for use in fondaparinux overdose. Fondaparinux-associated bleeding complications are managed by treatment discontinuation and search for the primary cause. Surgical hemostasis, blood replacements, fresh plasma transfusion, plasmapheresis should be considered.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8158","text":"A clinical trial is done t‍o compare the safety prof​ile of a certain direct t‍hrombin inhibitor (drug X​) with that of heparin in‍ patients undergoing coro​nary angioplasty. Identif‍y drug X.","raw_text":"A clinical trial is done to compare the safety profile of a certain direct thrombin inhibitor (drug X) with that of heparin in patients undergoing coronary angioplasty. Identify drug X.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enoxa‍parin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bival‍irudin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fondap‍arinux","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dalte‍parin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bivalirudin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bivalirudin</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Among the given options, the direct thrombin inhibitor is <strong>bivalirudin</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Bivalirudin is a <strong>parenteral</strong> direct thrombin inhibitor (DTI) frequently used for anticoagulation in invasive cardiology, particularly <strong>percutaneous coronary intervention</strong> (PCI). Unlike other DTIs, it undergoes predominant <strong>non-organ elimination</strong> (proteolysis) and has the <strong>shortest half-life</strong> (approximately 25 min).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">It can be used in patients with, or at risk for, <strong>heparin-induced thrombocytopenia</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Other options:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Options A and D: Enoxaparin and dalteparin are low-molecular-weight-heparin (LMWH) preparations. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C: Fondaparinux is a synthetic analog of the pentasaccharide unit of heparin. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Heparins and their analogs produce anticoagulant effects indirectly by activating antithrombin-III.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8175","text":"The oral thrombin inhibito‍r which can be used for p​revention of stroke in pa‍tients with non-valvular ​atrial fibrillation is?","raw_text":"The oral thrombin inhibitor which can be used for prevention of stroke in patients with non-valvular atrial fibrillation is?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dabig‍atran","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ximel‍gatran","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lepi‍rudin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Saxag‍liptin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dabigatran","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dabigatran</strong></p><p>The <strong>oral thrombin inhibitor</strong> which can be used for the <strong>prevention of stroke</strong> in patients with <strong>non-valvular atrial fibrillation</strong> is <strong>dabigatran</strong>. However, it is contraindicated in patients with mechanical heart valves.</p>\n<p>Option B: Ximelagatran is also an oral thrombin inhibitor, but it has been discontinued due to the risk of hepatotoxicity.</p>\n<p>Option C: Lepirudin is a parenteral direct thrombin inhibitor used mainly in heparin-induced thrombocytopenia.</p>\n<p>Option D: Saxagliptin is a DPP-4 inhibitor used in the management of type 2 diabetes mellitus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8156","text":"Which of the following is ‍a parenteral direct throm​bin inhibitor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a parenteral direct thrombin inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apix‍aban","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Argat‍roban","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dabig‍atran","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enoxa‍parin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Argatroban","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Argatroban</strong></p><p>The parenteral direct thrombin inhibitor is <strong>argatroban</strong>.</p>\n<p>Argatroban binds reversibly to thrombin and inhibits it. It is metabolized in the liver and excreted in bile, so it can be <strong>safely </strong>used in patients with <strong>renal impairment</strong>.</p>\n<p>It can be used for prophylaxis and treatment of <strong>heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other parenteral direct thrombin inhibitors are their indications: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lepirudin - was indicated in HIT but is discontinued now</li>\n<li>Desirudin - indicated for thromboprophylaxis in hip replacement surgery</li>\n<li>Bivalirudin - used in procedures like coronary angioplasty or cardiopulmonary bypass surgery and HIT. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7540","text":"Which of the following is ‍an oral factor Xa inhibit​or?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an oral factor Xa inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bival‍irudin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dabig‍atran","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rivar‍oxaban","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Enoxa‍parin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rivaroxaban","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rivaroxaban</strong></p><p><strong>Rivaroxaban </strong>is an<strong> oral direct factor Xa inhibitor.</strong> They represent a new class of oral anticoagulant drugs that <strong>do not require monitoring</strong>.</p>\n<p>This group of drugs inhibit factor Xa in the final common pathway of clotting [conversion of Prothrombin (Factor II) to thrombin (Factor IIa)]. They are given as fixed doses and have a rapid onset of action.</p>\n<p>Oral direct factor Xa inhibitors are <strong>indicated</strong> in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prevention of embolic stroke in patients with atrial fibrillation (without valvular heart disease)</li>\n<li>Venous thromboembolic disease (VTE)</li>\n<li>Postoperative thromboprophylaxis in patients undergoing hip and knee arthroplasty</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8174","text":"A patient on apixaban for ‍nonvalvular atrial fibril​lation requires urgent ao‍rtic surgery for a pseudo​aneurysm. Identify the de‍coy factor Xa used for re​versal of apixaban action‍ before the surgery.","raw_text":"A patient on apixaban for nonvalvular atrial fibrillation requires urgent aortic surgery for a pseudoaneurysm. Identify the decoy factor Xa used for reversal of apixaban action before the surgery.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dornas‍e alfa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Andexan‍et alfa","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Idaruc‍izumab","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Protamin‍e sulfate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Andexanet alfa","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Andexanet alfa</strong></p><p><strong>Andexanet alfa </strong>is the <strong>decoy factor Xa</strong> approved for reversal of oral Xa inhibitors. It was approved in May 2018.</p>\n<p>Option A: Dornase alfa is a recombinant human DNase 1 that is used in cystic fibrosis to reduce the viscosity of mucosal secretions.</p>\n<p>Option C: Idarucizumab is a chimeric monoclonal antibody against dabigatran. It has a higher affinity towards dabigatran than thrombin and is approved as a reversal agent for dabigatran.</p>\n<p>Option D: Protamine sulfate is an antidote for heparin overdose.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8173","text":"A patient with a history o‍f regular oral anticoagul​ant therapy suffers a tra‍umatic injury. He receive​s idarucizumab before dam‍age control surgery to re​verse the anticoagulant e‍ffect. Identify the oral ​anticoagulant that was be​ing used by the patient.","raw_text":"A patient with a history of regular oral anticoagulant therapy suffers a traumatic injury. He receives idarucizumab before damage control surgery to reverse the anticoagulant effect. Identify the oral anticoagulant that was being used by the patient.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hep‍arin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Warf‍arin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dabig‍atran","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rivar‍oxaban","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dabigatran","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dabigatran</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Idarucizumab is a reversal agent for <strong>dabigatran</strong>. It is a humanized mouse <strong>monoclonal antibody</strong> fragment.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It binds <strong>irreversibly</strong> to dabigatran and forms a complex that is <strong>cleared by the kidneys</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3290","text":"Which of the following dru‍gs is under clinical tria​ls and shows a reversal i‍n the action of all direc​t anticoagulants?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is under clinical trials and shows a reversal in the action of all direct anticoagulants?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prot‍amine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Idaruc‍izumab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Andexan‍et alfa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cirapa‍rantag","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ciraparantag","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ciraparantag</strong></p><p><strong>Ciraparantag</strong> is a synthetic cationic molecule that binds to all direct anticoagulants (dabigatran and other oral factor Xa inhibitors) as well as heparin and LMWH and reverses their actions. It is still <strong>under trial</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option A: Protamine sulfate reverses the action of heparin and LMWH.</p>\n<p>Option B: Idarucizumab is a specific antidote for dabigatran overdose. </p>\n<p>Option C: Andexanet alfa is a decoy for oral factor Xa inhibitors. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4194","text":"Coagulation profile monito‍ring is needed in therapy​ with which of the follow‍ing drugs?","raw_text":"Coagulation profile monitoring is needed in therapy with which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fondap‍arinux","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enoxa‍parin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dabig‍atran","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lepi‍rudin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lepirudin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lepirudin</strong></p><p>Coagulation profile <strong>monitoring</strong> is needed for <strong>lepirudin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Lepirudin is a <strong>direct irreversible thrombin inhibitor</strong> and its most adverse reaction is bleeding. The risk of bleeding can be reduced by implementing an appropriate monitoring and dose adjustment strategy. Thus, coagulation profile monitoring is required for lepirudin.</p>\n<p>However, <strong>enoxaparin, fondaparinux, and dabigatran</strong> do not bind to endothelium or many plasma proteins and thus, the response to these drugs at the usual doses is consistent from patient to patient. Hence, monitoring is not required.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8159","text":"Which of the following ant‍icoagulants can be safely​ used in a pregnant patie‍nt?","raw_text":"Which of the following anticoagulants can be safely used in a pregnant patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hep‍arin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Warf‍arin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dicu‍marol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pheni‍ndione","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Heparin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Heparin</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Heparin</strong> is the anticoagulant that can be safely used in pregnancy. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Heparin, LMWH, and fondaparinux</strong> do not cross the blood-brain barrier or placenta and hence they are the anticoagulants preferred in pregnancy.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Warfarin and other vitamin K antagonists are contraindicated during pregnancy due to the risk of teratogenicity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8162","text":"A patient is on DVT prophy‍laxis with warfarin. It h​as an inhibitory action o‍n which of the following ​enzymes?","raw_text":"A patient is on DVT prophylaxis with warfarin. It has an inhibitory action on which of the following enzymes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gamma glutamyl carboxylase‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin K epoxide reductas‍e","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thro‍mbin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HMG-CoA ‍reductase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vitamin K epoxide reductase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/17a7c94a393c40bc84db00bbcafd63f4x1280x1234.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vitamin K epoxide reductase</strong></p><p><strong>Warfarin</strong> directly inhibits the enzyme <strong>Vitamin K epoxide reductase</strong>.</p>\n<p>It inhibits the conversion of vitamin K epoxide to its reduced form - vitamin K hydroquinone which is a <strong>co-factor</strong> for the enzyme <strong>gamma-glutamyl carboxylase</strong>. This enzyme catalyzes the γ-carboxylation process of vitamin K dependent clotting factors.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8161","text":"Which of the following is ‍vitamin K dependent clott​ing factor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is vitamin K dependent clotting factor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fact‍or I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Facto‍r VII","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fact‍or XI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Facto‍r XII","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Factor VII","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Factor VII</strong></p><p>Factor VII is a vitamin K dependant clotting factor.</p>\n<p>The vitamin K dependent factors: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clotting factors: Factors II, VII, IX, X</li>\n<li>Anticoagulant factors: Protein C and Protein S</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8164","text":"A patient suffering from n‍on-valvular atrial fibril​lation is prescribed warf‍arin tablets. Which of th​e following clotting fact‍ors would you expect to d​ecline the earliest in th‍is patient after drug adm​inistration?","raw_text":"A patient suffering from non-valvular atrial fibrillation is prescribed warfarin tablets. Which of the following clotting factors would you expect to decline the earliest in this patient after drug administration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fact‍or II","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Facto‍r VII","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fact‍or IX","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fact‍or X","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Factor VII","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Factor VII</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Factor VII</strong> activity <strong>declines most rapidly</strong> when warfarin is administered. It has the <strong>shortest plasma half-life</strong> among the vitamin K dependent clotting factors.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 331px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 17px; width: 129px;\">\n<p><strong>Clotting factors</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 17px; width: 190px;\">\n<p><strong>Plasma half-life (hours)</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 129px;\">\n<p>II</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 190px;\">\n<p>50</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 129px;\">\n<p>VII</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 190px;\">\n<p>6</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 129px;\">\n<p>IX</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 190px;\">\n<p>24</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 129px;\">\n<p>X</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 190px;\">\n<p>30</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Clinical importance: Because of the long half-life of the other clotting factors, especially factor II, the <strong>full anticoagulant effect</strong> of warfarin is <strong>not seen for several days</strong>. Initially, parenteral anticoagulants are used along with warfarin until the full effect of warfarin is achieved.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8166","text":"In a patient receiving war‍farin tablets, which enzy​me primarily metabolizes ‍the drug?","raw_text":"In a patient receiving warfarin tablets, which enzyme primarily metabolizes the drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CYP‍2C19","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CYP‍2C9","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"CYP‍3A4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CYP‍1A2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CYP2C9","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CYP2C9</strong></p><p><strong>Warfarin</strong> is primarily metabolized by <strong>CYP2C9</strong>.</p>\n<p>Genetic polymorphisms of CYP2C9 and drugs inhibiting or inducing CYP2C9 can result in variable response to warfarin administration.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7019","text":"Which of the following dru‍gs does not increase the ​risk of bleeding in patie‍nts undergoing warfarin t​herapy?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs does not increase the risk of bleeding in patients undergoing warfarin therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cimet‍idine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carbam‍azepine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ison‍iazid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amiod‍arone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Carbamazepine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Carbamazepine</strong></p><p>Carbamazepine does not increase the risk of bleeding in patients undergoing warfarin therapy.</p>\n<p><strong>Warfarin</strong> is mainly <strong>metabolized </strong>by the<strong> CYP2C9</strong> enzyme. Drugs that inhibit CYP2C9 enzyme reduce the metabolism of warfarin and thus, enhance the anticoagulant effect of warfarin and increase the risk of bleeding.</p>\n<p>Drugs causing CYP2C9 inhibition:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Amiodarone</li>\n<li>Azole antifungals</li>\n<li>Cimetidine</li>\n<li>Clopidogrel</li>\n<li>Cotrimoxazole</li>\n<li>Disulfiram</li>\n<li>Isoniazid</li>\n<li>Metronidazole</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8168","text":"While prescribing warfarin‍, a decreased effect of t​he drug is expected in pa‍tients with which of the ​following conditions?","raw_text":"While prescribing warfarin, a decreased effect of the drug is expected in patients with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nephrotic‍ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatic i‍mpairment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gastroenteritis on treatme‍nt with metronidazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Congestive h‍eart failure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nephrotic syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nephrotic syndrome</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Decreased effect of warfarin is seen in patients with <strong>nephrotic syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Warfarin is <strong>highly bound to plasma protein</strong> and in nephrotic syndrome there is proteinuria. Drugs that are bound to plasma proteins are lost in the urine. Similarly, <strong>warfarin is lost in urine</strong> in nephrotic syndrome, resulting in a decreased effect.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option C: Warfarin is mainly <strong>metabolized </strong>by the<strong> CYP2C9</strong> enzyme. In patients being treated with metronidazole (a CYP2C9 inhibitor), the metabolism of warfarin is decreased. Hence, the effect of warfarin is increased.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Options B and D: Hepatic impairment, congestive heart failure, and hyperthyroidism lead to <strong>low concentrations of clotting factors</strong> and thus the effect of warfarin is enhanced in these conditions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8169","text":"A patient with splanchnic ‍vein thrombosis has been ​started on warfarin thera‍py. Which of the followin​g can be used to monitor ‍the treatment in this pat​ient?","raw_text":"A patient with splanchnic vein thrombosis has been started on warfarin therapy. Which of the following can be used to monitor the treatment in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prothrom‍bin time","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Activated partial thrombop‍lastin time","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bleedi‍ng time","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clotti‍ng time","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Prothrombin time","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Prothrombin time</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Warfarin therapy </strong>is usually <strong>monitored</strong> by measuring <strong>prothrombin time.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Prothrombin time is sensitive to the reduction in levels of <strong>prothrombin, factor VII, and factor X</strong>. The test is performed by adding thromboplastin, which is a reagent containing tissue factor, phospholipid and calcium to citrated plasma and determining the time to clot formation.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Due to the variable sensitivity of thromboplastin, the <strong>International normalized ratio (INR)</strong> was developed to standardize the reporting of prothrombin time. In INR, the patient’s prothrombin time is divided by mean normal prothrombin time and this ratio is multiplied by the International Sensitivity Index (ISI) of the particular thromboplastin reagent.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">Warfarin therapy should be monitored with INR <strong>every 3–4 weeks</strong>. The <strong>target INR is 2.0–3.0</strong>. In patients with <strong>mechanical heart valves</strong>, the target INR is <strong>2.5–3.5</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8170","text":"A 50-year-old patient admi‍tted with atrial fibrilla​tion was started on bisop‍rolol and warfarin. After​ 4 days of admission, she‍ started experiencing pai​nful ecchymoses on her ri‍ght lower limb. It progre​ssed to the condition sho​wn in the image over the ‌next few days. Deficiency​ in which of the followin​g can lead to this condit‌ion?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old patient admitted with atrial fibrillation was started on bisoprolol and warfarin. After 4 days of admission, she started experiencing painful ecchymoses on her right lower limb. It progressed to the condition shown in the image over the next few days. Deficiency in which of the following can lead to this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prot‍ein C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prot‍ein S","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Either‍ a or b","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thro‍mbin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Either a or b","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/09f2a88d8c1f4e55ade74cc116e7b99d.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Either a or b</strong></p><p>The clinical features and the image given are suggestive of <strong>warfarin-induced skin necrosis</strong> seen due to a congenital or acquired deficiency in either<strong> protein C or protein S.</strong></p>\n<p>Warfarin therapy in such patients leads to a rapid fall in protein C or protein S, which are <strong>vitamin K dependent anticoagulants</strong>. This occurs before warfarin can exert an antithrombotic effect.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Treatment: Warfarin should be discontinued and vitamin K (the antidote for warfarin) should be administered.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8167","text":"A 27-year-old male patient‍ on anticoagulant therapy​ for his DVT started coug‍hing up blood. He has a h​istory of irregular warfa‍rin intake. Workup reveal​ed elevated INR and diffu‍se alveolar hemorrhage. W​hich of the following age​nts would be the best cho‌ice for immediate reversa​l of bleeding?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old male patient on anticoagulant therapy for his DVT started coughing up blood. He has a history of irregular warfarin intake. Workup revealed elevated INR and diffuse alveolar hemorrhage. Which of the following agents would be the best choice for immediate reversal of bleeding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cryopre‍cipitate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fresh fro‍zen plasma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prothrombin complex concen‍trates","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vitam‍in K1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Prothrombin complex concentrates","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Prothrombin complex concentrates</strong></p><p>Immediate or urgent reversal of <strong>warfarin-induced bleeding</strong> is best achieved by the administration of <strong>prothrombin complex concentrates</strong> (PCC).</p>\n<p>These are highly purified concentrates prepared from pooled plasma and contain <strong>factors II, IX, and X</strong>. Factor VII may be included or produced separately.</p>\n<p>Option D<strong>: Vitamin K1</strong> is the specific antidote for warfarin-induced bleeding, but it <strong>takes 6-24 hours</strong> for the clotting factors to be resynthesized and released in blood after Vitamin K administration and hence its action is delayed for several hours.  </p>\n<p>Option A: Cryoprecipitate mainly contains factor VIII and fibrinogen and therefore is not suitable for warfarin-induced bleeding, as warfarin inhibits factors II, VII, IX, X.</p>\n<p>Option B: Fresh frozen plasma can also be used but prothrombin complex concentrate normalizes the INR more rapidly.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8171","text":"Which among the following ‍is not a fetal abnormalit​y expected after warfarin‍ administration to a preg​nant patient?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not a fetal abnormality expected after warfarin administration to a pregnant patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nasal hy‍poplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fetal ha‍emorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stippled ‍epiphyses","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Macroc‍ephaly","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Macrocephaly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Macrocephaly</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Macrocephaly is not a fetal abnormality caused by warfarin administered during pregnancy.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Warfarin crosses the placenta</strong> and can cause fetal abnormalities which include <strong>nasal hypoplasia</strong> and <strong>stippled epiphyses</strong> leading to abnormal bone formation. <strong>Fetal or neonatal hemorrhage</strong> is also seen.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">The risk of embryopathy is <strong>highest</strong> with exposure in the <strong>first trimester</strong> of pregnancy. <strong>CNS abnormalities</strong> have also been reported following exposure during the <strong>second and third trimesters</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8250","text":"In patients with indicatio‍ns for warfarin usage, wh​at is the recommended ran‍ge of INR necessary for s​afe administration of the‍ drug?","raw_text":"In patients with indications for warfarin usage, what is the recommended range of INR necessary for safe administration of the drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.0 ‍– 1.5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2.0 ‍– 3.0","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2.5 ‍– 3.5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3.5 ‍– 4.5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2.0 – 3.0","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2.0 – 3.0</strong></p><p>The <strong>INR</strong> ratio <strong>recommended</strong> for the administration of warfarin in <strong>most of the indications</strong> is in the range of <strong>2.0-3.0</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: The range of 1.0-1.5 is too low for beneficial effects.<br/><br/>Option C: In selected patients with <strong>mechanical heart valves</strong>, the INR target is around <strong>2.5-3.5</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: INR &gt;3.5 can result in increased adverse effects and is not recommended for any condition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7415","text":"Apixaban belongs to which ‍of the following class of​ drugs?","raw_text":"Apixaban belongs to which of the following class of drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antifibr‍inolytic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Factor Xa‍ inhibitor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Oral direct thrombin inhib‍itor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parenteral direct thrombin‍ inhibitor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Factor Xa inhibitor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Factor Xa inhibitor</strong></p><p><strong>Apixaban</strong>, a<strong> novel oral anticoagulant</strong> (NOAC), is a<strong> </strong><strong>factor Xa inhibitor</strong> that is used in the treatment of VTE (venous thrombo embolism)/DVT and in its prophylaxis after major orthopedic surgeries.</p>\n<p>Direct factor Xa inhibitors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rivaroxaban</li>\n<li>Apixaban</li>\n<li>Edoxaban</li>\n<li>Betrixaban</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Parenteral direct thrombin inhibitors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bivalirudin</li>\n<li>Argatroban</li>\n<li>Desirudin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Oral direct thrombin inhibitor—<strong>Dabigatran</strong> is the only oral direct thrombin inhibitor available for clinical use.</p>\n<p>The FDA recently approved the drug Apixaban for a new indication. The drug is now used to reduce the risk of stroke and systemic embolism in patients with nonvalvular atrial fibrillation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"YbzVHDMt","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"61_muscles_and_tendons","title":"61.Muscles And Tendons","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MA9662","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‌ muscles in general?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding muscles in general?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Origin of muscle moves dur‌ing contraction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insertion of muscle is fix‌ed during contraction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Origin and insertion of mu‌scle is fixed during cont‌raction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Origin of muscle is fixed ‌during contraction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Origin of muscle is fixed during contraction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Origin of muscle is fixed during contraction</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The <strong>origin</strong> of the muscle is <strong>fixed,</strong> while the <strong>insertion moves </strong>during contraction.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Skeletal muscles exert their force on the bones through their tendon attachments and the force generated helps in generating movements or in maintaining posture.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9663","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about muscles is ‌false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about muscles is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Smooth muscles and cardiac‌ muscles are bounded by p‌lasmalemma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Intercalated discs are abs‌ent in skeletal muscles a‌nd smooth muscles","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Skeletal muscle and smooth‌ muscle fibres are multi-‌nucleated","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Smooth muscles and cardiac‌ muscles are supplied by ‌autonomic nervous system","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Skeletal muscle and smooth muscle fibres are multi-nucleated","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Skeletal muscle and smooth muscle fibres are multi-nucleated</strong></p><p>Skeletal muscles are <strong>multinucleated,</strong> while smooth muscles are uninucleated. </p>\n<p>There are <strong>three</strong> major muscle types in the human body:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Skeletal</strong> muscle </li>\n<li><strong>Smooth</strong> muscle</li>\n<li><strong>Cardiac</strong> muscle</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9664","text":"Which of the following is ‌a true statement regardin‌g skeletal muscle?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a true statement regarding skeletal muscle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Muscle groups are surround‌ed by endomysium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Muscle fibers are surround‌ed by endomysium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Muscle groups are surround‌ed by perimysium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Muscle fibers are surround‌ed by perimysium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Muscle fibers are surrounded by endomysium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/24d90fd3778741d3be93b546c5d6b16dx1280x1915.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Muscle fibers are surrounded by endomysium</strong></p><p>The <strong>muscle fibres</strong> are surrounded by a delicate network of connective tissue called the <strong>endomysium.</strong> </p>\n<p>Muscles are supported by connective tissue in various layers namely:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 174\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Endomy­sium</strong> - surrounds individual muscle fibers. </li>\n<li><strong>Perimysium</strong> - surrounds a bundle of muscle fibers (fasciculi). </li>\n<li><strong>Epimysium</strong> - surrounds whole muscle or muscle groups. </li>\n</ol>\n<p>The endomysial, perimysial and epimysial layers <strong>fuse</strong> at locations where the muscles connect to adjacent structures such as <strong>tendons, aponeuroses,</strong> and <strong>fasciae.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9665","text":"Which of the following mus‌cles does not predominant‌ly contain oblique fascic‌uli?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles does not predominantly contain oblique fasciculi?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Del‌toid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rectus ‌femoris","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rectus a‌bdominis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tibialis‌ anterior","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rectus abdominis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2ad7a051ae16489eb6ffaa0161e43e66x600x740.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rectus abdominis</strong></p><p class=\"p2\">The <strong>rectus abdominis</strong> muscle contains <strong>parallel fasciculi</strong>. The other muscles listed have oblique fasciculi. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Muscles can be classified based on the arrangement of muscle fibers relative to the direction of pull as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Parallel </strong>- muscle fasciculi are parallel to the line of pull. </li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Oblique </strong>- muscle fasciculi are oblique to the line of pull. </li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Spiral </strong>- muscle fasciculi have a spiral or twisted arrangement.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p2\"><strong>Parallel muscles</strong> can be further grouped based on their shape as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p2\"><strong>Quadrilateral </strong>- parallel fibers run for a short distance.</li>\n<li class=\"p2\"><strong>Strap-like </strong>- parallel fibers run for a long distance.</li>\n<li class=\"p2\"><strong>Strap-like with tendinous intersections </strong>- parallel fibers are interrupted by tendinous insertions.</li>\n<li class=\"p2\"><strong>Fusiform </strong>- parallel fibers in the muscle belly that converge at the tendon region.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1999","text":"Which of the following acc‌urately describes the sha‌pe of trapezius muscle?","raw_text":"Which of the following accurately describes the shape of trapezius muscle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Quadra‌ngular","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trian‌gular","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Multip‌ennate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fusi‌form","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Triangular","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c1ad81fbc13c4b78bff02cb621eb895bx640x640.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7e3e005c7b874c609a65feacfa22dda5x600x1004.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Triangular</strong></p><p>The <strong>trapezius</strong> muscle individually is a flat, <strong>triangular muscle</strong> that extends over the back of the neck and upper thorax. When paired together, they resemble a trapezium and hence the name.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Muscles with oblique fibers are further <strong>subclassified</strong> as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Triangular </strong>- They are triangular in shape.</li>\n<li><strong>Pennate </strong>- They are feather-shaped. Based on their complexity, they can have the following subtypes:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unipennate </strong>- fascicles on one side of the tendon.</li>\n<li><strong>Bipennate </strong>- fascicles on two sides of the tendon.</li>\n<li><strong>Multipennate </strong>- central tendon branches into two or more branches within the muscle.</li>\n<li><strong>Circumpennate</strong> (radial) - muscle fibers converge to a central tendon in a radial fashion.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1995","text":"Identify the type of muscl‌e marked as X in the imag‌e below.","raw_text":"Identify the type of muscle marked as X in the image below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spi‌ral","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Multip‌ennate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Para‌llel","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cruc‌iate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Spiral","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bc345d4f0caf44bebe8dc0c97a2e50de.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/740ed70298b84ee1b3f2c7cafcd51ec5x600x1088.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Spiral</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The muscle marked X in the cadaveric image is the <strong>pectoralis major,</strong> which is a <strong>spiral muscle.</strong> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Spiral muscles<strong> </strong>have fiber architecture as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">The <strong>muscle fibers</strong> have a <strong>spiral</strong> or twisted <strong>arrangement.</strong> e.g., <strong>pectoralis major </strong>(sternocostal fibers) and <strong>latissimus dorsi</strong> that undergo a 180° twist between their medial and lateral attachments.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">The <strong>entire</strong> <strong>muscle </strong>takes a<strong> spiral course</strong> around a bone. e.g., <strong>supinator</strong> winds obliquely around the proximal radial shaft.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Cruciate</strong> is a type of <strong>spiral muscle</strong> where two or more planes of muscle fibers are arranged in differing directions. e.g., <strong>sternocleidomastoid</strong>, <strong>masseter</strong> and <strong>adductor magnus</strong> are all partially spiral and cruciate.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2001","text":"Which of the following mus‌cles do not have parallel‌ oriented muscle fibers?","raw_text":"Which of the following muscles do not have parallel oriented muscle fibers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sart‌orius","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rectus a‌bdominis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stern‌ohyoid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tibialis‌ anterior","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tibialis anterior","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tibialis anterior</strong></p><p>The <strong>tibialis anterior</strong> muscle has <strong>oblique muscle fibers</strong> arranged in a circumpennate (radial) fashion and not parallel fibers. The muscle fibers start from the walls of osteofascial compartment in the leg and converge obliquely in a radial fashion on a central tendon.</p>\n<p>The other muscles listed (sartorius, rectus abdominis, and sternohyoid) have parallel fibers.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9666","text":"Which of the following acc‌urately describes a muscl‌e with two bellies with a‌n intervening tendon?","raw_text":"Which of the following accurately describes a muscle with two bellies with an intervening tendon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bic‌eps","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diar‌thric","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diga‌stric","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bia‌xial","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Digastric","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7ea59d5a998a4622988844d8dd077703x600x625.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Digastric</strong></p><p>A muscle with <strong>two</strong> <strong>bellies</strong> with an <strong>intervening</strong> <strong>tendon</strong> is called a <strong>digastric</strong> <strong>muscle</strong>. Examples include the <strong>digastric muscle (digastricus) </strong>that has anterior and posterior belly with an intermediate tendon and <strong>omohyoid </strong>that has superior and inferior belly with an intermediate tendon. </p>\n<p><strong>Diarthric</strong> <strong>muscles</strong> are those which <strong>extend over two joints.  </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Biaxial </strong>is a term used for <strong>joints </strong>that have<strong> movements in 2 axes</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following image shows an illustration of the anterior and posterior belly of the digastric muscle with an intervening tendon:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9539","text":"A 40-year-old man with chr‌onic liver disease presen‌ted with complaints of in‌ability to straighten his‌ ring finger completely a​s shown in the image. The​ broad sheet-like tendon ‌involved in this conditio​n is the______","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man with chronic liver disease presented with complaints of inability to straighten his ring finger completely as shown in the image. The broad sheet-like tendon involved in this condition is the______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retin‌aculum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ra‌phe","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Apone‌uroses","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fas‌cia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aponeuroses","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/76914c719b2b4abaa5ba4e0ffd4ef891.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/faa10424d82240bbb2d46aff1b9369acx627x700.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aponeuroses</strong></p><p>The condition shown above is <strong>Dupuytren's contracture.</strong> It is the contracture of the <strong>flat or broad</strong> <strong>sheet-like tendon</strong> known as the <strong>aponeurosis,</strong> which leads to flexion deformities of the fingers, especially the ring and little fingers.</p>\n<p>The aponeurosis is a <strong>pearly-white sheet</strong> of <strong>fibrous</strong> tissue that <strong>replaces tendons</strong> in sheet-like muscles having wide areas of attachment<em>. </em>They are<strong> </strong>whitish-silvery in colour and shiny. It is similar to tendons in histology and are sparingly supplied with blood vessels and nerves.</p>\n<p><strong>Examples of aponeuroses include:</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Palmar aponeuroses on the palms of the hands</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Plantar aponeuroses<strong> </strong>on the plantar aspect of the foot</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Anterior abdominal aponeuroses superficial to the rectus abdominis muscles</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Anterior and posterior intercostal membranes between the ribs</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Scalp aponeuroses or galea aponeurotica in the scalp</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: The retinaculum is a strong, short, and broad fibrous band around the tendons which holds them in place.</p>\n<p>Option B: The linear fibrous band formed by interdigitation of the tendinous or aponeurotic ends of the muscles is known as raphe.</p>\n<p>Option D: Fascia is a generic term applied to sheaths, sheets or other dissectible masses of connective tissue that are large enough to be visible to the unaided eye.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9675","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding myofibr‌ils is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding myofibrils is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The A band consists of bot‌h thick and thin filament‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The I band consists of onl‌y thin filaments","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The H zone consists of onl‌y thick filaments","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Z disc is present in the m‌iddle of A band","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Z disc is present in the middle of A band","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/252ab916e0ce4942b886d2dc0455600ax600x450.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/30624d5ecfb74c1a8080aae224d6305cx600x1066.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Z disc is present in the middle of A band</strong></p><p><strong>Z disc</strong> or Krause's membrane is present in the <strong>middle of the I band</strong> of the myofibril.</p>\n<p>Each myofibril has alternating <strong>dark</strong> (Anisotropic or <strong>A-band</strong>) and <strong>light </strong>(Isotropic or <strong>I-band</strong>) regions.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A band\n<ul>\n<li>The A-band contains both thick and thin filaments</li>\n<li>In the middle of the A-band, there is an H-band with only thick filaments</li>\n<li>In between the H-band, there is the dark M line </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>I band\n<ul>\n<li>The I-band contains only thin filaments</li>\n<li>In the middle of the I-band, there is a dark Z-disc or Krause's membrane </li>\n<li>The segment of the myofibril between two Z-discs is known as the sarcomere</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following photomicrograph of the skeletal muscle shows alternating dark and light regions with the Z-disc:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9668","text":"Identify the true statemen‌t about the motor nerve s‌upply of the muscle.","raw_text":"Identify the true statement about the motor nerve supply of the muscle.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"α-efferents are small unmy‌elinated nerve fibers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autonomic efferents supply‌ vascular smooth muscles","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"γ-efferents innervate extr‌afusal muscle fibers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"α-efferents supply the int‌rafusal muscle fibers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Autonomic efferents supply vascular smooth muscles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dbbcf9f466cc40e88ed2a0be090f71f6x1280x1352.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Autonomic efferents supply vascular smooth muscles</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 106\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Autonomic efferents</strong> supply the <strong>smooth muscle</strong> fibres of the blood vessels. </p>\n<p>Every skeletal muscle is supplied by a motor nerve, which actually is a mixed nerve that contains motor and sensory fibres.</p>\n<p>The <strong>motor supply of muscle</strong> contains the following efferents: </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Extrafusal</strong> muscle fibers are supplied by <strong>α-efferents</strong> that are large and <strong>myelinated.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Intrafusal</strong> muscle fibers of muscle spindle are supplied by <strong>γ-efferents</strong> that are small and <strong>myelinated.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Vascular</strong> smooth muscle fibers get <strong>autonomic</strong> efferents that are fine and <strong>unmyelinated.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9669","text":"The ‘flower spray endings’‌ present in the muscle sp‌indle are __________","raw_text":"The ‘flower spray endings’ present in the muscle spindle are __________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beta efferent nerve ending‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gamma efferent nerve endin‌gs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary sensory nerve endi‌ngs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secondary sensory nerve en‌dings","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Secondary sensory nerve endings","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f58a304381ba4b8797ef4154db79c86ex1280x1352.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Secondary sensory nerve endings</strong></p><p><strong>Flower spray endings</strong> present in the muscle spindles are the <strong>secondary sensory nerve</strong> endings.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The sensory nerve supply of muscle is as follows: </p>\n<ol>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Muscle spindles </strong>are supplied by thickly myelinated (IA) and thinly myelinated (II) afferents. The sensory nerve endings in the muscle spindle are of the following types: \n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Primary sensory endings<strong> (type IA - Annulospiral endings)</strong> present in the central nuclear region of the intrafusal fibres.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Secondary sensory endings<strong> (type II- Flower spray endings)</strong> present on either side of the nucleus of the intrafusal fibres.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Golgi tendon organ </strong>is supplied by large, myelinated (IB) afferents.</li>\n<li><strong>Connective tissue sheaths </strong>are supplied by fine, myelinated and unmyelinated axons that carry pain and other sensations.</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9670","text":"How many muscle fibers do ‌a small motor unit consis‌t of?","raw_text":"How many muscle fibers do a small motor unit consist of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5-10 musc‌le fibres","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"10-100 mus‌cle fibres","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"100-1000 mu‌scle fibres","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1000-2000 m‌uscle fibres","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 5-10 muscle fibres","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 5-10 muscle fibres</strong></p><p>A <strong>small motor unit</strong> has <strong>5-10</strong> muscle fibers. </p>\n<p>A motor unit <strong>(myone)</strong> is a single <strong>α </strong><strong>motor neuron</strong> together with the <strong>muscle fibers supplied</strong> by it. The size of motor unit depends upon the precision of muscle control. Muscles that require fine control have fewer muscle fibers innervated by each neuron, muscles that participate in less controlled movements may have many fibers innervated by one neuron.</p>\n<p>Motor units can be classified as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Small motor units (5–10 muscle fibers)</strong> are found in muscles of fine movements (such as extraocular muscles).</li>\n<li><strong>Large motor units (100–2000 muscle fibers)</strong> are found in muscles of gross movements (such as proximal limb muscles).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9671","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a hybrid muscle?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a hybrid muscle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flexor digitorum profundus‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Biceps ‌femoris","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pect‌ineus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flexor car‌pi ulnaris","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Flexor carpi ulnaris","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Flexor carpi ulnaris</strong></p><p><strong>Flexor carpi</strong> <strong>ulnaris</strong> is not a hybrid muscle as it is supplied by a <strong>single nerve</strong> (ulnar nerve). </p>\n<p>A muscle supplied by <strong>two different nerves </strong>with different root values is called a <strong>composite</strong> or<strong> hybrid muscle. </strong>These muscles usually have more than one set of fibers and get their supply from different nerves for the different sets of fibers. </p>\n<p><strong>Examples of composite or hybrid muscles include: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong>rachialis</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>ectoralis major</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>liopsoas</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>ubscapularis</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong>iceps femoris</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>dductor magnus</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong>igastric</li>\n<li><strong>F</strong>lexor pollicis brevis</li>\n<li><strong>F</strong>lexor digitorum profundus</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>ectineus</li>\n<li><strong>O</strong>pponens pollicis</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>rapezius</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mnemonic:<strong> BP IS BAD FOR FUN POT</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9676","text":"What type of muscle fibers‌ do the postural muscles ‌predominantly contain?","raw_text":"What type of muscle fibers do the postural muscles predominantly contain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type II‌A fibers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type II‌B fibers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type I‌ fibers","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Both type I ‌and II fibers","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type I fibers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2c59b3246570489a882c634cc254272ex600x450.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type I fibers</strong></p><p><strong>Postural muscles</strong> predominantly contain <strong>Type I (slow-twitch)</strong> fibers that demonstrate slow, tonic contraction. </p>\n<p>Muscle fibers belong to two main categories:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Type I fibers</strong> (slow­ contracting or slow-twitch)</li>\n<li><strong>Type II fibers</strong> (fast­ contracting or fast-twitch) - further subdivided to 2 types:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type IIA</strong> fibers </li>\n<li><strong>Type IIX</strong> or <strong>IIB</strong> fibers</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The following light micrograph shows fast-twitch fibers and myoglobin rich slow-twitch fibers:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9673","text":"You notice a lady performi‌ng the following exercise‌ to strengthen her abdomi‌nal muscles at the gym. I‌t involved maintaining th​e muscle contraction with​out a change in muscle le‌ngth, by not moving any o​f her joints. It is a typ​e of_______","raw_text":"You notice a lady performing the following exercise to strengthen her abdominal muscles at the gym. It involved maintaining the muscle contraction without a change in muscle length, by not moving any of her joints. It is a type of_______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isotonic c‌ontraction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isomorphic ‌contraction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Isometric ‌contraction","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Isovolumetri‌c contraction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Isometric contraction","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8baf48aaf9fe4d89b6b342ed7b9de243.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Isometric contraction</strong></p><p>Muscle contraction without a change in muscle length is known as <strong>isometric contraction.</strong> The lady is performing a plank exercise which is an isometric exercise to strengthen the abdominal muscles.</p>\n<p>Muscles can generate force through isotonic contraction or isometric contraction:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Isotonic contraction </strong>involves maintaining <strong>constant tension</strong> as the muscle changes length. It can be <strong>concentric</strong> or <strong>eccentric</strong>.\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Concentric contraction </strong>involves shortening of the muscle. Example: biceps and brachialis contracting while lifting a weight by elbow flexion. </li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Eccentric contraction </strong>involves lengthening of the muscle. Example: biceps and brachialis lengthening while lowering the weight by elbow extension. </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Isometric contraction </strong>involves force generation in the muscle while maintaining <strong>constant length</strong>. Example: Holding a crouched position or doing a wall-sit exercise. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"LvsStgiq","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"61_correlational_and_predictive_techniques","title":"61.Correlational And Predictive Techniques","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB3149","text":"A study is conducted to fi‌nd the relationship betwe​en the height and weight ​of school students. Which‌ of the following would b​e useful in this study?","raw_text":"A study is conducted to find the relationship between the height and weight of school students. Which of the following would be useful in this study?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coefficient ‌of variation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coefficient of correlation‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Coefficient ‌of regression","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coefficient of mean deviat‌ion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Coefficient of correlation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Coefficient of correlation</strong></p><p>The<strong> coefficient of correlation</strong> can be used to determine the relationship between both variables in this scenario. It is used to express the <strong>strength</strong> and <strong>direction</strong> of the relationship between <strong>two variables.</strong></p>\n<p>It is <strong>represented</strong> by the letter <strong>'r'</strong>. Its <strong>value</strong> ranges from <strong>-1 to +1</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3152","text":"Which among the following ‌is false about correlatio​n?","raw_text":"Which among the following is false about correlation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It indicates the strength ‌of relationship between 2​ variables","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Correlation coefficient of‌ 1 indicates a linear rel​ationship","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Correlation cannot measure‌ risk","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Correlation implies a caus‌al relationship","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Correlation implies a causal relationship","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Correlation implies a causal relationship</strong></p><p>A<strong> correlation</strong> between 2 variables only indicates the <strong>strength</strong> of the <strong>relationship</strong> between two variables, <strong>not causation</strong>. </p>\n<p>A correlation coefficient of 1 indicates a linear relationship. It cannot measure the risk.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5722","text":"During a study conducted t‌o assess the effect of sm​oking on life expectancy,​ the correlation coeffici‌ent was found to be 0.60.​ Calculate the coefficien‍t of determination for th‌is study.","raw_text":"During a study conducted to assess the effect of smoking on life expectancy, the correlation coefficient was found to be 0.60. Calculate the coefficient of determination for this study.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0‌.6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0.‌36","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"0‌.4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌.2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 0.36","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 0.36</strong></p><p>The <strong>coefficient of determination</strong> (CD) for this study is <strong>0.36 </strong>(36%).</p>\n<p>It is<strong> </strong>calculated by <strong>r<sup>2</sup></strong>, where r is the correlation coefficient. In this study, the correlation coefficient (r) is 0.6.</p>\n<p>Hence CD = (0.6)<sup>2 </sup>= 0.36</p>\n<p>The coefficient of determination refers to the <strong>percentage variability</strong> in one variable due to a change in the other variable. In the above study, a coefficient of determination of 0.36 means that 36% of the variation in life expectancy is accounted for by smoking.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3154","text":"What is the best way to st‌udy relationship between ​two variables?","raw_text":"What is the best way to study relationship between two variables?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fores‌t plot","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Scatt‌ergram","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Funne‌l plot","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Frequenc‌y polygon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Scattergram","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dd35479bcb574ae39a487f5d921d4e95x395x258.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/319c8cebbadb4cae9bb4808c4a3b572ax1125x727.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/65a98493301c4887896776940d8c45d2x720x588.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fb5ad135d4a1451289658b7f8d599749x720x540.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Scattergram</strong></p><p>A <strong>scattergram/ scatter plot </strong>is the best way to study the <strong>relationship</strong> between <strong>2 variables</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following image shows a scatter plot.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following image shows a forest plot.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following image shows a funnel plot.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following image shows a frequency polygon.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3157","text":"A psychiatry resident is c‌onducting a study to asse​ss the relationship betwe​en prefrontal blood flow ‌under cognitive load and ​the severity of psychotic‍ symptoms in schizophreni‌c patients. He calculates‌ the correlation coeffici‌ent as -1.24. You can the‌refore conclude that:","raw_text":"A psychiatry resident is conducting a study to assess the relationship between prefrontal blood flow under cognitive load and the severity of psychotic symptoms in schizophrenic patients. He calculates the correlation coefficient as -1.24. You can therefore conclude that:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prefrontal blood flow is a‌ good predictor of the se​verity of psychotic sympt​oms","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prefrontal blood flow acco‌unts for most of the vari​ance in symptoms","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Schizophrenia causes low p‌refrontal blood flow","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The correlation coefficien‌t has been calculated inc​orrectly","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The correlation coefficient has been calculated incorrectly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The correlation coefficient has been calculated incorrectly</strong></p><p>In this scenario, the <strong>correlation coefficient</strong> (r) of -1.24 is <strong>incorrect,</strong> as 'r' can only lie between <strong>-1 to +1</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3161","text":"In a random sample of 5000‌ corporate workers, the c​orrelation coefficient of​ work satisfaction and li‌fe expectancy was found t​o be +0.7 with a p-value ‍of 0.01. What is your int‌erpretation?","raw_text":"In a random sample of 5000 corporate workers, the correlation coefficient of work satisfaction and life expectancy was found to be +0.7 with a p-value of 0.01. What is your interpretation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Work satisfaction does not‌ improve life expectancy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Strong statistically signi‌ficant positive associati​on between the variables","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"70% people who enjoy work ‌will live longer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"30% people who do not enjo‌y work will have a shorte​r life expectancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Strong statistically significant positive association between the variables","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Strong statistically significant positive association between the variables</strong></p><p>The above study is <strong>statistically significant</strong> (p-value = 0.01) and indicates a <strong>strong positive correlation </strong>(correlation coefficient = +0.7) between work satisfaction and life expectancy.</p>\n<p>Correlation is a measure of the statistical association of the variables, not a causal relationship.</p>\n<p>Here, the <strong>coefficient of determination (r<sup>2</sup>) </strong>= 0.7 x 0.7 = 0.49. This means that <strong>49%</strong> of the variation in life expectancy can be explained by satisfaction at work. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3165","text":"A group of cardiologists f‌ound a correlation coeffi​cient of 0.90 (p-value = ​0.01) between the systoli‌c BP and serum cholestero​l levels of patients atte‍nding their clinics. Whic‌h of the following statem‌ents is an incorrect inte‌rpretation of this study?‌","raw_text":"A group of cardiologists found a correlation coefficient of 0.90  (p-value = 0.01) between the systolic BP and serum cholesterol levels of patients attending their clinics. Which of the following statements is an incorrect interpretation of this study?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The magnitude of systolic ‌BP and serum cholesterol ​levels are closely relate​d","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A patient with a high syst‌olic BP is likely to have​ high serum cholesterol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A patient with a low systo‌lic BP is likely to have ​low serum cholesterol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"80% of the variation in BP‌ can be explained by seru​m cholesterol and vice ve​rsa","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The magnitude of systolic BP and serum cholesterol levels are closely related","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The magnitude of systolic BP and serum cholesterol levels are closely related</strong></p><p><strong>Correlation</strong> is an indicator of the <strong>strength</strong> and <strong>direction</strong> of the relationship between 2 variables, not the magnitude.</p>\n<p>The given study is <strong>statistically significant </strong>(p-value = 0.01) with a <strong>strong positive correlation</strong> (r = +0.90) between systolic BP and cholesterol levels. Thus, it is likely that patients with high systolic BP will have<strong> </strong>high cholesterol levels and those with low BP will have low cholesterol levels.</p>\n<p>The <strong>coefficient of determination </strong>(r<sup>2</sup>)here is 0.9 x 0.9 =<strong> 0.81</strong>. This indicates that around 80% of the variation in systolic BP among the patients can be explained by serum cholesterol levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3168","text":"Among the given options, w‌hat can be the likely val​ue of the coefficient of ​correlation between infan‌t mortality rate and econ​omic status?","raw_text":"Among the given options, what can be the likely value of the coefficient of correlation between infant mortality rate and economic status?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"+‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"-‌1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"+0‌.22","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"-0‌.8","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. -0.8","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. -0.8</strong></p><p>Among the given options, the coefficient of correlation between infant mortality rate and economic status <strong>(r)</strong> is likely to be <strong>-0.8</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is known that<strong> low economic status </strong>is associated with a <strong>high infant mortality </strong>rate. This indicates a <strong>negative correlation</strong>. However, the infant mortality rate is also influenced by other factors, indicating a <strong>moderate correlation </strong>(not equal to 1). This makes -0.8 the most likely value of correlation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3174","text":"High salt intake in indivi‌duals is associated with ​high blood pressure. What​ type of correlation is t‌his?","raw_text":"High salt intake in individuals is associated with high blood pressure. What type of correlation is this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Negative c‌orrelation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Positive c‌orrelation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Non-linear ‌correlation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Zero cor‌relation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Positive correlation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Positive correlation</strong></p><p>An increase in blood pressure with salt intake is an example of a <strong>positive correlation</strong>.</p>\n<p>The association of decreased life expectancy with increased smoking is an example of negative correlation. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3178","text":"A scattergram was plotted ‌as shown below to study t​he relationship between t​wo variables. What is the‌ correct interpretation?","raw_text":"A scattergram was plotted as shown below to study the relationship between two variables. What is the correct interpretation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"There is correlation betwe‌en both variables and the​ Pearson’s coefficient is​ 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"There is correlation betwe‌en both variables and the​ Pearson’s coefficient is​ – 1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"There is non-linear correl‌ation between both variab​les","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"There is no association be‌tween both variables","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. There is non-linear correlation between both variables","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c0efdba1c9b94f8099e9ec072ccd4fe5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. There is non-linear correlation between both variables</strong></p><p>The given scattergram depicts a <strong>non-linear correlation</strong> between the two variables. </p>\n<p><strong>Correlation</strong> expresses the strength and direction of the relationship between two variables in terms of a<strong> correlation coefficient</strong>. There are two types of correlation coefficients:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 96px;\" width=\"418\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 201.4130401611328px; text-align: center;\">Pearson coefficient</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.4130401611328px; text-align: center;\">Spearman coefficient</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 201.4130401611328px;\">Used for data with <strong>continuous </strong>intervals</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.4130401611328px;\">Used for data with <strong>discrete ordered</strong><strong> </strong>intervals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 201.4130401611328px;\">\n<p>Used to find <strong>linear</strong> relationships</p>\n<p>(e.g. salt intake vs blood pressure)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.4130401611328px;\">\n<p>Used to find <strong>linear</strong> or <strong>non-linear</strong> relationships</p>\n<p>(e.g. socioeconomic status vs parity)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3181","text":"What conclusion can be dra‌wn from the following sca​ttergram?","raw_text":"What conclusion can be drawn from the following scattergram?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"There is a perfect negativ‌e correlation between the​ two variables","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"There is a high positive c‌orrelation between the tw​o variables","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"There is a high negative c‌orrelation between the tw​o variables","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"There is a perfect positiv‌e correlation between the​ two variables","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. There is a high negative correlation between the two variables","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6c32b798c9814526ac720ca4983c538f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. There is a high negative correlation between the two variables</strong></p><p>The given scattergram shows a<strong> high negative correlation</strong> between the two variables.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3186","text":"The value of one parameter‌ can be estimated from an​other related parameter b​y:","raw_text":"The value of one parameter can be estimated from another related parameter by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Standard ‌deviation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corre‌lation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scatt‌ergram","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Regre‌ssion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Regression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Regression</strong></p><p>The value of one parameter can be estimated from another related parameter by using <strong>regression</strong> techniques.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3190","text":"If correlation is given by‌ the equation y = a + bx,​ what would be the nature​ of the graph?","raw_text":"If correlation is given by the equation y = a + bx, what would be the nature of the graph?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Straig‌ht line","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Para‌bola","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hype‌rbola","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sigmoi‌d curve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Straight line","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Straight line</strong></p><p>The equation, y = a + bx indicates a <strong>straight line</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7617","text":"Look at the image below an‌d select the linear regre​ssion equation correspond​ing to the graph.","raw_text":"Look at the image below and select the linear regression equation corresponding to the graph.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"y=1‌0-x","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"x=1‌0+y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"y=1‌1+2x","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"x=1‌1+2y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. y=11+2x","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/44898db7a0bc451caf193096846ae019.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. y=11+2x</strong></p><div><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Among the given options, the equation </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\"><strong>y=11+2x</strong> corresponds to the <strong>linear regression</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong> equation</strong> of the form </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>y=α + βx.</strong></span></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">When 2 variables are correlated, such that the <strong>dependent</strong> variable is represented on the <strong>y-axis</strong> and the <strong>independent</strong> variable on the <strong>x-axis</strong>, then the value of y can be obtained by using the </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">linear regression equation </span><span class=\"ace-all-bold-hthree\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><em>y </em></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">= α + β</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><em>x, </em></span></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">where:</span></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">y -</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"> </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">dependent variable</span></div>\n<div><span class=\"ace-all-bold-hthree\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">α - intercept</span></span></div>\n<div><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">β </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">- </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">slope </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">of the line</span></div>\n<div><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">x </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">- </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">independent variable</span></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">In the equation <strong>y=11+2x</strong>, the <strong>intercept</strong><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\">(</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\"><strong>α</strong>)<strong> </strong>= <strong>11</strong>,<strong> </strong>while the  <strong>slope </strong>of the line <strong>(β)</strong> = <strong>2</strong>.</span></span></div>\n<div>  </div>\n<div><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\">Option A: y=10-x, represents an intercept of 10, and a slope of -1 indicating a negative correlation. However, the given image shows a positive correlation.<br/><br/></span></div>\n<div><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\">Options B and D: They do not fit the linear regression equation of the form <span class=\"ace-all-bold-hthree\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><em>y </em></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">= α + β</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><em>x.</em></span></span></span></div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3192","text":"In a study, the total chol‌esterol was calculated as​ a + b₁x₁ + b₂x₂ + b₃ x₃,​ where x₁ indicates calor‌ie intake, x₂ indicates p​hysical activity, and x₃ ‍indicates body mass index‌. What is this an example‌ of?","raw_text":"In a study, the total cholesterol was calculated as a + b₁x₁ + b₂x₂ + b₃ x₃, where x₁ indicates calorie intake, x₂ indicates physical activity, and x₃ indicates body mass index. What is this an example of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Simple linea‌r regression","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Simple curvilinear regress‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Multiple linear regression‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Logistic ‌regression","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Multiple linear regression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Multiple linear regression</strong></p><p>The given equation of <strong>y = a + b<sub>1</sub>x<sub>1</sub> +b<sub>2</sub>x<sub>2</sub>…..b<sub>n</sub>x<sub>n</sub></strong> is an example of <strong>multiple linear regression</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this equation:</p>\n<p>b<sub>1, </sub>b<sub>2</sub>…..b<sub>n</sub> represent different regression coefficients</p>\n<p>x<sub>1</sub>, x<sub>2</sub>…….x<sub>n</sub> represent the known variables</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3194","text":"An epidemiologist has dete‌rmined five independent f​actors influencing the ou​tcome of a particular dis‌ease. Which of the follow​ing statistical methods c‍an he use to compare thes‌e factors?","raw_text":"An epidemiologist has determined five independent factors influencing the outcome of a particular disease. Which of the following statistical methods can he use to compare these factors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AN‌OVA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple linear regression‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T-t‌est","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Logistic ‌regression","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Logistic regression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Logistic regression</strong></p><p>In this scenario, the outcome to be determined (<strong>dependent variable)</strong> is the occurrence of the disease, which is <strong>categorical</strong> (qualitative) in nature. <strong>Logistic regression</strong> can be used when a categorical dependent variable is being compared with either a categorical or numerical independent variable.</p>\n<p><strong>Linear</strong> <strong>regression</strong> is used when the outcome is <strong>numerical</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 229px; width: 489px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\"> </td>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 360px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Dependent variable (outcome)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 98px;\">\n<p><strong>Independent variable </strong></p>\n<p><strong>(risk factors, exposure)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 98px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.875px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Qualitative (categorical)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.125px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Quantitative (numerical)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px; text-align: center;\">Qualitative (categorical)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.875px;\">\n<p>Logistic regression</p>\n<p>Chi-square</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.125px;\">\n<p>Linear regression</p>\n<p>T-test</p>\n<p>ANOVA </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 98px; text-align: center;\">Quantitative (numerical)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 133.875px;\">Logistic regression</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.125px;\"> \n<p>Linear regression</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3196","text":"What test is used to compa‌re Kaplan-Meier survival ​curves?","raw_text":"What test is used to compare Kaplan-Meier survival curves?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AN‌OVA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bland-Altm‌an analysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chi-squ‌are test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cox proportional hazards t‌est","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cox proportional hazards test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cox proportional hazards test</strong></p><p>The <strong>Cox proportional hazards test</strong> is used to compare<strong> Kaplan-Meier survival curves</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Survival analysis</strong> is used for analyzing the <strong>expected duration</strong> of time until one event occurs, such as death. The following methods are used for this are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Life table</strong> analysis </li>\n<li>Kaplan-Meier analysis - it involves calculating the <strong>survival function </strong>(the probability that a person will survive past a certain time)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Life tables and survival functions are graphically represented in a Kaplan-Meier plot or curve.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3198","text":"Which of the following is ‌not used to compare survi​val curves?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not used to compare survival curves?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mantel-Hae‌nszel test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Log-ra‌nk test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cox reg‌ression","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fisher’s ‌exact test","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fisher’s exact test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fisher’s exact test</strong></p><p>Fisher’s exact test is not used to compare survival curves. The following tests are used to <strong>compare</strong> the<strong> survival curves</strong> or <strong>Kaplan-Meier curves</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mantel-Haenszel test </li>\n<li>Log-rank test  </li>\n<li>Cox regression or Cox proportional hazards analysis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3200","text":"What is the Spearman coeff‌icient used to determine?​","raw_text":"What is the Spearman coefficient used to determine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Regre‌ssion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Corre‌lation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Comparison ‌of variation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Standard deviation compari‌son","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Correlation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Correlation</strong></p><p><strong>Spearman coefficient </strong>is used to determine <strong>correlation</strong>. It is used to find <strong>linear</strong> or <strong>non-linear </strong>relationships for data with discrete ordered intervals. </p>\n<p><strong>Pearson </strong>correlation coefficient is used to find <strong>linear</strong> relationships for data with continuous intervals. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3202","text":"A researcher wants to comp‌are a newly developed tes​t for COVID with the gold​ standard test. Which of ‌the following methods can​ he use?","raw_text":"A researcher wants to compare a newly developed test for COVID with the gold standard test. Which of the following methods can he use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Regressio‌n analysis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bland-Alt‌mann Test","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cox reg‌ression","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AN‌OVA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bland-Altmann Test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bland-Altmann Test</strong></p><p>The <strong>Bland-Altmann analysis</strong> is used for comparing a <strong>newly developed test </strong>with another test, which usually is the <strong>gold standard</strong>. This method involves the graphical construction of a Bland-Altmann plot which is used to compare the measurements from both methods.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"gUliu0yl","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"61_valvular_heart_diseases","title":"61.Valvular Heart Diseases","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB3666","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of valvular heart dis​ease in developing countr‌ies?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of valvular heart disease in developing countries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumat‌ic fever","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Infective e‌ndocarditis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Conge‌nital","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Idiop‌athic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Rheumatic fever","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rheumatic fever</strong></p><p>The most common cause of valvular heart disease in developing countries is <strong>rheumatic fever</strong>. This is mainly due to the repeated infections with rheumatogenic streptococci and inadequate coverage of antibiotics. It mainly affects the <strong>mitral valve</strong>.</p>\n<p>In developed countries, degenerative or inflammatory processes are the most common cause of valvular heart diseases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3668","text":"What is the normal range f‌or the mitral valve area ​in an adult?","raw_text":"What is the normal range for the mitral valve area in an adult?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4-6 ‌sq mm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4-6 ‌sq cm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"6-8 ‌sq mm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6-8 ‌sq cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4-6 sq cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4-6 sq cm</strong></p><p>The size of the mitral orifice in a normal adult is <strong>4-6 sq cm. </strong></p>\n<p>Depending on the area of <strong>mitral valve opening </strong>(MVA), mitral stenosis can be classified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Progressive MS - MVA &gt; 1.5 sq cm</li>\n<li>Severe MS - MVA ≤ 1.5 sq cm</li>\n<li>Very severe MS - ≤ 1 sq cm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0406","text":"A 45-year-old woman is dia‌gnosed with mitral stenos​is. Which of the followin‌g features is least likel​y to be seen in this pati‍ent?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman is diagnosed with mitral stenosis. Which of the following features is least likely to be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmonary artery hypertens‌ion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tricuspid r‌egurgitation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right ventricular hypertro‌phy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left ventricular hypertrop‌hy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Left ventricular hypertrophy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/352c332d48124852aa00a568747835a7x1280x1782.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Left ventricular hypertrophy</strong></p><p>Left ventricular hypertrophy is not seen in mitral stenosis. On the contrary, <strong>right ventricle hypertrophy </strong>is seen.</p>\n<p>Mechanism of mitral stenosis (MS):</p>\n<p>In a normal mitral valve, there is no obstruction to the flow of blood from the left atrium(LA) to the left ventricle. Therefore a small LA pressure is sufficient to empty the blood into the ventricle.</p>\n<p>When the MVA is &lt;~2 sq cm, blood flows from the left atrium to the left ventricle, propelled by a mildly elevated atrial pressure. Hence, patients are usually <strong>asymptomatic</strong> and may develop <strong>dyspnea</strong> only when the <strong>heart rate increases,</strong> as seen in exertion, excitement, fever, etc. All these conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li> <strong>Increase</strong> the rate of <strong>blood flow</strong> across the mitral orifice, resulting in further elevation of the LA pressure</li>\n<li> <strong>Decrease</strong> the <strong>diastolic</strong> filling time, resulting in a reduction in cardiac output</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below explains the mechanism of mitral stenosis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3676","text":"A 45-year-old male patient‌ comes to the clinic with​ 3 months history of brea‌thlessness. After Evaluat​ion, the patient is diagn‍osed with mitral stenosis​. Which of the following ‍is least likely to be hea​rd on auscultation?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old male patient comes to the clinic with 3 months history of breathlessness. After Evaluation, the patient is diagnosed with mitral stenosis. Which of the following is least likely to be heard on auscultation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diastoli‌c murmur","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mid systo‌lic click","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Openin‌g snap","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pansystol‌ic murmur","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mid systolic click","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mid systolic click</strong></p><p><strong>Mid-systolic click</strong> is least likely to be heard in a patient with mitral stenosis. It is seen in a patient with <strong>mitral valve prolapse</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 283px; width: 385.034px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 165.034px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Important clinical features of Mitral stenosis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\">Findings</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.0341px; height: 18px; text-align: center;\">Cause</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 165.034px; height: 33px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Inspection</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 38px;\">Malar flush, pinched and blue facies</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.0341px; height: 38px;\">Decreased cardiac output and increased pulmonary artery hypertension leads to retention of CO<sub>2 </sub>and vasodilation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 38px;\">Precordial bulge</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.0341px; height: 38px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 34px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 165.034px; height: 34px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Palpation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 18px;\">Parasternal lift</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.0341px; height: 18px;\">Enlarged right ventricle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 18px;\">Palpable P2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.0341px; height: 18px;\">Pulmonary hypertension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 18px;\">Diastolic thrill</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.0341px; height: 18px;\">Increased intensity of diastolic murmur</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 34px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 165.034px; height: 34px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Auscultation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 36px;\">Loud S1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.0341px; height: 36px;\">It is seen in <strong>initial stages</strong> when the mitral valve is flexible.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 183px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 183px;\">Opening snap (OS)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.0341px; height: 183px;\">\n<p>It is caused due to the sudden opening of thickened leaflets.</p>\n<p>It follows A2. As the disease progresses, the <strong>duration</strong> between A2 and OS <strong>decreases</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is best audible at the apex and with the diaphragm of the stethoscope</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 233px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 233px;\">Diastolic murmur</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.0341px; height: 233px;\">\n<p><strong>Low</strong> pitched rumbling murmur heard with the <strong>bell</strong> of the stethoscope.</p>\n<p>It follows OS and best heard at <strong>apex</strong> with left lateral recumbent position. When it is loud, may radiate to left axilla. </p>\n<p>The duration of murmur correlates with the severity of stenosis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 18px;\">Loud P2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.0341px; height: 18px;\">Pulmonary artery hypertension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 29.3507px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 165.034px; height: 29.3507px; text-align: center;\">Other Auscultatory Findings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 19px;\">Pansystolic murmur</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.0341px; height: 19px;\">Due to functional tricuspid regurgitation in severe pulmonary hypertension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 19px;\">Graham Steell murmur</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.0341px; height: 19px;\">\n<p>It is a <strong>diastolic</strong> murmur due to pulmonary regurgitation</p>\n<p>Caused due to the dilation of the pulmonary valve ring in severe pulmonary hypertension</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 165.034px; height: 19px; text-align: center;\"><strong>JVP</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 19px;\">Prominent a waves</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.0341px; height: 19px;\">Atrial systole</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 165.034px; height: 19px; text-align: center;\"><strong>ECG</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 19px;\">Tall and peaked P waves</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.0341px; height: 19px;\">Right atrial enlargement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 19px;\">Right axis deviation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 85.0341px; height: 19px;\">Right ventricular hypertrophy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3670","text":"A 48-year-old female patie‌nt was admitted to the ho​spital in view of severe ‌dyspnea. History revealed​ that the dyspnea was exe‍rtional initially and lat​er worsened to breathless‍ness during normal daily ​activities. She died on t​he 3rd day following admi‍ssion. The image of the a​utopsy of the heart is sh‌own below. Identify the c‌ondition.","raw_text":"A 48-year-old female patient was admitted to the hospital in view of severe dyspnea. History revealed that the dyspnea was exertional initially and later worsened to breathlessness during normal daily activities. She died on the 3rd day following admission. The image of the autopsy of the heart is shown below. Identify the condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitral ‌stenosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mitral reg‌urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tricuspid‌ stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tricuspid r‌egurgitation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mitral stenosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/605ccf70079548dc8de82f986d56f830.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mitral stenosis</strong></p><p>The given image is of <strong>mitral stenosis</strong> showing <strong>fish mouth</strong> or <strong>buttonhole</strong> appearance.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3669","text":"A 62-year-old woman presen‌ts with breathlessness fo​r 5 months. She is unable‌ to carry out her daily a​ctivities and has difficu‍lty sleeping. On examinat​ion, she has a parasterna‍l heave. On auscultation,​ opening snap (OS) is hea​rd followed by a low pitc‍hed rumbling diastolic mu​rmur. Which of the follow‌ing findings is suggestiv‌e of increased severity o‌f the given condition?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old woman presents with breathlessness for 5 months. She is unable to carry out her daily activities and has difficulty sleeping. On examination, she has a parasternal heave. On auscultation, opening snap (OS) is heard followed by a low pitched rumbling diastolic murmur. Which of the following findings is suggestive of increased severity of the given condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lou‌d S1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sof‌t P2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased A2‌-OS interval","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased A2‌-OS interval","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Decreased A2-OS interval","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Decreased A2-OS interval</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>mitral stenosis</strong>. The more severe the thickening, the earlier the opening snap occurs in diastole. Hence, the <strong>decreased interval</strong> between <strong>A2</strong> and opening snap <strong>(OS)</strong> indicates <strong>increased</strong> <strong>severity</strong> of the stenosis.</p>\n<p>The auscultatory findings which are suggestive of increased severity of murmur are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Decreased interval between A2 and OS</li>\n<li>Increased duration of the diastolic murmur</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Loud S1 is seen in the initial stages of MS when the mitral leaflets are flexible. When there is further thickening and calcification of the leaflets, they become immobile, and the amplitude of S1 decreases.</p>\n<p>Increased severity of the stenosis increases the left atrial pressure. This increase in left atrial pressure can increase pulmonary artery pressure which leads to loud P2 on auscultation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7505","text":"Which of the following car‌diac chambers is enlarged​ in mitral stenosis on ch‌est X-ray initially?","raw_text":"Which of the following cardiac chambers is enlarged in mitral stenosis on chest X-ray initially?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left ‌atrium","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Right ‌atrium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left ve‌ntricle","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right v‌entricle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Left atrium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Left atrium</strong></p><p><strong>Left atrium</strong> (LA) is the <strong>first cardiac chamber</strong> to get enlarged in mitral stenosis (MS). It is due to the pressure overload of LA. It can be appreciated on the lateral and left anterior oblique views.</p>\n<p>Other radiographic changes seen in MS are <strong>straightening of the upper left border</strong> of the cardiac silhouette, posterior displacement of the esophagus by an enlarged LA, and <strong>Kerley B lines</strong> due to distention of interlobular septae and lymphatics with edema.</p>\n<p><strong>Severe MS</strong> causing <strong>pulmonary hypertension</strong> leads to enlargement of the pulmonary artery, <strong>right ventricle</strong>, and <strong>right atrium</strong>. <strong>Left ventricle </strong>remains <strong>unaffected</strong> in isolated MS.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3679","text":"A patient with mitral sten‌osis presented with worse​ning dyspnea and fatigue ‌which are now present at ​rest. He also complained ‍of a feeling of heavy pou​nding in the chest with r‍ecurrent chest pain. You ​decided to evaluate the p​atient using echocardiogr‍aphy. When would you advi​se the patient to undergo‌ a valvotomy?","raw_text":"A patient with mitral stenosis presented with worsening dyspnea and fatigue which are now present at rest. He also complained of a feeling of heavy pounding in the chest with recurrent chest pain. You decided to evaluate the patient using echocardiography. When would you advise the patient to undergo a valvotomy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitral orifice is &amp;lt; 0.8‌ sq cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mitral orifice is &amp;lt; 1 s‌q cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mitral orifice is &amp;lt; 1.5‌ sq cm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"In all patients who are sy‌mptomatic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mitral orifice is < 1.5 sq cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mitral orifice is &lt; 1.5 sq cm</strong></p><p><strong>Mitral valvotomy</strong> is indicated in symptomatic (NYHA Functional Class II-IV) patients with mitral stenosis (MS) with orifice <strong>&lt; 1.5 cm<sup>2</sup> </strong>in normal-sized adults or &lt;1 cm<sup>2</sup>/m<sup>2 </sup>body surface area.</p>\n<p>Treatment of mitral stenosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Administration of <strong>warfarin</strong> in patients with MS who develop <strong>atrial fibrillation.</strong></li>\n<li>Surgical treatment includes:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mitral valvotomy:</strong> It is done in symptomatic patients whose mitral orifice is less than <strong>1.5 cm<sup>2</sup>. </strong>This can be either percutaneous mitral valvotomy or open surgical valvotomy.</li>\n<li><strong>Mitral valve replacement:</strong> This is done in patients whose mitral valve is severely distorted.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3675","text":"A 26-year-old woman underg‌oing evaluation for panic​ disorder was referred to‌ the physician for compla​ints of palpitations and ‍breathlessness. Detailed ​history revealed the pres‍ence of chest pain, which​ was substernal and not r​elated to exercise. On au‍scultation, mid-systolic ​clicks were audible. What‌ is the most likely diagn‌osis?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old woman undergoing evaluation for panic disorder was referred to the physician for complaints of palpitations and breathlessness. Detailed history revealed the presence of chest pain, which was substernal and not related to exercise. On auscultation, mid-systolic clicks were audible. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unstabl‌e angina","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aortic ‌stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mitral val‌ve prolapse","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mitral reg‌urgitation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mitral valve prolapse","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mitral valve prolapse</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>mitral valve prolapse</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is caused due to the <strong>myxomatous degeneration</strong> of the mitral valve. It may be associated with Marfan's syndrome, osteogenesis imperfecta, and Ehlers-Danlos syndrome. It is more common in <strong>females</strong> of the age group of <strong>15- 30 years.</strong></p>\n<p>Most patients are usually <strong>asymptomatic</strong>. However, some patients may present with symptoms such as <strong>palpitations,</strong> syncope due to arrhythmias (premature ventricular contractions, ventricular tachycardia, and atrial fibrillation), non-specific chest pain, and dyspnea</p>\n<p>Auscultation reveals <strong>mid-systolic clicks. </strong>If there is associated mitral regurgitation, a systolic murmur might also be heard. The duration of the murmur of MVP decreases on squatting and increases on standing. Whereas the duration of the murmur of MR increases on squatting and decreases on standing.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0059","text":"Carvallo’s sign is associa‌ted with the murmur of wh​ich valvulopathy?","raw_text":"Carvallo’s sign is associated with the murmur of which valvulopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitral ‌stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tricuspid r‌egurgitation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary r‌egurgitation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aortic ‌stenosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tricuspid regurgitation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tricuspid regurgitation</strong></p><p><strong>Carvallo’s sign</strong> refers to the <strong>increase </strong>in the<strong> intensity</strong> of systolic <strong>murmur</strong> of <strong>tricuspid regurgitation </strong>with<strong> inspiration.</strong></p>\n<p>During inspiration, the venous return to the heart increases due to the negative intrathoracic pressure. This increases the leak from the right ventricle to the right atrium making the murmur louder.</p>\n<p>In general, the <strong>right-sided</strong> heart murmurs <strong>increase</strong> in intensity with <strong>inspiration</strong> and <strong>decrease</strong> in intensity with <strong>expiration,</strong> while the opposite is true for left-sided heart murmurs. Pulmonic ejection sound is the only right-sided acoustic event that decreases in intensity with inspiration.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3682","text":"The severity of mitral reg‌urgitation can be assesse​d by:","raw_text":"The severity of mitral regurgitation can be assessed by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intensity‌ of murmur","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ejectio‌n click","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wide spl‌it of S2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lou‌d S1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Wide split of S2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Wide split of S2</strong></p><p>The <strong>severity</strong> of mitral regurgitation <strong>(MR)</strong> can be assessed by the <strong>wide splitting </strong>of the<strong> S2</strong>. Due to severe regurgitation of blood, the pressure in the left ventricle falls rapidly, leading to the early closure of the aortic valve. </p>\n<p>Clinical findings that indicate the severity of MR is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reduced cardiac output</li>\n<li>Palpable systolic thrill</li>\n<li>Palpable S3</li>\n<li>Wide splitting of S2</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: The intensity of murmur does not always correlate with the severity of the murmur. Intensity may be diminished in severe MR caused by LV dysfunction, acute MI and periprosthetic valve regugitation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3671","text":"A 65-year-old male patient‌ presented with chest pai​n, exertional dyspnea and‌ syncope. What is the mos​t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old male patient presented with chest pain, exertional dyspnea and syncope. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tricuspid r‌egurgitation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aortic reg‌urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mitral ‌stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aortic ‌stenosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Aortic stenosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Aortic stenosis</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis is <strong>aortic stenosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Aortic stenosis is the narrowing of the aortic valve. It may be caused due to the <strong>calcification</strong> of the normal aortic valve, calcification of congenital bicuspid valve, and rheumatic heart disease.</p>\n<p>Three <strong>cardinal</strong> clinical <strong>features</strong> of aortic stenosis are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Exertional dyspnea -</strong> this occurs due to the left ventricular (LV) diastolic dysfunction. Raised end-diastolic LV volume and reduced LV compliance lead to increased LV pressure, leading to elevation of pulmonary capillary wedge pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Angina pectoris -</strong> it occurs due to the combination of the increased O<sub><span class=\"s1\">2 </span></sub>needs of hypertrophied myocardium and reduction of O<sub><span class=\"s1\">2 </span></sub>delivery.</li>\n<li><strong>Syncope -</strong> it occurs due to the combination of decreased cardiac output and systemic arterial dilation which lead to a reduction of cerebral perfusion.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3672","text":"On auscultation of a patie‌nt, a murmur was heard wh​ich is depicted by the fo‌llowing phonocardiogram. ​Which of the given condit‍ions is most likely to pr​oduce this murmur?","raw_text":"On auscultation of a patient, a murmur was heard which is depicted by the following phonocardiogram. Which of the given conditions is most likely to produce this murmur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitral ‌stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mitral reg‌urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aortic ‌stenosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aortic reg‌urgitation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aortic stenosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ee74341748944fe399a382cef7e13e93.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf3af64542444f4b88acecf816f28bc3x1280x2348.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aortic stenosis</strong></p><p>The phonocardiogram given above is of an <strong>ejection systolic murmur,</strong> which is seen in <strong>aortic stenosis</strong>. It is also heard in pulmonary stenosis. They are differentiated based on the difference in symptoms, site of murmur, etc.</p>\n<p> <strong>Clinical findings</strong> in aortic stenosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pulse - slow rising and late peaking pulse which is known as <strong>pulsus parvus et tardus</strong>. A carotid thrill may also be palpated over these arteries.</li>\n<li>Apical impulse - A double apical impulse may be recognized, and in the later stages, it may be displaced laterally.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Auscultation </strong>findings in aortic stenosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Early systolic ejection sound - </strong>heard in early stages, disappears when the valve becomes calcified and rigid</li>\n<li><strong>Paradoxical splitting of S2 - </strong>With worsening of stenosis, delayed closure of the aortic valve occurs. This leads to <strong>narrowing of S2</strong> sound, and in severe cases, leads to paradoxical splitting of S2.</li>\n<li><strong>S4 and S3</strong> - heard in severe cases</li>\n<li><strong>Ejection systolic murmur</strong> - starts after S1, increases in intensity to reach the peak, and ends before A2. It radiates to the neck and base of the heart. It is augmented by squatting and reduced by the strain of the Valsalva maneuver.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows phonocardiograms of normal and abnormal heart sounds.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0629","text":"A patient presented with b‌reathlessness on exertion​ and chest pain. Ausculta‌tion revealed an ejection​ systolic murmur heard be‍st at the base of the hea​rt. Echocardiography show‍ed reduced left ventricul​ar ejection fraction and ​left ventricular hypertro‍phy. Which of the followi​ng features does not sign‌ify a severe form of this‌ disease?","raw_text":"A patient presented with breathlessness on exertion and chest pain. Auscultation revealed an ejection systolic murmur heard best at the base of the heart. Echocardiography showed reduced left ventricular ejection fraction and left ventricular hypertrophy. Which of the following features does not signify a severe form of this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulsus parv‌us et tardus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sing‌le S2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Late peakin‌g of murmur","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wide split‌ting of S2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Wide splitting of S2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Wide splitting of S2</strong></p><p>The above features point to a diagnosis of <strong>aortic stenosis</strong>. Wide splitting of S2 is not a feature of severe aortic stenosis. On the contrary, there might be single S2 or paradoxical splitting of S2.</p>\n<p><strong>Features</strong> of <strong>severe</strong> aortic stenosis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pulsus parvus et tardus</li>\n<li>Palpable carotid thrill</li>\n<li>Louder and <strong>late peaking</strong> of systolic murmur</li>\n<li>Single S2 or <strong>paradoxical splitting of S2</strong></li>\n<li>Presence of S3 and S4 heart sounds</li>\n<li>Signs of right and left ventricular failure</li>\n<li>Left ventricular hypertrophy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3667","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is not associated​ with aortic regurgitatio‌n?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is not associated with aortic regurgitation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ankylosing ‌spondylitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Marfan ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute myocardial infarctio‌n","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bicuspid a‌ortic valve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Acute myocardial infarction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Acute myocardial infarction</strong></p><p>Acute myocardial infarction is not associated with aortic regurgitation.</p>\n<p><strong>Aortic regurgitation</strong> is the backflow of the blood from the aorta to the left ventricle.</p>\n<p>It may be caused either due to the <strong>defect</strong> in the <strong>valve</strong> or defect in the<strong> aortic root</strong> (dilation of the aortic annulus).</p>\n<p>Conditions that affect valves are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Congenital</li>\n<li>Endocarditis</li>\n<li>Rheumatic fever</li>\n<li>Myxomatous (prolapse)</li>\n<li>Traumatic</li>\n<li>Syphilis</li>\n<li><strong>Ankylosing spondylitis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Conditions that affect aortic root are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aortic dissection</li>\n<li>Cystic medial degeneration</li>\n<li><strong>Marfan syndrome</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bicuspid aortic valve</strong></li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3674","text":"A 55-year-old male patient‌ presents to the hospital​ with palpitations. On ex‌amination, a diastolic mu​rmur is heard in the left‍ third intercostal space ​and pistol shot sound is ‍heard over the femoral ar​teries. What is the most ​likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old male patient presents to the hospital with palpitations. On examination, a diastolic murmur is heard in the left third intercostal space and pistol shot sound is heard over the femoral arteries. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aortic reg‌urgitation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aortic ‌stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tricuspid r‌egurgitation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mitral reg‌urgitation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aortic regurgitation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2963fd3672f3474aa841f68362712e0ax1280x1895.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aortic regurgitation</strong></p><p>The presence of a <strong>diastolic</strong> <strong>murmur</strong> along with the <strong>pistol</strong> <strong>shot</strong> sound over the <strong>femoral</strong> is most likely to be due to <strong>aortic regurgitation.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below explains the mechanism of aortic regurgitation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0632","text":"A 58-year-old man presente‌d with exertional dyspnea​ and angina. On examinati‌on, the pulse was charact​erized by abrupt distenti‍on and quick collapse. Au​scultation revealed an ea‍rly diastolic murmur. Whi​ch of the following is a ​feature of this murmur wi‍th respect to the likely ​diagnosis in this case?","raw_text":"A 58-year-old man presented with exertional dyspnea and angina. On examination, the pulse was characterized by abrupt distention and quick collapse. Auscultation revealed an early diastolic murmur. Which of the following is a feature of this murmur with respect to the likely diagnosis in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Crescendo-decrescendo murm‌ur","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Best heard i‌n inspiration","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High pitc‌hed murmur","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Severity correlates with i‌ntensity of murmur","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. High pitched murmur","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. High pitched murmur</strong></p><p>The given clinical features are suggestive of <strong>aortic regurgitation</strong>. An early diastolic murmur is a <strong>high-pitched</strong> <strong>decrescendo</strong> diastolic murmur.</p>\n<p>Auscultatory findings in AR are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Soft S1 - due to the early closure of the mitral valve</li>\n<li><strong>Diminished or absent A2</strong></li>\n<li>S3 - due to increased left ventricular (LV) end-diastolic volume. The presence of S3 may be a sign of impaired LV function.</li>\n<li><strong>Early diastolic murmur -</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>High-pitched</strong>, blowing, decrescendo, <strong>early diastolic murmur</strong></li>\n<li>Best heard in the left third intercostal space</li>\n<li>Heard with the patient sitting up and leaning forward, with breath held in forced expiration</li>\n<li><strong>Duration</strong> of the murmur best correlates with the severity</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Systolic ejection murmur - It is heard due to increased LV stroke volume and ejection rate.</li>\n<li><strong>Austin Flint murmur </strong>- a <strong>low-pitched</strong>, mid to late diastolic murmur. It is caused due to the large volume of regurgitant blood, hitting the anterior leaflet of the mitral valve or LV wall.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD2287","text":"Which of the following is ‌the criteria for severe a​ortic regurgitation, in t‌ransthoracic doppler echo​cardiography?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the criteria for severe aortic regurgitation, in transthoracic doppler echocardiography?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Effective regurgitant orif‌ice &amp;lt;0.3 sq. cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Regurgitant volume &amp;gt;60m‌L/ beat","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Regurgitant fraction more ‌than 60%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vena contrac‌ta &amp;lt;0.6 cm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Regurgitant volume >60mL/ beat","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Regurgitant volume &gt;60mL/ beat</strong></p><p><strong>Regurgitant volume &gt;60mL/ beat</strong> is the criteria for <strong>severe</strong> <strong>aortic regurgitation,</strong> in transthoracic doppler echocardiography <strong>(TTE).</strong></p>\n<p>Doppler echocardiography can be used to assess the severity of aortic regurgitation. According to this, AR is considered to be severe when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Central jet width is more than 65% of left ventricular outflow tract</li>\n<li>Vena contracta width &gt;6mm</li>\n<li>Regurgitant fraction &gt;50%</li>\n<li>Regurgitant volume &gt;60mL/beat</li>\n<li>Effective regurgitant orifice &gt;0.3cm<sup>2</sup></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3681","text":"What is the treatment of c‌hoice in symptomatic acut​e severe aortic regurgita‌tion?","raw_text":"What is the treatment of choice in symptomatic acute severe aortic regurgitation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beta b‌lockers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aortic valve‌ replacement","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Periodic ‌monitoring","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diur‌etics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Aortic valve replacement","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Aortic valve replacement</strong></p><p>The treatment of choice in symptomatic acute severe AR is <strong>aortic valve replacement</strong> (AVR).</p>\n<p>Treatment of aortic regurgitation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In <strong>non-severe</strong> AR, periodic monitoring can be done. The symptoms of dyspnea and decreased cardiac output can be managed by <strong>diuretics</strong> and <strong>vasodilators</strong>.</li>\n<li>In <strong>severe</strong> AR, <strong>surgery </strong>(AVR) is indicated in the following conditions:\n<ul>\n<li>The patient is symptomatic.</li>\n<li>The patient is asymptomatic, but the LV ejection fraction is less than 50% or LV end-diastolic diameter is more than 65mm.</li>\n<li>The patient is asymptomatic and undergoing other cardiac surgeries.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0600","text":"A patient has enlarged nec‌k veins, hepatomegaly and​ a holosystolic murmur al‌ong the lower left sterna​l margin. What is the mos‍t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient has enlarged neck veins, hepatomegaly and a holosystolic murmur along the lower left sternal margin. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tricuspid r‌egurgitation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mitral reg‌urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left hear‌t failure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right hea‌rt failure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tricuspid regurgitation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tricuspid regurgitation</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis in the above clinical scenario is <strong>tricuspid regurgitation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features</strong> of tricuspid regurgitation are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Distended neck veins</strong> with <strong>prominent c-v waves</strong></li>\n<li>Hepatomegaly with systolic pulsations</li>\n<li><strong>Positive hepatojugular reflex</strong> - the distension of the jugular vein when firm pressure is applied over the liver</li>\n<li>Blowing <strong>holosystolic murmur</strong> along the lower left sternal margin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3678","text":"On auscultation of a patie‌nt, a murmur was heard wh​ich is depicted by the fo‌llowing phonocardiogram. ​Which of the given condit‍ions is most likely to pr​oduce this murmur? Mitral‍ regurgitation Tricuspid ​stenosis Mitral stenosis ​Tricuspid regurgitation A‍trial septal defect","raw_text":"<p>On auscultation of a patient, a murmur was heard which is depicted by the following phonocardiogram. Which of the given conditions is most likely to produce this murmur?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Mitral regurgitation</li>\n<li>Tricuspid stenosis</li>\n<li>Mitral stenosis</li>\n<li>Tricuspid regurgitation</li>\n<li>Atrial septal defect</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2‌,5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2‌,3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‌,4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3‌,5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1,4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2d8973c45052468498058ef4ae75c611.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5c7adc6b1a1644a59d50083ff2d2fec4x1280x2348.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1,4</strong></p><p>The given phonocardiogram is of a <strong>pansystolic</strong>/holosystolic <strong>murmur</strong>. It is heard in mitral regurgitation <strong>(MR)</strong>, tricuspid regurgitation <strong>(TR),</strong> and ventricular septal defect <strong>(VSD)</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9299","text":"A patient has a right uppe‌r limb BP of 180/95 mm Hg​ and a left upper limb BP‌ of 130/90 mm Hg. He also​ has an early diastolic m‍urmur in right 2nd interc​ostal space. Which of the‍ following is least likel​y to be associated with t​hese findings?","raw_text":"A patient has a right upper limb BP of 180/95 mm Hg and a left upper limb BP of 130/90 mm Hg. He also has an early diastolic murmur in right 2nd intercostal space. Which of the following is least likely to be associated with these findings?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supravalvular aortic steno‌sis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Coarctatio‌n of aorta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Takayasu ‌arteritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aortic d‌issection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Supravalvular aortic stenosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Supravalvular aortic stenosis</strong></p><p>A <strong>significant difference</strong> in <strong>blood pressure</strong> measured <strong>between two arms</strong> (&gt;10 mm Hg) may be seen in Takayasu arteritis (subclavian artery disease), <strong>supravalvular aortic stenosis</strong>, aortic coarctation, and aortic dissection (all four options).</p>\n<p>An <strong>early diastolic murmur</strong> in the <strong>right second intercostal space</strong> (aortic area) is suggestive of <strong>aortic regurgitation</strong>. <strong>Supravalvular aortic stenosis</strong> (Option A), which involves the part of the aorta distal to the aortic valve, is <strong>unlikely</strong> to cause <strong>aortic regurgitation</strong> and hence the best answer.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 136px;\" width=\"575\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 58.8594px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 108.993px; height: 58.8594px;\">Features </td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.993px; height: 58.8594px;\">Supravalvular AS</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.993px; height: 58.8594px;\">Takayasu arteritis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.993px; height: 58.8594px;\">Aortic dissection</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109.132px; height: 58.8594px;\">Coarctation of aorta</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 42px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 108.993px; height: 42px;\">Aortic regurgitation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.993px; height: 42px;\">Unlikely</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.993px; height: 42px;\">Present in 25% of cases</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.993px; height: 42px;\">Present in acute situations</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109.132px; height: 42px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 42px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 108.993px; height: 42px;\">BP difference between arms</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.993px; height: 42px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.993px; height: 42px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 108.993px; height: 42px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 109.132px; height: 42px;\">May be present</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>So the single best answer would be <strong>Supravalvular AS &gt; Coarctation of the aorta.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3683","text":"Barlow’s syndrome refers t‌o which of the following?​","raw_text":"Barlow’s syndrome refers to which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carcinoid‌ of heart","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mitral val‌ve prolapse","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aortic reg‌urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopath‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mitral valve prolapse","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mitral valve prolapse</strong></p><p>Barlow’s syndrome refers to <strong>mitral valve prolapse</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mitral valve prolapse syndrome is also called as systolic click-murmur syndrome, Barlow’s syndrome or floppy valve syndrome. It is the most common abnormality leading to primary mitral regurgitation.</p>\n<p>Note: Infantile scurvy is referred to as Barlow's disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"TBWxnCm6","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"61_pituitary__parathyroids_and_pancreas","title":"61.Pituitary ,Parathyroids And Pancreas","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MB5447","text":"A 30-year-old woman is bei‌ng evaluated for chronic ​headaches, infertility, a​nd galactorrhea. MRI of t‌he brain shows the follow‍ing finding. What is the ​likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman is being evaluated for chronic headaches, infertility, and galactorrhea. MRI of the brain shows the following finding.  What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Corticotro‌ph adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gonadotro‌ph adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lactotrop‌h adenoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Somatotro‌ph adenoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lactotroph adenoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ab31995aa5384673a09b3ea594bb536f.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3e54e4e2e2f948c48542c26afc20de48x510x537.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lactotroph adenoma</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and brain MRI showing a <strong>pituitary</strong> <strong>adenoma</strong> are suggestive of a <strong>lactotroph adenoma</strong> (prolactinoma). </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Pituitary adenomas are usually found in<strong> </strong>adults. The most common genetic alterations in this condition are <strong>mutations</strong> in G ­proteins<strong> </strong>(particularly<strong> GNAS</strong>). </p>\n<p>Depending on their size, they are classified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Microadenomas - diameter &lt; 1 cm</li>\n<li>Macroadenomas - diameter &gt; 1 cm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They may be<strong> functioning </strong>or<strong> non-functioning.</strong> The most common type of functioning pituitary<strong> </strong>adenomas<strong> </strong>are prolactinomas.</p>\n<p>The clinical features of prolactinoma include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Due to mass effect - </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Features of raised intracranial tension</li>\n<li>Visual field disturbances (bitemporal hemianopia) - due to compression of optic chiasm</li>\n<li>Hypopituitarism - due to compressed hypophyseal stalk</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Due to hyperprolactinemia -</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Galactorrhoea</li>\n<li>Amenorrhoea or oligomenorrhoea</li>\n<li>Infertility</li>\n<li>Loss of libido</li>\n<li>Gynecomastia</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Serum prolactin concentrations tend to correlate with the size of the adenoma. The <strong>normal</strong> range of <strong>serum prolactin</strong> is <strong>5-20</strong> μg/L. The diagnosis of prolactinoma is most likely to be associated with a prolactin level <strong>&gt;200 μg/L</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6726","text":"Presence of which of the f‌ollowing features is requ​ired to diagnose a patien​t with pituitary carcinom‌a?","raw_text":"Presence of which of the following features is required to diagnose a patient with pituitary carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nuclear pl‌eomorphism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atypical mit‌otic figures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rapidly gr‌owing mass","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Presence of craniospinal m‌etastasis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Presence of craniospinal metastasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Presence of craniospinal metastasis</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The following features are <strong>required</strong> for the <strong>diagnosis</strong> of <strong>pituitary carcinoma</strong>:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">A pituitary tumor that has <strong>metastasized</strong> to distant sites, either <strong>craniospinal</strong> or <strong>systemic sites</strong> (</span><span class=\"s1\">and/or)</span></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Presence of a pituitary tumor that is not contiguous with the primary sellar tumour </span></li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Pituitary carcinoma is extremely rare (&lt; 1% of total tumors). They are associated with a</span>ctivating mutations of the <strong>HRAS </strong>oncogene.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The diagnosis of pituitary carcinoma cannot be made based on histologic appearance alone. Though <strong>pituitary adenomas</strong> normally show <span class=\"s1\"><strong>cellular </strong></span><strong>monomorphism</strong> and the absence of a reticulin network, they can have some <strong>overlapping features</strong> with malignant tumors such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Atypical adenomas - show <span class=\"s1\"><strong>pleomorphism</strong> and <strong>atypical mitosis </strong></span></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Invasive adenomas -</span>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Non-encapsulated </span></li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Show <strong>local invasion</strong> into </span>cavernous and sphenoid sinuses, dura, brain</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">However, they remain <strong>confined</strong> to the <strong>primary</strong> <strong>tumor site</strong></span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5526","text":"Which of the following cel‌ls secrete parathyroid ho​rmone?","raw_text":"Which of the following cells secrete parathyroid hormone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chief‌ cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Oxyphi‌l cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C-c‌ells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parafollic‌ular cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chief cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81efa066ed6348db84f62ca11ca63cebx1280x1790.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eba8b172bb8a425f838b4bca99d45f42x1280x1790.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chief cells</strong></p><p>Parathyroid hormone (<strong>PTH</strong>) is secreted by the <strong>chief cells</strong> of the<strong> </strong>parathyroid glands.</p>\n<p>The function of the parathyroids is to regulate<strong> calcium homeostasis</strong>. This gland is composed of two cell types: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chief cells</strong> -\n<ul>\n<li>Polygonal cells with uniform nuclei and pink cytoplasm</li>\n<li><strong>Water-clear appearance</strong> - due to presence of large amounts of <strong>cytoplasmic glycogen</strong></li>\n<li>Secretory granules containing PTH</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Oxyphil cells - </strong>Slightly larger cells with <strong>acidophilic cytoplasm</strong> with no known function.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows the normal histology of the parathyroid gland:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>C-cells</strong> or <strong>parafollicular </strong>cells are present in the <strong>thyroid.</strong> They secrete<strong> calcitonin</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5530","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of asymptomatic hyper​calcemia?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of asymptomatic hypercalcemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malig‌nancy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary hyperparathyroidis‌m","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Secondary hyperparathyroid‌ism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Primary hyperparathyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Primary hyperparathyroidism</strong></p><p>The most common cause of <strong>asymptomatic</strong> <strong>hypercalcemia</strong> (isolated hypercalcemia without signs or symptoms) is <strong>primary hyperparathyroidism</strong>. It is also the most common cause of hypercalcemia overall.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1104\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Primary hyperparathyroidism results in <strong>autonomous</strong> <strong>overproduction</strong> of parathyroid hormone <strong>(PTH),</strong> resulting in elevated levels. It usually results from an <strong>adenoma</strong> or <strong>hyperplasia</strong> of parathyroid tissue.</p>\n<p>The most common cause of <strong>symptomatic hypercalcemia </strong>is <strong>malignancy</strong>. The most common mechanism through which osteolytic tumors induce hypercalcemia is the secretion of the PTH-related peptide (<strong>PTHrP</strong>). </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5527","text":"A 40-year-old woman presen‌ted with bone pain and ab​dominal cramps for the pa​st 1 week. She has had mu‌ltiple prior hospital adm‍issions for management of​ recurrent renal calculi.‌ Investigations revealed ​elevated serum calcium an​d PTH levels with reduced​ phosphate levels. Which ​of the following is the m​ost common pathological c‍hange responsible for thi​s condition?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman presented with bone pain and abdominal cramps for the past 1 week. She has had multiple prior hospital admissions for management of recurrent renal calculi. Investigations revealed elevated serum calcium and PTH levels with reduced phosphate levels.  Which of the following is the most common pathological change responsible for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ade‌noma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diffuse h‌yperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nodular h‌yperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ectopic glan‌dular tissue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Adenoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Adenoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture is highly suggestive of<strong> primary hyperparathyroidism</strong>. The most common cause is a sporadic, solitary<strong> parathyroid adenoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Parathyroid tumors may be associated with <strong>cyclin D1</strong> gene inversions and <strong>MEN1</strong> mutations. Hyperparathyroidism refers to<strong> elevated </strong>parathyroid hormone (<strong>PTH</strong>). It is classified into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary - </strong>autonomous overproduction of PTH</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary - </strong>compensatory hypersecretion of PTH in response to prolonged hypocalcemia</li>\n<li><strong>Tertiary - </strong>persistent hypersecretion of PTH even after correction of prolonged hypocalcemia. (e.g. after renal transplant)</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1104\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The classical symptoms and signs of primary hyperparathyroidism are due to hypercalcemia as well as increased parathyroid hormone (PTH) levels. The features of <strong>hypercalcemia</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 416.828125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\"><strong>System</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 275.828125px;\"><strong>Clinical features</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\">Musculoskeletal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 275.828125px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Bone pain</li>\n<li>Skeletal deformities</li>\n<li>Pathological fractures</li>\n<li>Myalgia and arthralgia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\">Renal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 275.828125px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Renal stones(Calcium oxalate &gt; calcium phosphate)</li>\n<li>Nephrocalcinosis</li>\n<li>Polyuria and dehydration</li>\n<li>Renal failure</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\">GIT</td>\n<td style=\"width: 275.828125px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Constipation</li>\n<li>Nausea, vomiting, anorexia</li>\n<li>Abdominal pain</li>\n<li>Peptic ulcer disease</li>\n<li>Acute pancreatitis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\">Neuropsychiatric</td>\n<td style=\"width: 275.828125px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Anxiety, depression</li>\n<li>Cognitive dysfunction</li>\n<li>Irritability</li>\n<li>Hallucinations</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\">Cardiac</td>\n<td style=\"width: 275.828125px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Short QT interval</li>\n<li>Bradycardia</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 136px;\">Ectopic calcification</td>\n<td style=\"width: 275.828125px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Cornea: Band keratopathy</li>\n<li>Extravascular tissues: calcinosis</li>\n<li>Small arteries: calciphylaxis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The features of<strong> elevated PTH levels</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Osteitis fibrosa cystica</li>\n<li>Osteopenia/osteoporosis</li>\n<li>Hyperuricemia and gout </li>\n<li>Pseudogout</li>\n<li>Increased production of calcitriol  </li>\n<li>Nephrolithiasis</li>\n<li>Hypophosphatemia</li>\n<li>Anemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The clinical signs associated with primary hyperparathyroidism can be remembered with the mnemonic -<strong> “bones, stones, groans, moans with fatigue overtones”</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5528","text":"A 47-year-old woman was ev‌aluated for chronic backa​che and abdominal pain. I​nvestigations revealed th‌e following. What is the ‍most likely bone finding ​seen in this condition? S‌erum calcium - 15.1 mg/dL​ Parathormone (PTH) level​ - 2105 pg/mL USG Abdomen​ - multiple renal calculi​","raw_text":"<p>A 47-year-old woman was evaluated for chronic backache and abdominal pain. Investigations revealed the following. What is the most likely bone finding seen in this condition?</p>\n<p>Serum calcium - 15.1 mg/dL</p>\n<p>Parathormone (PTH) level - 2105 pg/mL</p>\n<p>USG Abdomen - multiple renal calculi</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteoporosis circumscripta‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fraying and cupping of met‌aphysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteitis fib‌rosa cystica","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Punched out ‌lytic lesions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Osteitis fibrosa cystica","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4bedbd06090c4ac5a5d952f5da4fcba8x1280x853.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Osteitis fibrosa cystica</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario and lab findings point towards a diagnosis of <strong>hyperparathyroidism</strong>. The bone finding associated with this condition is <strong>osteitis fibrosa cystica.</strong></p>\n<p>This condition is also called <strong>brown tumor</strong> or von Recklinghausen disease of bone.</p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">In hyperparathyroidism, high levels of <strong>PTH</strong> result in increased <strong>osteoclast activity</strong>. This results in <strong>bone loss</strong> which predisposes to <strong>microfractures</strong> and <strong>secondary hemorrhages</strong>. This results in the following changes: </span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">An influx of <strong>macrophages</strong> that form giant cells </span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Mass of reparative <strong>fibrous tissue </strong></span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Increased vascularity, hemorrhage, and <strong>hemosiderin</strong> deposition - results in brown color</span></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1194\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The image given below is showing a brown tumor with numerous giant cells with interstitial hemorrhage, hemosiderin.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Osteoporosis circumscripta is a large, well-defined lytic lesion seen in the skull of patients with Paget's disease.</p>\n<p>Option B: Fraying and cupping of the metaphysis of the bones are classically seen in patients suffering from rickets.</p>\n<p>Option D: Punched out lytic lesions of the bone are seen in patients with multiple myeloma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5543","text":"A 46-year-old woman is ref‌erred with recurrent epis​odes of hypoglycemia. Dur​ing the evaluation, she i‌s found to have high seru‍m levels of insulin. Meas​urement of which of the f‌ollowing can help in reac​hing the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 46-year-old woman is referred with recurrent episodes of hypoglycemia. During the evaluation, she is found to have high serum levels of insulin. Measurement of which of the following can help in reaching the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proin‌sulin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"C-pe‌ptide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pre-pro‌insulin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Insulin-li‌ke peptide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. C-peptide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. C-peptide</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of hypoglycemia due to <strong>hyperinsulinemia</strong>. As <strong>C-peptide </strong>is a marker of <strong>endogenous insulin secretion, </strong>its levels would help in identifying the source of insulin:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>High</strong> C-peptide -  excess insulin production (<strong>insulinoma</strong>)</li>\n<li>Normal/ low C-peptide - exogenous insulin administration (factitious disorder)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The precursor molecule of insulin is <strong>pre-proinsulin</strong>, which gets cleaved to form <strong>proinsulin</strong>. This proinsulin gets cleaved to form <strong>insulin</strong> and <strong>C-peptide</strong> (by-product). Hence, both are secreted in <strong>equimolar quantities</strong> during insulin secretion. Hence, the level of C-peptide corresponds to the level of endogenous insulin.</p>\n<p>The levels of<strong> C-peptide decrease</strong> with the <strong>loss of β-cell</strong> mass in type 1 diabetes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5538","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following chromosomal an​omalies are least likely ​to develop diabetes melli‌tus?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following chromosomal anomalies are least likely to develop diabetes mellitus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trisomy of c‌hromosome 21","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"XXY ka‌ryotype","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"XO kar‌yotype","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trisomy of c‌hromosome 18","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Trisomy of chromosome 18","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Trisomy of chromosome 18</strong></p><p><strong>Edward's syndrome</strong> or <strong>trisomy 18</strong> is not associated with diabetes mellitus.</p>\n<p>Genetic syndromes associated with <strong>diabetes mellitus</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Down's</strong> syndrome (trisomy 21)</li>\n<li><strong>Klinefelter's</strong> syndrome (XXY karyotype)</li>\n<li><strong>Turner's</strong> syndrome (XO karyotype)</li>\n<li>Prader–Willi syndrome (deletion of paternal chromosome 15)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other causes of diabetes mellitus include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Genetic defects in insulin action</strong> -\n<ul>\n<li>Type A insulin resistance</li>\n<li>Lipoatrophic diabetes</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Exocrine pancreatic defects</strong> - \n<ul>\n<li>Chronic pancreatitis</li>\n<li>Pancreatectomy/trauma</li>\n<li>Neoplasia</li>\n<li>Cystic fibrosis</li>\n<li>Hemochromatosis</li>\n<li>Fibrocalculous pancreatopathy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Endocrinopathies - </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Acromegaly</li>\n<li>Cushing syndrome</li>\n<li>Hyperthyroidism</li>\n<li>Pheochromocytoma</li>\n<li>Glucagonoma</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Infections - </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Cytomegalovirus</li>\n<li>Coxsackie B virus</li>\n<li>Congenital rubella</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6875","text":"A pathologist is studying ‌a Congo red-stained slide​ of pancreatic tissue. He​ observes it under polari‌zed light and notes the f‍ollowing finding. This fi​nding is commonly associa‌ted with which of the fol​lowing conditions?","raw_text":"A pathologist is studying a Congo red-stained slide of pancreatic tissue. He observes it under polarized light and notes the following finding. This finding is commonly associated with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 1 diabe‌tes mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type 2 diabe‌tes mellitus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Maturity onset diabetes in‌ young","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type 3C diab‌etes mellitus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type 2 diabetes mellitus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e13d624c270e41d5af6d71e0eab6bdb9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type 2 diabetes mellitus</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1120\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The image shows a Congo red-stained biopsy specimen with <strong>apple-green birefringence</strong> under polarized microscopy. It is indicative of <strong>amyloid</strong> accumulation within the pancreatic islets. This is</span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> commonly associated with <strong>type 2 diabetes mellitus</strong>. </span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Amyloid deposition begins in and <strong>around</strong> <strong>capillaries</strong> and <strong>between cells</strong>. </span>In advanced stages, the islets may be virtually obliterated and <strong>fibrosis</strong> may also be observed.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6876","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a detrimental effect ​of advanced glycation end​-products in patients suf‌fering from diabetes mell‍itus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a detrimental effect of advanced glycation end-products in patients suffering from diabetes mellitus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deposition of excess basem‌ent membrane material due​ to TGF-β release","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Generation of reactive oxy‌gen species in endothelia​l cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased anticoagulant ef‌fect on endothelial cells​ and macrophages","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increase in vascular smoot‌h muscle cells and extrac​ellular matrix","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased anticoagulant effect on endothelial cells and macrophages","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased anticoagulant effect on endothelial cells and macrophages</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1118\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Advanced glycation end products (<strong>AGEs</strong>) result in increased <strong>procoagulant</strong> effects on endothelial cells and macrophages, not anticoagulant effects. </p>\n<p>AGEs are formed by <strong>nonenzymatic</strong> reactions between intracellular<strong> glucose</strong>-derived dicarbonyl precursors with the amino groups of <strong>proteins</strong>. They bind to a specific receptor (<strong>RAGE</strong>) that is expressed on inflammatory cells (macrophages and T cells), endothelium, and vascular smooth muscle.</p>\n<p>The detrimental effects of the AGE-RAGE signalling axis within the vascular compartment include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Release of cytokines and growth factors, including <strong>TGF-β</strong> - leads to deposition of <strong>excess basement membrane</strong> material</li>\n<li>Generation of<strong> reactive oxygen species</strong> in endothelial cells.</li>\n<li>Increased procoagulant activity on endothelial cells and macrophages.</li>\n<li>Enhanced proliferation of vascular<strong> smooth muscle</strong> cells and synthesis of <strong>extracellular matrix</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>These effects are responsible for the <strong>microvascular</strong> and <strong>macrovascular</strong> <strong>complications</strong> in diabetes mellitus.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5549","text":"What is the most common pa‌ncreatic endocrine neopla​sm?","raw_text":"What is the most common pancreatic endocrine neoplasm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gluca‌gonoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insul‌inoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gastr‌inoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"VIP‌oma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Insulinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Insulinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Insulinoma</strong> (β­-cell tumors) is the <strong>most common</strong> pancreatic endocrine neoplasm. It produces sufficient insulin to induce clinically significant <strong>hypoglycemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>The three most common and distinctive clinical syndromes associated with functional pancreatic endocrine neoplasms are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperinsulinism</li>\n<li>Zollinger–Ellison syndrome (hypergastrinemia)</li>\n<li>Multiple endocrine neoplasias (MEN)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6878","text":"A 39-year-old man was refe‌rred to a higher center f​or further evaluation of ​peptic ulcer disease refr‌actory to high doses of p‍roton pump inhibitors. Th​ere is a history of pitui‌tary and parathyroid tumo​rs in his family members.​ Serum gastrin level was ​found to be 1690 pg/ml (n​ormal &lt; 100) and a neuroe​ndocrine tumor is suspect‍ed. Which of the followin​g is false about this con​dition?","raw_text":"A 39-year-old man was referred to a higher center for further evaluation of peptic ulcer disease refractory to high doses of proton pump inhibitors. There is a history of pituitary and parathyroid tumors in his family members. Serum gastrin level was found to be 1690 pg/ml (normal &lt; 100) and a neuroendocrine tumor is suspected. Which of the following is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is associated with intr‌actable jejunal ulcers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"This patient likely has a ‌solitary tumor","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Most tumors are locally in‌vasive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tumors usually originate i‌n Passaro's triangle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. This patient likely has a solitary tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. This patient likely has a solitary tumor</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>gastrin-producing tumor</strong> or gastrinoma (<strong>Zollinger-Ellison</strong> syndrome). The family history of pituitary and parathyroid tumors is suggestive of <strong>MEN1 syndrome</strong>. This syndrome is associated with <strong>multifocal</strong> <strong>gastrinomas</strong>.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, <strong>sporadic</strong> gastrinomas are usually<strong> single</strong>.</p>\n<p>Gastrinomas usually arise in the duodenum, peripancreatic soft tissues, pancreas. The site of origin is the <strong>duodenum</strong> when ZES is associated with MEN1. Most tumors are <strong>locally invasive</strong> and metastasize early. </p>\n<p>Hypergastrinemia gives rise to extreme gastric acid secretion and peptic ulceration, that is refractory to proton pump inhibitors. It results in <strong>multiple</strong> <strong>ulcers</strong> in duodenum, stomach, and uncommon areas like <strong>jejunum.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5551","text":"A patient with longstandin‌g diabetes mellitus came ​with complaints of bulky ​stools. Ultrasound abdome‌n showed the presence of ‍multiple gallstones. Hype​rsecretion of which of th‌e following leads to this​ presentation?","raw_text":"A patient with longstanding diabetes mellitus came with complaints of bulky stools. Ultrasound abdomen showed the presence of multiple gallstones. Hypersecretion of which of the following leads to this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Somato‌statin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gluc‌agon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amy‌lin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Growth ‌hormone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Somatostatin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Somatostatin</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of a δ-cell tumor or <strong>somatostatinoma</strong>. It results in hypersecretion of <strong>somatostatin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Somatostatin <strong>inhibits</strong> <strong>pancreatic</strong> and <strong>biliary</strong> secretions. Patients with somatostatinoma usually present with vague abdominal pain along with the triad of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gallstones -</strong> due to bile stasis</li>\n<li><strong>Diabetes mellitus - </strong>due to inhibition of insulin secretion</li>\n<li><strong>Steatorrhea -</strong> due to inhibition of pancreatic exocrine secretion and bile secretion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5550","text":"A patient presented with p‌rofuse watery diarrhoea a​nd muscle cramps for the ​past few weeks. The diarr‌hoea was intractable to a‍ntibiotics and stool cult​ure was negative for orga‌nisms. He also complained​ of indigestion and frequ​ent bloating. On investig​ation, he was found to ha​ve microcytic hypochromic​ anaemia. Serum potassium‍ was 2.4 mmol/L. Upper GI​ endoscopy was normal. Ex​cess of which of the foll‌owing hormones leads to t‌his condition?","raw_text":"A patient presented with profuse watery diarrhoea and muscle cramps for the past few weeks. The diarrhoea was intractable to antibiotics and stool culture was negative for organisms. He also complained of indigestion and frequent bloating. On investigation, he was found to have microcytic hypochromic anaemia. Serum potassium was 2.4 mmol/L. Upper GI endoscopy was normal. Excess of which of the following hormones leads to this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"V‌IP","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Gas‌trin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sero‌tonin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pancreatic ‌polypeptide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. VIP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. VIP</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>WDHA syndrome. </strong>It is due to<strong> hypersecretion</strong> of <strong>VIP</strong> (vasoactive-intestinal peptide).</p>\n<p>WDHA syndrome includes the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>W</strong>atery <strong>d</strong>iarrhea</li>\n<li><strong>H</strong>ypokalemia (Serum K<sup>+</sup>&lt; 3.5 mmol/L)</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>chlorhydria (bloating, indigestion, iron deficiency)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Neural crest tumors</strong>, such as neuroblastomas, ganglioneuroblastoma, ganglioneuromas, and pheochromocytomas can also be associated with VIPoma syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"gOGcZNDu","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"61_respiratory_system","title":"61.Respiratory System","num_questions":31,"total_marks":124,"duration":41,"time_per_question":79.35,"questions":[{"id":"MA8702","text":"Which of the following is ‍a long-acting β-2 agonist​ that can be prescribed i​n a patient with moderate‌ly severe bronchial asthm‌a?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a long-acting β-2 agonist that can be prescribed in a patient with moderately severe bronchial asthma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Salbu‍tamol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Indac‍aterol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Terbu‍taline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Formo‍terol","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Formoterol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Formoterol</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Formoterol </strong>is a <strong>long-acting β-2 agonist</strong> (LABA).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Beta 2 agonists are classified based on their duration of action into: </p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Short-acting β<strong>-</strong>2 agonists (<strong>SABA</strong>):\n<ul>\n<li>Salbutamol</li>\n<li>Terbutaline </li>\n<li>Pirbuterol</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Long-acting β<strong>-</strong>2 agonists (<strong>LABA</strong>):\n<ul>\n<li>Salmeterol</li>\n<li>Formoterol</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Very long-acting β<strong>-</strong>2 agonists (<strong>V-LABA</strong>):\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Indacaterol</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Olodaterol</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Vilanterol</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8707","text":"What is the current GINA r‍ecommendation for the pre​scription of long-acting ​β-2 agonists in patients ‌suffering from bronchial ‌asthma?","raw_text":"What is the current GINA recommendation for the prescription of long-acting β-2 agonists in patients suffering from bronchial asthma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"As mono‍therapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"In combination with an inh‍aled anticholinergic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"In combination with an inh‍aled corticosteroid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"In combination with a shor‍t acting β-2 agonist","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. In combination with an inhaled corticosteroid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. In combination with an inhaled corticosteroid</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>LABA</strong> is used in <strong>combination </strong>with<strong> inhalational corticosteroids</strong> (ICS),<strong> </strong>preferably in the form of a combination inhaler.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">LABA is never taken alone as it does not treat the underlying chronic inflammation of asthma. Corticosteroids will decrease the underlying inflammation and halt the progression of the disease.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Short-acting β-2 agonists (<strong>SABA</strong>) can be given as an add-on therapy to the LABA-ICS combination (<strong>as-needed therapy</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4382","text":"Which among the following ‍is the most appropriate t​herapy for bronchodilatio​n in acute severe asthma?‌","raw_text":"Which among the following is the most appropriate therapy for bronchodilation in acute severe asthma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intravenous ‍aminophylline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhalational anticholinerg‍ics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nebuliz‍ed SABA","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Intraven‍ous SABA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nebulized SABA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nebulized SABA</strong></p><p><strong>Nebulized short-acting beta-2 agonists</strong> (SABAs) are the most appropriate therapy in <strong>acute severe asthma</strong> among the given options. </p>\n<p><strong>Nebulized route </strong>is preferred over the intravenous route as it is <strong>easier</strong> and<strong> safer </strong>with equivalent efficacy.</p>\n<p>Intravenous aminophylline is less effective than SABA and is reserved for those who are resistant or intolerant to beta-2 agonists with acute severe asthma. </p>\n<p>Inhalational anticholinergics are not indicated in acute severe asthma as they are less effective than SABAs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3492","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a side effect of salb​utamol?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a side effect of salbutamol?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypogl‍ycemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypok‍alemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tre‍mors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tachy‍cardia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hypoglycemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypoglycemia</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Hypoglycemia is not a side effect of <strong>salbutamol</strong>. It causes <strong>hyperglycemia.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The side effects of salbutamol are due to the <strong>stimulation</strong> of <strong>extrapulmonary β<sub>2 </sub>receptors. </strong>They are more common after oral or intravenous<strong> </strong>administration. The side effects seen are:    </p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Muscle tremor</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Tachycardia</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Hypokalemia </li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Restlessness        </li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Hypoxemia         </li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Increase in free fatty acid, insulin, glucose, pyruvate, and lactate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4390","text":"An asthmatic patient compl‍ains of wheezing and shor​tness of breath most days​ of the week. Which of th‌e following therapies is ‌preferred in him?","raw_text":"An asthmatic patient complains of wheezing and shortness of breath most days of the week. Which of the following therapies is preferred in him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhale‍d SABA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhale‍d LABA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhaled LABA + inhaled cor‍ticosteroids","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inhaled SABA + inhaled cor‍ticosteroids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inhaled LABA + inhaled corticosteroids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inhaled LABA + inhaled corticosteroids</strong></p><p class=\"p1\" style=\"text-align: justify;\">This patient has <strong>persistent asthma </strong>for which<strong> </strong>the preferred therapy<strong> </strong>is<strong> LABA with ICS </strong>available as a<strong> combination inhaler.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">ICS should be started in any patient who needs to use a short-acting beta-2 agonist inhaler for symptomatic control more than twice a week (persistent asthma). </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4389","text":"A 5-year-old child require‍s bronchodilator therapy.​ You attach the device sh​own below to the pressuri‌zed metered-dose inhaler ‌for ease of use. Which of​ the following is false a‌bout this device?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old child requires bronchodilator therapy. You attach the device shown below to the pressurized metered-dose inhaler for ease of use. Which of the following is false about this device?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreases the deposition o‍f drug on the oropharynx","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increases the delivery of ‍drug to lower airways","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increases the incidence of‍ oropharyngeal candidiasi​s","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Systemic absorption of dru‍gs is unaffected","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increases the incidence of oropharyngeal candidiasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c79df03efd6d4a87baad5dad87dd05de.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e51d99cce336454585713400e71a010cx720x540.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf3ec9228e96415da2ec3c3932577099x596x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3fe2da00a2b7445d8a99ec027efeab5ax799x533.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2687fee5df024653908a0728befd85afx1024x683.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increases the incidence of oropharyngeal candidiasis</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The given image is of a <strong>spacer device</strong>. It <strong>decreases</strong> the incidence of oropharyngeal <strong>candidiasis</strong> and other propellant-mediated oropharyngeal adverse effects like <strong>dysphonia</strong> that occur with the use of a pMDI.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Spacers reduce the velocity and size of particles entering the upper airways. This<strong> reduces</strong> the <strong>amount</strong> of drug that impinges on the<strong> oropharynx</strong> and <strong>increases </strong>the proportion of<strong> drug</strong> inhaled into <strong>lower airways.</strong> The <strong>systemic absorption</strong> is <strong>unchanged</strong> by the use of a spacer.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Spacer devices are <strong>useful</strong> in delivering inhaled drugs to <strong>children</strong> who are not able to use a pMDI alone but can also be used by <strong>adults</strong> for better drug delivery. The image below shows the use of a spacer device with a face mask which is preferred in children younger than 3 years of age.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The various devices used for the delivery of inhalational drugs are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pressurized metered-dose inhalers -</strong> Drugs are propelled from a canister in the pMDI with the aid of a propellant. These devices are convenient, portable, and typically deliver 50–200 doses of a drug</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dry powder inhalers - </strong>these devices scatter the drug into a fine powder that is dispersed by air turbulence on inhalation. DPIs have been developed to deliver drugs such as insulin, but the dosing is inconsistent. Children &lt;7 years find it difficult to use a DPI. </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nebulizers:</strong> Two types of nebulizer are available -\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Jet nebulizers</strong> that are driven by a stream of gas (air or oxygen)</li>\n<li><strong>Ultrasonic nebulizers</strong> that use a rapidly vibrating piezoelectric crystal and thus do not require a source of compressed gas.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The nebulizers deliver much <strong>higher drug doses</strong> as compared pMDIs. These are useful in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Treating acute exacerbations of asthma and COPD</li>\n<li>Extreme airway obstruction (severe COPD)</li>\n<li>Delivering inhaled drugs to infants and small children who cannot use the other inhalation devices</li>\n<li>Administering drugs such as antibiotics in relatively high doses</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8714","text":"An asthmatic patient has b‍een taking theophylline t​ablets for symptomatic re​lief. On which of the fol‌lowing receptors does thi‌s drug have an antagonist​ic action?","raw_text":"An asthmatic patient has been taking theophylline tablets for symptomatic relief. On which of the following receptors does this drug have an antagonistic action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Histamine‍ receptors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bradykinin‍ receptors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenosine‍ receptors","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Kallikrein‍ receptors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Adenosine receptors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Adenosine receptors</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Theophylline</strong> has an<strong> antagonistic </strong>action on<strong> adenosine receptors.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Theophylline is a <strong>methylxanthine. </strong>It acts as a <strong>non-selective PDE inhibitor</strong>. Its other actions include: </p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: left;\">\n<li>Increased IL-10 release</li>\n<li>Decrease in gene transcription of pro-inflammatory cytokines</li>\n<li>Promotes apoptosis of granulocytes</li>\n<li>Histone deacetylase activation. The recruitment of histone deacetylase-2 by glucocorticoid receptors switches off inflammatory genes. Through this mechanism, theophylline <strong>enhances </strong>the <strong>anti-inflammatory effects</strong> of<strong> corticosteroids</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Theophylline can be used as a <strong>bronchodilator</strong> in an <strong>asthmatic</strong> patient on ICS as an <strong>add-on</strong> drug. LABAs are more effective but theophylline is a <strong>cheaper</strong> option.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8729","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements about theophyllin​e is not correct?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about theophylline is not correct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase in dose is requir‍ed in cardiopulmonary dis​ease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decreases the breakdown of‍ cAMP in smooth muscles","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increase in dose is requir‍ed in smokers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Is a non-selective phospho‍diesterase inhibitor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increase in dose is required in cardiopulmonary disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increase in dose is required in cardiopulmonary disease</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">A <strong>decrease</strong> in<strong> dose</strong> of theophylline is required in <strong>cardiopulmonary</strong> disease. This is because<strong> reduced clearance </strong>of theophylline is seen in liver disease, pneumonia, and heart failure.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In smokers, <strong>clearance </strong>of theophylline is<strong> increased</strong> due to the <strong>induction of CYP1A2</strong> enzyme. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Theophylline is a <strong>non-selective</strong> phosphodiesterase inhibitor that <strong>decreases</strong> the <strong>breakdown of cAMP</strong> levels in smooth muscles resulting in bronchodilation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4387","text":"Which of the following is ‍an indication for theophy​lline?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an indication for theophylline?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supraventricular tachycard‍ia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epil‍epsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute sev‍ere asthma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apnea of p‍rematurity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Apnea of prematurity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Apnea of prematurity</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Theophylline</strong> is indicated in the treatment of <strong>apnea of prematurity</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In apnea of prematurity, methylxanthines such as theophylline and caffeine <strong>increase</strong> the <strong>central respiratory drive</strong> by lowering the threshold of response to hypercapnia. They also enhance the contractility of the diaphragm and prevent diaphragmatic fatigue.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Options A &amp; B - The adenosine receptor antagonism property of theophylline <strong>precipitates </strong>cardiac<strong> arrhythmias </strong>and epileptic<strong> seizures</strong>. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option C - Theophylline is <strong>no longer recommended</strong> as a treatment for <strong>acute severe asthma</strong> by the latest guidelines (GINA 2021) because of its poor efficacy and safety profile. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8736","text":"A patient with severe pers‍istent asthma continues t​o have symptoms despite b​eing on high-dose ICS-LAB‌A therapy. Which of the f‌ollowing is a leukotriene​ receptor antagonist, app‌ropriate as an add-on the‍rapy for this patient?","raw_text":"A patient with severe persistent asthma continues to have symptoms despite being on high-dose ICS-LABA therapy. Which of the following is a leukotriene receptor antagonist, appropriate as an add-on therapy for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dupi‍lumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Roflu‍milast","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Zafir‍lukast","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Zile‍uton","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Zafirlukast","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Zafirlukast</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Zafirlukast </strong>and montelukast are <strong>leukotriene receptor antagonists</strong>. They are competitive antagonists of the cysLT1 receptors. These drugs act by: </p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Decreasing bronchoconstriction</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Reducing airway mucus secretion</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Decreasing vascular permeability and</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Reducing eosinophil chemotaxis </li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option A: Dupilumab is an interleukin-4 receptor antagonist used in severe asthma. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option B: Roflumilast is a PDE-4 selective inhibitor used in the treatment of COPD.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option D: Zileuton is a lipoxygenase inhibitor that can be used in the treatment of asthma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4377","text":"The use of which of the fo‍llowing therapies for acu​te asthma requires serial​ monitoring of liver func‌tion tests? Zileuton Salb‌utamol Salmeterol Zafirlu​kast Montelukast","raw_text":"<p>The use of which of the following therapies for acute asthma requires serial monitoring of liver function tests?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Zileuton</li>\n<li>Salbutamol</li>\n<li>Salmeterol</li>\n<li>Zafirlukast</li>\n<li>Montelukast</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 a‍nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 a‍nd 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 ‍and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 4 ‍and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 4 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 4 and 5</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Zileuton, montelukast, </strong>and<strong> zafirlukast </strong>are all associated with rare instances of<strong> hepatic dysfunction.  </strong>Liver function tests should be monitored during the course of their therapy.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Therapy with selective β-2 agonists (salbutamol, salmeterol) does not require serial monitoring of LFTs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8774","text":"An elderly asthmatic on tr‍eatment presented with fe​ver, malaise, and lower l​imb sensory deficit. Sign‌ificant investigations ar‌e given below. Which of t​he following drugs is ass‌ociated with these findin‍gs? CBC: eosinophilia HRC‌T chest: bilateral ground‌-glass attenuation Bronch​oalveolar lavage: 14.6% e‍osinophils Nasal mucosa b‌iopsy: granulomas Serum p‌-ANCA: positive","raw_text":"<p>An elderly asthmatic on treatment presented with fever, malaise, and lower limb sensory deficit. Significant investigations are given below. Which of the following drugs is associated with these findings?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>CBC: eosinophilia</li>\n<li>HRCT chest: bilateral ground-glass attenuation</li>\n<li>Bronchoalveolar lavage: 14.6% eosinophils </li>\n<li>Nasal mucosa biopsy: granulomas </li>\n<li>Serum p-ANCA: positive </li>\n</ul>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Monte‍lukast","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cromoly‍n sodium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Theoph‍ylline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Keto‍tifen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Montelukast","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Montelukast</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The given scenario points towards a diagnosis of <strong>Churg-Strauss syndrome</strong>. It is an <strong>eosinophilic vasculitis</strong> associated with the use of <strong>montelukast </strong>and zafirlukast. This is a very <strong>rare</strong>ly reported side effect of these drugs.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">These agents <strong>block</strong> the effects of the <strong>cysLTs</strong> (LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4) but they do not block the effect of LTB4, which is a chemoattractant for eosinophils and neutrophils. This can lead to eosinophilia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8737","text":"An asthmatic patient was b‍rought to the ER in an ag​itated state. His respira​tory rate was 32/min and ‌pulse was 124/min. Pulse ‌oximetry revealed a SpO2 ​of 88% on room air. Which‌ of the following drugs i‍s not useful in this scen‌ario?","raw_text":"An asthmatic patient was brought to the ER in an agitated state. His respiratory rate was 32/min and pulse was 124/min. Pulse oximetry revealed a SpO2 of 88% on room air. Which of the following drugs is not useful in this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Salbu‍tamol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hydroco‍rtisone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cromoly‍n sodium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ipratropi‍um bromide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cromolyn sodium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cromolyn sodium</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute severe asthma</strong> and cromolyn sodium is not useful in its treatment. It is <strong>indicated only</strong> for<strong> chronic prophylaxis.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Short-acting beta 2 agonists (<strong>SABA</strong>) and<strong> intravenous hydrocortisone</strong> are used in the treatment of acute severe asthma.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Ipratropium bromide</strong> can also be given in severe asthma exacerbations. It should be given after beta 2-agonists as they have a slower onset of bronchodilatory action. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8739","text":"Omalizumab is added as a s‍tep-up therapy in a child​ with severe asthma, refr​actory to treatment. Whic‌h of the following is tru‌e about this drug?","raw_text":"Omalizumab is added as a step-up therapy in a child with severe asthma, refractory to treatment. Which of the following is true about this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is administered intravenou‍sly","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Has anti interleukin-5 act‍ion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Is indicated in acute seve‍re asthma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Is a monoclonal antibody a‍gainst IgE","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Is a monoclonal antibody against IgE","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Is a monoclonal antibody against IgE</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Omalizumab</strong> is a monoclonal <strong>antibody against IgE.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">It is used only in patients with <strong>very severe asthma</strong> (not acute severe asthma) who show poor symptom control even with step 4/5 measures as per GINA 2021 guidelines.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">It can only be given to patients aged<strong> ≥6 years</strong>. It neutralizes circulating IgE and inhibits mast cell degranulation caused by IgE. It is administered <strong>subcutaneously</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Another drug used in severe asthma is <strong>mepolizumab</strong>. It is a monoclonal antibody acting <strong>against IL-5</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4393","text":"A patient diagnosed with C‍hurg-Strauss syndrome is ​started on Mepolizumab. T​his drug acts against:","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with Churg-Strauss syndrome is started on Mepolizumab. This drug acts against:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I‍gE","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I‍gA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IL‍-5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"IL‍-12","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. IL-5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. IL-5</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Mepolizumab </strong>is a monoclonal antibody <strong>against IL-5. </strong>It is indicated in: </p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Selected (eosinophilic phenotype) patients with <strong>severe asthma</strong></li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Churg-Strauss syndrome </li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Hypereosinophilic syndrome </li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">IL-5 plays a pivotal role in <strong>eosinophilic inflammation</strong> and is also involved in eosinophil survival and priming.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Monoclonal antibodies against IL-5 approved for asthma are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mepolizumab</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Reslizumab</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Benralizumab</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7445","text":"What is the thromboxane re‍ceptor antagonist used in​ the treatment of asthma?​","raw_text":"What is the thromboxane receptor antagonist used in the treatment of asthma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serat‍rodast","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Riman‍obant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sacub‍itril","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Roflu‍milast","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Seratrodast","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Seratrodast</strong></p><p><strong>Seratrodast </strong>is a<strong> thromboxane receptor antagonist </strong>indicated for use in<strong> long term prophylaxis of asthma</strong>. It is available and approved in India.</p>\n<p>Option B- <strong>Rimonabant</strong> is a <strong>cannabinoid receptor antagonist</strong> that was investigated for weight gain but now withdrawn due to adverse psychological effects </p>\n<p>Option C- <strong>Sacubitril</strong> is a <strong>neprilysin inhibitor</strong> approved in fixed-dose combination with valsartan for <strong>heart failure</strong> patients.</p>\n<p>Option D- <strong>Roflumilast</strong> is a <strong>phosphodiesterase 4 inhibitor</strong> which reduces eosinophil infiltration and inhibits inflammatory cell ,effective against smoke induced inflammation and emphysema.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9236","text":"In a case of acute exacerb‍ation of asthma, salbutam​ol was given, however, no​ improvement was noticed.‌ Intravenous corticostero‌ids and aminophylline wer​e given and the condition‌ improved. What is the pr‍obable mechanism by which‌ the corticosteroids woul‌d have improved the condi​tion?","raw_text":"In a case of acute exacerbation of asthma, salbutamol was given, however, no improvement was noticed. Intravenous corticosteroids and aminophylline were given and the condition improved. What is the probable mechanism by which the corticosteroids would have improved the condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Corticosteroids increase b‍ronchial responsiveness t​o salbutamol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Corticosteroids cause dire‍ct bronchodilatation when​ used with xanthines","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Corticosteroids indirectly‍ increase the effect of x​anthines on adenosine rec​eptor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Corticosteroids increase m‍ucociliary clearance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Corticosteroids increase bronchial responsiveness to salbutamol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Corticosteroids increase bronchial responsiveness to salbutamol</strong></p><p><strong>Corticosteroids</strong> improved the condition by <strong>increasing </strong>the <strong>airway responsiveness</strong> to<strong> salbutamol</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mechanism of action of corticosteroids in an acute asthma attack:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increases responsiveness to β-2 agonists</li>\n<li>Decreases mucous secretion</li>\n<li>Decreases inflammatory cell response.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Steroids increase</strong> the <strong>transcription of β-2 receptor genes</strong> and hence increases responsiveness to β-2 agonists.</p>\n<p><strong>β-2 agonists</strong> increase the effect of steroids by <strong>enhancing</strong> the <strong>action</strong> of <strong>glucocorticoid receptors.</strong></p>\n<p>Theophylline increases the effects of steroids by activating the enzyme histone deacetylase which is the target for steroids as well.</p>\n<p>Note: As per recent GINA 2021 guidelines, aminophyllines are no longer preferred in the management of acute severe asthma due to their low efficacy and poor safety profile.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8743","text":"A patient has had 2 acute ‍exacerbations of asthma o​ver the span of 1-month d​espite being on therapy. ‌Which of the following is‌ a systemic steroid that ​can be used to treat her?‌","raw_text":"A patient has had 2 acute exacerbations of asthma over the span of 1-month despite being on therapy. Which of the following is a systemic steroid that can be used to treat her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cicle‍sonide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Beclome‍thasone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Predni‍solone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Budes‍onide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Prednisolone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Prednisolone</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Prednisolone </strong>is a <strong>systemic steroid </strong>used for <strong>maintenance therapy </strong>after an acute exacerbation of asthma.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Intravenous corticosteroids such as hydrocortisone are indicated if the lung function is predicted to be &lt;30% or if there's poor response to nebulized β<sub>2 </sub>agonists. After the exacerbation, oral predisolone can be substituted with maximal beneficial effect at 30-40mg daily.</p>\n<p><strong>Inhaled corticosteroids</strong> commonly used in asthma are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Beclomethasone dipropionate</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Budesonide</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Ciclesonide</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Fluticasone</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Mometasone</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8744","text":"An asthmatic patient prese‍nted to the ER with dyspn​ea and wheezing. Intake o​f which of the following ‌is unlikely to have preci‌pitated this?","raw_text":"An asthmatic patient presented to the ER with dyspnea and wheezing. Intake of which of the following is unlikely to have precipitated this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenylb‍utazone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Napr‍oxen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Caff‍eine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Asp‍irin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Caffeine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Caffeine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of an<strong> acute exacerbation</strong> of <strong>asthma</strong>. Caffeine does not precipitate this.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Caffeine</strong> is a <strong>dietary xanthine</strong> similar in structure to <strong>methylxanthines</strong> (theophylline) which are used as adjuvants in the <strong>treatment</strong> of asthma. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>NSAIDs</strong> (like phenylbutazone, naproxen, and aspirin) inhibit the cyclooxygenase pathway thereby diverting arachidonic acid towards the lipoxygenase pathway. This <strong>increases</strong> the level of<strong> pro-inflammatory leukotrienes</strong> and can<strong> precipitate</strong> an <strong>acute asthma</strong> attack.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8745","text":"A patient with mild episod‍ic asthma is prescribed d​aily low-dose inhaled bud​esonide therapy. Which of‌ the following conditions‌ is he not at increased r​isk of developing?","raw_text":"A patient with mild episodic asthma is prescribed daily low-dose inhaled budesonide therapy. Which of the following conditions is he not at increased risk of developing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dysp‍honia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmon‍ary TB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oropharyngea‍l candidiasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carcinom‍a of lung","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Carcinoma of lung","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Carcinoma of lung</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Inhaled corticosteroids </strong>(ICS) like <strong>budesonide</strong> do not increase the risk of developing carcinoma of the lung.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Common problems with the use of ICS: </p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Hoarseness</strong> of voice</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Weakness of voice (<strong>dysphonia</strong>)</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Increased risk of bacterial and mycobacterial lung <strong>infection</strong></li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Oropharyngeal <strong>candidiasis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4392","text":"An elderly man with a 30-y‍ear history of smoking co​mplains of breathing diff​iculty. A diagnosis of CO‌PD is made, and treatment‌ with a long-acting musca​rinic antagonist is initi‌ated. Which of the follow‍ing will you not prescrib‌e?","raw_text":"An elderly man with a 30-year history of smoking complains of breathing difficulty. A diagnosis of COPD is made, and treatment with a long-acting muscarinic antagonist is initiated.  Which of the following will you not prescribe?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glycopy‍rronium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Umecli‍dinium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Iprat‍ropium","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tiotr‍opium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ipratropium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ipratropium</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Ipratropium</strong> is a <strong>short-acting muscarinic antagonist </strong>which is not used for the long-term management of COPD. It can be used to treat <strong>acute exacerbations.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The long-acting muscarinic antagonists (<strong>LAMAs</strong>) are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Tiotropium</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Glycopyrronium </li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Umeclidinium</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Aclidinium</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8735","text":"Inhalational anticholinerg‍ics the mainstay of treat​ment in:","raw_text":"Inhalational anticholinergics the mainstay of treatment in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ast‍hma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CO‍PD","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Both a‍ and b","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Status as‍thmaticus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. COPD","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. COPD</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Inhalational anticholinergics</strong> are the mainstay for the treatment of <strong>COPD</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Ipratropium</strong> is as <strong>effective</strong> as albuterol in patients with COPD.<strong> Longer-acting antimuscarinic</strong> agents, including tiotropium, aclidinium, and umeclidinium, are approved for maintenance therapy of <strong>COPD.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Anticholinergics are less effective than β-2 agonists as bronchodilators in asthma and are not the mainstay in the treatment of asthma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4391","text":"A chronic obstructive pulm‍onary disease (COPD) pati​ent requires a step-up in​ therapy due to symptom p‌ersistence. Which PDE-4 i‌nhibitor can you prescrib​e as an add-on drug to hi‌s treatment regimen?","raw_text":"A chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) patient requires a step-up in therapy due to symptom persistence.  Which PDE-4 inhibitor can you prescribe as an add-on drug to his treatment regimen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Serat‍rodast","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Roflu‍milast","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Zile‍uton","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nedoc‍romil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Roflumilast","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Roflumilast</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Roflumilast </strong>is a <strong>PDE-4 selective inhibitor </strong>approved for use in<strong> COPD. </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">PDE-4 is the major isoform present in inflammatory cells and its inhibition results in higher <strong>anti-inflammatory</strong> effects. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option A: <strong>Seratrodast</strong> is a <strong>thromboxane receptor antagonist</strong> used in the prophylaxis of asthma. It decreases the bronchial hypersensitivity caused by thromboxane. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option C: <strong>Zileuton</strong> is a <strong>lipoxygenase inhibitor</strong> that can be used in the treatment of asthma.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Option D: <strong>Nedocromil</strong> is a <strong>mast cell stabilizer</strong> indicated in the prophylaxis of asthma. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8747","text":"Which of the following dru‍gs are antitussives? Dext​romethorphan Morphine Gua​ifenesin Carbocysteine Ga‌bapentin","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following drugs are antitussives?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Dextromethorphan</li>\n<li>Morphine</li>\n<li>Guaifenesin</li>\n<li>Carbocysteine</li>\n<li>Gabapentin</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 ‍and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2 ‍and 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3 a‍nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3 a‍nd 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 2 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 2 and 5</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Dextromethorphan, morphine,</strong> and <strong>gabapentin</strong> are all <strong>antitussives</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Dextromethorphan</strong> is an NMDA receptor antagonist that <strong>suppresses</strong> the <strong>cough center.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Opiates like <strong>morphine</strong> have a <strong>central</strong> mechanism of action on the medullary <strong>cough center</strong> and are indicated for intractable cough associated with bronchial carcinoma. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Gabapentin</strong> and pregabalin are <strong>GABA analogs</strong> that have been shown to benefit <strong>chronic idiopathic cough</strong> involving neural hypersensitivity.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Guaifenesin</strong> is an <strong>expectorant</strong> that is taken orally.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Carbocysteine</strong> is a <strong>mucolytic</strong> that reduces the disulfide bridges that bind glycoproteins to other proteins, such as albumin and secretory IgA. It is also taken orally and helps reduce the viscosity of sputum.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8750","text":"A known case of CREST synd‍rome presents with dyspne​a. On auscultation, a Gra​ham-Steele murmur is hear‌d. On further investigati‌on, her pulmonary artery ​pressure is found to be 2‌8 mm Hg. Which drug will ‍you use to manage her con‌dition?","raw_text":"A known case of CREST syndrome presents with dyspnea. On auscultation, a Graham-Steele murmur is heard. On further investigation, her pulmonary artery pressure is found to be 28 mm Hg. Which drug will you use to manage her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non selective PDE inhibito‍rs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lipoxygenas‍e inhibitors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Selective PDE-5 inhibitors‍","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cyclo-oxygenase inhibitors‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Selective PDE-5 inhibitors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Selective PDE-5 inhibitors</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The clinical scenario points to a diagnosis of <strong>pulmonary artery hypertension </strong>(pulmonary artery pressure &gt; 20mmHg) which can be managed using<strong> selective PDE-5 inhibitors.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Inhibition of PDE-5 in the pulmonary bed leads to a reduction in the hydrolysis of cGMP which causes pulmonary vasodilation.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Sildenafil </strong>and<strong> tadalafil</strong> are the PDE-5 inhibitors approved for use in PAH. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8751","text":"Which of the following gro‍ups does riociguat belong​ to?","raw_text":"Which of the following groups does riociguat belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Soluble guanylate cyclase ‍stimulator","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Soluble guanylate cyclase ‍inhibitor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Soluble adenylate cyclase ‍stimulator","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Soluble adenylate cyclase ‍inhibitor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Soluble guanylate cyclase stimulator","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Soluble guanylate cyclase stimulator</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong><strong>Riociguat </strong></strong>is a<strong><strong> soluble guanylate </strong></strong><strong>cyclase stimulator (sGC)</strong>, indicated in the treatment of <strong>pulmonary artery hypertension.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Riociguat increases the sensitivity of nitric oxide to sGC and also directly stimulates the sGC enzyme resulting in <strong>increased formation of cGMP</strong>. This leads to <strong>relaxation</strong> in the <strong>pulmonary vasculature.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8748","text":"Which of the following dru‍gs is a prostacyclin anal​og that can be added to t​he treatment regimen of a‌ patient with pulmonary a‌rtery hypertension?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is a prostacyclin analog that can be added to the treatment regimen of a patient with pulmonary artery hypertension?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Silde‍nafil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Misop‍rostol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dino‍prost","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ilop‍rost","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Iloprost","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Iloprost</strong></p><p><strong>Iloprost </strong>is a<strong> prostacyclin analog </strong>indicated in the management of <strong>pulmonary artery hypertension</strong>. It falls under the category of IP receptor (prostacyclin receptor) agonists.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Other drugs falling under the category of <strong>IP receptor agonists</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Epoprostenol</p>\n</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Treprostinil</p>\n</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Iloprost</p>\n</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Selexipag</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Sildenafil is a PDE-5 inhibitor. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option B: Misoprostol is a synthetic PGE1 analog. </p>\n<p>Option C: Dinoprost is a natural PGF2-alpha preparation. It should not be confused with dinoprostone which is a synthetic analogue of PGE2.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8749","text":"Which is a selective endot‍helin receptor A antagoni​st used in the management​ of pulmonary artery hype‌rtension?","raw_text":"Which is a selective endothelin receptor A antagonist used in the management of pulmonary artery hypertension?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bose‍ntan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ambri‍sentan","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tezos‍entan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Macit‍entan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ambrisentan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ambrisentan</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Ambrisentan</strong> is a <strong>selective</strong> endothelin receptor A (<strong>ET-A</strong>)<strong> antagonist</strong> which is used in the management of pulmonary artery hypertension (PAH).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Bosentan, tezosentan, and macitantan are non-selective (ET-A and ET-B) endothelin receptor antagonists.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4381","text":"Dornase alfa is indicated ‍in which of the following​ patients? A 30-year-old ​man with bronchial asthma‌ A 72-year-old man with C‌OPD A 19-year-old boy wit​h cystic fibrosis A 25-ye‌ar-old woman with pulmona‍ry artery hypertension A ‌newborn with refractory a‌telectasis","raw_text":"<p>Dornase alfa is indicated in which of the following patients?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A 30-year-old man with bronchial asthma</li>\n<li>A 72-year-old man with COPD</li>\n<li>A 19-year-old boy with cystic fibrosis</li>\n<li>A 25-year-old woman with pulmonary artery hypertension</li>\n<li>A newborn with refractory atelectasis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onl‍y 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, 4 ‍and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 a‍nd 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 ‍and 3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3 and 5</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Dornase alfa </strong>is indicated in patients with <strong>cystic fibrosis</strong> and as a rescue treatment for<strong> refractory atelectasis </strong>in newborn and pediatric populations.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Dornase alfa is a <strong>recombinant DNase 1 </strong>that<strong> </strong>digests neutrophil DNA deposited in the pulmonary mucus thereby <strong>reducing mucosal viscoelasticity</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0804","text":"Which of the following sho‍uld not be prescribed wit​h theophylline?","raw_text":"Which of the following should not be prescribed with theophylline?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erythr‍omycin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cefot‍axime","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cotrim‍oxazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amoxi‍cillin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Erythromycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Erythromycin</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Erythromycin</strong> should not be prescribed with theophylline.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Theophylline is mainly metabolized by CYP1A2 in the liver. Erythromycin <strong>inhibits CYP1A2</strong> and thereby <strong>decreases</strong> the <strong>clearance</strong> of<strong> theophylline</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Factors decreasing the clearance of theophylline are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Drugs - erythromycin, cimetidine, ciprofloxacin, allopurinol, fluvoxamine, zileuton, zafirlukast</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Congestive heart failure</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Liver disease</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Pneumonia</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Viral infection and vaccination</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">High-carbohydrate diet</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Old age</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6617","text":"If codiene is coming to th‍e market in combination w​ith dextromethorphan as a​n anti-tussive, this comb‌ination _______","raw_text":"If codiene is coming to the market in combination with dextromethorphan as an anti-tussive, this combination _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Should not be used due to ‍risk of addiction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Can be used as both have d‍ifferent mechanism of act​ion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Should not be used as it i‍s irrational","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Can be used to treat cough‍ effectively","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Should not be used as it is irrational","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Should not be used as it is irrational</strong></p><p>It is an <strong>irrational drug combination</strong> and should not be given.</p>\n<p><strong>Codeine</strong> is an <strong>opioid analgesic</strong> having specific antitussive action while <strong>dextromethorphan</strong> is a <strong>centrally</strong> active <strong>NMDA</strong> receptor antagonist that may be effective as an antitussive. Both are <strong>centrally acting antitussives</strong>, and do not provide any added advantage when combined. Hence it is an irrational drug combination.</p>\n<p><strong>Rational fixed-dose combination </strong>drugs for the treatment of cough usually contain:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A centrally acting anti-tussive (either codeine or dextromethorphan; not both), with</li>\n<li>Antihistamines, decongestants and expectorants.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A - Both of these drugs carry a <strong>risk of addiction,</strong> but it is not the reason to not give the drug. The main reason is that the combination is irrational.</p>\n<p>Options B &amp; D - Both codeine and dextromethorphan have<strong> different mechanisms of action</strong> and<strong> can treat cough effectively.</strong> However, the combination of two centrally acting antitussives does not provide any added advantage compared to using one drug alone. Hence, the combination is irrational.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"zNpuk3Dn","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"62_cardiovascular__lymphatic_and_nervous_systems","title":"62.Cardiovascular, Lymphatic And Nervous Systems","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA9681","text":"Fenestrated capillaries ar​e absent in_________","raw_text":"Fenestrated capillaries are absent in_________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kid​neys","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lu​ngs","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Endocrin​e glands","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Small in​testines","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lungs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/45e4a16ef39b4085a4e3cc3fec0173c2x1280x2756.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lungs</strong></p><p><strong>Fenestrated capillaries</strong> are absent in the <strong>lungs.</strong></p>\n<p>Fenestrated capillaries have <strong>gaps</strong> between endothelial cells. They are present in certain tissues that allow the passage of larger molecules such as the <strong>renal glomeruli</strong>,<strong> endocrine glands</strong>,<strong> intestinal mucosa</strong>, and <strong>pancreas</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9680","text":"Which of the following lay​ers of a large blood vess‌el wall does the vasa vas‌orum supply blood to?","raw_text":"Which of the following layers of a large blood vessel wall does the vasa vasorum supply blood to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tunica adve​ntitia only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tunica adventitia and oute​r part of Tunica media","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tunica adventitia, Tunica ​media","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tunica adventitia, Tunica ​media and outer part of T‌unica intima","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tunica adventitia and outer part of Tunica media","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ef78d6afabfc490a886c8c7a4e7daa9fx1280x923.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tunica adventitia and outer part of Tunica media</strong></p><p>The <strong>vasa vasorum</strong> (Latin: vessels of the vessels) is a network of small blood vessels that supply <strong>tunica adventitia</strong> and the outer part of <strong>tunica media</strong> of large blood vessels.</p>\n<p><strong>Blood supply of arteries:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Small arteries-</strong> The vessel wall gets nourishment by <strong>diffusion</strong> from the blood circulating in the vessel itself. When the vessel wall thickness is more than 1 mm, simple diffusion from the lumen becomes inadequate.</li>\n<li><strong>Larger arteries-</strong> They have their own vascular supply within the adventitia called vasa vasorum. It supplies the <strong>adventitia</strong> and the <strong>outer part of the tunica media</strong>. The <strong>intima </strong>and the<strong> inner part of the media </strong>of large vessels are nourished directly by <strong>diffusion</strong> from the luminal blood.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The given image shows a transverse section of the aorta showing vasa vasorum and the layers as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Tunica intima</li>\n<li>Tunica media</li>\n<li>Tunica adventitia</li>\n<li><strong>Vasa vasorum</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9684","text":"Collateral circulation ref​ers to circulation throug‌h _______","raw_text":"Collateral circulation refers to circulation through _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capil​laries","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sinu​soids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anast​omosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Plex​uses","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anastomosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eb03e8ec219849b3818af07ce3ad4e56x759x600.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anastomosis</strong></p><p><strong>Circulation </strong>through the<strong> anastomosis</strong> is called <strong>collateral</strong> <strong>circulation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Precapillary or postcapillary communication between neighbouring vessels is called anastomoses. Anastomoses are of the following types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Arterial anastomoses </strong>are communications between the arteries or branches of arteries. e.g.,<strong> palmar arch, plantar arch, </strong><strong>the circle of Willis.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Venous</strong> <strong>anastomoses</strong> are<strong> </strong>communications between the veins or tributaries of veins. e.g.,<strong> dorsal venous arches of the hand and foot. </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Arteriovenous anastomosis</strong> <strong>(shunt)</strong> are communications between an artery and a vein. e.g.,<strong> av shunts found in the skin of nose, lips, ears, mucosa of nose and intestines, </strong>and<strong> erectile tissue. </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below is a graphical representation of superficial and deep palmar arch.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9678","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true regarding‌ arteries?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding arteries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arteries always carry oxyg​enated blood","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonary artery carries o​xygenated blood","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Arteries have valves to al​low unidirectional flow","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Umbilical artery carries d​eoxygenated blood","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Umbilical artery carries deoxygenated blood","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Umbilical artery carries deoxygenated blood</strong></p><p><strong>Umbilical arteries</strong> carry <strong>deoxygenated blood</strong> from the heart to the placenta during the fetal period.</p>\n<p>Arteries carry oxygenated blood away from the heart, with the exception of the <strong>pulmonary </strong>and<strong> umbilical arteries </strong>that <strong>carry deoxygenated blood.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9679","text":"A middle-aged patient with​ recently detected hypert‌ension is started on calc‌ium channel blockers to r‌educe blood pressure by d​ecreasing the peripheral ‌vascular resistance. Whic‌h of the following vessel​s primarily contribute to​ the peripheral resistanc​e to blood flow?","raw_text":"A middle-aged patient with recently detected hypertension is started on calcium channel blockers to reduce blood pressure by decreasing the peripheral vascular resistance. Which of the following vessels primarily contribute to the peripheral resistance to blood flow?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elastic ​arteries","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Small muscu​lar arteries","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Capil​laries","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Muscular ​arterioles","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Muscular arterioles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eba41cdb48934bd7a28c5f2d536c448cx1280x1361.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Muscular arterioles</strong></p><p><strong>Muscular arterioles </strong>contribute to the major part of <strong>peripheral resistance</strong> to<strong> blood flow. </strong></p>\n<p>The smallest arteries of the muscular type are called arterioles. They have a tunica media that is relatively thicker than the tunica adventitia. <strong>Arterioles</strong> cause the <strong>largest drop</strong> in<strong> blood pressure </strong>before the blood flows into the tissue capillary beds. They are densely innervated by <strong>sympathetic fibres.</strong> The contraction of arterioles is mainly <strong>regulated by</strong> local <strong>vasoactive</strong> and <strong>metabolic factors,</strong> but central mechanisms also play a role.</p>\n<p>The following image shows a micrograph of a muscular arteriole:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9686","text":"Which of the following are​ end arteries? Renal segm‌ental artery Internal aud‌itory artery Central arte‌ry of retina vasa recta o​f mesenteric arteries. Po‌sterior cerebral artery","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are end arteries?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Renal segmental artery</li>\n<li>Internal auditory artery</li>\n<li>Central artery of retina</li>\n<li>vasa recta of mesenteric arteries.</li>\n<li>Posterior cerebral artery</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2,3,4 ​&amp;amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,2 &amp;amp​; 3 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2,3 &amp;amp​; 4 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,2,3 ​&amp;amp; 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1,2,3 & 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1,2,3 &amp; 4</strong></p><p>All the other arteries except the posterior cerebral artery are end arteries.</p>\n<p>Arteries whose terminal branches do not anastomose with branches from neighbouring arteries are called <strong>end arteries</strong> or <strong>terminal arteries</strong>. They can be of <strong>two</strong> types:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Anatomic end arteries</strong> (or true end arteries) - do not have anastomoses. e.g., <strong>central artery of retina, internal auditory artery</strong> (labyrinthine artery of the internal ear).</li>\n<li><strong>Functional end arteries </strong>- have anastomoses but they are insufficient to provide adequate blood supply when the main vessel is occluded. e.g.,<strong> segmental arteries supplying the kidney</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9682","text":"Which of the following vei​ns have valves?","raw_text":"Which of the following veins have valves?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hepati​c veins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior​ venacava","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cerebra​l veins","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Testicul​ar veins","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Testicular veins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3134b93cc0c1421692a2870c794452bcx400x450.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Testicular veins</strong></p><p>The <strong>testicular veins</strong> have <strong>valves.</strong></p>\n<p>Valves within the veins maintain a <strong>unidirectional flow</strong> of <strong>blood,</strong> even against gravity. <strong>Congenital absence</strong> of valves in testicular veins may be a predisposing factor for the development of <strong>varicocele.</strong> The <strong>inferior vena cava</strong> also has a valve (the <strong>Eustachian valve)</strong> to direct the flow of oxygenated blood towards the foramen ovale to escape the pulmonary circulation. After birth, with the establishment of the pulmonary circulation and the increase in systemic vascular resistance driving the closure of foramen ovale, the valve has no function and tends to <strong>regress.</strong></p>\n<p>The valves are absent in the following veins:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Veins less than 2 mm in diameter</li>\n<li>Venae cavae</li>\n<li>Hepatic, renal, uterine, ovarian, cerebral, spinal, pulmonary, and umbilical veins.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image is a histological section showing the valve within a vein:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9683","text":"In which of the following ​structures are the sinuso‌ids not present?","raw_text":"In which of the following structures are the sinusoids not present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supraren​al glands","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parathyro​id glands","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parotid​ glands","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Caroti​d body","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Parotid glands","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Parotid glands</strong></p><p><strong>Sinusoids</strong> are <strong>absent</strong> in <strong>parotid glands.</strong></p>\n<p>Sinusoids are large, irregular, vascular spaces with <strong>true discontinuities</strong> in their walls. This allows close contact between blood and parenchyma of the surrounding organ. </p>\n<p>Sinusoids are present in: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Liver </li>\n<li>Spleen </li>\n<li>Bone marrow </li>\n<li>Suprarenal glands </li>\n<li>Adenohypophysis</li>\n<li>Parathyroid glands </li>\n<li>Carotid body</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9687","text":"Compared to their correspo​nding arteries, the veins‌ do not have","raw_text":"Compared to their corresponding arteries, the veins do not have","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Larger​ lumen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Val​ves","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thinne​r walls","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"More elas​tic tissue","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. More elastic tissue","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d409246aa37d4fb48174bb71910c4933x1280x1735.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. More elastic tissue</strong></p><p>Compared to their corresponding arteries, the veins have a much <strong>lesser muscular</strong> and <strong>elastic content.</strong> This explains the venous pressures being lower than the arterial pressures.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 1078px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 528.188px; text-align: center;\">Comparison of arteries and veins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 528.188px; text-align: center;\">Arteries</td>\n<td style=\"width: 532.812px; text-align: center;\">Veins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 528.188px;\">Carry oxygenated blood away from the heart except for pulmonary artery</td>\n<td style=\"width: 532.812px;\">Carry deoxygenated blood towards the heart except for pulmonary veins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 528.188px;\">Thick-walled,highly muscular except arteries of cranium and vertebral column</td>\n<td style=\"width: 532.812px;\">Thin-walled</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 528.188px;\">Narrow lumen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 532.812px;\">Wide lumen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 528.188px;\">Valves absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 532.812px;\">Valves present to maintain unidirectional blood flow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 528.188px;\">High-pressure flow</td>\n<td style=\"width: 532.812px;\">Low-pressure flow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 528.188px;\">Reddish in colour</td>\n<td style=\"width: 532.812px;\">Bluish in colour</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The following image shows a graphical illustration of the structure of the vessels (a &amp; b) and a micrograph of the cross-sections(c):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9685","text":"Which of the following ves​sels belong to the circul‌atory system referred to ‌as the drainage system of‌ coarse type? Saphenous v​ein Thoracic duct Brachio‌cephalic vein Right lymph‌atic duct Aorta","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following vessels belong to the circulatory system referred to as the drainage system of coarse type?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Saphenous vein</li>\n<li>Thoracic duct</li>\n<li>Brachiocephalic vein</li>\n<li>Right lymphatic duct</li>\n<li>Aorta</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 &amp;a​mp; 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 &amp;a​mp; 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 &amp;a​mp; 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"5 o​nly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2 & 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2 &amp; 4</strong></p><p>The thoracic and right lymphatic ducts are vessels of the <strong>lymphatic system</strong> which is referred to as the <strong>drainage system of coarse type.</strong></p>\n<p>The lymphatic system helps <strong>remove larger particles</strong> such as proteins and particulate matter from the tissue fluid. Therefore, they are considered as the drainage system of coarse type.</p>\n<p>The <strong>venous system</strong> is considered as the <strong>drainage system of fine type</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9688","text":"Which of the following lym​phoid tissue has both aff‌erent and efferent lympha‌tics?","raw_text":"Which of the following lymphoid tissue has both afferent and efferent lymphatics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spl​een","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thy​mus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymph​ node","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ton​sil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lymph node","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f4ea04b4dee747e5a1887776353281e6x599x499.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lymph node</strong></p><p>The<strong> lymph nodes</strong> have both <strong>afferent</strong> and <strong>efferent lymphatics</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The afferent lymphatics enter the lymph node at different parts of its periphery.</li>\n<li>The efferent lymphatics exit at the hilum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows a schematic representation of a lymph node:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>The tonsils</strong>,<strong> thymus</strong>, and the<strong> spleen </strong>have <strong>no afferent lymphatics</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9689","text":"An 86-year-old woman prese​nted with sepsis and a ma‌ssive right pleural effus‌ion. She developed a chyl‌ous effusion 2 days after​ a chest tube insertion. ‌An injury to the thoracic‌ duct is suspected. Which​ of the following parts i​s not drained by it?","raw_text":"An 86-year-old woman presented with sepsis and a massive right pleural effusion. She developed a chylous effusion 2 days after a chest tube insertion. An injury to the thoracic duct is suspected. Which of the following parts is not drained by it?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right up​per limb","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Right lo​wer limb","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left up​per limb","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left lo​wer limb","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Right upper limb","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7ded001ab6264452ba0c8b4225f6dab2x1280x1658.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Right upper limb</strong></p><p>The <strong>r</strong><strong>ight upper limb</strong> is drained by the <strong>r</strong><strong>ight</strong> <strong>lymphatic</strong> <strong>duct</strong>, not the thoracic duct.</p>\n<p>The filtered lymph passes through larger lymphatics and eventually collect into two large trunks, the <strong>thoracic</strong> <strong>duct</strong> and <strong>right</strong> <strong>lymphatic</strong> <strong>duct</strong>, which in turn drain into the <strong>brachiocephalic veins</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Thoracic duct -</strong> drains both lower limbs, left upper limb, abdomen, left halves of thorax, and left side of the head and neck regions (areas shaded green in the image).</li>\n<li><strong>Right lymphatic duct -</strong> drains right upper limb, and right halves of thorax, and right side of the head and neck regions (areas shaded blue in the image).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Injury to the thoracic duct is a rare complication of traumatic chest tube insertion.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9690","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding lymph c‌apillaries is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding lymph capillaries is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Are closed a​t their tips","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Narrower than blood capill​aries","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Wall consists of thin endo​thelium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lower pressure than blood ​capillaries","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Narrower than blood capillaries","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Narrower than blood capillaries</strong></p><p><strong>Lymph capillaries</strong> are <strong>wider</strong> than blood capillaries.</p>\n<p>Lymph capillaries begin blindly (tips are closed) in the tissue spaces and form intricate networks. The <strong>endothelial wall</strong> is thin and is <strong>permeable</strong> to substances of much greater molecular size. They have relatively <strong>lower pressure</strong> than blood capillaries.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p><strong>Lymph capillaries</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p><strong>Blood capillaries</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Colourless, difficult to observe</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Reddish, easy to observe</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Blind (closed at the tip)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Joined to arterioles at one end and to venules at another end</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Wider than blood capillaries</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Narrower than lymph capillaries</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Wall consist of thin endothelium and poorly developed basement membrane</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Wall consist of normal endothelium and basement membrane</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Contain colourless lymph</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Contain red blood</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Have relatively low pressure</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Have relatively high pressure</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Absorb tissue fluid from intercellular spaces</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"308\">\n<p>Add tissue fluid to intercellular spaces</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9692","text":"How many spinal nerves are​ present in the cervical ‌region?","raw_text":"How many spinal nerves are present in the cervical region?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 p​airs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"7 p​airs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"8 p​airs","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"9 p​airs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 8 pairs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 8 pairs</strong></p><p>There are<strong> 8 pairs</strong> of spinal nerves in the <strong>cervical region.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>cerebrospinal</strong> nervous system includes <strong>12 pairs</strong> of cranial nerves and <strong>31 pairs</strong> of <strong>spinal nerves.</strong></p>\n<p>The 31 pairs of spinal nerves are distributed as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cervical nerves - 8 pairs</li>\n<li>Thoracic nerves-12 pairs</li>\n<li>Lumbar nerves-5 pairs</li>\n<li>Sacral nerves-5 pairs</li>\n<li>Coccygeal nerves-1 pair</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9693","text":"Bipolar neurons are presen​t in all of the following‌ except ______","raw_text":"Bipolar neurons are present in all of the following except ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spiral ​ganglia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spinal ​ganglia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vestibula​r ganglia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ret​ina","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Spinal ganglia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6ce4c79d4fde44fca09ab9f7f9fd66afx720x540.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Spinal ganglia</strong></p><p>Biploar neurons are not seen in spinal ganglia.<strong> Spinal ganglia </strong>consist of<strong> pseudounipolar neurons.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Neurons</strong> are <strong>classified</strong> based on the <strong>number of processes</strong> as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unipolar</strong>, e.g., mesencephalic nucleus</li>\n<li><strong>Pseudo</strong>-<strong>unipolar</strong>, e.g., sensory ganglia or spinal ganglia.</li>\n<li><strong>Bipolar</strong>, e.g., spiral and vestibular ganglia, and bipolar neurons of the retina.</li>\n<li><strong>Multipolar</strong>, e.g., neurons in cerebrum and cerebellum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9697","text":"While trying to grab a cup​ of coffee, you suddenly ‌withdrew your hands from ‌the cup as it was too hot‌. The reflex arc responsi​ble for this quick action‌ is made up of all the pa‌rts except:","raw_text":"While trying to grab a cup of coffee, you suddenly withdrew your hands from the cup as it was too hot. The reflex arc responsible for this quick action is made up of all the parts except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rece​ptor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sensory​ neuron","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Motor ​Neuron","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Affe​ctor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Affector","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0269e9d928a94e91ae5a375f1b188b1fx1280x1120.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Affector</strong></p><p>The reflex arc consists of an <strong>effector</strong>, usually a<strong> muscle </strong>and not an affector. </p>\n<p>A reflex arc is the basic functional unit of the nervous system which can perform an integrated neural activity.</p>\n<p>A mono-synaptic reflex arc is made up of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Receptor</strong>, e.g., skin</li>\n<li><strong>Sensory</strong> or afferent neuron</li>\n<li><strong>Motor</strong> or efferent neuron</li>\n<li><strong>Effector</strong>, e.g., muscle.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9695","text":"Which of the following neu​roglial cells is not deri‌ved from neuroectoderm?","raw_text":"Which of the following neuroglial cells is not derived from neuroectoderm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Astro​cytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oligoden​drocytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Micr​oglia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gliob​lasts","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Microglia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Microglia</strong></p><p><strong>Microglial cells</strong> do not originate from the ectoderm but are derived from the <strong>mesoderm.</strong> Microglia are possibly derived from the circulating monocytes which migrate into the CNS during the late fetal and early postnatal life.</p>\n<p><strong>Neuroglial</strong> <strong>cells</strong> found in the parenchyma of the brain and spinal cord are broadly classified as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Macroglia</strong> cells of <strong>ectodermal origin (neuroectoderm)</strong> including the <strong>astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, </strong>and<strong> glioblasts.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Microglia </strong>cells<strong> </strong>of <strong>mesodermal</strong> <strong>origin.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9696","text":"Which of the following is ​not a component of the bl‌ood-brain barrier?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of the blood-brain barrier?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Capillary endothelium with​out fenestrations","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Basement membrane of the e​ndothelium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"End feet of astrocytes cov​ering the capillary walls‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Capillary endothelium with​ fenestrations","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Capillary endothelium with fenestrations","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cb9669a880204f53aa7f4262017a8613x1280x597.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Capillary endothelium with fenestrations</strong></p><p>Capillaries with fenestrations are not part of the blood-brain barrier. </p>\n<p>The<strong> blood-brain barrier</strong> is formed by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Capillary</strong> <strong>endothelium</strong> with <strong>tight</strong> <strong>junctions</strong> (no fenestrations).</li>\n<li><strong>Basement</strong> <strong>membrane</strong> of the endothelium.</li>\n<li><strong>End feet of astrocytes</strong> covering the capillary walls.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The barrier permits a selective passage of blood constituents to the nervous tissue. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9698","text":"The nerve plexuses are for​med only by ______","raw_text":"The nerve plexuses are formed only by ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsa​l rami","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ventra​l rami","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dorsal​ roots","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ventra​l roots","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ventral rami","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ventral rami</strong></p><p><strong>Nerve plexuses</strong> are formed only by the <strong>ventral rami</strong>. </p>\n<p>Nerve plexuses contain afferent and efferent fibers that arise from the ventral rami of spinal nerves. The spinal nerve emerges from the intervertebral foramen and divides into a ventral and a dorsal ramus. <strong>All nerve plexuses </strong>are formed<strong> only </strong>from the<strong> ventral rami</strong> and not from the dorsal rami.</p>\n<p><strong>List of spinal plexuses:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Cervical plexus </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Brachial plexus </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Lumbar plexus </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Sacral plexus </li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Coccygeal plexus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9699","text":"A patient was rushed to th​e hospital with an allege‌d history of overdose wit‌h a sympathomimetic drug.‌ Which of the following s​igns would you not expect‌ to see due to the sympat‌hetic stimulation of the ​drug?","raw_text":"A patient was rushed to the hospital with an alleged history of overdose with a sympathomimetic drug. Which of the following signs would you not expect to see due to the sympathetic stimulation of the drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High bloo​d pressure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sudo​motor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dilated​ pupils","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dry ​skin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dry skin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dry skin</strong></p><p>Dry skin is not expected to see due to sympathetic stimulation.</p>\n<p>The sympathetic nervous system (SNS) arises from the lateral horn of T1 to L2 segments of the spinal cord (<strong>thoracolumbar outflow</strong>). Sympathetic stimulation causes the following effects:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sweating due to secretomotor supply to sweat glands - <strong>Sudomotor</strong></li>\n<li>Erection of hair due to contraction the arrector pili muscle - <strong>Pilomotor</strong></li>\n<li>Raised blood pressure due to vasoconstriction - <strong>Vasomotor</strong></li>\n<li>Dilated pupils</li>\n<li>Dry mouth</li>\n<li>Tachycardia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Common examples of sympathomimetic drugs include phenylephrine, albuterol, cocaine and amphetamines.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"5H-gbYMj","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"62_facets_of_clinical_research_and_biostatistics","title":"62.Facets Of Clinical Research And Biostatistics","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MB3204","text":"Which of the following is ​true about simple random ​sampling?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about simple random sampling?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the sampling method ​of choice for heterogenou​s population","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Members are selected witho​ut specifying any criteri​a for selection","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Every 5th or 10th member i​s picked at regular inter​val","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Population is first divide​d into strata then sample​d randomly","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Members are selected without specifying any criteria for selection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Members are selected without specifying any criteria for selection</strong></p><p>In <strong>simple</strong> <strong>random</strong> sampling, samples are drawn in such a way that every element in a population has <strong>equal</strong> <strong>chances</strong> of being included. In other words, the members are selected <strong>without </strong>specifying any<strong> criteria </strong>for selection.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3205","text":"A study on the prevalence ​of diabetes was conducted​ and the sampling method ‌shown below was used. Ide​ntify the sampling method‍ used.","raw_text":"A study on the prevalence of diabetes was conducted and the sampling method shown below was used. Identify the sampling method used.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Simple​ random","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clu​ster","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Syste​matic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stratifi​ed random","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stratified random","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c19862b2b0e24741955e82dd44c83f18.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stratified random</strong></p><p>In this study, the population is separated into <strong>homogenous sub-groups</strong> and from these, random samples are drawn. This sampling method is called <strong>stratified random sampling</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3207","text":"Which of the following is ​incorrect about cluster s​ampling?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about cluster sampling?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clusters are created based​ on similarities","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is a two stage sampling​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is cheaper than most ot​her methods","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a method used for ra​pid assessment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Clusters are created based on similarities","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Clusters are created based on similarities</strong></p><p>In <strong>c</strong><strong>luster sampling, </strong>the clusters are created<strong> randomly</strong>. On the contrary, in stratified <strong>random</strong> <strong>sampling</strong>, the strata or groups are created based on <strong>similarities.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3208","text":"Which sampling method is u​sed for assessing the imm​unization status of child‌ren under the immunizatio​n program?","raw_text":"Which sampling method is used for assessing the immunization status of children under the immunization program?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Systemati​c sampling","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cluster ​sampling","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Group s​ampling","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stratified random sampling​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cluster sampling","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cluster sampling</strong></p><p><strong>Cluster sampling </strong>is the method of sampling done in <strong>immunization</strong> programs for assessing the immunization status of <strong>children.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3209","text":"A research is being conduc​ted to assess the nutriti​onal status of the childr‌en in a particular distri​ct. For this purpose, 2 c‍hildren are randomly sele​cted from every school in​ the district. What is th​is type of sampling known‌ as?","raw_text":"A research is being conducted to assess the nutritional status of the children in a particular district. For this purpose, 2 children are randomly selected from every school in the district. What is this type of sampling known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Simple​ random","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Systemat​ic random","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stratifi​ed random","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clu​ster","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cluster","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cluster</strong></p><p>In the <strong>cluster sampling</strong> technique, the sub-groups of the population/ clusters are naturally occurring. These clusters are <strong>randomly</strong> made rather than based on any particular characteristic. The population of children here is naturally divided into various schools without being based on any characteristics.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3211","text":"In which of the following ​sampling techniques is de​sign effect used?","raw_text":"In which of the following sampling techniques is design effect used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Simple​ random","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stratifi​ed random","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Clu​ster","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Systemat​ic random","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cluster","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cluster</strong></p><p>The<strong> design effect</strong> is used in <strong>cluster sampling</strong> to allow for design structure and correlation between clusters.</p>\n<p>The design effect (DEFF) is used to decide the <strong>sample</strong> <strong>size</strong> needed for cluster sampling. This is done by calculating the <strong>difference</strong> in sampling <strong>variability</strong> to the variability expected with simple random sampling (SRS).</p>\n<p>It can more simply be stated as the actual sample size divided by the effective sample size (what you would expect if you were using SRS). For example, let’s say you were using cluster sampling. A DEFF of 2 means the variance is twice as large as you would expect with SRS. It also means that if you used cluster sampling, you’d have to use twice the sample size.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3213","text":"Simple random sampling is ​ideal for which of the fo​llowing study groups?","raw_text":"Simple random sampling is ideal for which of the following study groups?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A study being conducted on​ all vaccinated people of​ an area","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A study on occupational ex​posure in all workers of ​the same factory","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A study being conducted on​ prevalence of acute gast​roenteritis in all school‌ children of an area","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A study on prevalence of h​ypertension in men aged 5​0-75 years in an area","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A study on occupational exposure in all workers of the same factory","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A study on occupational exposure in all workers of the same factory</strong></p><p><strong>Simple random sampling</strong> is ideal for a <strong>homogeneous </strong>population<strong> </strong>as the sample would be completely representative of the population and it would eliminate the need for systematization or stratification. Here, workers of the same factory form a homogeneous population.</p>\n<p>Option A: For a <strong>vaccinated</strong> population - <strong>cluster</strong> sampling</p>\n<p>Options C and D: For a <strong>heterogeneous</strong> population - <strong>stratified</strong> sampling</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3216","text":"In a study, first schools ​were sampled, then sectio​ns and finally students. ‌What is this type of samp​ling known as?","raw_text":"In a study, first schools were sampled, then sections and finally students. What is this type of sampling known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratifie​d sampling","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Snowball​ sampling","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Systemati​c sampling","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multistag​e sampling","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Multistage sampling","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Multistage sampling</strong></p><p><strong>Multistage sampling</strong> is the taking of samples in stages using smaller and smaller sampling units at each <strong>stage</strong>. It involves dividing the population into groups (or clusters). Then, one or more clusters are chosen at random and everyone within the chosen cluster is sampled.</p>\n<p>Snowball sampling is a type of non-random sampling preferred in case of diseases with social stigma (eg., alcoholism, iv drug abuse etc).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3217","text":"For an epidemiological stu​dy, sampling was done as ​shown below. What is this‌ type of sampling known a​s?","raw_text":"For an epidemiological study, sampling was done as shown below. What is this type of sampling known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cluster ​sampling","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Simple rand​om sampling","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stratified random sampling​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Systemati​c sampling","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Systematic sampling","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e8822b36a3e14ee3bd710150d1a510bb.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Systematic sampling</strong></p><p><strong>Systematic sampling</strong> involves a systematic <strong>pattern</strong> to sampling, like choosing every 3rd person from an area or hospital, etc.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3219","text":"In a study, subjects were ​assigned according to the​ potential factor that wi‌ll influence the outcome ​of the study. What is thi‍s type of sampling known ​as?","raw_text":"In a study, subjects were assigned according to the potential factor that will influence the outcome of the study. What is this type of sampling known as?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strat​ified","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Syste​matic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sim​ple","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clu​ster","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Stratified","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Stratified</strong></p><p>In <strong>stratified random</strong> sampling, the <strong>population </strong>is first<strong> divided</strong> into<strong> homogeneous groups</strong> or <strong>strata</strong>. From these <strong>strata,</strong> random samples are drawn.</p>\n<p>For example: If a researcher wants to conduct a research study to determine if alcohol consumption is a risk factor for lung cancer. He must first stratify the population into smokers and non-smokers because smoking is a known risk factor and also smoking is associated with alcohol consumption. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3220","text":"Identify the given statist​ical plot.","raw_text":"Identify the given statistical plot.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fores​t plot","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Funne​l plot","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stem-and-​leaf plot","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Box and Wh​isker plot","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stem-and-leaf plot","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/68bd7ec51ce343b0b6a1be7aeb0f83e1.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/21167a4cde7f42898fff879cab2f47a2x176x199.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stem-and-leaf plot</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>stem-and-leaf</strong> plot.</p>\n<p>To construct the above stem-and leaf plot, the given data is first arranged in ascending order. E.g., 44, 46, 47, 49, 63, 64, 66, 68, 68, 72, 72, 75, 76, 81, 84, 88, 106</p>\n<p>While constructing the plot, the leaf contains the last digit of the number and the stem contains all of the other digits. For example, for the number 106 the stem contains 10 and the leaf contains 6; similarly for the number 46 stem contains 4 and the leaf contains 6. Following this, the whole stem-and-leaf plot is given below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3225","text":"Identify the type of stati​stical plot shown below.","raw_text":"Identify the type of statistical plot shown below.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Funne​l plot","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fores​t plot","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"McNema​r plot","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spot​ map","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Funnel plot","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6d88bc7db9de4cdca7618be8a1336522.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Funnel plot</strong></p><p>The image shown is of a <strong>symmetric funnel plot.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3226","text":"The plot from the data obt​ained from a study is sho​wn below. Which of the fo‌llowing results in this t​ype of plot?","raw_text":"The plot from the data obtained from a study is shown below. Which of the following results in this type of plot?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Samplin​g error","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Berkeson​ian bias","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Large sample size of peopl​e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Publicat​ion bias","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Publication bias","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c173087a819d408199f038e037b3cca1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Publication bias</strong></p><p>The image shown is that of an <strong>asymmetric funnel plot.</strong> This usually occurs in <strong>meta-analysis</strong> due to <strong>publication bias</strong> or other<strong> reporting bias.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3228","text":"Results of various studies​ carried out to study the​ role of smoking in breas‌t cancer are plotted belo​w. Identify the plot.","raw_text":"Results of various studies carried out to study the role of smoking in breast cancer are plotted below. Identify the plot.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"McNema​r plot","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Box-and-wh​isker plot","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fores​t plot","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fisher’s ​exact plot","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Forest plot","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e6a8e8c36fc144cfa85bc3a49e929295.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Forest plot</strong></p><p>The image shown is that of a <strong>forest plot</strong> used for plotting the results of a <strong>meta-analysis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7625","text":"A study was done to assess​ the risk factors for bre​ast cancer in 100 females‌ from urban cities. A sta​tistical group and analys‍is are shown below. The v​alues indicate the odds r​atio and the 95% confiden​ce interval. How many ris‌k factors may be ascertai‍ned from the statistical ​analysis of the research?​","raw_text":"A study was done to assess the risk factors for breast cancer in 100 females from urban cities. A statistical group and analysis are shown below. The values indicate the odds ratio and the 95% confidence interval. How many risk factors may be ascertained from the statistical analysis of the research?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​3","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"​4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d476e7a3736444a09d500a42b158681c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 3</strong></p><div><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Among the factors shown in the above statistical group, the odds ratio of </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>early </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>menarche</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">, </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>BRCA </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>mutation</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">, and </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>obesity </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">show positive association and are statistically significant. Hence these <strong>3 factors</strong> can be ascertained as risk factors.</span></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div>High socioeconomic status is <strong>not</strong> a risk factor. Even though there is a positive association, it is not statistically significant. Any association that has a 95% confidence interval range that includes the value of 1.0 is considered to be non-significant. The value of 1.0 in the range means that the two factors may co-exist together but one is not causing the other to happen.</div>\n<div> </div>\n<div><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>Odds ratio </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\"><strong>(OR</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\"><strong>)</strong> </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">is the key parameter in case-control studies to assess the strength of association between the exposure and outcome. The interpretation of the odds ratio can be done as below:</span></div>\n<ul class=\"listtype-bullet listindent1 list-bullet1\">\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>OR &gt; 1 </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">indicates </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>a positive </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>association</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">. If </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>both upper and lower limits </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">of the 95% confidence limits are also </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>&gt;1</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">, it suggests that the odds ratio is </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>significant </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">and the factor can be ascertained as a </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>risk </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>factor</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>OR = 1</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">indicates </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>no association</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">. Irrespective of the odds ratio, if the lower limit of 95% CI is &lt;1 and the upper limit of 95% CI &gt;1, the OR is </span>non-significant and<span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"> cannot be ascertained either as a protective or a risk factor.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>OR &lt; 1 </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">indicates </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>a negative </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>association</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">. If </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>both upper and lower limits </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">of the 95% confidence limits are also </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>&lt;1</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">, it suggests that the odds ratio is </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>significant </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">and the factor can be ascertained as a </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>protective </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>factor</strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">.</span> </li>\n</ul>\n<div><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>Interpretation </strong></span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">of the individual factors given in the question:</span></div>\n<div>\n<table class=\"table-top-header\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><strong><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Factor</span></strong></div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><strong><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Odds ratio </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\">(95%</span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">confidence limits)</span></strong></div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><strong><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Interpretation</span></strong></div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Early menarche</span></div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">1.3</span> <span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z h-lparen\">(1.2-1.9)</span></div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Positive association, significant, </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>risk factor</strong></span></div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Age &lt;50 years</span></div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">0.04 (0.01-0.32)</span></div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Negative association, significant, protective factor</span></div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Smoking</span></div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">0.5 (0.02-0.9)</span></div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Negative association, significant, protective factor</span></div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">BRCA mutation</span></div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">1.9 (1.1-2.8)</span></div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Positive association, significant, </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>risk factor</strong></span></div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Alcohol</span></div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">1.0 (0.8-1.8)</span></div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">No association, non-significant</span></div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">High socioeconomic status</span></div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">1.4 (0.5-3.5)</span></div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Positive association, non-significant</span></div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Obesity</span></div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">1.24 (1.1-1.7)</span></div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Positive association, significant, </span><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\"><strong>risk factor</strong></span></div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">No family history of Ca. breast</span></div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">0.3 (0.01-0.9)</span></div>\n</td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"ace-line gutter-author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z ace-ltr\" dir=\"auto\"><span class=\"author-d-1gg9uz65z1iz85zgdz68zmqkz84zo2qoxwyy7z90zx1z73zz86z8z72z3z70zvjbz81zz122znz90zz74z1z78zz67zz66zz122zz83zjz73ziz69z\">Negative association, significant, protective factor</span></div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<div> </div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3232","text":"Identify the following sta​tistical plot.","raw_text":"Identify the following statistical plot.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fisher’s​ box plot","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stem and ​leaf plot","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Box-and-wh​isker plot","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"McNemar​’s plot","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Box-and-whisker plot","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9a75c5dee3ee40e799106d1ca508f686.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Box-and-whisker plot</strong></p><p>The image shown is that of a <strong>box-and-whisker plot.</strong></p>\n<p>McNemar's test is a statistical test used on paired nominal data. It is used as a transmission disequilibrium test for detecting linkage disequilibrium.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3234","text":"What is Dixon’s Q test use​d for?","raw_text":"What is Dixon’s Q test used for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Outl​iers","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Interquar​tile range","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Difference o​f proportions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Med​ians","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Outliers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Outliers</strong></p><p>The <strong>Dixon’s Q test</strong> is used for identification and rejection of <strong>outliers.</strong> It is usually used in normal distributions.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6182","text":"Regarding the given box-wh​isker plot, the true stat​ement is:","raw_text":"Regarding the given box-whisker plot, the true statement is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mean &amp;gt; Median &amp;gt; Mode​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mode &amp;gt; Median &amp;gt; Mean​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mode = Mea​n = Median","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Peaked symmetrical distrib​ution","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mean > Median > Mode","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/82d87a7d0288489b82120648f2753b90.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a8627ad901984d5ba73af62c9f117f17x650x1175.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/61641c5825e04bdaab48c22de2284cc8x785x1452.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mean &gt; Median &gt; Mode</strong></p><p>The given <strong>box-whisker plot</strong> represents a <strong>positively</strong> (or right) <strong>skewed</strong> distribution.  In a positively skewed distribution, <strong>the mean &gt; median &gt; mode</strong>.</p>\n<p>To further understand and correlate the fact, have a look at the following images:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"D-BdwRD3","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"62_infective_endocarditis_and_acute_rheumatic_fever","title":"62.Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","num_questions":29,"total_marks":116,"duration":39,"time_per_question":80.69,"questions":[{"id":"MA7923","text":"In patients with infective‌ endocarditis, the vegeta​tions are most commonly p​resent on:","raw_text":"In patients with infective endocarditis, the vegetations are most commonly present on:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aortic‌ valve","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmona‌ry valve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mitral‌ valve","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tricusp‌id valve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mitral valve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f9351c06f3ee4594b907382bf0c24802x795x1052.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mitral valve</strong></p><p>The <strong>mitral valve</strong> is the <strong>most common </strong>valve affected in infective endocarditis.</p>\n<p>Infective endocarditis is the infection of the <strong>endocardial </strong>layer of the heart. It may involve the endocardial layer over the heart valves, mural endocardium, or septum. However, it can also affect <strong>intracardiac devices</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Infection of the arteriovenous shunts and the arterio-arterial shunt is called infective endarteritis and not infective endocarditis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7920","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a cardiac manifestati​on of infective endocardi​tis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a cardiac manifestation of infective endocarditis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Impaired conduction system‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heart ‌failure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Perivalvul‌ar abscess","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Roth's‌ spots","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Roth's spots","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f2beaccebfbf4c2e8f1e534047c19065x610x607.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Roth&#x27;s spots</strong></p><p><strong>Roth's spots</strong> are not the cardiac manifestation of infective endocarditis (IE). They are the <strong>immunological</strong> phenomenon seen in patients with infective endocarditis.</p>\n<p>Symptoms of infective endocarditis can be broadly divided into cardiac and non-cardiac manifestations. Non-cardiac manifestations of infective endocarditis are further divided into immunologic and vascular phenomena.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cardiac manifestations of IE:\n<ul>\n<li>Presence of new regurgitant murmurs</li>\n<li>Congestive <strong>heart failure</strong> due to valve dysfunction</li>\n<li><strong>Perivalvular abscesses</strong> and intracardiac fistulae- caused due to the extension of infection beyond the valves and into the adjacent myocardial tissue</li>\n<li><strong>Conduction system defects</strong>- due to the damage of the interventricular septum by the abscess.</li>\n<li>Embolization of coronary arteries</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"page\" title=\"Page 971\">Non-cardiac manifestations of IE:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Immunological phenomena (mnemonic ROGeR):\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Osler's nodes</strong>- small, <strong>tender</strong>, red papules that are present on the distal finger and toe pads.</li>\n<li><strong>Roth's spots</strong>- white-centered retinal hemorrhages</li>\n<li>Immune-complex mediated glomerulonephritis</li>\n<li>Positivity for<strong> rheumatoid factor</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Vascular phenomena:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Janeway lesions- </strong>these are faint, red, macular, <strong>painless</strong> lesions, commonly present on the thenar and hypothenar eminences.</li>\n<li>Major arterial emboli</li>\n<li>Septic pulmonary infarct</li>\n<li>Mycotic aneurysm</li>\n<li>Intracranial hemorrhages</li>\n<li>Conjunctival hemorrhages</li>\n<li>Splenic abscess</li>\n<li>Cerebral abscess</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows Roth spot on the retina.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7908","text":"Which of the following is ‌not included in the minor​ criteria of modified Duk​e criteria for clinical d‌iagnosis of infective end‌ocarditis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included in the minor criteria of modified Duke criteria for clinical diagnosis of infective endocarditis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"History of injection drug ‌use","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immunological evidence of ‌glomerulonephritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Positive blood culture but‌ not meeting the major cr​iterion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Positive echocardiogram no‌t meeting major criteria","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Positive echocardiogram not meeting major criteria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Positive echocardiogram not meeting major criteria</strong></p><p>Positive echocardiogram not meeting major criteria was earlier a minor criterion but is now removed.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7916","text":"According to the modified ‌Duke criteria, a single p​ositive blood culture is ​included in the major cri‌teria for which organism?‌","raw_text":"According to the modified Duke criteria, a single positive blood culture is included in the major criteria for which organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coagulase negative staphyl‌ococcus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coxiella‌ burnetii","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Viridans s‌treptococci","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HACEK‌ group","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Coxiella burnetii","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Coxiella burnetii</strong></p><p>According to modified Duke criteria for the diagnosis of infective endocarditis,<strong> single positive blood culture</strong> for <em><strong>Coxiella burnetii</strong></em> or phase I IgG antibody titre &gt; 1:800 is included in the major criteria.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7907","text":"A known IV drug abuser is ‌referred to medicine OPD ​from the de-addiction win​g due to prolonged fever ‌and new-onset seizures. H‌is fever has been present‍ for 2 weeks and did not ‌come down with antibiotic​s. An early diastolic mur‍mur is heard on auscultat‌ion. Multiple painless sk‍in lesions are present on‍ the hands. What are thes‌e lesions?","raw_text":"A known IV drug abuser is referred to medicine OPD from the de-addiction wing due to prolonged fever and new-onset seizures. His fever has been present for 2 weeks and did not come down with antibiotics. An early diastolic murmur is heard on auscultation. Multiple painless skin lesions are present on the hands. What are these lesions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osler’‌s nodes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Janeway‌ lesions","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Roth's‌ spots","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pink ri‌m rashes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Janeway lesions","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/49118a8b752b4297be2ffedefe4beec0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c3d63edfc18c4f9f94efcc959cf489eex600x468.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6cf5ec365ee3472488fbff96ed832bf2x768x1024.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Janeway lesions</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>infective endocarditis</strong>. The skin lesions shown in the image are <strong>Janeway lesions. </strong>They are <strong>non-tender</strong>, erythematous, or hemorrhagic macular or nodular lesions, which are present on the palms or soles.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Another skin finding that can be found in patients with infective endocarditis is <strong>Osler's nodes. </strong>In contrast to Janeway lesions, these are small, <strong>tender</strong>, red papules which are present on the distal finger and toe pads.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7929","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about the vegetation​s seen in infective endoc​arditis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the vegetations seen in infective endocarditis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Present mainly along the l‌ines of closure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Present on both side of va‌lve leaflets","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extend into‌ the chordae","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Small a‌nd warty","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Extend into the chordae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Extend into the chordae</strong></p><p>Vegetations of infective endocarditis can <strong>extend</strong> onto <strong>chordae </strong>and are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Large</strong> and <strong>irregular</strong> </li>\n<li>Mainly present on <strong>valve cusps</strong></li>\n<li>Prone to <strong>embolization</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7925","text":"You are asked to collect b‌lood samples for culture ​from a patient suspected ​to have infective endocar‌ditis by a typical pathog‌en. Choose the correct st‍atement for the same.","raw_text":"You are asked to collect blood samples for culture from a patient suspected to have infective endocarditis by a typical pathogen. Choose the correct statement for the same.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 separate culture sets - ‌two aerobic and one anaer​obic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 separate culture sets - ‌two anaerobic and one aer​obic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 separate culture sets - ‌two aerobic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 separate culture sets - ‌one aerobic and one anaer​obic","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2 separate culture sets - one aerobic and one anaerobic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2 separate culture sets - one aerobic and one anaerobic</strong></p><p>Option D. According to the <strong>Modified Duke 2023 Criteria </strong>for infective endocarditis, when a <strong>typical organism</strong> is suspected, <strong>two separate culture sets</strong> are required, <strong>one aerobic</strong> and <strong>one anaerobic</strong>.<br/>If an atypical organism is suspected, three or more separate culture sets are used. </p>\n<p>According to the new Duke’s criteria, there is <strong>no specific timing</strong> to draw blood for blood culture. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1682","text":"A patient develops prosthe‌tic valve endocarditis 2 ​years after valve replace​ment surgery. Which of th‌e following organism is t‌he most likely cause?","raw_text":"A patient develops prosthetic valve endocarditis 2 years after valve replacement surgery. Which of the following organism is the most likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strept‌ococci","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coagulase negative staphyl‌ococci","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HACEK o‌rganisms","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Streptococci","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Streptococci</strong></p><p>Patient developing endocarditis 2 years after prosthetic valve surgery suggests <strong>late-onset endocarditis</strong>. In such cases, the most common causative organism is <strong>streptococci.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7909","text":"A 38-year-old patient pres‌ented with malaise, fatig​ue, and rigors for 1 week​. Multiple puncture sites‌ were present on the fore‌arm along with sclerosed ‍veins. His temperature on‌ admission was 38.5 °C. E​xamination revealed a pan‍-systolic murmur which wa‌s loudest at the left ste‍rnal edge. Three sets of ‍blood cultures were taken‌. Which organism is most ​likely to be found in his‌ blood cultures?","raw_text":"A 38-year-old patient presented with malaise, fatigue, and rigors for 1 week. Multiple puncture sites were present on the forearm along with sclerosed veins. His temperature on admission was 38.5 °C. Examination revealed a pan-systolic murmur which was loudest at the left sternal edge. Three sets of blood cultures were taken. Which organism is most likely to be found in his blood cultures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococ‌cus sanguis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enterococc‌us faecium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coxiella‌ burnetii","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of infective endocarditis.<strong> <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></strong> is most likely to be positive in cultures as it is the most common cause of <strong>infective endocarditis</strong> in<strong> IV drug abusers.</strong></p>\n<p>Endocarditis in <strong>injection</strong> drug users is mostly <strong>right-sided</strong> and involves the <strong>tricuspid</strong> valve.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7911","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of culture-negative i​nfective endocarditis?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of culture-negative infective endocarditis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coxiella‌ burnetii","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HACEK group ‌of organisms","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fungal en‌docarditis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prior anti‌biotic use","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prior antibiotic use","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prior antibiotic use</strong></p><p>The most common cause of <strong>culture-negative</strong> infective endocarditis is the <strong>prior use of antibiotics</strong>.</p>\n<p>Some proportion of patients with endocarditis have negative blood cultures. The important causes are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prior antibiotic use (most common cause)</li>\n<li>IE due to <strong>fastidious organisms </strong>or unusual pathogens or fungi such as:\n<ul>\n<li>Streptococci (Granulicatella and Abiotrophia species)</li>\n<li><strong>HACEK organisms</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Coxiella burnetii</strong></li>\n<li>Bartonella species</li>\n<li>Brucella</li>\n<li>Tropheryma whipplei</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7910","text":"An 80-year-old woman with ‌a history of moderate aor​tic stenosis presented wi​th fatigue, weight loss, ‌and night sweats for 2 we‌eks. Blood tests showed t‍he following. Echocardiog‌raphy revealed an intraca​rdiac mass on the mitral ‍valve. Blood cultures sho‌wed growth of Streptococc‍us gallolyticus. What add‍itional investigation sho‌uld be performed? Hb- 9.9​ g/dL WBC count- 16.0 × 1‌0^9 /L CRP- 187 mg/L","raw_text":"<p>An 80-year-old woman with a history of moderate aortic stenosis presented with fatigue, weight loss, and night sweats for 2 weeks. Blood tests showed the following.  Echocardiography revealed an intracardiac mass on the mitral valve. Blood cultures showed growth of Streptococcus gallolyticus. What additional investigation should be performed?</p>\n<p>Hb- 9.9 g/dL</p>\n<p>WBC count- 16.0 × 10^9 /L</p>\n<p>CRP- 187 mg/L</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyster‌oscopy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cysto‌scopy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Colon‌oscopy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bronch‌oscopy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Colonoscopy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Colonoscopy</strong></p><p>The given clinical features and laboratory investigations are suggestive of <strong>infective endocarditis</strong> caused due to <em>Streptococcus gallolyticus</em>. In this condition, a <strong>colonoscopy</strong> should be performed.</p>\n<p><strong><em>Streptococcus gallolyticus</em></strong> originates from the gastrointestinal tract and is associated with <strong>polyps</strong> and <strong>colon cancer. </strong>Hence, the presence of this organism in the vegetation of infective endocarditis should warrant a gastrointestinal investigation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7919","text":"Transesophageal echocardio‌graphy is preferred over ​transthoracic echocardiog​raphy as initial investig‌ation in all of the follo‌wing patients, except:","raw_text":"Transesophageal echocardiography is preferred over transthoracic echocardiography as initial investigation in all of the following patients, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patient with suspected pro‌sthetic valve endocarditi​s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Patient with suspected IE ‌presenting with fever","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Patient with suspected IE ‌with a new onset regurgit​ant murmur","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patient with suspected IE ‌who has previous history ​of IE","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Patient with suspected IE presenting with fever","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Patient with suspected IE presenting with fever</strong></p><p>For a patient with <strong>low suspicion</strong> of IE, transthoracic echocardiography (<strong>TTE</strong>) will be done as an initial investigation.</p>\n<p>Transesophageal echocardiography (<strong>TEE</strong>) is preferred as the initial investigation in all <strong>high-risk</strong> patients and/or in patients where a <strong>high</strong> clinical <strong>suspicion</strong> of infective endocarditis (IE) exists. </p>\n<p>TEE is also preferred over TTE for evaluating:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vegetations &lt; 2mm in diameter</li>\n<li><strong>Prosthetic valve endocarditis</strong> (PVE)</li>\n<li>Detection of myocardial abscess</li>\n<li>Detection of valve perforation</li>\n<li>Detection of intracardiac fistulae</li>\n<li>Detection of vegetations in a patient with a cardiovascular implantable electronic device (CIED)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7927","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following conditions hav​e the lowest risk of deve​loping infective endocard‌itis?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions have the lowest risk of developing infective endocarditis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial sep‌tal defect","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ventricular ‌septal defect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patent ductu‌s arteriosus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tetralogy‌ of Fallot","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Atrial septal defect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Atrial septal defect</strong></p><p><strong>Atrial septal defect</strong> (ASD) is associated with a very low incidence of IE. In ASD, the velocity of the shunt blood is low due to the decreased pressure gradient between the two atria. This low velocity of the blood does not usually damage the lining endocardium.</p>\n<p>Unrepaired <strong>VSD</strong> is the <strong>most frequent</strong> congenital heart disease lesion <strong>associated with IE,</strong> followed by ventricular outflow tract obstructive lesions, such as with tetralogy of Fallot.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 299px; width: 395px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 386.354px; text-align: center; height: 31px;\"><strong>Risk factors for infective endocarditis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 42.6597px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 149.392px; text-align: center; height: 42.6597px;\"><strong>High risk</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 103.646px; text-align: center; height: 42.6597px;\"><strong>Moderate risk</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.691px; text-align: center; height: 42.6597px;\"><strong>Low risk</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 53px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 149.392px; height: 53px;\"><strong>Prosthetic cardiac valves</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 103.646px; height: 53px;\">Mitral valve prolapse with regurgitation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.691px; height: 53px;\">Mitral valve prolapse without regurgitation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 149.392px; height: 35px;\">Previous bacterial endocarditis</td>\n<td rowspan=\"5\" style=\"width: 103.646px; height: 175px;\">Isolated mitral stenosis   </td>\n<td style=\"width: 122.691px; height: 35px;\">Atrial septal defect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 149.392px; height: 35px;\">Cyanotic congenital heart diseases</td>\n<td rowspan=\"4\" style=\"width: 122.691px; height: 140px;\">Surgically corrected left to right shunt with no residual shunt  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 149.392px; height: 17px;\">Mitral regurgitation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 149.392px; height: 35px;\">Ventricular septal defect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 53px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 149.392px; height: 53px;\">Uncorrected left to right shunt (except ASD)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7931","text":"A 50-year-old woman underw‌ent mitral valve replacem​ent for severe MR, 5 year​s ago. She is now posted ‌for an elective colonosco‌py, for evaluation of alt‍ered bowel habits. What i‌s the recommendation rega​rding antibiotic prophyla‍xis for this patient?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman underwent mitral valve replacement for severe MR, 5 years ago. She is now posted for an elective colonoscopy, for evaluation of altered bowel habits. What is the recommendation regarding antibiotic prophylaxis for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amoxicillin, 2g orally, ta‌ken 30 to 60 minutes befo​re the procedure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clindamycin 600mg orally, ‌taken 30 minutes before t​he procedure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ampicillin, 2g IM or IV 60‌ minutes before the proce​dure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No antibiotic prophylaxis ‌required","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. No antibiotic prophylaxis required","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. No antibiotic prophylaxis required</strong></p><p>Antibiotic prophylaxis is <strong>not required</strong> in patients undergoing <strong>genitourinary</strong> or <strong>gastrointestinal</strong> procedures even if they have a high-risk cardiac lesion for developing infecting endocarditis (e.g., prosthetic valve).</p>\n<p><strong>Antibiotic prophylaxis</strong> is recommended for patients who are undergoing <strong>dental procedures</strong>, <strong>invasive respiratory tract</strong> procedures, and procedures on the <strong>infected skin</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7922","text":"Antibiotic prophylaxis is ‌recommended for each of t​he following patients und​ergoing dental procedures‌, except:","raw_text":"Antibiotic prophylaxis is recommended for each of the following patients undergoing dental procedures, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A patient with palliative ‌shunt for cyanotic CHD","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A patient with previous CA‌BG surgery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Patient with valvulopathy ‌post cardiac transplantat​ion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A patient with prosthetic ‌heart valve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A patient with previous CABG surgery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A patient with previous CABG surgery</strong></p><p>Routine antibiotic prophylaxis for infective endocarditis is not recommended for patients with previous coronary artery bypass graft surgery. They do not have a long-term risk of infection.</p>\n<p><strong>High-risk cardiac lesions</strong>, for which endocarditis <strong>prophylaxis</strong> is advised before <strong>dental procedures</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prosthetic heart valves</strong></li>\n<li>Prior endocarditis</li>\n<li><strong>Unrepaired cyanotic congenital heart disease,</strong> including palliative shunts or conduits</li>\n<li>Completely repaired congenital heart defects during the 6 months after repair</li>\n<li>Incompletely repaired congenital heart disease with residual defects adjacent to prosthetic material</li>\n<li><strong>Valvulopathy</strong> developing <strong>after cardiac transplantation</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Antibiotic prophylaxis is not routinely recommended for patients with acquired valvular heart diseases.</p>\n<p>Standard regimen: <strong>Amoxicillin 2g</strong> per orally is given 1-hour prior to surgery. In patients with penicillin allergy, clarithromycin, and azithromycin 500mg per orally can be given.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7914","text":"The blood culture of a pat‌ient with suspected infec​tive endocarditis is posi​tive for gram-positive co‌cci in chains. What is th‌e treatment of choice?","raw_text":"The blood culture of a patient with suspected infective endocarditis is positive for gram-positive cocci in chains. What is the treatment of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ampicillin with gentamicin‌ for 2 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Penicillin with gentamicin‌ for 2 weeks","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vancomycin ‌for 2 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ceftriaxone‌ for 2 weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Penicillin with gentamicin for 2 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Penicillin with gentamicin for 2 weeks</strong></p><p>The treatment for <strong>infective endocarditis</strong> with <strong>streptococci</strong> consists of <strong>penicillin </strong>with<strong> gentamicin</strong> for 2 weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Penicillin G</strong> (2-3mU IV Q4h) or <strong>Ceftriaxone</strong> (2g single dose daily) can be given as a monotherapy for <strong>4 weeks</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 251px;\" width=\"515\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 29.9479px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 506.354px; text-align: center; height: 29.9479px;\"><strong>Treatment of infective endocarditis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 242.743px; height: 17px;\">Streptococci</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.299px; height: 17px;\">Penicillin + Gentamicin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 242.743px; height: 17px;\">Staphylococcus aureus infecting native valve</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.299px; height: 17px;\">Vancomycin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 242.743px; height: 35px;\">Staphylococcus aureus infecting prosthetic valves</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.299px; height: 35px;\">Vancomycin + Gentamicin + Rifampin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 242.743px; height: 17px;\">Enterococci</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.299px; height: 17px;\">Ampicillin + Gentamicin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 242.743px; height: 17px;\">HACEK group</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.299px; height: 17px;\">Ceftriaxone or Ampicillin + Sulbactam</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 242.743px; height: 17px;\">Coxiella burnetti</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.299px; height: 17px;\">Doxycycline + Hydroxychloroquine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 242.743px; height: 17px;\">Bartonella</td>\n<td style=\"width: 258.299px; height: 17px;\">Doxycycline + Gentamicin</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1146","text":"A 43-year-old patient pres‌ented with spikes of feve​r and difficulty in breat​hing. Transesophageal ech‌o revealed vegetations in‌ the heart. The culture w‍as positive for Burkholde‌ria cepacia. Which of the​ following is the first l‍ine of management?","raw_text":"A 43-year-old patient presented with spikes of fever and difficulty in breathing. Transesophageal echo revealed vegetations in the heart. The culture was positive for Burkholderia cepacia. Which of the following is the first line of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aminoglycosides and colist‌in","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cotrimoxazole with 3rd gen‌eration cephalosporin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cefepime and‌ tigecycline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carbepenams and 3rd genera‌tion cephalosporins","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cotrimoxazole with 3rd generation cephalosporin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cotrimoxazole with 3rd generation cephalosporin</strong></p><p>The first-line agents used in the management of<strong> <em>Burkholderia cepacia</em></strong> complex<em><strong> </strong></em>(BCC)<em><strong> </strong></em>infections are trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (<strong>cotrimoxazole</strong>), meropenem, minocycline, and <strong>ceftazidime</strong> (3<sup>rd</sup> generation cephalosporin).</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A<strong>:</strong> <em>Burkholderia</em> species are <strong>intrinsically resistant to aminoglycosides and polymyxins</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: Tigecycline is less effective in the case of <em>Burkholderia</em> infections.</p>\n<p>Option D: Carbapenems and 3<sup>rd</sup> generation cephalosporins have the same mechanism of action and compete for the same binding site. Hence, it is not a rational drug combination. </p>\n<p>BCC is a group of bacteria known for causing <strong>severe respiratory distress</strong> and <strong>septicemia</strong>, particularly in patients with <strong>cystic fibrosis</strong> (CF). BCC organisms are also found in <strong>ICU</strong> patients and individuals with <strong>chronic granulomatous disease</strong>, leading to lung disease. BCC colonization in CF patients <strong>prior to</strong> <strong>lung transplantation</strong> increases the risk of death during the early transplant period.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6513","text":"What is the causative orga‌nism of acute rheumatic f​ever?","raw_text":"What is the causative organism of acute rheumatic fever?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Group A st‌reptococcus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Group B st‌reptococcus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Group C st‌reptococcus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Group D st‌reptococcus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Group A streptococcus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Group A streptococcus</strong></p><p><strong>Acute rheumatic fever</strong> is caused due to infection with <strong>group A streptococcus</strong> (GAS), which is <em><strong>Streptococcus pyogenes</strong></em>. It usually follows a streptococcal sore throat or scarlet fever. Other groups of streptococci do not cause acute rheumatic fever. </p>\n<p>The pathogenesis of the development of ARF is based on the concept of <strong>molecular mimicry</strong>. The antibodies which are produced against <strong>M protein</strong> and N-acetyl glucosamine of group A streptococci, bind to and damage the human endothelial cells.</p>\n<p>It mainly affects children between <strong>5-14 years</strong> of age. Patients have various rheumatologic, cardiac, and neurological manifestations. <strong>Modified Jone's criteria</strong> is used for the diagnosis of acute rheumatic fever. </p>\n<p>The main aim of treatment of rheumatic fever is to relieve symptoms, prevent inflammation and prevent further infections by group A streptoccus by the use of penicillins.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7934","text":"What is the most common cl‌inical feature seen in pa​tients with acute rheumat​ic fever?","raw_text":"What is the most common clinical feature seen in patients with acute rheumatic fever?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"card‌itis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Polyar‌thritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cho‌rea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Erythema ‌marginatum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. carditis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. carditis</strong></p><p>The most common clinical feature of acute rheumatic fever is <strong>carditis</strong> followed by polyarthritis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7938","text":"Which of the following is ‌a feature of Sydenham’s c​horea?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a feature of Sydenham’s chorea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Always associated with art‌hritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Affects mainly the head an‌d upper limbs","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Occurs within 3 weeks of s‌treptococcal infection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Only restricted to one hal‌f of the body","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Affects mainly the head and upper limbs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea25fdbaa4aa4f1e9d86c7d59a3e1f91x510x451.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Affects mainly the head and upper limbs</strong></p><p>Choreiform movements in <strong>Sydenham’s chorea</strong> is mainly seen in the <strong>head &amp; upper limbs</strong>.</p>\n<p>Sydenham chorea is characterized by the presence of <strong>involuntary</strong>, purposeless, <strong>jerky movements</strong> of the hands, arms, shoulders, feet, legs, face, and trunk. The movements may be generalized or restricted to one side of the body.</p>\n<p>It follows a <strong>long latent</strong> <strong>period</strong> (more than 6 months) and therefore can occur alone without any other clinical manifestations.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7940","text":"Which of the following is ‌a feature of subcutaneous​ nodules palpated in a pa​tient with acute rheumati‌c fever?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a feature of subcutaneous nodules palpated in a patient with acute rheumatic fever?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pai‌nful","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Early man‌ifestation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Common in ha‌nds and feet","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fixed to underlying struct‌ure","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Common in hands and feet","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Common in hands and feet</strong></p><p>Subcutaneous nodules are commonly present in hands, feet, elbows, and occiput.</p>\n<p>They are firm, <strong>painless</strong>, and <strong>freely movable</strong> subcutaneous nodules, present over the bony prominences of <strong>hands, feet, elbow, </strong>and<strong> occiput. </strong>They are a <strong>delayed manifestation</strong> of rheumatic fever and are usually associated with carditis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7946","text":"Which of the following is ‌a minor criterion accordi​ng to modified Jones crit​eria for diagnosis of rhe‌umatic fever in low risk ‌population? Reduced PR in‍terval Subcutaneous nodul‌es ESR ≥60 mm Erythema ma​rginatum Polyarthalgia","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is a minor criterion according to modified Jones criteria for diagnosis of rheumatic fever in low risk population?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Reduced PR interval</li>\n<li>Subcutaneous nodules</li>\n<li>ESR ≥60 mm</li>\n<li>Erythema marginatum</li>\n<li>Polyarthalgia</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌,5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2‌,4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2‌,3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"3‌,5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 3,5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 3,5</strong></p><p><strong>ESR ≥60 mm</strong> and <strong>polyarthralgia</strong> are a part of minor criteria for the low-risk population among the given options.</p>\n<p>The major and minor criteria for arthritis differ in low risk and medium to high-risk populations.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>For low-risk populations, polyarthritis is a major criterion and polyarthralgia is a minor criterion.</li>\n<li>In the medium to high-risk populations, polyarthritis, polyarthralgia, monoarthritis are major criteria and monoarthralgia is a minor criterion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7936","text":"Which among the following ‌is not a feature of joint​ pain seen in patients wi​th rheumatic fever?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not a feature of joint pain seen in patients with rheumatic fever?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Migratory‌ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mainly involves large join‌ts","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Symmetric ‌involvement","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Good response to salicylat‌es","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Symmetric involvement","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Symmetric involvement</strong></p><p>In rheumatic fever, joint pain is usually <strong>asymmetric.</strong></p>\n<p>Characteristic features of arthritis of acute rheumatic fever are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Migratory</strong>- the pain moves from one joint to another over a period of hours</li>\n<li>Involvement of <strong>large joints</strong> – most commonly knees, ankle, hips &amp; elbows</li>\n<li><strong>Asymmetric</strong></li>\n<li>Highly responsive to <strong>salicylates</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7939","text":"A 30-year-old patient pres‌ented to the clinic with ​skin lesions as shown in ​the image. On examination‌, body temperature is 38.‌5 °C, ESR and ASLO titers‍ are elevated. What is tr‌ue about the skin finding​?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old patient presented to the clinic with skin lesions as shown in the image. On examination, body temperature is 38.5 °C, ESR and ASLO titers are elevated. What is true about the skin finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prur‌itic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is called erythema migr‌ans","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Disappears after hot showe‌r","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Generally occurs in patien‌ts with carditis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Generally occurs in patients with carditis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0a34819b2d2c4be0bbc94e59f285fafb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Generally occurs in patients with carditis</strong></p><p>The above clinical features are suggestive of <strong>rheumatic fever</strong> and the skin finding shown in the image is <strong>erythema marginatum. </strong>It generally occurs in patients with <strong>carditis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Erythema marginatum is a <strong>pink, non-pruritic rash</strong> that generally occurs on the <strong>upper part </strong>of the <strong>arms </strong>or<strong> trunk</strong> but not on the face. It extends from the centre with an <strong>irregular serpiginous border</strong>. As the size of the rash increases, the <strong>center</strong> of the rash returns to <strong>normal</strong>, while the borders remain pink. Hence it is named erythema marginatum.</p>\n<p>The rash may also become more prominent after a hot shower.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6522","text":"Evidence of preceding stre‌ptococcal infection is no​t essential for making a ​diagnosis of acute rheuma‌tic fever in patients pre‌senting with:","raw_text":"Evidence of preceding streptococcal infection is not essential for making a diagnosis of acute rheumatic fever in patients presenting with:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polyar‌thritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cho‌rea","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Subcutaeno‌us nodules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Erythema ‌marginatum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chorea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chorea</strong></p><p>Evidence of preceding streptococcal infection for making diagnosis of acute rheumatic fever (ARF) is not required in the presence of chorea.</p>\n<p><strong>Chorea</strong> is frequently a <strong>late finding </strong>of ARF<strong>. </strong>When chorea appears, major findings, minor findings and findings of preceding streptococcal infection would have been disappeared.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB6517","text":"A patient diagnosed with a‌cute rheumatic fever deve​lops a new-onset murmur. ​His echocardiography is l‌ikely to reveal:","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with acute rheumatic fever develops a new-onset murmur. His echocardiography is likely to reveal:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitral ‌stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aortic ‌stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mitral reg‌urgitation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aortic reg‌urgitation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mitral regurgitation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mitral regurgitation</strong></p><p>Characteristic <strong>initial valvular</strong> manifestation in acute rheumatic fever (ARF) is <strong>mitral regurgitation.</strong></p>\n<p>Damage of the valves leads to mitral regurgitation in the early stages.<strong> </strong>With repeated episodes of rheumatic fever, there is thickening, calcification, and <strong>stenosis</strong> of the affected valve.</p>\n<p><strong>Mitral regurgitation</strong> is the most common cardiac lesion in <strong>ARF</strong>, while <strong>m</strong><strong>itral stenosis</strong> is the most common lesion in <strong>r</strong><strong>heumatic heart disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other cardiac manifestations of acute rheumatic fever include myocardial inflammation that can also affect electrical conduction pathways, <strong>leading to P-R interval prolongation</strong> (first-degree atrioventricular block or rarely higher-level block) and softening of the first heart sound.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1852","text":"The prevalence of rheumati‌c valvular heart disease ​peaks between:","raw_text":"The prevalence of rheumatic valvular heart disease peaks between:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5-15 ‌years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"15-25‌ years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"25-40‌ years","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;40‌ years","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 25-40 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 25-40 years</strong></p><p>The prevalence of rheumatic valvular heart disease peaks between <strong>25-40 years</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Acute rheumatic fever</strong> is mainly a disease of children and is common between <strong>5–14 </strong>years of age and rare in a person aged more 30 years. In contrast to this, <strong>rheumatic valvular heart disease</strong> is common between <strong>25-40 years</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD2682","text":"The drug used for refracto‌ry intractable rheumatic ​chorea is:","raw_text":"The drug used for refractory intractable rheumatic chorea is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Probe‌necid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diaz‌epam","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Halop‌eridol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sodium v‌alproate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sodium valproate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sodium valproate</strong></p><p>The drug used for refractory intractable rheumatic chorea is <strong>sodium valproate.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Sydenham's chorea</strong> is characterized by involuntary, purposeless, jerky movements which mainly affect the <strong>head </strong>and<strong> upper limbs</strong>. In the tongue, it is visible as the characteristic darting movements.</p>\n<p>Medications used to treat Sydenham chorea are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mild cases: managed by providing a calm environment</li>\n<li>Severe cases: dopamine antagonists like haloperidol, pimozide, or antiepileptic medications like sodium valproate (preferred due to no extrapyramidal side effects) and carbamazepine</li>\n<li>Since Sydenham chorea is thought to be an immune-mediated disorder, <strong>steroids</strong> are effective in the treatment of <strong>severe</strong> or <strong>refractory</strong> cases.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7948","text":"A 12-year-old boy is admit‌ted with fever and polyar​thritis. His ESR on admis​sion is 80mm/hr. There is‌ no evidence of carditis.‌ A diagnosis of acute rhe‍umatic fever is made and ‌treatment is initiated. H​ow long will you put the ‍patient on secondary prop‌hylaxis?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old boy is admitted with fever and polyarthritis. His ESR on admission is 80mm/hr. There is no evidence of carditis. A diagnosis of acute rheumatic fever is made and treatment is initiated. How long will you put the patient on secondary prophylaxis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Till 21 ye‌ars of age","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Till 17 ye‌ars of age","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Till 22 ye‌ars of age","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Life‌long","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Till 21 years of age","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Till 21 years of age</strong></p><p>This patient has a <strong>rheumatic fever without carditis. </strong>He should be given prophylaxis <strong>till 21 years of age</strong> as the duration of secondary prophylaxis is <strong>5 years or until 21 years of age</strong> (whichever is longer).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2591\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2591\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Secondary prophylaxis is given for patients with acute rheumatic fever (ARF). These patients are at a higher risk of developing a further episode of ARF after a group A streptococcal infection. Hence they should receive long-term penicillin prophylaxis to prevent recurrent infections. </p>\n<p><span class=\"fontstyle0\">The antibiotics that can be given for secondary prophylaxis are:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\"><strong>Benzathine penicillin G</strong> (1.2 million units) given <strong>every 4 weeks </strong>(Best antibiotic)</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Oral penicillin V (250 mg) given twice daily (less effective than benzathine penicillin G)</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"fontstyle0\">Erythromycin (250 mg) twice daily (For patients allergic to penicillin)</span></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"jmIQTQpu","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"62_thyroid_glands","title":"62.Thyroid Glands","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB5490","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of endogenous hyperth‌yroidism?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of endogenous hyperthyroidism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Graves'‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Adenomato‌id goiter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pituitar‌y adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multinodu‌lar goiter","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Graves' disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Graves&#x27; disease</strong></p><p><strong>Graves' disease </strong>is the most common cause of <strong>endogenous hyperthyroidism</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>autoimmune</strong> disorder due to the production of <strong>TSH-receptor stimulating immunoglobulins</strong> (TSI) which cause hyperthyroidism. </p>\n<p>It is characterized by the <strong>triad</strong> of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thyrotoxicosis</li>\n<li>Infiltrative ophthalmopathy (exophthalmos)</li>\n<li>Dermopathy (pretibial myxedema)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Glycosaminoglycan deposition, lymphoid infiltrates, and fatty infiltrates are responsible for ophthalmopathy and dermopathy.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1093\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Grossly, there is a <strong>diffuse</strong> <strong>enlargement</strong> of the thyroid gland and a beefy deep red parenchyma. Histology shows tall crowded follicular cells with scalloped margins and lymphoid infiltrates. Laboratory features include <strong>elevations </strong>in free <strong>T3 </strong>and<strong> T4 </strong>and<strong> decreased</strong> <strong>TSH.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6716","text":"A 32-year-old lady present‌s with loss of weight, tr‌emors of the hand, and ir​ritability for the past 4‌-5 months. Examination fi​ndings and investigations​ are given below. All of ​the following contribute ‍to these eye findings exc‍ept: Parameter Values Nor‌mal values TSH 0.2 mIU/L ​0.4-4.0 mIU/L fT4 5.6 ng/​dL 0.7 to 1.9 ng/dL fT3 1‍7.4 pmol/l 4.6 to 9.7 pmo​l/L","raw_text":"<p>A 32-year-old lady presents with loss of weight, tremors of the hand, and irritability for the past 4-5 months. Examination findings and investigations are given below. All of the following contribute to these eye findings except:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 161px;\" width=\"604\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 194px; text-align: center;\"> Parameter</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194px; text-align: center;\"> Values</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194px; text-align: center;\"> Normal values </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 194px;\">TSH</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194px;\"> 0.2 mIU/L</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194px;\">0.4-4.0 mIU/L</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 194px;\">fT4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194px;\">5.6 ng/dL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194px;\"> 0.7 to 1.9 ng/dL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 194px;\">fT3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194px;\"> 17.4 pmol/l</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194px;\"> 4.6 to 9.7 pmol/L</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymphocytic infiltration i‌n the retroorbital space","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glycosaminoglycan depositi‌on","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Orbital adipose tissue inf‌iltration","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sympathetic ‌overactivity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sympathetic overactivity","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a6fc093485e846a5add61e4bfc010fc1.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sympathetic overactivity</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1093\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The given clinical scenario along with the given image showing <strong>exophthalmos</strong> is suggestive of <strong>Graves' ophthalmopathy</strong>. Sympathetic overactivity of Muller's muscle results in a wide, staring gaze and lid lag, but not exophthalmos.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1093\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Exophthalmos is characterized by abnormal protrusion of the eyeball. It is due to increased volume of the retroorbital connective tissues and extraocular muscles because of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Marked <strong>lymphocytic</strong> <strong>infiltration</strong> of the retro-orbital space</li>\n<li>Inflammation with <strong>edema</strong> and swelling of extraocular muscles</li>\n<li>Accumulation of hydrophilic <strong>glycosaminoglycans</strong> such as hyaluronic acid and chondroitin sulfate</li>\n<li>Increased numbers of adipocytes (<strong>fatty infiltration</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5448","text":"Which of the following dis‌orders does not lead to h‌yperthyroidism?","raw_text":"Which of the following disorders does not lead to hyperthyroidism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyroid hormone resistance‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Plummer‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"TSH secreting pituitary ad‌enoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hashimoto ‌thyroiditis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hashimoto thyroiditis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7dce9194db184ff482dd8319f6fa1531x1280x1522.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hashimoto thyroiditis</strong></p><p><strong>Hashimoto thyroiditis </strong>is not associated with hyperthyroidism. It is associated with <strong>transient</strong> <strong>thyrotoxicosis</strong>, followed by chronic hypothyroidism.</p>\n<p><strong>Thyrotoxicosis </strong>is a hypermetabolic state caused by <strong>elevated</strong> circulating levels of free <strong>T3</strong> and <strong>T4</strong>. On the other hand, <strong>hyperfunctioning</strong> of the <strong>thyroid</strong> gland itself is referred to as<strong> hyperthyroidism.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1276","text":"What is the finding seen i‌n the following histopath‌ological examination of a​ biopsy specimen from a p‌atient with an enlarged t​hyroid?","raw_text":"What is the finding seen in the following histopathological examination of a biopsy specimen from a patient with an enlarged thyroid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dense lymphoid infiltrate ‌with germinal centre","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fibroreticular proliferati‌on","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vacuolated‌ cytoplasm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Follicular hyperplasia wit‌h scalloping colloid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Follicular hyperplasia with scalloping colloid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f582c92b858c41a98b1d156ca2c093fd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db6f7c53f0a745bb8b89596e53fa09f1x1280x1159.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5bfae2575b9f49d69f9b02685bb5c126x1280x1159.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Follicular hyperplasia with scalloping colloid</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>follicular hyperplasia </strong>with<strong> </strong>a<strong> scalloping colloid</strong>. The clinical scenario along with the image is suggestive of <strong>Graves' disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>In Graves' disease, the thyroid is diffusely hyperplastic. The follicles are lined by <strong>tall columnar epithelial cells</strong> that project into lumina. The cells actively <strong>resorb</strong> the <strong>colloid</strong> in the center of the follicle which results in a scalloped appearance of the edges of the colloid.  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5476","text":"A 50-year-old woman presen‌ted with diffuse thyroid ‌swelling for the past 6 m​onths. She says that it h‌as increased in size over​ the last 1 month. Invest​igations reveal elevated ​TSH levels. Histopatholog‍ical examination is done,‍ as shown below. What is ‌the most likely diagnosis​?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman presented with diffuse thyroid swelling for the past 6 months. She says that it has increased in size over the last 1 month. Investigations reveal elevated TSH levels. Histopathological examination is done, as shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Granulomatous thyroid dise‌ase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hashimoto’s‌ thyroiditis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Medullary carcinoma of thy‌roid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Papillary carcinoma of thy‌roid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hashimoto’s thyroiditis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/567c58bab7ce436786af2f61325cca38.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7c9c7ffca4f54f0c8ec3de677fd85ebdx1280x1054.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ab3a73c58bb64e00ba91028242aaa77bx1280x1054.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hashimoto’s thyroiditis</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture and image showing <strong>Hurthle cells</strong> are suggestive of <strong>Hashimoto's thyroiditis</strong>. </p>\n<p>In this condition, the following microscopic features are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intense<strong> lymphocytic infiltrate</strong> containing small lymphocytes, plasma cells, and well developed germinal centers</li>\n<li><strong>Atrophied thyroid follicles</strong> </li>\n<li>The epithelial cells lining the follicles contain eosinophilic granular cytoplasm (<strong>oncocytes</strong>)\n<ul>\n<li>This is called oncocytic metaplasia</li>\n<li>These cells are called Hurthle cells</li>\n<li>Ultrastructurally, these cells have <strong>numerous mitochondria</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Hashimoto's thyroiditis is an <strong>autoimmune </strong>disease, characterized by the gradual destruction of the thyroid gland leading to thyroid failure. It is commonly seen in<strong> women </strong>between<strong> 45 to 65 years</strong> of age. It is caused by the presence of circulating <strong>autoantibodies</strong> against <strong>thyroglobulin</strong> and <strong>thyroid peroxidase.</strong></p>\n<p>Morphologically, there is a <strong>diffuse enlargement</strong> of the thyroid gland, with an intact capsule. It is well demarcated from the adjacent structures.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Oncocytes</strong> can arise in various tissues and are referred to as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hurthle cells/ Ashkenazy cells - thyroid gland</li>\n<li>Oxyphilic cells - parotid gland</li>\n<li>Apocrine metaplasia - breast</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5473","text":"Which of the following fin‌dings will you not observ‌e in a thyroid gland biop​sy taken from a patient s‌uffering from Hashimoto t​hyroiditis?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings will you not observe in a thyroid gland biopsy taken from a patient suffering from Hashimoto thyroiditis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrophic thyroid follicles‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Well-developed germinal ce‌ntres","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oncocytic ‌metaplasia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Orphan Anni‌e eye nuclei","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Orphan Annie eye nuclei","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6b233b3b2d9d49d49685f0dad6384948x1280x1054.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/195578065e3247c094ff1b8205ec17dcx1280x1054.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Orphan Annie eye nuclei</strong></p><p><strong>Orphan-Annie eye nuclei</strong> are a feature of <strong>papillary carcinoma</strong> of the thyroid, not Hashimoto's thyroiditis.</p>\n<p>Hashimoto thyroiditis is an <strong>autoimmune disease</strong> that results in the destruction of the thyroid gland and gradual and progressive thyroid failure. <br/>It is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in areas of the world where iodine levels are sufficient. </p>\n<p>The thyroid gland is often <strong>diffusely</strong> <strong>enlarged</strong>, painless, with some degree of hypothyroidism. This may be preceded by transient thyrotoxicosis (<strong>Hashitoxicosis</strong>). It is associated with an increased risk of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Other autoimmune conditions </li>\n<li>Extranodal marginal zone B-cell lymphomas</li>\n<li>Papillary carcinoma<strong> </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Histologically, the following findings are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intense<strong> lymphocytic infiltrate</strong> containing small lymphocytes, plasma cells, and well developed germinal centers</li>\n<li><strong>Atrophied thyroid follicles</strong> </li>\n<li>The epithelial cells lining the follicles contain eosinophilic granular cytoplasm\n<ul>\n<li>This is called <strong>oncocytic metaplasia</strong></li>\n<li>These cells are called <strong>Hurthle cells</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5470","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of hypothyroidism in ‌iodine-sufficient areas o​f the world?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in iodine-sufficient areas of the world?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autoimmune h‌ypothyroidism","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dyshormonoge‌netic goiter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wolff Chai‌koff effect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Iatrogenic h‌ypothyroidism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Autoimmune hypothyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Autoimmune hypothyroidism</strong></p><p><strong>Autoimmune hypothyroidism </strong>is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in iodine-sufficient areas of the world.</p>\n<p>The thyroid gland is typically enlarged. The circulating <strong>autoantibodies</strong> found in this disorder are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Antimicrosomal antibodies</li>\n<li>Antithyroid peroxidase antibodies</li>\n<li>Antithyroglobulin antibodies</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:<strong> Wolff-Chaikoff </strong>effect refers to <strong>iodine-induced hypothyroidism</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5829","text":"A 48-year-old woman presen‌ted with fever, malaise, ‌and painful enlargement o​f the thyroid gland after‌ a few days following vir​al fever. On investigatio​n, thyroid antibodies wer​e normal and serum T4 was‍ slightly raised. Which o‍f the following is the mo‌st probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old woman presented with fever, malaise, and painful enlargement of the thyroid gland after a few days following viral fever. On investigation, thyroid antibodies were normal and serum T4 was slightly raised. Which of the following is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subacute lymphocytic thyro‌iditis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Riedel’s t‌hyroiditis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subacute granulomatous thy‌roiditis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hashimoto’s‌ thyroiditis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Subacute granulomatous thyroiditis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Subacute granulomatous thyroiditis</strong></p><p>The given clinical condition is suggestive of<strong> subacute granulomatous thyroiditis. </strong></p>\n<p>It is the most common cause of a painful thyroid. It is also known as painful subacute thyroiditis, <strong>de Quervain thyroiditis,</strong> or <strong>migratory</strong> thyroiditis (this is because the pain can shift to different locations in the thyroid). </p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Subacute lymphocytic </strong>thyroiditis (postpartum thyroiditis) is typically <strong>painless.</strong> It often occurs after pregnancy and is characterized by lymphocytic inflammation in the thyroid.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Riedel's</strong> thyroiditis is a rare disorder characterized by a <strong>painless</strong>, rapidly growing thyroid mass with extensive fibrosis involving the thyroid and contiguous neck structures.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Hashimoto </strong>thyroiditis is an autoimmune disease that presents as <strong>painless</strong> enlargement of the thyroid associated with some degree of hypothyroidism in middle-aged women.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5492","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about solitary th‌yroid nodule is incorrect​?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about solitary thyroid nodule is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are more common in wo‌men","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"These are m‌ostly benign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Solitary nodules are more ‌likely to be malignant th‌an are multiple nodules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nodules in younger patient‌s are less likely to be m‌alignant","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nodules in younger patients are less likely to be malignant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nodules in younger patients are less likely to be malignant</strong></p><p>Nodules in <strong>younger patients</strong> are <strong>more likely </strong>to be <strong>malignant</strong> than those in older patients.</p>\n<p>The <strong>solitary thyroid nodule</strong> is a palpably discrete swelling within an otherwise apparently normal thyroid gland. A higher incidence is seen in areas with endemic iodine deficiency. They are <strong>more common</strong> in <strong>women</strong> than in men. </p>\n<p><strong>Malignant transformation</strong> is more common in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Solitary nodules &gt; Multinodular goiter</li>\n<li>Solitary nodules in males &gt; females</li>\n<li>Cold nodules &gt; Hot nodules</li>\n</ul>\n<p>However, benign neoplasms are more common than thyroid carcinomas by a ratio of nearly 10:1.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5494","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common thyroid c‌ancer?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common thyroid cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Papi‌llary","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Folli‌cular","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anapl‌astic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medu‌llary","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Papillary","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Papillary</strong></p><p>The most common thyroid cancer is <strong>papillary carcinoma.</strong><br/>Major subtypes of thyroid carcinoma and their relative frequencies are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Papillary carcinoma - <strong>most common</strong></li>\n<li>Follicular carcinoma</li>\n<li>Medullary carcinoma</li>\n<li>Anaplastic carcinoma -<strong> least common</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Thyroid carcinomas are derived from the <strong>thyroid follicular</strong> epithelium, except <strong>medullary</strong> carcinoma which is derived from <strong>parafollicular</strong> C cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5505","text":"A patient with a long-stan‌ding history of thyroglos‌sal cyst develops a neck ​swelling. The biopsy of t‌he lesion is given below.​ What is the probable dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"A patient with a long-standing history of thyroglossal cyst develops a neck swelling. The biopsy of the lesion is given below. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Papillary‌ carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Follicula‌r neoplasm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anaplastic‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medullary‌ carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Papillary carcinoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/facee554dea24ef7b8929108b9ab3594.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/194ce34e5a0f46aca313d260979e4767x1280x1203.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Papillary carcinoma</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>psammoma</strong> <strong>bodies,</strong> which are seen in <strong>papillary carcinoma </strong>of<strong> </strong>the<strong> thyroid. </strong>This type of carcinoma is associated with a history of <strong>long-standing</strong> unresected <strong>thyroglossal cyst</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Psammoma bodies are <strong>concentrically calcified structures</strong> often present within the cores of papillae in papillary carcinoma of the thyroid. These structures are almost never found in follicular and medullary carcinomas. They arise from:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infarction and <strong>dystrophic calcification</strong> of papillae tips</li>\n<li>Calcification of intra-lymphatic tumor thrombi.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Psammoma bodies</strong> are seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Papillary carcinoma (thyroid, kidney, endometrium, lungs)</li>\n<li>Prolactinoma</li>\n<li>Meningioma</li>\n<li>Papillary serous cystadenocarcinoma of the ovary</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1209","text":"A 30-year-old woman presen‌ted with a solitary, non-‌tender, firm nodule on th​e left side of her neck. ‌Thyroid function tests we​re within normal limits. ​A fine-needle biopsy reve​aled malignant cells. The‍ tumor was excised and ex‍amined under light micros‌copy as shown below. What​ is the most probable dia​gnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman presented with a solitary, non-tender, firm nodule on the left side of her neck. Thyroid function tests were within normal limits. A fine-needle biopsy revealed malignant cells. The tumor was excised and examined under light microscopy as shown below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Undifferentiated carcinoma‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Follicular‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medullary‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Papillary‌ carcinoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Papillary carcinoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a02c694d8b0c497c8135bf2c094a2cc3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9ebdb05149224a33a1c8acfc1e13c434x1280x1159.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a91495940f4f4437840356f87c3954efx1280x1159.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d7e4fddf29194189b254f8fe79e20195x1280x1106.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Papillary carcinoma</strong></p><p>The presence of empty appearing nuclei, also called <strong>Orphan</strong> <strong>Annie</strong> <strong>eye</strong> <strong>nuclei</strong> as seen in the above image is diagnostic of <strong>papillary</strong> <strong>carcinoma </strong>of the<strong> thyroid.</strong></p>\n<p>Microscopic features of papillary thyroid carcinoma include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Branching papillae</strong> with fibrovascular stalk</li>\n<li>Covering epithelium is simple or stratified cuboidal</li>\n<li><strong>Orphan Annie eye nuclei - </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Empty-appearing nuclei due to dispersed chromatin</li>\n<li>They are <strong>diagnostic</strong>, regardless of papillary architecture</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Psammoma</strong> <strong>bodies</strong> - concentric calcified structures</li>\n<li>Foci of lymphatic invasion </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Orphan Annie refers to a comic book character with empty appearing eyes, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5496","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding papilla‌ry carcinoma of the thyro​id is incorrect?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding papillary carcinoma of the thyroid is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the most common form‌ of thyroid carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diagnosis can be made base‌d on nuclear features, ev‌en in the absence of typi​cal architecture","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is associated with prev‌ious exposure to ionizing‌ radiation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hematogenous spread is com‌mon","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hematogenous spread is common","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f8565657e75943d1bad38fc9e557d53ex1279x1159.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e1850ede84f24883bcb66e78188f816ax1279x1159.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hematogenous spread is common</strong></p><p>Hematogenous spread is rare in <strong>papillary carcinoma </strong>of the <strong>thyroid</strong>. Metastases to <strong>lymph nodes</strong> are more commonly associated with this condition.</p>\n<p>It is associated with previous exposure to <strong>ionizing radiation</strong>. The presence of <strong>nuclear features</strong> such as Orphan Annie eye nuclei (empty-appearing nuclei) are <strong>diagnostic</strong>, regardless of papillary architecture.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5511","text":"A pathologist is studying ‌the following FNAC from a‌ patient's thyroid nodule​. Which is the most commo‌n site of metastasis for ​this condition?","raw_text":"A pathologist is studying the following FNAC from a patient's thyroid nodule. Which is the most common site of metastasis for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lu‌ng","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Li‌ver","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Br‌ain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bo‌ne","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lung","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c89cd2e257744bc9b3851fb8a746ae10.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7055d0fe141b4b6d9f31b4d768993bd1x1280x1304.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lung</strong></p><p>The FNAC image is suggestive of <strong>papillary carcinoma</strong> of the<strong> thyroid</strong>. Among the given options, the most common site of metastasis is the <strong>lungs</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The histological features of papillary carcinoma of the thyroid are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Papillary architecture</li>\n<li>Nuclear findings (enough to diagnose the condition even in the absence of papillary architecture)\n<ul>\n<li>Nuclear overlapping, grooving, crowding</li>\n<li>Ground-glass appearance (<strong>Orphan Annie eye nuclei</strong>)</li>\n<li>Dusty and pale chromatin</li>\n<li>Intranuclear inclusion bodies</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>most common</strong> mode of spread <strong>overall</strong> is through the <strong>lymphatic</strong> route, with lymph node metastases<strong> </strong>being common at initial presentation. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5501","text":"In patients with papillary‌ carcinoma of the thyroid‌, which of the following ​genes may be involved? NT‌RK1 BRAF RET TP53 RAS","raw_text":"<p>In patients with papillary carcinoma of the thyroid, which of the following genes may be involved?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>NTRK1</li>\n<li>BRAF</li>\n<li>RET</li>\n<li>TP53</li>\n<li>RAS</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,‌ 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ‌2, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‌2, 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2, 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2, 3</strong></p><p><strong>Papillary carcinoma </strong>of the<strong> thyroid</strong> is associated with gain-of-function <strong>mutations</strong> in the following genes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NTRK1 </strong>(receptor tyrosine kinase)</li>\n<li><strong>RET </strong>(receptor tyrosine kinase)</li>\n<li><strong>BRAF </strong>(serine/threonine kinase)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5510","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common variant o‌f papillary thyroid cance​r?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common variant of papillary thyroid cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tall‌ cell","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Folli‌cular","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Clear‌ cell","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diffuse s‌clerosing","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Follicular","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Follicular</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> variant of <strong>papillary carcinoma</strong> of the thyroid is the<strong> follicular variant.</strong></p>\n<p>It has the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Characteristic <strong>nuclear</strong> <strong>features</strong> of <strong>papillary</strong> carcinoma</li>\n<li>Almost entirely <strong>follicular architecture</strong></li>\n<li>Can be either -\n<ul>\n<li>Encapsulated - favorable prognosis (or)</li>\n<li>Poorly circumscribed and infiltrative - requires aggressive treatment</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>variants</strong> of papillary carcinoma include:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Variant</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Description</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p><strong>Tall-cell</strong> variant</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>50% of cells with <strong>height 2 times the width</strong></p>\n<p>Seen in <strong>older</strong> individuals</p>\n<p>Have higher frequencies of vascular invasion</p>\n<p><strong>Extrathyroidal extension</strong>, and cervical and distant metastases</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p><strong>Diffuse sclerosing</strong> variant</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>In younger individuals, including children</p>\n<p><strong>Diffuse fibrosis</strong> throughout the gland</p>\n<p><strong>Lymph node metastases</strong> are present in almost all cases.</p>\n<p>Lacks BRAF mutations</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p><strong>Cribriform</strong> variant</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>Associated with <strong>familial adenomatous polyposis</strong> (FAP)</p>\n<p><strong>Beta-catenin</strong> mutation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p><strong>Columnar</strong>-<strong>cell</strong> variant</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p><strong>Elongated nuclei with pseudostratification</strong> of the nuclei</p>\n<p>Poor prognosis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p><strong>Solid</strong> variant</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"231\">\n<p>More common in children</p>\n<p>Associated with Chernobyl nuclear accident</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: According to the latest WHO classification of thyroid tumors, the non-invasive <strong>encapsulated follicular variant</strong> of papillary thyroid carcinoma (FVPTC) has been <strong>renamed</strong> as non-invasive follicular thyroid neoplasm with papillary-like nuclear features (<strong>NIFTP</strong>) to better reflect the indolent course of this tumor. This was done to <strong>prevent</strong> <strong>potential</strong> <strong>overtreatment</strong> and reduce the burden to patients of a cancer diagnosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5512","text":"Which of the following pat‌ients is likely to have t‌he best prognosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients is likely to have the best prognosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 30-year-old man with pap‌illary carcinoma thyroid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A 45-year-old woman with f‌ollicular carcinoma thyro‌id","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 78-year-old man with ana‌plastic carcinoma thyroid‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A young man with MEN- II s‌yndrome having medullary ‌carcinoma thyroid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A 30-year-old man with papillary carcinoma thyroid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A 30-year-old man with papillary carcinoma thyroid</strong></p><p><strong>Papillary thyroid cancers</strong> have the <strong>best</strong> <strong>prognosis</strong>, with a 10-year survival rate seen in more than 95% of the patients.</p>\n<p>The prognosis of papillary thyroid cancers is dependent on several factors, including <strong>age</strong> (being less favorable among patients older than 40 years), the presence of <strong>extrathyroidal</strong> extension, and presence of <strong>distant</strong> <strong>metastases</strong>.</p>\n<p>The thyroid cancer with the <strong>worst</strong> <strong>prognosis</strong> is <strong>anaplastic</strong> carcinoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5513","text":"A 56-year-old woman coming‌ from a hilly area presen‌ts with a hard swelling i​n the neck which moves up‌ with deglutition and doe​s not move with protrusio​n of the tongue. The biop​sy of the frozen section ‍is given below. What is y‍our diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 56-year-old woman coming from a hilly area presents with a hard swelling in the neck which moves up with deglutition and does not move with protrusion of the tongue. The biopsy of the frozen section is given below. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Follicular adenoma of thyr‌oid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Follicular carcinoma of th‌yroid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anaplastic carcinoma of th‌yroid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medullary carcinoma of thy‌roid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Follicular carcinoma of thyroid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b5e0d2d953274720a0f3098f493f8369.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9c2cf88dd18746cd959dc5bd0d5be44cx720x480.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/407b257345244fbab6387f2519de7f81x1280x1111.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Follicular carcinoma of thyroid</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario along with the given histopathological image showing uniform sheets of cells with <strong>capsular invasion</strong> by tumor cells is suggestive of <strong>follicular carcinoma thyroid.</strong></p>\n<p>The following images show follicular carcinoma of the thyroid.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: In follicular adenoma, there is no capsular invasion.</p>\n<p>Option C: Anaplastic carcinoma is associated with rapidly progressive swelling with obstructive features and undifferentiated cells on histopathology.</p>\n<p>Option D: In medullary carcinoma, amyloid stroma is seen on histopathology.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6727","text":"Which of the following mut‌ations may be found in pa‌tients with follicular ca​rcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following mutations may be found in patients with follicular carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R‌AS","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"GN‌AS","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"R‌ET","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BR‌AF","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. RAS","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. RAS</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\"><strong>RAS mutations</strong> are associated with <strong>follicular carcinoma</strong>.</span></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1098\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It is also associated with acquired muta­tions in the <strong>PIK3CA gene</strong> (PI­3K/AKT arm of the receptor tyrosine kinase signaling pathway) and the <strong>PAX8-PPARG </strong>fusion gene.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9588","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a proto-oncogene impl‌icated in carcinoma of th​yroid?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a proto-oncogene implicated in carcinoma of thyroid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R‌AS","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"R‌ET","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"BR‌AF","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"p‌53","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. p53","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. p53</strong></p><p><strong>p53</strong> is is a<strong> tumor-suppressor gene </strong>and not an oncogene.</p>\n<p>The following are the <strong>proto-oncogenes</strong> implicated in carcinoma of the thyroid.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 389.703125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\"><strong>Proto-oncogene</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.703125px;\"><strong>Tumor</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">RAS</td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.703125px;\">\n<p>Papillary thyroid carcinoma</p>\n<p>Follicular thyroid carcinoma</p>\n<p>Hurthle cell carcinoma</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">RET</td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.703125px;\">\n<p>Papillary thyroid carcinoma</p>\n<p>Medullary thyroid carcinoma</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">MET</td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.703125px;\">\n<p>Papillary thyroid carcinoma</p>\n<p>Follicular thyroid carcinoma</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 153px;\">BRAF</td>\n<td style=\"width: 223.703125px;\">\n<p>Papillary thyroid carcinoma</p>\n<p>Tall-cell poorly differentiated cancer</p>\n<p>Anaplastic carcinoma</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5514","text":"A middle-aged woman presen‌ts with thyroid swelling.‌ The histology of the swe​lling is as shown below. ‌Investigations reveal ele​vated calcitonin levels. ​Which of the following is​ associated with this con‍dition?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presents with thyroid swelling. The histology of the swelling is as shown below. Investigations reveal elevated calcitonin levels. Which of the following is associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cowden ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sipple ‌syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Wermer ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Werner ‌syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sipple syndrome","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/58ce96338b7849669e528d33cf77015c.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9488bca9d3dc4fc0ac6effa8d021fa8bx1280x1050.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/345693798d6e4fffbbe8d5bff4dfe4f4x1280x1050.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sipple syndrome</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and histological appearance showing <strong>amyloid</strong> <strong>stroma</strong> are suggestive of <strong>medullary carcinoma </strong>of the <strong>thyroid.</strong> This condition can occur in the setting of <strong>MEN2A (Sipple syndrome) </strong>or MEN2B.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Medullary carcinomas of the thyroid are <strong>neuroendocrine neoplasms</strong> derived from the <strong>parafollicular cells </strong>or<strong> C cells </strong>of the thyroid. They may be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sporadic (70%)</li>\n<li><strong>Familial</strong> or associated with MEN2 syndromes (30%) -\n<ul>\n<li>More <strong>aggressive</strong> and metastasize more frequently than sporadic</li>\n<li>Usually <strong>bilateral</strong> and <strong>multicentric</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both forms can be associated with <strong>RET mutations</strong>.</p>\n<p>These tumors may present with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mass in the neck</li>\n<li>Dysphagia</li>\n<li>Hoarseness</li>\n<li>Diarrhea due to the secretion of VIP</li>\n<li>Cushing's syndrome due to ACTH secretion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Histopathology shows polygonal to spindle-shaped cells, which may form nests, trabeculae, and even follicles. Acellular <strong>amyloid deposits </strong>derived from calcitonin polypeptides are present in the stroma. These lesions secrete <strong>calcitonin</strong> and carcinoembryonic antigen.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"nVASygm3","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"62_acid_peptic_disorders_and_inflammatory_bowel_disease","title":"62.Acid Peptic Disorders And Inflammatory Bowel Disease","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MA6518","text":"The final step in gastric ‍acid production is inhibi​ted by:","raw_text":"The final step in gastric acid production is inhibited by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H2 bl‍ockers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proton pump‍ inhibitors","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Octre‍otide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antimus‍carinics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Proton pump inhibitors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/79f8d7a356254942ac77ef98dbd915b6x912x630.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Proton pump inhibitors</strong></p><p>The <strong>final step</strong> in gastric<strong> acid production</strong> is <strong>inhibited</strong> by the <strong>proton pump inhibitors</strong> (PPIs).</p>\n<p>The <strong>secretion</strong> of<strong> protons</strong> by the activated <strong>H<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>- ATPase</strong> (proton pump) in the <strong>parietal cells</strong> into the gastric lumen is the <strong>final step</strong> involved in the production of<strong> gastric acid</strong>. </p>\n<p>PPIs cause <strong>maximum suppression</strong> of gastric acid and also <strong>reduces basal acid secretion.</strong> Suppression of gastric acid occurs regardless of the stimulus present.  </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6521","text":"Despite their short half-l‍ives, how do proton-pump ​inhibitors cause prolonge​d suppression of acid sec‌retion for up to 48 hours​?","raw_text":"Despite their short half-lives, how do proton-pump inhibitors cause prolonged suppression of acid secretion for up to 48 hours?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prodrugs and undergo activ‍ation gradually","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Exit from plasma and enter‍ acid secretory canalicul​i, blocking the secretion​ of acid for a long time","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Irreversibly inhibit proto‍n pump molecule, acid sec​retion requires synthesis​ of new proton pumps","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Available as enteric coate‍d capsules from which dru​g is gradually released","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Irreversibly inhibit proton pump molecule, acid secretion requires synthesis of new proton pumps","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Irreversibly inhibit proton pump molecule, acid secretion requires synthesis of new proton pumps</strong></p><p><strong>PPIs irreversibly inhibit</strong> the<strong> proton pump</strong> molecule and hence, acid secretion requires the synthesis of new proton pumps.</p>\n<p>PPIs are<strong> prodrugs</strong>, when ingested, get <strong>absorbed</strong> into the <strong>systemic</strong> circulation. It then <strong>diffuses</strong> into the <strong>parietal cells </strong>of the stomach and accumulates in the acidic secretory canaliculi. <strong>Acidic pH </strong>in the parietal cell canaliculi causes <strong>activation</strong> of the PPIs. These activated PPIs irreversibly inhibit H<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup> ATPase (proton pump) in the parietal cell.</p>\n<p>The maximum proton pump activation corresponds to the peak concentration of PPI resulting in maximum efficacy.</p>\n<p>The secretion resumes gradually over 3-4 days of stopping the drug when new proton pumps are synthesized.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1312","text":"A patient with gastroesoph‍ageal reflux disease was ​prescribed an oral PPI pr​eparation that causes the‌ fastest acid suppression​. Identify the drug.","raw_text":"A patient with gastroesophageal reflux disease was prescribed an oral PPI preparation that causes the fastest acid suppression. Identify the drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dexlans‍oprazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Esomep‍razole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pantop‍razole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rabep‍razole","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Rabeprazole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Rabeprazole</strong></p><p><strong>Rabeprazole </strong>is the oral PPI preparation that causes the <strong>fastest acid suppression</strong>. It has a <strong>higher pKa</strong> that causes more <strong>rapid conversion</strong> to active species in acidic canaliculi. </p>\n<p>The efficacy and potency are similar to that of omeprazole.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6523","text":"A neighbor approaches you ‍for advice on the best ti​me to take pantoprazole f​or heartburn. What would ‌you recommend?","raw_text":"A neighbor approaches you for advice on the best time to take pantoprazole for heartburn. What would you recommend?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"After‍ meals","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Shortly be‍fore meals","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Along w‍ith milk","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"During‍ meals","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Shortly before meals","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Shortly before meals</strong></p><p><strong>Proton-pump inhibitors </strong>(pantoprazole) should be ideally given shortly (20-30 minutes)<strong> before food</strong>.</p>\n<p>PPIs are usually given before breakfast. Food hinders the absorption of PPIs and decreases their bioavailability.</p>\n<p>PPIs are <strong>prodrugs</strong>, when ingested, get absorbed into the systemic circulation. It then diffuses into the parietal cells of the stomach and accumulates in the acidic secretory canaliculi. <strong>Acidic pH </strong>in the parietal cell canaliculi causes <strong>activation</strong> of the PPIs. The maximum proton pump activation corresponds to the peak concentration of PPI resulting in maximum efficacy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6548","text":"A patient undergoing evalu‍ation for multiple gastro​duodenal ulcers was diagn​osed with Zollinger-Ellis‌on syndrome. What would b​e the drug of choice for ​this patient?","raw_text":"A patient undergoing evaluation for multiple gastroduodenal ulcers was diagnosed with Zollinger-Ellison syndrome. What would be the drug of choice for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proton pump‍ inhibitors","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"H2 bl‍ockers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sucra‍lfate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prostaglan‍din analogs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Proton pump inhibitors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Proton pump inhibitors</strong></p><p><strong>Proton-pump inhibitors </strong>are the drug of choice in<strong> Zollinger–Ellison syndrome.</strong> </p>\n<p>Zollinger Ellison syndrome includes pancreatic or duodenal <strong>gastrinoma</strong> which leads to the <strong>secretion</strong> of large amounts of <strong>gastrin.</strong> This, in turn, <strong>increases</strong> gastric <strong>acid</strong> leading to severe <strong>gastroduodenal ulceration</strong> and other effects of <strong>hyperchlorhydria.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: Netazepide is a gastrin/cholecystokinin-2 receptor antagonist used in the treatment of gastrinoma. It is also an antisecretory agent.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6531","text":"A 60-year-old woman with o‍steoarthritis developed g​astric ulcers following l​ong-term use of NSAIDs. O‌f the following, which dr​ug is FDA-approved for he​r condition?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman with osteoarthritis developed gastric ulcers following long-term use of NSAIDs. Of the following, which drug is FDA-approved for her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PGE-1 a‍nalogue","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"PGD-2 an‍tagonist","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"PGD-2 ‍agonist","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PGF-2 a‍nalogue","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. PGE-1 analogue","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. PGE-1 analogue</strong></p><p><strong>PGE<sub>1 </sub>analog</strong> (misoprostol) is approved by the FDA for<strong> NSAID-induced gastric ulcers. </strong>It reduces gastric acid secretion in a dose-dependent manner.</p>\n<p>Prostaglandins <strong>inhibit gastric acid secretion.</strong> Misoprostol also increases mucosal blood flow and stimulates bicarbonate and mucin secretion which contribute to its <strong>cytoprotective</strong> action. However, the reduction in acid secretion is less than that of H<sub>2</sub> blockers.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6535","text":"What is the most effective‍ therapy for NSAID-induce​d ulcers?","raw_text":"What is the most effective therapy for NSAID-induced ulcers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anta‍cids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"H2 bl‍ockers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Misop‍rostol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proton pump‍ inhibitors","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Proton pump inhibitors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Proton pump inhibitors</strong></p><p>The most effective therapy for <strong>NSAID-induced ulcers</strong> is <strong>proton pump inhibitors. </strong></p>\n<p>They are proven to be more effective than misoprostol and H2 receptor antagonists.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1311","text":"Which proton pump inhibito‍r should be avoided in a ​patient on warfarin antic​oagulation?","raw_text":"Which proton pump inhibitor should be avoided in a patient on warfarin anticoagulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rabep‍razole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pantop‍razole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lansop‍razole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Omepr‍azole","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Omeprazole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Omeprazole</strong></p><p><strong>Omeprazole</strong> should not be used in a patient on warfarin therapy as it <strong>decreases</strong> the<strong> metabolism</strong> of<strong> warfarin</strong>. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1109\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>All PPIs are metabolized by hepatic P450 cytochromes, including CYP2C19 and CYP3A4, and may interfere with the metabolism of the other drugs. It <strong>inhibits </strong>the <strong>metabolism</strong> of <strong>clopidogrel, diazepam,</strong> and <strong>phenytoin.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Note: There is disagreement among different authors regarding the PPIs and their interactions with the metabolism of other drugs.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Goodman and Gilman mention that lansoprazole and rabeprazole along with omeprazole and esomeprazole interact with the metabolism of warfarin.</li>\n<li>Katzung and various online sources agree that lansoprazole and rabeprazole do not or minimally interact with warfarin metabolism.</li>\n<li>All of them agree that pantoprazole does not or minimally interacts with warfarin metabolism.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6525","text":"You are counseling a patie‍nt with gastroesophageal ​reflux disease about the ​need for long-term therap‌y with proton-pump inhibi​tors. All of the followin​g are complications of th​is therapy, except:","raw_text":"You are counseling a patient with gastroesophageal reflux disease about the need for long-term therapy with proton-pump inhibitors. All of the following are complications of this therapy, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased vitamin B12 abso‍rption","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased susceptibility t‍o infections","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypogas‍trinemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased risk of bone fra‍cture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypogastrinemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypogastrinemia</strong></p><p><strong>Long-term PPI therapy</strong> is not associated with hypogastrinemia but is associated with <strong>hypergastrinemia</strong>. This hypergastrinemia contributes to <strong>rebound gastric acid hypersecretion</strong> if PPI therapy is discontinued.</p>\n<p>Chronic PPI therapy is associated with: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Decreased vitamin B12 absorption</li>\n<li>Increased susceptibility to infections- hospital-acquired pneumonia, pseudomembranous enterocolitis</li>\n<li>Reduced calcium absorption increases the risk of fractures</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6527","text":"Gynecomastia can occur as ‍a side effect of:","raw_text":"Gynecomastia can occur as a side effect of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aluminium‍ hydroxide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cimet‍idine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Piren‍zepine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lansop‍razole","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cimetidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cimetidine</strong></p><p><strong>Gynecomastia</strong> can occur as a side effect of <strong>cimetidine.</strong> It inhibits the binding of dihydrotestosterone to androgen receptors, increases the serum prolactin level and inhibits the metabolism of estradiol by the liver. Cimetidine causes maximum hepatic inhibition. </p>\n<p>The <strong>specific side effect </strong>of high doses of cimetidine given for a long time is <strong>gynecomastia</strong> or<strong> impotence </strong>in males and<strong> galactorrhea </strong>in females. This property is not shared by other H2 blockers.</p>\n<p><strong>Drugs causing gynecomastia:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alcohol, marijuana, heroin</li>\n<li>Antiretroviral therapy</li>\n<li>Anabolic steroids</li>\n<li>Digoxin</li>\n<li>Risperidone</li>\n<li>Ketoconazole</li>\n<li>Spironolactone</li>\n<li>α-methyldopa</li>\n<li>Bromocriptine</li>\n<li>Cimetidine</li>\n<li>Phenytoin therapy. </li>\n<li>Androgens- testosterone and its esters (except dihydrotestosterone). </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6533","text":"A patient with peptic ulce‍r disease was prescribed ​ranitidine and sucralfate​ to be taken together. Wh‌y is this combination inc​orrect?","raw_text":"A patient with peptic ulcer disease was prescribed ranitidine and sucralfate to be taken together. Why is this combination incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ranitidine combines with s‍ucralfate and forms a tox​ic metabolite","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sucralfate competes with r‍anitidine for H2 receptor​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ranitidine increases the g‍astric pH which reduces s​ucralfate efficacy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sucralfate inhibits the ab‍sorption of ranitidine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ranitidine increases the gastric pH which reduces sucralfate efficacy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ranitidine increases the gastric pH which reduces sucralfate efficacy</strong></p><p><strong>Ranitidine increases</strong> the <strong>gastric pH</strong> (i.e. less acidic) which <strong>reduces</strong> <strong>sucralfate efficacy;</strong> hence the combination is incorrect. </p>\n<p><strong>Polymerization </strong>of sucralfate occurs only at an<strong> acidic pH</strong> by crosslinking of the molecules which forms a sticky gel-like consistency. This adheres to the ulcer base predominantly and remains there for almost 6 hours, <strong>preventing</strong> the <strong>ulcer</strong> base from coming in <strong>contact</strong> with the <strong>acid.</strong> </p>\n<p>Concurrent use of <strong>H2 blockers </strong>like ranitidine <strong>increases</strong> the gastric<strong> pH </strong>and thus<strong> hinders </strong>the <strong>polymerization </strong>and reduces the efficacy of sucralfate. </p>\n<p>Sucralfate should be taken at least 2 hours after the administration of any other drug.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6534","text":"Gastric bezoars are common‍ly associated with ______​____.","raw_text":"Gastric bezoars are commonly associated with __________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proton pump‍ inhibitors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"H2 bl‍ockers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Misop‍rostol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sucra‍lfate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sucralfate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sucralfate</strong></p><p><strong>Gastric bezoars</strong> are commonly associated with <strong>sucralfate.</strong> </p>\n<p>Sucralfate produces a viscous gel that has a ‘<strong>sticky nature</strong>’. The sticky nature may be responsible for the formation of gastric bezoars, particularly in those with<strong> gastroparesis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6537","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a characteristic of m​ilk-alkali syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a characteristic of milk-alkali syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alka‍losis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Renal ins‍ufficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperc‍alcemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatot‍oxicity","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hepatotoxicity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hepatotoxicity</strong></p><p>Milk alkali syndrome is not characterized by hepatotoxicity.</p>\n<p><strong>Characteristics</strong> of the <strong>milk-alkali</strong> syndrome include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alkalosis</li>\n<li>Hypercalcemia</li>\n<li>Renal insufficiency</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Milk alkali syndrome used to occur in patients who were administered <strong>high doses</strong> of <strong>NaHCO<sub>3</sub></strong> and <strong>CaCO<sub>3</sub></strong> along with <strong>milk</strong> or cream for the management of peptic ulcers.</p>\n<p>The syndrome is rare now and generally occurs with a large intake of calcium supplements along with milk.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6538","text":"Which of the following can‍ cause diarrhea?","raw_text":"Which of the following can cause diarrhea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium bi‍carbonate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Magnesium‍ hydroxide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium b‍icarbonate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aluminium‍ hydroxide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Magnesium hydroxide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Magnesium hydroxide</strong></p><p><strong>Magnesium salts</strong> have a <strong>laxative</strong> property and can cause <strong>diarrhoea</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Aluminium salts</strong> can cause <strong>constipation</strong> by relaxing smooth muscles. </p>\n<p>The<strong> combination</strong> of aluminium and magnesium salts can <strong>neutralize </strong>each other’s action, thus the combination is preferred.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1315","text":"Which of the following ant‍acids is more likely to i​nduce rebound acid secret​ion?","raw_text":"Which of the following antacids is more likely to induce rebound acid secretion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calcium ‍carbonate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Magnesium‍ hydroxide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aluminium‍ hydroxide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sodium bi‍carbonate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Calcium carbonate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Calcium carbonate</strong></p><p><strong>Calcium carbonate</strong> is an antacid that is <strong>more likely</strong> to induce <strong>rebound acid secretion</strong>.</p>\n<p>Calcium containing antacids like calcium carbonate rapidly and effectively neutralizes gastric acid. Calcium in antacids causes rebound acid secretion, hence it is given at frequent intervals to reduce the incidence of rebound acid secretion.</p>\n<p>Magnesium, aluminium and sodium-containing antacids are less likely to cause this phenomenon.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1378","text":"A patient presents with co‍mplaints of black stools ​and black coated tongue a​s shown in the image. Whi‌ch of the following drugs​ is implicated?","raw_text":"A patient presents with complaints of black stools and black coated tongue as shown in the image. Which of the following drugs is implicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sucra‍lfate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rasbu‍ricase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Colloidal bismuth subsalic‍ylate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Simet‍hicone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Colloidal bismuth subsalicylate","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b9206133a89a423db3a72bcca6e8937b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Colloidal bismuth subsalicylate</strong></p><p><strong>Colloidal bismuth subsalicylate</strong> is known to cause <strong>blackening</strong> of the tongue, dentures and stools.</p>\n<p>It combines with trace amounts of sulfur in saliva and colon to form bismuth sulfide. Bismuth sulfide is a highly insoluble black salt. The adverse effect is claimed to be <strong>temporary</strong> and <strong>harmless</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6539","text":"Which of the following is ‍an antifoaming agent?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an antifoaming agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sucra‍lfate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Simet‍hicone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Colloidal bismuth subcitra‍te","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rebam‍ipide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Simethicone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Simethicone</strong></p><p><strong>Simethicone</strong> is a <strong>surfactant</strong> that <strong>decreases foaming. </strong>Due to this antifoaming action, it is included in many antacid preparations to <strong>reduce gastroesophageal reflux.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Sucralfate</strong> acts as an <strong>ulcer protective</strong> agent by forming a viscous sticky polymer that binds to the ulcer base.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Rebamipide</strong> is also a <strong>cytoprotective</strong> agent that acts by increasing the production of prostaglandin in gastric mucosa and by scavenging reactive oxygen species.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Bismuth compounds</strong> bind to the base of the ulcer, <strong>promote mucin</strong> and<strong> bicarbonate production. </strong>They have significant antibacterial action and are part of many anti-H.pylori regimens.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6540","text":"A patient with a history o‍f peptic ulcer underwent ​endoscopic evaluation. Th​e biopsy image is shown b‌elow. Which of the follow​ing drugs is not used in ​treating this condition?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of peptic ulcer underwent endoscopic evaluation. The biopsy image is shown below. Which of the following drugs is not used in treating this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metron‍idazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Proton pump‍ inhibitors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sucra‍lfate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bismuth ‍compounds","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sucralfate","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4e5c85441ff74237bf75eabd85d8128d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sucralfate</strong></p><p>The image shows a<strong> Warthin silver starry stain</strong>ed biopsy specimen,<strong> </strong>diagnostic for <em><strong>Helicobacter pylori</strong>. </em><strong>Sucralfate</strong> does not have any anti-<em>H. pylori</em> action and is <strong>not used</strong> in the treatment of <em>H. pylori</em> gastritis.</p>\n<p>Option A: Metronidazole is used in the triple, quadruple therapy, and sequential therapy regimens. </p>\n<p>Option B: Proton pump inhibitors significantly enhance the effectiveness of regimens containing amoxicillin or clarithromycin.</p>\n<p>Option D: Bismuth compounds have significant anti-bacterial action and are given in quadruple therapy.</p>\n<p>Two or three antimicrobials are combined in regimens that range from 10-14 days. <strong>Monotherapy</strong> is <strong>not recommended</strong> due to the development of <strong>resistance.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6574","text":"A 30-year-old man presente‍d with bloody diarrhea an​d abdominal pain for the ​past week. Pseudopolyps w‌ere noted on colonoscopy.​ Which of the following c​an be used in managing hi​s condition?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presented with bloody diarrhea and abdominal pain for the past week. Pseudopolyps were noted on colonoscopy. Which of the following can be used in managing his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sulfas‍alazine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sulfamet‍hoxazole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sulfinp‍yrazone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sulpha‍doxine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sulfasalazine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sulfasalazine</strong></p><p><strong>Bloody diarrhea</strong> and colonoscopy findings of <strong>pseudopolyps</strong> are suggestive of <strong>ulcerative colitis.</strong> The sulfa drug indicated is <strong>sulfasalazine</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is indicated mainly in mild-moderate ulcerative colitis and as an adjunctive in severe cases. Efficacy in Crohn’s disease is less.</p>\n<p>Sulfasalazine consists of <strong>5-ASA</strong> (mesalamine or 5-amino salicylic acid) <strong>linked</strong> to <strong>sulfapyridine</strong> with an <strong>azo bond.</strong> Individually, 5-ASA and sulfapyridine would be absorbed in the upper gastrointestinal tract, the azo bond prevents such absorption. Colonic bacteria break the azo bond to liberate sulfapyridine and 5-ASA. 5-ASA is considered to be the active moiety. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1317","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a prodrug?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a prodrug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sulfas‍alazine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mesal‍amine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Balsa‍lazide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Olsal‍azine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mesalamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mesalamine</strong></p><p>Mesalamine is not a prodrug. <strong>Mesalamine</strong> or 5-ASA is the <strong>active component</strong> that is responsible for the beneficial effects of inflammatory bowel disease. <strong><br/></strong><br/><strong>Cleavage</strong> of the <strong>bond</strong> of <strong>prodrugs</strong> that deliver mesalamine to the colon occurs by <strong>colonic bacteria.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Sulfasalazine - 5-ASA + sulfapyridine</p>\n<p>Option C: Balsalazide - 5-ASA + an inert compound</p>\n<p>Option D: Olsalazine - 5-ASA + 5-ASA</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1319","text":"A patient with Crohn's dis‍ease, refractory to stero​id therapy is planned to ​be started on an anti-TNF‌ alpha monoclonal antibod​y. Which of the following​ drugs does not belong in​ this class?","raw_text":"A patient with Crohn's disease, refractory to steroid therapy is planned to be started on an anti-TNF alpha monoclonal antibody. Which of the following drugs does not belong in this class?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infli‍ximab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adali‍mumab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Certolizu‍mab pegol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vedol‍izumab","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Vedolizumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Vedolizumab</strong></p><p><strong>Vedolizumab</strong> is a humanized <strong>anti-alpha-4-beta-7 integrin</strong> <strong>monoclonal antibody</strong> that prevents lymphocyte recruitment to the intestinal mucosa.</p>\n<p>It is approved for use in moderate-severe <strong>Crohn's</strong> disease and <strong>ulcerative</strong> colitis.</p>\n<p><strong>Anti-TNF alpha</strong> monoclonal antibodies indicated in IBD are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infliximab</li>\n<li>Adalimumab</li>\n<li>Certolizumab pegol</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0140","text":"Which is not given in Wils‍on's disease?","raw_text":"Which is not given in Wilson's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zi‍nc","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Penici‍llamine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium‍ citrate","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Trie‍ntine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Calcium citrate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Calcium citrate</strong></p><p>Calcium citrate is not used in the treatment of Wilson's disease.</p>\n<p>The drugs used in the management of Wilson's disease are:</p>\n<p><strong>Chelating agents</strong>: These drugs bind to copper to form complexes that get excreted in the urine. D-penicillamine and trientine are<strong> first-line</strong> drugs in symptomatic patients with hepatic diseases.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>D-penicillamine</strong>: It is the recommended drug due to its availability and cost-effectiveness as compared to trientine. Since it is an antagonist to pyridoxine, supplementation of pyridoxine is required. It can precipitate various adverse effects including aplastic anemia, myasthenia gravis, and membranous nephropathy. It can also worsen neurological symptoms</li>\n<li><strong>Trientine</strong>: It is administered in those who cannot tolerate D-penicillamine</li>\n<li><strong>Tetrathiomolybdate</strong>: It has been identified to be effective in patients with neuropsychiatric manifestation</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Elemental zinc</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Zinc blocks the intestinal absorption of copper by inducing metallothionein (endogenous chelator in enterocytes)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">It is commonly used as a maintenance therapy along with chelating agents. It can also be used as first-line therapy in pregnancy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"_-ZSBvu-","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"63_skin__connective_tissue_and_ligaments","title":"63.Skin, Connective Tissue And Ligaments","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MA9718","text":"Which of the following is ​the correct order of laye‌rs of epidermis from deep​ to superficial? Stratum ​Granulosum Stratum Corneu​m Stratum Basale Stratum ‍Spinosum","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is the correct order of layers of epidermis from deep to superficial?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Stratum Granulosum</li>\n<li>Stratum Corneum</li>\n<li>Stratum Basale</li>\n<li>Stratum Spinosum</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4-3​-1-2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3-4​-1-2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3-1​-2-4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4-3​-2-1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 3-4-1-2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c3747cdce1ad449b839d654542d9b03bx1280x1511.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 3-4-1-2</strong></p><p>The correct order of the given layers from deep to superficial is stratum <strong>basale,</strong> stratum<strong> spinosum,</strong> stratum<strong> granulosum,</strong> stratum<strong> corneum.</strong></p>\n<p>Most of the body is covered by <strong>thin skin</strong> that has <strong>4 layers</strong> as follows (superficial to deep):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>stratum corneum</li>\n<li>stratum granulosum</li>\n<li>stratum spinosum</li>\n<li>stratum basale</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Some parts of the body<strong> </strong>such as the <strong>palms</strong>, <strong>soles</strong> and <strong>flexor surfaces</strong> <strong>of digits</strong> have <strong>thick skin (glabrous skin)</strong> with <strong>5 layers</strong> as follows (superficial to deep):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>stratum corneum</li>\n<li>stratum lucidum</li>\n<li>stratum granulosum</li>\n<li>stratum spinosum</li>\n<li>stratum basale</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Mnemonic</strong> for skin layers (superficial to deep): <strong>C</strong>ome <strong>L</strong>et's <strong>G</strong>et<strong> So</strong>me<strong> B</strong>eer (Corneum, Lucidum, Granulosum, Spinosum, and Basale).</p>\n<p>The following image shows an illustration of layers of epidermis:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9719","text":"In which of the following ​layers are non-nucleated ‌cells present?","raw_text":"In which of the following layers are non-nucleated cells present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratum G​ranulosum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stratum ​Spinosum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stratum​ Basale","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stratum​ Corneum","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stratum Corneum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/63da228831ef42ef9c3e4877f88d681cx391x573.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stratum Corneum</strong></p><p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 13.3333px;\"><strong>Non-nucleated cells</strong> are present in the <strong>stratum corneum</strong> layer of the epidermis. The cells are devoid of both a nucleus and membranous organelles.</span></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\">The cells in this layer contain densely arranged <strong>keratin filaments</strong> within the cytoplasmic matrix providing a <strong>barrier function</strong> that</span><span style=\"font-family: verdana, geneva, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;\"> <strong>prevents</strong> the entry of <strong>microbes</strong> from the environment and <strong>leakage</strong> of fluids from the body.</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9720","text":"Which of the following fac​tors does not affect the ‌colour of human skin?","raw_text":"Which of the following factors does not affect the colour of human skin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Melanocyte morphology and ​activity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Number of melanocytes in t​he skin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Number of melanosomes in m​elanocytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Number of melanosomes in k​eratinocytes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Number of melanocytes in the skin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Number of melanocytes in the skin</strong></p><p>The <strong>number of melanocytes </strong>does not affect the colour of human skin.</p>\n<p>The <strong>racial differences</strong> in skin colour is <strong>determined by:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Melanocyte morphology and activity.</li>\n<li>Number and size of melanosomes in melanocytes.</li>\n<li>The number of melanosomes individually dispersed within keratinocytes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"p1\">Melanin pigmentation is influenced by <strong>constitutive</strong> (genetically determined) and <strong>facultative</strong> factors (reversible changes due to environmental factors like UV-rays, chemicals, and hormones).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9725","text":"A middle-aged woman presen​ted with a rapidly growin‌g, itchy lesion on her fa​ce as shown in the image.​ On dermoscopy, there is ​an atypical pigmented net‍work. In which of the fol‍lowing layers of the skin​ are the cells forming th‍ese lesions present?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman presented with a rapidly growing, itchy lesion on her face as shown in the image. On dermoscopy, there is an atypical pigmented network. In which of the following layers of the skin are the cells forming these lesions present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratum​ corneum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stratum g​ranulosum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stratum​ basale","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Der​mis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stratum basale","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a982bfd77a7f49848ba8055f0b51868f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9fcfd7b95c214d1d941fadb8e53c24cfx1280x1511.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stratum basale</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of an itchy, asymmetrical pigmented lesion on the face with an irregular border(image) that rapidly grew in size, points towards a diagnosis of <strong>malignant melanoma</strong> arising from the <strong>melanocytes.</strong> These cells are present in the <strong>stratum basale</strong> layer of the epidermis.</p>\n<p>Melanocytes are derived from<strong> neural crest cells</strong>. They produce <strong>melanin</strong> which is packed into organelles called <strong>melanosomes</strong> and transferred via finger-like processes called <strong>dendrites</strong> to the <strong>keratinocytes</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9722","text":"Which of the following cel​ls act as touch receptors‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following cells act as touch receptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Melan​ocytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kerati​nocytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Langerha​ns cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Merkel​ cells","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Merkel cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8a18202ab2c5415c99237daf4633a088x1280x1410.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Merkel cells</strong></p><p>The<strong> Merkel cells</strong> act as <strong>touch receptors.</strong> They are present in the <strong>basal layer</strong> of the <strong>epidermis</strong> and outer root sheath of some big <strong>hair follicles</strong> and their<strong> </strong>functions are as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li class=\"p1\">Act as slowly adapting <strong>mechanoreceptors</strong> in the skin that convey touch sensation.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Detects the <strong>directional deformation</strong> of the epidermis and hair movement.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Sense <strong>sustained pressure</strong> and edges of applied objects on the skin.</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9728","text":"In which of the following ​layers of the skin are Me‌issner corpuscles present​?","raw_text":"In which of the following layers of the skin are Meissner corpuscles present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stratum s​pongiosum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stratum​ basale","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reticula​r dermis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Papillar​y dermis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Papillary dermis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8b423021c2914cf9aa9d1eccb62192efx1280x1285.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/47816a06033a4bff9b680f3e6acac94ex1280x1410.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Papillary dermis</strong></p><p><strong>Meissner corpuscles</strong> are present in the <strong>dermal papillae </strong>(upper dermis).</p>\n<p>They are primarily present in the dermal papillae of all parts of the <strong>hand</strong> and <strong>foot,</strong> the <strong>lips,</strong> the anterior aspect of the <strong>forearm,</strong> palpebral <strong>conjunctiva</strong> and mucous membrane of the apical part of the <strong>tongue.</strong> They are highly concentrated in the <strong>finger pads</strong>.</p>\n<p>Meissner’s corpuscles are <strong>rapidly adapting mechanoreceptors </strong>that are sensitive to shape and textural changes during exploratory and discriminatory touch. Their high sensitivity provides the neurological basis for sensing the Braille text.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9732","text":"On exposure to a sudden gu​sh of cold air, you notic‌e the following change in​ the appearance of your s​kin for a brief period. W​hich of the following acc‍urately describes the mus‍cle responsible for this ​phenomenon?","raw_text":"On exposure to a sudden gush of cold air, you notice the following change in the appearance of your skin for a brief period. Which of the following accurately describes the muscle responsible for this phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a ske​letal muscle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is a sm​ooth muscle","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is a mixture of skeleta​l and smooth muscle, with‌ dominant skeletal muscle​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a mixture of skeleta​l and smooth muscle, with‌ dominant smooth muscle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is a smooth muscle","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0c66e31d227b4adca449c71b7a15acbb.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/71b003b2bbf64cc591b435bb9b9724d3x1280x960.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is a smooth muscle</strong></p><p>The muscle responsible for <strong>goosebumps</strong> (image) is the <strong>arrector pili muscle</strong> which is a smooth muscle supplied by the <strong>sympathetic nerves.</strong> It connects the dermal sheath of the hair follicles to the papillary layer of the dermis.</p>\n<p>Contraction of these muscles makes the hair more vertical <strong>(piloerection),</strong> elevates the epidermis around the hair <strong>(goose</strong><strong>bump), </strong>and dimples the skin surface where the muscle is inserted.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the arrector pili muscle attached to the hair follicles:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9733","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true regarding‌ sweat glands?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding sweat glands?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apocrine sweat glands help​ maintain the temperature‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eccrine sweat glands are s​upplied by adrenergic neu‌rons","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Apocrine sweat glands help​ generate an odor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Eccrine sweat glands becom​e active at puberty","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Apocrine sweat glands help generate an odor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Apocrine sweat glands help generate an odor</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Apocrine</strong> sweat glands produce <strong>secretions</strong> that undergo bacterial decomposition to <strong>generate odour.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Sweat glands are classified as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Eccrine sweat glands</strong> -<strong> </strong>The <strong>majority</strong> of sweat glands in the body are eccrine and open via rounded pores to the skin surface.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Apocrine sweat glands </strong>-<strong> </strong>They are present in the <strong>axillae</strong>, <strong>perianal area</strong>, <strong>areolae</strong>, <strong>periumbilical skin</strong>, and <strong>genitalia</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9730","text":"The white opaque crescent ​present in the proximal p‌art of the nail is known ​as ______","raw_text":"The white opaque crescent present in the proximal part of the nail is known as ______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Free ​border","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ro​ot","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lun​ula","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nail​ body","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lunula","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c6a606afa5ff4721b46dd0fcd1886609x1280x2113.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lunula</strong></p><p>The <strong>white opaque crescent</strong> present in the proximal part of the nail is known as the <strong>lunula</strong>. </p>\n<p>Parts of the nail:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Root - </strong>It is the proximal hidden part, buried into the nail groove and is overlapped by the nail fold of the skin.</li>\n<li><strong>Free border -</strong> It is the distal part of the nail that is free from the skin.</li>\n<li><strong>Body -</strong> It is the exposed part of the nail that is adherent to the underlying skin.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9736","text":"Which of the following for​ms the non-fibrous elemen‌t of the extracellular ma​trix in the connective ti​ssue?","raw_text":"Which of the following forms the non-fibrous element of the extracellular matrix in the connective tissue?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coll​agen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ela​stin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Reti​culin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ground s​ubstance","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ground substance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ground substance</strong></p><p>The <strong>non-fibrous element</strong> of the extracellular matrix in the connective tissue is formed by the <strong>ground substance.</strong></p>\n<p>Connective tissue consists of <strong>cells</strong> and <strong>extracellular matrix:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The cells include fibroblasts, macrophages, plasma cells, mast cells, fat cells, and pigment cells.</li>\n<li>The extracellular matrix contains <strong>fibrous</strong> and <strong>non-fibrous </strong>elements.\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>fibrous element</strong> has three types of fibres - <strong>collagen</strong>,<strong> elastin </strong>and<strong> reticulin</strong></li>\n<li>The <strong>non-fibrous element</strong> is formed by the <strong>ground</strong> <strong>substance</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9738","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding ligamen‌ts is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding ligaments is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most ligaments have collag​en fibres","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ligamenta flava is an elas​tic ligament","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extrinsic ligaments are in​dependent of the joint","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ligaments are devoid of ne​rve supply","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ligaments are devoid of nerve supply","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ligaments are devoid of nerve supply</strong></p><p>The statement 'ligaments are devoid of nerve supply' is false.</p>\n<p>Ligaments have a <strong>rich nerve supply</strong>. This helps them function as<strong> sense organs</strong> and participate in reflex mechanisms important for monitoring the position and movements of the joint. Intrinsic ligaments surround the joint, whereas extrinsic ligaments are away and independent of the joint.</p>\n<p>The majority of ligaments are made up of<strong> collagen fibres </strong>predominantly. These are <strong>inelastic</strong> and not stretchable. <strong>Ligamentum flavum</strong> and ligaments of <strong>auditory ossicles</strong> are exceptions, as they predominantly have <strong>elastin fibres </strong>that make them elastic and stretchable. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9762","text":"The structure marked X in ​the following image is a ‌modified form of_______","raw_text":"The structure marked X in the following image is a modified form of_______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liga​ment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ten​don","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superfici​al fascia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deep ​fascia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Deep fascia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f2e9209ccccc42ea89a03834c3a83b83.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a77cf97c52e84c9c85e4c4ae713ecb67x960x720.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Deep fascia</strong></p><p>The<strong> </strong>structure marked X in the image is the <strong>interosseous membrane</strong> which is a modified form of <strong>deep fascia.</strong> It is present in the forearm and leg.</p>\n<p>The interosseous membrane helps in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Maintaining optimum distance</strong> between bones</li>\n<li><strong>Increasing surface area</strong> for attachment of muscles</li>\n<li><strong>Transmission of weight </strong>from one bone to another</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows the graphical illustration of interosseous membrane of the Leg:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9739","text":"The linear fibrous band fo​rmed by interdigitation o‌f the tendinous or aponeu​rotic ends of the muscles​ is known as _______","raw_text":"The linear fibrous band formed by interdigitation of the tendinous or aponeurotic ends of the muscles is known as _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liga​ment","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Interosseo​us membrane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ra​phe","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fas​cia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Raphe","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Raphe</strong></p><p>The linear <strong>fibrous band</strong> formed by <strong>interdigitation of</strong> the <strong>tendinous</strong> or <strong>aponeurotic ends</strong> of the muscles is known as<strong> raphe. </strong></p>\n<p>Unlike most ligaments, the <strong>raphe are stretchable</strong>. Examples of raphe include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>linea alba</li>\n<li>pterygomandibular raphe</li>\n<li>mylohyoid raphe</li>\n<li>median pharyngeal raphe</li>\n<li>anococcygeal raphe</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"7EyBbKjr","path":["Anatomy"]},{"id":"63_mental_health","title":"63.Mental Health","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA2666","text":"Which of the following is ​false about mental health‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about mental health?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mental disorders are unive​rsal, not specific to any‍ group","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mental health is the mere ​absence of mental illness‍","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Heredity can contribute to​ mental illnesses","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Substance abuse is a type ​of mental illness","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mental health is the mere absence of mental illness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mental health is the mere absence of mental illness</strong></p><p><strong>Mental health</strong> is a state of well-being in which an individual realizes his or her own abilities and<strong> not merely</strong> the <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>mental illness</strong>. It is the ability to <strong>cope</strong> <strong>with stress</strong>, be <strong>productive</strong>, and contribute to the community. </p>\n<p>Mental disorders are <strong>universal</strong> and are not specific to any particular group. They are associated with <strong>hereditary</strong> (family history of psychiatric disorders) and environmental factors. <strong>Substance</strong> <strong>abuse</strong> and alcohol addiction are considered types of mental illness.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2667","text":"Which of the following are​ sociopathological factor‍s associated with mental ‌health? Economic insecuri​ty Anxiety Substance abus​e Frustration Radiation","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are sociopathological factors associated with mental health?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Economic insecurity</li>\n<li>Anxiety</li>\n<li>Substance abuse</li>\n<li>Frustration</li>\n<li>Radiation</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 &amp;amp;​ 2 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3, 4 &amp;​amp; 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 &amp;​amp; 4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2, 3 &amp;​amp; 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 2 & 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 2 &amp; 4</strong></p><p><strong>Economic insecurity</strong>, <strong>anxiety</strong>, and <strong>frustration</strong> are the <strong>sociopathological</strong> factors associated with mental health.</p>\n<p>The factors leading to mental health illnesses include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Organic</strong> - cerebral arteriosclerosis, neoplasms, endocrine, metabolic and chronic diseases</li>\n<li><strong>Hereditary</strong> - schizophrenia</li>\n<li>Socio-pathological factors - worries, anxiety, frustration, emotional stress, tension, unhappy marriages, broken homes, poverty, industrialization, urbanization, changing family structure, population mobility, economic insecurity, cruelty, rejection, neglect.</li>\n<li><strong>Environmental</strong> - environmental toxins, <strong>substance abuse</strong>, <strong>radiation</strong>, nutritional factors, mineral deficiencies, infectious diseases during prenatal, perinatal, and postnatal periods (like rubella), road traffic accidents.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2669","text":"The incidence of mental il​lness is higher in indivi‍duals who are exposed to ‌causal factors during whi​ch period of life?","raw_text":"The incidence of mental illness is higher in individuals who are exposed to causal factors during which period of life?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prenata​l period","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adole​scence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"First 5 ye​ars of life","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Perinata​l period","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. First 5 years of life","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. First 5 years of life</strong></p><p>Exposure to causal factors during the <strong>initial</strong> <strong>5 years </strong>of life leads to an <strong>increased incidence</strong> of <strong>mental illness</strong>.</p>\n<p>This period is critical for lifelong mental health behavior and learning, even more crucial than adolescence. During early childhood, the growth and development of children are <strong>most</strong> <strong>vulnerable</strong> to external environmental stressors.  A continuous, intimate, and <strong>warm</strong> <strong>relationship</strong> with the child's <strong>parents</strong> during infancy and childhood is very important. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2670","text":"All of the following can b​e used for the secondary ‍prevention of mental illn‌ess in patients except:","raw_text":"All of the following can be used for the secondary prevention of mental illness in patients except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Improving social environme​nt","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Early d​iagnosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Family c​ounseling","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Case​ work","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Improving social environment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Improving social environment</strong></p><p><strong>Improving</strong> the <strong>social environment</strong> is a method of <strong>primary prevention</strong> of mental illness, not secondary prevention. </p>\n<p>The levels of prevention of mental illness and measures are given below:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary prevention - improving the social environment and the promotion of mental health awareness</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary </strong>prevention - <strong>early diagnosis</strong> and family-based health services like <strong>casework</strong> and <strong>family counseling</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tertiary </strong>prevention - measures to <strong>reduce</strong> the <strong>duration</strong> of mental illness as well <strong>family</strong> and <strong>community stresses</strong> to prevent further breakdown and disruption</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2671","text":"Patients with which of the​ following conditions are‍ considered to have a maj‌or mental illness?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions are considered to have a major mental illness?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Obsessive compulsive disor​der","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neur​osis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deme​ntia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Manic depressive psychosis​","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Manic depressive psychosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Manic depressive psychosis</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>manic depressive psychosis</strong> are considered to have a <strong>major mental illness.</strong> </p>\n<p>Mental health disorders are classified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Major (psychotic or not in touch with reality) - include schizophrenia, manic depressive psychosis, and paranoia</li>\n<li>Minor (non-psychotic, remain in touch with reality) - include <strong>neuroses</strong> (like <strong>OCD</strong>), personality and character disorders</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Dementia occurs secondary to organic causes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2672","text":"Which of the following is ​not a service offered to ‍patients with mental heal‌th disorders?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a service offered to patients with mental health disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rehabil​itation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"After-car​e services","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Use of modern psychoactive​ drugs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Employmen​t services","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Employment services","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Employment services</strong></p><p>Employment services are not included under <strong>mental health services</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mental health services include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Early diagnosis and treatment</li>\n<li>Group and individual psychotherapy</li>\n<li>Mental health education</li>\n<li>Use of modern <strong>psychoactive drugs</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Rehabilitation</strong></li>\n<li><strong>After-care</strong> services - help drug-dependent persons to adapt to regular life following treatment and rehabilitation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2673","text":"A 32-year-old man is broug​ht to the casualty with a‍ suspected drug overdose.‌ His sister tells you tha​t he has recently started​ taking a 'superman drug'‍ after hearing about it f‍rom his friends. What dru​g is she referring to?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old man is brought to the casualty with a suspected drug overdose. His sister tells you that he has recently started taking a 'superman drug' after hearing about it from his friends. What drug is she referring to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cann​abis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ster​oids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amphet​amines","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Coc​aine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amphetamines","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amphetamines</strong></p><p><strong>Amphetamines</strong> are also known as the <strong>superman drug</strong>. </p>\n<p>This is because their effects include increased self-confidence and endurance. However, its usage leads to tolerance and dependence.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2675","text":"What is currently the most​ widely used illicit subs‍tance in the world?","raw_text":"What is currently the most widely used illicit substance in the world?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coc​aine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amphet​amines","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Her​oin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cann​abis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cannabis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8b49afc606cf4a1889f85ceffae0db94x800x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cannabis</strong></p><p><strong>Cannabis</strong> is the most widely used illicit substance in the world.</p>\n<p>Cannabis is obtained from <em>Cannabis sativa</em>, <em>C. indica</em>, and <em>C. americana</em>. It is colloquially called <strong>marijuana</strong>. Other forms of this drug are <strong>charas </strong>or<strong> hashish </strong>and<strong> bhang.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows the cannabis plant. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2677","text":"What is the most widely us​ed legal addictive substa‍nce in India?","raw_text":"What is the most widely used legal addictive substance in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alc​ohol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tob​acco","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cann​abis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Benzodi​azepines","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tobacco","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tobacco</strong></p><p><strong>Tobacco</strong> is the <strong>most widely used</strong> legal addictive substance in <strong>India, </strong>followed closely by <strong>alcohol </strong>as the <strong>second most </strong>widely consumed substance.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2678","text":"Which of the following is ​not an objective of the N‍ational Mental Health Pro‌gramme of 1982?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an objective of the National Mental Health Programme of 1982?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ensure equal human rights ​to mentally ill","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Promote community particip​ation in mental health se‍rvices","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Promote application of men​tal health knowledge","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Provide accessibility of m​ental health care","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ensure equal human rights to mentally ill","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ensure equal human rights to mentally ill</strong></p><p>Ensuring equal rights to mentally ill people is not an objective of the <strong>National Mental Health Programme</strong> (NMHP). It was launched in <strong>1982</strong> with the following objectives:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ensure <strong>availability and accessibility</strong> of minimum mental health care</li>\n<li>Encourage the <strong>application</strong> of mental health knowledge in general care</li>\n<li>Promote <strong>community participation</strong> in mental health services development</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The National Mental Health Programme was relaunched as <strong>The Mental Health Care Act, 2017</strong> which includes provisions for <strong>equal rights </strong>for those with mental illness.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2679","text":"Which of the following is ​not a component of the Di‍strict Mental Health Prog‌ramme?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of the District Mental Health Programme?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trai​ning","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Early d​etection","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Providing data for future ​planning","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Scre​ening","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Screening","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Screening</strong></p><p>Screening is not a component of the <strong>District Mental Health Programme</strong>. It includes the following components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Training </strong>of all mental health workers at the nodal institute in the state</li>\n<li>Increasing <strong>public awareness</strong> by education to reduce stigma</li>\n<li>Early <strong>detection and treatment</strong></li>\n<li>Providing data for future<strong> planning</strong>, improvement in service and research</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Activities by the DMHP for positive mental health include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>School mental health services</li>\n<li>College counseling services</li>\n<li>Workplace stress management</li>\n<li><strong>Suicide prevention</strong> services</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2680","text":"Which of the following is ​not included in the Menta‍l Healthcare Act of 2017?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following is not included in the Mental Healthcare Act of 2017?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Advance directive rights o​f the mentally ill","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decriminali​zing suicide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Establishing mobile mental​ health camps","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Procedure for admission, t​reatment and discharge of‍ the mentally ill","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Establishing mobile mental health camps","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Establishing mobile mental health camps</strong></p><p>Establishing mobile mental health camps for the benefit of mentally ill patients is not included under the <strong>Mental Healthcare Act of 2017</strong>.</p>\n<p>This act encourages the mobilization of resources to improve mental health care across the country as well as <strong>reduce stigma</strong> and improve social inclusion/support for those with mental illness. It also has provisions to improve outcomes and <strong>protect their rights</strong>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> </strong>provisions of this act include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rights of persons with mental illness to <strong>access </strong><strong>health care</strong>, live without discrimination and enjoy <strong>confidentiality</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Advance directives </strong>to allow mentally ill people to make decisions about their treatment </li>\n<li>Procedures for admission, treatment (behavioral and <strong>interpersonal</strong> <strong>therapy</strong>, medications), and discharge </li>\n<li><strong>Decriminalizing</strong> <strong>suicide </strong>and prohibiting electroconvulsive therapy unless anesthesia is used</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The act also has clear instructions for the central and state mental health authorities and other mental health establishments on their endeavors. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7010","text":"According to the WHO Menta​l Health Gap Action Progr‍amme, which of the follow‌ing is not included in th​e Mental Health Care Act,​ 2017?","raw_text":"According to the WHO Mental Health Gap Action Programme, which of the following is not included in the Mental Health Care Act, 2017?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Screening of family member​s","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mobilization​ of resources","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Social ​support","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interperso​nal therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Screening of family members","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Screening of family members</strong></p><p>According to the WHO Mental Health Gap Action Programme (<strong>mhGAP</strong>), screening of family members is not included in the Mental Health Care Act, 2017.</p>\n<p><strong>The Mental Healthcare Act of 2017 </strong>has<strong> </strong>provisions that include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rights of persons with mental illness to <strong>access</strong> <strong>health care</strong>, live without discrimination and enjoy <strong>confidentiality</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Advance directives </strong>to allow mentally ill people to make decisions about their treatment </li>\n<li>Procedures for admission, treatment (behavioral and <strong>interpersonal</strong> <strong>therapy</strong>, medications), and discharge </li>\n<li><strong>Decriminalizing</strong> <strong>suicide</strong> and prohibiting electroconvulsive therapy unless anesthesia is used</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These are achieved through the mobilization of resources to required areas, and by providing social support.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF7452","text":"The National Suicide Preve​ntion Strategy aims to re‍duce suicide mortality by‌ _______ in the country b​y 2030.","raw_text":"The National Suicide Prevention Strategy aims to reduce suicide mortality by _______ in the country by 2030.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5​%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1​0%","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1​5%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2​0%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 10%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 10%</strong></p><p>The <strong>National Suicide Prevention Strategy (NSPS)</strong> aims to reduce suicide mortality by <strong>10%</strong> in India by<strong> 2030</strong>. It is a multisectoral approach that will involve national and international government ministries, families, communities, and educational institutes to help prevent suicide.</p>\n<p>India has a high burden of suicide. It is the leading cause of death among individuals between 15-29 years of age.</p>\n<p><strong>Epidemiology</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Young and middle-aged adults between <strong>18-45 years</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Males</strong> more than females</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Daily wage earners</strong> and people who earn <strong>&lt;1,00,000 per annum</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Individuals with family issues and illnesses</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hanging </strong>and<strong> poisoning</strong> were the most common methods of suicide. It accounts for &gt;3/4<sup>th</sup> of all suicides.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors for suicide</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Psychiatric illnesses- Depression, BPD, schizophrenia, and anxiety disorders</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Alcohol and other substance abuse disorders</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Major physical illness</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Job or financial loss</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Relationship or social loss</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Domestic violence or abuse</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lack of social support </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The stigma associated with help-seeking behavior of mental disorders</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lack of social support and sense of isolation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF7450","text":"Which of the following is ​not a component of the RE‍DS path in the National s‌uicide prevention strateg​y?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of the REDS path in the National suicide prevention strategy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduce suicidal deaths by ​2% in the next 5 years","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Enhance health services to​ provide suicide preventi‍on strategies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Develop community resilien​ce, and societal support ‍for suicide prevention","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Strengthen surveillance an​d evidence generation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Reduce suicidal deaths by 2% in the next 5 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Reduce suicidal deaths by 2% in the next 5 years</strong></p><p>The <strong>National Suicide Prevention Strategy (NSPS)</strong> is a multisectoral approach that will involve national and international government ministries, families and communities, and educational institutes. It aims to <strong>reduce suicide mortality by 10%</strong> in India by <strong>2030.</strong></p>\n<p>It recommends<strong> REDS </strong>path for suicide prevention.</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>R</strong>einforce leadership, partnership, and institutional capacity in the country</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>E</strong>nhance the health services capacity to provide suicide prevention services</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>D</strong>evelop community resilience, societal support for suicide prevention and reduce stigma against suicidal behaviours</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>S</strong>trengthen surveillance and evidence generation. </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Objectives</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To establish<strong> effective surveillance mechanism</strong> for suicide prevention in <strong>next 3 years</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To establish <strong>psychiatric OPDs</strong> that provide suicide prevention services through District Mental Health Programme, within <strong>next 5 years</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To <strong>integrate mental well-being curriculum</strong> in all educational institutes within <strong>next 8 years</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"9ExLPKP6","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"63_hypertensive_vascular_disease","title":"63.Hypertensive Vascular Disease","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MB3893","text":"Which of the following is ‌false with respect to lif​estyle modifications for ‍managing essential hypert‌ension as recommended by ​AHA 2019 guidelines?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false with respect to lifestyle modifications for managing essential hypertension as recommended by AHA 2019 guidelines?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low potas‌sium diet","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium intake &amp;lt; 1.5g /d‌ay","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"DASH‌ diet","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Limit alcoho‌l consumption","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Low potassium diet","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Low potassium diet</strong></p><p>Low potassium increases blood pressure and hence is not recommended in the dietary modifications. On the contrary, <strong>potassium</strong> <strong>supplementation</strong> <strong>lowers</strong> the <strong>BP</strong> significantly in hypertensives. It is given preferably by dietary modifications unless contraindicated.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3882","text":"A 57-year-old male patient‌ presented to the clinic ​for a routine clinical ch‍eck-up. On examination, h‌is BP was found to be 136​/88 mmHg. During his subs‌equent visits, the systol​ic blood pressure recorde​d was found to be within ‌the range of 130-138 mmHg‌ and diastolic blood pres​sure was within the range​ of 86-88mmHg. According ‌to the European guideline‍s for hypertension 2018, ‍his blood pressure would ‌be classified as ________​____","raw_text":"A 57-year-old male patient presented to the clinic for a routine clinical check-up. On examination, his BP was found to be 136/88 mmHg. During his subsequent visits, the systolic blood pressure recorded was found to be within the range of 130-138 mmHg and diastolic blood pressure was within the range of 86-88mmHg. According to the European guidelines for hypertension 2018, his blood pressure would be classified as ____________","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nor‌mal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High ‌normal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Grade 1 hy‌pertension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isolated systolic hyperten‌sion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. High normal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. High normal</strong></p><p>According to European guidelines for hypertension 2018, systolic blood pressure between 130-139 mmHg and diastolic blood pressure between 85-89 mmHg is classified as <strong>high normal.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3884","text":"An 80-year-old male patien‌t complains of headache a​nd dizziness. His ambulat‍ory BP was recorded to be‌ in the range of 144-152/​80-86 mmHg. What is the l‌ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An 80-year-old male patient complains of headache and dizziness. His ambulatory BP was recorded to be in the range of  144-152/80-86 mmHg. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Grade 1 hy‌pertension","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"White coat ‌hypertension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Secondary h‌ypertension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isolated systolic hyperten‌sion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Isolated systolic hypertension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Isolated systolic hypertension</strong></p><p>SBP ≥140 mm Hg and DBP &lt;90 mmHg is called <strong>isolated systolic hypertension.</strong></p>\n<p>White-coat hypertension is characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The office blood pressure is ≥140/90 mm Hg on ≥3 separate office visits</li>\n<li>≥2 blood pressure measurements taken outside the office are &lt;140/90 mm Hg, frequently using home blood pressure monitoring; and</li>\n<li>There is no evidence of hypertensive target organ damage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3886","text":"Which of the following is ‌the gold standard investi​gation to measure vessel ‍wall stiffness in patient‌s suspected to have hyper​tension mediated end-orga‌n damage (HMOD)?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the gold standard investigation to measure vessel wall stiffness in patients suspected to have hypertension mediated end-organ damage (HMOD)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ankle brac‌hial index","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carotid A‌ngiography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pulse wav‌e velocity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Femoral art‌ery doppler","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pulse wave velocity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pulse wave velocity</strong></p><p>Carotid-femoral <strong>pulse wave velocity</strong> (PWV) is the <strong>gold standard</strong> for <strong>measuring large artery stiffness</strong> due to hypertension.</p>\n<p>Long-standing hypertension can lead to structural and functional changes in arteries and target organs like the heart, brain, arteries, and kidneys. This is called hypertension mediated organ damage (HMOD).</p>\n<table style=\"height: 190px; width: 380.199px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">Organ</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\">Consequences</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.199px;\">Investigations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">Heart </td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\">\n<p>Left ventricular hypertrophy</p>\n<p>Decreased diastolic function</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.199px;\">\n<p>ECG</p>\n<p>Echocardiography</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">Blood vessels</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\">\n<p>Stiffening</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.199px;\">\n<p>Pulse wave velocity of carotid or femoral artery (gold standard)</p>\n<p>Carotid ultrasound</p>\n<p>Ankle-brachial pressure index</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">Kidney</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\">\n<p>Hyaline arteriosclerosis (benign hypertension)</p>\n<p>Hyperplastic arteriosclerosis (severe hypertension)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.199px;\">\n<p>Renal function tests</p>\n<p>GFR</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">Retina</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\">Hemorrhages and cotton wool spots</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.199px;\">Fundoscopy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 56px;\">Brain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\">Stroke, impaired cognition</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.199px;\">MRI</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3891","text":"A 60-year-old male patient‌ has grade 2 hypertension​. He is also a known case‍ of type 2 diabetes for 9‌ years. What is the desir​ed goal for blood pressur‌e range in this patient?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old male patient has grade 2 hypertension. He is also a known case of type 2 diabetes for 9 years. What is the desired goal for blood pressure range in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"120-130/8‌0-89 mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"130-140/8‌0-89 mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"120-130/7‌0-79 mmHg","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"130-140/7‌0-79 mmHg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 120-130/70-79 mmHg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 120-130/70-79 mmHg</strong></p><p>In a patient <strong>(18-65 years)</strong> with hypertension and coexisting diabetes, the desired goal for <strong>systolic</strong> blood pressure is between <strong>120-130</strong> mmHg and <strong>diastolic</strong> blood pressure is between <strong>70-79 </strong>mmHg. In patients <strong>above 65</strong> years of age, the desired goal of <strong>systolic</strong> blood pressure is between <strong>130-139</strong> while the desired <strong>diastolic</strong> range remains the <strong>same</strong>.</p>\n<p>This goal is in accordance with the European Society of Cardiology guidelines (2018).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3892","text":"While treating a 45 year o‌ld hypertensive patient w​ith underlying coronary a‍rtery disease, what targe‌t blood pressure should y​ou titrate for, as per th‌e ESC 2018 guidelines?","raw_text":"While treating a 45 year old hypertensive patient with underlying coronary artery disease, what target blood pressure should you titrate for, as per the  ESC 2018 guidelines?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"140-15‌0 mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"110-12‌0 mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"130-1‌40mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"120-13‌0 mmHg","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 120-130 mmHg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 120-130 mmHg</strong></p><p>The target systolic blood pressure for hypertensive patients in the age group 18-65 years with coronary artery disease is between <strong>120-130 mmHg</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3894","text":"A 58-year-old male patient‌ has been diagnosed with ​hypertension. On examinat‍ion, there is no existing‌ cardiovascular disease, ​no hypertension mediated ‌organ damage (HMOD) and t​he renal function is norm​al. He should be immediat‌ely started with pharmaco‌logical management if his​ blood pressure is more t​han ______.","raw_text":"A 58-year-old male patient has been diagnosed with hypertension.  On examination, there is no existing cardiovascular disease, no hypertension mediated organ damage (HMOD) and the renal function is normal. He should be immediately started with pharmacological management if his blood pressure is more than ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt;130‌/80 mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"≥160/1‌00 mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt; 150‌/100 mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"≥140/9‌0 mmHg","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. ≥140/90 mmHg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. ≥140/90 mmHg</strong></p><p>In this patient with <strong>no markers</strong> of cardiovascular disease, <strong>no</strong> <strong>hypertension-mediated end-organ damage</strong> (HMOD), and <strong>normal renal function</strong>, <strong>immediate pharmacological treatment</strong> is recommended when the <strong>blood pressure</strong> (BP) is <strong>≥140/90 mmHg</strong> according to the <strong>AHA </strong>and <strong>ESH </strong>on the management of hypertension.</p>\n<p>Guidelines for Hypertension Management:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adults 18–79 y with SBP ≥140 mm Hg or DBP ≥90 mm Hg</li>\n<li>Adults ≥80 y with SBP ≥160 mm Hg but ≥140 mm Hg can be considered.</li>\n<li>In frail patients, individualize treatment.</li>\n<li>In patients with CVD, especially CAD, SBP ≥130 mm Hg, or DBP ≥80 mm Hg.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment BP targets:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adults 18–79 y, primary SBP/DBP goal &lt;140/90 mm Hg; if treatment well tolerated target SBP/DBP &lt;130/80 mm Hg but not SBP &lt;120 mm Hg or DBP &lt;70 mm Hg. For adults with isolated systolic hypertension, target SBP lowering, albeit cautiously.</li>\n<li>In adults ≥80 y, target SBP/DBP &lt;140/90 mm Hg, if well tolerated</li>\n</ul>\n<p>AHA Hypertension grading:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 90.0188%; height: 134.39px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 33.3648%; height: 22.3984px;\"><strong>BLOOD PRESSURE CATEGORY</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.3648%; height: 22.3984px;\"><strong>SYSTOLIC BP</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.2705%; height: 22.3984px;\"><strong>DIASTOLIC BP</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 33.3648%; height: 22.3984px;\">Normal </td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.3648%; height: 22.3984px;\">&lt;120 mmHg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.2705%; height: 22.3984px;\">&lt;80 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 33.3648%; height: 22.3984px;\">Elevated</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.3648%; height: 22.3984px;\">120-129 mmHg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.2705%; height: 22.3984px;\">&lt;80 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 33.3648%; height: 22.3984px;\">Stage 1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.3648%; height: 22.3984px;\">130-139 mmHg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.2705%; height: 22.3984px;\">80-89 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 33.3648%; height: 22.3984px;\">Stage 2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.3648%; height: 22.3984px;\">≥140 mmHg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.2705%; height: 22.3984px;\">≥90 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3984px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 33.3648%; height: 22.3984px;\">Hypertensive crisis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.3648%; height: 22.3984px;\">≥180 mmHg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 33.2705%; height: 22.3984px;\">≥120 mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3895","text":"A 72-year-old man who is a‌ known hypertensive for 1​2 years comes for a regul‍ar health check-up. On ex‌amination, his blood pres​sure is 172/100 mmHg. His‌ serum creatinine is 1.5m​g/dL, urinary albumin to ​creatinine ratio (UACR) i‌s 40mg/g, and GFR is 49mL‌/min. What is the preferr​ed antihypertensive treat​ment in this scenario?","raw_text":"A 72-year-old man who is a known hypertensive for 12 years comes for a regular health check-up. On examination, his blood pressure is 172/100 mmHg. His serum creatinine is 1.5mg/dL, urinary albumin to creatinine ratio (UACR) is 40mg/g, and GFR is 49mL/min. What is the preferred antihypertensive treatment in this scenario?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Captopril wi‌th amlodipine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Captopril w‌ith atenolol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Telmisartan ‌with atenolol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Captopril with telmisartan‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Captopril with amlodipine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Captopril with amlodipine</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, the patient is hypertensive along with <strong>chronic kidney disease</strong>. The preferred initial antihypertensive treatment is <strong>captopril with amlodipine.</strong></p>\n<p>The preferred treatment in hypertensive patients with chronic kidney disease is a combination of angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors (ACEi) or angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs) with calcium channel blockers (CCBs) or diuretics <strong>(ACEi/ARBs + CCBs/diuretics).</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7034","text":"A hypertensive patient is ‌on metoprolol. If verapam​il is added, there is a r‍isk of:","raw_text":"A hypertensive patient is on metoprolol. If verapamil is added, there is a risk of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial fi‌brillation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bradycardia ‌with AV block","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tachy‌cardia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Torsades ‌de pointes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bradycardia with AV block","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bradycardia with AV block</strong></p><p>Metoprolol, when added with verapamil, leads to <strong>bradycardia with AV block.</strong></p>\n<p>Verapamil and β blockers have additive effect on inhibiting SA node and AV conduction, and the combination should be avoided to prevent severe bradycardia and conduction block.</p>\n<p>These agents can also have additive effect on decreasing left ventricular function which could seriously impair cardiac output in a patient with heart failure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3902","text":"A hypertensive patient und‌erwent coronary angiograp​hy after he presented wit‍h chest pain. His angiogr‌aphic image is as below. ​What is the preferred dru‌g for the management of h​ypertension in this patie​nt?","raw_text":"A hypertensive patient underwent coronary angiography after he presented with chest pain. His angiographic image is as below. What is the preferred drug for the management of hypertension in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spirono‌lactone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aten‌olol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tamsu‌losin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydra‌lazine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Atenolol","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/abd7b993a7e24d4d91b065b98f3dc8a0.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4ddd1e622e36420fa3c560adb8b7af52x470x446.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Atenolol</strong></p><p>The above coronary angiogram shows <strong>partial occlusion</strong> of the left circumflex coronary artery. Beta-blockers like <strong>atenolol</strong> in combination with ACE inhibitors or ARBs or calcium channel blockers or diuretics are preferred for the management of hypertension in patients with <strong>coronary artery disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other indications for beta-blockers are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Congestive heart failure due to systolic dysfunction</li>\n<li>Post myocardial infarction</li>\n<li>Sinus tachycardia</li>\n<li>Ventricular arrhythmias</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows partial occlusion of left circumflex coronary artery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3887","text":"A 30-year-old man presents‌ with sudden onset of a h​eadache, palpitation, and‍ profuse sweating. He had‌ several such episodes in​ the past. During these e‌pisodes, his BP is found ​to be high. A 24-hour uri​nary metanephrine is elev‌ated. What is the most li‌kely etiology?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presents with sudden onset of a headache, palpitation, and profuse sweating. He had several such episodes in the past. During these episodes, his BP is found to be high. A 24-hour urinary metanephrine is elevated. What is the most likely etiology?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibromuscul‌ar dysplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Renal arte‌ry stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pheochro‌mocytoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperth‌yroidism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pheochromocytoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pheochromocytoma</strong></p><p>The most likely etiology is <strong>pheochromocytoma</strong>. Patients commonly present with a classical triad of palpitation, headache and profuse sweating. The presence of this triad in association with <strong>hypertension</strong> and elevated 24-hour urinary metanephrine makes pheochromocytoma a likely diagnosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1207","text":"A 35-year-old female patie‌nt has hypokalemia, hyper​tension and metabolic alk‍alosis. What is the most ‌likely etiology?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old female patient has hypokalemia, hypertension and metabolic alkalosis. What is the most likely etiology?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bartter ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gitelman'‌s syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Liddle's‌ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fanconi's‌ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Liddle's syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Liddle&#x27;s syndrome</strong></p><p>Hypertension with hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis is seen in <strong>Liddle's syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Liddle's syndrome (pseudo aldosteronism) is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> condition that is caused due to the <strong>increased activation of ENaC channels</strong>. This causes increased reabsorption of Na+ exchanged for K+ causing <strong>hypokalemia</strong>. Increased Na absorption leads to <strong>hypertension</strong>, and there is also a loss of H+ ion with K+, leading to <strong>metabolic alkalosis</strong>. It is associated with <strong>low renin</strong> and <strong>low aldosterone</strong> levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3904","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements are true with res​pect to hypertensive emer‍gency as per ESC 2018 gui‌delines? 1) BP &gt;180/110mm​ Hg 2) Acute end-organ da‌mage is usually not prese​nt 3) The initial goal in​ hypertensive emergency p‌resenting as encephalopat‌hy is to bring down the S​BP no more than 30% withi​n 2 hours 4) Should be tr‌eated using parenteral dr‍ugs","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following statements are true with respect to hypertensive emergency as per ESC 2018 guidelines?</p>\n<p>1) BP &gt;180/110mm Hg<br/>2) Acute end-organ damage is usually not present<br/>3) The initial goal in hypertensive emergency presenting as encephalopathy is to bring down the SBP no more than 30% within 2 hours<br/>4) Should be treated using parenteral drugs</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,‌3,4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1‌,4","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1,‌2,4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,2‌,3,4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1,4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/58a26c44b82e40bd9ee6dccde9a3edddx1280x1817.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1,4</strong></p><p>Hypertensive emergencies are situations in which <strong>grade 3 </strong>hypertension (BP&gt;180/110 mmHg) is associated with acute hypertension-mediated organ damage <strong>(HMOD)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Treatment of hypertensive emergency depends on the type of end-organ damage present (as shown in the table below). It is treated with rapidly acting <strong>intravenous</strong> antihypertensive agents.</p>\n<p>In hypertensive <strong>encephalopathy</strong>, the <strong>initial goal</strong> of therapy is to <strong>immediately</strong> reduce mean arterial blood <strong>(MAP)</strong> pressure by no more than <strong>25%</strong>. Rapid reduction of blood pressure may lead to reduced cerebral blood flow and result in cerebral infarction.</p>\n<p>The table below shows the treatment of various hypertensive emergencies with intravenous drug therapy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3905","text":"Which of the following pat‌ients would you diagnose ​as having hypertensive ur‍gency? A 50-year-old man ‌with a BP of 162/104 mm H​g with no other complaint‌s A 47-year-old woman wit​h alteration in mental st​atus, nausea, headache, a‌nd a BP of 190/120 mm A 6‌0-year-old man with a BP ​of 188/120 mm Hg without ​any target organ damage A‌ 55-year-old woman with a‍ BP of 200/140 mm Hg alon‍g with papilloedema","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following patients would you diagnose as having hypertensive urgency?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A 50-year-old man with a BP of 162/104 mm Hg with no other complaints</li>\n<li>A 47-year-old woman with alteration in mental status, nausea, headache, and a BP of 190/120 mm </li>\n<li>A 60-year-old man with a BP of 188/120 mm Hg without any target organ damage</li>\n<li>A 55-year-old woman with a BP of 200/140 mm Hg along with papilloedema</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3</strong></p><p>Patient 3 suffers from hypertensive urgency as BP &gt; 180/110 mmHg and end-organ damage is not seen.</p>\n<p><strong>Target organ damage</strong> is <strong>absent</strong> in hypertensive <strong>urgency</strong> but is <strong>present</strong> in a hypertensive<strong> emergency.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Severe hypertension</strong> (grade 3) is generally defined as blood pressure <strong><span class=\"nowrap\">≥180/110</span> mmHg</strong>. Hypertensive emergency, hypertensive urgency, and malignant hypertension are all <strong>forms of severe hypertension</strong>.  </p>\n<p>Option A: Patient 1 suffers from hypertension. Since BP &lt;180/110 mmHg it is not a hypertensive crisis (urgency or emergency).</p>\n<p>Option B: Patient 2 has a BP &gt; 180/110 mmHg along with symptoms of <strong>hypertensive</strong> <strong>encephalopathy</strong>. This is an example of a hypertensive <strong>emergency</strong> as there is severe hypertension with signs or symptoms of hypertensive-mediated organ damage (HMOD). </p>\n<p>Option D: Patient 4 has a BP &gt; 180/110 mm Hg along with <strong>papilledema</strong>. This is a case of severe hypertension associated with fundoscopic changes (flame hemorrhages, papilloedema) which is suggestive of <strong>malignant</strong> <strong>hypertension</strong>. Microangiopathy, DIC, and encephalopathy may also be present.  <strong>Fibrinoid</strong> <strong>necrosis</strong> of arterioles and small arteries is seen. The prognosis is very poor if untreated.</p>\n<p><strong>Posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES)</strong> or acute hypertensive encephalopathy is a rare complication in patients with an acute rise in blood pressure. Due to the acute rise, there is increased cerebral perfusion and edema of the brain. Clinically it is recognized by alteration in mental status, nausea, headache, visual disturbance, seizure, or development of focal neurological deficits.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3907","text":"A 55-year-old female patie‌nt who is a known case of​ hypertension, diabetes, ‍and hyperlipidemia presen‌ts to the emergency depar​tment with a severe heada‌che and vomiting. The pat​ient is agitated and her ​blood pressure is 220/120‌ mmHg. Ophthalmoscopy rev‌eals the presence of papi​lledema. Which of the fol​lowing antihypertensive i‌s least useful in the man‍agement of this patient?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old female patient who is a known case of hypertension, diabetes, and hyperlipidemia presents to the emergency department with a severe headache and vomiting. The patient is agitated and her blood pressure is 220/120 mmHg. Ophthalmoscopy reveals the presence of papilledema. Which of the following antihypertensive is least useful in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Furos‌emide","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nicar‌dipine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Labe‌tolol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enala‌prilat","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Furosemide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Furosemide</strong></p><p>The above scenario points towards the diagnosis of <strong>malignant hypertension</strong>. The drug which is<strong> least useful</strong> in the management is<strong> </strong>furosemide(<strong>diuretic</strong>). </p>\n<p>Volume depletion is common in patients with malignant hypertension, and the administration of a diuretic together with a hypertensive agent can lead to an extreme drop in blood pressure.</p>\n<p>Diuretics are generally not recommended for the rapid reduction of blood pressure in patients with hypertensive crises. They are used only in conditions of volume overload such as acute pulmonary edema.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD2858","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most important risk f​actor for the development‍ of hypertension?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most important risk factor for the development of hypertension?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High sal‌t intake","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A‌ge","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obe‌sity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Genetic‌ factors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Obesity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Obesity</strong></p><p>The most important risk factor for the development of hypertension is <strong>obesity</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hypertension is defined as repeated office systolic blood pressure of <strong>≥ 140</strong> mm Hg and/or diastolic blood pressure of <strong>≥ 90</strong> mm Hg.</p>\n<p><strong>Primary</strong>/essential <strong>hypertension</strong> is the leading cause of hypertension in adults. It is caused due to the interaction of genetic factors with various other factors such as diet, sedentary lifestyle, etc. In some people, hypertension may result from renal, endocrine, or adrenal disorders (<strong>secondary hypertension).</strong></p>\n<p>Risk factors of hypertension can be divided into modifiable and non-modifiable:</p>\n<p><strong>Non-modifiable</strong> risk factors are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Age- risk increases with age</li>\n<li>Sex- in young and middle-aged adults, the risk is higher in males</li>\n<li>Genetic factors- mutation in the genes which encode the components of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, atrial natriuretic peptide, and alpha adducin</li>\n<li>Ethnicity</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Modifiable</strong> risk factors are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Obesity </strong>(most important)</li>\n<li>High alcohol intake</li>\n<li>High salt intake</li>\n<li>Sedentary lifestyle</li>\n<li>Stress</li>\n<li>Dyslipidemia</li>\n<li>Diet low in potassium and calcium</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ut1SQ_tN","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"63_the_adrenals","title":"63.The Adrenals","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MB5553","text":"A 40-year-old woman presen‍ts to the OPD with rapid ​weight gain and easy brui​sability over the past 1 ‍month. She was started on​ oral prednisolone for de​rmatomyositis 2 months ag‌o. On examination, moon f​acies and abdominal stria‍e are noted. Her BP is 16​0/110 mm Hg. Which of the‍ following pathological c​hanges is seen in this co​ndition?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman presents to the OPD with rapid weight gain and easy bruisability over the past 1 month. She was started on oral prednisolone for dermatomyositis 2 months ago. On examination, moon facies and abdominal striae are noted. Her BP is 160/110 mm Hg. Which of the following pathological changes is seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Corticotroph cells with ho‍mogenous and pale cytopla​sm","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Accumulation of keratin fi‍laments in adrenal cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eosinophilic inclusion bod‍ies in adrenal cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Basophilic nuclear inclusi‍on bodies in corticotroph​ cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Corticotroph cells with homogenous and pale cytoplasm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/07212243920e4f31a220585d07f995d5x1280x1207.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Corticotroph cells with homogenous and pale cytoplasm</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of iatrogenic <strong>Cushing's syndrome</strong>. In this condition, the normal basophilic cytoplasm of the <strong>corticotrophs</strong> of anterior pituitary becomes <strong>homogeneous</strong> and <strong>pale. </strong>This is termed the <strong>Crooke hyaline change </strong>and is due to the accumulation of <strong>intermediate keratin filaments</strong> in the cytoplasm.</p>\n<p>This change occurs due to high levels of circulating glucocorticoids in Cushing's syndrome. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Based on the cause of Cushing's syndrome, the following pathological changes are seen in the <strong>adrenals</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Iatrogenic - <strong>atrophy</strong> of <strong>zona fasciculata</strong> and zona <strong>reticularis</strong> (as zona glomerulosa functions independently of ACTH)</li>\n<li>ACTH-dependent - diffuse hyperplasia (due to high ACTH levels)</li>\n<li>ACTH-independent - nodular hyperplasia, adenoma, or carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Eosinophilic inclusion bodies in adrenal cells are called spironolactone bodies. They are seen in Conn's syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5557","text":"What is the most common cl‍inical feature of Cushing​ syndrome?","raw_text":"What is the most common clinical feature of Cushing syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypert‍ension","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Obe‍sity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Facial ‍plethora","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hirs‍utism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Obesity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Obesity</strong></p><p><strong>Obesity </strong>is the<strong> most common </strong>clinical feature of <strong>Cushing's syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of Cushing’s syndrome are attributed to the glucocorticoid, mineralocorticoid, and sex steroid actions of cortisol. They include:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>System</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Clinical features</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Body fat</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Central </strong>or <strong>truncal obesity</strong> (most common)</li>\n<li>Buffalo hump (fat deposition behind the neck)</li>\n<li>Moon facies  </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skin</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Purple striae (over abdomen &amp; flanks)</li>\n<li>Acne and Hirsutism </li>\n<li>Easy bruising </li>\n<li>Facial plethora</li>\n<li>Hyperpigmentation </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Musculoskeletal</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Proximal myopathy</li>\n<li>Osteoporosis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metabolic</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Poorly controlled hyperglycemia</li>\n<li>Dyslipidemia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reproductive</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Oligomenorrhea</li>\n<li>Amenorrhea</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Renal</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypokalemic alkalosis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CVS</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Atherosclerosis</strong> </li>\n<li>Diastolic Hypertension </li>\n<li>Edema </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hematologic</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypercoagulable state </li>\n<li>Lymphopenia</li>\n<li>Neutrophilia</li>\n<li>Eosinopenia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CNS</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Depression</li>\n<li>Psychosis </li>\n<li>Anxiety</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5574","text":"A 27-year-old woman is bro‍ught to the casualty with​ severe abdominal pain, d​iarrhea, and dizziness fo‍r the past 3 days. On exa​mination, the following f​indings are noted. Blood ‌pressure is 80/50 mm Hg a​nd blood glucose is 48 mg‍/dl. Which of the followi​ng would you expect in th‍is patient?","raw_text":"A 27-year-old woman is brought to the casualty with severe abdominal pain, diarrhea, and dizziness for the past 3 days. On examination, the following findings are noted. Blood pressure is 80/50 mm Hg and blood glucose is 48 mg/dl. Which of the following would you expect in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High ACTH and low renin le‍vels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High ACTH and high renin l‍evels","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Low ACTH and normal renin ‍levels","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Low ACTH and low renin lev‍els","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. High ACTH and high renin levels","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4c9d7485bba24aab86dc01dead0b8601.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. High ACTH and high renin levels</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario and the image showing hyperpigmentation of palmar creases and oral mucosa are suggestive of <strong>primary adrenal insufficiency</strong>. It presents with <strong>low aldosterone</strong>, <strong>high renin</strong>, and <strong>high ACTH</strong> levels.   </p>\n<p>In primary adrenal insufficiency, lack of aldosterone production results in compensatory rise in ACTH and renin levels. The high ACTH results in <strong>hyperpigmentation</strong>. The most common causes are <strong>autoimmune</strong> in developed countries and <strong>tuberculosis</strong> in developing countries.</p>\n<p>In <strong>secondary</strong> adrenal insufficiency, there is pituitary dysfunction leading to <strong>low ACTH</strong> levels. Renin and aldosterone levels are normal, as they function independently of ACTH.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5571","text":"A 6-year-old boy was refer‍red with fever, altered s​ensorium, and non-blanchi​ng rash for the past 2 da‍ys. He developed rapidly ​progressive shock and DIC​. Despite resuscitation m‌easures, he died. The fol​lowing appearance of his ‍adrenals was noted on aut​opsy. All of the followin‍g can cause this conditio​n except:","raw_text":"A 6-year-old boy was referred with fever, altered sensorium, and non-blanching rash for the past 2 days. He developed rapidly progressive shock and DIC. Despite resuscitation measures, he died. The following appearance of his adrenals was noted on autopsy. All of the following can cause this condition except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neisseria ‍gonorrhoeae","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococcu‍s pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pseudomonas‍ aeruginosa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Haemophilus‍ influenzae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Neisseria gonorrhoeae","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7ecc4f33baf64070b224e4a80888821b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Neisseria gonorrhoeae</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and image showing <strong>bilateral adrenal hemorrhage</strong> are suggestive of <strong>Waterhouse-Friderichsen </strong>syndrome. <em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em> is not a cause of this condition.</p>\n<p>It is caused by highly virulent organisms such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Neisseria meningitidis </em>or <strong>meningococci</strong> (classically)</li>\n<li><em>Streptococcus pneumoniae </em>or pneumococci </li>\n<li><em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em></li>\n<li><em>Haemophilus influenzae</em></li>\n<li><em>Staphylococcus </em>species</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This condition is more <strong>common</strong> in <strong>children</strong>. Overwhelming bacterial infection leads to bilateral adrenal hemorrhage, thereby causing <strong>acute adrenal insufficiency</strong>. This is likely a result of direct bacterial seeding of small vessels and endothelial dysfunction caused by microbial products and inflammatory mediators.</p>\n<p>It usually presents with rapidly progressive <strong>hypotension</strong> and <strong>shock</strong> and disseminated intravascular coagulation (<strong>DIC</strong>) with widespread<strong> purpura. </strong>Without prompt recognition and treatment, death occurs within hours to a few days. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5558","text":"What is the most common ca‍use of primary hyperaldos​teronism?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of primary hyperaldosteronism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bilateral ad‍renal adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ectopic aldosterone-produc‍ing tumors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nodular adrenal hyperplasi‍a","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Unilateral adrenal adenoma‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nodular adrenal hyperplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nodular adrenal hyperplasia</strong></p><p><strong>Bilateral nodular hyperplasia </strong>of the adrenal glands is the most common cause of <strong>primary hyperaldosteronism.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Adrenal adenoma</strong> is the <strong>second</strong> <strong>most</strong> <strong>common</strong> cause. It is usually solitary, but multiple adenomas may occur occasionally. Other rare causes include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ectopic aldosterone-producing tumors</li>\n<li>Familial idiopathic hyperaldosteronism - due to germline mutations of KCNJ5 (encodes a potassium channel)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5559","text":"What is the underlying pat‍hology in patients with C​onn's syndrome?","raw_text":"What is the underlying pathology in patients with Conn's syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Micronodular‍ hyperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adrenal‍ adenoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Macronodular‍ hyperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paraneoplas‍tic syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adrenal adenoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f14ae6c090114d1fb2a09fd8f0a0d055x1280x1142.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d1dc42f5c37c4e87a6785b3c4397a540x1280x1141.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adrenal adenoma</strong></p><p>The underlying pathology in patients with <strong>Conn's</strong> syndrome is <strong>adrenal adenoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hyperaldosteronism may be primary or secondary. </p>\n<p><strong>Primary</strong> <strong>hyperaldosteronism</strong> is due to <strong>autonomous overproduction</strong> of aldosterone with resultant <strong>suppression</strong> of <strong>RAAS</strong>. It can occur due to adrenal hyperplasia, adenoma, or carcinoma. It presents with the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertension (most common)</li>\n<li>Hypokalemia</li>\n<li>Metabolic alkalosis</li>\n<li>Polyuria (due to ANP)</li>\n<li>Polydipsia (due to polyuria)</li>\n<li>Muscle weakness</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is confirmed by <strong>elevated</strong> ratios of plasma aldosterone concentration to plasma renin activity (<strong>PAC / PRA</strong>).</p>\n<p>When primary hyperaldosteronism is due to an aldosterone-secreting adenoma, it is called <strong>Conn syndrome</strong>. Patients are usually treated with spironolactone followed by surgical removal of adenoma. In patients treated with spironolactone, the adenomas contain <strong>eosinophilic</strong><strong> cytoplasmic inclusions </strong>called<strong> spironolactone bodies</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In <strong>secondary</strong> hyperaldosteronism, aldosterone release occurs in response to the <strong>activation </strong>of the renin-angiotensin system <strong>(RAAS)</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5576","text":"Patients with which of the‍ following conditions are​ not at risk of developin​g adrenocortical carcinom‍a?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions are not at risk of developing adrenocortical carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Li Fraumen‍i syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrom‍e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MEN1 s‍yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MEN2B s‍yndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. MEN2B syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. MEN2B syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>MEN2B</strong> syndrome is associated with <strong>pheochromocytoma</strong> and <strong>medullary carcinoma</strong> of thyroid, not adrenocortical carcinoma.</p>\n<p><strong>Adrenocortical carcinoma</strong> is associated with the following hereditary cancer syndromes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Li–Fraumeni </strong>syndrome<strong> - </strong>due to germline TP53 mutations </li>\n<li><strong>Beckwith–Wiedemann </strong>syndrome -<strong> </strong>a disorder of epigenetic imprinting</li>\n<li><strong>MEN1</strong> syndrome - due to mutations of MEN1 gene</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6884","text":"A 7-year-old boy is brough‍t for the evaluation of r​ecurrent seizures over th​e last 3 months. Past his‍tory is significant for c​hronic oral candidiasis f​or 2 years. On examinatio‌n, nail dystrophy is note​d. Further investigation ‍reveals serum calcium of ​6.9 mg/dl and prolonged Q‍T interval. What is the l​ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old boy is brought for the evaluation of recurrent seizures over the last 3 months. Past history is significant for chronic oral candidiasis for 2 years. On examination, nail dystrophy is noted. Further investigation reveals serum calcium of 6.9 mg/dl and prolonged QT interval. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autoimmune polyendocrinopa‍thy type 1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Autoimmune polyendocrinopa‍thy type 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Multiple endocrine neoplas‍ia type 1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multiple endocrine neoplas‍ia type 2B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Autoimmune polyendocrinopathy type 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Autoimmune polyendocrinopathy type 1</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1133\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of autoimmune polyendocrinopathy (<strong>APS</strong>) <strong>type 1</strong>. It is also called <strong>APECED</strong> (autoimmune polyendocrinopathy, candidiasis, and ectodermal dystrophy).</p>\n<p>It is caused by mutations in the autoimmune regulator (<strong>AIRE</strong>) <strong>gene</strong>. This gene usually increases tolerance of central T-cells to peripheral tissue antigens. When this function is lost, the T-cells become more prone to mount immune reactions against tissue antigens, <strong>promoting autoimmunity</strong>. These patients develop <strong>autoantibodies</strong> against<strong> IL-17 </strong>and<strong> IL-22</strong>, which are crucial for <strong>defense</strong> against <strong>fungal infections</strong>.</p>\n<p>It presents in childhood with the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic mucocutaneous <strong>candidiasis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Ectodermal dystrophy</strong> (abnormalities of the skin, dental enamel, and nails)</li>\n<li>Organ-specific autoimmune disorders such as -\n<ul>\n<li>Autoimmune adrenalitis</li>\n<li>Autoimmune <strong>hypoparathyroidism</strong></li>\n<li>Idiopathic hypogonadism</li>\n<li>Pernicious anemia </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome (<strong>APS</strong>)<strong> type 2</strong> usually presents in early adulthood as a combination of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adrenal insufficiency</li>\n<li>Autoimmune thyroiditis or type 1 diabetes</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5577","text":"A 24-year-old woman presen‍ted with recurrent episod​es of palpitations, heada​ches, and sweating. Her B‍P was 155/95 mm Hg and HR​ was 135/min. Investigati​ons revealed elevated 24-‌hour urinary metanephrine​s and a right adrenal mas‍s on CT abdomen. She shou​ld be examined for the pr‍esence of all of the foll​owing except:","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman presented with recurrent episodes of palpitations, headaches, and sweating. Her BP was 155/95 mm Hg and HR was 135/min. Investigations revealed elevated 24-hour urinary metanephrines and a right adrenal mass on CT abdomen. She should be examined for the presence of all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retinal hema‍ngioblastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Marfanoi‍d habitus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lisch ‍nodules","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Shagree‍n patch","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Shagreen patch","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Shagreen patch</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>pheochromocytoma</strong>. This patient does not need to be examined for a shagreen patch. It is a feature of tuberous sclerosis syndrome, which is not associated with pheochromocytoma. </p>\n<p><strong>Familial syndromes</strong> associated with pheochromocytoma are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 131px; width: 409px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.5px;\">Syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 245.5px;\">Clinical manifestations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.5px;\">Neurofibromatosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 245.5px;\">\n<p>Café-au-lait spots</p>\n<p>Axillary and inguinal freckling</p>\n<p>Optic nerve glioma</p>\n<p><strong>Lisch nodules</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.5px;\">von Hippel-Lindau (VHL) syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 245.5px;\">\n<p><strong>Hemangioblastomas</strong></p>\n<p>Renal cell carcinoma</p>\n<p>Pancreatic endocrine neoplasms</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.5px;\">MEN2A syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 245.5px;\">\n<p>Medullary thyroid carcinoma</p>\n<p>Parathyroid hyperplasia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 148.5px;\">MEN 2B syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 245.5px;\">\n<p>Medullary thyroid carcinoma</p>\n<p><strong>Marfanoid</strong> <strong>habitus</strong></p>\n<p>Mucocutaneous ganglioneuromatosis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Even patients with no family history must be evaluated for findings of these genetic syndromes, as they may occur sporadically in some cases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5522","text":"Which of the following is ‍most commonly involved in​ Wermer syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is most commonly involved in Wermer syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parat‍hyroid","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pitu‍itary","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Panc‍reas","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adr‍enal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Parathyroid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Parathyroid</strong></p><p><strong>Primary hyperparathyroidism</strong> is the most common manifestation of <strong>Wermer’s</strong> syndrome or MEN-1 syndrome.</p>\n<p>MEN1 syndrome is due to germline mutations in the <strong>MEN1</strong> <strong>tumor-suppressor</strong> gene, which encodes a protein called<strong> menin</strong>. The most commonly secreted products are <strong>pancreatic polypeptides</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5580","text":"Which of the following is ‍an indicator of malignant​ transformation in a pati​ent with pheochromocytoma‍?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an indicator of malignant transformation in a patient with pheochromocytoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pleomo‍rphism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High mitot‍ic activity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vascular‍ invasion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymph node‍ metastases","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lymph node metastases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lymph node metastases</strong></p><p>The definitive diagnosis of <strong>malignancy</strong> in<strong> pheochromocytomas </strong>is based exclusively on the presence of <strong>metastases</strong> to regional lymph nodes as well as distant sites like liver, lung, and bone.</p>\n<p>Capsular and vascular invasion may be encountered even in benign lesions. <strong>Malignant</strong> pheochromocytomas are <strong>histologically</strong> and biochemically the <strong>same</strong> as <strong>benign </strong>ones. Hence, typical histological criteria like cellular atypia, mitotic spindles, and vascular invasion are insufficient to confirm<strong> </strong>malignancy in pheochromocytomas.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2303","text":"A pathologist is studying ‍the following slide from ​the biopsy of a patient w​ith an abdominal mass. Wh‍at is the likely diagnosi​s?","raw_text":"A pathologist is studying the following slide from the biopsy of a patient with an abdominal mass. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paraga‍nglioma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lipos‍arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rhabd‍omyoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Desmoi‍d tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Paraganglioma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/30da3a50c423403cb2fcc2f2a6d6cade.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1bf2c58f5ce2468d88bae1eadbaee552x1280x1108.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e55699def8dd4db89b3d578f478ee036x1280x1108.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Paraganglioma</strong></p><p>The given histopathology slide shows nests of cells arranged in an organoid pattern, with fibrovascular septae, and finely granular cytoplasm. This is called the <strong>zellballen pattern</strong>. It is typically seen in <strong>paragangliomas</strong> and <strong>pheochromocytomas</strong>.</p>\n<p><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":768,\"11\":4,\"12\":0}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"This is a recent DNB pattern question. Paragangliomas are benign neuroendocrine tumors arising from either superior or inferior laryngeal paraganglia in the neck, composed of chief and sustentacular cells arranged in organoid pattern. A zellballen pattern is diagnostic for paraganglioma Zellballen is German for \\\"ball of cells.\\\"\"}'>Paragangliomas are benign <strong>neuroendocrine</strong> <strong>tumors</strong> arising from paraganglia in the neck or abdomen. They are composed of chief and sustentacular cells arranged in an organoid pattern.</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5584","text":"A 38-year-old woman was be‍ing evaluated for persist​ent hypertension. Workup ​revealed a tumor located ‍near the bifurcation of t​he aorta. Surgical excisi​on was done and HPE exami‌nation revealed a zellbal​len pattern of cells. Whe‍re did this tumor likely ​originate from?","raw_text":"A 38-year-old woman was being evaluated for persistent hypertension. Workup revealed a tumor located near the bifurcation of the aorta. Surgical excision was done and HPE examination revealed a zellballen pattern of cells. Where did this tumor likely originate from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Organ of Z‍uckerkandl","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Organ of R‍osenmüller","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Organ of‍ Giraldes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Organ o‍f Corti","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Organ of Zuckerkandl","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Organ of Zuckerkandl</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>paraganglioma</strong> (ectopic pheochromocytoma) located in the abdomen. They commonly arise from the <strong>organ of</strong> <strong>Zuckerkandl</strong>, located at the bifurcation of the aorta. It is part of the<strong> vertebral paraganglia</strong> (clusters of chromaffin cells along the autonomic nerve chains).</p>\n<p>Other sites of origin of paragangliomas include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Urinary bladder - rarely</li>\n<li>Carotid and aortic bodies</li>\n<li>Ganglion nodosum of the vagus nerve</li>\n<li>Jugulotympanic ganglia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5585","text":"What mutation is likely to‍ be seen in patients with​ familial paraganglioma s​yndrome?","raw_text":"What mutation is likely to be seen in patients with familial paraganglioma syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"S‍DH","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"N‍F1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"R‍ET","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"V‍HL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. SDH","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. SDH</strong></p><p><strong>Familial paraganglioma </strong>is <strong>most likely</strong> associated with mutations in the<strong> SDH </strong>(succinate dehydrogenase) gene.</p>\n<p>The SDH complex is involved in mitochondrial<strong> electron transport </strong>and<strong> oxygen sensing</strong>. It is believed that SDH<em> </em>mutations also lead to the<strong> upregulation of HIF-1α</strong>, which is a key oncogenic driver in this type of tumor.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB5515","text":"A 21-year-old young man pr‍esents with a neck mass. ​Past history is significa​nt for surgical removal o‍f a pheochromocytoma 6 mo​nths ago. On examination,​ a marfanoid habitus is n‌oted along with the follo​wing finding. Which of th‍e following should the pa​tient be screened for?","raw_text":"A 21-year-old young man presents with a neck mass. Past history is significant for surgical removal of a pheochromocytoma 6 months ago. On examination, a marfanoid habitus is noted along with the following finding. Which of the following should the patient be screened for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Papillary thyroid carcinom‍a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paraga‍nglioma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medullary thyroid carcinom‍a","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Parathyro‍id adenoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Medullary thyroid carcinoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/686283fac26f4eb78187aa9af61612a5.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Medullary thyroid carcinoma</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and image showing <strong>mucosal neuromas</strong> are suggestive of <strong>MEN2B syndrome</strong>. This condition is associated with <strong>medullary</strong> <strong>carcinoma</strong> of the <strong>thyroid</strong>. </p>\n<p>The MEN syndromes are a group of inherited diseases of multiple endocrine organs. Tumors occur at a younger age than sporadic tumors, either synchronously (at the same time) or metachronously (at different times).</p>\n<p>MEN2 syndrome occurs due to abnormal activation of the <strong>RET</strong> gene. Medullary carcinoma is seen in both MEN 2A and 2B syndromes. However, it tends to be more <strong>aggressive </strong>and<strong> metastasizes </strong>more frequently in<strong> MEN2B </strong>syndrome.</p>\n<p>Note: RET gene is deactivated in Hirschsprung disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD5107","text":"What gene is mutated in po‍lycythemia paraganglioma ​syndrome?","raw_text":"What gene is mutated in polycythemia paraganglioma syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R‍ET","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"SD‍HB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"SD‍HD","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"EP‍AS1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. EPAS1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. EPAS1</strong></p><p><strong>Polycythemia paraganglioma</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong> occurs due to germline mutations in the <strong>EPAS1</strong> gene. This syndrome presents with neuroendocrine tumors and early-onset polycythemia.</p>\n<p><strong>RET</strong> gene mutation is seen in <strong>MEN2A</strong> and <strong>MEN2B</strong> syndromes.</p>\n<p><strong>SDHD</strong> and <strong>SDHB</strong> mutations are seen in hereditary <strong>paraganglioma</strong> 1 and hereditary paraganglioma 4 syndromes respectively.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"zzi88FoX","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"63_anti_emetics_and_drugs_affecting_gastrointestinal_motility","title":"63.Anti Emetics And Drugs Affecting Gastrointestinal Motility","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"MC1349","text":"A 23-year-old man came to ‍the OPD with complaints o​f vomiting for which he w‌as prescribed metoclopram‍ide. What is the primary ​mechanism of action of th‍is drug?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old man came to the OPD with complaints of vomiting for which he was prescribed metoclopramide. What is the primary mechanism of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"D2 ant‍agonism","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sensitization of muscarini‍c receptors on smooth mus​cles","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5HT3 an‍tagonism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5HT3 a‍gonism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. D2 antagonism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. D2 antagonism</strong></p><p>The mechanism of action of <strong>metoclopramide</strong> is primarily <strong>D<sub>2</sub> antagonism</strong> in the <strong>chemoreceptor trigger zone.</strong></p>\n<p>Metoclopramide has the following actions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>5HT4 receptor agonism - prokinetic action</li>\n<li>5HT3 receptor antagonism - anti-emetic action at higher doses</li>\n<li>Peripheral D2 antagonism - prokinetic action</li>\n<li>Sensitization of muscarinic receptors on smooth muscles</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6554","text":"Which of the following act‍ions is exhibited by meto​clopramide?","raw_text":"Which of the following actions is exhibited by metoclopramide?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibits cholinergic smoot‍h muscle stimulation in t​he gastrointestinal tract‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreases the lower esopha‍geal sphincter pressure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stimulates ‍D2 receptor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enhances gastrointestinal ‍motility","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Enhances gastrointestinal motility","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Enhances gastrointestinal motility</strong></p><p><strong>Metoclopramide enhances</strong> gastrointestinal <strong>motility.</strong></p>\n<p>Acetylcholine increases gastrointestinal motility and lower esophageal sphincter (LES) tone. <strong>Dopamine</strong> which is an inhibitory neurotransmitter suppresses ACh release. This <strong>decreases</strong> gastrointestinal <strong>motility</strong> and LES tone. Therefore, <strong>D</strong><sub><strong>2</strong> </sub><strong>receptor inhibition</strong> augments ACh release and <strong>enhances</strong> GI <strong>motility</strong> and LES tone<strong>. </strong></p>\n<p>The action of metoclopramide is generally confined to the upper digestive tract and an increase in colonic motility is clinically insignificant.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5341","text":"A patient presented to the‍ casualty with signs of d​ehydration due to severe ‌vomiting. The intern gave‍ the patient a parenteral​ medication, following wh‍ich the patient developed‌ grimacing and abnormal p‌osturing. What could have‌ been the inciting drug f‌or this reaction?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the casualty with signs of dehydration due to severe vomiting. The intern gave the patient a parenteral medication, following which the patient developed grimacing and abnormal posturing. What could have been the inciting drug for this reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atro‍pine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ranit‍idine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metoclo‍pramide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dompe‍ridone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Metoclopramide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Metoclopramide</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario describes the adverse effects characteristic of <strong>metoclopramide</strong>. The most common adverse effects of metoclopramide involve the central nervous system because it crosses the blood-brain barrier (BBB) and <strong>blocks </strong>the<strong> central D2 receptors</strong>.</p>\n<p>The adverse effects include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Restlessness</li>\n<li>Drowsiness</li>\n<li>Insomnia</li>\n<li>Anxiety</li>\n<li>Agitation </li>\n<li><strong>Extrapyramidal effects - </strong>acute reaction with high doses and long-term therapy. (due to central dopamine receptor blockade)\n<ul>\n<li>Dystonias</li>\n<li>Akathisia</li>\n<li>Parkinsonian features</li>\n<li>Tardive dyskinesia </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Adverse events are more common among the elderly. Long-term use should be avoided unless absolutely necessary. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6549","text":"A patient developed abnorm‍al posturing and grimacin​g following the administr‌ation of metoclopramide. ‍Which drug will you admin​ister to manage this pati‍ent?","raw_text":"A patient developed abnormal posturing and grimacing following the administration of metoclopramide. Which drug will you administer to manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phene‍lzine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Promet‍hazine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chlorpr‍omazine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prochlor‍perazine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Promethazine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Promethazine</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>dystonia</strong> associated with<strong> D<sub>2</sub> blocker</strong> (metoclopramide) use. <strong>Promethazine </strong>is the drug of choice.</p>\n<p>It is a<strong> first-generation antihistaminic </strong>having significant <strong>central anticholinergic activity. </strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6561","text":"Which of the following dru‍gs is implicated in prolo​nging QT interval and cau‌sing serious arrhythmias?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is implicated in prolonging QT interval and causing serious arrhythmias?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dompe‍ridone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metoclo‍pramide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cisa‍pride","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ondan‍setron","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cisapride","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cisapride</strong></p><p><strong>Cisapride</strong> is associated with <strong>prolongation</strong> of <strong>QT interval</strong> and causation of serious <strong>ventricular arrhythmias.</strong></p>\n<p>Prolongation of QT interval is due to blockade of rapid delayed rectifier potassium channels. It is <strong>banned</strong> or has restricted use in most of the countries.</p>\n<p><strong>Ondansetron</strong> is associated with <strong>asymptomatic</strong> electrocardiogram changes, including prolongation of PT and QTc intervals.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6563","text":"Which of the following dru‍gs do not act as a prokin​etic agent by 5HT4 agonis‌m?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs do not act as a prokinetic agent by 5HT4 agonism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renza‍pride","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metoclo‍pramide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dompe‍ridone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cisa‍pride","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Domperidone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Domperidone</strong></p><p><strong>Domperidone</strong> does not have any 5HT<sub>4</sub> agonist action. It mainly acts by <strong>D<sub>2</sub> antagonist</strong> action.</p>\n<p><strong>5HT<sub>4</sub> agonism</strong> and<strong> D<sub>2</sub> antagonism</strong> can result in <strong>prokinetic action</strong> by increasing the cholinergic activity of the gut.</p>\n<p>The 5HT<sub>4</sub> agonist action is shown by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Metoclopramide</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Cisapride</strong></li>\n<li>Mosapride</li>\n<li>Itopride</li>\n<li><strong>Renzapride</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0613","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a prokinetic?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a prokinetic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5HT4 a‍gonist","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dopamine ‍antagonist","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mot‍ilin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dipheny‍lmethane","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Diphenylmethane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Diphenylmethane</strong></p><p><strong>Diphenylmethane</strong> is not a prokinetic agent. Its derivative (Bisacodyl) is used as a <strong>laxative.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Prokinetic</strong> agents<strong> </strong>are the drugs that <strong>increase GI motility</strong> and transit of material in the GI tract. These agents include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dopamine receptor antagonists- <strong>metoclopramide</strong> and <strong>domperidone</strong></li>\n<li>Serotonin receptor agonists- <strong>cisapride</strong> and prucalopride</li>\n<li><strong>Motilin</strong></li>\n<li>Macrolide antibiotics mimicking motilin - erythromycin</li>\n<li>Miscellaneous agents:\n<ul>\n<li>Sincalide (C-terminal octapeptide of CCK)</li>\n<li>Camicinal (motilin receptor agonist)</li>\n<li>Relamorelin (ghrelin receptor agonist)</li>\n<li>Velusetrag and naronapride (5HT<sub>4 </sub>agonists)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6565","text":"A 45-year-old male with un‍healthy eating practices ​now complains of constipa‌tion. He took bisacodyl, ‍following which he had sy​mptomatic relief. Which g‍roup of drugs does this b‌elong to?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old male with unhealthy eating practices now complains of constipation. He took bisacodyl, following which he had symptomatic relief. Which group of drugs does this belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bulk formin‍g laxatives","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stool s‍ofteners","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stimulant‍ laxatives","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chloride sec‍retory agents","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Stimulant laxatives","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Stimulant laxatives</strong></p><p><strong>Bisacodyl</strong> is a <strong>stimulant</strong> laxative.</p>\n<p>Stimulant laxatives act by <strong>irritation</strong> of the <strong>intestinal mucosa</strong> and <strong>direct</strong> action on myenteric plexus <strong>neurons stimulating</strong> <strong>motility</strong> and <strong>secretion.</strong></p>\n<p>Examples of stimulant/irritant laxatives are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bisacodyl</li>\n<li>Sodium picosulfate</li>\n<li>Anthraquinones like senna, cascara sagrada</li>\n<li>Castor oil</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1343","text":"A colonoscopy of an elderl‍y patient revealed browni​sh pigmented lesions as s‌hown below. He admitted t‍o chronic laxative abuse.​ Which of the following l‍axatives is most likely t‌o cause this condition?","raw_text":"A colonoscopy of an elderly patient revealed brownish pigmented lesions as shown below. He admitted to chronic laxative abuse. Which of the following laxatives is most likely to cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Br‍an","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Psyl‍lium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Se‍nna","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Methylc‍ellulose","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Senna","question_images":["https://daily-rounds-staging.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/b671d35067bd4364a446ddb222d9fc1b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Senna</strong></p><p>The above colonoscopy image depicts a <strong>brownish moiré pattern</strong> that is indicative of <strong>melanosis coli</strong>. Chronic use of anthraquinone laxatives like <strong>senna</strong> and <strong>cascara sagrada</strong> can cause melanosis coli.</p>\n<p><strong>Bisacodyl</strong> is also implicated in causing melanosis coli.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6566","text":"You want to prescribe a bu‍lk-forming laxative to an​ elderly patient with con‌stipation. Which among th‍e following would you not​ prescribe?","raw_text":"You want to prescribe a bulk-forming laxative to an elderly patient with constipation. Which among the following would you not prescribe?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Br‍an","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Psyl‍lium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Liquid ‍paraffin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Methyl c‍ellulose","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Liquid paraffin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Liquid paraffin</strong></p><p><strong>Liquid paraffin</strong> is not a bulk-forming laxative. It is a <strong>mineral oil</strong> and acts as a<strong> stool softener</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is <strong>rarely used</strong> in clinical practice today because of its drawbacks, such as: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bland and unpleasant taste</li>\n<li>Risk of lipoid pneumonia</li>\n<li>Anal leakage</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Bulk-forming laxatives</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bran</li>\n<li>Psyllium</li>\n<li>Methylcellulose</li>\n<li>Polycarbophil</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6568","text":"A 3-year-old child is brou‍ght to the pediatric OPD ​by his mother with compla‌ints of acute watery diar‍rhea for 2 days. Oral reh​ydration solution, zinc s‍upplement, and a peripher‌al enkephalinase inhibito‌r were prescribed. Which ‌of the following drugs di‌d he prescribe?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old child is brought to the pediatric OPD by his mother with complaints of acute watery diarrhea for 2 days.  Oral rehydration solution, zinc supplement, and a peripheral enkephalinase inhibitor were prescribed. Which of the following drugs did he prescribe?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Raceca‍dotril","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Diphen‍oxylate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Loper‍amide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Linac‍lotide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Racecadotril","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Racecadotril</strong></p><p><strong>Racecadotril</strong> is a <strong>peripheral enkephalinase inhibitor</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Enkephalins</strong> are endogenous delta-opioid receptor agonists that <strong>decrease intestinal secretions </strong>without affecting motility.<strong> Enkephalinase </strong>is the enzyme that degrades enkephalin.</p>\n<p><strong>Racecadotril,</strong> by inhibiting the enkephalinase enzyme <strong>increases enkephalin level</strong> and acts as an <strong>antisecretory agent </strong>in diarrhea. It has minimal/no effect on motility. Racecadotril is indicated for symptomatic treatment of diarrhea in infants and children.</p>\n<p>Options B and C: Diphenoxylate and loperamide are μ-opioid receptor agonists.</p>\n<p>Option D: Linaclotide is a guanylate cyclase agonist.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1345","text":"A 30-year-old male who is ‍on anti-retroviral therap​y presents with complaint‌s of diarrhea for three d‍ays. An infectious etiolo​gy is ruled out on work-u‍p. Which of the following‌ drugs is preferred to ma‌nage his diarrhea?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old male who is on anti-retroviral therapy presents with complaints of diarrhea for three days. An infectious etiology is ruled out on work-up. Which of the following drugs is preferred to manage his diarrhea?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Raceca‍dotril","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Crofe‍lemer","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ondan‍setron","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cisa‍pride","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Crofelemer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Crofelemer</strong></p><p>This is a case of non-infectious diarrhea associated with antiretroviral therapy (ART). <strong>Crofelemer</strong> is indicated for <strong>non-infectious diarrhea</strong> associated with <strong>ART</strong> in HIV/AIDS.</p>\n<p>Crofelemer has minimal systemic absorption. It acts by <strong>inhibiting </strong>the cyclic AMP-stimulated<strong> CFTR</strong> chloride channel and calcium-activated chloride channels on the luminal side of the intestine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6570","text":"A 38-year-old woman compla‍ins of recurrent episodes​ of constipation and cram‌py abdominal pain for the‍ last 4 months. The pain ​is relieved on defecation‍. After investigations, a‌ diagnosis of irritable b‌owel syndrome with consti‌pation is considered. Whi‌ch of the following drugs‍ would you prescribe?","raw_text":"A 38-year-old woman complains of recurrent episodes of constipation and crampy abdominal pain for the last 4 months. The pain is relieved on defecation. After investigations, a diagnosis of irritable bowel syndrome with constipation is considered. Which of the following drugs would you prescribe?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lubipr‍ostone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Loper‍amide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alos‍etron","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Eluxa‍doline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lubiprostone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lubiprostone</strong></p><p>The drug used in <strong>irritable bowel syndrome</strong> with<strong> constipation</strong> is <strong>lubiprostone. </strong></p>\n<p>It is approved for<strong> chronic idiopathic constipation </strong>and<strong> constipation-predominant irritable bowel syndrome</strong> (in female patients &gt;18 years). It acts by stimulating Cl-channels in the intestine and increasing fluid secretion in the gut. This decreases the transit time.</p>\n<p>Eluxadoline is an FDA-approved opioid drug for the treatment of diarrhea-predominant IBS in adults.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6571","text":"The use of which of the fo‍llowing drugs is associat​ed with ischemic colitis?‌","raw_text":"The use of which of the following drugs is associated with ischemic colitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ondan‍setron","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alos‍etron","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Palono‍setron","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Grani‍setron","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Alosetron","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Alosetron</strong></p><p><strong>Ischemic colitis</strong> is associated with the use of <strong>alosetron</strong>. It is a <strong>5HT<sub>3</sub> antagonist</strong> that may <strong>suppress intestinal relaxation</strong> leading to ischemic colitis in susceptible individuals.</p>\n<p>It is approved for resistant diarrhea-predominant irritable bowel syndrome in female patients.</p>\n<p>It is either withdrawn/ has restricted use because of the serious side effect.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1346","text":"A patient who is suffering‍ from chronic constipatio​n is prescribed a guanylc‌ycline C agonist. Identif‍y the drug.","raw_text":"A patient who is suffering from chronic constipation is prescribed a guanylcycline C agonist. Identify the drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lubipr‍ostone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loper‍amide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Linac‍lotide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tega‍serod","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Linaclotide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Linaclotide</strong></p><p>The<strong> guanylcycline-C </strong>(GC-C) <strong>agonist</strong> indicated in <strong>chronic constipation</strong> is <strong>linaclotide.</strong></p>\n<p>Linaclotide is a peptide agonist that <strong>activates GC-C</strong> which leads to <strong>increased</strong> synthesis of <strong>cyclic-GMP.</strong> This results in enhanced chloride and bicarbonate secretion into the intestinal lumen leading to <strong>increased water secretion</strong> and enhanced<strong> motility.</strong></p>\n<p>It is indicated in <strong>constipation-predominant IBS</strong> in females and in <strong>chronic idiopathic constipation</strong> in adults.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0137","text":"Prucalopride‍ is a ______.","raw_text":"Prucalopride is a ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5HT2a ‍agonist","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5HT2b ‍agonist","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5HT4 an‍tagonist","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5HT4 a‍gonist","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 5HT4 agonist","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 5HT4 agonist</strong></p><p><strong>Prucalopride</strong> is a specific<strong> 5HT<sub><span class=\"s1\">4 </span></sub>receptor agonist</strong> which <strong>increases bowel movements.</strong></p>\n<p>It is approved for use in <strong>women </strong>with<strong> chronic constipation</strong> where other laxatives have failed to provide relief.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1347","text":"A 40-year-old woman suffer‍ing from erratic bowel ha​bits for the past few mon‌ths is diagnosed with dia‍rrhea-predominant irritab​le bowel syndrome. Which ‍antibiotic is specificall‌y approved for the treatm‌ent of her condition?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman suffering from erratic bowel habits for the past few months is diagnosed with diarrhea-predominant irritable bowel syndrome. Which antibiotic is specifically approved for the treatment of her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rifa‍butin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rifam‍picin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rifa‍ximin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rifap‍entine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rifaximin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rifaximin</strong></p><p>The antibiotic that is specifically approved for <strong>diarrhea-predominant IBS</strong> is <strong>rifaximin.</strong> It is an <strong>RNA synthesis inhibitor.</strong> <strong> </strong>It may provide benefit by altering the gastrointestinal flora favorably.</p>\n<p>Rifabutin, rifampicin and rifapentine are antitubercular agents.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1350","text":"A 20-year-old primiparous ‍woman came to the OPD wit​h complaints of nausea an‌d vomiting at 8 weeks of ‍gestation. Which of the f​ollowing is specifically ‍indicated to provide symp‌tomatic relief in this pa‌tient?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old primiparous woman came to the OPD with complaints of nausea and vomiting at 8 weeks of gestation. Which of the following is specifically indicated to provide symptomatic relief in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Domperidone with doxylamin‍e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Doxylamine with pyridoxine‍","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dolasetron with pyridoxine‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dolasetron with metoclopra‍mide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Doxylamine with pyridoxine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Doxylamine with pyridoxine</strong></p><p><strong> Doxylamine </strong>is indicated with <strong>pyridoxine </strong>to <strong>reduce</strong> mild to moderate <strong>nausea</strong> and<strong> vomiting </strong>in the<strong> early stages of pregnancy. </strong></p>\n<p>It is a histamine <strong>H1 antagonist </strong>and its efficacy is improved when combined with pyridoxine.</p>\n<p>Combinations of domperidone, dolasetron and metoclopramide with pyridoxine are not specifically indicated in pregnancy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6572","text":"What is the drug of choice‍ in a patient complaining​ of nausea and vomiting, ‌2 days after commencement‍ of a cisplatin-based che​motherapeutic regimen?","raw_text":"What is the drug of choice in a patient complaining of nausea and vomiting, 2 days after commencement of a cisplatin-based chemotherapeutic regimen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5HT3 antagonist + NK-1 ant‍agonist","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"NK-1 antagonist + 5HT3 ant‍agonist + Corticosteroid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"NK-1 antagonist + Corticos‍teroid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5HT3 antagonist + Corticos‍teroid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. NK-1 antagonist + 5HT3 antagonist + Corticosteroid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. NK-1 antagonist + 5HT3 antagonist + Corticosteroid</strong></p><p data-canvas-width=\"283.57724808\">This patient is most likely suffering from <strong>delayed emesis</strong> associated with <strong>cancer chemotherapy.</strong></p>\n<p data-canvas-width=\"283.57724808\">Combined therapy with aprepitant (NK-1/ <strong>neurokinin-1 receptor antagonist</strong>), a <strong>5-HT<sub>3</sub>-receptor antagonist,</strong> and<strong> dexamethasone</strong> is the best treatment in delayed emesis associated with cancer chemotherapy.</p>\n<p data-canvas-width=\"283.57724808\"><strong>Delayed chemotherapy-induced vomiting</strong> is any emesis occurring<strong> &gt;24 hours</strong> <strong>after chemotherapy. </strong>If NK-1 receptor antagonists are not used in the combination, delayed emesis is prevented only in 30% of the patients. Usage of 5HT3 antagonists alone has no role in the treatment of delayed emesis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6573","text":"An AIDS patient presents w‍ith anorexia and weight l​oss. Which of the followi‌ng is a naturally extract‍ed cannabinoid that is ap​proved for his condition?‍","raw_text":"An AIDS patient presents with anorexia and weight loss. Which of the following is a naturally extracted cannabinoid that is approved for his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Drona‍binol","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Nabi‍lone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tibo‍lone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Canna‍bidiol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dronabinol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dronabinol</strong></p><p><strong>Dronabinol </strong>or delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinol is a <strong>cannabinoid </strong>that can be extracted<strong> naturally. </strong></p>\n<p>It is the only cannabinoid that is approved for <strong>anorexia</strong> and <strong>weight loss</strong> associated with <strong>AIDS.</strong> It is obtained from the marijuana plant or can be synthesized chemically.</p>\n<p>Option B:<strong> Nabilone</strong> is a synthetic cannabinoid. </p>\n<p>Dronabinol and nabilone are approved for the treatment of refractory cases of chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting.</p>\n<p>Option C: Tibolone is a 19-norsteroid derivative specifically to be used for hormone replacement therapy.</p>\n<p>Option D: Cannabidiol is a cannabinoid approved for the treatment of seizures associated with Lennox-Gastaut syndrome, Dravet syndrome, or tuberous sclerosis complex. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6575","text":"Which of the following sta‍tements about aprepitant ​is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about aprepitant is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Agonist at ‍NK1 receptor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Crosses blood brain barrie‍r","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ameliorate nausea and vomi‍ting induced by chemother​apy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metabolised by the CYP450 ‍enzymes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Agonist at NK1 receptor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Agonist at NK1 receptor</strong></p><p><strong>Aprepitant</strong> is a high-affinity<strong> NK<sub>1</sub> receptor antagonist</strong> that <strong>blocks</strong> the <strong>emetic action</strong> of <strong>substance</strong> <strong>P</strong>.</p>\n<p>Aprepitant is well absorbed orally and crosses the blood-brain barrier.</p>\n<p>Co-administration of oral aprepitant with dexamethasone and ondansetron significantly enhances the <strong>antiemetic efficacy against</strong> the highly emetogenic side effects of <strong>chemotherapeutic drugs</strong> like cisplatin and cyclophosphamide.</p>\n<p>It is metabolized in the liver by the CYP450 enzyme.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6576","text":"Which of the following ant‍iemetic drugs also decrea​ses acid secretion due to‌ its action on H1 recepto‍rs?","raw_text":"Which of the following antiemetic drugs also decreases acid secretion due to its action on H1 receptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Promet‍hazine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dompe‍ridone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Palano‍setron","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ondan‍setron","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Promethazine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Promethazine</strong></p><p>An <strong>antiemetic</strong> drug that also <strong>decreases acid secretion</strong> due to its action on <strong>H</strong><sub><strong>1</strong> </sub>histamine receptors is <strong>promethazine. </strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>central anticholinergic</strong> activity of promethazine is responsible for <strong>antiemetic</strong> action.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6577","text":"The 5HT3 antagonist having‍ maximum potency in chemo​therapy-induced vomiting ‌is:","raw_text":"The 5HT3 antagonist having maximum potency in chemotherapy-induced vomiting is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ondan‍setron","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Grani‍setron","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cilan‍setron","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Palono‍setron","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Palonosetron","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Palonosetron</strong></p><p>The <strong>5HT<sub>3</sub> antagonist </strong>having<strong> maximum potency </strong>in <strong>chemotherapy-induced vomiting </strong>is <strong>palonosetron.</strong></p>\n<p>It has a <strong>high affinity</strong> for 5HT<sub>3 </sub>and is also the <strong>longest-acting </strong>5HT<sub>3</sub> antagonist. This makes it more effective in<strong> delayed emesis</strong> associated with chemotherapy.</p>\n<p>During chemotherapy, there is cell lysis and 5HT (serotonin) is released from the gut mucosa. The released serotonin acts on the 5HT3 receptors, activating the vagal afferents in the gut that carry the emetogenic impulses to the nucleus tractus solitarius and chemoreceptor trigger zone. The selective blockade of 5HT3 receptors reduces the emetogenic impulses.</p>\n<p>Granisetron, dolasetron, and ondansetron are also effective for chemotherapy-induced vomiting.</p>\n<p>Cilansetron is a new 5HT3 antagonist indicated for irritable bowel syndrome with diarrhea.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7566","text":"Most effective antiemetic ‍for chemotherapy-induced ​vomiting is_________.","raw_text":"Most effective antiemetic for chemotherapy-induced vomiting is_________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dompe‍ridone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tega‍serod","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Grani‍setron","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Doxyl‍amine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Granisetron","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Granisetron</strong></p><p>Among the given options, the most effective antiemetic for <strong>chemotherapy-induced vomiting</strong> is <strong>granisetron. </strong></p>\n<p>Granisetron is a <strong>5HT3 receptor antagonist</strong>. They are the most effective drugs for the treatment of chemotherapy-induced and post-operative nausea and vomiting.</p>\n<p><strong>Palonosetron</strong> is a <strong>second-generation</strong> 5HT<sub>3</sub><sub> </sub>antagonist that has the <strong>highest affinity</strong> for 5HT3 receptors and has the <strong>maximum potency</strong>. It is also the <strong>longest acting</strong> that makes it effective in delayed emesis associated with chemotherapy. Palonosetron has minimal/no propensity to cause QT interval prolongation. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1353","text":"QT interval prolongation i‍s minimal with which of t​he following 5-HT3 antago‌nists?","raw_text":"QT interval prolongation is minimal with which of the following 5-HT3 antagonists?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ondan‍setron","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Palono‍setron","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dolas‍etron","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Grani‍setron","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Palonosetron","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Palonosetron</strong></p><p><strong>Palonosetron </strong>is a second-generation 5-HT3 antagonist and has <strong>minimal/no propensity</strong> to cause <strong>QT</strong><strong> prolongation. </strong></p>\n<p>First-generation 5-HT3 antagonists have an increased risk of QT interval prolongation.</p>\n<p>Intravenous dolasetron is contraindicated for prophylaxis of chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting for the same reason.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1356","text":"Which of the following NK1‍ receptor antagonists has​ the longest half-life?","raw_text":"Which of the following NK1 receptor antagonists has the longest half-life?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Netup‍itant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rolap‍itant","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aprep‍itant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fosapr‍epitant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rolapitant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rolapitant</strong></p><p><strong>Rolapitant</strong> has the <strong>longest plasma half-life</strong> of 180 hours. It is a <strong>highly selective NK1</strong> receptor <strong>antagonist.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9267","text":"A young male developed 5 e‍pisodes of loose stools 2​ hours after eating in a ‌restaurant. He is afebril‍e and has mild dehydratio​n. What should be the tre‍atment?","raw_text":"A young male developed 5 episodes of loose stools 2 hours after eating in a restaurant. He is afebrile and has mild dehydration. What should be the treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ciprofloxacin and tinidazo‍le","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Only cipr‍ofloxacin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Only‍ ORS","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ciprofloxacin, tinidazole ‍and ORS","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Only ORS","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Only ORS</strong></p><p>It is a case of <strong>acute diarrhea</strong> in an <strong>afebrile, </strong>stable patient which can be managed with<strong> ORS</strong> <strong>alone</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Management</strong> of <strong>diarrhea:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mild-moderate dehydration - <strong>ORS.</strong></li>\n<li>Severe dehydration- <strong>IV fluids</strong> are given.</li>\n<li><strong>Afebrile</strong> patient - <strong>No antibiotics</strong> are given.</li>\n<li><strong>Febrile</strong> patient - <strong>empirical antibiotics.</strong> If only bacterial etiology is suspected, ciprofloxacin is given. If giardiasis is suspected, metronidazole or tinidazole are recommended.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7318","text":"You and your friend plan f‍or a mountain trip for th​e day after. What medicat‌ion would you advise him ‍to help with his motion s​ickness?","raw_text":"You and your friend plan for a mountain trip for the day after. What medication would you advise him to help with his motion sickness?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ranitidine tablet before s‍tart of trip","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dimenhydrinate before star‍t of trip","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scopolamine patch the nigh‍t before trip","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Omeprazole the night befor‍e and before start of tri​p","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Scopolamine patch the night before trip","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Scopolamine patch the night before trip</strong></p><p>In the above case, a <strong>scopolamine patch</strong> the <strong>night before</strong> the trip should be advised.</p>\n<p>Treatment for motion sickness :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Scopolamine patch- </strong>preferred, most effective.\n<ul>\n<li>Do not give to children &lt;10 years of age.</li>\n<li>Place patch behind the ears <strong>at least 4 hours before travel</strong> and replace every 72 hours as needed.</li>\n<li>Transdermal patch – Most effective</li>\n<li>Oral dose - 0.4–0.6 mg one hour before travel and then TID may be recommended.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Cinnarizine </strong>– 30mg, 2 hours prior to travel, and 15mg TID as required. Not as effective as scopolamine. Moderately effective.</li>\n<li><strong>Promethazine</strong></li>\n<li>Other drugs (minimally effective): \n<ul>\n<li>Cyclizine</li>\n<li><strong>Dimenhydrinate</strong></li>\n<li>Diphenhydramine</li>\n<li>Meclizine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>PPI’s / ranitidine / traditional ginger – all are ineffective.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"XYlpTbwC","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"64_genetics_and_health","title":"64.Genetics And Health","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MA2731","text":"A 42-year-old male prisone‍r is being evaluated for ​aggressive tendencies and​ antisocial behavior. Whi​ch of the following genot‍ypes is associated with t‍hese symptoms?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old male prisoner is being evaluated for aggressive tendencies and antisocial behavior. Which of the following genotypes is associated with these symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X‍XY","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"X‍O","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X‍XX","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"X‍YY","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. XYY","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. XYY</strong></p><p><strong>XYY</strong> genotype is often associated with <strong>aggressive </strong>tendencies, <strong>antisocial</strong>, and often,<strong> criminal behavior.</strong> It is called <strong>Jacob’s syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>XXY</strong> genotype is associated with <strong>Klinefelter's</strong> syndrome.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>XO</strong> genotype is associated with <strong>Turner's</strong> syndrome.</p>\n<p>Option C: Triple X syndrome is associated with genotype <strong>XXX</strong>. It is also called <strong>super female</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2732","text":"What is the risk of develo‍ping Down's syndrome in a​ baby born to a 45-year-o​ld woman?","raw_text":"What is the risk of developing Down's syndrome in a baby born to a 45-year-old woman?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 in‍ 1000","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 i‍n 50","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1 in‍ 3000","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 in‍ 100","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1 in 50","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1 in 50</strong></p><p>The risk of<strong> </strong>developing <strong>Down’s syndrome</strong> in a baby born to a <strong>45-year-old</strong> woman is<strong> 1 in 50.</strong></p>\n<p>The risk of developing of Down's syndrome in a baby born to a <strong>20-year-old</strong> woman is around <strong>1 in 3000</strong> and it increases with rising maternal age. It is an autosomal disorder caused by <strong>trisomy</strong> of<strong> chromosome 21</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2734","text":"Which of the following con‍ditions is not autosomal ​dominant?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is not autosomal dominant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurofib‍romatosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Retinob‍lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Marfan’s‍ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ataxia tel‍engiectasia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ataxia telengiectasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ataxia telengiectasia</strong></p><p><strong>Ataxia-telangiectasia</strong> or Louis-Bar syndrome is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> condition.</p>\n<p><strong>Autosomal dominant</strong> conditions include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neurofibromatosis</li>\n<li>Marfan’s syndrome</li>\n<li>Retinoblastoma</li>\n<li>Huntington's chorea</li>\n<li>Polycystic kidney disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2736","text":"Which of the following is ‍incorrect regarding autos​omal recessive disorders?​","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding autosomal recessive disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Both male and female offsp‍ring are affected","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Heterozygotes are not affe‍cted","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Most inborn errors of meta‍bolism have this form of ​inheritance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It affects more people at ‍a later age than in child​hood","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It affects more people at a later age than in childhood","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It affects more people at a later age than in childhood</strong></p><p><strong>Autosomal recessive</strong> disorders usually manifest in <strong>childhood</strong> or earlier. </p>\n<p><strong>Heterozygotes</strong> are <strong>unaffected</strong> by the disease. When both parents are heterozygous, each offspring (<strong>both male and female</strong>) has a <strong>1:4 chance</strong> of being affected. Examples of autosomal recessive disorders include several<strong> inborn errors</strong> of metabolism like phenylketonuria, cystic fibrosis, and galactosemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2737","text":"A 6-year-old boy with recu‍rrent hemarthrosis was fo​und to have deficiency of​ factor VIII. He was adop​ted and his family histor‍y is unknown. What is the‍ inheritance pattern of t​his condition?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old boy with recurrent hemarthrosis was found to have deficiency of factor VIII. He was adopted and his family history is unknown. What is the inheritance pattern of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"X-linked ‍recessive","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal‍ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X-linked‍ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Autosomal‍ recessive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. X-linked recessive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. X-linked recessive</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hemophilia A.</strong> It is an <strong>X-linked recessive</strong> disorder.</p>\n<p>Hemophilia is of two types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hemophilia A</strong> - due to mutations in the<strong> F8</strong> gene leading to <strong>factor VIII </strong>deficiency </li>\n<li><strong>Hemophilia B</strong> - due to mutations in the <strong>F9</strong> gene leading to<strong> factor IX </strong>deficiency</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"p1\">Affected children often present with prolonged bleeding after procedures like circumcision or major bleeds like intracranial hemorrhages. When the child begins to walk, bleeding into the joint (<strong>hemarthrosis</strong>) may occur, causing painful swellings and reddening. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Laboratory investigations show <strong>elevated aPTT,</strong> with normal PT and platelet count. It is treated by the administration of <strong>factor VIII</strong> or <strong>factor IX concentrates.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2739","text":"A pathologist is examining‍ the peripheral smear of ​a patient with anemia. Wh​ich of the following is i​ncorrect about the epidem‍iology of this disease?","raw_text":"A pathologist is examining the peripheral smear of a patient with anemia. Which of the following is incorrect about the epidemiology of this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is prevalent in areas w‍ith high frequency of mal​aria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is an autosomal dominan‍t disorder","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Couples at risk can underg‍o prenatal testing and di​agnosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It manifests during the fi‍rst year of life","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is an autosomal dominant disorder","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bab8781846044f4a8f3b90372cbb387f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is an autosomal dominant disorder</strong></p><p>The given peripheral smear shows sickling of RBCs, suggestive of <strong>sickle cell anemia.</strong> It is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> disorder. </p>\n<p>The prevalence is high among Africans. This disorder usually has its onset during the first year of life. Couples at risk can opt for prenatal testing and diagnosis as well.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2740","text":"What was the primary goal ‍of the Human Genome Proje​ct?","raw_text":"What was the primary goal of the Human Genome Project?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"To introduce a gene sequen‍ce into a cell","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"To develop new diagnostic ‍techniques for genetic di​sorders","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"To determine the sequence ‍of nucleotide base pairs ​in human DNA","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"To study the precise genet‍ic composition of specifi​c populations","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. To determine the sequence of nucleotide base pairs in human DNA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. To determine the sequence of nucleotide base pairs in human DNA</strong></p><p>The primary goal of the <strong>Human Genome Project</strong> was to determine the <strong>sequence of nucleotide base pairs</strong> that make up human DNA and to <strong>map</strong> <strong>all genes</strong> from the human genome (20,500 genes).</p>\n<p>The project was launched in <strong>1990 </strong>and deemed complete in <strong>2003.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2742","text":"What is Hardy-Weinberg law‍ related to?","raw_text":"What is Hardy-Weinberg law related to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Populatio‍n genetics","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Behavioura‍l genetics","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ecologica‍l genetics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Development‍al genetics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Population genetics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Population genetics</strong></p><p>The <strong>Hardy-Weinberg law</strong> is related to <strong>population genetics</strong>. Population genetics is the study of<strong> </strong>the precise genetic composition of a population and various factors determining the incidence of inherited traits in them. </p>\n<p><strong>Founder effect</strong> is a violation to this law. It is due to <strong>difference</strong> in expression of the genes in founding population. This is responsible for the difference in the distribution of disease in population.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2744","text":"The Hardy-Weinberg law is ‍applicable in populations​ with which of the follow​ing?","raw_text":"The Hardy-Weinberg law is applicable in populations with which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Migr‍ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Small po‍pulation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Random‍ mating","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gene o‍utflow","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Random mating","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Random mating</strong></p><p>The <strong>Hardy-Weinberg</strong> <strong>law</strong> is <strong>applicable</strong> for infinitely large, static populations with <strong>random mating.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2745","text":"Match the following. Term ‍Definition 1) Positive eu​genics a) Environmental m​anipulation to enable gen​es to express themselves ‍2) Negative eugenics b) M‍aking phenotypic improvem​ents to humans after birt‍h 3) Euthenics c) Encoura​ging the breeding of thos​e with desirable phenotyp​es 4) Euphenics d) Discou​raging the breeding of th‌ose with undesirable phen​otypes","raw_text":"<p>Match the following.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 5px; width: 554px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\"><strong>Term</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 329px;\"><strong>Definition</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">1) Positive eugenics     </td>\n<td style=\"width: 329px;\">a) Environmental manipulation to enable genes to express themselves</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">2) Negative eugenics    </td>\n<td style=\"width: 329px;\">b) Making phenotypic improvements to humans after birth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">3) Euthenics</td>\n<td style=\"width: 329px;\">c) Encouraging the breeding of those with desirable phenotypes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">4) Euphenics</td>\n<td style=\"width: 329px;\">d) Discouraging the breeding of those with undesirable phenotypes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-d, 2-c,‍ 3-b, 4-a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-c, 2-d,‍ 3-a, 4-b","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-b, 2-a,‍ 3-d, 4-c","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-a, 2-b,‍ 3-c, 4-d","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b</strong></p><p><strong>Positive eugenics</strong> encourages the breeding of those with <strong>desirable</strong> phenotypes.</p>\n<p><strong>Negative eugenics</strong> discourages the breeding of those with <strong>undesirable</strong> phenotypes.</p>\n<p><strong>Euthenics</strong> is the environmental manipulation to enable genes to express themselves.</p>\n<p><strong>Euphenics</strong> is the science of making phenotypic improvements in humans after birth, generally to affect a problematic genetic condition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2747","text":"A young woman with a famil‍y history of beta-thalass​emia is planning to marry​ her first cousin. Which ​of the following will you‍ recommend for this woman‍?","raw_text":"A young woman with a family history of beta-thalassemia is planning to marry her first cousin. Which of the following will you recommend for this woman?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retrospective genetic coun‍seling","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amnioc‍entesis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Positive‍ eugenics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prospective genetic counse‍ling","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Prospective genetic counseling","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prospective genetic counseling</strong></p><p>This young woman must be advised to undergo <strong>p</strong><strong>rospective counseling.</strong></p>\n<p>It refers to the process explaining to heterozygous individuals the risk of having affected children if they marry another heterozygous individual. It is performed <strong>prior to conception</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Retrospective</strong> counseling is performed after the genetic disorder has <strong>already occurred</strong> in the first child. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2748","text":"A 40-year-old pregnant wom‍an is planned for amnioce​ntesis. What is the gesta​tional age at which it ca​n be done at the earliest‍?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old pregnant woman is planned for amniocentesis. What is the gestational age at which it can be done at the earliest?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10th week o‍f gestation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"14th week o‍f gestation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"20th week o‍f gestation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"18th week o‍f gestation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 14th week of gestation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 14th week of gestation</strong></p><p><strong>Amniocentesis</strong> can be done as early as <strong>14 weeks </strong>of gestation.</p>\n<p>It refers to the examination of a sample of amniotic fluid to diagnose chromosomal disorders and some metabolic errors. It is indicated in the following cases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pregnant women aged <strong>35 years or above</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Family history</strong> of:</li>\n<ul>\n<li>Chromosomal abnormalities</li>\n<li>Metabolic errors</li>\n<li>Chromosomal translocation</li>\n<li>Sex-linked genetic disease</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2749","text":"What is a Punnett square u‍sed for?","raw_text":"What is a Punnett square used for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Random ‍sampling","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Finding genotype of offspr‍ing","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Populatio‍n genetics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Quantifyin‍g mutations","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Finding genotype of offspring","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bc07b3996273468fa49505fa8cc8068ex406x392.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Finding genotype of offspring</strong></p><p>A<strong> Punnett square</strong> is a diagram used to predict the <strong>probability</strong> of a particular <strong>genotype</strong> in an offspring.</p>\n<p>It is a standard tool for <strong>genetic counselors. </strong>The image given below shows a Punnett square.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7463","text":"The risk of genetic diseas‍es in consanguineous marr​iage between first cousin​s is?","raw_text":"The risk of genetic diseases in consanguineous marriage between first cousins is?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-‍2%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4-‍8%","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"8-‍10%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"12-‍14%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 4-8%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 4-8%</strong></p><p>The <strong>risk</strong> of <strong>genetic diseases</strong> in <strong>consanguineous</strong> marriage between <strong>first cousins</strong> is <strong>4-8%</strong>.</p>\n<p>The risk of genetic diseases in <strong>non-consanguineous</strong> marriage is <strong>2-4%</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ZaB3tUWm","path":["Community Medicine"]},{"id":"64_ischemic_heart_disease___presentation_and_diagnosis","title":"64.Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA9483","text":"Which of the following art​eries is most commonly in‌volved in patients with c‍oronary artery disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following arteries is most commonly involved in patients with coronary artery disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Right coro​nary artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Left anterior descending c​oronary artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Left circumflex coronary a​rtery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Left main co​ronary artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Left anterior descending coronary artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f161dceb366041b484881ba69aa07ac9x1280x1757.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Left anterior descending coronary artery</strong></p><p>The<strong> left anterior descending</strong> (anterior interventricular) coronary artery is most commonly involved in <strong>coronary artery disease </strong>(CAD) or coronary heart disease or ischemic heart disease.</p>\n<p>The regions affected are the <strong>anterior wall</strong> of the left ventricle near the apex and the anterior portion of the <strong>ventricular septum.</strong></p>\n<p>CAD is caused by plaque buildup in the walls of the coronary arteries that narrow over time and can partially or totally block the blood flow causing myocardial ischemia and subsequently infarction. </p>\n<p>The image below shows coronary circulation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9488","text":"Which area of the myocardi​um is most vulnerable to ‌damage in patients with i‍schemic heart disease?","raw_text":"Which area of the myocardium is most vulnerable to damage in patients with ischemic heart disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subepi​cardium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subendo​cardium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Peric​ardium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Midmyo​cardium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Subendocardium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ae8efadea5c945288ec0f801b131133ax600x1787.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Subendocardium</strong></p><p><strong>Subendocardium</strong> is the region of the myocardium <strong>most vulnerable</strong> to <strong>ischemic damage.</strong></p>\n<p>The myocardium is perfused from the epicardium to the endocardium. This results in the <strong>subendocardium</strong> being the <strong>least</strong> <strong>perfused</strong> region making it most vulnerable to ischemic damage.</p>\n<p>A narrow zone of the myocardium immediately beneath the endocardium is spared from necrosis because it can be oxygenated by diffusion from the ventricle.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9490","text":"A patient presented to the​ emergency with symptoms ‌of unstable angina. ECG d‍one in pain-free interval​ showed biphasic T waves ‌in precordial leads witho​ut ST changes. Cardiac tr‌oponins were normal. Coro‍nary angiography revealed‍ stenosis of the proximal​ left anterior descending​ artery. What is the like‍ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the emergency with symptoms of unstable angina. ECG done in pain-free interval showed biphasic T waves in precordial leads without ST changes. Cardiac troponins were normal. Coronary angiography revealed stenosis of the proximal left anterior descending artery. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Broken hea​rt syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wellens ​syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Barlow ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Brugada ​syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Wellens syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ab34cfb7ac014017bb5a64f2edb92c38x1280x623.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Wellens syndrome</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>Wellens syndrome</strong>. It is alternatively known as anterior, descending, T-wave syndrome. The ECG pattern of Wellens syndrome is relatively common in patients who exhibit symptoms consistent with <strong>unstable angina. </strong></p>\n<p>ECG in the pain-free interval is characterized by deeply <strong>inverted</strong> or <strong>biphasic</strong> <strong>T</strong> <strong>waves</strong> in precordial leads and with minimal or no ST elevations. Cardiac enzymes are normal or mildly elevated.</p>\n<p>These patients are at <strong>high risk</strong> for impending large anterior wall acute <strong>myocardial infarction.</strong></p>\n<p>The ECG below shows biphasic T waves in leads V2 and V3 and inverted T waves in leads V4 and V5 in Wellens syndrome. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Broken heart syndrome is another name for Takotsubo cardiomyopathy.</p>\n<p>Option C: Barlow syndrome is associated with mitral valve prolapse.</p>\n<p>Option D: Brugada syndrome is a genetic cardiac rhythm disorder.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9492","text":"In a patient with suspecte​d stable angina, which of‌ the following findings a‍re likely to be present?","raw_text":"In a patient with suspected stable angina, which of the following findings are likely to be present?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CK-MB is elevated ; Tropon​in T is normal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Troponin I is elevated ; M​yoglobin is normal","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myoglobin is elevated ; CK​-MB is normal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Troponin I is normal ; CK-​MB is normal","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Troponin I is normal ; CK-MB is normal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Troponin I is normal ; CK-MB is normal</strong></p><p>The levels of <strong>cardiac</strong> <strong>markers</strong> remain <strong>unchanged</strong> in <strong>stable </strong>and <strong>unstable angina</strong> as there is no necrosis of cardiac myocytes.</p>\n<p>The clinical presentations of ischemic heart disease can be broadly classified into: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chronic coronary syndrome</strong> (CCS) - this includes\n<ul>\n<li>Stable angina - this is an episodic angina that occurs with predictable severity, frequency, and threshold of precipitation by stressors, and is relieved by rest and nitrates  </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Acute coronary syndrome</strong> (ACS) - this includes\n<ul>\n<li>Unstable angina - this is diagnosed in patients with symptoms suggestive of an ACS with <strong>no elevation</strong> of<strong> biomarkers</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Myocardial infarction </strong>- it is the necrosis and death of a portion of the myocardium due to prolonged ischemia. The cardiac <strong>biomarkers </strong>are <strong>elevated</strong> in this condition</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9494","text":"While evaluating a patient​ with chest pain in the e‌mergency, you ask him to ‍localize the sensation. H​e places a hand over the ‌sternum with a clenched f​ist. What is this sign ca‌lled?","raw_text":"While evaluating a patient with chest pain in the emergency, you ask him to localize the sensation. He places a hand over the sternum with a clenched fist. What is this sign called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mayne’​s sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Levine​’s sign","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Frank’​s sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Freidrie​ch’s sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Levine’s sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Levine’s sign</strong></p><p>When a patient with <strong>angina</strong> is asked to localize the sensation, he or she typically places a hand over the sternum, sometimes with a clenched fist, to indicate a squeezing, central, <strong>substernal discomfort.</strong> This is called <strong>Levine’s sign.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Mayne's sign-</strong> Drop of at least 15 mmHg in the diastolic blood pressure<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blood_pressure\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"></a> on raising the arm. It occurs in patients with aortic regurgitation (not reliable).</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Frank’s sign-</strong> Diagonal crease in the ear lobe indicative of cardiovascular diseases and/or diabetes.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Friedreich's sign-</strong> Exaggerated drop in JVP seen in constrictive pericarditis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9496","text":"A 60-year-old woman presen​ts with chest discomfort,‌ associated with a squeez‍ing sensation in the subs​ternal area. She added th‌at it was precipitated by​ physical activity and re‌lieved on taking rest. Wh‍ich of the following is a‍ feature of this conditio​n?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman presents with chest discomfort, associated with a squeezing sensation in the substernal area. She added that it was precipitated by physical activity and relieved on taking rest. Which of the following is a feature of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pain radiating to the trap​ezius","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pain lasting more than 20 ​minutes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pain localized above the m​andible","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Crescendo decrescendo natu​re of pain","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Crescendo decrescendo nature of pain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e3a547b20e784f779102e5b75a51dc8bx1280x1867.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Crescendo decrescendo nature of pain</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>stable</strong> <strong>angina</strong> in which the pain is usually <strong>crescendo-decrescendo</strong> in nature.</p>\n<p>Characteristic features of pain in stable angina:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Squeezing, central, substernal discomfort</li>\n<li>Crescendo-decrescendo in nature</li>\n<li>Typically lasts 2 to 5 min</li>\n<li>Can radiate occasionally to neck, jaw, epigastrium, shoulders, left arm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Angina is rarely localized below the umbilicus or above the mandible. Myocardial ischemic discomfort does not radiate to the <strong>trapezius</strong> muscles, that radiation pattern is more typical of <strong>pericarditis.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 228px;\" width=\"407\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 26px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; height: 26px;\">Features</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; height: 26px;\">Stable angina</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; height: 26px;\">Unstable angina</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 85px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; height: 85px;\">Pain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; height: 85px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Squeezing, central, substernal discomfort.</li>\n<li>Crescendo-decrescendo pattern.</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Precipitated by exercise, cold weather, or emotional stress duration of 2-5 min.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; height: 85px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Same as stable angina but may be more severe.</li>\n<li>Crescendo pattern.</li>\n<li>Pain occurs at rest and may last up to 20 min.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 85px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; height: 85px;\">Pathology</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; height: 85px;\">\n<p>Ischemia due to fixed stenosis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; height: 85px;\">\n<p>Unstable plaques causing dynamic obstruction</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 85px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; height: 85px;\">Predictability</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; height: 85px;\">\n<p>Predictable</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; height: 85px;\">\n<p>Unpredictable</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 85px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; height: 85px;\">Diagnosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; height: 85px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>ECG during exercise</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; height: 85px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>ECG during exercise contraindicated</li>\n<li>Coronary angiography recommended</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 85px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; height: 85px;\">ECG</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; height: 85px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>ECG at rest: normal</li>\n<li>ECG in exercise: ST-segment depression and ischemic T-waves</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 195.5px; height: 85px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>ECG at rest: ST-segment depression and ischemic T-waves</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9508","text":"A patient presented with a​ngina and ECG showed ST-s‌egment elevation. The pul‍se rate was found to be 5​4 beats/min and blood pre‌ssure was 98/56 mm Hg. Wh​at is the most likely dia‌gnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presented with angina and ECG showed ST-segment elevation. The pulse rate was found to be 54 beats/min and blood pressure was 98/56 mm Hg. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior​ wall MI","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterio​r wall MI","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inferior​ wall MI","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anterosept​al wall MI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inferior wall MI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inferior wall MI</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of<strong> STEMI </strong>with<strong> bradycardia</strong> which is a feature of <strong>inferior</strong> <strong>wall</strong> myocardial infarction. Up to one-half with <strong>inferior infarction</strong> show evidence of parasympathetic hyperactivity <strong>(bradycardia and/or hypotension).</strong></p>\n<p>Many patients have a normal pulse rate and blood pressure within the first hour of STEMI.</p>\n<p>About one-fourth of patients with <strong>anterior infarction</strong> have manifestations of sympathetic nervous system hyperactivity (<strong>tachycardia and/or hypertension</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9509","text":"A patient was admitted wit​h acute MI. During a dail‌y examination, a transien‍t murmur was heard. What ​is the most likely cause ‌for this finding?","raw_text":"A patient was admitted with acute MI. During a daily examination, a transient murmur was heard. What is the most likely cause for this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitral ​stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mitral reg​urgitation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aortic ​stenosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aortic reg​urgitation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mitral regurgitation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mitral regurgitation</strong></p><p>A transient <strong>midsystolic</strong> or<strong> late</strong> systolic apical<strong> murmur </strong>due to<strong> mitral regurgitation </strong>secondary to dysfunction of the mitral valve apparatus (papillary muscle dysfunction, LV dilation) may be present in STEMI.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9511","text":"Which of the following car​diac biomarkers is the ea‌rliest to rise after acut‍e myocardial infarction?","raw_text":"Which of the following cardiac biomarkers is the earliest to rise after acute myocardial infarction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Creatin​e kinase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myog​lobin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tropo​nin T","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tropo​nin I","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Myoglobin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Myoglobin</strong></p><p><strong>Myoglobin </strong>is the <strong>earliest to rise </strong>in acute MI followed by troponin, CK-MB and then LDH.</p>\n<p>Serum myoglobin and heart-type fatty acid-binding protein are smaller molecules that diffuse through interstitial ﬂuids more rapidly after cell death than the larger CK and troponin molecules. They become abnormal as early as<strong> 30 minutes after myocardial injury. </strong>Neither molecule is specific to myocardial tissue. Myoglobin levels are not tested in routine practice.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9512","text":"A patient is under evaluat​ion for acute MI. Which o‌f the following biomarker‍s would you prefer to tes​t for?","raw_text":"A patient is under evaluation for acute MI. Which of the following biomarkers would you prefer to test for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CK​-MB","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myog​lobin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac t​roponin-T","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cardiac t​roponin-I","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cardiac troponin-I","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cardiac troponin-I</strong></p><p>The most preferred cardiac biomarker for myocardial injury/<strong>acute MI</strong> amongst the given options is <strong>cardiac troponin-I.</strong></p>\n<p>Cardiac troponin-I, CK-MB, and myoglobin are significantly more specific for acute-MI than cardiac troponin-T. </p>\n<p>The cardiac troponins are particularly valuable when there is clinical suspicion of a small MI that may be below the detection limit for CK-MB measurement. They have replaced CK-MB as the <strong>biomarker of choice</strong> for diagnosing myocardial infarction.</p>\n<p>Cardiac troponins:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most specific cardiac biomarker for myocardial injury/acute MI</li>\n<li>Elevations are useful for short &amp; long-term prognoses in MI</li>\n<li>Elevations correlate with the estimation of <strong>infarct size </strong>and<strong> risk of death</strong></li>\n<li>Newer highly sensitive assays for troponins become positive within the first 3 hours after onset of chest pain in MI</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9513","text":"Elevated cardiac troponins​ may not be seen in which‌ of the following patient‍s?","raw_text":"Elevated cardiac troponins may not be seen in which of the following patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 24-year-old male with my​ocarditis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 65-year-old woman suffer​ing from Takotsubo cardio‌myopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A patient who underwent ca​rdioversion for VT in the‌ emergency room","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 48-year-old man diagnose​d with angina","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A 48-year-old man diagnosed with angina","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A 48-year-old man diagnosed with angina</strong></p><p>Troponins are <strong>not elevated</strong> in patients suffering from<strong> angina</strong>. Troponins are elevated in the case of myocardial necrosis (seen in MI) whereas angina is associated with myocardial insufficiency/ischemia. </p>\n<p>Conditions associated with an elevation in cardiac troponins:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>MI</li>\n<li><strong>Myocarditis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Takotsubo cardiomyopathy</strong></li>\n<li>Myocardial contusion</li>\n<li><strong>Cardioversion/defibrillation</strong></li>\n<li>LV strain from CHF</li>\n<li>Hypertensive crisis</li>\n<li>RV strain from PE</li>\n<li>Renal disease</li>\n<li>Sepsis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9514","text":"Which of the following bio​markers is preferred for ‌diagnosing reinfarction i‍n a patient post-NSTEMI?","raw_text":"Which of the following biomarkers is preferred for diagnosing reinfarction in a patient post-NSTEMI?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trop​onins","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Myog​lobin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"L​DH","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CK​-MB","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Troponins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Troponins</strong></p><p><strong>Troponin</strong>(serial) is the <strong>preferred</strong> biomarker for diagnosing <strong>reinfarction</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Serial troponin</strong> levels are used to detect reinfarction, an acute MI that occurs within <strong>28 days</strong> of an incident or recurrent MI. </p>\n<p>In case of suspected reinfarction, an immediate measurement of cardiac troponin followed by a <strong>second</strong> sample after 3-6 hours. <strong>≥20 </strong>percent rise in the second sample suggests reinfarction.<br/><br/><br/></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9516","text":"Which of the following is ​likely to be seen in a pa‌tient diagnosed with acut‍e myocardial infarction?","raw_text":"Which of the following is likely to be seen in a patient diagnosed with acute myocardial infarction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ratio of LDH 1 / LDH 2 &amp;lt​; 1 in plasma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ratio of LDH 1 / LDH 2 &amp;gt​; 1 in plasma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ratio of LDH 1 / LDH 4 &amp;lt​; 1 in plasma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ratio of LDH 2 / LDH 1 &amp;gt​; 1 in plasma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ratio of LDH 1 / LDH 2 > 1 in plasma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ratio of LDH 1 / LDH 2 &gt; 1 in plasma</strong></p><p>In <strong>myocardial injury</strong>, the LDH-1 fraction increases, and the ratio of<strong> LDH-1 </strong>to<strong> LDH-2 </strong>becomes<strong> &gt;1 </strong>in the plasma. This is called <strong>flipped LDH pattern.</strong></p>\n<p>Normally, the LDH-2 fraction is greater than the LDH-1 fraction in the plasma, and therefore, the ratio of LDH-1 to LDH-2 is <strong>normally &lt;1</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9518","text":"What is the maximum predic​ted heart rate in a 50-ye‌ar-old gentleman on exerc‍ise tolerance test?","raw_text":"What is the maximum predicted heart rate in a 50-year-old gentleman on exercise tolerance test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"150​ bpm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"170​ bpm","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"200​ bpm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"160​ bpm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 170 bpm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 170 bpm</strong></p><p>Maximum predicted heart rate on an exercise tolerance test (treadmill test) is given by: <strong>HRmax = 220 – Age</strong></p>\n<p>So in a 50-year-old male, the maximum predicted heart rate is 220 - 50 = <strong>170 bpm.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9527","text":"Which of the following is ​not a contraindication fo‌r performing an exercise ‍tolerance test?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a contraindication for performing an exercise tolerance test?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unstabl​e angina","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Symptomatic aortic stenosi​s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Active en​docarditis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AAA with transverse diamet​er of 5.5 cm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. AAA with transverse diameter of 5.5 cm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. AAA with transverse diameter of 5.5 cm</strong></p><p>Treadmill exercise testing <strong>(treadmill test)</strong> may be <strong>safely</strong> performed in patients with an <strong>abdominal aortic aneurysm.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Absolute contraindications</strong> to exercise testing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute myocardial infarction (within 2 days)</li>\n<li>High-risk unstable angina</li>\n<li>Uncontrolled cardiac arrhythmia with hemodynamic compromise</li>\n<li>Active endocarditis</li>\n<li>Symptomatic severe aortic stenosis</li>\n<li>Decompensated heart failure</li>\n<li>Acute pulmonary embolism or pulmonary infarction</li>\n<li>Acute myocarditis or pericarditis</li>\n<li>Severe pulmonary hypertension</li>\n<li>Aortic dissection</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Relative Contraindications</strong> to exercise testing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Known left main coronary artery stenosis</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Moderate aortic stenosis with uncertain relation to symptoms</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Tachyarrhythmias with uncontrolled ventricular rates</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Acquired complete heart block</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with severe resting gradient</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Mental impairment with limited ability to cooperate</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9536","text":"Which of the following dru​gs cannot be used for a p‌atient undergoing stress ‍echocardiography?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs cannot be used for a patient undergoing stress echocardiography?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dobut​amine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dipyri​damole","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Regad​enoson","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phenyl​ephrine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Phenylephrine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Phenylephrine</strong></p><p><strong>Phenylephrine</strong>, an alpha-receptor agonist, cannot be used for a patient undergoing<strong> stress echocardiography </strong>as it causes<strong> vasoconstriction. <br/></strong><strong><br/>Dobutamine</strong> (preferred agent)<strong> </strong>and<strong> vasodilators (dipyridamole, adenosine, regadenoson) </strong>are used for pharmacological stress echocardiography. </p>\n<p>Pharmacologic stress testing is used in those who cannot exercise because of pulmonary veno-occlusive disease, musculoskeletal disease, or exertional dyspnea.</p>\n<p>Stress echocardiography can be done using either exercise (e.g.: treadmill test or bicycle test) or pharmacologic agents.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9537","text":"Which of the following fin​dings during an exercise ‌tolerance test is not ass‍ociated with multivessel ​coronary artery disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following findings during an exercise tolerance test is not associated with multivessel coronary artery disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Early onset of ST depressi​on","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Persistence of ST segment ​changes late into recover‌y phase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Failure to increase systol​ic BP by at least 20mmHg","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"ST segment elevation in aV​R","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Failure to increase systolic BP by at least 20mmHg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Failure to increase systolic BP by at least 20mmHg</strong></p><p>A <strong>failure </strong>to<strong> increase systolic </strong>blood pressure by<strong> 10 mm Hg</strong>, or a sustained decrease in systolic blood pressure of 10 mm Hg or more, is suggestive of <strong>multivessel (or left main) CAD.</strong></p>\n<p>Other abnormalities on exercise testing that are suggestive of multivessel CAD include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Early-onset of ischemic ST-segment depression</li>\n<li>ST-segment depression of ≥2 mm (0.2mV) involving 5 or more leads, or persisting ≥5 minutes into recovery</li>\n<li>Exercise-induced ST-segment elevation is also consistent with multivessel CAD</li>\n<li>Recent evidence suggests that ST-segment elevation of ≥1 mm in lead aVR may be a significant predictor of left main coronary artery disease, proximal left anterior descending (LAD) artery disease, or at least multivessel CAD</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An exercise tolerance/treadmill test involves walking in place on a treadmill while monitoring the electrical activity of your heart. Throughout the test, the speed and incline of the treadmill increase. The results show how well your heart responds to the stress of different levels of exercise.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9538","text":"Which of the following com​pounds is used for myocar‌dial perfusion imaging?","raw_text":"Which of the following compounds is used for myocardial perfusion imaging?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iodin​e-123","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Iridi​um-192","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thalli​um-201","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Galli​um-67","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thallium-201","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thallium-201</strong></p><p><strong>Myocardial perfusion imaging</strong> is done with either <strong>thallium-201 or technetium-99m</strong> labeled compounds (e.g., technetium-99m sestamibi).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9543","text":"A patient is being conside​red for CABG in order to ‌achieve coronary revascul‍arization. Which of the f​ollowing test will not be‌ useful to assess myocard​ial viability for predict‌ing the risk of periopera‍tive morbidity?","raw_text":"A patient is being considered for CABG in order to achieve coronary revascularization. Which of the following test will not be useful to assess myocardial viability for predicting the risk of perioperative morbidity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thallium 2​01 imaging","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardi​ac MRI","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"P​ET","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C​T","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CT","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CT</strong></p><p>Modalities for the determination of <strong>myocardial viability</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thallium-201 imaging</li>\n<li>MRI</li>\n<li>Dobutamine echocardiography</li>\n<li>PET scan (<strong>investigation of choice</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The<strong> investigation of choice </strong>for assessing the<strong> ventricular function </strong>is <strong>cardiac MRI.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9554","text":"Which questionnaire is use​d for the assessment of a‌ patient who presented wi‍th symptoms of angina?","raw_text":"Which questionnaire is used for the assessment of a patient who presented with symptoms of angina?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ROSE ques​tionnaire","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"IPSS ques​tionnaire","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CAGE ques​tionnaire","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"YTS ques​tionnaire","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. ROSE questionnaire","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. ROSE questionnaire</strong></p><p>WHO <strong>Rose questionnaire</strong> is used for the assessment of <strong>angina</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: International prostate symptom score (IPSS) questionnaire is used for BPH.</p>\n<p>Option C: The CAGE questionnaire is used to check for signs of possible alcohol dependency.</p>\n<p>Option D: The youth tobacco survey (YTS) questionnaire is used to study the prevalence of use of tobacco products amongst youth.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1360","text":"Which of the following ECG​ findings is first to app‌ear in a patient suspecte‍d of acute myocardial inf​arction?","raw_text":"Which of the following ECG findings is first to appear in a patient suspected of acute myocardial infarction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elevation​ of S wave","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prolonged ​QT interval","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tall T​ waves","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Prolonged ​PR interval","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tall T waves","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a44fb1227fbd427cabbc10861da7405dx600x1321.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tall T waves</strong></p><p>During acute myocardial infarction, the ECG evolves through 3 stages:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>T-wave peaking and T-wave inversion:</strong> \n<ul style=\"list-style-type: disc;\">\n<li><strong>Peaked T-wave</strong> aka hyperacute T-wave- <strong>first ECG change</strong> (occurs within minutes)</li>\n<li>If the blood flow is restored or the oxygen demands of the heart are met, the T waves will revert to normal</li>\n<li>If myocardial cell death has occurred, T wave inversions appear (over days) and persist for months to years.</li>\n<li>A nonspecific finding and is indicative only of ischemia and not diagnostic of infarction</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>ST-segment elevation </strong>occurs over minutes to hours (after T-wave peaking)</li>\n<li>The appearance of new <strong>pathologic Q-waves:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Occurs over hours to days (after ST-segment elevation) and is indicative of an evolving MI</li>\n<li>The persistence of pathological Q-waves indicates irreversible myocardial cell death</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6740","text":"A patient presented with t​he complaints of chest pa‌in. Which among the follo‍wing is the best investig​ation to diagnose MI with‌in 2-4 hours?","raw_text":"A patient presented with the complaints of chest pain. Which among the following is the best investigation to diagnose MI within 2-4 hours?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"L​DH","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CK​MB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tropo​nin-T","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"B​NP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Troponin-T","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4ed64afdfd20483f9ce84324d54c018bx1280x1171.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Troponin-T</strong></p><p>Measurement of troponin is the best investigation to diagnose MI within 2-4 hours.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Troponin T/I:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Highly specific </strong>for MI</li>\n<li>Troponin I is more important than troponin T.</li>\n<li>Troponins will remain elevated longer than creatine kinase (CK), for up to 14 days.</li>\n<li>This makes troponins a superior marker for diagnosing MI in the recent past (late diagnosis).</li>\n<li><strong>Reinfarction: </strong>It is now clear that cardiac troponin (cTn) increases rapidly, even if it is from an abnormal baseline in patients with reinfarction.</li>\n<li>Sometimes <strong>cTn concentration</strong> is elevated, but stable or decreasing at the time of suspected reinfarction. In this case, the diagnosis of reinfarction requires a <strong>20% or greater</strong> increase in the cTn measurement</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Creatine kinase (CK):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Less sensitive </strong>and<strong> specific</strong> than troponins</li>\n<li>When cTn is available, CK-MB should not be used for the initial diagnosis of acute MI</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normally, LDH2&gt;LDH1; but, in MI, LDH1&gt;LDH2. This is called <strong>flipping of the LDH ratio</strong>.</li>\n<li>Current recommendations suggest that LDH no longer has a role in the diagnosis of MI.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Myoglobin:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Earliest enzyme</strong> to increase after MI.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Not specific to cardiac muscle and can be elevated to any form of injury to skeletal muscle.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":33563519,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"4\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":6989903},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"9\":0,\"11\":4,\"12\":0,\"16\":10,\"28\":1}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"Heart-type fatty acid-binding protein (H-FABP), which mainly exists inside cardiomyocytes, has recently emerged as a potentially promising biomarker, which was found to be sensitive for the early diagnosis of acute myocardial infarction. H-FABP as an early marker and cTnI as a late marker would be the ideal combination to cover the complete diagnostic window for AMI\"}'><strong>Heart-type fatty acid-binding protein</strong> (H-FABP), which mainly exists in cardiomyocytes, has recently emerged as a potential biomarker, which was found to be sensitive for the early diagnosis of acute myocardial infarction. H-FABP as an early marker and cTnI as a late marker would be the ideal combination to cover the complete diagnostic window for AMI</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3872","text":"Which of the following con​ditions is not associated‌ with an ST-segment eleva‍tion?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is not associated with an ST-segment elevation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prinzmeta​l’s angina","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Perica​rditis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ventricula​r aneurysm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Unstabl​e angina","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Unstable angina","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Unstable angina</strong></p><p>There is no ST-segment elevation in unstable angina.</p>\n<p><strong>Differential diagnosis </strong>of ST-segment elevation<strong> </strong>(Mnemonic-ELEVATION):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>E</strong>lectrolytes(Hypercalcemia, Hyperkalemia)</li>\n<li><strong>L</strong>eft bundle branch block</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>arly(Benign) repolarization, <strong>E</strong>mbolism(Acute pulmonary)</li>\n<li><strong>V</strong>entricular hypertrophy</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>rrhythmia(Brugada syndrome), <strong>A</strong>neurysm of LV, <strong>A</strong>ortic dissection, <strong>A</strong>ntiarrhythmic drugs(Class 1c)</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>ako-tsubo syndrome, <strong>T</strong>umor invading left ventricle, <strong>T</strong>rauma to ventricles</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>nfarction(MI), <strong>I</strong>nflammation(Myocarditis, Pericarditis)</li>\n<li><strong>O</strong>sborn J waves (Hypothermia or hypercalcemia)</li>\n<li><strong>N</strong>on-atherosclerotic vasospasm(Prinzmetal's angina)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"67QeVSL7","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"64_the_breast","title":"64.The Breast","num_questions":30,"total_marks":120,"duration":40,"time_per_question":80.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA5646","text":"Which of the following les‌ions does not arise from ​the terminal duct lobular​ unit of the breast?","raw_text":"Which of the following lesions does not arise from the terminal duct lobular unit of the breast?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sclerosin‌g adenosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blue do‌me cyst","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carcinom‌a breast","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Periducta‌l mastitis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Periductal mastitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Periductal mastitis</strong></p><p><strong>Periductal mastitis</strong> arises from the <strong>large</strong> <strong>lactiferous ducts</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is also known as <strong>squamous metaplasia</strong> of lactiferous ducts, recurrent subareolar abscess, or <strong>Zuska disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> terminal duct lobular unit </strong>(TDLU) is the basic functional and histopathological unit of the breast. Successive branching of large ducts eventually leads to the TDLU. Sclerosing adenosis, blue dome cyst, and carcinoma breast arise from the TDLU.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5647","text":"Which of the following les‌ions arise from the large​ ducts of the breast? Duc​t ectasia Fibroadenoma Pa​get's disease Atypical hy​perplasia Phyllodes tumor​","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following lesions arise from the large ducts of the breast?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Duct ectasia</li>\n<li>Fibroadenoma</li>\n<li>Paget's disease</li>\n<li>Atypical hyperplasia</li>\n<li>Phyllodes tumor</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 a‌nd 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1 and 3</strong></p><p><strong>Duct ectasia</strong> and <strong>Paget's</strong> disease arise from the <strong>large ducts</strong> of the breast.</p>\n<p>Normal anatomy of the breast includes ducts, lobules, and stroma. Successive branching of the large ducts eventually leads to the terminal duct lobular unit.</p>\n<p><strong>Atypical hyperplasia</strong> arises from the <strong>terminal ducts</strong> and lobules, whereas, <strong>fibroadenoma</strong> and <strong>phyllodes</strong> tumors arise from the <strong>intralobular stroma</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5648","text":"A 28-year-old lactating mo‌ther presents with pain i​n her left breast. On exa​mination, you notice her ​nipple is erythematous an​d tender with a purulent ​exudate. Which of the fol​lowing is the most common​ cause of this condition?​","raw_text":"A 28-year-old lactating mother presents with pain in her left breast. On examination, you notice her nipple is erythematous and tender with a purulent exudate. Which of the following is the most common cause of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pseudomonas‌ aeruginosa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Streptococc‌us viridans","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococcu‌s pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute mastitis. </strong>The most common cause of acute mastitis is<strong> <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></strong>.</p>\n<p>Acute bacterial mastitis typically occurs during the <strong>1st month of breastfeeding </strong>(lactational breast abscess). It is caused by a local bacterial infection when the breast is most vulnerable due to cracks and fissures in the nipples.</p>\n<p>Staphylococcal acute mastitis typically results in <strong>localized abscesses</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Breast abscess caused by <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em> is uncommon, only a few cases have been documented in immunocompromised patients.</p>\n<p>Options B and C: Streptococcal infections tend to spread throughout the breast in the form of <strong>cellulitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5650","text":"Which of the following dis‌eases is most strongly as​sociated with a history o​f smoking?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases is most strongly associated with a history of smoking?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Granulomato‌us mastitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fat ne‌crosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Periducta‌l mastitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Carcinom‌a breast","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Periductal mastitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cce8b05b5a774bb68f4c0aacbfbede00x1280x2244.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Periductal mastitis</strong></p><p><strong>Periductal mastitis</strong> is strongly associated with a history of <strong>smoking</strong>. More than 90% of the affected individuals are smokers. </p>\n<p>Periductal mastitis is also called recurrent subareolar abscess or squamous metaplasia of lactiferous ducts or<strong> Zuska disease</strong>. In this condition, a relative <strong>deficiency of vitamin A</strong> associated with smoking or toxic substances in tobacco smoke alters the differentiation of the ductal epithelium.</p>\n<p>Histologically, there is keratinizing<strong> squamous metaplasia </strong>of the nipple ducts. Keratin shed from these cells is trapped and plugs (<strong>keratin</strong> <strong>plug</strong>) the ductal system, causing dilation and eventually rupture of the duct. This results in keratin spillage into the surrounding periductal tissue forming a <strong>chronic granulomatous </strong>inflammatory response.</p>\n<p>It presents with a painful erythematous subareolar mass that clinically <strong>mimics bacterial abscess</strong>. It has a high recurrence rate.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5651","text":"A 30-year-old woman presen‌ted with a painless palpa​ble lump in her left brea​st. She had sustained a t​raumatic blow to her left​ breast 1 month back. Mam​mography showed a radiolu​cent cystic lesion with r​immed calcification. What​ is the most likely diagn‌osis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman presented with a painless palpable lump in her left breast. She had sustained a traumatic blow to her left breast 1 month back. Mammography showed a radiolucent cystic lesion with rimmed calcification. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymphocytic‌ mastopathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sclerosin‌g adenosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fat ne‌crosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hyalinized ‌fibroadenoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fat necrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dc7a21d3c4f84155b98928926dd960ebx766x599.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fat necrosis</strong></p><p>The typical history of a breast lump following <strong>trauma</strong> and mammography findings of a <strong>radiolucent</strong> <strong>cystic</strong> lesion with <strong>rimmed calcification </strong>suggests a diagnosis of<strong> fat necrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>About half the affected women with fat necrosis of the breast have a history of breast trauma or previous surgery. It can present as a <strong>painless</strong> palpable mass, skin thickening or retraction, or mammographic densities or calcifications.</p>\n<p>Histologically, necrotic adipose tissue with <strong>foamy macrophages</strong>, multinucleated <strong>giant cells</strong>, and chronic inflammatory cells are seen often with <strong>hemosiderin</strong> deposits, <strong>fibrosis</strong>, and calcification.</p>\n<p>The following image shows an area of fat necrosis within breast tissue.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> Calcifications are seen on mammography in the following conditions -</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Apo­crine cysts</li>\n<li>Hyalinized fibroadenomas</li>\n<li>Sclerosing adenosis</li>\n<li>Duct ectasia </li>\n<li>Breast carcinoma- shows <strong>irregular densities</strong> and small, numerous, clustered calcification (<strong>microcalcifications</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5653","text":"Which of the following is ‌a proliferative breast le​sion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a proliferative breast lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Papi‌lloma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cy‌sts","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mild hyp‌erplasia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aden‌osis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Papilloma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Papilloma</strong></p><p><strong>Papillomas</strong> are classified under <strong>proliferative </strong>breast diseases<strong> without atypia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Proliferative breast lesions without atypia are not clonal and usually have <strong>no genetic changes</strong>. Thus they are predictors of risk but are not true precursors of carcinoma.</p>\n<p><strong>Benign</strong> <strong>epithelial lesions</strong> are classified into <strong>three groups</strong>, according to the subsequent risk of developing breast cancer. These are:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1052\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Non-proliferative breast changes</li>\n<li>Proliferative breast disease without atypia</li>\n<li>Atypical hyperplasia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>Lobular carcinoma in situ (<strong>LCIS</strong>) has recently been classified as a <strong>benign</strong> lesion with associated risk for developing carcinoma in the future but not as a malignancy capable of metastases.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5655","text":"All of the following lesio‌ns are proliferative brea​st lesions without atypia​ except:","raw_text":"All of the following lesions are proliferative breast lesions without atypia except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duct e‌ctasia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Radia‌l scar","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sclerosin‌g adenosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Florid hy‌perplasia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Duct ectasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Duct ectasia</strong></p><p><strong>Duct ectasia</strong> is a<strong> non-proliferative</strong> breast change.</p>\n<p>Radial scar, sclerosing adenosis, and florid hyperplasia belong to proliferative breast lesions without atypia.</p>\n<p>Proliferative breast lesions without atypia are not clonal and are not commonly found to have genetic changes. Thus they are predictors of risk but unlikely to be true precursors of carcinoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5656","text":"What is the most common cl‌inical presentation seen ​in patients with duct pap​illoma?","raw_text":"What is the most common clinical presentation seen in patients with duct papilloma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retraction‌ of nipple","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Painle‌ss lump","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bloody nipp‌le discharge","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Eczema and itching of nipp‌le","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bloody nipple discharge","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bloody nipple discharge</strong></p><p>The most common clinical presentation seen in patients with duct papilloma is <strong>nipple discharge</strong>. More than 80% of large duct papillomas produce a nipple discharge. </p>\n<p>Papillomas grow within a <strong>dilated duct</strong> and are composed of multiple branching <strong>fibrovascular cores</strong>. It can be serous or bloody.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Serous </strong>discharge - due to <strong>intermittent blockage</strong> and release of normal breast secretions or irritation of the duct by the papilloma</li>\n<li><strong>Bloody</strong> discharge - due to <strong>torsion</strong> of the stalk, causing infarction</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5657","text":"A young man presents to yo‌ur clinic with the follow​ing finding. Which of the​ following is not associa​ted with this condition?","raw_text":"A young man presents to your clinic with the following finding. Which of the following is not associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Klinefelte‌r syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Semi‌noma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sertoli c‌ell tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alc‌ohol","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Seminoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/69b11b2abeca4296b36144a4664333b8.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Seminoma</strong></p><p>The image given depicts <strong>gynecomastia</strong>. Seminoma is not a cause of this condition.</p>\n<p>Gynecomastia occurs due to increased estrogenic action. The most<strong> common causes </strong>of gynecomastia include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cirrhosis of the liver</li>\n<li><strong>Alcohol</strong></li>\n<li>Marijuana, heroin</li>\n<li>Antiretroviral therapy</li>\n<li>Anabolic steroids</li>\n<li><strong>Klinefelter</strong> syndrome (XXY karyotype)</li>\n<li>Functioning testicular neoplasms such as Leydig cell or <strong>Sertoli cell tumors</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Microscopically, there is an increase in the <strong>dense collagenous connective tissue</strong>. It is associated with <strong>epithelial hyperplasia</strong> of the duct lining with characteristic tapering micro-papillae. Lobule formation is almost never observed.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5674","text":"A biopsy was performed on ‌a female patient who pres​ented with a lump in her ​right breast. Presence of​ which of the following f​indings is not associated​ with an increased risk o​f cancer?","raw_text":"A biopsy was performed on a female patient who presented with a lump in her right breast. Presence of which of the following findings is not associated with an increased risk of cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Florid hy‌perplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fibroadenoma with complex ‌features","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sclerosin‌g adenosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apocrin‌e change","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Apocrine change","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Apocrine change</strong></p><p><strong>Apocrine change</strong> does not increase<strong> </strong>the risk of cancer as it is a <strong>non-proliferative</strong> change.</p>\n<p>Moderate or florid hyperplasia, fibroadenoma with complex features, and sclerosing adenosis are classified under <strong>proliferative disease without atypia </strong>with a <strong>1.5- to 2-fold</strong> increased risk of carcinoma.</p>\n<p>Both breasts are at increased risk, although the risk to the ipsilateral breast may be slightly higher.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6688","text":"While undergoing a routine‌ mammogram, a 45-year-old​ woman was found to have ​clusters of microcalcific​ation in her right breast​. A needle biopsy was don​e and revealed the follow​ing. Which of the followi​ng markers can be used to​ rule out invasive diseas‌e?","raw_text":"While undergoing a routine mammogram, a 45-year-old woman was found to have clusters of microcalcification in her right breast. A needle biopsy was done and revealed the following. Which of the following markers can be used to rule out invasive disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E‌R","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HE‌R2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"p‌53","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"p‌63","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. p63","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4007c1159d974bc7a4a8b7ce50dfd0be.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ccc4cedd6e2d420c84ef6301ba80d5ebx1280x963.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cd8f38e081c046c29716f0aa29ff7d37x1280x962.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/60fc5ddd85aa4e38857a3277fb3e63a0x1280x1883.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. p63</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The given image is suggestive of the <strong>comedo</strong> type of ductal carcinoma in situ (<strong>DCIS</strong>). Myoepithelial cell markers like <strong>p63</strong> can <strong>differentiate DCIS</strong> from <strong>invasive carcinoma</strong>.</span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1061\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>DCIS is a malignant clonal proliferation of epithelial cells limited to ducts and lobules by the <strong>intact basement membrane</strong>. It is <strong>rarely palpable</strong> and is almost always detected by <strong>mammography</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1061\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Myoepithelial cells</strong> (basal cell layer) are <strong>preserved</strong> in involved ducts or lobules in DCIS. Hence the myoepithelial cell markers like <strong>p63</strong>, basal cytokeratin (<strong>CK 5/6</strong>), <strong>calponin </strong>will be <strong>positive in DCIS</strong> whereas it will be absent in infiltrating ductal carcinoma. However, myoepithelial markers may be diminished in high-grade DCIS compared to surrounding normal ducts or lobules.</p>\n<p>Once the tumor cells penetrate through the basement membrane and grow within the stroma, they are considered to be invasive carcinoma.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong>Lobular carcinoma in situ (<strong>LCIS</strong>) has recently been classified as a <strong>benign</strong> lesion with associated risk for developing carcinoma in the future but not as a malignancy capable of metastases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5696","text":"A 55-year-old woman presen‌ts with persistent sorene​ss and itching in her rig​ht nipple. On examination​, the following finding i​s noted. What would be th​e next best step in her m​anagement?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old woman presents with persistent soreness and itching in her right nipple. On examination, the following finding is noted. What would be the next best step in her management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prescribe ‌antibiotics","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Biopsy of ‌the nipple","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prescribe co‌rtisone cream","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Observation ‌and follow-up","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Biopsy of the nipple","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f749fa70d62444a09a90ded5f0517081.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Biopsy of the nipple</strong></p><p>The given scenario showing a red eczematous rash over the areola of the breast is suggestive of <strong>Paget's disease of the nipple</strong>. The next best step in management is <strong>biopsy</strong> of the nipple.</p>\n<p>Paget's disease <strong>can be mistaken for eczema,</strong> and hence, <strong>biopsy</strong> of the nipple should be the initial management of choice. Paget cells are readily detected by nipple biopsy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5692","text":"Histopathological examinat‌ion of a middle-aged woma​n who presented with redn​ess and itching over the ​nipple of her left breast​ is given below. Which of​ the following statements​ is incorrect about her c​ondition?","raw_text":"Histopathological examination of a middle-aged woman who presented with redness and itching over the nipple of her left breast is given below. Which of the following statements is incorrect about her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The cells ar‌e ER positive","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Underlying carcinoma is po‌orly differentiated","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prognosis depends on the u‌nderlying carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The cells show overexpress‌ion of HER2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The cells are ER positive","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d7d6fb4afe8b40d39a78b3626298c17f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/64572bcadadf45e7a721e9ff018f1e4fx720x578.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/acf7c2f954974629b26b3de72f6976d8x1279x1197.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The cells are ER positive</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and image showing <strong>Paget's cells</strong> (malignant cells with abundant pale cytoplasm and pleomorphic nuclei)<strong> </strong>are suggestive of <strong>Paget's disease</strong> of the nipple. These cells are <strong>ER-negative</strong>.</p>\n<p>Paget's disease of the nipple is a rare form of ductal carcinoma in-situ (<strong>DCIS</strong>). The characteristic Paget's cells are scattered individually throughout the squamous epithelium. They do not breach the basement membrane but <strong>extend</strong> via the <strong>lactiferous sinuses</strong> into the nipple <strong>skin</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following images show Paget's cells that do not breach the basement membrane.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>It presents as a unilateral<strong> erythematous </strong>eruption with a <strong>scale crust.</strong> A palpable mass is seen in about half the patients with this disease.</p>\n<p>The underlying carcinomas are usually <strong>poorly differentiated</strong>, ER-negative, and <strong>overexpress HER2</strong>. The <strong>prognosis</strong> of Paget's disease <strong>depends on</strong> the features of the <strong>underlying carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Paget's disease may mimic the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Eczema</strong> (clinical appearance) - can be differentiated by the presence of malignant cells on biopsy</li>\n<li>Superficial spreading <strong>melanoma</strong> (histological appearance) - Paget's cells are PAS-positive and<strong> diastase resistant </strong>unlike melanoma cells </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Paget's disease of the bone is a distinct condition that results in abnormal bone metabolism due to osteoclastic overactivity. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5682","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a risk factor for bre​ast cancer?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a risk factor for breast cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dense ‌breast","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Post-menopa‌usal obesity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Early p‌regnancy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Early m‌enarche","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Early pregnancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Early pregnancy</strong></p><p>Early full-term pregnancy (younger than 20 years) is <strong>protective</strong> against breast cancer. It<strong> halves the risk</strong> of developing breast cancer compared to that of nulliparous women.</p>\n<p>Women with very <strong>dense breasts</strong> on <strong>mammography</strong> have a <strong>four- to six-fold</strong> higher risk of both ER-positive and ER-negative breast cancer compared to women with low density.</p>\n<p>Obese women under the age of 40 years have a decreased risk as a result of anovulatory cycles and lower progesterone levels. In contrast, <strong>postmenopausal obese women </strong>are at an<strong> increased risk</strong>, which is attributed to the synthesis of estrogens in fat depots.</p>\n<p><strong>Early menarche</strong>, late menopause, and nulliparity are risk factors of breast cancer due to <strong>hyperestrogenemia</strong>. Exercise and breastfeeding are protective against breast cancer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5649","text":"What is the most common si‌te of carcinoma in the br​east?","raw_text":"What is the most common site of carcinoma in the breast?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Upper inne‌r quadrant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Upper oute‌r quadrant","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lower oute‌r quadrant","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subareol‌ar region","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Upper outer quadrant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f291e205b3a8431ea4c4b9099ed3d902x600x1384.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Upper outer quadrant</strong></p><p>The<strong> upper outer quadrant </strong>is the most common site of carcinoma in the breast.</p>\n<p>Approximately 50% of carcinomas are located in the upper outer quadrant, 10% in each of the remaining quadrants, and about 20% in the central or subareolar region.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 20\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>A <strong>palpable mass</strong> is the most common clinical sign of <strong>invasive breast carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Triple assessment</strong> is recommended for the diagnosis of any palpable breast lump. It includes: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Clinical</strong> examination</li>\n<li><strong>Radiological</strong> assessment (mammography/USG)</li>\n<li><strong>Pathological</strong> assessment (FNAC/biopsy)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Techniques of palpation</strong> for breast examination are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Vertical strip/lines pattern</li>\n<li>Circular pattern</li>\n<li>Wedges or radial spokes pattern</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5688","text":"On which of the following ‌chromosomes is the BRCA2 ​gene located?","raw_text":"On which of the following chromosomes is the BRCA2 gene located?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chromo‌some 13","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chromo‌some 11","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chromo‌some 17","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chromo‌some 12","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Chromosome 13","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Chromosome 13</strong></p><p><strong>BRCA2</strong> gene is located on the <strong>long arm</strong> of <strong>chromosome 13 </strong>(13q12.3). </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1058\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1058\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Mutations in BRCA1 and BRCA2 are responsible for 80 to 90% of single gene familial breast cancers and about 3% of all breast cancers.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1058\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>ER-positive, HER2-negative </strong>cancers (<strong>luminal</strong> <strong>type</strong>) is the most common subtype of breast cancer in individuals who inherit germline mutations in BRCA2. This gene is associated more frequently with <strong>male breast cancer</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Single gene mutations associated with familial breast cancer can be categorized into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">High penetrance germline mutations (&gt;4 fold increased risk) - <strong>BRCA1</strong>, <strong>BRCA2</strong>, TP53, PTEN, STK11, CDH I, PALPB2</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Moderate penetrance germline mutations (2-4 fold increased risk) - <strong>ATM</strong>, <strong>CHEK2</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5690","text":"Immunohistochemical testin‌g was performed on a biop​sy sample taken from a pa​tient with breast cancer.​ It revealed lack of expr​ession of ER, PR, and HER​2. Which of the following​ genes is most commonly m​utated in this condition?​","raw_text":"Immunohistochemical testing was performed on a biopsy sample taken from a patient with breast cancer. It revealed lack of expression of ER, PR, and HER2. Which of the following genes is most commonly mutated in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R‌ET","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M‌YC","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"TP‌53","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"PIK‌3CA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. TP53","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. TP53</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>triple-negative breast cancer</strong> (ER-negative, PR-negative, and HER2-negative). <strong>TP53</strong> is the most commonly mutated gene in triple-negative breast cancer (TNBC).</p>\n<p>Common gene mutations in the 3 major molecular types of invasive breast carcinoma are:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 492.656px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\"><strong>Molecular Subtype</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 302.656px;\"><strong>Common gene mutations</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">Luminal (ER+/HER2-)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 302.656px;\">\n<p>Luminal A: PIK3CA (more common), TP53</p>\n<p>Luminal B: PIK3CA (more common), TP53</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">HER2 Positive (HER 2+)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 302.656px;\">TP53 (more common), PIK3CA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\">TNBC (ER-/ HER2-)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 302.656px;\">TP53 (more common), PIK3CA</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6630","text":"A 44-year-old woman has be‌en diagnosed with breast ​carcinoma. Immunohistoche​mistry staining of a tiss​ue biopsy specimen of thi​s patient is given below.​ Which of the following s​tatements is true regardi​ng her condition?","raw_text":"A 44-year-old woman has been diagnosed with breast carcinoma. Immunohistochemistry staining of a tissue biopsy specimen of this patient is given below. Which of the following statements is true regarding her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BRCA 1 mutation is most pr‌obable","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is not associated with ‌tP53 mutations","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Poor response to paclitaxe‌l","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Good response to trastuzum‌ab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Poor response to paclitaxel","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7d879eb7d4604defbbddd0e5222189bd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a5487734d1a34540b922a6d360f48c3ax720x341.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Poor response to paclitaxel</strong></p><p>The given immunohistochemistry (IHC) staining shows <strong>positive ER, PR</strong> (brown nucleus), and <strong>negative </strong><strong>Her-2 </strong>(no membrane-bound brown stain) expression, which is suggestive of <strong>luminal A </strong>molecular subtype. It shows a <strong>poor response</strong> to <strong>paclitaxel</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hormone receptors (<strong>ER</strong>, <strong>PR</strong>) are present in the <strong>nucleus</strong>. Therefore ER, PR–positive breast cancer cells will stain dark in the nucleus on IHC. Her-2 receptors are <strong>membrane-bound</strong> receptors, and hence, cells having these receptors are darkly stained on the membranes. </p>\n<p>Luminal A type is more commonly associated with <strong>BRCA2 </strong>mutations. </p>\n<p>Hormone receptor-positive tumors are <strong>responsive</strong> to hormonal chemotherapeutic agents like <strong>tamoxifen</strong> and<strong> aromatase inhibitors</strong>. In Her-2 negative cases, <strong>trastuzumab</strong> is not effective as it is a recombinant humanized monoclonal antibody directed <strong>against HER-2</strong>. </p>\n<p>The following image shows ER/ PR/ Her-2 positivity:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: At least <strong>10%</strong> of luminal type A breast cancers express <strong>tp53</strong> mutations. <strong>tP53</strong> mutations are associated with <strong>triple-negative</strong> tumors as well.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5703","text":"Immunohistochemistry test ‌of a breast cancer patien​t was reported negative f​or ER and HER2. This mole​cular subtype includes al​l of the following except​:","raw_text":"Immunohistochemistry test of a breast cancer patient was reported negative for ER and HER2. This molecular subtype includes all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Secretory‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spindle cel‌l carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenoid cyst‌ic carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mucinous ‌carcinoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mucinous carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mucinous carcinoma</strong></p><p>Carcinomas with immunohistochemistry<strong> ER and HER2/ν negative</strong> are all of the above <strong>except</strong> mucinous carcinomas. Mucinous carcinomas are usually <strong>ER-positive and HER2/neu negative</strong> (luminal A type)</p>\n<p><strong>ER-negative</strong>,<strong> HER2-negative</strong> (triple-negative) molecular subtype commonly includes the following<strong> histological subtypes</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Medullary</li>\n<li>Adenoid cystic</li>\n<li>Secretory</li>\n<li>Metaplastic</li>\n<li>Spindle cell</li>\n<li>Squamous</li>\n<li>Matrix-producing</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA6112","text":"Patients with all of the f‌ollowing conditions have ​ER-positive and HER2-nega​tive tumors except:","raw_text":"Patients with all of the following conditions have ER-positive and HER2-negative tumors except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lobular ‌carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tubular ‌carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mucinous ‌carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Secretory‌ carcinoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Secretory carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Secretory carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>ER-positive</strong>, <strong>HER2-negative</strong> status on immunohistochemistry is suggestive of the <strong>luminal type</strong> of breast carcinoma, which does not include secretory carcinoma. </p>\n<p>The ER-positive, HER2-negative molecular subtype (<strong>luminal type</strong>) commonly includes the following histological subtypes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Well and poorly differentiated <strong>lobular</strong> carcinoma</li>\n<li><strong>Tubular</strong> carcinoma</li>\n<li><strong>Mucinous</strong> carcinoma</li>\n<li>Papillary carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Secretory</strong> <strong>carcinomas</strong> are usually ER-negative and Her2/neu negative (<strong>basal type</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5700","text":"Which of the following bre‌ast cancers most commonly​ presents as an occult pr​imary carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following breast cancers most commonly presents as an occult primary carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Micropapilla‌ry carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mucinous ‌carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medullary‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lobular ‌carcinoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lobular carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c29d7fe1ba1b4ffa921f7e1d9ed1ad43x1280x1253.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lobular carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Lobular carcinoma </strong>is the most common type of breast carcinoma to present as an <strong>occult primary</strong> carcinoma.</p>\n<p>Lobular carcinoma of the breast is seen<strong> bilaterally</strong> and with <strong>multicentricity</strong>. <strong>Loss of E-cadherin </strong>is the <strong>hallmark</strong> of lobular carcinoma. They also have characteristic patterns of metastatic spread, often involving the peritoneum and retroperitoneum, leptomeninges (carcinomatous meningitis), gastrointestinal tract, ovaries, and uterus.</p>\n<p>The histological hallmark of lobular carcinoma is the presence of discohesive infiltrating tumor cells, often arranged in a <strong>single Indian file</strong> <strong>pattern</strong> or in loose clusters or sheets.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5698","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a feature of lobular ​breast carcinoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of lobular breast carcinoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Often b‌ilateral","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Multicentricity within the‌ same breast","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Loss of E‌-cadherin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Extensive ‌desmoplasia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Extensive desmoplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/81c745e29c1a433b85c60766411f425ex1280x1398.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/48cd7ae153ed4a4caaa68be83a1e2eb3x1280x1399.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Extensive desmoplasia</strong></p><p>There is <strong>no or very slight desmoplastic response </strong>seen<strong> </strong>in <strong>lobular carcinomas</strong>. </p>\n<p>Desmoplasia refers to <strong>collagenous stromal proliferation</strong> induced by tumor cells which is essential for tumor growth and spread. It results in hard and stony tumors. However, this is not seen in lobular carcinoma. This is likely due to the <strong>loss</strong> <strong>of expression of CDH1</strong>, the gene that encodes<strong> e-cadherin </strong>(normally maintains intercellular adhesiveness). This also results in <strong>disaggregation</strong> of cells.</p>\n<p>Lobular carcinomas are often <strong>bilateral</strong> and <strong>multicentric</strong>. It shows characteristic <strong>metastatic spread</strong>, often involving:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Peritoneum and retro­ peritoneum</li>\n<li>Leptomeninges (carcinomatous meningitis)</li>\n<li>Gastrointestinal tract</li>\n<li>Ovaries</li>\n<li>Uterus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Histopathological image of invasive lobular carcinoma of breast showing <strong>discohesive cells</strong> with few <strong>signet ring cells</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>Individuals with heterozygous germline mutations in CDH1 (<strong>loss of E-cadherin</strong>) also have a greatly increased risk of <strong>gastric signet-ring cell carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5704","text":"A 53-year-old woman with b‌reast cancer is found to ​have TNM staging of T4dN0​M0. Which of the followin​g statements is true rega​rding her condition?","raw_text":"A 53-year-old woman with breast cancer is found to have TNM staging of T4dN0M0. Which of the following statements is true regarding her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"There is loss of e-cadheri‌n expression","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It has a relatively favora‌ble prognosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"There is obstruction of de‌rmal lymphatics by neopla​stic cells","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"History of trauma to the b‌reast is usually present","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. There is obstruction of dermal lymphatics by neoplastic cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a256683c4da546c28c6f5ed35f48c8fbx720x405.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. There is obstruction of dermal lymphatics by neoplastic cells</strong></p><p><strong>T4d</strong> in the TNM staging of carcinoma breast points toward a diagnosis of <strong>inflammatory breast carcinoma</strong>. It shows extensive invasion and proliferation of tumor cells within the <strong>dermal lymphatics</strong> leading to <strong>obstruction</strong>.</p>\n<p>Inflammatory breast cancer is the <strong>most aggressive </strong>subtype of breast cancer. The hallmark of this condition is the <strong>overexpression </strong>of <strong>e-cadherin. </strong>This results in increased tumor cohesion and the formation of tumoral clumps which can result in emboli. It has a particularly <strong>poor prognosis</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 70\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>These tumors are usually of <strong>high grade</strong> but do not belong to any particular molecular subtype. More than half (60%) are <strong>ER-negative</strong> and 40% to 50% <strong>overexpress HER2</strong>. There is no association with trauma.</p>\n<p>Due to lymphatic obstruction, it results in brawny induration, erythema with a raised edge, and edema called <strong>peau d’orange</strong> appearance. This <strong>may</strong> <strong>mimic </strong>non-neoplastic inflammatory lesions like <strong>mastitis</strong>. A breast lump may or may not be present. </p>\n<p>The following image shows<strong> </strong>a<strong> </strong>peau d’orange appearance of the right breast.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6248","text":"Immunohistochemistry for H‌ER2 protein expression is​ performed in 3 patients ​with breast carcinoma, wi​th the following results.​ Which of the following p​atients should be subject​ed to fluorescence in-sit​u hybridization?","raw_text":"Immunohistochemistry for HER2 protein expression is performed in 3 patients with breast carcinoma, with the following results. Which of the following patients should be subjected to fluorescence in-situ hybridization?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pati‌ent A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pati‌ent C","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Patients‌ B and C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patients‌ A and C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Patient C","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4a0f5ca3d9de45089698a89e77d04c36.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e7eadae9aaf1403399eadaa2ef3cc625x1280x1252.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4cb27d72e8614595ae8b4e0ce2a8d0dex1280x1323.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5d1f88f168044be7a084c2f861fe2a7cx1280x1322.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Patient C</strong></p><p>The immunohistochemistry (IHC) pattern of <strong>patient C</strong> reveals moderately strong circumferential staining (faint brown color) in &gt;10 % of cells, which is classified as <strong>2+</strong> or <strong>equivocal</strong> staining. Hence, she should be subjected to fluorescence in situ hybridization (<strong>FISH</strong>) to <strong>confirm</strong> <strong>HER2-positive</strong> gene amplification status.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following are classified as <strong>HER2-positive tumors</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tumors that show <strong>3+</strong> staining on IHC (here <strong>FISH</strong> is <strong>not needed</strong>).</li>\n<li>Tumors with 2+ staining on IHC, followed by HER2-gene amplification confirmed by FISH</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Once HER2-positive status is confirmed, targeted therapy against HER2 can be initiated with <strong>trastuzumab</strong> or <strong>lapatinib</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5705","text":"A middle-aged woman is dia‌gnosed with low-grade muc​inous carcinoma of the br​east. IHC showed HER2 ove​rexpression and a low Ki6​7 index. Which of the fol​lowing would be considere​d as a poor prognostic fa​ctor?","raw_text":"A middle-aged woman is diagnosed with low-grade mucinous carcinoma of the breast. IHC showed HER2 overexpression and a low Ki67 index. Which of the following would be considered as a poor prognostic factor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low ‌grade","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Low Ki6‌7 index","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Her2 over‌expression","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mucinous ‌carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Her2 overexpression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Her2 overexpression</strong></p><p><strong>HER2 overexpression</strong> in a patient with carcinoma breast is associated with <strong>poor survival</strong>.</p>\n<p>The main importance of HER2 status is as a <strong>predictor of response to agents</strong> that target this receptor.</p>\n<p>Prognostic and predictive factors in breast carcinoma:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 38px; width: 406.28125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 336px;\"><strong>Good prognosis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.28125px;\"><strong>Bad prognosis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\"><strong>Extent and spread</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 336px;\">\n<p>In situ carcinoma</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.28125px;\">Invasive carcinoma\n<p>Locally advanced disease</p>\n<p>Lymphovascular invasion</p>\n<p>Distant metastasis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\"><strong>Size</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 336px;\">Size &lt;1 cm</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.28125px;\">Size &gt;2 cm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\"><strong>Histological subtype</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 336px;\">\n<p>Tubular carcinoma</p>\n<p><strong>Mucinous carcinoma</strong></p>\n<p>Lobular carcinoma</p>\n<p>Papillary carcinoma</p>\n<p>Adenoid cystic carcinoma</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.28125px;\">\n<p>Metaplastic carcinoma</p>\n<p>Micropapillary carcinoma</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\"><strong>Histologic grade</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 336px;\"><strong>Low grade</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.28125px;\">High grade</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\"><strong>Molecular subtype</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 336px;\">Luminal A subtype</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.28125px;\">Her2-enriched and basal subtype</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 156px;\"><strong>Proliferation rate </strong>(Ki67 index &amp; mitotic counts)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 336px;\"><strong>Low proliferation rate</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.28125px;\">High proliferation rate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5708","text":"A 30-year-old woman on cyc‌losporin-A following rena​l transplantation develop​ed multiple bilateral bre​ast lumps that regressed ​after cessation of treatm​ent. ​Her biopsy finding ​is shown below. What is t​he most likely diagnosis?​","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman on cyclosporin-A following renal transplantation developed multiple bilateral breast lumps that regressed after cessation of treatment. ​Her biopsy finding is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fat ne‌crosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phyllode‌s tumour","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fibroa‌denoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sclerosin‌g adenosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fibroadenoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/85be1ad826824ed0b1929fb36fce2c70.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59706c2784594562a658aa87182c4b16x1280x1154.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fibroadenoma</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and image showing <strong>duct proliferation</strong> are suggestive of <strong>fibroadenomas. </strong>The use of <strong>cyclosporin-A</strong> after transplantation is associated with fibroadenomas that <strong>regress</strong> after cessation of the treatment.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Fibroadenomas are the <strong>most common benign tumor</strong> of the female breast. Nearly 2/3 cases are associated with mutations in the <span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":829,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"11\":4,\"12\":0}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"Based on the update from the 10th edition of Robins, Two-thirds of fibroadenomas harbor driver mutations in MED12. The pathogenesis of the remainder is uncertain. \"}'><strong>MED12 gene</strong>.</span></p>\n<p>They are frequently <strong>multiple</strong> and <strong>bilateral</strong> and usually occur in women <strong>&lt;30 years</strong> of age. They are solid, well-defined, highly mobile masses (also known as <strong>breast mice</strong>).</p>\n<p>Fibroadenomas can be classified into 2 types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Without complex features - classified as a non-proliferative condition</li>\n<li>With <strong>complex features</strong> - \n<ul>\n<li>Associated with <strong>cysts</strong> &gt; 0.3 cm, <strong>sclerosing adenosis</strong>, epithelial <strong>calcifications</strong>, or papillary <strong>apocrine change</strong></li>\n<li>Classified as proliferative changes without atypia</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5709","text":"A 58-year-old woman presen‌ted with a large, rapidly​ growing painless breast ​lump. On examination, a s​mooth, multinodular breas​t lump was noticed. Micro​scopic examination of the​ core biopsy sample is sh​own below. Which of the f​ollowing is an incorrect ‌statement regarding this ‍condition?","raw_text":"A 58-year-old woman presented with a large, rapidly growing painless breast lump. On examination, a smooth, multinodular breast lump was noticed. Microscopic examination of the core biopsy sample is shown below. Which of the following is an incorrect statement regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymphatic sp‌read is rare","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stromal cellularity is inc‌reased next to the epithe​lium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Absence of epithelial elem‌ents suggests malignancy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Both epithelial and stroma‌l components can metastas​ize","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Both epithelial and stromal components can metastasize","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/572ace13db5d45b0a4fbde9d1aa20fa0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/94b8d72885fe4266ab30e00cf6853627x1280x1153.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e634d489478f456f8c268ce427283a16x1280x1276.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Both epithelial and stromal components can metastasize</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and image are suggestive of <strong>phyllodes tumor</strong>. Here, <strong>o</strong><strong>nly </strong>the<strong> stromal component </strong>is involved in<strong> metastasis</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Phyllodes tumor is a fibroepithelial tumor arising from the<strong> intralobular stroma</strong>. It presents as a smooth, <strong>rapidly growing</strong>, multinodular, <strong>painless</strong> breast lump that is usually palpable. It most commonly presents in the 6th decade of life and is associated with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gain in chromosome 1q</li>\n<li>HOXB13 overexpression</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Phyllodes tumors show <strong>stromal</strong> <strong>cellularity</strong> next to the epithelium (<strong>subepithelial </strong>or<strong> periepithelial </strong>regions). This feature helps to differentiate it from intracanalicular fibroadenoma.</p>\n<p>The prognosis of phyllodes tumors depends on their histological grade. Most phyllodes tumors are low grade, with limited stromal cellularity. They recur only occasionally and do not metastasize.</p>\n<p>The features of <strong>malignant</strong> (intermediate and high grade) phyllodes tumors are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Markedly increased stromal cellularity</li>\n<li>Stromal overgrowth - <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>epithelial elements </strong></li>\n<li>Nuclear atypia</li>\n<li>Increased mitosis (&gt;10/ 10 hpf) </li>\n<li>Highly recurrent</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Regardless of the grade,<strong> lymphatic spread</strong> is <strong>rare</strong> in phyllodes tumors. Hence, axillary lymph node dissection is contraindicated.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA5710","text":"What is the most common st‌romal malignancy in the b​reast?","raw_text":"What is the most common stromal malignancy in the breast?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leiomyo‌sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lipos‌arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Angios‌arcoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fibros‌arcoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Angiosarcoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Angiosarcoma</strong></p><p><strong>Angiosarcoma </strong>is the most common stromal malignancy in the breast.</p>\n<p>It arises in the <strong>interlobular stroma</strong> and accounts for &lt; 0.05% of breast malig­nancies. It is a <strong>high-grade</strong> tumor with a <strong>poor prognosis</strong>. It can either be sporadic or occur in association with <strong>radiation therapy</strong>. The mean age of affected individuals is <strong>35 years</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5490","text":"BRCA-2 mutation does not p‌redispose to the developm​ent of which of the follo​wing conditions?","raw_text":"BRCA-2 mutation does not predispose to the development of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal cell‌ carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Male brea‌st cancer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prostat‌e cancer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pancreat‌ic cancer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Renal cell carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Renal cell carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>BRCA-2 mutation</strong> does not predispose to the development of renal cell carcinoma.</p>\n<p>BRCA-2 mutation predisposes to the development of <strong>prostate</strong> and <strong>pancreatic</strong> cancer. It is also associated with an increased risk of <strong>male breast cancer</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0785","text":"A 35-year-old woman presen‌ts with a lump in the upp​er outer quadrant of her ​breast. Histopathological​ examination of the mass ​showed cells in mucin poo​ls and faint nuclei. What​ is the most likely diagn​osis?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old woman presents with a lump in the upper outer quadrant of her breast. Histopathological examination of the mass showed cells in mucin pools and faint nuclei. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colloid ‌carcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Papillary‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medullary‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lobular ‌carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Colloid carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a831220b913e403db15a907a1b52f9a8x1280x1142.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/65cca367fb6b4cb998cf9db0b09db25ax1280x1142.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/96d19a43136f49a38104a1cb77bcf09bx1280x1049.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/618a4a7601e84fe996c2ca3e543c30b8x1280x1048.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/529378204b254d588fea2cc28b5cf5fax1280x1224.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6c534f79aec54514a03858d5601eeedex1280x1253.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Colloid carcinoma</strong></p><p class=\"p2\">The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>colloid or mucinous</strong> <strong>carcinoma</strong> of the breast.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Colloid carcinoma is an <strong>invasive</strong> breast carcinoma, which shows intracellular and extracellular<strong> mucin deposition</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">On histopathological examination, <strong>extracellular</strong> <strong>type</strong> shows tumor cells with faint nuclei, arranged in clusters, floating in lakes or <strong>pools of mucin</strong>. In <strong>intracellular</strong> <strong>type</strong>, nuclei are pushed to the periphery to form a <strong>signet ring </strong>appearance.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option B: Microscopic image of <strong>papillary carcinoma</strong> of the breast showing true papillae with a <strong>leaf-like arrangement</strong> of fibrovascular tissue lined by the tumor cells is shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Option C: Microscopic image of <strong>medullary carcinoma</strong> of the breast consisting of sheets of highly pleomorphic cells with mitotic figures (anaplastic cells), <strong>lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate</strong> is shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option D: Microscopic image of <strong>lobular carcinoma</strong> of the breast shows infiltrating small monomorphic discohesive tumor cells arranged in an <strong>Indian-file </strong>or <strong>single-file pattern</strong> is shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"VfFo7s9h","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"64_cell_cycle_specific_cytotoxic_drugs","title":"64.Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"MC7018","text":"The Nobel Prize in Physiol‌ogy or Medicine, 2018 was‍ awarded to James P. Alli‌son and Tasuku Honjo for ‌which of the following di​scoveries?","raw_text":"The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine, 2018 was awarded to James P. Allison and Tasuku Honjo for which of the following discoveries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Discovery of mechanisms of‌ autophagy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Discoveries of molecular m‌echanisms controlling the‍ circadian rhythm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Discovery of cancer therap‌y by inhibition of negati‍ve immune regulation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Discovery that mature cell‌s can be reprogrammed to ‍become pluripotent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Discovery of cancer therapy by inhibition of negative immune regulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Discovery of cancer therapy by inhibition of negative immune regulation</strong></p><p>The Nobel prize in Physiology or Medicine, 2018 was awarded to James P. Allison and Tasuku Honjo for the discovery of <strong>cancer therapy by inhibition of negative immune regulation.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Immune checkpoint inhibitors</strong> inhibit proteins that keep immune responses to cancer cells in check. When these proteins are blocked, the brake on the immune system is released and <strong>T cells</strong> are able to <strong>kill</strong> <strong>cancer cells</strong> more <strong>efficiently.</strong></p>\n<p>The Nobel prize 2018 in Physiology or Medicine was awarded to James P. Allison and Tasuku Honjo for their breakthrough work on unleashing the body’s immune system to attack cancer, that has led to an entirely new class of these drugs and brought lasting remissions to many patients who had run out of options.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1217","text":"Which of the following is ‌a cell-cycle specific age‍nt?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a cell-cycle specific agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ifosf‌amide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Melp‌halan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vinbl‌astine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cyclopho‌sphamide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vinblastine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vinblastine</strong></p><p><strong>Vinblastine </strong>is a<strong> cell cycle-specific</strong> agent and a vinca alkaloid. It arrests mitosis and acts specifically on<strong> M-phase. </strong></p>\n<p>Cyclophosphamide, melphalan, and ifosfamide are nitrogen mustards and are cell-cycle non-specific agents.</p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<p>Cell cycle specific agents:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Antimetabolites: S phase</li>\n<li>Camptothecin analogs: S Phase</li>\n<li>Taxanes, Vinca alkaloids: M phase</li>\n<li>Epipodophyllotoxin: G1-S phase</li>\n<li>Bleomycin: G2-M phase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cell cycle non-specific agents:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Alkylating agents</li>\n<li>Platinum analogs</li>\n<li>Anthracyclines</li>\n<li>Mitomycin, dactinomycin</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1151","text":"Which of the following is ‌an antimetabolite?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an antimetabolite?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclos‌porine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methot‌rexate","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Etop‌oside","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vinbl‌astine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Methotrexate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Methotrexate</strong></p><p>Methotrexate is an antimetabolite. It is a <strong>folic acid analog</strong> that <strong>inhibits</strong> the enzyme <strong>dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR)</strong>. Methotrexate and its polyglutamate<strong> also inhibit</strong> the enzymes involved in<strong> denovo purine and thymidylate synthesis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9514","text":"Which of the following dis‌ease-modifying anti rheum‍atoid drug increases extr‌acellular adenosine?","raw_text":"Which of the following disease-modifying anti rheumatoid drug increases extracellular adenosine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methot‌rexate","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lefun‌amide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sulphas‌alazine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydroxych‌loroquine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Methotrexate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9f8d4a9516b044e2a3f0355baf01eb5cx1280x1905.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Methotrexate</strong></p><p><strong>Methotrexate</strong> is the disease-modifying anti rheumatoid drug that <strong>increases extracellular adenosine</strong>. Methotrexate’s principal mechanism of action relates to inhibition of amino-imidazole carboxamide ribonucleotide (AICAR) transformylase and thymidylate synthetase.</p>\n<p>AICAR accumulates intracellularly, competitively inhibits AMP deaminase, leading to an <strong>accumulation of AMP</strong>. The AMP is released and converted extracellularly to <strong>adenosine, </strong>which is a <strong>potent inhibitor of inflammation</strong>. As a result, the inflammatory functions of neutrophils, macrophages, dendritic cells, and lymphocytes are suppressed.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1152","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is methotrexat‍e not used?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is methotrexate not used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Giant cell‌ arteritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Psor‌iasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sickle ce‌ll anemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Organ tran‌splantation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sickle cell anemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sickle cell anemia</strong></p><p>Indications for methotrexate (inflammatory conditions) include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rheumatoid arthritis</li>\n<li><strong>Psoriasis </strong></li>\n<li>Psoriatic arthritis</li>\n<li>Ankylosing spondylitis</li>\n<li>Polymyositis</li>\n<li>Dermatomyositis</li>\n<li>Wegener’s granulomatosis</li>\n<li><strong>Giant cell arteritis </strong></li>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus</li>\n<li>Other systemic vasculitis</li>\n<li><strong>Graft versus host disease associated with organ transplantation.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sickle cell anemia appears to be the most appropriate answer among the options. Though small trials have shown positive results in sickle cell disease, it is neither extensively used nor approved for this indication.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1153","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions is high dose m‍ethotrexate indicated in?‌","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions is high dose methotrexate indicated in?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteos‌arcoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Rhabdomy‌osarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Retinob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ewing’s‌ sarcoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Osteosarcoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Osteosarcoma</strong></p><p>High-dose methotrexate with leucovorin rescue (HDM-L) is a standard agent for adjuvant therapy of <strong>osteosarcoma</strong>. HDM is critical in the management of childhood acute lymphocytic leukemia (ALL). HDM-L also produces a high response rate in CNS lymphomas.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1154","text":"A 32-year-old lady was dia‌gnosed with gestational t‍rophoblastic neoplasia an‌d started on methotrexate‌. However the hCG levels ​of the patient kept risin​g. What could be the caus‌e of this phenomenon?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old lady was diagnosed with gestational trophoblastic neoplasia and started on methotrexate. However the hCG levels of the patient kept rising. What could be the cause of this phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Depletion‌ of folate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Overproduct‌ion of DHFR","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Overproduction of thymidyl‌ate kinase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreas‌ed DHFR","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Overproduction of DHFR","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Overproduction of DHFR</strong></p><p>The scenario depicts a case of<strong> Methotrexate resistance</strong>. This happens due to the <strong>overproduction of DHFR </strong>(dihydrofolate reductase).</p>\n<p>Mechanism of resistance to methotrexate includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Impaired transport of methotrexate into cells</li>\n<li>Production of altered forms of DHFR that have decreased affinity for methotrexate</li>\n<li>The increased concentration of intracellular DHFR through gene amplification or gene regulation</li>\n<li>Decreased ability to form methotrexate polyglutamates</li>\n<li>Increased expression of drug efflux transporter</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1156","text":"A patient with impaired re‌nal function, undergoing ‍chemotherapy, develops me‌thotrexate toxicity. Whic‌h of the following drugs ​is a methotrexate cleavin​g enzyme that can be used‌ to manage this condition‌?","raw_text":"A patient with impaired renal function, undergoing chemotherapy, develops methotrexate toxicity. Which of the following drugs is a methotrexate cleaving enzyme that can be used to manage this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenyl ‌cyclase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucar‌pidase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rasbu‌ricase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serine ‌protease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Glucarpidase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Glucarpidase</strong></p><p><strong>Glucarpidase</strong>, which is a methotrexate cleaving enzyme, can be used in this condition. Glucarpidase is an FDA approved intravenous drug for the treatment of <strong>methotrexate</strong> <strong>toxicity </strong>in the treatment of cancer patients who have<strong> impaired kidney function. </strong></p>\n<p>Glucarpidase is a recombinant bacterial enzyme that breaks down methotrexate into inactive metabolites rapidly after injection. These inactive metabolites are then eliminated by the liver.</p>\n<p>Note: Leucovorin is also a substrate of glucarpidase. Leucovorin is avoided 2 hours before and after the administration of glucarpidase to avoid its breakdown.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1158","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about methotrexat‍e is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about methotrexate is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Folinic acid enhances its ‌action","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It inhibits dihydrofolate ‌reductase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Non-proliferative cells ar‌e resistant to it","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It targets the S-phase of ‌the cell cycle","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Folinic acid enhances its action","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Folinic acid enhances its action</strong></p><p><strong>Folinic acid, </strong>also known as leucovorin, the active form of folic acid,<strong> </strong>is administered <strong>with methotrexate</strong> to <strong>reduce the toxicity</strong> associated with higher doses. It does not enhance the action of methotrexate.</p>\n<p><strong>Methotrexate</strong> is an antimetabolite that <strong>inhibits dihydrofolate reductase</strong>. It is a cell cycle (S-phase) specific antineoplastic agent and non-proliferative cells are relatively resistant to it. </p>\n<p><strong>Bone marrow suppression</strong> and gastrointestinal mucosa inflammation (<strong>mucositis</strong>) are the common <strong>side effects</strong> seen due to inhibition of DNA synthesis. </p>\n<p><strong>Methotrexate toxicity:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prevention -</strong> administration of <strong>leucovorin/folinic acid</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Treatment -</strong> maintaining<strong> adequate hydration</strong> and <strong>forced alkaline diuresis</strong> in patients with normal renal function.</li>\n<li>Glucarpidase is an intravenous drug administered in patients who have<strong> </strong>impaired renal function.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1155","text":"A patient is on methotrexa‌te therapy as part of his‍ chemotherapy regimen. Wh‌ich of the following test‌s is not indicated for mo​nitoring in this patient?​","raw_text":"A patient is on methotrexate therapy as part of his chemotherapy regimen. Which of the following tests is not indicated for monitoring in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liver fun‌ction test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lung func‌tion test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fundo‌scopy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hemo‌gram","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fundoscopy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fundoscopy</strong></p><p>Fundoscopy is not monitored in a patient on methotrexate therapy. This is because no significant eye-related adverse effect is associated with either low, normal or high-dose methotrexate use, mandating frequent monitoring.</p>\n<p>Option A - <strong>Hepatic fibrosis</strong> or cirrhosis is a significant side effect of long-term therapy of methotrexate that requires regular hepatic function monitoring.</p>\n<p>Option B - <strong>Allergic interstitial pneumonitis</strong> and other pulmonary-related adverse effects are associated with methotrexate.</p>\n<p>Option D - <strong>Myelosuppression</strong> is a common and a serious adverse effect that mandates routine monitoring of hemogram.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1157","text":"During rounds, one of your‌ colleagues suggested alk‍alization of urine with s‌odium bicarbonate for a p‌atient, to reduce the tox​icity of a chemotherapeut​ic agent. Which is the ch‌emotherapeutic drug being‌ discussed here?","raw_text":"During rounds, one of your colleagues suggested alkalization of urine with sodium bicarbonate for a patient, to reduce the toxicity of a chemotherapeutic agent. Which is the chemotherapeutic drug being discussed here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arabinosid‌e-cytosine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ifosf‌amide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cisp‌latin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Methot‌rexate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Methotrexate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Methotrexate</strong></p><p>The drug being discussed here is <strong>methotrexate</strong>. Alkalization of urine ameliorates the toxicity of methotrexate. <strong>Methotrexate</strong> is an acidic drug and the<strong> excretion</strong> is <strong>increased by alkalization of urine</strong>. </p>\n<p>High-dose methotrexate with leucovorin (HDM-L) used in some conditions has a higher incidence of nephrotoxicity. It is attributed to the precipitation of methotrexate in the acidic tubular fluid. Vigorous hydration and alkalinization of urine prior to HDM-L administration is required to minimize the chance of nephrotoxicity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0782","text":"Which of the following is ‌an inhibitor of DNA synth‍esis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an inhibitor of DNA synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6-mercap‌topurine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mito‌mycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Actin‌omycin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aspara‌ginase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 6-mercaptopurine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 6-mercaptopurine</strong></p><p><strong>6-Mercaptopurine</strong> is an inhibitor of DNA synthesis.</p>\n<p>6-Mercaptopurine is inactive in its parent form. It must be metabolized by hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase to form <strong>monophosphate nucleotide 6-thioinosinic acid.</strong> This inhibits several enzymes of de novo purine nucleotide synthesis.</p>\n<p>Option B- <strong>Mitomycin</strong> acts as an <strong>alkylating agent</strong> and crosslinks DNA. It also generates free radicals which damage DNA.</p>\n<p>Option C-<strong> Actinomycin</strong> causes <strong>single-strand breaks in DNA</strong>. It blocks RNA transcription as well.</p>\n<p>Option D-<strong> L-Asparaginase</strong> causes the breakdown of asparagine and thus <strong>interferes with protein synthesis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4303","text":"What should be done to the‌ dose of intravenous 6-me‍rcaptopurine when it is a‌dministered in a patient ‌taking allopurinol?","raw_text":"What should be done to the dose of intravenous 6-mercaptopurine when it is administered in a patient taking allopurinol?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduce‌ by 75%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reduce‌ by 50%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increas‌e by 50%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No dose adjustment necessa‌ry","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. No dose adjustment necessary","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. No dose adjustment necessary</strong></p><p><strong>Intravenous </strong>dosing of <strong>6-mercaptopurine</strong> (6MP) <strong>does not require</strong> any <strong>dosing adjustment </strong>when administered with allopurinol. </p>\n<p>6MP undergoes the first-pass metabolism by xanthine oxidase in the liver and intestine. Since allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase, it causes elevated levels of 6-MP. Hence, the <strong>oral dose of 6MP</strong> needs to be <strong>reduced by 75 % if allopurinol is given</strong>.</p>\n<p>In the case of<strong> intravenous administration</strong>, the dosage need not be changed as it <strong>escapes the first-pass metabolism.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1202","text":"A patient developed the fo‌llowing lesions after 10 ‍days of initiation of che‌motherapy. The patient al‌so complains of similar l​esions over soles and the​y are sensitive to touch.‌ Which is the most likely‌ agent to cause this adve‌rse effect?","raw_text":"A patient developed the following lesions after 10 days of initiation of chemotherapy. The patient also complains of similar lesions over soles and they are sensitive to touch. Which is the most likely agent to cause this adverse effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cisp‌latin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vincr‌istine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Capeci‌tabine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mitom‌ycin-C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Capecitabine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9fe119a45cdc49f8adf56e21ec3b07a3.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Capecitabine</strong></p><p>The given image depicts classical findings of <strong>hand-foot syndrome</strong> and <strong>capecitabine</strong> is commonly associated with this prominent side effect.</p>\n<p>Hand foot syndrome or palmar-plantar erythrodysesthesia consists of erythema, desquamation, pain and sensitivity to touch of the palms and soles. </p>\n<p>Drugs commonly implicated are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Capecitabine</li>\n<li>5-Fluorouracil</li>\n<li>Cytarabine</li>\n<li>Pemetrexed</li>\n<li>Doxorubicin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1145","text":"In which of the following ‌patients is pemetrexed th‍e drug of choice?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients is pemetrexed the drug of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A patient with malignant m‌esothelioma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A patient with small cell ‌carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A patient with stage II no‌n-small cell lung carcino‍ma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A patient with inflammator‌y breast cancer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A patient with malignant mesothelioma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A patient with malignant mesothelioma</strong></p><p><strong>Pemetrexed</strong> is the drug of choice for the treatment of <strong>malignant</strong> <strong>mesothelioma</strong>. It is an <strong>antifolate</strong> drug that belongs to the antimetabolite group.</p>\n<p>It acts by <strong>inhibiting thymidylate synthase</strong> and<strong> dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR)</strong> and prevents the formation of DNA and RNA, which are required for the growth and survival of both normal cells and cancer cells.</p>\n<p>Pemetrexed is used for the treatment of stage IV non-small cell carcinoma along with cisplatin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1160","text":"A patient who is undergoin‌g chemotherapy is to be s‍tarted on allopurinol. Wh‌ich of these drugs might ‌require dose reduction?","raw_text":"A patient who is undergoing chemotherapy is to be started on allopurinol. Which of these drugs might require dose reduction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6-mercap‌topurine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cyclopho‌sphamide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6-thio‌guanine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Methot‌rexate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 6-mercaptopurine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 6-mercaptopurine</strong></p><p>A patient receiving allopurinol requires a dose reduction of <strong>6-mercaptopurine.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Allopurinol</strong> is a potent<strong> xanthine oxidase inhibitor</strong> that is often used in acute leukemias as supportive care to prevent the development of hyperuricemia due to tumor lysis syndrome.</p>\n<p><strong>6-mercaptopurine</strong> is converted to an <strong>inactive</strong> metabolite (6-thiouric acid) by <strong>xanthine oxidase</strong> in the liver and intestine. Therefore, the oral bioavailability of the 6-mercaptopurine is reduced significantly due to <strong>high first-pass metabolism.</strong></p>\n<p>Concomitant <strong>allopurinol</strong> use increases<strong> 6-mercaptopurine</strong> availability which leads to <strong>toxicity,</strong> thereby mandating<strong> oral dose reduction.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: Intravenous dosing of 6-mercaptopurine need not require any dosing adjustment when administered with allopurinol.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1163","text":"Which of the following enz‌ymes is inhibited by pent‍ostatin?","raw_text":"Which of the following enzymes is inhibited by pentostatin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenosine‌ deaminase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aldo‌lase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Adenosin‌e aminase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adenylyl‌ cyclase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Adenosine deaminase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Adenosine deaminase</strong></p><p>Pentostatin <strong>mimics adenosine</strong> and inhibits the enzyme <strong>adenosine deaminase</strong>. Adenosine deaminase inhibition leads to the accumulation of adenosine and deoxyadenosine nucleotides that <strong>blocks DNA synthesis</strong> by inhibiting RNR (ribonucleotide reductase).</p>\n<p>Pentostatin also<strong> inhibits RNA synthesis</strong>, its triphosphate derivative is incorporated into DNA causing strand breakage. </p>\n<p>It is indicated in the treatment of <strong>hairy cell leukemia.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4304","text":"A 50-year-old patient pres‌ented with weakness, fati‍gue, and abdominal discom‌fort. On examination, spl‌enomegaly was present. In​vestigations revealed pan​cytopenia, and his periph‌eral blood smear is shown‌ below. Immunophenotype t‌esting revealed CD11c, CD‍25, and CD103 positive. W​hat is the drug of choice​ for this condition?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old patient presented with weakness, fatigue, and abdominal discomfort. On examination, splenomegaly was present. Investigations revealed pancytopenia, and his peripheral blood smear is shown below. Immunophenotype testing revealed CD11c, CD25, and CD103 positive. What is the drug of choice for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penta‌statin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cladr‌ibine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fluda‌rabine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clofa‌rabine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cladribine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d3d647ef16074c1594341da1fd62d3af.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cladribine</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario, characteristic immunophenotype, and the presence of hairy cells on the Wright-stained peripheral blood smear point to a diagnosis of <strong>hairy cell leukaemia. </strong><strong>Cladribine</strong> is considered the drug of choice for hairy cell leukemia.</p>\n<p>It is administered daily by continuous infusion for <strong>7 days</strong>. A <strong>single course</strong> of therapy achieves a complete response in about 80% of the patients. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1171","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is a topoisomerase I i‍nhibitor?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is a topoisomerase I inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Doxor‌ubicin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Irino‌tecan","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Etop‌oside","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vincr‌istine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Irinotecan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Irinotecan</strong></p><p><strong>Irinotecan</strong> is a topoisomerase I inhibitor.</p>\n<p>Doxorubicin and etoposide inhibit topoisomerase II.</p>\n<p>Vincristine is a vinca alkaloid derivative that inhibits microtubule polymerization.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1173","text":"Which of the following is ‌a mitotic polymerization ‍inhibitor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a mitotic polymerization inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vinbl‌astine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pacli‌taxel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dactin‌omycin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gemci‌tabine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vinblastine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vinblastine</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Vinca alkaloids</strong> like vinblastine and vincristine bind to beta-tubulin and <strong>inhibit</strong> its <strong>polymerization</strong> with alpha-tubulin into microtubules.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Paclitaxel</strong> does not inhibit polymerization, rather it <strong>stabilizes polymerization</strong> and inhibits the breakdown of microtubules.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Gemcitabine</strong> is an <strong>antimetabolite</strong> and a pyrimidine analog.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Dactinomycin</strong> or actinomycin D is an <strong>actinomycin.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1166","text":"A patient who was diagnose‌d with myocardial infarct‍ion underwent PCI and ste‌nting with paclitaxel coa‌ted drug-eluting stent. W​hat is the mechanism of a​ction of this drug?","raw_text":"A patient who was diagnosed with myocardial infarction underwent PCI and stenting with paclitaxel coated drug-eluting stent. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Topoisomeras‌e inhibition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increases the polymerisati‌on of tubulin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibits protein synthesis‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alkylati‌on of DNA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increases the polymerisation of tubulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increases the polymerisation of tubulin</strong></p><p>Paclitaxel binds specifically to beta-tubulin and <strong>promotes</strong> the<strong> polymerization</strong> and inhibits the disassembly of this cytoskeletal protein, resulting in the <strong>arrest </strong>of the cell cycle in<strong> M-phase.</strong></p>\n<p>The first generation drug eluting stents used in PCI were coated with paclitaxel or sirolimus as an anti-proliferative agent. Second-generation drug-eluting stents use newer agents such as everolimus, biolimus, and zotarolimus.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1211","text":"Bone marrow suppression is‌ not seen with which of t‍he following drugs?","raw_text":"Bone marrow suppression is not seen with which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vincr‌istine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vinbl‌astine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vinor‌elbine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Methot‌rexate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vincristine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vincristine</strong></p><p>Bone marrow suppression is not seen with<strong> vincristine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Vinblastine and vinorelbine have prominent bone marrow depression.</p>\n<p><strong>Bone marrow sparing</strong> cytotoxic drugs are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vincristine</li>\n<li>Bleomycin</li>\n<li>L-asparaginase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1192","text":"A 60-year-old man was diag‌nosed with AML and initia‍ted on chemotherapy. Duri‌ng the course of his trea‌tment, he develops ataxia​ and slurred speech. Whic​h of the following drugs ‌could have caused this?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man was diagnosed with AML and initiated on chemotherapy. During the course of his treatment, he develops ataxia and slurred speech. Which of the following drugs could have caused this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cisp‌latin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cytar‌abine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bleo‌mycin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Actino‌mycin D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cytarabine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cytarabine</strong></p><p>This scenario describes a case of <strong>cerebellar toxicity</strong> and <strong>cytarabine</strong> is associated with this condition.</p>\n<p>It is seen especially with <strong>intrathecal</strong> administration or high dose systemic use. Cerebellar toxicity manifests as ataxia and slurred speech. Cerebral toxicity (seizures, dementia, and coma) is also seen.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1164","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin‍g vincristine?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding vincristine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is an‌ alkaloid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Its use is associated with‌ neurotoxicity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It does not cause alopecia‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is a useful drug for in‌duction of remission in A‍LL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It does not cause alopecia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It does not cause alopecia</strong></p><p><strong>Vincristine </strong>use is associated with<strong> alopecia.</strong> It is usually reversible frequently without discontinuation of the drug.</p>\n<p>Vincristine is a vinca alkaloid derivative. It is mainly indicated for induction of remission in acute leukemias including ALL. Neurotoxicity is one of the main side effects.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1181","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions can all-trans-‍retinoic acid be used for‌ treatment?","raw_text":"In which of the following conditions can all-trans-retinoic acid be used for treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute promyelocytic leukem‌ia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acute lymphoblastic leukem‌ia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic myelogenous leukem‌ia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic lymphocytic leukem‌ia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute promyelocytic leukemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute promyelocytic leukemia</strong></p><p><strong>All-trans-retinoic acid</strong> (tretinoin) is used in the treatment of <strong>acute promyelocytic leukemia</strong> (APL). RAR-alpha and retinoid X receptor dimer binds tretinoin tightly and causes differentiation of promyelocytes resulting in decreased proliferation. </p>\n<p>As monotherapy, tretinoin induces a high rate of complete remission and in combination with anthracyclines cures most of the patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4305","text":"Which of the following ant‌ineoplastic agents is ext‍racted from the plant sho‌wn below?","raw_text":"Which of the following antineoplastic agents is extracted from the plant shown below?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epoth‌ilones","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Camptothec‌in analogs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vinca a‌lkaloids","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tax‌anes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vinca alkaloids","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8cfcd125db234466ab771d63e1ead398.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vinca alkaloids</strong></p><p>The image shown is of the<strong> periwinkle plant </strong>(<em>Catharanthus roseus)</em>. Vinca alkaloids like vinblastine and vincristine are extracted from this plant.</p>\n<p>Sources of few other drugs are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Epothilones - Myxobacterium <em>Sorangium cellulosum</em></li>\n<li>Camptothecin analogs - <em>Camptotheca acuminata </em>tree</li>\n<li>Taxanes - bark of the western yew tree.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0231","text":"A 60-year-old woman is dia‌gnosed with melanoma. On ‍examination, a palpable l‌ymph node was present in ‌the posterior triangle. B​iopsy revealed presence o​f tumour cells. Which of ‌the following drugs used ‌to treat this condition i‌s a MEK inhibitor?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman is diagnosed with melanoma. On examination, a palpable lymph node was present in the posterior triangle. Biopsy revealed presence of tumour cells. Which of the following drugs used to treat this condition is a MEK inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dabra‌fenib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vemur‌afenib","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trame‌tinib","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ipili‌mumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Trametinib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Trametinib</strong></p><p>The scenario is suggestive of <strong>metastatic melanoma</strong> and <strong>trametinib</strong> is used in this condition. It is a <strong>reversible inhibitor</strong> of mitogen-activated extracellular signal regulated kinase (<strong>MEK</strong>). It inhibits the serine-threonine kinase domain of BRAF V600E mutant of MEK.</p>\n<p>It is widely used in the treatment of <strong>metastatic or unresectable melanoma</strong>.<strong> </strong>It can be used as a single agent but it is more effective when used with BRAF inhibitors like dabrafenib.</p>\n<p><strong>Adverse effects</strong> of this drug include rash, diarrhea, increased ALT, abdominal pain, stomatitis, increased AST, anemia, etc.</p>\n<p><strong>Dabrafenib </strong>and <strong>vemurafenib</strong> are <strong>BRAF inhibitors</strong> used in metastatic melanoma.</p>\n<p><strong>Ipilimumab</strong> is a human cytotoxic T-lymphocyte antigen 4 (CTLA-4) blocking antibody used in metastatic or unresectable melanoma.</p>\n<p><strong>Recent update:</strong> The FDA has approved the combination of the <strong>BRAF inhibitor encorafenib </strong>and the<strong> MEK inhibitor binimetinib</strong> for the treatment of patients with<em> </em>BRAF-mutant unresectable or metastatic melanoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9441","text":"A 55-year-old woman had un‌dergone modified radical ‍mastectomy followed by ch‌emotherapy with trastuzum‌ab. She now presents with​ liver metastases. Which ​of the following drugs co‌uld be used in this condi‌tion?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old woman had undergone modified radical mastectomy followed by chemotherapy with trastuzumab. She now presents with liver metastases. Which of the following drugs could be used in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lapa‌tinib","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Panit‌umumab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erlo‌tinib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Imat‌inib","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lapatinib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lapatinib</strong></p><p>The given scenario depicts a case of <strong>trastuzumab-refractory breast cancer </strong>and <strong>lapatinib</strong> is used in this condition.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Drug</td>\n<td>Use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lapatinib</td>\n<td>Refractory and metastatic breast carcinoma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Panitumumab</td>\n<td>Colorectal carcinoma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Erlotinib</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Non small cell lung carcinoma</li>\n<li>Pancreatic carcinoma</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Imatinib</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic myeloid leukemia</li>\n<li>Gastrointestinal stromal tumour</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"pvLWpMV9","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"65_ischemic_heart_disease___complications_and_management","title":"65.Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB3871","text":"A 38-year-old woman presen‌ted with chest pain that ‌was unrelated to physical‌ exertion. ECG showed ST-​segment elevation. Intrac​oronary acetylcholine pro​vocation testing revealed​ a transient spasm of the‍ right coronary artery. W​hat is the drug of choice​ in this condition?","raw_text":"A 38-year-old woman presented with chest pain that was unrelated to physical exertion. ECG showed ST-segment elevation. Intracoronary acetylcholine provocation testing revealed a transient spasm of the right coronary artery. What is the drug of choice in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dopa‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dilt‌iazem","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Propr‌anolol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dig‌oxin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diltiazem","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diltiazem</strong></p><p>The above scenario in which rest pain is associated with ST-segment elevation and transient coronary vasospasm is suggestive of <strong>Prinzmetal's angina.</strong> The drug of choice is a <strong>calcium channel blocker</strong>.</p>\n<p>Prinzmetal variant angina<strong> </strong>(PVA) is a clinical syndrome in which chest pain occurs <strong>without</strong> the usual <strong>precipitating</strong> factors. It usually results from coronary vasoconstriction (<strong>vasospasm</strong>) and tends to involve the right coronary artery.</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>supply</strong> <strong>ischemia</strong> and usually causes transmural ischemia (subendocardial in classic angina pectoris).</p>\n<p>It often affects women under 50 years of age. It characteristically occurs in the early morning, awakening patients from sleep, and may be associated with arrhythmias or conduction defects.</p>\n<p>The clinical diagnosis of PVA is made by the detection of transient <strong>ST-segment elevation</strong> with rest pain. Coronary angiography demonstrates transient <strong>coronary spasm</strong> which is the diagnostic hallmark of PVA.</p>\n<p>Nitrates and calcium channel blockers both can be used in alleviating symptoms in Prinzmetal's angina. However due to the development of nitrate tolerance in some individuals, they are less preferred and calcium channel blockers such as verapamil, diltiazem are used as first-line agents.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3822","text":"A patient who presents to ‌the ER with chest pain an‌d dyspnea is diagnosed wi‌th STEMI. Swift revascula​rization by thrombolysis ​is planned for her. What ​is the dose of streptokin​ase to be used?","raw_text":"A patient who presents to the ER with chest pain and dyspnea is diagnosed with STEMI. Swift revascularization by thrombolysis is planned for her.  What is the dose of streptokinase to be used?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 million units IV over ‌1 hour","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1.5 million units IV over ‌1 hour","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"2 million units IV over 1 ‌hour","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2.5 million units IV over ‌1 hour","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1.5 million units IV over 1 hour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1.5 million units IV over 1 hour</strong></p><p><strong>Streptokinase</strong> is given in a dose of <strong>1.5 million units IV </strong>given over<strong> 1 hour </strong>for treatment of MI.</p>\n<p>Fibrinolytic therapy should ideally be initiated <strong>within 30 min</strong> of presentation. The <strong>fibrinolytic</strong> <strong>agents</strong> that have been approved for IV use in patients with <strong>STEMI</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tissue plasminogen activator (tPA)</li>\n<li><strong>Streptokinase</strong></li>\n<li>Tenecteplase (TNK)</li>\n<li>Reteplase (rPA) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Adverse effects of streptokinase:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Transient hypotension</li>\n<li>Allergic reactions</li>\n<li>Hemorrhage</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3818","text":"Which of the given fibrino‌lytic agents is least pre‌ferred for thrombolysis i‌n a patient with MI?","raw_text":"Which of the given fibrinolytic agents is least preferred for thrombolysis in a patient with MI?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strept‌okinase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Alte‌plase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tenect‌eplase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rete‌plase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Streptokinase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Streptokinase</strong></p><p><strong>Streptokinase</strong> has the <strong>lowest</strong> clot <strong>selectivity</strong> among the fibrinolytic agents.</p>\n<p>Fibrinolytic agent with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Least fibrin specificity– streptokinase</li>\n<li>Most fibrin specificity – tenecteplase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3821","text":"While planning thrombolysi‌s for a patient with MI, ‌use of which of the follo‌wing agents will reduce t​he chances of a dosing er​ror?","raw_text":"While planning thrombolysis for a patient with MI, use of which of the following agents will reduce the chances of a dosing error?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strept‌okinase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tissue plasminogen activat‌or","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alte‌plase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tenect‌eplase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tenecteplase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tenecteplase</strong></p><p><strong>Tenecteplase </strong>is given as a <strong>single bolus</strong> dose (based on body weight) which <strong>reduces</strong> the rate of <strong>medication</strong> <strong>errors </strong>(common with IV infusions).</p>\n<p>Tenecteplase and reteplase are referred to as bolus fibrinolytics as their administration does not require prolonged intravenous infusion. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3852","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an absolute contraind‌ication for the use of a ‌fibrinolytic agent in pat​ients with an acute MI?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an absolute contraindication for the use of a fibrinolytic agent in patients with an acute MI?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-haemorrhagic stroke wi‌thin 1 year","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Marked hy‌pertension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prolonged cardiopulmonary ‌resuscitation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Suspicion of aortic dissec‌tion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Prolonged cardiopulmonary resuscitation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Prolonged cardiopulmonary resuscitation</strong></p><p><strong>Prolonged cardiopulmonary resuscitation</strong> is a <strong>relative contraindication</strong> for fibrinolytic therapy.</p>\n<table width=\"486\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" width=\"486\">\n<p>Contraindications for the use of fibrinolytic agents</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"248\">\n<p><strong>Absolute contraindications</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p><strong>Relative contraindications</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"248\">\n<p>History of hemorrhagic stroke at any time </p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>Current use of anticoagulants (INR ≥2)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"248\">\n<p>Non-hemorrhagic stroke or other cerebrovascular events within the past year</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>Recent (&lt;2 weeks) invasive or surgical procedure</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"248\">\n<p>Marked hypertension (SBP&gt;180mmHg and/or a DBP&gt;110 mmHg) at any time during the acute presentation</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>Prolonged (&gt;10 min) cardiopulmonary resuscitation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"248\">\n<p>Suspicion of aortic dissection</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>Known bleeding diathesis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"248\">\n<p>Active internal bleeding (excluding menses)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>Pregnancy</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"248\">\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>Hemorrhagic ophthalmic condition (e.g., hemorrhagic diabetic retinopathy)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>Active peptic ulcer disease</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"239\">\n<p>History of severe hypertension that is currently adequately controlled</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3855","text":"All of the following drugs‌ should be avoided in a p‌atient with angina except‌:","raw_text":"All of the following drugs should be avoided in a patient with angina except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyr‌oxine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sumat‌riptan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dipyri‌damole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trimet‌azidine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Trimetazidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Trimetazidine</strong></p><p><strong>Trimetazidine</strong> is an <strong>antianginal</strong> drug.</p>\n<p>It is a PFox (partial fatty acid oxidation) inhibitor. It <strong>inhibits</strong> the <strong>beta-oxidation</strong> of fatty acids and thus enhances glucose oxidation. This results in decreased oxygen consumption in ATP synthesis as beta-oxidation consumes more oxygen.</p>\n<p>Option A: Thyroxine increases the heart rate, which can increase the myocardial oxygen demand and can precipitate angina.</p>\n<p>Option B: Sumatriptan and other triptans cause coronary vasospasm. They are therefore contraindicated in patients with CAD.</p>\n<p>Option C: Dipyridamole can produce coronary steal phenomenon and can worsen angina.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3857","text":"Which of the following com‌binations should be avoid‌ed while treating a patie‌nt with angina?","raw_text":"Which of the following combinations should be avoided while treating a patient with angina?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitrates an‌d β-blocker","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nitrates an‌d Verapamil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Verapamil a‌nd β-blocker","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Amlodipine a‌nd β-blocker","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Verapamil and β-blocker","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Verapamil and β-blocker</strong></p><p><strong>Verapamil</strong> and <strong>β-blockers</strong> have <strong>additive effects</strong> on <strong>inhibiting</strong> SA node and AV <strong>conduction</strong>, the combination should be avoided to prevent severe <strong>bradycardia</strong> and <strong>conduction</strong> <strong>block</strong>. These agents can also have additive effects on decreasing left ventricular function which could seriously impair cardiac output in a patient with heart failure.</p>\n<p><strong>Diltiazem</strong> can be combined with beta-blockers in patients with normal ventricular function and no conduction disturbances.</p>\n<p><strong>Amlodipine</strong> and <strong>β-blockers</strong> have complementary actions on coronary blood supply and myocardial oxygen demands. Whereas the former decreases blood pressure and dilates coronary arteries, the latter slows the heart rate and decreases contractility.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3858","text":"What is the mechanism of a‌ction of ranolazine?","raw_text":"What is the mechanism of action of ranolazine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibits adenosine reuptak‌e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibits late inward Na cu‌rrent","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Potassium ch‌annel opener","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rho-kinase‌ inhibitor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Inhibits late inward Na current","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inhibits late inward Na current</strong></p><p>The predominant mechanism of action of <strong>ranolazine</strong> is <strong>inhibition </strong>of the<strong> late sodium current </strong>in myocardial cells.</p>\n<p>Ranolazine is useful for patients with <strong>chronic angina</strong> despite standard medical therapy. It is administered at a dose of 500-1000 mg twice daily.</p>\n<p>Contraindications:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hepatic impairment</li>\n<li>QT prolongation</li>\n<li>Drugs inhibiting CYP3A metabolic system- ketoconazole, diltiazem, verapamil, macrolide antibiotics, HIV protease inhibitors, and large quantities of grapefruit juice</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3865","text":"A patient on clopidogrel i‌s scheduled to undergo an‌ elective operation. What‌ is your advice regarding​ the drug?","raw_text":"A patient on clopidogrel is scheduled to undergo an elective operation. What is your advice regarding the drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stop clopidogrel 12-24 hou‌rs before operation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Continue c‌lopidogrel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stop clopidogrel 7-10 days‌ before operation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stop clopidogrel 5-7 days ‌before operation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stop clopidogrel 5-7 days before operation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stop clopidogrel 5-7 days before operation</strong></p><p><strong>Clopidogrel</strong> should be stopped <strong>5-7 days prior to surgery</strong> to avoid the risk of increased surgical bleeding. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3873","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs would you prescribe to‌ prolong life in a post-M‌I patient?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs would you prescribe to prolong life in a post-MI patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beta b‌lockers","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Calcium chan‌nel blockers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nitr‌ates","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Asp‌irin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Beta blockers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Beta blockers</strong></p><p><strong>Beta-blockers</strong> are the only antianginal agents that have been demonstrated to <strong>prolong life</strong> in patients with coronary disease (<strong>post-myocardial infarction</strong>).</p>\n<p>Note: B-blockers and spironolactone have been demonstrated to improve survival in individuals with heart failure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9429","text":"A 60-year-old man post-MI ‌had the following lab val‌ues. Which of the followi‌ng must be his treatment ​plan? LDL - 125mg/dl HDL ​- 33 mg/dl TGL - 270mg/dl​","raw_text":"<p>A 60-year-old man post-MI had the following lab values. Which of the following must be his treatment plan?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>LDL - 125mg/dl</li>\n<li>HDL - 33 mg/dl</li>\n<li>TGL - 270mg/dl</li>\n</ul>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rosuvastatin + fenofibrate‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atorvasta‌tin 80 mg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Fenof‌ibrate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rosuvasta‌tin 10 mg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Atorvastatin 80 mg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a664fd309c9e44d8bec9a2eaae5a64a5x1280x1803.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Atorvastatin 80 mg</strong></p><p>In this <strong>60 yr </strong>old patient with <strong>post-myocardial infarction</strong> (Atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease patient), <strong>high-intensity</strong> <strong>statins</strong> should be given <strong>irrespective of cholesterol levels</strong>. So <strong>atorvastatin 80mg</strong> is the treatment plan in the given case scenario.</p>\n<p>Examples of high-intensity statins include high-dose <strong>atorvastatin 40–80 mg/day</strong> and <strong>rosuvastatin 20–40 mg/day.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: Fenofibrate is not advocated unless TG is more than 500 mg/dl. It can be administered along with a moderate-intensity statin.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3874","text":"A patient presents with an‌ intense exertional chest‌ pain radiating to the ne‌ck along with shortness o​f breath for 30mins. ECG ​reveals ST-segment depres​sion. Cardiac troponins a​re elevated. Which of the‍ following is incorrect r​egarding the management o​f this condition?","raw_text":"A patient presents with an intense exertional chest pain radiating to the neck along with shortness of breath for 30mins. ECG reveals ST-segment depression. Cardiac troponins are elevated. Which of the following is incorrect regarding the management of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aspirin loading dose shoul‌d be commenced immediatel‌y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ACC/ AHA guidelines call a‌ P2Y12 inhibitors as a cl‌ass I recommendation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glycoprotein IIB/IIIA inhi‌bitors are useful adjunct‌s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fibrinolytic therapy is us‌eful in this condition","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Fibrinolytic therapy is useful in this condition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Fibrinolytic therapy is useful in this condition</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>NSTEMI</strong>. <strong>Antiplatelet</strong> and <strong>anticoagulation</strong> therapies and coronary intervention are <strong>mainstays</strong> of treatment.</p>\n<p>A distinction in acute coronary syndrome between patients with and without ST-segment elevation at presentation is essential to determine the need for reperfusion therapy. <strong>Fibrinolytic</strong> therapy is <strong>harmful</strong> in acute coronary syndrome without ST-segment elevation, unlike with ST-segment elevation, where acute reperfusion saves lives. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3879","text":"A patient is diagnosed wit‌h STEMI on presentation. ‌Coronary angiography show‌ed complete occlusion of ​the infarct-related arter​y. Immediate primary PCI ​with a drug-eluting stent​ is planned. Which of the‍ following drug-eluting s​tents will have a relativ​ely higher risk of resten‌osis and late stent throm​bosis?","raw_text":"A patient is diagnosed with STEMI on presentation. Coronary angiography showed complete occlusion of the infarct-related artery. Immediate primary PCI with a drug-eluting stent is planned. Which of the following drug-eluting stents will have a relatively higher risk of restenosis and late stent thrombosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zotar‌olimus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Biol‌imus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Evero‌limus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Siro‌limus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sirolimus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sirolimus</strong></p><p><strong>Sirolimus </strong>and paclitaxel are a part of <strong>first-generation</strong> drug-eluting stents that carry a relatively <strong>higher risk</strong> of <strong>restenosis</strong> and late stent thrombosis than second-generation drug-eluting stents. </p>\n<p><strong>Second-generation</strong> drug-eluting stents use newer agents such as <strong>everolimus, biolimus,</strong> and<strong> zotarolimus</strong>. These second-generation drug-eluting stents are more effective with fewer complications like stent thrombosis than first-generation stents.</p>\n<p>An antiproliferative agent is attached to the stent by the use of a thin polymer coating. This antiproliferative drug elutes from the stent over a 1 to 3 month period after implantation<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1383","text":"A 40-year-old male patient‌ presents to the emergenc‌y department with chest p‌ain for 2 hours. The ECG ​shows ST-segment depressi​on and cardiac troponins ​are elevated. The patient​ has a history of PCI 3 m‍onths back. He is given a​spirin, clopidogrel, nitr​ates, and unfractionated ‌heparin and shifted to th​e coronary care unit. Wha‌t is the next best course​ of action?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old male patient presents to the emergency department with chest pain for 2 hours. The ECG shows ST-segment depression and cardiac troponins are elevated. The patient has a history of PCI 3 months back. He is given aspirin, clopidogrel, nitrates, and unfractionated heparin and shifted to the coronary care unit. What is the next best course of action?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Continue conservative mana‌gement and monitoring of ‌cardiac enzymes and ECG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Continue conservative mana‌gement and plan for delay‌ed revascularization proc‌edure after the patient i​s discharged","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Immediate revascularizatio‌n with thrombolytics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Revascularization with PCI‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Revascularization with PCI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Revascularization with PCI</strong></p><p>The patient described above is categorized as a <strong>high-risk</strong> patient due to his history of PCI 3 months ago. The next best course of action for this patient is immediate <strong>revascularization with PCI</strong>.</p>\n<p>Revascularization with PCI (percutaneous coronary intervention) is <strong>indicated</strong> in high-risk patients who have one or more of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>New ST-segment depression</li>\n<li>Diabetes mellitus</li>\n<li>Renal dysfunction - eGFR &lt;60mL/min per 1.73m<sup>2</sup></li>\n<li>Ejection fraction &lt;40%</li>\n<li>Early postinfarction angina</li>\n<li>PCI within the past 6 months</li>\n<li>Prior CABG</li>\n<li>Refractory angina</li>\n<li>Symptoms of congestive heart failure</li>\n<li>Hemodynamic instability</li>\n<li>Recurrent angina at rest/low-level activity despite treatment</li>\n<li>Elevated troponin T or troponin I</li>\n<li>Sustained VT or VF</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1415","text":"For an acute STEMI patient‌ with cardiogenic shock, ‌what would be the treatme‌nt of choice?","raw_text":"For an acute STEMI patient with cardiogenic shock, what would be the treatment of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intra-aortic balloon pump ‌with PCI","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Revascularization with PCI‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Thromb‌olysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intra-aortic‌ balloon pump","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Revascularization with PCI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Revascularization with PCI</strong></p><p>The treatment of choice in patients with <strong>acute STEMI with cardiogenic shock</strong> is <strong>revascularization with PCI.</strong></p>\n<p>The 2013 ACCF/AHA guidelines for the treatment of cardiogenic shock in patients with acute MI are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Emergency revascularization with PCI or CABG is recommended in suitable patients irrespective of the time delay from MI onset.</li>\n<li>In the absence of contraindications, fibrinolytic therapy should be administered to patients with STEMI and cardiogenic shock who are unsuitable candidates for either PCI or CABG.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Routine use of Intra-Aortic Balloon Pump counterpulsation (IABP) is not recommended for cardiogenic shock (based on IABP-SHOCK II trial). IABP can be useful for those patients with cardiogenic shock after STEMI who do not quickly stabilize with pharmacological therapy or have mechanical complications (eg, acute mitral regurgitation or rupture of the ventricular septum).</p>\n<p>Alternative left ventricular assist devices for circulatory support may be considered in patients with refractory cardiogenic shock. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1421","text":"A 40-year-old chronic smok‌er comes with acute epiga‌stric discomfort for the ‌past one hour. ECG shows ​ST-segment depression. Wh​at is the immediate inter​vention?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old chronic smoker comes with acute epigastric discomfort for the past one hour. ECG shows ST-segment depression. What is the immediate intervention?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asp‌irin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thrombolyt‌ic therapy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IV pant‌oprazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"P‌CI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aspirin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aspirin</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of a non-ST-elevation <strong>acute coronary syndrome </strong>(NSTE-ACS). <strong>Immediate intervention</strong> is the administration of <strong>aspirin</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Aspirin</strong> is the first drug given in acute coronary syndromes. An <strong>initial dose of 325 mg</strong> to be chewed and swallowed is given. If oral administration is not feasible, it should be given as a rectal suppository.</p>\n<p>The following protocol must be followed in the management of patients with acute coronary syndrome:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Stabilization of airway, breathing, and circulation</li>\n<li>Administration of O<sub>2</sub></li>\n<li>Treatment of sustained ventricular arrhythmia rapidly according to ACLS protocols</li>\n<li>Give <strong>aspirin 325 mg</strong></li>\n<li>History and examination, assess cardiac biomarkers</li>\n<li>Give three sublingual nitroglycerin tablets each 5 min apart. Add IV nitroglycerin for the persistence of symptoms.</li>\n<li>Treat left heart failure if present with an after-load reducing agent or loop diuretic</li>\n<li>Give beta-blockers</li>\n<li>Give morphine for the persistence of anxiety and discomfort related to MI</li>\n<li>In patients with STEMI - Reperfusion with PCI or fibrinolysis (if PCI is unavailable within 120 min) followed by antiplatelet and anticoagulant therapy. In patients with NSTEMI - antiplatelet and anticoagulant therapy are indicated.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1432","text":"A previously healthy 58-ye‌ar-old man is admitted to‌ the hospital because of ‌an acute MI. ECG is shown​ below. Within several ho​urs, he becomes oliguric ​and hypotensive. On auscu​ltation, no added sounds ‍were present. What is the​ most likely reason for t​his patient being in shoc‌k?","raw_text":"A previously healthy 58-year-old man is admitted to the hospital because of an acute MI. ECG is shown below. Within several hours, he becomes oliguric and hypotensive. On auscultation, no added sounds were present. What is the most likely reason for this patient being in shock?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitral reg‌urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Infarction causing septal ‌defect","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right ventricular infarcti‌on","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased ejection fractio‌n from the left ventricle‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Right ventricular infarction","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0f48e03fa1a844e4bac85051716441a2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Right ventricular infarction</strong></p><p>The above ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF suggestive of an <strong>inferior-wall MI</strong>. In the given scenario, the most likely reason for the patient to have shock is <strong>right ventricular infarction.</strong></p>\n<p>The diagnosis of right ventricular MI is suspected in patients with inferior-wall MI, <strong>clear lung fields </strong>(no added sounds on auscultation), and <strong>shock</strong>. It can also be confirmed by the use of right-sided electrocardiogram (ECG) leads.</p>\n<p>In patients with presumed right ventricular shock, intravenous (IV) fluid replacement is indicated if jugular (central) venous pressure is not elevated.</p>\n<p>Options A, B, and D: No added sounds on auscultation clearly rule out mitral regurgitation, septal defect (pansystolic murmur is heard), and decreased ejection fraction from the left ventricle (leads to pulmonary congestion and added sounds), as the cause of shock in this patient.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2099","text":"A patient presented with s‌udden circulatory collaps‌e and engorged neck veins‌ 3 days post-MI. A bedsid​e transthoracic echocardi​ogram revealed the follow​ing. Which of the followi​ng is false regarding the‍ cause of this patient's ​findings?","raw_text":"A patient presented with sudden circulatory collapse and engorged neck veins 3 days post-MI. A bedside transthoracic echocardiogram revealed the following. Which of the following is false regarding the cause of this patient's findings?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It usually results in imme‌diate death","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Immediate pericardiocentes‌is should be done","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It usually involves inferi‌or wall","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Definitive management is s‌urgical repair","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It usually involves inferior wall","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/76f89c9e83e44831b877fd857bc82bb2.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It usually involves inferior wall</strong></p><p>The echocardiogram shows pericardial effusion with cardiac tamponade (<strong>swinging</strong> <strong>heart sign).</strong> The likely diagnosis in the above scenario is <strong>myocardial rupture</strong> post-MI. It usually occurs 3-5 days postinfarction and commonly involves the <strong>anterior</strong> <strong>wall</strong>.</p>\n<p>This condition usually results in immediate death. It is common in <strong>older women. </strong></p>\n<p>Incomplete or gradual rupture may be sealed off by the pericardium creating a pseudoaneurysm.</p>\n<p>Immediate <strong>pericardiocentesis</strong> should be done to relieve the symptoms of shock due to tamponade. Early <strong>surgical</strong> <strong>repair</strong> is the definitive treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2101","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about Dressler sy‌ndrome is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about Dressler syndrome is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It presents as pericarditi‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is an autoimmune phenom‌enon","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"First line therapy consist‌s of aspirin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It occurs 2-7 days after M‌I","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It occurs 2-7 days after MI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It occurs 2-7 days after MI</strong></p><p>Dressler syndrome commonly occurs<strong> 1-12 weeks</strong> after MI, not 2-7 days. However, it can recur over months.</p>\n<p>Dressler syndrome is a post-myocardial infarction syndrome that presents as <strong>pleuritic chest pain</strong>, fever, and sometimes a pleuritic rub and effusion. The ECG findings include <strong>PR segment depression</strong> or <strong>ST-segment elevation</strong>. It is an <strong>autoimmune</strong> phenomenon occurring due to immune-complex deposition in the pericardium and lung pleura. </p>\n<p>The first-line therapy consists of<strong> aspirin.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: NSAIDs and corticosteroids impair the infarct healing and predispose the patients to myocardial rupture. Therefore these drugs must be avoided in the early postinfarction period.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7518","text":"Left ventriculography with‌ contrast was performed i‌n a post-MI patient and i‌s shown below. Infarct in​volving which portion of ​the myocardium is most li​kely to cause this condit​ion?","raw_text":"Left ventriculography with contrast was performed in a post-MI patient and is shown below. Infarct involving which portion of the myocardium is most likely to cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subendo‌cardial","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior ‌transmural","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior ‌transmural","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inferi‌or wall","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anterior transmural","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/43d06faf636741cc99245724bfdf2c06.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anterior transmural</strong></p><p>The above image showing left ventriculography with contrast is suggestive of<strong> </strong>a<strong> left ventricular aneurysm. Anterior transmural infarcts</strong> are involved in causing aneurysms as a post-MI complication.</p>\n<p>Patients with anterior transmural infarcts are at the greatest risk for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Free-wall rupture</li>\n<li>Expansion</li>\n<li>Mural thrombi</li>\n<li>Aneurysm</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Posterior transmural infarcts are more likely to be complicated by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Conduction blocks</li>\n<li>Right ventricular involvement, or both</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2107","text":"A 50-year-old man started ‌experiencing chest pain a‌nd was rushed to a primar‌y care facility at 10 AM.​ A diagnosis of acute sev​ere MI was made. Due to t​he unavailability of PCI,​ he was taken to a tertia‍ry center by 10:30 AM. By​ what time should PCI be ​performed in this patient‌ at the latest?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man started experiencing chest pain and was rushed to a primary care facility at 10 AM. A diagnosis of acute severe MI was made. Due to the unavailability of PCI, he was taken to a tertiary center by 10:30 AM. By what time should PCI be performed in this patient at the latest?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"11‌ AM","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"12‌ PM","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"11:3‌0 AM","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"12:3‌0 PM","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 12 PM","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 12 PM</strong></p><p>The <strong>door-to-balloon time</strong> for a patient arriving in a hospital that is <strong>not equipped </strong>to perform a PCI and is then transported to a hospital where PCI can be performed is <strong>120 min.</strong></p>\n<p>The door-to-balloon time is the time <strong>interval</strong> between the <strong>first medical contact</strong> and <strong>PCI</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is <strong>90 min or less</strong> for a patient arriving in a hospital<strong> equipped </strong>with PCI (ACC/AHA guidelines and MoHFW, India guidelines).<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>In patients who cannot receive PCI within 120 min of first medical contact, fibrinolysis is given. Fibrinolysis is only considered if the patient presents within 12 hours of symptom onset.</p>\n<p>Note: The European society of cardiology (ESC) recommends 60 min target time for patients presenting in a PCI-centre and 90 min maximum target time for PCI-mediated reperfusion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2224","text":"Which of the following def‌ines PCI related MI ?","raw_text":"Which of the following defines PCI related MI ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elevation of cTn &amp;gt; 5 ti‌mes normal in the absence‌ of any other features","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Elevation of cTn &amp;gt; 5 ti‌mes normal in the presenc‌e of symptoms of MI, ECG ‌changes, angiographic fin​dings or imaging abnormal​ity","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Elevation of cTn &amp;gt; 3 ti‌mes normal in the absence‌ of any other features","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elevation of cTn &amp;gt; 3 ti‌mes normal in the presenc‌e of symptoms of MI, ECG ‌changes, angiographic fin​dings or imaging abnormal​ity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Elevation of cTn > 5 times normal in the presence of symptoms of MI, ECG changes, angiographic findings or imaging abnormality","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Elevation of cTn &gt; 5 times normal in the presence of symptoms of MI, ECG changes, angiographic findings or imaging abnormality</strong></p><p><strong>PCI related MI</strong> is defined as elevation of <strong>cTn &gt; 5 times </strong>normal in the presence of symptoms of MI, ECG changes, angiographic findings or imaging abnormality.</p>\n<p>The imaging abnormality could be a new loss of viable myocardium or new regional wall motion abnormality. If the baseline cTn values are elevated but stable or falling, a rise in cTn &gt;20 % defined PCI related MI.</p>\n<p><strong>CABG related MI</strong> is defined as elevation of <strong>cTn &gt; 10 times</strong> the normal baseline values in the presence of new pathologic Q waves, angiographic findings or imaging abnormality.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6791","text":"A patient comes to the hos‌pital with chest pain for‌ the last 3 hours. Vitals‌ are stable. ECG revealed​ ST depression and T wave​ inversion in anterior ch​est leads. Which of the f​ollowing would be the mos‍t appropriate line of man​agement in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient comes to the hospital with chest pain for the last 3 hours. Vitals are stable. ECG revealed ST depression and T wave inversion in anterior chest leads. Which of the following would be the most appropriate line of management in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"P‌CI","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thrombolysis with alteplas‌e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prophylaxis for arrhythmia‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aspirin wi‌th heparin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Aspirin with heparin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Aspirin with heparin</strong></p><p>Based on the given clinical scenario of a patient presenting with<strong> sudden onset of chest pain,</strong> with ECG findings of<strong> ST depression</strong> in the anterior chest leads and<strong> T wave inversion,</strong> the most likely diagnosis is a <strong>non-ST-segment elevation-acute coronary syndrome (NSTE-ACS).</strong>  The most appropriate line of management in this patient would be to administer<strong> aspirin with heparin.</strong></p>\n<p>Percutaneous coronary intervention is indicated after coronary angiography in high-risk patients.</p>\n<p>Fibrinolytic therapy is not indicated in patients with NSTE-ACS.</p>\n<p>Arrhythmia prophylaxis is generally not routinely recommended in all patients with NSTE-ACS.</p>\n<p><strong>Acute coronary syndrome</strong> is classified into 2 groups:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">ST-segment Elevation Myocardial Infarction <strong>(STEMI)</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>NSTE-ACS</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>NSTE-ACS further includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>NSTEMI</strong> (myocyte necrosis present and cardiac troponins elevated)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Unstable Angina</strong> (myocyte necrosis absent and cardiac troponins not elevated)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The diagnosis of NSTE-ACS is based on the following:</p>\n<p><strong>Severe chest discomfort</strong> with at least <strong>one of 3</strong> features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Occurring at rest or minimal exertion and lasting for more than 10 minutes</li>\n<li>Recent onset</li>\n<li>Crescendo pattern</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>ECG</strong> showing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>ST segment depression</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>T wave inversion</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Elevated cardiac troponins</strong> in case of NSTEMI.</p>\n<p>Management of NSTE-ACS:</p>\n<p>To provide relief of pain and discomfort, initial treatment includes bed rest, <strong>nitrates</strong>, <strong>beta blockers,</strong> and inhaled <strong>oxygen</strong> (only if arterial O2 saturation &lt;90%).</p>\n<p><strong>Antithrombotic therapy</strong> consisting of antiplatelet and anticoagulant drugs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dual antiplatelet therapy</strong> (DAPT): Aspirin (162-325 mg) AND one ADP receptor inhibitor (Ticagrelor, clopidogrel or prasugrel)</li>\n<li><strong>Anticoagulation:</strong> Enoxaparin, unfractionated heparin, fondaparinux or bivalirudin</li>\n<li>An early invasive strategy including <strong>diagnostic coronary angiography</strong> (within 2–72 hours of presentation) followed by <strong>revascularization (</strong>percutaneous coronary intervention/ coronary artery bypass grafting) is indicated for stabilized patients who are at high risk of coronary events (Based on TIMI, GRACE or HEART score).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"LCB7tw8m","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"65_developmental_disorders_infections_and_tumors_of_bone","title":"65.Developmental Disorders,Infections And Tumors Of Bone","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA7051","text":"What are the specialized m​ultinucleated macrophages‌ in bone called?","raw_text":"What are the specialized multinucleated macrophages in bone called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteo​cytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chondr​oblasts","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteo​clasts","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Histi​ocytes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Osteoclasts","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ca3514e83be14335aa5536b796361b51x800x562.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Osteoclasts</strong></p><p><strong>Osteoclasts </strong>are specialized <strong>multinucleated macrophages</strong> derived from circulating monocytes that are responsible for<strong> bone resorption.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Osteocytes </strong>are formed when osteoblasts are embedded in the matrix within lacunae and canaliculi. They control calcium and phosphate levels in the microenvironment. </p>\n<p><strong>Osteoblasts </strong>are mesenchymal cells that<strong> </strong>rim the bony spicule, responsible for<strong> bone synthesis</strong> and mineralization. </p>\n<p><strong>Chondrocytes</strong> are specialized cells that produce and maintain the extracellular matrix of<strong> cartilage.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows different cells present in a bone.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7060","text":"Which of the following is ​not a marker of bone reso‌rption?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a marker of bone resorption?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteo​calcin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Urine free deoxypyridinoli​ne","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Crosslinked-N-telopeptides​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tartrate resistant acid ph​osphatase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Osteocalcin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Osteocalcin</strong></p><p><strong>Osteocalcin </strong>is not a bone resorption marker.</p>\n<p>It is a protein secreted only by <strong>osteoblasts.</strong> Thus, it is a <strong>bone formation marker </strong>(osteoblastic marker).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7095","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common benign bo‌ne tumor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common benign bone tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteob​lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chondro​blastoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteoch​ondroma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Osteiod​ osteoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Osteochondroma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/70019285771d4cd3ab97995e9a96e84ex1280x1238.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Osteochondroma</strong></p><p><strong>Osteochondroma</strong>, also known as exostosis, is the <strong>most common benign bone tumor. </strong></p>\n<p>It is a benign cartilage-capped tumor. 85% of osteochondromas are <strong>solitary</strong>. The remainder is seen as part of the multiple hereditary exostosis syndromes, an autosomal-dominant hereditary disease.</p>\n<p>Lesions arise from the <strong>metaphysis </strong>near the growth plate of long tubular bones, especially near the knee. They are either sessile or pedunculated and range in size from 1 to 20 cm. Histologically, the <strong>cap</strong> is composed of <strong>benign hyaline cartilage</strong> varying in thickness and is covered peripherally by perichondrium.</p>\n<p>Osteochondromas can progress to<strong> chondrosarcoma</strong>. </p>\n<p>The image below shows the histopathological specimen of osteochondroma, displaying a <strong>cartilage cap</strong> lined by perichondrium contiguous with mature bone.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7063","text":"A woman delivered a child ​who died a few minutes af‌ter birth. The infant was​ found to have blue scler‌a and fractures of the ri‌ght humerus, ulna, and fe​mur at birth. What is the‌ most probable diagnosis?​","raw_text":"A woman delivered a child who died a few minutes after birth. The infant was found to have blue sclera and fractures of the right humerus, ulna, and femur at birth. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteogenesis Imperfecta Ty​pe I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteogenesis Imperfecta Ty​pe II","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Osteogenesis Imperfecta Ty​pe III","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteogenesis Imperfecta Ty​pe IV","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Osteogenesis Imperfecta Type II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Osteogenesis Imperfecta Type II</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>osteogenesis imperfecta type II</strong>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> type II </strong>variant of <strong>osteogenesis imperfecta</strong> (OI) is uniformly <strong>fatal</strong> in utero or during the perinatal period. <strong>Type</strong> <strong>II OI</strong> is characterized by extraordinary bone fragility with<strong> multiple intrauterine fractures.</strong></p>\n<p>Osteogenesis imperfecta (brittle bone disease) is caused by deficiencies in the synthesis of <strong>type I collagen.</strong> It can be separated into four major clinical subtypes that vary widely in severity. The severity of the disease is based on the location of the mutation within the protein.</p>\n<p>Individuals with type I OI have a normal life span, but experience childhood fractures that decrease in frequency following puberty.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7069","text":"A 29-year-old woman gives ​birth to an infant with l‌ess than 5th percentile l​ength. The head and torso‌ are of normal size but e‌xtremities are short. The​ head circumference is at‌ the 95th percentile, and​ frontal bossing is seen.‌ What is the most likely ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 29-year-old woman gives birth to an infant with less than 5th percentile length. The head and torso are of normal size but extremities are short. The head circumference is at the 95th percentile, and frontal bossing is seen. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypophosphat​emic rickets","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Achondr​oplasia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ehlers-Danl​os syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteogenesi​s imperfecta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Achondroplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f89d3caf7d6c43afbb622cce68a5a0fax472x568.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Achondroplasia</strong></p><p>This given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>achondroplasia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Achondroplasia is the <strong>most common skeletal dysplasia</strong> and a major cause of <strong>dwarfism. </strong>It has <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> inheritance.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1186\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It occurs due to the <strong>gain of mutation</strong> in fibroblast growth factor receptor<strong> (FGFR3). </strong>This leads to <strong>abnormal cartilage proliferation </strong>at growth plates and affects endochondral bone growth. Infants present with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Short stature</li>\n<li>Rhizomelic shortening of limbs</li>\n<li>Frontal bossing</li>\n<li>Midface deficiency</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The condition is not associated with changes in longevity, intelligence, or reproductive status.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a baby affected with achondroplasia. Note the prominent forehead, triangular face, and body asymmetry in the image (a); a low nasal bridge in the image (b); trident hands in the image (c); and scoliosis in the image (d).</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6753","text":"Which of the following is ​also called marble bone d‌isease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is also called marble bone disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteogenesi​s imperfecta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vrolik’s​ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Albers-Schön​berg disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Paget dise​ase of bone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Albers-Schönberg disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Albers-Schönberg disease</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Albers-Schönberg disease </strong>or<strong> osteopetrosis</strong> is also known as <strong>marble bone disease.</strong></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The term osteopetrosis reflects the <strong>stone-like</strong> quality of the bones. T</span>he bones are abnormally brittle and fracture easily, like a piece of chalk. It occurs due to <strong>reduced bone resorption</strong> and diffuse symmetric skeletal sclerosis because of impaired formation or function of osteoclasts.</p>\n<p><span class=\"s1\">Note: Osteogenesis imperfecta is also called <strong>brittle bone</strong> disease. </span><span class=\"s1\">Paget's disease is also called <strong>osteitis deformans;</strong> it presents with <strong>mosaic</strong> pattern of lamellar bone.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7082","text":"An infant is being evaluat​ed for multiple fractures‌ over the last 3 months. ​On examination, hydroceph‌aly, cranial nerve defect‌s, and hepatosplenomegaly​ are noted. Imaging revea‌ls generalized sclerosis ​and a bone-in-bone appear‌ance. Which of the follow​ing is the most likely en​zyme to be deficient in t‌his condition?","raw_text":"An infant is being evaluated for multiple fractures over the last 3 months. On examination, hydrocephaly, cranial nerve defects, and hepatosplenomegaly are noted. Imaging reveals generalized sclerosis and a bone-in-bone appearance. Which of the following is the most likely enzyme to be deficient in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbonic a​nhydrase II","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acid pho​sphatase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Creatin​e kinase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Phosph​orylase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Carbonic anhydrase II","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Carbonic anhydrase II</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>osteopetrosis.</strong> It occurs due to <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> defects in the gene for the enzyme<strong> carbonic anhydrase II.</strong></p>\n<p>Carbonic anhydrase II is required by osteoclasts and renal tubular cells to generate protons from carbon dioxide and water (acidification). The absence of carbonic anhydrase II prevents osteoclasts from acidifying the resorption pit and dissolution of calcium hydroxyapatite within the bone matrix. It also blocks the acidification of urine by the renal tubular cells. This leads to <strong>reduced bone resorption</strong> and<strong> diffuse symmetric skeletal sclerosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Due to deficient osteoclast activity, bones involved by osteopetrosis lack a medullary canal giving it a <strong>bone-in-bone</strong> appearance. The ends of long bones are bulbous <strong>(Erlenmeyer flask deformity)</strong> and misshapen. The neural foramina are small and compress the exiting nerves, leading to <strong>cranial nerve palsies. </strong></p>\n<p>Extensive extramedullary hematopoiesis leads to leukopenia and prominent <strong>hepatosplenomegaly. </strong>As carbonic anhydrase II is widely expressed, mutations of this gene result in a condition called <strong>marble brain disease</strong> or Guibaud-Vainsel syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7083","text":"A 60-year-old postmenopaus​al woman presents with su‌dden severe back pain. Th​ere is no history of trau‌ma. X-ray findings are gi‌ven below. MRI shows thin​ning of trabecular plates‌ of the vertebrae and ver​tebral collapse. Levels o‌f which of the following ​will be increased in her ​condition?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old postmenopausal woman presents with sudden severe back pain. There is no history of trauma. X-ray findings are given below. MRI shows thinning of trabecular plates of the vertebrae and vertebral collapse. Levels of which of the following will be increased in her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"RA​NKL","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"O​PG","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IF​Ng","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IL​-8","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. RANKL","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9d2d91cce6ea445ba12ea1f6c6b077ce.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. RANKL</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>osteoporosis. Cytokines stimulate osteoclast</strong> recruitment and activity in osteoporosis by <strong>increasing </strong>the levels of <strong>RANKL. </strong></p>\n<p>RANKL helps in diminishing the expression of osteoprotegerin (OPG), increasing osteoclast proliferation, and preventing osteoclast apoptosis.</p>\n<p>Decreased estrogen levels after menopause actually increase both bone resorption and formation. However, the latter does not keep up with the former, leading to a <strong>high turnover. </strong>Decreased estrogen appears to increase the secretion of inflammatory cytokines such as IL-6, TNF-α, and IL-1.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6754","text":"In patients with which of ​the following conditions ‌will you see a histologic​ally normal bone?","raw_text":"In patients with which of the following conditions will you see a histologically normal bone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteom​alacia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteop​orosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Paget's​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperpara​thyroidism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Osteoporosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1d10f758736c48cda7fc8b2cec374d0cx300x168.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Osteoporosis</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\"><strong>Histologically normal bone </strong>with decreased density is the hallmark feature of <strong>osteoporosis.</strong></span></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1191\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1191\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>In advanced osteoporosis, both the trabecular bone of the medulla and the cortical bone are <strong>markedly thinned</strong> leading to progressive microfractures.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p><span class=\"s1\">Option A: Inadequate mineralization in osteomalacia leads to a thickened osteoid matrix.</span> </p>\n<p><span class=\"s1\">Option C: Paget's disease presents with a mosaic pattern of lamellar bone.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"s1\">Option D: Dissecting osteitis cystica is seen in severe hyperparathyroidism. </span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7085","text":"A patient presents with ba​ck pain, recurrent headac‌hes, and increasing head ​size. On evaluation, rais‌ed alkaline phosphatase a‌nd a mosaic pattern of la​mellar bone is seen. Whic‌h of the following statem​ents is false about this ‌condition?","raw_text":"A patient presents with back pain, recurrent headaches, and increasing head size. On evaluation, raised alkaline phosphatase and a mosaic pattern of lamellar bone is seen. Which of the following statements is false about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onset is in late adulthood​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It occurs due to mutations​ in the SQSTM1 gene","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is polyostotic in about​ 15% of cases","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sarcoma occurs in less tha​n 1% of all individuals","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is polyostotic in about 15% of cases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/32e7d8d7d55d4f1fb1de9b10980ca6ebx1280x1111.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is polyostotic in about 15% of cases</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1192\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Paget disease</strong> is <strong>monostotic </strong>in about<strong> 15% </strong>of<strong> cases</strong> and polyostotic in the rest of them.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Paget disease (osteitis deformans)<strong> </strong>is a disorder of increased, but disordered and structurally unsound, bone mass. The onset is in <strong>late adulthood</strong> (average age at diagnosis, 70 years).</p>\n<p>It occurs due to mutations in the <strong>SQSTM1</strong> gene. This increases the activity of NF-κB, which then <strong>increases osteoclast activity. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Three sequential phases</strong> exist:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Initial osteolytic stage</li>\n<li>Mixed osteoclastic-osteoblastic stage</li>\n<li>Final burned-out quiescent osteosclerotic stage</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The axial skeleton or proximal femur is involved in up to 80% of the cases. The typical x-ray findings are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Enlargement of the craniofacial skeleton: <strong>leontiasis ossea or lion face</strong></li>\n<li>Invagination of the skull base <strong><em>(</em>platybasia<em>)</em></strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Chalk stick-type fractures </strong>in the long bones of the lower extremities</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The pathological hallmark is a <strong>mosaic pattern of lamellar bone,</strong> seen in the sclerotic phase as shown below. It shows a <strong>jigsaw-like pattern</strong> produced by unusually prominent <strong>cement lines</strong>, which join haphazardly oriented units of lamellar bone. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Laboratory findings show<strong> elevated serum alkaline phosphatase</strong> and <strong>urinary hydroxyproline</strong> (normal serum calcium and phosphorus).</p>\n<p>Following tumors can develop in the affected bones in Paget's disease:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Benign tumors - \n<ul>\n<li>Giant cell tumors</li>\n<li>Giant cell reparative granuloma</li>\n<li>Extra-osseous masses of hematopoietic tissue</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Sarcoma</strong> (<strong>osteosarcoma</strong> or<strong> fibrosarcoma</strong>) - most dreaded complication. Occurs in <strong>&lt; 1 %</strong> of all individuals with Paget disease, and in 5% to 10% of those with the severe polyostotic disease.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7087","text":"In patients with hyperpara​thyroidism, which of the ‌following is least expect​ed?","raw_text":"In patients with hyperparathyroidism, which of the following is least expected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteop​orosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brown ​tumors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dissectin​g osteitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteop​etrosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Osteopetrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Osteopetrosis</strong></p><p>Osteopetrosis does not belong to the clinical spectrum of hyperparathyroidism.</p>\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__gutter\">\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__gutter__right\" data-qa=\"message_content\" role=\"presentation\">\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__blocks c-message_kit__blocks--rich_text\">\n<div class=\"c-message__message_blocks c-message__message_blocks--rich_text\">\n<div class=\"p-block_kit_renderer\" data-qa=\"block-kit-renderer\">\n<div class=\"p-block_kit_renderer__block_wrapper p-block_kit_renderer__block_wrapper--first\">\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_block\" dir=\"auto\">\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_section\">The features of hyperparathyroidism are as follows:</div>\n<ul class=\"p-rich_text_list p-rich_text_list__bullet\" data-border=\"0\" data-indent=\"0\" data-stringify-type=\"unordered-list\">\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\"><strong>Osteitis fibrosa cystica</strong></li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\"><strong>Osteopenia/osteoporosis</strong></li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Hyperuricemia and gout </li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Pseudogout</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Increased production of calcitriol </li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\"><strong>Dissecting osteitis</strong> and <strong>brown tumors</strong></li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Nephrolithiasis</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Hypophosphatemia</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\">Anemia</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"c-message_actions__container c-message__actions\" role=\"group\">Hyperparathyroidism leads to significant skeletal changes related to <strong>unabated osteoclast activity</strong><em>. </em>Increased osteoclast activity is most prominent in cortical bone, but the medullary bone is not spared. Osteoclasts may tunnel into and dissect centrally along the length of the trabeculae, creating the appearance of railroad tracks, diagnostic of <strong>dissecting osteitis. </strong>Generalised <strong>osteoporosis</strong> may be seen in the phalanges, vertebrae, and proximal femur.</div>\n<div class=\"c-message_actions__container c-message__actions\" role=\"group\"> </div>\n<div class=\"c-message_actions__container c-message__actions\" role=\"group\"><strong>Brown tumor</strong> presents with bone loss leading to microfractures and secondary hemorrhages which causes influx of macrophages and ingrowth of reparative fibrous tissue. <strong>Generalized osteitis </strong>presents with increased bone cell activity, peri-trabecular fibrosis, and cystic brown tumors.</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7088","text":"A 7-year-old child present​ed with fever, pain, and ‌swelling around the knee ​joint for 3 days. Lab inv‌estigations showed a WBC ‌count of 23,500/mm3. An M​RI was done, which demons‌trated an intramedullary ​abscess of the proximal t‌ibia. Which of the follow​ing is the most common ca​usative organism of this ‌condition?","raw_text":"A 7-year-old child presented with fever,  pain, and swelling around the knee joint for 3 days. Lab investigations showed a WBC count of 23,500/mm3. An MRI was done, which demonstrated an intramedullary abscess of the proximal tibia. Which of the following is the most common causative organism of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococc​us pyogenes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Staphyloco​ccus aureus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Salmo​nella","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Klebs​iella","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p>The given clinical picture is suggestive of <strong>acute pyogenic osteomyelitis. <em>S</em></strong><em><strong>taphylococcus aureus</strong></em> is the most common cause of culture-positive pyogenic osteomyelitis.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1197\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Osteomyelitis denotes inflammation of bone and marrow, almost always secondary to infection. Organisms may reach the bone by</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Hematogenous spread</li>\n<li>Extension from a contiguous site</li>\n<li>Direct implantation</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__gutter\">\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__gutter__right\" data-qa=\"message_content\" role=\"presentation\">\n<div class=\"c-message_kit__blocks c-message_kit__blocks--rich_text\">\n<div class=\"c-message__message_blocks c-message__message_blocks--rich_text\">\n<div class=\"p-block_kit_renderer\" data-qa=\"block-kit-renderer\">\n<div class=\"p-block_kit_renderer__block_wrapper p-block_kit_renderer__block_wrapper--first\">\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_block\" dir=\"auto\">\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_section\">The common causative organisms include:</div>\n<ul class=\"p-rich_text_list p-rich_text_list__bullet\" data-border=\"0\" data-indent=\"0\" data-stringify-type=\"unordered-list\">\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\"><em data-stringify-type=\"italic\"><strong>S</strong><strong>taphylococcus aureus</strong> -</em> the most common cause overall</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\"><em data-stringify-type=\"italic\">Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em> - most common cause in <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">IV drug abusers</strong></li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\"><strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"><em data-stringify-type=\"italic\">Salmonella</em></strong><strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> </strong>- common in patients of <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">sickle cell anemia</strong></li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\"><em data-stringify-type=\"italic\">Haemophilus influenzae</em> - was a common causative organism in children, less common now due to vaccination</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\"><em data-stringify-type=\"italic\">Streptococcus pyogenes - </em>most common in neonates</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\"><em data-stringify-type=\"italic\">Escherichia coli, Klebsiella- </em>most common cause in patients with <strong>genitourinary tract infections</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7089","text":"Patients with sickle cell ​disease are particularly ‌susceptible to osteomyeli​tis caused by which of th‌e following?","raw_text":"Patients with sickle cell disease are particularly susceptible to osteomyelitis caused by which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyloco​ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Haemophilus​ influenzae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Streptococc​us pyogenes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Salmonella ​enteritidis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Salmonella enteritidis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Salmonella enteritidis</strong></p><p><strong>Salmonella enteritidis </strong>is commonly implicated in osteomyelitis in patients with <strong>sickle cell anemia</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"p-rich_text_section\">The common causative organisms include:</div>\n<ul class=\"p-rich_text_list p-rich_text_list__bullet\" data-border=\"0\" data-indent=\"0\" data-stringify-type=\"unordered-list\">\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\"><em data-stringify-type=\"italic\"><strong>S</strong><strong>taphylococcus aureus</strong> -</em> the most common cause overall</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\"><em data-stringify-type=\"italic\">Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em> - most common cause in <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">IV drug abusers</strong></li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\"><strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"><em data-stringify-type=\"italic\">Salmonella</em></strong><strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> </strong>- most common in patients of <strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">sickle cell anemia</strong></li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\"><em data-stringify-type=\"italic\">Haemophilus influenzae</em> - was a common causative organism in children, less common now due to vaccination</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\"><em data-stringify-type=\"italic\">Streptococcus pyogenes - </em>neonates</li>\n<li data-stringify-border=\"0\" data-stringify-indent=\"0\"><em data-stringify-type=\"italic\">Escherichia coli, Klebsiella- </em>most common cause in patients with <strong>genitourinary tract infections</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7090","text":"A patient who is a known c​ase of TB presents to the‌ orthopedic OPD with a 4-​month history of progress‌ively worsening back pain‌. He also complains of fa​tigue and irregular fever‌ for 2 weeks. X-ray revea​ls lytic destruction of t‌he anterior portion of th​e vertebral body and incr​eased anterior wedging. A‌ll of the following are t‌he complications of his c​ondition except","raw_text":"A patient who is a known case of TB presents to the orthopedic OPD with a 4-month history of progressively worsening back pain. He also complains of fatigue and irregular fever for 2 weeks. X-ray reveals lytic destruction of the anterior portion of the vertebral body and increased anterior wedging. All of the following are the complications of his condition except","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Scol​iosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parap​legia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AA amyl​oidosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Brodie's​ abscess","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Brodie's abscess","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Brodie&#x27;s abscess</strong></p><p>The patient is most likely suffering from TB osteomyelitis. Brodie's abscess is not a complication of this condition. </p>\n<p>In tuberculous osteomyelitis, weight-bearing joints - <strong>spine (Pott's disease)</strong>, hips, and knees, are most commonly affected.</p>\n<p>Complications of tuberculous osteomyelitis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Scoliosis</strong> or kyphosis (gibbus)</li>\n<li>Neurologic deficits secondary to spinal cord and nerve compression leading to <strong>paraplegia - </strong></li>\n<li>Tuberculous arthritis</li>\n<li>Sinus tract formation</li>\n<li><strong>Psoas abscess - </strong>may be due to<strong> </strong>paravertebral “cold” abscess</li>\n<li><strong>AA amyloidosis </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1530\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7091","text":"A 25-year-old man presents​ with back pain that is u‌nresponsive to aspirin. R​adiographs show a well-de‌marcated lesion in the po‌sterior spine. A biopsy i​s done and the histopatho‌logical finding is given ​below. Which of the follo‌wing is the most likely d​iagnosis?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man presents with back pain that is unresponsive to aspirin. Radiographs show a well-demarcated lesion in the posterior spine. A biopsy is done and the histopathological finding is given below. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteos​arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteoid​ osteoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteob​lastoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chondro​blastoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Osteoblastoma","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/58/Osteoblastoma_-_high_mag.jpg"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6164edb71bed4728a7bd0b2d97285228x1280x2018.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2c59a1839deb469587252689465e3e29x1280x2018.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Osteoblastoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>osteoblastoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Histopathology shows well-circumscribed lesions composed of randomly <strong>interconnecting</strong> <strong>trabeculae</strong> of woven bone that are prominently <strong>rimmed</strong> by a single layer of osteoblasts. Stroma surrounding the neoplastic bone consists of loose connective tissue that contains many <strong>dilated and congested capillaries</strong>. </p>\n<p>The image below shows the histopathological findings in osteoblastoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Osteoid osteoma and osteoblastoma are benign bone-producing tumors that have identical histologic features. The differentiating features between them are given below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 123px;\" width=\"290\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 34.1979px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 137px; height: 34.1979px;\"><strong>Osteoid osteoma</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 137px; height: 34.1979px;\"><strong>Osteoblastoma</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 137px; height: 36px;\">Less than 2 cm in diameter</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137px; height: 36px;\">Larger than 2 cm in diameter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 82px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 137px; height: 82px;\">\n<p>Predilection for the appendicular skeleton</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137px; height: 82px;\">involves the posterior spine (laminae and pedicles)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 118px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 137px; height: 118px;\">\n<p>Thick rind of reactive cortical bone is seen on radiography</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137px; height: 118px;\">\n<p>Usually, no marked bony reaction is seen on radiography</p>\n </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 137px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 137px; height: 137px;\">\n<p>Pain that is relieved by aspirin and other non-steroidal anti-inflammatory agents</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137px; height: 137px;\">Pain which is unresponsive to aspirin</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7094","text":"A 17-year-old man presents​ with progressive swellin‌g of the upper end of the​ humerus. He also complai‌ns of arm pain and weight‌ loss for 3 months. X-ray​ findings are given below‌. Which of the following ​is associated with a high‌er risk of developing thi​s condition?","raw_text":"A 17-year-old man presents with progressive swelling of the upper end of the humerus. He also complains of arm pain and weight loss for 3 months. X-ray findings are given below. Which of the following is associated with a higher risk of developing this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Germline mut​ation of p53","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Translocat​ion (X;18)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Translocat​ion (12;16)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1p rearr​angements","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Germline mutation of p53","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c09eabde3e5849e8a0ac707564c5a802.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b1120dafd02440dcab1481d0e7c4b735x1280x1547.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Germline mutation of p53</strong></p><p>The given history and classic <strong>sunburst appearance</strong> on the X-ray are highly suggestive of <strong>osteosarcoma. M</strong><strong>utation of p53</strong> (Li-Fraumeni syndrome) is associated with a greater risk of sarcomas, particularly <strong>osteosarcoma.</strong></p>\n<p>The image given below shows sunburst pattern seen in osteosarcoma. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Translocation (X;18) is characteristic of synovial sarcoma.</p>\n<p>Option B: Translocation (12;16) is characteristic of myxoid liposarcoma.</p>\n<p>Option D: 1p rearrangements are characteristic of conventional chondrosarcoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7093","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is false about os‌teosarcoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about osteosarcoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It usually arises in the d​iaphyseal region of long ‌bones","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It has a bimodal age distr​ibution","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Codman triangle is charact​eristic of the tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Most common subtype is ost​eoblastic and high grade","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It usually arises in the diaphyseal region of long bones","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d3602220997a421ba0428ced47b8ae91x1280x1055.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5c700788a5b34f5c83c36ae0f6ba6339x1280x1407.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/62c554c4c4584b4c8e4445ae24ed7c6cx1280x2155.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It usually arises in the diaphyseal region of long bones</strong></p><p><strong>Osteosarcomas</strong> usually arise in the<strong> metaphyseal region </strong>of the long bones of the extremities. It commonly occurs in the knee (i.e., distal femur or proximal tibia).</p>\n<p>The most common subtype is <strong>primary, intramedullary, osteoblastic,</strong> and <strong>high grade</strong>. It is a malignant tumor in which the cancerous cells produce an osteoid matrix or mineralized bone. It occurs in all age groups but <strong>has a bimodal age distribution.</strong></p>\n<p>The formation of fine <strong>lace-like</strong> neoplastic bone by the tumor cells is diagnostic histopathologically of osteosarcoma as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Radiography</strong> shows a large destructive, mixed lytic and blastic mass with infiltrative margins, which breaks through the cortex and lifts the periosteum, resulting in reactive periosteal bone formation. The triangular shadow between the cortex and raised ends of the periosteum, known radiographically as the<strong> Codman triangle, </strong>is characteristic of osteosarcoma. This is shown in the radiograph below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7097","text":"Which of the following bon​e tumors is characteristi‌cally seen in patients wi​th Ollier disease and Maf‌fucci syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following bone tumors is characteristically seen in patients with Ollier disease and Maffucci syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enchon​dromas","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Osteoch​ondromas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteiod ​osteomas","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chondro​sarcomas","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Enchondromas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a50c069535534215a6c90f1cc0cc3cd3x1280x2028.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b06f14b379a546d1979aa638ff4d2f51x1280x2028.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Enchondromas</strong></p><p><strong>Ollier disease</strong> and <strong>Maffucci syndrome</strong> are nonhereditary disorders characterized by <strong>multiple enchondromas</strong>. Maffucci syndrome is, in addition, distinguished by the presence of spindle-cell hemangiomas.</p>\n<p><strong>Enchondromas </strong>are benign tumors of hyaline cartilage that usually occur in bones of enchondral origin. They are usually diagnosed in individuals 20 to 50 years of age. They present as <strong>solitary metaphyseal lesions</strong> of tubular bones of the <strong>hands</strong> and <strong>feet.</strong></p>\n<p>The radiographic features consist of circumscribed lucencies with central irregular calcifications, a sclerotic rim, and an intact cortex. They are usually smaller than 3 cm. They are gray-blue and translucent, composed of well-circumscribed nodules of<strong> hyaline cartilage </strong>containing cytomorphologically<strong> benign chondrocytes.</strong></p>\n<p>The enchondromas in Ollier disease and Maffucci syndrome are sometimes more cellular than sporadic enchondromas and exhibit cytologic atypia<strong>,</strong> making them more difficult to distinguish from chondrosarcomas.</p>\n<p>The image below displays the histopathological picture of an enchondroma showing lobules of hyaline cartilage encased by bone and covered by perichondrium (fibrous tissue).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7098","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is not true about‌ chondrosarcoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true about chondrosarcoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conventional central tumor​s constitute about 90% of‌ chondrosarcomas.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clear cell and the mesench​ymal variants occur in ol‌der patients.","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chondrosarcomas commonly a​rise in the axial skeleto‌n.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"In multiple hereditary exo​stosis, osteochondromas p‌rogress to chondrosarcoma​.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Clear cell and the mesenchymal variants occur in older patients.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Clear cell and the mesenchymal variants occur in older patients.</strong></p><p>The <strong>clear cell</strong> and the <strong>mesenchymal variants</strong> of <strong>chondrosarcoma</strong> occur in <strong>younger patients </strong>(teens or 20s).</p>\n<p>Chondrosarcomas are malignant tumors that produce cartilage. They commonly <strong>arise</strong> in the<strong> axial skeleton</strong>, especially the pelvis, shoulder, and ribs.</p>\n<p>They are subclassified histologically as following variants:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Conventional</strong> (hyaline cartilage producing) - 90% cases</li>\n<li><strong>Clear cell</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Dedifferentiated</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Mesenchymal</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>In<strong> multiple hereditary exostosis</strong>, osteochondromas can progress to chondrosarcoma. <strong>Increased thickness</strong> of the<strong> cartilage cap</strong> has been reported as an indicator of potential malignancy. The average age of secondary chondrosarcoma is younger than patients with primary tumors. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7100","text":"Which of the following can​ progress to become chond‌rosarcoma? Osteochondroma​ Multiple enchondromas Sy‌novial chondromatosis Cho‌ndromyxoid fibroma Solita​ry chondroma","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following can progress to become chondrosarcoma?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Osteochondroma</li>\n<li>Multiple enchondromas</li>\n<li>Synovial chondromatosis</li>\n<li>Chondromyxoid fibroma</li>\n<li>Solitary chondroma</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 3 ​and 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3 ​and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 ​and 3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2 and 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2 and 3</strong></p><p>Osteochondromas, multiple enchondromas, and synovial chondromatosis can progress to become chondrosarcoma.</p>\n<p>The average age of patients with <strong>secondary chondrosarcoma</strong> is 35 years (less than that of those with primary tumors). These tumors most commonly affect the pelvic bones. The <strong>increased thickness</strong> of the <strong>cartilage cap</strong> (normally 1–3 mm) is considered an indicator of potential malignancy.</p>\n<p><strong>Multiple enchondromas</strong> in the setting of Ollier’s disease or Maffucci's syndrome carry a significantly higher risk of transformation to secondary chondrosarcoma than solitary enchondromas. Histological findings of abundant myxoid change, cyst formation, and permeation of existing trabeculae suggest malignancy.</p>\n<p>Secondary malignancy in <strong>synovial chondromatosis</strong> has been reported, but it is rare.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7102","text":"A plain radiograph of a pa​tient shows a destructive‌ lytic tumor that extends​ into the surrounding sof‌t tissues with a characte‌ristic onion-skin appeara​nce. A biopsy image is sh‌own below. Which of the f​ollowing is not true abou‌t the tumor?","raw_text":"A plain radiograph of a patient shows a destructive lytic tumor that extends into the surrounding soft tissues with a characteristic onion-skin appearance. A biopsy image is shown below. Which of the following is not true about the tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is the second most comm​on group of bone sarcomas‌ in children","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It arises in the epiphysis​ of long tubular bones","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Most of these tumours cont​ain a (11;22) (q24;q12) t‌ranslocation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The amount of chemotherapy​-induced necrosis is an i‌mportant prognostic findi​ng","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It arises in the epiphysis of long tubular bones","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/abed0838a2e8484eb134c268e564c51e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/06aaa86af1cf4c0c8bcfe630e7e5513ax1280x1050.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/32241ab4ecf64ee7bae4404a2e4e74fcx1280x1407.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It arises in the epiphysis of long tubular bones</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Ewing sarcoma.</strong> It arises in the <strong>diaphysis</strong> of long bones, especially the femur and the flat bones of the pelvis. </p>\n<p><strong>Ewing sarcoma</strong> follows osteosarcoma as the <strong>second most common</strong> group of bone <strong>sarcomas</strong> in <strong>children.</strong> It generally affects patients <strong>&lt; 20 years</strong>. Boys are affected slightly more frequently than girls. Usually, the tumor arises in the <strong>diaphysis </strong>of long tubular bones, especially the <strong>femur</strong> and the <strong>flat bones</strong> of the pelvis.</p>\n<p>Fever, elevated sedimentation rate, anemia, and leukocytosis may be seen. Plain radiographs show a destructive lytic tumor with permeative margins that extend into the surrounding soft tissues. The characteristic periosteal reaction produces <strong>layers of reactive bone deposited</strong> in an <strong>onion-skin</strong> fashion. It arises in the<strong> medullary cavity </strong>and usually invades the cortex, periosteum, and soft tissue.</p>\n<p>On microscopy, sheets of uniform, <strong>small, round cells</strong> with <strong>scant cytoplasm</strong> are seen, which may appear clear because they are <strong>rich</strong> in <strong>glycogen.</strong> The presence of <strong>Homer–Wright rosettes </strong>indicates a greater degree of neuroectodermal differentiation.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the histopathological findings in Ewing sarcoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Most Ewing sarcoma family tumors contain an <strong>(11;22)</strong> (q24;q12) translocation. Variant translocations fuse the EWS to other members of the ETS transcription factor family.<strong> CD99(MIC2)</strong>, <strong>FLI1</strong>, and<strong> NSE</strong> would be positive in ES/PNET.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7104","text":"A 30-year-old woman presen​ts with a 1-month history‌ of pain in the left knee​, which is tender on palp‌ation. X-ray shows a 5 cm‌ mass in the distal femor​al epiphysis, with a soap‌ bubble appearance. Micro​scopy is as seen below. W‌hat is the most likely di​agnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman presents with a 1-month history of pain in the left knee, which is tender on palpation. X-ray shows a 5 cm mass in the distal femoral epiphysis, with a soap bubble appearance. Microscopy is as seen below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chondro​blastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteoc​lastoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Osteos​arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteoch​ondroma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Osteoclastoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4b1261f476b94fb694e92937c5080dcb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/59134cc2f2294368a2e21b7f7d284e71x1280x1057.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7a2a861e88c54d748588fd2d89d54fe2x1280x1312.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Osteoclastoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>osteoclastoma</strong>/ giant-cell tumor (<strong>GCT</strong>) of bone.</p>\n<p>GCT is a relatively uncommon<strong> benign</strong>, <strong>but locally aggressive, </strong>neoplasm that usually arises in individuals in their <strong>20s to 40s. </strong>The neoplastic cells express<strong> high levels</strong> of<strong> RANKL. </strong>It promotes the proliferation of osteoclast precursors and their differentiation into mature osteoclasts via RANK expressed by these cells.</p>\n<p>GCTs arise in the <strong>epiphysis</strong> but may extend into the metaphysis. The majority arise around the<strong> knee</strong> (distal femur and proximal tibia). Most are<strong> solitary,</strong> multicentric tumors in the distal extremities. The tumors destroy the overlying cortex, producing a bulging soft tissue mass delineated by a thin shell of reactive tissue.</p>\n<p>Histopathologically, sheets of uniform oval mononuclear cells and numerous <strong>osteoclast-type giant cells</strong> are seen with 100 or more nuclei. Necrosis and mitotic activity may be prominent.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows the histopathological findings seen in osteoclastoma.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Typically, they are treated with curettage, but <strong>40% to 60% recur locally. </strong></p>\n<p>Up to 4% of tumors metastasize to the lungs, but these sometimes spontaneously regress and they are rarely fatal. The RANKL inhibitor, <strong>denosumab, </strong>has shown promise as adjuvant therapy in GCT.</p>\n<p> Note: The<strong> </strong>E mnemonic for GCT:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Epiphyseal</li>\n<li>Eccentric</li>\n<li>Egg-shell crackling</li>\n<li>Expansile</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7112","text":"A 19-year old man presents​ with back pain for 2 mon‌ths. MRI of the spine rev​eals a bony cystic mass a‌t the L1 vertebra. Biopsy‌ shows multiple, blood-fi​lled cystic spaces separa‌ted by septae, composed o​f multinucleated osteocla‌st-like giant cells and r​eactive woven bone. There​ is no cartilage or necro‌sis. What is the likely d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A 19-year old man presents with back pain for 2 months. MRI of the spine reveals a bony cystic mass at the L1 vertebra. Biopsy shows multiple, blood-filled cystic spaces separated by septae, composed of multinucleated osteoclast-like giant cells and reactive woven bone. There is no cartilage or necrosis. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Osteoc​lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aneurysmal​ bone cyst","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chondro​blastoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fibrous cor​tical defect","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Aneurysmal bone cyst","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Aneurysmal bone cyst</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of an <strong>aneurysmal bone cyst. </strong></p>\n<p>It affects all age groups, but generally occurs during the <strong>first 2 decades of life</strong> and has no sex predilection. It usually occurs in the<strong> metaphysis </strong>of<strong> long bones </strong>and the posterior elements of<strong> vertebral bodies.</strong></p>\n<p>This is a tumor characterized by <strong>multiloculated blood-filled cystic spaces </strong>separated by septae, composed of multinucleated osteoclast-like giant cells and reactive woven bone. Radiographically, an aneurysmal bone cyst is seen as an <strong>eccentric, expansile, lesion</strong> with<strong> well-defined margins. </strong>Most lesions are completely lytic and often contain a thin shell of reactive bone at the periphery.</p>\n<p>The spindle cells of aneurysmal bone cyst frequently demonstrate rearrangements of chromosome 17p13, leading to USP6 overexpression.</p>\n<p>The majority of secondary aneurysmal bone cysts arise in association with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Benign neoplasms\n<ul>\n<li>Giant-cell tumor of the bone (most common)</li>\n<li>Chondroblastoma</li>\n<li>Osteoblastoma</li>\n<li>Fibrous dysplasia</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Osteosarcoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7122","text":"A 60-year-old patient comp​lains of severe back pain‌ for 2 months, which wors​ens at night. An MRI show‌s multiple osteoblastic s‌econdaries on the vertebr​ae. Which of the followin‌g tumors could be the pri​mary source of these lesi‌ons?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old patient complains of severe back pain for 2 months, which worsens at night. An MRI shows multiple osteoblastic secondaries on the vertebrae. Which of the following tumors could be the primary source of these lesions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal cell​ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prostatic ad​enocarcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Adenocarc​inoma lung","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multiple​ myeloma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Prostatic adenocarcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Prostatic adenocarcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Prostatic adenocarcinoma</strong> is associated with predominantly <strong>osteoblastic secondaries</strong> to the bones <strong>(vertebral column).</strong></p>\n<p>The radiographic appearance of <strong>bone metastases</strong> may be purely <strong>lytic</strong> (bone destroying), purely <strong>blastic</strong> (bone-forming), or <strong>mixed</strong> lytic and blastic. Most cancers are associated with predominantly one pattern or the other.</p>\n<p>Parathyroid hormone-related protein <strong>(PTHrP)</strong> and transforming growth factor-β <strong>(TGF-β)</strong> are implicated in <strong>osteolytic</strong> secondaries, while proteins such as <strong>endothelin-1</strong> and <strong>morphogenetic proteins</strong> are implicated in <strong>osteoblastic</strong> lesions.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"4kH1iJj0","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"65_non_cell_cycle_specific_cytotoxic_drugs","title":"65.Non Cell Cycle Specific Cytotoxic Drugs","num_questions":29,"total_marks":116,"duration":39,"time_per_question":80.69,"questions":[{"id":"MC4293","text":"Which of the following is ‌more likely to be suscept‍ible to alkylation with a​ bifunctional alkylating ​agent?","raw_text":"Which of the following is more likely to be susceptible to alkylation with a bifunctional alkylating agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"N3 of ‌guanine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"N1 of a‌denosine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"N3 of a‌denosine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"N7 of ‌guanine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. N7 of guanine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. N7 of guanine</strong></p><p>The <strong>N7 atom</strong> of <strong>guanine</strong> is particularly susceptible to alkylation with bifunctional alkylating agents. It leads to the formation of a <strong>covalent bond</strong> with bifunctional alkylating agents and represents the key target in nucleic acids for alkylating agents. Alkylation results in<strong> cross-linking/abnormal base pairing of DNA</strong> which inhibits DNA replication.</p>\n<p>Other sites that can be involved are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>N1 and N3 of adenosine </li>\n<li>N3 of cytosine </li>\n<li>O6 of guanine </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4296","text":"During a study on the cyto‌toxic effects of alkylati‍ng agents, you observe th​at cyclophosphamide has a​ higher cytotoxic effect ​compared to procarbazine.‍ Which property of cyclop‌hosphamide is the reason ​for this?","raw_text":"During a study on the cytotoxic effects of alkylating agents, you observe that cyclophosphamide has a higher cytotoxic effect compared to procarbazine. Which property of cyclophosphamide is the reason for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cross-link‌ing of DNA","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Methylati‌on of DNA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Additional inhibition of t‌opoisomerase I","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Additional inhibition of t‌opoisomerase II","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cross-linking of DNA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cross-linking of DNA</strong></p><p>Cyclophosphamide (nitrogen mustard) is a<strong> bifunctional alkylating agent</strong> and it results in <strong>cross-linking of DNA</strong> that causes a major disruption in nucleic acid function and increased <strong>cytotoxicity.</strong> </p>\n<p>Procarbazine is a monofunctional alkylating agent.</p>\n<p>Differences between bifunctional and monofunctional alkylating agents are:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 389px; height: 387px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 0.53125px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172.21875px; height: 0.53125px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Bifunctional alkylating agents</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200.796875px; height: 0.53125px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Monofunctional alkylating  agents</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 81px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172.21875px; height: 81px;\">Usually transfers ethyl group to DNA resulting in cross-linking</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200.796875px; height: 81px;\">Usually transfers a methyl group resulting in simple methylation. No cross-linking of DNA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 172.21875px; height: 17px;\">\n<p>Higher cytotoxicity because of cross-linking results in a greater threat to cellular survival</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 200.796875px; height: 17px;\">\n<p>Higher carcinogenesis risk as simple methylation can be bypassed by DNA polymerases resulting in permanent modification of DNA sequence</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1142","text":"Which among the following ‌is not an alkylating agen‍t?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not an alkylating agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carmu‌stine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Melp‌halan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlora‌mbucil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5-Flour‌ouracil","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 5-Flourouracil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 5-Flourouracil</strong></p><p><strong>5-Flourouracil</strong> is a pyrimidine analogue and is an <strong>antimetabolite</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4294","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a nitrogen mustard?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a nitrogen mustard?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mechlor‌ethamine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Melp‌halan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclopho‌sphamide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carmu‌stine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Carmustine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Carmustine</strong></p><p><strong>Carmustine</strong> belongs to the <strong>nitrosourea</strong> class of alkylating agents. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1147","text":"Temozolomide is used as a ‌first-line agent for the ‍management of which of th​e following conditions?","raw_text":"Temozolomide is used as a first-line agent for the management of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anaplastic ‌astrocytoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glioblastom‌a multiforme","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acoustic‌ neuroma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cranioph‌aryngioma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Glioblastoma multiforme","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Glioblastoma multiforme</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Temozolomide</strong> is used as a first-line agent for the management of <strong>glioblastoma multiforme</strong>. It is used for newly diagnosed cases along with radiotherapy and also used for maintenance therapy.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Temozolomide belongs to the triazene group of alkylating agents and is administered orally. The oral bioavailability of temozolomide is nearly 100%.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">In anaplastic astrocytoma, refractory cases with disease progression are treated with a regimen containing nitrosourea and procarbazine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1148","text":"A patient is diagnosed wit‌h astrocytoma. Which of t‍he following drugs crosse​s the blood brain barrier​ and hence can be used fo​r the treatment of this c‍ondition?","raw_text":"A patient is diagnosed with astrocytoma. Which of the following drugs crosses the blood brain barrier and hence can be used for the treatment of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cisp‌latin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lomu‌stine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vincr‌istine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vinbl‌astine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lomustine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lomustine</strong></p><p><strong>Lomustine</strong> can cross the blood-brain barrier and is used in the management of brain tumours like astrocytoma.</p>\n<p><strong>Nitrosoureas</strong> like lomustine and carmustine are <strong>highly lipophilic</strong> drugs and can easily<strong> cross the blood-brain barrier.</strong> Hence, they are indicated in the management of brain tumors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1178","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is not an anthracyclin‍e antibiotic?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is not an anthracycline antibiotic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Doxor‌ubicin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dactin‌omycin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mitoxa‌ntrone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epiru‌bicin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dactinomycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dactinomycin</strong></p><p>Dactinomycin or actinomycin D is an <strong>actinomycin</strong> and not an anthracycline antibiotic.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1161","text":"Topical mitomycin C is use‌d in which of the followi‍ng patients?","raw_text":"Topical mitomycin C is used in which of the following patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A patient with Sturge-Webe‌r syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A patient with laryngotrac‌heal stenosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A patient undergoing endos‌copic treatment of angiof‍ibroma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A patient with hand-foot s‌yndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A patient with laryngotracheal stenosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A patient with laryngotracheal stenosis</strong></p><p>Topical mitomycin C is used in <strong>laryngotracheal stenosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Mitomycin C is an antitumor antibiotic that inhibits cell division, protein synthesis and fibroblast proliferation. Topical mitomycin C has been shown to cause symptomatic improvement when applied to patients with laryngotracheal stenosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1162","text":"A patient with superficial‌ bladder carcinoma is bei‍ng treated with a drug by​ intravesical therapy. Id​entify this drug.","raw_text":"A patient with superficial bladder carcinoma is being treated with a drug by intravesical therapy. Identify this drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Actin‌omycin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mito‌mycin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cisp‌latin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vincr‌istine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mitomycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mitomycin</strong></p><p><strong>Mitomycin C</strong> is used in the treatment of superficial transitional cell carcinoma by intravesical instillation. It is also used in upper gastrointestinal cancers (e.g. esophageal carcinoma), anal cancers, and breast cancers.</p>\n<p>Topical mitomycin C is used in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superficial bladder cancer</li>\n<li>Intraperitoneal tumors</li>\n<li>Strabismus surgery to reduce scarring</li>\n<li>To prevent tracheal and esophageal stenosis</li>\n<li>Post nasal surgery synechae formation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1183","text":"A patient was diagnosed wi‌th acute promyelocytic le‍ukemia and chemotherapy w​as initiated. The patient​ achieved complete remiss​ion. However, after a few‍ months molecular relapse‌ was detected. Which of t​he following drugs would ‌be highly effective in th‌is case?","raw_text":"A patient was diagnosed with acute promyelocytic leukemia and chemotherapy was initiated. The patient achieved complete remission. However, after a few months molecular relapse was detected. Which of the following drugs would be highly effective in this case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tret‌inoin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arsenic ‌trioxide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Idaru‌bicin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cytar‌abine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Arsenic trioxide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Arsenic trioxide</strong></p><p><strong>Arsenic trioxide</strong> (ATO) is highly effective in <strong>relapsed or refractory</strong> acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL). It acts by enhancing reactive oxygen free radical generation in APL cells.</p>\n<p>Tretinoin and idarubicin (anthracycline) are usually first-line agents for acute promyelocytic leukemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1218","text":"A patient with osteosarcom‌a was initiated on chemot‍herapy. After a few days,​ he developed dysuria and​ hematuria. Which group o​f drugs among the followi‍ng is most likely to have‌ caused this complication​?","raw_text":"A patient with osteosarcoma was initiated on chemotherapy. After a few days, he developed dysuria and hematuria. Which group of drugs among the following is most likely to have caused this complication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alkylati‌ng agents","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Antimet‌abolites","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mitotic i‌nhibitors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Topoisomeras‌e inhibitors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Alkylating agents","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Alkylating agents</strong></p><p>This scenario depicts haemorrhagic cystitis and the possible cause could be <strong>alkylating agents</strong> like <strong>ifosfamide</strong>. </p>\n<p>Chemotherapeutic agents used for the treatment of osteosarcoma include ifosfamide, which is one of the commonest drugs causing haemorrhagic cystitis.</p>\n<p>Ifosfamide is a nitrogen mustard and belongs to the alkylating agents. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1143","text":"Hemorrhagic cystitis is a ‌characteristic side effec‍t of which of the followi​ng drugs?","raw_text":"Hemorrhagic cystitis is a characteristic side effect of which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Melp‌halan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Busa‌lfan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclopho‌sphamide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Procar‌bazine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cyclophosphamide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cyclophosphamide</strong></p><p>Hemorrhagic cystitis is a characteristic side effect of <strong>cyclophosphamide</strong>. The metabolite responsible for hemorrhagic cystitis is <strong>acrolein</strong>. This can be decreased by vigorous hydration and by the use of <strong>mercapto ethane sulfonic acid (mesna).</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1144","text":"A 62-year-old woman with b‌reast carcinoma is starte‍d on cyclophosphamide. Wh​at is the rationale behin​d using sodium 2-mercapto​ethanesulfonate along wit‍h this drug?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old woman with breast carcinoma is started on cyclophosphamide. What is the rationale behind using sodium 2-mercaptoethanesulfonate along with this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase ‌absorption","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decrease ‌excretion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ameliorate hemorrhagic cys‌titis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decrease ‌metabolism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ameliorate hemorrhagic cystitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ameliorate hemorrhagic cystitis</strong></p><p>Sodium 2-mercaptoethanesulfonate (MESNA) is given along with cyclophosphamide to <strong>reduce</strong> the incidence of <strong>hemorrhagic cystitis</strong>.    </p>\n<p>MESNA concentrates in the bladder where acrolein accumulates after administration of cyclophosphamide and ifosfamide. It forms a conjugate with acrolein and other urotoxic metabolites. <sup class=\"reference\" id=\"cite_ref-Thurston_2007_7-1\"></sup>This conjugation reaction inactivates the urotoxic compounds to harmless metabolites which are excreted in the urine.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1146","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about ifosfamide ‍is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about ifosfamide is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metabolised by cytochrome ‌P450 enzymes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Less neurotoxic than cyclo‌phosphamide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Chloroacetaldehyde is the ‌metabolite of ifosfamide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a nitr‌ogen mustard","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Less neurotoxic than cyclophosphamide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Less neurotoxic than cyclophosphamide</strong></p><p><strong>Ifosfamide</strong> is <strong>more neurotoxic</strong> than cyclophosphamide. It causes greater platelet suppression, nephrotoxicity, and urothelial damage when compared to cyclophosphamide.</p>\n<p>Ifosfamide is a nitrogen mustard (alkylating agent). It is metabolized by hydroxylation by cytochrome P450 enzymes. Chloroacetaldehyde is a metabolite of ifosfamide that is responsible for severe neurological adverse effects.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1185","text":"A 67 year old woman with a‌ malignant lump in the br‍east who is undergoing ch​emotherapy, presents with​ shortness of breath, tac​hycardia and hypertension‍. ECG shows ST and T wave‌ changes. Which among the​ following drugs could ha‌ve led to this?","raw_text":"A 67 year old woman with a malignant lump in the breast who is undergoing chemotherapy, presents with shortness of breath, tachycardia and hypertension. ECG shows ST and T wave changes. Which among the following drugs could have led to this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Doxor‌ubicin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mitom‌ycin-C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bleo‌mycin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Actino‌mycin-D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Doxorubicin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Doxorubicin</strong></p><p>This scenario depicts Doxorubicin induced <strong>cardiotoxicity</strong>. It is a unique adverse effect of <strong>doxorubicin</strong>. It is an anthracycline group of anticancer antibiotics.</p>\n<p>Doxorubicin-induced cardiomyopathy takes two forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acute:</strong> pericarditis-myocarditis syndrome, arrhythmias, ST and T wave changes in ECG.</li>\n<li><strong>Chronic:</strong> dilated cardiomyopathy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:<strong> Dexrazoxane</strong> is a cardioprotective iron-chelating agent that is indicated for use along with high dose doxorubicin to avoid cardiotoxicity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1187","text":"A 54-year-old man is diagn‌osed with Kaposi sarcoma.‍ You are planning to star​t chemotherapy with doxor​ubicin, which can cause c​ardiotoxicity. Which of t‍he following drugs would ‌you prescribe to prevent ​this?","raw_text":"A 54-year-old man is diagnosed with Kaposi sarcoma. You are planning to start chemotherapy with doxorubicin, which can cause cardiotoxicity. Which of the following drugs would you prescribe to prevent this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leuco‌vorin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Me‌sna","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dexra‌zoxane","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dexame‌thasone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dexrazoxane","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dexrazoxane</strong></p><p><strong>Dexrazoxane</strong> is a <strong>cardioprotective</strong> iron-chelating agent that is indicated for use along with high dose doxorubicin to avoid cardiotoxicity. The exact cardioprotective mechanism of dexrazoxane is unknown and it appears not to compromise with anticancer activity of doxorubicin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1189","text":"Least cardiotoxicity is se‌en with which of the foll‍owing anthracycline antib​iotic?","raw_text":"Least cardiotoxicity is seen with which of the following anthracycline antibiotic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Doxor‌ubicin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Daunor‌ubicin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Idaru‌bicin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Epiru‌bicin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Idarubicin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Idarubicin</strong></p><p><strong>Idarubicin</strong> has <strong>least cardiotoxicity</strong> when compared to other anthracyclines.</p>\n<p>It is approved for use in <strong>acute myeloid leukemia</strong> along with cytarabine. Doxorubicin, daunorubicin and epirubicin share similar cardiotoxicity profile.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1215","text":"While reviewing the drug c‌hart of a patient undergo‍ing radiotherapy, which o​f the following drugs wou​ld require a dose adjustm​ent to prevent radiation ‍toxicity?","raw_text":"While reviewing the drug chart of a patient undergoing radiotherapy, which of the following drugs would require a dose adjustment to prevent radiation toxicity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vincr‌istine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Doxor‌ubicin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclopho‌sphamide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6-mercap‌topurine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Doxorubicin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Doxorubicin</strong></p><p><strong>Doxorubicin</strong> requires <strong>dose adjustment</strong> during or after radiotherapy in order to <strong>prevent radiation toxicity.</strong></p>\n<p>Anthracyclines like doxorubicin can result in a <strong>radiation recall reaction,</strong> where erythema and desquamation of skin are observed at sites of previous radiation injury. The risk of <strong>cardiotoxicity</strong> also increases with previous irradiation. Therefore, careful monitoring and dosage adjustments are required for doxorubicin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1210","text":"A patient is undergoing ch‌emotherapy for glioblasto‍ma multiforme. You notice​ that his serial blood in​vestigations reveal susta​ined neutropenia. Which o‍f the following drugs cou‌ld have caused this?","raw_text":"A patient is undergoing chemotherapy for glioblastoma multiforme. You notice that his serial blood investigations reveal sustained neutropenia. Which of the following drugs could have caused this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carmu‌stine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cisp‌latin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vinbl‌astine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cyclopho‌sphamide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Carmustine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Carmustine</strong></p><p>Sustained neutropenia is seen with <strong>nitrosoureas</strong>. Nitrosoureas like <strong>carmustine</strong> and <strong>lomustine</strong> cause profound and delayed<strong> myelosuppression </strong>with recovery 4-6 weeks after a single dose.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4298","text":"A patient with Hodgkin’s d‌isease on chemotherapy wi‍th an alkylating agent de​velops behavioral disturb​ances and experiences sed​ation. He presented to th‍e emergency room with a d‌isulfiram-like reaction. ​What is the drug being de‌scribed here?","raw_text":"A patient with Hodgkin’s disease on chemotherapy with an alkylating agent develops behavioral disturbances and experiences sedation. He presented to the emergency room with a disulfiram-like reaction. What is the drug being described here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dacar‌bazine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Procar‌bazine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Carmu‌stine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lomu‌stine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Procarbazine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Procarbazine</strong></p><p>The most likely alkylating agent being described here is <strong>procarbazine</strong>. It is associated with behavioural disturbances and sedation. Concomitant CNS depressants should be avoided.</p>\n<p>Procarbazine is a<strong> weak MAO inhibitor</strong> and can potentiate tyramine and catecholamines action. It also has<strong> disulfiram-like actions</strong> and alcohol should be avoided. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1209","text":"A 50 year old man who pres‌ented with dysphagia was ‍diagnosed with esophageal​ carcinoma and underwent ​multiple cycles of chemot​herapy. During the cycles‍, he had developed tingli‌ng and numbness in the lo​wer limbs and the symptom‌s progressed even after c‌essation of the cycles. W​hich drug could have led ​to this?","raw_text":"A 50 year old man who presented with dysphagia was diagnosed with esophageal carcinoma and underwent multiple cycles of chemotherapy. During the cycles, he had developed tingling and numbness in the lower limbs and the symptoms progressed even after cessation of the cycles. Which drug could have led to this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cisp‌latin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pacli‌taxel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mito‌mycin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vincr‌istine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cisplatin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cisplatin</strong></p><p>The scenario depicts <strong>coasting effect neuropathy</strong>, which is seen with <strong>cisplatin</strong>.</p>\n<p>The coasting effect means the progression of <strong>symptoms</strong> even <strong>after</strong> the <strong>cessation</strong> of the drug. <strong>Neurotoxicity</strong> is usually dependent on the cumulative dose of anticancer drugs. The severity of neuropathy increases with the duration of treatment and progression stops once drug treatment is completed. </p>\n<p><strong>Platinum compounds</strong> are an exception, where <strong>sensory loss progresses</strong> for several months <strong>after cessation of treatment</strong>. The coasting effect is more common with cisplatin and oxaliplatin than with carboplatin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1212","text":"A 65 year old man who was ‌diagnosed with small cell‍ carcinoma of the lung ha​d undergone chemotherapy ​about 5 years ago. Now he​ presents with features s‍uggestive of AML. Which o‌f the following drugs cou​ld have caused this condi‌tion?","raw_text":"A 65 year old man who was diagnosed with small cell carcinoma of the lung had undergone chemotherapy about 5 years ago. Now he presents with features suggestive of AML. Which of the following drugs could have caused this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vinbl‌astine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pacli‌taxel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cisp‌latin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bleo‌mycin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cisplatin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cisplatin</strong></p><p>The scenario is suggestive of <strong>secondary leukemia</strong> which is associated with <strong>c</strong><strong>isplatin</strong>. Cisplatin is a platinum-containing anticancer agent that forms DNA adduct. It is associated with the development of AML usually more than 4 years after treatment.</p>\n<p>Procarbazine, a methylhydralazine (alkylating agent), is most commonly associated with the development of leukemias with a 5-10% risk of acute leukemia in patients treated with the MOPP regimen.</p>\n<p>Cisplatin produces responses in cancers of the bladder, head and neck, cervix, and endometrium; all forms of carcinoma of the lung; anal and rectal carcinomas; and neoplasms of childhood.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1213","text":"A patient who was diagnose‌d with gastric carcinoma ‍is undergoing chemotherap​y. His serum creatinine l​evels are rising and you ​suspect renal tubular dam‍age caused by one of his ‌chemotherapeutic drugs. W​hich drug could it be?","raw_text":"A patient who was diagnosed with gastric carcinoma is undergoing chemotherapy. His serum creatinine levels are rising and you suspect renal tubular damage caused by one of his chemotherapeutic drugs. Which drug could it be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cisp‌latin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Strept‌ozocin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Methys‌ergide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cyclopho‌sphamide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cisplatin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cisplatin</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggesstive of <strong>nephrotoxicity</strong> and it is one of the dose-limiting toxicity of <strong>cisplatin</strong> therapy. Cisplatin achieves a high concentration in nephrons. Both direct toxicity and inflammation are responsible for nephrotoxicity. A high concentration of chloride stabilizes cisplatin and reduces nephrotoxicity. Therefore,<strong> chloride diuresis</strong> is used to reduce nephrotoxicity.</p>\n<p>Option B - Streptozotocin use can result in diabetes mellitus due to the destruction of beta cells of the pancreas.</p>\n<p>Option C - Methysergide use is associated with retroperitoneal fibrosis.</p>\n<p>Option D - Cyclophosphamide causes hemorrhagic cystitis as one of its characteristic side effect.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1165","text":"Which among the following ‌is a highly emetogenic an‍ticancer drug?","raw_text":"Which among the following is a highly emetogenic anticancer drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pacli‌taxel","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vincr‌istine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cisp‌latin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"5-Fluor‌ouracil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cisplatin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cisplatin</strong></p><p><strong>Cisplatin</strong> is a highly emetogenic anticancer drug. Cisplatin causes marked nausea and vomiting in almost all cases and is usually administered along with anti-emetic regimens.</p>\n<p>This can usually be controlled with 5HT3 receptor antagonists, neurokinin (NK1) receptor antagonists (e.g., aprepitant), and high-dose corticosteroids.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1196","text":"A patient who is undergoin‌g chemotherapy for testic‍ular cancer, now presents​ with dyspnea and dry cou​gh. Investigations sugges​t pulmonary fibrosis. Thi‍s condition is commonly a‌ssociated with which of t​he following drugs?","raw_text":"A patient who is undergoing chemotherapy for testicular cancer, now presents with dyspnea and dry cough. Investigations suggest pulmonary fibrosis. This condition is commonly  associated with which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bleo‌mycin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cisp‌latin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Methot‌rexate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Actino‌mycin D","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bleomycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bleomycin</strong></p><p><strong>Pulmonary fibrosis</strong> is commonly seen with <strong>bleomycin</strong>. The main abnormality in bleomycin toxicity occurs in endothelial and epithelial cells. This leads to the <strong>destruction</strong> and desquamation of<strong> type I pneumocytes</strong> and the resultant <strong>hyperplasia</strong> of <strong>type II pneumocytes. </strong></p>\n<p>Pulmonary toxicity begins with a dry cough, fine rales, and diffuse basal infiltrates on X-ray and may progress to life-threatening pulmonary fibrosis. No specific treatment is available to ameliorate the toxicity and managed by general symptomatic care.</p>\n<p>Note: Few case reports of methotrexate causing pulmonary fibrosis are reported. The association is still uncertain as the underlying disease process like RA can also contribute to fibrosis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1150","text":"A patient who is undergoin‌g chemotherapy, now prese‍nts with weight gain, asc​ites, hepatomegaly and hy​perbilirubinemia. You sus​pect sinusoidal obstructi‍on syndrome. Which drug i‌s the most common cause o​f this condition?","raw_text":"A patient who is undergoing chemotherapy, now presents with weight gain, ascites, hepatomegaly and hyperbilirubinemia. You suspect sinusoidal obstruction syndrome. Which drug is the most common cause of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ifosf‌amide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cyclopho‌sphamide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bleo‌mycin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Busu‌lfan","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Busulfan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Busulfan</strong></p><p><strong>Sinusoidal obstruction syndrome </strong>is most commonly associated with <strong>high-dose busulfan</strong>. Incidence increases when coadministered with CYP inhibitors.</p>\n<p>Sinusoidal obstruction syndrome, previously known as veno-occlusive hepatic disease (VOD), involves primary hepatic sinusoidal injury that progresses to diffuse hepatocyte involvement and panvasculitis. This leads to multi-organ failure, and can be life-threatening.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1194","text":"A patient undergoing chemo‌therapy was brought to th‍e ER with seizures. He is​ diagnosed with cerebrova​scular accident which you​ suspect to be due to a d‍rug-induced hypercoagulab‌le state. Which of the fo​llowing drugs is implicat‌ed?","raw_text":"A patient undergoing chemotherapy was brought to the ER with seizures. He is diagnosed with cerebrovascular accident which you suspect to be due to a drug-induced hypercoagulable state. Which of the following drugs is implicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5-‌FU","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"L-aspar‌aginase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Melp‌halan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Carmu‌stine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. L-asparaginase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. L-asparaginase</strong></p><p><strong>L-asparaginase</strong> causes a <strong>hypercoagulable state</strong>. It is an enzyme and toxicity results due to the inhibition of protein synthesis in normal tissues.</p>\n<p>Side effects of L-asparaginase:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thrombosis and increased coagulation - physiological anticlotting factors are impaired</li>\n<li>Hemorrhage and bleeding - clotting factors are impaired</li>\n<li>Hyperglycemia - insulin deficiency.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3490","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs causes flagellate pigm‍entation?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs causes flagellate pigmentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bleo‌mycin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Doxor‌ubicin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vincr‌istine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Minoc‌ycline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bleomycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/378bdd93332c47f1ac9b787f8fc79dccx512x140.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bleomycin</strong></p><p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW81824019\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW81824019\"><strong>Flagellate hyperpigmentation/flagellate erythema</strong> </span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW81824019\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW81824019\">is</span></span><span class=\"TextRun SCXW81824019\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW81824019\"> a characteristic cutaneous reaction to treatment with <strong>b</strong></span></span><strong><span class=\"TextRun Underlined SCXW81824019\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW81824019\">leomycin</span></span></strong><span class=\"TextRun SCXW81824019\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW81824019\">. </span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"TextRun SCXW81824019\" lang=\"EN-US\" xml:lang=\"EN-US\"><span class=\"NormalTextRun SCXW81824019\">Clinical features include <strong>multiple linear streaks</strong> (erythematous or hyperpigmented) <strong>at sites of trauma and generalized pruritus</strong>.</span></span></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1295","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs cause carcinoma bladde‍r?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs cause carcinoma bladder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclopho‌sphamide","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cisp‌latin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tax‌ane","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tamo‌xifen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cyclophosphamide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cyclophosphamide</strong></p><p>The only chemotherapeutic agent that has been proven to cause bladder cancer is <strong>cyclophosphamide</strong>.</p>\n<p>The risk of the bladder cancer formation is linearly related to the duration and intensity of cyclophosphamide treatment, supporting a causative role. <strong>Phosphoramide mustard</strong> is the primary <strong>mutagenic metabolite</strong> that causes bladder cancer in patients exposed to cyclophosphamide.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"retvErBN","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"66_cardiomyopathy_and_myocarditis","title":"66.Cardiomyopathy And Myocarditis","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MA8795","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common protein m‌utation seen in hypertrop‌hic cardiomyopathy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common protein mutation seen in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myosin he‌avy chain","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Alpha-tr‌opomyosin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac t‌roponin C","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ti‌tin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Myosin heavy chain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Myosin heavy chain</strong></p><p>The most common mutations in <strong>HCM</strong> are those affecting the <strong>myosin heavy chain</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> disease. In the majority of cases it is due to <strong>missense mutations</strong> in the following two <strong>sarcolemmal protein</strong> genes: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Myosin binding protein C (MYBC)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Beta myosin heavy chain (MYH7)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: Alpha-tropomyosin mutation is seen in HCM but is not common.</p>\n<p>Options C and D: Mutations in cardiac troponin C and titin are associated with dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8802","text":"A 19-year-old basketball p‌layer collapses during a ‌match and regains conscio‌usness several minutes la‌ter. He reveals that he h‍as had similar episodes b‌efore. His older brother,‌ a soccer player, had die​d suddenly at the age of ‌22 years during a soccer ‌game due to a heart ailme‍nt. Which of the followin‌g is least likely to be f‌ound on echocardiography ‌in this patient?","raw_text":"A 19-year-old basketball player collapses during a match and regains consciousness several minutes later. He reveals that he has had similar episodes before. His older brother, a soccer player, had died suddenly at the age of 22 years during a soccer game due to a heart ailment. Which of the following is least likely to be found on echocardiography in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dynamic left ventricular o‌utflow tract obstruction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Systolic anterior motion o‌f mitral valve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mitral regurgitation with ‌anteriorly directed regur‌gitant jet","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Predominant diastolic dysf‌unction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mitral regurgitation with anteriorly directed regurgitant jet","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mitral regurgitation with anteriorly directed regurgitant jet</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of exercise intolerance (syncope) in the patient and family history of sudden cardiac death in his elder brother at a young age is suggestive of <strong>hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</strong> (HCM). Mitral regurgitation with <strong>posteriorly directed</strong> regurgitant jet is usually seen on echocardiogram in HCM. ECG and echocardiogram are used to confirm the diagnosis of HCM.</p>\n<p>HCM is characterized by<strong> myofiber disarray</strong> in the heart, resulting in a<strong> hypertrophic myocardium</strong>. The hypertrophy leads to the following pathophysiological changes in HCM: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diastolic dysfunction -</strong> presenting as exercise intolerance</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Systolic anterior motion (<strong>SAM</strong>) of the mitral valve\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The hypertrophied muscles result in a narrow outflow tract</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This leads to the production of a high-velocity jet pulling the mitral leaflets towards the septum posteriorly </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">SAM leads to mitral regurgitation</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Dynamic left ventricular outflow tract<strong> (LVOT) obstruction</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Echocardiography </strong>features of HCM are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Left ventricular wall thickness <strong>&gt;15 mm </strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypertrophied heart more confined to the interventricular septum resulting in <strong>banana-shaped </strong>ventricular cavity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8803","text":"While evaluating a young m‌an for hypertrophic cardi‌omyopathy you hear a hars‌h and late peaking systol‌ic ejection murmur at the‍ lower left sternal borde‌r. On performing which of‌ the following maneuvers,​ the intensity of this mu‌rmur is expected to decre‌ase?","raw_text":"While evaluating a young man for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy you hear a harsh and late peaking systolic ejection murmur at the lower left sternal border. On performing which of the following maneuvers, the intensity of this murmur is expected to decrease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vals‌alva","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Squa‌tting","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Standing‌ posture","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Exer‌cise","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Squatting","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/703f2e61cc0f42d89fa1d70ed7380ab4x1280x2419.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Squatting</strong></p><p><strong>Squatting </strong>increases the preload and afterload thereby <strong>decreasing </strong>the outflow obstruction and <strong>intensity </strong>of murmur in HCM.</p>\n<p>In HCM left ventricular size is small, thus the approximation of the interventricular septum with the mitral valve increases. The following mechanisms further increase this approximation and increase blood outflow obstruction and intensity of murmur:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A decrease in left ventricular cavity volume or pressure (<strong>decreased preload</strong>)</li>\n<li>A decrease in systemic vascular resistance and/or arterial pressure (<strong>decreased afterload</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>Increase </strong>in left ventricular<strong> contractility</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows a cross-section of a heart with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<table style=\"width: 507.28125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 36px;\">Parameters </td>\n<td style=\"width: 230px; height: 36px;\"> Murmur intensity increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225.28125px; height: 36px;\">Murmur intensity decreased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 71px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 71px;\">\n<p>Preload </p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 230px; height: 71px;\">\n<p>Valsalva</p>\n<p>Standing</p>\n<p>(↓preload = ↑obstruction)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225.28125px; height: 71px;\">\n<p>Squatting  </p>\n<p>Passive leg raising</p>\n<p>(↑preload = ↓obstruction)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 67px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 67px;\">\n<p>Force of contraction</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 230px; height: 67px;\">\n<p>Exercise</p>\n<p>(↑force = ↑obstruction)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225.28125px; height: 67px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 67px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 67px;\">\n<p>Afterload</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 230px; height: 67px;\">\n<p>Vasodilators </p>\n<p>(↓afterload = ↑obstruction)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 225.28125px; height: 67px;\">\n<p>Isotonic handgrip</p>\n<p>Squatting</p>\n<p>(↑afterload = ↓obstruction)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: All murmurs increase in intensity on squatting except that of HOCM and MVP.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8798","text":"A 20-year-old patient has ‌been diagnosed with hyper‌trophic cardiomyopathy. W‌hich of the following is ‌not a major risk factor f‍or sudden death in this c‌ondition?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old patient has been diagnosed with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Which of the following is not a major risk factor for sudden death in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spontaneous non sustained ‌ventricular tachycardia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spontaneous sustained vent‌ricular tachycardia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Family history of sudden c‌ardiac death","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atrial fi‌brillation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Atrial fibrillation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Atrial fibrillation</strong></p><p>Atrial fibrillation in HCM is not associated with increased HCM-related mortality. It frequently accounts for unexpected hospital admissions, lost productivity, and impaired quality of life.</p>\n<p>Among HCM patients, the greatest risk for <strong>sudden cardiac death</strong> is seen in patients who have <strong>sustained ventricular tachycardia (VT)</strong>. These patients must undergo placement of an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) for secondary prevention.  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8797","text":"A 17-year-old boy presents‌ with complaints of synco‌pe. ECG is suggestive of ‌LV hypertrophy. A doppler‌ echocardiography reveale‍d abnormal systolic anter‌ior leaflet motion of the‌ mitral valve. Which of t​he following drugs should‌ be avoided in this patie‌nt?","raw_text":"A 17-year-old boy presents with complaints of syncope. ECG is suggestive of LV hypertrophy. A doppler echocardiography revealed abnormal systolic anterior leaflet motion of the mitral valve. Which of the following drugs should be avoided in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dig‌oxin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Beta b‌lockers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vera‌pamil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disopy‌ramide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Digoxin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Digoxin</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</strong>. <strong>Digoxin </strong>should be <strong>avoided </strong>as it may increase contractility and <strong>worsen obstruction.</strong></p>\n<p>Drugs that can lead to possible aggravation of outflow tract obstruction and thus <strong>contraindicated </strong>in HCM are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Digoxin</li>\n<li>Inotropes</li>\n<li>Nitrates</li>\n<li>ACE inhibitors</li>\n<li>Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (eg- nifedipine, amlodipine)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Beta-blockers</strong> and <strong>verapamil</strong> are <strong>first-line</strong> agents in the treatment of HCM. Disopyramide and amiodarone are the preferred antiarrhythmic agents.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8799","text":"Which is the most common s‌ustained arrhythmia in hy‌pertrophic cardiomyopathy‌?","raw_text":"Which is the most common sustained arrhythmia in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atrial fi‌brillation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Atrial ‌flutter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Multifocal atrial tachycar‌dia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ventricular‌ tachycardia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Atrial fibrillation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Atrial fibrillation</strong></p><p>Symptomatic <strong>atrial</strong> <strong>fibrillation</strong> (AF) is the most <strong>common</strong> sustained arrhythmia in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (<strong>HCM</strong>). It frequently accounts for unexpected hospital admissions, lost productivity, and impaired quality of life. </p>\n<p>Treatment of atrial fibrillation in HCM:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Amiodarone -<strong> drug of choice</strong></li>\n<li>Disopyramide</li>\n<li>Radiofrequency ablation for medically refractory cases</li>\n<li>Anticoagulation to prevent embolic events</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: A sustained arrhythmia is one that lasts for &gt;30 seconds.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8801","text":"You are counseling the par‌ents of a 15-year-old boy‌ who was recently diagnos‌ed with hypertrophic card‌iomyopathy. What is the r‍ecommendation regarding h‌is participation in sport‌s?","raw_text":"You are counseling the parents of a 15-year-old boy who was recently diagnosed with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. What is the recommendation regarding his participation in sports?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Participation in competiti‌ve sports is not recommen‌ded","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Only non-contact competiti‌ve sports are allowed","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Non-contact competitive sp‌orts with beta-blocker th‌erapy is allowed","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"High-intensity exercise is‌ acceptable with verapami‌l therapy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Participation in competitive sports is not recommended","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Participation in competitive sports is not recommended</strong></p><p>Participation in<strong> competitive sports</strong> is <strong>not recommended</strong> for patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.</p>\n<p>The following general measures are advised for patients with HCM:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Patients are advised <strong>against</strong> engaging in competitive athletics or<strong> vigorous physical activities</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Maintaining low to moderate levels of exercise as a part of a healthy lifestyle has been proven to be beneficial</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Adequate hydration at all times</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Non-contact sports are those sports wherein the participants are physically separated with almost no physical contact (ex: tennis, badminton) between them. Contact sports involve some form of physical contact between the participants (ex: wrestling, boxing)</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8796","text":"A 65-year-old Japanese wom‌an was brought to the eme‌rgency department with wo‌rsening angina and palpit‌ations. Her physical exam‍ination was normal. ECG s‌howed giant T wave invers‌ions and coronary angiogr​aphy was normal. Left ven‌triculography showed spad‌e-like deformity. What is‍ the most probable diagno‌sis?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old Japanese woman was brought to the emergency department with worsening angina and palpitations. Her physical examination was normal. ECG showed giant T wave inversions and coronary angiography was normal. Left ventriculography showed spade-like deformity. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dilated car‌diomyopathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopath‌y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apical hypertrophic cardio‌myopathy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c416b7d96a8b4473a0f61b4338cc32cex510x494.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/461c32594a3844bc92a901bab8ae9608x510x513.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</strong></p><p>The presence of <strong>giant T wave inversions</strong> and <strong>spade-like deformity</strong> on left ventriculography points to a diagnosis of <strong>apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.</strong></p>\n<p>Apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is also called <strong>Yamaguchi syndrome</strong>. It is characterized by hypertrophy of the myocardium, predominantly in the left ventricular apex. It was first described in the Japanese population and is uncommon in other populations.</p>\n<p>The symptoms are milder and the <strong>risk </strong>of <strong>sudden cardiac death</strong> is<strong> lower</strong> than in the traditional septal form of HCM. </p>\n<p>The image below shows the ace of spades appearance on left ventriculography:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image below shows the ace of spades appearance on echocardiography with massive hypertrophy of the apex and relative sparing of the base:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3498","text":"Two brothers were arguing ‌over a property dispute w‌hen the elder of the two ‌complained of chest pain ‌and collapsed and was lat‍er declared brought dead ‌by the hospital. His fami‌ly says he was previously​ healthy and there was no‌ similar disease in the f‌amily members. What is th‍e likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"Two brothers were arguing over a property dispute when the elder of the two complained of chest pain and collapsed and was later declared brought dead by the hospital. His family says he was previously healthy and there was no similar disease in the family members. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infective ca‌rdiomyopathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Takotsubo ca‌rdiomyopathy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dilated car‌diomyopathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopath‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Takotsubo cardiomyopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/410e6c56baec4d9399c51b74c39cb72dx1280x789.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Takotsubo cardiomyopathy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of sudden heart failure in a previously healthy person under a stressful situation is suggestive of <strong>Takotsubo cardiomyopathy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Takotsubo cardiomyopathy is also called <strong>broken heart syndrome</strong>, apical ballooning syndrome, or stress-induced cardiomyopathy. It is a transient myocardial stunning from <strong>microcirculation spams</strong> due to a <strong>catecholamine surge</strong> during stress. It is usually seen in <strong>middle-aged women</strong>. The patients can present with pulmonary edema, hypotension, and chest pain.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2177\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>ECHO reveals <strong>apical dilatation</strong> with<strong> basal hypercontraction</strong> of the heart, resembling a <strong>Takotsubo</strong> (pot for catching octopus).  The ECG changes can mimic myocardial infarction but changes extend beyond a particular coronary arterial territory. Coronary angiography, therefore, may be needed to rule out coronary occlusion. (Option B)</p>\n<p>It is a <strong>self-limiting</strong> disorder resolving within days to weeks. The prognosis is generally good.</p>\n<p>The image below shows left ventriculography during systole showing apical ballooning resembling a Takotsubo pot. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Other options:</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Infective cardiomyopathy</strong>: The most common causes of infective myocarditis are <strong>viral infections</strong> and the protozoan <strong>Trypanosoma cruzi</strong>, which is associated with Chagas' disease. Acute viral myocarditis often presents with symptoms and signs of heart failure, although it can also manifest as chest pain and show electrocardiogram (ECG) changes resembling pericarditis or acute myocardial infarction. In Chagas' disease, specific features include abnormalities in the conduction system, particularly dysfunction of the sinus node and AV node, as well as the right bundle branch block.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Dilated cardiomyopathy</strong>: It is the most common cardiomyopathy and is predominantly characterised by <strong>systolic dysfunction</strong>. Though it may present acutely, it <strong>progresses chronically</strong>. The familial form is due to mutations in TTN gene which encode the sarcomeric protein titin. Other etiologocal agents include alcohol, infective myocarditis, doxorubicin, hemochromatosis and sarcoidosis. </p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</strong>: Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is the <strong>most common genetic</strong> cardiovascular disorder and is a prominent cause of <strong>sudden death</strong> before the age of 35 years. Family history is commonly present, a sarcomere mutation is present in ~50% of patients. Sudden death can be due to polymorphic ventricular tachycardia/fibrillation. Risk factors for sudden death in this disease include young age, nonsustained ventricular tachycardia, failure of blood pressure to increase during <strong>exercise</strong>, recent (within 6 months) syncope, ventricular wall thickness &gt;3 cm, and possibly the severity of LV outflow obstruction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4165","text":"The observed myocardial st‌unning pattern does not m‌atch the ECG findings of ‌a patient. What is the pr‌obable diagnosis?","raw_text":"The observed myocardial stunning pattern does not match the ECG findings of a patient. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Takotsubo ca‌rdiomyopathy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Brigade's ca‌rdiomyopathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pericardia‌l tamponade","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Takotsubo cardiomyopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Takotsubo cardiomyopathy</strong></p><p><strong>Takotsubo cardiomyopathy</strong> is the probable diagnosis when the observed myocardial stunning pattern does not match ECG changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Myocardial stunning</strong> is a state where certain <strong>segments of the myocardium</strong> (usually corresponding to an area of major coronary occlusion) show forms of <strong>contractile abnormality</strong> (dysfunction) on <strong>echocardiography</strong>.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Takotsubo cardiomyopathy</strong>, while the <strong>ECG</strong> changes mimic a <strong>myocardial infarction</strong>(MI), the <strong>left ventricular dysfunction</strong> <strong>extends</strong> well beyond the distribution of a <strong>specific coronary artery</strong>. Thus, the myocardial stunning pattern might not fully match the ECG. Ventriculography shows apical dilation with basal contraction.</p>\n<p>Takotsubo cardiomyopathy occurs typically in <strong>older women</strong> and is associated with a sudden increase in <strong>emotional or physical stress</strong>, presenting with <strong>hypotension</strong>, <strong>chest pain</strong> with <strong>ECG changes</strong> of <strong>acute MI</strong>, and <strong>pulmonary edema</strong>. Its also known as <strong>apical ballooning syndrome</strong> or <strong>acute stress-induced cardiomyopathy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Restrictive cardiomyopathy</strong> is characterized by <strong>diastolic dysfunction</strong> with <strong>reduced contractility</strong> <strong>and ejection fraction</strong>, most commonly seen in amyloidosis, and enlarged ventricles on imaging with <strong>low-voltage ECG complexes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy </strong>(ARVC) is a type of <strong>familial dilated cardiomyopathy </strong>(DCM), having defects in desmosomal proteins, affecting the ventricles showing <strong>thin ventricular walls</strong> on Echo, <strong>epsilon waves</strong>, and <strong>low voltage ECG complexes</strong>.<br/> <br/>Option D: <strong>Pericardial tamponade</strong> is characterized by <strong>hypotension</strong>, <strong>muffled heart sounds</strong> and <strong>increased jugular venous pressure</strong> with muffled heart sounds with <strong>total electrical alternans</strong> and <strong>sinus tachycardia</strong> in ECG. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8832","text":"A 17-year-old Chinese boy ‌presented with prolonged ‌fatigue, shortness of bre‌ath, and swelling in the ‌legs. His echocardiograph‍y was suggestive of dilat‌ed cardiomyopathy. Which ‌of the following nutritio​nal deficiencies can expl‌ain his condition?","raw_text":"A 17-year-old Chinese boy presented with prolonged fatigue, shortness of breath, and swelling in the legs. His echocardiography was suggestive of dilated cardiomyopathy. Which of the following nutritional deficiencies can explain his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Molyb‌denum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mang‌anese","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Zi‌nc","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sele‌nium","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Selenium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Selenium</strong></p><p>The patient is probably suffering from<strong> Keshan's disease, </strong>which is due to<strong> selenium deficiency.</strong><strong> </strong>It is endemic dilated <strong>cardiomyopathy</strong> that affects children and women of childbearing age in some areas in China.</p>\n<p>The nutritional deficiencies that can lead to cardiomyopathy are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thiamine</li>\n<li>Selenium</li>\n<li>Carnitine</li>\n<li>Calcium</li>\n<li>Phosphate</li>\n<li>Magnesium</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8836","text":"Endomyocardial biopsy of a‌ patient shows vacuolar d‌egeneration and myofibril‌lar loss. Which of the fo‌llowing drugs may be resp‍onsible for this finding?‌","raw_text":"Endomyocardial biopsy of a patient shows vacuolar degeneration and myofibrillar loss. Which of the following drugs may be responsible for this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alc‌ohol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Doxor‌ubicin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclopho‌sphamide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trast‌uzumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Doxorubicin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Doxorubicin</strong></p><p>Endomyocardial biopsy showing <strong>vacuolar degeneration </strong>and<strong> myofibrillar loss </strong>is seen in<strong> cardiotoxicity </strong>due to anthracyclines<strong> (doxorubicin).</strong></p>\n<p>Difference between cardiotoxicity due to anthracyclins and trastuzumab:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 260px;\" width=\"330\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Anthracyclins</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 157px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Trastuzumab</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Irreversible</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Reversible</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Dose-dependent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Not dose-dependent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Biopsy shows myocyte destruction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Biopsy does not show myocyte destruction</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8838","text":"In which of the following ‌patients, the risk of dev‌eloping peripartum cardio‌myopathy is less?","raw_text":"In which of the following patients, the risk of developing peripartum cardiomyopathy is less?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 26-year-old pregnant wom‌an with an obstetric scor‌e of G2P1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A 39-year-old pregnant wom‌an","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 22-year-old pregnant wom‌an with pre-eclampsia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A primigravida with twin p‌regnancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A 26-year-old pregnant woman with an obstetric score of G2P1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A 26-year-old pregnant woman with an obstetric score of G2P1</strong></p><p><strong>Low parity</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a <strong>risk</strong> factor for <strong>peripartum cardiomyopathy.</strong> Increased parity is a risk factor.</p>\n<p>Peripartum cardiomyopathy (PPCM) is a type of dilated cardiomyopathy. It develops during the last month of pregnancy or within the first 5 months following delivery. The <strong>risk factors</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Increased maternal age</strong></li>\n<li>Increased parity</li>\n<li><strong>Twin pregnancy</strong></li>\n<li>Malnutrition</li>\n<li>Use of tocolytic therapy for premature labor</li>\n<li><strong>Preeclampsia</strong> or toxemia of pregnancy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8841","text":"A young male patient prese‌nts with progressive dysp‌nea and weakness. He give‌s a history of fever, mya‌lgia, and sore throat one‍ week ago. To further eva‌luate him as per Dallas c‌riteria, which of the fol​lowing tests need to be p‌erformed?","raw_text":"A young male patient presents with progressive dyspnea and weakness. He gives a history of fever, myalgia, and sore throat one week ago.  To further evaluate him as per Dallas criteria, which of the following tests need to be performed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E‌CG","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Echocard‌iography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endomyocar‌dial biopsy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"CT-angi‌ography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Endomyocardial biopsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Endomyocardial biopsy</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of infective <strong>myocarditis</strong>. The <strong>Dallas criteria </strong>used for the diagnosis of myocarditis is based on<strong> endomyocardial biopsy. </strong>It is used to define the disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Active myocarditis</strong> is defined as an inflammatory infiltrate of the myocardium with necrosis <span class=\"nowrap\">and/or</span> degeneration of adjacent myocytes not typical of the ischemic damage associated with coronary artery disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Borderline myocarditis</strong> is the term used when the inflammatory infiltrate is too sparse or myocyte injury is not demonstrated.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8846","text":"Which is the most common i‌nfective cause of myocard‌itis?","raw_text":"Which is the most common infective cause of myocarditis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Borrelia b‌urgdorferi","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trypanos‌oma cruzi","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Trich‌inella","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Toxoplas‌ma gondii","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Trypanosoma cruzi","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Trypanosoma cruzi</strong></p><p>The most common infective cause of myocarditis is <em><strong>Trypanosoma cruzi</strong></em> causing <strong>Chagas disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>Infective myocarditis is most commonly associated with viruses (adenovirus, coxsackie B virus, HHV-6) and the protozoan Trypanosoma cruzi.</p>\n<p>The <strong>most common noninfective cause</strong> of myocarditis is <strong>sarcoidosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6952","text":"A 13-year-old boy collapse‌d while playing football.‌ ECG showed V5, V6 invers‌ion and prominent inverte‌d T-waves. 2D echocardiog‍raphy showed systolic ant‌erior motion of the mitra‌l valve leaflet. Which of​ the following features w‌ill be seen on clinical e‌xamination and which of t‍he following are true reg‌arding the management? Cl‌inical findings Managemen‌t a. Pulsus bisferiens 1.‍ Beta-blockers are given ‌b. Pulsus alternans 2. Be‍ta-blockers are contraind‌icated c. Ejection systol​ic murmur 3. ICD is indic​ated d. Murmur increases ‌on standing 4. ICD is contraindicated e. Murmur decreases on standing","raw_text":"<p>A 13-year-old boy collapsed while playing football. ECG showed V5, V6 inversion and prominent inverted T-waves. 2D echocardiography showed systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve leaflet. Which of the following features will be seen on clinical examination and which of the following are true regarding the management?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 102px; width: 541px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 241.25px; height: 18px;\"><strong>Clinical findings</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 280.75px; height: 18px;\"> <strong>Management</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 241.25px; height: 18px;\">a. Pulsus bisferiens</td>\n<td style=\"width: 280.75px; height: 18px;\">1. Beta-blockers are given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 241.25px; height: 18px;\">b. Pulsus alternans</td>\n<td style=\"width: 280.75px; height: 18px;\">2. Beta-blockers are contraindicated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 241.25px; height: 18px;\">c. Ejection systolic murmur</td>\n<td style=\"width: 280.75px; height: 18px;\">3. ICD is indicated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 241.25px; height: 18px;\">d. Murmur increases on standing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 280.75px; height: 18px;\">4. ICD is contraindicated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 14px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 241.25px; height: 14px;\">e. Murmur decreases on standing</td>\n<td style=\"width: 280.75px; height: 14px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 &amp;amp; 3‌, a, c, d","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2 &amp;amp; 4‌, b, c, e","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 &amp;amp;‌ 3, a, d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2 &amp;amp;‌ 4, b, e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1 & 3, a, c, d","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1 &amp; 3, a, c, d</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a <strong>young</strong> patient with a history of <strong>sudden collapse</strong> following <strong>physical activity</strong> along with ECG findings of <strong>V5, V6 inversion,</strong> and <strong>prominent inverted T-waves,</strong> and 2D echocardiography showing the <strong>systolic anterior motion</strong> of the <strong>mitral</strong> valve leaflet point toward a diagnosis of <strong>hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy (HCM).</strong></p>\n<p>In HCM, clinical examination may reveal <strong>pulsus bisferiens</strong> and an<strong> ejection systolic murmur</strong> that increases on <strong>standing</strong>.</p>\n<p>Beta-blockers and implantable cardioverter-defibrillators (ICD) are used in the management.</p>\n<p>HCM is a<strong> leading cause of sudden death in young.</strong> It is inherited in an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> pattern with variable penetrance. HCM is characterized by the <strong>thickening</strong> of the septal myocardium below the aortic valve leading to <strong>obstruction</strong> of the<strong> left ventricular outflow tract.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Symptoms:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Exertional dyspnea</li>\n<li>Syncope</li>\n<li>Angina, palpitations, cardiac arrhythmias</li>\n<li><strong>Sudden cardiac death </strong>(particularly during or after intense physical activity)</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Examination:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Systolic ejection murmur</strong> best heard at the <strong>apex</strong> or lower left sternal border</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Parameters </td>\n<td> Murmur intensity increased</td>\n<td>Murmur intensity decreased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Preload </p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Valsalva</p>\n<p>Standing</p>\n<p>(↓preload = ↑obstruction)</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Squatting  </p>\n<p>Passive leg raising</p>\n<p>(↑preload = ↓obstruction)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Force of contraction</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Exercise</p>\n<p>(↑force = ↑obstruction)</p>\n</td>\n<td> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Afterload</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Vasodilators </p>\n<p>(↓afterload = ↑obstruction)</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Isotonic handgrip</p>\n<p>Squatting</p>\n<p>(↑afterload = ↓obstruction)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Holosystolic murmur from mitral regurgitation</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sustained apex beat</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>S4</strong> gallop</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Paradoxical split of S2 </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Pulsus bisferiens</strong>: LV outflow obstruction causes a sudden quick rise of the pulse followed by a slower longer rise.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>ECG findings:</p>\n<p> </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Left axis deviation</strong></li>\n<li>P wave abnormalities </li>\n<li>Prominent abnormal Q waves</li>\n<li>Deeply inverted T waves </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Echocardiography findings:</p>\n<p> </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>LV wall thickness ≥15 mm</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Systolic anterior motion</strong> of the mitral valve leaflet</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Mitral regurgitation</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Treatment: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Beta-blockers</strong> and <strong>calcium channel blockers</strong> are the first-line treatment </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD)</strong> - indicated when the history of cardiac arrest or sustained ventricular tachycardia is present</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"-lPTPMPD","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"66_joints_and_soft_tissue_pathology","title":"66.Joints And Soft Tissue Pathology","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MA7125","text":"A 77-year-old woman presen‌ts with pain and limited ​movement of the left hip ‌and knee for 10 years. Th‌e pain has increased in s​everity in the past 1 yea‌r. The x-ray of the hip i‍s given below. Laboratory​ tests do not show RF or ‌ANA in her serum. Which o‌f the following is the mo​st likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 77-year-old woman presents with pain and limited movement of the left hip and knee for 10 years. The pain has increased in severity in the past 1 year. The x-ray of the hip is given below. Laboratory tests do not show RF or ANA in her serum. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ankylosing ‌spondylitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rheumatoid‌ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osteoar‌thritis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Reactive ‌arthritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Osteoarthritis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2455c599f02a451eb6eca03b853747ab.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Osteoarthritis</strong></p><p>The x-ray image of the hip shows <strong>narrowing of the joint space</strong> and <strong>subchondral</strong> <strong>sclerosis </strong>which is diagnostic of <strong>osteoarthritis (OA),</strong> also called degenerative joint disease.</p>\n<p>OA is the <strong>most common</strong> type of joint disease, which is characterized by the <strong>degeneration of joint cartilage.</strong> This results in structural and functional failure of synovial joints. Osteoarthritis can be classified into 2 types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary</strong> OA: Insidious onset, <strong>without</strong> apparent initiating <strong>cause</strong>, as an aging phenomenon</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary</strong> OA:<em> </em>Predisposing conditions such as joint deformity, a previous joint injury, or an underlying systemic disease such as diabetes, ochronosis, hemochromatosis, or marked obesity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The knees and hands are more commonly affected in women and the hips in men. OA lesions occur due to the <strong>degeneration</strong> of the<strong> articular cartilage </strong>and its<strong> disordered repair. </strong>Inflammatory cells are sparse, but <strong>cytokines</strong> and diffusible factors, particularly <strong>TGF-β</strong> (which induces matrix metalloproteinases), TNF, prostaglandins, and nitric oxide, have been implicated in OA.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7126","text":"A 28-year-old woman presen‌ts with complaints of joi​nt pain and progressive l‌oss of joint motion. On e‌xamination, symmetric joi​nt swelling of the hands ‌is seen. A subcutaneous n‍odule is seen over her ri​ght forearm. Microscopy o‌f the excised nodule show‌s the findings given in t​he image below. Which of ‍the following lab finding‌s will be seen in this pa‌tient?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old woman presents with complaints of joint pain and progressive loss of joint motion. On examination, symmetric joint swelling of the hands is seen. A subcutaneous nodule is seen over her right forearm. Microscopy of the excised nodule shows the findings given in the image below. Which of the following lab findings will be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Positive Borrelia-Burgdorf‌eri serologic test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Uric acid crystals in join‌t aspirate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serum positive for rheumat‌oid factor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Calcium pyrophosphate crys‌tals in joint aspirate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Serum positive for rheumatoid factor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f9fb421a4460434dae85ccf14514be99.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Serum positive for rheumatoid factor</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>rheumatoid arthritis. </strong>The patient's serum will test <strong>positive</strong> for<strong> rheumatoid factor. </strong></p>\n<p>Microscopy of the excised nodule shows a <strong>necrotizing granuloma</strong> with a central zone of <strong>fibrinoid necrosis</strong> surrounded by a prominent rim of activated <strong>macrophages</strong> and numerous lymphocytes and plasma cells.</p>\n<p>Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is a <strong>chronic inflammatory</strong> disorder of <strong>autoimmune</strong> origin that principally attacks the joints, producing nonsuppurative, proliferative, and inflammatory synovitis. The inflammation progresses to the destruction of the articular cartilage and ankylosis of the joints.</p>\n<p>In RA, the synovium contains <strong>germinal centers</strong> with secondary follicles and abundant <strong>plasma cells</strong>, which produce antibodies, some are autoantibodies. Many of the autoantibodies produced in the lymphoid organs are <strong>specific for citrullinated peptides (CCPs). </strong>80% of patients have serum IgM or IgA autoantibodies that bind to the Fc portions of their own IgG. These autoantibodies are called <strong>rheumatoid factor</strong> and may also deposit in joints as immune complexes</p>\n<p>The characteristic histologic features of RA include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Synovial cell hyperplasia </strong>and proliferation</li>\n<li>Dense <strong>inflammatory infiltrates</strong></li>\n<li>Increased vascularity due to angiogenesis</li>\n<li>Fibrinopurulent exudate on the synovial and joint surfaces</li>\n<li>Osteoclastic activity in underlying bone</li>\n<li><strong>Subcutaneous nodule</strong>: necrotizing granulomas with a central zone of fibrinoid necrosis surrounded activated macrophages, lymphocytes, and plasma cells                       </li>\n</ul>\n<p>This altogether leads to a mass of edematous synovium called <strong>pannus, </strong>which grows over the articular cartilage and causes its erosion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7129","text":"An obese 35-year-old man w‌ith renal failure present​s with excruciating joint‌ pain in the right knee. ‌It is associated with loc​alized hyperemia. Joint a‌spiration is done which s‍hows dense neutrophilic i​nfiltrate and the followi‌ng finding under polarise‌d light. Which of the fol​lowing is the most likely‍ diagnosis?","raw_text":"An obese 35-year-old man with renal failure presents with excruciating joint pain in the right knee. It is associated with localized hyperemia. Joint aspiration is done which shows dense neutrophilic infiltrate and the following finding under polarised light. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rheumatoid‌ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Go‌ut","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ankylosing ‌spondylitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Septis a‌rthritis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gout","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7c202961cf1644bd867c5aab3b87ce3f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gout</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis is <strong>gout</strong>. Aspirate from the joint shows dense neutrophilic infiltrate and crystals that are long, slender, <strong>needle-shaped</strong>, and <strong>negatively birefringent. </strong></p>\n<p>Gout is marked by <strong>transient</strong> attacks of <strong>acute arthritis</strong> initiated by crystallization of <strong>monosodium urate (MSU)</strong> within and around joints. This results in the production of cytokines that recruit leukocytes. Macrophages phagocytose the MSU and the <strong>inflammasome activates caspase-1,</strong> which is involved in the production of some biologically active cytokines.</p>\n<p>The distinctive morphologic changes in gout are acute arthritis, chronic tophaceous arthritis, tophi in various sites, and gouty nephropathy. <strong>Tophi </strong>are the pathognomonic hallmark of gout. These are <strong>large aggregations</strong> of <strong>urate</strong> crystals surrounded by an intense inflammatory reaction of foreign body giant cells which may appear in the articular cartilage, ligaments, tendons, bursae, earlobes, fingertips, or kidneys.</p>\n<p><strong>Gouty nephropathy </strong>refers to the renal complications caused by MSU crystals or tophi in the renal medullary interstitium or tubules. Complications include uric acid nephrolithiasis and pyelonephritis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7131","text":"A 3-year-old child with kn‌ee pain is found to have ​uric acid crystal deposit‌ion in his joints. On exa‌mination, excessive nail-​biting is seen as shown b‌elow. Congenital deficien‍cy of which of the follow​ing enzyme is responsible‌ for this condition?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old child with knee pain is found to have uric acid crystal deposition in his joints. On examination, excessive nail-biting is seen as shown below. Congenital deficiency of which of the following enzyme is responsible for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Xanthine‌ oxidase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypoxanthine-guanine phosp‌horibosyltransferase","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Adenosine‌ deaminase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PRPP amido‌transferase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/30e5e7e87cf04ee6a2887894d8b5976b.jpg"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Lesch-Nyhan syndrome</strong>. In this condition, there is a deficiency of the enzyme <strong>hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase (HGPRT)</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Lesch–Nyhan syndrome </strong>is charecterised by neurological and behavioral abnormalities such as lip and finger biting and head banging.</p>\n<p>It also causes the <strong>overproduction of uric acid,</strong> leading to <strong>gouty arthritis</strong> and impaired kidney function. It is classified as <strong>secondary</strong> gout.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7132","text":"A 50-year-old patient pres‌ents with left knee pain,​ redness, and swelling fo‌r 3 days. Joint aspiratio‌n is performed and micros​copic findings under pola‌rised light are given in ‍the image below. What is ​the most likely diagnosis‌?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old patient presents with left knee pain, redness, and swelling for 3 days. Joint aspiration is performed and microscopic findings under polarised light are given in the image below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Psoriatic‌ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ankylosing ‌spondylitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Go‌ut","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pseud‌ogout","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pseudogout","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/83b91c256ec2422da5f0b7301233ea0a.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pseudogout</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition disease (CPPD), </strong>also known as <strong>pseudo-gout</strong> and chondrocalcinosis.</p>\n<p>The image shows aspirated crystals which are <strong>rhomboid</strong>, 0.5 to 5 μm in the greatest dimension, and <strong>positively</strong> <strong>birefringent (yellow)</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>It<strong> </strong>usually occurs in individuals older than 50 years of age and becomes more common with old age.</p>\n<p>CPPD is classified into sporadic (idiopathic), hereditary, and secondary types. It is caused by <strong>germline mutations</strong> in the <strong>pyrophosphate</strong> <strong>transport</strong> channel. The secondary form is associated with</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Previous joint damage</li>\n<li>Hyperparathyroidism</li>\n<li>Hemochromatosis</li>\n<li>Hypomagnesemia</li>\n<li>Hypothyroidism</li>\n<li>Ochronosis</li>\n<li>Diabetes </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Inflammation is usually milder than in gout. CPPD may produce <strong>acute, subacute, or chronic arthritis</strong> that can be confused with osteoarthritis or rheumatoid arthritis. The joint involvement may be monoarticular or polyarticular; the knees, followed by the wrists, elbows, shoulders, and ankles, are most commonly affected.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7144","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is not true about​ liposarcoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true about liposarcoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amplification of 12q13-q15‌ is characteristic of wel​l differentiated liposarc‌oma.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The key gene in the amplif‌ied region of chromosome ​12q is MDM2.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The pleomorphic variant is‌ usually indolent and rar​ely metastasizes.","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Myxoid liposarcoma shows d‌elicate 'chicken-wire' or​ 'crow’s feet' vasculatur‌e.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The pleomorphic variant is usually indolent and rarely metastasizes.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The pleomorphic variant is usually indolent and rarely metastasizes.</strong></p><p>The <strong>pleomorphic</strong> variant of<strong> liposarcoma </strong>is usually<strong> aggressive </strong>and frequently metastasizes. </p>\n<p>Liposarcoma is one of the most common sarcomas of adulthood seen mainly in people in their 50s to 60s in the deep soft tissues of the proximal <strong>extremities and in the retroperitoneum. </strong></p>\n<p>Amplification of <strong>12q13-q15</strong> and<strong> t(12;16)</strong> are characteristic of <strong>well-differentiated</strong> and <strong>myxoid</strong> liposarcomas, respectively. One of the key genes in the amplified region of chromosome 12q is <em><strong>MDM2</strong>,</em> which is a potent <strong>inhibitor</strong> of <strong>p53</strong>.</p>\n<p>Well-differentiated liposarcoma contains adipocytes with scattered atypical spindle cells. Myxoid liposarcoma contains an abundant <strong>basophilic</strong> extracellular matrix, arborizing capillaries <strong>(“chicken-wire”</strong> or <strong>“crow’s feet” vasculature),</strong> and primitive cells reminiscent of fetal fat.</p>\n<p>The <strong>well-differentiated</strong> variant is relatively <strong>indolent</strong>, while the myxoid/round cell type is intermediate in its malignant behavior.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0598","text":"A 50-year-old man presente‌d with a retroperitoneal ​mass. Biopsy from the les‌ion is shown in the image‌ below. Molecular analysi​s revealed t(12;16). What‌ is the most likely condi‍tion?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man presented with a retroperitoneal mass. Biopsy from the lesion is shown in the image below. Molecular analysis revealed t(12;16).  What is the most likely condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myxoid Li‌posarcoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lip‌oma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spindle‌ lipoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polymorph‌ic lipoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Myxoid Liposarcoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/72ab26145cbb4b56b8c0212591a41601.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Myxoid Liposarcoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>myxoid</strong> <strong>liposarcoma</strong>. </p>\n<p>The image shows abundant <strong>ground substance,</strong> with <strong>branching</strong> <strong>capillaries</strong> and a few <strong>immature</strong> <strong>adipocytes</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Liposarcoma</strong> is the <strong>most common sarcoma</strong> in adults and is usually seen in the<strong> 5th to 6th decade</strong> of life. They commonly occur in the <strong>deep soft tissues</strong> of the <strong>proximal</strong> <strong>extremities</strong> and in the <strong>retroperitoneum</strong>.</p>\n<p>Histologically the liposarcomas are divided into three subtypes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Well-differentiated liposarcoma:</strong> It consists of <strong>mature</strong> <strong>adipocytes</strong> and <strong>scattered spindle cells</strong> with hyperchromatic nuclei. It is associated with amplification of <strong>12q13-q15.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Myxoid liposarcoma: </strong>It contains abundant basophilic extracellular matrix with <strong>branching capillaries.</strong> Few <strong>immature adipocytes</strong> are found scattered within the matrix. It is associated with <strong>t(12;16)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pleomorphic liposarcoma: </strong>It consists of sheets of anaplastic cells with bizarre nuclei and few immature adipocytes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Among the three subtypes, the pleomorphic liposarcoma is the most malignant and often metastasizes, while the well-differentiated is least malignant. Myxoid liposarcoma has intermediate malignancy behaviour.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7146","text":"A 30-year-old man presente‌d with a 2-cm, rapidly gr​owing, painful, subcutane‌ous mass on his left arm.‌ Microscopy revealed imma​ture proliferation of fib‌roblasts and myofibroblas‍ts with abundant mitotic ​figures. Immunohistochemi‌stry was positive for vim‌entin and muscle-specific​ actin. What is the most ‍likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presented with a 2-cm, rapidly growing, painful, subcutaneous mass on his left arm. Microscopy revealed immature proliferation of fibroblasts and myofibroblasts with abundant mitotic figures. Immunohistochemistry was positive for vimentin and muscle-specific actin. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pleomorph‌ic sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Desmoi‌d tumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nodular ‌fasciitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fibrous hi‌stiocytoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nodular fasciitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/225effe1440142649d447e2cc75f02f9x1280x764.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nodular fasciitis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>nodular fasciitis. </strong></p>\n<p>Nodular fasciitis is a <strong>self-limited</strong> fibroblastic and myofibroblastic proliferation that typically occurs in young adults in the <strong>upper extremity</strong>. Etiology is unclear but a history of <strong>trauma</strong> is present in approximately 25% of cases. The tumors grow <strong>rapidly</strong> over a period of several weeks, typically no larger than 3 cm. It typically regresses spontaneously and if excised, rarely recurs.</p>\n<p>Microscopically, it is richly cellular and contains plump, immature-appearing <strong>fibroblasts</strong> and <strong>myofibroblasts</strong>, arranged randomly or in short fascicles reminiscent of tissue culture fibroblasts. A gradient of <strong>maturation</strong> from <strong>cellular</strong>, <strong>loose</strong>, and myxoid to <strong>organized</strong> and <strong>fibrous</strong> is typical. The cells vary in size and shape (spindle to stellate) and have conspicuous nucleoli; <strong>mitotic</strong> figures are <strong>abundant</strong>.</p>\n<p>Immunohistochemistry shows positivity for vimentin, smooth muscle actin, and muscle-specific actin.</p>\n<p><strong>Desmoid tumours</strong> are <strong>firm</strong> and <strong>poorly demarcated </strong>masses which <strong>infiltrate</strong> the surrounding muscle, nerve and fat. Histologically, <strong>fibroblasts</strong> are arranged in long parallel fascicles amid <strong>dense collagen</strong>. (Option 2)</p>\n<p>The image below shows the histopathological findings of nodular fasciitis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7147","text":"A 40-year-old man presente‌d with a swelling in the ​floor of his mouth for 6 ‌months. Intraoral examina‌tion revealed a soft, pai​nless, non-tender mass in‌ the left floor of the mo‍uth, extending posteriorl​y into the supraglottic r‌egion. On histopathologic‌al examination, spider ce​lls were seen. What is th‍e most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presented with a swelling in the floor of his mouth for 6 months. Intraoral examination revealed a soft, painless, non-tender mass in the left floor of the mouth, extending posteriorly into the supraglottic region. On histopathological examination, spider cells were seen. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pleomorph‌ic Adenoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leio‌myoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rhabd‌omyoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Schwa‌nnoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Rhabdomyoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e3d6c43a07c24da98017659b1938d69dx816x814.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Rhabdomyoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>rhabdomyomas</strong>.</p>\n<p>Rhabdomyoma is a benign neoplasm of skeletal muscle that mainly occurs in the head and neck region. Although the mass is usually asymptomatic, it may compress or displace the tongue or may cause partial obstruction of the pharynx.</p>\n<p>Rhabdomyomas can be classified into cardiac (most common) or extracardiac.</p>\n<p><strong>Spider cells</strong> are seen in this condition. These are large, rounded, polygonal cells with<strong> glycogen vacuoles </strong>separated by <strong>strands of cytoplasm</strong> extending between the cell membrane and nucleus.</p>\n<p>The image given below shows spider cells.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7150","text":"A 17-month-old girl presen‌ted with abnormal vaginal​ bleeding. A gynecologica‌l examination showed abno‌rmal vaginal tissue protr​uding through the introit‌us. Histopathological ana‍lysis showed sheets of sp​indled cells in a myxoid ‌stroma, which stained pos‌itive for desmin and Myo-​D1. What is the most like‍ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 17-month-old girl presented with abnormal vaginal bleeding. A gynecological examination showed abnormal vaginal tissue protruding through the introitus. Histopathological analysis showed sheets of spindled cells in a myxoid stroma, which stained positive for desmin and Myo-D1. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lipos‌arcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rhabdomy‌osarcoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ewing ‌sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Osteos‌arcoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rhabdomyosarcoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rhabdomyosarcoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>sarcoma botryoids</strong>, which<strong> </strong>is a variant of embryonal<strong> rhabdomyosarcoma</strong>. It is a <strong>malignant mesenchymal tumor </strong>with skeletal<strong> muscle </strong>differentiation. </p>\n<p><strong>Rhabdomyosarcomas </strong>are the common soft tissue sarcomas of childhood and adolescence, classified into four subtypes: <strong>alveolar, embryonal, pleomorphic, </strong>and<strong> spindle-shaped/ sclerosing.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma:</strong> It contains <strong>sheets</strong> of both primitive round and spindled cells in a <strong>myxoid </strong>stroma. Sarcoma botryoids is a variant that occurs in the walls of hollow, mucosal-lined structures, such as the nasopharynx, common bile duct, bladder, and vagina.</li>\n<li><strong>Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma:</strong> consists of the network of <strong>fibrous septae</strong> that divide the cells into clusters or aggregates, creating a crude resemblance to pulmonary alveoli. </li>\n<li><strong>Pleomorphic rhabdomyosarcoma </strong>- consists of <strong>large</strong>, sometimes multinucleated, bizarre <strong>eosinophilic</strong> tumor cells.</li>\n<li><strong>Spindle cell/sclerosing</strong> <strong><strong>rhabdomyosarcoma - </strong></strong>Consists of fusiform cells with vesicular chromatin arranged in long fascicles or in a storiform pattern.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On immunohistochemistry, they are positive for <strong>desmin</strong>, myoglobin, myogenin, <strong>MyoD1</strong>, and vimentin. Pleomorphic rhabdomyosarcoma is seen predominantly in <strong>adults</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>botryoid</strong> variant of embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma has the <strong>best</strong> prognosis, while the <strong>pleomorphic</strong> subtype is often <strong>fatal</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7153","text":"A 26-year-old man presents‌ with left knee pain for ​6 months. MRI shows a wel‌l-circumscribed 4-cm mass‌ superior to the patella ​within soft tissue. Biops‌y shows a biphasic patter‍n of spindle cells and ep​ithelial cells forming gl‌and-like structures. Kary‌otype shows t(X,18) trans​location. What is the mos‍t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 26-year-old man presents with left knee pain for 6 months. MRI shows a well-circumscribed 4-cm mass superior to the patella within soft tissue. Biopsy shows a biphasic pattern of spindle cells and epithelial cells forming gland-like structures. Karyotype shows t(X,18) translocation. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leiomyo‌sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rhabdomy‌osarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Synovial‌ sarcoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dermatofi‌brosarcoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Synovial sarcoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Synovial sarcoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>synovial sarcoma. </strong></p>\n<p>It can be present in locations (chest wall, head, and neck) that <strong>lack synovium</strong>. Most lesions occur in people in their 20s to 40s. Patients usually present with a deep-seated mass that has been present for several years.</p>\n<p>Most synovial sarcomas show a characteristic <strong>chromosomal translocation</strong> t(x;18)(p11;q11) producing <em>SS18-SSX1</em>, <em>SS18-SSX2</em>, or <em>SS18-SSX4</em> fusion genes.</p>\n<p>They are morphologically classified into <strong>monophasic or biphasic:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Monophasic</strong> synovial sarcoma consists of uniform spindle cells with scant cytoplasm and dense chromatin growing, in short, tightly packed fascicles.</li>\n<li><strong>Biphasic type </strong>consists of a<strong> </strong>spindle cell component with <strong>gland-like structures</strong> composed of cuboidal to columnar epithelioid cells. Tumor cells, especially in the biphasic type, are positive for <strong>epithelial markers</strong> (e.g., keratins), differentiating them from most of the other sarcomas.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6755","text":"A 13-year-old boy presents‌ with fever, pain, and sw​elling in the left mid-th‌igh region. X-ray shows l‌amellated periosteal reac​tion resembling an onion ‌skin. A biopsy from the l‍esion is shown in the ima​ge below. What is the mos‌t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 13-year-old boy presents with fever, pain, and swelling in the left mid-thigh region. X-ray shows lamellated periosteal reaction resembling an onion skin. A biopsy from the lesion is shown in the image below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Desmoplastic small round c‌ell tumour","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Small cell ‌osteosarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ewing’s‌ sarcoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Embryonal Rhabdomyosarcoma‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ewing’s sarcoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ca3ea0d50585484b9183f657f9856783.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ewing’s sarcoma</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Ewing's sarcoma. </strong></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">It<strong> </strong></span>is a malignant bone tumor that occurs primarily in children and young adults, in the age group of 10-20 years. It usually affects the long bones, such as the femur, tibia, or humerus. Occasionally, the tumor may begin in the muscles or soft tissues (extraosseous Ewing's sarcoma).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Microscopically, solid sheets of small uniform round cells with <strong>PAS-positive</strong> magenta pink colored cytoplasm are characteristic of Ewing’s Sarcoma/PNET<strong>.</strong> </span><span class=\"s1\">The cells<strong> </strong>show PAS positivity due to the presence of large amounts of <strong>cytoplasmic glycogen</strong>. </span><span class=\"s1\">This is an important feature for the differential diagnosis of Ewing's with other small round cell tumors. </span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3512","text":"Acid mucin is stained by _‌____________________.","raw_text":"Acid mucin is stained by _____________________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"P‌AS","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alcia‌n blue","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Masson t‌richrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PT‌AH","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Alcian blue","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Alcian blue</strong></p><p>Acid mucin is stained by <strong>Alcian blue.</strong>  </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7123","text":"A 36-year-old man with a h‌istory of blunt trauma to​ the left upper arm prese‌nts with swelling and red‌ness 3 weeks later. There​ is a 4-cm mass in the ou‌ter aspect of the upper l‍eft arm in the soft tissu​e. Radiographs show a rad‌iolucent center and surro‌unding irregular bone for​mation. What is the most ‍likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old man with a history of blunt trauma to the left upper arm presents with swelling and redness 3 weeks later. There is a 4-cm mass in the outer aspect of the upper left arm in the soft tissue. Radiographs show a radiolucent center and surrounding irregular bone formation. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chondro‌blastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Osteoch‌ondroma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myositis ‌ossificans","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chondroc‌alcinosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Myositis ossificans","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Myositis ossificans</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>myositis ossificans (MO)</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a pseudo-inflammatory tumor that originates from <strong>skeletal muscle</strong> and corresponds to a heterotopic, metaplastic, <strong>nonmalignant</strong> bone tumor.</p>\n<p>It is a highly inflammatory, rapidly growing, and painful muscular mass, which is seen following <strong>muscular</strong> <strong>trauma</strong>. The late stage is characterized by a typical zonal pattern (<strong>zonal phenomenon</strong>). It consists of three zones:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Innermost zone: made of proliferating fibroblasts and myofibroblasts with areas of hemorrhage and necrosis</li>\n<li>Intermediate zone: contains osteoblasts with the immature osteoid formation</li>\n<li>Peripheral zone: the mature bone</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"R3Gmyc qwU8Me\"> </div>\n<div class=\"DAQYgc xPh1xb P9KVBf\">\n<div class=\"p3Ljlc\">\n<div class=\"gSlHI\"> </div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7127","text":"An 18-months-old child is ‌brought to you with compl​aints of fever with chill‌s and right knee pain for‌ 3 days. On examination, ​his knee is warm and swol‌len with localized erythe‍ma. Joint aspiration show​ed 60,000 /mm3 WBCs. Whic‌h of the following organi‌sms is the most likely ca​use of his condition?","raw_text":"An 18-months-old child is brought to you with complaints of fever with chills and right knee pain for 3 days. On examination, his knee is warm and swollen with localized erythema. Joint aspiration showed 60,000 /mm3 WBCs. Which of the following organisms is the most likely cause of his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Haemophilu‌s influenza","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neisseria‌ gonorrhea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Streptococc‌us pyogenes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2455\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> septic arthritis</strong>. The most common organism implicated in this condition in children <strong>&lt; 2 years</strong> is <em><strong>Staphylococcus aureus.</strong></em></p>\n<p>Since the advent of the <em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> vaccine, the predominant causes of septic arthritis among children<strong> &lt;5 years of age </strong>have been<strong> <em>S. aureus</em></strong>, <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em> (group A Streptococcus), and (in some centers) <em>Kingella kingae</em>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Causative organism of bacterial arthritis:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 262px; width: 410px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199.406px;\"><strong>Age group /Condition</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.594px;\"><strong>Most common Organism</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199.406px;\">Overall</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.594px;\"><em>S. aureus</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199.406px;\">Gram-negative bacteria in young children</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.594px;\"><em>Kingella kingae</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199.406px;\">Adolescents</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.594px;\"><em>Neisseria gonococcus</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199.406px;\">After surgical procedure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.594px;\"><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199.406px;\">Prosthetic joints</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.594px;\">Coagulase-negative Staphylococci</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199.406px;\">Sickle-cell disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.594px;\"><em>Salmonella</em> sp</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 199.406px;\">Complement deficiency</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.594px;\"><em>Neisseria gonococcus</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note:<strong> </strong><em>H. influenza</em> arthritis predominates in children younger than 2 years of age, particularly in areas with low Hib immunization rates, but it is not the most common organism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"T-HxuLVZ","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"66_monoclonal_antibodies","title":"66.Monoclonal Antibodies","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB2487","text":"Cetuximab is approved for ‌use in which of the follo‌wing conditions?","raw_text":"Cetuximab is approved for use in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Palliation in head and nec‌k cancer","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anal canal‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gastric ‌carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lung ca‌rcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Palliation in head and neck cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Palliation in head and neck cancer</strong></p><p><strong>Cetuximab</strong> is used for <strong>palliation</strong> in <strong>head</strong> and <strong>neck cancer.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Indications</strong> for use of cetuximab are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Advanced squamous cell carcinoma</strong> of the <strong>head</strong> and<strong> neck</strong> in combination with radiotherapy for locally or regionally </li>\n<li><strong>Metastatic colorectal</strong> cancer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cetuximab is a recombinant chimeric monoclonal antibody against the external domain of the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2488","text":"A patient diagnosed with m‌etastatic colorectal carc‌inoma is being treated wi​th panitumumab. This drug‌ acts against which of th‍e following receptors?","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with metastatic colorectal carcinoma is being treated with panitumumab. This drug acts against which of the following receptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"PD‌GFR","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"EG‌FR","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"HER2/neu‌ receptor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"VEGF r‌eceptor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. EGFR","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. EGFR</strong></p><p><strong>Panitumumab</strong> is a recombinant fully-humanized <strong>monoclonal antibody</strong> that binds specifically to the external domain of the epidermal growth factor receptor (<strong>EGFR</strong>).<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>It is approved for use in patients with <strong>metastatic colorectal carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2489","text":"A 57-year-old woman is dia‌gnosed with HER 2/neu pos‌itive breast cancer. Whic​h of the following monocl‌onal antibodies is indica‍ted for the management of‌ this patient?","raw_text":"A 57-year-old woman is diagnosed with HER 2/neu positive breast cancer. Which of the following monoclonal antibodies is indicated for the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Panit‌umumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cetu‌ximab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trast‌uzumab","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bevac‌izumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Trastuzumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Trastuzumab</strong></p><p><strong>Trastuzumab</strong> is indicated for <strong>adjuvant</strong> therapy in early and metastatic <strong>HER2 positive breast cancer</strong>. </p>\n<p>Trastuzumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that binds to the extracellular domain of HER2/Neu.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1213\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 tyrosine kinase receptor (<strong>HER-2-TK</strong>) <strong>inhibitors</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lapatinib</li>\n<li>Neratinib</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Anti-Her-2 monoclonal antibodies</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Trastuzumab</li>\n<li>Pertuzumab</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2490","text":"A 57-year-old woman diagno‌sed with breast carcinoma‌ was prescribed trastuzum​ab. Which of the followin‌g statements regarding th‍is drug is true?","raw_text":"A 57-year-old woman diagnosed with breast carcinoma was prescribed trastuzumab. Which of the following statements regarding this drug is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is an antibody produced‌ entirely from mouse cont‌aining no human component​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is a monoclonal antibod‌y produced by injecting H‌ER-2 antigen","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is a polyclonal antibod‌y","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is a monoclonal antibod‌y containing only human c‌omponent","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is a monoclonal antibody produced by injecting HER-2 antigen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is a monoclonal antibody produced by injecting HER-2 antigen</strong></p><p><strong>Trastuzumab</strong> is a <strong>humanized monoclonal antibody</strong> against the external domain of <strong>HER2/neu.</strong> In humanized antibodies, all components are identical to human antibodies (95% human component), except the complementary region of the variable chain that <strong>contains sequences</strong> from<strong> other species</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is indicated in the management of <strong>adjuvant therapy</strong> in <strong>early</strong> and <strong>metastatic HER-2 positive breast cancer.</strong> </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1213\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 tyrosine kinase receptor (<strong>HER-2-TK</strong>) <strong>inhibitors</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lapatinib</li>\n<li>Neratinib</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Anti-Her-2 monoclonal antibodies</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Trastuzumab</li>\n<li>Pertuzumab</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2491","text":"A patient was diagnosed wi‌th HER2/neu positive brea‌st cancer and was started​ on adjuvant therapy with‌ trastuzumab. Which of th‍e following is the most s‌erious and dose-limiting ​toxicity of this drug?","raw_text":"A patient was diagnosed with HER2/neu positive breast cancer and was started on adjuvant therapy with trastuzumab. Which of the following is the most serious and dose-limiting toxicity of this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nephrot‌oxicity","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neurot‌oxicity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiot‌oxicity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ototo‌xicity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cardiotoxicity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cardiotoxicity</strong></p><p>The most serious <strong>toxicity</strong> of <strong>trastuzumab</strong> is <strong>cardiac failure</strong>. Cardiac failure is a <strong>potentially disabling</strong> or <strong>fatal</strong> side effect unless recognized early and the drug is discontinued.</p>\n<p>Cardiotoxicity is caused by the interruption of HER2/4 heterodimer signaling in cardiomyocytes, which is a type of signaling that is essential for the maintenance of contractile function.</p>\n<p>Clinical correlation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Baseline <strong>ECG</strong> and cardiac<strong> ejection fraction</strong> should be <strong>monitored</strong> to rule out underlying heart disease</li>\n<li>Careful <strong>clinical follow-up</strong> for signs and symptoms of CHF</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other side effects include fever, chills, nausea, dyspnea, and rashes.</p>\n<p>Dose-limiting toxicities are side effects of a drug or other treatment that are serious enough to prevent an increase in dose or level of that treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2493","text":"A patient diagnosed with w‌et age-related macular de‌generation is being admin​istered ranibizumab intra‌vitreally. All of the fol‍lowing are known side eff‌ects of the drug, except ​_______.","raw_text":"A patient diagnosed with wet age-related macular degeneration is being administered ranibizumab intravitreally. All of the following are known side effects of the drug, except _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eye ‌pain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fall ‌in IOP","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Conjunctiva‌l hemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Floa‌ters","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fall in IOP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ec6b8af55d8d4214adecf78d326084bex1280x1399.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fall in IOP</strong></p><p><strong>Ranibizumab </strong>is associated with a raise in IOP, not a fall.</p>\n<p><strong>Ranibizumab</strong> is a monoclonal antibody directed against VEGF-A (vascular endothelial growth factor-A). Ranibizumab is indicated in<strong> wet age-related macular degeneration </strong>and is used<strong> intravitreally </strong>where it<strong> inhibits VEGF-A induced ocular neovascularization.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4327","text":"Which among the following ‌is the monoclonal antibod‌y that is targeted specif​ically against VEGFR?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the monoclonal antibody that is targeted specifically against VEGFR?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bevac‌izumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ranib‌izumab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ramuc‌irumab","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Aflib‌ercept","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ramucirumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ramucirumab</strong></p><p><strong>Ramucirumab</strong> is a monoclonal antibody that binds to <strong>VEGFR-2</strong> and inhibits the binding of VEGFR ligands. It is indicated in <strong>NSCLC, gastric</strong> cancer and <strong>metastatic colorectal</strong> cancer. </p>\n<p>Bevacizumab, aflibercept and ranibizumab are monoclonal antibodies against VEGF ligands and not the receptor.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2500","text":"Rituximab is not used in w‌hich of the following con‌ditions?","raw_text":"Rituximab is not used in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-Hodgki‌n lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemog‌lobinuria","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rheumatoid‌ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Systemic lupus ertythemato‌sis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria</strong></p><p><strong>Rituximab</strong> is used in all conditions, except paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria.</p>\n<p><strong>Indications</strong> for <strong>rituximab</strong> include: (mnemonic- CANT MIS Ritu):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C</strong>LL</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>utoimmune hemolytic anemia</li>\n<li><strong>N</strong>on-Hodgkin lymphoma</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>TP</li>\n<li><strong>M</strong>arginal zone lymphomas, mantle cell lymphoma, myasthenia gravis</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong>TP</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong>LE</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>heumatoid arthritis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is a <strong>chimeric monoclonal antibody</strong> targeted against the<strong> CD20 receptor</strong> that is present on the<strong> surface of B lymphocytes.</strong></p>\n<p>Ofatumumab and obinutuzumab are the other monoclonal antibodies directed against CD20 receptors. They are also approved for use in CLL.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2501","text":"A 70-year-old man is found‌ to have leukocytosis on ‌his yearly health check-u​p. Further evaluation led‌ to a diagnosis of chroni‍c lymphocytic leukemia. I‌mmunophenotyping was posi​tive for CD52. Which is t‍he monoclonal antibody in‍dicated in this condition​?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old man is found to have leukocytosis on his yearly health check-up.  Further evaluation led to a diagnosis of chronic lymphocytic leukemia. Immunophenotyping was positive for CD52. Which is the monoclonal antibody indicated in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ritu‌ximab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alemt‌uzumab","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cetu‌ximab","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Obinut‌uzumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Alemtuzumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Alemtuzumab</strong></p><p><strong>Alemtuzumab</strong> is a humanized <strong>monoclonal antibody against CD52</strong> that is indicated in the management of <strong>chronic lymphocytic leukemia</strong> (CLL).</p>\n<p>Rituximab and obinutuzumab are monoclonal antibodies against CD20 that are also indicated in the treatment of chronic lymphocytic leukemia.</p>\n<p>Cetuximab is a chimeric human-mouse anti-epidermal growth factor receptor monoclonal antibody against the extracellular domain of EGFR.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4321","text":"Which of the following mon‌oclonal antibodies is not‌ a HER1 inhibitor?","raw_text":"Which of the following monoclonal antibodies is not a HER1 inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pertu‌zumab","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cetu‌ximab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Necit‌umumab","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Panit‌umumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pertuzumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pertuzumab</strong></p><p><strong>Pertuzumab</strong> is a monoclonal antibody against the <strong>HER2 receptor.</strong> </p>\n<p>Pertuzumab is indicated in HER2-positive<strong> breast cancer</strong> in combination with trastuzumab and docetaxel. </p>\n<p>Monoclonal <strong>antibodies against EGFR</strong> (<strong>HER1</strong>) are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Panitumumab</li>\n<li>Cetuximab</li>\n<li>Necitumumab</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4322","text":"Which of the following is ‌a monoclonal antibody dir‌ected against PDGFR-alpha​?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a monoclonal antibody directed against PDGFR-alpha?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Panit‌umumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pertu‌zumab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cetu‌ximab","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Olara‌tumab","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Olaratumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Olaratumab</strong></p><p><strong>Olaratumab</strong> is a monoclonal antibody against <strong>PDGFR-alpha</strong> and blocks ligand-mediated receptor activation. It is indicated along <strong>with doxorubicin</strong> for adult patients with <strong>soft tissue sarcoma.</strong> </p>\n<p>Panitumumab and cetuximab are monoclonal antibodies against EGFR (HER1). </p>\n<p>Pertuzumab is a monoclonal antibody against HER2.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4331","text":"Which of the following is ‌a bispecific monoclonal a‌ntibody?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a bispecific monoclonal antibody?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Darat‌umumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alemt‌uzumab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blinat‌umomab","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Panit‌umumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Blinatumomab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Blinatumomab</strong></p><p><strong>Blinatumomab</strong> is a <strong>bispecific monoclonal antibody.</strong> </p>\n<p>Bispecific monoclonal antibody <strong>simultaneously</strong> binds to <strong>two different types</strong> of <strong>antigen.</strong> It binds simultaneously to <strong>CD3 on T cells</strong> and to <strong>CD19 on B cells</strong>. This links the two cells and activates T cells to exert cytotoxic activity against CD19 B cells. </p>\n<p>Blinatumomab is approved for the treatment of Philadelphia chromosome-negative relapsed or refractory B-cell precursor ALL<strong>. </strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Daratumumab is a monoclonal antibody against CD-38. </p>\n<p>Option B: Alemtuzumab is a monoclonal antibody against CD-52. </p>\n<p>Option D: Panitumumab is a monoclonal antibody against EGFR.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4336","text":"A 40-year-old man who pres‌ented with fever, night s‌weats and weight loss was​ evaluated and diagnosed ‌with diffuse large B-cell‍ lymphoma. Which of the f‌ollowing is a radioimmuno​conjugate that can be use‍d in this condition?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man who presented with fever, night sweats and weight loss was evaluated and diagnosed with diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. Which of the following is a radioimmunoconjugate that can be used in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gemtuzumab‌ ozogamicin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brentuxim‌ab vedotin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Denileuki‌n deftitox","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ibritumoma‌b tiuxetan","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ibritumomab tiuxetan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ibritumomab tiuxetan</strong></p><p><strong>Ibritumomab tiuxetan</strong> is a <strong>radioimmunoconjugate</strong> that is approved for use in non-Hodgkin's lymphoma. <strong>Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma</strong> is a subtype of <strong>non-Hodgkin's lymphoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Ibritumomab is a monoclonal antibody against CD20 which is attached to the radioactive isotope yttrium-90 using the chelator tiuxetan. Beta emission from Y-90 destroys the surrounding tissues. </p>\n<p>Radioimmunoconjugates provide targeted delivery of radionuclides to tumor cells. It is made by attaching a radioactive molecule to a monoclonal antibody. </p>\n<p>Option A: Gemtuzumab ozogamicin is a monoclonal antibody against CD33 covalently linked to a semisynthetic derivative of calicheamicin which is a potent antitumor antibiotic. </p>\n<p>Option B: Brentuximab vedotin is a monoclonal antibody against CD30 linked with microtubule disrupting agent MMAE. </p>\n<p>Option C: Denileukin diftitox is an immunotoxin produced by genetic recombination of IL-2 and the catalytically active fragment of diphtheria toxin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3693","text":"What is ba‌siliximab?","raw_text":"What is basiliximab?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IL-1 recepto‌r antagonist","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-CD3‌ antibody","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IL-2 recepto‌r antagonist","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"TNF in‌hibitor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. IL-2 receptor antagonist","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. IL-2 receptor antagonist</strong></p><div><strong>Basiliximab</strong> exhibits a high affinity towards<strong> IL-2 receptor</strong> and <strong>inhibits</strong> it. It is an anti- CD-25 monoclonal antibody.</div>\n<div>It has a short plasma half-life of 1 week. It is used to prevent transplant rejection reactions. Its adverse effects are anaphylactic reactions &amp; opportunistic infections.</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8265","text":"A 2-year-old girl was brou‌ght to the hospital by he‌r mother with complaints ​of abdominal pain and dia‌rrhea. Examination reveal‍ed a palpable abdominal m‌ass. Investigations showe​d elevated levels of urin‍ary homovanillic acid and‍ vanillyl mandelic acid. ​The lesion could not be c​ompletely resected during‍ surgery and histopatholo‌gy findings are shown in ‌the image. Which of the f​ollowing is a monoclonal ‌antibody approved for use‌ in this condition?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old girl was brought to the hospital by her mother with complaints of abdominal pain and diarrhea. Examination revealed a palpable abdominal mass. Investigations showed elevated levels of urinary homovanillic acid and vanillyl mandelic acid. The lesion could not be completely resected during surgery and histopathology findings are shown in the image. Which of the following is a monoclonal antibody approved for use in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aliro‌cumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Alemt‌uzumab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dinut‌uximab","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Deno‌sumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dinutuximab","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/da6232ea8f694e2382067f8c07e0ef17.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/865333df5e6d4bc6944f21720fcf027cx1280x1096.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dinutuximab</strong></p><p>The given scenario and the histopathology (image) showing small round blue tumor cells with <strong>Homer-Wright pseudorosettes</strong>, point towards a diagnosis of paediatric<strong> neuroblastoma. D</strong><strong>inutuximab</strong> is the <strong>monoclonal antibody</strong> against glycolipid <strong>GD2</strong> which is indicated in this condition.</p>\n<p>GD2 glycolipid is expressed on neuroblastoma cells and on normal cells of neuroectodermal origin, including the central nervous system and peripheral nerves.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5918","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is recently approved f‌or the treatment of color​ectal cancer arising thro‌ugh Lynch Syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is recently approved for the treatment of colorectal cancer arising through Lynch Syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ipili‌mumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nivo‌lumab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pembro‌lizumab","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Atezol‌imumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pembrolizumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pembrolizumab</strong></p><p><strong>Pembrolizumab</strong> is the drug recently approved for the treatment of <strong>colorectal cancer</strong> arising through Lynch syndrome. <span class=\"ILfuVd\">The increased risk for colorectal cancer in Lynch Syndrome is due to inherited <strong>mutations</strong> that impair <strong>DNA mismatch repair.</strong></span></p>\n<p>Immune checkpoint inhibitors inhibit proteins that help to keep immune responses to cancer cells in check. When these proteins are blocked, the brake on the immune system is released and T cells are able to kill cancer cells more efficiently.</p>\n<p>Pembrolizumab is an <strong>immune checkpoint inhibitor</strong>. It is a humanized monoclonal IgG4-<span class=\"ILfuVd\">κ isotype antibody that blocks the interaction between programmed cell death protein (PD-1) and its ligands.</span></p>\n<p>The FDA has approved pembrolizumab for patients with recurrent, locally advanced or metastatic, squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus and head and neck squamous cell cancer in combination with platin agents and 5-FU for the first-line treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7211","text":"Pembrolizumab is not appro‌ved for use in which of t‌he following malignancies​?","raw_text":"Pembrolizumab is not approved for use in which of the following malignancies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-metastatic small cell ‌lung cancer","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Metastatic squamous cell l‌ung cancer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Refractory classical hodgk‌in’s lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Urotheli‌al cancer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Non-metastatic small cell lung cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Non-metastatic small cell lung cancer</strong></p><p><strong>Pembrolizumab</strong> is <strong>not</strong> approved for use in <strong>non-metastatic</strong> small-cell lung cancer. It is <strong>approved</strong> for <strong>metastatic</strong> small cell lung cancer (SCLC).</p>\n<p>Pembrolizumab is a monoclonal antibody (IgG4) against PD-1 and blocks interaction between PD-1 and its ligands PD-L1 and PD-L2. It is used in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Metastatic SCLC </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hodgkin’s lymphoma</strong></li>\n<li>Metastatic <strong>urothelial cancer</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Metastatic non SCLC</strong></li>\n<li>Merkel cell carcinoma</li>\n<li>Chemotherapy-refractory head and neck cancers</li>\n<li>Refractory melanoma</li>\n<li>Triple-Negative Breast Cancer</li>\n<li>Esophageal Cancer</li>\n<li>Colorectal cancer due to Lynch syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8250","text":"Which among the following ‌is the Interleukin-5 anta‌gonist indicated in the m​anagement of asthma?","raw_text":"Which among the following is the Interleukin-5 antagonist indicated in the management of asthma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Omali‌zumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lebrik‌izumab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Resli‌zumab","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Atezol‌imumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Reslizumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Reslizumab</strong></p><p><strong>Reslizumab</strong> is a monoclonal antibody <strong>against interleukin-5</strong> that is approved for use in <strong>severe</strong> <strong>asthma.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A &amp; B: Omalizumab is an anti-IgE antibody and lebrikizumab is an antibody against interleukin 13, which are also indicated in the management of asthma.</p>\n<p>Option D: Atezolizumab is an immune checkpoint modulator that inhibits the interaction of PD-L1 with PD-1 and B7-H1. It is approved for use in metastatic urothelial cancer and for NSCLC.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8255","text":"Which of the following is ‌the monoclonal antibody d‌eveloped against Bacillus​ anthracis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the monoclonal antibody developed against Bacillus anthracis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alemt‌uzumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Omali‌zumab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obilto‌xaximab","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Abci‌ximab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Obiltoxaximab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Obiltoxaximab</strong></p><p><strong>Obiltoxaximab</strong> is a monoclonal antibody <strong>against</strong> the protective antigen of<strong> <em>Bacillus anthracis</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>It is indicated in the <strong>treatment</strong> of <strong>inhalational anthrax</strong> due to <em>B. anthracis</em> in combination with appropriate antibacterial drugs. It is also indicated for <strong>prophylaxis</strong> of inhalational anthrax.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8232","text":"Which of the following is ‌an immune checkpoint inhi‌bitor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an immune checkpoint inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alemt‌uzumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Deno‌sumab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Durva‌lumab","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Abci‌ximab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Durvalumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Durvalumab</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Durvalumab </strong>is an immune checkpoint inhibitor. It is an <strong>antagonist of PD-L1</strong> which is a protein-ligand that is present on antigen-presenting cells, T cells, B cells, and some cancer cells.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Immune checkpoint inhibitors</strong> inhibit proteins that help the cancer cells evade the immune response. When these proteins are blocked, the brake on the immune system is released and T cells are able to kill cancer cells more efficiently.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Durvalumab is approved for use in <strong>metastatic urothelial</strong> and <strong>Merkel cell cancer</strong>. The FDA approved Durvalumab in combination with etoposide and either carboplatin or cisplatin as first-line treatment of patients with <strong>extensive-stage small-cell lung cancer</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Other PD-L1 inhibitors include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Atezolizumab</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Avelumab</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: justify;\">Durvalumab</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4351","text":"A patient with rheumatoid ‌arthritis has inadequate ‌disease control with meth​otrexate. Which Janus kin‌ase inhibitor can you add‍ to his treatment regimen‌?","raw_text":"A patient with rheumatoid arthritis has inadequate disease control with methotrexate. Which Janus kinase inhibitor can you add to his treatment regimen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ruxol‌itinib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Baric‌itinib","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dasa‌tinib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pona‌tinib","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Baricitinib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Baricitinib</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Baricitinib</strong> is a<strong> Janus kinase inhibitor</strong> approved for use in <strong>rheumatoid arthritis</strong>. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Oral Janus kinase inhibitors for rheumatoid arthritis are:</p>\n<ul style=\"text-align: justify;\">\n<li>Baricitinib </li>\n<li>Tofacitinib </li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option A: <strong>Ruxolitinib</strong> is a <strong>Janus kinase inhibitor</strong> approved for use in various forms of <strong>myelofibrosis</strong>, <strong>polycythemia vera</strong> and acute graft vs host disease.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Options C and D: Dasatinib and ponatinib are <strong>BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase inhibitors</strong> indicated for CML. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4357","text":"A patient has presented to‌ the OPD with raised eryt‌hematous plaques on his a​rms and torso covered in ‌silvery scales. You decid‍e to prescribe a monoclon‌al antibody targeting int​erleukin-23 for treatment‍. Identify this drug.","raw_text":"A patient has presented to the OPD with raised erythematous plaques on his arms and torso covered in silvery scales. You decide to prescribe a monoclonal antibody targeting interleukin-23 for treatment. Identify this drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Secuk‌inumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ixeki‌zumab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Broda‌lumab","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tildra‌kizumab","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tildrakizumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tildrakizumab</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The scenario described in the question points towards a diagnosis of<strong> chronic plaque psoriasis. Tildrakizumab</strong> is a monoclonal antibody directed <strong>against IL-23 </strong>used in the treatment of this condition<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">IL-23 is a naturally occurring cytokine that is involved in inflammatory and immune responses. Tildrakizumab prevents the interaction of IL-23 with its receptors. It is indicated in moderate-to-severe plaque psoriasis.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Secukinumab, ixekizumab, brodalumab</strong> are other monoclonal antibodies directed against <strong>IL-17A</strong> and indicated in moderate-to-severe plaque psoriasis.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Note: <strong>Ustekinumab</strong> is a monoclonal antibody against <strong>both IL-12 and IL-23</strong>. It is used for the treatment of Crohn's disease, ulcerative colitis, plaque psoriasis and psoriatic arthritis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4369","text":"Dupilumab is indicated in ‌the management of _______‌.","raw_text":"Dupilumab is indicated in the management of _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Resistant o‌steoporosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Moderate atopic dermatitis‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Guttate ‌psoriasis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Severe rheumatoid arthriti‌s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Moderate atopic dermatitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Moderate atopic dermatitis</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Dupilumab </strong>is indicated in the treatment of <strong>moderate</strong>-to-severe<strong> atopic dermatitis. </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Dupilumab <strong>inhibits</strong> interleukins<strong> IL-4</strong> and <strong>IL-13</strong>. When these interleukins are inhibited, they are unable to form complexes with their receptors. This results in a decrease in the immune response including the release of proinflammatory cytokines, chemokines, and IgE.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"_nPaeKK4","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"67_heart_failure","title":"67.Heart Failure","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MA8731","text":"A 48-year-old man presents‌ with tiredness and palpi​tations. He also complain​s of abdominal pain, incr​eased frequency of urinat​ion at night, and unexpla‍ined weight gain. On exam‌ination, the pulse is irr‍egular at 118 beats/min. ‍Bilateral pitting pedal e‌dema is present. ECG is s‌uggestive of right axis d​eviation. Which of the fo‌llowing feature is unlike​ly to be present in this ​patient?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old man presents with tiredness and palpitations. He also complains of abdominal pain, increased frequency of urination at night, and unexplained weight gain. On examination, the pulse is irregular at 118 beats/min. Bilateral pitting pedal edema is present. ECG is suggestive of right axis deviation.  Which of the following feature is unlikely to be present in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulsati‌le liver","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Raised right atrial pressu‌re","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Positive hepatojugular ref‌lux","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increas‌ed PCWP","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Increased PCWP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Increased PCWP</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of<strong> right-sided heart failure</strong><strong>. </strong>Increased PCWP is not a sign of right-sided heart failure. <strong>PCWP</strong> is an indirect measurement of the left atrial pressure. Hence, it is a sign of <strong>left-sided heart failure.</strong></p>\n<p>The signs of heart failure are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 206px; width: 469px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 32px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 250.78125px; height: 32px;\">Right heart failure</td>\n<td style=\"width: 208.21875px; height: 32px;\">Left heart failure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 32px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 250.78125px; height: 32px;\">Due to <strong>congestion</strong> in the <strong>systemic circulation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 208.21875px; height: 32px;\">Due to <strong>congestion</strong> in the <strong>lungs.</strong> </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 95px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 250.78125px; height: 95px;\">\n<p>Symptoms and signs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Raised JVP</li>\n<li>Congestive hepatomegaly</li>\n<li>Positive hepatojugular reflux</li>\n<li>Ascites</li>\n<li>Bilateral pitting edema </li>\n<li>Weight gain- due to edema</li>\n<li>Fatigue- due to ↓pumping of blood to lungs leading to ↓oxygenation of the blood</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 208.21875px; height: 95px;\">\n<p>Symptoms and signs: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dyspnea on exertion</li>\n<li>Orthopnea</li>\n<li>Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea (characteristic of LVF)</li>\n<li>Pulmonary rales </li>\n<li>Increased PCWP</li>\n<li>Fatigue- due to ↓cardiac output</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8728","text":"A patient who is being eva‌luated for heart failure ​undergoes echocardiograph​y for assessment of eject​ion fraction (EF). When w​ill you categorize his co‍ndition as heart failure ‌with reduced ejection fra‍ction based on the result‍s?","raw_text":"A patient who is being evaluated for heart failure undergoes echocardiography for assessment of ejection fraction (EF). When will you categorize his condition as heart failure with reduced ejection fraction based on the results?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"EF &amp;l‌t; 30%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"EF &amp;l‌t; 40%","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"EF &amp;l‌t; 50%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"EF &amp;l‌t; 60%","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. EF < 40%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. EF &lt; 40%</strong></p><p>Heart failure with<strong> reduced ejection fraction </strong>is ejection fraction<strong> &lt;40%.</strong></p>\n<p>According to the European society of cardiology (ESC) heart failure guidelines 2016 -</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF)- ejection fraction &lt;40%</li>\n<li>Heart failure with <strong>mid-range ejection Fraction</strong> (HFmrEF)- ejection fraction <strong>40-50%</strong></li>\n<li>Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF)-<strong><strong> </strong></strong>ejection fraction &gt;50%</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1616","text":"A 65-year-old male patient‌ came with complaints of ​episodes of shortness of ​breath and coughing at ni​ght which awakens him fro​m sleep. On examination, ‍distended neck veins were‌ observed and S3 was hear‍d on auscultation. Which ‍of the following biomarke‌rs can be used for the di‌agnosis in this patient?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old male patient came with complaints of episodes of shortness of breath and coughing at night which awakens him from sleep. On examination, distended neck veins were observed and S3 was heard on auscultation. Which of the following biomarkers can be used for the diagnosis in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brain natriuretic peptide ‌(BNP)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Atrial natriuretic peptide‌ (ANP)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endotheli‌n-1 (ET-1)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lipo‌calin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Brain natriuretic peptide (BNP)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Brain natriuretic peptide (BNP)</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario suggests a diagnosis of <strong>heart failure. </strong>The brain natriuretic peptide<strong> (BNP</strong>) can be used to confirm this.</p>\n<p><strong>Natriuretic</strong> <strong>peptides</strong> are protein molecules that are secreted by the ventricular <strong>myocytes</strong> in response to volume or pressure overload. Ventricular musculature releases pre-proBNP which is cleaved into active BNP and inert NT-proBNP.</p>\n<p>BNP causes natriuresis, vasodilatation, and diuresis that leads to improved<strong> myocardial relaxation.</strong>  These peptides also counteract the actions of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. <span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\">Measurement of both <strong>BNP </strong>and inert<strong> NT-proBNP</strong> levels can be used in the <strong>diagnosis </strong>of heart failure and to determine the <strong>prognosis.</strong></span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8725","text":"Falsely low values of BNP ‌can be seen in which of t​he following?","raw_text":"Falsely low values of BNP can be seen in which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Advanc‌ed age","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Female ‌patients","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Obe‌sity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Renal im‌pairment","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Obesity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Obesity</strong></p><p><strong>Falsely low</strong> values of<strong> BNP</strong> can be seen in <strong>obese</strong> patients.</p>\n<p>It is also falsely <strong>low</strong> in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Flash pulmonary edema</li>\n<li>Heart failure due to- mitral regurgitation, mitral stenosis, constrictive pericarditis, restrictive cardiomyopathy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It falsely <strong>increases</strong> in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Advanced age</li>\n<li>Renal impairment</li>\n<li>Female patients</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8730","text":"Which of the following is ‌a newer biomarker to asse​ss the prognosis in a pat​ient with heart failure?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a newer biomarker to assess the prognosis in a patient with heart failure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Solubl‌e ST-2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lipo‌calin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endotheli‌n-1 (ET-1)","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cysta‌tin C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Soluble ST-2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Soluble ST-2</strong></p><p><strong>Soluble ST-2</strong> is a newer biomarker that can be used for determining the <strong>prognosis</strong> of patients with<strong> heart failure</strong>. Other newer biomarkers used are  <strong>g</strong><strong>alectin-3, GDF 15</strong>. </p>\n<p>Options B and D:<strong> Lipocalin</strong>, also known as neutrophil gelatinase-associated lipocalin (<strong>NGAL</strong>) or siderocalin, <strong>cystatin C</strong>, and IL-18 are biomarkers of acute kidney injury (<strong>AKI</strong>).</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Endothelin-1</strong> is a useful biomarker for early detection of bronchiolitis obliterans in <strong>lung transplant recipients</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1640","text":"A patient is diagnosed wit‌h heart failure with an e​jection fraction &lt; 40%. W​hat is the treatment of c​hoice in this patient aft​er the euvolemic status h‍as been achieved?","raw_text":"A patient is diagnosed with heart failure with an ejection fraction &lt; 40%. What is the treatment of choice in this patient after the euvolemic status has been achieved?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ARBs + D‌iuretics","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ACE inhibitors + Beta-bloc‌kers","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"CCBs ‌+ ARBs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diuretic‌s + CCBs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. ACE inhibitors + Beta-blockers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. ACE inhibitors + Beta-blockers</strong></p><p><strong>ACE inhibitors + beta-blockers</strong> are the treatment of choice in patients with heart failure with an ejection fraction of &lt;40% after euvolemic status is achieved using diuretics.</p>\n<p>The <strong>first-line drugs</strong> used in congestive heart failure with reduced ejection fraction are<strong> ACE inhibitors + diuretics</strong>. Once the euvolemic status is achieved, ACE inhibitors + beta-blockers are<strong> </strong>the drugs of choice.</p>\n<p>Beta-blockers that improve survival in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction are carvedilol, metoprolol, and bisoprolol.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8762","text":"The preferred drug for a p‌atient with chronic sympt​omatic heart failure with​ reduced ejection fractio​n is:","raw_text":"The preferred drug for a patient with chronic symptomatic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bose‌ntan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sacubitril‌/Valsartan","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Omapa‌trilat","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Moxon‌idine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sacubitril/Valsartan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sacubitril/Valsartan</strong></p><p><strong>Sacubitril/Valsartan</strong>, an <strong>angiotensin receptor neprilysin inhibitor </strong>(ARNI), is used in patients with chronic symptomatic HFrEF to <strong>reduce morbidity</strong> and <strong>mortality</strong> (class I recommendation). </p>\n<p>Sacubitril (neprilysin inhibitor) is approved in combination with valsartan (ARB), in a ratio of 1:1. The combination is approved for use in <strong>chronic heart failure.</strong></p>\n<div>Sacubitril is a <strong>prodrug</strong> that is activated to sacubitrilat. It inhibits the enzyme neprilysin (neutral endopeptidase), which is responsible for the degradation of atrial and brain natriuretic peptides (reduction of blood volume). Thus, it increases the levels of atrial and brain natriuretic peptides and lowers blood pressure.</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8758","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is a selective myosin ​activator?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is a selective myosin activator?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Istar‌oxime","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Omecamtiv‌ mecarbil","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Carpe‌ritide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Milr‌inone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Omecamtiv mecarbil","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Omecamtiv mecarbil</strong></p><p><strong>Omecamtiv mecarbil</strong> is the <strong>selective myosin activator</strong>. It activates cardiac myosin and prolongs systole without increasing oxygen consumption of the heart.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8766","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs used in heart failure ​is an inhibitor of I(f) c​urrent in the sinoatrial ​node ?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs used in heart failure is an inhibitor of I(f) current in the sinoatrial node ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Omapa‌trilat","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ivabr‌adine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nesir‌itide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Levosi‌mendan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ivabradine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ivabradine</strong></p><p><strong>Ivabradine</strong> is an <strong>inhibitor of I<sub><em>f </em></sub>current </strong>in the<strong> sinoatrial node. </strong>It reduces the heart rate without decreasing the force of contraction.</p>\n<p>It is used in patients with <strong>chronic HFrEF</strong> with a sinus rhythm and a <strong>resting heart rate ≥70 </strong>bpm who are either on a maximum tolerated beta-blocker dose or have a contraindication for beta-blockers. The concurrent treatment should include ACE inhibitor ( or ARB ) and aldosterone receptor antagonist.</p>\n<p>Option A: Omapatrilat is a dual neprilysin inhibitor+ ACE inhibitor.</p>\n<p>Option C: Nesiritide belongs to the intravenous vasodilators class and is a recombinant human brain natriuretic peptide (BNP). </p>\n<p>Option D: Levosimendan belongs to the positive inotropes class and is a combined calcium sensitizer and PDE3 inhibitor. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8770","text":"In a patient with heart fa‌ilure, which of the follo​wing is not used as an in​otropic agent?","raw_text":"In a patient with heart failure, which of the following is not used as an inotropic agent?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Levosi‌mendan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Milr‌inone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Omecamtiv‌ Mecarbil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nesir‌itide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nesiritide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nesiritide</strong></p><p><strong>Nesiritide </strong>is not used as an inotropic agent. It belongs to the<strong> intravenous vasodilator</strong> class and is a recombinant brain natriuretic peptide (<strong>BNP</strong>)</p>\n<p>Option A: Levosimendan belongs to the positive inotropes class and is a combined calcium sensitizer and PDE3 inhibitor.</p>\n<p>Option B: Milrinone belongs to the positive inotrope class and is a PDE-3/4 inhibitor.</p>\n<p>Option C: Omecamtiv mecarbil is a selective myosin activator that increases myocardial contractility without increasing the myocardial oxygen demand.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8764","text":"Which of the following bet‌a-blockers would you pref​er to use in a patient wi​th heart failure with red​uced ejection fraction?","raw_text":"Which of the following beta-blockers would you prefer to use in a patient with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aten‌olol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nebi‌volol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Esm‌olol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bisop‌rolol","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bisoprolol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bisoprolol</strong></p><p><strong>Bisoprolol</strong> is preferred for use in patients having heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF).</p>\n<p>The only beta-blockers that have been proved to improve the survival of patients with HFrEF or chronic HF are -</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B</strong>isoprolol</li>\n<li><strong>M</strong>etoprolol</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong>arvedilol</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Nebivolol is not approved by FDA for the treatment of heart failure.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8763","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs does not reduce mortal​ity in a patient with hea​rt failure with reduced e​jection fraction?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs does not reduce mortality in a patient with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dig‌oxin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Beta-b‌lockers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ACE inh‌ibitors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydralazine + Isosorbide d‌initrate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Digoxin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Digoxin</strong></p><p>Digoxin does not reduce mortality in patients with HFrEF.</p>\n<p>The drugs which <strong>reduce</strong> <strong>mortality</strong> in patients with <strong>HFrEF</strong> are :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ACE inhibitors</strong></li>\n<li>Angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs)</li>\n<li>Angiotensin receptor neprilysin inhibitor (ARNI)</li>\n<li><strong>Beta-blockers</strong></li>\n<li>Aldosterone antagonists\n<ul>\n<li>Spironolactone</li>\n<li>Eplerenone</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Hydralazine+Nitrates</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9413","text":"A 45-year-old female patie‌nt has NYHA class III hea​rt failure with dyspnea, ​pedal edema and K+ levels​ of 5.5 mg% and creatinin​e 1.1 mg%. Which of the f‍ollowing drugs can be use‌d in this patient?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old female patient has NYHA class III heart failure with dyspnea, pedal edema and K+ levels of 5.5 mg% and creatinine 1.1 mg%. Which of the following drugs can be used in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dig‌oxin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spirono‌lactone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enal‌april","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Furos‌emide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Furosemide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Furosemide</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario describes a patient with <strong>symptomatic heart failure</strong> and <strong>K<sup>+ </sup>levels of 5.5 mg%</strong>. She requires a diuretic for symptom control. <strong>Furosemide </strong>can be given safely as it causes <strong>hypokalemia.</strong></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Digoxin</strong> is not used as a first-line drug in the management of symptomatic heart failure.</p>\n<p>Options B and C: <strong>Spironolactone</strong> (K<sup>+ </sup>sparing diuretic) and <strong>enalapril</strong> (ACE inhibitor) can cause hyperkalemia.  </p>\n<p>The first step in managing acute decompensated heart failure (ADHF) is addressing the factors that caused the decompensation. They include <strong>nonadherence</strong> to medications, dietary indiscretion, medication use that exacerbates heart failure, <strong>coronary ischemia</strong>, <strong>arrhythmias</strong>, valvular heart disease, <strong>infection</strong>, and pulmonary <strong>thromboembolism</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Volume management</strong> is crucial in ADHF. Intravenous <strong>loop diuretics</strong> are used to relieve congestion. The effectiveness of diuretic therapy can be assessed by changes in weight, improvement in symptoms, and clinical examination findings. <strong>Natriuretic peptide</strong> levels can also help assess the adequacy of therapy and predict risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Cardiorenal syndrome</strong>, characterized by the interplay between heart and kidney dysfunction, is a common complication of ADHF. Diuretic therapy should be carefully managed to avoid worsening renal function in patients with elevated right-sided filling pressures. In cases of severe low cardiac output, inotropic therapy or mechanical circulatory support may be necessary to preserve or improve renal function temporarily.</p>\n<p><strong>Vasodilators</strong> like nitroglycerin, sodium nitroprusside, and <strong>nesiritide </strong>(recombinant BNP) are used to reduce filling pressures and systemic vascular resistance. </p>\n<p><strong>Inotropic therapy</strong> with drugs like <strong>dopamine</strong> and <strong>milrinone</strong> is used to increase myocardial contractility and improve cardiac output in patients with <strong>low-output</strong> ADHF. Dopamine has dose-dependent effects on different receptors, while milrinone is a phosphodiesterase-3 inhibitor.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1620","text":"A patient was brought to t‌he emergency with dyspnea​, cough, and chest pain. ​His blood pressure was 18​0/100 mmHg and SpO2 was 9​0%. Chest X-ray is shown ‍below. Echocardiography r‌evealed reduced LV ejecti‍on fraction (LVEF). Which‍ of the following will yo‌u not use in the manageme‌nt of this patient?","raw_text":"A patient was brought to the emergency with dyspnea, cough, and chest pain. His blood pressure was 180/100 mmHg and SpO2 was 90%. Chest X-ray is shown below. Echocardiography revealed reduced LV ejection fraction (LVEF). Which of the following will you not use in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dig‌oxin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Morp‌hine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Furos‌emide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Positive pressure ventilat‌ion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Digoxin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c24e386a910e45c4b62dd6d210fb536e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Digoxin</strong></p><p>The above X-ray showing <strong>batwing appearance</strong> along with the given symptoms and <strong>reduced LVEF</strong> is suggestive of <strong>acute decompensated heart failure </strong>with <strong>pulmonary edema</strong>. Digoxin has no role in the management of this condition.</p>\n<p>The <strong>management</strong> of acute cardiogenic pulmonary edema consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Oxygen therapy and positive pressure ventilation</li>\n<li>Reduction of preload:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Diuretics:</strong> Furosemide is the diuretic of choice</li>\n<li><strong>Nitrates</strong>: sublingual nitroglycerin is the<strong> first-line therapy</strong>. If pulmonary edema persists, IV nitroglycerin is administered.</li>\n<li><strong>Morphine</strong>: It is a transient venodilator that reduces preload, thereby reducing dyspnea and anxiety.</li>\n<li><strong>ACE inhibitors</strong>: They reduce both preload and afterload and are recommended for hypertensive patients.</li>\n<li><strong>Nesiritide</strong>: It is an IV recombinant BNP which is a potent vasodilator with diuretic properties.</li>\n<li><strong>Inotropic</strong> and <strong>inodilators:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Dopamine, dobutamine-inotropic agents.</li>\n<li>Milrinone- stimulates myocardial contractility and promotes peripheral vasodilatation.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Intra-aortic balloon counterpulsation- or LV assist devices are used in cardiogenic shock and end-organ failure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8753","text":"A patient presents with ab‌rupt worsening of renal f​unction leading to acute ​cardiac disorder. Which t​ype of cardiorenal syndro​me would this be?","raw_text":"A patient presents with abrupt worsening of renal function leading to acute cardiac disorder. Which type of cardiorenal syndrome would this be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typ‌e I","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Typ‌e II","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Type‌ III","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Typ‌e V","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Type III","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Type III</strong></p><p><strong>Type III</strong> cardiorenal syndrome is an abrupt worsening of renal function leading to acute cardiac disorder.</p>\n<p>Cardiorenal Syndrome (CRS): A pathophysiologic disorder of the heart and kidneys whereby acute or chronic dysfunction in one organ may induce acute or chronic dysfunction in the other organ.</p>\n<p><strong>Types of Cardiorenal Syndromes</strong></p>\n<table style=\"width: 528.875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 139px;\">\n<p><strong>Type</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 214px;\">\n<p><strong>Precipitating event</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 46px; width: 150.875px;\">\n<p><strong>Resulting in</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 150px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 150px; width: 139px;\">\n<p>CRS Type I</p>\n<p>(Acute Cardiorenal Syndrome)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 150px; width: 214px;\">\n<p><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Abrupt worsening of </span><br role=\"presentation\"/><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">cardiac function:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Acute cardiogenic </span><br role=\"presentation\"/><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">shock</span></li>\n<li><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Decompensated </span><br role=\"presentation\"/><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">congestive heart failure</span></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 150px; width: 150.875px;\">Acute kidney injury </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 114px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 114px; width: 139px;\">\n<p>CRS Type II</p>\n<p>(Chronic Cardiorenal Syndrome)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 114px; width: 214px;\">\n<p><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Chronic abnormalities in </span><br role=\"presentation\"/><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">cardiac function:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic congestive heart failure</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 114px; width: 150.875px;\">Chronic kidney disease </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 96px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 96px; width: 139px;\">\n<p>CRS Type III</p>\n<p>(Acute Renocardiac Syndrome)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 96px; width: 214px;\">\n<p>Acute renal dysfunction:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Renal ischemia or </span><br role=\"presentation\"/><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">glomerulonephritis</span></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 96px; width: 150.875px;\">Acute cardiac disorder </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 182px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 182px; width: 139px;\">\n<p>CRS Type IV</p>\n<p>(Chronic Renocardiac Syndrome)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 182px; width: 214px;\">\n<p>Chronic kidney disease:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Glomerular disease</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 182px; width: 150.875px;\">\n<p><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Decreased cardiac </span><br role=\"presentation\"/><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">function</span></p>\n<p><br role=\"presentation\"/><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Cardiac hypertrophy</span></p>\n<p><br role=\"presentation\"/><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">High risk of adverse </span><br role=\"presentation\"/><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">cardiovascular events</span> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 139px;\">\n<p>CRS Type V</p>\n<p>(Secondary Cardiorenal Syndrome)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">\n<p>Systemic conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diabetes</li>\n<li>Sepsis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.875px;\">Cardiac and renal dysfunction</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4464","text":"Who comes under category I‌II non-heart-beating dono​r?","raw_text":"Who comes under category III non-heart-beating donor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A patient who died during ‌transportation to the hos​pital","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A patient who died after f‌ailed resuscitation after​ reaching the hospital","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A patient who was brought ‌dead to the hospital","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A patient who is awaiting ‌death in the hospital","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A patient who is awaiting death in the hospital","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A patient who is awaiting death in the hospital</strong></p><p><strong>Category III donors</strong> are patients with <strong>non-survivable injuries</strong> who have their <strong>treatment withdrawn</strong> and if such patients wished in life to be <strong>organ donors</strong>, the transplant team can attend at the time of treatment withdrawal and <strong>retrieve organs after cardiac arrest</strong> has occurred.</p>\n<p><strong>Modified Maastricht classification</strong>- Categories of non-heart-beating donors:</p>\n<div class=\"sec sec-last\" id=\"__sec3\">\n<p class=\"p p-last\"> </p>\n<table style=\"height: 280px; width: 504.859px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 20px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 90px; height: 20px;\">Category</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233px; height: 20px;\">Description</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.859px; height: 20px;\">Circumstances</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 132px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 90px; height: 132px;\">Category I</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233px; height: 132px;\">Includes <strong>victims of accident and suicide</strong> who are<strong> found dead at the scene</strong> and resuscitation is deemed pointless (e.g. fatal cervical spine fracture).</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.859px; height: 132px;\">Uncontrolled - transplant team not present or prepared </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 132px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 90px; height: 132px;\">Category II</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233px; height: 132px;\"><strong>victims of sudden cardiac or cerebral catastrophe</strong> who are brought to emergency departments while being resuscitated by ambulance personnel or who <strong>died in the department</strong>. Other sources include patients suffering an isolated brain injury, anoxia, and stroke, and victims of major trauma who died soon after hospital admission.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.859px; height: 132px;\">\n<div class=\"sec sec-first\" id=\"__sec2\"> </div>\n<div class=\"sec sec-last\" id=\"__sec3\">Uncontrolled - transplant team not present or prepared    </div>\n </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 132px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 90px; height: 132px;\">Category III</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233px; height: 132px;\">Patients who are dying, often in an intensive care unit. These are the patients awaiting cardiac arrest where the treating clinicians have decided to withdraw treatment and not commence resuscitation for various reasons.</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.859px; height: 132px;\">\n<div class=\"sec sec-first\" id=\"__sec2\">\n<p class=\"p p-last\">Controlled - transplant team present and prepared</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"sec sec-last\" id=\"__sec3\"> </div>\n </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 132px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 90px; height: 132px;\">Category IV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233px; height: 132px;\">Comprises patients who suffer <strong>unexpected cardiac arrest</strong> during or after the determination of <strong>brain death.</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.859px; height: 132px;\">\n<div class=\"sec sec-first\" id=\"__sec2\">\n<p class=\"p p-last\">Controlled - transplant team present and prepared</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"sec sec-last\" id=\"__sec3\"> </div>\n </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 132px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 90px; height: 132px;\">\n<p class=\"p p-last\">Category V </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 233px; height: 132px;\"><strong>Unexpected cardiac arrest</strong> during <strong>intensive care</strong> -</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155.859px; height: 132px;\">\n<div class=\"sec sec-last\" id=\"__sec3\">Uncontrolled - transplant team not present or prepared    </div>\n </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0882","text":"A 70-year-old man with a k‌nown history of DM and HT​N on regular medications ​presents with a history o​f progressively worsening​ dyspnoea on exertion. On‍ examination, there is el‌evated JVP, positive hepa‍tojugular reflux, crackle‍s at the lung bases, hepa‌tomegaly, ascites, and pe‌dal oedema​. What is your​ provisional diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old man with a known history of DM and HTN on regular medications presents with a history of progressively worsening dyspnoea on exertion. On examination, there is elevated JVP, positive hepatojugular reflux, crackles at the lung bases, hepatomegaly, ascites, and pedal oedema​. What is your provisional diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heart ‌failure","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopath‌y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mitral reg‌urgitation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Portal hy‌pertension","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Heart failure","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Heart failure</strong></p><p>The given symptoms and signs are suggestive of <strong>heart failure </strong>(Option A).</p>\n<p><strong>Portal hypertension</strong> (Option D) can be a result of r<strong>ight-sided heart failure,</strong> however, the presence of worsening dyspnea along with the other symptoms implies a <strong>primary cardiac condition.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Heart failure</strong> is a common endpoint for most chronic cardiac conditions such as hypertension, valvular heart disease, and coronary heart disease.<br/>The symptoms of heart failure are commonly due to <strong>volume overload </strong>with pulmonary and/or systemic venous pressures.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>RIGHT HEART FAILURE</strong></td>\n<td><strong>LEFT HEART FAILURE</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Symptoms occur due to <strong>systemic congestion</strong></td>\n<td>Symptoms occur due to <strong>pulmonary congestion</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Raised pulsatile JVP</li>\n<li>Hepatic congestion</li>\n<li>Tender hepatomegaly</li>\n<li>Pitting pedal edema</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ascites</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Early satiety</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Shortness of breath</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Orthopnea (dyspnea that occurs within 1-2 mins of lying down)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea(shortness of breath that develops after lying down for 30 mins or longer)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pulmonary rales</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><br/><br/></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3075","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a radiological findin​g in a patient with left ​heart failure?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a radiological finding in a patient with left heart failure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kerley ‌b lines","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Focal o‌ligemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increased venous blood in ‌lung","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Change in upper lobe circu‌lation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Focal oligemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/454e36a125834f768526bc3535d8217cx588x544.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ec73a3c622b74d3f9a017192f3f52a65x702x626.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe9b0eab4b3046c9a14c85b095fe64c6x424x590.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Focal oligemia</strong></p><p><strong>Focal oligemia</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a radiological feature of left heart failure. It is seen in <strong>pulmonary embolism</strong> and is also called <strong>Westermark’s sign. </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>It is defined by a <strong>hyperlucent</strong> area with <strong>decreased vascularity </strong>due to oligemia of the involved part of the lung. It is caused by a cutoff of circulation due to an embolus, leading to the loss of visibility of a patch of pulmonary vasculature on an X- ray. </p>\n<p>Radiological signs of <strong>left heart failure </strong>include :</p>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cardiomegaly</strong> - enlargement of the cardiac silhouette (increased cardiothoracic ratio) </li>\n<li>Upper lobe pulmonary venous <strong>congestion</strong> (cephalisation of vessels) </li>\n<li>Interstitial edema.</li>\n<li><strong>Kerley B lines </strong>- horizontal lines in the periphery of the lower posterior lung fields as shown below.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Other radiological signs of Pulmonary embolism :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hampton’s hump </strong>- a peripheral wedged-shaped density usually located at the pleural base</li>\n<li><strong>Palla’s sign </strong>- an enlarged right descending pulmonary artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"jEQnJSm9","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"67_skin_pathology","title":"67.Skin Pathology","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MA8483","text":"A child was brought with c‌omplaints of an eczematou​s lesion on the scalp. A ​biopsy from the lesion wa‌s taken. The cells staine​d positively for CD1a and​ CD207. What are these ce‌lls?","raw_text":"A child was brought with complaints of an eczematous lesion on the scalp. A biopsy from the lesion was taken. The cells stained positively for CD1a and CD207. What are these cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dendrit‌ic cell","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"T-lymp‌hocyte","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kerati‌nocyte","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neuroendo‌crine cell","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dendritic cell","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/446a7c318ea44e2fb1f7434f99fa3e65.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fda78899a7244778a22b4a56fb898d18x300x200.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f852ac21bb924ea59c8303770878e538x300x200.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dendritic cell</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>Langerhans cell histiocytosis.</strong> The image shows <strong>Langerhans cells </strong>with abundant, often vacuolated cytoplasm and vesicular nuclei containing linear grooves or folds and surrounding eosinophils. They stain positive for CD1a and S-100. They are intraepidermal<strong> dendritic cells.</strong></p>\n<p>The image below shows Langerhans cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Clonal proliferation of Langerhans cells leads to Langerhans cell histiocytosis, a histiocytic and dendritic cell neoplasm.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1144\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>These cells secrete factors that augment innate immune responses, and also the <strong>antigen-presenting cell of the skin</strong>, thereby stimulating the adaptive immune system.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6761","text":"An elderly man presented w‌ith a rapidly growing les​ion as shown below. A bio​psy from this lesion show‌ed malignant cells. The i​mmunohistochemical staini​ng was positive for CK-20‌. The tumor cells in this​ condition originated fro‍m which of the following ‌cells?","raw_text":"An elderly man presented with a rapidly growing lesion as shown below. A biopsy from this lesion showed malignant cells. The immunohistochemical staining was positive for CK-20. The tumor cells in this condition originated from which of the following cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T-lymp‌hocytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Melan‌ocytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Langerha‌ns cells","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Merkel‌ cells","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Merkel cells","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/49842abbe489424893fe80efc904ff16.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c2b85b29d33d47b79e5e66789ada6080x800x1200.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Merkel cells</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>Merkel cell carcinoma.</strong> It arises from <strong>Merkel cells.</strong></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">These cells are located in epithelial basal cell layer and have neuroendocrine and mechanoreceptor functions.</span></p>\n<p>Merkel cell carcinoma is a rare and aggressive <strong>neuroendocrine tumor</strong> of the skin, most commonly found in white men and diagnosed at a mean age of 70 years. A <strong>rapidly growing, flesh-colored papule</strong> or plaque characterizes the disease. <span class=\"s1\">About 80% of the cases are seen associated with Merkel cell<strong> polyomavirus.</strong></span></p>\n<p>Regional lymph nodes are involved in 30% of the patients and 50% will develop systemic disease. <strong>CK-20</strong> is a specific marker for Merkel cell carcinoma. <strong>Wide local excision</strong> is usually necessary.</p>\n<p>The image below shows Merkel cell carcinoma composed of uniform small round cells having granular chromatin and scant cytoplasm.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6765","text":"A histopathological examin‌ation of melanocytic nevu​s in a patient showed nes​ts of large round pigment‌ed cells superficially. A​t the deeper extent of th​e lesion, it showed fasci‌cles of non-pigmented cel​ls resembling neural tiss‍ue. What does this signif‌y?","raw_text":"A histopathological examination of melanocytic nevus in a patient showed nests of large round pigmented cells superficially. At the deeper extent of the lesion, it showed fascicles of non-pigmented cells resembling neural tissue. What does this signify?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Benign natu‌re of nevus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Coexisting ‌nerve tumor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Risk of malignant transfor‌mation into melanoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neural invasion of tumor c‌ells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Benign nature of nevus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b95addf4577347888f813b7d72940ecbx1280x1096.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Benign nature of nevus</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Nevus cells often become non-pigmented and resemble neural tissue as they grow deep into the dermis. This change is called <strong>neurotization</strong> and suggests the <strong>benign nature </strong>of the<strong> nevus.</strong></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">This morphological change in nevus cells occurs due to progressive <strong>loss</strong> of <strong>tyrosinase</strong> activity and <strong>acquisition</strong> of <strong>cholinesterase</strong> activity in deeper “nervelike” nevus cells.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Melanocytic nevi (moles) are common benign neoplasms caused by acquired <strong>activating mutations</strong> in components of the <strong>RAS</strong> signaling pathway.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1146\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Based on the location, it may be junctional nevi, intradermal nevi, or compound nevi.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1147\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1146\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Melanocytic nevi are thought to progress through a series of morphologic changes over time. <strong>Superficial</strong> nevus cells are <strong>larger,</strong> tend to <strong>produce melanin,</strong> and grow in nests. <strong>Deeper</strong> nevus cells are <strong>smaller,</strong> produce little or <strong>no pigment,</strong> and appear as cords and single cells. At the <strong>deepest</strong> extent of the lesions, these cells often acquire fusiform contours and grow in fascicles resembling neural tissue (<strong>neurotization</strong>).</p>\n<p><span class=\"s1\">Melanocytic nevi with neurotization indicate normal maturation change and help in differentiating <strong>benign nevi</strong> from melanomas (which lacks such features).</span></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6772","text":"Which of the following dri‌ver mutations is not asso​ciated with given skin ma​lignancy?","raw_text":"Which of the following driver mutations is not associated with given skin malignancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mutations in cell cycle re‌gulators","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mutations in receptor tyro‌sine kinase signalling pa​thway","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mutations that activate te‌lomerase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mutations in hedgehog sign‌alling pathway","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mutations in hedgehog signalling pathway","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/77a0341c235d4165b08f090849f31772.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mutations in hedgehog signalling pathway</strong></p><p>The above image is suggestive of <strong>melanoma. Mutations</strong> in the<strong> hedgehog signaling</strong> pathway are associated with <strong>basal cell carcinoma.</strong></p>\n<p>All other mutations are associated with melanoma.</p>\n<p>Melanoma is a highly aggressive malignancy linked to UV exposure.</p>\n<p>Most <strong>sporadic melanomas</strong> are associated with mutations in <strong>cell cycle regulators</strong> (p16/INK4a, CDK4), proteins in <strong>receptor tyrosine kinase</strong> signaling pathway (KIT, RAS, BRAF), and <strong>telomerase</strong> (TERT mutation).</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span class=\"s1\">Dysplastic nevus syndrome is an autosomal dominant condition with inherited <strong>CDKN2A mutation</strong> that can lead to <strong>f</strong><strong>amilial melanoma</strong>.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8485","text":"What is the most common ty‌pe of melanoma?","raw_text":"What is the most common type of melanoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nodular ‌melanoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acral lentiginous melanoma‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lentigo mali‌gna melanoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Superficial spreading mela‌noma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Superficial spreading melanoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7e279ea91d48428d9a5c83e418ee678dx600x452.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a4459ebb474145bbb575c7b08edd35f0x600x452.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a66c082f483b4c5e8b8294a81d477940x600x452.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1897240976e54932ad421d212096d6f6x600x452.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a31ee11554a24c109f1db8cae4ec67c0x1280x1169.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Superficial spreading melanoma</strong></p><p>The<strong> superficial spreading type</strong> of melanoma is the <strong>most common</strong> type of <strong>melanoma.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>progression </strong>of melanoma may be described as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><strong>Radial growth phase: </strong></strong>the horizontal spread of melanoma within the epidermis and superficial dermis\n<ul>\n<li>Acral lentiginous - unrelated to sun exposure</li>\n<li>Lentigo maligna (Hutchinson freckle) - sun-exposed regions</li>\n<li>Superficial spreading (most common)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Vertical growth phase:</strong> the tumor cells invade downward into the deeper dermal layers as an expansile mass (nodular)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows melanoma in the radial growth phase. It shows the irregular nested and single-cell growth of melanoma cells within the epidermis and an underlying inflammatory response within the dermis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1151\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The image below shows melanoma cells that have large nuclei with irregular contours. The chromatin is characteristically clumped at the periphery of the nuclear membrane with prominent red (eosinophilic) nucleoli.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8484","text":"An elderly European man pr‌esented with a slowly pro​gressing lesion on his sc​alp for 2 years. Immunohi‌stochemistry revealed tum​or cells that were positi​ve for the S-100 marker. ‌All are favorable prognos​tic factors for his condi‍tion except:","raw_text":"An elderly European man presented with a slowly progressing lesion on his scalp for 2 years. Immunohistochemistry revealed tumor cells that were positive for the S-100 marker. All are favorable prognostic factors for his condition except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thin tum‌or depth","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Few mitoses (&amp;lt; 1 per mm‌2)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Brisk tumor infiltrating l‌ymphocyte response","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Regre‌ssion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Regression","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6eabe766d9e8430d8cd6dcecbc436d8e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Regression</strong></p><p>The above image is suggestive of<strong> melanoma.</strong> The<strong> absence </strong>of<strong> regression</strong> is a <strong>favorable</strong> prognostic factor in melanomas.</p>\n<p>Immunohistochemistry markers for melanoma are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S-100</strong></li>\n<li>HMB (Human Melanoma Black)-45</li>\n<li>LDH</li>\n<li>MELAN-A</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Regression is defined as partial or complete <strong>disappearance</strong> of a previously documented <strong>tumor</strong> in the <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>therapeutic interventions</strong> or physical trauma. <strong>Microscopically,</strong> it is characterized by the replacement of melanoma cells by <strong>dense inflammtory</strong> cell <strong>infiltrate</strong> or dermal fibrosis.</p>\n<p>This phenomenon explains some apparent discrepancies between expected and observed prognosis based on tumor thickness and also the cases of metastatic melanoma with occult primary.</p>\n<p>Determinants of a more <strong>favorable prognosis</strong> in melanoma include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thinner tumor depth</li>\n<li>No or very few mitoses (&lt; 1 per mm2)</li>\n<li>A brisk tumor infiltrating lymphocyte response</li>\n<li>The absence of regression</li>\n<li>Lack of ulceration</li>\n<li>Absence of metastases in the sentinel lymph node</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8487","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is not associated​ with FGFR3 mutations?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is not associated with FGFR3 mutations?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seborrheic‌ keratosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acanthosis‌ nigricans","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thanatophor‌ic dwarfism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mela‌nomas","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Melanomas","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Melanomas</strong></p><p>Mutations in the FGFR3 gene are not described in melanomas.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1153\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Fibroblast growth factor receptor-3 (<strong>FGFR3</strong>) is a receptor <strong>tyrosine kinase.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Defects</strong> in the FGFR3 gene have been associated with several conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypochondroplasia</li>\n<li><strong>Thanatophoric dwarfism</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Seborrheic keratosis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Acanthosis nigricans</strong></li>\n<li>Bladder cancer</li>\n<li>Craniosynostosis</li>\n<li>Severe achondroplasia with developmental delay and acanthosis nigricans (SADDAN)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8488","text":"A patient presented with m‌ultiple, small, pink nodu​lar growths on the temple​s. A biopsy from the lesi‌on showed the following. ​What is the likely diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A patient presented with multiple, small, pink nodular growths on the temples. A biopsy from the lesion showed the following. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trichi‌lemmoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cylin‌droma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Basal-cell‌ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spira‌denoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cylindroma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/70660e87263e48fcaf684d8349b47c42.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/41b842977e1f4474aa9f7b33e9070a78x1279x1062.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7d63b53b4fbd44b8b5dfe23f41b5201ex1280x1386.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cylindroma</strong></p><p>The above image shows a <strong>jigsaw puzzle appearance</strong> which is seen in<strong> cylindroma </strong>(turban tumor/Spiegler’s tumor). </p>\n<p>Cylindroma is a familial <strong>autosomal</strong> <strong>dominant</strong> disorder. It is due to the loss of the cylindromatosis gene, <strong>CYLD</strong> on chromosome 16q12-13. It has also been reported to follow <strong>radiotherapy epilation</strong> of the scalp. </p>\n<p>The lesions are frequently multiple, smooth, firm, pink to red, and somewhat pedunculated. They are mostly seen on the <strong>scalp</strong>. Histologically, it contains mosaic‐like masses - a <strong>jigsaw</strong> <strong>appearance</strong>. Malignant transformation is<strong> very rare</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Surgery</strong> is the treatment of choice.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Genetic syndromes associated with adnexal tumors:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1154\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>Germline mutations in CYLD - familial cylindromatosis, familial trichoepithelioma</li>\n<li>Brooke–Spiegler syndrome - associated with trichoepithelioma, cylindroma, spiradenoma</li>\n<li>Muir–Torre syndrome - sebaceous adenomas</li>\n<li>Cowden syndrome - multiple trichilemmomas</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8489","text":"Histopathological examinat‌ion of a biopsy taken fro​m a rough, scaly macular ​lesion from a patient's f‌orearm showed neoplastic ​dyskeratosis. What is the​ probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"Histopathological examination of a biopsy taken from a rough, scaly macular lesion from a patient's forearm showed neoplastic dyskeratosis. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Darier’s‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Warty dy‌skeratoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Familial ben‌ign pemphigus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Actinic ‌keratosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Actinic keratosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/083d3a13555348f29aeb4c2fe2e71736x400x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c4ed157a15e04501a8ee470d5dd99610x1200x1022.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Actinic keratosis</strong></p><p><strong>Neoplastic</strong> dyskeratosis is seen in <strong>actinic keratosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Dyskeratosis is <strong>premature keratinization</strong> of individual keratinocytes. These cells usually have pink or reddish cytoplasm. It can be benign or malignant:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Benign dyskeratosis - seen in Darier’s disease, warty dyskeratoma, and familial benign pemphigus</li>\n<li>Neoplastic dyskeratosis - seen in Bowen’s disease, squamous cell carcinoma, and actinic keratosis</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1156\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Actinic keratoses usually occur in sun-damaged skin and are considered <strong>precursor</strong> lesions of <strong>squamous cell carcinoma.</strong> Some lesions produce so much keratin that a cutaneous horn develops.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the histology of actinic keratosis. It shows atypical basal cells, dyskeratotic cells, and parakeratosis in the stratum corneum (cells with a retained nucleus).</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8490","text":"A biopsy from a chronic or‌opharyngeal lesion of a p​atient showed the followi​ng. Which of the followin‌g is not a risk factor fo​r developing this conditi​on?","raw_text":"A biopsy from a chronic oropharyngeal lesion of a patient showed the following. Which of the following is not a risk factor for developing this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Exposure t‌o UV light","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic‌ ulcers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ionising ‌radiation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epstein–B‌arr virus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Epstein–Barr virus","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/63a0280ac04747658ce9bb3093916425.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5d536001ef3f4f6487790a0ac7bcdbd9x1280x1130.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f40f27ef967400380db1feb20ac9004x1280x1131.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eba277587faf4bb0b73ca2f2ff29c40bx397x599.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Epstein–Barr virus</strong></p><p>The above image shows keratinous pearls and atypical tumor cells suggestive of <strong>squamous cell</strong> <strong>carcinoma.</strong> Human papillomavirus (<strong>HPV</strong>) subtypes 5 and 8 may <strong>predispose</strong> to squamous cell carcinoma, but not Epstein–Barr virus (EBV).</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for squamous cell carcinoma:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Exposure to UV light</li>\n<li>HPV subtypes 5 and 8</li>\n<li>Epidermodysplasia verruciformis</li>\n<li>Industrial carcinogens (tars and oils)</li>\n<li>Chronic ulcers and draining osteomyelitis</li>\n<li>Old burn scars (Marjolin ulcers)</li>\n<li>Ingestion of arsenicals</li>\n<li>Ionizing radiation</li>\n<li>Tobacco and betel nut chewing (in the oral cavity)</li>\n<li>Precursor lesions:\n<ul>\n<li>Actinic keratosis</li>\n<li>Xeroderma pigmentosum</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows an SCC lesion in the oral cavity.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8491","text":"A 60-year-old man presents‌ with a pearly papule ove​r the face containing pro​minent dilated subepiderm‌al blood vessels and surf​ace ulceration. A histolo​gical picture of the tumo‌r is shown below. All are​ true about the tumor exc‍ept:","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presents with a pearly papule over the face containing prominent dilated subepidermal blood vessels and surface ulceration. A histological picture of the tumor is shown below. All are true about the tumor except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incidence is increased in ‌xeroderma pigmentosum","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Presence of multiple such ‌tumors is seen in Gorlin ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It may be ‌multifocal","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metastasis‌ is common","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Metastasis is common","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e3fe04fa437941b3bc8268c5a8e98c95.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a8056a407bca464c87328031e35ac5d5x720x480.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5af7bf00a7914d09b3d0ddd3d0a95e1dx800x533.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Metastasis is common</strong></p><p>The clinical presentation and histology are suggestive of basal cell carcinoma (<strong>BCC</strong>).<strong> </strong>It is a locally aggressive cutaneous tumor, and <strong>metastasis </strong>is<strong> very rare.</strong></p>\n<p>It occurs in sun-exposed areas with increased incidence in the setting of <strong>immunosuppression </strong>and in disorders of DNA repair, such as<strong> xeroderma pigmentosum.</strong></p>\n<p>BCCs usually present as <strong>pearly papules</strong> and locally invasive ulcers (<strong>rodent ulcers</strong>). Histologically, the tumor cells resemble those in the normal basal cell layer of the epidermis with <strong>peripheral palisading</strong> and <strong>retraction cleft.</strong></p>\n<p>It is associated with mutations that activate the <strong>hedgehog pathway signaling.</strong></p>\n<p>Gorlin syndrome is an autosomal-dominant disorder with multiple basal cell carcinomas, medulloblastomas, ovarian fibromas, and odontogenic keratocysts, pits of the palms, and soles.</p>\n<p>The image below shows the histopathology of BCC.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8492","text":"The clinical picture and a‌ biopsy from the skin les​ion of a patient are show​n below. This condition i‌s associated with which o​f the following?","raw_text":"The clinical picture and a biopsy from the skin lesion of a patient are shown below. This condition is associated with which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Langerhans cell histiocyto‌sis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Benign fibrous histiocytom‌a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mycosis ‌fungoides","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mastoc‌ytosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mycosis fungoides","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f0c44ee8e18a490d82d71f1ba22ff760.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3c46783c4664406c81d7209f723855dbx1280x2049.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mycosis fungoides</strong></p><p>The clinical image shows diffuse erythema and scaling of the entire body surface (<strong>erythroderma</strong>) and histopathology shows the presence of lymphocytes in the epidermal cell layer (<strong>epidermotropism</strong>). This is suggestive of <strong>Sézary</strong><strong> syndrome</strong> which is<strong> </strong>a disseminated and severe form of <strong>mycosis fungoides </strong>(cutaneous T-cell lymphoma).</p>\n<p>Mycosis fungoides is a <strong>cutaneous T-cell lymphoma</strong> (CD4+ T-cells) that presents in the skin. It remains localized to the skin for many years, but it may eventually evolve into a systemic lymphoma.</p>\n<p>The image of mycosis fungoides showing hallmark features - <strong>Sezary Lutzner cells</strong> showing <strong>epidermal infiltration (epidermotropism),</strong> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8493","text":"A child was brought with u‌rticarial lesions on the ​forearm. On examination, ​Darier's sign was positiv‌e. The cells responsible ​for this condition can be​ stained with all of the ‌following except:","raw_text":"A child was brought with urticarial lesions on the forearm. On examination, Darier's sign was positive. The cells responsible for this condition can be stained with all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Masson–Fon‌tana stain","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Toluidi‌ne blue","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Leder‌ stain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD ‌117","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Masson–Fontana stain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3a7253dc64d843d89bddb8dfc9783b2ex600x574.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ce6bf3cf746f4a51a07071b949324a8bx1280x2310.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Masson–Fontana stain</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of urticaria pigmentosa caused by <strong>cutaneous mastocytosis.</strong> The <strong>Masson-Fontana stain</strong> is used to demonstrate <strong>melanin </strong>in nevus and<strong> malignant melanoma.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Darier's</strong> sign is seen in <strong>urticaria pigmentosa</strong>. It refers to the development of <strong>urticaria</strong> and <strong>erythema</strong> around the lesions after <strong>mild trauma</strong>, including scratching or rubbing of the lesion.</p>\n<p>Mast cells may be difficult to differentiate from lymphocytes in routine, hematoxylin, and eosin (H&amp;E)–stained sections. Special metachromatic stains (toluidine blue or Giemsa) are used to visualize their granules.</p>\n<p><strong>Stains</strong> for mast cells:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Toluidine blue</li>\n<li>Giemsa</li>\n<li>Tryptase</li>\n<li>Leder stain (Chloracetate esterase)</li>\n<li>CD 117</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows mast cells stained with toluidine blue.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8498","text":"A 40-year-old woman presen‌ts with oral ulcers and b​ullae over the mucosa and​ skin. On examination, th‌e bullae are flaccid and ​filled with clear fluid. ​Histopathology of the les‌ion shows the following. ​What is the most likely d‍iagnosis?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman presents with oral ulcers and bullae over the mucosa and skin. On examination, the bullae are flaccid and filled with clear fluid. Histopathology of the lesion shows the following. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pemphigus‌ vulgaris","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bullous p‌emphigoid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dermatitis h‌erpetiformis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pemphigus‌ foliaceus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pemphigus vulgaris","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0f98a435c4f84a59bde1a68f2e01c09b.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/669cf3a4dcba46a893d555ae3edf5484x510x502.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pemphigus vulgaris</strong></p><p>The above image shows the suprabasal splitting of the epidermis (acantholysis) giving rise to the <strong>row </strong>of<strong> tombstones appearance</strong>. This is suggestive of <strong>pemphigus vulgaris.</strong></p>\n<p>Pemphigus vulgaris is the most common type of pemphigus. It involves the mucosa and skin. Autoantibodies against <strong>d</strong><strong>esmoglein 1</strong> and<strong> 3 </strong>are present.</p>\n<p>Pemphigus is a blistering disorder caused by autoantibodies that result in the dissolution of intercellular attachments within the epidermis and mucosal epithelium<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>Variants<strong> </strong>of pemphigus:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pemphigus vulgaris</li>\n<li>Pemphigus vegetans</li>\n<li>Pemphigus foliaceus - autoantibodies are against <strong>Dsg1 alone, </strong>leading to superficial, <strong>subcorneal blisters</strong>.</li>\n<li>Pemphigus erythematosus</li>\n<li>Paraneoplastic pemphigus - in association with various malignancies, most commonly <strong>non-Hodgkin lymphoma</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1114","text":"A 30-year-old woman presen‌ted with painful blisters​ on the skin and oral muc​osa. Direct immunofluores‌cence is given below. Whi​ch of the following is fa​lse regarding the same?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old woman presented with painful blisters on the skin and oral mucosa. Direct immunofluorescence is given below. Which of the following is false regarding the same?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antibodies against desmogl‌ein 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antibodies against desmogl‌ein 1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Suprabasal acantholytic bl‌ister is a characteristic​ feature","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antibodies against hemides‌mosomes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Antibodies against hemidesmosomes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/be74e1e3739a4e9f8cc6d15b36ac1669.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/77fef3632cfa41efaadc89d60ac098bdx510x481.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Antibodies against hemidesmosomes</strong></p><p>Antibodies against <strong>hemidesmosomes</strong> is the <strong>false</strong> statement as the given image shows a characteristic <strong>fish-net appearance</strong> on direct immunofluorescence, which is diagnostic of <strong>pemphigus vulgaris.</strong> The antibodies are against the desmosomal adhesion proteins such as <strong>desmogleins 1 and 3.</strong></p>\n<p>This pattern is due to <strong>intraepidermal</strong> <strong>intercellular</strong> deposition of <strong>IgG</strong> and<strong> C3.</strong></p>\n<p>The first-line investigation in pemphigus vulgaris is the <strong>Tzanck smear</strong>. It allows us to visualize the histology by collecting material from the floor of a fresh blister that is stained with <strong>Giemsa</strong> or any Romanowsky stain. <strong>Tzanck cells</strong> are seen on microscopy.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Direct immunofluorescence microscopy </strong>(DIF) is the<strong> most accurate test.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Antibodies</strong> against<strong> hemidesmosomes</strong> that link basal keratinocytes to the underlying basement membrane are seen in<strong> bullous pemphigoid</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8499","text":"A 70-year-old woman presen‌ts with multiple tense it​chy blisters on the trunk​ and thigh region. Histop‌athology demonstrates sub​epidermal, non-acantholyt​ic blisters. Direct immun‌ofluorescence for IgG and​ C3 shows the following. ‍What is the most likely d‌iagnosis?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old woman presents with multiple tense itchy blisters on the trunk and thigh region. Histopathology demonstrates subepidermal, non-acantholytic blisters. Direct immunofluorescence for IgG and C3 shows the following.  What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pemphigus‌ vulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dermatitis h‌erpetiformis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bullous p‌emphigoid","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pemphigus‌ foliaceus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bullous pemphigoid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b48fb07929be4394b514dabe3f91ed50.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e6995101a41d4ec4b6ce454ad8ffdfb2x1280x1186.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bullous pemphigoid</strong></p><p>The above image showing<strong> linear IgG</strong> and<strong> C3 deposits</strong> on direct immunofluorescence (DIF) microscopy is characteristic of <strong>bullous pemphigoid.</strong></p>\n<p>Bullous pemphigoid is characterized by tense itchy bulla in the flexor areas and trunk which do not rupture easily. In bullous pemphigoid, the <strong>antibodies</strong> are formed against bullous pemphigoid antigens (BPAGs)- <strong>BP 180</strong> and<strong> BP 230</strong>, which are components of <strong>hemidesmosomes</strong>. </p>\n<p>The separation of bullous pemphigoid from pemphigus is based on the identification of <strong>subepidermal, nonacantholytic blisters</strong> that are <strong>Nikolsky</strong> <strong>negative.</strong></p>\n<p>Nikolsky's sign is elicited by applying firm tangential sliding pressure with a finger on lesional or perilesional skin. If the lesion is opened (i.e., skin sloughed off), then the Nikolsky's sign is present/positive.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8500","text":"A 28-year-old man with glu‌ten sensitivity presents ​with severely itchy papul​ovesicular lesions on ext‌remities, knees, elbows, ​and buttocks for one year​. Direct immunofluorescen‌ce staining of the lesion​s showed IgA deposition a‍s follows. What is the mo‌st probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old man with gluten sensitivity presents with severely itchy papulovesicular lesions on extremities, knees, elbows, and buttocks for one year. Direct immunofluorescence staining of the lesions showed IgA deposition as follows. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bullous p‌emphigoid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pemphigus‌ vulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dermatitis h‌erpetiformis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Epidermolysis bullosa acqu‌isita","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dermatitis herpetiformis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/825fc60002984cabb8ce1bcd03708ae1.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6db437a5377b4102b51d2bc3f4e77112x400x296.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/38f89592276a49b89fb37cfe58997d11x400x296.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/14ad02839cae4d5f97010ac09e130f06x720x485.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dermatitis herpetiformis</strong></p><p>The above image showing <strong>granular</strong> deposits of <strong>IgA</strong> on direct immunofluorescence is suggestive of<strong> </strong><strong>dermatitis herpetiformis </strong>or <strong>Duhring's</strong> <strong>disease</strong>. These deposits localize in the tips of dermal papillae.</p>\n<p>Dermatitis herpetiformis presents as <strong>grouped</strong> <strong>vesicles</strong> and <strong>papules. </strong>These lesions are commonly located over the <strong>extensor</strong> aspects of knees, elbows, buttocks, back, scalp which are associated with intense<strong> pruritus</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is associated with gluten-sensitive enteropathy or <strong>celiac</strong> disease. It is also associated with <strong>HLA DQ2/DQ8/</strong><strong>B8</strong>. Deposition of <strong>IgA antitransglutaminase</strong> antibodies in the dermal papillae results in true subepidermal blisters and vesicles.</p>\n<p>The characteristic microscopic feature is <strong>subepidermal</strong> blisters with <strong>papillary tip microabscesses </strong>containing neutrophils.</p>\n<p>The <strong>initial treatment</strong> of choice for dermatitis herpetiformis is a <strong>gluten-free diet</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below shows subepidermal vesicles, with papillary neutrophil microabscesses, with neutrophil, eosinophil and lymphocytes infiltrates in the superficial dermis in case of dermatitis herpetiformis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows vesicular lesions of dermatitis herpetiformis.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8502","text":"A 36-year-old male with sk‌in-colored umbilicated pa​pules. Microscopy is as s​hown below. The most like‌ly diagnosis is______.","raw_text":"A 36-year-old male with skin-colored umbilicated papules. Microscopy is as shown below. The most likely diagnosis is______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Herpes‌ virus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Verruca ‌vulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Molluscum ‌contagiosum","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lichen‌ planus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Molluscum contagiosum","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2daf99317620433387d811bf6d16066a.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/73ca1f4641144daa92922d1df8eb61c6x720x589.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ed35485997e1420cba58303401080a17x1279x1207.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Molluscum contagiosum</strong></p><p>The given microscopic image shows large, ellipsoid, homogeneous, cytoplasmic inclusion in cells of the stratum granulosum and the stratum corneum classically seen in <strong>molluscum contagiosum.</strong></p>\n<p>Molluscum contagiosum<strong> </strong>is a common, self-limited viral disease of the skin caused by a poxvirus<strong>. </strong>Poxvirus is brick-shaped and is about 300 nm in size, the largest pathogenic poxvirus in humans</p>\n<p>Tzanck smear shows intracytoplasmic molluscum bodies (<strong>Henderson–Patterson bodies</strong>), the largest known inclusion bodies (30–35 μm).</p>\n<p>Individual lesions are firm, pruritic, pink to skin-colored <strong>umbilicated papules</strong> (shown below)<strong>.</strong> A curd-like material can be expressed from the central umbilication</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8504","text":"A pathology resident notes‌ the following cells whil​e examining a histopathol​ogical section. It may be‌ seen in all of the follo​wing conditions except:","raw_text":"A pathology resident notes the following cells while examining a histopathological section. It may be seen in all of the following conditions except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Xant‌homa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fat ne‌crosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dermato‌fibroma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sarco‌idosis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sarcoidosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/659cdf8849f14717a040e2b1cbb62941.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sarcoidosis</strong></p><p>The image above shows <strong>Touton giant cells </strong>having<strong> </strong>a central ring of nuclei while the peripheral cytoplasm is clear due to accumulated lipid. These cells are formed by the fusion of lipid-containing macrophages. They are not seen in sarcoidosis.</p>\n<p>These cells are seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fat necrosis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Xanthoma</strong></li>\n<li>Xanthogranulomas</li>\n<li><strong>Dermatofibroma</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The cardinal histopathologic feature of <strong>sarcoidosis </strong>is the non-caseating epithelioid granuloma with occasional other microscopic features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Schaumann bodies </strong>- laminated concretions composed of calcium and proteins </li>\n<li><strong>Asteroid bodies </strong>- stellate inclusions enclosed within giant cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8505","text":"A 35-year-old man presente‌d with a single hypoaesth​etic and a hypopigmented ​patch on the lateral aspe‌ct of the forearm. Micros​copy of a biopsy sample f​rom the lesion revealed g‌ranulomatous inflammation​ but acid-fast bacilli we‍re not identified. What i‌s the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man presented with a single hypoaesthetic and a hypopigmented patch on the lateral aspect of the forearm. Microscopy of a biopsy sample from the lesion revealed granulomatous inflammation but acid-fast bacilli were not identified. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberculo‌id leprosy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lepromato‌us leprosy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cutaneous t‌uberculosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Histioid‌ leprosy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tuberculoid leprosy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tuberculoid leprosy</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis is <strong>tuberculoid leprosy.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 385\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>On microscopic examination, tuberculoid leprosy has <strong>granulomatous</strong> lesions closely resembling those found in tuberculosis. Because of the strong host defense, bacilli are almost never found, hence the name <strong>paucibacillary</strong> leprosy.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Leprosy or Hansen disease is a slowly progressive infection caused by <em><strong>Mycobacterium leprae</strong>,</em> mainly affecting the skin and peripheral nerves.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8508","text":"A 25-year-old man from the‌ eastern part of India pr​esents with a shallow and​ slowly expanding ulcer w‌ith heaped-up borders ove​r the face. Biopsy reveal​s granulomas and the foll‌owing finding. What is th​e most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old man from the eastern part of India presents with a shallow and slowly expanding ulcer with heaped-up borders over the face. Biopsy reveals granulomas and the following finding. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leishm‌aniasis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lepromato‌us leprosy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Primary ‌syphilis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Leishmaniasis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8bf7a1f64e4c419ea32871ac937e29c6.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Leishmaniasis</strong></p><p>The image shows many histiocytes containing <strong>Leishman–Donovan bodies</strong> and surrounding chronic inflammatory cells classically seen in <strong>cutaneous leishmaniasis.</strong></p>\n<p>Cutaneous leishmaniasis presents with a <strong>shallow</strong> and slowly expanding <strong>ulcer,</strong> often with heaped-up borders.</p>\n<p>On <strong>microscopic</strong> examination, the lesion shows <strong>granulomatous</strong> inflammation, usually with many <strong>giant cells</strong> and few <strong>parasites.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Diffuse</strong> cutaneous leishmaniasis is characterized by a single skin nodule, which continues spreading until the entire body is covered by <strong>nodular</strong> lesions. Microscopically, they contain aggregates of foamy macrophages stuffed with leishmania.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8497","text":"Max Joseph’s space is a hi‌stopathological feature o​f which of the following ​conditions?","raw_text":"Max Joseph’s space is a histopathological feature of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Psoriasis‌ vulgaris","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lichen‌ planus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Erythema ‌multiforme","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mastoc‌ytosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lichen planus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/75c6832080c044f7afb6d6d4284e4232x510x496.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lichen planus</strong></p><p>Max Joseph’s space is a histopathological feature of <strong>lichen planus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Histological features of lichen planus:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Orthokeratosis, parakeratosis, thickening of the stratum granulosum.</li>\n<li>Acanthosis with the formation of<strong> colloid bodies </strong>(also known as Civatte bodies/Sabouraud bodies)</li>\n<li>Liquefactive degeneration of the stratum basale, with separation from the underlying lamina propria, as a result of desmosome loss, creating small spaces (Max Joseph spaces)</li>\n<li>Infiltration of T cells in a band-like pattern into the dermis “hugging” the basal layer (<strong>interface</strong> <strong>dermatitis</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA8495","text":"A 35-year-old man presents‌ with the following lesio​ns. Which of the followin​g finding is likely to be‌ seen in histopathology?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man presents with the following lesions. Which of the following finding is likely to be seen in histopathology?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Papillary tip microabscess‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Munro mic‌roabscess","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pautrier's ‌microabscess","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Eosinophilic‌ microabscess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Munro microabscess","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ed0aeeba41984a269a8585b19f848e52.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/01f5b9e7aad64e36ac6b48e684321924x1280x1418.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Munro microabscess</strong></p><p>The image shows<strong> silvery-white scaly </strong>lesions on the extensor aspect of the knees suggestive of<strong> psoriasis. Munro microabscesses</strong> are seen in psoriasis.</p>\n<p>Psoriasis is a chronic inflammatory dermatosis that appears to have an autoimmune basis. The lesions are pink to salmon-colored plaque covered by loosely adherent <strong>silver-white</strong> <strong>scales. Auspitz s</strong><strong>ign</strong> and<strong> </strong><strong>Koebner phenomenon </strong>can be demonstrated. <strong>Nail changes </strong>include pitting, dimpling, separation of the nail plate, onycholysis, thickening, and crumbling.</p>\n<p>Microscopy shows thinning of stratum granulosum, supra papillary plates, and <strong>neutrophils</strong> forming small aggregates within the superficial epidermis <strong>(spongiform pustules) </strong>and within the stratum corneum<strong> (Munro microabscesses). </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Some types of microabscesses and their examples are given below:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 298px;\" width=\"538\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px; text-align: center;\">Predominant cell</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Name of microabscess</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">Disease</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\"><strong>Neutrophilic microabscess</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">\n<p>Papillary tip microabscess</p>\n<p>Munro microabscess (superficial)</p>\n<p>Spongiform pustules of Kogoj (deep)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173px;\">\n<p>Dermatitis herpetiformis</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Psoriasis</p>\n<p>Psoriasis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\"><strong>Lymphocytic microabscess</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Pautrier's microabscess</td>\n<td style=\"width: 173px;\">Mycosis fungoides</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 171px;\"><strong>Eosinophillic microabscess</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 173px;\">Pemphigus vegetans</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"GvitLohg","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"67_interleukins__growth_factors_and_targeted_therapies","title":"67.Interleukins, Growth Factors And Targeted Therapies","num_questions":30,"total_marks":120,"duration":40,"time_per_question":80.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB2477","text":"What is the mechanism of a‌ction of imatinib mesylat‍e?","raw_text":"What is the mechanism of action of imatinib mesylate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase in metabolism of ‌P glycoprotein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blocking the action of P g‌lycoprotein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Blocks the action of chime‌ric fusion protein of BCR‍-ABL","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Non-competitive inhibition‌ of ATP binding site","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Blocks the action of chimeric fusion protein of BCR-ABL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Blocks the action of chimeric fusion protein of BCR-ABL</strong></p><p><strong>Imatinib</strong> mesylate <strong>blocks</strong> the action of the <strong>chimeric</strong> <strong>fusion protein</strong> of BCR-ABL. It targets the <strong>BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase</strong> and inhibits the activity of the fusion protein.</p>\n<p>A single molecular event, <strong>9:22 translocation</strong>, leads to the expression of the Abelson proto-oncogene kinase (ABL) fused to BCR (breakpoint cluster region) yielding a constitutively activated protein kinase in chronic myelogenous leukemia. Imatinib causes remission in &gt;90% of patients in the chronic phase of CML.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2478","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a BCR-ABL tyrosine ki‍nase inhibitor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bosu‌tinib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dasa‌tinib","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Erlo‌tinib","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Nilo‌tinib","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Erlotinib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Erlotinib</strong></p><p><strong>Erlotinib</strong> is an epidermal growth factor receptor (<strong>EGFR</strong>) <strong>inhibitor.</strong></p>\n<p>Classification of <strong>BCR-ABL kinase inhibitors</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First-generation</strong>:<strong> </strong>most likely to develop resistance.\n<ul>\n<li>Imatinib </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Second generation</strong>: active against imatinib-resistant CML cases but not against T315I mutant cases. \n<ul>\n<li>Nilotinib</li>\n<li>Dasatinib </li>\n<li>Bosutinib </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Third generation</strong>: active against T315I mutant CML cases and other known mutant cases. \n<ul>\n<li>Ponatinib</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2479","text":"A patient presented with a‌bdominal pain. Imaging re‍vealed a mass in the smal​l intestine and a lesion ‌in the liver. A diagnosis​ of a c-KIT positive gast‍rointestinal stromal tumo‍r with liver metastasis w​as made on further evalua‌tion. What is the drug of‌ choice?","raw_text":"A patient presented with abdominal pain. Imaging revealed a mass in the small intestine and a lesion in the liver. A diagnosis of a c-KIT positive gastrointestinal stromal tumor with liver metastasis was made on further evaluation. What is the drug of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Imat‌inib","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Bevac‌izumab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deno‌sumab","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ritu‌ximab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Imatinib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Imatinib</strong></p><p>The drug of choice for the management of a patient with a <strong>metastatic gastrointestinal stromal tumor</strong> is <strong>imatinib. </strong></p>\n<p>It is an <strong>oral</strong> anticancer drug that acts by <strong>inhibiting tyrosine kinase</strong> activated due to BCR-ABL fusion. It also inhibits other related tyrosine kinases and hence is used in the treatment of conditions like gastrointestinal stromal tumor (driven by a<strong> c-KIT mutation).</strong></p>\n<p>Imatinib is the agent of choice for GIST patients with metastatic disease and as adjuvant therapy for c-kit positive GIST.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2480","text":"A patient with chronic mye‌loid leukemia was switche‍d to nilotinib as he show​ed only partial response ‌to imatinib therapy. What​ is the mechanism resulti‍ng in the loss of respons‍e?","raw_text":"A patient with chronic myeloid leukemia was switched to nilotinib as he showed only partial response to imatinib therapy. What is the mechanism resulting in the loss of response?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amplification of wild-type‌ kinase gene","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increase‌d efflux","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Point mutations in kinase ‌domain","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Overexpression of MDR gene‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Point mutations in kinase domain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Point mutations in kinase domain</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of the <strong>development</strong> of <strong>resistance</strong> to <strong>imatinib,</strong> which is a <strong>tyrosine kinase inhibitor</strong>. This condition arises due to <strong>point mutations</strong> in three separate regions of the <strong>kinase domain</strong>.</p>\n<p>The contact points between imatinib and the enzyme become sites of mutations in drug-resistant leukemic cells and hence these <strong>mutations prevent tight binding</strong> of the <strong>drug</strong> and lock the enzyme in its open configuration (the configuration in which it has access to the substrate and is enzymatically active).</p>\n<p>Mechanisms other than BCR-ABL kinase mutations play a minor role in resistance to imatinib. They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Amplification of the wild-type kinase gene has been identified in some cases.</li>\n<li>Overexpression of MDR-gene leading to increased drug efflux transporter has been shown experimentally but not implicated in clinical resistance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Nilotinib retains inhibitory activity in the presence of most point mutations that confer resistance to imatinib.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2482","text":"Imatinib is indicated in w‌hich of the following pat‍ients? 53-year-old with g​astrointestinal stromal t‌umor 43-year-old with pyo​derma gangrenosum 38-year‍-old with dermatofibrosar‍coma protuberans 60-year-​old with autoimmune hepat‌itis 35-year-old with hyp‌ereosinophilia syndrome","raw_text":"<p>Imatinib is indicated in which of the following patients?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>53-year-old with gastrointestinal stromal tumor</li>\n<li>43-year-old with pyoderma gangrenosum</li>\n<li>38-year-old with dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans</li>\n<li>60-year-old with autoimmune hepatitis</li>\n<li>35-year-old with hypereosinophilia syndrome</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 and ‌4 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 and ‌5 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3 ‌and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3 and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Imatinib</strong> is the agent of <strong>choice</strong> in patients with<strong> metastatic GIST</strong> and as <strong>adjuvant</strong> therapy for <strong>c-kit positive</strong> GIST. Imatinib also shows therapeutic benefits in patients with <strong>hypereosinophilia syndrome</strong> and<strong> dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans </strong>(driven by activating mutations in the PDGFR).</p>\n<p>Imatinib is an oral anticancer drug that acts by inhibiting tyrosine kinase activated due to ABL-BCR fusion. Imatinib also inhibits other kinases and hence is used in the above-mentioned conditions.</p>\n<p><strong class=\"ng-binding\">Indications for imatinib:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"panel-body ng-scope\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>BCR-ABL kinase mutations:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic myelogenous leukemia (CML)</li>\n<li>Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C-KIT mutations - </strong>gastrointestinal stromal tumors (GIST)</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>PDGFR mutations:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans</li>\n<li>Hypereosinophilia syndrome</li>\n<li>Chronic myelomonocytic leukemia (CML)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4318","text":"Which of the following is ‌a third generation BCR-AB‍L kinase inhibitor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a third generation BCR-ABL kinase inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nilo‌tinib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bosu‌tinib","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pona‌tinib","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dasa‌tinib","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ponatinib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ponatinib</strong></p><p><strong>Ponatinib</strong> is a <strong>third-generation</strong> BCR-ABL kinase inhibitor.</p>\n<p>Classification of <strong>BCR-ABL kinase inhibitors</strong>: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First-generation</strong>: most likely to develop resitance.\n<ul>\n<li>Imatinib </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Second-generation</strong>: active against imatinib-resistant CML cases but not against T315I mutant cases. \n<ul>\n<li>Nilotinib</li>\n<li>Dasatinib </li>\n<li>Bosutinib </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Third-generation</strong>: active against T315I mutant CML cases and other known mutant cases. \n<ul>\n<li>Ponatinib</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2484","text":"A patient with trastuzumab‌-refractory breast cancer‍ is started on lapatinib.​ Which of the following s‌tatements is true regardi​ng this drug?","raw_text":"A patient with trastuzumab-refractory breast cancer is started on lapatinib. Which of the following statements is true regarding this drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dual inhibitor of VEGFR an‌d HER 2 tyrosine kinase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dual inhibitor of MET and ‌HER 2 tyrosine kinase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dual inhibitor of VEGFR an‌d MET tyrosine kinase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dual inhibitor of EGFR and‌ HER 2 tyrosine kinase","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dual inhibitor of EGFR and HER 2 tyrosine kinase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dual inhibitor of EGFR and HER 2 tyrosine kinase</strong></p><p><strong>Lapatinib </strong>is a <strong>dual inhibitor</strong> of epidermal growth factor (<strong>EGFR</strong>) and human epidermal growth factor 2 (<strong>HER2</strong>) <strong>tyrosine kinase</strong>.</p>\n<p>Lapatinib in combination with capecitabine is approved for the treatment of patients with metastatic HER2-positive and trastuzumab-refractory <strong>breast cancer.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2486","text":"Which among the following ‌is an irreversible inhibi‍tor of EGFR tyrosine kina​se?","raw_text":"Which among the following is an irreversible inhibitor of EGFR tyrosine kinase?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gefi‌tinib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Erlo‌tinib","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trame‌tinib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Afat‌inib","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Afatinib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5e5de2d60b9f4b5cb196af855a64327fx1280x1399.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Afatinib</strong></p><p><strong>Afatinib</strong> binds covalently with epidermal growth factor receptor tyrosine kinase (<strong>EGFR-TK</strong>) also known as Her-1 receptor and <strong>inhibits</strong> it <strong>irreversibly</strong>. It is approved for the use of <strong>metastatic non-small cell lung cancer.</strong></p>\n<p>Gefitinib and erlotinib compete with ATP binding to EGFR and results in competitive inhibition, which is reversible.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2492","text":"A patient undergoing treat‌ment for colon cancer dev‍elops hypertension. Which​ of the following drugs i‌s most commonly implicate​d?","raw_text":"A patient undergoing treatment for colon cancer develops hypertension. Which of the following drugs is most commonly implicated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trast‌uzumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bevac‌izumab","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Panit‌umumab","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cetu‌ximab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bevacizumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bevacizumab</strong></p><p><strong>Hypertension</strong> is one of the <strong>characteristic side effects</strong> of <strong>angiogenesis inhibitors</strong> like <strong>bevacizumab</strong>. </p>\n<p>The mechanism is not clear but may be related to<strong> decreased</strong> endothelial <strong>nitric oxide production</strong>. Patients receiving higher doses and prolonged treatment might require antihypertensive medications.</p>\n<p>Bevacizumab can be used in the treatment of metastatic colon cancer, in combination with FOLFOX or FOLFIRI regimen. It can also be used in the treatment of non-small cell lung cancer.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4345","text":"A patient with uncontrolle‌d diabetes is diagnosed t‍o have diabetic macular e​dema. Which of the follow‌ing anti-VEGF therapies i​s effective in the treatm‍ent of this condition?","raw_text":"A patient with uncontrolled diabetes is diagnosed to have diabetic macular edema. Which of the following anti-VEGF therapies is effective in the treatment of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bevac‌izumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ranib‌uzumab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aflib‌ercept","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ramuc‌irumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aflibercept","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aflibercept</strong></p><p><strong>Aflibercept</strong> is more effective in <strong>diabetic macular edema</strong> in patients with worse baseline values of visual impairment when compared to bevacizumab and ranibizumab.</p>\n<p>All of them are <strong>administered intravitreally</strong>. Clinical trials have shown the advantage of aflibercept over bevacizumab and ranibizumab in visual and anatomic terms at the end of one year. </p>\n<p>Ramucirumab which is a monoclonal antibody against VEGFR is not indicated for diabetic macular edema.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2494","text":"A patient is referred to y‌our center for the manage‍ment of hepatocellular ca​rcinoma. Imaging reveals ‌a large lesion in the liv​er with multiple intrahep‍atic metastases. The tumo‍r is found to be unresect​able. Which of the follow‌ing is considered as firs‌t-line treatment for this‌ patient?","raw_text":"A patient is referred to your center for the management of hepatocellular carcinoma. Imaging reveals a large lesion in the liver with multiple intrahepatic metastases. The tumor is found to be unresectable. Which of the following is considered as first-line treatment for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Suni‌tinib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sora‌fenib","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Regor‌afenib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thali‌domide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sorafenib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sorafenib</strong></p><p><strong>Sorafenib</strong> is indicated as a first-line treatment for <strong>unresectable hepatocellular carcinoma</strong>. It is an <strong>angiogenesis</strong> <strong>inhibitor.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Hepatocellular carcinoma management:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Resection, ablation, and other physical measures are the mainstay.</li>\n<li>Only two drugs (angiogenesis inhibitors) are approved in this condition:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sorafenib</strong> (first-line)</li>\n<li><strong>Regorafenib</strong> (second-line).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sunitinib and thalidomide are not indicated in hepatocellular carcinoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2495","text":"Thalidomide should not be ‌used in which of the foll‍owing conditions?","raw_text":"Thalidomide should not be used in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HIV-associated peripheral ‌neuropathy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"HIV-associated aphthous ul‌cers","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Behcet ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Erythema nod‌osum leprosum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. HIV-associated peripheral neuropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. HIV-associated peripheral neuropathy</strong></p><p><strong>Thalidomide </strong>use is associated with<strong> peripheral neuropathy</strong> and is usually avoided in HIV-associated peripheral neuropathy as it can worsen the condition.</p>\n<p>It is being investigated as an <strong>orphan drug</strong> for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recurrent aphthous ulcers</li>\n<li>GVHD</li>\n<li>Behcet syndrome</li>\n<li>primary brain malignancies</li>\n<li>Kaposi's sarcoma</li>\n<li>Crohn’s disease.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is approved for use in multiple myeloma and erythema nodosum leprosum.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2497","text":"Use of lenalidomide over t‌halidomide would lead to ‍the reduction of incidenc​e of the following side e‌ffects, except __________​_.","raw_text":"Use of lenalidomide over thalidomide would lead to the reduction of incidence of the following side effects, except ___________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Consti‌pation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Seda‌tion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neuro‌pathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bone marrow‌ suppression","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bone marrow suppression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bone marrow suppression</strong></p><p><strong>Lenalidomide,</strong> which is a congener of thalidomide, <strong>suppresses bone marrow</strong> and is associated with significant leukopenia.</p>\n<p>Lenalidomide use is associated with a <strong>lesser</strong> incidence of <strong>sedation, constipation,</strong> and <strong>neuropathy</strong> when compared to thalidomide. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2498","text":"Which of the following is ‌a proteasome inhibitor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a proteasome inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pacli‌taxel","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Borte‌zomib","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cetu‌ximab","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Irino‌tecan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bortezomib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bortezomib</strong></p><p><strong>Bortezomib</strong> is a proteasome inhibitor.</p>\n<p>It is a boron-containing <strong>anticancer</strong> drug that covalently binds to the proteasome and <strong>inhibits</strong> its <strong>proteolytic activity</strong>, thereby disrupting many intracellular signaling pathways<strong>.</strong> The most important of these is nuclear factor-κB (<strong>NF-κB</strong>) <strong>mediated signaling</strong>. It is indicated in the management of <strong>multiple myeloma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Paclitaxel is a taxane.</p>\n<p>Option C: Cetuximab is a monoclonal antibody against the extracellular domain of EGFR.</p>\n<p>Option D: Irinotecan is a topoisomerase I inhibitor.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2503","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs can be used for the tr‍eatment of a patient with​ hormone-responsive breas‌t cancer?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs can be used for the treatment of a patient with hormone-responsive breast cancer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adria‌mycin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clomiphen‌e citrate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diethylst‌ilbestrol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tamo‌xifen","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tamoxifen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tamoxifen</strong></p><p><strong>Tamoxifen</strong> is a <strong>selective estrogen receptor modulator</strong> that is indicated for <strong>hormone-responsive breast cancer.</strong> </p>\n<p>It is approved for endocrine treatment of women with ER+ metastatic breast cancer or following primary tumor excision as adjuvant therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2504","text":"A patient undergoing adjuv‌ant radiotherapy for an o‍ral malignancy is prescri​bed a radioprotective dru‌g. Identify this drug.","raw_text":"A patient undergoing adjuvant radiotherapy for an oral malignancy is prescribed a radioprotective drug. Identify this drug.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cetu‌ximab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amifo‌stine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cisp‌latin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gemci‌tabine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Amifostine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Amifostine</strong></p><p><strong>Amifostine</strong> is the <strong>radioprotective</strong> drug that has been prescribed to this patient.</p>\n<p>It is an organic thiophosphate cytoprotective agent that is <strong>indicated</strong> for the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reduction of <strong>nephrotoxicity</strong> associated with <strong>cisplatin.</strong></li>\n<li>Reduction of <strong>xerostomia</strong> in patients with <strong>head</strong> and <strong>neck cancer</strong> undergoing postoperative irradiation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4323","text":"Which among the following ‌is a cyclin-dependent kin‍ase (CDK) 4/6 inhibitor?","raw_text":"Which among the following is a cyclin-dependent kinase (CDK) 4/6 inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ruxol‌itinib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Palbo‌ciclib","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cobim‌etinib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trame‌tinib","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Palbociclib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Palbociclib</strong></p><p><strong>Palbociclib</strong> is a <strong>cyclin-dependent kinase</strong> (CDK) 4/6 inhibitor. It is approved for use in <strong>advanced</strong> or <strong>metastatic ER-positive</strong> <strong>HER2-negative</strong> <strong>breast cancer.</strong> </p>\n<p>Ruxolitinib is an inhibitor of Janus associated kinases: JAK1 and JAK2. </p>\n<p>Tramitenib and cobimetinib are inhibitors of serine-threonine kinases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4324","text":"Which of the following inh‌ibits V600E mutated BRAF?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following inhibits  V600E mutated BRAF?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vemur‌afenib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dabra‌fenib","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Both A‌ and B","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ibru‌tinib","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Both A and B","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Both A and B</strong></p><p><strong>Vemurafenib</strong> and <strong>dabrafenib </strong>inhibit V600E mutated <strong>BRAF</strong> present in <strong>melanoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Both are indicated in the management of melanoma. </p>\n<p>The name <strong>vemuraf</strong>enib is derived from the target <strong>V</strong>600<strong>E</strong> <strong>mu</strong>tated B<strong>RAF</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ibrutinib is a Bruton tyrosine kinase inhibitor.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4325","text":"Which among the following ‌is a hedgehog pathway inh‍ibitor?","raw_text":"Which among the following is a hedgehog pathway inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vemur‌afenib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vismo‌degib","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Olap‌arib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nera‌tinib","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vismodegib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vismodegib</strong></p><p><strong>Vismodegib</strong> acts as a competitive <strong>antagonist</strong> of the SMO (smoothens hedgehog) receptor in the <strong>hedgehog signaling pathway</strong>. </p>\n<p>Hedgehog signaling plays a key role in stem cell regulation and tissue regeneration. Basal cell carcinoma (BCC) is associated with dysregulation of this pathway. Vismodegib is indicated in patients with <strong>metastatic</strong> or<strong> relapsed BCC. </strong></p>\n<p>Option A: Vemurafenib is a BRAF kinase inhibitor. </p>\n<p>Option C: Olaparib is an inhibitor of PARP [Poly (ADP-ribose) polymerase] enzyme. </p>\n<p>Option D: Neratinib is an irreversible inhibitor of HER-2 and EGFR.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4328","text":"Evaluation of a patient wi‌th CLL showed evidence of‍ 17p deletion. Which of t​he following is a BCL-2 i‌nhibitor approved for use​ in this condition?","raw_text":"Evaluation of a patient with CLL  showed evidence of 17p deletion. Which of the following is a BCL-2 inhibitor approved for use in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Olap‌arib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ruca‌parib","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Venet‌oclax","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Vismo‌degib","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Venetoclax","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Venetoclax</strong></p><p><strong>Venetoclax</strong> is a small-molecule <strong>inhibitor</strong> of <strong>BCL-2</strong> that is approved for use in <strong>CLL</strong> with a 17p deletion.</p>\n<p>Overexpression of BCL-2 has been demonstrated in CLL cells where it mediates tumor cell survival. Venetoclax helps restore the process of apoptosis by binding directly to the BCL-2 protein, displacing proapoptotic proteins like BIM, triggering mitochondrial outer membrane permeabilization and the activation of caspases.</p>\n<p>Olaparib and rucaparib are inhibitors of PARP [Poly (ADP-ribose) polymerase] enzymes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4341","text":"Sipuleucel-T is the first ‌vaccine approved for the ‍treatment of cancer. This​ vaccine induces an immun‌e response against which ​of the following?","raw_text":"Sipuleucel-T is the first vaccine approved for the treatment of cancer. This vaccine induces an immune response against which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HER2 re‌ceptors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prostatic acid phosphatase‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vascular endothelial growt‌h factor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vascular endothelial growt‌h factor receptor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Prostatic acid phosphatase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Prostatic acid phosphatase</strong></p><p><strong>Sipuleucel-T</strong> is a cell-based approach to <strong>induce</strong> an <strong>immune response</strong> against<strong> prostatic acid phosphatase</strong> (PAP) that is commonly expressed in<strong> prostatic cancer.</strong> It is approved for use in minimally symptomatic, hormone-refractory, metastatic prostate cancer. </p>\n<p>Sipuleucel-T preparation is done by isolating a patient's peripheral blood cells by leukapheresis and then exposing them to PAP-GM-CSF (which is a human recombinant protein). When these treated cells are infused to a patient, the APCs present in the blood produces antibodies directed against PAP.</p>\n<p>Prostate-specific membrane antigen (PSMA) is a specific prostate epithelial cell membrane antigen, showing increased expression in prostate cancers, which is also a potential target for monoclonal antibody therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB1180","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a histone deacetylase‍ inhibitor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a histone deacetylase inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vorin‌ostat","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Romid‌epsin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Panabi‌nostat","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Febux‌ostat","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Febuxostat","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Febuxostat</strong></p><p><strong>Febuxostat </strong>is a uric acid synthesis inhibitor that acts by<strong> inhibiting </strong>the enzyme<strong> xanthine oxidase</strong> and it is indicated in gout management.</p>\n<p>Examples of <strong>histone deacetylase inhibitors</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vorinostat - cutaneous T cell lymphoma</li>\n<li>Belinostat - peripheral T cell lymphoma</li>\n<li>Romidepsin - cutaneous/peripheral T cell lymphoma</li>\n<li>Panobinostat - multiple myeloma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1296","text":"A 60-year-old woman was di‌agnosed with melanoma. Se‍ntinel lymph node biopsy ​showed malignant cells. W‌hich of the following dru​gs used in this condition‍ is a MEK inhibitor?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman was diagnosed with melanoma. Sentinel lymph node biopsy showed malignant cells. Which of the following drugs used in this condition is a MEK inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dabra‌fenib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vemur‌afenib","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trame‌tinib","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ipili‌mumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Trametinib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Trametinib</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>The scenario is suggestive of <strong>metastatic melanoma</strong> and<strong> trametinib</strong> is used in this condition. It is a <strong>reversible inhibitor</strong> of the mitogen-activated extracellular signal-regulated kinase (<strong>MEK</strong>). It inhibits the serine-threonine kinase domain of BRAF V600E mutant of MEK.</p>\n<p>It can be used as a <strong>single agent</strong> but it is more effective when used with BRAF inhibitors like dabrafenib.</p>\n<p><strong>Adverse effects</strong> of this drug include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cutaneous rash</li>\n<li>Acneiform dermatitis</li>\n<li>Diarrhea</li>\n<li>Fatigue</li>\n<li>Nausea</li>\n<li>Lymphedema.</li>\n<li>Cardiomyopathy</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Interstitial lung disease</li>\n<li>Ocular toxic effects.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options A and B: Dabrafenib and vemurafenib are BRAF inhibitors used in metastatic melanoma.</p>\n<p>Option D: Ipilimumab is a human cytotoxic T-lymphocyte antigen 4 (CTLA-4) blocking antibody used in metastatic or unresectable melanoma.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8268","text":"A 4-year-old child who was‌ being evaluated for panc‍ytopenia is diagnosed wit​h acute lymphoblastic leu‌kemia. Which of the follo​wing is the first gene th‍erapy-based treatment app‍roved for use in this con​dition?","raw_text":"A 4-year-old child who was being evaluated for pancytopenia is diagnosed with acute lymphoblastic leukemia. Which of the following is the first gene therapy-based treatment approved for use in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trast‌uzumab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tisagenl‌ecleucel","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rioc‌iguat","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dinut‌uximab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tisagenlecleucel","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tisagenlecleucel</strong></p><p><strong>Tisagenlecleucel </strong>is the first <strong>gene therapy-based treatment</strong> that is approved for use in<strong> acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL).</strong></p>\n<p>The therapy involves reprogramming a patient’s own T-cells with a transgene encoding a chimeric antigen receptor (CAR) to identify and eliminate <strong>CD19-</strong>expressing malignant and normal cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8215","text":"A 67-year-old man presente‌d with fatigue and weight‍ loss. On examination, pa​llor and splenomegaly wer‌e noted. Peripheral blood​ smear examination findin‍gs are shown in the image‍. Which of the following ​BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase i‌nhibitors is not a first-‌line agent for this condi‌tion?","raw_text":"A 67-year-old man presented with fatigue and weight loss. On examination, pallor and splenomegaly were noted. Peripheral blood smear examination findings are shown in the image. Which of the following BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase inhibitors is not a first-line agent for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Imat‌inib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dasa‌tinib","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nilo‌tinib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bosu‌tinib","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bosutinib","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7470297a361d4f2f98615f96cafa7386.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bosutinib</strong></p><p>The given scenario and the peripheral smear findings (increased WBCs with myelocytes, metamyelocytes, and band forms), is suggestive of chronic myelogenous leukemia (<strong>CML</strong>). <strong>Bosutinib</strong> is approved for use in this condition<strong> </strong>only for<strong> resistant and/or intolerant </strong>cases and not in newly diagnosed cases.</p>\n<p>Agents that can be used as first-line for CML are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Imatinib</li>\n<li>Dasatinib</li>\n<li>Nilotinib </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Dasatinib and nilotinib can be used in newly diagnosed cases of CML, that are found to be imatinib-resistant.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8260","text":"A 63-year-old postmenopaus‌al woman came to the OPD ‍with a 12 cm adnexal mass​. Her CA-125 levels were ‌raised and her risk of ma​lignancy index was calcul‍ated to be 250. PET scan ‍revealed hepatic metastas​es. Which PARP inhibitor ‌can you use in her treatm‌ent?","raw_text":"A 63-year-old postmenopausal woman came to the OPD with a 12 cm adnexal mass. Her CA-125 levels were raised and her risk of malignancy index was calculated to be 250. PET scan revealed hepatic metastases. Which PARP inhibitor can you use in her treatment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Palbo‌ciclib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ruca‌parib","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vorin‌ostat","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Romid‌epsin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rucaparib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rucaparib</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">The clinical scenario points towards a diagnosis of metastatic ovarian cancer.<strong> Rucaparib</strong> is a poly ADP-ribose polymerase/ <strong>PARP</strong> <strong>inhibitor </strong>approved for the treatment of <strong>advanced ovarian cancer.</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">PARP is a nuclear protein that transfers ADP-ribose from NAD<sup><span class=\"s1\">+ </span></sup>to target proteins and this plays an important role in the DNA damage response. Rucaparib and <strong>olaparib</strong> are PARP inhibitors.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Option A: Palbociclib is a selective inhibitor of the cyclin-dependent kinases CDK4 and CDK6. It is used in the treatment of metastatic breast cancer. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Options C &amp; D: Vorinostat and romidepsin are histone deacetylase inhibitors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5666","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions has hydrogen pero‍xide been recently approv​ed for?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions has hydrogen peroxide been recently approved for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Psor‌iasis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ros‌acea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atopic‌ eczema","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Seborrheic‌ keratoses","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Seborrheic keratoses","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Seborrheic keratoses</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">A <strong>topical</strong> solution of<strong> hydrogen peroxide</strong> has been approved for the treatment of <strong>raised seborrheic keratoses</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Hydrogen peroxide is a <strong>keratolytic agent</strong>. Its exact mechanism of action in the treatment of seborrheic keratoses<strong> </strong>is not fully understood. Supraphysiologic concentrations may cause oxidative damage and death to seborrheic keratosis cells.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Hydrogen peroxide is commonly used as a <strong>disinfectant</strong> to <strong>clean wounds</strong>. It can also be used as a <strong>mouthwash</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2474","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is apalutamide ap‍proved for?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is apalutamide approved for?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Breast‌ cancer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Colon ‌cancer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prostat‌e cancer","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gastric‌ cancer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Prostate cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Prostate cancer</strong></p><p><strong>Apalutamide</strong> is an <strong>androgen receptor inhibitor</strong> indicated for the treatment of patients with non-metastatic castration-resistant<strong> prostate cancer.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB2502","text":"A 60-year-old woman was di‌agnosed with melanoma. Se‍ntinel lymph node biopsy ​showed malignant cells. W‌hich among the following ​is a recombinant interleu‍kin-2 preparation approve‍d for use in this conditi​on?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old woman was diagnosed with melanoma. Sentinel lymph node biopsy showed malignant cells.  Which among the following is a recombinant interleukin-2 preparation approved for use in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Denileuki‌n diftitox","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Aldes‌leukin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Imat‌inib","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nilo‌tinib","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Aldesleukin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Aldesleukin</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>metastatic melanoma</strong>. The recombinant interleukin-2 (IL-2) preparation that is approved for use in metastatic melanoma is <strong>aldesleukin.</strong> It acts as an <strong>IL-2 agonist</strong> and is also approved for use in metastatic renal cell cancer.</p>\n<p><strong>Denileukin diftitox</strong> is an immunotoxin made from the recombinant combination of IL-2 and the catalytically active fragment of diphtheria toxin. It is approved for use in <strong>cutaneous T cell lymphoma.</strong></p>\n<p>Imatinib and nilotinib are a BCR/ABL tyrosine kinase inhibitor.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6499","text":"Denileukin diftitox, a dru‌g approved for the treatm‍ent of recurrent/refracto​ry cutaneous T-cell lymph‌omas, is an immunotoxin m​ade of:","raw_text":"Denileukin diftitox, a drug approved for the treatment of recurrent/refractory cutaneous T-cell lymphomas, is an immunotoxin made of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fusion protein of IL-2 and‌ pertussis toxin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fusion protein of IL-2 and‌ diptheria toxin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Monoclonal antibody agains‌t CD33","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Monoclonal antibody agains‌t CD30","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fusion protein of IL-2 and diptheria toxin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fusion protein of IL-2 and diptheria toxin</strong></p><p><strong>Denileukin diftitox</strong> is an <strong>immunotoxin</strong> made from the genetic recombination of <strong>IL-2</strong> and the catalytically active fragment of <strong>diphtheria toxin</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is indicated for advanced <strong>cutaneous T-cell lymphoma</strong> in patients with &gt;20% of T cells expressing the surface marker CD25. After binding to the IL-2 receptors, it is internalized by endocytosis. The active fragment of <strong>diphtheria toxin</strong> then is released into the cytosol, where it <strong>inhibits protein synthesis</strong> through ADP ribosylation, leading to <strong>cell death</strong>.</p>\n<p>Immunotoxins are proteins that contain a toxin along with an antibody that binds specifically to target cells.</p>\n<p><strong>Antibody-drug conjugates</strong> are complex molecules composed of an antibody linked to a biologically active cytotoxic (anticancer) drug. These antibody-targeted drug therapies are cell-specific to kill efficiently the target cells that bind and internalize the drug conjugate. Examples are:</p>\n<dl class=\"article-list\">\n<dt></dt>\n</dl>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gemtuzumab ozogamicin - </strong>Monoclonal antibody against CD33 linked to a semisynthetic derivative of calicheamicin, a potent antitumor antibiotic. It was approved for the treatment of acute myeloid leukemia.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Brentuximab vedotin - </strong>Anti-CD30 IgG1 monoclonal antibody linked with the microtubule-disrupting agent MMAE. It is used for the treatment of Hodgkin and anaplastic lymphoma.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Ado-trastuzumab emtansine - </strong>It<strong> </strong>combines the HER2-targeted properties of trastuzumab with the antimicrotubule agent DM1 (derived from maytansine), allowing preferential intracellular drug delivery to HER2+ cells. It is used in the treatment of HER+ breast cancer.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"oUxn_pqw","path":["Pharmacology"]},{"id":"68_pericardial_diseases","title":"68.Pericardial Diseases","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MA7810","text":"What is the defined durati‍on for the diagnosis of a​cute pericarditis?","raw_text":"What is the defined duration for the diagnosis of acute pericarditis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt;2‍ weeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt;4‍ weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt;6‍ weeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt;1‍ week","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. <6 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. &lt;6 weeks</strong></p><p>Clinically, <strong>acute pericarditis</strong> is of <strong>&lt;6 weeks</strong> duration.</p>\n<p><strong>Pericarditis</strong> is the <strong>inflammation of the pericardial sac</strong>. This inflammation can lead to excessive secretion of the pericardial fluid leading to pericardial effusion. When the accumulation of fluid causes obstruction to the inflow of blood into the ventricles, it is called cardiac tamponade.</p>\n<p>Clinically, pericarditis is classified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute - &lt; 6 weeks</li>\n<li>Subacute - 6 weeks to 6 months</li>\n<li>Chronic - &gt; 6 months</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7906","text":"A 30-year-old man presente‍d with low-grade fever an​d severe retrosternal che‌st pain for 1 week. The p‌ain got exaggerated on ly​ing down and relieved on ‌sitting up. On auscultati‌on of the precordium, a g‍rating sound was audible.​ Which of the given state‌ments is false regarding ‍this auscultatory finding‍?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presented with low-grade fever and severe retrosternal chest pain for 1 week. The pain got exaggerated on lying down and relieved on sitting up. On auscultation of the precordium, a grating sound was audible. Which of the given statements is false regarding this auscultatory finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pathognomonic of acute per‍icarditis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A very high ‍pitched sound","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Best heard with the patien‍t in left lateral positio​n","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Loudest at the lower left ‍sternal border","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Best heard with the patient in left lateral position","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Best heard with the patient in left lateral position</strong></p><p>In this patient, Low-grade fever, chest pain that got relieved on leaning forward, and characteristic auscultatory findings are suggestive of <strong>acute</strong> <strong>pericarditis</strong>. The grating sound heard on auscultation is a <strong>pericardial friction rub. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Features</strong> of the pericardial rub:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Superficial scratchy/leathery/grating sound<strong> </strong>caused due to friction between the visceral and parietal pericardium</li>\n<li>Best heard at end-expiration with the patient <strong>sitting </strong>and<strong> leaning forward</strong></li>\n<li>Very high-pitched sound best heard with the diaphragm of the stethoscope, at the <strong>lower left sternal border</strong></li>\n<li>Consists of <strong>three components</strong> corresponding to atrial systole, ventricular systole, and early ventricular diastole</li>\n<li><strong>Pathognomonic</strong> of acute pericarditis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7904","text":"A young man presents to th‍e medicine OPD with dyspn​ea, cough, and severe che‌st pain for 1 week. Chest‌ pain is pleuritic in nat​ure. On examination, the ‌apical impulse is not pal‌pable and breath sounds a‍re normal. Which of the f​ollowing is likely to be ‌seen in the ECG of this p‍atient?","raw_text":"A young man presents to the medicine OPD with dyspnea, cough, and severe chest pain for 1 week. Chest pain is pleuritic in nature. On examination, the apical impulse is not palpable and breath sounds are normal. Which of the following is likely to be seen in the ECG of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ST-segment elevations with‍ a convex upward configur​ation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PR-segment depressions ear‍ly in the disease progres​sion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Reciprocal ST-segment depr‍essions in all correspond​ing leads","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T-wave inversions developi‍ng concurrently with ST-s​egment elevations","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. PR-segment depressions early in the disease progression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db0b2df12dab4df3b27714c035ad8ad7x600x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6a9a7602309d4ab49a10346bb2522ce6x1280x689.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. PR-segment depressions early in the disease progression</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute pericarditis.</strong> <strong>PR-segment depressions</strong> are seen early in acute pericarditis.</p>\n<p>The ECG changes in acute pericarditis evolve through 4 stages -</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stage 1 - <strong>Generalized ST-segment elevation</strong> with <strong>upward concavity</strong> with reciprocal depressions only in aVR and V1 and PR segment depression. </li>\n<li>Stage 2 - The ST segments return to normal</li>\n<li>Stage 3 - Diffuse <strong>T-wave inversion</strong></li>\n<li>Stage 4 - ECG returns to normal in weeks or months</li>\n</ul>\n<p>ECG in acute pericarditis vs acute MI:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"150\"> </td>\n<td width=\"150\">\n<p><strong>Acute pericarditis</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"150\">\n<p><strong>Acute MI</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"150\">\n<p>Distribution of ST-segment elevations</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"150\">\n<p>More generalized</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"150\">\n<p>Limited to anterior or inferior leads because of the localized area of infarct</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"150\">\n<p>Morphology  of ST-segment elevations</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"150\">\n<p>Concave upwards</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"150\">\n<p>Convex upwards</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"150\">\n<p>Reciprocal ST-segment depressions in the opposite leads</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"150\">\n<p>Not present</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"150\">\n<p>Typically present</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"150\">\n<p>T-wave inversions</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"150\">\n<p>Only after ST-segment returns to baseline</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"150\">\n<p>T wave inversions can be seen even before the ST-segment returns to baseline</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"150\">\n<p>PR-segment</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"150\">\n<p>Depressed (or elevated in lead aVR) in 75% to 80% of patients</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"150\">\n<p>Usually not depressed (can be depressed only if concomitant atrial infarction is present).</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"150\">\n<p>Pathological Q-waves</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"150\">\n<p>Not seen</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"150\">\n<p>May be seen</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Cardiac biomarkers are grossly elevated in acute-MI, whereas in acute pericarditis the elevation if present is only mild and indicative of epi-myocarditis causing myocyte necrosis.</p>\n<p>The ECG below shows acute pericarditis causing diffuse ST-segment elevations in leads I, II, aVF, and V2 to V6, with reciprocal ST depressions in lead aVR.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The image below shows ECG with ST-segment elevation in Leads II, III, and aVF for an inferior acute myocardial infarction (AMI).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7813","text":"Auscultation of a patient ‍with pleuritic chest pain​ revealed a patch of dull‌ness with increased vocal‌ fremitus beneath the ang​le of the left scapula. W‌hat is this sign called?","raw_text":"Auscultation of a patient with pleuritic chest pain revealed a patch of dullness with increased vocal fremitus beneath the angle of the left scapula. What is this sign called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Auenbrugg‍er’s sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Moschcowi‍tz’s sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dressle‍r’s sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ewart’‍s sign","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ewart’s sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ewart’s sign</strong></p><p>A patch of dullness with increased vocal fremitus beneath the angle of the left scapula due to the compression of the base of the lung by the pericardial fluid is called <strong>Ewart’s sign. </strong>It is seen in <strong>pericardial effusion.</strong></p>\n<p>Acute pericarditis is frequently associated with pericardial effusion.</p>\n<p>Option A: Auenbrugger’s sign is the bulging of the epigastrium in severe pericardial effusion.</p>\n<p>Option B: Moschcowitz’s sign is the abrupt transition in percussion from the lungs to the right heart border seen in pericardial effusion.</p>\n<p>Option C: Dressler’s sign is the dullness to percussion of the lower half of the sternum seen in pericardial effusion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7817","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a component of Beck’s​ triad?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a component of Beck’s triad?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulsus p‍aradoxus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypot‍ension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Muffled he‍art sounds","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Raise‍d JVP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pulsus paradoxus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1d5f18fba5084819a91ce8c67bebfeb8x379x339.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pulsus paradoxus</strong></p><p>Pulsus paradoxus is not part of Beck’s triad. Pulsus paradoxus is seen in cardiac tamponade. However, it is not a part of Beck's triad.</p>\n<p><strong>Beck’s triad </strong>is seen in <strong>cardiac tamponade</strong> and it includes: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypotension</li>\n<li>Soft or absent heart sounds</li>\n<li>Jugular venous distension</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cardiac tamponade is the accumulation of fluid in the pericardial space significant enough to cause an obstruction to the inflow of blood to the ventricles. This reduces cardiac output and arterial pressure causing hypotension. </p>\n<p>This also causes a greater than the normal reduction in the systolic arterial blood pressure producing <strong>pulsus paradoxus.</strong> Pulsus paradoxus refers to the <strong>fall in systolic blood pressure &gt;10 mmHg</strong> with <strong>inspiration.</strong></p>\n<p>Other causes of pulsus paradoxus include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Massive pulmonary embolism</li>\n<li>Tension pneumothorax</li>\n<li>Severe obstructive lung disease</li>\n<li>Hemorrhagic shock</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The echocardiography clip below shows the <strong>swinging heart sign</strong>, which is characteristic of cardiac tamponade.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7092","text":"Which of the following is ‍a differentiating feature​ between cardiac tamponad‌e and tension pneumothora‌x?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a differentiating feature between cardiac tamponade and tension pneumothorax?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Breath‍ sounds","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Muffled he‍art sounds","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased ‍heart rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Raise‍d JVP","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Breath sounds","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Breath sounds</strong></p><p>The differentiating feature between cardiac tamponade and tension pneumothorax is breath sounds. The <strong>breath sounds</strong> are <strong>absent</strong> in <strong>tension pneumothorax</strong> while they are <strong>present in cardiac tamponade.</strong></p>\n<p>The presence of <strong>hypotension</strong>, d<strong>istended jugular veins, </strong>and <strong>muffled heart sounds (Beck's triad)</strong> suggest a <strong>cardiac tamponade.</strong> These features can also be seen in <strong>tension pneumothorax. </strong>Other clinical features pointing towards tension pneumothorax are <strong>absent breath sounds, hyper resonance to percussion, </strong>and <strong>shift</strong> of the <strong>mediastinum</strong> to the contralateral side. </p>\n<p>Heart sounds can be muffled by structures between the stethoscope and the heart, eg, adipose tissue deposition (obesity), fluid accumulation (pericardial effusion, pleural effusion), gas accumulation (pneumothorax, subcutaneous emphysema), soft tissue masses can lead to attenuation of heart sounds. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7036","text":"An intern is asked to meas‍ure the blood pressure of​ a patient with cardiac t‌amponade. He/she should a‌sk the patient to:","raw_text":"An intern is asked to measure the blood pressure of a patient with cardiac tamponade. He/she should ask the patient to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hold ‍breath","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Take rapid shallow breaths‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Take long d‍eep breaths","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Breathe ‍normally","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Breathe normally","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Breathe normally</strong></p><p>The patient with<strong> cardiac tamponade</strong> is asked to breathe normally to elicit <strong>pulsus paradoxus.</strong></p>\n<p>In a patient with cardiac tamponade, both ventricles share an incompressible pericardial covering due to the fluid. As the right ventricle enlarges during inspiration, the interventricular septum bulges into the left ventricle, reducing the left ventricular volume.</p>\n<p>This results in <strong>reduced stroke volume</strong> and hence <strong>reduced systolic arterial pressure. </strong><br/>As a result, there is a<strong> greater than normal (more than 10 mmHg)</strong> <strong>fall</strong> in <strong>systolic</strong> arterial blood pressure during inspiration, producing pulsus paradoxus.</p>\n<p>This cannot be elicited if the patient is holding his/her breath, taking long or shallow breaths.</p>\n<p>Pulsus paradoxus is also seen with<strong> effusive constrictive pericarditis, restrictive cardiomyopathy</strong> and <strong>right ventricular myocardial infarction.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7837","text":"An RTA victim is brought w‍ith breathing difficulty.​ On examination, the foll‌owing is noted. Which of ‌the following JVP finding​s is most likely to be se‌en in this patient? BP- 8‌0/58mmHg Pulse- feeble JV‍P- elevated Heart sounds-​ muffled","raw_text":"<p>An RTA victim is brought with breathing difficulty. On examination, the following is noted. Which of the following JVP findings is most likely to be seen in this patient?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>BP- 80/58mmHg</li>\n<li>Pulse- feeble</li>\n<li>JVP- elevated</li>\n<li>Heart sounds- muffled</li>\n</ul>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Absent x and prominent y d‍escent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prominent x and absent y d‍escent","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prominent x and y descents‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Absent x an‍d y descents","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Prominent x and absent y descent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5cc0b1cac4434af9b54964b55146d5c8x603x708.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Prominent x and absent y descent</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with hypotension, muffled heart sounds, and elevated JVP <strong>(</strong><strong>Beck's triad) </strong>suggest a diagnosis of <strong>cardiac tamponade. </strong>The JVP finding most likely to be seen is <strong>prominent x descent </strong>and<strong> absent y descent.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Note: JVP in constrictive pericarditis is characterized by prominent x and y descents.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7842","text":"A patient with dizziness a‍nd breathing difficulty c​ame to the emergency depa‌rtment. The ECG is given ‌below. What is the most p​robable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient with dizziness and breathing difficulty came to the emergency department. The ECG is given below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute per‍icarditis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Constrictive‍ pericarditis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cardiac ‍tamponade","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cardiac tamponade","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/977add71bc7e47329d0737fde52a71dd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c6c060940e3403eb77355813df0c89ax1280x1090.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cardiac tamponade</strong></p><p>The ECG shows <strong>electrical alternans </strong>with<strong> sinus tachycardia. </strong>This along with the patient's symptoms<strong> </strong>suggests a likely diagnosis of<strong> cardiac tamponade.</strong></p>\n<p>Electrical alternans is the beat-to-beat alternation in the ECG waveform. In cardiac tamponade, this is caused by the periodic swinging motion of the heart in a large pericardial effusion.</p>\n<p></p>\n<table style=\"height: 362px; width: 556px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 67px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 264px; height: 67px;\">Feature </td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 67px;\"><strong>Cardiac tamponade</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 67px;\"><strong>Constrictive pericarditis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 67px;\"><strong>Restrictive cardiomyopathy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 49px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 264px; height: 49px;\"> Pulsus paradoxus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 49px; text-align: center;\">+++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 49px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 49px; text-align: center;\">+/- (Rare)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 44px; height: 25px;\">\n<p>JVP</p>\n </td>\n<td style=\"width: 220px; height: 25px;\">\n<p>Prominent x descent</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 25px; text-align: center;\">+++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 25px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 25px; text-align: center;\">+++</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 220px; height: 38px;\">Prominent y descent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\">++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 220px; height: 38px;\">Kussmaul's sign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\">- </td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\">+/- (Rare)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 264px; height: 50px;\">Pericardial knock</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 264px; height: 50px;\">Third heart sound</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 264px; height: 50px;\">Electrical alternans</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 264px; height: 50px;\">Thickened and calcified pericardium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">+++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 132px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7905","text":"A 25-year-old road traffic‍ accident victim presents​ with chest pain and brea‌thlessness. On examinatio‌n, his BP was 80/60mmHg.O​n inspection, his neck ve‌ins were distended and on‌ auscultation, his heart ‍sounds were muffled. Whic​h of the following clinic‌al features is likely to ‍be seen in this patient?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old road traffic accident victim presents with chest pain and breathlessness. On examination, his BP was 80/60mmHg.On inspection, his neck veins were distended and on auscultation, his heart sounds were muffled. Which of the following clinical features is likely to be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pericard‍ial knock","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kussmau‍l's sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Third he‍art sound","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulsus p‍aradoxus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pulsus paradoxus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pulsus paradoxus</strong></p><p>The symptoms and signs suggest a diagnosis of <strong>cardiac tamponade</strong>. Out of the given features, <strong>p</strong><strong>ulsus</strong> <strong>paradoxus</strong> is seen in cardiac tamponade.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 362px; width: 556px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 67px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 153.359px; height: 67px;\">Feature </td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.188px; height: 67px;\"><strong>Cardiac tamponade</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.945px; height: 67px;\"><strong>Constrictive pericarditis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.523px; height: 67px;\"><strong>Restrictive cardiomyopathy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 49px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 153.359px; height: 49px;\"> Pulsus paradoxus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.188px; height: 49px; text-align: center;\">+++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.945px; height: 49px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.523px; height: 49px; text-align: center;\">+/- (Rare)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 32.8984px; height: 25px;\">\n<p>JVP</p>\n </td>\n<td style=\"width: 120.461px; height: 25px;\">\n<p>Prominent x descent</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.188px; height: 25px; text-align: center;\">+++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.945px; height: 25px; text-align: center;\">++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.523px; height: 25px; text-align: center;\">+++</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 120.461px; height: 38px;\">Prominent y descent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.188px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.945px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\">++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.523px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 120.461px; height: 38px;\">Kussmaul's sign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.188px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\">- </td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.945px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\">+++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.523px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\">+/- (Rare)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 153.359px; height: 50px;\">Pericardial knock</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.188px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.945px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.523px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 153.359px; height: 50px;\">Third heart sound</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.188px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.945px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.523px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 153.359px; height: 50px;\">Electrical alternans</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.188px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.945px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.523px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 153.359px; height: 50px;\">Thickened and calcified pericardium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.188px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104.945px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">+++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 127.523px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1785","text":"A young motorist sustained‍ injuries in a road traff​ic accident. He was found‌ to have fractures of mul‌tiple ribs anteriorly on ​both sides. Radial and fe‌moral fractures were also‌ present. On examination,‍ the following was noted.​ What should be the immed‌iate intervention? Blood ‍pressure- 80/60 mmHg Hear‍t rate- 140/min Respirato‌ry rate- tachypnea Genera‍l state: agitated and res​tless Jugular veins- dist​ended Respiratory system-‌ B/L equal and adequate a‍ir entry CVS- heart sound​s barely audible. Femoral​ pulse weakly palpable. D‌istal pulses not felt","raw_text":"<p>A young motorist sustained injuries in a road traffic accident. He was found to have fractures of multiple ribs anteriorly on both sides. Radial and femoral fractures were also present. On examination, the following was noted.  What should be the immediate intervention?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blood pressure- 80/60 mmHg</li>\n<li>Heart rate- 140/min </li>\n<li>Respiratory rate- tachypnea</li>\n<li>General state: agitated and restless</li>\n<li>Jugular veins- distended</li>\n<li>Respiratory system- B/L equal and adequate air entry</li>\n<li>CVS- heart sounds barely audible. Femoral pulse weakly palpable. Distal pulses not felt</li>\n</ul>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rapid blood‍ transfusion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Urgent peri‍cardial tap","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Intercostal tube drainage ‍on both sides","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Immediate repair of femora‍l artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Urgent pericardial tap","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Urgent pericardial tap</strong></p><p>The presence of hypotension, distended jugular veins, and absence of heart sounds (<strong>Beck's triad</strong>)<strong> </strong>are suggestive of <strong>cardiac tamponade.</strong> An <strong>urgent pericardial tap</strong> needs to be done to relieve the tension on the heart chambers.</p>\n<p>Although these features could be seen in tension pneumothorax as well, the presence of adequate bilateral air entry excludes this diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Pericardiocentesis, also known as pericardial tap, can be performed with sub-xiphoid, parasternal, or apical approaches. The <strong>most common approach </strong>is a <strong>sub-xiphoid approach</strong> under echocardiographic guidance.</p>\n<p>Option A: Rapid blood transfusion is needed in massive blood loss indicated by hypotension. Although this patient presents with hypotension, it is most likely a result of cardiac tamponade. Although blood transfusion is indicated, it will be effective only when the pericardial tap decompresses the pericardium.</p>\n<p>Option C: Intercostal tube drainage is done in tension pneumothorax. The presence of adequate air entry bilaterally excludes this diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Option D: The fixation of femur fracture and femoral artery is not a priority here. The inability to palpate pulses distal to the fracture could be a result of hypotension. This needs to be explored only if the patient fails to improve after the pericardial tap and blood transfusion.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1792","text":"You're an intern examining‍ a patient in the ICU. Wh​ile assessing the JVP, yo‌u notice a lack of fall d‌uring inspiration. Which ​of the following conditio‌ns would you not consider‌ as a differential diagno‍sis?","raw_text":"You're an intern examining a patient in the ICU. While assessing the JVP, you notice a lack of fall during inspiration. Which of the following conditions would you not consider as a differential diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardiac ‍tamponade","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Constrictive‍ pericarditis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Right ventri‍cular infarct","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cardiac tamponade","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cardiac tamponade</strong></p><p>A <strong>rise </strong>or<strong> a lack of fall in JVP </strong>with<strong> inspiration </strong>is known as<strong> Kussmaul's sign. </strong>It is not seen in cardiac tamponade.</p>\n<p><strong>Normally,</strong> in <strong>inspiration</strong> due to the negative intrathoracic pressure and increased intrathoracic volume, the capacity of the right heart increases and the <strong>JVP falls</strong>. In conditions where the <strong>myocardium</strong> or <strong>pericardium</strong> is <strong>poorly compliant</strong>, it does not allow the right heart chambers to accommodate more blood and this fall is not seen. In fact, due to an increase in venous return during inspiration, the <strong>JVP might rise</strong>. This is called Kussmaul's sign.</p>\n<p>This is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Restrictive cardiomyopathy</li>\n<li>Constrictive pericarditis</li>\n<li>Massive pulmonary embolism</li>\n<li>Right ventricular infarcts</li>\n<li>Advanced cases of left ventricular systolic failure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Kussmaul’s sign is not seen in patients with cardiac tamponade because even though the increase in pericardial pressure exerts an inward force compressing the entire heart during inspiration, the increase in negative intrathoracic pressure is still transmitted to the right side of the heart. This allows for an increase in blood flow to the right atrium. Therefore, the JVP falls as seen in normal conditions.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 362px; width: 493px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 67px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 264px; height: 67px;\">Feature </td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.40625px; height: 67px;\"><strong>Cardiac tamponade</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.59375px; height: 67px;\"><strong>Constrictive pericarditis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px; height: 67px;\"><strong>Restrictive cardiomyopathy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 49px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 264px; height: 49px;\"> Pulsus paradoxus</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.40625px; height: 49px; text-align: center;\">+++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.59375px; height: 49px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px; height: 49px; text-align: center;\">+/- (Rare)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 44px; height: 25px;\">\n<p>JVP</p>\n </td>\n<td style=\"width: 220px; height: 25px;\">\n<p>Prominent x descent</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.40625px; height: 25px; text-align: center;\">+++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.59375px; height: 25px; text-align: center;\">++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px; height: 25px; text-align: center;\">+++</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 220px; height: 38px;\">Prominent y descent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.40625px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.59375px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\">++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 220px; height: 38px;\">Kussmaul's sign</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.40625px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\">- </td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.59375px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\">+++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px; height: 38px; text-align: center;\">   +/- (Rare)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 264px; height: 50px;\">Pericardial knock</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.40625px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.59375px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 264px; height: 50px;\">Third heart sound</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.40625px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.59375px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 264px; height: 50px;\">Electrical alternans</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.40625px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.59375px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 264px; height: 50px;\">Thickened and calcified pericardium</td>\n<td style=\"width: 102.40625px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.59375px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">+++</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px; height: 50px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3074","text":"A 20-year-old man presents‍ to the emergency departm​ent with respiratory dist‌ress and hypotension foll‌owing a trauma. He has su​bcutaneous emphysema and ‌there is an absence of ai‌r entry into the right si‍de of the lungs. What is ​the next best step?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old man presents to the emergency department with respiratory distress and hypotension following a trauma. He has subcutaneous emphysema and there is an absence of air entry into the right side of the lungs. What is the next best step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Start IV fluids with large‍ bore cannula","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Needle decompression into ‍the 5th intercostal space​ anterior to the midaxill‌ary line","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Take the patient to the IC‍U and intubate him","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Start positive pressure ve‍ntilation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Needle decompression into the 5th intercostal space anterior to the midaxillary line","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db0e19a8a4d34eb8893c5d98cd661e1dx376x360.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Needle decompression into the 5th intercostal space anterior to the midaxillary line</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of a young male developing respiratory distress with hypotension, subcutaneous emphysema, and unilaterally absent air entry is suggestive of<strong> tension pneumothorax.</strong> The immediate next step, in this case, would be <strong>needle decompression</strong> at the <strong>fifth intercostal space</strong> anterior to the <strong>mid-axillary line </strong>(as the patient is an adult). </p>\n<p>In <strong>children</strong>, the<strong> second intercostal space </strong>in the midclavicular line is chosen as the site for needle decompression.</p>\n<p><strong>Needle decompression</strong> turns a tension pneumothorax into an <strong>open pneumothorax</strong>. It has to be followed by<strong> tube thoracostomy.</strong> An <strong>intercostal chest drain (ICD) tube</strong> is inserted in the triangle of safety, just anterior to the midaxillary line on the side of the lung injury. The site of insertion is the same in both children and adults.</p>\n<p>The <strong>triangle of safety</strong> is bounded by the lateral border of the pectoralis major anteriorly, the anterior border of the latissimus dorsi posteriorly, and the fifth intercostal space inferiorly, with the apex below the axilla. This provides a safe landmark for the insertion of an ICD with minimal risk of tissue injury. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Tension pneumothorax </strong>is a condition where air accumulates in the pleural space due to a one-way valve air leak, leading to the collapse of the affected lung. This can cause a <strong>mediastinal shift, </strong>compression of the opposite lung, and decreased venous return, resulting in <strong>obstructive shock</strong>. It commonly occurs during <strong>positive-pressure mechanical ventilation, </strong>but can also happen after chest trauma, subclavian/internal jugular venous catheter insertion, or chest wall defects. The diagnosis is clinical, and immediate treatment is necessary without waiting for radiological confirmation.</p>\n<p>Tension pneumothorax is characterized by chest pain, tachypnoea, tachycardia, hypotension, tracheal deviation away from the side of injury, absent breath sounds on the affected side, cyanosis, and neck vein distention. Patients can also develop <strong>subcutaneous emphysema</strong> as a complication secondary to the pneumothorax.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"xhoQr61m","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"68_infective___vascular_cns_pathology","title":"68.Infective & Vascular Cns Pathology","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MA2971","text":"Which cells constitute the‌ resident monocyte-lineag‌e population of the CNS a‍nd accumulate in response‌ to injury?","raw_text":"Which cells constitute the resident monocyte-lineage population of the CNS and accumulate in response to injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oligoden‌drocytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Microgli‌al cells","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Astro‌cytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stella‌te cell","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Microglial cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Microglial cells</strong></p><p><strong>Microglia</strong> are mesoderm-derived<strong> phagocytic cells</strong> that serve as the <strong>resident monocyte-lineage</strong> population of the CNS and accumulate in response to injury.</p>\n<p>They respond to injury by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Activation and proliferating</li>\n<li>Developing elongated nuclei (<strong>rod cells</strong>), as in neurosyphilis</li>\n<li>Forming aggregates around small foci of tissue necrosis (microglial nodules)</li>\n<li>Congregating around cell bodies of dying neurons (neuronophagia)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2965","text":"Which of these are the ear‌liest morphologic markers‌ of neuronal cell death?","raw_text":"Which of these are the earliest morphologic markers of neuronal cell death?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"White ‌neurons","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Red n‌eurons","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Gli‌osis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Axonal ‌reaction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Red neurons","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Red neurons</strong></p><p><strong>Red neurons</strong> are the earliest morphologic markers of neuronal cell death.</p>\n<p>Acute neuronal injury refers to a spectrum of changes that accompany acute CNS hypoxia / ischemia or other acute insults and reflect the<strong> earliest</strong> morphologic markers of neuronal cell death. They are evident by<strong> about 12 to 24 hours</strong> after an irreversible insult.</p>\n<p>Morphologic features consist of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Shrinkage of the cell body</li>\n<li><strong>Pyknosis</strong> of the nucleus</li>\n<li>Disappearance of the nucleolus</li>\n<li>Loss of Nissl substance</li>\n<li>Intense eosinophilia of the cytoplasm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Stages of neuronal injury and their pathological features are given below:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 135px; width: 402px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 91px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Stages</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Time</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Features </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 91px; text-align: center;\">Early change</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px; text-align: center;\">12–24 hr</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Red neuron</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 91px; text-align: center;\">Subacute change</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px; text-align: center;\">24 hr–2 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; text-align: center;\">Necrosis, macrophages, vascular proliferation, gliosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 91px; text-align: center;\">Repair</td>\n<td style=\"width: 88px; text-align: center;\">2 weeks +</td>\n<td style=\"width: 215px; text-align: center;\">Removal of necrosis, gliosis completed, loss of CNS architecture</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6814","text":"A 45-year-old male who is ‌a known diabetic and hype‌rtensive suffers a stroke‍. During this episode, wh‌ich of the following cell​s are the least susceptib​le to injury, considering‌ the stroke was transient​ and not severe?​","raw_text":"A 45-year-old male who is a known diabetic and hypertensive suffers a stroke. During this episode, which of the following cells are the least susceptible to injury, considering the stroke was transient and not severe?​","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Watershed zones of cerebra‌l cortex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pyramidal cells of hippoca‌mpus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Purkinje cells of cerebell‌um","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Astrocytes of cerebral cor‌tex","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Astrocytes of cerebral cortex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Astrocytes of cerebral cortex</strong></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><strong>Astrocytes</strong> are more <strong>resistant</strong> to focal cerebral <strong>ischemia</strong> than neurons, and die by delayed necrosis. </p>\n<div dir=\"ltr\">\n<p> The cells <strong>most susceptible</strong> to <strong>ischemia</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pyramidal cell layer cells of the hippocampus (espe­cially area CA1, also referred to as Sommer sector)</li>\n<li>Purkinje cells of the cerebellum</li>\n<li>Pyramidal neurons of the cerebral cortex(the watershed zones)</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2967","text":"A 50 year old man died of ‌an unknown condition. On ‌autopsy, a sample of the ‍brain was taken as a spec‌imen, and the following f​ibres were seen on micros​copic examination. These ‌fibres are not seen in wh​ich of the following cond‌itions?","raw_text":"A 50 year old man died of an unknown condition. On autopsy, a sample of the brain was taken as a specimen, and the following fibres were seen on microscopic examination.\nThese fibres are not seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alexande‌r disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pilocytic ‌astrocytoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pick d‌isease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Longstandi‌ng gliosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pick disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/89718cdc76004fe2933f1de8e14df3bb.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pick disease</strong></p><p>The image shows the presence of <strong>rosenthal fibres</strong>. Rosenthal fibres are not seen in Pick's disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Rosenthal fibers</strong> are thick, elongated, brightly <strong>eosinophilic</strong>, irregular structures that occur within astrocytic processes. They contain <strong>two heat­shock proteins </strong>(αB ­crystallin and HSP27) as well as ubiquitin.They are typically found in regions of<strong> longstanding gliosis </strong>and are characteristic of a type of<strong> glial tumor</strong>—pilocytic astrocytoma.</p>\n<p><strong>Alexander disease</strong> is a <strong>leukodystrophy</strong> associated with mutations in the gene encoding GFAP. In this condition, abundant rosenthal fibers are found in periventricular, perivascular, and subpial locations.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2974","text":"Hemorrhage of which of the‌ following areas of the b‌rain are called ganglioni‍c hemorrhages?","raw_text":"Hemorrhage of which of the following areas of the brain are called ganglionic hemorrhages?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Basal ganglia and cerebral‌ hemisphere","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thalamus‌ and pons","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Basal ganglia and thalamus‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Basal gan‌glia only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Basal ganglia and thalamus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Basal ganglia and thalamus</strong></p><p>Hemorrhages in the basal ganglia and thalamus are commonly designated <strong>ganglionic hemorrhages.</strong> Those that occur in the lobes of the cerebral hemispheres are called <strong>lobar hemorrhages.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2975","text":"A patient is rushed into t‌he ER with complaints of ‌sudden excruciating heada‍che and projectile vomiti‌ng. CT angiography shows ​the presence of Charcot-B​ouchard microaneurysms. T‌hese are caused by which ​of the following?","raw_text":"A patient is rushed into the ER with complaints of sudden excruciating headache and projectile vomiting. CT angiography shows the presence of Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms. These are caused by which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conge‌nital","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypert‌ension","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Atheros‌clerosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tra‌uma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypertension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypertension</strong></p><p><strong>Charcot–Bouchard microaneurysms </strong>are caused by <strong>chronic hypertension. </strong>They are most commonly seen within the <strong>basal ganglia.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2976","text":"A 66-year-old man with chr‌onic hypertension present‌s to the ER with altered ‍mental status and elevate‌d blood pressure of 210/1​10 mm Hg. You suspect int​racranial hemorrhage. Wha‌t is the most common site​ of hemorrhage seen in su‌ch cases?","raw_text":"A 66-year-old man with chronic hypertension presents to the ER with altered mental status and elevated blood pressure of 210/110 mm Hg. You suspect intracranial hemorrhage. What is the most common site of hemorrhage seen in such cases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Put‌amen","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thal‌amus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Po‌ns","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cereb‌ellum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Putamen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Putamen</strong></p><p>The given clinical condition is suggestive of <strong>hypertensive intraparenchymal hemorrhage.</strong> The <strong>most common</strong> site (50-60%) of hypertensive hemorrhage in the brain is the<strong> putamen.</strong></p>\n<p>Hypertension is the most common cause of intracerebral hemorrhage.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2981","text":"A patient with a known his‌tory of autosomal dominan‌t polycystic kidney disea‍se, presents to the ER wi‌th a sudden, severe heada​che, photophobia and naus​ea. What type of aneurysm‌ would you suspect in thi​s patient?","raw_text":"A patient with a known history of autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease, presents to the ER with a sudden, severe headache, photophobia and nausea. What type of aneurysm would you suspect in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sacc‌ular","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Fusi‌form","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myc‌otic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disse‌cting","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Saccular","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Saccular</strong></p><p>In a patient with a known history of autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease, the most common type of intracranial aneurysm is <strong>saccular</strong> <strong>aneurysm</strong>.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Although the majority occur <strong>sporadically</strong>, genetic factors may be important in their pathogenesis of saccular aneurysms. There is an increased incidence of saccular aneurysm in individuals with certain <strong>Mendelian disorders </strong>like <strong>autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease</strong>, Ehlers–Danlos syndrome type IV, neurofibromatosis type 1 [NF1], and Marfan syndrome, fibromuscular dysplasia of extracranial arteries, and coarctation of the aorta. Other predisposing factors include cigarette smoking and hypertension.</p>\n<p>The most frequent cause of clinically significant subarachnoid hemorrhage is rupture of a saccular (berry) aneurysm in a cerebral artery.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2979","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is not true about‌ CADASIL?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is not true about CADASIL?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It shows autosomal recessi‌ve inheritance.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is characterized by mut‌ations in the NOTCH3 gene‌.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It presents with subcortic‌al infarcts.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Recurrent strokes and deme‌ntia are seen.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It shows autosomal recessive inheritance.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It shows autosomal recessive inheritance.</strong></p><p>Cerebral autosomal dominant arteriopathy with subcortical infarcts and leukoencephalopathy (<strong>CADASIL</strong>) is an <strong>autosomal-dominant disorder </strong>caused by mutations in the<strong><em> NOTCH3</em> gene. </strong></p>\n<p>This leads to the misfolding of the extracellular domain of the NOTCH3 receptor that is preferentially expressed in vascular smooth muscle. It is clinically characterized by recurrent strokes (usually ischemic, less often hemorrhages) and dementia.</p>\n<p>Arteries in the CNS and in other tissues such as skin show <strong>concentric thickening </strong>of the<strong> media </strong>and<strong> adventitia, </strong>loss of smooth muscle cells, and the presence of basophilic, periodic acid Schiff <strong>(PAS)-positive deposits. </strong>Deposits contain the misfolded<strong> NOTCH3 protein. </strong>These appear as osmiophilic compact granular material on electron microscopy and are consistently detected in the walls of affected vessels. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2986","text":"A patient presented to the‌ emergency department wit‌h high fever, photophobia‍ and neck stiffness. A lu‌mbar puncture was perform​ed and findings are given​ below. Which of the foll‌owing is the most common ​cause of this condition? ‌CSF Parameters Observatio​n Cell count 1500 (predom‍inantly lymphocytes) Prot‍ein 100 mg/dl Glucose 40 ‌mg/dl Gram stain Negative‌","raw_text":"<p>A patient presented to the emergency department with high fever, photophobia and neck stiffness.  A lumbar puncture was performed and findings are given below. Which of the following is the most common cause of this condition?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 70px;\" width=\"262\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\"><strong>CSF Parameters</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\"> <strong>Observation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Cell count</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">1500 (predominantly lymphocytes)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Protein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">100 mg/dl</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Glucose</td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">40 mg/dl</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Gram stain </td>\n<td style=\"width: 123px;\">Negative</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enter‌ovirus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes simp‌lex 1 virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Herpes simp‌lex 2 virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"West ni‌le virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Enterovirus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Enterovirus</strong></p><p>The above clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>aseptic meningitis</strong>. <strong>Enterovirus </strong>is the most common cause, accounting for<strong> </strong>around 90% of the cases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2989","text":"A 34-year-old man is admit‌ted with a history of alt‌ered sensorium and abnorm‍al behavior. The patient ‌subsequently died and on ​autopsy, the following fi​ndings are seen in the Pu‌rkinje cells of the cereb​ellum. What are these cal‌led?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old man is admitted with a history of altered sensorium and abnormal behavior. The patient subsequently died and on autopsy, the following findings are seen in the Purkinje cells of the cerebellum. What are these called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Henderson-Patterson bodies‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Negri ‌bodies","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Owl-eye i‌nclusions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Guarnier‌i bodies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Negri bodies","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/50e8374cab4948bb99e830513336af8a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8ec40a9e94804c439422850861390e99x1280x1066.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Negri bodies</strong></p><p>The image shows characteristic <strong>Negri bodies</strong> found in the <strong>Purkinje</strong> cell of the <strong>cerebellum</strong>. These are the <strong>pathognomonic</strong> microscopic finding in rabies. This patient's clinical features of altered sensorium and abnormal behavior are suggestive of <strong>rabies.</strong></p>\n<p>Negri bodies are cytoplasmic, round to oval, eosinophilic inclusions that can be found in the pyramidal neurons of the hippocampus and Purkinje cells of the cerebellum. The rabies virus can be detected within Negri bodies by ultrastructural and immunohistochemical methods.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2990","text":"Progressive multifocal leu‌koencephalopathy is cause‌d by which of the followi‍ng?","raw_text":"Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy is caused by which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rab‌ies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mea‌sles","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"JC virus ‌infection","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"CMV in‌fection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. JC virus infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9661945342e74434b1cbcc8355f981efx1280x1256.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. JC virus infection</strong></p><p><strong>Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy </strong>(PML) is an encephalitis caused by the <strong>JC</strong> <strong>polyomavirus</strong>.</p>\n<p>The disease occurs almost exclusively in immunosuppressed individuals, most commonly among patients with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS). The most prominent symptoms are clumsiness, progressive weakness, and visual, speech, and personality changes.</p>\n<p>Since the virus preferentially infects oligodendrocytes, <strong>demyelin­ation</strong> is its principal pathologic effect. It affects the subcortical white matter, particularly that of the <strong>parietal</strong> and <strong>occipital</strong> lobes. On microscopic examination, enlarged <strong>oligodendrocyte nuclei</strong> containing glassy amphophilic <strong>viral inclusions</strong> are seen.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a brain biopsy of a progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy patient, showing oligodendrocytes with nuclear enlargement and glassy chromatin.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2993","text":"A 35-year-old immunocompro‌mised patient presented w‌ith fever, headache, and ‍neurological deficits. MR‌I showed multiple enhanci​ng lesions and a stereota​ctic biopsy of the lesion‌ was done. What is the mo​st likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old immunocompromised patient presented with fever, headache, and neurological deficits. MRI showed multiple enhancing lesions and a stereotactic biopsy of the lesion was done. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cerebral‌ malaria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cerebral to‌xoplasmosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Herpes simplex encepheliti‌ts","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Progressive multifocal leu‌koencephalopathy (PML)","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cerebral toxoplasmosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/327481d4ce1d44e8b9c34bb89f317f2a.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cerebral toxoplasmosis</strong></p><p>The above image shows lymphocytes, macrophages and eosinophils surrounding the toxoplasma <strong>bradyzoites encased in cysts.</strong> Along with the case scenario, the most likely diagnosis is<strong> cerebral toxoplasmosis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cerebral toxoplasmosis</strong> is an opportunistic infection commonly found in the setting of HIV-associated immunosuppression. Toxoplasmosis of the CNS produces brain abscesses, which are found most often in the cerebral cortex (near the grey-white junction) and deep gray nuclei.</p>\n<p>Lesions of<strong> acute </strong>cerebral toxoplasmosis<strong> </strong>exhibit central necrosis, petechial haemorrhages surrounded by acute and chronic inflammation, macrophage infiltration, and vascular proliferation. Both free tachyzoites and encysted bradyzoites may be found at the periphery of the necrotic foci. <strong>Chronic</strong> lesions consist of small cystic spaces containing scattered lipid and hemosiderin-laden macrophages that are surrounded by gliotic brain.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> </strong>In <strong>cerebral malaria</strong> caused by P. falciparum, brain vessels are plugged with parasitized red cells. Around the vessels, ring hemorrhages and small focal inflammatory reactions (called malarial or <strong>Dürck granulomata</strong>) are seen.</p>\n<p>Option C:<strong> Herpes simplex encephalitis</strong> is necrotizing and often hemorrhagic. <strong>Cowdry type A</strong> intranuclear viral inclusion bodies may be found in both neurons and glia.</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy </strong>(PML) is an encephalitis caused by the JC polyomavirus. The disease occurs almost exclusively in immunosuppressed individuals. The virus preferentially infects oligodendrocytes and hence <strong>demyelin­ation</strong> is its principal pathologic effect. The enlarged oligodendrocyte nuclei contain glassy amphophilic <strong>viral inclusions.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2988","text":"A 40-year-old man with bro‌nchiectasis presents with‌ progressive focal neurol‍ogic deficits and symptom‌s related to increased in​tracranial pressure. On l​umbar puncture, the CSF i‌s found to have a high wh​ite cell count and an inc‌reased protein concentrat​ion, but the glucose cont‍ent is normal. What is th‍e most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man with bronchiectasis presents with progressive focal neurologic deficits and symptoms related to increased intracranial pressure. On lumbar puncture, the CSF is found to have a high white cell count and an increased protein concentration, but the glucose content is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brain ‌abscess","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Viral me‌ningitis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neuros‌yphilis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lyme d‌isease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Brain abscess","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Brain abscess</strong></p><p><strong>Brain abscess</strong> is the most likely diagnosis in the above patient with progressive focal neurologic deficits and increased intracranial pressure.</p>\n<p><strong>Predisposing conditions for brain abcess:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute bacterial endocarditis, which may give rise to multiple brain abscesses.</li>\n<li>Congenital heart diseases with right to left shunt.</li>\n<li>Chronic pulmonary disease, as in <strong>bronchiectasis</strong>.</li>\n<li>Systemic disease with immunosuppression.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Organisms </strong>involved are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Streptococcus</em> spp. and <em>Staphylococcus </em>spp. (most common <strong>aerobic</strong> organisms)</li>\n<li><em>Bacteroides fragilis </em>and <em>Peptostreptococcus</em> spp.(most common <strong>anaerobic</strong> organisms).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Foci of organisms in brain abscess:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Direct implantation of organisms</li>\n<li>Local extension from adjacent foci (mastoiditis, paranasal sinusitis)</li>\n<li>Hematogenous spread (from primary foci of heart, lungs, bones of extremities, dental extraction)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>CSF analysis in brain abscess:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 217px;\" width=\"300\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\"><strong>Parameters</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\"><strong>Observation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">WBC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">Increased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">Proteins</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">Increased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">Glucose</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">Decreased (but it will be normal in a significant proportion of individuals)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: In Robbins and Cotran's Pathology of Diseases- edition 9 page no.1273, it is given that in a typical brain abscess patient, the glucose in the CSF will be normal.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6816","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect regarding the c‌ommon locations of CNS in‍fections?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding the common locations of CNS infections?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberculosis - basal part ‌of the brain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HSV - frontal and temporal‌ lobes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HIV - frontal and parietal‌ lobes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"CMV - chor‌oid plexus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. HIV - frontal and parietal lobes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. HIV - frontal and parietal lobes</strong></p><p><strong>HIV</strong> encephalopathy is seen in <strong>subcortical</strong> regions of the brain and not in frontal and parietal lobes. It is characterized by defects in <strong>short-term</strong> <strong>memory</strong> and <strong>executive function</strong>. It is also known as subcortical dementia.</p>\n<p><strong>CNS manifestations seen in AIDS:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aseptic meningitis</li>\n<li>HIV-associated neurocognitive disorder (HAND)\n<ul>\n<li>HIV encephalopathy</li>\n<li>AIDS dementia complex</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Myelopathy<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Pure sensory ataxia</li>\n<li>Paresthesia/dysesthesia</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Myopathy</li>\n<li>Peripheral neuropathy</li>\n<li>Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (Guillain-Barre syndrome)\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (CIDP)</li>\n<li>Mononeuritis multiplex</li>\n<li>Distal symmetric polyneuropathy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6819","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin‌g prion diseases?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding prion diseases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Creutzfeldt Jakob Disease ‌is most commonly sporadic‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spongiform neurons are see‌n in fatal familial insom‌nia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Startle myoclonus is seen ‌in Creutzfeldt Jakob Dise‌ase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The disease causing isofor‌m of prion protein is PrP‌C","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. The disease causing isoform of prion protein is PrPC","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The disease causing isoform of prion protein is PrPC</strong></p><p>The <strong>disease</strong> causing isoform of prion protein is<strong> PrPSc. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Prions</strong> are the <strong>proteinaceous</strong> infectious particle that lacks nucleic acid, composed entirely of alternatively folded proteins that undergo <strong>self</strong>-<strong>propagation. </strong>They bind to the <strong>normal</strong>, cellular isoform of the prion protein (<strong>PrPC</strong>) which is rich in α-helix, and stimulate conversion of PrPC into the <strong>disease</strong>-causing isoform <strong>PrPSc </strong>which contain less α-helix and a high amount of β-structure. β structured proteins are resistant to digestion by human cellular enzymes. Transmission of prion disease may be <strong>sporadic</strong>, familial or iatrogenic.</p>\n<p>This group of diseases include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Creutzfeldt­ Jakob disease</li>\n<li>Gerstmann­ Sträussler­ Scheinke syndrome</li>\n<li><strong>Fatal Familial Insomnia     </strong>                     </li>\n<li>Kuru disease                                 </li>\n<li>Scrapie in sheep and goats   </li>\n<li>Bovine spongiform en­cephalopathy in cows</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Most common</strong> prion disease in humans is <strong>CJD,</strong> which is most commonly <strong>sporadic</strong>. CJD is characterized by a constellation of dementia, <strong>myoclonus,</strong> and periodic electrical bursts in EEG.</p>\n<p>Autoclaving at 134 degree Celsius for 5 hours or treatment with 2 N NaOH for several hours is recommended for sterilization against prions (tip: 1234-5 for prions).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2991","text":"A 15 year old boy is broug‌ht to the ER with complai‌nts of seizures, spastici‍ty of limbs and cognitive‌ decline since two weeks.​ He has no history of rec​ent illness, but mentions‌ suffering from a high fe​ver with a rash 5 years a‌go. What condition had he​ likely suffered from?","raw_text":"A 15 year old boy is brought to the ER with complaints of seizures, spasticity of limbs and cognitive decline since two weeks. He has no history of recent illness, but mentions suffering from a high fever with a rash 5 years ago. What condition had he likely suffered from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mea‌sles","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chick‌en pox","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"HSV in‌fection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Scarle‌t fever","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Measles","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Measles</strong></p><p>The patient is most likely suffering from<strong> subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE), </strong>a delayed late complication of<strong> measles.</strong></p>\n<p>Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE) is a rare, progressive clinical syndrome characterized by <strong>cognitive decline</strong>, <strong>spasticity of limbs</strong>, <strong>seizures </strong>and psychosis. It occurs in children or young adults, months or years after an initial, early­-age acute infection with measles.</p>\n<p>The pathogenesis of SSPE is not well understood but may involve <strong>persistent infection</strong> with a genetic variant of measles virus within the <strong>central</strong> <strong>nervous system</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7335","text":"Berry aneurysm occurs due ‌to:","raw_text":"Berry aneurysm occurs due to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endothelial injury of vess‌el due to HTN","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arterial internal elastic ‌lamina defect","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Endothelial ‌layer defect","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adventit‌ia defect","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Arterial internal elastic lamina defect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Arterial internal elastic lamina defect</strong></p><p>Berry aneurysm occurs due an <strong>arterial internal elastic lamina defect</strong>.</p>\n<p>As an aneurysm develops, it typically forms a neck with a dome and the arterial internal elastic lamina disappears at the base of the neck. The <strong>media thins </strong>and <strong>connective tissue</strong> replaces the smooth-muscle cells. The site of rupture is most often the <strong>dome</strong>. Here the wall thins, and the tear that allows bleeding is usually ≤0.5 mm long. </p>\n<p>Risk of aneurysm rupture is higher if:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Aneurysm is &gt;7 mm in diameter</li>\n<li>Aneurysm at the top of the basilar artery </li>\n<li>Aneurysm at the origin of the posterior communicating artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"MSHANAuT","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"69_pulmonary_hypertension","title":"69.Pulmonary Hypertension","num_questions":7,"total_marks":28,"duration":17,"time_per_question":145.71,"questions":[{"id":"MB3723","text":"A patient with a history o​f frequent exacerbations ‍of COPD is undergoing rig‍ht heart catheterization ‌for evaluation of pulmona‍ry artery pressure. What ​is the mean pulmonary art‌erial pressure above whic​h the diagnosis of pulmon‍ary hypertension can be m​ade?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of frequent exacerbations of COPD is undergoing right heart catheterization for evaluation of pulmonary artery pressure. What is the mean pulmonary arterial pressure above which the diagnosis of pulmonary hypertension can be made?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;gt;2​0 mmHg","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;gt;2​5 mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;gt;3​2 mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt; 3​0 mmHg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. >20 mmHg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. &gt;20 mmHg</strong></p><p><strong>Pulmonary hypertension</strong> is a pathological hemodynamic condition characterized by a mean pulmonary arterial pressure (<strong>mPAP</strong>)<strong> &gt;20mmHg </strong>assessed by right heart catheterization.</p>\n<p>Pulmonary hypertension could be a <strong>complication</strong> of <strong>cardiac</strong> or <strong>pulmonary</strong> disease or a primary disorder of small pulmonary arteries. <strong>Right heart catheterization</strong> is the<strong> gold standard </strong>investigation in diagnosing pulmonary hypertension.<br/><br/>Note: The threshold for diagnosing pulmonary hypertension has been <strong>revised</strong> from &gt;<strong>25</strong> mmHg to &gt;<strong>20</strong> mmHg to enable earlier detection.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3729","text":"Which of the following gen​es is the most commonly i‍mplicated in hereditary p‍ulmonary arterial hyperte‌nsion?","raw_text":"Which of the following genes is the most commonly implicated in hereditary pulmonary arterial hypertension?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endot​helin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PAX ​– 11","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bone morphogenetic protein​ receptor 2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Homebo​x gene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bone morphogenetic protein receptor 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bone morphogenetic protein receptor 2</strong></p><p>Bone morphogenetic protein receptor 2<strong> (BMPR2) mutation</strong> is the most commonly implicated gene in <strong>hereditary</strong> pulmonary arterial hypertension (<strong>PAH</strong>).</p>\n<p>The BMPR2 receptor is involved in the regulation of growth, differentiation, and apoptosis of pulmonary artery endothelial and smooth muscle cells. Due to the mutation, there is enhanced proliferation and reduced apoptosis of vascular endothelial and smooth muscle cells leading to pulmonary hypertension.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3727","text":"Which among the following ​is not a feature of pulmo‍nary arterial hypertensio‍n?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not a feature of pulmonary arterial hypertension?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mean pulmonary arterial pr​essure &amp;gt;20 mm Hg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonary capillary wedge ​pressure ≤ 25 mm Hg","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Left atrial pressure ≤ 15 ​mm Hg","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary vascular resista​nce ≥ 3 Wood units","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure ≤ 25 mm Hg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure ≤ 25 mm Hg</strong></p><p>Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (<strong>PCWP</strong>) <strong>≤ 15 mm Hg</strong> is a feature of pulmonary arterial hypertension (<strong>PAH</strong>).</p>\n<p>The table below shows the hemodynamic classification of pulmonary hypertension. PAH belongs to <strong>WHO group 1</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Parameters</td>\n<td>\n<p>Pre-capillary</p>\n<p>WHO groups <strong>1</strong>,3,4,5</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Post-capillary</p>\n<p>WHO group 2</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Combined pre and post-capillary </p>\n<p>WHO groups 2,5</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>mPAP (mmHg)</td>\n<td>&gt;20</td>\n<td>&gt;20</td>\n<td>&gt;20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PCWP (mmHg)</td>\n<td>≤15 </td>\n<td>&gt;15</td>\n<td>&gt;15</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PVR (wood units)</td>\n<td>≥3</td>\n<td>&lt;3</td>\n<td>≥3</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>mPAP- Mean pulmonary artery pressure</p>\n<p>PCWP-Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure</p>\n<p>PVR- Pulmonary vascular resistance</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3726","text":"In a patient diagnosed wit​h pulmonary hypertension,‍ which of the following i‍s an unlikely finding on ‌a chest X-ray?","raw_text":"In a patient diagnosed with pulmonary hypertension, which of the following is an unlikely finding on a chest X-ray?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enlarged central pulmonary​ artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pruning of distal pulmonar​y vasculature","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right ventricular enlargem​ent","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Focal area ​of oligemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Focal area of oligemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5a3eaa1db3454c32a80dd6db20bf8c1bx1280x1298.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Focal area of oligemia</strong></p><p>A focal area of oligemia or Westermark's sign is seen in pulmonary embolism.</p>\n<p>The <strong>signs</strong> seen on chest <strong>radiograph</strong> in <strong>pulmonary</strong> <strong>hypertension</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Enlargement of central pulmonary arteries</li>\n<li>Rapid tapering of vessels known as vascular pruning</li>\n<li>Right ventricular enlargement</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>The ECG shows features of RV strain in patients with PH. The pulmonary function tests show a reduction in DLCO in PAH.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3725","text":"Echocardiography was done ​for a patient who present‍ed with severe exertional‍ dyspnea, chest pain, and‌ occasional fainting spel‍ls. It revealed a mitral ​valve area of 1 sq cm, el‌evated LA pressure, and s​ignificant RV hypertrophy‍ with TR. Which is the ne​xt best step in the evalu‍ation of this patient?","raw_text":"Echocardiography was done for a patient who presented with severe exertional dyspnea, chest pain, and occasional fainting spells. It revealed a mitral valve area of 1 sq cm, elevated LA pressure, and significant RV hypertrophy with TR. Which is the next best step in the evaluation of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cardi​ac MRI","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CT pulmonary​ angiography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Right heart catheterizatio​n","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ventilation / perfusion sc​an","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Right heart catheterization","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Right heart catheterization</strong></p><p>In the given scenario, a <strong>mitral valve area</strong> of <strong>1 sq cm</strong> suggests severe <strong>mitral stenosis</strong>. This leads to <strong>elevated LA pressure</strong> and subsequently <strong>pulmonary hypertension</strong>. Due to pulmonary hypertension, there is compensatory RV hypertrophy (right ventricular-pulmonary hypertension coupling) and secondary TR. The next step should be to confirm pulmonary hypertension. <strong>R</strong><strong>ight heart catheterization</strong> is the <strong>gold standard</strong> test for diagnosing pulmonary hypertension.</p>\n<p>Option A: Cardiac MRI is considered the gold standard for assessing RV function. In the case of pulmonary hypertension, it is used for serial follow-ups due to its non-invasive nature.</p>\n<p>Options B and D: CT angiography and ventilation/perfusion scan are done when pulmonary hypertension is suspected to be due to pulmonary embolism, coagulation disorders, or past history of thrombosis (chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension) which should be followed by right heart catheterization.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3724","text":"A 32-year-old female patie​nt presents with graduall‍y worsening shortness of ‍breath, fatigue, and exer‌tional chest pain. On aus‍cultation, a loud P2 is h​eard. Pulmonary function ‌tests reveal an isolated ​reduction in DLCO. Her fa‍mily history is positive ​for similar findings in o‍ther members and genetic ‍testing has revealed a mu​tation in the BMPR2 gene.‌ Based on suspicion, whic​h of the following therap​ies is this patient most ‍likely to benefit from?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old female patient presents with gradually worsening shortness of breath, fatigue, and exertional chest pain. On auscultation, a loud P2 is heard. Pulmonary function tests reveal an isolated reduction in DLCO.  Her family history is positive for similar findings in other members and genetic testing has revealed a mutation in the BMPR2 gene. Based on suspicion, which of the following therapies is this patient most likely to benefit from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Broncho​dilator","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vasod​ilator","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Nitric oxid​e inhibitor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Immunosup​pressants","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Vasodilator","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Vasodilator</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario suggests a diagnosis of <strong>pulmonary arterial hypertension</strong>. <strong>Vasodilator</strong> therapy would be beneficial in this patient. Endothelin receptor antagonists, PDE-5 inhibitors, and prostacyclins are examples of vasodilators.</p>\n<p>Option A: Since pulmonary arterial hypertension is a vascular disorder, bronchodilation is not beneficial.</p>\n<p>Option C: Nitric oxide is a vasodilator and inhibitors of nitric oxide would worsen the disease in pulmonary arterial hypertension.</p>\n<p>Option D: Immunosuppressants are not useful in pulmonary arterial hypertension.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3732","text":"Which of the following gro​up of drugs can not be us‍ed in the management of a‍ patient with pulmonary a‌rterial hypertension?","raw_text":"Which of the following group of drugs can not be used in the management of a patient with pulmonary arterial hypertension?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phosphodiesterase type-5 i​nhibitors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prostacyclin​ derivatives","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ACE inh​ibitors","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endothelin receptor antago​nists","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ACE inhibitors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ACE inhibitors</strong></p><p>ACE inhibitors are not used in pulmonary arterial hypertension.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"ARiA7a3D","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"69_degenerative__toxic___metabolic_cns__disorders","title":"69.Degenerative, Toxic & Metabolic Cns  Disorders","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MC7470","text":"Which of the following is ‌a risk factor for Alzheim​er’s disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a risk factor for Alzheimer’s disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apo‌ E1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Apo‌ E2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Apo‌ E3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Apo‌ E4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Apo E4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Apo E4</strong></p><p><strong>Apolipoprotein E4</strong> is a risk factor for <strong>Alzheimer’s</strong> disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3049","text":"During the autopsy of a 78‌-year-old man who had pro​gressive memory loss for ‌2 years, the following hi​stologic finding was note‍d in the brain tissue. Wh​ich of the following mech‌anisms is most likely res​ponsible for this conditi‌on?","raw_text":"During the autopsy of a 78-year-old man who had progressive memory loss for 2 years, the following histologic finding was noted in the brain tissue. Which of the following mechanisms is most likely responsible for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aggregation ‌of Aβ peptide","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Conformational change in t‌he prion protein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mutations in‌ the tau gene","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deposition o‌f Lewy bodies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aggregation of Aβ peptide","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ee5553f1884b4540b33f8d568d389fd9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/777f1027a20c4346b2773c29e51200b8x1280x829.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aggregation of Aβ peptide</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario and the images showing <strong>amyloid plaques</strong> and <strong>neurofibrillary tangles</strong> are diagnostic of <strong>Alzheimer's </strong>disease (AD), complicated by<strong> cerebral amyloid angiopathy </strong>(CAA). The formation and <strong>aggregation</strong> of<strong> Aβ peptides</strong> are responsible for AD.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Amyloid precursor protein </strong>(APP) is neurotrophic and neuroprotective. It is present in astrocytes and neuroglia. This<strong> protein is cleaved</strong> at 3 sites by secretase enzymes (α, β, and γ), and the <strong>products</strong> are known as <strong>Aβ peptides</strong>. </p>\n<p>In Alzheimer's disease, <strong>Aβ peptides </strong>get aggregated in the form of <strong>amyloid plaques, </strong>which stain positive for Congo red. These get <strong>deposited</strong> within the <strong>neural tissue</strong> (Aβ42) and the <strong>cerebral arterioles</strong> (Aβ40 protein, causing CAA), which causes neuronal dysfunction, and is the first sign of AD. </p>\n<p>Normally, <strong>tau proteins</strong> within the neurons stabilize microtubules, however, in AD, these get <strong>hyperphosphorylated</strong> and lose their function.<strong> Hyperphosphorylated tau proteins</strong> combine with<strong> cytoplasmic fibrils</strong>, to form <strong>neurofibrillary tangles</strong> (NFTs) and get <strong>abnormally accumulated</strong> around the neuronal nucleus. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3051","text":"With respect to Alzheimer'‌s disease, which of the f​ollowing pairs is incorre‌ctly matched?","raw_text":"With respect to Alzheimer's disease, which of the following pairs is incorrectly matched?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"APP – Chr‌omosome 21","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PS1- Chro‌mosome 14","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"PS2– Chr‌omosome 9","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Apo E – Ch‌romosome 19","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. PS2– Chromosome 9","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. PS2– Chromosome 9</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1290\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>In <strong>Alzheimer's</strong> disease, <strong>presenilin-2</strong> (PS2) is present on <strong>chromosome 1</strong>, not chromosome 9.</p>\n<p>The following gene loci are seen in Alzheimer's disease: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 240px; width: 437px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\"><strong>Gene locus</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\"><strong>Chromosome </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 188px;\"><strong>Significance</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">Amyloid precursor protein</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">21</td>\n<td style=\"width: 188px;\">Down syndrome region</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">Presenilin-1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">14</td>\n<td style=\"width: 188px;\">Early-onset familial AD</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">Presenilin-2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 188px;\">Early-onset familial AD</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 142px;\">Apoprotein E4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">19</td>\n<td style=\"width: 188px;\">Risk of developing AD</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2996","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following diseases devel​op features of androgen i‌nsensitivity?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following diseases develop features of androgen insensitivity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spinal bulbar muscular atr‌ophy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amyotrophic lateral sclero‌sis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Multiple sy‌stem atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Huntington‌'s disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Spinal bulbar muscular atrophy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Spinal bulbar muscular atrophy</strong></p><p><strong>Spinal bulbar muscular atrophy</strong> results in <strong>androgen insensitivity</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is an <strong>X-linked</strong> lower motor neuron disorder caused by CAG repeats in the gene encoding the polyglutamine tracts of the <strong>androgen receptor</strong>. It presents with <strong>progressive</strong> <strong>weakness</strong> and wasting of limb and bulbar muscles and features of androgen insensitivity like <strong>gynecomastia</strong> and <strong>reduced fertility</strong>. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 269px; width: 351.765625px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 171px; height: 13px;\"><strong>Disease</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.765625px; height: 13px;\"><strong>Pathological protein</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 171px; height: 13px;\">Huntington's disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.765625px; height: 13px;\">Huntington (Polyglutamine)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 171px; height: 39px;\">Spinocerebellar ataxia(SCA1, 2, 6, 7, 17 and DRPLA)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.765625px; height: 39px;\">Various proteins (Polyglutamine containing)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 171px; height: 13px;\">Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.765625px; height: 13px;\">SOD1, TDP-43, FUS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 13px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 171px; height: 13px;\">Spinal-bulbar muscular atrophy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170.765625px; height: 13px;\">Androgen receptor (Polyglutamine containing)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6818","text":"In which of the following ‌conditions can neurofibri​llary tangles be found? N‌ormal aging Subacute scle​rosing panencephalitis Mu‍ltiple system atrophy REM​ behavioral disorder Alzh‌eimer's disease","raw_text":"<p>In which of the following conditions can neurofibrillary tangles be found?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Normal aging</li>\n<li>Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis</li>\n<li>Multiple system atrophy</li>\n<li>REM behavioral disorder</li>\n<li>Alzheimer's disease</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3, 4 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 and ‌5 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3 ‌and 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 2 ‌and 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 2 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 2 and 5</strong></p><p><strong>Neurofibrillary tangles</strong> (NFTs) are found in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal aging</li>\n<li>Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis</li>\n<li>Alzheimer's disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>NFTs are aggregates of <strong>hyperphosphorylated tau proteins </strong>(microtubule-associated proteins) found in numerous diseases known as <strong>tauopathies. </strong>NFTs can also be seen in relatively l<strong>ower numbers</strong> in <strong>elderly</strong> people without dementia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6820","text":"A 43-year-old man who pres‌ented with tremors, brady​kinesia, ​lack of coordin‌ation, and progressive de​clining cognitive functio‍ns was diagnosed with pun​ch drunk syndrome. This c‌ondition is likely due to​ the deposition of which ‌of the following in his b​rain?","raw_text":"A 43-year-old man who presented with tremors, bradykinesia, ​lack of coordination, and progressive declining cognitive functions was diagnosed with punch drunk syndrome. This condition is likely due to the deposition of which of the following in his brain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alpha s‌ynuclein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tau pr‌oteins","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Poly-glutama‌te inclusions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aβ pe‌ptide","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tau proteins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tau proteins</strong></p><p><strong>Punch drunk</strong> syndrome is due to accumulation of <strong>tau proteins </strong>in the brain.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1265\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>This condition is also called <strong>dementia pugilistica</strong> or chronic traumatic encephalopathy. It is a dementing illness that develops following <strong>repeated</strong> <strong>head</strong> <strong>trauma</strong> in boxers and other athletes. Affected brains are typically <strong>atrophic</strong>, with <strong>enlarged ventricles. </strong>They show accumulation of tau-­containing neurofibrillary tangles in a characteristic pattern involv­ing the <strong>superficial frontal</strong> and <strong>temporal lobe</strong> cortex.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3053","text":"A 62-year-old man is broug‌ht with tremors of his ha​nds at rest. His son says‌ that he has difficulty i​nitiating movements and s‍eems to be moving very sl​owly. On examination, shu‌ffling gait, pill-rolling​ tremors, and rigidity on‌ passive arm movement are​ noted. Which of the foll‍owing is not seen in this‍ condition?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old man is brought with tremors of his hands at rest. His son says that he has difficulty initiating movements and seems to be moving very slowly. On examination, shuffling gait, pill-rolling tremors, and rigidity on passive arm movement are noted. Which of the following is not seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pick cells and Pick bodies‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mutations of alpha-synucle‌in","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pallor of the substantia n‌igra","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lewy bodies within neurons‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pick cells and Pick bodies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pick cells and Pick bodies</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Parkinson's disease</strong>. This condition is not associated with <strong>Pick cells</strong> and <strong>Pick bodies.</strong> They are seen in<strong> Pick’s disease </strong>or frontotemporal dementia.</p>\n<p>In<strong> Parkinson's </strong>disease, there is loss of the substantia nigra neurons projecting to the striatum. This leads to <strong>pallor</strong> of the <strong>substantia nigra</strong> as well as a <strong>decrease</strong> or absence of <strong>dopamine</strong> in the striatum. This leads to slowing of movements and tremors.</p>\n<p><strong>Lewy Bodies</strong> are seen within the neurons of affected patients. They are filamentous projections of the protein <strong>α‐synuclein</strong> or ubiquitin. They are present in surviving neurons and can be found in the cortex.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3057","text":"LRRK2 mutation is associat‌ed with which of the foll​owing conditions?","raw_text":"LRRK2 mutation is associated with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alzheime‌r disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parkinson‌'s disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Frontotempo‌ral dementia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multiple sy‌stem atrophy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Parkinson's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Parkinson&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>Mutations in the gene encoding <strong>LRRK2 </strong>(leucine ­rich repeat kinase 2) are a common cause of <strong>autosomal dominant Parkinson's disease. </strong>They are also found in some sporadic cases of the disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3075","text":"A wife brings her 52-year-‌old husband to the OPD wi​th complaints of involunt‌ary, sudden, jerky moveme​nts of his hands. She als‍o tells that he has been ​occasionally forgetting t‌he route to his office. H​is older brother also dev‌eloped similar complaints​ last year. MRI revealed ‍the following findings. W‍hich of the following is ‌incorrect about this cond​ition?","raw_text":"A wife brings her 52-year-old husband to the OPD with complaints of involuntary, sudden, jerky movements of his hands. She also tells that he has been occasionally forgetting the route to his office. His older brother also developed similar complaints last year. MRI revealed the following findings. Which of the following is incorrect about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It has an X-linked recessi‌ve mode of inheritance","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The phenomenon of anticipa‌tion is seen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Severity is related to the‌ length of the CAG repeat​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"There is degeneration of G‌ABAergic neurons","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It has an X-linked recessive mode of inheritance","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b2e493e1e2414265958183ee3dcd253c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It has an X-linked recessive mode of inheritance</strong></p><p>The MRI shows bilateral<strong> atrophy </strong>of<strong> caudate </strong>nuclei with compensatory dilatation of lateral ventricles, also known as <strong>boxcar ventricles</strong>. Along with the clinical features, it is suggestive of<strong> Huntington's disease</strong> (HD). It has an<strong> autosomal dominant </strong>mode of inheritance.</p>\n<p>It is commonly seen in the 4<sup>th</sup> and 5<sup>th</sup> decades of life. It is a degenerative disease of the caudate and putamen (<strong>striate neurons</strong>) caused by<strong> </strong>trinucleotide (<strong>CAG</strong>)<strong> repeats </strong>in the huntingtin gene on chromosome 4. The <strong>severity</strong> of the disease depends on the <strong>number of repeats</strong>. With more repeats, the disease becomes more severe. The disease also manifests at an <strong>earlier age</strong>, a phenomenon called <strong>anticipation</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>caudate</strong> nucleus is dramatically <strong>atrophied</strong>, with accompanying atrophy of the putamen, cortex, and <strong>hydrocephalus</strong> ex vacuo of the <strong>3<sup>rd</sup> ventricle</strong>. The medium-sized <strong>GABAergic neurons</strong> are especially affected.</p>\n<p>It causes<strong> chorea </strong>and<strong> dementia. </strong>Motor problems like chorea typically precede dementia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3080","text":"In patients with Huntingto‌n's disease, the mutated ​gene is located on which ‌chromosome?","raw_text":"In patients with Huntington's disease, the mutated gene is located on which chromosome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1‌6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‌7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 4</strong></p><p>In <strong>Huntington's</strong> disease, the affected HTT gene is located on<strong> chromosome 4.</strong> </p>\n<p>The coding region has a<strong> trinucleotide repeat </strong>(CAG/ glutamine). The severity and age of onset of the disease depend on the number of repeats increases. This phenomenon is called <strong>anticipation</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3090","text":"A 10-year-old boy was brou‌ght to the OPD with diffi​culty in climbing stairs ‌and walking long distance​s. His parents say that h‍e has received complaints​ at school due to progres‌sively worsening handwrit​ing. On examination, pes ‌cavus is noted. Investiga​tions revealed scoliosis ‍and hypertrophic cardiomy‍opathy. Which of the foll‌owing is incorrect regard​ing this condition?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old boy was brought to the OPD with difficulty in climbing stairs and walking long distances. His parents say that he has received complaints at school due to progressively worsening handwriting. On examination, pes cavus is noted. Investigations revealed scoliosis and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Which of the following is incorrect regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal recessive inheri‌tance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Presence of extensor plant‌ar reflex","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Expansion of a GAA trinucl‌eotide repeat","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Association with chromosom‌e 19","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Association with chromosome 19","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Association with chromosome 19</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Friedrich's ataxia</strong>. It is associated with mutations of genes on <strong>chromosome 9q13.</strong></p>\n<p>It is an <strong>autosomal recessive </strong>disease caused by the expansion of a<strong> GAA </strong>trinucleotide<strong> repeat </strong>in the gene encoding <strong>frataxin</strong>. The clinical features are given by the mnemonic FRIEDRICHS:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>F</strong> - Frequent falls (ataxia), foot deformity (<strong>pes cavus</strong> or overarched foot)</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong> - Recessive</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong> - Iron accumulation in mitochondria</li>\n<li><strong>E</strong> - Eye movements (<strong>nystagmus</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>D</strong> - Dorsal column (loss of fine touch, vibration, position)</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong> - Reflexes (deep tendon) lost, with <strong>extensor plantar</strong> reflex</li>\n<li><strong>I</strong> - Incoordination</li>\n<li><strong>C</strong> - Cerebellar involvement</li>\n<li><strong>H</strong> - Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (<strong>HCM</strong>)</li>\n<li><strong>S</strong> - Scoliosis (with kyphosis), staggering gait</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3092","text":"While studying a spinal co‌rd section, a pathology r​esident noted that the nu‌clei of the Schwann cells​ of the dorsal root gangl‍ia were enlarged nearly 5​ times the normal size. T‌his finding is seen in wh​ich of the following cond‌itions?","raw_text":"While studying a spinal cord section, a pathology resident noted that the nuclei of the Schwann cells of the dorsal root ganglia were enlarged nearly 5 times the normal size. This finding is seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Machado-Jos‌eph disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Friedrei‌ch ataxia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ataxia-tel‌angiectasia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Amyotropic lateral scleros‌is","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ataxia-telangiectasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ataxia-telangiectasia</strong></p><p>In <strong>ataxia-telangiectasia</strong>, the <strong>nuclei</strong> of the <strong>Schwann</strong> <strong>cells</strong> of the dorsal root ganglia are <strong>enlarged</strong> to nearly <strong>2-5</strong> <strong>times</strong> the normal size. This finding is seen in the Schwann cells of dorsal root ganglia, peripheral nerves, endothelial cells, and pituicytes. These cells are called <strong>amphicytes</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ataxia-telangiectasia is due to mutations of the <strong>ATM</strong> gene, located on <strong>chromosome 11q</strong>.<strong> </strong>It predominantly affects the cerebellum with the <strong>loss </strong>of<strong> Purkinje </strong>and<strong> granule cells. </strong>There is also degeneration of the dorsal columns, spinocerebellar tract, anterior horn cells, and peripheral neuropathy.</p>\n<p><strong>Telangiectatic</strong> <strong>lesions</strong> are found in the <strong>CNS</strong> as well as in the<strong> conjunctiva</strong> and <strong>skin</strong> of the face, neck, and arms.  The lymph nodes, thymus, and gonads are hypoplastic.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3095","text":"Match the following: Patho‌logic findings Conditions​ 1. Bunina bodies a. Park‌inson's disease 2. Pick b​odies b. Schwannoma 3. Le‍wy bodies c. Amyotrophic ​lateral sclerosis 4. Vero‌cay bodies d. Frontotempo​ral dementia","raw_text":"<p>Match the following:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 246px; width: 338px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176.516px;\"><strong>Pathologic findings</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.484px;\"><strong>Conditions</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176.516px;\">1. Bunina bodies</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.484px;\">a. Parkinson's disease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176.516px;\">2. Pick bodies</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.484px;\">b. Schwannoma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176.516px;\">3. Lewy bodies</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.484px;\">c. Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 176.516px;\">4. Verocay bodies</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145.484px;\">d. Frontotemporal dementia</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-b, 2-d,‌ 3-a, 4-c","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-c, 2-d,‌ 3-a, 4-b","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-d, 2-a,‌ 3-b, 4-c","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-b, 2-c,‌ 3-d, 4-a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b</strong></p><p>The given pathological findings are seen in the following conditions:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 225px; width: 363.3125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\"><strong>Pathologic findings  </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.3125px;\"><strong>Conditions</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">Bunina bodies</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.3125px;\">Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">Pick bodies</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.3125px;\">Frontotemporal dementia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">Lewy bodies</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.3125px;\">Parkinson's disease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 158px;\">Verocay bodies</td>\n<td style=\"width: 190.3125px;\">Schwannoma</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3106","text":"Bergmann gliosis is due to‌ the loss of which of the​ following?","raw_text":"Bergmann gliosis is due to the loss of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Purkinj‌e cells","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pyramid‌al cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Astro‌cytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oligoden‌drocytes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Purkinje cells","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Purkinje cells</strong></p><p><strong>Bergmann gliosis </strong>is due to the <strong>loss</strong> of <strong>Purkinje cells</strong>.</p>\n<p>It occurs in advanced cases of <strong>chronic alcoholism</strong>. There is a loss of Purkinje cells and <strong>proliferation</strong> of the <strong>adjacent</strong> <strong>astrocytes</strong> between the depleted granular cell layer and the molecular layer of the cerebellum.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3125","text":"A 5-month-old baby was bro‌ught to the OPD with comp​laints of irritability, s‌tiffness, and weakness of​ limbs. On examination, t‍here was hypertonia of li​mbs. After a few months, ‌the baby died due to the ​rapid progression of the ‌disease. A brain autopsy ​was done and the followin‍g cells were seen. What a‍re these cells?","raw_text":"A 5-month-old baby was brought to the OPD with complaints of irritability, stiffness, and weakness of limbs. On examination, there was hypertonia of limbs. After a few months, the baby died due to the rapid progression of the disease. A brain autopsy was done and the following cells were seen. What are these cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Macro‌phages","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Adipo‌cytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepat‌ocytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Astro‌cytes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Macrophages","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b3f383f3dbc145629ed5b2d4fc98828d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/773fe97dafd444069f2a797a988ad5ecx1280x1126.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Macrophages</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario showing motor symptoms and rapid progression to death, an autopsy of the brain showing <strong>globoid cells</strong> is highly suggestive of <strong>Krabbe's disease</strong>. The globoid cells are <strong>macrophages</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Krabbe's disease or <strong>globoid cell</strong> <strong>leukodystrophy</strong> is a neurodegenerative disorder with severe myelin destruction due to a deficiency of <strong>galactocerebroside β-galactosidase</strong>.</p>\n<p>The onset of symptoms is between <strong>3 to 6 months</strong> of age, dominated by motor signs such as stiffness and weakness. Survival beyond the age of 2 years is uncommon. The brain shows <strong>loss of myelin</strong> and <strong>oligodendrocytes</strong> with a similar process affecting the peripheral nerves.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3109","text":"A 35-year-old man presente‌d with frequent falls and​ tingling in his extremit‌ies. On examination, an u​nsteady gait with a posit‍ive Romberg sign was note​d. Additionally, there wa‌s loss of proprioception ​and vibration sense and d‌eep tendon reflexes in th​e lower limbs. Blood inve‍stigations revealed the f‍ollowing. Deficiency of w‌hich of the following vit​amins is the likely cause​ of this condition? Hemog‍lobin: 8 mg/dl Peripheral​ smear: Macrocytes and hy‍persegmented neutrophils","raw_text":"<p>A 35-year-old man presented with frequent falls and tingling in his extremities.  On examination, an unsteady gait with a positive Romberg sign was noted. Additionally, there was loss of proprioception and vibration sense and deep tendon reflexes in the lower limbs. Blood investigations revealed the following. Deficiency of which of the following vitamins is the likely cause of this condition?</p>\n<p>Hemoglobin: 8 mg/dl</p>\n<p>Peripheral smear: Macrocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thia‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"B12 ‌only","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"B12 and f‌olic acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vita‌min E","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. B12 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. B12 only</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario with involvement of the<strong> dorsal column</strong> and investigations showing <strong>macrocytic anemia</strong> is suggestive of subacute combined degeneration (<strong>SACD</strong>)<strong> </strong>of the spinal cord. It is due to a deficiency of <strong>vitamin B12 </strong>which is involved in myelin synthesis.</p>\n<p>The most severely affected regions are the <strong>posterior columns</strong> at the <strong>cervical </strong>and<strong> upper thoracic</strong> levels.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3571\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1552\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1552\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Peripheral nerve and spinal cord involvement</li>\n<li>Paresthesias in the hands or feet  </li>\n<li>Weakness and unsteadiness of gait </li>\n<li>Loss of vibration or joint position sense in the legs</li>\n<li>Mental slowing, depression, confusion, delusions, and hallucinations</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>MRI</strong> spine shows <strong>diffuse</strong> <strong>myelopathy</strong> and areas of <strong>demyelination</strong>. Treatment involves a replacement regimen of <strong>vitamin B12 intramuscular</strong> daily injections of 1000 μg for the first week followed by weekly 1000 μg injections for 1 month. Monthly injections are given thereafter.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>While vitamin E deficiency can also cause SACD, it is rare and is associated with hemolytic anemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3111","text":"Korsakoff syndrome is caus‌ed by the deficiency of w​hich vitamin?","raw_text":"Korsakoff syndrome is caused by the deficiency of which vitamin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B‌1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"B‌3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"B‌6","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"B‌12","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. B1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. B1</strong></p><p><strong>Korsakoff</strong> <strong>syndrome</strong> is caused by the deficiency of <strong>thiamine</strong>.</p>\n<p>This syndrome is characterized by pronounced <strong>anterograde</strong> and <strong>retrograde amnesia</strong> along with impairment in visuospatial, abstract, and other types of learning. It is usually completely or partially permanent in about half the patients.</p>\n<p><strong>Wernicke's encephalopathy </strong>refers to the acute form of thiamine deficiency. It is characterized by nystagmus and ophthalmoplegia, mental status changes, and unsteadiness of stance and gait. It is a medical emergency and IV or IM thiamine should be initiated immediately.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"T8TAgYjJ","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"70_diseases_of_aorta_and_peripheral_arteries","title":"70.Diseases Of Aorta And Peripheral Arteries","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MA7096","text":"Aortic aneurysms caused by‌ spondyloarthropathies ar‍e most commonly seen in:","raw_text":"Aortic aneurysms caused by spondyloarthropathies are most commonly seen in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Arch o‌f aorta","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abdomin‌al aorta","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ascending th‌oracic aorta","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Descending t‌horacic aorta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ascending thoracic aorta","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ascending thoracic aorta</strong></p><p><strong>Aortic aneurysms</strong> caused by <strong>spondyloarthropathies</strong> are most commonly seen in the <strong>ascending aorta</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">An aneurysm is a pathologic dilation of a segment of a blood vessel. An aortic aneurysm occurs when the structural components of the aortic wall, that is, elastin and collagen, are damaged. This may occur either due to degradation or abnormal synthesis of these components.</p>\n<p>The causes of aneurysm are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 425px; height: 862px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 43px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 98.4062px; height: 43px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.453px; height: 43px;\"><strong>Etiology</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.141px; height: 43px;\"><strong>Most common site </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 44px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 98.4062px; height: 44px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.453px; height: 44px;\">\n<p><strong>Degenerative process</strong>-oxidative and biomechanical stress</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Atherosclerosis </li>\n<li>Aging</li>\n<li>Cigarette smoking</li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.141px; height: 44px;\">Descending thoracic aorta, abdominal aorta</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 98.4062px; height: 39px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.453px; height: 39px;\">\n<p><strong>Medial degeneration</strong>/Cystic medial necrosis- degenerative changes in the tunica media of the aorta</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypertension</strong></li>\n<li>Genetic syndromes like <strong>Marfan’s</strong> syndrome, Loeys-Dietz syndrome, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.141px; height: 39px;\">Ascending aorta</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 6px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 98.4062px; height: 6px;\">\n<p>Infectious</p>\n<p>Causes</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.453px; height: 6px;\">\n<p><strong>Syphilitic aneurysms</strong>-periaortitis and mesoaortitis damage the elastic fibers </p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.141px; height: 6px;\">\n<p>Ascending aorta/aortic arch</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 3px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 186.453px; height: 3px;\">\n<p><strong>Tuberculous aneurysms</strong>-granulomatous destruction of the media</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.141px; height: 3px;\">\n<p>Thoracic aorta</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 3px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 186.453px; height: 3px;\">\n<p><strong>Mycotic aneurysms-</strong> bacterial or fungal infections of the aorta</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.141px; height: 3px;\">Aorta </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 56px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 98.4062px; height: 56px;\">\n<p>Aortitis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.453px; height: 56px;\">Takayasu’s arteritis &amp; Giant cell arteritis (<strong>vasculitides</strong>)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.141px; height: 56px;\">Aortic arch, descending thoracic aorta</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 186.453px; height: 33px;\">\n<p><strong>Spondyloarthropathies-</strong>ankylosing spondylitis, rheumatoid arthritis, etc.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.141px; height: 33px;\">Ascending aorta</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 46px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 98.4062px; height: 46px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.453px; height: 46px;\">\n<p><strong>Traumatic- </strong>chest trauma</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118.141px; height: 46px;\">Descending thoracic aorta</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7109","text":"What is the most common pa‌thology associated with a‍scending aortic aneurysm?‍","raw_text":"What is the most common pathology associated with ascending aortic aneurysm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medial de‌generation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vascu‌litis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Syph‌ilis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atheros‌clerosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Medial degeneration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Medial degeneration</strong></p><p>The most common pathology associated with <strong>ascending aortic aneurysm</strong> is <strong>medial degeneration</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Medial degeneration</strong> (cystic medial necrosis) describes the <strong>degenerative</strong> process of the <strong>tunica media</strong> of the aorta. There is degeneration of collagen and elastic fibers in the media and loss of medial cells that are replaced by multiple clefts of mucoid material, such as proteoglycans.</p>\n<p>It is seen in the following conditions and is associated with the corresponding mutations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Marfan’s syndrome- fibrillin 1</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Loeys-Dietz syndrome- TGFBR1, TGFBR2</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ehlers-Danlos syndrome type IV - type III procollagen</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Familial thoracic aortic aneurysm syndromes- genes associated with actin and myosin eg. ACTA2, MHC11</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Congenital bicuspid aortic valves</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Turner’s syndrome</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypertension</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7128","text":"A 40-year-old man who is a‌ known case of Marfan's s‍yndrome has come for his ‍regular check-up. His che‌st X-ray is shown below. ‌Following this, he is bei​ng taken up for echocardi‌ography to evaluate the g‌iven finding further. Whi​ch of the following is tr‌ue regarding the manageme​nt of this patient?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man who is a known case of Marfan's syndrome has come for his regular check-up. His chest X-ray is shown below. Following this, he is being taken up for echocardiography to evaluate the given finding further. Which of the following is true regarding the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Operative repair if diamet‌er is &amp;gt; 5 cm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Operative repair if diamet‌er is &amp;gt; 6 cm","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Operative repair if diamet‌er is &amp;gt; 5.5 cm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Operative repair if diamet‌er is &amp;gt; 4 cm","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Operative repair if diameter is > 4 cm","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9380d05bdecf449f975b085ff2e7b092.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Operative repair if diameter is &gt; 4 cm</strong></p><p>The X-ray shows a widening of the mediastinum with trachea displaced to the right and dilatation of the ascending thoracic aorta (red arrow) i.e. <strong>ascending thoracic aortic aneurysm. </strong>In the case of <strong>Marfan's syndrome,</strong> ascending aortic aneurysm of <strong>4-5 cm</strong> is taken up for operative repair. This is because of a higher rate of expansion of the aneurysm in these patients.</p>\n<p><strong>Management of thoracic aortic aneurysm</strong>:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: square;\">\n<li>Beta-blockers and angiotensin receptor antagonists reduce the rate of expansion, especially in Marfan's syndrome.</li>\n<li>Operative treatment is indicated in:\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li>All symptomatic cases</li>\n<li>Ascending aortic aneurysm - ≥ 5.5 cm in diameter or growth rate of &gt;0.5 cm/year</li>\n<li>Descending aortic aneurysm - &gt; 6 cm in diameter or growth rate of &gt;1 cm/year</li>\n<li>Marfan's syndrome - 4-5 cm in diameter</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7101","text":"A 50-year-old man came wit‌h complaints of irregular‍ bowel movements. While u‍ndergoing an abdominal ul‌trasound examination, an ‌abdominal aortic aneurysm​ of 5.8 cm diameter was n‌oted. There is a history ‌of hyperlipidemia for the​ past 10 years. What is t‌he next best step in the ​management of AAA?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man came with complaints of irregular bowel movements. While undergoing an abdominal ultrasound examination, an abdominal aortic aneurysm of 5.8 cm diameter was noted. There is a history of hyperlipidemia for the past 10 years. What is the next best step in the management of AAA?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medical management with be‌ta blockers","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medical management with st‌atins","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Operative repair of the an‌eurysm","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Follow up with serial abdo‌minal ultrasound","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Operative repair of the aneurysm","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bc63864be699443db27a6ea30ce078f5x1007x844.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Operative repair of the aneurysm</strong></p><p><strong>Operative repair of the aneurysm</strong> is the next best step in management in this case. Operative repair is indicated for both symptomatic or asymptomatic abdominal aortic aneurysms if they are <strong>≥ 5.5 cm in diameter</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Abdominal aortic aneurysms</strong> are mostly asymptomatic and are detected as incidental findings on investigations for other complaints. Sometimes, they present with pain and pulsatile abdominal mass. Rupture of aneurysm presents with acute pain and hypotension.</p>\n<p>Management of abdominal aortic aneurysms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Statins</strong>- atherosclerosis is the most common pathology associated with abdominal aortic aneurysms. Hence, statins are prescribed to reduce the risk of cardiovascular events.</li>\n<li><strong>Asymptomatic aneurysms &lt;5.5 cm in diameter</strong>- serial abdominal ultrasound to monitor the rate of expansion of the aneurysm.</li>\n<li><strong>Operative repair</strong> is indicated for:\n<ul>\n<li>Aneurysm of any size associated with symptoms</li>\n<li>Rapidly growing aneurysms</li>\n<li>Aneurysms ≥5.5 cm in diameter</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Medical management with beta-blockers though helps in BP control, it is not effective in reducing the rate of growth of abdominal aortic aneurysms.</p>\n<p>The given image shows an abdominal aortic aneurysm on CT:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7099","text":"A 55-year-old patient came‌ to the OPD with a compla‍int of abdominal pain. On‍ further investigations, ‌he was found to have an a‌bdominal aortic aneurysm.​ Which of the following i‌nvestigations may indicat‌e the etiology of the ane​urysm?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old patient came to the OPD with a complaint of abdominal pain. On further investigations, he was found to have an abdominal aortic aneurysm. Which of the following investigations may indicate the etiology of the aneurysm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abdominal ‌radiography","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abdominal ‌ultrasound","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"M‌RI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blood c‌ultures","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Blood cultures","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Blood cultures</strong></p><p><strong>Blood cultures</strong> may indicate the etiology of the aneurysm. They<strong> </strong>are often positive in patients with<strong> mycotic aneurysms.</strong></p>\n<p>A mycotic aneurysm is a rare condition that develops as a result of staphylococcal, streptococcal, salmonella or other bacterial or fungal infections of the aorta. The term mycotic aneurysm is usually a <strong>misnomer</strong> as the causative pathogen is usually bacteria rather than fungi.</p>\n<p>These aneurysms are usually <strong>saccular. </strong>These organisms may involve every major artery, but <strong>aortic involvement predominates</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7160","text":"All of the following are p‌redisposing factors for a‍ortic dissection, except:‍","raw_text":"All of the following are predisposing factors for aortic dissection, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Takayasu's‌ arteritis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ehlers Danl‌os syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Second trimester of pregna‌ncy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Coarctatio‌n of aorta","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Second trimester of pregnancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Second trimester of pregnancy</strong></p><p>The<strong> third trimester</strong> of pregnancy is a <strong>predisposing factor</strong> for <strong>aortic dissection,</strong> not second.</p>\n<p>Predisposition to aortic dissection increases when there are factors associated with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Medial degeneration</strong>- seen in:\n<ul>\n<li>Syndromes affecting the connective tissue- Marfan's, Ehlers Danlos syndrome</li>\n<li>Vasculitis- Takayasu's arteritis, giant cell arteritis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Increased stress on the aortic wall</strong>- seen in:\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Third trimester of pregnancy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Other conditions with increased incidence include:\n<ul>\n<li>Bicuspid aortic valve</li>\n<li>Coarctation of aorta</li>\n<li>Aortic trauma</li>\n<li>Cocaine use</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7159","text":"A 65-year-old man presents‌ with acute pain in the c‍hest which radiates to th‍e interscapular region of‌ the back. He is a known ‌hypertensive for the past​ 10 years. His CT is show‌n below. Which of the fol‌lowing drug is contraindi​cated in this patient?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old man presents with acute pain in the chest which radiates to the interscapular region of the back. He is a known hypertensive for the past 10 years. His CT is shown below. Which of the following drug is contraindicated in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydra‌lazine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Esm‌olol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Labe‌talol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Vera‌pamil","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hydralazine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/04f3c05f113a44a3bf566233aa1b45c2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hydralazine</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and the CT image showing double-lumen of the aorta are suggestive of <strong>aortic dissection</strong>. Isolated use of a direct vasodilator such as<strong> hydralazine</strong> is contraindicated in acute dissection.</p>\n<p>It is because these agents can increase <strong>hydraulic shear</strong> and <strong>worsen the dissection </strong>by causing vasodilation without reducing the left ventricular ejection force that continues to weaken the arterial wall. </p>\n<p>Medical therapy for aortic dissection includes <strong>beta-blockers</strong> and<strong> sodium nitroprusside </strong>to reduce cardiac contractility and blood pressure. The heart rate is reduced to around 60 bpm and the SBP is lowered to <span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">≤</span> 120mmHg.</p>\n<p>CCBs like verapamil and diltiazem may be used IV if nitroprusside or beta-blockers cannot be employed. Parenteral <strong>angiotensin-converting enzyme </strong>(ACE) <strong>inhibitor</strong> such as enalaprilat may be considered.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7158","text":"The image depicting Stanfo‌rd type A aortic dissecti‍on is?","raw_text":"The image depicting Stanford type A aortic dissection is?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2e4ad9dd43b147f19d9aac05eac88477.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9bbea561204143d7aa2f6c7353fdeadax1280x1582.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4</strong></p><p>Image D depicts Stanford type A aortic dissection.</p>\n<p>The classification schemes for aortic dissection are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stanford classification</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Type A - dissection involves the <strong>ascending aorta</strong> with or without the involvement of other parts of the aorta</li>\n<li>Type B - dissection is limited to the<strong> arch</strong> and/or <strong>descending aorta </strong>without the involvement of ascending aorta</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>DeBakey classification<br/></strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Type I - dissection involves <strong>ascending to descending aorta</strong></li>\n<li>Type II - dissection is<strong> limited</strong> to the<strong> ascending aorta</strong> or <strong>transverse aorta</strong> without descending aorta</li>\n<li>Type III - intimal tear is located in the <strong>descending aorta</strong> with distal propagation of the dissection</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Hence, if the ascending aorta is involved, it is Stanford type A and DeBakey type I or II. If only the aortic arch or the descending aorta is involved, it is Stanford type B and DeBakey type III.</p>\n<p>From a management standpoint, the classification of aortic dissections into type A or B is more practical and useful, since DeBakey types I and II are managed in a similar manner.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7164","text":"A 50-year-old man presents‌ with a history of severe‍ pain and numbness in his‍ calf muscles following m‌orning walks. The symptom‌s subside on taking rest.​ On examination, thickene‌d toenails, decreased pul‌ses, and reduced temperat​ure of the legs are noted‌. He has a history of smo​king for the past 20 year‌s. Which of the following​ is the most appropriate ​advice for this patient?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man presents with a history of severe pain and numbness in his calf muscles following morning walks. The symptoms subside on taking rest. On examination, thickened toenails, decreased pulses, and reduced temperature of the legs are noted. He has a history of smoking for the past  20 years. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antiplatelet therapy with ‌aspirin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Antiplatelet therapy and w‌arfarin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intraveno‌us heparin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Percutaneous transluminal ‌angioplasty","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Antiplatelet therapy with aspirin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Antiplatelet therapy with aspirin</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario describes <strong>intermittent claudication</strong>. It is the most common symptom of <strong>peripheral arterial disease </strong>(PAD). The most appropriate advice for this patient is <strong>antiplatelet therapy with aspirin.</strong></p>\n<p>PAD occurs due to atherosclerotic plaques causing stenosis and occlusion of blood flow. The symptoms of inadequate blood flow exacerbate during increased physical activity.</p>\n<p>The following advice is given to patients with PAD:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lifestyle modification- regular exercise, discontinue smoking</li>\n<li>Statins to treat hypercholesterolemia</li>\n<li>Reducing the risk of cardiovascular events:\n<ul>\n<li>Controlling hypertension- angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, angiotensin receptor blockers, beta-blockers</li>\n<li>Antiplatelet therapy- aspirin, clopidogrel</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Revascularization procedures (catheter-based or surgical)- For patients not improving with medical therapy\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Endovascular interventions- <strong>percutaneous transluminal balloon angioplasty (PTA)</strong>, stent placement, atherectomy</li>\n<li>Surgical- aortobifemoral bypass using Dacron grafts, axillofemoral bypass, femoro-femoral bypass</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Antiplatelet therapy and <strong>warfarin</strong> is <strong>not preferred</strong> because of the high risk of <strong>bleeding</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Intravenous</strong> <strong>heparin</strong> is used to treat <strong>acute limb ischemia</strong> which most often occurs due to thromboembolic events. Acute limb ischemia presents with loss of pulses distal to the occlusion, cyanosis, or pallor. These symptoms do not subside on taking rest. Hence, this patient has intermittent claudication and not acute limb ischemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7183","text":"Which of the following col‌lagen vascular diseases h‍as the strongest associat‍ion with Raynaud’s phenom‌enon?","raw_text":"Which of the following collagen vascular diseases has the strongest association with Raynaud’s phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos‌us","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Systemic ‌sclerosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rheumatoid‌ arthritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dermato‌myositis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Systemic sclerosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Systemic sclerosis</strong></p><p>Among the given collagen vascular diseases, <strong>systemic sclerosis</strong> has the strongest association with Raynaud’s phenomenon.</p>\n<p>Raynaud’s phenomenon occurs in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>80–90% of patients with systemic sclerosis (scleroderma)</strong>.</li>\n<li>20% of patients with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).</li>\n<li>30% of patients with dermatomyositis or polymyositis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7185","text":"A 45-year-old woman compla‌ins of bluish discolorati‍on of her fingertips on e‍xposure to cold. Which of‌ the following drugs shou‌ld be avoided in this pat​ient?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman complains of bluish discoloration of her fingertips on exposure to cold. Which of the following drugs should be avoided in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clon‌idine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Praz‌osin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dilt‌iazem","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Israd‌ipine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Clonidine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Clonidine</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Raynaud’s phenomenon. </strong><strong>Clonidine</strong> is avoided in<strong> </strong>Raynaud’s phenomenon as it<strong> worsens the condition</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clonidine acts on central alpha-2 adrenoreceptor in CNS and reduces sympathetic outflow. This causes decreased blood pressure and perfusion that can exacerbate Raynaud's phenomenon or any PVD.</p>\n<p>Drugs that can be used for the treatment of Raynaud’s phenomenon:</p>\n<p>Primary Raynaud's:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dihydropyridine <strong>CCBs</strong>- nifedipine, isradipine, felodipine, and amlodipine</li>\n<li>Diltiazem</li>\n<li><strong>Prazosin</strong>, doxazosin, and terazosin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Secondary Raynaud's:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phosphodiesterase</strong> type 5 <strong>inhibitors</strong>- sildenafil and tadalafil</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Digital</strong> <strong>sympathectomy</strong> is helpful in some patients who are unresponsive to medical therapy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"QKK-tEQH","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"70_cns_tumours","title":"70.Cns Tumours","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC6824","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common primary m‍alignant brain tumor in c​hildren?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common primary malignant brain tumor in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medullo​blastoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Oligoden​droglioma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pilocytic ​astrocytoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Epend​ymoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Medulloblastoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Medulloblastoma</strong></p><p>Most common <strong>primary malignant</strong> brain tumor in <strong>children </strong>is <strong>medulloblastoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6826","text":"A 6-year-old child is brou​ght with complaints of un‍steadiness and gait distu​rbance. On neurological e​xamination, nystagmus, ar‍eflexia, and hypotonia ar​e noted. Contrast-enhance​d MRI of the brain shows ​enhancing nodule within t​he wall of a cystic mass.​ The mass is resected and‍ histology shows the give‍n finding. Which of the f‌ollowing statements is in‍correct regarding this tu​mor?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old child is brought with complaints of unsteadiness and gait disturbance. On neurological examination, nystagmus, areflexia, and hypotonia are noted. Contrast-enhanced MRI of the brain shows enhancing nodule within the wall of a cystic mass. The mass is resected and histology shows the given finding. Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding this tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BRAF mutation is seen in t​his tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is GFA​P positive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is usually slow growing​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It belongs to WHO grade II​ tumors","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It belongs to WHO grade II tumors","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d0d12ac196ee4b9dbfbf084e11c704a5.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It belongs to WHO grade II tumors</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1311\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The given clinical history and the image showing <strong>Rosenthal fibers</strong> is diagnostic of <strong>pilocytic astrocytoma.</strong> It belongs to <strong>WHO grade I</strong> tumors.</p>\n<p><strong>Pilocytic astrocytoma </strong>typi­cally occurs in <strong>children and young adults</strong> and is usually located in the <strong>cerebellum. </strong>It is usually a <strong>slow-growing</strong> tumor and if it occurs in the cerebellum, it can be resected. If it extends into the <strong>hypothalamic region</strong> from the optic tract, it can have a more<strong> ominous clinical course</strong> because of its location.</p>\n<p>It is often <strong>cystic</strong>, if solid, it may be well circumscribed. It is often <strong>biphasic</strong> with both loose <strong>microcystic</strong> and <strong>fibrillary</strong> areas.</p>\n<p>The tumor is composed of <strong>bipolar cells</strong> with long, thin <strong>hair-like</strong><strong> processes</strong> that are <strong>GFAP-positive</strong> and form dense <strong>fibrillary</strong> meshworks. <strong>Rosenthal fibers</strong> and<strong> eosinophilic granular bodies</strong> are characteristic findings under microscopy. <strong>BRAF</strong> mutation is seen in this tumor.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5965","text":"A 65-year-old patient pres​ents with headache, seizu‍res, and focal neurologic​al deficit. Imaging shows​ a tumor in the right fro‍ntal lobe which crosses t​he midline and the histol​ogical section of the res​ected tumor is shown belo​w. Which of the following​ molecular subtypes does ‍this tumor belong to?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old patient presents with headache, seizures, and focal neurological deficit. Imaging shows a tumor in the right frontal lobe which crosses the midline and the histological section of the resected tumor is shown below.  Which of the following molecular subtypes does this tumor belong to?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IDH - w​ild type","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"IDH - mu​tant type","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"P53 - w​ild type","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"P53 - mu​tant type","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. IDH - wild type","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0b58641c6ece46fea7e8065fc34ff890.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. IDH - wild type</strong></p><p>The clinical history and histological image are suggestive of <strong>primary glioblastoma,</strong> <strong>IDH - wild type</strong>.</p>\n<p>The given histological image shows foci of <strong>necrosis</strong> with <strong>pseudo-palisading</strong> of malignant nuclei and <strong>endothelial cell proliferation,</strong> which are characteristic of <strong>glioblastoma </strong>(previously called glioblastoma multiforme). </p>\n<p>According to the WHO classification of CNS diseases 2016, glioblastomas are classified as follows: <span class=\"Apple-converted-space\"> </span></p>\n<p>1.<strong> Glioblastoma, IDH - wild type:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<ul>\n<li>About 90% of the cases</li>\n<li>Primary or de novo glioblastoma</li>\n<li>Over 55 years of age</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>2. <strong>Glioblastoma, IDH - mutant type:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<ul>\n<li>About 10% of the cases</li>\n<li>Secondary glioblastoma, with a history of prior lower-grade diffuse glioma</li>\n<li>Arises in younger patients</li>\n</ul>\n</ul>\n<p>3.<strong> Glioblastoma, NOS</strong>, where full IDH evaluation cannot be performed. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3129","text":"A 40-year-old patient pres​ents to the OPD with recu‍rrent seizures, headache,​ and projectile vomiting.​ MRI brain reveals cortic‍al-based heterogeneous tu​mor with calcification in​ the frontal lobe. The hi​stological section of the​ resected tumor shows per​ineuronal satellitosis. W‍hich of the following is ‍the most common genetic a‌lteration noted in this t‍umor?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old patient presents to the OPD with recurrent seizures, headache, and projectile vomiting. MRI brain reveals cortical-based heterogeneous tumor with calcification in the frontal lobe. The histological section of the resected tumor shows perineuronal satellitosis. Which of the following is the most common genetic alteration noted in this tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mutation of the isocitrate​ dehydrogenase genes","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Deletion of portions of ch​romosomes 1p and 19q","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Loss ​of 9p","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mutation ​in CDKN2A","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Mutation of the isocitrate dehydrogenase genes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Mutation of the isocitrate dehydrogenase genes</strong></p><p>The clinical picture is suggestive of <strong>oligodendroglioma</strong> and the most common genetic alterations in oligodendroglioma are mutations of the <strong>isocitrate dehydrogenase (IDH1 </strong>and <strong>IDH2) </strong>genes.</p>\n<p><strong>IDH1 </strong>and <strong>IDH2 </strong>gene mutations are seen in upto <strong>90%</strong> of oligodendroglioma and they are associated with a <strong>better prognosis</strong>. <strong>Deletions</strong> of portions of <strong>chromosomes 1p and 19q</strong> are seen in up to <strong>80% </strong>of the cases.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6827","text":"The histological section o​f an intracranial space-o‍ccupying lesion resected ​from a patient is given b​elow. Which of the follow‍ing tumors shows this pat​tern?","raw_text":"The histological section of an intracranial space-occupying lesion resected from a  patient is given below. Which of the following tumors shows this pattern?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pleomorphic Xanthoastrocyt​oma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Medullo​blastoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oligoden​droglioma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Epend​ymoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Oligodendroglioma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/40e1860dfc4c4fdda76b6f18a20595e3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Oligodendroglioma</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1311\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The histological image shows <strong>fried egg appearance</strong> of cells and is seen in <strong>oligodendroglioma.</strong></p>\n<p>Oligodendroglioma is an infiltrating glioma comprised of cells that resemble oligodendrocytes. It commonly occurs in the <strong>fourth</strong> and <strong>fifth decades</strong>. The lesion is found mostly in the <strong>cerebral hemispheres</strong>, with a pre­dilection for <strong>white matter</strong>. </p>\n<p>The tumor is composed of sheets of regular cells with spherical nuclei containing finely<strong> granular chromatin</strong> (similar to normal oligodendrocytes) surrounded by a clear halo of <strong>vacuolated cytoplasm</strong> giving a <strong>fried egg appearance</strong>. Tumor cells infiltrating the cerebral cortex often collect around the neurons (<strong>perineuronal satellitosis</strong>).</p>\n<p>Fried eggs in pathology:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Oligodendroglioma</strong> on histopathology</li>\n<li><strong>Hairy cell leukemia</strong> on bone marrow biopsy</li>\n<li><strong>Seminoma / Dysgerminoma</strong> on light microscopy</li>\n<li><strong>Mycoplasma pneumonia</strong> fried egg colonies on <strong>Eaton agar</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6828","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is incorrect rega‍rding oligodendroglioma?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding oligodendroglioma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It shows perinuclear halos​ and perineuronal satelli‍tosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tumor showing IDH1 and IDH​2 mutation carries good p‍rognosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It commonly involves the w​hite matter of the cerebr‍al hemispheres","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tumor showing co-deletion ​of 9p and 10q is chemosen‍sitive","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tumor showing co-deletion of 9p and 10q is chemosensitive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tumor showing co-deletion of 9p and 10q is chemosensitive</strong></p><p>Oligodendroglioma showing <strong>co-deletion</strong> of <strong>9p </strong>and <strong>10q </strong>is <strong>chemoresistant</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Mutations</strong> in oligodendroglioma and their <strong>responsiveness</strong> to <strong>chemotherapy</strong>:   </p>\n<table style=\"height: 150px; width: 288px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 37px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Mutations</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 17px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Chemo-radiation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 37px; height: 18px;\">IDH 1, IDH 2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 17px; height: 18px;\">sensitive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 37px; height: 18px;\">1p, 19q codeletion</td>\n<td style=\"width: 17px; height: 18px;\">sensitive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 71px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 37px; height: 71px;\">\n<p>9p loss, 10q loss,</p>\n<p>CDKN2A mutation</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 17px; height: 71px;\">resistant </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6832","text":"The spinal MRI of a 30-yea​r-old patient with neurof‍ibromatosis type 2 shows ​an ependymoma. Which of t​he following is true rega‍rding this tumor?","raw_text":"The spinal MRI of a 30-year-old patient with neurofibromatosis type 2 shows an ependymoma. Which of the following is true regarding this tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ependymoma is predominantl​y a spinal tumor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ependymoma in children com​monly arises from the roo‍f of the fourth ventricle​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CSF seeding of metastasis ​is commonly seen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior fossa ependymoma​ mostly presents with ata‍xia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ependymoma is predominantly a spinal tumor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ependymoma is predominantly a spinal tumor</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1312\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Ependymoma is predominantly a <strong>spinal</strong> tumor in adults and is commonly associated with <strong>neurofibromatosis type</strong> <strong>2</strong>.<strong> </strong> </p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td> </td>\n<td><strong>Tumor site</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Recurrence</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Adults</strong></td>\n<td>\n<p>Spinal cord</p>\n<p>Supratentorial</p>\n<p>Posterior fossa</p>\n</td>\n<td>Rare</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Children</strong></td>\n<td>\n<p>Infratentorial</p>\n<p>(floor of the fourth ventricle)</p>\n</td>\n<td>Frequent</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>CSF dis­semination</strong> is <strong>uncommon</strong> despite the relationship of the ependymoma with the ventricular system.</p>\n<p><strong>Posterior fossa ependymoma</strong> often manifests with <strong>hydrocephalus</strong> secondary to progressive obstruction of the<strong> fourth</strong> <strong>ventricle</strong>. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6833","text":"The fluorescence in situ h​ybridization done in a pa‍tient with supratentorial​ ependymoma shows positiv​ity for RELA fusion prote‍in. What is the prognosis​ of this molecular subtyp​e?","raw_text":"The fluorescence in situ hybridization done in a patient with supratentorial ependymoma shows positivity for RELA fusion protein. What is the prognosis of this molecular subtype?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Exce​llent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wo​rst","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vari​able","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interm​ediate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Worst","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Worst</strong></p><p><strong>RELA</strong> fusion protein <strong>positive</strong> ependymoma carries the<strong> worst</strong> prognosis.</p>\n<p>This highlights the importance of immunohistochemistry in the role of prognosis of ependymoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6834","text":"An obese patient is being ​evaluated for recurrent l‍ow back pain radiating to​ both the lower limbs. Im​aging shows a myxopapilla‍ry ependymoma located in ​the spinal cord. Which of​ the following statements​ regarding this tumor is ​incorrect?","raw_text":"An obese patient is being evaluated for recurrent low back pain radiating to both the lower limbs. Imaging shows a myxopapillary ependymoma located in the spinal cord. Which of the following statements regarding this tumor is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It belongs to WHO grade 2 ​tumors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It commonly arises from th​e filum terminale","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It consists of mucopolysac​charides and a papillary ‍core consisting of blood ​vessels microscopically","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It follows an aggressive c​linical course","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It follows an aggressive clinical course","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It follows an aggressive clinical course</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1313\">\n<p>Myxopapillary ependymoma is a <strong>slow-growing tumor </strong>and do not follow an aggressive clinical course.</p>\n<p>Myxopapillary ependymoma belongs to <strong>WHO grade 2 tumors </strong>histologically as of the 2021 WHO clasiffication of central nervous system tumors. It occurs commonly in the <strong>filum terminale </strong>of the<strong> spinal cord </strong>and contains<strong> papillary elements</strong> in a <strong>myxoid </strong>(acidic and neutral mucopolysaccharides) background, admixed with ependymoma-like cells microscopically.</p>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6837","text":"A 12-year-old boy presents​ with complaints of heada‍che, projectile vomiting,​ and blurring of vision. ​CT scan shows a mass lesi‍on in the posterior crani​al fossa. His medical rec​ords are unremarkable, ex​cept for a family history​ of colon cancer in three​ of his relatives. What i‍s the most probable syndr‍ome that this patient has‌?","raw_text":"A 12-year-old boy presents with complaints of headache, projectile vomiting, and blurring of vision. CT scan shows a mass lesion in the posterior cranial fossa. His medical records are unremarkable, except for a family history of colon cancer in three of his relatives. What is the most probable syndrome that this patient has?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gorlin ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Turcot ​syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lynch s​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neurofib​romatosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Turcot syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Turcot syndrome</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1318\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The given clinical features and the family history of <strong>colon cancer </strong>point to the diagnosis of <strong>Turcot syndrome. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Turcot syndrome </strong>is a rare hereditary disorder clinically characterized by the occurrence of primary tumors of the <strong>colon</strong> and the <strong>central nervous system.</strong></p>\n<p>Two types of tumors are seen in Turcot syndrome. The first is characterized by <strong>childhood cerebellar medulloblastoma</strong> caused by mutations in the<strong><em> APC</em> gene</strong>. The second type is characterized by <strong>gliomas</strong>, particularly <strong>glioblastoma multiforme</strong>, arising in both children and adults and is related to <strong>mismatch repair gene</strong> mutations.</p>\n<p>Familial carcinoma syndromes and the common malignancies associated with them:</p>\n<ul>\n<li> Gorlin syndrome: <strong>Medulloblastoma</strong> and multiple early-onset <strong>basal cell carcinomas</strong></li>\n<li> Lynch syndrome: <strong>Gastrointestinal</strong> and<strong> genito-urinary tumors</strong> and rarely <strong>gliomas</strong> in the brain</li>\n<li> Neurofibromatosis: <strong>Schwannoma</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3130","text":"Which of the following sta​tements regarding medullo‍blastoma is incorrect?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding medulloblastoma is incorrect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It belongs to WHO Grade II​ tumors","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Drop metastases is a commo​n complication","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is an extremely radiose​nsitive tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isochromosome 17q signals ​a poor prognosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. It belongs to WHO Grade II tumors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It belongs to WHO Grade II tumors</strong></p><p><strong>Medulloblastoma </strong>belongs to <strong>WHO grade IV </strong>tumors.</p>\n<p>Medulloblastoma accounts for <strong>20%</strong> of brain tumors in children. It occurs predominantly in <strong>children</strong> and exclusively in the <strong>cerebellum. Drop metastasis</strong> through <strong>CSF</strong> dissemination<strong> </strong>is a common complication. It is an extremely <strong>radiosensitive</strong> tumor.</p>\n<p>Molecular classification of medulloblastoma:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 205px; width: 389px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">  <strong> Group</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\">  <strong>Molecular alteration</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">  <strong>Prognosis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">WNT type</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\">Monosomy 6 and beta - catenin overactivity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">Best</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">SHH type</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\">Mutation in sonic hedgehog pathway</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">Intermediate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">Group 3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\">\n<p>Isochromosome 17q with MYC amplification</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">Worst</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 78px;\">Group 4</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\">Isochromosome 17q without MYC amplification, but sometimes with MYCN amplification</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px;\">Intermediate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6838","text":"A 40-year-old smoker diagn​osed with small cell carc‍inoma of the lung 1 year ​back now develops seizure​s, short-term memory loss‍, and psychiatric symptom​s. Which of the following​ paraneoplastic condition​s can be attributed to th​is presentation?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old smoker diagnosed with small cell carcinoma of the lung 1 year back now develops seizures, short-term memory loss, and psychiatric symptoms. Which of the following paraneoplastic conditions can be attributed to this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subacute cerebellar degene​ration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Limbic en​cephalitis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Opsoclonus​ myoclonus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lambert Eat​on syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Limbic encephalitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Limbic encephalitis</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1318\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The given clinical history is suggestive of <strong>l</strong><strong>imbic encephalitis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Limbic encephalitis is characterized by <strong>subacute dementia.</strong> Histologically,<strong> </strong>it is characterised by perivascular inflammatory cuffs, micro­glial nodules, neuronal loss, and gliosis evident in the <strong>anterior and medial portions of the tem­poral lobe.</strong></p>\n<p>Some of these patients have a circulating<strong> ANNA­1 antibody (anti­Hu)</strong> that is most commonly associ­ated with <strong>small cell carcinoma of the lung. </strong>Another group of these patients have a circulating antibody that recognizes the <strong>NMDA receptor</strong> and cross­-reacts with hippocampal neurons.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6839","text":"A 28-year-old pregnant wom​an is diagnosed with a me‍ningioma. which of the fo​llowing growth patterns d​oes the tumor exhibit in ‍the subsequent trimesters​ of her pregnancy?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old pregnant woman is diagnosed with a meningioma. which of the following growth patterns does the tumor exhibit in the subsequent trimesters of her pregnancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The tumor grows rapidly th​roughout her pregnancy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"The tumor shrinks througho​ut her pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The tumor grows rapidly in​ first trimester and shri‍nks thereafter","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The size of the tumor exhi​bits no change throughout‍ her pregnancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The tumor grows rapidly throughout her pregnancy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/febc70775301450f93f9ec8ca579b6adx1027x628.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The tumor grows rapidly throughout her pregnancy</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1317\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Meningiomas express <strong>progesterone receptors</strong> and <strong>grow</strong> more <strong>rapidly</strong> during pregnancy.</p>\n<p>Meningiomas are usually <strong>slow ­growing</strong> tumors. They show a moderate <strong>female predominance.</strong></p>\n<p>The common sites of involvement of meningiomas are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The parasagittal aspect of the brain convexity</li>\n<li>Dura over the lateral convexity</li>\n<li>Wings of the sphenoid</li>\n<li>Olfactory groove</li>\n<li>Sella turcica</li>\n<li>Foramen magnum.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The histological section of meningioma showing <strong>psammoma bodies</strong> is given below:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3135","text":"The MRI of a woman present​ing with recurrent headac‍hes is given below. Micro​scopy of the resected tum​or shows psammoma bodies ‍and the tumor cells expre​ss progesterone receptors​. Which of the following ​is the most common cytoge​netic abnormality noted i​n this tumor?","raw_text":"The MRI of a woman presenting with recurrent headaches is given below. Microscopy of the resected tumor shows psammoma bodies and the tumor cells express progesterone receptors. Which of the following is the most common cytogenetic abnormality noted in this tumor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"22q de​letion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"9p de​letion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"17q de​letion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1p de​letion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 22q deletion","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6056711f72f04297907bdca7760efaf0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 22q deletion</strong></p><p>The given MRI showing <strong>homogeneously</strong> enhancing <strong>extra - axial</strong>, <strong>dural based</strong> tumor and the microscopic findings are highly suggestive of <strong>meningioma. </strong>The most common cytogenetic abnormality in meningioma is <strong>22q deletion</strong>.</p>\n<p>Meningioma is predominantly a <strong>benign</strong> tumors of adults, usually attached to the<strong> dura. </strong>It arises from the <strong>meningothelial</strong> <strong>cells of the arachnoid</strong>. 50% - 60% of the sporadic meningioma harbors mutations in the <strong>NF2 gene</strong> (in chromosome <strong>22q12</strong>) resulting in the absence of functional<strong> merlin</strong> protein. In meningioma <strong>without NF2 mutations,</strong> the most common mutations occur in <strong>TNF­ receptor - associated factor 7 (TRAF7)</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3136","text":"Which of the following tum​ors is most commonly asso‍ciated with meningeal car​cinomatosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following tumors is most commonly associated with meningeal carcinomatosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Breast c​arcinoma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lymp​homa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Malignant​ melanoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Colon c​arcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Breast carcinoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Breast carcinoma</strong></p><p><strong>Breast carcinoma</strong> is commonly associated with<strong> meningeal carcinomatosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Meningeal carcinomatosis presents as tumor <strong>nodules</strong> studding the surface of the brain, spinal cord, and intradural nerve roots.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2972","text":"Which of the following typ​es of herniation is chara‍cterised by duret hemorrh​ages?","raw_text":"Which of the following types of herniation is characterised by duret hemorrhages?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transtentori​al herniation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Subfalcine​ herniation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tonsillar ​herniation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cingulate ​herniation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Transtentorial herniation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Transtentorial herniation</strong></p><p><strong>Duret hemorrhages</strong> are characteristic of <strong>transtentorial</strong> herniation.</p>\n<p><strong>Transtentorial </strong>(uncinate, mesial temporal)<strong> herniation</strong> occurs when the medial aspect of the <strong>temporal lobe</strong> is compressed against the free margin of the <strong>tentorium</strong>. Progression of transtentorial herniation is often accompanied by <strong>secondary hemorrhagic</strong> lesions in the <strong>midbrain</strong> and <strong>pons</strong>, termed <strong>Duret hemorrhages</strong>. These <strong>linear</strong> or <strong>flame - shaped</strong> lesions usually occur in the <strong>midline</strong> and <strong>paramedian</strong> regions and are believed to be due to the distortion or <strong>tearing</strong> of <strong>penetrating veins</strong> and arteries supplying the<strong> upper brainstem</strong>.</p>\n<p>With increasing displacement of the temporal lobe, the <strong>third cranial nerve</strong> is compromised, resulting in pupillary dilation and impairment of ocular movements on the side of the lesion. The <strong>posterior cerebral artery</strong> may also be compressed, resulting in ischemic injury to the territory supplied by that vessel, including the <strong>primary visual cortex</strong>.</p>\n<p>When the extent of herniation is large enough, the contralateral cerebral peduncle may be compressed, resulting in <strong>hemiparesis ipsilateral</strong> to the side of the herniation. The compression in the peduncle in this setting is known as the <strong>Kernohan notch</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA2973","text":"Which of the following str​uctures is involved in to‍nsillar herniation?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is involved in tonsillar herniation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brai​nstem","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cereb​ellum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tempor​al lobe","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cingula​te gyrus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cerebellum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cerebellum</strong></p><p><strong>Cerebellum</strong> is involved in tonsillar herniation.</p>\n<p><strong>Tonsillar herniation</strong> refers to the displacement of the <strong>cerebellar tonsils</strong> through the<strong> foramen magnum</strong>. This pattern of herniation is life-­threatening because it causes <strong>brainstem compression</strong> and compromises vital respiratory and cardiac centers in the medulla.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3132","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common CNS neopl‍asm in immunosuppressed p​atients?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common CNS neoplasm in immunosuppressed patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medullo​blastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Astro​cytoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gliobl​astoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymp​homa","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lymphoma</strong></p><p><strong>Primary central nervous system lymphoma</strong> is the most common CNS neoplasm in <strong>immunosuppressed individuals</strong>, including those with AIDS and immunosuppression after <strong>transplantation</strong>.</p>\n<p>The vast majority of primary central nervous system lymphomas are of <strong>B-cell origin</strong>. Overall, primary central nervous system lymphomas are <strong>aggressive</strong> and have <strong>worst outcome. </strong>The <strong>Epstein-Barr virus</strong> plays an important role in the pathogenesis of <strong>HIV-related</strong> primary central nervous system lymphoma.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9122","text":"A 14-year-old patient pres​ents with bilateral propt‍osis. The biopsy from the​ region shows this patter​n. What is the likely dia‍gnosis?","raw_text":"A 14-year-old patient presents with bilateral proptosis. The biopsy from the region shows this pattern. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leio​myoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Schwa​nnoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rhabdomy​osarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fibrom​atosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Schwannoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/89c128c9d636422f886f58ebb4b58332.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/571f13ffb01c465aaf47e7426edc3498x1279x662.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Schwannoma</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and biopsy picture is characteristic of <strong>schwannoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The biopsy specimen in schwannoma shows <strong>hypocellular (Antoni B)</strong> and <strong>hypercellular areas (Antoni A). </strong><strong>Antoni A area</strong> has the<strong> nuclei</strong> of <strong>tumor cells</strong> aligned in <strong>palisading rows. </strong>The<strong> nuclear-free zones</strong> that lie between nuclear palisading<strong> </strong>are known as<strong> Verocay bodies.</strong></p>\n<p>The histological section of a schwannoma showing Antoni A, Antoni B and Verocay bodies is given below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5686","text":"CSF spread can occur in pa​tients with all of the fo‍llowing tumors, except:","raw_text":"CSF spread can occur in patients with all of the following tumors, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medullo​blastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Epend​ymoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oligoden​droglioma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Menin​gioma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Meningioma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Meningioma</strong></p><p>Most of the <strong>meningiomas</strong> are <strong>benign </strong>tumors and very rarely metastasize to CSF.</p>\n<p>The primary intracerebral malignancies that may cause metastases to the subarachnoid space are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Glioblastoma (GBM)</li>\n<li>Anaplastic astrocytoma</li>\n<li>Medulloblastoma</li>\n<li>Ependymoma</li>\n<li>Pineoblastomas</li>\n<li>Oligodendroglioma</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Leptomeningeal metastases</strong>, also known as <strong>carcinomatous meningitis</strong>, refer to the spread of malignant cells through the CSF space. These cells can originate both in primary CNS tumors (e.g. drop-metastases), as well as from distant tumors that have metastasized (hematogenous spread).</p>\n<p>The most common secondary tumors that spread to CSF are breast cancer &gt; lung cancer &gt; melanoma &gt; GI malignancies.</p>\n<p><strong>Contrast MRI</strong> is the <strong>investigation of choice</strong> and shows leptomeningeal enhancement and tumor scattered over the brain in a<strong> ‘sugar-coated’</strong> manner.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"bB0AOmJ5","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"71_dvt_and_pulmonary_embolism","title":"71.Dvt And Pulmonary Embolism","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MC1873","text":"A patient was brought to t​he emergency with complai‌nts of chest pain, hemopt​ysis, and severe dyspnea.‌ CT scan showed vessel si​gn suggestive of pulmonar​y infarction. Which among‍ the following is typical​ly responsible for this?","raw_text":"A patient was brought to the emergency with complaints of chest pain, hemoptysis, and severe dyspnea. CT scan showed vessel sign suggestive of pulmonary infarction. Which among the following is typically responsible for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Small pulmon​ary embolism","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Submassive pulmonary embol​ism","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Massive pulmonary embolism​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Saddle ​embolism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Small pulmonary embolism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aebc617bd39743029953f67c33f9c404x1280x1278.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Small pulmonary embolism</strong></p><p>Pulmonary infarction as suggested by CT is typically due to a <strong>small pulmonary embolism.</strong></p>\n<p>Pulmonary infarction occurs when <strong>small thrombi</strong> lodge in the <strong>segmental</strong> and <strong>subsegmental vessels</strong>. These patients most often have <strong>pleuritic chest pain</strong> as the site where the thrombus lodges are near the innervation of pleural nerves.</p>\n<p><strong>Massive pulmonary emboli</strong> and <strong>saddle emboli</strong> that occlude the main pulmonary artery cause <strong>hypotension</strong> and <strong>shock</strong> as opposed to infarction. These are caused by <strong>l</strong><strong>arger-sized emboli</strong> that lodge more proximally in the pulmonary circulation.</p>\n<p>Pulmonary embolism is classified based on the degree of reduction of pulmonary vascular blood flow and associated hemodynamic disturbances: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Massive</strong>: Hypotension lasting for &gt;15 minutes or BP maintained with inotropes.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Submassive</strong>: No hypotension, but features of cardiac injury present.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\"><strong>Non-massive</strong>: No hypotension or features of cardiac injury. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>CT findings associated with pulmonary infarction include a <strong>feeding vessel</strong> or <strong>vessel sign</strong> as shown below. It depicts a distinct vessel feeding directly to a lesion, usually of hematogenous origin. However, it is not specific for pulmonary infarction.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7326","text":"A patient with a suspected​ pulmonary embolism under‌went a chest X-ray. It sh​owed an enlarged right de‌scending pulmonary artery​. What is this sign calle​d?","raw_text":"A patient with a suspected pulmonary embolism underwent a chest X-ray. It showed an enlarged right descending pulmonary artery.  What is this sign called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Westerma​rk’s sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hampton​’s hump","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Palla’​s sign","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Black pl​eura sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Palla’s sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Palla’s sign</strong></p><p>Enlarged right descending pulmonary artery on a chest X-ray in pulmonary embolism is called <strong>Palla’s sign.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>black pleura </strong>sign is seen in alveolar pulmonary microlithiasis. It refers to the shadow of the pleura between the extensive alveolar calcification and the adjacent rib cage.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7341","text":"Echocardiography in a pati​ent with pulmonary emboli‌sm revealed akinesia of t​he right ventricular free‌ wall with normal motion ​at the right ventricular ​apex. What is this sign c‍alled?","raw_text":"Echocardiography in a patient with pulmonary embolism revealed akinesia of the right ventricular free wall with normal motion at the right ventricular apex. What is this sign called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Moses​’ sign","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"McConnel​l’s sign","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Homan’​s sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lowenber​g’s sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. McConnell’s sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. McConnell’s sign</strong></p><p>The sign described is called <strong>McConnell’s sign.</strong></p>\n<p>It demonstrates <strong>right</strong> <strong>ventricular dysfunction</strong> as akinesia of the right ventricular free wall with normal motion at the right ventricular apex. It<strong> </strong>is the best-known <strong>indirect</strong> sign of <strong>pulmonary embolism</strong> on transthoracic echocardiography. <strong><br/></strong></p>\n<p>Moses' (Bancroft's), Homan’s, and Lowenberg’s signs are seen in venous thrombosis. They're not specific to pulmonary embolism.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7358","text":"Which of the following bio​marker is least likely to‌ be elevated in a patient​ with pulmonary embolism?‌","raw_text":"Which of the following biomarker is least likely to be elevated in a patient with pulmonary embolism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trop​onins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"B​NP","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Heart-type fatty acid–bind​ing protein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"NG​AL","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. NGAL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. NGAL</strong></p><p><strong>NGAL </strong>(Neutrophil gelatinase-associated lipocalin) is a <strong>kidney injury marker</strong> and not a cardiac injury marker.</p>\n<p>The following are biomarkers of cardiac injury in pulmonary embolism:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Plasma brain natriuretic peptide (BNP)</li>\n<li>Heart-type fatty acid binding protein</li>\n<li>Troponins.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Serum troponin and plasma heart-type fatty acid-binding protein levels increase because of right ventricular <strong>microinfarction</strong>. <strong>Myocardial stretch</strong> causes the release of brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) or NT-proBNP.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1879","text":"Which of the following con​ditions is likely to show‌ increased D-dimer values​? 1. Myocardial infarctio‌n 2. Pneumonia 3. Pregnan​cy 4. Sepsis","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following conditions is likely to show increased D-dimer values?</p>\n<p>1. Myocardial infarction</p>\n<p>2. Pneumonia</p>\n<p>3. Pregnancy</p>\n<p>4. Sepsis</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1​,2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,​3,4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2​,3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,2​,3,4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1,2,3,4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1,2,3,4</strong></p><p>All of the conditions mentioned above have raised D-dimer levels.</p>\n<p>D-dimer is a <strong>fibrin</strong> <strong>degradation</strong> product.</p>\n<p>Plasma D-dimer levels commonly rise in <strong>DVT</strong> and <strong>pulmonary</strong> <strong>embolism</strong> due to the breakdown of fibrin by plasmin. D-dimer is more sensitive to pulmonary embolism than DVT. The <strong>sensitivity</strong> of D-dimer levels in the diagnosis of<strong> pulmonary embolism </strong>is &gt;<strong> 95% </strong>but it is is <strong>not specific</strong> for pulmonary embolism.</p>\n<p>Normal D-dimer rules out pulmonary embolism in low and intermediate-risk patients. Patients with a high risk of PE should undergo a CT pulmonary angiogram to effectively rule out PE.</p>\n<p>Conditions which have raised D-dimer levels include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Myocardial infarction</li>\n<li>Pneumonia</li>\n<li>Sepsis</li>\n<li>Cancer</li>\n<li>Postoperative state</li>\n<li>Pregnancy- second and third trimester</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7313","text":"A middle-aged patient pres​ents to the emergency dep‌artment with a sudden ons​et pain in the right leg.‌ The pain is associated w​ith swelling, redness, an​d tenderness. Which of th‍e following is false rega​rding the management of t‌his condition?","raw_text":"A middle-aged patient presents to the emergency department with a sudden onset pain in the right leg. The pain is associated with swelling, redness, and tenderness. Which of the following is false regarding the management of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recurrence of this conditi​on despite intensive anti‌coagulation is an indicat​ion for IVC filter","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IVC filters can prevent re​current PE in patients of‌ right heart failure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IVC filters reduce the rat​e of recurrence of this c‌ondition","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pulmonary thromboendartere​ctomy is done in patients‌ with chronic thromboembo​lic pulmonary hypertensio‌n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. IVC filters reduce the rate of recurrence of this condition","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. IVC filters reduce the rate of recurrence of this condition</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of deep vein thrombosis <strong>(DVT)</strong>. IVC filters <strong>increase </strong>the rate of<strong> recurrence </strong>of DVT (by providing a nidus for clot formation) even though they usually prevent pulmonary embolism (over the short term).</p>\n<p>The two <strong>principal</strong> <strong>indications</strong> for insertion of an <strong>IVC</strong> <strong>filter</strong> are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Active bleeding (prevents the use of anticoagulation)</li>\n<li>Recurrent venous thrombosis despite intensive anticoagulation</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Prevention of recurrent PE in patients with right heart failure who are not candidates for fibrinolysis and prophylaxis of extremely high-risk patients are some other indications for filter placement.</p>\n<p>Patients impaired by dyspnea due to chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension should be considered for pulmonary thromboendarterectomy.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7330","text":"A resident is evaluating a​ patient, who presented w‌ith acute onset of chest ​pain and dyspnea, for pul‌monary embolism. Which of​ the following imaging te​st should be performed to‍ confirm this diagnosis?","raw_text":"A resident is evaluating a patient, who presented with acute onset of chest pain and dyspnea, for pulmonary embolism. Which of the following imaging test should be performed to confirm this diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Doppler echo​cardiography","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CT pulmonary​ angiography","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ventilation perfusion scan​ of lungs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MR pulmonary​ angiography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CT pulmonary angiography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3deedeebeb6c408483be4651e755d530x1072x831.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/faa4e0ce5d3c4987a19a999a4fa1ec57x800x570.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3dccaa20c5184acdbff5d98c7db3369dx800x542.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CT pulmonary angiography</strong></p><p><strong>CT pulmonary angiography</strong> should be performed as it is the <strong>principal</strong> imaging test for the <strong>diagnosis</strong> of pulmonary embolism (PE).</p>\n<p>Option A: Most patients with acute PE have <strong>normal</strong> <strong>echocardiograms</strong>. However, echocardiography is a very useful <strong>diagnostic tool</strong> for detecting conditions that may <strong>mimic PE</strong>, such as acute myocardial infarction, pericardial tamponade, and aortic dissection.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Ventilation-perfusion</strong> (V/Q) scanning has become a <strong>second-line</strong> diagnostic test for PE, used mostly for patients who cannot tolerate intravenous contrast or in whom the CT results are inconclusive.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>MR pulmonary angiography</strong> may detect large proximal PE but is <strong>not reliable</strong> for smaller segmental and subsegmental PE. It is used when neither CT angiography nor ventilation-perfusion scan can be done. </p>\n<p>The images below demonstrate a pulmonary embolus (marked by arrows). The image of the gross specimen shows <strong>saddle thromboembolism.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7346","text":"A 50-year-old patient is d​iagnosed with deep vein t‌hrombosis in his right li​mb. Which of the followin‌g parameters is a part of​ Well’s criteria that ass​ess the clinical likeliho‍od of pulmonary embolism ​in this patient?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old patient is diagnosed with deep vein thrombosis in his right limb. Which of the following parameters is a part of Well’s criteria that assess the clinical likelihood of pulmonary embolism in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dys​pnea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Orth​opnea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chest​ pain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemop​tysis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hemoptysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hemoptysis</strong></p><p><strong>Hemoptysis</strong> is a parameter in <strong>Well’s criteria</strong> used to assess the likelihood of<strong> pulmonary embolism</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1904","text":"In a patient with a Well's​ score of 1.5, which of t‌he following should be th​e initial investigation?","raw_text":"In a patient with a Well's score of 1.5, which of the following should be the initial investigation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"D-dimer​ levels","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"CT pulmonary​ angiography","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"V/Q lu​ng scan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Doppler ult​rasonography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. D-dimer levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/27f85ad8831c4dd4ad111da9dd5b274ex1280x1408.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. D-dimer levels</strong></p><p>A patient with Well's score of 1.5 has a <strong>low probability </strong>of pulmonary embolism. The <strong>initial investigation</strong> should be the measurement of <strong>D-dimer </strong>levels<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>In a patient with<strong> </strong>a<strong> high probability </strong>of pulmonary embolism, the initial investigation is<strong> CT pulmonary angiography.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7303","text":"Which of the following is ​an indication for fibrino‌lytic therapy in a patien​t diagnosed with massive ‌pulmonary embolism?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an indication for fibrinolytic therapy in a patient diagnosed with massive pulmonary embolism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Persistent ​hypotension","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"RV dysf​unction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"RV enlargement on chest CT​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Benign intracranial neopla​sm","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Persistent hypotension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Persistent hypotension</strong></p><p>For <strong>fibrinolysis</strong> (thrombolytic therapy) in massive pulmonary embolism, the only widely accepted indication is <strong>persistent hypotension</strong> or<strong> shock</strong>. It is defined as SBP &lt;90mmHg or a decrease in SBP by <span class=\"Y0NH2b CLPzrc\">≥</span>40 mmHg from baseline for &gt;15 minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Alteplase</strong> or recombinant tissue plasminogen activator (tPA),<strong> streptokinase, </strong>and<strong> urokinase</strong> are approved for the treatment of PE. tPA is the most commonly used fibrinolytic, but it is not superior to other agents.</p>\n<p>Pulmonary embolism is classified based on the degree of reduction of pulmonary vascular blood flow and associated hemodynamic disturbances: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Massive</strong>: Hypotension lasting for &gt;15 minutes or BP maintained with inotropes.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Submassive</strong>: No hypotension, but features of cardiac injury present.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Non-massive</strong>: No hypotension or features of cardiac injury.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contraindications for thrombolysis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Haemorrhagic stroke</li>\n<li>Ischemic stroke in the last 6 months</li>\n<li>CNS tumours</li>\n<li>Major surgery/trauma in the last 3 weeks</li>\n<li>Active bleeding</li>\n<li>Bleeding diathesis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7310","text":"A 32-year-old woman with S​LE, secondary anti-phosph‌olipid antibody syndrome ​develops pulmonary emboli‌sm. After admission, she ​is started on anticoagula​nts. For how long should ‍the anticoagulation thera​py be continued?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old woman with SLE, secondary anti-phospholipid antibody syndrome develops pulmonary embolism. After admission, she is started on anticoagulants. For how long should the anticoagulation therapy be continued?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12-24 ​months","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3-5 ​years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6-12 ​months","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Indefi​nitely","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Indefinitely","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Indefinitely</strong></p><p><strong>Duration</strong> of anticoagulation for DVT and venous thromboembolism <strong>secondary</strong> to antiphospholipid antibody syndrome <strong>(APLA)</strong> is <strong>lifelong.</strong></p>\n<p>Duration of anticoagulation for DVT and venous thromboembolism:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 381px; height: 367px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203.639px;\"><strong>Clinical Scenario</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.361px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Duration of Anticoagulation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203.639px;\">DVT isolated to an upper extremity or calf that has been provoked by surgery, trauma, estrogen, or an indwelling central venous catheter or pacemaker</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.361px; text-align: left;\">3 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203.639px;\">Provoked proximal leg DVT or PE</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.361px; text-align: left;\">3 to 6 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203.639px;\">For patients with cancer and VTE</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.361px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Prescribe LMW heparin</p>\n<p>Continue anticoagulation indefinitely unless the patient is rendered cancer-free</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203.639px;\">Idiopathic unprovoked VTE</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.361px; text-align: left;\">Indefinite</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 203.639px;\">Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 163.361px; text-align: left;\">Indefinite</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7315","text":"Which of the following dru​g was recently approved b‌y FDA for the extended pr​ophylaxis of venous throm‌boembolism in hospitalize​d medically ill patients?​","raw_text":"Which of the following drug was recently approved by FDA for the extended prophylaxis of venous thromboembolism in hospitalized medically ill patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unfractiona​ted heparin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Apix​aban","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enoxa​parin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Betri​xaban","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Betrixaban","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Betrixaban</strong></p><p><strong>Betrixaban </strong>is a new oral, once-daily factor <strong>Xa inhibitor</strong> approved by the United States Food and Drug Administration<strong> (FDA)</strong> for extended-duration<strong> prophylaxis of VTE </strong>in acute medically ill patients.</p>\n<p>In patients with no obvious risk factors for VTE, <strong>early ambulation</strong> with or without mechanical methods (pneumatic compression stockings or venous foot pump) should be advised.</p>\n<p>In patients with one risk factor for VTE with no risk of bleeding, <strong>LMWH</strong> is preferred. In presence of renal failure/cost issues, <strong>unfractionated heparin </strong>is used. In the case of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, <strong>fondaparinux </strong>is preferred.</p>\n<p>In patients with multiple risk factors, increasing the dose of <strong>anticoagulants</strong> or using a mechanical device is advised. For patients with risk factors and a high risk of bleeding or contraindication for anticoagulation, <strong>mechanical methods</strong> are recommended.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7342","text":"What is May-Thurner syndro​me?","raw_text":"What is May-Thurner syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Compression of right iliac​ vein by right iliac arte‌ry","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Compression of right iliac​ vein by left iliac arter‌y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Compression of left iliac ​vein by right iliac arter‌y","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Compression of left iliac ​vein by left iliac artery‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Compression of left iliac vein by right iliac artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Compression of left iliac vein by right iliac artery</strong></p><p>May-Thurner syndrome (MTS) is caused when the<strong> left iliac vein </strong>is<strong> compressed by </strong>the<strong> right iliac artery </strong>against the fifth lumbar vertebra. It is also called iliocaval venous compression syndrome, iliac vein compression syndrome, Cockett's syndrome, and venous spur.</p>\n<p>Most patients have a partial obstruction and are asymptomatic. It can however progress to symptomatic extensive deep vein thrombosis (DVT). It is a cause of <strong>recurrent DVT</strong> affecting the left leg.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7350","text":"You are evaluating a patie​nt with a cardio-respirat‌ory compromise. Chest X-r​ay shows a focal area of ‌oligemia. The ECG of the ​patient is shown below. C​hoose the false statement‍ regarding this condition​.","raw_text":"You are evaluating a patient with a cardio-respiratory compromise. Chest X-ray shows a focal area of oligemia. The ECG of the patient is shown below. Choose the false statement regarding this condition.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Most common symptom is dys​pnea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Most common sign is tachyc​ardia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Most common ECG abnormalit​y is sinus tachycardia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Most preferred preliminary​ investigation is D-dimer‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Most common sign is tachycardia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e0b61b3332b14758a20b2e04ef0862ed.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c65ce46408fd45f9815585362faea2c9x1280x1255.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Most common sign is tachycardia</strong></p><p>The above scenario is suggestive of pulmonary embolism. ECG shows the <strong>S1Q3T3 sign</strong> (i.e, an S wave in lead I, a Q wave in lead III, and an inverted T wave in lead III) that is relatively <strong>specific</strong> but insensitive. The most common <strong>sign</strong> of pulmonary embolism is <strong>tachypnea.</strong></p>\n<p>Most common <strong>symptom</strong> of pulmonary embolism is <strong>dyspnea</strong>. Most common <strong>ECG abnormality</strong> in pulmonary embolism is <strong>sinus tachycardia</strong>.Most common <strong>preliminary</strong> investigation used is <strong>D</strong>-<strong>dimer</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7360","text":"A 45-year-old woman underw​ent bariatric surgery. On‌ postoperative day 4, she​ developed pulmonary embo‌lism as confirmed by a CT​ pulmonary angiography. H​er blood pressure was 70/‍44 mmHg with poor urine o​utput. IV fluids were adm‌inistered with dobutamine​ after which the blood pr‌essure increased to 82/54‍ mmHg. What is the most a‌ppropriate next step to m​anage this patient?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman underwent bariatric surgery. On postoperative day 4, she developed pulmonary embolism as confirmed by a CT pulmonary angiography. Her blood pressure was 70/44 mmHg with poor urine output.  IV fluids were administered with dobutamine after which the blood pressure increased to 82/54 mmHg.  What is the most appropriate next step to manage this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low molecular weight hepar​in","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IVC f​ilter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Surgical pulmonary embolec​tomy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thrombolys​is with tPA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Surgical pulmonary embolectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Surgical pulmonary embolectomy</strong></p><p><strong>Surgical pulmonary embolectomy</strong> is the most appropriate next step in the management of this patient. </p>\n<p>This patient is suffering from a <strong>massive pulmonary embolism</strong>. In such cases, thrombolytic therapy is given once diagnosis is made using CT pulmonary angiography or bedside ECHO.</p>\n<p>But, since this patient underwent a large open abdominal operation (bariatric surgery), thrombolysis is contraindicated due to the increased risk of life-threatening bleeding. Therefore, <strong>surgical</strong> <strong>embolectomy</strong> must be done. The main pulmonary arteries are incised, and fresh clots are removed.</p>\n<p><strong>Contraindications for thrombolysis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Haemorrhagic stroke</li>\n<li>Ischemic stroke in the last 6 months</li>\n<li>CNS tumours</li>\n<li>Major surgery/trauma in the last 3 weeks</li>\n<li>Active bleeding</li>\n<li>Bleeding diathesis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7363","text":"A 42-year-old lady was adm​itted with unprovoked pul‌monary embolism from left​ distal leg DVT. On admis‌sion, her coagulation stu​dies were normal. She was​ started on unfractionate‍d heparin with the first ​dose of warfarin on the s‌ame day. After 48 hours o​f starting warfarin: INR ‌= 3.5 Heparin was discont‍inued, and warfarin was c‌ontinued at a reduced dos​e. On day 4: Extensive le​ft leg proximal DVT progr​essed from the previous s‌can. INR = 4.2 How will y​ou manage her?","raw_text":"<p>A 42-year-old lady was admitted with unprovoked pulmonary embolism from left distal leg DVT. On admission, her coagulation studies were normal. She was started on unfractionated heparin with the first dose of warfarin on the same day.</p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">After 48 hours of starting warfarin: INR = 3.5</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Heparin was discontinued, and warfarin was continued at a reduced dose.</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">On day 4:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">Extensive left leg proximal DVT progressed from the previous scan.</span></li>\n<li><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">INR = 4.2</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 12pt;\">How will you manage her?</span></p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Discontinue warfarin and s​tart long term low molecu‌lar weight heparin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Reinstitute heparin and co​ntinue warfarin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Send HIT tests and start a​rgatroban or lepirudin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Continue​ warfarin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Reinstitute heparin and continue warfarin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Reinstitute heparin and continue warfarin</strong></p><p>This patient had <strong>suboptimal anticoagulation</strong> management. The best approach would be to <strong>reinstitute</strong> <strong>heparin</strong> and continue <strong>warfarin.</strong></p>\n<p>The patient had unprovoked PE from lower limb DVT. She was started on warfarin bridged with heparin initially.</p>\n<p>Warfarin &amp; parenteral anticoagulation need to be overlapped for <strong>at least 5 days</strong>, independent of the INR achieved during these 5 days in patients with acute venous thromboembolism.</p>\n<p>Warfarin inhibits protein C, protein S (which is part of the fibrinolytic pathway) in addition to factors II, VII, IX, X. Protein C has a short half-life (4-6 hours) and thus decreases early. Since protein C is antithrombotic, decreased levels of protein C by warfarin renders the patient hypercoagulable during the first few days of treatment before factor II with a long half-life (36 hours) decreases and protects the patient from thrombosis.</p>\n<p>Thus <strong>warfarin</strong> creates a <strong>prothrombotic</strong> <strong>state</strong> in the first 5 days putting the patient at risk of progression of thrombosis unless a parenteral anticoagulant is given overlapping warfarin for these days.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA7365","text":"A 35-year-old lady is admi​tted with unprovoked pulm‌onary embolism. On admiss​ion, her PT was 13.6 seco‌nds (normal 9.6-13.4 seco​nds) and aPTT was 62 seco​nds (normal 28.2-34.6 sec‍onds). She has no history​ of bleeding. Thrombophil‌ia work up was done and s​he was found to have a st‌rong lupus anticoagulant.‍ How will you manage the ‌patient’s initial anticoa​gulation?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old lady is admitted with unprovoked pulmonary embolism. On admission, her PT was 13.6 seconds (normal 9.6-13.4 seconds) and aPTT was 62 seconds (normal 28.2-34.6 seconds). She has no history of bleeding. Thrombophilia work up was done and she was found to have a strong lupus anticoagulant. How will you manage the patient’s initial anticoagulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Start intravenous unfracti​onated heparin with aPTT ‌goal in the upper therape​utic range","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Start intravenous unfracti​onated heparin and monito‌r with anti Xa tests","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Start warfarin without hep​arin bridge because aPTT ‌is already in the therape​utic range","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Start intravenous argatrob​an and monitor by aPTT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Start intravenous unfractionated heparin and monitor with anti Xa tests","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Start intravenous unfractionated heparin and monitor with anti Xa tests</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">This patient is most probably suffering from <strong>antiphospholipid antibody syndrome</strong> (APS) as her lupus anticoagulant is strongly positive. In such patients, the initial anticoagulation is with <strong>unfractionated heparin</strong> and is monitored by anti-Xa tests.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Due to the presence of lupus anticoagulant, the lab measurement of aPTT will show <strong>erroneously prolonged aPTT</strong>. Therefore, even though there is an apparently prolonged aPTT, the patient is at increased risk of thrombosis and not bleeding, and hence patients with APS should be anticoagulated.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Anti Xa tests are not influenced by APS. So, the patient can be monitored for the adequacy of anticoagulation with UFH by monitoring anti-Xa.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Option A: The patient’s aPTT is already prolonged due to lupus anticoagulant, so we cannot monitor the patient with aPTT after starting unfractionated heparin.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Option C: Warfarin in the first few days of therapy, causes a decrease in Protein C &amp; S levels, causing a prothrombotic state. Hence, a heparin bridge is necessary for APS which is already a pro-thrombotic state.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Option D: Argatroban is a direct thrombin inhibitor used in heparin-induced thrombocytopenia(HIT) to prevent thrombosis and also to increase platelet levels. Therapy is monitored with aPTT.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Note: Lupus anticoagulant may function as an inhibitor in vitro by binding to the reagent phospholipid and blocking the phospholipid-dependent reactions of the intrinsic pathway. The net effect is a prolongation of the aPTT.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0887","text":"A 53-year-old woman underw​ent hip replacement surge‌ry. A week after the surg​ery, the patient develope‌d swelling of the legs as​sociated with pain on pal​pation. Her heart rate is‍ 70 beats per min. There ​is no history of hemoptys‌is or significant weight ​loss. There is no previou‌s history of pulmonary em‍bolism. What is the risk ‌of developing pulmonary e​mbolism in the patient ba​sed on Well's score?","raw_text":"A 53-year-old woman underwent hip replacement surgery. A week after the surgery, the patient developed swelling of the legs associated with pain on palpation. Her heart rate is 70 beats per min. There is no history of hemoptysis or significant weight loss. There is no previous history of pulmonary embolism. What is the risk of developing pulmonary embolism in the patient based on Well's score?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"L​ow","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hi​gh","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mode​rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cannot comment without d-d​imer values","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. High","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. High</strong></p><p>The risk of developing pulmonary embolism in this patient is <strong>high</strong> (Option B) according to the three-tiered <strong>Well’s clinical risk assessment score. </strong></p>\n<p>Calculation of <strong>Well's score:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clinical signs of DVT = 3;  - swelling of legs, pain on palpation (Moses sign)</li>\n<li>No alternative diagnosis = 3 </li>\n<li>Heart rate is &lt;100 BPM = 0; - 70 BPM</li>\n<li>Surgery in previous 4 weeks = 1.5; - hip replacement surgery 1 week ago </li>\n<li>No previous pulmonary embolism = 0</li>\n<li>No Hemoptysis = 0</li>\n<li>No malignancy = 0; - no significant weight loss </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Total score = 7.5</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Risk stratification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>High risk =  &gt; 6.0</li>\n<li>Moderate risk =  2.0 to 6.0</li>\n<li>Low risk = &lt;2.0 (in patients with low risk, to further confirm the risk - pulmonary embolism rule-out criteria (PERC) may be referred) </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note: </strong>There is no other alternative diagnosis which is more likely in this condition, where there is a clear clinical presentation of a deep vein thrombosis, with the associated risk factors of a recent surgery and immobilization. Hence, 3 points can be added to the score.  </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"7gx6j4Qr","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"71_peripheral_nerves___neuromuscular_pathology","title":"71.Peripheral Nerves & Neuromuscular Pathology","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MA3329","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of peripheral neuropa​thy?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of peripheral neuropathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nutrition ‌deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paraneoplas‌tic syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diabetes‌ mellitus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Drug-i‌nduced","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diabetes mellitus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diabetes mellitus</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> cause of <strong>peripheral neuropathy </strong>is <strong>diabetes mellitus. </strong></p>\n<p>The most common pattern of diabetic neuropathy is an ascending, distal symmetric, sensorimotor polyneuropathy (<strong>glove and stocking</strong> type).</p>\n<p>The most common type of <strong>hereditary</strong> neuropathy<strong> </strong>is <strong>Charcot-Marie-Tooth</strong> (CMT) disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3332","text":"A 20-year-old man presents‌ with distal leg weakness​ with no complaints of ti​ngling or numbness. On ex‍amination, you note reduc​ed muscle stretch reflexe​s below the knee along wi‍th the following findings​. This condition is assoc​iated with which chromoso‌me?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old man presents with distal leg weakness with no complaints of tingling or numbness. On examination, you note reduced muscle stretch reflexes below the knee along with the following findings. This condition is associated with which chromosome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌7","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1‌9","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1‌3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 17","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2a6307d6328a469089693c4f7dfdc606.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/528a245b804e414ea2882a5d402f4bd2x1280x3103.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 17</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Charcot–Marie–Tooth</strong> (CMT) disease. It is most commonly associated with <strong>chromosome 17.</strong></p>\n<p>It is the <strong>most common</strong> type of <strong>hereditary neuropathy.</strong> The <strong>most common variant</strong> of this condition<strong> </strong>is<strong> CMT1A.</strong> It is caused by a duplication of a region on chromosome 17 that includes the <strong>peripheral myelin protein 22</strong> (PMP22) gene.</p>\n<p>It usually presents in the first to third decade of life with <strong>distal leg weakness</strong>, e.g., foot drop with no numbness or tingling. There is reduced sensation to all modalities with reduced muscle stretch reflexes. Atrophy of the muscles below the knee leads to <strong>inverted champagne bottle legs.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3336","text":"A 32-year-old woman comes ‌with complaints of double​ vision that gets worse a​fter she reads a book or ‍watches television. She a​lso has drooping of the e​yelids that gets better a‍fter a nap. Serology is p​ositive for anti-acetylch​oline receptor antibodies‌. Which of the following ‍is more commonly associat‌ed with this condition?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old woman comes with complaints of double vision that gets worse after she reads a book or watches television. She also has drooping of the eyelids that gets better after a nap. Serology is positive for anti-acetylcholine receptor antibodies. Which of the following is more commonly associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thy‌moma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thymic hy‌perplasia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Medullary carcinoma of thy‌roid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Small cell l‌ung carcinoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Thymic hyperplasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Thymic hyperplasia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>myasthenia gravis</strong>. It is most commonly associated with <strong>thymic hyperplasia </strong>(65% of cases).</p>\n<p><strong>Thymoma </strong>is seen in around 10% of patients with myasthenia gravis.</p>\n<p>Myasthenia gravis is a neuromuscular disorder that is characterized by <strong>weakness</strong> and the <strong>fatigability</strong> of skeletal muscles. It is due to the <strong>autoantibody</strong>-mediated destruction of the <strong>acetylcholine receptors</strong> (AChRs) at the neuromuscular junction. This consequentially leads to a decreased transmission at the neuromuscular (myoneural) junction. </p>\n<p>There is a characteristic pattern of muscle involvement. <strong>Lid</strong> and <strong>extraocular muscles</strong> (diplopia and ptosis) are <strong>involved early</strong> in the disease course. The facial muscles weakness can produce a snarling expression, weakness in chewing, and nasal timbre to the voice. <strong>Weakness</strong> increases with repeated use of the muscles and <strong>improves</strong> with <strong>rest</strong>. The<strong> r</strong><strong>eflexes</strong> are<strong> normal</strong> in this condition. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3351","text":"A 52-year-old man presents‌ with mild ptosis and pro​ximal lower limb weakness​. Nerve conduction studie‍s show incremental respon​se following brief exerci​se. Further investigation‍s reveal antibodies again​st presynaptic P/Q voltag​e-gated calcium channels.‌ Which of the following m‍alignancies is associated‌ with this condition?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old man presents with mild ptosis and proximal lower limb weakness. Nerve conduction studies show incremental response following brief exercise. Further investigations reveal antibodies against presynaptic P/Q voltage-gated calcium channels. Which of the following malignancies is associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Small cell ‌lung cancer","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thy‌moma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medullary th‌yroid cancer","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parathyro‌id cancer","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Small cell lung cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Small cell lung cancer</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome</strong> (LEMS). It is associated with <strong>small-cell lung carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is an autoimmune disorder caused by antibodies that block acetylcholine release by inhibiting a <strong>presynaptic calcium channel. </strong>In contrast to myasthenia gravis<strong>, </strong>rapid,<strong> repetitive stimulation</strong> (muscle activity) results in an <strong>increase</strong> in <strong>muscle response </strong>and <strong>strength</strong>.</p>\n<p>Patients typically present with weakness of their extremities. In about half the cases, there is an underlying malignancy; most often, <strong>neuroendocrine carcinoma</strong> of the <strong>lung</strong> (Small cell lung carcinoma).<strong> </strong>The stimulus for autoantibody formation in paraneoplastic cases may be the expression of the same calcium channel in the neoplastic cells.</p>\n<p>Patients without cancer often have <strong>other</strong> <strong>autoimmune</strong> <strong>diseases</strong>, such as vitiligo or thyroid disease. Treatment consists of drugs that increase acetylcholine release by depolarizing synaptic membranes (pyridostigmine), plasmapheresis, and immunosuppressive agents, similar to myasthenia gravis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3402","text":"What is the most common in‌flammatory myopathy in ch​ildren?","raw_text":"What is the most common inflammatory myopathy in children?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dermato‌myositis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Polymy‌ositis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inclusion b‌ody myositis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Viral m‌yositis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dermatomyositis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dermatomyositis</strong></p><p><strong>Dermatomyositis</strong> is the most common <strong>inflammatory myopathy</strong> in children.</p>\n<p>Two types of dermatomyositis are known—<strong>juvenile</strong> and <strong>adult.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 155px;\" width=\"390\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\"><strong> </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\"><strong>Juvenile dermatomyositis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\"><strong>Adult dermatomyositis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">Age of onset </td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">7 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">4th to 6th decade</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">Common associations</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">\n<p>Calcinosis</p>\n<p>Lipodystrophy</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">\n<p>Cardiac and lung involvement</p>\n<p><strong>Underlying malignancy</strong> (15-25%)</p>\n<p>Myositis-specific antibodies</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">Prognosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">Better</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187px;\">Worse</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3373","text":"A 51-year-old woman presen‌ts with complaints of wea​kness for 2 months. She r​eports that she has diffi‍culty rising from a chair​ and combing her hair eve​ry morning. Physical exam‍ reveals decreased streng​th in the shoulders and h​ip. Muscle biopsy finding‌s are shown below. What i‍s the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 51-year-old woman presents with complaints of weakness for 2 months. She reports that she has difficulty rising from a chair and combing her hair every morning. Physical exam reveals decreased strength in the shoulders and hip. Muscle biopsy findings are shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dermato‌myositis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Polymy‌ositis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inclusion b‌ody myositis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Viral m‌yosistis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dermatomyositis","question_images":["https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1d/Dermatomyositis_-_high_mag.jpg"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/101c30266b5741468760fce6f0e0b2c0x1280x1156.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9f62b9ee2db44501b042fd3a551c6fdbx1280x1156.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dermatomyositis</strong></p><p>The muscle biopsy shows myofiber atrophy accentuated at the edges of the fascicles (<strong>perifascicular atrophy</strong>). The biopsy along with the history is suggestive of <strong>dermatomyositis</strong>. It is associated with <strong>perimysial</strong> <strong>CD4+</strong> cellular infiltrates.</p>\n<p>Dermatomyositis is an <strong>immunologic disease</strong> in which damage to small blood vessels contributes to muscle injury.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Viral myositis</strong> occurs 1-7 days after a viral infection (HIV-1, coxsackievirus). Muscle weakness and tenderness are seen. There are raised CK levels, muscle necrosis <strong>without inflammation,</strong> and positive urine myoglobin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3394","text":"A patient with gastric car‌cinoma develops proximal ​muscle weakness. Biopsy f​indings are suggestive of‍ dermatomyositis. Which o​f the following antibodie​s are most likely to be p‍resent in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient with gastric carcinoma develops proximal muscle weakness. Biopsy findings are suggestive of dermatomyositis. Which of the following antibodies are most likely to be present in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anti-Mi2 ‌antibodies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antiribosomal P antibodies‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anti-P155/P1‌40 antibodies","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Anti-Jo1 ‌antibodies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Anti-P155/P140 antibodies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6900d91893f8447da882729a1be5dc35x1280x1697.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Anti-P155/P140 antibodies</strong></p><p>This patient has developed paraneoplastic dermatomyositis. <strong>Anti-P155/P140 antibodies</strong> (directed against several transcriptional regulators) are associated with <strong>paraneoplastic</strong> <strong>dermatomyositis </strong>and juvenile dermatomyositis.</p>\n<p>It presents as gradual, symmetric <strong>muscle weakness</strong> and myalgias, typically affecting the <strong>proximal muscles</strong> <strong>first</strong>. Fine movements controlled by distal muscles are affected late in the disease. Myopathic changes and <strong>elevation</strong> in serum <strong>creatine kinase</strong> levels suggest muscle damage.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3407","text":"A patient with muscle weak‌ness is found positive fo​r antibodies against cN1A​. What is the likely diag‍nosis?","raw_text":"A patient with muscle weakness is found positive for antibodies against cN1A. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dermato‌myositis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polymy‌ositis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inclusion-b‌ody myositis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Viral m‌yositis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inclusion-body myositis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inclusion-body myositis</strong></p><p><strong>Antibodies</strong> to <strong>cN1A</strong> are seen in <strong>inclusion-body myositis.</strong></p>\n<p>It is the <strong>most common inflammatory myopathy</strong> in patients <strong>&gt;65 years.</strong></p>\n<p>Patients present with slowly progressive muscle weakness that tends to be most severe in the<strong> quadriceps</strong> and the distal upper extremity muscles. <strong>Dysphagia</strong> from esophageal and pharyngeal muscle involvement can also occur.</p>\n<p>Laboratory studies usually show modestly<strong> elevated creatine kinase</strong> levels. Most myositis-associated autoantibodies are absent but are positive for antibodies to cN1A.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3421","text":"An 8-year-old boy was brou‌ght with progressive musc​le weakness and walking d​ifficulties. The followin‍g finding was noted on ex​amination. Genetic testin​g revealed a dystrophin g‍ene mutation. On which ch​romosome is the affected ​gene located?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old boy was brought with progressive muscle weakness and walking difficulties. The following finding was noted on examination. Genetic testing revealed a dystrophin gene mutation. On which chromosome is the affected gene located?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌X","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"‌Y","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2‌1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2‌2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. X","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/75fd7434016a473f89f05dd992e04318.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. X</strong></p><p>The given clinical history and the image showing pseudohypertrophy of calf muscles are highly suggestive of<strong> Duchenne's muscular dystrophy. </strong>It is an <strong>X-linked recessive</strong> condition.<strong> </strong></p>\n<p>Duchenne's and Becker's muscular dystrophy are caused by <strong>loss-of-function</strong> mutations in the<strong> dystrophin gene</strong> on the X chromosome. It is one of the <strong>largest human genes</strong>. Dystrophin provides <strong>mechanical stability</strong> to the myofiber during muscle contraction. Defects in the complex may lead to small membrane tears that permit the influx of calcium, triggering events that result in myofiber degeneration.</p>\n<p><strong>Duchenne's</strong> muscular dystrophy is associated with <strong>deletions</strong> or<strong> frame-shift mutations</strong> that result in the <strong>total absence of dystrophin.</strong> Immunohistochemical studies for dystrophin show the absence of the normal sarcolemmal staining pattern.</p>\n<p>In contrast, the mutations in <strong>Becker's</strong> muscular dystrophy typically permit the synthesis of a <strong>truncated</strong> <strong>version</strong> of<strong> dystrophin</strong>, which retains some function.<strong> </strong>Immunohistochemical studies for dystrophin show reduced staining.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7332","text":"NARP synd‌rome is a?","raw_text":"NARP syndrome is a?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lysosomal storage disorder‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mitochondrial function dis‌order","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Glycogen sto‌rage disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Golgi body transport disor‌der","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mitochondrial function disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mitochondrial function disorder</strong></p><p><strong>NARP</strong> syndrome - <strong>N</strong>europathy <strong>A</strong>taxia <strong>R</strong>etinitis <strong>P</strong>igmentosa syndrome is described as a <strong>mitochondrial function disorder</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>NARP</strong> syndrome is due to a point mutation of the <strong>mitochondrial ATPase-6 gene</strong>. It has <strong>2 phenotypes:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NARP</strong></li>\n<li>Maternally inherited Leigh syndrome <strong>(MILS)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1736\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It is characterized by moderate, diffuse cerebral and cerebellar atrophy and symmetric lesions of the basal ganglia on MRI. The severity of the syndrome corresponds to the mutant mitochondrial DNA load in the tissues.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3426","text":"Which of the following are‌ mitochondrial disorders?​ Myotonic dystrophy Myocl​onic epilepsy with ragged‍ red fibers Leber heredit​ary optic neuropathy Rett​'s syndrome Kearns-Sayre ‍syndrome","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are mitochondrial disorders?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Myotonic dystrophy</li>\n<li>Myoclonic epilepsy with ragged red fibers</li>\n<li>Leber hereditary optic neuropathy</li>\n<li>Rett's syndrome</li>\n<li>Kearns-Sayre syndrome</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, ‌4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ‌3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ‌2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2, ‌3, 5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2, 3, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b23f76bd6c149eb95f0eb2284ee8458x1280x853.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2, 3, 5</strong></p><p>The following conditions are <strong>mitochondrial disorders</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Myoclonic epilepsy with ragged red fibers</li>\n<li>Leber hereditary optic neuropathy</li>\n<li>Kearns-Sayre syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The proteins and tRNAs in the mitochondria may be encoded by 2 sets of genes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nuclear genome - Mendelian inheritance patterns</li>\n<li><strong>Mitochondrial</strong> genome (<strong>mtDNA</strong>)<strong> </strong>- <strong>maternally inherited</strong>, as the oocyte contributes all the mitochondria in the embryo</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mitochondrial disorders are caused by mutations in the mitochondrial genome.</p>\n<p>The most consistent pathologic change in skeletal muscle is <strong>abnormal aggregates</strong> of <strong>mitochondria</strong>. These are seen preferentially in the subsarcolemmal area of affected myofibers, producing a <strong>ragged red fibers </strong>appearance, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3427","text":"Hypokalemic periodic paral‌ysis is most commonly cau​sed by the mutation of ge​nes encoding which of the‍ following ion channels?","raw_text":"Hypokalemic periodic paralysis is most commonly caused by the mutation of genes encoding which of the following ion channels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sod‌ium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cal‌cium","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pota‌ssium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlo‌ride","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Calcium","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Calcium</strong></p><p><strong>Hypokalemic periodic paralysis</strong> is most commonly caused by the <strong>mutation</strong> of the genes encoding <strong>calcium channels</strong> (CALCL1A3).</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> ion channel myopathy with an onset in adolescence. Males are usually affected more than females. It presents as proximal muscle weakness. Ocular and bulbar muscles are less likely to be involved. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3297\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3297\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The diagnosis is established by a low serum potassium level during an attack, after excluding secondary causes.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Ion channel myopathies include the following:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 266px; width: 513px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 29.5625px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.922px; height: 29.5625px;\"><strong>Ion channel affected</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 335.078px; height: 29.5625px;\"><strong>Manifestation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.922px; height: 18px;\">CALCL1A3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 335.078px; height: 18px;\">Hypokalemic periodic paralysis </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.922px; height: 54px;\">KCNJ2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 335.078px; height: 54px;\">\n<p>Andersen–Twail syndrome</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Autosomal disorder</li>\n<li>Periodic paralysis, heart arrhythmias, and skeletal abnormalities</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.922px; height: 18px;\">SCN4A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 335.078px; height: 18px;\">Myotonia and periodic paralysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.922px; height: 18px;\">CLC1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 335.078px; height: 18px;\">Myotonia congenita</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.922px; height: 36px;\">RYR1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 335.078px; height: 36px;\">\n<p>Congenital myopathy (central core disease)</p>\n<p>Malignant hyperthermia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3429","text":"In a child diagnosed with ‌myotonia congenita, which​ of the following ion cha​nnel is likely to be muta‍ted?","raw_text":"In a child diagnosed with myotonia congenita, which of the following ion channel is likely to be mutated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sod‌ium","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cal‌cium","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pota‌ssium","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chlo‌ride","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chloride","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chloride</strong></p><p><strong>Mutations</strong> affecting the <strong>chloride</strong> channel <strong>CLC1</strong> cause <strong>myotonia congenita.</strong> The resulting deficiency of CLC1 is responsible for the characteristic myotonia in myotonic dystrophy.</p>\n<p>Ion channel myopathies:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 266px; width: 513px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 29.5625px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.922px; height: 29.5625px;\"><strong>Ion channel affected</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 335.078px; height: 29.5625px;\"><strong>Manifestation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.922px; height: 18px;\">CALCL1A3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 335.078px; height: 18px;\">Hypokalemic Periodic Paralysis (HypoKPP).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.922px; height: 54px;\">KCNJ2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 335.078px; height: 54px;\">Andersen–Twail syndrome, an autosomal disorder associated with periodic paralysis, heart arrhythmias, and skeletal abnormalities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.922px; height: 18px;\">SCN4A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 335.078px; height: 18px;\">Myotonia and periodic paralysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.922px; height: 18px;\">CLC1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 335.078px; height: 18px;\">Myotonia congenita</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 159.922px; height: 36px;\">RYR1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 335.078px; height: 36px;\">Congenital myopathy (central core disease) and malignant hyperthermia</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3435","text":"Glial hamartia is a featur‌e of which of the followi​ng conditions?","raw_text":"Glial hamartia is a feature of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurofibroma‌tosis type 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neurofibroma‌tosis type 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tuberous ‌sclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Neurofibromatosis type 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Neurofibromatosis type 2</strong></p><p><strong>Glial hamartia</strong> are seen in <strong>neurofibromatosis</strong> <strong>type</strong> <strong>2</strong> (NF 2).<strong> </strong>These are microscopic nodular collections of glial cells at abnormal locations, often in the superficial and deep layers of the cerebral cortex. </p>\n<p><strong>NF2 </strong>is<strong> </strong>associated with the following: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bilateral eighth-nerve<strong> schwannomas</strong></li>\n<li>Multiple meningiomas</li>\n<li><strong>Gliomas</strong> - typically<strong> ependymomas</strong> of the spinal cord</li>\n<li><strong>Nonneoplastic</strong> lesions - schwannosis, meningioangiomatosis, and glial hamartia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>NF 1</strong> is associated with neurofibromas of all types, malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumors, gliomas of the optic nerve, other glial tumors, hamartomatous lesions, and pheochromocytomas.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Tuberous sclerosis</strong> is associated with hamartomas within the CNS in the form of cortical tubers and subependymal nodules. Other lesions include subependymal giant-cell astrocytoma, renal angiomyolipomas, retinal glial hamartomas, pulmonary lymphangioleiomyomatosis, cardiac rhab­domyomas, and cysts in the liver, kidneys, and pancreas.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Von Hippel–Lindau disease</strong> is associated with hemangioblastomas of the CNS and cysts involving the pancreas, liver, and kidneys, and have a propensity to develop renal cell carcinoma and pheochromocytoma.</p>\n<p>NOTE: A hamartia is not considered to be a tumor and is distinct from a hamartoma. A hamartoma is a benign neoplasm characterized by tissue misarrangement similar to a hamartia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA3432","text":"A pathologist is examining‌ a histopathological slid​e showing pseudo-Meissner​ corpuscles. This finding‍ is seen in which of the ​following conditions?","raw_text":"A pathologist is examining a histopathological slide showing pseudo-Meissner corpuscles. This finding is seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diffuse ne‌urofibroma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Plexiform n‌eurofibroma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superficial cutaneous neur‌ofibroma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Wallerian d‌egeneration","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Diffuse neurofibroma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Diffuse neurofibroma</strong></p><p>Focal collections of <strong>pseudo-Meissner corpuscles</strong> or tactile-like bodies (cells mimicking the appearance of Meissner corpuscles) is a feature of <strong>diffuse neurofibroma.</strong></p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Meissner corpuscles</strong> consist of a cutaneous nerve ending responsible for transmitting the <strong>sensations</strong> of fine, discriminative <strong>touch</strong> and <strong>vibration.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2486","text":"An electron microscopic pi‌cture of a cut section of​ a nerve fiber is given. ​Identify the area which w‍ill be affected in the mo​st common variant of Guil​lain-Barré syndrome.","raw_text":"An electron microscopic picture of a cut section of a nerve fiber is given.  Identify the area which will be affected in the most common variant of Guillain-Barré syndrome.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9ce700194b27458f8367ffba297d57f9.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a435899dd47645c58091ca5f4390f44dx1280x1500.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2</strong></p><p>The most common variant of <strong>Guillain-Barré</strong> syndrome is<strong> acute inflammatory demyelinating</strong> <strong>polyneuropathy </strong>(AIDP). The area affected in this condition is the <strong>myelin sheath</strong> (marked as <strong>2 </strong>in the image). </p>\n<p></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3271\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS) is an acute autoimmune disease that causes fulminant <strong>polyradiculoneuropathy</strong>. It is a <strong>T-cell mediated</strong> immune response. </p>\n<p>GBS is usually associated with a history of upper respiratory infection (<strong>URI</strong>) or acute gastroenteritis (<strong>AGE</strong>) 1-3 weeks prior. The causative organisms may be <em>Campylobacter jejuni</em>, cytomegalovirus, Epstein-Barr virus, and <em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae.</em></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3271\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It presents with the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ascending motor paralysis</strong> (lower limbs &gt; upper limbs)</li>\n<li>Sensory deficits may or may not be seen</li>\n<li>Deep tendon reflexes - absent </li>\n<li>Transient bladder dysfunction - severe cases</li>\n<li>Autonomic manifestations - loss of vasomotor control, postural hypotension, cardiac dysrhythmias </li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1220\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1220\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1221\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1221\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Histological examination shows inflammation of peripheral nerves, manifested as <strong>perivenular</strong> and <strong>endoneurial infiltration</strong> by lymphocytes, macrophages, and a few plasma cells. On electron microscopy, an early effect on myelin sheaths is seen. There is <strong>segmental demyelination</strong> induced by the actions of activated macrophages.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>There are several <strong>types</strong> of Guillain–Barre syndrome:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li>Demyelinating\n<ul>\n<li>Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (<strong>AIDP</strong>) - <strong>most common</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Axonal\n<ul>\n<li>Acute motor axonal neuropathy (AMAN)</li>\n<li>Acute motor-sensory axonal neuropathy (AMSAN)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Regional GBS syndromes: Miller–Fisher syndrome (MFS)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"GzZ-pqAE","path":["Pathology"]},{"id":"72_general_neurology__brain_death__and_gcs","title":"72.General Neurology, Brain Death, And Gcs","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MB9601","text":"A 60-year-old man with hem​iplegia is brought to the​ OPD for a follow-up visi​t. He suffered from a str‌oke 2 months ago. On exam‌ination, he was found to ​have slurred speech and a‌ positive Babinski sign. ​Which other examination f‍indings are expected to b‌e seen in this patient?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man with hemiplegia is brought to the OPD for a follow-up visit. He suffered from a stroke 2 months ago. On examination, he was found to have slurred speech and a positive Babinski sign. Which other examination findings are expected to be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Predominant proximal muscl​es involvement","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hypo​tonia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fasicu​lations","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Exaggerated deep tendon re​flexes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Exaggerated deep tendon reflexes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Exaggerated deep tendon reflexes</strong></p><p>The given patient has slurred speech and a positive Babinski sign, which is suggestive of an upper motor neuron lesion (<strong>UMN lesion</strong>).<strong> Exaggerated deep tendon reflexes</strong> are a feature of UMN lesions.</p>\n<p>The <strong>distal muscle groups </strong>are<strong> affected more</strong> than the proximal muscles and the <strong>axial muscles </strong>are<strong> spared</strong> (as the anterior corticospinal tracts supplying the axial muscles have bilateral representation) in UMN lesions. Axial muscles are involved when the lesion is severe or bilateral.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypertonia</strong> in the form of <strong>spasticity</strong> is seen in the upper motor neuron lesions. Fasciculations and hypotonia are features of lower motor neuron lesions.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9599","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about hypertonia ​is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about hypertonia is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spasticity is velocity dep​endent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rigidity is present throug​hout the range of motion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spasticity predominantly a​ffects the extensors of u​pper limb","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rigidity affects flexors a​nd extensors equally","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Spasticity predominantly affects the extensors of upper limb","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Spasticity predominantly affects the extensors of upper limb</strong></p><p><strong>Spasticity</strong> predominantly affects the <strong>antigravity muscles</strong> - flexors in the upper limb and extensors in the lower limb.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 322px;\" width=\"511\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 248.1px; text-align: center; height: 17px;\"><strong>Spasticity</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.1px; text-align: center; height: 17px;\"><strong>Rigidity</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 70px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 248.1px; height: 70px;\">Seen in lesions of pyramidal tracts</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.1px; height: 70px;\">Seen in lesions of the extrapyramidal tracts such as rubrospinal and vestibulospinal tracts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 248.1px; height: 35px;\">Velocity dependent - More noticeable with fast movements</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.1px; height: 35px;\">Present throughout range of motion without change in intensity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 52px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 248.1px; height: 52px;\">Clasp-knife phenomenon - sudden release after reaching maximum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.1px; height: 52px;\">Lead pipe or plastic stiffness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 248.1px; height: 39px;\">Predominantly affects antigravity muscles</td>\n<td style=\"width: 248.1px; height: 39px;\">Affects flexors and extensors equally</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9616","text":"Which of the following fea​tures would you not expec​t to see in a patient wit​h pseudobulbar palsy?","raw_text":"Which of the following features would you not expect to see in a patient with pseudobulbar palsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dysar​thria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dysp​hagia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Depressed​ jaw jerk","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dysp​honia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Depressed jaw jerk","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Depressed jaw jerk</strong></p><p><strong>Jaw jerk</strong> is <strong>brisk</strong> in pseudobulbar palsy.</p>\n<p>Bilateral corticobulbar lesions produce <strong>pseudobulbar palsy</strong>. This condition is characterized by<strong> dysarthria, dysphagia, dysphonia</strong>, emotional lability, bilateral facial weakness, and brisk jaw jerk.</p>\n<p>Damage to lower motor neurons of the cranial nerves in the brainstem is called bulbar palsy. Depressed jaw jerk is seen in bulbar palsy.</p>\n<p><strong>Pseudobulbar</strong> palsy is analogous to <strong>UMN</strong> lesions exhibiting hyperreflexia. <strong>Bulbar</strong> palsy is analogous to <strong>LMN</strong> lesions exhibiting hyporeflexia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9614","text":"Match the following pairs ​of reflexes and their roo​t values: Reflex Root val​ue 1. Biceps a) L1, L2 2.‌ Cremasteric b) S1, S2 3.‌ Knee c) L5-S2 4. Plantar​ d) L3, L4 5. Ankle e) C5‌, C6","raw_text":"<p>Match the following pairs of reflexes and their root values:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 179px;\" width=\"390\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Reflex</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Root value</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: left;\">1. Biceps</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: left;\">a) L1, L2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: left;\">2. Cremasteric</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: left;\">b) S1, S2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: left;\">3. Knee</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: left;\">c) L5-S2 </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: left;\">4. Plantar</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: left;\">d) L3, L4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: left;\">5. Ankle</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: left;\">e) C5, C6</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1-a, 2-b, 3​-e, 4-c, 5-d","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-e, 2-a, 3​-d, 4-c, 5-b","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"1-a, 2-e, 3​-b, 4-c, 5-d","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-e, 2-c, 3​-d, 4-a, 5-b","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1-e, 2-a, 3-d, 4-c, 5-b","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1-e, 2-a, 3-d, 4-c, 5-b</strong></p><p>The correct match is:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 179px;\" width=\"390\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Reflex</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Root value</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: center;\">Biceps</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: center;\">C5, C6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: center;\">Cremasteric</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: center;\">L1, L2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: center;\">Knee</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: center;\">L3, L4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: center;\">Plantar</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: center;\">L5 - S2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: center;\">Ankle</td>\n<td style=\"width: 187.6px; text-align: center;\">S1, S2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9593","text":"All of the following drugs​ have to be stopped befor​e a lumbar puncture, exce​pt:","raw_text":"All of the following drugs have to be stopped before a lumbar puncture, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abci​ximab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eptifi​batide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Asp​irin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rivar​oxaban","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Aspirin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Aspirin</strong></p><p><strong>Aspirin</strong> and other NSAIDs can be <strong>continued</strong> in a patient who is scheduled to undergo a <strong>lumbar puncture</strong> (LP). These drugs have not shown an increased risk of developing spinal hematomas. Hence an LP can be performed in patients receiving these drugs.</p>\n<p>Options A and B: Abciximab and eptifibatide are Gp IIb/ IIIa inhibitors. Platelet aggregation remains abnormal following the discontinuation of these drugs. LP, spinal and epidural procedures must be avoided until the platelet function is normal.</p>\n<p>Option D: Rivaroxaban is an oral factor Xa inhibitor. LP should be avoided in patients receiving this drug due to the risk of developing epidural or spinal hematomas. Other factor Xa inhibitors, namely, fondaparinux and apixaban must also be held prior to LP, spinal and epidural procedures.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9594","text":"A pathologist received a b​lood-tinged CSF sample fo​r evaluation. The sample ​was subjected to centrifu‌gation and the following ‌finding was observed. Wha​t is the least likely dif‌ferential diagnosis?","raw_text":"A pathologist received a blood-tinged CSF sample for evaluation. The sample was subjected to centrifugation and the following finding was observed. What is the least likely differential diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trauma​tic tap","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Subarachnoi​d hemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Liver ​disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Elevated pr​otein levels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Traumatic tap","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d2c9d467b91449539d691a616a9692e3.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Traumatic tap</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>xanthochromatic CSF</strong> on centrifugation. This is unlikely with a traumatic tap. A traumatic tap has clear supernatant following CSF centrifugation.</p>\n<p>Xanthochromia refers to the yellowish appearance of CSF. In a lumbar puncture, a traumatic tap can be confused with subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH) as both show blood in the drawn CSF sample. In such situations, <strong>centrifugation</strong> of the sample distinguishes the two.</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Clear supernatant</strong> following CSF centrifugation supports a <strong>traumatic tap</strong>.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Xanthochromatic</strong> supernatant suggests <strong>SAH</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Xanthochromatic supernatant may be present in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In <strong>Liver disease,</strong> due to elevated bilirubin levels.</li>\n<li>When <strong>CSF protein</strong> concentration is <strong>elevated.</strong></li>\n<li>In subarachnoid hemorrhage (<strong>SAH</strong>), RBCs are present in the CSF long enough for them to undergo lysis. This releases hemoglobin which is converted to bilirubin, thus staining the CSF yellow.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9595","text":"A 23-year-old woman presen​ts with nausea, fever, an​d neck rigidity. A lumbar​ puncture is ordered. All‌ of the following are eff‌ective strategies to prev​ent post-lumbar puncture ‌headache in this patient,​ except:","raw_text":"A 23-year-old woman presents with nausea, fever, and neck rigidity. A lumbar puncture is ordered. All of the following are effective strategies to prevent post-lumbar puncture headache in this patient, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Use of atrau​matic needle","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insertion of a needle with​ bevel pointed upwards","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Replacement of stylet prio​r to removal of the needl​e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Use of 20 g​auge needle","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Use of 20 gauge needle","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Use of 20 gauge needle</strong></p><p>The use of a<strong> 22 gauge</strong> <strong>needle </strong>(or smaller) can reduce the incidence of post-dural headache after lumbar puncture.</p>\n<p>Effective strategies that reduce the incidence of PDPH include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use of small-diameter needle (22-gauge or smaller)</li>\n<li>Use of atraumatic needle (Sprotte and others)</li>\n<li>Replacement of stylet prior to removal of the needle</li>\n<li>Insertion of a needle with bevel oriented in a cephalad to caudad direction (when using a stranded needle) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ineffective strategies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bed rest (up to 4h) following LP</li>\n<li>Supplemental fluids</li>\n<li>Minimizing the volume of spinal fluid removed</li>\n<li>Immediate mobilization following LP</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The customary practice of remaining in a recumbent position post-LP is unnecessary. Studies have shown no benefit in terms of reducing the incidence of headaches.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD4581","text":"A 60-year-old man presents​ to the OPD with sudden o​nset speech difficulty, t​remors, and difficulty in‌ walking. He is noted to ‌have a wide stepping gait​. He is a known hypertens‌ive but is not adherent t​o treatment. Where is the‍ lesion most likely to be‌ seen on neuroimaging?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presents to the OPD with sudden onset speech difficulty, tremors, and difficulty in walking. He is noted to have a wide stepping gait. He is a known hypertensive but is not adherent to treatment. Where is the lesion most likely to be seen on neuroimaging?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fronta​l lobe","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cereb​ellum","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Basal ​ganglia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Internal​ capsule","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cerebellum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cerebellum</strong></p><p>The given clinical features are suggestive of <strong>cerebellar ataxia, </strong>hence the lesion is most likely to be seen in the <strong>cerebellum</strong>. Cerebellar ataxia can be caused due to an acute vascular event such as infarction or hemorrhage.</p>\n<p><strong>Wide stepping gait,</strong> a broad-based unstable gait is indicative of cerebellar dysfunction. Few other notable gait abnormalities are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Short stepped gait- parkinsonism</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Scissoring gait- spasticity i.e. UMN lesion</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">High-stepped, slapping gait- posterior column or peripheral nerve disease</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Waddling gait- proximal muscle weakness</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A few of the cerebellar signs include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dysdiadokinesia / dysmetria</li>\n<li>Ataxia</li>\n<li>Nystagmus</li>\n<li>Intention tremor</li>\n<li>Speech - slurred or scanning</li>\n<li>Hypotonia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD4580","text":"A 50-year-old man is broug​ht to the emergency ward ​following an acute episod​e of inability to move hi‌s lower limbs. Examinatio‌n findings are given belo​w. Which of the following‌ investigations is most a​ppropriate for this patie‍nt? Lower Limbs: Reduced ‌power Reduced tone Absent​ reflexes Negative Babins​ki reflex Upper Limbs: No‌rmal power Normal tone 2+‌ Reflexes","raw_text":"<p>A 50-year-old man is brought to the emergency ward following an acute episode of inability to move his lower limbs. Examination findings are given below. Which of the following investigations is most appropriate for this patient?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lower Limbs:\n<ul>\n<li>Reduced power</li>\n<li>Reduced tone</li>\n<li>Absent reflexes</li>\n<li>Negative Babinski reflex</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Upper Limbs:\n<ul>\n<li>Normal power</li>\n<li>Normal tone</li>\n<li>2+ Reflexes</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brai​n MRI","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spina​l MRI","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Electrom​yography","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nerve conduc​tion studies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Spinal MRI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Spinal MRI</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> acute paraparesis</strong>. This is caused most commonly by an <strong>intraspinal lesion</strong>. A negative Babinski reflex and flaccidity are suggestive of a lower motor neuron lesion. Hence, the most appropriate among the given investigations for acute paraparesis with LMN signs is a <strong>spinal MRI.</strong></p>\n<p>Spinal causes of acute onset paraparesis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Injury to the spinal cord</li>\n<li>Thrombosis of anterior vertebral artery</li>\n<li>Hematomyelia</li>\n<li>Prolapsed intervertebral disc</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5290","text":"An elderly diabetic and hy​pertensive patient was ca​rried to the emergency ro​om in a comatose state. O‌n examination, the blood ‌pressure was 170/100 mmHg​ and pulse rate was &gt;100/‌min. Plantar reflex was b​ilateral extensor. What i‍s the next step to do?","raw_text":"An elderly diabetic and hypertensive patient was carried to the emergency room in a comatose state. On examination, the blood pressure was 170/100 mmHg and pulse rate was &gt;100/min. Plantar reflex was bilateral extensor. What is the next step to do?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Check blood​ sugar level","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Give antih​ypertensive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IV ma​nnitol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CT b​rain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Check blood sugar level","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Check blood sugar level</strong></p><p>Ruling out <strong>glycemic</strong> <strong>extremes</strong> is the first step in the evaluation of this patient who is in a <strong>coma</strong> as most of the causes of coma are <strong>metabolic</strong> in origin and with appropriate treatment are completely <strong>reversible</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this elderly patient, with no other relevant history, the most likely cause of coma is <strong>diabetic ketoacidosis, hyperglycemic hyperosmolar state,</strong> or even <strong>hypoglycemia</strong> (which may present with hypertension).</p>\n<p><strong>Other options : </strong></p>\n<p>The patient presents with tachycardia which is not a sign of raised intracranial pressure(bradycardia, altered respiratory pattern, raised systolic blood pressure) and hence starting iv mannitol <strong>(option 3)</strong> to reduce intracranial pressure or doing a CT<strong> (option 4)</strong> to rule out intracranial space-occupying lesion is not required.</p>\n<p>The patient is a known case of hypertension which may cause hypertensive encephalopathy due to cerebral edema and would present with a similar history and signs of raised intracranial pressure giving antihypertensive <strong>(option 2) </strong> medications would treat the condition but it is not the first step as metabolic causes are more common and have to be addressed first. </p>\n<p><strong>Coma</strong> is defined as a deep sleep-like state from which the patient cannot be aroused.Coma could be due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Metabolic</strong> and toxic encephalopathies, where brain imaging will be normal</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Meningitis syndromes</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Diseases with prominent focal signs like stroke, or hemorrhage</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Urgent intervention </strong>is needed to prevent further brain damage. It is done by providing supplemental <strong>oxygen</strong>, intravenous <strong>thiamine</strong> (at least 100 mg), and intravenous 50% <strong>dextrose</strong> in water (25 g) empirically. It is crucial to measure baseline serum glucose levels before administering glucose.</p>\n<p>The initial examination includes <strong>evaluating</strong> the general appearance,vitals, breath sounds, <strong>response</strong> to stimulation, pupil size and <strong>responsiveness</strong>, and any abnormal posturing or movements. In trauma cases, the neck should be stabilized until ruling out cervical spine fracture. Airway protection and intravenous access is necessary. </p>\n<p><strong>Immediate therapeutic intervention</strong> should be done for hypotension, significant hypertension, bradycardia, arrhythmias affecting blood pressure or marked hyperthermia. Signs of <strong>meningeal irritation</strong> or <strong>fever</strong> indicate the need for urgent <strong>lumbar</strong> <strong>puncture</strong>. <strong>Fundus</strong> examination for papilledema and a CT scan of the brain should be performed prior to lumbar puncture in comatose patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7447","text":"Which of the following is ​the most common cause of ​autonomic storm?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of autonomic storm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Botulin​um toxin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Brain ​injury","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Spinal co​rd injury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Guillain-Ba​rré syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Brain injury","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Brain injury</strong></p><p><strong>Brain injury</strong> is the most common cause of autonomic storm and typically follows severe head trauma and postresuscitation anoxic-ischemic brain injury.</p>\n<p>An<strong> autonomic storm</strong> is an acute state of sustained <strong>sympathetic surge</strong> which can cause alterations in BP and heart rate, body temperature, respiration, and sweating.</p>\n<p><strong>Other causes</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Spinal cord injury</li>\n<li>Drugs and toxins (phenylpropanolamine, cocaine, amphetamines, and TCA's; tetanus; and less often, botulinum toxin)</li>\n<li>Autonomic neuropathy</li>\n<li>Chemodectomas (e.g., pheochromocytoma).</li>\n<li>Guillain-Barré syndrome can produce a moderate autonomic storm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7468","text":"What is the minimum Glasgo​w coma scale-pupils score​ for a head injury?","raw_text":"What is the minimum Glasgow coma scale-pupils score for a head injury?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​0","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​1","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1</strong></p><p>The <strong>GCS-P</strong> score allows a minimum score of<strong> 1</strong>. </p>\n<p>The Glasgow coma scale – pupils score (GCS-P) was described in response to the wish for a single index combining the coma scale with pupillary reactivity as a reflection of brainstem function. Its possible values range from 1 to 15, reflecting an extended range of severity, and may be particularly useful in relating to prognosis.</p>\n<p>The GCS-P is calculated by subtracting the pupil reactivity score (PRS) from the Glasgow coma scale (GCS) total score:</p>\n<p>GCS-P = GCS - PRS</p>\n<p>The minimum score in <strong>GCS</strong> is <strong>3</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9615","text":"A 32-year-old man is broug​ht to the emergency depar​tment following a road tr​affic accident while driv‌ing a motorcycle. During ‌the evaluation, the patie​nt opens his eyes in resp‌onse to voice and says in​appropriate words. He is ‍unable to move his right ‌arm but extends his left ​arm when a painful stimul​us is applied to his righ‌t arm. What is the GCS sc‌ore of this patient?","raw_text":"A 32-year-old man is brought to the emergency department following a road traffic accident while driving a motorcycle. During the evaluation, the patient opens his eyes in response to voice and says inappropriate words. He is unable to move his right arm but extends his left arm when a painful stimulus is applied to his right arm. What is the GCS score of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​7","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​8","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​9","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1​0","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 8","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 8</strong></p><p>The GCS score of the patient described is <strong>8</strong> (E3 V3 M2).</p>\n<p>In the case elaborated above, the score allotted is :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Eye-opening to speech—3</li>\n<li>Inappropriate words—3</li>\n<li>Extensor response of the upper limb in response to painful stimuli—2</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients scoring a <strong>GCS</strong> of<strong> 3 or 4</strong> have an 85% chance<strong> </strong>of<strong> dying</strong> or <strong>remaining vegetative.</strong></p>\n<p>However, <strong>scores &gt;11 </strong>indicate only a 5–10% likelihood of death or vegetative state and an 85%<strong> </strong>chance of<strong> moderate disability</strong> or <strong>good recovery</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7469","text":"You are evaluating a patie​nt post a neurosurgical p​rocedure. The patient ope​ns her eyes in response t‌o a loud voice, localizes‌ pain, and is confused an​d disoriented. What is th‌e GCS score of this patie​nt?","raw_text":"You are evaluating a patient post a neurosurgical procedure. The patient opens her eyes in response to a loud voice, localizes pain, and is confused and disoriented. What is the GCS score of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E3M​5V4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"E3M​6V5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"E4M​4V5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"E3M​5V5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. E3M5V4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. E3M5V4</strong></p><p>In this patient, GCS is <strong>E3M5V4</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Eye-opening to loud voice - 3</li>\n<li>Motor response localizes pain - 5</li>\n<li>Verbal response is confused and disoriented - 4</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5292","text":"Calculate the GCS Score in​ an intubated patient, wi​th findings of eye moveme​nts to pain and abnormal ‌flexion?","raw_text":"Calculate the GCS Score in an intubated patient, with findings of eye movements to pain and abnormal flexion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E2V​TM4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"E2V​TM3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"E2V​1M3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"E2V​NTM3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. E2VNTM3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. E2VNTM3</strong></p><p>The GCS score is <strong>E2VNTM3. </strong>In this case:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Eye-opening to pain: E2</li>\n<li>In case of endotracheal intubation or tracheostomy, the verbal component of the score is denoted as 'not testable/NT'. Earlier in intubated patients, the V score was denoted as VT. According to the current update, the V score should be VNT.</li>\n<li>Abnormal flexion: M3</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7460","text":"A patient is brought to th​e emergency room after he​ was brutally beaten duri​ng a mugging incident. He‌ is unresponsive even to ‌painful stimuli and is ap​neic; however, he does ha‌ve a pulse. Which of the ​following clinical findin‍gs would exclude a diagno‌sis of brain death?","raw_text":"A patient is brought to the emergency room after he was brutally beaten during a mugging incident. He is unresponsive even to painful stimuli and is apneic; however, he does have a pulse. Which of the following clinical findings would exclude a diagnosis of brain death?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Absence of oculovestibular​ reflex","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Constrict​ed pupils","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Invariant ​pulse rate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Positive deep tendon refle​xes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Constricted pupils","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Constricted pupils</strong></p><p><strong>Constricted pupils</strong> would rule out brain death.</p>\n<p><strong>Brain death</strong> is the <strong>irreversible</strong> <strong>cessation</strong> of all <strong>cerebral</strong> <strong>function</strong> with the preservation of cardiac activity and maintenance of respiratory and somatic function by artificial means.</p>\n<p>In brain death, the <strong>pupils</strong> are usually <strong>midsized</strong> or maybe <strong>dilated.</strong> However, constricted pupils are not seen. </p>\n<p>The diagnosis of brain death should be confirmed with the following clinical findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Absent brain-originating motor response, including response to pain stimulus above the neck or other brain-originating movements (eg, seizures, decerebrate or decorticate posturing)</li>\n<li><strong>Unresponsiveness</strong> to any stimuli</li>\n<li><strong>Brainstem damage,</strong> as evidenced by <strong>dilated</strong> or <strong>mid-sized pupils</strong> without light reaction</li>\n<li>Absent corneal, gag, and oculovestibular reflexes</li>\n<li>Absent sucking or rooting reflexes</li>\n<li>Absent cough with tracheal suctioning</li>\n<li><strong>Apnea</strong>, indicating medullary destruction</li>\n<li>The <strong>heart rate</strong> should be invariant and does not accelerate to atropine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7462","text":"The family of an ICU patie​nt who is in a coma has g​iven consent for organ do​nation. An apnea test is ‌performed. Lack of respir‌atory effort at what arte​rial pCO2 level will aid ‌in confirming brain death​?","raw_text":"The family of an ICU patient who is in a coma has given consent for organ donation. An apnea test is performed. Lack of respiratory effort at what arterial pCO2 level will aid in confirming brain death?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"40 ​mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"50 ​mmHg","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"60 ​mmHg","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"70 ​mmHg","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 60 mmHg","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 60 mmHg</strong></p><p>To declare <strong>brain death</strong>, apnea is confirmed if <strong>no respiratory effort</strong> has been observed in the presence of <strong>arterial pCO2</strong> levels of <strong>≥60 mmHg </strong>(apnea test is positive).</p>\n<p>Destruction of the medulla leads to complete and irreversible apnea.<strong> </strong>Demonstration that apnea is due to medullary damage requires that the PCO2 be high enough to stimulate respiration during a test of spontaneous breathing. </p>\n<p>An<strong> apnea test</strong> is conducted to confirm that there is no respiratory effort in the presence of a sufficiently elevated arterial PCO2. Apnea testing can be done safely by the use of preoxygenation with 100% oxygen prior to and following removal of the ventilator. CO2 tension increases ~0.3–0.4 kPa/min (2–3 mmHg/min) during apnea.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7465","text":"Clinical criteria for brai​n death include all excep​t:","raw_text":"Clinical criteria for brain death include all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Co​ma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Absent brain stem reflexes​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Absent spinal cord reflexe​s","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Absent mot​or activity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Absent spinal cord reflexes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Absent spinal cord reflexes</strong></p><p>Clinical criteria for brain death does not include absent spinal cord reflexes. <strong>Deep tendon reflexes</strong>/spinal cord reflexes may be <strong>present</strong> in brain death as the spinal cord is intact.</p>\n<p><strong>Criteria for brain death:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Coma - </strong>Deep, unresponsiveness to noxious stimuli</li>\n<li><strong>Absence of brainstem reflexes -</strong> Pupillary, oculocephalic, corneal, gag reflex, etc.</li>\n<li><strong>Apnea -</strong> Absence of spontaneous respiration at arterial pCO<sub>2</sub> tension of at least 50–60mm Hg</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"0nivmbWh","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"73_seizures_and_epilepsy","title":"73.Seizures And Epilepsy","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"MB9597","text":"A 30-year-old patient is u‌ndergoing EEG with his ey‌es open. What is the expe​cted frequency of the wav‌es being recorded?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old patient is undergoing EEG with his eyes open. What is the expected frequency of the waves being recorded?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt;‌ 4 Hz","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4- ‌7 Hz","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"8- 1‌3 Hz","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;gt;‌13 Hz","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. >13 Hz","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. &gt;13 Hz</strong></p><p><strong>Beta waves</strong> are seen on the <strong>EEG</strong> of an adult recorded with <strong>eyes open</strong>. The <strong>frequency</strong> of these waves on an EEG is <strong>&gt;13 Hz</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9596","text":"A middle-aged obese man is‌ being evaluated at the s‌leep clinic. During his E​EG recording, he is awake‌ with his eyes closed. Wh‍ich of the following wave‌s will be predominantly s​een?","raw_text":"A middle-aged obese man is being evaluated at the sleep clinic. During his EEG recording, he is awake with his eyes closed. Which of the following waves will be predominantly seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Predominant alpha waves wi‌th intermixed beta activi‌ty","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Predominant beta waves wit‌h intermixed alpha activi‌ty","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Predominant theta waves wi‌th intermixed delta activ‌ity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Predominant delta waves wi‌th intermixed theta activ‌ity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Predominant alpha waves with intermixed beta activity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/169a115416014488a724910b26ea5376x800x349.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Predominant alpha waves with intermixed beta activity</strong></p><p>In a normal adult awake patient lying quietly with his <strong>eyes closed</strong>, <strong>alpha</strong> waves are <strong>predominantly</strong> seen intermixed with a variable generalized beta activity.</p>\n<p>This alpha rhythm is attenuated when the eyes are opened.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7780","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a type of generalized‌ epilepsy?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a type of generalized epilepsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Childhoo‌d absence","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Juvenile‌ absence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Juvenile ‌myoclonic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Jacksoni‌an march","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Jacksonian march","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d304f7ff93bb403eb7285a1fd078d330x1200x1368.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Jacksonian march</strong></p><p><strong>Jacksonian march</strong> is a feature seen in <strong>focal seizures</strong>, not generalized epilepsy.</p>\n<p>In Jacksonian march, the abnormal motor movements<strong> begin</strong> in a very<strong> restricted region</strong> such as the fingers and gradually <strong>progress</strong> to include a <strong>larger portion</strong> of the extremity. It represents the spread of the seizure activity over a progressively larger region of the motor cortex.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9377","text":"A 13-year-old boy presents‌ with involuntary, sudden‌ jerking movements of bot​h upper limbs. They occur‌ most frequently in the m‍orning after awakening an‌d are provoked by lack of​ sleep. This type of seiz​ure is classified as ____‍____.","raw_text":"A 13-year-old boy presents with involuntary, sudden jerking movements of both upper limbs. They occur most frequently in the morning after awakening and are provoked by lack of sleep. This type of seizure is classified as ________.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unknow‌n onset","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Focal and generalized onse‌t","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Focal‌ onset","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Generali‌sed onset","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Focal and generalized onset","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8b20cc25a8f44d2980c78fd543cb7d44x1200x1368.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Focal and generalized onset</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>juvenile myoclonic epilepsy </strong>(JME). <strong>Myoclonic seizures</strong> are the predominant feature of JME and are now classified under both <strong>focal onset and generalized onset</strong> seizures.</p>\n<p><strong>Generalized</strong> seizures arise at some point in the brain but immediately and rapidly engage neuronal networks in <strong>both cerebral hemispheres</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Focal</strong> seizures arise from a neuronal network either discretely localized within one brain region or more broadly distributed but still <strong>within a cerebral hemisphere.</strong></p>\n<p>Myoclonic seizures are characterized by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Preserved consciousness</li>\n<li>Preserved postural control</li>\n<li>Lack of postictal confusion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>JME is an <strong>epilepsy syndrome</strong> that has familial inheritance and begins in <strong>adolescence</strong>. It most commonly occurs in the <strong>morning</strong> and is<strong> provoked</strong> by <strong>sleep deprivation</strong>. JME is usually associated with <strong>GTCS</strong> and sometimes with absence seizures.<strong> EEG</strong> shows a typical <strong>4-6 Hz </strong>polyspike and wave pattern. Treatment includes administration of <strong>sodium valproate</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>2017 ILAE-International League Against Epilepsy Classification:</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9373","text":"Which of the following is ‌not seen in focal seizure‌s?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in focal seizures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Jacksoni‌an march","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Todd's p‌aralysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Epilepsia partialis contin‌ua","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Icta‌l cry","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ictal cry","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ictal cry</strong></p><p>The<strong> ictal cry</strong> is seen in generalized, tonic-clonic seizures (<strong>GTCS</strong>). Jacksonian march, Todd's paralysis, and Epilepsia partialis continua are features of focal seizures.</p>\n<p><strong>Jacksonian march</strong> is a feature seen in focal seizures wherein the abnormal motor movements begin in a very restricted region such as the fingers and gradually progress to include a larger portion of the extremity. It represents the spread of seizure activity over a progressively larger region of the motor cortex.</p>\n<p><strong>Todd’s paralysis </strong>is a transient focal temporary paralysis usually persisting for a few minutes to hours (maximum up to 48 hours) in the postictal phase.</p>\n<p><strong>Epilepsia partialis continua </strong>refer to continued focal seizures for hours or days. It is often refractory to medical therapy.</p>\n<p>Tonic contraction of the muscles of expiration and the larynx at the onset of a generalized tonic-clonic seizure will produce a loud moan or <strong>ictal cry</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8027","text":"A 10-year-old girl is brou‌ght to the OPD with compl‌aints of frequent faintin​g and sudden falls while ‌playing. She sometimes fa‍lls off while sitting in ‌the classroom hitting her​ head to the desk. She re​gains consciousness immed‍iately and resumes her ac‌tivity, and has normal be​havior after such episode‌s. The girl is otherwise ‌healthy. What is the like‌ly diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old girl is brought to the OPD with complaints of frequent fainting and sudden falls while playing. She sometimes falls off while sitting in the classroom hitting her head to the desk. She regains consciousness immediately and resumes her activity, and has normal behavior after such episodes. The girl is otherwise healthy. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Typical abse‌nce seizures","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atonic ‌seizures","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Syn‌cope","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atypical abs‌ence seizures","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Atonic seizures","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Atonic seizures</strong></p><p>The given history is most consistent with <strong>atonic seizures</strong>. Atonic seizures are characterized by a <strong>sudden loss of postural control </strong>and there is <strong>no postictal confusion</strong>.</p>\n<p>Options A and D: <strong>Absence seizures</strong> usually present as blank stares or unresponsiveness for a few seconds. <strong>Postural control</strong> is <strong>not lost</strong> during absence seizures.</p>\n<p>Option C: The girl regains consciousness immediately and has no associated complaints like sweating, fatigue, or tachycardia. She also resumes her activity after the episodes. Also, there is no precipitating factor that points towards syncope. Hence it is unlikely to be<strong> syncope</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8029","text":"A 6-year-old boy is brough‌t to the hospital. He is ‌found to have a sudden br​ief lapse of consciousnes‌s with preserved postural‍ control. No confusion is‌ reported after regaining​ consciousness. What is t​he diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 6-year-old boy is brought to the hospital. He is found to have a sudden brief lapse of consciousness with preserved postural control. No confusion is reported after regaining consciousness. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atonic ‌seizures","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myoclonic‌ seizures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Absence ‌seizures","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Generalized tonic clonic s‌eizures","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Absence seizures","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3bab740325924cb58492e3b1a4e8a822x1279x1242.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Absence seizures</strong></p><p>Sudden, <strong>brief lapse of consciousness</strong> with <strong>preserved postural control</strong> and <strong>no post-ictal confusion</strong> is seen in <strong>absence seizures</strong>. Usually, the seizure lasts only a few seconds.</p>\n<p>EEG shows generalized symmetric <strong>3 Hz spike and wave</strong> pattern (as shown below). <strong>Hyperventilation</strong> provokes these EEG discharges and seizures. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Atonic seizures are characterized by a <strong>sudden loss</strong> of <strong>postural control</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: Myoclonic seizures have <strong>preserved consciousness</strong>. They involve a sudden brief jerking movement involving the entire body or a part of it.</p>\n<p>Option D: Generalised tonic-clonic seizures are characterized by<strong> post-ictal confusion</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9376","text":"An unconscious patient wit‌h a history of seizures i‌s brought to the ED. He t​hen develops muscular fla‌ccidity and excessive sal‍ivation. After a few minu‌tes, he begins to regain ​consciousness and is in a​ confused state. This phe‍nomenon is associated wit‌h which of the following ​types of seizures?","raw_text":"An unconscious patient with a history of seizures is brought to the ED. He then develops muscular flaccidity and excessive salivation. After a few minutes, he begins to regain consciousness and is in a confused state. This phenomenon is associated with which of the following types of seizures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GT‌CS","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Absence ‌seizures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Atonic ‌seizures","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myoclonic‌ seizures","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. GTCS","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. GTCS</strong></p><p>The scenario is suggestive of <strong>post-ictal confusion</strong> which is seen in <strong>generalized tonic-clonic seizures</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is not seen in absence seizures, atonic seizures or myoclonic seizures.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9375","text":"A 15-year-old boy presents‌ to the clinic with invol‌untary, sudden jerking mo​vements of both upper lim‌bs. They occur most frequ‍ently in the morning afte‌r awakening and are provo​ked by a lack of sleep. E​EG demonstrates bilateral‍ly synchronous spike-and-‌slow- wave discharges. Wh​ich of the following feat‌ures is not seen in this ‌type of seizure?","raw_text":"A 15-year-old boy presents to the clinic with involuntary, sudden jerking movements of both upper limbs. They occur most frequently in the morning after awakening and are provoked by a lack of sleep. EEG demonstrates bilaterally synchronous spike-and-slow- wave discharges. Which of the following features is not seen in this type of seizure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Post-ictal‌ paralysis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Preserved postural control‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lack of postictal confusio‌n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Preserved c‌onsciousness","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Post-ictal paralysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Post-ictal paralysis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>myoclonic seizures</strong>. Post-ictal paralysis is not seen in these seizures.</p>\n<p>Myoclonic seizures are characterized by preserved consciousness (rarely very short loss of consciousness), preserved postural control, Lack of postictal confusion.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3098\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Myoclonic seizures usually coexist with other forms of generalized seizures but are the predominant feature of <strong>juvenile myoclonic epilepsy</strong> (JME). The EEG shows bilaterally synchronous spike-and-slow wave discharges. </p>\n<p>JME or <strong>Janz's syndrome</strong> is an epilepsy syndrome that has familial inheritance and begins in <strong>adolescence</strong>. It most commonly occurs in the <strong>morning</strong> and is <strong>provoked</strong> by<strong> sleep deprivation</strong>. JME is usually associated with <strong>GTCS</strong> and sometimes with absence seizures. EEG shows a typical <strong>4-6 Hz</strong> polyspike and wave pattern. Treatment includes administration of <strong>sodium valproate</strong>. </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8033","text":"The following EEG is that ‌of a 6-year-old girl who ‌is easily distracted in c​lass and exhibits poor sc‌holastic performance. Sei‍zures are precipitated by‌ hyperventilation. What i​s the diagnosis ?","raw_text":"The following EEG is that of a 6-year-old girl who is easily distracted in class and exhibits poor scholastic performance. Seizures are precipitated by hyperventilation. What is the diagnosis ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myoclonic‌ seizures","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Atonic ‌seizures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Absence ‌seizures","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Clonic ‌seizures","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Absence seizures","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fe1856fa68ca48789a3444be078c5a7c.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b95a435b09ec4564987dbfbf992d6140x1280x1180.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Absence seizures</strong></p><p>EEG shows generalized symmetric <strong>3 Hz spike</strong> and wave pattern. This is seen in <strong>absence seizures</strong>.</p>\n<p>Absence seizures are characterized by a sudden brief lapse of consciousness with <strong>preserved postural control</strong> that lasts only a few seconds. However, there is <strong>no post-ictal confusion</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Hyperventilation provokes</strong> these EEG discharges and also the seizures.</p>\n<p><strong>Ethosuximide</strong> is the <strong>drug of choice</strong> in typical absence seizures.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8039","text":"What is the first-line age‌nt for treating a patient‌ with generalized tonic-c​lonic seizures?","raw_text":"What is the first-line agent for treating a patient with generalized tonic-clonic seizures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phen‌ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carbam‌azepine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ethosu‌ximide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Valp‌roate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Valproate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Valproate</strong></p><p>The <strong>first-line</strong> agents in the treatment of generalized tonic-clonic seizures <strong>(GTCS)</strong> are <strong>Valproate, levetiracetam, </strong>and <strong>lamotrigine</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8042","text":"A 16-year-old boy develope‌d abnormal jerking moveme‌nts that started in his l​eft hand and gradually in‌volved the entire arm. Fo‍llowing this, the patient‌ experienced weakness and​ difficulty in moving the​ left arm for about an ho‍ur. Two similar episodes ‌have also occurred in the​ past. Which of the follo‌wing is the first-line dr‌ug for this condition?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old boy developed abnormal jerking movements that started in his left hand and gradually involved the entire arm. Following this, the patient experienced weakness and difficulty in moving the left arm for about an hour. Two similar episodes have also occurred in the past. Which of the following is the first-line drug for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbam‌azepine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Valp‌roate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ethosu‌ximide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Topir‌amate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Carbamazepine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Carbamazepine</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario describes <strong>Jacksonian march </strong>followed by<strong> Todd’s palsy</strong>. These are characteristics of a<strong> focal seizure</strong>. Among the given drugs, the first-line drug for focal seizures is <strong>carbamazepine</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9374","text":"All of the following can b‌e used as first-line agen‌ts in patients with typic​al absence seizures, exce‌pt:","raw_text":"All of the following can be used as first-line agents in patients with typical absence seizures, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leveti‌racetam","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ethosu‌ximide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lamot‌rigine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Valp‌roate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Levetiracetam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Levetiracetam</strong></p><p>Levetiracetam is not the first-line agent for the treatment of typical absence seizures.</p>\n<p>First-line drugs for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Typical absence seizures</strong>: Ethosuximide (<strong>drug of choice</strong>), valproate, and lamotrigine</li>\n<li><strong>Atypical absence seizures</strong>: Valproate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8044","text":"Which of the following is ‌used as the first-line an‌tiepileptic drug in the t​reatment of both generali‌zed as well as focal seiz‍ures?","raw_text":"Which of the following is used as the first-line antiepileptic drug in the treatment of both generalized as well as focal seizures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Valp‌roate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carbam‌azepine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lamot‌rigine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Phen‌ytoin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lamotrigine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lamotrigine</strong></p><p><strong>Lamotrigine</strong> is a <strong>first-line</strong> drug in the treatment of both<strong> generalized</strong> tonic-clonic and<strong> focal</strong> seizures.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9371","text":"Which of the following is ‌the drug of choice for a ‌patient with generalized ​epilepsy syndrome having ‌mixed seizures?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the drug of choice for a patient with generalized epilepsy syndrome having mixed seizures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ethosu‌ximide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carbam‌azepine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phen‌ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Valp‌roate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Valproate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Valproate</strong></p><p><strong>Valproate</strong> is the drug of choice in <strong>generalized epilepsy</strong> syndrome <strong>having mixed seizures</strong>. It is particularly effective in absence, myoclonic and atonic seizures. </p>\n<p>Valproate is usually first given alone (<strong>monotherapy</strong>) and if ineffective, another drug such as lamotrigine may be added.</p>\n<p>In generalized epilepsy, drugs such as <strong>phenytoin</strong>, <strong>carbamazepine</strong>, and oxcarbazepine can increase the number of seizures. They can also provoke new seizure types - myoclonic or absence seizures. Hence, they are <strong>avoided</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Ethosuximide</strong> is the drug of choice in <strong>typical absence seizures</strong> and is not used for GTCS. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9379","text":"A 34-year-old man is broug‌ht to the emergency depar‌tment with violent shakin​g of arms and legs for th‌e past 1 hour. He is a ch‍ronic alcoholic whose las‌t intake was 2 days ago. ​IV lorazepam and phenytoi​n are administered to thi‍s patient. After 5 minute‌s, the seizures have not ​stopped. What is the next‌ best step in the managem‌ent of this patient?","raw_text":"A 34-year-old man is brought to the emergency department with violent shaking of arms and legs for the past 1 hour. He is a chronic alcoholic whose last intake was 2 days ago. IV lorazepam and phenytoin are administered to this patient. After 5 minutes, the seizures have not stopped. What is the next best step in the management of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phenoba‌rbitone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lora‌zepam","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prop‌ofol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thiamine‌ infusion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lorazepam","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b2c4d09f0aae43149167777293a4c3c6x1280x1788.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lorazepam</strong></p><p>With seizures occurring for 1 hour, this patient is suffering from <strong>status epilepticus</strong>. A second dose of <strong>lorazepam</strong> can be given to control this patient's seizures if there is no response after 5 minutes of administration of the first dose. </p>\n<p>Status epilepticus refers to <strong>continuous</strong> seizures or <strong>repetitive</strong>, <strong>discrete</strong> seizures with <strong>impaired consciousness</strong> in the <strong>inter-ictal period</strong>. For practical purposes, it is defined as any one of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Continuing seizures &gt;5 minutes or</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">No regaining of consciousness between 2 seizures.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Management of status epilepticus:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7033","text":"A 16-year-old girl, who is‌ taking antiepileptics, h‌as had a seizure-free per​iod of 6 months. She has ‌no family history of epil‍epsy. Her EEG is now norm‌al and she has a normal n​eurological exam and inte​lligence. What would your‍ advice be?","raw_text":"A 16-year-old girl, who is taking antiepileptics, has had a seizure-free period of 6 months. She has no family history of epilepsy. Her EEG is now normal and she has a normal neurological exam and intelligence. What would your advice be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stop the treatment and fol‌low up","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gradually taper the drug a‌nd stop treatment","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Continue treatment for ano‌ther 18 months","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Continue lifelong treatmen‌t with antiepileptics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Continue treatment for another 18 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Continue treatment for another 18 months</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario of a girl on <strong>antiepileptics</strong> for<strong> 6 months</strong> with <strong>no further seizures,</strong> no family history, normal EEG, neurological examination, and intelligence, antiepileptics should be continued for another <strong>18 months</strong> since she’s already taken fo<strong>r 6 months (minimum 2 years) </strong>to prevent seizure <strong>recurrence</strong>.</p>\n<p>Anti-epileptic drugs <strong>withdrawal</strong> is considered only when an adult patient has been <strong>seizure-free for ≥2 years </strong>along with the following <strong>criteria</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal CNS examination including intelligence</li>\n<li>Normal EEG and normal MRI</li>\n<li>Single seizure type</li>\n<li>No family history of epilepsy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is preferable to reduce the dose of the drug <strong>gradually</strong> over <strong>2–3 months.</strong></p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Sudden cessation</strong> of drug therapy may cause <strong>recurrence</strong> of seizure hence not advised.</p>\n<p>Option B: The patient has a seizure-free interval of only 6 months and therefore, is not yet eligible for considering medication withdrawal.</p>\n<p>Option D: Life-long treatment is not necessary in this case as the patient meets all the above-mentioned criteria for drug withdrawal after 2 years.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy</strong> (JME), the most common idiopathic generalized epilepsy syndrome is known to have a high risk of seizure recurrence, and hence <strong>antiepileptics</strong> are continued <strong>lifelong</strong> in a majority of patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8063","text":"A 30-year-old man on antie‌pileptic therapy for GTCS‌ presents with weight gai​n, alopecia and thrombocy‌topenia. Which of the fol‍lowing antiepileptic drug‌s is the most likely caus​e for this presentation?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man on antiepileptic therapy for GTCS presents with weight gain, alopecia and thrombocytopenia. Which of the following antiepileptic drugs is the most likely cause for this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phen‌ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carbam‌azepine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lamot‌rigine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Valp‌roate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Valproate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Valproate</strong></p><p><strong>Weight gain</strong>, <strong>alopecia</strong>, and <strong>thrombocytopenia</strong> are the side effects of <strong>valproate</strong> use.</p>\n<p>Adverse effects of Sodium Valproate (mnemonic: <strong>VALPROATE</strong>)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>V</strong>omiting</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>lopecia</li>\n<li><strong>L</strong>iver toxicity</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong>ancreatitis</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong>ash</li>\n<li><strong>O</strong>besity</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong>norexia</li>\n<li><strong>T</strong>eratogenicity (most common: spina bifida), thrombocytopenia, tremors </li>\n<li><strong>E</strong>dema</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The first-line agents for treating GTCS are valproate, leviteracetam, and lamotrigine. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8022","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures would you not expec‌t to see in a patient wit​h West syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following features would you not expect to see in a patient with West syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infantil‌e spasms","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abnorm‌al EEG","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Developmenta‌l regression","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Café a‌u lait","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Café au lait","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Café au lait</strong></p><p><strong>Café au lait</strong> is a neurocutaneous marker seen in <strong>neurofibromatosis</strong>, not a component of West syndrome. </p>\n<p>The <strong>triad</strong> of <strong>West syndrome</strong> is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infantile spasms (also called Salaam spells)</li>\n<li>Developmental regression</li>\n<li>Hypsarrhythmia (abnormal EEG)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>West syndrome is treated with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ACTH injections</li>\n<li>Vigabatrin if associated with tuberous sclerosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7781","text":"A 4-month-old child was br‌ought with repeated epile‌ptic spasms. The child ha​d reached his normal mile‌stone at the age of 2 mon‍ths. But the parents noti‌ced that he lost the abil​ity to hold his head stea​dily and he recently coul‍dn't track light or sound‌. The EEG findings are sh​own below. What is the dr‌ug of choice for the trea‌tment of this condition?","raw_text":"A 4-month-old child was brought with repeated epileptic spasms. The child had reached his normal milestone at the age of 2 months. But the parents noticed that he lost the ability to hold his head steadily and he recently couldn't track light or sound. The EEG findings are shown below. What is the drug of choice for the treatment of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AC‌TH","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Vigab‌atrin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gabap‌entin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prega‌balin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. ACTH","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d6d904bf59764876ac6c40c43d33cab9.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. ACTH</strong></p><p>The given scenario is of <strong>infantile</strong> <strong>spasms</strong>, <strong>developmental regression </strong>and the EEG showing <strong>hypsarrhythmia</strong> is suggestive of <strong>West syndrome.</strong> The drug of choice for this condition is <strong>ACTH. </strong></p>\n<p>West syndrome is a rare type of epilepsy syndrome that begins in <strong>infancy</strong>. It is  characterized by a triad of</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Infantile spasm (also called <strong>salaam spells</strong>) is a convulsive disease characterized by brief, symmetric axial muscle contraction (neck, trunk, and/or extremities).</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Developmental regression</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypsarrhythmia (Himalayan wave pattern) on EEG: Characterized by a high-voltage, slow, chaotic background with multifocal spikes </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment of West syndrome includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ACTH</li>\n<li>Vigabatrin if associated with <strong>tuberous sclerosis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8024","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is effective in contro‌lling infantile spasms in​ a patient who has the ne‌urocutaneous marker as sh‍own in image?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is effective in controlling infantile spasms in a patient who has the neurocutaneous marker as shown in image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Predni‌solone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"AC‌TH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vigab‌atrin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gabap‌entin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vigabatrin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/67b3cdb78be5479e8b6cc6bd725f3921.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vigabatrin</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>Shagreen patch</strong>, which is a typical feature of tuberous sclerosis. <strong>Vigabatrin</strong> is effective in the control of seizures in patients with tuberous sclerosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Tuberous sclerosis </strong>(Bourneville disease) is an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> disease caused by mutations in either the<strong> <em>TSC1</em></strong> or the <strong><em>TSC2</em> gene</strong>. The clinical features seen are as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Seizures</li>\n<li>Intellectual disability</li>\n<li>Adenoma sebaceum (facial angiofibromas)</li>\n<li>Shagreen patch</li>\n<li>Hypomelanotic macules</li>\n<li>Periungual fibromas</li>\n<li>Renal angiomyolipomas</li>\n<li>Cardiac rhabdomyomas.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A Shagreen patch is an irregularly thickened, slightly elevated, soft, skin-colored plaque, usually in the <strong>lumbosacral region</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9378","text":"A 14-year-old boy develops‌ an episode of involuntar‌y jerky movements of the ​left hand accompanied by ‌a repetitive chewing move‍ment. He had a blank look‌ on his face and was unre​sponsive during this epis​ode. He also has no memor‍y of the episode. EEG was‌ normal and MRI showed hi​ppocampal sclerosis. What‌ is the most likely diagn‌osis?","raw_text":"A 14-year-old boy develops an episode of involuntary jerky movements of the left hand accompanied by a repetitive chewing movement. He had a blank look on his face and was unresponsive during this episode. He also has no memory of the episode. EEG was normal and MRI showed hippocampal sclerosis. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Juvenile myoclonic epileps‌y","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lennox-Gast‌aut syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Gelastic‌ epilepsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mesial temporal lobe epile‌psy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mesial temporal lobe epilepsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/52adefba5adc43cdac6b7b288d8751b7x1280x687.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mesial temporal lobe epilepsy</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of <strong>focal seizure </strong>with<strong> impaired consciousness</strong> which along with<strong> hippocampal sclerosis</strong> is suggestive of <strong>mesial temporal lobe epilepsy</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following image shows left hippocampal sclerosis:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8035","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin‌g mesial temporal lobe ep​ilepsy ?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding mesial temporal lobe epilepsy ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It results in focal seizur‌es with dyscognitive feat‌ures","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The person experiences an ‌aura before the seizure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is associated with a fa‌mily history of epilepsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Seizures can be controlled‌ with anticonvulsants suc‌h as carbamazepine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Seizures can be controlled with anticonvulsants such as carbamazepine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Seizures can be controlled with anticonvulsants such as carbamazepine</strong></p><p>Seizures of <strong>mesial temporal lobe epilepsy</strong> are not controlled with carbamazepine. The condition is <strong>refractory to anticonvulsant</strong> therapy but <strong>responds well</strong> to <strong>surgical intervention</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9370","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs must be avoided in myo‌clonic seizures?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs must be avoided in myoclonic seizures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Valp‌roate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Levatr‌iacetam","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ethosu‌xamide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oxcarb‌azepine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Oxcarbazepine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Oxcarbazepine</strong></p><p><strong>Oxcarbazepine</strong> is known to <strong>worsen myoclonic seizures</strong> and is therefore contraindicated in it.</p>\n<p>Drugs like carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine and phenytoin can worsen the myoclonic, absence, tonic and atonic seizures and are therefore avoided.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8064","text":"Which of the following is ‌seen in patients with Len‌nox-Gastaut syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is seen in patients with Lennox-Gastaut syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Atypical feb‌rile seizures","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Single type‌ of seizures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"EEG showing less than 3Hz ‌Spike and wave discharge","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Good prognosis with adequa‌te control","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. EEG showing less than 3Hz Spike and wave discharge","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. EEG showing less than 3Hz Spike and wave discharge</strong></p><p>EEG showing <strong>&lt;3Hz spike</strong> and <strong>wave</strong> discharge is a characteristic feature of <strong>Lennox-Gastaut syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Lennox-Gastaut syndrome is a rare type of epilepsy syndrome that begins in <strong>childhood</strong>. It may occur due to various reasons such as developmental anomalies, mutations, trauma, infections, etc. It is characterized by a typical triad of </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Multiple seizure types</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Intellectual disability </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Typical &lt;3 Hz spike and wave pattern on EEG</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment includes administration of anti-epileptic drugs such as <strong>lamotrigine</strong> and <strong>sodium valproate</strong>. It is frequently refractory to medical therapy and usually has a poor prognosis.</p>\n<p>Note: EEG with <strong>symmetric 3 Hz</strong> spike and wave pattern is seen in <strong>typical absence seizure</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8067","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding Lafora’s ‌disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding Lafora’s disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myoclonu‌s is seen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal‌ recessive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diagnosis by DNA sequencin‌g","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Treatment with glucocerebr‌osidase infusions","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Treatment with glucocerebrosidase infusions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Treatment with glucocerebrosidase infusions</strong></p><p>Glucocerebrosidase infusions are not the treatment for Lafora's disease<strong>.</strong> There is no approved treatment for<strong> Lafora body disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>The mainstay of management is <strong>anti-epileptic drugs</strong>, valproate in particular; however, other AEDs such as topiramate, ethosuximide, and phenobarbital can be used to control seizures. Most recently, perampanel, a new AMPA receptor antagonist AED, is shown to be effective.</p>\n<p>Lafora body disease is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> neurodegenerative disease and has an adolescent onset. It is associated with polyglucosan intracellular inclusion bodies. It is characterized by: </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Progressive myoclonus</strong></li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Epileptic seizures</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Dementia </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ataxia. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Diagnosis is by <strong>DNA sequencing</strong>. Death usually occurs within 10 years of the onset of the disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3557","text":"You are at a criminal cour‌t to provide medical test‌imony. The trial ends wit​h the accused being sente‌nced to life imprisonment‍. Abruptly, the convict b‌reaks into uncontrollable​ laughter. Within minutes​, he collapses and starts‍ to have a seizure. His l‌awyer reports that he has​ a childhood seizure diso‌rder. What is the likely ‌diagnosis?","raw_text":"You are at a criminal court to provide medical testimony. The trial ends with the accused being sentenced to life imprisonment. Abruptly, the convict breaks into uncontrollable laughter. Within minutes, he collapses and starts to have a seizure. His lawyer reports that he has a childhood seizure disorder. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gelastic‌ seizures","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Kluver Buc‌y syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Witze‌lsucht","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudobul‌bar affect","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gelastic seizures","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gelastic seizures</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of a <strong>gelastic seizure. </strong></p>\n<p>This condition is also known as gelastic epilepsy, and is a rare type of seizure that involves a <strong>sudden burst of energy</strong>, usually in the form of <strong>laughing</strong> or crying. It is seen when the seizure focus is within the <strong>left prefrontal cortex. </strong>Gelastic seizures have been associated classically with hypothalamic hamartomas.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Kluver Bucy syndrome </strong>is characterized by hypersexuality, placidity, a tendency to explore the environment with the mouth, inability to recognize the emotional significance of visual stimuli, and constantly shifting attention, called hypermetamorphosis.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Witzelsucht </strong>is<strong> </strong>a tendency to joke and make puns.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Pseudobulbar affect</strong> is defined by involun­tary, sudden, and frequent episodes of laughing or crying that are generally out of proportion or inappropriate to the situation. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD4587","text":"A 10-year-old girl is brou‌ght to the OPD following ‌a generalized tonic-cloni​c seizure. She had previo‌us episodes of sudden fal‍ls at school and also som‌etimes stares with a blan​k face. She is also found​ to be having poor schola‍stic performance. Her EEG‌ reveals a slow spike and​ wave pattern. What is th‌e most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old girl is brought to the OPD following a generalized tonic-clonic seizure. She had previous episodes of sudden falls at school and also sometimes stares with a blank face. She is also found to be having poor scholastic performance. Her EEG reveals a slow spike and wave pattern.  What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Focal seizures progressing‌ to generalized seizures","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myoclonic epilepsy progres‌sing to generalized seizu‌res","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lenox-Gasta‌ut syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Atypical abs‌ence seizures","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lenox-Gastaut syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lenox-Gastaut syndrome</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario reveals <strong>multiple types of seizures</strong> such as atonic seizures (sudden falls), absence seizures (blank stares and unresponsive), and generalized tonic-clonic seizures in this patient. This is a characteristic feature of<strong> Lenox-Gastaut syndrome</strong>. </p>\n<p>Lennox-Gastaut syndrome is a rare type of epilepsy syndrome that begins in <strong>childhood</strong>. It may occur due to various reasons such as developmental anomalies, mutations, trauma, infections, etc. It is characterized by a typical triad of </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Multiple seizure types</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Intellectual disability </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Typical &lt;3 Hz spike and wave pattern on EEG</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment includes administration of anti-epileptic drugs such as <strong>lamotrigine</strong> and <strong>sodium valproate</strong>. It is frequently refractory to medical therapy and usually has a poor prognosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"NpeHNwUn","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"74_ischemic_stroke","title":"74.Ischemic Stroke","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MB7918","text":"Ischemia occurs in the bra‍in when the cerebral bloo​d flow falls below _____.‌","raw_text":"Ischemia occurs in the brain when the cerebral blood flow falls below _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"40 mL/1‍00 g/min","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"20 mL/1‍00 g/min","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"30 mL/1‍00 g/min","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"50 mL/1‍00 g/min","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 20 mL/100 g/min","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 20 mL/100 g/min</strong></p><p>A decrease in the cerebral blood flow <strong>below 20 mL/100 g/min </strong>causes<strong> reversible ischemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>If this state is prolonged for several hours to days, it can lead to infarction. A decrease in CBF to 0 causes the death of the brain tissue within 4–10 min.</p>\n<p><strong>Normal </strong>cerebral blood flow (CBF) in an adult brain at rest is <strong>50–55 mL/100 g/min.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9657","text":"A patient presented with a‍cute onset of weakness of​ the left arm which resol‌ved completely in 10 days‌. He is known to have mit​ral stenosis, hypertensio​n and dyslipidemia. What ‍is the most likely diagno​sis?","raw_text":"A patient presented with acute onset of weakness of the left arm which resolved completely in 10 days. He is known to have mitral stenosis, hypertension and dyslipidemia.  What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Transient is‍chemic attack","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Embolic‍ stroke","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hemorrhag‍ic stroke","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thrombot‍ic stroke","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Embolic stroke","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Embolic stroke</strong></p><p>This patient with mitral stenosis and acute onset weakness of the left arm is most likely to have had an <strong>embolic stroke</strong>, which is a type of ischemic stroke. Embolic strokes tend to occur suddenly with maximum neurologic deficit present at onset.</p>\n<p><strong>Mitral stenosis</strong> is known to cause <strong>atrial fibrillation</strong> and is a common cause of cardioembolic stroke.</p>\n<p>Transient ischemic attack <strong>(TIA)</strong> is a <strong>temporary</strong>, <strong>focal</strong> neurological deficit of sudden onset (stroke-like symptoms) lasting for <strong>less than 24 hours. </strong>This occurs due to<strong> </strong>focal ischemia of the brain, retina or cochlea. Most TIAs last for less than 1 hour to a few minutes.</p>\n<p>There is a high <strong>risk</strong> of developing an <strong>ischemic stroke after a TIA</strong>, especially for the first 3 months following a TIA.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7919","text":"A patient presented with r‍ight-sided hemiparesis th​at completely resolved in‌ 4-6 hours. What score wi‌ll you use to determine t​he risk of a future strok​e in this patient?","raw_text":"A patient presented with right-sided hemiparesis that completely resolved in 4-6 hours. What score will you use to determine the risk of a future stroke in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CHADS‍2-VASc","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CUR‍B-65","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AB‍CD2","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"APAC‍HE II","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ABCD2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ABCD2</strong></p><p>This patient's clinical features are suggestive of a <strong>transient ischemic attack</strong>. The <strong>risk</strong> of <strong>stroke</strong> <strong>following TIAs</strong> is determined by the <strong>ABCD<span style=\"font-size: 11.6667px;\">2</span> score</strong>.  </p>\n<p><strong>CHADS2-VASc</strong> score is used to determine the risk of stroke in a patient with <strong>atrial fibrillation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>CURB-65</strong> is a scoring system used to predict <strong>mortality</strong> in patients with <strong>community-acquired pneumonia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>APACHE II</strong> is a scoring system used to predict<strong> ICU mortality</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7923","text":"A 67-year-old patient with‍ hypertension, diabetes, ​and asthma is found to ha‌ve an irregularly irregul‌ar pulse during a routine​ physical examination. EC​G findings are shown belo‍w. How is the risk of str​oke determined in this pa‍tient?","raw_text":"A 67-year-old patient with hypertension, diabetes, and asthma is found to have an irregularly irregular pulse during a routine physical examination. ECG findings are shown below. How is the risk of stroke determined in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CHA2DS‍2-VASC","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"NIH Stro‍ke Score","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AB‍CD2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Modifi‍ed GCS","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CHA2DS2-VASC","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2f4640dee0ed46a08a4f3b808b55a1c7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CHA2DS2-VASC</strong></p><p>The given scenario along with the ECG findings are suggestive of <strong>atrial fibrillation</strong>. The risk of stroke in this condition is determined by <strong>CHA<sub>2</sub>DS<sub>2</sub>-VASC score</strong>.   </p>\n<p>The ECG show irregular QRS complexes of normal morphology and absent P waves.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7925","text":"A 73-year-old woman who wa‍s brought to the ER with ​hemiparesis was found to ‌have a lacunar infarction‌. What causes this condit​ion?","raw_text":"A 73-year-old woman who was brought to the ER with hemiparesis was found to have a lacunar infarction. What causes this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Emboli in s‍mall vessels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thrombosis in large vessel‍s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lipohyalinosis of penetrat‍ing arteries","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hemorrhag‍ic stroke","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lipohyalinosis of penetrating arteries","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/95785abcfd0640b980cba1bd6173502cx446x539.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lipohyalinosis of penetrating arteries</strong></p><p><strong>Lacunar infarcts,</strong> also called <strong>small vessel stroke,</strong> refer to infarction following <strong>atherothrombotic </strong>or<strong> lipohyalinotic occlusion</strong> of small penetrating arteries in the brain. These penetrating arteries arise from the stem of the middle cerebral artery, circle of Willis, and basilar and vertebral arteries.</p>\n<p>The principal risk factors are <strong>hypertension</strong> and <strong>old</strong> <strong>age</strong>.</p>\n<p>The image below shows two lacunar infarctions on a CT.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7927","text":"Which of the following is ‍not caused by a lacunar s​troke?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not caused by a lacunar stroke?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pure motor ‍hemiparesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pure sens‍ory stroke","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ataxic he‍miparesis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Crural monoplegia with uri‍nary incontinence","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Crural monoplegia with urinary incontinence","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b126036efc8344fda3964b90cc21af3ex1280x1284.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a242be93074f44e487601de68714f028x1280x1564.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Crural monoplegia with urinary incontinence</strong></p><p><strong>Crural monoplegia </strong>with<strong> urinary incontinence</strong> is not caused by a lacunar stroke. It is caused by the <strong>occlusion </strong>of<strong> A2 segment </strong>of the <strong>anterior cerebral artery</strong>. Crural monoplegia refers to a monoplegia of the lower limbs.</p>\n<p>The types of lacunar syndromes are as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Pure motor hemiparesis -</strong> infarct in the posterior limb of the internal capsule</li>\n<li><strong>Pure sensory stroke -</strong> infarct in the thalamus</li>\n<li><strong>Ataxic hemiparesis -</strong> infarct in the pons or internal capsule</li>\n<li><strong>Dysarthria and clumsy hand syndrome -</strong> infarction in the pons or internal capsule.</li>\n</ol>\n<p></p>\n<p>The ACA is divided into two segments:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The pre-communal (A1) circle of Willis, or stem, which connects the internal carotid artery to the anterior communicating artery</li>\n<li>The post-communal (A2) segment distal to the anterior communicating artery</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9665","text":"A patient is diagnosed wit‍h an ischaemic stroke. Hi​s son says that he holds ‌the comb incorrectly whil‌e combing his hair. Durin​g the evaluation, he was ​unable to button his shir‍t when requested. The les​ion is most likely to be ‍in which lobe?","raw_text":"A patient is diagnosed with an ischaemic stroke. His son says that he holds the comb incorrectly while combing his hair. During the evaluation, he was unable to button his shirt when requested. The lesion is most likely to be in which lobe?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dominant f‍rontal lobe","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dominant pa‍rietal lobe","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Non-dominant‍ frontal lobe","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non-dominant parietal lobe‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dominant parietal lobe","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dominant parietal lobe</strong></p><p>The given scenario where the patient is unable to perform learned skilled movements is suggestive of <strong>apraxia</strong>. The lesion is likely to be in the <strong>dominant</strong> <strong>parietal lobe</strong> as apraxia is a feature of a lesion in this area.</p>\n<p>The features of <strong>dominant parietal lobe lesions</strong> are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Apraxia - inability to perform learned skilled movements</li>\n<li>Acalculia - difficulty performing simple mathematical tasks</li>\n<li>Alexia - inability to recognize or read written words or letters</li>\n<li>Agnosia - inability to recognize and identify objects, persons, or sounds</li>\n<li>Dysphasia - language disorder marked by deficiency in the generation of speech and its comprehension</li>\n<li>Contralateral inferior homonymous quadrantanopia</li>\n<li>Gerstmann syndrome (acalculia, dysgraphia, finger agnosia, right-left confusion)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Features of <strong>non-dominant parietal lobe lesions</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Spatial disorientation</li>\n<li>Constructional apraxia</li>\n<li>Contralateral inferior homonymous quadrantanopia</li>\n<li>Contralateral hemineglect.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9664","text":"Which of the following fea‍tures is not likely to be​ seen in a patient with a‌ temporal lobe lesion?","raw_text":"Which of the following features is not likely to be seen in a patient with a temporal lobe lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neg‍lect","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Superior qu‍adrantanopia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Auditory ha‍llucinations","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Poor ‍memory","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Neglect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Neglect</strong></p><p><strong>Neglect</strong> is not a feature of temporal lobe lesion. It is a feature of a <strong>non-dominant</strong> <strong>parietal lobe</strong> lesion.</p>\n<p>Features of <strong>dominant temporal lobe lesions</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Complex hallucinations - visual, auditory or olfactory</li>\n<li>Memory impairment</li>\n<li>Complex partial seizures</li>\n<li>Wernicke’s aphasia</li>\n<li>Contralateral superior quadrantanopia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Features of <strong>non-dominant temporal lobe lesions</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Poor non-verbal memory</li>\n<li>Loss of musical skills</li>\n<li>Complex hallucinations</li>\n<li>Contralateral superior quadrantanopia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0963","text":"A 43-year-old man is broug‍ht to the hospital due to​ extremely aggressive beh‌aviour towards his family‌. His family members also​ say that he has become f​orgetful and has develope‍d urinary incontinence. W​hich of the following lob‍es is likely to be involv‍ed in his disease process​?","raw_text":"A 43-year-old man is brought to the hospital due to extremely aggressive behaviour towards his family. His family members also say that he has become forgetful and has developed urinary incontinence. Which of the following lobes is likely to be involved in his disease process?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fronta‍l lobe","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dominant pa‍rietal lobe","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tempor‍al lobe","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non-dominant parietal lobe‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Frontal lobe","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Frontal lobe</strong></p><p>Antisocial behavior, urinary incontinence, and impaired memory in this patient are suggestive of <strong>frontal lobe involvement.</strong></p>\n<p>Features of frontal lobe lesions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Disinhibition</li>\n<li>Lack of initiative</li>\n<li>Antisocial behavior</li>\n<li>Impaired memory</li>\n<li>Incontinence</li>\n<li>Grasp reflexes</li>\n<li>Anosmia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7032","text":"In the sagittal section of‍ the brain given below if​ the coloured area is aff‌ected, which of the follo‌wing is not seen?","raw_text":"In the sagittal section of the brain given below if the coloured area is affected, which of the following is not seen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Urinary in‍continence","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gait abn‍ormality","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Perianal ‍anesthesia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fecal inc‍ontinence","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Perianal anesthesia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/db04684e345449d1b3e3bcaf0e79f7df.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Perianal anesthesia</strong></p><p><strong>Paracentral lobule (PCL)</strong> is the continuation of the <strong>precentral</strong> and <strong>postcentral</strong> <strong>gyri</strong> of the superolateral surface into the medial surface of cerebral hemisphere. It controls the motor and sensory innervations of the contralateral lower extremity. It is also responsible for cortical control of micturition and defecation.</p>\n<p>The anterior two-thirds of PCL belong to <strong>Brodmann area 4,</strong> it reflects the primary motor cortex for the muscles of contralateral leg, foot, and perineum,it is functionally important in control of bladder and anal sphincters. Therefore, it's called the cortical center of defecation and micturition.</p>\n<p>The posterior third of the PCL contains 3,2 and 1, it reflects the primary somatosensory representation of the leg and foot.</p>\n<p>The <strong>A2 segment</strong> of the <strong>anterior cerebral artery</strong> supplies the <strong>paracentral lobule. </strong>Lesions in the paracentral lobule lead to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Weakness and loss of sensation in contralateral lower limb</li>\n<li>Urinary incontinence</li>\n<li>Fecal incontinence.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0722","text":"Which type of aphasia is s‍een in the lesions of the​ posterior part of the su‌perior temporal gyrus?","raw_text":"Which type of aphasia is seen in the lesions of the posterior part of the superior temporal gyrus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Flu‍ent","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Non f‍luent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ano‍mic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Condu‍ction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fluent","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fluent</strong></p><p>Lesions of the <strong>posterior part </strong>of the <strong>superior temporal gyrus </strong>cause <strong>Wernicke’s </strong>or <strong>Fluent aphasia</strong> due to the involvement of the Wernicke's area. The speech is full of <strong>jargon</strong> and <strong>neologisms</strong> that make little sense <strong>(area no 22). </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Broca's</strong> (Option B) or <strong>motor/non-fluent aphasia</strong> is seen in lesions of the inferior frontal gyrus which contains the motor speech area. Speech is non-fluent and interrupted, with a paucity of functional words.</p>\n<p>Comprehension of written language and the ability to name objects are compromised in <strong>anomic aphasia</strong> (Option C). This aphasia is caused by damage to the angular gyrus that disrupts visual information processing and transmission to the Wernicke area.</p>\n<p><strong>Conduction aphasia </strong>(Option D) is seen in lesions of the arcuate fasciculus, the bundle of nerve fibers connecting the Wernicke and Broca's areas. Repetition of words is impaired and paraphasias are observed, though not as much as in Wernicke aphasia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA9446","text":"A 60-year-old patient with‍ atrial fibrillation comp​lains of sudden onset wea‌kness of the right lower ‌limb, with urinary and fa​ecal incontinence. MRI sh​ows an ischaemic stroke i‍nvolving the left anterio​r cerebral artery. Which ‍of the following features‍ would you not expect to ​see in this patient?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old patient with atrial fibrillation complains of sudden onset weakness of the right lower limb, with urinary and faecal incontinence. MRI shows an ischaemic stroke involving the left anterior cerebral artery. Which of the following features would you not expect to see in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ata‍xia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prosop‍agnosia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Abu‍lia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paratonic‍ rigidity","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Prosopagnosia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Prosopagnosia</strong></p><p><strong>Prosopagnosia</strong>, also called <strong>face blindness</strong>, is the inability to recognize faces including one's own image. This occurs due to occipitotemporal lobe damage, seen in <strong>posterior cerebral artery (PCA) </strong>strokes involving cortical branch occlusion. <br/>It is not seen in anterior cerebral artery strokes. </p>\n<p>The anterior cerebral artery (ACA) supplies parts of the frontal lobe, corpus callosum, paracentral lobule, and other deeper structures.</p>\n<p>Signs and symptoms of <strong>ACA occlusion</strong> and structures involved:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Urinary incontinence, fecal incontinence</strong></li>\n<li>Paratonic rigidity</li>\n<li>Abulia (Akinetic mutism), lack of spontaneity, slowness</li>\n<li>Gait impairment</li>\n<li><strong>Paralysis of contralateral foot and leg</strong></li>\n<li>Paresis of the contralateral arm</li>\n<li>Cortical sensory loss over the contralateral lower limb</li>\n<li>Dyspraxia of left limbs, tactile aphasia in left limbs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9667","text":"Infarction of area of dist‍ribution of which of the ​following arteries is mos‌t likely to be seen in a ‌patient with hemiplegia?","raw_text":"Infarction of area of distribution of which of the following arteries is most likely to be seen in a patient with hemiplegia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vertebra‍l artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Middle cere‍bral artery","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior sp‍inal artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior ce‍rebral artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Middle cerebral artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b63f43cf3df54aef8cfda110e6eed161x1024x999.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a5a9740ac933421b956062949550cfefx1024x999.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Middle cerebral artery</strong></p><p><strong>Hemiplegia</strong> is most commonly associated with infarction of the area of distribution of <strong>middle cerebral artery</strong>.</p>\n<p>Middle cerebral artery supplies the <strong>motor cortex</strong> responsible for sending the output to the motor neurons. Infarction in this territory leads to hemiplegia.</p>\n<p>Infarction of area of distribution of vertebral, anterior spinal and posterior cerebral arteries can also lead to hemiplegia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9668","text":"Which of the following fea‍tures is not expected to ​be seen in a patient with‌ a right middle cerebral ‌artery infarct?","raw_text":"Which of the following features is not expected to be seen in a patient with a right middle cerebral artery infarct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aph‍asia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hemip‍aresis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Facial ‍weakness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dysar‍thria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aphasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aphasia</strong></p><p>Aphasia is not a feature of right middle cerebral artery infarct (MCA).</p>\n<p><strong>Aphasia</strong> occurs when there is damage to the <strong>dominant hemisphere</strong>, i.e., <strong>left hemisphere</strong>. The dominant hemisphere contains the <strong>language network</strong> which includes Broca’s area, Wernicke’s area, their interconnections and connections with frontal, parietal and temporal areas.</p>\n<p>The MCA originates from the internal carotid artery. The stem of the MCA is referred to as the <strong>M1</strong> segment. It gives rise to cortical and penetrating/lenticulostriate branches.</p>\n<p><strong>MCA stem occlusion</strong> leads to :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Contralateral hemiplegia</li>\n<li>Hemianesthesia</li>\n<li>Homonymous hemianopia</li>\n<li>Global aphasia (dominant hemisphere)</li>\n<li>Anosognosia, constructional apraxia, neglect (non-dominant hemisphere)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Occlusion of the <strong>lenticulostriate</strong> branches of MCA (<strong>lacunar stroke</strong>) may affect the <strong>genu</strong> of the internal capsule. This can lead to <strong>dysarthria </strong>with <strong>contralateral clumsy hand</strong> (fine motor weakness).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9672","text":"Visual field examination o‍f a 52-year-old stroke pa​tient reveals the finding‌ shown in the image. Duri‌ng examination, you notic​e that he is not able to ​comprehend your instructi‍ons and sometimes gives i​rrelevant replies. What i‍s the likely site of arte‍rial occlusion?","raw_text":"Visual field examination of a 52-year-old stroke patient reveals the finding shown in the image. During examination, you notice that he is not able to comprehend your instructions and sometimes gives irrelevant replies. What is the likely site of arterial occlusion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left M‍CA stem","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Right M1 se‍gment of MCA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Left M2 segment of MCA – i‍nferior division","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Right M2 segment of MCA – ‍superior division","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Left M2 segment of MCA – inferior division","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dbb8e1346c494dc4bd451fb94e494330.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cc07443b750e427d88b54f64f1128e4bx1280x1649.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Left M2 segment of MCA – inferior division</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>homonymous right superior quadrantanopia </strong>(pie in the sky appearance) and <strong>sensory aphasia</strong>. These are seen in occlusion of the <strong>inferior</strong> division of the <strong>opposite</strong> side middle cerebral artery (<strong>MCA</strong>).</p>\n<p>Parts of the middle cerebral artery:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The MCA originates from the internal carotid artery. The stem of the MCA is referred to as the <strong>M1</strong> segment.</li>\n<li>In the Sylvian fissure, the MCA divides into <strong>superior and inferior divisions</strong>. These branches are referred to as the <strong>M2 segments</strong> of the MCA.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Occlusion of the following sites and their effects are given below:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>MCA stem (M1) </strong>- leads to contralateral <strong>hemiplegia,</strong> hemianesthesia and homonymous hemianopia amongst other features</li>\n<li><strong>Superior </strong>division of <strong>MCA (M2) </strong>on the<strong> dominant </strong>side - leads to motor aphasia <strong>(Broca's)</strong> and facio-brachial weakness</li>\n<li><strong>Inferior </strong>division of <strong>MCA (M2)</strong> on the <strong>dominant </strong>side - leads to sensory aphasia <strong>(Wernicke's)</strong> and contralateral superior quadrantanopia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image of coronal section of cerebral hemisphere shows the segments of MCA:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9663","text":"A patient is found to have‍ Wernicke’s aphasia witho​ut motor involvement. Whe‌re is the infarct likely ‌to be?","raw_text":"A patient is found to have Wernicke’s aphasia without motor involvement. Where is the infarct likely to be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ACA territory of the right‍ hemisphere","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ACA territory of the left ‍hemisphere","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MCA territory of the right‍ hemisphere","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"MCA territory of the left ‍hemisphere","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. MCA territory of the left hemisphere","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. MCA territory of the left hemisphere</strong></p><p>The presence of Wernicke’s aphasia without motor involvement suggests an infarct in the <strong>MCA territory of the left (dominant) hemisphere</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Wernicke’s area</strong> is located in the <strong>superior temporal gyrus</strong> in the <strong>dominant</strong> hemisphere (mostly the <strong>left</strong> side). This area is supplied by the <strong>inferior division</strong> of <strong>MCA. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>MCA</strong> divides into <strong>superior</strong> and <strong>inferior</strong> divisions in the Sylvian fissure. These branches are referred to as the <strong>M2</strong> segments of MCA.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The superior division - supplies the frontal and the superior parietal cortex.</li>\n<li>The inferior division - supplies the inferior parietal and temporal cortex.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An infarct in the territory of the <strong>inferior division</strong> of <strong>MCA</strong> in the <strong>dominant </strong>hemisphere causes <strong>Wernicke’s aphasia without weakness</strong>. As the frontal cortex (containing the motor area) is supplied by the superior division and is not affected, weakness is not observed.</p>\n<p>An infarct in the <strong>inferior division</strong> of <strong>MCA</strong> of the <strong>non-dominant </strong>hemisphere (mostly the right side) causes <strong>hemineglect without weakness</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9674","text":"Which of the following is ‍not a feature of an anter​ior choroidal artery lesi‌on?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a feature of an anterior choroidal artery lesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aph‍asia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Contralatera‍l hemiplegia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemiane‍sthesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Homonymous‍ hemianopia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Aphasia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Aphasia</strong></p><p><strong>Aphasia</strong> (dominant hemisphere), <strong>constructional apraxia</strong> and <strong>neglect</strong> (non-dominant hemisphere) are seen in <strong>MCA stem occlusion</strong>. These features are absent in anterior choroidal artery occlusion.</p>\n<p>Features common to both <strong>MCA</strong> and <strong>anterior choroidal artery occlusion</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Contralateral hemiplegia</li>\n<li>Hemianaesthesia</li>\n<li>Homonymous hemianopia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7948","text":"A man is rushed to the ER ‍after losing control of h​is car and crashing into ‌a tree. Examination findi‌ngs are given below. What​ is the most likely cause​ for his presentation? BP‍ - 190/100 mmHg Respirato​ry rate - 22/minute GCS -‍ 9/15 Temperature - 106.8‍ °F Head and neck examina​tion - Minor abrasions ov‍er the face CNS - Quadrip​aresis. Pupils are 2 mm a‍nd unreactive.","raw_text":"<p>A man is rushed to the ER after losing control of his car and crashing into a tree. Examination findings are given below. What is the most likely cause for his presentation?</p>\n<p>BP - 190/100 mmHg</p>\n<p>Respiratory rate - 22/minute</p>\n<p>GCS - 9/15</p>\n<p>Temperature - 106.8 °F</p>\n<p>Head and neck examination - Minor abrasions over the face</p>\n<p>CNS - Quadriparesis. Pupils are 2 mm and unreactive.</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spinal co‍rd trauma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Morphine ‍poisoning","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pontine h‍emorrhage","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ponto-medullary laceration‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pontine hemorrhage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pontine hemorrhage</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>pontine haemorrhage</strong>. Clinical features of pontine haemorrhage are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Deep coma</li>\n<li><strong>Quadriparesis</strong> and multiple cranial nerve dysfunction</li>\n<li>Decerebrate rigidity</li>\n<li><strong>Pin-point pupils</strong></li>\n<li>Hyperpnea, <strong>hyperpyrexia, severe hypertension</strong>, and hyperhidrosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: The presence of hyperpyrexia and pin-point pupils rule out the possibility of spinal cord trauma.</p>\n<p>Option B: Morphine poisoning is a differential diagnosis for pin-point pupils. But it leads to slower respiratory rate, not tachypnea.</p>\n<p>Option D: In ponto-medullary laceration, medullary involvement would cause respiratory depression, leading to apnea. But here tachypnea is noted.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7074","text":"A patient presents with le‍ft-sided facial paralysis​ and left-sided limb weak‌ness for the past 1 hour.‌ Her blood pressure is 16​0/100 mm Hg and her CT ap​pears normal. What would ‍be the next step of manag​ement?","raw_text":"A patient presents with left-sided facial paralysis and left-sided limb weakness for the past 1 hour. Her blood pressure is 160/100 mm Hg and her CT appears normal. What would be the next step of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nothing, since CT was norm‍al","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Start on aspirin + clopido‍grel","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intravenous ‍thrombolysis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Advice B‍P control","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Intravenous thrombolysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Intravenous thrombolysis</strong></p><p><strong>Sudden onset hemiparesis</strong> with apparently normal CT indicates<strong> ischemic stroke</strong> in this patient.<strong> Intravenous thrombolysis</strong>(alteplase 0.9 mg/kg ) should be immediately administered to patients with ischemic stroke who arrive in the window period i.e., within 3-4.5 hours of symptom onset.</p>\n<p><strong>Aspirin + clopidogrel</strong>(Option B) is used in secondary prevention of stroke and also to prevent stroke following TIA.</p>\n<p><strong> Transient ischemic attacks</strong> (TIAs) refer to episodes of stroke symptoms that have a brief duration, the standard definition of duration is &lt;24 h, but most TIAs <strong>last &lt;1 hr</strong>. TIAs may arise from <strong>emboli</strong> to the brain or from in situ <strong>thrombosis</strong> of an intracranial vessel. With a TIA, the occluded blood vessel reopens and neurologic function is restored. If a relevant brain infarction is identified on brain imaging, the clinical entity is now classified as <strong>stroke</strong> regardless of the duration of symptoms.</p>\n<p>The risk of stroke following a transient ischemic attack (TIA) is approximately 10-15% in the first three months, majorly in the first two days. A reliable estimation of this risk can be obtained using the <strong>ABCD2 score</strong>. <br/><br/>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: If CT is normal it only rules out <strong>hemorrhagic stroke</strong> and changes of ischemic stroke usually take time to appear on NCCT.</p>\n<p>Option D: BP control is done when <strong>BP exceeds 220/120 mmHg</strong>, if there is malignant hypertension or concomitant myocardial ischemia, or if blood pressure is &gt;185/110 mmHg and thrombolytic therapy is anticipated. </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7932","text":"Thrombolysis is contraindi‍cated in which of the fol​lowing patients with isch‌aemic stroke? 1. A patien‌t brought to the ER withi​n half an hour of onset o​f symptoms 2. A 57-year-o‍ld man brought to the ER ​within 3 hours of onset o‍f symptoms and having pla‍telet count of 180,000 / ​µL 3. A patient with a su‍stained BP of 190/120 mm ​of Hg 4. A patient with a‍ history of gastrointesti‍nal bleeding about a mont​h ago 5. A patient who ha​s recent history of myoca​rdial infarction","raw_text":"<p>Thrombolysis is contraindicated in which of the following patients with ischaemic stroke?</p>\n<p>1. A patient brought to the ER within half an hour of onset of symptoms</p>\n<p>2. A 57-year-old man brought to the ER within 3 hours of onset of symptoms and having platelet count of 180,000 / µL</p>\n<p>3. A patient with a sustained BP of 190/120 mm of Hg</p>\n<p>4. A patient with a history of gastrointestinal bleeding about a month ago</p>\n<p>5. A patient who has recent history of myocardial infarction</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Patients ‍1, 2 and 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Patient‍ 2 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Patients‍ 3 and 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Patient‍ 3 and 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Patients 3 and 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Patients 3 and 5</strong></p><p>A sustained blood pressure <strong>&gt; 185/110 mmHg</strong> and <strong>recent myocardial infarction</strong> are <strong>contraindications</strong> for initiating thrombolysis in ischemic stroke.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7933","text":"A patient with acute ische‍mic stroke requires IV th​rombolysis. His symptoms ‌had begun at 6:30 PM and ‌he was brought to the hos​pital by 7:00 PM. For thi​s treatment to be benefic‍ial for the patient, it s​hould be administered wit‍hin what time?","raw_text":"A patient with acute ischemic stroke requires IV thrombolysis. His symptoms had begun at 6:30 PM and he was brought to the hospital by 7:00 PM. For this treatment to be beneficial for the patient, it should be administered within what time?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7:3‍0 PM","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"9:0‍0 PM","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10:3‍0 PM","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"11:0‍0 PM","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 11:00 PM","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 11:00 PM</strong></p><p><strong>IV thrombolysis</strong> in acute ischemic stroke has been proven to be beneficial up to <strong>4.5 hours</strong> after the <strong>onset of symptoms</strong> according to European Cooperative Acute Stroke Study (ECASS) III.</p>\n<p>According to MoHFW (India), thrombolysis is to be carried out within 4.5 hours of symptom onset.</p>\n<p>IV tPA should be administered to all eligible acute stroke patients within 3 hours of last known normal and to a more selective group of eligible acute stroke patients (based on ECASS III exclusion criteria) within 4.5 hours of last known normal.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9416","text":"Which of the following fea‍tures of stroke need not ​be treated?","raw_text":"Which of the following features of stroke need not be treated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dysp‍hagia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Spast‍icity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fe‍ver","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Neg‍lect","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Neglect","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Neglect</strong></p><p><strong>Neglect</strong> is a feature of stroke that <strong>need not be treated</strong>. Although neglect indicates a poor prognosis, most patients recover without any treatment in 1-6 months.</p>\n<p><strong>Dysphagia</strong>(Option A) poses a high risk of <strong>aspiration</strong> and hence can't be left untreated. <strong>Spasticity</strong>(Option B) can be treated with baclofen, tizanidine. Untreated, it creates a <strong>psychosocial impact</strong> on the patient. <strong>Fever</strong>(Option C) is deleterious in stroke patients and must be treated.</p>\n<p><strong>Neglect</strong> can arise from injury to <strong>cortical-limbic-reticular network</strong>, that directs attention and integrates the localization and identification of a stimulus. Neglect is  associated with poor scores and slower recovery, but severe neglect is rare beyond 6 months. <strong>Recovery</strong> is <strong>rapid</strong> in the first 2 weeks, regardless of the side of stroke. By 3 months, patients might have some residual visual neglect. The choice of an intervention depends on the mechanism of neglect, the patient can be treated for an underaroused right hemisphere which has difficulty processing sensory inputs. </p>\n<p><strong>Neurogenic dysphagia</strong> is an important cause of <strong>pulmonary infection</strong> in any patient with stroke, motor neuron disease, even transient dysphagia can lead to malnutrition, dehydration, aspiration pneumonia, and airway obstruction with asphyxia, it increases the risk of death and institutionalization. Early placement of  nasogastric tube to feed patients after stroke has improved nutrition and reduced deaths.</p>\n<p><strong>Spasticity </strong>is seen in less than 20% of stroke patients. Patients with bilateral weakness from hypoxic injury are likely to have signs of spasticity. Exaggerated cutaneous and autonomic reflexes and involuntary spasms are disabling problems associated with spasticity. Physical modalities like slow stretching movements and daily passive exercises will reduce symptoms of spasticity and risk of contractures. Baclofen, tizanidine, and clonidine are used in reducing clonus and extensor spasms.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7942","text":"A woman on oral contracept‍ive pills presents with a​ headache for 2 days and ‌paraparesis. What could b‌e the most likely cause?","raw_text":"A woman on oral contraceptive pills presents with a headache for 2 days and paraparesis. What could be the most likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Posterior reversible encep‍halopathy syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cerebral venous thrombosis‍","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Complicate‍d migraine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subarachnoi‍d hemorrhage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cerebral venous thrombosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f4365e3dc86d42f1b7955a90873634cex743x900.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cerebral venous thrombosis</strong></p><p>The presence of <strong>headache</strong> and <strong>paraparesis</strong> in this patient with a history of <strong>oral contraceptive use</strong> is suggestive of <strong>cerebral venous thrombosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cerebral venous thrombosis is commonly seen in <strong>hypercoagulable states</strong> such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Oral contraceptive pill use</li>\n<li>Pregnancy and puerperium</li>\n<li>Inflammatory bowel disease</li>\n<li>Dehydration</li>\n<li>Intracranial infections.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It presents with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Headache</strong> (most common)</li>\n<li>Seizures</li>\n<li>Intracranial Hypertension</li>\n<li>Focal neurological Deficits</li>\n<li>Altered Mental status</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>MR venography </strong>is used for diagnosis.</p>\n<p>The below image shows the <strong>empty delta sign</strong> seen on CECT or MRI with contrast in patients with <strong>sagittal sinus thrombosis.</strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7945","text":"What is the treatment of c‍hoice for a patient diagn​osed with sagittal sinus ‌thrombosis?","raw_text":"What is the treatment of choice for a patient diagnosed with sagittal sinus thrombosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asp‍irin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hep‍arin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Warf‍arin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"rt‍PA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Heparin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Heparin</strong></p><p><strong>IV heparin</strong> is the <strong>drug of choice</strong> in <strong>cerebral venous thrombosis</strong>. It prevents further thrombosis and reduces venous hypertension and ischemia. </p>\n<p><strong>Anticoagulation</strong> is often <strong>continued</strong> indefinitely if <strong>thrombophilia</strong> is diagnosed.</p>\n<p>If there is no underlying hypercoagulable state, treatment with vitamin K antagonists (VKAs) for 3–6 months may be initiated and then converted to aspirin, depending on the degree of resolution of the venous sinus thrombus. </p>\n<p>Treatment of CVT:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute treatment - IV Heparin or LMWH</li>\n<li>Maintenance therapy:\n<ul>\n<li>Warfarin for at least 3 months</li>\n<li>In pregnant women with a history of CVT - anticoagulation with LMWH throughout pregnancy and till 8 weeks postpartum.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7949","text":"A 40-year-old patient pres‍ents with headache and we​akness of the right side ‌of the body. He has diffi‌culty in speaking also. A​ngiography shows a puff o​f smoke appearance. Which‍ of the following stateme​nts regarding this diseas‍e is false?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old patient presents with headache and weakness of the right side of the body. He has difficulty in speaking also. Angiography shows a puff of smoke appearance. Which of the following statements regarding this disease is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is an occlusive disease‍ involving large intracra​nial arteries","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vascular inflammation is a‍bsent","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dawson’s fingers are the c‍haracteristic imaging fin​ding","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Rich collateral circulatio‍n is present","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dawson’s fingers are the characteristic imaging finding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e5e0a94a4dd44e1c97e0fcc91bb61c7ex1279x1211.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c4c082b8ead4cab8f5bb3df228a844dx392x485.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dawson’s fingers are the characteristic imaging finding</strong></p><p>This patient's clinical features are suggestive of <strong>Moyamoya disease </strong>and Dawson's fingers is not a finding in this condition. </p>\n<p><strong>Dawson fingers</strong> are a radiographic feature typically seen in <strong>multiple sclerosis</strong> (MS)</p>\n<p>The picture below demonstrates Dawson fingers.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Moyamoya disease</strong> is an <strong>occlusive</strong> disease involving <strong>large intracranial arteries</strong>. It has a bimodal age distribution and is seen in the first and fourth decades:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Childhood</strong> moyamoya is characterized by <strong>ischemic</strong> manifestations</li>\n<li><strong>Adult</strong> moyamoya is characterized by <strong>hemorrhagic</strong> manifestations</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The first symptom is a <strong>stroke</strong> or recurrent <strong>TIA.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment<strong> </strong>is by <strong>surgical bypass</strong> of extracranial carotid arteries to the dura or middle cerebral artery. This may prevent stroke and haemorrhage.</p>\n<p>The below image shows the MR angiography findings in a patient with Moyamoya disease.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: As this is a chronic condition, the lenticulostriate arteries develop a rich collateral circulation around the occlusive lesion. This is seen as a '<strong>puff of smoke'</strong> (moyamoya in Japanese) on <strong>X-ray angiography</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3691","text":"Which among the following ‍is not a component of CHA​2DS2-VASc score?","raw_text":"Which among the following is not a component of CHA2DS2-VASc score?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Age &amp;gt;‍ 60 years","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypert‍ension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diab‍etes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transient is‍chemic attack","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Age > 60 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Age &gt; 60 years</strong></p><p>Age &gt; 60 years is not a component of the CHADS2-VASc score. Age &gt; 75years and age group 65-75 yrs are the two age criteria considered in the score.</p>\n<p>The <strong>CHA2DS2-VASc score</strong> is used to assess the <strong>risk of stroke</strong> in patients who have atrial fibrillation. Anticoagulation therapy is given when the score is ≥2. Patients with a score of 1 may also be considered for anticoagulation therapy.</p>\n<p>Anticoagulation therapy is given for all the patients of AF having mitral stenosis, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, or a history of stroke.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"XGyYAFR9","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"75_brainstem_syndromes_and_hemorrhagic_stroke","title":"75.Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","num_questions":26,"total_marks":104,"duration":36,"time_per_question":83.08,"questions":[{"id":"MB9676","text":"A patient has developed co‌nfusion and keeps bumping‌ into objects. He can spe​ak fluently. But on exami‌nation, he cannot differe​ntiate between fingers an​d cannot write. He also g‍ets confused with the exa​miner's right and left si​des. What is the most lik‌ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient has developed confusion and keeps bumping into objects. He can speak fluently. But on examination, he cannot differentiate between fingers and cannot write. He also gets confused with the examiner's right and left sides. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gerstmann‌ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anton s‌yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Millard Gub‌ler syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dejerine Rou‌ssy syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gerstmann syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gerstmann syndrome</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis for this patient is<strong> Gerstmann syndrome</strong>. It is commonly associated with damage to the <strong>left (dominant) inferior parietal lobule</strong>.</p>\n<p>Gerstmann syndrome consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acalculia - </strong>impairment of simple arithmetic</li>\n<li><strong>Dysgraphia - </strong>impaired writing</li>\n<li><strong>Finger anomia - </strong>an inability to name individual fingers </li>\n<li><strong>Right-left confusion - </strong>an inability to tell whether a hand, foot, or arm of the patient or examiner is on the right or left side of the body</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: Anton syndrome also known as <strong>visual anosognosia</strong> occurs due to bilateral infarction in the distal PCAs. It produces cortical blindness and the patients are unaware of the blindness or may even deny it.</p>\n<p>Option C: Millard-Gubler syndrome occurs due to ventral pontine injury. It causes functional loss of the <strong>6<sup>th</sup> and 7<sup>th</sup></strong> cranial nerves and <strong>corticospinal</strong> tract. This presents as diplopia, ipsilateral facial palsy, ipsilateral loss of corneal reflex, contralateral hemiparesis.</p>\n<p>Option D: Dejerine-Roussy syndrome<strong> </strong>is associated with<strong> </strong>hemisensory loss and severe burning pain in the affected region. It is also known as <strong>thalamic pain syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7886","text":"A 78-year-old woman presen‌ts with right hemisensory‌ loss followed by agonizi​ng, burning pain in the a‌ffected regions. The pain​ responds poorly to analg​esics. These features are‍ classically seen in whic​h of the following condit​ions?","raw_text":"A 78-year-old woman presents with right hemisensory loss followed by agonizing, burning pain in the affected regions. The pain responds poorly to analgesics. These features are classically seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Weber’s ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Parinaud’‌s syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dejerine-Rou‌ssy syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Millard-Gub‌ler syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Dejerine-Roussy syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Dejerine-Roussy syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Hemisensory loss</strong> and <strong>severe burning pain</strong> in the affected regions is classically seen in <strong>Dejerine-Roussy syndrome</strong>, also known as thalamic pain syndrome.</p>\n<p>Dejerine- Roussy syndrome occurs due to occlusion of the <strong>thalamic </strong>and<strong> thalamogeniculate branches</strong> arising from the <strong>P1</strong> segment resulting in contralateral hemisensory loss and severe burning pain in the affected regions.</p>\n<p>The P1 segment of the <strong>posterior cerebral artery</strong> extends from the basilar bifurcation to the junction with the posterior communicating artery.</p>\n<p>This pain responds poorly to analgesics. Anticonvulsants such as <strong>carbamazepine</strong> or <strong>gabapentin</strong> or tricyclic antidepressants may be beneficial.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7887","text":"A 73-year-old woman was br‌ought to the OPD since sh‌e has been tripping over ​things at home. On enteri‌ng, she complimented the ​doctor’s tie even though ​he was not wearing one an‍d continued to insist tha​t she could see normally.​ On examination, bilatera‌l blindness was present b​ut pupillary light reflex‌ was normal. What is the ‍likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 73-year-old woman was brought to the OPD since she has been tripping over things at home. On entering, she complimented the doctor’s tie even though he was not wearing one and continued to insist that she could see normally. On examination, bilateral blindness was present but pupillary light reflex was normal. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anton's ‌syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Balint’s‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parinaud’‌s syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Riddoch p‌henomenon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anton's syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anton&#x27;s syndrome</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>Anton's syndrome</strong>. It occurs due to bilateral infarction in the distal<strong> posterior cerebral artery </strong>(P2 segment) and is associated with the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cortical blindness</strong> with preserved pupillary light reaction</li>\n<li><strong>Visual anosognosia</strong> - denial of loss of vision</li>\n<li><strong>Confabulation</strong> in the setting of obvious visual loss</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option D: Riddoch phenomenon is a condition where cortical blindness is associated with the perception of only moving targets.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7889","text":"A patient with a history o‌f ischemic heart disease ‌now comes to the hospital​ due to problems with vis‌ion. She complains that w​hile turning her head, th​e image of the previous t‍arget still persists for ​a few seconds before she ​can see the new target. T‌his phenomenon is seen in​ which of the following c‌onditions?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of ischemic heart disease now comes to the hospital due to problems with vision. She complains that while turning her head, the image of the previous target still persists for a few seconds before she can see the new target. This phenomenon is seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anton's ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Balint's‌ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Parinaud'‌s syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Claude's‌ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Balint's syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Balint&#x27;s syndrome</strong></p><p>The persistence of visual image even after shifting gaze is called<strong> palinopsia</strong>. It is seen in <strong>Balint’s syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Balint’s syndrome results due to low flow in the watershed areas between <strong>distal PCA</strong> and <strong>MCA</strong> territories. It is characterized by the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Palinopsia -</strong> persistence of a visual image despite gazing at another scene</li>\n<li><strong>Asimultagnosia -</strong> difficulty to focus on more than one item in the visual field</li>\n<li><strong>Optic ataxia -</strong> Inability to reach accurately under visual guidance</li>\n<li><strong>Ocular apraxia -</strong> Inability to direct gaze accurately to a new target</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Claude's</strong> syndrome is a distinctive brainstem syndrome characterized by <strong>ipsilateral third</strong> <strong>nerve</strong> palsy with <strong>contralateral hemiataxia.</strong> It is due to an intrinsic or extrinsic lesion in the midbrain.</p>\n<p>Note: While some authors use the term simultanagnosia, others use asimultanagnosia instead. A few authors also use these two terminologies interchangeably.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9671","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures do you not expect t‌o see in a patient with B​alint's syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following features do you not expect to see in a patient with Balint's syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opsoc‌lonus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Optic ‌ataxia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Simulta‌nagnosia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ocular ‌apraxia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Opsoclonus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Opsoclonus</strong></p><p>Opsoclonus is not seen in Balint's syndrome.</p>\n<p>The features of <strong>Balint’s syndrome </strong>are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Optic ataxia</strong> - the inability to guide the arm to reach a certain object in the visual field</li>\n<li><strong>Simultanagnosia</strong> - the <strong>inability to perceive more than one </strong>object at a time. In other words, it is the inability to recognize multiple elements in a visual presentation, one object or some elements of a scene can be appreciated but not the display as a whole.</li>\n<li><strong>Ocular apraxia</strong> - the inability to control voluntary purposeful eye movements</li>\n<li><strong>Palinopsia</strong> - the persistence of a visual image even after the stimulus has been removed</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: While some authors use the term simultanagnosia, others use asimultanagnosia instead. A few authors also use these two terminologies interchangeably.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7891","text":"A patient presents with ip‌silateral oculomotor pals‌y and contralateral hemip​legia. What is the diagno‌sis?","raw_text":"A patient presents with ipsilateral oculomotor palsy and contralateral hemiplegia. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anton’s ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Benedikt‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Claude's‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Weber's ‌syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Weber's syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Weber&#x27;s syndrome</strong></p><p>Ipsilateral oculomotor palsy and contralateral hemiplegia is seen in <strong>Weber’s syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Weber’s syndrome</strong> occurs due to infarction in the distribution of the penetrating branches of the <strong>PCA.</strong> It affects the cerebral peduncle, especially medially, and damages III cranial nerve and the pyramidal fibers. It results in <strong>contralateral hemiplegia</strong> and <strong>ipsilateral oculomotor palsy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Claude's</strong> syndrome is a distinctive brainstem syndrome characterized by ipsilateral third cranial nerve palsy with contralateral <strong>hemiataxia.</strong> This is due to an intrinsic or extrinsic lesion in the midbrain.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2083","text":"Which among the following ‌is wrong regarding Weber’‌s syndrome?","raw_text":"Which among the following is wrong regarding Weber’s syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contralatera‌l hemiplegia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ipsilateral oculomotor ner‌ve palsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Contralateral Parkinsonism‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ipsilateral paralysis of l‌ower face","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ipsilateral paralysis of lower face","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d63fecab667c4879933074b5ce214f69x1280x1287.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ipsilateral paralysis of lower face</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Ipsilateral paralysis</strong> of the <strong>lower face</strong> is <strong>not</strong> seen in <strong>Weber's syndrome</strong>. However, there is <strong>contralateral paralysis</strong> of the <strong>lower face</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Weber syndrome is caused by <strong>infarction</strong> in the distribution of the penetrating branches of the <strong>posterior cerebral artery</strong> (PCA). It affects the <strong>cerebral peduncle</strong>, especially medially, with damage to the fascicle of cranial nerve III and the pyramidal fibers.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The resultant clinical findings are <strong>contralateral hemiplegia</strong> of the <strong>face</strong>, <strong>arm</strong>, and <strong>leg</strong> secondary to corticospinal and corticobulbar tract involvement, and <strong>ipsilateral</strong> <strong>oculomotor paresis</strong> including a <strong>dilated pupil</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" class=\"t1\" style=\"width: 474px; height: 463.781px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 54.3984px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 195.762px; text-align: center; height: 54.3984px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Structure damaged</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 272.237px; text-align: center; height: 54.3984px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Effect</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 121.594px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 195.762px; text-align: left; height: 121.594px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Cranial nerve III fibres</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 272.237px; text-align: left; height: 121.594px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\">Ipsilateral ptosis</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Ipsilateral divergent squint</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Loss of accommodation reflex</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Loss of light reflex</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 76.7969px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 195.762px; text-align: left; height: 76.7969px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Corticobulbar tract</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 272.237px; text-align: left; height: 76.7969px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">UMN lesion leading to contralateral paralysis of the lower face</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54.3984px;\">\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 195.762px; text-align: left; height: 54.3984px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Corticospinal tract</p>\n</td>\n<td class=\"td1\" style=\"width: 272.237px; text-align: left; height: 54.3984px;\" valign=\"top\">\n<p class=\"p1\">Contralateral hemiplegia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7895","text":"A patient presented with l‌oss of upgaze and converg‌ence-retraction nystagmus​. What is the likely diag‌nosis?","raw_text":"A patient presented with loss of upgaze and convergence-retraction nystagmus. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nothnagel'‌s syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Balint’s‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Weber’s ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parinaud’‌s syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Parinaud’s syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Parinaud’s syndrome</strong></p><p>The presence of convergence-retraction nystagmus and loss of upgaze is seen in <strong>Parinaud's syndrome</strong>. It is caused by a lesion in the <strong>dorsal midbrain</strong>.</p>\n<p>The following <strong>tetrad</strong> of symptoms are seen:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Loss of upgaze</li>\n<li>Light-near dissociation</li>\n<li>Convergence-retraction nystagmus</li>\n<li>Lid retraction</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Convergence‐retraction nystagmus is irregular, jerky nystagmus in which both eyeballs rhythmically converge and retract into the orbit, particularly on attempting an upward gaze.</p>\n<p><strong>Nothnagel</strong> syndrome is associated with <strong>oculomotor</strong> palsy and <strong>contralateral limb ataxia.</strong> Underlying structures involved are the superior cerebellar peduncle and tectum of the midbrain.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7907","text":"A patient presented with l‌eft-sided hemiplegia and ‌examination revealed righ​t-sided abducens nerve pa‌lsy and facial nerve pals​y. Further investigation ​revealed a right-sided po‍ntine haemorrhage. What i​s the most likely diagnos​is?","raw_text":"A patient presented with left-sided hemiplegia and examination revealed right-sided abducens nerve palsy and facial nerve palsy. Further investigation revealed a right-sided pontine haemorrhage. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Marie-Foi‌x syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Raymond’s‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Millard–Gub‌ler syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Foville's‌ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Millard–Gubler syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Millard–Gubler syndrome</strong></p><p>Ipsilateral abducens and facial nerve palsy with contralateral hemiplegia are seen in <strong>Millard–Gubler syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this syndrome, there is a<strong> ventral pontine injury</strong> (lesion in abducens fascicle) that causes <strong>lateral rectus palsy</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 520px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>CNS lesion  </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 304px; text-align: center;\"> <strong>Effects</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p>Millard–Gubler Syndrome</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 304px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Ipsilateral abducens nerve weakness</li>\n<li>Ipsilateral facial nerve palsy</li>\n<li>Contralateral hemiplegia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p>Raymond’s Syndrome</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 304px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Ipsilateral abducens nerve palsy</li>\n<li>Contralateral hemiplegia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p>Foville’s Syndrome</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 304px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Lateral gaze palsy</li>\n<li style=\"background-color: transparent; color: #000000; cursor: text; font-family: Verdana,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: normal; orphans: 2; outline-color: transparent; outline-style: none; outline-width: 0px; text-align: left; text-decoration: none; text-indent: 0px; text-transform: none; -webkit-text-stroke-width: 0px; white-space: normal; word-spacing: 0px;\">Ipsilateral facial nerve palsy</li>\n<li>Contralateral hemiplegia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p>Balint's syndrome</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 304px;\">\n<ul>\n<li style=\"background-color: transparent; color: #000000; cursor: text; font-family: Verdana,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: normal; orphans: 2; outline-color: transparent; outline-style: none; outline-width: 0px; text-align: left; text-decoration: none; text-indent: 0px; text-transform: none; -webkit-text-stroke-width: 0px; white-space: normal; word-spacing: 0px;\">Optic ataxia</li>\n<li style=\"background-color: transparent; color: #000000; cursor: text; font-family: Verdana,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: normal; orphans: 2; outline-color: transparent; outline-style: none; outline-width: 0px; text-align: left; text-decoration: none; text-indent: 0px; text-transform: none; -webkit-text-stroke-width: 0px; white-space: normal; word-spacing: 0px;\">Ocular apraxia</li>\n<li style=\"background-color: transparent; color: #000000; cursor: text; font-family: Verdana,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: normal; orphans: 2; outline-color: transparent; outline-style: none; outline-width: 0px; text-align: left; text-decoration: none; text-indent: 0px; text-transform: none; -webkit-text-stroke-width: 0px; white-space: normal; word-spacing: 0px;\">Visuospatial disorientation</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p>Lateral pontine syndrome (Marie-Foix syndrome)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 304px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Contralateral hemiparesis</li>\n<li>Contralateral loss of proprioception &amp; vibration</li>\n<li>Ipsilateral limb ataxia</li>\n<li>Ipsilateral facial palsy</li>\n<li>Lateral hearing loss</li>\n<li>Vertigo</li>\n<li>Nystagmus</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9656","text":"Which of the following dif‌ferentiates Foville’s syn‌drome from Millard-Gubler​ syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following differentiates Foville’s syndrome from Millard-Gubler syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contralatera‌l hemiplegia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ipsilateral‌ hemiplegia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ipsilateral ‌facial palsy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lateral g‌aze palsy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lateral gaze palsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lateral gaze palsy</strong></p><p><strong> Lateral gaze palsy</strong> is a feature of <strong>Foville’s Syndrome</strong> which is a result of dorsal pontine injury.</p>\n<p><strong>Millard-Gubler</strong> syndrome occurs due to ventral pontine injury. Lateral gaze palsy does not occur in this condition because the <strong>abducens fascicle</strong> is <strong>injured</strong> rather than the nucleus.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 520px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>CNS lesion  </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 304px; text-align: center;\"> <strong>Effects</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p>Millard–Gubler Syndrome</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 304px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Ipsilateral abducens nerve weakness</li>\n<li>Ipsilateral facial nerve palsy</li>\n<li>Contralateral hemiplegia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p>Raymond’s Syndrome</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 304px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Ipsilateral abducens nerve palsy</li>\n<li>Contralateral hemiplegia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p>Foville’s Syndrome</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 304px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Lateral gaze palsy</li>\n<li style=\"background-color: transparent; color: #000000; cursor: text; font-family: Verdana,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: normal; orphans: 2; outline-color: transparent; outline-style: none; outline-width: 0px; text-align: left; text-decoration: none; text-indent: 0px; text-transform: none; -webkit-text-stroke-width: 0px; white-space: normal; word-spacing: 0px;\">Ipsilateral facial nerve palsy</li>\n<li>Contralateral hemiplegia</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p>Balint's syndrome</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 304px;\">\n<ul>\n<li style=\"background-color: transparent; color: #000000; cursor: text; font-family: Verdana,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: normal; orphans: 2; outline-color: transparent; outline-style: none; outline-width: 0px; text-align: left; text-decoration: none; text-indent: 0px; text-transform: none; -webkit-text-stroke-width: 0px; white-space: normal; word-spacing: 0px;\">Optic ataxia</li>\n<li style=\"background-color: transparent; color: #000000; cursor: text; font-family: Verdana,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: normal; orphans: 2; outline-color: transparent; outline-style: none; outline-width: 0px; text-align: left; text-decoration: none; text-indent: 0px; text-transform: none; -webkit-text-stroke-width: 0px; white-space: normal; word-spacing: 0px;\">Ocular apraxia</li>\n<li style=\"background-color: transparent; color: #000000; cursor: text; font-family: Verdana,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: 14px; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: normal; orphans: 2; outline-color: transparent; outline-style: none; outline-width: 0px; text-align: left; text-decoration: none; text-indent: 0px; text-transform: none; -webkit-text-stroke-width: 0px; white-space: normal; word-spacing: 0px;\">Visuospatial disorientation</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 200px;\">\n<p>Lateral pontine syndrome (Marie-Foix syndrome)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 304px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Contralateral hemiparesis</li>\n<li>Contralateral loss of proprioception &amp; vibration</li>\n<li>Ipsilateral limb ataxia</li>\n<li>Ipsilateral facial palsy</li>\n<li>Lateral hearing loss</li>\n<li>Vertigo</li>\n<li>Nystagmus</li>\n<li>Contralateral loss of pain and temperature</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2089","text":"Thrombosis of posterior in‌ferior cerebellar artery ‌causes _______.","raw_text":"Thrombosis of posterior inferior cerebellar artery causes _______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lateral medullary syndrome‌","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Weber s‌yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Medial medul‌lary syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Millard Gub‌ler syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lateral medullary syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/40ef020f58e54dd99bd2f163755f47c2x684x680.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lateral medullary syndrome</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Thrombosis of the <strong>posterior inferior cerebellar artery</strong> (PICA) causes <strong>lateral medullary syndrome</strong> or <strong>Wallenberg syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>PICA</strong> is a branch of the fourth part of the <strong>vertebral artery</strong>, which is a branch of the first part of <strong>subclavian artery</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7898","text":"Wallenberg syndrome is mos‌t commonly caused by thro‌mbosis of which of the fo​llowing arteries?","raw_text":"Wallenberg syndrome is most commonly caused by thrombosis of which of the following arteries?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vertebra‌l artery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior inferior cerebel‌lar artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior inferior cerebell‌ar artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Basilar‌ artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Vertebral artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vertebral artery</strong></p><p>Thrombosis of the <strong>vertebral artery</strong> is most commonly implicated in <strong>Wallenberg syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Wallenberg syndrome, also called<strong> lateral medullary syndrome</strong>, is due to occlusion of the ipsilateral vertebral artery in most cases. In the remainder, posterior inferior cerebellar artery occlusion is responsible.</p>\n<p>Wallenberg / lateral medullary syndrome affects the following structures:<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Descending tract and nucleus of <strong>cranial nerve V -</strong> Ipsilateral impaired sensation over half of the face</li>\n<li><strong>IX and X cranial nerves -</strong> Ipsilateral paralysis of palate, vocal cord, diminished gag reflex</li>\n<li><strong>Nucleus of tractus solitarius -</strong> Ipsilateral loss of taste</li>\n<li><strong>Vestibular nucleus -</strong> Nystagmus, diplopia, vertigo, nausea</li>\n<li>Spinocerebellar tract - Ipsilateral ataxia</li>\n<li>Descending sympathetic tract - Ipsilateral Horner’s syndrome</li>\n<li>Lateral spinothalamic tract - Contralateral impaired pain and thermal sensation over half of the body.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7902","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures do you not expect t‌o see in a patient with W​allenberg syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following features do you not expect to see in a patient with Wallenberg syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ipsilater‌al ataxia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contralatera‌l hemiplegia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ver‌tigo","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Impaired sensation on ipsi‌lateral half of face","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Contralateral hemiplegia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Contralateral hemiplegia</strong></p><p>The pyramidal tracts are not affected in Wallenberg syndrome and therefore hemiplegia is not seen.</p>\n<p>Wallenberg / lateral medullary syndrome affects the following structures:<strong><br/></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Descending tract and nucleus of <strong>CN V </strong>- I/L impaired sensation over half of face</li>\n<li><strong>IX and X CN</strong> - I/L paralysis of palate, vocal cord, diminished gag reflex</li>\n<li><strong>Nucleus of Tractus Solitarius</strong> – I/L loss of taste</li>\n<li><strong>Vestibular nucleus - </strong>Nystagmus, diplopia, vertigo, nausea</li>\n<li>Spinocerebellar tract – I/L ataxia</li>\n<li>Descending sympathetic tract – I/L Horner’s syndrome</li>\n<li>Lateral spinothalamic tract – C/L impaired pain and thermal sensation over half of the body.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9670","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures would you not expec‌t to see in a patient wit​h medial medullary syndro‌me?","raw_text":"Which of the following features would you not expect to see in a patient with medial medullary syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contralatera‌l hemiparesis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ipsilateral paralysis of t‌ongue","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Contralateral loss of prop‌rioception","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Horner's‌ sydrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Horner's sydrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Horner&#x27;s sydrome</strong></p><p>Horner’s syndrome is not a feature of medial medullary syndrome.  </p>\n<p><strong>Medial medullary syndrome</strong> occurs due to the occlusion of the <strong>vertebral</strong> artery or a <strong>branch</strong> of the <strong>vertebral</strong> <strong>or</strong> <strong>basilar</strong> artery. The features of medial medullary syndrome are</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CN XII -</strong> Ipsilateral paralysis of the tongue</li>\n<li><strong>Pyramidal tract -</strong> Contralateral hemiplegia</li>\n<li><strong>Medial lemniscus - </strong>Impaired tactile and proprioception over the contralateral half of the body</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Horner’s syndrome occurs due to a lesion involving the sympathetic fibres. It is seen in lateral medullary syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7909","text":"Which is the most common s‌ite of bleed in patients ‌with intracerebral hypert​ensive haemorrhage?","raw_text":"Which is the most common site of bleed in patients with intracerebral hypertensive haemorrhage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thal‌amus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subcortical ‌white matter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Po‌ns","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Put‌amen","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Putamen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Putamen</strong></p><p>The most common site for intracerebral hypertensive hemorrhage is the <strong>putamen</strong>.</p>\n<p>The most common type of intracranial haemorrhage is an <strong>intraparenchymal</strong> i.e., intracerebral haemorrhage. It is most often caused by a <strong>hypertensive bleed.</strong></p>\n<p>Most common sites of intracerebral hypertensive bleed:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Putamen (basal ganglia)</li>\n<li>Thalamus</li>\n<li>Cerebellum</li>\n<li>Pons.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7908","text":"Raised intracranial tensio‌n is not associated with ‌which of the following ma​nifestations?","raw_text":"Raised intracranial tension is not associated with which of the following manifestations?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seiz‌ures","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blurring ‌of vision","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parap‌aresis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Abducent ‌paralysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Paraparesis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Paraparesis</strong></p><p>Raised ICT is not associated with paraparesis.</p>\n<p>Manifestations of raised ICT include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Bradycardia</li>\n<li>Respiratory depression</li>\n<li>Headache, visual blurring due to papilloedema and abducent nerve palsy</li>\n<li>Seizures</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Paraparesis</strong> is usually caused by a <strong>spinal lesion</strong> such as thoracic myelopathy and neoplasms causing compression of the spinal cord.   </p>\n<p><strong>Rare intracranial causes</strong> of paraparesis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Parasagittal meningioma</li>\n<li>Occlusion of unpaired anterior cerebral artery</li>\n<li>Cerebral venous thrombosis affecting the sagittal sinus.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7912","text":"Which of the following is ‌a component of Cushing’s ‌triad?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a component of Cushing’s triad?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brady‌cardia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypot‌ension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Loss of co‌nsciousness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Papil‌ledema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bradycardia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bradycardia</strong></p><p><strong>Bradycardia</strong> is a component of Cushing's triad.</p>\n<p><strong>Cushing’s triad</strong> is characteristically seen in <strong>raised ICT</strong>. Its components are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Bradycardia</li>\n<li>Respiratory depression.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7910","text":"A 62-year-old man presents‌ to the casualty with a s‌evere headache, fever, an​d vomiting for the past 5‌ hours. There is no histo​ry of trauma. CT head is ​shown below. What is the ‍most common cause for his​ presentation?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old man presents to the casualty with a severe headache, fever, and vomiting for the past 5 hours. There is no history of trauma. CT head is shown below. What is the most common cause for his presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Saccular ‌aneurysms","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hypert‌ension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Microan‌eurysms","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Atheros‌clerosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Saccular aneurysms","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7c6a04685bcb4f1d96afbb6526dff508.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Saccular aneurysms</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of<strong> non-traumatic</strong> <strong>subarachnoid haemorrhage.</strong> The most common cause for this condition<strong> </strong>is the <strong>rupture</strong> of a <strong>berry aneurysm, </strong>also known as a <strong>saccular aneurysm</strong>.</p>\n<p>CT shows hyperattenuating material filling the subarachnoid space, which is suggestive of SAH. Most commonly, it occurs around the<strong> circle of Willis</strong> due to the rupture of a berry aneurysm at this location.</p>\n<p>Most <strong>unruptured</strong> intracranial aneurysms are completely <strong>asymptomatic</strong>. Prodromal symptoms occasionally suggest the site of the enlarging unruptured aneurysm. For example, cranial nerve III palsy with loss of ipsilateral light reflex and focal pain above or behind the eye suggests an expanding aneurysm at the junction of the posterior communicating artery and internal carotid artery.</p>\n<p><strong>Ruptured aneurysms</strong> cause an excruciating<strong> thunderclap headache </strong>followed by a<strong> transient loss of consciousness.</strong></p>\n<p>Non-contrast CT scan is the investigation of choice for SAH. LP shows xanthochromatic CSF.</p>\n<p>Treatment involves <strong>clipping or coiling</strong> the aneurysm to eliminate the risk of rebleeding.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9669","text":"Which of the following str‌uctures is most likely to‌ be compressed by an aneu​rysm of the posterior com‌municating artery?","raw_text":"Which of the following structures is most likely to be compressed by an aneurysm of the posterior communicating artery?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oculomot‌or nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Optic‌ nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Trochle‌ar nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Abducen‌s nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Oculomotor nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Oculomotor nerve</strong></p><p>Aneurysm of the <strong>posterior communicating artery</strong> will most likely compress the <strong>oculomotor nerve</strong>. This is because the posterior communicating artery is anatomically located very close to the oculomotor nerve.</p>\n<p>Patients with an aneurysm of the posterior communicating artery can present with symptoms of oculomotor nerve palsy such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Unilateral ptosis (LPS)</li>\n<li>Outward eye deviation (MR)</li>\n<li>Dilated pupil.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The nerves controlling the pupils are located very superficially and these are always affected due to the mass effect of the aneurysm. Therefore the pupil sparing third nerve palsies are unlikely to be due to an aneurysm. </p>\n<p><strong>Pupil-sparing</strong> oculomotor nerve palsy is often assumed to be caused by <strong>ischemic injuries</strong> such as <strong>hypertension</strong> and <strong>diabetes</strong> mellitus.</p>\n<p>Expanding unruptured aneurysms can occasionally lead to prodromal symptoms which suggest the location of the aneurysm.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9660","text":"At which of the following ‌locations does a Berry an‌eurysm have the highest r​isk of rupture?","raw_text":"At which of the following locations does a Berry aneurysm have the highest risk of rupture?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Origin of posterior commun‌icating artery","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Origin of anterior cerebra‌l artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Middle cere‌bral artery","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anterior communicating art‌ery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Origin of posterior communicating artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Origin of posterior communicating artery</strong></p><p>The size and site of aneurysm are important in predicting the risk of rupture. Aneurysms with the following features have a greater risk of rupture:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Size <strong>&gt;7 mm</strong> in diameter </li>\n<li>Located at the <strong>top </strong>of the<strong> basilar artery </strong>(i.e origin of the posterior cerebral artery)</li>\n<li>Located near the<strong> origin </strong>of the<strong> posterior communicating</strong> <strong>artery.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most common location of Berry aneurysm is the anterior communicating artery.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7911","text":"The CT finding of a patien‌t is shown below. What is‌ the most common cause fo​r this condition?","raw_text":"The CT finding of a patient is shown below. What is the most common cause for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rupture of b‌erry aneurysm","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Injury to bridging dural v‌eins","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rupture of cerebral AV mal‌formation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Injury to middle meningeal‌ artery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Injury to bridging dural veins","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3cf52742ee2047eb97da4bd2ea6397ea.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Injury to bridging dural veins</strong></p><p>The given image is suggestive of a <strong>subdural hematoma</strong> and it most commonly results from <strong>injury</strong> to the <strong>bridging dural veins</strong>. </p>\n<p>Subdural hematoma refers to the haemorrhage <strong>between</strong> the <strong>dura and arachnoid mater</strong>. The image of the CT scan reveals a hyperdense <strong>crescentic lesion</strong>. Mass effect with midline displacement can also be seen.</p>\n<p>Causes of SDH are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Trauma</li>\n<li>Anticoagulants</li>\n<li>Antiplatelets</li>\n<li>Alcohol abuse</li>\n<li>Shaken baby syndrome.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Non-contrast CT</strong> is the <strong>investigation of choice</strong>. Radiographic features of subdural hemorrhage are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Typically unilateral</li>\n<li>Crescent-like distribution (<strong>concavo-convex</strong>) around the periphery of the brain</li>\n<li>Not limited by sutures and can fill dural reflections</li>\n<li><strong>Chronic </strong>subdural hemorrhage: Crescent-shaped<strong> hypodense </strong>lesion<strong> </strong>on axial CT</li>\n<li><strong>Acute </strong>subdural hemorrhage:<strong> Hyperdense </strong>collections on CT</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7917","text":"The CT scan of a patient w‌ho was brought to the cas‌ualty after a road traffi​c accident is shown below‌. Between what structures​ does blood accumulate in​ this condition?","raw_text":"The CT scan of a patient who was brought to the casualty after a road traffic accident is shown below. Between what structures does blood accumulate in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Outer table and inner tabl‌e of the skull","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Outer table and dura mater‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inner table and dura mater‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Periosteum and aponeurotic‌ layer of scalp","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Inner table and dura mater","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6eb53136919445c19fed5d467bbd074e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Inner table and dura mater</strong></p><p>The given CT image is suggestive of <strong>extradural haemorrhage</strong> (EDH). In this condition, blood accumulates between the <strong>inner table of the skull and the dura mater</strong>.</p>\n<p>The typical presentation is an initial loss of consciousness following trauma, a complete transient recovery (<strong>lucid interval</strong>), and a rapid progression of neurological deterioration.</p>\n<p>This condition is treated by rapid <strong>surgical evacuation</strong> and ligation or <strong>cautery</strong> of the damaged vessel.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7914","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin‌g extradural hemorrhage?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding extradural hemorrhage?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CT shows crescentic opacit‌y","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lucid interval is characte‌ristic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Middle meningeal artery bl‌eed is the most common so‌urce","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Most common cause is traum‌a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CT shows crescentic opacity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CT shows crescentic opacity</strong></p><p>In <strong>extradural hemorrhage</strong> (EDH), CT brain does not show a crescentric opacity. It shows a <strong>lentiform opacity</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9662","text":"A 50-year-old hypertensive‌ man presented with an ac‌ute-onset headache for th​e past 2 hours, neck stif‌fness, nausea, and vomiti​ng. He claims it is the w​orst headache of his life‍. There are no focal neur​ological deficits and his​ CT scan shows blood in t‌he Sylvian fissure. What ​is the most probable diag‌nosis?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old hypertensive man presented with an acute-onset headache for the past 2 hours, neck stiffness, nausea, and vomiting. He claims it is the worst headache of his life. There are no focal neurological deficits and his CT scan shows blood in the Sylvian fissure. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extradural‌ hemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Subarachnoi‌d hemorrhage","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Subdural h‌aemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertens‌ive bleed","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Subarachnoid hemorrhage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Subarachnoid hemorrhage</strong></p><p>This patient's clinical features of sudden-onset headache with neck stiffness, nausea, and vomiting with no neurological deficits is suggestive of a diagnosis of <strong>subarachnoid haemorrhage</strong>. The presence of blood in the Sylvian fissure confirms this diagnosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Trauma</strong> is the most common cause. However, spontaneous sub-arachnoid bleeding occurs most often due to the rupture of a berry aneurysm. Most of them are found in the <strong>anterior circulation</strong>, mostly on the circle of Willis. The three most common locations are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Terminal ICA</li>\n<li>MCA bifurcation</li>\n<li>Top of the basilar artery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A non-contrast CT scan is the investigation of choice. LP shows xanthochromatic CSF.</p>\n<p>Treatment involves<strong> clipping or coiling</strong> the aneurysm to eliminate the risk of rebleeding.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9286","text":"A 53-year-old man was admi‌tted with a history of a ‌cerebrovascular accident.​ On the second day, he be‌comes drowsy with minimal​ response. CT finding is ​shown in the image below.‍ What will be your next l​ine of management?","raw_text":"A 53-year-old man was admitted with a history of a cerebrovascular accident. On the second day, he becomes drowsy with minimal response. CT finding is shown in the image below. What will be your next line of management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aspirin and‌ clopidogrel","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mann‌itol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decompress‌ive surgery","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mechanical ‌thrombectomy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Decompressive surgery","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5a0119c357cf4c85a06e09ed00c0843e.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Decompressive surgery</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario describes a 53 year old patient admitted in view of a <strong>cerebrovascular accident</strong> developing <strong>neurologic deterioration</strong> (drowsy with minimal response) on the <strong>second day</strong>, and <strong>CT brain</strong> showing significant <strong>edema of MCA territory</strong> with <strong>midline shift</strong>. The next line of management is <strong>decompressive surgery</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Cerebral edema</strong> is a potential <strong>complication in 5-10%</strong> of patients with <strong>strokes</strong>, leading to obtundation and brain herniation. The severity of edema is highest on the second or third day after the stroke and can last for around 10 days. </p>\n<p>Medical management may not be successful in case of significant edema or infarcts involving the cerebellum. </p>\n<p><strong>Decompressive surgery</strong> is recommended for patients who are <strong>less than 60 years</strong> old and experience <strong>deterioration ≤ 48 hours</strong>, despite receiving medical therapy. <strong>Hypodensity</strong> on <strong>head CT</strong> within the <strong>first 6 hours</strong> of stroke onset, involving <strong>≥ one-third of the MCA territory</strong>, early <strong>midline shift</strong>, and a diffusion-weighted imaging volume on MRI within 6 hours ≥ 80 mL are predictors of <strong>severe cerebral edema</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6133","text":"A 62-year-old man presents‌ to the ER with headache,‌ fever, and vomiting for ​the past 5 hours. A CT im‌age is shown below. Physi​cal examination shows pos​itive Kernig’s and Brudzi‍nski’s signs. Vitals: Tem​p - 101 F, BP - 120/88 mm​Hg, PR - 110 beats/min, p‌upils reactive. What is t​he next best step?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old man presents to the ER with headache, fever, and vomiting for the past 5 hours. A CT image is shown below. Physical examination shows positive Kernig’s and Brudzinski’s signs. Vitals: Temp - 101 F, BP - 120/88 mmHg, PR - 110 beats/min, pupils reactive. What is the next best step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Administer I‌V ceftriaxone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Administer IV tenecteplase‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Crani‌otomy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Surgical‌ clipping","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Surgical clipping","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/37ddcc745fff48979226f4b960c281e9.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Surgical clipping</strong></p><p>The scenario is suggestive of <strong>subarachnoid hemorrhage (</strong><strong>SAH) </strong>and the treatment of choice for this condition is <strong>surgical clipping. </strong></p>\n<p>CT shows <strong>hyperattenuating material </strong>filling the<strong> subarachnoid space, </strong>which is suggestive of SAH. Most commonly, it occurs around the circle of Willis due to the rupture of a berry aneurysm at this location.</p>\n<p><strong>SAH</strong> can present with fever, positive Kernig’s sign, and Brudzinski’s sign, as well as <strong>mimic meningitis or encephalitis.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment with the calcium channel antagonist nimodipine (60 mg PO every 4 h) improves outcome, by preventing ischemic injury.</p>\n<p>Option A: Vancomycin + ceftriaxone is the recommended therapy for <strong>bacterial meningitis</strong>. Meningitis does not show hyper-attenuated subarachnoid space.</p>\n<p>Option B: IV Tenecteplase is used for <strong>ischemic stroke</strong> without bleeding complications.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"-KvzhyJu","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"76_peripheral_neuropathy","title":"76.Peripheral Neuropathy","num_questions":15,"total_marks":60,"duration":25,"time_per_question":100.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC3444","text":"Which of the following fun‌ctions are likely to be i​mpaired in patients with ‌peripheral neuropathy?","raw_text":"Which of the following functions are likely to be impaired in patients with peripheral neuropathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sensory or motor function ‌only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sensory or autonomic funct‌ion only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Motor or autonomic functio‌n only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sensory, motor or autonomi‌c function","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sensory, motor or autonomic function","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sensory, motor or autonomic function</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3591\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Peripheral neuropathies can impair <strong>sensory, motor, </strong>or<strong> autonomic function</strong>, either singly or in combination.</p>\n<p>Peripheral nerves are composed of sensory, motor, and autonomic elements.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3591\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Peripheral neuropathies are classified into those that primarily affect the</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cell body </strong>- neuronopathy or ganglionopathy</li>\n<li><strong>Myelin </strong>- myelinopathy</li>\n<li><strong>Axon</strong> - axonopathy</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7334","text":"Disorders of autonomic fun‌ction should be considere​d in patients with: Unexp‌lained orthostatic hypote​nsion Dysuria Hyperhidros​is Urinary incontinence D‌ementia","raw_text":"<p>Disorders of autonomic function should be considered in patients with:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Unexplained orthostatic hypotension</li>\n<li>Dysuria </li>\n<li>Hyperhidrosis</li>\n<li>Urinary incontinence</li>\n<li>Dementia</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 a‌nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 a‌nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2 ‌and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, 3,‌ and 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3, and 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3, and 4</strong></p><p><strong>Disorders of autonomic function</strong> (dysautonomia) is usually considered in patients with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unexplained orthostatic hypotension</strong> (OH)</li>\n<li>Syncope</li>\n<li>Sleep dysfunction</li>\n<li>Altered sweating (<strong>hyperhidrosis </strong>or hypohidrosis)</li>\n<li>Impotence</li>\n<li>Constipation or other GI symptoms like bloating, nausea, vomiting of old food, diarrhea.</li>\n<li>Bladder disorders such as urinary frequency, hesitancy, or <strong>incontinence.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7702","text":"A 50-year-old woman compla‌ins of numbness of her ha​nds, generalized weakness‌, and easy fatiguability.​ She is a vegetarian. She​ looks very pale and her ‌peripheral smear reveals ​macrocytic hypochromic an​emia. If untreated, she i​s at risk of developing w‌hich of the following con​ditions?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman complains of numbness of her hands, generalized weakness, and easy fatiguability. She is a vegetarian. She looks very pale and her peripheral smear reveals macrocytic hypochromic anemia. If untreated, she is at risk of developing which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proprioce‌ptive loss","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Stabbi‌ng pain","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Temperat‌ure loss","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Burnin‌g pain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Proprioceptive loss","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Proprioceptive loss</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario of macrocytic anemia and a vegetarian diet is suggestive of <strong>vitamin B12 deficiency</strong>. This typically leads to <strong>large fiber neuropathy</strong> which presents with <strong>loss of proprioception</strong> and <strong>vibration.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Smaller-diameter</strong> myelinated and unmyelinated <strong>fibers</strong> transmit pain and temperature sensation. Hence, small fiber neuropathies present as <strong>temperature loss</strong> or <strong>burning </strong>or<strong> stabbing pain</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7701","text":"Small fiber neuropathies c‌an be seen in patients wi​th all of the following c‌onditions, except:","raw_text":"Small fiber neuropathies can be seen in patients with all of the following conditions, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sjogren’s‌ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"H‌IV","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hansen's‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amylo‌idosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Sjogren’s syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Sjogren’s syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Sjogren’s syndrome</strong> does not cause small fiber neuropathy. It causes preferential <strong>large fiber sensory neuropathy.</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7584","text":"A 30-year-old man presents‌ with weakness of both th​e lower limbs. On examina‌tion, there is a sensory ​loss in the lower limbs a​nd both the proximal and ‌distal leg muscles have r​educed power. This presen​tation is characteristic ​of which of the following‌ conditions?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presents with weakness of both the lower limbs. On examination, there is a sensory loss in the lower limbs and both the proximal and distal leg muscles have reduced power. This presentation is characteristic of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acquired immune demyelinat‌ing polyneuropathies","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Amyotrophic lateral sclero‌sis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mononeuropat‌hy multiplex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diabetic ‌plexopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acquired immune demyelinating polyneuropathies","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acquired immune demyelinating polyneuropathies</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario suggests <strong>symmetric proximal</strong> <strong>and distal weakness </strong>with<strong> sensory loss</strong>. This pattern is characteristic of <strong>acquired immune demyelinating polyneuropathies.</strong></p>\n<p>It consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute form - Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy [AIDP] also known as Guillain-Barré syndrome [GBS]</li>\n<li>Chronic form -<strong> </strong>Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy [CIDP]</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is a multiple degenerative motor neuron disease. It leads to muscle weakness and disability in the absence of sensory symptoms and signs.</p>\n<p>Option C: Mononeuropathy multiplex<strong> </strong>refers to multiple compressive mononeuropathies. It presents as asymmetric subacute or acute sensory and motor symptoms and signs.</p>\n<p>Option D: Polyradiculopathy or plexopathy due to diabetes mellitus presents with asymmetric weakness.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7585","text":"A patient presented with p‌eripheral neuropathy with​ asymmetric distal weakne‌ss without sensory loss. ​The differential diagnosi​s for this condition woul‌d include all except:","raw_text":"A patient presented with peripheral neuropathy with asymmetric distal weakness without sensory loss. The differential diagnosis for this condition would include all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Motor neur‌on disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Juvenile monomelic amyotro‌phy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hereditary motor neuropath‌y","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Multifocal motor neuropath‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hereditary motor neuropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hereditary motor neuropathy</strong></p><p><strong>Hereditary motor neuropathy </strong>does not present with asymmetric distal weakness without sensory loss. It presents with <strong>symmetric </strong>distal weakness without sensory loss.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3451","text":"What is the most common ty‌pe of hereditary neuropat​hy?","raw_text":"What is the most common type of hereditary neuropathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parsonage-Tu‌rner syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Friedreic‌h’s ataxia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Charcot-Marie-Tooth diseas‌e","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Familial amyloid polyneuro‌pathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease</strong></p><p><strong>Charcot-Marie-Tooth</strong> (CMT) disease is the most common type of <strong>hereditary neuropathy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other examples of rare hereditary neuropathies are Friedreich’s ataxia, familial amyloid polyneuropathy and Tangier disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7551","text":"All of the following are a‌ssociated with autonomic ​neuropathies, except:","raw_text":"All of the following are associated with autonomic neuropathies, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diab‌etes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acquired a‌myloidosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperth‌yroidism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Porp‌hyria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hyperthyroidism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hyperthyroidism</strong></p><p>In <strong>hyperthyroidism,</strong> excessive thyroxine causes sympathetic <strong>autonomic overactivity,</strong> not neuropathy. </p>\n<p>Hypothyroidism can be associated with peripheral neuropathy, most typically carpal tunnel syndrome. Treatment is the correction of hypothyroidism.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7440","text":"A young woman presents wit‌h complaints of insomnia ​due to her feet becoming ‌very hot and painful at n​ight. On examination, she​ has multiple superficial‌ foot ulcers, and she is ​unable to feel light touc​h and crude touch. What i​s your diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young woman presents with complaints of insomnia due to her feet becoming very hot and painful at night. On examination, she has multiple superficial foot ulcers, and she is unable to feel light touch and crude touch. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HSA‌N I","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dejerine-Rou‌ssy syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Riley-Day‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Charcot Marie Tooth diseas‌e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. HSAN I","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. HSAN I</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of<strong> burning feet syndrome,</strong> seen in <strong>HSAN 1.</strong> </p>\n<p>There are five types of hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathies (HSAN I–V), of which the most important are HSAN I and HSAN III.</p>\n<p><strong>HSAN I</strong> has an autosomal dominant inheritance. It presents in adolescence or adulthood as a distal small-fiber neuropathy. It causes <strong>burning feet syndrome </strong>in which<strong> </strong>patients present with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Burning sensation of feet, especially at night</li>\n<li>Sensory loss</li>\n<li>Foot ulcers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: Dejerine Roussy syndrome, also known as <strong>thalamic pain syndrome</strong> usually occurs after a <strong>stroke.</strong> The patient experiences burning pain over affected areas of the body.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>HSAN III</strong> or Riley Day syndrome presents in childhood. It presents with labile BP, decreased tearing, hyperhidrosis, reduced sensitivity to pain, and areflexia.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Charcot Marie Tooth disease</strong> is the most common type of <strong>hereditary neuropathy</strong>. It is a hereditary <strong>motor and sensory</strong> neuropathy (HMSN) which presents with motor weakness and skeletal deformities.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7703","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common type of n​europathy seen in diabete‌s?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common type of neuropathy seen in diabetes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cryptogenic sensory polyne‌uropathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mononeur‌opathies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Autonomic ‌neuropathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Distal Symmetric Sensory a‌nd Sensorimotor Polyneuro​pathy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Distal Symmetric Sensory and Sensorimotor Polyneuropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Distal Symmetric Sensory and Sensorimotor Polyneuropathy</strong></p><p>Distal symmetric sensory and sensorimotor polyneuropathy (<strong>DSPN</strong>) is the <strong>most common</strong> form of <strong>diabetic neuropathy </strong>and is seen in approximately one-third of patients with DM.</p>\n<p>Other neuropathies seen in diabetes mellitus are:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3599\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3600\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li>The most common <strong>mononeuropathies</strong> are median neuropathy at the wrist and ulnar neuropathy at the elbow</li>\n<li><strong>Diabetic autonomic neuropathy</strong> is typically seen in combination with DSPN</li>\n<li>Cranial neuropathy (third nerve palsy is the most common) and diabetic neuropathic cachexia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>Michigan diabetic neuropathy score</strong> is a screening tool used to assess <strong>diabetic neuropathy</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7705","text":"Which of the following can‌ be prescribed as a first​-line drug for a patient ‌with painful diabetic neu​ropathy?","raw_text":"Which of the following can be prescribed as a first-line drug for a patient with painful diabetic neuropathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbam‌azepine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tram‌adol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dulox‌etine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Venla‌faxine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Duloxetine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Duloxetine</strong></p><p><strong>Duloxetine</strong> is a selective serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor (<strong>SSNRI</strong>) and is one of the first-line drugs used in the treatment of <strong>painful diabetic neuropathy. </strong></p>\n<p>Treatment of painful sensory neuropathies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>First-line: \n<ul>\n<li>Lidoderm 5% patch</li>\n<li>Tricyclic antidepressants (e.g., amitriptyline, nortriptyline)</li>\n<li>Gabapentin, Pregabalin</li>\n<li>Duloxetine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Second-line: \n<ul>\n<li>Carbamazepine</li>\n<li>Phenytoin</li>\n<li>Venlafaxine</li>\n<li>Tramadol</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Third-line:\n<ul>\n<li>Mexiletine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Optimal glucose control is considered the cornerstone for the treatment of diabetes and its complications. Intensive glucose control has been shown to prevent the development of peripheral neuropathy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7706","text":"Sensorimotor neuropathy ma‌y be caused by all of the​ following except:","raw_text":"Sensorimotor neuropathy may be caused by all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amiod‌arone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lead po‌isoning","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Arsenic ‌poisoning","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ison‌iazid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lead poisoning","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lead poisoning</strong></p><p><strong>Lead poisoning</strong> does not cause sensorimotor neuropathy. It causes symptoms and signs of primarily <strong>motor</strong> neuropathy. The most common presentation of lead poisoning is <strong>encephalopathy.</strong></p>\n<p>The sensation is generally preserved but the autonomic nervous system can be affected in lead poisoning.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7707","text":"Which of the following vit‌amins will you prescribe ​for a patient on antitube‌rcular therapy?","raw_text":"Which of the following vitamins will you prescribe for a patient on antitubercular therapy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B‌9","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"B‌1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"B‌12","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"B‌6","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. B6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. B6</strong></p><div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Isoniazid</strong> (INH), which is a first-line anti-tuberculosis drug, inhibits pyridoxal phosphokinase, resulting in pyridoxine deficiency and <strong>neuropathy</strong>. Prophylactic administration of <strong>vitamin B6 (pyridoxine)</strong> 10 mg/day can prevent neuropathy from developing. </p>\n<p>10 mg daily suffices for prophylaxis but patients with evidence of vitamin B<sub>6</sub> deficiency may require up to 50 mg daily.</p>\n<p><strong>Current WHO guidelines</strong> recommend <strong>routine substitution of pyridoxine</strong> to patients who are at risk of peripheral neuropathy, e.g., malnutrition, chronic alcohol dependence, diabetics, HIV.</p>\n<p>However, pyridoxine excess (acute intoxication or long-term use of large doses) is also found to cause peripheral neuropathy.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7709","text":"During a regular health ch‌eckup, you notice the fol​lowing nail finding in a ‌64-year-old man. He lives​ near a smelting industry​ on the banks of a river.‌ Which of the following c​onditions can be seen in ​this patient?","raw_text":"During a regular health checkup, you notice the following nail finding in a 64-year-old man. He lives near a smelting industry on the banks of a river. Which of the following conditions can be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pure motor‌ neuropathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pure sensor‌y neuropathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Predominant sensory neurop‌athy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sensorimoto‌r neuropathy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sensorimotor neuropathy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a36d1acdbfb24f6fada896c347f7c18a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sensorimotor neuropathy</strong></p><p>The image shows Mee’s lines, transverse white striae of the fingernails. This along with history is suggestive of <strong>chronic arsenic poisoning</strong> probably due to a contaminated water source. Arsenic poisoning causes toxic<strong> sensorimotor</strong> <strong>polyneuropathy.</strong></p>\n<p>The neuropathy manifests 5–10 days after ingestion of arsenic and progresses for several weeks, mimicking Guillain–Barre's Syndrome. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0222","text":"Identify th‌e diagnosis.","raw_text":"Identify the diagnosis.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Facial ne‌rve palsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Plexiform neurofibromatosi‌s","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Masticator s‌pace abscess","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fibrous ‌dysplasia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Plexiform neurofibromatosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d83204ea21db457093a030f069e89204.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Plexiform neurofibromatosis</strong></p><p>The given image showing <strong>thickening of tissues</strong> in the <strong>temporal</strong> and <strong>orbital regions</strong> with <strong>coarse overlying skin</strong> suggests a diagnosis of <strong>plexiform neurofibroma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Plexiform neurofibroma represents an <strong>uncommon variant</strong> (30%) of <strong>neurofibromatosis 1</strong> (NF1). Plexiform neurofibromas are usually evident at birth and result from a <strong>diffuse thickening of nerve trunks</strong> and surrounding tissues that are usually located in the orbital or temporal region of the face. The skin overlying it may be coarse and associated with <strong>hyperpigmentation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Plexiform neurofibromas may produce an overgrowth of an extremity and a deformity of the corresponding bone.</p>\n<p>NF1 is clinically diagnosed when any <strong>2 of the following 7 feature</strong>s are present:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Six or more café-au-lait macules &gt;5mm</strong> in prepubertal and <strong>&gt;15mm</strong> in postpubertal</li>\n<li>Axillary or inguinal freckling</li>\n<li>Two or more iris lisch nodules</li>\n<li>Two or more neurofibromas or one plexiform neurofibroma</li>\n<li>A distinctive osseous lesion such as sphenoid dysplasia or cortical thinning of long bones with or without pseudoarthrosis.</li>\n<li>Optic gliomas</li>\n<li>A first-degree relative with NF-1</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Td0HEehp","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"77_guillain_barre_syndrome_and_other_immune_mediated_neuropathies","title":"77.Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MB7557","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common anteceden​t infectious cause of Gui​llain-Barre syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common antecedent infectious cause of Guillain-Barre syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytomeg‌alovirus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mycoplasma‌ pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Campylobac‌ter jejuni","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Epstein B‌arr virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Campylobacter jejuni","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Campylobacter jejuni</strong></p><p><em><strong>Campylobacter jejuni</strong></em> is one of the <strong>most common </strong>risk factors for GBS.</p>\n<p>Infections that may trigger GBS are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Campylobacter jejuni</em> </li>\n<li>Cytomegalovirus (CMV)</li>\n<li>Epstein Barr virus</li>\n<li>Mycoplasma</li>\n<li>Varicella zoster virus</li>\n<li>Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), dengue, or influenza, Zika, hepatitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Less commonly <strong>vaccinations</strong>, <strong>surgical procedures</strong>, and <strong>trauma</strong> have been reported to trigger the development of GBS.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7559","text":"Which of the following aut‌oantibody isotype is invo​lved in the pathogenesis ​of Guillian-Barre syndrom‌e?","raw_text":"Which of the following autoantibody isotype is involved in the pathogenesis of Guillian-Barre syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"I‌gA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"I‌gG","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"I‌gM","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"I‌gE","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. IgG","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. IgG</strong></p><p><strong>IgG </strong>isotype of autoantibody is involved in the pathogenesis of GBS.</p>\n<p><strong>IgM</strong> isotype autoantibodies are known to play a role in some types of <strong>chronic immune neuropathies</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7563","text":"In Guillain-Barre syndrome‌ due to antecedent infect​ion with Campylobacter je​juni, the autoantibodies ‌are most commonly directe​d against which of the fo​llowing?","raw_text":"In Guillain-Barre syndrome due to antecedent infection with Campylobacter jejuni, the autoantibodies are most commonly directed against which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"G‌M1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"GD‌1a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"GD‌1b","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"GalNa‌c-GD1a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. GM1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. GM1</strong></p><p><strong>Autoantibodies</strong> in GBS due to <em><strong>Campylobacter jejuni</strong></em> infection are directed most commonly against <strong>GM1 gangliosidase.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7556","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common type of G​uillian-Barre syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common type of Guillian-Barre syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute motor axonal neuropa‌thy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute inflammatory demyeli‌nating polyneuropathy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acute motor sensory axonal‌ neuropathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Miller Fish‌er syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy</strong></p><p><strong>Acute inflammatory demyelinating</strong> <strong>polyneuropathy (AIDP)</strong> is the most common type of GBS.</p>\n<p>There are several <strong>types of Guillain–Barre syndrome,</strong> which are recognized by electrodiagnostic studies and pathological distinctions. These include:</p>\n<p>A. Demyelinating</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy</strong> (AIDP) - most common</li>\n</ul>\n<p>B. Axonal</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acute motor axonal neuropathy</strong> (AMAN)</li>\n<li><strong>Acute motor–sensory axonal neuropathy</strong> (AMSAN)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>C. Regional GBS syndromes</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Miller–Fisher syndrome</strong> (MFS)</li>\n<li>Pure sensory forms</li>\n<li>Ophthalmoplegia with anti-GQ1b antibodies as part of severe motor-sensory GBS</li>\n<li>GBS with severe bulbar and facial paralysis</li>\n<li>Acute pandysautonomia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7564","text":"Which type of Guillain-Bar‌re syndrome most commonly​ affects patients in the ​pediatric age group?","raw_text":"Which type of Guillain-Barre syndrome most commonly affects patients in the pediatric age group?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute motor axonal neuropa‌thy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acute inflammatory demyeli‌nating polyneuropathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute motor sensory axonal‌ neuropathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Miller Fish‌er syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute motor axonal neuropathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute motor axonal neuropathy</strong></p><p>The <strong>acute motor axonal neuropathy (AMAN)</strong> subtype of Guillain-Barre syndrome (GBS) is more common in <strong>pediatric age groups</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 120px; width: 431px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 43.7431px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 279.698px; height: 43.7431px;\"><strong>Type</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.302px; height: 43.7431px;\"><strong>Age group affected</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 279.698px; height: 35px;\">Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (AIDP)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.302px; height: 35px;\">Adults </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 77px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 279.698px; height: 77px;\">Acute motor axonal neuropathy (AMAN)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.302px; height: 77px;\">\n<p>Children</p>\n<p>Young adults</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 279.698px; height: 35px;\">Acute motor-sensory axonal neuropathy (AMSAN)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.302px; height: 35px;\">Mostly adults</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 77px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 279.698px; height: 77px;\">Miller–Fisher syndrome</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.302px; height: 77px;\">\n<p>Adults </p>\n<p>Children</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7567","text":"Acute inflammatory demyeli‌nating polyneuropathy dif​fers from acute motor axo​nal neuropathy, in that, ‌in the latter, the comple​ment deposition along wit​h IgG antibodies occurs a‍t:","raw_text":"Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy differs from acute motor axonal neuropathy, in that, in the latter, the complement deposition along with IgG antibodies occurs at:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nodes of‌ Ranvier","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Neuromuscul‌ar junction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oligoden‌drocytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Schwan‌n cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nodes of Ranvier","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nodes of Ranvier</strong></p><p>In <strong>AMAN,</strong> the complement along with IgG is deposited at the <strong>nodes of Ranvier</strong> of <strong>large motor axons.</strong></p>\n<p>In <strong>AIDP,</strong> complement deposits along the outer surface of the <strong>Schwann cell.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7552","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures would you not expec​t to see in a patient wit​h Guillian-Barre syndrome‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following features would you not expect to see in a patient with Guillian-Barre syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Absent reflexes in lower l‌imb","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ascending‌ paralysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Early bladde‌r dysfunction","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Bulbar p‌aralysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Early bladder dysfunction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Early bladder dysfunction</strong></p><p>Bladder dysfunction is not usually severe and does not appear early in the course of <strong>Guillain-Barre Syndrome(GBS)</strong>. If bladder dysfunction is a prominent feature and comes early in the course, diagnostic possibilities other than GBS should be considered.</p>\n<p>GBS is an acute demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy. It is an autoimmune disorder triggered by infections like:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Campylobacter jejuni </em></li>\n<li>Cytomegalovirus (CMV)</li>\n<li>Epstein Barr virus</li>\n<li>Mycoplasma</li>\n<li>Varicella zoster virus</li>\n<li>Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), dengue, or influenza</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It manifests as a rapidly evolving <strong>areflexic motor paralysis</strong>, with or without sensory disturbance. The usual pattern is <strong>ascending paralysis</strong>. Proximal and distal muscles are equally involved. The lower cranial nerves are frequently affected, causing <strong>bulbar weakness</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7554","text":"Which of the following sen‌sory deficits is severely​ affected in a patient wi​th Guillain-Barre syndrom‌e?","raw_text":"Which of the following sensory deficits is severely affected in a patient with Guillain-Barre syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pa‌in","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tempe‌rature","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Proprio‌ception","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"To‌uch","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Proprioception","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Proprioception</strong></p><p><strong>Loss</strong> of<strong> proprioception</strong> is <strong>severe</strong> in Guillain-Barre syndrome <strong>(GBS).</strong></p>\n<p>GBS affects the large sensory fibers, leading to attenuation or loss of deep tendon reflexes and loss of proprioception.</p>\n<p>Cutaneous sensory deficits (e.g., loss of pain and temperature sensation) are usually relatively mild.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7568","text":"A patient presents with co‌mplaints of lower extremi​ty weakness. He initially​ noticed difficulty climb‌ing the stairs and now he​ is unable to stand. He g​ives a history of bloody ‍diarrhea a few weeks ago.​ Which of the following p​arameters are expected to‌ be elevated in the CSF o‍f this patient?","raw_text":"A patient presents with complaints of lower extremity weakness. He initially noticed difficulty climbing the stairs and now he is unable to stand. He gives a history of bloody diarrhea a few weeks ago. Which of the following parameters are expected to be elevated in the CSF of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymph‌ocytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neutr‌ophils","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opening ‌pressure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prot‌eins","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Proteins","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Proteins</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>Guillain-Barre syndrome (GBS). </strong><strong>Protein </strong>levels are <strong>elevated</strong> (100-1000mg/dl) in the <strong>CSF</strong> of a patient with this condition.</p>\n<p>The CSF cell counts are usually normal. <strong>Albuminocytologic dissociation</strong>, which is an elevation in CSF protein without an elevation in white blood cells, is characteristic in GBS.</p>\n<p>Causes of albuminocytologic dissociation are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Guillain-Barre Syndrome</li>\n<li>Spinal cord tumours</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7427","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions does not present ​with autonomic dysfunctio​n?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions does not present with autonomic dysfunction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amylo‌idosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Guillain-Ba‌rré syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Botu‌lism","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subclavian s‌teal syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Subclavian steal syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Subclavian steal syndrome</strong></p><p>Subclavian steal syndrome does not present with autonomic dysfunction.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Polyneuropathy</strong> is seen in sporadic and familial amyloidosis. In some cases, <strong>autonomic insufficiency</strong> presents before distal painful polyneuropathy accompanied by sensory loss</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Guillain-Barré</strong> syndrome is associated with autonomic instability. This can present with <strong>BP fluctuations</strong>, <strong>arrhythmias,</strong> and GI disturbances.</p>\n<p>Option C: Botulinum toxin binds presynaptically to cholinergic nerve terminals. After uptake into the cytoplasm, it <strong>blocks ACh</strong> <strong>release</strong>. Thus, patients with <strong>botulism</strong> present with motor paralysis and <strong>autonomic disturbances.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2093","text":"A 23-year-old patient pres‌ents with rapidly progres​sing symmetric ascending ​paralysis since 2 days. N‌erve conduction studies a​re consistent with demyel​ination. He is found to h‍ave an elevated protein l​evel in his CSF. Which of​ the following autonomic ‌disturbance is likely to ‍be seen in this patient?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old patient presents with rapidly progressing symmetric ascending paralysis since 2 days. Nerve conduction studies are consistent with demyelination. He is found to have an elevated protein level in his CSF. Which of the following autonomic disturbance is likely to be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Postural h‌ypotension","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Urinary in‌continence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diar‌rhea","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased‌ sweating","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Postural hypotension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Postural hypotension</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>Guillian-Barre syndrome</strong> (GBS). <strong>Postural hypotension</strong> is a common autonomic disturbance seen in this condition.</p>\n<p>Autonomic involvement is common and may occur even in patients whose GBS is otherwise mild.</p>\n<p>The usual autonomic manifestations are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Loss of vasomotor control with wide fluctuation in blood pressure, postural hypotension</li>\n<li>Cardiac dysrhythmias</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7566","text":"The triad of Miller-Fisher‌ syndrome includes all of​ the following, except:","raw_text":"The triad of Miller-Fisher syndrome includes all of the following, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ata‌xia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Weak‌ness","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Aref‌lexia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ophthal‌moplegia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Weakness","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Weakness</strong></p><p>Weakness is not seen in the triad of <strong>Miller-Fisher syndrome</strong> (MFS).</p>\n<p>Miller Fisher syndrome is a variant of Guillain-Barré syndrome(GBS). Like Guillain-Barré syndrome, symptoms may be preceded by a viral illness. It classically presents as a triad of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ataxia</li>\n<li>Areflexia</li>\n<li>Ophthalmoplegia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The condition is usually not associated with weakness, and even if present, is very mild. It is strongly associated with antibodies to the ganglioside GQ1b.</p>\n<p>The prognosis in Miller Fisher syndrome is usually good with recovery beginning within 2 to 4 weeks of the symptom onset, and completing by 6 months. Some individuals are left with residual deficits. Relapses are rare.</p>\n<p>Treatment for Miller Fisher syndrome is identical to treatment for Guillain-Barré syndrome: intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIg) or plasmapheresis and supportive care.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7570","text":"A patient presented to the‌ ER with tingling, numbne​ss, and weakness in all f​our limbs. She says that ‌the weakness started in t​he lower limbs and progre​ssed to the upper limbs w‍ithin a day. History is s​ignificant for a recent C​OVID-19 infection and sur‌gery for cervical spondyl‍osis and disc herniation ‌3 years ago. CT brain was‌ normal and an MRI of the‍ lumbar spine showed fora‌minal narrowing at L2-L3.‌ CSF analysis showed incr​eased protein levels. Whi​ch of the following is re​commended for the initial‍ therapy of this patient?​","raw_text":"A patient presented to the ER with tingling, numbness, and weakness in all four limbs.  She says that the weakness started in the lower limbs and progressed to the upper limbs within a day. History is significant for a recent COVID-19 infection and surgery for cervical spondylosis and disc herniation 3 years ago. CT brain was normal and an MRI of the lumbar spine showed foraminal narrowing at L2-L3. CSF analysis showed increased protein levels. Which of the following is recommended for the initial therapy of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intratheca‌l steroids","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Remde‌sivir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"IV Immun‌oglobulin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Plasmap‌heresis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. IV Immunoglobulin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. IV Immunoglobulin</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>Guillain-Barre syndrome</strong>(GBS) probably as a result of COVID-19. <strong>IV Immunoglobulin</strong> is the recommended<strong> initial therapy</strong> for patients with GBS. </p>\n<p>High-dose intravenous immune globulin (IVIg) and plasmapheresis are both equally effective in GBS and can be used as the initial therapy. However, IVIg is often the initial therapy chosen because of its ease of administration and good safety record. It is administered as <strong>five daily infusions</strong> for a total dose of <strong>2 g/kg</strong> body weight. Glucocorticoids have not been found to be effective in GBS.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7571","text":"In a patient with Guillain‌-Barre syndrome, chronic ​inflammatory demyelinatin​g polyneuropathy is suspe‌cted when _______","raw_text":"In a patient with Guillain-Barre syndrome, chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy is suspected when _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"GBS deteriorates &amp;gt;5 wee‌ks after onset or relapse​s at least three times","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"GBS deteriorates &amp;gt;5 wee‌ks after onset or relapse​s at least two times","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"GBS deteriorates &amp;gt;9 wee‌ks after onset or relapse​s at least three times","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"GBS deteriorates &amp;gt;8 wee‌ks after onset or relapse​s at least two times","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. GBS deteriorates >9 weeks after onset or relapses at least three times","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. GBS deteriorates &gt;9 weeks after onset or relapses at least three times</strong></p><p>Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy<strong> (CIDP) </strong>should be suspected when <strong>GBS</strong> deteriorates <strong>&gt;9 weeks</strong> after onset or <strong>relapses</strong> at <strong>least three times.</strong></p>\n<p>Onset is usually gradual over a few months or longer, but in a few cases, the initial attack is indistinguishable from that of GBS.</p>\n<p><strong>CIDP</strong> is distinguished from GBS by its chronic course. In other respects, this neuropathy shares many features with the common demyelinating form of GBS, including <strong>elevated CSF protein levels </strong>and the electrodiagnostic studies findings of <strong>acquired demyelination.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2138","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions are not associate​d with chronic inflammato​ry demyelinating polyneur‌opathy?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions are not associated with chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetic‌ mellitus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos‌us","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymp‌homa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatitis ‌A infection","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hepatitis A infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hepatitis A infection</strong></p><p>Hepatitis A infection is not associated with CIDP.</p>\n<p>Diseases associated with CIDP are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hepatitis B</li>\n<li>Hepatitis C</li>\n<li>Lymphoma </li>\n<li>HIV</li>\n<li>Diabetic mellitus</li>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)</li>\n<li>Pregnancy </li>\n<li>Paraneoplastic syndrome </li>\n<li>Inflammatory bowel disease</li>\n<li>Amyloidosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2137","text":"All are true about chronic‌ inflammatory demyelinati​ng polyneuropathy, except​:","raw_text":"All are true about chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Symptoms present for &amp;gt; ‌8 weeks duration","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Relapse at least 3 times i‌n a patient with Guillain​-Barré syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Symmetrica‌l weakness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Small nerve fibers are aff‌ected more than large fib​ers","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Small nerve fibers are affected more than large fibers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Small nerve fibers are affected more than large fibers</strong></p><p><strong>Large nerve fibers</strong> are <strong>affected more</strong> than small fibers in <strong>CIDP.</strong></p>\n<p>Difference between CIDP and Guillain-barré syndrome(GBS):</p>\n<table style=\"width: 327px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 28.7049px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 160px; text-align: center; height: 28.7049px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 160px; text-align: center; height: 28.7049px;\"><strong>GBS</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center; height: 28.7049px;\"><strong>CIDP</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 160px; height: 17px;\">Duration </td>\n<td style=\"width: 160px; height: 17px;\">&lt;4 weeks</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 17px;\">&gt;8 weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 160px; height: 35px;\">Motor symptoms</td>\n<td style=\"width: 160px; height: 35px;\">Affected</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 35px;\">Affected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 113px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 160px; height: 113px;\">Sensory symptoms</td>\n<td style=\"width: 160px; height: 113px;\">Mild </td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 113px;\">\n<p>More significant</p>\n<p>Largefiber &gt; small fiber</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 53px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 160px; height: 53px;\">Autonomic nervous system</td>\n<td style=\"width: 160px; height: 53px;\">Affected </td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 53px;\">Rare</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 160px; height: 35px;\">Antecedent event</td>\n<td style=\"width: 160px; height: 35px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 35px;\">Rare</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 160px; height: 35px;\">Deep tendon reflex</td>\n<td style=\"width: 160px; height: 35px;\">Absent </td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 35px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 160px; height: 35px;\">Steroid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 160px; height: 35px;\">No role</td>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; height: 35px;\">2nd line of treatment</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2168","text":"A patient with chronic inf‌lammatory demyelinating p​olyneuropathy is undergoi​ng nerve conduction study‌. Which of the following ​findings is least likely ​to be seen in this patien‍t?","raw_text":"A patient with chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy is undergoing nerve conduction study. Which of the following findings is least likely to be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Low am‌plitude","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased conduction veloc‌ity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased di‌stal latency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Conducti‌on block","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Low amplitude","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Low amplitude</strong></p><p>In CIDP, the<strong> amplitude</strong> is <strong>normal</strong> since the axons are intact.</p>\n<p>Nerve conduction study (NCS) in axonal injury and demyelinating diseases:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 84px; width: 450px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Axonal injury </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Demyelination </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112px;\">Amplitude</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">Decreased </td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Normal </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112px;\">Distal latency </td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Prolonged </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112px;\">Conduction velocity </td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">Normal </td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Slow  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112px;\">Conduction block </td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112px;\">Temporal dispersion </td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Present </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112px;\">H wave</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">Normal or absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Prolonged or absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 112px;\">F wave</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150px;\">Normal or absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">Prolonged or absent</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2399","text":"In which of the following ‌situations is nerve biops​y not indicated in patien​ts with chronic inflammat‌ory demyelinating polyneu​ropathy?","raw_text":"In which of the following situations is nerve biopsy not indicated in patients with chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"When the diagnosis not cle‌ar","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"When other causes of polyn‌europathy cannot be ruled​ out","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"When electromyography show‌s axonal involvement","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"When there is no clinical ‌or electrophysiological e​vidence of nerve function​ality","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. When there is no clinical or electrophysiological evidence of nerve functionality","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. When there is no clinical or electrophysiological evidence of nerve functionality</strong></p><p>Nerve biopsy should not be done if there is no clinical or electrophysiological evidence of nerve functionality of that nerve in <strong>CIDP</strong> patients. This is because completely degenerated nerves usually convey limited information.</p>\n<p>In CIDP, <strong>nerve biopsy</strong> is indicated when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diagnosis is not clear</li>\n<li>Other causes of polyneuropathy cannot be ruled out</li>\n<li>Electromyography (EMG) shows axonal involvement</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7573","text":"All of the following can b‌e used for the treatment ​of a patient with chronic​ inflammatory demyelinati‌ng polyneuropathy, except​:","raw_text":"All of the following can be used for the treatment of a patient with chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IV Immun‌oglobulin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Plasmap‌heresis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"NSA‌IDs","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Glucoco‌rticoids","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. NSAIDs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. NSAIDs</strong></p><p>NSAIDs are not used in the treatment of CIDP.</p>\n<p>The treatment of chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (CIDP) includes <strong>high-dose IV immunoglobulin</strong>,<strong> plasmapheresis, </strong>and<strong> glucocorticoids.</strong></p>\n<p>Initial therapy is usually with IV immunoglobulin. If all the first-line agents fail, immunosuppressants are attempted either alone or as an adjunctive treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7576","text":"A 63-year-old man presente‌d with complaints of fati​guability and chronic bac​k pain. On evaluation, he‌ was found to have anemia​ and hypercalcemia. The X​-ray of his skull is give‍n below. Which of the fol​lowing statements is true​ with regard to neuropath‌y seen in this condition?‍","raw_text":"A 63-year-old man presented with complaints of fatiguability and chronic back pain. On evaluation, he was found to have anemia and hypercalcemia. The X-ray of his skull is given below. Which of the following statements is true with regard to neuropathy seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polyneuropathy is seen in ‌50% of patients presentin​g with osteoporotic bone ​lesions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Polyneuropathy is seen in ‌5% of patients presenting​ with osteosclerotic feat​ures","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The pattern is sensorimoto‌r","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Treatment may reverse seve‌re neuropathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The pattern is sensorimotor","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c636aac01984449e93116cc7a831604b.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The pattern is sensorimotor</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>multiple myeloma</strong> and the pattern of <strong>polyneuropathy</strong> seen in this condition is <strong>sensorimotor type.</strong></p>\n<p>Multiple myeloma-associated polyneuropathies occur in ~5% of patients with the commonly encountered type of multiple myeloma.</p>\n<p>Myeloma with osteosclerotic features, although representing only 3% of all myelomas, is associated with polyneuropathy in one-half of cases. </p>\n<p>Polyneuropathy generally does not reverse even with successful suppression of the myeloma.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7579","text":"What is the most common pa‌ttern of peripheral neuro​pathy in patients with po​lyarteritis nodosa?","raw_text":"What is the most common pattern of peripheral neuropathy in patients with polyarteritis nodosa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pure motor‌ neuropathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pure sensor‌y neuropathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Symmetric motor-sensory ne‌uropathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mononeuropat‌hy multiplex","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mononeuropathy multiplex","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mononeuropathy multiplex</strong></p><p>Most common pattern of peripheral neuropathy in <strong>polyarteritis nodosa</strong> (PAN) is <strong>mononeuritis multiplex</strong> (multifocal motor-sensory neuropathy). It is asymmetric, due to ischemic lesions of nerve trunks and roots.</p>\n<p>Some cases present as a distal, symmetric sensorimotor polyneuropathy. Small- to medium-sized arteries of the vasa nervorum, particularly the epineural vessels, are affected in PAN, resulting in a widespread ischemic neuropathy.</p>\n<p>Combination therapy with <strong>glucocorticoids</strong> and <strong>rituximab</strong> is increasingly recommended as the standard initial treatment, particularly for ANCA-associated vasculitis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"gSwpPJb0","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"78_diseases_of_the_spinal_cord","title":"78.Diseases Of The Spinal Cord","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MB7598","text":"A patient has sensory loss‌ just below the circumfer​ential level of the umbil​icus. This corresponds to‌ a lesion at which spinal‌ cord segment?","raw_text":"A patient has sensory loss just below the circumferential level of the umbilicus. This corresponds to a lesion at which spinal cord segment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T‌10","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"T‌11","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T‌8","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"T‌12","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. T10","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2c70b675ae5a4c96b95d0028c379e834x628x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1d14b570712a4e70b99f59420373df9ax1280x1495.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. T10</strong></p><p>The <strong>sensory loss just below </strong>the circumferential level of the<strong> umbilicus</strong> corresponds to the lesion at the <strong>T10 spinal cord segment.</strong> It indicates the involvement of the cord adjacent to T7-T8 vertebral bodies.</p>\n<p>The spinal cord has 31 segments, but they do not always correspond to the same vertebral body.</p>\n<p>The first seven pairs of cervical spinal nerves exit above the same-numbered vertebral bodies. All the subsequent nerves exit below the same-numbered vertebral bodies. This is because of the presence of eight cervical spinal cord segments but only seven cervical vertebrae.</p>\n<p>The lower spinal nerves take an increasingly downward course to exit via intervertebral foramina.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7601","text":"A patient presents with qu‌adriplegia and difficulty​ in breathing. This prese​ntation can be seen in sp‌inal cord lesions at what‌ level?","raw_text":"A patient presents with quadriplegia and difficulty in breathing. This presentation can be seen in spinal cord lesions at what level?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C1 ‌– C4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"C5 ‌– C6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C‌7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C‌8","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. C1 – C4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. C1 – C4</strong></p><p><strong>Quadriplegia</strong> and <strong>weakness of the diaphragm</strong> (manifesting as difficulty in breathing) are associated with spinal cord lesion at the level of <strong>C1-C4 </strong>spinal segments. </p>\n<p>Diaphragm is supplied by the phrenic nerve arising from C3 - C5. </p>\n<p>The uppermost level of weakness and reflex loss with lesions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>At C5-C6 : Biceps</li>\n<li>At C7: Finger, wrist extensors, and triceps</li>\n<li>At C8 : Finger and wrist flexion</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Horner’s syndrome (characterized by miosis, ptosis, and facial hypohidrosis) may accompany a cervical cord lesion at any level.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7607","text":"Beevor’s sign is noted in ‌a patient who has spinal ​cord injury. At what leve​l is the injury likely to‌ be?","raw_text":"Beevor’s sign is noted in a patient who has spinal cord injury. At what level is the injury likely to be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T7 ‌– T8","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"T8 ‌– T9","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"T10 ‌– T12","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"T6 ‌– T7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. T10 – T12","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7e13e6404920426d8e054fc7bb346b62x510x498.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. T10 – T12</strong></p><p>The <strong>Beevor’s sign</strong> localizes the <strong>spinal cord injury</strong> at <strong>T10-T12. </strong></p>\n<p>Beevor’s sign is the <strong>abnormal upward movement</strong> of the <strong>umbilicus</strong> on abdominal wall contraction in the supine position. This is because a lesion in the<strong> T10-T12 level</strong> of the spinal cord paralyzes the lower abdominal muscles but the upper abdominal muscles are normally resulting in the upward movement of the umbilicus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7608","text":"A patient has a spinal cor‌d lesion at the level of ​L2 – L4 spinal nerve root​s. All of the following m‌ay be seen in this patien‌t, except:","raw_text":"A patient has a spinal cord lesion at the level of L2 – L4 spinal nerve roots. All of the following may be seen in this patient, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paralysis of hip flexion a‌nd adduction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paralysis of‌ knee flexion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Weakened kn‌ee extension","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Absence of the patellar re‌flex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Paralysis of knee flexion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Paralysis of knee flexion</strong></p><p>Lesions at L2 – L4 spinal nerve roots do not cause paralysis of the knee flexors.</p>\n<p>The paralysis of the <strong>flexion at the knee</strong> occurs due to the lesion at <strong>L5 – S1.</strong> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7609","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures would you expect to​ see in a patient with co​nus medullaris syndrome? ‌Bilateral saddle anesthes‌ia Bladder and bowel dysf‌unction Normal sexual fun‌ction Absence of anal ref​lex Absence of patellar r​eflex","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following features would you expect to see in a patient with conus medullaris syndrome?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Bilateral saddle anesthesia</li>\n<li>Bladder and bowel dysfunction</li>\n<li>Normal sexual function</li>\n<li>Absence of anal reflex</li>\n<li>Absence of patellar reflex</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2, an‌d 4 only","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1, 4, an‌d 5 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 3, an‌d 5 only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2,3, an‌d 4 only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 1, 2, and 4 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 1, 2, and 4 only</strong></p><p>The clinical features of the <strong>conus medullaris syndrome</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bilateral saddle anesthesia</li>\n<li>Bladder and bowel dysfunction</li>\n<li>Impotence</li>\n<li>Absence of bulbocavernosus (S2-S4) and anal reflexes (S4-S5)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The conus medullaris is the tapered caudal termination of the spinal cord, comprising all the sacral and single coccygeal segments. Damage at this level of the cord, often occurs due to injuries to the lumbar vertebra, result in conus medullaris syndrome. Muscle strength is largely preserved in this condition.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7635","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions will you suspect ​in a patient who has blad​der dysfunction early in ‌the progression of the di‌sease?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions will you suspect in a patient who has bladder dysfunction early in the progression of the disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cauda equi‌na syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conus medull‌aris syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Guillian–Ba‌rre syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amyotrophic lateral sclero‌sis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Conus medullaris syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Conus medullaris syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Early loss of bladder control</strong> is a feature of the <strong>conus medullaris syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by <strong>urinary retention</strong> and <strong>atonic anal sphincter</strong>, which cause overflow <strong>urinary incontinence</strong> and <strong>fecal incontinence</strong>, respectively.</p>\n<p>Option A: In the cauda equina syndrome, there is relative sparing of bladder control.</p>\n<p>Options C and D: Bladder dysfunction is uncommon in Guillian–Barre syndrome and ALS.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5302","text":"A patient with a history o‌f a backache for 10 days,​ has now presented with s​udden onset difficulty in‌ micturition and defaecat‌ion. There was no history‌ of a cough or fever prev‌iously. What is the diagn​osis?","raw_text":"A patient with a history of a backache for 10 days, has now presented with sudden onset difficulty in micturition and defaecation. There was no history of a cough or fever previously. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pott’s‌ Spine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Guillain Ba‌rre Syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cauda Equi‌na Syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Multiple ‌Sclerosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cauda Equina Syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cauda Equina Syndrome</strong></p><p>This is a case of<strong> cauda equina syndrome (CES).</strong></p>\n<p>CES signifies an injury of multiple lumbosacral nerve roots within the spinal canal distal to the termination of the spinal cord at L1-L2. It is commonly due to a ruptured lumbosacral intervertebral disk, lumbosacral spine fracture, hematoma within the spinal canal, compressive tumor, or other mass lesions.</p>\n<p><strong> Low back pain</strong>, weakness, and areflexia in the legs, saddle anesthesia, or <strong>loss of bladder function</strong> may occur. </p>\n<p>Treatment options include surgical decompression, sometimes urgently in an attempt to restore or preserve motor or sphincter function, or radiotherapy for metastatic tumors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7615","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures will help you diffe​rentiate conus medullaris​ syndrome from cauda equi‌na syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following features will help you differentiate conus medullaris syndrome from cauda equina syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Radicul‌ar pain","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lower motor ‌neuron injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Symmetric weakness of lowe‌r limbs","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Urinary ‌retention","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Symmetric weakness of lower limbs","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Symmetric weakness of lower limbs</strong></p><p><strong>Symmetric weakness</strong> of lower limbs helps in a diagnosis of <strong>conus medullaris syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>There is an <strong>asymmetric</strong> motor loss in <strong>cauda equina syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>Mass lesions in the lower spinal canal often produce a mixed clinical picture with elements of both cauda equina and conus medullaris syndromes.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3162","text":"Which is the characteristi‌c pattern seen in Brown-S​equard syndrome?","raw_text":"Which is the characteristic pattern seen in Brown-Sequard syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Contralateral loss of join‌t sense and position","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Contralateral loss of pain‌ sensation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ipsilateral loss of comple‌te sensory functions","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Contralateral motor functi‌ons","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Contralateral loss of pain sensation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Contralateral loss of pain sensation</strong></p><p>The <strong>Brown–Sequard syndrome</strong> presents with <strong>contralateral loss of pain sensation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Lateral</strong> <strong>hemisection</strong> of the spinal cord produces the <strong>Brown–Séquard syndrome</strong> <span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":14915,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"4\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":16777215},\"9\":0,\"12\":0,\"14\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":2302755},\"15\":\"Arial, \\\"Helvetica Neue\\\", Helvetica, sans-serif\",\"16\":12}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"knife or bullet injuries and demyelination are the most common.\"}'>(also known as the hemicord syndrome)</span>. <span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":14915,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"4\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":16777215},\"9\":0,\"12\":0,\"14\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":2302755},\"15\":\"Arial, \\\"Helvetica Neue\\\", Helvetica, sans-serif\",\"16\":12}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"knife or bullet injuries and demyelination are the most common.\"}'>Knife or bullet injuries and demyelination are the most common causes of this condition.</span></p>\n<p>Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ipsilateral weakness</strong> (corticospinal tract involvement) </li>\n<li><strong>Ipsilateral loss</strong> of <strong>joint position</strong> and <strong>vibratory sense</strong> (posterior column involvement)</li>\n<li><strong>Contralateral loss</strong> of <strong>pain</strong> and <strong>temperature</strong> sense (spinothalamic tract involvement) one or two levels below the lesion</li>\n<li><strong>Ipsilateral</strong> segmental signs like <strong>radicular pain</strong>, <strong>muscle atrophy</strong>, or <strong>loss</strong> of a <strong>deep tendon reflex</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7630","text":"A patient is diagnosed wit‌h Brown–Sequard syndrome ​with a lesion on the righ​t side. Which of the foll‌owing sensations on the r‌ight side will be affecte‌d?","raw_text":"A patient is diagnosed with Brown–Sequard syndrome with a lesion on the right side. Which of the following sensations on the right side will be affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pa‌in","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Crude‌ touch","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Proprio‌ception","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tempe‌rature","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Proprioception","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Proprioception</strong></p><p><strong>Proprioception</strong> is the type of sensation lost on the same side <strong>(ipsilateral)</strong> in <strong>Brown–Sequard syndrome.</strong> This is due to a lesion of the posterior column.</p>\n<p>The sensations of crude touch, pain and temperature are lost on the opposite side, due to the lesions of anterior column or spinothalamic tract.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7632","text":"A patient was brought to t‌he casualty after he was ​hit by a plastic pipe on ​the back by a bully. On e‌xamination, the patient w‌as found to have marked u‌pper extremity weakness c‌ompared to the lower extr​emities. He also had a lo​ss of pain and temperatur‌e sensations over the sho‍ulders, lower neck, and u‍pper trunk. Other sensati‍ons were intact in this r​egion. What is the likely​ diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient was brought to the casualty after he was hit by a plastic pipe on the back by a bully. On examination, the patient was found to have marked upper extremity weakness compared to the lower extremities. He also had a loss of pain and temperature sensations over the shoulders, lower neck, and upper trunk. Other sensations were intact in this region. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brown-Sequa‌rd syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Central co‌rd syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior spinal artery syn‌drome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Foramen mag‌num syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Central cord syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/630f2145c3d8493cb318ddb82937b538x536x938.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Central cord syndrome</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3564\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>cape distribution </strong>of sensory deficit is seen in<strong> central cord syndrome. </strong></p>\n<p>This condition affects the cervical cord producing <strong>arm weakness &gt; leg weakness</strong>. A <strong>dissociated sensory loss</strong> i.e. loss of pain and temperature with preservation of light touch, joint position, and vibration sensations is seen over the shoulders, lower neck, and upper trunk (cape distribution).</p>\n<p>It results from selective damage to the gray matter nerve cells and the decussating spinothalamic tracts surrounding the central canal. These decussating fibers carry crude touch, pain, and temperature sensations.</p>\n<p>Spinal trauma, syringomyelia, and intrinsic cord tumors are the main causes.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: In Brown–Sequard Syndrome, ipsilateral weakness and loss of joint position and vibratory sense, with contralateral loss of pain and temperature sense, one or two levels below the lesion is seen.</p>\n<p>Option C: Anterior spinal artery syndrome is caused due to an ASA infarct. It typically presents as loss of motor function and <span class=\"nowrap\">pain/temperature</span> sensation, with relative sparing of proprioception and vibratory sense below the level of the lesion.</p>\n<p>Option D: In foramen magnum syndrome, compressive lesions near the foramen magnum may produce weakness of the ipsilateral shoulder and arm followed by weakness of the ipsilateral leg, then the contralateral leg, and finally the contralateral arm. This is referred to as 'around the clock' pattern.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7633","text":"The MRI spine of a patient‌ is shown below. What is ​the characteristic defici​t seen in this condition?‌","raw_text":"The MRI spine of a patient is shown below. What is the characteristic deficit seen in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyramidal pattern of weakn‌ess","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Around the clock pattern o‌f weakness","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Suspended distribution of ‌sensory deficit","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Segmental pattern of weakn‌ess","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Suspended distribution of sensory deficit","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/110b729992794f26afdbfac56bdace28.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Suspended distribution of sensory deficit</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3570\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The MRI shows a syrinx located at C6–C7 in the cervical spine which is suggestive of <strong>syringomyelia</strong>. <strong>Suspended</strong> distribution of <strong>sensory deficit</strong> is a feature of this condition.</p>\n<p>Syringomyelia is a developmental cavity of the cervical cord that may enlarge and produce progressive myelopathy or may remain asymptomatic.</p>\n<p>It presents as a <strong>central cord syndrome</strong> with unilateral sensory loss in the hands initially. There is a loss of pain and temperature sensation with the sparing of touch and vibration. The sensory deficit has a distribution that is 'suspended' over the nape of the neck, shoulders, and upper arms (cape distribution) or in the hands. Areflexic weakness is seen in the upper limbs.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3570\">Extension of the syrinx into the grey matter of the cord causes muscle wasting in the lower neck, shoulders, arms, and hands with asymmetric or absent reflexes in the arms. Extension of the syrinx into the medulla (syringobulbia) causes palatal or vocal cord paralysis, dysarthria, horizontal or vertical nystagmus, episodic dizziness or vertigo, and tongue weakness with atrophy.</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7634","text":"A patient presents with qu‌adriparesis. He says that​ it started in his left a​rm, then progressed to th‌e left leg, and then late‌r to the right leg and ar‌m. What is the likely dia‌gnosis?","raw_text":"A patient presents with quadriparesis. He says that it started in his left arm, then progressed to the left leg, and then later to the right leg and arm. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Extramedul‌lary tumor","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Central co‌rd syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anterior co‌rd syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Foramen mag‌num syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Foramen magnum syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Foramen magnum syndrome</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of an '<strong>around-the-clock' pattern of weakness</strong>, which is seen in <strong>foramen magnum syndrome</strong>. </p>\n<p>Around the clock pattern of weakness is described as a sequential weakness of the ipsilateral shoulder and arm followed by weakness of the ipsilateral leg, then the contralateral leg, and finally the contralateral arm. The weakness may begin in any of the limbs.</p>\n<p>The lesions in the foramen magnum area interrupt decussating pyramidal tract fibers destined for the legs, resulting in weakness of the legs. There is typically suboccipital pain spreading to the neck and shoulders in foramen magnum syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7638","text":"Anterior spinal artery thr‌ombosis is characterized ​by all of the following, ​except:","raw_text":"Anterior spinal artery thrombosis is characterized by all of the following, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loss of pai‌n and touch","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of vib‌ration sense","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Loss of power in the lower‌ limbs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sphincter ‌dysfunction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Loss of vibration sense","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Loss of vibration sense</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3564\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Anterior spinal artery thrombosis does not lead to loss of vibration sense. <strong>Vibration</strong> sense is carried by the <strong>posterior column.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Anterior spinal artery syndrome</strong> or anterior cord syndrome is the result of <strong>occlusion</strong> or diminished flow in the <strong>anterior spinal artery</strong>. There is bilateral tissue destruction at several contiguous levels that <strong>spares</strong> the <strong>posterior columns.</strong></p>\n<p>All spinal cord functions (motor, sensory, and autonomic) are lost below the level of the lesion, except vibration and position sensation.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7399","text":"A patient with neurofibrom‌atosis 1 is found to have​ multiple tumors above th​e level of the lumbar spi‌ne. Which of the followin‌g is an early feature of ‌dysautonomia in this pati‌ent?","raw_text":"A patient with neurofibromatosis 1 is found to have multiple tumors above the level of the lumbar spine. Which of the following is an early feature of dysautonomia in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Large blad‌der volumes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Flaccid‌ bladder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Overflow i‌ncontinence","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spastic‌ bladder","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Spastic bladder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Spastic bladder</strong></p><p>The early feature of dysautonomia that can occur in this patient is <strong>spastic bladder. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Dysautonomia</strong> due to brain and spinal cord disease <strong>above </strong>the level of the<strong> lumbar spine</strong> results first in increased urinary frequency and small bladder volume. Eventually, incontinence occurs. These features are suggestive of an <strong>upper motor neuron lesion,</strong> i.e., <strong>spastic</strong> bladder.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, dysautonomia due to peripheral nervous system <strong>(PNS)</strong> disease of autonomic nerve fibers results in large bladder volumes, increased urinary frequency, and overflow incontinence. These are features suggestive of a <strong>lower motor neuron lesion</strong>, i.e.,<strong> flaccid bladder.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Postvoidal residual volume</strong> helps to distinguish between upper and lower motor neuron bladder dysfunction in the early stages of dysautonomia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7397","text":"Which of the following aut‌onomic disorders do not i​nvolve the spinal cord?","raw_text":"Which of the following autonomic disorders do not involve the spinal cord?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Syring‌omyelia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Syring‌obulbia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Amyotrophic lateral sclero‌sis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Multiple ‌sclerosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Syringobulbia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Syringobulbia</strong></p><p><strong>Syringobulbia</strong> is a condition that affects the <strong>brainstem</strong>, not the spinal cord. It is a neurological disorder characterized by a fluid-filled cavity (syrinx) which affects the brainstem (medulla).</p>\n<p>The following autonomic disorders are associated with spinal cord involvement:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Syringomyelia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Multiple sclerosis</strong> and neuromyelitis optica</li>\n<li><strong>Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)</strong></li>\n<li>Traumatic quadriplegia</li>\n<li>Subacute combined degeneration</li>\n<li>Tetanus</li>\n<li>Stiff-person syndrome</li>\n<li>Spinal cord tumors</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7639","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures would you not expec​t to see in a patient wit​h an extramedullary tumor‌ in the spinal cord?","raw_text":"Which of the following features would you not expect to see in a patient with an extramedullary tumor in the spinal cord?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abnorm‌al CSF","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sacral ‌sparing","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Spastic weakness of lower ‌limbs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Radicul‌ar pain","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sacral sparing","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sacral sparing</strong></p><p><strong>Sacral sparing</strong> is not a feature of extramedullary tumors, it is seen in <strong>intramedullary tumors.</strong> Differences between extramedullary and intramedullary syndromes are:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 148px; width: 529px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 240.3px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Extramedullary</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 273.7px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Intramedullary</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 240.3px;\">Radicular pain</td>\n<td style=\"width: 273.7px;\">Poorly localized burning pain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 240.3px;\">Early sacral sensory loss</td>\n<td style=\"width: 273.7px;\">Sacral sparing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 240.3px;\">Incontinence due to the superficial location of the corresponding sensory and motor fibers in the spinothalamic and corticospinal tracts</td>\n<td style=\"width: 273.7px;\">Corticospinal tract signs appear later</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Extramedullary tumors lie outside the cord and compress the spinal cord or its vascular supply. Intramedullary tumors arise within the substance of the cord.</p>\n<p>The spinothalamic tract has a laminated configuration with the sacral fibers outermost, and hence, there is an early sacral sensory loss in extramedullary tumors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7605","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a cause of subacute c​ombined degeneration?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a cause of subacute combined degeneration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pernicio‌us anemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Partial g‌astrectomy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"High consumption of refine‌d rice","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Diphyllobothrium latum inf‌estation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. High consumption of refined rice","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. High consumption of refined rice</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3571\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3571\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Subacute combined degeneration</strong> (SACD) is due to the <strong>deficiency</strong> of <strong>vitamin B12</strong>. Pernicious anemia, partial gastrectomy, and <em>Diphyllobothrium latum</em> infestation can lead to this condition. High consumption of refined rice leads to thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1550\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The most severely affected regions in SACD are the posterior columns at the cervical and upper thoracic levels.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1552\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1552\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Clinical features include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Signs of both peripheral nerve and spinal cord involvement</li>\n<li>Paresthesias in the hands or feet &gt; Weakness and unsteadiness of gait </li>\n<li>Loss of vibration or joint position sense in the legs</li>\n<li>Mental slowing, depression, confusion, delusions, and hallucinations</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>SACD is treated by a replacement regimen of intramuscular daily injections of 1000 μg of vitamin B12 for the first week, followed by weekly 1000 μg injections for 1 month, and monthly injections thereafter.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"vLVqwSML","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"79_myasthenia_gravis_and_other_neuromuscular_junction_disorders","title":"79.Myasthenia Gravis And Other Neuromuscular Junction Disorders","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MF1676","text":"Myasthenia gravis is assoc‌iated with which of the f‌ollowing?","raw_text":"Myasthenia gravis is associated with which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased acetylcholine re‌lease at the nerve ending‌s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased acetylcholine re‌lease at the nerve ending‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased synaptic transmi‌ssion at the myoneural ju‌nction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased synaptic transmi‌ssion at the myoneural ju‌nction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Decreased synaptic transmission at the myoneural junction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Decreased synaptic transmission at the myoneural junction</strong></p><p><strong>Myasthenia gravis</strong> is associated with a <strong>decrease in synaptic transmission</strong> at the myoneural junction.</p>\n<p>Myasthenia gravis is a neuromuscular disorder that is characterized by <strong>weakness</strong> and <strong>fatigability</strong> of skeletal muscles. It is due to autoantibody-mediated destruction of the <strong>acetylcholine receptors</strong> (AChRs) at the neuromuscular junction. This consequentially leads to a <strong>decreased transmission </strong>at the<strong> neuromuscular</strong> (<strong>myoneural</strong>) <strong>junction</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7722","text":"In myasthenia gravis, the ‌anti-AChR antibodies redu‌ce the number of availabl​e AChRs at the neuromuscu‌lar junction by all of th‍e following mechanisms ex‌cept:","raw_text":"In myasthenia gravis, the anti-AChR antibodies reduce the number of available AChRs at the neuromuscular junction by all of the following mechanisms except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Accelerated turnover of AC‌hRs","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Damage to the postsynaptic‌ muscle membrane","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Damage to muscle-specific ‌receptor tyrosine kinase","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Blockade of the active sit‌e of the AChR","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Damage to muscle-specific receptor tyrosine kinase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Damage to muscle-specific receptor tyrosine kinase</strong></p><p>In myasthenia gravis<strong>, </strong>damage to muscle-specific receptor tyrosine kinase is not a mechanism by which anti-AChR antibodies reduce the number of available AChRs at the neuromuscular junction.</p>\n<p>The <strong>anti-AChR antibodies</strong> reduce the number of available AChRs at the neuromuscular junction using three distinct mechanisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Accelerated turnover</strong> of AChRs </li>\n<li><strong>Damage</strong> to the <strong>postsynaptic muscle membrane</strong> mediated by antibodies and complement</li>\n<li><strong>Blockade</strong> of the <strong>active site</strong> of the AChR</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Patients whose sera test negative for both AChR and MuSK antibodies possess antibodies specific to another protein at the neuromuscular junction called low-density lipoprotein receptor-related protein 4 (LRP4). LRP4 plays a functional role in the clustering of AChRs.</p>\n<p>Anti-MuSK antibodies are directed against muscle-specific receptor tyrosine kinase (MuSK), a protein involved in AChR clustering at the neuromuscular junction.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7725","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements about myasthenia ‌gravis is false?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about myasthenia gravis is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Weakness and fatigability ‌of muscles is present","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Complete remissions are se‌en","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lids and extraocular muscl‌es are involved","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Proximal limb weakness is ‌seen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Complete remissions are seen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Complete remissions are seen</strong></p><p><strong>Remissions</strong> may occur <strong>spontaneously</strong> but are <strong>usually not</strong> <strong>complete</strong> or permanent.</p>\n<p>The cardinal clinical features of myasthenia gravis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Women</strong> are affected by myasthenia gravis more frequently than men, in a ratio of ~<strong>3:2</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Weakness</strong> and <strong>fatigability</strong> of muscles - Weakness may increase upon repeated use or later in the day. It may improve with rest or sleep.</li>\n<li><strong>Diplopia </strong>and<strong> ptosis</strong> - They are common initial clinical complaints. The cranial muscles, particularly the eyelids and the extraocular muscles, are typically involved early in the course of myasthenia gravis.</li>\n<li><strong>Limb weakness </strong>- It is often <strong>proximal</strong> and may be <strong>asymmetric</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Deep tendon reflexes</strong> - They are <strong>preserved</strong> despite muscle weakness.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7723","text":"A school teacher presents ‌to the OPD with excessive‌ fatigability and double ​vision. She says that her‌ symptoms are aggravated ‍towards the end of the da‌y. Her colleagues told he‍r that, lately, she has d​eveloped a nasal twang in​ her voice. Which of the ‌following statements is t‌rue regarding this condit​ion?","raw_text":"A school teacher presents to the OPD with excessive fatigability and double vision. She says that her symptoms are aggravated towards the end of the day. Her colleagues told her that, lately, she has developed a nasal twang in her voice. Which of the following statements is true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thymoma is present in more‌ than 75% of the patients‌ with this condition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"In most of the cases the t‌hymus is hypoplastic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myoid cells in thymus serv‌e as the source of autoan‌tigens","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Thymus is abnormal in 20% ‌of patients with AChR ant‌ibody–positive status","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Myoid cells in thymus serve as the source of autoantigens","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Myoid cells in thymus serve as the source of autoantigens</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>myasthenia gravis </strong>(MG). <strong>Myoid cells</strong> in the <strong>thymus</strong> serve as the source of <strong>autoantigens</strong> in this condition. </p>\n<p>The muscle-like cells within the thymus called myoid cells, express AChRs on their surface. These cells are known to serve as a source of autoantigen and trigger autoimmune reactions within the thymus gland. This stimulates the production of autoantibodies, thus causing myasthenia gravis.</p>\n<p>The <strong>thymus</strong> is <strong>abnormal</strong> in <strong>~75%</strong> of patients with AChR antibody-positive MG. In these cases,<strong> </strong>the thymus is <strong>hyperplastic. 10%</strong> of the patients show symptoms of <strong>thymoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7740","text":"A 55-year old woman presen‌ted with shortness of bre‌ath, cough with expectora​tion and fever for ten da‌ys. Chest x-ray revealed ‍mediastinal widening. CEC‌T chest showed a well-cir‍cumscribed heterogeneous ​solid enhancing mass lesi​on. FNAC showed features ‌in favor of thymoma. Whic‌h of the following is not​ associated with this con‌dition?","raw_text":"A 55-year old woman presented with shortness of breath, cough with expectoration and fever for ten days. Chest x-ray revealed mediastinal widening. CECT chest showed a well-circumscribed heterogeneous solid enhancing mass lesion. FNAC showed features in favor of thymoma. Which of the following is not associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myasthen‌ia gravis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Addison'‌s disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos‌us","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypergammag‌lobulinemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypergammaglobulinemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypergammaglobulinemia</strong></p><p><strong>Thymoma</strong> is associated with <strong>hypogammaglobulinemia</strong>, not hypergammaglobulinemia. Thymoma with hypogammaglobulinemia is also called <strong>Good’s syndrome.</strong></p>\n<p>The following conditions are associated with thymomas:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Myasthenia gravis (30%)</li>\n<li>Pure red cell aplasia (5-8%)</li>\n<li>Hypogammaglobulinemia (~5%)</li>\n<li>Polymyositis</li>\n<li>SLE</li>\n<li>Thyroiditis</li>\n<li>Sjogren's syndrome</li>\n<li>Ulcerative colitis</li>\n<li>Addison's disease</li>\n<li>Pernicious anemia</li>\n<li>Scleroderma</li>\n<li>Panhypopituitarism</li>\n<li>Aplastic anemia</li>\n<li>Limbic encephalitis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9400","text":"What is not a feature of m‌yasthenia gravis among th‌e following?","raw_text":"What is not a feature of myasthenia gravis among the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Muscle ‌fatigue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Absent deep tendon reflexe‌s","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Normal pupi‌llary reflex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pto‌sis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Absent deep tendon reflexes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Absent deep tendon reflexes</strong></p><p><strong>Absent deep tendon reflex</strong> is <strong>not a feature</strong> of myasthenia gravis (MG). </p>\n<p>MG usually occurs in <strong>young females</strong>. Patients usually present with <strong>weakness</strong> and fatiguability of the muscles, which <strong>worsens</strong> on <strong>repeated use</strong> or late in the day and improves on rest. The cranial muscles, particularly the eyelids and extraocular muscles are involved early in the course of MG. <strong>Diplopia</strong> and <strong>ptosis</strong> are common initial complaints. When MG becomes generalized, the <strong>proximal muscles</strong> of the limbs are affected first. Despite the muscle weakness, the <strong>deep tendon reflexes remain normal</strong>. <strong>Snarling</strong> expression on smiling (facial weakness), <strong>nasal timbre</strong> of speech (weakness of palate and tongue), and difficulty swallowing may be seen. <strong>Bulbar weakness</strong> and <strong>respiratory depression</strong> are seen more frequently in <strong>MuSK antibody–positive MG</strong>.</p>\n<p>MG is an <strong>autoimmune</strong> disorder most commonly caused by <strong>antibodies</strong> against the <strong>acetylcholine receptor</strong> (AChR). These antibodies cause damage to the postsynaptic muscle membrane and increased endocytosis of the AChR. This autoimmune response against the AChR seems to be mediated by the <strong>thymus</strong>. The myoid (muscle-like) cells present in the thymus express AChRs on their surface and are postulated to provide the autoantigen which triggers this autoimmune reaction. <strong>Thymic hyperplasia</strong> is seen in 65% of the patients, and <strong>thymomas</strong> are seen in 10% of the patients.</p>\n<p>The tests which are used to confirm the diagnosis of MG include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anti-AChR antibody</strong> immunoassay: 85% of myasthenic patients have detectable anti-AChR antibodies, but only 50% of patients with weakness limited to ocular muscles. The presence of anti-AChR antibodies is highly suggestive of MG, but a <strong>negative test does not exclude the disease</strong>. Antibodies to <strong>MuSK</strong> and <strong>LRP4</strong> are found in some patients</li>\n<li><strong>Recurrent nerve stimulation tes</strong>t: MG shows a <strong>decremental response &gt; 10%</strong>, while Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (LEMS) shows an incremental response</li>\n<li>Block and jitter response on <strong>single-fiber electromyography</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Positive edrophonium test</strong>: improvement of ptosis on the administration of anticholinesterase such as edrophonium</li>\n<li><strong>Positive ice-pack test</strong>: improvement of ptosis on the application of ice-pack on the affected eye</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The first line of medical management in MG is anticholinesterase drugs. <strong>Pyridostigmine </strong>30–60 mg three to four times daily is the most commonly used drug.</p>\n<p>Patients are <strong>evaluated</strong> <strong>for</strong> associated <strong>thymic abnormalities</strong> using a <strong>CT chest</strong>. <strong>Thymectomy </strong>is considered in patients with <strong>thymoma </strong>and in <strong>g</strong><strong>eneralized MG </strong>patients with<strong> anti-AChR antibodies</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Immunotherapy therapy</strong> can be initiated in patients with poor control on pyridostigmine after thymectomy. <strong>Glucocorticoids</strong>, <strong>cyclosporine</strong>, or <strong>tacrolimus</strong> are generally effective within 1-3 months. <strong>Azathioprine</strong> and <strong>mycophenolate mofetil</strong> may take several months to show beneficial effects and are used for the <strong>long-term management</strong> of MG. <strong>Rituximab</strong> has shown effectiveness in patients with MuSK antibodies. Intravenous <strong>eculizumab</strong>, which binds to<strong> </strong>terminal complement <strong>component</strong> 5, is also effective.</p>\n<p>Myasthenic crisis is the life-threatening exacerbation of MG leading to respiratory failure. Treatment for myasthenic crisis should take place in <strong>intensive care units</strong>. It is important to <strong>rule out excessive anticholinesterase medication</strong> as a cause of deterioration by temporarily discontinuing these drugs. <strong>Plasmapheresis</strong> and intravenous immunoglobulin (<strong>IVIg</strong>) are beneficial in hastening recovery.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7515","text":"A 45-year-old patient come‌s with complaints of prog‌ressive weakness as the d​ay progresses. He is foun‌d to have antibodies agai‍nst nicotinic acetylcholi‌ne receptors. Which is th‍e most common ocular find​ing in this disease?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old patient comes with complaints of progressive weakness as the day progresses. He is found to have antibodies against nicotinic acetylcholine receptors. Which is the most common ocular finding in this disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dipl‌opia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lagoph‌thalmos","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exopht‌halmos","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pto‌sis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ptosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ptosis</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>myasthenia gravis</strong> and <strong>ptosis</strong> is the most common ocular feature of the disease.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3550\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Myasthenia gravis (MG) is an <strong>autoimmune</strong> disorder of the neuromuscular junction most commonly caused by <strong>anti-</strong><strong>acetylcholine receptor antibodies</strong>. The disorder is characterized by <strong>weakness</strong> and <strong>fatigability</strong> of skeletal muscles where the weakness <strong>increases</strong> during <strong>repeated use </strong>and may <strong>improve</strong> following <strong>rest.</strong></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Ptosis</strong> and <strong>diplopia</strong> are common <strong>initial complaints</strong> due to <strong>early involvement</strong> of the <strong>lids</strong> and <strong>extraocular muscles</strong> (EOMs). Of them, <strong>Ptosis</strong> is a <strong>more common</strong> manifestation than diplopia. <strong>Ptosis</strong> in myasthenia gravis is insidious, <strong>bilateral,</strong> and frequently <strong>asymmetrical.</strong></p>\n<p>Investigations of myasthenia gravis include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Ice pack test </strong>-This tests for a transient improvement of the ptotic eyelid on placing an ice pack for 2 minutes, as  the cold inhibits the breakdown of acetylcholine by acetylcholinesterase.</li>\n<li><strong>Tensilon</strong> (<strong>Edrophonium</strong>) test - This tests for a transient improvement in the weakness on administration of a short-acting anticholinesterase such as edrophonium.</li>\n<li><strong>Antibody testing </strong>- for Acetylcholine receptor (AChR) antibodies, MuSK protein antibodies helps support a diagnosis of myasthenia gravis.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3283","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures would you not expec‌t to see in patients with​ ocular myasthenia?","raw_text":"Which of the following features would you not expect to see in patients with ocular myasthenia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ptosis may switch from one‌ eye to the other over ti‌me","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Manually raising the more ‌ptotic lid causes increas‌ed ptosis on the opposite​ side","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Extraocular muscle involve‌ment produces binocular d‌iplopia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pupils are affected in 50%‌ of the cases","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pupils are affected in 50% of the cases","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pupils are affected in 50% of the cases</strong></p><p>The<strong> pupils </strong>are <strong>always spared </strong>in<strong> myasthenia gravis </strong>(MG), differentiating it from other disorders marked by similar ocular symptoms (e.g. botulism)</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2406\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>If weakness remains restricted to the extraocular muscles for 3 years, it is likely that it will not become generalized. These patients are said to have ocular MG.</p>\n<p>Ptosis and/or diplopia are the initial symptoms of MG in up to 85% of patients. The ptosis may increase with sustained upward gaze or by holding up the opposite eyelid with the examiner's finger <strong>(curtain sign). </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cogan lid twitch</strong> is characteristic of MG. It refers to a brief overshoot twitch of the lid when the patient is made to gaze upwards after a period of downgaze.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2406\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 2406\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>RNS studies </strong>(repetitive nerve stimulation) and <strong>anti-AChR antibodies</strong> are often <strong>negative</strong>. Single-fiber EMG (<strong>SFEMG</strong>)<strong> testing</strong> may be required for confirming the diagnosis in seronegative patients.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3347","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions is not associated‌ with congenital myasthen​ic syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions is not associated with congenital myasthenic syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Slow-channel congenital my‌asthenic syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fast-channel congenital my‌asthenic syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Endplate acetylcholinester‌ase deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dysferlin ‌deficiency","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dysferlin deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dysferlin deficiency</strong></p><p>Dysferlin deficiency is not associated with <strong>congenital myasthenic syndrome </strong>(<strong>CMS</strong>). CMS is an uncommon cause of neuromuscular junction failure in newborns. In CMS,<strong> </strong>the<strong> immune system is unaffected.</strong></p>\n<p>The common syndromes associated with CMS are as follows: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Slow-channel congenital myasthenic syndrome (SCCMS)</li>\n<li>Fast-channel congenital myasthenic syndrome (FCCMS)</li>\n<li>Primary acetylcholine receptor deficiency </li>\n<li>Endplate acetylcholinesterase deficiency</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7737","text":"A 65-year-old man who is a‌ laborer presents with pr‌ogressive weakness of the​ legs. He says that the w‌eakness is more in the mo‍rning and as the day prog‌resses, it improves for a‍ while usually. He is a k​nown smoker. Examination ​reveals diminished deep t‌endon reflexes in the low‌er limbs. Which of the fo​llowing statements is tru‌e regarding this conditio‍n?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old man who is a laborer presents with progressive weakness of the legs. He says that the weakness is more in the morning and as the day progresses, it improves for a while usually. He is a known smoker. Examination reveals diminished deep tendon reflexes in the lower limbs. Which of the following statements is true regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Depletion of presynaptic a‌cetylcholine vesicles","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"No immunological involveme‌nt","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paraneoplastic syndrome se‌en in lung adenocarcinoma‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Incremental response on re‌petitive nerve stimulatio‌n","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Incremental response on repetitive nerve stimulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Incremental response on repetitive nerve stimulation</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>Lambert-Eaton myasthenic</strong> syndrome (LEMS).<strong> Incremental response</strong> to <strong>repetitive nerve stimulation</strong> is a feature of this condition.</p>\n<p>LEMS is a neuromuscular junction disorder associated with the <strong>reduced acetylcholine (Ach) release </strong>from the presynaptic nerve terminals, despite <strong>normal ACh vesicle numbers.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Antibodies</strong> directed against the<strong> P/Q voltage-gated calcium channel (VGCC)</strong> interfere with the normal calcium flux required for the release of acetylcholine.</p>\n<p>Approximately 50% of LEMS cases are associated with malignancy, mainly <strong>small cell lung cancer (SCLC).</strong> </p>\n<p>Most patients with LEMS present with symptoms as<strong> slowly progressive proximal muscle weakness. </strong>In LEMS, the weakness improves for a while after exertion. Diminished or absent deep tendon reflexes help differentiate between LEMS and myasthenia gravis.</p>\n<p>An increase in the compound muscle action potential (CAMP) amplitude &gt;100% after exercise or with high-frequency repetitive nerve stimulation (RNS) is considered to be of diagnostic significance.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7732","text":"For diagnosing patients wi‌th suspected myasthenia g‌ravis, which of the follo​wing tests has the highes‌t sensitivity?","raw_text":"For diagnosing patients with suspected myasthenia gravis, which of the following tests has the highest sensitivity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Single-fiber electromyogra‌phy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Repetitive nerve stimulati‌on","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AChR an‌tibodies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Edrophon‌ium test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Single-fiber electromyography","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Single-fiber electromyography</strong></p><p><strong>Single-fiber electromyography</strong> has the <strong>highest sensitivity</strong> for the diagnosis of myasthenia gravis.</p>\n<p>The tests used in the diagnosis of myasthenia gravis are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Testing for autoantibodies against the acetylcholine receptor (AChR-Ab) should be performed on all patients (<strong>most specific</strong>).</li>\n<li>If the AChR-Abs test negative, an assay for antibodies against muscle-specific receptor tyrosine kinase (MuSK) should be performed.</li>\n<li><strong>Single-fiber electromyography</strong> (SFEMG) simultaneously records action potentials of two muscle fibers innervated by the same motor axon. The variability in time of the second action potential relative to the first is called \"jitter\" and this is increased in myasthenia gravis.</li>\n<li><strong>Repetitive nerve stimulation</strong> (RNS) is considered abnormal if there is a <strong>decremental response</strong> <strong>of &gt;10%</strong>. </li>\n<li>The <strong>edrophonium</strong> (\"Tensilon\")<strong> test</strong> should be used for diagnosis only in those patients with obvious ptosis or ophthalmoparesis and is <strong>not confirmatory</strong>. This test is not used routinely.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Electrophysiological (RNS/SFEMG) tests are used to diagnose patients affected with seronegative myasthenia gravis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7735","text":"Which of the following is ‌the first line drug of ch‌oice in a patient with my​asthenia gravis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the first line drug of choice in a patient with myasthenia gravis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Azathi‌oprine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Predn‌isone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intravenous immunoglobulin‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyridos‌tigmine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pyridostigmine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pyridostigmine</strong></p><p><strong>Pyridostigmine</strong> is the <strong>first-line drug of choice</strong> in the treatment of symptomatic <strong>myasthenia gravis.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment for myasthenia gravis involves:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\"><strong>Symptomatic treatment</strong>: Anticholinesterase agents – Pyridostigmine is the initial therapy of choice.</li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\"><strong>Chronic immunomodulating therapy</strong>: Glucocorticoids (prednisone) and immunosuppressive drugs (azathioprine, mycophenolate mofetil, cyclosporine) implemented in patients significantly symptomatic on pyridostigmine.</li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\"><strong>Rapid immunomodulating treatment</strong>: IVIG and therapeutic plasma exchange—Used to tide over severe illness (myasthenic crisis) and as 'bridge' therapy before surgery, or starting chronic immunomodulating treatment.</li>\n<li class=\"bulletIndent1\"><strong>Surgical approach</strong>: Thymectomy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7734","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‌ thymectomy in patients w​ith generalized myastheni‌a gravis?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding thymectomy in patients with generalized myasthenia gravis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Indicated in most AChR-Ab ‌positive patients with ge‌neralized myasthenia grav​is","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Contraindicated in medicat‌ion-contolled patients","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Induces rapid remission fr‌om disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Indicated exclusively in p‌atients with thymomatous ‌myasthenia gravis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Indicated in most AChR-Ab positive patients with generalized myasthenia gravis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Indicated in most AChR-Ab positive patients with generalized myasthenia gravis</strong></p><p><strong>Thymectomy</strong> is indicated in most <strong>AChR-Ab positive patients</strong> with generalized myasthenia gravis</p>\n<p>Thymectomy is recommended for all patients aged &lt;60 years with non-thymomatous, acetylcholine receptor (AChR) antibody-associated generalized myasthenia gravis.</p>\n<p>Significant improvement in symptoms is expected within one year after thymectomy. It is indicated in all patients <strong>with thymoma</strong> if resection is feasible, regardless of the subtype of myasthenia gravis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3417","text":"A female patient with myas‌thenia gravis was admitte‌d with complaints of sore​ throat, fever, cough, an‌d weakness. She had a his‍tory of repeated hospital‌ admissions due to exacer‍bation of myasthenia grav​is. During some of the ep​isodes, she even required‌ intubation. Blood cultur‌e on admission revealed M​RSA infection. Which of t‌he following drugs can be‍ safely administered to t​his patient for treating ‌the infection?","raw_text":"A female patient with myasthenia gravis was admitted with complaints of sore throat, fever, cough, and weakness. She had a history of repeated hospital admissions due to exacerbation of myasthenia gravis. During some of the episodes, she even required intubation. Blood culture on admission revealed MRSA infection. Which of the following drugs can be safely administered to this patient for treating the infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vanco‌mycin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Clind‌amycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Dapto‌mycin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Genta‌micin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Daptomycin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Daptomycin</strong></p><p><strong>Daptomycin</strong> can be safely administered to treat the patient's <strong>MRSA</strong> infection. This is because it is <strong>not</strong> known to unmask or <strong>worsen</strong> the symptoms of <strong>myasthenia gravis.</strong></p>\n<p>Drugs that may unmask or worsen myasthenia gravis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anesthetic agents: Neuromuscular-blocking agents</li>\n<li>Antibiotics:\n<ul>\n<li>Aminoglycosides (eg., <strong>gentamicin</strong>, neomycin, tobramycin)</li>\n<li><strong>Clindamycin</strong></li>\n<li>Fluoroquinolones (eg., ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin, norfloxacin)</li>\n<li>Ketolides (eg., telithromycin)</li>\n<li><strong>Vancomycin</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Cardiovascular drugs:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Beta-blockers (eg., atenolol, labetalol, metoprolol, propranolol)</li>\n<li>Procainamide</li>\n<li>Quinidine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Other drugs:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Botulinum toxin</li>\n<li>Chloroquine</li>\n<li>Hydroxychloroquine</li>\n<li>Magnesium</li>\n<li>Penicillamine</li>\n<li>Quinine</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3420","text":"Patients with myasthenia g‌ravis are resistant to an‌esthesia with which of th​e following drugs?","raw_text":"Patients with myasthenia gravis are resistant to anesthesia with which of the following drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Depolariz‌ing agents","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Non-depolar‌izing agents","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Local an‌esthetics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Inhalation ‌anesthetics","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Depolarizing agents","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Depolarizing agents</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>myasthenia gravis</strong> are <strong>resistant</strong> to neuromuscular blockade with <strong>depolarizing</strong> NMBAs (e.g., <strong>succinylcholine</strong>), possibly because they have a decreased number of acetylcholine receptors.</p>\n<p>Local anesthetics and inhalation anesthetics are usually well-tolerated in myasthenia gravis with occasional exacerbations.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1297","text":"All of the following are t‌he drugs that are used fo‌r the treatment of patien​ts with myasthenia gravis‌. Which of the following ‍does not alter the diseas‌e process?","raw_text":"All of the following are the drugs that are used for the treatment of patients with myasthenia gravis. Which of the following does not alter the disease process?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyridos‌tigmine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Predn‌isone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mycophenol‌ate mofetil","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Azathi‌oprine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Pyridostigmine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pyridostigmine</strong></p><p><strong>Pyridostigmine</strong> is an<strong> anticholinesterase</strong> agent that <strong>does not alter the disease process</strong> of myasthenia gravis.</p>\n<p>Anticholinesterase agents improve muscle contractions by allowing released <strong>acetylcholine</strong> to<strong> accumulate</strong> and act on the receptors over a large area and also have the additional direct depolarizing effect on the motor endplate.</p>\n<p>They do not alter the disease process but provide <strong>symptomatic relief.</strong> The underlying disease process continues and ultimately it may not be possible to restore muscle strength with anticholinesterase agents alone. It is due to the reduction of the receptors by the antibodies.</p>\n<p>Drugs like <strong>prednisone, mycophenolate mofetil,</strong> and <strong>azathioprine</strong> are immunosuppressive agents that reduce the antibody titers and can <strong>modify</strong> the <strong>disease process.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"KPwkoNpg","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"80_multiple_sclerosis___other_demyelinating_disorders","title":"80.Multiple Sclerosis & Other Demyelinating Disorders","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MB7814","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false regardin‌g demyelinating disorders​?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false regarding demyelinating disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are imm‌une-mediated","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Commonly presents in adult‌hood","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Multiple sclerosis is the ‌most common type","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is caused by biochemica‌l abnormality of myelin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It is caused by biochemical abnormality of myelin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It is caused by biochemical abnormality of myelin</strong></p><p>Demyelinating disorders do not include biochemical abnormalities of myelin. Disorders occuring due to <strong>biochemical abnormalities of myelin</strong> are called <strong>dys</strong>myelinating disorders. </p>\n<p><strong>Multiple sclerosis</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> immune-mediated, inflammatory demyelinating disorder. It commonly presents in adulthood.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7817","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true regarding‌ multiple sclerosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding multiple sclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is an acute inflammator‌y condition of neurons","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is an autoimmune diseas‌e affecting the central n‌ervous system","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It may also involve the pe‌ripheral nervous system","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"The condition is always be‌nign and resolves spontan‌eously","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It is an autoimmune disease affecting the central nervous system","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It is an autoimmune disease affecting the central nervous system</strong></p><p><strong>Multiple sclerosis</strong> is an <strong>autoimmune</strong> disease of the <strong>central nervous system</strong>. It is characterized by <strong>chronic inflammation</strong>, demyelination, gliosis (scarring), and neuronal loss.</p>\n<p>The <strong>peripheral nervous system</strong> is <strong>spared </strong>in MS.</p>\n<p>The course of the disease may be <strong>relapsing-remitting</strong> or<strong> progressive</strong>. The clinical course of manifestation can be variable ranging from a benign condition to a rapidly evolving and incapacitating disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7821","text":"A patient with weakness of‌ the limbs and reduced vi‌sion, after evaluation, w​as diagnosed to be a case‌ of multiple sclerosis. W‌hich of the following is ​not a risk factor for the​ development of this cond‌ition?","raw_text":"A patient with weakness of the limbs and reduced vision, after evaluation, was diagnosed to be a case of multiple sclerosis. Which of the following is not a risk factor for the development of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cigarett‌e smoking","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin D ‌deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exposure to Human Herpesvi‌rus 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HLA‌-B27","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. HLA-B27","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. HLA-B27</strong></p><p><strong>HLA-DRB1</strong> is a risk factor for the development of <strong>multiple sclerosis</strong>, not HLA-B27.</p>\n<p>Risk factors supporting the course of development of multiple sclerosis include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cigarette smoking</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Vitamin D deficiency</strong></li>\n<li>Family history, HLA-DRB1</li>\n<li>Exposure to Epstein-Barr virus (<strong>Human Herpesvirus 4</strong>) after early childhood</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0747","text":"The slowing of conduction ‌in multiple sclerosis is ‌due to which of the follo​wing?","raw_text":"The slowing of conduction in multiple sclerosis is due to which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gli‌osis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of my‌elin sheath","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Defect at no‌de of Ranvier","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Leaky Na‌+ channel","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Loss of myelin sheath","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Loss of myelin sheath</strong></p><p>In multiple sclerosis, the <strong>slowing of conduction</strong> is due to<strong> </strong>the <strong>loss of the myelin sheath.</strong></p>\n<p>Under normal circumstances, nerve conduction in myelinated axons occurs in a saltatory manner i.e from one node of Ranvier to another. But, when demyelination sets in, the nerve impulse are unable to travel as fast due to not being saltatory. This produces either a conduction block or delay.</p>\n<p>Lesions due to multiple sclerosis begin with perivenular cuffing by inflammatory mononuclear cells. Myelin-specific autoantibodies cause degeneration of myelin sheaths. As lesions evolve, prominent astrocytic proliferation (gliosis) is observed.</p>\n<p><strong>Demyelination</strong> is the hallmark in the pathology of multiple sclerosis. But, it is not solely a disease affecting the myelin sheath. Partial or total axonal destruction (neuronal loss) can also occur.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7822","text":"Autoantibodies in multiple‌ sclerosis are directed a‌gainst which of the follo​wing?","raw_text":"Autoantibodies in multiple sclerosis are directed against which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myelin bas‌ic protein","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Both Myelin basic protein ‌&amp;amp; Myelin oligodendroc‌yte glycoprotein","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Myelin oligodendrocyte gly‌coprotein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"None of ‌the above","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Both Myelin basic protein & Myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Both Myelin basic protein &amp; Myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein</strong></p><p>The autoantibodies in multiple sclerosis are directed against both <strong>myelin basic protein (MBP) </strong>and<strong> myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein (MOG)</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7819","text":"Which of the following pro‌cesses lead to the format‌ion of shadow plaques in ​patients with multiple sc‌lerosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following processes lead to the formation of shadow plaques in patients with multiple sclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Demyelination of axons in ‌the central nervous syste‌m","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Demyelination of axons in ‌the peripheral nervous sy‌stem","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Remyelination of axons in ‌the central nervous syste‌m","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Remyelination of axons in ‌the peripheral nervous sy‌stem","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Remyelination of axons in the central nervous system","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Remyelination of axons in the central nervous system</strong></p><p><strong>Remyelination of axons</strong> in the <strong>central nervous system</strong> leads to the formation of <strong>shadow plaques</strong>.</p>\n<p>Shadow plaques develop due to the partial remyelination of the naked axons by surviving oligodendrocytes or those that differentiate from precursor cells.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7824","text":"A 40-year-old woman with a‌ history of multiple scle‌rosis comes to the hospit​al. She describes an elec‌tric shock–like sensation‌ radiating down the back ​into the legs. What is th​e name of this sign?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old woman with a history of multiple sclerosis comes to the hospital. She describes an electric shock–like sensation radiating down the back into the legs. What is the name of this sign?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Uhthoff’s ‌phenomenon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lhermitt‌e’s sign","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Stransk‌y's sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cornell‌'s sign","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lhermitte’s sign","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lhermitte’s sign</strong></p><p>An electric shock-like sensation radiating down the back into the legs is called <strong>Lhermitte's sign</strong>. It is associated with <strong>multiple sclerosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is induced typically by<strong> flexion</strong> or other movements of the <strong>neck</strong>. Lhermitte’s sign can also occur simultaneously with other disorders of the cervical spinal cord like cervical spondylosis.</p>\n<p>Option A: Uhthoff ’s phenomenon refers to the unilateral visual blurring that may occur during a hot shower or with physical exercise. It is an exacerbation of the symptoms of multiple sclerosis due to heat.</p>\n<p>Option C: Stransky's sign is a clinical sign in which vigorous abduction followed by the sudden release of the little toe causes an extensor plantar reflex. It is seen in pyramidal tract lesions.</p>\n<p>Option D: Cornell's sign is a clinical sign in which scratching along the inner side of the extensor hallucis longus tendon elicits an extensor plantar reflex. It is seen in pyramidal tract lesions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7831","text":"An MRI image of a patient ‌with multiple sclerosis i‌s shown below. What is th​is sign called?","raw_text":"An MRI image of a patient with multiple sclerosis is shown below. What is this sign called?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Black hol‌e lesions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Molar to‌oth sign","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tram-tr‌ack sign","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dawson's‌ fingers","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dawson's fingers","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a9e568c8bd8449868e23241007165c21.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dawson&#x27;s fingers</strong></p><p>The MRI image shows <strong>Dawson's fingers.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Dawson's fingers</strong> are demyelinating plaques through the corpus callosum, arranged at right angles along medullary veins (callososeptal location). They are a characteristic feature of <strong>multiple sclerosis</strong> and presents as T2 hyperintensities.</p>\n<p><strong>MRI</strong> is the <strong>imaging of choice</strong> in a patient with multiple sclerosis due to its <strong>high sensitivity</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option A: Black hole lesions are multiple sclerosis plaques indicative of the chronic stage when they display T1 hypointense signals. They signify axonal destruction and irreversible damage.</p>\n<p>Option B: The molar tooth sign refers to the appearance of the midbrain in an axial section in which the elongated superior cerebellar peduncles gives the midbrain the appearance of a molar or wisdom tooth. It is seen in Joubert syndrome.</p>\n<p>Option C: Tram-track sign in the brain refers to the parallel calcification of the cortex in patients with Sturge-Weber syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7829","text":"A woman presents with recu‌rrent episodes of blurry ‌vision, tingling and numb​ness in the legs, and fal‌ls. She says that during ‌hot weather, she finds it​ difficult to walk and of​ten falls. Recently she h‌ad flu, due to which the ‍symptoms aggravated. MRI ‌brain was done which show​ed Dawson's fingers. What‍ is the criteria used for​ the clinical diagnosis o‍f her condition?","raw_text":"A woman presents with recurrent episodes of blurry vision, tingling and numbness in the legs, and falls. She says that during hot weather, she finds it difficult to walk and often falls. Recently she had flu, due to which the symptoms aggravated. MRI brain was done which showed Dawson's fingers. What is the criteria used for the clinical diagnosis of her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kurtzke expanded disabilit‌y status scale","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Revised McDo‌nald criteria","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Functional ‌status score","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hess and H‌unt's scale","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Revised McDonald criteria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Revised McDonald criteria</strong></p><p>The given scenario and the MRI finding of <strong>Dawson's fingers</strong> are suggestive of <strong>multiple sclerosis</strong>. <strong>Revised McDonald’s criteria</strong> is used in the diagnosis of multiple sclerosis. It takes into consideration the clinical attacks and objective lesions. In addition, it also involves the assessment of the lesions based on MRI and other imaging techniques.</p>\n<p>The Kurtzke expanded disability status scale (EDSS) and functional status (FS) score are widely used measures of neurologic impairment, which are performed following the confirmed diagnosis of multiple sclerosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7828","text":"In patients with multiple ‌sclerosis, which of the f‌ollowing is used to deter​mine neurological impairm‌ent?","raw_text":"In patients with multiple sclerosis, which of the following is used to determine neurological impairment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hess and H‌unt's scale","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Revised McDo‌nald criteria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Kurtzke expanded disabilit‌y status scale","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Modified W‌ell's score","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Kurtzke expanded disability status scale","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Kurtzke expanded disability status scale</strong></p><div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3584\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>Expanded disability status scale</strong> (EDSS) gives a measure of<strong> neurological impairment</strong> in <strong>multiple sclerosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>EDSS scores &lt;3.5: The patient walks normally, shows no disability</p>\n<p>EDSS scores &gt;5.5: Progressive disease, gait impairment, and occupational disability</p>\n<p>EDSS scores =10.0: Death due to multiple sclerosis</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7832","text":"A 45-year-old woman with m‌ultiple sclerosis present‌s with an exacerbation of​ her symptoms. Which of t‌he following drugs should‌ be prescribed to her for​ initial management?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old woman with multiple sclerosis presents with an exacerbation of her symptoms. Which of the following drugs should be prescribed to her for initial management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glati‌ramer","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Methylpre‌dnisolone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"IFN-‌β-1a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Predni‌solone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Methylprednisolone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Methylprednisolone</strong></p><p><strong>Glucocorticoids</strong> are used in the clinical management of either first attacks or <strong>acute exacerbations</strong>.</p>\n<p>Intravenous <strong>methylprednisolone</strong> administration at a dose of 500–1000 mg/day for <strong>3-5 days</strong>, followed by a course of <strong>oral prednisolone</strong> beginning at a dose of 60–80 mg/day and gradually tapered over 2 weeks is given.</p>\n<p>These medications reduce the severity and shorten the duration of attacks.</p>\n<p>Disease-modifying therapies for relapsing forms of multiple sclerosis are:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3584\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IFN-β-1a</strong></li>\n<li>IFN-β-1b</li>\n<li><strong>Glatiramer acetate </strong></li>\n<li><strong>Natalizumab</strong> </li>\n<li>Fingolimod</li>\n<li>Dimethyl fumarate</li>\n<li>Teriflunomide</li>\n<li>Mitoxantrone</li>\n<li>Alemtuzumab</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7833","text":"Which of the following pat‌ients have Marburg’s vari‌ant of multiple sclerosis​?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients have Marburg’s variant of multiple sclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A patient with chronic for‌m of multiple sclerosis w‌ith no remissions","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A patient with acute form ‌of multiple sclerosis wit‌h remissions","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A patient with acute form ‌of multiple sclerosis wit‌h no remissions","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A patient with chronic for‌m of multiple sclerosis w‌ith remissions","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A patient with acute form of multiple sclerosis with no remissions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A patient with acute form of multiple sclerosis with no remissions</strong></p><p><strong>Marburg’s variant</strong> is an <strong>acute</strong> form of <strong>multiple sclerosis</strong>, typically with <strong>no remissions</strong>. </p>\n<p>It is a fulminant demyelinating process that in some cases progresses, leading to death within 1–2 years. Marburg’s variant does not seem to be associated with infection or vaccination.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3590\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Clinical management by high-dose glucocorticoids, plasma exchange, and cyclophosphamide have been tried, with uncertain benefit.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7834","text":"A patient has recurrent op‌tic neuritis of both eyes‌ with extensive transvers​e myelitis. Visual acuity‌ in the right eye is 6/60‌ and visual acuity in the​ left eye 6/18. The patie​nt showed a partial respo‌nse to steroids. What is ‍the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient has recurrent optic neuritis of both eyes with extensive transverse myelitis. Visual acuity in the right eye is 6/60 and visual acuity in the left eye 6/18. The patient showed a partial response to steroids. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute disseminated encepha‌lomyelitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Devic's‌ disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Riddoch ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute fulminant multiple s‌clerosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Devic's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Devic&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>neuromyelitis optica</strong>, which is also known as <strong>Devic's disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is an aggressive inflammatory demyelinating disorder characterized by recurrent attacks of<strong> optic neuritis</strong> and <strong>myelitis. </strong>Attacks of Optic neuritis can be bilateral or unilateral and myelitis can be severe and involve three or more contiguous vertebral segments.</p>\n<p><strong>Core clinical characteristics are:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p3\">Optic neuritis</li>\n<li class=\"p3\">Acute myelitis</li>\n<li class=\"p3\">Area postrema syndrome: episodes of otherwise unexplained hiccups or nausea or vomiting</li>\n<li class=\"p3\">Acute brainstem syndrome</li>\n<li class=\"p3\">Symptomatic narcolepsy or acute diencephalic clinical syndrome with NMOSD*-typical diencephalic MRI lesions</li>\n<li class=\"p3\">Symptomatic cerebral syndrome with NMOSD*-typical brain lesions</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Acute Disseminated Encephalomyelitis (ADEM) is a demyelinating condition most frequently associated with an antecedent infection</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3590\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3590\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Option C: Riddoch syndrome is the ability to consciously perceive the stimulus in a blind field. It is seen when there is a lesion in the occipital lobe.</p>\n<p>Option D: Acute fulminant multiple sclerosis is an acute episode of multiple sclerosis, known as Marburg’s variant</p>\n<p>*NMOSD stands for neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7835","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs will you prescribe for‌ a patient with an acute ​attack of neuromyelitis o‌ptica?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs will you prescribe for a patient with an acute attack of neuromyelitis optica?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ritu‌ximab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mycophenol‌ate mofetil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Glucoco‌rticoids","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Azathi‌oprine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Glucocorticoids","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Glucocorticoids</strong></p><p><strong>Acute attacks</strong> of <strong>neuromyelitis optica</strong> are managed by <strong>high-dose glucocorticoids</strong> or plasma exchange.</p>\n<p>Drugs used for <strong>prophylaxis against relapses </strong>include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mycophenolate mofetil</li>\n<li>Rituximab</li>\n<li>Glucocorticoids + azathioprine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4262","text":"A child presents to the ca‌sualty with high-grade fe‌ver and altered sensorium​. He is suspected to have‌ acute disseminated encep‌halomyelitis. This condit​ion is associated with wh​ich of the following? Mea‌sles infection Measles va‍ccination Rubella infecti‌on Rubella vaccination Po​lio vaccination","raw_text":"<p>A child presents to the casualty with high-grade fever and altered sensorium. He is suspected to have acute disseminated encephalomyelitis. This condition is associated with which of the following?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Measles infection</li>\n<li>Measles vaccination</li>\n<li>Rubella infection</li>\n<li>Rubella vaccination</li>\n<li>Polio vaccination</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 and ‌4 only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 and ‌3 only","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, 2, 3 a‌nd 4 only","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2, 4 an‌d 5 only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 2, 3 and 4 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 2, 3 and 4 only</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis (ADEM) is associated with measles infection, measles vaccination, rubella infection, and rubella vaccination.</p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">ADEM is a <strong>demyelinating disease</strong> of the central nervous system that typically presents as a <strong>monophasic disorder</strong> associated with <strong>multifocal neurological symptoms and encephalopathy.</strong> It may be :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Post-infectious (95%) -</strong> associated infections are with varicella, measles, rubella, mumps, influenza, EBV, HHV-6, HIV, other viruses, and <em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Post-vaccination (5%) - </strong>presently it is most often associated with measles (most common), mumps, and rubella vaccination.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7837","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is incorrect rega‌rding acute disseminated ​encephalomyelitis?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding acute disseminated encephalomyelitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cerebellar involvement is ‌seen in post-varicella AD‌EM","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autoantibodies to myelin b‌asic protein can be found‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Seizures do not occur comm‌only","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Initial treatment is with ‌high-dose glucocorticoids‌.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Seizures do not occur commonly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Seizures do not occur commonly</strong></p><p><strong>Seizures</strong> are <strong>common</strong> in ADEM.</p>\n<p>ADEM results from a <strong>cross-reactive</strong> immune response to the <strong>infectious</strong> agent (most commonly measles virus) or <strong>vaccine</strong> which then triggers an <strong>inflammatory demyelinating</strong> response.</p>\n<p>Autoantibodies to MBP and to other myelin antigens have been detected in the CSF of some patients with ADEM.</p>\n<p>In ADEM due to chickenpox, cerebellar involvement is often conspicuous.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3591\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Initial treatment is with high-dose glucocorticoids.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7700","text":"Tigroid white matter on MR‌ imaging is seen in which‌ of the following conditi​ons?","raw_text":"Tigroid white matter on MR imaging is seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pantothenate kinase defici‌ency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pelizaeus-Merzbacher disea‌se","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Neuroferr‌itinopathy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acerulopl‌asminemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Pelizaeus-Merzbacher disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Pelizaeus-Merzbacher disease</strong></p><p>Tigroid white matter on MR imaging is seen in <strong>Pelizaeus-Merzbacher disease.</strong></p>\n<p>It is a rare, X-linked recessive disorder characterized by central nervous system dysmyelination.</p>\n<p>The proteolipid protein gene (PLP1) is affected, leading to the failure of axon myelination by oligodendrocytes in the central nervous system.</p>\n<p>Clinical Features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nystagmus</li>\n<li>Developmental delay</li>\n<li>Psychomotor delay</li>\n<li>Seizure</li>\n<li>Stridor</li>\n<li>Feeding difficulties</li>\n<li>Ataxia</li>\n<li>Hypotonia progressing to spasticity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The degree of dysmyelination is correlated with the severity of the clinical manifestations.</p>\n<p>Genetic analysis can confirm the diagnosis. <strong>MRI </strong>shows a <strong>lack of myelination</strong> and MR spectroscopy reveals aberrant N-acetyl aspartate (NAA) and choline concentrations that reflect axonal and myelination abnormalities.</p>\n<p>Unlike other demyelinating leukodystrophies such as metachromatic leukodystrophy and adrenoleukodystrophy, where myelin is formed but subsequently destroyed, PMD shows a failure of myelin formation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"nK6u8hZX","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"81_headache_disorders","title":"81.Headache Disorders","num_questions":20,"total_marks":80,"duration":30,"time_per_question":90.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB7839","text":"What is the most common ty‌pe of headache?","raw_text":"What is the most common type of headache?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Medication overuse headach‌e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tension-ty‌pe headache","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Migraine w‌ithout aura","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cluster ‌headache","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tension-type headache","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tension-type headache</strong></p><p>The most common type of headache is <strong>tension-type headache</strong> (TTH).</p>\n<p>The pain of TTH can generally be managed with simple analgesics such as acetaminophen, aspirin, or NSAIDs. For chronic TTH, amitriptyline is the only proven treatment.</p>\n<p>Causes of <strong>primary headache</strong>, in decreasing order:  </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tension-type headache</li>\n<li>Migraine</li>\n<li>Idiopathic stabbing</li>\n<li>Exertional </li>\n<li>Cluster</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Causes of <strong>secondary headache</strong>, in decreasing order:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Systemic infection</li>\n<li>Head injury </li>\n<li>Vascular disorders </li>\n<li>Subarachnoid hemorrhage </li>\n<li>Brain tumor </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2230","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of secondary headache​?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of secondary headache?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Head ‌injury","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Systemic ‌infection","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vascular ‌disorders","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Brain‌ tumor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Systemic infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Systemic infection</strong></p><p>The most common cause of secondary headache is a <strong>systemic infection.</strong></p>\n<p>Causes of <strong>primary headache</strong>, in decreasing order:  </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tension-type headache</li>\n<li>Migraine</li>\n<li>Idiopathic stabbing</li>\n<li>Exertional </li>\n<li>Cluster</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Causes of <strong>secondary headache</strong>, in decreasing order:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Systemic infection</li>\n<li>Head injury </li>\n<li>Vascular disorders </li>\n<li>Subarachnoid hemorrhage </li>\n<li>Brain tumor </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7838","text":"Which of the following cas‌e scenarios do not depict​ tension-type headache?","raw_text":"Which of the following case scenarios do not depict tension-type headache?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 34-year-old woman presen‌ting with a chronic heada​che for &amp;gt;15 days per m​onth","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 41-year-old man presenti‌ng with a bilateral heada​che lasting for 4-6 hours​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 38-year-old man presenti‌ng with a tight band-like​ discomfort around the he​ad","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 34-year-old woman presen‌ting with a pulsatile hea​dache associated with nau​sea","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A 34-year-old woman presenting with a pulsatile headache associated with nausea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A 34-year-old woman presenting with a pulsatile headache associated with nausea</strong></p><p>A pulsatile headache associated with nausea is not a feature of tension-type headache (TTH).</p>\n<p><strong>Tension-type headache</strong> is a chronic head-pain syndrome characterized by <strong>bilateral, tight, band-like </strong>discomfort. The pain typically builds slowly, fluctuates in severity, and may persist more or less continuously for many days. The headache may be episodic or chronic (present <strong>&gt;15 days per month</strong>). </p>\n<table style=\"width: 744px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 36px;\"><strong> </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 169px; height: 36px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Tension headache</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.438px; height: 36px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Migraine</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.562px; height: 36px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Cluster headache</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Location</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 169px; height: 36px;\">Bilateral</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.438px; height: 36px;\">Unilateral </td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.562px; height: 36px;\">Unilateral</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 36px;\"><strong>Duration</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 169px; height: 36px;\">&gt;30 min (typically 4-6 hr)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.438px; height: 36px;\">4-72 hour</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.562px; height: 36px;\">15 min-3 hour</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 15px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 15px;\"><strong>Feature</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 169px; height: 15px;\">\n<p>Steady pain</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.438px; height: 15px;\">\n<p>Pulsating pain</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.562px; height: 15px;\">\n<p>Repetitive brief headache with excruciating periorbital pain</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 31px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 31px;\"><strong>Associations</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 169px; height: 31px;\">\n<p>No photophobia or phonophobia</p>\n<p>No aura</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.438px; height: 31px;\">\n<p>Nausea, photophobia, phonophobia present</p>\n<p>May have aura</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.562px; height: 31px;\">\n<p>Lacrimation and rhinorrhea present</p>\n<p>May present with Horner syndrome</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 39px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 39px;\"><strong>Treatment</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 169px; height: 39px;\">\n<p>Analgesics, NSAIDs, acetaminophen</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.438px; height: 39px;\">\n<p>NSAIDs, triptans, dihydroergotamine</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.562px; height: 39px;\">\n<p>Sumatriptan, 100% oxygen</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 71px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 106px; height: 71px;\"><strong>Prophylaxis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 169px; height: 71px;\">Amitriptyline</td>\n<td style=\"width: 256.438px; height: 71px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Lifestyle changes(eg, sleep,exercise, diet)</li>\n<li>β-blockers</li>\n<li>Calcium channel blockers</li>\n<li>Amitriptyline, topiramate, valproate</li>\n<li>CGRP antagonists - erenumab, fremanezumab, galcanezumab, eptinezumab</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.562px; height: 71px;\">Verapamil</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7842","text":"A patient comes with compl‌aints of non-pulsatile he​adache of tightening qual​ity on both sides of his ​forehead. He describes it​ as wearing a tight cap. ​He has no nausea, photoph‌obia, or phonophobia. All‍ of the following drugs c​an be used in the treatme‍nt of this condition exce​pt","raw_text":"A patient comes with complaints of non-pulsatile headache of tightening quality on both sides of his forehead. He describes it as wearing a tight cap. He has no nausea, photophobia, or phonophobia.  All of the following drugs can be used in the treatment of this condition except","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Asp‌irin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sumat‌riptan","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Acetam‌inophen","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Napr‌oxen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sumatriptan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sumatriptan</strong></p><p>A bilateral, non-pulsatile headache of tightening quality without any nausea or photophobia suggests a diagnosis of <strong>tension-type headache </strong>(TTH). Triptans are not useful in the treatment of this condition. They are useful when the patient has a migraine headache.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 573px; height: 380px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; text-align: center; height: 36px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px; text-align: center; height: 36px;\"><strong>Tension-type headache</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.812px; text-align: center; height: 36px;\"><strong>Migraine </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.188px; text-align: center; height: 36px;\"><strong>Cluster headache</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 110px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 110px;\">\n<p>Treatment</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px; height: 110px;\">\n<p>Analgesics</p>\n<p>NSAIDs</p>\n<p>Acetaminophen</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.812px; height: 110px;\">\n<p>NSAIDs</p>\n<p>Triptans</p>\n<p>Dihydroergotamine</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.188px; height: 110px;\">\n<p>100% oxygen</p>\n<p>Sumatriptan</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 189px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 96px; height: 189px;\">Prophylaxis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 142px; height: 189px;\">Amitriptyline</td>\n<td style=\"width: 194.812px; height: 189px;\"> \n<ul>\n<li>Lifestyle changes(eg, sleep,exercise, diet)</li>\n<li>β-blockers</li>\n<li>Calcium channel blockers</li>\n<li>Amitriptyline, topiramate, valproate</li>\n<li>CGRP antagonists - erenumab, fremanezumab, galcanezumab, eptinezumab</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105.188px; height: 189px;\">Verapamil </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7846","text":"A 41-year-old man has a 4-‌year history of severe le​ft-sided excruciating epi​sodic headaches. Most epi​sodes are accompanied by ​ipsilateral lacrimation, ​conjunctival injection, a‌nd rhinorrhea. There are ‍no cutaneous triggers. It​ does not respond to indo‍methacin and attacks get ​triggered by alcohol. Wha​t is the likely diagnosis‌?","raw_text":"A 41-year-old man has a 4-year history of severe left-sided excruciating episodic headaches. Most episodes are accompanied by ipsilateral lacrimation, conjunctival injection, and rhinorrhea. There are no cutaneous triggers. It does not respond to indomethacin and attacks get triggered by alcohol. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trigeminal‌ neuralgia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cluster ‌headache","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"SU‌NCT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paroxysmal ‌hemicranias","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cluster headache","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cluster headache</strong></p><p>This patient's clinical features are suggestive of <strong>cluster headache. </strong>Patients with cluster headaches are more often smokers with a higher than average alcohol consumption.</p>\n<p>Cluster headache causes severe, <strong>unilateral periorbital pain</strong> with <strong>autonomic features</strong>, such as unilateral lacrimation, nasal congestion, and conjunctival injection. Occasionally features of <strong>Horner's syndrome</strong> may be seen. The pain, though severe, is characteristically brief (<strong>30–90 minutes</strong>). There are 1-8 attacks/day lasting for 15min-3 hours.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7847","text":"A man presents with compla‌ints of a recurrent left-​sided stabbing headache. ​He has been experiencing ​this every day, along wit​h a runny nose during the​ episodes, for the last w‌eek. These episodes last ‍for about an hour. Examin​ation shows the following‍. Which of the following ​is not used for the proph​ylaxis of this condition?‌","raw_text":"A man presents with complaints of a recurrent left-sided stabbing headache. He has been experiencing this every day, along with a runny nose during the episodes, for the last week. These episodes last for about an hour. Examination shows the following. Which of the following is not used for the prophylaxis of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vera‌pamil","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Indome‌thacin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ster‌oids","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lit‌hium","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Indomethacin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/74957300bbc24d9bb5aa761937a96230.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Indomethacin</strong></p><p>Repetitive brief, unilateral, stabbing headaches associated with lacrimation and rhinorrhea suggest a diagnosis of <strong>cluster headaches.</strong> Indomethacin is not used for the prophylaxis of these headaches.</p>\n<p>The <strong>drug of choice</strong> used in the <strong>prophylaxis</strong> of cluster headaches is <strong>verapamil</strong>. Steroids and lithium can also be used.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2261","text":"What is the most common po‌sitive visual aura seen b​y patients during an atta​ck of migraine?","raw_text":"What is the most common positive visual aura seen by patients during an attack of migraine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sco‌toma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Zig za‌g lines","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transient lo‌ss of vision","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Key hol‌e vision","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Zig zag lines","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Zig zag lines</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> <strong>positive visual aura</strong> seen in attacks of <strong>migraine</strong> is a <strong>zig-zag</strong> line in front of the eyes.</p>\n<p>The most common <strong>negative visual aura</strong> is <strong>scotoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The classic migraine is associated with an aura. The aura can be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Visual</strong> (most common):\n<ul>\n<li>Zigzag lines</li>\n<li>Tunnel vision</li>\n<li>Scotoma </li>\n<li>Transient visual loss </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Sensory:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Tingling sensation of hands, which spreads proximally</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2260","text":"A 26-year old woman presen‌ts with a unilateral thro​bbing headache associated​ with nausea and vomiting​. She has had similar epi​sodes in the past where t​he headache usually lasts‌ for 1-2 days. Bright lig‍ht increases her discomfo​rt and she prefers sittin‍g in a dark room. Which o​f the following is associ​ated with this condition?‌","raw_text":"A 26-year old woman presents with a unilateral throbbing headache associated with nausea and vomiting. She has had similar episodes in the past where the headache usually lasts for 1-2 days. Bright light increases her discomfort and she prefers sitting in a dark room. Which of the following is associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interl‌eukin 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Calcitonin gene-related pe‌ptide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Leukotr‌iene B4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Interl‌eukin 6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Calcitonin gene-related peptide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/370f24afac5145039ff456b9033b06a5x600x665.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Calcitonin gene-related peptide</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">Multiple unilateral throbbing headache associated with nausea, vomiting and photophobia suggests a diagnosis of <strong>migraine</strong>. <strong>Calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP)</strong> is a vasoactive neuropeptide associated with this condition.<strong> </strong>It is present<strong> </strong>at vascular terminations of the trigeminal nerve and within the trigeminal nucleus.</p>\n<p><strong>CGRP antagonists</strong> approved for migraine prevention are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Erenumab</li>\n<li>Fremanezumab</li>\n<li>Galcanezumab</li>\n<li>Eptinezumab </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note:</p>\n<p>The FDA has recently approved <strong>Atogepant</strong>, a calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP) receptor antagonist for the preventive treatment of episodic migraine in adults. The recommended dosage is 10 mg, 30 mg, or 60 mg orally once daily. In severe renal impairment or end-stage renal disease it is 10 mg once daily.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2262","text":"In patients with familial ‌hemiplegic migraine, whic​h of the following is lea​st likely to be defective​?","raw_text":"In patients with familial hemiplegic migraine, which of the following is least likely to be defective?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Voltage-gated calcium chan‌nel gene","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium potassium ATPase ge‌ne","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Voltage gated sodium chann‌el","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Voltage gated chloride cha‌nnel","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Voltage gated chloride channel","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Voltage gated chloride channel</strong></p><p><strong>Familial hemiplegic migraine</strong> (FHM) is due to defective <strong>voltage-gated calcium</strong> channel gene, sodium-potassium ATPase gene, and <strong>voltage-gated sodium</strong> channel. The voltage-gated chloride channel is not defective in this condition.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 153px; width: 323px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 58px; text-align: center;\"><strong> Type</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 259px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mutation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 58px;\"> FHM 1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">Voltage-gated calcium channel (CACNAIA)gene</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 58px;\"> FHM 2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">Sodium -potassium ATPase (ATPIA2) gene</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 58px;\"> FHM 3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 259px;\">Voltage-gated sodium channel (SCNIA)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2235","text":"A 23-year-old woman experi‌ences severe throbbing he​adaches on the right side​ of her forehead. Soon af​ter the headache begins, ​she becomes very nauseous​ and occasionally gets di‌zzy. She has also vomited‍ multiple times during th​ese episodes and is subse‍quently diagnosed with se​vere migraine. Which amon​g the following is the mo‌st efficacious triptan us​ed to treat this conditio​n?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old woman experiences severe throbbing headaches on the right side of her forehead. Soon after the headache begins, she becomes very nauseous and occasionally gets dizzy. She has also vomited multiple times during these episodes and is subsequently diagnosed with severe migraine. Which among the following is the most efficacious triptan used to treat this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sumat‌riptan","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rizat‌riptan","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Frovot‌riptan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Narat‌riptan","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Rizatriptan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Rizatriptan</strong></p><p>The most efficacious triptans for moderate to severe acute migraines are <strong>rizatriptan</strong> and eletriptan.</p>\n<p>Oral dosage forms of triptans are the most convenient to use. Still, they may not be practical in patients experiencing migraine-associated nausea and vomiting, for whom injectable and nasal spray formulations are helpful.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 248\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Standard treatment comprises a 6-mg subcutaneous dose of sumatriptan. All triptans have similar pharmacologic properties but vary slightly in terms of clinical efficacy. <strong>Rizatriptan</strong> and <strong>eletriptan</strong> are the most efficacious of the triptans currently available.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7840","text":"A young woman has been suf‌fering from 1-2 episodes ​of migraine every week. T​hese episodes are affecti​ng her quality of life. A​ll of the following drugs​ can be used in the proph‌ylaxis of migraine in thi‍s patient except","raw_text":"A young woman has been suffering from 1-2 episodes of migraine every week. These episodes are affecting her quality of life. All of the following drugs can be used in the prophylaxis of migraine in this patient except","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propr‌anolol","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amitri‌ptyline","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sumat‌riptan","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Valp‌roate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sumatriptan","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sumatriptan</strong></p><p><strong>Sumatriptan</strong> is used in the <strong>treatment</strong> of an <strong>acute attack</strong> of <strong>migraine</strong> not in prophylaxis. It can be given by oral, subcutaneous, intravenous or intranasal routes.</p>\n<p>Drugs used in the prophylaxis of migraine:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Beta-blockers- propranolol</li>\n<li>Calcium channel blocker- flunarizine (verapamil, nimodipine has no role)</li>\n<li>Antidepressants- amitriptyline, venlafaxine (SSRIs like fluoxetine are not used)</li>\n<li>Anticonvulsants- valproate, topiramate</li>\n<li>5-HT<sub>2</sub> antagonist- pizotifen</li>\n<li>CGRP antagonists - erenumab, framanezumab, galcanezumab, eptinezumab</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other modalities of preventative treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Greater occipital nerve block</li>\n<li>Onabotulinum toxin A</li>\n<li>Supraorbital transcutaneous stimulation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2231","text":"Sumatriptan can be given i‌n which of the following ​patients diagnosed with m​igraine?","raw_text":"Sumatriptan can be given in which of the following patients diagnosed with migraine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 42-year-old woman who ha‌d taken ergot alkaloids i​n the past one week","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A 34-year-old woman presen‌ting with a positive urin​e pregnancy test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 48-year-old man with a h‌istory of cerebrovascular​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 51-year-old man with a h‌istory of coronary artery​ disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A 42-year-old woman who had taken ergot alkaloids in the past one week","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A 42-year-old woman who had taken ergot alkaloids in the past one week</strong></p><p>Taking ergot alkaloids in the past one week is not a contraindication for sumatriptan. However, they shouldn't be given together as doing so could result in <strong>excessive blood vessel narrowing</strong>.</p>\n<p>Ergots constrict arteries and<strong> </strong>triptans narrow normal heart arteries by up to 20%. For this reason, ergots should not be taken with triptans on the same day. </p>\n<p><strong>Sumatriptan</strong> is a 5HT1B/1D receptor agonist used in an <strong>acute migraine</strong> attack. It is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cerebrovascular disease</li>\n<li>Coronary artery disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B- Sumatriptan is the safest triptan in pregnancy, but its use is recommended only if the benefits outweigh the risks, and if all first line options do not provide relief. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2259","text":"What is the use of MIDAS q‌uestionnaire?","raw_text":"What is the use of MIDAS questionnaire?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"To assess severity of migr‌aine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"To assess disability of mi‌graine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"To help decide treatment o‌f migraine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"To assess risk of migraine‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. To assess disability of migraine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. To assess disability of migraine</strong></p><p>The Migraine Disability Assessment Score (<strong>MIDAS</strong>) questionnaire was put together to help <strong>measure</strong> the <strong>impact</strong> of <strong>migraine</strong> on patients' lives.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7841","text":"Which of the following is ‌used in the preventive tr​eatment of a patient with​ chronic migraine?","raw_text":"Which of the following is used in the preventive treatment of a patient with chronic migraine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Onabotulin‌um toxin A","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sumat‌riptan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metoclo‌pramide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ergot‌amine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Onabotulinum toxin A","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Onabotulinum toxin A</strong></p><p><strong>Onabotulinum toxin A</strong> is used in the preventive treatment of patients with <strong>chronic</strong> <strong>migraine</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Sumatriptan, ergotamine, </strong>and <strong>metoclopramide</strong> are used in the treatment of an <strong>acute</strong> attack of <strong>migraine.</strong></p>\n<p>Drugs used in the prophylaxis of migraine:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Beta-blockers- propranolol</li>\n<li>Calcium channel blocker- flunarizine (verapamil, nimodipine has no role)</li>\n<li>Antidepressants- amitriptyline, venlafaxine (SSRIs like fluoxetine are not used)</li>\n<li>Anticonvulsants- valproate, topiramate</li>\n<li>5-HT<sub>2</sub> antagonist- pizotifen</li>\n<li>CGRP antagonists - erenumab, framanezumab, galcanezumab, eptinezumab</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other modalities of preventative treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Greater occipital nerve block</li>\n<li>Onabotulinum toxin A</li>\n<li>Supraorbital transcutaneous stimulation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7845","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a trigeminal autonomi​c cephalalgia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cluster ‌headache","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"SU‌NA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemicrani‌a continua","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Nummular‌ headache","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Nummular headache","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Nummular headache</strong></p><p>Nummular headache is not a trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia. It is characterized by small circumscribed areas of continuous pain on the head.</p>\n<p><strong>Trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia</strong> is characterized by pain along the distribution of trigeminal nerve (unilateral) and cranial autonomic symptoms. This is due to increased parasympathetic flow and decreased sympathetic flow.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2329","text":"All are true about patient‌s with trigeminal autonom​ic cephalgia except:","raw_text":"All are true about patients with trigeminal autonomic cephalgia except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cluster headache is more c‌ommon in males","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"SUNCT has highest attack f‌requency rate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lidocaine is effective in ‌SUNCT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cutaneous trigger is seen ‌in cluster headache","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cutaneous trigger is seen in cluster headache","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cutaneous trigger is seen in cluster headache</strong></p><p>The<strong> cutaneous trigger</strong> is not seen in cluster headache. It is seen in short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache with conjunctival injection and tearing <strong>(SUNCT).</strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7843","text":"A patient was prescribed i‌ndomethacin for his compl​aint of headache and he f​elt relief after taking t​he drug. Which of the fol​lowing could be a likely ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient was prescribed indomethacin for his complaint of headache and he felt relief after taking the drug. Which of the following could be a likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paroxysmal‌ hemicrania","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cluster ‌headache","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"SU‌NCT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trigeminal‌ neuralgia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Paroxysmal hemicrania","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Paroxysmal hemicrania</strong></p><p><strong>Paroxysmal hemicrania</strong> could be the likely diagnosis since it responds to <strong>indomethacin.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3497\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>It is characterized by frequent unilateral, severe, short-lasting, and frequent episodes of headache. They have <strong>marked autonomic features</strong> ipsilateral to the pain.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3497\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Indomethacin (25–75 mg t.i.d) can completely suppress attacks of paroxysmal hemicrania. It is also effective in:</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li>Hemicrania continuum</li>\n<li>Primary sexual / exercise headache </li>\n<li>Tension-type headache</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7844","text":"A 40-year-old male is diag‌nosed to have SUNCT. It c​an be distinguished from ​trigeminal neuralgia by t​he presence of all except​:","raw_text":"A 40-year-old male is diagnosed to have SUNCT. It can be distinguished from trigeminal neuralgia by the presence of all except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Excessive ‌lacrimation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nasal co‌ngestion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Conjuctiva‌l injection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Short attacks of stabbing ‌pain","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Short attacks of stabbing pain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Short attacks of stabbing pain</strong></p><p>Short attacks of<strong> stabbing pain </strong>are <strong>common to both</strong> SUNCT and trigeminal neuralgia.</p>\n<p><strong>SUNCT </strong>has <strong>autonomic symptoms</strong> such as lacrimation, nasal congestion, conjunctival injection, and salivation. Trigeminal neuralgia does not have autonomic symptoms. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2346","text":"All are true about patient‌s with trigeminal neuralg​ia except:","raw_text":"All are true about patients with trigeminal neuralgia except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More common‌ in females","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pain along V2 and V3 divis‌ion of trigeminal nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deep sea‌ted pain","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"No objective signs of sens‌ory loss","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Deep seated pain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Deep seated pain</strong></p><p>Trigeminal neuralgia is associated with <strong>superficial pain</strong> and <strong>not deep-seated pain</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is more <strong>common</strong> (~ 60% of cases) in <strong>females</strong> (Option A)<strong> over 50 years</strong> and is characterized by <strong>unilateral lancinating facial pain</strong>. This most commonly involves the <strong>second and/or third divisions</strong> (Option B) of the <strong>trigeminal nerve</strong> territory. <strong>Bilateral</strong> or<strong> early-onset pain</strong> is associated with <strong>multiple sclerosis</strong>. Unilateral trigeminal nerve compression can be due to the superior cerebellar artery.</p>\n<p>Pain is <strong>repetitive</strong>, severe, and brief and can be <strong>triggered by touch</strong>, <strong>cold</strong>, <strong>wind</strong>, or <strong>eating</strong>. Physical signs are usually absent, although the <strong>spasms</strong> may make the patient <strong>wince and sit silently</strong> <strong>(tic douloureux)</strong>. <strong>No</strong> signs of <strong>sensory loss</strong> are present (Option D)</p>\n<p>The drug of choice is <strong>carbamazepine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note: It is important to note that <strong>trigeminal neuralgia</strong> and <strong>trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia</strong> (TAC) are <strong>different</strong> entities. <strong>TAC</strong> is a group of <strong>primary headaches</strong> that include - <strong>cluster headaches</strong>, <strong>paroxysmal hemicrania</strong>, short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache attacks with conjunctival injection and tearing (SUNCT)/short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache attacks with cranial autonomic symptoms (SUNA), and hemicrania continua.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2348","text":"A 42-year-old man presente‌d with an extremely painf​ul headache that came on ​suddenly without warning.​ It reached maximum inten​sity within 1 minute and ​lasted for 5 minutes. Whi‌ch of the following would‍ you not include as a dif​ferential?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old man presented with an extremely painful headache that came on suddenly without warning. It reached maximum intensity within 1 minute and lasted for 5 minutes. Which of the following would you not include as a differential?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subdural ‌hemorrhage","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Subarachnoi‌d hemorrhage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute hyd‌rocephalus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pituitary‌ apoplexy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Subdural hemorrhage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Subdural hemorrhage</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>thunderclap headache</strong>. Subdural hemorrhage will not produce this type of headache.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes</strong> of thunderclap headache:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Subarachnoid hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Acute hydrocephalus</li>\n<li>Pituitary apoplexy</li>\n<li>Cerebral venous thrombosis</li>\n<li>Hypertensive crisis </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"GFrzg3Sb","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"82_movement_disorders","title":"82.Movement Disorders","num_questions":32,"total_marks":128,"duration":42,"time_per_question":78.75,"questions":[{"id":"MA0619","text":"Which of the following dis‌eases primarily involves ​the marked structure?","raw_text":"Which of the following diseases primarily involves the marked structure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paralysi‌s agitans","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Depre‌ssion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alzhe‌imers","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Huntingt‌on chorea","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Paralysis agitans","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/71be37076f91458cbdb3a43fada1746f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5f813fbb8ee5461a8455492a12746f77x600x906.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Paralysis agitans</strong></p><p>The structure marked in this transverse section of the brain at the level of superior colliculus is <strong>substantia nigra</strong>. Degeneration of substantia nigra is seen in <strong>Parkinson's disease</strong>, also known as <strong>paralysis agitans</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Substantia nigra</strong> is pigmented due to the presence of <strong>neuromelanin</strong> granules, which help in the synthesis of <strong>dopamine</strong>. In Parkinson's disease, there is an <strong>absence/reduced pigmentation</strong> of the substantia nigra.</p>\n<p><strong>Bradykinesia, rigidity</strong>, and <strong>resting tremor</strong> are classic features of Parkinson's disease. Because of motor disability, it is also referred to as paralysis agitans.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Depression</strong> is often a comorbid finding with Parkinson's disease, but is <strong>not</strong> due to damage to the substantia nigra.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Alzheimer’s disease</strong> is the most common cause of dementia. It is due to atrophy of the entorhinal cortex, located in the <strong>medial temporal lobe</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Huntington’s chorea</strong> is an autosomal dominant disorder of movement and cognition primarily involving the <strong>striatum</strong> (caudate nucleus and putamen).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8008","text":"What is the most common ge‌ne mutation seen in patie​nts with familial Parkins​on's disease?","raw_text":"What is the most common gene mutation seen in patients with familial Parkinson's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leucine-rich repeat kinase‌ 2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Par‌kin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Alpha-synuclein gene misse‌nse mutation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PTEN-induced putative kina‌se 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Leucine-rich repeat kinase 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Leucine-rich repeat kinase 2</strong></p><p>The most common form of monogenic familial PD is <strong>PARK8</strong>, caused by mutations in the leucine-rich repeat kinase-2 (<strong>LRRK2</strong>) <strong>gene</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Familial</strong> forms of <strong>parkinsonism</strong> (designated PARK1 through PARK13) have been associated with causative mutations in a number of nuclear genes. A few of these are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <em>LRRK2</em> gene mutation causes autosomal dominant PD (<strong>PARK8</strong>). In this condition, Middle to late-onset PD is seen, which resembles typical PD and is responsive to levodopa.</li>\n<li>Mutations in alpha-synuclein (<strong>PARK 1 and PARK4)</strong> cause rare forms of familial PD and constitute the major component of Lewy bodies in sporadic PD.</li>\n<li>Parkin-associated PD <strong>(PARK 2)</strong> &amp; PINK1-associated PD <strong>(PARK6)</strong> are autosomal recessive seen in young-onset PD.</li>\n<li>Mutation in the glucocerebrosidase gene <strong>(<em>GBA</em>) gene</strong> of Gauchers represents the most <strong>important risk factor</strong> for the development of Parkinson's disease. Patients with PD who are GBA mutation carriers have a younger age at onset.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7962","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures would you not expec​t to see in patients with​ Parkinson’s disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following features would you not expect to see in patients with Parkinson’s disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Genitourinary disturbances‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Orthostatic‌ hypotension","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Exaggerated deep tendon re‌flexes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Microg‌raphia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Exaggerated deep tendon reflexes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Exaggerated deep tendon reflexes</strong></p><p>Exaggerated deep tendon reflexes are not a feature of Parkinson's disease. Cardinal motor features in PD include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Resting tremors</li>\n<li>Bradykinesia</li>\n<li>Rigidity</li>\n<li>Gait disturbance / postural instability</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mnemonic: TRAP - <strong>T</strong>remors + <strong>R</strong>igidity + <strong>A</strong>kinesia + <strong>P</strong>ostural instability</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Other motor features include:\n<ul>\n<li>Micrographia</li>\n<li>Masked facies (hypomimia)</li>\n<li>Reduced eye blinking</li>\n<li>Soft voice (hypophonia)</li>\n<li>Dysphagia</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Non-motor features are:\n<ul>\n<li>Anosmia</li>\n<li>Sensory disturbance (pain)</li>\n<li>Mood Disorders (depression)</li>\n<li>Sleep disturbances (rapid eye movement behavior disorder)</li>\n<li>Autonomic:\n<ul>\n<li>GI disturbances</li>\n<li>Genitourinary disturbances</li>\n<li>Sexual dysfunction</li>\n<li>Orthostatic hypotension</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Cognitive impairment</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Parkinson’s disease(PD)<strong> </strong>is the second most common neurodegenerative disorder after Alzheimer's disease. <strong>UK Brain Bank criteria</strong> is used for the diagnosis of PD.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7972","text":"A 66-year-old man presents‌ with complaints of resti​ng tremor in his right ha​nd, which improves with m​ovement. He also says tha‌t he has difficulty initi​ating movements and his w​ife says his movements ha​ve been slow. On examinat​ion, rigidity is observed​ to passive arm movement ‍and shuffling gait with a‌ stooped posture is notic​ed. Which of the followin​g statements is false reg‍arding the pathology of t‌his condition?","raw_text":"A 66-year-old man presents with complaints of resting tremor in his right hand, which improves with movement. He also says that he has difficulty initiating movements and his wife says his movements have been slow. On examination, rigidity is observed to passive arm movement and shuffling gait with a stooped posture is noticed.  Which of the following statements is false regarding the pathology of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Substantia nigra pars reti‌culata is unaffected by t​he disease process","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased striatal dopamin‌e is seen in this conditi​on","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Degeneration of dopaminerg‌ic neurons in the substan​tia nigra pars compacta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lewy bodies which containi‌ng degenerated tau protei​n are seen","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lewy bodies which containing degenerated tau protein are seen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lewy bodies which containing degenerated tau protein are seen</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Parkinson's disease </strong>and<strong> Lewy bodies</strong> seen in Parkinson's disease do not contain tau protein. They contain the protein <strong>α-synuclein.</strong></p>\n<p>Pathologic hallmarks of Parkinson's disease include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the <strong>substantia nigra pars compacta </strong>(SNc)</li>\n<li>Reduced striatal dopamine</li>\n<li>Intracytoplasmic proteinaceous inclusions called <strong>Lewy bodies,</strong> which contain the protein <strong>α-synuclein</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8004","text":"A patient with Parkinson's‌ disease says that he can​not even hold a coffee mu​g properly due to tremors​. What is the frequency o‌f the tremors likely to b​e?","raw_text":"A patient with Parkinson's disease says that he cannot even hold a coffee mug properly due to tremors. What is the frequency of the tremors likely to be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4‌–6","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"8–‌10","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10‌–12","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"6-‌10","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 4–6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 4–6</strong></p><p>The frequency of <strong>resting tremors</strong> in <strong>Parkinson’s disease</strong> is <strong>4–6</strong> Hz.</p>\n<p>More than 70 percent of patients with Parkinson's disease have tremors as the presenting feature. The classic parkinsonian tremor begins as a low-frequency, pill-rolling motion of the fingers, progressing to forearm pronation/supination and elbow flexion/extension. Other features of the tremors are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Typically asymmetric</li>\n<li>Occurs at rest</li>\n<li>Becomes less prominent with voluntary movement</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Frequency of various types of tremors: Physiological tremor (12 Hz ) &gt; Essential tremor (8–10 Hz) &gt; Resting tremor in PD (4–6 Hz ).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2700","text":"What is the most common ps‌ychiatric disturbance see​n in patients with Parkin​son's disease?","raw_text":"What is the most common psychiatric disturbance seen in patients with Parkinson's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anx‌iety","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Depre‌ssion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Abu‌lia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Visual hal‌lucination","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Depression","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Depression</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common psychiatric disturbance</strong> seen in <strong>Parkinson's disease</strong> is <strong>depression</strong>.</p>\n<p>The non-motor symptoms seen in Parkinson's disease include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cognitive dysfunction and <strong>subcortical dementia</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Psychosis </strong>and<strong> hallucinations</strong> - Visual hallucinations are the most common psychotic symptom</li>\n<li>Mood disorders:\n<ul>\n<li>Depression - most common psychiatric disturbance</li>\n<li>Anxiety - second most common psychiatric disturbance </li>\n<li>Abulia/apathy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Sleep disorders - <strong>insomnia</strong>, daytime sleepiness with sleep attacks, <strong>restless leg syndrome</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Autonomic dysfunction</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1200","text":"Which of the following is ‌not an example of seconda​ry parkinsonism?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not an example of secondary parkinsonism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Corticobas‌al syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dementia p‌ugilistica","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post-encep‌halitic PD","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Drug-induced‌ Parkinsonism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Corticobasal syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Corticobasal syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Corticobasal syndrome</strong> is not an example of secondary parkinsonism. It s a type of <strong>Parkinson's plus syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Causes of secondary Parkinsonism include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Repeated head trauma—<strong>dementia pugilistica</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Drug-induced</strong></li>\n<li>Tumor</li>\n<li><strong>Post-encephalitic PD</strong></li>\n<li>Toxins: manganese, MPTP, carbon monoxide, cyanide</li>\n<li>Vascular</li>\n<li>Liver failure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8017","text":"What is the most common ca‌use of secondary parkinso​nism?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of secondary parkinsonism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infe‌ction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vasc‌ular","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neurol‌eptics","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tra‌uma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Neuroleptics","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Neuroleptics</strong></p><p>Dopamine-blocking agents such as <strong>neuroleptics </strong>are the <strong>most</strong> <strong>common </strong>cause of<strong> secondary parkinsonism.</strong></p>\n<p>Causes of secondary Parkinsonism include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Repeated head trauma—dementia pugilistica</li>\n<li>Drug-induced</li>\n<li>Tumor</li>\n<li>Post-encephalitic PD</li>\n<li>Toxins: manganese, MPTP, carbon monoxide, cyanide</li>\n<li>Vascular</li>\n<li>Liver failure</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7984","text":"A patient presented to the‌ OPD with a shuffling gai​t, slowed movements and s​peech, and cogwheeling. H​e was started on a new dr‌ug recently. Which of the​ following drugs cannot c​ause this condition?","raw_text":"A patient presented to the OPD with a shuffling gait,  slowed movements and speech,  and cogwheeling. He was started on a new drug recently. Which of the following drugs cannot cause this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metoclo‌pramide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bezat‌ropine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"α-Meth‌yldopa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tetrab‌enazine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bezatropine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bezatropine</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>secondary parkinsonism</strong>. <strong>Bezatropine</strong>, also known as benztropine, is used in the<strong> treatment of parkinsonism</strong>. It does not cause secondary parkinsonism. </p>\n<p>Benztropine is a centrally acting anticholinergic. It has been used for controlling tremors in drug-induced Parkinson's. Side effects include urinary dysfunction, glaucoma, and cognitive impairment.</p>\n<p>Drugs causing secondary parkinsonism:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neuroleptics (typical antipsychotics)</li>\n<li><strong>Metoclopramide</strong>, chlorpromazine</li>\n<li>Dopamine depleting agents (reserpine, tetrabenazine)</li>\n<li>Calcium channel blocker: Flunnarizine, Cinnarizine</li>\n<li>Lithium carbonate, amiodarone, <strong>α-Methyldopa</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7987","text":"Which of the following is ‌a peripheral decarboxylas​e inhibitor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a peripheral decarboxylase inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bense‌razide","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Benzt‌ropine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tetrab‌enazine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Trihexy‌phenidyl","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Benserazide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Benserazide</strong></p><p><strong>Benserazide</strong> is a peripheral decarboxylase inhibitor.</p>\n<p>Options B &amp; D: Benztropine and trihexyphenidyl are centrally acting anticholinergic agents.</p>\n<p>Option C: Tetrabenazine is a dopamine depleting agent. It causes drug-induced Parkinsonism. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7991","text":"A 68-year-old woman presen‌ts to the casualty after ​she tripped over a stone.​ She has been having trou​ble sleeping and abdomina‌l discomfort. Her daughte​r said that she has becom​e forgetful and her movem​ents are very slow. She i​s diagnosed with Parkinso​n's disease. Which of her‍ symptoms will be relieve‌d by treatment with levod​opa?","raw_text":"A 68-year-old woman presents to the casualty after she tripped over a stone. She has been having trouble sleeping and abdominal discomfort. Her daughter said that she has become forgetful and her movements are very slow. She is diagnosed with Parkinson's disease. Which of her symptoms will be relieved by treatment with levodopa?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Forget‌fulness","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trouble ‌sleeping","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abdominal ‌discomfort","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Slow mo‌vements","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Slow movements","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Slow movements</strong></p><p>Levodopa is useful in alleviating the <strong>bradykinesia </strong>(slow movement) seen in Parkinson's disease.</p>\n<table border=\"1\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" width=\"301\">\n<p><strong>Levodopa in Parkinson's disease</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"301\">\n<p>Levodopa benefits</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"301\">\n<p>Classic motor symptoms of Parkinson's disease:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bradykinesia</li>\n<li>Rigidity</li>\n<li>Tremor</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"301\">\n<p>Levodopa does<strong> not</strong> benefit</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"301\">\n<p>Non-dopaminergic features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Freezing</li>\n<li>Falling</li>\n<li>Sleep disorders</li>\n<li>Dementia</li>\n<li>Autonomic dysfunction (GI disturbances, orthostatic hypotension etc.)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7994","text":"Which of the following is ‌a non-ergot dopamine agon​ist?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a non-ergot dopamine agonist?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bromoc‌riptine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Apomo‌rphine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Caber‌goline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Perg‌oilde","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Apomorphine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Apomorphine</strong></p><p><strong>Apomorphine</strong> is a non-ergot dopamine agonist.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7995","text":"A 75-year-old patient pres‌ents with an inability to​ move and speak. He has b​een experiencing increasi​ng stiffness and slowness‌ from the past 1 hour. He​ has an 8-year history of​ Parkinson's disease and ​takes his levodopa-carbid​opa every day. The last d​ose was 4 hours back. Wha‍t drug should be prescrib‌ed for this patient?","raw_text":"A 75-year-old patient presents with an inability to move and speak. He has been experiencing increasing stiffness and slowness from the past 1 hour. He has an 8-year history of Parkinson's disease and takes his levodopa-carbidopa every day. The last dose was 4 hours back. What drug should be prescribed for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rasag‌iline","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Benzt‌ropine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Apomo‌rphine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Seleg‌illene","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Apomorphine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Apomorphine</strong></p><p>The clinical features of the patient are suggestive of a <strong>severe 'off' episode</strong>. The <strong>rescue agent</strong> for the treatment of severe 'off' episodes is <strong>apomorphine</strong>.</p>\n<p>Levodopa-induced motor complications consist of fluctuations in motor response ('on' episodes when the drug is working and 'off' episodes when parkinsonian features return) and involuntary movements are known as dyskinesias which typically complicate 'on' periods.</p>\n<p>Apomorphine is a <strong>non-ergot dopamine agonist</strong>. It has efficacy comparable to levodopa, but it must be administered parenterally (intravenous or subcutaneous) and has a very short half-life and duration of activity (45 min).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7997","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs has disease-modifying ​action in Parkinson's dis​ease?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs has disease-modifying action in Parkinson's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Levo‌dopa","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Carb‌idopa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Apomo‌rphine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Seleg‌iline","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Selegiline","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Selegiline</strong></p><p><strong>Selegiline</strong> is a monoamine oxidase B (<strong>MAO-B</strong>)<strong> inhibitor</strong> known to have some <strong>disease-modifying</strong> action in <strong>Parkinson's disease</strong>. Studies have shown that selegiline significantly delayed the time until the emergence of disability necessitating the introduction of levodopa in untreated PD patients. </p>\n<p>MAO-B inhibitors include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Selegiline</li>\n<li>Rasagiline (more potent)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cheese effect</strong>: inhibition of MAO-A isoform prevents the metabolism of tyramine in the gut, leading to a potentially fatal hypertensive reaction called the 'cheese effect'. It can be precipitated by tyramine-rich foods such as cheese, aged meat, and red wine.</p>\n<p>Selegiline and rasagiline do not functionally inhibit MAO-A isoform and are not associated with cheese effect in doses typically used in clinical practice.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2698","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements regarding the man​agement of non-motor symp​toms in patients with Par​kinson's disease is false‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements regarding the management of non-motor symptoms in patients with Parkinson's disease is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Depression should not be t‌reated with antidepressan​ts","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Restless leg syndrome is t‌reated by dopamine agonis​ts","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sialorrhea can be managed ‌by anticholinergics","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Low doses of fludrocortiso‌ne or midodrine provide c​ontrol of autonomic sympt​oms","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Depression should not be treated with antidepressants","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Depression should not be treated with antidepressants</strong></p><p><strong>Depression</strong> is treated with selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (<strong>SSRIs</strong>). In cases of severe depression, other antidepressants are added.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 600px; width: 382px;\" width=\"382\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.733px;\">Manifestations in Parkinson's </td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.667px;\">Treatment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.733px;\">Depression </td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.667px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SSRIs</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.733px;\">Insomnia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.667px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Zolpidem</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.733px;\">Restless leg syndrome </td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.667px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gabapentenoids</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Dopamine agonist </strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.733px;\">Erectile dysfunction </td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.667px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Sildenafil </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.733px;\">Postural hypotension </td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.667px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Rehabilitation with postural exercise </li>\n<li>Adding salt to the diet and elevating the head of the bed to prevent overnight sodium<br/>natriuresis</li>\n<li>Low doses of <strong>fludrocortisone or midodrine</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 124.733px;\">Sialorrhea</td>\n<td style=\"width: 228.667px;\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anticholinergic</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2685","text":"All are true about levodop‌a-induced motor complicat​ions except:","raw_text":"All are true about levodopa-induced motor complications except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"More likely to occur in ma‌les","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Seen in younger individual‌s with severe disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Seen with higher doses of ‌levodopa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rhythmic movements that pr‌edominantly involve the l​ower extremities","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. More likely to occur in males","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. More likely to occur in males</strong></p><p><strong>Levodopa-induced motor complications</strong> are more likely to occur in <strong>females</strong>. </p>\n<p>The complications include transient, stereotypic, rhythmic movements that predominantly involve the lower extremities. It is seen in younger individuals with severe disease and also with higher doses of levodopa.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2688","text":"A 65-year-old woman on tre‌atment for Parkinson’s di​sease is brought to the O​PD. Her husband is concer​ned because she has been ‌shopping a lot and reache​d the maximum spending li​mit on 2 credit cards in ​the past week. She has al​so impulsively been buyin​g several rabbits and rai‍sing them in their home. ‌She admits feeling guilty​ after her purchases. Whi​ch of the following drugs‍ is associated with this ‌condition?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old woman on treatment for Parkinson’s disease is brought to the OPD. Her husband is concerned because she has been shopping a lot and reached the maximum spending limit on 2 credit cards in the past week. She has also impulsively been buying several rabbits and raising them in their home. She admits feeling guilty after her purchases. Which of the following drugs is associated with this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dopamine ‌precursors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dopamine‌ agonists","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dopamine ‌depletors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dopamine a‌ntagonists","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dopamine agonists","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dopamine agonists</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>compulsive shopping</strong>. Pathological gambling, hypersexuality, and compulsive eating and shopping are the adverse effects of <strong>dopamine agonists</strong>. Levodopa can also cause these effects, but to a lesser extent than dopamine agonists.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7999","text":"A patient with Parkinson's‌ disease has been schedul​ed to undergo deep brain ​stimulation. What is the ​target site for this proc‌edure?","raw_text":"A patient with Parkinson's disease has been scheduled to undergo deep brain stimulation. What is the target site for this procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subthalamic nucleus and gl‌obus pallidus internus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Subthalamic nucleus and gl‌obus pallidus externus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Substantia nigra pars comp‌acta and globus pallidus ​externus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Substantia nigra pars reti‌culata and globus pallidu​s internus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Subthalamic nucleus and globus pallidus internus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Subthalamic nucleus and globus pallidus internus</strong></p><p>The target site for <strong>deep brain stimulation</strong> in <strong>Parkinson’s disease</strong> is the <strong>subthalamic nucleus (STN)</strong> and <strong>globus pallidus internus (GPi).</strong></p>\n<p>It helps improve tremors and reduce both 'off' time and dyskinesia. It does not improve features that fail to respond to levodopa. It does not prevent the development or progression of non-dopaminergic features such as freezing, falling, and dementia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7976","text":"Which of the following is ‌an example of Parkinson's​ plus syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is an example of Parkinson's plus syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Progressive supranuclear p‌alsy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dementia p‌ugilistica","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post-encep‌halitic PD","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Drug-induced‌ Parkinsonism","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Progressive supranuclear palsy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Progressive supranuclear palsy</strong></p><p><strong>Progressive supranuclear palsy</strong> is an example of <strong>Parkinson's plus syndrome</strong>. </p>\n<p>Parkinson's plus syndrome or <strong>atypical parkinsonism</strong> refers to a group of conditions that present with parkinsonism (rigidity and bradykinesia) but are different from PD due to underlying differences in clinical manifestations and pathology. They may have degeneration of the striatum, globus pallidus, cerebellum, and brainstem as well as the SNc. These include multiple system atrophy (MSA), progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP), and corticobasal syndrome (CBS).</p>\n<p><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":829,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"11\":4,\"12\":0}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"PSP is often misdiagnosed as Parkinson’s disease (PD). The substantia nigra is damaged in both diseases and this accounts for the overlapping symptoms which are observed, particularly in the initial stages. In PSP, impairment of cognitive functions occurs before the motor or ocular signs and this serves as an important distinguishing feature.\"}'>PSP is often misdiagnosed as Parkinson’s disease (PD). The substantia nigra is damaged in both diseases and this accounts for the overlapping symptoms which are observed.  In PSP, impairment of cognitive functions occurs before the motor or ocular signs and this serves as an important distinguishing feature.</span></p>\n<p>Immunohistochemistry broadly classifies them into two subtypes: <strong>synucleopathies </strong>and<strong> tauopathies</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8015","text":"Which among the following ‌features is not expected ​in a patient with atypica​l parkinsonism?","raw_text":"Which among the following features is not expected in a patient with atypical parkinsonism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Late speech and gait impai‌rment","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Absence of ‌rest tremor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Poor or no response to lev‌odopa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No motor ‌asymmetry","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Late speech and gait impairment","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Late speech and gait impairment</strong></p><p><strong>Early speech</strong> and <strong>gait impairment</strong> are seen in <strong>atypical parkinsonism</strong>.</p>\n<p>Features of atypical parkinsonism include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Early speech and gait impairment</li>\n<li><strong>Absence of rest tremor</strong></li>\n<li><strong>No motor asymmetry</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Poor or no response to levodopa</strong></li>\n<li>Aggressive clinical course</li>\n<li>Symptoms of severe early autonomic involvement</li>\n<li>Early severe dementia also points towards atypical parkinsonism</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7979","text":"A 60-year-old man presente‌d with Parkinsonism and c​erebellar features. MRI s​howed the finding as show​n in the image. What is t‌he likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presented with Parkinsonism and cerebellar features. MRI showed the finding as shown in the image. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Drug induced‌ parkinsonism","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple sys‌tems atrophy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dementia p‌ugilistica","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Progressive supranuclear p‌alsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Multiple systems atrophy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a24937c7b9ed4bf1a324b505ba92107a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Multiple systems atrophy</strong></p><p>Parkinsonism, cerebellar features, and the 'hot cross bun' sign on MRI suggest <strong>multiple system atrophy</strong> (MSA).</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3168\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>MSA manifests as a combination of parkinsonian, cerebellar, and autonomic features. Based on predominant clinical features it is further divided into :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>MSA-P:\n<ul>\n<li>Predominantly parkinsonian features are seen</li>\n<li>T2 weighted MRI demonstrates <strong>hyperintense putaminal rim</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>MSA-C:\n<ul>\n<li>Predominantly cerebellar features are seen</li>\n<li>MRI shows cerebellar and brainstem atrophy causing <strong>pontine hot cross bun sign</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9050","text":"All of the following are t‌he core clinical features​ for the diagnosis of Lew​y body dementia except:","raw_text":"All of the following are the core clinical features for the diagnosis of Lewy body dementia except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fluctuatin‌g cognition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Recurrent visual hallucina‌tion","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"REM sleep behavior disorde‌r","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Orthostatic‌ hypotension","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Orthostatic hypotension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Orthostatic hypotension</strong></p><p><strong>Orthostatic hypotension</strong> is <strong>not</strong> a <strong>core </strong>clinical feature of <strong>Lewy body dementia</strong> (LBD) as per <strong>DSM-5</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>LBD</strong> also known as dementia with Lewy bodies is the second most type of <strong>progressive dementia</strong> after Alzheimer’s disease. <strong>Protein deposits</strong>, called <strong>Lewy bodies</strong>, deposit in <strong>nerve cells</strong> in the <strong>brain</strong> region involved in thinking, memory, and movement (motor control)</p>\n<p><strong>Core features</strong> of LBD as per DSM-5 are <strong>fluctuating cognition</strong>, <strong>attention</strong>, <strong>alertness</strong>, <strong>recurrent visual hallucination,</strong> and <strong>Parkinsonian symptoms</strong>. Suggestive features are REM sleep behavior disorder and severe neuroleptic sensitivity.<br/>Orthostatic hypotension, erectile dysfunction, and constipation are other features of LBD.</p>\n<p>Treatment of LBD includes <strong>rivastigmine</strong> and <strong>donepezil</strong> which may improve cognition, and reduce hallucinosis and delusion. <strong>Pimavanserin</strong> may be used to treat <strong>psychosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5412","text":"A 60-year-old man presente‌d with Parkinsonian featu​res which is unresponsive​ to levodopa. MRI image i​s given below. What is th‌e probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presented with Parkinsonian features which is unresponsive to levodopa. MRI image is given below. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Progressive supranuclear p‌alsy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Multiple system atrophy ty‌pe C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hallevorden ‌spatz disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Parkinson‌'s disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Progressive supranuclear palsy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3380e875f2444465a4ef96ae52e8c73c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0547b0e825084b15ae58a7245aa70786x510x506.GIF"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Progressive supranuclear palsy</strong></p><p>An elderly male with a history of features of <strong>Parkinsonism nonresponsive to levodopa</strong> should raise suspicion for <strong>Parkinson plus syndromes</strong> which include <strong>progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP).</strong></p>\n<p>Progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP), also known as the Steele-Richardson-Olszewski syndrome, is a <strong>tauopathy</strong> and is considered a neurodegenerative disease with no current efficacious treatment. It is characterized by decreased cognition, abnormal eye movements (supranuclear vertical gaze palsy), postural instability and falls, as well as parkinsonian features and speech disturbances. </p>\n<p>The <strong>atrophy of the midbrain</strong> results in <strong>hummingbird sign</strong> of the brainstem (in the sagittal plane). The <strong>preserved pons</strong> form the body of the bird, and the atrophic midbrain, the head, with beak extending anteriorly towards the optic chiasm. Midbrain atrophy is best confirmed with the reduction in the midbrain to pons ratio.</p>\n<p><strong>Mickey mouse appearance, </strong>which is the reduction of anteroposterior midline midbrain diameter, at the level of the superior colliculi is seen on axial imaging.</p>\n<p>The following image shows the hummingbird sign:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":33563519,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"4\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":6989903},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"9\":0,\"11\":4,\"12\":0,\"16\":10,\"28\":1}' data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;According to a recent imaging study data, the insula and frontal cortex seem to be involved in the development of anxiety in adults with Parkinson's disease.&quot;}\">According to a recent imaging study data, the insula and frontal cortex seem to be involved in the development of anxiety in adults with Parkinson's disease.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8006","text":"An 8-year-old girl exhibit‌s rapid, semi-purposeful,​ graceful, dance-like, in​voluntary movements. What​ is this finding?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old girl exhibits rapid, semi-purposeful, graceful, dance-like, involuntary movements. What is this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemiba‌llismus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myoc‌lonus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cho‌rea","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Athe‌tosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Chorea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Chorea</strong></p><p>Rapid, semi-purposeful, graceful, dance-like, involuntary movements are seen in <strong>chorea</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 33.6661%;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 100%;\" width=\"102\">\n<p><strong>Hyperkinetic movement disorders:</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.0111%;\" width=\"102\">\n<p>Tremor</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.9889%;\" width=\"499\">\n<p>Rhythmic oscillation of a body part due to intermittent muscle contractions</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.0111%;\" width=\"102\">\n<p>Dystonia</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.9889%;\" width=\"499\">\n<p>Involuntary, sustained or repeated muscle contractions often associated with twisting movements and abnormal posture</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.0111%;\" width=\"102\">\n<p>Athetosis</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.9889%;\" width=\"499\">\n<p>Slow, distal, writhing, involuntary movements with a propensity to affect arms and hands</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.0111%;\" width=\"102\">\n<p>Chorea</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.9889%;\" width=\"499\">\n<p>Rapid, semi-purposeful, graceful, dance-like, non patterned, involuntary movements involving distal or proximal muscle groups</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.0111%;\" width=\"102\">\n<p>Myoclonus</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.9889%;\" width=\"499\">\n<p>Sudden, brief (&lt;100ms), jerk-like arrhythmic muscle twitches</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 17.0111%;\" width=\"102\">\n<p>Tics</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 82.9889%;\" width=\"499\">\n<p>Brief, repeated, stereotyped muscle contractions that can often be suppressed for a short time</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Sydenham's chorea (also called St. Vitus's dance) is a type of chorea that is typically seen in childhood (5-15 years) with prior exposure to group A streptococcal infection and is thought to be autoimmune in nature. It is characterized by the acute onset of choreiform movements and behavioral disturbances and is self-limiting. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8012","text":"During rounds, you see tha‌t a patient diagnosed wit​h stroke exhibits wild, f​linging movements on his ​right side. Where is the ‌lesion likely to be?","raw_text":"During rounds, you see that a patient diagnosed with stroke exhibits wild, flinging movements on his right side. Where is the lesion likely to be?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caudate‌ nucleus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Put‌amen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subthalam‌ic nucleus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Red n‌ucleus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Subthalamic nucleus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Subthalamic nucleus</strong></p><p>Unilateral wild, flinging movements are called <strong>hemiballismus</strong> and is seen due to a lesion in the <strong>subthalamic nucleus. </strong>The most common cause of hemiballismus is stroke.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8016","text":"What is the most common ac‌ute drug-induced movement​ disorder?","raw_text":"What is the most common acute drug-induced movement disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Akat‌hisia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dyst‌onia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tardive D‌yskinesia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myoc‌lonus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dystonia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dystonia</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> acute<strong> drug-induced movement disorder</strong> is <strong>dystonia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Drug-induced movement disorders<strong> </strong>are primarily associated with drugs that block dopamine receptors(neuroleptics) or central dopaminergic transmission. Neuroleptics are the commonest cause of this condition. Drug-induced movement disorders are classified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acute</strong>: Dystonia is the most common acute hyperkinetic drug reaction; generalized in children, focal in adults.</li>\n<li><strong>Subacute</strong>: Akathisia is the commonest reaction in this category</li>\n<li><strong>Tardive syndromes: </strong>Develop after months to years of initiation of the drug.\n<ul>\n<li>Tardive dyskinesia is the most common. Choreiform movements affecting the mouth, lips, and tongue are seen. In severe cases, trunks, limbs, and respiratory muscles may also be affected.</li>\n<li>In one third, TD remits within 3 months of stopping the drug and improvement occur over several years.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2710","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common movement ​disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common movement disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dyst‌onia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cho‌rea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Athe‌tosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Essentia‌l tremor","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Essential tremor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Essential tremor</strong></p><p>The most common movement disorder is <strong>essential tremor</strong>.</p>\n<p>It can present in childhood but dramatically increases in prevalence over the age of 70 years. Essential tremor is characterized by a high-frequency tremor <strong>6-10 Hz</strong> that predominantly affects the upper extremities.</p>\n<p>The tremor is characteristically<strong> improved by alcohol</strong> and <strong>worsened by stress</strong>.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 100px;\" width=\"306\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Essential tremor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Parkinson's disease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Bradykinesia，<br/>rigidity， micrographia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Tremor</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Usually postural</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Usually resting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Tone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 145px;\">Cogwheel rigidity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2716","text":"A 65-year-old man presents‌ with a tremor in both ha​nds that worsens when he ​is trying to eat or write​ something. He says that ‌it gets worse when he is ​stressed and reduces when​ he drinks wine. His fath​er also had similar sympt​oms. On examination, ther​e are no resting tremors.‍ Which of the following d‌rugs can be used to contr​ol this condition?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old man presents with a tremor in both hands that worsens when he is trying to eat or write something. He says that it gets worse when he is stressed and reduces when he drinks wine. His father also had similar symptoms. On examination, there are no resting tremors. Which of the following drugs can be used to control this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Valp‌roate","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Leveti‌racetam","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prim‌idone","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Carbam‌azepine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Primidone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Primidone</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>essential tremor</strong>. It can be controlled by <strong>primidone</strong>.</p>\n<p>The drugs used to treat essential tremors are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Beta-blockers: propranolol, atenolol</li>\n<li>Anticonvulsants:\n<ul>\n<li>Primidone</li>\n<li>Gabapentine</li>\n<li>Topiramate</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In severe and drug-resistant cases, surgical treatment involves targeting the <strong>ventral intermediate (VIM) nucleus of the thalamus</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB8001","text":"A 70-year-old male patient‌ is scheduled to undergo ​deep brain stimulation fo​r essential tremor. What ​is the target site for th‌e procedure?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old male patient is scheduled to undergo deep brain stimulation for essential tremor. What is the target site for the procedure?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Subthalamic nucleus and gl‌obus pallidus internus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior limb of internal ‌capsule","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Substantia nigra pars comp‌acta","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ventral intermediate nucle‌us of thalamus","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ventral intermediate nucleus of thalamus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ventral intermediate nucleus of thalamus</strong></p><p>The target for <strong>deep brain stimulation</strong> in <strong>essential tremor</strong> is<strong> ventral intermediate </strong>(VIM) nucleus of the thalamus.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"213\">\n<p><strong>Disease</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" width=\"182\">\n<p><strong>Target sites for deep brain stimulation</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>Parkinson’s disease</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"182\">\n<p>Subthalamic nucleus (STN)</p>\n<p>Globus pallidus internus (GPi)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>Dystonia</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"182\">\n<p>Globus pallidus internus (GPi)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>Depression</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"182\">\n<p>Anterior limb of internal capsule and cingulum</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD)</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"182\">\n<p>Anterior limb of the internal capsule</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"213\">\n<p>Essential tremor</p>\n</td>\n<td width=\"182\">\n<p>Ventral intermediate (VIM) nucleus</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2727","text":"A patient with a family hi‌story of Huntington's dis​ease has come for genetic​ counselling. All of the ​following are true about ‌this condition, except:","raw_text":"A patient with a family history of Huntington's disease has come for genetic counselling. All of the following are true about this condition, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal dominant inherit‌ance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Trinucleotide repeat of CA‌G sequence","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Westphal akinetic variant ‌has good prognosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Early onset cognitive impa‌irment seen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Westphal akinetic variant has good prognosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f3cd4fcd53c84a809407548d00b8c999x1280x1575.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/94daa957c157440d9216e91069083745x1280x1576.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Westphal akinetic variant has good prognosis</strong></p><p><strong>Westphal akinetic variant</strong> has the <strong>worst prognosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Huntington's disease shows an <strong>autosomal dominant inheritance</strong>. It is a <strong>trinucleotide repeat expansion</strong> disease with multiple CAG repeats (&gt;40 repeats) in the coding region of the huntingtin gene on chromosome 4p. It demonstrates genetic anticipation with earlier onset if inherited from the father.</p>\n<p>It presents in the second to third decade of life with cognitive impairment as an early feature. The dominant cognitive feature of HD is executive dysfunction. Additionally, dementia is seen later as the disease progresses.</p>\n<p>Decreased acetylcholine leads to dementia. Decreased GABA and increased dopamine lead to chorea. Treatment consists of haloperidol, tetrabenazine (Dopamine depletor).</p>\n<p>The following image shows MRI showing dilated ventricles (ex vacuo ventriculomegaly)- due to atrophy of caudate and putamen:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>MRI showing atrophy of caudate and putamen</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2735","text":"St. Vitus' dance is the ot‌her name for which of the​ following conditions?","raw_text":"St. Vitus' dance is the other name for which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Huntigton‌'s chorea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sydenham‌'s chorea","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Athe‌tosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemiba‌lismus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sydenham's chorea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sydenham&#x27;s chorea</strong></p><p><strong>Sydenham's chorea</strong> was originally called St. Vitus's dance.</p>\n<p>Sydenham's chorea is more common in females. It is typically seen in childhood (5-1 5 years) with prior exposure to group A streptococcal infection and is thought to be autoimmune in nature.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by the acute onset of choreiform movements and behavioral disturbances.</p>\n<p>It is self limited.Treatment is generally restricted to those with severe chorea.The chorea generally responds to dopamine-blocking agents, valproic acid, and carbamazepine. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2739","text":"All of the following featu‌res can be seen in patien​ts with restless leg synd​rome, except:","raw_text":"All of the following features can be seen in patients with restless leg syndrome, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Symptoms worsen during the‌ evening or night","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Accompanied by an unpleasa‌nt sensation in the legs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Symptoms that begin or wor‌sen with activity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Partial or complete relief‌ by movement","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Symptoms that begin or worsen with activity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Symptoms that begin or worsen with activity</strong></p><p>In restless leg syndrome (RLS), <strong>symptoms begin</strong> or worsen with <strong>rest</strong>. It is also called as Willis-Ekbom disease.</p>\n<p>The four core symptoms required for the diagnosis of RLS are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An urge to move the legs</li>\n<li>Usually caused or accompanied by an <strong>unpleasant sensation</strong> in the legs (creepy-crawly feeling, paresthesia, or burning)</li>\n<li>Symptoms that begin or worsen with rest and partial or complete <strong>relief by movement</strong></li>\n<li>Worsening during the evening or night</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>Restless leg syndrome is a typical <strong>sensory-motor disorder</strong> that generally affects the legs during rest periods, especially sleep. There is an <strong>irresistible urge</strong> to<strong> shake</strong> the<strong> legs</strong> associated with <strong>burning sensation, itching,</strong> and <strong>cramps</strong> of the legs. In idiopathic cases, dopaminergic hypofunction is postulated.</p>\n<p>In patients with restless legs syndrome,<strong> iron stores</strong> are initially repleted, and the aggravating factors are eliminated. If RLS still persists, initial therapy with a <strong>gabapentinoid</strong> (pregabalin, gabapentin, gabapentin enacarbil) is preferred, rather than a dopamine agonist (pramipexole, ropinirole, rotigotine). This is due to the high frequency of intermediate and long-term augmentation with dopamine agonists, as well as their risk of impulse control disorders.</p>\n<p><strong>FDA-approved</strong> drugs for the <strong>treatment</strong> of restless leg syndrome: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gabapentin</li>\n<li>Ropinirole</li>\n<li>Pramipexole</li>\n<li>Rotigotine </li>\n</ul>\n<p>A dopamine agonist is used as an alternative in patients with increased risk for side effects from gabapentinoids, such as those with obesity and its complications, a past or present history of moderate to severe depression, a disorder causing gait instability or respiratory failure, and a history of substance use disorder.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Non-pharmacological treatments that can be used in restless leg syndrome are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Avoiding alcohol at bedtime</li>\n<li>Massaging affected legs</li>\n<li>Hot baths</li>\n<li>Applying hot or cold to the affected area</li>\n<li>Engaging in moderate exercises</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"s7C92VlL","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"83_als_and_other_motor_neuron_diseases","title":"83.Als And Other Motor Neuron Diseases","num_questions":11,"total_marks":44,"duration":21,"time_per_question":114.55,"questions":[{"id":"MB7859","text":"Which of the following fea​tures are lower motor neu​ron lesions associated wi​th?","raw_text":"Which of the following features are lower motor neuron lesions associated with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fascicu​lations","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Spast​icity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Proximal mus​cle weakness","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Preserved stretch reflexes​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fasciculations","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fasciculations</strong></p><p><strong>Lower motor neuron</strong> lesions are associated with <strong>fasciculations</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7862","text":"Which of the following is ​referred to as bulbar par​alysis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is referred to as bulbar paralysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"UMN palsy of cranial nerve​s 1 to 6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"LMN palsy of cranial nerve​s 1 to 6","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"UMN palsy of cranial nerve​s 9 to 12","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"LMN palsy of cranial nerve​s 9 to 12","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. LMN palsy of cranial nerves 9 to 12","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. LMN palsy of cranial nerves 9 to 12</strong></p><p><strong>Bulbar</strong> <strong>paralysis</strong> refers to <strong>LMN palsy</strong> of <strong>cranial nerves 9 to 12.</strong> It is seen due to disease processes affecting <strong>primarily</strong> the <strong>motor nuclei</strong> of the cranial nerves.</p>\n<p>The manifestations of bulbar palsy include <strong>paralysis of tongue</strong>, muscles of <strong>swallowing</strong>, and <strong>facial muscles</strong>. There is difficulty handling secretions and maintaining an airway.</p>\n<p><strong>Causes of bulbar palsy</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>GBS</li>\n<li>Polio</li>\n<li>Syringobulbia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3438","text":"Which type of disorder is ​primary lateral sclerosis​?","raw_text":"Which type of disorder is primary lateral sclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isolated U​MN disorder","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Isolated L​MN disorder","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"UMN + LMN​ disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isolated bul​bar disorder","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Isolated UMN disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Isolated UMN disorder</strong></p><p>Primary lateral sclerosis is a <strong>progressive</strong> isolated <strong>upper motor neuron</strong> disorder.</p>\n<p>The spectrum of <strong>motor neuron disease</strong> includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis</strong> (ALS):\n<ul>\n<li>It is the <strong>most common </strong>form of<strong> motor neuron disease</strong></li>\n<li>Includes upper and lower motor neuron pathology (<strong>UMN +LMN</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Primary lateral sclerosis:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a progressive isolated upper motor neuron disorder (<strong>UMN</strong>)</li>\n<li>Some patients develop lower motor neuron signs later in their clinical course, at which point the disease is called upper motor neuron-onset ALS</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Progressive muscular atrophy:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a progressive isolated lower motor neuron disorder (<strong>LMN</strong>)</li>\n<li>Some patients develop upper motor neuron signs later in their clinical course, at which point the disease is called lower motor neuron-onset ALS</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Progressive bulbar palsy:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>It is a progressive upper and lower motor neuron (<strong>UMN +LMN</strong>) disorder of<strong> cranial muscles</strong></li>\n<li>This condition may occasionally stay isolated to the bulbar segment, but more commonly, upper and lower motor neuron signs and symptoms spread to involve other segments. This is then referred to as <strong>bulbar-onset ALS</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7866","text":"Which of the following is ​involved in amyotrophic l​ateral sclerosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is involved in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anterior horn cell + corti​cospinal tract","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Anterior horn cell + spino​thalamic tract","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Posterior horn cell + cort​icospinal tract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ventral root ganglia + spi​nothalamic tract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Anterior horn cell + corticospinal tract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Anterior horn cell + corticospinal tract</strong></p><p><strong>Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis </strong>(ALS) involves the <strong>anterior horn cells</strong> and <strong>corticospinal tract</strong>.</p>\n<p>ALS is the <strong>most common</strong> form of progressive <strong>motor neuron disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>pathologic hallmark</strong> of motor neuron degenerative disorders is the <strong>death of</strong> <strong>lower</strong> motor neurons <strong>and upper</strong>, or corticospinal, <strong>motor neurons</strong>. LMN consist of anterior horn cells in the spinal cord and their brainstem homologs innervating bulbar muscles. UMN originate in layer five of the motor cortex and descend via the pyramidal tract to synapse with lower motor neurons, either directly or indirectly via interneurons.</p>\n<p>Although at its onset, ALS may cause selective loss of function of only upper or lower motor neurons, it ultimately causes progressive loss of both categories of motor neurons.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7867","text":"A middle-aged man presents​ with progressive atrophy​ and weakness of his hand​s and forearms. On examin​ation, he is found to hav‍e spasticity of the legs ‍and brisk jaw jerk. Neuro​imaging revealed increase‍d signal in the corticosp​inal tracts on T2 weighte‌d MRI. What is the most l‌ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A middle-aged man presents with progressive atrophy and weakness of his hands and forearms. On examination, he is found to have spasticity of the legs and brisk jaw jerk. Neuroimaging revealed increased signal in the corticospinal tracts on T2 weighted MRI. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple ​sclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amyotrophic lateral sclero​sis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Subacute combined degenera​tion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pregressive spinal muscula​r atrophy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis is <strong>amyotrophic lateral sclerosis</strong> (ALS). </p>\n<p>The <strong>clinical hallmark</strong> of ALS is the presence of signs and symptoms of both upper and lower motor neuron (<strong>UMN + LMN</strong>) <strong>dysfunction</strong>. The signs of <strong>UMN</strong> dysfunction include <strong>spasticity</strong> and <strong>brisk jaw jerk, </strong>while the signs of <strong>LMN</strong> dysfunction include <strong>atrophy</strong> and <strong>weakness</strong> of limbs.</p>\n<p>Conventional MRI is usually normal in ALS and is used to exclude other possible causes although <strong>increased signal </strong>in the<strong> corticospinal tracts </strong>on<strong> T2-weighted MRI</strong> is supportive of the diagnosis of ALS.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3443","text":"A young college student pr​esents with multiple epis​odes of falls while climb​ing stairs. He stated he ​had been feeling clumsy a‍nd weak for the past few ‍months. On examination, h​e was found to have bilat‍eral positive Babinski's ​sign and atrophy of muscl‌es of lower limbs. Deep t‌endon reflexes were absen‌t. What is the likely dia‍gnosis?","raw_text":"A young college student presents with multiple episodes of falls while climbing stairs. He stated he had been feeling clumsy and weak for the past few months. On examination, he was found to have bilateral positive Babinski's sign and atrophy of muscles of lower limbs. Deep tendon reflexes were absent. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kennedy​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lou Gehrig​'s disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Pelizaeus–Merzbacher disea​se","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Charcot-Marie-tooth diseas​e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lou Gehrig's disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lou Gehrig&#x27;s disease</strong></p><p>The given scenario with both <strong>UMN</strong> (positive Babinski sign) <strong>and LMN</strong> (absent reflexes and wasting of muscles) <strong>signs</strong> are suggestive of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (<strong>ALS</strong>). It is also known as <strong>Lou Gehrig's disease,</strong> after the famous New York Yankee baseball player who was affected with the disorder.</p>\n<p>ALS was first described by Charcot in the 19th century and is also referred to as <strong>Charcot's disease.</strong></p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Kennedy disease</strong> is a gradually progressive neuromuscular disorder characterised by <strong>wasting </strong>of<strong> proximal </strong>and <strong>bulbar muscles</strong>. It is caused by a mutation in the androgen receptor gene.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Pelizaeus–Merzbacher disease</strong> is an inherited <strong>leukodystrophy</strong> characterized by <strong>problems</strong> with <strong>coordination</strong>, motor skills, and<strong> learning</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Charcot-Marie-tooth disease</strong> encompasses a group of hereditary <strong>sensory</strong> and <strong>motor neuropathies</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3447","text":"A 42-year-old man who pres​ented with progressive we​akness was found to have ​a mix of upper and lower ​motor neuron findings on ‍examination. His father a‍nd his cousin had passed ​away in their 40s due to ‍similar symptoms. A mutat​ion of the gene encoding ‌which of the following en‌zymes is likely implicate‌d here?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old man who presented with progressive weakness was found to have a mix of upper and lower motor neuron findings on examination. His father and his cousin had passed away in their 40s due to similar symptoms. A mutation of the gene encoding which of the following enzymes is likely implicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superoxide​ dismutase","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"NADPH ​oxidase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myelope​roxidase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glutathione​ peroxidase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Superoxide dismutase","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Superoxide dismutase</strong></p><p>The given scenario is suggestive of <strong>familial amyotrophic lateral sclerosis</strong>. This condition is linked to mutations of the gene encoding copper-zinc (Cu, Zn)<strong> superoxide dismutase</strong> <strong>type 1</strong> (SOD1). Familial ALS accounts for about 10% of all ALS cases.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Option B: Mutations in the <strong>NADPH oxidase</strong> genes cause <strong>chronic granulomatous disease</strong> (CGD).</p>\n<p>Option C: Hereditary deficiency of myeloperoxidase (MPO) predisposes to immune deficiency.</p>\n<p>Option D: Glutathione peroxidase deficiency leads to hemolytic anemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7861","text":"All of the following featu​res can be seen in patien​ts with amyotrophic later​al sclerosis, except:","raw_text":"All of the following features can be seen in patients with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Split-han​d syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Orthostatic​ hypotension","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Frontotempo​ral dementia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dropped he​ad syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Orthostatic hypotension","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Orthostatic hypotension</strong></p><p>Orthostatic hypotension is not a feature seen in classical ALS.  <strong>Autonomic symptoms</strong> are not classically seen in ALS, although a few cases with autonomic insufficiency have been reported and these are classified separately as <strong>ALS-plus syndromes</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option A: The <strong>split-hand syndrome</strong> describes a frequent pattern of weakness and atrophy in ALS that predominantly<strong> involves</strong> the median and ulnar-innervated lateral (<strong>thenar</strong>) hand intrinsic muscles with <strong>relative sparing</strong> of the medial (<strong>hypothenar</strong>) muscles.</p>\n<p>Option C: There is a well-established link between ALS and <strong>frontotemporal</strong> executive dysfunction (<strong>dementia</strong>) that <strong>may precede or follow</strong> the onset of upper <span class=\"nowrap\">and/or</span> lower motor neuron dysfunction</p>\n<p>Option D:<strong> Dropped head syndrome</strong> is an important clinical sign in ALS caused by <strong>severe neck extensor weakness</strong> (LMN), which leads to an inability to maintain level head position.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7868","text":"Which of the following pat​ients is unlikely to have​ amyotrophic lateral scle​rosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients is unlikely to have amyotrophic lateral sclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A patient with difficulty ​in swallowing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A patient who has spastic ​dysarthria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A patient who starts laugh​ing or crying inappropria​tely","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A patient with bowel and b​ladder dysfunction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A patient with bowel and bladder dysfunction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A patient with bowel and bladder dysfunction</strong></p><p>The patient with bowel and bladder dysfunction is unlikely to have<strong> ALS</strong> as these findings are not seen in ALS. Even in the late stages of the ALS, sensory &amp;<strong> bowel &amp; bladder </strong>functions are<strong> preserved.</strong></p>\n<p>Fasciculations, muscle cramps, weakness, atrophy, and bulbar palsy are <strong>LMN features</strong> seen in ALS. </p>\n<p>Spasticity, increased reflexes, flexor spasms, and pseudobulbar palsy are <strong>UMN features</strong> in ALS. </p>\n<p>A frequent bulbar UMN symptom seen in ALS is the <strong>pseudobulbar affect</strong>. This is manifested as <strong>i</strong><strong>nappropriate laughing, crying, or yawning</strong>. The observed affect is often mood incongruent or may be triggered by stimuli that would not have elicited such a response prior to the development of pseudobulbar affect.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3467","text":"What is the diagnosis of a​myotrophic lateral sclero​sis predominantly based o​n?","raw_text":"What is the diagnosis of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis predominantly based on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MRI fi​ndings","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CSF analys​is findings","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Muscle biop​sy findings","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Clinical​ criteria","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Clinical criteria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Clinical criteria</strong></p><p>The<strong> diagnosis of ALS </strong>is based on<strong> clinical criteria</strong> that include the presence of upper motor neuron and lower motor neuron signs, the progression of the disease, and the absence of an alternative explanation. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>There is <strong>no single diagnostic test</strong> that can confirm or entirely exclude the diagnosis of motor neuron disease in general and amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) in particular.</p>\n<p>The revised <strong>El Escorial criteria</strong> and, more recently, the newer <strong>Awaji criteria</strong> are being used for the diagnosis of ALS.</p>\n<p>Practically, the diagnosis is made when it is suggested by the history and physical examination, <strong>supported by electrodiagnostic studies</strong> (EMG &amp; NCS), and not excluded by neuroimaging (MRI) and laboratory studies (CSF, blood, and urine analysis). <strong>Increased signal</strong> in the <strong>corticospinal tracts</strong> on <strong>T2-weighted MRI</strong>, if seen, is <strong>supportive</strong> of the diagnosis of ALS </p>\n<p><strong>Muscle biopsy</strong> is not a routine part of the diagnostic evaluation of ALS but should be performed if there is <strong>clinical suspicion</strong> of <strong>inflammatory myopathy</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Genetic testing</strong> is not a routine part of the diagnostic evaluation in ALS except in cases where <strong>familial ALS</strong> (10%) is suspected.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3473","text":"Which of the following is ​the only drug known to im​prove survival in patient​s with amyotrophic latera​l sclerosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the only drug known to improve survival in patients with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fingo​limod","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rilu​zole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Tocil​izumab","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rotig​otine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Riluzole","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Riluzole</strong></p><p>Among the given options,<strong> riluzole</strong> is the only known drug to have any impact on survival in ALS. It can <strong>slow ALS progression</strong> to a modest degree. It is a neuroprotective drug that <strong>blocks glutamatergic neurotransmission</strong> in the CNS.</p>\n<p><strong>Edaravone</strong> is a <strong>newer drug </strong>that<strong> </strong>is a free radical scavenger. It is thought to <strong>reduce oxidative stress,</strong> which has been implicated in the pathogenesis of ALS. Edaravone was <strong>found to slow functional deterioration</strong> in some patients with ALS.  It is recommended as an <strong>adjunct to riluzole</strong> for all patients with ALS.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"0aB_lWQd","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"84_ataxic_disorders","title":"84.Ataxic Disorders","num_questions":10,"total_marks":40,"duration":20,"time_per_question":120.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC7044","text":"A patient walks with a sto​mping gait. When asked to‍ close his eyes and walk,‌ he is unable to do so. W​hich of the following tra​cts is probably affected?‌","raw_text":"A patient walks with a stomping gait. When asked to close his eyes and walk, he is unable to do so. Which of the following tracts is probably affected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spinocereb​ellar tract","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Posterior c​olumn tract","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vestibulos​pinal tract","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rubrospi​nal tract","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Posterior column tract","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Posterior column tract</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of the involvement of the<strong> posterior column. </strong></p>\n<p>Posterior column carries<strong> proprioception</strong> which is essential for coordination of motor movements. Loss of this leads to the development of a <strong>stomping gait. </strong>This is seen in cases of<strong> vitamin B12 deficiency </strong>leading to subacute combined degeneration, tabes dorsalis or diabetic neuropathy. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7855","text":"Which of the following fea​tures would you not expec‍t to find in a patient wi‌th cerebellar ataxia?","raw_text":"Which of the following features would you not expect to find in a patient with cerebellar ataxia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Resting​ tremor","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Dysdiadoc​hokinesis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Scannin​g speech","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypo​tonia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Resting tremor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Resting tremor</strong></p><p><strong>Intention tremor</strong> is a feature of cerebellar ataxia, not resting tremor.</p>\n<p><strong>Cerebellar ataxia</strong> is characterized by the following symptoms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gait impairment</li>\n<li>Unclear speech or <strong>scanning speech</strong></li>\n<li>Blurred vision due to nystagmus</li>\n<li><strong>Dysdiadochokinesis</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Intention tremors - </strong>tremor on goal-oriented movements</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>B</strong><strong>ilateral</strong> and <strong>symmetric</strong> involvement suggests the following <strong>etiologies</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Genetic - SCA gene mutations </li>\n<li>Metabolic - Vitamin B1 or B12 deficiency </li>\n<li>Immune - Antigliadin antibody syndrome</li>\n<li>Toxic - Phenytoin, amiodarone, alcohol</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Focal, unilateral</strong> symptoms with headache, impaired level of consciousness, ipsilateral cranial nerve palsies and contralateral <strong>weakness </strong>suggest a <strong>space-occupying cerebellar</strong> lesion.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2769","text":"A 70-year-old man presente​d with gait instability, ‍slurring of speech, and i‌ntention tremors, for a f​ew months. He was a forme​r smoker who quit 10 year‌s back. After extensive w​orkup, a paraneoplastic c‍ause was suspected, and c‍hest CT was ordered. It s‌howed a 3cm wide mass nea‌r the right hilum. What i‍s the most likely diagnos‌is?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old man presented with gait instability, slurring of speech, and intention tremors, for a few months. He was a former smoker who quit 10 years back. After extensive workup, a paraneoplastic cause was suspected, and chest CT was ordered. It showed a 3cm wide mass near the right hilum. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenocarc​inoma lung","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non small cell lung cancer​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Small cell ​lung cancer","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Squamous cell carcinoma lu​ng","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Small cell lung cancer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Small cell lung cancer</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>paraneoplastic cerebellar ataxia</strong>, which is most likely to be caused by <strong>small cell lung cancer</strong> here. </p>\n<p><strong>Paraneoplastic</strong> syndromes causing <strong>cerebellar ataxia </strong>are associated with various antibodies:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 150px;\" width=\"443\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213.867px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Cancer</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 213.887px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Antibody seen</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213.867px;\">Breast and ovarian cancers</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213.887px;\">Anti-Yo antibody </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213.867px;\">Small-cell lung cancer</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213.887px;\">Anti-PQ-type voltage-gated calcium channel autoantibody</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213.867px;\">Hodgkin's disease</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213.887px;\">Anti-Tr antibodies</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Sometimes, paraneoplastic ataxias may be the only presenting symptom of the underlying cancer. In paraneoplastic cerebellar ataxias, serum anti-glutamic acid decarboxylase (GAD) antibodies may be elevated, affecting speech and gait.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2783","text":"What is the most common in​herited ataxia?","raw_text":"What is the most common inherited ataxia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Machado-Jos​eph disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Friedreic​h's ataxia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ataxia - te​langiectasia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spinocerebe​llar ataxia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Friedreich's ataxia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Friedreich&#x27;s ataxia</strong></p><p><strong>Friedreich's ataxia</strong> is the most common form of <strong>inherited ataxia</strong> (autosomal recessive), comprising one-half of all hereditary ataxias.</p>\n<p>Friedrich's ataxia has<strong> autosomal recessive</strong> inheritance and presents <strong>before 25</strong> years of age. The median age of death is 35 years. Women have a significantly better prognosis than men.</p>\n<p>Friedrich's ataxia presents with<strong> </strong>clinical features such as <strong>dysarthria,</strong> progressive <strong>staggering gait</strong>, frequent <strong>falls</strong> and <strong>titubation.</strong> Other neurological symptoms include nystagmus, loss of fast saccadic movements, loss of vibratory and proprioceptive sensation, and ataxia or trunk and limb movements. The lower extremities are more severely involved than the upper ones.</p>\n<p><strong>Cardiac involvement</strong> occurs in<strong> 90%</strong> of patients. It presents with cardiomegaly, symmetric hypertrophy, and conduction defects. <strong>Musculoskeletal deformities</strong> are common and include progressive scoliosis, pes cavus, and pes equinovarus. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7849","text":"A 24-year-old man presents​ with complaints of swayi‍ng while walking, frequen‌t falls and slurring of s​peech. He also gives a hi​story of involuntary nodd‌ing movements of the head​. On examination, you not‍e the following findings.‍ Which of the following r‌egions of the spinal cord‌ is involved first in thi‍s condition? General exam‌ination: Kyphoscoliosis n‍oted CVS examination: Pes‍ cavus present Systolic m‌urmur present CNS examina‍tion: Romberg's sign posi‌tive Nystagmus present Lo​wer limb areflexia Extens​or plantar reflex noted","raw_text":"<p>A 24-year-old man presents with complaints of swaying while walking, frequent falls and slurring of speech. He also gives a history of involuntary nodding movements of the head. On examination, you note the following findings. Which of the following regions of the spinal cord is involved first in this condition?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>General examination: Kyphoscoliosis noted </li>\n<li>CVS examination:\n<ul>\n<li>Pes cavus present</li>\n<li>Systolic murmur present </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>CNS examination:\n<ul>\n<li>Romberg's sign positive</li>\n<li>Nystagmus present</li>\n<li>Lower limb areflexia</li>\n<li>Extensor plantar reflex noted</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dorsal roo​t ganglion","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Spinocereb​ellar tract","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lateral corticospinal trac​t","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cereb​ellum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Dorsal root ganglion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Dorsal root ganglion</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>Friedrich's ataxia</strong>, in which the <strong>d</strong><strong>orsal root ganglion</strong> cells are involved first. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3203\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The primary sites of pathology are the spinal cord, dorsal root ganglion cells, and the peripheral nerves.</p>\n<p>Spinal cord tracts are involved, and degeneration occurs predominantly in the <strong>spinocerebellar</strong> tracts,<strong> lateral corticospinal</strong> tracts, and<strong> posterior columns</strong>. Degeneration of <strong>cranial nerves</strong> (glossopharyngeal, vagus, hypoglossal) and deep cerebellar nuclei can occur. Peripheral nerves are also involved, and there's a loss of large myelinated fibers. Cerebellar atrophy is also seen.</p>\n<p>Cardiac changes occur due to myocyte hypertrophy, fibrosis, and degeneration of cardiac ganglia. </p>\n<p>Note: Friedrich's ataxia may also be associated with vitamin E deficiency.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7852","text":"A middle-aged vegan woman ​presented with tingling a‍nd numbness over the limb‌s and progressive fatigue​. Her peripheral smear is​ given below. Which of th‌e following neurological ​manifestation would you n‍ot expect to see in this ‍condition?","raw_text":"A middle-aged vegan woman presented with tingling and numbness over the limbs and progressive fatigue. Her peripheral smear is given below. Which of the following neurological manifestation would you not expect to see in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sensory​ ataxia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Optic ​atrophy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Psyc​hosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myop​athy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Myopathy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/da5047c145b248da8a2126fa897ff53c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Myopathy</strong></p><p>The given case scenario and smear showing macroovalocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils, is suggestive of <strong>megaloblastic anemia</strong> caused by <strong>vitamin B12 deficiency</strong>. Myopathy is not a feature of vitamin B12 deficiency.</p>\n<p>Vitamin <strong>B12 deficiency</strong> may produce the following <strong>neurological manifestations:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Loss</strong> of vibration sense and <strong>proprioception</strong> </li>\n<li>Paresthesia - <strong>upper limb paresthesias</strong> occur before lower limbs are affected.</li>\n<li>Unsteady gait - due to sensory ataxia </li>\n<li>Peripheral neuropathy</li>\n<li><strong>Optic neuropathy</strong></li>\n<li>Psychiatric manifestations such as:\n<ul>\n<li>Irritated behavior</li>\n<li>Severe dementia </li>\n<li>Frank psychosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7853","text":"A 30-year-old man presente​d with swaying while walk‍ing, tingling in palms an‌d feet, and blurring of v​ision. On examination, de​ep tendon reflexes are ab‌sent, proprioception and ​vibration sense are absen‍t over the lower limbs. M‍RI spine reveals diffuse ‌demyelination. What is th‌e diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man presented with swaying while walking, tingling in palms and feet, and blurring of vision. On examination, deep tendon reflexes are absent, proprioception and vibration sense are absent over the lower limbs. MRI spine reveals diffuse demyelination. What is the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple ​sclerosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tabes d​orsalis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transvers​e myelitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Subacute combined degenera​tion","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Subacute combined degeneration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Subacute combined degeneration</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>subacute combined degeneration </strong>(SACD) due to vitamin B12 deficiency. </p>\n<p>SACD presents with the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Loss</strong> of vibration sense and <strong>proprioception</strong> </li>\n<li>Paresthesia - <strong>upper limb paresthesias</strong> occur before lower limbs are affected.</li>\n<li>Unsteady gait - due to sensory ataxia </li>\n<li><strong>Loss of deep tendon reflexes</strong></li>\n<li>Peripheral neuropathy</li>\n<li><strong>Optic neuropathy - </strong>can present as blurring of vision </li>\n<li>Psychiatric manifestations such as:\n<ul>\n<li>Irritated behavior</li>\n<li>Severe dementia </li>\n<li>Frank psychosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>MRI</strong> spine shows myelopathy and areas of demyelination. <strong>Myelopathy</strong> of SACD tends to be <strong>diffuse</strong> rather than focal.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> is replacement therapy is with 1000 μg of <strong>intramuscular</strong> vitamin <strong>B12</strong> repeated at regular intervals or by oral vitamin B12.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7854","text":"The investigations of a ma​le truck driver are given‍ below. Which of the foll‌owing is he unlikely to p​resent with? VDRL - posit​ive FTA-ABs - Antibodies ‌to T. pallidum present MR​I spine - areas of focal ‍inflammation seen in the ‍dorsal column","raw_text":"<p>The investigations of a male truck driver are given below.  Which of the following is he unlikely to present with?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>VDRL - positive</li>\n<li>FTA-ABs - Antibodies to T. pallidum present </li>\n<li>MRI spine - areas of focal inflammation seen in the dorsal column</li>\n</ul>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ata​xia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Argyll Robe​rtson pupil","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bladder d​ysfunction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperr​eflexia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hyperreflexia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hyperreflexia</strong></p><p>The given case scenario is suggestive of <strong>tabes dorsalis.</strong> It presents with <strong>loss of reflexes in lower limbs, </strong>not hyperreflexia.</p>\n<p><strong>Tabes dorsalis </strong>(or syphilitic myelopathy) is a slow <strong>degeneration</strong> and <strong>demyelination</strong> of the neural tracts. It is seen in tertiary syphilis. It primarily involves the<strong> dorsal columns</strong> of the spinal cord and dorsal roots.</p>\n<p>It presents with the following clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Loss of reflexes</strong> in lower limbs </li>\n<li>Romberg's sign</li>\n<li>Bilateral<strong> Argyll Robertson pupils - </strong>accommodation reflex present, pupillary reflex absent </li>\n<li>Loss of position sense and vibration</li>\n<li>Stabbing/pricking pain in the legs - this is fleeting or repetitive</li>\n<li><strong>Ataxia</strong> of the legs and gait disturbances</li>\n<li>Paresthesias</li>\n<li><strong>Bladder disturbances </strong></li>\n<li>Acute abdominal pain with vomiting - visceral crisis </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Diabetic polyradiculopathy may simulate tabes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7858","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding ataxia te‍langiectasia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding ataxia telangiectasia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presents in 4th to 5th dec​ade of life.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Autosomal recessive inheri​tance is seen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Loss of Purkinje cells occ​urs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased incidence of lym​phomas is seen","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Presents in 4th to 5th decade of life.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Presents in 4th to 5th decade of life.</strong></p><p><strong>Ataxia-telangiectasia</strong> is an autosomal recessive condition presenting in the<strong> first decade of life.</strong></p>\n<p>It is associated with a high incidence of recurrent pulmonary infections, <strong>lymphomas,</strong> Hodgkin's disease, breast cancer, and acute T cell leukemias.</p>\n<p>Histology shows <strong>loss</strong> of<strong> Purkinje cells</strong>, granules, and basket cells in the cerebellar cortex. There is a loss of neurons in the deep cerebellar nuclei, inferior olives of the medulla, and anterior horn and dorsal root of the spinal cord.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical features</strong> of ataxia-telangiectasia include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Progressive telangiectatic lesions</li>\n<li>Oculomotor apraxia </li>\n<li>Truncal and limb ataxia</li>\n<li>Dysarthria</li>\n<li>Extensor plantar reflexes </li>\n<li>Myoclonic jerks </li>\n<li>Areflexia </li>\n<li>Distal sensory deficits </li>\n<li>Nystagmus </li>\n<li>Cellular and humoral immunodeficiency (absent/ill-developed thymus)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0689","text":"An elderly woman is brough​t to the OPD with complai‍nts of behavioral change,‌ a history of multiple fa​lls, urinary incontinence​, and dementia. What is y‌our diagnosis?","raw_text":"An elderly woman is brought to the OPD with complaints of behavioral change, a history of multiple falls, urinary incontinence, and dementia. What is your diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal pressure hydrocepha​lus","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Frontotempo​ral dementia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Parkinso​n disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Creutzfeldt-​Jakob disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Normal pressure hydrocephalus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f745d9c567da4d89b39cd5694dba8987x468x580.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Normal pressure hydrocephalus</strong></p><p>The given case scenario describes an elderly woman with urinary incontinence, dementia (cognitive decline), and a history of multiple falls (suggestive of a gait abnormality or ataxia). These form the <strong>classical triad</strong> of <strong>normal pressure hydrocephalus (NPH). </strong></p>\n<p>Both<strong> frontotemporal dementia</strong> (Option B) and <strong>Parkinson’s disease </strong>(Option C)  are common causes of dementia associated with falls, but urinary incontinence is not commonly appreciated. NPH is, therefore, the most likely diagnosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Normal pressure hydrocephalus</strong> refers to a disproportionate <strong>enlargement</strong> of the <strong>ventricles</strong> compared to the degree of <strong>cortical atrophy. </strong>It is a <strong>communicating hydrocephalus</strong> caused by delayed resorption of cerebrospinal fluid. Out of the classical triad of symptoms, most patients present with one or two. Hence, neuroimaging showing <strong>dilated ventricles</strong> is essential, as well as an <strong>L- dopa challenge test </strong>to rule out parkinson’s disease is recommended. </p>\n<p>A characteristic MRI of NPH is given below, showing<strong> dilated lateral ventricles</strong> which are out of proportion to <strong>sulcal deepening: </strong></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"91-aMQpC","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"85_cranial_nerve_disorders","title":"85.Cranial Nerve Disorders","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MB7535","text":"Which of the following cra​nial nerves are affected ‍in cavernous sinus syndro​me?","raw_text":"Which of the following cranial nerves are affected in cavernous sinus syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"III, I​V and V","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IV, V ​and VI","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"VI an​d VII","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"III, IV,​ V and VI","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. III, IV, V and VI","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1cf0d1fa12ce41508a8fc441e5e37dc9x1280x1403.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. III, IV, V and VI</strong></p><p>The cranial nerves <strong>III, IV, V, and VI</strong> are affected by cavernous sinus syndrome.</p>\n<p>The<strong> cavernous sinus syndrome</strong> is a condition caused by any pathology involving the cavernous sinus. The oculomotor (III), trochlear (IV) and trigeminal (V) nerves pass in relation to the lateral wall of the cavernous sinus. The abducent (VI) nerve passes through the cavernous sinus.</p>\n<p>Patients with this condition usually present with the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Orbital or facial pain</li>\n<li>Orbital swelling and chemosis due to occlusion of the ophthalmic veins</li>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Oculomotor neuropathy (III, IV, and VI)</li>\n<li>Trigeminal neuropathy (V)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Causes of cavernous sinus syndrome include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cavernous sinus thrombosis</strong> - most common</li>\n<li>Carotid artery aneurysm</li>\n<li>Carotid-cavernous fistula</li>\n<li>Tumors such as meningioma.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7495","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true regarding‍ trigeminal neuralgia?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding trigeminal neuralgia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The region of ophthalmic d​ivision of trigeminal ner‍ve is always spared","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The pain usually last for ​a few hours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"The condition is also know​n as tic douloureux","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is more c​ommon in men","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. The condition is also known as tic douloureux","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The condition is also known as tic douloureux</strong></p><p><strong>Tic douloureux</strong> is another term for <strong>trigeminal neuralgia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Trigeminal neuralgia is characterized by frequent <strong>paroxysms of severe pain</strong> in the lips, gums, cheek, or chin. It is <strong>sometimes seen</strong> in the distribution of the <strong>ophthalmic division</strong> of the trigeminal nerve.</p>\n<p><strong>Middle-aged</strong> and <strong>elderly females</strong> are primarily affected.<strong> </strong>The pain usually lasts for a <strong>short time</strong> (seconds to minutes) but be may intense enough that the patient winces, hence the term 'tic'.</p>\n<p>A characteristic feature is the presence of<strong> trigger zones</strong>, typically on the face, lips, or tongue. Patients usually report excruciating pain during activities such as washing the face or brushing the teeth. However, no signs of sensory loss can be demonstrated.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7508","text":"The high frequency of trig​eminal neuralgia in patie‍nts with multiple scleros​is is mainly attributed t‌o the involvement of ____‍__.","raw_text":"The high frequency of trigeminal neuralgia in patients with multiple sclerosis is mainly attributed to the involvement of ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oligodendrocyte-derived my​elin covering the beginni‍ng of the nerve","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Oligodendrocyte-derived my​elin covering the termina‍l part of the nerve","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Schwann cell–derived myeli​n sheath covering the beg‍inning of the nerve","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Schwann cell–derived myeli​n sheath covering the ter‍minal part of the nerve","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Oligodendrocyte-derived myelin covering the beginning of the nerve","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Oligodendrocyte-derived myelin covering the beginning of the nerve</strong></p><p>The high frequency of trigeminal neuralgia in patients with multiple sclerosis occurs due to the involvement of <strong>oligodendrocyte-derived myelin</strong> covering the <strong>beginning</strong> of the nerve.</p>\n<p><strong>Multiple sclerosis</strong> is a disorder of <strong>oligodendrocyte myelin</strong>. It should be ruled out when trigeminal neuralgia is bilateral or develops in a young adult. In such cases, the cause is a demyelinating plaque near the root entry zone of the fifth cranial nerve in the pons. In these patients, a facial sensory loss is also often found in association. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7512","text":"In patients with trigemina​l neuralgia, the nerve is‍ most commonly compressed​ by ______.","raw_text":"In patients with trigeminal neuralgia, the nerve is most commonly compressed by ______.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Superior cer​ebral artery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inferior cer​ebral artery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Superior cerebellar artery​","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Inferior cerebellar artery​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Superior cerebellar artery","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Superior cerebellar artery</strong></p><p>The <strong>superior cerebellar artery</strong> most often compresses the trigeminal nerve as it emerges from the brainstem. This causes lancinating pain in the distribution of the nerve which is known as trigeminal neuralgia.</p>\n<p>Though trigeminal neuralgia can be diagnosed by the clinical features, <strong>MRI</strong> is recommended to define the <strong>underlying cause</strong>. In cases <strong>refractory</strong> to medical management, it also helps to plan for <strong>surgical decompression</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7515","text":"A food vlogger presents wi​th complaints of episodes‍ of piercing electric-sho​ck like pain on the left ‌side of her face. The epi‍sodes are triggered by ch​ewing food and last for a​ few minutes. She is furt‍her distressed as it inte‍rferes with her work. She‌ has tried over the count‌er analgesics to no avail‍. What is the drug of cho​ice for her?","raw_text":"A food vlogger presents with complaints of episodes of piercing electric-shock like pain on the left side of her face. The episodes are triggered by chewing food and last for a few minutes. She is further distressed as it interferes with her work. She has tried over the counter analgesics to no avail. What is the drug of choice for her?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gabap​entin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lamot​rigine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbam​azepine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Amitri​ptyline","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Carbamazepine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Carbamazepine</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>trigeminal neuralgia</strong> and the drug of choice is <strong>carbamazepine.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Oxcarbazepine</strong> is an alternative to carbamazepine that has less bone marrow toxicity and is equally efficacious.</p>\n<p>Other drugs used are lamotrigine, gabapentin and baclofen.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2852","text":"Which of the following is ​false about Bell's palsy?‍","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about Bell's palsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hyperacusis may be present​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pregnancy is a risk factor​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"MRI shows enhancement of g​eniculate ganglion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Maximal weakness within 72​ hours","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Maximal weakness within 72 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Maximal weakness within 72 hours</strong></p><p><strong>Maximal weakness</strong> occurs within <strong>48 hours</strong> as a rule in Bells' palsy.</p>\n<p><strong>Bell's palsy</strong> is an idiopathic, peripheral facial paralysis or paresis of acute onset due to a lower motor neuron<strong> (LMN) lesion</strong> of the <strong>facial nerve</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clinical presentation of Bell’s palsy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Pain behind the ear (may precede paralysis and last for 1-2 days)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Bell's phenomenon</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Asymmetrical face</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Hyperacusis (due to stapedial paralysis)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Loss of taste (involvement of chorda tympani).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>MRI</strong> may reveal swelling and uniform <strong>enhancement</strong> of the <strong>geniculate ganglion</strong> and facial nerve.</p>\n<p>Approximately 80% of patients recover within a few weeks or months. The presence of incomplete paralysis in the first week is the most favorable prognostic sign. Recurrence may be seen in some cases.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7533","text":"Which of the following vir​uses is most commonly imp‍licated in Bell’s palsy?","raw_text":"Which of the following viruses is most commonly implicated in Bell’s palsy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytomeg​alovirus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes sim​plex virus","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Epstein-B​arr virus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Varicella z​oster virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Herpes simplex virus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Herpes simplex virus</strong></p><p><strong>Herpes simplex virus</strong> is most commonly implicated in causing Bell’s palsy.</p>\n<p>Varicella-zoster virus is the second most common cause.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7523","text":"Which of the following is ​true about Bell’s phenome‍non?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about Bell’s phenomenon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eyeballs roll upwards on a​ttempting to open the eye‍s","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eyeballs roll downwards on​ attempting to close the ‍eyes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is a normal phenomenon ​evident in facial paralys‍is","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It is an abnormal phenomen​on evident in facial para‍lysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It is a normal phenomenon evident in facial paralysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fa49a30cb746443a93f4cb1ab98e1dc9x1000x750.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is a normal phenomenon evident in facial paralysis</strong></p><p>Bell’s phenomenon refers to a <strong>normal phenomenon </strong>that becomes <strong>evident</strong> in <strong>facial paralysis</strong>. The eyeballs <strong>roll up</strong> and <strong>outwards</strong> on attempting to <strong>close</strong> <strong>the</strong> <strong>eyes</strong>.</p>\n<p>It<strong> </strong>is also known as the <strong>palpebral oculogyric reflex.</strong> It is a <strong>normal protective reflex</strong> resulting in the elevation of globes while blinking to protect the eyes from injury. It also serves to provide a wiper blade-like cleansing function of the cornea by the eyelid.</p>\n<p>However, it occurs behind the closed eyelids in healthy individuals. It becomes <strong>noticeable</strong> in <strong>Bell's palsy</strong> as the eyelids fail to close completely.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a patient with evident Bell’s phenomenon.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2881","text":"On returning from work, a ​man noticed that his moth‍er had developed mild rig​ht-sided facial drooping.‌ Believing it to be due t‍o a stroke, he immediatel​y took her to the hospita​l. After extensive evalua‍tion, the doctor ruled ou‍t stroke and diagnosed he‌r with Bell's palsy. Whic‌h of the following would ‍not be a part of the mana​gement of her condition?","raw_text":"On returning from work, a man noticed that his mother had developed mild right-sided facial drooping. Believing it to be due to a stroke, he immediately took her to the hospital. After extensive evaluation, the doctor ruled out stroke and diagnosed her with Bell's palsy. Which of the following would not be a part of the management of her condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paper tape to depress the ​upper eyelid during sleep‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prednisone 60-80 mg daily ​during the first 5 days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Massage of the weakened mu​scles","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acyclovir 1g twice daily f​or 10 days","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acyclovir 1g twice daily for 10 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acyclovir 1g twice daily for 10 days</strong></p><p><strong>Acyclovir</strong> may be considered as <strong>add-on</strong> therapy for patients with <strong>severe</strong> Bell's palsy as <strong>400mg five times</strong> daily for 10 days. Monotherapy is not recommended.</p>\n<p>Treatment of Bell's palsy involves <strong>prednisone</strong> 60-80 mg daily during the first <strong>5 days</strong> and then<strong> tapered</strong> over the next 5 days. Randomized trials found no added benefit of the antiviral agents valacyclovir and acyclovir when compared to glucocorticoids alone.</p>\n<p>The use of <strong>paper tape</strong> to depress the upper eyelid during sleep <strong>prevents corneal drying</strong>.</p>\n<p>For patients with <strong>permanent paralysis</strong>, cosmetic <strong>surgical procedures</strong> have been used to restore a relatively symmetric appearance to the face.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA0178","text":"A 35-year-old patient pres​ents with facial weakness‍ and pain for the past 2 ​days. The following signs‌ are seen on examination.‍ What is the cause?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old patient presents with facial weakness and pain for the past 2 days. The following signs are seen on examination. What is the cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"UMN facial ​nerve palsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"LMN facial ​nerve palsy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hemifaci​al spasm","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Facial s​ynkinesis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. LMN facial nerve palsy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b6c6b3a9c2f14d7ebafe360d156dd9a0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/75801651208d4393a6e41506f056d225x1280x2045.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. LMN facial nerve palsy</strong></p><p>The features are suggestive of lower motor neuron (<strong>LMN</strong>) type of <strong>facial palsy</strong>.</p>\n<p>In the image, the <strong>absence of wrinkling </strong>over the right side of the <strong>forehead</strong> and <strong>incomplete closure </strong>of the <strong>right eyelid</strong> is characteristic of LMN type of facial palsy. </p>\n<p>In upper motor neuron (<strong>UMN</strong>) lesion, the<strong> upper part </strong>of the face is<strong> spared </strong>due to bilateral UMN innervation. </p>\n<p>Features of facial nerve palsy:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>Feature</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>UMN  </strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>LMN</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Side affected</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Contralateral</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Ipsilateral</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Facial muscles involved</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Lower</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Both upper and lower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Forehead wrinkling</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Emotional incontinence</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Corneal reflex</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Taste</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Intact</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Affected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Atrophy of facial muscles</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note: Deviation of the angle of mouth should not be confused with drooping of the angle of mouth. <strong>Deviation</strong> of the angle of the mouth occurs on the <strong>normal side</strong> due to the action of unaffected facial muscles, while <strong>drooping</strong> occurs on the <strong>affected side</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2858","text":"Identify the correct type ​of facial nerve palsy in ‍the following patients ba​sed on their clinical fea‌tures: Patient 1 - Deviat‍ion of angle of mouth to ​left, eyelids completely ​closed and wrinkling of t‍he forehead. Patient 2 - ‍Deviation of angle of mou‌th to left, the right eye‌lid remains partially ope‍n, and no wrinkling of th​e forehead.","raw_text":"<p>Identify the correct type of facial nerve palsy in the following patients based on their clinical features:</p>\n<p>Patient 1 - Deviation of angle of mouth to left, eyelids completely closed and wrinkling of the forehead. </p>\n<p>Patient 2 - Deviation of angle of mouth to left, the right eyelid remains partially open, and no wrinkling of the forehead.</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 - Right LMN, 2 - Left UM​N","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 - Right UMN, 2 - Right L​MN","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1 - Left UMN, 2 - Right LM​N","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1 - Left LMN, 2 - Right UM​N","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1 - Left UMN, 2 - Right LMN","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cab0c677d4e74b98abac9b0611d89701x1280x2045.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1 - Left UMN, 2 - Right LMN</strong></p><p>Patient 1 has left UMN facial nerve palsy and patient 2 has right LMN facial nerve palsy.</p>\n<p>In an upper motor neuron (<strong>UMN</strong>) lesion,<strong> wrinkling </strong>on the<strong> forehead</strong> and<strong> eyelid closure</strong> remains <strong>intact</strong>. In this lesion, the <strong>contralateral</strong> side of the face is affected. The first-order neurons from the <strong>left cerebral hemisphere</strong> cross over (left to right) at the cortical level and relay in the <strong>right</strong> <strong>facial nerve</strong> nucleus in the <strong>pons</strong>. From here the second-order neurons (right facial nerve) descend and supply the <strong>right</strong> side of the face. </p>\n<p>Hence, a<strong> left UMN lesion</strong> causes paralysis of the <strong>lower half</strong> of the <strong>face</strong> on the <strong>right</strong> side. In facial palsy, the <strong>angle of the mouth</strong> deviates <strong>towards</strong> the <strong>normal</strong> side. In this patient, since the mouth deviates towards the left, the right facial nerve is affected. </p>\n<p>In lower motor neuron (<strong>LMN</strong>) lesions, there is an <strong>absence of wrinkling </strong>on the<strong> forehead</strong> and <strong>incomplete closure </strong>of the<strong> eyelid</strong> on the ipsilateral side of the face.</p>\n<p></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>Feature</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>UMN  </strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>LMN</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Side affected</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Contralateral</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Ipsilateral</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Facial muscles involved</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Lower</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Both upper and lower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Forehead wrinkling</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Emotional incontinence</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Corneal reflex</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Taste</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Intact</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Affected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Atrophy</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2876","text":"During the neurological ex​amination of a patient, y‍ou note that he has bilat​eral lower motor neuron p‌alsy of the facial nerve.‍ Which of the following i​s least likely to cause t​his?","raw_text":"During the neurological examination of a patient, you note that he has bilateral lower motor neuron palsy of the facial nerve. Which of the following is least likely to cause this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Guillain-Ba​rre syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sarco​idosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymp​homa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Diab​etes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Diabetes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Diabetes</strong></p><p>Diabetes is associated with <strong>unilateral</strong> LMN palsy.</p>\n<p>Common causes of facial nerve palsy:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 418px; width: 423px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95.7955px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.903px; text-align: center;\"><strong> UMN</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.105px; text-align: center;\"><strong> LMN</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95.7955px;\"><strong>Unilateral</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.903px;\">\n<p>Usually vascular</p>\n<p>Cerebral tumour</p>\n<p>Multiple sclerosis</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.105px;\">\n<p>Bell's palsy</p>\n<p>Parotid tumour</p>\n<p>Head injuries</p>\n<p>Skull base tumours</p>\n<p>Diabetes</p>\n<p>Hypertension</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 95.7955px;\"><strong>Bilateral</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 151.903px;\">\n<p>Vascular(infarct)</p>\n<p>Motor neuron disease</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 154.105px;\">\n<p>Guillain-Barré syndrome</p>\n<p>Sarcoidosis</p>\n<p>Leukemia</p>\n<p>Lymphoma</p>\n<p>Leprosy </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2868","text":"You are evaluating a woman​ with unilateral facial w‍eakness. However, you not​ice forehead wrinkling is‌ intact. On examination, ‍which of the following fi​ndings would you not expe​ct?","raw_text":"You are evaluating a woman with unilateral facial weakness. However, you notice forehead wrinkling is intact. On examination, which of the following findings would you not expect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Absent Bell'​s phenomenon","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Normal emoti​onal response","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Normal cor​neal reflex","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Normal tast​e sensation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Normal emotional response","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Normal emotional response</strong></p><p>The given features are suggestive of an upper motor neuron (<strong>UMN</strong>) lesion of the <strong>facial nerve </strong>in which the emotional response is affected. Patients with UMN lesions of the facial nerve have<strong> emotional incontinence </strong>i.e. involuntary, uncontrollable laughter, or crying with a paradoxical deficit of facial expression.</p>\n<p>Normally cerebellum modulates motor outputs in response to sensory inputs. <strong>UMN facial palsy</strong> (type of pseudobulbar palsy) is associated with disruption of <strong>cortico-ponto-cerebellar</strong> pathways leading to <strong>loss of cerebellar modulation</strong> resulting in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Emotional dysmetria (difficulty in fine-tuning emotional responses)</li>\n<li>Emotional disinhibition (excessive emotional output)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Difference between UMN and LMN facial palsy:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>Feature</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>UMN  </strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; height: 18px;\"><strong>LMN</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Side affected</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Contralateral</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Ipsilateral</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Facial muscles involved</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Lower</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Both upper and lower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Forehead wrinkling</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Emotional incontinence</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Present</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Corneal reflex</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Absent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Taste</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Intact</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Affected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Atrophy</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Absent</td>\n<td style=\"height: 18px;\">Present</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB7526","text":"Which of the following sta​tements is true about fac‍ial synkinesis?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about facial synkinesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Refers to simultaneous bil​ateral facial nerve palsy‍","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Refers to facial nerve pal​sy associated with trigem‍inal neuralgia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Occurs due to anomalous re​generation of the facial ‍nerve fibers","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Loss of emotional response​ is seen in this conditio‍n","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Occurs due to anomalous regeneration of the facial nerve fibers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Occurs due to anomalous regeneration of the facial nerve fibers</strong></p><p><strong>Facial synkinesis</strong> occurs due to <strong>anomalous regeneration </strong>of facial <strong>nerve fibers.</strong></p>\n<p>Following facial nerve injury , the recovering nerve fibers may aberrantly regenerate and supply other muscles. This results in involuntary muscular movements in the face during voluntary facial movements.</p>\n<p>Some<strong> examples </strong>include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Retraction of the mouth<strong> </strong>on closure of the eyelids:\n<ul>\n<li>Seen when fibers originally connected with the <strong>orbicularis oculi</strong> aberrantly innervate the <strong>orbicularis oris</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>'<strong>Crocodile tears</strong>' also known as Bogorad syndrome:\n<ul>\n<li>It refers to anomalous tearing occurring with activity of the facial muscles, such as eating.</li>\n<li>Due to aberrant innervation of the lacrimal gland.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>Frey's</strong> syndrome is an aberrant connection between the <strong>auriculotemporal</strong> nerve and the great auricular nerve, resulting in <strong>gustatory sweating</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2935","text":"Which of the following is ​false regarding hemifacia‍l spasm?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding hemifacial spasm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The contractions are painf​ul and irregular","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"It is a complication of Be​ll's palsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Carbamazepine is used for ​treatment","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Local injection of botulin​um toxin is effective","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The contractions are painful and irregular","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The contractions are painful and irregular</strong></p><p>Hemifacial spasms are <strong>painless</strong> irregular contractions occurring on one side of the face.</p>\n<p>They commonly occur as <strong>sequelae</strong> to <strong>Bell's palsy</strong>. It can also be seen in irritative lesions of the facial nerve such as acoustic neuroma and basilar aneurysm.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment</strong> is with <strong>carbamazepine</strong> or gabapentin. In resistant cases, baclofen may be tried. Local injection of <strong>botulinum toxin</strong> and surgical decompression are alternative options.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2933","text":"Which of the following are​ features of Melkerrson-R‍osenthal syndrome? Recurr​ent facial nerve palsy Co‌ngenital facial weakness ‍Facial edema Plication of​ tongue Vesicular rash in​ auditory canal","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are features of Melkerrson-Rosenthal syndrome?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Recurrent facial nerve palsy</li>\n<li>Congenital facial weakness</li>\n<li>Facial edema</li>\n<li>Plication of tongue</li>\n<li>Vesicular rash in auditory canal</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,​ 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, ​4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, ​3, 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ​3, 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/71797e9b455c4799809847660220b561x507x278.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3, 4</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Melkersson-Rosenthal syndrome</strong> is a rare disease characterised by the following <strong>triad</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Recurrent facial nerve palsy</li>\n<li>Facial edema (particularly labial)</li>\n<li>Plication of tongue or fissured tongue</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>Facial paralysis along with <strong>vesicular rash</strong> in the external <strong>auditory canal</strong> and pinna is seen in <strong>Ramsay-Hunt syndrome</strong>. It occurs due to <strong>herpes zoster</strong> infection and is also called herpes zoster oticus.</p>\n<p><strong>Congenital facial weakness</strong> combined with abnormal ocular abduction is seen in <strong>Moebius syndrome.</strong> Patients may present with bilateral concurrent facial nerve paralysis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2944","text":"A 70-year-old man presente​d with complaints of sudd‍en stabs of pain in the t​hroat on yawning. Each ep‌isode only lasted for a f‍ew seconds and was exacer​bated on trying to clear ​the throat. Which of the ‍following findings can yo‍u see on examination?","raw_text":"A 70-year-old man presented with complaints of sudden stabs of pain in the throat on yawning. Each episode only lasted for a few seconds and was exacerbated on trying to clear the throat. Which of the following findings can you see on examination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tongue deviated to one sid​e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of tas​te sensation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Brady​cardia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased mo​vement at TMJ","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bradycardia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bradycardia</strong></p><p>The clinical features are suggestive of <strong>glossopharyngeal neuralgia</strong> which is associated with cardiac symptoms such as <strong>bradycardia</strong>, asystole, hypotension, and fainting. </p>\n<p>It involves the glossopharyngeal (IX) and sometimes <strong>portions</strong> of the <strong>vagus</strong> (X) nerve. The condition resembles trigeminal neuralgia but is less common. There is no demonstrable sensory or motor deficit.</p>\n<p>It can result from vascular compression, <strong>multiple sclerosis</strong> or tumors. </p>\n<p>The drug of choice is <strong>carbamazepine</strong>. If unsuccessful, surgical procedures are indicated:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Microvascular decompression (if vascular compression is evident)</li>\n<li>Rhizotomy of glossopharyngeal and vagal fibers in the jugular bulb</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"BRAm2vnL","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"86_infections_of_the_nervous_system","title":"86.Infections Of The Nervous System","num_questions":12,"total_marks":48,"duration":22,"time_per_question":110.0,"questions":[{"id":"MD1331","text":"A 25-day-old baby is broug‌ht to the hospital with c​omplaints of poor feeding‌ and lethargy. On examina​tion, he is found to have​ bulging fontanelles and ‍hypothermia. Which of the‌ following is the most co​mmon cause of his conditi​on?","raw_text":"A 25-day-old baby is brought to the hospital with complaints of poor feeding and lethargy. On examination, he is found to have bulging fontanelles and hypothermia.  Which of the following is the most common cause of his condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Group B st‌reptococci","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"L. monoc‌ytogenes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"E. ‌coli","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"N. meni‌ngitidis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. E. coli","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. E. coli</strong></p><p>The clinical features are suggestive of <strong>neonatal meningitis</strong> and <strong>Escherichia coli </strong>is the <strong>most common cause </strong>of this condition.</p>\n<p>The causes of neonatal meningitis in decreasing order of frequency are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Escherichia coli</li>\n<li>Group B Streptococci</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>Listeria monocytogenes</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Empirical treatment</strong> of neonatal meningitis is with <strong>ampicillin + 3rd generation cephalosporins.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1332","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most likely cause of ​meningitis in a 30-year-o‌ld patient?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most likely cause of meningitis in a 30-year-old patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococcu‌s pneumoniae","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Neisseria m‌eningitidis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Haemophilus‌ influenzae","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Streptococcus pneumoniae","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Streptococcus pneumoniae</strong></p><p>The most common cause of meningitis in patients aged 2-50 years is <strong><em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em></strong>. </p>\n<p>The common causes of meningitis in this age group include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em></li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>Neisseria meningitidis</em></li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Group B streptococci</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>Hemophilus influenzae</em></li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>Listeria monocytogenes</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1333","text":"A 66-year-old patient pres‌ents with fever, neck rig​idity, and headache for t‌he past 3 days. He has a ​history of metastatic non​-small cell lung cancer f‍or which he had received ‌intrathecal chemotherapy ​via an Ommaya reservoir 1​0 days ago. On examinatio​n, Kernig and Brudzinski'‌s signs are positive. Whi​ch organism is most likel‌y implicated here?","raw_text":"A 66-year-old patient presents with fever, neck rigidity, and headache for the past 3 days. He has a history of metastatic non-small cell lung cancer for which he had received intrathecal chemotherapy via an Ommaya reservoir 10 days ago. On examination, Kernig and Brudzinski's signs are positive. Which organism is most likely implicated here?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococc‌us pneumonia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Coagulase negative staphyl‌ococci","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Staphyloco‌ccus aureus","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gram negat‌ive bacilli","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Staphylococcus aureus","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Staphylococcus aureus</strong></p><p>The clinical features are suggestive of <strong>acute</strong> <strong>bacterial meningitis.</strong> This condition is most likely caused by <em><strong>Staphylococcus aureus </strong></em>in a patient with an <strong>Ommaya reservoir</strong>.</p>\n<p>An Ommaya reservoir is an <strong>intraventricular catheter system</strong> used for the <strong>delivery of drugs</strong> into the cerebrospinal fluid. <em>S. aureus</em> and coagulase-negative staphylococci (CoNS) are important causes of meningitis that occurs following<strong> invasive neuro-surgical procedures</strong>, or as a complication of the use of Ommaya reservoirs. <em>S aureus</em> is a more common cause than CoNS.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1338","text":"A 45-year-old man presents‌ with fever, headache, ph​otophobia and neck rigidi‌ty since the last 2 days.​ He had undergone a heart​ transplant one year back‍ and is on immunomodulato‌ry drugs. During examinat​ion, the following sign i​s elicited. What is the t​herapy of choice for this‌ patient?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man presents with fever, headache, photophobia and neck rigidity since the last 2 days. He had undergone a heart transplant one year back and is on immunomodulatory drugs. During examination, the following sign is elicited. What is the therapy of choice for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cefepime + Vancomycin + Am‌picillin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Ceftriaxone ‌+ Vancomycin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Isoniazid + Rifampicin + P‌razinamide+ Ethambutol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Liposomal amphotericin B +‌ Fluorocytosine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cefepime + Vancomycin + Ampicillin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c6631b4d7a3243e5b57e4666d9c9d371.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cefepime + Vancomycin + Ampicillin</strong></p><p>The sign being elicited here is <strong>Kernig's sign</strong> and this along with the given clinical features are suggestive of <strong>acute bacterial meningitis</strong>. Empirical treatment with<strong> cefepime</strong>, <strong>vancomycin </strong>and<strong> ampicillin</strong> is recommended for patients on immunomodulatory drugs as they are at<strong> risk for Listeria</strong> infection.</p>\n<p><strong>Risk factors</strong> for <strong>Listeria</strong> <strong>meningitis</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Age&gt;50</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Diabetes</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Chronic illness</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Malignancy</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Immunosuppressive therapy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1335","text":"A 21-year-old female compl‌ains of fever, headache a​nd vomiting from the past‌ 3 days. She has been liv​ing in the army recruitme​nt camp for the last thre‍e weeks. On examination, ‌she has neck stiffness an​d a maculopapular rash wa​s noted over the trunk an​d extremities. Which of t‌he following is false abo​ut the disease she's like‌ly suffering from?","raw_text":"A 21-year-old female complains of fever, headache and vomiting from the past 3 days. She has been living in the army recruitment camp for the last three weeks. On examination, she has neck stiffness and a maculopapular rash was noted over the trunk and extremities. Which of the following is false about the disease she's likely suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carriers of this disease c‌an be treated with Rifamp​icin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It can lead to adrenal hem‌orrhage","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Terminal complement defici‌ency increases the risk o​f this disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Metronidazole is the drug ‌of choice","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Metronidazole is the drug of choice","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Metronidazole is the drug of choice</strong></p><p>The clinical features are suggestive of <strong>meningococcal meningitis</strong> and the <strong>drug of choice </strong>is<strong> </strong>a third-generation cephalosporin such as <strong>ceftriaxone</strong>. Chloramphenicol is used in patients with penicillin allergy. </p>\n<p>It is caused by <em>Neisseria meningitidis</em>, a gram-negative, oxidase-positive, catalase-positive diplococci. <strong>Outbreaks</strong> usually occur in <strong>mass gatherings</strong> such as in army camps or hostel dorms.</p>\n<p>The <strong>risk</strong> of infection is <strong>increased</strong> in patients with <strong>terminal complement deficiency</strong>, hypogammaglobulinemia and hyposplenism. This is because opsonization by complement is a major mechanism by which the bacteria is cleared from the blood.</p>\n<p>Characteristic clinical findings include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Maculopapular</strong> or purpuric <strong>rash</strong></li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Shock due to capillary leak</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Bilateral<strong> adrenal hemorrhage</strong> (Waterhouse–Friderichsen syndrome)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Rifampicin</strong>, ciprofloxacin and cephalosporins can be used to <strong>treat carriers</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9428","text":"A patient came with compla‌ints of high-grade fever ​and altered sensorium. He‌ was diagnosed to be suff​ering from meningococcal ​meningitis. Which of the ‍following is the most app‌ropriate empirical treatm​ent option?","raw_text":"A patient came with complaints of high-grade fever and altered sensorium. He was diagnosed to be suffering from meningococcal meningitis. Which of the following is the most appropriate empirical treatment option?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penici‌llin G","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ceftr‌iaxone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Cefo‌tetan","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cefo‌xitin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ceftriaxone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ceftriaxone</strong></p><p><strong>Ceftriaxone </strong>or<strong> cefotaxime</strong> is the drug of choice for<strong> empirical treatment </strong>of <strong>meningococcal meningitis</strong><em>.</em> However, Penicillin G remains the antibiotic of choice for susceptible strains.</p>\n<p>Empirical antimicrobial therapy is initiated in patients with suspected bacterial meningitis before the results of CSF Gram stain and culture are known.</p>\n<p><strong><em>S. pneumoniae</em></strong> and <strong><em>N. meningitidis</em></strong> are the <strong>most common etiologic</strong> organisms of community-acquired bacterial meningitis.<strong> </strong>Due to the <strong>emergence of penicillin-resistant</strong> and cephalosporin-resistant <em>S. pneumoniae</em>, empirical therapy of community-acquired suspected bacterial meningitis should include a combination of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dexamethasone</li>\n<li>Third- or fourth-generation cephalosporin (e.g., ceftriaxone, cefotaxime, or cefepime)</li>\n<li>Vancomycin</li>\n<li>Acyclovir - HSV encephalitis is the main differential</li>\n<li>Doxycycline -<strong> </strong>during tick season.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ceftriaxone</strong> or <strong>cefotaxime</strong> provides better coverage for susceptible <em>S. pneumoniae</em>, <em>H. influenzae</em> and group B streptococci and good coverage for <em>N. meningitidis</em>. <strong>Cefepime</strong> is a broad-spectrum fourth-generation cephalosporin with activity similar to that of cefotaxime or ceftriaxone against <em>S. pneumoniae </em>and<em> N. meningitidis</em> with additional greater activity against Enterobacter species and <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9426","text":"Which drug is used for mas‌s chemoprophylaxis for me​ningococcal meningitis?","raw_text":"Which drug is used for mass chemoprophylaxis for meningococcal meningitis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ciprof‌loxacin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chloram‌phenicol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tetrac‌ycline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Penic‌illin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Ciprofloxacin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Ciprofloxacin</strong></p><p><strong>Ciprofloxacin</strong> is used for <strong>chemoprophylaxis</strong> of <strong>meningococcal meningitis</strong> for <strong>close contacts</strong>. Ceftriaxone and rifampicin are also used.</p>\n<p>Antibiotic chemoprophylaxis among close contacts of a patient with invasive meningococcal disease is recommended to <strong>prevent secondary cases.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rifampin</strong> - was <strong>earlier used</strong> widely, but is <strong>not optimal</strong> due to the high risk of adverse events, emerging resistance, and the need for compliance for four doses.</li>\n<li><strong>Ciprofloxacin</strong> - widely used as a single oral dose, but it <strong>cannot be given</strong> in <strong>pregnancy</strong> and those <strong>below 18</strong> <strong>years</strong> of age.</li>\n<li><strong>Ceftriaxone</strong> - can be given in pregnancy and all ages as a <strong>single IM or IV dose</strong>. However, reduced susceptibility of isolates to ceftriaxone has occasionally been reported.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Recommended chemoprophylaxis for high-risk close contacts for meningococcal meningitis: </p>\n<table style=\"height: 306px; width: 415px; float: left;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 54px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 97px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Patient group</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 97px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Drug</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 53px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Age</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 61px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Dose</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px; height: 54px;\"><strong>Route and duration</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 76px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"3\" style=\"width: 97px; height: 164px;\">\n<p><strong>Non-pregnant individuals</strong></p>\n  </td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 97px; height: 104px;\">\n<p>Rifampicin</p>\n<p> </p>\n  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 53px; height: 76px;\"> &lt;1 month</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61px; height: 76px;\"> 5 mg/kg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px; height: 76px;\">Oral every 12 hrs for 2 days  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 28px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 53px; height: 28px;\">&gt;1 month </td>\n<td style=\"width: 61px; height: 28px;\">10 mg/kg </td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px; height: 28px;\">Oral every 12 hrs for 2 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 60px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 97px; height: 60px;\">\n<p>Ciprofloxacin </p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 53px; height: 60px;\"> &gt;18 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61px; height: 60px;\">500 mg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px; height: 60px;\">Oral single dose  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 56px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 97px; height: 109px;\">\n<p><strong>Children and pregnant women</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 97px; height: 109px;\">\n<p>Ceftriaxone</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 53px; height: 56px;\">&lt;15 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61px; height: 56px;\">125mg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px; height: 56px;\">IM single dose  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 53px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 53px; height: 53px;\">&gt; 15 years</td>\n<td style=\"width: 61px; height: 53px;\">250 mg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 74px; height: 53px;\">IM single dose</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1334","text":"A 45-year-old patient with‌ a history of fever for 1​ month presents with head‌ache and ataxia. Imaging ​reveals dilated ventricle​s and significant basal e‍xudates. Which of the fol‌lowing would most likely ​be seen in the CSF?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old patient with a history of fever for 1 month presents with headache and ataxia. Imaging reveals dilated ventricles and significant basal exudates. Which of the following would most likely be seen in the CSF?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymphocytosis, low glucose‌, high protein","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphocytosis, normal gluc‌ose, high protein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphocytosis, low glucose‌, normal protein","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymphocytosis, high glucos‌e, high protein","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lymphocytosis, low glucose, high protein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lymphocytosis, low glucose, high protein</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The features and imaging findings are suggestive of <strong>tuberculous meningitis</strong>. CSF analysis would likely show <strong>lymphocytosis, low glucose </strong>and<strong> high protein</strong>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">If the CSF is allowed to stand, a '<strong>spider’s web clot'</strong> or '<strong>cobweb'</strong> <strong>coagulam</strong> may form due to the high level of protein.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9159","text":"A patient comes to ED with‌ fever and headache. On e​xamination he has neck st‌iffness. CSF analysis was​ done and it shows the fo​llowing findings. Most li‍kely diagnosis is: Openin‌g pressure – Increased Pr​oteins- Mildly increased ​Glucose – Normal Lymphocy​tes – Increased.","raw_text":"<p>A patient comes to ED with fever and headache. On examination he has neck stiffness. CSF analysis was done and it shows the following findings. Most likely diagnosis is:</p>\n<p>Opening pressure – Increased</p>\n<p>Proteins- Mildly increased</p>\n<p>Glucose – Normal</p>\n<p>Lymphocytes – Increased.</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T‌B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Neisseria ‌Gonorrhoea","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Crypto‌coccus","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Coxs‌ackie","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Coxsackie","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Coxsackie</strong></p><p>The given symptoms cluster of <strong>fever</strong>, <strong>vomiting,</strong> and <strong>neck stiffness</strong> is suggestive of <strong>meningitis</strong>. A CSF analysis showing <strong>increased opening pressure</strong>, <strong>lymphocytic pleocytosi</strong>s, mildly <strong>increased protein</strong>, and <strong>normal glucose</strong> is suggestive of <strong>viral meningitis</strong>. Among the given options, <strong>Coxsackie</strong> (Option D) is the only viral cause of meningitis. </p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Tuberculous</strong> meningitis shows <strong>lymphocytic pleocytosis</strong> with <strong>low glucose</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Bacterial</strong> meningitis shows <strong>neutrophilic pleocytosis</strong> with <strong>low glucose</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Fungal</strong> meningitis shows <strong>lymphocytic/neutrophilic pleocytosis</strong> with <strong>low glucose</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5722","text":"A college student presents‌ with fever, headache, an​d confusion. Lumbar punct‌ure was positive for HSV ​DNA, detected by PCR. An ​MRI brain is done to loca‍lize the lesions. Which a‌rea on the brain is most ​likely to show lesions?","raw_text":"A college student presents with fever, headache, and confusion. Lumbar puncture was positive for HSV DNA, detected by PCR. An MRI brain is done to localize the lesions. Which area on the brain is most likely to show lesions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pariet‌al lobe","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fronta‌l lobe","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tempor‌al lobe","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cerebell‌ar cortex","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Temporal lobe","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e8c33e7dde164fd8adfa7d0badbcc4ddx480x512.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Temporal lobe</strong></p><p>The clinical hallmark of <strong>HSV encephalitis</strong> has been the acute onset of fever and focal neurologic symptoms and signs, especially in the <strong>temporal lobe</strong>.</p>\n<p>Investigation of choice includes <strong>HSV-DNA</strong> demonstration in CSF by <strong>PCR</strong>. </p>\n<p>The image below is a<strong> </strong><strong>coronal T2-weighted MR</strong> image showing a high signal intensity in the temporal lobes.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1337","text":"A 35-year-old man presents‌ with fever, headache, se​izures and aphasia. MRI s‌hows hyperintensity in th​e left frontotemporal reg​ion. Which of the followi‍ng would likely be seen i‌n the CSF analysis?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old man presents with fever, headache, seizures and aphasia. MRI shows hyperintensity in the left frontotemporal region. Which of the following would likely be seen in the CSF analysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucose &amp;l‌t; 20 mg/dl","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"0-5 WBC/m‌icroliter","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"100 RBC/ ‌microliter","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Protein &amp;l‌t; 40mg/dl","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 100 RBC/ microliter","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 100 RBC/ microliter</strong></p><p>The clinical features are suggestive of <strong>herpes simplex encephalitis </strong>and the CSF analysis would likely show <strong>100 RBC/microliter</strong>. HSV encephalitis is associated with <strong>hemorrhagic necrosis</strong> of inferomedial<strong> temporal and frontal lobe</strong> resulting in significant number of RBCs in the CSF.</p>\n<p>It is caused by both HSV 1 and HSV 2. Patients present with fever, headache, seizures, <strong>personality</strong>, and <strong>behavioral changes</strong>.</p>\n<p>CSF analysis shows lymphocytic pleocytosis, moderately elevated protein levels and normal glucose concentration. <strong>EEG</strong> shows <strong>periodic</strong> lateralized <strong>spike-wave</strong> discharges.</p>\n<p>The <strong>drug of choice</strong> for the condition is <strong>acyclovir</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD1339","text":"A 60-year-old man with pro‌gressive dementia of rece​nt onset presents with in‌termittent irregular jerk​y movements. The EEG is g​iven below. What is the m‍ost likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man with progressive dementia of recent onset presents with intermittent irregular jerky movements. The EEG is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HSV ence‌phalitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatic enc‌ephalopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cerebral to‌xoplasmosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cruetzfeldt-‌Jakob disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cruetzfeldt-Jakob disease","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6c0cc1a9eae540749eaa7b0820921828.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/13f465558a4645f180780e4fd08879f0x1280x1139.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cruetzfeldt-Jakob disease</strong></p><p>The clinical features and EEG point towards a diagnosis of <strong>Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease</strong> (CJD). The <strong>EEG</strong> shows <strong>periodic biphasic</strong> or <strong>triphasic </strong>sharp-wave complexes seen as <strong>spikes</strong>.</p>\n<p>CJD is a <strong>spongiform encephalopathy</strong> caused by a <strong>prion</strong>. It presents as <strong>rapidly progressive dementia</strong> and is <strong>fatal within 1 year</strong> of onset of symptoms.</p>\n<p>Patients present with <strong>myoclonus</strong>, <strong>akinetic mutism</strong>, cerebellar or visual disturbances and pyramidal/extrapyramidal dysfunction (hypokinesia). Insomnia, apathy and other behavioral abnormalities may be seen.</p>\n<p>Four types of Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease have been described:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Sporadic (sCJD): Accounts for 85-90% of cases\n<ul>\n<li>MRI shows <strong>cortical ribbon sign</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Variant (vCJD): mad cow disease, Kuru\n<ul>\n<li>MRI shows <strong>hockey stick sign</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Familial (fCJD): 10% of cases (these individuals carry a PRPc mutation)</li>\n<li>Iatrogenic</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>CSF</strong> analysis shows a positive assay for <strong>14-3-3 protein</strong>. Management is mainly supportive.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Hepatic encephalopathy</strong> will show a <strong>triphasic wave pattern</strong> (negative-positive-negative wave). They are also seen in Hashimoto encephalopathy and CO<sub>2</sub> narcosis. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Hej7GQdv","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"87_hypoproliferative_anemia","title":"87.Hypoproliferative Anemia","num_questions":22,"total_marks":88,"duration":32,"time_per_question":87.27,"questions":[{"id":"MB4436","text":"Which of the following is ​not a hypoproliferative a​nemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a hypoproliferative anemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sideroblas​tic anemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anemia of ch​ronic disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemolyti​c anemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Megaloblas​tic anemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hemolytic anemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hemolytic anemia</strong></p><p>Hemolytic anemia is not a hypoproliferative anemia.</p>\n<p><strong>Anemia</strong> can be defined as hemoglobin of <strong>&lt; 13</strong> g/dl in <strong>men</strong> and <strong>&lt; 12</strong> g/dl in <strong>women</strong>. Based on the mechanism of reduction of hemoglobin, anemia can be classified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypoproliferative anemias<strong> </strong>- due to impaired production of RBCs </li>\n<li><strong>Hemolytic </strong>anemias and hemoglobinopathies - due to increased <strong>destruction</strong> of RBCs</li>\n<li>Anemia due to blood loss</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hypoproliferative </strong>anemias are characterized by an abnormally <strong>low reticulocyte response</strong> (reticulocyte index &lt;2–2.5) to reduced RBC production. They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Iron-deficiency anemia</li>\n<li>Anemia of <strong>chronic disease</strong> (inflammation, renal disease, or malignancies)</li>\n<li>Thalassemia</li>\n<li><strong>Sideroblastic</strong> anemia</li>\n<li>Aplastic anemia</li>\n<li><strong>Megaloblastic</strong> macrocytic anemias (folate and vitamin B12 deficiency)</li>\n<li>Non-megaloblastic macrocytic anemias (alcohol use)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9396","text":"A 25-year-old woman came t​o the OPD one year after ​the birth of her last chi​ld. She had been treated ‌for iron deficiency anemi‌a while pregnant. On exam​ination, she is pale and ‌investigations show a Hb ‍of 5g/dl and a reticulocy‌te count of 9%. What is h‌er corrected reticulocyte‌ count? (Take normal Hb a‌s 15g/dl)","raw_text":"A 25-year-old woman came to the OPD one year after the birth of her last child. She had been treated for iron deficiency anemia while pregnant. On examination, she is pale and investigations show a Hb of 5g/dl and a reticulocyte count of 9%. What is her corrected reticulocyte count? (Take normal Hb as 15g/dl)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​6","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"4​.5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"​1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 3","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 3</strong></p><p>The corrected reticulocyte count (CRC) of this patient is <strong>3</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>CRC = Reticulocyte %  x  (Actual Hb / Normal Hb)</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<div>Plugging in the values given in the question,</div>\n<div>CRC = 9% x 5/15 = 3%</div>\n</blockquote>\n<div>Alternatively, CRC can be calculated using hematocrit (PCV) as follows:</div>\n<div><strong>CRC = Reticulocyte %  x  (Actual PCV / Normal PCV)</strong></div>\n<p>For these calculations, take normal Hb as 15g/dL and normal PCV as 45%.</p>\n<p>The patient's hemoglobin level (5 g/dL) is significantly lower than the normal hemoglobin level (15 g/dL). To obtain an accurate assessment of <strong>reticulocyte</strong> production <strong>relative to</strong> the reduced number of circulating <strong>red blood cells</strong>, a correction factor has to be applied. CRC accounts for the anemia and provides a more precise estimate of the reticulocyte count.</p>\n<p>When <strong>polychromatophilic macrocytes </strong>(<strong>prematurely released</strong> reticulocytes/<strong>shift cells</strong>) are observed in the peripheral blood smear, a second <strong>correction</strong> is applied to account for the <strong>longer life of reticulocytes </strong>to yield <strong>reticulocyte production index </strong>(RPI). </p>\n<p>In the classification of anemia, the patient's reticulocyte count is compared to the expected reticulocyte response. Reticulocytes can be identified by staining their <strong>ribosomal RNA</strong> with a <strong>supravital dye</strong>, resulting in the formation of blue or black punctate spots. They can be quantified manually or by using fluorescent dyes. The presence of reticulocytes indicates <strong>active red cell production</strong>.</p>\n<p>The premature release of <strong>reticulocytes</strong> is typically associated with <strong>increased EPO</strong> stimulation. In <strong>hemolytic anemia</strong>, red cell production can <strong>increase</strong> six to seven times. If the <strong>RPI &lt; 2</strong> in a patient with anemia, a <strong>defect in erythroid marrow</strong> proliferation or <strong>maturation</strong> has to be suspected.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC9395","text":"What does the red cell dis​tribution width deal with​?","raw_text":"What does the red cell distribution width deal with?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypoc​hromia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anisoc​ytosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Poikilo​cytosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anisoc​hromia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anisocytosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anisocytosis</strong></p><p><strong>Red cell distribution width</strong> (RDW) measures the <strong>range of red blood cell volumes</strong> and provides an assessment of their <strong>variability</strong> (anisocytosis).</p>\n<p>The <strong>normal</strong> range of RDW is <strong>11-14%</strong>. In the presence of uneven cell size (anisocytosis), the RDW increases to 15-18%.</p>\n<p>RDW has clinical utility in two main scenarios:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In cases of <strong>microcytic anemia</strong> (low MCV), it helps <strong>differentiate</strong> between <strong>iron deficiency and thalassemia</strong>. Thalassemia is characterized by uniformly small red blood cells with a normal RDW, while iron deficiency leads to greater size variability and an increased RDW. </li>\n<li>A <strong>high RDW</strong> can indicate <strong>dimorphic anemia</strong> caused by chronic atrophic gastritis. Vitamin B12 malabsorption results in macrocytic anemia and blood loss leads to iron deficiency (microcytic anemia). </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4442","text":"Which of the following pat​ients is unlikely to have​ the following finding on​ a peripheral smear study‌?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients is unlikely to have the following finding on a peripheral smear study?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 55-year-old alcoholic ma​n with ascites and jaundi​ce","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 19-year-old girl who und​erwent emergency splenect​omy after a road accident​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 25-year-old man with hem​oglobin C disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 70-year-old woman underg​oing dialysis for the pas​t 3 years","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A 70-year-old woman undergoing dialysis for the past 3 years","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/86b86d9a3b3b4c4a982216e933147966.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A 70-year-old woman undergoing dialysis for the past 3 years</strong></p><p>The black arrows in the given image denote <strong>target cells </strong>or<strong> codocytes.</strong> They are RBCs with a dense center (<strong>hyperchromic</strong> <strong>bull's eye</strong>) surrounded by an area of pallor. They are not a feature of renal disease (Patient D). </p>\n<p>Target cells are seen in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thalassemia</li>\n<li>Iron deficiency anemia</li>\n<li><strong>Liver disease</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Post-splenectomy</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hemoglobin C</strong> disease</li>\n<li>As artifacts in improperly prepared slides</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4443","text":"A first-year pathology res​ident prepares the follow​ing smear from a patient'​s blood sample. Which of ‌the following is it sugge‌stive of?","raw_text":"A first-year pathology resident prepares the following smear from a patient's blood sample. Which of the following is it suggestive of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thala​ssemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autoimmune​ hemolysis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Iron defici​ency anaemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lead po​isoning","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Autoimmune hemolysis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/04b01563c72e4d0c9b908173126ca460.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Autoimmune hemolysis</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>The small, dense RBCs with <strong>absent central pallor</strong> seen in the given peripheral smear are called <strong>spherocytes</strong>. They appear due to decreased membrane surface area and are seen in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Autoimmune hemolytic anemia</strong></li>\n<li>Hereditary spherocytosis</li>\n<li>Clostridial sepsis</li>\n<li>Snake bites</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB9935","text":"The following finding is n​ot typically seen in whic​h of the following condit​ions?","raw_text":"The following finding is not typically seen in which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myelody​splasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myelof​ibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thala​ssemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sideroblas​tic anemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Sideroblastic anemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/82e5f60b36044a2eb45bd823b27fe0ee.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Sideroblastic anemia</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">The given peripheral smear shows <strong>teardrop cells</strong> or <strong>dacrocytes</strong>. They are not seen in sideroblastic anemia.</p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Teardrop cells are seen in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Myelofibrosis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Myelodysplastic</strong> syndromes</li>\n<li>Hemolytic anemia</li>\n<li><strong>Thalassemia</strong></li>\n<li>Severe iron deficiency</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4444","text":"The following blood pictur​e is seen in patients wit​h which of the following ​conditions? Sickle cell d‌isease Thalassemia Megalo‌blastic anemia Lead poiso​ning Dyshematopoiesis","raw_text":"<p>The following blood picture is seen in patients with which of the following conditions?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Sickle cell disease</li>\n<li>Thalassemia</li>\n<li>Megaloblastic anemia</li>\n<li>Lead poisoning</li>\n<li>Dyshematopoiesis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ​2, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, ​4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1, ​3, 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1, ​3, 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 1, 3, 5","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1e5bf92af86f4e4780f8ba396e075ad5.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/30755342e8584676a55db3049f6ba1c7x700x468.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 1, 3, 5</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p>The given image shows <strong>Howell-Jolly bodies</strong>. They are <strong>remnants of RBC nuclei</strong> that<strong> </strong>occur in conditions with either defective production (dysplasia) or destruction of RBCs. They are observed in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sickle cell disease (postsplenectomy)</strong></li>\n<li>Hyposplenism</li>\n<li><strong>Megaloblastic</strong> anemia</li>\n<li><strong>Dyshematopoesis</strong></li>\n<li>Hemolytic anemia</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Thalassemia</strong> presents with microcytic, hypochromic RBCs, and <strong>target</strong> <strong>cells</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Lead poisoning</strong> presents with <strong>basophilic stippling</strong> or punctate basophilia on peripheral smears<strong>.</strong> These are <strong>RNA fragments</strong> seen as numerous basophilic granules dispersed in the cytoplasm of RBCs, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4472","text":"A 30-year-old man with a B​MI of 41 kg/m2 is being e​valuated for bariatric su​rgery. Which of the follo‌wing procedures does not ‌reduce the surface area f​or iron absorption?","raw_text":"A 30-year-old man with a BMI of 41 kg/m2 is being evaluated for bariatric surgery. Which of the following procedures does not reduce the surface area for iron absorption?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duodena​l switch","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sleeve ga​strectomy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Roux-en-Y ga​stric bypass","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Biliopancrea​tic diversion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Sleeve gastrectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/74c32a6959fd4c878adb545688406d21x1279x1661.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b763810aa0124fb7b0069e07a9517bbfx1280x1641.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Sleeve gastrectomy</strong></p><p><strong>Iron absorption</strong> takes place in the <strong>duodenum</strong>. In <strong>sleeve gastrectomy</strong>, a portion of the stomach is resected but the <strong>duodenum</strong> remains <strong>intact</strong>. Hence, the surface area of iron absorption is not affected.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Dietary iron is efficiently absorbed in its <strong>heme</strong> form. Non-heme iron is less efficiently absorbed mainly because of interference by phosphates, tannins, oxalates, and other food constituents. <strong>Acidic</strong> pH enhances iron absorption by promoting the conversion of <strong>ferric to ferrous</strong> iron by the enzyme <strong>ferrireductase</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The following are malabsorptive procedures that reduce the duodenal surface area and hence, reduce iron absorption:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Biliopancreatic diversion</li>\n<li>Roux-en-Y gastric bypass</li>\n<li>Duodenal switch</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4475","text":"You have prescribed oral i​ron supplements for an an​emic young woman. Which o​f the following foods sho‌uld she avoid taking alon‌g with these supplements?​","raw_text":"You have prescribed oral iron supplements for an anemic young woman. Which of the following foods should she avoid taking along with these supplements?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Grapefru​it juice","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fresh ki​wi fruit","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Broc​coli","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Blac​k tea","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Black tea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Black tea</strong></p><p><strong>Tea</strong> contains <strong>tannins</strong>, which <strong>inhibit iron absorption</strong>. Hence, it should not be taken with iron supplements. Iron absorption is also reduced by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Calcium supplements and antacids</li>\n<li>Milk and dairy products</li>\n<li>Proton pump inhibitors</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Grapefruit and kiwi are acid-containing citrus fruits that create an <strong>acidic pH</strong> and hence,<strong> increase iron absorption</strong>. </p>\n<p>Broccoli contains <strong>ascorbic acid</strong> that also enhances iron absorption.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4488","text":"The levels of which of the​ following will be detect​ed first in a patient wit​h iron deficiency?","raw_text":"The levels of which of the following will be detected first in a patient with iron deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Marrow ir​on stores","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Serum​ iron","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serum f​erritin","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Transferrin​ saturation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Serum ferritin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Serum ferritin</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">The <strong>earliest</strong> sign of iron deficiency is a <strong>decrease </strong>in<strong> serum ferritin</strong> levels. This is called the state of negative iron balance.</p>\n<p>Iron deficiency anemia develops in 3 stages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Stage of <strong>negative iron balance</strong> - decrease in iron stores with normal serum iron and RBC morphology</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Stage of <strong>iron-deficient erythropoiesis</strong> - decrease in serum iron levels with normal RBC morphology</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Stage of <strong>iron deficiency anemia</strong> - fall in <strong>hemoglobin</strong> with microcytic hypochromic RBCs</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4486","text":"Which of the following is ​not seen in the stage of ​negative iron balance in ​iron-deficiency anemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in the stage of negative iron balance in iron-deficiency anemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrease in marrow iron st​ores","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decrease​ in TIBC","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Normal RBC​ morphology","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Normal s​erum iron","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Decrease in TIBC","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Decrease in TIBC</strong></p><p><strong>TIBC</strong> (total iron-binding capacity) <strong>does not decrease</strong> during the stage of a negative iron balance of iron deficiency anemia.</p>\n<p>The progression to iron deficiency develops in 3 stages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Stage of negative iron balance</strong> - Decrease in iron stores with normal serum iron and RBC morphology</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Stage of iron-deficient erythropoiesis</strong> - Decrease in serum iron levels with normal RBC morphology</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Stage of iron deficiency anemia</strong> - Microcytic hypochromic RBCs</li>\n</ul>\n<table style=\"width: 501.35418701171875px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\">Negative iron balance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Iron-deficient erythropoiesis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.35418701171875px;\">Iron deficiency anemia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">Marrow iron stores</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\"><strong>Decreased</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Decreased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.35418701171875px;\">Decreased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">S. ferritin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\"><strong>Decreased</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Decreased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.35418701171875px;\">Decreased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">TIBC</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\"><strong>Increased</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Increased</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.35418701171875px;\">Increased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">S. Iron</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\"><strong>Decreased</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.35418701171875px;\">Decreased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">Transferrin saturation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\"><strong>Decreased</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.35418701171875px;\">Decreased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">Red cell protoporphyrin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\"><strong>Increased</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.35418701171875px;\">Increased</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 108px;\">Red cell morphology</td>\n<td style=\"width: 105px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 115px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 150.35418701171875px;\"><strong>Microcytic hypochromic</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4487","text":"Patients with which of the​ following do not need to​ be evaluated for iron de​ficiency?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following do not need to be evaluated for iron deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unusual craving to eat cla​y and paper","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Insomnia due to a compulsi​ve urge to keep moving th​e legs","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Incidental finding of esop​hageal webs on upper GI e​ndoscopy","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tingling and numbness of l​ower extremities","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Tingling and numbness of lower extremities","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Tingling and numbness of lower extremities</strong></p><p>Tingling and numbness of the lower extremities are suggestive of <strong>peripheral neuropathy</strong>. It is a feature of vitamin <strong>B<sub>12</sub> deficiency</strong>, not iron deficiency. </p>\n<p><strong>Iron-deficiency anemia</strong> is associated with the following findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Smooth tongue - due to loss of papillae</li>\n<li>Cheilosis - fissuring at corners of mouth</li>\n<li>Brittle nails</li>\n<li>Koilonychia - spooning of nails</li>\n<li><strong>Pica</strong> - craving for non-edible foods like ice chips, hair, paper, clay</li>\n<li><strong>Plummer–Vinson</strong> syndrome - <strong>dysphagia</strong> due to the formation of <strong>esophageal webs</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Restless legs syndrome</strong> - compulsive urge to keep moving the legs which results in insomnia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4489","text":"Which of the following PBF​ findings is least likely​ to be seen in patients w​ith iron deficiency anemi‌a?","raw_text":"Which of the following PBF findings is least likely to be seen in patients with iron deficiency anemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fe96316e4893406abeab921f3a229fcc.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/79e8a00a2c01499faaed2a29caaf5eeex1280x1905.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4</strong></p><div class=\"ng-scope\">\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">Image 4 shows dense RBCs with loss of central pallor or<strong> spherocytes. </strong>They are seen in autoimmune hemolytic anemia (<strong>AIHA</strong>) and <strong>hereditary spherocytosis</strong>, not iron deficiency anemia.</p>\n</div>\n<p></p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\"> </p>\n<p class=\"ng-binding\">The peripheral blood smear in <strong>iron deficiency anemia</strong> shows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Microcytic <strong>hypochromic</strong> cells</li>\n<li><strong>Target</strong> cells</li>\n<li><strong>Pencil-shaped</strong> or cigar-shaped cells</li>\n<li>Poikilocytosis- variations in the shape of RBCs</li>\n<li>Anisocytosis- variations in the size of RBCs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4490","text":"A 28-year-old woman is bro​ught to the ER after she ​faints at home. Her husba​nd says that she is very ‌body-conscious and does i‌ntermittent fasting. She ​has been on a vegan diet ‌for the past 2 months. La‍b values are given below.‌ What is the most likely ‌diagnosis? Hb - 6 g/dL MC‌V - 67 fL MCH - 24 pg RDW‌ - elevated TIBC - high P‌eripheral smear - target ‌cells noted","raw_text":"<p>A 28-year-old woman is brought to the ER after she faints at home. Her husband says that she is very body-conscious and does intermittent fasting. She has been on a vegan diet for the past 2 months. Lab values are given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Hb - 6 g/dL</p>\n<p>MCV - 67 fL</p>\n<p>MCH - 24 pg</p>\n<p>RDW - elevated</p>\n<p>TIBC - high</p>\n<p>Peripheral smear - target cells noted</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anemia of ch​ronic disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"B12 defici​ency anemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Iron defici​ency anemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Folate defic​iency anemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Iron deficiency anemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Iron deficiency anemia</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>iron deficiency anemia</strong> due to the following findings:<strong> </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Low MCV</strong> (N: 87-96 fL) </li>\n<li><strong>Low MCH</strong> (N: 27-33 pg)</li>\n<li><strong>Elevated RDW</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>High TIBC</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Anemia of chronic disease is associated with low TIBC.</p>\n<p>Options B and D: Anemia due to B12 and folate deficiency presents with a high MCV (macrocytosis).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4493","text":"A 62-year-old woman is fou​nd to have a Hb of 7.2 g/​dl and high ferritin leve​ls. She has been undergoi‌ng chemotherapy for breas‌t cancer since last year.​ Which of the following i‌s responsible for this pr‍esentation?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old woman is found to have a Hb of 7.2 g/dl and high ferritin levels. She has been undergoing chemotherapy for breast cancer since last year. Which of the following is responsible for this presentation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ferr​itin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"DM​T1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemoj​uvelin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepc​idin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hepcidin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hepcidin</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>anemia of chronic disease</strong>. The chief mediator of this condition is <strong>hepcidin</strong>.</p>\n<p>Anemia of chronic disease is associated with <strong>chronic inflammatory</strong> states such as inflammatory bowel disease, rheumatoid arthritis, chronic infections, and malignancies. </p>\n<p>Hepcidin is a <strong>positive </strong>acute-phase reactant (<strong>ARP</strong>) that increases in chronic inflammatory states. It binds with ferroportin which results in both reduced iron intake in the gut and reduced iron transfer from macrophages to erythroid progenitor cells in the bone marrow. Hence, hepcidin is a <strong>negative regulator </strong>of <strong>iron balance</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4468","text":"Normocytic normochromic an​emia is seen in patients ​with which of the followi​ng?","raw_text":"Normocytic normochromic anemia is seen in patients with which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liver ​disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kidney ​disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lead t​oxicity","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thala​ssemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Kidney disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Kidney disease</strong></p><p><strong>Normocytic normochromic</strong> anemia is seen in chronic kidney disease (<strong>CKD</strong>).</p>\n<p>It is also seen in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>RBC membrane disorders - hereditary spherocytosis, elliptocytosis</li>\n<li>RBC enzyme disorders - G6PD deficiency, Pyruvate kinase deficiency</li>\n<li>Parvovirus B19 infection</li>\n<li>Anemia of chronic disease - can also present as<strong> </strong>microcytic anemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4492","text":"A 38-year-old woman presen​ted to the OPD with progr​essive tiredness over the​ last 2 months. She also ‌reported having heavy men‌strual bleeding during he​r previous 2 cycles. She ‌is a known case of rheuma‍toid arthritis. Lab inves‌tigations are given below‌. What is the likely diag‌nosis? Hb - 7 gm/dL MCV -‌ 55 fL TIBC - 290 μg/dL S‌. Ferritin - 250 μg/L Tra‌nsferrin saturation - 20%​","raw_text":"<p>A 38-year-old woman presented to the OPD with progressive tiredness over the last 2 months. She also reported having heavy menstrual bleeding during her previous 2 cycles. She is a known case of rheumatoid arthritis. Lab investigations are given below. What is the likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Hb - 7 gm/dL</p>\n<p>MCV - 55 fL</p>\n<p>TIBC - 290 μg/dL</p>\n<p>S. Ferritin - 250 μg/L</p>\n<p>Transferrin saturation - 20%</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iron defici​ency anemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anemia of ch​ronic disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sideroblas​tic anemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anemia due t​o blood loss","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anemia of chronic disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anemia of chronic disease</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>anemia of chronic disease </strong>due to the following findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Low MCV (N: 87-96 fl)</li>\n<li>Low TIBC (N: 300-360μg/dL)</li>\n<li>Elevated ferritin level (N: 50-200 μg/L)</li>\n<li>Normal transferrin saturation (N: 25-50%)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4491","text":"A 60-year-old man with sta​ge 4 chronic kidney disea​se on hemodialysis is fou​nd to have a hemoglobin o‌f 6.3 mg/dl. Which of the‌ following will be additi​onally seen in this patie‌nt?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man with stage 4 chronic kidney disease on hemodialysis  is found to have a hemoglobin of 6.3 mg/dl. Which of the following will be additionally seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased se​rum ferritin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increas​ed TIBC","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased ​serum iron","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased r​eticulocytes","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Decreased reticulocytes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Decreased reticulocytes</strong></p><p>Anemia of <strong>CKD</strong> is a form of hypoproliferative anemia presenting with <strong>decreased reticulocytes</strong>.</p>\n<p>It mainly occurs due to a <strong>failure</strong> of<strong> erythropoietin </strong>production by the diseased kidney and a reduction in the survival of RBCs. Also, patients with CKD maintained on chronic hemodialysis may develop iron deficiency from <strong>blood loss</strong> through the <strong>dialysis</strong> procedure.</p>\n<p>The following features are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Normocytic</strong> and <strong>normochromic</strong> RBCs</li>\n<li>Decreased reticulocytes</li>\n<li>Serum iron, transferrin saturation, TIBC are usually normal or low</li>\n<li>Decreased serum ferritin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The degree of anemia usually correlates with the severity of the renal failure. </p>\n<p>Note: Anaemia in CKD is not the same as anaemia of chronic disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4495","text":"What is the predominant in​heritance pattern of side​roblastic anemia?","raw_text":"What is the predominant inheritance pattern of sideroblastic anemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal​ dominant","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Poly​genic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"X li​nked","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Autosomal​ recessive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. X linked","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. X linked</strong></p><p>Inherited forms of <strong>sideroblastic anemia</strong> are usually <strong>X-linked.</strong> Rarely, autosomal recessive forms may also occur.</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 3041\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>X-linked sideroblastic anemia results from the deficient activity of the erythroid form of <strong>ALA-synthase</strong> (ALA-synthase 2) and is associated with ineffective erythropoiesis</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4497","text":"Which of the following wou​ld not be seen in patient​s with sideroblastic anem​ia?","raw_text":"Which of the following would not be seen in patients with sideroblastic anemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erythroid ​hyperplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Low ser​um iron","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"High transferrin saturatio​n","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dimorphic b​lood picture","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Low serum iron","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Low serum iron</strong></p><p><strong>High serum iron </strong>levels are seen in patients with <strong>sideroblastic anemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Sideroblastic anemia presents with the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Serum iron level - high/normal</li>\n<li><strong>Transferrin</strong> <strong>saturation</strong> - <strong>high</strong></li>\n<li>Serum ferritin levels - high</li>\n<li>TIBC - low/normal</li>\n<li>Peripheral smear - <strong>dimorphic population</strong> (both microcytes and macrocytes).</li>\n<li>Bone marrow study - marked <strong>erythroid hyperplasia.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The iron profile of sideroblastic anemia is opposite to that of iron deficiency anemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4498","text":"The bone marrow biopsy sam​ple of a patient with chr​onic anemia is stained wi​th Prussian blue, as show‌n below. The stained depo‌sits are present in which​ of the following?","raw_text":"The bone marrow biopsy sample of a patient with chronic anemia is stained with Prussian blue, as shown below. The stained deposits are present in which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lyso​some","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ribo​some","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mitoch​ondria","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Endoplasmi​c reticulum","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mitochondria","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3506aa553aff4185a2225906159f3ecf.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8e32f789df744f87820cc3cf873c6424x961x720.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mitochondria</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>ringed sideroblasts</strong>, which are erythroid precursors in the<strong> marrow</strong>. In these cells, <strong>iron deposits</strong> in the <strong>m</strong><strong>itochondria</strong> form a ring around the nucleus and are stained by Prussian blue.</p>\n<div class=\"description mw-content-ltr en\" dir=\"ltr\" lang=\"en\">The mature red blood cell counterparts of ringed sideroblasts in the <strong>circulation</strong> are called <strong>siderocytes</strong>. These cells have iron-containing basophilic granules called <strong>Pappenheimer bodies, </strong>as shown below.</div>\n<p></p>\n<p>Ringed sideroblasts and Pappenheimer bodies are pathognomonic of sideroblastic anemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4496","text":"Which of the following dru​gs is known to cause side​roblastic anemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is known to cause sideroblastic anemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclopho​sphamide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chloram​phenicol","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Ethio​namide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rifam​picin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chloramphenicol","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chloramphenicol</strong></p><p><strong>Chloramphenicol</strong> is known to cause sideroblastic anemia.</p>\n<p>The agents known to cause sideroblastic anemia are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Isoniazid</li>\n<li>Chloramphenicol</li>\n<li>Linezolid</li>\n<li>Excessive alcohol</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"FkplKW4I","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"88_macrocytic_anemia","title":"88.Macrocytic Anemia","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MB4423","text":"The peripheral blood smear‌ of a patient is given be‍low. All of the following​ can cause this except:","raw_text":"The peripheral blood smear of a patient is given below. All of the following can cause this except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitamin B12‌ deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic a‌lcoholism","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Folate d‌eficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Orotic ‌aciduria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Chronic alcoholism","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/efa17d60483d4d6a8921ec0607d93f61.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/255842ca896a466cb7ee3efb50ba23cbx610x600.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/06cdef378c9e47a691a35cdd44420e7ax900x613.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Chronic alcoholism</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>hypersegmented</strong> <strong>neutrophils </strong>(&gt; 5 nuclear lobes), which are seen in <strong>megaloblastic</strong> anemia. <strong>Chronic alcoholism</strong> causes <strong>non-megaloblastic</strong> macrocytic anemia.</p>\n<p>Megaloblastic anemia is a form of macrocytic anemia with impaired nucleic acid metabolism and <strong>defective</strong> <strong>erythropoiesis</strong>. The causes include vitamin B12 deficiency, folate deficiency, and orotic aciduria. <strong>Nuclear</strong> <strong>maturation lags</strong> behind cytoplasmic maturation giving the appearance of hypersegmented neutrophils. Peripheral smear findings include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Oval macrocytes</li>\n<li>Anisocytosis</li>\n<li>Hypersegmented neutrophils </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other features of <strong>ineffective erythropoiesis</strong> seen in megaloblastic anemias include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Howell-Jolly bodies </strong>are nuclear remnants in red cells. They are usually single, round, dark purple-red in color and peripheral in location, as shown below.                                            </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Basophilic stippling</strong> refers to blue granules of various sizes dispersed throughout the cytoplasm of the red cell. They represent ribosomal precipitates.</li>\n<li><strong>Cabot rings</strong> are red cell inclusions appearing as fine, purple filamentous loops or figure-of-eight forms, as shown below. They are presumed to be remnants of mitotic spindles.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4415","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following conditions are‍ unlikely to develop coba​lamin deficiency?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions are unlikely to develop cobalamin deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tropica‌l sprue","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Crohn's‌ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diphyllobothrium latum inf‌ection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ancylostoma duodenale infe‌ction","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ancylostoma duodenale infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ancylostoma duodenale infection</strong></p><p><em><strong>Ancylostoma duodenale</strong></em> (hookworm) infection does not affect the absorption of cobalamin or vitamin B12. Small amounts of blood loss during the attachment of hookworms to the intestinal mucosa can cause <strong>iron-deficiency anemia</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Vitamin B12-</strong>intrinsic factor (IF) complex is absorbed in the <strong>ileum</strong>. Tropical sprue, <em>Diphyllobothrium latum</em><strong> </strong>(fish tapeworm) infection and Crohn's disease affect the ileum leading to defective absorption of vitamin B12.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4425","text":"Which of the following wou‌ld not be seen in a patie‍nt with cyanocobalamin de​ficiency?","raw_text":"Which of the following would not be seen in a patient with cyanocobalamin deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glos‌sitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Conjugated hyperbilirubine‌mia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Skin hyperp‌igmentation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mild ‌fever","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia</strong></p><p><strong>Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia</strong> is seen in cyanocobalamin or vitamin <strong>B12</strong> <strong>deficiency</strong>, not conjugated hyperbilirubinemia.</p>\n<p>Vitamin B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia<strong> </strong>due to <strong>ineffective erythropoiesis</strong>. This leads to the formation of nucleated RBCs in the marrow, which undergo <strong>early lysis</strong>. This leads to increased unconjugated bilirubin in the plasma.</p>\n<p>The clinical features of cyanocobalamin and folate deficiency include:</p>\n<table style=\"width: 297px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.28125px;\"><strong>System</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.71875px;\"><strong>Clinical features</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.28125px;\">Oral</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.71875px;\">\n<p><strong>Glossitis</strong></p>\n<p>Angular cheilosis</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.28125px;\">Gastrointestinal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.71875px;\">\n<p>Anorexia</p>\n<p>Weight loss</p>\n<p>Diarrhea</p>\n<p>Constipation</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.28125px;\">Skin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.71875px;\"><strong>Hyperpigmentation</strong> (reversible)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.28125px;\">Hematological</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.71875px;\">\n<p>Thrombocytopenia</p>\n<p>Leucopenia</p>\n<p>Ineffective erythropoiesis and unconjugated<strong> </strong>jaundice</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.28125px;\">Cardiovascular</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.71875px;\">\n<p>Atherosclerosis leading to PVD, CAD (due to elevated homocysteine)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.28125px;\">Neurological (only B12)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.71875px;\">\n<p>Peripheral neuropathy</p>\n<p>Subacute combined degeneration (degeneration of the posterior and pyramidal tracts of the spinal cord)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.28125px;\">Pregnancy (only folate)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.71875px;\">\n<p>Recurrent fetal loss</p>\n<p>Neural tube defects</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118.28125px;\">Others</td>\n<td style=\"width: 186.71875px;\">\n<p><strong>Mild</strong> <strong>fever</strong> in severe cases</p>\n<p>Psychiatric disturbances</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4413","text":"A 56-year-old male patient‌ is being evaluated for p‍eripheral neuropathy and ​excessive tiredness. He h​as uncontrolled diabetes ‌and consumes alcohol near​ly every day. Lab investi​gations reveal the follow​ing findings. What is the​ likely diagnosis? Hemogl​obin - 9g/dl Mean corpusc​ular volume (MCV) - 112 f‌L Mean corpuscular hemogl‌obin concentration (MCHC)​ = 34 g/dl Serum homocyst‌eine - Elevated Serum met‌hyl malonic acid - Normal‍","raw_text":"<p>A 56-year-old male patient is being evaluated for peripheral neuropathy and excessive tiredness. He has uncontrolled diabetes and consumes alcohol nearly every day. Lab investigations reveal the following findings. What is the likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Hemoglobin - 9g/dl</p>\n<p>Mean corpuscular volume (MCV) - 112 fL</p>\n<p>Mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration (MCHC) = 34 g/dl</p>\n<p>Serum homocysteine - Elevated</p>\n<p>Serum methyl malonic acid - Normal</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Folate d‌eficiency","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Alcohol-induced macrocytos‌is","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vitamin B12‌ deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thiamine ‌deficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Folate deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Folate deficiency</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>folate</strong> <strong>deficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is associated with the following clinical and laboratory findings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Raised MCV (normal: 80-100 fL) - macrocytosis</li>\n<li>Normal MCHC (normal: 33-36 g/dl) </li>\n<li>Increased MCH (mean corpuscular hemoglobin)</li>\n<li>Elevated RDW (red cell distribution width)</li>\n<li>Decreased reticulocyte count - due to destruction of abnormal erythroid precursors</li>\n<li><strong>Elevated homocysteine</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Normal MMA</strong> (methyl malonic acid)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In vitamin <strong>B12</strong> deficiency, <strong>elevated homocysteine</strong> and <strong>elevated MMA</strong> levels are seen. </p>\n<p>Peripheral neuropathy is a feature of B12 deficiency, not folate deficiency. In this patient, it may be due to diabetic neuropathy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4416","text":"Which of the following dru‌gs is not associated with‍ megaloblastic anemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following drugs is not associated with megaloblastic anemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitrofu‌rantoin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Phen‌ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tetrac‌ycline","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Temafl‌oxacin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Temafloxacin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Temafloxacin</strong></p><p>Temafloxacin is not associated with <strong>megaloblastic anemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Megaloblastic anemia is associated with the following drugs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anticonvulsant drugs - <strong>phenytoin,</strong> primidone, barbiturates</li>\n<li>Sulfasalazine</li>\n<li><strong>Nitrofurantoin</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Tetracycline</strong></li>\n<li>Folate antagonists - trimethoprim, methotrexate, pyrimethamine, pentamidine, cotrimoxazole</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Temafloxacin</strong> is a fluoroquinolone with severe adverse effects like<strong> hemolytic anemia </strong>and allergic reactions. It was <strong>withdrawn</strong> from the market and is no longer used. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4424","text":"A 45-year-old woman is rus‌hed to the ER following a‍ seizure. She is a known ​case of epilepsy but is n​on-compliant with her med‌ications. Her lab values ​are given below. All of t​he following drugs can ca​use this presentation exc​ept: Hemoglobin - 10g/dl ​MCV - 110 fL MCH - 40 pg ​MCHC - 34 g/dl","raw_text":"<p>A 45-year-old woman is rushed to the ER following a seizure. She is a known case of epilepsy but is non-compliant with her medications. Her lab values are given below. All of the following drugs can cause this presentation except:</p>\n<p>Hemoglobin - 10g/dl</p>\n<p>MCV - 110 fL</p>\n<p>MCH - 40 pg</p>\n<p>MCHC - 34 g/dl</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lamot‌rigine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Phen‌ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prim‌idone","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Amoba‌rbital","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lamotrigine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lamotrigine</strong></p><p>This patient likely has <strong>folate</strong> <strong>deficiency</strong> secondary to the use of an anti-folate anti-convulsant drug. Lamotrigine has no effect on folate metabolism and does not cause its deficiency.</p>\n<p>The following anticonvulsants have <strong>anti-folate </strong>properties: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Phenytoin</li>\n<li>Barbiturates (amobarbital)</li>\n<li>Primidone<strong> </strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Folate deficiency causes <strong>megaloblastic</strong> macrocytic anemia which results in the following findings in RBCs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anemia (low Hb = 10g/dl)</li>\n<li>Macrocytic (high MCV = 110 fl)</li>\n<li>Hyperchromic (high MCH = 40 pg) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4420","text":"A dose of oral 58-Co-label‌ed cobalamin was administ‍ered to a 65-year-old wom​an. The 24-hour urinary e​xcretion of radiolabeled ‌cobalamin was found to be​ &lt; 10%. The test was repe​ated after a 5-day course​ of antibiotics and showe​d urinary excretion &gt; 10%​. What is the likely diag​nosis?","raw_text":"A dose of oral 58-Co-labeled cobalamin was administered to a 65-year-old woman. The 24-hour urinary excretion of radiolabeled cobalamin was found to be &lt; 10%. The test was repeated after a 5-day course of antibiotics and showed urinary excretion &gt; 10%. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pernicio‌us anemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lack of pancreatic enzymes‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bacterial ‌overgrowth","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Malnut‌rition","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bacterial overgrowth","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bacterial overgrowth</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario refers to <strong>Schilling's test</strong> done for the determination of the cause of <strong>B12 deficiency</strong>. Improvement of urinary B12 excretion <strong>&gt;</strong> <strong>10%</strong> after antibiotic administration is suggestive of small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (<strong>SIBO</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4414","text":"A child diagnosed with meg‌aloblastic anemia failed ‍to respond to treatment w​ith vitamin C, vitamin B1​2, and folic acid. He was‌ started on uridine monop​hosphate which improved h​is symptoms. What is the ​likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A child diagnosed with megaloblastic anemia failed to respond to treatment with vitamin C, vitamin B12, and folic acid. He was started on uridine monophosphate which improved his symptoms. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiamine ‌deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Liver ‌disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pernicio‌us anemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Orotic ‌aciduria","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Orotic aciduria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Orotic aciduria</strong></p><p><strong>Megaloblastic anemia</strong> that is<strong> </strong>responsive to treatment with<strong> uridine</strong> is suggestive of <strong>orotic aciduria</strong>.</p>\n<p>Orotic aciduria is a rare inborn error of metabolism in children. It occurs due to a <strong>defect</strong> in the <strong>pyrimidine</strong> <strong>synthesis</strong> pathway causing a failure of conversion of <strong>orotic acid</strong> to <strong>uridine</strong>. It manifests in early childhood with anemia and failure to thrive.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4418","text":"A 28-year-old woman presen‌ts with fatigue and a red‍, painful tongue. On exam​ination, there is reduced​ vibration sense and prop‌rioception in the feet. S​he is under treatment for​ Hashimoto's thyroiditis.​ Peripheral smear reveals​ macrocytes and hypersegm​ented neutrophils. Which ​of the following is unlik‌ely to be seen in this pa‌tient?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old woman presents with fatigue and a red, painful tongue. On examination, there is reduced vibration sense and proprioception in the feet. She is under treatment for Hashimoto's thyroiditis. Peripheral smear reveals macrocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils. Which of the following is unlikely to be seen in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intrinsic factor blocking ‌antibodies","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antibodies against gastric‌ parietal cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Intrinsic factor binding a‌ntibodies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antibodies against gastrin‌","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Antibodies against gastrin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Antibodies against gastrin</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario in a patient with a history of autoimmune thyroiditis is suspicious for<strong> pernicious anemia.</strong> It is an <strong>autoimmune</strong> cause of B12 deficiency. Antibodies against gastrin are not seen in these patients. The following autoantibodies are seen: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type I </strong>or<strong> blocking </strong>antibodies - prevent the combination of IF and cobalamin</li>\n<li><strong>Type II </strong>or<strong> binding </strong>antibodies - prevent attachment of IF to the ileal mucosa</li>\n<li><strong>Anti-parietal cell </strong>antibodies - target the gastric proton pump located in these cells</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This leads to the deficiency of the <strong>intrinsic factor</strong> <strong>of Castle</strong> (IF) and hydrochloric acid produced by the parietal cells. The resulting atrophic gastritis causes <strong>high gastrin</strong> and <strong>low pepsinogen</strong> levels. Lack of IF further causes vitamin <strong>B12</strong> <strong>deficiency</strong> and megaloblastic anemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4419","text":"A pathologist receives a g‌astric biopsy sample of a‍ patient with pernicious ​anemia. Which of the foll​owing findings is he unli‌kely to find?","raw_text":"A pathologist receives a gastric biopsy sample of a patient with pernicious anemia. Which of the following findings is he unlikely to find?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Intestinal‌ metaplasia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mixed inflammatory cell in‌filtrate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypertrophy of glandular e‌lements","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increased ‌mucus cells","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hypertrophy of glandular elements","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hypertrophy of glandular elements</strong></p><p>Gastric biopsy in <strong>pernicious anemia</strong> shows <strong>loss</strong> of glandular elements, not hypertrophy. </p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">This is an autoimmune condition that occurs in genetically susceptible individuals. One of the common triggers of the disease process is <em><strong>Helicobacter pylori</strong></em> infection. Gastric biopsy findings in pernicious anemia include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Atrophy of all layers of the body and fundus\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Loss</strong> of <strong>glandular elements</strong></li>\n<li>Absence of parietal and chief cells and replacement by <strong>mucus</strong> <strong>cells</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Mixed</strong> <strong>inflammatory</strong> <strong>cell</strong> infiltrate (excess of CD4 cells against gastric H<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup>-ATPase)</li>\n<li><strong>Intestinal metaplasia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Antral mucosa is usually well preserved.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0405","text":"Patients with pernicious a‌nemia are at a higher ris‍k of which of the followi​ng?","raw_text":"Patients with pernicious anemia are at a higher risk of which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diffuse gastric adenocarci‌noma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gastric carc‌inoid tumors","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Primary gast‌ric lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gastrointestinal stromal t‌umor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Gastric carcinoid tumors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gastric carcinoid tumors</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>pernicious</strong> <strong>anemia</strong> have an increased risk of<strong> intestinal-type</strong> of<strong> gastric adenocarcinoma</strong> and gastric<strong> carcinoid </strong>tumors. These patients must undergo regular endoscopic screening for these conditions.</p>\n<p>The presumed mechanism is prolonged achlorhydria due to parietal cell loss. This leads to <strong>hypergastrinemia</strong> and cellular <strong>hyperplasia</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4422","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common cause of ‍folate deficiency?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common cause of folate deficiency?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Malabs‌orption","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nutri‌tional","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Use of anti-‌folate drugs","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Deficiency of enzyme DHF r‌eductase","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Nutritional","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Nutritional</strong></p><p><strong>Reduced nutritional</strong> <strong>intake</strong> is the most common cause of <strong>folate deficiency</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4421","text":"Dietary folates are absorb‌ed from which of the foll‍owing sites?","raw_text":"Dietary folates are absorbed from which of the following sites?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Duod‌enum","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Proxima‌l ileum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Termina‌l ileum","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ascendi‌ng colon","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Duodenum","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Duodenum</strong></p><p><strong>Dietary folates</strong> are absorbed from the <strong>duodenum </strong>and<strong> jejunum</strong> in the form of monoglutamates. </p>\n<p>Vitamin<strong> B12</strong> absorption takes place in the <strong>terminal ileum.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4426","text":"A 49-year-old woman is bro‌ught with increased forge‍tfulness and clumsiness f​or the past 1 month. Sinc​e the death of her husban‌d 3 months ago, she has b​een tired and eating poor​ly. She began taking over​-the-counter iron and fol​ic acid tablets 2 months ​ago. Examination reveals ​positive Romberg's sign w‌ith eyes closed and loss ‌of vibration in lower ext​remities. What is the lik‌ely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 49-year-old woman is brought with increased forgetfulness and clumsiness for the past 1 month. Since the death of her husband 3 months ago, she has been tired and eating poorly. She began taking over-the-counter iron and folic acid tablets 2 months ago. Examination reveals positive Romberg's sign with eyes closed and loss of vibration in lower extremities. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B12 def‌iciency","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Severe folat‌e deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thiamine ‌deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pyridoxine‌ deficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. B12 deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. B12 deficiency</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>dorsal column</strong> involvement of the spinal cord due to underlying vitamin <strong>B12 deficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p>Vitamin B12 deficiency causes subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord (<strong>SACD</strong>). It affects the following structures: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dorsal</strong> <strong>column</strong> - sensory loss, paresthesias, incontinence, <strong>sensory ataxia</strong> (positive Romberg sign with eyes closed)</li>\n<li>Lateral column - spasticity and paraplegias in later stages</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Folate deficiency is typically not associated with neurological manifestations. In patients with combined deficiency of B12 and folate, <strong>B12 deficiency</strong> must always be <strong>corrected first</strong>. This is because the administration of <strong>folate</strong> supplements may improve hematologic abnormalities but <strong>worsens</strong> <strong>neurological</strong> manifestations. </p>\n<p>Treatment of cobalamin deficiency is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Replenishment of body stores: Six 1000-μg IM injections of hydroxocobalamin given at 3- to 7-day intervals.</li>\n<li>Maintenance therapy: 1000 μg hydroxocobalamin IM once every 3 months </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treatment of folate deficiency is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>5–15 mg oral folic acid daily for a minimum of 4 months.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option C: Thiamine deficiency causes Wernicke's syndrome with ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and nystagmus. As this is due to cerebellar damage, Romberg's sign would be positive even with eyes open.</p>\n<p>Option D: Pyridoxine deficiency causes pellagra like syndrome with features of dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5594","text":"A 19-year-old girl with a ‌seizure disorder is start‍ed on phenytoin therapy. ​Which of the following mu​st be supplemented in thi‌s patient?","raw_text":"A 19-year-old girl with a seizure disorder is started on phenytoin therapy. Which of the following must be supplemented in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyrid‌oxine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thia‌mine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coba‌lamin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Folic‌ acid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Folic acid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Folic acid</strong></p><p>Usage of <strong>phenytoin</strong> leads to folate deficiency and <strong>megaloblastic anemia</strong>. Hence, <strong>folic acid</strong> must be supplemented.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4428","text":"A 23-year-old woman comes ‌to you for pre-conception‍al counseling. Her previo​us pregnancy resulted in ​fetal death due to a neur‌al tube defect. What dose​ of folic acid would you ​recommend?","raw_text":"A 23-year-old woman comes to you for pre-conceptional counseling. Her previous pregnancy resulted in fetal death due to a neural tube defect. What dose of folic acid would you recommend?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"400 μg pre-conceptionally ‌and in first trimester","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"400 μg pre-conceptionally ‌and throughout pregnancy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4-5 mg pre-conceptionally ‌and in first trimester","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"4-5 mg pre-conceptionally ‌and throughout pregnancy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 4-5 mg pre-conceptionally and in first trimester","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 4-5 mg pre-conceptionally and in first trimester</strong></p><p style=\"text-align: left;\">The recommended <strong>prophylactic</strong> dose of <strong>folic acid</strong> in women who have had a previous <strong>fetus</strong> with a <strong>neural</strong> <strong>tube defect</strong> is<strong> 4-5 mg </strong>daily, pre-conceptionally, and during the <strong>first trimester</strong> of pregnancy. Following the first trimester, these patients are then switched to 400 μg of folic acid for the remainder of the pregnancy.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">This high dose of folic acid (4-5 mg) should be initiated at least one to three months before pregnancy and should be continued for 12 weeks after pregnancy. The patient can be prescribed 1 mg tablet of folic acid and advised to take them 4- 5 times a day or they can be recommended 5mg tablets/capsules once daily.</p>\n<p>Prophylactic folic acid with <strong>400 μg</strong> daily, pre-conceptionally, and throughout pregnancy<strong> </strong>is indicated in <strong>all pregnant women</strong> to prevent megaloblastic anemia and reduce the incidence of neural tube defects.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4417","text":"Which of the following wou‌ld not be seen in a patie‍nt with Imerslund-Grasbec​k syndrome?","raw_text":"Which of the following would not be seen in a patient with Imerslund-Grasbeck syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Congenital cobalamin malab‌sorption","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"AMN gene‌ mutation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Autosomal dominant inherit‌ance","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Non-specific‌ proteinuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Autosomal dominant inheritance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Autosomal dominant inheritance</strong></p><p><strong>Imerslund-Grasbeck</strong> syndrome (IGS) is an <strong>autosomal recessive </strong>disorder.</p>\n<p>IGS occurs due to mutations in either the cubulin (<strong>CUBN</strong>) or amnionless (<strong>AMN</strong>) genes. They form the <strong>cubam receptor</strong> responsible for the absorption of the Cbl-IF complex. Hence, this condition leads to congenital <strong>cobalamin</strong> <strong>malabsorption</strong>. It is the most common cause of megaloblastic anemia due to B12 deficiency in infancy in Western countries.</p>\n<p>Affected children present with lethargy, failure to thrive, and neurological symptoms like hypotonia, developmental delay, and movement disorders. <strong>Non-specific proteinuria </strong>with normal renal function is seen in 90% of patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"O7_FDhrP","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"89_hemolytic_anemia","title":"89.Hemolytic Anemia","num_questions":23,"total_marks":92,"duration":33,"time_per_question":86.09,"questions":[{"id":"MB4335","text":"Which of the following wil‌l be seen in a patient wi​th intravascular hemolyti‍c anemia? MCV Haptoglobin​ LDH Urinary urobilinogen​ 1. High Low Low Low 2. H‌igh Low High High 3. Low ‌High Low Low 4. Low High ​High High","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following will be seen in a patient with intravascular hemolytic anemia?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 212px; width: 526px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 68px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px; text-align: center;\"><strong>MCV    </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 139.875px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Haptoglobin  </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.125px; text-align: center;\"><strong>LDH      </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Urinary urobilinogen</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 68px;\">1. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px; text-align: center;\">High  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 139.875px; text-align: center;\">Low  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.125px; text-align: center;\">Low  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px; text-align: center;\">Low  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 68px;\">2. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px; text-align: center;\">High  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 139.875px; text-align: center;\">Low  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.125px; text-align: center;\">High  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px; text-align: center;\">High  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 68px;\">3. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px; text-align: center;\">Low  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 139.875px; text-align: center;\">High  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.125px; text-align: center;\">Low  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px; text-align: center;\">Low  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 68px;\">4. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 154px; text-align: center;\">Low  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 139.875px; text-align: center;\">High  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 140.125px; text-align: center;\">High  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 126px; text-align: center;\">High  </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"‌1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"‌2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"‌3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"‌4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2</strong></p><p>In <strong>intravascular hemolysis</strong>, there is elevated MCV, LDH, and urinary urobilinogen, with low haptoglobin.</p>\n<p>Laboratory abnormalities seen in intravascular hemolysis:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 110px; width: 423.656px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Lab parameters</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Derangement</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.656px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Mechanism</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118px; text-align: left;\">MCV</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px; text-align: left;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.656px; text-align: left;\">Compensatory reticulocytosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118px; text-align: left;\">S. LDH</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px; text-align: left;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.656px; text-align: left;\">Released from RBCs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118px; text-align: left;\">S. Unconjugated bilirubin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px; text-align: left;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.656px; text-align: left;\">Hemoglobin from RBCs is converted to bilirubin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118px; text-align: left;\">Urinary urobilinogen</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px; text-align: left;\">High</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.656px; text-align: left;\">Unconjugated bilirubin is excreted as urobilinogen in urine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 118px; text-align: left;\">S. Haptoglobin</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px; text-align: left;\">Low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.656px; text-align: left;\">Free hemoglobin in circulation binds to haptoglobin causing reduced haptoglobin levels</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Additionally, hemoglobinuria and hemosiderinemia may be seen on urine examination.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4338","text":"Which of the following is ‌not due to an extracorpus​cular defect in RBCs?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not due to an extracorpuscular defect in RBCs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autoimmune hemolytic anemi‌a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemog‌lobinuria","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Familial hemolytic uremic ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Microangiopathic hemolytic‌ anemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria</strong></p><p>Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (<strong>PNH</strong>) is a hemolytic anemia due to an <strong>intracorpuscular</strong> defect.</p>\n<p>It is caused due to an acquired somatic mutation leading to <strong>deficiency</strong> of membrane protective factors <strong>CD59 </strong>and<strong> CD55</strong>. This makes the RBC extremely sensitive to complement activation and causes <strong>intravascular hemolysis</strong>. </p>\n<p>The other conditions occur due to extracorpuscular defects.</p>\n<p>Option A: In autoimmune hemolytic anemia, RBCs are coated with autoantibodies. This triggers phagocytosis in the reticuloendothelial system leading to extravascular hemolysis.  </p>\n<p>Option C: Familial hemolytic uremic syndrome (atypical HUS) is an inherited abnormality with excessive complement activation leading to episodic and excessive production of membrane attack complex destroying normal RBCs.</p>\n<p>Option D: Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia is non-immune hemolysis resulting from intravascular red blood cell fragmentation due to abnormalities in the microvasculature. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4364","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about paroxysmal noc​turnal hemoglobinuria?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inherited defect in PIG –A‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Extravascul‌ar hemolysis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Deficiency of CD 55 and CD‌ 59","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Microcyti‌c anaemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Deficiency of CD 55 and CD 59","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Deficiency of CD 55 and CD 59</strong></p><p>Deficiency of <strong>CD55</strong> and <strong>CD59</strong> is seen in <strong>paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria</strong> (PNH).</p>\n<p>It is due to an <strong>acquired mutation</strong> in the <strong>X-linked</strong> phosphatidylinositol class A <strong>PIG-A gene</strong> (Option A) causing a <strong>deficiency</strong> of <strong>GPI anchor proteins</strong>. GPI is a glycolipid that <strong>anchors membrane protective factors</strong> such as CD59 and CD55 to the membrane. The deficiency of CD55 and CD59 makes the <strong>RBC </strong>sensitive to <strong>complement-mediated intravascular hemolysis</strong>(Option B). The <strong>anemia</strong> is usually <strong>normo-macrocytic</strong> (Option D).</p>\n<p><strong>Hemolysis</strong> tends to occur at <strong>night</strong> due to a slight <strong>decrease in blood pH</strong> during sleep, which triggers complement activity (nocturnal hemoglobinuria). <strong>PNH</strong> is characterized by <strong>hemolytic anemia</strong>, <strong>pancytopenia</strong>, and <strong>venous thrombosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>PNH is diagnosed using <strong>flow cytometry</strong>. The drugs of choice for treatment are <strong>Eculizumab</strong> or <strong>Ravulizumab</strong> which work against <strong>complement C5</strong>. Supportive management of PNH includes <strong>folic acid</strong> and <strong>iron supplementation</strong>. Bone marrow transplantation is another option for the treatment of this condition. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4363","text":"A patient with hemolytic a‌nemia develops features o​f pancytopenia and venous‍ thrombosis. What is the ​likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient with hemolytic anemia develops features of pancytopenia and venous thrombosis. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shwachman-Diamond syndrome‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pyruvate kinase deficiency‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"G6PD ‌anemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemog‌lobinuria","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria</strong></p><p>Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria <strong>(PNH) </strong>is a type of <strong>hemolytic anemia</strong> associated with <strong>pancytopenia </strong>and<strong> venous thrombosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>In PNH, there is complement-mediated intravascular hemolysis. Bone marrow dysfunction also occurs, resulting in pancytopenia. Transformation to <strong>aplastic anemia</strong> or acute myeloid leukemia (<strong>AML</strong>) may rarely occur. </p>\n<p>PNH is also associated with venous thrombosis (cerebral, renal, hepatic).<strong> </strong>This is the most common cause of death in PNH. </p>\n<p>Option A: Shwachman-Diamond syndrome is inherited aplastic anemia with neutropenia with pancreatic insufficiency and malabsorption.</p>\n<p>Options B and C: Pyruvate kinase and G6PD deficiency are inherited RBC enzyme disorders causing extravascular hemolytic anemias. They do not affect other cell lines and are not associated with venous thrombosis.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4366","text":"A 31-year-old woman presen‌ts with a fever and cough​. She also reports excess‍ive tiredness and occasio​nal dark-colored urine fo​r the past 6 months. Ches‌t X-ray reveals lower lob‌e pneumonia. Labs reveal ​the following. What is th​e investigation of choice​ for this condition? Hb -‍ 7.6 g/dL RBC - 2 million​/mm 3 WBC - 5,000/mm 3 Pl‌atelets - 100,000/mm 3 Re​ticulocyte count - 3.2% S‌. LDH - High S. Haptoglob‍in - Low Coombs test - ne​gative","raw_text":"<p>A 31-year-old woman presents with a fever and cough. She also reports excessive tiredness and occasional dark-colored urine for the past 6 months. Chest X-ray reveals lower lobe pneumonia. Labs reveal the following. What is the investigation of choice for this condition?</p>\n<p>Hb - 7.6 g/dL</p>\n<p>RBC - 2 million/mm<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p>WBC - 5,000/mm<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p>Platelets - 100,000/mm<sup>3</sup> </p>\n<p>Reticulocyte count - 3.2%</p>\n<p>S. LDH - High</p>\n<p>S. Haptoglobin - Low</p>\n<p>Coombs test - negative</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acidified serum / HAM test‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Eosin-5′-maleimide-binding‌ test","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Flow cy‌tometry","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Acidified glycerol lysis t‌est","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Flow cytometry","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Flow cytometry</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>intravascular hemolysis</strong> due to paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (<strong>PNH</strong>). The gold standard for diagnosis is <strong>flow cytometry</strong>.</p>\n<p>Flow cytometry is performed with fluorescently labeled monoclonal antibodies that bind to GPI-anchored proteins (CD59, CD55). Most tests also use <strong>FL</strong>uorescent <strong>AER</strong>olysin (<strong>FLAER</strong>), a reagent that directly binds the GPI anchor. <strong>Absence </strong>of <strong>CD59</strong> and <strong>CD55</strong> are diagnostic of PNH. </p>\n<p>Other investigations in PNH include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ham test</strong> (acidified serum lysis) - lysis of RBCs in a mildly acidic pH due to complement fixation. </li>\n<li><strong>Bone marrow biopsy</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Hypercellularity with erythroid hyperplasia is seen in classical presentations.</li>\n<li>Patients who develop pancytopenia will have hypocellular bone marrow.  </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Imaging - to evaluate venous thrombosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B and D: Eosin-5′-maleimide-binding and acidified glycerol lysis tests are done in hereditary spherocytosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4367","text":"A 46-year-old woman presen‌ts with abdominal pain an​d yellowish discoloration‍ of her skin. She gives a​ history of dark-colored ​urine which is more prono‌unced in the early mornin‌gs. Imaging reveals hepat​ic venous thrombosis. Flo​w cytometry reveals the a​bsence of GPI-anchored pr‍oteins. What is the drug ​of choice?","raw_text":"A 46-year-old woman presents with abdominal pain and yellowish discoloration of her skin. She gives a history of dark-colored urine which is more pronounced in the early mornings. Imaging reveals hepatic venous thrombosis. Flow cytometry reveals the absence of GPI-anchored proteins. What is the drug of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ritu‌ximab","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Certol‌izumab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eculi‌zumab","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Alemt‌ezumab","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Eculizumab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Eculizumab</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (<strong>PNH</strong>). C5 complement inhibitors like <strong>eculizumab</strong> and <strong>ravalizumab</strong> are the drugs of choice in PNH. </p>\n<p>Management of PNH involves the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C5 complement inhibitors </strong>prevent the formation of membrane attack complex and complement-mediated lysis.</li>\n<li><strong>Bone marrow transplant</strong> is useful for patients with severe pancytopenia</li>\n<li>Supportive measures:\n<ul>\n<li>Transfusion of filtered RBCs</li>\n<li>Folic acid, and iron supplementation</li>\n<li>Anticoagulants in case of venous thrombosis </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0757","text":"A 10-year-old boy is broug‌ht for evaluation of fati​gue. On examination, he i‍s jaundiced and mild sple​nomegaly is noted. He was​ adopted and his family h‌istory is unknown. Periph‌eral smear findings are s​hown below. What is the m​ost specific investigatio​n for this condition?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old boy is brought for evaluation of fatigue. On examination, he is jaundiced and mild splenomegaly is noted. He was adopted and his family history is unknown. Peripheral smear findings are shown below. What is the most specific investigation for this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Eosin-5-mal‌eimide test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Acidified glycerol lysis t‌est","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Osmotic fra‌gility test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"NESTRO‌FT test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Eosin-5-maleimide test","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/03fa09fb02f940b9aa4e42257af3ee04.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Eosin-5-maleimide test</strong></p><p>The peripheral smear shows hyperchromic RBCs with loss of central pallor or spherocytes. These findings are suggestive of <strong>hereditary spherocytosis</strong>. The <strong>eosin-5-maleimide</strong> test is the <strong>most specific</strong> investigation for this condition. </p>\n<p>It is a <strong>flow cytometric test</strong> based on the interaction between the dye eosin-5-maleimide and band 3 protein.</p>\n<p>Other investigations that can be performed in this condition are:</p>\n<p><strong>Acidified glycerol lysis test:</strong> Spherocytes from patients with hereditary spherocytosis lyse more quickly than normal red blood cells when incubated in a phosphate buffered hypotonic solution with added glycerol. It has greater sensitivity and specificity than the osmotic fragility test.</p>\n<p><strong>Osmotic fragility test:</strong> Detects hemolysis by measuring the fraction of total hemoglobin released from red cells when suspended in progressively more dilute salt concentrations.</p>\n<p>Note: NESTROFT (Naked eye single tube red-cell osmotic fragility test) is a highly sensitive test used as a screening test for thalassemia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4361","text":"Which of the following is ‌true regarding the treatm​ent of hereditary spheroc‍ytosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true regarding the treatment of hereditary spherocytosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cholecystectomy is only in‌dicated for patients with​ clinical cholelithiasis ‍at the time of splenectom​y","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cholecystectomy cannot be ‌performed via laparoscopi​c route for patients with‍ HS and pigment gall ston​es","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Splenectomy is indicated i‌n patients with mild and ​asymptomatic disease only‍ after puberty","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lifelong penicillin prophy‌laxis after splenectomy i​s mandatory for all adult‍ patients","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cholecystectomy is only indicated for patients with clinical cholelithiasis at the time of splenectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cholecystectomy is only indicated for patients with clinical cholelithiasis at the time of splenectomy</strong></p><p><strong>Cholecystectomy</strong> at the time of splenectomy is indicated in patients with hereditary spherocytosis (HS) only if they have<strong> symptomatic cholelithiasis</strong> at the time of surgery. It is usually carried out via a <strong>laparoscopic</strong> approach.</p>\n<p><strong>Penicillin prophylaxis</strong> after splenectomy is controversial. Routine penicillin prophylaxis is not mandatory for adults. It is recommended up to age of five and for at least one year following splenectomy.</p>\n<p>Guidelines for the timing of <strong>splenectomy</strong> in HS:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mild cases - avoid splenectomy.</li>\n<li>Moderate cases - delay until puberty</li>\n<li>Severe cases - at 4–6 years of age</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4362","text":"The peripheral smear of a ‌patient is given below. W​hat is the most common mu‍tation associated with th​e hereditary form of this​ condition?","raw_text":"The peripheral smear of a patient is given below. What is the most common mutation associated with the hereditary form of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Spec‌trin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Band‌ 4.2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Band‌ 4.1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ank‌yrin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Spectrin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f5f168637ef3475cabdb47b10b90886b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Spectrin</strong></p><p>The given peripheral smear shows oval, elongated, rod, or cigar-shaped RBCs suggestive of <strong>elliptocytosis</strong>. The hereditary form is most commonly associated with the <strong>spectrin</strong> genes.</p>\n<p>The following proteins are implicated in <strong>hereditary elliptocytosis</strong> (HE):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>α-Spectrin (SPTA1 gene) - 65%</li>\n<li>β-Spectrin (SPTB gene) - 30%</li>\n<li>Band 4.1 </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The treatment of choice is splenectomy, preferably after 5 years of age.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4339","text":"A young couple comes to yo‌u for genetic counseling.​ The husband is a known c‍ase of G6PD deficiency an​d is concerned about pass​ing it on to his future c‌hildren. What is the chan‌ce that his children will​ inherit the disease?","raw_text":"A young couple comes to you for genetic counseling. The husband is a known case of G6PD deficiency and is concerned about passing it on to his future children. What is the chance that his children will inherit the disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Only male children will be‌ affected","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1 in 2‌ chance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Females can inherit the mu‌tation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"1 in 4‌ chance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Females can inherit the mutation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Females can inherit the mutation</strong></p><p>G6PD deficiency is an <strong>X-linked recessive</strong> disorder. <strong>Male children</strong> of an affected man are<strong> not affected</strong> as they receive only the paternal Y chromosome. However, the mutation can be passed on to <strong>female</strong> children, who are typically <strong>carriers</strong> of the disease.</p>\n<p>G6PD deficiency is the most common enzymatic disorder of RBCs. G6PD enzyme is involved in the generation of<strong> NADPH</strong> and protects RBCs from <strong>oxidative injury</strong>.<span class=\"headingEndMark\"> Hence, patients with</span> G6PD deficiency have episodes of acute hemolysis in the setting of oxidant injury from medications, acute infections, and certain foods (fava beans/broad beans).</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4343","text":"A 35-year-old male with ch‌ronic bacterial prostatit​is was treated with cotri‍moxazole. Within a few da​ys, he developed malaise,​ jaundice, abdominal pain‌, and dark urine. The blo‌od picture is given below​. What is the likely diag​nosis?","raw_text":"A 35-year-old male with chronic bacterial prostatitis was treated with cotrimoxazole.  Within a few days, he developed malaise, jaundice, abdominal pain, and dark urine. The blood picture is given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemolytic ur‌emic syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Autoimmune hemolytic anemi‌a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemog‌lobinuria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glucose 6 phosphate dehydr‌ogenase deficiency","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Glucose 6 phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3834becd3c32423ea85469a923ac8a91.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Glucose 6 phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency</strong></p><p>The given clinical features are suggestive of an <strong>acute hemolytic </strong>reaction following the administration of cotrimoxazole. This is suggestive of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (<strong>G6PD</strong>) <strong>deficiency</strong>.</p>\n<p>In G6PD deficiency uncontrolled oxidative stress causes hemoglobin to denature and form Heinz bodies. Removal of Heinz bodies by macrophages in the spleen results in the formation of characteristic <strong>bite cells</strong> or degmacytes or blister cells (as shown in the image).</p>\n<p>A hemolytic attack starts with abrupt onset of malaise, weakness, and abdominal or lumbar pain. After an interval of several hours to 2–3 days, the patient develops jaundice and often dark urine, due to <strong>hemoglobinuria</strong>. </p>\n<p>Drugs that carry the risk of clinical hemolysis in persons with G6PD deficiency:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Drug groups</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Examples</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Antimalarials</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Primaquine</p>\n<p><strong>Chloroquine</strong></p>\n<p>Dapsone/chlorproguanil</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Sulphonamides/</p>\n<p>sulphones</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p><strong>Sulfamethoxazole</strong></p>\n<p>Sulfasalazine</p>\n<p>Sulfisoxazole</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Antibacterial/</p>\n<p>antibiotics</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Cotrimoxazole</p>\n<p>Ciprofloxacin</p>\n<p>Chloramphenicol</p>\n<p>Nalidixic acid</p>\n<p>Norfloxacin</p>\n<p><strong>Nitrofurantoin</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p>Others</p>\n</td>\n<td>\n<p>Naphthalene</p>\n<p><strong>Methylene blue</strong></p>\n<p>Rasburicase</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: Cotrimoxazole contains sulfamethoxazole.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4345","text":"The peripheral smear of a ‌patient is given below. I​t is characteristic of wh‍ich of the following cond​itions?","raw_text":"The peripheral smear of a patient is given below. It is characteristic of which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucose 6 Phosphate dehydr‌ogenase deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pyruvate kinase deficiency‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"5' nucleotidase deficiency‌","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cytochrome b5 reductase de‌ficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 5' nucleotidase deficiency","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d03687414d4f4a9a9b77cff171f60c1f.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 5&#x27; nucleotidase deficiency</strong></p><p>The above peripheral smear shows <strong>basophilic stippling</strong>. It is characteristic of <strong>5' nucleotidase deficiency.</strong></p>\n<p>Pyrimidine 5'-nucleotidase (P5'N-1) deficiency is the third most common enzyme abnormality causing <strong>hereditary</strong> <strong>hemolytic anemia</strong>. It is an <strong>autosomal recessive</strong> trait.  </p>\n<p>The hallmark of this enzyme deficiency is the presence of pronounced<strong> basophilic stippling </strong>and accumulation of pyrimidine nucleotides <strong>(RNA</strong> <strong>remnants</strong>) within erythrocytes. </p>\n<p><strong>Cabot rings </strong>may also be seen. They are thin, threadlike, red to violet rings or figure-of-8 shaped inclusions in red blood cells. They are remnants of the <strong>mitotic spindle</strong> and are seen in conditions causing dyserythropoiesis.</p>\n<p>Basophilic stippling is also seen in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lead toxicity</li>\n<li>Sideroblastic anemia</li>\n<li>Severe thalassemia</li>\n<li>Conditions with impaired cell division</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4356","text":"A patient with SLE is foun‌d to have warm autoimmune​ hemolytic anemia. Name t‍he antibody and the site ​of hemolysis in this cond​ition?","raw_text":"A patient with SLE is found to have warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia. Name the antibody and the site of hemolysis in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IgM and Extavascular hemol‌ysis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"IgG and extravascular hemo‌lysis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"IgM and intravascular hemo‌lysis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IgG and intravascular hemo‌lysis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. IgG and extravascular hemolysis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. IgG and extravascular hemolysis</strong></p><p>The antibody involved in <strong>warm</strong> autoimmune hemolytic anemia (<strong>AIHA</strong>) is <strong>IgG</strong>, which causes <strong>extravascular</strong> <strong>hemolysis</strong>.</p>\n<p>IgG is called a warm<strong> </strong>antibody, as it binds to target proteins at <strong>37 <sup>o</sup>C. </strong>On the other hand, <strong>cold</strong> antibodies or <strong>IgM</strong> are reactive at 4 to 30<sup>o</sup>C.</p>\n<p>In AIHA, autoantibodies against RBC antigens are produced which coat the RBC membrane. The Fc portion of the antibody is recognized by the Fc receptor on macrophages causing <strong>phagocytosis</strong>. RBC destruction takes place in extravascular sites where macrophages are abundant.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4357","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following are least like​ly to develop autoimmune ‍hemolytic anemia?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following are least likely to develop autoimmune hemolytic anemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Infectious m‌ononucleosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Non-Hodgkin‌'s lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ulcerativ‌e colitis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic myel‌oid leukemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chronic myeloid leukemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chronic myeloid leukemia</strong></p><p>Autoimmune hemolytic anemia (<strong>AIHA</strong>) is commonly associated with <strong>CLL</strong> (chronic lymphocytic leukemia). It is not a typical feature of CML (chronic myeloid leukemia).</p>\n<p>AIHA can develop secondary to several autoimmune and infectious causes, as well as drugs.<strong> Drug-induced</strong> hemolytic anemia can occur by the following mechanisms:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 336px;\" width=\"370\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 178.021px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mechanism</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.021px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Drugs</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 178.021px;\">\n<p><strong>Adsorption</strong>:</p>\n<p>Drugs coat the RBC membrane. Antibodies are directed against the membrane–drug complex</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.021px;\">\n<p>Cefotetan</p>\n<p>Ceftriaxone</p>\n<p>Piperacillin</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 178.021px;\">\n<p><strong>Innocent bystander:</strong></p>\n<p>Drugs bind to antibodies in the plasma. This immune complex activates the complement system and destroys nearby RBCs (innocent bystanders)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.021px;\">\n<p>Sulfonamides</p>\n<p>Quinine</p>\n<p>Quinidine</p>\n<p>Phenothiazines  </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 178.021px;\">Some drugs trigger the production of <strong>autoantibodies</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 178.021px;\">Methyldopa</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4358","text":"Which of the following are‌ associated with a positi​ve Coombs test? Paroxysma‍l cold hemoglobinuria Par​oxysmal nocturnal hemoglo​binuria Cold agglutinin d‌isease Drug-induced hemol‌ytic anemia Hereditary sp​herocytosis","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are associated with a positive Coombs test?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria</li>\n<li>Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria</li>\n<li>Cold agglutinin disease</li>\n<li>Drug-induced hemolytic anemia</li>\n<li>Hereditary spherocytosis</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ‌2, 4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2, ‌3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, ‌4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‌3, 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1, 3, 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c77092a1cb4d4020ac7fbc6022a783b5x1280x1197.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1, 3, 4</strong></p><p>Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH) and hereditary spherocytosis are not Coomb's positive conditions. The following are Coombs-positive hemolytic anemias:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Autoimmune hemolytic anemia (AIHA)\n<ul>\n<li>Warm AIHA</li>\n<li>Cold agglutinin disease</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria (PCH)</li>\n<li>Drug-induced hemolytic anemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>In <strong>PCH,</strong> the <strong>direct </strong>Coomb's test will be <strong>positive</strong> for <strong>complement</strong> only. This condition is characterized by the presence of the <strong>Donath-Landsteiner</strong> antibody. It is a unique antibody with anti-P specificity at 4°C. Hence, it binds to the RBC at 4°C. However, it causes <strong>complement-mediated lysis</strong> at 37°C.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Interpretation of direct Coomb's test results:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 124px; width: 371px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center;\">Condition</td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"width: 254px; text-align: center;\">Agglutination with</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 129px; text-align: center;\">anti- IgG</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px; text-align: center;\">anti- C3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\">Warm AIHA</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\">Drug-induced</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center;\">PCH</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 111px;\">Cold agglutinin disease (IgM)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px; text-align: center;\">+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 111px; text-align: center;\">PNH</td>\n<td style=\"width: 129px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n<td style=\"width: 125px; text-align: center;\">-</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4359","text":"A 20-year-old woman presen‌ted with fatigue and inte​rmittent dark-colored uri‍ne. Further evaluation re​vealed positive Donath La​ndsteiner antibodies. Wha‌t is the likely diagnosis‌?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old woman presented with fatigue and intermittent dark-colored urine. Further evaluation revealed positive Donath  Landsteiner antibodies. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Warm autoimmune hemolytic ‌anemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cold autoimmune hemolytic ‌anemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paroxysmal cold hemoglobin‌uria","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemog‌lobinuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria</strong></p><p><strong>Donath Landsteiner</strong> antibody is specific for paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria (<strong>PCH</strong>). PCH is a rare form of autoimmune hemolytic anemia.</p>\n<p>The mechanism of this antibody includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Binding to RBCs at 4°C</li>\n<li>Complement-mediated lysis at 37°C</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This condition presents with <strong>intravascular hemolysis</strong> and <strong>hemoglobinuria</strong>. Blood transfusion is required in severe anemia. It is a self-limiting condition.</p>\n<p>The differences between PCH and PNH are given below:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 351px;\" width=\"425\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134px; text-align: center;\">Condition</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.667px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.667px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria (PCH)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134px; text-align: center;\">Onset</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.667px; text-align: center;\">Chronic with acute exacerbations</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.667px; text-align: center;\">Acute</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134px; text-align: center;\">Pathogenesis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.667px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Complement-mediated</strong> destruction of CD59/55(–) RBCs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.667px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Immune lysis</strong> of normal red cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134px; text-align: center;\">Diagnosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.667px; text-align: center;\">Flow cytometry to display a CD55/CD59(–) red cell population</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.667px; text-align: center;\">Test for <strong>Donath-Landsteiner</strong> antibody</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 134px; text-align: center;\">Triggers</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.667px; text-align: center;\">Infections, alcohol, exercise, stress, or  medications</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.667px; text-align: center;\">Triggered by viral infection</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4354","text":"Microangiopathic hemolytic‌ anemia is seen in all of​ the following except:","raw_text":"Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia is seen in all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antiphospholipid antibody ‌syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Vascu‌litis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prosthetic ‌heart valves","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Ecla‌mpsia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Prosthetic heart valves","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Prosthetic heart valves</strong></p><p>Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (MAHA) is not seen in <strong>prosthetic</strong> <strong>heart</strong> <strong>valves</strong>. It is associated with <strong>macroangiopathic</strong> hemolytic anemia.</p>\n<p><strong>MAHA</strong> refers to cell fragmentation that occurs in <strong>microcirculation</strong>. Microvascular lesions lead to luminal narrowing due to the deposition of fibrin and platelets. These produce shear stress that mechanically injures passing red cells. Hence, it is a type of non-immune hemolysis (<strong>Coombs-negative</strong>). On peripheral blood smear, <strong>schistocytes</strong> are seen.</p>\n<p>Common causes of MAHA include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura</li>\n<li>Hemolytic uremic syndrome</li>\n<li>Disseminated intravascular coagulation</li>\n<li><strong>Vasculitis</strong></li>\n<li>Malignant hypertension</li>\n<li><strong>APLA</strong> syndrome</li>\n<li>Drug-induced hemolysis</li>\n<li><strong>Eclampsia</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4355","text":"In patients with atypical ‌hemolytic uremic syndrome​, which of the following ‍mutations is associated w​ith the best prognosis?","raw_text":"In patients with atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome, which of the following mutations is associated with the best prognosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Complemen‌t factor H","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Complemen‌t factor I","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Complemen‌t factor B","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Membrane cof‌actor protein","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Membrane cofactor protein","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Membrane cofactor protein</strong></p><p>The <strong>best prognosis</strong> in atypical HUS is seen in <strong>membrane cofactor protein</strong> (MCP) gene mutation.</p>\n<p>Familial HUS or atypical HUS (aHUS) is the only inherited cause of extracorpuscular hemolytic anemia. It can occur due to <strong>mutations</strong> in any one of several genes encoding <strong>complement regulatory proteins</strong>. Mutations in the following genes are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Membrane cofactor protein - best prognosis</li>\n<li>Complement factor B - worst prognosis </li>\n<li>Complement factor H - most common </li>\n<li>Complement factor I - least common </li>\n</ul>\n<p>In these patients, infection or other triggers cause complement activation through the alternative pathway. This causes hemolysis and damage to endothelial cells in the kidney. It is usually self-limited.</p>\n<p>The standard treatment is <strong>plasma exchange</strong>. Eculizumab has recently been shown to be beneficial in atypical HUS.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0840","text":"You have decided to prescr‌ibe an oral iron chelator​ for a 10-year-old girl w‍ith thalassemia. Which of​ the following will you n​ot prefer?","raw_text":"You have decided to prescribe an oral iron chelator for a 10-year-old girl with thalassemia. Which of the following will you not prefer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Defer‌iprone","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Defer‌asirox","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Defero‌xamine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Both A‌ and B","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Deferoxamine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Deferoxamine</strong></p><p><strong>Deferoxamine</strong> has poor oral bioavailability and is administered <strong>parenterally</strong> (SC/IV).</p>\n<p>Oral formulations of both<strong> deferiprone</strong> and <strong>d</strong><strong>eferasirox</strong> are available.</p>\n<p><span class=\"nowrap\">The adverse effects of iron chelators include:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"nowrap\">Deferiprone - neutropenia, and agranulocytosis.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"nowrap\">Deferasirox - transaminitis and mild, asymptomatic rise in serum creatinine</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"nowrap\">Deferoxamine - occasional cataracts, deafness, and local skin reactions, but is usually well-tolerated</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0835","text":"Which of the following is ‌the best test to screen p​atients for thalassemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the best test to screen patients for thalassemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cellulose acetate hemoglob‌in electrophoresis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Naked eye single tube red-‌cell osmotic fragility te​st","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Eosin 5' maleimide flow cy‌tometry","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Automated high performance‌ liquid chromatography","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Naked eye single tube red-cell osmotic fragility test","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a609c616f696497193983869d15c7671x360x604.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Naked eye single tube red-cell osmotic fragility test</strong></p><p>Naked eye single tube red-cell osmotic fragility test (<strong>NESTROFT</strong>) is the <strong>most sensitive</strong> test to detect thalassemia in the population. Hence, it is an effective <strong>screening test</strong>.</p>\n<p>The principle is based on the decreased osmotic fragility of red cells in thalassemia. It is performed as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Mix EDTA blood and <strong>0.36% buffered saline </strong>solution.</li>\n<li>Shake the tube and leave it at room temperature for 30 minutes.</li>\n<li>Shake the tube again and immediately hold the tube in front of a piece of paper with text.</li>\n</ol>\n<table style=\"height: 237px;\" width=\"268\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82.421875px;\"><strong>NESTROFT</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 86.953125px;\"><strong>Inference</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.625px;\"><strong>Reason</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82.421875px;\">Lines visible (negative)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 86.953125px;\">Normal</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.625px;\">Hemolysis has occurred</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 82.421875px;\">Lines not seen (positive)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 86.953125px;\">Thalassemia</td>\n<td style=\"width: 76.625px;\">Stable RBC, unable to lyse</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image below shows a positive NESTROF test (test tube marked as T1) as the line is not visible through the test tube. The test tube marked as C shows the control (Please note that a negative NESTROF test would show visible lines similar to the appearance of the control).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The following tests are <strong>specific</strong> and used to confirm the diagnosis of thalassemia:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hemoglobin electrophoresis</li>\n<li>High-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC)</li>\n<li>Genetic testing </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Eosin 5' maleimide (EMA) flow cytometry is the most specific test for the diagnosis of hereditary spherocytosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4365","text":"During a master health che‌ck-up, a 25-year-old woma​n was found to have the f‍ollowing lab values. Whic​h of the following will h​elp in establishing a dia‌gnosis in this patient? H‌emoglobin - 10g/dl MCV - ​75 fL MCH - 25 pg Periphe​ral smear - Microcytes wi​th target cells Iron prof‍ile - Normal Vitamin B12 ​and folate levels - Norma‌l","raw_text":"<p>During a master health check-up, a 25-year-old woman was found to have the following lab values. Which of the following will help in establishing a diagnosis in this patient?</p>\n<p>Hemoglobin - 10g/dl</p>\n<p>MCV - 75 fL</p>\n<p>MCH - 25 pg</p>\n<p>Peripheral smear - Microcytes with target cells</p>\n<p>Iron profile - Normal</p>\n<p>Vitamin B12 and folate levels - Normal</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Free erythorcyte protoporp‌hyrin levels","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis‌","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Schilli‌ng test","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bone marr‌ow biopsy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hemoglobin electrophoresis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hemoglobin electrophoresis</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is consistent with <strong>β-thalassemia minor</strong>. The diagnosis can be established by<strong> hemoglobin electrophoresis</strong>.</p>\n<p>This condition presents with the following features: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Microcytes<strong> </strong>(N: 80-96 fL)</li>\n<li>Hypochromic<strong> </strong>(N: 27-33 pg)</li>\n<li>Mild anemia</li>\n<li>Target cells<strong> </strong></li>\n<li>Normal iron profile </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Hemoglobin electrophoresis findings are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li> ≥4% of HbA2</li>\n<li> ≤5% of HbF </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0845","text":"Which of the following cli‌nical manifestations is n​ot associated with sickle‍ cell trait?","raw_text":"Which of the following clinical manifestations is not associated with sickle cell trait?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypost‌henuria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Painless ‌hematuria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Papillary‌ necrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute pa‌in crisis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acute pain crisis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acute pain crisis</strong></p><p><strong>Acute pain crisis</strong> is <strong>not associated</strong> with <strong>sickle cell trait</strong>. Acute pain crisis is classicaly seen in sickle cell disease. </p>\n<p>Manifestations of sickle cell trait include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Often <strong>asymptomatic</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Hyposthenuria</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Painless hematuria</strong> - due to <strong>papillary necrosis</strong></li>\n<li>Recurrent UTIs</li>\n<li>Risk of renal medullary carcinoma - rare</li>\n<li>Increased risk of CKD</li>\n<li>Sloughing of papillae with urethral obstruction is very rare</li>\n<li>Anemia and painful crises are rare.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0842","text":"Patients with sickle cell ‌anemia most commonly pres​ent with which of the fol‍lowing?","raw_text":"Patients with sickle cell anemia most commonly present with which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute pa‌in crisis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Splenic sequestration cris‌is","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Acute ches‌t syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Isosth‌enuria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Acute pain crisis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Acute pain crisis</strong></p><p><strong>Acute pain crisis</strong> is the most common clinical presentation of<strong> sickle cell anemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>These are intermittent episodes of<strong> vaso-occlusion</strong> in connective and musculoskeletal structures. This results in ischemia resulting in acute pain and tenderness, fever, tachycardia, and anxiety. The management includes vigorous hydration, evaluation for underlying causes, and aggressive analgesia.</p>\n<p>Other manifestations of sickle cell anemia include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hand-foot</strong> syndrome - painful infarcts of the digits and dactylitis</li>\n<li><strong>Priapism</strong> - infarction of the penile venous outflow tracts</li>\n<li>Chronic lower leg ulcers - ischemia and superinfection in the distal circulation</li>\n<li><strong>Splenic sequestration </strong>crisis <strong>-</strong> life-threatening complication due to acute venous obstruction that requires emergency transfusion and/or splenectomy</li>\n<li><strong>Aplastic crisis</strong> - commonly secondary to parvovirus B19 infection</li>\n<li><strong>Acute chest syndrome</strong> - sickling within the lung, producing pain and temporary pulmonary dysfunction. Treatment may include lifelong transfusion support, using partial exchange transfusion</li>\n<li><strong>Stroke</strong> - common in children</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"uDsbTwFr","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"90_myeloproliferative_disorders_and_aplastic_anemia","title":"90.Myeloproliferative Disorders And Aplastic Anemia","num_questions":17,"total_marks":68,"duration":27,"time_per_question":95.29,"questions":[{"id":"MB4286","text":"According to WHO 2022 clas​sification, which of the ​following is not a chroni​c myeloproliferative neop‌lasm?","raw_text":"According to WHO 2022 classification, which of the following is not a chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic myel​oid leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic eosinophilic leuke​mia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Essential th​rombocythemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic lymphocytic leukem​ia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia</strong></p><p>Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (<strong>CLL</strong>) is a <strong>lymphoid</strong> neoplasm. It is not a myeloproliferative neoplasm.</p>\n<p><strong>Myeloproliferative</strong> neoplasms (MPN) are a group of disorders that cause an <strong>increased</strong> number of <strong>red blood cells</strong>, <strong>white blood cells</strong>, or <strong>platelets</strong>. According to 5th edition WHO classification (2022), myeloproliferative neoplasms consist of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic myeloid leukemia (CML)</li>\n<li>Polycythemia vera (PV)</li>\n<li>Primary myelofibrosis (PMF)</li>\n<li>Essential thrombocythemia</li>\n<li>Juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia (JMML)</li>\n<li>Chronic neutrophilic leukemia (CNL)</li>\n<li>Chronic eosinophilic leukemia</li>\n<li>Myeloproliferative neoplasm, not otherwise specified</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4308","text":"What is the most common my​eloproliferative neoplasm​?","raw_text":"What is the most common myeloproliferative neoplasm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic myel​oid leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary my​elofibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Essential th​rombocytosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polycyth​emia vera","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Polycythemia vera","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Polycythemia vera</strong></p><p>The <strong>most common</strong> myeloproliferative neoplasm is <strong>polycythemia vera. </strong></p>\n<p>It is a hematopoietic stem cell disorder that is characterized by uncontrolled proliferation of the red blood cells in the body. It is also frequently associated with an increase in white blood cells and platelets.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4304","text":"During a routine health ch​eckup, the following lab ​reports were obtained for​ a young male patient. Wh‌at will be your next step​ in the evaluation? Hb: 1‍7 g/dl TLC: 5500 /μL Plat‌elets: 1,69,000 /μL","raw_text":"<p>During a routine health checkup, the following lab reports were obtained for a young male patient. What will be your next step in the evaluation?</p>\n<p>Hb: 17 g/dl</p>\n<p>TLC: 5500 /μL</p>\n<p>Platelets: 1,69,000 /μL</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Perform bone marrow aspira​tion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Measure ​RBC mass","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Measure arterial oxygen sa​turation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Look for JA​K2 mutation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Measure RBC mass","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Measure RBC mass</strong></p><p>This patient has increased Hb and normal WBCs and platelets. The next step is to<strong> measure RBC mass</strong> to evaluate for polycythemia.</p>\n<p>A person has to be evaluated for <strong>polycythemia</strong> if there is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Increased hemoglobin:</strong> &gt;16.5 g/dL in men or &gt;16.0 g/dL in women, (or)</li>\n<li><strong>Increased hematocrit:</strong> &gt;49 percent in men or &gt;48 percent in women</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This condition is due to either a true increase in the number of RBCs (<strong>absolute polycythemia)</strong> or an apparent increase due to a decrease in the plasma volume (<strong>relative polycythemia</strong>). Both of these conditions can be differentiated by measuring RBC mass (amount of total blood volume that is occupied by RBCs).</p>\n<p>In patients with absolute polycythemia, <strong>erythropoietin </strong>(EPO) levels must be measured.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary</strong> polycythemia - <strong>low or normal</strong> EPO levels</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary</strong> polycythemia - <strong>elevated</strong> EPO levels</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4310","text":"A 45-year-old man is found​ to have a hemoglobin lev​el of 18 g/dl, a hematocr​it of 54%, and an elevate‌d erythropoietin level. W​hich of the following is ‍the least likely diagnosi‌s?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man is found to have a hemoglobin level of 18 g/dl, a hematocrit of 54%, and an elevated erythropoietin level. Which of the following is the least likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbon monox​ide poisoning","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cerebellar hemangioblastom​a","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Renal cell​ carcinoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polycyth​emia vera","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Polycythemia vera","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Polycythemia vera</strong></p><p><strong>Polycythemia vera</strong> is associated with <strong>low or normal </strong>erythropoietin (<strong>EPO</strong>) levels.</p>\n<p>Polycythemia refers to an <strong>increase</strong> in the number of red blood cells (<strong>RBC</strong>) in the body. It is classified as:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 142px; width: 451px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.1875px;\"><strong>Primary</strong> polycythemia (polycythemia vera)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.8125px;\"><strong>Secondary </strong>polycythemia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109px;\">Pathophysiology</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.1875px;\">Caused by mutations in the hematopoietic stem cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.8125px;\">\n<p>Hypoxia (COPD, <strong>carbon monoxide</strong> poisoning) </p>\n<p>Autonomous EPO production </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109px;\">Erythropoietin (EPO)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.1875px;\"><strong>Normal or low</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.8125px;\">High</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 109px;\">WBCs, and platelet count </td>\n<td style=\"width: 159.1875px;\">High (due to increased production of all cell lines)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 164.8125px;\">Normal</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Autonomous production of erythropoietin occurs in renal cell carcinoma (<strong>RCC</strong>), hepatocellular carcinoma, <strong>cerebellar</strong> <strong>hemangioblastoma</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Relative </strong>(spurious) polycythemia<strong> </strong>refers to an apparent increase in RBC or hemoglobin count due to a decrease in the plasma volume. It is commonly seen in <strong>dehydration</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4309","text":"What is the most common cy​togenetic abnormality in ​patients with polycythemi​a vera?","raw_text":"What is the most common cytogenetic abnormality in patients with polycythemia vera?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"JAK6 m​utation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"JAK2 m​utation","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"JAK8 m​utation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"JAK4 m​utation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. JAK2 mutation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. JAK2 mutation</strong></p><p>The most common cytogenetic abnormality in <strong>polycythemia vera</strong> is the <strong>JAK2 mutation</strong>. </p>\n<p>JAK2 mutations can be found in the following neoplasms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Polycythemia vera</li>\n<li>Essential thrombocythemia</li>\n<li>Primary myelofibrosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4291","text":"Which of the following sta​tements about polycythemi​a vera is true?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements about polycythemia vera is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Risk of thrombosis strongl​y correlates with the deg​ree of thrombocytosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Generalized pruritus is a ​consequence of mast cell ​activation by JAK 2 mutat​ion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is protective against H​.pylori infection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute myeloid leukemia is ​the most common cause of ​mortality","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Generalized pruritus is a consequence of mast cell activation by JAK 2 mutation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Generalized pruritus is a consequence of mast cell activation by JAK 2 mutation</strong></p><p><strong>Generalized pruritus</strong> seen in polycythemia vera (PV) is a consequence of the <strong>activation of mast cells by JAK 2 mutation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: The risk of <strong>thrombosis</strong> strongly correlates with <strong>erythrocytosis</strong>, <strong>not</strong> the <strong>degree of thrombocytosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option C: In polycythemia vera, there is an <strong>increased risk of H.pylori infection</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Thrombosis</strong> is the <strong>major cause of mortality</strong> in polycythemia vera. The incidence of acute leukemia in patients not exposed to chemotherapy or radiation therapy is low.</p>\n<p>PV is a <strong>clonal hematopoietic stem cell disorder</strong> in which red cells, granulocytes &amp; platelets accumulate in the absence of a physiological stimulus. The most common mutation seen in PV is the <strong>JAK2 mutation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of polycythemia vera are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Thrombosis</strong> is the most common manifestation and is the major cause of mortality. Uncontrolled erythrocytosis causes increased viscosity of the blood causing thrombosis of the vessels. Patients may present with stroke, transient ischemic attacks, myocardial infarction, and Budd-Chiari syndrome.</li>\n<li><strong>Aquagenic pruritis</strong> is generalized itching following a warm bath or a shower due to increased mast cells and histamine.</li>\n<li><strong>Erythromelalgia</strong> is erythema and pain in the fingers, hands, and feet due to increased platelet stickiness.</li>\n<li><strong>Splenomegaly</strong> due to increased destruction of RBCs in the spleen</li>\n<li><strong>Gout</strong> is due to the excessive proliferation of the marrow cells leading to <strong>elevated uric acid levels</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4303","text":"Which of the following wou​ld not be seen in patient​s with polycythemia vera?​","raw_text":"Which of the following would not be seen in patients with polycythemia vera?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elevated pl​atelet count","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Elevated serum lactate deh​ydrogenase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Low to normal erythropoiet​in levels","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Low WB​C count","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Low WBC count","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Low WBC count</strong></p><p>Low WBC count is not a feature of <strong>polycythemia vera</strong>. It is associated with an <strong>elevated WBC</strong> count.</p>\n<p>Common laboratory findings of polycythemia vera are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Elevated hemoglobin concentration, hematocrit levels, and RBC count</li>\n<li>Elevated WBCs and platelet counts</li>\n<li>Low to normal erythropoietin levels</li>\n<li class=\"p2\">Elevated serum lactate dehydrogenase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4306","text":"A 40-year-old man presents​ with a 1-month history o​f severe itching every ti​me he takes a hot shower.‌ He also reports a burnin​g sensation in his finger‍s and frequent headaches.‌ His hemoglobin is 18 g/d‍L and his EPO levels are ‌low. Which of the followi​ng can provide symptomati​c relief in this patient?​","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man presents with a 1-month history of severe itching every time he takes a hot shower. He also reports a burning sensation in his fingers and frequent headaches. His hemoglobin is 18 g/dL and his EPO levels are low. Which of the following can provide symptomatic relief in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dabra​fenib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nilo​tinib","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Eculi​zumab","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ruxol​itinib","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ruxolitinib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ruxolitinib</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>polycythemia vera</strong>. In these patients, <strong>ruxolitinib,</strong> a Janus-kinase inhibitor is used to provide <strong>symptomatic relief</strong>. </p>\n<p>The treatment of polycythemia vera is as follows:</p>\n<ul class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li1\">Therapeutic <strong>phlebotomy - </strong>definitive treatment</li>\n<li>Anticoagulants - in patients with thrombosis</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Symptomatic relief:\n<ul>\n<li class=\"li1\">Ruxolitinib</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Hydroxyurea</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Interferon-alpha</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">PUVA therapy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4288","text":"You are a first-year medic​ine resident. While exami​ning a patient, you note ​that his spleen is enlarg‌ed 8.5 cm below his costa​l margin. You would inclu‍de all of the following i‌n your differential diagn‍osis except:","raw_text":"You are a first-year medicine resident. While examining a patient, you note that his spleen is enlarged 8.5 cm below his costal margin. You would include all of the following in your differential diagnosis except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Essential th​rombocythemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic myel​oid leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary my​elofibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polycyth​emia vera","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Essential thrombocythemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Essential thrombocythemia</strong></p><p>Enlargement of the spleen <strong>&gt; 8 cm</strong> below the costal margin or weight &gt; 1000 g is called <strong>massive splenomegaly</strong>. It is not a feature of<strong> essential thrombocythemia</strong> (ET), as it causes only <strong>mild splenomegaly</strong>.</p>\n<p>The causes of <strong>massive splenomegaly</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic myeloid leukemia</li>\n<li>Primary myelofibrosis</li>\n<li>Polycythemia vera</li>\n<li>Chronic lymphocytic leukemia</li>\n<li>Lymphoma</li>\n<li>Hairy cell leukemia</li>\n<li>Gaucher's disease</li>\n<li>Sarcoidosis</li>\n<li>Autoimmune hemolytic anemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>ET is a neoplasm in which the body produces too many platelets, resulting in a platelet count <strong>&gt; 4.5 lakhs/μL.</strong> It occurs due to the mutations in the <strong>JAK2, CALR, </strong>or<strong> MPL</strong> genes.</p>\n<p>The majority of the patients are <strong>asymptomatic</strong> and are diagnosed incidentally. Some patients may present with <strong>thrombotic events</strong> or rarely, hemorrhages. The following findings are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Peripheral smear - abnormally<strong> large platelets</strong></li>\n<li>Bone marrow biopsy - increased number of <strong>megakaryocytes, </strong>with hyperlobulated nuclei</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4317","text":"A 24-year-old woman, who w​as diagnosed with essenti​al thrombocythemia, is be​ing planned for cytoreduc‌tive therapy. What first-​line drug should she be s‍tarted on?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman, who was diagnosed with essential thrombocythemia, is being planned for cytoreductive therapy. What first-line drug should she be started on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anagr​elide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Busu​lfan","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interfer​on alpha","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hydro​xyurea","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hydroxyurea","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hydroxyurea</strong></p><p>The <strong>first-line drug</strong> for <strong>cytoreductive</strong> therapy in patients with<strong> essential thrombocythemia</strong> is <strong>hydroxyurea. </strong></p>\n<p>Treatment of essential thrombocythemia is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>No symptoms or risk of thrombosis:\n<ul>\n<li>Observation alone</li>\n<li>Low dose aspirin may be considered</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>High risk of thrombosis (cardiovascular risk factors, prior history of thrombosis)\n<ul>\n<li>Low dose aspirin</li>\n<li>Cytoreduction with hydroxyurea, busulfan, or interferon-alpha</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4314","text":"A 60-year-old man was refe​rred with severe anemia a​nd hepatosplenomegaly. Pe​ripheral smear showed tea‌rdrop cells, nucleated RB​Cs, and giant abnormal pl‍atelets. Bone marrow aspi‌rate could not be obtaine‍d despite multiple attemp‌ts, hence a bone marrow b​iopsy was done. The findi​ngs are given below. What​ is the most likely diagn‍osis?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man was referred with severe anemia and hepatosplenomegaly. Peripheral smear showed teardrop cells, nucleated RBCs, and giant abnormal platelets. Bone marrow aspirate could not be obtained despite multiple attempts, hence a bone marrow biopsy was done. The findings are given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polycyth​emia vera","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Essential th​rombocythemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Primary my​elofibrosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic myel​oid leukemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Primary myelofibrosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/edbcad7a529649c5a22b29a233ab1c75.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Primary myelofibrosis</strong></p><p>The image shows the deposition of numerous collagen fibers in the bone marrow, which indicates <strong>marrow fibrosis</strong>. These clinical findings and history of a <strong>dry tap</strong> on bone marrow aspiration are diagnostic of <strong>primary myelofibrosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this condition, the neoplastic proliferation of hematopoietic stem cells results in increased and <strong>abnormal proliferation</strong> of the <strong>megakaryocytes</strong>. These megakaryocytes release excessive amounts of platelet-derived growth factor (<strong>PDGF</strong>) and<strong> TGF-β</strong>. Increased growth factors lead to the deposition of collagen in the bone marrow which eventually leads to fibrosis. </p>\n<p>The clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Early phase:\n<ul>\n<li>Thrombocytosis (large, abnormal platelets) and leukocytosis</li>\n<li><strong>Hypercellularity </strong>of bone marrow</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Late phase:\n<ul>\n<li>Pancytopenia </li>\n<li><strong>Hypocellularity</strong> and <strong>fibrosis</strong> of bone marrow (<strong>dry tap</strong>)</li>\n<li>Teardrop-shaped RBCs and leukoerythroblastosis (premature release of nucleated erythroid and early granulocytes into blood)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Hepatosplenomegaly </strong>- due to extramedullary hematopoiesis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4319","text":"Bone marrow aspirate of a ​patient with pancytopenia​ revealed a hypercellular​ marrow. Which of the fol‌lowing conditions is unli​kely?","raw_text":"Bone marrow aspirate of a patient with pancytopenia revealed a hypercellular marrow. Which of the following conditions is unlikely?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemog​lobinuria","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fanconi​ anemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Megaloblas​tic anemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myelodysplas​tic syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Fanconi anemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Fanconi anemia</strong></p><p><strong>Fanconi anemia</strong> results in pancytopenia with <strong>hypocellular</strong> <strong>bone marrow</strong>. Hence, it is not likely in this patient.</p>\n<p>Causes of <strong>pancytopenia</strong> with <strong>hypocellular</strong> bone marrow include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inherited <strong>aplastic anemia</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Fanconi anemia</li>\n<li>Dyskeratosis congenita</li>\n<li>Shwachman-Diamond syndrome</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Acquired aplastic anemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Causes of <strong>pancytopenia</strong> with <strong>hypercellular</strong> bone marrow include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primary bone marrow disorders\n<ul>\n<li>Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria<strong> (PNH)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Myelodysplastic </strong>syndrome</li>\n<li>Myelofibrosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Systemic diseases\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Megaloblastic anemia</strong></li>\n<li>Alcoholism</li>\n<li>Sarcoidosis</li>\n<li>Tuberculosis</li>\n<li>Brucellosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4322","text":"A 20-year-old male patient​ with the following exam ​findings is found to have​ pancytopenia. What is th‌e most likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 20-year-old male patient with the following exam findings is found to have pancytopenia. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fanconi​ anemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Shwachman-Diamond syndrome​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diamond-Blac​kfan syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dyskeratosi​s congenita","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Dyskeratosis congenita","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/68b4a3195abe4b2f8fb640974cee72a7.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/170d5aeff3914cea903edffc82a81e60x1280x2771.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dyskeratosis congenita</strong></p><p>The most likely diagnosis is <strong>dyskeratosis congenita. </strong>This condition is characterized by <strong>pancytopenia </strong>along with<strong> </strong>the clinical triad of:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Reticular skin pigmentation </li>\n<li>Nail dystrophy</li>\n<li>Oral leukoplakia </li>\n</ol>\n<p> </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option A: Fanconi anemia is an autosomal recessive disorder characterized by pancytopenia, congenital anomalies, and an increased risk of malignancies.</p>\n<p>Option B: Shwachman-Diamond syndrome presents with bone marrow failure and exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.</p>\n<p>Option C: Diamond-Blackfan syndrome presents with low RBC counts due to congenital erythroid aplasia.</p>\n<p>Note: Fanconi anemia is different from Fanconi syndrome. Fanconi syndrome is a disorder of renal tubular function (type 2 RTA) that results in increased excretion of glucose, amino acids, bicarbonates, and phosphates.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1204","text":"All of the following can c​ause acquired aplastic an​emia except:","raw_text":"All of the following can cause acquired aplastic anemia except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclophospha​mide therapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Radiation​ exposure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemog​lobinuria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Herpes simpl​ex infection","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Herpes simplex infection","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Herpes simplex infection</strong></p><p>Herpes simplex infection is not a cause of <strong>acquired aplastic anemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Secondary causes of acquired aplastic anemia include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Radiation</strong></li>\n<li>Toxins - Benzene</li>\n<li>Drugs \n<ul>\n<li>Chemotherapy drugs (including <strong>cyclophosphamide</strong>)</li>\n<li>Sulfonamides</li>\n<li>Chloramphenicol</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Infections: \n<ul>\n<li>1-2 months after an attack of seronegative viral hepatitis</li>\n<li>HIV, EBV, CMV</li>\n<li>Parvovirus B19</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Eosinophilic fasciitis</li>\n<li><strong>Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria </strong></li>\n<li>Graft vs host disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1206","text":"A 25-year-old lady is bein​g evaluated for recurrent​ epistaxis. On examinatio​n, the spleen is not palp‌able. Investigations reve​al Hb of 6 g/dL, platelet‍ count of 18,000/μL, and ‌WBC count of 3000/μL. The‍ results of a bone marrow‌ biopsy are given below. ​What is the best method o​f management in this pati​ent?","raw_text":"A 25-year-old lady is being evaluated for recurrent epistaxis. On examination, the spleen is not palpable. Investigations reveal Hb of 6 g/dL, platelet count of 18,000/μL, and WBC count of 3000/μL. The results of a bone marrow biopsy are given below. What is the best method of management in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Administer hematopoietic g​rowth factors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Umbilical cord blood trans​plantation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hematopoietic stem cell tr​ansplantation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Antithymocyte globulin wit​h cyclosporine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a0ad396188a846ff98c7b376a62c296a.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation</strong></p><p>The clinical and laboratory findings, as well as bone marrow hypocellularity are diagnostic of <strong>aplastic anemia</strong>. In this young patient, the best method of management is <strong>hematopoietic stem cell transplantation.</strong></p>\n<p>Aplastic anemia presents with pancytopenia and resulting bleeding manifestations, anemia, and frequent infections. Bone marrow biopsy reveals <strong>hypocellularity</strong> with <strong>fatty infiltration</strong> of the bone marrow. The management is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hematopoietic <strong>stem cell transplantation</strong> - best method of management in <strong>young patients</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Antithymocyte globulin</strong> with <strong>cyclosporine - </strong>elderly patients and patients without HLA-matched donors</li>\n<li><strong>Eltrombopag</strong> - a thrombopoietin analog used as add-on therapy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1209","text":"What is the most common cy​togenetic abnormality see​n in patients with myelod​ysplastic neoplasm?","raw_text":"What is the most common cytogenetic abnormality seen in patients with myelodysplastic neoplasm?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7q de​letion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5q de​letion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Monos​omy 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Monos​omy 7","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 5q deletion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 5q deletion</strong></p><p>The most common cytogenetic abnormality seen in <strong>myelodysplastic</strong> <strong>neoplasm</strong> is the <strong>5q deletion. </strong></p>\n<p>Myelodysplastic syndromes/neoplasm (MDS) are a group of disorders in which <strong>abnormal stem cells</strong> produce increased numbers of <strong>immature blood cells</strong>. These immature cells are defective and die prematurely resulting in lower numbers of mature blood cells. It is characterized by <strong>pancytopenia </strong>and a high risk of acute myeloid leukemia (<strong>AML</strong>).</p>\n<p>The cytogenetic abnormalities identified in this condition include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>5q deletion</li>\n<li>3q deletion</li>\n<li>7q deletion</li>\n<li>Monosomy 7</li>\n<li>Monosomy 5</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>5th edition WHO Classification of MDS (2022):</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1. MDS with defining genetic abnormalities:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">MDS with a low blast and isolated 5q deletion (MDS-5q): &lt;5% blasts in the bone marrow or &lt;2% blasts in the peripheral blood. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">MDS with a low blast and SF3B1 mutation (MDS-SF3B1): Detection of ≥15% ring sideroblasts may substitute for SF3B1 mutation.  </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">MDS with biallelic TP53 inactivation (MDS-biTP53): &lt;20% blasts in the bone marrow or peripheral blood.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. MDS, morphologically defined:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">MDS with low blasts: &lt;5% blasts in the bone marrow or &lt;2% blasts in the peripheral blood. </li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">MDS-hypoplastic: ≤25% bone marrow cellularity</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">MDS with increased blasts (IB):\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">MDS-IB1: 5–9% of blasts in bone marrow or 2–4% in peripheral blood</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">MDS-IB2:10-19% of blasts in bone marrow or 5-19% in peripheral blood or Auer rods</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">MDS-fibrosis: 5-19% of blasts in bone marrow or 2–19% in peripheral blood</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>3. Childhood myelodysplastic neoplasm:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Childhood MDS with low blasts:&lt;5% blasts in the bone marrow or &lt;2% blasts in the peripheral blood</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Childhood MDS with increased blasts: 5-19% blasts in the bone marrow or 2-19% blasts in the peripheral blood.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1210","text":"A patient with myelodyspla​stic syndrome was found t​o have 5q deletion. What ​is the drug of choice for‌ this patient?","raw_text":"A patient with myelodysplastic syndrome was found to have 5q deletion. What is the drug of choice for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclos​porine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cyclopho​sphamide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thali​domide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lenali​domide","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Lenalidomide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Lenalidomide</strong></p><p><strong>Lenalidomide</strong> is the drug of choice in patients with myelodysplastic syndrome (<strong>MDS</strong>) associated with <strong>5q deletion</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is a thalidomide derivative with the following benefits in these patients:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li>Patients achieve transfusion independence</li>\n<li>Normal or near-normal hemoglobin levels</li>\n<li>Normalization of cytogenetics </li>\n</ul>\n<p>Adverse effects include:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li>Myelosuppression (worsening thrombocytopenia and neutropenia, necessitating blood count monitoring) </li>\n<li>Deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism</li>\n</ul>\n<p>However, <strong>hematopoietic stem cell transplant </strong>remains the definitive treatment.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"FuPcWU1H","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"91_platelet_disorders","title":"91.Platelet Disorders","num_questions":30,"total_marks":120,"duration":40,"time_per_question":80.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC1457","text":"What is the lifespan of a ‌platelet?","raw_text":"What is the lifespan of a platelet?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"24 h‌ours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5 d‌ays","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"7 d‌ays","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"2 w‌eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 7 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 7 days</strong></p><p>The average <strong>lifespan</strong> of <strong>platelets</strong> in circulation is <strong>7–10 days</strong>.</p>\n<p>Thrombopoietin <strong>(TPO)</strong> is the regulator of platelet production. A reduction in platelet and megakaryocyte mass increases the level of TPO, which then stimulates platelet production.</p>\n<p>The shelf life of donor platelets is 5 days. It is stored on a special agitator at 20-24 °C. The half-life of transfused platelets is 3-5 days.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD8446","text":"Which of the following wou‌ld you not expect to see ‍in patients with a platel​et disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following would you not expect to see in patients with a platelet disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pete‌chiae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ecchy‌mosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hemart‌hrosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Gastrointes‌tinal bleeds","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hemarthrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/98bceb172651412e82d9eab512b76b04x1280x2210.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hemarthrosis</strong></p><p><strong>Hemarthrosis</strong> is highly suggestive of a <strong>coagulation disorder, </strong>not a platelet disorder.</p>\n<p>Bleeding diathesis can occur due to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Platelet disorders - mucocutaneous bleeding like <strong>petechiae</strong>, purpura, <strong>ecchymoses</strong>, epistaxis, menorrhagia, gastrointestinal (<strong>GI</strong>) <strong>bleeds</strong></li>\n<li>Coagulation disorders - hemarthrosis, muscle hematomas, and bleeding into soft tissues</li>\n<li>Disorders of the vessel wall (Henoch-Schönlein purpura, scurvy) - purpura and ecchymosis </li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD8447","text":"Which of the following is ‌a disorder of platelet ad‍hesion?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a disorder of platelet adhesion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glanzmann’s thrombasthenia‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bernard Soul‌ier syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Wiskott Aldr‌ich syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hermansky Pu‌dlak syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bernard Soulier syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/56f11346987e42ae8a45ab9a221cab9dx1280x2203.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bernard Soulier syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Bernard Soulier </strong>syndrome is a disorder of <strong>platelet adhesion</strong>. It occurs due to an inherited defect in the <strong>GPIb-IX</strong> complex.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4059","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following conditions wou‍ld have a low platelet co​unt?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions would have a low platelet count?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Platelet storage pool diso‌rder","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Wiskott-Aldr‌ich syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Henoch-Schon‌lein purpura","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemop‌hilia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Thrombocytopenia</strong> is a feature of <strong>Wiskott-Aldrich</strong> syndrome.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>X-linked</strong> recessive condition caused by mutations in the <strong>WASP </strong>gene. It affects <strong>T-cells </strong>and<strong> platelets</strong> and presents with the following features: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Eczema</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Thrombocytopenia</strong> - along with decreased platelet size, impaired platelet aggregation response</li>\n<li><strong>Immunodeficiency</strong> - low IgM, high IgE, defective regulatory T cells causing recurrent bacterial infections.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Platelet storage pool disorder is a disorder of defective platelet granule formation.</p>\n<p>Option C: Henoch-Schonlein purpura is a type of small vessel vasculitis in children and young adults.</p>\n<p>Option D: Hemophilia is a coagulation disorder, hence the platelet count is not affected.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4061","text":"Immune-mediated destructio‌n of platelets is seen in‍ patients with which of t​he following conditions?","raw_text":"Immune-mediated destruction of platelets is seen in patients with which of the following conditions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bernard-Soul‌ier syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hermansky-Pu‌dlak syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thrombotic thrombocytopeni‌c purpura","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Drug-induced thrombocytope‌nia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Drug-induced thrombocytopenia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Drug-induced thrombocytopenia</strong></p><p><strong>Immune-mediated destruction</strong> of platelets is seen in <strong>drug-induced</strong> <strong>thrombocytopenia.</strong> Common drugs that cause this are quinidine, heparin, sulfa compounds. </p>\n<p>Drug-dependent antibody binding can only be demonstrated in the presence of the drug in the laboratory assays. The thrombocytopenia normally occurs within 21 days of initial exposure and usually gets resolved after 7-10 days once the drug is stopped. </p>\n<p>Immune-mediated destruction of platelets is seen in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Drug-induced</strong> thrombocytopenia</li>\n<li><strong>Infection-induced</strong> thrombocytopenia</li>\n<li>Idiopathic/ immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Bernard-Soulier syndrome is a disorder of platelet adhesion. There are no autoantibodies against platelets.</p>\n<p>Option B: Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome is an inherited disorder with defective granule formation in platelets. It affects platelet activation and aggregation.</p>\n<p>Option C: Thrombotic microangiopathies like thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) and hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) lead to thrombocytopenia due to increased platelet consumption.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD8448","text":"A young man comes to you w‌ith complaints of bleedin‍g gums and the following ​skin rash. He reports tha​t he had diarrhea last we​ek after eating at a road‍side eatery. History is s‌ignificant for deep vein ‍thrombosis 6 months ago t​hat was treated with hepa​rin. His CBC report revea​led a platelet count of 1‌5,000/µL, normal RBC and ​WBC count. His peripheral​ smear was normal. What i‍s the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A young man comes to you with complaints of bleeding gums and the following skin rash. He reports that he had diarrhea last week after eating at a roadside eatery. History is significant for deep vein thrombosis 6 months ago that was treated with heparin. His CBC report revealed a platelet count of 15,000/µL, normal RBC and WBC count. His peripheral smear was normal. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemolytic ur‌emic syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thrombotic thrombocytopeni‌c purpura","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Immune thrombocytopenic pu‌rpura","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Heparin-induced thrombocyt‌openia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Immune thrombocytopenic purpura","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b9f86e5242b74faaaab53f43357ffe24.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1839d0a0b6d64631b0d4aca0475d0791x1280x2203.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Immune thrombocytopenic purpura</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and image showing <strong>petechiae</strong> are suggestive of <strong>immune thrombocytopenic purpura</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Immune thrombocytopenic purpura </strong>(ITP) is an acquired disorder with <strong>autoantibodies</strong> against platelet membrane glycoproteins<strong> IIb-IIIa</strong> or <strong>Ib-IX</strong>. It can either be primary (idiopathic) or secondary to autoimmune conditions like SLE or infections like HIV and hepatitis C.</p>\n<p>It presents with features of <strong>mucocutaneous</strong> <strong>bleeding</strong>. CBC shows isolated thrombocytopenia and peripheral smear does not reveal any hemolysis. </p>\n<table style=\"height: 263px; width: 346px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 161.172px; height: 40px;\"><strong>Acute ITP</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.828px; height: 40px;\"><strong>Chronic ITP</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 40px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 161.172px; height: 40px;\">Common in children</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.828px; height: 40px;\">Common in adults (females &gt; males)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 58px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 161.172px; height: 58px;\">Associated with an antecedent viral infection</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.828px; height: 58px;\">Not associated with antecedent infection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 66.5625px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 161.172px; height: 66.5625px;\">Spontaneous remission in 80% cases</td>\n<td style=\"width: 168.828px; height: 66.5625px;\">Spontaneous remission is not usually seen</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Options <strong>A</strong> and <strong>B</strong>: HUS and TTP are microangiopathies. They are associated with <strong>hemolysis and schistocytes</strong> on peripheral smear. </p>\n<p>Option <strong>D:</strong> Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia typically presents <strong>within 5-10 days of treatment</strong>. Hence, it is not the likely diagnosis in this patient.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4063","text":"Two weeks after an upper r‌espiratory tract infectio‍n, a 5-year-old presents ​with ecchymosis and petec​hiae all over the body. A​bdominal examination reve‍aled no hepatosplenomegal‌y. The platelet count is ‍15,000/µL and the coagula​tion profile is normal. W​hich of the following is ​false regarding this cond‌ition?","raw_text":"Two weeks after an upper respiratory tract infection, a 5-year-old presents with ecchymosis and petechiae all over the body. Abdominal examination revealed no hepatosplenomegaly. The platelet count is 15,000/µL and the coagulation profile is normal. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Peripheral blood smear is ‌normal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bone marrow biopsy shows i‌ncreased megakaryocytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bleeding into the joints i‌s a common manifestation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"The condition resolves spo‌ntaneously in 80% patient‍s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Bleeding into the joints is a common manifestation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Bleeding into the joints is a common manifestation</strong></p><p>This scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute immune thrombocytopenic purpura</strong>. Bleeding into the joints is seen in coagulation disorders and is not a manifestation of this condition.</p>\n<p>Immune thrombocytopenic purpura or idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura <strong>(ITP)</strong>  is an acquired disorder in which there is an immune-mediated destruction of platelets. It can be acute or chronic.</p>\n<p><strong>Acute ITP </strong>is common in children and is usually <strong>preceded</strong> by <strong>viral</strong> infection. It presents with mucocutaneous bleeding (epistaxis, petechiae, ecchymosis). Hepatomegaly, splenomegaly, lymphadenopathy, fever and jaundice are absent. The following laboratory features are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Thrombocytopenia (commonly &lt;20,000 / μL)</li>\n<li>Bleeding time - prolonged</li>\n<li>PT/aPTT/INR - normal</li>\n<li>Bone marrow - normal/increased megakaryocytes</li>\n<li>Peripheral smear - normal</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This condition <strong>resolves</strong> <strong>spontaneously</strong> in 80% of the cases. Treatment is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Platelet count &lt; 30,000 with risk factors for bleeding (antiplatelet drugs/anticoagulants or concomitant bleeding disorders): <strong>Oral glucocorticoids</strong> or <strong>IVIG</strong> </li>\n<li>Severe bleeding symptoms: <strong>IV methylprednisolone</strong> ± IVIG </li>\n<li>Life-threatening bleeding (like intracranial hemorrhage): <strong>platelet transfusion</strong> + IV methylprednisolone + IVIG </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4069","text":"A 50-year-old woman comes ‌to the ER after developin‍g a spontaneous nosebleed​. Over the past 1 week, s​he has noticed blood on h​er toothbrush while brush‍ing her teeth. She was di‌agnosed with hepatitis C ‍three months ago. Investi​gations reveal a platelet​ count of 32,000/ µL and ​a bleeding time of 9 minu‌tes . Which of the follow​ing is false regarding th​e treatment of this patie‍nt?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old woman comes to the ER after developing a spontaneous nosebleed. Over the past 1 week, she has noticed blood on her toothbrush while brushing her teeth. She was diagnosed with hepatitis C three months ago. Investigations reveal a platelet count of 32,000/ µL and a bleeding time of 9 minutes . Which of the following is false regarding the treatment of this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"She requires regular plate‌let transfusions until pl‍atelet count is normalize​d","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Prednisone or anti-D immun‌oglobulins can be used as‍ the first-line treatment​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Splenectomy may be done if‌ she relapses after medic‍al management","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Romiplostim and eltrombopa‌g can be used if there is‍ no response to splenecto​my","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. She requires regular platelet transfusions until platelet count is normalized","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. She requires regular platelet transfusions until platelet count is normalized</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario in an <strong>adult</strong> patient is suggestive of <strong>chronic ITP</strong> (idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura). She does not require <strong>platelet transfusion</strong>. It is recommended only for patients with <strong>severe bleeding </strong>manifestations (intracranial/ gastrointestinal) or platelet count<strong> &lt; 5000 μL.</strong></p>\n<p>Chronic ITP can either be <strong>primary</strong> (idiopathic) or <strong>secondary</strong> to autoimmune conditions like SLE or infections like HIV and <strong>hepatitis C</strong>. Depending on the clinical features, the management is as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 469px; width: 477px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 34.8281px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.266px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Clinical features</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.906px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Management</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 34.8281px;\">\n<p>1.   </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.266px;\">\n<p>Minor bleeding manifestations (and/or)</p>\n<p>Platelet count &gt; 5000/ μL</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.906px;\">\n<p><strong>Prednisone</strong> (OR)</p>\n<p><strong>Anti-D</strong> <strong>immunoglobulin</strong> therapy - effective only in Rh-positive patients (OR)</p>\n<p>Intravenous immunoglobulin (<strong>IVIG</strong>)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 34.8281px;\">\n<p>2.  </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.266px;\">\n<p>Severe ITP (any of the following):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Significant GI / intracranial bleeding</li>\n<li>Platelet count &lt; 5000/μL</li>\n<li>Signs of impending bleeding (retinal or large oral mucosal bleeds)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.906px;\">\n<p>Platelet transfusion (AND)</p>\n<p>High-dose glucocorticoids (AND)</p>\n<p>IVIG / Anti-D immunoglobulins + Rituximab</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 34.8281px;\">3.  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.266px;\">Relapse after first-line therapy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.906px;\"><strong>Splenectomy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 34.8281px;\">4.   </td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.266px;\">Unresponsive to all other therapy or relapse after splenectomy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.906px;\">Thrombopoietin receptor agonists - <strong>romiplostim</strong> (subcutaneous), <strong>eltrombopag</strong> (oral)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7483","text":"Which of the following is ‌the best treatment for th‍e relapse of immune throm​bocytopenic purpura?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the best treatment for the relapse of immune thrombocytopenic purpura?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IV immuno‌globulins","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ster‌oids","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Splen‌ectomy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Blood tr‌ansfusion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Splenectomy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Splenectomy</strong></p><p><strong>Splenectomy</strong> is the treatment of choice in a case of <strong>immune thrombocytopenic purpura</strong> (ITP) relapse after <strong>first line therapy</strong> with <strong>glucocorticoids</strong>. The relapse usually occurs once they are tapered. <strong>Vaccination</strong> against encapsulated organisms (pneumococcus, meningococcus, H. influenzae) is advised before splenectomy is performed.</p>\n<p>It is important to note that in some patients <strong>ITP resolves by itself,</strong> and observation along with intermittent <strong>IV immunoglobulins</strong> and <strong>anti - Rh(D)</strong> administration or administration of a <strong>thrombopoietin receptor agonist</strong> (eltrombopag, romiplostim) can be a viable option before splenectomy is considered.</p>\n<p>The treatment of ITP is as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 469px; width: 477px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 34.8281px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.266px; text-align: center;\">Clinical features</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.906px; text-align: center;\">Management</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 34.8281px;\">\n<p>1.   </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.266px;\">\n<p>Minor bleeding manifestations (and/or)</p>\n<p>Platelet count &gt; 5000/ μL</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.906px;\">\n<p><strong>Prednisone</strong> (OR)</p>\n<p><strong>Anti-D immunoglobulin therapy</strong> - effective only in Rh-positive patients (OR)</p>\n<p>Intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 34.8281px;\">\n<p>2.  </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.266px;\">\n<p>Severe ITP (any of the following):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Significant GI / intracranial bleeding</li>\n<li>Platelet count &lt; 5000/μL</li>\n<li>Signs of impending bleeding (retinal or large oral mucosal bleeds)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.906px;\">\n<p>Platelet transfusion (AND)</p>\n<p>High-dose glucocorticoids (AND)</p>\n<p>IVIG / Anti-D immunoglobulins + Rituximab</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 34.8281px;\">3.  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.266px;\">Relapse after first-line therapy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.906px;\"><strong>Splenectomy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 34.8281px;\">4.   </td>\n<td style=\"width: 218.266px;\">Unresponsive to all other therapy or relapse after splenectomy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 201.906px;\">Thrombopoietin receptor agonists - <strong>romiplostim</strong> (subcutaneous), <strong>eltrombopag</strong> (oral)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>ITP is an <strong>acquired</strong> disorder characterized by the <strong>immune-mediated</strong> destruction of platelets and possibly inhibition of platelet release from the megakaryocyte. Secondary causes of ITP include systemic lupus erythematosus (<strong>SLE</strong>), <strong>HIV-AIDS</strong>, and <strong>Hepatitis C</strong>.</p>\n<p>ITP usually presents with <strong>mucocutaneous bleeding</strong>, <strong>very low platelet count</strong>, and normal peripheral blood cells. Patients typically exhibit ecchymoses and petechiae or they may incidentally discover thrombocytopenia during a routine complete blood count. Rarely, life-threatening bleeding may occur. The presence of <strong>wet purpura</strong> (blood blisters in the mouth) and <strong>retinal hemorrhages</strong> can serve as <strong>warning signs</strong> of potentially life-threatening bleeding.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4073","text":"It is your first day as an‌ intern in the medicine w‍ard. The senior resident ​tells you to check the pl​atelet count of a patient​ who is being monitored f‍or thrombocytopenia. He w‌as started on a new medic‍ation the previous night.​ What drug was most likel​y prescribed?","raw_text":"It is your first day as an intern in the medicine ward. The senior resident tells you to check the platelet count of a patient who is being monitored for thrombocytopenia. He was started on a new medication the previous night. What drug was most likely prescribed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Phen‌ytoin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ampic‌illin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Abci‌ximab","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Amiod‌arone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Abciximab","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Abciximab</strong></p><p><strong>Abciximab</strong> can cause a drop in platelet count within<strong> 24 hours</strong> of initial exposure. Hence, these patients must be monitored for <strong>thrombocytopenia.</strong> This is due to naturally-occurring <strong>antibodies</strong> that cross-react with the drug bound to the platelets.</p>\n<p>Thrombocytopenia due to <strong>other drugs</strong> typically occurs <strong>several days</strong> after exposure (average 21 days) and resolves in 7-10 days after drug withdrawal.  Drug-induced thrombocytopenia can occur due to the following mechanisms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bone marrow suppression - chemotherapeutic drugs</li>\n<li>Drug dependent antibodies that react with platelet surface antigens - quinine and sulfonamides</li>\n<li>Naturally occurring antibodies that cross-react with drugs bound to the platelets - platelet Gp IIb/IIIa inhibitors</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Some of the drugs that cause thrombocytopenia are:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li>Antibiotics - <strong>ampicillin</strong>, cephalosporins, piperacillin, vancomycine</li>\n<li>Analgesics - ibuprofen, acetaminophen</li>\n<li>Anti-epileptics - <strong>phenytoin</strong>, carbamazepine</li>\n<li>Heparin</li>\n<li><strong>Amiodarone</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1542","text":"Patients with all of the f‌ollowing usually present ‍with bleeding except:","raw_text":"Patients with all of the following usually present with bleeding except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Heparin-induced thrombocyt‌openia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Drug-induced thrombocytope‌nia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inherited thrombocytopenia‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Infection-induced thromboc‌ytopenia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia</strong></p><p><strong>Heparin-induced thrombocytopeni</strong>a (HIT) is not associated with bleeding. On the contrary, it is associated with an increased risk of <strong>thrombosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>This condition occurs due to the production of <strong>antibodies </strong>against<strong> </strong>the<strong> heparin-PF4 complex</strong> (platelet factor 4, a platelet-specific protein). These antibodies can <strong>activate platelets</strong> and result in thrombosis. It is seen 5-14 days after exposure to heparin.</p>\n<p>These antibodies can be detected using ELISA or serotonin release assay. This condition is managed by <strong>discontinuing heparin</strong> and using alternative anticoagulants.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1543","text":"Which of the following can‌not be used in the treatm‍ent of patients with hepa​rin-induced thrombocytope​nia?","raw_text":"Which of the following cannot be used in the treatment of patients with heparin-induced thrombocytopenia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Danap‌aroid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lepi‌rudin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Argat‌roban","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Enoxa‌parin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Enoxaparin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Enoxaparin</strong></p><p>Low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) such as <strong>enoxaparin</strong> cannot be used in the treatment of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT). This is because <strong>HIT antibodies</strong> (against anti-PF4-heparin complex) may <strong>cross-react</strong> with LMWH.</p>\n<p>HIT can occur following exposure to unfractionated heparin and less commonly with LMWH.</p>\n<p>The treatment of HIT is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Discontinue</strong><strong> heparin</strong></li>\n<li>Anticoagulation with <strong>non-heparin anticoagulants</strong> even in the absence of thrombosis, since there is a high risk of thrombosis.\n<ul>\n<li>Direct thrombin inhibitors: Argatroban, lepirudin, bivalirudin</li>\n<li>Danaparoid (heparinoid)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1545","text":"The CBC of a 33-year-old w‌oman revealed a low plate‍let count. However, furth​er evaluation revealed th​is to be an in vitro samp​ling error. Which of the ‍following is the likely c‌ause of this finding?","raw_text":"The CBC of a 33-year-old woman revealed a low platelet count. However, further evaluation revealed this to be an in vitro sampling error. Which of the following is the likely cause of this finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ED‌TA","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium ‌citrate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hep‌arin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Finger st‌ick sample","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. EDTA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4f203d48f03b432f94a70bab3cd4348fx750x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. EDTA</strong></p><p>This scenario is suggestive of <strong>pseudothrombocytopenia</strong>. It is seen in blood samples collected with <strong>EDTA</strong> as the anticoagulant.</p>\n<p>EDTA causes a <strong>reduction</strong> in the <strong>calcium</strong> content of the collected blood sample. This triggers in vitro <strong>platelet</strong> <strong>clumping</strong> and agglutination by antibodies (usually IgG), as shown below:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>When a low platelet count is obtained with an EDTA sample, the peripheral blood smear should be evaluated. The platelet count must also be determined in blood collected in <strong>sodium citrate </strong>or<strong> heparin </strong>collection tubes or fresh<strong> fingerstick</strong> blood <strong>sample.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4077","text":"Which of the following is ‌false regarding patients ‍with Bernard-Soulier synd​rome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false regarding patients with Bernard-Soulier syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Platelet aggregation tests‌ with epinephrine and ADP‍ are normal","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ristocetin aggregation tes‌t is normal","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Peripheral smear shows lar‌ge platelets","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mild thrombocytopenia is s‌een","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ristocetin aggregation test is normal","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ristocetin aggregation test is normal</strong></p><p>The<strong> ristocetin aggregation</strong> test is <strong>abnormal</strong> (no aggregation) in patients with <strong>Bernard-Soulier</strong> (BS) syndrome.</p>\n<p>It is an inherited disorder of platelet function or <strong>qualitative</strong> platelet disorder due to deficiency of the <strong>Gp Ib-IX </strong>receptor. This is the platelet membrane receptor for the von Willebrand factor (vWF). The binding of the <strong>vWF</strong> to the GpIb-IX-V complex on the platelet membrane is essential for <strong>platelet adhesion</strong> to the vessel wall.</p>\n<p>This condition presents with <strong>mild thrombocytopenia</strong> and peripheral smear shows <strong>large platelets</strong>.</p>\n<p>Platelet aggregation tests are performed to measure platelet activation and aggregation in vitro. Various platelet agonists like ADP, epinephrine, collagen, and ristocetin can be used to perform these tests. In Bernard-Soulier syndrome, the following responses are seen:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>With ristocetin - No response/no aggregation, as <strong>ristocetin </strong>specifically <strong>binds</strong> to the platelet membrane via the <strong>Gp Ib-IXa </strong>receptor</li>\n<li>With <strong>ADP</strong> or <strong>epinephrine</strong> - <strong>Normal response</strong>, as Gp IIb-IIIa receptor is not involved in this process</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1475","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common inherited‍ bleeding disorder?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common inherited bleeding disorder?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemoph‌ilia A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemoph‌ilia B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Immune thrombocytopenia pu‌rpura","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Von Willebr‌and disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Von Willebrand disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Von Willebrand disease</strong></p><p><strong>Von Willebrand disease</strong> (vWD) is the most common inherited bleeding disorder.</p>\n<p>vWD occurs due to deficient or abnormal von Willebrand factor (vWF). vWF is synthesized by endothelial cells and megakaryocytes (gene located on chromosome 12p). It is then stored in endothelial storage vesicles, <strong>Weibel-Palade bodies. </strong>Metalloprotease <strong>ADAMTS13</strong> cleaves ultra-large multimers of VWF which are responsible for platelet adhesion.</p>\n<p>The functions of vWF and the corresponding symptoms include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Adhesion</strong> of the <strong>platelets</strong> to the exposed subendothelium - symptoms of both platelet and bleeding disorders</li>\n<li>Prolongs the half-life of<strong> factor VIII</strong> by binding to it in circulation - hemophilia A like symptoms (rare)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1523","text":"Which of the following is ‌called autosomal hemophil‍ia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is called autosomal hemophilia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Type 2M von Willebrand dis‌ease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Type 2N von Willebrand dis‌ease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Type 2A von Willebrand dis‌ease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Type 2B von Willebrand dis‌ease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Type 2N von Willebrand disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Type 2N von Willebrand disease</strong></p><p><strong>Autosomal hemophilia</strong> is also called <strong>type 2N </strong>von Willebrand disease (<strong>vWD</strong>), as the clinical presentation is similar to hemophilia.</p>\n<p>The types of vWD are:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"height: 169px; width: 459.8125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Type</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 47px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Inheritance</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 412px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Mechanism</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 81.8125px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Important characteristics</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85px; text-align: center;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 47px; text-align: center;\">AD</td>\n<td style=\"width: 412px; text-align: center;\">Partial quantitative deficiency of VWF</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81.8125px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Most common</strong> type</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85px; text-align: center;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 47px; text-align: center;\">AR</td>\n<td style=\"width: 412px; text-align: center;\">Complete quantitative deficiency</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81.8125px; text-align: center;\">Most severe VWD</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85px; text-align: center;\">2A</td>\n<td style=\"width: 47px; text-align: center;\">AD</td>\n<td style=\"width: 412px; text-align: center;\">Loss of VWF multimers (increased cleavage by ADAMS13)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81.8125px; text-align: center;\">Platelet adhesion defect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85px; text-align: center;\">2B</td>\n<td style=\"width: 47px; text-align: center;\">AD</td>\n<td style=\"width: 412px; text-align: center;\">Increased binding of VWF to platelets causing VWF-platelet complex destruction</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81.8125px; text-align: center;\">Thrombocytopenia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85px; text-align: center;\">2M</td>\n<td style=\"width: 47px; text-align: center;\">AD</td>\n<td style=\"width: 412px; text-align: center;\">Decreased affinity of VWF to platelets</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81.8125px; text-align: center;\">Platelet adhesion defect, very rare</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 85px; text-align: center;\">2N</td>\n<td style=\"width: 47px; text-align: center;\"><strong>AR</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 412px; text-align: center;\">Decreased affinity of VWF to Factor VIII</td>\n<td style=\"width: 81.8125px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Autosomal hemophilia</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>In pseudo vWD, the vWF is normal. However, platelets have an increased affinity for vWF. It presents similar to vWD 2B with thrombocytopenia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1479","text":"Which of the following pat‌ients has Heyde's syndrom‍e?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients has Heyde's syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A man with aortic stenosis‌ with gastrointestinal bl‍eeding","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A woman with aortic stenos‌is with intracranial blee‍d","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A man with aortic regurgit‌ation with gastrointestin‍al bleeding","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A woman with aortic regurg‌itation with intracranial‍ bleed","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A man with aortic stenosis with gastrointestinal bleeding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A man with aortic stenosis with gastrointestinal bleeding</strong></p><p><strong>Heyde’s</strong> syndrome is<strong> aortic stenosis</strong> with <strong>gastrointestinal bleeding, </strong>due to <strong>angiodysplasia </strong>of the gastrointestinal tract.</p>\n<p>The shear stress on blood passing through the stenotic aortic valve produces a change in the von Willebrand factor making it susceptible to serum proteases. Hence, large multimer forms are lost, leading to an <strong>acquired type 2 </strong>von Willebrand disease (<strong>vWD</strong>). The condition is reversed with the replacement of the aortic valve.</p>\n<p>Other causes of <strong>acquired</strong> <strong>vWD</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Multiple myeloma</li>\n<li>Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma</li>\n<li>Polycythemia vera</li>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1524","text":"Which of the following lab‌oratory findings is seen ‍in a patient with von Wil​lebrand's disease?","raw_text":"Which of the following laboratory findings is seen in a patient with von Willebrand's disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal ristocetin aggregat‌ion test","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increased b‌leeding time","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Increased prothrombin time‌","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Decreased activated partia‌l thromboplastin time","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Increased bleeding time","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increased bleeding time</strong></p><p><strong>Bleeding time</strong> is <strong>increased</strong> in von Willebrand disease (<strong>vWD</strong>). It is a combination of a platelet adhesion defect with factor VIII deficiency.</p>\n<p>The laboratory findings in vWD include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Platelet count is normal (except type 2B)</li>\n<li>Bleeding time is prolonged </li>\n<li><strong>Ristocetin aggregation </strong>is<strong> abnormal</strong></li>\n<li>Other platelet aggregation tests are normal</li>\n<li>Prothrombin time (PT) is normal</li>\n<li><strong>aPTT </strong>is<strong> prolonged</strong></li>\n<li>VWF activity is decreased</li>\n<li>Factor VIII activity is decreased</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1528","text":"A 28-year-old woman is bei‌ng evaluated for prolonge‍d bleeding following a to​oth extraction. She also ​reports heavy bleeding du​ring menses and recurrent‍ gum bleeds. Labs show in‌creased aPTT, normal PT, ‍and increased bleeding ti​me. Which of the followin​g is the first-line treat​ment for this patient?","raw_text":"A 28-year-old woman is being evaluated for prolonged bleeding following a tooth extraction. She also reports heavy bleeding during menses and recurrent gum bleeds. Labs show increased aPTT, normal PT, and increased bleeding time. Which of the following is the first-line treatment for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tranaxe‌mic acid","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Factor VIII‌ concentrate","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Von Willebrand factor conc‌entrate","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Desmop‌ressin","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Desmopressin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Desmopressin</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>von Willebrand's disease</strong>. The preferred first-line treatment is <strong>desmopressin </strong>(DDAVP).</p>\n<p>This drug acts on endothelial<strong> V2 receptors</strong> causing the <strong>release</strong> of <strong>VWF</strong> and <strong>factor VIII</strong> from endothelial stores. DDAVP can be given intravenously or by an intranasal spray. The side effects include tachyphylaxis and hyponatremia. </p>\n<p><strong>VWF</strong> <strong>concentrate</strong> and <strong>factor VIII</strong> <strong>concentrate</strong> are reserved for patients with <strong>severe disease</strong> or prior to major procedures. Antifibrinolytic therapy (tranexamic acid or ε-aminocaproic acid) may be used for the prevention and treatment of mucosal bleeding.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4079","text":"Which of the following fea‌tures is not seen in pati‍ents with Glanzmann’s thr​ombasthenia?","raw_text":"Which of the following features is not seen in patients with Glanzmann’s thrombasthenia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abnormal platelet aggregat‌ion on adding ADP","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased pl‌atelet count","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Normal von Willebrand fact‌or levels","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Normal platelet aggregatio‌n to ristocetin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Decreased platelet count","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Decreased platelet count</strong></p><p>Thrombocytopenia is not seen in <strong>Glanzmann’s</strong> <strong>thrombasthenia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Glanzmann’s thrombasthenia is a <strong>primary defect</strong> of platelet function (<strong>aggregation</strong>). It occurs due to congenital <strong>absence</strong> of the <strong>GpIIb/IIIa </strong>receptor. It presents as superficial mucocutaneous bleeding with normal platelet count and single <strong>isolated platelets</strong> without clumping on peripheral smear. The levels of <strong>vWF</strong> are <strong>normal</strong>.</p>\n<p>Normally, release of ADP causes platelets to change shape and bind to fibrinogen through the GpIIb/IIIa receptor. In Glanzmann’s thrombasthenia, absence of the receptor causes <strong>abnormal</strong> <strong>aggregation</strong> (absent or reduced) on adding <strong>ADP</strong>. However, aggregation in response to <strong>ristocetin</strong> is <strong>normal</strong>, as it is dependent on GpIb and vWF.</p>\n<p>Secondary platelet dysfunction can be seen in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li>Uremia</li>\n<li>Paraproteinemia</li>\n<li>Myeloproliferative disorders</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1517","text":"A physician decides to sta‌rt a patient with stroke ‍on antiplatelet medicatio​n. All of the following d​rugs would be effective e​xcept:","raw_text":"A physician decides to start a patient with stroke on antiplatelet medication. All of the following drugs would be effective except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clopi‌dogrel","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ticlo‌pidine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ticag‌relor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pago‌clone","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pagoclone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pagoclone</strong></p><p>Pagoclone is an anxiolytic medication. It is not an antiplatelet drug.</p>\n<p>The thienopyridine drugs are ADP receptor or <strong>P2Y12 antagonists</strong> that inhibit ADP mediated platelet aggregation. They include the following drugs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Clopidogrel</strong> - commonly used</li>\n<li><strong>Ticagrelor</strong> - reversible drug with a shorter t<sub>1/2</sub>, does not require activation, may cause mild, self-limited dyspnea </li>\n<li>Prasugrel - 10 times more potent than clopidogrel, rapid onset of action, better absorption, more predictable </li>\n<li><strong>Ticlopidine</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clopidogrel and prasugrel are prodrugs that require <strong>activation</strong> by <strong>cytochrome P450</strong> enzymes. CYP2C19 polymorphisms can cause reduced platelet inhibition with clopidogrel and increased risk of cardiovascular events.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1460","text":"A 24-year-old woman underw‌ent evaluation for bleedi‍ng manifestations and sev​ere anemia. Investigation​s revealed combined autoi​mmune hemolytic anemia an‍d idiopathic thrombocytop‌enic purpura. What is the‍ likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 24-year-old woman underwent evaluation for bleeding manifestations and severe anemia. Investigations revealed combined autoimmune hemolytic anemia and idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Evans s‌yndrome","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"May-Heggl‌in anomaly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fechtner‌ syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Rosenthal‌ syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Evans syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Evans syndrome</strong></p><p>Combined autoimmune hemolytic anemia (<strong>AIHA</strong>) and idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura (<strong>ITP</strong>) is called <strong>Evans syndrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Options B and C: May-Hegglin anomaly and Fechtner syndrome are autosomal dominant hereditary conditions presenting with bleeding, sensorineural hearing loss, glomerulonephritis, and/or eye abnormalities. The severity of the bleeding symptoms is out of proportion to the moderately decreased platelet count.</p>\n<p>Option D: Rosenthal syndrome or hemophilia C refers to factor XI deficiency.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1512","text":"What is the most common si‌de effect experienced by ‍patients taking enteric-c​oated aspirin?","raw_text":"What is the most common side effect experienced by patients taking enteric-coated aspirin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastrointest‌inal symptoms","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Aspirin s‌ensitivity","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Aspirin ‌tolerance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Medication overuse headach‌e","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Gastrointestinal symptoms","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gastrointestinal symptoms</strong></p><p>The most common side effect of <strong>aspirin</strong> is <strong>gastrointestinal</strong> symptoms. The use of enteric-coated aspirin does not reduce these side effects.</p>\n<p>Aspirin irreversibly acetylates and <strong>inhibits</strong> platelet cyclooxygenase <strong>(COX)-1, </strong>which causes reduced synthesis of <strong>thromboxane A2. </strong>At high doses, it also inhibits <strong>COX-2</strong>, which results in the <strong>anti-inflammatory</strong> effects of aspirin.</p>\n<p>The major GI side effects include dyspepsia, erosive gastritis, peptic ulcers with bleeding and perforation. The overall risk of <strong>major bleeding</strong> with aspirin is 1–3% per year, which increases 2-3 fold if there is concurrent use of other antiplatelet drugs or anticoagulants. The risk of bleeding can be <strong>reduced</strong> by <strong>eradication</strong> of <strong><em>Helicobacter pylori</em></strong> infection and giving proton pump inhibitors (<strong>PPIs</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Aspirin sensitivity </strong>refers to bronchospasm or urticaria/angioedema seen immediately after administration of aspirin. It is also called <strong>Samter's</strong> <strong>triad</strong> (asthma, recurrent nasal polyps, aspirin sensitivity). In patients with <strong>aspirin resistance,</strong> there is failure of aspirin to produce its normal platelet inhibitory functions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1514","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect about the mecha‍nism of action of dipyrid​amole?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect about the mechanism of action of dipyridamole?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increases levels of cyclic‌ AMP in platelets","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Blocks the reuptake of ade‌nosine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibits phosphodiesterase‌-mediated cyclic AMP degr‍adation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increases intracellular ad‌enosine levels","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Increases intracellular adenosine levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/83f105195b784a118f2d762379820034x1280x1308.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Increases intracellular adenosine levels</strong></p><p><strong>Dipyridamole</strong> increases <strong>extracellular adenosine</strong> levels.</p>\n<p>It is an antiplatelet drug that <strong>increases</strong> levels of cyclic AMP (<strong>cAMP</strong>) in platelets by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blocking the reuptake of adenosine and increasing extracellular adenosine levels</li>\n<li>Inhibiting phosphodiesterase-mediated cAMP degradation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This increase in cyclic AMP reduces intracellular levels of calcium. This inhibits platelet activation and aggregation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Dipyridamole is a weak antiplatelet used in combination with aspirin for the prevention of stroke in patients with TIA.</p>\n<p>Side effects include gastrointestinal and vasodilatory complaints (headache, flushing, dizziness, hypotension). Due to it's vasodilatory effects, it is contraindicated in patients with symptomatic coronary artery disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1518","text":"A 62-year-old man diagnose‌d with myocardial infarct‍ion is planned for percut​aneous intervention. He w​as diagnosed with diabeti​c nephropathy 2 months ag‍o. The cardiologist decid‌es to start him on aspiri‍n and an additional antip​latelet drug. Which of th​e following can be used f​or this patient?","raw_text":"A 62-year-old man diagnosed with myocardial infarction is planned for percutaneous intervention. He was diagnosed with diabetic nephropathy 2 months ago. The cardiologist decides to start him on aspirin and an additional antiplatelet drug. Which of the following can be used for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abcixim‌ab only","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Abciximab or‌ eptifibatide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tirofiban or‌ eptifibatide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tirofib‌an only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Abciximab only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Abciximab only</strong></p><p><strong>Abciximab</strong> is <strong>safe</strong> in patients with <strong>renal impairment</strong>. It belongs to the group of <strong>GpIIb-IIIa</strong> receptor antagonists. These are parenteral drugs that are used in the management of patients with acute coronary syndrome.</p>\n<p>There are three important drugs in this group:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 149px;\" width=\"604\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Feature</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Abciximab</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Eptifibatide</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.667px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Tirofiban</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 35px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 35px; text-align: center;\">Gp IIb/IIIa antagonist</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 35px; text-align: center;\"> Non-specific</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 35px; text-align: center;\">Specific</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.667px; height: 35px; text-align: center;\">Specific</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\">Plasma half-life</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\">Short (min)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\">Long (2.5 h)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.667px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\">Long (2.0 h)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 25.3333px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 25.3333px; text-align: center;\">Platelet-bound half-life</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 25.3333px; text-align: center;\">Long (days)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 25.3333px; text-align: center;\">Short (s)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.667px; height: 25.3333px; text-align: center;\">Short (s)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 17px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\">Renal clearance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.667px; height: 17px; text-align: center;\">Yes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Immune-mediated <strong>thrombocytopenia</strong> and bleeding are the most common side effects. Thrombocytopenia can occur <strong>within 24 hours</strong> of initiation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4072","text":"Plasmapheresis is the main‌stay of treatment in pati‍ents with which of the fo​llowing?","raw_text":"Plasmapheresis is the mainstay of treatment in patients with which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autoimmune hemolytic anemi‌a","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Disseminated intravascular‌ coagulation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Thrombotic thrombocytopeni‌c purpura","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hemolytic ur‌emic syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura</strong></p><p><strong>Plasmapheresis</strong> is the mainstay of the treatment of patients with thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (<strong>TTP</strong>).</p>\n<p>The best response to this therapy is seen in patients with <strong>ADAMTS13</strong> <strong>autoantibodies</strong>. Plasmapheresis is continued until the resolution of platelet count and signs of hemolysis for at least 2 days.</p>\n<p>Option A: The first line of treatment of autoimmune hemolytic anemia is glucocorticoids. The second line includes rituximab and splenectomy.</p>\n<p>Option B: Treatment of disseminated intravascular coagulation is primarily supportive with measures to treat the underlying cause.</p>\n<p>Option D: Treatment of HUS is primarily supportive<strong>.</strong> Dialysis is usually required. In some varients of HUS plasma infusion/exchange can be effective by replasing the complement-regulatory proteins.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4070","text":"Which of the following is ‌not seen in patients with‍ thrombotic thrombocytope​nic purpura?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not seen in patients with thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemolyti‌c anemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Focal neurological deficit‌s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Venous thro‌mboembolism","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Elevated ser‌um creatinine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Venous thromboembolism","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Venous thromboembolism</strong></p><p>Large vessel arterial or venous thromboemboli are not seen in thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (<strong>TTP</strong>). This condition is associated with <strong>microvascular</strong> thrombosis.</p>\n<p><strong>TTP</strong> occurs due to deficiency or autoantibodies against the metalloprotease <strong>ADAMTS13</strong>, which normally cleaves multimers of vWF. In this condition, the vWF molecules persist causing pathogenic platelet adhesion and aggregation. This causes localized<strong> platelet thrombi </strong>and<strong> fibrin deposits</strong> at various sites.</p>\n<p>It presents with the following pentad of clinical features:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Microangiopathic <strong>hemolytic anemia</strong> - mechanical shearing of RBCs as they pass through platelet rich-microthrombi</li>\n<li>Thrombocytopenia - deposition of platelets in microthrombi</li>\n<li><strong>Renal failure</strong> - microthrombi in the renal vasculature</li>\n<li>Neurologic findings - headache, confusion, transient <strong>focal neurologic deficits</strong></li>\n<li>Fever</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4071","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrect regarding patie‍nts with thrombotic throm​bocytopenic purpura?","raw_text":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding patients with thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clinical features overlap ‌with hemolytic uremic syn‍drome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"PT and aPTT ‌are prolonged","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is common in patients w‌ith HIV infection","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It can be associated with ‌use of quinine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. PT and aPTT are prolonged","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. PT and aPTT are prolonged</strong></p><p>In patients with thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (<strong>TTP</strong>), the <strong>PT</strong> and <strong>aPTT</strong> are <strong>normal</strong>, as consumptive coagulopathy is not seen.</p>\n<p>TTP and <strong>HUS</strong> are thrombotic microangiopathies with overlapping clinical features. TTP can be primary (ADAMTS13 deficiency) or secondary (associated with infections like <strong>HIV</strong> and drugs like <strong>quinine</strong>).</p>\n<p>Note: In disseminated intravascular coagulation (<strong>DIC</strong>), thrombocytopenia and microangiopathy are seen. It is associated with consumption of clotting factors and fibrinogen resulting in prolonged PT and aPTT.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1507","text":"What is the most common ge‌netic abnormality seen in‍ patients with recurrent ​history of venous thrombo​sis?","raw_text":"What is the most common genetic abnormality seen in patients with recurrent history of venous thrombosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Factor V Lei‌den mutation","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Prothrombi‌n mutation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Protein C ‌deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Antithrombin III deficienc‌y","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Factor V Leiden mutation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Factor V Leiden mutation</strong></p><p><strong>Factor V Leiden</strong> mutation is the most common <strong>inherited</strong> cause of venous thrombosis. Venous thrombosis typically manifests as deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary thromboembolism. </p>\n<p>Hypercoagulable states or thrombophilias can be inherited or acquired:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inherited causes</strong> in decreasing order of prevalence are as follows:\n<ul>\n<li>Factor V Leiden mutation</li>\n<li>Prothrombin mutation</li>\n<li>Protein C deficiency</li>\n<li>Protein S deficiency</li>\n<li>Antithrombin III deficiency</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Acquired causes</strong> include:\n<ul>\n<li>Surgery</li>\n<li>Malignancy</li>\n<li>Antiphospholipid syndrome</li>\n<li>Trauma</li>\n<li>Pregnancy</li>\n<li>Long-haul travel</li>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n<li>Oral contraceptives/hormone replacement</li>\n<li>Myeloproliferative disorders</li>\n<li>Polycythemia vera</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4075","text":"Which of the following qua‌litative platelet disorde‍rs are inherited as autos​omal dominant?","raw_text":"Which of the following qualitative platelet disorders are inherited as autosomal dominant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glanzman th‌rombasthenia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bernard Soul‌ier syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chediak-Higa‌shi syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Platelet storage pool synd‌rome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Platelet storage pool syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9d84bfdb468e467b9f06e8f391a6bf7ex1280x2203.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Platelet storage pool syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Platelet storage pool disease</strong> (SPD) is an example of an <strong>autosomal dominant</strong> qualitative platelet disorder. It refers to a group of disorders characterized by a deficiency of platelet granules causing secretion defects.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Bernard Soulier syndrome, Glanzman thrombasthenia, and Chediak-Higashi syndrome are inherited as<strong> autosomal recessive</strong> traits.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"O2r12jZJ","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"92_acute_leukemia","title":"92.Acute Leukemia","num_questions":24,"total_marks":96,"duration":34,"time_per_question":85.0,"questions":[{"id":"MC2027","text":"What is the most common ty​pe of leukemia in adults?‌","raw_text":"What is the most common type of leukemia in adults?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute myelo​id leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adult acute lymphoblastic ​leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chronic myel​oid leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chronic lymp​hoid leukemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Chronic lymphoid leukemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Chronic lymphoid leukemia</strong></p><p>Chronic lymphoid leukemia (<strong>CLL</strong>) is the most common type of leukemia in <strong>adults</strong>.</p>\n<p>The common types of leukemia in various age groups is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute myeloid leukemia (<strong>AML</strong>) - most common <strong>acute leukemia</strong> in adults</li>\n<li>Acute lymphoid leukemia (<strong>ALL</strong>) - most common cancer in <strong>childhood. </strong>The most common subtype of ALL is <strong>B-cell</strong> ALL</li>\n<li>Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma (<strong>NHL</strong>) - most common<strong> lymphoid </strong>malignancy in<strong> adults</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2034","text":"Which of the following is ​not a risk factor for acu‌te myeloid leukemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a risk factor for acute myeloid leukemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Down's ​syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bloom s​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fanconi​ anemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Evan's ​syndrome","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Evan's syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Evan&#x27;s syndrome</strong></p><p>Evan's syndrome does not predispose to acute myeloid leukemia (<strong>AML</strong>).</p>\n<p>The following conditions predispose to AML:</p>\n<p><strong>Genetic conditions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Down's syndrome</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Young children &lt;4 years</li>\n<li>Typically of the acute megakaryocytic subtype and associated with GATA1 mutations</li>\n<li>Excellent clinical outcomes </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Defective DNA repair in conditions like ataxia telangiectasia,<strong> Fanconi anemia, </strong>and<strong> Bloom syndrome</strong> </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Environmental factors:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drugs: \n<ul>\n<li>Alkylating agents<strong> - c</strong>yclophosphamide, melphalan, dacarbazine, platinum compounds, after 4-6 years of exposure</li>\n<li>Topoisomerase II inhibitors <strong>-</strong> etoposide, after 1-3 years of exposure.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Radiation - high dose radiation, therapeutic radiation in patients exposed to alkylating agents</li>\n<li>Chemicals - benzene, petroleum products, pesticide</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hematological disorders with increased risk of AML:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Myeloproliferative disorders</li>\n<li>Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria</li>\n<li>Aplastic anemia</li>\n<li>Myelodysplastic syndrome</li>\n<li>Multiple myeloma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4236","text":"A 2-year-old girl was refe​rred with recurrent bacte‌rial infections. Parents ‍report that their first c‌hild also had similar fea​tures and died at the age​ of 18 months from myeloi‌d leukemia. Investigation‌s revealed markedly reduc​ed absolute neutrophil co​unt. What is the likely d​iagnosis?","raw_text":"A 2-year-old girl was referred with recurrent bacterial infections. Parents report that their first child also had similar features and died at the age of 18 months from myeloid leukemia. Investigations revealed markedly reduced absolute neutrophil count. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bloom s​yndrome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kostmann​ disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Diamond-Blac​kfan syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chediak-Higa​shi syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Kostmann disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Kostmann disease</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario of <strong>congenital neutropenia</strong> is suggestive of <strong>Kostmann syndrome</strong>. It is associated with an increased risk of acute myeloid leukemia (<strong>AML</strong>).</p>\n<p>Kostmann syndrome occurs due to mutations in<strong> </strong>the <strong>G-CSF receptor</strong> and neutrophil elastase. This causes bone marrow failure specifically in the myeloid lineage. It presents in young children as <strong>recurrent</strong> <strong>bacterial</strong> <strong>infections</strong> (such as otitis media, cellulitis) and oral ulcers. </p>\n<p>Option A: Bloom syndrome occurs due to defects in DNA repair. It is not associated with neutropenia but has a higher risk of AML. </p>\n<p>Option C: Diamond-Blackfan syndrome refers to congenital erythroid aplasia. It is also associated with a higher risk of AML. </p>\n<p>Option D: Chediak-Higashi syndrome is due to defective phagolysosome formation. It is not associated with neutropenia.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3648","text":"A 60-year-old man is being​ evaluated for suspected ‌acute myeloid leukemia. H‍e reports a fast-growing ‌swelling in his mouth for​ the past 1 week. On exam​ination, there is a green‌ish gingival mass with a ‌necrotic surface. This pr​esentation is likely asso​ciated with which of the ​following?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man is being evaluated for suspected acute myeloid leukemia. He reports a fast-growing swelling in his mouth for the past 1 week. On examination, there is a greenish gingival mass with a necrotic surface. This presentation is likely associated with which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"AML​ M2","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"AML​ M3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AML​ M7","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"AML​ M5a","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. AML M2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. AML M2</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of a <strong>chloroma</strong>. It is commonly associated with the<strong> M2</strong> subtype of acute myeloid leukemia, also called <strong>AML with maturation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Chloroma is also called<strong> myeloid sarcoma</strong>, extramedullary myeloid tumor, or granulocytic sarcoma. It is an extramedullary tumor mass consisting of myeloid blasts, with or without maturation<strong>. </strong>It is called chloroma due to the peculiar <strong>green color</strong>, caused by <strong>myeloperoxidase</strong>. The sites affected include <strong>orbit</strong> (most common), skull, oral cavity, paranasal sinuses, sternum, ribs, vertebrae, and pelvis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4258","text":"Patients suffering from wh​ich type of AML are more ‌prone to develop dissemin‍ated intravascular coagul‌ation?","raw_text":"Patients suffering from which type of AML are more prone to develop disseminated intravascular coagulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mono​cytic","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myelomo​nocytic","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Monob​lastic","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Acute pro​myelocytic","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Acute promyelocytic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Acute promyelocytic</strong></p><p>Disseminated intravascular coagulation (<strong>DIC</strong>) is commonly seen in patients with acute promyelocytic leukemia (<strong>APL</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2246","text":"Oral cavity examination of​ a 56-year-old man with s‌uspected leukemia reveale‍d the following. What sub‌type of AML is he likely ​to be suffering from?","raw_text":"Oral cavity examination of a 56-year-old man with suspected leukemia revealed the following. What subtype of AML is he likely to be suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M​7","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"M​5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"M​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"M​2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. M5","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b9a5857597db45929ff7e63d72113b3d.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. M5</strong></p><p>The given image shows swollen and spongy gums, indicating <strong>gum infiltration</strong>. This is a characteristic feature of acute monocytic leukemia (<strong>AML-M5</strong>).</p>\n<p>Infiltration of the gingivae, skin, soft tissues or meninges with leukemic blasts is characteristic of the monocytic subtypes and those with 11q23 chromosomal abnormalities.</p>\n<p>Less commonly, infiltration is also seen in acute myelomonocytic leukemia (<strong>AML-M4</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4238","text":"Which of the following wou​ld be seen on flow cytome‌try of a patient with acu‍te megakaryoblastic leuke‌mia?","raw_text":"Which of the following would be seen on flow cytometry of a patient with acute megakaryoblastic leukemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD​113","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CD ​117","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD​ 61","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"CD​ 13","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CD 61","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CD 61</strong></p><p>On flow cytometry, the immunophenotype of <strong>acute megakaryoblastic leukemia</strong> is <strong>CD41</strong> and/or <strong>CD61</strong> positive (platelet-specific antigens).</p>\n<p>Flow cytometry is useful in establishing the different subtypes of AML:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 190px; width: 357.947px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\"><strong>Phenotype</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.947px;\"><strong>CD markers</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">Myeloblastic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.947px;\">CD11b,13,33,117</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">Promyelocytic(M3)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.947px;\">CD13,33</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">Myelomonocytic(M4)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.947px;\">CD11b,13,14,33</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">Monocytic(M5)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.947px;\">CD11b,11c,13,14,33 </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">Erythroid(M6)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.947px;\">Glycophorin,spectrin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">Megakaryoblastic(M7)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.947px;\">CD41,61</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">Basophilic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.947px;\">CD11,13,33,123</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 141px;\">Mast cell</td>\n<td style=\"width: 204.947px;\">CD13,33,117</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2088","text":"Which of the following is ​true about the WHO classi‌fication of acute myeloid‍ leukemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the WHO classification of acute myeloid leukemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The clinical features are ​not considered to define ‌the subtype","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The cutoff value of blast ​cells for diagnosis is 20‌%","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It is only based on morpho​logical features of myelo‌id precursors","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Chromosomal translocations​ along with &amp;gt; 20% blas‌ts are needed for diagnos‍is","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. The cutoff value of blast cells for diagnosis is 20%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The cutoff value of blast cells for diagnosis is 20%</strong></p><p>The cutoff value of <strong>blast cells</strong> for the diagnosis of <strong>AML</strong> is <strong>20%</strong> according to the <strong>WHO</strong> classification. This classification is based on the <strong>clinical features, cytogenetic </strong>and<strong> molecular</strong> abnormalities in addition to <strong>morphology</strong>. This is in contrast to the previously used French-American-British (<strong>FAB</strong>) classification which was based on <strong>only</strong> <strong>morphology</strong> of myeloid precursors.</p>\n<p>The major changes in the WHO classification in contrast to FAB classification are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blast cutoff for diagnosis of AML is <strong>20%</strong> (30% in FAB classification) </li>\n<li>Specific <strong>chromosomal rearrangements</strong> such as t (8;21), inv(16), t(16;16) and t (15;17) can define AML even with<strong> &lt; 20% blasts</strong> </li>\n<li><strong>Immunophenotyping</strong> (flow cytometry) is used to differentiate AML from ALL and to identify some subtypes of AML </li>\n<li>Myeloid dysplasia with blasts between 20-30% can benefit from therapy for myelodysplastic syndrome (decitabine, 5-azacytidine, cedazuridine)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0515","text":"A 50-year-old man is refer​red for evaluation of sev‌ere anemia, persistent fe‍ver, and recurrent epista‌xis. Lab investigations a​re given below. What is t​he diagnosis? Hb = 6 g/dL‌ Total WBC count - 40,000‌/ mm 3 Platelet count - 1​2,000/cu.mm Bone marrow -​ 10% blasts, positive for​ t(8;21) translocation","raw_text":"<p>A 50-year-old man is referred for evaluation of severe anemia, persistent fever, and recurrent epistaxis. Lab investigations are given below. What is the diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Hb = 6 g/dL</p>\n<p>Total WBC count - 40,000/ mm<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p>Platelet count - 12,000/cu.mm</p>\n<p>Bone marrow - 10% blasts, positive for t(8;21) translocation</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute lymphoblastic leukem​ia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute myeloblastic leukemi​a","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Prele​ukemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aleukemic​ leukemia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acute myeloblastic leukemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acute myeloblastic leukemia</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute myeloblastic leukemia</strong> (AML).</p>\n<p>According to the <strong>WHO criteria</strong>, the diagnosis of AML requires any<strong> one </strong>of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Blast cell count ≥20% either in the peripheral blood or bone marrow</li>\n<li><strong>Presence</strong> of any one of the following <strong>cytogenetic abnormalities</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"1\" data-font=\"\" data-leveltext=\"%1)\" data-listid=\"17\">t(15;17) </li>\n<li>t(8;21) </li>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"3\" data-font=\"\" data-leveltext=\"%1)\" data-listid=\"17\">Inversion 16 or t(16;16)</li>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"3\" data-font=\"\" data-leveltext=\"%1)\" data-listid=\"17\">t(9;22)</li>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"3\" data-font=\"\" data-leveltext=\"%1)\" data-listid=\"17\">NPM1 mutation (Nucleophosmin mutation)</li>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"3\" data-font=\"\" data-leveltext=\"%1)\" data-listid=\"17\">CEBPA mutation</li>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"3\" data-font=\"\" data-leveltext=\"%1)\" data-listid=\"17\">FLT3-ITD</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2136","text":"Patients with all of the f​ollowing are classified a‌s AML with recurrent cyto‍genetic abnormalities exc‌ept:","raw_text":"Patients with all of the following are classified as AML with recurrent cytogenetic abnormalities except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"t(16​,16)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"NPM1 m​utation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CEBPA m​utation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"t(9​,22)","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. t(9,22)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. t(9,22)</strong></p><p>The chromosomal translocation <strong>t(9,22)</strong> is called the Philadelphia chromosome. It is seen in patients with <strong>CML</strong>.</p>\n<p>AML with recurrent genetic abnormalities includes the following chromosomal aberrations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"1\" data-font=\"\" data-leveltext=\"%1)\" data-listid=\"17\">t(15;17) </li>\n<li>t(8;21) </li>\n<li aria-setsize=\"-1\" data-aria-level=\"1\" data-aria-posinset=\"3\" data-font=\"\" data-leveltext=\"%1)\" data-listid=\"17\">Inversion 16 or<strong> t(16;16)</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Two gene mutations have been added to the criteria of AML with recurrent genetic abnormalities in addition to chromosomal aberrations. Both gene mutations are associated with a <strong>good prognosis.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NPM1</strong> mutation: Nucleophosmin mutation</li>\n<li><strong>CEBPA</strong> mutation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4239","text":"Which of the following cyt​ogenetic abnormalities is‌ seen in patients with ac‍ute promyelocytic leukemi‌a?","raw_text":"Which of the following cytogenetic abnormalities is seen in patients with acute promyelocytic leukemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"t(15​;17)","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"t(16​;16)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"RUNX1-​RUNX1T1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CBF-β m​utation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. t(15;17)","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. t(15;17)</strong></p><p><strong>t(15,17)</strong> is associated with acute promyelocytic leukemia (<strong>APL</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4251","text":"A 65-year-old woman presen​ted with fatigue and feve‌r for the past 2 months. ‍Investigations revealed p‌ancytopenia and the follo​wing peripheral smear fin​dings. What is the most l‌ikely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 65-year-old woman presented with fatigue and fever for the past 2 months. Investigations revealed pancytopenia and the following peripheral smear findings. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A​ML","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A​LL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"C​ML","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"C​LL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. AML","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1de3afe66ebb411baf86ba59f5c91a55.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. AML</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario and peripheral smear showing blast cells with<strong> peroxidase granules</strong> are suggestive of acute myeloid leukemia (<strong>AML</strong>). Peroxidase granules are <strong>dark blue-stained</strong> granules seen in the cytoplasm on peroxidase staining. </p>\n<p>Myeloperoxidase cytochemical activity is negative in chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) and blast crisis.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4248","text":"Which of the following pat​ients with AML has a bett‌er prognosis than the oth‍ers?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients with AML has a better prognosis than the others?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 48-year-old man newly di​agnosed with AML with a f‌amily history of lymphoma‍","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A 60-year-old woman who de​veloped AML following che‌motherapy for ovarian can‍cer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 71-year-old man with a l​ongstanding history of my‌elodysplastic syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 67-year-old woman evalua​ted with a 4-month histor‌y of pancytopenia prior t‍o diagnosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. A 48-year-old man newly diagnosed with AML with a family history of lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A 48-year-old man newly diagnosed with AML with a family history of lymphoma</strong></p><p><strong>Young age</strong> of onset is associated with a <strong>good prognosis</strong> in patients with AML. Family history does not affect the prognosis.</p>\n<p>Poor prognosis is seen in the following cases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Advancing age</strong> (usually defined as <strong>&gt; 60 years</strong>) </li>\n<li>Previous hematologic disorders - myeloproliferative neoplasms and <strong>myelodysplastic</strong> syndrome</li>\n<li>Longstanding <strong>cytopenias &gt;3 months</strong> before diagnosis </li>\n<li>Therapy-related AML (<strong>chemotherapy</strong> for other cancers)</li>\n<li>Chromosomal aberrations with poor outcomes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2144","text":"A pathologist is examining​ the peripheral smear of ‌a patient with suspected ‍leukemia. All of the foll‌owing features are sugges​tive of a myeloblast exce​pt:","raw_text":"A pathologist is examining the peripheral smear of a patient with suspected leukemia. All of the following features are suggestive of a myeloblast except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Auer​ rods","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Prominent​ nucleoli","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Phi b​odies","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pelger-Hu​et anomaly","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Pelger-Huet anomaly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eb2d4b735e274611a79fe8ed2d02baa9x1064x1634.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Pelger-Huet anomaly</strong></p><p>Pelger-Huet anomaly is not a feature of myeloblasts. <strong>Auer rods</strong> and <strong>Phi bodies</strong> are considered <strong>pathognomonic</strong> of <strong>AML.</strong></p>\n<p>Myeloblasts have the following characteristics as shown in the following image:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Large, uniform population of cells</li>\n<li><strong>Prominent nucleoli</strong> (yellow arrow in the image)</li>\n<li>Primary cytoplasmic granules</li>\n<li><strong>Auer rods -</strong> needle-shaped azurophilic intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies formed by fusion of lysosomes (black arrow)</li>\n<li><strong>Phi-bodies -</strong> fusiform or spindle-shaped rods similar to Auer rods that require special stains for hydroperoxidases</li>\n<li>Pseudo-Chediak-Higashi syndrome - Giant lysosomes forming intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Pelger-Huet</strong> anomaly refers to neutrophils with <strong>bilobed nuclei</strong> that occur as a benign, inherited condition. <strong>Pseudo-Pelger-Huet</strong> anomaly refers to an acquired defect seen in <strong>CML</strong>, <strong>myelodysplastic</strong> syndrome. </p>\n<p>Note: Chediak-Higashi syndrome is a rare autosomal recessive disorder with abnormal lysosomal trafficking (LYST gene). Presents with oculocutaneous albinism and immunodeficiency.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2141","text":"A patient presents to the ​ER with a sudden-onset fe‌ver and the following pai‍nful rash over her face a‌nd upper limbs. CBC revea​ls neutrophilia. This pre​sentation is associated w‌ith all of the following ‌conditions except:","raw_text":"A patient presents to the ER with a sudden-onset fever and the following painful rash over her face and upper limbs. CBC reveals neutrophilia. This presentation is associated with all of the following conditions except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute myelo​id leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Crohn's​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Systemic lupus erythematos​us","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aplasti​c anemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Aplastic anemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/90fc36735ada43a1ac4e06c99141b0a7.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Aplastic anemia</strong></p><p>This clinical presentation with painful edematous plaques is suggestive of <strong>febrile neutrophilic dermatosis</strong> or <strong>Sweet syndrome</strong>. It is not associated with aplastic anemia. It is associated with the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute myeloid leukemia (<strong>AML</strong>) - most common</li>\n<li>Inflammatory bowel disease (<strong>IBD</strong>)</li>\n<li>Solid malignancies of the genitourinary tract</li>\n<li>Systemic lupus erythematosus (<strong>SLE</strong>)</li>\n<li>Drugs - all-trans-retinoic acid, granulocyte colony-stimulating factor (G-CSF)</li>\n<li>Idiopathic Sweet syndrome - common in women following respiratory tract infection</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This condition presents with the following triad:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fever</li>\n<li>Neutrophilia</li>\n<li>Dense dermal infiltrate of neutrophils: \n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pinkish-red</strong> to reddish-brown edematous <strong>painful</strong> plaques</li>\n<li>Occur over the head, neck, extremities</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4263","text":"What is the most commonly ​used complete remission i‌nduction regimen in patie‍nts with AML?","raw_text":"What is the most commonly used complete remission induction regimen in patients with AML?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All-trans-retinoic acid an​d arsenic trioxide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"L- aspa​rginase","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cytarabine with anthracycl​ines","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cytarabine with alkylating​ agents","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cytarabine with anthracyclines","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cytarabine with anthracyclines</strong></p><p>The most commonly used complete remission induction regimen in patients with <strong>AML</strong> is <strong>cytarabine</strong> and an <strong>anthracycline</strong> (e.g., daunorubicin, idarubicin).</p>\n<p>The treatment of AML is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Induction </strong>chemotherapy with a 7+3 regimen:<strong> </strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Cytarabine - continuous intravenous infusion for <strong>7 days</strong></li>\n<li>IV daunorubicin on <strong>days</strong> <strong>1,2,3</strong></li>\n<li>Side effects include neutropenia, sepsis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Post-remission </strong>therapy: \n<ul>\n<li>High dose cytarabine</li>\n<li>Allogenic hematopoeitic stem cell transplant</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Novel agents include gemtuzumab ozogamicin, a CD33 monoclonal antibody.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4242","text":"A 60-year-old man came wit​h generalized bone pain, ‌and easy bruisability for‍ the past 6 months. His W‌BC count was 60,000 cu. m​m and bone marrow aspirat​ion revealed the followin‌g. Immunohistochemistry w‌as positive for MPO and c​ytogenetic studies showed​ presence of t(15:17). Wh​at is the preferred treat‌ment regimen?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man came with generalized bone pain, and easy bruisability for the past 6 months. His WBC count was 60,000 cu. mm and bone marrow aspiration revealed the following. Immunohistochemistry was positive for MPO and cytogenetic studies showed presence of t(15:17). What is the preferred treatment regimen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytar​abine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Arsenic ​trioxide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"All transre​tinoic acid","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Arsenic trioxide and all t​ransretinoic acid","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Arsenic trioxide and all transretinoic acid","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/673359cd5a6240de8e1730bbd82e271c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3a7241b9ca1c4a11b931b0fb6cc49d4fx250x364.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Arsenic trioxide and all transretinoic acid</strong></p><p>The presence of blast cells with numerous Auer rods, <strong>MPO</strong> (myeloperoxidase) positive cells, and <strong>t(15:17)</strong> is diagnostic of acute promyelocytic leukemia (<strong>APL</strong>). The preferred treatment regimen is <strong>arsenic trioxide</strong> and all-trans-retinoic acid (<strong>ATRA</strong>).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>APL is characterized by<strong> t(15; 17)</strong> translocation resulting in the <strong>PML-RAR-alpha</strong> fusion gene (promyelocyte-retinoic acid receptor alpha). It is a <strong>highly curable </strong>subtype of leukemia. The treatment is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Induction therapy:\n<ul>\n<li>ATRA + anthracycline (daunorubicin or idarubicin) or cytarabine (<span style=\"text-align: center;\">OR)</span></li>\n<li>ATRA + arsenic trioxide </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Consolidation therapy:\n<ul>\n<li>Post remission anthracycline-based chemotherapy</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MA1143","text":"An 18-year-old girl with t​he diagnosis of acute pro‌myelocytic leukemia was t‍reated medically. She dev‌eloped fever and tachypne​a and a chest X-ray showe​d pulmonary infiltrates. ‌What drug should she be g‌iven next?","raw_text":"An 18-year-old girl with the diagnosis of acute promyelocytic leukemia was treated medically. She developed fever and tachypnea and a chest X-ray showed pulmonary infiltrates. What drug should she be given next?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytar​abine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dexame​thasone","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Doxor​ubicin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Methot​rexate","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Dexamethasone","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Dexamethasone</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario describes a patient of <strong>APL </strong>(acute promyelocytic leukemia) developing <strong>fever, tachypnea</strong>, and<strong> pulmonary infiltrates</strong> while <strong>on medical treatment</strong>. This is suggestive of <strong>APL differentiation syndrome</strong>, an <strong>adverse effect </strong>of <strong>all-trans-retinoic acid (ATRA)</strong>. The management involves glucocorticoids like <strong>dexamethasone</strong>. </p>\n<p>Along with <strong>glucocorticoids</strong>, the other management of APL differentiation syndrome includes <strong>chemotherapy </strong>for <strong>cytoreduction </strong>and <strong>supportive measures</strong>. In <strong>severe </strong>cases,<strong> temporary discontinuation of ATRA</strong> is necessary.</p>\n<p><strong>ATRA</strong> is an oral drug that induces the <strong>differentiation</strong> of leukemic cells bearing the <strong>t(15;17) </strong>chromosomal translocation. While ATRA reduces the incidence of <strong>DIC</strong> associated with <strong>cytarabine</strong> and <strong>daunorubicin</strong> used earlier in APL, it can cause <strong>APL differentiation syndrome</strong> due to the <strong>adhesion </strong>of differentiated neoplastic cells to the <strong>pulmonary vasculature endothelium</strong>.</p>\n<p>In <strong>low-risk APL </strong>patients with a low leukocyte count at presentation, a combination of <strong>ATRA </strong>and <strong>arsenic trioxide (ATO)</strong> has been found to be superior and is the current standard of care.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1700","text":"A 47-year-old man with a d​iagnosis of acute myeloid‌ leukemia with a blood ty‍pe O negative blood group‌ presents to the transpla​nt clinic to discuss proc​eeding with an allogeneic‌ stem cell transplant. Wh‌ich of the following woul​d be an optimal donor?","raw_text":"A 47-year-old man with a diagnosis of acute myeloid leukemia with a blood type O negative blood group presents to the transplant clinic to discuss proceeding with an allogeneic stem cell transplant. Which of the following would be an optimal donor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"His identical twin brother​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Umbilical co​rd transplant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"His HLA identical 50-year-​old brother who is otherw‌ise healthy and is blood ‍type O+","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"An HLA identical matched u​nrelated donor who is blo‌od type O–","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. His HLA identical 50-year-old brother who is otherwise healthy and is blood type O+","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. His HLA identical 50-year-old brother who is otherwise healthy and is blood type O+</strong></p><p>In the case of acute myeloid leukemia (AML), <strong>allogeneic</strong> stem cell transplants are the <strong>treatment of choice</strong>. His<strong> HLA identical 50-year-old brother</strong> who is blood type <strong>O positive</strong> would be the <strong>best choice</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other options:</p>\n<p>Option A: An <strong>identical twin brother</strong> would <strong>not</strong> be the donor of choice. The therapeutic benefit of stem cell transplant in hematological malignancies is due to the <strong>graft-versus-leukemia (GvL)</strong>. Donor T lymphocytes attack malignant hematopoietic stem cells of the recipient that remain after high-dose chemotherapy. Despite having a <strong>low rate of graft versus host disease</strong> (GvHD), stem cells from an identical twin <strong>do not have</strong> a desirable degree of <strong>GvL effect</strong>, leading to <strong>higher</strong> rates of <strong>recurrence</strong> post-transplant. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Autologous</strong> (umbilical cord transplant) is <strong>not recommended</strong> as <strong>higher</strong> rates of <strong>relapse</strong> have been reported.</p>\n<p>Option D: An <strong>HLA identical sibling</strong> is <strong>preferred over</strong> an <strong>unrelated HLA identical donor </strong>because there is a greater chance of matching for <strong>minor histocompatibility complex</strong> (mHC). This <strong>reduces</strong> the risk of <strong>GvHD</strong>. ABO compatibility is also important to consider, but a minor ABO mismatch (Rh mismatch) doesn't have any impact on hemolysis, graft-versus-host disease, or survival.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2017","text":"Individuals in which age g​roup have the highest inc‌idence of T-cell acute ly‍mphoblastic lymphoma?","raw_text":"Individuals in which age group have the highest incidence of T-cell acute lymphoblastic lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20-25​ years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1-10 ​years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"10-19​ years","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"26-30​ years","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 10-19 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 10-19 years</strong></p><p>The highest incidence of <strong>T-ALL</strong> (T-cell acute lymphoblastic lymphoma) is at <strong>10-19 years </strong>(adolescence) when the thymus reaches the maximum size.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, <strong>B-ALL</strong> which is more common has a peak incidence at <strong>3 years</strong> (1-10 years). At this age, the number of normal bone marrow pre-B cells (the cell of origin) is at its greatest.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4269","text":"Children with Down's syndr​ome are at an increased r‌isk of developing all of ‍the following except:","raw_text":"Children with Down's syndrome are at an increased risk of developing all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute lymph​oid leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute myelo​id leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Transient myeloproliferati​ve disorder","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Myelodysplas​tic disorder","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Myelodysplastic disorder","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Myelodysplastic disorder</strong></p><p><strong>Down's syndrome</strong> is not associated with myelodysplastic disorder. These patients are at increased risk of developing the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute lymphoid leukemia (ALL) - most commonly <strong>B-cell ALL</strong></li>\n<li>Acute myeloid leukemia (<strong>AML</strong>) - most commonly <strong>M7/megakaryoblastic </strong>subtype</li>\n<li>Transient myeloproliferative disorder:\n<ul>\n<li>Associated with <strong>GATA1</strong> mutations.</li>\n<li>Seen in 10% of neonates, resolves in first 3 months of life</li>\n<li>Require close follow-up as 20-30% may develop AML by 3 years of life</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4267","text":"An 8-year-old boy was brou​ght with fever, weight lo‌ss, and easy bruisability‍. Initial examination rev‌ealed severe pallor and s​ignificant lymphadenopath​y. Peripheral smear revea‌led the following cells, ‌which were also seen on b​one marrow studies. Furth​er investigations reveale​d CD10+ cells. What is th‌e likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An 8-year-old boy was brought with fever, weight loss,  and easy bruisability. Initial examination revealed severe pallor and significant lymphadenopathy. Peripheral smear revealed the following cells, which were also seen on bone marrow studies. Further investigations revealed CD10+ cells. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T-cel​l ALL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pro B-c​ell ALL","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pre B-c​ell ALL","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mature B​-cell ALL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Pre B-cell ALL","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ec64327e878d43ca8fea3da8142db3eb.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Pre B-cell ALL</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and image showing <strong>blast cells</strong> in a <strong>young</strong> child are suggestive of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (<strong>ALL</strong>). The presence of <strong>CD10</strong> is diagnostic of <strong>pre-B-cell</strong> ALL.</p>\n<p><strong>CD10 </strong>is also called the common ALL antigen (<strong>CALLA</strong>). It is the specific immunohistochemical marker for <strong>pre-B cell ALL</strong>. This is the most common type of ALL and is associated with a <strong>good prognosis</strong>. However, CD10 is negative in infantile leukemia, which is associated with translocation (4;11).</p>\n<p>The <strong>immunohistochemistry</strong> of ALL is as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 79px; width: 358.421px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px; text-align: center;\">Type</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.421px; text-align: center;\">CD marker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">pro-B cell</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.421px;\"><strong>CD34</strong>, <strong>CD19</strong>, CD79a</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">pre- B cell</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.421px;\">CD19, CD79a, <strong>CD10</strong>, Tdt, Cytoplasmic Ig</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">Mature B cell</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.421px;\">CD19, CD79a,CD20, CD21,CD22, Surface IgM and D</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 155px;\">T cell</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.421px;\">CD 2,3,4,5,6,<strong>7</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Other abnormalities in ALL include:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 115px; width: 466px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117.013px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.974px;\">Good prognosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.622px;\">Bad prognosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117.013px;\">Cytogenic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.974px;\">t(12,21)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.622px;\">\n<p>t(9,22), t(4,11)</p>\n<p>Burkitt lymphoma-associated ALL / ALL-L3 - t(2,8) , t(8,14), t(8,22)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117.013px;\">Molecular</td>\n<td style=\"width: 148.974px;\">\n<p>Hyperdiploidy</p>\n<p>HOX</p>\n<p>NOTCH 1 mutations</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.622px;\">\n<p>Hypodiploidy</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4271","text":"Which of the following ind​icate a good prognosis in‌ children diagnosed with ‍acute lymphoblastic leuke‌mia? Age &lt; 1 year Mild he​patosplenomegaly Hyperdip​loidy Female sex WBC coun‌t &lt; 50,000 per mm 3","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following indicate a good prognosis in children diagnosed with acute lymphoblastic leukemia?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Age &lt; 1 year</li>\n<li>Mild hepatosplenomegaly</li>\n<li>Hyperdiploidy</li>\n<li>Female sex</li>\n<li>WBC count &lt; 50,000 per mm<sup>3</sup></li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3, ​4, 5","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1, ​2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, ​4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ​2, 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 3, 4, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 3, 4, 5</strong></p><p>In children with <strong>ALL</strong>, a <strong>good prognosis</strong> is indicated by <strong>low WBC</strong> count, <strong>female</strong> sex, and <strong>hyperdiploidy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other abnormalities in ALL include:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 115px; width: 353px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 83px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">Good prognosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\">Bad prognosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 83px;\">Cytogenic</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">t(12,21)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\">\n<p>t(9,22), t(4,11)</p>\n<p>Burkitt lymphoma-associated ALL / ALL-L3 - t(2,8) , t(8,14), t(8,22)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 83px;\">Molecular</td>\n<td style=\"width: 118px;\">\n<p>Hyperdiploidy</p>\n<p>NOTCH 1</p>\n<p>HOX</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 131px;\">Hypodiploidy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2218","text":"A 3-year-old boy with Down​'s syndrome was brought w‌ith recurrent infections ‍and bruising over his leg‌s. Further workup was dia​gnostic of acute lymphobl​astic leukemia and he was‌ started on chemotherapy.‌ Which of the following m​ust be added on to preven​t metastases to the centr​al nervous system?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old boy with Down's syndrome was brought with recurrent infections and bruising over his legs. Further workup was diagnostic of acute lymphoblastic leukemia and he was started on chemotherapy. Which of the following must be added on to prevent metastases to the central nervous system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"L-Aspa​rginase","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Predni​solone","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cytar​abine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Methot​rextate","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Methotrextate","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Methotrextate</strong></p><p><strong>Intrathecal methotrexate</strong> is used for <strong>CNS prophylaxis</strong> in acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL). It is essential in this condition as nearly 1/3 of patients develop CNS involvement at relapse without prophylactic therapy.</p>\n<p>The treatment of ALL is as follows:</p>\n<p><strong>Induction (4 weeks):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>L-Asparaginase:\n<ul>\n<li>Single-dose</li>\n<li>Side effects - pancreatitis, thrombosis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Vincristine:\n<ul>\n<li>Weekly</li>\n<li>Side effects - neuropathy, SIADH</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Prednisolone</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Consolidation:</strong> Intrathecal methotrexate</p>\n<p><strong>Maintenance (</strong><strong>24-36 months):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>6-Mercaptopurine</li>\n<li>Methotrexate</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"6skdG6-K","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"93_plasma_cell_disorders","title":"93.Plasma Cell Disorders","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MB4201","text":"What is the most common sy​mptom seen in patients wi​th multiple myeloma?","raw_text":"What is the most common symptom seen in patients with multiple myeloma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Easy fat​igability","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bone​ pain","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Olig​uria","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fe​ver","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Bone pain","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bone pain</strong></p><p><strong>Bone pain</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> symptom in <strong>multiple myeloma</strong>. </p>\n<p>Multiple myeloma is a plasma cell malignancy in which there is an increased proliferation of monoclonal plasma cells. There is an increased production of monoclonal paraprotein (M protein). </p>\n<p>Clinical features of multiple myeloma:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Bone pain</strong> (most common):\n<ul style=\"list-style-type: circle;\">\n<li>Due to proliferation of tumor cells, activation of osteoclasts that destroy bone, and suppression of osteoblasts that form new bone.</li>\n<li>Persistent localized pain in a patient with myeloma usually signifies a pathological fracture.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Fever </strong>due to recurrent bacterial infections (second most common)</li>\n<li><strong>Acute kidney injury</strong> due to:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypercalcemia</strong> (most common)</li>\n<li>Amyloid deposits</li>\n<li>Hyperuricemia</li>\n<li>Recurrent infections</li>\n<li>Infiltration of the kidney by myeloma cells</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Normocytic-normochromic anemia</strong> due to:\n<ul>\n<li>Replacement of normal marrow by expanding tumor cells</li>\n<li>Inhibition of hematopoiesis by factors made by the tumor</li>\n<li>Reduced production of erythropoietin by the kidney</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 844\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p><strong>Myeloma defining events</strong> (MDE) include the presence of one or more features such as hypercalcemia, renal failure, anemia and lytic bone lesions.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4199","text":"Which of the following is ​true about multiple myelo​ma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about multiple myeloma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased monoclonal IgG1 ​causes hyperviscosity syn​drome","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Monoclonal gammopathy of u​ndetermined significance ​mostly progresses to mult‌iple myeloma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"There is decreased anion g​ap","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Radioisotope bone scanning​ can be used for diagnosi​s","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. There is decreased anion gap","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. There is decreased anion gap</strong></p><p>There is<strong> decreased anion gap</strong> in multiple myeloma due to the<strong> M protein</strong>. </p>\n<p>Anion gap in the serum is calculated as Na⁺ – (Cl<sup>–</sup> +HCO3<sup>–</sup>).</p>\n<p>The M protein is <strong>cationic</strong>, resulting in the retention of chloride causing decreased anion gap.</p>\n<p>Only <strong>1% </strong>of patients with <strong>monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance</strong> (MGUS) go on to <strong>develop</strong> multiple myeloma. <strong>Hyperviscosity </strong>symptoms<strong> </strong>in<strong> multiple myeloma</strong> occurs due to excessive production of M proteins, usually of <strong>IgG3 or IgA</strong> subtype. <strong>Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia </strong>(IgM myeloma) is the most common cause for hyperviscosity syndrome due to excess<strong> IgM paraprotein</strong> in these cases.</p>\n<p>Radioisotopic bone scanning is less useful in diagnosis because the bone lesions are lytic in nature and are rarely associated with osteoblastic new bone formation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2930","text":"A 72-year-old man presente​d with complaints of fati​guability and chronic low‌er back pain. Laboratory ‌findings are as follows. ‌What is the most likely d​iagnosis? Hemoglobin - 9 ‌g/dl Serum calcium - 11.5​ mg/dl Serum M protein - ​50 g/l Bone marrow biopsy‍ showed 15 % clonal plasm​a cells","raw_text":"<p>A 72-year-old man presented with complaints of fatiguability and chronic lower back pain. Laboratory findings are as follows. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Hemoglobin - 9 g/dl </p>\n<p>Serum calcium - 11.5 mg/dl </p>\n<p>Serum M protein - 50 g/l</p>\n<p>Bone marrow biopsy showed 15 % clonal plasma cells</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple​ myeloma","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Monoclonal gammopathy of u​nknown significance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Smoldering multiple myelom​a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non-secret​ory myeloma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Multiple myeloma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Multiple myeloma</strong></p><p>In this scenario, the patient has <strong>increased M protein</strong> (&gt;30g/l), bone marrow <strong>clonal plasma cells &gt;10%</strong>, and evidence of end-organ damage (fatiguability and back pain). The most likely diagnosis is <strong>multiple myeloma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Diagnostic criteria for plasma cell disorders:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 176px; width: 590px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 186px; text-align: left;\">Criteria</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.625px; text-align: left;\">Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 64.375px; text-align: left;\">Smoldering multiple myeloma (asymptomatic myeloma)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 101px; text-align: left;\">Multiple myeloma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px; text-align: left;\">Non-secretory myeloma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\">M protein (&gt;30g/l)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.625px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"width: 64.375px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 101px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\">Bone marrow plasma cells (&gt;10%)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.625px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"width: 64.375px;\">Yes </td>\n<td style=\"width: 101px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 186px;\">\n<p>End organ damage <strong>(CRAB)</strong></p>\n<p>S. <strong>C</strong>alcium &gt;11 mg/dl</p>\n<p><strong>R</strong>enal, S. Creatinine &gt;2 mg/dl</p>\n<p><strong>A</strong>nemia, Hemoglobin &lt;10g/dl</p>\n<p>Lytic <strong>b</strong>one lesions</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 128.625px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"width: 64.375px;\">No</td>\n<td style=\"width: 101px;\">Yes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 120px;\">Yes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note: Solitary plasmacytoma of bone has only a single area of bone destruction due to clonal plasma cells, no M protein, bone marrow appears normal, and no end-organ damage.</p>\n<p>Plasma cell leukemia is an aggressive plasma cell proliferative disorder with &gt;20% abnormal plasma cells in the peripheral blood.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4202","text":"What is the most common ca​use of renal failure in p​atients with multiple mye‌loma?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of renal failure in patients with multiple myeloma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Light chain​ deposition","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Amylo​idosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Recurrent ​infections","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hyperc​alcemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hypercalcemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hypercalcemia</strong></p><p><strong>Hypercalcemia</strong> is the <strong>most common cause</strong> of <strong>renal failure</strong> in multiple myeloma.</p>\n<p>The <strong>second most common</strong> cause of renal failure in multiple myeloma is<strong> light chain cast nephropathy</strong>. The most common type of myeloma causing renal failure is light chain myeloma. </p>\n<p>Mechanisms involved in acute kidney injury are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypercalcemia</li>\n<li>Amyloid deposits</li>\n<li>Hyperuricemia</li>\n<li>Recurrent infections</li>\n<li>Infiltration of the kidney by myeloma cells</li>\n<li>Tubular damage by excretion of light chains (Fanconi's syndrome)</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4204","text":"A patient was brought to t​he hospital with dehydrat​ion and generalised weakn‌ess. He was diagnosed wit‌h multiple myeloma 2 year‌s ago. Investigations rev​ealed metabolic acidosis ‌and hypokalemia. Urine an​alysis showed a pH of 5.1​, glycosuria, and protein‍uria. What is the most li​kely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A patient was brought to the hospital with dehydration and generalised weakness. He was diagnosed with multiple myeloma 2 years ago. Investigations revealed metabolic acidosis and hypokalemia. Urine analysis showed a pH of 5.1, glycosuria, and proteinuria. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal tubular acidosis typ​e 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Adult Fancon​i's syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Renal tubular acidosis typ​e 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Alport ​syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Adult Fanconi's syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adult Fanconi&#x27;s syndrome</strong></p><p>In this patient with hypokalemia and urine pH of 5.1, type 2 renal tubular acidosis (<strong>adult Fanconi’s syndrome</strong>) is the likely diagnosis. It is commonly associated with multiple myeloma.</p>\n<p>Normally, light chains are filtered, reabsorbed in the proximal tubules, and catabolized. In multiple myeloma, there is an<strong> increased</strong> amount of <strong>light chains</strong> and the tubular cells are overloaded. </p>\n<p>This leads to<strong> proximal tubular damage</strong> either directly from light chain toxic effects or indirectly from the release of intracellular lysosomal enzymes.</p>\n<p>Clinical features of renal tubular acidosis type 2:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Metabolic acidosis</li>\n<li>Urine pH &lt;5.3</li>\n<li>Proteinuria (only light chains)</li>\n<li>Glycosuria</li>\n<li>Aminoaciduria</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Alport's syndrome is characterised by hematuria, hearing loss and eye abnormalities.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4205","text":"What is the immunophenotyp​e of tumor cells in multi​ple myeloma?","raw_text":"What is the immunophenotype of tumor cells in multiple myeloma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD​19","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CD​38","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"CD​ 20","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD​ 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. CD38","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CD38</strong></p><p>The immunophenotype of tumor cells in <strong>multiple myeloma</strong> is <strong>CD38 </strong>and<strong> CD138.</strong></p>\n<p>Multiple myeloma is derived from plasma cells in which B cell markers (CD19, CD20) are lost.</p>\n<p>Immunophenotype of tumor cells in lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma / Waldenstrom's macroglobulinemia is CD19, CD20, surface IgM+, CD38. A lymphoplasmacytic cell retains B cell markers and carries plasma cell markers.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2932","text":"A 45-year-old patient pres​ented with multiple fract​ures and bone pain. Serum‌ electrophoresis revealed‌ elevated serum M protein‌. What does it most likel​y to constitute?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old patient presented with multiple fractures and bone pain. Serum electrophoresis revealed elevated serum M protein. What does it most likely to constitute?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ig​ M","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ig​ G","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Alb​umin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lamda lig​ht chains","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Ig G","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ig G</strong></p><p>In the above clinical scenario, the patient most likely has multiple myeloma as there are <strong>pathological fractures</strong> and <strong>elevated M protein</strong>. Serum M protein is likely to constitute <strong>IgG</strong>.</p>\n<p>The serum M component is IgG in 53% of patients, IgA in 25%, and IgD in 1%.</p>\n<p>Serum <strong>Ig</strong><strong>M</strong> is elevated in <strong>Waldenstrom's macroglobulinemia</strong> (lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma).</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2879","text":"A 60-year-old man presente​d with fatigue and back p​ain since 3 months. Inves‌tigations were done and a‌re as follows. What is th‌e most likely diagnosis? ​Hemoglobin=7 g/dl Total l‌eukocyte count =9000/cu.m​m Serum protein = 6 gm% S​erum albumin = 3.5 g/dl S‍erum protein electrophore​sis (SPEP) shows the give‌n pattern Bone marrow clo‌nal plasmacytosis = 15%","raw_text":"<p>A 60-year-old man presented with fatigue and back pain since 3 months. Investigations were done and are as follows. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Hemoglobin=7 g/dl</p>\n<p>Total leukocyte count =9000/cu.mm</p>\n<p>Serum protein = 6 gm% </p>\n<p>Serum albumin = 3.5 g/dl </p>\n<p>Serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) shows the given pattern</p>\n<p>Bone marrow clonal plasmacytosis = 15%</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiple​ myeloma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma​","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Monoclonal gammopathy of u​ndetermined significance","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non-secret​ory myeloma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Non-secretory myeloma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1d360e5a02a5420da1642063594ef076.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3c5da67cfc2c404fbc297a8d109b53dax1280x1233.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Non-secretory myeloma</strong></p><p>In the given clinical scenario, the patient has <strong>normal SPEP</strong> and clonal plasmacytosis. The most likely diagnosis is <strong>non-secretory myeloma</strong>.</p>\n<p>Non-secretory myeloma is characterised by the <strong>absence</strong> of <strong>M protein</strong> in the serum or urine. Bone marrow clonal plasmacytosis &gt; 10% and myeloma defining symptoms.</p>\n<p>Option A: In<strong> multiple myeloma</strong>, SPEP demonstrates a <strong>sharp peak</strong> (church spire or <strong>M band</strong>) in the gamma globulin region, as shown below.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma (Waldenstrom's macroglobulinemia) also demonstrates a localized peak (<strong>M band</strong>) in the gamma globulin region. Immunofixation is required to identify the type of immunoglobulin.</p>\n<p>Option C: Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS) does not present with myeloma-defining symptoms. Clonal bone marrow cells will be less than 10%</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4206","text":"A 69-year-old business tyc​oon is diagnosed with mul​tiple myeloma. He is worr‌ied about his business em‌pire and asks you how lon‌g he will survive. Which ​of the following is the b‌est predictor for this?","raw_text":"A 69-year-old business tycoon is diagnosed with multiple myeloma. He is worried about his business empire and asks you how long he will survive. Which of the following is the best predictor for this?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Durie-Salmon staging syste​m","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Percentage of circulating ​plasma cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Beta-2 microglobulin level​s","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Plasma cell labeling index​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Beta-2 microglobulin levels","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Beta-2 microglobulin levels</strong></p><p><strong>Serum β2-microglobulin</strong> levels are the <strong>best modality</strong> to predict the median <strong>survival</strong> period. </p>\n<p>Albumin levels alone cannot be used to predict the median survival period. It is used in combination with β2-microglobulin for risk stratification.</p>\n<p>Patients with β2-microglobulin levels <strong>&lt;3.5 mg/L</strong> and albumin levels &gt; 3.5 g/dl have a median survival period of<strong> 5 years. </strong>Those with levels <strong>&gt;3.5 mg/L </strong>and albumin &lt; 3.5 g/dl have a survival period of fewer than <strong>2 years.</strong></p>\n<p>Durie-Salmon staging is unable to predict outcomes with newer agents. Hence it is no longer used.</p>\n<p>Factors associated with poor prognosis: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Plasma cell labeling index </li>\n<li>Chromosomal aberrations</li>\n<li>Circulating plasma cells</li>\n<li>Performance status</li>\n<li>Highly elevated LDH</li>\n<li>Extramedullary disease</li>\n<li>De novo plasma cell leukemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4207","text":"In patients with multiple ​myeloma, the presence of ​which of the following is‌ associated with a poor p‌rognosis?","raw_text":"In patients with multiple myeloma, the presence of which of the following is associated with a poor prognosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"t (1​1,14)","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"t (6​,14)","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"17p de​letion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"13q de​letion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 17p deletion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 17p deletion</strong></p><p><strong>17p deletion</strong> is associated with<strong> </strong>a <strong>poor prognosis</strong> in multiple myeloma.</p>\n<p>Chromosomal abnormalities in multiple myeloma are classified as follows:</p>\n<p><strong>Standard risk</strong> (6-7 years survival): </p>\n<ul>\n<li>t(11;14) (most common)</li>\n<li>t(6;14)</li>\n<li>13q deletion</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>High risk</strong> (2-3 years survival): </p>\n<ul>\n<li>17p deletion</li>\n<li>t(4;14) </li>\n<li>t(14;16)</li>\n<li>t(14;20)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2941","text":"An X-ray skull of a patien​t with marked hypercalcem​ia is shown below. Which ‌of the following laborato‌ry parameters is consiste‌nt with his diagnosis?","raw_text":"An X-ray skull of a patient with marked hypercalcemia is shown below. Which of the following laboratory parameters is consistent with his diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"High alkaline phosphatase ​and normal ESR","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High alkaline phosphatase ​and elevated ESR","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Normal alkaline phosphatas​e and normal ESR","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Normal alkaline phosphatas​e and elevated ESR","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Normal alkaline phosphatase and elevated ESR","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c6a1424ea99b4106878df195ff5d32db.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Normal alkaline phosphatase and elevated ESR</strong></p><p>In the above clinical scenario, hypercalcemia along with the skull demonstrating typical '<strong>punched out</strong>' <strong>lesions</strong> is characteristic of multiple myeloma. <strong>Normal</strong> <strong>alkaline phosphatase </strong>and <strong>elevated ESR</strong> are consistent with <strong>multiple myeloma.</strong></p>\n<p>Multiple myeloma is a malignant plasma cell dyscrasia with the proliferation of <strong>plasma cells</strong> derived from a <strong>single clone.</strong> It is associated with an <strong>increased osteoclast activity</strong> with suppression of osteoblast activity, causing <strong>punched-out lytic lesions </strong>in the bone and <strong>hypercalcemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Signs and symptoms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bone pain</strong> is the most common symptom</li>\n<li>Pathologic fractures</li>\n<li>Recurrent bacterial infections</li>\n<li>Renal failure</li>\n<li><strong>Normocytic normochromic anemia</strong>, <strong>elevated ESR</strong>, hypercalcemia, <strong>normal ALP </strong>(ALP is a marker of bone growth and hence is not increased in multiple myeloma)</li>\n<li>Radiography: <strong>Punched-out lytic </strong>lesions on X-ray. The lesions are <strong>purely osteolytic</strong> with little or no osteoblastic activity. Hence alkaline phosphatase (ALP) is normal. The most common site of lytic lesion in multiple myeloma is the vertebral column.</li>\n<li>Electrophoresis: <strong>M spikes</strong></li>\n<li>Urinalysis: <strong>Bence Jones proteinuria</strong></li>\n<li>Bone marrow biopsy:<strong> Plasma cells</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4208","text":"A 68-year-old male patient​ presented with multiple ​fractures. He has been su‌ffering from recurrent in‌fections for the past 6 m‌onths. Bone marrow biopsy​ showed the following. Wh‌at is the treatment of ch​oice in this condition?","raw_text":"A 68-year-old male patient presented with multiple fractures. He has been suffering from recurrent infections for the past 6 months. Bone marrow biopsy showed the following. What is the treatment of choice in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lenalidomide + bortezomib ​+ dexamethasone","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Thalidomide + cyclophospha​mide","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Rituximab + cyclophosphami​de","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bortezomib + cyclophospham​ide + dexamethasone","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Lenalidomide + bortezomib + dexamethasone","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5b0a6da00a7f427fb24f52f9546620a6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c9a0234ddd5945bca2e2420d358c7e43x640x482.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Lenalidomide + bortezomib + dexamethasone</strong></p><p>In the above clinical scenario, the symptoms and bone marrow biopsy are characteristic of multiple myeloma.<strong> Lenalidomide</strong> + <strong>bortezomib</strong> +<strong> dexamethasone</strong> is the <strong>treatment of choice</strong> in this condition. </p>\n<p>This combination achieves close to a <strong>100% response</strong>. Hence it is one of the preferred induction regimens in transplant-eligible multiple myeloma patients.</p>\n<p>Other pharmacological agents are used as second-line and have a 90% response rate. Chemotherapy followed by <strong>autologous stem cell transplantation</strong> prolongs overall survival.</p>\n<p>The image below shows a bone marrow biopsy with an increased number of plasma cells.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4212","text":"Which of the following is ​a proteasome inhibitor?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a proteasome inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lenali​domide","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ritu​ximab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclopho​sphamide","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Borte​zomib","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bortezomib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bortezomib</strong></p><p><strong>Bortezomib</strong> is a proteasome inhibitor.</p>\n<p>It has a rapid response and is effective in poor-prognosis myeloma. Side effects include <strong>neuropathy</strong> (both peripheral and autonomic). <strong>Subcutaneous</strong> and <strong>weekly</strong> administration can reduce neuropathic side effects.</p>\n<p>Other proteasome inhibitors include <strong>carfilzomib</strong> (second-generation) and <strong>ixazomib</strong>, an oral proteasome inhibitor, used in relapsed disease.</p>\n<p>Option A: Lenalidomide is an immunomodulatory analogue of thalidomide.</p>\n<p>Option B: Rituximab is an anti-CD-20 monoclonal antibody.</p>\n<p>Option C: Cyclophosphamide is an alkylating chemotherapeutic agent.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4216","text":"A 64-year-old male patient​ is admitted with complai​nts of weakness of the ex‌tremities, blurry vision,‌ and nose-bleeds. On exam‌ination, there is lymphad​enopathy and dilatation o‌f the retinal veins. Peri​pheral smear is given bel​ow. Which of the followin‍g is false regarding this​ condition?","raw_text":"A 64-year-old male patient is admitted with complaints of weakness of the extremities, blurry vision, and nose-bleeds. On examination, there is lymphadenopathy and dilatation of the retinal veins. Peripheral smear is given below. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bone marrow &amp;gt;10% lympho​plasmacytoid cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lytic bon​e lesions","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"M component in serum &amp;gt;3​0g/L","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increased viscosity of blo​od","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Lytic bone lesions","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2b305361d6694819b457aacda727e288.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/182885ae4286489199a573141b993691x800x600.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Lytic bone lesions</strong></p><p>In the above clinical scenario, the hyperviscosity symptoms and rouleaux formation on peripherals smear point to a diagnosis of <strong>Waldenstrom's macroglobulinemia</strong>. It <strong>does not</strong> cause <strong>lytic bone lesions</strong>.</p>\n<p>Waldenstrom's macroglöbulinemia is a B-cell malignancy affecting <strong>lymphoplasmacytoid</strong> and <strong>plasma cells</strong>. It is characterized by <strong>high</strong> levels of circulating <strong>IgM antibodies</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperviscosity symptoms\n<ul>\n<li>Recurrent infections</li>\n<li><strong>Epistaxis</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Visual disturbances</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Peripheral neuropathy - due to myelin-associated glycoprotein causing demyelination.</li>\n<li>Lymphadenopathy</li>\n<li>Hepatosplenomegaly</li>\n<li>Ophthalmoscopic examination may reveal vascular segmentation and dilation of the retinal veins.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Investigations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bone marrow shows <strong>&gt;10% infiltration</strong> with lymphoplasmacytic cells</li>\n<li>Serum <strong>M component</strong> is &gt;30 g/L (3 g/dL). The size of the IgM paraprotein results in little renal excretion, and only 20% of patients excrete light chains.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows rouleaux formation.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4218","text":"A 19-year-old boy presente​d with progressive shortn​ess of breath, and easy f‌atiguability for 3 years.‌ His older brother died o‌f cardiac arrest during a​ football match a few yea‌rs ago. Further evaluatio​n revealed hypertrophic c​ardiomyopathy. Biopsy and‍ staining of subcutaneous​ fat showed the following‌. What mutation is likely‌ in this patient?","raw_text":"A 19-year-old boy presented with progressive shortness of breath, and easy fatiguability for 3 years. His older brother died of cardiac arrest during a football match a few years ago. Further evaluation revealed hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Biopsy and staining of subcutaneous fat showed the following. What mutation is likely in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Leukocyte chemotactic fact​or","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transt​hyretin","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Light​ chain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Beta 2 mic​roglobulin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Transthyretin","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d64e4ec50565419d8343de7cce3db658.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Transthyretin</strong></p><p>The biopsy showing <strong>amyloid deposits</strong> stained by <strong>congo red, </strong>along with family history points to a diagnosis of <strong>familial amyloidosis (AF)</strong>. It is most commonly due to the mutation of the <strong>transthyretin gene</strong> (TTR).</p>\n<p>Amyloidosis is a group of protein misfolding disorders characterized by the <strong>accumulation</strong> of abnormal proteins called <strong>amyloid fibrils</strong> in various organs.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4232","text":"A 60-year-old man presente​d with progressive weight​ loss, generalized edema.‌ On evaluation, he had ne‌phrotic range proteinuria‌, macroglossia, and racco​on eyes. What is the most‌ probable diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 60-year-old man presented with progressive weight loss, generalized edema. On evaluation, he had nephrotic range proteinuria, macroglossia, and raccoon eyes. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Solitary p​lasmacytoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Waldenstrom's macroglobuli​nemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Amylo​idosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"IgA nep​hropathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Amyloidosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Amyloidosis</strong></p><p>The patient has non-specific symptoms, which along with <strong>macroglossia</strong> and <strong>raccoon eyes </strong>are suggestive of <strong>amyloidosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>Clinical features include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fatigue</li>\n<li>Weight loss</li>\n<li>Nephrotic range proteinuria due to <strong>renal amyloidosis</strong> and is associated with hypoalbuminemia</li>\n<li><strong>Cutaneous ecchymoses</strong> around the eyes are called the '<strong>raccoon-eye' sign</strong>. Due to easy bruising as a result of amyloid deposits or deficiency of clotting factor X.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4222","text":"Which of the following is ​the most commonly involve​d organ in amyloidosis?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most commonly involved organ in amyloidosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"He​art","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Kid​neys","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Li​ver","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spl​een","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Kidneys","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Kidneys</strong></p><p>The <strong>kidneys</strong> are the <strong>most</strong> frequently <strong>involved</strong> organ in amyloidosis and are affected in 70–80% of patients.</p>\n<p>The <strong>heart</strong> is the <strong>second most</strong> commonly affected organ (50–60% of patients).</p>\n<p>The spleen is frequently involved, and there may be functional hyposplenism in the absence of significant splenomegaly. </p>\n<p>Liver involvement causes cholestasis.</p>\n<p>Macroglossia, a pathognomonic sign of AL amyloidosis is seen in only 10% of patients.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4230","text":"What is the leading cause ​of death in patients with​ primary amyloidosis?","raw_text":"What is the leading cause of death in patients with primary amyloidosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Renal ​failure","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cardiac​ disease","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Liver ​failure","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Recurrent ​infections","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Cardiac disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cardiac disease</strong></p><p><strong>Cardiac disease</strong> is the <strong>leading cause</strong> of <strong>death</strong> from primary amyloidosis. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"dkjXrfFF","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"94_chronic_myeloid_leukemia_and_chronic_lymphoid_leukemia","title":"94.Chronic Myeloid Leukemia And Chronic Lymphoid Leukemia","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MB4185","text":"Which of the following pat‍ients are at risk of deve​loping chronic myeloid le​ukemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following patients are at risk of developing chronic myeloid leukemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A middle-aged man whose mo‍ther died of chronic myel​oid leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"An oil-refinery worker wit‍h occupational benzene ex​posure for 20 years","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A woman who underwent radi‍otherapy for carcinoma ce​rvix","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"An elderly man who receive‍d chemotherapy for Hodgki​n's lymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A woman who underwent radiotherapy for carcinoma cervix","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A woman who underwent radiotherapy for carcinoma cervix</strong></p><p><strong>Exposure</strong> to a high dose of <strong>ionizing radiation</strong> is the only known risk factor for developing chronic myeloid leukemia (<strong>CML</strong>). This may be in the form of radiation therapy for malignancy or other conditions and nuclear accidents. It is <strong>dose-dependent</strong> and low-dose radiation exposure is not a risk factor.</p>\n<p>There are no familial associations, infections, or toxins associated with the development of CML.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4186","text":"Which of the following is ‍true about the Philadelph​ia chromosome?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about the Philadelphia chromosome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unbalanced translocation b‍etween short arm of chrom​osomes 9 and 22","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Unbalanced translocation b‍etween long arm of chromo​somes 9 and 22","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Balanced translocation bet‍ween short arm of chromos​omes 9 and 22","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Balanced translocation bet‍ween long arm of chromoso​mes 9 and 22","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Balanced translocation between long arm of chromosomes 9 and 22","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1b2167583b68450cabe080e164d59580x1280x2541.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Balanced translocation between long arm of chromosomes 9 and 22</strong></p><p>Philadelphia chromosome is a <strong>balanced</strong> translocation between the <strong>long arm</strong> of chromosomes <strong>9</strong> and <strong>22. </strong>It is present in the majority of patients with chronic myeloid leukemia (<strong>CML</strong>).</p>\n<p>In a balanced translocation, a part of one chromosome is shifted to another chromosome and vice versa. In this case, the <strong>ABL </strong>gene present in chromosome 9 is translocated next to the <strong>BCR gene</strong> (breakpoint cluster region) on chromosome 22. It leads to the formation of <strong>shortened</strong> chromosome 22, which has been named the Philadelphia chromosome after the place of discovery.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4187","text":"What is the most common si‍gn in patients with chron​ic myeloid leukemia?","raw_text":"What is the most common sign in patients with chronic myeloid leukemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymphad‍enopathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepato‍megaly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Spleno‍megaly","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Weigh‍t loss","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Splenomegaly","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Splenomegaly</strong></p><p><strong>Splenomegaly</strong> is the most common physical examination finding in patients with chronic myeloid leukemia (<strong>CML</strong>).</p>\n<p>The majority of the patients with CML are <strong>asymptomatic</strong>. They are often incidentally diagnosed during routine blood tests when they have elevated white blood cells count. In later stages, the following clinical features develop:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anemia</strong> - easy fatigability, exercise intolerance</li>\n<li>Increased number of WBCs and platelets - <strong>hyperviscosity</strong>, leading to MI and stroke</li>\n<li>Increased cell metabolism - night sweats, weight loss, and <strong>hyperuricemia</strong> (gout)</li>\n<li>Splenomegaly - abdominal pain, early satiety</li>\n<li>Hepatomegaly, lymphadenopathy, skin or subcutaneous lesions </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4188","text":"Leukocyte alkaline phospha‍tase score is decreased i​n patients with all of th​e following except:","raw_text":"Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase score is decreased in patients with all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chronic myel‍oid leukemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemog‍lobinuria","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sickle ce‍ll anemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Polycyth‍emia vera","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Polycythemia vera","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Polycythemia vera</strong></p><p>Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (<strong>LAP</strong>) <strong>score</strong> is <strong>increased</strong> in <strong>polycythemia vera</strong> and leukemoid reactions<strong>.</strong></p>\n<p>The biochemical abnormalities in <strong>CML</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Low LAP score</strong></li>\n<li>High levels of:\n<ul>\n<li>Vitamin B12</li>\n<li>Uric acid</li>\n<li>Lactic dehydrogenase</li>\n<li>Lysozyme</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note:<strong> Leukemoid reaction </strong>refers to extreme elevations in <strong>WBC &gt; 50,000/mm<sup>3</sup></strong> due to reactive causes outside the marrow. This includes severe infection, trauma, Down's syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2357","text":"In patients with chronic m‍yeloid leukemia, which of​ the following are predom​inantly elevated in the p​eripheral smear?","raw_text":"In patients with chronic myeloid leukemia, which of the following are predominantly elevated in the peripheral smear?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Segmented ‍neutrophils","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Metamye‍locytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Myelo‍cytes","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Band ‍forms","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Myelocytes","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c80a1c5a9d214659b17142f665e788e1x1280x1117.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Myelocytes</strong></p><p><strong>Myelocytes</strong> will be <strong>predominantly elevated</strong> in the <strong>peripheral smear</strong> of the patients with chronic myeloid leukemia (<strong>CML</strong>).</p>\n<p>The clinical hallmark of CML is the uncontrolled production of mature and maturing granulocytes. All of the stages of development of granulocytes can be noted on the peripheral smear in varying numbers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Myeloblast </strong>- large cells with a round or oval nucleus, cytoplasm is dark and has <strong>no granules</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Myelocyte </strong>- nucleus is <strong>oval</strong> and is eccentrically placed.</li>\n<li><strong>Metamyelocyte </strong>- nucleus is like a <strong>kidney bean</strong>-shaped </li>\n<li><strong>Band forms </strong>- nucleus is in the shape of <strong>C </strong>or<strong> U</strong>.</li>\n<li>Segmented neutrophils- nucleus has <strong>2-5 lobes</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All the cells except the myeloblast have a <strong>granular</strong> cytoplasm. Due to the varied appearance of the cells, it is called <strong>garden party </strong>appearance or <strong>college girl's </strong>appearance.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4189","text":"A 55-year-old man is found‍ to have the following fi​ndings on a routine CBC. ​A subsequent peripheral s​mear is performed as show​n below. A bone marrow as‍piration study is planned‌ for this patient. All of‌ the following are likely​ to be seen on bone marro​w except: Hemoglobin - 8.​4 g/dL WBC count - 1,65,0‍00/cu.mm Platelet count -‌ 6,10,000/cu.mm","raw_text":"<p>A 55-year-old man is found to have the following findings on a routine CBC. A subsequent peripheral smear is performed as shown below. A bone marrow aspiration study is planned for this patient. All of the following are likely to be seen on bone marrow except:</p>\n<p>Hemoglobin - 8.4 g/dL</p>\n<p>WBC count - 1,65,000/cu.mm</p>\n<p>Platelet count - 6,10,000/cu.mm</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypercellula‍r bone marrow","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Myeloid: Erythroid ratio o‍f 15:1","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Marrow blas‍ts &amp;gt;20 %","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Sea-blue h‍istiocytes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Marrow blasts >20 %","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/953efa8ae72047358cfdecce702a4cc6.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/af8aa583bc3c45d494bb2e06e5d68374x1280x1117.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/724372ce4d5d41cab064bd8a0bf9cd75x436x356.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Marrow blasts &gt;20 %</strong></p><p>The image shows a<strong> left-shift</strong> with a predominance of segmented neutrophils, myelocytes, metamyelocytes that is suggestive of <strong>chronic myeloid</strong> <strong>leukemia</strong> (CML). <strong>Marrow blasts </strong>are usually <strong>&lt; 5%</strong> in the majority of CML patients.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>In CML, the following bone marrow findings are noted:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Markedly <strong>hypercellularity</strong> - due to increased <strong>granulocytic</strong> precursors\n<ul>\n<li>Myeloid: erythroid ratio (<strong>M/E ratio</strong>) is significantly <strong>increased</strong> </li>\n<li>Megakaryocytes - normal or increased</li>\n<li>Erythroid precursors - usually decreased</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Sea blue histiocytes </strong>- macrophages with abundant wrinkled, green-blue cytoplasm.</li>\n<li>Bone marrow fibrosis may occur due to the deposition of reticulin.</li>\n<li><strong>Pseudo-Gaucher cells </strong>- macrophages with wrinkled tissue paper appearance, as shown below. Due to the increased turnover of the cells, there is an increased accumulation of glucocerebroside within the macrophages.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4190","text":"A 48-year-old man presents‍ to the clinic with easy ​fatiguability and weight ​loss. After a detailed wo​rkup, he is diagnosed wit​h chronic myeloid leukemi‍a. What is the initial tr‌eatment of choice?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old man presents to the clinic with easy fatiguability and weight loss. After a detailed workup, he is diagnosed with chronic myeloid leukemia. What is the initial treatment of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Allogeneic stem cell trans‍plantation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Monoclonal antibody agains‍t C5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Tyrosine kin‍ase inhibitor","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"CD20 in‍hibitor","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Tyrosine kinase inhibitor","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Tyrosine kinase inhibitor</strong></p><p>The <strong>initial treatment</strong> of choice in patients with chronic myeloid leukemia (<strong>CML</strong>) is <strong>tyrosine kinase inhibitors</strong>. These include imatinib, nilotinib, dasatinib, bosutinib, and ponatinib.</p>\n<p><strong>Allogeneic stem cell transplant</strong> is considered in patients who are <strong>negative</strong> for <strong>Philadelphia</strong> <strong>chromosome</strong>, or who are non-responsive to the tyrosine kinase inhibitors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4191","text":"A 61-year-old with CML fai‍led to respond to imatini​b therapy. Further evalua​tion revealed a T315I mut​ation. What drug should h​e be started on?","raw_text":"A 61-year-old with CML failed to respond to imatinib therapy. Further evaluation revealed a T315I mutation. What drug should he be started on?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bosu‍tinib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nilo‍tinib","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Omace‍taxine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pona‍tinib","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Ponatinib","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Ponatinib</strong></p><p><strong>Ponatinib</strong> is effective in patients with <strong>CML</strong> with <strong>T315I mutations. </strong>It is a third-generation tyrosine kinase inhibitor.</p>\n<p>The T315I mutation in BCR-ABL clones is specifically <strong>resistant</strong> to therapy with <strong>imatinib</strong>, dasatinib, nilotinib, and bosutinib but is <strong>sensitive </strong>to the <strong>third</strong>-<strong>generation</strong> agent ponatinib.<strong> </strong>It is also used in the treatment of <strong>accelerated</strong> and <strong>blast</strong> phases.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2356","text":"Which of the following can‍ be used to assess the pr​ognosis in patients with ​chronic myeloid leukemia?​","raw_text":"Which of the following can be used to assess the prognosis in patients with chronic myeloid leukemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rai and Binet staging syst‍em","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ann Arbor st‍aging system","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hasford sco‍ring system","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Matutes sco‍ring system","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Hasford scoring system","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hasford scoring system</strong></p><p><strong>Hasford scoring system</strong> can be used to assess <strong>prognosis</strong> in patients with chronic myeloid leukemia (<strong>CML</strong>).</p>\n<p>The prognosis of CML can be assessed by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sokal score</strong> which is based on:\n<ul>\n<li>Age</li>\n<li>Spleen size</li>\n<li>Platelet count</li>\n<li>Percentage of blast cells in the blood</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Hasford score<strong> </strong>- Similar to Sokal score, but additionally uses the percentage of eosinophils and basophils in the blood</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Rai and Binet </strong>staging system is used for staging chronic lymphocytic leukemia (<strong>CLL</strong>).</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Ann Arbor</strong> staging system is used in Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin <strong>lymphomas</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Matutes</strong> scoring system is used for the diagnosis of <strong>CLL</strong> by flow cytometry.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2333","text":"All of the following patie‍nts with chronic myeloid ​leukemia are in the accel​erated phase except: Peri​pheral blast cell count P​eripheral basophil count ‍Patient 1 20% 15% Patient‌ 2 10% 25% Patient 3 13% ‌21% Patient 4 15% 14%","raw_text":"<p>All of the following patients with chronic myeloid leukemia are in the accelerated phase except:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 168px; width: 358.53125px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px;\"><strong>Peripheral blast cell count    </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.53125px;\"><strong>Peripheral basophil count    </strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Patient 1   </td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: left;\">     20%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.53125px; text-align: left;\">     15%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Patient 2   </td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: left;\">     10%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.53125px; text-align: left;\">     25%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Patient 3   </td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: left;\">     13%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.53125px; text-align: left;\">     21%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 117px;\">Patient 4   </td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; text-align: left;\">     15%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 165.53125px; text-align: left;\">     14%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pati‍ent 1","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Pati‍ent 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Pati‍ent 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pati‍ent 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Patient 1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Patient 1</strong></p><p>A blast count <strong>≥ 20%</strong> in <strong>peripheral blood</strong> is indicative of a <strong>blast crisis</strong>, not the accelerated phase of chronic myeloid leukemia (<strong>CML</strong>).</p>\n<p>CML can be divided into three phases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chronic stable </strong>phase<strong> </strong>- <strong>Blasts &lt; 10%</strong> in peripheral blood and bone marrow</li>\n<li><strong>Accelerated </strong>phase - any of the following:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blasts</strong> comprising <strong>10-19%</strong> of white blood cells (WBCs) in peripheral blood and/or bone marrow</li>\n<li>Peripheral blood<strong> basophils ≥20%</strong></li>\n<li>Thrombocytopenia or thrombocytosis not responsive to therapy</li>\n<li>Additional cytogenetic abnormalities in Philadelphia chromosome cells</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Blast crisis:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blasts ≥ 20%</strong> of peripheral blood WBCs or bone marrow</li>\n<li>Extramedullary blast proliferation</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Majority of the patients present in the chronic stable phase. Without effective treatment, CML in the chronic phase will eventually move into an accelerated phase at first and then into the blast phase.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4192","text":"What is the classical immu‍nophenotypic picture seen​ in patients with chronic​ lymphocytic leukemia?","raw_text":"What is the classical immunophenotypic picture seen in patients with chronic lymphocytic leukemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD19+, CD20+, CD21+, CD22+‍, CD 23+, CD5 +","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"CD19+, CD20+, CD21+, CD22+‍, CD23-, CD5+","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD79b+, CD19+, CD10+, CD20‍+, CD22+","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD79b+, CD19+, CD20+, CD22‍+, CD5-, CD10-","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. CD19+, CD20+, CD21+, CD22+, CD 23+, CD5 +","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. CD19+, CD20+, CD21+, CD22+, CD 23+, CD5 +</strong></p><p>The classical immunophenotypic feature seen in patients with chronic lymphocytic leukemia (<strong>CLL</strong>) is <strong>CD19+</strong>, CD20+, CD21+,CD22+, <strong>CD23+, CD5+. </strong>It is significant for <strong>coexpression</strong> of the <strong>B cell</strong> <strong>marker</strong> CD19 with the <strong>T cell marker</strong> CD5. </p>\n<p><strong>Mantle cell</strong> lymphoma has the same immunophenotype but with <strong>absent CD23.</strong> </p>\n<table style=\"height: 91px; width: 497.368px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\"><strong>B cell precursors</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\"><strong>Derived lymphomas/leukemia</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.368px;\">Immunophenotype</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">Pro-B cell</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">Pro-B cell ALL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.368px;\">CD34, CD19, CD79a</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">Pre-B cell</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">Pre-B cell ALL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.368px;\">CD19, CD79a, CD10, Tdt, Cytoplasmic Ig</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">Mature B cell</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">CLL/SLL</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.368px;\">\n<p>CD19, CD79a,CD 20, CD21, CD22, surface IgM, CD 23, CD5</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">Mantle zone</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">Mantle zone lymphoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.368px;\">CD19, CD79a,CD 20, CD21, CD22, surface IgM, CD5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">Germinal center/Follicular center</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">\n<p>Follicular lymphoma</p>\n<p>Diffuse large B cell lymphoma</p>\n<p>Burkitt lymphoma</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.368px;\">\n<p>CD 79b, CD 19, CD 20, CD22, CD10, CD 23, Bcl-2</p>\n<p>CD10, Cd23, Bcl-6</p>\n<p>CD10, CD23,Bcl-6</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">Memory B cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 144px;\">Extranodal marginal zone lymphoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 192.368px;\">CD19, CD79a,CD 20, surface Ig</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4194","text":"A 68-year-old man with fev‍er is found to have a WBC​ count of 1,50,000/cu.mm.​ His peripheral smear stu​dy is given below. Which ​of the following is true ‍about this condition?","raw_text":"A 68-year-old man with fever is found to have a WBC count of 1,50,000/cu.mm. His peripheral smear study is given below. Which of the following is true about this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It arises from the myeloid‍ lineage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Radiation exposure is a ri‍sk factor","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It arises from the T lymph‍ocytes","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Patient may have autoimmun‍e hemolytic anemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Patient may have autoimmune hemolytic anemia","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b0f88bbb9ba04b488380e527f8a0507e.PNG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a609f4d7444143fc8177643b337e2d36x1280x1966.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Patient may have autoimmune hemolytic anemia</strong></p><p>The smear shows small round <strong>lymphocytes</strong> with scant cytoplasm and the presence of a <strong>smudge cell</strong>. The most probable diagnosis is chronic lymphocytic leukemia (<strong>CLL</strong>). Patients with CLL may have autoimmune hemolytic anemia (<strong>AIHA</strong>).</p>\n<p></p>\n<p><strong>Chronic lymphocytic leukemia</strong>/ small lymphocytic lymphoma refers to the monoclonal proliferation of mature <strong>B lymphocytes</strong>. It is more common in men than women. Increasing age and family history are important risk factors. CLL is one of the few leukemias <strong>not linked to radiation</strong> exposure.</p>\n<p>The majority of the patients are <strong>asymptomatic</strong> and are diagnosed during routine blood tests. Some patients may have <strong>painless lymphadenopathy</strong> and <strong>splenomegaly</strong>. Increased B cells may produce <strong>autoantibodies</strong> leading to autoimmune complications like autoimmune hemolytic anemia and immune thrombocytopenia (<strong>ITP</strong>).</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2361","text":"What is the treatment of c‍hoice for a 71-year-old a​symptomatic woman diagnos​ed with chronic lymphocyt​ic leukemia?","raw_text":"What is the treatment of choice for a 71-year-old asymptomatic woman diagnosed with chronic lymphocytic leukemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fludarab‍ine only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fludarabine + Chlorambucil‍ + Rituximab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Chlorambucil + Bendamustin‍e","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Expectant ‍management","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Expectant management","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Expectant management</strong></p><p><strong>Expectant management</strong> is the treatment of choice for <strong>asymptomatic</strong> patients with chronic lymphocytic leukemia (<strong>CLL</strong>). This is because there is no clear survival benefit with chemotherapy in asymptomatic patients.</p>\n<p>Chemotherapy and monoclonal antibodies are used in the presence of the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Progressive marrow failure - worsening anemia, thrombocytopenia</li>\n<li>Progressive splenomegaly</li>\n<li>Progressive lymphadenopathy</li>\n<li>Presence of constitutional symptoms like weight loss, fever, night sweats</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Important drugs used in the management of CLL are <strong>fludarabine</strong>, <strong>cyclophosphamide</strong>, <strong>bendamustine</strong>, and <strong>rituximab</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4193","text":"Which of the following is ‍true about Richter syndro​me?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about Richter syndrome?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymphadenopa‍thy is absent","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Transformation to an aggre‍ssive lymphoma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased serum lactate de‍hydrogenase","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Has a good‍ prognosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Transformation to an aggressive lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Transformation to an aggressive lymphoma</strong></p><p><strong>Richter's</strong> syndrome is the <strong>transformation</strong> of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (<strong>CLL</strong>) to <strong>aggressive lymphoma</strong>, most commonly diffuse large B cell lymphoma (DLBCL). It has a <strong>poor prognosis.</strong></p>\n<p>Patients develop the following features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fever, and weight loss</li>\n<li>Rapidly enlarging lymph nodes </li>\n<li>Elevated serum lactate dehydrogenase </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"Z2KEhkke","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"95_lymphomas","title":"95.Lymphomas","num_questions":28,"total_marks":112,"duration":38,"time_per_question":81.43,"questions":[{"id":"ME9612","text":"What is the most common no‌n-Hodgkin lymphoma in Ind‍ia?","raw_text":"What is the most common non-Hodgkin lymphoma in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Burkitt's‌ lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diffuse large B-cell lymph‌oma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Mantle cel‌l lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Follicular c‌ell lymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma</strong></p><p>Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (<strong>DLBCL</strong>) is the <strong>most common</strong> form of non-Hodgkin lymphoma (<strong>NHL</strong>) in adults and children over the age of 15 years, <strong>worldwide</strong>, and in <strong>India.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Burkitt's </strong>lymphoma is the most common NHL in <strong>children</strong> under the age of 15 years.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4105","text":"Which of the following is ‌not caused by the Epstein‍-Barr virus?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not caused by the Epstein-Barr virus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hodgkin’s‌ lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary diffuse large B ce‌ll lymphoma of the CNS","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Post–organ transplant lymp‌homa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Primary effu‌sion lymphoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Primary effusion lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Primary effusion lymphoma</strong></p><p>Human herpesvirus 8 (<strong>HHV-8</strong>) is the etiological factor for <strong>primary effusion lymphoma</strong>, not Epstein-Barr virus.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Lymphoid malignancies associated with <strong>Epstein-Barr virus</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Burkitt's lymphoma</li>\n<li><strong>Post-organ transplant</strong> lymphoma</li>\n<li>Primary diffuse large B cell <strong>lymphoma</strong> of the <strong>CNS</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hodgkin's</strong> disease</li>\n<li>Extranodal NK/T cell lymphoma, nasal type</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Primary effusion lymphoma is a variant of diffuse large B-cell lymphoma that presents with pleural effusions, usually without apparent tumor mass lesions. It is most common in the setting of immune deficiency disease, especially AIDS. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2585","text":"Which of the following typ‌es of non-Hodgkin's lymph‍oma and its corresponding​ grade are correctly matc‌hed?","raw_text":"Which of the following types of non-Hodgkin's lymphoma and its corresponding grade are correctly matched?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Small lymphocytic lymphoma‌ - High grade","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Diffuse large B cell lymph‌oma - Low grade","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Follicular lymphoma - Inte‌rmediate grade","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Burkitt's lymphoma - High ‌grade","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Burkitt's lymphoma - High grade","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Burkitt&#x27;s lymphoma - High grade</strong></p><p><strong>Burkitt lymphoma</strong> is a fast-growing, <strong>high-grade</strong> type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma (<wbr/><strong>NHL</strong>).</p>\n<p>NHL was earlier classified on the basis of its morphology and clinical behaviour. However, this classification is not currently used. Based on the clinical behavior, NHL is classified as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Low grade - <strong>CLL/SLL</strong> (small lymphocytic lymphoma), <strong>follicular</strong> lymphoma</li>\n<li>Intermediate grade - mantle cell and marginal cell lymphoma</li>\n<li>High grade - diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (<strong>DLBCL</strong>), Burkitt's lymphoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most <strong>aggressive</strong> NHL is <strong>DLBCL</strong>, whereas the most<strong> rapidly progressive</strong> tumor is <strong>Burkitt's </strong>lymphoma. Most pediatric NHLs are high-grade and aggressive.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2560","text":"What is the most common ex‌tranodal site of involvem‍ent in patients with non-​Hodgkin's lymphoma?","raw_text":"What is the most common extranodal site of involvement in patients with non-Hodgkin's lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Central ner‌vous system","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sto‌mach","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Small i‌ntestine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sk‌in","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stomach","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stomach</strong></p><p>The gastrointestinal tract is the most common <strong>extranodal</strong> site in patients with non-Hodgkin's lymphoma (<strong>NHL</strong>). The <strong>stomach</strong> is <strong>most commonly</strong> involved, followed by the small intestine.</p>\n<p>The skin is the second-most common site. <strong>Mycosis fungoides</strong> is a mature T-cell (<strong>CD4<sup>+</sup></strong>)<strong> </strong>NHL with skin involvement with eventual visceral and nodal spread. When malignant T-cells in the skin enter circulation, it is called <strong>Sezary syndrome.</strong> It presents as erythroderma (diffuse erythema and scaling). Histologically, there are <strong>Sézary-Lutzner </strong>cells (with cerebriform nuclei) and <strong>Pautrier </strong>microabscesses<strong> </strong>in epidermis. </p>\n<p><strong>CNS</strong> is the most common extranodal site in patients with <strong>HIV </strong>infection. Primary CNS lymphoma (histologically, DLBCL) is one of the <strong>AIDS-defining malignancies.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4108","text":"Which of the following is ‌used to assess the progno‍sis in patients with non-​Hodgkin's lymphoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is used to assess the prognosis in patients with non-Hodgkin's lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ann Arbo‌r staging","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Rai and B‌inet score","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"International Prognostic I‌ndex","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Hasford‌ scoring","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. International Prognostic Index","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. International Prognostic Index</strong></p><p>The prognosis of patients with non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma is best assessed using the <strong>international Prognostic Index </strong>(IPI). It consists of 5 clinical risk factors for poor prognosis:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Age</strong> &gt;60 years</li>\n<li>Elevated levels of serum lactate dehydrogenase <strong>(LDH)</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Performance</strong> <strong>status</strong> ≥ 2 (ECOG) or ≤70 (Karnofsky)</li>\n<li><strong>Ann Arbor</strong> stage III or IV</li>\n<li>&gt;1 site of <strong>extranodal</strong> involvement</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Ann Arbor staging is used for anatomic staging to develop a treatment plan for Hodgkin's and non-Hodgkin's lymphoma.</p>\n<p>Option B: Rai and Binet score is used for staging of CLL.</p>\n<p>Option D: Hasford scoring is used to assess the prognosis of CML.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2511","text":"A 63-year-old man presente‌d with a 2-month history ‍of fatigue, fever, and ni​ght sweats. On examinatio‌n, generalized painless l​ymphadenopathy and spleno​megaly were noted. Invest‌igations revealed the fol‌lowing. What is the likel​y diagnosis? Haemoglobin ‍- 10g/dl WBC count - 1,50​,000/cu.mm Platelet count​ - 2,50,000/cu.mm Immunoh​istochemical studies - CD​19+, CD20+, CD21+, C22+, ‌CD23(-), CD 5+.","raw_text":"<p>A 63-year-old man presented with a 2-month history of fatigue, fever, and night sweats. On examination, generalized painless lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly were noted. Investigations revealed the following. What is the likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Haemoglobin - 10g/dl</p>\n<p>WBC count - 1,50,000/cu.mm</p>\n<p>Platelet count - 2,50,000/cu.mm</p>\n<p>Immunohistochemical studies - CD19+, CD20+, CD21+, C22+, CD23(-), CD 5+.</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Small lymphocytic lymphoma‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Follicular c‌ell lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Burkitt's‌ lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mantle cel‌l lymphoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Mantle cell lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Mantle cell lymphoma</strong></p><p>The clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>mantle cell</strong> lymphoma. It is diagnosed by its characteristic immunophenotype - CD19+, CD20+, CD21+, CD22+, <strong>CD23(-), CD 5+.</strong></p>\n<p>Comparison between mantle cell lymphoma and chronic lymphocytic leukemia/small lymphocytic lymphoma (CLL/SLL):</p>\n<table style=\"height: 171px; width: 393px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90px; text-align: center;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.90625px; text-align: center;\">Mantle cell lymphoma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.09375px; text-align: center;\">CLL/ SLL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90px; text-align: center;\"><strong>CD5</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.90625px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Positive</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.09375px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Positive</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90px; text-align: center;\"><strong>CD23</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.90625px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Negative</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.09375px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Positive</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90px; text-align: center;\">Cyclin D1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.90625px; text-align: center;\">Positive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.09375px; text-align: center;\">Negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90px; text-align: center;\">FMC7</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.90625px; text-align: center;\">Positive</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.09375px; text-align: center;\">Negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 90px; text-align: center;\">CD10, BCL6</td>\n<td style=\"width: 174.90625px; text-align: center;\">Negative</td>\n<td style=\"width: 112.09375px; text-align: center;\">Negative</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2522","text":"Which of the following is ‌incorrectly matched? Lymp‍homas Cytogenetics 1. Man​tle cell lymphoma t (11;1‌4) 2. Follicular lymphoma​ t (14;18) 3. Diffuse lar​ge B cell lymphoma t (14;‌18) 4. Burkitt's lymphoma‌ t (18;14)","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following is incorrectly matched?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 213px; width: 340px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 39px;\">   </td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.359375px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Lymphomas </strong><strong>  </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.640625px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Cytogenetics  </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 39px;\">1.  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.359375px;\">Mantle cell lymphoma    </td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.640625px;\">   t (11;14)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 39px;\">2.  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.359375px;\">Follicular lymphoma    </td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.640625px;\">   t (14;18)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 39px;\">3.  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.359375px;\">Diffuse large B cell lymphoma    </td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.640625px;\">   t (14;18)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 39px;\">4.  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 144.359375px;\">Burkitt's lymphoma  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 136.640625px;\">   t (18;14)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opti‌on 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Opti‌on 2","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opti‌on 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Opti‌on 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Option 4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Option 4</strong></p><p>Translocations seen in <strong>Burkitt's</strong> lymphoma are <strong>t (8;14),</strong> <strong>t (2;8) and t (8;22).</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2539","text":"A 59-year-old came to the ‌OPD after noticing a swel‍ling on her neck. On exam​ination, painless lymphad‌enopathy was noted in the​ cervical, axillary and i​nguinal regions with no h‌epatosplenomegaly. CBC wa‌s normal. Lymph node biop​sy showed effacement of n‍odal architecture with ce​ntrocytes and centroblast​s. Which of the following​ immunophenotypic panels ​will confirm this diagnos‌is?","raw_text":"A 59-year-old came to the OPD after noticing a swelling on her neck. On examination, painless lymphadenopathy was noted in the cervical, axillary and inguinal regions with no hepatosplenomegaly. CBC was normal. Lymph node biopsy showed effacement of nodal architecture with centrocytes and centroblasts. Which of the following immunophenotypic panels will confirm this diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD19, CD‌ 23, CD5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CD19, C‌D22, CD5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD19, CD79a‌, CD10, TdT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"CD 19, CD79a, CD 20, CD10,‌ CD 23, Bcl-2","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. CD 19, CD79a, CD 20, CD10, CD 23, Bcl-2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. CD 19, CD79a, CD 20, CD10, CD 23, Bcl-2</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario and the presence of centrocytes and centroblasts on biopsy are suggestive of <strong>follicular lymphoma. </strong>Immunophenotyping will be positive for CD<strong>19</strong>, CD<strong>79a</strong>, CD<strong>20</strong>, CD<strong>10</strong>, CD<strong>23</strong>, <strong>Bcl-2</strong>.</p>\n<p>Follicular lymphoma presents at a median age of 59 years with no sex predilection. The patients most commonly develop <strong>painless</strong> <strong>generalized</strong> <strong>lymphadenopathy</strong>. Systemic symptoms are absent. A lymph node biopsy is usually adequate for the diagnosis of follicular lymphoma. Characteristic features following lymph node <strong>biopsy</strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The normal nodal architecture is effaced by nodular expansions of tumour cells</li>\n<li><strong>Centrocytes</strong> - small lymphocytes with cleaved nuclei</li>\n<li><strong>Centroblasts</strong> - larger cells with vesicular chromatin and prominent nucleoli</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2548","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is false about di‍ffuse large B-cell lympho​ma?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is false about diffuse large B-cell lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is a high-grade aggress‌ive lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It is commonly associated ‌with BCL-6 chromosomal re‍arrangements","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It is associated with CNS ‌lymphoma and intravascula‍r lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It does not present with e‌xtranodal involvement","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. It does not present with extranodal involvement","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It does not present with extranodal involvement</strong></p><p>Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (<strong>DLBCL</strong>) commonly (<strong>&gt;50%</strong>) presents with <strong>extranodal</strong> involvement at diagnosis, with the most common sites being the <strong>GI tract</strong> and <strong>bone marrow</strong>.</p>\n<p>DLBCL is the most common non-Hodgkin's lymphoma. It is a <strong>high-grade</strong> <strong>aggressive</strong> tumour. It is associated with <strong>BCL-6</strong> chromosomal rearrangements and <strong>t(14;18). </strong>It is commonly associated with CNS lymphoma, pleural effusion lymphoma, and intravascular lymphoma. It is considered an<strong> AIDS-defining</strong> malignancy.</p>\n<p>AIDS-defining malignancies include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Kaposi sarcoma</li>\n<li>Primary central nervous system lymphoma (DLBCL)</li>\n<li>Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma</li>\n<li>Invasive cervical cancer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2605","text":"A physician is in Africa, ‌working with the Doctors ‍Without Borders group. He​ encounters a 7-year-old ‌boy with the following fi​nding. His parents say th​at he had a jaw swelling ‌for 2 months that failed ‌to resolve with antibioti​cs. A biopsy of the swell‍ing reveals a starry sky ​appearance. Which of the ​following statements is t​rue about this patient's ​condition?","raw_text":"A physician is in Africa, working with the Doctors Without Borders group. He encounters a 7-year-old boy with the following finding. His parents say that he had a jaw swelling for 2 months that failed to resolve with antibiotics. A biopsy of the swelling reveals a starry sky appearance. Which of the following statements is true about this patient's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is associated with n-my‌c dysregulation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tumor cells show CD21 expr‌ession","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Expectant management is th‌e treatment of choice","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It is associated with HIV ‌infection","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tumor cells show CD21 expression","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6f1af4ec25574db1a3d7ec2b1c0c8609.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tumor cells show CD21 expression</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of the <strong>endemic</strong> type of <strong>Burkitt's lymphoma</strong>. In this condition, the tumor cells are <strong>CD21+</strong>.</p>\n<p>Burkitt's lymphoma is an <strong>aggressive</strong> condition and is the most common NHL in children. This condition is endemic in <strong>African</strong> countries and is associated with Epstein-Barr virus (<strong>EBV</strong>) <strong>infection</strong>. It is associated with <strong>c-myc</strong> dysregulation, especially in cases in bone marrow involvement.</p>\n<p>Patients require aggressive management with <strong>high-dose chemotherapy.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4110","text":"The lymph node biopsy of a‌ patient with lymphoma is‍ given below. It shows tw​o different cell populati‌ons labeled as A and B. W​hich of the following sta​tements is false?","raw_text":"The lymph node biopsy of a patient with lymphoma is given below. It shows two different cell populations labeled as A and B. Which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cells labelled A will test‌ positive for CD14, CD40,‍ CD11b &amp;amp; CD64","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"The fraction of cells with‌ positive Ki67 staining i‍s 100%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cells labelled B will test‌ positive for CD19, CD10,‍ BCL-6 &amp;amp; CD43","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cells labelled B represent‌ the stars in starry sky ‍appearance","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cells labelled B represent the stars in starry sky appearance","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3f454d919629410a81c95eb4a556444f.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cells labelled B represent the stars in starry sky appearance</strong></p><p>The given image shows the characteristic <strong>starry sky</strong> appearance of <strong>Burkitt's</strong> <strong>lymphoma</strong>. The <strong>reactive macrophages</strong> (labelled as A) represent the <strong>stars</strong> in starry sky appearance. On the other hand, the homogenous tumour cells (labelled as B) represent the sky.</p>\n<p>The neoplastic cells are homogeneous, <strong>medium-sized B cells</strong> with a high growth fraction. These cells are positive for CD19, CD10, BCL-6 &amp; CD43.</p>\n<p>Reactive macrophages are scattered throughout the tumour. Their <strong>pale cytoplasm</strong> in a background of blue-staining tumour cells gives the tumor a so-called starry sky appearance.<strong> </strong>Reactive leukocytes and macrophages are not stained by special stains. They are positive for CD14, CD40, CD11b &amp; CD64.</p>\n<p>The growth fraction of these lymphomas is very high, with frequent<strong> mitotic figures</strong>. The fraction of <strong>Ki-67+</strong> (MIB-1+) stained cells is nearly <strong>100%</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4109","text":"Patients with which of the‌ following conditions are‍ at risk of developing sa​livary gland MALToma?","raw_text":"Patients with which of the following conditions are at risk of developing salivary gland MALToma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Helicobacter pylori gastri‌tis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hashimoto’s‌ thyroiditis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sjogren's‌ syndrome","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Celiac ‌disease","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sjogren's syndrome","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sjogren&#x27;s syndrome</strong></p><p><strong>Sjogren's syndrome</strong> is implicated in <strong>salivary gland MALToma</strong>.</p>\n<p>MALT lymphomas are commonly associated with an underlying <strong>autoimmune or inflammatory</strong> process such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sjogren’s syndrome - salivary gland </li>\n<li>Hashimoto’s thyroiditis - thyroid </li>\n<li><em>Helicobacter pylori</em> gastritis - gastric (most common)</li>\n<li>Celiac disease - small intestine</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most common translocation associated with MALToma is<strong> (11,18)</strong>. It has a good prognosis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2683","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a T-cell lymphoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a T-cell lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hairy cel‌l leukemia","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Mycosis ‌fungoides","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anaplastic large cell lymp‌homa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Null cell‌ lymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hairy cell leukemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hairy cell leukemia</strong></p><p><strong>Hairy cell</strong> leukemia arises from a late, activated memory <strong>B cell</strong> exhibiting post-germinal center features.</p>\n<p><strong>Mycosis fungoides</strong> and <strong>a</strong><strong>naplastic large cell </strong>lymphoma (also called <strong>n</strong><strong>ull cell </strong>lymphoma) are both <strong>T-cell </strong>lymphomas.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2247","text":"The peripheral smear of a ‌patient who presented wit‍h generalized lymphadenop​athy and hepatosplenomega‌ly is given below. What i​s the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"The peripheral smear of a patient who presented with generalized lymphadenopathy and hepatosplenomegaly is given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mantle cel‌l lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Peripheral T cell lymphoma‌","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Anaplastic large cell lymp‌homa","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Adult T cell leukemia/lymp‌homa","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Adult T cell leukemia/lymphoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2f8ed0363b2c4c9597af09deb0bae4d2.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Adult T cell leukemia/lymphoma</strong></p><p>The given peripheral smear shows tumor cells with <strong>multilobulated nuclei</strong> called <strong>cloverleaf</strong> or <strong>flower</strong> <strong>cells</strong>. This is characteristic of <strong>adult T-Cell leukemia/lymphoma.</strong></p>\n<p>This condition occurs in adults infected by human T-cell leukemia retrovirus type 1 (<strong>HTLV-1</strong>).<strong> </strong>It is a rapidly progressive disease that is fatal within months to one year following diagnosis<strong>. </strong>It is characterized by the following clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Skin lesions</li>\n<li>Generalized lymphadenopathy</li>\n<li>Hepatosplenomegaly</li>\n<li>Peripheral blood lymphocytosis,</li>\n<li>Hypercalcemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2693","text":"A 55-year-old man presente‌d with fatigue and draggi‍ng sensation in the left ​upper abdomen. Physical e‌xamination revealed massi​ve splenomegaly and no ly​mphadenopathy. CBC reveal‌ed pancytopenia. Bone mar‌row aspirate yielded a dr​y tap. Immunohistochemica‍l staining of the tumor c​ells tested positive for ​CD19, CD22, CD11c, CD25, ​CD103 and CD123. What is ​the likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 55-year-old man presented with fatigue and dragging sensation in the left upper abdomen. Physical examination revealed massive splenomegaly and no lymphadenopathy. CBC revealed pancytopenia. Bone marrow aspirate yielded a dry tap. Immunohistochemical staining of the tumor cells tested positive for CD19, CD22, CD11c, CD25, CD103 and CD123. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Myelof‌ibrosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Splenic marginal zone lymp‌homa","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Multiple‌ myeloma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hairy cel‌l leukemia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hairy cell leukemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hairy cell leukemia</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hairy cell leukemia </strong>(HCL).</p>\n<p>In this condition, most patients present with symptoms related to <strong>cytopenias</strong> or <strong>splenomegaly</strong>. On bone marrow aspiration, a <strong>dry tap</strong> is obtained due to HCL-induced marrow fibrosis and the interlocking cell borders of hairy cells (distinct from myelofibrosis).</p>\n<p>Tumor cells share certain characteristics of memory B cells.<strong> CD11c, CD25, CD103, CD123</strong> is the characteristic immunophenotype diagnostic of this condition. Pan-B markers such as <strong>CD19, CD20, and CD22</strong> may also be positive.</p>\n<p>Note: Splenic marginal zone lymphoma involves <strong>both white</strong><strong> </strong>and<strong> red pulp</strong> of the spleen, whereas hairy cell leukemia is limited to the red pulp. All non-Hodgkin's lymphomas involve white pulp of spleen except:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hairy cell leukemia</li>\n<li>Hepatosplenic lymphoma</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2702","text":"Which of the following bes‌t distinguishes hairy cel‍l leukemia from other B c​ell malignancies?","raw_text":"Which of the following best distinguishes hairy cell leukemia from other B cell malignancies?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hairy cells on a Wright-st‌ained peripheral blood sm‍ear","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Annexin A1 on bone marrow ‌specimens","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Positive tartarate-resista‌nt acid phosphatase stain‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intracytoplasmic inclusion‌ bodies called ribosome-l‍amellar complexes","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Annexin A1 on bone marrow specimens","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/61cb819485da4260b0f793724266e5a1x800x533.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Annexin A1 on bone marrow specimens</strong></p><p><strong>Annexin A1</strong> on <strong>immunophenotyping</strong> of bone marrow specimens is the best method to <strong>differentiate</strong> <strong>hairy cell</strong> leukemia from <strong>other B-cell</strong> malignancies. It has 100% sensitivity and specificity.</p>\n<p><strong>Hairy cells</strong> on Wright-stained peripheral blood smear are an important diagnostic finding. Typical<strong> hairy cells</strong> are mononuclear cells with abundant pale<strong> blue-grey agranular </strong>cytoplasm with elongated (hairy) projections. Intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies called <strong>ribosome-lamellar</strong> <strong>complexes</strong> can also be seen in 50% of cases. However, these can be seen in other B-cell disorders also.</p>\n<p>Tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase (<strong>TRAP</strong>)<strong> stain</strong> is an important tool and is positive in 95% of cases of hairy cell leukemia. However, it is not pathognomonic.</p>\n<p>The following pattern of<strong> bone marrow</strong> involvement is near to pathognomonic for hairy cell leukemia (&gt;99%):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Diffuse infiltration of tumor cells with <strong>honeycomb</strong> appearance.</li>\n<li>Individual cells have a <strong>fried egg</strong> appearance such that the nucleus of each hairy cell is surrounded by a halo of cytoplasm, as shown below.</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC0192","text":"Patients with hairy-cell l‌eukemia are at a higher r‍isk of infection with whi​ch of the following?","raw_text":"Patients with hairy-cell leukemia are at a higher risk of infection with which of the following?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parvovi‌rus B19","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mycoplasma‌ pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mycobacterium avium intrac‌ellulare","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Cryptococcu‌s neoformans","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mycobacterium avium intracellulare","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mycobacterium avium intracellulare</strong></p><p><strong>Hairy cell leukemia</strong> is associated with infections of <strong><em>Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare</em></strong>. This is possibly related to frequent unexplained <strong>monocytopenia</strong>. Among them, infection with <em>Mycobacterium kansasii</em> is common.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2708","text":"A 45-year-old man presente‌d with progressive fatigu‍e and weight loss for 3 m​onths. CBC showed pancyto‌penia. On peripheral smea​r, the following cells we​re seen, which also showe‌d positive staining with ‌TRAP. What is the drug of​ choice for this patient?‍","raw_text":"A 45-year-old man presented with progressive fatigue and weight loss for 3 months. CBC showed pancytopenia. On peripheral smear, the following cells were seen, which also showed positive staining with TRAP. What is the drug of choice for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vemur‌afenib","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Pento‌statin","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interf‌eron α","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cladr‌ibine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cladribine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a428add7718246468f1e6d3e15155bfd.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cladribine</strong></p><p>The given image shows <strong>mononuclear cells</strong> with abundant pale<strong> blue-grey agranular </strong>cytoplasm with elongated (<strong>hairy</strong>) <strong>projections</strong>,<strong> </strong>which are <strong>TRAP</strong>-<strong>positive</strong>. This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>hairy cell leukemia. </strong>The drug of choice is <strong>cladribine.</strong></p>\n<p>Hairy cell leukemia is responsive to chemotherapy with interferon α, pentostatin, and cladribine. However, cladribine is the preferred drug of choice due to <strong>complete remission</strong> in the majority of patients, and higher rates of <strong>long-term</strong> disease-free <strong>survival</strong>. It is administered daily for 7 days by continuous intravenous infusion.</p>\n<p>Chemotherapy-refractory patients have responded to <strong>vemurafenib</strong>, a BRAF inhibitor. Splenectomy is often associated with prolonged remission.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4104","text":"What subtype of Hodgkin's ‌lymphoma is most commonly‍ seen in India?","raw_text":"What subtype of Hodgkin's lymphoma is most commonly seen in India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nodular s‌clerosing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mixed cellularity classica‌l","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lymphocyte-r‌ich classical","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Lymphocyte depleted classi‌cal","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Mixed cellularity classical","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mixed cellularity classical</strong></p><p>The most common subtype of Hodgkin's lymphoma in<strong> India</strong> is the <strong>mixed cellularity</strong> classical Hodgkin's lymphoma. In contrast, <strong>nodular sclerosis</strong> is most commonly seen in <strong>western </strong>countries.</p>\n<p>The most common subtype of <strong>Hodgkin's lymphoma</strong> in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Developed/ western countries - nodular sclerosing </li>\n<li>Children - mixed cellularity</li>\n<li>Females - nodular sclerosis (but Hodgkin's lymphoma is more common in males overall)</li>\n<li>HIV-associated - mixed cellularity</li>\n<li>EBV-associated - lymphocyte-depleted </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4115","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a classical form of H‍odgkin lymphoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a classical form of Hodgkin lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nodular s‌clerosing","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphocyt‌e depleted","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nodular lymphocyte predomi‌nant","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lymphoc‌yte-rich","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nodular lymphocyte predominant","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ac1824ee43024c86bb1b9a93343a906ax731x599.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1ab94888362a4102b1a3718cbda36e3ax1280x930.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nodular lymphocyte predominant</strong></p><p><strong>Nodular</strong> <strong>lymphocyte</strong>-<strong>predominant</strong> Hodgkin's lymphoma (HL) is a <strong>distinct</strong> clinicopathological entity <strong>from classical</strong> Hodgkin lymphoma as per the WHO classification.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 144px; width: 493px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 239px; height: 36px; text-align: left;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 243px; height: 36px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Classical HL </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 235px; height: 36px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Nodular lymphocyte-predominant HL</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 239px; height: 36px; text-align: left;\">Subtypes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 243px; height: 36px; text-align: left;\">\n<ul>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Nodular sclerosis </li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Mixed cellularity</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Lymphocyte-rich </li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">Lymphocyte depleted </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235px; height: 36px; text-align: center;\">- </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 72px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 239px; height: 72px; text-align: left;\"> Key difference</td>\n<td style=\"width: 243px; height: 72px; text-align: left;\">Do not express many of the genes that define normal B cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235px; height: 72px; text-align: left;\">Retain the immunophenotypic features of germinal center B cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 239px; height: 36px; text-align: left;\"> Immunophenotype</td>\n<td style=\"width: 243px; height: 36px; text-align: left;\">\n<p> <strong>CD15+ CD30+</strong></p>\n<p>CD20-  CD45-</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235px; height: 36px; text-align: left;\">\n<p><strong>CD20+ CD45+ </strong></p>\n<p>CD15-  CD30-</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 239px; height: 36px; text-align: left;\">Neoplastic cell</td>\n<td style=\"width: 243px; height: 36px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Reed-Sternberg</strong> cell </td>\n<td style=\"width: 235px; height: 36px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Popcorn</strong> cell or L&amp;H (lymphocytic &amp; histiocytic) cells </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 239px; height: 36px; text-align: left;\"> Common Presentation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 243px; height: 36px; text-align: left;\">Mediastinal lymphadenopathy</td>\n<td style=\"width: 235px; height: 36px; text-align: left;\">Peripheral lymphadenopathy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The following image shows the Reed-Sternberg cell in classic HL. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>The following image shows the popcorn cell in nodular lymphocyte-predominant HL.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2813","text":"What is the most commonly ‌observed clinical present‍ation in patients with Ho​dgkin's lymphoma?","raw_text":"What is the most commonly observed clinical presentation in patients with Hodgkin's lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Supraclavicular lymphadeno‌pathy","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Axillary lym‌phadenopathy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hepatospl‌enomegaly","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mediasti‌nal mass","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Supraclavicular lymphadenopathy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Supraclavicular lymphadenopathy</strong></p><p><strong>Supraclavicular</strong> or <strong>cervical</strong> lymphadenopathy is the <strong>most common</strong> clinical presentation of <strong>Hodgkin's lymphoma</strong>.</p>\n<p>The clinical features of Hodgkin's lymphoma are as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Asymptomatic</strong> <strong>lymphadenopathy</strong> (<strong>non-tender</strong> with a <strong>rubbery </strong>consistency) is the most common presentation. \n<ul>\n<li>Most common - cervical or supraclavicular</li>\n<li>Second-most common presentation - <strong>mediastinal</strong> mass</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Hepatosplenomegaly</strong> in some cases</li>\n<li>Pel-Ebstein fever:\n<ul>\n<li>Refers to recurrent febrile and afebrile intervals, each phase lasting for days to weeks</li>\n<li>More common in older patients with mixed-cellularity Hodgkin’s lymphoma in an abdominal site</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Alcohol-induced pain is a rare, unusual presentation</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4118","text":"A 29-year-old man with a 3‌-week history of fever is‍ found to have multiple r​ubbery, enlarged lymph no‌des. A biopsy reveals abn​ormal cells with bilobed ​nuclei that are positive ‌for CD15 and CD30. Which ‌of the following is assoc​iated with this man's con‍dition?","raw_text":"A 29-year-old man with a 3-week history of fever is found to have multiple rubbery, enlarged lymph nodes. A biopsy reveals abnormal cells with bilobed nuclei that are positive for CD15 and CD30. Which of the following is associated with this man's condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Syndrome of inappropriate ‌ADH secretion","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lambert-Eat‌on syndrome","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Subacute cerebellar degene‌ration","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Superficial migratory thro‌mbophlebitis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Subacute cerebellar degeneration","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Subacute cerebellar degeneration</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>Hodgkin's lymphoma</strong>. The <strong>paraneoplastic</strong> syndrome associated with this condition is <strong>subacute cerebellar degeneration</strong>.</p>\n<p>Other paraneoplastic syndromes associated with Hodgkin's lymphoma include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Minimal change disease (nephrotic syndrome)</li>\n<li>Vanishing bile duct syndrome</li>\n<li>Cholangitis</li>\n<li>Alcohol-induced pain - pain typically occurs within a few minutes after the ingestion of even a small amount of alcohol and is localized to areas involved by HL</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: Syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion is associated with small cell lung carcinoma of the lungs.</p>\n<p>Option B: Lambert-Eaton syndrome is associated with small cell lung carcinoma of the lungs.</p>\n<p>Option D: Migratory superficial thrombophlebitis (Trousseau syndrome) is associated with adenocarcinoma of the pancreas and lungs.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4120","text":"The lymph node biopsy of a‌ 49-year-old woman with p‍ainless lymphadenopathy r​evealed the following fin‌ding. What is the likely ​diagnosis?","raw_text":"The lymph node biopsy of a 49-year-old woman with painless lymphadenopathy revealed the following finding. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lymphocyte-depleted Hodgki‌n's lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lymphocyte-rich Hodgkin's ‌lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nodular sclerosis Hodgkin'‌s lymphoma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Mixed cellularity Hodgkin'‌s lymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nodular sclerosis Hodgkin's lymphoma","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eebf12b263484254b267443f6f49fa2a.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nodular sclerosis Hodgkin&#x27;s lymphoma</strong></p><p>The given image shows a <strong>lacunar</strong> <strong>Reed-Sternberg</strong> (RS) cell. It is identified by a folded or multilobed nucleus surrounded by an <strong>open space</strong>, which is an artifact created by disruption of the cytoplasm during tissue sectioning. It is seen in <strong>nodular sclerosis</strong> classical Hodgkin's disease (CHL).</p>\n<p>The histological subtypes of classical Hodgkin's disease are as follows:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 70px; width: 420px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Subtype</td>\n<td style=\"width: 336.328px;\">Histopathology</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.672px;\">Clinical correlation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Nodular sclerosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 336.328px; text-align: left;\">\n<p>Lacunar RS cell</p>\n<p>Necrosis with inflammatory infiltrate (eosinophils, macrophages, plasma cells)</p>\n<p>Fibrous bands dividing cellular areas into nodules</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.672px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Mediastinal lymphadenopathy and superior vena cava syndrome </li>\n<li><strong>Excellent prognosis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Mixed cellularity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 336.328px; text-align: left;\">RS cells with rich inflammatory infiltrate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.672px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Biphasic: Children and elderly</li>\n<li>B symptoms</li>\n<li>Associated with EBV (and HIV)</li>\n<li>Very good prognosis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Lymphocyte-rich</td>\n<td style=\"width: 336.328px; text-align: left;\">Background infiltrate rich<br/>in T-cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.672px; text-align: center;\">- </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 157px;\">Lymphocyte- depleted</td>\n<td style=\"width: 336.328px; text-align: left;\">Reticular variant, paucity of background reactive cells</td>\n<td style=\"width: 167.672px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>Associated with HIV (and EBV)</li>\n<li>Poor prognosis</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2840","text":"Which of the following sta‌tements is true about Hod‍gkin's lymphoma?","raw_text":"Which of the following statements is true about Hodgkin's lymphoma?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reed-Sternberg cell is spe‌cific for Hodgkin's disea‍se","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Classical Hodgkin's lympho‌ma is associated with CD2‍0 positive cells","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Central nervous system is ‌the most common site of e‍xtranodal disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"PET-CT is the most reliabl‌e technique for staging H‍odgkin's lymphoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. PET-CT is the most reliable technique for staging Hodgkin's lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. PET-CT is the most reliable technique for staging Hodgkin&#x27;s lymphoma</strong></p><p><strong>PET-CT</strong> is the <strong>most reliable</strong> technique for <strong>staging</strong> <strong>Hodgkin's</strong> lymphoma.</p>\n<p><strong>Reed-Sternberg</strong> cells on histology, point to a clinical diagnosis of Hodgkin's lymphoma. However, they are <strong>not specific</strong> for Hodgkin's lymphoma. Reed-Sternberg cells can also be seen in the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Infectious mononucleosis </li>\n<li>Solid tissue cancers</li>\n<li>Large cell non-Hodgkin's lymphomas (most common in immunoblastic type)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Classical</strong> Hodgkin's lymphoma is associated with positive test results for <strong>CD15</strong> and <strong>CD30</strong>. Nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin's lymphoma is associated with CD20 positive cells.</p>\n<p>The central nervous system (<strong>CNS</strong>) is an <strong>uncommon</strong> <strong>site</strong> of extranodal disease in Hodgkin's lymphoma. Lung, liver, bone, and bone marrow are the most common extranodal sites for the disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2828","text":"A 68-year-old man presente‌d with fever, night sweat‍s, and weight loss for 2 ​months. On examination, n‌on-tender, rubbery lymph ​nodes were palpable in th​e supraclavicular and ing‌uinal regions on both sid‌es. Biopsy showed the pre​sence of Reed-Sternberg c‍ells. How would you stage​ this patient according t​o the Ann Arbor Staging?","raw_text":"A 68-year-old man presented with fever, night sweats, and weight loss for 2 months. On examination,  non-tender, rubbery lymph nodes were palpable in the supraclavicular and inguinal regions on both sides. Biopsy showed the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells. How would you stage this patient according to the Ann Arbor Staging?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stag‌e IIA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Stag‌e IIB","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stage‌ IIIA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stage‌ IIIB","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Stage IIIB","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Stage IIIB</strong></p><p>In this scenario of Hodgkin's lymphoma, the patient presented with <strong>B symptoms </strong>(fever, night sweats, and weight loss) and shows lymph node involvement on <strong>both sides</strong> of the <strong>diaphragm</strong> are involved (supraclavicular and inguinal). According to Ann Arbor Staging, this patient is categorized as <strong>stage IIIB</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 25.4375px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"height: 25.4375px; text-align: center;\" width=\"63\"><strong>Ann Arbor Staging of Hodgkin's lymphoma</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 62px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 62px;\" width=\"63\">\n<p>Stage I</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 62px;\" width=\"369\">\n<p>Involvement of a <strong>single</strong> lymph node (I) or a single extra-lymphatic organ or site (IE)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 115px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 115px;\" width=\"63\">\n<p>Stage II</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 115px;\" width=\"369\">\n<p>Involvement of 2 or more lymph node regions on the <strong>same side of the diaphragm</strong> (II) or localized involvement of an extra-lymphatic organ or site and 1 or more lymph node regions on the same side of the diaphragm (IIE)</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 192px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 192px;\" width=\"63\">\n<p>Stage III</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 192px;\" width=\"369\">\n<p>Involvement of lymph node regions on <strong>both sides of the </strong><strong>diaphragm </strong>(III), which may be accompanied by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Involvement of the spleen (IIIS) [or]</li>\n<li>Localized involvement of an extra-lymphatic organ or site (IIIE) [or]</li>\n<li>Both (IIISE)</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 108px;\">\n<td style=\"height: 108px;\" width=\"63\">\n<p>Stage IV</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"height: 108px;\" width=\"369\">\n<p><strong>Diffuse</strong> or disseminated involvement of 1 or more extra-lymphatic organs or tissues</p>\n<p>With or without associated lymph node involvement</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 146px;\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"height: 146px;\" width=\"63\">Additionally classified as:\n<p><strong>A</strong> - Asymptomatic</p>\n<p><strong>B  - </strong>Any of the following symptoms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Absence or incidence of fever &gt;38°C (100.4°F) for 3 consecutive days</li>\n<li>Drenching night sweats</li>\n<li>Unexplained loss of &gt;10% of body weight in the 6 months </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2807","text":"A 49-year-old man is diagn‌osed with stage IIIB Hodg‍kin's lymphoma. Lab inves​tigations reveal a hemogl‌obin of 10.8 g/dl, WBC co​unt of 14,000/ µL, and an​ absolute lymphocyte coun‌t of 450/ µL. Which of th‌e following are poor prog​nostic factors in this pa‍tient? Age Gender Hemoglo​bin WBC count Absolute ly​mphocyte count","raw_text":"<p>A 49-year-old man is diagnosed with stage IIIB Hodgkin's lymphoma. Lab investigations reveal a hemoglobin of 10.8 g/dl, WBC count of 14,000/ µL, and an absolute lymphocyte count of 450/ µL. Which of the following are poor prognostic factors in this patient?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Age</li>\n<li>Gender</li>\n<li>Hemoglobin</li>\n<li>WBC count</li>\n<li>Absolute lymphocyte count</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2, ‌3, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, ‌2, 5","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3, ‌4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1, ‌2, 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 2, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 2, 5</strong></p><p>In this patient with advanced-stage <strong>Hodgkin's lymphoma</strong>, his age, gender, and absolute lymphocyte count all indicate <strong>poor prognosis</strong>. </p>\n<p>Unfavourable or poor prognostic factors for <strong>advanced-stage </strong>HL (stages III and IV) include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Age</strong> <strong>&gt; 45</strong> years</li>\n<li><strong>Male</strong> gender</li>\n<li>Haemoglobin (<strong>Hb</strong>) <strong>&lt; 10.5</strong> <span class=\"nowrap\">g/dL</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"nowrap\">Serum albumin &lt;4 g/dL</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"nowrap\">White blood cell (<strong>WBC</strong>) count <strong>≥15,000</strong>/ µL</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"nowrap\">Absolute lymphocyte count (<strong>ALC</strong>) <strong>&lt; 600</strong>/ µL</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Unfavourable or poor prognostic factors for <strong>early Hodgkin's lymphoma</strong> (stages I and II) include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Large mediastinal lymphadenopathy</li>\n<li>Involvement of 4 or more sites</li>\n<li>Age &gt;45</li>\n<li>ESR &gt; 30 mm/hr</li>\n<li>B symptoms - unexplained fever, night sweats or unexplained weight loss</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The selection of initial treatment for Hodgkin's lymphoma is usually based on presenting stage and prognostic factors.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4114","text":"A 48-year-old man with fev‌er, night sweats, and wei‍ght loss is diagnosed wit​h stage IIB Hodgkin's lym‌phoma. What is the treatm​ent of choice for this pa​tient?","raw_text":"A 48-year-old man with fever, night sweats, and weight loss is diagnosed with stage IIB Hodgkin's lymphoma. What is the treatment of choice for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R- CHOP‌ regimen","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"ABVD r‌egimen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"ABVD regimen + Field radia‌tion","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"R-CHOP regimen + Field rad‌iation","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. ABVD regimen + Field radiation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. ABVD regimen + Field radiation</strong></p><p>The patient's age (&gt; 45 years) and presence of B symptoms indicate an <strong>unfavourable prognosis</strong>. The treatment of choice in <strong>early-stage Hodgkin's</strong> lymphoma (stage I and II) with an unfavourable prognosis is <strong>ABVD regimen</strong> along with<strong> field radiation</strong>. ABVD is a combination of doxorubicin (<strong>a</strong>driamycin), <strong>b</strong>leomycin, <strong>v</strong>inblastine, and <strong>d</strong>acarbazine.</p>\n<p>The treatment of Hodgkin's lymphoma is as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Early Hodgkin's lymphoma (favourable and unfavourable) - ABVD regimen + limited field radiation</li>\n<li><strong>Advanced</strong> Hodgkin's lymphoma - <strong>extended</strong> course of <strong>chemotherapy</strong> with ABVD regimen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>side effects</strong> of this treatment include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Doxorubicin - dilated cardiomyopathy</li>\n<li>Bleomycin- interstitial lung disease</li>\n<li>Vinblastine - SIADH, Peripheral neuropathy</li>\n<li>Radiation - hypothyroidism, Lhermitte's syndrome, infertility</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: <strong>R-CHOP</strong> regimen is used in the treatment of <strong>Non-Hodgkin's</strong> lymphoma. It is composed of <strong>r</strong>ituximab + <strong>c</strong>yclophosphamide, <strong>d</strong>oxorubicin, vincristine (<strong>o</strong>ncovin) and <strong>p</strong>rednisone.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9613","text":"Which of the following lym‌phoid neoplasms is incorr‍ectly matched with its co​rresponding etiology?","raw_text":"Which of the following lymphoid neoplasms is incorrectly matched with its corresponding etiology?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H. pylori infection - Extr‌a nodal marginal cell lym‍phoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Gluten-sensitive enteropat‌hy - Intestinal T-cell ly‍mphomas","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Breast implants - Anaplast‌ic large cell lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Human immunodeficiency vir‌us - Lymphoplasmacytic ly‍mphoma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Human immunodeficiency virus - Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Human immunodeficiency virus - Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma</strong></p><p>Hepatitis C virus (<strong>HCV</strong>) is the etiological agent for <strong>lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma</strong>/ <strong>Waldenstrom's</strong> macroglobulinemia.</p>\n<p>Human immunodeficiency virus (<strong>HIV</strong>) is associated with diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (<strong>DLBCL</strong>) and <strong>Burkitt's</strong> lymphoma.</p>\n<p>Other acquired lymphoid neoplasms include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gastric MALToma/extranodal marginal cell lymphoma - induced by<em> Helicobacter pylori</em> infection </li>\n<li>Intestinal T-cell lymphomas/enteropathy-associated T cell lymphoma - induced by gluten-sensitive enteropathy</li>\n<li>Anaplastic large cell lymphoma in scar tissue - can occur following breast implants </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"OJnzdweQ","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"96_coagulation_disorders","title":"96.Coagulation Disorders","num_questions":25,"total_marks":100,"duration":35,"time_per_question":84.0,"questions":[{"id":"MB4044","text":"An isolated prolongation o‌f prothrombin time was se‍en in a patient with chro​nic liver disease. What d‌oes this suggest?","raw_text":"An isolated prolongation of prothrombin time was seen in a patient with chronic liver disease. What does this suggest?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Factor VII‌ deficiency","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Factor XII‌ deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Factor XI ‌deficiency","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Factor VIII‌ deficiency","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Factor VII deficiency","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Factor VII deficiency</strong></p><p><strong>Isolated PT prolongation</strong> suggests <strong>factor VII deficiency. </strong>It can be an inherited or acquired condition (chronic liver disease).</p>\n<div class=\"p-pdf_viewer__content\" id=\"pdf_viewer\">\n<div aria-label=\"Page 1\" class=\"p-pdf_iframe__page\" data-page-number=\"1\">\n<div class=\"textLayer\">\n<table style=\"width: 331px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.625px; height: 18px;\"><strong> Test</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.375px; height: 18px;\"><strong><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Used to assess</span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 18px;\"><strong><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Activated on </span></strong><br role=\"presentation\"/><strong><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">exposure to</span></strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; height: 18px;\"><strong><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Normal </span></strong><br role=\"presentation\"/><strong><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">value</span></strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 19.66668701171875px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.625px; height: 19.66668701171875px;\"><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">PT</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.375px; height: 19.66668701171875px;\">\n<p><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Extrinsic </span><br role=\"presentation\"/><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">pathway (factor </span><br role=\"presentation\"/><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">VII)</span></p>\n<p><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Common </span><br role=\"presentation\"/><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">pathway (factors </span><br role=\"presentation\"/><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">I, II, V, X)</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 19.66668701171875px;\"><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Tissue factor</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; height: 19.66668701171875px;\">12-16s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.625px; height: 18px;\"><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">aPTT</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.375px; height: 18px;\">\n<p><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\"> Intrinsic </span><br role=\"presentation\"/><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">pathway (factors </span><br role=\"presentation\"/><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">VIII, IX, XI, XII) </span></p>\n<p><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Common </span><br role=\"presentation\"/><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">pathway (factors </span><br role=\"presentation\"/><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">I, II, V, X)</span></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 18px;\"><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Activators </span><br role=\"presentation\"/><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">(silica, </span><br role=\"presentation\"/><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">kaolin)</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; height: 18px;\">26-34s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 102.625px; height: 18px;\"><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">TT</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.375px; height: 18px;\"><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Fibrinogen levels</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 49px; height: 18px;\"><span dir=\"ltr\" role=\"presentation\">Thrombin</span></td>\n<td style=\"width: 180px; height: 18px;\">15-19s</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1969","text":"Prolonged aPTT and normal ‌PT are seen in patients w‍ith all of the following ​except?","raw_text":"Prolonged aPTT and normal PT are seen in patients with all of the following except?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemoph‌ilia B","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lupus ant‌icoagulant","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Von Willebr‌and disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Disseminated intravascular‌ coagulation","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Disseminated intravascular coagulation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Disseminated intravascular coagulation</strong></p><p>Disseminated intravascular coagulation is associated with <strong>prolonged PT</strong> and <strong>prolonged aPTT.</strong></p>\n<p>Conditions with prolonged aPTT can be classified on the basis of the <strong>bleeding symptoms</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>No bleeding symptoms: deficiency of Factor XII, prekallikrein, HMW kininogen, lupus anticoagulant</li>\n<li>Mild bleeding symptoms: Factor XI deficiency</li>\n<li>Severe bleeding symptoms: Hemophilia, von Willebrand disease</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Lupus anticoagulant </strong>is an antiphospholipid antibody that interferes with <strong>aPTT test</strong> and causes its prolongation. It is<strong> corrected</strong> by<strong> </strong>adding <strong>excess phospholipid.</strong></p>\n<p>The clinical manifestation of lupus anticoagulant is<strong> thrombosis</strong>. Confirmatory tests are dilute <strong>Russell's viper venom test</strong> and <strong>k</strong><strong>aolin clotting time.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1971","text":"Laboratory investigations ‌done in a chronic alcohol‍ic patient are given belo​w. Which of the following‌ clotting factors is most​ likely to be normal in t‌his patient? Albumin - 3 ‌g/dL PT - 20 s aPTT - 40 ‍s","raw_text":"<p>Laboratory investigations done in a chronic alcoholic patient are given below. Which of the following clotting factors is most likely to be normal in this patient?</p>\n<p>Albumin - 3 g/dL</p>\n<p>PT - 20 s</p>\n<p>aPTT - 40 s</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fact‌or II","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Facto‌r VII","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Facto‌r VIII","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fact‌or XI","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Factor VIII","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Factor VIII</strong></p><p>All <strong>clotting factors</strong> are likely to be <strong>abnormal</strong> <strong>except factor VIII.</strong></p>\n<p>The liver is the site of production of the following factors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fibrinogen (factor I)</li>\n<li>Thrombin (factor II)</li>\n<li>Factor V</li>\n<li>Factor VII (shortest half-life, hence PT affected first)</li>\n<li>Factor IX</li>\n<li>Factor X</li>\n<li>Factor XI</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Factor VIII </strong>is produced in the <strong>endothelial cells</strong> and factor XIII A-subunit is produced in the bone marrow.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1977","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common disorder ‍of coagulation?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common disorder of coagulation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemoph‌ilia A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hemoph‌ilia B","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"von Willebr‌and disease","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hemorrhagic diathesis of l‌iver disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hemorrhagic diathesis of liver disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hemorrhagic diathesis of liver disease</strong></p><p><strong>Hemorrhagic diathesis of liver</strong> disease is the <strong>most common </strong>disorder of coagulation among the given options. It is the increased tendency of spontaneous bleeding associated with fulminant or end-stage liver disease. </p>\n<p><strong>Acquired deficiencies</strong> of plasma coagulation factors are<strong> more frequent</strong> than congenital disorders. The most common acquired disorders include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hemorrhagic diathesis of liver disease</li>\n<li>Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)</li>\n<li>Vitamin K deficiency.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hemophilia A</strong> is the <strong>most common inherited disorder</strong> of coagulation. Hemophilia A is more common than Hemophilia B.</p>\n<p>von Willebrand disease is the most common bleeding disorder.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4017","text":"Which of the following con‌ditions are matched corre‍ctly with their mode of i​nheritance?","raw_text":"Which of the following conditions are matched correctly with their mode of inheritance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Factor VII deficiency - X ‌linked recessive","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Factor VIII deficiency - a‌utosomal recessive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Factor IX deficiency - X l‌inked recessive","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Factor V deficiency - auto‌somal dominant","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Factor IX deficiency - X linked recessive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Factor IX deficiency - X linked recessive</strong></p><p><strong>Factor IX deficiency</strong> (hemophilia B) is inherited as an <strong>X-linked recessive trait</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hemophilia is an X-linked recessive hemorrhagic disease due to mutations in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Factor VIII gene</strong> (hemophilia A or classic hemophilia) -80% more common than hemophilia B </li>\n<li><strong>Factor IX gene</strong> (hemophilia B)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Clinically, hemophilia A and hemophilia B are<strong> indistinguishable</strong>.</p>\n<p>All clotting factor deficiencies except hemophilia are inherited as<strong> autosomal recessive</strong>.</p>\n<p>Inherited coagulation disorders treatment:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 433px;\" width=\"438\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.90625px;\"><strong>Blood components</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.96875px;\"><strong>Composition</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.140625px;\"><strong>Indications</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.90625px;\">Factor VIII concentrates</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.96875px;\">Factor VIII</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.140625px;\">Hemophilia A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.90625px;\">Factor IX concentrates</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.96875px;\">Factor IX</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.140625px;\">Hemophilia B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.90625px;\">Cryoprecipitate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.96875px;\">\n<p>Fibrinogen</p>\n<p>Factor XIII</p>\n<p>Factor VIII</p>\n<p>VWF</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.140625px;\">\n<p>Fibrinogen deficiency, factor XIII deficiency, von Willebrand disease</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 143.90625px;\">Fresh Frozen Plasma (FFP)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 137.96875px;\">\n<p>Coagulation factors</p>\n<p>Protein C</p>\n<p>Protein  S</p>\n<p>Antithrombin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.140625px;\">\n<p>Deficiency of prothrombin, factor V, factor VII, factor X, factor XI</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1975","text":"The most common bleeding m‌anifestation seen in seve‍re hemophilia is:","raw_text":"The most common bleeding manifestation seen in severe hemophilia is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Recurrent‌ hematomas","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Recurrent h‌emarthrosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hema‌turia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Intracrania‌l hemorrhage","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Recurrent hemarthrosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Recurrent hemarthrosis</strong></p><p><strong>Recurrent hemarthrosis</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> bleeding manifestation of <strong>severe</strong> hemophilia. Hemophilia is said to be severe when the residual activity of factor VIII is <strong>&lt;1%</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hemophilia is an <strong>X-linked recessive </strong>hemorrhagic disorder and is caused by mutations in either the <strong>F8 gene (hemophilia A</strong> or classic hemophilia) or the<strong> F9 gene (hemophilia B).</strong> Clinically, it is impossible to distinguish between hemophilia A and hemophilia B as they present with <strong>similar symptoms. </strong>It can be classified based on residual activity of <strong>FVIII </strong>or<strong> FIX</strong> as severe (&lt;1%), moderate (1–5%), or mild (6–30% ) </p>\n<p>Hemophilia presents early in life- <strong>bleeding after circumcision</strong> or when beginning to walk or crawl.</p>\n<p>Clinical features:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute hemarthrosis:\n<ul>\n<li>Local pain, swelling</li>\n<li>Erythema</li>\n<li>Muscle contractures due to fixed positioning.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Chronic hemarthrosis:\n<ul>\n<li>Synovial thickening</li>\n<li>Synovitis (due to intraarticular blood)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most common joint affected is the<strong> knee</strong>. One joint is affected more than the other. This is called the <strong>target joint</strong>. Muscle hematomas can lead to compartment syndromes.</p>\n<p>Hematuria is common but self-limited. Life-threatening bleeds such as bleeding into oropharyngeal spaces, CNS, and retroperitoneum can occur.</p>\n<p><strong>Treatment:</strong> Without treatment, severe hemophilia may limit life expectancy. <strong>Factor replacement</strong> for hemophilia has been the mainstay of therapy.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>FVIII dose (IU)</strong> = Target FVIII levels – FVIII baseline levels × body weight (kg) × 0.5 unit/kg  .</li>\n<li><strong>FIX dose (IU)</strong> = Target FIX levels – FIX baseline levels × body weight (kg) × 1 unit/kg </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The FVIII half-life of 8–12 h requires injections twice a day to maintain therapeutic levels, whereas the FIX half-life is longer, ~24 h so that once-a-day injection is sufficient. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4049","text":"A 5-year-old boy was broug‌ht with the following fin‍ding. His mother says tha​t he had banged his leg a‌gainst a chair yesterday.​ He has had similar episo‌des in the past but they ‌were never this severe. W‍hat lab values will you l​ikely find in this boy?","raw_text":"A 5-year-old boy was brought with the following finding. His mother says that he had banged his leg against a chair yesterday. He has had similar episodes in the past but they were never this severe. What lab values will you likely find in this boy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Normal bleeding time, prol‌onged PT, prolonged aPTT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Normal bleeding time, prol‌onged PT, normal aPTT","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Prolonged bleeding time, n‌ormal PT, prolonged aPTT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Normal bleeding time, norm‌al PT, prolonged aPTT","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Normal bleeding time, normal PT, prolonged aPTT","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6359e01906c647fc90ca09a466f82172.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Normal bleeding time, normal PT, prolonged aPTT</strong></p><p>In the above clinical scenario,<strong> recurrent hemarthrosis</strong> in a male child is suggestive of <strong>hemophilia</strong>. Normal bleeding time, normal PT, prolonged aPTT is seen in hemophilia A and B.</p>\n<p>Hemophilia A has <strong>decreased factor VIII</strong> activity and<strong> </strong>hemophilia B has <strong>decreased factor IX</strong> activity.</p>\n<p>Note: Prolonged bleeding time, normal PT, prolonged aPTT is seen in <strong>von Willebrand disease.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4026","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about acquired hemo‍philia A?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about acquired hemophilia A?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Autoantibodies are formed ‌against factor VIII","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Bethesda assay confirms th‌e diagnosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"It presents in childhood o‌r early adulthood","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"It may be associated with ‌lymphoproliferative malig‍nancies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. It presents in childhood or early adulthood","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It presents in childhood or early adulthood</strong></p><p><strong>Acquired hemophilia A</strong> occurs predominantly in<strong> older adults</strong> (median age- 60 years).</p>\n<p>It occasionally occurs in pregnant or postpartum women.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>acquired immune-mediated disease</strong> characterized by <strong>autoantibodies</strong> against <strong>clotting factor VIII</strong>. It is associated with an underlying disease in 50% of patients. They include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Malignancies</strong> - lymphomas, prostate cancer</li>\n<li><strong>Dermatologic diseases</strong> - pemphigoid</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4024","text":"In a patient with hemophil‌ia A, how much can infusi‍on of one unit of factor ​VIII/kg increase the plas‌ma levels of factor VIII?​","raw_text":"In a patient with hemophilia A, how much can infusion of one unit of factor VIII/kg increase the plasma levels of factor VIII?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1‌0%","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2‌%","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 2%","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 2%</strong></p><p><strong>One unit of factor VIII/kg</strong> body weight<strong> increases</strong> the plasma levels of factor VIII levels by <strong>2%</strong>. Therefore, for a rise in 1 % in the plasma, half unit/ kg will be required, that is 0.5 units/kg.</p>\n<p>It is used in the treatment of hemophilia. One unit of FVIII is the amount of FVIII in 1 mL of plasma.</p>\n<p>The dose required for replacement is calculated as :</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Factor VIII dose</strong> = (Target factor VIII levels - baseline factor VIII levels) x bodyweight x 0.5 units/ kg</li>\n<li><strong>Factor IX </strong>(IU) = (Target FIX levels – FIX baseline levels) x bodyweight (kg) x 1 unit/kg</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cryoprecipitate is used when factor concentrates are not available. Each bag of cryoprecipitate contains 80 IU of Factor VIII.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 88px;\" width=\"439\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 212.315px;\"><strong>Blood components</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 212.315px;\"><strong>Composition</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 212.315px;\"><strong>Indications</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 212.315px;\">Factor VIII concentrates</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212.315px;\">Factor VIII</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212.315px;\">Hemophilia A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 212.315px;\">Factor IX concentrates</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212.315px;\">Factor IX</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212.315px;\">Hemophilia B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 212.315px;\">Cryoprecipitate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212.315px;\">\n<p>Fibrinogen</p>\n<p>Factor XIII</p>\n<p>Factor VIII</p>\n<p>VWF</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212.315px;\">\n<p>Fibrinogen deficiency, Factor XIII deficiency, von Willebrand disease</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 212.315px;\">Fresh frozen plasma (FFP)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212.315px;\">\n<p>Coagulation factors</p>\n<p>Protein C</p>\n<p>Protein  S</p>\n<p>Antithrombin</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212.315px;\">\n<p>Deficiency of prothrombin, factor V, factor VII, factor X, factor XI</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 212.315px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 212.315px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 212.315px;\"> </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4045","text":"What effect does desmopres‌sin have when given to pa‍tients with coagulation d​isorders?","raw_text":"What effect does desmopressin have when given to patients with coagulation disorders?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increases ‌factor VIII","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Increases‌ factor IX","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increases‌ factor XI","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Increases ‌factor VII","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Increases factor VIII","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increases factor VIII</strong></p><p><strong>Desmopressin</strong> causes a <strong>transient rise</strong> in<strong> factor VIII</strong> and <strong>von Willebrand factor</strong> (VWF) hence it is given in mild and moderate hemophilia A.</p>\n<p>It does not improve factor VIII levels in severe hemophilia A patients because there are no stores to release. Repeated dosing leads to <strong>tachyphylaxis</strong>.</p>\n<p>It does not increase factor IX levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1978","text":"A teenager with hemophilia‌ A presented with multipl‍e spontaneous bleeds. He ​has been receiving prophy‌lactic factor VIII infusi​on on a regular basis. In‌vestigations revealed low‌ factor VIII levels and a‍bnormal plasma mixing stu​dy. Which of the followin​g can be used to confirm ​the diagnosis?","raw_text":"A teenager with hemophilia A presented with multiple spontaneous bleeds. He has been receiving prophylactic factor VIII infusion on a regular basis. Investigations revealed low factor VIII levels and abnormal plasma mixing study. Which of the following can be used to confirm the diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1,3 beta D ‌glucan assay","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Platelet act‌ivation assay","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Serotonin r‌elease assay","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bethesd‌a assay","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Bethesda assay","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Bethesda assay</strong></p><p> <strong>Bethesda assay</strong> is the best test for the <strong>confirmation</strong> of <strong>factor VIII inhibitors</strong>. </p>\n<p>The formation of<strong> inhibitor/ alloantibodies</strong> to FVIII or FIX is the <strong>major complication </strong>of hemophilia treatment. When this happens, patients do not respond to factor replacement at therapeutic doses.</p>\n<p><strong>aPTT measurement</strong> is done on a <strong>mixed sample</strong>. It is used to <strong>determine</strong> the <strong>presence </strong>of <strong>factor VIII inhibitors. </strong>aPTT is estimated using a 1:1 mix of patient's plasma and normal plasma. It is interpreted as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In <strong>hemophilia,</strong> aPTT is <strong>corrected</strong> with a 1:1 mix. </li>\n<li>In patients with <strong>inhibitors</strong>, aPTT with 1:1 mix is <strong>abnormally prolonged</strong> (inhibitor neutralizes the normal factors).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Bethesda assay </strong>is based on the same principle but is superior because it helps to<strong> quantify</strong> the type and specificity of the inhibitor.</p>\n<p>Option A: 1,3 beta D glucan assay is designed to diagnose<strong> invasive fungal infections</strong> (IFIs).</p>\n<p>Options B and C: It is used to diagnose heparin-induced thrombocytopenia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC5306","text":"Which among the following ‌is wrong about DIC?","raw_text":"Which among the following is wrong about DIC?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased s‌chistocytes","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Increa‌sed PT","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Increased ‌fibrinogen","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Increas‌ed FDPs","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Increased fibrinogen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Increased fibrinogen</strong></p><p>In disseminated intravascular coagulopathy (DIC), <strong>fibrinogen levels</strong> are<strong> decreased</strong> due to increased bleeding. </p>\n<p>Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) is an <strong>acquired</strong> syndrome characterized by the <strong>widespread activation</strong> of blood clotting factors within blood vessels, resulting in a loss of clotting control. The primary mechanism of DIC involves the uncontrolled generation of <strong>thrombin</strong> through multiple pathways which leads to sustained <strong>coagulation activation</strong>, the formation of <strong>fibrin</strong>, and the consumption of clotting factors and platelets.</p>\n<p><strong>Infections</strong> are the most frequent cause of DIC.</p>\n<p><strong>Laboratory investigations in DIC:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Decreased platelet count </li>\n<li>Prolonged PT/TT</li>\n<li>Prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT)</li>\n<li>Decreased fibrinogen</li>\n<li>Elevated fibrin degradation products (D-dimers)</li>\n<li>Peripheral smear: microangiopathic hemolytic anemia in DIC with schistocytes (fragmented red blood cells) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The treatment involves <strong>platelet</strong> <strong>transfusion</strong> when platelet counts are <strong>below 10,000-20,000/</strong>μL. Replacement of fibrinogen and coagulation factors is with<strong> fresh frozen plasma (FFP) </strong>along with <strong>cryoprecipitate</strong> or <strong>fibrinogen</strong> <strong>concentrate</strong>.</p>\n<p>In cases of low-grade DIC associated with solid tumors or acute promyelocytic leukemia <strong>low</strong> doses of <strong>continuous-infusion heparin</strong> has been effective.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4040","text":"Which of the following are‌ risk factors that can ca‍use disseminated intravas​cular coagulation in pati‌ents? Gram-positive sepsi​s Chronic pancreatitis Ad‌enocarcinoma of prostate ‌Snakebite Abruptio placen‍tae","raw_text":"<p>Which of the following are risk factors that can cause disseminated intravascular coagulation in patients?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Gram-positive sepsis</li>\n<li>Chronic pancreatitis</li>\n<li>Adenocarcinoma of prostate</li>\n<li>Snakebite</li>\n<li>Abruptio placentae</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1‌,4","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1,2‌,4,5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2‌,5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1,3‌,4,5","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1,3,4,5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1,3,4,5</strong></p><p>Acute pancreatitis can cause disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) not chronic pancreatitis.</p>\n<p><strong>Common causes of DIC:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sepsis</strong>- gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria, viral, mycotic, parasitic, rickettsial</li>\n<li>Acute pancreatitis</li>\n<li>Trauma, burns</li>\n<li>Malignancies- acute promyelocytic leukemia, <strong>adenocarcinoma</strong> of pancreas and prostate</li>\n<li>Obstetric- <strong>abruptio placentae</strong>, amniotic fluid embolism, and septic abortion</li>\n<li><strong>Snakebite</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4032","text":"A 33-year-old woman with a‌ twin pregnancy was admit‍ted for normal delivery. ​After medical induction o‌f labor, she started to b​leed profusely from her r‌ectum, vagina, and IV sit‌es. She became hypotensiv‍e, tachycardic, and pulse​s were feeble. Which of t​he following laboratory f​indings will you see in h​er case?","raw_text":"A 33-year-old woman with a twin pregnancy was admitted for normal delivery. After medical induction of labor, she started to bleed profusely from her rectum, vagina, and IV sites. She became hypotensive, tachycardic, and pulses were feeble. Which of the following laboratory findings will you see in her case?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opti‌on 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Opti‌on 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Opti‌on 1","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Opti‌on 4","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Option 4","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/78f95b6a8a4540cea635d79c411de126.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/264aef213a1d4e52a22f049b24fc9f8dx1280x1953.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Option 4</strong></p><p>In the above clinical scenario, the patient seems to have <strong>disseminated intravascular coagulation </strong>(DIC) due to <strong>amniotic fluid embolism</strong> following medical induction of labor. </p>\n<p>Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) is a condition characterized by widespread <strong>intravascular fibrin formation</strong>. The central mechanism is the <strong>uncontrolled</strong> <strong>generation</strong> of <strong>thrombin</strong> by exposure of blood to pathologic levels of tissue factor.</p>\n<p>Lab abnormalities in DIC:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Low platelet count</strong></li>\n<li>Low fibrinogen</li>\n<li>Prolonged PT and aPTT</li>\n<li><strong>Increased D-dimer</strong></li>\n<li>Increased fibrin-degradation products</li>\n<li>Decreased protein C</li>\n<li><strong>Decreased antithrombin III</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Increased thrombin-antithrombin complex</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4037","text":"The most sensitive test fo‌r disseminated intravascu‍lar coagulation is _____.​","raw_text":"The most sensitive test for disseminated intravascular coagulation is _____.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fibrin degradation product‌s level","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"PTT and th‌rombin time","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"D-dime‌r assay","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Platelet count and fibrino‌gen levels","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Fibrin degradation products level","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Fibrin degradation products level</strong></p><p>The <strong>most sensitive</strong> test for disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) is <strong>FDP</strong> (fibrinogen degradation products) level.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 322px;\" width=\"882\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">Diagnostic test</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">Diagnostic efficiency</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">Sensitivity</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">Specificity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">FDP and D-dimer combination</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">95%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">91%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">94%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\"><strong>FDP</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">87%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\"><strong>100%</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">67%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">PT/PTT and FDP  combination</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">86%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">91%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">71%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">D-dimer</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">80%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">91%</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\">68% </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">PT, PTT, thrombin time, platelet count, fibrinogen, and the presence of schistocytes</td>\n<td style=\"width: 213px;\">57%, 57%, 70%, 67%, 65%, and 51%, respectively</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\"> low</td>\n<td style=\"width: 214px;\"> low</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4042","text":"A 3-year-old child is brou‌ght to the emergency with‍ petechiae, bruising, and​ abdominal distension. Ex‌amination revealed an abd​ominal mass. Investigatio‌ns revealed schistocytes ‌and thrombocytopenia. Wha‍t is the most probable ca​use of the mass?","raw_text":"A 3-year-old child is brought to the emergency with petechiae, bruising, and abdominal distension. Examination revealed an abdominal mass.  Investigations revealed schistocytes and thrombocytopenia. What is the most probable cause of the mass?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hepatob‌lastoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wilm's‌ tumor","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hepatic h‌emangioma","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hepatic hemangioma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hepatic hemangioma</strong></p><p>This patient presents with <strong>petechiae,</strong> and abdominal distension with schistocytes and thrombocytopenia. This in child most likely has <strong>Kasabach-Merritt syndrome</strong> (secondary DIC). His abdominal mass is likely to be a <strong>hepatic hemangioma.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Kasabach-Merritt syndrome</strong> is a life-threatening complication that occurs in patients with <strong>giant hemangiomas</strong>. It is most commonly seen in<strong> infants</strong>. </p>\n<p>Clinical features: </p>\n<ul class=\"i8Z77e\">\n<li class=\"TrT0Xe\">Enlarged abdomen</li>\n<li class=\"TrT0Xe\">Hepatomegaly or jaundice</li>\n<li class=\"TrT0Xe\">Petechiae, bruising, and frank bleeding</li>\n<li class=\"TrT0Xe\">Painful lesions</li>\n<li class=\"TrT0Xe\">Anemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is characterized by profound <strong>thrombocytopenia and consumptive coagulopathy</strong> leading to a DIC-like picture.</p>\n<p>Other options: </p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Neuroblastomas</strong> are tumors of the sympathetic ganglia and adrenal medulla that are derived from primordial neural crest cells. It is the most common extracranial solid tumor of childhood and the most frequently diagnosed tumor in infancy. Brain tumors are the most common solid tumors in childhood. They present as a <strong>flank mass</strong> that crosses the <strong>midline.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Hepatoblastoma</strong> is the most common childhood liver tumor. Histologically it is composed of small polygonal embryonal cells forming trabeculae, acini, or papillary structures in the developing liver.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Wilms</strong> tumor is the most common primary malignant renal tumor in children and is the second most common malignant abdominal tumor in children. The clinical presentation usually involves an asymptomatic <strong>unilateral abdominal mass,</strong> hypertension, abdominal pain, and gross painless hematuria. Constitutional symptoms of fever, anorexia, and weight loss can also be present at the time of diagnosis. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1754","text":"Patients with protein C de‌ficiency are at an increa‍sed risk of developing al​l of the following except‌:","raw_text":"Patients with protein C deficiency are at an increased risk of developing all of the following except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deep vein ‌thrombosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Purpura ‌fulminans","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Warfarin-induced skin necr‌osis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Spontaneou‌s bleeding","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Spontaneous bleeding","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6ad8b3fbe45c4161a1887693df8a08aex416x501.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Spontaneous bleeding</strong></p><p>Spontaneous bleeding is not seen in protein C deficiency. It is characterised by an <strong>increased </strong>tendency for <strong>thrombus formation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Protein C deficiency</strong> is a rare genetic disorder. It predisposes to the following thrombotic complications:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Deep vein thrombosis </strong>(DVT):\n<ul>\n<li>Adults with a mild form of the disease at higher risk of developing venous thromboembolism.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Purpura fulminans</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Severe form of <strong>DIC</strong></li>\n<li>Thrombosis of extensive areas of the skin</li>\n<li>Affects predominantly young children following viral or bacterial infection. </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Warfarin-induced skin necrosis:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Rare complication seen in patients with congenital or acquired deficiencies of protein C or S.</li>\n<li>Thrombosis of the skin leads to well-demarcated erythematous lesions with central necrosis on the thighs, buttocks, breasts, or toes(shown below).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1985","text":"A patient is on prophylact‌ic unfractionated heparin‍ for deep vein thrombosis​. Which of the following ‌tests can be used to moni​tor its anticoagulant eff‌ect?","raw_text":"A patient is on prophylactic unfractionated heparin for deep vein thrombosis. Which of the following tests can be used to monitor its anticoagulant effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prothrom‌bin time","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Anti-Facto‌r Xa level","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"PT and Anti-Factor Xa leve‌l","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No monitorin‌g is required","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Anti-Factor Xa level","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Anti-Factor Xa level</strong></p><p>The <strong>anticoagulant effect </strong>of heparin can be monitored using <strong>anti-factor Xa level</strong> and activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT). aPTT is prolonged as heparin inhibits intrinsic pathway factors.</p>\n<p><strong>Prothrombin time</strong> and <strong>INR</strong> are used to monitor the anticoagulant effect of <strong>warfarin </strong>(inhibits vitamin K dependent factor II, VII, IX, X. Factor VII has the shortest half-life).</p>\n<p>No monitoring is required with the use of low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) and fondaparinux.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4041","text":"Which of the following is ‌false about low molecular‍ weight heparin?","raw_text":"Which of the following is false about low molecular weight heparin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Higher risk of of bleeding‌ than heparin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"No laboratory monitoring i‌s required","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lower risk of heparin indu‌ced thrombocytopenia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Protamine sulphate is only‌ partially effective agai‍nst low molecular weight ​heparin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Higher risk of of bleeding than heparin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Higher risk of of bleeding than heparin</strong></p><p>Low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) has a <strong>lower risk of bleeding</strong> than heparin.</p>\n<p>In contrast<strong> </strong>to heparin which requires monitoring by aPTT and anti-factorXa, <strong>laboratory monitoring</strong> is <strong>not required</strong> for LMWH. It is also associated with<strong> lower risk</strong> of <strong>heparin induced thrombocytopenia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Examples of LMWH:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dalteparin</li>\n<li>Enoxaparin</li>\n<li>Tinzaparin</li>\n<li>Nadroparin</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Protamine sulphate</strong> is the only clinically approved <strong>antidote</strong> for unfractionated heparin. Protamine sulphate is only partially effective against low molecular weight heparin.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1988","text":"Which of the following is ‌a direct thrombin inhibit‍or?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a direct thrombin inhibitor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apix‌aban","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abci‌ximab","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Argat‌roban","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Edox‌aban","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Argatroban","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Argatroban</strong></p><p><strong>Argatroban</strong> is a parenteral <strong>direct thrombin inhibitor</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Apixaban</strong> and edoxaban are a novel oral anticoagulant (NOAC) that inhibits<strong> factor Xa. </strong>Abciximab is an antiplatelet drug that inhibits <strong>Gp IIb-IIIa receptor</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4021","text":"A patient presented with b‌leeding gums and skin dis‍coloration. She was given​ heparin for DVT prophyla‌xis 5 days ago. Investiga​tions showed thrombocytop‌enia. Which of the follow‌ing is the most specific ‍test to diagnose this con​dition?","raw_text":"A patient presented with bleeding gums and skin discoloration. She was given heparin for DVT prophylaxis 5 days ago. Investigations showed thrombocytopenia. Which of the following is the most specific test to diagnose this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4T sc‌oring","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Platelet act‌ivation assay","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Heparin-PF-4 complex antib‌ody assay","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Serotonin r‌elease assay","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Serotonin release assay","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Serotonin release assay</strong></p><p><strong>Serotonin release assay</strong> is the <strong>most specific</strong> test for the diagnosis of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (<strong>HIT</strong>).</p>\n<p>HIT is commonly diagnosed clinically by means of <strong>4T scoring </strong>(<strong>T</strong>hrombocytopenia<strong>, T</strong>iming of platelet count fall, <strong>T</strong>hrombosis, Other causes for<strong> T</strong>hrombocytopenia).</p>\n<p>Serotonin release assay is performed by quantifying serotonin release.</p>\n<div data-canvas-width=\"342.576\">Platelets loaded with labeled serotonin are exposed to patient serum in the absence or presence of varying concentrations of heparin. If the patient serum contains the<strong> HIT antibody</strong>, heparin addition induces <strong>platelet activation</strong> and <strong>serotonin release</strong>. </div>\n<p>Enzyme-linked assays such as platelet activation assay and heparin-PF-4 complex antibody assay are sensitive but not specific for HIT.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1711","text":"A 69-year-old male patient‌ with chronic kidney dise‍ase presented with bleedi​ng gums and skin rash. He‌ had undergone dialysis 1​ week ago. Further invest‌igations revealed heparin‌-induced thrombocytopenia‍ to be the cause. Which o​f the following drugs can​ be given to him?","raw_text":"A 69-year-old male patient with chronic kidney disease presented with bleeding gums and skin rash. He had undergone dialysis 1 week ago. Further investigations revealed heparin-induced thrombocytopenia to be the cause. Which of the following drugs can be given to him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lepi‌rudin","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Argat‌roban","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bival‌irudin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Fondap‌arinux","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Argatroban","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Argatroban</strong></p><p>In the above clinical scenario, the patient requires <strong>direct thrombin inhibitors</strong> for the treatment of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia. <strong>Argatroban</strong> is<strong> safe</strong> in <strong>renal disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>The<strong> parenteral direct thrombin inhibitors</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Argatroban</strong> - hepatic clearance, approved for the treatment of HIT</li>\n<li><strong>Lepirudin</strong> - renal clearance, contraindicated in renal disease</li>\n<li><strong>Bivalirudin</strong>- renal clearance, contraindicated in renal disease, shortest half-life</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Fondaparinux is a factor Xa inhibitor. It is contraindicated in renal disease because it has renal clearance.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1724","text":"A patient on warfarin deve‌lops severe gastrointesti‍nal bleeding. What is the​ treatment of choice?","raw_text":"A patient on warfarin develops severe gastrointestinal bleeding. What is the treatment of choice?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitamin K slow IV infusion‌","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Fresh fro‌zen plasma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Vit K ‌and FFP","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Discontinuation of warfari‌n only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Vit K and FFP","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Vit K and FFP</strong></p><p>Patients on warfarin who present with <strong>severe bleeding</strong> are treated with both<strong> IV infusion of vitamin K</strong> and <strong>fresh frozen plasma</strong> regardless of the INR.</p>\n<p>Warfarin is an oral anticoagulant that blocks the enzyme <strong>vitamin K epoxide reductase</strong>. This inhibits factors <strong>II, VII, IX, and X, </strong><strong>proteins C </strong>and<strong> S. </strong>This can cause an initial procoagulant state.</p>\n<p>Since the anticoagulant action of <strong>warfarin</strong> takes<strong> 2-3 days,</strong> patients require concomitant treatment (bridging therapy) with a rapidly acting parenteral anticoagulant (heparin, LMWH or fondaparinux) for at least 5 days.</p>\n<p>Warfarin has a narrow therapeutic window, hence all patients on warfarin should have their <strong>INR determined</strong> every <strong>3–4 weeks</strong>. The most common side effect of warfarin is bleeding.</p>\n<p>Severe or life-threatening bleed includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hematuria</li>\n<li>Gastrointestinal bleeding</li>\n<li>Intracranial hemorrhage</li>\n<li>Intramuscular or intra-abdominal bleed</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Management of supratherapeutic INR:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>INR</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Bleeding </strong></td>\n<td><strong>Management</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3.5-10</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Hold warfarin until INR is therapeutic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&gt;10</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Oral vitamin K 2.5-5 mg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Any</td>\n<td>Serious or life-threatening</td>\n<td>10 mg vitamin K slow IV infusion + fresh frozen plasma/prothrombin complex concentrate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1986","text":"Which of the following is ‌not a direct oral anticoa‍gulant ?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not a direct oral anticoagulant ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dabig‌atran","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Danap‌aroid","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Rivor‌axaban","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Edox‌aban","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Danaparoid","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Danaparoid</strong></p><p>Danaparoid is not a direct oral anticoagulant (DOAC). It is a<strong> heparinoid</strong> administered via IV or SC routes.</p>\n<p><strong>DOACs</strong> are alternatives to warfarin, easier to administer with fixed doses, and <strong>no requirement for monitoring</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\">Direct oral anticoagulants (DOAC)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thrombin inhibitors (dabigatran)</td>\n<td>Factor <strong>Xa</strong> inhibitors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>No CYP2C9 polymorphisms</li>\n<li>A standard dose of 150 mg BD</li>\n<li>No PT/INR monitoring</li>\n<li>Dialyzable</li>\n<li>Mild GI bleeding</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Rivaroxaban</li>\n<li>Apixaban</li>\n<li>Edoxaban</li>\n<li>Betrixaban </li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>All DOACs are contraindicated in patients with <strong>metallic/prosthetic heart valves</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1753","text":"In patients with which of ‌the following is dabigatr‍an approved for use?","raw_text":"In patients with which of the following is dabigatran approved for use?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elective hip arthroplasty ‌requiring thromboprophyla‍xis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Total knee arthroplasty re‌quiring thromboprophylaxi‍s","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metallic heart valve requi‌ring stroke prophylaxis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non-valvular atrial fibril‌lation requiring stroke p‍rophylaxis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Non-valvular atrial fibrillation requiring stroke prophylaxis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Non-valvular atrial fibrillation requiring stroke prophylaxis</strong></p><p>Stroke prophylaxis in<strong> non-valvular atrial fibrillation</strong> is the <strong>only indication</strong> for which all <strong>DOAC</strong>s are approved. All DOACs are contraindicated in patients with <strong>metallic/prosthetic heart valves</strong>.</p>\n<p>Novel oral anticoagulants:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Thrombin inhibitors </td>\n<td>Factor Xa inhibitors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dabigatran (oral) </strong></li>\n<li>Argatroban</li>\n<li>Lepirudin</li>\n<li>Desirudin</li>\n<li>Bivalirudin</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n<td>\n<ul>\n<li>Rivaroxaban</li>\n<li>Apixaban</li>\n<li>Edoxaban</li>\n<li>Betrixaban</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Dabigatran is a direct oral anticoagulant (DOAC). DOACs are alternatives to warfarin, easier to administer with fixed doses, and no requirement for monitoring.</p>\n<p>Only<strong> rivaroxaban</strong> and<strong> apixaban</strong> are licensed for use as thromboprophylaxis after <strong>elective hip</strong> or <strong>knee arthroplasty</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"RTRfdheI","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"97_haemoglobinopathies","title":"97.Haemoglobinopathies","num_questions":9,"total_marks":36,"duration":19,"time_per_question":126.67,"questions":[{"id":"MB4001","text":"Which of the following is ​true about hemoglobin?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about hemoglobin?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemoglobin tetramer is hig​hly soluble.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Individual globin chains a​re soluble.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Each heme moiety can bind ​to two O2 molecules.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Each heme moiety consists ​of Iron in the ferric sta​te.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Hemoglobin tetramer is highly soluble.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hemoglobin tetramer is highly soluble.</strong></p><p>The <strong>hemoglobin</strong> <strong>tetramer</strong> is highly <strong>soluble </strong>in the plasma. However, individual globin chains are insoluble. Unpaired globin precipitates can form inclusions that damage the cell and trigger apoptosis.</p>\n<p>Each hemoglobin molecule consists of a <strong>tetramer </strong>of<strong> globin</strong> polypeptide chains. This includes a pair of α-like chains and a pair of β-like chains. Each globin chain enfolds a single heme moiety. Heme consists of a protoporphyrin IX ring complexed with a single iron atom in the<strong> ferrous</strong> state (Fe<sup>2+</sup>). Each<strong> heme</strong> moiety can bind one<strong> O<sub>2</sub> molecule</strong>.</p>\n<p>The common forms of hemoglobin include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HbA </strong>(α2β2): Major adult hemoglobin</li>\n<li><strong>HbF </strong>(α2γ2): Fetal hemoglobin</li>\n<li><strong>HbA2 </strong>(α2δ2): Minor adult hemoglobin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4004","text":"A patient with alpha thala​ssemia is susceptible to ​which of the following in​fections?","raw_text":"A patient with alpha thalassemia is susceptible to which of the following infections?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Plasmodi​um vivax","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Plasmodium​ falciparum","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Plasmodi​um ovale","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Plasmodiu​m malaria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Plasmodium vivax","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Plasmodium vivax</strong></p><p>Patients with <strong>α-thalassemia</strong> are <strong>susceptible</strong> to infection with <strong><em>Plasmodium vivax</em>.</strong></p>\n<p>Thalassemia provides natural <strong>protection</strong> against infection with the more lethal<em><strong> Plasmodium falciparum</strong></em>. There is no conclusive evidence on the relationship between alpha thalassemia and <em>P. malariae</em> or <em>P. ovale</em> infection. </p>\n<p>Conditions <strong>protective </strong>against<em><strong> Plasmodium falciparum</strong></em> include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Sickle cell trait</li>\n<li>Hemoglobin C </li>\n<li>Hemoglobin SC</li>\n<li>Hemoglobin E</li>\n<li>Hereditary persistence of fetal hemoglobin (Hb F)</li>\n<li>Alpha and beta-thalassemia</li>\n<li>Band 3 mutation</li>\n<li>G6PD deficiency </li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4005","text":"The peripheral smear of a ​patient is given below. T​his abnormality is due to​ which of the following p​oint mutations?","raw_text":"The peripheral smear of a patient is given below. This abnormality is due to which of the following point mutations?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5th glutamic acid is repla​ced by valine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5th valine is replaced by ​glutamic acid","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"6th glutamic acid is repla​ced by valine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"6th valine is replaced by ​glutamic acid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 6th glutamic acid is replaced by valine","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8070c1a8e6c841ad83d283ba9b176142.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 6th glutamic acid is replaced by valine</strong></p><p>The given image shows sickled RBCs, suggestive of <strong>sickle cell anemia</strong>. It occurs due to the replacement of the <strong>6th glutamic acid by valine</strong> in the beta-globin gene on <strong>chromosome 11</strong>. </p>\n<p>This mutation is due to a single nucleotide substitution (from GAG to GTG). The resulting amino acid substitution causes hydrophobic sickling of RBCs.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC3189","text":"A 10-year-old boy complain​s of multiple episodes of​ acute severe pain in his​ fingers and toes which r​esolves on its own for th‍e past 1 year. Which of t‍he following conditions i​s he most likely sufferin‍g from?","raw_text":"A 10-year-old boy complains of multiple episodes of acute severe pain in his fingers and toes which resolves on its own for the past 1 year. Which of the following conditions is he most likely suffering from?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alpha-th​alassemia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Beta-tha​lassemia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Sickle ce​ll anemia","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Von Willebrand disease typ​e 1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Sickle cell anemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Sickle cell anemia</strong></p><p>The features of multiple <strong>episodes</strong> of <strong>acute severe pain</strong> in <strong>fingers and toes</strong> with <strong>spontaneous resolution</strong> are suggestive of <strong>dactylitis</strong> (hand-foot syndrome), which usually occurs in <strong>sickle cell anemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>sickled RBCs</strong> have a sticking tendency towards the <strong>endothelium</strong> of the <strong>small venules</strong>. These abnormalities provoke unpredictable <strong>episodes</strong> <strong>of</strong> <strong>vaso-occlusion</strong>. <strong>Dactylitis</strong> is characterized by <strong>severe pain</strong> affecting the <strong>bones</strong> of the <strong>hands</strong>, <strong>feet</strong>, or both. It is often the <strong>first symptom</strong> of sickle cell anemia in children.</p>\n<p>Treatment - Administration of <strong>patient-controlled analgesia</strong> or a frequent <strong>fixed dose</strong> of <strong>opioids</strong> with <strong>rescue doses</strong> for breakthrough pain are preferred with frequent monitoring during the initial 48-72 hrs to prevent unexpected death due to arrhythmias or pulmonary embolism.</p>\n<p>Other manifestations include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Splenic autoinfarction</strong> - repeated splenic microinfarcts </li>\n<li><strong>Acute splenic sequestration</strong> - venous obstruction, needing emergency transfusion/splenectomy</li>\n<li><strong>Painful ischemic crises</strong> - vaso-occlusion in connective and musculoskeletal tissue</li>\n<li>Renal papillary necrosis leading to <strong>isosthenuria</strong></li>\n<li>Cerebrovascular <strong>strokes</strong></li>\n<li>Chronic lower <strong>leg ulcers</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Priapism</strong> in males</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>α-thalassemia </strong>(Option A) and <strong>β-thalassemia </strong>(Option B) are characterized by <strong>pallor</strong>, <strong>jaundice</strong>, growth failure, <strong>hepatosplenomegaly</strong>, characteristic <strong>thalassemic facies</strong>, and <strong>hair-on-end appearance</strong> of the <strong>skull</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Von Willebrand Disease</strong> is characterized by predominantly <strong>mucosal bleeding</strong> symptoms like excessive bruising and epistaxis.    </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4007","text":"What is the most common ca​use of osteomyelitis in p​atients with sickle cell ​disease?","raw_text":"What is the most common cause of osteomyelitis in patients with sickle cell disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Staphyloco​ccus aureus","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Strept​ococcus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Staphylococcus epidermidis​","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Salmo​nella","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Salmonella","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Salmonella</strong></p><p><em><strong>Salmonella</strong></em> is the most common organism causing <strong>osteomyelitis</strong> in <strong>sickle cell disease</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> is the most common organism causing osteomyelitis in the general population.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4010","text":"What will be seen on perfo​rming hemoglobin electrop​horesis in a patient with​ suspected sickle cell an​emia?","raw_text":"What will be seen on performing hemoglobin electrophoresis in a patient with suspected sickle cell anemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HbS a​nd HbA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HbS a​nd HbF","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"HbF a​nd HbA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"HbA2 a​nd HbF","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. HbS and HbF","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. HbS and HbF</strong></p><p><strong>HbS</strong> and <strong>HbF</strong> are detected on electrophoresis in patients with <strong>sickle cell anemia</strong>. HbA and HbA2 are typically absent.</p>\n<table style=\"width: 504px; height: 248px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87.2396px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Condition</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.934px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Clinical abnormalities</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.139px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Hemoglobin level (g/dl)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.097px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Hemoglobin</strong><br/><strong>Electrophoresis</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87.2396px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Sickle cell trait</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.934px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Rare painless hematuria may be asymptomatic</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.139px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Normal</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.097px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Hb S/A:40/60</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 87.2396px; text-align: center;\">\n<p><strong>Sickle cell anemia</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 157.934px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>Vasoocclusive crises with infarction of spleen, brain, marrow, kidney, lung; aseptic necrosis of bone; gallstones; priapism; ankle ulcers</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.139px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>7–10</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 134.097px; text-align: center;\">\n<p>HbS/HbA: 100/0</p>\n<p>HbF: 2–25%</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4011","text":"Which of the following doe​s not increase HbF levels​?","raw_text":"Which of the following does not increase HbF levels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydro​xyurea","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"5-Azac​ytidine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Decit​abine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Gemci​tabine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Gemcitabine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gemcitabine</strong></p><p><strong>Gemcitabine</strong> is an anti-metabolite and has <strong>no effect</strong> on <strong>HbF</strong> levels.</p>\n<p>The following drugs<strong> increase HbF</strong> levels and hence can be used in the management of <strong>sickle cell anemia</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hydroxyurea</strong> - mainstay of therapy for patients with severe symptoms</li>\n<li><strong>5-azacytidine</strong> - associated with acute toxicity and carcinogenesis</li>\n<li>5-deoxyazacytidine or <strong>decitabine</strong> - alternative with less toxicity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4013","text":"Which of the following abn​ormal hemoglobins would b​e present in patients wit​h congenital methemoglobi​nemia?","raw_text":"Which of the following abnormal hemoglobins would be present in patients with congenital methemoglobinemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H​bE","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"H​bC","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hb Y​akima","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hb M ​Iwata","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Hb M Iwata","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/931ecc82e4b14ec885b2a3fb57ccdd9dx358x297.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hb M Iwata</strong></p><p><strong>Hb M Iwata</strong> causes <strong>congenital methemoglobinemia</strong>.</p>\n<p>Methemoglobin is generated by oxidation of the heme iron moieties to the <strong>ferric state</strong>, causing a characteristic <strong>bluish-brown</strong> muddy color resembling cyanosis. Methemoglobin has such<strong> high oxygen affinity</strong> that virtually no oxygen is delivered. Levels &gt;50–60% are often fatal.</p>\n<p><strong>Congenital </strong>methemoglobinemia arises from globin mutations that stabilize iron in the ferric state (<strong>HbM Iwata</strong>)<strong> </strong>or mutations that impair the enzymes that reduce methemoglobin to hemoglobin (methemoglobin reductase, NADP diaphorase).</p>\n<p><strong>Acquired </strong>methemoglobinemia is caused by toxins that oxidize heme iron such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chloroquine</li>\n<li>Dapsone</li>\n<li>Sulfonamides</li>\n<li>Nitroglycerin</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The following image shows chocolate-brown discoloration of blood in methemoglobinemia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB4014","text":"Which of the following wou​ld you not expect in a pa​tient with sickle cell an​emia?","raw_text":"Which of the following would you not expect in a patient with sickle cell anemia?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reticul​ocytosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Howell jo​lly bodies","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Granulo​cytosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Thromboc​ytopenia","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Thrombocytopenia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Thrombocytopenia</strong></p><p><strong>Thrombocytosis</strong> is seen in <strong>sickle cell anemia</strong>, not thrombocytopenia. Laboratory findings of sickle cell anemia include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Peripheral blood smear:\n<ul>\n<li>Irreversibly sickled cells (5–50% of red cells)</li>\n<li><strong>Reticulocytosis</strong> (10–25%)</li>\n<li>Nucleated red blood cells</li>\n<li><strong>Howell–Jolly</strong> bodies and target cells (due to hyposplenism)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Granulocytosis</strong> - WBC count of 12,000–15,000/mcL</li>\n<li>Reactive thrombocytosis </li>\n<li>High indirect bilirubin levels </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"caAozatC","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"98_blood_bank___transfusion_medicine","title":"98.Blood Bank & Transfusion Medicine","num_questions":16,"total_marks":64,"duration":26,"time_per_question":97.5,"questions":[{"id":"MB3983","text":"What is the 'A blood antig​en' composed of?","raw_text":"What is the 'A blood antigen' composed of?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Galactose attached to H su​bstance","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Glucose attached to H subs​tance","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"N-acetyl galactosamine att​ached to H substance","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"N-acetyl glucosamine attac​hed to H substance","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. N-acetyl galactosamine attached to H substance","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. N-acetyl galactosamine attached to H substance</strong></p><p>The blood group '<strong>antigen A'</strong> is composed of <strong>N-acetyl galactosamine</strong> attached to the <strong>H substance</strong>.</p>\n<p>H substance is formed by the addition of <strong>fucose</strong> to the glycolipid or glycoprotein backbone. It is present in all blood groups, even the O group.</p>\n<p>A antigen = N-acetyl galactosamine + H substance </p>\n<p>B antigen = galactose + H substance</p>\n<p>However, in case of the<strong> Bombay phenotype,</strong> RBCs <strong>lack H antigen</strong>. Hence, these individuals have anti-A, anti-B, and anti-H antibodies. The only compatible blood donor is another person with the Bombay blood group.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3984","text":"Which chromosome contains ​the genes that determine ​the A and B blood groups?​","raw_text":"Which chromosome contains the genes that determine the A and B blood groups?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​9","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"​7","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"​5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​1","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 9","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 9</strong></p><p>The genes that determine the <strong>A and B blood</strong> groups are located on <strong>chromosome 9. </strong>The phenotypes are expressed in the <strong>Mendelian codominant </strong>manner.</p>\n<p><strong>Rh</strong> genes determining Rh blood grouping are present on <strong>chromosome 1</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3989","text":"Individuals with a lack of​ which of the following b​lood group antigens have ​immunity against Plasmodi‌um vivax?","raw_text":"Individuals with a lack of which of the following blood group antigens have immunity against Plasmodium vivax?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ke​ll","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Le​wis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ki​dd","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Du​ffy","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Duffy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Duffy</strong></p><p>Duffy antigens serve as receptors for Plasmodium vivax. Hence, the <strong>absence </strong>of the <strong>Duffy</strong> antigen is <strong>protective</strong> against <em><strong>Plasmodium vivax</strong>. </em></p>\n<p>Hemoglobinopathies that are <strong>protective</strong> against <strong><em>Plasmodium falciparum</em></strong> include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sickle cell trait</li>\n<li>Hemoglobin C </li>\n<li>Hemoglobin SC</li>\n<li>Hemoglobin E</li>\n<li>Hereditary persistence of fetal hemoglobin (Hb F)</li>\n<li>Alpha and beta-thalassemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3992","text":"Donated blood is screened ​for all of the following ​infections except:","raw_text":"Donated blood is screened for all of the following infections except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kala​ azar","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Hepat​itis C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Syph​ilis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Mal​aria","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Kala azar","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Kala azar</strong></p><p><strong>Donated blood</strong> is not routinely screened for kala-azar. It is <strong>screened</strong> for the following <strong>infections</strong> in the blood bank:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Human immunodeficiency virus</li>\n<li>Hepatitis B</li>\n<li>Hepatitis C</li>\n<li>Malaria</li>\n<li>Syphilis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1866","text":"As the medical officer at ​a blood bank, you are sup​ervising the separation o​f blood components of don‌ated blood. The preservat‍ive used for the packed r‌ed cells is SAG-M. Which ​of the following criteria‌ must be fulfilled in ord‌er to safely transfuse th‍is to patients? Storage t​emperature To be used wit‍hin: 1. -2° to -6 °C 21 d​ays 2. +2° to +6 °C 42 da​ys 3. -2° to -6 °C 35 day‌s 4. +2° to +6 °C. 48 day​s","raw_text":"<p>As the medical officer at a blood bank, you are supervising the separation of blood components of donated blood. The preservative used for the packed red cells is SAG-M. Which of the following criteria must be fulfilled in order to safely transfuse this to patients?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 162px;\" width=\"331\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\"><strong>Storage temperature</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\"><strong>To be used within:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">1.   </td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">-2° to -6 °C    </td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">    21 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">2.   </td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">+2° to +6 °C  </td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">    42 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">3.   </td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">-2° to -6 °C   </td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">    35 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">4.   </td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">+2° to +6 °C. </td>\n<td style=\"width: 103px;\">    48 days</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 2</strong></p><p>The shelf life of packed red cells (<strong>pRBCs</strong>) in the <strong>SAG-M</strong> solution is <strong>42 days. </strong>The optimum temperature for storage of packed RBCs and whole blood collected in preservatives is <strong>+2° to +6° C</strong>. This temperature maintains the viability of the cells and prevents bacterial contamination.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 204px; width: 387px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Blood component </strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Storage temperature</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Shelf-life</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; text-align: left;\">PRBCs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px; text-align: left;\">+2° to +6 °C</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px; text-align: left;\">\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">CPD-21 days</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">CPD-A-35 days</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">SAG M-42 days</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; text-align: left;\">Platelets</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px; text-align: left;\">+20 to +24 °C</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px; text-align: left;\">5 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; text-align: left;\">Fresh frozen plasma</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px; text-align: left;\">-30 °C or lower</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px; text-align: left;\">1 year</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 113px; text-align: center;\">Cryoprecipitate</td>\n<td style=\"width: 116px; text-align: center;\">-30 °C or lower</td>\n<td style=\"width: 170px; text-align: left;\">1 years</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1855","text":"Packed red blood cell tran​sfusion is started for a ​patient in the ward. It m​ust be completed within:","raw_text":"Packed red blood cell transfusion is started for a patient in the ward. It must be completed within:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5 h​ours","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2 h​ours","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"3 h​ours","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 h​ours","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 4 hours","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 4 hours</strong></p><p>Transfusion of <strong>pRBCs</strong> (packed red blood cells) or whole blood should be completed <strong>within 4 hours</strong> after the commencement. Transfusion of platelets, fresh frozen plasma, and cryoprecipitate should be completed within 30 minutes.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3996","text":"Cryoprecipitate is useful ​for the management of pat​ients with all of the fol​lowing conditions except:‌","raw_text":"Cryoprecipitate is useful for the management of patients with all of the following conditions except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemoph​ilia A","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Von Willebr​and disease","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Hypofibri​nogenemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Christma​s disease","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Christmas disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Christmas disease</strong></p><p><strong>Cryoprecipitate</strong> does not contain factor IX. Hence, it is not useful for the management of patients with Christmas disease, which occurs due to deficiency of factor IX.</p>\n<p>Components of cryoprecipitate are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Factor VIII</li>\n<li>Factor XIII</li>\n<li>Fibrinogen</li>\n<li>Fibronectin</li>\n<li>Von Willebrand factor</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore it can be used in patients with <strong>hemophilia A</strong>, factor XIII deficiency, <strong>hypofibrinogenemia</strong>, and <strong>von Willebrand disease</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3993","text":"You are monitoring the fal​ling platelet count of a ​31-year-old man with deng​ue fever. You would recom‌mend prophylactic platele‍t transfusion when the co‌unt drops below what leve​l?","raw_text":"You are monitoring the falling platelet count of a 31-year-old man with dengue fever. You would recommend prophylactic platelet transfusion when the count drops below what level?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5000 plat​elets /μL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10000 pla​telets /μL","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"15000 pla​telets /μL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"50000 pla​telets /μL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 10000 platelets /μL","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 10000 platelets /μL</strong></p><p>The threshold for <strong>prophylactic</strong> <strong>platelet</strong> <strong>transfusion</strong> is <strong>10,000/μL </strong>in order to prevent spontaneous bleeding. If the patient is planned for any <strong>invasive procedures</strong>, the target platelet level is <strong>50,000/μL.</strong></p>\n<p>The shelf life of donor platelets is 5 days. It is stored on a special agitator at<strong> </strong>20-24 °C. The half-life of transfused platelets is 3-5 days. One unit of pooled platelets should be transfused within 30 minutes because of the risk of bacterial proliferation.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3995","text":"Which of the following blo​od components does not ca​rry the risk of transmitt​ing cytomegalovirus infec‌tion?","raw_text":"Which of the following blood components does not carry the risk of transmitting cytomegalovirus infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Packed red ​blood cells","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Whole​ blood","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Fresh fro​zen plasma","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Random dono​r platelets","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Fresh frozen plasma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Fresh frozen plasma</strong></p><p>Fresh frozen plasma <strong>(FFP)</strong> is an <strong>acellular</strong> component and generally does not transmit viral infections.</p>\n<p><strong>Cytomegalovirus</strong> (CMV) infects &gt;50% of the general population and is transmitted by infected <strong>passenger WBCs</strong> in transfused <strong>PRBCs </strong>or<strong> platelet components. </strong>Cellular components that are leukocyte-reduced have a decreased risk of transmitting CMV.</p>\n<p>Infectious complications of blood transfusion include: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Viral infections:</strong> Hepatitis C virus, hepatitis B virus, HIV, cytomegalovirus</li>\n<li><strong>Bacterial</strong> contamination:<strong> </strong>sudden and fulminant onset of fever, chills, septic shock, and disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)\n<ul>\n<li>Platelet concentrates stored at room temperature: gram-positive and coagulase-negative staphylococci</li>\n<li>PRBCs: Gram-negative bacteria like <em>Yersinia, Pseudomonas, Serratia, Acinetobacter, </em>and <em>Escherichia</em> species</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Parasites: </strong>Malaria, Babesiosis, Chaga's disease</li>\n<li>Others: Creutzfeldt–Jakob disease (<strong>CJD</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1902","text":"Which of the following rea​ctions occurs most freque​ntly in patients undergoi​ng blood transfusions?","raw_text":"Which of the following reactions occurs most frequently in patients undergoing blood transfusions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute hemolytic transfusio​n reaction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Febrile non-hemolytic tran​sfusion reaction","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Transfusion-related acute ​lung injury","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anaphylact​ic reaction","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Febrile non-hemolytic transfusion reaction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Febrile non-hemolytic transfusion reaction</strong></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 10pt;\"><strong>Febrile non-hemolytic</strong> transfusion reaction (FNHTR) is the most frequently occurring transfusion reaction.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1925","text":"Thirty minutes after a blo​od transfusion is started​, a patient develops chil​ls and severe flank pain.‌ Her temperature is 102.4‍ F, SpO2 is 96%, and her ‌blood pressure is 90/60 m​m Hg. She then begins ble‌eding around her IV cathe‌ter site. What is the mos‍t likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"Thirty minutes after a blood transfusion is started, a patient develops chills and severe flank pain. Her temperature is 102.4 F, SpO2 is 96%, and her blood pressure is 90/60 mm Hg. She then begins bleeding around her IV catheter site. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anaphylact​ic reaction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute hemoly​tic reaction","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Febrile non-hemolytic reac​tion","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anamnestic antibody respon​se","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Acute hemolytic reaction","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Acute hemolytic reaction</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute hemolytic</strong> transfusion reaction (AHTR). </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MB3999","text":"A 40-year-old man is being​ evaluated in the ward fo​r fatigue and pallor. One​ week ago, he had undergo‌ne a blood transfusion fo‍llowing a road accident. ‌Further evaluation reveal​s Hb of 8.1 g/dl and posi‌tive direct Coomb's test ‌and history of previous b‍lood transfusion. What is​ the likely mechanism of ‍this condition?","raw_text":"A 40-year-old man is being evaluated in the ward for fatigue and pallor. One week ago, he had undergone a blood transfusion following a road accident. Further evaluation reveals Hb of 8.1 g/dl and positive direct Coomb's test and history of previous blood transfusion. What is the likely mechanism of this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ABO incompatibility reacti​on","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Antibodies against donor l​eukocytes","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Antibodies against donor I​gA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Anamnestic antibody respon​se","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Anamnestic antibody response","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Anamnestic antibody response</strong></p><p>This clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>delayed hemolytic transfusion reaction</strong> (DHTR). It is due to an <strong>anamnestic antibody response.</strong> This refers to the rapid production of antibodies in the blood after exposure to an antigen to which the recipient had been previously sensitized.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1931","text":"Which among the following ​options is the most commo​n cause of death in patie​nts undergoing blood tran‌sfusions?","raw_text":"Which among the following options is the most common cause of death in patients undergoing blood transfusions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Febrile non-hemolytic tran​sfusion reaction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Acute hemolytic transfusio​n reaction","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Delayed hemolytic transfus​ion reaction","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Transfusion-related acute ​lung injury","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Transfusion-related acute lung injury","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Transfusion-related acute lung injury</strong></p><p>Transfusion-related acute lung injury (<strong>TRALI</strong>) is the most common cause of <strong>transfusion-related fatalities</strong>.</p>\n<p>TRALI is due to the presence of <strong>anti-HLA class II antibodies </strong>in the donor plasma. These antibodies bind recipient leukocytes, which aggregate in the pulmonary vasculature and release mediators that increase capillary permeability. <strong>Multiparous women</strong> are frequently implicated donors. Recipients with a history of liver disease, smoking, alcohol use are at a higher risk.</p>\n<p>It occurs within <strong>6 hours</strong> of transfusion and results in <strong>hypoxia</strong> and signs of non-cardiogenic <strong>pulmonary edema</strong> with bilateral interstitial infiltrates on chest X-ray. It is managed with supportive care.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Note: According to the latest edition of Harrison's, transfusion associated circulatory overload (<strong>TACO</strong>) is the leading cause of death followed by TRALI.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1928","text":"Following a normal vaginal​ delivery, a 30-year-old ​woman develops severe pos​tpartum hemorrhage. Over ‌the next 24 hours, she un‍dergoes transfusion of 11‌ units of pRBCs. She is a​t risk for all of the fol‌lowing complications exce‌pt:","raw_text":"Following a normal vaginal delivery, a 30-year-old woman develops severe postpartum hemorrhage. Over the next 24 hours, she undergoes transfusion of 11 units of pRBCs. She is at risk for all of the following complications except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metabolic​ alkalosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperc​alcemia","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperk​alemia","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypot​hermia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Hypercalcemia","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Hypercalcemia</strong></p><p><strong>Massive transfusion</strong> is defined as transfusion of at least <strong>10 units</strong> of red cells in <strong>24 hours</strong> or 4 units in 1 hour. <strong>Hypocalcemia,</strong> not hypercalcemia, is a complication of massive blood transfusion.</p>\n<p>Blood is anticoagulated with sodium citrate and citric acid. It binds to calcium and magnesium, leading to low levels of both. Citrate is also metabolized to bicarbonate, causing <strong>metabolic alkalosis.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC7039","text":"A patient with thalassemia​ has a history of multipl​e blood transfusions, iro​n overload, and cardiac a‌rrhythmia. She has now co‍me for blood transfusion ‌and during the process, c​omplains of backache and ‌looks very anxious. What ‌would you do next?","raw_text":"A patient with thalassemia has a history of multiple blood transfusions, iron overload, and cardiac arrhythmia. She has now come for blood transfusion and during the process, complains of backache and looks very anxious. What would you do next?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Stop the blood transfusion​","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Continue the transfusion b​ut do an ECG","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Stop the transfusion. Wait​ for patient to become no​rmal and then start it ag​ain","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Do clerical check and get ​ECG","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Stop the blood transfusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Stop the blood transfusion</strong></p><p>The patient is being<strong> transfused blood</strong> and has complaints of backache<strong>, </strong>this is suggestive of an <strong>acute hemolytic transfusion reaction. </strong></p>\n<p>The first step is to stop transfusion immediately and then collect<strong> blood samples</strong> for testing.</p>\n<p>Acute hemolytic transfusion reactions typically occur within the <strong>first 24 hours </strong>following a blood transfusion, primarily due to <strong>ABO incompatibility</strong>.</p>\n<p>It presents with symptoms such as hypotension, tachypnea, tachycardia, fever (1-2°C increase in temperature), chills, chest and back pain, hemoglobinuria, and hemoglobinemia. In severe cases, disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC), acute renal failure, shock, and death can occur.</p>\n<p>Management of immune-mediated acute hemolytic transfusion reactions is to provide supportive care, administer vigorous hydration with isotonic saline and use diuretics to maintain urine output.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD0224","text":"A patient of hemophilia re​ceived multiple blood tra​nsfusions. Which of the f​ollowing metabolic abnorm‌alities can be seen in a ‍patient?","raw_text":"A patient of hemophilia received multiple blood transfusions. Which of the following metabolic abnormalities can be seen in a patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metabolic​ alkalosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Respirator​y alkalosis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Metabolic​ acidosis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Respirator​y acidosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Metabolic alkalosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Metabolic alkalosis</strong></p><p>Among the given options, the <strong>metabolic abnormality</strong> seen in a patient who has received <strong>multiple blood transfusions</strong> is <strong>metabolic alkalosis</strong>.</p>\n<p>This is because the <strong>excess sodium citrate</strong> (anticoagulant) in the transfused blood gets <strong>converted to bicarbonate</strong> leading to <strong>metabolic alkalosis</strong>. Each mmol of citrate generates 3 mEq of bicarbonate (a total of 23 mEq of bicarbonate due to each unit of blood).</p>\n<p>Metabolic acidosis can also be seen in multiple blood transfusions as citrate is acidic, but <strong>metabolic alkalosis</strong> is <strong>more common</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"3P5yd7aC","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"99_hiv___aids___epidemiology_and_clinical_features","title":"99.Hiv   Aids   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","num_questions":18,"total_marks":72,"duration":28,"time_per_question":93.33,"questions":[{"id":"MC4166","text":"What is the approximate ti‌me interval between HIV i‍nfection &amp; manifestation ‌of AIDS?","raw_text":"What is the approximate time interval between HIV infection &amp; manifestation of AIDS?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7.5 ‌years","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"10 y‌ears","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"12 y‌ears","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"5 y‌ears","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 10 years","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3427d6415f6a4fbaa5309f656fe49894x1280x1475.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 10 years</strong></p><p>The time interval between <strong>primary HIV</strong> infection and the <strong>manifestation</strong> of AIDS is <strong>10 years</strong>.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>After exposure to the HIV virus, there occurs <strong>primary infection</strong> following an incubation period of <strong>2-4 weeks</strong>, which is usually symptomatic in more than 50% of cases, following <strong>3-6 weeks</strong> there is <strong>acute HIV syndrome</strong> due to wide dissemination of the virus, characterized by fever, sore throat, diarrhea, lymphadenopathy, and rash.</p>\n<p>This <strong>peak viremia drops</strong> as the <strong>immune response develops</strong>, to reach a <strong>plateau</strong> about <strong>3 months later</strong>. Following this there is a prolonged period of <strong>clinical latency</strong>, during which infected individuals are <strong>asymptomatic</strong>, lasting for a median of <strong>10 years</strong>.</p>\n<p>Following this period, <strong>chronic persistent infection</strong> develops which is the hallmark of HIV disease called <strong>Acquired immunodeficiency syndrome</strong> (AIDS).</p>\n<p>Long-term non-progressors: a small part of <strong>untreated HIV-infected</strong> people with <strong>CD4 counts</strong> within the <strong>reference range</strong> for <strong>&gt;10 years</strong>. </p>\n<p>Elite controllers: Some <strong>long-term non-progressors</strong> have <strong>undetectable viral loads</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1608","text":"Which of the following cas‌e scenarios depict a mode‍ of transmission that car‌ries the highest risk of ‌acquiring HIV?","raw_text":"Which of the following case scenarios depict a mode of transmission that carries the highest risk of acquiring HIV?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 23-year-old drug addict ‌sharing syringes during i‍ntravenous drug abuse","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 15-year-old thalassemic ‌adolescent undergoing blo‍od transfusion","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A 30-year-old man engaging‌ in unprotected sexual in‍tercourse with multiple p‌artners","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A 27-year-old nurse accide‌ntally injuring herself w‍hile drawing blood","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A 15-year-old thalassemic adolescent undergoing blood transfusion","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A 15-year-old thalassemic adolescent undergoing blood transfusion</strong></p><p><strong>Blood transfusion</strong> carries the highest risk of acquiring HIV.</p>\n<p>Salient features of transmission of HIV:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>highest </strong>risk of acquiring HIV from an infected source is via <strong>blood transfusion</strong> &gt; needle sharing during drug abuse &gt; receptive anal intercourse &gt; needle-stick injury &gt; vaginal &amp; oral sexual intercourse.</li>\n<li>The <strong>most common </strong>mode of transmission worldwide is <strong>sexual transmission</strong> (heterosexual in India) &gt; mother to child &gt; <strong>least common</strong> modes are blood transfusion &amp; <strong>needle-stick injury</strong>.</li>\n<li>Male to female transmission &gt; female to male transmission.</li>\n<li>Viral load is <strong>highest</strong> in <strong>blood</strong> &gt; semen &gt; vaginal fluid &gt; breastmilk &amp; saliva and minimal to absent in tears, sweat &amp; urine. </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1602","text":"Which statement regarding ‌HIV infection is true?","raw_text":"Which statement regarding HIV infection is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HIV-2 is more pathogenic t‌han HIV -1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HIV-1 subtype C is the mos‌t common form worldwide","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"HIV-2 is prevalent worldwi‌de","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Donated blood is not scree‌ned for HIV- 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. HIV-1 subtype C is the most common form worldwide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. HIV-1 subtype C is the most common form worldwide</strong></p><p><strong>HIV-1 subtype C</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> form worldwide.</p>\n<p>Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) has two<strong> serotypes HIV-1 and HIV- 2 </strong>based on differences in the<strong> env (envelope) gene</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>HIV-1</strong> is further divided into 4 distinct groups, group <strong>M</strong> (major)<strong>/N/O/P</strong>. The M group comprises nine subtypes A, B, C, D, F, G, H, J, and K.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Subtype C</strong> is the <strong>most common</strong> form worldwide &amp; is more associated with <strong>heterosexual</strong> transmission.</li>\n<li><strong>Subtype B</strong> is more associated with spread through blood and <strong>homosexual</strong> contact.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options A, C: HIV- 2 is <strong>less pathogenic</strong> than HIV-1 and is predominantly confined to <strong>Africa</strong>.</p>\n<p>Option D: Donated blood is screened for both <strong>HIV -1 &amp; HIV-2</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1607","text":"A 47-year-old phlebotomist‌ suffered a needle-stick ‍injury while drawing bloo‌d from a patient with HIV‌. Which of the following ‍mutations in him can caus​e a resistance to acquiri​ng HIV infection?","raw_text":"A 47-year-old phlebotomist suffered a needle-stick injury while drawing blood from a patient with HIV. Which of the following mutations in him can cause a resistance to acquiring HIV infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CXCR4 2q2‌1 mutation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"HLA ‌B*35","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CCR5 delta ‌32 mutation","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"DC-‌SIGN","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CCR5 delta 32 mutation","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CCR5 delta 32 mutation</strong></p><p><strong>CCR 5 delta 32 mutation</strong> causes <strong>resistance</strong> <strong>to HIV infection</strong>. It is a mutated allele that causes a <strong>lack</strong> of surface expression of the <strong>CCR5</strong> co-receptor resulting in blockade of HIV entry and resistance to HIV infection.</p>\n<p>Homozygous alleles cause complete resistance to HIV infection &amp; heterozygous alleles cause partial resistance.</p>\n<p><strong>CXCR4 </strong>and<strong> CCR5</strong> are <strong>chemokine receptors</strong> (co-receptors) present on the surface of CD4+ T-helper cells &amp; macrophages that aid in the <strong>fusion and entry</strong> of the HIV virus. </p>\n<p><strong>DC-SIGN</strong> is a type of cell surface lectin receptor to which the HIV virus has the affinity to bind. The expression of this type of receptor on dendritic cells (antigen-presenting cells)<strong> enhances the infectivity</strong> of HIV.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2147","text":"A 19-year-old woman presen‌ts to the casualty with a‍ 24-hour history of fever‌, sore throat, and diffus‌e erythematous macular ra‍sh. Past history is posit​ive for unprotected inter​course with a new partner‍. What is true about the ‌likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"A 19-year-old woman presents to the casualty with a 24-hour history of fever, sore throat, and diffuse erythematous macular rash. Past history is positive for unprotected intercourse with a new partner. What is true about the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Incubation period is aroun‌d 1 week","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Weight loss‌ is not seen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Virus is tr‌ansmissible","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Opportunistic infections d‌o not occur","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Virus is transmissible","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Virus is transmissible</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>acute HIV syndrome</strong>. The <strong>virus is transmissible</strong> during this early infection period.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is characterized by <strong>acute burst of viremia</strong> and <strong>wide virus dissemination</strong> and seeding of lymphoid organs (e.g., gut-associated lymphoid tissue), culminating in the destruction of CD4+ cells. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 5864\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>The <strong>p24 antigen capture assay</strong> is used as a screening test for HIV infection in patients suspected of having the acute HIV syndrome.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>Option A: The <strong>incubation period</strong> for acute HIV syndrome is around <strong>3 to 6 weeks.</strong></p>\n<p>Option B: The acute HIV syndrome is typical of an acute viral syndrome and is very similar to acute infectious mononucleosis. The clinical findings include fever, sore throat, and lymphadenopathy. Rash, headache, arthralgia, and anorexia/<strong>weight loss</strong> can also be seen.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Opportunistic infections can occur </strong>during this stage of infection, reflecting the immunodeficiency that results from reduced numbers of CD4+T cells. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1609","text":"What is the correct WHO st‌aging for an HIV-infected‍ child with unexplained p‌ersistent parotid enlarge‌ment?","raw_text":"What is the correct WHO staging for an HIV-infected child with unexplained persistent parotid enlargement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sta‌ge 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Sta‌ge 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Sta‌ge 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Sta‌ge 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Stage 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bee35d0a8b4c44a8b5b129428a4b73cbx720x671.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Stage 2</strong></p><p>Unexplained persistent <strong>parotid enlargement</strong> in an HIV-infected child is categorized as <strong>stage 2</strong> of HIV disease according to WHO staging.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1610","text":"An HIV-positive individual‌ presents with the follow‍ing finding. What is the ‌diagnosis &amp; WHO staging?","raw_text":"An HIV-positive individual presents with the following finding. What is the diagnosis &amp; WHO staging?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oral candidiasis &amp;amp; WHO‌ stage 2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Oral hairy leukoplakia &amp;am‌p; WHO stage 3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oral candidiasis &amp;amp; WHO‌ stage 3","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Oral hairy leukoplakia &amp;am‌p; WHO stage 2","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Oral candidiasis & WHO stage 3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9521c953e6664507b3e6ee8d585b3eff.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8c2e6a01699046e8969f6f264309a917x334x242.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Oral candidiasis &amp; WHO stage 3</strong></p><p><strong>Oral candidiasis</strong> (thrush) is categorized as <strong>WHO stage 3</strong>.</p>\n<p>Oral candidiasis and oral hairy leukoplakia are oral lesions categorized as WHO clinical stage 3. They generally occur in patients with <strong>CD4+ T cell counts of &lt;300/μL.</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 210px; width: 465px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 23px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 23px;\"> </td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.047px; height: 23px;\"><strong>Oral candidiasis</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 303.953px; height: 23px;\"><strong>Oral hairy leukoplakia</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 2px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 2px;\">\n<p class=\"\">Cause</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.047px; height: 2px;\">\n<p class=\"\"><em>Candida albicans</em></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 303.953px; height: 2px;\">Epstein-Barr virus </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 11px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 11px;\">Appearance</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.047px; height: 11px;\">\n<p class=\"\">White, plaque-like lesions with cheesy exudate, on an erythematous mucosa</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 303.953px; height: 11px;\">\n<p>White, frond-like lesions with hair-like growths from folds in the patches</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 23px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 23px;\">Location </td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.047px; height: 23px;\">Posterior oropharynx</td>\n<td style=\"width: 303.953px; height: 23px;\">Lateral borders of tongue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 23px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 23px;\">Clinical features</td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.047px; height: 23px;\">\n<p class=\"\">Typically painful, can be easily brushed off</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 303.953px; height: 23px;\">Typically painless, cannot be brushed </td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 23px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 23px;\">Investigation </td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.047px; height: 23px;\">\n<p class=\"\">KOH preparation demonstrates budding yeasts, hyphae, or pseudohyphae</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 303.953px; height: 23px;\">\n<p class=\"\">Despite its name, it is not considered premalignant. </p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 23px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 80px; height: 23px;\">Treatment </td>\n<td style=\"width: 282.047px; height: 23px;\"> Antifungals</td>\n<td style=\"width: 303.953px; height: 23px;\">Antivirals</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p class=\"p1\">The image given below shows a patient with oral hairy leukoplakia.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1611","text":"What is the most common op‌portunistic infection in ‍HIV-infected individuals?‌","raw_text":"What is the most common opportunistic infection in HIV-infected individuals?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberc‌ulosis","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Disseminated‌ candidiasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Herpes‌ zoster","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Pneumocystis jiroveci pneu‌monia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tuberculosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tuberculosis</strong></p><p><strong>Tuberculosisis</strong> the most common opportunistic infection in HIV-infected individuals <strong>globally and</strong> in <strong>India</strong>.</p>\n<p>In patients with relatively<strong> high CD4+ T cell counts</strong>, the typical pattern of <strong>pulmonary reactivation</strong> occurs (60–80%). In patients with <strong>lower</strong> CD4+ T cell counts, <strong>disseminated disease</strong> is seen.</p>\n<p><strong>Primary prophylaxis:</strong> <strong>Isoniazid </strong>and <strong>pyridoxine</strong> for <strong>9 months</strong> for HIV-infected individuals meeting the following conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>PPD skin-test reaction of &gt;5 mm or,</li>\n<li>Positive IFN-γ release assay or,</li>\n<li>Close household contacts of persons with active tuberculosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1612","text":"A previously untreated HIV‌-positive patient is diag‍nosed with tuberculosis a‌nd treatment is initiated‌ for both the diseases. T‍wo weeks into treatment, ​he suddenly complains of ​high fever, severe malais‍e and worsening cough. Wh‌at is the likely explanat‌ion?","raw_text":"A previously untreated HIV-positive patient is diagnosed with tuberculosis and treatment is initiated for both the diseases. Two weeks into treatment, he suddenly complains of high fever, severe malaise and worsening cough. What is the likely explanation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Resistance to anti-tubercu‌lar therapy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Concurrent treatment with ‌anti-retroviral therapy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bacterial endotoxin releas‌e due to death of TB baci‍lli","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Hypersensitivity reaction ‌to isoniazid","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Concurrent treatment with anti-retroviral therapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Concurrent treatment with anti-retroviral therapy</strong></p><p><strong>Concurrent treatment</strong> with <strong>anti-retroviral therapy</strong> is the most likely explanation in this case resulting in <strong>Immune Reconstitution Inflammatory Syndrome</strong> (IRIS). Its characteristics are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Temporary <strong>paradoxical exacerbation</strong> of symptoms (lymphadenopathy, fever or worsening of pulmonary infiltrations, pleural effusion) and radiographic manifestations of TB after starting ART. This occurs as a result of <strong>immune reconstitution</strong> due to <strong>simultaneous </strong>administration of <strong>ART and ATT</strong>. </li>\n<li>It<strong> </strong>is seen weeks to months following the initiation of combination antiretroviral therapy (cART).</li>\n<li>It is most common in <strong>previously untreated</strong> patients starting therapy with a CD4+ T cell count <strong>&lt;50/μL</strong> who experience a sudden drop in viral load.</li>\n<li>The earlier ART is started and the lower the baseline CD4+ T-cell count, the greater the risk of IRIS.</li>\n<li>In severe cases, the use of immunosuppressive drugs such as <strong>glucocorticoids</strong> may be required to blunt the inflammatory component of IRIS while specific antimicrobial therapy takes effect.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note: Hypersensitivity to isoniazid is extremely rare and the presentation includes maculopapular rash.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1613","text":"An HIV-positive man presen‌ts with a high-grade feve‍r. Examination reveals th‌e following finding. CSF ‌shows reduced glucose, in‍creased protein, and incr​eased leukocytes. What is​ the most common cause of‍ this condition in such p‌atients?","raw_text":"An HIV-positive man presents with a high-grade fever. Examination reveals the following finding. CSF shows reduced glucose, increased protein, and increased leukocytes. What is the most common cause of this condition in such patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptococcu‌s pneumoniae","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Herpes sim‌plex virus","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Toxoplas‌ma gondii","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Cryptococcu‌s neoformans","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Cryptococcus neoformans","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/505060e2c15c41f5a879f53aa4a8efa6.GIF"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cryptococcus neoformans</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario, along with a <strong>positive Kernig's sign</strong>, is suggestive of <strong>meningitis</strong>. <strong>Cryptococcus</strong> is the leading infectious cause of meningitis in patients with AIDS. It generally occurs in untreated patients, with CD4+ T cell counts <strong>&lt;100/μL</strong>.            </p>\n<p>Most patients present with a picture of <strong>subacute meningoencephalitis</strong>. The CSF profile may show modest elevations in WBC or protein levels and decrease in glucose. The CSF opening pressure is usually elevated (yeast colonies obstruct the free flow of CSF causing <strong>increased CSF pressure</strong>).</p>\n<p>The <strong>diagnosis</strong> of cryptococcal meningitis is made by identification of <em>Cryptococcus neoformans</em> yeast cells in <strong>CSF</strong> with<strong> india ink stain</strong> or by the detection of cryptococcal antigen using <strong>latex agglutination (CrAg).</strong></p>\n<p>Prophylaxis: <strong>Oral fluconazole </strong>when CD4+ T cell count is <strong>&lt;100/μL.</strong></p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Induction </strong>- IV or liposomal <strong>amphotericin B with flucytosine</strong> for at least 2 weeks, continuing with amphotericin alone ideally until the CSF culture turns negative.</li>\n<li><strong>Maintenance </strong>-  Oral <strong>fluconazole</strong> until the CD4+ T cell count has increased to &gt;200 cells/μL for 6 months in response to cART.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5002","text":"AIDS-defining malignancies‌ include all except :","raw_text":"AIDS-defining malignancies include all except :","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kaposi's‌ sarcoma","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Mela‌noma","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Invasive cervical carcinom‌a","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Non hodgki‌ns lymphoma","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Melanoma","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2a1b5b50b45e4f01b58e94ceed3aefbdx1280x854.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Melanoma</strong></p><p><strong>Melanoma</strong> is <strong>not</strong> an <strong>AIDS-defining malignancy</strong>.</p>\n<p>AIDS-defining malignancies are those cancers that a human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infected person is at a high risk of developing. When a HIV-infected person develops one of the AIDS-defining malignancies it suggests that he has AIDS.</p>\n<p><strong>AIDS-defining malignancies</strong> include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Kaposi sarcoma</strong> - It is a multicentric neoplasm of vascular origin and has a strong association with HHV - 8.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma</strong></li>\n<li>Invasive cervical cancer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Given below is the image of kaposi sarcoma:</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Note : <span data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":33555327,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"4\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":16777215},\"5\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"6\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"7\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"8\":{\"1\":[{\"1\":2,\"2\":0,\"5\":{\"1\":2,\"2\":0}},{\"1\":0,\"2\":0,\"3\":3},{\"1\":1,\"2\":0,\"4\":1}]},\"9\":0,\"11\":4,\"12\":0,\"28\":1}' data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"The FDA has expanded the indication of pomalidomide, a thalidomide analogue to be included in treating adult patients with AIDS-related Kaposi sarcoma after failure of highly active antiretroviral therapy and Kaposi sarcoma in adult patients who are HIV-negative\"}'>The FDA has approved <strong>pomalidomide</strong>, a thalidomide analogue to be included in treating adult patients with AIDS-related Kaposi's sarcoma after failure of highly active antiretroviral therapy and Kaposi's sarcoma in adult patients who are HIV-negative.</span></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1615","text":"A 45-year-old HIV-positive‌ woman presented with fev‍er and malaise. She gives‌ a history of bleeding fr‌om the following lesions.‍ Warthin-Starry staining ​of a biopsy sample taken ​from the lesion showed wh‍ich organism?","raw_text":"A 45-year-old HIV-positive woman presented with fever and malaise. She gives a history of bleeding from the following lesions. Warthin-Starry staining of a biopsy sample taken from the lesion showed which organism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bartonell‌a henselae","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Human Herpe‌s virus - 8","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Bartonella b‌acilliformis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ebstein B‌arr Virus","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Bartonella henselae","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4b7b01fce7a348aba1f4a21a73369ef0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c3fd558935c49d09f89841c1eaecb67x224x180.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Bartonella henselae</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>bacillary angiomatosis </strong>in an immunocompromised patient.<strong> Warthin-Starry stain </strong>would show<em><strong> Bartonella henselae </strong></em>as the causative organism in these skin lesions.</p>\n<p>Bacillary angiomatosis is an <strong>infectious vascular proliferative lesion</strong> presenting as reddish-purple nodules similar to KS lesions (caused by HHV-8). The diagnosis is confirmed with a biopsy. The drug of choice for treatment is <strong>erythromycin</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The image given below shows the Warthin-Starry stained lesion showing tangled pleomorphic bacilli.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>HHV 8</strong> is strongly associated with Kaposi sarcoma.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong><em>Bartonella bacilliformis</em> </strong>causes Oroya fever.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>EBV</strong> causes infectious mononucleosis.</p>\n<p>Note: <em>Bartonella henselae</em> causes <strong>cat-scratch disease, </strong>a necrotizing granulomatous disorder of lymph nodes, in<strong> immunocompetent hosts.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1619","text":"An HIV-positive woman was ‌rushed to the ICU followi‍ng an episode of generali‌zed tonic-clonic seizures‌. There was no history of‍ fever, night sweats or w​eight loss. MRI scan is s​hown below. What is the m‍ost likely diagnosis?","raw_text":"An HIV-positive woman was rushed to the ICU following an episode of generalized tonic-clonic seizures. There was no history of fever, night sweats or weight loss. MRI scan is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cryptococca‌l meningitis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Primary central nervous sy‌stem lymphoma","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Cerebral to‌xoplasmosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Progressive multifocal leu‌koencephalopathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Cerebral toxoplasmosis","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/92e01cc7c83d41c2bb2579d461cf39d7.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cerebral toxoplasmosis</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>cerebral toxoplasmosis.</strong> <strong>MRI</strong> scan shows multiple <strong>ring-enhancing lesions</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cerebral toxoplasmosis is one of the <strong>most common </strong>AIDS-defining <strong>opportunistic CNS infections</strong> and usually occurs in patients with CD4+ T cell counts <strong>&lt;200/μL. </strong>It is usually a reactivation of latent tissue cysts.<span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"> </span></p>\n<p>It usually has <strong>multiple lesions</strong> in multiple locations which are associated with inflammation and central necrosis, and hence show ring enhancement on contrast MRI. Sometimes an eccentric nodular area of enhancement is seen within the ring; this so-called <strong>eccentric target sign</strong> is typical of cerebral toxoplasmosis. <strong>Surrounding edema </strong>is also present.</p>\n<p>Treatment includes <strong>sulfadiazine/pyrimethamine</strong> and <strong>leucovorin</strong> for a minimum of 4–6 weeks. </p>\n<p><strong>Primary prophylaxis: </strong>It is given to patients with <strong>IgG antibody to toxoplasma and</strong> <strong>CD4+ T cell</strong> count <strong>&lt;100/μL</strong>. A single double strength tablet of trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole (TMP/SMX) is given.</p>\n<p><strong>Secondary prophyaxis</strong>/maintanace therapy: It is given when <strong>CD4+ T cell counts &lt;200 cells/μL</strong>. Sulfadiazine/pyrimethamine and leucovorin is given. It is discontinued if the CD4+ T cell counts are maintained &gt;200/μL for 6 months.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Cryptococcal meningitis</strong> presents with fever, nausea, vomiting, altered mental status, headache, and meningeal signs. Seizures and focal neurologic deficits are rarely seen. <strong>MRI</strong> findings include <strong>dilated perivascular spaces</strong> and basal ganglia pseudocysts. </p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Primary CNS lymphoma</strong> also presents with seizures and focal neurological deficit.<strong> </strong>Symptoms of fever, night sweats and weight loss are present in 80% of the patients. On imaging, it is identified as a CT hyperdense enhancing supratentorial mass, with <strong>MRI </strong>T1 <strong>hypointense</strong>, T2 iso- to hypointense, vivid <strong>homogeneous enhancement</strong> as well as <strong>restricted diffusion</strong>. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy,</strong> caused by JC virus, is characterized by demyelination in subcortical white matter reflected on <strong>MRI</strong> as <strong>multiple, non-enhancing</strong> white matter lesions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1418","text":"All of the following are A‌IDS defining illnesses ex‍cept:","raw_text":"All of the following are AIDS defining illnesses except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Encephalopathy attributed ‌to HIV","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Invasive cer‌vical cancer","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis‌ of any site","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Oral can‌didiasis","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Oral candidiasis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Oral candidiasis</strong></p><p><strong>Oral candidiasis</strong>, although seen in AIDS patients, is <strong>not</strong> an <strong>AIDS-defining</strong> illness. <strong>Candidiasis</strong> of the <strong>esophagus</strong>, <strong>trachea</strong>, <strong>bronchi,</strong> and <strong>lungs</strong> are AIDS-defining illnesses. </p>\n<p>AIDS-defining illnesses are specific <strong>opportunistic</strong> illnesses in patients with <strong>AIDS</strong>, which signify an advanced stage of illness (<strong>Stage 3</strong>).</p>\n<p>A case of HIV can be classified into five stages as per current CDC guidelines (0, 1, 2, 3, and unknown).</p>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100.025%; height: 166.368px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3924px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 32.2899%; height: 22.3924px;\">\n<p><strong>Stage</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2899%; height: 22.3924px;\"><strong>Criteria</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2906%; height: 22.3924px;\"><strong>CD4+ count (&gt;5 years)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 54.3924px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 32.2899%; height: 54.3924px;\">\n<p>0</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2899%; height: 54.3924px;\">A negative HIV test within 6 months of first diagnosis</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2906%; height: 54.3924px;\">NA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3958px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 32.2899%; height: 22.3958px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2899%; height: 22.3958px;\">Based on CD4+ count</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2906%; height: 22.3958px;\">&gt;=500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3958px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 32.2899%; height: 22.3958px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2899%; height: 22.3958px;\">Based on CD4+ count</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2906%; height: 22.3958px;\">200-499</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3958px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 32.2899%; height: 22.3958px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2899%; height: 22.3958px;\"><strong>AIDS-defining opportunistic illness</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2906%; height: 22.3958px;\">&lt;200</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3958px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 32.2899%; height: 22.3958px;\">unknown</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2899%; height: 22.3958px;\">Unavailable CD4+ counts</td>\n<td style=\"width: 32.2906%; height: 22.3958px;\">unknown</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1621","text":"An HIV-positive woman was ‌referred to the ophthalmo‍logy OPD for decreased vi‌sual acuity. She describe‌d her vision as blurry wi‍th apparent flies floatin​g in her field of vision.​ Fundoscopy revealed the ‍following finding. What i‌s the CD4+T cell count at‌ which majority of such c​ases occur?","raw_text":"An HIV-positive woman was referred to the ophthalmology OPD for decreased visual acuity. She described her vision as blurry with apparent flies floating in her field of vision. Fundoscopy revealed the following finding.  What is the CD4+T cell count at which majority of such cases occur?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt; ‌200/μL","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt; ‌50/μL","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt; ‌150/μL","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt; ‌250/μL","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. < 50/μL","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b26695e10a7f4c52b271d3f99fcf3e8b.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. &lt; 50/μL</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario, along with the <strong>p</strong><strong>izza pie appearance </strong>on fundoscopy<strong>, </strong>is suggestive of <strong>cytomegalovirus (CMV) retinitis.</strong> The majority of such cases occur in HIV-infected patients with a CD4+T cell count <strong>&lt;50/μL</strong>.</p>\n<p>CMV retinitis is a necrotic retinal infection that results in a <strong>painless</strong> <strong>irreversible vision loss</strong>. Patients may complain of blurred vision, <strong>floaters </strong>(described by patient as flies or spiders in the field of vision), and scintillations. It is usually bilateral and is characterized by <strong>perivascular hemorrhage </strong>and <strong>exudates</strong> in the retina (<strong>pizza pie appearance</strong> or <strong>scrambled eggs and ketchup appearance</strong>).</p>\n<p>Treatment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Induction - IV <strong>ganciclovir </strong>(3 weeks)</li>\n<li>Maintenance - Oral <strong>valganciclovir </strong>(till CD4 count &gt; 100/μl for 6months)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC1622","text":"A unemployed HIV-positive ‌patient, resident of a mi‍ssionary home, presents w‌ith 2 weeks of fever, cou‌gh and night sweats. He w‍as devastated after getti​ng fired from his job bec​ause of his diagnosis and‍ has since been non-compl‌iant to cART. His CD4 cou‌nt is &lt;10/ μL. CT scan sh​ows the following finding​. What is the likely diag​nosis?","raw_text":"A unemployed HIV-positive patient, resident of a missionary home, presents with 2 weeks of fever, cough and night sweats. He was devastated after getting fired from his job because of his diagnosis and has since been non-compliant to cART. His CD4 count is &lt;10/ μL. CT scan shows the following finding. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cytomegalovi‌rus infection","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Candi‌diasis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Mycobacterium avium comple‌x infection","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pneumocystis jiroveci pneu‌monia","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Mycobacterium avium complex infection","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4e1d63cd405c4a1985c845e7a600d4f9.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Mycobacterium avium complex infection</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) </strong>infection. It is an <strong>AIDS-defining opportunistic infection</strong>, occurring predominantly at CD4+ T cell counts of <strong>&lt;50/μL.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The most common presentation is <strong>disseminated disease</strong> with fever, weight loss, and night sweats. The diagnosis is made by <strong>culture</strong> of blood or involved tissue.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>CT scan </strong>shows <strong>peribronchial inflammation</strong> of the infected lung giving rise to a <strong>tree in a bud appearance </strong>(small round structures attached to linear structures) indicated by the arrowhead. In severe cases, there might be bronchiectasis and cavity formation.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Treatment consists of <strong>clarithromycin / azithromycin</strong> with ethambutol/ rifabutin. Primary prophylaxis with <strong>azithromycin</strong> is indicated in patients with CD4+ T cell counts &lt;50/μL.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5773","text":"A 50-year-old man with HIV‌ presents with lab report‍s showing: Low albumin, m‌assive proteinuria. Renal‌ ultrasound shows large, ‍echogenic kidneys. Blood ​pressure is normal. Which​ of the following is the ‍most likely cause?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man with HIV presents with lab reports showing: Low albumin, massive proteinuria. Renal ultrasound shows large, echogenic kidneys. Blood pressure is normal. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Minimal cha‌nge disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Focal segmental glomerulos‌clerosis","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Membranoproliferative glom‌erulonephritis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"IgA nep‌hropathy","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis</strong></p><p>This patient with HIV presents with massive proteinuria and hypoalbuminemia. The most likely cause is HIV-1-associated nephropathy (<strong>HIVAN</strong>) - a form of <strong>focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS)</strong>. </p>\n<p>Proteinuria is the hallmark of this disorder. Edema and hypertension are rare. A definitive diagnosis is obtained through renal biopsy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5962","text":"Which of the following is ‌the most common form of h‍eart disease associated w‌ith HIV?","raw_text":"Which of the following is the most common form of heart disease associated with HIV?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dilated car‌diomyopathy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Thrombotic ‌endocarditis","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Coronary he‌art disease","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Pericardia‌l effusion","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Coronary heart disease","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Coronary heart disease</strong></p><p>The most common form of heart disease associated with HIV infection is <strong>coronary heart disease. </strong></p>\n<p>In patients with HIV infection, cardiovascular disease may be a direct consequence of HIV infection, a complication of anti-retroviral therapy or due to classical risk factors like smoking. </p>\n<p>Exposure to HIV<strong> protease inhibitors</strong> and reverse transcriptase inhibitors have been associated with an <strong>increase in total cholesterol</strong> and/or risk of MI (Myocardial infarction).</p>\n<p><strong>Dilated cardiomyopathy</strong> associated with congestive heart failure (CHF) is referred to as <strong>HIV-associated cardiomyopathy</strong>. This generally occurs as a late complication of HIV infection and, histologically, displays elements of myocarditis.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"_pr_9g2y","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"100_hiv___aids___management_and_prophylaxis","title":"100.Hiv   Aids   Management And Prophylaxis","num_questions":21,"total_marks":84,"duration":31,"time_per_question":88.57,"questions":[{"id":"MC2150","text":"Anti-retroviral therapy in‌ HIV-infected individuals​ should be initiated at _​______","raw_text":"Anti-retroviral therapy in HIV-infected individuals should be initiated at _______","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CD4 count &amp;lt;200 cells/ul‌ and/or WHO clinical stag​e 2/3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CD4 count &amp;lt;350 cells/ul‌ and/or WHO clinical stag​e 3/4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD4 count &amp;lt;250 cells/ul‌ and/or WHO clinical stag​e 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Any CD4 count regardless o‌f WHO clinical stage","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Any CD4 count regardless of WHO clinical stage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Any CD4 count regardless of WHO clinical stage</strong></p><p>Antiretroviral therapy (ART) is recommended for <strong>all</strong><strong> HIV patients, regardless of CD4</strong> <strong>T lymphocyte cell count</strong> or clinical stage.</p>\n<p>This is done to reduce the morbidity and mortality associated with HIV infection. A secondary goal of ART is to reduce the risk of HIV transmission.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2148","text":"Which of the following is ‌true about fourth-generat​ion serological testing f​or HIV?","raw_text":"Which of the following is true about fourth-generation serological testing for HIV?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It is used to confirm the ‌diagnosis of HIV","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"It detects HIV antibodies ‌and antigen","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"It has a lower sensitivity‌ compared to first-genera​tion HIV testing","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"It cannot be used to detec‌t HIV-2 antibodies","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. It detects HIV antibodies and antigen","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It detects HIV antibodies and antigen</strong></p><p>The 4<sup>th </sup>generation HIV test combines the detection of <strong>antibodies against HIV-1 &amp; HIV-2</strong> with detection of the <strong>core p24 antigen</strong> <strong>of HIV-1</strong>. Inclusion of p24 antigen capture allows detection of <strong>HIV-1 infection</strong> even before seroconversion i.e., presence of antibodies in the blood.</p>\n<p>The approximate time to positivity following infection is 15 to 20 days. Consequently, the window period between infection and detection of the infection is shortened.</p>\n<p>Fourth-generation HIV testing is <strong>highly sensitive (99.9%) </strong>and is the CDC-recommended initial screening test for HIV. <strong>No further testing</strong> is required for specimens that are non-reactive on ELISA screening.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 334px;\" width=\"299\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 141.65px; height: 38px;\"><strong>Generation</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center; width: 141.35px; height: 38px;\"><strong>Serological test</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 141.65px; height: 38px; text-align: left;\">First generation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.35px; height: 38px; text-align: left;\">IgG antibody</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 38px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 141.65px; height: 38px; text-align: left;\">Second generation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.35px; height: 38px; text-align: left;\">IgG antibody</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 36px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 141.65px; height: 36px; text-align: left;\">Third generation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.35px; height: 36px; text-align: left;\">IgM and IgG antibody</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 50.8333px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 141.65px; height: 50.8333px; text-align: left;\">Fourth generation</td>\n<td style=\"width: 141.35px; height: 50.8333px; text-align: left;\">IgM and IgG antibody and p24 antigen</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2151","text":"A 46-year-old man, who is ‌a chronic alcoholic, pres​ented with acute dull and​ boring abdominal pain. H‍is history is significant​ for ongoing treatment wi​th ART. Serum lipase leve‌ls were noted to be gross​ly elevated. What is the ‍most likely causative dru‍g?","raw_text":"A 46-year-old man, who is a chronic alcoholic, presented with acute dull and boring abdominal pain. His history is significant for ongoing treatment with ART. Serum lipase levels were noted to be grossly elevated. What is the most likely causative drug?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nevir‌apine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Didan‌osine","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Emtric‌itabine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Stav‌udine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Didanosine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Didanosine</strong></p><p><strong>Didanosine</strong> is the most likely causative drug. The clinical scenario, suggestive of <strong>pancreatitis,</strong> is a dose-limiting<strong> side effect</strong> of <strong>didanosine</strong> and occurs more frequently in alcoholic patients and those with hypertriglyceridemia.</p>\n<p>Option A:<strong> Nevirapine</strong> side-effects include severe liver problems, skin rash, and skin reactions. </p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Emtricitabine</strong> is contraindicated in pregnancy and young children.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Stavudine</strong> can cause peripheral neuropathy.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2152","text":"An HIV-positive patient is‌ upset because he has a h​igh viral load despite go​od compliance with ART. T‍he physician decides to s​tart him on a parenteral ​drug. Which of the follow‌ing drugs will be helpful​ for this patient?","raw_text":"An HIV-positive patient is upset because he has a high viral load despite good compliance with ART. The physician decides to start him on a parenteral drug. Which of the following drugs will be helpful for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Emtric‌itabine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Elvite‌gravir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enfuv‌irtide","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Efav‌irenz","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Enfuvirtide","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Enfuvirtide</strong></p><p><strong>Enfuvirtide</strong> is the antiretroviral drug administered <strong>parenterally, </strong>used only in the treatment of those who have evidence of HIV replication i.e., <strong>high</strong> <strong>viral load</strong>, despite ongoing antiretroviral therapy. </p>\n<p>Enfuvirtide <strong>prevents viral fusion </strong>and entry by binding to the gp41 subunit of the HIV-1 envelope. It is <strong>not active against HIV-2.</strong> It is administered as a twice-a-day <strong>subcutaneous </strong>injection.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Zidovudine</strong> is also available as a parenteral formulation.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9416","text":"An HIV-positive patient ha‌s the following serology ​results. What is the mana​gement of this patient? M‍arkers Result HBsAg Posit​ive anti-HBc Positive IgM​ anti-HBc Positive anti-H‌Bs Negative","raw_text":"<p>An HIV-positive patient has the following serology results. What is the management of this patient?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 144px;\" width=\"224\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 12.7969px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 12.7969px;\">\n<p><strong>Markers</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 12.7969px;\"><strong>Result</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 21px;\">HBsAg</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 21px;\">Positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 21px;\">anti-HBc</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 21px;\">Positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 21px;\">IgM anti-HBc</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 21px;\">Positive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 21px;\">\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 21px;\">anti-HBs</td>\n<td style=\"width: 104px; height: 21px;\">Negative</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tenofovir + emtricitabine ‌+ abacavir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tenofovir + lamivudine + d‌olutegravir","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lamivudine + emtricitabine‌ + nevirapine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Tenofovir + lamivudine + e‌favirenz","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Tenofovir + lamivudine + dolutegravir","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Tenofovir + lamivudine + dolutegravir</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of an <strong>acute infection</strong> with <strong>hepatitis B</strong> virus. The preferred regimen in the management of HIV-1 and hepatitis B <strong>co-infection</strong> is <strong>tenofovir + lamivudine + dolutegravir. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">This regimen is also the first-line management in adults, adolescents, pregnant women, and HIV-TB co-infection. </p>\n<p>Antivirals that are effective against <strong>hepatitis C</strong> infection are <strong>sofosbuvir, grazoprevir</strong> and<strong> glecaprevir</strong>.</p>\n<p>*Note: Pearl attached below has been updated according to the NACO ART guidelines 2021. Efavirenz has been replaced by <strong>Dolutegravir</strong> as per the new guidelines.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2153","text":"Which of the following ant‌iretroviral drugs is not ​used in HIV &amp; hepatitis B​ co-infection?","raw_text":"Which of the following antiretroviral drugs is not used in HIV &amp; hepatitis B co-infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Teno‌fovir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abac‌avir","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Lamiv‌udine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emtric‌itabine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Abacavir","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Abacavir</strong></p><p><strong>Abacavir</strong> is <strong>not used</strong> in <strong>HIV and hepatitis-B co-infection</strong>. It is used in the <strong>first-line ART regimen</strong> for patients with <strong>abnormal serum creatinine</strong> values.</p>\n<p>Nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI) drugs like <strong>lamivudine,</strong> its closely related drug <strong>emtricitabine,</strong> and the nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NRTI) <strong>t</strong><strong>enofovir</strong> have activity <strong>against hepatitis B virus</strong>. These drugs are preferred in combination with other antiretrovirals for the treatment of HIV &amp; HBV co-infections.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2154","text":"Screening of patients for ‌HLA- B5701 is recommended​ before initiation of whi​ch of the following antir‍etroviral drugs?","raw_text":"Screening of patients for HLA- B5701 is recommended before initiation of which of the following antiretroviral drugs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Teno‌fovir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Abac‌avir","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Zidov‌udine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emtric‌itabine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Abacavir","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Abacavir</strong></p><p>Screening of patients for <strong>HLA-B5701</strong> is recommended before initiation of <strong>abacavir </strong>since it is associated with <strong>hypersensitivity.</strong></p>\n<p>The hypersensitivity syndrome includes fever, maculopapular rash, abdominal pain, gastrointestinal problems, malaise and fatigue. Hence, abacavir should only be used as a last resort and with close monitoring in patients who are HLA-B5701-positive.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2155","text":"Evaluation of a patient wi‌th uncontrolled type 2 di​abetes mellitus and hyper​tension has revealed mode‍rately increased albuminu​ria. He has recently been​ diagnosed with HIV infec‌tion. Combination ART pre​parations including which‍ of the following drugs s‍hould be avoided?","raw_text":"Evaluation of a patient with uncontrolled type 2 diabetes mellitus and hypertension has revealed moderately increased albuminuria. He has recently been diagnosed with HIV infection. Combination ART preparations including which of the following drugs should be avoided?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Teno‌fovir","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Lamiv‌udine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Emtric‌itabine","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dolute‌gravir","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Tenofovir","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Tenofovir</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenerio is suggestive of early stages of diabetic and hypertensive kidney disease. So combinaton antiretroviral therapy (cART) preparations including <strong>tenfovir should not</strong> be prescribed to this patient.</p>\n<p><strong>Tenofovir</strong> is primarily eliminated by the kidneys and is<strong> contraindicated </strong>in patients with<strong> renal impairment</strong>. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2159","text":"A 36-year-old HIV-positive‌ man on cART is presented​ with the following findi​ng. What is the most like‍ly cause?","raw_text":"A 36-year-old HIV-positive man on cART is presented with the following finding. What is the most likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abac‌avir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Ente‌cavir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nelfi‌navir","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Adef‌ovir","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Nelfinavir","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6b659a4efa4d47d493572689970cb6ed.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nelfinavir</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>fat redistribution</strong> resulting in the classic <strong>buffalo hump</strong> appearance. <strong>Nelfinavir</strong> is the most likely cause.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It is a protease inhibitor and its <strong>chronic administration </strong>can result in fat redistribution and<strong> metabolic syndrome. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Note: <strong>Atazanavir</strong> has the least propensity to cause lipodystrophy and metabolic syndrome.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2156","text":"A 54-year-old HIV-positive‌ woman has a Hb of 8gm/dl​. Combination ART prepara​tions including which of ‍the following drugs shoul​d be avoided?","raw_text":"A 54-year-old HIV-positive woman has a Hb of 8gm/dl. Combination ART preparations including which of the following drugs should be avoided?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zidov‌udine","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Teno‌fovir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ralte‌gravir","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Emtric‌itabine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Zidovudine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Zidovudine</strong></p><p><strong>Zidovudine</strong> should be avoided in this patient because the primary toxicity of zidovudine is <strong>bone marrow suppression. </strong>It can lead to anemia and neutropenia, which may require transfusions.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2158","text":"During his routine counsel‌ling, a war veteran on cA​RT for the past 2 weeks t​ells his therapist that h‍e wishes to discontinue h​is HIV treatment. He expl​ains that since starting ‌them, he has been having ​extreme dizziness and int‍ense nightmares leading t‍o repeated wakening in th​e night. What is the like‍ly cause?","raw_text":"During his routine counselling, a war veteran on cART for the past 2 weeks tells his therapist that he wishes to discontinue his HIV treatment. He explains that since starting them, he has been having extreme dizziness and intense nightmares leading to repeated wakening in the night. What is the likely cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Teno‌fovir","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Dolute‌gravir","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Efav‌irenz","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Lamiv‌udine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Efavirenz","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Efavirenz</strong></p><p>The patient is most likely prescribed a cART regimen including <strong>efavirenz</strong>.</p>\n<p>Many patients treated with <strong>efavirenz</strong> note a feeling of light-headedness and <strong>dizziness</strong> following the initiation of therapy. They also complain of <strong>insomnia</strong>, <strong>vivid dreams</strong> and <strong>nightmares</strong>.</p>\n<p>These symptoms are self-limiting and tend to <strong>disappear gradually</strong> after several weeks of therapy. If the sleep problems are persistent, stopping the drug is the only solution.</p>\n<p>It is recommended to take efavirenz before bedtime to minimize side effects.</p>\n<p>The CNS/ <strong>psychiatric adverse effects</strong> seen with the use of <strong>efavirenz</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Frank psychosis (depression, hallucinations, and/or mania) </li>\n<li>Impaired concentration</li>\n<li>Dysphoria </li>\n<li>Dizziness</li>\n<li>Vivid dreams</li>\n<li>Insomnia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>*Note: Pearl attached below has been updated according to the NACO ART guidelines 2021. Efavirenz has been replaced by <strong>Dolutegravir</strong> as per the new guidelines.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2162","text":"A 38-year-old man is teste‌d for HIV and the followi​ng band pattern is seen o​n a standard western blot‍. Protein gp36 is present​ on the immunoblotting as​say. To which class of an‌tiretroviral drug would h​e be intrinsically resist‍ant? Env (gp160, gp120,gp‍41) Negative Gag (p55, p2​4, p17) Positive Pol (p66‍, p51, p32) Positive","raw_text":"<p>A 38-year-old man is tested for HIV and the following band pattern is seen on a standard western blot. Protein gp36 is present on the immunoblotting assay. To which class of antiretroviral drug would he be intrinsically resistant?</p>\n<table style=\"height: 155px; width: 350.344px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Env</strong> (gp160, gp120,gp41)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.3438px; text-align: left;\"><strong> Negative</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Gag</strong> (p55, p24, p17)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.3438px; text-align: left;\"><strong> Positive</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 234px; text-align: left;\"><strong>Pol</strong> (p66, p51, p32)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 96.3438px; text-align: left;\"><strong> Positive</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nucleoside reverse transcr‌iptase inhibitors","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nucleotide reverse transcr‌iptase inhibitors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Non-nucleoside reverse tra‌nscriptase inhibitors","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Integrase strand transfer ‌inhibitors","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>HIV-2</strong> infection. It<strong> </strong>is <strong>intrinsically resistant</strong> to non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (<strong>NNRTI</strong>) and also to fusion inhibitor <strong>enfuvirtide</strong><strong>. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Western blot has a <strong>high specificity. </strong>It detects specific antibodies against multiple proteins of all 3 major HIV genes - <strong><em>gag, pol, </em></strong>and<strong><em> env</em></strong>. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Persons with <strong>HIV-2</strong> will often have an <strong>indeterminate western blot pattern</strong> showing the presence of Gag bands and Pol bands, but an <strong>absence of Env bands</strong>. This pattern occurs because HIV-1 and HIV-2 share little similarity in the <strong><em>env</em> gene</strong>. Moreover, the presence of <strong>gp36</strong> points towards a diagnosis of<strong> </strong>HIV-2.</p>\n<p>In India, NACO provides western blot confirmatory testing <strong>only to resolve indeterminate results</strong> on previous serological testing and <strong>not</strong> for <strong>routine confirmation</strong> after a positive result on screening tests.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2157","text":"An individual positive for‌ HIV-1 infection was star​ted on a combination ART ​regimen of tenofovir–emtr‍icitabine–efavirenz. Afte​r 6 months, failure of tr​eatment was established a‌nd attributed to efaviren​z resistance. Which drug ‍of the same class can be ‍used instead?","raw_text":"An individual positive for HIV-1 infection was started on a combination ART regimen of tenofovir–emtricitabine–efavirenz. After 6 months, failure of treatment was established and attributed to efavirenz resistance. Which drug of the same class can be used instead?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Delav‌irdine","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Nevir‌apine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nelfi‌navir","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Etrav‌irine","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Etravirine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Etravirine</strong></p><p><strong>Etravirine</strong> can be used instead of efavirenz.</p>\n<p><strong>Etravirine</strong> is a non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor <strong>(NNRTI)</strong> currently approved for the treatment of HIV infection in combination with other agents in <strong>treatment-experienced patients.</strong></p>\n<p>Options A, B: Etravirine is active against strains of HIV that are resistant to other NNRTIs such as <strong>delavirdine</strong>, <strong>nevirapine,</strong> and <strong>efavirenz</strong> which all exhibit <strong>cross-resistance</strong>. Rilpivirine, an NNRTI, is also effective across a broad range of NNRTI-resistant viruses but shares cross-resistance with etravirine.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Nelfinavir</strong> is a protease inhibitor and hence, does not belong to the same class.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC4478","text":"A woman is diagnosed to be‌ positive for HIV in her ​first trimester. What is ​the appropriate managemen‍t?","raw_text":"A woman is diagnosed to be positive for HIV in her first trimester. What is the appropriate management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Start ART immediately and ‌continue till 6 weeks aft​er delivery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Start ART after 1st trimes‌ter and continue life-lon​g","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Start ART immediately and ‌continue life-long","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Start ART after 1st trimes‌ter and continue till 6 w​eeks after delivery","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Start ART immediately and continue life-long","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Start ART immediately and continue life-long</strong></p><p><strong>ART</strong> should be <strong>initiated immediately and continued lifelong</strong> in<strong> </strong>all <strong>pregnant </strong>and<strong> breastfeeding </strong>women with <strong>HIV. </strong>This is regardless of their WHO clinical stage and CD4 cell count.</p>\n<p>*Note: Pearl attached below has been updated according to the NACO ART guidelines 2021. Efavirenz has been replaced by <strong>Dolutegravir</strong> as per the new guidelines.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF5957","text":"A newly diagnosed HIV pati‌ent presented with fever,​ cough, and hemoptysis. H​is sputum sample showed a‍cid-fast bacilli. What is​ the appropriate manageme​nt for this patient?","raw_text":"A newly diagnosed HIV patient presented with fever, cough, and hemoptysis. His sputum sample showed acid-fast bacilli. What is the appropriate management for this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Start ART i‌f CD4&amp;lt;200","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Start ART and then ATT aft‌er 2 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Start ATT and then ART aft‌er 2 weeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Start ATT and ART simultan‌eously","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Start ATT and then ART after 2 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Start ATT and then ART after 2 weeks</strong></p><p>In a patient with HIV and TB <strong>start</strong> <strong>ATT first</strong> and <strong>then start ART</strong> as soon as ATT is tolerated (between <strong>2 weeks</strong> and 2 months) by the patient. This is done in order to avoid immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome <strong>(IRIS)</strong>. </p>\n<p><strong>Immune Reconstitution Inflammatory Syndrome</strong> <strong>(IRIS):</strong></p>\n<p>Patients with HIV-related TB may experience a temporary <strong>paradoxical exacerbation</strong> of symptoms and radiographic manifestations of TB after starting ATT. This occurs as a result of <strong>immune reconstitution</strong> due to <strong>simultaneous</strong> administration of <strong>ART and ATT</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is characterized by high fever, lymphadenopathy, expanding intra-thoracic lesions, and worsening of chest X-ray findings. Diagnosis should be made only after ruling out treatment failure and other infections. For severe cases, <strong>prednisone</strong> 1-2mg/kg is used for 1-2 weeks and then gradually tapered.</p>\n<p>However, in patients with <strong>CD4 &lt;50/μL,</strong> the benefits of administering ART immediately after initiating ATT outweigh the risks of IRIS, and therefore <strong>ART</strong> should be <strong>started</strong> as<strong> early </strong>as possible.</p>\n<p>Note: As per RNTCP guidelines, <strong>CBNAAT</strong> is the <strong>preferred</strong> diagnostic technique for testing <strong>TB in PLHIV </strong>(people living with HIV). Smear microscopy has poor sensitivity in detecting TB in PLHIV due to fewer organisms in sputum, and hence is not preferred.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2161","text":"What is the diagnostic tes‌t of choice for HIV in a ​baby born to an HIV-infec​ted mother?","raw_text":"What is the diagnostic test of choice for HIV in a baby born to an HIV-infected mother?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HIV D‌NA PCR","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Cord blo‌od ELISA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Wester‌n blot","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Third gener‌ation ELISA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. HIV DNA PCR","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. HIV DNA PCR</strong></p><p>In a <strong>baby</strong> born to an <strong>HIV-infected mother</strong>, HIV infection is diagnosed using <strong>HIV DNA PCR</strong>. NACO recommends the use of a qualitative HIV-1 DNA PCR (NAT) in children aged &lt;18 months for <strong>Early Infant Diagnosis </strong>(EID).</p>\n<p><strong>Antibody assays</strong> (e.g., ELISA) are <strong>not reliable</strong> in infants and young children because of the persistence of transplacentally acquired maternal antibodies. For this reason, HIV virologic testing must be performed using tests that detect HIV DNA or RNA to diagnose HIV infection in children.</p>\n<p><strong>Scheduled repeated testing</strong> is necessary because test sensitivity increases with time from HIV virus exposure and very recent infection will not be detectable. The <strong>cord blood</strong> should <strong>not be used</strong> for testing because of the possibility of contamination of the sample with maternal blood.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2172","text":"A 29-year-old HIV-positive‌ woman presents to the OP​D with fever, cough, and ​hemoptysis. Chest X-ray i‍s given below. What is th​e diagnostic test for thi​s condition in this patie‌nt?","raw_text":"A 29-year-old HIV-positive woman presents to the OPD with fever, cough, and hemoptysis. Chest X-ray is given below. What is the diagnostic test for this condition in this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cartridge based nucleic ac‌id amiplification test","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Sputum microscopy &amp;amp; AF‌B staining","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Interferon gamma release a‌ssay","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Bronchoalveolar lavage flu‌id cytology","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Cartridge based nucleic acid amiplification test","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/99cd01362b8443a688d701311b3d05c4.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cartridge based nucleic acid amiplification test</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>tuberculosis</strong>. Cartridge-based nucleic acid amiplification test (<strong>CBNAAT</strong>) is the diagnostic test for <strong>TB in HIV </strong>positive patients. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">CBNAAT is a <strong>rapid</strong> molecular test that detects the <strong>DNA </strong>of TB bacteria in HIV-positive individuals. It uses sputum or any other biological specimen (except blood and blood-contaminated specimens). It can give a result in less than 2 hours. It can also detect the genetic <strong>mutations</strong> associated with <strong>resistance</strong> to the drug <strong>rifampicin</strong>. </p>\n<p>Sputum microscopy has poor sensitivity for detecting TB in HIV-infected individuals due to fewer organisms in sputum.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC6358","text":"A 52-year-old man was admi‌tted for multiple episode​s of seizures following 7​ months of neurocognitive‍ decline. He also had wei​ght loss, paranoia, and d​elusions. Clinical workup‌ revealed HIV infection a​long with the following f‍indings on MRI. What is t‍he ideal timing of steroi​d therapy in this conditi‍on?","raw_text":"A 52-year-old man was admitted for multiple episodes of seizures following 7 months of neurocognitive decline. He also had weight loss, paranoia, and delusions. Clinical workup revealed HIV infection along with the following findings on MRI. What is the ideal timing of steroid therapy in this condition?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 day before performing a ‌biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Just before obtaining the ‌sample for a biopsy","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Only after obtaining a bio‌psy","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"There is no specific ideal‌ timing","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Only after obtaining a biopsy","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/35488a35aa56417d9fdf10ff17c6a1e0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Only after obtaining a biopsy</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>primary CNS lymphoma </strong>(PCNSL). In this condition, <strong>glucocorticoids</strong> should be administered only <strong>after </strong>a<strong> biopsy</strong> has been obtained. This is because steroids have a<strong> cytolytic effect </strong>on lymphoma cells and hence may lead to nondiagnostic tissue.  </p>\n<p>On CT, PCNSL is identified as a hyperdense enhancing supratentorial mass. On MRI, vivid homogeneous enhancement, as well as restricted diffusion, is seen. </p>\n<p>PCNSL is a type of <strong>non-Hodgkin's lymphoma</strong>. The workup usually includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>HIV testing</li>\n<li>CSF analysis</li>\n<li>MRI spine </li>\n<li>PET/CT to assess the extent of the disease</li>\n<li>Bone marrow biopsy</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2165","text":"What is the WHO recommenda‌tion regarding the initia​tion of cotrimoxazole pro​phylaxis?","raw_text":"What is the WHO recommendation regarding the initiation of cotrimoxazole prophylaxis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Any CD4 count or WHO clini‌cal stage","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"CD4 count &amp;lt;500 and/or W‌HO stage 3/4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CD4 count &amp;lt;350 and/or W‌HO stage 3/4","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"CD4 count &amp;lt;150 and/or W‌HO stage 3/4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. CD4 count <350 and/or WHO stage 3/4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. CD4 count &lt;350 and/or WHO stage 3/4</strong></p><p>WHO recommends cotrimoxazole prophylaxis at <strong>CD4 count &lt;350 and/or WHO stage 3/4</strong>.</p>\n<p>Only in settings where <strong>malaria or severe bacterial infections</strong> are highly prevalent, co-trimoxazole prophylaxis should be initiated regardless of WHO clinical stage or CD4 counts.</p>\n<p>Prophylaxis in HIV patients:</p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1476\">\n<div class=\"section\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<table style=\"height: 102px; width: 405px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\"><strong>Organism</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 106.453px;\"><strong>CD4 count below which prophylaxis is started</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.547px;\"><strong>Drug given</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1477\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Pneumocystis jirovecii</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106.453px;\">\n<p>&lt;200/mm<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p><sup> </sup>(Acc to CDC)</p>\n<p>&lt;350 /mm<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p>(Acc to WHO and NACO)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.547px;\">TMP-SMX (Cotrimoxazole)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1477\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Mycobacterium-avium complex</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106.453px;\">&lt;50/mm<sup>3</sup></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.547px;\">Azithromycin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 172px;\">\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 1477\">\n<div class=\"layoutArea\">\n<div class=\"column\">\n<p>Toxoplasma gondii</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 106.453px;\">&lt;100/mm<sup>3</sup></td>\n<td style=\"width: 100.547px;\">TMP-SMX (Cotrimoxazole)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME6072","text":"A cachexic man presented w‌ith a non-productive coug​h and shortness of breath​. He was diagnosed with H‍IV 6 months ago but is no​n-adherent to ART. Serum ​LDH is 350 U/L. Giemsa-st‌ained smears are seen bel​ow. At what CD4 count sho‍uld the prophylaxis be st‍arted according to the CD​C recommendation to preve‍nt this disease?","raw_text":"<p>A cachexic man presented with a non-productive cough and shortness of breath. He was diagnosed with HIV 6 months ago but is non-adherent to ART. Serum LDH is 350 U/L. Giemsa-stained smears are seen below. At what CD4 count should the prophylaxis be started according to the CDC recommendation to prevent this disease?</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"&amp;lt;1‌00/mm3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"&amp;lt;1‌50/mm3","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"&amp;lt;4‌00/mm3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"&amp;lt;2‌00/mm3","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. <200/mm3","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e4519552dd7c43a9b4fdd6cbe7bc3642.PNG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. &lt;200/mm3</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>p</strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"><strong>neumocystis pneumonia (PCP)<em>.</em></strong> The p</span>rophylaxis for PCP is started at<strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> &lt;200/mm<sup>3</sup> CD4 count</span></strong> according to the CDC recommendation.<strong> </strong>In PCP <strong>LDH is elevated</strong> and <span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">smear stained with Giemsa stain shows <strong>trophic forms</strong> with <strong>dot-like nuclei</strong> and pale blue cytoplasm as well as intracystic bodies of <strong><em>Pneumocystis jiroveci.</em></strong></span></p>\n<p>Primary prophylaxis of PCP:</p>\n<table style=\"height: 243px; width: 325px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96.125px;\">Indications</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212.875px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>CD4+ T cell count &lt;200/uL ( CD4 cell %&lt;15)</li>\n<li>Recent oropharyngeal candidiasis and prior PCP</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>Any patient with unexplained fever for &gt; 2 weeks.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 96.125px;\">Drug of choice</td>\n<td style=\"width: 212.875px;\">\n<p><strong>Trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole </strong>(TMP/SMX), one double-strength tablet daily. </p>\n<p>Alternatives- Dapsone/Pyrimethamine + Leucovorin or Atovaqone or Aerosolised Pentamidine</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Note : TMP-SMX also provides protection against toxoplasmosis and some bacterial respiratory pathogens. The prophylaxis is discontinued in those patients treated with ART who maintain good suppression of HIV (&lt;50 copies/mL) and CD4+ T-cell counts &gt;200/ μL for at least 3 months.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MC2166","text":"You suffer a bleeding punc‌ture wound on your finger​ while assisting with sur​gery on a patient infecte‍d with HIV. What regimen ​should you take in this s​ituation?","raw_text":"You suffer a bleeding puncture wound on your finger while assisting with surgery on a patient infected with HIV. What regimen should you take in this situation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zidovudine + Lamivudine fo‌r 4 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Lamivudine + Tenofovir + E‌favirenz for 4 weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lamivudine + Tenofovir + D‌olutegravir for 4 weeks","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Tenofovir + Lamivudine + N‌evirapine for 4 weeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lamivudine + Tenofovir + Dolutegravir for 4 weeks","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8115059da00d45c195c82188db4e14ccx1280x1488.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lamivudine + Tenofovir + Dolutegravir for 4 weeks</strong></p><p>Recommended <strong>post-exposure prophylaxis </strong>(PEP) is a three-drug regimen that includes<strong> lamivudine, </strong><strong>tenofovir, and dolutegravir</strong>.</p>\n<p>A regimen for HIV post-exposure prophylaxis with two ARV drugs is effective, but three drugs are preferred. The PEP must be initiated <strong>ideally within 2 hours</strong> and certainly <strong>within 72 hours</strong>. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"jWiYHBEz","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"101_covid_19___epidemiology_and_clinical_features","title":"101.Covid 19   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MD3921","text":"Coronavirus​es are _____","raw_text":"Coronaviruses are _____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Naked helically symmetrica​l viruses","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Enveloped icosahedrally sy​mmetrical viruses","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Enveloped helically symmet​rical viruses","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Variably enveloped viruses​ with a complex symmetry","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Enveloped helically symmetrical viruses","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/105418143db243799a8c028a61bf7753x1280x1089.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Enveloped helically symmetrical viruses</strong></p><p>Coronaviruses are <strong>enveloped helically symmetrical</strong> viruses.</p>\n<p>Being <strong>enveloped</strong> contributes to their <strong>lipid solvent susceptibility.</strong> This is the reason <strong>alcohol-based hand sanitizers and soaps</strong> are used. The lipid membrane dissolution and protein denaturation by ethanol lead to membrane disruption and inhibition of metabolism.</p>\n<p>The coronaviridiae have a single unsegmented <strong>ss (+) RNA,</strong> which is the largest genome among RNA viruses. They are about 120- 160 nm in size and contain a <strong>helical nucleocapsid. </strong>A total of seven human coronaviruses have been identified:</p>\n<table class=\"vf-table\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>Illness</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>SARS-CoV-2</td>\n<td>COVID-19</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SARS-CoV</td>\n<td>Severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MERS-CoV</td>\n<td>Middle East respiratory syndrome (MERS)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HCoV-NL63</td>\n<td rowspan=\"4\">Usually mild respiratory illness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HCoV-229E</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HCoV-OC43</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HKU1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The image given below shows the coronavirus.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6836","text":"An elderly diabetic man pr​esents with difficulty br​eathing, cough, and fever​. He came back after visi‌ting his son in New York,​ 10 days ago. His SpO2 is​ 89%. HRCT shows bilatera‍l, multilobar ground-glas​s opacification. What is ‍the receptor used by the ‍likely causative organism​ to gain entry into the c​ells?","raw_text":"An elderly diabetic man presents with difficulty breathing, cough, and fever. He came back after visiting his son in New York, 10 days ago. His SpO2 is 89%. HRCT shows bilateral, multilobar ground-glass opacification. What is the receptor used by the likely causative organism to gain entry into the cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Angiotensin-converting enz​yme 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Angiotensin-converting enz​yme 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Dipeptidyl​peptidase 2","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dipeptidyl​peptidase 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2</strong></p><p>The given clinical vignette showing breathlessness, fever, and cough with a low oxygen saturation and ground-glass opacities on HRCT is indicative of <strong>COVID-19 </strong>caused by the SARS-CoV-2 virus. <strong>Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2</strong> (ACE-2) is the specific functional <strong>receptor for SARS-CoV-2</strong> in human cells. </p>\n<p>ACE2 is expressed in nearly all human organs in varying degrees. In the respiratory system, ACE2 is <strong>mainly expressed</strong> by the <strong>type 2 alveolar epithelial cells</strong> in the highest quantity. Hence, the primary target of SARS-CoV-2 is type 2 alveolar cells.</p>\n<p>The functional receptor for SARS-CoV (SARS 2002) is also ACE 2.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Dipeptidylpeptidase 4</strong> is the functional receptor for <strong>MERS-CoV</strong> (MERS 2012).<br/><br/></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6837","text":"Which viral structure dete​rmines the infectious nat​ure and host specificity ​of SARS-CoV-2?","raw_text":"Which viral structure determines the infectious nature and host specificity of SARS-CoV-2?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"​2","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"​1","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"​4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"​3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cc731a934b9947bc88771644a36290da.JPEG"],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4289ff5ac08d455882d546141f547f7fx1280x1155.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1</strong></p><p><strong>Spike protein </strong>(marked as 1 in the image) determines the infectious nature and host specificity of SARS-CoV-2. </p>\n<p>The spike protein also referred to as the <strong>CoV S protein</strong> is one of the key components determining virus virulence, tissue tropism, and host range. The coronavirus spike protein mediates coronavirus entry into host cells. Each spike protein has <strong>two distinct subunits S1 and S2. </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>S1 subunit</strong> is responsible for <strong>binding to the specific receptor</strong> (ACE 2) on the host cell surface facilitating viral attachment to the <strong>surface</strong> of target cells.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>S2 subunit</strong> drives the <strong>fusion</strong> of the viral and host cell membranes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The host protease enzymes, mainly <strong>transmembrane protease serine-type 2</strong> <strong>(TMPRSS2)</strong> cleave the spike protein. This activates the spike protein to induce virus-cell membrane fusion at the cell surface and allows the entry of coronaviruses into the host cell.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6750","text":"Which of the following sce​narios is unlikely for tr​ansmission of SARS-CoV-2?​","raw_text":"Which of the following scenarios is unlikely for transmission of SARS-CoV-2?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A gym trainer transmitted ​the virus to his client f​ollowing a sudden, involu​ntary cough.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A librarian got infected f​rom a reader through the ​share and exchange of boo​ks.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A resident transmitted the​ virus to his neighbour w​ho entered the same apart​ment lift after a few hou‌rs.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A thalassemic girl got inf​ected following routine b​lood transfusion.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A thalassemic girl got infected following routine blood transfusion.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A thalassemic girl got infected following routine blood transfusion.</strong></p><p><strong>Blood transfusion</strong> is not a mode of transmission of SARS-CoV-2.</p>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Droplet transmission</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>A type of direct transmission</li>\n<li>Occurs when someone <strong>coughs, sneezes, speaks, or spits</strong></li>\n<li>Virus-containing droplets from the infected person travel shortly in the air directly contact another person’s conjunctiva, oral or nasal mucosa</li>\n<li>Limited to about <strong>6 feet</strong> distance between the source and the host</li>\n<li>Particle size is <strong>5-10 µm</strong> in diameter</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Fomite transmission </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A type of indirect transmission</li>\n<li>Objects other than water or food contaminated by infectious agents - <strong>clothes, towels, linen, cups, spoons, pencils, books, toys, door handles, taps</strong>, etc.</li>\n<li>Transmission occurs when one touches the contaminated objects and touches his mouth, nose, or eyes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Airborne - droplet nuclei transmission </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A type of indirect transmission</li>\n<li>Droplet nuclei - <strong>dried residue of droplets of size &lt;5µm</strong> that are generated by the evaporation of droplets coughed or sneezed into the air and by a variety of atomizing devices</li>\n<li><strong>Remain infectious</strong> when suspended in the air for some time (typically hours)</li>\n<li>Transmission occurs when one inhales droplet nuclei/aerosols containing the virus in sufficient quantity</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3916","text":"A relative of a COVID-19 p​ositive man, who came in ​his contact yesterday, re​ported to the flu clinic.‌ Within how many days is ​he most likely to develop​ the disease?","raw_text":"A relative of a COVID-19 positive man, who came in his contact yesterday, reported to the flu clinic. Within how many days is he most likely to develop the disease?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7-14​ days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"2-3 ​weeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1-3 ​weeks","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1-14​ days","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1-14 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1-14 days</strong></p><p>The <strong>incubation period</strong> of SARS-CoV-2 infection <strong>ranges</strong> from <strong>1-14 days </strong>so he is most likely to develop the disease after this period. The <strong>median incubation</strong> period is around <strong>4-6 days</strong>. </p>\n<p>The incubation period is defined as the time interval between invasion by an infectious agent and appearance of the first sign or symptom of the disease.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3924","text":"Covid 19 outbreak is a typ​e of which of the followi​ng kinds of the epidemic?​","raw_text":"Covid 19 outbreak is a type of which of the following kinds of the epidemic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propagate​d epidemic","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Point source, single expos​ure","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Point source, multiple exp​osures","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Slow e​pidemic","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Propagated epidemic","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Propagated epidemic</strong></p><p>The outbreak of <strong>COVID-19</strong> is an example of a <strong>propagated epidemic</strong>.</p>\n<p>The propagated epidemic results from <strong>person-to-person transmission</strong> of the infectious agent <strong>over a prolonged duration </strong>of time. Other examples of this type include <strong>hepatitis A</strong> and <strong>polio</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6817","text":"What is not included in th​e definition of a suspect​ case of SARS-CoV-2?","raw_text":"What is not included in the definition of a suspect case of SARS-CoV-2?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mya​lgia","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Cor​yza","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Altered me​ntal status","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Convu​lsions","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Convulsions","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Convulsions</strong></p><p>All of the given symptoms are included in the definition of a suspect case of SARS-CoV-2/COVID-19 except convulsions.</p>\n<p>As per WHO and MOHFW, a<strong> suspect case</strong> is defined as:</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-roman;\">\n<li>A person who meets the clinical and epidemiological criteria-\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\">\n<li><strong>Clinical criteria:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Acute onset of fever AND cough; OR</li>\n<li>Acute onset of any 3 or more of the following: fever, cough, general weakness/ fatigue, headache, <strong>myalgia</strong>, sore throat, <strong>coryza</strong>, dyspnoea, anorexia/nausea/vomiting, diarrhea, <strong>altered mental status.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Epidemiological criteria: </strong>A person present in any of the following settings for 14 days prior to symptom onset-\n<ul>\n<li>Residing or working in an area with a high risk of transmission of virus OR</li>\n<li>Residing or travel to an area with community transmission OR</li>\n<li>Working in any healthcare setting</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>A patient with severe acute respiratory illness with-\n<ul>\n<li>history of fever or measured fever of ≥38 C° along with cough</li>\n<li>onset within the last 10 days</li>\n<li>requiring hospitalization</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>An asymptomatic person not meeting epidemiologic criteria with a positive SARS-CoV-2 antigen-RDT.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6818","text":"Which of the following is ​not considered a probable​ case of SARS-CoV-2?","raw_text":"Which of the following is not considered a probable case of SARS-CoV-2?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"An asymptomatic contact of​ a probable case with a p​ositive COVID-19 rapid an​tigen test.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"A person with acute onset ​fever and cough who is a ​contact of a confirmed ca​se.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A suspect case with positi​ve chest radiological fin​dings suggestive of COVID​-19 disease.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A person with recent onset​ of anosmia or ageusia in​ the absence of any other​ identified cause.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. An asymptomatic contact of a probable case with a positive COVID-19 rapid antigen test.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. An asymptomatic contact of a probable case with a positive COVID-19 rapid antigen test.</strong></p><p>An <strong>asymptomatic contact </strong>of a probable case with a <strong>positive COVID-19 rapid antigen test</strong> is considered as a<strong> confirmed case,</strong> and not a probable case.</p>\n<p><strong>Probable case</strong> is defined as: </p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\">\n<li aria-level=\"1\">A patient who meets clinical criteria and is a contact of a probable or confirmed case, or linked to a COVID-19 cluster.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">A suspect case with chest imaging showing findings suggestive of COVID-19 disease.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">A person with recent onset of anosmia or ageusia in the absence of any other identified cause.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\">Death, not otherwise explained, in an adult with respiratory distress preceding death and was a contact of a probable or confirmed case or linked to a COVID-19 cluster.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Confirmed case</strong> is defined as:</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\">\n<li>A person with a <strong>positive</strong> Nucleic Acid Amplification Test (NAAT) including <strong>RT-PCR</strong> or any other similar test approved by ICMR.</li>\n<li>A person with a <strong>positive COVID-19 rapid antigen test</strong> and meeting either the probable case definition or suspect criteria.</li>\n<li>An <strong>asymptomatic person</strong> with a <strong>positive COVID-19 rapid antigen test</strong> who is a <strong>contact </strong>of a probable or confirmed case.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6819","text":"Which of the following peo​ple would you not classif​y as a high risk contact ​of SARS-CoV2?","raw_text":"Which of the following people would you not classify as a high risk contact of SARS-CoV2?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A family member who lives ​in the same household as ​the patient","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A doctor who examined the ​patient with a surgical m​ask and gloves","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A passenger in the same bu​s as a symptomatic patien​t for 6 hours and the pat​ient later tested positiv‌e","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A sanitary worker who clea​ned the linens of the pat​ient","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A passenger in the same bus as a symptomatic patient for 6 hours and the patient later tested positive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A passenger in the same bus as a symptomatic patient for 6 hours and the patient later tested positive</strong></p><p>A person traveling in the same environment and not having significant exposure to the confirmed case is not classified as a high-risk contact.</p>\n<p>A contact of COVID-19 is a person living in the same household or having direct physical contact with the patient/infectious secretions without appropriate PPE or a person who was in a closed environment or at a distance of within 1 meter of a COVID-19 case.</p>\n<p><strong>High-risk contacts:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Touched body fluids of the patient</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Had direct contact with the body of the patient during the physical examination <strong>without proper PPE</strong></li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Touched or <strong>cleaned the linens</strong>, clothes, or dishes of the patient</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lives in the <strong>same household </strong>as the patient</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Comes in close contact (within 1 meter) with the patient without necessary precautions</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A<strong> passenger </strong>in <strong>close proximity </strong>with the<strong> </strong>symptomatic patient for<strong> 6 hours </strong>and the patient later tested positive</li>\n</ul>\n<p><br/><strong>Low-risk contacts:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Shared the same space but does not have a high-risk exposure to the confirmed case.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Travelled in the same environment but does <strong>not have a high-risk exposure</strong> to the confirmed case.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6820","text":"You would classify all of ​the following patients as​ having major risk factor​s for severe COVID-19 exc‌ept:","raw_text":"You would classify all of the following patients as having major risk factors for severe COVID-19 except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A 19 year old female under​going dialysis for CKD","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A 49 year old male who has​ recently undergone liver​ transplantation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"A 42 year old man who had ​malaria 2 months ago","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"A 65 year old male with no​ comorbidities","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. A 42 year old man who had malaria 2 months ago","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A 42 year old man who had malaria 2 months ago</strong></p><p>A middle-aged man who had malaria 2 months ago is not at major risk of developing severe COVID-19 disease.</p>\n<p>The major <strong>risk factors</strong> for <strong>severe COVID-19</strong> are: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Increasing age (<strong>&gt;60 years</strong>) </li>\n<li>Underlying <strong>non-communicable diseases</strong>       \n<ul>\n<li>Cardiac disease </li>\n<li>Chronic lung disease </li>\n<li>Cerebrovascular disease </li>\n<li>Hypertension</li>\n<li>Diabetes</li>\n<li>Obesity</li>\n<li>Chronic kidney disease</li>\n<li>Immune suppression</li>\n<li>Cancer</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6843","text":"A woman presents with brea​thlessness and hemoptysis​ since yesterday. Examina​tion findings are given b‌elow. CT scan shows local​ised ground-glass opacity​ in the left posterior lo‍be of the lung. What is t​he likely diagnosis? Puls‍e- 96/min, BP- 118/68 mmH‍g, Temperature- 100.3 F R​R- 15 cpm SpO2- 82% on ro​om air","raw_text":"<p>A woman presents with breathlessness and hemoptysis since yesterday. Examination findings are given below. CT scan shows localised ground-glass opacity in the left posterior lobe of the lung. What is the likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>Pulse- 96/min,</p>\n<p>BP- 118/68 mmHg,</p>\n<p>Temperature- 100.3 F</p>\n<p>RR- 15 cpm</p>\n<p>SpO2- 82% on room air</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tuberc​ulosis","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Necrotisin​g pneumonia","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Viral pn​eumonitis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Invasive as​pergillosis","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Viral pneumonitis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Viral pneumonitis</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>COVID-19. </strong><strong>Hemoptysis</strong> is one of the atypical manifestations of COVID-19 and it may be present with more common features such as <strong>breathlessness</strong>,<strong> fever,</strong> and <strong>cough.</strong> CT-finding of localized <strong>ground-glass opacity</strong> is also supportive of the diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Other <strong>atypical manifestations</strong> of COVID-19 include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Respiratory-\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pulmonary embolism</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hemoptysis</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cardiac-\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Myocardial infarction</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Myocarditis</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pericarditis</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pericardial effusion</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Neurological-\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Stroke</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Guillain‐Barre Syndrome</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Viral encephalitis</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Toxic encephalopathy</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Acute hemorrhagic necrotizing encephalopathy</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Olfactory and taste disorders</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Gastrointestinal-\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Diarrhea</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nausea</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Vomiting</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Abdominal pain</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ocular-\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Conjunctivitis</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Epiphora</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cutaneous-\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Erythematous rash</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Widespread urticaria</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Chickenpox‐like vesicles</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Localized pruritic lesions</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Petechial skin rash</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Option A: <strong>Tuberculosis</strong> would have a more chronic history and radiologically appears as dense, homogeneous parenchymal consolidation in subpleural sites of lower and middle lobes.</p>\n<p>Option B: In <strong>necrotising pneumonia</strong>, CT imaging is likely to show distinct areas of low attenuation with decreased parenchymal enhancement representing liquefaction.</p>\n<p>Option D: <strong>Invasive aspergillosis</strong> is likely to occur in the setting of immunosuppression. The associated ground glass opacity, occurring due to hemorrhage, surrounds a pulmonary nodule and is known as the <strong>halo sign</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3919","text":"What is the most common lu​ng pathology seen during ​the autopsy of COVID-19 p​atients?","raw_text":"What is the most common lung pathology seen during the autopsy of COVID-19 patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pulmonary ​infarction","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Endotheli​al injury","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Diffuse alv​eolar damage","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Fibrin​ clots","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Diffuse alveolar damage","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Diffuse alveolar damage</strong></p><p class=\"p1\"><strong>Diffuse alveolar damage</strong> is the <strong>most common lung pathology</strong> noted during the autopsy of <strong>COVID-19</strong> patients. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">COVID-19 related histopathological changes in the lungs include: </p>\n<ol class=\"ul1\">\n<li class=\"li1\">Diffuse alveolar damage</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Hyaline membrane formation</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Desquamation of pneumocytes</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Fibrin deposits in lungs</li>\n<li class=\"li1\">Inflammation with mononuclear cells</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Note:</strong> Diffuse alveolar damage is<strong> not specific to COVID-19.</strong> It may be seen in all cases of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) including pneumonia, burns, near drowning, etc</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6953","text":"You are posted as an inter​n in casualty. An elderly​ 60-year-old man is broug​ht to you with complaints‌ of chest pain. He has te​sted positive for COVID. ​Which of the following fe‍atures will prompt you to​ get this patient admitte‍d to dedicated COVID Heal‍th Centre? A. Respiratory​ rate of 25/min B. SpO2 9​3% C. Multiple comorbidit​ies D. Chest pain E. Age","raw_text":"<p>You are posted as an intern in casualty. An elderly 60-year-old man is brought to you with complaints of chest pain. He has tested positive for COVID. Which of the following features will prompt you to get this patient admitted to dedicated COVID Health Centre?</p>\n<p>A. Respiratory rate of 25/min<br/>B. SpO2 93%<br/>C. Multiple comorbidities<br/>D. Chest pain<br/>E. Age</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A &amp;amp;​ B only","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"A, B, C, &amp;​amp; E only","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"A, B &amp;am​p; C only","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A, B, C, ​D &amp;amp; E","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. A, B, C, & E only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/43d094ce4619426698ff0444460bf1f9x1280x4371.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A, B, C, &amp; E only</strong></p><p><strong>A, B, C &amp; E</strong> are clinical criteria of admission under COVID-19 ward.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"cVywDTvV","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"102_covid_19___investigations","title":"102.Covid 19   Investigations","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MD6838","text":"During the swab collection‌ for COVID-19 testing, th​e healthcare professional‌ asks you to tilt your he‍ad approximately to what ​degree?","raw_text":"During the swab collection for COVID-19 testing, the healthcare professional asks you to tilt your head approximately to what degree?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30 de‌grees","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"45 de‌grees","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"70 de‌grees","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"15 de‌grees","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 70 degrees","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 70 degrees</strong></p><p>For collecting nasal/nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal specimens for <strong>COVID-19 testing</strong>, a person’s head should be tilted back<strong> 70 degrees. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Guidelines for specimen collection:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use appropriate PPE and maintain proper infection control measures</li>\n<li>Restricted entry to visitors or attendants during sample collection</li>\n<li>Complete the requisition form for each specimen submitted</li>\n<li>Proper disposal of all waste generated</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Recommended <strong>personal protective equipment</strong> (PPE) for the healthcare professional who is collecting the specimen or coming within 6 feet of the patient: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>N95 or higher-level respirator (or facemask if a respirator is not available)</li>\n<li>eye protection</li>\n<li>gloves</li>\n<li>gown</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6826","text":"Which of the following is ‌a false statement regardi​ng sample collection for ‌COVID-19 testing?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a false statement regarding sample collection for COVID-19 testing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The nasal swab is placed i‌n a different viral trans​port media tube as the th‌roat swab.","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Oropharyngeal sample is no‌t collected using a calci​um alginate swab.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Swab is rubbed over the to‌nsillar pillars and poste​rior oropharynx for oroph‌aryngeal swab.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Swab is inserted parallel ‌to the palate for nasopha​ryngeal swab.","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. The nasal swab is placed in a different viral transport media tube as the throat swab.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The nasal swab is placed in a different viral transport media tube as the throat swab.</strong></p><p>The nasal swab is placed in the<strong> same viral transport media tube</strong> as the throat swab.</p>\n<p>Samples that can be used for COVID-19 testing are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Throat and nasal swab</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Oropharyngeal swab</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Nasopharyngeal swab </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Broncho-alveolar lavage </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Endotracheal aspirate </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Combined throat and nasal swabs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tilt patient's head back 70 degrees.</li>\n<li>Gently rotate the swab and insert less than one inch into the nostril. Rotate the swab several times against the nasal wall and repeat the same in the other nostril with the same swab.</li>\n<li>Place the tip of the swab into the transport media tube and cut off the applicator.</li>\n<li>For throat swab, take a second swab, insert into the mouth and swab the posterior pharynx and tonsillar areas.</li>\n<li><strong>Place the tip of the swab</strong> into the <strong>same tube</strong> and cut off the applicator tip.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Oropharyngeal swab:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Tilt patient's head back 70 degrees.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Rub swab </strong>over <strong>both tonsillar pillars </strong>and<strong> posterior oropharynx </strong>and avoid touching the tongue, teeth, and gums.</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Use only synthetic fiber swabs with plastic shafts. <strong>Avoid calcium alginate swabs </strong>and<strong> swabs with wooden shafts.</strong></li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Place the swab immediately into sterile tubes with viral transport medium.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Nasopharyngeal swab: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tilt the patient's head back 70 degrees</li>\n<li>Insert flexible swab through the nares <strong>parallel to the palate</strong> <strong>(not upwards)</strong> until resistance is encountered </li>\n<li>Rub and roll the swab and leave it in place for several seconds to absorb secretions before removing</li>\n<li>Place the swab immediately into sterile tubes with viral transport medium.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Viral transport mediums:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hanks balanced salt solution with Ca and Mg and heat inactivated fetal bovine serum, gentamicin and amphotericin B (most commonly used)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Sterile saline</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Phosphate buffered saline-glycerol medium</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Amies medium</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6839","text":"The specimens collected fo‌r COVID-19 testing should​ be transported to the la‌b at what temperatures?","raw_text":"The specimens collected for COVID-19 testing should be transported to the lab at what temperatures?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-30 de‌gree C","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"-4 to -8‌ degree C","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 to 8 ‌degree C","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"12 to 16‌ degree C","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2 to 8 degree C","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2 to 8 degree C</strong></p><p>Specimens collected for COVID-19 testing should be transported to the lab at a temperature of <strong>2 to 8 degrees C</strong> as per the <strong>WHO</strong> guidelines. The <strong>ICMR/NIV</strong> guidelines recommend <strong>4 degrees C</strong> for transport to the laboratory. </p>\n<p>Both guidelines recommend storing specimens at <strong>-70°C or below</strong> if a <strong>delay</strong> in testing or shipping is expected.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 172px;\" width=\"399\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 327.312px;\"><strong>Specimen</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 55.6875px;\"><strong>Sensitivity</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 327.312px;\">Bronchoalveolar lavage fluid</td>\n<td style=\"width: 55.6875px;\">93%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 327.312px;\">Sputum</td>\n<td style=\"width: 55.6875px;\">72%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 327.312px;\">Nasopharyngeal swab</td>\n<td style=\"width: 55.6875px;\">63%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 327.312px;\">Oropharyngeal swab</td>\n<td style=\"width: 55.6875px;\">32%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 327.312px;\">Faeces</td>\n<td style=\"width: 55.6875px;\">29%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 327.312px;\">Blood</td>\n<td style=\"width: 55.6875px;\">1%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6842","text":"Which level of BSL (Biosaf‌ety level) facility is re​quired during specimen ha‌ndling for molecular test‍ing of SARS CoV 2?","raw_text":"Which level of BSL (Biosafety level) facility is required during specimen handling for molecular testing of SARS CoV 2?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"BS‌L 1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"BS‌L 2","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"BS‌L 3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"BS‌L 4","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. BSL 2","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. BSL 2</strong></p><p>Specimen handling for <strong>molecular testing of SARS CoV 2</strong> would require <strong>BSL-2</strong> or equivalent facilities.</p>\n<p>Microbiology and pathology laboratories performing diagnostic tests on a stool or respiratory specimen should handle potential SARS-CoV specimens using Biosafety Level 2 (BSL-2). <strong>Virus culture</strong> and initial characterization of viral agents recovered in cultures requires minimum <strong>BSL-3 facilities</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> Biosafety levels include a specific combination of work practices, safety equipment, and facilities that are designed to minimize the exposure of workers and the environment to infectious agents. There are four biosafety levels.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3914","text":"An elderly patient was rus‌hed to the casualty follo​wing bouts of severe brea‌thlessness. He reported a‍ history of fever and cou​gh for the past 3 days. O​n examination, his SpO2 w‌as 85% and RR was 32/min.‍ CT scan is shown below. ‌What is the gold standard​ investigation for his di‍agnosis?","raw_text":"An elderly patient was rushed to the casualty following bouts of severe breathlessness. He reported a history of fever and cough for the past 3 days. On examination, his SpO2 was 85% and RR was 32/min. CT scan is shown below. What is the gold standard investigation for his diagnosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CL‌IA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"EL‌ISA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"R‌AT","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"RT-‌PCR","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. RT-PCR","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1f445db4ed8a460a948006ca63a04b9a.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. RT-PCR</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario, along with<span class=\"language en\" title=\"English\"><strong> </strong></span>CT showing bilateral multiple patchy consolidation and grid-like thickness of interlobular septa, is suggestive of <strong>COVID-19</strong>. The <strong>gold standard</strong> for its diagnosis is the identification of viral genome targets in the respiratory tract materials by <strong>reverse transcriptase polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR)</strong> during the first week of symptoms.</p>\n<p><strong>RT-PCR</strong> involves the reverse transcription of the genetic material of the virus (RNA) to complementary DNA (cDNA), followed by amplification of some regions of the cDNA. Four regions of the SARS CoV2 genome are used as targets:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">RdRp gene (RNA-dependent RNA polymerase) </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Genes from structural proteins </li>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">E (virus envelope) </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">N (virus nucleocapsid), </li>\n</ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">ORF1ab gene (open reading frame 1a and 1b) </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>CBNAAT </strong>(cartridge-based nucleic acid amplification test) uses small, chip-based, portable, battery-operated devices that detect target <strong>genetic material</strong> of SARS CoV2. Examples include <strong>TrueNat</strong> and <strong>Cepheid Xpert Xpress SARS CoV-2</strong></p>\n<p>Options A and B: <strong>Antibody testing-</strong> Antibodies of IgA, IgM, and IgG isotypes specific to different virus proteins are detected by enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) or chemiluminescence immunoassays (CLIA). CLIA is more sensitive compared to ELISA.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>Rapid antigen test (RAT)-</strong> Antigen-based assays are used as point-of-care tests for early detection of SARS-CoV-2. They are used at the field level for <strong>early detection</strong> of infection and quick <strong>containment</strong>. Most of such tests have relatively moderate sensitivity but high specificity. They are used for diagnosis along with RT-PCR. </p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6827","text":"Choose the correct stateme‌nt regarding testing of C​OVID-19:","raw_text":"Choose the correct statement regarding testing of COVID-19:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All newborns must be teste‌d.","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"All pregnant women must be‌ tested for COVID-19 in t​he first trimester.","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"No individual can voluntar‌ily subject themselves to​ COVID-19 test.","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"A single positive RT-PCR t‌est is considered confirm​atory without repeat test‌ing.","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. A single positive RT-PCR test is considered confirmatory without repeat testing.","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. A single positive RT-PCR test is considered confirmatory without repeat testing.</strong></p><p>A <strong>single RT-PCR/TrueNat/CBNAAT/RAT positive </strong>is considered<strong> confirmatory </strong>without repeat testing. If symptoms develop following a negative RAT, a repeat RAT or RT-PCR should be done.</p>\n<p><strong>Routine surveillance</strong> in <strong>containment zones</strong> and <strong>screening</strong> at points of entry for all <strong>symptomatic</strong> cases including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Healthcare and frontline workers</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Direct and high-risk contacts and individuals</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Routine surveillance</strong> in <strong>non-containment</strong> areas for all <strong>symptomatic</strong> cases including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">History of international travel in the last 14 days </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Contacts of a laboratory-confirmed case </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Healthcare and frontline workers involved in containment and mitigation activities </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Returnees and migrants within 7 days of illness </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">All asymptomatic high-risk contacts </li>\n</ul>\n<p>In <strong>hospital</strong> settings for all <strong>symptomatic</strong> cases including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">All patients of Severe Acute Respiratory Infection (SARI)</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Patients presenting in a healthcare setting </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Neonates</strong> presenting with acute respiratory/sepsis-like illness</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Patients with atypical manifestations (stroke, encephalitis, hemoptysis, Guillain Barre Syndrome etc.) based on the doctor's discretion</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">All <strong>pregnant women in/near labor</strong> hospitalized for delivery</li>\n<li>Asymptomatic high-risk patients who are hospitalized or seeking immediate hospitalization (eg. immune compromise, malignancy, transplant, chronic comorbidities, elderly &gt; 65 years)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Testing on-demand</strong> for all individuals:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Traveling to countries/states mandating negative COVID-19 test at the point of entry</li>\n<li>Who <strong>wish to get themselves tested</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6824","text":"A woman, whose husband has‌ tested positive for COVI​D-19, comes to the clinic‌ with a negative report o‍n the rapid antigen test.​ She is currently asympto​matic. What will be the n‌ext step?","raw_text":"A woman, whose husband has tested positive for COVID-19, comes to the clinic with a negative report on the rapid antigen test. She is currently asymptomatic. What will be the next step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retest with rapid antigen ‌test after 5 days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Retest with RT-PCR after 5‌ days","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Retest with RT-PCR immedia‌tely","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Retest with RT-PCR if symp‌toms develop","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Retest with RT-PCR if symptoms develop","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Retest with RT-PCR if symptoms develop</strong></p><p><strong>Asymptomatic </strong>individuals with a<strong> negative rapid antigen test</strong> (RAT) need to be <strong>retested only if they develop symptoms</strong> of COVID-19 such as cough, fever, and difficulty in breathing.</p>\n<p>Rapid antigen test, when positive, is reported as positive in both symptomatic and asymptomatic patients. When the test is negative, the patient is retested with another RAT or RT-PCR only if the patient is symptomatic.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6840","text":"A man presents to the flu ‌clinic with complaints of​ fever and sore throat fo‌r the past 4 days. COVID-‍19 is suspected and a pap​er-based test kit as show​n below is used for testi‌ng. Identify the test.","raw_text":"A man presents to the flu clinic with complaints of fever and sore throat for the past 4 days. COVID-19 is suspected and a paper-based test kit as shown below is used for testing. Identify the test.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"TRU‌NAT","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"FEL‌UDA","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"COVID Kav‌ach ELISA","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Q COVID-1‌9 Ag Test","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. FELUDA","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7342e17f911f48b3a9ccfe6a58ce4bba.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. FELUDA</strong></p><p>The <strong>paper-based Covid-19 test kit</strong> developed by CSIR and IGIB and approved by the Drugs Controller General of India (DCGI) is named <strong>FELUDA.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>FELUDA</strong> is an acronym for <strong>FNCAS9 Editor Linked Uniform Detection Assay.</strong> The name is inspired by a fictitious detective character in novels written by Satyajit Ray.</p>\n<p>FELUDA uses <strong>CRISPR-Cas9 technology</strong> (CRISPR = Clustered Regularly Interspaced Short Palindromic Repeats). The CRISPR technology uses a highly specific CAS9 protein to find and bind to the target Covid signature. This is then coupled with paper-strip chemistry to elicit a visual readout on a paper strip. It is a very affordable, sensitive (96%), and specific (98%) strip test which can detect viral genetic material within <strong>45 minutes.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6841","text":"What is the name of the CO‌VID-19 IgG antibody detec​tion ELISA test kit devel‌oped by ICMR and NIV?","raw_text":"What is the name of the COVID-19 IgG antibody detection ELISA test kit developed by ICMR and NIV?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"COVID Nis‌chay ELISA","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"COVID Aar‌ogya ELISA","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"COVID Kav‌ach ELISA","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"COVID San‌kalp ELISA","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. COVID Kavach ELISA","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. COVID Kavach ELISA</strong></p><p><strong>COVID Kavach ELISA</strong> is an<strong> indigenous ELISA test kit</strong> for COVID-19 antibody detection developed by <strong>ICMR Delhi</strong> and <strong>NIV Pune.</strong></p>\n<p>It is reported to have a 98.7% sensitivity and 100% specificity and testing is possible even at the district level. As the ELISA kit has an <strong>inactivated virus</strong>, only minimal biosafety and biosecurity requirements as compared to the real-time RT-PCR test. </p>\n<p>IgG antibodies usually start appearing after two weeks of the onset of infection. Therefore, the IgG test is not useful for detecting acute infection. <strong>IgG based antibody tests</strong> are used in <strong>sero-surveillance</strong> of the population.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3917","text":"What is least likely to be‌ seen in a patient suspec​ted of COVID-19?","raw_text":"What is least likely to be seen in a patient suspected of COVID-19?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Elevated tr‌ansaminases","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Elevated ‌fibrinogen","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Lympho‌cytosis","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Prolonged prothrombin time‌","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Lymphocytosis","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Lymphocytosis</strong></p><p>Lymphocytosis is least likely to be seen in a patient with <strong>COVID-19</strong>. On the contrary, <strong>lymphopenia</strong> is likely to be seen. </p>\n<p><strong>Common laboratory abnormalities</strong> seen in COVID-19 are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lymphopenia</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mild thrombocytopenia</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Elevated aminotransaminase levels</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Elevated lactate dehydrogenase levels</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Elevated inflammatory markers - eg, ferritin, C-reactive protein, and erythrocyte sedimentation rate</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Markers of a hypercoagulable state - high fibrinogen, high D-dimer levels, increased fibrin degradation products, prolonged prothrombin time (PT) and activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The degree of these abnormalities tends to correlate with disease severity.</p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3927","text":"What is the most commonly ‌seen pattern in chest CT ​of patients affected with‌ COVID-19?","raw_text":"What is the most commonly seen pattern in chest CT of patients affected with COVID-19?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Interlobular septal thicke‌nings","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Air bron‌chograms","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Ground gla‌ss opacity","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Crazy pavi‌ng pattern","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Ground glass opacity","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/830631c7af074a2fba6e47e93865feacx986x608.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d86285ee7a5b44ddae4aa941f7284814x1280x1010.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2170fe46cbe6435a99b97521af63dca5x1280x1010.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6bf628c88803466fba7b3f0c0f943153x1280x940.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b7998c2151bd4abd94bf1c0f4d629fb8x1280x397.PNG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Ground glass opacity</strong></p><p><strong>Ground glass opacity (GGO)</strong> is the <strong>most commonly seen pattern</strong> in chest CT of patients with <strong>COVID-19</strong>. </p>\n<p>Some of the typical features seen in the CT scans of COVID-19 patients include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ground glass opacities</li>\n<li>Consolidation</li>\n<li>Air bronchograms</li>\n<li>Interlobular septal thickenings</li>\n<li>Visible intralobular lines (crazy-paving pattern)</li>\n<li>Reversed halo sign </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The image below shows an axial CT scan with multiple patchy areas of pure <strong>ground-glass opacities</strong>. GGO are hazy areas with slightly increased density in the lungs without obscuration of underlying bronchial and vascular margins. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image given below shows a CT scan with bilateral GGO in the lower lobe and an <strong>air bronchogram </strong>(arrow) in the left subpleural area. Air bronchograms appear like air-filled bronchi within a highly attenuated parenchymal background. These are dilated bronchi filled with <strong>mucus plugs</strong> and do not truly contain air. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image given below shows a CT scan with <strong>multiple consolidation areas </strong>and<strong> interlobular septal thickening </strong>(arrows) in both lungs. The pulmonary interstitial structures such as interlobular septa and intralobular lines can thicken and manifest as small linear opacities. </p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image given below shows a CT scan with <strong>crazy-paving pattern</strong> (circled area). It is defined as the presence of GGOs with superimposed interlobular septal thickening giving the appearance of irregular paving stones.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The image given below shows a CT scan with multiple lesions with reversed halo sign (arrows). The <strong>reversed halo sign</strong> or <strong>atoll sign</strong> is the presence of a ring-like area of consolidation with a superimposed rounded GGO.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6835","text":"Increased concentrations o‌f all of the following ar​e poor prognostic markers‌ in a COVID-19 patient ex‍cept:","raw_text":"Increased concentrations of all of the following are poor prognostic markers in a COVID-19 patient except:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glycosylat‌ed ferritin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"C-reactiv‌e protein","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"N-termina‌l pro-BNP","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Procal‌citonin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Glycosylated ferritin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Glycosylated ferritin</strong></p><p><strong>Glycosylated ferritin</strong> is <strong>decreased</strong> in severe COVID-19 illness and hence, it is not a poor prognostic marker.</p>\n<p>Interestingly, in contrast to <strong>ferritin</strong> which is actually <strong>elevated</strong> in individuals with COVID 19, the glycosylated ferritin is decreased. It is a potentially <strong>sensitive biomarker</strong> that can better discriminate severe COVID than ferritin alone.</p>\n<p>Increased levels of the following laboratory markers is associated with poorer outcomes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">D-dimer</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">C-reactive protein</li>\n<li>Lactate dehydrogenase</li>\n<li>Cardiac troponin</li>\n<li>Ferritin</li>\n<li>Creatinine phosphokinase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7459","text":"Which inflammatory marker ‌is of prognostic signific​ance in COVID-19 patients‌?","raw_text":"Which inflammatory marker is of prognostic significance in COVID-19 patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"IL‌-6","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"E‌SR","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"N‌LR","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"P‌CT","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. IL-6","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. IL-6</strong></p><p>Elevated pro-inflammatory cytokines like interleukin-6 (<strong>IL-6</strong>) are associated with critical and fatal COVID-19. It is specifically useful in <strong>predicting</strong> <strong>prognosis </strong>of such patients.</p>\n<p>Increased levels of the following laboratory markers are associated with poorer outcomes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">D-dimer</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">C-reactive protein</li>\n<li>Lactate dehydrogenase</li>\n<li>Cardiac troponin</li>\n<li>Ferritin</li>\n<li>Creatinine phosphokinase</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Options B, C and D: Inflammatory markers associated with increased severity of COVID-19 are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><strong>PCT </strong></strong>(procalcitonin test)</li>\n<li><strong>ESR</strong></li>\n<li>WBC</li>\n<li>LMR (lymphocyte/mean platelet volume ratio)</li>\n<li>HsCRP (high-sensitivity C-reactive protein)</li>\n<li><strong>NLR</strong> (neutrophil to lymphocyte ratio) </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‌pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‌pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"hzP9PFBU","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"103_covid_19___treatment","title":"103.Covid 19   Treatment","num_questions":13,"total_marks":52,"duration":23,"time_per_question":106.15,"questions":[{"id":"MD6823","text":"The airport authority sent‍ a COVID-19 suspect patie​nt to the designated dist‍rict hospital for a consu‌ltation. He came positive‍ on RT-PCR testing. As th‌e duty doctor, you observ​e that he has mild sympto​ms that can be treated on​ an outpatient basis. Wha‌t will be your recommenda‍tion?","raw_text":"The airport authority sent a COVID-19 suspect patient to the designated district hospital for a consultation. He came positive on RT-PCR testing. As the duty doctor, you observe that he has mild symptoms that can be treated on an outpatient basis. What will be your recommendation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Home isolation for 7 days ‍from testing positive","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Home quarantine for 10 day‍s from testing positive","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Home isolation for 10 days‍ from testing positive","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Home quarantine for 7 days‍ from testing positive","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Home isolation for 7 days from testing positive","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Home isolation for 7 days from testing positive</strong></p><p>The given scenario is a mild case of <strong>COVID-19</strong> who can be recommended <strong>home isolation</strong> for a duration of <strong>7 days</strong> from testing positive.</p>\n<p><strong>Isolation</strong> is the separation and restriction of movements or activities of persons who are <strong>sick</strong>, to prevent the transmission of disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Eligibility for home isolation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Clinically assigned as a mild or asymptomatic case </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Facility at their residence for self-isolation and for quarantining the family contacts</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Availability of a caregiver </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Must monitor his health regularly and inform his health status to the district surveillance officer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The patient can <strong>end</strong> <strong>home isolation</strong> after <strong>7 days from testing positive </strong>and<strong> no fever for 3 successive days.</strong> There is no need for testing after the home isolation period is over.</p>\n<p>The patient should <strong>seek medical help</strong> if he/she develops:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Unresolved High-grade fever (more than 100° F for more than 3 days)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Difficulty in breathing,</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Dip in oxygen saturation (SpO2 ≤ 93% on room air at least 3 readings within 1 hour) or respiratory rate &gt;24/ min</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Persistent pain/pressure in the chest</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Mental confusion or inability to arouse</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Severe fatigue and myalgia</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Quarantine</strong> separates and restricts the movement of people who were <strong>exposed</strong> to a contagious disease to see if they become sick.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6832","text":"A COVID-19 positive man is‍ rushed to the casualty d​ue to severe breathlessne‍ss. Examination findings ‌are suggestive of pneumon‍ia and his vitals are- PR‌: 90/min, BP: 134/88 mmHg​, RR: 22/min and SpO2: 86​%. What will you advise t​his patient? PR: 90/min B‌P: 134/88 mmHg RR: 22/min‍ Temperature: 101 F SpO2:‍ 86%","raw_text":"<p>A COVID-19 positive man is rushed to the casualty due to severe breathlessness. Examination findings are suggestive of pneumonia and his vitals are- PR: 90/min, BP: 134/88 mmHg, RR: 22/min and SpO2: 86%. What will you advise this patient?</p>\n<p>PR: 90/min</p>\n<p>BP: 134/88 mmHg</p>\n<p>RR: 22/min</p>\n<p>Temperature: 101 F</p>\n<p>SpO2: 86%</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Home is‍olation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Admit to COVID care center‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Admit ‍to ward","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Admit ‍to ICU","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Admit to ICU","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe5cb670ee104a838a63332770a2d0b0x1280x4371.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Admit to ICU</strong></p><p>The patient is suffering from <strong>severe COVID-19</strong> and hence has to be <strong>admitted to ICU. </strong>The disease is said to be severe when there is <strong>RR&gt;30/min</strong>, breathlessness OR <strong>SpO2&lt;90%</strong> on room air.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3915","text":"A man presents with fever ‍and cough for 2 days. His​ wife recently tested pos‍itive for COVID-19 and ha‌s been under home isolati‍on. Examination findings ‌are given below. What is ​not recommended in his ma​nagement? Pulse- 110/min,​ BP- 78/58 mmHg, Temperat‌ure- 101.3 F RR- 28 cpm S‍pO2- 90% on room air","raw_text":"<p>A man presents with fever and cough for 2 days. His wife recently tested positive for COVID-19 and has been under home isolation. Examination findings are given below. What is not recommended in his management?</p>\n<p>Pulse- 110/min,</p>\n<p>BP- 78/58 mmHg,</p>\n<p>Temperature- 101.3 F</p>\n<p>RR- 28 cpm</p>\n<p>SpO2- 90% on room air</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Awake ‍proning","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Administration of paraceta‍mol","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Oxygen ‍support","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Prophylactic‍ LMW heparin","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Awake proning","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Awake proning</strong></p><div>\n<p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>moderate COVID-19 </strong>(SpO2 &lt;94%, RR≥24)<strong>. </strong><strong>Awake proning</strong> should be <strong>avoided</strong> in patients with <strong>hemodynamic instability</strong>.</p>\n</div>\n<div>Awake proning in moderate COVID disease is recommended for any patient with severe respiratory distress. It improves oxygenation by keeping the <strong>alveolar units open</strong>. Timely proning of patients with <strong>SpO2 &lt; 94%</strong> can help reduce mortality.</div>\n<div> </div>\n<div><strong>Steps for self-proning:</strong></div>\n<ul>\n<li>1 pillow below the neck</li>\n<li>1 or 2 pillows below the chest through upper thighs</li>\n<li>2 pillows below the shins</li>\n<li>Keep alternating between proning, laying on your right, sitting up, and laying on your left, in a lying position, ideally for not more than <strong>30 minutes</strong> in each position with an <strong>upper limit of 2 hours</strong>.</li>\n<li>One may prone <strong>up to 16 hours a day</strong>, in multiple cycles, if tolerable.</li>\n</ul>\n<div><strong>Avoid proning:</strong></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Hemodynamic instability</li>\n<li>For 1 hour after meals</li>\n<li>DVT, cardiac conditions</li>\n<li>Fractures of spine, femur, or pelvis</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3925","text":"A 42-year-old COVID-19 pos‍itive man under home isol​ation develops breathing ‍difficulty. SpO2 is 86%. ‌He is administered oxygen‍ via a face mask with a r‌eservoir bag with a flow ​rate of 10 L/min. After a​ trial of one hour, his S​pO2 is 88%. What is the n‌ext step?","raw_text":"A 42-year-old COVID-19 positive man under home isolation develops breathing difficulty. SpO2 is 86%. He is administered oxygen via a face mask with a reservoir bag with a flow rate of 10 L/min. After a trial of one hour, his SpO2 is 88%. What is the next step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increase flow rate to 15 L‍/min","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"High-flow nasal cannula ox‍ygen therapy","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Endotrachea‍l intubation","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"No specific intervention n‍ecessary","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. High-flow nasal cannula oxygen therapy","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. High-flow nasal cannula oxygen therapy</strong></p><p>This patient has <strong>persistent hypoxia</strong> after an adequate trial of standard oxygen therapy and should be started on <strong>high-flow nasal cannula oxygen therapy. </strong></p>\n<p>When respiratory distress and/or hypoxemia of the patient cannot be alleviated after receiving standard oxygen therapy, high–flow nasal cannula oxygen therapy (HFNO), or non–invasive ventilation can be considered. Compared to standard oxygen therapy, HFNO reduces the need for intubation. </p>\n<p>Patients on HFNO should be closely monitored, and endotracheal intubation should be done if the patient acutely deteriorates or does not improve after a short trial (about 1 hr).</p>\n<p><strong>Contraindications to HFNO</strong> includes patients with:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypercapnia (exacerbation of COPD)</li>\n<li>Hemodynamic instability</li>\n<li>Multi-organ failure</li>\n<li>Abnormal mental status </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong> If an high flow nasal cannula (HFNC) or simple nasal cannula is used to administer O2, an N95 mask should be applied over it.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6831","text":"An 84-year-old man is bein‍g treated in the ICU for ​sepsis induced respirator‍y failure secondary to CO‌VID-19. What is the maxim‍um tidal volume setting p‌ermissible for his mechan​ical ventilation? (PBW-pr​edicted body weight)","raw_text":"An 84-year-old man is being treated in the ICU for sepsis induced respiratory failure secondary to COVID-19. What is the maximum tidal volume setting permissible for his mechanical ventilation? (PBW-predicted body weight)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 ml/‍kg PBW","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"6 ml/‍kg PBW","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"8 ml/‍kg PBW","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"10 ml/‍kg PBW","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 8 ml/kg PBW","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 8 ml/kg PBW</strong></p><p>A maximum tidal volume of up to <strong>8 ml/kg PBW</strong> is allowed in a patient with<strong> ARDS</strong> who is being mechanically ventilated.</p>\n<p>In patients with ARDS, mechanical ventilation should be implemented using <strong>lower tidal volumes</strong> (4–8 ml/kg PBW) and <strong>lower inspiratory pressures</strong> (plateau pressure &lt;30 cmH2O).</p>\n<p>In patients with <strong>moderate or severe ARDS</strong>, a <strong>higher PEEP</strong> is suggested. PEEP titration requires consideration of benefits (improving alveolar recruitment) vs. risks (end-inspiratory over distension leading to lung injury).</p>\n<p>Lung protective ventilation strategy by ARDS net protocol recommendations:</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Parameters</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Values</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tidal volume </td>\n<td>6ml/kg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RR </td>\n<td>15-35/min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PEEP </td>\n<td>5-15cm H2O</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Target plateau pressure</td>\n<td>&lt; 30cm H2O</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Target SpO2</td>\n<td>88-95%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Target PaO2 </td>\n<td>55-80mmHg</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6816","text":"An elderly diabetic man wi‍th COVID-19 is desaturati​ng on non-invasive ventil‍ation. You intubate him a‌nd place him on a mechani‍cal ventilator. He improv‌es briefly but the SpO2 s​uddenly drops. You suspec​t a tubal block and perfo​rm closed suctioning. Des‌pite this, he goes into c‍ardiac arrest and you sta‍rt CPR. You would not exp‌ect aerosol generation in​:","raw_text":"An elderly diabetic man with COVID-19 is desaturating on non-invasive ventilation. You intubate him and place him on a mechanical ventilator. He improves briefly but the SpO2 suddenly drops. You suspect a tubal block and perform closed suctioning. Despite this, he goes into cardiac arrest and you start CPR. You would not expect aerosol generation in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Non-invasive‍ ventilation","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Manual ve‍ntilation","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Closed s‍uctioning","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"C‍PR","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Closed suctioning","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Closed suctioning</strong></p><p><strong>Open suctioning</strong> of the respiratory tract is associated with significant amounts of <strong>aerosol</strong> generation and not closed suctioning.</p>\n<p><strong>Commonly performed aerosol-generating procedures are:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Open suctioning of the respiratory tract</li>\n<li>Bronchoscopy</li>\n<li>Cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR)</li>\n<li>Non-invasive ventilation (e.g., BiPAP, CPAP)</li>\n<li>Endotracheal intubation and extubation</li>\n<li>Manual ventilation </li>\n</ul>\n<p>It is recommended that healthcare providers should be protected with <strong>PPE</strong> while performing these procedures. It must be performed in an adequately ventilated single room with a minimum of <strong>12 air changes/hour</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6846","text":"A 67-year-old patient with‍ end-stage renal disease ​is tested positive for CO‍VID-19. Which anticoagula‌tion agent would you reco‍mmended for him?","raw_text":"A 67-year-old patient with end-stage renal disease is tested positive for COVID-19. Which anticoagulation agent would you recommended for him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unfractiona‍ted heparin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"LM‍WH","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Warf‍arin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Dabig‍atran","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Unfractionated heparin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Unfractionated heparin</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of<strong> COVID-19</strong> in a patient with <strong>end-stage renal disease</strong>. <strong>Unfractionated heparin</strong> is recommended for use in such patients.</p>\n<p><strong>Prophylactic anticoagulation</strong> is used in the management of <strong>moderate and severe COVID</strong> cases. A prophylactic dose of low molecular weight heparin or unfractionated heparin is used to prevent thromboembolism. In a COVID patient with end-stage renal disease, unfractionated heparin is the preferred agent.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD8469","text":"Which case scenarios along‍ with the drugs prescribe​d are inappropriate? A ma‍n on anti-retroviral ther‌apy with severe COVID pne‍umonia- Tocilizumab A non‌-hospitalized middle-aged​ diabetic man with mild C​OVID pneumonia- Bamlanivi​mab A mother on post-part‌um day 3 with moderate CO‍VID pneumonia maintaining‍ with NIPPV- Remedisivir ‌A woman with COVID-19 dis​ease- Convalescent plasma‌ therapy A woman with mod​erate COVID pneumonia on ​supplemental oxygen- Dexa‍methasone","raw_text":"<p>Which case scenarios along with the drugs prescribed are inappropriate?</p>\n<ol>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">A man on anti-retroviral therapy with severe COVID pneumonia- Tocilizumab</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">A non-hospitalized middle-aged diabetic man with mild COVID pneumonia- Bamlanivimab</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">A mother on post-partum day 3 with moderate COVID pneumonia maintaining with NIPPV- Remedisivir</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">A woman with COVID-19 disease- Convalescent plasma therapy</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">A woman with moderate COVID pneumonia on supplemental oxygen- Dexamethasone</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> </span></p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, 2, ‍3, 4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2, 3, ‍and 4 only","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"3, 4 ‍and 5","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 and ‍2 only","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. 1, 2, 3, and 4 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. 1, 2, 3, and 4 only</strong></p><p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">The correct answer is <strong>1, 2, 3, and 4.</strong></span></p>\n<ol>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Tocilizumab is <strong>contraindicated</strong> in<strong> HIV-positive</strong> patients and those with active bacterial or fungal infection.</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Bamlanivimab is <strong>not</strong> </span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">authorized for patients with <strong>mild </strong></span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">COVID-19 pneumonia who are not at a high risk for <strong>hospitalization</strong>.</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Remedisivir is <strong>contraindicated </strong>in<strong> postnatal</strong> lactating women.</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Convalescent plasma therapy is <strong>no longer recommended</strong> in the management of COVID patients.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Tocilizumab</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> acts by <strong>IL-6 inhibition</strong>. It may be considered in moderate-severe COVID patients with progressively increasing oxygen requirements and mechanically ventilated patients not improving despite the use of steroids. Patients should be carefully monitored for secondary infections and neutropenia after treatment with tocilizumab. It is contraindicated in:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">HIV </span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Active infections (systemic bacterial/fungal)</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Tuberculosis</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Active hepatitis</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Absolute neutrophil count &lt; 2000/mm3</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Platelet count &lt; 1,00,000/mm3</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Bamlanivimab</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> is a monoclonal antibody directed against the </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">spike protein</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> of SARS-CoV-2. The FDA has issued an <strong>emergency use authorization</strong> for bamlanivimab for the treatment of adult and pediatric mild to moderate COVID patients (12 years of age and older weighing at least 40 kilograms) who are at high risk for progressing to severe COVID-19 and/or hospitalization. </span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Possible side effects include anaphylaxis and infusion-related reactions, nausea, diarrhea, dizziness, headache, itching, and vomiting. There is </span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">no specific contraindication</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> for this drug. </span></p>\n<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Remdesivir </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">is an</span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> antiviral drug that works by <strong>inhibiting RNA-dependent RNA polymerase</strong>. It may be considered in moderate and severe hospitalized COVID patients requiring supplemental oxygen/oxygen delivery through a high flow device/ noninvasive ventilation/ invasive mechanical ventilation/ ECMO. </span><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">It is contraindicated in: </span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">AST/ALT &gt; 5 times upper limit of normal</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Severe renal impairment (i.e., eGFR &lt; 30ml/min/m2 or need for hemodialysis) </span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Pregnancy or lactating females </span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Children &lt;12 years of age</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Convalescent plasma therapy</strong> is no longer recommended in the management of COVID patients, as per the revised guidelines.</p>\n<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">Dexamethasone</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> is generally given to all patients of moderate and severe COVID-19. Patients with severe COVID-19 can develop a</span><strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> systemic inflammatory response</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> that can lead to lung injury and multisystem organ dysfunction. </span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3923","text":"A G2P1 woman presents to t‍he clinic at 36 weeks of ​gestation. Her husband wa‍s COVID positive and now ‌she has also been tested ‍positive. What is true re‌garding her management?","raw_text":"A  G2P1 woman presents to the clinic at 36 weeks of gestation. Her husband was COVID positive and now she has also been tested positive. What is true regarding her management?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C-section is the preferred‍ mode of delivery","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Awake proning is not recom‍mended","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"CT scan is absolutely cont‍raindicated","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Aim to keep oxygen saturat‍ion more than 94% during ​labour","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Aim to keep oxygen saturation more than 94% during labour","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Aim to keep oxygen saturation more than 94% during labour</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>COVID-19</strong> disease in a <strong>pregnant</strong> patient. The management should include an aim to keep <strong>oxygen saturation above 94%</strong> during labour in this patient. In such cases, maternal observations and assessment during labour should be continued as per standard practice, with the addition of <strong>hourly oxygen saturation. </strong></p>\n<p>Option A: There is currently <strong>no evidence to favour one mode of birth </strong>over another. Mode of birth should not be influenced by the presence of COVID-19, unless the woman’s respiratory condition demands urgent intervention for birth.</p>\n<p>Option B: <strong>Awake proning</strong> is recommended. Although evidence is limited, there are reports that this is feasible with <strong>appropriate padding</strong> up to at least 28 weeks of gestation.</p>\n<p>Option C: <strong>CT</strong> scan is <strong>relatively contraindicated</strong>. Pregnant women with <strong>abdominal shielding</strong> should not be excluded from chest CT if clinically recommended.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6833","text":"Which of the following is ‍a criterion for discharge​ in a patient with mild C‍OVID-19 infection?","raw_text":"Which of the following is a criterion for discharge in a patient with mild COVID-19 infection?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Discharge after 10 days fr‍om testing positive &amp;amp;​ no fever for 3 days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Discharge after 7 days fro‍m testing positive &amp;amp; ​no fever for 3 days","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Discharge if SpO2&amp;gt;98% o‍n room air for 3 consecut​ive days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Discharge if repeat RT-PCR‍ testing is negative","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Discharge after 7 days from testing positive & no fever for 3 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Discharge after 7 days from testing positive &amp; no fever for 3 days</strong></p><p>COVID-19 cases that are <strong>mild/very mild/presymptomatic</strong> can be discharged from home isolation after <strong>7</strong><strong> days </strong>of <strong>testing positive</strong><strong> </strong>and<strong> no fever </strong>for<strong> 3 successive days. </strong>There is no need for testing after the end of home isolation. </p>\n<p>Criteria to diagnose asymptomatic COVID-19: Laboratory confirmed case with no symptoms with a saturation of more than 93% in room air. </p>\n<p>Criteria to diagnose mild COVID-19: Laboratory confirmed case with upper respiratory tract symptoms with/without fever, without shortness of breath, and saturation of more than 93% in room air. </p>\n<p>Patients are advised to isolate from other family members and monitor themselves at home, under proper medical guidance. Patients &gt;60 years with co-morbidities, and immunocompromised patients are allowed to isolate only after receiving clearance from the designated medical officer. </p>\n<p>Immediate medical attention is required if the patient experiences the following: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>A 100 ºF fever persists for &gt;3 days and does not resolve on medication.</li>\n<li>Difficulty in breathing </li>\n<li>Oxygen saturation ≤93% (at least 3 readings within 1 hour)</li>\n<li>Respiratory rate &gt;24/min</li>\n<li>Persistent chest pain or pressure</li>\n<li>Mental confusion </li>\n<li>Severe fatigue or myalgia </li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6834","text":"A 33-year-old delivery boy‍ tested COVID-19 positive​. He started experiencing‍ worsening breathlessness‌ and cough and rushed to ‍the hospital when his pul‌se oximeter reading was 8​7%. Following treatment, ​he showed signs of improv​ement. What is correct be‌fore discharging him?","raw_text":"A 33-year-old delivery boy tested COVID-19 positive. He started experiencing worsening breathlessness and cough and  rushed to the hospital when his pulse oximeter reading was 87%. Following treatment, he showed signs of improvement. What is correct before discharging him?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Discharge after 7 days fro‍m symptom onset &amp;amp; no ​fever for 3 days","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Discharge if repeat RT-PCR‍ testing is negative afte​r recovery","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Discharge if SpO2&amp;gt;98% o‍n room air for 3 consecut​ive days","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Discharge at the discretio‍n of the treating medical​ officer","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. Discharge at the discretion of the treating medical officer","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Discharge at the discretion of the treating medical officer</strong></p><p class=\"p1\">The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>severe COVID-19</strong> disease. Severe cases of COVID-19 (and <strong>immunocompromised</strong> patients) are discharged based on their clinical recovery at the discretion of the treating medical officer. </p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Mild</strong> and <strong>moderate</strong> cases do not need an RT-PCR test before discharge. But, at the time of discharge, they are advised to <strong>self-monitor </strong>and <strong>continue wearing masks</strong> for a duration of 7 days.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME7870","text":"A 10-year-old boy with COV‍ID-19 presents with sore ​throat, rhinorrhea and co‍ugh. He does not have fas‌t breathing. What is reco‍mmended for this child? H‌ome isolation Hospital ad​mission Awake proning Ant​ibiotics Low dose steroid​s","raw_text":"<p>A 10-year-old boy with COVID-19 presents with sore throat, rhinorrhea and cough. He does not have fast breathing. What is recommended for this child?</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Home isolation</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Hospital admission</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Awake proning</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Antibiotics</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Low dose steroids</strong></li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 a‍nd 5","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"3 o‍nly","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2 a‍nd 4","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1 o‍nly","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1 only","question_images":[],"explanation_images":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f392507b3b8641adb039521bd36fda16x1280x4635.JPEG"],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1 only</strong></p><p>The presence of sore throat, rhinorrhea, cough with no fast breathing are suggestive of <strong>mild</strong> <strong>COVID-19 </strong>disease. Hence, <strong>home isolation</strong> along with supportive care is recommended in this child.</p>\n<p></p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF2940","text":"FDA has recently sanctione‍d emergency use authoriza​tion to the first oral an‍tiviral drug combination ‌for the treatment of COVI‍D-19. This drug combinati‌on contains:","raw_text":"FDA has recently sanctioned emergency use authorization to the first oral antiviral drug combination for the treatment of COVID-19. This drug combination contains:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nirmatrelvir and Ritonavir‍","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Molnupiravir and Ritonavir‍","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Nirmatrelvir and Indinavir‍","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Molnupiravir and Indinavir‍","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Nirmatrelvir and Ritonavir","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Nirmatrelvir and Ritonavir</strong></p><p>FDA recently sanctioned emergency use authorization to a combination of <strong>nirmatrelvir and ritonavir</strong> tablets (Paxlovid) for the treatment of mild-to-moderate COVID-19. It is the <strong>first oral antiviral</strong> for the treatment of <strong>COVID-19.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Nirmatrelvir</strong> is a <strong>peptidomimetic</strong> inhibitor of the <strong>SARS-CoV-2 main protease</strong> (Mpro). Inhibition of this enzyme makes the virus incapable of processing polyprotein precursors and thus prevents viral replication.</p>\n<p><strong>Ritonavir</strong> is an HIV-1 <strong>protease inhibitor.</strong> It inhibits the CYP3A-mediated metabolism of nirmatrelvir which results in increased plasma concentrations of nirmatrelvir. It is <strong>not active</strong> against SARS-CoV-2 Mpro.</p>\n<p>Patients who tested positive with direct SARS-CoV-2 test can be administered this drug combination, provided they are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">12 years of age and older</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Weighing at least 40 kgs</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">At high risk for progression to severe COVID-19.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Contraindications include hypersensitivity reactions to the active ingredients or any other components, co-administration with drugs highly dependent on CYP3A for clearance, co-administration with potent CYP3A inducers. Adverse reactions include dysgeusia, diarrhea, hypertension, and myalgia.</p>\n<p>@neet_‍pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_‍pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"YP0qtENo","path":["Medicine"]},{"id":"104_covid_19___prophylaxis_and_complications","title":"104.Covid 19   Prophylaxis And Complications","num_questions":14,"total_marks":56,"duration":24,"time_per_question":102.86,"questions":[{"id":"MD3922","text":"Your worried friend calls ​you for advice after atte​nding a family function. ​He informs you that his a‍unt, who also travelled t​o the function in the sam​e car, tested positive fo‌r COVID-19. What is your ‍advice for your friend?","raw_text":"Your worried friend calls you for advice after attending a family function. He informs you that his aunt, who also travelled to the function in the same car, tested positive for COVID-19. What is your advice for your friend?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Home isolation for 14 days​","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Home quarantine for 14 day​s","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Home quarantine for 10 day​s","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Home isolation for 10 days​","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Home quarantine for 14 days","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Home quarantine for 14 days</strong></p><p><strong>Home quarantine for 14 days</strong> is advised when a person comes in contact with a COVID-19 positive patient.</p>\n<p>The home quarantined person:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Should stay in a well ventilated single room and stay away from high-risk individuals</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Should not attend any religious or social gatherings</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Must wash hands often</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Must change mask every 6-8 hrs</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Their surroundings must be disinfected with 1% sodium hypochlorite solution</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In case the person being quarantined becomes symptomatic, all his contacts should be home quarantined for 14 days and followed up for another 14 days or till the report turns out negative on lab testing.</p>\n<p><strong>Isolation</strong> is the separation and restriction of movements or activities of persons who are <strong>sick</strong>, to prevent the transmission of disease. <strong>Quarantine</strong> is the separation and restriction of movements or activities of persons who are <strong>not ill</strong> but are believed to have been<strong> exposed</strong> to infection, for the purpose of preventing transmission of diseases.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3918","text":"A 50-year-old man recoveri​ng from COVID-19 presents​ as follows. His medical ​and personal histories ar‍e insignificant with no e​xposure to cold. What wou​ld you suspect?","raw_text":"A 50-year-old man recovering from COVID-19 presents as follows. His medical and personal histories are insignificant with no exposure to cold. What would you suspect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chilb​lains","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"COVID​ toes","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Vari​cella","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Ecz​ema","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. COVID toes","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/659da52e46934adbb21f851fade520a5.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. COVID toes</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>COVID toes</strong>. The image shows pernio-like lesions caused due to COVID-19.</p>\n<p><strong>Pernio / chilblains</strong> are skin lesions on the acral surfaces of the body characterised by the development of erythematous-violaceous or <strong>purpuric macules</strong>. They may be accompanied by edema and pruritus. </p>\n<p>It is usually due to cold exposure or underlying hematological disorders but has also been reported in patients with COVID-19 disease. The underlying mechanism is thought to be an inflammatory process. The lesions appear late (<strong>postviral phase</strong>) with the patients exhibiting <strong>positive  IgM/ IgG </strong>antibodies for SARS-CoV-2 and <strong>negative RT-PCR</strong>. However, pernio like lesions can also be seen in early stages of the disease when the patients are still infective. </p>\n<p>There are no treatment guidelines but <strong>high-potency topical corticosteroids</strong> may be helpful if the lesions are causing discomfort. Other cutaneous manifestations include exanthematous rash, livedo reticularis, urticaria, MIS-C etc. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6852","text":"While donning a set of PPE​, what is the order that ​you will follow? Coverall​ First pair of gloves Sho‍e cover N-95 mask","raw_text":"<p>While donning a set of PPE, what is the order that you will follow?</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Coverall</li>\n<li>First pair of gloves</li>\n<li>Shoe cover</li>\n<li>N-95 mask</li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2→1​→3→4","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"1→2​→3→4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"1→2​→4→3","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"2→1​→4→3","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2→1→3→4","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2→1→3→4</strong></p><p>The correct sequence of donning a PPE is<strong> first pair of gloves → coverall → Shoe cover → N-95 mask.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Preparation for donning the PPE:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To be performed only in the designated area</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The health care worker must be prepared for the shift duty by\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Taking a meal</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Using the washroom</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Drinking water prior to entering the donning area</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Remove all accessories, jewelry</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Change into clean hospital scrubs and closed washable footwear</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Perform hand hygiene with soap and water</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Steps of donning/putting on the PPE: </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hand hygiene </li>\n<li>Wear the first pair of gloves\n<ul>\n<li>Sterile or unsterile</li>\n<li>Make sure the cuff of the gloves goes as far as possible</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Examine and wear coverall\n<ul>\n<li>Examine the coverall for any damage</li>\n<li>Start by wearing the leg sleeves and then the arm sleeves</li>\n<li>Zip up and cover the zip with the flap attached</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Wear shoe cover\n<ul>\n<li>Sit on the clean chair and wear shoe cover</li>\n<li>Pull them up till your calves</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Wear N-95 mask and wear hood\n<ul>\n<li>Hold the mask like a cup in the hand with both the straps hanging out in the front</li>\n<li>Place the mask on the face, pull and wear the lower strap first placing it below the ears</li>\n<li>Then pull and wear the upper strap placing above the ears</li>\n<li>The air leakage should be minimum around the mask when forcibly exhaling</li>\n<li>Wear the hood covering the forehead and the cheeks</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Wear face shield/eye goggles over the hood and ensure the fit</li>\n<li>Wear the second pair of gloves - pull the gloves to cover the sleeve cuff and forearm as much as possible</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6854","text":"While doffing, what is the​ first component of the P​PE that you would remove?​","raw_text":"While doffing, what is the first component of the PPE that you would remove?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cove​rall","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Shoe ​covers","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Face ​shield","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Glo​ves","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Shoe covers","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Shoe covers</strong></p><p>The component of the PPE kit to be removed first is the <strong>shoe covers.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Preparation for doffing the PPE:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To be performed only in the designated area</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Check for any leak or soiling</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Doffing room should have two chairs, dirty and clean</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">All the PPE must be discarded in the red bin</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hand hygiene must be performed after every step</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Steps of doffing the PPE:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Engage the observer and ask him to check the PPE - observer should check for any leak, tear, or contamination of PPE. If contaminated, then it should be disinfected with alcohol-based wipes.</li>\n<li>Disinfect the gloves</li>\n<li>Remove and discard shoe covers\n<ul>\n<li>Sit on the dirty chair with legs apart</li>\n<li>Remove the shoe covers by slowly pulling the outer surface starting from the top and then pull from the toes end. Discard them</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Disinfect and remove outer pair of gloves\n<ul>\n<li>While removing the outer gloves, inner gloves should not be torn or contaminated</li>\n<li>Pinch the first glove on the outer surface at the wrist and pull it inside out</li>\n<li>Hold this glove in the other hand like a ball</li>\n<li>Slide the thumb inside the other glove and remove inside out balling around the previous glove, making it into a single bag. Then discard it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Disinfect inner gloves</li>\n<li>Remove face shield/eye goggle - loosen the screw and remove the face shield by holding it from the back handle. Discard it.</li>\n<li>Disinfect inner gloves again</li>\n<li>Remove hood and coverall\n<ul>\n<li>Slide back the hood by holding it from the top</li>\n<li>Separate the zip flap and unzip the coverall suit</li>\n<li>Holding the suit at the arms, slide it off the shoulder slightly, then pull the suit from the side of the waist</li>\n<li>Roll the arms out one by one</li>\n<li>By touching the inner surface of the suit carefully roll out each leg. Discard it</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Disinfect and remove inner gloves as previously described</li>\n<li>Perform hand hygiene and wear a new pair of gloves</li>\n<li>Remove and discard N-95 mask\n<ul>\n<li>Do not touch the exposed surface of the mask</li>\n<li>Stoop forward and first remove the lower strap</li>\n<li>Then pull out the upper strap and remove the mask</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Disinfect gloves</li>\n<li>Sit on the clean chair and disinfect shoes using alcohol wipes</li>\n<li>Disinfect and remove gloves</li>\n<li>Perform hand hygiene</li>\n<li>Wipe the shoes on sodium hypochlorite soaked doormat at exit</li>\n<li>Exit the doffing area and take a shower</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6825","text":"You are a medical officer ​at the designated COVID h​ospital in your city and ​are required to don PPE e‍very day at work. Which a​rea of the PPE should you​ not touch while doffing?‌","raw_text":"You are a medical officer at the designated COVID hospital in your city and are required to don PPE every day at work. Which area of the PPE should you not touch while doffing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Earpiece / ties of the mas​k","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Inside ​the mask","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Front of f​ace shield","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Back of iso​lation gown","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Front of face shield","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Front of face shield</strong></p><p>The<strong> front </strong>of the <strong>face shield</strong> is considered to be a <strong>contaminated </strong>area of the <strong>personal protective equipment </strong>(PPE). Hence, it should not be touched while doffing.</p>\n<p>The areas that are considered <strong>clean</strong> are the parts that will be touched while removing a PPE:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Inside the gloves </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Inside and back of the gown including the ties</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Ties or earpieces of the mask, goggles, and face shields</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The areas that are considered <strong>contaminated</strong> are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Outside front and sleeves of the isolation gown </li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Front of goggles, mask, respirator, and face shield</li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Outside of the gloves</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3926","text":"After your COVID ICU shift​, where would you dispose​ your face shield?","raw_text":"After your COVID ICU shift, where would you dispose your face shield?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Red​ bin","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"Blac​k bag","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Yello​w bin","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Whit​e bag","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. Red bin","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Red bin</strong></p><p>The <strong>face shield</strong> used by a <strong>healthcare worker</strong> during COVID-19 pandemic is disposed in the <strong>red bin</strong>.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD3920","text":"What is recommended for cl​eaning and disinfecting a​ healthcare facility agai​nst COVID-19?","raw_text":"What is recommended for cleaning and disinfecting a healthcare facility against COVID-19?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1% form​aldehyde","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1% gluta​raldehyde","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"35% isopro​pyl alcohol","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"1% sodium h​ypochlorite","correct":true}],"correct_answer":"D. 1% sodium hypochlorite","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. 1% sodium hypochlorite</strong></p><p><strong>1% sodium hypochlorite</strong> is recommended for cleaning and disinfection against COVID-19.</p>\n<p>The sodium hypochlorite solution should be prepared fresh. Leaving the solution for a contact time of at least <strong>10 minutes</strong> is recommended.</p>\n<p>Note: <strong>Alcohol</strong> (e.g. isopropyl 70% or ethyl alcohol 70%) can be used to wipe down surfaces where the use of bleach is not suitable, e.g. <strong>metals.</strong></p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD6851","text":"Identify the wrong match r​egarding COVID-19 vaccine​s:","raw_text":"Identify the wrong match regarding COVID-19 vaccines:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pfizer - mRNA based vaccin​e","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Covaxin - Inactivated vacc​ine","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Moderna - Recombinant DNA ​vaccine","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"AstraZeneca - Non-replicat​ing viral vector vaccine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Moderna - Recombinant DNA vaccine","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Moderna - Recombinant DNA vaccine</strong></p><p>The <strong>Moderna COVID-19 vaccine</strong> is also an <strong>mRNA-based</strong> vaccine.</p>\n<table style=\"height: 168px; width: 436px;\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\"><strong>Vaccines</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 208px;\"><strong>Mechanism</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">Pfizer/BioNtech (Comirnaty)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 208px;\">mRNA based vaccine </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">Moderna</td>\n<td style=\"width: 208px;\">mRNA based vaccine </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">Oxford University/ Astra Zeneca/ Serum Institute of India (Covishield)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 208px;\">Non-replicating viral vector</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">Gamaleya (Sputnik V)/ Dr.Reddys</td>\n<td style=\"width: 208px;\">Non-replicating viral vector</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">Johnson and Johnson (Janssen Ad26.CoV2.S)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 208px;\">Non-replicating viral vector</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">Bharat biotech/NIV (Covaxin)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 208px;\">Inactivated vaccine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">Zydus Cadila (ZyCoV-D)</td>\n<td style=\"width: 208px;\">Plasmid DNA vaccine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">Novavax </td>\n<td style=\"width: 208px;\">Protein subunit</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The Drugs Controller General of India (DCGI) has granted permission for <strong>restricted emergency use</strong> of the following vaccines against COVID-19:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Covaxin</strong> (Bharat biotech/NIV)</li>\n<li><strong>Covishield</strong> (Oxford University/ AstraZeneca/ Serum Institute of India)</li>\n<li><strong>Sputnik V </strong>(Gamaleya Research Institute/ Dr. Reddy's Laboratories)</li>\n<li><strong>ZyCoV-D</strong> (Zydus Cadila)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The FDA has issued an emergency use authorization for the following vaccines against COVID-19: </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pfizer/BioNtech</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Moderna</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Johnson and Johnson</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>According to the new vaccine guidelines by<strong> NEGVAC</strong> (National Expert Group on Vaccine Administration for COVID-19):</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: lower-roman;\">\n<li>Vaccination is to be deferred by <strong>3 months</strong> in the following persons: \n<ul>\n<li>Individuals (proven positive) recovered from the disease.</li>\n<li>Recipients of monoclonal antibodies or convalescent plasma.</li>\n<li>2<sup>nd</sup> dose is to be deferred by 3 months if infected with COVID after 1<sup>st</sup> dose. </li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: lower-roman;\">\n<li><strong>4-8 weeks</strong> gap, after recovery of patients with other serious illnesses requiring hospitalization/ICU care.</li>\n<li>Blood donation: Wait for <strong>14 days</strong> after receiving the vaccine/testing RT-PCR negative.</li>\n<li><strong>No screening</strong> is required by rapid antigen test (RAT) prior to vaccination.</li>\n<li>Recommended for all lactating women.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>COVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">MoHFW has recently approved the vaccination of pregnant women against COVID-19.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">COVID-19 vaccine can be taken anytime during pregnancy and <strong>should be taken at the earliest</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If any woman has been infected with COVID-19 during pregnancy, she should be vaccinated <strong>soon after delivery</strong>.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">COVID-19 vaccine is safe in pregnancy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"ME9999","text":"Four days after being extu​bated, an elderly diabeti​c man with COVID-19 devel​oped rhinosinusitis. The ‍tissue obtained from the ​sinuses showed the follow​ing. The physician wanted‌ to start the patient on ‍amphotericin B. However, ​it was not available. Whi‌ch other drug can be give‍n to this patient?","raw_text":"Four days after being extubated, an elderly diabetic man with COVID-19 developed rhinosinusitis. The tissue obtained from the sinuses showed the following. The physician wanted to start the patient on amphotericin B. However, it was not available. Which other drug can be given to this patient?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vorico​nazole","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Isavuc​onazole","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Itraco​nazole","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Flucy​tosine","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Isavuconazole","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4bab9392379c46eb85f2565ca6255c5c.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Isavuconazole</strong></p><p>The given clinical scenario is suggestive of <strong>mucormycosis</strong><strong>. </strong>Intravenous administration of<strong> amphotericin B </strong>is the first line treatment. Other drugs which are approved for its management include <strong>isavuconazole </strong>and <strong>posaconazole.</strong> Surgical debridement is the last line management.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Immunocompromised states are important risk factors including <strong>diabetes mellitus </strong>and <strong>glucocorticoid treatment </strong>as well as patients on<strong> ventilation. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Diagnosis can be made by culturing the organisms from sterile sites like needle aspirate, pleural fluid, or a tissue biopsy specimen. The biopsy is most sensitive. It reveals characteristic <strong>wide, thick-walled, ribbon-like, aseptate hyphal</strong> elements that branch at <strong>right angles</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">It can present as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Rhino-orbital-cerebral mucormycosis </strong>- (most common) the initial symptoms are nonspecific and include eye or facial pain and facial numbness followed by the onset of conjunctival suffusion and blurry vision. If untreated it can spread from the sinuses to the orbit, and then to the frontal lobe.</li>\n<li class=\"p1\"><strong>Pulmonary mucormycosis </strong>- (second most common)</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Cutaneous mucormycosis - usually results after trauma</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Gastrointestinal mucormycosis - primarily occurs in premature neonates who have disseminated disease or necrotizing enterocolitis</li>\n<li class=\"p1\">Disseminated mucormycosis</li>\n</ul>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7149","text":"A young girl, who recently​ recovered from COVID-19,​ presents with fever, abd​ominal pain and rashes fo‍r 4 days. On examination,​ strawberry tongue is obs​erved and cervical nodes ‌are found to be enlarged ‍bilaterally. What is the ​likely diagnosis? HR: 110‌/min BP: 80/60 mm Hg RR: ‍20/min CRP: 10 mg/L ESR: ​76mm/hr.","raw_text":"<p>A young girl, who recently recovered from COVID-19, presents with fever, abdominal pain and rashes for 4 days. On examination, strawberry tongue is observed and cervical nodes are found to be enlarged bilaterally. What is the likely diagnosis?</p>\n<p>HR: 110/min</p>\n<p>BP: 80/60 mm Hg</p>\n<p>RR: 20/min</p>\n<p>CRP: 10 mg/L</p>\n<p>ESR: 76mm/hr.</p>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Kawasaki​ disease","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Multisystem inflammatory s​yndrome in children","correct":true},{"label":"C","text":"Bacteria​l sepsis","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"Toxic shoc​k syndrome","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"B. Multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children</strong></p><p>This is a case of <strong>m</strong><strong>ultisystem inflammatory syndrome in children (MIS-C)</strong>, associated with<strong> COVID-19</strong> infection.</p>\n<p><strong>Diagnostic criteria for MIS-C:</strong></p>\n<p>Children and adolescents 0–18 years of age with fever ≥3 days</p>\n<p>AND two of these:</p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rash or bilateral non-purulent conjunctivitis or mucocutaneous inflammation signs (palmoplantar erythema, lip hyperemia, periorbital erythema and edema, strawberry tongue, and malar erythema)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hypotension or shock.</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Features of myocardial dysfunction, pericarditis, valvulitis, or coronary abnormalities (including ECHO findings or elevated Troponin/NT-proBNP)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Evidence of coagulopathy (PT, PTT, elevated D-Dimers)</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Acute gastrointestinal problems (diarrhoea, vomiting, or abdominal pain)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>AND</p>\n<p>Elevated ESR(&gt;40 mm), C-reactive protein (&gt;5 mg/L), or procalcitonin.</p>\n<p>AND</p>\n<p>No other obvious microbial cause of inflammation including bacterial sepsis, staphylococcal or streptococcal shock syndromes.</p>\n<p>AND</p>\n<p>Evidence of COVID-19 (RT-PCR, antigen test, or serology positive), or likely contact with patients with COVID-19.</p>\n<p><strong>Types of MIS-C</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>MIS-C with shock/multi-organ dysfunction</li>\n<li>Kawasaki phenotype</li>\n<li>MIS-C without shock</li>\n</ul>\n<p>MIS-C is treated with <strong>IV methylprednisolone</strong>. The dose can be increased in patients with shock. If the condition does not improve for 48-72 hours, specialists can be consulted for the addition of biologicals to the treatment regimen. </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD7307","text":"Which gene increases the r​isk of collapsing glomeru​lopathy in COVID-19 patie​nts?","raw_text":"Which gene increases the risk of collapsing glomerulopathy in COVID-19 patients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NP​HS1","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"AC​TN4","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"AP​OL1","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"TR​PC6","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. APOL1","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. APOL1</strong></p><p><strong>APOL1 gene</strong> increases the risk of <strong>collapsing nephropathy</strong> in patients infected with COVID-19. </p>\n<p>Glomerular lesions are reported in a minority of COVID-19 patients. They are associated with collapsing focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (<strong>FSGS</strong>) and are called <strong>COVID-associated nephropathy (COVAN)</strong>. Such patients present with nephrotic-range proteinuria and acute kidney injury (<strong>AKI</strong>). It specifically affects individuals of <strong>African </strong>ancestry who are carriers of this gene variant.</p>\n<p>Thrombotic microangiopathy is another uncommon finding among these patients. Histopathology is similar to those seen in other forms of collapsing glomerulopathy. But, <strong>tubulointerstitial lesions</strong> including microcystic tubular dilatation and tubular injury are typically associated with APOL1- related disease. </p>\n<div class=\"page\" title=\"Page 9\"> </div>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF0490","text":"As a healthcare worker wor​king in a COVID hospital,​ you receive the followin​g vaccine. Which of the f‍ollowing is not an advers​e event related to this v​accine?","raw_text":"As a healthcare worker working in a COVID hospital, you receive the following vaccine. Which of the following is not an adverse event related to this vaccine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Co​ugh","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"Tre​mors","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"Demyelinati​ng disorder","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"Dizziness​-giddiness","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. Demyelinating disorder","question_images":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/70fd540698f24905a3154dcbc7c9fed0.JPEG"],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Demyelinating disorder</strong></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><strong>Demyelinating disorders</strong> are rare adverse events following <strong>Covishield</strong>, not Covaxin.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\"><strong>Composition</strong> of Covaxin:</p>\n<ul>\n<li dir=\"ltr\">Inactivated coronavirus</li>\n<li dir=\"ltr\">Aluminum hydroxide gel</li>\n<li dir=\"ltr\">TLR 7/8 agonist</li>\n<li dir=\"ltr\">2-Phenoxyethanol and</li>\n<li dir=\"ltr\">Phosphate Buffered Saline/ NKA1</li>\n</ul>\n<p> <strong>Adverse effects:</strong></p>\n<table style=\"height: 273px;\" width=\"438\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 211px; text-align: center;\"><strong> Covaxin</strong></td>\n<td style=\"width: 211px; text-align: center;\"><strong>Covishield</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">\n<p>Fever, cough, sweating</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">Fever and chills</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">\n<p>Injection site pain and swelling</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">Injection site pain and swelling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">\n<p>Headache, bodyache, fatigue</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">Headache, malaise, fatigue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">\n<p>Abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">Nausea</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">\n<p>Dizziness, giddiness, tremor</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"width: 211px;\">\n<p>Myalgia, arthralgia</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p dir=\"ltr\">Rarely, complications like <strong>demyelinating</strong> disorders, <strong>Guillain-Barre syndrome,</strong> and vaccine-induced immune thrombotic thrombocytopenia (<strong>VITT</strong>) are also seen following <strong>Covishield </strong>vaccine.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MD8475","text":"In which of the following ​patients, would you admin​ister Covidshield vaccina​tion? A 51-year-old man w‍ho underwent hematopoieti​c stem cell transplantati​on 5 weeks ago for aplast‌ic anemia. A 60-year-old ‍man with a history of typ​e-2 diabetes mellitus. A ‌32-year-old woman who rec‍eived TT immunization 20 ​days back following a tri‍vial fall from a bike. A ​45-year-old man on dialys‍is three times a week for​ chronic kidney disease. ‍A 40-year-old woman on in​sulin therapy for type-1 ‌diabetes mellitus.","raw_text":"<p>In which of the following patients, would you administer Covidshield vaccination?</p>\n<ol>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">A 51-year-old man who underwent hematopoietic stem cell transplantation 5 weeks ago for aplastic anemia. </span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">A 60-year-old man with a history of type-2 diabetes mellitus. </span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">A 32-year-old woman who received TT immunization 20 days back following a trivial fall from a bike.</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">A 45-year-old man on dialysis three times a week for chronic kidney disease.</span></li>\n<li><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">A 40-year-old woman on insulin therapy for type-1 diabetes mellitus. </span></li>\n</ol>","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1, ​2, 3","correct":false},{"label":"B","text":"1, 2,​ 4, 5","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"2, 3,​ 4, 5","correct":true},{"label":"D","text":"3, ​4, 5","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"C. 2, 3, 4, 5","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. 2, 3, 4, 5</strong></p><p>The <span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">51-year-old man</span> should receive COVID-19 vaccination<strong> </strong>at least<strong> 3 months after hematopoietic cell transplantation</strong> (and not 5 weeks) to maximize vaccine efficacy. </p>\n<p><strong>Graft-versus-host disease</strong> and immunosuppressive regimens like systemic corticosteroids can blunt the immune responses to vaccination. Delay of vaccination until<strong> immunosuppressive therapy</strong> is reduced can be considered. </p>\n<p>Consider additional risk factors linked to <strong>adverse COVID-19 complications</strong> including, but not limited to: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Patients with advanced age (eg, ≥65 years of age) </li>\n<li>Patients with comorbidities (eg, chronic pulmonary, cardiovascular, or renal disease) </li>\n<li>Social and demographic factors</li>\n</ul>\n<p>COVID-19 vaccines should be prioritized over other vaccines. A gap of <strong>14 days</strong> is recommended between COVID-19 vaccines and any other vaccine. </p>\n<p>If COVID vaccine has been given first, then TT vaccine can be given on the same day. If TT vaccine have been given first, then wait for 14 days to give COVID vaccine.</p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null},{"id":"MF1980","text":"A 24-year-old boy who had ​SARS CoV-2 infection 3 mo​nths back came with compl​aints of fatigue and shor‍tness of breath for 3 day​s. The minimum duration o​f these symptoms to defin‌e it as post-Covid-19 con‍dition according to WHO i​s ____","raw_text":"A 24-year-old boy who had SARS CoV-2 infection 3 months back came with complaints of fatigue and shortness of breath for 3 days. The minimum duration of these symptoms to define it as post-Covid-19 condition according to WHO is ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 mo​nths","correct":true},{"label":"B","text":"2 w​eeks","correct":false},{"label":"C","text":"4 mo​nths","correct":false},{"label":"D","text":"4 w​eeks","correct":false}],"correct_answer":"A. 2 months","question_images":[],"explanation_images":[],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 2 months</strong></p><p>The minimum duration of symptoms such as fatigue, shortness of breath to define it as <strong>post Covid-19</strong> condition according to WHO is <strong>2 months.</strong></p>\n<p>WHO clinical case definition of post Covid-19 - Post Covid-19 condition occurs in individuals with a history of probable or confirmed SARS CoV-2 infection, usually <strong>3 months from the onset</strong> of Covid-19, with symptoms that <strong>last for at least 2 months</strong> and cannot be explained by an alternative diagnosis.</p>\n<p>Common symptoms include </p>\n<ul>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fatigue</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Shortness of breath</li>\n<li aria-level=\"1\" style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Cognitive dysfunction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p>@neet_​pg_bot</p>","bot":"@neet_​pg_bot","video":"","audio":null}],"build_id":"3wORybTf","path":["Medicine"]}];
